if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-1184"]="VOLUME ONE Chapter 1. Marseilles—The Arrival Chapter 2. Father and Son Chapter 3. The Catalans Chapter 4. Conspiracy Chapter 5. The Marriage Feast Chapter 6. The Deputy Procureur du Roi Chapter 7. The Examination Chapter 8. The Château d’If Chapter 9. The Evening of the Betrothal Chapter 10. The King’s Closet at the Tuileries Chapter 11. The Corsican Ogre Chapter 12. Father and Son Chapter 13. The Hundred Days Chapter 14. The Two Prisoners Chapter 15. Number 34 and Number 27 Chapter 16. A Learned Italian Chapter 17. The Abbé’s Chamber Chapter 18. The Treasure Chapter 19. The Third Attack Chapter 20. The Cemetery of the Château d’If Chapter 21. The Island of Tiboulen Chapter 22. The Smugglers Chapter 23. The Island of Monte Cristo Chapter 24. The Secret Cave Chapter 25. The Unknown Chapter 26. The Pont du Gard Inn Chapter 27. The Story\nVOLUME TWO Chapter 28. The Prison Register Chapter 29. The House of Morrel & Son Chapter 30. The Fifth of September Chapter 31. Italy: Sinbad the Sailor Chapter 32. The Waking Chapter 33. Roman Bandits Chapter 34. The Colosseum Chapter 35. La Mazzolata Chapter 36. The Carnival at Rome. Chapter 37. The Catacombs of Saint Sebastian Chapter 38. The Rendezvous Chapter 39. The Guests Chapter 40. The Breakfast Chapter 41. The Presentation Chapter 42. Monsieur Bertuccio Chapter 43. The House at Auteuil Chapter 44. The Vendetta Chapter 45. The Rain of Blood Chapter 46. Unlimited Credit Chapter 47. The Dappled Grays\n\nVOLUME THREE Chapter 48. Ideology Chapter 49. Haydée Chapter 50. The Morrel Family Chapter 51. Pyramus and Thisbe Chapter 52. Toxicology Chapter 53. Robert le Diable Chapter 54. A Flurry in Stocks Chapter 55. Major Cavalcanti Chapter 56. Andrea Cavalcanti Chapter 57. In the Lucern Patch Chapter 58. M. Noirtier de Villefort Chapter 59. The Will Chapter 60. The Telegraph Chapter 61. How a Gardener May Get Rid of the Dormice Chapter 62. Ghosts Chapter 63. The Dinner Chapter 64. The Beggar Chapter 65. A Conjugal Scene Chapter 66. Matrimonial Projects Chapter 67. The Office of the King’s Attorney Chapter 68. A Summer Ball Chapter 69. The Inquiry Chapter 70. The Ball Chapter 71. Bread and Salt Chapter 72. Madame de Saint-Méran Chapter 73. The Promise\nVOLUME FOUR Chapter 74. The Villefort Family Vault Chapter 75. A Signed Statement Chapter 76. Progress of Cavalcanti the Younger Chapter 77. Haydée Chapter 78. We hear From Yanina Chapter 79. The Lemonade Chapter 80. The Accusation Chapter 81. The Room of the Retired Baker Chapter 82. The Burglary Chapter 83. The Hand of God Chapter 84. Beauchamp Chapter 85. The Journey Chapter 86. The Trial Chapter 87. The Challenge Chapter 88. The Insult Chapter 89. The Night Chapter 90. The Meeting Chapter 91. Mother and Son Chapter 92. The Suicide Chapter 93. Valentine Chapter 94. Maximilian’s Avowal Chapter 95. Father and Daughter\n\n# Chapter 1. Marseilles—The Arrival\n\nVOLUME FIVE Chapter 96. The Contract Chapter 97. The Departure for Belgium Chapter 98. The Bell and Bottle Tavern Chapter 99. The Law Chapter 100. The Apparition Chapter 101. Locusta Chapter 102. Valentine Chapter 103. Maximilian Chapter 104. Danglars’ Signature Chapter 105. The Cemetery of Père-Lachaise Chapter 106. Dividing the Proceeds Chapter 107. The Lions’ Den Chapter 108. The Judge Chapter 109. The Assizes Chapter 110. The Indictment Chapter 111. Expiation Chapter 112. The Departure Chapter 113. The Past Chapter 114. Peppino Chapter 115. Luigi Vampa’s Bill of Fare Chapter 116. The Pardon Chapter 117. The Fifth of October\nOn the 24th of February, 1815, the look-out at Notre-Dame de la Garde signalled the three-master, the _Pharaon_ from Smyrna, Trieste, and Naples.\n\nAs usual, a pilot put off immediately, and rounding the Château d’If, got on board the vessel between Cape Morgiou and Rion island.\nImmediately, and according to custom, the ramparts of Fort Saint-Jean were covered with spectators; it is always an event at Marseilles for a ship to come into port, especially when this ship, like the _Pharaon_, has been built, rigged, and laden at the old Phocée docks, and belongs to an owner of the city.\n\nThe ship drew on and had safely passed the strait, which some volcanic shock has made between the Calasareigne and Jaros islands; had doubled Pomègue, and approached the harbor under topsails, jib, and spanker, but so slowly and sedately that the idlers, with that instinct which is the forerunner of evil, asked one another what misfortune could have happened on board. However, those experienced in navigation saw plainly that if any accident had occurred, it was not to the vessel herself, for she bore down with all the evidence of being skilfully handled, the anchor a-cockbill, the jib-boom guys already eased off, and standing by the side of the pilot, who was steering the _Pharaon_ towards the narrow entrance of the inner port, was a young man, who, with activity and vigilant eye, watched every motion of the ship, and repeated each direction of the pilot.\nThe vague disquietude which prevailed among the spectators had so much affected one of the crowd that he did not await the arrival of the vessel in harbor, but jumping into a small skiff, desired to be pulled alongside the _Pharaon_, which he reached as she rounded into La Réserve basin.\n\nWhen the young man on board saw this person approach, he left his station by the pilot, and, hat in hand, leaned over the ship’s bulwarks.\n\nHe was a fine, tall, slim young fellow of eighteen or twenty, with black eyes, and hair as dark as a raven’s wing; and his whole appearance bespoke that calmness and resolution peculiar to men accustomed from their cradle to contend with danger.\n\n“Ah, is it you, Dantès?” cried the man in the skiff. “What’s the matter? and why have you such an air of sadness aboard?”\n\n“A great misfortune, M. Morrel,” replied the young man, “a great misfortune, for me especially! Off Civita Vecchia we lost our brave Captain Leclere.”\n\n“And the cargo?” inquired the owner, eagerly.\n\n“Is all safe, M. Morrel; and I think you will be satisfied on that head. But poor Captain Leclere——”\n\n“What happened to him?” asked the owner, with an air of considerable resignation. “What happened to the worthy captain?”\n\n“He died.”\n\n“Fell into the sea?”\n\n“No, sir, he died of brain-fever in dreadful agony.” Then turning to the crew, he said, “Bear a hand there, to take in sail!”\n\nAll hands obeyed, and at once the eight or ten seamen who composed the crew, sprang to their respective stations at the spanker brails and outhaul, topsail sheets and halyards, the jib downhaul, and the topsail clewlines and buntlines. The young sailor gave a look to see that his orders were promptly and accurately obeyed, and then turned again to the owner.\n\n“And how did this misfortune occur?” inquired the latter, resuming the interrupted conversation.\n\n0023m\n“Alas, sir, in the most unexpected manner. After a long talk with the harbor-master, Captain Leclere left Naples greatly disturbed in mind. In twenty-four hours he was attacked by a fever, and died three days afterwards. We performed the usual burial service, and he is at his rest, sewn up in his hammock with a thirty-six-pound shot at his head and his heels, off El Giglio island. We bring to his widow his sword and cross of honor. It was worth while, truly,” added the young man with a melancholy smile, “to make war against the English for ten years, and to die in his bed at last, like everybody else.”\n\n“Why, you see, Edmond,” replied the owner, who appeared more comforted at every moment, “we are all mortal, and the old must make way for the young. If not, why, there would be no promotion; and since you assure me that the cargo——”\n\n“Is all safe and sound, M. Morrel, take my word for it; and I advise you not to take 25,000 francs for the profits of the voyage.”\n\nThen, as they were just passing the Round Tower, the young man shouted: “Stand by there to lower the topsails and jib; brail up the spanker!”\n\nThe order was executed as promptly as it would have been on board a man-of-war.\n\n“Let go—and clue up!” At this last command all the sails were lowered, and the vessel moved almost imperceptibly onwards.\n\n“Now, if you will come on board, M. Morrel,” said Dantès, observing the owner’s impatience, “here is your supercargo, M. Danglars, coming out of his cabin, who will furnish you with every particular. As for me, I must look after the anchoring, and dress the ship in mourning.”\n\nThe owner did not wait for a second invitation. He seized a rope which Dantès flung to him, and with an activity that would have done credit to a sailor, climbed up the side of the ship, while the young man, going to his task, left the conversation to Danglars, who now came towards the owner. He was a man of twenty-five or twenty-six years of age, of unprepossessing countenance, obsequious to his superiors, insolent to his subordinates; and this, in addition to his position as responsible agent on board, which is always obnoxious to the sailors, made him as much disliked by the crew as Edmond Dantès was beloved by them.\n\n“Well, M. Morrel,” said Danglars, “you have heard of the misfortune that has befallen us?”\n\n“Yes—yes: poor Captain Leclere! He was a brave and an honest man.”\n\n“And a first-rate seaman, one who had seen long and honorable service, as became a man charged with the interests of a house so important as that of Morrel & Son,” replied Danglars.\n\n“But,” replied the owner, glancing after Dantès, who was watching the anchoring of his vessel, “it seems to me that a sailor needs not be so old as you say, Danglars, to understand his business, for our friend Edmond seems to understand it thoroughly, and not to require instruction from anyone.”\n\n“Yes,” said Danglars, darting at Edmond a look gleaming with hate. “Yes, he is young, and youth is invariably self-confident. Scarcely was the captain’s breath out of his body when he assumed the command without consulting anyone, and he caused us to lose a day and a half at the Island of Elba, instead of making for Marseilles direct.”\n0025m\n\n“As to taking command of the vessel,” replied Morrel, “that was his duty as captain’s mate; as to losing a day and a half off the Island of Elba, he was wrong, unless the vessel needed repairs.”\n\n“The vessel was in as good condition as I am, and as, I hope you are, M. Morrel, and this day and a half was lost from pure whim, for the pleasure of going ashore, and nothing else.”\n\n“Dantès,” said the shipowner, turning towards the young man, “come this way!”\n\n“In a moment, sir,” answered Dantès, “and I’m with you.” Then calling to the crew, he said, “Let go!”\n\nThe anchor was instantly dropped, and the chain ran rattling through the port-hole. Dantès continued at his post in spite of the presence of the pilot, until this manœuvre was completed, and then he added, “Half-mast the colors, and square the yards!”\n\n“You see,” said Danglars, “he fancies himself captain already, upon my word.”\n\n“And so, in fact, he is,” said the owner.\n\n“Except your signature and your partner’s, M. Morrel.”\n\n“And why should he not have this?” asked the owner; “he is young, it is true, but he seems to me a thorough seaman, and of full experience.”\n\nA cloud passed over Danglars’ brow.\n\n“Your pardon, M. Morrel,” said Dantès, approaching, “the vessel now rides at anchor, and I am at your service. You hailed me, I think?”\n\nDanglars retreated a step or two. “I wished to inquire why you stopped at the Island of Elba?”\n\n“I do not know, sir; it was to fulfil the last instructions of Captain Leclere, who, when dying, gave me a packet for Marshal Bertrand.”\n\n“Then did you see him, Edmond?”\n\n“Who?”\n\n“The marshal.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nMorrel looked around him, and then, drawing Dantès on one side, he said suddenly—\n\n“And how is the emperor?”\n\n“Very well, as far as I could judge from the sight of him.”\n\n“You saw the emperor, then?”\n\n“He entered the marshal’s apartment while I was there.”\n\n“And you spoke to him?”\n\n“Why, it was he who spoke to me, sir,” said Dantès, with a smile.\n\n“And what did he say to you?”\n\n“Asked me questions about the vessel, the time she left Marseilles, the course she had taken, and what was her cargo. I believe, if she had not been laden, and I had been her master, he would have bought her. But I told him I was only mate, and that she belonged to the firm of Morrel & Son. ‘Ah, yes,’ he said, ‘I know them. The Morrels have been shipowners from father to son; and there was a Morrel who served in the same regiment with me when I was in garrison at Valence.’”\n\n“_Pardieu!_ and that is true!” cried the owner, greatly delighted. “And that was Policar Morrel, my uncle, who was afterwards a captain. Dantès, you must tell my uncle that the emperor remembered him, and you will see it will bring tears into the old soldier’s eyes. Come, come,” continued he, patting Edmond’s shoulder kindly, “you did very right, Dantès, to follow Captain Leclere’s instructions, and touch at Elba, although if it were known that you had conveyed a packet to the marshal, and had conversed with the emperor, it might bring you into trouble.”\n0027m\n\n“How could that bring me into trouble, sir?” asked Dantès; “for I did not even know of what I was the bearer; and the emperor merely made such inquiries as he would of the first comer. But, pardon me, here are the health officers and the customs inspectors coming alongside.” And the young man went to the gangway. As he departed, Danglars approached, and said,—\n\n“Well, it appears that he has given you satisfactory reasons for his landing at Porto-Ferrajo?”\n\n“Yes, most satisfactory, my dear Danglars.”\n\n“Well, so much the better,” said the supercargo; “for it is not pleasant to think that a comrade has not done his duty.”\n\n“Dantès has done his,” replied the owner, “and that is not saying much. It was Captain Leclere who gave orders for this delay.”\n\n“Talking of Captain Leclere, has not Dantès given you a letter from him?”\n\n“To me?—no—was there one?”\n\n“I believe that, besides the packet, Captain Leclere confided a letter to his care.”\n\n“Of what packet are you speaking, Danglars?”\n\n“Why, that which Dantès left at Porto-Ferrajo.”\n\n“How do you know he had a packet to leave at Porto-Ferrajo?”\n\nDanglars turned very red.\n\n“I was passing close to the door of the captain’s cabin, which was half open, and I saw him give the packet and letter to Dantès.”\n\n“He did not speak to me of it,” replied the shipowner; “but if there be any letter he will give it to me.”\n\nDanglars reflected for a moment. “Then, M. Morrel, I beg of you,” said he, “not to say a word to Dantès on the subject. I may have been mistaken.”\n\nAt this moment the young man returned; Danglars withdrew.\n\n“Well, my dear Dantès, are you now free?” inquired the owner.\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\n“You have not been long detained.”\n\n“No. I gave the custom-house officers a copy of our bill of lading; and as to the other papers, they sent a man off with the pilot, to whom I gave them.”\n\n“Then you have nothing more to do here?”\n\n“No—everything is all right now.”\n\n“Then you can come and dine with me?”\n\n“I really must ask you to excuse me, M. Morrel. My first visit is due to my father, though I am not the less grateful for the honor you have done me.”\n\n0029m\n“Right, Dantès, quite right. I always knew you were a good son.”\n\n“And,” inquired Dantès, with some hesitation, “do you know how my father is?”\n\n“Well, I believe, my dear Edmond, though I have not seen him lately.”\n\n“Yes, he likes to keep himself shut up in his little room.”\n\n“That proves, at least, that he has wanted for nothing during your absence.”\n\nDantès smiled. “My father is proud, sir, and if he had not a meal left, I doubt if he would have asked anything from anyone, except from Heaven.”\n\n“Well, then, after this first visit has been made we shall count on you.”\n\n“I must again excuse myself, M. Morrel, for after this first visit has been paid I have another which I am most anxious to pay.”\n\n“True, Dantès, I forgot that there was at the Catalans someone who expects you no less impatiently than your father—the lovely Mercédès.”\n\nDantès blushed.\n\n“Ah, ha,” said the shipowner, “I am not in the least surprised, for she has been to me three times, inquiring if there were any news of the _Pharaon_. _Peste!_ Edmond, you have a very handsome mistress!”\n\n“She is not my mistress,” replied the young sailor, gravely; “she is my betrothed.”\n\n“Sometimes one and the same thing,” said Morrel, with a smile.\n\n“Not with us, sir,” replied Dantès.\n\n“Well, well, my dear Edmond,” continued the owner, “don’t let me detain you. You have managed my affairs so well that I ought to allow you all the time you require for your own. Do you want any money?”\n\n“No, sir; I have all my pay to take—nearly three months’ wages.”\n\n“You are a careful fellow, Edmond.”\n\n“Say I have a poor father, sir.”\n\n“Yes, yes, I know how good a son you are, so now hasten away to see your father. I have a son too, and I should be very wroth with those who detained him from me after a three months’ voyage.”\n\n“Then I have your leave, sir?”\n\n“Yes, if you have nothing more to say to me.”\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“Captain Leclere did not, before he died, give you a letter for me?”\n\n“He was unable to write, sir. But that reminds me that I must ask your leave of absence for some days.”\n\n“To get married?”\n\n“Yes, first, and then to go to Paris.”\n\n“Very good; have what time you require, Dantès. It will take quite six weeks to unload the cargo, and we cannot get you ready for sea until three months after that; only be back again in three months, for the _Pharaon_,” added the owner, patting the young sailor on the back, “cannot sail without her captain.”\n\n“Without her captain!” cried Dantès, his eyes sparkling with animation; “pray mind what you say, for you are touching on the most secret wishes of my heart. Is it really your intention to make me captain of the _Pharaon_?”\n\n“If I were sole owner we’d shake hands on it now, my dear Dantès, and call it settled; but I have a partner, and you know the Italian proverb—_Chi ha compagno ha padrone_—‘He who has a partner has a master.’ But the thing is at least half done, as you have one out of two votes. Rely on me to procure you the other; I will do my best.”\n\n“Ah, M. Morrel,” exclaimed the young seaman, with tears in his eyes, and grasping the owner’s hand, “M. Morrel, I thank you in the name of my father and of Mercédès.”\n\n“That’s all right, Edmond. There’s a providence that watches over the deserving. Go to your father; go and see Mercédès, and afterwards come to me.”\n\n“Shall I row you ashore?”\n\n“No, thank you; I shall remain and look over the accounts with Danglars. Have you been satisfied with him this voyage?”\n\n“That is according to the sense you attach to the question, sir. Do you mean is he a good comrade? No, for I think he never liked me since the day when I was silly enough, after a little quarrel we had, to propose to him to stop for ten minutes at the island of Monte Cristo to settle the dispute—a proposition which I was wrong to suggest, and he quite right to refuse. If you mean as responsible agent when you ask me the question, I believe there is nothing to say against him, and that you will be content with the way in which he has performed his duty.”\n\n“But tell me, Dantès, if you had command of the _Pharaon_ should you be glad to see Danglars remain?”\n\n“Captain or mate, M. Morrel, I shall always have the greatest respect for those who possess the owners’ confidence.”\n\n“That’s right, that’s right, Dantès! I see you are a thoroughly good fellow, and will detain you no longer. Go, for I see how impatient you are.”\n\n“Then I have leave?”\n\n“Go, I tell you.”\n\n“May I have the use of your skiff?”\n\n“Certainly.”\n\n“Then, for the present, M. Morrel, farewell, and a thousand thanks!”\n\n“I hope soon to see you again, my dear Edmond. Good luck to you.”\n\nThe young sailor jumped into the skiff, and sat down in the stern sheets, with the order that he be put ashore at La Canebière. The two oarsmen bent to their work, and the little boat glided away as rapidly as possible in the midst of the thousand vessels which choke up the narrow way which leads between the two rows of ships from the mouth of the harbor to the Quai d’Orléans.\n\nThe shipowner, smiling, followed him with his eyes until he saw him spring out on the quay and disappear in the midst of the throng, which from five o’clock in the morning until nine o’clock at night, swarms in the famous street of La Canebière,—a street of which the modern Phocéens are so proud that they say with all the gravity in the world, and with that accent which gives so much character to what is said, “If Paris had La Canebière, Paris would be a second Marseilles.” On turning round the owner saw Danglars behind him, apparently awaiting orders, but in reality also watching the young sailor,—but there was a great difference in the expression of the two men who thus followed the movements of Edmond Dantès.\n\n# Chapter 2. Father and Son\n\nWe will leave Danglars struggling with the demon of hatred, and endeavoring to insinuate in the ear of the shipowner some evil suspicions against his comrade, and follow Dantès, who, after having traversed La Canebière, took the Rue de Noailles, and entering a small house, on the left of the Allées de Meilhan, rapidly ascended four flights of a dark staircase, holding the baluster with one hand, while with the other he repressed the beatings of his heart, and paused before a half-open door, from which he could see the whole of a small room.\n\nThis room was occupied by Dantès’ father. The news of the arrival of the _Pharaon_ had not yet reached the old man, who, mounted on a chair, was amusing himself by training with trembling hand the nasturtiums and sprays of clematis that clambered over the trellis at his window. Suddenly, he felt an arm thrown around his body, and a well-known voice behind him exclaimed, “Father—dear father!”\n\nThe old man uttered a cry, and turned round; then, seeing his son, he fell into his arms, pale and trembling.\n\n“What ails you, my dearest father? Are you ill?” inquired the young man, much alarmed.\n\n“No, no, my dear Edmond—my boy—my son!—no; but I did not expect you; and joy, the surprise of seeing you so suddenly—Ah, I feel as if I were going to die.”\n\n“Come, come, cheer up, my dear father! ’Tis I—really I! They say joy never hurts, and so I came to you without any warning. Come now, do smile, instead of looking at me so solemnly. Here I am back again, and we are going to be happy.”\n\n“Yes, yes, my boy, so we will—so we will,” replied the old man; “but how shall we be happy? Shall you never leave me again? Come, tell me all the good fortune that has befallen you.”\n\n“God forgive me,” said the young man, “for rejoicing at happiness derived from the misery of others, but, Heaven knows, I did not seek this good fortune; it has happened, and I really cannot pretend to lament it. The good Captain Leclere is dead, father, and it is probable that, with the aid of M. Morrel, I shall have his place. Do you understand, father? Only imagine me a captain at twenty, with a hundred louis pay, and a share in the profits! Is this not more than a poor sailor like me could have hoped for?”\n\n“Yes, my dear boy,” replied the old man, “it is very fortunate.”\n\n“Well, then, with the first money I touch, I mean you to have a small house, with a garden in which to plant clematis, nasturtiums, and honeysuckle. But what ails you, father? Are you not well?”\n\n“’Tis nothing, nothing; it will soon pass away”—and as he said so the old man’s strength failed him, and he fell backwards.\n\n“Come, come,” said the young man, “a glass of wine, father, will revive you. Where do you keep your wine?”\n\n“No, no; thanks. You need not look for it; I do not want it,” said the old man.\n\n“Yes, yes, father, tell me where it is,” and he opened two or three cupboards.\n\n“It is no use,” said the old man, “there is no wine.”\n\n“What, no wine?” said Dantès, turning pale, and looking alternately at the hollow cheeks of the old man and the empty cupboards. “What, no wine? Have you wanted money, father?”\n\n“I want nothing now that I have you,” said the old man.\n\n“Yet,” stammered Dantès, wiping the perspiration from his brow,—“yet I gave you two hundred francs when I left, three months ago.”\n\n“Yes, yes, Edmond, that is true, but you forgot at that time a little debt to our neighbor, Caderousse. He reminded me of it, telling me if I did not pay for you, he would be paid by M. Morrel; and so, you see, lest he might do you an injury——”\n\n“Well?”\n“Why, I paid him.”\n\n“But,” cried Dantès, “it was a hundred and forty francs I owed Caderousse.”\n\n“Yes,” stammered the old man.\n\n“And you paid him out of the two hundred francs I left you?”\n\nThe old man nodded.\n\n“So that you have lived for three months on sixty francs,” muttered Edmond.\n\n“You know how little I require,” said the old man.\n\n“Heaven pardon me,” cried Edmond, falling on his knees before his father.\n\n“What are you doing?”\n\n“You have wounded me to the heart.”\n\n“Never mind it, for I see you once more,” said the old man; “and now it’s all over—everything is all right again.”\n0035m\n\n“Yes, here I am,” said the young man, “with a promising future and a little money. Here, father, here!” he said, “take this—take it, and send for something immediately.” And he emptied his pockets on the table, the contents consisting of a dozen gold pieces, five or six five-franc pieces, and some smaller coin. The countenance of old Dantès brightened.\n\n“Whom does this belong to?” he inquired.\n\n“To me, to you, to us! Take it; buy some provisions; be happy, and tomorrow we shall have more.”\n\n“Gently, gently,” said the old man, with a smile; “and by your leave I will use your purse moderately, for they would say, if they saw me buy too many things at a time, that I had been obliged to await your return, in order to be able to purchase them.”\n\n“Do as you please; but, first of all, pray have a servant, father. I will not have you left alone so long. I have some smuggled coffee and most capital tobacco, in a small chest in the hold, which you shall have tomorrow. But, hush, here comes somebody.”\n\n“’Tis Caderousse, who has heard of your arrival, and no doubt comes to congratulate you on your fortunate return.”\n\n“Ah, lips that say one thing, while the heart thinks another,” murmured Edmond. “But, never mind, he is a neighbor who has done us a service on a time, so he’s welcome.”\n\nAs Edmond paused, the black and bearded head of Caderousse appeared at the door. He was a man of twenty-five or six, and held a piece of cloth, which, being a tailor, he was about to make into a coat-lining.\n\n“What, is it you, Edmond, back again?” said he, with a broad Marseillaise accent, and a grin that displayed his ivory-white teeth.\n\n“Yes, as you see, neighbor Caderousse; and ready to be agreeable to you in any and every way,” replied Dantès, but ill-concealing his coldness under this cloak of civility.\n\n“Thanks—thanks; but, fortunately, I do not want for anything; and it chances that at times there are others who have need of me.” Dantès made a gesture. “I do not allude to you, my boy. No!—no! I lent you money, and you returned it; that’s like good neighbors, and we are quits.”\n\n“We are never quits with those who oblige us,” was Dantès’ reply; “for when we do not owe them money, we owe them gratitude.”\n\n“What’s the use of mentioning that? What is done is done. Let us talk of your happy return, my boy. I had gone on the quay to match a piece of mulberry cloth, when I met friend Danglars. ‘You at Marseilles?’—‘Yes,’ says he.\n\n“‘I thought you were at Smyrna.’—‘I was; but am now back again.’\n\n“‘And where is the dear boy, our little Edmond?’\n\n“‘Why, with his father, no doubt,’ replied Danglars. And so I came,” added Caderousse, “as fast as I could to have the pleasure of shaking hands with a friend.”\n\n0037m\n“Worthy Caderousse!” said the old man, “he is so much attached to us.”\n\n“Yes, to be sure I am. I love and esteem you, because honest folks are so rare. But it seems you have come back rich, my boy,” continued the tailor, looking askance at the handful of gold and silver which Dantès had thrown on the table.\n\nThe young man remarked the greedy glance which shone in the dark eyes of his neighbor. “Eh,” he said, negligently, “this money is not mine. I was expressing to my father my fears that he had wanted many things in my absence, and to convince me he emptied his purse on the table. Come, father” added Dantès, “put this money back in your box—unless neighbor Caderousse wants anything, and in that case it is at his service.”\n\n“No, my boy, no,” said Caderousse. “I am not in any want, thank God, my living is suited to my means. Keep your money—keep it, I say;—one never has too much;—but, at the same time, my boy, I am as much obliged by your offer as if I took advantage of it.”\n\n“It was offered with good will,” said Dantès.\n\n“No doubt, my boy; no doubt. Well, you stand well with M. Morrel I hear,—you insinuating dog, you!”\n\n“M. Morrel has always been exceedingly kind to me,” replied Dantès.\n\n“Then you were wrong to refuse to dine with him.”\n\n“What, did you refuse to dine with him?” said old Dantès; “and did he invite you to dine?”\n\n“Yes, my dear father,” replied Edmond, smiling at his father’s astonishment at the excessive honor paid to his son.\n\n“And why did you refuse, my son?” inquired the old man.\n\n“That I might the sooner see you again, my dear father,” replied the young man. “I was most anxious to see you.”\n\n“But it must have vexed M. Morrel, good, worthy man,” said Caderousse. “And when you are looking forward to be captain, it was wrong to annoy the owner.”\n\n“But I explained to him the cause of my refusal,” replied Dantès, “and I hope he fully understood it.”\n\n“Yes, but to be captain one must do a little flattery to one’s patrons.”\n\n“I hope to be captain without that,” said Dantès.\n\n“So much the better—so much the better! Nothing will give greater pleasure to all your old friends; and I know one down there behind the Saint Nicolas citadel who will not be sorry to hear it.”\n\n“Mercédès?” said the old man.\n\n“Yes, my dear father, and with your permission, now I have seen you, and know you are well and have all you require, I will ask your consent to go and pay a visit to the Catalans.”\n\n“Go, my dear boy,” said old Dantès; “and Heaven bless you in your wife, as it has blessed me in my son!”\n\n“His wife!” said Caderousse; “why, how fast you go on, father Dantès; she is not his wife yet, as it seems to me.”\n\n“No, but according to all probability she soon will be,” replied Edmond.\n\n“Yes—yes,” said Caderousse; “but you were right to return as soon as possible, my boy.”\n\n“And why?”\n\n“Because Mercédès is a very fine girl, and fine girls never lack followers; she particularly has them by dozens.”\n\n“Really?” answered Edmond, with a smile which had in it traces of slight uneasiness.\n\n0039m\n“Ah, yes,” continued Caderousse, “and capital offers, too; but you know, you will be captain, and who could refuse you then?”\n\n“Meaning to say,” replied Dantès, with a smile which but ill-concealed his trouble, “that if I were not a captain——”\n\n“Eh—eh!” said Caderousse, shaking his head.\n\n“Come, come,” said the sailor, “I have a better opinion than you of women in general, and of Mercédès in particular; and I am certain that, captain or not, she will remain ever faithful to me.”\n\n“So much the better—so much the better,” said Caderousse. “When one is going to be married, there is nothing like implicit confidence; but never mind that, my boy,—go and announce your arrival, and let her know all your hopes and prospects.”\n\n“I will go directly,” was Edmond’s reply; and, embracing his father, and nodding to Caderousse, he left the apartment.\n\nCaderousse lingered for a moment, then taking leave of old Dantès, he went downstairs to rejoin Danglars, who awaited him at the corner of the Rue Senac.\n\n“Well,” said Danglars, “did you see him?”\n\n“I have just left him,” answered Caderousse.\n\n“Did he allude to his hope of being captain?”\n\n“He spoke of it as a thing already decided.”\n\n“Indeed!” said Danglars, “he is in too much hurry, it appears to me.”\n\n“Why, it seems M. Morrel has promised him the thing.”\n\n“So that he is quite elated about it?”\n\n“Why, yes, he is actually insolent over the matter—has already offered me his patronage, as if he were a grand personage, and proffered me a loan of money, as though he were a banker.”\n\n“Which you refused?”\n\n“Most assuredly; although I might easily have accepted it, for it was I who put into his hands the first silver he ever earned; but now M. Dantès has no longer any occasion for assistance—he is about to become a captain.”\n\n“Pooh!” said Danglars, “he is not one yet.”\n\n“_Ma foi!_ it will be as well if he is not,” answered Caderousse; “for if he should be, there will be really no speaking to him.”\n\n“If we choose,” replied Danglars, “he will remain what he is; and perhaps become even less than he is.”\n\n“What do you mean?”\n\n“Nothing—I was speaking to myself. And is he still in love with the Catalane?”\n\n“Over head and ears; but, unless I am much mistaken, there will be a storm in that quarter.”\n\n0041m\n“Explain yourself.”\n\n“Why should I?”\n\n“It is more important than you think, perhaps. You do not like Dantès?”\n\n“I never like upstarts.”\n\n“Then tell me all you know about the Catalane.”\n\n“I know nothing for certain; only I have seen things which induce me to believe, as I told you, that the future captain will find some annoyance in the vicinity of the Vieilles Infirmeries.”\n\n“What have you seen?—come, tell me!”\n\n“Well, every time I have seen Mercédès come into the city she has been accompanied by a tall, strapping, black-eyed Catalan, with a red complexion, brown skin, and fierce air, whom she calls cousin.”\n\n“Really; and you think this cousin pays her attentions?”\n\n“I only suppose so. What else can a strapping chap of twenty-one mean with a fine wench of seventeen?”\n\n“And you say that Dantès has gone to the Catalans?”\n\n“He went before I came down.”\n\n“Let us go the same way; we will stop at La Réserve, and we can drink a glass of La Malgue, whilst we wait for news.”\n\n“Come along,” said Caderousse; “but you pay the score.”\n\n“Of course,” replied Danglars; and going quickly to the designated place, they called for a bottle of wine, and two glasses.\n\nPère Pamphile had seen Dantès pass not ten minutes before; and assured that he was at the Catalans, they sat down under the budding foliage of the planes and sycamores, in the branches of which the birds were singing their welcome to one of the first days of spring.\n\n# Chapter 3. The Catalans\n\nBeyond a bare, weather-worn wall, about a hundred paces from the spot where the two friends sat looking and listening as they drank their wine, was the village of the Catalans. Long ago this mysterious colony quitted Spain, and settled on the tongue of land on which it is to this day. Whence it came no one knew, and it spoke an unknown tongue. One of its chiefs, who understood Provençal, begged the commune of Marseilles to give them this bare and barren promontory, where, like the sailors of old, they had run their boats ashore. The request was granted; and three months afterwards, around the twelve or fifteen small vessels which had brought these gypsies of the sea, a small village sprang up. This village, constructed in a singular and picturesque manner, half Moorish, half Spanish, still remains, and is inhabited by descendants of the first comers, who speak the language of their fathers. For three or four centuries they have remained upon this small promontory, on which they had settled like a flight of seabirds, without mixing with the Marseillaise population, intermarrying, and preserving their original customs and the costume of their mother-country as they have preserved its language.\n\nOur readers will follow us along the only street of this little village, and enter with us one of the houses, which is sunburned to the beautiful dead-leaf color peculiar to the buildings of the country, and within coated with whitewash, like a Spanish posada. A young and beautiful girl, with hair as black as jet, her eyes as velvety as the gazelle’s, was leaning with her back against the wainscot, rubbing in her slender delicately moulded fingers a bunch of heath blossoms, the flowers of which she was picking off and strewing on the floor; her arms, bare to the elbow, brown, and modelled after those of the Arlesian Venus, moved with a kind of restless impatience, and she tapped the earth with her arched and supple foot, so as to display the pure and full shape of her well-turned leg, in its red cotton, gray and blue clocked, stocking. At three paces from her, seated in a chair which he balanced on two legs, leaning his elbow on an old worm-eaten table, was a tall young man of twenty, or two-and-twenty, who was looking at her with an air in which vexation and uneasiness were mingled. He questioned her with his eyes, but the firm and steady gaze of the young girl controlled his look.\n\n“You see, Mercédès,” said the young man, “here is Easter come round again; tell me, is this the moment for a wedding?”\n\n“I have answered you a hundred times, Fernand, and really you must be very stupid to ask me again.”\n\n“Well, repeat it,—repeat it, I beg of you, that I may at last believe it! Tell me for the hundredth time that you refuse my love, which had your mother’s sanction. Make me understand once for all that you are trifling with my happiness, that my life or death are nothing to you. Ah, to have dreamed for ten years of being your husband, Mercédès, and to lose that hope, which was the only stay of my existence!”\n\n“At least it was not I who ever encouraged you in that hope, Fernand,” replied Mercédès; “you cannot reproach me with the slightest coquetry. I have always said to you, ‘I love you as a brother; but do not ask from me more than sisterly affection, for my heart is another’s.’ Is not this true, Fernand?”\n\n“Yes, that is very true, Mercédès,” replied the young man, “Yes, you have been cruelly frank with me; but do you forget that it is among the Catalans a sacred law to intermarry?”\n\n0045m\n“You mistake, Fernand; it is not a law, but merely a custom, and, I pray of you, do not cite this custom in your favor. You are included in the conscription, Fernand, and are only at liberty on sufferance, liable at any moment to be called upon to take up arms. Once a soldier, what would you do with me, a poor orphan, forlorn, without fortune, with nothing but a half-ruined hut and a few ragged nets, the miserable inheritance left by my father to my mother, and by my mother to me? She has been dead a year, and you know, Fernand, I have subsisted almost entirely on public charity. Sometimes you pretend I am useful to you, and that is an excuse to share with me the produce of your fishing, and I accept it, Fernand, because you are the son of my father’s brother, because we were brought up together, and still more because it would give you so much pain if I refuse. But I feel very deeply that this fish which I go and sell, and with the produce of which I buy the flax I spin,—I feel very keenly, Fernand, that this is charity.”\n\n“And if it were, Mercédès, poor and lone as you are, you suit me as well as the daughter of the first shipowner or the richest banker of Marseilles! What do such as we desire but a good wife and careful housekeeper, and where can I look for these better than in you?”\n\n“Fernand,” answered Mercédès, shaking her head, “a woman becomes a bad manager, and who shall say she will remain an honest woman, when she loves another man better than her husband? Rest content with my friendship, for I say once more that is all I can promise, and I will promise no more than I can bestow.”\n\n“I understand,” replied Fernand, “you can endure your own wretchedness patiently, but you are afraid to share mine. Well, Mercédès, beloved by you, I would tempt fortune; you would bring me good luck, and I should become rich. I could extend my occupation as a fisherman, might get a place as clerk in a warehouse, and become in time a dealer myself.”\n\n“You could do no such thing, Fernand; you are a soldier, and if you remain at the Catalans it is because there is no war; so remain a fisherman, and contented with my friendship, as I cannot give you more.”\n\n“Well, I will do better, Mercédès. I will be a sailor; instead of the costume of our fathers, which you despise, I will wear a varnished hat, a striped shirt, and a blue jacket, with an anchor on the buttons. Would not that dress please you?”\n\n“What do you mean?” asked Mercédès, with an angry glance,—“what do you mean? I do not understand you?”\n\n“I mean, Mercédès, that you are thus harsh and cruel with me, because you are expecting someone who is thus attired; but perhaps he whom you await is inconstant, or if he is not, the sea is so to him.”\n\n“Fernand,” cried Mercédès, “I believed you were good-hearted, and I was mistaken! Fernand, you are wicked to call to your aid jealousy and the anger of God! Yes, I will not deny it, I do await, and I do love him of whom you speak; and, if he does not return, instead of accusing him of the inconstancy which you insinuate, I will tell you that he died loving me and me only.” The young girl made a gesture of rage. “I understand you, Fernand; you would be revenged on him because I do not love you; you would cross your Catalan knife with his dirk. What end would that answer? To lose you my friendship if he were conquered, and see that friendship changed into hate if you were victor. Believe me, to seek a quarrel with a man is a bad method of pleasing the woman who loves that man. No, Fernand, you will not thus give way to evil thoughts. Unable to have me for your wife, you will content yourself with having me for your friend and sister; and besides,” she added, her eyes troubled and moistened with tears, “wait, wait, Fernand; you said just now that the sea was treacherous, and he has been gone four months, and during these four months there have been some terrible storms.”\n\nFernand made no reply, nor did he attempt to check the tears which flowed down the cheeks of Mercédès, although for each of these tears he would have shed his heart’s blood; but these tears flowed for another. He arose, paced a while up and down the hut, and then, suddenly stopping before Mercédès, with his eyes glowing and his hands clenched,—“Say, Mercédès,” he said, “once for all, is this your final determination?”\n\n“I love Edmond Dantès,” the young girl calmly replied, “and none but Edmond shall ever be my husband.”\n\n“And you will always love him?”\n\n“As long as I live.”\n\nFernand let fall his head like a defeated man, heaved a sigh that was like a groan, and then suddenly looking her full in the face, with clenched teeth and expanded nostrils, said,—“But if he is dead——”\n\n“If he is dead, I shall die too.”\n\n“If he has forgotten you——”\n\n“Mercédès!” called a joyous voice from without,—“Mercédès!”\n\n“Ah,” exclaimed the young girl, blushing with delight, and fairly leaping in excess of love, “you see he has not forgotten me, for here he is!” And rushing towards the door, she opened it, saying, “Here, Edmond, here I am!”\n\nFernand, pale and trembling, drew back, like a traveller at the sight of a serpent, and fell into a chair beside him. Edmond and Mercédès were clasped in each other’s arms. The burning Marseilles sun, which shot into the room through the open door, covered them with a flood of light. At first they saw nothing around them. Their intense happiness isolated them from all the rest of the world, and they only spoke in broken words, which are the tokens of a joy so extreme that they seem rather the expression of sorrow. Suddenly Edmond saw the gloomy, pale, and threatening countenance of Fernand, as it was defined in the shadow. By a movement for which he could scarcely account to himself, the young Catalan placed his hand on the knife at his belt.\n\n“Ah, your pardon,” said Dantès, frowning in his turn; “I did not perceive that there were three of us.” Then, turning to Mercédès, he inquired, “Who is this gentleman?”\n\n“One who will be your best friend, Dantès, for he is my friend, my cousin, my brother; it is Fernand—the man whom, after you, Edmond, I love the best in the world. Do you not remember him?”\n\n“Yes!” said Dantès, and without relinquishing Mercédès’ hand clasped in one of his own, he extended the other to the Catalan with a cordial air. But Fernand, instead of responding to this amiable gesture, remained mute and trembling. Edmond then cast his eyes scrutinizingly at the agitated and embarrassed Mercédès, and then again on the gloomy and menacing Fernand. This look told him all, and his anger waxed hot.\n\n“I did not know, when I came with such haste to you, that I was to meet an enemy here.”\n\n“An enemy!” cried Mercédès, with an angry look at her cousin. “An enemy in my house, do you say, Edmond! If I believed that, I would place my arm under yours and go with you to Marseilles, leaving the house to return to it no more.”\n\nFernand’s eye darted lightning. “And should any misfortune occur to you, dear Edmond,” she continued with the same calmness which proved to Fernand that the young girl had read the very innermost depths of his sinister thought, “if misfortune should occur to you, I would ascend the highest point of the Cape de Morgiou and cast myself headlong from it.”\n\nFernand became deadly pale. “But you are deceived, Edmond,” she continued. “You have no enemy here—there is no one but Fernand, my brother, who will grasp your hand as a devoted friend.”\n\nAnd at these words the young girl fixed her imperious look on the Catalan, who, as if fascinated by it, came slowly towards Edmond, and offered him his hand. His hatred, like a powerless though furious wave, was broken against the strong ascendancy which Mercédès exercised over him. Scarcely, however, had he touched Edmond’s hand when he felt he had done all he could do, and rushed hastily out of the house.\n\n“Oh,” he exclaimed, running furiously and tearing his hair—“Oh, who will deliver me from this man? Wretched—wretched that I am!”\n\n“Hallo, Catalan! Hallo, Fernand! where are you running to?” exclaimed a voice.\n\nThe young man stopped suddenly, looked around him, and perceived Caderousse sitting at table with Danglars, under an arbor.\n\n“Well”, said Caderousse, “why don’t you come? Are you really in such a hurry that you have no time to pass the time of day with your friends?”\n\n“Particularly when they have still a full bottle before them,” added Danglars. Fernand looked at them both with a stupefied air, but did not say a word.\n\n“He seems besotted,” said Danglars, pushing Caderousse with his knee. “Are we mistaken, and is Dantès triumphant in spite of all we have believed?”\n\n“Why, we must inquire into that,” was Caderousse’s reply; and turning towards the young man, said, “Well, Catalan, can’t you make up your mind?”\n\nFernand wiped away the perspiration steaming from his brow, and slowly entered the arbor, whose shade seemed to restore somewhat of calmness to his senses, and whose coolness somewhat of refreshment to his exhausted body.\n\n“Good-day,” said he. “You called me, didn’t you?” And he fell, rather than sat down, on one of the seats which surrounded the table.\n\n“I called you because you were running like a madman, and I was afraid you would throw yourself into the sea,” said Caderousse, laughing. “Why, when a man has friends, they are not only to offer him a glass of wine, but, moreover, to prevent his swallowing three or four pints of water unnecessarily!”\n\nFernand gave a groan, which resembled a sob, and dropped his head into his hands, his elbows leaning on the table.\n\n“Well, Fernand, I must say,” said Caderousse, beginning the conversation, with that brutality of the common people in which curiosity destroys all diplomacy, “you look uncommonly like a rejected lover;” and he burst into a hoarse laugh.\n\n“Bah!” said Danglars, “a lad of his make was not born to be unhappy in love. You are laughing at him, Caderousse.”\n\n“No,” he replied, “only hark how he sighs! Come, come, Fernand,” said Caderousse, “hold up your head, and answer us. It’s not polite not to reply to friends who ask news of your health.”\n\n“My health is well enough,” said Fernand, clenching his hands without raising his head.\n\n“Ah, you see, Danglars,” said Caderousse, winking at his friend, “this is how it is; Fernand, whom you see here, is a good and brave Catalan, one of the best fishermen in Marseilles, and he is in love with a very fine girl, named Mercédès; but it appears, unfortunately, that the fine girl is in love with the mate of the _Pharaon_; and as the _Pharaon_ arrived today—why, you understand!”\n\n“No; I do not understand,” said Danglars.\n\n“Poor Fernand has been dismissed,” continued Caderousse.\n\n“Well, and what then?” said Fernand, lifting up his head, and looking at Caderousse like a man who looks for someone on whom to vent his anger; “Mercédès is not accountable to any person, is she? Is she not free to love whomsoever she will?”\n\n“Oh, if you take it in that sense,” said Caderousse, “it is another thing. But I thought you were a Catalan, and they told me the Catalans were not men to allow themselves to be supplanted by a rival. It was even told me that Fernand, especially, was terrible in his vengeance.”\n\nFernand smiled piteously. “A lover is never terrible,” he said.\n\n“Poor fellow!” remarked Danglars, affecting to pity the young man from the bottom of his heart. “Why, you see, he did not expect to see Dantès return so suddenly—he thought he was dead, perhaps; or perchance faithless! These things always come on us more severely when they come suddenly.”\n\n“Ah, _ma foi_, under any circumstances!” said Caderousse, who drank as he spoke, and on whom the fumes of the wine began to take effect,—“under any circumstances Fernand is not the only person put out by the fortunate arrival of Dantès; is he, Danglars?”\n\n“No, you are right—and I should say that would bring him ill-luck.”\n\n“Well, never mind,” answered Caderousse, pouring out a glass of wine for Fernand, and filling his own for the eighth or ninth time, while Danglars had merely sipped his. “Never mind—in the meantime he marries Mercédès—the lovely Mercédès—at least he returns to do that.”\n\nDuring this time Danglars fixed his piercing glance on the young man, on whose heart Caderousse’s words fell like molten lead.\n\n“And when is the wedding to be?” he asked.\n\n“Oh, it is not yet fixed!” murmured Fernand.\n\n“No, but it will be,” said Caderousse, “as surely as Dantès will be captain of the _Pharaon_—eh, Danglars?”\n\nDanglars shuddered at this unexpected attack, and turned to Caderousse, whose countenance he scrutinized, to try and detect whether the blow was premeditated; but he read nothing but envy in a countenance already rendered brutal and stupid by drunkenness.\n\n“Well,” said he, filling the glasses, “let us drink to Captain Edmond Dantès, husband of the beautiful Catalane!”\n\nCaderousse raised his glass to his mouth with unsteady hand, and swallowed the contents at a gulp. Fernand dashed his on the ground.\n\n“Eh, eh, eh!” stammered Caderousse. “What do I see down there by the wall, in the direction of the Catalans? Look, Fernand, your eyes are better than mine. I believe I see double. You know wine is a deceiver; but I should say it was two lovers walking side by side, and hand in hand. Heaven forgive me, they do not know that we can see them, and they are actually embracing!”\n\nDanglars did not lose one pang that Fernand endured.\n\n“Do you know them, Fernand?” he said.\n\n“Yes,” was the reply, in a low voice. “It is Edmond and Mercédès!”\n\n“Ah, see there, now!” said Caderousse; “and I did not recognize them! Hallo, Dantès! hello, lovely damsel! Come this way, and let us know when the wedding is to be, for Fernand here is so obstinate he will not tell us.”\n\n“Hold your tongue, will you?” said Danglars, pretending to restrain Caderousse, who, with the tenacity of drunkards, leaned out of the arbor. “Try to stand upright, and let the lovers make love without interruption. See, look at Fernand, and follow his example; he is well-behaved!”\n\n0051m\nFernand, probably excited beyond bearing, pricked by Danglars, as the bull is by the bandilleros, was about to rush out; for he had risen from his seat, and seemed to be collecting himself to dash headlong upon his rival, when Mercédès, smiling and graceful, lifted up her lovely head, and looked at them with her clear and bright eyes. At this Fernand recollected her threat of dying if Edmond died, and dropped again heavily on his seat. Danglars looked at the two men, one after the other, the one brutalized by liquor, the other overwhelmed with love.\n\n“I shall get nothing from these fools,” he muttered; “and I am very much afraid of being here between a drunkard and a coward. Here’s an envious fellow making himself boozy on wine when he ought to be nursing his wrath, and here is a fool who sees the woman he loves stolen from under his nose and takes on like a big baby. Yet this Catalan has eyes that glisten like those of the vengeful Spaniards, Sicilians, and Calabrians, and the other has fists big enough to crush an ox at one blow. Unquestionably, Edmond’s star is in the ascendant, and he will marry the splendid girl—he will be captain, too, and laugh at us all, unless”—a sinister smile passed over Danglars’ lips—“unless I take a hand in the affair,” he added.\n\n“Hallo!” continued Caderousse, half-rising, and with his fist on the table, “hallo, Edmond! do you not see your friends, or are you too proud to speak to them?”\n\n“No, my dear fellow!” replied Dantès, “I am not proud, but I am happy, and happiness blinds, I think, more than pride.”\n\n“Ah, very well, that’s an explanation!” said Caderousse. “How do you do, Madame Dantès?”\n\nMercédès courtesied gravely, and said—“That is not my name, and in my country it bodes ill fortune, they say, to call a young girl by the name of her betrothed before he becomes her husband. So call me Mercédès, if you please.”\n\n“We must excuse our worthy neighbor, Caderousse,” said Dantès, “he is so easily mistaken.”\n\n“So, then, the wedding is to take place immediately, M. Dantès,” said Danglars, bowing to the young couple.\n\n“As soon as possible, M. Danglars; today all preliminaries will be arranged at my father’s, and tomorrow, or next day at latest, the wedding festival here at La Réserve. My friends will be there, I hope; that is to say, you are invited, M. Danglars, and you, Caderousse.”\n\n“And Fernand,” said Caderousse with a chuckle; “Fernand, too, is invited!”\n\n“My wife’s brother is my brother,” said Edmond; “and we, Mercédès and I, should be very sorry if he were absent at such a time.”\n\nFernand opened his mouth to reply, but his voice died on his lips, and he could not utter a word.\n\n“Today the preliminaries, tomorrow or next day the ceremony! You are in a hurry, captain!”\n\n“Danglars,” said Edmond, smiling, “I will say to you as Mercédès said just now to Caderousse, ‘Do not give me a title which does not belong to me’; that may bring me bad luck.”\n\n“Your pardon,” replied Danglars, “I merely said you seemed in a hurry, and we have lots of time; the _Pharaon_ cannot be under weigh again in less than three months.”\n\n“We are always in a hurry to be happy, M. Danglars; for when we have suffered a long time, we have great difficulty in believing in good fortune. But it is not selfishness alone that makes me thus in haste; I must go to Paris.”\n\n“Ah, really?—to Paris! and will it be the first time you have ever been there, Dantès?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Have you business there?”\n\n“Not of my own; the last commission of poor Captain Leclere; you know to what I allude, Danglars—it is sacred. Besides, I shall only take the time to go and return.”\n\n“Yes, yes, I understand,” said Danglars, and then in a low tone, he added, “To Paris, no doubt to deliver the letter which the grand marshal gave him. Ah, this letter gives me an idea—a capital idea! Ah; Dantès, my friend, you are not yet registered number one on board the good ship _Pharaon_;” then turning towards Edmond, who was walking away, “A pleasant journey,” he cried.\n\n“Thank you,” said Edmond with a friendly nod, and the two lovers continued on their way, as calm and joyous as if they were the very elect of heaven.\n\n# Chapter 4. Conspiracy\n\nDanglars followed Edmond and Mercédès with his eyes until the two lovers disappeared behind one of the angles of Fort Saint Nicolas; then, turning round, he perceived Fernand, who had fallen, pale and trembling, into his chair, while Caderousse stammered out the words of a drinking-song.\n\n“Well, my dear sir,” said Danglars to Fernand, “here is a marriage which does not appear to make everybody happy.”\n\n“It drives me to despair,” said Fernand.\n\n“Do you, then, love Mercédès?”\n\n“I adore her!”\n\n“For long?”\n\n“As long as I have known her—always.”\n\n“And you sit there, tearing your hair, instead of seeking to remedy your condition; I did not think that was the way of your people.”\n\n“What would you have me do?” said Fernand.\n\n“How do I know? Is it my affair? I am not in love with Mademoiselle Mercédès; but for you—in the words of the gospel, seek, and you shall find.”\n\n“I have found already.”\n\n“What?”\n\n“I would stab the man, but the woman told me that if any misfortune happened to her betrothed, she would kill herself.”\n\n“Pooh! Women say those things, but never do them.”\n\n“You do not know Mercédès; what she threatens she will do.”\n\n“Idiot!” muttered Danglars; “whether she kill herself or not, what matter, provided Dantès is not captain?”\n\n“Before Mercédès should die,” replied Fernand, with the accents of unshaken resolution, “I would die myself!”\n\n“That’s what I call love!” said Caderousse with a voice more tipsy than ever. “That’s love, or I don’t know what love is.”\n\n“Come,” said Danglars, “you appear to me a good sort of fellow, and hang me, I should like to help you, but——”\n\n“Yes,” said Caderousse, “but how?”\n\n“My dear fellow,” replied Danglars, “you are three parts drunk; finish the bottle, and you will be completely so. Drink then, and do not meddle with what we are discussing, for that requires all one’s wit and cool judgment.”\n\n“I—drunk!” said Caderousse; “well that’s a good one! I could drink four more such bottles; they are no bigger than cologne flasks. Père Pamphile, more wine!”\n\nAnd Caderousse rattled his glass upon the table.\n\n“You were saying, sir——” said Fernand, awaiting with great anxiety the end of this interrupted remark.\n\n“What was I saying? I forget. This drunken Caderousse has made me lose the thread of my sentence.”\n\n“Drunk, if you like; so much the worse for those who fear wine, for it is because they have bad thoughts which they are afraid the liquor will extract from their hearts;” and Caderousse began to sing the two last lines of a song very popular at the time:\n\n‘Tous les méchants sont buveurs d’eau;\n\nC’est bien prouvé par le déluge.’1\n\n“You said, sir, you would like to help me, but——”\n\n“Yes; but I added, to help you it would be sufficient that Dantès did not marry her you love; and the marriage may easily be thwarted, methinks, and yet Dantès need not die.”\n\n“Death alone can separate them,” remarked Fernand.\n\n“You talk like a noodle, my friend,” said Caderousse; “and here is Danglars, who is a wide-awake, clever, deep fellow, who will prove to you that you are wrong. Prove it, Danglars. I have answered for you. Say there is no need why Dantès should die; it would, indeed, be a pity he should. Dantès is a good fellow; I like Dantès. Dantès, your health.”\n\nFernand rose impatiently. “Let him run on,” said Danglars, restraining the young man; “drunk as he is, he is not much out in what he says. Absence severs as well as death, and if the walls of a prison were between Edmond and Mercédès they would be as effectually separated as if he lay under a tombstone.”\n0056m\n\n“Yes; but one gets out of prison,” said Caderousse, who, with what sense was left him, listened eagerly to the conversation, “and when one gets out and one’s name is Edmond Dantès, one seeks revenge——”\n\n“What matters that?” muttered Fernand.\n\n“And why, I should like to know,” persisted Caderousse, “should they put Dantès in prison? he has neither robbed, nor killed, nor murdered.”\n\n“Hold your tongue!” said Danglars.\n\n“I won’t hold my tongue!” replied Caderousse; “I say I want to know why they should put Dantès in prison; I like Dantès; Dantès, your health!” and he swallowed another glass of wine.\n\n0057m\nDanglars saw in the muddled look of the tailor the progress of his intoxication, and turning towards Fernand, said, “Well, you understand there is no need to kill him.”\n\n“Certainly not, if, as you said just now, you have the means of having Dantès arrested. Have you that means?”\n\n“It is to be found for the searching. But why should I meddle in the matter? it is no affair of mine.”\n\n“I know not why you meddle,” said Fernand, seizing his arm; “but this I know, you have some motive of personal hatred against Dantès, for he who himself hates is never mistaken in the sentiments of others.”\n\n“I! motives of hatred against Dantès? None, on my word! I saw you were unhappy, and your unhappiness interested me; that’s all; but since you believe I act for my own account, adieu, my dear friend, get out of the affair as best you may;” and Danglars rose as if he meant to depart.\n\n“No, no,” said Fernand, restraining him, “stay! It is of very little consequence to me at the end of the matter whether you have any angry feeling or not against Dantès. I hate him! I confess it openly. Do you find the means, I will execute it, provided it is not to kill the man, for Mercédès has declared she will kill herself if Dantès is killed.”\n\nCaderousse, who had let his head drop on the table, now raised it, and looking at Fernand with his dull and fishy eyes, he said, “Kill Dantès! who talks of killing Dantès? I won’t have him killed—I won’t! He’s my friend, and this morning offered to share his money with me, as I shared mine with him. I won’t have Dantès killed—I won’t!”\n\n“And who has said a word about killing him, muddlehead?” replied Danglars. “We were merely joking; drink to his health,” he added, filling Caderousse’s glass, “and do not interfere with us.”\n\n“Yes, yes, Dantès’ good health!” said Caderousse, emptying his glass, “here’s to his health! his health—hurrah!”\n\n“But the means—the means?” said Fernand.\n\n“Have you not hit upon any?” asked Danglars.\n\n“No!—you undertook to do so.”\n\n“True,” replied Danglars; “the French have the superiority over the Spaniards, that the Spaniards ruminate, while the French invent.”\n\n“Do you invent, then,” said Fernand impatiently.\n\n“Waiter,” said Danglars, “pen, ink, and paper.”\n\n“Pen, ink, and paper,” muttered Fernand.\n\n“Yes; I am a supercargo; pen, ink, and paper are my tools, and without my tools I am fit for nothing.”\n\n“Pen, ink, and paper, then,” called Fernand loudly.\n\n“There’s what you want on that table,” said the waiter.\n\n“Bring them here.” The waiter did as he was desired.\n\n0059m\n“When one thinks,” said Caderousse, letting his hand drop on the paper, “there is here wherewithal to kill a man more sure than if we waited at the corner of a wood to assassinate him! I have always had more dread of a pen, a bottle of ink, and a sheet of paper, than of a sword or pistol.”\n\n“The fellow is not so drunk as he appears to be,” said Danglars. “Give him some more wine, Fernand.” Fernand filled Caderousse’s glass, who, like the confirmed toper he was, lifted his hand from the paper and seized the glass.\n\nThe Catalan watched him until Caderousse, almost overcome by this fresh assault on his senses, rested, or rather dropped, his glass upon the table.\n\n“Well!” resumed the Catalan, as he saw the final glimmer of Caderousse’s reason vanishing before the last glass of wine.\n\n“Well, then, I should say, for instance,” resumed Danglars, “that if after a voyage such as Dantès has just made, in which he touched at the Island of Elba, someone were to denounce him to the king’s procureur as a Bonapartist agent——”\n\n“I will denounce him!” exclaimed the young man hastily.\n\n“Yes, but they will make you then sign your declaration, and confront you with him you have denounced; I will supply you with the means of supporting your accusation, for I know the fact well. But Dantès cannot remain forever in prison, and one day or other he will leave it, and the day when he comes out, woe betide him who was the cause of his incarceration!”\n\n“Oh, I should wish nothing better than that he would come and seek a quarrel with me.”\n\n“Yes, and Mercédès! Mercédès, who will detest you if you have only the misfortune to scratch the skin of her dearly beloved Edmond!”\n\n“True!” said Fernand.\n\n“No, no,” continued Danglars; “if we resolve on such a step, it would be much better to take, as I now do, this pen, dip it into this ink, and write with the left hand (that the writing may not be recognized) the denunciation we propose.” And Danglars, uniting practice with theory, wrote with his left hand, and in a writing reversed from his usual style, and totally unlike it, the following lines, which he handed to Fernand, and which Fernand read in an undertone:\n\n“The honorable, the king’s attorney, is informed by a friend of the throne and religion, that one Edmond Dantès, mate of the ship _Pharaon_, arrived this morning from Smyrna, after having touched at Naples and Porto-Ferrajo, has been intrusted by Murat with a letter for the usurper, and by the usurper with a letter for the Bonapartist committee in Paris. Proof of this crime will be found on arresting him, for the letter will be found upon him, or at his father’s, or in his cabin on board the _Pharaon_.”\n\n“Very good,” resumed Danglars; “now your revenge looks like common sense, for in no way can it revert to yourself, and the matter will thus work its own way; there is nothing to do now but fold the letter as I am doing, and write upon it, ‘To the king’s attorney,’ and that’s all settled.” And Danglars wrote the address as he spoke.\n\n“Yes, and that’s all settled!” exclaimed Caderousse, who, by a last effort of intellect, had followed the reading of the letter, and instinctively comprehended all the misery which such a denunciation must entail. “Yes, and that’s all settled; only it will be an infamous shame;” and he stretched out his hand to reach the letter.\n\n“Yes,” said Danglars, taking it from beyond his reach; “and as what I say and do is merely in jest, and I, amongst the first and foremost, should be sorry if anything happened to Dantès—the worthy Dantès—look here!” And taking the letter, he squeezed it up in his hands and threw it into a corner of the arbor.\n\n“All right!” said Caderousse. “Dantès is my friend, and I won’t have him ill-used.”\n\n“And who thinks of using him ill? Certainly neither I nor Fernand,” said Danglars, rising and looking at the young man, who still remained seated, but whose eye was fixed on the denunciatory sheet of paper flung into the corner.\n\n“In this case,” replied Caderousse, “let’s have some more wine. I wish to drink to the health of Edmond and the lovely Mercédès.”\n\n“You have had too much already, drunkard,” said Danglars; “and if you continue, you will be compelled to sleep here, because unable to stand on your legs.”\n\n“I?” said Caderousse, rising with all the offended dignity of a drunken man, “I can’t keep on my legs? Why, I’ll wager I can go up into the belfry of the Accoules, and without staggering, too!”\n\n“Done!” said Danglars, “I’ll take your bet; but tomorrow—today it is time to return. Give me your arm, and let us go.”\n\n“Very well, let us go,” said Caderousse; “but I don’t want your arm at all. Come, Fernand, won’t you return to Marseilles with us?”\n\n“No,” said Fernand; “I shall return to the Catalans.”\n\n“You’re wrong. Come with us to Marseilles—come along.”\n\n“I will not.”\n\n“What do you mean? you will not? Well, just as you like, my prince; there’s liberty for all the world. Come along, Danglars, and let the young gentleman return to the Catalans if he chooses.”\n\nDanglars took advantage of Caderousse’s temper at the moment, to take him off towards Marseilles by the Porte Saint-Victor, staggering as he went.\n\nWhen they had advanced about twenty yards, Danglars looked back and saw Fernand stoop, pick up the crumpled paper, and putting it into his pocket then rush out of the arbor towards Pillon.\n\n“Well,” said Caderousse, “why, what a lie he told! He said he was going to the Catalans, and he is going to the city. Hallo, Fernand! You are coming, my boy!”\n\n“Oh, you don’t see straight,” said Danglars; “he’s gone right by the road to the Vieilles Infirmeries.”\n\n“Well,” said Caderousse, “I should have sworn that he turned to the right—how treacherous wine is!”\n\n“Come, come,” said Danglars to himself, “now the thing is at work and it will effect its purpose unassisted.”\n\n# Chapter 5. The Marriage Feast\n\nThe morning’s sun rose clear and resplendent, touching the foamy waves into a network of ruby-tinted light.\n\nThe feast had been made ready on the second floor at La Réserve, with whose arbor the reader is already familiar. The apartment destined for the purpose was spacious and lighted by a number of windows, over each of which was written in golden letters for some inexplicable reason the name of one of the principal cities of France; beneath these windows a wooden balcony extended the entire length of the house. And although the entertainment was fixed for twelve o’clock, an hour previous to that time the balcony was filled with impatient and expectant guests, consisting of the favored part of the crew of the _Pharaon_, and other personal friends of the bridegroom, the whole of whom had arrayed themselves in their choicest costumes, in order to do greater honor to the occasion.\n\nVarious rumors were afloat to the effect that the owners of the _Pharaon_ had promised to attend the nuptial feast; but all seemed unanimous in doubting that an act of such rare and exceeding condescension could possibly be intended.\n\nDanglars, however, who now made his appearance, accompanied by Caderousse, effectually confirmed the report, stating that he had recently conversed with M. Morrel, who had himself assured him of his intention to dine at La Réserve.\n\nIn fact, a moment later M. Morrel appeared and was saluted with an enthusiastic burst of applause from the crew of the _Pharaon_, who hailed the visit of the shipowner as a sure indication that the man whose wedding feast he thus delighted to honor would ere long be first in command of the ship; and as Dantès was universally beloved on board his vessel, the sailors put no restraint on their tumultuous joy at finding that the opinion and choice of their superiors so exactly coincided with their own.\n\nWith the entrance of M. Morrel, Danglars and Caderousse were despatched in search of the bridegroom to convey to him the intelligence of the arrival of the important personage whose coming had created such a lively sensation, and to beseech him to make haste.\n\nDanglars and Caderousse set off upon their errand at full speed; but ere they had gone many steps they perceived a group advancing towards them, composed of the betrothed pair, a party of young girls in attendance on the bride, by whose side walked Dantès’ father; the whole brought up by Fernand, whose lips wore their usual sinister smile.\n\nNeither Mercédès nor Edmond observed the strange expression of his countenance; they were so happy that they were conscious only of the sunshine and the presence of each other.\n\nHaving acquitted themselves of their errand, and exchanged a hearty shake of the hand with Edmond, Danglars and Caderousse took their places beside Fernand and old Dantès,—the latter of whom attracted universal notice.\n\nThe old man was attired in a suit of glistening watered silk, trimmed with steel buttons, beautifully cut and polished. His thin but wiry legs were arrayed in a pair of richly embroidered clocked stockings, evidently of English manufacture, while from his three-cornered hat depended a long streaming knot of white and blue ribbons. Thus he came along, supporting himself on a curiously carved stick, his aged countenance lit up with happiness, looking for all the world like one of the aged dandies of 1796, parading the newly opened gardens of the Luxembourg and Tuileries.\n\nBeside him glided Caderousse, whose desire to partake of the good things provided for the wedding party had induced him to become reconciled to the Dantès, father and son, although there still lingered in his mind a faint and unperfect recollection of the events of the preceding night; just as the brain retains on waking in the morning the dim and misty outline of a dream.\n\n0065m\nAs Danglars approached the disappointed lover, he cast on him a look of deep meaning, while Fernand, as he slowly paced behind the happy pair, who seemed, in their own unmixed content, to have entirely forgotten that such a being as himself existed, was pale and abstracted; occasionally, however, a deep flush would overspread his countenance, and a nervous contraction distort his features, while, with an agitated and restless gaze, he would glance in the direction of Marseilles, like one who either anticipated or foresaw some great and important event.\n\nDantès himself was simply, but becomingly, clad in the dress peculiar to the merchant service—a costume somewhat between a military and a civil garb; and with his fine countenance, radiant with joy and happiness, a more perfect specimen of manly beauty could scarcely be imagined.\n\nLovely as the Greek girls of Cyprus or Chios, Mercédès boasted the same bright flashing eyes of jet, and ripe, round, coral lips. She moved with the light, free step of an Arlesienne or an Andalusian. One more practiced in the arts of great cities would have hid her blushes beneath a veil, or, at least, have cast down her thickly fringed lashes, so as to have concealed the liquid lustre of her animated eyes; but, on the contrary, the delighted girl looked around her with a smile that seemed to say: “If you are my friends, rejoice with me, for I am very happy.”\n\nAs soon as the bridal party came in sight of La Réserve, M. Morrel descended and came forth to meet it, followed by the soldiers and sailors there assembled, to whom he had repeated the promise already given, that Dantès should be the successor to the late Captain Leclere. Edmond, at the approach of his patron, respectfully placed the arm of his affianced bride within that of M. Morrel, who, forthwith conducting her up the flight of wooden steps leading to the chamber in which the feast was prepared, was gayly followed by the guests, beneath whose heavy tread the slight structure creaked and groaned for the space of several minutes.\n\n“Father,” said Mercédès, stopping when she had reached the centre of the table, “sit, I pray you, on my right hand; on my left I will place him who has ever been as a brother to me,” pointing with a soft and gentle smile to Fernand; but her words and look seemed to inflict the direst torture on him, for his lips became ghastly pale, and even beneath the dark hue of his complexion the blood might be seen retreating as though some sudden pang drove it back to the heart.\n\nDuring this time, Dantès, at the opposite side of the table, had been occupied in similarly placing his most honored guests. M. Morrel was seated at his right hand, Danglars at his left; while, at a sign from Edmond, the rest of the company ranged themselves as they found it most agreeable.\n\nThen they began to pass around the dusky, piquant, Arlesian sausages, and lobsters in their dazzling red cuirasses, prawns of large size and brilliant color, the echinus with its prickly outside and dainty morsel within, the clovis, esteemed by the epicures of the South as more than rivalling the exquisite flavor of the oyster, North. All the delicacies, in fact, that are cast up by the wash of waters on the sandy beach, and styled by the grateful fishermen “fruits of the sea.”\n\n“A pretty silence truly!” said the old father of the bridegroom, as he carried to his lips a glass of wine of the hue and brightness of the topaz, and which had just been placed before Mercédès herself. “Now, would anybody think that this room contained a happy, merry party, who desire nothing better than to laugh and dance the hours away?”\n\n“Ah,” sighed Caderousse, “a man cannot always feel happy because he is about to be married.”\n\n“The truth is,” replied Dantès, “that I am too happy for noisy mirth; if that is what you meant by your observation, my worthy friend, you are right; joy takes a strange effect at times, it seems to oppress us almost the same as sorrow.”\n\nDanglars looked towards Fernand, whose excitable nature received and betrayed each fresh impression.\n\n“Why, what ails you?” asked he of Edmond. “Do you fear any approaching evil? I should say that you were the happiest man alive at this instant.”\n\n“And that is the very thing that alarms me,” returned Dantès. “Man does not appear to me to be intended to enjoy felicity so unmixed; happiness is like the enchanted palaces we read of in our childhood, where fierce, fiery dragons defend the entrance and approach; and monsters of all shapes and kinds, requiring to be overcome ere victory is ours. I own that I am lost in wonder to find myself promoted to an honor of which I feel myself unworthy—that of being the husband of Mercédès.”\n\n“Nay, nay!” cried Caderousse, smiling, “you have not attained that honor yet. Mercédès is not yet your wife. Just assume the tone and manner of a husband, and see how she will remind you that your hour is not yet come!”\n\nThe bride blushed, while Fernand, restless and uneasy, seemed to start at every fresh sound, and from time to time wiped away the large drops of perspiration that gathered on his brow.\n\n“Well, never mind that, neighbor Caderousse; it is not worthwhile to contradict me for such a trifle as that. ’Tis true that Mercédès is not actually my wife; but,” added he, drawing out his watch, “in an hour and a half she will be.”\n\nA general exclamation of surprise ran round the table, with the exception of the elder Dantès, whose laugh displayed the still perfect beauty of his large white teeth. Mercédès looked pleased and gratified, while Fernand grasped the handle of his knife with a convulsive clutch.\n\n“In an hour?” inquired Danglars, turning pale. “How is that, my friend?”\n\n“Why, thus it is,” replied Dantès. “Thanks to the influence of M. Morrel, to whom, next to my father, I owe every blessing I enjoy, every difficulty has been removed. We have purchased permission to waive the usual delay; and at half-past two o’clock the Mayor of Marseilles will be waiting for us at the city hall. Now, as a quarter-past one has already struck, I do not consider I have asserted too much in saying, that, in another hour and thirty minutes Mercédès will have become Madame Dantès.”\n0069m\n\nFernand closed his eyes, a burning sensation passed across his brow, and he was compelled to support himself by the table to prevent his falling from his chair; but in spite of all his efforts, he could not refrain from uttering a deep groan, which, however, was lost amid the noisy felicitations of the company.\n\n“Upon my word,” cried the old man, “you make short work of this kind of affair. Arrived here only yesterday morning, and married today at three o’clock! Commend me to a sailor for going the quick way to work!”\n\n“But,” asked Danglars, in a timid tone, “how did you manage about the other formalities—the contract—the settlement?”\n\n“The contract,” answered Dantès, laughingly, “it didn’t take long to fix that. Mercédès has no fortune; I have none to settle on her. So, you see, our papers were quickly written out, and certainly do not come very expensive.” This joke elicited a fresh burst of applause.\n\n“So that what we presumed to be merely the betrothal feast turns out to be the actual wedding dinner!” said Danglars.\n\n“No, no,” answered Dantès; “don’t imagine I am going to put you off in that shabby manner. Tomorrow morning I start for Paris; four days to go, and the same to return, with one day to discharge the commission entrusted to me, is all the time I shall be absent. I shall be back here by the first of March, and on the second I give my real marriage feast.”\n\nThis prospect of fresh festivity redoubled the hilarity of the guests to such a degree, that the elder Dantès, who, at the commencement of the repast, had commented upon the silence that prevailed, now found it difficult, amid the general din of voices, to obtain a moment’s tranquillity in which to drink to the health and prosperity of the bride and bridegroom.\n\nDantès, perceiving the affectionate eagerness of his father, responded by a look of grateful pleasure; while Mercédès glanced at the clock and made an expressive gesture to Edmond.\n\nAround the table reigned that noisy hilarity which usually prevails at such a time among people sufficiently free from the demands of social position not to feel the trammels of etiquette. Such as at the commencement of the repast had not been able to seat themselves according to their inclination rose unceremoniously, and sought out more agreeable companions. Everybody talked at once, without waiting for a reply and each one seemed to be contented with expressing his or her own thoughts.\n\nFernand’s paleness appeared to have communicated itself to Danglars. As for Fernand himself, he seemed to be enduring the tortures of the damned; unable to rest, he was among the first to quit the table, and, as though seeking to avoid the hilarious mirth that rose in such deafening sounds, he continued, in utter silence, to pace the farther end of the salon.\n\nCaderousse approached him just as Danglars, whom Fernand seemed most anxious to avoid, had joined him in a corner of the room.\n\n“Upon my word,” said Caderousse, from whose mind the friendly treatment of Dantès, united with the effect of the excellent wine he had partaken of, had effaced every feeling of envy or jealousy at Dantès’ good fortune,—“upon my word, Dantès is a downright good fellow, and when I see him sitting there beside his pretty wife that is so soon to be. I cannot help thinking it would have been a great pity to have served him that trick you were planning yesterday.”\n\n“Oh, there was no harm meant,” answered Danglars; “at first I certainly did feel somewhat uneasy as to what Fernand might be tempted to do; but when I saw how completely he had mastered his feelings, even so far as to become one of his rival’s attendants, I knew there was no further cause for apprehension.” Caderousse looked full at Fernand—he was ghastly pale.\n\n“Certainly,” continued Danglars, “the sacrifice was no trifling one, when the beauty of the bride is concerned. Upon my soul, that future captain of mine is a lucky dog! Gad! I only wish he would let me take his place.”\n\n“Shall we not set forth?” asked the sweet, silvery voice of Mercédès; “two o’clock has just struck, and you know we are expected in a quarter of an hour.”\n0071m\n\n“To be sure!—to be sure!” cried Dantès, eagerly quitting the table; “let us go directly!”\n\nHis words were re-echoed by the whole party, with vociferous cheers.\n\nAt this moment Danglars, who had been incessantly observing every change in Fernand’s look and manner, saw him stagger and fall back, with an almost convulsive spasm, against a seat placed near one of the open windows. At the same instant his ear caught a sort of indistinct sound on the stairs, followed by the measured tread of soldiery, with the clanking of swords and military accoutrements; then came a hum and buzz as of many voices, so as to deaden even the noisy mirth of the bridal party, among whom a vague feeling of curiosity and apprehension quelled every disposition to talk, and almost instantaneously the most deathlike stillness prevailed.\n\nThe sounds drew nearer. Three blows were struck upon the panel of the door. The company looked at each other in consternation.\n\n“I demand admittance,” said a loud voice outside the room, “in the name of the law!” As no attempt was made to prevent it, the door was opened, and a magistrate, wearing his official scarf, presented himself, followed by four soldiers and a corporal. Uneasiness now yielded to the most extreme dread on the part of those present.\n\n“May I venture to inquire the reason of this unexpected visit?” said M. Morrel, addressing the magistrate, whom he evidently knew; “there is doubtless some mistake easily explained.”\n\n“If it be so,” replied the magistrate, “rely upon every reparation being made; meanwhile, I am the bearer of an order of arrest, and although I most reluctantly perform the task assigned me, it must, nevertheless, be fulfilled. Who among the persons here assembled answers to the name of Edmond Dantès?”\n\nEvery eye was turned towards the young man who, spite of the agitation he could not but feel, advanced with dignity, and said, in a firm voice:\n\n“I am he; what is your pleasure with me?”\n\n“Edmond Dantès,” replied the magistrate, “I arrest you in the name of the law!”\n\n“Me!” repeated Edmond, slightly changing color, “and wherefore, I pray?”\n\n“I cannot inform you, but you will be duly acquainted with the reasons that have rendered such a step necessary at the preliminary examination.”\n\nM. Morrel felt that further resistance or remonstrance was useless. He saw before him an officer delegated to enforce the law, and perfectly well knew that it would be as unavailing to seek pity from a magistrate decked with his official scarf, as to address a petition to some cold marble effigy. Old Dantès, however, sprang forward. There are situations which the heart of a father or a mother cannot be made to understand. He prayed and supplicated in terms so moving, that even the officer was touched, and, although firm in his duty, he kindly said, “My worthy friend, let me beg of you to calm your apprehensions. Your son has probably neglected some prescribed form or attention in registering his cargo, and it is more than probable he will be set at liberty directly he has given the information required, whether touching the health of his crew, or the value of his freight.”\n\n“What is the meaning of all this?” inquired Caderousse, frowningly, of Danglars, who had assumed an air of utter surprise.\n\n0073m\n“How can I tell you?” replied he; “I am, like yourself, utterly bewildered at all that is going on, and cannot in the least make out what it is about.” Caderousse then looked around for Fernand, but he had disappeared.\n\nThe scene of the previous night now came back to his mind with startling clearness. The painful catastrophe he had just witnessed appeared effectually to have rent away the veil which the intoxication of the evening before had raised between himself and his memory.\n\n“So, so,” said he, in a hoarse and choking voice, to Danglars, “this, then, I suppose, is a part of the trick you were concerting yesterday? All I can say is, that if it be so, ’tis an ill turn, and well deserves to bring double evil on those who have projected it.”\n\n“Nonsense,” returned Danglars, “I tell you again I have nothing whatever to do with it; besides, you know very well that I tore the paper to pieces.”\n\n“No, you did not!” answered Caderousse, “you merely threw it by—I saw it lying in a corner.”\n\n“Hold your tongue, you fool!—what should you know about it?—why, you were drunk!”\n\n“Where is Fernand?” inquired Caderousse.\n\n“How do I know?” replied Danglars; “gone, as every prudent man ought to be, to look after his own affairs, most likely. Never mind where he is, let you and I go and see what is to be done for our poor friends.”\n\nDuring this conversation, Dantès, after having exchanged a cheerful shake of the hand with all his sympathizing friends, had surrendered himself to the officer sent to arrest him, merely saying, “Make yourselves quite easy, my good fellows, there is some little mistake to clear up, that’s all, depend upon it; and very likely I may not have to go so far as the prison to effect that.”\n0075m\n\n“Oh, to be sure!” responded Danglars, who had now approached the group, “nothing more than a mistake, I feel quite certain.”\n\nDantès descended the staircase, preceded by the magistrate, and followed by the soldiers. A carriage awaited him at the door; he got in, followed by two soldiers and the magistrate, and the vehicle drove off towards Marseilles.\n\n“Adieu, adieu, dearest Edmond!” cried Mercédès, stretching out her arms to him from the balcony.\n\nThe prisoner heard the cry, which sounded like the sob of a broken heart, and leaning from the coach he called out, “Good-bye, Mercédès—we shall soon meet again!” Then the vehicle disappeared round one of the turnings of Fort Saint Nicholas.\n\n“Wait for me here, all of you!” cried M. Morrel; “I will take the first conveyance I find, and hurry to Marseilles, whence I will bring you word how all is going on.”\n\n“That’s right!” exclaimed a multitude of voices, “go, and return as quickly as you can!”\n\nThis second departure was followed by a long and fearful state of terrified silence on the part of those who were left behind. The old father and Mercédès remained for some time apart, each absorbed in grief; but at length the two poor victims of the same blow raised their eyes, and with a simultaneous burst of feeling rushed into each other’s arms.\n\nMeanwhile Fernand made his appearance, poured out for himself a glass of water with a trembling hand; then hastily swallowing it, went to sit down at the first vacant place, and this was, by mere chance, placed next to the seat on which poor Mercédès had fallen half fainting, when released from the warm and affectionate embrace of old Dantès. Instinctively Fernand drew back his chair.\n\n“He is the cause of all this misery—I am quite sure of it,” whispered Caderousse, who had never taken his eyes off Fernand, to Danglars.\n\n“I don’t think so,” answered the other; “he’s too stupid to imagine such a scheme. I only hope the mischief will fall upon the head of whoever wrought it.”\n\n“You don’t mention those who aided and abetted the deed,” said Caderousse.\n\n“Surely,” answered Danglars, “one cannot be held responsible for every chance arrow shot into the air.”\n\n“You can, indeed, when the arrow lights point downward on somebody’s head.”\n\nMeantime the subject of the arrest was being canvassed in every different form.\n\n“What think you, Danglars,” said one of the party, turning towards him, “of this event?”\n\n“Why,” replied he, “I think it just possible Dantès may have been detected with some trifling article on board ship considered here as contraband.”\n\n“But how could he have done so without your knowledge, Danglars, since you are the ship’s supercargo?”\n\n“Why, as for that, I could only know what I was told respecting the merchandise with which the vessel was laden. I know she was loaded with cotton, and that she took in her freight at Alexandria from Pastret’s warehouse, and at Smyrna from Pascal’s; that is all I was obliged to know, and I beg I may not be asked for any further particulars.”\n\n“Now I recollect,” said the afflicted old father; “my poor boy told me yesterday he had got a small case of coffee, and another of tobacco for me!”\n\n“There, you see,” exclaimed Danglars. “Now the mischief is out; depend upon it the custom-house people went rummaging about the ship in our absence, and discovered poor Dantès’ hidden treasures.”\n\nMercédès, however, paid no heed to this explanation of her lover’s arrest. Her grief, which she had hitherto tried to restrain, now burst out in a violent fit of hysterical sobbing.\n\n“Come, come,” said the old man, “be comforted, my poor child; there is still hope!”\n\n“Hope!” repeated Danglars.\n\n“Hope!” faintly murmured Fernand, but the word seemed to die away on his pale agitated lips, and a convulsive spasm passed over his countenance.\n\n“Good news! good news!” shouted forth one of the party stationed in the balcony on the lookout. “Here comes M. Morrel back. No doubt, now, we shall hear that our friend is released!”\n\nMercédès and the old man rushed to meet the shipowner and greeted him at the door. He was very pale.\n\n“What news?” exclaimed a general burst of voices.\n\n“Alas, my friends,” replied M. Morrel, with a mournful shake of his head, “the thing has assumed a more serious aspect than I expected.”\n\n“Oh, indeed—indeed, sir, he is innocent!” sobbed forth Mercédès.\n\n“That I believe!” answered M. Morrel; “but still he is charged——”\n\n“With what?” inquired the elder Dantès.\n\n“With being an agent of the Bonapartist faction!” Many of our readers may be able to recollect how formidable such an accusation became in the period at which our story is dated.\n\nA despairing cry escaped the pale lips of Mercédès; the old man sank into a chair.\n\n“Ah, Danglars!” whispered Caderousse, “you have deceived me—the trick you spoke of last night has been played; but I cannot suffer a poor old man or an innocent girl to die of grief through your fault. I am determined to tell them all about it.”\n\n“Be silent, you simpleton!” cried Danglars, grasping him by the arm, “or I will not answer even for your own safety. Who can tell whether Dantès be innocent or guilty? The vessel did touch at Elba, where he quitted it, and passed a whole day in the island. Now, should any letters or other documents of a compromising character be found upon him, will it not be taken for granted that all who uphold him are his accomplices?”\n\nWith the rapid instinct of selfishness, Caderousse readily perceived the solidity of this mode of reasoning; he gazed, doubtfully, wistfully, on Danglars, and then caution supplanted generosity.\n\n“Suppose we wait a while, and see what comes of it,” said he, casting a bewildered look on his companion.\n\n“To be sure!” answered Danglars. “Let us wait, by all means. If he be innocent, of course he will be set at liberty; if guilty, why, it is no use involving ourselves in a conspiracy.”\n\n“Let us go, then. I cannot stay here any longer.”\n\n“With all my heart!” replied Danglars, pleased to find the other so tractable. “Let us take ourselves out of the way, and leave things for the present to take their course.”\n\nAfter their departure, Fernand, who had now again become the friend and protector of Mercédès, led the girl to her home, while some friends of Dantès conducted his father, nearly lifeless, to the Allées de Meilhan.\n\nThe rumor of Edmond’s arrest as a Bonapartist agent was not slow in circulating throughout the city.\n\n“Could you ever have credited such a thing, my dear Danglars?” asked M. Morrel, as, on his return to the port for the purpose of gleaning fresh tidings of Dantès, from M. de Villefort, the assistant procureur, he overtook his supercargo and Caderousse. “Could you have believed such a thing possible?”\n\n“Why, you know I told you,” replied Danglars, “that I considered the circumstance of his having anchored at the Island of Elba as a very suspicious circumstance.”\n\n“And did you mention these suspicions to any person beside myself?”\n\n0079m\n“Certainly not!” returned Danglars. Then added in a low whisper, “You understand that, on account of your uncle, M. Policar Morrel, who served under the _other_ government, and who does not altogether conceal what he thinks on the subject, you are strongly suspected of regretting the abdication of Napoleon. I should have feared to injure both Edmond and yourself, had I divulged my own apprehensions to a soul. I am too well aware that though a subordinate, like myself, is bound to acquaint the shipowner with everything that occurs, there are many things he ought most carefully to conceal from all else.”\n\n“’Tis well, Danglars—’tis well!” replied M. Morrel. “You are a worthy fellow; and I had already thought of your interests in the event of poor Edmond having become captain of the _Pharaon_.”\n\n“Is it possible you were so kind?”\n\n“Yes, indeed; I had previously inquired of Dantès what was his opinion of you, and if he should have any reluctance to continue you in your post, for somehow I have perceived a sort of coolness between you.”\n\n“And what was his reply?”\n\n“That he certainly did think he had given you offence in an affair which he merely referred to without entering into particulars, but that whoever possessed the good opinion and confidence of the ship’s owners would have his preference also.”\n\n“The hypocrite!” murmured Danglars.\n\n“Poor Dantès!” said Caderousse. “No one can deny his being a noble-hearted young fellow.”\n\n“But meanwhile,” continued M. Morrel, “here is the _Pharaon_ without a captain.”\n\n“Oh,” replied Danglars, “since we cannot leave this port for the next three months, let us hope that ere the expiration of that period Dantès will be set at liberty.”\n\n“No doubt; but in the meantime?”\n\n“I am entirely at your service, M. Morrel,” answered Danglars. “You know that I am as capable of managing a ship as the most experienced captain in the service; and it will be so far advantageous to you to accept my services, that upon Edmond’s release from prison no further change will be requisite on board the _Pharaon_ than for Dantès and myself each to resume our respective posts.”\n\n“Thanks, Danglars—that will smooth over all difficulties. I fully authorize you at once to assume the command of the _Pharaon_, and look carefully to the unloading of her freight. Private misfortunes must never be allowed to interfere with business.”\n\n“Be easy on that score, M. Morrel; but do you think we shall be permitted to see our poor Edmond?”\n\n“I will let you know that directly I have seen M. de Villefort, whom I shall endeavor to interest in Edmond’s favor. I am aware he is a furious royalist; but, in spite of that, and of his being king’s attorney, he is a man like ourselves, and I fancy not a bad sort of one.”\n\n“Perhaps not,” replied Danglars; “but I hear that he is ambitious, and that’s rather against him.”\n\n“Well, well,” returned M. Morrel, “we shall see. But now hasten on board, I will join you there ere long.”\n\nSo saying, the worthy shipowner quitted the two allies, and proceeded in the direction of the Palais de Justice.\n0081m\n\n“You see,” said Danglars, addressing Caderousse, “the turn things have taken. Do you still feel any desire to stand up in his defence?”\n\n“Not the slightest, but yet it seems to me a shocking thing that a mere joke should lead to such consequences.”\n\n“But who perpetrated that joke, let me ask? neither you nor myself, but Fernand; you knew very well that I threw the paper into a corner of the room—indeed, I fancied I had destroyed it.”\n\n“Oh, no,” replied Caderousse, “that I can answer for, you did not. I only wish I could see it now as plainly as I saw it lying all crushed and crumpled in a corner of the arbor.”\n\n“Well, then, if you did, depend upon it, Fernand picked it up, and either copied it or caused it to be copied; perhaps, even, he did not take the trouble of recopying it. And now I think of it, by Heavens, he may have sent the letter itself! Fortunately, for me, the handwriting was disguised.”\n\n“Then you were aware of Dantès being engaged in a conspiracy?”\n\n“Not I. As I before said, I thought the whole thing was a joke, nothing more. It seems, however, that I have unconsciously stumbled upon the truth.”\n\n“Still,” argued Caderousse, “I would give a great deal if nothing of the kind had happened; or, at least, that I had had no hand in it. You will see, Danglars, that it will turn out an unlucky job for both of us.”\n\n“Nonsense! If any harm come of it, it should fall on the guilty person; and that, you know, is Fernand. How can we be implicated in any way? All we have got to do is, to keep our own counsel, and remain perfectly quiet, not breathing a word to any living soul; and you will see that the storm will pass away without in the least affecting us.”\n\n“Amen!” responded Caderousse, waving his hand in token of adieu to Danglars, and bending his steps towards the Allées de Meilhan, moving his head to and fro, and muttering as he went, after the manner of one whose mind was overcharged with one absorbing idea.\n\n“So far, then,” said Danglars, mentally, “all has gone as I would have it. I am, temporarily, commander of the _Pharaon_, with the certainty of being permanently so, if that fool of a Caderousse can be persuaded to hold his tongue. My only fear is the chance of Dantès being released. But, there, he is in the hands of Justice; and,” added he with a smile, “she will take her own.” So saying, he leaped into a boat, desiring to be rowed on board the _Pharaon_, where M. Morrel had agreed to meet him.\n\n# Chapter 6. The Deputy Procureur du Roi\n\nIn one of the aristocratic mansions built by Puget in the Rue du Grand Cours opposite the Medusa fountain, a second marriage feast was being celebrated, almost at the same hour with the nuptial repast given by Dantès. In this case, however, although the occasion of the entertainment was similar, the company was strikingly dissimilar. Instead of a rude mixture of sailors, soldiers, and those belonging to the humblest grade of life, the present assembly was composed of the very flower of Marseilles society,—magistrates who had resigned their office during the usurper’s reign; officers who had deserted from the imperial army and joined forces with Condé; and younger members of families, brought up to hate and execrate the man whom five years of exile would convert into a martyr, and fifteen of restoration elevate to the rank of a god.\n\nThe guests were still at table, and the heated and energetic conversation that prevailed betrayed the violent and vindictive passions that then agitated each dweller of the South, where unhappily, for five centuries religious strife had long given increased bitterness to the violence of party feeling.\n\nThe emperor, now king of the petty Island of Elba, after having held sovereign sway over one-half of the world, counting as his subjects a small population of five or six thousand souls,—after having been accustomed to hear the “_Vive Napoléons_” of a hundred and twenty millions of human beings, uttered in ten different languages,—was looked upon here as a ruined man, separated forever from any fresh connection with France or claim to her throne.\n\nThe magistrates freely discussed their political views; the military part of the company talked unreservedly of Moscow and Leipsic, while the women commented on the divorce of Josephine. It was not over the downfall of the man, but over the defeat of the Napoleonic idea, that they rejoiced, and in this they foresaw for themselves the bright and cheering prospect of a revivified political existence.\n\nAn old man, decorated with the cross of Saint Louis, now rose and proposed the health of King Louis XVIII. It was the Marquis de Saint-Méran. This toast, recalling at once the patient exile of Hartwell and the peace-loving King of France, excited universal enthusiasm; glasses were elevated in the air _à l’Anglaise_, and the ladies, snatching their bouquets from their fair bosoms, strewed the table with their floral treasures. In a word, an almost poetical fervor prevailed.\n\n“Ah,” said the Marquise de Saint-Méran, a woman with a stern, forbidding eye, though still noble and distinguished in appearance, despite her fifty years—“ah, these revolutionists, who have driven us from those very possessions they afterwards purchased for a mere trifle during the Reign of Terror, would be compelled to own, were they here, that all true devotion was on our side, since we were content to follow the fortunes of a falling monarch, while they, on the contrary, made their fortune by worshipping the rising sun; yes, yes, they could not help admitting that the king, for whom we sacrificed rank, wealth, and station was truly our ‘Louis the well-beloved,’ while their wretched usurper has been, and ever will be, to them their evil genius, their ‘Napoleon the accursed.’ Am I not right, Villefort?”\n\n“I beg your pardon, madame. I really must pray you to excuse me, but—in truth—I was not attending to the conversation.”\n\n“Marquise, marquise!” interposed the old nobleman who had proposed the toast, “let the young people alone; let me tell you, on one’s wedding day there are more agreeable subjects of conversation than dry politics.”\n\n“Never mind, dearest mother,” said a young and lovely girl, with a profusion of light brown hair, and eyes that seemed to float in liquid crystal, “’tis all my fault for seizing upon M. de Villefort, so as to prevent his listening to what you said. But there—now take him—he is your own for as long as you like. M. Villefort, I beg to remind you my mother speaks to you.”\n\n“If the marquise will deign to repeat the words I but imperfectly caught, I shall be delighted to answer,” said M. de Villefort.\n\n“Never mind, Renée,” replied the marquise, with a look of tenderness that seemed out of keeping with her harsh dry features; but, however all other feelings may be withered in a woman’s nature, there is always one bright smiling spot in the desert of her heart, and that is the shrine of maternal love. “I forgive you. What I was saying, Villefort, was, that the Bonapartists had not our sincerity, enthusiasm, or devotion.”\n\n“They had, however, what supplied the place of those fine qualities,” replied the young man, “and that was fanaticism. Napoleon is the Mahomet of the West, and is worshipped by his commonplace but ambitious followers, not only as a leader and lawgiver, but also as the personification of equality.”\n\n“He!” cried the marquise: “Napoleon the type of equality! For mercy’s sake, then, what would you call Robespierre? Come, come, do not strip the latter of his just rights to bestow them on the Corsican, who, to my mind, has usurped quite enough.”\n0085m\n\n“Nay, madame; I would place each of these heroes on his right pedestal—that of Robespierre on his scaffold in the Place Louis Quinze; that of Napoleon on the column of the Place Vendôme. The only difference consists in the opposite character of the equality advocated by these two men; one is the equality that elevates, the other is the equality that degrades; one brings a king within reach of the guillotine, the other elevates the people to a level with the throne. Observe,” said Villefort, smiling, “I do not mean to deny that both these men were revolutionary scoundrels, and that the 9th Thermidor and the 4th of April, in the year 1814, were lucky days for France, worthy of being gratefully remembered by every friend to monarchy and civil order; and that explains how it comes to pass that, fallen, as I trust he is forever, Napoleon has still retained a train of parasitical satellites. Still, marquise, it has been so with other usurpers—Cromwell, for instance, who was not half so bad as Napoleon, had his partisans and advocates.”\n\n“Do you know, Villefort, that you are talking in a most dreadfully revolutionary strain? But I excuse it, it is impossible to expect the son of a Girondin to be free from a small spice of the old leaven.” A deep crimson suffused the countenance of Villefort.\n\n“’Tis true, madame,” answered he, “that my father was a Girondin, but he was not among the number of those who voted for the king’s death; he was an equal sufferer with yourself during the Reign of Terror, and had well-nigh lost his head on the same scaffold on which your father perished.”\n\n“True,” replied the marquise, without wincing in the slightest degree at the tragic remembrance thus called up; “but bear in mind, if you please, that our respective parents underwent persecution and proscription from diametrically opposite principles; in proof of which I may remark, that while my family remained among the staunchest adherents of the exiled princes, your father lost no time in joining the new government; and that while the Citizen Noirtier was a Girondin, the Count Noirtier became a senator.”\n\n“Dear mother,” interposed Renée, “you know very well it was agreed that all these disagreeable reminiscences should forever be laid aside.”\n\n“Suffer me, also, madame,” replied Villefort, “to add my earnest request to Mademoiselle de Saint-Méran’s, that you will kindly allow the veil of oblivion to cover and conceal the past. What avails recrimination over matters wholly past recall? For my own part, I have laid aside even the name of my father, and altogether disown his political principles. He was—nay, probably may still be—a Bonapartist, and is called Noirtier; I, on the contrary, am a staunch royalist, and style myself de Villefort. Let what may remain of revolutionary sap exhaust itself and die away with the old trunk, and condescend only to regard the young shoot which has started up at a distance from the parent tree, without having the power, any more than the wish, to separate entirely from the stock from which it sprung.”\n\n“Bravo, Villefort!” cried the marquis; “excellently well said! Come, now, I have hopes of obtaining what I have been for years endeavoring to persuade the marquise to promise; namely, a perfect amnesty and forgetfulness of the past.”\n\n“With all my heart,” replied the marquise; “let the past be forever forgotten. I promise you it affords _me_ as little pleasure to revive it as it does you. All I ask is, that Villefort will be firm and inflexible for the future in his political principles. Remember, also, Villefort, that we have pledged ourselves to his majesty for your fealty and strict loyalty, and that at our recommendation the king consented to forget the past, as I do” (and here she extended to him her hand)—“as I now do at your entreaty. But bear in mind, that should there fall in your way anyone guilty of conspiring against the government, you will be so much the more bound to visit the offence with rigorous punishment, as it is known you belong to a suspected family.”\n\n“Alas, madame,” returned Villefort, “my profession, as well as the times in which we live, compels me to be severe. I have already successfully conducted several public prosecutions, and brought the offenders to merited punishment. But we have not done with the thing yet.”\n0087m\n\n“Do you, indeed, think so?” inquired the marquise.\n\n“I am, at least, fearful of it. Napoleon, in the Island of Elba, is too near France, and his proximity keeps up the hopes of his partisans. Marseilles is filled with half-pay officers, who are daily, under one frivolous pretext or other, getting up quarrels with the royalists; from hence arise continual and fatal duels among the higher classes of persons, and assassinations in the lower.”\n\n“You have heard, perhaps,” said the Comte de Salvieux, one of M. de Saint-Méran’s oldest friends, and chamberlain to the Comte d’Artois, “that the Holy Alliance purpose removing him from thence?”\n\n“Yes; they were talking about it when we left Paris,” said M. de Saint-Méran; “and where is it decided to transfer him?”\n\n“To Saint Helena.”\n\n“For heaven’s sake, where is that?” asked the marquise.\n\n“An island situated on the other side of the equator, at least two thousand leagues from here,” replied the count.\n\n“So much the better. As Villefort observes, it is a great act of folly to have left such a man between Corsica, where he was born, and Naples, of which his brother-in-law is king, and face to face with Italy, the sovereignty of which he coveted for his son.”\n\n“Unfortunately,” said Villefort, “there are the treaties of 1814, and we cannot molest Napoleon without breaking those compacts.”\n\n“Oh, well, we shall find some way out of it,” responded M. de Salvieux. “There wasn’t any trouble over treaties when it was a question of shooting the poor Duc d’Enghien.”\n\n“Well,” said the marquise, “it seems probable that, by the aid of the Holy Alliance, we shall be rid of Napoleon; and we must trust to the vigilance of M. de Villefort to purify Marseilles of his partisans. The king is either a king or no king; if he be acknowledged as sovereign of France, he should be upheld in peace and tranquillity; and this can best be effected by employing the most inflexible agents to put down every attempt at conspiracy—’tis the best and surest means of preventing mischief.”\n\n“Unfortunately, madame,” answered Villefort, “the strong arm of the law is not called upon to interfere until the evil has taken place.”\n\n“Then all he has got to do is to endeavor to repair it.”\n\n“Nay, madame, the law is frequently powerless to effect this; all it can do is to avenge the wrong done.”\n\n“Oh, M. de Villefort,” cried a beautiful young creature, daughter to the Comte de Salvieux, and the cherished friend of Mademoiselle de Saint-Méran, “do try and get up some famous trial while we are at Marseilles. I never was in a law-court; I am told it is so very amusing!”\n\n“Amusing, certainly,” replied the young man, “inasmuch as, instead of shedding tears as at the fictitious tale of woe produced at a theatre, you behold in a law-court a case of real and genuine distress—a drama of life. The prisoner whom you there see pale, agitated, and alarmed, instead of—as is the case when a curtain falls on a tragedy—going home to sup peacefully with his family, and then retiring to rest, that he may recommence his mimic woes on the morrow,—is removed from your sight merely to be reconducted to his prison and delivered up to the executioner. I leave you to judge how far your nerves are calculated to bear you through such a scene. Of this, however, be assured, that should any favorable opportunity present itself, I will not fail to offer you the choice of being present.”\n\n“For shame, M. de Villefort!” said Renée, becoming quite pale; “don’t you see how you are frightening us?—and yet you laugh.”\n\n“What would you have? ’Tis like a duel. I have already recorded sentence of death, five or six times, against the movers of political conspiracies, and who can say how many daggers may be ready sharpened, and only waiting a favorable opportunity to be buried in my heart?”\n\n“Gracious heavens, M. de Villefort,” said Renée, becoming more and more terrified; “you surely are not in earnest.”\n\n“Indeed I am,” replied the young magistrate with a smile; “and in the interesting trial that young lady is anxious to witness, the case would only be still more aggravated. Suppose, for instance, the prisoner, as is more than probable, to have served under Napoleon—well, can you expect for an instant, that one accustomed, at the word of his commander, to rush fearlessly on the very bayonets of his foe, will scruple more to drive a stiletto into the heart of one he knows to be his personal enemy, than to slaughter his fellow-creatures, merely because bidden to do so by one he is bound to obey? Besides, one requires the excitement of being hateful in the eyes of the accused, in order to lash one’s self into a state of sufficient vehemence and power. I would not choose to see the man against whom I pleaded smile, as though in mockery of my words. No; my pride is to see the accused pale, agitated, and as though beaten out of all composure by the fire of my eloquence.” Renée uttered a smothered exclamation.\n\n“Bravo!” cried one of the guests; “that is what I call talking to some purpose.”\n\n“Just the person we require at a time like the present,” said a second.\n\n“What a splendid business that last case of yours was, my dear Villefort!” remarked a third; “I mean the trial of the man for murdering his father. Upon my word, you killed him ere the executioner had laid his hand upon him.”\n\n“Oh, as for parricides, and such dreadful people as that,” interposed Renée, “it matters very little what is done to them; but as regards poor unfortunate creatures whose only crime consists in having mixed themselves up in political intrigues——”\n\n“Why, that is the very worst offence they could possibly commit; for, don’t you see, Renée, the king is the father of his people, and he who shall plot or contrive aught against the life and safety of the parent of thirty-two millions of souls, is a parricide upon a fearfully great scale?”\n\n“I don’t know anything about that,” replied Renée; “but, M. de Villefort, you have promised me—have you not?—always to show mercy to those I plead for.”\n\n“Make yourself quite easy on that point,” answered Villefort, with one of his sweetest smiles; “you and I will always consult upon our verdicts.”\n\n“My love,” said the marquise, “attend to your doves, your lap-dogs, and embroidery, but do not meddle with what you do not understand. Nowadays the military profession is in abeyance and the magisterial robe is the badge of honor. There is a wise Latin proverb that is very much in point.”\n\n“_Cedant arma togæ_,” said Villefort with a bow.\n\n“I cannot speak Latin,” responded the marquise.\n\n“Well,” said Renée, “I cannot help regretting you had not chosen some other profession than your own—a physician, for instance. Do you know I always felt a shudder at the idea of even a _destroying_ angel?”\n\n“Dear, good Renée,” whispered Villefort, as he gazed with unutterable tenderness on the lovely speaker.\n\n“Let us hope, my child,” cried the marquis, “that M. de Villefort may prove the moral and political physician of this province; if so, he will have achieved a noble work.”\n\n“And one which will go far to efface the recollection of his father’s conduct,” added the incorrigible marquise.\n\n“Madame,” replied Villefort, with a mournful smile, “I have already had the honor to observe that my father has—at least, I hope so—abjured his past errors, and that he is, at the present moment, a firm and zealous friend to religion and order—a better royalist, possibly, than his son; for he has to atone for past dereliction, while I have no other impulse than warm, decided preference and conviction.” Having made this well-turned speech, Villefort looked carefully around to mark the effect of his oratory, much as he would have done had he been addressing the bench in open court.\n\n“Do you know, my dear Villefort,” cried the Comte de Salvieux, “that is exactly what I myself said the other day at the Tuileries, when questioned by his majesty’s principal chamberlain touching the singularity of an alliance between the son of a Girondin and the daughter of an officer of the Duc de Condé; and I assure you he seemed fully to comprehend that this mode of reconciling political differences was based upon sound and excellent principles. Then the king, who, without our suspecting it, had overheard our conversation, interrupted us by saying, ‘Villefort’—observe that the king did not pronounce the word Noirtier, but, on the contrary, placed considerable emphasis on that of Villefort—‘Villefort,’ said his majesty, ‘is a young man of great judgment and discretion, who will be sure to make a figure in his profession; I like him much, and it gave me great pleasure to hear that he was about to become the son-in-law of the Marquis and Marquise de Saint-Méran. I should myself have recommended the match, had not the noble marquis anticipated my wishes by requesting my consent to it.’”\n\n“Is it possible the king could have condescended so far as to express himself so favorably of me?” asked the enraptured Villefort.\n\n“I give you his very words; and if the marquis chooses to be candid, he will confess that they perfectly agree with what his majesty said to him, when he went six months ago to consult him upon the subject of your espousing his daughter.”\n0091m\n\n“That is true,” answered the marquis.\n\n“How much do I owe this gracious prince! What is there I would not do to evince my earnest gratitude!”\n\n“That is right,” cried the marquise. “I love to see you thus. Now, then, were a conspirator to fall into your hands, he would be most welcome.”\n\n“For my part, dear mother,” interposed Renée, “I trust your wishes will not prosper, and that Providence will only permit petty offenders, poor debtors, and miserable cheats to fall into M. de Villefort’s hands,—then I shall be contented.”\n\n“Just the same as though you prayed that a physician might only be called upon to prescribe for headaches, measles, and the stings of wasps, or any other slight affection of the epidermis. If you wish to see me the king’s attorney, you must desire for me some of those violent and dangerous diseases from the cure of which so much honor redounds to the physician.”\n\nAt this moment, and as though the utterance of Villefort’s wish had sufficed to effect its accomplishment, a servant entered the room, and whispered a few words in his ear. Villefort immediately rose from table and quitted the room upon the plea of urgent business; he soon, however, returned, his whole face beaming with delight. Renée regarded him with fond affection; and certainly his handsome features, lit up as they then were with more than usual fire and animation, seemed formed to excite the innocent admiration with which she gazed on her graceful and intelligent lover.\n\n“You were wishing just now,” said Villefort, addressing her, “that I were a doctor instead of a lawyer. Well, I at least resemble the disciples of Esculapius in one thing [people spoke in this style in 1815], that of not being able to call a day my own, not even that of my betrothal.”\n\n“And wherefore were you called away just now?” asked Mademoiselle de Saint-Méran, with an air of deep interest.\n\n“For a very serious matter, which bids fair to make work for the executioner.”\n\n“How dreadful!” exclaimed Renée, turning pale.\n\n“Is it possible?” burst simultaneously from all who were near enough to the magistrate to hear his words.\n\n“Why, if my information prove correct, a sort of Bonapartist conspiracy has just been discovered.”\n\n“Can I believe my ears?” cried the marquise.\n\n“I will read you the letter containing the accusation, at least,” said Villefort:\n\n“‘The king’s attorney is informed by a friend to the throne and the religious institutions of his country, that one named Edmond Dantès, mate of the ship _Pharaon_, this day arrived from Smyrna, after having touched at Naples and Porto-Ferrajo, has been the bearer of a letter from Murat to the usurper, and again taken charge of another letter from the usurper to the Bonapartist club in Paris. Ample corroboration of this statement may be obtained by arresting the above-mentioned Edmond Dantès, who either carries the letter for Paris about with him, or has it at his father’s abode. Should it not be found in the possession of father or son, then it will assuredly be discovered in the cabin belonging to the said Dantès on board the _Pharaon_.’”\n\n“But,” said Renée, “this letter, which, after all, is but an anonymous scrawl, is not even addressed to you, but to the king’s attorney.”\n\n0093m\n“True; but that gentleman being absent, his secretary, by his orders, opened his letters; thinking this one of importance, he sent for me, but not finding me, took upon himself to give the necessary orders for arresting the accused party.”\n\n“Then the guilty person is absolutely in custody?” said the marquise.\n\n“Nay, dear mother, say the accused person. You know we cannot yet pronounce him guilty.”\n\n“He is in safe custody,” answered Villefort; “and rely upon it, if the letter is found, he will not be likely to be trusted abroad again, unless he goes forth under the especial protection of the headsman.”\n\n“And where is the unfortunate being?” asked Renée.\n\n“He is at my house.”\n\n“Come, come, my friend,” interrupted the marquise, “do not neglect your duty to linger with us. You are the king’s servant, and must go wherever that service calls you.”\n\n“Oh, Villefort!” cried Renée, clasping her hands, and looking towards her lover with piteous earnestness, “be merciful on this the day of our betrothal.”\n\nThe young man passed round to the side of the table where the fair pleader sat, and leaning over her chair said tenderly:\n\n“To give you pleasure, my sweet Renée, I promise to show all the lenity in my power; but if the charges brought against this Bonapartist hero prove correct, why, then, you really must give me leave to order his head to be cut off.”\n\nRenée shuddered at the word _cut_, for the growth in question had a head.\n\n“Never mind that foolish girl, Villefort,” said the marquise. “She will soon get over these things.” So saying, Madame de Saint-Méran extended her dry bony hand to Villefort, who, while imprinting a son-in-law’s respectful salute on it, looked at Renée, as much as to say, “I must try and fancy ’tis your dear hand I kiss, as it should have been.”\n\n“These are mournful auspices to accompany a betrothal,” sighed poor Renée.\n\n“Upon my word, child!” exclaimed the angry marquise, “your folly exceeds all bounds. I should be glad to know what connection there can possibly be between your sickly sentimentality and the affairs of the state!”\n\n“Oh, mother!” murmured Renée.\n\n“Nay, madame, I pray you pardon this little traitor. I promise you that to make up for her want of loyalty, I will be most inflexibly severe;” then casting an expressive glance at his betrothed, which seemed to say, “Fear not, for your dear sake my justice shall be tempered with mercy,” and receiving a sweet and approving smile in return, Villefort departed with paradise in his heart.\n\n# Chapter 7. The Examination\n\nNo sooner had Villefort left the salon, than he assumed the grave air of a man who holds the balance of life and death in his hands. Now, in spite of the nobility of his countenance, the command of which, like a finished actor, he had carefully studied before the glass, it was by no means easy for him to assume an air of judicial severity. Except the recollection of the line of politics his father had adopted, and which might interfere, unless he acted with the greatest prudence, with his own career, Gérard de Villefort was as happy as a man could be. Already rich, he held a high official situation, though only twenty-seven. He was about to marry a young and charming woman, whom he loved, not passionately, but reasonably, as became a deputy attorney of the king; and besides her personal attractions, which were very great, Mademoiselle de Saint-Méran’s family possessed considerable political influence, which they would, of course, exert in his favor. The dowry of his wife amounted to fifty thousand crowns, and he had, besides, the prospect of seeing her fortune increased to half a million at her father’s death. These considerations naturally gave Villefort a feeling of such complete felicity that his mind was fairly dazzled in its contemplation.\n\nAt the door he met the commissary of police, who was waiting for him. The sight of this officer recalled Villefort from the third heaven to earth; he composed his face, as we have before described, and said, “I have read the letter, sir, and you have acted rightly in arresting this man; now inform me what you have discovered concerning him and the conspiracy.”\n\n“We know nothing as yet of the conspiracy, monsieur; all the papers found have been sealed up and placed on your desk. The prisoner himself is named Edmond Dantès, mate on board the three-master the _Pharaon_, trading in cotton with Alexandria and Smyrna, and belonging to Morrel & Son, of Marseilles.”\n\n“Before he entered the merchant service, had he ever served in the marines?”\n\n“Oh, no, monsieur, he is very young.”\n\n“How old?”\n\n“Nineteen or twenty at the most.”\n\nAt this moment, and as Villefort had arrived at the corner of the Rue des Conseils, a man, who seemed to have been waiting for him, approached; it was M. Morrel.\n\n“Ah, M. de Villefort,” cried he, “I am delighted to see you. Some of your people have committed the strangest mistake—they have just arrested Edmond Dantès, mate of my vessel.”\n\n“I know it, monsieur,” replied Villefort, “and I am now going to examine him.”\n\n“Oh,” said Morrel, carried away by his friendship, “you do not know him, and I do. He is the most estimable, the most trustworthy creature in the world, and I will venture to say, there is not a better seaman in all the merchant service. Oh, M. de Villefort, I beseech your indulgence for him.”\n\nVillefort, as we have seen, belonged to the aristocratic party at Marseilles, Morrel to the plebeian; the first was a royalist, the other suspected of Bonapartism. Villefort looked disdainfully at Morrel, and replied coldly:\n\n“You are aware, monsieur, that a man may be estimable and trustworthy in private life, and the best seaman in the merchant service, and yet be, politically speaking, a great criminal. Is it not true?”\n\nThe magistrate laid emphasis on these words, as if he wished to apply them to the owner himself, while his eyes seemed to plunge into the heart of one who, interceding for another, had himself need of indulgence. Morrel reddened, for his own conscience was not quite clear on politics; besides, what Dantès had told him of his interview with the grand-marshal, and what the emperor had said to him, embarrassed him. He replied, however, in a tone of deep interest:\n\n“I entreat you, M. de Villefort, be, as you always are, kind and equitable, and give him back to us soon.” This _give us_ sounded revolutionary in the deputy’s ears.\n\n“Ah, ah,” murmured he, “is Dantès then a member of some Carbonari society, that his protector thus employs the collective form? He was, if I recollect, arrested in a tavern, in company with a great many others.” Then he added, “Monsieur, you may rest assured I shall perform my duty impartially, and that if he be innocent you shall not have appealed to me in vain; should he, however, be guilty, in this present epoch, impunity would furnish a dangerous example, and I must do my duty.”\n\n0097m\nAs he had now arrived at the door of his own house, which adjoined the Palais de Justice, he entered, after having, coldly saluted the shipowner, who stood, as if petrified, on the spot where Villefort had left him. The antechamber was full of police agents and gendarmes, in the midst of whom, carefully watched, but calm and smiling, stood the prisoner. Villefort traversed the antechamber, cast a side glance at Dantès, and taking a packet which a gendarme offered him, disappeared, saying, “Bring in the prisoner.”\n\nRapid as had been Villefort’s glance, it had served to give him an idea of the man he was about to interrogate. He had recognized intelligence in the high forehead, courage in the dark eye and bent brow, and frankness in the thick lips that showed a set of pearly teeth. Villefort’s first impression was favorable; but he had been so often warned to mistrust first impulses, that he applied the maxim to the impression, forgetting the difference between the two words. He stifled, therefore, the feelings of compassion that were rising, composed his features, and sat down, grim and sombre, at his desk. An instant after Dantès entered. He was pale, but calm and collected, and saluting his judge with easy politeness, looked round for a seat, as if he had been in M. Morrel’s salon. It was then that he encountered for the first time Villefort’s look,—that look peculiar to the magistrate, who, while seeming to read the thoughts of others, betrays nothing of his own.\n\n“Who and what are you?” demanded Villefort, turning over a pile of papers, containing information relative to the prisoner, that a police agent had given to him on his entry, and that, already, in an hour’s time, had swelled to voluminous proportions, thanks to the corrupt espionage of which “the accused” is always made the victim.\n\n“My name is Edmond Dantès,” replied the young man calmly; “I am mate of the _Pharaon_, belonging to Messrs. Morrel & Son.”\n\n“Your age?” continued Villefort.\n\n“Nineteen,” returned Dantès.\n\n“What were you doing at the moment you were arrested?”\n\n“I was at the festival of my marriage, monsieur,” said the young man, his voice slightly tremulous, so great was the contrast between that happy moment and the painful ceremony he was now undergoing; so great was the contrast between the sombre aspect of M. de Villefort and the radiant face of Mercédès.\n\n“You were at the festival of your marriage?” said the deputy, shuddering in spite of himself.\n\n“Yes, monsieur; I am on the point of marrying a young girl I have been attached to for three years.” Villefort, impassive as he was, was struck with this coincidence; and the tremulous voice of Dantès, surprised in the midst of his happiness, struck a sympathetic chord in his own bosom—he also was on the point of being married, and he was summoned from his own happiness to destroy that of another. “This philosophic reflection,” thought he, “will make a great sensation at M. de Saint-Méran’s;” and he arranged mentally, while Dantès awaited further questions, the antithesis by which orators often create a reputation for eloquence. When this speech was arranged, Villefort turned to Dantès.\n\n0099m\n“Go on, sir,” said he.\n\n“What would you have me say?”\n\n“Give all the information in your power.”\n\n“Tell me on which point you desire information, and I will tell all I know; only,” added he, with a smile, “I warn you I know very little.”\n\n“Have you served under the usurper?”\n\n“I was about to be mustered into the Royal Marines when he fell.”\n\n“It is reported your political opinions are extreme,” said Villefort, who had never heard anything of the kind, but was not sorry to make this inquiry, as if it were an accusation.\n\n“My political opinions!” replied Dantès. “Alas, sir, I never had any opinions. I am hardly nineteen; I know nothing; I have no part to play. If I obtain the situation I desire, I shall owe it to M. Morrel. Thus all my opinions—I will not say public, but private—are confined to these three sentiments,—I love my father, I respect M. Morrel, and I adore Mercédès. This, sir, is all I can tell you, and you see how uninteresting it is.” As Dantès spoke, Villefort gazed at his ingenuous and open countenance, and recollected the words of Renée, who, without knowing who the culprit was, had besought his indulgence for him. With the deputy’s knowledge of crime and criminals, every word the young man uttered convinced him more and more of his innocence. This lad, for he was scarcely a man,—simple, natural, eloquent with that eloquence of the heart never found when sought for; full of affection for everybody, because he was happy, and because happiness renders even the wicked good—extended his affection even to his judge, spite of Villefort’s severe look and stern accent. Dantès seemed full of kindness.\n\n_“Pardieu!”_ said Villefort, “he is a noble fellow. I hope I shall gain Renée’s favor easily by obeying the first command she ever imposed on me. I shall have at least a pressure of the hand in public, and a sweet kiss in private.” Full of this idea, Villefort’s face became so joyous, that when he turned to Dantès, the latter, who had watched the change on his physiognomy, was smiling also.\n\n“Sir,” said Villefort, “have you any enemies, at least, that you know.”\n\n“I have enemies?” replied Dantès; “my position is not sufficiently elevated for that. As for my disposition, that is, perhaps, somewhat too hasty; but I have striven to repress it. I have had ten or twelve sailors under me, and if you question them, they will tell you that they love and respect me, not as a father, for I am too young, but as an elder brother.”\n\n“But you may have excited jealousy. You are about to become captain at nineteen—an elevated post; you are about to marry a pretty girl, who loves you; and these two pieces of good fortune may have excited the envy of someone.”\n\n“You are right; you know men better than I do, and what you say may possibly be the case, I confess; but if such persons are among my acquaintances I prefer not to know it, because then I should be forced to hate them.”\n\n“You are wrong; you should always strive to see clearly around you. You seem a worthy young man; I will depart from the strict line of my duty to aid you in discovering the author of this accusation. Here is the paper; do you know the writing?” As he spoke, Villefort drew the letter from his pocket, and presented it to Dantès. Dantès read it. A cloud passed over his brow as he said:\n\n“No, monsieur, I do not know the writing, and yet it is tolerably plain. Whoever did it writes well. I am very fortunate,” added he, looking gratefully at Villefort, “to be examined by such a man as you; for this envious person is a real enemy.” And by the rapid glance that the young man’s eyes shot forth, Villefort saw how much energy lay hid beneath this mildness.\n\n“Now,” said the deputy, “answer me frankly, not as a prisoner to a judge, but as one man to another who takes an interest in him, what truth is there in the accusation contained in this anonymous letter?” And Villefort threw disdainfully on his desk the letter Dantès had just given back to him.\n\n“None at all. I will tell you the real facts. I swear by my honor as a sailor, by my love for Mercédès, by the life of my father——”\n\n“Speak, monsieur,” said Villefort. Then, internally, “If Renée could see me, I hope she would be satisfied, and would no longer call me a decapitator.”\n\n“Well, when we quitted Naples, Captain Leclere was attacked with a brain fever. As we had no doctor on board, and he was so anxious to arrive at Elba, that he would not touch at any other port, his disorder rose to such a height, that at the end of the third day, feeling he was dying, he called me to him. ‘My dear Dantès,’ said he, ‘swear to perform what I am going to tell you, for it is a matter of the deepest importance.’\n\n“‘I swear, captain,’ replied I.\n\n“‘Well, as after my death the command devolves on you as mate, assume the command, and bear up for the Island of Elba, disembark at Porto-Ferrajo, ask for the grand-marshal, give him this letter—perhaps they will give you another letter, and charge you with a commission. You will accomplish what I was to have done, and derive all the honor and profit from it.’\n\n“‘I will do it, captain; but perhaps I shall not be admitted to the grand-marshal’s presence as easily as you expect?’\n\n“‘Here is a ring that will obtain audience of him, and remove every difficulty,’ said the captain. At these words he gave me a ring. It was time—two hours after he was delirious; the next day he died.”\n\n“And what did you do then?”\n\n“What I ought to have done, and what everyone would have done in my place. Everywhere the last requests of a dying man are sacred; but with a sailor the last requests of his superior are commands. I sailed for the Island of Elba, where I arrived the next day; I ordered everybody to remain on board, and went on shore alone. As I had expected, I found some difficulty in obtaining access to the grand-marshal; but I sent the ring I had received from the captain to him, and was instantly admitted. He questioned me concerning Captain Leclere’s death; and, as the latter had told me, gave me a letter to carry on to a person in Paris. I undertook it because it was what my captain had bade me do. I landed here, regulated the affairs of the vessel, and hastened to visit my affianced bride, whom I found more lovely than ever. Thanks to M. Morrel, all the forms were got over; in a word I was, as I told you, at my marriage feast; and I should have been married in an hour, and tomorrow I intended to start for Paris, had I not been arrested on this charge which you as well as I now see to be unjust.”\n\n“Ah,” said Villefort, “this seems to me the truth. If you have been culpable, it was imprudence, and this imprudence was in obedience to the orders of your captain. Give up this letter you have brought from Elba, and pass your word you will appear should you be required, and go and rejoin your friends.\n\n“I am free, then, sir?” cried Dantès joyfully.\n\n“Yes; but first give me this letter.”\n\n“You have it already, for it was taken from me with some others which I see in that packet.”\n\n“Stop a moment,” said the deputy, as Dantès took his hat and gloves. “To whom is it addressed?”\n\n_“To Monsieur Noirtier, Rue Coq-Héron, Paris.”_ Had a thunderbolt fallen into the room, Villefort could not have been more stupefied. He sank into his seat, and hastily turning over the packet, drew forth the fatal letter, at which he glanced with an expression of terror.\n\n“M. Noirtier, Rue Coq-Héron, No. 13,” murmured he, growing still paler.\n\n“Yes,” said Dantès; “do you know him?”\n\n“No,” replied Villefort; “a faithful servant of the king does not know conspirators.”\n\n0103m\n“It is a conspiracy, then?” asked Dantès, who after believing himself free, now began to feel a tenfold alarm. “I have, however, already told you, sir, I was entirely ignorant of the contents of the letter.”\n\n“Yes; but you knew the name of the person to whom it was addressed,” said Villefort.\n\n“I was forced to read the address to know to whom to give it.”\n\n“Have you shown this letter to anyone?” asked Villefort, becoming still more pale.\n\n“To no one, on my honor.”\n\n“Everybody is ignorant that you are the bearer of a letter from the Island of Elba, and addressed to M. Noirtier?”\n\n“Everybody, except the person who gave it to me.”\n\n“And that was too much, far too much,” murmured Villefort. Villefort’s brow darkened more and more, his white lips and clenched teeth filled Dantès with apprehension. After reading the letter, Villefort covered his face with his hands.\n\n“Oh,” said Dantès timidly, “what is the matter?” Villefort made no answer, but raised his head at the expiration of a few seconds, and again perused the letter.\n\n“And you say that you are ignorant of the contents of this letter?”\n\n“I give you my word of honor, sir,” said Dantès; “but what is the matter? You are ill—shall I ring for assistance?—shall I call?”\n\n“No,” said Villefort, rising hastily; “stay where you are. It is for me to give orders here, and not you.”\n\n“Monsieur,” replied Dantès proudly, “it was only to summon assistance for you.”\n\n“I want none; it was a temporary indisposition. Attend to yourself; answer me.” Dantès waited, expecting a question, but in vain. Villefort fell back on his chair, passed his hand over his brow, moist with perspiration, and, for the third time, read the letter.\n\n“Oh, if he knows the contents of this!” murmured he, “and that Noirtier is the father of Villefort, I am lost!” And he fixed his eyes upon Edmond as if he would have penetrated his thoughts.\n\n“Oh, it is impossible to doubt it,” cried he, suddenly.\n\n“In heaven’s name!” cried the unhappy young man, “if you doubt me, question me; I will answer you.” Villefort made a violent effort, and in a tone he strove to render firm:\n\n“Sir,” said he, “I am no longer able, as I had hoped, to restore you immediately to liberty; before doing so, I must consult the trial justice; what my own feeling is you already know.”\n\n“Oh, monsieur,” cried Dantès, “you have been rather a friend than a judge.”\n0105m\n\n“Well, I must detain you some time longer, but I will strive to make it as short as possible. The principal charge against you is this letter, and you see——” Villefort approached the fire, cast it in, and waited until it was entirely consumed.\n\n“You see, I destroy it?”\n“Oh,” exclaimed Dantès, “you are goodness itself.”\n\n“Listen,” continued Villefort; “you can now have confidence in me after what I have done.”\n\n“Oh, command, and I will obey.”\n\n“Listen; this is not a command, but advice I give you.”\n\n“Speak, and I will follow your advice.”\n\n“I shall detain you until this evening in the Palais de Justice. Should anyone else interrogate you, say to him what you have said to me, but do not breathe a word of this letter.”\n\n“I promise.” It was Villefort who seemed to entreat, and the prisoner who reassured him.\n\n“You see,” continued he, glancing toward the grate, where fragments of burnt paper fluttered in the flames, “the letter is destroyed; you and I alone know of its existence; should you, therefore, be questioned, deny all knowledge of it—deny it boldly, and you are saved.”\n\n“Be satisfied; I will deny it.”\n\n“It was the only letter you had?”\n\n“It was.”\n“Swear it.”\n\n“I swear it.”\n\nVillefort rang. A police agent entered. Villefort whispered some words in his ear, to which the officer replied by a motion of his head.\n\n“Follow him,” said Villefort to Dantès. Dantès saluted Villefort and retired. Hardly had the door closed when Villefort threw himself half-fainting into a chair.\n\n“Alas, alas,” murmured he, “if the procureur himself had been at Marseilles I should have been ruined. This accursed letter would have destroyed all my hopes. Oh, my father, must your past career always interfere with my successes?” Suddenly a light passed over his face, a smile played round his set mouth, and his haggard eyes were fixed in thought.\n\n“This will do,” said he, “and from this letter, which might have ruined me, I will make my fortune. Now to the work I have in hand.” And after having assured himself that the prisoner was gone, the deputy procureur hastened to the house of his betrothed.\n\n# Chapter 8. The Château d’If\n\n0107m\nThe commissary of police, as he traversed the antechamber, made a sign to two gendarmes, who placed themselves one on Dantès’ right and the other on his left. A door that communicated with the Palais de Justice was opened, and they went through a long range of gloomy corridors, whose appearance might have made even the boldest shudder. The Palais de Justice communicated with the prison,—a sombre edifice, that from its grated windows looks on the clock-tower of the Accoules. After numberless windings, Dantès saw a door with an iron wicket. The commissary took up an iron mallet and knocked thrice, every blow seeming to Dantès as if struck on his heart. The door opened, the two gendarmes gently pushed him forward, and the door closed with a loud sound behind him. The air he inhaled was no longer pure, but thick and mephitic,—he was in prison.\n\nHe was conducted to a tolerably neat chamber, but grated and barred, and its appearance, therefore, did not greatly alarm him; besides, the words of Villefort, who seemed to interest himself so much, resounded still in his ears like a promise of freedom. It was four o’clock when Dantès was placed in this chamber. It was, as we have said, the 1st of March, and the prisoner was soon buried in darkness. The obscurity augmented the acuteness of his hearing; at the slightest sound he rose and hastened to the door, convinced they were about to liberate him, but the sound died away, and Dantès sank again into his seat. At last, about ten o’clock, and just as Dantès began to despair, steps were heard in the corridor, a key turned in the lock, the bolts creaked, the massy oaken door flew open, and a flood of light from two torches pervaded the apartment.\n\nBy the torchlight Dantès saw the glittering sabres and carbines of four gendarmes. He had advanced at first, but stopped at the sight of this display of force.\n\n“Are you come to fetch me?” asked he.\n\n“Yes,” replied a gendarme.\n\n“By the orders of the deputy procureur?”\n\n“I believe so.” The conviction that they came from M. de Villefort relieved all Dantès’ apprehensions; he advanced calmly, and placed himself in the centre of the escort. A carriage waited at the door, the coachman was on the box, and a police officer sat beside him.\n\n“Is this carriage for me?” said Dantès.\n\n“It is for you,” replied a gendarme.\n\nDantès was about to speak; but feeling himself urged forward, and having neither the power nor the intention to resist, he mounted the steps, and was in an instant seated inside between two gendarmes; the two others took their places opposite, and the carriage rolled heavily over the stones.\n\nThe prisoner glanced at the windows—they were grated; he had changed his prison for another that was conveying him he knew not whither. Through the grating, however, Dantès saw they were passing through the Rue Caisserie, and by the Rue Saint-Laurent and the Rue Taramis, to the quay. Soon he saw the lights of La Consigne.\n\nThe carriage stopped, the officer descended, approached the guardhouse, a dozen soldiers came out and formed themselves in order; Dantès saw the reflection of their muskets by the light of the lamps on the quay.\n\n“Can all this force be summoned on my account?” thought he.\n\nThe officer opened the door, which was locked, and, without speaking a word, answered Dantès’ question; for he saw between the ranks of the soldiers a passage formed from the carriage to the port. The two gendarmes who were opposite to him descended first, then he was ordered to alight and the gendarmes on each side of him followed his example. They advanced towards a boat, which a custom-house officer held by a chain, near the quay.\n\nThe soldiers looked at Dantès with an air of stupid curiosity. In an instant he was placed in the stern-sheets of the boat, between the gendarmes, while the officer stationed himself at the bow; a shove sent the boat adrift, and four sturdy oarsmen impelled it rapidly towards the Pilon. At a shout from the boat, the chain that closes the mouth of the port was lowered and in a second they were, as Dantès knew, in the Frioul and outside the inner harbor.\n\nThe prisoner’s first feeling was of joy at again breathing the pure air—for air is freedom; but he soon sighed, for he passed before La Réserve, where he had that morning been so happy, and now through the open windows came the laughter and revelry of a ball. Dantès folded his hands, raised his eyes to heaven, and prayed fervently.\n0111m\n\nThe boat continued her voyage. They had passed the Tête de Mort, were now off the Anse du Pharo, and about to double the battery. This manœuvre was incomprehensible to Dantès.\n\n“Whither are you taking me?” asked he.\n\n“You will soon know.”\n\n“But still——”\n\n“We are forbidden to give you any explanation.” Dantès, trained in discipline, knew that nothing would be more absurd than to question subordinates, who were forbidden to reply; and so he remained silent.\n\nThe most vague and wild thoughts passed through his mind. The boat they were in could not make a long voyage; there was no vessel at anchor outside the harbor; he thought, perhaps, they were going to leave him on some distant point. He was not bound, nor had they made any attempt to handcuff him; this seemed a good augury. Besides, had not the deputy, who had been so kind to him, told him that provided he did not pronounce the dreaded name of Noirtier, he had nothing to apprehend? Had not Villefort in his presence destroyed the fatal letter, the only proof against him?\n\nHe waited silently, striving to pierce through the darkness.\n\nThey had left the Ile Ratonneau, where the lighthouse stood, on the right, and were now opposite the Point des Catalans. It seemed to the prisoner that he could distinguish a feminine form on the beach, for it was there Mercédès dwelt. How was it that a presentiment did not warn Mercédès that her lover was within three hundred yards of her?\n\nOne light alone was visible; and Dantès saw that it came from Mercédès’ chamber. Mercédès was the only one awake in the whole settlement. A loud cry could be heard by her. But pride restrained him and he did not utter it. What would his guards think if they heard him shout like a madman?\n\nHe remained silent, his eyes fixed upon the light; the boat went on, but the prisoner thought only of Mercédès. An intervening elevation of land hid the light. Dantès turned and perceived that they had got out to sea. While he had been absorbed in thought, they had shipped their oars and hoisted sail; the boat was now moving with the wind.\n\nIn spite of his repugnance to address the guards, Dantès turned to the nearest gendarme, and taking his hand,\n\n“Comrade,” said he, “I adjure you, as a Christian and a soldier, to tell me where we are going. I am Captain Dantès, a loyal Frenchman, thought accused of treason; tell me where you are conducting me, and I promise you on my honor I will submit to my fate.”\n\nThe gendarme looked irresolutely at his companion, who returned for answer a sign that said, “I see no great harm in telling him now,” and the gendarme replied:\n\n“You are a native of Marseilles, and a sailor, and yet you do not know where you are going?”\n\n“On my honor, I have no idea.”\n\n“Have you no idea whatever?”\n\n“None at all.”\n\n“That is impossible.”\n\n“I swear to you it is true. Tell me, I entreat.”\n\n“But my orders.”\n\n“Your orders do not forbid your telling me what I must know in ten minutes, in half an hour, or an hour. You see I cannot escape, even if I intended.”\n\n“Unless you are blind, or have never been outside the harbor, you must know.”\n\n“I do not.”\n\n“Look round you then.” Dantès rose and looked forward, when he saw rise within a hundred yards of him the black and frowning rock on which stands the Château d’If. This gloomy fortress, which has for more than three hundred years furnished food for so many wild legends, seemed to Dantès like a scaffold to a malefactor.\n\n“The Château d’If?” cried he, “what are we going there for?”\n\nThe gendarme smiled.\n\n“I am not going there to be imprisoned,” said Dantès; “it is only used for political prisoners. I have committed no crime. Are there any magistrates or judges at the Château d’If?”\n\n“There are only,” said the gendarme, “a governor, a garrison, turnkeys, and good thick walls. Come, come, do not look so astonished, or you will make me think you are laughing at me in return for my good nature.”\n\nDantès pressed the gendarme’s hand as though he would crush it.\n\n“You think, then,” said he, “that I am taken to the Château d’If to be imprisoned there?”\n\n“It is probable; but there is no occasion to squeeze so hard.”\n\n“Without any inquiry, without any formality?”\n\n“All the formalities have been gone through; the inquiry is already made.”\n\n“And so, in spite of M. de Villefort’s promises?”\n\n“I do not know what M. de Villefort promised you,” said the gendarme, “but I know we are taking you to the Château d’If. But what are you doing? Help, comrades, help!”\n\nBy a rapid movement, which the gendarme’s practiced eye had perceived, Dantès sprang forward to precipitate himself into the sea; but four vigorous arms seized him as his feet quitted the bottom of the boat. He fell back cursing with rage.\n\n“Good!” said the gendarme, placing his knee on his chest; “this is the way you keep your word as a sailor! Believe soft-spoken gentlemen again! Hark ye, my friend, I have disobeyed my first order, but I will not disobey the second; and if you move, I will blow your brains out.” And he levelled his carbine at Dantès, who felt the muzzle against his temple.\n\nFor a moment the idea of struggling crossed his mind, and of so ending the unexpected evil that had overtaken him. But he bethought him of M. de Villefort’s promise; and, besides, death in a boat from the hand of a gendarme seemed too terrible. He remained motionless, but gnashing his teeth and wringing his hands with fury.\n\nAt this moment the boat came to a landing with a violent shock. One of the sailors leaped on shore, a cord creaked as it ran through a pulley, and Dantès guessed they were at the end of the voyage, and that they were mooring the boat.\n\nHis guards, taking him by the arms and coat-collar, forced him to rise, and dragged him towards the steps that lead to the gate of the fortress, while the police officer carrying a musket with fixed bayonet followed behind.\n\nDantès made no resistance; he was like a man in a dream; he saw soldiers drawn up on the embankment; he knew vaguely that he was ascending a flight of steps; he was conscious that he passed through a door, and that the door closed behind him; but all this indistinctly as through a mist. He did not even see the ocean, that terrible barrier against freedom, which the prisoners look upon with utter despair.\n\nThey halted for a minute, during which he strove to collect his thoughts. He looked around; he was in a court surrounded by high walls; he heard the measured tread of sentinels, and as they passed before the light he saw the barrels of their muskets shine.\n\nThey waited upwards of ten minutes. Certain Dantès could not escape, the gendarmes released him. They seemed awaiting orders. The orders came.\n\n“Where is the prisoner?” said a voice.\n\n“Here,” replied the gendarmes.\n\n“Let him follow me; I will take him to his cell.”\n\n“Go!” said the gendarmes, thrusting Dantès forward.\n\nThe prisoner followed his guide, who led him into a room almost under ground, whose bare and reeking walls seemed as though impregnated with tears; a lamp placed on a stool illumined the apartment faintly, and showed Dantès the features of his conductor, an under-jailer, ill-clothed, and of sullen appearance.\n\n0113m\n“Here is your chamber for tonight,” said he. “It is late, and the governor is asleep. Tomorrow, perhaps, he may change you. In the meantime there is bread, water, and fresh straw; and that is all a prisoner can wish for. Goodnight.” And before Dantès could open his mouth—before he had noticed where the jailer placed his bread or the water—before he had glanced towards the corner where the straw was, the jailer disappeared, taking with him the lamp and closing the door, leaving stamped upon the prisoner’s mind the dim reflection of the dripping walls of his dungeon.\n\nDantès was alone in darkness and in silence—cold as the shadows that he felt breathe on his burning forehead. With the first dawn of day the jailer returned, with orders to leave Dantès where he was. He found the prisoner in the same position, as if fixed there, his eyes swollen with weeping. He had passed the night standing, and without sleep. The jailer advanced; Dantès appeared not to perceive him. He touched him on the shoulder. Edmond started.\n\n“Have you not slept?” said the jailer.\n\n“I do not know,” replied Dantès. The jailer stared.\n\n“Are you hungry?” continued he.\n\n“I do not know.”\n\n“Do you wish for anything?”\n\n“I wish to see the governor.”\n\nThe jailer shrugged his shoulders and left the chamber.\n\nDantès followed him with his eyes, and stretched forth his hands towards the open door; but the door closed. All his emotion then burst forth; he cast himself on the ground, weeping bitterly, and asking himself what crime he had committed that he was thus punished.\n\nThe day passed thus; he scarcely tasted food, but walked round and round the cell like a wild beast in its cage. One thought in particular tormented him: namely, that during his journey hither he had sat so still, whereas he might, a dozen times, have plunged into the sea, and, thanks to his powers of swimming, for which he was famous, have gained the shore, concealed himself until the arrival of a Genoese or Spanish vessel, escaped to Spain or Italy, where Mercédès and his father could have joined him. He had no fears as to how he should live—good seamen are welcome everywhere. He spoke Italian like a Tuscan, and Spanish like a Castilian; he would have been free, and happy with Mercédès and his father, whereas he was now confined in the Château d’If, that impregnable fortress, ignorant of the future destiny of his father and Mercédès; and all this because he had trusted to Villefort’s promise. The thought was maddening, and Dantès threw himself furiously down on his straw. The next morning at the same hour, the jailer came again.\n\n“Well,” said the jailer, “are you more reasonable today?” Dantès made no reply.\n\n“Come, cheer up; is there anything that I can do for you?”\n\n“I wish to see the governor.”\n\n“I have already told you it was impossible.”\n\n“Why so?”\n\n“Because it is against prison rules, and prisoners must not even ask for it.”\n\n“What is allowed, then?”\n\n“Better fare, if you pay for it, books, and leave to walk about.”\n\n“I do not want books, I am satisfied with my food, and do not care to walk about; but I wish to see the governor.”\n\n“If you worry me by repeating the same thing, I will not bring you any more to eat.”\n\n“Well, then,” said Edmond, “if you do not, I shall die of hunger—that is all.”\n\nThe jailer saw by his tone he would be happy to die; and as every prisoner is worth ten sous a day to his jailer, he replied in a more subdued tone.\n\n“What you ask is impossible; but if you are very well behaved you will be allowed to walk about, and some day you will meet the governor, and if he chooses to reply, that is his affair.”\n\n“But,” asked Dantès, “how long shall I have to wait?”\n\n“Ah, a month—six months—a year.”\n\n“It is too long a time. I wish to see him at once.”\n\n“Ah,” said the jailer, “do not always brood over what is impossible, or you will be mad in a fortnight.”\n\n“You think so?”\n\n“Yes; we have an instance here; it was by always offering a million of francs to the governor for his liberty that an abbé became mad, who was in this chamber before you.”\n\n0119m\n“How long has he left it?”\n\n“Two years.”\n\n“Was he liberated, then?”\n\n“No; he was put in a dungeon.”\n\n“Listen!” said Dantès. “I am not an abbé, I am not mad; perhaps I shall be, but at present, unfortunately, I am not. I will make you another offer.”\n\n“What is that?”\n\n“I do not offer you a million, because I have it not; but I will give you a hundred crowns if, the first time you go to Marseilles, you will seek out a young girl named Mercédès, at the Catalans, and give her two lines from me.”\n0120m\n\n“If I took them, and were detected, I should lose my place, which is worth two thousand francs a year; so that I should be a great fool to run such a risk for three hundred.”\n\n“Well,” said Dantès, “mark this; if you refuse at least to tell Mercédès I am here, I will some day hide myself behind the door, and when you enter I will dash out your brains with this stool.”\n\n“Threats!” cried the jailer, retreating and putting himself on the defensive; “you are certainly going mad. The abbé began like you, and in three days you will be like him, mad enough to tie up; but, fortunately, there are dungeons here.”\n\nDantès whirled the stool round his head.\n\n“All right, all right,” said the jailer; “all right, since you will have it so. I will send word to the governor.”\n\n“Very well,” returned Dantès, dropping the stool and sitting on it as if he were in reality mad. The jailer went out, and returned in an instant with a corporal and four soldiers.\n\n“By the governor’s orders,” said he, “conduct the prisoner to the tier beneath.”\n\n“To the dungeon, then,” said the corporal.\n\n“Yes; we must put the madman with the madmen.” The soldiers seized Dantès, who followed passively.\n\nHe descended fifteen steps, and the door of a dungeon was opened, and he was thrust in. The door closed, and Dantès advanced with outstretched hands until he touched the wall; he then sat down in the corner until his eyes became accustomed to the darkness. The jailer was right; Dantès wanted but little of being utterly mad.\n\n# Chapter 9. The Evening of the Betrothal\n\nVillefort had, as we have said, hastened back to Madame de Saint-Méran’s in the Place du Grand Cours, and on entering the house found that the guests whom he had left at table were taking coffee in the salon. Renée was, with all the rest of the company, anxiously awaiting him, and his entrance was followed by a general exclamation.\n\n“Well, Decapitator, Guardian of the State, Royalist, Brutus, what is the matter?” said one. “Speak out.”\n\n“Are we threatened with a fresh Reign of Terror?” asked another.\n\n“Has the Corsican ogre broken loose?” cried a third.\n\n“Marquise,” said Villefort, approaching his future mother-in-law, “I request your pardon for thus leaving you. Will the marquis honor me by a few moments’ private conversation?”\n\n“Ah, it is really a serious matter, then?” asked the marquis, remarking the cloud on Villefort’s brow.\n\n“So serious that I must take leave of you for a few days; so,” added he, turning to Renée, “judge for yourself if it be not important.”\n\n“You are going to leave us?” cried Renée, unable to hide her emotion at this unexpected announcement.\n\n“Alas,” returned Villefort, “I must!”\n\n“Where, then, are you going?” asked the marquise.\n\n“That, madame, is an official secret; but if you have any commissions for Paris, a friend of mine is going there tonight, and will with pleasure undertake them.” The guests looked at each other.\n\n“You wish to speak to me alone?” said the marquis.\n\n“Yes, let us go to the library, please.” The marquis took his arm, and they left the salon.\n\n“Well,” asked he, as soon as they were by themselves, “tell me what it is?”\n\n“An affair of the greatest importance, that demands my immediate presence in Paris. Now, excuse the indiscretion, marquis, but have you any landed property?”\n\n“All my fortune is in the funds; seven or eight hundred thousand francs.”\n\n“Then sell out—sell out, marquis, or you will lose it all.”\n\n0123m\n“But how can I sell out here?”\n\n“You have a broker, have you not?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Then give me a letter to him, and tell him to sell out without an instant’s delay, perhaps even now I shall arrive too late.”\n\n“The deuce you say!” replied the marquis, “let us lose no time, then!”\n\nAnd, sitting down, he wrote a letter to his broker, ordering him to sell out at the market price.\n\n“Now, then,” said Villefort, placing the letter in his pocketbook, “I must have another!”\n\n“To whom?”\n“To the king.”\n\n“To the king?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“I dare not write to his majesty.”\n\n“I do not ask you to write to his majesty, but ask M. de Salvieux to do so. I want a letter that will enable me to reach the king’s presence without all the formalities of demanding an audience; that would occasion a loss of precious time.”\n\n“But address yourself to the keeper of the seals; he has the right of entry at the Tuileries, and can procure you audience at any hour of the day or night.”\n\n“Doubtless; but there is no occasion to divide the honors of my discovery with him. The keeper would leave me in the background, and take all the glory to himself. I tell you, marquis, my fortune is made if I only reach the Tuileries the first, for the king will not forget the service I do him.”\n\n“In that case go and get ready. I will call Salvieux and make him write the letter.”\n\n“Be as quick as possible, I must be on the road in a quarter of an hour.”\n\n“Tell your coachman to stop at the door.”\n\n“You will present my excuses to the marquise and Mademoiselle Renée, whom I leave on such a day with great regret.”\n\n“You will find them both here, and can make your farewells in person.”\n\n“A thousand thanks—and now for the letter.”\n\nThe marquis rang, a servant entered.\n\n“Say to the Comte de Salvieux that I would like to see him.”\n\n“Now, then, go,” said the marquis.\n\n“I shall be gone only a few moments.”\n\nVillefort hastily quitted the apartment, but reflecting that the sight of the deputy procureur running through the streets would be enough to throw the whole city into confusion, he resumed his ordinary pace. At his door he perceived a figure in the shadow that seemed to wait for him. It was Mercédès, who, hearing no news of her lover, had come unobserved to inquire after him.\n\nAs Villefort drew near, she advanced and stood before him. Dantès had spoken of Mercédès, and Villefort instantly recognized her. Her beauty and high bearing surprised him, and when she inquired what had become of her lover, it seemed to him that she was the judge, and he the accused.\n\n“The young man you speak of,” said Villefort abruptly, “is a great criminal, and I can do nothing for him, mademoiselle.” Mercédès burst into tears, and, as Villefort strove to pass her, again addressed him.\n\n“But, at least, tell me where he is, that I may know whether he is alive or dead,” said she.\n0125m\n\n“I do not know; he is no longer in my hands,” replied Villefort.\n\nAnd desirous of putting an end to the interview, he pushed by her, and closed the door, as if to exclude the pain he felt. But remorse is not thus banished; like Virgil’s wounded hero, he carried the arrow in his wound, and, arrived at the salon, Villefort uttered a sigh that was almost a sob, and sank into a chair.\n\nThen the first pangs of an unending torture seized upon his heart. The man he sacrificed to his ambition, that innocent victim immolated on the altar of his father’s faults, appeared to him pale and threatening, leading his affianced bride by the hand, and bringing with him remorse, not such as the ancients figured, furious and terrible, but that slow and consuming agony whose pangs are intensified from hour to hour up to the very moment of death. Then he had a moment’s hesitation. He had frequently called for capital punishment on criminals, and owing to his irresistible eloquence they had been condemned, and yet the slightest shadow of remorse had never clouded Villefort’s brow, because they were guilty; at least, he believed so; but here was an innocent man whose happiness he had destroyed. In this case he was not the judge, but the executioner.\n\nAs he thus reflected, he felt the sensation we have described, and which had hitherto been unknown to him, arise in his bosom, and fill him with vague apprehensions. It is thus that a wounded man trembles instinctively at the approach of the finger to his wound until it be healed, but Villefort’s was one of those that never close, or if they do, only close to reopen more agonizing than ever. If at this moment the sweet voice of Renée had sounded in his ears pleading for mercy, or the fair Mercédès had entered and said, “In the name of God, I conjure you to restore me my affianced husband,” his cold and trembling hands would have signed his release; but no voice broke the stillness of the chamber, and the door was opened only by Villefort’s valet, who came to tell him that the travelling carriage was in readiness.\n\nVillefort rose, or rather sprang, from his chair, hastily opened one of the drawers of his desk, emptied all the gold it contained into his pocket, stood motionless an instant, his hand pressed to his head, muttered a few inarticulate sounds, and then, perceiving that his servant had placed his cloak on his shoulders, he sprang into the carriage, ordering the postilions to drive to M. de Saint-Méran’s. The hapless Dantès was doomed.\n\nAs the marquis had promised, Villefort found the marquise and Renée in waiting. He started when he saw Renée, for he fancied she was again about to plead for Dantès. Alas, her emotions were wholly personal: she was thinking only of Villefort’s departure.\n\nShe loved Villefort, and he left her at the moment he was about to become her husband. Villefort knew not when he should return, and Renée, far from pleading for Dantès, hated the man whose crime separated her from her lover.\n\n0127m\nMeanwhile what of Mercédès? She had met Fernand at the corner of the Rue de la Loge; she had returned to the Catalans, and had despairingly cast herself on her couch. Fernand, kneeling by her side, took her hand, and covered it with kisses that Mercédès did not even feel. She passed the night thus. The lamp went out for want of oil, but she paid no heed to the darkness, and dawn came, but she knew not that it was day. Grief had made her blind to all but one object—that was Edmond.\n\n“Ah, you are there,” said she, at length, turning towards Fernand.\n\n“I have not quitted you since yesterday,” returned Fernand sorrowfully.\n\nM. Morrel had not readily given up the fight. He had learned that Dantès had been taken to prison, and he had gone to all his friends, and the influential persons of the city; but the report was already in circulation that Dantès was arrested as a Bonapartist agent; and as the most sanguine looked upon any attempt of Napoleon to remount the throne as impossible, he met with nothing but refusal, and had returned home in despair, declaring that the matter was serious and that nothing more could be done.\n\nCaderousse was equally restless and uneasy, but instead of seeking, like M. Morrel, to aid Dantès, he had shut himself up with two bottles of black currant brandy, in the hope of drowning reflection. But he did not succeed, and became too intoxicated to fetch any more drink, and yet not so intoxicated as to forget what had happened. With his elbows on the table he sat between the two empty bottles, while spectres danced in the light of the unsnuffed candle—spectres such as Hoffmann strews over his punch-drenched pages, like black, fantastic dust.\n\nDanglars alone was content and joyous—he had got rid of an enemy and made his own situation on the _Pharaon_ secure. Danglars was one of those men born with a pen behind the ear, and an inkstand in place of a heart. Everything with him was multiplication or subtraction. The life of a man was to him of far less value than a numeral, especially when, by taking it away, he could increase the sum total of his own desires. He went to bed at his usual hour, and slept in peace.\n\nVillefort, after having received M. de Salvieux’s letter, embraced Renée, kissed the marquise’s hand, and shaken that of the marquis, started for Paris along the Aix road.\n\nOld Dantès was dying with anxiety to know what had become of Edmond. But we know very well what had become of Edmond.\n\n# Chapter 10. The King’s Closet at the Tuileries\n\nWe will leave Villefort on the road to Paris, travelling—thanks to trebled fees—with all speed, and passing through two or three apartments, enter at the Tuileries the little room with the arched window, so well known as having been the favorite closet of Napoleon and Louis XVIII., and now of Louis Philippe.\n\nThere, seated before a walnut table he had brought with him from Hartwell, and to which, from one of those fancies not uncommon to great people, he was particularly attached, the king, Louis XVIII., was carelessly listening to a man of fifty or fifty-two years of age, with gray hair, aristocratic bearing, and exceedingly gentlemanly attire, and meanwhile making a marginal note in a volume of Gryphius’s rather inaccurate, but much sought-after, edition of Horace—a work which was much indebted to the sagacious observations of the philosophical monarch.\n\n“You say, sir——” said the king.\n\n“That I am exceedingly disquieted, sire.”\n\n“Really, have you had a vision of the seven fat kine and the seven lean kine?”\n\n“No, sire, for that would only betoken for us seven years of plenty and seven years of scarcity; and with a king as full of foresight as your majesty, scarcity is not a thing to be feared.”\n\n“Then of what other scourge are you afraid, my dear Blacas?”\n\n“Sire, I have every reason to believe that a storm is brewing in the south.”\n\n“Well, my dear duke,” replied Louis XVIII., “I think you are wrongly informed, and know positively that, on the contrary, it is very fine weather in that direction.” Man of ability as he was, Louis XVIII. liked a pleasant jest.\n\n“Sire,” continued M. de Blacas, “if it only be to reassure a faithful servant, will your majesty send into Languedoc, Provence, and Dauphiné, trusty men, who will bring you back a faithful report as to the feeling in these three provinces?”\n\n“_Canimus surdis_,” replied the king, continuing the annotations in his Horace.\n\n“Sire,” replied the courtier, laughing, in order that he might seem to comprehend the quotation, “your majesty may be perfectly right in relying on the good feeling of France, but I fear I am not altogether wrong in dreading some desperate attempt.”\n\n“By whom?”\n“By Bonaparte, or, at least, by his adherents.”\n\n“My dear Blacas,” said the king, “you with your alarms prevent me from working.”\n\n“And you, sire, prevent me from sleeping with your security.”\n\n“Wait, my dear sir, wait a moment; for I have such a delightful note on the _Pastor quum traheret_—wait, and I will listen to you afterwards.”\n\nThere was a brief pause, during which Louis XVIII. wrote, in a hand as small as possible, another note on the margin of his Horace, and then looking at the duke with the air of a man who thinks he has an idea of his own, while he is only commenting upon the idea of another, said:\n\n“Go on, my dear duke, go on—I listen.”\n\n“Sire,” said Blacas, who had for a moment the hope of sacrificing Villefort to his own profit, “I am compelled to tell you that these are not mere rumors destitute of foundation which thus disquiet me; but a serious-minded man, deserving all my confidence, and charged by me to watch over the south” (the duke hesitated as he pronounced these words), “has arrived by post to tell me that a great peril threatens the king, and so I hastened to you, sire.”\n\n“_Mala ducis avi domum_,” continued Louis XVIII., still annotating.\n\n“Does your majesty wish me to drop the subject?”\n\n“By no means, my dear duke; but just stretch out your hand.”\n\n“Which?”\n“Whichever you please—there to the left.”\n\n“I tell you to the left, and you are looking to the right; I mean on my left—yes, there. You will find yesterday’s report of the minister of police. But here is M. Dandré himself;” and M. Dandré, announced by the chamberlain-in-waiting, entered.\n\n“Come in,” said Louis XVIII., with repressed smile, “come in, Baron, and tell the duke all you know—the latest news of M. de Bonaparte; do not conceal anything, however serious,—let us see, the Island of Elba is a volcano, and we may expect to have issuing thence flaming and bristling war—_bella, horrida bella_.”\n\nM. Dandré leaned very respectfully on the back of a chair with his two hands, and said:\n\n“Has your majesty perused yesterday’s report?”\n\n“Yes, yes; but tell the duke himself, who cannot find anything, what the report contains—give him the particulars of what the usurper is doing in his islet.”\n\n“Monsieur,” said the baron to the duke, “all the servants of his majesty must approve of the latest intelligence which we have from the Island of Elba. Bonaparte——”\n\nM. Dandré looked at Louis XVIII., who, employed in writing a note, did not even raise his head. “Bonaparte,” continued the baron, “is mortally wearied, and passes whole days in watching his miners at work at Porto-Longone.”\n\n“And scratches himself for amusement,” added the king.\n\n“Scratches himself?” inquired the duke, “what does your majesty mean?”\n\n“Yes, indeed, my dear duke. Did you forget that this great man, this hero, this demigod, is attacked with a malady of the skin which worries him to death, _prurigo_?”\n\n“And, moreover, my dear duke,” continued the minister of police, “we are almost assured that, in a very short time, the usurper will be insane.”\n\n“Insane?”\n\n“Raving mad; his head becomes weaker. Sometimes he weeps bitterly, sometimes laughs boisterously, at other time he passes hours on the seashore, flinging stones in the water and when the flint makes ‘duck-and-drake’ five or six times, he appears as delighted as if he had gained another Marengo or Austerlitz. Now, you must agree that these are indubitable symptoms of insanity.”\n\n“Or of wisdom, my dear baron—or of wisdom,” said Louis XVIII., laughing; “the greatest captains of antiquity amused themselves by casting pebbles into the ocean—see Plutarch’s life of Scipio Africanus.”\n\nM. de Blacas pondered deeply between the confident monarch and the truthful minister. Villefort, who did not choose to reveal the whole secret, lest another should reap all the benefit of the disclosure, had yet communicated enough to cause him the greatest uneasiness.\n\n“Well, well, Dandré,” said Louis XVIII., “Blacas is not yet convinced; let us proceed, therefore, to the usurper’s conversion.” The minister of police bowed.\n\n“The usurper’s conversion!” murmured the duke, looking at the king and Dandré, who spoke alternately, like Virgil’s shepherds. “The usurper converted!”\n\n“Decidedly, my dear duke.”\n\n“In what way converted?”\n\n“To good principles. Tell him all about it, baron.”\n\n“Why, this is the way of it,” said the minister, with the gravest air in the world: “Napoleon lately had a review, and as two or three of his old veterans expressed a desire to return to France, he gave them their dismissal, and exhorted them to ‘serve the good king.’ These were his own words, of that I am certain.”\n\n“Well, Blacas, what think you of this?” inquired the king triumphantly, and pausing for a moment from the voluminous scholiast before him.\n\n“I say, sire, that the minister of police is greatly deceived or I am; and as it is impossible it can be the minister of police as he has the guardianship of the safety and honor of your majesty, it is probable that I am in error. However, sire, if I might advise, your majesty will interrogate the person of whom I spoke to you, and I will urge your majesty to do him this honor.”\n\n“Most willingly, duke; under your auspices I will receive any person you please, but you must not expect me to be too confiding. Baron, have you any report more recent than this, dated the 20th February, and this is the 3rd of March?”\n\n“No, sire, but I am hourly expecting one; it may have arrived since I left my office.”\n\n“Go thither, and if there be none—well, well,” continued Louis XVIII., “make one; that is the usual way, is it not?” and the king laughed facetiously.\n\n“Oh, sire,” replied the minister, “we have no occasion to invent any; every day our desks are loaded with most circumstantial denunciations, coming from hosts of people who hope for some return for services which they seek to render, but cannot; they trust to fortune, and rely upon some unexpected event in some way to justify their predictions.”\n\n“Well, sir, go,” said Louis XVIII., “and remember that I am waiting for you.”\n\n“I will but go and return, sire; I shall be back in ten minutes.”\n\n“And I, sire,” said M. de Blacas, “will go and find my messenger.”\n\n“Wait, sir, wait,” said Louis XVIII. “Really, M. de Blacas, I must change your armorial bearings; I will give you an eagle with outstretched wings, holding in its claws a prey which tries in vain to escape, and bearing this device—_Tenax_.”\n0133m\n\n“Sire, I listen,” said De Blacas, biting his nails with impatience.\n\n“I wish to consult you on this passage, ‘_Molli fugiens anhelitu_,’ you know it refers to a stag flying from a wolf. Are you not a sportsman and a great wolf-hunter? Well, then, what do you think of the _molli anhelitu_?”\n\n“Admirable, sire; but my messenger is like the stag you refer to, for he has posted two hundred and twenty leagues in scarcely three days.”\n\n“Which is undergoing great fatigue and anxiety, my dear duke, when we have a telegraph which transmits messages in three or four hours, and that without getting in the least out of breath.”\n\n“Ah, sire, you recompense but badly this poor young man, who has come so far, and with so much ardor, to give your majesty useful information. If only for the sake of M. de Salvieux, who recommends him to me, I entreat your majesty to receive him graciously.”\n\n“M. de Salvieux, my brother’s chamberlain?”\n\n“Yes, sire.”\n\n“He is at Marseilles.”\n\n“And writes me thence.”\n\n“Does he speak to you of this conspiracy?”\n\n“No; but strongly recommends M. de Villefort, and begs me to present him to your majesty.”\n\n“M. de Villefort!” cried the king, “is the messenger’s name M. de Villefort?”\n\n“Yes, sire.”\n\n“And he comes from Marseilles?”\n\n“In person.”\n\n“Why did you not mention his name at once?” replied the king, betraying some uneasiness.\n\n“Sire, I thought his name was unknown to your majesty.”\n\n“No, no, Blacas; he is a man of strong and elevated understanding, ambitious, too, and, _pardieu!_ you know his father’s name!”\n\n“His father?”\n\n“Yes, Noirtier.”\n\n“Noirtier the Girondin?—Noirtier the senator?”\n\n“He himself.”\n\n“And your majesty has employed the son of such a man?”\n\n“Blacas, my friend, you have but limited comprehension. I told you Villefort was ambitious, and to attain this ambition Villefort would sacrifice everything, even his father.”\n\n“Then, sire, may I present him?”\n\n“This instant, duke! Where is he?”\n\n“Waiting below, in my carriage.”\n\n“Seek him at once.”\n\n“I hasten to do so.”\n\nThe duke left the royal presence with the speed of a young man; his really sincere royalism made him youthful again. Louis XVIII. remained alone, and turning his eyes on his half-opened Horace, muttered:\n\n“_Justum et tenacem propositi virum_.”\n\nM. de Blacas returned as speedily as he had departed, but in the antechamber he was forced to appeal to the king’s authority. Villefort’s dusty garb, his costume, which was not of courtly cut, excited the susceptibility of M. de Brezé, who was all astonishment at finding that this young man had the audacity to enter before the king in such attire. The duke, however, overcame all difficulties with a word—his majesty’s order; and, in spite of the protestations which the master of ceremonies made for the honor of his office and principles, Villefort was introduced.\n\nThe king was seated in the same place where the duke had left him. On opening the door, Villefort found himself facing him, and the young magistrate’s first impulse was to pause.\n\n“Come in, M. de Villefort,” said the king, “come in.”\n\nVillefort bowed, and advancing a few steps, waited until the king should interrogate him.\n\n“M. de Villefort,” said Louis XVIII., “the Duc de Blacas assures me you have some interesting information to communicate.”\n\n“Sire, the duke is right, and I believe your majesty will think it equally important.”\n\n0137m\n“In the first place, and before everything else, sir, is the news as bad in your opinion as I am asked to believe?”\n\n“Sire, I believe it to be most urgent, but I hope, by the speed I have used, that it is not irreparable.”\n\n“Speak as fully as you please, sir,” said the king, who began to give way to the emotion which had showed itself in Blacas’s face and affected Villefort’s voice. “Speak, sir, and pray begin at the beginning; I like order in everything.”\n\n“Sire,” said Villefort, “I will render a faithful report to your majesty, but I must entreat your forgiveness if my anxiety leads to some obscurity in my language.” A glance at the king after this discreet and subtle exordium, assured Villefort of the benignity of his august auditor, and he went on:\n\n“Sire, I have come as rapidly to Paris as possible, to inform your majesty that I have discovered, in the exercise of my duties, not a commonplace and insignificant plot, such as is every day got up in the lower ranks of the people and in the army, but an actual conspiracy—a storm which menaces no less than your majesty’s throne. Sire, the usurper is arming three ships, he meditates some project, which, however mad, is yet, perhaps, terrible. At this moment he will have left Elba, to go whither I know not, but assuredly to attempt a landing either at Naples, or on the coast of Tuscany, or perhaps on the shores of France. Your majesty is well aware that the sovereign of the Island of Elba has maintained his relations with Italy and France?”\n\n“I am, sir,” said the king, much agitated; “and recently we have had information that the Bonapartist clubs have had meetings in the Rue Saint-Jacques. But proceed, I beg of you. How did you obtain these details?”\n\n“Sire, they are the results of an examination which I have made of a man of Marseilles, whom I have watched for some time, and arrested on the day of my departure. This person, a sailor, of turbulent character, and whom I suspected of Bonapartism, has been secretly to the Island of Elba. There he saw the grand-marshal, who charged him with an oral message to a Bonapartist in Paris, whose name I could not extract from him; but this mission was to prepare men’s minds for a return (it is the man who says this, sire)—a return which will soon occur.”\n\n“And where is this man?”\n\n“In prison, sire.”\n\n“And the matter seems serious to you?”\n\n“So serious, sire, that when the circumstance surprised me in the midst of a family festival, on the very day of my betrothal, I left my bride and friends, postponing everything, that I might hasten to lay at your majesty’s feet the fears which impressed me, and the assurance of my devotion.”\n\n“True,” said Louis XVIII., “was there not a marriage engagement between you and Mademoiselle de Saint-Méran?”\n\n“Daughter of one of your majesty’s most faithful servants.”\n\n“Yes, yes; but let us talk of this plot, M. de Villefort.”\n\n“Sire, I fear it is more than a plot; I fear it is a conspiracy.”\n\n“A conspiracy in these times,” said Louis XVIII., smiling, “is a thing very easy to meditate, but more difficult to conduct to an end, inasmuch as, re-established so recently on the throne of our ancestors, we have our eyes open at once upon the past, the present, and the future. For the last ten months my ministers have redoubled their vigilance, in order to watch the shore of the Mediterranean. If Bonaparte landed at Naples, the whole coalition would be on foot before he could even reach Piombino; if he land in Tuscany, he will be in an unfriendly territory; if he land in France, it must be with a handful of men, and the result of that is easily foretold, execrated as he is by the population. Take courage, sir; but at the same time rely on our royal gratitude.”\n\n“Ah, here is M. Dandré!” cried de Blacas. At this instant the minister of police appeared at the door, pale, trembling, and as if ready to faint. Villefort was about to retire, but M. de Blacas, taking his hand, restrained him.\n\n# Chapter 11. The Corsican Ogre\n\nAt the sight of this agitation Louis XVIII. pushed from him violently the table at which he was sitting.\n\n“What ails you, baron?” he exclaimed. “You appear quite aghast. Has your uneasiness anything to do with what M. de Blacas has told me, and M. de Villefort has just confirmed?” M. de Blacas moved suddenly towards the baron, but the fright of the courtier pleaded for the forbearance of the statesman; and besides, as matters were, it was much more to his advantage that the prefect of police should triumph over him than that he should humiliate the prefect.\n\n“Sire,——” stammered the baron.\n\n“Well, what is it?” asked Louis XVIII. The minister of police, giving way to an impulse of despair, was about to throw himself at the feet of Louis XVIII., who retreated a step and frowned.\n\n“Will you speak?” he said.\n\n“Oh, sire, what a dreadful misfortune! I am, indeed, to be pitied. I can never forgive myself!”\n\n“Monsieur,” said Louis XVIII., “I command you to speak.”\n\n“Well, sire, the usurper left Elba on the 26th February, and landed on the 1st of March.”\n\n“And where? In Italy?” asked the king eagerly.\n\n“In France, sire,—at a small port, near Antibes, in the Gulf of Juan.”\n\n“The usurper landed in France, near Antibes, in the Gulf of Juan, two hundred and fifty leagues from Paris, on the 1st of March, and you only acquired this information today, the 3rd of March! Well, sir, what you tell me is impossible. You must have received a false report, or you have gone mad.”\n\n“Alas, sire, it is but too true!” Louis made a gesture of indescribable anger and alarm, and then drew himself up as if this sudden blow had struck him at the same moment in heart and countenance.\n\n“In France!” he cried, “the usurper in France! Then they did not watch over this man. Who knows? they were, perhaps, in league with him.”\n\n“Oh, sire,” exclaimed the Duc de Blacas, “M. Dandré is not a man to be accused of treason! Sire, we have all been blind, and the minister of police has shared the general blindness, that is all.”\n\n“But——” said Villefort, and then suddenly checking himself, he was silent; then he continued, “Your pardon, sire,” he said, bowing, “my zeal carried me away. Will your majesty deign to excuse me?”\n\n“Speak, sir, speak boldly,” replied Louis. “You alone forewarned us of the evil; now try and aid us with the remedy.”\n\n“Sire,” said Villefort, “the usurper is detested in the south; and it seems to me that if he ventured into the south, it would be easy to raise Languedoc and Provence against him.”\n\n“Yes, assuredly,” replied the minister; “but he is advancing by Gap and Sisteron.”\n\n“Advancing—he is advancing!” said Louis XVIII. “Is he then advancing on Paris?” The minister of police maintained a silence which was equivalent to a complete avowal.\n\n“And Dauphiné, sir?” inquired the king, of Villefort. “Do you think it possible to rouse that as well as Provence?”\n\n“Sire, I am sorry to tell your majesty a cruel fact; but the feeling in Dauphiné is quite the reverse of that in Provence or Languedoc. The mountaineers are Bonapartists, sire.”\n\n“Then,” murmured Louis, “he was well informed. And how many men had he with him?”\n\n“I do not know, sire,” answered the minister of police.\n\n“What, you do not know! Have you neglected to obtain information on that point? Of course it is of no consequence,” he added, with a withering smile.\n\n“Sire, it was impossible to learn; the despatch simply stated the fact of the landing and the route taken by the usurper.”\n\n“And how did this despatch reach you?” inquired the king. The minister bowed his head, and while a deep color overspread his cheeks, he stammered out:\n\n“By the telegraph, sire.” Louis XVIII. advanced a step, and folded his arms over his chest as Napoleon would have done.\n\n0141m\n“So then,” he exclaimed, turning pale with anger, “seven conjoined and allied armies overthrew that man. A miracle of heaven replaced me on the throne of my fathers after five-and-twenty years of exile. I have, during those five-and-twenty years, spared no pains to understand the people of France and the interests which were confided to me; and now, when I see the fruition of my wishes almost within reach, the power I hold in my hands bursts and shatters me to atoms!”\n\n“Sire, it is fatality!” murmured the minister, feeling that the pressure of circumstances, however light a thing to destiny, was too much for any human strength to endure.\n\n“What our enemies say of us is then true. We have learnt nothing, forgotten nothing! If I were betrayed as he was, I would console myself; but to be in the midst of persons elevated by myself to places of honor, who ought to watch over me more carefully than over themselves,—for my fortune is theirs—before me they were nothing—after me they will be nothing, and perish miserably from incapacity—ineptitude! Oh, yes, sir, you are right—it is fatality!”\n\nThe minister quailed before this outburst of sarcasm. M. de Blacas wiped the moisture from his brow. Villefort smiled within himself, for he felt his increased importance.\n\n“To fall,” continued King Louis, who at the first glance had sounded the abyss on which the monarchy hung suspended,—“to fall, and learn of that fall by telegraph! Oh, I would rather mount the scaffold of my brother, Louis XVI., than thus descend the staircase at the Tuileries driven away by ridicule. Ridicule, sir—why, you know not its power in France, and yet you ought to know it!”\n\n“Sire, sire,” murmured the minister, “for pity’s——”\n\n“Approach, M. de Villefort,” resumed the king, addressing the young man, who, motionless and breathless, was listening to a conversation on which depended the destiny of a kingdom. “Approach, and tell monsieur that it is possible to know beforehand all that he has not known.”\n\n“Sire, it was really impossible to learn secrets which that man concealed from all the world.”\n\n“Really impossible! Yes—that is a great word, sir. Unfortunately, there are great words, as there are great men; I have measured them. Really impossible for a minister who has an office, agents, spies, and fifteen hundred thousand francs for secret service money, to know what is going on at sixty leagues from the coast of France! Well, then, see, here is a gentleman who had none of these resources at his disposal—a gentleman, only a simple magistrate, who learned more than you with all your police, and who would have saved my crown, if, like you, he had the power of directing a telegraph.” The look of the minister of police was turned with concentrated spite on Villefort, who bent his head in modest triumph.\n\n“I do not mean that for you, Blacas,” continued Louis XVIII.; “for if you have discovered nothing, at least you have had the good sense to persevere in your suspicions. Any other than yourself would have considered the disclosure of M. de Villefort insignificant, or else dictated by venal ambition.” These words were an allusion to the sentiments which the minister of police had uttered with so much confidence an hour before.\n\nVillefort understood the king’s intent. Any other person would, perhaps, have been overcome by such an intoxicating draught of praise; but he feared to make for himself a mortal enemy of the police minister, although he saw that Dandré was irrevocably lost. In fact, the minister, who, in the plenitude of his power, had been unable to unearth Napoleon’s secret, might in despair at his own downfall interrogate Dantès and so lay bare the motives of Villefort’s plot. Realizing this, Villefort came to the rescue of the crest-fallen minister, instead of aiding to crush him.\n\n“Sire,” said Villefort, “the suddenness of this event must prove to your majesty that the issue is in the hands of Providence; what your majesty is pleased to attribute to me as profound perspicacity is simply owing to chance, and I have profited by that chance, like a good and devoted servant—that’s all. Do not attribute to me more than I deserve, sire, that your majesty may never have occasion to recall the first opinion you have been pleased to form of me.” The minister of police thanked the young man by an eloquent look, and Villefort understood that he had succeeded in his design; that is to say, that without forfeiting the gratitude of the king, he had made a friend of one on whom, in case of necessity, he might rely.\n\n“’Tis well,” resumed the king. “And now, gentlemen,” he continued, turning towards M. de Blacas and the minister of police, “I have no further occasion for you, and you may retire; what now remains to do is in the department of the minister of war.”\n\n“Fortunately, sire,” said M. de Blacas, “we can rely on the army; your majesty knows how every report confirms their loyalty and attachment.”\n\n“Do not mention reports, duke, to me, for I know now what confidence to place in them. Yet, speaking of reports, baron, what have you learned with regard to the affair in the Rue Saint-Jacques?”\n\n“The affair in the Rue Saint-Jacques!” exclaimed Villefort, unable to repress an exclamation. Then, suddenly pausing, he added, “Your pardon, sire, but my devotion to your majesty has made me forget, not the respect I have, for that is too deeply engraved in my heart, but the rules of etiquette.”\n\n“Go on, go on, sir,” replied the king; “you have today earned the right to make inquiries here.”\n\n“Sire,” interposed the minister of police, “I came a moment ago to give your majesty fresh information which I had obtained on this head, when your majesty’s attention was attracted by the terrible event that has occurred in the gulf, and now these facts will cease to interest your majesty.”\n\n“On the contrary, sir,—on the contrary,” said Louis XVIII., “this affair seems to me to have a decided connection with that which occupies our attention, and the death of General Quesnel will, perhaps, put us on the direct track of a great internal conspiracy.” At the name of General Quesnel, Villefort trembled.\n\n“Everything points to the conclusion, sire,” said the minister of police, “that death was not the result of suicide, as we first believed, but of assassination. General Quesnel, it appears, had just left a Bonapartist club when he disappeared. An unknown person had been with him that morning, and made an appointment with him in the Rue Saint-Jacques; unfortunately, the general’s valet, who was dressing his hair at the moment when the stranger entered, heard the street mentioned, but did not catch the number.” As the police minister related this to the king, Villefort, who looked as if his very life hung on the speaker’s lips, turned alternately red and pale. The king looked towards him.\n\n“Do you not think with me, M. de Villefort, that General Quesnel, whom they believed attached to the usurper, but who was really entirely devoted to me, has perished the victim of a Bonapartist ambush?”\n\n“It is probable, sire,” replied Villefort. “But is this all that is known?”\n\n“They are on the track of the man who appointed the meeting with him.”\n\n“On his track?” said Villefort.\n\n“Yes, the servant has given his description. He is a man of from fifty to fifty-two years of age, dark, with black eyes covered with shaggy eyebrows, and a thick moustache. He was dressed in a blue frock-coat, buttoned up to the chin, and wore at his button-hole the rosette of an officer of the Legion of Honor. Yesterday a person exactly corresponding with this description was followed, but he was lost sight of at the corner of the Rue de la Jussienne and the Rue Coq-Héron.” Villefort leaned on the back of an armchair, for as the minister of police went on speaking he felt his legs bend under him; but when he learned that the unknown had escaped the vigilance of the agent who followed him, he breathed again.\n\n“Continue to seek for this man, sir,” said the king to the minister of police; “for if, as I am all but convinced, General Quesnel, who would have been so useful to us at this moment, has been murdered, his assassins, Bonapartists or not, shall be cruelly punished.” It required all Villefort’s coolness not to betray the terror with which this declaration of the king inspired him.\n\n“How strange,” continued the king, with some asperity; “the police think that they have disposed of the whole matter when they say, ‘A murder has been committed,’ and especially so when they can add, ‘And we are on the track of the guilty persons.’”\n\n“Sire, your majesty will, I trust, be amply satisfied on this point at least.”\n\n“We shall see. I will no longer detain you, M. de Villefort, for you must be fatigued after so long a journey; go and rest. Of course you stopped at your father’s?” A feeling of faintness came over Villefort.\n0145m\n\n“No, sire,” he replied, “I alighted at the Hotel de Madrid, in the Rue de Tournon.”\n\n“But you have seen him?”\n\n“Sire, I went straight to the Duc de Blacas.”\n\n“But you will see him, then?”\n\n“I think not, sire.”\n\n“Ah, I forgot,” said Louis, smiling in a manner which proved that all these questions were not made without a motive; “I forgot you and M. Noirtier are not on the best terms possible, and that is another sacrifice made to the royal cause, and for which you should be recompensed.”\n\n“Sire, the kindness your majesty deigns to evince towards me is a recompense which so far surpasses my utmost ambition that I have nothing more to ask for.”\n\n“Never mind, sir, we will not forget you; make your mind easy. In the meanwhile” (the king here detached the cross of the Legion of Honor which he usually wore over his blue coat, near the cross of St. Louis, above the order of Notre-Dame-du-Mont-Carmel and St. Lazare, and gave it to Villefort)—“in the meanwhile take this cross.”\n\n“Sire,” said Villefort, “your majesty mistakes; this is an officer’s cross.”\n\n“_Ma foi!_” said Louis XVIII., “take it, such as it is, for I have not the time to procure you another. Blacas, let it be your care to see that the brevet is made out and sent to M. de Villefort.” Villefort’s eyes were filled with tears of joy and pride; he took the cross and kissed it.\n\n“And now,” he said, “may I inquire what are the orders with which your majesty deigns to honor me?”\n\n“Take what rest you require, and remember that if you are not able to serve me here in Paris, you may be of the greatest service to me at Marseilles.”\n\n“Sire,” replied Villefort, bowing, “in an hour I shall have quitted Paris.”\n\n“Go, sir,” said the king; “and should I forget you (kings’ memories are short), do not be afraid to bring yourself to my recollection. Baron, send for the minister of war. Blacas, remain.”\n\n“Ah, sir,” said the minister of police to Villefort, as they left the Tuileries, “you entered by luck’s door—your fortune is made.”\n\n“Will it be long first?” muttered Villefort, saluting the minister, whose career was ended, and looking about him for a hackney-coach. One passed at the moment, which he hailed; he gave his address to the driver, and springing in, threw himself on the seat, and gave loose to dreams of ambition.\n\nTen minutes afterwards Villefort reached his hotel, ordered horses to be ready in two hours, and asked to have his breakfast brought to him. He was about to begin his repast when the sound of the bell rang sharp and loud. The valet opened the door, and Villefort heard someone speak his name.\n0147m\n\n“Who could know that I was here already?” said the young man. The valet entered.\n\n“Well,” said Villefort, “what is it?—Who rang?—Who asked for me?”\n\n“A stranger who will not send in his name.”\n\n“A stranger who will not send in his name! What can he want with me?”\n\n“He wishes to speak to you.”\n\n“To me?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Did he mention my name?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“What sort of person is he?”\n\n“Why, sir, a man of about fifty.”\n\n“Short or tall?”\n\n“About your own height, sir.”\n\n“Dark or fair?”\n\n“Dark,—very dark; with black eyes, black hair, black eyebrows.”\n\n“And how dressed?” asked Villefort quickly.\n\n“In a blue frock-coat, buttoned up close, decorated with the Legion of Honor.”\n\n“It is he!” said Villefort, turning pale.\n\n0148m\n“Eh, _pardieu!_” said the individual whose description we have twice given, entering the door, “what a great deal of ceremony! Is it the custom in Marseilles for sons to keep their fathers waiting in their anterooms?”\n\n“Father!” cried Villefort, “then I was not deceived; I felt sure it must be you.”\n\n“Well, then, if you felt so sure,” replied the new-comer, putting his cane in a corner and his hat on a chair, “allow me to say, my dear Gérard, that it was not very filial of you to keep me waiting at the door.”\n\n“Leave us, Germain,” said Villefort. The servant quitted the apartment with evident signs of astonishment.\n\n# Chapter 12. Father and Son\n\nM. Noirtier—for it was, indeed, he who entered—looked after the servant until the door was closed, and then, fearing, no doubt, that he might be overheard in the antechamber, he opened the door again, nor was the precaution useless, as appeared from the rapid retreat of Germain, who proved that he was not exempt from the sin which ruined our first parents. M. Noirtier then took the trouble to close and bolt the antechamber door, then that of the bedchamber, and then extended his hand to Villefort, who had followed all his motions with surprise which he could not conceal.\n\n“Well, now, my dear Gérard,” said he to the young man, with a very significant look, “do you know, you seem as if you were not very glad to see me?”\n\n“My dear father,” said Villefort, “I am, on the contrary, delighted; but I so little expected your visit, that it has somewhat overcome me.”\n\n“But, my dear fellow,” replied M. Noirtier, seating himself, “I might say the same thing to you, when you announce to me your wedding for the 28th of February, and on the 3rd of March you turn up here in Paris.”\n\n“And if I have come, my dear father,” said Gérard, drawing closer to M. Noirtier, “do not complain, for it is for you that I came, and my journey will be your salvation.”\n\n“Ah, indeed!” said M. Noirtier, stretching himself out at his ease in the chair. “Really, pray tell me all about it, for it must be interesting.”\n\n“Father, you have heard speak of a certain Bonapartist club in the Rue Saint-Jacques?”\n\n“No. 53; yes, I am vice-president.”\n\n“Father, your coolness makes me shudder.”\n\n“Why, my dear boy, when a man has been proscribed by the mountaineers, has escaped from Paris in a hay-cart, been hunted over the plains of Bordeaux by Robespierre’s bloodhounds, he becomes accustomed to most things. But go on, what about the club in the Rue Saint-Jacques?”\n\n“Why, they induced General Quesnel to go there, and General Quesnel, who quitted his own house at nine o’clock in the evening, was found the next day in the Seine.”\n\n0151m\n“And who told you this fine story?”\n\n“The king himself.”\n“Well, then, in return for your story,” continued Noirtier, “I will tell you another.”\n\n“My dear father, I think I already know what you are about to tell me.”\n\n“Ah, you have heard of the landing of the emperor?”\n\n“Not so loud, father, I entreat of you—for your own sake as well as mine. Yes, I heard this news, and knew it even before you could; for three days ago I posted from Marseilles to Paris with all possible speed, half-desperate at the enforced delay.”\n\n“Three days ago? You are crazy. Why, three days ago the emperor had not landed.”\n\n“No matter, I was aware of his intention.”\n\n“How did you know about it?”\n\n“By a letter addressed to you from the Island of Elba.”\n\n“To me?”\n\n“To you; and which I discovered in the pocket-book of the messenger. Had that letter fallen into the hands of another, you, my dear father, would probably ere this have been shot.” Villefort’s father laughed.\n\n“Come, come,” said he, “will the Restoration adopt imperial methods so promptly? Shot, my dear boy? What an idea! Where is the letter you speak of? I know you too well to suppose you would allow such a thing to pass you.”\n\n“I burnt it, for fear that even a fragment should remain; for that letter must have led to your condemnation.”\n\n“And the destruction of your future prospects,” replied Noirtier; “yes, I can easily comprehend that. But I have nothing to fear while I have you to protect me.”\n\n“I do better than that, sir—I save you.”\n\n“You do? Why, really, the thing becomes more and more dramatic—explain yourself.”\n\n“I must refer again to the club in the Rue Saint-Jacques.”\n\n“It appears that this club is rather a bore to the police. Why didn’t they search more vigilantly? they would have found——”\n\n“They have not found; but they are on the track.”\n\n“Yes, that the usual phrase; I am quite familiar with it. When the police is at fault, it declares that it is on the track; and the government patiently awaits the day when it comes to say, with a sneaking air, that the track is lost.”\n\n“Yes, but they have found a corpse; the general has been killed, and in all countries they call that a murder.”\n\n“A murder do you call it? why, there is nothing to prove that the general was murdered. People are found every day in the Seine, having thrown themselves in, or having been drowned from not knowing how to swim.”\n\n“Father, you know very well that the general was not a man to drown himself in despair, and people do not bathe in the Seine in the month of January. No, no, do not be deceived; this was murder in every sense of the word.”\n\n“And who thus designated it?”\n\n“The king himself.”\n\n“The king! I thought he was philosopher enough to allow that there was no murder in politics. In politics, my dear fellow, you know, as well as I do, there are no men, but ideas—no feelings, but interests; in politics we do not kill a man, we only remove an obstacle, that is all. Would you like to know how matters have progressed? Well, I will tell you. It was thought reliance might be placed in General Quesnel; he was recommended to us from the Island of Elba; one of us went to him, and invited him to the Rue Saint-Jacques, where he would find some friends. He came there, and the plan was unfolded to him for leaving Elba, the projected landing, etc. When he had heard and comprehended all to the fullest extent, he replied that he was a royalist. Then all looked at each other,—he was made to take an oath, and did so, but with such an ill grace that it was really tempting Providence to swear thus, and yet, in spite of that, the general was allowed to depart free—perfectly free. Yet he did not return home. What could that mean? why, my dear fellow, that on leaving us he lost his way, that’s all. A murder? really, Villefort, you surprise me. You, a deputy procureur, to found an accusation on such bad premises! Did I ever say to you, when you were fulfilling your character as a royalist, and cut off the head of one of my party, ‘My son, you have committed a murder?’ No, I said, ‘Very well, sir, you have gained the victory; tomorrow, perchance, it will be our turn.’”\n\n“But, father, take care; when our turn comes, our revenge will be sweeping.”\n\n“I do not understand you.”\n\n“You rely on the usurper’s return?”\n\n“We do.”\n\n“You are mistaken; he will not advance two leagues into the interior of France without being followed, tracked, and caught like a wild beast.”\n\n“My dear fellow, the emperor is at this moment on the way to Grenoble; on the 10th or 12th he will be at Lyons, and on the 20th or 25th at Paris.”\n\n“The people will rise.”\n\n“Yes, to go and meet him.”\n\n“He has but a handful of men with him, and armies will be despatched against him.”\n\n“Yes, to escort him into the capital. Really, my dear Gérard, you are but a child; you think yourself well informed because the telegraph has told you, three days after the landing, ‘The usurper has landed at Cannes with several men. He is pursued.’ But where is he? what is he doing? You do not know at all, and in this way they will chase him to Paris, without drawing a trigger.”\n\n“Grenoble and Lyons are faithful cities, and will oppose to him an impassable barrier.”\n\n“Grenoble will open her gates to him with enthusiasm—all Lyons will hasten to welcome him. Believe me, we are as well informed as you, and our police are as good as your own. Would you like a proof of it? well, you wished to conceal your journey from me, and yet I knew of your arrival half an hour after you had passed the barrier. You gave your direction to no one but your postilion, yet I have your address, and in proof I am here the very instant you are going to sit at table. Ring, then, if you please, for a second knife, fork, and plate, and we will dine together.”\n\n“Indeed!” replied Villefort, looking at his father with astonishment, “you really do seem very well informed.”\n\n“Eh? the thing is simple enough. You who are in power have only the means that money produces—we who are in expectation, have those which devotion prompts.”\n\n“Devotion!” said Villefort, with a sneer.\n\n“Yes, devotion; for that is, I believe, the phrase for hopeful ambition.”\n\nAnd Villefort’s father extended his hand to the bell-rope, to summon the servant whom his son had not called. Villefort caught his arm.\n\n“Wait, my dear father,” said the young man, “one word more.”\n\n“Say on.”\n\n“However stupid the royalist police may be, they do know one terrible thing.”\n\n“What is that?”\n\n“The description of the man who, on the morning of the day when General Quesnel disappeared, presented himself at his house.”\n\n“Oh, the admirable police have found that out, have they? And what may be that description?”\n\n“Dark complexion; hair, eyebrows, and whiskers black; blue frock-coat, buttoned up to the chin; rosette of an officer of the Legion of Honor in his button-hole; a hat with wide brim, and a cane.”\n\n“Ah, ha, that’s it, is it?” said Noirtier; “and why, then, have they not laid hands on him?”\n\n“Because yesterday, or the day before, they lost sight of him at the corner of the Rue Coq-Héron.”\n\n“Didn’t I say that your police were good for nothing?”\n\n“Yes; but they may catch him yet.”\n\n“True,” said Noirtier, looking carelessly around him, “true, if this person were not on his guard, as he is;” and he added with a smile, “He will consequently make a few changes in his personal appearance.” At these words he rose, and put off his frock-coat and cravat, went towards a table on which lay his son’s toilet articles, lathered his face, took a razor, and, with a firm hand, cut off the compromising whiskers. Villefort watched him with alarm not devoid of admiration.\n\nHis whiskers cut off, Noirtier gave another turn to his hair; took, instead of his black cravat, a colored neckerchief which lay at the top of an open portmanteau; put on, in lieu of his blue and high-buttoned frock-coat, a coat of Villefort’s of dark brown, and cut away in front; tried on before the glass a narrow-brimmed hat of his son’s, which appeared to fit him perfectly, and, leaving his cane in the corner where he had deposited it, he took up a small bamboo switch, cut the air with it once or twice, and walked about with that easy swagger which was one of his principal characteristics.\n\n“Well,” he said, turning towards his wondering son, when this disguise was completed, “well, do you think your police will recognize me now.”\n\n“No, father,” stammered Villefort; “at least, I hope not.”\n\n“And now, my dear boy,” continued Noirtier, “I rely on your prudence to remove all the things which I leave in your care.”\n\n“Oh, rely on me,” said Villefort.\n\n“Yes, yes; and now I believe you are right, and that you have really saved my life; be assured I will return the favor hereafter.”\n\nVillefort shook his head.\n\n“You are not convinced yet?”\n\n“I hope at least, that you may be mistaken.”\n\n“Shall you see the king again?”\n\n“Perhaps.”\n\n“Would you pass in his eyes for a prophet?”\n\n“Prophets of evil are not in favor at the court, father.”\n\n“True, but some day they do them justice; and supposing a second restoration, you would then pass for a great man.”\n\n“Well, what should I say to the king?”\n\n“Say this to him: ‘Sire, you are deceived as to the feeling in France, as to the opinions of the towns, and the prejudices of the army; he whom in Paris you call the Corsican ogre, who at Nevers is styled the usurper, is already saluted as Bonaparte at Lyons, and emperor at Grenoble. You think he is tracked, pursued, captured; he is advancing as rapidly as his own eagles. The soldiers you believe to be dying with hunger, worn out with fatigue, ready to desert, gather like atoms of snow about the rolling ball as it hastens onward. Sire, go, leave France to its real master, to him who acquired it, not by purchase, but by right of conquest; go, sire, not that you incur any risk, for your adversary is powerful enough to show you mercy, but because it would be humiliating for a grandson of Saint Louis to owe his life to the man of Arcola, Marengo, Austerlitz.’ Tell him this, Gérard; or, rather, tell him nothing. Keep your journey a secret; do not boast of what you have come to Paris to do, or have done; return with all speed; enter Marseilles at night, and your house by the back-door, and there remain, quiet, submissive, secret, and, above all, inoffensive; for this time, I swear to you, we shall act like powerful men who know their enemies. Go, my son—go, my dear Gérard, and by your obedience to my paternal orders, or, if you prefer it, friendly counsels, we will keep you in your place. This will be,” added Noirtier, with a smile, “one means by which you may a second time save me, if the political balance should some day take another turn, and cast you aloft while hurling me down. Adieu, my dear Gérard, and at your next journey alight at my door.”\n\nNoirtier left the room when he had finished, with the same calmness that had characterized him during the whole of this remarkable and trying conversation. Villefort, pale and agitated, ran to the window, put aside the curtain, and saw him pass, cool and collected, by two or three ill-looking men at the corner of the street, who were there, perhaps, to arrest a man with black whiskers, and a blue frock-coat, and hat with broad brim.\n\nVillefort stood watching, breathless, until his father had disappeared at the Rue Bussy. Then he turned to the various articles he had left behind him, put the black cravat and blue frock-coat at the bottom of the portmanteau, threw the hat into a dark closet, broke the cane into small bits and flung it in the fire, put on his travelling-cap, and calling his valet, checked with a look the thousand questions he was ready to ask, paid his bill, sprang into his carriage, which was ready, learned at Lyons that Bonaparte had entered Grenoble, and in the midst of the tumult which prevailed along the road, at length reached Marseilles, a prey to all the hopes and fears which enter into the heart of man with ambition and its first successes.\n\n# Chapter 13. The Hundred Days\n\nM. Noirtier was a true prophet, and things progressed rapidly, as he had predicted. Everyone knows the history of the famous return from Elba, a return which was unprecedented in the past, and will probably remain without a counterpart in the future.\n\nLouis XVIII. made but a faint attempt to parry this unexpected blow; the monarchy he had scarcely reconstructed tottered on its precarious foundation, and at a sign from the emperor the incongruous structure of ancient prejudices and new ideas fell to the ground. Villefort, therefore, gained nothing save the king’s gratitude (which was rather likely to injure him at the present time) and the cross of the Legion of Honor, which he had the prudence not to wear, although M. de Blacas had duly forwarded the brevet.\n\nNapoleon would, doubtless, have deprived Villefort of his office had it not been for Noirtier, who was all powerful at court, and thus the Girondin of ’93 and the Senator of 1806 protected him who so lately had been his protector. All Villefort’s influence barely enabled him to stifle the secret Dantès had so nearly divulged. The king’s procureur alone was deprived of his office, being suspected of royalism.\n\nHowever, scarcely was the imperial power established—that is, scarcely had the emperor re-entered the Tuileries and begun to issue orders from the closet into which we have introduced our readers,—he found on the table there Louis XVIII.’s half-filled snuff-box,—scarcely had this occurred when Marseilles began, in spite of the authorities, to rekindle the flames of civil war, always smouldering in the south, and it required but little to excite the populace to acts of far greater violence than the shouts and insults with which they assailed the royalists whenever they ventured abroad.\n0159m\n\nOwing to this change, the worthy shipowner became at that moment—we will not say all powerful, because Morrel was a prudent and rather a timid man, so much so, that many of the most zealous partisans of Bonaparte accused him of “moderation”—but sufficiently influential to make a demand in favor of Dantès.\n\nVillefort retained his place, but his marriage was put off until a more favorable opportunity. If the emperor remained on the throne, Gérard required a different alliance to aid his career; if Louis XVIII. returned, the influence of M. de Saint-Méran, like his own, could be vastly increased, and the marriage be still more suitable. The deputy procureur was, therefore, the first magistrate of Marseilles, when one morning his door opened, and M. Morrel was announced.\n\nAnyone else would have hastened to receive him; but Villefort was a man of ability, and he knew this would be a sign of weakness. He made Morrel wait in the antechamber, although he had no one with him, for the simple reason that the king’s procureur always makes everyone wait, and after passing a quarter of an hour in reading the papers, he ordered M. Morrel to be admitted.\n\nMorrel expected Villefort would be dejected; he found him as he had found him six weeks before, calm, firm, and full of that glacial politeness, that most insurmountable barrier which separates the well-bred from the vulgar man.\n\nHe had entered Villefort’s office expecting that the magistrate would tremble at the sight of him; on the contrary, he felt a cold shudder all over him when he saw Villefort sitting there with his elbow on his desk, and his head leaning on his hand. He stopped at the door; Villefort gazed at him as if he had some difficulty in recognizing him; then, after a brief interval, during which the honest shipowner turned his hat in his hands,\n\n“M. Morrel, I believe?” said Villefort.\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\n“Come nearer,” said the magistrate, with a patronizing wave of the hand, “and tell me to what circumstance I owe the honor of this visit.”\n\n“Do you not guess, monsieur?” asked Morrel.\n\n“Not in the least; but if I can serve you in any way I shall be delighted.”\n\n“Everything depends on you.”\n\n“Explain yourself, pray.”\n\n“Monsieur,” said Morrel, recovering his assurance as he proceeded, “do you recollect that a few days before the landing of his majesty the emperor, I came to intercede for a young man, the mate of my ship, who was accused of being concerned in correspondence with the Island of Elba? What was the other day a crime is today a title to favor. You then served Louis XVIII., and you did not show any favor—it was your duty; today you serve Napoleon, and you ought to protect him—it is equally your duty; I come, therefore, to ask what has become of him?”\n0161m\n\nVillefort by a strong effort sought to control himself. “What is his name?” said he. “Tell me his name.”\n\n“Edmond Dantès.”\n\nVillefort would probably have rather stood opposite the muzzle of a pistol at five-and-twenty paces than have heard this name spoken; but he did not blanch.\n\n“Dantès,” repeated he, “Edmond Dantès.”\n\n“Yes, monsieur.” Villefort opened a large register, then went to a table, from the table turned to his registers, and then, turning to Morrel,\n\n“Are you quite sure you are not mistaken, monsieur?” said he, in the most natural tone in the world.\n\nHad Morrel been a more quick-sighted man, or better versed in these matters, he would have been surprised at the king’s procureur answering him on such a subject, instead of referring him to the governors of the prison or the prefect of the department. But Morrel, disappointed in his expectations of exciting fear, was conscious only of the other’s condescension. Villefort had calculated rightly.\n\n“No,” said Morrel; “I am not mistaken. I have known him for ten years, the last four of which he was in my service. Do not you recollect, I came about six weeks ago to plead for clemency, as I come today to plead for justice. You received me very coldly. Oh, the royalists were very severe with the Bonapartists in those days.”\n\n“Monsieur,” returned Villefort, “I was then a royalist, because I believed the Bourbons not only the heirs to the throne, but the chosen of the nation. The miraculous return of Napoleon has conquered me, the legitimate monarch is he who is loved by his people.”\n\n“That’s right!” cried Morrel. “I like to hear you speak thus, and I augur well for Edmond from it.”\n\n“Wait a moment,” said Villefort, turning over the leaves of a register; “I have it—a sailor, who was about to marry a young Catalan girl. I recollect now; it was a very serious charge.”\n\n“How so?”\n\n“You know that when he left here he was taken to the Palais de Justice.”\n\n“Well?”\n\n“I made my report to the authorities at Paris, and a week after he was carried off.”\n\n“Carried off!” said Morrel. “What can they have done with him?”\n\n“Oh, he has been taken to Fenestrelles, to Pignerol, or to the Sainte-Marguérite islands. Some fine morning he will return to take command of your vessel.”\n\n“Come when he will, it shall be kept for him. But how is it he is not already returned? It seems to me the first care of government should be to set at liberty those who have suffered for their adherence to it.”\n\n“Do not be too hasty, M. Morrel,” replied Villefort. “The order of imprisonment came from high authority, and the order for his liberation must proceed from the same source; and, as Napoleon has scarcely been reinstated a fortnight, the letters have not yet been forwarded.”\n\n“But,” said Morrel, “is there no way of expediting all these formalities—of releasing him from arrest?”\n\n“There has been no arrest.”\n\n“How?”\n\n“It is sometimes essential to government to cause a man’s disappearance without leaving any traces, so that no written forms or documents may defeat their wishes.”\n\n“It might be so under the Bourbons, but at present——”\n\n“It has always been so, my dear Morrel, since the reign of Louis XIV. The emperor is more strict in prison discipline than even Louis himself, and the number of prisoners whose names are not on the register is incalculable.” Had Morrel even any suspicions, so much kindness would have dispelled them.\n\n“Well, M. de Villefort, how would you advise me to act?” asked he.\n\n“Petition the minister.”\n\n“Oh, I know what that is; the minister receives two hundred petitions every day, and does not read three.”\n\n“That is true; but he will read a petition countersigned and presented by me.”\n\n“And will you undertake to deliver it?”\n\n“With the greatest pleasure. Dantès was then guilty, and now he is innocent, and it is as much my duty to free him as it was to condemn him.” Villefort thus forestalled any danger of an inquiry, which, however improbable it might be, if it did take place would leave him defenceless.\n\n“But how shall I address the minister?”\n\n“Sit down there,” said Villefort, giving up his place to Morrel, “and write what I dictate.”\n\n“Will you be so good?”\n\n“Certainly. But lose no time; we have lost too much already.”\n\n“That is true. Only think what the poor fellow may even now be suffering.”\n\nVillefort shuddered at the suggestion; but he had gone too far to draw back. Dantès must be crushed to gratify Villefort’s ambition.\n\nVillefort dictated a petition, in which, from an excellent intention, no doubt, Dantès’ patriotic services were exaggerated, and he was made out one of the most active agents of Napoleon’s return. It was evident that at the sight of this document the minister would instantly release him. The petition finished, Villefort read it aloud.\n\n“That will do,” said he; “leave the rest to me.”\n\n“Will the petition go soon?”\n\n“Today.”\n\n“Countersigned by you?”\n\n“The best thing I can do will be to certify the truth of the contents of your petition.” And, sitting down, Villefort wrote the certificate at the bottom.\n\n“What more is to be done?”\n\n“I will do whatever is necessary.” This assurance delighted Morrel, who took leave of Villefort, and hastened to announce to old Dantès that he would soon see his son.\n\nAs for Villefort, instead of sending to Paris, he carefully preserved the petition that so fearfully compromised Dantès, in the hopes of an event that seemed not unlikely,—that is, a second restoration. Dantès remained a prisoner, and heard not the noise of the fall of Louis XVIII.’s throne, or the still more tragic destruction of the empire.\n\nTwice during the Hundred Days had Morrel renewed his demand, and twice had Villefort soothed him with promises. At last there was Waterloo, and Morrel came no more; he had done all that was in his power, and any fresh attempt would only compromise himself uselessly.\n\nLouis XVIII. remounted the throne; Villefort, to whom Marseilles had become filled with remorseful memories, sought and obtained the situation of king’s procureur at Toulouse, and a fortnight afterwards he married Mademoiselle de Saint-Méran, whose father now stood higher at court than ever.\n\nAnd so Dantès, after the Hundred Days and after Waterloo, remained in his dungeon, forgotten of earth and heaven.\n\nDanglars comprehended the full extent of the wretched fate that overwhelmed Dantès; and, when Napoleon returned to France, he, after the manner of mediocre minds, termed the coincidence, _a decree of Providence_. But when Napoleon returned to Paris, Danglars’ heart failed him, and he lived in constant fear of Dantès’ return on a mission of vengeance. He therefore informed M. Morrel of his wish to quit the sea, and obtained a recommendation from him to a Spanish merchant, into whose service he entered at the end of March, that is, ten or twelve days after Napoleon’s return. He then left for Madrid, and was no more heard of.\n\nFernand understood nothing except that Dantès was absent. What had become of him he cared not to inquire. Only, during the respite the absence of his rival afforded him, he reflected, partly on the means of deceiving Mercédès as to the cause of his absence, partly on plans of emigration and abduction, as from time to time he sat sad and motionless on the summit of Cape Pharo, at the spot from whence Marseilles and the Catalans are visible, watching for the apparition of a young and handsome man, who was for him also the messenger of vengeance. Fernand’s mind was made up; he would shoot Dantès, and then kill himself. But Fernand was mistaken; a man of his disposition never kills himself, for he constantly hopes.\n\nDuring this time the empire made its last conscription, and every man in France capable of bearing arms rushed to obey the summons of the emperor. Fernand departed with the rest, bearing with him the terrible thought that while he was away, his rival would perhaps return and marry Mercédès. Had Fernand really meant to kill himself, he would have done so when he parted from Mercédès. His devotion, and the compassion he showed for her misfortunes, produced the effect they always produce on noble minds—Mercédès had always had a sincere regard for Fernand, and this was now strengthened by gratitude.\n\n“My brother,” said she, as she placed his knapsack on his shoulders, “be careful of yourself, for if you are killed, I shall be alone in the world.” These words carried a ray of hope into Fernand’s heart. Should Dantès not return, Mercédès might one day be his.\n\n0165m\nMercédès was left alone face to face with the vast plain that had never seemed so barren, and the sea that had never seemed so vast. Bathed in tears she wandered about the Catalan village. Sometimes she stood mute and motionless as a statue, looking towards Marseilles, at other times gazing on the sea, and debating as to whether it were not better to cast herself into the abyss of the ocean, and thus end her woes. It was not want of courage that prevented her putting this resolution into execution; but her religious feelings came to her aid and saved her.\n\nCaderousse was, like Fernand, enrolled in the army, but, being married and eight years older, he was merely sent to the frontier. Old Dantès, who was only sustained by hope, lost all hope at Napoleon’s downfall. Five months after he had been separated from his son, and almost at the hour of his arrest, he breathed his last in Mercédès’ arms. M. Morrel paid the expenses of his funeral, and a few small debts the poor old man had contracted.\n\nThere was more than benevolence in this action; there was courage; the south was aflame, and to assist, even on his death-bed, the father of so dangerous a Bonapartist as Dantès, was stigmatized as a crime.\n\n# Chapter 14. The Two Prisoners\n\nA year after Louis XVIII.’s restoration, a visit was made by the inspector-general of prisons. Dantès in his cell heard the noise of preparation,—sounds that at the depth where he lay would have been inaudible to any but the ear of a prisoner, who could hear the splash of the drop of water that every hour fell from the roof of his dungeon. He guessed something uncommon was passing among the living; but he had so long ceased to have any intercourse with the world, that he looked upon himself as dead.\n\nThe inspector visited, one after another, the cells and dungeons of several of the prisoners, whose good behavior or stupidity recommended them to the clemency of the government. He inquired how they were fed, and if they had any request to make. The universal response was, that the fare was detestable, and that they wanted to be set free.\n\nThe inspector asked if they had anything else to ask for. They shook their heads. What could they desire beyond their liberty? The inspector turned smilingly to the governor.\n\n“I do not know what reason government can assign for these useless visits; when you see one prisoner, you see all,—always the same thing,—ill fed and innocent. Are there any others?”\n\n“Yes; the dangerous and mad prisoners are in the dungeons.”\n\n“Let us visit them,” said the inspector with an air of fatigue. “We must play the farce to the end. Let us see the dungeons.”\n\n“Let us first send for two soldiers,” said the governor. “The prisoners sometimes, through mere uneasiness of life, and in order to be sentenced to death, commit acts of useless violence, and you might fall a victim.”\n\n“Take all needful precautions,” replied the inspector.\n\nTwo soldiers were accordingly sent for, and the inspector descended a stairway, so foul, so humid, so dark, as to be loathsome to sight, smell, and respiration.\n\n“Oh,” cried the inspector, “who can live here?”\n\n“A most dangerous conspirator, a man we are ordered to keep the most strict watch over, as he is daring and resolute.”\n\n“He is alone?”\n\n“Certainly.”\n\n“How long has he been there?”\n\n“Nearly a year.”\n\n“Was he placed here when he first arrived?”\n\n“No; not until he attempted to kill the turnkey, who took his food to him.”\n\n“To kill the turnkey?”\n\n“Yes, the very one who is lighting us. Is it not true, Antoine?” asked the governor.\n\n“True enough; he wanted to kill me!” returned the turnkey.\n\n“He must be mad,” said the inspector.\n\n“He is worse than that,—he is a devil!” returned the turnkey.\n\n“Shall I complain of him?” demanded the inspector.\n\n“Oh, no; it is useless. Besides, he is almost mad now, and in another year he will be quite so.”\n\n“So much the better for him,—he will suffer less,” said the inspector. He was, as this remark shows, a man full of philanthropy, and in every way fit for his office.\n\n“You are right, sir,” replied the governor; “and this remark proves that you have deeply considered the subject. Now we have in a dungeon about twenty feet distant, and to which you descend by another stair, an old abbé, formerly leader of a party in Italy, who has been here since 1811, and in 1813 he went mad, and the change is astonishing. He used to weep, he now laughs; he grew thin, he now grows fat. You had better see him, for his madness is amusing.”\n\n“I will see them both,” returned the inspector; “I must conscientiously perform my duty.”\n\nThis was the inspector’s first visit; he wished to display his authority.\n\n“Let us visit this one first,” added he.\n\n“By all means,” replied the governor, and he signed to the turnkey to open the door. At the sound of the key turning in the lock, and the creaking of the hinges, Dantès, who was crouched in a corner of the dungeon, whence he could see the ray of light that came through a narrow iron grating above, raised his head. Seeing a stranger, escorted by two turnkeys holding torches and accompanied by two soldiers, and to whom the governor spoke bareheaded, Dantès, who guessed the truth, and that the moment to address himself to the superior authorities was come, sprang forward with clasped hands.\n\nThe soldiers interposed their bayonets, for they thought that he was about to attack the inspector, and the latter recoiled two or three steps. Dantès saw that he was looked upon as dangerous. Then, infusing all the humility he possessed into his eyes and voice, he addressed the inspector, and sought to inspire him with pity.\n\nThe inspector listened attentively; then, turning to the governor, observed, “He will become religious—he is already more gentle; he is afraid, and retreated before the bayonets—madmen are not afraid of anything; I made some curious observations on this at Charenton.” Then, turning to the prisoner, “What is it you want?” said he.\n\n“I want to know what crime I have committed—to be tried; and if I am guilty, to be shot; if innocent, to be set at liberty.”\n\n“Are you well fed?” said the inspector.\n\n“I believe so; I don’t know; it’s of no consequence. What matters really, not only to me, but to officers of justice and the king, is that an innocent man should languish in prison, the victim of an infamous denunciation, to die here cursing his executioners.”\n\n“You are very humble today,” remarked the governor; “you are not so always; the other day, for instance, when you tried to kill the turnkey.”\n\n“It is true, sir, and I beg his pardon, for he has always been very good to me, but I was mad.”\n\n“And you are not so any longer?”\n\n“No; captivity has subdued me—I have been here so long.”\n\n“So long?—when were you arrested, then?” asked the inspector.\n\n“The 28th of February, 1815, at half-past two in the afternoon.”\n\n“Today is the 30th of July, 1816,—why, it is but seventeen months.”\n\n“Only seventeen months,” replied Dantès. “Oh, you do not know what is seventeen months in prison!—seventeen ages rather, especially to a man who, like me, had arrived at the summit of his ambition—to a man, who, like me, was on the point of marrying a woman he adored, who saw an honorable career opened before him, and who loses all in an instant—who sees his prospects destroyed, and is ignorant of the fate of his affianced wife, and whether his aged father be still living! Seventeen months’ captivity to a sailor accustomed to the boundless ocean, is a worse punishment than human crime ever merited. Have pity on me, then, and ask for me, not intelligence, but a trial; not pardon, but a verdict—a trial, sir, I ask only for a trial; that, surely, cannot be denied to one who is accused!”\n\n“We shall see,” said the inspector; then, turning to the governor, “On my word, the poor devil touches me. You must show me the proofs against him.”\n\n“Certainly; but you will find terrible charges.”\n\n“Monsieur,” continued Dantès, “I know it is not in your power to release me; but you can plead for me—you can have me tried—and that is all I ask. Let me know my crime, and the reason why I was condemned. Uncertainty is worse than all.”\n\n“Go on with the lights,” said the inspector.\n\n“Monsieur,” cried Dantès, “I can tell by your voice you are touched with pity; tell me at least to hope.”\n\n“I cannot tell you that,” replied the inspector; “I can only promise to examine into your case.”\n\n“Oh, I am free—then I am saved!”\n\n“Who arrested you?”\n\n“M. Villefort. See him, and hear what he says.”\n\n“M. Villefort is no longer at Marseilles; he is now at Toulouse.”\n\n“I am no longer surprised at my detention,” murmured Dantès, “since my only protector is removed.”\n\n“Had M. de Villefort any cause of personal dislike to you?”\n\n“None; on the contrary, he was very kind to me.”\n\n“I can, then, rely on the notes he has left concerning you?”\n\n“Entirely.”\n\n“That is well; wait patiently, then.”\n\nDantès fell on his knees, and prayed earnestly. The door closed; but this time a fresh inmate was left with Dantès—Hope.\n0173m\n\n“Will you see the register at once,” asked the governor, “or proceed to the other cell?”\n\n“Let us visit them all,” said the inspector. “If I once went up those stairs. I should never have the courage to come down again.”\n\n“Ah, this one is not like the other, and his madness is less affecting than this one’s display of reason.”\n\n“What is his folly?”\n\n“He fancies he possesses an immense treasure. The first year he offered government a million of francs for his release; the second, two; the third, three; and so on progressively. He is now in his fifth year of captivity; he will ask to speak to you in private, and offer you five millions.”\n\n“How curious!—what is his name?”\n\n“The Abbé Faria.”\n\n“No. 27,” said the inspector.\n\n“It is here; unlock the door, Antoine.”\n\nThe turnkey obeyed, and the inspector gazed curiously into the chamber of the _mad abbé_, as the prisoner was usually called.\n\nIn the centre of the cell, in a circle traced with a fragment of plaster detached from the wall, sat a man whose tattered garments scarcely covered him. He was drawing in this circle geometrical lines, and seemed as much absorbed in his problem as Archimedes was when the soldier of Marcellus slew him. He did not move at the sound of the door, and continued his calculations until the flash of the torches lighted up with an unwonted glare the sombre walls of his cell; then, raising his head, he perceived with astonishment the number of persons present. He hastily seized the coverlet of his bed, and wrapped it round him.\n\n“What is it you want?” said the inspector.\n\n“I, monsieur,” replied the abbé with an air of surprise,—“I want nothing.”\n\n“You do not understand,” continued the inspector; “I am sent here by government to visit the prison, and hear the requests of the prisoners.”\n\n“Oh, that is different,” cried the abbé; “and we shall understand each other, I hope.”\n\n“There, now,” whispered the governor, “it is just as I told you.”\n\n“Monsieur,” continued the prisoner, “I am the Abbé Faria, born at Rome. I was for twenty years Cardinal Spada’s secretary; I was arrested, why, I know not, toward the beginning of the year 1811; since then I have demanded my liberty from the Italian and French government.”\n\n“Why from the French government?”\n\n“Because I was arrested at Piombino, and I presume that, like Milan and Florence, Piombino has become the capital of some French department.”\n\n“Ah,” said the inspector, “you have not the latest news from Italy?”\n\n“My information dates from the day on which I was arrested,” returned the Abbé Faria; “and as the emperor had created the kingdom of Rome for his infant son, I presume that he has realized the dream of Machiavelli and Cæsar Borgia, which was to make Italy a united kingdom.”\n\n“Monsieur,” returned the inspector, “Providence has changed this gigantic plan you advocate so warmly.”\n\n“It is the only means of rendering Italy strong, happy, and independent.”\n\n“Very possibly; only I am not come to discuss politics, but to inquire if you have anything to ask or to complain of.”\n\n“The food is the same as in other prisons,—that is, very bad; the lodging is very unhealthful, but, on the whole, passable for a dungeon; but it is not that which I wish to speak of, but a secret I have to reveal of the greatest importance.”\n\n“We are coming to the point,” whispered the governor.\n\n“It is for that reason I am delighted to see you,” continued the abbé, “although you have disturbed me in a most important calculation, which, if it succeeded, would possibly change Newton’s system. Could you allow me a few words in private.”\n\n“What did I tell you?” said the governor.\n\n“You knew him,” returned the inspector with a smile.\n\n“What you ask is impossible, monsieur,” continued he, addressing Faria.\n\n0175m\n“But,” said the abbé, “I would speak to you of a large sum, amounting to five millions.”\n\n“The very sum you named,” whispered the inspector in his turn.\n\n“However,” continued Faria, seeing that the inspector was about to depart, “it is not absolutely necessary for us to be alone; the governor can be present.”\n\n“Unfortunately,” said the governor, “I know beforehand what you are about to say; it concerns your treasures, does it not?” Faria fixed his eyes on him with an expression that would have convinced anyone else of his sanity.\n\n“Of course,” said he; “of what else should I speak?”\n\n“Mr. Inspector,” continued the governor, “I can tell you the story as well as he, for it has been dinned in my ears for the last four or five years.”\n\n“That proves,” returned the abbé, “that you are like those of Holy Writ, who having eyes see not, and having ears hear not.”\n\n“My dear sir, the government is rich and does not want your treasures,” replied the inspector; “keep them until you are liberated.” The abbé’s eyes glistened; he seized the inspector’s hand.\n\n“But what if I am not liberated,” cried he, “and am detained here until my death? this treasure will be lost. Had not government better profit by it? I will offer six millions, and I will content myself with the rest, if they will only give me my liberty.”\n\n“On my word,” said the inspector in a low tone, “had I not been told beforehand that this man was mad, I should believe what he says.”\n\n“I am not mad,” replied Faria, with that acuteness of hearing peculiar to prisoners. “The treasure I speak of really exists, and I offer to sign an agreement with you, in which I promise to lead you to the spot where you shall dig; and if I deceive you, bring me here again,—I ask no more.”\n\nThe governor laughed. “Is the spot far from here?”\n\n“A hundred leagues.”\n\n“It is not ill-planned,” said the governor. “If all the prisoners took it into their heads to travel a hundred leagues, and their guardians consented to accompany them, they would have a capital chance of escaping.”\n\n“The scheme is well known,” said the inspector; “and the abbé’s plan has not even the merit of originality.”\nThen turning to Faria, “I inquired if you are well fed?” said he.\n\n“Swear to me,” replied Faria, “to free me if what I tell you prove true, and I will stay here while you go to the spot.”\n\n“Are you well fed?” repeated the inspector.\n\n“Monsieur, you run no risk, for, as I told you, I will stay here; so there is no chance of my escaping.”\n\n“You do not reply to my question,” replied the inspector impatiently.\n\n“Nor you to mine,” cried the abbé. “You will not accept my gold; I will keep it for myself. You refuse me my liberty; God will give it me.” And the abbé, casting away his coverlet, resumed his place, and continued his calculations.\n0177m\n\n“What is he doing there?” said the inspector.\n\n“Counting his treasures,” replied the governor.\n\nFaria replied to this sarcasm with a glance of profound contempt. They went out. The turnkey closed the door behind them.\n\n“He was wealthy once, perhaps?” said the inspector.\n\n“Or dreamed he was, and awoke mad.”\n\n“After all,” said the inspector, “if he had been rich, he would not have been here.”\n\nSo the matter ended for the Abbé Faria. He remained in his cell, and this visit only increased the belief in his insanity.\n\nCaligula or Nero, those treasure-seekers, those desirers of the impossible, would have accorded to the poor wretch, in exchange for his wealth, the liberty he so earnestly prayed for. But the kings of modern times, restrained by the limits of mere probability, have neither courage nor desire. They fear the ear that hears their orders, and the eye that scrutinizes their actions. Formerly they believed themselves sprung from Jupiter, and shielded by their birth; but nowadays they are not inviolable.\n\nIt has always been against the policy of despotic governments to suffer the victims of their persecutions to reappear. As the Inquisition rarely allowed its victims to be seen with their limbs distorted and their flesh lacerated by torture, so madness is always concealed in its cell, from whence, should it depart, it is conveyed to some gloomy hospital, where the doctor has no thought for man or mind in the mutilated being the jailer delivers to him. The very madness of the Abbé Faria, gone mad in prison, condemned him to perpetual captivity.\n\nThe inspector kept his word with Dantès; he examined the register, and found the following note concerning him:\n\n_Edmond Dantès:_\n\nViolent Bonapartist; took an active part in the return from Elba.\n\nThe greatest watchfulness and care to be exercised.\n\nThis note was in a different hand from the rest, which showed that it had been added since his confinement. The inspector could not contend against this accusation; he simply wrote, _Nothing to be done._\n\nThis visit had infused new vigor into Dantès; he had, till then, forgotten the date; but now, with a fragment of plaster, he wrote the date, 30th July, 1816, and made a mark every day, in order not to lose his reckoning again. Days and weeks passed away, then months—Dantès still waited; he at first expected to be freed in a fortnight. This fortnight expired, he decided that the inspector would do nothing until his return to Paris, and that he would not reach there until his circuit was finished, he therefore fixed three months; three months passed away, then six more. Finally ten months and a half had gone by and no favorable change had taken place, and Dantès began to fancy the inspector’s visit but a dream, an illusion of the brain.\n\nAt the expiration of a year the governor was transferred; he had obtained charge of the fortress at Ham. He took with him several of his subordinates, and amongst them Dantès’ jailer. A new governor arrived; it would have been too tedious to acquire the names of the prisoners; he learned their numbers instead. This horrible place contained fifty cells; their inhabitants were designated by the numbers of their cell, and the unhappy young man was no longer called Edmond Dantès—he was now number 34.\n\n# Chapter 15. Number 34 and Number 27\n\nDantès passed through all the stages of torture natural to prisoners in suspense. He was sustained at first by that pride of conscious innocence which is the sequence to hope; then he began to doubt his own innocence, which justified in some measure the governor’s belief in his mental alienation; and then, relaxing his sentiment of pride, he addressed his supplications, not to God, but to man. God is always the last resource. Unfortunates, who ought to begin with God, do not have any hope in him till they have exhausted all other means of deliverance.\n\nDantès asked to be removed from his present dungeon into another, even if it were darker and deeper, for a change, however disadvantageous, was still a change, and would afford him some amusement. He entreated to be allowed to walk about, to have fresh air, books, and writing materials. His requests were not granted, but he went on asking all the same. He accustomed himself to speaking to the new jailer, although the latter was, if possible, more taciturn than the old one; but still, to speak to a man, even though mute, was something. Dantès spoke for the sake of hearing his own voice; he had tried to speak when alone, but the sound of his voice terrified him.\n\nOften, before his captivity, Dantès’ mind had revolted at the idea of assemblages of prisoners, made up of thieves, vagabonds, and murderers. He now wished to be amongst them, in order to see some other face besides that of his jailer; he sighed for the galleys, with the infamous costume, the chain, and the brand on the shoulder. The galley-slaves breathed the fresh air of heaven, and saw each other. They were very happy.\n\nHe besought the jailer one day to let him have a companion, were it even the mad abbé. The jailer, though rough and hardened by the constant sight of so much suffering, was yet a man. At the bottom of his heart he had often had a feeling of pity for this unhappy young man who suffered so; and he laid the request of number 34 before the governor; but the latter sapiently imagined that Dantès wished to conspire or attempt an escape, and refused his request. Dantès had exhausted all human resources, and he then turned to God.\n\nAll the pious ideas that had been so long forgotten, returned; he recollected the prayers his mother had taught him, and discovered a new meaning in every word; for in prosperity prayers seem but a mere medley of words, until misfortune comes and the unhappy sufferer first understands the meaning of the sublime language in which he invokes the pity of heaven! He prayed, and prayed aloud, no longer terrified at the sound of his own voice, for he fell into a sort of ecstasy. He laid every action of his life before the Almighty, proposed tasks to accomplish, and at the end of every prayer introduced the entreaty oftener addressed to man than to God: “Forgive us our trespasses as we forgive them that trespass against us.” Yet in spite of his earnest prayers, Dantès remained a prisoner.\n\nThen gloom settled heavily upon him. Dantès was a man of great simplicity of thought, and without education; he could not, therefore, in the solitude of his dungeon, traverse in mental vision the history of the ages, bring to life the nations that had perished, and rebuild the ancient cities so vast and stupendous in the light of the imagination, and that pass before the eye glowing with celestial colors in Martin’s Babylonian pictures. He could not do this, he whose past life was so short, whose present so melancholy, and his future so doubtful. Nineteen years of light to reflect upon in eternal darkness! No distraction could come to his aid; his energetic spirit, that would have exalted in thus revisiting the past, was imprisoned like an eagle in a cage. He clung to one idea—that of his happiness, destroyed, without apparent cause, by an unheard-of fatality; he considered and reconsidered this idea, devoured it (so to speak), as the implacable Ugolino devours the skull of Archbishop Roger in the Inferno of Dante.\n\nRage supplanted religious fervor. Dantès uttered blasphemies that made his jailer recoil with horror, dashed himself furiously against the walls of his prison, wreaked his anger upon everything, and chiefly upon himself, so that the least thing,—a grain of sand, a straw, or a breath of air that annoyed him, led to paroxysms of fury. Then the letter that Villefort had showed to him recurred to his mind, and every line gleamed forth in fiery letters on the wall like the _mene, mene, tekel upharsin_ of Belshazzar. He told himself that it was the enmity of man, and not the vengeance of Heaven, that had thus plunged him into the deepest misery. He consigned his unknown persecutors to the most horrible tortures he could imagine, and found them all insufficient, because after torture came death, and after death, if not repose, at least the boon of unconsciousness.\n\nBy dint of constantly dwelling on the idea that tranquillity was death, and if punishment were the end in view other tortures than death must be invented, he began to reflect on suicide. Unhappy he, who, on the brink of misfortune, broods over ideas like these!\n\nBefore him is a dead sea that stretches in azure calm before the eye; but he who unwarily ventures within its embrace finds himself struggling with a monster that would drag him down to perdition. Once thus ensnared, unless the protecting hand of God snatch him thence, all is over, and his struggles but tend to hasten his destruction. This state of mental anguish is, however, less terrible than the sufferings that precede or the punishment that possibly will follow. There is a sort of consolation at the contemplation of the yawning abyss, at the bottom of which lie darkness and obscurity.\n\nEdmond found some solace in these ideas. All his sorrows, all his sufferings, with their train of gloomy spectres, fled from his cell when the angel of death seemed about to enter. Dantès reviewed his past life with composure, and, looking forward with terror to his future existence, chose that middle line that seemed to afford him a refuge.\n\n“Sometimes,” said he, “in my voyages, when I was a man and commanded other men, I have seen the heavens overcast, the sea rage and foam, the storm arise, and, like a monstrous bird, beating the two horizons with its wings. Then I felt that my vessel was a vain refuge, that trembled and shook before the tempest. Soon the fury of the waves and the sight of the sharp rocks announced the approach of death, and death then terrified me, and I used all my skill and intelligence as a man and a sailor to struggle against the wrath of God. But I did so because I was happy, because I had not courted death, because to be cast upon a bed of rocks and seaweed seemed terrible, because I was unwilling that I, a creature made for the service of God, should serve for food to the gulls and ravens. But now it is different; I have lost all that bound me to life, death smiles and invites me to repose; I die after my own manner, I die exhausted and broken-spirited, as I fall asleep when I have paced three thousand times round my cell,—that is thirty thousand steps, or about ten leagues.”\n\nNo sooner had this idea taken possession of him than he became more composed, arranged his couch to the best of his power, ate little and slept less, and found existence almost supportable, because he felt that he could throw it off at pleasure, like a worn-out garment. Two methods of self-destruction were at his disposal. He could hang himself with his handkerchief to the window bars, or refuse food and die of starvation. But the first was repugnant to him. Dantès had always entertained the greatest horror of pirates, who are hung up to the yard-arm; he would not die by what seemed an infamous death. He resolved to adopt the second, and began that day to carry out his resolve.\n0185m\n\nNearly four years had passed away; at the end of the second he had ceased to mark the lapse of time. Dantès said, “I wish to die,” and had chosen the manner of his death, and fearful of changing his mind, he had taken an oath to die. “When my morning and evening meals are brought,” thought he, “I will cast them out of the window, and they will think that I have eaten them.”\n\nHe kept his word; twice a day he cast out, through the barred aperture, the provisions his jailer brought him—at first gayly, then with deliberation, and at last with regret. Nothing but the recollection of his oath gave him strength to proceed. Hunger made viands once repugnant, now acceptable; he held the plate in his hand for an hour at a time, and gazed thoughtfully at the morsel of bad meat, of tainted fish, of black and mouldy bread. It was the last yearning for life contending with the resolution of despair; then his dungeon seemed less sombre, his prospects less desperate. He was still young—he was only four or five-and-twenty—he had nearly fifty years to live. What unforseen events might not open his prison door, and restore him to liberty? Then he raised to his lips the repast that, like a voluntary Tantalus, he refused himself; but he thought of his oath, and he would not break it. He persisted until, at last, he had not sufficient strength to rise and cast his supper out of the loophole. The next morning he could not see or hear; the jailer feared he was dangerously ill. Edmond hoped he was dying.\n\nThus the day passed away. Edmond felt a sort of stupor creeping over him which brought with it a feeling almost of content; the gnawing pain at his stomach had ceased; his thirst had abated; when he closed his eyes he saw myriads of lights dancing before them like the will-o’-the-wisps that play about the marshes. It was the twilight of that mysterious country called Death!\n\nSuddenly, about nine o’clock in the evening, Edmond heard a hollow sound in the wall against which he was lying.\n\nSo many loathsome animals inhabited the prison, that their noise did not, in general, awake him; but whether abstinence had quickened his faculties, or whether the noise was really louder than usual, Edmond raised his head and listened. It was a continual scratching, as if made by a huge claw, a powerful tooth, or some iron instrument attacking the stones.\n\nAlthough weakened, the young man’s brain instantly responded to the idea that haunts all prisoners—liberty! It seemed to him that heaven had at length taken pity on him, and had sent this noise to warn him on the very brink of the abyss. Perhaps one of those beloved ones he had so often thought of was thinking of him, and striving to diminish the distance that separated them.\n\nNo, no, doubtless he was deceived, and it was but one of those dreams that forerun death!\n\nEdmond still heard the sound. It lasted nearly three hours; he then heard a noise of something falling, and all was silent.\n\nSome hours afterwards it began again, nearer and more distinct. Edmond was intensely interested. Suddenly the jailer entered.\n\nFor a week since he had resolved to die, and during the four days that he had been carrying out his purpose, Edmond had not spoken to the attendant, had not answered him when he inquired what was the matter with him, and turned his face to the wall when he looked too curiously at him; but now the jailer might hear the noise and put an end to it, and so destroy a ray of something like hope that soothed his last moments.\n\nThe jailer brought him his breakfast. Dantès raised himself up and began to talk about everything; about the bad quality of the food, about the coldness of his dungeon, grumbling and complaining, in order to have an excuse for speaking louder, and wearying the patience of his jailer, who out of kindness of heart had brought broth and white bread for his prisoner.\n\nFortunately, he fancied that Dantès was delirious; and placing the food on the rickety table, he withdrew. Edmond listened, and the sound became more and more distinct.\n\n“There can be no doubt about it,” thought he; “it is some prisoner who is striving to obtain his freedom. Oh, if I were only there to help him!”\n\nSuddenly another idea took possession of his mind, so used to misfortune, that it was scarcely capable of hope—the idea that the noise was made by workmen the governor had ordered to repair the neighboring dungeon.\n\nIt was easy to ascertain this; but how could he risk the question? It was easy to call his jailer’s attention to the noise, and watch his countenance as he listened; but might he not by this means destroy hopes far more important than the short-lived satisfaction of his own curiosity? Unfortunately, Edmond’s brain was still so feeble that he could not bend his thoughts to anything in particular. He saw but one means of restoring lucidity and clearness to his judgment. He turned his eyes towards the soup which the jailer had brought, rose, staggered towards it, raised the vessel to his lips, and drank off the contents with a feeling of indescribable pleasure.\n\nHe had the resolution to stop with this. He had often heard that shipwrecked persons had died through having eagerly devoured too much food. Edmond replaced on the table the bread he was about to devour, and returned to his couch—he did not wish to die. He soon felt that his ideas became again collected—he could think, and strengthen his thoughts by reasoning. Then he said to himself:\n\n“I must put this to the test, but without compromising anybody. If it is a workman, I need but knock against the wall, and he will cease to work, in order to find out who is knocking, and why he does so; but as his occupation is sanctioned by the governor, he will soon resume it. If, on the contrary, it is a prisoner, the noise I make will alarm him, he will cease, and not begin again until he thinks everyone is asleep.”\n\nEdmond rose again, but this time his legs did not tremble, and his sight was clear; he went to a corner of his dungeon, detached a stone, and with it knocked against the wall where the sound came. He struck thrice.\n\nAt the first blow the sound ceased, as if by magic.\n\nEdmond listened intently; an hour passed, two hours passed, and no sound was heard from the wall—all was silent there.\n\nFull of hope, Edmond swallowed a few mouthfuls of bread and water, and, thanks to the vigor of his constitution, found himself well-nigh recovered.\n\nThe day passed away in utter silence—night came without recurrence of the noise.\n\n“It is a prisoner,” said Edmond joyfully. His brain was on fire, and life and energy returned.\n\nThe night passed in perfect silence. Edmond did not close his eyes.\n\nIn the morning the jailer brought him fresh provisions—he had already devoured those of the previous day; he ate these listening anxiously for the sound, walking round and round his cell, shaking the iron bars of the loophole, restoring vigor and agility to his limbs by exercise, and so preparing himself for his future destiny. At intervals he listened to learn if the noise had not begun again, and grew impatient at the prudence of the prisoner, who did not guess he had been disturbed by a captive as anxious for liberty as himself.\n\nThree days passed—seventy-two long tedious hours which he counted off by minutes!\n\nAt length one evening, as the jailer was visiting him for the last time that night, Dantès, with his ear for the hundredth time at the wall, fancied he heard an almost imperceptible movement among the stones. He moved away, walked up and down his cell to collect his thoughts, and then went back and listened.\n\nThe matter was no longer doubtful. Something was at work on the other side of the wall; the prisoner had discovered the danger, and had substituted a lever for a chisel.\n\nEncouraged by this discovery, Edmond determined to assist the indefatigable laborer. He began by moving his bed, and looked around for anything with which he could pierce the wall, penetrate the moist cement, and displace a stone.\n\nHe saw nothing, he had no knife or sharp instrument, the window grating was of iron, but he had too often assured himself of its solidity. All his furniture consisted of a bed, a chair, a table, a pail, and a jug. The bed had iron clamps, but they were screwed to the wood, and it would have required a screw-driver to take them off. The table and chair had nothing, the pail had once possessed a handle, but that had been removed.\n0189m\n\nDantès had but one resource, which was to break the jug, and with one of the sharp fragments attack the wall. He let the jug fall on the floor, and it broke in pieces.\n\nDantès concealed two or three of the sharpest fragments in his bed, leaving the rest on the floor. The breaking of his jug was too natural an accident to excite suspicion. Edmond had all the night to work in, but in the darkness he could not do much, and he soon felt that he was working against something very hard; he pushed back his bed, and waited for day.\n\nAll night he heard the subterranean workman, who continued to mine his way. Day came, the jailer entered. Dantès told him that the jug had fallen from his hands while he was drinking, and the jailer went grumblingly to fetch another, without giving himself the trouble to remove the fragments of the broken one. He returned speedily, advised the prisoner to be more careful, and departed.\n\nDantès heard joyfully the key grate in the lock; he listened until the sound of steps died away, and then, hastily displacing his bed, saw by the faint light that penetrated into his cell, that he had labored uselessly the previous evening in attacking the stone instead of removing the plaster that surrounded it.\n\nThe damp had rendered it friable, and Dantès was able to break it off—in small morsels, it is true, but at the end of half an hour he had scraped off a handful; a mathematician might have calculated that in two years, supposing that the rock was not encountered, a passage twenty feet long and two feet broad, might be formed.\n\nThe prisoner reproached himself with not having thus employed the hours he had passed in vain hopes, prayer, and despondency. During the six years that he had been imprisoned, what might he not have accomplished?\n\nThis idea imparted new energy, and in three days he had succeeded, with the utmost precaution, in removing the cement, and exposing the stone-work. The wall was built of rough stones, among which, to give strength to the structure, blocks of hewn stone were at intervals imbedded. It was one of these he had uncovered, and which he must remove from its socket.\n\nDantès strove to do this with his nails, but they were too weak. The fragments of the jug broke, and after an hour of useless toil, Dantès paused with anguish on his brow.\n\nWas he to be thus stopped at the beginning, and was he to wait inactive until his fellow workman had completed his task? Suddenly an idea occurred to him—he smiled, and the perspiration dried on his forehead.\n\nThe jailer always brought Dantès’ soup in an iron saucepan; this saucepan contained soup for both prisoners, for Dantès had noticed that it was either quite full, or half empty, according as the turnkey gave it to him or to his companion first.\n\nThe handle of this saucepan was of iron; Dantès would have given ten years of his life in exchange for it.\n\n0191m\nThe jailer was accustomed to pour the contents of the saucepan into Dantès’ plate, and Dantès, after eating his soup with a wooden spoon, washed the plate, which thus served for every day. Now when evening came Dantès put his plate on the ground near the door; the jailer, as he entered, stepped on it and broke it.\n\nThis time he could not blame Dantès. He was wrong to leave it there, but the jailer was wrong not to have looked before him. The jailer, therefore, only grumbled. Then he looked about for something to pour the soup into; Dantès’ entire dinner service consisted of one plate—there was no alternative.\n\n“Leave the saucepan,” said Dantès; “you can take it away when you bring me my breakfast.”\n\nThis advice was to the jailer’s taste, as it spared him the necessity of making another trip. He left the saucepan.\n\nDantès was beside himself with joy. He rapidly devoured his food, and after waiting an hour, lest the jailer should change his mind and return, he removed his bed, took the handle of the saucepan, inserted the point between the hewn stone and rough stones of the wall, and employed it as a lever. A slight oscillation showed Dantès that all went well. At the end of an hour the stone was extricated from the wall, leaving a cavity a foot and a half in diameter.\n\nDantès carefully collected the plaster, carried it into the corner of his cell, and covered it with earth. Then, wishing to make the best use of his time while he had the means of labor, he continued to work without ceasing. At the dawn of day he replaced the stone, pushed his bed against the wall, and lay down. The breakfast consisted of a piece of bread; the jailer entered and placed the bread on the table.\n\n“Well, don’t you intend to bring me another plate?” said Dantès.\n\n“No,” replied the turnkey; “you destroy everything. First you break your jug, then you make me break your plate; if all the prisoners followed your example, the government would be ruined. I shall leave you the saucepan, and pour your soup into that. So for the future I hope you will not be so destructive.”\n\nDantès raised his eyes to heaven and clasped his hands beneath the coverlet. He felt more gratitude for the possession of this piece of iron than he had ever felt for anything. He had noticed, however, that the prisoner on the other side had ceased to labor; no matter, this was a greater reason for proceeding—if his neighbor would not come to him, he would go to his neighbor. All day he toiled on untiringly, and by the evening he had succeeded in extracting ten handfuls of plaster and fragments of stone. When the hour for his jailer’s visit arrived, Dantès straightened the handle of the saucepan as well as he could, and placed it in its accustomed place. The turnkey poured his ration of soup into it, together with the fish—for thrice a week the prisoners were deprived of meat. This would have been a method of reckoning time, had not Dantès long ceased to do so. Having poured out the soup, the turnkey retired.\n\nDantès wished to ascertain whether his neighbor had really ceased to work. He listened—all was silent, as it had been for the last three days. Dantès sighed; it was evident that his neighbor distrusted him. However, he toiled on all the night without being discouraged; but after two or three hours he encountered an obstacle. The iron made no impression, but met with a smooth surface; Dantès touched it, and found that it was a beam. This beam crossed, or rather blocked up, the hole Dantès had made; it was necessary, therefore, to dig above or under it. The unhappy young man had not thought of this.\n\n“Oh, my God, my God!” murmured he, “I have so earnestly prayed to you, that I hoped my prayers had been heard. After having deprived me of my liberty, after having deprived me of death, after having recalled me to existence, my God, have pity on me, and do not let me die in despair!”\n0193m\n\n“Who talks of God and despair at the same time?” said a voice that seemed to come from beneath the earth, and, deadened by the distance, sounded hollow and sepulchral in the young man’s ears. Edmond’s hair stood on end, and he rose to his knees.\n\n“Ah,” said he, “I hear a human voice.” Edmond had not heard anyone speak save his jailer for four or five years; and a jailer is no man to a prisoner—he is a living door, a barrier of flesh and blood adding strength to restraints of oak and iron.\n\n“In the name of Heaven,” cried Dantès, “speak again, though the sound of your voice terrifies me. Who are you?”\n\n“Who are you?” said the voice.\n\n“An unhappy prisoner,” replied Dantès, who made no hesitation in answering.\n\n“Of what country?”\n\n“A Frenchman.”\n\n“Your name?”\n\n“Edmond Dantès.”\n\n“Your profession?”\n\n“A sailor.”\n\n“How long have you been here?”\n\n“Since the 28th of February, 1815.”\n\n“Your crime?”\n\n“I am innocent.”\n\n“But of what are you accused?”\n\n“Of having conspired to aid the emperor’s return.”\n\n“What! For the emperor’s return?—the emperor is no longer on the throne, then?”\n\n“He abdicated at Fontainebleau in 1814, and was sent to the Island of Elba. But how long have you been here that you are ignorant of all this?”\n\n“Since 1811.”\n\nDantès shuddered; this man had been four years longer than himself in prison.\n\n“Do not dig any more,” said the voice; “only tell me how high up is your excavation?”\n\n“On a level with the floor.”\n\n“How is it concealed?”\n\n“Behind my bed.”\n\n“Has your bed been moved since you have been a prisoner?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“What does your chamber open on?”\n\n“A corridor.”\n\n“And the corridor?”\n\n“On a court.”\n\n“Alas!” murmured the voice.\n\n“Oh, what is the matter?” cried Dantès.\n\n“I have made a mistake owing to an error in my plans. I took the wrong angle, and have come out fifteen feet from where I intended. I took the wall you are mining for the outer wall of the fortress.”\n\n“But then you would be close to the sea?”\n\n“That is what I hoped.”\n\n“And supposing you had succeeded?”\n\n“I should have thrown myself into the sea, gained one of the islands near here—the Isle de Daume or the Isle de Tiboulen—and then I should have been safe.”\n\n“Could you have swum so far?”\n\n“Heaven would have given me strength; but now all is lost.”\n“All?”\n\n“Yes; stop up your excavation carefully, do not work any more, and wait until you hear from me.”\n\n“Tell me, at least, who you are?”\n\n“I am—I am No. 27.”\n\n“You mistrust me, then,” said Dantès. Edmond fancied he heard a bitter laugh resounding from the depths.\n\n“Oh, I am a Christian,” cried Dantès, guessing instinctively that this man meant to abandon him. “I swear to you by him who died for us that naught shall induce me to breathe one syllable to my jailers; but I conjure you do not abandon me. If you do, I swear to you, for I have got to the end of my strength, that I will dash my brains out against the wall, and you will have my death to reproach yourself with.”\n\n“How old are you? Your voice is that of a young man.”\n\n“I do not know my age, for I have not counted the years I have been here. All I do know is, that I was just nineteen when I was arrested, the 28th of February, 1815.”\n\n“Not quite twenty-six!” murmured the voice; “at that age he cannot be a traitor.”\n\n“Oh, no, no,” cried Dantès. “I swear to you again, rather than betray you, I would allow myself to be hacked in pieces!”\n\n“You have done well to speak to me, and ask for my assistance, for I was about to form another plan, and leave you; but your age reassures me. I will not forget you. Wait.”\n\n“How long?”\n\n“I must calculate our chances; I will give you the signal.”\n\n“But you will not leave me; you will come to me, or you will let me come to you. We will escape, and if we cannot escape we will talk; you of those whom you love, and I of those whom I love. You must love somebody?”\n\n“No, I am alone in the world.”\n\n“Then you will love me. If you are young, I will be your comrade; if you are old, I will be your son. I have a father who is seventy if he yet lives; I only love him and a young girl called Mercédès. My father has not yet forgotten me, I am sure, but God alone knows if she loves me still; I shall love you as I loved my father.”\n\n“It is well,” returned the voice; “tomorrow.”\n\nThese few words were uttered with an accent that left no doubt of his sincerity; Dantès rose, dispersed the fragments with the same precaution as before, and pushed his bed back against the wall. He then gave himself up to his happiness. He would no longer be alone. He was, perhaps, about to regain his liberty; at the worst, he would have a companion, and captivity that is shared is but half captivity. Plaints made in common are almost prayers, and prayers where two or three are gathered together invoke the mercy of heaven.\n\nAll day Dantès walked up and down his cell. He sat down occasionally on his bed, pressing his hand on his heart. At the slightest noise he bounded towards the door. Once or twice the thought crossed his mind that he might be separated from this unknown, whom he loved already; and then his mind was made up—when the jailer moved his bed and stooped to examine the opening, he would kill him with his water jug. He would be condemned to die, but he was about to die of grief and despair when this miraculous noise recalled him to life.\n\nThe jailer came in the evening. Dantès was on his bed. It seemed to him that thus he better guarded the unfinished opening. Doubtless there was a strange expression in his eyes, for the jailer said, “Come, are you going mad again?”\n\nDantès did not answer; he feared that the emotion of his voice would betray him. The jailer went away shaking his head. Night came; Dantès hoped that his neighbor would profit by the silence to address him, but he was mistaken. The next morning, however, just as he removed his bed from the wall, he heard three knocks; he threw himself on his knees.\n\n“Is it you?” said he; “I am here.”\n\n“Is your jailer gone?”\n\n“Yes,” said Dantès; “he will not return until the evening; so that we have twelve hours before us.”\n\n“I can work, then?” said the voice.\n\n“Oh, yes, yes; this instant, I entreat you.”\n\nIn a moment that part of the floor on which Dantès was resting his two hands, as he knelt with his head in the opening, suddenly gave way; he drew back smartly, while a mass of stones and earth disappeared in a hole that opened beneath the aperture he himself had formed. Then from the bottom of this passage, the depth of which it was impossible to measure, he saw appear, first the head, then the shoulders, and lastly the body of a man, who sprang lightly into his cell.\n0197m\n\n# Chapter 16. A Learned Italian\n\nSeizing in his arms the friend so long and ardently desired, Dantès almost carried him towards the window, in order to obtain a better view of his features by the aid of the imperfect light that struggled through the grating.\n\nHe was a man of small stature, with hair blanched rather by suffering and sorrow than by age. He had a deep-set, penetrating eye, almost buried beneath the thick gray eyebrow, and a long (and still black) beard reaching down to his breast. His thin face, deeply furrowed by care, and the bold outline of his strongly marked features, betokened a man more accustomed to exercise his mental faculties than his physical strength. Large drops of perspiration were now standing on his brow, while the garments that hung about him were so ragged that one could only guess at the pattern upon which they had originally been fashioned.\n\nThe stranger might have numbered sixty or sixty-five years; but a certain briskness and appearance of vigor in his movements made it probable that he was aged more from captivity than the course of time. He received the enthusiastic greeting of his young acquaintance with evident pleasure, as though his chilled affections were rekindled and invigorated by his contact with one so warm and ardent. He thanked him with grateful cordiality for his kindly welcome, although he must at that moment have been suffering bitterly to find another dungeon where he had fondly reckoned on discovering a means of regaining his liberty.\n\n“Let us first see,” said he, “whether it is possible to remove the traces of my entrance here—our future tranquillity depends upon our jailers being entirely ignorant of it.”\n\nAdvancing to the opening, he stooped and raised the stone easily in spite of its weight; then, fitting it into its place, he said:\n\n“You removed this stone very carelessly; but I suppose you had no tools to aid you.”\n\n“Why,” exclaimed Dantès, with astonishment, “do you possess any?”\n\n“I made myself some; and with the exception of a file, I have all that are necessary,—a chisel, pincers, and lever.”\n0201m\n\n“Oh, how I should like to see these products of your industry and patience.”\n\n“Well, in the first place, here is my chisel.”\n\nSo saying, he displayed a sharp strong blade, with a handle made of beechwood.\n\n“And with what did you contrive to make that?” inquired Dantès.\n\n“With one of the clamps of my bedstead; and this very tool has sufficed me to hollow out the road by which I came hither, a distance of about fifty feet.”\n\n“Fifty feet!” responded Dantès, almost terrified.\n\n“Do not speak so loud, young man—don’t speak so loud. It frequently occurs in a state prison like this, that persons are stationed outside the doors of the cells purposely to overhear the conversation of the prisoners.”\n\n“But they believe I am shut up alone here.”\n\n“That makes no difference.”\n\n“And you say that you dug your way a distance of fifty feet to get here?”\n\n“I do; that is about the distance that separates your chamber from mine; only, unfortunately, I did not curve aright; for want of the necessary geometrical instruments to calculate my scale of proportion, instead of taking an ellipsis of forty feet, I made it fifty. I expected, as I told you, to reach the outer wall, pierce through it, and throw myself into the sea; I have, however, kept along the corridor on which your chamber opens, instead of going beneath it. My labor is all in vain, for I find that the corridor looks into a courtyard filled with soldiers.”\n\n“That’s true,” said Dantès; “but the corridor you speak of only bounds _one_ side of my cell; there are three others—do you know anything of their situation?”\n\n“This one is built against the solid rock, and it would take ten experienced miners, duly furnished with the requisite tools, as many years to perforate it. This adjoins the lower part of the governor’s apartments, and were we to work our way through, we should only get into some lock-up cellars, where we must necessarily be recaptured. The fourth and last side of your cell faces on—faces on—stop a minute, now where does it face?”\n\nThe wall of which he spoke was the one in which was fixed the loophole by which light was admitted to the chamber. This loophole, which gradually diminished in size as it approached the outside, to an opening through which a child could not have passed, was, for better security, furnished with three iron bars, so as to quiet all apprehensions even in the mind of the most suspicious jailer as to the possibility of a prisoner’s escape. As the stranger asked the question, he dragged the table beneath the window.\n\n“Climb up,” said he to Dantès.\n\nThe young man obeyed, mounted on the table, and, divining the wishes of his companion, placed his back securely against the wall and held out both hands. The stranger, whom as yet Dantès knew only by the number of his cell, sprang up with an agility by no means to be expected in a person of his years, and, light and steady on his feet as a cat or a lizard, climbed from the table to the outstretched hands of Dantès, and from them to his shoulders; then, bending double, for the ceiling of the dungeon prevented him from holding himself erect, he managed to slip his head between the upper bars of the window, so as to be able to command a perfect view from top to bottom.\n\nAn instant afterwards he hastily drew back his head, saying, “I thought so!” and sliding from the shoulders of Dantès as dextrously as he had ascended, he nimbly leaped from the table to the ground.\n\n“What was it that you thought?” asked the young man anxiously, in his turn descending from the table.\n\nThe elder prisoner pondered the matter. “Yes,” said he at length, “it is so. This side of your chamber looks out upon a kind of open gallery, where patrols are continually passing, and sentries keep watch day and night.”\n\n“Are you quite sure of that?”\n\n“Certain. I saw the soldier’s shape and the top of his musket; that made me draw in my head so quickly, for I was fearful he might also see me.”\n\n“Well?” inquired Dantès.\n\n“You perceive then the utter impossibility of escaping through your dungeon?”\n\n“Then——” pursued the young man eagerly.\n\n“Then,” answered the elder prisoner, “the will of God be done!” And as the old man slowly pronounced those words, an air of profound resignation spread itself over his careworn countenance. Dantès gazed on the man who could thus philosophically resign hopes so long and ardently nourished with an astonishment mingled with admiration.\n\n“Tell me, I entreat of you, who and what you are?” said he at length. “Never have I met with so remarkable a person as yourself.”\n\n“Willingly,” answered the stranger; “if, indeed, you feel any curiosity respecting one, now, alas, powerless to aid you in any way.”\n\n“Say not so; you can console and support me by the strength of your own powerful mind. Pray let me know who you really are?”\n\nThe stranger smiled a melancholy smile. “Then listen,” said he. “I am the Abbé Faria, and have been imprisoned as you know in this Château d’If since the year 1811; previously to which I had been confined for three years in the fortress of Fenestrelle. In the year 1811 I was transferred to Piedmont in France. It was at this period I learned that the destiny which seemed subservient to every wish formed by Napoleon, had bestowed on him a son, named king of Rome even in his cradle. I was very far then from expecting the change you have just informed me of; namely, that four years afterwards, this colossus of power would be overthrown. Then who reigns in France at this moment—Napoleon II.?”\n\n“No, Louis XVIII.”\n\n“The brother of Louis XVI.! How inscrutable are the ways of Providence—for what great and mysterious purpose has it pleased Heaven to abase the man once so elevated, and raise up him who was so abased?”\n\nDantès’ whole attention was riveted on a man who could thus forget his own misfortunes while occupying himself with the destinies of others.\n\n“Yes, yes,” continued he, “’Twill be the same as it was in England. After Charles I., Cromwell; after Cromwell, Charles II., and then James II., and then some son-in-law or relation, some Prince of Orange, a stadtholder who becomes a king. Then new concessions to the people, then a constitution, then liberty. Ah, my friend!” said the abbé, turning towards Dantès, and surveying him with the kindling gaze of a prophet, “you are young, you will see all this come to pass.”\n\n“Probably, if ever I get out of prison!”\n\n“True,” replied Faria, “we are prisoners; but I forget this sometimes, and there are even moments when my mental vision transports me beyond these walls, and I fancy myself at liberty.”\n\n“But wherefore are you here?”\n\n“Because in 1807 I dreamed of the very plan Napoleon tried to realize in 1811; because, like Machiavelli, I desired to alter the political face of Italy, and instead of allowing it to be split up into a quantity of petty principalities, each held by some weak or tyrannical ruler, I sought to form one large, compact, and powerful empire; and, lastly, because I fancied I had found my Cæsar Borgia in a crowned simpleton, who feigned to enter into my views only to betray me. It was the plan of Alexander VI. and Clement VII., but it will never succeed now, for they attempted it fruitlessly, and Napoleon was unable to complete his work. Italy seems fated to misfortune.” And the old man bowed his head.\n\nDantès could not understand a man risking his life for such matters. Napoleon certainly he knew something of, inasmuch as he had seen and spoken with him; but of Clement VII. and Alexander VI. he knew nothing.\n\n“Are you not,” he asked, “the priest who here in the Château d’If is generally thought to be—ill?”\n\n“Mad, you mean, don’t you?”\n\n“I did not like to say so,” answered Dantès, smiling.\n\n“Well, then,” resumed Faria with a bitter smile, “let me answer your question in full, by acknowledging that I am the poor mad prisoner of the Château d’If, for many years permitted to amuse the different visitors with what is said to be my insanity; and, in all probability, I should be promoted to the honor of making sport for the children, if such innocent beings could be found in an abode devoted like this to suffering and despair.”\n\nDantès remained for a short time mute and motionless; at length he said:\n\n“Then you abandon all hope of escape?”\n\n“I perceive its utter impossibility; and I consider it impious to attempt that which the Almighty evidently does not approve.”\n\n“Nay, be not discouraged. Would it not be expecting too much to hope to succeed at your first attempt? Why not try to find an opening in another direction from that which has so unfortunately failed?”\n\n“Alas, it shows how little notion you can have of all it has cost me to effect a purpose so unexpectedly frustrated, that you talk of beginning over again. In the first place, I was four years making the tools I possess, and have been two years scraping and digging out earth, hard as granite itself; then what toil and fatigue has it not been to remove huge stones I should once have deemed impossible to loosen. Whole days have I passed in these Titanic efforts, considering my labor well repaid if, by night-time I had contrived to carry away a square inch of this hard-bound cement, changed by ages into a substance unyielding as the stones themselves; then to conceal the mass of earth and rubbish I dug up, I was compelled to break through a staircase, and throw the fruits of my labor into the hollow part of it; but the well is now so completely choked up, that I scarcely think it would be possible to add another handful of dust without leading to discovery. Consider also that I fully believed I had accomplished the end and aim of my undertaking, for which I had so exactly husbanded my strength as to make it just hold out to the termination of my enterprise; and now, at the moment when I reckoned upon success, my hopes are forever dashed from me. No, I repeat again, that nothing shall induce me to renew attempts evidently at variance with the Almighty’s pleasure.”\n\nDantès held down his head, that the other might not see how joy at the thought of having a companion outweighed the sympathy he felt for the failure of the abbé’s plans.\n\nThe abbé sank upon Edmond’s bed, while Edmond himself remained standing. Escape had never once occurred to him. There are, indeed, some things which appear so impossible that the mind does not dwell on them for an instant. To undermine the ground for fifty feet—to devote three years to a labor which, if successful, would conduct you to a precipice overhanging the sea—to plunge into the waves from the height of fifty, sixty, perhaps a hundred feet, at the risk of being dashed to pieces against the rocks, should you have been fortunate enough to have escaped the fire of the sentinels; and even, supposing all these perils past, then to have to swim for your life a distance of at least three miles ere you could reach the shore—were difficulties so startling and formidable that Dantès had never even dreamed of such a scheme, resigning himself rather to death.\n\nBut the sight of an old man clinging to life with so desperate a courage, gave a fresh turn to his ideas, and inspired him with new courage. Another, older and less strong than he, had attempted what he had not had sufficient resolution to undertake, and had failed only because of an error in calculation. This same person, with almost incredible patience and perseverance, had contrived to provide himself with tools requisite for so unparalleled an attempt. Another had done all this; why, then, was it impossible to Dantès? Faria had dug his way through fifty feet, Dantès would dig a hundred; Faria, at the age of fifty, had devoted three years to the task; he, who was but half as old, would sacrifice six; Faria, a priest and savant, had not shrunk from the idea of risking his life by trying to swim a distance of three miles to one of the islands—Daume, Rattonneau, or Lemaire; should a hardy sailor, an experienced diver, like himself, shrink from a similar task; should he, who had so often for mere amusement’s sake plunged to the bottom of the sea to fetch up the bright coral branch, hesitate to entertain the same project? He could do it in an hour, and how many times had he, for pure pastime, continued in the water for more than twice as long! At once Dantès resolved to follow the brave example of his energetic companion, and to remember that what has once been done may be done again.\n\nAfter continuing some time in profound meditation, the young man suddenly exclaimed, “I have found what you were in search of!”\n\nFaria started: “Have you, indeed?” cried he, raising his head with quick anxiety; “pray, let me know what it is you have discovered?”\n\n“The corridor through which you have bored your way from the cell you occupy here, extends in the same direction as the outer gallery, does it not?”\n0207m\n\n“It does.”\n\n“And is not above fifteen feet from it?”\n\n“About that.”\n\n“Well, then, I will tell you what we must do. We must pierce through the corridor by forming a side opening about the middle, as it were the top part of a cross. This time you will lay your plans more accurately; we shall get out into the gallery you have described; kill the sentinel who guards it, and make our escape. All we require to insure success is courage, and that you possess, and strength, which I am not deficient in; as for patience, you have abundantly proved yours—you shall now see me prove mine.”\n\n“One instant, my dear friend,” replied the abbé; “it is clear you do not understand the nature of the courage with which I am endowed, and what use I intend making of my strength. As for patience, I consider that I have abundantly exercised that in beginning every morning the task of the night before, and every night renewing the task of the day. But then, young man (and I pray of you to give me your full attention), then I thought I could not be doing anything displeasing to the Almighty in trying to set an innocent being at liberty—one who had committed no offence, and merited not condemnation.”\n\n“And have your notions changed?” asked Dantès with much surprise; “do you think yourself more guilty in making the attempt since you have encountered me?”\n\n“No; neither do I wish to incur guilt. Hitherto I have fancied myself merely waging war against circumstances, not men. I have thought it no sin to bore through a wall, or destroy a staircase; but I cannot so easily persuade myself to pierce a heart or take away a life.”\n\nA slight movement of surprise escaped Dantès.\n\n“Is it possible,” said he, “that where your liberty is at stake you can allow any such scruple to deter you from obtaining it?”\n\n“Tell me,” replied Faria, “what has hindered you from knocking down your jailer with a piece of wood torn from your bedstead, dressing yourself in his clothes, and endeavoring to escape?”\n\n“Simply the fact that the idea never occurred to me,” answered Dantès.\n\n“Because,” said the old man, “the natural repugnance to the commission of such a crime prevented you from thinking of it; and so it ever is because in simple and allowable things our natural instincts keep us from deviating from the strict line of duty. The tiger, whose nature teaches him to delight in shedding blood, needs but the sense of smell to show him when his prey is within his reach, and by following this instinct he is enabled to measure the leap necessary to permit him to spring on his victim; but man, on the contrary, loathes the idea of blood—it is not alone that the laws of social life inspire him with a shrinking dread of taking life; his natural construction and physiological formation——”\n\nDantès was confused and silent at this explanation of the thoughts which had unconsciously been working in his mind, or rather soul; for there are two distinct sorts of ideas, those that proceed from the head and those that emanate from the heart.\n\n0209m\n“Since my imprisonment,” said Faria, “I have thought over all the most celebrated cases of escape on record. They have rarely been successful. Those that have been crowned with full success have been long meditated upon, and carefully arranged; such, for instance, as the escape of the Duc de Beaufort from the Château de Vincennes, that of the Abbé Dubuquoi from For l’Evêque; of Latude from the Bastille. Then there are those for which chance sometimes affords opportunity, and those are the best of all. Let us, therefore, wait patiently for some favorable moment, and when it presents itself, profit by it.”\n\n“Ah,” said Dantès, “you might well endure the tedious delay; you were constantly employed in the task you set yourself, and when weary with toil, you had your hopes to refresh and encourage you.”\n\n“I assure you,” replied the old man, “I did not turn to that source for recreation or support.”\n\n“What did you do then?”\n\n“I wrote or studied.”\n\n“Were you then permitted the use of pens, ink, and paper?”\n\n“Oh, no,” answered the abbé; “I had none but what I made for myself.”\n\n“You made paper, pens and ink?”\n“Yes.”\n\nDantès gazed with admiration, but he had some difficulty in believing. Faria saw this.\n\n“When you pay me a visit in my cell, my young friend,” said he, “I will show you an entire work, the fruits of the thoughts and reflections of my whole life; many of them meditated over in the shades of the Colosseum at Rome, at the foot of St. Mark’s column at Venice, and on the borders of the Arno at Florence, little imagining at the time that they would be arranged in order within the walls of the Château d’If. The work I speak of is called _A Treatise on the Possibility of a General Monarchy in Italy_, and will make one large quarto volume.”\n\n“And on what have you written all this?”\n\n“On two of my shirts. I invented a preparation that makes linen as smooth and as easy to write on as parchment.”\n\n“You are, then, a chemist?”\n\n“Somewhat; I know Lavoisier, and was the intimate friend of Cabanis.”\n\n“But for such a work you must have needed books—had you any?”\n\n“I had nearly five thousand volumes in my library at Rome; but after reading them over many times, I found out that with one hundred and fifty well-chosen books a man possesses, if not a complete summary of all human knowledge, at least all that a man need really know. I devoted three years of my life to reading and studying these one hundred and fifty volumes, till I knew them nearly by heart; so that since I have been in prison, a very slight effort of memory has enabled me to recall their contents as readily as though the pages were open before me. I could recite you the whole of Thucydides, Xenophon, Plutarch, Titus Livius, Tacitus, Strada, Jornandes, Dante, Montaigne, Shakespeare, Spinoza, Machiavelli, and Bossuet. I name only the most important.”\n\n“You are, doubtless, acquainted with a variety of languages, so as to have been able to read all these?”\n\n“Yes, I speak five of the modern tongues—that is to say, German, French, Italian, English, and Spanish; by the aid of ancient Greek I learned modern Greek—I don’t speak it so well as I could wish, but I am still trying to improve myself.”\n\n“Improve yourself!” repeated Dantès; “why, how can you manage to do so?”\n\n“Why, I made a vocabulary of the words I knew; turned, returned, and arranged them, so as to enable me to express my thoughts through their medium. I know nearly one thousand words, which is all that is absolutely necessary, although I believe there are nearly one hundred thousand in the dictionaries. I cannot hope to be very fluent, but I certainly should have no difficulty in explaining my wants and wishes; and that would be quite as much as I should ever require.”\n\nStronger grew the wonder of Dantès, who almost fancied he had to do with one gifted with supernatural powers; still hoping to find some imperfection which might bring him down to a level with human beings, he added, “Then if you were not furnished with pens, how did you manage to write the work you speak of?”\n\n“I made myself some excellent ones, which would be universally preferred to all others if once known. You are aware what huge whitings are served to us on _maigre_ days. Well, I selected the cartilages of the heads of these fishes, and you can scarcely imagine the delight with which I welcomed the arrival of each Wednesday, Friday, and Saturday, as affording me the means of increasing my stock of pens; for I will freely confess that my historical labors have been my greatest solace and relief. While retracing the past, I forget the present; and traversing at will the path of history I cease to remember that I am myself a prisoner.”\n\n“But the ink,” said Dantès; “of what did you make your ink?”\n\n“There was formerly a fireplace in my dungeon,” replied Faria, “but it was closed up long ere I became an occupant of this prison. Still, it must have been many years in use, for it was thickly covered with a coating of soot; this soot I dissolved in a portion of the wine brought to me every Sunday, and I assure you a better ink cannot be desired. For very important notes, for which closer attention is required, I pricked one of my fingers, and wrote with my own blood.”\n\n“And when,” asked Dantès, “may I see all this?”\n\n“Whenever you please,” replied the abbé.\n\n“Oh, then let it be directly!” exclaimed the young man.\n\n“Follow me, then,” said the abbé, as he re-entered the subterranean passage, in which he soon disappeared, followed by Dantès.\n\n# Chapter 17. The Abbé’s Chamber\n\nAfter having passed with tolerable ease through the subterranean passage, which, however, did not admit of their holding themselves erect, the two friends reached the further end of the corridor, into which the abbé’s cell opened; from that point the passage became much narrower, and barely permitted one to creep through on hands and knees. The floor of the abbé’s cell was paved, and it had been by raising one of the stones in the most obscure corner that Faria had been able to commence the laborious task of which Dantès had witnessed the completion.\n\nAs he entered the chamber of his friend, Dantès cast around one eager and searching glance in quest of the expected marvels, but nothing more than common met his view.\n\n“It is well,” said the abbé; “we have some hours before us—it is now just a quarter past twelve o’clock.” Instinctively Dantès turned round to observe by what watch or clock the abbé had been able so accurately to specify the hour.\n\n“Look at this ray of light which enters by my window,” said the abbé, “and then observe the lines traced on the wall. Well, by means of these lines, which are in accordance with the double motion of the earth, and the ellipse it describes round the sun, I am enabled to ascertain the precise hour with more minuteness than if I possessed a watch; for that might be broken or deranged in its movements, while the sun and earth never vary in their appointed paths.”\n\nThis last explanation was wholly lost upon Dantès, who had always imagined, from seeing the sun rise from behind the mountains and set in the Mediterranean, that it moved, and not the earth. A double movement of the globe he inhabited, and of which he could feel nothing, appeared to him perfectly impossible. Each word that fell from his companion’s lips seemed fraught with the mysteries of science, as worthy of digging out as the gold and diamonds in the mines of Guzerat and Golconda, which he could just recollect having visited during a voyage made in his earliest youth.\n\n“Come,” said he to the abbé, “I am anxious to see your treasures.”\n\nThe abbé smiled, and, proceeding to the disused fireplace, raised, by the help of his chisel, a long stone, which had doubtless been the hearth, beneath which was a cavity of considerable depth, serving as a safe depository of the articles mentioned to Dantès.\n0213m\n\n“What do you wish to see first?” asked the abbé.\n\n“Oh, your great work on the monarchy of Italy!”\n\nFaria then drew forth from his hiding-place three or four rolls of linen, laid one over the other, like folds of papyrus. These rolls consisted of slips of cloth about four inches wide and eighteen long; they were all carefully numbered and closely covered with writing, so legible that Dantès could easily read it, as well as make out the sense—it being in Italian, a language he, as a Provençal, perfectly understood.\n\n“There,” said he, “there is the work complete. I wrote the word _finis_ at the end of the sixty-eighth strip about a week ago. I have torn up two of my shirts, and as many handkerchiefs as I was master of, to complete the precious pages. Should I ever get out of prison and find in all Italy a printer courageous enough to publish what I have composed, my literary reputation is forever secured.”\n\n“I see,” answered Dantès. “Now let me behold the curious pens with which you have written your work.”\n\n“Look!” said Faria, showing to the young man a slender stick about six inches long, and much resembling the size of the handle of a fine painting-brush, to the end of which was tied, by a piece of thread, one of those cartilages of which the abbé had before spoken to Dantès; it was pointed, and divided at the nib like an ordinary pen. Dantès examined it with intense admiration, then looked around to see the instrument with which it had been shaped so correctly into form.\n\n“Ah, yes,” said Faria; “the penknife. That’s my masterpiece. I made it, as well as this larger knife, out of an old iron candlestick.” The penknife was sharp and keen as a razor; as for the other knife, it would serve a double purpose, and with it one could cut and thrust.\n\nDantès examined the various articles shown to him with the same attention that he had bestowed on the curiosities and strange tools exhibited in the shops at Marseilles as the works of the savages in the South Seas from whence they had been brought by the different trading vessels.\n\n“As for the ink,” said Faria, “I told you how I managed to obtain that—and I only just make it from time to time, as I require it.”\n\n“One thing still puzzles me,” observed Dantès, “and that is how you managed to do all this by daylight?”\n\n“I worked at night also,” replied Faria.\n\n“Night!—why, for Heaven’s sake, are your eyes like cats’, that you can see to work in the dark?”\n\n“Indeed they are not; but God has supplied man with the intelligence that enables him to overcome the limitations of natural conditions. I furnished myself with a light.”\n\n“You did? Pray tell me how.”\n“I separated the fat from the meat served to me, melted it, and so made oil—here is my lamp.” So saying, the abbé exhibited a sort of torch very similar to those used in public illuminations.\n\n“But how do you procure a light?”\n\n“Oh, here are two flints and a piece of burnt linen.”\n\n“And matches?”\n\n“I pretended that I had a disorder of the skin, and asked for a little sulphur, which was readily supplied.”\n\nDantès laid the different things he had been looking at on the table, and stood with his head drooping on his breast, as though overwhelmed by the perseverance and strength of Faria’s mind.\n\n0215m\n“You have not seen all yet,” continued Faria, “for I did not think it wise to trust all my treasures in the same hiding-place. Let us shut this one up.” They put the stone back in its place; the abbé sprinkled a little dust over it to conceal the traces of its having been removed, rubbed his foot well on it to make it assume the same appearance as the other, and then, going towards his bed, he removed it from the spot it stood in. Behind the head of the bed, and concealed by a stone fitting in so closely as to defy all suspicion, was a hollow space, and in this space a ladder of cords between twenty-five and thirty feet in length. Dantès closely and eagerly examined it; he found it firm, solid, and compact enough to bear any weight.\n\n“Who supplied you with the materials for making this wonderful work?”\n\n“I tore up several of my shirts, and ripped out the seams in the sheets of my bed, during my three years’ imprisonment at Fenestrelle; and when I was removed to the Château d’If, I managed to bring the ravellings with me, so that I have been able to finish my work here.”\n\n“And was it not discovered that your sheets were unhemmed?”\n\n“Oh, no, for when I had taken out the thread I required, I hemmed the edges over again.”\n\n“With what?”\n\n“With this needle,” said the abbé, as, opening his ragged vestments, he showed Dantès a long, sharp fish-bone, with a small perforated eye for the thread, a small portion of which still remained in it.\n\n“I once thought,” continued Faria, “of removing these iron bars, and letting myself down from the window, which, as you see, is somewhat wider than yours, although I should have enlarged it still more preparatory to my flight; however, I discovered that I should merely have dropped into a sort of inner court, and I therefore renounced the project altogether as too full of risk and danger. Nevertheless, I carefully preserved my ladder against one of those unforeseen opportunities of which I spoke just now, and which sudden chance frequently brings about.”\n\nWhile affecting to be deeply engaged in examining the ladder, the mind of Dantès was, in fact, busily occupied by the idea that a person so intelligent, ingenious, and clear-sighted as the abbé might probably be able to solve the dark mystery of his own misfortunes, where he himself could see nothing.\n\n“What are you thinking of?” asked the abbé smilingly, imputing the deep abstraction in which his visitor was plunged to the excess of his awe and wonder.\n\n“I was reflecting, in the first place,” replied Dantès, “upon the enormous degree of intelligence and ability you must have employed to reach the high perfection to which you have attained. What would you not have accomplished if you had been free?”\n\n“Possibly nothing at all; the overflow of my brain would probably, in a state of freedom, have evaporated in a thousand follies; misfortune is needed to bring to light the treasures of the human intellect. Compression is needed to explode gunpowder. Captivity has brought my mental faculties to a focus; and you are well aware that from the collision of clouds electricity is produced—from electricity, lightning, from lightning, illumination.”\n\n“No,” replied Dantès. “I know nothing. Some of your words are to me quite empty of meaning. You must be blessed indeed to possess the knowledge you have.”\n\nThe abbé smiled. “Well,” said he, “but you had another subject for your thoughts; did you not say so just now?”\n“I did!”\n\n“You have told me as yet but one of them—let me hear the other.”\n\n“It was this,—that while you had related to me all the particulars of your past life, you were perfectly unacquainted with mine.”\n\n“Your life, my young friend, has not been of sufficient length to admit of your having passed through any very important events.”\n\n“It has been long enough to inflict on me a great and undeserved misfortune. I would fain fix the source of it on man that I may no longer vent reproaches upon Heaven.”\n\n“Then you profess ignorance of the crime with which you are charged?”\n\n“I do, indeed; and this I swear by the two beings most dear to me upon earth,—my father and Mercédès.”\n\n“Come,” said the abbé, closing his hiding-place, and pushing the bed back to its original situation, “let me hear your story.”\n\nDantès obeyed, and commenced what he called his history, but which consisted only of the account of a voyage to India, and two or three voyages to the Levant, until he arrived at the recital of his last cruise, with the death of Captain Leclere, and the receipt of a packet to be delivered by himself to the grand marshal; his interview with that personage, and his receiving, in place of the packet brought, a letter addressed to a Monsieur Noirtier—his arrival at Marseilles, and interview with his father—his affection for Mercédès, and their nuptial feast—his arrest and subsequent examination, his temporary detention at the Palais de Justice, and his final imprisonment in the Château d’If. From this point everything was a blank to Dantès—he knew nothing more, not even the length of time he had been imprisoned. His recital finished, the abbé reflected long and earnestly.\n\n“There is,” said he, at the end of his meditations, “a clever maxim, which bears upon what I was saying to you some little while ago, and that is, that unless wicked ideas take root in a naturally depraved mind, human nature, in a right and wholesome state, revolts at crime. Still, from an artificial civilization have originated wants, vices, and false tastes, which occasionally become so powerful as to stifle within us all good feelings, and ultimately to lead us into guilt and wickedness. From this view of things, then, comes the axiom that if you visit to discover the author of any bad action, seek first to discover the person to whom the perpetration of that bad action could be in any way advantageous. Now, to apply it in your case,—to whom could your disappearance have been serviceable?”\n\n“To no one, by Heaven! I was a very insignificant person.”\n\n“Do not speak thus, for your reply evinces neither logic nor philosophy; everything is relative, my dear young friend, from the king who stands in the way of his successor, to the employee who keeps his rival out of a place. Now, in the event of the king’s death, his successor inherits a crown,—when the employee dies, the supernumerary steps into his shoes, and receives his salary of twelve thousand livres. Well, these twelve thousand livres are his civil list, and are as essential to him as the twelve millions of a king. Everyone, from the highest to the lowest degree, has his place on the social ladder, and is beset by stormy passions and conflicting interests, as in Descartes’ theory of pressure and impulsion. But these forces increase as we go higher, so that we have a spiral which in defiance of reason rests upon the apex and not on the base. Now let us return to your particular world. You say you were on the point of being made captain of the _Pharaon_?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“And about to become the husband of a young and lovely girl?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Now, could anyone have had any interest in preventing the accomplishment of these two things? But let us first settle the question as to its being the interest of anyone to hinder you from being captain of the _Pharaon_. What say you?”\n\n“I cannot believe such was the case. I was generally liked on board, and had the sailors possessed the right of selecting a captain themselves, I feel convinced their choice would have fallen on me. There was only one person among the crew who had any feeling of ill-will towards me. I had quarelled with him some time previously, and had even challenged him to fight me; but he refused.”\n\n“Now we are getting on. And what was this man’s name?”\n\n“Danglars.”\n\n“What rank did he hold on board?”\n\n“He was supercargo.”\n\n“And had you been captain, should you have retained him in his employment?”\n\n“Not if the choice had remained with me, for I had frequently observed inaccuracies in his accounts.”\n\n“Good again! Now then, tell me, was any person present during your last conversation with Captain Leclere?”\n\n“No; we were quite alone.”\n\n“Could your conversation have been overheard by anyone?”\n\n“It might, for the cabin door was open—and—stay; now I recollect,—Danglars himself passed by just as Captain Leclere was giving me the packet for the grand marshal.”\n\n“That’s better,” cried the abbé; “now we are on the right scent. Did you take anybody with you when you put into the port of Elba?”\n\n“Nobody.”\n\n“Somebody there received your packet, and gave you a letter in place of it, I think?”\n\n“Yes; the grand marshal did.”\n\n“And what did you do with that letter?”\n\n“Put it into my portfolio.”\n\n“You had your portfolio with you, then? Now, how could a sailor find room in his pocket for a portfolio large enough to contain an official letter?”\n\n“You are right; it was left on board.”\n\n“Then it was not till your return to the ship that you put the letter in the portfolio?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“And what did you do with this same letter while returning from Porto-Ferrajo to the vessel?”\n\n“I carried it in my hand.”\n\n“So that when you went on board the _Pharaon_, everybody could see that you held a letter in your hand?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Danglars, as well as the rest?”\n\n“Danglars, as well as others.”\n\n“Now, listen to me, and try to recall every circumstance attending your arrest. Do you recollect the words in which the information against you was formulated?”\n\n“Oh yes, I read it over three times, and the words sank deeply into my memory.”\n\n“Repeat it to me.”\n\nDantès paused a moment, then said, “This is it, word for word: ‘The king’s attorney is informed by a friend to the throne and religion, that one Edmond Dantès, mate on board the _Pharaon_, this day arrived from Smyrna, after having touched at Naples and Porto-Ferrajo, has been intrusted by Murat with a packet for the usurper; again, by the usurper, with a letter for the Bonapartist Club in Paris. This proof of his guilt may be procured by his immediate arrest, as the letter will be found either about his person, at his father’s residence, or in his cabin on board the _Pharaon_.’”\n\nThe abbé shrugged his shoulders. “The thing is clear as day,” said he; “and you must have had a very confiding nature, as well as a good heart, not to have suspected the origin of the whole affair.”\n\n“Do you really think so? Ah, that would indeed be infamous.”\n\n“How did Danglars usually write?”\n\n“In a handsome, running hand.”\n\n“And how was the anonymous letter written?”\n\nAgain the abbé smiled. “Disguised.”\n\n“It was very boldly written, if disguised.”\n\n“Stop a bit,” said the abbé, taking up what he called his pen, and, after dipping it into the ink, he wrote on a piece of prepared linen, with his left hand, the first two or three words of the accusation. Dantès drew back, and gazed on the abbé with a sensation almost amounting to terror.\n\n“How very astonishing!” cried he at length. “Why your writing exactly resembles that of the accusation.”\n\n“Simply because that accusation had been written with the left hand; and I have noticed that——”\n\n“What?”\n“That while the writing of different persons done with the right hand varies, that performed with the left hand is invariably uniform.”\n\n“You have evidently seen and observed everything.”\n\n“Let us proceed.”\n\n“Oh, yes, yes!”\n\n“Now as regards the second question.”\n\n“I am listening.”\n\n“Was there any person whose interest it was to prevent your marriage with Mercédès?”\n\n“Yes; a young man who loved her.”\n\n“And his name was——”\n\n“Fernand.”\n\n“That is a Spanish name, I think?”\n\n“He was a Catalan.”\n\n“You imagine him capable of writing the letter?”\n\n“Oh, no; he would more likely have got rid of me by sticking a knife into me.”\n\n“That is in strict accordance with the Spanish character; an assassination they will unhesitatingly commit, but an act of cowardice, never.”\n\n“Besides,” said Dantès, “the various circumstances mentioned in the letter were wholly unknown to him.”\n\n“You had never spoken of them yourself to anyone?”\n\n“To no one.”\n\n“Not even to your mistress?”\n\n“No, not even to my betrothed.”\n\n“Then it is Danglars.”\n\n“I feel quite sure of it now.”\n\n“Wait a little. Pray, was Danglars acquainted with Fernand?”\n\n“No—yes, he was. Now I recollect——”\n\n“What?”\n\n“To have seen them both sitting at table together under an arbor at Père Pamphile’s the evening before the day fixed for my wedding. They were in earnest conversation. Danglars was joking in a friendly way, but Fernand looked pale and agitated.”\n\n“Were they alone?”\n\n“There was a third person with them whom I knew perfectly well, and who had, in all probability made their acquaintance; he was a tailor named Caderousse, but he was very drunk. Stay!—stay!—How strange that it should not have occurred to me before! Now I remember quite well, that on the table round which they were sitting were pens, ink, and paper. Oh, the heartless, treacherous scoundrels!” exclaimed Dantès, pressing his hand to his throbbing brows.\n\n“Is there anything else I can assist you in discovering, besides the villany of your friends?” inquired the abbé with a laugh.\n\n“Yes, yes,” replied Dantès eagerly; “I would beg of you, who see so completely to the depths of things, and to whom the greatest mystery seems but an easy riddle, to explain to me how it was that I underwent no second examination, was never brought to trial, and, above all, was condemned without ever having had sentence passed on me?”\n\n“That is altogether a different and more serious matter,” responded the abbé. “The ways of justice are frequently too dark and mysterious to be easily penetrated. All we have hitherto done in the matter has been child’s play. If you wish me to enter upon the more difficult part of the business, you must assist me by the most minute information on every point.”\n\n“Pray ask me whatever questions you please; for, in good truth, you see more clearly into my life than I do myself.”\n\n“In the first place, then, who examined you,—the king’s attorney, his deputy, or a magistrate?”\n\n“The deputy.”\n\n“Was he young or old?”\n\n“About six or seven-and-twenty years of age, I should say.”\n\n“So,” answered the abbé. “Old enough to be ambitious, but too young to be corrupt. And how did he treat you?”\n\n“With more of mildness than severity.”\n\n“Did you tell him your whole story?”\n“I did.”\n\n“And did his conduct change at all in the course of your examination?”\n\n“He did appear much disturbed when he read the letter that had brought me into this scrape. He seemed quite overcome by my misfortune.”\n\n“By your misfortune?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Then you feel quite sure that it was your misfortune he deplored?”\n\n“He gave me one great proof of his sympathy, at any rate.”\n\n“And that?”\n\n“He burnt the sole evidence that could at all have criminated me.”\n\n“What? the accusation?”\n\n“No; the letter.”\n\n“Are you sure?”\n\n“I saw it done.”\n\n“That alters the case. This man might, after all, be a greater scoundrel than you have thought possible.”\n\n“Upon my word,” said Dantès, “you make me shudder. Is the world filled with tigers and crocodiles?”\n\n“Yes; and remember that two-legged tigers and crocodiles are more dangerous than the others.”\n\n“Never mind; let us go on.”\n\n“With all my heart! You tell me he burned the letter?”\n\n“He did; saying at the same time, ‘You see I thus destroy the only proof existing against you.’”\n\n“This action is somewhat too sublime to be natural.”\n\n“You think so?”\n\n“I am sure of it. To whom was this letter addressed?”\n\n“To M. Noirtier, Rue Coq-Héron, No. 13, Paris.”\n\n“Now can you conceive of any interest that your heroic deputy could possibly have had in the destruction of that letter?”\n\n“Why, it is not altogether impossible he might have had, for he made me promise several times never to speak of that letter to anyone, assuring me he so advised me for my own interest; and, more than this, he insisted on my taking a solemn oath never to utter the name mentioned in the address.”\n\n“Noirtier!” repeated the abbé; “Noirtier!—I knew a person of that name at the court of the Queen of Etruria,—a Noirtier, who had been a Girondin during the Revolution! What was your deputy called?”\n\n“De Villefort!” The abbé burst into a fit of laughter, while Dantès gazed on him in utter astonishment.\n\n“What ails you?” said he at length.\n\n“Do you see that ray of sunlight?”\n\n“I do.”\n“Well, the whole thing is more clear to me than that sunbeam is to you. Poor fellow! poor young man! And you tell me this magistrate expressed great sympathy and commiseration for you?”\n\n“He did.”\n\n“And the worthy man destroyed your compromising letter?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“And then made you swear never to utter the name of Noirtier?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Why, you poor short-sighted simpleton, can you not guess who this Noirtier was, whose very name he was so careful to keep concealed? This Noirtier was his father!”\n\nHad a thunderbolt fallen at the feet of Dantès, or hell opened its yawning gulf before him, he could not have been more completely transfixed with horror than he was at the sound of these unexpected words. Starting up, he clasped his hands around his head as though to prevent his very brain from bursting, and exclaimed, “His father! his father!”\n\n“Yes, his father,” replied the abbé; “his right name was Noirtier de Villefort.”\n\nAt this instant a bright light shot through the mind of Dantès, and cleared up all that had been dark and obscure before. The change that had come over Villefort during the examination, the destruction of the letter, the exacted promise, the almost supplicating tones of the magistrate, who seemed rather to implore mercy than to pronounce punishment,—all returned with a stunning force to his memory. He cried out, and staggered against the wall like a drunken man, then he hurried to the opening that led from the abbé’s cell to his own, and said, “I must be alone, to think over all this.”\n\nWhen he regained his dungeon, he threw himself on his bed, where the turnkey found him in the evening visit, sitting with fixed gaze and contracted features, dumb and motionless as a statue. During these hours of profound meditation, which to him had seemed only minutes, he had formed a fearful resolution, and bound himself to its fulfilment by a solemn oath.\n\nDantès was at length roused from his reverie by the voice of Faria, who, having also been visited by his jailer, had come to invite his fellow-sufferer to share his supper. The reputation of being out of his mind, though harmlessly and even amusingly so, had procured for the abbé unusual privileges. He was supplied with bread of a finer, whiter quality than the usual prison fare, and even regaled each Sunday with a small quantity of wine. Now this was a Sunday, and the abbé had come to ask his young companion to share the luxuries with him.\n\nDantès followed him; his features were no longer contracted, and now wore their usual expression, but there was that in his whole appearance that bespoke one who had come to a fixed and desperate resolve. Faria bent on him his penetrating eye.\n\n“I regret now,” said he, “having helped you in your late inquiries, or having given you the information I did.”\n\n“Why so?” inquired Dantès.\n\n“Because it has instilled a new passion in your heart—that of vengeance.”\n\nDantès smiled. “Let us talk of something else,” said he.\n\nAgain the abbé looked at him, then mournfully shook his head; but in accordance with Dantès’ request, he began to speak of other matters. The elder prisoner was one of those persons whose conversation, like that of all who have experienced many trials, contained many useful and important hints as well as sound information; but it was never egotistical, for the unfortunate man never alluded to his own sorrows. Dantès listened with admiring attention to all he said; some of his remarks corresponded with what he already knew, or applied to the sort of knowledge his nautical life had enabled him to acquire. A part of the good abbé’s words, however, were wholly incomprehensible to him; but, like the aurora which guides the navigator in northern latitudes, opened new vistas to the inquiring mind of the listener, and gave fantastic glimpses of new horizons, enabling him justly to estimate the delight an intellectual mind would have in following one so richly gifted as Faria along the heights of truth, where he was so much at home.\n\n“You must teach me a small part of what you know,” said Dantès, “if only to prevent your growing weary of me. I can well believe that so learned a person as yourself would prefer absolute solitude to being tormented with the company of one as ignorant and uninformed as myself. If you will only agree to my request, I promise you never to mention another word about escaping.”\n\nThe abbé smiled.\n\n“Alas, my boy,” said he, “human knowledge is confined within very narrow limits; and when I have taught you mathematics, physics, history, and the three or four modern languages with which I am acquainted, you will know as much as I do myself. Now, it will scarcely require two years for me to communicate to you the stock of learning I possess.”\n\n“Two years!” exclaimed Dantès; “do you really believe I can acquire all these things in so short a time?”\n\n“Not their application, certainly, but their principles you may; to learn is not to know; there are the learners and the learned. Memory makes the one, philosophy the other.”\n\n“But cannot one learn philosophy?”\n\n“Philosophy cannot be taught; it is the application of the sciences to truth; it is like the golden cloud in which the Messiah went up into heaven.”\n\n“Well, then,” said Dantès, “What shall you teach me first? I am in a hurry to begin. I want to learn.”\n\n“Everything,” said the abbé. And that very evening the prisoners sketched a plan of education, to be entered upon the following day. Dantès possessed a prodigious memory, combined with an astonishing quickness and readiness of conception; the mathematical turn of his mind rendered him apt at all kinds of calculation, while his naturally poetical feelings threw a light and pleasing veil over the dry reality of arithmetical computation, or the rigid severity of geometry. He already knew Italian, and had also picked up a little of the Romaic dialect during voyages to the East; and by the aid of these two languages he easily comprehended the construction of all the others, so that at the end of six months he began to speak Spanish, English, and German.\n\nIn strict accordance with the promise made to the abbé, Dantès spoke no more of escape. Perhaps the delight his studies afforded him left no room for such thoughts; perhaps the recollection that he had pledged his word (on which his sense of honor was keen) kept him from referring in any way to the possibilities of flight. Days, even months, passed by unheeded in one rapid and instructive course. At the end of a year Dantès was a new man. Dantès observed, however, that Faria, in spite of the relief his society afforded, daily grew sadder; one thought seemed incessantly to harass and distract his mind. Sometimes he would fall into long reveries, sigh heavily and involuntarily, then suddenly rise, and, with folded arms, begin pacing the confined space of his dungeon. One day he stopped all at once, and exclaimed:\n\n“Ah, if there were no sentinel!”\n\n“There shall not be one a minute longer than you please,” said Dantès, who had followed the working of his thoughts as accurately as though his brain were enclosed in crystal so clear as to display its minutest operations.\n\n“I have already told you,” answered the abbé, “that I loathe the idea of shedding blood.”\n\n“And yet the murder, if you choose to call it so, would be simply a measure of self-preservation.”\n\n“No matter! I could never agree to it.”\n\n“Still, you have thought of it?”\n\n“Incessantly, alas!” cried the abbé.\n\n“And you have discovered a means of regaining our freedom, have you not?” asked Dantès eagerly.\n\n“I have; if it were only possible to place a deaf and blind sentinel in the gallery beyond us.”\n\n“He shall be both blind and deaf,” replied the young man, with an air of determination that made his companion shudder.\n\n“No, no,” cried the abbé; “impossible!”\n\nDantès endeavored to renew the subject; the abbé shook his head in token of disapproval, and refused to make any further response. Three months passed away.\n\n“Are you strong?” the abbé asked one day of Dantès. The young man, in reply, took up the chisel, bent it into the form of a horseshoe, and then as readily straightened it.\n\n“And will you engage not to do any harm to the sentry, except as a last resort?”\n\n“I promise on my honor.”\n\n“Then,” said the abbé, “we may hope to put our design into execution.”\n\n“And how long shall we be in accomplishing the necessary work?”\n\n“At least a year.”\n\n“And shall we begin at once?”\n\n“At once.”\n\n“We have lost a year to no purpose!” cried Dantès.\n\n“Do you consider the last twelve months to have been wasted?” asked the abbé.\n\n“Forgive me!” cried Edmond, blushing deeply.\n\n“Tut, tut!” answered the abbé, “man is but man after all, and you are about the best specimen of the genus I have ever known. Come, let me show you my plan.”\n\nThe abbé then showed Dantès the sketch he had made for their escape. It consisted of a plan of his own cell and that of Dantès, with the passage which united them. In this passage he proposed to drive a level as they do in mines; this level would bring the two prisoners immediately beneath the gallery where the sentry kept watch; once there, a large excavation would be made, and one of the flag-stones with which the gallery was paved be so completely loosened that at the desired moment it would give way beneath the feet of the soldier, who, stunned by his fall, would be immediately bound and gagged by Dantès before he had power to offer any resistance. The prisoners were then to make their way through one of the gallery windows, and to let themselves down from the outer walls by means of the abbé’s ladder of cords.\n\nDantès’ eyes sparkled with joy, and he rubbed his hands with delight at the idea of a plan so simple, yet apparently so certain to succeed. That very day the miners began their labors, with a vigor and alacrity proportionate to their long rest from fatigue and their hopes of ultimate success. Nothing interrupted the progress of the work except the necessity that each was under of returning to his cell in anticipation of the turnkey’s visits. They had learned to distinguish the almost imperceptible sound of his footsteps as he descended towards their dungeons, and happily, never failed of being prepared for his coming. The fresh earth excavated during their present work, and which would have entirely blocked up the old passage, was thrown, by degrees and with the utmost precaution, out of the window in either Faria’s or Dantès’ cell, the rubbish being first pulverized so finely that the night wind carried it far away without permitting the smallest trace to remain.\n\nMore than a year had been consumed in this undertaking, the only tools for which had been a chisel, a knife, and a wooden lever; Faria still continuing to instruct Dantès by conversing with him, sometimes in one language, sometimes in another; at others, relating to him the history of nations and great men who from time to time have risen to fame and trodden the path of glory. The abbé was a man of the world, and had, moreover, mixed in the first society of the day; he wore an air of melancholy dignity which Dantès, thanks to the imitative powers bestowed on him by nature, easily acquired, as well as that outward polish and politeness he had before been wanting in, and which is seldom possessed except by those who have been placed in constant intercourse with persons of high birth and breeding.\n\nAt the end of fifteen months the level was finished, and the excavation completed beneath the gallery, and the two workmen could distinctly hear the measured tread of the sentinel as he paced to and fro over their heads. Compelled, as they were, to await a night sufficiently dark to favor their flight, they were obliged to defer their final attempt till that auspicious moment should arrive; their greatest dread now was lest the stone through which the sentry was doomed to fall should give way before its right time, and this they had in some measure provided against by propping it up with a small beam which they had discovered in the walls through which they had worked their way. Dantès was occupied in arranging this piece of wood when he heard Faria, who had remained in Edmond’s cell for the purpose of cutting a peg to secure their rope-ladder, call to him in a tone indicative of great suffering. Dantès hastened to his dungeon, where he found him standing in the middle of the room, pale as death, his forehead streaming with perspiration, and his hands clenched tightly together.\n\n“Gracious heavens!” exclaimed Dantès, “what is the matter? what has happened?”\n\n“Quick! quick!” returned the abbé, “listen to what I have to say.”\n\nDantès looked in fear and wonder at the livid countenance of Faria, whose eyes, already dull and sunken, were surrounded by purple circles, while his lips were white as those of a corpse, and his very hair seemed to stand on end.\n\n“Tell me, I beseech you, what ails you?” cried Dantès, letting his chisel fall to the floor.\n\n“Alas,” faltered out the abbé, “all is over with me. I am seized with a terrible, perhaps mortal illness; I can feel that the paroxysm is fast approaching. I had a similar attack the year previous to my imprisonment. This malady admits but of one remedy; I will tell you what that is. Go into my cell as quickly as you can; draw out one of the feet that support the bed; you will find it has been hollowed out for the purpose of containing a small phial you will see there half-filled with a red-looking fluid. Bring it to me—or rather—no, no!—I may be found here, therefore help me back to my room while I have the strength to drag myself along. Who knows what may happen, or how long the attack may last?”\n\nIn spite of the magnitude of the misfortune which thus suddenly frustrated his hopes, Dantès did not lose his presence of mind, but descended into the passage, dragging his unfortunate companion with him; then, half-carrying, half-supporting him, he managed to reach the abbé’s chamber, when he immediately laid the sufferer on his bed.\n\n“Thanks,” said the poor abbé, shivering as though his veins were filled with ice. “I am about to be seized with a fit of catalepsy; when it comes to its height I shall probably lie still and motionless as though dead, uttering neither sigh nor groan. On the other hand, the symptoms may be much more violent, and cause me to fall into fearful convulsions, foam at the mouth, and cry out loudly. Take care my cries are not heard, for if they are it is more than probable I should be removed to another part of the prison, and we be separated forever. When I become quite motionless, cold, and rigid as a corpse, then, and not before,—be careful about this,—force open my teeth with the knife, pour from eight to ten drops of the liquor contained in the phial down my throat, and I may perhaps revive.”\n\n“Perhaps!” exclaimed Dantès in grief-stricken tones.\n\n“Help! help!” cried the abbé, “I—I—die—I——”\n0229m\n\nSo sudden and violent was the fit that the unfortunate prisoner was unable to complete the sentence; a violent convulsion shook his whole frame, his eyes started from their sockets, his mouth was drawn on one side, his cheeks became purple, he struggled, foamed, dashed himself about, and uttered the most dreadful cries, which, however, Dantès prevented from being heard by covering his head with the blanket. The fit lasted two hours; then, more helpless than an infant, and colder and paler than marble, more crushed and broken than a reed trampled under foot, he fell back, doubled up in one last convulsion, and became as rigid as a corpse.\n\nEdmond waited till life seemed extinct in the body of his friend, then, taking up the knife, he with difficulty forced open the closely fixed jaws, carefully administered the appointed number of drops, and anxiously awaited the result. An hour passed away and the old man gave no sign of returning animation. Dantès began to fear he had delayed too long ere he administered the remedy, and, thrusting his hands into his hair, continued gazing on the lifeless features of his friend. At length a slight color tinged the livid cheeks, consciousness returned to the dull, open eyeballs, a faint sigh issued from the lips, and the sufferer made a feeble effort to move.\n\n“He is saved! he is saved!” cried Dantès in a paroxysm of delight.\n\nThe sick man was not yet able to speak, but he pointed with evident anxiety towards the door. Dantès listened, and plainly distinguished the approaching steps of the jailer. It was therefore near seven o’clock; but Edmond’s anxiety had put all thoughts of time out of his head.\n\nThe young man sprang to the entrance, darted through it, carefully drawing the stone over the opening, and hurried to his cell. He had scarcely done so before the door opened, and the jailer saw the prisoner seated as usual on the side of his bed. Almost before the key had turned in the lock, and before the departing steps of the jailer had died away in the long corridor he had to traverse, Dantès, whose restless anxiety concerning his friend left him no desire to touch the food brought him, hurried back to the abbé’s chamber, and raising the stone by pressing his head against it, was soon beside the sick man’s couch. Faria had now fully regained his consciousness, but he still lay helpless and exhausted on his miserable bed.\n\n“I did not expect to see you again,” said he feebly, to Dantès.\n\n“And why not?” asked the young man. “Did you fancy yourself dying?”\n\n“No, I had no such idea; but, knowing that all was ready for flight, I thought you might have made your escape.”\n\nThe deep glow of indignation suffused the cheeks of Dantès.\n\n“Without you? Did you really think me capable of that?”\n\n“At least,” said the abbé, “I now see how wrong such an opinion would have been. Alas, alas! I am fearfully exhausted and debilitated by this attack.”\n\n“Be of good cheer,” replied Dantès; “your strength will return.” And as he spoke he seated himself near the bed beside Faria, and took his hands. The abbé shook his head.\n\n“The last attack I had,” said he, “lasted but half an hour, and after it I was hungry, and got up without help; now I can move neither my right arm nor leg, and my head seems uncomfortable, which shows that there has been a suffusion of blood on the brain. The third attack will either carry me off, or leave me paralyzed for life.”\n\n“No, no,” cried Dantès; “you are mistaken—you will not die! And your third attack (if, indeed, you should have another) will find you at liberty. We shall save you another time, as we have done this, only with a better chance of success, because we shall be able to command every requisite assistance.”\n\n“My good Edmond,” answered the abbé, “be not deceived. The attack which has just passed away, condemns me forever to the walls of a prison. None can fly from a dungeon who cannot walk.”\n\n“Well, we will wait,—a week, a month, two months, if need be,—and meanwhile your strength will return. Everything is in readiness for our flight, and we can select any time we choose. As soon as you feel able to swim we will go.”\n\n“I shall never swim again,” replied Faria. “This arm is paralyzed; not for a time, but forever. Lift it, and judge if I am mistaken.”\n\nThe young man raised the arm, which fell back by its own weight, perfectly inanimate and helpless. A sigh escaped him.\n\n“You are convinced now, Edmond, are you not?” asked the abbé. “Depend upon it, I know what I say. Since the first attack I experienced of this malady, I have continually reflected on it. Indeed, I expected it, for it is a family inheritance; both my father and grandfather died of it in a third attack. The physician who prepared for me the remedy I have twice successfully taken, was no other than the celebrated Cabanis, and he predicted a similar end for me.”\n0233m\n\n“The physician may be mistaken!” exclaimed Dantès. “And as for your poor arm, what difference will that make? I can take you on my shoulders, and swim for both of us.”\n\n“My son,” said the abbé, “you, who are a sailor and a swimmer, must know as well as I do that a man so loaded would sink before he had done fifty strokes. Cease, then, to allow yourself to be duped by vain hopes, that even your own excellent heart refuses to believe in. Here I shall remain till the hour of my deliverance arrives, and that, in all human probability, will be the hour of my death. As for you, who are young and active, delay not on my account, but fly—go—I give you back your promise.”\n\n“It is well,” said Dantès. “Then I shall also remain.” Then, rising and extending his hand with an air of solemnity over the old man’s head, he slowly added, “By the blood of Christ I swear never to leave you while you live.”\n\nFaria gazed fondly on his noble-minded, single-hearted, high-principled young friend, and read in his countenance ample confirmation of the sincerity of his devotion and the loyalty of his purpose.\n\n“Thanks,” murmured the invalid, extending one hand. “I accept. You may one of these days reap the reward of your disinterested devotion. But as I cannot, and you will not, quit this place, it becomes necessary to fill up the excavation beneath the soldier’s gallery; he might, by chance, hear the hollow sound of his footsteps, and call the attention of his officer to the circumstance. That would bring about a discovery which would inevitably lead to our being separated. Go, then, and set about this work, in which, unhappily, I can offer you no assistance; keep at it all night, if necessary, and do not return here tomorrow till after the jailer has visited me. I shall have something of the greatest importance to communicate to you.”\n\nDantès took the hand of the abbé in his, and affectionately pressed it. Faria smiled encouragingly on him, and the young man retired to his task, in the spirit of obedience and respect which he had sworn to show towards his aged friend.\n\n# Chapter 18. The Treasure\n\nWhen Dantès returned next morning to the chamber of his companion in captivity, he found Faria seated and looking composed. In the ray of light which entered by the narrow window of his cell, he held open in his left hand, of which alone, it will be recollected, he retained the use, a sheet of paper, which, from being constantly rolled into a small compass, had the form of a cylinder, and was not easily kept open. He did not speak, but showed the paper to Dantès.\n\n“What is that?” he inquired.\n\n“Look at it,” said the abbé with a smile.\n\n“I have looked at it with all possible attention,” said Dantès, “and I only see a half-burnt paper, on which are traces of Gothic characters inscribed with a peculiar kind of ink.”\n\n“This paper, my friend,” said Faria, “I may now avow to you, since I have the proof of your fidelity—this paper is my treasure, of which, from this day forth, one-half belongs to you.”\n\nThe sweat started forth on Dantès’ brow. Until this day and for how long a time!—he had refrained from talking of the treasure, which had brought upon the abbé the accusation of madness. With his instinctive delicacy Edmond had preferred avoiding any touch on this painful chord, and Faria had been equally silent. He had taken the silence of the old man for a return to reason; and now these few words uttered by Faria, after so painful a crisis, seemed to indicate a serious relapse into mental alienation.\n\n“Your treasure?” stammered Dantès. Faria smiled.\n\n“Yes,” said he. “You have, indeed, a noble nature, Edmond, and I see by your paleness and agitation what is passing in your heart at this moment. No, be assured, I am not mad. This treasure exists, Dantès, and if I have not been allowed to possess it, you will. Yes—you. No one would listen or believe me, because everyone thought me mad; but you, who must know that I am not, listen to me, and believe me so afterwards if you will.”\n\n“Alas,” murmured Edmond to himself, “this is a terrible relapse! There was only this blow wanting.” Then he said aloud, “My dear friend, your attack has, perhaps, fatigued you; had you not better repose awhile? Tomorrow, if you will, I will hear your narrative; but today I wish to nurse you carefully. Besides,” he said, “a treasure is not a thing we need hurry about.”\n\n“On the contrary, it is a matter of the utmost importance, Edmond!” replied the old man. “Who knows if tomorrow, or the next day after, the third attack may not come on? and then must not all be over? Yes, indeed, I have often thought with a bitter joy that these riches, which would make the wealth of a dozen families, will be forever lost to those men who persecute me. This idea was one of vengeance to me, and I tasted it slowly in the night of my dungeon and the despair of my captivity. But now I have forgiven the world for the love of you; now that I see you, young and with a promising future,—now that I think of all that may result to you in the good fortune of such a disclosure, I shudder at any delay, and tremble lest I should not assure to one as worthy as yourself the possession of so vast an amount of hidden wealth.”\n\nEdmond turned away his head with a sigh.\n\n“You persist in your incredulity, Edmond,” continued Faria. “My words have not convinced you. I see you require proofs. Well, then, read this paper, which I have never shown to anyone.”\n\n“Tomorrow, my dear friend,” said Edmond, desirous of not yielding to the old man’s madness. “I thought it was understood that we should not talk of that until tomorrow.”\n\n“Then we will not talk of it until tomorrow; but read this paper today.”\n\n“I will not irritate him,” thought Edmond, and taking the paper, of which half was wanting,—having been burnt, no doubt, by some accident,—he read:\n\n“this treasure, which may amount to two...\n\nof Roman crowns in the most distant a...\n\nof the second opening wh...\n\ndeclare to belong to him alo...\n\nheir.\n\n“25th April, 149’”\n\n“Well!” said Faria, when the young man had finished reading it.\n\n“Why,” replied Dantès, “I see nothing but broken lines and unconnected words, which are rendered illegible by fire.”\n\n“Yes, to you, my friend, who read them for the first time; but not for me, who have grown pale over them by many nights’ study, and have reconstructed every phrase, completed every thought.”\n\n“And do you believe you have discovered the hidden meaning?”\n\n“I am sure I have, and you shall judge for yourself; but first listen to the history of this paper.”\n\n“Silence!” exclaimed Dantès. “Steps approach—I go—adieu!”\n\nAnd Dantès, happy to escape the history and explanation which would be sure to confirm his belief in his friend’s mental instability, glided like a snake along the narrow passage; while Faria, restored by his alarm to a certain amount of activity, pushed the stone into place with his foot, and covered it with a mat in order the more effectually to avoid discovery.\n\nIt was the governor, who, hearing of Faria’s illness from the jailer, had come in person to see him.\n\nFaria sat up to receive him, avoiding all gestures in order that he might conceal from the governor the paralysis that had already half stricken him with death. His fear was lest the governor, touched with pity, might order him to be removed to better quarters, and thus separate him from his young companion. But fortunately this was not the case, and the governor left him, convinced that the poor madman, for whom in his heart he felt a kind of affection, was only troubled with a slight indisposition.\n\nDuring this time, Edmond, seated on his bed with his head in his hands, tried to collect his scattered thoughts. Faria, since their first acquaintance, had been on all points so rational and logical, so wonderfully sagacious, in fact, that he could not understand how so much wisdom on all points could be allied with madness. Was Faria deceived as to his treasure, or was all the world deceived as to Faria?\n\nDantès remained in his cell all day, not daring to return to his friend, thinking thus to defer the moment when he should be convinced, once for all, that the abbé was mad—such a conviction would be so terrible!\n\nBut, towards the evening after the hour for the customary visit had gone by, Faria, not seeing the young man appear, tried to move and get over the distance which separated them. Edmond shuddered when he heard the painful efforts which the old man made to drag himself along; his leg was inert, and he could no longer make use of one arm. Edmond was obliged to assist him, for otherwise he would not have been able to enter by the small aperture which led to Dantès’ chamber.\n\n“Here I am, pursuing you remorselessly,” he said with a benignant smile. “You thought to escape my munificence, but it is in vain. Listen to me.”\n\nEdmond saw there was no escape, and placing the old man on his bed, he seated himself on the stool beside him.\n\n“You know,” said the abbé, “that I was the secretary and intimate friend of Cardinal Spada, the last of the princes of that name. I owe to this worthy lord all the happiness I ever knew. He was not rich, although the wealth of his family had passed into a proverb, and I heard the phrase very often, ‘As rich as a Spada.’ But he, like public rumor, lived on this reputation for wealth; his palace was my paradise. I was tutor to his nephews, who are dead; and when he was alone in the world, I tried by absolute devotion to his will, to make up to him all he had done for me during ten years of unremitting kindness. The cardinal’s house had no secrets for me. I had often seen my noble patron annotating ancient volumes, and eagerly searching amongst dusty family manuscripts. One day when I was reproaching him for his unavailing searches, and deploring the prostration of mind that followed them, he looked at me, and, smiling bitterly, opened a volume relating to the History of the City of Rome. There, in the twentieth chapter of the Life of Pope Alexander VI., were the following lines, which I can never forget:—\n\n“‘The great wars of Romagna had ended; Cæsar Borgia, who had completed his conquest, had need of money to purchase all Italy. The pope had also need of money to bring matters to an end with Louis XII. King of France, who was formidable still in spite of his recent reverses; and it was necessary, therefore, to have recourse to some profitable scheme, which was a matter of great difficulty in the impoverished condition of exhausted Italy. His holiness had an idea. He determined to make two cardinals.’\n\n“By choosing two of the greatest personages of Rome, especially rich men—_this_ was the return the Holy Father looked for. In the first place, he could sell the great appointments and splendid offices which the cardinals already held; and then he had the two hats to sell besides. There was a third point in view, which will appear hereafter.\n\n“The pope and Cæsar Borgia first found the two future cardinals; they were Giovanni Rospigliosi, who held four of the highest dignities of the Holy See, and Cæsar Spada, one of the noblest and richest of the Roman nobility; both felt the high honor of such a favor from the pope. They were ambitious, and Cæsar Borgia soon found purchasers for their appointments. The result was, that Rospigliosi and Spada paid for being cardinals, and eight other persons paid for the offices the cardinals held before their elevation, and thus eight hundred thousand crowns entered into the coffers of the speculators.\n\n0239m\n“It is time now to proceed to the last part of the speculation. The pope heaped attentions upon Rospigliosi and Spada, conferred upon them the insignia of the cardinalate, and induced them to arrange their affairs and take up their residence at Rome. Then the pope and Cæsar Borgia invited the two cardinals to dinner. This was a matter of dispute between the Holy Father and his son. Cæsar thought they could make use of one of the means which he always had ready for his friends, that is to say, in the first place, the famous key which was given to certain persons with the request that they go and open a designated cupboard. This key was furnished with a small iron point,—a negligence on the part of the locksmith. When this was pressed to effect the opening of the cupboard, of which the lock was difficult, the person was pricked by this small point, and died next day. Then there was the ring with the lion’s head, which Cæsar wore when he wanted to greet his friends with a clasp of the hand. The lion bit the hand thus favored, and at the end of twenty-four hours, the bite was mortal.\n\n“Cæsar proposed to his father, that they should either ask the cardinals to open the cupboard, or shake hands with them; but Alexander VI. replied: ‘Now as to the worthy cardinals, Spada and Rospigliosi, let us ask both of them to dinner, something tells me that we shall get that money back. Besides, you forget, Cæsar, an indigestion declares itself immediately, while a prick or a bite occasions a delay of a day or two.’ Cæsar gave way before such cogent reasoning, and the cardinals were consequently invited to dinner.\n\n“The table was laid in a vineyard belonging to the pope, near San Pierdarena, a charming retreat which the cardinals knew very well by report. Rospigliosi, quite set up with his new dignities, went with a good appetite and his most ingratiating manner. Spada, a prudent man, and greatly attached to his only nephew, a young captain of the highest promise, took paper and pen, and made his will. He then sent word to his nephew to wait for him near the vineyard; but it appeared the servant did not find him.\n\n“Spada knew what these invitations meant; since Christianity, so eminently civilizing, had made progress in Rome, it was no longer a centurion who came from the tyrant with a message, ‘Cæsar wills that you die.’ but it was a legate _à latere_, who came with a smile on his lips to say from the pope, ‘His holiness requests you to dine with him.’\n\n“Spada set out about two o’clock to San Pierdarena. The pope awaited him. The first sight that attracted the eyes of Spada was that of his nephew, in full costume, and Cæsar Borgia paying him most marked attentions. Spada turned pale, as Cæsar looked at him with an ironical air, which proved that he had anticipated all, and that the snare was well spread.\n\n“They began dinner and Spada was only able to inquire of his nephew if he had received his message. The nephew replied no; perfectly comprehending the meaning of the question. It was too late, for he had already drunk a glass of excellent wine, placed for him expressly by the pope’s butler. Spada at the same moment saw another bottle approach him, which he was pressed to taste. An hour afterwards a physician declared they were both poisoned through eating mushrooms. Spada died on the threshold of the vineyard; the nephew expired at his own door, making signs which his wife could not comprehend.\n\n“Then Cæsar and the pope hastened to lay hands on the heritage, under pretense of seeking for the papers of the dead man. But the inheritance consisted in this only, a scrap of paper on which Spada had written:—‘I bequeath to my beloved nephew my coffers, my books, and, amongst others, my breviary with the gold corners, which I beg he will preserve in remembrance of his affectionate uncle.’\n\n“The heirs sought everywhere, admired the breviary, laid hands on the furniture, and were greatly astonished that Spada, the rich man, was really the most miserable of uncles—no treasures—unless they were those of science, contained in the library and laboratories. That was all. Cæsar and his father searched, examined, scrutinized, but found nothing, or at least very little; not exceeding a few thousand crowns in plate, and about the same in ready money; but the nephew had time to say to his wife before he expired: ‘Look well among my uncle’s papers; there is a will.’\n\n“They sought even more thoroughly than the august heirs had done, but it was fruitless. There were two palaces and a vineyard behind the Palatine Hill; but in these days landed property had not much value, and the two palaces and the vineyard remained to the family since they were beneath the rapacity of the pope and his son. Months and years rolled on. Alexander VI. died, poisoned,—you know by what mistake. Cæsar, poisoned at the same time, escaped by shedding his skin like a snake; but the new skin was spotted by the poison till it looked like a tiger’s. Then, compelled to quit Rome, he went and got himself obscurely killed in a night skirmish, scarcely noticed in history.\n\n“After the pope’s death and his son’s exile, it was supposed that the Spada family would resume the splendid position they had held before the cardinal’s time; but this was not the case. The Spadas remained in doubtful ease, a mystery hung over this dark affair, and the public rumor was, that Cæsar, a better politician than his father, had carried off from the pope the fortune of the two cardinals. I say the two, because Cardinal Rospigliosi, who had not taken any precaution, was completely despoiled.\n\n“Up to this point,” said Faria, interrupting the thread of his narrative, “this seems to you very meaningless, no doubt, eh?”\n\n“Oh, my friend,” cried Dantès, “on the contrary, it seems as if I were reading a most interesting narrative; go on, I beg of you.”\n\n“I will. The family began to get accustomed to their obscurity. Years rolled on, and amongst the descendants some were soldiers, others diplomatists; some churchmen, some bankers; some grew rich, and some were ruined. I come now to the last of the family, whose secretary I was—the Count of Spada. I had often heard him complain of the disproportion of his rank with his fortune; and I advised him to invest all he had in an annuity. He did so, and thus doubled his income. The celebrated breviary remained in the family, and was in the count’s possession. It had been handed down from father to son; for the singular clause of the only will that had been found, had caused it to be regarded as a genuine relic, preserved in the family with superstitious veneration. It was an illuminated book, with beautiful Gothic characters, and so weighty with gold, that a servant always carried it before the cardinal on days of great solemnity.\n\n“At the sight of papers of all sorts,—titles, contracts, parchments, which were kept in the archives of the family, all descending from the poisoned cardinal, I in my turn examined the immense bundles of documents, like twenty servitors, stewards, secretaries before me; but in spite of the most exhaustive researches, I found—nothing. Yet I had read, I had even written a precise history of the Borgia family, for the sole purpose of assuring myself whether any increase of fortune had occurred to them on the death of the Cardinal Cæsar Spada; but could only trace the acquisition of the property of the Cardinal Rospigliosi, his companion in misfortune.\n\n“I was then almost assured that the inheritance had neither profited the Borgias nor the family, but had remained unpossessed like the treasures of the Arabian Nights, which slept in the bosom of the earth under the eyes of the genie. I searched, ransacked, counted, calculated a thousand and a thousand times the income and expenditure of the family for three hundred years. It was useless. I remained in my ignorance, and the Count of Spada in his poverty.\n\n“My patron died. He had reserved from his annuity his family papers, his library, composed of five thousand volumes, and his famous breviary. All these he bequeathed to me, with a thousand Roman crowns, which he had in ready money, on condition that I would have anniversary masses said for the repose of his soul, and that I would draw up a genealogical tree and history of his house. All this I did scrupulously. Be easy, my dear Edmond, we are near the conclusion.\n\n“In 1807, a month before I was arrested, and a fortnight after the death of the Count of Spada, on the 25th of December (you will see presently how the date became fixed in my memory), I was reading, for the thousandth time, the papers I was arranging, for the palace was sold to a stranger, and I was going to leave Rome and settle at Florence, intending to take with me twelve thousand francs I possessed, my library, and the famous breviary, when, tired with my constant labor at the same thing, and overcome by a heavy dinner I had eaten, my head dropped on my hands, and I fell asleep about three o’clock in the afternoon.\n\n0243m\n“I awoke as the clock was striking six. I raised my head; I was in utter darkness. I rang for a light, but, as no one came, I determined to find one for myself. It was indeed but anticipating the simple manners which I should soon be under the necessity of adopting. I took a wax-candle in one hand, and with the other groped about for a piece of paper (my match-box being empty), with which I proposed to get a light from the small flame still playing on the embers. Fearing, however, to make use of any valuable piece of paper, I hesitated for a moment, then recollected that I had seen in the famous breviary, which was on the table beside me, an old paper quite yellow with age, and which had served as a marker for centuries, kept there by the request of the heirs. I felt for it, found it, twisted it up together, and putting it into the expiring flame, set light to it.\n\n“But beneath my fingers, as if by magic, in proportion as the fire ascended, I saw yellowish characters appear on the paper. I grasped it in my hand, put out the flame as quickly as I could, lighted my taper in the fire itself, and opened the crumpled paper with inexpressible emotion, recognizing, when I had done so, that these characters had been traced in mysterious and sympathetic ink, only appearing when exposed to the fire; nearly one-third of the paper had been consumed by the flame. It was that paper you read this morning; read it again, Dantès, and then I will complete for you the incomplete words and unconnected sense.”\n\nFaria, with an air of triumph, offered the paper to Dantès, who this time read the following words, traced with an ink of a reddish color resembling rust:\n\n“This 25th day of April, 1498, be...\n\nAlexander VI., and fearing that not...\n\nhe may desire to become my heir, and re...\n\nand Bentivoglio, who were poisoned,...\n\nmy sole heir, that I have bu...\n\nand has visited with me, that is, in...\n\nIsland of Monte Cristo, all I poss...\n\njewels, diamonds, gems; that I alone...\n\nmay amount to nearly two mil...\n\nwill find on raising the twentieth ro...\n\ncreek to the east in a right line. Two open...\n\nin these caves; the treasure is in the furthest a...\n\nwhich treasure I bequeath and leave en...\n\nas my sole heir.\n\n“25th April, 1498.\n\n“Cæs...\n\n“And now,” said the abbé, “read this other paper;” and he presented to Dantès a second leaf with fragments of lines written on it, which Edmond read as follows:\n\n“...ing invited to dine by his Holiness\n\n...content with making me pay for my hat,\n\n...serves for me the fate of Cardinals Caprara\n\n...I declare to my nephew, Guido Spada\n\n...ried in a place he knows\n\n...the caves of the small\n\n...essed of ingots, gold, money,\n\n...know of the existence of this treasure, which\n\n...lions of Roman crowns, and which he\n\n...ck from the small\n\n...ings have been made\n\n...ngle in the second;\n\n...tire to him\n\n...ar † Spada.”\n\nFaria followed him with an excited look.\n\n“And now,” he said, when he saw that Dantès had read the last line, “put the two fragments together, and judge for yourself.” Dantès obeyed, and the conjointed pieces gave the following:\n\n0245m\n“This 25th day of April, 1498, be...ing invited to dine by his Holiness Alexander VI., and fearing that not...content with making me pay for my hat, he may desire to become my heir, and re...serves for me the fate of Cardinals Caprara and Bentivoglio, who were poisoned,...I declare to my nephew, Guido Spada, my sole heir, that I have bu...ried in a place he knows and has visited with me, that is, in...the caves of the small Island of Monte Cristo, all I poss...essed of ingots, gold, money, jewels, diamonds, gems; that I alone...know of the existence of this treasure, which may amount to nearly two mil...lions of Roman crowns, and which he will find on raising the twentieth ro...ck from the small creek to the east in a right line. Two open...ings have been made in these caves; the treasure is in the furthest a...ngle in the second; which treasure I bequeath and leave en...tire to him as my sole heir. “25th April, 1498. “Cæs...ar † Spada.”\n\n“Well, do you comprehend now?” inquired Faria.\n\n“It is the declaration of Cardinal Spada, and the will so long sought for,” replied Edmond, still incredulous.\n\n“Yes; a thousand times, yes!”\n\n“And who completed it as it now is?”\n\n“I did. Aided by the remaining fragment, I guessed the rest; measuring the length of the lines by those of the paper, and divining the hidden meaning by means of what was in part revealed, as we are guided in a cavern by the small ray of light above us.”\n\n“And what did you do when you arrived at this conclusion?”\n\n“I resolved to set out, and did set out at that very instant, carrying with me the beginning of my great work, the unity of the Italian kingdom; but for some time the imperial police (who at this period, quite contrary to what Napoleon desired so soon as he had a son born to him, wished for a partition of provinces) had their eyes on me; and my hasty departure, the cause of which they were unable to guess, having aroused their suspicions, I was arrested at the very moment I was leaving Piombino.\n\n“Now,” continued Faria, addressing Dantès with an almost paternal expression, “now, my dear fellow, you know as much as I do myself. If we ever escape together, half this treasure is yours; if I die here, and you escape alone, the whole belongs to you.”\n\n“But,” inquired Dantès hesitating, “has this treasure no more legitimate possessor in the world than ourselves?”\n\n“No, no, be easy on that score; the family is extinct. The last Count of Spada, moreover, made me his heir, bequeathing to me this symbolic breviary, he bequeathed to me all it contained; no, no, make your mind satisfied on that point. If we lay hands on this fortune, we may enjoy it without remorse.”\n\n“And you say this treasure amounts to——”\n\n“Two millions of Roman crowns; nearly thirteen millions of our money.”2\n\n“Impossible!” said Dantès, staggered at the enormous amount.\n\n“Impossible? and why?” asked the old man. “The Spada family was one of the oldest and most powerful families of the fifteenth century; and in those times, when other opportunities for investment were wanting, such accumulations of gold and jewels were by no means rare; there are at this day Roman families perishing of hunger, though possessed of nearly a million in diamonds and jewels, handed down by entail, and which they cannot touch.”\n\nEdmond thought he was in a dream—he wavered between incredulity and joy.\n\n“I have only kept this secret so long from you,” continued Faria, “that I might test your character, and then surprise you. Had we escaped before my attack of catalepsy, I should have conducted you to Monte Cristo; now,” he added, with a sigh, “it is you who will conduct me thither. Well, Dantès, you do not thank me?”\n\n“This treasure belongs to you, my dear friend,” replied Dantès, “and to you only. I have no right to it. I am no relation of yours.”\n\n“You are my son, Dantès,” exclaimed the old man. “You are the child of my captivity. My profession condemns me to celibacy. God has sent you to me to console, at one and the same time, the man who could not be a father, and the prisoner who could not get free.”\n\nAnd Faria extended the arm of which alone the use remained to him to the young man, who threw himself upon his neck and wept.\n\n# Chapter 19. The Third Attack\n\nNow that this treasure, which had so long been the object of the abbé’s meditations, could insure the future happiness of him whom Faria really loved as a son, it had doubled its value in his eyes, and every day he expatiated on the amount, explaining to Dantès all the good which, with thirteen or fourteen millions of francs, a man could do in these days to his friends; and then Dantès’ countenance became gloomy, for the oath of vengeance he had taken recurred to his memory, and he reflected how much ill, in these times, a man with thirteen or fourteen millions could do to his enemies.\n\nThe abbé did not know the Island of Monte Cristo; but Dantès knew it, and had often passed it, situated twenty-five miles from Pianosa, between Corsica and the Island of Elba, and had once touched there. This island was, always had been, and still is, completely deserted. It is a rock of almost conical form, which looks as though it had been thrust up by volcanic force from the depth to the surface of the ocean. Dantès drew a plan of the island for Faria, and Faria gave Dantès advice as to the means he should employ to recover the treasure. But Dantès was far from being as enthusiastic and confident as the old man. It was past a question now that Faria was not a lunatic, and the way in which he had achieved the discovery, which had given rise to the suspicion of his madness, increased Edmond’s admiration of him; but at the same time Dantès could not believe that the deposit, supposing it had ever existed, still existed; and though he considered the treasure as by no means chimerical, he yet believed it was no longer there.\n\nHowever, as if fate resolved on depriving the prisoners of their last chance, and making them understand that they were condemned to perpetual imprisonment, a new misfortune befell them; the gallery on the sea side, which had long been in ruins, was rebuilt. They had repaired it completely, and stopped up with vast masses of stone the hole Dantès had partly filled in. But for this precaution, which, it will be remembered, the abbé had made to Edmond, the misfortune would have been still greater, for their attempt to escape would have been detected, and they would undoubtedly have been separated. Thus a new, a stronger, and more inexorable barrier was interposed to cut off the realization of their hopes.\n\n“You see,” said the young man, with an air of sorrowful resignation, to Faria, “that God deems it right to take from me any claim to merit for what you call my devotion to you. I have promised to remain forever with you, and now I could not break my promise if I would. The treasure will be no more mine than yours, and neither of us will quit this prison. But my real treasure is not that, my dear friend, which awaits me beneath the sombre rocks of Monte Cristo, it is your presence, our living together five or six hours a day, in spite of our jailers; it is the rays of intelligence you have elicited from my brain, the languages you have implanted in my memory, and which have taken root there with all their philological ramifications. These different sciences that you have made so easy to me by the depth of the knowledge you possess of them, and the clearness of the principles to which you have reduced them—this is my treasure, my beloved friend, and with this you have made me rich and happy. Believe me, and take comfort, this is better for me than tons of gold and cases of diamonds, even were they not as problematical as the clouds we see in the morning floating over the sea, which we take for _terra firma_, and which evaporate and vanish as we draw near to them. To have you as long as possible near me, to hear your eloquent speech,—which embellishes my mind, strengthens my soul, and makes my whole frame capable of great and terrible things, if I should ever be free,—so fills my whole existence, that the despair to which I was just on the point of yielding when I knew you, has no longer any hold over me; and this—this is my fortune—not chimerical, but actual. I owe you my real good, my present happiness; and all the sovereigns of the earth, even Cæsar Borgia himself, could not deprive me of this.”\n\nThus, if not actually happy, yet the days these two unfortunates passed together went quickly. Faria, who for so long a time had kept silence as to the treasure, now perpetually talked of it. As he had prophesied would be the case, he remained paralyzed in the right arm and the left leg, and had given up all hope of ever enjoying it himself. But he was continually thinking over some means of escape for his young companion, and anticipating the pleasure he would enjoy. For fear the letter might be some day lost or stolen, he compelled Dantès to learn it by heart; and Dantès knew it from the first to the last word. Then he destroyed the second portion, assured that if the first were seized, no one would be able to discover its real meaning. Whole hours sometimes passed while Faria was giving instructions to Dantès,—instructions which were to serve him when he was at liberty. Then, once free, from the day and hour and moment when he was so, he could have but one only thought, which was, to gain Monte Cristo by some means, and remain there alone under some pretext which would arouse no suspicions; and once there, to endeavor to find the wonderful caverns, and search in the appointed spot,—the appointed spot, be it remembered, being the farthest angle in the second opening.\n\nIn the meanwhile the hours passed, if not rapidly, at least tolerably. Faria, as we have said, without having recovered the use of his hand and foot, had regained all the clearness of his understanding, and had gradually, besides the moral instructions we have detailed, taught his youthful companion the patient and sublime duty of a prisoner, who learns to make something from nothing. They were thus perpetually employed,—Faria, that he might not see himself grow old; Dantès, for fear of recalling the almost extinct past which now only floated in his memory like a distant light wandering in the night. So life went on for them as it does for those who are not victims of misfortune and whose activities glide along mechanically and tranquilly beneath the eye of Providence.\n\nBut beneath this superficial calm there were in the heart of the young man, and perhaps in that of the old man, many repressed desires, many stifled sighs, which found vent when Faria was left alone, and when Edmond returned to his cell.\n\nOne night Edmond awoke suddenly, believing that he heard someone calling him. He opened his eyes upon utter darkness. His name, or rather a plaintive voice which essayed to pronounce his name, reached him. He sat up in bed and a cold sweat broke out upon his brow. Undoubtedly the call came from Faria’s dungeon.\n\n“Alas,” murmured Edmond; “can it be?”\n\nHe moved his bed, drew up the stone, rushed into the passage, and reached the opposite extremity; the secret entrance was open. By the light of the wretched and wavering lamp, of which we have spoken, Dantès saw the old man, pale, but yet erect, clinging to the bedstead. His features were writhing with those horrible symptoms which he already knew, and which had so seriously alarmed him when he saw them for the first time.\n\n“Alas, my dear friend,” said Faria in a resigned tone, “you understand, do you not, and I need not attempt to explain to you?”\n\nEdmond uttered a cry of agony, and, quite out of his senses, rushed towards the door, exclaiming, “Help, help!”\n\nFaria had just sufficient strength to restrain him.\n\n“Silence,” he said, “or you are lost. We must now only think of you, my dear friend, and so act as to render your captivity supportable or your flight possible. It would require years to do again what I have done here, and the results would be instantly destroyed if our jailers knew we had communicated with each other. Besides, be assured, my dear Edmond, the dungeon I am about to leave will not long remain empty; some other unfortunate being will soon take my place, and to him you will appear like an angel of salvation. Perhaps he will be young, strong, and enduring, like yourself, and will aid you in your escape, while I have been but a hindrance. You will no longer have half a dead body tied to you as a drag to all your movements. At length Providence has done something for you; he restores to you more than he takes away, and it was time I should die.”\n\nEdmond could only clasp his hands and exclaim, “Oh, my friend, my friend, speak not thus!” and then resuming all his presence of mind, which had for a moment staggered under this blow, and his strength, which had failed at the words of the old man, he said, “Oh, I have saved you once, and I will save you a second time!” And raising the foot of the bed, he drew out the phial, still a third filled with the red liquor.\n\n“See,” he exclaimed, “there remains still some of the magic draught. Quick, quick! tell me what I must do this time; are there any fresh instructions? Speak, my friend; I listen.”\n\n“There is not a hope,” replied Faria, shaking his head, “but no matter; God wills it that man whom he has created, and in whose heart he has so profoundly rooted the love of life, should do all in his power to preserve that existence, which, however painful it may be, is yet always so dear.”\n\n“Oh, yes, yes!” exclaimed Dantès; “and I tell you that I will save you yet.”\n\n“Well, then, try. The cold gains upon me. I feel the blood flowing towards my brain. These horrible chills, which make my teeth chatter and seem to dislocate my bones, begin to pervade my whole frame; in five minutes the malady will reach its height, and in a quarter of an hour there will be nothing left of me but a corpse.”\n\n“Oh!” exclaimed Dantès, his heart wrung with anguish.\n\n“Do as you did before, only do not wait so long, all the springs of life are now exhausted in me, and death,” he continued, looking at his paralyzed arm and leg, “has but half its work to do. If, after having made me swallow twelve drops instead of ten, you see that I do not recover, then pour the rest down my throat. Now lift me on my bed, for I can no longer support myself.”\n\nEdmond took the old man in his arms, and laid him on the bed.\n\n“And now, my dear friend,” said Faria, “sole consolation of my wretched existence,—you whom Heaven gave me somewhat late, but still gave me, a priceless gift, and for which I am most grateful,—at the moment of separating from you forever, I wish you all the happiness and all the prosperity you so well deserve. My son, I bless thee!”\n\nThe young man cast himself on his knees, leaning his head against the old man’s bed.\n\n“Listen, now, to what I say in this my dying moment. The treasure of the Spadas exists. God grants me the boon of vision unrestricted by time or space. I see it in the depths of the inner cavern. My eyes pierce the inmost recesses of the earth, and are dazzled at the sight of so much riches. If you do escape, remember that the poor abbé, whom all the world called mad, was not so. Hasten to Monte Cristo—avail yourself of the fortune—for you have indeed suffered long enough.”\n\nA violent convulsion attacked the old man. Dantès raised his head and saw Faria’s eyes injected with blood. It seemed as if a flow of blood had ascended from the chest to the head.\n\n“Adieu, adieu!” murmured the old man, clasping Edmond’s hand convulsively—“adieu!”\n\n“Oh, no,—no, not yet,” he cried; “do not forsake me! Oh, succor him! Help—help—help!”\n\n“Hush! hush!” murmured the dying man, “that they may not separate us if you save me!”\n\n“You are right. Oh, yes, yes; be assured I shall save you! Besides, although you suffer much, you do not seem to be in such agony as you were before.”\n\n“Do not mistake! I suffer less because there is in me less strength to endure. At your age we have faith in life; it is the privilege of youth to believe and hope, but old men see death more clearly. Oh, ’tis here—’tis here—’tis over—my sight is gone—my senses fail! Your hand, Dantès! Adieu! adieu!”\n\nAnd raising himself by a final effort, in which he summoned all his faculties, he said,—“Monte Cristo, forget not Monte Cristo!” And he fell back on the bed.\n\nThe crisis was terrible, and a rigid form with twisted limbs, swollen eyelids, and lips flecked with bloody foam, lay on the bed of torture, in place of the intellectual being who so lately rested there.\n\nDantès took the lamp, placed it on a projecting stone above the bed, whence its tremulous light fell with strange and fantastic ray on the distorted countenance and motionless, stiffened body. With steady gaze he awaited confidently the moment for administering the restorative.\n\nWhen he believed that the right moment had arrived, he took the knife, pried open the teeth, which offered less resistance than before, counted one after the other twelve drops, and watched; the phial contained, perhaps, twice as much more. He waited ten minutes, a quarter of an hour, half an hour,—no change took place. Trembling, his hair erect, his brow bathed with perspiration, he counted the seconds by the beating of his heart. Then he thought it was time to make the last trial, and he put the phial to the purple lips of Faria, and without having occasion to force open his jaws, which had remained extended, he poured the whole of the liquid down his throat.\n0255m\n\nThe draught produced a galvanic effect, a violent trembling pervaded the old man’s limbs, his eyes opened until it was fearful to gaze upon them, he heaved a sigh which resembled a shriek, and then his convulsed body returned gradually to its former immobility, the eyes remaining open.\n\nHalf an hour, an hour, an hour and a half elapsed, and during this period of anguish, Edmond leaned over his friend, his hand applied to his heart, and felt the body gradually grow cold, and the heart’s pulsation become more and more deep and dull, until at length it stopped; the last movement of the heart ceased, the face became livid, the eyes remained open, but the eyeballs were glazed.\n\nIt was six o’clock in the morning, the dawn was just breaking, and its feeble ray came into the dungeon, and paled the ineffectual light of the lamp. Strange shadows passed over the countenance of the dead man, and at times gave it the appearance of life. While the struggle between day and night lasted, Dantès still doubted; but as soon as the daylight gained the pre-eminence, he saw that he was alone with a corpse. Then an invincible and extreme terror seized upon him, and he dared not again press the hand that hung out of bed, he dared no longer to gaze on those fixed and vacant eyes, which he tried many times to close, but in vain—they opened again as soon as shut. He extinguished the lamp, carefully concealed it, and then went away, closing as well as he could the entrance to the secret passage by the large stone as he descended.\n\nIt was time, for the jailer was coming. On this occasion he began his rounds at Dantès’ cell, and on leaving him he went on to Faria’s dungeon, taking thither breakfast and some linen. Nothing betokened that the man knew anything of what had occurred. He went on his way.\n\nDantès was then seized with an indescribable desire to know what was going on in the dungeon of his unfortunate friend. He therefore returned by the subterraneous gallery, and arrived in time to hear the exclamations of the turnkey, who called out for help. Other turnkeys came, and then was heard the regular tramp of soldiers. Last of all came the governor.\n\nEdmond heard the creaking of the bed as they moved the corpse, heard the voice of the governor, who asked them to throw water on the dead man’s face; and seeing that, in spite of this application, the prisoner did not recover, they sent for the doctor. The governor then went out, and words of pity fell on Dantès’ listening ears, mingled with brutal laughter.\n\n“Well, well,” said one, “the madman has gone to look after his treasure. Good journey to him!”\n\n“With all his millions, he will not have enough to pay for his shroud!” said another.\n\n“Oh,” added a third voice, “the shrouds of the Château d’If are not dear!”\n\n0257m\n“Perhaps,” said one of the previous speakers, “as he was a churchman, they may go to some expense in his behalf.”\n\n“They may give him the honors of the sack.”\n\nEdmond did not lose a word, but comprehended very little of what was said. The voices soon ceased, and it seemed to him as if everyone had left the cell. Still he dared not to enter, as they might have left some turnkey to watch the dead. He remained, therefore, mute and motionless, hardly venturing to breathe. At the end of an hour, he heard a faint noise, which increased. It was the governor who returned, followed by the doctor and other attendants. There was a moment’s silence,—it was evident that the doctor was examining the dead body. The inquiries soon commenced.\n\nThe doctor analyzed the symptoms of the malady to which the prisoner had succumbed, and declared that he was dead. Questions and answers followed in a nonchalant manner that made Dantès indignant, for he felt that all the world should have for the poor abbé a love and respect equal to his own.\n\n“I am very sorry for what you tell me,” said the governor, replying to the assurance of the doctor, “that the old man is really dead; for he was a quiet, inoffensive prisoner, happy in his folly, and required no watching.”\n\n“Ah,” added the turnkey, “there was no occasion for watching him; he would have stayed here fifty years, I’ll answer for it, without any attempt to escape.”\n\n“Still,” said the governor, “I believe it will be requisite, notwithstanding your certainty, and not that I doubt your science, but in discharge of my official duty, that we should be perfectly assured that the prisoner is dead.”\n\nThere was a moment of complete silence, during which Dantès, still listening, knew that the doctor was examining the corpse a second time.\n\n“You may make your mind easy,” said the doctor; “he is dead. I will answer for that.”\n\n“You know, sir,” said the governor, persisting, “that we are not content in such cases as this with such a simple examination. In spite of all appearances, be so kind, therefore, as to finish your duty by fulfilling the formalities described by law.”\n\n“Let the irons be heated,” said the doctor; “but really it is a useless precaution.”\n\nThis order to heat the irons made Dantès shudder. He heard hasty steps, the creaking of a door, people going and coming, and some minutes afterwards a turnkey entered, saying:\n\n“Here is the brazier, lighted.”\n\nThere was a moment’s silence, and then was heard the crackling of burning flesh, of which the peculiar and nauseous smell penetrated even behind the wall where Dantès was listening in horror. The perspiration poured forth upon the young man’s brow, and he felt as if he should faint.\n\n“You see, sir, he is really dead,” said the doctor; “this burn in the heel is decisive. The poor fool is cured of his folly, and delivered from his captivity.”\n\n“Wasn’t his name Faria?” inquired one of the officers who accompanied the governor.\n\n0259m\n“Yes, sir; and, as he said, it was an ancient name. He was, too, very learned, and rational enough on all points which did not relate to his treasure; but on that, indeed, he was intractable.”\n\n“It is the sort of malady which we call monomania,” said the doctor.\n\n“You had never anything to complain of?” said the governor to the jailer who had charge of the abbé.\n\n“Never, sir,” replied the jailer, “never; on the contrary, he sometimes amused me very much by telling me stories. One day, too, when my wife was ill, he gave me a prescription which cured her.”\n\n“Ah, ah!” said the doctor, “I did not know that I had a rival; but I hope, governor, that you will show him all proper respect in consequence.”\n\n“Yes, yes, make your mind easy, he shall be decently interred in the newest sack we can find. Will that satisfy you?”\n\n“Must this last formality take place in your presence, sir?” inquired a turnkey.\n\n“Certainly. But make haste—I cannot stay here all day.” Other footsteps, going and coming, were now heard, and a moment afterwards the noise of rustling canvas reached Dantès’ ears, the bed creaked, and the heavy footfall of a man who lifts a weight sounded on the floor; then the bed again creaked under the weight deposited upon it.\n\n“This evening,” said the governor.\n\n“Will there be any mass?” asked one of the attendants.\n\n“That is impossible,” replied the governor. “The chaplain of the château came to me yesterday to beg for leave of absence, in order to take a trip to Hyères for a week. I told him I would attend to the prisoners in his absence. If the poor abbé had not been in such a hurry, he might have had his requiem.”\n\n“Pooh, pooh;” said the doctor, with the impiety usual in persons of his profession; “he is a churchman. God will respect his profession, and not give the devil the wicked delight of sending him a priest.” A shout of laughter followed this brutal jest. Meanwhile the operation of putting the body in the sack was going on.\n\n“This evening,” said the governor, when the task was ended.\n\n“At what hour?” inquired a turnkey.\n\n“Why, about ten or eleven o’clock.”\n\n“Shall we watch by the corpse?”\n\n“Of what use would it be? Shut the dungeon as if he were alive—that is all.”\n\nThen the steps retreated, and the voices died away in the distance; the noise of the door, with its creaking hinges and bolts ceased, and a silence more sombre than that of solitude ensued,—the silence of death, which was all-pervasive, and struck its icy chill to the very soul of Dantès.\n\nThen he raised the flag-stone cautiously with his head, and looked carefully around the chamber. It was empty, and Dantès emerged from the tunnel.\n\n# Chapter 20. The Cemetery of the Château d’If\n\nOn the bed, at full length, and faintly illuminated by the pale light that came from the window, lay a sack of canvas, and under its rude folds was stretched a long and stiffened form; it was Faria’s last winding-sheet,—a winding-sheet which, as the turnkey said, cost so little. Everything was in readiness. A barrier had been placed between Dantès and his old friend. No longer could Edmond look into those wide-open eyes which had seemed to be penetrating the mysteries of death; no longer could he clasp the hand which had done so much to make his existence blessed. Faria, the beneficent and cheerful companion, with whom he was accustomed to live so intimately, no longer breathed. He seated himself on the edge of that terrible bed, and fell into melancholy and gloomy reverie.\n\nAlone! he was alone again! again condemned to silence—again face to face with nothingness! Alone!—never again to see the face, never again to hear the voice of the only human being who united him to earth! Was not Faria’s fate the better, after all—to solve the problem of life at its source, even at the risk of horrible suffering?\n\nThe idea of suicide, which his friend had driven away and kept away by his cheerful presence, now hovered like a phantom over the abbé’s dead body.\n\n“If I could die,” he said, “I should go where he goes, and should assuredly find him again. But how to die? It is very easy,” he went on with a smile; “I will remain here, rush on the first person that opens the door, strangle him, and then they will guillotine me.”\n\nBut excessive grief is like a storm at sea, where the frail bark is tossed from the depths to the top of the wave. Dantès recoiled from the idea of so infamous a death, and passed suddenly from despair to an ardent desire for life and liberty.\n\n“Die? oh, no,” he exclaimed—“not die now, after having lived and suffered so long and so much! Die? yes, had I died years ago; but now to die would be, indeed, to give way to the sarcasm of destiny. No, I want to live; I shall struggle to the very last; I will yet win back the happiness of which I have been deprived. Before I die I must not forget that I have my executioners to punish, and perhaps, too, who knows, some friends to reward. Yet they will forget me here, and I shall die in my dungeon like Faria.”\n\nAs he said this, he became silent and gazed straight before him like one overwhelmed with a strange and amazing thought. Suddenly he arose, lifted his hand to his brow as if his brain were giddy, paced twice or thrice round the dungeon, and then paused abruptly by the bed.\n\n“Just God!” he muttered, “whence comes this thought? Is it from thee? Since none but the dead pass freely from this dungeon, let me take the place of the dead!”\n\nWithout giving himself time to reconsider his decision, and, indeed, that he might not allow his thoughts to be distracted from his desperate resolution, he bent over the appalling shroud, opened it with the knife which Faria had made, drew the corpse from the sack, and bore it along the tunnel to his own chamber, laid it on his couch, tied around its head the rag he wore at night around his own, covered it with his counterpane, once again kissed the ice-cold brow, and tried vainly to close the resisting eyes, which glared horribly, turned the head towards the wall, so that the jailer might, when he brought the evening meal, believe that he was asleep, as was his frequent custom; entered the tunnel again, drew the bed against the wall, returned to the other cell, took from the hiding-place the needle and thread, flung off his rags, that they might feel only naked flesh beneath the coarse canvas, and getting inside the sack, placed himself in the posture in which the dead body had been laid, and sewed up the mouth of the sack from the inside.\n\nHe would have been discovered by the beating of his heart, if by any mischance the jailers had entered at that moment. Dantès might have waited until the evening visit was over, but he was afraid that the governor would change his mind, and order the dead body to be removed earlier. In that case his last hope would have been destroyed.\n\nNow his plans were fully made, and this is what he intended to do. If while he was being carried out the grave-diggers should discover that they were bearing a live instead of a dead body, Dantès did not intend to give them time to recognize him, but with a sudden cut of the knife, he meant to open the sack from top to bottom, and, profiting by their alarm, escape; if they tried to catch him, he would use his knife to better purpose.\n\n0263m\nIf they took him to the cemetery and laid him in a grave, he would allow himself to be covered with earth, and then, as it was night, the grave-diggers could scarcely have turned their backs before he would have worked his way through the yielding soil and escaped. He hoped that the weight of earth would not be so great that he could not overcome it. If he was detected in this and the earth proved too heavy, he would be stifled, and then—so much the better, all would be over.\n\nDantès had not eaten since the preceding evening, but he had not thought of hunger, nor did he think of it now. His situation was too precarious to allow him even time to reflect on any thought but one.\n\nThe first risk that Dantès ran was, that the jailer, when he brought him his supper at seven o’clock, might perceive the change that had been made; fortunately, twenty times at least, from misanthropy or fatigue, Dantès had received his jailer in bed, and then the man placed his bread and soup on the table, and went away without saying a word. This time the jailer might not be as silent as usual, but speak to Dantès, and seeing that he received no reply, go to the bed, and thus discover all.\n\nWhen seven o’clock came, Dantès’ agony really began. His hand placed upon his heart was unable to redress its throbbings, while, with the other he wiped the perspiration from his temples. From time to time chills ran through his whole body, and clutched his heart in a grasp of ice. Then he thought he was going to die. Yet the hours passed on without any unusual disturbance, and Dantès knew that he had escaped the first peril. It was a good augury.\n\nAt length, about the hour the governor had appointed, footsteps were heard on the stairs. Edmond felt that the moment had arrived, summoned up all his courage, held his breath, and would have been happy if at the same time he could have repressed the throbbing of his veins. The footsteps—they were double—paused at the door—and Dantès guessed that the two grave-diggers had come to seek him—this idea was soon converted into certainty, when he heard the noise they made in putting down the hand-bier.\n\nThe door opened, and a dim light reached Dantès’ eyes through the coarse sack that covered him; he saw two shadows approach his bed, a third remaining at the door with a torch in its hand. The two men, approaching the ends of the bed, took the sack by its extremities.\n\n“He’s heavy, though, for an old and thin man,” said one, as he raised the head.\n\n“They say every year adds half a pound to the weight of the bones,” said another, lifting the feet.\n\n“Have you tied the knot?” inquired the first speaker.\n\n“What would be the use of carrying so much more weight?” was the reply, “I can do that when we get there.”\n\n“Yes, you’re right,” replied the companion.\n\n“What’s the knot for?” thought Dantès.\n\nThey deposited the supposed corpse on the bier. Edmond stiffened himself in order to play the part of a dead man, and then the party, lighted by the man with the torch, who went first, ascended the stairs. Suddenly he felt the fresh and sharp night air, and Dantès knew that the mistral was blowing. It was a sensation in which pleasure and pain were strangely mingled.\n\nThe bearers went on for twenty paces, then stopped, putting the bier down on the ground. One of them went away, and Dantès heard his shoes striking on the pavement.\n\n“Where am I?” he asked himself.\n\n“Really, he is by no means a light load!” said the other bearer, sitting on the edge of the hand-barrow.\n\nDantès’ first impulse was to escape, but fortunately he did not attempt it.\n\n“Give us a light,” said the other bearer, “or I shall never find what I am looking for.”\n\nThe man with the torch complied, although not asked in the most polite terms.\n\n“What can he be looking for?” thought Edmond. “The spade, perhaps.”\n\nAn exclamation of satisfaction indicated that the grave-digger had found the object of his search. “Here it is at last,” he said, “not without some trouble, though.”\n\n“Yes,” was the answer, “but it has lost nothing by waiting.”\n\nAs he said this, the man came towards Edmond, who heard a heavy metallic substance laid down beside him, and at the same moment a cord was fastened round his feet with sudden and painful violence.\n\n“Well, have you tied the knot?” inquired the grave-digger, who was looking on.\n\n“Yes, and pretty tight too, I can tell you,” was the answer.\n\n“Move on, then.” And the bier was lifted once more, and they proceeded.\n\nThey advanced fifty paces farther, and then stopped to open a door, then went forward again. The noise of the waves dashing against the rocks on which the château is built, reached Dantès’ ear distinctly as they went forward.\n\n“Bad weather!” observed one of the bearers; “not a pleasant night for a dip in the sea.”\n\n“Why, yes, the abbé runs a chance of being wet,” said the other; and then there was a burst of brutal laughter.\n\nDantès did not comprehend the jest, but his hair stood erect on his head.\n\n“Well, here we are at last,” said one of them.\n\n“A little farther—a little farther,” said the other. “You know very well that the last was stopped on his way, dashed on the rocks, and the governor told us next day that we were careless fellows.”\n\nThey ascended five or six more steps, and then Dantès felt that they took him, one by the head and the other by the heels, and swung him to and fro.\n\n“One!” said the grave-diggers, “two! three!”\n\nAnd at the same instant Dantès felt himself flung into the air like a wounded bird, falling, falling, with a rapidity that made his blood curdle. Although drawn downwards by the heavy weight which hastened his rapid descent, it seemed to him as if the fall lasted for a century. At last, with a horrible splash, he darted like an arrow into the ice-cold water, and as he did so he uttered a shrill cry, stifled in a moment by his immersion beneath the waves.\n\nDantès had been flung into the sea, and was dragged into its depths by a thirty-six-pound shot tied to his feet.\n\nThe sea is the cemetery of the Château d’If.\n\n# Chapter 21. The Island of Tiboulen\n\n0267m\nDantès, although stunned and almost suffocated, had sufficient presence of mind to hold his breath, and as his right hand (prepared as he was for every chance) held his knife open, he rapidly ripped up the sack, extricated his arm, and then his body; but in spite of all his efforts to free himself from the shot, he felt it dragging him down still lower. He then bent his body, and by a desperate effort severed the cord that bound his legs, at the moment when it seemed as if he were actually strangled. With a mighty leap he rose to the surface of the sea, while the shot dragged down to the depths the sack that had so nearly become his shroud.\n\nDantès waited only to get breath, and then dived, in order to avoid being seen. When he arose a second time, he was fifty paces from where he had first sunk. He saw overhead a black and tempestuous sky, across which the wind was driving clouds that occasionally suffered a twinkling star to appear; before him was the vast expanse of waters, sombre and terrible, whose waves foamed and roared as if before the approach of a storm. Behind him, blacker than the sea, blacker than the sky, rose phantom-like the vast stone structure, whose projecting crags seemed like arms extended to seize their prey, and on the highest rock was a torch lighting two figures.\n\nHe fancied that these two forms were looking at the sea; doubtless these strange grave-diggers had heard his cry. Dantès dived again, and remained a long time beneath the water. This was an easy feat to him, for he usually attracted a crowd of spectators in the bay before the lighthouse at Marseilles when he swam there, and was unanimously declared to be the best swimmer in the port. When he came up again the light had disappeared.\n\nHe must now get his bearings. Ratonneau and Pomègue are the nearest islands of all those that surround the Château d’If, but Ratonneau and Pomègue are inhabited, as is also the islet of Daume. Tiboulen and Lemaire were therefore the safest for Dantès’ venture. The islands of Tiboulen and Lemaire are a league from the Château d’If; Dantès, nevertheless, determined to make for them. But how could he find his way in the darkness of the night?\n\nAt this moment he saw the light of Planier, gleaming in front of him like a star. By leaving this light on the right, he kept the Island of Tiboulen a little on the left; by turning to the left, therefore, he would find it. But, as we have said, it was at least a league from the Château d’If to this island. Often in prison Faria had said to him, when he saw him idle and inactive:\n\n“Dantès, you must not give way to this listlessness; you will be drowned if you seek to escape, and your strength has not been properly exercised and prepared for exertion.”\n\nThese words rang in Dantès’ ears, even beneath the waves; he hastened to cleave his way through them to see if he had not lost his strength. He found with pleasure that his captivity had taken away nothing of his power, and that he was still master of that element on whose bosom he had so often sported as a boy.\n\nFear, that relentless pursuer, clogged Dantès’ efforts. He listened for any sound that might be audible, and every time that he rose to the top of a wave he scanned the horizon, and strove to penetrate the darkness. He fancied that every wave behind him was a pursuing boat, and he redoubled his exertions, increasing rapidly his distance from the château, but exhausting his strength. He swam on still, and already the terrible château had disappeared in the darkness. He could not see it, but he _felt_ its presence.\n\nAn hour passed, during which Dantès, excited by the feeling of freedom, continued to cleave the waves.\n\n“Let us see,” said he, “I have swum above an hour, but as the wind is against me, that has retarded my speed; however, if I am not mistaken, I must be close to Tiboulen. But what if I were mistaken?”\n\nA shudder passed over him. He sought to tread water, in order to rest himself; but the sea was too violent, and he felt that he could not make use of this means of recuperation.\n\n“Well,” said he, “I will swim on until I am worn out, or the cramp seizes me, and then I shall sink;” and he struck out with the energy of despair.\n\nSuddenly the sky seemed to him to become still darker and more dense, and heavy clouds seemed to sweep down towards him; at the same time he felt a sharp pain in his knee. He fancied for a moment that he had been shot, and listened for the report; but he heard nothing. Then he put out his hand, and encountered an obstacle and with another stroke knew that he had gained the shore.\n\nBefore him rose a grotesque mass of rocks, that resembled nothing so much as a vast fire petrified at the moment of its most fervent combustion. It was the Island of Tiboulen. Dantès rose, advanced a few steps, and, with a fervent prayer of gratitude, stretched himself on the granite, which seemed to him softer than down. Then, in spite of the wind and rain, he fell into the deep, sweet sleep of utter exhaustion. At the expiration of an hour Edmond was awakened by the roar of thunder. The tempest was let loose and beating the atmosphere with its mighty wings; from time to time a flash of lightning stretched across the heavens like a fiery serpent, lighting up the clouds that rolled on in vast chaotic waves.\n\nDantès had not been deceived—he had reached the first of the two islands, which was, in fact, Tiboulen. He knew that it was barren and without shelter; but when the sea became more calm, he resolved to plunge into its waves again, and swim to Lemaire, equally arid, but larger, and consequently better adapted for concealment.\n\nAn overhanging rock offered him a temporary shelter, and scarcely had he availed himself of it when the tempest burst forth in all its fury. Edmond felt the trembling of the rock beneath which he lay; the waves, dashing themselves against it, wetted him with their spray. He was safely sheltered, and yet he felt dizzy in the midst of the warring of the elements and the dazzling brightness of the lightning. It seemed to him that the island trembled to its base, and that it would, like a vessel at anchor, break moorings, and bear him off into the centre of the storm.\n\nHe then recollected that he had not eaten or drunk for four-and-twenty hours. He extended his hands, and drank greedily of the rainwater that had lodged in a hollow of the rock.\n\nAs he rose, a flash of lightning, that seemed to rive the remotest heights of heaven, illumined the darkness. By its light, between the Island of Lemaire and Cape Croiselle, a quarter of a league distant, Dantès saw a fishing-boat driven rapidly like a spectre before the power of winds and waves. A second after, he saw it again, approaching with frightful rapidity. Dantès cried at the top of his voice to warn them of their danger, but they saw it themselves. Another flash showed him four men clinging to the shattered mast and the rigging, while a fifth clung to the broken rudder. The men he beheld saw him undoubtedly, for their cries were carried to his ears by the wind. Above the splintered mast a sail rent to tatters was waving; suddenly the ropes that still held it gave way, and it disappeared in the darkness of the night like a vast sea-bird.\n\nAt the same moment a violent crash was heard, and cries of distress. Dantès from his rocky perch saw the shattered vessel, and among the fragments the floating forms of the hapless sailors. Then all was dark again.\n\nDantès ran down the rocks at the risk of being himself dashed to pieces; he listened, he groped about, but he heard and saw nothing—the cries had ceased, and the tempest continued to rage. By degrees the wind abated, vast gray clouds rolled towards the west, and the blue firmament appeared studded with bright stars. Soon a red streak became visible in the horizon, the waves whitened, a light played over them, and gilded their foaming crests with gold. It was day.\n\nDantès stood mute and motionless before this majestic spectacle, as if he now beheld it for the first time; and indeed since his captivity in the Château d’If he had forgotten that such scenes were ever to be witnessed. He turned towards the fortress, and looked at both sea and land. The gloomy building rose from the bosom of the ocean with imposing majesty and seemed to dominate the scene. It was about five o’clock. The sea continued to get calmer.\n\n“In two or three hours,” thought Dantès, “the turnkey will enter my chamber, find the body of my poor friend, recognize it, seek for me in vain, and give the alarm. Then the tunnel will be discovered; the men who cast me into the sea and who must have heard the cry I uttered, will be questioned. Then boats filled with armed soldiers will pursue the wretched fugitive. The cannon will warn everyone to refuse shelter to a man wandering about naked and famished. The police of Marseilles will be on the alert by land, whilst the governor pursues me by sea. I am cold, I am hungry. I have lost even the knife that saved me. Oh, my God, I have suffered enough surely! Have pity on me, and do for me what I am unable to do for myself.”\n\nAs Dantès (his eyes turned in the direction of the Château d’If) uttered this prayer, he saw off the farther point of the Island of Pomègue a small vessel with lateen sail skimming the sea like a gull in search of prey; and with his sailor’s eye he knew it to be a Genoese tartan. She was coming out of Marseilles harbor, and was standing out to sea rapidly, her sharp prow cleaving through the waves.\n\n“Oh,” cried Edmond, “to think that in half an hour I could join her, did I not fear being questioned, detected, and conveyed back to Marseilles! What can I do? What story can I invent? under pretext of trading along the coast, these men, who are in reality smugglers, will prefer selling me to doing a good action. I must wait. But I cannot—I am starving. In a few hours my strength will be utterly exhausted; besides, perhaps I have not been missed at the fortress. I can pass as one of the sailors wrecked last night. My story will be accepted, for there is no one left to contradict me.”\n\nAs he spoke, Dantès looked toward the spot where the fishing-vessel had been wrecked, and started. The red cap of one of the sailors hung to a point of the rock and some timbers that had formed part of the vessel’s keel, floated at the foot of the crag. In an instant Dantès’ plan was formed. He swam to the cap, placed it on his head, seized one of the timbers, and struck out so as to cut across the course the vessel was taking.\n\n“I am saved!” murmured he. And this conviction restored his strength.\n\nHe soon saw that the vessel, with the wind dead ahead, was tacking between the Château d’If and the tower of Planier. For an instant he feared lest, instead of keeping in shore, she should stand out to sea; but he soon saw that she would pass, like most vessels bound for Italy, between the islands of Jaros and Calaseraigne.\n\nHowever, the vessel and the swimmer insensibly neared one another, and in one of its tacks the tartan bore down within a quarter of a mile of him. He rose on the waves, making signs of distress; but no one on board saw him, and the vessel stood on another tack. Dantès would have shouted, but he knew that the wind would drown his voice.\n\nIt was then he rejoiced at his precaution in taking the timber, for without it he would have been unable, perhaps, to reach the vessel—certainly to return to shore, should he be unsuccessful in attracting attention.\n\nDantès, though almost sure as to what course the vessel would take, had yet watched it anxiously until it tacked and stood towards him. Then he advanced; but before they could meet, the vessel again changed her course. By a violent effort he rose half out of the water, waving his cap, and uttering a loud shout peculiar to sailors. This time he was both seen and heard, and the tartan instantly steered towards him. At the same time, he saw they were about to lower the boat.\n\nAn instant after, the boat, rowed by two men, advanced rapidly towards him. Dantès let go of the timber, which he now thought to be useless, and swam vigorously to meet them. But he had reckoned too much upon his strength, and then he realized how serviceable the timber had been to him. His arms became stiff, his legs lost their flexibility, and he was almost breathless.\n\nHe shouted again. The two sailors redoubled their efforts, and one of them cried in Italian, “Courage!”\n\nThe word reached his ear as a wave which he no longer had the strength to surmount passed over his head. He rose again to the surface, struggled with the last desperate effort of a drowning man, uttered a third cry, and felt himself sinking, as if the fatal cannon shot were again tied to his feet. The water passed over his head, and the sky turned gray. A convulsive movement again brought him to the surface. He felt himself seized by the hair, then he saw and heard nothing. He had fainted.\n\nWhen he opened his eyes Dantès found himself on the deck of the tartan. His first care was to see what course they were taking. They were rapidly leaving the Château d’If behind. Dantès was so exhausted that the exclamation of joy he uttered was mistaken for a sigh.\n\nAs we have said, he was lying on the deck. A sailor was rubbing his limbs with a woollen cloth; another, whom he recognized as the one who had cried out “Courage!” held a gourd full of rum to his mouth; while the third, an old sailor, at once the pilot and captain, looked on with that egotistical pity men feel for a misfortune that they have escaped yesterday, and which may overtake them tomorrow.\n\nA few drops of the rum restored suspended animation, while the friction of his limbs restored their elasticity.\n\n“Who are you?” said the pilot in bad French.\n\n“I am,” replied Dantès, in bad Italian, “a Maltese sailor. We were coming from Syracuse laden with grain. The storm of last night overtook us at Cape Morgiou, and we were wrecked on these rocks.”\n\n“Where do you come from?”\n\n“From these rocks that I had the good luck to cling to while our captain and the rest of the crew were all lost. I saw your vessel, and fearful of being left to perish on the desolate island, I swam off on a piece of wreckage to try and intercept your course. You have saved my life, and I thank you,” continued Dantès. “I was lost when one of your sailors caught hold of my hair.”\n\n“It was I,” said a sailor of a frank and manly appearance; “and it was time, for you were sinking.”\n\n“Yes,” returned Dantès, holding out his hand, “I thank you again.”\n\n“I almost hesitated, though,” replied the sailor; “you looked more like a brigand than an honest man, with your beard six inches, and your hair a foot long.”\n\nDantès recollected that his hair and beard had not been cut all the time he was at the Château d’If.\n\n“Yes,” said he, “I made a vow, to our Lady of the Grotto not to cut my hair or beard for ten years if I were saved in a moment of danger; but today the vow expires.”\n\n“Now what are we to do with you?” said the captain.\n\n“Alas, anything you please. My captain is dead; I have barely escaped; but I am a good sailor. Leave me at the first port you make; I shall be sure to find employment.”\n\n“Do you know the Mediterranean?”\n\n“I have sailed over it since my childhood.”\n\n“You know the best harbors?”\n\n“There are few ports that I could not enter or leave with a bandage over my eyes.”\n\n“I say, captain,” said the sailor who had cried “Courage!” to Dantès, “if what he says is true, what hinders his staying with us?”\n\n“If he says true,” said the captain doubtingly. “But in his present condition he will promise anything, and take his chance of keeping it afterwards.”\n\n“I will do more than I promise,” said Dantès.\n\n“We shall see,” returned the other, smiling.\n\n“Where are you going?” asked Dantès.\n\n“To Leghorn.”\n\n“Then why, instead of tacking so frequently, do you not sail nearer the wind?”\n\n“Because we should run straight on to the Island of Rion.”\n\n“You shall pass it by twenty fathoms.”\n\n“Take the helm, and let us see what you know.”\n\nThe young man took the helm, felt to see if the vessel answered the rudder promptly and seeing that, without being a first-rate sailor, she yet was tolerably obedient.\n\n“To the sheets,” said he. The four seamen, who composed the crew, obeyed, while the pilot looked on. “Haul taut.”\n\nThey obeyed.\n\n“Belay.” This order was also executed; and the vessel passed, as Dantès had predicted, twenty fathoms to windward.\n\n“Bravo!” said the captain.\n\n“Bravo!” repeated the sailors. And they all looked with astonishment at this man whose eye now disclosed an intelligence and his body a vigor they had not thought him capable of showing.\n\n“You see,” said Dantès, quitting the helm, “I shall be of some use to you, at least during the voyage. If you do not want me at Leghorn, you can leave me there, and I will pay you out of the first wages I get, for my food and the clothes you lend me.”\n\n“Ah,” said the captain, “we can agree very well, if you are reasonable.”\n\n“Give me what you give the others, and it will be all right,” returned Dantès.\n\n“That’s not fair,” said the seaman who had saved Dantès; “for you know more than we do.”\n\n“What is that to you, Jacopo?” returned the Captain. “Everyone is free to ask what he pleases.”\n\n“That’s true,” replied Jacopo; “I only make a remark.”\n\n“Well, you would do much better to find him a jacket and a pair of trousers, if you have them.”\n\n“No,” said Jacopo; “but I have a shirt and a pair of trousers.”\n\n“That is all I want,” interrupted Dantès. Jacopo dived into the hold and soon returned with what Edmond wanted.\n\n“Now, then, do you wish for anything else?” said the patron.\n\n“A piece of bread and another glass of the capital rum I tasted, for I have not eaten or drunk for a long time.” He had not tasted food for forty hours. A piece of bread was brought, and Jacopo offered him the gourd.\n\n“Larboard your helm,” cried the captain to the steersman. Dantès glanced that way as he lifted the gourd to his mouth; then paused with hand in mid-air.\n\n“Hollo! what’s the matter at the Château d’If?” said the captain.\n\nA small white cloud, which had attracted Dantès’ attention, crowned the summit of the bastion of the Château d’If. At the same moment the faint report of a gun was heard. The sailors looked at one another.\n\n“What is this?” asked the captain.\n\n“A prisoner has escaped from the Château d’If, and they are firing the alarm gun,” replied Dantès. The captain glanced at him, but he had lifted the rum to his lips and was drinking it with so much composure, that suspicions, if the captain had any, died away.\n0277m\n\n“Pretty strong rum!” said Dantès, wiping his brow with his sleeve.\n\n“At any rate,” murmured he, “if it be, so much the better, for I have made a rare acquisition.”\n0279m\n\nUnder pretence of being fatigued, Dantès asked to take the helm; the steersman, glad to be relieved, looked at the captain, and the latter by a sign indicated that he might abandon it to his new comrade. Dantès could thus keep his eyes on Marseilles.\n\n“What is the day of the month?” asked he of Jacopo, who sat down beside him.\n\n“The 28th of February.”\n\n“In what year?”\n\n“In what year—you ask me in what year?”\n\n“Yes,” replied the young man, “I ask you in what year!”\n\n“You have forgotten then?”\n\n“I got such a fright last night,” replied Dantès, smiling, “that I have almost lost my memory. I ask you what year is it?”\n\n“The year 1829,” returned Jacopo.\n\nIt was fourteen years, day for day, since Dantès’ arrest. He was nineteen when he entered the Château d’If; he was thirty-three when he escaped. A sorrowful smile passed over his face; he asked himself what had become of Mercédès, who must believe him dead. Then his eyes lighted up with hatred as he thought of the three men who had caused him so long and wretched a captivity. He renewed against Danglars, Fernand, and Villefort the oath of implacable vengeance he had made in his dungeon.\n\nThis oath was no longer a vain menace; for the fastest sailor in the Mediterranean would have been unable to overtake the little tartan, that with every stitch of canvas set was flying before the wind to Leghorn.\n\n# Chapter 22. The Smugglers\n\nDantès had not been a day on board before he had a very clear idea of the men with whom his lot had been cast. Without having been in the school of the Abbé Faria, the worthy master of _La Jeune Amélie_ (the name of the Genoese tartan) knew a smattering of all the tongues spoken on the shores of that large lake called the Mediterranean, from the Arabic to the Provençal, and this, while it spared him interpreters, persons always troublesome and frequently indiscreet, gave him great facilities of communication, either with the vessels he met at sea, with the small boats sailing along the coast, or with the people without name, country, or occupation, who are always seen on the quays of seaports, and who live by hidden and mysterious means which we must suppose to be a direct gift of Providence, as they have no visible means of support. It is fair to assume that Dantès was on board a smuggler.\n\nAt first the captain had received Dantès on board with a certain degree of distrust. He was very well known to the customs officers of the coast; and as there was between these worthies and himself a perpetual battle of wits, he had at first thought that Dantès might be an emissary of these industrious guardians of rights and duties, who perhaps employed this ingenious means of learning some of the secrets of his trade. But the skilful manner in which Dantès had handled the lugger had entirely reassured him; and then, when he saw the light plume of smoke floating above the bastion of the Château d’If, and heard the distant report, he was instantly struck with the idea that he had on board his vessel one whose coming and going, like that of kings, was accompanied with salutes of artillery. This made him less uneasy, it must be owned, than if the new-comer had proved to be a customs officer; but this supposition also disappeared like the first, when he beheld the perfect tranquillity of his recruit.\n\nEdmond thus had the advantage of knowing what the owner was, without the owner knowing who he was; and however the old sailor and his crew tried to “pump” him, they extracted nothing more from him; he gave accurate descriptions of Naples and Malta, which he knew as well as Marseilles, and held stoutly to his first story. Thus the Genoese, subtle as he was, was duped by Edmond, in whose favor his mild demeanor, his nautical skill, and his admirable dissimulation, pleaded. Moreover, it is possible that the Genoese was one of those shrewd persons who know nothing but what they should know, and believe nothing but what they should believe.\n\nIn this state of mutual understanding, they reached Leghorn. Here Edmond was to undergo another trial; he was to find out whether he could recognize himself, as he had not seen his own face for fourteen years. He had preserved a tolerably good remembrance of what the youth had been, and was now to find out what the man had become. His comrades believed that his vow was fulfilled. As he had twenty times touched at Leghorn, he remembered a barber in St. Ferdinand Street; he went there to have his beard and hair cut. The barber gazed in amazement at this man with the long, thick and black hair and beard, which gave his head the appearance of one of Titian’s portraits. At this period it was not the fashion to wear so large a beard and hair so long; now a barber would only be surprised if a man gifted with such advantages should consent voluntarily to deprive himself of them. The Leghorn barber said nothing and went to work.\n\nWhen the operation was concluded, and Edmond felt that his chin was completely smooth, and his hair reduced to its usual length, he asked for a looking-glass. He was now, as we have said, three-and-thirty years of age, and his fourteen years’ imprisonment had produced a great transformation in his appearance.\n\nDantès had entered the Château d’If with the round, open, smiling face of a young and happy man, with whom the early paths of life have been smooth, and who anticipates a future corresponding with his past. This was now all changed. The oval face was lengthened, his smiling mouth had assumed the firm and marked lines which betoken resolution; his eyebrows were arched beneath a brow furrowed with thought; his eyes were full of melancholy, and from their depths occasionally sparkled gloomy fires of misanthropy and hatred; his complexion, so long kept from the sun, had now that pale color which produces, when the features are encircled with black hair, the aristocratic beauty of the man of the north; the profound learning he had acquired had besides diffused over his features a refined intellectual expression; and he had also acquired, being naturally of a goodly stature, that vigor which a frame possesses which has so long concentrated all its force within itself.\n0283m\n\nTo the elegance of a nervous and slight form had succeeded the solidity of a rounded and muscular figure. As to his voice, prayers, sobs, and imprecations had changed it so that at times it was of a singularly penetrating sweetness, and at others rough and almost hoarse.\n\nMoreover, from being so long in twilight or darkness, his eyes had acquired the faculty of distinguishing objects in the night, common to the hyena and the wolf. Edmond smiled when he beheld himself; it was impossible that his best friend—if, indeed, he had any friend left—could recognize him; he could not recognize himself.\n\nThe master of _La Jeune Amélie_, who was very desirous of retaining amongst his crew a man of Edmond’s value, had offered to advance him funds out of his future profits, which Edmond had accepted. His next care on leaving the barber’s who had achieved his first metamorphosis was to enter a shop and buy a complete sailor’s suit—a garb, as we all know, very simple, and consisting of white trousers, a striped shirt, and a cap.\n\nIt was in this costume, and bringing back to Jacopo the shirt and trousers he had lent him, that Edmond reappeared before the captain of the lugger, who had made him tell his story over and over again before he could believe him, or recognize in the neat and trim sailor the man with thick and matted beard, hair tangled with seaweed, and body soaking in seabrine, whom he had picked up naked and nearly drowned. Attracted by his prepossessing appearance, he renewed his offers of an engagement to Dantès; but Dantès, who had his own projects, would not agree for a longer time than three months.\n\n_La Jeune Amélie_ had a very active crew, very obedient to their captain, who lost as little time as possible. He had scarcely been a week at Leghorn before the hold of his vessel was filled with printed muslins, contraband cottons, English powder, and tobacco on which the excise had forgotten to put its mark. The master was to get all this out of Leghorn free of duties, and land it on the shores of Corsica, where certain speculators undertook to forward the cargo to France.\n\nThey sailed; Edmond was again cleaving the azure sea which had been the first horizon of his youth, and which he had so often dreamed of in prison. He left Gorgone on his right and La Pianosa on his left, and went towards the country of Paoli and Napoleon.\n\nThe next morning going on deck, as he always did at an early hour, the patron found Dantès leaning against the bulwarks gazing with intense earnestness at a pile of granite rocks, which the rising sun tinged with rosy light. It was the Island of Monte Cristo.\n\n_La Jeune Amélie_ left it three-quarters of a league to the larboard and kept on for Corsica. Dantès thought, as they passed so closely to the island whose name was so interesting to him, that he had only to leap into the sea and in half an hour be at the promised land. But then what could he do without instruments to discover his treasure, without arms to defend himself? Besides, what would the sailors say? What would the patron think? He must wait.\n\nFortunately, Dantès had learned how to wait; he had waited fourteen years for his liberty, and now he was free he could wait at least six months or a year for wealth. Would he not have accepted liberty without riches if it had been offered to him? Besides, were not those riches chimerical?—offspring of the brain of the poor Abbé Faria, had they not died with him? It is true, the letter of the Cardinal Spada was singularly circumstantial, and Dantès repeated it to himself, from one end to the other, for he had not forgotten a word.\n\n0285m\nEvening came, and Edmond saw the island tinged with the shades of twilight, and then disappear in the darkness from all eyes but his own, for he, with vision accustomed to the gloom of a prison, continued to behold it last of all, for he remained alone upon deck. The next morn broke off the coast of Aleria; all day they coasted, and in the evening saw fires lighted on land; the position of these was no doubt a signal for landing, for a ship’s lantern was hung up at the mast-head instead of the streamer, and they came to within a gunshot of the shore. Dantès noticed that the captain of _La Jeune Amélie_ had, as he neared the land, mounted two small culverins, which, without making much noise, can throw a four ounce ball a thousand paces or so.\n\nBut on this occasion the precaution was superfluous, and everything proceeded with the utmost smoothness and politeness. Four shallops came off with very little noise alongside the lugger, which, no doubt, in acknowledgement of the compliment, lowered her own shallop into the sea, and the five boats worked so well that by two o’clock in the morning all the cargo was out of _La Jeune Amélie_ and on _terra firma_. The same night, such a man of regularity was the patron of _La Jeune Amélie_, the profits were divided, and each man had a hundred Tuscan livres, or about eighty francs.\n\nBut the voyage was not ended. They turned the bowsprit towards Sardinia, where they intended to take in a cargo, which was to replace what had been discharged. The second operation was as successful as the first, _La Jeune Amélie_ was in luck. This new cargo was destined for the coast of the Duchy of Lucca, and consisted almost entirely of Havana cigars, sherry, and Malaga wines.\n\nThere they had a bit of a skirmish in getting rid of the duties; the excise was, in truth, the everlasting enemy of the patron of _La Jeune Amélie_. A customs officer was laid low, and two sailors wounded; Dantès was one of the latter, a ball having touched him in the left shoulder. Dantès was almost glad of this affray, and almost pleased at being wounded, for they were rude lessons which taught him with what eye he could view danger, and with what endurance he could bear suffering. He had contemplated danger with a smile, and when wounded had exclaimed with the great philosopher, “Pain, thou art not an evil.”\n\nHe had, moreover, looked upon the customs officer wounded to death, and, whether from heat of blood produced by the encounter, or the chill of human sentiment, this sight had made but slight impression upon him. Dantès was on the way he desired to follow, and was moving towards the end he wished to achieve; his heart was in a fair way of petrifying in his bosom. Jacopo, seeing him fall, had believed him killed, and rushing towards him raised him up, and then attended to him with all the kindness of a devoted comrade.\n\nThis world was not then so good as Doctor Pangloss believed it, neither was it so wicked as Dantès thought it, since this man, who had nothing to expect from his comrade but the inheritance of his share of the prize-money, manifested so much sorrow when he saw him fall. Fortunately, as we have said, Edmond was only wounded, and with certain herbs gathered at certain seasons, and sold to the smugglers by the old Sardinian women, the wound soon closed. Edmond then resolved to try Jacopo, and offered him in return for his attention a share of his prize-money, but Jacopo refused it indignantly.\n\nAs a result of the sympathetic devotion which Jacopo had from the first bestowed on Edmond, the latter was moved to a certain degree of affection. But this sufficed for Jacopo, who instinctively felt that Edmond had a right to superiority of position—a superiority which Edmond had concealed from all others. And from this time the kindness which Edmond showed him was enough for the brave seaman.\n\nThen in the long days on board ship, when the vessel, gliding on with security over the azure sea, required no care but the hand of the helmsman, thanks to the favorable winds that swelled her sails, Edmond, with a chart in his hand, became the instructor of Jacopo, as the poor Abbé Faria had been his tutor. He pointed out to him the bearings of the coast, explained to him the variations of the compass, and taught him to read in that vast book opened over our heads which they call heaven, and where God writes in azure with letters of diamonds.\n\nAnd when Jacopo inquired of him, “What is the use of teaching all these things to a poor sailor like me?” Edmond replied, “Who knows? You may one day be the captain of a vessel. Your fellow-countryman, Bonaparte, became emperor.” We had forgotten to say that Jacopo was a Corsican.\n\nTwo months and a half elapsed in these trips, and Edmond had become as skilful a coaster as he had been a hardy seaman; he had formed an acquaintance with all the smugglers on the coast, and learned all the Masonic signs by which these half pirates recognize each other. He had passed and re-passed his Island of Monte Cristo twenty times, but not once had he found an opportunity of landing there.\n\nHe then formed a resolution. As soon as his engagement with the patron of _La Jeune Amélie_ ended, he would hire a small vessel on his own account—for in his several voyages he had amassed a hundred piastres—and under some pretext land at the Island of Monte Cristo. Then he would be free to make his researches, not perhaps entirely at liberty, for he would be doubtless watched by those who accompanied him. But in this world we must risk something. Prison had made Edmond prudent, and he was desirous of running no risk whatever. But in vain did he rack his imagination; fertile as it was, he could not devise any plan for reaching the island without companionship.\n\nDantès was tossed about on these doubts and wishes, when the patron, who had great confidence in him, and was very desirous of retaining him in his service, took him by the arm one evening and led him to a tavern on the Via del’ Oglio, where the leading smugglers of Leghorn used to congregate and discuss affairs connected with their trade. Already Dantès had visited this maritime Bourse two or three times, and seeing all these hardy free-traders, who supplied the whole coast for nearly two hundred leagues in extent, he had asked himself what power might not that man attain who should give the impulse of his will to all these contrary and diverging minds. This time it was a great matter that was under discussion, connected with a vessel laden with Turkey carpets, stuffs of the Levant, and cashmeres. It was necessary to find some neutral ground on which an exchange could be made, and then to try and land these goods on the coast of France. If the venture was successful the profit would be enormous, there would be a gain of fifty or sixty piastres each for the crew.\n\nThe patron of _La Jeune Amélie_ proposed as a place of landing the Island of Monte Cristo, which being completely deserted, and having neither soldiers nor revenue officers, seemed to have been placed in the midst of the ocean since the time of the heathen Olympus by Mercury, the god of merchants and robbers, classes of mankind which we in modern times have separated if not made distinct, but which antiquity appears to have included in the same category.\n\nAt the mention of Monte Cristo Dantès started with joy; he rose to conceal his emotion, and took a turn around the smoky tavern, where all the languages of the known world were jumbled in a _lingua franca_.\n\nWhen he again joined the two persons who had been discussing the matter, it had been decided that they should touch at Monte Cristo and set out on the following night. Edmond, being consulted, was of opinion that the island afforded every possible security, and that great enterprises to be well done should be done quickly.\n\nNothing then was altered in the plan, and orders were given to get under weigh next night, and, wind and weather permitting, to make the neutral island by the following day.\n\n# Chapter 23. The Island of Monte Cristo\n\n0289m\nThus, at length, by one of the unexpected strokes of fortune which sometimes befall those who have for a long time been the victims of an evil destiny, Dantès was about to secure the opportunity he wished for, by simple and natural means, and land on the island without incurring any suspicion. One night more and he would be on his way.\n\nThe night was one of feverish distraction, and in its progress visions, good and evil, passed through Dantès’ mind. If he closed his eyes, he saw Cardinal Spada’s letter written on the wall in characters of flame—if he slept for a moment the wildest dreams haunted his brain. He ascended into grottos paved with emeralds, with panels of rubies, and the roof glowing with diamond stalactites. Pearls fell drop by drop, as subterranean waters filter in their caves. Edmond, amazed, wonderstruck, filled his pockets with the radiant gems and then returned to daylight, when he discovered that his prizes had all changed into common pebbles. He then endeavored to re-enter the marvellous grottos, but they had suddenly receded, and now the path became a labyrinth, and then the entrance vanished, and in vain did he tax his memory for the magic and mysterious word which opened the splendid caverns of Ali Baba to the Arabian fisherman. All was useless, the treasure disappeared, and had again reverted to the genii from whom for a moment he had hoped to carry it off.\n\nThe day came at length, and was almost as feverish as the night had been, but it brought reason to the aid of imagination, and Dantès was then enabled to arrange a plan which had hitherto been vague and unsettled in his brain. Night came, and with it the preparation for departure, and these preparations served to conceal Dantès’ agitation. He had by degrees assumed such authority over his companions that he was almost like a commander on board; and as his orders were always clear, distinct, and easy of execution, his comrades obeyed him with celerity and pleasure.\n\nThe old patron did not interfere, for he too had recognized the superiority of Dantès over the crew and himself. He saw in the young man his natural successor, and regretted that he had not a daughter, that he might have bound Edmond to him by a more secure alliance. At seven o’clock in the evening all was ready, and at ten minutes past seven they doubled the lighthouse just as the beacon was kindled. The sea was calm, and, with a fresh breeze from the south-east, they sailed beneath a bright blue sky, in which God also lighted up in turn his beacon lights, each of which is a world. Dantès told them that all hands might turn in, and he would take the helm. When the Maltese (for so they called Dantès) had said this, it was sufficient, and all went to their bunks contentedly.\n\nThis frequently happened. Dantès, cast from solitude into the world, frequently experienced an imperious desire for solitude; and what solitude is more complete, or more poetical, than that of a ship floating in isolation on the sea during the obscurity of the night, in the silence of immensity, and under the eye of Heaven?\n\nNow this solitude was peopled with his thoughts, the night lighted up by his illusions, and the silence animated by his anticipations. When the patron awoke, the vessel was hurrying on with every sail set, and every sail full with the breeze. They were making nearly ten knots an hour. The Island of Monte Cristo loomed large in the horizon. Edmond resigned the lugger to the master’s care, and went and lay down in his hammock; but, in spite of a sleepless night, he could not close his eyes for a moment.\n\nTwo hours afterwards he came on deck, as the boat was about to double the Island of Elba. They were just abreast of Mareciana, and beyond the flat but verdant Island of La Pianosa. The peak of Monte Cristo reddened by the burning sun, was seen against the azure sky. Dantès ordered the helmsman to put down his helm, in order to leave La Pianosa to starboard, as he knew that he should shorten his course by two or three knots. About five o’clock in the evening the island was distinct, and everything on it was plainly perceptible, owing to that clearness of the atmosphere peculiar to the light which the rays of the sun cast at its setting.\n\nEdmond gazed very earnestly at the mass of rocks which gave out all the variety of twilight colors, from the brightest pink to the deepest blue; and from time to time his cheeks flushed, his brow darkened, and a mist passed over his eyes. Never did a gamester, whose whole fortune is staked on one cast of the die, experience the anguish which Edmond felt in his paroxysms of hope.\n\nNight came, and at ten o’clock they anchored. _La Jeune Amélie_ was first at the rendezvous. In spite of his usual command over himself, Dantès could not restrain his impetuosity. He was the first to jump on shore; and had he dared, he would, like Lucius Brutus, have “kissed his mother earth.” It was dark, but at eleven o’clock the moon rose in the midst of the ocean, whose every wave she silvered, and then, “ascending high,” played in floods of pale light on the rocky hills of this second Pelion.\n\nThe island was familiar to the crew of _La Jeune Amélie_,—it was one of her regular haunts. As to Dantès, he had passed it on his voyage to and from the Levant, but never touched at it. He questioned Jacopo.\n\n“Where shall we pass the night?” he inquired.\n\n“Why, on board the tartan,” replied the sailor.\n\n“Should we not do better in the grottos?”\n\n“What grottos?”\n\n“Why, the grottos—caves of the island.”\n\n“I do not know of any grottos,” replied Jacopo.\n\nThe cold sweat sprang forth on Dantès’ brow.\n\n“What, are there no grottos at Monte Cristo?” he asked.\n\n“None.”\n\nFor a moment Dantès was speechless; then he remembered that these caves might have been filled up by some accident, or even stopped up, for the sake of greater security, by Cardinal Spada. The point was, then, to discover the hidden entrance. It was useless to search at night, and Dantès therefore delayed all investigation until the morning. Besides, a signal made half a league out at sea, and to which _La Jeune Amélie_ replied by a similar signal, indicated that the moment for business had come.\n\nThe boat that now arrived, assured by the answering signal that all was well, soon came in sight, white and silent as a phantom, and cast anchor within a cable’s length of shore.\n\nThen the landing began. Dantès reflected, as he worked, on the shout of joy which, with a single word, he could evoke from all these men, if he gave utterance to the one unchanging thought that pervaded his heart; but, far from disclosing this precious secret, he almost feared that he had already said too much, and by his restlessness and continual questions, his minute observations and evident preoccupation, aroused suspicions. Fortunately, as regarded this circumstance at least, his painful past gave to his countenance an indelible sadness, and the glimmerings of gayety seen beneath this cloud were indeed but transitory.\n\nNo one had the slightest suspicion; and when next day, taking a fowling-piece, powder, and shot, Dantès declared his intention to go and kill some of the wild goats that were seen springing from rock to rock, his wish was construed into a love of sport, or a desire for solitude. However, Jacopo insisted on following him, and Dantès did not oppose this, fearing if he did so that he might incur distrust. Scarcely, however, had they gone a quarter of a league when, having killed a kid, he begged Jacopo to take it to his comrades, and request them to cook it, and when ready to let him know by firing a gun. This and some dried fruits and a flask of Monte Pulciano, was the bill of fare.\n\nDantès went on, looking from time to time behind and around about him. Having reached the summit of a rock, he saw, a thousand feet beneath him, his companions, whom Jacopo had rejoined, and who were all busy preparing the repast which Edmond’s skill as a marksman had augmented with a capital dish.\n\nEdmond looked at them for a moment with the sad and gentle smile of a man superior to his fellows.\n\n“In two hours’ time,” said he, “these persons will depart richer by fifty piastres each, to go and risk their lives again by endeavoring to gain fifty more; then they will return with a fortune of six hundred francs, and waste this treasure in some city with the pride of sultans and the insolence of nabobs. At this moment hope makes me despise their riches, which seem to me contemptible. Yet perchance tomorrow deception will so act on me, that I shall, on compulsion, consider such a contemptible possession as the utmost happiness. Oh, no!” exclaimed Edmond, “that will not be. The wise, unerring Faria could not be mistaken in this one thing. Besides, it were better to die than to continue to lead this low and wretched life.”\n\nThus Dantès, who but three months before had no desire but liberty had now not liberty enough, and panted for wealth. The cause was not in Dantès, but in Providence, who, while limiting the power of man, has filled him with boundless desires.\n\nMeanwhile, by a cleft between two walls of rock, following a path worn by a torrent, and which, in all human probability, human foot had never before trod, Dantès approached the spot where he supposed the grottos must have existed. Keeping along the shore, and examining the smallest object with serious attention, he thought he could trace, on certain rocks, marks made by the hand of man.\n\nTime, which encrusts all physical substances with its mossy mantle, as it invests all things of the mind with forgetfulness, seemed to have respected these signs, which apparently had been made with some degree of regularity, and probably with a definite purpose. Occasionally the marks were hidden under tufts of myrtle, which spread into large bushes laden with blossoms, or beneath parasitical lichen. So Edmond had to separate the branches or brush away the moss to know where the guide-marks were. The sight of marks renewed Edmond’s fondest hopes. Might it not have been the cardinal himself who had first traced them, in order that they might serve as a guide for his nephew in the event of a catastrophe, which he could not foresee would have been so complete. This solitary place was precisely suited to the requirements of a man desirous of burying treasure. Only, might not these betraying marks have attracted other eyes than those for whom they were made? and had the dark and wondrous island indeed faithfully guarded its precious secret?\n0295m\n\nIt seemed, however, to Edmond, who was hidden from his comrades by the inequalities of the ground, that at sixty paces from the harbor the marks ceased; nor did they terminate at any grotto. A large round rock, placed solidly on its base, was the only spot to which they seemed to lead. Edmond concluded that perhaps instead of having reached the end of the route he had only explored its beginning, and he therefore turned round and retraced his steps.\n\nMeanwhile his comrades had prepared the repast, had got some water from a spring, spread out the fruit and bread, and cooked the kid. Just at the moment when they were taking the dainty animal from the spit, they saw Edmond springing with the boldness of a chamois from rock to rock, and they fired the signal agreed upon. The sportsman instantly changed his direction, and ran quickly towards them. But even while they watched his daring progress, Edmond’s foot slipped, and they saw him stagger on the edge of a rock and disappear. They all rushed towards him, for all loved Edmond in spite of his superiority; yet Jacopo reached him first.\n\nHe found Edmond lying prone, bleeding, and almost senseless. He had rolled down a declivity of twelve or fifteen feet. They poured a little rum down his throat, and this remedy which had before been so beneficial to him, produced the same effect as formerly. Edmond opened his eyes, complained of great pain in his knee, a feeling of heaviness in his head, and severe pains in his loins. They wished to carry him to the shore; but when they touched him, although under Jacopo’s directions, he declared, with heavy groans, that he could not bear to be moved.\n\nIt may be supposed that Dantès did not now think of his dinner, but he insisted that his comrades, who had not his reasons for fasting, should have their meal. As for himself, he declared that he had only need of a little rest, and that when they returned he should be easier. The sailors did not require much urging. They were hungry, and the smell of the roasted kid was very savory, and your tars are not very ceremonious. An hour afterwards they returned. All that Edmond had been able to do was to drag himself about a dozen paces forward to lean against a moss-grown rock.\n\nBut, instead of growing easier, Dantès’ pains appeared to increase in violence. The old patron, who was obliged to sail in the morning in order to land his cargo on the frontiers of Piedmont and France, between Nice and Fréjus, urged Dantès to try and rise. Edmond made great exertions in order to comply; but at each effort he fell back, moaning and turning pale.\n\n“He has broken his ribs,” said the commander, in a low voice. “No matter; he is an excellent fellow, and we must not leave him. We will try and carry him on board the tartan.”\n\nDantès declared, however, that he would rather die where he was than undergo the agony which the slightest movement cost him.\n\n“Well,” said the patron, “let what may happen, it shall never be said that we deserted a good comrade like you. We will not go till evening.”\n\nThis very much astonished the sailors, although, not one opposed it. The patron was so strict that this was the first time they had ever seen him give up an enterprise, or even delay in its execution. Dantès would not allow that any such infraction of regular and proper rules should be made in his favor.\n\n“No, no,” he said to the patron, “I was awkward, and it is just that I pay the penalty of my clumsiness. Leave me a small supply of biscuit, a gun, powder, and balls, to kill the kids or defend myself at need, and a pickaxe, that I may build a shelter if you delay in coming back for me.”\n\n“But you’ll die of hunger,” said the patron.\n\n“I would rather do so,” was Edmond’s reply, “than suffer the inexpressible agonies which the slightest movement causes me.”\n\nThe patron turned towards his vessel, which was rolling on the swell in the little harbor, and, with sails partly set, would be ready for sea when her toilet should be completed.\n\n“What are we to do, Maltese?” asked the captain. “We cannot leave you here so, and yet we cannot stay.”\n\n“Go, go!” exclaimed Dantès.\n\n“We shall be absent at least a week,” said the patron, “and then we must run out of our course to come here and take you up again.”\n\n“Why,” said Dantès, “if in two or three days you hail any fishing-boat, desire them to come here to me. I will pay twenty-five piastres for my passage back to Leghorn. If you do not come across one, return for me.” The patron shook his head.\n\n“Listen, Captain Baldi; there’s one way of settling this,” said Jacopo. “Do you go, and I will stay and take care of the wounded man.”\n\n“And give up your share of the venture,” said Edmond, “to remain with me?”\n\n“Yes,” said Jacopo, “and without any hesitation.”\n\n“You are a good fellow and a kind-hearted messmate,” replied Edmond, “and heaven will recompense you for your generous intentions; but I do not wish anyone to stay with me. A day or two of rest will set me up, and I hope I shall find among the rocks certain herbs most excellent for bruises.”\n\nA peculiar smile passed over Dantès’ lips; he squeezed Jacopo’s hand warmly, but nothing could shake his determination to remain—and remain alone.\n\nThe smugglers left with Edmond what he had requested and set sail, but not without turning about several times, and each time making signs of a cordial farewell, to which Edmond replied with his hand only, as if he could not move the rest of his body.\n\nThen, when they had disappeared, he said with a smile,—“’Tis strange that it should be among such men that we find proofs of friendship and devotion.” Then he dragged himself cautiously to the top of a rock, from which he had a full view of the sea, and thence he saw the tartan complete her preparations for sailing, weigh anchor, and, balancing herself as gracefully as a water-fowl ere it takes to the wing, set sail.\n\nAt the end of an hour she was completely out of sight; at least, it was impossible for the wounded man to see her any longer from the spot where he was. Then Dantès rose more agile and light than the kid among the myrtles and shrubs of these wild rocks, took his gun in one hand, his pickaxe in the other, and hastened towards the rock on which the marks he had noted terminated.\n\n“And now,” he exclaimed, remembering the tale of the Arabian fisherman, which Faria had related to him, “now, Open Sesame!”\n\n# Chapter 24. The Secret Cave\n\nThe sun had nearly reached the meridian, and his scorching rays fell full on the rocks, which seemed themselves sensible of the heat. Thousands of grasshoppers, hidden in the bushes, chirped with a monotonous and dull note; the leaves of the myrtle and olive trees waved and rustled in the wind. At every step that Edmond took he disturbed the lizards glittering with the hues of the emerald; afar off he saw the wild goats bounding from crag to crag. In a word, the island was inhabited, yet Edmond felt himself alone, guided by the hand of God.\n\nHe felt an indescribable sensation somewhat akin to dread—that dread of the daylight which even in the desert makes us fear we are watched and observed. This feeling was so strong that at the moment when Edmond was about to begin his labor, he stopped, laid down his pickaxe, seized his gun, mounted to the summit of the highest rock, and from thence gazed round in every direction.\n\nBut it was not upon Corsica, the very houses of which he could distinguish; or on Sardinia; or on the Island of Elba, with its historical associations; or upon the almost imperceptible line that to the experienced eye of a sailor alone revealed the coast of Genoa the proud, and Leghorn the commercial, that he gazed. It was at the brigantine that had left in the morning, and the tartan that had just set sail, that Edmond fixed his eyes.\n\nThe first was just disappearing in the straits of Bonifacio; the other, following an opposite direction, was about to round the Island of Corsica.\n\nThis sight reassured him. He then looked at the objects near him. He saw that he was on the highest point of the island,—a statue on this vast pedestal of granite, nothing human appearing in sight, while the blue ocean beat against the base of the island, and covered it with a fringe of foam. Then he descended with cautious and slow step, for he dreaded lest an accident similar to that he had so adroitly feigned should happen in reality.\n\nDantès, as we have said, had traced the marks along the rocks, and he had noticed that they led to a small creek, which was hidden like the bath of some ancient nymph. This creek was sufficiently wide at its mouth, and deep in the centre, to admit of the entrance of a small vessel of the lugger class, which would be perfectly concealed from observation.\n\nThen following the clew that, in the hands of the Abbé Faria, had been so skilfully used to guide him through the Dædalian labyrinth of probabilities, he thought that the Cardinal Spada, anxious not to be watched, had entered the creek, concealed his little barque, followed the line marked by the notches in the rock, and at the end of it had buried his treasure. It was this idea that had brought Dantès back to the circular rock. One thing only perplexed Edmond, and destroyed his theory. How could this rock, which weighed several tons, have been lifted to this spot, without the aid of many men?\n\nSuddenly an idea flashed across his mind. Instead of raising it, thought he, they have lowered it. And he sprang from the rock in order to inspect the base on which it had formerly stood.\n\nHe soon perceived that a slope had been formed, and the rock had slid along this until it stopped at the spot it now occupied. A large stone had served as a wedge; flints and pebbles had been inserted around it, so as to conceal the orifice; this species of masonry had been covered with earth, and grass and weeds had grown there, moss had clung to the stones, myrtle-bushes had taken root, and the old rock seemed fixed to the earth.\n0301m\n\nDantès dug away the earth carefully, and detected, or fancied he detected, the ingenious artifice. He attacked this wall, cemented by the hand of time, with his pickaxe. After ten minutes’ labor the wall gave way, and a hole large enough to insert the arm was opened.\n\nDantès went and cut the strongest olive-tree he could find, stripped off its branches, inserted it in the hole, and used it as a lever. But the rock was too heavy, and too firmly wedged, to be moved by anyone man, were he Hercules himself. Dantès saw that he must attack the wedge. But how?\n\nHe cast his eyes around, and saw the horn full of powder which his friend Jacopo had left him. He smiled; the infernal invention would serve him for this purpose.\n\nWith the aid of his pickaxe, Dantès, after the manner of a labor-saving pioneer, dug a mine between the upper rock and the one that supported it, filled it with powder, then made a match by rolling his handkerchief in saltpetre. He lighted it and retired.\n\nThe explosion soon followed; the upper rock was lifted from its base by the terrific force of the powder; the lower one flew into pieces; thousands of insects escaped from the aperture Dantès had previously formed, and a huge snake, like the guardian demon of the treasure, rolled himself along in darkening coils, and disappeared.\n\nDantès approached the upper rock, which now, without any support, leaned towards the sea. The intrepid treasure-seeker walked round it, and, selecting the spot from whence it appeared most susceptible to attack, placed his lever in one of the crevices, and strained every nerve to move the mass.\n\nThe rock, already shaken by the explosion, tottered on its base. Dantès redoubled his efforts; he seemed like one of the ancient Titans, who uprooted the mountains to hurl against the father of the gods. The rock yielded, rolled over, bounded from point to point, and finally disappeared in the ocean.\n\nOn the spot it had occupied was a circular space, exposing an iron ring let into a square flag-stone.\n\nDantès uttered a cry of joy and surprise; never had a first attempt been crowned with more perfect success. He would fain have continued, but his knees trembled, and his heart beat so violently, and his sight became so dim, that he was forced to pause.\n\nThis feeling lasted but for a moment. Edmond inserted his lever in the ring and exerted all his strength; the flag-stone yielded, and disclosed steps that descended until they were lost in the obscurity of a subterraneous grotto.\n\nAnyone else would have rushed on with a cry of joy. Dantès turned pale, hesitated, and reflected.\n\n“Come,” said he to himself, “be a man. I am accustomed to adversity. I must not be cast down by the discovery that I have been deceived. What, then, would be the use of all I have suffered? The heart breaks when, after having been elated by flattering hopes, it sees all its illusions destroyed. Faria has dreamed this; the Cardinal Spada buried no treasure here; perhaps he never came here, or if he did, Cæsar Borgia, the intrepid adventurer, the stealthy and indefatigable plunderer, has followed him, discovered his traces, pursued them as I have done, raised the stone, and descending before me, has left me nothing.”\n\nHe remained motionless and pensive, his eyes fixed on the gloomy aperture that was open at his feet.\n\n“Now that I expect nothing, now that I no longer entertain the slightest hopes, the end of this adventure becomes simply a matter of curiosity.” And he remained again motionless and thoughtful.\n\n“Yes, yes; this is an adventure worthy a place in the varied career of that royal bandit. This fabulous event formed but a link in a long chain of marvels. Yes, Borgia has been here, a torch in one hand, a sword in the other, and within twenty paces, at the foot of this rock, perhaps two guards kept watch on land and sea, while their master descended, as I am about to descend, dispelling the darkness before his awe-inspiring progress.”\n0303m\n\n“But what was the fate of the guards who thus possessed his secret?” asked Dantès of himself.\n\n“The fate,” replied he, smiling, “of those who buried Alaric, and were interred with the corpse.”\n\n“Yet, had he come,” thought Dantès, “he would have found the treasure, and Borgia, he who compared Italy to an artichoke, which he could devour leaf by leaf, knew too well the value of time to waste it in replacing this rock. I will go down.”\n\nThen he descended, a smile on his lips, and murmuring that last word of human philosophy, “Perhaps!”\n\nBut instead of the darkness, and the thick and mephitic atmosphere he had expected to find, Dantès saw a dim and bluish light, which, as well as the air, entered, not merely by the aperture he had just formed, but by the interstices and crevices of the rock which were visible from without, and through which he could distinguish the blue sky and the waving branches of the evergreen oaks, and the tendrils of the creepers that grew from the rocks.\n\nAfter having stood a few minutes in the cavern, the atmosphere of which was rather warm than damp, Dantès’ eye, habituated as it was to darkness, could pierce even to the remotest angles of the cavern, which was of granite that sparkled like diamonds.\n\n“Alas,” said Edmond, smiling, “these are the treasures the cardinal has left; and the good abbé, seeing in a dream these glittering walls, has indulged in fallacious hopes.”\n\nBut he called to mind the words of the will, which he knew by heart. “In the farthest angle of the second opening,” said the cardinal’s will. He had only found the first grotto; he had now to seek the second. Dantès continued his search. He reflected that this second grotto must penetrate deeper into the island; he examined the stones, and sounded one part of the wall where he fancied the opening existed, masked for precaution’s sake.\n\nThe pickaxe struck for a moment with a dull sound that drew out of Dantès’ forehead large drops of perspiration. At last it seemed to him that one part of the wall gave forth a more hollow and deeper echo; he eagerly advanced, and with the quickness of perception that no one but a prisoner possesses, saw that there, in all probability, the opening must be.\n\nHowever, he, like Cæsar Borgia, knew the value of time; and, in order to avoid fruitless toil, he sounded all the other walls with his pickaxe, struck the earth with the butt of his gun, and finding nothing that appeared suspicious, returned to that part of the wall whence issued the consoling sound he had before heard.\n\nHe again struck it, and with greater force. Then a singular thing occurred. As he struck the wall, pieces of stucco similar to that used in the ground work of arabesques broke off, and fell to the ground in flakes, exposing a large white stone. The aperture of the rock had been closed with stones, then this stucco had been applied, and painted to imitate granite. Dantès struck with the sharp end of his pickaxe, which entered someway between the interstices.\n\nIt was there he must dig.\n\nBut by some strange play of emotion, in proportion as the proofs that Faria, had not been deceived became stronger, so did his heart give way, and a feeling of discouragement stole over him. This last proof, instead of giving him fresh strength, deprived him of it; the pickaxe descended, or rather fell; he placed it on the ground, passed his hand over his brow, and remounted the stairs, alleging to himself, as an excuse, a desire to be assured that no one was watching him, but in reality because he felt that he was about to faint.\n\nThe island was deserted, and the sun seemed to cover it with its fiery glance; afar off, a few small fishing boats studded the bosom of the blue ocean.\n\nDantès had tasted nothing, but he thought not of hunger at such a moment; he hastily swallowed a few drops of rum, and again entered the cavern.\n\nThe pickaxe that had seemed so heavy, was now like a feather in his grasp; he seized it, and attacked the wall. After several blows he perceived that the stones were not cemented, but had been merely placed one upon the other, and covered with stucco; he inserted the point of his pickaxe, and using the handle as a lever, with joy soon saw the stone turn as if on hinges, and fall at his feet.\n\nHe had nothing more to do now, but with the iron tooth of the pickaxe to draw the stones towards him one by one. The aperture was already sufficiently large for him to enter, but by waiting, he could still cling to hope, and retard the certainty of deception. At last, after renewed hesitation, Dantès entered the second grotto.\n\nThe second grotto was lower and more gloomy than the first; the air that could only enter by the newly formed opening had the mephitic smell Dantès was surprised not to find in the outer cavern. He waited in order to allow pure air to displace the foul atmosphere, and then went on.\n\nAt the left of the opening was a dark and deep angle. But to Dantès’ eye there was no darkness. He glanced around this second grotto; it was, like the first, empty.\n\nThe treasure, if it existed, was buried in this corner. The time had at length arrived; two feet of earth removed, and Dantès’ fate would be decided.\n\nHe advanced towards the angle, and summoning all his resolution, attacked the ground with the pickaxe. At the fifth or sixth blow the pickaxe struck against an iron substance. Never did funeral knell, never did alarm-bell, produce a greater effect on the hearer. Had Dantès found nothing he could not have become more ghastly pale.\n\nHe again struck his pickaxe into the earth, and encountered the same resistance, but not the same sound.\n\n“It is a casket of wood bound with iron,” thought he.\n\nAt this moment a shadow passed rapidly before the opening; Dantès seized his gun, sprang through the opening, and mounted the stair. A wild goat had passed before the mouth of the cave, and was feeding at a little distance. This would have been a favorable occasion to secure his dinner; but Dantès feared lest the report of his gun should attract attention.\n\nHe thought a moment, cut a branch of a resinous tree, lighted it at the fire at which the smugglers had prepared their breakfast, and descended with this torch.\n\nHe wished to see everything. He approached the hole he had dug, and now, with the aid of the torch, saw that his pickaxe had in reality struck against iron and wood. He planted his torch in the ground and resumed his labor.\n\nIn an instant a space three feet long by two feet broad was cleared, and Dantès could see an oaken coffer, bound with cut steel; in the middle of the lid he saw engraved on a silver plate, which was still untarnished, the arms of the Spada family—viz., a sword, _en pale_, on an oval shield, like all the Italian armorial bearings, and surmounted by a cardinal’s hat.\n\nDantès easily recognized them, Faria had so often drawn them for him. There was no longer any doubt: the treasure was there—no one would have been at such pains to conceal an empty casket. In an instant he had cleared every obstacle away, and he saw successively the lock, placed between two padlocks, and the two handles at each end, all carved as things were carved at that epoch, when art rendered the commonest metals precious.\n\nDantès seized the handles, and strove to lift the coffer; it was impossible. He sought to open it; lock and padlock were fastened; these faithful guardians seemed unwilling to surrender their trust. Dantès inserted the sharp end of the pickaxe between the coffer and the lid, and pressing with all his force on the handle, burst open the fastenings. The hinges yielded in their turn and fell, still holding in their grasp fragments of the wood, and the chest was open.\n0307m\n\nEdmond was seized with vertigo; he cocked his gun and laid it beside him. He then closed his eyes as children do in order that they may see in the resplendent night of their own imagination more stars than are visible in the firmament; then he re-opened them, and stood motionless with amazement.\n\nThree compartments divided the coffer. In the first, blazed piles of golden coin; in the second, were ranged bars of unpolished gold, which possessed nothing attractive save their value; in the third, Edmond grasped handfuls of diamonds, pearls, and rubies, which, as they fell on one another, sounded like hail against glass.\n\nAfter having touched, felt, examined these treasures, Edmond rushed through the caverns like a man seized with frenzy; he leaped on a rock, from whence he could behold the sea. He was alone—alone with these countless, these unheard-of treasures! Was he awake, or was it but a dream? Was it a transient vision, or was he face to face with reality?\n\nHe would fain have gazed upon his gold, and yet he had not strength enough; for an instant he leaned his head in his hands as if to prevent his senses from leaving him, and then rushed madly about the rocks of Monte Cristo, terrifying the wild goats and scaring the sea-fowls with his wild cries and gestures; then he returned, and, still unable to believe the evidence of his senses, rushed into the grotto, and found himself before this mine of gold and jewels.\n\nThis time he fell on his knees, and, clasping his hands convulsively, uttered a prayer intelligible to God alone. He soon became calmer and more happy, for only now did he begin to realize his felicity.\n\nHe then set himself to work to count his fortune. There were a thousand ingots of gold, each weighing from two to three pounds; then he piled up twenty-five thousand crowns, each worth about eighty francs of our money, and bearing the effigies of Alexander VI. and his predecessors; and he saw that the complement was not half empty. And he measured ten double handfuls of pearls, diamonds, and other gems, many of which, mounted by the most famous workmen, were valuable beyond their intrinsic worth.\n\nDantès saw the light gradually disappear, and fearing to be surprised in the cavern, left it, his gun in his hand. A piece of biscuit and a small quantity of rum formed his supper, and he snatched a few hours’ sleep, lying over the mouth of the cave.\n\nIt was a night of joy and terror, such as this man of stupendous emotions had already experienced twice or thrice in his lifetime.\n\n# Chapter 25. The Unknown\n\nDay, for which Dantès had so eagerly and impatiently waited with open eyes, again dawned. With the first light Dantès resumed his search. Again he climbed the rocky height he had ascended the previous evening, and strained his view to catch every peculiarity of the landscape; but it wore the same wild, barren aspect when seen by the rays of the morning sun which it had done when surveyed by the fading glimmer of eve.\n\nDescending into the grotto, he lifted the stone, filled his pockets with gems, put the box together as well and securely as he could, sprinkled fresh sand over the spot from which it had been taken, and then carefully trod down the earth to give it everywhere a uniform appearance; then, quitting the grotto, he replaced the stone, heaping on it broken masses of rocks and rough fragments of crumbling granite, filling the interstices with earth, into which he deftly inserted rapidly growing plants, such as the wild myrtle and flowering thorn, then carefully watering these new plantations, he scrupulously effaced every trace of footsteps, leaving the approach to the cavern as savage-looking and untrodden as he had found it. This done, he impatiently awaited the return of his companions. To wait at Monte Cristo for the purpose of watching like a dragon over the almost incalculable riches that had thus fallen into his possession satisfied not the cravings of his heart, which yearned to return to dwell among mankind, and to assume the rank, power, and influence which are always accorded to wealth—that first and greatest of all the forces within the grasp of man.\n\nOn the sixth day, the smugglers returned. From a distance Dantès recognized the rig and handling of _La Jeune Amélie_, and dragging himself with affected difficulty towards the landing-place, he met his companions with an assurance that, although considerably better than when they quitted him, he still suffered acutely from his late accident. He then inquired how they had fared in their trip. To this question the smugglers replied that, although successful in landing their cargo in safety, they had scarcely done so when they received intelligence that a guard-ship had just quitted the port of Toulon and was crowding all sail towards them. This obliged them to make all the speed they could to evade the enemy, when they could but lament the absence of Dantès, whose superior skill in the management of a vessel would have availed them so materially. In fact, the pursuing vessel had almost overtaken them when, fortunately, night came on, and enabled them to double the Cape of Corsica, and so elude all further pursuit. Upon the whole, however, the trip had been sufficiently successful to satisfy all concerned; while the crew, and particularly Jacopo, expressed great regrets that Dantès had not been an equal sharer with themselves in the profits, which amounted to no less a sum than fifty piastres each.\n0311m\n\nEdmond preserved the most admirable self-command, not suffering the faintest indication of a smile to escape him at the enumeration of all the benefits he would have reaped had he been able to quit the island; but as _La Jeune Amélie_ had merely come to Monte Cristo to fetch him away, he embarked that same evening, and proceeded with the captain to Leghorn.\n\nArrived at Leghorn, he repaired to the house of a Jew, a dealer in precious stones, to whom he disposed of four of his smallest diamonds for five thousand francs each. Dantès half feared that such valuable jewels in the hands of a poor sailor like himself might excite suspicion; but the cunning purchaser asked no troublesome questions concerning a bargain by which he gained a round profit of at least eighty per cent.\n\nThe following day Dantès presented Jacopo with an entirely new vessel, accompanying the gift by a donation of one hundred piastres, that he might provide himself with a suitable crew and other requisites for his outfit, upon condition that he would go at once to Marseilles for the purpose of inquiring after an old man named Louis Dantès, residing in the Allées de Meilhan, and also a young woman called Mercédès, an inhabitant of the Catalan village.\n\nJacopo could scarcely believe his senses at receiving this magnificent present, which Dantès hastened to account for by saying that he had merely been a sailor from whim and a desire to spite his family, who did not allow him as much money as he liked to spend; but that on his arrival at Leghorn he had come into possession of a large fortune, left him by an uncle, whose sole heir he was. The superior education of Dantès gave an air of such extreme probability to this statement that it never once occurred to Jacopo to doubt its accuracy.\n\nThe term for which Edmond had engaged to serve on board _La Jeune Amélie_ having expired, Dantès took leave of the captain, who at first tried all his powers of persuasion to induce him to remain as one of the crew, but having been told the history of the legacy, he ceased to importune him further.\n\nThe following morning Jacopo set sail for Marseilles, with directions from Dantès to join him at the Island of Monte Cristo.\n\nHaving seen Jacopo fairly out of the harbor, Dantès proceeded to make his final adieus on board _La Jeune Amélie_, distributing so liberal a gratuity among her crew as to secure for him the good wishes of all, and expressions of cordial interest in all that concerned him. To the captain he promised to write when he had made up his mind as to his future plans. Then Dantès departed for Genoa.\n\nAt the moment of his arrival a small yacht was under trial in the bay; this yacht had been built by order of an Englishman, who, having heard that the Genoese excelled all other builders along the shores of the Mediterranean in the construction of fast-sailing vessels, was desirous of possessing a specimen of their skill; the price agreed upon between the Englishman and the Genoese builder was forty thousand francs. Dantès, struck with the beauty and capability of the little vessel, applied to its owner to transfer it to him, offering sixty thousand francs, upon condition that he should be allowed to take immediate possession. The proposal was too advantageous to be refused, the more so as the person for whom the yacht was intended had gone upon a tour through Switzerland, and was not expected back in less than three weeks or a month, by which time the builder reckoned upon being able to complete another. A bargain was therefore struck. Dantès led the owner of the yacht to the dwelling of a Jew; retired with the latter for a few minutes to a small back parlor, and upon their return the Jew counted out to the shipbuilder the sum of sixty thousand francs in bright gold pieces.\n\nThe delighted builder then offered his services in providing a suitable crew for the little vessel, but this Dantès declined with many thanks, saying he was accustomed to cruise about quite alone, and his principal pleasure consisted in managing his yacht himself; the only thing the builder could oblige him in would be to contrive a sort of secret closet in the cabin at his bed’s head, the closet to contain three divisions, so constructed as to be concealed from all but himself. The builder cheerfully undertook the commission, and promised to have these secret places completed by the next day, Dantès furnishing the dimensions and plan in accordance with which they were to be constructed.\n0313m\n\nTwo hours afterward Dantès sailed from the port of Genoa, under the inspection of an immense crowd drawn together by curiosity to see the rich Spanish nobleman who preferred managing his own yacht. But their wonder was soon changed to admiration at seeing the perfect skill with which Dantès handled the helm. The boat, indeed, seemed to be animated with almost human intelligence, so promptly did it obey the slightest touch; and Dantès required but a short trial of his beautiful craft to acknowledge that the Genoese had not without reason attained their high reputation in the art of shipbuilding.\n\nThe spectators followed the little vessel with their eyes as long as it remained visible; they then turned their conjectures upon her probable destination. Some insisted she was making for Corsica, others the Island of Elba; bets were offered to any amount that she was bound for Spain; while Africa was positively reported by many persons as her intended course; but no one thought of Monte Cristo.\n\nYet thither it was that Dantès guided his vessel, and at Monte Cristo he arrived at the close of the second day; his boat had proved herself a first-class sailor, and had come the distance from Genoa in thirty-five hours. Dantès had carefully noted the general appearance of the shore, and, instead of landing at the usual place, he dropped anchor in the little creek. The island was utterly deserted, and bore no evidence of having been visited since he went away; his treasure was just as he had left it.\n\nEarly on the following morning he commenced the removal of his riches, and ere nightfall the whole of his immense wealth was safely deposited in the compartments of the secret locker.\n\nA week passed by. Dantès employed it in manœuvring his yacht round the island, studying it as a skilful horseman would the animal he destined for some important service, till at the end of that time he was perfectly conversant with its good and bad qualities. The former Dantès proposed to augment, the latter to remedy.\n\nUpon the eighth day he discerned a small vessel under full sail approaching Monte Cristo. As it drew near, he recognized it as the boat he had given to Jacopo. He immediately signalled it. His signal was returned, and in two hours afterwards the new-comer lay at anchor beside the yacht.\n\nA mournful answer awaited each of Edmond’s eager inquiries as to the information Jacopo had obtained. Old Dantès was dead, and Mercédès had disappeared.\n\nDantès listened to these melancholy tidings with outward calmness; but, leaping lightly ashore, he signified his desire to be quite alone. In a couple of hours he returned. Two of the men from Jacopo’s boat came on board the yacht to assist in navigating it, and he gave orders that she should be steered direct to Marseilles. For his father’s death he was in some manner prepared; but he knew not how to account for the mysterious disappearance of Mercédès.\n\nWithout divulging his secret, Dantès could not give sufficiently clear instructions to an agent. There were, besides, other particulars he was desirous of ascertaining, and those were of a nature he alone could investigate in a manner satisfactory to himself. His looking-glass had assured him, during his stay at Leghorn, that he ran no risk of recognition; moreover, he had now the means of adopting any disguise he thought proper. One fine morning, then, his yacht, followed by the little fishing-boat, boldly entered the port of Marseilles, and anchored exactly opposite the spot from whence, on the never-to-be-forgotten night of his departure for the Château d’If, he had been put on board the boat destined to convey him thither.\n0315m\n\nStill Dantès could not view without a shudder the approach of a gendarme who accompanied the officers deputed to demand his bill of health ere the yacht was permitted to hold communication with the shore; but with that perfect self-possession he had acquired during his acquaintance with Faria, Dantès coolly presented an English passport he had obtained from Leghorn, and as this gave him a standing which a French passport would not have afforded, he was informed that there existed no obstacle to his immediate debarkation.\n\nThe first person to attract the attention of Dantès, as he landed on the Canebière, was one of the crew belonging to the _Pharaon_. Edmond welcomed the meeting with this fellow—who had been one of his own sailors—as a sure means of testing the extent of the change which time had worked in his own appearance. Going straight towards him, he propounded a variety of questions on different subjects, carefully watching the man’s countenance as he did so; but not a word or look implied that he had the slightest idea of ever having seen before the person with whom he was then conversing.\n\nGiving the sailor a piece of money in return for his civility, Dantès proceeded onwards; but ere he had gone many steps he heard the man loudly calling him to stop.\n\nDantès instantly turned to meet him.\n\n“I beg your pardon, sir,” said the honest fellow, in almost breathless haste, “but I believe you made a mistake; you intended to give me a two-franc piece, and see, you gave me a double Napoleon.”\n\n“Thank you, my good friend. I see that I have made a trifling mistake, as you say; but by way of rewarding your honesty I give you another double Napoleon, that you may drink to my health, and be able to ask your messmates to join you.”\n\nSo extreme was the surprise of the sailor, that he was unable even to thank Edmond, whose receding figure he continued to gaze after in speechless astonishment. “Some nabob from India,” was his comment.\n\nDantès, meanwhile, went on his way. Each step he trod oppressed his heart with fresh emotion; his first and most indelible recollections were there; not a tree, not a street, that he passed but seemed filled with dear and cherished memories. And thus he proceeded onwards till he arrived at the end of the Rue de Noailles, from whence a full view of the Allées de Meilhan was obtained. At this spot, so pregnant with fond and filial remembrances, his heart beat almost to bursting, his knees tottered under him, a mist floated over his sight, and had he not clung for support to one of the trees, he would inevitably have fallen to the ground and been crushed beneath the many vehicles continually passing there. Recovering himself, however, he wiped the perspiration from his brows, and stopped not again till he found himself at the door of the house in which his father had lived.\n\nThe nasturtiums and other plants, which his father had delighted to train before his window, had all disappeared from the upper part of the house.\n\nLeaning against the tree, he gazed thoughtfully for a time at the upper stories of the shabby little house. Then he advanced to the door, and asked whether there were any rooms to be let. Though answered in the negative, he begged so earnestly to be permitted to visit those on the fifth floor, that, in despite of the oft-repeated assurance of the _concierge_ that they were occupied, Dantès succeeded in inducing the man to go up to the tenants, and ask permission for a gentleman to be allowed to look at them.\n\nThe tenants of the humble lodging were a young couple who had been scarcely married a week; and seeing them, Dantès sighed heavily. Nothing in the two small chambers forming the apartments remained as it had been in the time of the elder Dantès; the very paper was different, while the articles of antiquated furniture with which the rooms had been filled in Edmond’s time had all disappeared; the four walls alone remained as he had left them.\n\nThe bed belonging to the present occupants was placed as the former owner of the chamber had been accustomed to have his; and, in spite of his efforts to prevent it, the eyes of Edmond were suffused in tears as he reflected that on that spot the old man had breathed his last, vainly calling for his son.\n\nThe young couple gazed with astonishment at the sight of their visitor’s emotion, and wondered to see the large tears silently chasing each other down his otherwise stern and immovable features; but they felt the sacredness of his grief, and kindly refrained from questioning him as to its cause, while, with instinctive delicacy, they left him to indulge his sorrow alone.\n0317m\n\nWhen he withdrew from the scene of his painful recollections, they both accompanied him downstairs, reiterating their hope that he would come again whenever he pleased, and assuring him that their poor dwelling would ever be open to him.\n\nAs Edmond passed the door on the fourth floor, he paused to inquire whether Caderousse the tailor still dwelt there; but he received for reply, that the person in question had got into difficulties, and at the present time kept a small inn on the route from Bellegarde to Beaucaire.\n\nHaving obtained the address of the person to whom the house in the Allées de Meilhan belonged, Dantès next proceeded thither, and, under the name of Lord Wilmore (the name and title inscribed on his passport), purchased the small dwelling for the sum of twenty-five thousand francs, at least ten thousand more than it was worth; but had its owner asked half a million, it would unhesitatingly have been given.\n\nThe very same day the occupants of the apartments on the fifth floor of the house, now become the property of Dantès, were duly informed by the notary who had arranged the necessary transfer of deeds, etc., that the new landlord gave them their choice of any of the rooms in the house, without the least augmentation of rent, upon condition of their giving instant possession of the two small chambers they at present inhabited.\n\nThis strange event aroused great wonder and curiosity in the neighborhood of the Allées de Meilhan, and a multitude of theories were afloat, none of which was anywhere near the truth. But what raised public astonishment to a climax, and set all conjecture at defiance, was the knowledge that the same stranger who had in the morning visited the Allées de Meilhan had been seen in the evening walking in the little village of the Catalans, and afterwards observed to enter a poor fisherman’s hut, and to pass more than an hour in inquiring after persons who had either been dead or gone away for more than fifteen or sixteen years.\n\nBut on the following day the family from whom all these particulars had been asked received a handsome present, consisting of an entirely new fishing-boat, with two seines and a tender.\n\nThe delighted recipients of these munificent gifts would gladly have poured out their thanks to their generous benefactor, but they had seen him, upon quitting the hut, merely give some orders to a sailor, and then springing lightly on horseback, leave Marseilles by the Porte d’Aix.\n\n# Chapter 26. The Pont du Gard Inn\n\nSuch of my readers as have made a pedestrian excursion to the south of France may perchance have noticed, about midway between the town of Beaucaire and the village of Bellegarde,—a little nearer to the former than to the latter,—a small roadside inn, from the front of which hung, creaking and flapping in the wind, a sheet of tin covered with a grotesque representation of the Pont du Gard. This modern place of entertainment stood on the left-hand side of the post road, and backed upon the Rhône. It also boasted of what in Languedoc is styled a garden, consisting of a small plot of ground, on the side opposite to the main entrance reserved for the reception of guests. A few dingy olives and stunted fig-trees struggled hard for existence, but their withered dusty foliage abundantly proved how unequal was the conflict. Between these sickly shrubs grew a scanty supply of garlic, tomatoes, and eschalots; while, lone and solitary, like a forgotten sentinel, a tall pine raised its melancholy head in one of the corners of this unattractive spot, and displayed its flexible stem and fan-shaped summit dried and cracked by the fierce heat of the sub-tropical sun.\n\nAll these trees, great or small, were turned in the direction to which the Mistral blows, one of the three curses of Provence, the others being the Durance and the Parliament.\n\nIn the surrounding plain, which more resembled a dusty lake than solid ground, were scattered a few miserable stalks of wheat, the effect, no doubt, of a curious desire on the part of the agriculturists of the country to see whether such a thing as the raising of grain in those parched regions was practicable. Each stalk served as a perch for a grasshopper, which regaled the passers-by through this Egyptian scene with its strident, monotonous note.\n\nFor about seven or eight years the little tavern had been kept by a man and his wife, with two servants,—a chambermaid named Trinette, and a hostler called Pecaud. This small staff was quite equal to all the requirements, for a canal between Beaucaire and Aiguemortes had revolutionized transportation by substituting boats for the cart and the stagecoach. And, as though to add to the daily misery which this prosperous canal inflicted on the unfortunate innkeeper, whose utter ruin it was fast accomplishing, it was situated between the Rhône from which it had its source and the post-road it had depleted, not a hundred steps from the inn, of which we have given a brief but faithful description.\n\nThe innkeeper himself was a man of from forty to fifty-five years of age, tall, strong, and bony, a perfect specimen of the natives of those southern latitudes; he had dark, sparkling, and deep-set eyes, hooked nose, and teeth white as those of a carnivorous animal; his hair, like his beard, which he wore under his chin, was thick and curly, and in spite of his age but slightly interspersed with a few silvery threads. His naturally dark complexion had assumed a still further shade of brown from the habit the unfortunate man had acquired of stationing himself from morning till eve at the threshold of his door, on the lookout for guests who seldom came, yet there he stood, day after day, exposed to the meridional rays of a burning sun, with no other protection for his head than a red handkerchief twisted around it, after the manner of the Spanish muleteers. This man was our old acquaintance, Gaspard Caderousse.\n\nHis wife, on the contrary, whose maiden name had been Madeleine Radelle, was pale, meagre, and sickly-looking. Born in the neighborhood of Arles, she had shared in the beauty for which its women are proverbial; but that beauty had gradually withered beneath the devastating influence of the slow fever so prevalent among dwellers by the ponds of Aiguemortes and the marshes of Camargue. She remained nearly always in her second-floor chamber, shivering in her chair, or stretched languid and feeble on her bed, while her husband kept his daily watch at the door—a duty he performed with so much the greater willingness, as it saved him the necessity of listening to the endless plaints and murmurs of his helpmate, who never saw him without breaking out into bitter invectives against fate; to all of which her husband would calmly return an unvarying reply, in these philosophic words:\n\n“Hush, La Carconte. It is God’s pleasure that things should be so.”\n\nThe sobriquet of La Carconte had been bestowed on Madeleine Radelle from the fact that she had been born in a village, so called, situated between Salon and Lambesc; and as a custom existed among the inhabitants of that part of France where Caderousse lived of styling every person by some particular and distinctive appellation, her husband had bestowed on her the name of La Carconte in place of her sweet and euphonious name of Madeleine, which, in all probability, his rude guttural language would not have enabled him to pronounce.\n\nStill, let it not be supposed that amid this affected resignation to the will of Providence, the unfortunate innkeeper did not writhe under the double misery of seeing the hateful canal carry off his customers and his profits, and the daily infliction of his peevish partner’s murmurs and lamentations.\n\n0323m\nLike other dwellers in the south, he was a man of sober habits and moderate desires, but fond of external show, vain, and addicted to display. During the days of his prosperity, not a festivity took place without himself and wife being among the spectators. He dressed in the picturesque costume worn upon grand occasions by the inhabitants of the south of France, bearing equal resemblance to the style adopted both by the Catalans and Andalusians; while La Carconte displayed the charming fashion prevalent among the women of Arles, a mode of attire borrowed equally from Greece and Arabia. But, by degrees, watch-chains, necklaces, parti-colored scarves, embroidered bodices, velvet vests, elegantly worked stockings, striped gaiters, and silver buckles for the shoes, all disappeared; and Gaspard Caderousse, unable to appear abroad in his pristine splendor, had given up any further participation in the pomps and vanities, both for himself and wife, although a bitter feeling of envious discontent filled his mind as the sound of mirth and merry music from the joyous revellers reached even the miserable hostelry to which he still clung, more for the shelter than the profit it afforded.\n\nCaderousse, then, was, as usual, at his place of observation before the door, his eyes glancing listlessly from a piece of closely shaven grass—on which some fowls were industriously, though fruitlessly, endeavoring to turn up some grain or insect suited to their palate—to the deserted road, which led away to the north and south, when he was aroused by the shrill voice of his wife, and grumbling to himself as he went, he mounted to her chamber, first taking care, however, to set the entrance door wide open, as an invitation to any chance traveller who might be passing.\n\nAt the moment Caderousse quitted his sentry-like watch before the door, the road on which he so eagerly strained his sight was void and lonely as a desert at midday. There it lay stretching out into one interminable line of dust and sand, with its sides bordered by tall, meagre trees, altogether presenting so uninviting an appearance, that no one in his senses could have imagined that any traveller, at liberty to regulate his hours for journeying, would choose to expose himself in such a formidable Sahara.\n\nNevertheless, had Caderousse but retained his post a few minutes longer, he might have caught a dim outline of something approaching from the direction of Bellegarde; as the moving object drew nearer, he would easily have perceived that it consisted of a man and horse, between whom the kindest and most amiable understanding appeared to exist. The horse was of Hungarian breed, and ambled along at an easy pace. His rider was a priest, dressed in black, and wearing a three-cornered hat; and, despite of the ardent rays of a noonday sun, the pair came on with a fair degree of rapidity.\n\nHaving arrived before the Pont du Gard, the horse stopped, but whether for his own pleasure or that of his rider would have been difficult to say. However that might have been, the priest, dismounting, led his steed by the bridle in search of some place to which he could secure him. Availing himself of a handle that projected from a half-fallen door, he tied the animal safely and having drawn a red cotton handkerchief, from his pocket, wiped away the perspiration that streamed from his brow, then, advancing to the door, struck thrice with the end of his iron-shod stick.\n\nAt this unusual sound, a huge black dog came rushing to meet the daring assailant of his ordinarily tranquil abode, snarling and displaying his sharp white teeth with a determined hostility that abundantly proved how little he was accustomed to society. At that moment a heavy footstep was heard descending the wooden staircase that led from the upper floor, and, with many bows and courteous smiles, the host of the Pont du Gard besought his guest to enter.\n\n0319m\n“You are welcome, sir, most welcome!” repeated the astonished Caderousse. “Now, then, Margotin,” cried he, speaking to the dog, “will you be quiet? Pray don’t heed him, sir!—he only barks, he never bites. I make no doubt a glass of good wine would be acceptable this dreadfully hot day.” Then perceiving for the first time the garb of the traveller he had to entertain, Caderousse hastily exclaimed: “A thousand pardons! I really did not observe whom I had the honor to receive under my poor roof. What would the abbé please to have? What refreshment can I offer? All I have is at his service.”\n\nThe priest gazed on the person addressing him with a long and searching gaze—there even seemed a disposition on his part to court a similar scrutiny on the part of the innkeeper; then, observing in the countenance of the latter no other expression than extreme surprise at his own want of attention to an inquiry so courteously worded, he deemed it as well to terminate this dumb show, and therefore said, speaking with a strong Italian accent, “You are, I presume, M. Caderousse?”\n\n“Yes, sir,” answered the host, even more surprised at the question than he had been by the silence which had preceded it; “I am Gaspard Caderousse, at your service.”\n\n“Gaspard Caderousse,” rejoined the priest. “Yes,—Christian and surname are the same. You formerly lived, I believe in the Allées de Meilhan, on the fourth floor?”\n\n0325m\n“I did.”\n\n“And you followed the business of a tailor?”\n\n“True, I was a tailor, till the trade fell off. It is so hot at Marseilles, that really I believe that the respectable inhabitants will in time go without any clothing whatever. But talking of heat, is there nothing I can offer you by way of refreshment?”\n\n“Yes; let me have a bottle of your best wine, and then, with your permission, we will resume our conversation from where we left off.”\n\n“As you please, sir,” said Caderousse, who, anxious not to lose the present opportunity of finding a customer for one of the few bottles of Cahors still remaining in his possession, hastily raised a trap-door in the floor of the apartment they were in, which served both as parlor and kitchen.\n\nUpon issuing forth from his subterranean retreat at the expiration of five minutes, he found the abbé seated upon a wooden stool, leaning his elbow on a table, while Margotin, whose animosity seemed appeased by the unusual command of the traveller for refreshments, had crept up to him, and had established himself very comfortably between his knees, his long, skinny neck resting on his lap, while his dim eye was fixed earnestly on the traveller’s face.\n\n“Are you quite alone?” inquired the guest, as Caderousse placed before him the bottle of wine and a glass.\n\n“Quite, quite alone,” replied the man—“or, at least, practically so, for my poor wife, who is the only person in the house besides myself, is laid up with illness, and unable to render me the least assistance, poor thing!”\n\n“You are married, then?” said the priest, with a show of interest, glancing round as he spoke at the scanty furnishings of the apartment.\n\n“Ah, sir,” said Caderousse with a sigh, “it is easy to perceive I am not a rich man; but in this world a man does not thrive the better for being honest.” The abbé fixed on him a searching, penetrating glance.\n\n“Yes, honest—I can certainly say that much for myself,” continued the innkeeper, fairly sustaining the scrutiny of the abbé’s gaze; “I can boast with truth of being an honest man; and,” continued he significantly, with a hand on his breast and shaking his head, “that is more than everyone can say nowadays.”\n0327m\n\n“So much the better for you, if what you assert be true,” said the abbé; “for I am firmly persuaded that, sooner or later, the good will be rewarded, and the wicked punished.”\n\n“Such words as those belong to your profession,” answered Caderousse, “and you do well to repeat them; but,” added he, with a bitter expression of countenance, “one is free to believe them or not, as one pleases.”\n\n“You are wrong to speak thus,” said the abbé; “and perhaps I may, in my own person, be able to prove to you how completely you are in error.”\n\n“What mean you?” inquired Caderousse with a look of surprise.\n\n“In the first place, I must be satisfied that you are the person I am in search of.”\n\n“What proofs do you require?”\n\n“Did you, in the year 1814 or 1815, know anything of a young sailor named Dantès?”\n\n“Dantès? Did I know poor dear Edmond? Why, Edmond Dantès and myself were intimate friends!” exclaimed Caderousse, whose countenance flushed darkly as he caught the penetrating gaze of the abbé fixed on him, while the clear, calm eye of the questioner seemed to dilate with feverish scrutiny.\n\n“You remind me,” said the priest, “that the young man concerning whom I asked you was said to bear the name of Edmond.”\n\n“Said to bear the name!” repeated Caderousse, becoming excited and eager. “Why, he was so called as truly as I myself bore the appellation of Gaspard Caderousse; but tell me, I pray, what has become of poor Edmond? Did you know him? Is he alive and at liberty? Is he prosperous and happy?”\n\n“He died a more wretched, hopeless, heart-broken prisoner than the felons who pay the penalty of their crimes at the galleys of Toulon.”\n\nA deadly pallor followed the flush on the countenance of Caderousse, who turned away, and the priest saw him wiping the tears from his eyes with the corner of the red handkerchief twisted round his head.\n\n“Poor fellow, poor fellow!” murmured Caderousse. “Well, there, sir, is another proof that good people are never rewarded on this earth, and that none but the wicked prosper. Ah,” continued Caderousse, speaking in the highly colored language of the South, “the world grows worse and worse. Why does not God, if he really hates the wicked, as he is said to do, send down brimstone and fire, and consume them altogether?”\n\n“You speak as though you had loved this young Dantès,” observed the abbé, without taking any notice of his companion’s vehemence.\n\n“And so I did,” replied Caderousse; “though once, I confess, I envied him his good fortune. But I swear to you, sir, I swear to you, by everything a man holds dear, I have, since then, deeply and sincerely lamented his unhappy fate.”\n\nThere was a brief silence, during which the fixed, searching eye of the abbé was employed in scrutinizing the agitated features of the innkeeper.\n\n“You knew the poor lad, then?” continued Caderousse.\n\n“I was called to see him on his dying bed, that I might administer to him the consolations of religion.”\n\n“And of what did he die?” asked Caderousse in a choking voice.\n\n“Of what, think you, do young and strong men die in prison, when they have scarcely numbered their thirtieth year, unless it be of imprisonment?” Caderousse wiped away the large beads of perspiration that gathered on his brow.\n0329m\n\n“But the strangest part of the story is,” resumed the abbé, “that Dantès, even in his dying moments, swore by his crucified Redeemer, that he was utterly ignorant of the cause of his detention.”\n\n“And so he was,” murmured Caderousse. “How should he have been otherwise? Ah, sir, the poor fellow told you the truth.”\n\n“And for that reason, he besought me to try and clear up a mystery he had never been able to penetrate, and to clear his memory should any foul spot or stain have fallen on it.”\n\nAnd here the look of the abbé, becoming more and more fixed, seemed to rest with ill-concealed satisfaction on the gloomy depression which was rapidly spreading over the countenance of Caderousse.\n\n“A rich Englishman,” continued the abbé, “who had been his companion in misfortune, but had been released from prison during the second restoration, was possessed of a diamond of immense value; this jewel he bestowed on Dantès upon himself quitting the prison, as a mark of his gratitude for the kindness and brotherly care with which Dantès had nursed him in a severe illness he underwent during his confinement. Instead of employing this diamond in attempting to bribe his jailers, who might only have taken it and then betrayed him to the governor, Dantès carefully preserved it, that in the event of his getting out of prison he might have wherewithal to live, for the sale of such a diamond would have quite sufficed to make his fortune.”\n\n“Then, I suppose,” asked Caderousse, with eager, glowing looks, “that it was a stone of immense value?”\n\n“Why, everything is relative,” answered the abbé. “To one in Edmond’s position the diamond certainly was of great value. It was estimated at fifty thousand francs.”\n\n“Bless me!” exclaimed Caderousse, “fifty thousand francs! Surely the diamond was as large as a nut to be worth all that.”\n\n“No,” replied the abbé, “it was not of such a size as that; but you shall judge for yourself. I have it with me.”\n\nThe sharp gaze of Caderousse was instantly directed towards the priest’s garments, as though hoping to discover the location of the treasure. Calmly drawing forth from his pocket a small box covered with black shagreen, the abbé opened it, and displayed to the dazzled eyes of Caderousse the sparkling jewel it contained, set in a ring of admirable workmanship.\n\n“And that diamond,” cried Caderousse, almost breathless with eager admiration, “you say, is worth fifty thousand francs?”\n\n“It is, without the setting, which is also valuable,” replied the abbé, as he closed the box, and returned it to his pocket, while its brilliant hues seemed still to dance before the eyes of the fascinated innkeeper.\n\n“But how comes the diamond in your possession, sir? Did Edmond make you his heir?”\n\n“No, merely his testamentary executor. ‘I once possessed four dear and faithful friends, besides the maiden to whom I was betrothed’ he said; ‘and I feel convinced they have all unfeignedly grieved over my loss. The name of one of the four friends is Caderousse.’” The innkeeper shivered.\n\n“‘Another of the number,’” continued the abbé, without seeming to notice the emotion of Caderousse, “‘is called Danglars; and the third, in spite of being my rival, entertained a very sincere affection for me.’”\n\nA fiendish smile played over the features of Caderousse, who was about to break in upon the abbé’s speech, when the latter, waving his hand, said, “Allow me to finish first, and then if you have any observations to make, you can do so afterwards. ‘The third of my friends, although my rival, was much attached to me,—his name was Fernand; that of my betrothed was’—Stay, stay,” continued the abbé, “I have forgotten what he called her.”\n\n“Mercédès,” said Caderousse eagerly.\n\n“True,” said the abbé, with a stifled sigh, “Mercédès it was.”\n\n“Go on,” urged Caderousse.\n\n“Bring me a _carafe_ of water,” said the abbé.\n\nCaderousse quickly performed the stranger’s bidding; and after pouring some into a glass, and slowly swallowing its contents, the abbé, resuming his usual placidity of manner, said, as he placed his empty glass on the table:\n\n“Where did we leave off?”\n\n“The name of Edmond’s betrothed was Mercédès.”\n\n“To be sure. ‘You will go to Marseilles,’ said Dantès,—for you understand, I repeat his words just as he uttered them. Do you understand?”\n\n“Perfectly.”\n\n“‘You will sell this diamond; you will divide the money into five equal parts, and give an equal portion to these good friends, the only persons who have loved me upon earth.’”\n\n“But why into five parts?” asked Caderousse; “you only mentioned four persons.”\n\n“Because the fifth is dead, as I hear. The fifth sharer in Edmond’s bequest, was his own father.”\n\n“Too true, too true!” ejaculated Caderousse, almost suffocated by the contending passions which assailed him, “the poor old man did die.”\n\n“I learned so much at Marseilles,” replied the abbé, making a strong effort to appear indifferent; “but from the length of time that has elapsed since the death of the elder Dantès, I was unable to obtain any particulars of his end. Can you enlighten me on that point?”\n\n“I do not know who could if I could not,” said Caderousse. “Why, I lived almost on the same floor with the poor old man. Ah, yes, about a year after the disappearance of his son the poor old man died.”\n\n“Of what did he die?”\n\n“Why, the doctors called his complaint gastro-enteritis, I believe; his acquaintances say he died of grief; but I, who saw him in his dying moments, I say he died of——”\n\nCaderousse paused.\n\n“Of what?” asked the priest, anxiously and eagerly.\n\n“Why, of downright starvation.”\n\n“Starvation!” exclaimed the abbé, springing from his seat. “Why, the vilest animals are not suffered to die by such a death as that. The very dogs that wander houseless and homeless in the streets find some pitying hand to cast them a mouthful of bread; and that a man, a Christian, should be allowed to perish of hunger in the midst of other men who call themselves Christians, is too horrible for belief. Oh, it is impossible!—utterly impossible!”\n\n“What I have said, I have said,” answered Caderousse.\n\n“And you are a fool for having said anything about it,” said a voice from the top of the stairs. “Why should you meddle with what does not concern you?”\n\nThe two men turned quickly, and saw the sickly countenance of La Carconte peering between the baluster rails; attracted by the sound of voices, she had feebly dragged herself down the stairs, and, seated on the lower step, head on knees, she had listened to the foregoing conversation.\n\n“Mind your own business, wife,” replied Caderousse sharply. “This gentleman asks me for information, which common politeness will not permit me to refuse.”\n\n“Politeness, you simpleton!” retorted La Carconte. “What have you to do with politeness, I should like to know? Better study a little common prudence. How do you know the motives that person may have for trying to extract all he can from you?”\n\n“I pledge you my word, madam,” said the abbé, “that my intentions are good; and that your husband can incur no risk, provided he answers me candidly.”\n\n“Ah, that’s all very fine,” retorted the woman. “Nothing is easier than to begin with fair promises and assurances of nothing to fear; but when poor, silly folks, like my husband there, have been persuaded to tell all they know, the promises and assurances of safety are quickly forgotten; and at some moment when nobody is expecting it, behold trouble and misery, and all sorts of persecutions, are heaped on the unfortunate wretches, who cannot even see whence all their afflictions come.”\n\n“Nay, nay, my good woman, make yourself perfectly easy, I beg of you. Whatever evils may befall you, they will not be occasioned by my instrumentality, that I solemnly promise you.”\n\nLa Carconte muttered a few inarticulate words, then let her head again drop upon her knees, and went into a fit of ague, leaving the two speakers to resume the conversation, but remaining so as to be able to hear every word they uttered. Again the abbé had been obliged to swallow a draught of water to calm the emotions that threatened to overpower him.\n\nWhen he had sufficiently recovered himself, he said, “It appears, then, that the miserable old man you were telling me of was forsaken by everyone. Surely, had not such been the case, he would not have perished by so dreadful a death.”\n\n“Why, he was not altogether forsaken,” continued Caderousse, “for Mercédès the Catalan and Monsieur Morrel were very kind to him; but somehow the poor old man had contracted a profound hatred for Fernand—the very person,” added Caderousse with a bitter smile, “that you named just now as being one of Dantès’ faithful and attached friends.”\n\n“And was he not so?” asked the abbé.\n\n“Gaspard, Gaspard!” murmured the woman, from her seat on the stairs, “mind what you are saying!”\n\nCaderousse made no reply to these words, though evidently irritated and annoyed by the interruption, but, addressing the abbé, said, “Can a man be faithful to another whose wife he covets and desires for himself? But Dantès was so honorable and true in his own nature, that he believed everybody’s professions of friendship. Poor Edmond, he was cruelly deceived; but it was fortunate that he never knew, or he might have found it more difficult, when on his deathbed, to pardon his enemies. And, whatever people may say,” continued Caderousse, in his native language, which was not altogether devoid of rude poetry, “I cannot help being more frightened at the idea of the malediction of the dead than the hatred of the living.”\n\n“Imbecile!” exclaimed La Carconte.\n\n“Do you, then, know in what manner Fernand injured Dantès?” inquired the abbé of Caderousse.\n\n“Do I? No one better.”\n\n“Speak out then, say what it was!”\n\n“Gaspard!” cried La Carconte, “do as you will; you are master—but if you take my advice you’ll hold your tongue.”\n\n“Well, wife,” replied Caderousse, “I don’t know but what you’re right!”\n\n“So you will say nothing?” asked the abbé.\n\n“Why, what good would it do?” asked Caderousse. “If the poor lad were living, and came to me and begged that I would candidly tell which were his true and which his false friends, why, perhaps, I should not hesitate. But you tell me he is no more, and therefore can have nothing to do with hatred or revenge, so let all such feeling be buried with him.”\n\n“You prefer, then,” said the abbé, “that I should bestow on men you say are false and treacherous, the reward intended for faithful friendship?”\n\n“That is true enough,” returned Caderousse. “You say truly, the gift of poor Edmond was not meant for such traitors as Fernand and Danglars; besides, what would it be to them? no more than a drop of water in the ocean.”\n\n“Remember,” chimed in La Carconte, “those two could crush you at a single blow!”\n\n“How so?” inquired the abbé. “Are these persons, then, so rich and powerful?”\n\n“Do you not know their history?”\n\n“I do not. Pray relate it to me!”\n\nCaderousse seemed to reflect for a few moments, then said, “No, truly, it would take up too much time.”\n\n“Well, my good friend,” returned the abbé, in a tone that indicated utter indifference on his part, “you are at liberty, either to speak or be silent, just as you please; for my own part, I respect your scruples and admire your sentiments; so let the matter end. I shall do my duty as conscientiously as I can, and fulfil my promise to the dying man. My first business will be to dispose of this diamond.”\n\nSo saying, the abbé again drew the small box from his pocket, opened it, and contrived to hold it in such a light, that a bright flash of brilliant hues passed before the dazzled gaze of Caderousse.\n\n“Wife, wife!” cried he in a hoarse voice, “come here!”\n\n“Diamond!” exclaimed La Carconte, rising and descending to the chamber with a tolerably firm step; “what diamond are you talking about?”\n\n“Why, did you not hear all we said?” inquired Caderousse. “It is a beautiful diamond left by poor Edmond Dantès, to be sold, and the money divided between his father, Mercédès, his betrothed bride, Fernand, Danglars, and myself. The jewel is worth at least fifty thousand francs.”\n\n“Oh, what a magnificent jewel!” cried the astonished woman.\n\n“The fifth part of the profits from this stone belongs to us then, does it not?” asked Caderousse.\n\n“It does,” replied the abbé; “with the addition of an equal division of that part intended for the elder Dantès, which I believe myself at liberty to divide equally with the four survivors.”\n\n“And why among us four?” inquired Caderousse.\n\n“As being the friends Edmond esteemed most faithful and devoted to him.”\n\n“I don’t call those friends who betray and ruin you,” murmured the wife in her turn, in a low, muttering voice.\n\n“Of course not!” rejoined Caderousse quickly; “no more do I, and that was what I was observing to this gentleman just now. I said I looked upon it as a sacrilegious profanation to reward treachery, perhaps crime.”\n\n“Remember,” answered the abbé calmly, as he replaced the jewel and its case in the pocket of his cassock, “it is your fault, not mine, that I do so. You will have the goodness to furnish me with the address of both Fernand and Danglars, in order that I may execute Edmond’s last wishes.”\n\nThe agitation of Caderousse became extreme, and large drops of perspiration rolled from his heated brow. As he saw the abbé rise from his seat and go towards the door, as though to ascertain if his horse were sufficiently refreshed to continue his journey, Caderousse and his wife exchanged looks of deep meaning.\n\n0335m\n“There, you see, wife,” said the former, “this splendid diamond might all be ours, if we chose!”\n\n“Do you believe it?”\n\n“Why, surely a man of his holy profession would not deceive us!”\n\n“Well,” replied La Carconte, “do as you like. For my part, I wash my hands of the affair.”\n\nSo saying, she once more climbed the staircase leading to her chamber, her body convulsed with chills, and her teeth rattling in her head, in spite of the intense heat of the weather. Arrived at the top stair, she turned round, and called out, in a warning tone, to her husband, “Gaspard, consider well what you are about to do!”\n\n“I have both reflected and decided,” answered he.\n\nLa Carconte then entered her chamber, the flooring of which creaked beneath her heavy, uncertain tread, as she proceeded towards her armchair, into which she fell as though exhausted.\n\n“Well,” asked the abbé, as he returned to the apartment below, “what have you made up your mind to do?”\n\n“To tell you all I know,” was the reply.\n\n“I certainly think you act wisely in so doing,” said the priest. “Not because I have the least desire to learn anything you may please to conceal from me, but simply that if, through your assistance, I could distribute the legacy according to the wishes of the testator, why, so much the better, that is all.”\n\n“I hope it may be so,” replied Caderousse, his face flushed with cupidity.\n\n“I am all attention,” said the abbé.\n\n“Stop a minute,” answered Caderousse; “we might be interrupted in the most interesting part of my story, which would be a pity; and it is as well that your visit hither should be made known only to ourselves.”\n\nWith these words he went stealthily to the door, which he closed, and, by way of still greater precaution, bolted and barred it, as he was accustomed to do at night.\n\nDuring this time the abbé had chosen his place for listening at his ease. He removed his seat into a corner of the room, where he himself would be in deep shadow, while the light would be fully thrown on the narrator; then, with head bent down and hands clasped, or rather clenched together, he prepared to give his whole attention to Caderousse, who seated himself on the little stool, exactly opposite to him.\n\n“Remember, this is no affair of mine,” said the trembling voice of La Carconte, as though through the flooring of her chamber she viewed the scene that was enacting below.\n\n“Enough, enough!” replied Caderousse; “say no more about it; I will take all the consequences upon myself.”\nAnd he began his story.\n\nFirst, sir,” said Caderousse, “you must make me a promise.”\n\n“What is that?” inquired the abbé.\n\n“Why, if you ever make use of the details I am about to give you, that you will never let anyone know that it was I who supplied them; for the persons of whom I am about to talk are rich and powerful, and if they only laid the tips of their fingers on me, I should break to pieces like glass.”\n\n“Make yourself easy, my friend,” replied the abbé. “I am a priest, and confessions die in my breast. Recollect, our only desire is to carry out, in a fitting manner, the last wishes of our friend. Speak, then, without reserve, as without hatred; tell the truth, the whole truth; I do not know, never may know, the persons of whom you are about to speak; besides, I am an Italian, and not a Frenchman, and belong to God, and not to man, and I shall shortly retire to my convent, which I have only quitted to fulfil the last wishes of a dying man.”\n\nThis positive assurance seemed to give Caderousse a little courage.\n\n“Well, then, under these circumstances,” said Caderousse, “I will, I even believe I ought to undeceive you as to the friendship which poor Edmond thought so sincere and unquestionable.”\n\n“Begin with his father, if you please.” said the abbé; “Edmond talked to me a great deal about the old man for whom he had the deepest love.”\n\n“The history is a sad one, sir,” said Caderousse, shaking his head; “perhaps you know all the earlier part of it?”\n\n“Yes.” answered the abbé; “Edmond related to me everything until the moment when he was arrested in a small cabaret close to Marseilles.”\n\n“At La Réserve! Oh, yes; I can see it all before me this moment.”\n\n“Was it not his betrothal feast?”\n\n“It was and the feast that began so gayly had a very sorrowful ending; a police commissary, followed by four soldiers, entered, and Dantès was arrested.”\n\n“Yes, and up to this point I know all,” said the priest. “Dantès himself only knew that which personally concerned him, for he never beheld again the five persons I have named to you, or heard mention of anyone of them.”\n\n“Well, when Dantès was arrested, Monsieur Morrel hastened to obtain the particulars, and they were very sad. The old man returned alone to his home, folded up his wedding suit with tears in his eyes, and paced up and down his chamber the whole day, and would not go to bed at all, for I was underneath him and heard him walking the whole night; and for myself, I assure you I could not sleep either, for the grief of the poor father gave me great uneasiness, and every step he took went to my heart as really as if his foot had pressed against my breast.\n\n“The next day Mercédès came to implore the protection of M. de Villefort; she did not obtain it, however, and went to visit the old man; when she saw him so miserable and heart-broken, having passed a sleepless night, and not touched food since the previous day, she wished him to go with her that she might take care of him; but the old man would not consent. ‘No,’ was the old man’s reply, ‘I will not leave this house, for my poor dear boy loves me better than anything in the world; and if he gets out of prison he will come and see me the first thing, and what would he think if I did not wait here for him?’ I heard all this from the window, for I was anxious that Mercédès should persuade the old man to accompany her, for his footsteps over my head night and day did not leave me a moment’s repose.”\n\n“But did you not go upstairs and try to console the poor old man?” asked the abbé.\n\n“Ah, sir,” replied Caderousse, “we cannot console those who will not be consoled, and he was one of these; besides, I know not why, but he seemed to dislike seeing me. One night, however, I heard his sobs, and I could not resist my desire to go up to him, but when I reached his door he was no longer weeping but praying. I cannot now repeat to you, sir, all the eloquent words and imploring language he made use of; it was more than piety, it was more than grief, and I, who am no canter, and hate the Jesuits, said then to myself, ‘It is really well, and I am very glad that I have not any children; for if I were a father and felt such excessive grief as the old man does, and did not find in my memory or heart all he is now saying, I should throw myself into the sea at once, for I could not bear it.’”\n\n“Poor father!” murmured the priest.\n\n“From day to day he lived on alone, and more and more solitary. M. Morrel and Mercédès came to see him, but his door was closed; and, although I was certain he was at home, he would not make any answer. One day, when, contrary to his custom, he had admitted Mercédès, and the poor girl, in spite of her own grief and despair, endeavored to console him, he said to her,—‘Be assured, my dear daughter, he is dead; and instead of expecting him, it is he who is awaiting us; I am quite happy, for I am the oldest, and of course shall see him first.’\n\n“However well disposed a person may be, why, you see we leave off after a time seeing persons who are in sorrow, they make one melancholy; and so at last old Dantès was left all to himself, and I only saw from time to time strangers go up to him and come down again with some bundle they tried to hide; but I guessed what these bundles were, and that he sold by degrees what he had to pay for his subsistence. At length the poor old fellow reached the end of all he had; he owed three quarters’ rent, and they threatened to turn him out; he begged for another week, which was granted to him. I know this, because the landlord came into my apartment when he left his.\n\n“For the first three days I heard him walking about as usual, but, on the fourth I heard nothing. I then resolved to go up to him at all risks. The door was closed, but I looked through the keyhole, and saw him so pale and haggard, that believing him very ill, I went and told M. Morrel and then ran on to Mercédès. They both came immediately, M. Morrel bringing a doctor, and the doctor said it was inflammation of the bowels, and ordered him a limited diet. I was there, too, and I never shall forget the old man’s smile at this prescription.\n\n“From that time he received all who came; he had an excuse for not eating any more; the doctor had put him on a diet.”\n\nThe abbé uttered a kind of groan.\n\n“The story interests you, does it not, sir?” inquired Caderousse.\n\n“Yes,” replied the abbé, “it is very affecting.”\n\n“Mercédès came again, and she found him so altered that she was even more anxious than before to have him taken to her own home. This was M. Morrel’s wish also, who would fain have conveyed the old man against his consent; but the old man resisted, and cried so that they were actually frightened. Mercédès remained, therefore, by his bedside, and M. Morrel went away, making a sign to the Catalan that he had left his purse on the chimney-piece; but, availing himself of the doctor’s order, the old man would not take any sustenance; at length (after nine days of despair and fasting), the old man died, cursing those who had caused his misery, and saying to Mercédès, ‘If you ever see my Edmond again, tell him I die blessing him.’”\n\nThe abbé rose from his chair, made two turns round the chamber, and pressed his trembling hand against his parched throat.\n\n“And you believe he died——”\n\n“Of hunger, sir, of hunger,” said Caderousse. “I am as certain of it as that we two are Christians.”\n\nThe abbé, with a shaking hand, seized a glass of water that was standing by him half-full, swallowed it at one gulp, and then resumed his seat, with red eyes and pale cheeks.\n\n“This was, indeed, a horrid event,” said he in a hoarse voice.\n\n“The more so, sir, as it was men’s and not God’s doing.”\n\n“Tell me of those men,” said the abbé, “and remember too,” he added in an almost menacing tone, “you have promised to tell me everything. Tell me, therefore, who are these men who killed the son with despair, and the father with famine?”\n\n“Two men jealous of him, sir; one from love, and the other from ambition,—Fernand and Danglars.”\n\n“How was this jealousy manifested? Speak on.”\n\n“They denounced Edmond as a Bonapartist agent.”\n\n“Which of the two denounced him? Which was the real delinquent?”\n\n“Both, sir; one with a letter, and the other put it in the post.”\n\n“And where was this letter written?”\n\n“At La Réserve, the day before the betrothal feast.”\n\n“’Twas so, then—’twas so, then,” murmured the abbé. “Oh, Faria, Faria, how well did you judge men and things!”\n\n“What did you please to say, sir?” asked Caderousse.\n\n“Nothing, nothing,” replied the priest; “go on.”\n\n“It was Danglars who wrote the denunciation with his left hand, that his writing might not be recognized, and Fernand who put it in the post.”\n\n“But,” exclaimed the abbé suddenly, “you were there yourself.”\n\n“I!” said Caderousse, astonished; “who told you I was there?”\n\nThe abbé saw he had overshot the mark, and he added quickly,—“No one; but in order to have known everything so well, you must have been an eye-witness.”\n\n“True, true!” said Caderousse in a choking voice, “I was there.”\n\n“And did you not remonstrate against such infamy?” asked the abbé; “if not, you were an accomplice.”\n\n“Sir,” replied Caderousse, “they had made me drink to such an excess that I nearly lost all perception. I had only an indistinct understanding of what was passing around me. I said all that a man in such a state could say; but they both assured me that it was a jest they were carrying on, and perfectly harmless.”\n\n“Next day—next day, sir, you must have seen plain enough what they had been doing, yet you said nothing, though you were present when Dantès was arrested.”\n\n“Yes, sir, I was there, and very anxious to speak; but Danglars restrained me. ‘If he should really be guilty,’ said he, ‘and did really put in to the Island of Elba; if he is really charged with a letter for the Bonapartist committee at Paris, and if they find this letter upon him, those who have supported him will pass for his accomplices.’ I confess I had my fears, in the state in which politics then were, and I held my tongue. It was cowardly, I confess, but it was not criminal.”\n0341m\n\n“I understand—you allowed matters to take their course, that was all.”\n\n“Yes, sir,” answered Caderousse; “and remorse preys on me night and day. I often ask pardon of God, I swear to you, because this action, the only one with which I have seriously to reproach myself in all my life, is no doubt the cause of my abject condition. I am expiating a moment of selfishness, and so I always say to La Carconte, when she complains, ‘Hold your tongue, woman; it is the will of God.’” And Caderousse bowed his head with every sign of real repentance.\n\n“Well, sir,” said the abbé, “you have spoken unreservedly; and thus to accuse yourself is to deserve pardon.”\n\n“Unfortunately, Edmond is dead, and has not pardoned me.”\n\n“He did not know,” said the abbé.\n\n“But he knows it all now,” interrupted Caderousse; “they say the dead know everything.”\n\nThere was a brief silence; the abbé rose and paced up and down pensively, and then resumed his seat.\n\n“You have two or three times mentioned a M. Morrel,” he said; “who was he?”\n\n“The owner of the _Pharaon_ and patron of Dantès.”\n\n“And what part did he play in this sad drama?” inquired the abbé.\n\n“The part of an honest man, full of courage and real regard. Twenty times he interceded for Edmond. When the emperor returned, he wrote, implored, threatened, and so energetically, that on the second restoration he was persecuted as a Bonapartist. Ten times, as I told you, he came to see Dantès’ father, and offered to receive him in his own house; and the night or two before his death, as I have already said, he left his purse on the mantelpiece, with which they paid the old man’s debts, and buried him decently; and so Edmond’s father died, as he had lived, without doing harm to anyone. I have the purse still by me—a large one, made of red silk.”\n\n“And,” asked the abbé, “is M. Morrel still alive?”\n\n“Yes,” replied Caderousse.\n\n“In that case,” replied the abbé, “he should be a man blessed of God, rich, happy.”\n\nCaderousse smiled bitterly. “Yes, happy as myself,” said he.\n\n“What! M. Morrel unhappy?” exclaimed the abbé.\n\n“He is reduced almost to the last extremity—nay, he is almost at the point of dishonor.”\n\n“How?”\n“Yes,” continued Caderousse, “so it is; after five-and-twenty years of labor, after having acquired a most honorable name in the trade of Marseilles, M. Morrel is utterly ruined; he has lost five ships in two years, has suffered by the bankruptcy of three large houses, and his only hope now is in that very _Pharaon_ which poor Dantès commanded, and which is expected from the Indies with a cargo of cochineal and indigo. If this ship founders, like the others, he is a ruined man.”\n\n“And has the unfortunate man wife or children?” inquired the abbé.\n\n“Yes, he has a wife, who through everything has behaved like an angel; he has a daughter, who was about to marry the man she loved, but whose family now will not allow him to wed the daughter of a ruined man; he has, besides, a son, a lieutenant in the army; and, as you may suppose, all this, instead of lessening, only augments his sorrows. If he were alone in the world he would blow out his brains, and there would be an end.”\n\n“Horrible!” ejaculated the priest.\n\n“And it is thus heaven recompenses virtue, sir,” added Caderousse. “You see, I, who never did a bad action but that I have told you of—am in destitution, with my poor wife dying of fever before my very eyes, and I unable to do anything in the world for her; I shall die of hunger, as old Dantès did, while Fernand and Danglars are rolling in wealth.”\n\n“How is that?”\n\n“Because their deeds have brought them good fortune, while honest men have been reduced to misery.”\n\n“What has become of Danglars, the instigator, and therefore the most guilty?”\n\n“What has become of him? Why, he left Marseilles, and was taken, on the recommendation of M. Morrel, who did not know his crime, as cashier into a Spanish bank. During the war with Spain he was employed in the commissariat of the French army, and made a fortune; then with that money he speculated in the funds, and trebled or quadrupled his capital; and, having first married his banker’s daughter, who left him a widower, he has married a second time, a widow, a Madame de Nargonne, daughter of M. de Servieux, the king’s chamberlain, who is in high favor at court. He is a millionaire, and they have made him a baron, and now he is the Baron Danglars, with a fine residence in the Rue du Mont-Blanc, with ten horses in his stables, six footmen in his antechamber, and I know not how many millions in his strongbox.”\n\n“Ah!” said the abbé, in a peculiar tone, “he is happy.”\n\n“Happy? Who can answer for that? Happiness or unhappiness is the secret known but to one’s self and the walls—walls have ears but no tongue; but if a large fortune produces happiness, Danglars is happy.”\n\n“And Fernand?”\n\n“Fernand? Why, much the same story.”\n\n“But how could a poor Catalan fisher-boy, without education or resources, make a fortune? I confess this staggers me.”\n\n“And it has staggered everybody. There must have been in his life some strange secret that no one knows.”\n\n“But, then, by what visible steps has he attained this high fortune or high position?”\n\n“Both, sir—he has both fortune and position—both.”\n\n“This must be impossible!”\n\n“It would seem so; but listen, and you will understand. Some days before the return of the emperor, Fernand was drafted. The Bourbons left him quietly enough at the Catalans, but Napoleon returned, a special levy was made, and Fernand was compelled to join. I went too; but as I was older than Fernand, and had just married my poor wife, I was only sent to the coast. Fernand was enrolled in the active army, went to the frontier with his regiment, and was at the battle of Ligny. The night after that battle he was sentry at the door of a general who carried on a secret correspondence with the enemy. That same night the general was to go over to the English. He proposed to Fernand to accompany him; Fernand agreed to do so, deserted his post, and followed the general.\n\n“Fernand would have been court-martialed if Napoleon had remained on the throne, but his action was rewarded by the Bourbons. He returned to France with the epaulet of sub-lieutenant, and as the protection of the general, who is in the highest favor, was accorded to him, he was a captain in 1823, during the Spanish war—that is to say, at the time when Danglars made his early speculations. Fernand was a Spaniard, and being sent to Spain to ascertain the feeling of his fellow-countrymen, found Danglars there, got on very intimate terms with him, won over the support of the royalists at the capital and in the provinces, received promises and made pledges on his own part, guided his regiment by paths known to himself alone through the mountain gorges which were held by the royalists, and, in fact, rendered such services in this brief campaign that, after the taking of Trocadero, he was made colonel, and received the title of count and the cross of an officer of the Legion of Honor.”\n\n“Destiny! destiny!” murmured the abbé.\n\n“Yes, but listen: this was not all. The war with Spain being ended, Fernand’s career was checked by the long peace which seemed likely to endure throughout Europe. Greece only had risen against Turkey, and had begun her war of independence; all eyes were turned towards Athens—it was the fashion to pity and support the Greeks. The French government, without protecting them openly, as you know, gave countenance to volunteer assistance. Fernand sought and obtained leave to go and serve in Greece, still having his name kept on the army roll.\n0345m\n\nSome time after, it was stated that the Comte de Morcerf (this was the name he bore) had entered the service of Ali Pasha with the rank of instructor-general. Ali Pasha was killed, as you know, but before he died he recompensed the services of Fernand by leaving him a considerable sum, with which he returned to France, when he was gazetted lieutenant-general.”\n\n“So that now——?” inquired the abbé.\n\n“So that now,” continued Caderousse, “he owns a magnificent house—No. 27, Rue du Helder, Paris.”\n\nThe abbé opened his mouth, hesitated for a moment, then, making an effort at self-control, he said, “And Mercédès—they tell me that she has disappeared?”\n\n“Disappeared,” said Caderousse, “yes, as the sun disappears, to rise the next day with still more splendor.”\n\n“Has she made a fortune also?” inquired the abbé, with an ironical smile.\n\n“Mercédès is at this moment one of the greatest ladies in Paris,” replied Caderousse.\n\n“Go on,” said the abbé; “it seems as if I were listening to the story of a dream. But I have seen things so extraordinary, that what you tell me seems less astonishing than it otherwise might.”\n\n“Mercédès was at first in the deepest despair at the blow which deprived her of Edmond. I have told you of her attempts to propitiate M. de Villefort, her devotion to the elder Dantès. In the midst of her despair, a new affliction overtook her. This was the departure of Fernand—of Fernand, whose crime she did not know, and whom she regarded as her brother. Fernand went, and Mercédès remained alone.\n\n“Three months passed and still she wept—no news of Edmond, no news of Fernand, no companionship save that of an old man who was dying with despair. One evening, after a day of accustomed vigil at the angle of two roads leading to Marseilles from the Catalans, she returned to her home more depressed than ever. Suddenly she heard a step she knew, turned anxiously around, the door opened, and Fernand, dressed in the uniform of a sub-lieutenant, stood before her.\n\n“It was not the one she wished for most, but it seemed as if a part of her past life had returned to her.\n\n“Mercédès seized Fernand’s hands with a transport which he took for love, but which was only joy at being no longer alone in the world, and seeing at last a friend, after long hours of solitary sorrow. And then, it must be confessed, Fernand had never been hated—he was only not precisely loved. Another possessed all Mercédès’ heart; that other was absent, had disappeared, perhaps was dead. At this last thought Mercédès burst into a flood of tears, and wrung her hands in agony; but the thought, which she had always repelled before when it was suggested to her by another, came now in full force upon her mind; and then, too, old Dantès incessantly said to her, ‘Our Edmond is dead; if he were not, he would return to us.’\n\n“The old man died, as I have told you; had he lived, Mercédès, perchance, had not become the wife of another, for he would have been there to reproach her infidelity. Fernand saw this, and when he learned of the old man’s death he returned. He was now a lieutenant. At his first coming he had not said a word of love to Mercédès; at the second he reminded her that he loved her.\n\n“Mercédès begged for six months more in which to await and mourn for Edmond.”\n\n“So that,” said the abbé, with a bitter smile, “that makes eighteen months in all. What more could the most devoted lover desire?” Then he murmured the words of the English poet, “‘Frailty, thy name is woman.’”\n\n“Six months afterwards,” continued Caderousse, “the marriage took place in the church of Accoules.”\n\n“The very church in which she was to have married Edmond,” murmured the priest; “there was only a change of bridegrooms.”\n\n“Well, Mercédès was married,” proceeded Caderousse; “but although in the eyes of the world she appeared calm, she nearly fainted as she passed La Réserve, where, eighteen months before, the betrothal had been celebrated with him whom she might have known she still loved, had she looked to the bottom of her heart. Fernand, more happy, but not more at his ease—for I saw at this time he was in constant dread of Edmond’s return—Fernand was very anxious to get his wife away, and to depart himself. There were too many unpleasant possibilities associated with the Catalans, and eight days after the wedding they left Marseilles.”\n\n“Did you ever see Mercédès again?” inquired the priest.\n\n“Yes, during the Spanish war, at Perpignan, where Fernand had left her; she was attending to the education of her son.”\n\nThe abbé started. “Her son?” said he.\n\n“Yes,” replied Caderousse, “little Albert.”\n\n“But, then, to be able to instruct her child,” continued the abbé, “she must have received an education herself. I understood from Edmond that she was the daughter of a simple fisherman, beautiful but uneducated.”\n\n“Oh,” replied Caderousse, “did he know so little of his lovely betrothed? Mercédès might have been a queen, sir, if the crown were to be placed on the heads of the loveliest and most intelligent. Fernand’s fortune was already waxing great, and she developed with his growing fortune. She learned drawing, music—everything. Besides, I believe, between ourselves, she did this in order to distract her mind, that she might forget; and she only filled her head in order to alleviate the weight on her heart. But now her position in life is assured,” continued Caderousse; “no doubt fortune and honors have comforted her; she is rich, a countess, and yet——”\n\nCaderousse paused.\n\n“And yet what?” asked the abbé.\n\n“Yet, I am sure, she is not happy,” said Caderousse.\n\n“What makes you believe this?”\n\n“Why, when I found myself utterly destitute, I thought my old friends would, perhaps, assist me. So I went to Danglars, who would not even receive me. I called on Fernand, who sent me a hundred francs by his valet-de-chambre.”\n\n“Then you did not see either of them?”\n\n“No, but Madame de Morcerf saw me.”\n\n“How was that?”\n\n“As I went away a purse fell at my feet—it contained five-and-twenty louis; I raised my head quickly, and saw Mercédès, who at once shut the blind.”\n\n“And M. de Villefort?” asked the abbé.\n\n“Oh, he never was a friend of mine, I did not know him, and I had nothing to ask of him.”\n\n“Do you not know what became of him, and the share he had in Edmond’s misfortunes?”\n\n“No; I only know that some time after Edmond’s arrest, he married Mademoiselle de Saint-Méran, and soon after left Marseilles; no doubt he has been as lucky as the rest; no doubt he is as rich as Danglars, as high in station as Fernand. I only, as you see, have remained poor, wretched, and forgotten.”\n\n“You are mistaken, my friend,” replied the abbé; “God may seem sometimes to forget for a time, while his justice reposes, but there always comes a moment when he remembers—and behold—a proof!”\n\nAs he spoke, the abbé took the diamond from his pocket, and giving it to Caderousse, said, “Here, my friend, take this diamond, it is yours.”\n\n“What, for me only?” cried Caderousse, “ah, sir, do not jest with me!”\n\n“This diamond was to have been shared among his friends. Edmond had one friend only, and thus it cannot be divided. Take the diamond, then, and sell it; it is worth fifty thousand francs, and I repeat my wish that this sum may suffice to release you from your wretchedness.”\n0351m\n\n“Oh, sir,” said Caderousse, putting out one hand timidly, and with the other wiping away the perspiration which bedewed his brow,—“Oh, sir, do not make a jest of the happiness or despair of a man.”\n\n“I know what happiness and what despair are, and I never make a jest of such feelings. Take it, then, but in exchange——”\n\nCaderousse, who touched the diamond, withdrew his hand.\n\nThe abbé smiled.\n\n“In exchange,” he continued, “give me the red silk purse that M. Morrel left on old Dantès’ chimney-piece, and which you tell me is still in your hands.”\n\nCaderousse, more and more astonished, went toward a large oaken cupboard, opened it, and gave the abbé a long purse of faded red silk, round which were two copper runners that had once been gilt. The abbé took it, and in return gave Caderousse the diamond.\n\n“Oh, you are a man of God, sir,” cried Caderousse; “for no one knew that Edmond had given you this diamond, and you might have kept it.”\n\n“Which,” said the abbé to himself, “you would have done.” The abbé rose, took his hat and gloves. “Well,” he said, “all you have told me is perfectly true, then, and I may believe it in every particular.”\n\n“See, sir,” replied Caderousse, “in this corner is a crucifix in holy wood—here on this shelf is my wife’s testament; open this book, and I will swear upon it with my hand on the crucifix. I will swear to you by my soul’s salvation, my faith as a Christian, I have told everything to you as it occurred, and as the recording angel will tell it to the ear of God at the day of the last judgment!”\n\n“’Tis well,” said the abbé, convinced by his manner and tone that Caderousse spoke the truth. “’Tis well, and may this money profit you! Adieu; I go far from men who thus so bitterly injure each other.”\n\nThe abbé with difficulty got away from the enthusiastic thanks of Caderousse, opened the door himself, got out and mounted his horse, once more saluted the innkeeper, who kept uttering his loud farewells, and then returned by the road he had travelled in coming.\n\nWhen Caderousse turned around, he saw behind him La Carconte, paler and trembling more than ever.\n\n“Is, then, all that I have heard really true?” she inquired.\n\n“What? That he has given the diamond to us only?” inquired Caderousse, half bewildered with joy; “yes, nothing more true! See, here it is.”\n\nThe woman gazed at it a moment, and then said, in a gloomy voice, “Suppose it’s false?”\n\nCaderousse started and turned pale.\n\n“False!” he muttered. “False! Why should that man give me a false diamond?”\n0349m\n\n“To get your secret without paying for it, you blockhead!”\n\nCaderousse remained for a moment aghast under the weight of such an idea.\n\n“Oh!” he said, taking up his hat, which he placed on the red handkerchief tied round his head, “we will soon find out.”\n\n“In what way?”\n\n“Why, the fair is on at Beaucaire, there are always jewellers from Paris there, and I will show it to them. Look after the house, wife, and I shall be back in two hours,” and Caderousse left the house in haste, and ran rapidly in the direction opposite to that which the priest had taken.\n\n“Fifty thousand francs!” muttered La Carconte when left alone; “it is a large sum of money, but it is not a fortune.”\n20009m\n\n# Chapter 28. The Prison Register\n\n20019m\nThe day after that in which the scene we have just described had taken place on the road between Bellegarde and Beaucaire, a man of about thirty or two-and-thirty, dressed in a bright blue frock coat, nankeen trousers, and a white waistcoat, having the appearance and accent of an Englishman, presented himself before the mayor of Marseilles.\n\n“Sir,” said he, “I am chief clerk of the house of Thomson & French, of Rome. We are, and have been these ten years, connected with the house of Morrel & Son, of Marseilles. We have a hundred thousand francs or thereabouts loaned on their securities, and we are a little uneasy at reports that have reached us that the firm is on the brink of ruin. I have come, therefore, express from Rome, to ask you for information.”\n\n“Sir,” replied the mayor. “I know very well that during the last four or five years misfortune has seemed to pursue M. Morrel. He has lost four or five vessels, and suffered by three or four bankruptcies; but it is not for me, although I am a creditor myself to the amount of ten thousand francs, to give any information as to the state of his finances. Ask of me, as mayor, what is my opinion of M. Morrel, and I shall say that he is a man honorable to the last degree, and who has up to this time fulfilled every engagement with scrupulous punctuality. This is all I can say, sir; if you wish to learn more, address yourself to M. de Boville, the inspector of prisons, No. 15, Rue de Nouailles; he has, I believe, two hundred thousand francs in Morrel’s hands, and if there be any grounds for apprehension, as this is a greater amount than mine, you will most probably find him better informed than myself.”\n\nThe Englishman seemed to appreciate this extreme delicacy, made his bow and went away, proceeding with a characteristic British stride towards the street mentioned.\n\nM. de Boville was in his private room, and the Englishman, on perceiving him, made a gesture of surprise, which seemed to indicate that it was not the first time he had been in his presence. As to M. de Boville, he was in such a state of despair, that it was evident all the faculties of his mind, absorbed in the thought which occupied him at the moment, did not allow either his memory or his imagination to stray to the past.\n\nThe Englishman, with the coolness of his nation, addressed him in terms nearly similar to those with which he had accosted the mayor of Marseilles.\n\n“Oh, sir,” exclaimed M. de Boville, “your fears are unfortunately but too well founded, and you see before you a man in despair. I had two hundred thousand francs placed in the hands of Morrel & Son; these two hundred thousand francs were the dowry of my daughter, who was to be married in a fortnight, and these two hundred thousand francs were payable, half on the 15th of this month, and the other half on the 15th of next month. I had informed M. Morrel of my desire to have these payments punctually, and he has been here within the last half-hour to tell me that if his ship, the _Pharaon_, did not come into port on the 15th, he would be wholly unable to make this payment.”\n\n“But,” said the Englishman, “this looks very much like a suspension of payment.”\n\n“It looks more like bankruptcy!” exclaimed M. de Boville despairingly.\n\nThe Englishman appeared to reflect a moment, and then said, “From which it would appear, sir, that this credit inspires you with considerable apprehension?”\n\n“To tell you the truth, I consider it lost.”\n\n“Well, then, I will buy it of you!”\n\n“You?”\n“Yes, I!”\n\n“But at a tremendous discount, of course?”\n\n“No, for two hundred thousand francs. Our house,” added the Englishman with a laugh, “does not do things in that way.”\n\n“And you will pay——”\n\n“Ready money.”\n\n20023m\nAnd the Englishman drew from his pocket a bundle of bank-notes, which might have been twice the sum M. de Boville feared to lose. A ray of joy passed across M. de Boville’s countenance, yet he made an effort at self-control, and said:\n\n“Sir, I ought to tell you that, in all probability, you will not realize six per cent of this sum.”\n\n“That’s no affair of mine,” replied the Englishman, “that is the affair of the house of Thomson & French, in whose name I act. They have, perhaps, some motive to serve in hastening the ruin of a rival firm. But all I know, sir, is, that I am ready to hand you over this sum in exchange for your assignment of the debt. I only ask a brokerage.”\n\n“Of course, that is perfectly just,” cried M. de Boville. “The commission is usually one and a half; will you have two—three—five per cent, or even more? Whatever you say.”\n\n“Sir,” replied the Englishman, laughing, “I am like my house, and do not do such things—no, the commission I ask is quite different.”\n\n“Name it, sir, I beg.”\n\n“You are the inspector of prisons?”\n\n“I have been so these fourteen years.”\n\n“You keep the registers of entries and departures?”\n\n“I do.”\n\n“To these registers there are added notes relative to the prisoners?”\n\n“There are special reports on every prisoner.”\n\n“Well, sir, I was educated at Rome by a poor devil of an abbé, who disappeared suddenly. I have since learned that he was confined in the Château d’If, and I should like to learn some particulars of his death.”\n\n“What was his name?”\n\n“The Abbé Faria.”\n\n“Oh, I recollect him perfectly,” cried M. de Boville; “he was crazy.”\n\n“So they said.”\n\n“Oh, he was, decidedly.”\n\n“Very possibly; but what sort of madness was it?”\n\n“He pretended to know of an immense treasure, and offered vast sums to the government if they would liberate him.”\n\n“Poor devil!—and he is dead?”\n\n“Yes, sir, five or six months ago, last February.”\n\n“You have a good memory, sir, to recollect dates so well.”\n\n“I recollect this, because the poor devil’s death was accompanied by a singular incident.”\n\n“May I ask what that was?” said the Englishman with an expression of curiosity, which a close observer would have been astonished at discovering in his phlegmatic countenance.\n\n“Oh dear, yes, sir; the abbé’s dungeon was forty or fifty feet distant from that of one of Bonaparte’s emissaries,—one of those who had contributed the most to the return of the usurper in 1815, a very resolute and very dangerous man.”\n\n“Indeed!” said the Englishman.\n\n“Yes,” replied M. de Boville; “I myself had occasion to see this man in 1816 or 1817, and we could only go into his dungeon with a file of soldiers. That man made a deep impression on me; I shall never forget his countenance!”\n\n20025m\nThe Englishman smiled imperceptibly.\n\n“And you say, sir,” he interposed, “that the two dungeons——”\n\n“Were separated by a distance of fifty feet; but it appears that this Edmond Dantès——”\n\n“This dangerous man’s name was——”\n\n“Edmond Dantès. It appears, sir, that this Edmond Dantès had procured tools, or made them, for they found a tunnel through which the prisoners held communication with one another.”\n\n“This tunnel was dug, no doubt, with an intention of escape?”\n\n“No doubt; but unfortunately for the prisoners, the Abbé Faria had an attack of catalepsy, and died.”\n\n“That must have cut short the projects of escape.”\n\n“For the dead man, yes,” replied M. de Boville, “but not for the survivor; on the contrary, this Dantès saw a means of accelerating his escape. He, no doubt, thought that prisoners who died in the Château d’If were interred in an ordinary burial-ground, and he conveyed the dead man into his own cell, took his place in the sack in which they had sewed up the corpse, and awaited the moment of interment.”\n\n“It was a bold step, and one that showed some courage,” remarked the Englishman.\n\n“As I have already told you, sir, he was a very dangerous man; and, fortunately, by his own act disembarrassed the government of the fears it had on his account.”\n\n“How was that?”\n\n“How? Do you not comprehend?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“The Château d’If has no cemetery, and they simply throw the dead into the sea, after fastening a thirty-six-pound cannon-ball to their feet.”\n\n“Well?” observed the Englishman as if he were slow of comprehension.\n\n“Well, they fastened a thirty-six-pound ball to his feet, and threw him into the sea.”\n\n“Really!” exclaimed the Englishman.\n\n“Yes, sir,” continued the inspector of prisons. “You may imagine the amazement of the fugitive when he found himself flung headlong over the rocks! I should like to have seen his face at that moment.”\n\n“That would have been difficult.”\n\n“No matter,” replied De Boville, in supreme good-humor at the certainty of recovering his two hundred thousand francs,—“no matter, I can fancy it.” And he shouted with laughter.\n\n“So can I,” said the Englishman, and he laughed too; but he laughed as the English do, “at the end of his teeth.”\n\n“And so,” continued the Englishman who first gained his composure, “he was drowned?”\n\n“Unquestionably.”\n\n“So that the governor got rid of the dangerous and the crazy prisoner at the same time?”\n\n“Precisely.”\n20027m\n\n“But some official document was drawn up as to this affair, I suppose?” inquired the Englishman.\n\n“Yes, yes, the mortuary deposition. You understand, Dantès’ relations, if he had any, might have some interest in knowing if he were dead or alive.”\n\n“So that now, if there were anything to inherit from him, they may do so with easy conscience. He is dead, and no mistake about it.”\n\n“Oh, yes; and they may have the fact attested whenever they please.”\n\n“So be it,” said the Englishman. “But to return to these registers.”\n\n“True, this story has diverted our attention from them. Excuse me.”\n\n“Excuse you for what? For the story? By no means; it really seems to me very curious.”\n\n“Yes, indeed. So, sir, you wish to see all relating to the poor abbé, who really was gentleness itself.”\n\n“Yes, you will much oblige me.”\n\n“Go into my study here, and I will show it to you.”\n\nAnd they both entered M. de Boville’s study. Everything was here arranged in perfect order; each register had its number, each file of papers its place. The inspector begged the Englishman to seat himself in an armchair, and placed before him the register and documents relative to the Château d’If, giving him all the time he desired for the examination, while De Boville seated himself in a corner, and began to read his newspaper. The Englishman easily found the entries relative to the Abbé Faria; but it seemed that the history which the inspector had related interested him greatly, for after having perused the first documents he turned over the leaves until he reached the deposition respecting Edmond Dantès. There he found everything arranged in due order,—the accusation, examination, Morrel’s petition, M. de Villefort’s marginal notes. He folded up the accusation quietly, and put it as quietly in his pocket; read the examination, and saw that the name of Noirtier was not mentioned in it; perused, too, the application dated 10th April, 1815, in which Morrel, by the deputy procureur’s advice, exaggerated with the best intentions (for Napoleon was then on the throne) the services Dantès had rendered to the imperial cause—services which Villefort’s certificates rendered indisputable. Then he saw through the whole thing. This petition to Napoleon, kept back by Villefort, had become, under the second restoration, a terrible weapon against him in the hands of the king’s attorney. He was no longer astonished when he searched on to find in the register this note, placed in a bracket against his name:\n\nEdmond Dantès.\n\nAn inveterate Bonapartist; took an active part in the return from the Island of Elba.\n\nTo be kept in strict solitary confinement, and to be closely watched and guarded.\n\nBeneath these lines was written in another hand: “See note above—nothing can be done.”\n\nHe compared the writing in the bracket with the writing of the certificate placed beneath Morrel’s petition, and discovered that the note in the bracket was the same writing as the certificate—that is to say, was in Villefort’s handwriting.\n\n20029m\nAs to the note which accompanied this, the Englishman understood that it might have been added by some inspector who had taken a momentary interest in Dantès’ situation, but who had, from the remarks we have quoted, found it impossible to give any effect to the interest he had felt.\n\nAs we have said, the inspector, from discretion, and that he might not disturb the Abbé Faria’s pupil in his researches, had seated himself in a corner, and was reading _Le Drapeau Blanc_. He did not see the Englishman fold up and place in his pocket the accusation written by Danglars under the arbor of La Réserve, and which had the postmark, “Marseilles, 27th February, delivery 6 o’clock, P.M.”\n\nBut it must be said that if he had seen it, he attached so little importance to this scrap of paper, and so much importance to his two hundred thousand francs, that he would not have opposed whatever the Englishman might do, however irregular it might be.\n\n“Thanks,” said the latter, closing the register with a slam, “I have all I want; now it is for me to perform my promise. Give me a simple assignment of your debt; acknowledge therein the receipt of the cash, and I will hand you over the money.”\n\nHe rose, gave his seat to M. de Boville, who took it without ceremony, and quickly drew up the required assignment, while the Englishman counted out the bank-notes on the other side of the desk.\n\n# Chapter 29. The House of Morrel & Son\n\nAnyone who had quitted Marseilles a few years previously, well acquainted with the interior of Morrel’s warehouse, and had returned at this date, would have found a great change. Instead of that air of life, of comfort, and of happiness that permeates a flourishing and prosperous business establishment—instead of merry faces at the windows, busy clerks hurrying to and fro in the long corridors—instead of the court filled with bales of goods, re-echoing with the cries and the jokes of porters, one would have immediately perceived all aspect of sadness and gloom. Out of all the numerous clerks that used to fill the deserted corridor and the empty office, but two remained. One was a young man of three or four-and-twenty, who was in love with M. Morrel’s daughter, and had remained with him in spite of the efforts of his friends to induce him to withdraw; the other was an old one-eyed cashier, called “Cocles,” or “Cock-eye,” a nickname given him by the young men who used to throng this vast now almost deserted bee-hive, and which had so completely replaced his real name that he would not, in all probability, have replied to anyone who addressed him by it.\n\nCocles remained in M. Morrel’s service, and a most singular change had taken place in his position; he had at the same time risen to the rank of cashier, and sunk to the rank of a servant. He was, however, the same Cocles, good, patient, devoted, but inflexible on the subject of arithmetic, the only point on which he would have stood firm against the world, even against M. Morrel; and strong in the multiplication-table, which he had at his fingers’ ends, no matter what scheme or what trap was laid to catch him.\n\nIn the midst of the disasters that befell the house, Cocles was the only one unmoved. But this did not arise from a want of affection; on the contrary, from a firm conviction. Like the rats that one by one forsake the doomed ship even before the vessel weighs anchor, so all the numerous clerks had by degrees deserted the office and the warehouse. Cocles had seen them go without thinking of inquiring the cause of their departure. Everything was as we have said, a question of arithmetic to Cocles, and during twenty years he had always seen all payments made with such exactitude, that it seemed as impossible to him that the house should stop payment, as it would to a miller that the river that had so long turned his mill should cease to flow.\n\nNothing had as yet occurred to shake Cocles’ belief; the last month’s payment had been made with the most scrupulous exactitude; Cocles had detected an overbalance of fourteen sous in his cash, and the same evening he had brought them to M. Morrel, who, with a melancholy smile, threw them into an almost empty drawer, saying:\n\n“Thanks, Cocles; you are the pearl of cashiers.”\n\nCocles went away perfectly happy, for this eulogium of M. Morrel, himself the pearl of the honest men of Marseilles, flattered him more than a present of fifty crowns. But since the end of the month M. Morrel had passed many an anxious hour.\n\nIn order to meet the payments then due; he had collected all his resources, and, fearing lest the report of his distress should get bruited abroad at Marseilles when he was known to be reduced to such an extremity, he went to the Beaucaire fair to sell his wife’s and daughter’s jewels and a portion of his plate. By this means the end of the month was passed, but his resources were now exhausted. Credit, owing to the reports afloat, was no longer to be had; and to meet the one hundred thousand francs due on the 15th of the present month, and the one hundred thousand francs due on the 15th of the next month to M. de Boville, M. Morrel had, in reality, no hope but the return of the _Pharaon_, of whose departure he had learnt from a vessel which had weighed anchor at the same time, and which had already arrived in harbor.\n\nBut this vessel which, like the _Pharaon_, came from Calcutta, had been in for a fortnight, while no intelligence had been received of the _Pharaon_.\n\n20033m\nSuch was the state of affairs when, the day after his interview with M. de Boville, the confidential clerk of the house of Thomson & French of Rome, presented himself at M. Morrel’s.\n\nEmmanuel received him; this young man was alarmed by the appearance of every new face, for every new face might be that of a new creditor, come in anxiety to question the head of the house. The young man, wishing to spare his employer the pain of this interview, questioned the new-comer; but the stranger declared that he had nothing to say to M. Emmanuel, and that his business was with M. Morrel in person.\n\nEmmanuel sighed, and summoned Cocles. Cocles appeared, and the young man bade him conduct the stranger to M. Morrel’s apartment. Cocles went first, and the stranger followed him. On the staircase they met a beautiful girl of sixteen or seventeen, who looked with anxiety at the stranger.\n\n“M. Morrel is in his room, is he not, Mademoiselle Julie?” said the cashier.\n\n“Yes; I think so, at least,” said the young girl hesitatingly. “Go and see, Cocles, and if my father is there, announce this gentleman.”\n\n“It will be useless to announce me, mademoiselle,” returned the Englishman. “M. Morrel does not know my name; this worthy gentleman has only to announce the confidential clerk of the house of Thomson & French of Rome, with whom your father does business.”\n\nThe young girl turned pale and continued to descend, while the stranger and Cocles continued to mount the staircase. She entered the office where Emmanuel was, while Cocles, by the aid of a key he possessed, opened a door in the corner of a landing-place on the second staircase, conducted the stranger into an antechamber, opened a second door, which he closed behind him, and after having left the clerk of the house of Thomson & French alone, returned and signed to him that he could enter.\n\nThe Englishman entered, and found Morrel seated at a table, turning over the formidable columns of his ledger, which contained the list of his liabilities. At the sight of the stranger, M. Morrel closed the ledger, arose, and offered a seat to the stranger; and when he had seen him seated, resumed his own chair. Fourteen years had changed the worthy merchant, who, in his thirty-sixth year at the opening of this history, was now in his fiftieth; his hair had turned white, time and sorrow had ploughed deep furrows on his brow, and his look, once so firm and penetrating, was now irresolute and wandering, as if he feared being forced to fix his attention on some particular thought or person.\n\nThe Englishman looked at him with an air of curiosity, evidently mingled with interest. “Monsieur,” said Morrel, whose uneasiness was increased by this examination, “you wish to speak to me?”\n\n“Yes, monsieur; you are aware from whom I come?”\n\n“The house of Thomson & French; at least, so my cashier tells me.”\n\n“He has told you rightly. The house of Thomson & French had 300,000 or 400,000 francs to pay this month in France; and, knowing your strict punctuality, have collected all the bills bearing your signature, and charged me as they became due to present them, and to employ the money otherwise.”\n\nMorrel sighed deeply, and passed his hand over his forehead, which was covered with perspiration.\n\n“So then, sir,” said Morrel, “you hold bills of mine?”\n\n“Yes, and for a considerable sum.”\n\n“What is the amount?” asked Morrel with a voice he strove to render firm.\n\n20035m\n“Here is,” said the Englishman, taking a quantity of papers from his pocket, “an assignment of 200,000 francs to our house by M. de Boville, the inspector of prisons, to whom they are due. You acknowledge, of course, that you owe this sum to him?”\n\n“Yes; he placed the money in my hands at four and a half per cent nearly five years ago.”\n\n“When are you to pay?”\n\n“Half the 15th of this month, half the 15th of next.”\n\n“Just so; and now here are 32,500 francs payable shortly; they are all signed by you, and assigned to our house by the holders.”\n\n“I recognize them,” said Morrel, whose face was suffused, as he thought that, for the first time in his life, he would be unable to honor his own signature. “Is this all?”\n\n“No, I have for the end of the month these bills which have been assigned to us by the house of Pascal, and the house of Wild & Turner of Marseilles, amounting to nearly 55,000 francs; in all, 287,500 francs.”\n\nIt is impossible to describe what Morrel suffered during this enumeration. “Two hundred and eighty-seven thousand five hundred francs,” repeated he.\n\n“Yes, sir,” replied the Englishman. “I will not,” continued he, after a moment’s silence, “conceal from you, that while your probity and exactitude up to this moment are universally acknowledged, yet the report is current in Marseilles that you are not able to meet your liabilities.”\n\nAt this almost brutal speech Morrel turned deathly pale.\n\n“Sir,” said he, “up to this time—and it is now more than four-and-twenty years since I received the direction of this house from my father, who had himself conducted it for five-and-thirty years—never has anything bearing the signature of Morrel & Son been dishonored.”\n\n“I know that,” replied the Englishman. “But as a man of honor should answer another, tell me fairly, shall you pay these with the same punctuality?”\n\nMorrel shuddered, and looked at the man, who spoke with more assurance than he had hitherto shown.\n\n“To questions frankly put,” said he, “a straightforward answer should be given. Yes, I shall pay, if, as I hope, my vessel arrives safely; for its arrival will again procure me the credit which the numerous accidents, of which I have been the victim, have deprived me; but if the _Pharaon_ should be lost, and this last resource be gone——”\n\nThe poor man’s eyes filled with tears.\n\n“Well,” said the other, “if this last resource fail you?”\n\n“Well,” returned Morrel, “it is a cruel thing to be forced to say, but, already used to misfortune, I must habituate myself to shame. I fear I shall be forced to suspend payment.”\n\n“Have you no friends who could assist you?”\n\nMorrel smiled mournfully.\n\n“In business, sir,” said he, “one has no friends, only correspondents.”\n\n“It is true,” murmured the Englishman; “then you have but one hope.”\n\n“But one.”\n“The last?”\n\n“The last.”\n\n“So that if this fail——”\n\n“I am ruined,—completely ruined!”\n\n“As I was on my way here, a vessel was coming into port.”\n\n“I know it, sir; a young man, who still adheres to my fallen fortunes, passes a part of his time in a belvedere at the top of the house, in hopes of being the first to announce good news to me; he has informed me of the arrival of this ship.”\n\n“And it is not yours?”\n\n“No, she is a Bordeaux vessel, _La Gironde_; she comes from India also; but she is not mine.”\n\n“Perhaps she has spoken to the _Pharaon_, and brings you some tidings of her?”\n\n“Shall I tell you plainly one thing, sir? I dread almost as much to receive any tidings of my vessel as to remain in doubt. Uncertainty is still hope.” Then in a low voice Morrel added,—“This delay is not natural. The _Pharaon_ left Calcutta the 5th of February; she ought to have been here a month ago.”\n\n“What is that?” said the Englishman. “What is the meaning of that noise?”\n\n“Oh, my God!” cried Morrel, turning pale, “what is it?”\n\nA loud noise was heard on the stairs of people moving hastily, and half-stifled sobs. Morrel rose and advanced to the door; but his strength failed him and he sank into a chair. The two men remained opposite one another, Morrel trembling in every limb, the stranger gazing at him with an air of profound pity. The noise had ceased; but it seemed that Morrel expected something—something had occasioned the noise, and something must follow. The stranger fancied he heard footsteps on the stairs; and that the footsteps, which were those of several persons, stopped at the door. A key was inserted in the lock of the first door, and the creaking of hinges was audible.\n\n“There are only two persons who have the key to that door,” murmured Morrel, “Cocles and Julie.”\n\nAt this instant the second door opened, and the young girl, her eyes bathed with tears, appeared. Morrel rose tremblingly, supporting himself by the arm of the chair. He would have spoken, but his voice failed him.\n\n“Oh, father!” said she, clasping her hands, “forgive your child for being the bearer of evil tidings.”\n\nMorrel again changed color. Julie threw herself into his arms.\n\n“Oh, father, father!” murmured she, “courage!”\n\n“The _Pharaon_ has gone down, then?” said Morrel in a hoarse voice. The young girl did not speak; but she made an affirmative sign with her head as she lay on her father’s breast.\n\n“And the crew?” asked Morrel.\n\n“Saved,” said the girl; “saved by the crew of the vessel that has just entered the harbor.”\n\nMorrel raised his two hands to heaven with an expression of resignation and sublime gratitude.\n\n“Thanks, my God,” said he, “at least thou strikest but me alone.”\n\nA tear moistened the eye of the phlegmatic Englishman.\n\n“Come in, come in,” said Morrel, “for I presume you are all at the door.”\n\nScarcely had he uttered those words when Madame Morrel entered weeping bitterly. Emmanuel followed her, and in the antechamber were visible the rough faces of seven or eight half-naked sailors. At the sight of these men the Englishman started and advanced a step; then restrained himself, and retired into the farthest and most obscure corner of the apartment. Madame Morrel sat down by her husband and took one of his hands in hers, Julie still lay with her head on his shoulder, Emmanuel stood in the centre of the chamber and seemed to form the link between Morrel’s family and the sailors at the door.\n\n“How did this happen?” said Morrel.\n\n“Draw nearer, Penelon,” said the young man, “and tell us all about it.”\n\nAn old seaman, bronzed by the tropical sun, advanced, twirling the remains of a hat between his hands.\n\n“Good-day, M. Morrel,” said he, as if he had just quitted Marseilles the previous evening, and had just returned from Aix or Toulon.\n\n“Good-day, Penelon,” returned Morrel, who could not refrain from smiling through his tears, “where is the captain?”\n\n“The captain, M. Morrel,—he has stayed behind sick at Palma; but please God, it won’t be much, and you will see him in a few days all alive and hearty.”\n\n“Well, now tell your story, Penelon.”\n20039m\n\nPenelon rolled his quid in his cheek, placed his hand before his mouth, turned his head, and sent a long jet of tobacco-juice into the antechamber, advanced his foot, balanced himself, and began.\n\n“You see, M. Morrel,” said he, “we were somewhere between Cape Blanc and Cape Boyador, sailing with a fair breeze, south-south-west after a week’s calm, when Captain Gaumard comes up to me—I was at the helm I should tell you—and says, ‘Penelon, what do you think of those clouds coming up over there?’ I was just then looking at them myself. ‘What do I think, captain? Why I think that they are rising faster than they have any business to do, and that they would not be so black if they didn’t mean mischief.’—‘That’s my opinion too,’ said the captain, ‘and I’ll take precautions accordingly. We are carrying too much canvas. Avast, there, all hands! Take in the studding-sails and stow the flying jib.’ It was time; the squall was on us, and the vessel began to heel. ‘Ah,’ said the captain, ‘we have still too much canvas set; all hands lower the mainsail!’ Five minutes after, it was down; and we sailed under mizzen-topsails and top-gallant sails. ‘Well, Penelon,’ said the captain, ‘what makes you shake your head?’ ‘Why,’ I says, ‘I still think you’ve got too much on.’ ‘I think you’re right,’ answered he, ‘we shall have a gale.’ ‘A gale? More than that, we shall have a tempest, or I don’t know what’s what.’ You could see the wind coming like the dust at Montredon; luckily the captain understood his business. ‘Take in two reefs in the top-sails,’ cried the captain; ‘let go the bowlin’s, haul the brace, lower the top-gallant sails, haul out the reef-tackles on the yards.’”\n20041m\n\n“That was not enough for those latitudes,” said the Englishman; “I should have taken four reefs in the topsails and furled the spanker.”\n\nHis firm, sonorous, and unexpected voice made everyone start. Penelon put his hand over his eyes, and then stared at the man who thus criticized the manœuvres of his captain.\n\n“We did better than that, sir,” said the old sailor respectfully; “we put the helm up to run before the tempest; ten minutes after we struck our top-sails and scudded under bare poles.”\n\n“The vessel was very old to risk that,” said the Englishman.\n\n“Eh, it was that that did the business; after pitching heavily for twelve hours we sprung a leak. ‘Penelon,’ said the captain, ‘I think we are sinking, give me the helm, and go down into the hold.’ I gave him the helm, and descended; there was already three feet of water. ‘All hands to the pumps!’ I shouted; but it was too late, and it seemed the more we pumped the more came in. ‘Ah,’ said I, after four hours’ work, ‘since we are sinking, let us sink; we can die but once.’ ‘Is that the example you set, Penelon?’ cries the captain; ‘very well, wait a minute.’ He went into his cabin and came back with a brace of pistols. ‘I will blow the brains out of the first man who leaves the pump,’ said he.”\n\n“Well done!” said the Englishman.\n\n20043m\n“There’s nothing gives you so much courage as good reasons,” continued the sailor; “and during that time the wind had abated, and the sea gone down, but the water kept rising; not much, only two inches an hour, but still it rose. Two inches an hour does not seem much, but in twelve hours that makes two feet, and three we had before, that makes five. ‘Come,’ said the captain, ‘we have done all in our power, and M. Morrel will have nothing to reproach us with, we have tried to save the ship, let us now save ourselves. To the boats, my lads, as quick as you can.’ Now,” continued Penelon, “you see, M. Morrel, a sailor is attached to his ship, but still more to his life, so we did not wait to be told twice; the more so, that the ship was sinking under us, and seemed to say, ‘Get along—save yourselves.’ We soon launched the boat, and all eight of us got into it. The captain descended last, or rather, he did not descend, he would not quit the vessel; so I took him round the waist, and threw him into the boat, and then I jumped after him. It was time, for just as I jumped the deck burst with a noise like the broadside of a man-of-war. Ten minutes after she pitched forward, then the other way, spun round and round, and then good-bye to the _Pharaon_. As for us, we were three days without anything to eat or drink, so that we began to think of drawing lots who should feed the rest, when we saw _La Gironde_; we made signals of distress, she perceived us, made for us, and took us all on board. There now, M. Morrel, that’s the whole truth, on the honor of a sailor; is not it true, you fellows there?” A general murmur of approbation showed that the narrator had faithfully detailed their misfortunes and sufferings.\n\n“Well, well,” said M. Morrel, “I know there was no one in fault but destiny. It was the will of God that this should happen, blessed be his name. What wages are due to you?”\n\n“Oh, don’t let us talk of that, M. Morrel.”\n\n“Yes, but we will talk of it.”\n\n“Well, then, three months,” said Penelon.\n\n“Cocles, pay two hundred francs to each of these good fellows,” said Morrel. “At another time,” added he, “I should have said, Give them, besides, two hundred francs over as a present; but times are changed, and the little money that remains to me is not my own, so do not think me mean on this account.”\n\nPenelon turned to his companions, and exchanged a few words with them.\n\n“As for that, M. Morrel,” said he, again turning his quid, “as for that——”\n\n“As for what?”\n\n“The money.”\n“Well——”\n\n“Well, we all say that fifty francs will be enough for us at present, and that we will wait for the rest.”\n\n“Thanks, my friends, thanks!” cried Morrel gratefully; “take it—take it; and if you can find another employer, enter his service; you are free to do so.”\n\nThese last words produced a prodigious effect on the seaman. Penelon nearly swallowed his quid; fortunately he recovered.\n\n“What, M. Morrel!” said he in a low voice, “you send us away; you are then angry with us!”\n\n“No, no,” said M. Morrel, “I am not angry, quite the contrary, and I do not send you away; but I have no more ships, and therefore I do not want any sailors.”\n\n“No more ships!” returned Penelon; “well, then, you’ll build some; we’ll wait for you.”\n\n“I have no money to build ships with, Penelon,” said the poor owner mournfully, “so I cannot accept your kind offer.”\n\n“No more money? Then you must not pay us; we can scud, like the _Pharaon_, under bare poles.”\n\n“Enough, enough!” cried Morrel, almost overpowered; “leave me, I pray you; we shall meet again in a happier time. Emmanuel, go with them, and see that my orders are executed.”\n\n“At least, we shall see each other again, M. Morrel?” asked Penelon.\n\n“Yes; I hope so, at least. Now go.” He made a sign to Cocles, who went first; the seamen followed him and Emmanuel brought up the rear. “Now,” said the owner to his wife and daughter, “leave me; I wish to speak with this gentleman.”\n20045m\n\nAnd he glanced towards the clerk of Thomson & French, who had remained motionless in the corner during this scene, in which he had taken no part, except the few words we have mentioned. The two women looked at this person whose presence they had entirely forgotten, and retired; but, as she left the apartment, Julie gave the stranger a supplicating glance, to which he replied by a smile that an indifferent spectator would have been surprised to see on his stern features. The two men were left alone. “Well, sir,” said Morrel, sinking into a chair, “you have heard all, and I have nothing further to tell you.”\n\n“I see,” returned the Englishman, “that a fresh and unmerited misfortune has overwhelmed you, and this only increases my desire to serve you.”\n\n“Oh, sir!” cried Morrel.\n\n“Let me see,” continued the stranger, “I am one of your largest creditors.”\n\n“Your bills, at least, are the first that will fall due.”\n\n“Do you wish for time to pay?”\n\n“A delay would save my honor, and consequently my life.”\n\n“How long a delay do you wish for?”\n\nMorrel reflected. “Two months,” said he.\n\n“I will give you three,” replied the stranger.\n\n“But,” asked Morrel, “will the house of Thomson & French consent?”\n\n“Oh, I take everything on myself. Today is the 5th of June.”\n“Yes.”\n\n“Well, renew these bills up to the 5th of September; and on the 5th of September at eleven o’clock (the hand of the clock pointed to eleven), I shall come to receive the money.”\n\n“I shall expect you,” returned Morrel; “and I will pay you—or I shall be dead.” These last words were uttered in so low a tone that the stranger could not hear them. The bills were renewed, the old ones destroyed, and the poor ship-owner found himself with three months before him to collect his resources. The Englishman received his thanks with the phlegm peculiar to his nation; and Morrel, overwhelming him with grateful blessings, conducted him to the staircase. The stranger met Julie on the stairs; she pretended to be descending, but in reality she was waiting for him. “Oh, sir”—said she, clasping her hands.\n\n“Mademoiselle,” said the stranger, “one day you will receive a letter signed ‘Sinbad the Sailor.’ Do exactly what the letter bids you, however strange it may appear.”\n\n“Yes, sir,” returned Julie.\n\n“Do you promise?”\n\n“I swear to you I will.”\n\n“It is well. Adieu, mademoiselle. Continue to be the good, sweet girl you are at present, and I have great hopes that Heaven will reward you by giving you Emmanuel for a husband.”\n\nJulie uttered a faint cry, blushed like a rose, and leaned against the baluster. The stranger waved his hand, and continued to descend. In the court he found Penelon, who, with a rouleau of a hundred francs in either hand, seemed unable to make up his mind to retain them. “Come with me, my friend,” said the Englishman; “I wish to speak to you.”\n\n# Chapter 30. The Fifth of September\n\nThe extension provided for by the agent of Thomson & French, at the moment when Morrel expected it least, was to the poor shipowner so decided a stroke of good fortune that he almost dared to believe that fate was at length grown weary of wasting her spite upon him. The same day he told his wife, Emmanuel, and his daughter all that had occurred; and a ray of hope, if not of tranquillity, returned to the family. Unfortunately, however, Morrel had not only engagements with the house of Thomson & French, who had shown themselves so considerate towards him; and, as he had said, in business he had correspondents, and not friends. When he thought the matter over, he could by no means account for this generous conduct on the part of Thomson & French towards him; and could only attribute it to some such selfish argument as this: “We had better help a man who owes us nearly 300,000 francs, and have those 300,000 francs at the end of three months than hasten his ruin, and get only six or eight per cent of our money back again.”\n\nUnfortunately, whether through envy or stupidity, all Morrel’s correspondents did not take this view; and some even came to a contrary decision. The bills signed by Morrel were presented at his office with scrupulous exactitude, and, thanks to the delay granted by the Englishman, were paid by Cocles with equal punctuality. Cocles thus remained in his accustomed tranquillity. It was Morrel alone who remembered with alarm, that if he had to repay on the 15th the 50,000 francs of M. de Boville, and on the 30th the 32,500 francs of bills, for which, as well as the debt due to the inspector of prisons, he had time granted, he must be a ruined man.\n\nThe opinion of all the commercial men was that, under the reverses which had successively weighed down Morrel, it was impossible for him to remain solvent. Great, therefore, was the astonishment when at the end of the month, he cancelled all his obligations with his usual punctuality. Still confidence was not restored to all minds, and the general opinion was that the complete ruin of the unfortunate shipowner had been postponed only until the end of the month.\n\nThe month passed, and Morrel made extraordinary efforts to get in all his resources. Formerly his paper, at any date, was taken with confidence, and was even in request. Morrel now tried to negotiate bills at ninety days only, and none of the banks would give him credit. Fortunately, Morrel had some funds coming in on which he could rely; and, as they reached him, he found himself in a condition to meet his engagements when the end of July came.\n\nThe agent of Thomson & French had not been again seen at Marseilles; the day after, or two days after his visit to Morrel, he had disappeared; and as in that city he had had no intercourse but with the mayor, the inspector of prisons, and M. Morrel, his departure left no trace except in the memories of these three persons. As to the sailors of the _Pharaon_, they must have found snug berths elsewhere, for they also had disappeared.\n\nCaptain Gaumard, recovered from his illness, had returned from Palma. He delayed presenting himself at Morrel’s, but the owner, hearing of his arrival, went to see him. The worthy shipowner knew, from Penelon’s recital, of the captain’s brave conduct during the storm, and tried to console him. He brought him also the amount of his wages, which Captain Gaumard had not dared to apply for.\n\nAs he descended the staircase, Morrel met Penelon, who was going up. Penelon had, it would seem, made good use of his money, for he was newly clad. When he saw his employer, the worthy tar seemed much embarrassed, drew on one side into the corner of the landing-place, passed his quid from one cheek to the other, stared stupidly with his great eyes, and only acknowledged the squeeze of the hand which Morrel as usual gave him by a slight pressure in return. Morrel attributed Penelon’s embarrassment to the elegance of his attire; it was evident the good fellow had not gone to such an expense on his own account; he was, no doubt, engaged on board some other vessel, and thus his bashfulness arose from the fact of his not having, if we may so express ourselves, worn mourning for the _Pharaon_ longer. Perhaps he had come to tell Captain Gaumard of his good luck, and to offer him employment from his new master.\n\n“Worthy fellows!” said Morrel, as he went away, “may your new master love you as I loved you, and be more fortunate than I have been!”\n\n20049m\nAugust rolled by in unceasing efforts on the part of Morrel to renew his credit or revive the old. On the 20th of August it was known at Marseilles that he had left town in the mailcoach, and then it was said that the bills would go to protest at the end of the month, and that Morrel had gone away and left his chief clerk Emmanuel, and his cashier Cocles, to meet the creditors. But, contrary to all expectation, when the 31st of August came, the house opened as usual, and Cocles appeared behind the grating of the counter, examined all bills presented with the usual scrutiny, and, from first to last, paid all with the usual precision. There came in, moreover, two drafts which M. Morrel had fully anticipated, and which Cocles paid as punctually as the bills which the shipowner had accepted. All this was incomprehensible, and then, with the tenacity peculiar to prophets of bad news, the failure was put off until the end of September.\n\nOn the 1st, Morrel returned; he was awaited by his family with extreme anxiety, for from this journey to Paris they hoped great things. Morrel had thought of Danglars, who was now immensely rich, and had lain under great obligations to Morrel in former days, since to him it was owing that Danglars entered the service of the Spanish banker, with whom he had laid the foundations of his vast wealth. It was said at this moment that Danglars was worth from six to eight millions of francs, and had unlimited credit. Danglars, then, without taking a crown from his pocket, could save Morrel; he had but to pass his word for a loan, and Morrel was saved. Morrel had long thought of Danglars, but had kept away from some instinctive motive, and had delayed as long as possible availing himself of this last resource. And Morrel was right, for he returned home crushed by the humiliation of a refusal.\n\nYet, on his arrival, Morrel did not utter a complaint, or say one harsh word. He embraced his weeping wife and daughter, pressed Emmanuel’s hand with friendly warmth, and then going to his private room on the second floor had sent for Cocles.\n\n“Then,” said the two women to Emmanuel, “we are indeed ruined.”\n\nIt was agreed in a brief council held among them, that Julie should write to her brother, who was in garrison at Nîmes, to come to them as speedily as possible. The poor women felt instinctively that they required all their strength to support the blow that impended. Besides, Maximilian Morrel, though hardly two-and-twenty, had great influence over his father.\n\nHe was a strong-minded, upright young man. At the time when he decided on his profession his father had no desire to choose for him, but had consulted young Maximilian’s taste. He had at once declared for a military life, and had in consequence studied hard, passed brilliantly through the Polytechnic School, and left it as sub-lieutenant of the 53rd of the line. For a year he had held this rank, and expected promotion on the first vacancy. In his regiment Maximilian Morrel was noted for his rigid observance, not only of the obligations imposed on a soldier, but also of the duties of a man; and he thus gained the name of “the stoic.” We need hardly say that many of those who gave him this epithet repeated it because they had heard it, and did not even know what it meant.\n\nThis was the young man whom his mother and sister called to their aid to sustain them under the serious trial which they felt they would soon have to endure. They had not mistaken the gravity of this event, for the moment after Morrel had entered his private office with Cocles, Julie saw the latter leave it pale, trembling, and his features betraying the utmost consternation. She would have questioned him as he passed by her, but the worthy creature hastened down the staircase with unusual precipitation, and only raised his hands to heaven and exclaimed:\n\n“Oh, mademoiselle, mademoiselle, what a dreadful misfortune! Who could ever have believed it!”\n\nA moment afterwards Julie saw him go upstairs carrying two or three heavy ledgers, a portfolio, and a bag of money.\n\nMorrel examined the ledgers, opened the portfolio, and counted the money. All his funds amounted to 6,000 or 8,000 francs, his bills receivable up to the 5th to 4,000 or 5,000, which, making the best of everything, gave him 14,000 francs to meet debts amounting to 287,500 francs. He had not even the means for making a possible settlement on account.\n\nHowever, when Morrel went down to his dinner, he appeared very calm. This calmness was more alarming to the two women than the deepest dejection would have been. After dinner Morrel usually went out and used to take his coffee at the club of the Phocéens, and read the _Semaphore_; this day he did not leave the house, but returned to his office.\n\nAs to Cocles, he seemed completely bewildered. For part of the day he went into the courtyard, seated himself on a stone with his head bare and exposed to the blazing sun. Emmanuel tried to comfort the women, but his eloquence faltered. The young man was too well acquainted with the business of the house, not to feel that a great catastrophe hung over the Morrel family. Night came, the two women had watched, hoping that when he left his room Morrel would come to them, but they heard him pass before their door, and trying to conceal the noise of his footsteps. They listened; he went into his sleeping-room, and fastened the door inside. Madame Morrel sent her daughter to bed, and half an hour after Julie had retired, she rose, took off her shoes, and went stealthily along the passage, to see through the keyhole what her husband was doing.\n\nIn the passage she saw a retreating shadow; it was Julie, who, uneasy herself, had anticipated her mother. The young lady went towards Madame Morrel.\n\n“He is writing,” she said.\n\nThey had understood each other without speaking. Madame Morrel looked again through the keyhole, Morrel was writing; but Madame Morrel remarked, what her daughter had not observed, that her husband was writing on stamped paper. The terrible idea that he was writing his will flashed across her; she shuddered, and yet had not strength to utter a word.\n\nNext day M. Morrel seemed as calm as ever, went into his office as usual, came to his breakfast punctually, and then, after dinner, he placed his daughter beside him, took her head in his arms, and held her for a long time against his bosom. In the evening, Julie told her mother, that although he was apparently so calm, she had noticed that her father’s heart beat violently.\n\nThe next two days passed in much the same way. On the evening of the 4th of September, M. Morrel asked his daughter for the key of his study. Julie trembled at this request, which seemed to her of bad omen. Why did her father ask for this key which she always kept, and which was only taken from her in childhood as a punishment? The young girl looked at Morrel.\n\n“What have I done wrong, father,” she said, “that you should take this key from me?”\n\n“Nothing, my dear,” replied the unhappy man, the tears starting to his eyes at this simple question,—“nothing, only I want it.”\n\nJulie made a pretence to feel for the key. “I must have left it in my room,” she said.\n\nAnd she went out, but instead of going to her apartment she hastened to consult Emmanuel.\n\n“Do not give this key to your father,” said he, “and tomorrow morning, if possible, do not quit him for a moment.”\n\nShe questioned Emmanuel, but he knew nothing, or would not say what he knew.\n\nDuring the night, between the 4th and 5th of September, Madame Morrel remained listening for every sound, and, until three o’clock in the morning, she heard her husband pacing the room in great agitation. It was three o’clock when he threw himself on the bed. The mother and daughter passed the night together. They had expected Maximilian since the previous evening. At eight o’clock in the morning Morrel entered their chamber. He was calm; but the agitation of the night was legible in his pale and careworn visage. They did not dare to ask him how he had slept. Morrel was kinder to his wife, more affectionate to his daughter, than he had ever been. He could not cease gazing at and kissing the sweet girl. Julie, mindful of Emmanuel’s request, was following her father when he quitted the room, but he said to her quickly:\n\n“Remain with your mother, dearest.” Julie wished to accompany him. “I wish you to do so,” said he.\n\nThis was the first time Morrel had ever so spoken, but he said it in a tone of paternal kindness, and Julie did not dare to disobey. She remained at the same spot standing mute and motionless. An instant afterwards the door opened, she felt two arms encircle her, and a mouth pressed her forehead. She looked up and uttered an exclamation of joy.\n20053m\n\n“Maximilian, my dearest brother!” she cried.\n\nAt these words Madame Morrel rose, and threw herself into her son’s arms.\n\n“Mother,” said the young man, looking alternately at Madame Morrel and her daughter, “what has occurred—what has happened? Your letter has frightened me, and I have come hither with all speed.”\n\n“Julie,” said Madame Morrel, making a sign to the young man, “go and tell your father that Maximilian has just arrived.”\n\nThe young lady rushed out of the apartment, but on the first step of the staircase she found a man holding a letter in his hand.\n\n“Are you not Mademoiselle Julie Morrel?” inquired the man, with a strong Italian accent.\n\n“Yes, sir,” replied Julie with hesitation; “what is your pleasure? I do not know you.”\n\n“Read this letter,” he said, handing it to her. Julie hesitated. “It concerns the best interests of your father,” said the messenger.\n\nThe young girl hastily took the letter from him. She opened it quickly and read:\n\n“Go this moment to the Allées de Meilhan, enter the house No. 15, ask the porter for the key of the room on the fifth floor, enter the apartment, take from the corner of the mantelpiece a purse netted in red silk, and give it to your father. It is important that he should receive it before eleven o’clock. You promised to obey me implicitly. Remember your oath.\n\n“Sinbad the Sailor.”\n\nThe young girl uttered a joyful cry, raised her eyes, looked round to question the messenger, but he had disappeared. She cast her eyes again over the note to peruse it a second time, and saw there was a postscript. She read:\n\n“It is important that you should fulfil this mission in person and alone. If you go accompanied by any other person, or should anyone else go in your place, the porter will reply that he does not know anything about it.”\n\nThis postscript decreased greatly the young girl’s happiness. Was there nothing to fear? was there not some snare laid for her? Her innocence had kept her in ignorance of the dangers that might assail a young girl of her age. But there is no need to know danger in order to fear it; indeed, it may be observed, that it is usually unknown perils that inspire the greatest terror.\n\nJulie hesitated, and resolved to take counsel. Yet, through a singular impulse, it was neither to her mother nor her brother that she applied, but to Emmanuel. She hastened down and told him what had occurred on the day when the agent of Thomson & French had come to her father’s, related the scene on the staircase, repeated the promise she had made, and showed him the letter.\n\n“You must go, then, mademoiselle,” said Emmanuel.\n\n“Go there?” murmured Julie.\n\n“Yes; I will accompany you.”\n\n“But did you not read that I must be alone?” said Julie.\n\n“And you shall be alone,” replied the young man. “I will await you at the corner of the Rue du Musée, and if you are so long absent as to make me uneasy, I will hasten to rejoin you, and woe to him of whom you shall have cause to complain to me!”\n\n“Then, Emmanuel?” said the young girl with hesitation, “it is your opinion that I should obey this invitation?”\n\n“Yes. Did not the messenger say your father’s safety depended upon it?”\n\n“But what danger threatens him, then, Emmanuel?” she asked.\n\nEmmanuel hesitated a moment, but his desire to make Julie decide immediately made him reply.\n\n“Listen,” he said; “today is the 5th of September, is it not?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Today, then, at eleven o’clock, your father has nearly three hundred thousand francs to pay?”\n\n“Yes, we know that.”\n\n“Well, then,” continued Emmanuel, “we have not fifteen thousand francs in the house.”\n\n“What will happen then?”\n\n“Why, if today before eleven o’clock your father has not found someone who will come to his aid, he will be compelled at twelve o’clock to declare himself a bankrupt.”\n\n“Oh, come, then, come!” cried she, hastening away with the young man.\n\nDuring this time, Madame Morrel had told her son everything. The young man knew quite well that, after the succession of misfortunes which had befallen his father, great changes had taken place in the style of living and housekeeping; but he did not know that matters had reached such a point. He was thunderstruck. Then, rushing hastily out of the apartment, he ran upstairs, expecting to find his father in his study, but he rapped there in vain.\n\nWhile he was yet at the door of the study he heard the bedroom door open, turned, and saw his father. Instead of going direct to his study, M. Morrel had returned to his bedchamber, which he was only this moment quitting. Morrel uttered a cry of surprise at the sight of his son, of whose arrival he was ignorant. He remained motionless on the spot, pressing with his left hand something he had concealed under his coat. Maximilian sprang down the staircase, and threw his arms round his father’s neck; but suddenly he recoiled, and placed his right hand on Morrel’s breast.\n\n“Father,” he exclaimed, turning pale as death, “what are you going to do with that brace of pistols under your coat?”\n\n“Oh, this is what I feared!” said Morrel.\n\n“Father, father, in Heaven’s name,” exclaimed the young man, “what are these weapons for?”\n\n“Maximilian,” replied Morrel, looking fixedly at his son, “you are a man, and a man of honor. Come, and I will explain to you.”\n\nAnd with a firm step Morrel went up to his study, while Maximilian followed him, trembling as he went. Morrel opened the door, and closed it behind his son; then, crossing the anteroom, went to his desk on which he placed the pistols, and pointed with his finger to an open ledger. In this ledger was made out an exact balance-sheet of his affairs. Morrel had to pay, within half an hour, 287,500 francs. All he possessed was 15,257 francs.\n\n“Read!” said Morrel.\n\nThe young man was overwhelmed as he read. Morrel said not a word. What could he say? What need he add to such a desperate proof in figures?\n\n“And have you done all that is possible, father, to meet this disastrous result?” asked the young man, after a moment’s pause.\n\n“I have,” replied Morrel.\n\n“You have no money coming in on which you can rely?”\n\n“None.”\n\n“You have exhausted every resource?”\n\n“All.”\n\n“And in half an hour,” said Maximilian in a gloomy voice, “our name is dishonored!”\n\n“Blood washes out dishonor,” said Morrel.\n\n“You are right, father; I understand you.” Then extending his hand towards one of the pistols, he said, “There is one for you and one for me—thanks!”\n\nMorrel caught his hand. “Your mother—your sister! Who will support them?”\n\nA shudder ran through the young man’s frame. “Father,” he said, “do you reflect that you are bidding me to live?”\n\n“Yes, I do so bid you,” answered Morrel, “it is your duty. You have a calm, strong mind, Maximilian. Maximilian, you are no ordinary man. I make no requests or commands; I only ask you to examine my position as if it were your own, and then judge for yourself.”\n\nThe young man reflected for a moment, then an expression of sublime resignation appeared in his eyes, and with a slow and sad gesture he took off his two epaulets, the insignia of his rank.\n\n“Be it so, then, my father,” he said, extending his hand to Morrel, “die in peace, my father; I will live.”\n\nMorrel was about to cast himself on his knees before his son, but Maximilian caught him in his arms, and those two noble hearts were pressed against each other for a moment.\n\n“You know it is not my fault,” said Morrel.\n20057m\n\nMaximilian smiled. “I know, father, you are the most honorable man I have ever known.”\n\n“Good, my son. And now there is no more to be said; go and rejoin your mother and sister.”\n\n“My father,” said the young man, bending his knee, “bless me!” Morrel took the head of his son between his two hands, drew him forward, and kissing his forehead several times said:\n\n“Oh, yes, yes, I bless you in my own name, and in the name of three generations of irreproachable men, who say through me, ‘The edifice which misfortune has destroyed, Providence may build up again.’ On seeing me die such a death, the most inexorable will have pity on you. To you, perhaps, they will accord the time they have refused to me. Then do your best to keep our name free from dishonor. Go to work, labor, young man, struggle ardently and courageously; live, yourself, your mother and sister, with the most rigid economy, so that from day to day the property of those whom I leave in your hands may augment and fructify. Reflect how glorious a day it will be, how grand, how solemn, that day of complete restoration, on which you will say in this very office, ‘My father died because he could not do what I have this day done; but he died calmly and peaceably, because in dying he knew what I should do.’”\n\n“My father, my father!” cried the young man, “why should you not live?”\n\n“If I live, all would be changed; if I live, interest would be converted into doubt, pity into hostility; if I live I am only a man who has broken his word, failed in his engagements—in fact, only a bankrupt. If, on the contrary, I die, remember, Maximilian, my corpse is that of an honest but unfortunate man. Living, my best friends would avoid my house; dead, all Marseilles will follow me in tears to my last home. Living, you would feel shame at my name; dead, you may raise your head and say, ‘I am the son of him you killed, because, for the first time, he has been compelled to break his word.’”\n\nThe young man uttered a groan, but appeared resigned.\n\n“And now,” said Morrel, “leave me alone, and endeavor to keep your mother and sister away.”\n\n“Will you not see my sister once more?” asked Maximilian. A last but final hope was concealed by the young man in the effect of this interview, and therefore he had suggested it. Morrel shook his head. “I saw her this morning, and bade her adieu.”\n\n“Have you no particular commands to leave with me, my father?” inquired Maximilian in a faltering voice.\n\n“Yes; my son, and a sacred command.”\n\n“Say it, my father.”\n\n“The house of Thomson & French is the only one who, from humanity, or, it may be, selfishness—it is not for me to read men’s hearts—has had any pity for me. Its agent, who will in ten minutes present himself to receive the amount of a bill of 287,500 francs, I will not say granted, but offered me three months. Let this house be the first repaid, my son, and respect this man.”\n\n“Father, I will,” said Maximilian.\n\n“And now, once more, adieu,” said Morrel. “Go, leave me; I would be alone. You will find my will in the secretaire in my bedroom.”\n\nThe young man remained standing and motionless, having but the force of will and not the power of execution.\n\n“Hear me, Maximilian,” said his father. “Suppose I were a soldier like you, and ordered to carry a certain redoubt, and you knew I must be killed in the assault, would you not say to me, as you said just now, ‘Go, father; for you are dishonored by delay, and death is preferable to shame!’”\n\n“Yes, yes,” said the young man, “yes;” and once again embracing his father with convulsive pressure, he said, “Be it so, my father.”\n\nAnd he rushed out of the study. When his son had left him, Morrel remained an instant standing with his eyes fixed on the door; then putting forth his arm, he pulled the bell. After a moment’s interval, Cocles appeared.\n\nIt was no longer the same man—the fearful revelations of the three last days had crushed him. This thought—the house of Morrel is about to stop payment—bent him to the earth more than twenty years would otherwise have done.\n\n“My worthy Cocles,” said Morrel in a tone impossible to describe, “do you remain in the antechamber. When the gentleman who came three months ago—the agent of Thomson & French—arrives, announce his arrival to me.”\n\nCocles made no reply; he made a sign with his head, went into the anteroom, and seated himself. Morrel fell back in his chair, his eyes fixed on the clock; there were seven minutes left, that was all. The hand moved on with incredible rapidity, he seemed to see its motion.\n\nWhat passed in the mind of this man at the supreme moment of his agony cannot be told in words. He was still comparatively young, he was surrounded by the loving care of a devoted family, but he had convinced himself by a course of reasoning, illogical perhaps, yet certainly plausible, that he must separate himself from all he held dear in the world, even life itself. To form the slightest idea of his feelings, one must have seen his face with its expression of enforced resignation and its tear-moistened eyes raised to heaven. The minute hand moved on. The pistols were loaded; he stretched forth his hand, took one up, and murmured his daughter’s name. Then he laid it down, seized his pen, and wrote a few words. It seemed to him as if he had not taken a sufficient farewell of his beloved daughter. Then he turned again to the clock, counting time now not by minutes, but by seconds.\n\nHe took up the deadly weapon again, his lips parted and his eyes fixed on the clock, and then shuddered at the click of the trigger as he cocked the pistol. At this moment of mortal anguish the cold sweat came forth upon his brow, a pang stronger than death clutched at his heart-strings. He heard the door of the staircase creak on its hinges—the clock gave its warning to strike eleven—the door of his study opened. Morrel did not turn round—he expected these words of Cocles, “The agent of Thomson & French.”\n\nHe placed the muzzle of the pistol between his teeth. Suddenly he heard a cry—it was his daughter’s voice. He turned and saw Julie. The pistol fell from his hands.\n\n“My father!” cried the young girl, out of breath, and half dead with joy—“saved, you are saved!” And she threw herself into his arms, holding in her extended hand a red, netted silk purse.\n\n20061m\n“Saved, my child!” said Morrel; “what do you mean?”\n\n“Yes, saved—saved! See, see!” said the young girl.\n\nMorrel took the purse, and started as he did so, for a vague remembrance reminded him that it once belonged to himself. At one end was the receipted bill for the 287,000 francs, and at the other was a diamond as large as a hazel-nut, with these words on a small slip of parchment: _Julie’s Dowry_.\n\nMorrel passed his hand over his brow; it seemed to him a dream. At this moment the clock struck eleven. He felt as if each stroke of the hammer fell upon his heart.\n\n“Explain, my child,” he said, “Explain, my child,” he said, “explain—where did you find this purse?”\n\n“In a house in the Allées de Meilhan, No. 15, on the corner of a mantelpiece in a small room on the fifth floor.”\n\n“But,” cried Morrel, “this purse is not yours!” Julie handed to her father the letter she had received in the morning.\n\n“And did you go alone?” asked Morrel, after he had read it.\n\n“Emmanuel accompanied me, father. He was to have waited for me at the corner of the Rue du Musée, but, strange to say, he was not there when I returned.”\n\n“Monsieur Morrel!” exclaimed a voice on the stairs; “Monsieur Morrel!”\n\n“It is his voice!” said Julie. At this moment Emmanuel entered, his countenance full of animation and joy.\n\n“The _Pharaon_!” he cried; “the _Pharaon_!”\n\n“What!—what!—the _Pharaon_! Are you mad, Emmanuel? You know the vessel is lost.”\n\n“The _Pharaon_, sir—they signal the _Pharaon_! The _Pharaon_ is entering the harbor!”\n\nMorrel fell back in his chair, his strength was failing him; his understanding weakened by such events, refused to comprehend such incredible, unheard-of, fabulous facts. But his son came in.\n\n“Father,” cried Maximilian, “how could you say the _Pharaon_ was lost? The lookout has signalled her, and they say she is now coming into port.”\n20063m\n\n“My dear friends,” said Morrel, “if this be so, it must be a miracle of heaven! Impossible, impossible!”\n\nBut what was real and not less incredible was the purse he held in his hand, the acceptance receipted—the splendid diamond.\n\n“Ah, sir,” exclaimed Cocles, “what can it mean?—the _Pharaon_?”\n\n“Come, dear ones,” said Morrel, rising from his seat, “let us go and see, and Heaven have pity upon us if it be false intelligence!”\n\nThey all went out, and on the stairs met Madame Morrel, who had been afraid to go up into the study. In a moment they were at the Canebière. There was a crowd on the pier. All the crowd gave way before Morrel. “The _Pharaon_! the _Pharaon_!” said every voice.\n\nAnd, wonderful to see, in front of the tower of Saint-Jean, was a ship bearing on her stern these words, printed in white letters, “The _Pharaon_, Morrel & Son, of Marseilles.” She was the exact duplicate of the other _Pharaon_, and loaded, as that had been, with cochineal and indigo. She cast anchor, clued up sails, and on the deck was Captain Gaumard giving orders, and good old Penelon making signals to M. Morrel. To doubt any longer was impossible; there was the evidence of the senses, and ten thousand persons who came to corroborate the testimony.\n\nAs Morrel and his son embraced on the pier-head, in the presence and amid the applause of the whole city witnessing this event, a man, with his face half-covered by a black beard, and who, concealed behind the sentry-box, watched the scene with delight, uttered these words in a low tone:\n\n“Be happy, noble heart, be blessed for all the good thou hast done and wilt do hereafter, and let my gratitude remain in obscurity like your good deeds.”\n\n20064m\nAnd with a smile expressive of supreme content, he left his hiding-place, and without being observed, descended one of the flights of steps provided for debarkation, and hailing three times, shouted “Jacopo, Jacopo, Jacopo!”\n\nThen a launch came to shore, took him on board, and conveyed him to a yacht splendidly fitted up, on whose deck he sprung with the activity of a sailor; thence he once again looked towards Morrel, who, weeping with joy, was shaking hands most cordially with all the crowd around him, and thanking with a look the unknown benefactor whom he seemed to be seeking in the skies.\n\n“And now,” said the unknown, “farewell kindness, humanity, and gratitude! Farewell to all the feelings that expand the heart! I have been Heaven’s substitute to recompense the good—now the god of vengeance yields to me his power to punish the wicked!”\n\nAt these words he gave a signal, and, as if only awaiting this signal, the yacht instantly put out to sea.\n\n# Chapter 31. Italy: Sinbad the Sailor\n\nTowards the beginning of the year 1838, two young men belonging to the first society of Paris, the Viscount Albert de Morcerf and the Baron Franz d’Épinay, were at Florence. They had agreed to see the Carnival at Rome that year, and that Franz, who for the last three or four years had inhabited Italy, should act as _cicerone_ to Albert.\n\nAs it is no inconsiderable affair to spend the Carnival at Rome, especially when you have no great desire to sleep on the Piazza del Popolo, or the Campo Vaccino, they wrote to Signor Pastrini, the proprietor of the Hôtel de Londres, Piazza di Spagna, to reserve comfortable apartments for them. Signor Pastrini replied that he had only two rooms and a parlor on the third floor, which he offered at the low charge of a louis per diem. They accepted his offer; but wishing to make the best use of the time that was left, Albert started for Naples. As for Franz, he remained at Florence, and after having passed a few days in exploring the paradise of the Cascine, and spending two or three evenings at the houses of the Florentine nobility, he took a fancy into his head (having already visited Corsica, the cradle of Bonaparte) to visit Elba, the waiting-place of Napoleon.\n\nOne evening he cast off the painter of a sailboat from the iron ring that secured it to the dock at Leghorn, wrapped himself in his coat and lay down, and said to the crew,—“To the Island of Elba!”\n\nThe boat shot out of the harbor like a bird and the next morning Franz disembarked at Porto-Ferrajo. He traversed the island, after having followed the traces which the footsteps of the giant have left, and re-embarked for Marciana.\n\nTwo hours after he again landed at Pianosa, where he was assured that red partridges abounded. The sport was bad; Franz only succeeded in killing a few partridges, and, like every unsuccessful sportsman, he returned to the boat very much out of temper.\n\n“Ah, if your excellency chose,” said the captain, “you might have capital sport.”\n\n“Where?”\n\n“Do you see that island?” continued the captain, pointing to a conical pile rising from the indigo sea.\n\n“Well, what is this island?”\n\n“The Island of Monte Cristo.”\n\n“But I have no permission to shoot over this island.”\n\n“Your excellency does not require a permit, for the island is uninhabited.”\n\n“Ah, indeed!” said the young man. “A desert island in the midst of the Mediterranean must be a curiosity.”\n\n“It is very natural; this island is a mass of rocks, and does not contain an acre of land capable of cultivation.”\n\n“To whom does this island belong?”\n\n“To Tuscany.”\n\n“What game shall I find there!”\n\n“Thousands of wild goats.”\n\n“Who live upon the stones, I suppose,” said Franz with an incredulous smile.\n\n“No, but by browsing the shrubs and trees that grow out of the crevices of the rocks.”\n\n“Where can I sleep?”\n\n“On shore in the grottos, or on board in your cloak; besides, if your excellency pleases, we can leave as soon as you like—we can sail as well by night as by day, and if the wind drops we can use our oars.”\n\nAs Franz had sufficient time, and his apartments at Rome were not yet available, he accepted the proposition. Upon his answer in the affirmative, the sailors exchanged a few words together in a low tone. “Well,” asked he, “what now? Is there any difficulty in the way?”\n\n“No.” replied the captain, “but we must warn your excellency that the island is an infected port.”\n\n“What do you mean?”\n\n“Monte Cristo although uninhabited, yet serves occasionally as a refuge for the smugglers and pirates who come from Corsica, Sardinia, and Africa, and if it becomes known that we have been there, we shall have to perform quarantine for six days on our return to Leghorn.”\n\n“The deuce! That puts a different face on the matter. Six days! Why, that’s as long as the Almighty took to make the world! Too long a wait—too long.”\n\n“But who will say your excellency has been to Monte Cristo?”\n\n“Oh, I shall not,” cried Franz.\n\n“Nor I, nor I,” chorused the sailors.\n\n“Then steer for Monte Cristo.”\n\nThe captain gave his orders, the helm was put up, and the boat was soon sailing in the direction of the island. Franz waited until all was in order, and when the sail was filled, and the four sailors had taken their places—three forward, and one at the helm—he resumed the conversation. “Gaetano,” said he to the captain, “you tell me Monte Cristo serves as a refuge for pirates, who are, it seems to me, a very different kind of game from the goats.”\n\n“Yes, your excellency, and it is true.”\n\n“I knew there were smugglers, but I thought that since the capture of Algiers, and the destruction of the regency, pirates existed only in the romances of Cooper and Captain Marryat.”\n\n“Your excellency is mistaken; there are pirates, like the bandits who were believed to have been exterminated by Pope Leo XII., and who yet, every day, rob travellers at the gates of Rome. Has not your excellency heard that the French _chargé d’affaires_ was robbed six months ago within five hundred paces of Velletri?”\n\n“Oh, yes, I heard that.”\n\n“Well, then, if, like us, your excellency lived at Leghorn, you would hear, from time to time, that a little merchant vessel, or an English yacht that was expected at Bastia, at Porto-Ferrajo, or at Civita Vecchia, has not arrived; no one knows what has become of it, but, doubtless, it has struck on a rock and foundered. Now this rock it has met has been a long and narrow boat, manned by six or eight men, who have surprised and plundered it, some dark and stormy night, near some desert and gloomy island, as bandits plunder a carriage in the recesses of a forest.”\n\n“But,” asked Franz, who lay wrapped in his cloak at the bottom of the boat, “why do not those who have been plundered complain to the French, Sardinian, or Tuscan governments?”\n\n“Why?” said Gaetano with a smile.\n\n“Yes, why?”\n\n“Because, in the first place, they transfer from the vessel to their own boat whatever they think worth taking, then they bind the crew hand and foot, they attach to everyone’s neck a four-and-twenty-pound ball, a large hole is chopped in the vessel’s bottom, and then they leave her. At the end of ten minutes the vessel begins to roll heavily and settle down. First one gun’l goes under, then the other. Then they lift and sink again, and both go under at once. All at once there’s a noise like a cannon—that’s the air blowing up the deck. Soon the water rushes out of the scupper-holes like a whale spouting, the vessel gives a last groan, spins round and round, and disappears, forming a vast whirlpool in the ocean, and then all is over, so that in five minutes nothing but the eye of God can see the vessel where she lies at the bottom of the sea. Do you understand now,” said the captain, “why no complaints are made to the government, and why the vessel never reaches port?”\n\nIt is probable that if Gaetano had related this previous to proposing the expedition, Franz would have hesitated, but now that they had started, he thought it would be cowardly to draw back. He was one of those men who do not rashly court danger, but if danger presents itself, combat it with the most unalterable coolness. Calm and resolute, he treated any peril as he would an adversary in a duel,—calculated its probable method of approach; retreated, if at all, as a point of strategy and not from cowardice; was quick to see an opening for attack, and won victory at a single thrust.\n\n“Bah!” said he, “I have travelled through Sicily and Calabria—I have sailed two months in the Archipelago, and yet I never saw even the shadow of a bandit or a pirate.”\n\n“I did not tell your excellency this to deter you from your project,” replied Gaetano, “but you questioned me, and I have answered; that’s all.”\n\n“Yes, and your conversation is most interesting; and as I wish to enjoy it as long as possible, steer for Monte Cristo.”\n\nThe wind blew strongly, the boat made six or seven knots an hour, and they were rapidly reaching the end of their voyage. As they drew near the island seemed to lift from the sea, and the air was so clear that they could already distinguish the rocks heaped on one another, like cannon balls in an arsenal, with green bushes and trees growing in the crevices. As for the sailors, although they appeared perfectly tranquil yet it was evident that they were on the alert, and that they carefully watched the glassy surface over which they were sailing, and on which a few fishing-boats, with their white sails, were alone visible.\n\nThey were within fifteen miles of Monte Cristo when the sun began to set behind Corsica, whose mountains appeared against the sky, showing their rugged peaks in bold relief; this mass of rock, like the giant Adamastor, rose dead ahead, a formidable barrier, and intercepting the light that gilded its massive peaks so that the voyagers were in shadow. Little by little the shadow rose higher and seemed to drive before it the last rays of the expiring day; at last the reflection rested on the summit of the mountain, where it paused an instant, like the fiery crest of a volcano, then gloom gradually covered the summit as it had covered the base, and the island now only appeared to be a gray mountain that grew continually darker; half an hour after, the night was quite dark.\n\nFortunately, the mariners were used to these latitudes, and knew every rock in the Tuscan Archipelago; for in the midst of this obscurity Franz was not without uneasiness—Corsica had long since disappeared, and Monte Cristo itself was invisible; but the sailors seemed, like the lynx, to see in the dark, and the pilot who steered did not evince the slightest hesitation.\n\nAn hour had passed since the sun had set, when Franz fancied he saw, at a quarter of a mile to the left, a dark mass, but he could not precisely make out what it was, and fearing to excite the mirth of the sailors by mistaking a floating cloud for land, he remained silent; suddenly a great light appeared on the strand; land might resemble a cloud, but the fire was not a meteor.\n\n“What is this light?” asked he.\n\n“Hush!” said the captain; “it is a fire.”\n\n“But you told me the island was uninhabited?”\n\n“I said there were no fixed habitations on it, but I said also that it served sometimes as a harbor for smugglers.”\n\n“And for pirates?”\n“And for pirates,” returned Gaetano, repeating Franz’s words. “It is for that reason I have given orders to pass the island, for, as you see, the fire is behind us.”\n\n“But this fire?” continued Franz. “It seems to me rather reassuring than otherwise; men who did not wish to be seen would not light a fire.”\n\n“Oh, that goes for nothing,” said Gaetano. “If you can guess the position of the island in the darkness, you will see that the fire cannot be seen from the side or from Pianosa, but only from the sea.”\n\n“You think, then, this fire indicates the presence of unpleasant neighbors?”\n\n“That is what we must find out,” returned Gaetano, fixing his eyes on this terrestrial star.\n\n“How can you find out?”\n\n“You shall see.”\n\nGaetano consulted with his companions, and after five minutes’ discussion a manœuvre was executed which caused the vessel to tack about, they returned the way they had come, and in a few minutes the fire disappeared, hidden by an elevation of the land. The pilot again changed the course of the boat, which rapidly approached the island, and was soon within fifty paces of it. Gaetano lowered the sail, and the boat came to rest. All this was done in silence, and from the moment that their course was changed not a word was spoken.\n\nGaetano, who had proposed the expedition, had taken all the responsibility on himself; the four sailors fixed their eyes on him, while they got out their oars and held themselves in readiness to row away, which, thanks to the darkness, would not be difficult. As for Franz, he examined his arms with the utmost coolness; he had two double-barrelled guns and a rifle; he loaded them, looked at the priming, and waited quietly.\n\nDuring this time the captain had thrown off his vest and shirt, and secured his trousers round his waist; his feet were naked, so he had no shoes and stockings to take off; after these preparations he placed his finger on his lips, and lowering himself noiselessly into the sea, swam towards the shore with such precaution that it was impossible to hear the slightest sound; he could only be traced by the phosphorescent line in his wake. This track soon disappeared; it was evident that he had touched the shore.\n\nEveryone on board remained motionless for half an hour, when the same luminous track was again observed, and the swimmer was soon on board.\n\n“Well?” exclaimed Franz and the sailors in unison.\n\n“They are Spanish smugglers,” said he; “they have with them two Corsican bandits.”\n\n“And what are these Corsican bandits doing here with Spanish smugglers?”\n\n“Alas,” returned the captain with an accent of the most profound pity, “we ought always to help one another. Very often the bandits are hard pressed by gendarmes or carbineers; well, they see a vessel, and good fellows like us on board, they come and demand hospitality of us; you can’t refuse help to a poor hunted devil; we receive them, and for greater security we stand out to sea. This costs us nothing, and saves the life, or at least the liberty, of a fellow-creature, who on the first occasion returns the service by pointing out some safe spot where we can land our goods without interruption.”\n\n“Ah!” said Franz, “then you are a smuggler occasionally, Gaetano?”\n\n“Your excellency, we must live somehow,” returned the other, smiling impenetrably.\n\n“Then you know the men who are now on Monte Cristo?”\n\n“Oh, yes, we sailors are like freemasons, and recognize each other by signs.”\n\n“And do you think we have nothing to fear if we land?”\n\n“Nothing at all; smugglers are not thieves.”\n\n“But these two Corsican bandits?” said Franz, calculating the chances of peril.\n\n“It is not their fault that they are bandits, but that of the authorities.”\n\n“How so?”\n“Because they are pursued for having made a stiff, as if it was not in a Corsican’s nature to revenge himself.”\n\n“What do you mean by having made a stiff?—having assassinated a man?” said Franz, continuing his investigation.\n\n“I mean that they have killed an enemy, which is a very different thing,” returned the captain.\n\n“Well,” said the young man, “let us demand hospitality of these smugglers and bandits. Do you think they will grant it?”\n\n“Without doubt.”\n\n“How many are they?”\n\n“Four, and the two bandits make six.”\n\n“Just our number, so that if they prove troublesome, we shall be able to hold them in check; so, for the last time, steer to Monte Cristo.”\n\n“Yes, but your excellency will permit us to take all due precautions.”\n\n“By all means, be as wise as Nestor and as prudent as Ulysses; I do more than permit, I exhort you.”\n\n“Silence, then!” said Gaetano.\n\nEveryone obeyed. For a man who, like Franz, viewed his position in its true light, it was a grave one. He was alone in the darkness with sailors whom he did not know, and who had no reason to be devoted to him; who knew that he had several thousand francs in his belt, and who had often examined his weapons,—which were very beautiful,—if not with envy, at least with curiosity. On the other hand, he was about to land, without any other escort than these men, on an island which had, indeed, a very religious name, but which did not seem to Franz likely to afford him much hospitality, thanks to the smugglers and bandits. The history of the scuttled vessels, which had appeared improbable during the day, seemed very probable at night; placed as he was between two possible sources of danger, he kept his eye on the crew, and his gun in his hand.\n\nThe sailors had again hoisted sail, and the vessel was once more cleaving the waves. Through the darkness Franz, whose eyes were now more accustomed to it, could see the looming shore along which the boat was sailing, and then, as they rounded a rocky point, he saw the fire more brilliant than ever, and about it five or six persons seated. The blaze illumined the sea for a hundred paces around. Gaetano skirted the light, carefully keeping the boat in the shadow; then, when they were opposite the fire, he steered to the centre of the circle, singing a fishing song, of which his companions sung the chorus.\n\nAt the first words of the song the men seated round the fire arose and approached the landing-place, their eyes fixed on the boat, evidently seeking to know who the new-comers were and what were their intentions. They soon appeared satisfied and returned (with the exception of one, who remained at the shore) to their fire, at which the carcass of a goat was roasting. When the boat was within twenty paces of the shore, the man on the beach, who carried a carbine, presented arms after the manner of a sentinel, and cried, “Who comes there?” in Sardinian.\n\nFranz coolly cocked both barrels. Gaetano then exchanged a few words with this man which the traveller did not understand, but which evidently concerned him.\n\n“Will your excellency give your name, or remain _incognito_?” asked the captain.\n\n“My name must rest unknown,” replied Franz; “merely say I am a Frenchman travelling for pleasure.”\n\nAs soon as Gaetano had transmitted this answer, the sentinel gave an order to one of the men seated round the fire, who rose and disappeared among the rocks. Not a word was spoken, everyone seemed occupied, Franz with his disembarkment, the sailors with their sails, the smugglers with their goat; but in the midst of all this carelessness it was evident that they mutually observed each other.\n\nThe man who had disappeared returned suddenly on the opposite side to that by which he had left; he made a sign with his head to the sentinel, who, turning to the boat, said, “_S’accommodi_.” The Italian _s’accommodi_ is untranslatable; it means at once, “Come, enter, you are welcome; make yourself at home; you are the master.” It is like that Turkish phrase of Molière’s that so astonished the bourgeois gentleman by the number of things implied in its utterance.\n\nThe sailors did not wait for a second invitation; four strokes of the oar brought them to land; Gaetano sprang to shore, exchanged a few words with the sentinel, then his comrades disembarked, and lastly came Franz. One of his guns was swung over his shoulder, Gaetano had the other, and a sailor held his rifle; his dress, half artist, half dandy, did not excite any suspicion, and, consequently, no disquietude. The boat was moored to the shore, and they advanced a few paces to find a comfortable bivouac; but, doubtless, the spot they chose did not suit the smuggler who filled the post of sentinel, for he cried out:\n\n“Not that way, if you please.”\n\nGaetano faltered an excuse, and advanced to the opposite side, while two sailors kindled torches at the fire to light them on their way.\n\nThey advanced about thirty paces, and then stopped at a small esplanade surrounded with rocks, in which seats had been cut, not unlike sentry-boxes. Around in the crevices of the rocks grew a few dwarf oaks and thick bushes of myrtles. Franz lowered a torch, and saw by the mass of cinders that had accumulated that he was not the first to discover this retreat, which was, doubtless, one of the halting-places of the wandering visitors of Monte Cristo.\n\nAs for his suspicions, once on _terra firma_, once that he had seen the indifferent, if not friendly, appearance of his hosts, his anxiety had quite disappeared, or rather, at sight of the goat, had turned to appetite. He mentioned this to Gaetano, who replied that nothing could be more easy than to prepare a supper when they had in their boat, bread, wine, half a dozen partridges, and a good fire to roast them by.\n\n“Besides,” added he, “if the smell of their roast meat tempts you, I will go and offer them two of our birds for a slice.”\n\n“You are a born diplomat,” returned Franz; “go and try.”\n\nMeanwhile the sailors had collected dried sticks and branches with which they made a fire. Franz waited impatiently, inhaling the aroma of the roasted meat, when the captain returned with a mysterious air.\n\n“Well,” said Franz, “anything new?—do they refuse?”\n\n“On the contrary,” returned Gaetano, “the chief, who was told you were a young Frenchman, invites you to sup with him.”\n\n“Well,” observed Franz, “this chief is very polite, and I see no objection—the more so as I bring my share of the supper.”\n\n“Oh, it is not that; he has plenty, and to spare, for supper; but he makes one condition, and rather a peculiar one, before he will receive you at his house.”\n\n“His house? Has he built one here, then?”\n\n“No, but he has a very comfortable one all the same, so they say.”\n\n“You know this chief, then?”\n\n“I have heard talk of him.”\n\n“Favorably or otherwise?”\n“Both.”\n\n“The deuce!—and what is this condition?”\n\n“That you are blindfolded, and do not take off the bandage until he himself bids you.”\n\nFranz looked at Gaetano, to see, if possible, what he thought of this proposal. “Ah,” replied he, guessing Franz’s thought, “I know this is a serious matter.”\n\n“What should you do in my place?”\n\n“I, who have nothing to lose,—I should go.”\n20075m\n\n“You would accept?”\n\n“Yes, were it only out of curiosity.”\n\n“There is something very peculiar about this chief, then?”\n\n“Listen,” said Gaetano, lowering his voice, “I do not know if what they say is true”—he stopped to see if anyone was near.\n\n“What do they say?”\n\n“That this chief inhabits a cavern to which the Pitti Palace is nothing.”\n\n“What nonsense!” said Franz, reseating himself.\n\n“It is no nonsense; it is quite true. Cama, the pilot of the _Saint Ferdinand_, went in once, and he came back amazed, vowing that such treasures were only to be heard of in fairy tales.”\n\n“Do you know,” observed Franz, “that with such stories you make me think of Ali Baba’s enchanted cavern?”\n\n“I tell you what I have been told.”\n\n“Then you advise me to accept?”\n\n“Oh, I don’t say that; your excellency will do as you please; I should be sorry to advise you in the matter.”\n\nFranz pondered the matter for a few moments, concluded that a man so rich could not have any intention of plundering him of what little he had, and seeing only the prospect of a good supper, accepted. Gaetano departed with the reply. Franz was prudent, and wished to learn all he possibly could concerning his host. He turned towards the sailor, who, during this dialogue, had sat gravely plucking the partridges with the air of a man proud of his office, and asked him how these men had landed, as no vessel of any kind was visible.\n\n“Never mind that,” returned the sailor, “I know their vessel.”\n\n“Is it a very beautiful vessel?”\n\n“I would not wish for a better to sail round the world.”\n\n“Of what burden is she?”\n\n“About a hundred tons; but she is built to stand any weather. She is what the English call a yacht.”\n\n“Where was she built?”\n\n“I know not; but my own opinion is she is a Genoese.”\n\n“And how did a leader of smugglers,” continued Franz, “venture to build a vessel designed for such a purpose at Genoa?”\n\n“I did not say that the owner was a smuggler,” replied the sailor.\n\n“No; but Gaetano did, I thought.”\n\n“Gaetano had only seen the vessel from a distance, he had not then spoken to anyone.”\n\n“And if this person be not a smuggler, who is he?”\n\n“A wealthy signor, who travels for his pleasure.”\n\n“Come,” thought Franz, “he is still more mysterious, since the two accounts do not agree.”\n\n“What is his name?”\n\n“If you ask him, he says Sinbad the Sailor; but I doubt if it be his real name.”\n\n“Sinbad the Sailor?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“On the sea.”\n\n“What country does he come from?”\n\n“I do not know.”\n\n“Have you ever seen him?”\n\n“Sometimes.”\n\n“What sort of a man is he?”\n\n“Your excellency will judge for yourself.”\n\n“Where will he receive me?”\n\n“No doubt in the subterranean palace Gaetano told you of.”\n\n“Have you never had the curiosity, when you have landed and found this island deserted, to seek for this enchanted palace?”\n\n“Oh, yes, more than once, but always in vain; we examined the grotto all over, but we never could find the slightest trace of any opening; they say that the door is not opened by a key, but a magic word.”\n\n“Decidedly,” muttered Franz, “this is an Arabian Nights’ adventure.”\n\n“His excellency waits for you,” said a voice, which he recognized as that of the sentinel. He was accompanied by two of the yacht’s crew.\n\nFranz drew his handkerchief from his pocket, and presented it to the man who had spoken to him. Without uttering a word, they bandaged his eyes with a care that showed their apprehensions of his committing some indiscretion. Afterwards he was made to promise that he would not make the least attempt to raise the bandage. He promised.\n\nThen his two guides took his arms, and he went on, guided by them, and preceded by the sentinel. After going about thirty paces, he smelt the appetizing odor of the kid that was roasting, and knew thus that he was passing the bivouac; they then led him on about fifty paces farther, evidently advancing towards that part of the shore where they would not allow Gaetano to go—a refusal he could now comprehend.\n\nPresently, by a change in the atmosphere, he knew that they were entering a cave; after going on for a few seconds more he heard a crackling, and it seemed to him as though the atmosphere again changed, and became balmy and perfumed. At length his feet touched on a thick and soft carpet, and his guides let go their hold of him. There was a moment’s silence, and then a voice, in excellent French, although, with a foreign accent, said:\n\n“Welcome, sir. I beg you will remove your bandage.”\n\nIt may be supposed, then, Franz did not wait for a repetition of this permission, but took off the handkerchief, and found himself in the presence of a man from thirty-eight to forty years of age, dressed in a Tunisian costume, that is to say, a red cap with a long blue silk tassel, a vest of black cloth embroidered with gold, pantaloons of deep red, large and full gaiters of the same color, embroidered with gold like the vest, and yellow slippers; he had a splendid cashmere round his waist, and a small sharp and crooked cangiar was passed through his girdle.\n\nAlthough of a paleness that was almost livid, this man had a remarkably handsome face; his eyes were penetrating and sparkling; his nose, quite straight, and projecting direct from the brow, was of the pure Greek type, while his teeth, as white as pearls, were set off to admiration by the black moustache that encircled them.\n\nHis pallor was so peculiar, that it seemed to pertain to one who had been long entombed, and who was incapable of resuming the healthy glow and hue of life. He was not particularly tall, but extremely well made, and, like the men of the South, had small hands and feet. But what astonished Franz, who had treated Gaetano’s description as a fable, was the splendor of the apartment in which he found himself.\n\nThe entire chamber was lined with crimson brocade, worked with flowers of gold. In a recess was a kind of divan, surmounted with a stand of Arabian swords in silver scabbards, and the handles resplendent with gems; from the ceiling hung a lamp of Venetian glass, of beautiful shape and color, while the feet rested on a Turkey carpet, in which they sunk to the instep; tapestry hung before the door by which Franz had entered, and also in front of another door, leading into a second apartment which seemed to be brilliantly illuminated.\n\nThe host gave Franz time to recover from his surprise, and, moreover, returned look for look, not even taking his eyes off him.\n\n“Sir,” he said, after a pause, “a thousand excuses for the precaution taken in your introduction hither; but as, during the greater portion of the year, this island is deserted, if the secret of this abode were discovered, I should doubtless, find on my return my temporary retirement in a state of great disorder, which would be exceedingly annoying, not for the loss it occasioned me, but because I should not have the certainty I now possess of separating myself from all the rest of mankind at pleasure. Let me now endeavor to make you forget this temporary unpleasantness, and offer you what no doubt you did not expect to find here—that is to say, a tolerable supper and pretty comfortable beds.”\n\n“_Ma foi_, my dear sir,” replied Franz, “make no apologies. I have always observed that they bandage people’s eyes who penetrate enchanted palaces, for instance, those of Raoul in the _Huguenots_, and really I have nothing to complain of, for what I see makes me think of the wonders of the _Arabian Nights_.”\n20079m\n\n“Alas! I may say with Lucullus, if I could have anticipated the honor of your visit, I would have prepared for it. But such as is my hermitage, it is at your disposal; such as is my supper, it is yours to share, if you will. Ali, is the supper ready?”\n\nAt this moment the tapestry moved aside, and a Nubian, black as ebony, and dressed in a plain white tunic, made a sign to his master that all was prepared in the dining-room.\n\n“Now,” said the unknown to Franz, “I do not know if you are of my opinion, but I think nothing is more annoying than to remain two or three hours together without knowing by name or appellation how to address one another. Pray observe, that I too much respect the laws of hospitality to ask your name or title. I only request you to give me one by which I may have the pleasure of addressing you. As for myself, that I may put you at your ease, I tell you that I am generally called ‘Sinbad the Sailor.’”\n\n“And I,” replied Franz, “will tell you, as I only require his wonderful lamp to make me precisely like Aladdin, that I see no reason why at this moment I should not be called Aladdin. That will keep us from going away from the East whither I am tempted to think I have been conveyed by some good genius.”\n\n“Well, then, Signor Aladdin,” replied the singular Amphitryon, “you heard our repast announced, will you now take the trouble to enter the dining-room, your humble servant going first to show the way?”\n\nAt these words, moving aside the tapestry, Sinbad preceded his guest. Franz now looked upon another scene of enchantment; the table was splendidly covered, and once convinced of this important point he cast his eyes around him. The dining-room was scarcely less striking than the room he had just left; it was entirely of marble, with antique bas-reliefs of priceless value; and at the four corners of this apartment, which was oblong, were four magnificent statues, having baskets in their hands. These baskets contained four pyramids of most splendid fruit; there were Sicily pine-apples, pomegranates from Malaga, oranges from the Balearic Isles, peaches from France, and dates from Tunis.\n\nThe supper consisted of a roast pheasant garnished with Corsican blackbirds; a boar’s ham with jelly, a quarter of a kid with tartar sauce, a glorious turbot, and a gigantic lobster. Between these large dishes were smaller ones containing various dainties. The dishes were of silver, and the plates of Japanese china.\n\nFranz rubbed his eyes in order to assure himself that this was not a dream. Ali alone was present to wait at table, and acquitted himself so admirably, that the guest complimented his host thereupon.\n\n“Yes,” replied he, while he did the honors of the supper with much ease and grace—“yes, he is a poor devil who is much devoted to me, and does all he can to prove it. He remembers that I saved his life, and as he has a regard for his head, he feels some gratitude towards me for having kept it on his shoulders.”\n\nAli approached his master, took his hand, and kissed it.\n\n“Would it be impertinent, Signor Sinbad,” said Franz, “to ask you the particulars of this kindness?”\n\n20081m\n“Oh, they are simple enough,” replied the host. “It seems the fellow had been caught wandering nearer to the harem of the Bey of Tunis than etiquette permits to one of his color, and he was condemned by the Bey to have his tongue cut out, and his hand and head cut off; the tongue the first day, the hand the second, and the head the third. I always had a desire to have a mute in my service, so learning the day his tongue was cut out, I went to the Bey, and proposed to give him for Ali a splendid double-barreled gun, which I knew he was very desirous of having. He hesitated a moment, he was so very desirous to complete the poor devil’s punishment. But when I added to the gun an English cutlass with which I had shivered his highness’s yataghan to pieces, the Bey yielded, and agreed to forgive the hand and head, but on condition that the poor fellow never again set foot in Tunis. This was a useless clause in the bargain, for whenever the coward sees the first glimpse of the shores of Africa, he runs down below, and can only be induced to appear again when we are out of sight of that quarter of the globe.”\n\nFranz remained a moment silent and pensive, hardly knowing what to think of the half-kindness, half-cruelty, with which his host related the brief narrative.\n\n“And like the celebrated sailor whose name you have assumed,” he said, by way of changing the conversation, “you pass your life in travelling?”\n\n“Yes. I made a vow at a time when I little thought I should ever be able to accomplish it,” said the unknown with a singular smile; “and I made some others also which I hope I may fulfil in due season.”\n\nAlthough Sinbad pronounced these words with much calmness, his eyes gave forth gleams of extraordinary ferocity.\n\n“You have suffered a great deal, sir?” said Franz inquiringly.\n\nSinbad started and looked fixedly at him, as he replied, “What makes you suppose so?”\n\n“Everything,” answered Franz,—“your voice, your look, your pallid complexion, and even the life you lead.”\n\n“I?—I live the happiest life possible, the real life of a pasha. I am king of all creation. I am pleased with one place, and stay there; I get tired of it, and leave it; I am free as a bird and have wings like one; my attendants obey my slightest wish. Sometimes I amuse myself by delivering some bandit or criminal from the bonds of the law. Then I have my mode of dispensing justice, silent and sure, without respite or appeal, which condemns or pardons, and which no one sees. Ah, if you had tasted my life, you would not desire any other, and would never return to the world unless you had some great project to accomplish there.”\n\n“Revenge, for instance!” observed Franz.\n\nThe unknown fixed on the young man one of those looks which penetrate into the depth of the heart and thoughts. “And why revenge?” he asked.\n\n“Because,” replied Franz, “you seem to me like a man who, persecuted by society, has a fearful account to settle with it.”\n\n“Ah!” responded Sinbad, laughing with his singular laugh, which displayed his white and sharp teeth. “You have not guessed rightly. Such as you see me I am, a sort of philosopher, and one day perhaps I shall go to Paris to rival Monsieur Appert, and the man in the little blue cloak.”\n\n“And will that be the first time you ever took that journey?”\n\n“Yes; it will. I must seem to you by no means curious, but I assure you that it is not my fault I have delayed it so long—it will happen one day or the other.”\n20083m\n\n“And do you propose to make this journey very shortly?”\n\n“I do not know; it depends on circumstances which depend on certain arrangements.”\n\n“I should like to be there at the time you come, and I will endeavor to repay you, as far as lies in my power, for your liberal hospitality displayed to me at Monte Cristo.”\n\n“I should avail myself of your offer with pleasure,” replied the host, “but, unfortunately, if I go there, it will be, in all probability, _incognito_.”\n\nThe supper appeared to have been supplied solely for Franz, for the unknown scarcely touched one or two dishes of the splendid banquet to which his guest did ample justice. Then Ali brought on the dessert, or rather took the baskets from the hands of the statues and placed them on the table. Between the two baskets he placed a small silver cup with a silver cover. The care with which Ali placed this cup on the table roused Franz’s curiosity. He raised the cover and saw a kind of greenish paste, something like preserved angelica, but which was perfectly unknown to him. He replaced the lid, as ignorant of what the cup contained as he was before he had looked at it, and then casting his eyes towards his host he saw him smile at his disappointment.\n\n“You cannot guess,” said he, “what there is in that small vase, can you?”\n\n“No, I really cannot.”\n\n“Well, then, that green preserve is nothing less than the ambrosia which Hebe served at the table of Jupiter.”\n\n“But,” replied Franz, “this ambrosia, no doubt, in passing through mortal hands has lost its heavenly appellation and assumed a human name; in vulgar phrase, what may you term this composition, for which, to tell the truth, I do not feel any particular desire?”\n\n“Ah, thus it is that our material origin is revealed,” cried Sinbad; “we frequently pass so near to happiness without seeing, without regarding it, or if we do see and regard it, yet without recognizing it. Are you a man for the substantials, and is gold your god? taste this, and the mines of Peru, Guzerat, and Golconda are opened to you. Are you a man of imagination—a poet? taste this, and the boundaries of possibility disappear; the fields of infinite space open to you, you advance free in heart, free in mind, into the boundless realms of unfettered reverie. Are you ambitious, and do you seek after the greatnesses of the earth? taste this, and in an hour you will be a king, not a king of a petty kingdom hidden in some corner of Europe like France, Spain, or England, but king of the world, king of the universe, king of creation; without bowing at the feet of Satan, you will be king and master of all the kingdoms of the earth. Is it not tempting what I offer you, and is it not an easy thing, since it is only to do thus? look!”\n\nAt these words he uncovered the small cup which contained the substance so lauded, took a teaspoonful of the magic sweetmeat, raised it to his lips, and swallowed it slowly with his eyes half shut and his head bent backwards. Franz did not disturb him whilst he absorbed his favorite sweetmeat, but when he had finished, he inquired:\n\n“What, then, is this precious stuff?”\n\n“Did you ever hear,” he replied, “of the Old Man of the Mountain, who attempted to assassinate Philippe Auguste?”\n\n“Of course I have.”\n\n“Well, you know he reigned over a rich valley which was overhung by the mountain whence he derived his picturesque name. In this valley were magnificent gardens planted by Hassen-ben-Sabah, and in these gardens isolated pavilions. Into these pavilions he admitted the elect, and there, says Marco Polo, gave them to eat a certain herb, which transported them to Paradise, in the midst of ever-blooming shrubs, ever-ripe fruit, and ever-lovely virgins. What these happy persons took for reality was but a dream; but it was a dream so soft, so voluptuous, so enthralling, that they sold themselves body and soul to him who gave it to them, and obedient to his orders as to those of a deity, struck down the designated victim, died in torture without a murmur, believing that the death they underwent was but a quick transition to that life of delights of which the holy herb, now before you, had given them a slight foretaste.”\n\n“Then,” cried Franz, “it is hashish! I know that—by name at least.”\n\n“That is it precisely, Signor Aladdin; it is hashish—the purest and most unadulterated hashish of Alexandria,—the hashish of Abou-Gor, the celebrated maker, the only man, the man to whom there should be built a palace, inscribed with these words, _A grateful world to the dealer in happiness_.”\n\n“Do you know,” said Franz, “I have a very great inclination to judge for myself of the truth or exaggeration of your eulogies.”\n\n“Judge for yourself, Signor Aladdin—judge, but do not confine yourself to one trial. Like everything else, we must habituate the senses to a fresh impression, gentle or violent, sad or joyous. There is a struggle in nature against this divine substance,—in nature which is not made for joy and clings to pain. Nature subdued must yield in the combat, the dream must succeed to reality, and then the dream reigns supreme, then the dream becomes life, and life becomes the dream. But what changes occur! It is only by comparing the pains of actual being with the joys of the assumed existence, that you would desire to live no longer, but to dream thus forever. When you return to this mundane sphere from your visionary world, you would seem to leave a Neapolitan spring for a Lapland winter—to quit paradise for earth—heaven for hell! Taste the hashish, guest of mine—taste the hashish.”\n\nFranz’s only reply was to take a teaspoonful of the marvellous preparation, about as much in quantity as his host had eaten, and lift it to his mouth.\n\n“_Diable!_” he said, after having swallowed the divine preserve. “I do not know if the result will be as agreeable as you describe, but the thing does not appear to me as palatable as you say.”\n\n“Because your palate his not yet been attuned to the sublimity of the substances it flavors. Tell me, the first time you tasted oysters, tea, porter, truffles, and sundry other dainties which you now adore, did you like them? Could you comprehend how the Romans stuffed their pheasants with assafœtida, and the Chinese eat swallows’ nests? Eh? no! Well, it is the same with hashish; only eat for a week, and nothing in the world will seem to you to equal the delicacy of its flavor, which now appears to you flat and distasteful. Let us now go into the adjoining chamber, which is your apartment, and Ali will bring us coffee and pipes.”\n\nThey both arose, and while he who called himself Sinbad—and whom we have occasionally named so, that we might, like his guest, have some title by which to distinguish him—gave some orders to the servant, Franz entered still another apartment.\n\nIt was simply yet richly furnished. It was round, and a large divan completely encircled it. Divan, walls, ceiling, floor, were all covered with magnificent skins as soft and downy as the richest carpets; there were heavy-maned lion-skins from Atlas, striped tiger-skins from Bengal; panther-skins from the Cape, spotted beautifully, like those that appeared to Dante; bear-skins from Siberia, fox-skins from Norway, and so on; and all these skins were strewn in profusion one on the other, so that it seemed like walking over the most mossy turf, or reclining on the most luxurious bed.\n\nBoth laid themselves down on the divan; chibouques with jasmine tubes and amber mouthpieces were within reach, and all prepared so that there was no need to smoke the same pipe twice. Each of them took one, which Ali lighted and then retired to prepare the coffee.\n\nThere was a moment’s silence, during which Sinbad gave himself up to thoughts that seemed to occupy him incessantly, even in the midst of his conversation; and Franz abandoned himself to that mute reverie, into which we always sink when smoking excellent tobacco, which seems to remove with its fume all the troubles of the mind, and to give the smoker in exchange all the visions of the soul. Ali brought in the coffee.\n\n“How do you take it?” inquired the unknown; “in the French or Turkish style, strong or weak, sugar or none, cool or boiling? As you please; it is ready in all ways.”\n\n“I will take it in the Turkish style,” replied Franz.\n\n“And you are right,” said his host; “it shows you have a tendency for an Oriental life. Ah, those Orientals; they are the only men who know how to live. As for me,” he added, with one of those singular smiles which did not escape the young man, “when I have completed my affairs in Paris, I shall go and die in the East; and should you wish to see me again, you must seek me at Cairo, Bagdad, or Ispahan.”\n20087m\n\n“_Ma foi_,” said Franz, “it would be the easiest thing in the world; for I feel eagle’s wings springing out at my shoulders, and with those wings I could make a tour of the world in four-and-twenty hours.”\n\n“Ah, yes, the hashish is beginning its work. Well, unfurl your wings, and fly into superhuman regions; fear nothing, there is a watch over you; and if your wings, like those of Icarus, melt before the sun, we are here to ease your fall.”\n\nHe then said something in Arabic to Ali, who made a sign of obedience and withdrew, but not to any distance.\n\nAs to Franz a strange transformation had taken place in him. All the bodily fatigue of the day, all the preoccupation of mind which the events of the evening had brought on, disappeared as they do at the first approach of sleep, when we are still sufficiently conscious to be aware of the coming of slumber. His body seemed to acquire an airy lightness, his perception brightened in a remarkable manner, his senses seemed to redouble their power, the horizon continued to expand; but it was not the gloomy horizon of vague alarms, and which he had seen before he slept, but a blue, transparent, unbounded horizon, with all the blue of the ocean, all the spangles of the sun, all the perfumes of the summer breeze; then, in the midst of the songs of his sailors,—songs so clear and sonorous, that they would have made a divine harmony had their notes been taken down,—he saw the Island of Monte Cristo, no longer as a threatening rock in the midst of the waves, but as an oasis in the desert; then, as his boat drew nearer, the songs became louder, for an enchanting and mysterious harmony rose to heaven, as if some Loreley had decreed to attract a soul thither, or Amphion, the enchanter, intended there to build a city.\n\nAt length the boat touched the shore, but without effort, without shock, as lips touch lips; and he entered the grotto amidst continued strains of most delicious melody. He descended, or rather seemed to descend, several steps, inhaling the fresh and balmy air, like that which may be supposed to reign around the grotto of Circe, formed from such perfumes as set the mind a-dreaming, and such fires as burn the very senses; and he saw again all he had seen before his sleep, from Sinbad, his singular host, to Ali, the mute attendant; then all seemed to fade away and become confused before his eyes, like the last shadows of the magic lantern before it is extinguished, and he was again in the chamber of statues, lighted only by one of those pale and antique lamps which watch in the dead of the night over the sleep of pleasure.\n\nThey were the same statues, rich in form, in attraction, and poesy, with eyes of fascination, smiles of love, and bright and flowing hair. They were Phryne, Cleopatra, Messalina, those three celebrated courtesans. Then among them glided like a pure ray, like a Christian angel in the midst of Olympus, one of those chaste figures, those calm shadows, those soft visions, which seemed to veil its virgin brow before these marble wantons.\n\nThen the three statues advanced towards him with looks of love, and approached the couch on which he was reposing, their feet hidden in their long white tunics, their throats bare, hair flowing like waves, and assuming attitudes which the gods could not resist, but which saints withstood, and looks inflexible and ardent like those with which the serpent charms the bird; and then he gave way before looks that held him in a torturing grasp and delighted his senses as with a voluptuous kiss.\n\nIt seemed to Franz that he closed his eyes, and in a last look about him saw the vision of modesty completely veiled; and then followed a dream of passion like that promised by the Prophet to the elect. Lips of stone turned to flame, breasts of ice became like heated lava, so that to Franz, yielding for the first time to the sway of the drug, love was a sorrow and voluptuousness a torture, as burning mouths were pressed to his thirsty lips, and he was held in cool serpent-like embraces. The more he strove against this unhallowed passion the more his senses yielded to its thrall, and at length, weary of a struggle that taxed his very soul, he gave way and sank back breathless and exhausted beneath the kisses of these marble goddesses, and the enchantment of his marvellous dream.\n\n# Chapter 32. The Waking\n\nWhen Franz returned to himself, he seemed still to be in a dream. He thought himself in a sepulchre, into which a ray of sunlight in pity scarcely penetrated. He stretched forth his hand, and touched stone; he rose to his seat, and found himself lying on his bournous in a bed of dry heather, very soft and odoriferous. The vision had fled; and as if the statues had been but shadows from the tomb, they had vanished at his waking.\n\nHe advanced several paces towards the point whence the light came, and to all the excitement of his dream succeeded the calmness of reality. He found that he was in a grotto, went towards the opening, and through a kind of fanlight saw a blue sea and an azure sky. The air and water were shining in the beams of the morning sun; on the shore the sailors were sitting, chatting and laughing; and at ten yards from them the boat was at anchor, undulating gracefully on the water.\n\nThere for some time he enjoyed the fresh breeze which played on his brow, and listened to the dash of the waves on the beach, that left against the rocks a lace of foam as white as silver. He was for some time without reflection or thought for the divine charm which is in the things of nature, specially after a fantastic dream; then gradually this view of the outer world, so calm, so pure, so grand, reminded him of the illusiveness of his vision, and once more awakened memory. He recalled his arrival on the island, his presentation to a smuggler chief, a subterranean palace full of splendor, an excellent supper, and a spoonful of hashish.\n\nIt seemed, however, even in the very face of open day, that at least a year had elapsed since all these things had passed, so deep was the impression made in his mind by the dream, and so strong a hold had it taken of his imagination. Thus every now and then he saw in fancy amid the sailors, seated on a rock, or undulating in the vessel, one of the shadows which had shared his dream with looks and kisses. Otherwise, his head was perfectly clear, and his body refreshed; he was free from the slightest headache; on the contrary, he felt a certain degree of lightness, a faculty for absorbing the pure air, and enjoying the bright sunshine more vividly than ever.\n\nHe went gayly up to the sailors, who rose as soon as they perceived him; and the patron, accosting him, said:\n\n“The Signor Sinbad has left his compliments for your excellency, and desires us to express the regret he feels at not being able to take his leave in person; but he trusts you will excuse him, as very important business calls him to Malaga.”\n\n“So, then, Gaetano,” said Franz, “this is, then, all reality; there exists a man who has received me in this island, entertained me right royally, and has departed while I was asleep?”\n\n“He exists as certainly as that you may see his small yacht with all her sails spread; and if you will use your glass, you will, in all probability, recognize your host in the midst of his crew.”\n\nSo saying, Gaetano pointed in a direction in which a small vessel was making sail towards the southern point of Corsica. Franz adjusted his telescope, and directed it towards the yacht. Gaetano was not mistaken. At the stern the mysterious stranger was standing up looking towards the shore, and holding a spy-glass in his hand. He was attired as he had been on the previous evening, and waved his pocket-handkerchief to his guest in token of adieu. Franz returned the salute by shaking his handkerchief as an exchange of signals. After a second, a slight cloud of smoke was seen at the stern of the vessel, which rose gracefully as it expanded in the air, and then Franz heard a slight report.\n\n“There, do you hear?” observed Gaetano; “he is bidding you adieu.”\n\nThe young man took his carbine and fired it in the air, but without any idea that the noise could be heard at the distance which separated the yacht from the shore.\n\n“What are your excellency’s orders?” inquired Gaetano.\n\n“In the first place, light me a torch.”\n\n“Ah, yes, I understand,” replied the patron, “to find the entrance to the enchanted apartment. With much pleasure, your excellency, if it would amuse you; and I will get you the torch you ask for. But I too have had the idea you have, and two or three times the same fancy has come over me; but I have always given it up. Giovanni, light a torch,” he added, “and give it to his excellency.”\n\nGiovanni obeyed. Franz took the lamp, and entered the subterranean grotto, followed by Gaetano. He recognized the place where he had awaked by the bed of heather that was there; but it was in vain that he carried his torch all round the exterior surface of the grotto. He saw nothing, unless that, by traces of smoke, others had before him attempted the same thing, and, like him, in vain. Yet he did not leave a foot of this granite wall, as impenetrable as futurity, without strict scrutiny; he did not see a fissure without introducing the blade of his hunting sword into it, or a projecting point on which he did not lean and press in the hopes it would give way. All was vain; and he lost two hours in his attempts, which were at last utterly useless. At the end of this time he gave up his search, and Gaetano smiled.\n\nWhen Franz appeared again on the shore, the yacht only seemed like a small white speck on the horizon. He looked again through his glass, but even then he could not distinguish anything.\n\nGaetano reminded him that he had come for the purpose of shooting goats, which he had utterly forgotten. He took his fowling-piece, and began to hunt over the island with the air of a man who is fulfilling a duty, rather than enjoying a pleasure; and at the end of a quarter of an hour he had killed a goat and two kids. These animals, though wild and agile as chamois, were too much like domestic goats, and Franz could not consider them as game. Moreover, other ideas, much more enthralling, occupied his mind. Since, the evening before, he had really been the hero of one of the tales of the _Thousand and One Nights_, and he was irresistibly attracted towards the grotto.\n\nThen, in spite of the failure of his first search, he began a second, after having told Gaetano to roast one of the two kids. The second visit was a long one, and when he returned the kid was roasted and the repast ready. Franz was sitting on the spot where he was on the previous evening when his mysterious host had invited him to supper; and he saw the little yacht, now like a sea-gull on the wave, continuing her flight towards Corsica.\n\n“Why,” he remarked to Gaetano, “you told me that Signor Sinbad was going to Malaga, while it seems he is in the direction of Porto-Vecchio.”\n\n“Don’t you remember,” said the patron, “I told you that among the crew there were two Corsican brigands?”\n\n“True; and he is going to land them,” added Franz.\n\n“Precisely so,” replied Gaetano. “Ah, he is one who fears neither God nor Satan, they say, and would at any time run fifty leagues out of his course to do a poor devil a service.”\n\n20093m\n“But such services as these might involve him with the authorities of the country in which he practices this kind of philanthropy,” said Franz.\n\n“And what cares he for that,” replied Gaetano with a laugh, “or any authorities? He smiles at them. Let them try to pursue him! Why, in the first place, his yacht is not a ship, but a bird, and he would beat any frigate three knots in every nine; and if he were to throw himself on the coast, why, is he not certain of finding friends everywhere?”\n\nIt was perfectly clear that the Signor Sinbad, Franz’s host, had the honor of being on excellent terms with the smugglers and bandits along the whole coast of the Mediterranean, and so enjoyed exceptional privileges. As to Franz, he had no longer any inducement to remain at Monte Cristo. He had lost all hope of detecting the secret of the grotto; he consequently despatched his breakfast, and, his boat being ready, he hastened on board, and they were soon under way. At the moment the boat began her course they lost sight of the yacht, as it disappeared in the gulf of Porto-Vecchio. With it was effaced the last trace of the preceding night; and then supper, Sinbad, hashish, statues,—all became a dream for Franz.\n\nThe boat sailed on all day and all night, and next morning, when the sun rose, they had lost sight of Monte Cristo.\n\nWhen Franz had once again set foot on shore, he forgot, for the moment at least, the events which had just passed, while he finished his affairs of pleasure at Florence, and then thought of nothing but how he should rejoin his companion, who was awaiting him at Rome.\n\nHe set out, and on the Saturday evening reached the Place de la Douane by the mail-coach. An apartment, as we have said, had been retained beforehand, and thus he had but to go to Signor Pastrini’s hotel. But this was not so easy a matter, for the streets were thronged with people, and Rome was already a prey to that low and feverish murmur which precedes all great events; and at Rome there are four great events in every year,—the Carnival, Holy Week, Corpus Christi, and the Feast of St. Peter.\n\nAll the rest of the year the city is in that state of dull apathy, between life and death, which renders it similar to a kind of station between this world and the next—a sublime spot, a resting-place full of poetry and character, and at which Franz had already halted five or six times, and at each time found it more marvellous and striking.\n\nAt last he made his way through the mob, which was continually increasing and getting more and more turbulent, and reached the hotel. On his first inquiry he was told, with the impertinence peculiar to hired hackney-coachmen and innkeepers with their houses full, that there was no room for him at the Hôtel de Londres. Then he sent his card to Signor Pastrini, and asked for Albert de Morcerf. This plan succeeded; and Signor Pastrini himself ran to him, excusing himself for having made his excellency wait, scolding the waiters, taking the candlestick from the porter, who was ready to pounce on the traveller and was about to lead him to Albert, when Morcerf himself appeared.\n\nThe apartment consisted of two small rooms and a parlor. The two rooms looked on to the street—a fact which Signor Pastrini commented upon as an inappreciable advantage. The rest of the floor was hired by a very rich gentleman who was supposed to be a Sicilian or Maltese; but the host was unable to decide to which of the two nations the traveller belonged.\n\n“Very good, signor Pastrini,” said Franz; “but we must have some supper instantly, and a carriage for tomorrow and the following days.”\n\n“As to supper,” replied the landlord, “you shall be served immediately; but as for the carriage——”\n\n“What as to the carriage?” exclaimed Albert. “Come, come, Signor Pastrini, no joking; we must have a carriage.”\n\n“Sir,” replied the host, “we will do all in our power to procure you one—this is all I can say.”\n\n“And when shall we know?” inquired Franz.\n\n“Tomorrow morning,” answered the innkeeper.\n\n“Oh, the deuce! then we shall pay the more, that’s all, I see plainly enough. At Drake’s or Aaron’s one pays twenty-five lire for common days, and thirty or thirty-five lire a day more for Sundays and feast days; add five lire a day more for extras, that will make forty, and there’s an end of it.”\n\n“I am afraid if we offer them double that we shall not procure a carriage.”\n\n“Then they must put horses to mine. It is a little worse for the journey, but that’s no matter.”\n\n“There are no horses.”\n\nAlbert looked at Franz like a man who hears a reply he does not understand.\n\n“Do you understand that, my dear Franz—no horses?” he said, “but can’t we have post-horses?”\n\n“They have been all hired this fortnight, and there are none left but those absolutely requisite for posting.”\n\n“What are we to say to this?” asked Franz.\n\n“I say, that when a thing completely surpasses my comprehension, I am accustomed not to dwell on that thing, but to pass to another. Is supper ready, Signor Pastrini?”\n\n“Yes, your excellency.”\n\n“Well, then, let us sup.”\n\n“But the carriage and horses?” said Franz.\n\n“Be easy, my dear boy; they will come in due season; it is only a question of how much shall be charged for them.” Morcerf then, with that delighted philosophy which believes that nothing is impossible to a full purse or well-lined pocketbook, supped, went to bed, slept soundly, and dreamed he was racing all over Rome at Carnival time in a coach with six horses.\n\n# Chapter 33. Roman Bandits\n\nThe next morning Franz woke first, and instantly rang the bell. The sound had not yet died away when Signor Pastrini himself entered.\n\n“Well, excellency,” said the landlord triumphantly, and without waiting for Franz to question him, “I feared yesterday, when I would not promise you anything, that you were too late—there is not a single carriage to be had—that is, for the three last days”\n\n“Yes,” returned Franz, “for the very three days it is most needed.”\n\n“What is the matter?” said Albert, entering; “no carriage to be had?”\n\n“Just so,” returned Franz, “you have guessed it.”\n\n“Well, your Eternal City is a nice sort of place.”\n\n“That is to say, excellency,” replied Pastrini, who was desirous of keeping up the dignity of the capital of the Christian world in the eyes of his guest, “that there are no carriages to be had from Sunday to Tuesday evening, but from now till Sunday you can have fifty if you please.”\n\n“Ah, that is something,” said Albert; “today is Thursday, and who knows what may arrive between this and Sunday?”\n\n“Ten or twelve thousand travellers will arrive,” replied Franz, “which will make it still more difficult.”\n\n“My friend,” said Morcerf, “let us enjoy the present without gloomy forebodings for the future.”\n\n“At least we can have a window?”\n\n“Where?”\n\n“In the Corso.”\n\n“Ah, a window!” exclaimed Signor Pastrini,—“utterly impossible; there was only one left on the fifth floor of the Doria Palace, and that has been let to a Russian prince for twenty sequins a day.”\n\nThe two young men looked at each other with an air of stupefaction.\n\n“Well,” said Franz to Albert, “do you know what is the best thing we can do? It is to pass the Carnival at Venice; there we are sure of obtaining gondolas if we cannot have carriages.”\n\n“Ah, the devil, no,” cried Albert; “I came to Rome to see the Carnival, and I will, though I see it on stilts.”\n\n“Bravo! an excellent idea. We will disguise ourselves as monster pulchinellos or shepherds of the Landes, and we shall have complete success.”\n\n“Do your excellencies still wish for a carriage from now to Sunday morning?”\n\n“_Parbleu!_” said Albert, “do you think we are going to run about on foot in the streets of Rome, like lawyers’ clerks?”\n\n“I hasten to comply with your excellencies’ wishes; only, I tell you beforehand, the carriage will cost you six piastres a day.”\n\n“And, as I am not a millionaire, like the gentleman in the next apartments,” said Franz, “I warn you, that as I have been four times before at Rome, I know the prices of all the carriages; we will give you twelve piastres for today, tomorrow, and the day after, and then you will make a good profit.”\n\n“But, excellency”—said Pastrini, still striving to gain his point.\n\n“Now go,” returned Franz, “or I shall go myself and bargain with your _affettatore_, who is mine also; he is an old friend of mine, who has plundered me pretty well already, and, in the hope of making more out of me, he will take a less price than the one I offer you; you will lose the preference, and that will be your fault.”\n\n“Do not give yourselves the trouble, excellency,” returned Signor Pastrini, with the smile peculiar to the Italian speculator when he confesses defeat; “I will do all I can, and I hope you will be satisfied.”\n\n“And now we understand each other.”\n\n“When do you wish the carriage to be here?”\n\n“In an hour.”\n\n“In an hour it will be at the door.”\n\nAn hour after the vehicle was at the door; it was a hack conveyance which was elevated to the rank of a private carriage in honor of the occasion, but, in spite of its humble exterior, the young men would have thought themselves happy to have secured it for the last three days of the Carnival.\n\n“Excellency,” cried the _cicerone_, seeing Franz approach the window, “shall I bring the carriage nearer to the palace?”\n\nAccustomed as Franz was to the Italian phraseology, his first impulse was to look round him, but these words were addressed to him. Franz was the “excellency,” the vehicle was the “carriage,” and the Hôtel de Londres was the “palace.” The genius for laudation characteristic of the race was in that phrase.\n\nFranz and Albert descended, the carriage approached the palace; their excellencies stretched their legs along the seats; the _cicerone_ sprang into the seat behind.\n\n“Where do your excellencies wish to go?” asked he.\n\n“To Saint Peter’s first, and then to the Colosseum,” returned Albert. But Albert did not know that it takes a day to see Saint Peter’s, and a month to study it. The day was passed at Saint Peter’s alone.\n\nSuddenly the daylight began to fade away; Franz took out his watch—it was half-past four. They returned to the hotel; at the door Franz ordered the coachman to be ready at eight. He wished to show Albert the Colosseum by moonlight, as he had shown him Saint Peter’s by daylight. When we show a friend a city one has already visited, we feel the same pride as when we point out a woman whose lover we have been.\n\nHe was to leave the city by the Porta del Popolo, skirt the outer wall, and re-enter by the Porta San Giovanni; thus they would behold the Colosseum without finding their impressions dulled by first looking on the Capitol, the Forum, the Arch of Septimus Severus, the Temple of Antoninus and Faustina, and the Via Sacra.\n\nThey sat down to dinner. Signor Pastrini had promised them a banquet; he gave them a tolerable repast. At the end of the dinner he entered in person. Franz thought that he came to hear his dinner praised, and began accordingly, but at the first words he was interrupted.\n\n“Excellency,” said Pastrini, “I am delighted to have your approbation, but it was not for that I came.”\n\n“Did you come to tell us you have procured a carriage?” asked Albert, lighting his cigar.\n\n“No; and your excellencies will do well not to think of that any longer; at Rome things can or cannot be done; when you are told anything cannot be done, there is an end of it.”\n\n“It is much more convenient at Paris,—when anything cannot be done, you pay double, and it is done directly.”\n\n“That is what all the French say,” returned Signor Pastrini, somewhat piqued; “for that reason, I do not understand why they travel.”\n\n“But,” said Albert, emitting a volume of smoke and balancing his chair on its hind legs, “only madmen, or blockheads like us, ever do travel. Men in their senses do not quit their hotel in the Rue du Helder, their walk on the Boulevard de Gand, and the Café de Paris.”\n\nIt is of course understood that Albert resided in the aforesaid street, appeared every day on the fashionable walk, and dined frequently at the only restaurant where you can really dine, that is, if you are on good terms with its waiters.\n\nSignor Pastrini remained silent a short time; it was evident that he was musing over this answer, which did not seem very clear.\n\n“But,” said Franz, in his turn interrupting his host’s meditations, “you had some motive for coming here, may I beg to know what it was?”\n20099m\n\n“Ah, yes; you have ordered your carriage at eight o’clock precisely?”\n\n“I have.”\n\n“You intend visiting _Il Colosseo_.”\n\n“You mean the Colosseum?”\n\n“It is the same thing. You have told your coachman to leave the city by the Porta del Popolo, to drive round the walls, and re-enter by the Porta San Giovanni?”\n\n“These are my words exactly.”\n\n“Well, this route is impossible.”\n\n“Impossible!”\n\n“Very dangerous, to say the least.”\n\n“Dangerous!—and why?”\n\n“On account of the famous Luigi Vampa.”\n\n“Pray, who may this famous Luigi Vampa be?” inquired Albert; “he may be very famous at Rome, but I can assure you he is quite unknown at Paris.”\n\n“What! do you not know him?”\n\n“I have not that honor.”\n\n“You have never heard his name?”\n\n“Never.”\n\n“Well, then, he is a bandit, compared to whom the Decesaris and the Gasparones were mere children.”\n\n“Now then, Albert,” cried Franz, “here is a bandit for you at last.”\n\n“I forewarn you, Signor Pastrini, that I shall not believe one word of what you are going to tell us; having told you this, begin. ‘Once upon a time——’ Well, go on.”\n\nSignor Pastrini turned toward Franz, who seemed to him the more reasonable of the two; we must do him justice,—he had had a great many Frenchmen in his house, but had never been able to comprehend them.\n\n“Excellency,” said he gravely, addressing Franz, “if you look upon me as a liar, it is useless for me to say anything; it was for your interest I——”\n\n“Albert does not say you are a liar, Signor Pastrini,” said Franz, “but that he will not believe what you are going to tell us,—but I will believe all you say; so proceed.”\n\n“But if your excellency doubt my veracity——”\n\n“Signor Pastrini,” returned Franz, “you are more susceptible than Cassandra, who was a prophetess, and yet no one believed her; while you, at least, are sure of the credence of half your audience. Come, sit down, and tell us all about this Signor Vampa.”\n\n“I had told your excellency he is the most famous bandit we have had since the days of Mastrilla.”\n\n“Well, what has this bandit to do with the order I have given the coachman to leave the city by the Porta del Popolo, and to re-enter by the Porta San Giovanni?”\n\n20101m\n“This,” replied Signor Pastrini, “that you will go out by one, but I very much doubt your returning by the other.”\n\n“Why?” asked Franz.\n\n“Because, after nightfall, you are not safe fifty yards from the gates.”\n\n“On your honor, is that true?” cried Albert.\n\n“Count,” returned Signor Pastrini, hurt at Albert’s repeated doubts of the truth of his assertions, “I do not say this to you, but to your companion, who knows Rome, and knows, too, that these things are not to be laughed at.”\n\n“My dear fellow,” said Albert, turning to Franz, “here is an admirable adventure; we will fill our carriage with pistols, blunderbusses, and double-barrelled guns. Luigi Vampa comes to take us, and we take him—we bring him back to Rome, and present him to his holiness the Pope, who asks how he can repay so great a service; then we merely ask for a carriage and a pair of horses, and we see the Carnival in the carriage, and doubtless the Roman people will crown us at the Capitol, and proclaim us, like Curtius and Horatius Cocles, the preservers of their country.”\n\nWhilst Albert proposed this scheme, Signor Pastrini’s face assumed an expression impossible to describe.\n\n“And pray,” asked Franz, “where are these pistols, blunderbusses, and other deadly weapons with which you intend filling the carriage?”\n\n“Not out of my armory, for at Terracina I was plundered even of my hunting-knife. And you?”\n\n“I shared the same fate at Aquapendente.”\n\n“Do you know, Signor Pastrini,” said Albert, lighting a second cigar at the first, “that this practice is very convenient for bandits, and that it seems to be due to an arrangement of their own.”\n\nDoubtless Signor Pastrini found this pleasantry compromising, for he only answered half the question, and then he spoke to Franz, as the only one likely to listen with attention. “Your excellency knows that it is not customary to defend yourself when attacked by bandits.”\n\n“What!” cried Albert, whose courage revolted at the idea of being plundered tamely, “not make any resistance!”\n\n“No, for it would be useless. What could you do against a dozen bandits who spring out of some pit, ruin, or aqueduct, and level their pieces at you?”\n\n“Eh, _parbleu!_—they should kill me.”\n\nThe innkeeper turned to Franz with an air that seemed to say, “Your friend is decidedly mad.”\n\n“My dear Albert,” returned Franz, “your answer is sublime, and worthy the ‘_Let him die_,’ of Corneille, only, when Horace made that answer, the safety of Rome was concerned; but, as for us, it is only to gratify a whim, and it would be ridiculous to risk our lives for so foolish a motive.”\n\nAlbert poured himself out a glass of _lacryma Christi_, which he sipped at intervals, muttering some unintelligible words.\n\n“Well, Signor Pastrini,” said Franz, “now that my companion is quieted, and you have seen how peaceful my intentions are, tell me who is this Luigi Vampa. Is he a shepherd or a nobleman?—young or old?—tall or short? Describe him, in order that, if we meet him by chance, like Jean Sbogar or Lara, we may recognize him.”\n\n“You could not apply to anyone better able to inform you on all these points, for I knew him when he was a child, and one day that I fell into his hands, going from Ferentino to Alatri, he, fortunately for me, recollected me, and set me free, not only without ransom, but made me a present of a very splendid watch, and related his history to me.”\n\n“Let us see the watch,” said Albert.\n\nSignor Pastrini drew from his fob a magnificent Bréguet, bearing the name of its maker, of Parisian manufacture, and a count’s coronet.\n\n“Here it is,” said he.\n\n“_Peste!_” returned Albert, “I compliment you on it; I have its fellow”—he took his watch from his waistcoat pocket—“and it cost me 3,000 francs.”\n\n“Let us hear the history,” said Franz, motioning Signor Pastrini to seat himself.\n\n“Your excellencies permit it?” asked the host.\n\n“_Pardieu!_” cried Albert, “you are not a preacher, to remain standing!”\n\nThe host sat down, after having made each of them a respectful bow, which meant that he was ready to tell them all they wished to know concerning Luigi Vampa.\n\n“You tell me,” said Franz, at the moment Signor Pastrini was about to open his mouth, “that you knew Luigi Vampa when he was a child—he is still a young man, then?”\n\n“A young man? he is only two-and-twenty;—he will gain himself a reputation.”\n\n“What do you think of that, Albert?—at two-and-twenty to be thus famous?”\n\n“Yes, and at his age, Alexander, Cæsar, and Napoleon, who have all made some noise in the world, were quite behind him.”\n\n“So,” continued Franz, “the hero of this history is only two-and-twenty?”\n\n“Scarcely so much.”\n\n“Is he tall or short?”\n\n“Of the middle height—about the same stature as his excellency,” returned the host, pointing to Albert.\n\n“Thanks for the comparison,” said Albert, with a bow.\n\n“Go on, Signor Pastrini,” continued Franz, smiling at his friend’s susceptibility. “To what class of society does he belong?”\n\n“He was a shepherd-boy attached to the farm of the Count of San-Felice, situated between Palestrina and the Lake of Gabri; he was born at Pampinara, and entered the count’s service when he was five years old; his father was also a shepherd, who owned a small flock, and lived by the wool and the milk, which he sold at Rome. When quite a child, the little Vampa displayed a most extraordinary precocity. One day, when he was seven years old, he came to the curate of Palestrina, and asked to be taught to read; it was somewhat difficult, for he could not quit his flock; but the good curate went every day to say mass at a little hamlet too poor to pay a priest and which, having no other name, was called Borgo; he told Luigi that he might meet him on his return, and that then he would give him a lesson, warning him that it would be short, and that he must profit as much as possible by it. The child accepted joyfully. Every day Luigi led his flock to graze on the road that leads from Palestrina to Borgo; every day, at nine o’clock in the morning, the priest and the boy sat down on a bank by the wayside, and the little shepherd took his lesson out of the priest’s breviary. At the end of three months he had learned to read. This was not enough—he must now learn to write. The priest had a writing teacher at Rome make three alphabets—one large, one middling, and one small; and pointed out to him that by the help of a sharp instrument he could trace the letters on a slate, and thus learn to write. The same evening, when the flock was safe at the farm, the little Luigi hastened to the smith at Palestrina, took a large nail, heated and sharpened it, and formed a sort of stylus. The next morning he gathered an armful of pieces of slate and began. At the end of three months he had learned to write. The curate, astonished at his quickness and intelligence, made him a present of pens, paper, and a penknife. This demanded new effort, but nothing compared to the first; at the end of a week he wrote as well with this pen as with the stylus. The curate related the incident to the Count of San-Felice, who sent for the little shepherd, made him read and write before him, ordered his attendant to let him eat with the domestics, and to give him two piastres a month. With this, Luigi purchased books and pencils. He applied his imitative powers to everything, and, like Giotto, when young, he drew on his slate sheep, houses, and trees. Then, with his knife, he began to carve all sorts of objects in wood; it was thus that Pinelli, the famous sculptor, had commenced.\n20105m\n\n“A girl of six or seven—that is, a little younger than Vampa—tended sheep on a farm near Palestrina; she was an orphan, born at Valmontone and was named Teresa. The two children met, sat down near each other, let their flocks mingle together, played, laughed, and conversed together; in the evening they separated the Count of San-Felice’s flock from those of Baron Cervetri, and the children returned to their respective farms, promising to meet the next morning. The next day they kept their word, and thus they grew up together. Vampa was twelve, and Teresa eleven. And yet their natural disposition revealed itself. Beside his taste for the fine arts, which Luigi had carried as far as he could in his solitude, he was given to alternating fits of sadness and enthusiasm, was often angry and capricious, and always sarcastic. None of the lads of Pampinara, Palestrina, or Valmontone had been able to gain any influence over him or even to become his companion. His disposition (always inclined to exact concessions rather than to make them) kept him aloof from all friendships. Teresa alone ruled by a look, a word, a gesture, this impetuous character, which yielded beneath the hand of a woman, and which beneath the hand of a man might have broken, but could never have been bended. Teresa was lively and gay, but coquettish to excess. The two piastres that Luigi received every month from the Count of San-Felice’s steward, and the price of all the little carvings in wood he sold at Rome, were expended in ear-rings, necklaces, and gold hairpins. So that, thanks to her friend’s generosity, Teresa was the most beautiful and the best-attired peasant near Rome.\n\n“The two children grew up together, passing all their time with each other, and giving themselves up to the wild ideas of their different characters. Thus, in all their dreams, their wishes, and their conversations, Vampa saw himself the captain of a vessel, general of an army, or governor of a province. Teresa saw herself rich, superbly attired, and attended by a train of liveried domestics. Then, when they had thus passed the day in building castles in the air, they separated their flocks, and descended from the elevation of their dreams to the reality of their humble position.\n\n“One day the young shepherd told the count’s steward that he had seen a wolf come out of the Sabine mountains, and prowl around his flock. The steward gave him a gun; this was what Vampa longed for. This gun had an excellent barrel, made at Brescia, and carrying a ball with the precision of an English rifle; but one day the count broke the stock, and had then cast the gun aside. This, however, was nothing to a sculptor like Vampa; he examined the broken stock, calculated what change it would require to adapt the gun to his shoulder, and made a fresh stock, so beautifully carved that it would have fetched fifteen or twenty piastres, had he chosen to sell it. But nothing could be farther from his thoughts.\n\n“For a long time a gun had been the young man’s greatest ambition. In every country where independence has taken the place of liberty, the first desire of a manly heart is to possess a weapon, which at once renders him capable of defence or attack, and, by rendering its owner terrible, often makes him feared. From this moment Vampa devoted all his leisure time to perfecting himself in the use of his precious weapon; he purchased powder and ball, and everything served him for a mark—the trunk of some old and moss-grown olive-tree, that grew on the Sabine mountains; the fox, as he quitted his earth on some marauding excursion; the eagle that soared above their heads: and thus he soon became so expert, that Teresa overcame the terror she at first felt at the report, and amused herself by watching him direct the ball wherever he pleased, with as much accuracy as if he placed it by hand.\n\n20107m\n“One evening a wolf emerged from a pine-wood near which they were usually stationed, but the wolf had scarcely advanced ten yards ere he was dead. Proud of this exploit, Vampa took the dead animal on his shoulders, and carried him to the farm. These exploits had gained Luigi considerable reputation. The man of superior abilities always finds admirers, go where he will. He was spoken of as the most adroit, the strongest, and the most courageous _contadino_ for ten leagues around; and although Teresa was universally allowed to be the most beautiful girl of the Sabines, no one had ever spoken to her of love, because it was known that she was beloved by Vampa. And yet the two young people had never declared their affection; they had grown together like two trees whose roots are mingled, whose branches intertwined, and whose intermingled perfume rises to the heavens. Only their wish to see each other had become a necessity, and they would have preferred death to a day’s separation.\n\n“Teresa was sixteen, and Vampa seventeen. About this time, a band of brigands that had established itself in the Lepini mountains began to be much spoken of. The brigands have never been really extirpated from the neighborhood of Rome. Sometimes a chief is wanted, but when a chief presents himself he rarely has to wait long for a band of followers.\n\n“The celebrated Cucumetto, pursued in the Abruzzo, driven out of the kingdom of Naples, where he had carried on a regular war, had crossed the Garigliano, like Manfred, and had taken refuge on the banks of the Amasine between Sonnino and Juperno. He strove to collect a band of followers, and followed the footsteps of Decesaris and Gasparone, whom he hoped to surpass. Many young men of Palestrina, Frascati, and Pampinara had disappeared. Their disappearance at first caused much disquietude; but it was soon known that they had joined Cucumetto. After some time Cucumetto became the object of universal attention; the most extraordinary traits of ferocious daring and brutality were related of him.\n\n“One day he carried off a young girl, the daughter of a surveyor of Frosinone. The bandit’s laws are positive; a young girl belongs first to him who carries her off, then the rest draw lots for her, and she is abandoned to their brutality until death relieves her sufferings. When their parents are sufficiently rich to pay a ransom, a messenger is sent to negotiate; the prisoner is hostage for the security of the messenger; should the ransom be refused, the prisoner is irrevocably lost. The young girl’s lover was in Cucumetto’s troop; his name was Carlini. When she recognized her lover, the poor girl extended her arms to him, and believed herself safe; but Carlini felt his heart sink, for he but too well knew the fate that awaited her. However, as he was a favorite with Cucumetto, as he had for three years faithfully served him, and as he had saved his life by shooting a dragoon who was about to cut him down, he hoped the chief would have pity on him. He took Cucumetto one side, while the young girl, seated at the foot of a huge pine that stood in the centre of the forest, made a veil of her picturesque head-dress to hide her face from the lascivious gaze of the bandits. There he told the chief all—his affection for the prisoner, their promises of mutual fidelity, and how every night, since he had been near, they had met in some neighboring ruins.\n20109m\n\n“It so happened that night that Cucumetto had sent Carlini to a village, so that he had been unable to go to the place of meeting. Cucumetto had been there, however, by accident, as he said, and had carried the maiden off. Carlini besought his chief to make an exception in Rita’s favor, as her father was rich, and could pay a large ransom. Cucumetto seemed to yield to his friend’s entreaties, and bade him find a shepherd to send to Rita’s father at Frosinone.\n\n“Carlini flew joyfully to Rita, telling her she was saved, and bidding her write to her father, to inform him what had occurred, and that her ransom was fixed at three hundred piastres. Twelve hours’ delay was all that was granted—that is, until nine the next morning. The instant the letter was written, Carlini seized it, and hastened to the plain to find a messenger. He found a young shepherd watching his flock. The natural messengers of the bandits are the shepherds who live between the city and the mountains, between civilized and savage life. The boy undertook the commission, promising to be in Frosinone in less than an hour. Carlini returned, anxious to see his mistress, and announce the joyful intelligence. He found the troop in the glade, supping off the provisions exacted as contributions from the peasants; but his eye vainly sought Rita and Cucumetto among them.\n\n“He inquired where they were, and was answered by a burst of laughter. A cold perspiration burst from every pore, and his hair stood on end. He repeated his question. One of the bandits rose, and offered him a glass filled with Orvietto, saying, ‘To the health of the brave Cucumetto and the fair Rita.’ At this moment Carlini heard a woman’s cry; he divined the truth, seized the glass, broke it across the face of him who presented it, and rushed towards the spot whence the cry came. After a hundred yards he turned the corner of the thicket; he found Rita senseless in the arms of Cucumetto. At the sight of Carlini, Cucumetto rose, a pistol in each hand. The two brigands looked at each other for a moment—the one with a smile of lasciviousness on his lips, the other with the pallor of death on his brow. A terrible battle between the two men seemed imminent; but by degrees Carlini’s features relaxed, his hand, which had grasped one of the pistols in his belt, fell to his side. Rita lay between them. The moon lighted the group.\n\n“‘Well,’ said Cucumetto, ‘have you executed your commission?’\n\n“‘Yes, captain,’ returned Carlini. ‘At nine o’clock tomorrow Rita’s father will be here with the money.’\n\n“‘It is well; in the meantime, we will have a merry night; this young girl is charming, and does credit to your taste. Now, as I am not egotistical, we will return to our comrades and draw lots for her.’\n\n“‘You have determined, then, to abandon her to the common law?’ said Carlini.\n\n“‘Why should an exception be made in her favor?’\n\n“‘I thought that my entreaties——’\n\n“‘What right have you, any more than the rest, to ask for an exception?’\n\n“‘It is true.’\n\n“‘But never mind,’ continued Cucumetto, laughing, ‘sooner or later your turn will come.’ Carlini’s teeth clenched convulsively.\n\n“‘Now, then,’ said Cucumetto, advancing towards the other bandits, ‘are you coming?’\n\n“‘I follow you.’\n\n20111m\n“Cucumetto departed, without losing sight of Carlini, for, doubtless, he feared lest he should strike him unawares; but nothing betrayed a hostile design on Carlini’s part. He was standing, his arms folded, near Rita, who was still insensible. Cucumetto fancied for a moment the young man was about to take her in his arms and fly; but this mattered little to him now Rita had been his; and as for the money, three hundred piastres distributed among the band was so small a sum that he cared little about it. He continued to follow the path to the glade; but, to his great surprise, Carlini arrived almost as soon as himself.\n\n“‘Let us draw lots! let us draw lots!’ cried all the brigands, when they saw the chief.\n\n“Their demand was fair, and the chief inclined his head in sign of acquiescence. The eyes of all shone fiercely as they made their demand, and the red light of the fire made them look like demons. The names of all, including Carlini, were placed in a hat, and the youngest of the band drew forth a ticket; the ticket bore the name of Diavolaccio. He was the man who had proposed to Carlini the health of their chief, and to whom Carlini replied by breaking the glass across his face. A large wound, extending from the temple to the mouth, was bleeding profusely. Diavolaccio, seeing himself thus favored by fortune, burst into a loud laugh.\n\n“‘Captain,’ said he, ‘just now Carlini would not drink your health when I proposed it to him; propose mine to him, and let us see if he will be more condescending to you than to me.’\n\n“Everyone expected an explosion on Carlini’s part; but to their great surprise, he took a glass in one hand and a flask in the other, and filling it,—\n\n“‘Your health, Diavolaccio,’ said he calmly, and he drank it off, without his hand trembling in the least. Then sitting down by the fire, ‘My supper,’ said he; ‘my expedition has given me an appetite.’\n\n“‘Well done, Carlini!’ cried the brigands; ‘that is acting like a good fellow;’ and they all formed a circle round the fire, while Diavolaccio disappeared.\n\n“Carlini ate and drank as if nothing had happened. The bandits looked on with astonishment at this singular conduct until they heard footsteps. They turned round, and saw Diavolaccio bearing the young girl in his arms. Her head hung back, and her long hair swept the ground. As they entered the circle, the bandits could perceive, by the firelight, the unearthly pallor of the young girl and of Diavolaccio. This apparition was so strange and so solemn, that everyone rose, with the exception of Carlini, who remained seated, and ate and drank calmly. Diavolaccio advanced amidst the most profound silence, and laid Rita at the captain’s feet. Then everyone could understand the cause of the unearthly pallor in the young girl and the bandit. A knife was plunged up to the hilt in Rita’s left breast. Everyone looked at Carlini; the sheath at his belt was empty.\n\n“‘Ah, ah,’ said the chief, ‘I now understand why Carlini stayed behind.’\n\n“All savage natures appreciate a desperate deed. No other of the bandits would, perhaps, have done the same; but they all understood what Carlini had done.\n\n“‘Now, then,’ cried Carlini, rising in his turn, and approaching the corpse, his hand on the butt of one of his pistols, ‘does anyone dispute the possession of this woman with me?’\n\n“‘No,’ returned the chief, ‘she is thine.’\n\n“Carlini raised her in his arms, and carried her out of the circle of firelight. Cucumetto placed his sentinels for the night, and the bandits wrapped themselves in their cloaks, and lay down before the fire. At midnight the sentinel gave the alarm, and in an instant all were on the alert. It was Rita’s father, who brought his daughter’s ransom in person.\n\n“‘Here,’ said he, to Cucumetto, ‘here are three hundred piastres; give me back my child.\n\n“But the chief, without taking the money, made a sign to him to follow. The old man obeyed. They both advanced beneath the trees, through whose branches streamed the moonlight. Cucumetto stopped at last, and pointed to two persons grouped at the foot of a tree.\n\n“‘There,’ said he, ‘demand thy child of Carlini; he will tell thee what has become of her;’ and he returned to his companions.\n\n“The old man remained motionless; he felt that some great and unforeseen misfortune hung over his head. At length he advanced toward the group, the meaning of which he could not comprehend. As he approached, Carlini raised his head, and the forms of two persons became visible to the old man’s eyes. A woman lay on the ground, her head resting on the knees of a man, who was seated by her; as he raised his head, the woman’s face became visible. The old man recognized his child, and Carlini recognized the old man.\n\n“‘I expected thee,’ said the bandit to Rita’s father.\n\n“‘Wretch!’ returned the old man, ‘what hast thou done?’ and he gazed with terror on Rita, pale and bloody, a knife buried in her bosom. A ray of moonlight poured through the trees, and lighted up the face of the dead.\n\n“‘Cucumetto had violated thy daughter,’ said the bandit; ‘I loved her, therefore I slew her; for she would have served as the sport of the whole band.’ The old man spoke not, and grew pale as death. ‘Now,’ continued Carlini, ‘if I have done wrongly, avenge her;’ and withdrawing the knife from the wound in Rita’s bosom, he held it out to the old man with one hand, while with the other he tore open his vest.\n\n“‘Thou hast done well!’ returned the old man in a hoarse voice; ‘embrace me, my son.’\n\n20115m\nCarlini threw himself, sobbing like a child, into the arms of his mistress’s father. These were the first tears the man of blood had ever wept.\n\n“‘Now,’ said the old man, ‘aid me to bury my child.’ Carlini fetched two pickaxes; and the father and the lover began to dig at the foot of a huge oak, beneath which the young girl was to repose. When the grave was formed, the father embraced her first, and then the lover; afterwards, one taking the head, the other the feet, they placed her in the grave. Then they knelt on each side of the grave, and said the prayers of the dead. Then, when they had finished, they cast the earth over the corpse, until the grave was filled. Then, extending his hand, the old man said; ‘I thank you, my son; and now leave me alone.’\n\n“‘Yet——’ replied Carlini.\n\n“‘Leave me, I command you.’\n\n“Carlini obeyed, rejoined his comrades, folded himself in his cloak, and soon appeared to sleep as soundly as the rest. It had been resolved the night before to change their encampment. An hour before daybreak, Cucumetto aroused his men, and gave the word to march. But Carlini would not quit the forest, without knowing what had become of Rita’s father. He went toward the place where he had left him. He found the old man suspended from one of the branches of the oak which shaded his daughter’s grave. He then took an oath of bitter vengeance over the dead body of the one and the tomb of the other. But he was unable to complete this oath, for two days afterwards, in an encounter with the Roman carbineers, Carlini was killed. There was some surprise, however, that, as he was with his face to the enemy, he should have received a ball between his shoulders. That astonishment ceased when one of the brigands remarked to his comrades that Cucumetto was stationed ten paces in Carlini’s rear when he fell. On the morning of the departure from the forest of Frosinone he had followed Carlini in the darkness, and heard this oath of vengeance, and, like a wise man, anticipated it.\n\n“They told ten other stories of this bandit chief, each more singular than the other. Thus, from Fondi to Perusia, everyone trembles at the name of Cucumetto.\n\n“These narratives were frequently the theme of conversation between Luigi and Teresa. The young girl trembled very much at hearing the stories; but Vampa reassured her with a smile, tapping the butt of his good fowling-piece, which threw its ball so well; and if that did not restore her courage, he pointed to a crow, perched on some dead branch, took aim, touched the trigger, and the bird fell dead at the foot of the tree. Time passed on, and the two young people had agreed to be married when Vampa should be twenty and Teresa nineteen years of age. They were both orphans, and had only their employers’ leave to ask, which had been already sought and obtained. One day when they were talking over their plans for the future, they heard two or three reports of firearms, and then suddenly a man came out of the wood, near which the two young persons used to graze their flocks, and hurried towards them. When he came within hearing, he exclaimed:\n‘I am pursued; can you conceal me?’\n\n“They knew full well that this fugitive must be a bandit; but there is an innate sympathy between the Roman brigand and the Roman peasant and the latter is always ready to aid the former. Vampa, without saying a word, hastened to the stone that closed up the entrance to their grotto, drew it away, made a sign to the fugitive to take refuge there, in a retreat unknown to everyone, closed the stone upon him, and then went and resumed his seat by Teresa. Instantly afterwards four carbineers, on horseback, appeared on the edge of the wood; three of them appeared to be looking for the fugitive, while the fourth dragged a brigand prisoner by the neck. The three carbineers looked about carefully on every side, saw the young peasants, and galloping up, began to question them. They had seen no one.\n\n“‘That is very annoying,’ said the brigadier; for the man we are looking for is the chief.’\n\n“‘Cucumetto?’ cried Luigi and Teresa at the same moment.\n\n“‘Yes,’ replied the brigadier; ‘and as his head is valued at a thousand Roman crowns, there would have been five hundred for you, if you had helped us to catch him.’ The two young persons exchanged looks. The brigadier had a moment’s hope. Five hundred Roman crowns are three thousand lire, and three thousand lire are a fortune for two poor orphans who are going to be married.\n\n“‘Yes, it is very annoying,’ said Vampa; ‘but we have not seen him.’\n\n“Then the carbineers scoured the country in different directions, but in vain; then, after a time, they disappeared. Vampa then removed the stone, and Cucumetto came out. Through the crevices in the granite he had seen the two young peasants talking with the carbineers, and guessed the subject of their parley. He had read in the countenances of Luigi and Teresa their steadfast resolution not to surrender him, and he drew from his pocket a purse full of gold, which he offered to them. But Vampa raised his head proudly; as to Teresa, her eyes sparkled when she thought of all the fine gowns and gay jewellery she could buy with this purse of gold.\n\n“Cucumetto was a cunning fiend, and had assumed the form of a brigand instead of a serpent, and this look from Teresa showed to him that she was a worthy daughter of Eve, and he returned to the forest, pausing several times on his way, under the pretext of saluting his protectors.\n\n“Several days elapsed, and they neither saw nor heard of Cucumetto. The time of the Carnival was at hand. The Count of San-Felice announced a grand masked ball, to which all that were distinguished in Rome were invited. Teresa had a great desire to see this ball. Luigi asked permission of his protector, the steward, that she and he might be present amongst the servants of the house. This was granted. The ball was given by the Count for the particular pleasure of his daughter Carmela, whom he adored. Carmela was precisely the age and figure of Teresa, and Teresa was as handsome as Carmela. On the evening of the ball Teresa was attired in her best, her most brilliant ornaments in her hair, and gayest glass beads,—she was in the costume of the women of Frascati. Luigi wore the very picturesque garb of the Roman peasant at holiday time. They both mingled, as they had leave to do, with the servants and peasants.\n\n“The _festa_ was magnificent; not only was the villa brilliantly illuminated, but thousands of colored lanterns were suspended from the trees in the garden; and very soon the palace overflowed to the terraces, and the terraces to the garden-walks. At each cross-path was an orchestra, and tables spread with refreshments; the guests stopped, formed quadrilles, and danced in any part of the grounds they pleased. Carmela was attired like a woman of Sonnino. Her cap was embroidered with pearls, the pins in her hair were of gold and diamonds, her girdle was of Turkey silk, with large embroidered flowers, her bodice and skirt were of cashmere, her apron of Indian muslin, and the buttons of her corset were of jewels. Two of her companions were dressed, the one as a woman of Nettuno, and the other as a woman of La Riccia. Four young men of the richest and noblest families of Rome accompanied them with that Italian freedom which has not its parallel in any other country in the world. They were attired as peasants of Albano, Velletri, Civita-Castellana, and Sora. We need hardly add that these peasant costumes, like those of the young women, were brilliant with gold and jewels.\n\n“Carmela wished to form a quadrille, but there was one lady wanting. Carmela looked all around her, but not one of the guests had a costume similar to her own, or those of her companions. The Count of San-Felice pointed out Teresa, who was hanging on Luigi’s arm in a group of peasants.\n\n“‘Will you allow me, father?’ said Carmela.\n\n“‘Certainly,’ replied the count, ‘are we not in Carnival time?’\n\n“Carmela turned towards the young man who was talking with her, and saying a few words to him, pointed with her finger to Teresa. The young man looked, bowed in obedience, and then went to Teresa, and invited her to dance in a quadrille directed by the count’s daughter. Teresa felt a flush pass over her face; she looked at Luigi, who could not refuse his assent. Luigi slowly relinquished Teresa’s arm, which he had held beneath his own, and Teresa, accompanied by her elegant cavalier, took her appointed place with much agitation in the aristocratic quadrille. Certainly, in the eyes of an artist, the exact and strict costume of Teresa had a very different character from that of Carmela and her companions; and Teresa was frivolous and coquettish, and thus the embroidery and muslins, the cashmere waist-girdles, all dazzled her, and the reflection of sapphires and diamonds almost turned her giddy brain.\n\n“Luigi felt a sensation hitherto unknown arising in his mind. It was like an acute pain which gnawed at his heart, and then thrilled through his whole body. He followed with his eye each movement of Teresa and her cavalier; when their hands touched, he felt as though he should swoon; every pulse beat with violence, and it seemed as though a bell were ringing in his ears. When they spoke, although Teresa listened timidly and with downcast eyes to the conversation of her cavalier, as Luigi could read in the ardent looks of the good-looking young man that his language was that of praise, it seemed as if the whole world was turning round with him, and all the voices of hell were whispering in his ears ideas of murder and assassination. Then fearing that his paroxysm might get the better of him, he clutched with one hand the branch of a tree against which he was leaning, and with the other convulsively grasped the dagger with a carved handle which was in his belt, and which, unwittingly, he drew from the scabbard from time to time.\n\n“Luigi was jealous!\n\n“He felt that, influenced by her ambitions and coquettish disposition, Teresa might escape him.\n\n“The young peasant girl, at first timid and scared, soon recovered herself. We have said that Teresa was handsome, but this is not all; Teresa was endowed with all those wild graces which are so much more potent than our affected and studied elegancies. She had almost all the honors of the quadrille, and if she were envious of the Count of San-Felice’s daughter, we will not undertake to say that Carmela was not jealous of her. And with overpowering compliments her handsome cavalier led her back to the place whence he had taken her, and where Luigi awaited her. Twice or thrice during the dance the young girl had glanced at Luigi, and each time she saw that he was pale and that his features were agitated, once even the blade of his knife, half drawn from its sheath, had dazzled her eyes with its sinister glare. Thus, it was almost tremblingly that she resumed her lover’s arm. The quadrille had been most perfect, and it was evident there was a great demand for a repetition, Carmela alone objecting to it, but the Count of San-Felice besought his daughter so earnestly, that she acceded.\n\n“One of the cavaliers then hastened to invite Teresa, without whom it was impossible for the quadrille to be formed, but the young girl had disappeared.\n\n“The truth was, that Luigi had not felt the strength to support another such trial, and, half by persuasion and half by force, he had removed Teresa toward another part of the garden. Teresa had yielded in spite of herself, but when she looked at the agitated countenance of the young man, she understood by his silence and trembling voice that something strange was passing within him. She herself was not exempt from internal emotion, and without having done anything wrong, yet fully comprehended that Luigi was right in reproaching her. Why, she did not know, but yet she did not the less feel that these reproaches were merited.\n\n“However, to Teresa’s great astonishment, Luigi remained mute, and not a word escaped his lips the rest of the evening. When the chill of the night had driven away the guests from the gardens, and the gates of the villa were closed on them for the _festa_ in-doors, he took Teresa quite away, and as he left her at her home, he said:\n\n“‘Teresa, what were you thinking of as you danced opposite the young Countess of San-Felice?’\n\n“‘I thought,’ replied the young girl, with all the frankness of her nature, ‘that I would give half my life for a costume such as she wore.’\n\n“‘And what said your cavalier to you?’\n\n“‘He said it only depended on myself to have it, and I had only one word to say.’\n\n“‘He was right,’ said Luigi. ‘Do you desire it as ardently as you say?’\n\n“‘Yes.’\n\n“‘Well, then, you shall have it!’\n\n“The young girl, much astonished, raised her head to look at him, but his face was so gloomy and terrible that her words froze to her lips. As Luigi spoke thus, he left her. Teresa followed him with her eyes into the darkness as long as she could, and when he had quite disappeared, she went into the house with a sigh.\n\n20121m\n“That night a memorable event occurred, due, no doubt, to the imprudence of some servant who had neglected to extinguish the lights. The Villa of San-Felice took fire in the rooms adjoining the very apartment of the lovely Carmela. Awakened in the night by the light of the flames, she sprang out of bed, wrapped herself in a dressing-gown, and attempted to escape by the door, but the corridor by which she hoped to fly was already a prey to the flames. She then returned to her room, calling for help as loudly as she could, when suddenly her window, which was twenty feet from the ground, was opened, a young peasant jumped into the chamber, seized her in his arms, and with superhuman skill and strength conveyed her to the turf of the grass-plot, where she fainted. When she recovered, her father was by her side. All the servants surrounded her, offering her assistance. An entire wing of the villa was burnt down; but what of that, as long as Carmela was safe and uninjured?\n\n“Her preserver was everywhere sought for, but he did not appear; he was inquired after, but no one had seen him. Carmela was greatly troubled that she had not recognized him.\n\n“As the count was immensely rich, excepting the danger Carmela had run,—and the marvellous manner in which she had escaped, made that appear to him rather a favor of Providence than a real misfortune,—the loss occasioned by the conflagration was to him but a trifle.\n\n“The next day, at the usual hour, the two young peasants were on the borders of the forest. Luigi arrived first. He came toward Teresa in high spirits, and seemed to have completely forgotten the events of the previous evening. The young girl was very pensive, but seeing Luigi so cheerful, she on her part assumed a smiling air, which was natural to her when she was not excited or in a passion.\n\n“Luigi took her arm beneath his own, and led her to the door of the grotto. Then he paused. The young girl, perceiving that there was something extraordinary, looked at him steadfastly.\n\n“‘Teresa,’ said Luigi, ‘yesterday evening you told me you would give all the world to have a costume similar to that of the count’s daughter.’\n\n“‘Yes,’ replied Teresa with astonishment; ‘but I was mad to utter such a wish.’\n\n“‘And I replied, “Very well, you shall have it.”’\n\n“‘Yes,’ replied the young girl, whose astonishment increased at every word uttered by Luigi, ‘but of course your reply was only to please me.’\n\n“‘I have promised no more than I have given you, Teresa,’ said Luigi proudly. ‘Go into the grotto and dress yourself.’\n\n“At these words he drew away the stone, and showed Teresa the grotto, lighted up by two wax lights, which burnt on each side of a splendid mirror; on a rustic table, made by Luigi, were spread out the pearl necklace and the diamond pins, and on a chair at the side was laid the rest of the costume.\n\n“Teresa uttered a cry of joy, and, without inquiring whence this attire came, or even thanking Luigi, darted into the grotto, transformed into a dressing-room.\n\n“Luigi pushed the stone behind her, for on the crest of a small adjacent hill which cut off the view toward Palestrina, he saw a traveller on horseback, stopping a moment, as if uncertain of his road, and thus presenting against the blue sky that perfect outline which is peculiar to distant objects in southern climes. When he saw Luigi, he put his horse into a gallop and advanced toward him.\n\n“Luigi was not mistaken. The traveller, who was going from Palestrina to Tivoli, had mistaken his way; the young man directed him; but as at a distance of a quarter of a mile the road again divided into three ways, and on reaching these the traveller might again stray from his route, he begged Luigi to be his guide.\n\n“Luigi threw his cloak on the ground, placed his carbine on his shoulder, and freed from his heavy covering, preceded the traveller with the rapid step of a mountaineer, which a horse can scarcely keep up with. In ten minutes Luigi and the traveller reached the cross-roads. On arriving there, with an air as majestic as that of an emperor, he stretched his hand towards that one of the roads which the traveller was to follow.\n\n“‘That is your road, excellency, and now you cannot again mistake.’\n\n“‘And here is your recompense,’ said the traveller, offering the young herdsman some small pieces of money.\n\n“‘Thank you,’ said Luigi, drawing back his hand; ‘I render a service, I do not sell it.’\n\n“‘Well,’ replied the traveller, who seemed used to this difference between the servility of a man of the cities and the pride of the mountaineer, ‘if you refuse wages, you will, perhaps, accept a gift.’\n\n“‘Ah, yes, that is another thing.’\n\n“‘Then,’ said the traveller, ‘take these two Venetian sequins and give them to your bride, to make herself a pair of earrings.’\n\n“‘And then do you take this poniard,’ said the young herdsman; ‘you will not find one better carved between Albano and Civita-Castellana.’\n\n“‘I accept it,’ answered the traveller, ‘but then the obligation will be on my side, for this poniard is worth more than two sequins.’\n\n“‘For a dealer perhaps; but for me, who engraved it myself, it is hardly worth a piastre.’\n\n“‘What is your name?’ inquired the traveller.\n\n“‘Luigi Vampa,’ replied the shepherd, with the same air as he would have replied, Alexander, King of Macedon. ‘And yours?’\n\n“‘I,’ said the traveller, ‘am called Sinbad the Sailor.’”\n\nFranz d’Épinay started with surprise.\n\n“Sinbad the Sailor?” he said.\n\n“Yes,” replied the narrator; “that was the name which the traveller gave to Vampa as his own.”\n\n“Well, and what may you have to say against this name?” inquired Albert; “it is a very pretty name, and the adventures of the gentleman of that name amused me very much in my youth, I must confess.”\n\nFranz said no more. The name of Sinbad the Sailor, as may well be supposed, awakened in him a world of recollections, as had the name of the Count of Monte Cristo on the previous evening.\n\n“Proceed!” said he to the host.\n\n“Vampa put the two sequins haughtily into his pocket, and slowly returned by the way he had gone. As he came within two or three hundred paces of the grotto, he thought he heard a cry. He listened to know whence this sound could proceed. A moment afterwards he thought he heard his own name pronounced distinctly.\n\n“The cry proceeded from the grotto. He bounded like a chamois, cocking his carbine as he went, and in a moment reached the summit of a hill opposite to that on which he had perceived the traveller. Three cries for help came more distinctly to his ear. He cast his eyes around him and saw a man carrying off Teresa, as Nessus, the centaur, carried Deianira.\n\n“This man, who was hastening towards the wood, was already three-quarters of the way on the road from the grotto to the forest. Vampa measured the distance; the man was at least two hundred paces in advance of him, and there was not a chance of overtaking him. The young shepherd stopped, as if his feet had been rooted to the ground; then he put the butt of his carbine to his shoulder, took aim at the ravisher, followed him for a second in his track, and then fired.\n\n“The ravisher stopped suddenly, his knees bent under him, and he fell with Teresa in his arms. The young girl rose instantly, but the man lay on the earth struggling in the agonies of death. Vampa then rushed towards Teresa; for at ten paces from the dying man her legs had failed her, and she had dropped on her knees, so that the young man feared that the ball that had brought down his enemy, had also wounded his betrothed.\n\n“Fortunately, she was unscathed, and it was fright alone that had overcome Teresa. When Luigi had assured himself that she was safe and unharmed, he turned towards the wounded man. He had just expired, with clenched hands, his mouth in a spasm of agony, and his hair on end in the sweat of death. His eyes remained open and menacing. Vampa approached the corpse, and recognized Cucumetto.\n\n“From the day on which the bandit had been saved by the two young peasants, he had been enamoured of Teresa, and had sworn she should be his. From that time he had watched them, and profiting by the moment when her lover had left her alone, had carried her off, and believed he at length had her in his power, when the ball, directed by the unerring skill of the young herdsman, had pierced his heart. Vampa gazed on him for a moment without betraying the slightest emotion; while, on the contrary, Teresa, shuddering in every limb, dared not approach the slain ruffian but by degrees, and threw a hesitating glance at the dead body over the shoulder of her lover. Suddenly Vampa turned toward his mistress:\n\n“‘Ah,’ said he—‘good, good! You are dressed; it is now my turn to dress myself.’\n20125m\n\n“Teresa was clothed from head to foot in the garb of the Count of San-Felice’s daughter. Vampa took Cucumetto’s body in his arms and conveyed it to the grotto, while in her turn Teresa remained outside. If a second traveller had passed, he would have seen a strange thing,—a shepherdess watching her flock, clad in a cashmere grown, with ear-rings and necklace of pearls, diamond pins, and buttons of sapphires, emeralds, and rubies. He would, no doubt, have believed that he had returned to the times of Florian, and would have declared, on reaching Paris, that he had met an Alpine shepherdess seated at the foot of the Sabine Hill.\n\n“At the end of a quarter of an hour Vampa quitted the grotto; his costume was no less elegant than that of Teresa. He wore a vest of garnet-colored velvet, with buttons of cut gold; a silk waistcoat covered with embroidery; a Roman scarf tied round his neck; a cartridge-box worked with gold, and red and green silk; sky-blue velvet breeches, fastened above the knee with diamond buckles; garters of deerskin, worked with a thousand arabesques, and a hat whereon hung ribbons of all colors; two watches hung from his girdle, and a splendid poniard was in his belt.\n\n“Teresa uttered a cry of admiration. Vampa in this attire resembled a painting by Léopold Robert or Schnetz. He had assumed the entire costume of Cucumetto. The young man saw the effect produced on his betrothed, and a smile of pride passed over his lips.\n\n“‘Now,’ he said to Teresa, ‘are you ready to share my fortune, whatever it may be?’\n\n“‘Oh, yes!’ exclaimed the young girl enthusiastically.\n\n“‘And follow me wherever I go?’\n\n“‘To the world’s end.’\n\n“‘Then take my arm, and let us on; we have no time to lose.’\n\n“The young girl did so without questioning her lover as to where he was conducting her, for he appeared to her at this moment as handsome, proud, and powerful as a god. They went towards the forest, and soon entered it.\n\n“We need scarcely say that all the paths of the mountain were known to Vampa; he therefore went forward without a moment’s hesitation, although there was no beaten track, but he knew his path by looking at the trees and bushes, and thus they kept on advancing for nearly an hour and a half. At the end of this time they had reached the thickest part of the forest. A torrent, whose bed was dry, led into a deep gorge. Vampa took this wild road, which, enclosed between two ridges, and shadowed by the tufted umbrage of the pines, seemed, but for the difficulties of its descent, that path to Avernus of which Virgil speaks. Teresa had become alarmed at the wild and deserted look of the plain around her, and pressed closely against her guide, not uttering a syllable; but as she saw him advance with even step and composed countenance, she endeavored to repress her emotion.\n\n“Suddenly, about ten paces from them, a man advanced from behind a tree and aimed at Vampa.\n\n“‘Not another step,’ he said, ‘or you are a dead man.’\n\n“‘What, then,’ said Vampa, raising his hand with a gesture of disdain, while Teresa, no longer able to restrain her alarm, clung closely to him, ‘do wolves rend each other?’\n\n“‘Who are you?’ inquired the sentinel.\n\n“‘I am Luigi Vampa, shepherd of the San-Felice farm.’\n\n“‘What do you want?’\n\n“‘I would speak with your companions who are in the glade at Rocca Bianca.’\n\n“‘Follow me, then,’ said the sentinel; ‘or, as you know your way, go first.’\n\n“Vampa smiled disdainfully at this precaution on the part of the bandit, went before Teresa, and continued to advance with the same firm and easy step as before. At the end of ten minutes the bandit made them a sign to stop. The two young persons obeyed. Then the bandit thrice imitated the cry of a crow; a croak answered this signal.\n\n“‘Good!’ said the sentry, ‘you may now go on.’\n\n“Luigi and Teresa again set forward; as they went on Teresa clung tremblingly to her lover at the sight of weapons and the glistening of carbines through the trees. The retreat of Rocca Bianca was at the top of a small mountain, which no doubt in former days had been a volcano—an extinct volcano before the days when Remus and Romulus had deserted Alba to come and found the city of Rome.\n\n“Teresa and Luigi reached the summit, and all at once found themselves in the presence of twenty bandits.\n\n“‘Here is a young man who seeks and wishes to speak to you,’ said the sentinel.\n\n“‘What has he to say?’ inquired the young man who was in command in the chief’s absence.\n\n“‘I wish to say that I am tired of a shepherd’s life,’ was Vampa’s reply.\n\n“‘Ah, I understand,’ said the lieutenant; ‘and you seek admittance into our ranks?’\n\n“‘Welcome!’ cried several bandits from Ferrusino, Pampinara, and Anagni, who had recognized Luigi Vampa.\n\n“‘Yes, but I came to ask something more than to be your companion.’\n\n“‘And what may that be?’ inquired the bandits with astonishment.\n\n“‘I come to ask to be your captain,’ said the young man.\n\n“The bandits shouted with laughter.\n\n“‘And what have you done to aspire to this honor?’ demanded the lieutenant.\n\n“‘I have killed your chief, Cucumetto, whose dress I now wear; and I set fire to the villa San-Felice to procure a wedding-dress for my betrothed.’\n\n“An hour afterwards Luigi Vampa was chosen captain, vice Cucumetto, deceased.”\n\n20129m\n“Well, my dear Albert,” said Franz, turning towards his friend; “what think you of citizen Luigi Vampa?”\n\n“I say he is a myth,” replied Albert, “and never had an existence.”\n\n“And what may a myth be?” inquired Pastrini.\n\n“The explanation would be too long, my dear landlord,” replied Franz.\n“And you say that Signor Vampa exercises his profession at this moment in the environs of Rome?”\n\n“And with a boldness of which no bandit before him ever gave an example.”\n\n“Then the police have vainly tried to lay hands on him?”\n\n“Why, you see, he has a good understanding with the shepherds in the plains, the fishermen of the Tiber, and the smugglers of the coast. They seek for him in the mountains, and he is on the waters; they follow him on the waters, and he is on the open sea; then they pursue him, and he has suddenly taken refuge in the islands, at Giglio, Giannutri, or Monte Cristo; and when they hunt for him there, he reappears suddenly at Albano, Tivoli, or La Riccia.”\n\n“And how does he behave towards travellers?”\n\n“Alas! his plan is very simple. It depends on the distance he may be from the city, whether he gives eight hours, twelve hours, or a day wherein to pay their ransom; and when that time has elapsed he allows another hour’s grace. At the sixtieth minute of this hour, if the money is not forthcoming, he blows out the prisoner’s brains with a pistol-shot, or plants his dagger in his heart, and that settles the account.”\n\n“Well, Albert,” inquired Franz of his companion, “are you still disposed to go to the Colosseum by the outer wall?”\n\n“Quite so,” said Albert, “if the way be picturesque.”\n\nThe clock struck nine as the door opened, and a coachman appeared.\n\n“Excellencies,” said he, “the coach is ready.”\n\n“Well, then,” said Franz, “let us to the Colosseum.”\n\n“By the Porta del Popolo or by the streets, your excellencies?”\n\n“By the streets, _morbleu!_ by the streets!” cried Franz.\n\n“Ah, my dear fellow,” said Albert, rising, and lighting his third cigar, “really, I thought you had more courage.”\n\nSo saying, the two young men went down the staircase, and got into the carriage.\n\n# Chapter 34. The Colosseum\n\n20131m\nFranz had so managed his route, that during the ride to the Colosseum they passed not a single ancient ruin, so that no preliminary impression interfered to mitigate the colossal proportions of the gigantic building they came to admire. The road selected was a continuation of the Via Sistina; then by cutting off the right angle of the street in which stands Santa Maria Maggiore and proceeding by the Via Urbana and San Pietro in Vincoli, the travellers would find themselves directly opposite the Colosseum.\n\nThis itinerary possessed another great advantage,—that of leaving Franz at full liberty to indulge his deep reverie upon the subject of Signor Pastrini’s story, in which his mysterious host of Monte Cristo was so strangely mixed up. Seated with folded arms in a corner of the carriage, he continued to ponder over the singular history he had so lately listened to, and to ask himself an interminable number of questions touching its various circumstances without, however, arriving at a satisfactory reply to any of them.\n\nOne fact more than the rest brought his friend “Sinbad the Sailor” back to his recollection, and that was the mysterious sort of intimacy that seemed to exist between the brigands and the sailors; and Pastrini’s account of Vampa’s having found refuge on board the vessels of smugglers and fishermen, reminded Franz of the two Corsican bandits he had found supping so amicably with the crew of the little yacht, which had even deviated from its course and touched at Porto-Vecchio for the sole purpose of landing them. The very name assumed by his host of Monte Cristo and again repeated by the landlord of the Hôtel de Londres, abundantly proved to him that his island friend was playing his philanthropic part on the shores of Piombino, Civita Vecchia, Ostia, and Gaëta, as on those of Corsica, Tuscany, and Spain; and further, Franz bethought him of having heard his singular entertainer speak both of Tunis and Palermo, proving thereby how largely his circle of acquaintances extended.\n\nBut however the mind of the young man might be absorbed in these reflections, they were at once dispersed at the sight of the dark frowning ruins of the stupendous Colosseum, through the various openings of which the pale moonlight played and flickered like the unearthly gleam from the eyes of the wandering dead. The carriage stopped near the Meta Sudans; the door was opened, and the young men, eagerly alighting, found themselves opposite a _cicerone_, who appeared to have sprung up from the ground, so unexpected was his appearance.\nThe usual guide from the hotel having followed them, they had paid two conductors, nor is it possible, at Rome, to avoid this abundant supply of guides; besides the ordinary _cicerone_, who seizes upon you directly you set foot in your hotel, and never quits you while you remain in the city, there is also a special _cicerone_ belonging to each monument—nay, almost to each part of a monument. It may, therefore, be easily imagined there is no scarcity of guides at the Colosseum, that wonder of all ages, which Martial thus eulogizes:\n\n“Let Memphis cease to boast the barbarous miracles of her pyramids, and the wonders of Babylon be talked of no more among us; all must bow to the superiority of the gigantic labor of the Cæsars, and the many voices of Fame spread far and wide the surpassing merits of this incomparable monument.”\n\nAs for Albert and Franz, they essayed not to escape from their _ciceronian_ tyrants; and, indeed, it would have been so much the more difficult to break their bondage, as the guides alone are permitted to visit these monuments with torches in their hands. Thus, then, the young men made no attempt at resistance, but blindly and confidingly surrendered themselves into the care and custody of their conductors.\nFranz had already made seven or eight similar excursions to the Colosseum, while his less favored companion trod for the first time in his life the classic ground forming the monument of Flavius Vespasian; and, to his credit be it spoken, his mind, even amid the glib loquacity of the guides, was duly and deeply touched with awe and enthusiastic admiration of all he saw; and certainly no adequate notion of these stupendous ruins can be formed save by such as have visited them, and more especially by moonlight, at which time the vast proportions of the building appear twice as large when viewed by the mysterious beams of a southern moonlit sky, whose rays are sufficiently clear and vivid to light the horizon with a glow equal to the soft twilight of a western clime.\n\nScarcely, therefore, had the reflective Franz walked a hundred steps beneath the interior porticoes of the ruin, when, abandoning Albert to the guides (who would by no means yield their prescriptive right of carrying their victims through the routine regularly laid down, and as regularly followed by them, but dragged the unconscious visitor to the various objects with a pertinacity that admitted of no appeal, beginning, as a matter of course, with the “Lions’ Den”, the “Hall of the Gladiators” and finishing with “Cæsar’s Podium”), to escape a jargon and mechanical survey of the wonders by which he was surrounded, Franz ascended a half-dilapidated staircase, and, leaving them to follow their monotonous round, seated himself at the foot of a column, and immediately opposite a large aperture, which permitted him to enjoy a full and undisturbed view of the gigantic dimensions of the majestic ruin.\n\nFranz had remained for nearly a quarter of an hour perfectly hidden by the shadow of the vast column at whose base he had found a resting-place, and from whence his eyes followed the motions of Albert and his guides, who, holding torches in their hands, had emerged from a vomitorium at the opposite extremity of the Colosseum, and then again disappeared down the steps conducting to the seats reserved for the Vestal virgins, resembling, as they glided along, some restless shades following the flickering glare of so many _ignes fatui_. All at once his ear caught a sound resembling that of a stone rolling down the staircase opposite the one by which he had himself ascended. There was nothing remarkable in the circumstance of a fragment of granite giving way and falling heavily below; but it seemed to him that the substance that fell gave way beneath the pressure of a foot, and also that someone, who endeavored as much as possible to prevent his footsteps from being heard, was approaching the spot where he sat.\n\nConjecture soon became certainty, for the figure of a man was distinctly visible to Franz, gradually emerging from the staircase opposite, upon which the moon was at that moment pouring a full tide of silvery brightness.\n\nThe stranger thus presenting himself was probably a person who, like Franz, preferred the enjoyment of solitude and his own thoughts to the frivolous gabble of the guides. And his appearance had nothing extraordinary in it; but the hesitation with which he proceeded, stopping and listening with anxious attention at every step he took, convinced Franz that he expected the arrival of some person.\n\nBy a sort of instinctive impulse, Franz withdrew as much as possible behind his pillar.\n\nAbout ten feet from the spot where he and the stranger were, the roof had given way, leaving a large round opening, through which might be seen the blue vault of heaven, thickly studded with stars.\n\nAround this opening, which had, possibly, for ages permitted a free entrance to the brilliant moonbeams that now illumined the vast pile, grew a quantity of creeping plants, whose delicate green branches stood out in bold relief against the clear azure of the firmament, while large masses of thick, strong fibrous shoots forced their way through the chasm, and hung floating to and fro, like so many waving strings.\n\nThe person whose mysterious arrival had attracted the attention of Franz stood in a kind of half-light, that rendered it impossible to distinguish his features, although his dress was easily made out. He wore a large brown mantle, one fold of which, thrown over his left shoulder, served likewise to mask the lower part of his countenance, while the upper part was completely hidden by his broad-brimmed hat. The lower part of his dress was more distinctly visible by the bright rays of the moon, which, entering through the broken ceiling, shed their refulgent beams on feet cased in elegantly made boots of polished leather, over which descended fashionably cut trousers of black cloth.\n20135m\n\nFrom the imperfect means Franz had of judging, he could only come to one conclusion,—that the person whom he was thus watching certainly belonged to no inferior station of life.\n\nSome few minutes had elapsed, and the stranger began to show manifest signs of impatience, when a slight noise was heard outside the aperture in the roof, and almost immediately a dark shadow seemed to obstruct the flood of light that had entered it, and the figure of a man was clearly seen gazing with eager scrutiny on the immense space beneath him; then, as his eye caught sight of him in the mantle, he grasped a floating mass of thickly matted boughs, and glided down by their help to within three or four feet of the ground, and then leaped lightly on his feet. The man who had performed this daring act with so much indifference wore the Transtevere costume.\n\n“I beg your excellency’s pardon for keeping you waiting,” said the man, in the Roman dialect, “but I don’t think I’m many minutes after my time, ten o’clock has just struck by the clock of Saint John Lateran.”\n\n“Say not a word about being late,” replied the stranger in purest Tuscan; “’tis I who am too soon. But even if you had caused me to wait a little while, I should have felt quite sure that the delay was not occasioned by any fault of yours.”\n\n“Your excellency is perfectly right in so thinking,” said the man; “I came here direct from the Castle of St. Angelo, and I had an immense deal of trouble before I could get a chance to speak to Beppo.”\n\n“And who is Beppo?”\n\n“Oh, Beppo is employed in the prison, and I give him so much a year to let me know what is going on within his holiness’s castle.”\n\n“Indeed! You are a provident person, I see.”\n\n“Why, you see, no one knows what may happen. Perhaps some of these days I may be entrapped, like poor Peppino and may be very glad to have some little nibbling mouse to gnaw the meshes of my net, and so help me out of prison.”\n\n“Briefly, what did you learn?”\n\n“That two executions of considerable interest will take place the day after tomorrow at two o’clock, as is customary at Rome at the commencement of all great festivals. One of the culprits will be _mazzolato_;3 he is an atrocious villain, who murdered the priest who brought him up, and deserves not the smallest pity. The other sufferer is sentenced to be _decapitato_;4 and he, your excellency, is poor Peppino.”\n\n“The fact is, that you have inspired not only the pontifical government, but also the neighboring states, with such extreme fear, that they are glad of all opportunity of making an example.”\n\n“But Peppino did not even belong to my band; he was merely a poor shepherd, whose only crime consisted in furnishing us with provisions.”\n\n“Which makes him your accomplice to all intents and purposes. But mark the distinction with which he is treated; instead of being knocked on the head as you would be if once they caught hold of you, he is simply sentenced to be guillotined, by which means, too, the amusements of the day are diversified, and there is a spectacle to please every spectator.”\n\n“Without reckoning the wholly unexpected one I am preparing to surprise them with.”\n\n“My good friend,” said the man in the cloak, “excuse me for saying that you seem to me precisely in the mood to commit some wild or extravagant act.”\n\n“Perhaps I am; but one thing I have resolved on, and that is, to stop at nothing to restore a poor devil to liberty, who has got into this scrape solely from having served me. I should hate and despise myself as a coward did I desert the brave fellow in his present extremity.”\n\n“And what do you mean to do?”\n\n“To surround the scaffold with twenty of my best men, who, at a signal from me, will rush forward directly Peppino is brought for execution, and, by the assistance of their stilettos, drive back the guard, and carry off the prisoner.”\n\n“That seems to me as hazardous as uncertain, and convinces me that my scheme is far better than yours.”\n\n“And what is your excellency’s project?”\n\n“Just this. I will so advantageously bestow 2,000 piastres, that the person receiving them shall obtain a respite till next year for Peppino; and during that year, another skilfully placed 1,000 piastres will afford him the means of escaping from his prison.”\n\n“And do you feel sure of succeeding?”\n\n“_Pardieu!_” exclaimed the man in the cloak, suddenly expressing himself in French.\n\n“What did your excellency say?” inquired the other.\n\n“I said, my good fellow, that I would do more single-handed by the means of gold than you and all your troop could effect with stilettos, pistols, carbines, and blunderbusses included. Leave me, then, to act, and have no fears for the result.”\n\n“At least, there can be no harm in myself and party being in readiness, in case your excellency should fail.”\n\n“None whatever. Take what precautions you please, if it is any satisfaction to you to do so; but rely upon my obtaining the reprieve I seek.”\n\n“Remember, the execution is fixed for the day after tomorrow, and that you have but one day to work in.”\n\n“And what of that? Is not a day divided into twenty-four hours, each hour into sixty minutes, and every minute sub-divided into sixty seconds? Now in 86,400 seconds very many things can be done.”\n\n“And how shall I know whether your excellency has succeeded or not.”\n\n“Oh, that is very easily arranged. I have engaged the three lower windows at the Café Rospoli; should I have obtained the requisite pardon for Peppino, the two outside windows will be hung with yellow damasks, and the centre with white, having a large cross in red marked on it.”\n\n“And whom will you employ to carry the reprieve to the officer directing the execution?”\n\n“Send one of your men, disguised as a penitent friar, and I will give it to him. His dress will procure him the means of approaching the scaffold itself, and he will deliver the official order to the officer, who, in his turn, will hand it to the executioner; in the meantime, it will be as well to acquaint Peppino with what we have determined on, if it be only to prevent his dying of fear or losing his senses, because in either case a very useless expense will have been incurred.”\n\n“Your excellency,” said the man, “you are fully persuaded of my entire devotion to you, are you not?”\n\n“Nay, I flatter myself that there can be no doubt of it,” replied the cavalier in the cloak.\n\n“Well, then, only fulfil your promise of rescuing Peppino, and henceforward you shall receive not only devotion, but the most absolute obedience from myself and those under me that one human being can render to another.”\n\n“Have a care how far you pledge yourself, my good friend, for I may remind you of your promise at some, perhaps, not very distant period, when I, in my turn, may require your aid and influence.”\n\n“Let that day come sooner or later, your excellency will find me what I have found you in this my heavy trouble; and if from the other end of the world you but write me word to do such or such a thing, you may regard it as done, for done it shall be, on the word and faith of——”\n\n“Hush!” interrupted the stranger; “I hear a noise.”\n\n“’Tis some travellers, who are visiting the Colosseum by torchlight.”\n\n“’Twere better we should not be seen together; those guides are nothing but spies, and might possibly recognize you; and, however I may be honored by your friendship, my worthy friend, if once the extent of our intimacy were known, I am sadly afraid both my reputation and credit would suffer thereby.”\n\n“Well, then, if you obtain the reprieve?”\n\n“The middle window at the Café Rospoli will be hung with white damask, bearing a red cross.”\n\n“And if you fail?”\n\n“Then all three windows will have yellow draperies.”\n\n“And then?”\n\n“And then, my good fellow, use your daggers in any way you please, and I further promise you to be there as a spectator of your prowess.”\n\n“We understand each other perfectly, then. Adieu, your excellency; depend upon me as firmly as I do upon you.”\n\nSaying these words, the Transteverin disappeared down the staircase, while his companion, muffling his features more closely than before in the folds of his mantle, passed almost close to Franz, and descended to the arena by an outward flight of steps. The next minute Franz heard himself called by Albert, who made the lofty building re-echo with the sound of his friend’s name. Franz, however, did not obey the summons till he had satisfied himself that the two men whose conversation he had overheard were at a sufficient distance to prevent his encountering them in his descent. In ten minutes after the strangers had departed, Franz was on the road to the Piazza di Spagna, listening with studied indifference to the learned dissertation delivered by Albert, after the manner of Pliny and Calpurnius, touching the iron-pointed nets used to prevent the ferocious beasts from springing on the spectators.\n\nFranz let him proceed without interruption, and, in fact, did not hear what was said; he longed to be alone, and free to ponder over all that had occurred. One of the two men, whose mysterious meeting in the Colosseum he had so unintentionally witnessed, was an entire stranger to him, but not so the other; and though Franz had been unable to distinguish his features, from his being either wrapped in his mantle or obscured by the shadow, the tones of his voice had made too powerful an impression on him the first time he had heard them for him ever again to forget them, hear them when or where he might. It was more especially when this man was speaking in a manner half jesting, half bitter, that Franz’s ear recalled most vividly the deep sonorous, yet well-pitched voice that had addressed him in the grotto of Monte Cristo, and which he heard for the second time amid the darkness and ruined grandeur of the Colosseum. And the more he thought, the more entire was his conviction, that the person who wore the mantle was no other than his former host and entertainer, “Sinbad the Sailor.”\n20139m\n\nUnder any other circumstances, Franz would have found it impossible to resist his extreme curiosity to know more of so singular a personage, and with that intent have sought to renew their short acquaintance; but in the present instance, the confidential nature of the conversation he had overheard made him, with propriety, judge that his appearance at such a time would be anything but agreeable. As we have seen, therefore, he permitted his former host to retire without attempting a recognition, but fully promising himself a rich indemnity for his present forbearance should chance afford him another opportunity.\n\nIn vain did Franz endeavor to forget the many perplexing thoughts which assailed him; in vain did he court the refreshment of sleep. Slumber refused to visit his eyelids and the night was passed in feverish contemplation of the chain of circumstances tending to prove the identity of the mysterious visitant to the Colosseum with the inhabitant of the grotto of Monte Cristo; and the more he thought, the firmer grew his opinion on the subject.\n\nWorn out at length, he fell asleep at daybreak, and did not awake till late. Like a genuine Frenchman, Albert had employed his time in arranging for the evening’s diversion; he had sent to engage a box at the Teatro Argentina; and Franz, having a number of letters to write, relinquished the carriage to Albert for the whole of the day.\n\nAt five o’clock Albert returned, delighted with his day’s work; he had been occupied in leaving his letters of introduction, and had received in return more invitations to balls and routs than it would be possible for him to accept; besides this, he had seen (as he called it) all the remarkable sights at Rome. Yes, in a single day he had accomplished what his more serious-minded companion would have taken weeks to effect. Neither had he neglected to ascertain the name of the piece to be played that night at the Teatro Argentina, and also what performers appeared in it. The opera of _Parisina_ was announced for representation, and the principal actors were Coselli, Moriani, and La Specchia.\n\nThe young men, therefore, had reason to consider themselves fortunate in having the opportunity of hearing one of the best works by the composer of _Lucia di Lammermoor_, supported by three of the most renowned vocalists of Italy.\n\nAlbert had never been able to endure the Italian theatres, with their orchestras from which it is impossible to see, and the absence of balconies, or open boxes; all these defects pressed hard on a man who had had his stall at the Bouffes, and had shared a lower box at the Opera. Still, in spite of this, Albert displayed his most dazzling and effective costumes each time he visited the theatres; but, alas, his elegant toilet was wholly thrown away, and one of the most worthy representatives of Parisian fashion had to carry with him the mortifying reflection that he had nearly overrun Italy without meeting with a single adventure.\n\nSometimes Albert would affect to make a joke of his want of success; but internally he was deeply wounded, and his self-love immensely piqued, to think that Albert de Morcerf, the most admired and most sought after of any young person of his day, should thus be passed over, and merely have his labor for his pains. And the thing was so much the more annoying, as, according to the characteristic modesty of a Frenchman, Albert had quitted Paris with the full conviction that he had only to show himself in Italy to carry all before him, and that upon his return he should astonish the Parisian world with the recital of his numerous love-affairs.\n\nAlas, poor Albert! None of those interesting adventures fell in his way; the lovely Genoese, Florentines, and Neapolitans were all faithful, if not to their husbands, at least to their lovers, and thought not of changing even for the splendid appearance of Albert de Morcerf; and all he gained was the painful conviction that the ladies of Italy have this advantage over those of France, that they are faithful even in their infidelity.\n\nYet he could not restrain a hope that in Italy, as elsewhere, there might be an exception to the general rule.\n\nAlbert, besides being an elegant, well-looking young man, was also possessed of considerable talent and ability; moreover, he was a viscount—a recently created one, certainly, but in the present day it is not necessary to go as far back as Noah in tracing a descent, and a genealogical tree is equally estimated, whether dated from 1399 or merely 1815; but to crown all these advantages, Albert de Morcerf commanded an income of 50,000 livres, a more than sufficient sum to render him a personage of considerable importance in Paris. It was therefore no small mortification to him to have visited most of the principal cities in Italy without having excited the most trifling observation.\n\nAlbert, however, hoped to indemnify himself for all these slights and indifferences during the Carnival, knowing full well that among the different states and kingdoms in which this festivity is celebrated, Rome is the spot where even the wisest and gravest throw off the usual rigidity of their lives, and deign to mingle in the follies of this time of liberty and relaxation. The Carnival was to commence on the morrow; therefore Albert had not an instant to lose in setting forth the programme of his hopes, expectations, and claims to notice.\n\nWith this design he had engaged a box in the most conspicuous part of the theatre, and exerted himself to set off his personal attractions by the aid of the most rich and elaborate toilet. The box taken by Albert was in the first circle; although each of the three tiers of boxes is deemed equally aristocratic, and is, for this reason, generally styled the “nobility’s boxes,” and although the box engaged for the two friends was sufficiently capacious to contain at least a dozen persons, it had cost less than would be paid at some of the French theatres for one admitting merely four occupants.\n\nAnother motive had influenced Albert’s selection of his seat,—who knew but that, thus advantageously placed, he might not in truth attract the notice of some fair Roman, and an introduction might ensue that would procure him the offer of a seat in a carriage, or a place in a princely balcony, from which he might behold the gayeties of the Carnival?\nThese united considerations made Albert more lively and anxious to please than he had hitherto been. Totally disregarding the business of the stage, he leaned from his box and began attentively scrutinizing the beauty of each pretty woman, aided by a powerful opera-glass; but, alas, this attempt to attract notice wholly failed; not even curiosity had been excited, and it was but too apparent that the lovely creatures, into whose good graces he was desirous of stealing, were all so much engrossed with themselves, their lovers, or their own thoughts, that they had not so much as noticed him or the manipulation of his glass.\n\nThe truth was, that the anticipated pleasures of the Carnival, with the “Holy Week” that was to succeed it, so filled every fair breast, as to prevent the least attention being bestowed even on the business of the stage. The actors made their entries and exits unobserved or unthought of; at certain conventional moments, the spectators would suddenly cease their conversation, or rouse themselves from their musings, to listen to some brilliant effort of Moriani’s, a well-executed recitative by Coselli, or to join in loud applause at the wonderful powers of La Specchia; but that momentary excitement over, they quickly relapsed into their former state of preoccupation or interesting conversation.\n\nTowards the close of the first act, the door of a box which had been hitherto vacant was opened; a lady entered to whom Franz had been introduced in Paris, where indeed, he had imagined she still was. The quick eye of Albert caught the involuntary start with which his friend beheld the new arrival, and, turning to him, he said hastily:\n\n“Do you know the woman who has just entered that box?”\n\n“Yes; what do you think of her?”\n\n“Oh, she is perfectly lovely—what a complexion! And such magnificent hair! Is she French?”\n\n“No; a Venetian.”\n\n“And her name is——”\n\n“Countess G——.”\n\n“Ah, I know her by name!” exclaimed Albert; “she is said to possess as much wit and cleverness as beauty. I was to have been presented to her when I met her at Madame Villefort’s ball.”\n\n“Shall I assist you in repairing your negligence?” asked Franz.\n\n“My dear fellow, are you really on such good terms with her as to venture to take me to her box?”\n\n“Why, I have only had the honor of being in her society and conversing with her three or four times in my life; but you know that even such an acquaintance as that might warrant my doing what you ask.”\n\nAt that instant, the countess perceived Franz, and graciously waved her hand to him, to which he replied by a respectful inclination of the head. “Upon my word,” said Albert, “you seem to be on excellent terms with the beautiful countess.”\n\n“You are mistaken in thinking so,” returned Franz calmly; “but you merely fall into the same error which leads so many of our countrymen to commit the most egregious blunders,—I mean that of judging the habits and customs of Italy and Spain by our Parisian notions; believe me, nothing is more fallacious than to form any estimate of the degree of intimacy you may suppose existing among persons by the familiar terms they seem upon; there is a similarity of feeling at this instant between ourselves and the countess—nothing more.”\n\n“Is there, indeed, my good fellow? Pray tell me, is it sympathy of heart?”\n\n“No; of taste,” continued Franz gravely.\n\n“And in what manner has this congeniality of mind been evinced?”\n\n“By the countess’s visiting the Colosseum, as we did last night, by moonlight, and nearly alone.”\n\n“You were with her, then?”\n\n“I was.”\n\n“And what did you say to her?”\n\n“Oh, we talked of the illustrious dead of whom that magnificent ruin is a glorious monument!”\n\n“Upon my word,” cried Albert, “you must have been a very entertaining companion alone, or all but alone, with a beautiful woman in such a place of sentiment as the Colosseum, and yet to find nothing better to talk about than the dead! All I can say is, if ever I should get such a chance, the living should be my theme.”\n\n20143m\n“And you will probably find your theme ill-chosen.”\n\n“But,” said Albert, breaking in upon his discourse, “never mind the past; let us only remember the present. Are you not going to keep your promise of introducing me to the fair subject of our remarks?”\n\n“Certainly, directly the curtain falls on the stage.”\n\n“What a confounded long time this first act lasts. I believe, on my soul, that they never mean to finish it.”\n\n“Oh, yes, they will; only listen to that charming finale. How exquisitely Coselli sings his part.”\n\n“But what an awkward, inelegant fellow he is.”\n\n“Well, then, what do you say to La Specchia? Did you ever see anything more perfect than her acting?”\n\n“Why, you know, my dear fellow, when one has been accustomed to Malibran and Sontag, such singers as these don’t make the same impression on you they perhaps do on others.”\n\n“At least, you must admire Moriani’s style and execution.”\n\n“I never fancied men of his dark, ponderous appearance singing with a voice like a woman’s.”\n\n“My good friend,” said Franz, turning to him, while Albert continued to point his glass at every box in the theatre, “you seem determined not to approve; you are really too difficult to please.”\n\nThe curtain at length fell on the performances, to the infinite satisfaction of the Viscount of Morcerf, who seized his hat, rapidly passed his fingers through his hair, arranged his cravat and wristbands, and signified to Franz that he was waiting for him to lead the way.\n\nFranz, who had mutely interrogated the countess, and received from her a gracious smile in token that he would be welcome, sought not to retard the gratification of Albert’s eager impatience, but began at once the tour of the house, closely followed by Albert, who availed himself of the few minutes required to reach the opposite side of the theatre to settle the height and smoothness of his collar, and to arrange the lappets of his coat. This important task was just completed as they arrived at the countess’s box.\n\nAt the knock, the door was immediately opened, and the young man who was seated beside the countess, in obedience to the Italian custom, instantly rose and surrendered his place to the strangers, who, in turn, would be expected to retire upon the arrival of other visitors.\nFranz presented Albert as one of the most distinguished young men of the day, both as regarded his position in society and extraordinary talents; nor did he say more than the truth, for in Paris and the circle in which the viscount moved, he was looked upon and cited as a model of perfection. Franz added that his companion, deeply grieved at having been prevented the honor of being presented to the countess during her sojourn in Paris, was most anxious to make up for it, and had requested him (Franz) to remedy the past misfortune by conducting him to her box, and concluded by asking pardon for his presumption in having taken it upon himself to do so.\n\nThe countess, in reply, bowed gracefully to Albert, and extended her hand with cordial kindness to Franz; then, inviting Albert to take the vacant seat beside her, she recommended Franz to take the next best, if he wished to view the ballet, and pointed to the one behind her own chair.\n\nAlbert was soon deeply engrossed in discoursing upon Paris and Parisian matters, speaking to the countess of the various persons they both knew there. Franz perceived how completely he was in his element; and, unwilling to interfere with the pleasure he so evidently felt, took up Albert’s glass, and began in his turn to survey the audience.\n\nSitting alone, in the front of a box immediately opposite, but situated on the third row, was a woman of exquisite beauty, dressed in a Greek costume, which evidently, from the ease and grace with which she wore it, was her national attire. Behind her, but in deep shadow, was the outline of a masculine figure; but the features of this latter personage it was not possible to distinguish. Franz could not forbear breaking in upon the apparently interesting conversation passing between the countess and Albert, to inquire of the former if she knew who was the fair Albanian opposite, since beauty such as hers was well worthy of being observed by either sex.\n\n“All I can tell about her,” replied the countess, “is, that she has been at Rome since the beginning of the season; for I saw her where she now sits the very first night of the season, and since then she has never missed a performance. Sometimes she is accompanied by the person who is now with her, and at others she is merely attended by a black servant.”\n\n“And what do you think of her personal appearance?”\n\n“Oh, I consider her perfectly lovely—she is just my idea of what Medora must have been.”\n\nFranz and the countess exchanged a smile, and then the latter resumed her conversation with Albert, while Franz returned to his previous survey of the house and company. The curtain rose on the ballet, which was one of those excellent specimens of the Italian school, admirably arranged and put on the stage by Henri, who has established for himself a great reputation throughout Italy for his taste and skill in the choreographic art—one of those masterly productions of grace, method, and elegance in which the whole _corps de ballet_, from the principal dancers to the humblest supernumerary, are all engaged on the stage at the same time; and a hundred and fifty persons may be seen exhibiting the same attitude, or elevating the same arm or leg with a simultaneous movement, that would lead you to suppose that but one mind, one act of volition, influenced the moving mass.\n\nThe ballet was called _Poliska_.\n\nHowever much the ballet might have claimed his attention, Franz was too deeply occupied with the beautiful Greek to take any note of it; while she seemed to experience an almost childlike delight in watching it, her eager, animated looks contrasting strongly with the utter indifference of her companion, who, during the whole time the piece lasted, never even moved, not even when the furious, crashing din produced by the trumpets, cymbals, and Chinese bells sounded their loudest from the orchestra. Of this he took no heed, but was, as far as appearances might be trusted, enjoying soft repose and bright celestial dreams.\nThe ballet at length came to a close, and the curtain fell amid the loud, unanimous plaudits of an enthusiastic and delighted audience.\n\nOwing to the very judicious plan of dividing the two acts of the opera with a ballet, the pauses between the performances are very short, the singers in the opera having time to repose themselves and change their costume, when necessary, while the dancers are executing their pirouettes and exhibiting their graceful steps.\n\nThe overture to the second act began; and, at the first sound of the leader’s bow across his violin, Franz observed the sleeper slowly arise and approach the Greek girl, who turned around to say a few words to him, and then, leaning forward again on the railing of her box, she became as absorbed as before in what was going on.\n\nThe countenance of the person who had addressed her remained so completely in the shade, that, though Franz tried his utmost, he could not distinguish a single feature. The curtain rose, and the attention of Franz was attracted by the actors; and his eyes turned from the box containing the Greek girl and her strange companion to watch the business of the stage.\n\nMost of my readers are aware that the second act of _Parisina_ opens with the celebrated and effective duet in which Parisina, while sleeping, betrays to Azzo the secret of her love for Ugo. The injured husband goes through all the emotions of jealousy, until conviction seizes on his mind, and then, in a frenzy of rage and indignation, he awakens his guilty wife to tell her that he knows her guilt and to threaten her with his vengeance.\n\nThis duet is one of the most beautiful, expressive and terrible conceptions that has ever emanated from the fruitful pen of Donizetti. Franz now listened to it for the third time; yet its notes, so tenderly expressive and fearfully grand as the wretched husband and wife give vent to their different griefs and passions, thrilled through the soul of Franz with an effect equal to his first emotions upon hearing it. Excited beyond his usual calm demeanor, Franz rose with the audience, and was about to join the loud, enthusiastic applause that followed; but suddenly his purpose was arrested, his hands fell by his sides, and the half-uttered “bravos” expired on his lips.\n\nThe occupant of the box in which the Greek girl sat appeared to share the universal admiration that prevailed; for he left his seat to stand up in front, so that, his countenance being fully revealed, Franz had no difficulty in recognizing him as the mysterious inhabitant of Monte Cristo, and the very same person he had encountered the preceding evening in the ruins of the Colosseum, and whose voice and figure had seemed so familiar to him.\n\nAll doubt of his identity was now at an end; his singular host evidently resided at Rome. The surprise and agitation occasioned by this full confirmation of Franz’s former suspicion had no doubt imparted a corresponding expression to his features; for the countess, after gazing with a puzzled look at his face, burst into a fit of laughter, and begged to know what had happened.\n\n“Countess,” returned Franz, totally unheeding her raillery, “I asked you a short time since if you knew any particulars respecting the Albanian lady opposite; I must now beseech you to inform me who and what is her husband?”\n\n“Nay,” answered the countess, “I know no more of him than yourself.”\n\n“Perhaps you never before noticed him?”\n\n“What a question—so truly French! Do you not know that we Italians have eyes only for the man we love?”\n\n“True,” replied Franz.\n\n“All I can say is,” continued the countess, taking up the _lorgnette_, and directing it toward the box in question, “that the gentleman, whose history I am unable to furnish, seems to me as though he had just been dug up; he looks more like a corpse permitted by some friendly grave-digger to quit his tomb for a while, and revisit this earth of ours, than anything human. How ghastly pale he is!”\n\n“Oh, he is always as colorless as you now see him,” said Franz.\n\n“Then you know him?” almost screamed the countess. “Oh, pray do, for heaven’s sake, tell us all about—is he a vampire, or a resuscitated corpse, or what?”\n\n“I fancy I have seen him before; and I even think he recognizes me.”\n\n“And I can well understand,” said the countess, shrugging up her beautiful shoulders, as though an involuntary shudder passed through her veins, “that those who have once seen that man will never be likely to forget him.”\n\nThe sensation experienced by Franz was evidently not peculiar to himself; another, and wholly uninterested person, felt the same unaccountable awe and misgiving.\n\n“Well.” inquired Franz, after the countess had a second time directed her _lorgnette_ at the box, “what do you think of our opposite neighbor?”\n20147m\n\n“Why, that he is no other than Lord Ruthven himself in a living form.”\n\nThis fresh allusion to Byron5 drew a smile to Franz’s countenance; although he could but allow that if anything was likely to induce belief in the existence of vampires, it would be the presence of such a man as the mysterious personage before him.\n\n“I must positively find out who and what he is,” said Franz, rising from his seat.\n\n“No, no,” cried the countess; “you must not leave me. I depend upon you to escort me home. Oh, indeed, I cannot permit you to go.”\n\n“Is it possible,” whispered Franz, “that you entertain any fear?”\n\n“I’ll tell you,” answered the countess. “Byron had the most perfect belief in the existence of vampires, and even assured me that he had seen them. The description he gave me perfectly corresponds with the features and character of the man before us. Oh, he is the exact personification of what I have been led to expect! The coal-black hair, large bright, glittering eyes, in which a wild, unearthly fire seems burning,—the same ghastly paleness. Then observe, too, that the woman with him is altogether unlike all others of her sex. She is a foreigner—a stranger. Nobody knows who she is, or where she comes from. No doubt she belongs to the same horrible race he does, and is, like himself, a dealer in magical arts. I entreat of you not to go near him—at least tonight; and if tomorrow your curiosity still continues as great, pursue your researches if you will; but tonight you neither can nor shall. For that purpose I mean to keep you all to myself.”\n\nFranz protested he could not defer his pursuit till the following day, for many reasons.\n\n“Listen to me,” said the countess, “and do not be so very headstrong. I am going home. I have a party at my house tonight, and therefore cannot possibly remain till the end of the opera. Now, I cannot for one instant believe you so devoid of gallantry as to refuse a lady your escort when she even condescends to ask you for it.”\n\nThere was nothing else left for Franz to do but to take up his hat, open the door of the box, and offer the countess his arm. It was quite evident, by her manner, that her uneasiness was not feigned; and Franz himself could not resist a feeling of superstitious dread—so much the stronger in him, as it arose from a variety of corroborative recollections, while the terror of the countess sprang from an instinctive belief, originally created in her mind by the wild tales she had listened to till she believed them truths. Franz could even feel her arm tremble as he assisted her into the carriage. Upon arriving at her hotel, Franz perceived that she had deceived him when she spoke of expecting company; on the contrary, her own return before the appointed hour seemed greatly to astonish the servants.\n\n“Excuse my little subterfuge,” said the countess, in reply to her companion’s half-reproachful observation on the subject; “but that horrid man had made me feel quite uncomfortable, and I longed to be alone, that I might compose my startled mind.”\n\nFranz essayed to smile.\n\n“Nay,” said she, “do not smile; it ill accords with the expression of your countenance, and I am sure it does not spring from your heart. However, promise me one thing.”\n\n“What is it?”\n\n“Promise me, I say.”\n\n“I will do anything you desire, except relinquish my determination of finding out who this man is. I have more reasons than you can imagine for desiring to know who he is, from whence he came, and whither he is going.”\n\n“Where he comes from I am ignorant; but I can readily tell you where he is going to, and that is down below, without the least doubt.”\n\n“Let us only speak of the promise you wished me to make,” said Franz.\n\n“Well, then, you must give me your word to return immediately to your hotel, and make no attempt to follow this man tonight. There are certain affinities between the persons we quit and those we meet afterwards. For heaven’s sake, do not serve as a conductor between that man and me. Pursue your chase after him tomorrow as eagerly as you please; but never bring him near me, if you would not see me die of terror. And now, good-night; go to your rooms, and try to sleep away all recollections of this evening. For my own part, I am quite sure I shall not be able to close my eyes.”\n\nSo saying, the countess quitted Franz, leaving him unable to decide whether she were merely amusing herself at his expense, or whether her fears and agitations were genuine.\n\nUpon his return to the hotel, Franz found Albert in his dressing-gown and slippers, listlessly extended on a sofa, smoking a cigar.\n\n“My dear fellow!” cried he, springing up, “is it really you? Why, I did not expect to see you before tomorrow.”\n\n“My dear Albert,” replied Franz, “I am glad of this opportunity to tell you, once and forever, that you entertain a most erroneous notion concerning Italian women. I should have thought the continual failures you have met with in all your own love affairs might have taught you better by this time.”\n\n“Upon my soul, these women would puzzle the very Devil to read them aright. Why, here—they give you their hand—they press yours in return—they keep up a whispering conversation—permit you to accompany them home. Why, if a Parisian were to indulge in a quarter of these marks of flattering attention, her reputation would be gone forever.”\n\n“And the very reason why the women of this fine country, ‘where sounds the _si_,’ as Dante writes, put so little restraint on their words and actions, is because they live so much in public, and have really nothing to conceal. Besides, you must have perceived that the countess was really alarmed.”\n\n“At what? At the sight of that respectable gentleman sitting opposite to us in the same box with the lovely Greek girl? Now, for my part, I met them in the lobby after the conclusion of the piece; and hang me, if I can guess where you took your notions of the other world from. I can assure you that this hobgoblin of yours is a deuced fine-looking fellow—admirably dressed. Indeed, I feel quite sure, from the cut of his clothes, they are made by a first-rate Paris tailor—probably Blin or Humann. He was rather too pale, certainly; but then, you know, paleness is always looked upon as a strong proof of aristocratic descent and distinguished breeding.”\n\nFranz smiled; for he well remembered that Albert particularly prided himself on the entire absence of color in his own complexion.\n\n“Well, that tends to confirm my own ideas,” said Franz, “that the countess’s suspicions were destitute alike of sense and reason. Did he speak in your hearing? and did you catch any of his words?”\n\n“I did; but they were uttered in the Romaic dialect. I knew that from the mixture of Greek words. I don’t know whether I ever told you that when I was at college I was rather—rather strong in Greek.”\n\n“He spoke the Romaic language, did he?”\n\n“I think so.”\n\n“That settles it,” murmured Franz. “’Tis he, past all doubt.”\n\n“What do you say?”\n\n“Nothing, nothing. But tell me, what were you thinking about when I came in?”\n\n“Oh, I was arranging a little surprise for you.”\n\n“Indeed. Of what nature?”\n\n“Why, you know it is quite impossible to procure a carriage.”\n\n“Certainly; and I also know that we have done all that human means afforded to endeavor to get one.”\n\n“Now, then, in this difficulty a bright idea has flashed across my brain.”\n\nFranz looked at Albert as though he had not much confidence in the suggestions of his imagination.\n\n“I tell you what, M. Franz,” cried Albert, “you deserve to be called out for such a misgiving and incredulous glance as that you were pleased to bestow on me just now.”\n\n“And I promise to give you the satisfaction of a gentleman if your scheme turns out as ingenious as you assert.”\n\n“Well, then, hearken to me.”\n\n“I listen.”\n\n“You agree, do you not, that obtaining a carriage is out of the question?”\n“I do.”\n\n“Neither can we procure horses?”\n\n“True; we have offered any sum, but have failed.”\n\n“Well, now, what do you say to a cart? I dare say such a thing might be had.”\n\n“Very possibly.”\n\n“And a pair of oxen?”\n\n“As easily found as the cart.”\n\n“Then you see, my good fellow, with a cart and a couple of oxen our business can be managed. The cart must be tastefully ornamented; and if you and I dress ourselves as Neapolitan reapers, we may get up a striking tableau, after the manner of that splendid picture by Léopold Robert. It would add greatly to the effect if the countess would join us in the costume of a peasant from Puzzoli or Sorrento. Our group would then be quite complete, more especially as the countess is quite beautiful enough to represent a Madonna.”\n\n“Well,” said Franz, “this time, M. Albert, I am bound to give you credit for having hit upon a most capital idea.”\n\n“And quite a national one, too,” replied Albert with gratified pride. “A mere masque borrowed from our own festivities. Ha, ha, ye Romans! you thought to make us, unhappy strangers, trot at the heels of your processions, like so many lazzaroni, because no carriages or horses are to be had in your beggarly city. But you don’t know us; when we can’t have one thing we invent another.”\n\n“And have you communicated your triumphant idea to anybody?”\n\n“Only to our host. Upon my return home I sent for him, and I then explained to him what I wished to procure. He assured me that nothing would be easier than to furnish all I desired. One thing I was sorry for; when I bade him have the horns of the oxen gilded, he told me there would not be time, as it would require three days to do that; so you see we must do without this little superfluity.”\n\n“And where is he now?”\n“Who?”\n\n“Our host.”\n\n“Gone out in search of our equipage, by tomorrow it might be too late.”\n\n“Then he will be able to give us an answer tonight.”\n\n“Oh, I expect him every minute.”\n\nAt this instant the door opened, and the head of Signor Pastrini appeared. “_Permesso_?” inquired he.\n\n“Certainly—certainly,” cried Franz. “Come in, my host.”\n\n“Now, then,” asked Albert eagerly, “have you found the desired cart and oxen?”\n\n“Better than that!” replied Signor Pastrini, with the air of a man perfectly well satisfied with himself.\n\n“Take care, my worthy host,” said Albert, “_better_ is a sure enemy to _well_.”\n\n“Let your excellencies only leave the matter to me,” returned Signor Pastrini in a tone indicative of unbounded self-confidence.\n\n“But what _have_ you done?” asked Franz. “Speak out, there’s a worthy fellow.”\n\n“Your excellencies are aware,” responded the landlord, swelling with importance, “that the Count of Monte Cristo is living on the same floor with yourselves!”\n\n“I should think we did know it,” exclaimed Albert, “since it is owing to that circumstance that we are packed into these small rooms, like two poor students in the back streets of Paris.”\n\n“When, then, the Count of Monte Cristo, hearing of the dilemma in which you are placed, has sent to offer you seats in his carriage and two places at his windows in the Palazzo Rospoli.” The friends looked at each other with unutterable surprise.\n\n“But do you think,” asked Albert, “that we ought to accept such offers from a perfect stranger?”\n\n“What sort of person is this Count of Monte Cristo?” asked Franz of his host.\n\n“A very great nobleman, but whether Maltese or Sicilian I cannot exactly say; but this I know, that he is noble as a Borghese and rich as a gold mine.”\n\n“It seems to me,” said Franz, speaking in an undertone to Albert, “that if this person merited the high panegyrics of our landlord, he would have conveyed his invitation through another channel, and not permitted it to be brought to us in this unceremonious way. He would have written—or——”\n\nAt this instant someone knocked at the door.\n\n“Come in,” said Franz.\n\nA servant, wearing a livery of considerable style and richness, appeared at the threshold, and, placing two cards in the landlord’s hands, who forthwith presented them to the two young men, he said:\n\n“Please to deliver these, from the Count of Monte Cristo to Vicomte Albert de Morcerf and M. Franz d’Épinay. The Count of Monte Cristo,” continued the servant, “begs these gentlemen’s permission to wait upon them as their neighbor, and he will be honored by an intimation of what time they will please to receive him.”\n\n“Faith, Franz,” whispered Albert, “there is not much to find fault with here.”\n\n“Tell the count,” replied Franz, “that we will do ourselves the pleasure of calling on him.”\n\nThe servant bowed and retired.\n\n“That is what I call an elegant mode of attack,” said Albert, “You were quite correct in what you said, Signor Pastrini. The Count of Monte Cristo is unquestionably a man of first-rate breeding and knowledge of the world.”\n\n“Then you accept his offer?” said the host.\n\n“Of course we do,” replied Albert. “Still, I must own I am sorry to be obliged to give up the cart and the group of reapers—it would have produced such an effect! And were it not for the windows at the Palazzo Rospoli, by way of recompense for the loss of our beautiful scheme, I don’t know but what I should have held on by my original plan. What say you, Franz?”\n\n“Oh, I agree with you; the windows in the Palazzo Rospoli alone decided me.”\n\nThe truth was, that the mention of two places in the Palazzo Rospoli had recalled to Franz the conversation he had overheard the preceding evening in the ruins of the Colosseum between the mysterious unknown and the Transteverin, in which the stranger in the cloak had undertaken to obtain the freedom of a condemned criminal; and if this muffled-up individual proved (as Franz felt sure he would) the same as the person he had just seen in the Teatro Argentina, then he should be able to establish his identity, and also to prosecute his researches respecting him with perfect facility and freedom.\n\nFranz passed the night in confused dreams respecting the two meetings he had already had with his mysterious tormentor, and in waking speculations as to what the morrow would produce. The next day must clear up every doubt; and unless his near neighbor and would-be friend, the Count of Monte Cristo, possessed the ring of Gyges, and by its power was able to render himself invisible, it was very certain he could not escape this time.\n\nEight o’clock found Franz up and dressed, while Albert, who had not the same motives for early rising, was still soundly asleep. The first act of Franz was to summon his landlord, who presented himself with his accustomed obsequiousness.\n\n“Pray, Signor Pastrini,” asked Franz, “is not some execution appointed to take place today?”\n\n“Yes, your excellency; but if your reason for inquiry is that you may procure a window to view it from, you are much too late.”\n\n“Oh, no,” answered Franz, “I had no such intention; and even if I had felt a wish to witness the spectacle, I might have done so from Monte Pincio; could I not?”\n\n“Ah!” exclaimed mine host, “I did not think it likely your excellency would have chosen to mingle with such a rabble as are always collected on that hill, which, indeed, they consider as exclusively belonging to themselves.”\n\n“Very possibly I may not go,” answered Franz; “but in case I feel disposed, give me some particulars of today’s executions.”\n\n“What particulars would your excellency like to hear?”\n\n“Why, the number of persons condemned to suffer, their names, and description of the death they are to die.”\n\n“That happens just lucky, your excellency! Only a few minutes ago they brought me the _tavolettas_.”\n\n“What are they?”\n\n“Sort of wooden tablets hung up at the corners of streets the evening before an execution, on which is pasted up a paper containing the names of the condemned persons, their crimes, and mode of punishment. The reason for so publicly announcing all this is, that all good and faithful Catholics may offer up their prayers for the unfortunate culprits, and, above all, beseech of Heaven to grant them a sincere repentance.”\n\n“And these tablets are brought to you that you may add your prayers to those of the faithful, are they?” asked Franz somewhat incredulously.\n\n“Oh, dear, no, your excellency! I have not time for anybody’s affairs but my own and those of my honorable guests; but I make an agreement with the man who pastes up the papers, and he brings them to me as he would the playbills, that in case any person staying at my hotel should like to witness an execution, he may obtain every requisite information concerning the time and place etc.”\n\n“Upon my word, that is a most delicate attention on your part, Signor Pastrini,” cried Franz.\n\n“Why, your excellency,” returned the landlord, chuckling and rubbing his hands with infinite complacency, “I think I may take upon myself to say I neglect nothing to deserve the support and patronage of the noble visitors to this poor hotel.”\n\n“I see that plainly enough, my most excellent host, and you may rely upon me to proclaim so striking a proof of your attention to your guests wherever I go. Meanwhile, oblige me by a sight of one of these _tavolettas_.”\n\n“Nothing can be easier than to comply with your excellency’s wish,” said the landlord, opening the door of the chamber; “I have caused one to be placed on the landing, close by your apartment.”\n\nThen, taking the tablet from the wall, he handed it to Franz, who read as follows:\n\n“‘The public is informed that on Wednesday, February 23rd, being the first day of the Carnival, executions will take place in the Piazza del Popolo, by order of the Tribunal of the Rota, of two persons, named Andrea Rondolo, and Peppino, otherwise called Rocca Priori; the former found guilty of the murder of a venerable and exemplary priest, named Don César Torlini, canon of the church of St. John Lateran; and the latter convicted of being an accomplice of the atrocious and sanguinary bandit, Luigi Vampa, and his band. The first-named malefactor will be _mazzolato_, the second culprit _decapitato_.\n\n“‘The prayers of all good Christians are entreated for these unfortunate men, that it may please God to awaken them to a sense of their guilt, and to grant them a hearty and sincere repentance for their crimes.’”\n\nThis was precisely what Franz had heard the evening before in the ruins of the Colosseum. No part of the programme differed,—the names of the condemned persons, their crimes, and mode of punishment, all agreed with his previous information. In all probability, therefore, the Transteverin was no other than the bandit Luigi Vampa himself, and the man shrouded in the mantle the same he had known as “Sinbad the Sailor,” but who, no doubt, was still pursuing his philanthropic expedition in Rome, as he had already done at Porto-Vecchio and Tunis.\n\nTime was getting on, however, and Franz deemed it advisable to awaken Albert; but at the moment he prepared to proceed to his chamber, his friend entered the room in perfect costume for the day. The anticipated delights of the Carnival had so run in his head as to make him leave his pillow long before his usual hour.\n\n“Now, my excellent Signor Pastrini,” said Franz, addressing his landlord, “since we are both ready, do you think we may proceed at once to visit the Count of Monte Cristo?”\n\n“Most assuredly,” replied he. “The Count of Monte Cristo is always an early riser; and I can answer for his having been up these two hours.”\n\n“Then you really consider we shall not be intruding if we pay our respects to him directly?”\n20155m\n\n“Oh, I am quite sure. I will take all the blame on myself if you find I have led you into an error.”\n\n“Well, then, if it be so, are you ready, Albert?”\n\n“Perfectly.”\n\n“Let us go and return our best thanks for his courtesy.”\n\n“Yes, let us do so.”\n\nThe landlord preceded the friends across the landing, which was all that separated them from the apartments of the count, rang at the bell, and, upon the door being opened by a servant, said:\n\n“_I signori Francesi_.”\n\nThe domestic bowed respectfully, and invited them to enter. They passed through two rooms, furnished in a luxurious manner they had not expected to see under the roof of Signor Pastrini, and were shown into an elegantly fitted-up drawing-room. The richest Turkey carpets covered the floor, and the softest and most inviting couches, easy-chairs, and sofas, offered their high-piled and yielding cushions to such as desired repose or refreshment. Splendid paintings by the first masters were ranged against the walls, intermingled with magnificent trophies of war, while heavy curtains of costly tapestry were suspended before the different doors of the room.\n\n“If your excellencies will please to be seated,” said the man, “I will let the count know that you are here.”\n\nAnd with these words he disappeared behind one of the tapestried _portières_. As the door opened, the sound of a _guzla_ reached the ears of the young men, but was almost immediately lost, for the rapid closing of the door merely allowed one rich swell of harmony to enter. Franz and Albert looked inquiringly at each other, then at the gorgeous furnishings of the apartment. Everything seemed more magnificent at a second view than it had done at their first rapid survey.\n\n“Well,” said Franz to his friend, “what think you of all this?”\n\n“Why, upon my soul, my dear fellow, it strikes me that our elegant and attentive neighbor must either be some successful stock-jobber who has speculated in the fall of the Spanish funds, or some prince travelling _incog_.”\n\n“Hush, hush!” replied Franz; “we shall ascertain who and what he is—he comes!”\n\nAs Franz spoke, he heard the sound of a door turning on its hinges, and almost immediately afterwards the tapestry was drawn aside, and the owner of all these riches stood before the two young men. Albert instantly rose to meet him, but Franz remained, in a manner, spellbound on his chair; for in the person of him who had just entered he recognized not only the mysterious visitant to the Colosseum, and the occupant of the box at the Teatro Argentina, but also his extraordinary host of Monte Cristo.\n20157m\n\n# Chapter 35. La Mazzolata\n\nGentlemen,” said the Count of Monte Cristo as he entered, “I pray you excuse me for suffering my visit to be anticipated; but I feared to disturb you by presenting myself earlier at your apartments; besides, you sent me word that you would come to me, and I have held myself at your disposal.”\n\n“Franz and I have to thank you a thousand times, count,” returned Albert; “you extricated us from a great dilemma, and we were on the point of inventing a very fantastic vehicle when your friendly invitation reached us.”\n\n“Indeed,” returned the count, motioning the two young men to sit down. “It was the fault of that blockhead Pastrini, that I did not sooner assist you in your distress. He did not mention a syllable of your embarrassment to me, when he knows that, alone and isolated as I am, I seek every opportunity of making the acquaintance of my neighbors. As soon as I learned I could in any way assist you, I most eagerly seized the opportunity of offering my services.”\n\nThe two young men bowed. Franz had, as yet, found nothing to say; he had come to no determination, and as nothing in the count’s manner manifested the wish that he should recognize him, he did not know whether to make any allusion to the past, or wait until he had more proof; besides, although sure it was he who had been in the box the previous evening, he could not be equally positive that this was the man he had seen at the Colosseum. He resolved, therefore, to let things take their course without making any direct overture to the count. Moreover, he had this advantage, he was master of the count’s secret, while the count had no hold on Franz, who had nothing to conceal. However, he resolved to lead the conversation to a subject which might possibly clear up his doubts.\n\n“Count,” said he, “you have offered us places in your carriage, and at your windows in the Rospoli Palace. Can you tell us where we can obtain a sight of the Piazza del Popolo?”\n\n“Ah,” said the count negligently, looking attentively at Morcerf, “is there not something like an execution upon the Piazza del Popolo?”\n\n“Yes,” returned Franz, finding that the count was coming to the point he wished.\n\n“Stay, I think I told my steward yesterday to attend to this; perhaps I can render you this slight service also.”\n\nHe extended his hand, and rang the bell thrice.\n\n“Did you ever occupy yourself,” said he to Franz, “with the employment of time and the means of simplifying the summoning your servants? I have. When I ring once, it is for my valet; twice, for my majordomo; thrice, for my steward,—thus I do not waste a minute or a word. Here he is.”\n\nA man of about forty-five or fifty entered, exactly resembling the smuggler who had introduced Franz into the cavern; but he did not appear to recognize him. It was evident he had his orders.\n\n“Monsieur Bertuccio,” said the count, “you have procured me windows looking on the Piazza del Popolo, as I ordered you yesterday.”\n\n“Yes, excellency,” returned the steward; “but it was very late.”\n\n“Did I not tell you I wished for one?” replied the count, frowning.\n\n“And your excellency has one, which was let to Prince Lobanieff; but I was obliged to pay a hundred——”\n\n“That will do—that will do, Monsieur Bertuccio; spare these gentlemen all such domestic arrangements. You have the window, that is sufficient. Give orders to the coachman; and be in readiness on the stairs to conduct us to it.”\n\nThe steward bowed, and was about to quit the room.\n\n“Ah!” continued the count, “be good enough to ask Pastrini if he has received the _tavoletta_, and if he can send us an account of the execution.”\n\n“There is no need to do that,” said Franz, taking out his tablets; “for I saw the account, and copied it down.”\n\n“Very well, you can retire, M. Bertuccio; I need you no longer. Let us know when breakfast is ready. These gentlemen,” added he, turning to the two friends, “will, I trust, do me the honor to breakfast with me?”\n\n“But, my dear count,” said Albert, “we shall abuse your kindness.”\n\n“Not at all; on the contrary, you will give me great pleasure. You will, one or other of you, perhaps both, return it to me at Paris. M. Bertuccio, lay covers for three.”\n\nHe then took Franz’s tablets out of his hand. “‘We announce,’ he read, in the same tone with which he would have read a newspaper, ‘that today, the 23rd of February, will be executed Andrea Rondolo, guilty of murder on the person of the respected and venerated Don César Torlini, canon of the church of St. John Lateran, and Peppino, called Rocca Priori, convicted of complicity with the detestable bandit Luigi Vampa, and the men of his band.’\n\n“Hum! ‘The first will be _mazzolato_, the second _decapitato_.’ Yes,” continued the count, “it was at first arranged in this way; but I think since yesterday some change has taken place in the order of the ceremony.”\n\n“Really?” said Franz.\n\n“Yes, I passed the evening at the Cardinal Rospigliosi’s, and there mention was made of something like a pardon for one of the two men.”\n\n“For Andrea Rondolo?” asked Franz.\n\n“No,” replied the count, carelessly; “for the other (he glanced at the tablets as if to recall the name), for Peppino, called Rocca Priori. You are thus deprived of seeing a man guillotined; but the _mazzolata_ still remains, which is a very curious punishment when seen for the first time, and even the second, while the other, as you must know, is very simple. The _mandaïa_6 never fails, never trembles, never strikes thirty times ineffectually, like the soldier who beheaded the Count of Chalais, and to whose tender mercy Richelieu had doubtless recommended the sufferer. Ah,” added the count, in a contemptuous tone, “do not tell me of European punishments, they are in the infancy, or rather the old age, of cruelty.”\n\n“Really, count,” replied Franz, “one would think that you had studied the different tortures of all the nations of the world.”\n\n“There are, at least, few that I have not seen,” said the count coldly.\n\n“And you took pleasure in beholding these dreadful spectacles?”\n\n“My first sentiment was horror, the second indifference, the third curiosity.”\n\n“Curiosity—that is a terrible word.”\n\n“Why so? In life, our greatest preoccupation is death; is it not then, curious to study the different ways by which the soul and body can part; and how, according to their different characters, temperaments, and even the different customs of their countries, different persons bear the transition from life to death, from existence to annihilation? As for myself, I can assure you of one thing,—the more men you see die, the easier it becomes to die yourself; and in my opinion, death may be a torture, but it is not an expiation.”\n\n“I do not quite understand you,” replied Franz; “pray explain your meaning, for you excite my curiosity to the highest pitch.”\n\n“Listen,” said the count, and deep hatred mounted to his face, as the blood would to the face of any other. “If a man had by unheard-of and excruciating tortures destroyed your father, your mother, your betrothed,—a being who, when torn from you, left a desolation, a wound that never closes, in your breast,—do you think the reparation that society gives you is sufficient when it interposes the knife of the guillotine between the base of the occiput and the trapezal muscles of the murderer, and allows him who has caused us years of moral sufferings to escape with a few moments of physical pain?”\n\n“Yes, I know,” said Franz, “that human justice is insufficient to console us; she can give blood in return for blood, that is all; but you must demand from her only what it is in her power to grant.”\n\n“I will put another case to you,” continued the count; “that where society, attacked by the death of a person, avenges death by death. But are there not a thousand tortures by which a man may be made to suffer without society taking the least cognizance of them, or offering him even the insufficient means of vengeance, of which we have just spoken? Are there not crimes for which the impalement of the Turks, the augers of the Persians, the stake and the brand of the Iroquois Indians, are inadequate tortures, and which are unpunished by society? Answer me, do not these crimes exist?”\n\n“Yes,” answered Franz; “and it is to punish them that duelling is tolerated.”\n\n“Ah, duelling,” cried the count; “a pleasant manner, upon my soul, of arriving at your end when that end is vengeance! A man has carried off your mistress, a man has seduced your wife, a man has dishonored your daughter; he has rendered the whole life of one who had the right to expect from Heaven that portion of happiness God has promised to everyone of his creatures, an existence of misery and infamy; and you think you are avenged because you send a ball through the head, or pass a sword through the breast, of that man who has planted madness in your brain, and despair in your heart. And remember, moreover, that it is often he who comes off victorious from the strife, absolved of all crime in the eyes of the world. No, no,” continued the count, “had I to avenge myself, it is not thus I would take revenge.”\n\n“Then you disapprove of duelling? You would not fight a duel?” asked Albert in his turn, astonished at this strange theory.\n\n“Oh, yes,” replied the count; “understand me, I would fight a duel for a trifle, for an insult, for a blow; and the more so that, thanks to my skill in all bodily exercises, and the indifference to danger I have gradually acquired, I should be almost certain to kill my man. Oh, I would fight for such a cause; but in return for a slow, profound, eternal torture, I would give back the same, were it possible; an eye for an eye, a tooth for a tooth, as the Orientalists say,—our masters in everything,—those favored creatures who have formed for themselves a life of dreams and a paradise of realities.”\n\n“But,” said Franz to the count, “with this theory, which renders you at once judge and executioner of your own cause, it would be difficult to adopt a course that would forever prevent your falling under the power of the law. Hatred is blind, rage carries you away; and he who pours out vengeance runs the risk of tasting a bitter draught.”\n\n“Yes, if he be poor and inexperienced, not if he be rich and skilful; besides, the worst that could happen to him would be the punishment of which we have already spoken, and which the philanthropic French Revolution has substituted for being torn to pieces by horses or broken on the wheel. What matters this punishment, as long as he is avenged? On my word, I almost regret that in all probability this miserable Peppino will not be beheaded, as you might have had an opportunity then of seeing how short a time the punishment lasts, and whether it is worth even mentioning; but, really this is a most singular conversation for the Carnival, gentlemen; how did it arise? Ah, I recollect, you asked for a place at my window; you shall have it; but let us first sit down to table, for here comes the servant to inform us that breakfast is ready.”\n\nAs he spoke, a servant opened one of the four doors of the apartment, saying:\n\n“_Al suo commodo!_”\n\nThe two young men arose and entered the breakfast-room.\n\nDuring the meal, which was excellent, and admirably served, Franz looked repeatedly at Albert, in order to observe the impressions which he doubted not had been made on him by the words of their entertainer; but whether with his usual carelessness he had paid but little attention to him, whether the explanation of the Count of Monte Cristo with regard to duelling had satisfied him, or whether the events which Franz knew of had had their effect on him alone, he remarked that his companion did not pay the least regard to them, but on the contrary ate like a man who for the last four or five months had been condemned to partake of Italian cookery—that is, the worst in the world.\n\nAs for the count, he just touched the dishes; he seemed to fulfil the duties of a host by sitting down with his guests, and awaited their departure to be served with some strange or more delicate food. This brought back to Franz, in spite of himself, the recollection of the terror with which the count had inspired the Countess G——, and her firm conviction that the man in the opposite box was a vampire.\n\nAt the end of the breakfast Franz took out his watch.\n\n“Well,” said the count, “what are you doing?”\n\n“You must excuse us, count,” returned Franz, “but we have still much to do.”\n\n“What may that be?”\n\n“We have no masks, and it is absolutely necessary to procure them.”\n\n“Do not concern yourself about that; we have, I think, a private room in the Piazza del Popolo; I will have whatever costumes you choose brought to us, and you can dress there.”\n\n“After the execution?” cried Franz.\n\n“Before or after, whichever you please.”\n\n“Opposite the scaffold?”\n\n“The scaffold forms part of the _fête_.”\n\n“Count, I have reflected on the matter,” said Franz, “I thank you for your courtesy, but I shall content myself with accepting a place in your carriage and at your window at the Rospoli Palace, and I leave you at liberty to dispose of my place at the Piazza del Popolo.”\n\n“But I warn you, you will lose a very curious sight,” returned the count.\n\n“You will describe it to me,” replied Franz, “and the recital from your lips will make as great an impression on me as if I had witnessed it. I have more than once intended witnessing an execution, but I have never been able to make up my mind; and you, Albert?”\n\n“I,” replied the viscount,—“I saw Castaing executed, but I think I was rather intoxicated that day, for I had quitted college the same morning, and we had passed the previous night at a tavern.”\n\n“Besides, it is no reason because you have not seen an execution at Paris, that you should not see one anywhere else; when you travel, it is to see everything. Think what a figure you will make when you are asked, ‘How do they execute at Rome?’ and you reply, ‘I do not know!’ And, besides, they say that the culprit is an infamous scoundrel, who killed with a log of wood a worthy canon who had brought him up like his own son. _Diable!_ when a churchman is killed, it should be with a different weapon than a log, especially when he has behaved like a father. If you went to Spain, would you not see the bull-fights? Well, suppose it is a bull-fight you are going to see? Recollect the ancient Romans of the Circus, and the sports where they killed three hundred lions and a hundred men. Think of the eighty thousand applauding spectators, the sage matrons who took their daughters, and the charming Vestals who made with the thumb of their white hands the fatal sign that said, ‘Come, despatch the dying.’”\n\n“Shall you go, then, Albert?” asked Franz.\n\n“_Ma foi_, yes; like you, I hesitated, but the count’s eloquence decides me.”\n\n“Let us go, then,” said Franz, “since you wish it; but on our way to the Piazza del Popolo, I wish to pass through the Corso. Is this possible, count?”\n\n“On foot, yes, in a carriage, no.”\n\n“I will go on foot, then.”\n\n“Is it important that you should go that way?”\n\n“Yes, there is something I wish to see.”\n\n“Well, we will go by the Corso. We will send the carriage to wait for us on the Piazza del Popolo, by the Via del Babuino, for I shall be glad to pass, myself, through the Corso, to see if some orders I have given have been executed.”\n\n“Excellency,” said a servant, opening the door, “a man in the dress of a penitent wishes to speak to you.”\n\n“Ah! yes,” returned the count, “I know who he is, gentlemen; will you return to the salon? you will find good cigars on the centre table. I will be with you directly.”\n\nThe young men rose and returned into the salon, while the count, again apologizing, left by another door. Albert, who was a great smoker, and who had considered it no small sacrifice to be deprived of the cigars of the Café de Paris, approached the table, and uttered a cry of joy at perceiving some veritable _puros_.\n\n“Well,” asked Franz, “what think you of the Count of Monte Cristo?”\n\n“What do I think?” said Albert, evidently surprised at such a question from his companion; “I think he is a delightful fellow, who does the honors of his table admirably; who has travelled much, read much, is, like Brutus, of the Stoic school, and moreover,” added he, sending a volume of smoke up towards the ceiling, “that he has excellent cigars.”\n\nSuch was Albert’s opinion of the count, and as Franz well knew that Albert professed never to form an opinion except upon long reflection, he made no attempt to change it.\n\n“But,” said he, “did you observe one very singular thing?”\n\n“What?”\n\n“How attentively he looked at you.”\n\n“At me?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nAlbert reflected. “Ah,” replied he, sighing, “that is not very surprising; I have been more than a year absent from Paris, and my clothes are of a most antiquated cut; the count takes me for a provincial. The first opportunity you have, undeceive him, I beg, and tell him I am nothing of the kind.”\n\nFranz smiled; an instant after the count entered.\n\n“I am now quite at your service, gentlemen,” said he. “The carriage is going one way to the Piazza del Popolo, and we will go another; and, if you please, by the Corso. Take some more of these cigars, M. de Morcerf.”\n\n“With all my heart,” returned Albert; “Italian cigars are horrible. When you come to Paris, I will return all this.”\n\n“I will not refuse; I intend going there soon, and since you allow me, I will pay you a visit. Come, we have not any time to lose, it is half-past twelve—let us set off.”\n\nAll three descended; the coachman received his master’s orders, and drove down the Via del Babuino. While the three gentlemen walked along the Piazza di Spagna and the Via Frattina, which led directly between the Fiano and Rospoli palaces, Franz’s attention was directed towards the windows of that last palace, for he had not forgotten the signal agreed upon between the man in the mantle and the Transtevere peasant.\n\n“Which are your windows?” asked he of the count, with as much indifference as he could assume.\n\n“The three last,” returned he, with a negligence evidently unaffected, for he could not imagine with what intention the question was put.\n\nFranz glanced rapidly towards the three windows. The side windows were hung with yellow damask, and the centre one with white damask and a red cross. The man in the mantle had kept his promise to the Transteverin, and there could now be no doubt that he was the count.\n\nThe three windows were still untenanted. Preparations were making on every side; chairs were placed, scaffolds were raised, and windows were hung with flags. The masks could not appear; the carriages could not move about; but the masks were visible behind the windows, the carriages, and the doors.\n\nFranz, Albert, and the count continued to descend the Corso. As they approached the Piazza del Popolo, the crowd became more dense, and above the heads of the multitude two objects were visible: the obelisk, surmounted by a cross, which marks the centre of the square, and in front of the obelisk, at the point where the three streets, del Babuino, del Corso, and di Ripetta, meet, the two uprights of the scaffold, between which glittered the curved knife of the _mandaïa_.\n\nAt the corner of the street they met the count’s steward, who was awaiting his master. The window, let at an exorbitant price, which the count had doubtless wished to conceal from his guests, was on the second floor of the great palace, situated between the Via del Babuino and the Monte Pincio. It consisted, as we have said, of a small dressing-room, opening into a bedroom, and, when the door of communication was shut, the inmates were quite alone. On chairs were laid elegant masquerade costumes of blue and white satin.\n20167m\n\n“As you left the choice of your costumes to me,” said the count to the two friends, “I have had these brought, as they will be the most worn this year; and they are most suitable, on account of the _confetti_ (sweetmeats), as they do not show the flour.”\n\nFranz heard the words of the count but imperfectly, and he perhaps did not fully appreciate this new attention to their wishes; for he was wholly absorbed by the spectacle that the Piazza del Popolo presented, and by the terrible instrument that was in the centre.\n\nIt was the first time Franz had ever seen a guillotine,—we say guillotine, because the Roman _mandaïa_ is formed on almost the same model as the French instrument.7 The knife, which is shaped like a crescent, that cuts with the convex side, falls from a less height, and that is all the difference.\n\nTwo men, seated on the movable plank on which the victim is laid, were eating their breakfasts, while waiting for the criminal. Their repast consisted apparently of bread and sausages. One of them lifted the plank, took out a flask of wine, drank some, and then passed it to his companion. These two men were the executioner’s assistants.\n\nAt this sight Franz felt the perspiration start forth upon his brow.\n\nThe prisoners, transported the previous evening from the Carceri Nuove to the little church of Santa Maria del Popolo, had passed the night, each accompanied by two priests, in a chapel closed by a grating, before which were two sentinels, who were relieved at intervals. A double line of carbineers, placed on each side of the door of the church, reached to the scaffold, and formed a circle around it, leaving a path about ten feet wide, and around the guillotine a space of nearly a hundred feet.\n\nAll the rest of the square was paved with heads. Many women held their infants on their shoulders, and thus the children had the best view. The Monte Pincio seemed a vast amphitheatre filled with spectators; the balconies of the two churches at the corner of the Via del Babuino and the Via di Ripetta were crammed; the steps even seemed a parti-colored sea, that was impelled towards the portico; every niche in the wall held its living statue. What the count said was true—the most curious spectacle in life is that of death.\n\nAnd yet, instead of the silence and the solemnity demanded by the occasion, laughter and jests arose from the crowd. It was evident that the execution was, in the eyes of the people, only the commencement of the Carnival.\n\nSuddenly the tumult ceased, as if by magic, and the doors of the church opened. A brotherhood of penitents, clothed from head to foot in robes of gray sackcloth, with holes for the eyes, and holding in their hands lighted tapers, appeared first; the chief marched at the head.\n20169m\n\nBehind the penitents came a man of vast stature and proportions. He was naked, with the exception of cloth drawers at the left side of which hung a large knife in a sheath, and he bore on his right shoulder a heavy iron sledge-hammer.\n\nThis man was the executioner.\n\nHe had, moreover, sandals bound on his feet by cords.\n\nBehind the executioner came, in the order in which they were to die, first Peppino and then Andrea. Each was accompanied by two priests. Neither had his eyes bandaged.\n\nPeppino walked with a firm step, doubtless aware of what awaited him. Andrea was supported by two priests. Each of them, from time to time, kissed the crucifix a confessor held out to them.\n\nAt this sight alone Franz felt his legs tremble under him. He looked at Albert—he was as white as his shirt, and mechanically cast away his cigar, although he had not half smoked it. The count alone seemed unmoved—nay, more, a slight color seemed striving to rise in his pale cheeks. His nostrils dilated like those of a wild beast that scents its prey, and his lips, half opened, disclosed his white teeth, small and sharp like those of a jackal. And yet his features wore an expression of smiling tenderness, such as Franz had never before witnessed in them; his black eyes especially were full of kindness and pity.\n\nHowever, the two culprits advanced, and as they approached their faces became visible. Peppino was a handsome young man of four or five-and-twenty, bronzed by the sun; he carried his head erect, and seemed on the watch to see on which side his liberator would appear. Andrea was short and fat; his visage, marked with brutal cruelty, did not indicate age; he might be thirty. In prison he had suffered his beard to grow; his head fell on his shoulder, his legs bent beneath him, and his movements were apparently automatic and unconscious.\n\n“I thought,” said Franz to the count, “that you told me there would be but one execution.”\n\n“I told you true,” replied he coldly.\n\n“And yet here are two culprits.”\n\n“Yes; but only one of these two is about to die; the other has many years to live.”\n\n“If the pardon is to come, there is no time to lose.”\n\n“And see, here it is,” said the count. At the moment when Peppino reached the foot of the _mandaïa_, a priest arrived in some haste, forced his way through the soldiers, and, advancing to the chief of the brotherhood, gave him a folded paper. The piercing eye of Peppino had noticed all. The chief took the paper, unfolded it, and, raising his hand, “Heaven be praised, and his Holiness also,” said he in a loud voice; “here is a pardon for one of the prisoners!”\n\n“A pardon!” cried the people with one voice; “a pardon!”\n\nAt this cry Andrea raised his head.\n\n“Pardon for whom?” cried he.\n\nPeppino remained breathless.\n\n“A pardon for Peppino, called Rocca Priori,” said the principal friar. And he passed the paper to the officer commanding the carbineers, who read and returned it to him.\n\n“For Peppino!” cried Andrea, who seemed roused from the torpor in which he had been plunged. “Why for him and not for me? We ought to die together. I was promised he should die with me. You have no right to put me to death alone. I will not die alone—I will not!”\n\nAnd he broke from the priests struggling and raving like a wild beast, and striving desperately to break the cords that bound his hands. The executioner made a sign, and his two assistants leaped from the scaffold and seized him.\n\n“What is going on?” asked Franz of the count; for, as all the talk was in the Roman dialect, he had not perfectly understood it.\n\n“Do you not see?” returned the count, “that this human creature who is about to die is furious that his fellow-sufferer does not perish with him? and, were he able, he would rather tear him to pieces with his teeth and nails than let him enjoy the life he himself is about to be deprived of. Oh, man, man—race of crocodiles,” cried the count, extending his clenched hands towards the crowd, “how well do I recognize you there, and that at all times you are worthy of yourselves!”\n\nMeanwhile Andrea and the two executioners were struggling on the ground, and he kept exclaiming, “He ought to die!—he shall die!—I will not die alone!”\n\n“Look, look,” cried the count, seizing the young men’s hands; “look, for on my soul it is curious. Here is a man who had resigned himself to his fate, who was going to the scaffold to die—like a coward, it is true, but he was about to die without resistance. Do you know what gave him strength? do you know what consoled him? It was, that another partook of his punishment—that another partook of his anguish—that another was to die before him! Lead two sheep to the butcher’s, two oxen to the slaughterhouse, and make one of them understand that his companion will not die; the sheep will bleat for pleasure, the ox will bellow with joy. But man—man, whom God created in his own image—man, upon whom God has laid his first, his sole commandment, to love his neighbor—man, to whom God has given a voice to express his thoughts—what is his first cry when he hears his fellow-man is saved? A blasphemy. Honor to man, this masterpiece of nature, this king of the creation!”\n\nAnd the count burst into a laugh; a terrible laugh, that showed he must have suffered horribly to be able thus to laugh.\n\nHowever, the struggle still continued, and it was dreadful to witness. The two assistants carried Andrea up to the scaffold; the people all took part against Andrea, and twenty thousand voices cried, “Put him to death! put him to death!”\n\nFranz sprang back, but the count seized his arm, and held him before the window.\n\n“What are you doing?” said he. “Do you pity him? If you heard the cry of ‘Mad dog!’ you would take your gun—you would unhesitatingly shoot the poor beast, who, after all, was only guilty of having been bitten by another dog. And yet you pity a man who, without being bitten by one of his race, has yet murdered his benefactor; and who, now unable to kill anyone, because his hands are bound, wishes to see his companion in captivity perish. No, no—look, look!”\n20172m\n\nThe recommendation was needless. Franz was fascinated by the horrible spectacle.\n\nThe two assistants had borne Andrea to the scaffold, and there, in spite of his struggles, his bites, and his cries, had forced him to his knees. During this time the executioner had raised his mace, and signed to them to get out of the way; the criminal strove to rise, but, ere he had time, the mace fell on his left temple. A dull and heavy sound was heard, and the man dropped like an ox on his face, and then turned over on his back.\n\nThe executioner let fall his mace, drew his knife, and with one stroke opened his throat, and mounting on his stomach, stamped violently on it with his feet. At every stroke a jet of blood sprang from the wound.\n\nThis time Franz could contain himself no longer, but sank, half fainting, into a seat.\n\nAlbert, with his eyes closed, was standing grasping the window-curtains.\n\nThe count was erect and triumphant, like the Avenging Angel!\n\n# Chapter 36. The Carnival at Rome\n\nWhen Franz recovered his senses, he saw Albert drinking a glass of water, of which, to judge from his pallor, he stood in great need; and the count, who was assuming his masquerade costume. He glanced mechanically towards the piazza—the scene was wholly changed; scaffold, executioners, victims, all had disappeared; only the people remained, full of noise and excitement. The bell of Monte Citorio, which only sounds on the pope’s decease and the opening of the Carnival, was ringing a joyous peal.\n\n“Well,” asked he of the count, “what has, then, happened?”\n\n“Nothing,” replied the count; “only, as you see, the Carnival has commenced. Make haste and dress yourself.”\n\n“In fact,” said Franz, “this horrible scene has passed away like a dream.”\n\n“It is but a dream, a nightmare, that has disturbed you.”\n\n“Yes, that I have suffered; but the culprit?”\n\n“That is a dream also; only he has remained asleep, while you have awakened; and who knows which of you is the most fortunate?”\n\n“But Peppino—what has become of him?”\n\n“Peppino is a lad of sense, who, unlike most men, who are happy in proportion as they are noticed, was delighted to see that the general attention was directed towards his companion. He profited by this distraction to slip away among the crowd, without even thanking the worthy priests who accompanied him. Decidedly man is an ungrateful and egotistical animal. But dress yourself; see, M. de Morcerf sets you the example.”\n\nAlbert was drawing on the satin pantaloon over his black trousers and varnished boots.\n\n“Well, Albert,” said Franz, “do you feel much inclined to join the revels? Come, answer frankly.”\n\n“_Ma foi_, no,” returned Albert. “But I am really glad to have seen such a sight; and I understand what the count said—that when you have once habituated yourself to a similar spectacle, it is the only one that causes you any emotion.”\n\n20175m\n“Without reflecting that this is the only moment in which you can study character,” said the count; “on the steps of the scaffold death tears off the mask that has been worn through life, and the real visage is disclosed. It must be allowed that Andrea was not very handsome, the hideous scoundrel! Come, dress yourselves, gentlemen, dress yourselves.”\n\nFranz felt it would be ridiculous not to follow his two companions’ example. He assumed his costume, and fastened on the mask that scarcely equalled the pallor of his own face. Their toilet finished, they descended; the carriage awaited them at the door, filled with sweetmeats and bouquets. They fell into the line of carriages.\n\nIt is difficult to form an idea of the perfect change that had taken place. Instead of the spectacle of gloomy and silent death, the Piazza del Popolo presented a spectacle of gay and noisy mirth and revelry. A crowd of masks flowed in from all sides, emerging from the doors, descending from the windows. From every street and every corner drove carriages filled with clowns, harlequins, dominoes, mummers, pantomimists, Transteverins, knights, and peasants, screaming, fighting, gesticulating, throwing eggs filled with flour, confetti, nosegays, attacking, with their sarcasms and their missiles, friends and foes, companions and strangers, indiscriminately, and no one took offence, or did anything but laugh.\n\nFranz and Albert were like men who, to drive away a violent sorrow, have recourse to wine, and who, as they drink and become intoxicated, feel a thick veil drawn between the past and the present. They saw, or rather continued to see, the image of what they had witnessed; but little by little the general vertigo seized them, and they felt themselves obliged to take part in the noise and confusion.\n\nA handful of confetti that came from a neighboring carriage, and which, while it covered Morcerf and his two companions with dust, pricked his neck and that portion of his face uncovered by his mask like a hundred pins, incited him to join in the general combat, in which all the masks around him were engaged. He rose in his turn, and seizing handfuls of confetti and sweetmeats, with which the carriage was filled, cast them with all the force and skill he was master of.\n20177m\n\nThe strife had fairly begun, and the recollection of what they had seen half an hour before was gradually effaced from the young men’s minds, so much were they occupied by the gay and glittering procession they now beheld.\n\nAs for the Count of Monte Cristo, he had never for an instant shown any appearance of having been moved. Imagine the large and splendid Corso, bordered from one end to the other with lofty palaces, with their balconies hung with carpets, and their windows with flags. At these balconies are three hundred thousand spectators—Romans, Italians, strangers from all parts of the world, the united aristocracy of birth, wealth, and genius. Lovely women, yielding to the influence of the scene, bend over their balconies, or lean from their windows, and shower down confetti, which are returned by bouquets; the air seems darkened with the falling confetti and flying flowers. In the streets the lively crowd is dressed in the most fantastic costumes—gigantic cabbages walk gravely about, buffaloes’ heads bellow from men’s shoulders, dogs walk on their hind legs; in the midst of all this a mask is lifted, and, as in Callot’s Temptation of St. Anthony, a lovely face is exhibited, which we would fain follow, but from which we are separated by troops of fiends. This will give a faint idea of the Carnival at Rome.\n\nAt the second turn, the count stopped the carriage, and requested permission to withdraw, leaving the vehicle at their disposal. Franz looked up—they were opposite the Rospoli Palace. At the centre window, the one hung with white damask with a red cross, was a blue domino, beneath which Franz’s imagination easily pictured the beautiful Greek of the Argentina.\n\n“Gentlemen,” said the count, springing out, “when you are tired of being actors, and wish to become spectators of this scene, you know you have places at my windows. In the meantime, dispose of my coachman, my carriage, and my servants.”\n\nWe have forgotten to mention, that the count’s coachman was attired in a bear-skin, exactly resembling Odry’s in _The Bear and the Pasha_; and the two footmen behind were dressed up as green monkeys, with spring masks, with which they made grimaces at everyone who passed.\n\nFranz thanked the count for his attention. As for Albert, he was busily occupied throwing bouquets at a carriage full of Roman peasants that was passing near him. Unfortunately for him, the line of carriages moved on again, and while he descended the Piazza del Popolo, the other ascended towards the Palazzo di Venezia.\n\n“Ah, my dear fellow,” said he to Franz; “you did not see?”\n\n“What?”\n\n“There,—that calash filled with Roman peasants.”\n“No.”\n\n“Well, I am convinced they are all charming women.”\n\n“How unfortunate that you were masked, Albert,” said Franz; “here was an opportunity of making up for past disappointments.”\n\n“Oh,” replied he, half laughing, half serious; “I hope the Carnival will not pass without some amends in one shape or the other.”\n\nBut, in spite of Albert’s hope, the day passed unmarked by any incident, excepting two or three encounters with the carriage full of Roman peasants. At one of these encounters, accidentally or purposely, Albert’s mask fell off. He instantly rose and cast the remainder of the bouquets into the carriage. Doubtless one of the charming females Albert had detected beneath their coquettish disguise was touched by his gallantry; for, as the carriage of the two friends passed her, she threw a bunch of violets. Albert seized it, and as Franz had no reason to suppose it was meant for him, he suffered Albert to retain it. Albert placed it in his button-hole, and the carriage went triumphantly on.\n\n“Well,” said Franz to him; “there is the beginning of an adventure.”\n\n“Laugh if you please—I really think so. So I will not abandon this bouquet.”\n\n“_Pardieu_,” returned Franz, laughing, “in token of your ingratitude.”\n\nThe jest, however, soon appeared to become earnest; for when Albert and Franz again encountered the carriage with the _contadini_, the one who had thrown the violets to Albert, clapped her hands when she beheld them in his button-hole.\n\n“Bravo, bravo,” said Franz; “things go wonderfully. Shall I leave you? Perhaps you would prefer being alone?”\n\n“No,” replied he; “I will not be caught like a fool at a first disclosure by a rendezvous under the clock, as they say at the opera-balls. If the fair peasant wishes to carry matters any further, we shall find her, or rather, she will find us tomorrow; then she will give me some sign or other, and I shall know what I have to do.”\n\n“On my word,” said Franz, “you are as wise as Nestor and prudent as Ulysses, and your fair Circe must be very skilful or very powerful if she succeed in changing you into a beast of any kind.”\n\nAlbert was right; the fair unknown had resolved, doubtless, to carry the intrigue no farther; for although the young men made several more turns, they did not again see the calash, which had turned up one of the neighboring streets. Then they returned to the Rospoli Palace; but the count and the blue domino had also disappeared; the two windows, hung with yellow damask, were still occupied by the persons whom the count had invited.\n\nAt this moment the same bell that had proclaimed the beginning of the mascherata sounded the retreat. The file on the Corso broke the line, and in a second all the carriages had disappeared. Franz and Albert were opposite the Via delle Muratte; the coachman, without saying a word, drove up it, passed along the Piazza di Spagna and the Rospoli Palace and stopped at the door of the hotel. Signor Pastrini came to the door to receive his guests.\n\nFranz hastened to inquire after the count, and to express regret that he had not returned in sufficient time; but Pastrini reassured him by saying that the Count of Monte Cristo had ordered a second carriage for himself, and that it had gone at four o’clock to fetch him from the Rospoli Palace.\n\nThe count had, moreover, charged him to offer the two friends the key of his box at the Argentina. Franz questioned Albert as to his intentions; but Albert had great projects to put into execution before going to the theatre; and instead of making any answer, he inquired if Signor Pastrini could procure him a tailor.\n\n“A tailor,” said the host; “and for what?”\n\n“To make us between now and tomorrow two Roman peasant costumes,” returned Albert.\n\nThe host shook his head.\n\n“To make you two costumes between now and tomorrow? I ask your excellencies’ pardon, but this is quite a French demand; for the next week you will not find a single tailor who would consent to sew six buttons on a waistcoat if you paid him a crown a piece for each button.”\n\n“Then I must give up the idea?”\n\n“No; we have them ready-made. Leave all to me; and tomorrow, when you awake, you shall find a collection of costumes with which you will be satisfied.”\n\n“My dear Albert,” said Franz, “leave all to our host; he has already proved himself full of resources; let us dine quietly, and afterwards go and see _l’Italienne à Alger!_\n\n“Agreed,” returned Albert; “but remember, Signor Pastrini, that both my friend and myself attach the greatest importance to having tomorrow the costumes we have asked for.”\n\nThe host again assured them they might rely on him, and that their wishes should be attended to; upon which Franz and Albert mounted to their apartments, and proceeded to disencumber themselves of their costumes. Albert, as he took off his dress, carefully preserved the bunch of violets; it was his token reserved for the morrow.\n\nThe two friends sat down to table; but they could not refrain from remarking the difference between the Count of Monte Cristo’s table and that of Signor Pastrini. Truth compelled Franz, in spite of the dislike he seemed to have taken to the count, to confess that the advantage was not on Pastrini’s side. During dessert, the servant inquired at what time they wished for the carriage. Albert and Franz looked at each other, fearing really to abuse the count’s kindness. The servant understood them.\n\n“His excellency the Count of Monte Cristo had,” he said, “given positive orders that the carriage was to remain at their lordships’ orders all day, and they could therefore dispose of it without fear of indiscretion.”\n\nThey resolved to profit by the count’s courtesy, and ordered the horses to be harnessed, while they substituted evening dress for that which they had on, and which was somewhat the worse for the numerous combats they had sustained.\n\n20181m\nThis precaution taken, they went to the theatre, and installed themselves in the count’s box. During the first act, the Countess G—— entered. Her first look was at the box where she had seen the count the previous evening, so that she perceived Franz and Albert in the place of the very person concerning whom she had expressed so strange an opinion to Franz. Her opera-glass was so fixedly directed towards them, that Franz saw it would be cruel not to satisfy her curiosity; and, availing himself of one of the privileges of the spectators of the Italian theatres, who use their boxes to hold receptions, the two friends went to pay their respects to the countess. Scarcely had they entered, when she motioned to Franz to assume the seat of honor. Albert, in his turn, sat behind.\n\n“Well,” said she, hardly giving Franz time to sit down, “it seems you have nothing better to do than to make the acquaintance of this new Lord Ruthven, and you are already the best friends in the world.”\n\n“Without being so far advanced as that, my dear countess,” returned Franz, “I cannot deny that we have abused his good nature all day.”\n\n“All day?”\n\n“Yes; this morning we breakfasted with him; we rode in his carriage all day, and now we have taken possession of his box.”\n\n“You know him, then?”\n\n“Yes, and no.”\n“How so?”\n\n“It is a long story.”\n\n“Tell it to me.”\n\n“It would frighten you too much.”\n\n“So much the more reason.”\n\n“At least wait until the story has a conclusion.”\n\n“Very well; I prefer complete histories; but tell me how you made his acquaintance? Did anyone introduce you to him?”\n\n“No; it was he who introduced himself to us.”\n\n“When?”\n\n“Last night, after we left you.”\n\n“Through what medium?”\n\n“The very prosaic one of our landlord.”\n\n“He is staying, then, at the Hôtel de Londres with you?”\n\n“Not only in the same hotel, but on the same floor.”\n\n“What is his name; for, of course, you know?”\n\n“The Count of Monte Cristo.”\n\n“That is not a family name?”\n\n“No, it is the name of the island he has purchased.”\n\n“And he is a count?”\n\n“A Tuscan count.”\n\n“Well, we must put up with that,” said the countess, who was herself from one of the oldest Venetian families. “What sort of a man is he?”\n\n“Ask the Vicomte de Morcerf.”\n\n“You hear, M. de Morcerf, I am referred to you,” said the countess.\n\n“We should be very hard to please, madam,” returned Albert, “did we not think him delightful. A friend of ten years’ standing could not have done more for us, or with a more perfect courtesy.”\n\n“Come,” observed the countess, smiling, “I see my vampire is only some millionaire, who has taken the appearance of Lara in order to avoid being confounded with M. de Rothschild; and you have seen her?”\n\n“Her?”\n20183m\n\n“The beautiful Greek of yesterday.”\n\n“No; we heard, I think, the sound of her _guzla_, but she remained perfectly invisible.”\n\n“When you say invisible,” interrupted Albert, “it is only to keep up the mystery; for whom do you take the blue domino at the window with the white curtains?”\n\n“Where was this window with white hangings?” asked the countess.\n\n“At the Rospoli Palace.”\n\n“The count had three windows at the Rospoli Palace?”\n\n“Yes. Did you pass through the Corso?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Well, did you notice two windows hung with yellow damask, and one with white damask with a red cross? Those were the count’s windows.”\n\n“Why, he must be a nabob. Do you know what those three windows were worth?”\n\n“Two or three hundred Roman crowns?”\n\n“Two or three thousand.”\n\n“The deuce!”\n\n“Does his island produce him such a revenue?”\n\n“It does not bring him a bajocco.”\n\n“Then why did he purchase it?”\n\n“For a whim.”\n\n“He is an original, then?”\n\n“In reality,” observed Albert, “he seemed to me somewhat eccentric; were he at Paris, and a frequenter of the theatres, I should say he was a poor devil literally mad. This morning he made two or three exits worthy of Didier or Anthony.”\n\nAt this moment a fresh visitor entered, and, according to custom, Franz gave up his seat to him. This circumstance had, moreover, the effect of changing the conversation; an hour afterwards the two friends returned to their hotel.\n\nSignor Pastrini had already set about procuring their disguises for the morrow; and he assured them that they would be perfectly satisfied. The next morning, at nine o’clock, he entered Franz’s room, followed by a tailor, who had eight or ten Roman peasant costumes on his arm; they selected two exactly alike, and charged the tailor to sew on each of their hats about twenty yards of ribbon, and to procure them two of the long silk sashes of different colors with which the lower orders decorate themselves on fête days.\n\nAlbert was impatient to see how he looked in his new dress—a jacket and breeches of blue velvet, silk stockings with clocks, shoes with buckles, and a silk waistcoat. This picturesque attire set him off to great advantage; and when he had bound the scarf around his waist, and when his hat, placed coquettishly on one side, let fall on his shoulder a stream of ribbons, Franz was forced to confess that costume has much to do with the physical superiority we accord to certain nations. The Turks used to be so picturesque with their long and flowing robes, but are they not now hideous with their blue frocks buttoned up to the chin, and their red caps, which make them look like a bottle of wine with a red seal? Franz complimented Albert, who looked at himself in the glass with an unequivocal smile of satisfaction. They were thus engaged when the Count of Monte Cristo entered.\n20185m\n\n“Gentlemen,” said he, “although a companion is agreeable, perfect freedom is sometimes still more agreeable. I come to say that today, and for the remainder of the Carnival, I leave the carriage entirely at your disposal. The host will tell you I have three or four more, so that you will not inconvenience me in any way. Make use of it, I pray you, for your pleasure or your business.”\n\nThe young men wished to decline, but they could find no good reason for refusing an offer which was so agreeable to them. The Count of Monte Cristo remained a quarter of an hour with them, conversing on all subjects with the greatest ease. He was, as we have already said, perfectly well acquainted with the literature of all countries. A glance at the walls of his salon proved to Franz and Albert that he was a connoisseur of pictures. A few words he let fall showed them that he was no stranger to the sciences, and he seemed much occupied with chemistry. The two friends did not venture to return the count the breakfast he had given them; it would have been too absurd to offer him in exchange for his excellent table the very inferior one of Signor Pastrini. They told him so frankly, and he received their excuses with the air of a man who appreciated their delicacy. Albert was charmed with the count’s manners, and he was only prevented from recognizing him for a perfect gentleman by reason of his varied knowledge.\n\nThe permission to do what he liked with the carriage pleased him above all, for the fair peasants had appeared in a most elegant carriage the preceding evening, and Albert was not sorry to be upon an equal footing with them. At half-past one they descended, the coachman and footman had put on their livery over their disguises, which gave them a more ridiculous appearance than ever, and which gained them the applause of Franz and Albert. Albert had fastened the faded bunch of violets to his button-hole. At the first sound of the bell they hastened into the Corso by the Via Vittoria.\n\nAt the second turn, a bunch of fresh violets, thrown from a carriage filled with harlequins, indicated to Albert that, like himself and his friend, the peasants had changed their costume also; and whether it was the result of chance, or whether a similar feeling had possessed them both, while he had donned their costume, they had assumed his.\n\nAlbert placed the fresh bouquet in his button-hole, but he kept the faded one in his hand; and when he again met the calash, he raised it to his lips, an action which seemed greatly to amuse not only the fair lady who had thrown it, but her joyous companions also. The day was as gay as the preceding one, perhaps even more animated and noisy; the count appeared for an instant at his window, but when they again passed he had disappeared. It is almost needless to say that the flirtation between Albert and the fair peasant continued all day.\n\nIn the evening, on his return, Franz found a letter from the embassy, informing him that he would have the honor of being received by his holiness the next day. At each previous visit he had made to Rome, he had solicited and obtained the same favor; and incited as much by a religious feeling as by gratitude, he was unwilling to quit the capital of the Christian world without laying his respectful homage at the feet of one of St. Peter’s successors who has set the rare example of all the virtues. He did not then think of the Carnival, for in spite of his condescension and touching kindness, one cannot incline one’s self without awe before the venerable and noble old man called Gregory XVI.\n\nOn his return from the Vatican, Franz carefully avoided the Corso; he brought away with him a treasure of pious thoughts, to which the mad gayety of the maskers would have been profanation.\n\nAt ten minutes past five Albert entered overjoyed. The harlequin had reassumed her peasant’s costume, and as she passed she raised her mask. She was charming. Franz congratulated Albert, who received his congratulations with the air of a man conscious that they are merited. He had recognized by certain unmistakable signs, that his fair _incognita_ belonged to the aristocracy. He had made up his mind to write to her the next day.\n\nFranz remarked, while he gave these details, that Albert seemed to have something to ask of him, but that he was unwilling to ask it. He insisted upon it, declaring beforehand that he was willing to make any sacrifice the other wished.\n\nAlbert let himself be pressed just as long as friendship required, and then avowed to Franz that he would do him a great favor by allowing him to occupy the carriage alone the next day. Albert attributed to Franz’s absence the extreme kindness of the fair peasant in raising her mask. Franz was not sufficiently egotistical to stop Albert in the middle of an adventure that promised to prove so agreeable to his curiosity and so flattering to his vanity. He felt assured that the perfect indiscretion of his friend would duly inform him of all that happened; and as, during three years that he had travelled all over Italy, a similar piece of good fortune had never fallen to his share, Franz was by no means sorry to learn how to act on such an occasion. He therefore promised Albert that he would content himself the morrow with witnessing the Carnival from the windows of the Rospoli Palace.\n\nThe next morning he saw Albert pass and repass, holding an enormous bouquet, which he doubtless meant to make the bearer of his amorous epistle. This belief was changed into certainty when Franz saw the bouquet (conspicuous by a circle of white camellias) in the hand of a charming harlequin dressed in rose-colored satin.\n\nThe evening was no longer joy, but delirium. Albert nothing doubted but that the fair unknown would reply in the same manner. Franz anticipated his wishes by saying that the noise fatigued him, and that he should pass the next day in writing and looking over his journal. Albert was not deceived, for the next evening Franz saw him enter triumphantly shaking a folded paper which he held by one corner.\n\n“Well,” said he, “was I mistaken?”\n\n“She has answered you!” cried Franz.\n“Read.”\n\nThis word was pronounced in a manner impossible to describe. Franz took the letter, and read:\n\n“Tuesday evening, at seven o’clock, descend from your carriage opposite the Via dei Pontefici, and follow the Roman peasant who snatches your torch from you. When you arrive at the first step of the church of San Giacomo, be sure to fasten a knot of rose-colored ribbons to the shoulder of your harlequin costume, in order that you may be recognized. Until then you will not see me. —Constancy and Discretion.”\n\n“Well,” asked he, when Franz had finished, “what do you think of that?”\n\n“I think that the adventure is assuming a very agreeable appearance.”\n\n“I think so, also,” replied Albert; “and I very much fear you will go alone to the Duke of Bracciano’s ball.”\n\nFranz and Albert had received that morning an invitation from the celebrated Roman banker.\n\n“Take care, Albert,” said Franz. “All the nobility of Rome will be present, and if your fair _incognita_ belong to the higher class of society, she must go there.”\n\n“Whether she goes there or not, my opinion is still the same,” returned Albert. “You have read the letter?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You know how imperfectly the women of the _mezzo cito_ are educated in Italy?” (This is the name of the lower class.)\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Well, read the letter again. Look at the writing, and find if you can, any blemish in the language or orthography.” The writing was, in reality, charming, and the orthography irreproachable.\n\n“You are born to good fortune,” said Franz, as he returned the letter.\n\n“Laugh as much as you will,” replied Albert, “I am in love.”\n\n“You alarm me,” cried Franz. “I see that I shall not only go alone to the Duke of Bracciano’s, but also return to Florence alone.”\n\n“If my unknown be as amiable as she is beautiful,” said Albert, “I shall fix myself at Rome for six weeks, at least. I adore Rome, and I have always had a great taste for archæology.”\n20189m\n\n“Come, two or three more such adventures, and I do not despair of seeing you a member of the Academy.”\n\nDoubtless Albert was about to discuss seriously his right to the academic chair when they were informed that dinner was ready. Albert’s love had not taken away his appetite. He hastened with Franz to seat himself, free to recommence the discussion after dinner. After dinner, the Count of Monte Cristo was announced. They had not seen him for two days. Signor Pastrini informed them that business had called him to Civita Vecchia. He had started the previous evening, and had only returned an hour since. He was charming. Whether he kept a watch over himself, or whether by accident he did not sound the acrimonious chords that in other circumstances had been touched, he was tonight like everybody else.\n\nThe man was an enigma to Franz. The count must feel sure that Franz recognized him; and yet he had not let fall a single word indicating any previous acquaintance between them. On his side, however great Franz’s desire was to allude to their former interview, the fear of being disagreeable to the man who had loaded him and his friend with kindness prevented him from mentioning it.\n\nThe count had learned that the two friends had sent to secure a box at the Argentina Theatre, and were told they were all let. In consequence, he brought them the key of his own—at least such was the apparent motive of his visit. Franz and Albert made some difficulty, alleging their fear of depriving him of it; but the count replied that, as he was going to the Palli Theatre, the box at the Argentina Theatre would be lost if they did not profit by it. This assurance determined the two friends to accept it.\n\nFranz had by degrees become accustomed to the count’s pallor, which had so forcibly struck him at their first meeting. He could not refrain from admiring the severe beauty of his features, the only defect, or rather the principal quality of which was the pallor. Truly, a Byronic hero! Franz could not, we will not say see him, but even think of him without imagining his stern head upon Manfred’s shoulders, or beneath Lara’s helmet. His forehead was marked with the line that indicates the constant presence of bitter thoughts; he had the fiery eyes that seem to penetrate to the very soul, and the haughty and disdainful upper lip that gives to the words it utters a peculiar character that impresses them on the minds of those to whom they are addressed.\n\nThe count was no longer young. He was at least forty; and yet it was easy to understand that he was formed to rule the young men with whom he associated at present. And, to complete his resemblance with the fantastic heroes of the English poet, the count seemed to have the power of fascination. Albert was constantly expatiating on their good fortune in meeting such a man. Franz was less enthusiastic; but the count exercised over him also the ascendency a strong mind always acquires over a mind less domineering. He thought several times of the project the count had of visiting Paris; and he had no doubt but that, with his eccentric character, his characteristic face, and his colossal fortune, he would produce a great effect there. And yet he did not wish to be at Paris when the count was there.\n\nThe evening passed as evenings mostly pass at Italian theatres; that is, not in listening to the music, but in paying visits and conversing. The Countess G—— wished to revive the subject of the count, but Franz announced he had something far newer to tell her, and, in spite of Albert’s demonstrations of false modesty, he informed the countess of the great event which had preoccupied them for the last three days. As similar intrigues are not uncommon in Italy, if we may credit travellers, the comtess did not manifest the least incredulity, but congratulated Albert on his success. They promised, upon separating, to meet at the Duke of Bracciano’s ball, to which all Rome was invited.\n\nThe heroine of the bouquet kept her word; she gave Albert no sign of her existence the morrow or the day after.\n\nAt length Tuesday came, the last and most tumultuous day of the Carnival. On Tuesday, the theatres open at ten o’clock in the morning, as Lent begins after eight at night. On Tuesday, all those who through want of money, time, or enthusiasm, have not been to see the Carnival before, mingle in the gayety, and contribute to the noise and excitement. From two o’clock till five Franz and Albert followed in the _fête_, exchanging handfuls of _confetti_ with the other carriages and the pedestrians, who crowded amongst the horses’ feet and the carriage wheels without a single accident, a single dispute, or a single fight.\n\nThe _fêtes_ are veritable pleasure days to the Italians. The author of this history, who has resided five or six years in Italy, does not recollect to have ever seen a ceremony interrupted by one of those events so common in other countries. Albert was triumphant in his harlequin costume. A knot of rose-colored ribbons fell from his shoulder almost to the ground. In order that there might be no confusion, Franz wore his peasant’s costume.\n\nAs the day advanced, the tumult became greater. There was not on the pavement, in the carriages, at the windows, a single tongue that was silent, a single arm that did not move. It was a human storm, made up of a thunder of cries, and a hail of sweetmeats, flowers, eggs, oranges, and nosegays.\n\nAt three o’clock the sound of fireworks, let off on the Piazza del Popolo and the Piazza di Venezia (heard with difficulty amid the din and confusion) announced that the races were about to begin.\n\nThe races, like the _moccoli_, are one of the episodes peculiar to the last days of the Carnival. At the sound of the fireworks the carriages instantly broke ranks, and retired by the adjacent streets. All these evolutions are executed with an inconceivable address and marvellous rapidity, without the police interfering in the matter. The pedestrians ranged themselves against the walls; then the trampling of horses and the clashing of steel were heard. A detachment of carbineers, fifteen abreast, galloped up the Corso in order to clear it for the _barberi_. When the detachment arrived at the Piazza di Venezia, a second volley of fireworks was discharged, to announce that the street was clear.\n\nAlmost instantly, in the midst of a tremendous and general outcry, seven or eight horses, excited by the shouts of three hundred thousand spectators, passed by like lightning. Then the Castle of Saint Angelo fired three cannon to indicate that number three had won.\n\nImmediately, without any other signal, the carriages moved on, flowing on towards the Corso, down all the streets, like torrents pent up for a while, which again flow into the parent river; and the immense stream again continued its course between its two granite banks.\n\nA new source of noise and movement was added to the crowd. The sellers of _moccoletti_ entered on the scene. The _moccoli_, or _moccoletti_, are candles which vary in size from the pascal taper to the rushlight, and which give to each actor in the great final scene of the Carnival two very serious problems to grapple with,—first, how to keep his own _moccoletto_ alight; and secondly, how to extinguish the _moccoletti_ of others. The _moccoletto_ is like life: man has found but one means of transmitting it, and that one comes from God. But he has discovered a thousand means of taking it away, and the devil has somewhat aided him. The _moccoletto_ is kindled by approaching it to a light. But who can describe the thousand means of extinguishing the _moccoletto_?—the gigantic bellows, the monstrous extinguishers, the superhuman fans. Everyone hastened to purchase _moccoletti_—Franz and Albert among the rest.\n\nThe night was rapidly approaching; and already, at the cry of “_Moccoletti_!” repeated by the shrill voices of a thousand vendors, two or three stars began to burn among the crowd. It was a signal. At the end of ten minutes fifty thousand lights glittered, descending from the Palazzo di Venezia to the Piazza del Popolo, and mounting from the Piazza del Popolo to the Palazzo di Venezia. It seemed like the _fête_ of Jack-o’-lanterns.\n\nIt is impossible to form any idea of it without having seen it. Suppose that all the stars had descended from the sky and mingled in a wild dance on the face of the earth; the whole accompanied by cries that were never heard in any other part of the world. The _facchino_ follows the prince, the Transteverin the citizen, everyone blowing, extinguishing, relighting. Had old Æolus appeared at this moment, he would have been proclaimed king of the _moccoli_, and Aquilo the heir-presumptive to the throne.\n\nThis battle of folly and flame continued for two hours; the Corso was light as day; the features of the spectators on the third and fourth stories were visible.\n\nEvery five minutes Albert took out his watch; at length it pointed to seven. The two friends were in the Via dei Pontefici. Albert sprang out, bearing his _moccoletto_ in his hand. Two or three masks strove to knock his _moccoletto_ out of his hand; but Albert, a first-rate pugilist, sent them rolling in the street, one after the other, and continued his course towards the church of San Giacomo.\n\nThe steps were crowded with masks, who strove to snatch each other’s torches. Franz followed Albert with his eyes, and saw him mount the first step.\n\nInstantly a mask, wearing the well-known costume of a peasant woman, snatched his _moccoletto_ from him without his offering any resistance. Franz was too far off to hear what they said; but, without doubt, nothing hostile passed, for he saw Albert disappear arm-in-arm with the peasant girl. He watched them pass through the crowd for some time, but at length he lost sight of them in the Via Macello.\n\nSuddenly the bell that gives the signal for the end of the Carnival sounded, and at the same instant all the _moccoletti_ were extinguished as if by enchantment. It seemed as though one immense blast of the wind had extinguished everyone.\n\nFranz found himself in utter darkness. No sound was audible save that of the carriages that were carrying the maskers home; nothing was visible save a few lights that burnt behind the windows.\n\nThe Carnival was over.\n\n# Chapter 37. The Catacombs of Saint Sebastian\n\n20193m\nIn his whole life, perhaps, Franz had never before experienced so sudden an impression, so rapid a transition from gayety to sadness, as in this moment. It seemed as though Rome, under the magic breath of some demon of the night, had suddenly changed into a vast tomb. By a chance, which added yet more to the intensity of the darkness, the moon, which was on the wane, did not rise until eleven o’clock, and the streets which the young man traversed were plunged in the deepest obscurity.\n\nThe distance was short, and at the end of ten minutes his carriage, or rather the count’s, stopped before the Hôtel de Londres.\n\nDinner was waiting, but as Albert had told him that he should not return so soon, Franz sat down without him. Signor Pastrini, who had been accustomed to see them dine together, inquired into the cause of his absence, but Franz merely replied that Albert had received on the previous evening an invitation which he had accepted.\n\nThe sudden extinction of the _moccoletti_, the darkness which had replaced the light, and the silence which had succeeded the turmoil, had left in Franz’s mind a certain depression which was not free from uneasiness. He therefore dined very silently, in spite of the officious attention of his host, who presented himself two or three times to inquire if he wanted anything.\n\nFranz resolved to wait for Albert as late as possible. He ordered the carriage, therefore, for eleven o’clock, desiring Signor Pastrini to inform him the moment that Albert returned to the hotel.\n\nAt eleven o’clock Albert had not come back. Franz dressed himself, and went out, telling his host that he was going to pass the night at the Duke of Bracciano’s. The house of the Duke of Bracciano is one of the most delightful in Rome, the duchess, one of the last heiresses of the Colonnas, does its honors with the most consummate grace, and thus their _fêtes_ have a European celebrity.\n\nFranz and Albert had brought to Rome letters of introduction to them, and their first question on his arrival was to inquire the whereabouts of his travelling companion. Franz replied that he had left him at the moment they were about to extinguish the _moccoli_, and that he had lost sight of him in the Via Macello.\n\n“Then he has not returned?” said the duke.\n\n“I waited for him until this hour,” replied Franz.\n\n“And do you know whither he went?”\n\n“No, not precisely; however, I think it was something very like a rendezvous.”\n\n“_Diavolo!_” said the duke, “this is a bad day, or rather a bad night, to be out late; is it not, countess?”\n\nThese words were addressed to the Countess G——, who had just arrived, and was leaning on the arm of Signor Torlonia, the duke’s brother.\n\n“I think, on the contrary, that it is a charming night,” replied the countess, “and those who are here will complain of but one thing, that of its too rapid flight.”\n\n“I am not speaking,” said the duke with a smile, “of the persons who are here; the men run no other danger than that of falling in love with you, and the women of falling ill of jealousy at seeing you so lovely; I meant persons who were out in the streets of Rome.”\n\n“Ah,” asked the countess, “who is out in the streets of Rome at this hour, unless it be to go to a ball?”\n\n“Our friend, Albert de Morcerf, countess, whom I left in pursuit of his unknown about seven o’clock this evening,” said Franz, “and whom I have not seen since.”\n\n“And don’t you know where he is?”\n\n“Not at all.”\n\n“Is he armed?”\n\n“He is in masquerade.”\n\n“You should not have allowed him to go,” said the duke to Franz; “you, who know Rome better than he does.”\n\n“You might as well have tried to stop number three of the _barberi_, who gained the prize in the race today,” replied Franz; “and then moreover, what could happen to him?”\n\n“Who can tell? The night is gloomy, and the Tiber is very near the Via Macello.” Franz felt a shudder run through his veins at observing that the feeling of the duke and the countess was so much in unison with his own personal disquietude.\n\n“I informed them at the hotel that I had the honor of passing the night here, duke,” said Franz, “and desired them to come and inform me of his return.”\n\n“Ah,” replied the duke, “here I think, is one of my servants who is seeking you.”\n\nThe duke was not mistaken; when he saw Franz, the servant came up to him.\n\n“Your excellency,” he said, “the master of the Hôtel de Londres has sent to let you know that a man is waiting for you with a letter from the Viscount of Morcerf.”\n\n“A letter from the viscount!” exclaimed Franz.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“And who is the man?”\n\n“I do not know.”\n\n“Why did he not bring it to me here?”\n\n“The messenger did not say.”\n\n“And where is the messenger?”\n\n“He went away directly he saw me enter the ball-room to find you.”\n\n“Oh,” said the countess to Franz, “go with all speed—poor young man! Perhaps some accident has happened to him.”\n\n“I will hasten,” replied Franz.\n\n“Shall we see you again to give us any information?” inquired the countess.\n\n“Yes, if it is not any serious affair, otherwise I cannot answer as to what I may do myself.”\n\n“Be prudent, in any event,” said the countess.\n\n“Oh! pray be assured of that.”\n\nFranz took his hat and went away in haste. He had sent away his carriage with orders for it to fetch him at two o’clock; fortunately the Palazzo Bracciano, which is on one side in the Corso, and on the other in the Square of the Holy Apostles, is hardly ten minutes’ walk from the Hôtel de Londres.\n\nAs he came near the hotel, Franz saw a man in the middle of the street. He had no doubt that it was the messenger from Albert. The man was wrapped up in a large cloak. He went up to him, but, to his extreme astonishment, the stranger first addressed him.\n\n“What wants your excellency of me?” inquired the man, retreating a step or two, as if to keep on his guard.\n\n“Are not you the person who brought me a letter,” inquired Franz, “from the Viscount of Morcerf?”\n\n“Your excellency lodges at Pastrini’s hotel?”\n\n“I do.”\n\n“Your excellency is the travelling companion of the viscount?”\n\n“I am.”\n\n“Your excellency’s name——”\n\n“Is the Baron Franz d’Épinay.”\n\n20199m\n“Then it is to your excellency that this letter is addressed.”\n\n“Is there any answer?” inquired Franz, taking the letter from him.\n\n“Yes—your friend at least hopes so.”\n\n“Come upstairs with me, and I will give it to you.”\n\n“I prefer waiting here,” said the messenger, with a smile.\n\n“And why?”\n\n“Your excellency will know when you have read the letter.”\n\n“Shall I find you here, then?”\n\n“Certainly.”\n\nFranz entered the hotel. On the staircase he met Signor Pastrini. “Well?” said the landlord.\n\n“Well—what?” responded Franz.\n\n“You have seen the man who desired to speak with you from your friend?” he asked of Franz.\n\n“Yes, I have seen him,” he replied, “and he has handed this letter to me. Light the candles in my apartment, if you please.”\n\nThe innkeeper gave orders to a servant to go before Franz with a light. The young man had found Signor Pastrini looking very much alarmed, and this had only made him the more anxious to read Albert’s letter; and so he went instantly towards the waxlight, and unfolded it. It was written and signed by Albert. Franz read it twice before he could comprehend what it contained. It was thus worded:\n\n“My dear Fellow,\n\n“The moment you have received this, have the kindness to take the letter of credit from my pocket-book, which you will find in the square drawer of the _secrétaire_; add your own to it, if it be not sufficient. Run to Torlonia, draw from him instantly four thousand piastres, and give them to the bearer. It is urgent that I should have this money without delay. I do not say more, relying on you as you may rely on me.\n\n“Your friend,\n\n“Albert de Morcerf.\n\n“P.S.—I now believe in Italian _banditti_.”\n\nBelow these lines were written, in a strange hand, the following in Italian:\n\n“_Se alle sei della mattina le quattro mille piastre non sono nelle mie mani, alla sette il Conte Alberto avrà cessato di vivere_.\n\n“Luigi Vampa.”\n\n“_If by six in the morning the four thousand piastres are not in my hands, by seven o’clock the Count Albert will have ceased to live_.”\n\nThis second signature explained everything to Franz, who now understood the objection of the messenger to coming up into the apartment; the street was safer for him. Albert, then, had fallen into the hands of the famous bandit chief, in whose existence he had for so long a time refused to believe.\n\nThere was no time to lose. He hastened to open the _secrétaire_, and found the pocket-book in the drawer, and in it the letter of credit. There were in all six thousand piastres, but of these six thousand Albert had already expended three thousand.\n\nAs to Franz, he had no letter of credit, as he lived at Florence, and had only come to Rome to pass seven or eight days; he had brought but a hundred louis, and of these he had not more than fifty left. Thus seven or eight hundred piastres were wanting to them both to make up the sum that Albert required. True, he might in such a case rely on the kindness of Signor Torlonia. He was, therefore, about to return to the Palazzo Bracciano without loss of time, when suddenly a luminous idea crossed his mind.\n\nHe remembered the Count of Monte Cristo. Franz was about to ring for Signor Pastrini, when that worthy presented himself.\n\n“My dear sir,” he said, hastily, “do you know if the count is within?”\n\n“Yes, your excellency; he has this moment returned.”\n\n“Is he in bed?”\n\n“I should say no.”\n\n“Then ring at his door, if you please, and request him to be so kind as to give me an audience.”\n\nSignor Pastrini did as he was desired, and returning five minutes after, he said:\n\n“The count awaits your excellency.”\n\nFranz went along the corridor, and a servant introduced him to the count. He was in a small room which Franz had not yet seen, and which was surrounded with divans. The count came towards him.\n\n“Well, what good wind blows you hither at this hour?” said he; “have you come to sup with me? It would be very kind of you.”\n\n“No; I have come to speak to you of a very serious matter.”\n\n“A serious matter,” said the count, looking at Franz with the earnestness usual to him; “and what may it be?”\n\n“Are we alone?”\n\n“Yes,” replied the count, going to the door, and returning. Franz gave him Albert’s letter.\n\n“Read that,” he said.\n\nThe count read it.\n\n“Well, well!” said he.\n\n“Did you see the postscript?”\n\n“I did, indeed.\n\n“_‘Se alle sei della mattina le quattro mille piastre non sono nelle mie mani, alla sette il conte Alberto avrà cessato di vivere. _\n\n“‘Luigi Vampa.’”\n\n“What think you of that?” inquired Franz.\n\n“Have you the money he demands?”\n\n“Yes, all but eight hundred piastres.”\n\nThe count went to his _secrétaire_, opened it, and pulling out a drawer filled with gold, said to Franz, “I hope you will not offend me by applying to anyone but myself.”\n\n“You see, on the contrary, I come to you first and instantly,” replied Franz.\n\n“And I thank you; have what you will;” and he made a sign to Franz to take what he pleased.\n\n“Is it absolutely necessary, then, to send the money to Luigi Vampa?” asked the young man, looking fixedly in his turn at the count.\n\n“Judge for yourself,” replied he. “The postscript is explicit.”\n\n“I think that if you would take the trouble of reflecting, you could find a way of simplifying the negotiation,” said Franz.\n\n“How so?” returned the count, with surprise.\n\n“If we were to go together to Luigi Vampa, I am sure he would not refuse you Albert’s freedom.”\n\n“What influence can I possibly have over a bandit?”\n\n“Have you not just rendered him a service that can never be forgotten?”\n\n“What is that?”\n\n“Have you not saved Peppino’s life?”\n\n“Well, well,” said the count, “who told you that?”\n\n“No matter; I know it.” The count knit his brows, and remained silent an instant.\n\n“And if I went to seek Vampa, would you accompany me?”\n\n“If my society would not be disagreeable.”\n\n“Be it so. It is a lovely night, and a walk without Rome will do us both good.”\n\n“Shall I take any arms?”\n\n“For what purpose?”\n\n“Any money?”\n\n“It is useless. Where is the man who brought the letter?”\n\n“In the street.”\n\n“He awaits the answer?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“I must learn where we are going. I will summon him hither.”\n\n“It is useless; he would not come up.”\n\n“To your apartments, perhaps; but he will not make any difficulty at entering mine.”\n\nThe count went to the window of the apartment that looked on to the street, and whistled in a peculiar manner. The man in the mantle quitted the wall, and advanced into the middle of the street. “_Salite!_” said the count, in the same tone in which he would have given an order to his servant. The messenger obeyed without the least hesitation, but rather with alacrity, and, mounting the steps at a bound, entered the hotel; five seconds afterwards he was at the door of the room.\n\n“Ah, it is you, Peppino,” said the count. But Peppino, instead of answering, threw himself on his knees, seized the count’s hand, and covered it with kisses. “Ah,” said the count, “you have, then, not forgotten that I saved your life; that is strange, for it is a week ago.”\n20203m\n\n“No, excellency; and never shall I forget it,” returned Peppino, with an accent of profound gratitude.\n\n“Never? That is a long time; but it is something that you believe so. Rise and answer.”\n\nPeppino glanced anxiously at Franz.\n\n“Oh, you may speak before his excellency,” said he; “he is one of my friends. You allow me to give you this title?” continued the count in French, “it is necessary to excite this man’s confidence.”\n\n“You can speak before me,” said Franz; “I am a friend of the count’s.”\n\n“Good!” returned Peppino. “I am ready to answer any questions your excellency may address to me.”\n\n“How did the Viscount Albert fall into Luigi’s hands?”\n\n“Excellency, the Frenchman’s carriage passed several times the one in which was Teresa.”\n\n“The chief’s mistress?”\n\n“Yes. The Frenchman threw her a bouquet; Teresa returned it—all this with the consent of the chief, who was in the carriage.”\n\n“What?” cried Franz, “was Luigi Vampa in the carriage with the Roman peasants?”\n\n“It was he who drove, disguised as the coachman,” replied Peppino.\n\n“Well?” said the count.\n\n“Well, then, the Frenchman took off his mask; Teresa, with the chief’s consent, did the same. The Frenchman asked for a rendezvous; Teresa gave him one—only, instead of Teresa, it was Beppo who was on the steps of the church of San Giacomo.”\n\n“What!” exclaimed Franz, “the peasant girl who snatched his _mocoletto_ from him——”\n\n“Was a lad of fifteen,” replied Peppino. “But it was no disgrace to your friend to have been deceived; Beppo has taken in plenty of others.”\n\n“And Beppo led him outside the walls?” said the count.\n\n“Exactly so; a carriage was waiting at the end of the Via Macello. Beppo got in, inviting the Frenchman to follow him, and he did not wait to be asked twice. He gallantly offered the right-hand seat to Beppo, and sat by him. Beppo told him he was going to take him to a villa a league from Rome; the Frenchman assured him he would follow him to the end of the world. The coachman went up the Via di Ripetta and the Porta San Paolo; and when they were two hundred yards outside, as the Frenchman became somewhat too forward, Beppo put a brace of pistols to his head, the coachman pulled up and did the same. At the same time, four of the band, who were concealed on the banks of the Almo, surrounded the carriage. The Frenchman made some resistance, and nearly strangled Beppo; but he could not resist five armed men, and was forced to yield. They made him get out, walk along the banks of the river, and then brought him to Teresa and Luigi, who were waiting for him in the catacombs of St. Sebastian.”\n\n“Well,” said the count, turning towards Franz, “it seems to me that this is a very likely story. What do you say to it?”\n\n“Why, that I should think it very amusing,” replied Franz, “if it had happened to anyone but poor Albert.”\n\n“And, in truth, if you had not found me here,” said the count, “it might have proved a gallant adventure which would have cost your friend dear; but now, be assured, his alarm will be the only serious consequence.”\n\n“And shall we go and find him?” inquired Franz.\n\n“Oh, decidedly, sir. He is in a very picturesque place—do you know the catacombs of St. Sebastian?”\n\n“I was never in them; but I have often resolved to visit them.”\n\n“Well, here is an opportunity made to your hand, and it would be difficult to contrive a better. Have you a carriage?”\n\n“No.”\n“That is of no consequence; I always have one ready, day and night.”\n\n“Always ready?”\n\n“Yes. I am a very capricious being, and I should tell you that sometimes when I rise, or after my dinner, or in the middle of the night, I resolve on starting for some particular point, and away I go.”\n\nThe count rang, and a footman appeared.\n\n“Order out the carriage,” he said, “and remove the pistols which are in the holsters. You need not awaken the coachman; Ali will drive.”\n\nIn a very short time the noise of wheels was heard, and the carriage stopped at the door. The count took out his watch.\n\n“Half-past twelve,” he said. “We might start at five o’clock and be in time, but the delay may cause your friend to pass an uneasy night, and therefore we had better go with all speed to extricate him from the hands of the infidels. Are you still resolved to accompany me?”\n\n“More determined than ever.”\n\n“Well, then, come along.”\n\nFranz and the count went downstairs, accompanied by Peppino. At the door they found the carriage. Ali was on the box, in whom Franz recognized the dumb slave of the grotto of Monte Cristo. Franz and the count got into the carriage. Peppino placed himself beside Ali, and they set off at a rapid pace. Ali had received his instructions, and went down the Corso, crossed the Campo Vaccino, went up the Strada San Gregorio, and reached the gates of St. Sebastian. Then the porter raised some difficulties, but the Count of Monte Cristo produced a permit from the governor of Rome, allowing him to leave or enter the city at any hour of the day or night; the portcullis was therefore raised, the porter had a louis for his trouble, and they went on their way.\n\nThe road which the carriage now traversed was the ancient Appian Way, and bordered with tombs. From time to time, by the light of the moon, which began to rise, Franz imagined that he saw something like a sentinel appear at various points among the ruins, and suddenly retreat into the darkness on a signal from Peppino.\n\nA short time before they reached the Baths of Caracalla the carriage stopped, Peppino opened the door, and the count and Franz alighted.\n\n“In ten minutes,” said the count to his companion, “we shall be there.”\n\nHe then took Peppino aside, gave him an order in a low voice, and Peppino went away, taking with him a torch, brought with them in the carriage. Five minutes elapsed, during which Franz saw the shepherd going along a narrow path that led over the irregular and broken surface of the Campagna; and finally he disappeared in the midst of the tall red herbage, which seemed like the bristling mane of an enormous lion.\n\n“Now,” said the count, “let us follow him.”\n\nFranz and the count in their turn then advanced along the same path, which, at the distance of a hundred paces, led them over a declivity to the bottom of a small valley. They then perceived two men conversing in the obscurity.\n\n“Ought we to go on?” asked Franz of the count; “or should we pause?”\n\n“Let us go on; Peppino will have warned the sentry of our coming.”\n\nOne of the two men was Peppino, and the other a bandit on the lookout. Franz and the count advanced, and the bandit saluted them.\n\n“Your excellency,” said Peppino, addressing the count, “if you will follow me, the opening of the catacombs is close at hand.”\n\n“Go on, then,” replied the count. They came to an opening behind a clump of bushes and in the midst of a pile of rocks, by which a man could scarcely pass. Peppino glided first into this crevice; after they got along a few paces the passage widened. Peppino passed, lighted his torch, and turned to see if they came after him. The count first reached an open space and Franz followed him closely. The passageway sloped in a gentle descent, enlarging as they proceeded; still Franz and the count were compelled to advance in a stooping posture, and were scarcely able to proceed abreast of one another. They went on a hundred and fifty paces in this way, and then were stopped by, “Who comes there?” At the same time they saw the reflection of a torch on a carbine barrel.\n\n“A friend!” responded Peppino; and, advancing alone towards the sentry, he said a few words to him in a low tone; and then he, like the first, saluted the nocturnal visitors, making a sign that they might proceed.\n\nBehind the sentinel was a staircase with twenty steps. Franz and the count descended these, and found themselves in a mortuary chamber. Five corridors diverged like the rays of a star, and the walls, dug into niches, which were arranged one above the other in the shape of coffins, showed that they were at last in the catacombs. Down one of the corridors, whose extent it was impossible to determine, rays of light were visible. The count laid his hand on Franz’s shoulder.\n\n“Would you like to see a camp of bandits in repose?” he inquired.\n\n“Exceedingly,” replied Franz.\n\n“Come with me, then. Peppino, put out the torch.” Peppino obeyed, and Franz and the count were in utter darkness, except that fifty paces in advance of them a reddish glare, more evident since Peppino had put out his torch, was visible along the wall.\n\nThey advanced silently, the count guiding Franz as if he had the singular faculty of seeing in the dark. Franz himself, however, saw his way more plainly in proportion as he went on towards the light, which served in some manner as a guide. Three arcades were before them, and the middle one was used as a door. These arcades opened on one side into the corridor where the count and Franz were, and on the other into a large square chamber, entirely surrounded by niches similar to those of which we have spoken.\n\nIn the midst of this chamber were four stones, which had formerly served as an altar, as was evident from the cross which still surmounted them. A lamp, placed at the base of a pillar, lighted up with its pale and flickering flame the singular scene which presented itself to the eyes of the two visitors concealed in the shadow.\n\nA man was seated with his elbow leaning on the column, and was reading with his back turned to the arcades, through the openings of which the new-comers contemplated him. This was the chief of the band, Luigi Vampa. Around him, and in groups, according to their fancy, lying in their mantles, or with their backs against a sort of stone bench, which went all round the columbarium, were to be seen twenty brigands or more, each having his carbine within reach. At the other end, silent, scarcely visible, and like a shadow, was a sentinel, who was walking up and down before a grotto, which was only distinguishable because in that spot the darkness seemed more dense than elsewhere.\n\nWhen the count thought Franz had gazed sufficiently on this picturesque tableau, he raised his finger to his lips, to warn him to be silent, and, ascending the three steps which led to the corridor of the columbarium, entered the chamber by the middle arcade, and advanced towards Vampa, who was so intent on the book before him that he did not hear the noise of his footsteps.\n\n“Who comes there?” cried the sentinel, who was less abstracted, and who saw by the lamp-light a shadow approaching his chief. At this challenge, Vampa rose quickly, drawing at the same moment a pistol from his girdle. In a moment all the bandits were on their feet, and twenty carbines were levelled at the count.\n\n“Well,” said he in a voice perfectly calm, and no muscle of his countenance disturbed, “well, my dear Vampa, it appears to me that you receive a friend with a great deal of ceremony.”\n\n20207m\n“Ground arms,” exclaimed the chief, with an imperative sign of the hand, while with the other he took off his hat respectfully; then, turning to the singular personage who had caused this scene, he said, “Your pardon, your excellency, but I was so far from expecting the honor of a visit, that I did not really recognize you.”\n\n“It seems that your memory is equally short in everything, Vampa,” said the count, “and that not only do you forget people’s faces, but also the conditions you make with them.”\n\n“What conditions have I forgotten, your excellency?” inquired the bandit, with the air of a man who, having committed an error, is anxious to repair it.\n\n“Was it not agreed,” asked the count, “that not only my person, but also that of my friends, should be respected by you?”\n\n“And how have I broken that treaty, your excellency?”\n\n“You have this evening carried off and conveyed hither the Viscount Albert de Morcerf. Well,” continued the count, in a tone that made Franz shudder, “this young gentleman is one of _my friends_—this young gentleman lodges in the same hotel as myself—this young gentleman has been up and down the Corso for eight hours in my private carriage, and yet, I repeat to you, you have carried him off, and conveyed him hither, and,” added the count, taking the letter from his pocket, “you have set a ransom on him, as if he were an utter stranger.”\n\n“Why did you not tell me all this—you?” inquired the brigand chief, turning towards his men, who all retreated before his look. “Why have you caused me thus to fail in my word towards a gentleman like the count, who has all our lives in his hands? By heavens! if I thought one of you knew that the young gentleman was the friend of his excellency, I would blow his brains out with my own hand!”\n20211m\n\n“Well,” said the count, turning towards Franz, “I told you there was some mistake in this.”\n\n“Are you not alone?” asked Vampa with uneasiness.\n\n“I am with the person to whom this letter was addressed, and to whom I desired to prove that Luigi Vampa was a man of his word. Come, your excellency,” the count added, turning to Franz, “here is Luigi Vampa, who will himself express to you his deep regret at the mistake he has committed.”\n\nFranz approached, the chief advancing several steps to meet him.\n\n“Welcome among us, your excellency,” he said to him; “you heard what the count just said, and also my reply; let me add that I would not for the four thousand piastres at which I had fixed your friend’s ransom, that this had happened.”\n\n“But,” said Franz, looking round him uneasily, “where is the viscount?—I do not see him.”\n\n“Nothing has happened to him, I hope,” said the count frowningly.\n\n“The prisoner is there,” replied Vampa, pointing to the hollow space in front of which the bandit was on guard, “and I will go myself and tell him he is free.”\n\nThe chief went towards the place he had pointed out as Albert’s prison, and Franz and the count followed him.\n\n“What is the prisoner doing?” inquired Vampa of the sentinel.\n\n“_Ma foi_, captain,” replied the sentry, “I do not know; for the last hour I have not heard him stir.”\n\n“Come in, your excellency,” said Vampa. The count and Franz ascended seven or eight steps after the chief, who drew back a bolt and opened a door. Then, by the gleam of a lamp, similar to that which lighted the columbarium, Albert was to be seen wrapped up in a cloak which one of the bandits had lent him, lying in a corner in profound slumber.\n\n“Come,” said the count, smiling with his own peculiar smile, “not so bad for a man who is to be shot at seven o’clock tomorrow morning.”\n\nVampa looked at Albert with a kind of admiration; he was not insensible to such a proof of courage.\n\n“You are right, your excellency,” he said; “this must be one of your friends.”\n\nThen going to Albert, he touched him on the shoulder, saying, “Will your excellency please to awaken?”\n\nAlbert stretched out his arms, rubbed his eyelids, and opened his eyes.\n\n“Oh,” said he, “is it you, captain? You should have allowed me to sleep. I had such a delightful dream. I was dancing the galop at Torlonia’s with the Countess G——.” Then he drew his watch from his pocket, that he might see how time sped.\n\n“Half-past one only?” said he. “Why the devil do you rouse me at this hour?”\n\n“To tell you that you are free, your excellency.”\n\n“My dear fellow,” replied Albert, with perfect ease of mind, “remember, for the future, Napoleon’s maxim, ‘Never awaken me but for bad news;’ if you had let me sleep on, I should have finished my galop, and have been grateful to you all my life. So, then, they have paid my ransom?”\n\n“No, your excellency.”\n\n“Well, then, how am I free?”\n\n“A person to whom I can refuse nothing has come to demand you.”\n\n“Come hither?”\n\n“Yes, hither.”\n\n“Really? Then that person is a most amiable person.”\n\nAlbert looked around and perceived Franz. “What,” said he, “is it you, my dear Franz, whose devotion and friendship are thus displayed?”\n\n“No, not I,” replied Franz, “but our neighbor, the Count of Monte Cristo.”\n\n“Oh, my dear count,” said Albert gayly, arranging his cravat and wristbands, “you are really most kind, and I hope you will consider me as under eternal obligations to you, in the first place for the carriage, and in the next for this visit,” and he put out his hand to the count, who shuddered as he gave his own, but who nevertheless did give it.\n\nThe bandit gazed on this scene with amazement; he was evidently accustomed to see his prisoners tremble before him, and yet here was one whose gay temperament was not for a moment altered; as for Franz, he was enchanted at the way in which Albert had sustained the national honor in the presence of the bandit.\n\n“My dear Albert,” he said, “if you will make haste, we shall yet have time to finish the night at Torlonia’s. You may conclude your interrupted galop, so that you will owe no ill-will to Signor Luigi, who has, indeed, throughout this whole affair acted like a gentleman.”\n\n“You are decidedly right, and we may reach the Palazzo by two o’clock. Signor Luigi,” continued Albert, “is there any formality to fulfil before I take leave of your excellency?”\n\n“None, sir,” replied the bandit, “you are as free as air.”\n\n“Well, then, a happy and merry life to you. Come, gentlemen, come.”\n\nAnd Albert, followed by Franz and the count, descended the staircase, crossed the square chamber, where stood all the bandits, hat in hand.\n\n“Peppino,” said the brigand chief, “give me the torch.”\n\n“What are you going to do?” inquired the count.\n\n“I will show you the way back myself,” said the captain; “that is the least honor that I can render to your excellency.”\n\nAnd taking the lighted torch from the hands of the herdsman, he preceded his guests, not as a servant who performs an act of civility, but like a king who precedes ambassadors. On reaching the door, he bowed.\n\n“And now, your excellency,” added he, “allow me to repeat my apologies, and I hope you will not entertain any resentment at what has occurred.”\n\n“No, my dear Vampa,” replied the count; “besides, you compensate for your mistakes in so gentlemanly a way, that one almost feels obliged to you for having committed them.”\n\n20214m\n“Gentlemen,” added the chief, turning towards the young men, “perhaps the offer may not appear very tempting to you; but if you should ever feel inclined to pay me a second visit, wherever I may be, you shall be welcome.”\n\nFranz and Albert bowed. The count went out first, then Albert. Franz paused for a moment.\n\n“Has your excellency anything to ask me?” said Vampa with a smile.\n\n“Yes, I have,” replied Franz; “I am curious to know what work you were perusing with so much attention as we entered.”\n\n“Cæsar’s _Commentaries_,” said the bandit, “it is my favorite work.”\n\n“Well, are you coming?” asked Albert.\n\n“Yes,” replied Franz, “here I am,” and he, in his turn, left the caves. They advanced to the plain.\n\n“Ah, your pardon,” said Albert, turning round; “will you allow me, captain?”\n\nAnd he lighted his cigar at Vampa’s torch.\n\n“Now, my dear count,” he said, “let us on with all the speed we may. I am enormously anxious to finish my night at the Duke of Bracciano’s.”\n\nThey found the carriage where they had left it. The count said a word in Arabic to Ali, and the horses went on at great speed.\n\nIt was just two o’clock by Albert’s watch when the two friends entered into the dancing-room. Their return was quite an event, but as they entered together, all uneasiness on Albert’s account ceased instantly.\n\n“Madame,” said the Viscount of Morcerf, advancing towards the countess, “yesterday you were so condescending as to promise me a galop; I am rather late in claiming this gracious promise, but here is my friend, whose character for veracity you well know, and he will assure you the delay arose from no fault of mine.”\n\nAnd as at this moment the orchestra gave the signal for the waltz, Albert put his arm round the waist of the countess, and disappeared with her in the whirl of dancers.\n\nIn the meanwhile Franz was considering the singular shudder that had passed over the Count of Monte Cristo at the moment when he had been, in some sort, forced to give his hand to Albert.\n\n# Chapter 38. The Rendezvous\n\nThe first words that Albert uttered to his friend, on the following morning, contained a request that Franz would accompany him on a visit to the count; true, the young man had warmly and energetically thanked the count on the previous evening; but services such as he had rendered could never be too often acknowledged. Franz, who seemed attracted by some invisible influence towards the count, in which terror was strangely mingled, felt an extreme reluctance to permit his friend to be exposed alone to the singular fascination that this mysterious personage seemed to exercise over him, and therefore made no objection to Albert’s request, but at once accompanied him to the desired spot, and, after a short delay, the count joined them in the salon.\n\n“My dear count,” said Albert, advancing to meet him, “permit me to repeat the poor thanks I offered last night, and to assure you that the remembrance of all I owe to you will never be effaced from my memory; believe me, as long as I live, I shall never cease to dwell with grateful recollection on the prompt and important service you rendered me; and also to remember that to you I am indebted even for my life.”\n\n“My very good friend and excellent neighbor,” replied the count, with a smile, “you really exaggerate my trifling exertions. You owe me nothing but some trifle of 20,000 francs, which you have been saved out of your travelling expenses, so that there is not much of a score between us;—but you must really permit me to congratulate you on the ease and unconcern with which you resigned yourself to your fate, and the perfect indifference you manifested as to the turn events might take.”\n\n“Upon my word,” said Albert, “I deserve no credit for what I could not help, namely, a determination to take everything as I found it, and to let those bandits see, that although men get into troublesome scrapes all over the world, there is no nation but the French that can smile even in the face of grim Death himself. All that, however, has nothing to do with my obligations to you, and I now come to ask you whether, in my own person, my family, or connections, I can in any way serve you? My father, the Comte de Morcerf, although of Spanish origin, possesses considerable influence, both at the court of France and Madrid, and I unhesitatingly place the best services of myself, and all to whom my life is dear, at your disposal.”\n\n“Monsieur de Morcerf,” replied the count, “your offer, far from surprising me, is precisely what I expected from you, and I accept it in the same spirit of hearty sincerity with which it is made;—nay, I will go still further, and say that I had previously made up my mind to ask a great favor at your hands.”\n\n“Oh, pray name it.”\n\n“I am wholly a stranger to Paris—it is a city I have never yet seen.”\n\n“Is it possible,” exclaimed Albert, “that you have reached your present age without visiting the finest capital in the world? I can scarcely credit it.”\n\n“Nevertheless, it is quite true; still, I agree with you in thinking that my present ignorance of the first city in Europe is a reproach to me in every way, and calls for immediate correction; but, in all probability, I should have performed so important, so necessary a duty, as that of making myself acquainted with the wonders and beauties of your justly celebrated capital, had I known any person who would have introduced me into the fashionable world, but unfortunately I possessed no acquaintance there, and, of necessity, was compelled to abandon the idea.”\n\n“So distinguished an individual as yourself,” cried Albert, “could scarcely have required an introduction.”\n\n“You are most kind; but as regards myself, I can find no merit I possess, save that, as a millionaire, I might have become a partner in the speculations of M. Aguado and M. Rothschild; but as my motive in travelling to your capital would not have been for the pleasure of dabbling in stocks, I stayed away till some favorable chance should present itself of carrying my wish into execution. Your offer, however, smooths all difficulties, and I have only to ask you, my dear M. de Morcerf” (these words were accompanied by a most peculiar smile), “whether you undertake, upon my arrival in France, to open to me the doors of that fashionable world of which I know no more than a Huron or a native of Cochin-China?”\n\n“Oh, that I do, and with infinite pleasure,” answered Albert; “and so much the more readily as a letter received this morning from my father summons me to Paris, in consequence of a treaty of marriage (my dear Franz, do not smile, I beg of you) with a family of high standing, and connected with the very cream of Parisian society.”\n\n“Connected by marriage, you mean,” said Franz, laughingly.\n\n“Well, never mind how it is,” answered Albert, “it comes to the same thing in the end. Perhaps by the time you return to Paris, I shall be quite a sober, staid father of a family! A most edifying representative I shall make of all the domestic virtues—don’t you think so? But as regards your wish to visit our fine city, my dear count, I can only say that you may command me and mine to any extent you please.”\n\n“Then it is settled,” said the count, “and I give you my solemn assurance that I only waited an opportunity like the present to realize plans that I have long meditated.”\n\nFranz did not doubt that these plans were the same concerning which the count had dropped a few words in the grotto of Monte Cristo, and while the count was speaking the young man watched him closely, hoping to read something of his purpose in his face, but his countenance was inscrutable especially when, as in the present case, it was veiled in a sphinx-like smile.\n\n“But tell me now, count,” exclaimed Albert, delighted at the idea of having to chaperon so distinguished a person as Monte Cristo; “tell me truly whether you are in earnest, or if this project of visiting Paris is merely one of the chimerical and uncertain air castles of which we make so many in the course of our lives, but which, like a house built on the sand, is liable to be blown over by the first puff of wind?”\n\n“I pledge you my honor,” returned the count, “that I mean to do as I have said; both inclination and positive necessity compel me to visit Paris.”\n\n“When do you propose going thither?”\n\n“Have you made up your mind when you shall be there yourself?”\n\n“Certainly I have; in a fortnight or three weeks’ time, that is to say, as fast as I can get there!”\n\n“Nay,” said the Count; “I will give you three months ere I join you; you see I make an ample allowance for all delays and difficulties.\n\n“And in three months’ time,” said Albert, “you will be at my house?”\n\n“Shall we make a positive appointment for a particular day and hour?” inquired the count; “only let me warn you that I am proverbial for my punctilious exactitude in keeping my engagements.”\n\n“Day for day, hour for hour,” said Albert; “that will suit me to a dot.”\n\n“So be it, then,” replied the count, and extending his hand towards a calendar, suspended near the chimney-piece, he said, “today is the 21st of February;” and drawing out his watch, added, “it is exactly half-past ten o’clock. Now promise me to remember this, and expect me the 21st of May at the same hour in the forenoon.”\n\n“Capital!” exclaimed Albert; “your breakfast shall be waiting.”\n\n“Where do you live?”\n\n“No. 27, Rue du Helder.”\n\n“Have you bachelor’s apartments there? I hope my coming will not put you to any inconvenience.”\n\n“I reside in my father’s house, but occupy a pavilion at the farther side of the courtyard, entirely separated from the main building.”\n\n“Quite sufficient,” replied the count, as, taking out his tablets, he wrote down “No. 27, Rue du Helder, 21st May, half-past ten in the morning.”\n\n“Now then,” said the count, returning his tablets to his pocket, “make yourself perfectly easy; the hand of your time-piece will not be more accurate in marking the time than myself.”\n\n“Shall I see you again ere my departure?” asked Albert.\n\n“That depends; when do you leave?”\n\n“Tomorrow evening, at five o’clock.”\n\n“In that case I must say adieu to you, as I am compelled to go to Naples, and shall not return hither before Saturday evening or Sunday morning. And you, baron,” pursued the count, addressing Franz, “do you also depart tomorrow?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“For France?”\n\n“No, for Venice; I shall remain in Italy for another year or two.”\n\n“Then we shall not meet in Paris?”\n\n“I fear I shall not have that honor.”\n\n“Well, since we must part,” said the count, holding out a hand to each of the young men, “allow me to wish you both a safe and pleasant journey.”\n\nIt was the first time the hand of Franz had come in contact with that of the mysterious individual before him, and unconsciously he shuddered at its touch, for it felt cold and icy as that of a corpse.\n\n20219m\n“Let us understand each other,” said Albert; “it is agreed—is it not?—that you are to be at No. 27, in the Rue du Helder, on the 21st of May, at half-past ten in the morning, and your word of honor passed for your punctuality?”\n\n“The 21st of May, at half-past ten in the morning, Rue du Helder, No. 27,” replied the count.\n\nThe young men then rose, and bowing to the count, quitted the room.\n\n“What is the matter?” asked Albert of Franz, when they had returned to their own apartments; “you seem more than commonly thoughtful.”\n\n“I will confess to you, Albert,” replied Franz, “the count is a very singular person, and the appointment you have made to meet him in Paris fills me with a thousand apprehensions.”\n\n“My dear fellow,” exclaimed Albert, “what can there possibly be in that to excite uneasiness? Why, you must have lost your senses.”\n\n“Whether I am in my senses or not,” answered Franz, “that is the way I feel.”\n\n“Listen to me, Franz,” said Albert; “I am glad that the occasion has presented itself for saying this to you, for I have noticed how cold you are in your bearing towards the count, while he, on the other hand, has always been courtesy itself to us. Have you anything particular against him?”\n\n“Possibly.”\n\n“Did you ever meet him previously to coming hither?”\n\n“I have.”\n\n“And where?”\n\n“Will you promise me not to repeat a single word of what I am about to tell you?”\n\n“I promise.”\n\n“Upon your honor?”\n\n“Upon my honor.”\n\n“Then listen to me.”\n\nFranz then related to his friend the history of his excursion to the Island of Monte Cristo and of his finding a party of smugglers there, and the two Corsican bandits with them. He dwelt with considerable force and energy on the almost magical hospitality he had received from the count, and the magnificence of his entertainment in the grotto of the _Thousand and One Nights_.\n\nHe recounted, with circumstantial exactitude, all the particulars of the supper, the hashish, the statues, the dream, and how, at his awakening, there remained no proof or trace of all these events, save the small yacht, seen in the distant horizon driving under full sail toward Porto-Vecchio.\n\nThen he detailed the conversation overheard by him at the Colosseum, between the count and Vampa, in which the count had promised to obtain the release of the bandit Peppino,—an engagement which, as our readers are aware, he most faithfully fulfilled.\n\nAt last he arrived at the adventure of the preceding night, and the embarrassment in which he found himself placed by not having sufficient cash by six or seven hundred piastres to make up the sum required, and finally of his application to the count and the picturesque and satisfactory result that followed. Albert listened with the most profound attention.\n\n“Well,” said he, when Franz had concluded, “what do you find to object to in all you have related? The count is fond of travelling, and, being rich, possesses a vessel of his own. Go but to Portsmouth or Southampton, and you will find the harbors crowded with the yachts belonging to such of the English as can afford the expense, and have the same liking for this amusement. Now, by way of having a resting-place during his excursions, avoiding the wretched cookery—which has been trying its best to poison me during the last four months, while you have manfully resisted its effects for as many years,—and obtaining a bed on which it is possible to slumber, Monte Cristo has furnished for himself a temporary abode where you first found him; but, to prevent the possibility of the Tuscan government taking a fancy to his enchanted palace, and thereby depriving him of the advantages naturally expected from so large an outlay of capital, he has wisely enough purchased the island, and taken its name. Just ask yourself, my good fellow, whether there are not many persons of our acquaintance who assume the names of lands and properties they never in their lives were masters of?”\n\n“But,” said Franz, “the Corsican bandits that were among the crew of his vessel?”\n\n“Why, really the thing seems to me simple enough. Nobody knows better than yourself that the bandits of Corsica are not rogues or thieves, but purely and simply fugitives, driven by some sinister motive from their native town or village, and that their fellowship involves no disgrace or stigma; for my own part, I protest that, should I ever go to Corsica, my first visit, ere even I presented myself to the mayor or prefect, should be to the bandits of Colomba, if I could only manage to find them; for, on my conscience, they are a race of men I admire greatly.”\n\n“Still,” persisted Franz, “I suppose you will allow that such men as Vampa and his band are regular villains, who have no other motive than plunder when they seize your person. How do you explain the influence the count evidently possessed over those ruffians?”\n\n“My good friend, as in all probability I own my present safety to that influence, it would ill become me to search too closely into its source; therefore, instead of condemning him for his intimacy with outlaws, you must give me leave to excuse any little irregularity there may be in such a connection; not altogether for preserving my life, for my own idea was that it never was in much danger, but certainly for saving me 4,000 piastres, which, being translated, means neither more nor less than 24,000 livres of our money—a sum at which, most assuredly, I should never have been estimated in France, proving most indisputably,” added Albert with a laugh, “that no prophet is honored in his own country.”\n\n“Talking of countries,” replied Franz, “of what country is the count, what is his native tongue, whence does he derive his immense fortune, and what were those events of his early life—a life as marvellous as unknown—that have tinctured his succeeding years with so dark and gloomy a misanthropy? Certainly these are questions that, in your place, I should like to have answered.”\n\n“My dear Franz,” replied Albert, “when, upon receipt of my letter, you found the necessity of asking the count’s assistance, you promptly went to him, saying, ‘My friend Albert de Morcerf is in danger; help me to deliver him.’ Was not that nearly what you said?”\n20222m\n\n“It was.”\n\n“Well, then, did he ask you, ‘Who is M. Albert de Morcerf? how does he come by his name—his fortune? what are his means of existence? what is his birthplace? of what country is he a native?’ Tell me, did he put all these questions to you?”\n\n“I confess he asked me none.”\n\n“No; he merely came and freed me from the hands of Signor Vampa, where, I can assure you, in spite of all my outward appearance of ease and unconcern, I did not very particularly care to remain. Now, then, Franz, when, for services so promptly and unhesitatingly rendered, he but asks me in return to do for him what is done daily for any Russian prince or Italian nobleman who may pass through Paris—merely to introduce him into society—would you have me refuse? My good fellow, you must have lost your senses to think it possible I could act with such cold-blooded policy.”\n\nAnd this time it must be confessed that, contrary to the usual state of affairs in discussions between the young men, the effective arguments were all on Albert’s side.\n\n“Well,” said Franz with a sigh, “do as you please my dear viscount, for your arguments are beyond my powers of refutation. Still, in spite of all, you must admit that this Count of Monte Cristo is a most singular personage.”\n\n“He is a philanthropist,” answered the other; “and no doubt his motive in visiting Paris is to compete for the Monthyon prize, given, as you are aware, to whoever shall be proved to have most materially advanced the interests of virtue and humanity. If my vote and interest can obtain it for him, I will readily give him the one and promise the other. And now, my dear Franz, let us talk of something else. Come, shall we take our luncheon, and then pay a last visit to St. Peter’s?”\n\nFranz silently assented; and the following afternoon, at half-past five o’clock, the young men parted. Albert de Morcerf to return to Paris, and Franz d’Épinay to pass a fortnight at Venice.\n\nBut, ere he entered his travelling carriage, Albert, fearing that his expected guest might forget the engagement he had entered into, placed in the care of a waiter at the hotel a card to be delivered to the Count of Monte Cristo, on which, beneath the name of Viscount Albert de Morcerf, he had written in pencil:\n\n“27, _Rue du Helder, on the_ 21_st May, half-past ten_ A.M.”\n\n# Chapter 39. The Guests\n\nIn the house in the Rue du Helder, where Albert had invited the Count of Monte Cristo, everything was being prepared on the morning of the 21st of May to do honor to the occasion. Albert de Morcerf inhabited a pavilion situated at the corner of a large court, and directly opposite another building, in which were the servants’ apartments. Two windows only of the pavilion faced the street; three other windows looked into the court, and two at the back into the garden.\n\nBetween the court and the garden, built in the heavy style of the imperial architecture, was the large and fashionable dwelling of the Count and Countess of Morcerf.\n\nA high wall surrounded the whole of the property, surmounted at intervals by vases filled with flowers, and broken in the centre by a large gate of gilded iron, which served as the carriage entrance. A small door, close to the lodge of the _concierge_, gave ingress and egress to the servants and masters when they were on foot.\n\nIt was easy to discover that the delicate care of a mother, unwilling to part from her son, and yet aware that a young man of the viscount’s age required the full exercise of his liberty, had chosen this habitation for Albert. There were not lacking, however, evidences of what we may call the intelligent egoism of a youth who is charmed with the indolent, careless life of an only son, and who lives as it were in a gilded cage. By means of the two windows looking into the street, Albert could see all that passed; the sight of what is going on is necessary to young men, who always want to see the world traverse their horizon, even if that horizon is only a public thoroughfare. Then, should anything appear to merit a more minute examination, Albert de Morcerf could follow up his researches by means of a small gate, similar to that close to the _concierge’s_ door, and which merits a particular description.\n\nIt was a little entrance that seemed never to have been opened since the house was built, so entirely was it covered with dust and dirt; but the well-oiled hinges and locks told quite another story. This door was a mockery to the _concierge_, from whose vigilance and jurisdiction it was free, and, like that famous portal in the _Arabian Nights_, opening at the “_Sesame_” of Ali Baba, it was wont to swing backward at a cabalistic word or a concerted tap from without from the sweetest voices or whitest fingers in the world.\n\nAt the end of a long corridor, with which the door communicated, and which formed the antechamber, was, on the right, Albert’s breakfast-room, looking into the court, and on the left the salon, looking into the garden. Shrubs and creeping plants covered the windows, and hid from the garden and court these two apartments, the only rooms into which, as they were on the ground floor, the prying eyes of the curious could penetrate.\n\nOn the floor above were similar rooms, with the addition of a third, formed out of the antechamber; these three rooms were a salon, a boudoir, and a bedroom. The salon downstairs was only an Algerian divan, for the use of smokers. The boudoir upstairs communicated with the bedchamber by an invisible door on the staircase; it was evident that every precaution had been taken. Above this floor was a large _atelier_, which had been increased in size by pulling down the partitions—a pandemonium, in which the artist and the dandy strove for pre-eminence.\n\nThere were collected and piled up all Albert’s successive caprices, hunting-horns, bass-viols, flutes—a whole orchestra, for Albert had had not a taste but a fancy for music; easels, palettes, brushes, pencils—for music had been succeeded by painting; foils, boxing-gloves, broadswords, and single-sticks—for, following the example of the fashionable young men of the time, Albert de Morcerf cultivated, with far more perseverance than music and drawing, the three arts that complete a dandy’s education, i.e., fencing, boxing, and single-stick; and it was here that he received Grisier, Cooks, and Charles Leboucher.\n\nThe rest of the furniture of this privileged apartment consisted of old cabinets, filled with Chinese porcelain and Japanese vases, Lucca della Robbia _faïences_, and Palissy platters; of old armchairs, in which perhaps had sat Henry IV. or Sully, Louis XIII. or Richelieu—for two of these armchairs, adorned with a carved shield, on which were engraved the fleur-de-lis of France on an azure field, evidently came from the Louvre, or, at least, some royal residence.\n\nOver these dark and sombre chairs were thrown splendid stuffs, dyed beneath Persia’s sun, or woven by the fingers of the women of Calcutta or of Chandernagor. What these stuffs did there, it was impossible to say; they awaited, while gratifying the eyes, a destination unknown to their owner himself; in the meantime they filled the place with their golden and silky reflections.\n\nIn the centre of the room was a Roller and Blanchet “baby grand” piano in rosewood, but holding the potentialities of an orchestra in its narrow and sonorous cavity, and groaning beneath the weight of the _chefs-d’œuvre_ of Beethoven, Weber, Mozart, Haydn, Grétry, and Porpora.\n\nOn the walls, over the doors, on the ceiling, were swords, daggers, Malay creeses, maces, battle-axes; gilded, damasked, and inlaid suits of armor; dried plants, minerals, and stuffed birds, their flame-colored wings outspread in motionless flight, and their beaks forever open. This was Albert’s favorite lounging place.\n\nHowever, the morning of the appointment, the young man had established himself in the small salon downstairs. There, on a table, surrounded at some distance by a large and luxurious divan, every species of tobacco known,—from the yellow tobacco of Petersburg to the black of Sinai, and so on along the scale from Maryland and Porto Rico, to Latakia,—was exposed in pots of crackled earthenware of which the Dutch are so fond; beside them, in boxes of fragrant wood, were ranged, according to their size and quality, puros, regalias, havanas, and manillas; and, in an open cabinet, a collection of German pipes, of chibouques, with their amber mouth-pieces ornamented with coral, and of narghiles, with their long tubes of morocco, awaiting the caprice or the sympathy of the smokers.\n\nAlbert had himself presided at the arrangement, or, rather, the symmetrical derangement, which, after coffee, the guests at a breakfast of modern days love to contemplate through the vapor that escapes from their mouths, and ascends in long and fanciful wreaths to the ceiling.\n\nAt a quarter to ten, a valet entered; he composed, with a little groom named John, and who only spoke English, all Albert’s establishment, although the cook of the hotel was always at his service, and on great occasions the count’s _chasseur_ also. This valet, whose name was Germain, and who enjoyed the entire confidence of his young master, held in one hand a number of papers, and in the other a packet of letters, which he gave to Albert. Albert glanced carelessly at the different missives, selected two written in a small and delicate hand, and enclosed in scented envelopes, opened them and perused their contents with some attention.\n\n“How did these letters come?” said he.\n\n“One by the post, Madame Danglars’ footman left the other.”\n\n“Let Madame Danglars know that I accept the place she offers me in her box. Wait; then, during the day, tell Rosa that when I leave the Opera I will sup with her as she wishes. Take her six bottles of different wine—Cyprus, sherry, and Malaga, and a barrel of Ostend oysters; get them at Borel’s, and be sure you say they are for me.”\n\n“At what o’clock, sir, do you breakfast?”\n20227m\n\n“What time is it now?”\n\n“A quarter to ten.”\n\n“Very well, at half past ten. Debray will, perhaps, be obliged to go to the minister—and besides” (Albert looked at his tablets), “it is the hour I told the count, 21st May, at half past ten; and though I do not much rely upon his promise, I wish to be punctual. Is the countess up yet?”\n\n“If you wish, I will inquire.”\n\n“Yes, ask her for one of her _liqueur_ cellarets, mine is incomplete; and tell her I shall have the honor of seeing her about three o’clock, and that I request permission to introduce someone to her.”\n\nThe valet left the room. Albert threw himself on the divan, tore off the cover of two or three of the papers, looked at the theatre announcements, made a face seeing they gave an opera, and not a ballet; hunted vainly amongst the advertisements for a new tooth-powder of which he had heard, and threw down, one after the other, the three leading papers of Paris, muttering,\n\n“These papers become more and more stupid every day.”\n\nA moment after, a carriage stopped before the door, and the servant announced M. Lucien Debray. A tall young man, with light hair, clear gray eyes, and thin and compressed lips, dressed in a blue coat with beautifully carved gold buttons, a white neckcloth, and a tortoiseshell eye-glass suspended by a silken thread, and which, by an effort of the superciliary and zygomatic muscles, he fixed in his eye, entered, with a half-official air, without smiling or speaking.\n\n“Good-morning, Lucien, good-morning,” said Albert; “your punctuality really alarms me. What do I say? punctuality! You, whom I expected last, you arrive at five minutes to ten, when the time fixed was half-past! Has the ministry resigned?”\n\n“No, my dear fellow,” returned the young man, seating himself on the divan; “reassure yourself; we are tottering always, but we never fall, and I begin to believe that we shall pass into a state of immobility, and then the affairs of the Peninsula will completely consolidate us.”\n\n“Ah, true; you drive Don Carlos out of Spain.”\n\n“No, no, my dear fellow, do not confound our plans. We take him to the other side of the French frontier, and offer him hospitality at Bourges.”\n\n“At Bourges?”\n\n“Yes, he has not much to complain of; Bourges is the capital of Charles VII. Do you not know that all Paris knew it yesterday, and the day before it had already transpired on the Bourse, and M. Danglars (I do not know by what means that man contrives to obtain intelligence as soon as we do) made a million!”\n\n“And you another order, for I see you have a blue ribbon at your button-hole.”\n\n“Yes; they sent me the order of Charles III.,” returned Debray carelessly.\n\n“Come, do not affect indifference, but confess you were pleased to have it.”\n\n“Oh, it is very well as a finish to the toilet. It looks very neat on a black coat buttoned up.”\n\n“And makes you resemble the Prince of Wales or the Duke of Reichstadt.”\n\n“It is for that reason you see me so early.”\n\n“Because you have the order of Charles III., and you wish to announce the good news to me?”\n\n“No, because I passed the night writing letters,—five-and-twenty despatches. I returned home at daybreak, and strove to sleep; but my head ached and I got up to have a ride for an hour. At the Bois de Boulogne, _ennui_ and hunger attacked me at once,—two enemies who rarely accompany each other, and who are yet leagued against me, a sort of Carlo-republican alliance. I then recollected you gave a breakfast this morning, and here I am. I am hungry, feed me; I am bored, amuse me.”\n\n“It is my duty as your host,” returned Albert, ringing the bell, while Lucien turned over, with his gold-mounted cane, the papers that lay on the table. “Germain, a glass of sherry and a biscuit. In the meantime, my dear Lucien, here are cigars—contraband, of course—try them, and persuade the minister to sell us such instead of poisoning us with cabbage leaves.”\n\n“_Peste!_ I will do nothing of the kind; the moment they come from government you would find them execrable. Besides, that does not concern the home but the financial department. Address yourself to M. Humann, section of the indirect contributions, corridor A., No. 26.”\n\n“On my word,” said Albert, “you astonish me by the extent of your knowledge. Take a cigar.”\n\n“Really, my dear Albert,” replied Lucien, lighting a manilla at a rose-colored taper that burnt in a beautifully enamelled stand—“how happy you are to have nothing to do. You do not know your own good fortune!”\n\n“And what would you do, my dear diplomatist,” replied Morcerf, with a slight degree of irony in his voice, “if you did nothing? What? private secretary to a minister, plunged at once into European cabals and Parisian intrigues; having kings, and, better still, queens, to protect, parties to unite, elections to direct; making more use of your cabinet with your pen and your telegraph than Napoleon did of his battle-fields with his sword and his victories; possessing five-and-twenty thousand francs a year, besides your place; a horse, for which Château-Renaud offered you four hundred louis, and which you would not part with; a tailor who never disappoints you; with the opera, the jockey-club, and other diversions, can you not amuse yourself? Well, I will amuse you.”\n\n“How?”\n\n“By introducing to you a new acquaintance.”\n\n“A man or a woman?”\n\n“A man.”\n\n“I know so many men already.”\n\n“But you do not know this man.”\n\n“Where does he come from—the end of the world?”\n\n“Farther still, perhaps.”\n\n“The deuce! I hope he does not bring our breakfast with him.”\n\n“Oh, no; our breakfast comes from my father’s kitchen. Are you hungry?”\n\n“Humiliating as such a confession is, I am. But I dined at M. de Villefort’s, and lawyers always give you very bad dinners. You would think they felt some remorse; did you ever remark that?”\n\n“Ah, depreciate other persons’ dinners; you ministers give such splendid ones.”\n\n“Yes; but we do not invite people of fashion. If we were not forced to entertain a parcel of country boobies because they think and vote with us, we should never dream of dining at home, I assure you.”\n\n“Well, take another glass of sherry and another biscuit.”\n\n“Willingly. Your Spanish wine is excellent. You see we were quite right to pacify that country.”\n\n“Yes; but Don Carlos?”\n\n“Well, Don Carlos will drink Bordeaux, and in ten years we will marry his son to the little queen.”\n\n“You will then obtain the Golden Fleece, if you are still in the ministry.”\n\n“I think, Albert, you have adopted the system of feeding me on smoke this morning.”\n\n“Well, you must allow it is the best thing for the stomach; but I hear Beauchamp in the next room; you can dispute together, and that will pass away the time.”\n\n“About what?”\n\n“About the papers.”\n\n“My dear friend,” said Lucien with an air of sovereign contempt, “do I ever read the papers?”\n\n“Then you will dispute the more.”\n\n“M. Beauchamp,” announced the servant. “Come in, come in,” said Albert, rising and advancing to meet the young man. “Here is Debray, who detests you without reading you, so he says.”\n\n“He is quite right,” returned Beauchamp; “for I criticise him without knowing what he does. Good-day, commander!”\n\n“Ah, you know that already,” said the private secretary, smiling and shaking hands with him.\n\n“_Pardieu!_”\n\n“And what do they say of it in the world?”\n\n“In which world? we have so many worlds in the year of grace 1838.”\n\n“In the entire political world, of which you are one of the leaders.”\n\n“They say that it is quite fair, and that sowing so much red, you ought to reap a little blue.”\n\n“Come, come, that is not bad!” said Lucien. “Why do you not join our party, my dear Beauchamp? With your talents you would make your fortune in three or four years.”\n\n“I only await one thing before following your advice; that is, a minister who will hold office for six months. My dear Albert, one word, for I must give poor Lucien a respite. Do we breakfast or dine? I must go to the Chamber, for our life is not an idle one.”\n\n“You only breakfast; I await two persons, and the instant they arrive we shall sit down to table.”\n\n# Chapter 40. The Breakfast\n\nAnd what sort of persons do you expect to breakfast?” said Beauchamp.\n\n“A gentleman, and a diplomatist.”\n\n“Then we shall have to wait two hours for the gentleman, and three for the diplomatist. I shall come back to dessert; keep me some strawberries, coffee, and cigars. I shall take a cutlet on my way to the Chamber.”\n\n“Do not do anything of the sort; for were the gentleman a Montmorency, and the diplomatist a Metternich, we will breakfast at eleven; in the meantime, follow Debray’s example, and take a glass of sherry and a biscuit.”\n\n“Be it so; I will stay; I must do something to distract my thoughts.”\n\n“You are like Debray, and yet it seems to me that when the minister is out of spirits, the opposition ought to be joyous.”\n\n“Ah, you do not know with what I am threatened. I shall hear this morning that M. Danglars make a speech at the Chamber of Deputies, and at his wife’s this evening I shall hear the tragedy of a peer of France. The devil take the constitutional government, and since we had our choice, as they say, at least, how could we choose that?”\n\n“I understand; you must lay in a stock of hilarity.”\n\n“Do not run down M. Danglars’ speeches,” said Debray; “he votes for you, for he belongs to the opposition.”\n\n“_Pardieu_, that is exactly the worst of all. I am waiting until you send him to speak at the Luxembourg, to laugh at my ease.”\n\n“My dear friend,” said Albert to Beauchamp, “it is plain that the affairs of Spain are settled, for you are most desperately out of humor this morning. Recollect that Parisian gossip has spoken of a marriage between myself and Mlle. Eugénie Danglars; I cannot in conscience, therefore, let you run down the speeches of a man who will one day say to me, ‘Vicomte, you know I give my daughter two millions.’”\n\n“Ah, this marriage will never take place,” said Beauchamp. “The king has made him a baron, and can make him a peer, but he cannot make him a gentleman, and the Count of Morcerf is too aristocratic to consent, for the paltry sum of two million francs, to a _mésalliance_. The Viscount of Morcerf can only wed a marchioness.”\n\n“But two million francs make a nice little sum,” replied Morcerf.\n\n“It is the social capital of a theatre on the boulevard, or a railroad from the Jardin des Plantes to La Râpée.”\n\n“Never mind what he says, Morcerf,” said Debray, “do you marry her. You marry a money-bag label, it is true; well, but what does that matter? It is better to have a blazon less and a figure more on it. You have seven martlets on your arms; give three to your wife, and you will still have four; that is one more than M. de Guise had, who so nearly became King of France, and whose cousin was Emperor of Germany.”\n\n“On my word, I think you are right, Lucien,” said Albert absently.\n\n“To be sure; besides, every millionaire is as noble as a bastard—that is, he can be.”\n\n“Do not say that, Debray,” returned Beauchamp, laughing, “for here is Château-Renaud, who, to cure you of your mania for paradoxes, will pass the sword of Renaud de Montauban, his ancestor, through your body.”\n\n“He will sully it then,” returned Lucien; “for I am low—very low.”\n\n“Oh, heavens,” cried Beauchamp, “the minister quotes Béranger, what shall we come to next?”\n\n“M. de Château-Renaud—M. Maximilian Morrel,” said the servant, announcing two fresh guests.\n\n“Now, then, to breakfast,” said Beauchamp; “for, if I remember, you told me you only expected two persons, Albert.”\n\n“Morrel,” muttered Albert—“Morrel—who is he?”\n\nBut before he had finished, M. de Château-Renaud, a handsome young man of thirty, gentleman all over,—that is, with the figure of a Guiche and the wit of a Mortemart,—took Albert’s hand.\n\n“My dear Albert,” said he, “let me introduce to you M. Maximilian Morrel, captain of Spahis, my friend; and what is more—however the man speaks for himself—my preserver. Salute my hero, viscount.”\n\nAnd he stepped on one side to give place to a young man of refined and dignified bearing, with large and open brow, piercing eyes, and black moustache, whom our readers have already seen at Marseilles, under circumstances sufficiently dramatic not to be forgotten. A rich uniform, half French, half Oriental, set off his graceful and stalwart figure, and his broad chest was decorated with the order of the Legion of Honor. The young officer bowed with easy and elegant politeness.\n\n“Monsieur,” said Albert with affectionate courtesy, “the count of Château-Renaud knew how much pleasure this introduction would give me; you are his friend, be ours also.”\n\n“Well said,” interrupted Château-Renaud; “and pray that, if you should ever be in a similar predicament, he may do as much for you as he did for me.”\n\n“What has he done?” asked Albert.\n\n“Oh, nothing worth speaking of,” said Morrel; “M. de Château-Renaud exaggerates.”\n\n“Not worth speaking of?” cried Château-Renaud; “life is not worth speaking of!—that is rather too philosophical, on my word, Morrel. It is very well for you, who risk your life every day, but for me, who only did so once——”\n\n“We gather from all this, baron, that Captain Morrel saved your life.”\n\n“Exactly so.”\n\n“On what occasion?” asked Beauchamp.\n\n“Beauchamp, my good fellow, you know I am starving,” said Debray: “do not set him off on some long story.”\n\n“Well, I do not prevent your sitting down to table,” replied Beauchamp, “Château-Renaud can tell us while we eat our breakfast.”\n\n“Gentlemen,” said Morcerf, “it is only a quarter past ten, and I expect someone else.”\n\n“Ah, true, a diplomatist!” observed Debray.\n\n“Diplomat or not, I don’t know; I only know that he charged himself on my account with a mission, which he terminated so entirely to my satisfaction, that had I been king, I should have instantly created him knight of all my orders, even had I been able to offer him the Golden Fleece and the Garter.”\n\n“Well, since we are not to sit down to table,” said Debray, “take a glass of sherry, and tell us all about it.”\n\n“You all know that I had the fancy of going to Africa.”\n\n“It is a road your ancestors have traced for you,” said Albert gallantly.\n\n“Yes? but I doubt that your object was like theirs—to rescue the Holy Sepulchre.”\n\n“You are quite right, Beauchamp,” observed the young aristocrat. “It was only to fight as an amateur. I cannot bear duelling ever since two seconds, whom I had chosen to arrange an affair, forced me to break the arm of one of my best friends, one whom you all know—poor Franz d’Épinay.”\n\n“Ah, true,” said Debray, “you did fight some time ago; about what?”\n20235m\n\n“The devil take me, if I remember,” returned Château-Renaud. “But I recollect perfectly one thing, that, being unwilling to let such talents as mine sleep, I wished to try upon the Arabs the new pistols that had been given to me. In consequence I embarked for Oran, and went from thence to Constantine, where I arrived just in time to witness the raising of the siege. I retreated with the rest, for eight-and-forty hours. I endured the rain during the day, and the cold during the night tolerably well, but the third morning my horse died of cold. Poor brute—accustomed to be covered up and to have a stove in the stable, the Arabian finds himself unable to bear ten degrees of cold in Arabia.”\n\n“That’s why you want to purchase my English horse,” said Debray, “you think he will bear the cold better.”\n\n“You are mistaken, for I have made a vow never to return to Africa.”\n\n“You were very much frightened, then?” asked Beauchamp.\n\n“Well, yes, and I had good reason to be so,” replied Château-Renaud. “I was retreating on foot, for my horse was dead. Six Arabs came up, full gallop, to cut off my head. I shot two with my double-barrelled gun, and two more with my pistols, but I was then disarmed, and two were still left; one seized me by the hair (that is why I now wear it so short, for no one knows what may happen), the other swung a yataghan, and I already felt the cold steel on my neck, when this gentleman whom you see here charged them, shot the one who held me by the hair, and cleft the skull of the other with his sabre. He had assigned himself the task of saving a man’s life that day; chance caused that man to be myself. When I am rich I will order a statue of Chance from Klagmann or Marochetti.”\n\n“Yes,” said Morrel, smiling, “it was the 5th of September, the anniversary of the day on which my father was miraculously preserved; therefore, as far as it lies in my power, I endeavor to celebrate it by some——”\n\n20237m\n“Heroic action,” interrupted Château-Renaud. “I was chosen. But that is not all—after rescuing me from the sword, he rescued me from the cold, not by sharing his cloak with me, like St. Martin, but by giving me the whole; then from hunger by sharing with me—guess what?”\n\n“A Strasbourg pie?” asked Beauchamp.\n\n“No, his horse; of which we each of us ate a slice with a hearty appetite. It was very hard.”\n\n“The horse?” said Morcerf, laughing.\n\n“No, the sacrifice,” returned Château-Renaud; “ask Debray if he would sacrifice his English steed for a stranger?”\n\n“Not for a stranger,” said Debray, “but for a friend I might, perhaps.”\n\n“I divined that you would become mine, count,” replied Morrel; “besides, as I had the honor to tell you, heroism or not, sacrifice or not, that day I owed an offering to bad fortune in recompense for the favors good fortune had on other days granted to us.”\n\n“The history to which M. Morrel alludes,” continued Château-Renaud, “is an admirable one, which he will tell you some day when you are better acquainted with him; today let us fill our stomachs, and not our memories. What time do you breakfast, Albert?”\n\n“At half-past ten.”\n\n“Precisely?” asked Debray, taking out his watch.\n\n“Oh, you will give me five minutes’ grace,” replied Morcerf, “for I also expect a preserver.”\n\n“Of whom?”\n\n“Of myself,” cried Morcerf; “_parbleu!_ do you think I cannot be saved as well as anyone else, and that there are only Arabs who cut off heads? Our breakfast is a philanthropic one, and we shall have at table—at least, I hope so—two benefactors of humanity.”\n\n“What shall we do?” said Debray; “we have only one Monthyon prize.”\n\n“Well, it will be given to someone who has done nothing to deserve it,” said Beauchamp; “that is the way the Academy mostly escapes from the dilemma.”\n\n“And where does he come from?” asked Debray. “You have already answered the question once, but so vaguely that I venture to put it a second time.”\n\n“Really,” said Albert, “I do not know; when I invited him three months ago, he was then at Rome, but since that time who knows where he may have gone?”\n\n“And you think him capable of being exact?” demanded Debray.\n\n“I think him capable of everything.”\n\n“Well, with the five minutes’ grace, we have only ten left.”\n\n“I will profit by them to tell you something about my guest.”\n\n“I beg pardon,” interrupted Beauchamp; “are there any materials for an article in what you are going to tell us?”\n\n“Yes, and for a most curious one.”\n\n“Go on, then, for I see I shall not get to the Chamber this morning, and I must make up for it.”\n\n“I was at Rome during the last Carnival.”\n\n“We know that,” said Beauchamp.\n\n“Yes, but what you do not know is that I was carried off by bandits.”\n\n“There are no bandits,” cried Debray.\n\n“Yes there are, and most hideous, or rather most admirable ones, for I found them ugly enough to frighten me.”\n\n“Come, my dear Albert,” said Debray, “confess that your cook is behindhand, that the oysters have not arrived from Ostend or Marennes, and that, like Madame de Maintenon, you are going to replace the dish by a story. Say so at once; we are sufficiently well-bred to excuse you, and to listen to your history, fabulous as it promises to be.”\n\n“And I say to you, fabulous as it may seem, I tell it as a true one from beginning to end. The brigands had carried me off, and conducted me to a gloomy spot, called the Catacombs of Saint Sebastian.”\n\n“I know it,” said Château-Renaud; “I narrowly escaped catching a fever there.”\n\n“And I did more than that,” replied Morcerf, “for I caught one. I was informed that I was prisoner until I paid the sum of 4,000 Roman crowns—about 24,000 francs. Unfortunately, I had not above 1,500. I was at the end of my journey and of my credit. I wrote to Franz—and were he here he would confirm every word—I wrote then to Franz that if he did not come with the four thousand crowns before six, at ten minutes past I should have gone to join the blessed saints and glorious martyrs in whose company I had the honor of being; and Signor Luigi Vampa, such was the name of the chief of these bandits, would have scrupulously kept his word.”\n\n“But Franz did come with the four thousand crowns,” said Château-Renaud. “A man whose name is Franz d’Épinay or Albert de Morcerf has not much difficulty in procuring them.”\n\n“No, he arrived accompanied simply by the guest I am going to present to you.”\n\n“Ah, this gentleman is a Hercules killing Cacus, a Perseus freeing Andromeda.”\n\n“No, he is a man about my own size.”\n\n“Armed to the teeth?”\n\n“He had not even a knitting-needle.”\n\n“But he paid your ransom?”\n\n“He said two words to the chief and I was free.”\n\n“And they apologized to him for having carried you off?” said Beauchamp.\n\n“Just so.”\n\n“Why, he is a second Ariosto.”\n\n“No, his name is the Count of Monte Cristo.”\n\n“There is no Count of Monte Cristo” said Debray.\n\n“I do not think so,” added Château-Renaud, with the air of a man who knows the whole of the European nobility perfectly.\n\n“Does anyone know anything of a Count of Monte Cristo?”\n\n“He comes possibly from the Holy Land, and one of his ancestors possessed Calvary, as the Mortemarts did the Dead Sea.”\n\n“I think I can assist your researches,” said Maximilian. “Monte Cristo is a little island I have often heard spoken of by the old sailors my father employed—a grain of sand in the centre of the Mediterranean, an atom in the infinite.”\n\n“Precisely!” cried Albert. “Well, he of whom I speak is the lord and master of this grain of sand, of this atom; he has purchased the title of count somewhere in Tuscany.”\n\n“He is rich, then?”\n\n“I believe so.”\n\n“But that ought to be visible.”\n\n“That is what deceives you, Debray.”\n\n“I do not understand you.”\n\n“Have you read the _Arabian Nights_?”\n\n“What a question!”\n\n“Well, do you know if the persons you see there are rich or poor, if their sacks of wheat are not rubies or diamonds? They seem like poor fishermen, and suddenly they open some mysterious cavern filled with the wealth of the Indies.”\n\n“Which means?”\n\n“Which means that my Count of Monte Cristo is one of those fishermen. He has even a name taken from the book, since he calls himself Sinbad the Sailor, and has a cave filled with gold.”\n\n“And you have seen this cavern, Morcerf?” asked Beauchamp.\n\n“No, but Franz has; for heaven’s sake, not a word of this before him. Franz went in with his eyes blindfolded, and was waited on by mutes and by women to whom Cleopatra was a painted strumpet. Only he is not quite sure about the women, for they did not come in until after he had taken hashish, so that what he took for women might have been simply a row of statues.”\n\nThe two young men looked at Morcerf as if to say,—“Are you mad, or are you laughing at us?”\n\n“And I also,” said Morrel thoughtfully, “have heard something like this from an old sailor named Penelon.”\n\n“Ah,” cried Albert, “it is very lucky that M. Morrel comes to aid me; you are vexed, are you not, that he thus gives a clew to the labyrinth?”\n\n“My dear Albert,” said Debray, “what you tell us is so extraordinary.”\n\n“Ah, because your ambassadors and your consuls do not tell you of them—they have no time. They are too much taken up with interfering in the affairs of their countrymen who travel.”\n\n“Now you get angry, and attack our poor agents. How will you have them protect you? The Chamber cuts down their salaries every day, so that now they have scarcely any. Will you be ambassador, Albert? I will send you to Constantinople.”\n\n“No, lest on the first demonstration I make in favor of Mehemet Ali, the Sultan send me the bowstring, and make my secretaries strangle me.”\n\n“You say very true,” responded Debray.\n\n“Yes,” said Albert, “but this has nothing to do with the existence of the Count of Monte Cristo.”\n\n“_Pardieu!_ everyone exists.”\n\n“Doubtless, but not in the same way; everyone has not black slaves, a princely retinue, an arsenal of weapons that would do credit to an Arabian fortress, horses that cost six thousand francs apiece, and Greek mistresses.”\n\n“Have you seen the Greek mistress?”\n\n“I have both seen and heard her. I saw her at the theatre, and heard her one morning when I breakfasted with the count.”\n\n“He eats, then?”\n“Yes; but so little, it can hardly be called eating.”\n\n“He must be a vampire.”\n\n“Laugh, if you will; the Countess G——, who knew Lord Ruthven, declared that the count was a vampire.”\n\n“Ah, capital,” said Beauchamp. “For a man not connected with newspapers, here is the pendant to the famous sea-serpent of the _Constitutionnel_.”\n\n“Wild eyes, the iris of which contracts or dilates at pleasure,” said Debray; “facial angle strongly developed, magnificent forehead, livid complexion, black beard, sharp and white teeth, politeness unexceptionable.”\n\n“Just so, Lucien,” returned Morcerf; “you have described him feature for feature. Yes, keen and cutting politeness. This man has often made me shudder; and one day when we were viewing an execution, I thought I should faint, more from hearing the cold and calm manner in which he spoke of every description of torture, than from the sight of the executioner and the culprit.”\n\n“Did he not conduct you to the ruins of the Colosseum and suck your blood?” asked Beauchamp.\n\n“Or, having delivered you, make you sign a flaming parchment, surrendering your soul to him as Esau did his birth-right?”\n\n“Rail on, rail on at your ease, gentlemen,” said Morcerf, somewhat piqued. “When I look at you Parisians, idlers on the Boulevard de Gand or the Bois de Boulogne, and think of this man, it seems to me we are not of the same race.”\n\n“I am highly flattered,” returned Beauchamp.\n\n“At the same time,” added Château-Renaud, “your Count of Monte Cristo is a very fine fellow, always excepting his little arrangements with the Italian banditti.”\n\n“There are no Italian banditti,” said Debray.\n\n“No vampire,” cried Beauchamp.\n\n“No Count of Monte Cristo” added Debray. “There is half-past ten striking, Albert.”\n20243m\n\n“Confess you have dreamed this, and let us sit down to breakfast,” continued Beauchamp.\n\nBut the sound of the clock had not died away when Germain announced, “His excellency the Count of Monte Cristo.” The involuntary start everyone gave proved how much Morcerf’s narrative had impressed them, and Albert himself could not wholly refrain from manifesting sudden emotion. He had not heard a carriage stop in the street, or steps in the antechamber; the door had itself opened noiselessly. The count appeared, dressed with the greatest simplicity, but the most fastidious dandy could have found nothing to cavil at in his toilet. Every article of dress—hat, coat, gloves, and boots—was from the first makers. He seemed scarcely five-and-thirty. But what struck everybody was his extreme resemblance to the portrait Debray had drawn. The count advanced, smiling, into the centre of the room, and approached Albert, who hastened towards him holding out his hand in a ceremonial manner.\n\n“Punctuality,” said Monte Cristo, “is the politeness of kings, according to one of your sovereigns, I think; but it is not the same with travellers. However, I hope you will excuse the two or three seconds I am behindhand; five hundred leagues are not to be accomplished without some trouble, and especially in France, where, it seems, it is forbidden to beat the postilions.”\n\n“My dear count,” replied Albert, “I was announcing your visit to some of my friends, whom I had invited in consequence of the promise you did me the honor to make, and whom I now present to you. They are the Count of Château-Renaud, whose nobility goes back to the twelve peers, and whose ancestors had a place at the Round Table; M. Lucien Debray, private secretary to the minister of the interior; M. Beauchamp, an editor of a paper, and the terror of the French government, but of whom, in spite of his national celebrity, you perhaps have not heard in Italy, since his paper is prohibited there; and M. Maximilian Morrel, captain of Spahis.”\n\nAt this name the count, who had hitherto saluted everyone with courtesy, but at the same time with coldness and formality, stepped a pace forward, and a slight tinge of red colored his pale cheeks.\n\n“You wear the uniform of the new French conquerors, monsieur,” said he; “it is a handsome uniform.”\n\nNo one could have said what caused the count’s voice to vibrate so deeply, and what made his eye flash, which was in general so clear, lustrous, and limpid when he pleased.\n\n“You have never seen our Africans, count?” said Albert.\n\n“Never,” replied the count, who was by this time perfectly master of himself again.\n\n“Well, beneath this uniform beats one of the bravest and noblest hearts in the whole army.”\n\n“Oh, M. de Morcerf,” interrupted Morrel.\n\n“Let me go on, captain. And we have just heard,” continued Albert, “of a new deed of his, and so heroic a one, that, although I have seen him today for the first time, I request you to allow me to introduce him as my friend.”\n\nAt these words it was still possible to observe in Monte Cristo the concentrated look, changing color, and slight trembling of the eyelid that show emotion.\n\n“Ah, you have a noble heart,” said the count; “so much the better.”\n\nThis exclamation, which corresponded to the count’s own thought rather than to what Albert was saying, surprised everybody, and especially Morrel, who looked at Monte Cristo with wonder. But, at the same time, the intonation was so soft that, however strange the speech might seem, it was impossible to be offended at it.\n20245m\n\n“Why should he doubt it?” said Beauchamp to Château-Renaud.\n\n“In reality,” replied the latter, who, with his aristocratic glance and his knowledge of the world, had penetrated at once all that was penetrable in Monte Cristo, “Albert has not deceived us, for the count is a most singular being. What say you, Morrel!”\n\n“_Ma foi_, he has an open look about him that pleases me, in spite of the singular remark he has made about me.”\n\n“Gentlemen,” said Albert, “Germain informs me that breakfast is ready. My dear count, allow me to show you the way.” They passed silently into the breakfast-room, and everyone took his place.\n\n“Gentlemen,” said the count, seating himself, “permit me to make a confession which must form my excuse for any improprieties I may commit. I am a stranger, and a stranger to such a degree, that this is the first time I have ever been at Paris. The French way of living is utterly unknown to me, and up to the present time I have followed the Eastern customs, which are entirely in contrast to the Parisian. I beg you, therefore, to excuse if you find anything in me too Turkish, too Italian, or too Arabian. Now, then, let us breakfast.”\n\n“With what an air he says all this,” muttered Beauchamp; “decidedly he is a great man.”\n\n“A great man in his own country,” added Debray.\n\n“A great man in every country, M. Debray,” said Château-Renaud.\n\nThe count was, it may be remembered, a most temperate guest. Albert remarked this, expressing his fears lest, at the outset, the Parisian mode of life should displease the traveller in the most essential point.\n\n“My dear count,” said he, “I fear one thing, and that is, that the fare of the Rue du Helder is not so much to your taste as that of the Piazza di Spagna. I ought to have consulted you on the point, and have had some dishes prepared expressly.”\n\n“Did you know me better,” returned the count, smiling, “you would not give one thought of such a thing for a traveller like myself, who has successively lived on macaroni at Naples, polenta at Milan, olla podrida at Valencia, pilau at Constantinople, curry in India, and swallows’ nests in China. I eat everywhere, and of everything, only I eat but little; and today, that you reproach me with my want of appetite, is my day of appetite, for I have not eaten since yesterday morning.”\n\n“What,” cried all the guests, “you have not eaten for four-and-twenty hours?”\n\n“No,” replied the count; “I was forced to go out of my road to obtain some information near Nîmes, so that I was somewhat late, and therefore I did not choose to stop.”\n\n“And you ate in your carriage?” asked Morcerf.\n\n“No, I slept, as I generally do when I am weary without having the courage to amuse myself, or when I am hungry without feeling inclined to eat.”\n\n“But you can sleep when you please, monsieur?” said Morrel.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You have a recipe for it?”\n\n“An infallible one.”\n\n“That would be invaluable to us in Africa, who have not always any food to eat, and rarely anything to drink.”\n\n“Yes,” said Monte Cristo; “but, unfortunately, a recipe excellent for a man like myself would be very dangerous applied to an army, which might not awake when it was needed.”\n\n“May we inquire what is this recipe?” asked Debray.\n\n“Oh, yes,” returned Monte Cristo; “I make no secret of it. It is a mixture of excellent opium, which I fetched myself from Canton in order to have it pure, and the best hashish which grows in the East—that is, between the Tigris and the Euphrates. These two ingredients are mixed in equal proportions, and formed into pills. Ten minutes after one is taken, the effect is produced. Ask Baron Franz d’Épinay; I think he tasted them one day.”\n\n“Yes,” replied Morcerf, “he said something about it to me.”\n\n“But,” said Beauchamp, who, as became a journalist, was very incredulous, “you always carry this drug about you?”\n\n“Always.”\n“Would it be an indiscretion to ask to see those precious pills?” continued Beauchamp, hoping to take him at a disadvantage.\n\n“No, monsieur,” returned the count; and he drew from his pocket a marvellous casket, formed out of a single emerald and closed by a golden lid which unscrewed and gave passage to a small greenish colored pellet about the size of a pea. This ball had an acrid and penetrating odor. There were four or five more in the emerald, which would contain about a dozen. The casket passed around the table, but it was more to examine the admirable emerald than to see the pills that it passed from hand to hand.\n\n“And is it your cook who prepares these pills?” asked Beauchamp.\n\n“Oh, no, monsieur,” replied Monte Cristo; “I do not thus betray my enjoyments to the vulgar. I am a tolerable chemist, and prepare my pills myself.”\n\n“This is a magnificent emerald, and the largest I have ever seen,” said Château-Renaud, “although my mother has some remarkable family jewels.”\n\n“I had three similar ones,” returned Monte Cristo. “I gave one to the Sultan, who mounted it in his sabre; another to our holy father the Pope, who had it set in his tiara, opposite to one nearly as large, though not so fine, given by the Emperor Napoleon to his predecessor, Pius VII. I kept the third for myself, and I had it hollowed out, which reduced its value, but rendered it more commodious for the purpose I intended.”\n\nEveryone looked at Monte Cristo with astonishment; he spoke with so much simplicity that it was evident he spoke the truth, or that he was mad. However, the sight of the emerald made them naturally incline to the former belief.\n\n“And what did these two sovereigns give you in exchange for these magnificent presents?” asked Debray.\n\n“The Sultan, the liberty of a woman,” replied the Count; “the Pope, the life of a man; so that once in my life I have been as powerful as if heaven had brought me into the world on the steps of a throne.”\n\n“And it was Peppino you saved, was it not?” cried Morcerf; “it was for him that you obtained pardon?”\n\n“Perhaps,” returned the count, smiling.\n\n“My dear count, you have no idea what pleasure it gives me to hear you speak thus,” said Morcerf. “I had announced you beforehand to my friends as an enchanter of the _Arabian Nights_, a wizard of the Middle Ages; but the Parisians are so subtle in paradoxes that they mistake for caprices of the imagination the most incontestable truths, when these truths do not form a part of their daily existence. For example, here is Debray who reads, and Beauchamp who prints, every day, ‘A member of the Jockey Club has been stopped and robbed on the Boulevard;’ ‘four persons have been assassinated in the Rue St. Denis’ or ‘the Faubourg St. Germain;’ ‘ten, fifteen, or twenty thieves, have been arrested in a _café_ on the Boulevard du Temple, or in the Thermes de Julien,’—and yet these same men deny the existence of the bandits in the Maremma, the Campagna di Romana, or the Pontine Marshes. Tell them yourself that I was taken by bandits, and that without your generous intercession I should now have been sleeping in the Catacombs of St. Sebastian, instead of receiving them in my humble abode in the Rue du Helder.”\n\n“Ah,” said Monte Cristo “you promised me never to mention that circumstance.”\n\n“It was not I who made that promise,” cried Morcerf; “it must have been someone else whom you have rescued in the same manner, and whom you have forgotten. Pray speak of it, for I shall not only, I trust, relate the little I do know, but also a great deal I do not know.”\n\n“It seems to me,” returned the count, smiling, “that you played a sufficiently important part to know as well as myself what happened.”\n\n20249m\n“Well, you promise me, if I tell all I know, to relate, in your turn, all that I do not know?”\n\n“That is but fair,” replied Monte Cristo.\n\n“Well,” said Morcerf, “for three days I believed myself the object of the attentions of a masque, whom I took for a descendant of Tullia or Poppæa, while I was simply the object of the attentions of a _contadina_, and I say _contadina_ to avoid saying peasant girl. What I know is, that, like a fool, a greater fool than he of whom I spoke just now, I mistook for this peasant girl a young bandit of fifteen or sixteen, with a beardless chin and slim waist, and who, just as I was about to imprint a chaste salute on his lips, placed a pistol to my head, and, aided by seven or eight others, led, or rather dragged me, to the Catacombs of St. Sebastian, where I found a highly educated brigand chief perusing Cæsar’s _Commentaries_, and who deigned to leave off reading to inform me, that unless the next morning, before six o’clock, four thousand piastres were paid into his account at his banker’s, at a quarter past six I should have ceased to exist. The letter is still to be seen, for it is in Franz d’Épinay’s possession, signed by me, and with a postscript of M. Luigi Vampa. This is all I know, but I know not, count, how you contrived to inspire so much respect in the bandits of Rome who ordinarily have so little respect for anything. I assure you, Franz and I were lost in admiration.”\n\n“Nothing more simple,” returned the count. “I had known the famous Vampa for more than ten years. When he was quite a child, and only a shepherd, I gave him a few gold pieces for showing me my way, and he, in order to repay me, gave me a poniard, the hilt of which he had carved with his own hand, and which you may have seen in my collection of arms. In after years, whether he had forgotten this interchange of presents, which ought to have cemented our friendship, or whether he did not recollect me, he sought to take me, but, on the contrary, it was I who captured him and a dozen of his band. I might have handed him over to Roman justice, which is somewhat expeditious, and which would have been particularly so with him; but I did nothing of the sort—I suffered him and his band to depart.”\n\n“With the condition that they should sin no more,” said Beauchamp, laughing. “I see they kept their promise.”\n\n“No, monsieur,” returned Monte Cristo “upon the simple condition that they should respect myself and my friends. Perhaps what I am about to say may seem strange to you, who are socialists, and vaunt humanity and your duty to your neighbor, but I never seek to protect a society which does not protect me, and which I will even say, generally occupies itself about me only to injure me; and thus by giving them a low place in my esteem, and preserving a neutrality towards them, it is society and my neighbor who are indebted to me.”\n\n“Bravo,” cried Château-Renaud; “you are the first man I ever met sufficiently courageous to preach egotism. Bravo, count, bravo!”\n\n“It is frank, at least,” said Morrel. “But I am sure that the count does not regret having once deviated from the principles he has so boldly avowed.”\n\n“How have I deviated from those principles, monsieur?” asked Monte Cristo, who could not help looking at Morrel with so much intensity, that two or three times the young man had been unable to sustain that clear and piercing glance.\n\n“Why, it seems to me,” replied Morrel, “that in delivering M. de Morcerf, whom you did not know, you did good to your neighbor and to society.”\n\n“Of which he is the brightest ornament,” said Beauchamp, drinking off a glass of champagne.\n\n“My dear count,” cried Morcerf, “you are at fault—you, one of the most formidable logicians I know—and you must see it clearly proved that instead of being an egotist, you are a philanthropist. Ah, you call yourself Oriental, a Levantine, Maltese, Indian, Chinese; your family name is Monte Cristo; Sinbad the Sailor is your baptismal appellation, and yet the first day you set foot in Paris you instinctively display the greatest virtue, or rather the chief defect, of us eccentric Parisians,—that is, you assume the vices you have not, and conceal the virtues you possess.”\n\n“My dear vicomte,” returned Monte Cristo, “I do not see, in all I have done, anything that merits, either from you or these gentlemen, the pretended eulogies I have received. You were no stranger to me, for I knew you from the time I gave up two rooms to you, invited you to breakfast with me, lent you one of my carriages, witnessed the Carnival in your company, and saw with you from a window in the Piazza del Popolo the execution that affected you so much that you nearly fainted. I will appeal to any of these gentlemen, could I leave my guest in the hands of a hideous bandit, as you term him? Besides, you know, I had the idea that you could introduce me into some of the Paris salons when I came to France. You might some time ago have looked upon this resolution as a vague project, but today you see it was a reality, and you must submit to it under penalty of breaking your word.”\n\n“I will keep it,” returned Morcerf; “but I fear that you will be much disappointed, accustomed as you are to picturesque events and fantastic horizons. Amongst us you will not meet with any of those episodes with which your adventurous existence has so familiarized you; our Chimborazo is Mortmartre, our Himalaya is Mount Valérien, our Great Desert is the plain of Grenelle, where they are now boring an artesian well to water the caravans. We have plenty of thieves, though not so many as is said; but these thieves stand in far more dread of a policeman than a lord. France is so prosaic, and Paris so civilized a city, that you will not find in its eighty-five departments—I say eighty-five, because I do not include Corsica—you will not find, then, in these eighty-five departments a single hill on which there is not a telegraph, or a grotto in which the commissary of police has not put up a gaslamp. There is but one service I can render you, and for that I place myself entirely at your orders, that is, to present, or make my friends present, you everywhere; besides, you have no need of anyone to introduce you—with your name, and your fortune, and your talent” (Monte Cristo bowed with a somewhat ironical smile) “you can present yourself everywhere, and be well received. I can be useful in one way only—if knowledge of Parisian habits, of the means of rendering yourself comfortable, or of the bazaars, can assist, you may depend upon me to find you a fitting dwelling here. I do not dare offer to share my apartments with you, as I shared yours at Rome—I, who do not profess egotism, but am yet egotist _par excellence_; for, except myself, these rooms would not hold a shadow more, unless that shadow were feminine.”\n\n“Ah,” said the count, “that is a most conjugal reservation; I recollect that at Rome you said something of a projected marriage. May I congratulate you?”\n\n“The affair is still in projection.”\n\n“And he who says in ‘projection,’ means already decided,” said Debray.\n\n“No,” replied Morcerf, “my father is most anxious about it; and I hope, ere long, to introduce you, if not to my wife, at least to my betrothed—Mademoiselle Eugénie Danglars.”\n\n“Eugénie Danglars,” said Monte Cristo; “tell me, is not her father Baron Danglars?”\n\n“Yes,” returned Morcerf, “a baron of a new creation.”\n\n“What matter,” said Monte Cristo “if he has rendered the State services which merit this distinction?”\n\n“Enormous ones,” answered Beauchamp. “Although in reality a Liberal, he negotiated a loan of six millions for Charles X., in 1829, who made him a baron and chevalier of the Legion of Honor; so that he wears the ribbon, not, as you would think, in his waistcoat-pocket, but at his button-hole.”\n\n“Ah,” interrupted Morcerf, laughing, “Beauchamp, Beauchamp, keep that for the _Corsaire_ or the _Charivari_, but spare my future father-in-law before me.” Then, turning to Monte Cristo, “You just now spoke his name as if you knew the baron?”\n\n“I do not know him,” returned Monte Cristo; “but I shall probably soon make his acquaintance, for I have a credit opened with him by the house of Richard & Blount, of London, Arstein & Eskeles of Vienna, and Thomson & French at Rome.” As he pronounced the two last names, the count glanced at Maximilian Morrel. If the stranger expected to produce an effect on Morrel, he was not mistaken—Maximilian started as if he had been electrified.\n\n“Thomson & French,” said he; “do you know this house, monsieur?”\n\n20253m\n“They are my bankers in the capital of the Christian world,” returned the count quietly. “Can my influence with them be of any service to you?”\n\n“Oh, count, you could assist me perhaps in researches which have been, up to the present, fruitless. This house, in past years, did ours a great service, and has, I know not for what reason, always denied having rendered us this service.”\n\n“I shall be at your orders,” said Monte Cristo bowing.\n\n“But,” continued Morcerf, “_à propos_ of Danglars,—we have strangely wandered from the subject. We were speaking of a suitable habitation for the Count of Monte Cristo. Come, gentlemen, let us all propose some place. Where shall we lodge this new guest in our great capital?”\n\n“Faubourg Saint-Germain,” said Château-Renaud. “The count will find there a charming hotel, with a court and garden.”\n\n“Bah! Château-Renaud,” returned Debray, “you only know your dull and gloomy Faubourg Saint-Germain; do not pay any attention to him, count—live in the Chaussée d’Antin, that’s the real centre of Paris.”\n\n“Boulevard de l’Opéra,” said Beauchamp; “the second floor—a house with a balcony. The count will have his cushions of silver cloth brought there, and as he smokes his chibouque, see all Paris pass before him.”\n\n“You have no idea, then, Morrel?” asked Château-Renaud; “you do not propose anything.”\n\n“Oh, yes,” returned the young man, smiling; “on the contrary, I have one, but I expected the count would be tempted by one of the brilliant proposals made him, yet as he has not replied to any of them, I will venture to offer him a suite of apartments in a charming hotel, in the Pompadour style, that my sister has inhabited for a year, in the Rue Meslay.”\n\n“You have a sister?” asked the count.\n\n“Yes, monsieur, a most excellent sister.”\n\n“Married?”\n\n“Nearly nine years.”\n\n“Happy?” asked the count again.\n\n“As happy as it is permitted to a human creature to be,” replied Maximilian. “She married the man she loved, who remained faithful to us in our fallen fortunes—Emmanuel Herbaut.”\n\nMonte Cristo smiled imperceptibly.\n\n“I live there during my leave of absence,” continued Maximilian; “and I shall be, together with my brother-in-law Emmanuel, at the disposition of the Count, whenever he thinks fit to honor us.”\n\n“One minute,” cried Albert, without giving Monte Cristo the time to reply. “Take care, you are going to immure a traveller, Sinbad the Sailor, a man who comes to see Paris; you are going to make a patriarch of him.”\n20255m\n\n“Oh, no,” said Morrel; “my sister is five-and-twenty, my brother-in-law is thirty, they are gay, young, and happy. Besides, the count will be in his own house, and only see them when he thinks fit to do so.”\n\n“Thanks, monsieur,” said Monte Cristo; “I shall content myself with being presented to your sister and her husband, if you will do me the honor to introduce me; but I cannot accept the offer of anyone of these gentlemen, since my habitation is already prepared.”\n\n“What,” cried Morcerf; “you are, then, going to a hotel—that will be very dull for you.”\n\n“Was I so badly lodged at Rome?” said Monte Cristo smiling.\n\n“_Parbleu!_ at Rome you spent fifty thousand piastres in furnishing your apartments, but I presume that you are not disposed to spend a similar sum every day.”\n\n“It is not that which deterred me,” replied Monte Cristo; “but as I determined to have a house to myself, I sent on my valet de chambre, and he ought by this time to have bought the house and furnished it.”\n\n“But you have, then, a valet de chambre who knows Paris?” said Beauchamp.\n\n“It is the first time he has ever been in Paris. He is black, and cannot speak,” returned Monte Cristo.\n\n“It is Ali!” cried Albert, in the midst of the general surprise.\n\n“Yes, Ali himself, my Nubian mute, whom you saw, I think, at Rome.”\n\n“Certainly,” said Morcerf; “I recollect him perfectly. But how could you charge a Nubian to purchase a house, and a mute to furnish it?—he will do everything wrong.”\n\n“Undeceive yourself, monsieur,” replied Monte Cristo; “I am quite sure, that, on the contrary, he will choose everything as I wish. He knows my tastes, my caprices, my wants. He has been here a week, with the instinct of a hound, hunting by himself. He will arrange everything for me. He knew, that I should arrive today at ten o’clock; he was waiting for me at nine at the Barrière de Fontainebleau. He gave me this paper; it contains the number of my new abode; read it yourself,” and Monte Cristo passed a paper to Albert.\n\n“Ah, that is really original,” said Beauchamp.\n\n“And very princely,” added Château-Renaud.\n\n“What, do you not know your house?” asked Debray.\n\n“No,” said Monte Cristo; “I told you I did not wish to be behind my time; I dressed myself in the carriage, and descended at the viscount’s door.” The young men looked at each other; they did not know if it was a comedy Monte Cristo was playing, but every word he uttered had such an air of simplicity, that it was impossible to suppose what he said was false—besides, why should he tell a falsehood?\n\n“We must content ourselves, then,” said Beauchamp, “with rendering the count all the little services in our power. I, in my quality of journalist, open all the theatres to him.”\n\n“Thanks, monsieur,” returned Monte Cristo, “my steward has orders to take a box at each theatre.”\n\n“Is your steward also a Nubian?” asked Debray.\n\n“No, he is a countryman of yours, if a Corsican is a countryman of anyone’s. But you know him, M. de Morcerf.”\n\n“Is it that excellent M. Bertuccio, who understands hiring windows so well?”\n\n“Yes, you saw him the day I had the honor of receiving you; he has been a soldier, a smuggler—in fact, everything. I would not be quite sure that he has not been mixed up with the police for some trifle—a stab with a knife, for instance.”\n\n“And you have chosen this honest citizen for your steward,” said Debray. “Of how much does he rob you every year?”\n\n“On my word,” replied the count, “not more than another. I am sure he answers my purpose, knows no impossibility, and so I keep him.”\n\n“Then,” continued Château-Renaud, “since you have an establishment, a steward, and a hotel in the Champs-Élysées, you only want a mistress.” Albert smiled. He thought of the fair Greek he had seen in the count’s box at the Argentina and Valle theatres.\n\n“I have something better than that,” said Monte Cristo; “I have a slave. You procure your mistresses from the opera, the Vaudeville, or the Variétés; I purchased mine at Constantinople; it cost me more, but I have nothing to fear.”\n\n“But you forget,” replied Debray, laughing, “that we are Franks by name and franks by nature, as King Charles said, and that the moment she puts her foot in France your slave becomes free.”\n\n“Who will tell her?”\n\n“The first person who sees her.”\n\n“She only speaks Romaic.”\n\n“That is different.”\n\n“But at least we shall see her,” said Beauchamp, “or do you keep eunuchs as well as mutes?”\n\n“Oh, no,” replied Monte Cristo; “I do not carry brutalism so far. Everyone who surrounds me is free to quit me, and when they leave me will no longer have any need of me or anyone else; it is for that reason, perhaps, that they do not quit me.”\n\nThey had long since passed to dessert and cigars.\n\n“My dear Albert,” said Debray, rising, “it is half-past two. Your guest is charming, but you leave the best company to go into the worst sometimes. I must return to the minister’s. I will tell him of the count, and we shall soon know who he is.”\n\n“Take care,” returned Albert; “no one has been able to accomplish that.”\n\n“Oh, we have three millions for our police; it is true they are almost always spent beforehand, but, no matter, we shall still have fifty thousand francs to spend for this purpose.”\n\n“And when you know, will you tell me?”\n\n“I promise you. _Au revoir_, Albert. Gentlemen, good morning.”\n\nAs he left the room, Debray called out loudly, “My carriage.”\n\n“Bravo,” said Beauchamp to Albert; “I shall not go to the Chamber, but I have something better to offer my readers than a speech of M. Danglars.”\n\n“For heaven’s sake, Beauchamp,” returned Morcerf, “do not deprive me of the merit of introducing him everywhere. Is he not peculiar?”\n\n“He is more than that,” replied Château-Renaud; “he is one of the most extraordinary men I ever saw in my life. Are you coming, Morrel?”\n\n“Directly I have given my card to the count, who has promised to pay us a visit at Rue Meslay, No. 14.”\n\n“Be sure I shall not fail to do so,” returned the count, bowing.\n\nAnd Maximilian Morrel left the room with the Baron de Château-Renaud, leaving Monte Cristo alone with Morcerf.\n\n# Chapter 41. The Presentation\n\nWhen Albert found himself alone with Monte Cristo, “My dear count,” said he, “allow me to commence my services as _cicerone_ by showing you a specimen of a bachelor’s apartment. You, who are accustomed to the palaces of Italy, can amuse yourself by calculating in how many square feet a young man who is not the worst lodged in Paris can live. As we pass from one room to another, I will open the windows to let you breathe.”\n\nMonte Cristo had already seen the breakfast-room and the salon on the ground floor. Albert led him first to his _atelier_, which was, as we have said, his favorite apartment. Monte Cristo quickly appreciated all that Albert had collected here—old cabinets, Japanese porcelain, Oriental stuffs, Venetian glass, arms from all parts of the world—everything was familiar to him; and at the first glance he recognized their date, their country, and their origin.\n\nMorcerf had expected he should be the guide; on the contrary, it was he who, under the count’s guidance, followed a course of archæology, mineralogy, and natural history.\n\nThey descended to the first floor; Albert led his guest into the salon. The salon was filled with the works of modern artists; there were landscapes by Dupré, with their long reeds and tall trees, their lowing oxen and marvellous skies; Delacroix’s Arabian cavaliers, with their long white burnouses, their shining belts, their damasked arms, their horses, who tore each other with their teeth while their riders contended fiercely with their maces; _aquarelles_ of Boulanger, representing Notre Dame de Paris with that vigor that makes the artist the rival of the poet; there were paintings by Diaz, who makes his flowers more beautiful than flowers, his suns more brilliant than the sun; designs by Decamp, as vividly colored as those of Salvator Rosa, but more poetic; _pastels_ by Giraud and Müller, representing children like angels and women with the features of a virgin; sketches torn from the album of Dauzats’ “Travels in the East,” that had been made in a few seconds on the saddle of a camel, or beneath the dome of a mosque—in a word, all that modern art can give in exchange and as recompense for the art lost and gone with ages long since past.\n\nAlbert expected to have something new this time to show to the traveller, but, to his great surprise, the latter, without seeking for the signatures, many of which, indeed, were only initials, named instantly the author of every picture in such a manner that it was easy to see that each name was not only known to him, but that each style associated with it had been appreciated and studied by him. From the salon they passed into the bedchamber; it was a model of taste and simple elegance. A single portrait, signed by Léopold Robert, shone in its carved and gilded frame. This portrait attracted the Count of Monte Cristo’s attention, for he made three rapid steps in the chamber, and stopped suddenly before it.\n\nIt was the portrait of a young woman of five or six-and-twenty, with a dark complexion, and light and lustrous eyes, veiled beneath long lashes. She wore the picturesque costume of the Catalan fisherwomen, a red and black bodice, and golden pins in her hair. She was looking at the sea, and her form was outlined on the blue ocean and sky. The light was so faint in the room that Albert did not perceive the pallor that spread itself over the count’s visage, or the nervous heaving of his chest and shoulders. Silence prevailed for an instant, during which Monte Cristo gazed intently on the picture.\n\n“You have there a most charming mistress, viscount,” said the count in a perfectly calm tone; “and this costume—a ball costume, doubtless—becomes her admirably.”\n\n“Ah, monsieur,” returned Albert, “I would never forgive you this mistake if you had seen another picture beside this. You do not know my mother; she it is whom you see here. She had her portrait painted thus six or eight years ago. This costume is a fancy one, it appears, and the resemblance is so great that I think I still see my mother the same as she was in 1830. The countess had this portrait painted during the count’s absence. She doubtless intended giving him an agreeable surprise; but, strange to say, this portrait seemed to displease my father, and the value of the picture, which is, as you see, one of the best works of Léopold Robert, could not overcome his dislike to it. It is true, between ourselves, that M. de Morcerf is one of the most assiduous peers at the Luxembourg, a general renowned for theory, but a most mediocre amateur of art. It is different with my mother, who paints exceedingly well, and who, unwilling to part with so valuable a picture, gave it to me to put here, where it would be less likely to displease M. de Morcerf, whose portrait, by Gros, I will also show you. Excuse my talking of family matters, but as I shall have the honor of introducing you to the count, I tell you this to prevent you making any allusions to this picture. The picture seems to have a malign influence, for my mother rarely comes here without looking at it, and still more rarely does she look at it without weeping. This disagreement is the only one that has ever taken place between the count and countess, who are still as much united, although married more than twenty years, as on the first day of their wedding.”\n20261m\n\nMonte Cristo glanced rapidly at Albert, as if to seek a hidden meaning in his words, but it was evident the young man uttered them in the simplicity of his heart.\n\n“Now,” said Albert, “that you have seen all my treasures, allow me to offer them to you, unworthy as they are. Consider yourself as in your own house, and to put yourself still more at your ease, pray accompany me to the apartments of M. de Morcerf, he whom I wrote from Rome an account of the services you rendered me, and to whom I announced your promised visit, and I may say that both the count and countess anxiously desire to thank you in person. You are somewhat _blasé_ I know, and family scenes have not much effect on Sinbad the Sailor, who has seen so many others. However, accept what I propose to you as an initiation into Parisian life—a life of politeness, visiting, and introductions.”\n\nMonte Cristo bowed without making any answer; he accepted the offer without enthusiasm and without regret, as one of those conventions of society which every gentleman looks upon as a duty. Albert summoned his servant, and ordered him to acquaint M. and Madame de Morcerf of the arrival of the Count of Monte Cristo. Albert followed him with the count. When they arrived at the antechamber, above the door was visible a shield, which, by its rich ornaments and its harmony with the rest of the furniture, indicated the importance the owner attached to this blazon. Monte Cristo stopped and examined it attentively.\n\n“Azure seven merlets, or, placed bender,” said he. “These are, doubtless, your family arms? Except the knowledge of blazons, that enables me to decipher them, I am very ignorant of heraldry—I, a count of a fresh creation, fabricated in Tuscany by the aid of a commandery of St. Stephen, and who would not have taken the trouble had I not been told that when you travel much it is necessary. Besides, you must have something on the panels of your carriage, to escape being searched by the custom-house officers. Excuse my putting such a question to you.”\n\n“It is not indiscreet,” returned Morcerf, with the simplicity of conviction. “You have guessed rightly. These are our arms, that is, those of my father, but they are, as you see, joined to another shield, which has gules, a silver tower, which are my mother’s. By her side I am Spanish, but the family of Morcerf is French, and, I have heard, one of the oldest of the south of France.”\n\n“Yes,” replied Monte Cristo “these blazons prove that. Almost all the armed pilgrims that went to the Holy Land took for their arms either a cross, in honor of their mission, or birds of passage, in sign of the long voyage they were about to undertake, and which they hoped to accomplish on the wings of faith. One of your ancestors had joined the Crusades, and supposing it to be only that of St. Louis, that makes you mount to the thirteenth century, which is tolerably ancient.”\n\n“It is possible,” said Morcerf; “my father has in his study a genealogical tree which will tell you all that, and on which I made commentaries that would have greatly edified d’Hozier and Jaucourt. At present I no longer think of it, and yet I must tell you that we are beginning to occupy ourselves greatly with these things under our popular government.”\n\n“Well, then, your government would do well to choose from the past something better than the things that I have noticed on your monuments, and which have no heraldic meaning whatever. As for you, viscount,” continued Monte Cristo to Morcerf, “you are more fortunate than the government, for your arms are really beautiful, and speak to the imagination. Yes, you are at once from Provence and Spain; that explains, if the portrait you showed me be like, the dark hue I so much admired on the visage of the noble Catalan.”\n\nIt would have required the penetration of Œdipus or the Sphinx to have divined the irony the count concealed beneath these words, apparently uttered with the greatest politeness. Morcerf thanked him with a smile, and pushed open the door above which were his arms, and which, as we have said, opened into the salon. In the most conspicuous part of the salon was another portrait. It was that of a man, from five to eight-and-thirty, in the uniform of a general officer, wearing the double epaulet of heavy bullion, that indicates superior rank, the ribbon of the Legion of Honor around his neck, which showed he was a commander, and on the right breast, the star of a grand officer of the order of the Saviour, and on the left that of the grand cross of Charles III., which proved that the person represented by the picture had served in the wars of Greece and Spain, or, what was just the same thing as regarded decorations, had fulfilled some diplomatic mission in the two countries.\n\nMonte Cristo was engaged in examining this portrait with no less care than he had bestowed upon the other, when another door opened, and he found himself opposite to the Count of Morcerf in person.\n\nHe was a man of forty to forty-five years, but he seemed at least fifty, and his black moustache and eyebrows contrasted strangely with his almost white hair, which was cut short, in the military fashion. He was dressed in plain clothes, and wore at his button-hole the ribbons of the different orders to which he belonged.\n\nHe entered with a tolerably dignified step, and some little haste. Monte Cristo saw him advance towards him without making a single step. It seemed as if his feet were rooted to the ground, and his eyes on the Count of Morcerf.\n\n“Father,” said the young man, “I have the honor of presenting to you the Count of Monte Cristo, the generous friend whom I had the good fortune to meet in the critical situation of which I have told you.”\n\n“You are most welcome, monsieur,” said the Count of Morcerf, saluting Monte Cristo with a smile, “and monsieur has rendered our house, in preserving its only heir, a service which insures him our eternal gratitude.”\n\nAs he said these words, the count of Morcerf pointed to a chair, while he seated himself in another opposite the window.\n\nMonte Cristo, in taking the seat Morcerf offered him, placed himself in such a manner as to remain concealed in the shadow of the large velvet curtains, and read on the careworn and livid features of the count a whole history of secret griefs written in each wrinkle time had planted there.\n\n“The countess,” said Morcerf, “was at her toilet when she was informed of the visit she was about to receive. She will, however, be in the salon in ten minutes.”\n\n“It is a great honor to me,” returned Monte Cristo, “to be thus, on the first day of my arrival in Paris, brought in contact with a man whose merit equals his reputation, and to whom fortune has for once been equitable, but has she not still on the plains of Mitidja, or in the mountains of Atlas, a marshal’s staff to offer you?”\n\n“Oh,” replied Morcerf, reddening slightly, “I have left the service, monsieur. Made a peer at the Restoration, I served through the first campaign under the orders of Marshal Bourmont. I could, therefore, expect a higher rank, and who knows what might have happened had the elder branch remained on the throne? But the Revolution of July was, it seems, sufficiently glorious to allow itself to be ungrateful, and it was so for all services that did not date from the imperial period. I tendered my resignation, for when you have gained your epaulets on the battle-field, you do not know how to manœuvre on the slippery grounds of the salons. I have hung up my sword, and cast myself into politics. I have devoted myself to industry; I study the useful arts. During the twenty years I served, I often wished to do so, but I had not the time.”\n\n“These are the ideas that render your nation superior to any other,” returned Monte Cristo. “A gentleman of high birth, possessor of an ample fortune, you have consented to gain your promotion as an obscure soldier, step by step—this is uncommon; then become general, peer of France, commander of the Legion of Honor, you consent to again commence a second apprenticeship, without any other hope or any other desire than that of one day becoming useful to your fellow-creatures; this, indeed, is praiseworthy,—nay, more, it is sublime.”\n20265m\n\nAlbert looked on and listened with astonishment; he was not used to see Monte Cristo give vent to such bursts of enthusiasm.\n\n“Alas,” continued the stranger, doubtless to dispel the slight cloud that covered Morcerf’s brow, “we do not act thus in Italy; we grow according to our race and our species, and we pursue the same lines, and often the same uselessness, all our lives.”\n\n“But, monsieur,” said the Count of Morcerf, “for a man of your merit, Italy is not a country, and France opens her arms to receive you; respond to her call. France will not, perhaps, be always ungrateful. She treats her children ill, but she always welcomes strangers.”\n\n“Ah, father,” said Albert with a smile, “it is evident you do not know the Count of Monte Cristo; he despises all honors, and contents himself with those written on his passport.”\n\n“That is the most just remark,” replied the stranger, “I ever heard made concerning myself.”\n\n“You have been free to choose your career,” observed the Count of Morcerf, with a sigh; “and you have chosen the path strewed with flowers.”\n\n“Precisely, monsieur,” replied Monte Cristo with one of those smiles that a painter could never represent or a physiologist analyze.\n\n“If I did not fear to fatigue you,” said the general, evidently charmed with the count’s manners, “I would have taken you to the Chamber; there is a debate very curious to those who are strangers to our modern senators.”\n\n“I shall be most grateful, monsieur, if you will, at some future time, renew your offer, but I have been flattered with the hope of being introduced to the countess, and I will therefore wait.”\n\n“Ah, here is my mother,” cried the viscount.\n\nMonte Cristo, turned round hastily, and saw Madame de Morcerf at the entrance of the salon, at the door opposite to that by which her husband had entered, pale and motionless; when Monte Cristo turned round, she let fall her arm, which for some unknown reason had been resting on the gilded door-post. She had been there some moments, and had heard the last words of the visitor. The latter rose and bowed to the countess, who inclined herself without speaking.\n\n“Ah! good heavens, madame,” said the count, “are you ill, or is it the heat of the room that affects you?”\n\n“Are you ill, mother?” cried the viscount, springing towards her.\n\nShe thanked them both with a smile.\n\n“No,” returned she, “but I feel some emotion on seeing, for the first time, the man without whose intervention we should have been in tears and desolation. Monsieur,” continued the countess, advancing with the majesty of a queen, “I owe to you the life of my son, and for this I bless you. Now, I thank you for the pleasure you give me in thus affording me the opportunity of thanking you as I have blessed you, from the bottom of my heart.”\n\nThe count bowed again, but lower than before; he was even paler than Mercédès.\n\n“Madame,” said he, “the count and yourself recompense too generously a simple action. To save a man, to spare a father’s feelings, or a mother’s sensibility, is not to do a good action, but a simple deed of humanity.”\n\nAt these words, uttered with the most exquisite sweetness and politeness, Madame de Morcerf replied:\n\n“It is very fortunate for my son, monsieur, that he found such a friend, and I thank God that things are thus.”\n\nAnd Mercédès raised her fine eyes to heaven with so fervent an expression of gratitude, that the count fancied he saw tears in them. M. de Morcerf approached her.\n\n“Madame,” said he. “I have already made my excuses to the count for quitting him, and I pray you to do so also. The sitting commences at two; it is now three, and I am to speak.”\n\n“Go, then, and monsieur and I will strive our best to forget your absence,” replied the countess, with the same tone of deep feeling. “Monsieur,” continued she, turning to Monte Cristo, “will you do us the honor of passing the rest of the day with us?”\n\n“Believe me, madame, I feel most grateful for your kindness, but I got out of my travelling carriage at your door this morning, and I am ignorant how I am installed in Paris, which I scarcely know; this is but a trifling inquietude, I know, but one that may be appreciated.”\n\n“We shall have the pleasure another time,” said the countess; “you promise that?”\n\nMonte Cristo inclined himself without answering, but the gesture might pass for assent.\n\n“I will not detain you, monsieur,” continued the countess; “I would not have our gratitude become indiscreet or importunate.”\n\n“My dear Count,” said Albert, “I will endeavor to return your politeness at Rome, and place my coupé at your disposal until your own be ready.”\n\n“A thousand thanks for your kindness, viscount,” returned the Count of Monte Cristo “but I suppose that M. Bertuccio has suitably employed the four hours and a half I have given him, and that I shall find a carriage of some sort ready at the door.”\n\nAlbert was used to the count’s manner of proceeding; he knew that, like Nero, he was in search of the impossible, and nothing astonished him, but wishing to judge with his own eyes how far the count’s orders had been executed, he accompanied him to the door of the house. Monte Cristo was not deceived. As soon as he appeared in the Count of Morcerf’s antechamber, a footman, the same who at Rome had brought the count’s card to the two young men, and announced his visit, sprang into the vestibule, and when he arrived at the door the illustrious traveller found his carriage awaiting him. It was a _coupé_ of Koller’s building, and with horses and harness for which Drake had, to the knowledge of all the lions of Paris, refused on the previous day seven hundred guineas.\n\n“Monsieur,” said the count to Albert, “I do not ask you to accompany me to my house, as I can only show you a habitation fitted up in a hurry, and I have, as you know, a reputation to keep up as regards not being taken by surprise. Give me, therefore, one more day before I invite you; I shall then be certain not to fail in my hospitality.”\n\n“If you ask me for a day, count, I know what to anticipate; it will not be a house I shall see, but a palace. You have decidedly some genius at your control.”\n\n“_Ma foi_, spread that idea,” replied the Count of Monte Cristo, putting his foot on the velvet-lined steps of his splendid carriage, “and that will be worth something to me among the ladies.”\n\nAs he spoke, he sprang into the vehicle, the door was closed, but not so rapidly that Monte Cristo failed to perceive the almost imperceptible movement which stirred the curtains of the apartment in which he had left Madame de Morcerf.\n\nWhen Albert returned to his mother, he found her in the boudoir reclining in a large velvet armchair, the whole room so obscure that only the shining spangle, fastened here and there to the drapery, and the angles of the gilded frames of the pictures, showed with some degree of brightness in the gloom. Albert could not see the face of the countess, as it was covered with a thin veil she had put on her head, and which fell over her features in misty folds, but it seemed to him as though her voice had altered. He could distinguish amid the perfumes of the roses and heliotropes in the flower-stands, the sharp and fragrant odor of volatile salts, and he noticed in one of the chased cups on the mantle-piece the countess’s smelling-bottle, taken from its shagreen case, and exclaimed in a tone of uneasiness, as he entered:\n\n“My dear mother, have you been ill during my absence?”\n\n“No, no, Albert, but you know these roses, tuberoses, and orange-flowers throw out at first, before one is used to them, such violent perfumes.”\n\n“Then, my dear mother,” said Albert, putting his hand to the bell, “they must be taken into the antechamber. You are really ill, and just now were so pale as you came into the room——”\n\n“Was I pale, Albert?”\n\n“Yes; a pallor that suits you admirably, mother, but which did not the less alarm my father and myself.”\n\n“Did your father speak of it?” inquired Mercédès eagerly.\n\n“No, madame; but do you not remember that he spoke of the fact to you?”\n20269m\n\n“Yes, I do remember,” replied the countess.\n\nA servant entered, summoned by Albert’s ring of the bell.\n\n“Take these flowers into the anteroom or dressing-room,” said the viscount; “they make the countess ill.”\n\nThe footman obeyed his orders. A long pause ensued, which lasted until all the flowers were removed.\n\n“What is this name of Monte Cristo?” inquired the countess, when the servant had taken away the last vase of flowers, “is it a family name, or the name of the estate, or a simple title?”\n\n“I believe, mother, it is merely a title. The count purchased an island in the Tuscan archipelago, and, as he told you today, has founded a commandery. You know the same thing was done for Saint Stephen of Florence, Saint George Constantinian of Parma, and even for the Order of Malta. Except this, he has no pretension to nobility, and calls himself a chance count, although the general opinion at Rome is that the count is a man of very high distinction.”\n\n“His manners are admirable,” said the countess, “at least, as far as I could judge in the few minutes he remained here.”\n\n“They are perfect mother, so perfect, that they surpass by far all I have known in the leading aristocracy of the three proudest nobilities of Europe—the English, the Spanish, and the German.”\n\nThe countess paused a moment; then, after a slight hesitation, she resumed.\n\n“You have seen, my dear Albert—I ask the question as a mother—you have seen M. de Monte Cristo in his house, you are quicksighted, have much knowledge of the world, more tact than is usual at your age, do you think the count is really what he appears to be?”\n\n“What does he appear to be?”\n\n“Why, you have just said,—a man of high distinction.”\n\n“I told you, my dear mother, he was esteemed such.”\n\n“But what is your own opinion, Albert?”\n\n“I must tell you that I have not come to any decided opinion respecting him, but I think him a Maltese.”\n\n“I do not ask you of his origin but what he is.”\n\n“Ah! what he is; that is quite another thing. I have seen so many remarkable things in him, that if you would have me really say what I think, I shall reply that I really do look upon him as one of Byron’s heroes, whom misery has marked with a fatal brand; some Manfred, some Lara, some Werner, one of those wrecks, as it were, of some ancient family, who, disinherited of their patrimony, have achieved one by the force of their adventurous genius, which has placed them above the laws of society.”\n“You say——”\n\n“I say that Monte Cristo is an island in the midst of the Mediterranean, without inhabitants or garrison, the resort of smugglers of all nations, and pirates of every flag. Who knows whether or not these industrious worthies do not pay to their feudal lord some dues for his protection?”\n\n“That is possible,” said the countess, reflecting.\n\n“Never mind,” continued the young man, “smuggler or not, you must agree, mother dear, as you have seen him, that the Count of Monte Cristo is a remarkable man, who will have the greatest success in the salons of Paris. Why, this very morning, in my rooms, he made his _entrée_ amongst us by striking every man of us with amazement, not even excepting Château-Renaud.”\n\n“And what do you suppose is the count’s age?” inquired Mercédès, evidently attaching great importance to this question.\n\n“Thirty-five or thirty-six, mother.”\n\n“So young,—it is impossible,” said Mercédès, replying at the same time to what Albert said as well as to her own private reflection.\n\n“It is the truth, however. Three or four times he has said to me, and certainly without the slightest premeditation, ‘at such a period I was five years old, at another ten years old, at another twelve,’ and I, induced by curiosity, which kept me alive to these details, have compared the dates, and never found him inaccurate. The age of this singular man, who is of no age, is then, I am certain, thirty-five. Besides, mother, remark how vivid his eye, how raven-black his hair, and his brow, though so pale, is free from wrinkles,—he is not only vigorous, but also young.”\n\nThe countess bent her head, as if beneath a heavy wave of bitter thoughts.\n\n“And has this man displayed a friendship for you, Albert?” she asked with a nervous shudder.\n\n“I am inclined to think so.”\n\n“And—do—you—like—him?”\n\n“Why, he pleases me in spite of Franz d’Épinay, who tries to convince me that he is a being returned from the other world.”\n\nThe countess shuddered.\n\n“Albert,” she said, in a voice which was altered by emotion, “I have always put you on your guard against new acquaintances. Now you are a man, and are able to give me advice; yet I repeat to you, Albert, be prudent.”\n\n“Why, my dear mother, it is necessary, in order to make your advice turn to account, that I should know beforehand what I have to distrust. The count never plays, he only drinks pure water tinged with a little sherry, and is so rich that he cannot, without intending to laugh at me, try to borrow money. What, then, have I to fear from him?”\n\n“You are right,” said the countess, “and my fears are weakness, especially when directed against a man who has saved your life. How did your father receive him, Albert? It is necessary that we should be more than complaisant to the count. M. de Morcerf is sometimes occupied, his business makes him reflective, and he might, without intending it——”\n\n“Nothing could be in better taste than my father’s demeanor, madame,” said Albert; “nay, more, he seemed greatly flattered at two or three compliments which the count very skilfully and agreeably paid him with as much ease as if he had known him these thirty years. Each of these little tickling arrows must have pleased my father,” added Albert with a laugh. “And thus they parted the best possible friends, and M. de Morcerf even wished to take him to the Chamber to hear the speakers.”\n\nThe countess made no reply. She fell into so deep a reverie that her eyes gradually closed. The young man, standing up before her, gazed upon her with that filial affection which is so tender and endearing with children whose mothers are still young and handsome. Then, after seeing her eyes closed, and hearing her breathe gently, he believed she had dropped asleep, and left the apartment on tiptoe, closing the door after him with the utmost precaution.\n\n“This devil of a fellow,” he muttered, shaking his head; “I said at the time he would create a sensation here, and I measure his effect by an infallible thermometer. My mother has noticed him, and he must therefore, perforce, be remarkable.”\n\nHe went down to the stables, not without some slight annoyance, when he remembered that the Count of Monte Cristo had laid his hands on a “turnout” which sent his bays down to second place in the opinion of connoisseurs.\n\n“Most decidedly,” said he, “men are not equal, and I must beg my father to develop this theorem in the Chamber of Peers.”\n\n# Chapter 42. Monsieur Bertuccio\n\nMeanwhile the count had arrived at his house; it had taken him six minutes to perform the distance, but these six minutes were sufficient to induce twenty young men who knew the price of the equipage they had been unable to purchase themselves, to put their horses in a gallop in order to see the rich foreigner who could afford to give 20,000 francs apiece for his horses.\n\nThe house Ali had chosen, and which was to serve as a town residence to Monte Cristo, was situated on the right hand as you ascend the Champs-Élysées. A thick clump of trees and shrubs rose in the centre, and masked a portion of the front; around this shrubbery two alleys, like two arms, extended right and left, and formed a carriage-drive from the iron gates to a double portico, on every step of which stood a porcelain vase, filled with flowers. This house, isolated from the rest, had, besides the main entrance, another in the Rue de Ponthieu. Even before the coachman had hailed the _concierge_, the massy gates rolled on their hinges—they had seen the Count coming, and at Paris, as everywhere else, he was served with the rapidity of lightning. The coachman entered and traversed the half-circle without slackening his speed, and the gates were closed ere the wheels had ceased to sound on the gravel. The carriage stopped at the left side of the portico, two men presented themselves at the carriage-window; the one was Ali, who, smiling with an expression of the most sincere joy, seemed amply repaid by a mere look from Monte Cristo. The other bowed respectfully, and offered his arm to assist the count in descending.\n\n“Thanks, M. Bertuccio,” said the count, springing lightly up the three steps of the portico; “and the notary?”\n\n“He is in the small salon, excellency,” returned Bertuccio.\n\n“And the cards I ordered to be engraved as soon as you knew the number of the house?”\n\n“Your excellency, it is done already. I have been myself to the best engraver of the Palais Royal, who did the plate in my presence. The first card struck off was taken, according to your orders, to the Baron Danglars, Rue de la Chaussée d’Antin, No. 7; the others are on the mantle-piece of your excellency’s bedroom.”\n\n“Good; what o’clock is it?”\n\n“Four o’clock.”\n\nMonte Cristo gave his hat, cane, and gloves to the same French footman who had called his carriage at the Count of Morcerf’s, and then he passed into the small salon, preceded by Bertuccio, who showed him the way.\n\n“These are but indifferent marbles in this antechamber,” said Monte Cristo. “I trust all this will soon be taken away.”\n\nBertuccio bowed. As the steward had said, the notary awaited him in the small salon. He was a simple-looking lawyer’s clerk, elevated to the extraordinary dignity of a provincial scrivener.\n\n“You are the notary empowered to sell the country house that I wish to purchase, monsieur?” asked Monte Cristo.\n\n“Yes, count,” returned the notary.\n\n“Is the deed of sale ready?”\n\n“Yes, count.”\n\n“Have you brought it?”\n\n“Here it is.”\n\n“Very well; and where is this house that I purchase?” asked the count carelessly, addressing himself half to Bertuccio, half to the notary. The steward made a gesture that signified, “I do not know.” The notary looked at the count with astonishment.\n\n“What!” said he, “does not the count know where the house he purchases is situated?”\n\n“No,” returned the count.\n\n“The count does not know?”\n\n“How should I know? I have arrived from Cadiz this morning. I have never before been at Paris, and it is the first time I have ever even set my foot in France.”\n\n“Ah, that is different; the house you purchase is at Auteuil.”\n\nAt these words Bertuccio turned pale.\n\n“And where is Auteuil?” asked the count.\n\n“Close by here, monsieur,” replied the notary—“a little beyond Passy; a charming situation, in the heart of the Bois de Boulogne.”\n\n“So near as that?” said the Count; “but that is not in the country. What made you choose a house at the gates of Paris, M. Bertuccio?”\n\n“I,” cried the steward with a strange expression. “His excellency did not charge me to purchase this house. If his excellency will recollect—if he will think——”\n\n“Ah, true,” observed Monte Cristo; “I recollect now. I read the advertisement in one of the papers, and was tempted by the false title, ‘a country house.’”\n\n“It is not yet too late,” cried Bertuccio, eagerly; “and if your excellency will intrust me with the commission, I will find you a better at Enghien, at Fontenay-aux-Roses, or at Bellevue.”\n\n“Oh, no,” returned Monte Cristo negligently; “since I have this, I will keep it.”\n\n“And you are quite right,” said the notary, who feared to lose his fee. “It is a charming place, well supplied with spring-water and fine trees; a comfortable habitation, although abandoned for a long time, without reckoning the furniture, which, although old, is yet valuable, now that old things are so much sought after. I suppose the count has the tastes of the day?”\n\n“To be sure,” returned Monte Cristo; “it is very convenient, then?”\n\n“It is more—it is magnificent.”\n\n“_Peste!_ let us not lose such an opportunity,” returned Monte Cristo. “The deed, if you please, Mr. Notary.”\n\nAnd he signed it rapidly, after having first run his eye over that part of the deed in which were specified the situation of the house and the names of the proprietors.\n\n“Bertuccio,” said he, “give fifty-five thousand francs to monsieur.”\n\nThe steward left the room with a faltering step, and returned with a bundle of bank-notes, which the notary counted like a man who never gives a receipt for money until after he is sure it is all there.\n\n“And now,” demanded the count, “are all the forms complied with?”\n\n“All, sir.”\n\n“Have you the keys?”\n\n“They are in the hands of the concierge, who takes care of the house, but here is the order I have given him to install the count in his new possessions.”\n\n“Very well;” and Monte Cristo made a sign with his hand to the notary, which said, “I have no further need of you; you may go.”\n\n“But,” observed the honest notary, “the count is, I think, mistaken; it is only fifty thousand francs, everything included.”\n\n“And your fee?”\n\n“Is included in this sum.”\n\n“But have you not come from Auteuil here?”\n\n“Yes, certainly.”\n\n“Well, then, it is but fair that you should be paid for your loss of time and trouble,” said the count; and he made a gesture of polite dismissal.\n\nThe notary left the room backwards, and bowing down to the ground; it was the first time he had ever met a similar client.\n\n“See this gentleman out,” said the count to Bertuccio. And the steward followed the notary out of the room.\n\nScarcely was the count alone, when he drew from his pocket a book closed with a lock, and opened it with a key which he wore round his neck, and which never left him. After having sought for a few minutes, he stopped at a leaf which had several notes, and compared them with the deed of sale, which lay on the table, and recalling his _souvenirs_—\n\n“‘Auteuil, Rue de la Fontaine, No. 28;’ it is indeed the same,” said he; “and now, am I to rely upon an avowal extorted by religious or physical terror? However, in an hour I shall know all. Bertuccio!” cried he, striking a light hammer with a pliant handle on a small gong. “Bertuccio!”\n\nThe steward appeared at the door.\n\n“Monsieur Bertuccio,” said the count, “did you never tell me that you had travelled in France?”\n\n“In some parts of France—yes, excellency.”\n\n“You know the environs of Paris, then?”\n\n“No, excellency, no,” returned the steward, with a sort of nervous trembling, which Monte Cristo, a connoisseur in all emotions, rightly attributed to great disquietude.\n\n“It is unfortunate,” returned he, “that you have never visited the environs, for I wish to see my new property this evening, and had you gone with me, you could have given me some useful information.”\n\n“To Auteuil!” cried Bertuccio, whose copper complexion became livid—“I go to Auteuil?”\n\n“Well, what is there surprising in that? When I live at Auteuil, you must come there, as you belong to my service.”\n\nBertuccio hung down his head before the imperious look of his master, and remained motionless, without making any answer.\n\n“Why, what has happened to you?—are you going to make me ring a second time for the carriage?” asked Monte Cristo, in the same tone that Louis XIV. pronounced the famous, “I have been almost obliged to wait.” Bertuccio made but one bound to the antechamber, and cried in a hoarse voice:\n\n“His excellency’s horses!”\n\nMonte Cristo wrote two or three notes, and, as he sealed the last, the steward appeared.\n\n“Your excellency’s carriage is at the door,” said he.\n\n“Well, take your hat and gloves,” returned Monte Cristo.\n\n“Am I to accompany you, your excellency?” cried Bertuccio.\n\n“Certainly, you must give the orders, for I intend residing at the house.”\n\n20277m\nIt was unexampled for a servant of the count’s to dare to dispute an order of his, so the steward, without saying a word, followed his master, who got into the carriage, and signed to him to follow, which he did, taking his place respectfully on the front seat.\n\n# Chapter 43. The House at Auteuil\n\nMonte Cristo noticed, as they descended the staircase, that Bertuccio signed himself in the Corsican manner; that is, had formed the sign of the cross in the air with his thumb, and as he seated himself in the carriage, muttered a short prayer. Anyone but a man of exhaustless thirst for knowledge would have had pity on seeing the steward’s extraordinary repugnance for the count’s projected drive without the walls; but the count was too curious to let Bertuccio off from this little journey. In twenty minutes they were at Auteuil; the steward’s emotion had continued to augment as they entered the village. Bertuccio, crouched in the corner of the carriage, began to examine with a feverish anxiety every house they passed.\n\n“Tell them to stop at Rue de la Fontaine, No. 28,” said the count, fixing his eyes on the steward, to whom he gave this order.\n\nBertuccio’s forehead was covered with perspiration; however, he obeyed, and, leaning out of the window, he cried to the coachman,—“Rue de la Fontaine, No. 28.” No. 28 was situated at the extremity of the village; during the drive night had set in, and darkness gave the surroundings the artificial appearance of a scene on the stage. The carriage stopped, the footman sprang off the box and opened the door.\n\n“Well,” said the count, “you do not get out, M. Bertuccio—you are going to stay in the carriage, then? What are you thinking of this evening?”\n\nBertuccio sprang out, and offered his shoulder to the count, who, this time, leaned upon it as he descended the three steps of the carriage.\n\n“Knock,” said the count, “and announce me.”\n\nBertuccio knocked, the door opened, and the concierge appeared.\n\n“What is it?” asked he.\n\n“It is your new master, my good fellow,” said the footman. And he held out to the concierge the notary’s order.\n\n“The house is sold, then?” demanded the concierge; “and this gentleman is coming to live here?”\n\n“Yes, my friend,” returned the count; “and I will endeavor to give you no cause to regret your old master.”\n\n“Oh, monsieur,” said the concierge, “I shall not have much cause to regret him, for he came here but seldom; it is five years since he was here last, and he did well to sell the house, for it did not bring him in anything at all.”\n\n“What was the name of your old master?” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“The Marquis of Saint-Méran. Ah, I am sure he has not sold the house for what he gave for it.”\n\n“The Marquis of Saint-Méran!” returned the count. “The name is not unknown to me; the Marquis of Saint-Méran!” and he appeared to meditate.\n\n“An old gentleman,” continued the concierge, “a staunch follower of the Bourbons; he had an only daughter, who married M. de Villefort, who had been the king’s attorney at Nîmes, and afterwards at Versailles.”\n\nMonte Cristo glanced at Bertuccio, who became whiter than the wall against which he leaned to prevent himself from falling.\n\n“And is not this daughter dead?” demanded Monte Cristo; “I fancy I have heard so.”\n\n“Yes, monsieur, one-and-twenty years ago; and since then we have not seen the poor marquis three times.”\n\n“Thanks, thanks,” said Monte Cristo, judging from the steward’s utter prostration that he could not stretch the cord further without danger of breaking it. “Give me a light.”\n\n“Shall I accompany you, monsieur?”\n\n“No, it is unnecessary; Bertuccio will show me a light.”\n\nAnd Monte Cristo accompanied these words by the gift of two gold pieces, which produced a torrent of thanks and blessings from the concierge.\n\n“Ah, monsieur,” said he, after having vainly searched on the mantle-piece and the shelves, “I have not got any candles.”\n\n“Take one of the carriage-lamps, Bertuccio,” said the count, “and show me the apartments.”\n\nThe steward obeyed in silence, but it was easy to see, from the manner in which the hand that held the light trembled, how much it cost him to obey. They went over a tolerably large ground floor; a first floor consisted of a salon, a bathroom, and two bedrooms; near one of the bedrooms they came to a winding staircase that led down to the garden.\n\n“Ah, here is a private staircase,” said the count; “that is convenient. Light me, M. Bertuccio, and go first; we will see where it leads to.”\n\n“Monsieur,” replied Bertuccio, “it leads to the garden.”\n\n“And, pray, how do you know that?”\n\n“It ought to do so, at least.”\n\n“Well, let us be sure of that.”\n\nBertuccio sighed, and went on first; the stairs did, indeed, lead to the garden. At the outer door the steward paused.\n\n“Go on, Monsieur Bertuccio,” said the count.\n\nBut he who was addressed stood there, stupefied, bewildered, stunned; his haggard eyes glanced around, as if in search of the traces of some terrible event, and with his clenched hands he seemed striving to shut out horrible recollections.\n\n“Well!” insisted the Count.\n\n“No, no,” cried Bertuccio, setting down the lantern at the angle of the interior wall. “No, monsieur, it is impossible; I can go no farther.”\n\n“What does this mean?” demanded the irresistible voice of Monte Cristo.\n\n“Why, you must see, your excellency,” cried the steward, “that this is not natural; that, having a house to purchase, you purchase it exactly at Auteuil, and that, purchasing it at Auteuil, this house should be No. 28, Rue de la Fontaine. Oh, why did I not tell you all? I am sure you would not have forced me to come. I hoped your house would have been some other one than this; as if there was not another house at Auteuil than that of the assassination!”\n\n“What, what!” cried Monte Cristo, stopping suddenly, “what words do you utter? Devil of a man, Corsican that you are—always mysteries or superstitions. Come, take the lantern, and let us visit the garden; you are not afraid of ghosts with me, I hope?”\n\nBertuccio raised the lantern, and obeyed. The door, as it opened, disclosed a gloomy sky, in which the moon strove vainly to struggle through a sea of clouds that covered her with billows of vapor which she illumined for an instant, only to sink into obscurity. The steward wished to turn to the left.\n\n“No, no, monsieur,” said Monte Cristo. “What is the use of following the alleys? Here is a beautiful lawn; let us go on straight forwards.”\n\nBertuccio wiped the perspiration from his brow, but obeyed; however, he continued to take the left hand. Monte Cristo, on the contrary, took the right hand; arrived near a clump of trees, he stopped. The steward could not restrain himself.\n\n“Move, monsieur—move away, I entreat you; you are exactly in the spot!”\n“What spot?”\n\n“Where he fell.”\n20281m\n\n“My dear Monsieur Bertuccio,” said Monte Cristo, laughing, “control yourself; we are not at Sartène or at Corte. This is not a Corsican _maquis_ but an English garden; badly kept, I own, but still you must not calumniate it for that.”\n\n“Monsieur, I implore you do not stay there!”\n\n“I think you are going mad, Bertuccio,” said the count coldly. “If that is the case, I warn you, I shall have you put in a lunatic asylum.”\n\n“Alas! excellency,” returned Bertuccio, joining his hands, and shaking his head in a manner that would have excited the count’s laughter, had not thoughts of a superior interest occupied him, and rendered him attentive to the least revelation of this timorous conscience. “Alas! excellency, the evil has arrived!”\n\n“M. Bertuccio,” said the count, “I am very glad to tell you, that while you gesticulate, you wring your hands and roll your eyes like a man possessed by a devil who will not leave him; and I have always observed, that the devil most obstinate to be expelled is a secret. I knew you were a Corsican. I knew you were gloomy, and always brooding over some old history of the vendetta; and I overlooked that in Italy, because in Italy those things are thought nothing of. But in France they are considered in very bad taste; there are gendarmes who occupy themselves with such affairs, judges who condemn, and scaffolds which avenge.”\n\nBertuccio clasped his hands, and as, in all these evolutions, he did not let fall the lantern, the light showed his pale and altered countenance. Monte Cristo examined him with the same look that, at Rome, he had bent upon the execution of Andrea, and then, in a tone that made a shudder pass through the veins of the poor steward—\n\n“The Abbé Busoni, then told me an untruth,” said he, “when, after his journey in France, in 1829, he sent you to me, with a letter of recommendation, in which he enumerated all your valuable qualities. Well, I shall write to the abbé; I shall hold him responsible for his _protégé’s_ misconduct, and I shall soon know all about this assassination. Only I warn you, that when I reside in a country, I conform to all its code, and I have no wish to put myself within the compass of the French laws for your sake.”\n\n“Oh, do not do that, excellency; I have always served you faithfully,” cried Bertuccio, in despair. “I have always been an honest man, and, as far as lay in my power, I have done good.”\n\n“I do not deny it,” returned the count; “but why are you thus agitated. It is a bad sign; a quiet conscience does not occasion such paleness in the cheeks, and such fever in the hands of a man.”\n\n“But, your excellency,” replied Bertuccio hesitatingly, “did not the Abbé Busoni, who heard my confession in the prison at Nîmes, tell you that I had a heavy burden upon my conscience?”\n\n“Yes; but as he said you would make an excellent steward, I concluded you had stolen—that was all.”\n\n“Oh, your excellency!” returned Bertuccio in deep contempt.\n\n“Or, as you are a Corsican, that you had been unable to resist the desire of making a ‘stiff,’ as you call it.”\n\n“Yes, my good master,” cried Bertuccio, casting himself at the count’s feet, “it was simply vengeance—nothing else.”\n\n“I understand that, but I do not understand what it is that galvanizes you in this manner.”\n\n“But, monsieur, it is very natural,” returned Bertuccio, “since it was in this house that my vengeance was accomplished.”\n\n“What! my house?”\n\n“Oh, your excellency, it was not yours, then.”\n\n“Whose, then? The Marquis de Saint-Méran, I think, the concierge said. What had you to revenge on the Marquis de Saint-Méran?”\n\n“Oh, it was not on him, monsieur; it was on another.”\n\n“This is strange,” returned Monte Cristo, seeming to yield to his reflections, “that you should find yourself without any preparation in a house where the event happened that causes you so much remorse.”\n\n“Monsieur,” said the steward, “it is fatality, I am sure. First, you purchase a house at Auteuil—this house is the one where I have committed an assassination; you descend to the garden by the same staircase by which he descended; you stop at the spot where he received the blow; and two paces farther is the grave in which he had just buried his child. This is not chance, for chance, in this case, is too much like Providence.”\n\n“Well, amiable Corsican, let us suppose it is Providence. I always suppose anything people please, and, besides, you must concede something to diseased minds. Come, collect yourself, and tell me all.”\n\n“I have related it but once, and that was to the Abbé Busoni. Such things,” continued Bertuccio, shaking his head, “are only related under the seal of confession.”\n\n“Then,” said the count, “I refer you to your confessor. Turn Chartreux or Trappist, and relate your secrets, but, as for me, I do not like anyone who is alarmed by such phantasms, and I do not choose that my servants should be afraid to walk in the garden of an evening. I confess I am not very desirous of a visit from the commissary of police, for, in Italy, justice is only paid when silent—in France she is paid only when she speaks. _Peste!_ I thought you somewhat Corsican, a great deal smuggler, and an excellent steward; but I see you have other strings to your bow. You are no longer in my service, Monsieur Bertuccio.”\n\n“Oh, your excellency, your excellency!” cried the steward, struck with terror at this threat, “if that is the only reason I cannot remain in your service, I will tell all, for if I quit you, it will only be to go to the scaffold.”\n\n“That is different,” replied Monte Cristo; “but if you intend to tell an untruth, reflect it were better not to speak at all.”\n\n“No, monsieur, I swear to you, by my hopes of salvation, I will tell you all, for the Abbé Busoni himself only knew a part of my secret; but, I pray you, go away from that plane-tree. The moon is just bursting through the clouds, and there, standing where you do, and wrapped in that cloak that conceals your figure, you remind me of M. de Villefort.”\n\n“What!” cried Monte Cristo, “it was M. de Villefort?”\n\n“Your excellency knows him?”\n\n“The former royal attorney at Nîmes?”\n\n“Who married the Marquis of Saint-Méran’s daughter?”\n\n“Yes.”\n“Who enjoyed the reputation of being the most severe, the most upright, the most rigid magistrate on the bench?”\n\n“Well, monsieur,” said Bertuccio, “this man with this spotless reputation——”\n\n“Well?”\n\n“Was a villain.”\n\n“Bah,” replied Monte Cristo, “impossible!”\n\n“It is as I tell you.”\n\n“Ah, really,” said Monte Cristo. “Have you proof of this?”\n\n“I had it.”\n\n“And you have lost it; how stupid!”\n\n“Yes; but by careful search it might be recovered.”\n\n“Really,” returned the count, “relate it to me, for it begins to interest me.”\n\nAnd the count, humming an air from _Lucia_, went to sit down on a bench, while Bertuccio followed him, collecting his thoughts. Bertuccio remained standing before him.\n20285m\n\n# Chapter 44. The Vendetta\n\nAt what point shall I begin my story, your excellency?” asked Bertuccio.\n\n“Where you please,” returned Monte Cristo, “since I know nothing at all of it.”\n\n“I thought the Abbé Busoni had told your excellency.”\n\n“Some particulars, doubtless, but that is seven or eight years ago, and I have forgotten them.”\n\n“Then I can speak without fear of tiring your excellency.”\n\n“Go on, M. Bertuccio; you will supply the want of the evening papers.”\n\n“The story begins in 1815.”\n\n“Ah,” said Monte Cristo, “1815 is not yesterday.”\n\n“No, monsieur, and yet I recollect all things as clearly as if they had happened but then. I had a brother, an elder brother, who was in the service of the emperor; he had become lieutenant in a regiment composed entirely of Corsicans. This brother was my only friend; we became orphans—I at five, he at eighteen. He brought me up as if I had been his son, and in 1814 he married. When the emperor returned from the Island of Elba, my brother instantly joined the army, was slightly wounded at Waterloo, and retired with the army beyond the Loire.”\n\n“But that is the history of the Hundred Days, M. Bertuccio,” said the count; “unless I am mistaken, it has been already written.”\n\n“Excuse me, excellency, but these details are necessary, and you promised to be patient.”\n\n“Go on; I will keep my word.”\n\n“One day we received a letter. I should tell you that we lived in the little village of Rogliano, at the extremity of Cap Corse. This letter was from my brother. He told us that the army was disbanded, and that he should return by Châteauroux, Clermont-Ferrand, Le Puy, and Nîmes; and, if I had any money, he prayed me to leave it for him at Nîmes, with an innkeeper with whom I had dealings.”\n\n“In the smuggling line?” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“Eh, your excellency? Everyone must live.”\n\n“Certainly; go on.”\n\n“I loved my brother tenderly, as I told your excellency, and I resolved not to send the money, but to take it to him myself. I possessed a thousand francs. I left five hundred with Assunta, my sister-in-law, and with the other five hundred I set off for Nîmes. It was easy to do so, and as I had my boat and a lading to take in at sea, everything favored my project. But, after we had taken in our cargo, the wind became contrary, so that we were four or five days without being able to enter the Rhône. At last, however, we succeeded, and worked up to Arles. I left the boat between Bellegarde and Beaucaire, and took the road to Nîmes.”\n\n“We are getting to the story now?”\n\n“Yes, your excellency; excuse me, but, as you will see, I only tell you what is absolutely necessary. Just at this time the famous massacres took place in the south of France. Three brigands, called Trestaillon, Truphemy, and Graffan, publicly assassinated everybody whom they suspected of Bonapartism. You have doubtless heard of these massacres, your excellency?”\n\n“Vaguely; I was far from France at that period. Go on.”\n\n“As I entered Nîmes, I literally waded in blood; at every step you encountered dead bodies and bands of murderers, who killed, plundered, and burned. At the sight of this slaughter and devastation I became terrified, not for myself—for I, a simple Corsican fisherman, had nothing to fear; on the contrary, that time was most favorable for us smugglers—but for my brother, a soldier of the empire, returning from the army of the Loire, with his uniform and his epaulets, there was everything to apprehend. I hastened to the innkeeper. My misgivings had been but too true. My brother had arrived the previous evening at Nîmes, and, at the very door of the house where he was about to demand hospitality, he had been assassinated. I did all in my power to discover the murderers, but no one durst tell me their names, so much were they dreaded. I then thought of that French justice of which I had heard so much, and which feared nothing, and I went to the king’s attorney.”\n\n“And this king’s attorney was named Villefort?” asked Monte Cristo carelessly.\n\n“Yes, your excellency; he came from Marseilles, where he had been deputy procureur. His zeal had procured him advancement, and he was said to be one of the first who had informed the government of the departure from the Island of Elba.”\n\n“Then,” said Monte Cristo “you went to him?”\n\n“‘Monsieur,’ I said, ‘my brother was assassinated yesterday in the streets of Nîmes, I know not by whom, but it is your duty to find out. You are the representative of justice here, and it is for justice to avenge those she has been unable to protect.’\n\n“‘Who was your brother?’ asked he.\n\n“‘A lieutenant in the Corsican battalion.’\n\n“‘A soldier of the usurper, then?’\n\n“‘A soldier of the French army.’\n\n“‘Well,’ replied he, ‘he has smitten with the sword, and he has perished by the sword.’\n\n“‘You are mistaken, monsieur,’ I replied; ‘he has perished by the poniard.’\n\n“‘What do you want me to do?’ asked the magistrate.\n\n“‘I have already told you—avenge him.’\n\n“‘On whom?’\n\n“‘On his murderers.’\n\n“‘How should I know who they are?’\n\n“‘Order them to be sought for.’\n\n“‘Why, your brother has been involved in a quarrel, and killed in a duel. All these old soldiers commit excesses which were tolerated in the time of the emperor, but which are not suffered now, for the people here do not like soldiers of such disorderly conduct.’\n\n“‘Monsieur,’ I replied, ‘it is not for myself that I entreat your interference—I should grieve for him or avenge him, but my poor brother had a wife, and were anything to happen to me, the poor creature would perish from want, for my brother’s pay alone kept her. Pray, try and obtain a small government pension for her.’\n\n“‘Every revolution has its catastrophes,’ returned M. de Villefort; ‘your brother has been the victim of this. It is a misfortune, and government owes nothing to his family. If we are to judge by all the vengeance that the followers of the usurper exercised on the partisans of the king, when, in their turn, they were in power, your brother would be today, in all probability, condemned to death. What has happened is quite natural, and in conformity with the law of reprisals.’\n\n“‘What,’ cried I, ‘do you, a magistrate, speak thus to me?’\n\n“‘All these Corsicans are mad, on my honor,’ replied M. de Villefort; ‘they fancy that their countryman is still emperor. You have mistaken the time, you should have told me this two months ago, it is too late now. Go now, at once, or I shall have you put out.’\n\n“I looked at him an instant to see if there was anything to hope from further entreaty. But he was a man of stone. I approached him, and said in a low voice, ‘Well, since you know the Corsicans so well, you know that they always keep their word. You think that it was a good deed to kill my brother, who was a Bonapartist, because you are a royalist. Well, I, who am a Bonapartist also, declare one thing to you, which is, that I will kill you. From this moment I declare the vendetta against you, so protect yourself as well as you can, for the next time we meet your last hour has come.’ And before he had recovered from his surprise, I opened the door and left the room.”\n\n“Well, well,” said Monte Cristo, “such an innocent looking person as you are to do those things, M. Bertuccio, and to a king’s attorney at that! But did he know what was meant by the terrible word ‘vendetta’?”\n\n“He knew so well, that from that moment he shut himself in his house, and never went out unattended, seeking me high and low. Fortunately, I was so well concealed that he could not find me. Then he became alarmed, and dared not stay any longer at Nîmes, so he solicited a change of residence, and, as he was in reality very influential, he was nominated to Versailles. But, as you know, a Corsican who has sworn to avenge himself cares not for distance, so his carriage, fast as it went, was never above half a day’s journey before me, who followed him on foot. The most important thing was, not to kill him only—for I had an opportunity of doing so a hundred times—but to kill him without being discovered—at least, without being arrested. I no longer belonged to myself, for I had my sister-in-law to protect and provide for.\n\n“For three months I watched M. de Villefort, for three months he took not a step out-of-doors without my following him. At length I discovered that he went mysteriously to Auteuil. I followed him thither, and I saw him enter the house where we now are, only, instead of entering by the great door that looks into the street, he came on horseback, or in his carriage, left the one or the other at the little inn, and entered by the gate you see there.”\n\nMonte Cristo made a sign with his head to show that he could discern in the darkness the door to which Bertuccio alluded.\n\n“As I had nothing more to do at Versailles, I went to Auteuil, and gained all the information I could. If I wished to surprise him, it was evident this was the spot to lie in wait for him. The house belonged, as the concierge informed your excellency, to M. de Saint-Méran, Villefort’s father-in-law. M. de Saint-Méran lived at Marseilles, so that this country house was useless to him, and it was reported to be let to a young widow, known only by the name of ‘the Baroness.’\n\n“One evening, as I was looking over the wall, I saw a young and handsome woman who was walking alone in that garden, which was not overlooked by any windows, and I guessed that she was awaiting M. de Villefort. When she was sufficiently near for me to distinguish her features, I saw she was from eighteen to nineteen, tall and very fair. As she had a loose muslin dress on and as nothing concealed her figure, I saw she would ere long become a mother. A few moments after, the little door was opened and a man entered. The young woman hastened to meet him. They threw themselves into each other’s arms, embraced tenderly, and returned together to the house. The man was M. de Villefort; I fully believed that when he went out in the night he would be forced to traverse the whole of the garden alone.”\n20291m\n\n“And,” asked the count, “did you ever know the name of this woman?”\n\n“No, excellency,” returned Bertuccio; “you will see that I had no time to learn it.”\n\n“Go on.”\n\n“That evening,” continued Bertuccio, “I could have killed the procureur, but as I was not sufficiently acquainted with the neighborhood, I was fearful of not killing him on the spot, and that if his cries were overheard I might be taken; so I put it off until the next occasion, and in order that nothing should escape me, I took a chamber looking into the street bordered by the wall of the garden. Three days after, about seven o’clock in the evening, I saw a servant on horseback leave the house at full gallop, and take the road to Sèvres. I concluded that he was going to Versailles, and I was not deceived. Three hours later, the man returned covered with dust, his errand was performed, and two minutes after, another man on foot, muffled in a mantle, opened the little door of the garden, which he closed after him. I descended rapidly; although I had not seen Villefort’s face, I recognized him by the beating of my heart. I crossed the street, and stopped at a post placed at the angle of the wall, and by means of which I had once before looked into the garden.\n\n“This time I did not content myself with looking, but I took my knife out of my pocket, felt that the point was sharp, and sprang over the wall. My first care was to run to the door; he had left the key in it, taking the simple precaution of turning it twice in the lock. Nothing, then, preventing my escape by this means, I examined the grounds. The garden was long and narrow; a stretch of smooth turf extended down the middle, and at the corners were clumps of trees with thick and massy foliage, that made a background for the shrubs and flowers. In order to go from the door to the house, or from the house to the door, M. de Villefort would be obliged to pass by one of these clumps of trees.\n\n20293m\n“It was the end of September; the wind blew violently. The faint glimpses of the pale moon, hidden momentarily by masses of dark clouds that were sweeping across the sky, whitened the gravel walks that led to the house, but were unable to pierce the obscurity of the thick shrubberies, in which a man could conceal himself without any fear of discovery. I hid myself in the one nearest to the path Villefort must take, and scarcely was I there when, amidst the gusts of wind, I fancied I heard groans; but you know, or rather you do not know, your excellency, that he who is about to commit an assassination fancies that he hears low cries perpetually ringing in his ears. Two hours passed thus, during which I imagined I heard moans repeatedly. Midnight struck. As the last stroke died away, I saw a faint light shine through the windows of the private staircase by which we have just descended. The door opened, and the man in the mantle reappeared.\n\n“The terrible moment had come, but I had so long been prepared for it that my heart did not fail in the least. I drew my knife from my pocket again, opened it, and made ready to strike. The man in the mantle advanced towards me, but as he drew near I saw that he had a weapon in his hand. I was afraid, not of a struggle, but of a failure. When he was only a few paces from me, I saw that what I had taken for a weapon was only a spade. I was still unable to divine for what reason M. de Villefort had this spade in his hands, when he stopped close to the thicket where I was, glanced round, and began to dig a hole in the earth. I then perceived that he was hiding something under his mantle, which he laid on the grass in order to dig more freely. Then, I confess, curiosity mingled with hatred; I wished to see what Villefort was going to do there, and I remained motionless, holding my breath. Then an idea crossed my mind, which was confirmed when I saw the procureur lift from under his mantle a box, two feet long, and six or eight inches deep. I let him place the box in the hole he had made, then, while he stamped with his feet to remove all traces of his occupation, I rushed on him and plunged my knife into his breast, exclaiming:\n\n“‘I am Giovanni Bertuccio; thy death for my brother’s; thy treasure for his widow; thou seest that my vengeance is more complete than I had hoped.’\n\n“I know not if he heard these words; I think he did not, for he fell without a cry. I felt his blood gush over my face, but I was intoxicated, I was delirious, and the blood refreshed, instead of burning me. In a second I had disinterred the box; then, that it might not be known I had done so, I filled up the hole, threw the spade over the wall, and rushed through the door, which I double-locked, carrying off the key.”\n\n“Ah,” said Monte Cristo “it seems to me this was nothing but murder and robbery.”\n\n“No, your excellency,” returned Bertuccio; “it was a vendetta followed by restitution.”\n\n“And was the sum a large one?”\n\n“It was not money.”\n\n“Ah, I recollect,” replied the count; “did you not say something of an infant?”\n\n“Yes, excellency; I hastened to the river, sat down on the bank, and with my knife forced open the lock of the box. In a fine linen cloth was wrapped a new-born child. Its purple visage, and its violet-colored hands showed that it had perished from suffocation, but as it was not yet cold, I hesitated to throw it into the water that ran at my feet. After a moment I fancied that I felt a slight pulsation of the heart, and as I had been assistant at the hospital at Bastia, I did what a doctor would have done—I inflated the lungs by blowing air into them, and at the expiration of a quarter of an hour, it began to breathe, and cried feebly. In my turn I uttered a cry, but a cry of joy.\n\n“‘God has not cursed me then,’ I cried, ‘since he permits me to save the life of a human creature, in exchange for the life I have taken away.’”\n20295m\n\n“And what did you do with the child?” asked Monte Cristo. “It was an embarrassing load for a man seeking to escape.”\n\n“I had not for a moment the idea of keeping it, but I knew that at Paris there was an asylum where they receive such creatures. As I passed the city gates I declared that I had found the child on the road, and I inquired where the asylum was; the box confirmed my statement, the linen proved that the infant belonged to wealthy parents, the blood with which I was covered might have proceeded from the child as well as from anyone else. No objection was raised, but they pointed out the asylum, which was situated at the upper end of the Rue d’Enfer, and after having taken the precaution of cutting the linen in two pieces, so that one of the two letters which marked it was on the piece wrapped around the child, while the other remained in my possession, I rang the bell, and fled with all speed. A fortnight after I was at Rogliano, and I said to Assunta:\n\n“‘Console thyself, sister; Israel is dead, but he is avenged.’\n\n“She demanded what I meant, and when I had told her all,—‘Giovanni,’ said she, ‘you should have brought this child with you; we would have replaced the parents it has lost, have called it Benedetto, and then, in consequence of this good action, God would have blessed us.’ In reply I gave her the half of the linen I had kept in order to reclaim him if we became rich.”\n\n“What letters were marked on the linen?” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“An H and an N, surmounted by a baron’s coronet.”\n\n“By heaven, M. Bertuccio, you make use of heraldic terms; where did you study heraldry?”\n\n“In your service, excellency, where everything is learned.”\n\n“Go on, I am curious to know two things.”\n\n“What are they, your excellency?”\n\n“What became of this little boy? for I think you told me it was a boy, M. Bertuccio.”\n\n“No excellency, I do not recollect telling you that.”\n\n“I thought you did; I must have been mistaken.”\n\n“No, you were not, for it was in reality a little boy. But your excellency wished to know two things; what was the second?”\n\n“The second was the crime of which you were accused when you asked for a confessor, and the Abbé Busoni came to visit you at your request in the prison at Nîmes.”\n\n“The story will be very long, excellency.”\n\n“What matter? you know I take but little sleep, and I do not suppose you are very much inclined for it either.” Bertuccio bowed, and resumed his story.\n\n“Partly to drown the recollections of the past that haunted me, partly to supply the wants of the poor widow, I eagerly returned to my trade of smuggler, which had become more easy since that relaxation of the laws which always follows a revolution. The southern districts were ill-watched in particular, in consequence of the disturbances that were perpetually breaking out in Avignon, Nîmes, or Uzès. We profited by this respite on the part of the government to make friends everywhere. Since my brother’s assassination in the streets of Nîmes, I had never entered the town; the result was that the innkeeper with whom we were connected, seeing that we would no longer come to him, was forced to come to us, and had established a branch to his inn, on the road from Bellegarde to Beaucaire, at the sign of the Pont du Gard. We had thus, at Aigues-Mortes, Martigues, or Bouc, a dozen places where we left our goods, and where, in case of necessity, we concealed ourselves from the gendarmes and custom-house officers. Smuggling is a profitable trade, when a certain degree of vigor and intelligence is employed; as for myself, brought up in the mountains, I had a double motive for fearing the gendarmes and custom-house officers, as my appearance before the judges would cause an inquiry, and an inquiry always looks back into the past. And in my past life they might find something far more grave than the selling of smuggled cigars, or barrels of brandy without a permit. So, preferring death to capture, I accomplished the most astonishing deeds, and which, more than once, showed me that the too great care we take of our bodies is the only obstacle to the success of those projects which require rapid decision, and vigorous and determined execution. In reality, when you have once devoted your life to your enterprises, you are no longer the equal of other men, or, rather, other men are no longer your equals, and whosoever has taken this resolution, feels his strength and resources doubled.”\n\n“Philosophy, M. Bertuccio,” interrupted the count; “you have done a little of everything in your life.”\n\n“Oh, excellency!”\n\n“No, no; but philosophy at half-past ten at night is somewhat late; yet I have no other observation to make, for what you say is correct, which is more than can be said for all philosophy.”\n\n“My journeys became more and more extensive and more productive. Assunta took care of all, and our little fortune increased. One day as I was setting off on an expedition, ‘Go,’ said she; ‘at your return I will give you a surprise.’ I questioned her, but in vain; she would tell me nothing, and I departed. Our expedition lasted nearly six weeks; we had been to Lucca to take in oil, to Leghorn for English cottons, and we ran our cargo without opposition, and returned home full of joy. When I entered the house, the first thing I beheld in the middle of Assunta’s chamber was a cradle that might be called sumptuous compared with the rest of the furniture, and in it a baby seven or eight months old. I uttered a cry of joy; the only moments of sadness I had known since the assassination of the procureur were caused by the recollection that I had abandoned this child. For the assassination itself I had never felt any remorse. Poor Assunta had guessed all. She had profited by my absence, and furnished with the half of the linen, and having written down the day and hour at which I had deposited the child at the asylum, had set off for Paris, and had reclaimed it. No objection was raised, and the infant was given up to her. Ah, I confess, your excellency, when I saw this poor creature sleeping peacefully in its cradle, I felt my eyes filled with tears. ‘Ah, Assunta,’ cried I, ‘you are an excellent woman, and Heaven will bless you.’”\n\n“This,” said Monte Cristo, “is less correct than your philosophy,—it is only faith.”\n\n“Alas, your excellency is right,” replied Bertuccio, “and God made this infant the instrument of our punishment. Never did a perverse nature declare itself more prematurely, and yet it was not owing to any fault in his bringing up. He was a most lovely child, with large blue eyes, of that deep color that harmonizes so well with the blond complexion; only his hair, which was too light, gave his face a most singular expression, and added to the vivacity of his look, and the malice of his smile.\n\n“Unfortunately, there is a proverb which says that ‘red is either altogether good or altogether bad.’ The proverb was but too correct as regarded Benedetto, and even in his infancy he manifested the worst disposition. It is true that the indulgence of his foster-mother encouraged him. This child, for whom my poor sister would go to the town, five or six leagues off, to purchase the earliest fruits and the most tempting sweetmeats, preferred to Palma grapes or Genoese preserves, the chestnuts stolen from a neighbor’s orchard, or the dried apples in his loft, when he could eat as well of the nuts and apples that grew in my garden.\n\n“One day, when Benedetto was about five or six, our neighbor Wasilio, who, according to the custom of the country, never locked up his purse or his valuables—for, as your excellency knows, there are no thieves in Corsica—complained that he had lost a louis out of his purse; we thought he must have made a mistake in counting his money, but he persisted in the accuracy of his statement. One day, Benedetto, who had been gone from the house since morning, to our great anxiety, did not return until late in the evening, dragging a monkey after him, which he said he had found chained to the foot of a tree. For more than a month past, the mischievous child, who knew not what to wish for, had taken it into his head to have a monkey. A boatman, who had passed by Rogliano, and who had several of these animals, whose tricks had greatly diverted him, had, doubtless, suggested this idea to him. ‘Monkeys are not found in our woods chained to trees,’ said I; ‘confess how you obtained this animal.’ Benedetto maintained the truth of what he had said, and accompanied it with details that did more honor to his imagination than to his veracity. I became angry; he began to laugh, I threatened to strike him, and he made two steps backwards. ‘You cannot beat me,’ said he; ‘you have no right, for you are not my father.’\n20299m\n\n“We never knew who had revealed this fatal secret, which we had so carefully concealed from him; however, it was this answer, in which the child’s whole character revealed itself, that almost terrified me, and my arm fell without touching him.\n\n“The boy triumphed, and this victory rendered him so audacious, that all the money of Assunta, whose affection for him seemed to increase as he became more unworthy of it, was spent in caprices she knew not how to contend against, and follies she had not the courage to prevent. When I was at Rogliano everything went on properly, but no sooner was my back turned than Benedetto became master, and everything went ill. When he was only eleven, he chose his companions from among the young men of eighteen or twenty, the worst characters in Bastia, or, indeed, in Corsica, and they had already, for some mischievous pranks, been several times threatened with a prosecution. I became alarmed, as any prosecution might be attended with serious consequences. I was compelled, at this period, to leave Corsica on an important expedition; I reflected for a long time, and with the hope of averting some impending misfortune, I resolved that Benedetto should accompany me.\n\n“I hoped that the active and laborious life of a smuggler, with the severe discipline on board, would have a salutary effect on his character, which was now well-nigh, if not quite, corrupt. I spoke to Benedetto alone, and proposed to him to accompany me, endeavoring to tempt him by all the promises most likely to dazzle the imagination of a child of twelve. He heard me patiently, and when I had finished, burst out laughing.\n\n“‘Are you mad, uncle?’ (he called me by this name when he was in good humor); ‘do you think I am going to change the life I lead for your mode of existence—my agreeable indolence for the hard and precarious toil you impose on yourself, exposed to the bitter frost at night, and the scorching heat by day, compelled to conceal yourself, and when you are perceived, receive a volley of bullets, all to earn a paltry sum? Why, I have as much money as I want; mother Assunta always furnishes me when I ask for it! You see that I should be a fool to accept your offer.’\n\n“The arguments, and his audacity, perfectly stupefied me. Benedetto rejoined his associates, and I saw him from a distance point me out to them as a fool.”\n\n“Sweet child,” murmured Monte Cristo.\n\n“Oh, had he been my own son,” replied Bertuccio, “or even my nephew, I would have brought him back to the right road, for the knowledge that you are doing your duty gives you strength, but the idea that I was striking a child whose father I had killed, made it impossible for me to punish him. I gave my sister, who constantly defended the unfortunate boy, good advice, and as she confessed that she had several times missed money to a considerable amount, I showed her a safe place in which to conceal our little treasure for the future. My mind was already made up. Benedetto could read, write, and cipher perfectly, for when the fit seized him, he learned more in a day than others in a week. My intention was to enter him as a clerk in some ship, and without letting him know anything of my plan, to convey him some morning on board; by this means his future treatment would depend upon his own conduct. I set off for France, after having fixed upon the plan. Our cargo was to be landed in the Gulf of Lyons, and this was a difficult thing to do because it was then the year 1829. The most perfect tranquillity was restored, and the vigilance of the custom-house officers was redoubled, and their strictness was increased at this time, in consequence of the fair at Beaucaire.\n20301m\n\n“Our expedition made a favorable beginning. We anchored our vessel—which had a double hold, where our goods were concealed—amidst a number of other vessels that bordered the banks of the Rhône from Beaucaire to Arles. On our arrival we began to discharge our cargo in the night, and to convey it into the town, by the help of the innkeeper with whom we were connected.\n\n“Whether success rendered us imprudent, or whether we were betrayed, I know not; but one evening, about five o’clock, our little cabin-boy came breathlessly, to inform us that he had seen a detachment of custom-house officers advancing in our direction. It was not their proximity that alarmed us, for detachments were constantly patrolling along the banks of the Rhône, but the care, according to the boy’s account, that they took to avoid being seen. In an instant we were on the alert, but it was too late; our vessel was surrounded, and amongst the custom-house officers I observed several gendarmes, and, as terrified at the sight of their uniforms as I was brave at the sight of any other, I sprang into the hold, opened a port, and dropped into the river, dived, and only rose at intervals to breathe, until I reached a ditch that had recently been made from the Rhône to the canal that runs from Beaucaire to Aigues-Mortes. I was now safe, for I could swim along the ditch without being seen, and I reached the canal in safety. I had designedly taken this direction. I have already told your excellency of an innkeeper from Nîmes who had set up a little tavern on the road from Bellegarde to Beaucaire.”\n\n“Yes,” said Monte Cristo “I perfectly recollect him; I think he was your colleague.”\n\n“Precisely,” answered Bertuccio; “but he had, seven or eight years before this period, sold his establishment to a tailor at Marseilles, who, having almost ruined himself in his old trade, wished to make his fortune in another. Of course, we made the same arrangements with the new landlord that we had with the old; and it was of this man that I intended to ask shelter.”\n\n“What was his name?” inquired the count, who seemed to become somewhat interested in Bertuccio’s story.\n\n“Gaspard Caderousse; he had married a woman from the village of Carconte, and whom we did not know by any other name than that of her village. She was suffering from malarial fever, and seemed dying by inches. As for her husband, he was a strapping fellow of forty, or five-and-forty, who had more than once, in time of danger, given ample proof of his presence of mind and courage.”\n\n“And you say,” interrupted Monte Cristo “that this took place towards the year——”\n\n“1829, your excellency.”\n\n“In what month?”\n\n“The beginning or the end?”\n\n“The evening of the 3rd.”\n\n20303m\n“Ah,” said Monte Cristo “the evening of the 3rd of June, 1829. Go on.”\n\n“It was from Caderousse that I intended demanding shelter, and, as we never entered by the door that opened onto the road, I resolved not to break through the rule, so climbing over the garden-hedge, I crept amongst the olive and wild fig trees, and fearing that Caderousse might have some guest, I entered a kind of shed in which I had often passed the night, and which was only separated from the inn by a partition, in which holes had been made in order to enable us to watch an opportunity of announcing our presence.\n\n“My intention was, if Caderousse was alone, to acquaint him with my presence, finish the meal the custom-house officers had interrupted, and profit by the threatened storm to return to the Rhône, and ascertain the state of our vessel and its crew. I stepped into the shed, and it was fortunate I did so, for at that moment Caderousse entered with a stranger.\n\n“I waited patiently, not to overhear what they said, but because I could do nothing else; besides, the same thing had occurred often before. The man who was with Caderousse was evidently a stranger to the South of France; he was one of those merchants who come to sell jewellery at the Beaucaire fair, and who during the month the fair lasts, and during which there is so great an influx of merchants and customers from all parts of Europe, often have dealings to the amount of 100,000 to 150,000 francs. Caderousse entered hastily. Then, seeing that the room was, as usual, empty, and only guarded by the dog, he called to his wife, ‘Hello, Carconte,’ said he, ‘the worthy priest has not deceived us; the diamond is real.’\n\n“An exclamation of joy was heard, and the staircase creaked beneath a feeble step. ‘What do you say?’ asked his wife, pale as death.\n\n“‘I say that the diamond is real, and that this gentleman, one of the first jewellers of Paris, will give us 50,000 francs for it. Only, in order to satisfy himself that it really belongs to us, he wishes you to relate to him, as I have done already, the miraculous manner in which the diamond came into our possession. In the meantime please to sit down, monsieur, and I will fetch you some refreshment.’\n\n“The jeweller examined attentively the interior of the inn and the apparent poverty of the persons who were about to sell him a diamond that seemed to have come from the casket of a prince.\n\n“‘Relate your story, madame,’ said he, wishing, no doubt, to profit by the absence of the husband, so that the latter could not influence the wife’s story, to see if the two recitals tallied.\n\n“‘Oh,’ returned she, ‘it was a gift of heaven. My husband was a great friend, in 1814 or 1815, of a sailor named Edmond Dantès. This poor fellow, whom Caderousse had forgotten, had not forgotten him, and at his death he bequeathed this diamond to him.’\n\n“‘But how did he obtain it?’ asked the jeweller; ‘had he it before he was imprisoned?’\n\n“‘No, monsieur; but it appears that in prison he made the acquaintance of a rich Englishman, and as in prison he fell sick, and Dantès took the same care of him as if he had been his brother, the Englishman, when he was set free, gave this stone to Dantès, who, less fortunate, died, and, in his turn, left it to us, and charged the excellent abbé, who was here this morning, to deliver it.’\n\n“‘The same story,’ muttered the jeweller; ‘and improbable as it seemed at first, it may be true. There’s only the price we are not agreed about.’\n\n“‘How not agreed about?’ said Caderousse. ‘I thought we agreed for the price I asked.’\n\n“‘That is,’ replied the jeweller, ‘I offered 40,000 francs.’\n\n‘Forty thousand,’ cried La Carconte; ‘we will not part with it for that sum. The abbé told us it was worth 50,000 without the setting.’\n\n“‘What was the abbé’s name?’ asked the indefatigable questioner.\n\n“‘The Abbé Busoni,’ said La Carconte.\n\n“‘He was a foreigner?’\n\n“‘An Italian from the neighborhood of Mantua, I believe.’\n\n“‘Let me see this diamond again,’ replied the jeweller; ‘the first time you are often mistaken as to the value of a stone.’\n\n“Caderousse took from his pocket a small case of black shagreen, opened, and gave it to the jeweller. At the sight of the diamond, which was as large as a hazel-nut, La Carconte’s eyes sparkled with cupidity.”\n\n“And what did you think of this fine story, eavesdropper?” said Monte Cristo; “did you credit it?”\n\n“Yes, your excellency. I did not look on Caderousse as a bad man, and I thought him incapable of committing a crime, or even a theft.”\n\n“That did more honor to your heart than to your experience, M. Bertuccio. Had you known this Edmond Dantès, of whom they spoke?”\n\n“No, your excellency, I had never heard of him before, and never but once afterwards, and that was from the Abbé Busoni himself, when I saw him in the prison at Nîmes.”\n\n“Go on.”\n\n“The jeweller took the ring, and drawing from his pocket a pair of steel pliers and a small set of copper scales, he took the stone out of its setting, and weighed it carefully.\n\n“‘I will give you 45,000,’ said he, ‘but not a sou more; besides, as that is the exact value of the stone, I brought just that sum with me.’\n\n“‘Oh, that’s no matter,’ replied Caderousse, ‘I will go back with you to fetch the other 5,000 francs.’\n\n“‘No,’ returned the jeweller, giving back the diamond and the ring to Caderousse, ‘no, it is worth no more, and I am sorry I offered so much, for the stone has a flaw in it, which I had not seen. However, I will not go back on my word, and I will give 45,000.’\n\n“‘At least, replace the diamond in the ring,’ said La Carconte sharply.\n\n“‘Ah, true,’ replied the jeweller, and he reset the stone.\n\n“‘No matter,’ observed Caderousse, replacing the box in his pocket, ‘someone else will purchase it.’\n\n“‘Yes,’ continued the jeweller; ‘but someone else will not be so easy as I am, or content himself with the same story. It is not natural that a man like you should possess such a diamond. He will inform against you. You will have to find the Abbé Busoni; and abbés who give diamonds worth two thousand louis are rare. The law would seize it, and put you in prison; if at the end of three or four months you are set at liberty, the ring will be lost, or a false stone, worth three francs, will be given you, instead of a diamond worth 50,000 or perhaps 55,000 francs; from which you must allow that one runs considerable risk in purchasing.’\n\n“Caderousse and his wife looked eagerly at each other.\n\n“‘No,’ said Caderousse, ‘we are not rich enough to lose 5,000 francs.’\n\n“‘As you please, my dear sir,’ said the jeweller; ‘I had, however, as you see, brought you the money in bright coin.’ And he drew from his pocket a handful of gold, and held it sparkling before the dazzled eyes of the innkeeper, and in the other hand he held a packet of bank-notes.\n\n“There was evidently a severe struggle in the mind of Caderousse; it was plain that the small shagreen case, which he turned over and over in his hand, did not seem to him commensurate in value to the enormous sum which fascinated his gaze. He turned towards his wife.\n\n“‘What do you think of this?’ he asked in a low voice.\n\n“‘Let him have it—let him have it,’ she said. ‘If he returns to Beaucaire without the diamond, he will inform against us, and, as he says, who knows if we shall ever again see the Abbé Busoni?—in all probability we shall never see him.’\n\n“‘Well, then, so I will!’ said Caderousse; ‘so you may have the diamond for 45,000 francs. But my wife wants a gold chain, and I want a pair of silver buckles.’\n\n“The jeweller drew from his pocket a long flat box, which contained several samples of the articles demanded. ‘Here,’ he said, ‘I am very straightforward in my dealings—take your choice.’\n\n“The woman selected a gold chain worth about five louis, and the husband a pair of buckles, worth perhaps fifteen francs.\n\n“‘I hope you will not complain now?’ said the jeweller.\n\n“‘The abbé told me it was worth 50,000 francs,’ muttered Caderousse.\n\n“‘Come, come—give it to me! What a strange fellow you are,’ said the jeweller, taking the diamond from his hand. ‘I give you 45,000 francs—that is, 2,500 livres of income,—a fortune such as I wish I had myself, and you are not satisfied!’\n\n“‘And the five-and-forty thousand francs,’ inquired Caderousse in a hoarse voice, ‘where are they? Come—let us see them.’\n\n“‘Here they are,’ replied the jeweller, and he counted out upon the table 15,000 francs in gold, and 30,000 francs in bank-notes.\n\n“‘Wait whilst I light the lamp,’ said La Carconte; ‘it is growing dark, and there may be some mistake.’ In fact, night had come on during this conversation, and with night the storm which had been threatening for the last half-hour. The thunder growled in the distance; but it was apparently not heard by the jeweller, Caderousse, or La Carconte, absorbed as they were all three with the demon of gain. I myself felt a strange kind of fascination at the sight of all this gold and all these bank-notes; it seemed to me that I was in a dream, and, as it always happens in a dream, I felt myself riveted to the spot. Caderousse counted and again counted the gold and the notes, then handed them to his wife, who counted and counted them again in her turn. During this time, the jeweller made the diamond play and sparkle in the lamplight, and the gem threw out jets of light which made him unmindful of those which—precursors of the storm—began to play in at the windows.\n\n“‘Well,’ inquired the jeweller, ‘is the cash all right?’\n\n“‘Yes,’ said Caderousse. ‘Give me the pocket-book, La Carconte, and find a bag somewhere.’\n\n“La Carconte went to a cupboard, and returned with an old leathern pocket-book and a bag. From the former she took some greasy letters, and put in their place the bank-notes, and from the bag took two or three crowns of six livres each, which, in all probability, formed the entire fortune of the miserable couple.\n\n“‘There,’ said Caderousse; ‘and now, although you have wronged us of perhaps 10,000 francs, will you have your supper with us? I invite you with good-will.’\n\n“‘Thank you,’ replied the jeweller, ‘it must be getting late, and I must return to Beaucaire—my wife will be getting uneasy.’ He drew out his watch, and exclaimed, ‘_Morbleu!_ nearly nine o’clock—why, I shall not get back to Beaucaire before midnight! Good-night, my friends. If the Abbé Busoni should by any accident return, think of me.’\n\n“‘In another week you will have left Beaucaire,’ remarked Caderousse, ‘for the fair ends in a few days.’\n\n“‘True, but that makes no difference. Write to me at Paris, to M. Joannes, in the Palais Royal, arcade Pierre, No. 45. I will make the journey on purpose to see him, if it is worth while.’\n\n“At this moment there was a tremendous clap of thunder, accompanied by a flash of lightning so vivid, that it quite eclipsed the light of the lamp.\n20307m\n\n“‘See here,’ exclaimed Caderousse. ‘You cannot think of going out in such weather as this.’\n\n“‘Oh, I am not afraid of thunder,’ said the jeweller.\n\n“‘And then there are robbers,’ said La Carconte. ‘The road is never very safe during fair time.’\n\n“‘Oh, as to the robbers,’ said Joannes, ‘here is something for them,’ and he drew from his pocket a pair of small pistols, loaded to the muzzle. ‘Here,’ said he, ‘are dogs who bark and bite at the same time, they are for the two first who shall have a longing for your diamond, Friend Caderousse.’\n\n“Caderousse and his wife again interchanged a meaning look. It seemed as though they were both inspired at the same time with some horrible thought. ‘Well, then, a good journey to you,’ said Caderousse.\n\n“‘Thanks,’ replied the jeweller. He then took his cane, which he had placed against an old cupboard, and went out. At the moment when he opened the door, such a gust of wind came in that the lamp was nearly extinguished. ‘Oh,’ said he, ‘this is very nice weather, and two leagues to go in such a storm.’\n\n“‘Remain,’ said Caderousse. ‘You can sleep here.’\n\n“‘Yes; do stay,’ added La Carconte in a tremulous voice; ‘we will take every care of you.’\n\n“‘No; I must sleep at Beaucaire. So, once more, good-night.’ Caderousse followed him slowly to the threshold. ‘I can see neither heaven nor earth,’ said the jeweller, who was outside the door. ‘Do I turn to the right, or to the left hand?’\n\n“‘To the right,’ said Caderousse. ‘You cannot go wrong—the road is bordered by trees on both sides.’\n\n“‘Good—all right,’ said a voice almost lost in the distance.\n\n“‘Close the door,’ said La Carconte; ‘I do not like open doors when it thunders.’\n\n“‘Particularly when there is money in the house, eh?’ answered Caderousse, double-locking the door.\n\n20311m\n“He came into the room, went to the cupboard, took out the bag and pocket-book, and both began, for the third time, to count their gold and bank-notes. I never saw such an expression of cupidity as the flickering lamp revealed in those two countenances. The woman, especially, was hideous; her usual feverish tremulousness was intensified, her countenance had become livid, and her eyes resembled burning coals.\n\n“‘Why,’ she inquired in a hoarse voice, ‘did you invite him to sleep here tonight?’\n\n“‘Why?’ said Caderousse with a shudder; ‘why, that he might not have the trouble of returning to Beaucaire.’\n\n“‘Ah,’ responded the woman, with an expression impossible to describe; ‘I thought it was for something else.’\n\n“‘Woman, woman—why do you have such ideas?’ cried Caderousse; ‘or, if you have them, why don’t you keep them to yourself?’\n\n“‘Well,’ said La Carconte, after a moment’s pause, ‘you are not a man.’\n\n“‘What do you mean?’ added Caderousse.\n\n“‘If you had been a man, you would not have let him go from here.’\n\n“‘Woman!’\n\n“‘Or else he should not have reached Beaucaire.’\n\n“‘Woman!’\n\n“‘The road takes a turn—he is obliged to follow it—while alongside of the canal there is a shorter road.’\n\n“‘Woman!—you offend the good God. There—listen!’\n\nAnd at this moment there was a tremendous peal of thunder, while the livid lightning illumined the room, and the thunder, rolling away in the distance, seemed to withdraw unwillingly from the cursed abode. ‘Mercy!’ said Caderousse, crossing himself.\n20312m\n\n“At the same moment, and in the midst of the terrifying silence which usually follows a clap of thunder, they heard a knocking at the door. Caderousse and his wife started and looked aghast at each other.\n\n“‘Who’s there?’ cried Caderousse, rising, and drawing up in a heap the gold and notes scattered over the table, and which he covered with his two hands.\n\n“‘It is I,’ shouted a voice.\n\n“‘And who are you?’\n\n“‘Eh, _pardieu!_ Joannes, the jeweller.’\n\n“‘Well, and you said I offended the good God,’ said La Carconte with a horrid smile. ‘Why, the good God sends him back again.’ Caderousse sank pale and breathless into his chair.\n\n“La Carconte, on the contrary, rose, and going with a firm step towards the door, opened it, saying, as she did so:\n\n“‘Come in, dear M. Joannes.’\n\n“‘_Ma foi_,’ said the jeweller, drenched with rain, ‘I am not destined to return to Beaucaire tonight. The shortest follies are best, my dear Caderousse. You offered me hospitality, and I accept it, and have returned to sleep beneath your friendly roof.’\n\n“Caderousse stammered out something, while he wiped away the sweat that started to his brow. La Carconte double-locked the door behind the jeweller.”\n\n# Chapter 45. The Rain of Blood\n\nAs the jeweller returned to the apartment, he cast around him a scrutinizing glance—but there was nothing to excite suspicion, if it did not exist, or to confirm it, if it were already awakened. Caderousse’s hands still grasped the gold and bank-notes, and La Carconte called up her sweetest smiles while welcoming the reappearance of their guest.\n\n“‘Well, well,’ said the jeweller, ‘you seem, my good friends, to have had some fears respecting the accuracy of your money, by counting it over so carefully directly I was gone.’\n\n“‘Oh, no,’ answered Caderousse, ‘that was not my reason, I can assure you; but the circumstances by which we have become possessed of this wealth are so unexpected, as to make us scarcely credit our good fortune, and it is only by placing the actual proof of our riches before our eyes that we can persuade ourselves that the whole affair is not a dream.’\n\n“The jeweller smiled. ‘Have you any other guests in your house?’ inquired he.\n\n“‘Nobody but ourselves,’ replied Caderousse; ‘the fact is, we do not lodge travellers—indeed, our tavern is so near the town, that nobody would think of stopping here.’\n\n“‘Then I am afraid I shall very much inconvenience you.’\n\n“‘Inconvenience us? Not at all, my dear sir,’ said La Carconte in her most gracious manner. ‘Not at all, I assure you.’\n\n“‘But where will you manage to stow me?’\n\n“‘In the chamber overhead.’\n\n“‘Surely that is where you yourselves sleep?’\n\n“‘Never mind that; we have a second bed in the adjoining room.’\n\n“Caderousse stared at his wife with much astonishment.\n\n“The jeweller, meanwhile, was humming a song as he stood warming his back at the fire La Carconte had kindled to dry the wet garments of her guest; and this done, she next occupied herself in arranging his supper, by spreading a napkin at the end of the table, and placing on it the slender remains of their dinner, to which she added three or four fresh-laid eggs. Caderousse had once more parted with his treasure—the banknotes were replaced in the pocket-book, the gold put back into the bag, and the whole carefully locked in the cupboard. He then began pacing the room with a pensive and gloomy air, glancing from time to time at the jeweller, who stood reeking with the steam from his wet clothes, and merely changing his place on the warm hearth, to enable the whole of his garments to be dried.\n\n“‘There,’ said La Carconte, as she placed a bottle of wine on the table, ‘supper is ready whenever you are.’\n\n“‘And you?’ asked Joannes.\n\n“‘I don’t want any supper,’ said Caderousse.\n\n“‘We dined so very late,’ hastily interposed La Carconte.\n\n“‘Then it seems I am to eat alone,’ remarked the jeweller.\n\n“‘Oh, we shall have the pleasure of waiting upon you,’ answered La Carconte, with an eager attention she was not accustomed to manifest even to guests who paid for what they took.\n\n“From time to time Caderousse darted on his wife keen, searching glances, but rapid as the lightning flash. The storm still continued.\n\n“‘There, there,’ said La Carconte; ‘do you hear that? upon my word, you did well to come back.’\n\n“‘Nevertheless,’ replied the jeweller, ‘if by the time I have finished my supper the tempest has at all abated, I shall make another start.’\n\n“‘It’s the mistral,’ said Caderousse, ‘and it will be sure to last till tomorrow morning.’ He sighed heavily.\n\n“‘Well,’ said the jeweller, as he placed himself at table, ‘all I can say is, so much the worse for those who are abroad.’\n\n“‘Yes,’ chimed in La Carconte, ‘they will have a wretched night of it.’\n\n“The jeweller began eating his supper, and the woman, who was ordinarily so querulous and indifferent to all who approached her, was suddenly transformed into the most smiling and attentive hostess. Had the unhappy man on whom she lavished her assiduities been previously acquainted with her, so sudden an alteration might well have excited suspicion in his mind, or at least have greatly astonished him. Caderousse, meanwhile, continued to pace the room in gloomy silence, sedulously avoiding the sight of his guest; but as soon as the stranger had completed his repast, the agitated innkeeper went eagerly to the door and opened it.\n\n“‘I believe the storm is over,’ said he.\n\n“But as if to contradict his statement, at that instant a violent clap of thunder seemed to shake the house to its very foundation, while a sudden gust of wind, mingled with rain, extinguished the lamp he held in his hand.\n\n“Trembling and awe-struck, Caderousse hastily shut the door and returned to his guest, while La Carconte lighted a candle by the smouldering ashes that glimmered on the hearth.\n\n“‘You must be tired,’ said she to the jeweller; ‘I have spread a pair of white sheets on your bed; go up when you are ready, and sleep well.’\n\n“Joannes stayed for a while to see whether the storm seemed to abate in its fury, but a brief space of time sufficed to assure him that, instead of diminishing, the violence of the rain and thunder momentarily increased; resigning himself, therefore, to what seemed inevitable, he bade his host good-night, and mounted the stairs. He passed over my head and I heard the flooring creak beneath his footsteps. The quick, eager glance of La Carconte followed him as he ascended, while Caderousse, on the contrary, turned his back, and seemed most anxiously to avoid even glancing at him.\n\n“All these circumstances did not strike me as painfully at the time as they have since done; in fact, all that had happened (with the exception of the story of the diamond, which certainly did wear an air of improbability), appeared natural enough, and called for neither apprehension nor mistrust; but, worn out as I was with fatigue, and fully purposing to proceed onwards directly the tempest abated, I determined to obtain a few hours’ sleep. Overhead I could accurately distinguish every movement of the jeweller, who, after making the best arrangements in his power for passing a comfortable night, threw himself on his bed, and I could hear it creak and groan beneath his weight.\n\n“Insensibly my eyelids grew heavy, deep sleep stole over me, and having no suspicion of anything wrong, I sought not to shake it off. I looked into the kitchen once more and saw Caderousse sitting by the side of a long table upon one of the low wooden stools which in country places are frequently used instead of chairs; his back was turned towards me, so that I could not see the expression of his countenance—neither should I have been able to do so had he been placed differently, as his head was buried between his two hands. La Carconte continued to gaze on him for some time, then shrugging her shoulders, she took her seat immediately opposite to him.\n\n“At this moment the expiring embers threw up a fresh flame from the kindling of a piece of wood that lay near, and a bright light flashed over the room. La Carconte still kept her eyes fixed on her husband, but as he made no sign of changing his position, she extended her hard, bony hand, and touched him on the forehead.\n\n20317m\n“Caderousse shuddered. The woman’s lips seemed to move, as though she were talking; but because she merely spoke in an undertone, or my senses were dulled by sleep, I did not catch a word she uttered. Confused sights and sounds seemed to float before me, and gradually I fell into a deep, heavy slumber. How long I had been in this unconscious state I know not, when I was suddenly aroused by the report of a pistol, followed by a fearful cry. Weak and tottering footsteps resounded across the chamber above me, and the next instant a dull, heavy weight seemed to fall powerless on the staircase. I had not yet fully recovered consciousness, when again I heard groans, mingled with half-stifled cries, as if from persons engaged in a deadly struggle. A cry more prolonged than the others and ending in a series of groans effectually roused me from my drowsy lethargy. Hastily raising myself on one arm, I looked around, but all was dark; and it seemed to me as if the rain must have penetrated through the flooring of the room above, for some kind of moisture appeared to fall, drop by drop, upon my forehead, and when I passed my hand across my brow, I felt that it was wet and clammy.\n\n“To the fearful noises that had awakened me had succeeded the most perfect silence—unbroken, save by the footsteps of a man walking about in the chamber above. The staircase creaked, he descended into the room below, approached the fire and lit a candle.\n\n“The man was Caderousse—he was pale and his shirt was all bloody. Having obtained the light, he hurried upstairs again, and once more I heard his rapid and uneasy footsteps.\n\n“A moment later he came down again, holding in his hand the small shagreen case, which he opened, to assure himself it contained the diamond,—seemed to hesitate as to which pocket he should put it in, then, as if dissatisfied with the security of either pocket, he deposited it in his red handkerchief, which he carefully rolled round his head.\n\n“After this he took from his cupboard the bank-notes and gold he had put there, thrust the one into the pocket of his trousers, and the other into that of his waistcoat, hastily tied up a small bundle of linen, and rushing towards the door, disappeared in the darkness of the night.\n\n“Then all became clear and manifest to me, and I reproached myself with what had happened, as though I myself had done the guilty deed. I fancied that I still heard faint moans, and imagining that the unfortunate jeweller might not be quite dead, I determined to go to his relief, by way of atoning in some slight degree, not for the crime I had committed, but for that which I had not endeavored to prevent. For this purpose I applied all the strength I possessed to force an entrance from the cramped spot in which I lay to the adjoining room. The poorly fastened boards which alone divided me from it yielded to my efforts, and I found myself in the house. Hastily snatching up the lighted candle, I hurried to the staircase; about midway a body was lying quite across the stairs. It was that of La Carconte. The pistol I had heard had doubtless been fired at her. The shot had frightfully lacerated her throat, leaving two gaping wounds from which, as well as the mouth, the blood was pouring in floods. She was stone dead. I strode past her, and ascended to the sleeping chamber, which presented an appearance of the wildest disorder. The furniture had been knocked over in the deadly struggle that had taken place there, and the sheets, to which the unfortunate jeweller had doubtless clung, were dragged across the room. The murdered man lay on the floor, his head leaning against the wall, and about him was a pool of blood which poured forth from three large wounds in his breast; there was a fourth gash, in which a long table knife was plunged up to the handle.\n\n“I stumbled over some object; I stooped to examine—it was the second pistol, which had not gone off, probably from the powder being wet. I approached the jeweller, who was not quite dead, and at the sound of my footsteps and the creaking of the floor, he opened his eyes, fixed them on me with an anxious and inquiring gaze, moved his lips as though trying to speak, then, overcome by the effort, fell back and expired.\n\n“This appalling sight almost bereft me of my senses, and finding that I could no longer be of service to anyone in the house, my only desire was to fly. I rushed towards the staircase, clutching my hair, and uttering a groan of horror.\n\n“Upon reaching the room below, I found five or six custom-house officers, and two or three gendarmes—all heavily armed. They threw themselves upon me. I made no resistance; I was no longer master of my senses. When I strove to speak, a few inarticulate sounds alone escaped my lips.\n\n“As I noticed the significant manner in which the whole party pointed to my blood-stained garments, I involuntarily surveyed myself, and then I discovered that the thick warm drops that had so bedewed me as I lay beneath the staircase must have been the blood of La Carconte. I pointed to the spot where I had concealed myself.\n\n“‘What does he mean?’ asked a gendarme.\n\n“One of the officers went to the place I directed.\n\n“‘He means,’ replied the man upon his return, ‘that he got in that way;’ and he showed the hole I had made when I broke through.\n\n“Then I saw that they took me for the assassin. I recovered force and energy enough to free myself from the hands of those who held me, while I managed to stammer forth:\n\n“‘I did not do it! Indeed, indeed I did not!’\n\n“A couple of gendarmes held the muzzles of their carbines against my breast.\n\n“‘Stir but a step,’ said they, ‘and you are a dead man.’\n\n“‘Why should you threaten me with death,’ cried I, ‘when I have already declared my innocence?’\n\n“‘Tush, tush,’ cried the men; ‘keep your innocent stories to tell to the judge at Nîmes. Meanwhile, come along with us; and the best advice we can give you is to do so unresistingly.’\n\n“Alas, resistance was far from my thoughts. I was utterly overpowered by surprise and terror; and without a word I suffered myself to be handcuffed and tied to a horse’s tail, and thus they took me to Nîmes.\n\n“I had been tracked by a customs-officer, who had lost sight of me near the tavern; feeling certain that I intended to pass the night there, he had returned to summon his comrades, who just arrived in time to hear the report of the pistol, and to take me in the midst of such circumstantial proofs of my guilt as rendered all hopes of proving my innocence utterly futile. One only chance was left me, that of beseeching the magistrate before whom I was taken to cause every inquiry to be made for the Abbé Busoni, who had stopped at the inn of the Pont du Gard on that morning.\n\n“If Caderousse had invented the story relative to the diamond, and there existed no such person as the Abbé Busoni, then, indeed, I was lost past redemption, or, at least, my life hung upon the feeble chance of Caderousse himself being apprehended and confessing the whole truth.\n\n“Two months passed away in hopeless expectation on my part, while I must do the magistrate the justice to say that he used every means to obtain information of the person I declared could exculpate me if he would. Caderousse still evaded all pursuit, and I had resigned myself to what seemed my inevitable fate. My trial was to come on at the approaching assizes; when, on the 8th of September—that is to say, precisely three months and five days after the events which had perilled my life—the Abbé Busoni, whom I never ventured to believe I should see, presented himself at the prison doors, saying he understood one of the prisoners wished to speak to him; he added, that having learned at Marseilles the particulars of my imprisonment, he hastened to comply with my desire.\n\n“You may easily imagine with what eagerness I welcomed him, and how minutely I related the whole of what I had seen and heard. I felt some degree of nervousness as I entered upon the history of the diamond, but, to my inexpressible astonishment, he confirmed it in every particular, and to my equal surprise, he seemed to place entire belief in all I said.\n\n“And then it was that, won by his mild charity, seeing that he was acquainted with all the habits and customs of my own country, and considering also that pardon for the only crime of which I was really guilty might come with a double power from lips so benevolent and kind, I besought him to receive my confession, under the seal of which I recounted the Auteuil affair in all its details, as well as every other transaction of my life. That which I had done by the impulse of my best feelings produced the same effect as though it had been the result of calculation. My voluntary confession of the assassination at Auteuil proved to him that I had not committed that of which I stood accused. When he quitted me, he bade me be of good courage, and to rely upon his doing all in his power to convince my judges of my innocence.\n\n“I had speedy proofs that the excellent abbé was engaged in my behalf, for the rigors of my imprisonment were alleviated by many trifling though acceptable indulgences, and I was told that my trial was to be postponed to the assizes following those now being held.\n\n“In the interim it pleased Providence to cause the apprehension of Caderousse, who was discovered in some distant country, and brought back to France, where he made a full confession, refusing to make the fact of his wife’s having suggested and arranged the murder any excuse for his own guilt. The wretched man was sentenced to the galleys for life, and I was immediately set at liberty.”\n\n“And then it was, I presume,” said Monte Cristo “that you came to me as the bearer of a letter from the Abbé Busoni?”\n\n“It was, your excellency; the benevolent abbé took an evident interest in all that concerned me.\n\n“‘Your mode of life as a smuggler,’ said he to me one day, ‘will be the ruin of you; if you get out, don’t take it up again.’\n\n“‘But how,’ inquired I, ‘am I to maintain myself and my poor sister?’\n\n“‘A person, whose confessor I am,’ replied he, ‘and who entertains a high regard for me, applied to me a short time since to procure him a confidential servant. Would you like such a post? If so, I will give you a letter of introduction to him.’\n\n“‘Oh, father,’ I exclaimed, ‘you are very good.’\n\n“‘But you must swear solemnly that I shall never have reason to repent my recommendation.’\n\n“I extended my hand, and was about to pledge myself by any promise he would dictate, but he stopped me.\n\n“‘It is unnecessary for you to bind yourself by any vow,’ said he; ‘I know and admire the Corsican nature too well to fear you. Here, take this,’ continued he, after rapidly writing the few lines I brought to your excellency, and upon receipt of which you deigned to receive me into your service, and proudly I ask whether your excellency has ever had cause to repent having done so?”\n\n“No,” replied the count; “I take pleasure in saying that you have served me faithfully, Bertuccio; but you might have shown more confidence in me.”\n\n“I, your excellency?”\n\n“Yes; you. How comes it, that having both a sister and an adopted son, you have never spoken to me of either?”\n\n20323m\n“Alas, I have still to recount the most distressing period of my life. Anxious as you may suppose I was to behold and comfort my dear sister, I lost no time in hastening to Corsica, but when I arrived at Rogliano I found a house of mourning, the consequences of a scene so horrible that the neighbors remember and speak of it to this day. Acting by my advice, my poor sister had refused to comply with the unreasonable demands of Benedetto, who was continually tormenting her for money, as long as he believed there was a sou left in her possession. One morning he threatened her with the severest consequences if she did not supply him with what he desired, and disappeared and remained away all day, leaving the kind-hearted Assunta, who loved him as if he were her own child, to weep over his conduct and bewail his absence. Evening came, and still, with all the patient solicitude of a mother, she watched for his return.\n\n“As the eleventh hour struck, he entered with a swaggering air, attended by two of the most dissolute and reckless of his boon companions. She stretched out her arms to him, but they seized hold of her, and one of the three—none other than the accursed Benedetto exclaimed:\n\n“‘Put her to torture and she’ll soon tell us where her money is.’\n\n“It unfortunately happened that our neighbor, Wasilio, was at Bastia, leaving no person in his house but his wife; no human creature beside could hear or see anything that took place within our dwelling. Two held poor Assunta, who, unable to conceive that any harm was intended to her, smiled in the face of those who were soon to become her executioners. The third proceeded to barricade the doors and windows, then returned, and the three united in stifling the cries of terror incited by the sight of these preparations, and then dragged Assunta feet foremost towards the brazier, expecting to wring from her an avowal of where her supposed treasure was secreted. In the struggle her clothes caught fire, and they were obliged to let go their hold in order to preserve themselves from sharing the same fate. Covered with flames, Assunta rushed wildly to the door, but it was fastened; she flew to the windows, but they were also secured; then the neighbors heard frightful shrieks; it was Assunta calling for help. The cries died away in groans, and next morning, as soon as Wasilio’s wife could muster up courage to venture abroad, she caused the door of our dwelling to be opened by the public authorities, when Assunta, although dreadfully burnt, was found still breathing; every drawer and closet in the house had been forced open, and the money stolen. Benedetto never again appeared at Rogliano, neither have I since that day either seen or heard anything concerning him.\n\n“It was subsequently to these dreadful events that I waited on your excellency, to whom it would have been folly to have mentioned Benedetto, since all trace of him seemed entirely lost; or of my sister, since she was dead.”\n\n“And in what light did you view the occurrence?” inquired Monte Cristo.\n\n“As a punishment for the crime I had committed,” answered Bertuccio. “Oh, those Villeforts are an accursed race!”\n\n“Truly they are,” murmured the count in a lugubrious tone.\n\n“And now,” resumed Bertuccio, “your excellency may, perhaps, be able to comprehend that this place, which I revisit for the first time—this garden, the actual scene of my crime—must have given rise to reflections of no very agreeable nature, and produced that gloom and depression of spirits which excited the notice of your excellency, who was pleased to express a desire to know the cause. At this instant a shudder passes over me as I reflect that possibly I am now standing on the very grave in which lies M. de Villefort, by whose hand the ground was dug to receive the corpse of his child.”\n\n“Everything is possible,” said Monte Cristo, rising from the bench on which he had been sitting; “even,” he added in an inaudible voice, “even that the procureur be not dead. The Abbé Busoni did right to send you to me,” he went on in his ordinary tone, “and you have done well in relating to me the whole of your history, as it will prevent my forming any erroneous opinions concerning you in future. As for that Benedetto, who so grossly belied his name, have you never made any effort to trace out whither he has gone, or what has become of him?”\n\n“No; far from wishing to learn whither he has betaken himself, I should shun the possibility of meeting him as I would a wild beast. Thank God, I have never heard his name mentioned by any person, and I hope and believe he is dead.”\n\n“Do not think so, Bertuccio,” replied the count; “for the wicked are not so easily disposed of, for God seems to have them under his special watch-care to make of them instruments of his vengeance.”\n\n“So be it,” responded Bertuccio, “all I ask of heaven is that I may never see him again. And now, your excellency,” he added, bowing his head, “you know everything—you are my judge on earth, as the Almighty is in heaven; have you for me no words of consolation?”\n\n“My good friend, I can only repeat the words addressed to you by the Abbé Busoni. Villefort merited punishment for what he had done to you, and, perhaps, to others. Benedetto, if still living, will become the instrument of divine retribution in some way or other, and then be duly punished in his turn. As far as you yourself are concerned, I see but one point in which you are really guilty. Ask yourself, wherefore, after rescuing the infant from its living grave, you did not restore it to its mother? There was the crime, Bertuccio—that was where you became really culpable.”\n\n“True, excellency, that was the crime, the real crime, for in that I acted like a coward. My first duty, directly I had succeeded in recalling the babe to life, was to restore it to its mother; but, in order to do so, I must have made close and careful inquiry, which would, in all probability, have led to my own apprehension; and I clung to life, partly on my sister’s account, and partly from that feeling of pride inborn in our hearts of desiring to come off untouched and victorious in the execution of our vengeance. Perhaps, too, the natural and instinctive love of life made me wish to avoid endangering my own. And then, again, I am not as brave and courageous as was my poor brother.”\n\nBertuccio hid his face in his hands as he uttered these words, while Monte Cristo fixed on him a look of inscrutable meaning. After a brief silence, rendered still more solemn by the time and place, the count said, in a tone of melancholy wholly unlike his usual manner:\n\n“In order to bring this conversation to a fitting termination (the last we shall ever hold upon this subject), I will repeat to you some words I have heard from the lips of the Abbé Busoni. For all evils there are two remedies—time and silence. And now leave me, Monsieur Bertuccio, to walk alone here in the garden. The very circumstances which inflict on you, as a principal in the tragic scene enacted here, such painful emotions, are to me, on the contrary, a source of something like contentment, and serve but to enhance the value of this dwelling in my estimation. The chief beauty of trees consists in the deep shadow of their umbrageous boughs, while fancy pictures a moving multitude of shapes and forms flitting and passing beneath that shade. Here I have a garden laid out in such a way as to afford the fullest scope for the imagination, and furnished with thickly grown trees, beneath whose leafy screen a visionary like myself may conjure up phantoms at will. This to me, who expected but to find a blank enclosure surrounded by a straight wall, is, I assure you, a most agreeable surprise. I have no fear of ghosts, and I have never heard it said that so much harm had been done by the dead during six thousand years as is wrought by the living in a single day. Retire within, Bertuccio, and tranquillize your mind. Should your confessor be less indulgent to you in your dying moments than you found the Abbé Busoni, send for me, if I am still on earth, and I will soothe your ears with words that shall effectually calm and soothe your parting soul ere it goes forth to traverse the ocean called eternity.”\n\nBertuccio bowed respectfully, and turned away, sighing heavily. Monte Cristo, left alone, took three or four steps onwards, and murmured:\n\n“Here, beneath this plane-tree, must have been where the infant’s grave was dug. There is the little door opening into the garden. At this corner is the private staircase communicating with the sleeping apartment. There will be no necessity for me to make a note of these particulars, for there, before my eyes, beneath my feet, all around me, I have the plan sketched with all the living reality of truth.”\n\nAfter making the tour of the garden a second time, the count re-entered his carriage, while Bertuccio, who perceived the thoughtful expression of his master’s features, took his seat beside the driver without uttering a word. The carriage proceeded rapidly towards Paris.\n\nThat same evening, upon reaching his abode in the Champs-Élysées, the Count of Monte Cristo went over the whole building with the air of one long acquainted with each nook or corner. Nor, although preceding the party, did he once mistake one door for another, or commit the smallest error when choosing any particular corridor or staircase to conduct him to a place or suite of rooms he desired to visit. Ali was his principal attendant during this nocturnal survey. Having given various orders to Bertuccio relative to the improvements and alterations he desired to make in the house, the Count, drawing out his watch, said to the attentive Nubian:\n\n“It is half-past eleven o’clock; Haydée will soon be here. Have the French attendants been summoned to await her coming?”\n\nAli extended his hands towards the apartments destined for the fair Greek, which were so effectually concealed by means of a tapestried entrance, that it would have puzzled the most curious to have divined their existence. Ali, having pointed to the apartments, held up three fingers of his right hand, and then, placing it beneath his head, shut his eyes, and feigned to sleep.\n\n“I understand,” said Monte Cristo, well acquainted with Ali’s pantomime; “you mean to tell me that three female attendants await their new mistress in her sleeping-chamber.”\n\nAli, with considerable animation, made a sign in the affirmative.\n\n“Madame will be tired tonight,” continued Monte Cristo, “and will, no doubt, wish to rest. Desire the French attendants not to weary her with questions, but merely to pay their respectful duty and retire. You will also see that the Greek servants hold no communication with those of this country.”\n\nHe bowed. Just at that moment voices were heard hailing the concierge. The gate opened, a carriage rolled down the avenue, and stopped at the steps. The count hastily descended, presented himself at the already opened carriage door, and held out his hand to a young woman, completely enveloped in a green silk mantle heavily embroidered with gold. She raised the hand extended towards her to her lips, and kissed it with a mixture of love and respect. Some few words passed between them in that sonorous language in which Homer makes his gods converse. The young woman spoke with an expression of deep tenderness, while the count replied with an air of gentle gravity.\n\nPreceded by Ali, who carried a rose-colored flambeau in his hand, the young lady, who was no other than the lovely Greek who had been Monte Cristo’s companion in Italy, was conducted to her apartments, while the count retired to the pavilion reserved for himself. In another hour every light in the house was extinguished, and it might have been thought that all its inmates slept.\n\n# Chapter 46. Unlimited Credit\n\nAbout two o’clock the following day a calash, drawn by a pair of magnificent English horses, stopped at the door of Monte Cristo and a person, dressed in a blue coat, with buttons of a similar color, a white waistcoat, over which was displayed a massive gold chain, brown trousers, and a quantity of black hair descending so low over his eyebrows as to leave it doubtful whether it were not artificial so little did its jetty glossiness assimilate with the deep wrinkles stamped on his features—a person, in a word, who, although evidently past fifty, desired to be taken for not more than forty, bent forwards from the carriage door, on the panels of which were emblazoned the armorial bearings of a baron, and directed his groom to inquire at the porter’s lodge whether the Count of Monte Cristo resided there, and if he were within.\n\nWhile waiting, the occupant of the carriage surveyed the house, the garden as far as he could distinguish it, and the livery of servants who passed to and fro, with an attention so close as to be somewhat impertinent. His glance was keen but showed cunning rather than intelligence; his lips were straight, and so thin that, as they closed, they were drawn in over the teeth; his cheek-bones were broad and projecting, a never-failing proof of audacity and craftiness; while the flatness of his forehead, and the enlargement of the back of his skull, which rose much higher than his large and coarsely shaped ears, combined to form a physiognomy anything but prepossessing, save in the eyes of such as considered that the owner of so splendid an equipage must needs be all that was admirable and enviable, more especially when they gazed on the enormous diamond that glittered in his shirt, and the red ribbon that depended from his button-hole.\n\nThe groom, in obedience to his orders, tapped at the window of the porter’s lodge, saying:\n\n“Pray, does not the Count of Monte Cristo live here?”\n\n“His excellency does reside here,” replied the concierge; “but——” added he, glancing an inquiring look at Ali. Ali returned a sign in the negative.\n\n“But what?” asked the groom.\n\n“His excellency does not receive visitors today.”\n\n“Then here is my master’s card, the Baron Danglars. You will take it to the count, and say that, although in haste to attend the Chamber, my master came out of his way to have the honor of calling upon him.”\n\n“I never speak to his excellency,” replied the concierge; “the valet de chambre will carry your message.”\n\n“Well?” asked Danglars.\n\nThe man, somewhat crest-fallen by the rebuke he had received, repeated what the concierge had said.\n\n“Bless me,” murmured Baron Danglars, “this must surely be a prince instead of a count by their styling him ‘excellency,’ and only venturing to address him by the medium of his valet de chambre. However, it does not signify; he has a letter of credit on me, so I must see him when he requires his money.”\n\nThen, throwing himself back in his carriage, Danglars called out to his coachman, in a voice that might be heard across the road, “To the Chamber of Deputies.”\n\nApprised in time of the visit paid him, Monte Cristo had, from behind the blinds of his pavilion, as minutely observed the baron, by means of an excellent lorgnette, as Danglars himself had scrutinized the house, garden, and servants.\n\n“That fellow has a decidedly bad countenance,” said the count in a tone of disgust, as he shut up his glass into its ivory case. “How comes it that all do not retreat in aversion at sight of that flat, receding, serpent-like forehead, round, vulture-shaped head, and sharp-hooked nose, like the beak of a buzzard? Ali,” cried he, striking at the same time on the brazen gong. Ali appeared. “Summon Bertuccio,” said the count. Almost immediately Bertuccio entered the apartment.\n\n“Did your excellency desire to see me?” inquired he.\n\n“I did,” replied the count. “You no doubt observed the horses standing a few minutes since at the door?”\n\n“Certainly, your excellency. I noticed them for their remarkable beauty.”\n\n“Then how comes it,” said Monte Cristo with a frown, “that, when I desired you to purchase for me the finest pair of horses to be found in Paris, there is another pair, fully as fine as mine, not in my stables?”\n\nAt the look of displeasure, added to the angry tone in which the count spoke, Ali turned pale and held down his head.\n\n“It is not your fault, my good Ali,” said the count in the Arabic language, and with a gentleness none would have thought him capable of showing, either in voice or face—“it is not your fault. You do not understand the points of English horses.”\n\nThe countenance of poor Ali recovered its serenity.\n\n“Permit me to assure your excellency,” said Bertuccio, “that the horses you speak of were not to be sold when I purchased yours.”\n\nMonte Cristo shrugged his shoulders. “It seems, sir steward,” said he, “that you have yet to learn that all things are to be sold to such as care to pay the price.”\n\n“His excellency is not, perhaps, aware that M. Danglars gave 16,000 francs for his horses?”\n\n“Very well. Then offer him double that sum; a banker never loses an opportunity of doubling his capital.”\n\n“Is your excellency really in earnest?” inquired the steward.\n\nMonte Cristo regarded the person who durst presume to doubt his words with the look of one equally surprised and displeased.\n\n“I have to pay a visit this evening,” replied he. “I desire that these horses, with completely new harness, may be at the door with my carriage.”\n\nBertuccio bowed, and was about to retire; but when he reached the door, he paused, and then said, “At what o’clock does your excellency wish the carriage and horses to be ready?”\n\n“At five o’clock,” replied the count.\n\n“I beg your excellency’s pardon,” interposed the steward in a deprecating manner, “for venturing to observe that it is already two o’clock.”\n\n“I am perfectly aware of that fact,” answered Monte Cristo calmly. Then, turning towards Ali, he said, “Let all the horses in my stables be led before the windows of your young lady, that she may select those she prefers for her carriage. Request her also to oblige me by saying whether it is her pleasure to dine with me; if so, let dinner be served in her apartments. Now, leave me, and desire my valet de chambre to come hither.”\n\nScarcely had Ali disappeared when the valet entered the chamber.\n\n“Monsieur Baptistin,” said the count, “you have been in my service one year, the time I generally give myself to judge of the merits or demerits of those about me. You suit me very well.”\n\nBaptistin bowed low.\n\n“It only remains for me to know whether I also suit you?”\n\n“Oh, your excellency!” exclaimed Baptistin eagerly.\n\n“Listen, if you please, till I have finished speaking,” replied Monte Cristo. “You receive 1,500 francs per annum for your services here—more than many a brave subaltern, who continually risks his life for his country, obtains. You live in a manner far superior to many clerks who work ten times harder than you do for their money. Then, though yourself a servant, you have other servants to wait upon you, take care of your clothes, and see that your linen is duly prepared for you. Again, you make a profit upon each article you purchase for my toilet, amounting in the course of a year to a sum equalling your wages.”\n\n“Nay, indeed, your excellency.”\n\n“I am not condemning you for this, Monsieur Baptistin; but let your profits end here. It would be long indeed ere you would find so lucrative a post as that you have now the good fortune to fill. I neither ill-use nor ill-treat my servants by word or action. An error I readily forgive, but wilful negligence or forgetfulness, never. My commands are ordinarily short, clear, and precise; and I would rather be obliged to repeat my words twice, or even three times, than they should be misunderstood. I am rich enough to know whatever I desire to know, and I can promise you I am not wanting in curiosity. If, then, I should learn that you had taken upon yourself to speak of me to anyone favorably or unfavorably, to comment on my actions, or watch my conduct, that very instant you would quit my service. You may now retire. I never caution my servants a second time—remember that.”\n\nBaptistin bowed, and was proceeding towards the door.\n\n“I forgot to mention to you,” said the count, “that I lay yearly aside a certain sum for each servant in my establishment; those whom I am compelled to dismiss lose (as a matter of course) all participation in this money, while their portion goes to the fund accumulating for those domestics who remain with me, and among whom it will be divided at my death. You have been in my service a year, your fund has already begun to accumulate—let it continue to do so.”\n\nThis address, delivered in the presence of Ali, who, not understanding one word of the language in which it was spoken, stood wholly unmoved, produced an effect on M. Baptistin only to be conceived by such as have occasion to study the character and disposition of French domestics.\n\n“I assure your excellency,” said he, “that at least it shall be my study to merit your approbation in all things, and I will take M. Ali as my model.”\n\n“By no means,” replied the count in the most frigid tones; “Ali has many faults mixed with most excellent qualities. He cannot possibly serve you as a pattern for your conduct, not being, as you are, a paid servant, but a mere slave—a dog, who, should he fail in his duty towards me, I should not discharge from my service, but kill.”\n\nBaptistin opened his eyes with astonishment.\n\n“You seem incredulous,” said Monte Cristo, who repeated to Ali in the Arabic language what he had just been saying to Baptistin in French.\n\nThe Nubian smiled assentingly to his master’s words, then, kneeling on one knee, respectfully kissed the hand of the count. This corroboration of the lesson he had just received put the finishing stroke to the wonder and stupefaction of M. Baptistin. The count then motioned the valet de chambre to retire, and to Ali to follow to his study, where they conversed long and earnestly together. As the hand of the clock pointed to five the count struck thrice upon his gong. When Ali was wanted one stroke was given, two summoned Baptistin, and three Bertuccio. The steward entered.\n\n“My horses,” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“They are at the door harnessed to the carriage as your excellency desired. Does your excellency wish me to accompany him?”\n\n“No, the coachman, Ali, and Baptistin will go.”\n\nThe count descended to the door of his mansion, and beheld his carriage drawn by the very pair of horses he had so much admired in the morning as the property of Danglars. As he passed them he said:\n\n“They are extremely handsome certainly, and you have done well to purchase them, although you were somewhat remiss not to have procured them sooner.”\n\n“Indeed, your excellency, I had very considerable difficulty in obtaining them, and, as it is, they have cost an enormous price.”\n\n“Does the sum you gave for them make the animals less beautiful,” inquired the count, shrugging his shoulders.\n\n“Nay, if your excellency is satisfied, it is all that I could wish. Whither does your excellency desire to be driven?”\n\n“To the residence of Baron Danglars, Rue de la Chaussée d’Antin.”\n\nThis conversation had passed as they stood upon the terrace, from which a flight of stone steps led to the carriage-drive. As Bertuccio, with a respectful bow, was moving away, the count called him back.\n\n“I have another commission for you, M. Bertuccio,” said he; “I am desirous of having an estate by the seaside in Normandy—for instance, between Le Havre and Boulogne. You see I give you a wide range. It will be absolutely necessary that the place you may select have a small harbor, creek, or bay, into which my corvette can enter and remain at anchor. She draws only fifteen feet. She must be kept in constant readiness to sail immediately I think proper to give the signal. Make the requisite inquiries for a place of this description, and when you have met with an eligible spot, visit it, and if it possess the advantages desired, purchase it at once in your own name. The corvette must now, I think, be on her way to Fécamp, must she not?”\n20333m\n\n“Certainly, your excellency; I saw her put to sea the same evening we quitted Marseilles.”\n\n“And the yacht.”\n\n“Was ordered to remain at Martigues.”\n\n“’Tis well. I wish you to write from time to time to the captains in charge of the two vessels so as to keep them on the alert.”\n\n“And the steamboat?”\n\n“She is at Châlons?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“The same orders for her as for the two sailing vessels.”\n\n“Very good.”\n\n“When you have purchased the estate I desire, I want constant relays of horses at ten leagues apart along the northern and southern road.”\n\n“Your excellency may depend upon me.”\n\nThe Count made a gesture of satisfaction, descended the terrace steps, and sprang into his carriage, which was whirled along swiftly to the banker’s house.\n\nDanglars was engaged at that moment, presiding over a railroad committee. But the meeting was nearly concluded when the name of his visitor was announced. As the count’s title sounded on his ear he rose, and addressing his colleagues, who were members of one or the other Chamber, he said:\n\n“Gentlemen, pardon me for leaving you so abruptly; but a most ridiculous circumstance has occurred, which is this,—Thomson & French, the Roman bankers, have sent to me a certain person calling himself the Count of Monte Cristo, and have given him an unlimited credit with me. I confess this is the drollest thing I have ever met with in the course of my extensive foreign transactions, and you may readily suppose it has greatly roused my curiosity. I took the trouble this morning to call on the pretended count—if he were a real count he wouldn’t be so rich. But, would you believe it, ‘He was not receiving.’ So the master of Monte Cristo gives himself airs befitting a great millionaire or a capricious beauty. I made inquiries, and found that the house in the Champs-Élysées is his own property, and certainly it was very decently kept up. But,” pursued Danglars with one of his sinister smiles, “an order for unlimited credit calls for something like caution on the part of the banker to whom that order is given. I am very anxious to see this man. I suspect a hoax is intended, but the instigators of it little knew whom they had to deal with. ‘They laugh best who laugh last!’”\n\nHaving delivered himself of this pompous address, uttered with a degree of energy that left the baron almost out of breath, he bowed to the assembled party and withdrew to his drawing-room, whose sumptuous furnishings of white and gold had caused a great sensation in the Chaussée d’Antin. It was to this apartment he had desired his guest to be shown, with the purpose of overwhelming him at the sight of so much luxury. He found the count standing before some copies of Albano and Fattore that had been passed off to the banker as originals; but which, mere copies as they were, seemed to feel their degradation in being brought into juxtaposition with the gaudy colors that covered the ceiling.\n\nThe count turned round as he heard the entrance of Danglars into the room. With a slight inclination of the head, Danglars signed to the count to be seated, pointing significantly to a gilded armchair, covered with white satin embroidered with gold. The count sat down.\n20335m\n\n“I have the honor, I presume, of addressing M. de Monte Cristo.”\n\nThe count bowed.\n\n“And I of speaking to Baron Danglars, chevalier of the Legion of Honor, and member of the Chamber of Deputies?”\n\nMonte Cristo repeated all the titles he had read on the baron’s card.\n\nDanglars felt the irony and compressed his lips.\n\n“You will, I trust, excuse me, monsieur, for not calling you by your title when I first addressed you,” he said, “but you are aware that we are living under a popular form of government, and that I am myself a representative of the liberties of the people.”\n\n“So much so,” replied Monte Cristo, “that while you call yourself baron you are not willing to call anybody else count.”\n\n“Upon my word, monsieur,” said Danglars with affected carelessness, “I attach no sort of value to such empty distinctions; but the fact is, I was made baron, and also chevalier of the Legion of Honor, in return for services rendered, but——”\n\n“But you have discarded your titles after the example set you by Messrs. de Montmorency and Lafayette? That was a noble example to follow, monsieur.”\n\n“Why,” replied Danglars, “not entirely so; with the servants,—you understand.”\n\n“I see; to your domestics you are ‘my lord,’ the journalists style you ‘monsieur,’ while your constituents call you ‘citizen.’ These are distinctions very suitable under a constitutional government. I understand perfectly.”\n\nAgain Danglars bit his lips; he saw that he was no match for Monte Cristo in an argument of this sort, and he therefore hastened to turn to subjects more congenial.\n\n“Permit me to inform you, Count,” said he, bowing, “that I have received a letter of advice from Thomson & French, of Rome.”\n\n“I am glad to hear it, baron,—for I must claim the privilege of addressing you after the manner of your servants. I have acquired the bad habit of calling persons by their titles from living in a country where barons are still barons by right of birth. But as regards the letter of advice, I am charmed to find that it has reached you; that will spare me the troublesome and disagreeable task of coming to you for money myself. You have received a regular letter of advice?”\n\n“Yes,” said Danglars, “but I confess I didn’t quite comprehend its meaning.”\n\n“Indeed?”\n\n“And for that reason I did myself the honor of calling upon you, in order to beg for an explanation.”\n\n“Go on, monsieur. Here I am, ready to give you any explanation you desire.”\n\n“Why,” said Danglars, “in the letter—I believe I have it about me”—here he felt in his breast-pocket—“yes, here it is. Well, this letter gives the Count of Monte Cristo unlimited credit on our house.”\n\n“Well, baron, what is there difficult to understand about that?”\n\n“Merely the term _unlimited_—nothing else, certainly.”\n\n“Is not that word known in France? The people who wrote are Anglo-Germans, you know.”\n\n“Oh, as for the composition of the letter, there is nothing to be said; but as regards the competency of the document, I certainly have doubts.”\n\n“Is it possible?” asked the count, assuming all air and tone of the utmost simplicity and candor. “Is it possible that Thomson & French are not looked upon as safe and solvent bankers? Pray tell me what you think, baron, for I feel uneasy, I can assure you, having some considerable property in their hands.”\n\n“Thomson & French are perfectly solvent,” replied Danglars, with an almost mocking smile; “but the word _unlimited_, in financial affairs, is so extremely vague.”\n\n“Is, in fact, unlimited,” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“Precisely what I was about to say,” cried Danglars. “Now what is vague is doubtful; and it was a wise man who said, ‘when in doubt, keep out.’”\n\n“Meaning to say,” rejoined Monte Cristo, “that however Thomson & French may be inclined to commit acts of imprudence and folly, the Baron Danglars is not disposed to follow their example.”\n\n“Not at all.”\n\n“Plainly enough; Messrs. Thomson & French set no bounds to their engagements while those of M. Danglars have their limits; he is a wise man, according to his own showing.”\n\n“Monsieur,” replied the banker, drawing himself up with a haughty air, “the extent of my resources has never yet been questioned.”\n\n“It seems, then, reserved for me,” said Monte Cristo coldly, “to be the first to do so.”\n\n“By what right, sir?”\n\n“By right of the objections you have raised, and the explanations you have demanded, which certainly must have some motive.”\n\nOnce more Danglars bit his lips. It was the second time he had been worsted, and this time on his own ground. His forced politeness sat awkwardly upon him, and approached almost to impertinence. Monte Cristo on the contrary, preserved a graceful suavity of demeanor, aided by a certain degree of simplicity he could assume at pleasure, and thus possessed the advantage.\n\n“Well, sir,” resumed Danglars, after a brief silence, “I will endeavor to make myself understood, by requesting you to inform me for what sum you propose to draw upon me?”\n\n“Why, truly,” replied Monte Cristo, determined not to lose an inch of the ground he had gained, “my reason for desiring an ‘unlimited’ credit was precisely because I did not know how much money I might need.”\n\nThe banker thought the time had come for him to take the upper hand. So throwing himself back in his armchair, he said, with an arrogant and purse-proud air:\n\n“Let me beg of you not to hesitate in naming your wishes; you will then be convinced that the resources of the house of Danglars, however limited, are still equal to meeting the largest demands; and were you even to require a million——”\n\n“I beg your pardon,” interposed Monte Cristo.\n\n“I said a million,” replied Danglars, with the confidence of ignorance.\n\n“But could I do with a million?” retorted the count. “My dear sir, if a trifle like that could suffice me, I should never have given myself the trouble of opening an account. A million? Excuse my smiling when you speak of a sum I am in the habit of carrying in my pocket-book or dressing-case.”\n\nAnd with these words Monte Cristo took from his pocket a small case containing his visiting-cards, and drew forth two orders on the treasury for 500,000 francs each, payable at sight to the bearer. A man like Danglars was wholly inaccessible to any gentler method of correction. The effect of the present revelation was stunning; he trembled and was on the verge of apoplexy. The pupils of his eyes, as he gazed at Monte Cristo dilated horribly.\n\n“Come, come,” said Monte Cristo, “confess honestly that you have not perfect confidence in Thomson & French. I understand, and foreseeing that such might be the case, I took, in spite of my ignorance of affairs, certain precautions. See, here are two similar letters to that you have yourself received; one from the house of Arstein & Eskeles of Vienna, to Baron Rothschild, the other drawn by Baring of London, upon M. Lafitte. Now, sir, you have but to say the word, and I will spare you all uneasiness by presenting my letter of credit to one or other of these two firms.”\n\nThe blow had struck home, and Danglars was entirely vanquished; with a trembling hand he took the two letters from the count, who held them carelessly between finger and thumb, and proceeded to scrutinize the signatures, with a minuteness that the count might have regarded as insulting, had it not suited his present purpose to mislead the banker.\n\n“Oh, sir,” said Danglars, after he had convinced himself of the authenticity of the documents he held, and rising as if to salute the power of gold personified in the man before him,—“three letters of unlimited credit! I can be no longer mistrustful, but you must pardon me, my dear count, for confessing to some degree of astonishment.”\n\n“Nay,” answered Monte Cristo, with the most gentlemanly air, “’tis not for such trifling sums as these that your banking house is to be incommoded. Then, you can let me have some money, can you not?”\n\n“Whatever you say, my dear count; I am at your orders.”\n\n“Why,” replied Monte Cristo, “since we mutually understand each other—for such I presume is the case?” Danglars bowed assentingly. “You are quite sure that not a lurking doubt or suspicion lingers in your mind?”\n\n“Oh, my dear count,” exclaimed Danglars, “I never for an instant entertained such a feeling towards you.”\n\n“No, you merely wished to be convinced, nothing more; but now that we have come to so clear an understanding, and that all distrust and suspicion are laid at rest, we may as well fix a sum as the probable expenditure of the first year, suppose we say six millions to——”\n\n“Six millions!” gasped Danglars—“so be it.”\n\n“Then, if I should require more,” continued Monte Cristo in a careless manner, “why, of course, I should draw upon you; but my present intention is not to remain in France more than a year, and during that period I scarcely think I shall exceed the sum I mentioned. However, we shall see. Be kind enough, then, to send me 500,000 francs tomorrow. I shall be at home till midday, or if not, I will leave a receipt with my steward.”\n\n“The money you desire shall be at your house by ten o’clock tomorrow morning, my dear count,” replied Danglars. “How would you like to have it? in gold, silver, or notes?”\n\n“Half in gold, and the other half in bank-notes, if you please,” said the count, rising from his seat.\n\n“I must confess to you, count,” said Danglars, “that I have hitherto imagined myself acquainted with the degree of all the great fortunes of Europe, and still wealth such as yours has been wholly unknown to me. May I presume to ask whether you have long possessed it?”\n\n“It has been in the family a very long while,” returned Monte Cristo, “a sort of treasure expressly forbidden to be touched for a certain period of years, during which the accumulated interest has doubled the capital. The period appointed by the testator for the disposal of these riches occurred only a short time ago, and they have only been employed by me within the last few years. Your ignorance on the subject, therefore, is easily accounted for. However, you will be better informed as to me and my possessions ere long.”\n\nAnd the count, while pronouncing these latter words, accompanied them with one of those ghastly smiles that used to strike terror into poor Franz d’Épinay.\n\n“With your tastes, and means of gratifying them,” continued Danglars, “you will exhibit a splendor that must effectually put us poor miserable millionaires quite in the shade. If I mistake not you are an admirer of paintings, at least I judged so from the attention you appeared to be bestowing on mine when I entered the room. If you will permit me, I shall be happy to show you my picture gallery, composed entirely of works by the ancient masters—warranted as such. Not a modern picture among them. I cannot endure the modern school of painting.”\n\n“You are perfectly right in objecting to them, for this one great fault—that they have not yet had time to become old.”\n\n“Or will you allow me to show you several fine statues by Thorwaldsen, Bartoloni, and Canova?—all foreign artists, for, as you may perceive, I think but very indifferently of our French sculptors.”\n\n“You have a right to be unjust to them, monsieur; they are your compatriots.”\n\n“But all this may come later, when we shall be better known to each other. For the present, I will confine myself (if perfectly agreeable to you) to introducing you to the Baroness Danglars—excuse my impatience, my dear count, but a client like you is almost like a member of the family.”\n\nMonte Cristo bowed, in sign that he accepted the proffered honor; Danglars rang and was answered by a servant in a showy livery.\n\n“Is the baroness at home?” inquired Danglars.\n\n“Yes, my lord,” answered the man.\n\n“And alone?”\n\n“No, my lord, madame has visitors.”\n\n“Have you any objection to meet any persons who may be with madame, or do you desire to preserve a strict _incognito_?”\n\n“No, indeed,” replied Monte Cristo with a smile, “I do not arrogate to myself the right of so doing.”\n\n“And who is with madame?—M. Debray?” inquired Danglars, with an air of indulgence and good-nature that made Monte Cristo smile, acquainted as he was with the secrets of the banker’s domestic life.\n\n“Yes, my lord,” replied the servant, “M. Debray is with madame.”\n\nDanglars nodded his head; then, turning to Monte Cristo, said, “M. Lucien Debray is an old friend of ours, and private secretary to the Minister of the Interior. As for my wife, I must tell you, she lowered herself by marrying me, for she belongs to one of the most ancient families in France. Her maiden name was De Servières, and her first husband was Colonel the Marquis of Nargonne.”\n\n“I have not the honor of knowing Madame Danglars; but I have already met M. Lucien Debray.”\n\n“Ah, indeed?” said Danglars; “and where was that?”\n\n“At the house of M. de Morcerf.”\n\n“Ah! you are acquainted with the young viscount, are you?”\n\n“We were together a good deal during the Carnival at Rome.”\n\n“True, true,” cried Danglars. “Let me see; have I not heard talk of some strange adventure with bandits or thieves hid in ruins, and of his having had a miraculous escape? I forget how, but I know he used to amuse my wife and daughter by telling them about it after his return from Italy.”\n\n“Her ladyship is waiting to receive you, gentlemen,” said the servant, who had gone to inquire the pleasure of his mistress.\n\n“With your permission,” said Danglars, bowing, “I will precede you, to show you the way.”\n\n“By all means,” replied Monte Cristo; “I follow you.”\n\n# Chapter 47. The Dappled Grays\n\nThe baron, followed by the count, traversed a long series of apartments, in which the prevailing characteristics were heavy magnificence and the gaudiness of ostentatious wealth, until he reached the boudoir of Madame Danglars—a small octagonal-shaped room, hung with pink satin, covered with white Indian muslin. The chairs were of ancient workmanship and materials; over the doors were painted sketches of shepherds and shepherdesses, after the style and manner of Boucher; and at each side pretty medallions in crayons, harmonizing well with the furnishings of this charming apartment, the only one throughout the great mansion in which any distinctive taste prevailed. The truth was, it had been entirely overlooked in the plan arranged and followed out by M. Danglars and his architect, who had been selected to aid the baron in the great work of improvement solely because he was the most fashionable and celebrated decorator of the day. The decorations of the boudoir had then been left entirely to Madame Danglars and Lucien Debray. M. Danglars, however, while possessing a great admiration for the antique, as it was understood during the time of the Directory, entertained the most sovereign contempt for the simple elegance of his wife’s favorite sitting-room, where, by the way, he was never permitted to intrude, unless, indeed, he excused his own appearance by ushering in some more agreeable visitor than himself; and even then he had rather the air and manner of a person who was himself introduced, than that of being the presenter of another, his reception being cordial or frigid, in proportion as the person who accompanied him chanced to please or displease the baroness.\n\nMadame Danglars (who, although past the first bloom of youth, was still strikingly handsome) was now seated at the piano, a most elaborate piece of cabinet and inlaid work, while Lucien Debray, standing before a small work-table, was turning over the pages of an album.\n\nLucien had found time, preparatory to the count’s arrival, to relate many particulars respecting him to Madame Danglars. It will be remembered that Monte Cristo had made a lively impression on the minds of all the party assembled at the breakfast given by Albert de Morcerf; and although Debray was not in the habit of yielding to such feelings, he had never been able to shake off the powerful influence excited in his mind by the impressive look and manner of the count, consequently the description given by Lucien to the baroness bore the highly-colored tinge of his own heated imagination. Already excited by the wonderful stories related of the count by de Morcerf, it is no wonder that Madame Danglars eagerly listened to, and fully credited, all the additional circumstances detailed by Debray. This posing at the piano and over the album was only a little ruse adopted by way of precaution. A most gracious welcome and unusual smile were bestowed on M. Danglars; the count, in return for his gentlemanly bow, received a formal though graceful courtesy, while Lucien exchanged with the count a sort of distant recognition, and with Danglars a free and easy nod.\n\n“Baroness,” said Danglars, “give me leave to present to you the Count of Monte Cristo, who has been most warmly recommended to me by my correspondents at Rome. I need but mention one fact to make all the ladies in Paris court his notice, and that is, that he has come to take up his abode in Paris for a year, during which brief period he proposes to spend six millions of money. That means balls, dinners, and lawn parties without end, in all of which I trust the count will remember us, as he may depend upon it we shall him, in our own humble entertainments.”\n\nIn spite of the gross flattery and coarseness of this address, Madame Danglars could not forbear gazing with considerable interest on a man capable of expending six millions in twelve months, and who had selected Paris for the scene of his princely extravagance.\n\n“And when did you arrive here?” inquired she.\n\n“Yesterday morning, madame.”\n\n“Coming, as usual, I presume, from the extreme end of the globe? Pardon me—at least, such I have heard is your custom.”\n\n“Nay, madame. This time I have merely come from Cadiz.”\n\n“You have selected a most unfavorable moment for your first visit. Paris is a horrible place in summer. Balls, parties, and _fêtes_ are over; the Italian opera is in London; the French opera everywhere except in Paris. As for the Théatre Français, you know, of course, that it is nowhere. The only amusements left us are the indifferent races at the Champ-de-Mars and Satory. Do you propose entering any horses at either of these races, count?”\n\n“I shall do whatever they do at Paris, madame, if I have the good fortune to find someone who will initiate me into the prevalent ideas of amusement.”\n\n“Are you fond of horses, count?”\n\n“I have passed a considerable part of my life in the East, madame, and you are doubtless aware that the Orientals value only two things—the fine breeding of their horses and the beauty of their women.”\n\n“Nay, count,” said the baroness, “it would have been somewhat more gallant to have placed the ladies first.”\n\n“You see, madame, how rightly I spoke when I said I required a preceptor to guide me in all my sayings and doings here.”\n\nAt this instant the favorite attendant of Madame Danglars entered the boudoir; approaching her mistress, she spoke some words in an undertone. Madame Danglars turned very pale, then exclaimed:\n\n“I cannot believe it; the thing is impossible.”\n\n“I assure you, madame,” replied the woman, “it is as I have said.”\n\nTurning impatiently towards her husband, Madame Danglars demanded, “Is this true?”\n\n“Is what true, madame?” inquired Danglars, visibly agitated.\n\n“What my maid tells me.”\n\n“But what does she tell you?”\n\n“That when my coachman was about to harness the horses to my carriage, he discovered that they had been removed from the stables without his knowledge. I desire to know what is the meaning of this?”\n\n“Be kind enough, madame, to listen to me,” said Danglars.\n\n“Oh, yes; I will listen, monsieur, for I am most curious to hear what explanation you will give. These two gentlemen shall decide between us; but, first, I will state the case to them. Gentlemen,” continued the baroness, “among the ten horses in the stables of Baron Danglars, are two that belong exclusively to me—a pair of the handsomest and most spirited creatures to be found in Paris. But to you, at least, M. Debray, I need not give a further description, because to you my beautiful pair of dappled grays were well known. Well, I had promised Madame de Villefort the loan of my carriage to drive tomorrow to the Bois; but when my coachman goes to fetch the grays from the stables they are gone—positively gone. No doubt M. Danglars has sacrificed them to the selfish consideration of gaining some thousands of paltry francs. Oh, what a detestable crew they are, these mercenary speculators!”\n\n“Madame,” replied Danglars, “the horses were not sufficiently quiet for you; they were scarcely four years old, and they made me extremely uneasy on your account.”\n\n“Nonsense,” retorted the baroness; “you could not have entertained any alarm on the subject, because you are perfectly well aware that I have had for a month in my service the very best coachman in Paris. But, perhaps, you have disposed of the coachman as well as the horses?”\n\n“My dear love, pray do not say any more about them, and I promise you another pair exactly like them in appearance, only more quiet and steady.”\n\nThe baroness shrugged her shoulders with an air of ineffable contempt, while her husband, affecting not to observe this unconjugal gesture, turned towards Monte Cristo and said,—“Upon my word, count, I am quite sorry not to have met you sooner. You are setting up an establishment, of course?”\n\n“Why, yes,” replied the count.\n\n“I should have liked to have made you the offer of these horses. I have almost given them away, as it is; but, as I before said, I was anxious to get rid of them upon any terms. They were only fit for a young man.”\n\n“I am much obliged by your kind intentions towards me,” said Monte Cristo; “but this morning I purchased a very excellent pair of carriage-horses, and I do not think they were dear. There they are. Come, M. Debray, you are a connoisseur, I believe, let me have your opinion upon them.”\n\nAs Debray walked towards the window, Danglars approached his wife.\n\n“I could not tell you before others,” said he in a low tone, “the reason of my parting with the horses; but a most enormous price was offered me this morning for them. Some madman or fool, bent upon ruining himself as fast as he can, actually sent his steward to me to purchase them at any cost; and the fact is, I have gained 16,000 francs by the sale of them. Come, don’t look so angry, and you shall have 4,000 francs of the money to do what you like with, and Eugénie shall have 2,000. There, what do you think now of the affair? Wasn’t I right to part with the horses?”\n\nMadame Danglars surveyed her husband with a look of withering contempt.\n\n“Great heavens?” suddenly exclaimed Debray.\n\n“What is it?” asked the baroness.\n\n“I cannot be mistaken; there are your horses! The very animals we were speaking of, harnessed to the count’s carriage!”\n\n“My dappled grays?” demanded the baroness, springing to the window. “’Tis indeed they!” said she.\n\nDanglars looked absolutely stupefied.\n\n“How very singular,” cried Monte Cristo with well-feigned astonishment.\n\n“I cannot believe it,” murmured the banker. Madame Danglars whispered a few words in the ear of Debray, who approached Monte Cristo, saying, “The baroness wishes to know what you paid her husband for the horses.”\n\n“I scarcely know,” replied the count; “it was a little surprise prepared for me by my steward, and cost me—well, somewhere about 30,000 francs.”\n\nDebray conveyed the count’s reply to the baroness. Poor Danglars looked so crest-fallen and discomfited that Monte Cristo assumed a pitying air towards him.\n\n“See,” said the count, “how very ungrateful women are. Your kind attention, in providing for the safety of the baroness by disposing of the horses, does not seem to have made the least impression on her. But so it is; a woman will often, from mere wilfulness, prefer that which is dangerous to that which is safe. Therefore, in my opinion, my dear baron, the best and easiest way is to leave them to their fancies, and allow them to act as they please, and then, if any mischief follows, why, at least, they have no one to blame but themselves.”\n\nDanglars made no reply; he was occupied in anticipations of the coming scene between himself and the baroness, whose frowning brow, like that of Olympic Jove, predicted a storm. Debray, who perceived the gathering clouds, and felt no desire to witness the explosion of Madame Danglars’ rage, suddenly recollected an appointment, which compelled him to take his leave; while Monte Cristo, unwilling by prolonging his stay to destroy the advantages he hoped to obtain, made a farewell bow and departed, leaving Danglars to endure the angry reproaches of his wife.\n20347m\n\n“Excellent,” murmured Monte Cristo to himself, as he came away. “All has gone according to my wishes. The domestic peace of this family is henceforth in my hands. Now, then, to play another master-stroke, by which I shall gain the heart of both husband and wife—delightful! Still,” added he, “amid all this, I have not yet been presented to Mademoiselle Eugénie Danglars, whose acquaintance I should have been glad to make. But,” he went on with his peculiar smile, “I am here in Paris, and have plenty of time before me—by and by will do for that.”\n\nWith these reflections he entered his carriage and returned home. Two hours afterwards, Madame Danglars received a most flattering epistle from the count, in which he entreated her to receive back her favorite “dappled grays,” protesting that he could not endure the idea of making his entry into the Parisian world of fashion with the knowledge that his splendid equipage had been obtained at the price of a lovely woman’s regrets. The horses were sent back wearing the same harness she had seen on them in the morning; only, by the count’s orders, in the centre of each rosette that adorned either side of their heads, had been fastened a large diamond.\n\nTo Danglars Monte Cristo also wrote, requesting him to excuse the whimsical gift of a capricious millionaire, and to beg the baroness to pardon the Eastern fashion adopted in the return of the horses.\n\nDuring the evening, Monte Cristo quitted Paris for Auteuil, accompanied by Ali. The following day, about three o’clock, a single blow struck on the gong summoned Ali to the presence of the count.\n\n“Ali,” observed his master, as the Nubian entered the chamber, “you have frequently explained to me how more than commonly skilful you are in throwing the lasso, have you not?”\n\nAli drew himself up proudly, and then returned a sign in the affirmative.\n\n“I thought I did not mistake. With your lasso you could stop an ox?”\n\nAgain Ali repeated his affirmative gesture.\n\n“Or a tiger?”\n\nAli bowed his head in token of assent.\n\n“A lion even?”\n\nAli sprung forwards, imitating the action of one throwing the lasso, then of a strangled lion.\n\n“I understand,” said Monte Cristo; “you wish to tell me you have hunted the lion?”\n\nAli smiled with triumphant pride as he signified that he had indeed both chased and captured many lions.\n\n“But do you believe you could arrest the progress of two horses rushing forwards with ungovernable fury?”\n\nThe Nubian smiled.\n\n“It is well,” said Monte Cristo. “Then listen to me. Ere long a carriage will dash past here, drawn by the pair of dappled gray horses you saw me with yesterday; now, at the risk of your own life, you must manage to stop those horses before my door.”\n\nAli descended to the street, and marked a straight line on the pavement immediately at the entrance of the house, and then pointed out the line he had traced to the count, who was watching him. The count patted him gently on the shoulder, his usual mode of praising Ali, who, pleased and gratified with the commission assigned him, walked calmly towards a projecting stone forming the angle of the street and house, and, seating himself thereon, began to smoke his chibouque, while Monte Cristo re-entered his dwelling, perfectly assured of the success of his plan.\n\nStill, as five o’clock approached, and the carriage was momentarily expected by the count, the indication of more than common impatience and uneasiness might be observed in his manner. He stationed himself in a room commanding a view of the street, pacing the chamber with restless steps, stopping merely to listen from time to time for the sound of approaching wheels, then to cast an anxious glance on Ali; but the regularity with which the Nubian puffed forth the smoke of his chibouque proved that he at least was wholly absorbed in the enjoyment of his favorite occupation.\n\nSuddenly a distant sound of rapidly advancing wheels was heard, and almost immediately a carriage appeared, drawn by a pair of wild, ungovernable horses, while the terrified coachman strove in vain to restrain their furious speed.\n\nIn the vehicle was a young woman and a child of about seven or eight clasped in each other’s arms. Terror seemed to have deprived them even of the power of uttering a cry. The carriage creaked and rattled as it flew over the rough stones, and the slightest obstacle under the wheels would have caused disaster; but it kept on in the middle of the road, and those who saw it pass uttered cries of terror.\n\nAli suddenly cast aside his chibouque, drew the lasso from his pocket, threw it so skilfully as to catch the forelegs of the near horse in its triple fold, and suffered himself to be dragged on for a few steps by the violence of the shock, then the animal fell over on the pole, which snapped, and therefore prevented the other horse from pursuing its way. Gladly availing himself of this opportunity, the coachman leaped from his box; but Ali had promptly seized the nostrils of the second horse, and held them in his iron grasp, till the beast, snorting with pain, sunk beside his companion.\n\nAll this was achieved in much less time than is occupied in the recital. The brief space had, however, been sufficient for a man, followed by a number of servants, to rush from the house before which the accident had occurred, and, as the coachman opened the door of the carriage, to take from it a lady who was convulsively grasping the cushions with one hand, while with the other she pressed to her bosom the young boy, who had lost consciousness. Monte Cristo carried them both to the salon, and deposited them on a sofa.\n\n“Compose yourself, madame,” said he; “all danger is over.” The woman looked up at these words, and, with a glance far more expressive than any entreaties could have been, pointed to her child, who still continued insensible. “I understand the nature of your alarms, madame,” said the count, carefully examining the child, “but I assure you there is not the slightest occasion for uneasiness; your little charge has not received the least injury; his insensibility is merely the effects of terror, and will soon pass.”\n\n“Are you quite sure you do not say so to tranquillize my fears? See how deadly pale he is! My child, my darling Edward; speak to your mother—open your dear eyes and look on me once again! Oh, sir, in pity send for a physician; my whole fortune shall not be thought too much for the recovery of my boy.”\n\nWith a calm smile and a gentle wave of the hand, Monte Cristo signed to the distracted mother to lay aside her apprehensions; then, opening a casket that stood near, he drew forth a phial of Bohemian glass incrusted with gold, containing a liquid of the color of blood, of which he let fall a single drop on the child’s lips. Scarcely had it reached them, ere the boy, though still pale as marble, opened his eyes, and eagerly gazed around him. At this, the delight of the mother was almost frantic.\n\n“Where am I?” exclaimed she; “and to whom am I indebted for so happy a termination to my late dreadful alarm?”\n\n“Madame,” answered the count, “you are under the roof of one who esteems himself most fortunate in having been able to save you from a further continuance of your sufferings.”\n\n“My wretched curiosity has brought all this about,” pursued the lady. “All Paris rung with the praises of Madame Danglars’ beautiful horses, and I had the folly to desire to know whether they really merited the high praise given to them.”\n\n“Is it possible,” exclaimed the count with well-feigned astonishment, “that these horses belong to the baroness?”\n\n“They do, indeed. May I inquire if you are acquainted with Madame Danglars?”\n\n20351m\n“I have that honor; and my happiness at your escape from the danger that threatened you is redoubled by the consciousness that I have been the unwilling and the unintentional cause of all the peril you have incurred. I yesterday purchased these horses of the baron; but as the baroness evidently regretted parting with them, I ventured to send them back to her, with a request that she would gratify me by accepting them from my hands.”\n\n“You are, then, doubtless, the Count of Monte Cristo, of whom Hermine has talked to me so much?”\n\n“You have rightly guessed, madame,” replied the count.\n\n“And I am Madame Héloïse de Villefort.”\n\nThe count bowed with the air of a person who hears a name for the first time.\n\n“How grateful will M. de Villefort be for all your goodness; how thankfully will he acknowledge that to you alone he owes the existence of his wife and child! Most certainly, but for the prompt assistance of your intrepid servant, this dear child and myself must both have perished.”\n\n“Indeed, I still shudder at the fearful danger you were placed in.”\n\n“I trust you will allow me to recompense worthily the devotion of your man.”\n\n“I beseech you, madame,” replied Monte Cristo “not to spoil Ali, either by too great praise or rewards. I cannot allow him to acquire the habit of expecting to be recompensed for every trifling service he may render. Ali is my slave, and in saving your life he was but discharging his duty to me.”\n\n“Nay,” interposed Madame de Villefort, on whom the authoritative style adopted by the count made a deep impression, “nay, but consider that to preserve my life he has risked his own.”\n\n“His life, madame, belongs not to him; it is mine, in return for my having myself saved him from death.”\n\nMadame de Villefort made no further reply; her mind was utterly absorbed in the contemplation of the person who, from the first instant she saw him, had made so powerful an impression on her.\n\nDuring the evident preoccupation of Madame de Villefort, Monte Cristo scrutinized the features and appearance of the boy she kept folded in her arms, lavishing on him the most tender endearments. The child was small for his age, and unnaturally pale. A mass of straight black hair, defying all attempts to train or curl it, fell over his projecting forehead, and hung down to his shoulders, giving increased vivacity to eyes already sparkling with a youthful love of mischief and fondness for every forbidden enjoyment. His mouth was large, and the lips, which had not yet regained their color, were particularly thin; in fact, the deep and crafty look, giving a predominant expression to the child’s face, belonged rather to a boy of twelve or fourteen than to one so young. His first movement was to free himself by a violent push from the encircling arms of his mother, and to rush forward to the casket from whence the count had taken the phial of elixir; then, without asking permission of anyone, he proceeded, in all the wilfulness of a spoiled child unaccustomed to restrain either whims or caprices, to pull the corks out of all the bottles.\n\n“Touch nothing, my little friend,” cried the count eagerly; “some of those liquids are not only dangerous to taste, but even to inhale.”\n\nMadame de Villefort became very pale, and, seizing her son’s arm, drew him anxiously toward her; but, once satisfied of his safety, she also cast a brief but expressive glance on the casket, which was not lost upon the count. At this moment Ali entered. At sight of him Madame de Villefort uttered an expression of pleasure, and, holding the child still closer towards her, she said:\n\n“Edward, dearest, do you see that good man? He has shown very great courage and resolution, for he exposed his own life to stop the horses that were running away with us, and would certainly have dashed the carriage to pieces. Thank him, then, my child, in your very best manner; for, had he not come to our aid, neither you nor I would have been alive to speak our thanks.”\n\nThe child stuck out his lips and turned away his head in a disdainful manner, saying, “He’s too ugly.”\n\n20353m\nThe count smiled as if the child bade fair to realize his hopes, while Madame de Villefort reprimanded her son with a gentleness and moderation very far from conveying the least idea of a fault having been committed.\n\n“This lady,” said the Count, speaking to Ali in the Arabic language, “is desirous that her son should thank you for saving both their lives; but the boy refuses, saying you are too ugly.”\n\nAli turned his intelligent countenance towards the boy, on whom he gazed without any apparent emotion; but the spasmodic working of the nostrils showed to the practiced eye of Monte Cristo that the Arab had been wounded to the heart.\n\n“Will you permit me to inquire,” said Madame de Villefort, as she arose to take her leave, “whether you usually reside here?”\n\n“No, I do not,” replied Monte Cristo; “it is a small place I have purchased quite lately. My place of abode is No. 30, Avenue des Champs-Élysées; but I see you have quite recovered from your fright, and are, no doubt, desirous of returning home. Anticipating your wishes, I have desired the same horses you came with to be put to one of my carriages, and Ali, he whom you think so very ugly,” continued he, addressing the boy with a smiling air, “will have the honor of driving you home, while your coachman remains here to attend to the necessary repairs of your calash. As soon as that important business is concluded, I will have a pair of my own horses harnessed to convey it direct to Madame Danglars.”\n\n“I dare not return with those dreadful horses,” said Madame de Villefort.\n\n“You will see,” replied Monte Cristo, “that they will be as different as possible in the hands of Ali. With him they will be gentle and docile as lambs.”\n\nAli had, indeed, given proof of this; for, approaching the animals, who had been got upon their legs with considerable difficulty, he rubbed their foreheads and nostrils with a sponge soaked in aromatic vinegar, and wiped off the sweat and foam that covered their mouths. Then, commencing a loud whistling noise, he rubbed them well all over their bodies for several minutes; then, undisturbed by the noisy crowd collected round the broken carriage, Ali quietly harnessed the pacified animals to the count’s chariot, took the reins in his hands, and mounted the box, when to the utter astonishment of those who had witnessed the ungovernable spirit and maddened speed of the same horses, he was actually compelled to apply his whip in no very gentle manner before he could induce them to start; and even then all that could be obtained from the celebrated “dappled grays,” now changed into a couple of dull, sluggish, stupid brutes, was a slow, pottering pace, kept up with so much difficulty that Madame de Villefort was more than two hours returning to her residence in the Faubourg Saint-Honoré.\n20355m\n\nScarcely had the first congratulations upon her marvellous escape been gone through when she wrote the following letter to Madame Danglars:—\n\n“Dear Hermine,—I have just had a wonderful escape from the most imminent danger, and I owe my safety to the very Count of Monte Cristo we were talking about yesterday, but whom I little expected to see today. I remember how unmercifully I laughed at what I considered your eulogistic and exaggerated praises of him; but I have now ample cause to admit that your enthusiastic description of this wonderful man fell far short of his merits. Your horses got as far as Ranelagh, when they darted forward like mad things, and galloped away at so fearful a rate, that there seemed no other prospect for myself and my poor Edward but that of being dashed to pieces against the first object that impeded their progress, when a strange-looking man,—an Arab, a negro, or a Nubian, at least a black of some nation or other—at a signal from the count, whose domestic he is, suddenly seized and stopped the infuriated animals, even at the risk of being trampled to death himself; and certainly he must have had a most wonderful escape. The count then hastened to us, and took us into his house, where he speedily recalled my poor Edward to life. He sent us home in his own carriage. Yours will be returned to you tomorrow. You will find your horses in bad condition, from the results of this accident; they seem thoroughly stupefied, as if sulky and vexed at having been conquered by man. The count, however, has commissioned me to assure you that two or three days’ rest, with plenty of barley for their sole food during that time, will bring them back to as fine, that is as terrifying, a condition as they were in yesterday.\n20356m\n\nAdieu! I cannot return you many thanks for the drive of yesterday; but, after all, I ought not to blame you for the misconduct of your horses, more especially as it procured me the pleasure of an introduction to the Count of Monte Cristo,—and certainly that illustrious personage, apart from the millions he is said to be so very anxious to dispose of, seemed to me one of those curiously interesting problems I, for one, delight in solving at any risk, even if it were to necessitate another drive to the Bois behind your horses.\n\nEdward endured the accident with miraculous courage—he did not utter a single cry, but fell lifeless into my arms; nor did a tear fall from his eyes after it was over. I doubt not you will consider these praises the result of blind maternal affection, but there is a soul of iron in that delicate, fragile body. Valentine sends many affectionate remembrances to your dear Eugénie. I embrace you with all my heart.\n\nHéloïse de Villefort.\n\nP.S.—Do pray contrive some means for me to meet the Count of Monte Cristo at your house. I must and will see him again. I have just made M. de Villefort promise to call on him, and I hope the visit will be returned.\n\nThat night the adventure at Auteuil was talked of everywhere. Albert related it to his mother; Château-Renaud recounted it at the Jockey Club, and Debray detailed it at length in the salons of the minister; even Beauchamp accorded twenty lines in his journal to the relation of the count’s courage and gallantry, thereby celebrating him as the greatest hero of the day in the eyes of all the feminine members of the aristocracy.\n\nVast was the crowd of visitors and inquiring friends who left their names at the residence of Madame de Villefort, with the design of renewing their visit at the right moment, of hearing from her lips all the interesting circumstances of this most romantic adventure.\n\nAs for M. de Villefort, he fulfilled the predictions of Héloïse to the letter,—donned his dress suit, drew on a pair of white gloves, ordered the servants to attend the carriage dressed in their full livery, and drove that same night to No. 30 in the Avenue des Champs-Élysées.\n30009m\n\n# Chapter 48. Ideology\n\n30011m\nIf the Count of Monte Cristo had been for a long time familiar with the ways of Parisian society, he would have appreciated better the significance of the step which M. de Villefort had taken. Standing well at court, whether the king regnant was of the older or younger branch, whether the government was doctrinaire liberal, or conservative; looked upon by all as a man of talent, since those who have never experienced a political check are generally so regarded; hated by many, but warmly supported by others, without being really liked by anybody, M. de Villefort held a high position in the magistracy, and maintained his eminence like a Harlay or a Molé. His drawing-room, under the regenerating influence of a young wife and a daughter by his first marriage, scarcely eighteen, was still one of the well-regulated Paris salons where the worship of traditional customs and the observance of rigid etiquette were carefully maintained. A freezing politeness, a strict fidelity to government principles, a profound contempt for theories and theorists, a deep-seated hatred of ideality,—these were the elements of private and public life displayed by M. de Villefort.\n\n30023m\nM. de Villefort was not only a magistrate, he was almost a diplomatist. His relations with the former court, of which he always spoke with dignity and respect, made him respected by the new one, and he knew so many things, that not only was he always carefully considered, but sometimes consulted. Perhaps this would not have been so had it been possible to get rid of M. de Villefort; but, like the feudal barons who rebelled against their sovereign, he dwelt in an impregnable fortress. This fortress was his post as king’s attorney, all the advantages of which he exploited with marvellous skill, and which he would not have resigned but to be made deputy, and thus to replace neutrality by opposition.\n\nOrdinarily M. de Villefort made and returned very few visits. His wife visited for him, and this was the received thing in the world, where the weighty and multifarious occupations of the magistrate were accepted as an excuse for what was really only calculated pride, a manifestation of professed superiority—in fact, the application of the axiom, _Pretend to think well of yourself, and the world will think well of you_, an axiom a hundred times more useful in society nowadays than that of the Greeks, “Know thyself,” a knowledge for which, in our days, we have substituted the less difficult and more advantageous science of _knowing others_.\n\nTo his friends M. de Villefort was a powerful protector; to his enemies, he was a silent, but bitter opponent; for those who were neither the one nor the other, he was a statue of the law-made man. He had a haughty bearing, a look either steady and impenetrable or insolently piercing and inquisitorial. Four successive revolutions had built and cemented the pedestal upon which his fortune was based.\n\nM. de Villefort had the reputation of being the least curious and the least wearisome man in France. He gave a ball every year, at which he appeared for a quarter of an hour only,—that is to say, five-and-forty minutes less than the king is visible at his balls. He was never seen at the theatres, at concerts, or in any place of public resort. Occasionally, but seldom, he played at whist, and then care was taken to select partners worthy of him—sometimes they were ambassadors, sometimes archbishops, or sometimes a prince, or a president, or some dowager duchess.\n\nSuch was the man whose carriage had just now stopped before the Count of Monte Cristo’s door. The valet de chambre announced M. de Villefort at the moment when the count, leaning over a large table, was tracing on a map the route from St. Petersburg to China.\n\nThe procureur entered with the same grave and measured step he would have employed in entering a court of justice. He was the same man, or rather the development of the same man, whom we have heretofore seen as assistant attorney at Marseilles. Nature, according to her way, had made no deviation in the path he had marked out for himself. From being slender he had now become meagre; once pale, he was now yellow; his deep-set eyes were hollow, and the gold spectacles shielding his eyes seemed to be an integral portion of his face. He dressed entirely in black, with the exception of his white tie, and his funeral appearance was only mitigated by the slight line of red ribbon which passed almost imperceptibly through his button-hole, and appeared like a streak of blood traced with a delicate brush.\n\nAlthough master of himself, Monte Cristo, scrutinized with irrepressible curiosity the magistrate whose salute he returned, and who, distrustful by habit, and especially incredulous as to social prodigies, was much more despised to look upon “the noble stranger,” as Monte Cristo was already called, as an adventurer in search of new fields, or an escaped criminal, rather than as a prince of the Holy See, or a sultan of the _Thousand and One Nights_.\n\n“Sir,” said Villefort, in the squeaky tone assumed by magistrates in their oratorical periods, and of which they cannot, or will not, divest themselves in society, “sir, the signal service which you yesterday rendered to my wife and son has made it a duty for me to offer you my thanks. I have come, therefore, to discharge this duty, and to express to you my overwhelming gratitude.”\n\nAnd as he said this, the “eye severe” of the magistrate had lost nothing of its habitual arrogance. He spoke in a voice of the procureur-general, with the rigid inflexibility of neck and shoulders which caused his flatterers to say (as we have before observed) that he was the living statue of the law.\n\n“Monsieur,” replied the count, with a chilling air, “I am very happy to have been the means of preserving a son to his mother, for they say that the sentiment of maternity is the most holy of all; and the good fortune which occurred to me, monsieur, might have enabled you to dispense with a duty which, in its discharge, confers an undoubtedly great honor; for I am aware that M. de Villefort is not usually lavish of the favor which he now bestows on me,—a favor which, however estimable, is unequal to the satisfaction which I have in my own consciousness.”\n\nVillefort, astonished at this reply, which he by no means expected, started like a soldier who feels the blow levelled at him over the armor he wears, and a curl of his disdainful lip indicated that from that moment he noted in the tablets of his brain that the Count of Monte Cristo was by no means a highly bred gentleman.\n\nHe glanced around, in order to seize on something on which the conversation might turn, and seemed to fall easily on a topic. He saw the map which Monte Cristo had been examining when he entered, and said:\n\n“You seem geographically engaged, sir? It is a rich study for you, who, as I learn, have seen as many lands as are delineated on this map.”\n\n“Yes, sir,” replied the count; “I have sought to make of the human race, taken in the mass, what you practice every day on individuals—a physiological study. I have believed it was much easier to descend from the whole to a part than to ascend from a part to the whole. It is an algebraic axiom, which makes us proceed from a known to an unknown quantity, and not from an unknown to a known; but sit down, sir, I beg of you.”\n\nMonte Cristo pointed to a chair, which the procureur was obliged to take the trouble to move forwards himself, while the count merely fell back into his own, on which he had been kneeling when M. Villefort entered. Thus the count was halfway turned towards his visitor, having his back towards the window, his elbow resting on the geographical chart which furnished the theme of conversation for the moment,—a conversation which assumed, as in the case of the interviews with Danglars and Morcerf, a turn analogous to the persons, if not to the situation.\n\n“Ah, you philosophize,” replied Villefort, after a moment’s silence, during which, like a wrestler who encounters a powerful opponent, he took breath; “well, sir, really, if, like you, I had nothing else to do, I should seek a more amusing occupation.”\n\n“Why, in truth, sir,” was Monte Cristo’s reply, “man is but an ugly caterpillar for him who studies him through a solar microscope; but you said, I think, that I had nothing else to do. Now, really, let me ask, sir, have you?—do you believe you have anything to do? or to speak in plain terms, do you really think that what you do deserves being called anything?”\n\nVillefort’s astonishment redoubled at this second thrust so forcibly made by his strange adversary. It was a long time since the magistrate had heard a paradox so strong, or rather, to say the truth more exactly, it was the first time he had ever heard of it. The procureur exerted himself to reply.\n\n“Sir,” he responded, “you are a stranger, and I believe you say yourself that a portion of your life has been spent in Oriental countries, so you are not aware how human justice, so expeditious in barbarous countries, takes with us a prudent and well-studied course.”\n\n“Oh, yes—yes, I do, sir; it is the _pede claudo_ of the ancients. I know all that, for it is with the justice of all countries especially that I have occupied myself—it is with the criminal procedure of all nations that I have compared natural justice, and I must say, sir, that it is the law of primitive nations, that is, the law of retaliation, that I have most frequently found to be according to the law of God.”\n\n“If this law were adopted, sir,” said the procureur, “it would greatly simplify our legal codes, and in that case the magistrates would not (as you just observed) have much to do.”\n\n“It may, perhaps, come to this in time,” observed Monte Cristo; “you know that human inventions march from the complex to the simple, and simplicity is always perfection.”\n\n“In the meanwhile,” continued the magistrate, “our codes are in full force, with all their contradictory enactments derived from Gallic customs, Roman laws, and Frank usages; the knowledge of all which, you will agree, is not to be acquired without extended labor; it needs tedious study to acquire this knowledge, and, when acquired, a strong power of brain to retain it.”\n\n“I agree with you entirely, sir; but all that even you know with respect to the French code, I know, not only in reference to that code, but as regards the codes of all nations. The English, Turkish, Japanese, Hindu laws, are as familiar to me as the French laws, and thus I was right, when I said to you, that relatively (you know that everything is relative, sir)—that relatively to what I have done, you have very little to do; but that relatively to all I have learned, you have yet a great deal to learn.”\n\n“But with what motive have you learned all this?” inquired Villefort, in astonishment.\n\nMonte Cristo smiled.\n\n“Really, sir,” he observed, “I see that in spite of the reputation which you have acquired as a superior man, you look at everything from the material and vulgar view of society, beginning with man, and ending with man—that is to say, in the most restricted, most narrow view which it is possible for human understanding to embrace.”\n\n“Pray, sir, explain yourself,” said Villefort, more and more astonished, “I really do—not—understand you—perfectly.”\n\n“I say, sir, that with the eyes fixed on the social organization of nations, you see only the springs of the machine, and lose sight of the sublime workman who makes them act; I say that you do not recognize before you and around you any but those office-holders whose commissions have been signed by a minister or king; and that the men whom God has put above those office-holders, ministers, and kings, by giving them a mission to follow out, instead of a post to fill—I say that they escape your narrow, limited field of observation. It is thus that human weakness fails, from its debilitated and imperfect organs. Tobias took the angel who restored him to light for an ordinary young man. The nations took Attila, who was doomed to destroy them, for a conqueror similar to other conquerors, and it was necessary for both to reveal their missions, that they might be known and acknowledged; one was compelled to say, ‘I am the angel of the Lord’; and the other, ‘I am the hammer of God,’ in order that the divine essence in both might be revealed.”\n\n“Then,” said Villefort, more and more amazed, and really supposing he was speaking to a mystic or a madman, “you consider yourself as one of those extraordinary beings whom you have mentioned?”\n\n“And why not?” said Monte Cristo coldly.\n\n“Your pardon, sir,” replied Villefort, quite astounded, “but you will excuse me if, when I presented myself to you, I was unaware that I should meet with a person whose knowledge and understanding so far surpass the usual knowledge and understanding of men. It is not usual with us corrupted wretches of civilization to find gentlemen like yourself, possessors, as you are, of immense fortune—at least, so it is said—and I beg you to observe that I do not inquire, I merely repeat;—it is not usual, I say, for such privileged and wealthy beings to waste their time in speculations on the state of society, in philosophical reveries, intended at best to console those whom fate has disinherited from the goods of this world.”\n\n“Really, sir,” retorted the count, “have you attained the eminent situation in which you are, without having admitted, or even without having met with exceptions? and do you never use your eyes, which must have acquired so much _finesse_ and certainty, to divine, at a glance, the kind of man by whom you are confronted? Should not a magistrate be not merely the best administrator of the law, but the most crafty expounder of the chicanery of his profession, a steel probe to search hearts, a touchstone to try the gold which in each soul is mingled with more or less of alloy?”\n\n“Sir,” said Villefort, “upon my word, you overcome me. I really never heard a person speak as you do.”\n\n“Because you remain eternally encircled in a round of general conditions, and have never dared to raise your wings into those upper spheres which God has peopled with invisible or exceptional beings.”\n\n“And you allow then, sir, that spheres exist, and that these marked and invisible beings mingle amongst us?”\n\n“Why should they not? Can you see the air you breathe, and yet without which you could not for a moment exist?”\n\n“Then we do not see those beings to whom you allude?”\n\n“Yes, we do; you see them whenever God pleases to allow them to assume a material form. You touch them, come in contact with them, speak to them, and they reply to you.”\n\n“Ah,” said Villefort, smiling, “I confess I should like to be warned when one of these beings is in contact with me.”\n\n“You have been served as you desire, monsieur, for you were warned just now, and I now again warn you.”\n\n“Then you yourself are one of these marked beings?”\n\n“Yes, monsieur, I believe so; for until now, no man has found himself in a position similar to mine. The dominions of kings are limited either by mountains or rivers, or a change of manners, or an alteration of language. My kingdom is bounded only by the world, for I am not an Italian, or a Frenchman, or a Hindu, or an American, or a Spaniard—I am a cosmopolite. No country can say it saw my birth. God alone knows what country will see me die. I adopt all customs, speak all languages. You believe me to be a Frenchman, for I speak French with the same facility and purity as yourself. Well, Ali, my Nubian, believes me to be an Arab; Bertuccio, my steward, takes me for a Roman; Haydée, my slave, thinks me a Greek. You may, therefore, comprehend, that being of no country, asking no protection from any government, acknowledging no man as my brother, not one of the scruples that arrest the powerful, or the obstacles which paralyze the weak, paralyzes or arrests me. I have only two adversaries—I will not say two conquerors, for with perseverance I subdue even them,—they are time and distance. There is a third, and the most terrible—that is my condition as a mortal being. This alone can stop me in my onward career, before I have attained the goal at which I aim, for all the rest I have reduced to mathematical terms. What men call the chances of fate—namely, ruin, change, circumstances—I have fully anticipated, and if any of these should overtake me, yet it will not overwhelm me. Unless I die, I shall always be what I am, and therefore it is that I utter the things you have never heard, even from the mouths of kings—for kings have need, and other persons have fear of you. For who is there who does not say to himself, in a society as incongruously organized as ours, ‘Perhaps some day I shall have to do with the king’s attorney’?”\n\n“But can you not say that, sir? The moment you become an inhabitant of France, you are naturally subjected to the French law.”\n\n“I know it sir,” replied Monte Cristo; “but when I visit a country I begin to study, by all the means which are available, the men from whom I may have anything to hope or to fear, till I know them as well as, perhaps better than, they know themselves. It follows from this, that the king’s attorney, be he who he may, with whom I should have to deal, would assuredly be more embarrassed than I should.”\n\n“That is to say,” replied Villefort with hesitation, “that human nature being weak, every man, according to your creed, has committed faults.”\n\n“Faults or crimes,” responded Monte Cristo with a negligent air.\n\n“And that you alone, amongst the men whom you do not recognize as your brothers—for you have said so,” observed Villefort in a tone that faltered somewhat—“you alone are perfect.”\n\n“No, not perfect,” was the count’s reply; “only impenetrable, that’s all. But let us leave off this strain, sir, if the tone of it is displeasing to you; I am no more disturbed by your justice than are you by my second-sight.”\n\n“No, no,—by no means,” said Villefort, who was afraid of seeming to abandon his ground. “No; by your brilliant and almost sublime conversation you have elevated me above the ordinary level; we no longer talk, we rise to dissertation. But you know how the theologians in their collegiate chairs, and philosophers in their controversies, occasionally say cruel truths; let us suppose for the moment that we are theologizing in a social way, or even philosophically, and I will say to you, rude as it may seem, ‘My brother, you sacrifice greatly to pride; you may be above others, but above you there is God.’”\n\n30029m\n“Above us all, sir,” was Monte Cristo’s response, in a tone and with an emphasis so deep that Villefort involuntarily shuddered. “I have my pride for men—serpents always ready to threaten everyone who would pass without crushing them under foot. But I lay aside that pride before God, who has taken me from nothing to make me what I am.”\n\n“Then, count, I admire you,” said Villefort, who, for the first time in this strange conversation, used the aristocratic form to the unknown personage, whom, until now, he had only called monsieur. “Yes, and I say to you, if you are really strong, really superior, really pious, or impenetrable, which you were right in saying amounts to the same thing—then be proud, sir, for that is the characteristic of predominance. Yet you have unquestionably some ambition.”\n\n“I have, sir.”\n\n“And what may it be?”\n\n“I too, as happens to every man once in his life, have been taken by Satan into the highest mountain in the earth, and when there he showed me all the kingdoms of the world, and as he said before, so said he to me, ‘Child of earth, what wouldst thou have to make thee adore me?’ I reflected long, for a gnawing ambition had long preyed upon me, and then I replied, ‘Listen,—I have always heard of Providence, and yet I have never seen him, or anything that resembles him, or which can make me believe that he exists. I wish to be Providence myself, for I feel that the most beautiful, noblest, most sublime thing in the world, is to recompense and punish.’ Satan bowed his head, and groaned. ‘You mistake,’ he said, ‘Providence does exist, only you have never seen him, because the child of God is as invisible as the parent. You have seen nothing that resembles him, because he works by secret springs, and moves by hidden ways. All I can do for you is to make you one of the agents of that Providence.’ The bargain was concluded. I may sacrifice my soul, but what matters it?” added Monte Cristo. “If the thing were to do again, I would again do it.”\n\nVillefort looked at Monte Cristo with extreme amazement.\n\n“Count,” he inquired, “have you any relations?”\n\n“No, sir, I am alone in the world.”\n\n“So much the worse.”\n\n“Why?” asked Monte Cristo.\n\n“Because then you might witness a spectacle calculated to break down your pride. You say you fear nothing but death?”\n\n“I did not say that I feared it; I only said that death alone could check the execution of my plans.”\n\n“And old age?”\n\n“My end will be achieved before I grow old.”\n\n“And madness?”\n\n“I have been nearly mad; and you know the axiom,—_non bis in idem_. It is an axiom of criminal law, and, consequently, you understand its full application.”\n30031m\n\n“Sir,” continued Villefort, “there is something to fear besides death, old age, and madness. For instance, there is apoplexy—that lightning-stroke which strikes but does not destroy you, and yet which brings everything to an end. You are still yourself as now, and yet you are yourself no longer; you who, like Ariel, verge on the angelic, are but an inert mass, which, like Caliban, verges on the brutal; and this is called in human tongues, as I tell you, neither more nor less than apoplexy. Come, if so you will, count, and continue this conversation at my house, any day you may be willing to see an adversary capable of understanding and anxious to refute you, and I will show you my father, M. Noirtier de Villefort, one of the most fiery Jacobins of the French Revolution; that is to say, he had the most remarkable audacity, seconded by a most powerful organization—a man who has not, perhaps, like yourself seen all the kingdoms of the earth, but who has helped to overturn one of the greatest; in fact, a man who believed himself, like you, one of the envoys, not of God, but of a supreme being; not of Providence, but of fate. Well, sir, the rupture of a blood-vessel on the lobe of the brain has destroyed all this, not in a day, not in an hour, but in a second. M. Noirtier, who, on the previous night, was the old Jacobin, the old senator, the old Carbonaro, laughing at the guillotine, the cannon, and the dagger—M. Noirtier, playing with revolutions—M. Noirtier, for whom France was a vast chess-board, from which pawns, rooks, knights, and queens were to disappear, so that the king was checkmated—M. Noirtier, the redoubtable, was the next morning _poor M. Noirtier_, the helpless old man, at the tender mercies of the weakest creature in the household, that is, his grandchild, Valentine; a dumb and frozen carcass, in fact, living painlessly on, that time may be given for his frame to decompose without his consciousness of its decay.”\n\n“Alas, sir,” said Monte Cristo “this spectacle is neither strange to my eye nor my thought. I am something of a physician, and have, like my fellows, sought more than once for the soul in living and in dead matter; yet, like Providence, it has remained invisible to my eyes, although present to my heart. A hundred writers since Socrates, Seneca, St. Augustine, and Gall, have made, in verse and prose, the comparison you have made, and yet I can well understand that a father’s sufferings may effect great changes in the mind of a son. I will call on you, sir, since you bid me contemplate, for the advantage of my pride, this terrible spectacle, which must have been so great a source of sorrow to your family.”\n\n“It would have been so unquestionably, had not God given me so large a compensation. In contrast with the old man, who is dragging his way to the tomb, are two children just entering into life—Valentine, the daughter by my first wife—Mademoiselle Renée de Saint-Méran—and Edward, the boy whose life you have this day saved.”\n\n“And what is your deduction from this compensation, sir?” inquired Monte Cristo.\n\n“My deduction is,” replied Villefort, “that my father, led away by his passions, has committed some fault unknown to human justice, but marked by the justice of God. That God, desirous in his mercy to punish but one person, has visited this justice on him alone.”\n\nMonte Cristo with a smile on his lips, uttered in the depths of his soul a groan which would have made Villefort fly had he but heard it.\n\n“Adieu, sir,” said the magistrate, who had risen from his seat; “I leave you, bearing a remembrance of you—a remembrance of esteem, which I hope will not be disagreeable to you when you know me better; for I am not a man to bore my friends, as you will learn. Besides, you have made an eternal friend of Madame de Villefort.”\n\nThe count bowed, and contented himself with seeing Villefort to the door of his cabinet, the procureur being escorted to his carriage by two footmen, who, on a signal from their master, followed him with every mark of attention. When he had gone, Monte Cristo breathed a profound sigh, and said:\n\n“Enough of this poison, let me now seek the antidote.”\n\nThen sounding his bell, he said to Ali, who entered:\n\n“I am going to madame’s chamber—have the carriage ready at one o’clock.”\n\n# Chapter 49. Haydée\n\nIt will be recollected that the new, or rather old, acquaintances of the Count of Monte Cristo, residing in the Rue Meslay, were no other than Maximilian, Julie, and Emmanuel.\n\nThe very anticipations of delight to be enjoyed in his forthcoming visits—the bright, pure gleam of heavenly happiness it diffused over the almost deadly warfare in which he had voluntarily engaged, illumined his whole countenance with a look of ineffable joy and calmness, as, immediately after Villefort’s departure, his thoughts flew back to the cheering prospect before him, of tasting, at least, a brief respite from the fierce and stormy passions of his mind. Even Ali, who had hastened to obey the Count’s summons, went forth from his master’s presence in charmed amazement at the unusual animation and pleasure depicted on features ordinarily so stern and cold; while, as though dreading to put to flight the agreeable ideas hovering over his patron’s meditations, whatever they were, the faithful Nubian walked on tiptoe towards the door, holding his breath, lest its faintest sound should dissipate his master’s happy reverie.\n\nIt was noon, and Monte Cristo had set apart one hour to be passed in the apartments of Haydée, as though his oppressed spirit could not all at once admit the feeling of pure and unmixed joy, but required a gradual succession of calm and gentle emotions to prepare his mind to receive full and perfect happiness, in the same manner as ordinary natures demand to be inured by degrees to the reception of strong or violent sensations.\n\nThe young Greek, as we have already said, occupied apartments wholly unconnected with those of the count. The rooms had been fitted up in strict accordance with Oriental ideas; the floors were covered with the richest carpets Turkey could produce; the walls hung with brocaded silk of the most magnificent designs and texture; while around each chamber luxurious divans were placed, with piles of soft and yielding cushions, that needed only to be arranged at the pleasure or convenience of such as sought repose.\n\nHaydée had three French maids, and one who was a Greek. The first three remained constantly in a small waiting-room, ready to obey the summons of a small golden bell, or to receive the orders of the Romaic slave, who knew just enough French to be able to transmit her mistress’s wishes to the three other waiting-women; the latter had received most peremptory instructions from Monte Cristo to treat Haydée with all the deference they would observe to a queen.\n\nThe young girl herself generally passed her time in the chamber at the farther end of her apartments. This was a sort of boudoir, circular, and lighted only from the roof, which consisted of rose-colored glass. Haydée was reclining upon soft downy cushions, covered with blue satin spotted with silver; her head, supported by one of her exquisitely moulded arms, rested on the divan immediately behind her, while the other was employed in adjusting to her lips the coral tube of a rich narghile, through whose flexible pipe she drew the smoke fragrant by its passage through perfumed water. Her attitude, though perfectly natural for an Eastern woman would, in a European, have been deemed too full of coquettish straining after effect.\n\nHer dress, which was that of the women of Epirus, consisted of a pair of white satin trousers, embroidered with pink roses, displaying feet so exquisitely formed and so delicately fair, that they might well have been taken for Parian marble, had not the eye been undeceived by their movements as they constantly shifted in and out of a pair of little slippers with upturned toes, beautifully ornamented with gold and pearls. She wore a blue and white-striped vest, with long open sleeves, trimmed with silver loops and buttons of pearls, and a sort of bodice, which, closing only from the centre to the waist, exhibited the whole of the ivory throat and upper part of the bosom; it was fastened with three magnificent diamond clasps. The junction of the bodice and drawers was entirely concealed by one of the many-colored scarves, whose brilliant hues and rich silken fringe have rendered them so precious in the eyes of Parisian belles.\n\nTilted on one side of her head she had a small cap of gold-colored silk, embroidered with pearls; while on the other a purple rose mingled its glowing colors with the luxuriant masses of her hair, of which the blackness was so intense that it was tinged with blue.\n\nThe extreme beauty of the countenance, that shone forth in loveliness that mocked the vain attempts of dress to augment it, was peculiarly and purely Grecian; there were the large, dark, melting eyes, the finely formed nose, the coral lips, and pearly teeth, that belonged to her race and country.\n\nAnd, to complete the whole, Haydée was in the very springtide and fulness of youthful charms—she had not yet numbered more than nineteen or twenty summers.\n\nMonte Cristo summoned the Greek attendant, and bade her inquire whether it would be agreeable to her mistress to receive his visit. Haydée’s only reply was to direct her servant by a sign to withdraw the tapestried curtain that hung before the door of her boudoir, the framework of the opening thus made serving as a sort of border to the graceful tableau presented by the young girl’s picturesque attitude and appearance.\n\nAs Monte Cristo approached, she leaned upon the elbow of the arm that held the narghile, and extending to him her other hand, said, with a smile of captivating sweetness, in the sonorous language spoken by the women of Athens and Sparta:\n\n“Why demand permission ere you enter? Are you no longer my master, or have I ceased to be your slave?”\n\nMonte Cristo returned her smile.\n\n“Haydée,” said he, “you well know.”\n\n“Why do you address me so coldly—so distantly?” asked the young Greek. “Have I by any means displeased you? Oh, if so, punish me as you will; but do not—do not speak to me in tones and manner so formal and constrained.”\n\n“Haydée,” replied the count, “you know that you are now in France, and are free.”\n\n“Free to do what?” asked the young girl.\n\n“Free to leave me.”\n\n“Leave you? Why should I leave you?”\n\n“That is not for me to say; but we are now about to mix in society—to visit and be visited.”\n\n“I don’t wish to see anybody but you.”\n\n“And should you see one whom you could prefer, I would not be so unjust——”\n\n“I have never seen anyone I preferred to you, and I have never loved anyone but you and my father.”\n\n“My poor child,” replied Monte Cristo, “that is merely because your father and myself are the only men who have ever talked to you.”\n\n“I don’t want anybody else to talk to me. My father said I was his ‘joy’—you style me your ‘love,’—and both of you have called me ‘my child.’”\n\n“Do you remember your father, Haydée?”\n\nThe young Greek smiled.\n\n“He is here, and here,” said she, touching her eyes and her heart.\n\n“And where am I?” inquired Monte Cristo laughingly.\n\n“You?” cried she, with tones of thrilling tenderness, “you are everywhere!” Monte Cristo took the delicate hand of the young girl in his, and was about to raise it to his lips, when the simple child of nature hastily withdrew it, and presented her cheek.\n\n“You now understand, Haydée,” said the count, “that from this moment you are absolutely free; that here you exercise unlimited sway, and are at liberty to lay aside or continue the costume of your country, as it may suit your inclination. Within this mansion you are absolute mistress of your actions, and may go abroad or remain in your apartments as may seem most agreeable to you. A carriage waits your orders, and Ali and Myrtho will accompany you whithersoever you desire to go. There is but one favor I would entreat of you.”\n\n“Speak.”\n\n“Guard carefully the secret of your birth. Make no allusion to the past; nor upon any occasion be induced to pronounce the names of your illustrious father or ill-fated mother.”\n\n“I have already told you, my lord, that I shall see no one.”\n\n“It is possible, Haydée, that so perfect a seclusion, though conformable with the habits and customs of the East, may not be practicable in Paris. Endeavor, then, to accustom yourself to our manner of living in these northern climes as you did to those of Rome, Florence, Milan, and Madrid; it may be useful to you one of these days, whether you remain here or return to the East.”\n\nThe young girl raised her tearful eyes towards Monte Cristo as she said with touching earnestness, “Whether _we_ return to the East, you mean to say, my lord, do you not?”\n\n“My child,” returned Monte Cristo “you know full well that whenever we part, it will be no fault or wish of mine; the tree forsakes not the flower—the flower falls from the tree.”\n\n“My lord,” replied Haydée, “I never will leave you, for I am sure I could not exist without you.”\n\n“My poor girl, in ten years I shall be old, and you will be still young.”\n\n“My father had a long white beard, but I loved him; he was sixty years old, but to me he was handsomer than all the fine youths I saw.”\n\n“Then tell me, Haydée, do you believe you shall be able to accustom yourself to our present mode of life?”\n\n“Shall I see you?”\n\n“Every day.”\n\n“Then what do you fear, my lord?”\n\n“You might find it dull.”\n\n“No, my lord. In the morning, I shall rejoice in the prospect of your coming, and in the evening dwell with delight on the happiness I have enjoyed in your presence; then too, when alone, I can call forth mighty pictures of the past, see vast horizons bounded only by the towering mountains of Pindus and Olympus. Oh, believe me, that when three great passions, such as sorrow, love, and gratitude fill the heart, _ennui_ can find no place.”\n\n“You are a worthy daughter of Epirus, Haydée, and your charming and poetical ideas prove well your descent from that race of goddesses who claim your country as their birthplace. Depend on my care to see that your youth is not blighted, or suffered to pass away in ungenial solitude; and of this be well assured, that if you love me as a father, I love you as a child.”\n\n“You are wrong, my lord. The love I have for you is very different from the love I had for my father. My father died, but I did not die. If you were to die, I should die too.”\n\nThe count, with a smile of profound tenderness, extended his hand, and she carried it to her lips.\n\nMonte Cristo, thus attuned to the interview he proposed to hold with Morrel and his family, departed, murmuring as he went these lines of Pindar, “Youth is a flower of which love is the fruit; happy is he who, after having watched its silent growth, is permitted to gather and call it his own.” The carriage was prepared according to orders, and stepping lightly into it, the count drove off at his usual rapid pace.\n\n# Chapter 50. The Morrel Family\n\nIn a very few minutes the count reached No. 7 in the Rue Meslay. The house was of white stone, and in a small court before it were two small beds full of beautiful flowers. In the concierge that opened the gate the count recognized Cocles; but as he had but one eye, and that eye had become somewhat dim in the course of nine years, Cocles did not recognize the count.\n\nThe carriages that drove up to the door were compelled to turn, to avoid a fountain that played in a basin of rockwork,—an ornament that had excited the jealousy of the whole quarter, and had gained for the place the appellation of _The Little Versailles_. It is needless to add that there were gold and silver fish in the basin. The house, with kitchens and cellars below, had above the ground floor, two stories and attics. The whole of the property, consisting of an immense workshop, two pavilions at the bottom of the garden, and the garden itself, had been purchased by Emmanuel, who had seen at a glance that he could make of it a profitable speculation. He had reserved the house and half the garden, and building a wall between the garden and the workshops, had let them upon lease with the pavilions at the bottom of the garden. So that for a trifling sum he was as well lodged, and as perfectly shut out from observation, as the inhabitants of the finest mansion in the Faubourg St. Germain.\n\nThe breakfast-room was finished in oak; the salon in mahogany, and the furnishings were of blue velvet; the bedroom was in citronwood and green damask. There was a study for Emmanuel, who never studied, and a music-room for Julie, who never played. The whole of the second story was set apart for Maximilian; it was precisely similar to his sister’s apartments, except that for the breakfast-parlor he had a billiard-room, where he received his friends. He was superintending the grooming of his horse, and smoking his cigar at the entrance of the garden, when the count’s carriage stopped at the gate.\n\nCocles opened the gate, and Baptistin, springing from the box, inquired whether Monsieur and Madame Herbault and Monsieur Maximilian Morrel would see his excellency the Count of Monte Cristo.\n\n“The Count of Monte Cristo?” cried Morrel, throwing away his cigar and hastening to the carriage; “I should think we would see him. Ah, a thousand thanks, count, for not having forgotten your promise.”\n\nAnd the young officer shook the count’s hand so warmly, that Monte Cristo could not be mistaken as to the sincerity of his joy, and he saw that he had been expected with impatience, and was received with pleasure.\n\n“Come, come,” said Maximilian, “I will serve as your guide; such a man as you are ought not to be introduced by a servant. My sister is in the garden plucking the dead roses; my brother is reading his two papers, _la Presse_ and _les Débats_, within six steps of her; for wherever you see Madame Herbault, you have only to look within a circle of four yards and you will find M. Emmanuel, and ‘reciprocally,’ as they say at the Polytechnic School.”\n\nAt the sound of their steps a young woman of twenty to five-and-twenty, dressed in a silk morning gown, and busily engaged in plucking the dead leaves off a noisette rose-tree, raised her head. This was Julie, who had become, as the clerk of the house of Thomson & French had predicted, Madame Emmanuel Herbault. She uttered a cry of surprise at the sight of a stranger, and Maximilian began to laugh.\n\n“Don’t disturb yourself, Julie,” said he. “The count has only been two or three days in Paris, but he already knows what a fashionable woman of the Marais is, and if he does not, you will show him.”\n\n“Ah, monsieur,” returned Julie, “it is treason in my brother to bring you thus, but he never has any regard for his poor sister. Penelon, Penelon!”\n\nAn old man, who was digging busily at one of the beds, stuck his spade in the earth, and approached, cap in hand, striving to conceal a quid of tobacco he had just thrust into his cheek. A few locks of gray mingled with his hair, which was still thick and matted, while his bronzed features and determined glance well suited an old sailor who had braved the heat of the equator and the storms of the tropics.\n\n“I think you hailed me, Mademoiselle Julie?” said he.\n\nPenelon had still preserved the habit of calling his master’s daughter “Mademoiselle Julie,” and had never been able to change the name to Madame Herbault.\n\n“Penelon,” replied Julie, “go and inform M. Emmanuel of this gentleman’s visit, and Maximilian will conduct him to the salon.”\n\nThen, turning to Monte Cristo,—“I hope you will permit me to leave you for a few minutes,” continued she; and without awaiting any reply, disappeared behind a clump of trees, and escaped to the house by a lateral alley.\n30041m\n\n“I am sorry to see,” observed Monte Cristo to Morrel, “that I cause no small disturbance in your house.”\n\n“Look there,” said Maximilian, laughing; “there is her husband changing his jacket for a coat. I assure you, you are well known in the Rue Meslay.”\n\n“Your family appears to be a very happy one,” said the count, as if speaking to himself.\n\n“Oh, yes, I assure you, count, they want nothing that can render them happy; they are young and cheerful, they are tenderly attached to each other, and with twenty-five thousand francs a year they fancy themselves as rich as Rothschild.”\n\n“Five-and-twenty thousand francs is not a large sum, however,” replied Monte Cristo, with a tone so sweet and gentle, that it went to Maximilian’s heart like the voice of a father; “but they will not be content with that. Your brother-in-law is a barrister? a doctor?”\n\n“He was a merchant, monsieur, and had succeeded to the business of my poor father. M. Morrel, at his death, left 500,000 francs, which were divided between my sister and myself, for we were his only children. Her husband, who, when he married her, had no other patrimony than his noble probity, his first-rate ability, and his spotless reputation, wished to possess as much as his wife. He labored and toiled until he had amassed 250,000 francs; six years sufficed to achieve this object. Oh, I assure you, sir, it was a touching spectacle to see these young creatures, destined by their talents for higher stations, toiling together, and through their unwillingness to change any of the customs of their paternal house, taking six years to accomplish what less scrupulous people would have effected in two or three. Marseilles resounded with their well-earned praises. At last, one day, Emmanuel came to his wife, who had just finished making up the accounts.\n\n“‘Julie,’ said he to her, ‘Cocles has just given me the last rouleau of a hundred francs; that completes the 250,000 francs we had fixed as the limits of our gains. Can you content yourself with the small fortune which we shall possess for the future? Listen to me. Our house transacts business to the amount of a million a year, from which we derive an income of 40,000 francs. We can dispose of the business, if we please, in an hour, for I have received a letter from M. Delaunay, in which he offers to purchase the good-will of the house, to unite with his own, for 300,000 francs. Advise me what I had better do.’\n\n“‘Emmanuel,’ returned my sister, ‘the house of Morrel can only be carried on by a Morrel. Is it not worth 300,000 francs to save our father’s name from the chances of evil fortune and failure?’\n\n“‘I thought so,’ replied Emmanuel; ‘but I wished to have your advice.’\n\n“‘This is my counsel:—Our accounts are made up and our bills paid; all we have to do is to stop the issue of any more, and close our office.’\n\n“This was done instantly. It was three o’clock; at a quarter past, a merchant presented himself to insure two ships; it was a clear profit of 15,000 francs.\n\n“‘Monsieur,’ said Emmanuel, ‘have the goodness to address yourself to M. Delaunay. We have quitted business.’\n\n“‘How long?’ inquired the astonished merchant.\n\n30043m\n“‘A quarter of an hour,’ was the reply.\n\n“And this is the reason, monsieur,” continued Maximilian, “of my sister and brother-in-law having only 25,000 francs a year.”\n\nMaximilian had scarcely finished his story, during which the count’s heart had swelled within him, when Emmanuel entered wearing a hat and coat. He saluted the count with the air of a man who is aware of the rank of his guest; then, after having led Monte Cristo around the little garden, he returned to the house.\n\nA large vase of Japan porcelain, filled with flowers that loaded the air with their perfume, stood in the salon. Julie, suitably dressed, and her hair arranged (she had accomplished this feat in less than ten minutes), received the count on his entrance. The songs of the birds were heard in an aviary hard by, and the branches of laburnums and rose acacias formed an exquisite framework to the blue velvet curtains. Everything in this charming retreat, from the warble of the birds to the smile of the mistress, breathed tranquillity and repose.\n\nThe count had felt the influence of this happiness from the moment he entered the house, and he remained silent and pensive, forgetting that he was expected to renew the conversation, which had ceased after the first salutations had been exchanged. The silence became almost painful when, by a violent effort, tearing himself from his pleasing reverie:\n\n“Madame,” said he at length, “I pray you to excuse my emotion, which must astonish you who are only accustomed to the happiness I meet here; but contentment is so new a sight to me, that I could never be weary of looking at yourself and your husband.”\n\n“We are very happy, monsieur,” replied Julie; “but we have also known unhappiness, and few have ever undergone more bitter sufferings than ourselves.”\n\nThe count’s features displayed an expression of the most intense curiosity.\n\n“Oh, all this is a family history, as Château-Renaud told you the other day,” observed Maximilian. “This humble picture would have but little interest for you, accustomed as you are to behold the pleasures and the misfortunes of the wealthy and industrious; but such as we are, we have experienced bitter sorrows.”\n\n“And God has poured balm into your wounds, as he does into those of all who are in affliction?” said Monte Cristo inquiringly.\n\n“Yes, count,” returned Julie, “we may indeed say he has, for he has done for us what he grants only to his chosen; he sent us one of his angels.”\n\nThe count’s cheeks became scarlet, and he coughed, in order to have an excuse for putting his handkerchief to his mouth.\n\n“Those born to wealth, and who have the means of gratifying every wish,” said Emmanuel, “know not what is the real happiness of life, just as those who have been tossed on the stormy waters of the ocean on a few frail planks can alone realize the blessings of fair weather.”\n\nMonte Cristo rose, and without making any answer (for the tremulousness of his voice would have betrayed his emotion) walked up and down the apartment with a slow step.\n\n“Our magnificence makes you smile, count,” said Maximilian, who had followed him with his eyes.\n\n“No, no,” returned Monte Cristo, pale as death, pressing one hand on his heart to still its throbbings, while with the other he pointed to a crystal cover, beneath which a silken purse lay on a black velvet cushion. “I was wondering what could be the significance of this purse, with the paper at one end and the large diamond at the other.”\n\n“Count,” replied Maximilian, with an air of gravity, “those are our most precious family treasures.”\n\n“The stone seems very brilliant,” answered the count.\n\n“Oh, my brother does not allude to its value, although it has been estimated at 100,000 francs; he means, that the articles contained in this purse are the relics of the angel I spoke of just now.”\n\n“This I do not comprehend; and yet I may not ask for an explanation, madame,” replied Monte Cristo bowing. “Pardon me, I had no intention of committing an indiscretion.”\n\n“Indiscretion,—oh, you make us happy by giving us an excuse for expatiating on this subject. If we wanted to conceal the noble action this purse commemorates, we should not expose it thus to view. Oh, would we could relate it everywhere, and to everyone, so that the emotion of our unknown benefactor might reveal his presence.”\n\n“Ah, really,” said Monte Cristo in a half-stifled voice.\n\n“Monsieur,” returned Maximilian, raising the glass cover, and respectfully kissing the silken purse, “this has touched the hand of a man who saved my father from suicide, us from ruin, and our name from shame and disgrace,—a man by whose matchless benevolence we poor children, doomed to want and wretchedness, can at present hear everyone envying our happy lot. This letter” (as he spoke, Maximilian drew a letter from the purse and gave it to the count)—“this letter was written by him the day that my father had taken a desperate resolution, and this diamond was given by the generous unknown to my sister as her dowry.”\n\nMonte Cristo opened the letter, and read it with an indescribable feeling of delight. It was the letter written (as our readers know) to Julie, and signed “Sinbad the Sailor.”\n\n“Unknown you say, is the man who rendered you this service—unknown to you?”\n\n“Yes; we have never had the happiness of pressing his hand,” continued Maximilian. “We have supplicated Heaven in vain to grant us this favor, but the whole affair has had a mysterious meaning that we cannot comprehend—we have been guided by an invisible hand,—a hand as powerful as that of an enchanter.”\n\n“Oh,” cried Julie, “I have not lost all hope of some day kissing that hand, as I now kiss the purse which he has touched. Four years ago, Penelon was at Trieste—Penelon, count, is the old sailor you saw in the garden, and who, from quartermaster, has become gardener—Penelon, when he was at Trieste, saw on the quay an Englishman, who was on the point of embarking on board a yacht, and he recognized him as the person who called on my father the fifth of June, 1829, and who wrote me this letter on the fifth of September. He felt convinced of his identity, but he did not venture to address him.”\n\n“An Englishman,” said Monte Cristo, who grew uneasy at the attention with which Julie looked at him. “An Englishman you say?”\n\n“Yes,” replied Maximilian, “an Englishman, who represented himself as the confidential clerk of the house of Thomson & French, at Rome. It was this that made me start when you said the other day, at M. de Morcerf’s, that Messrs. Thomson & French were your bankers. That happened, as I told you, in 1829. For God’s sake, tell me, did you know this Englishman?”\n\n“But you tell me, also, that the house of Thomson & French have constantly denied having rendered you this service?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Then is it not probable that this Englishman may be someone who, grateful for a kindness your father had shown him, and which he himself had forgotten, has taken this method of requiting the obligation?”\n\n“Everything is possible in this affair, even a miracle.”\n\n“What was his name?” asked Monte Cristo.\n\n“He gave no other name,” answered Julie, looking earnestly at the count, “than that at the end of his letter—‘Sinbad the Sailor.’”\n\n“Which is evidently not his real name, but a fictitious one.”\n\nThen, noticing that Julie was struck with the sound of his voice:\n\n“Tell me,” continued he, “was he not about my height, perhaps a little taller, with his chin imprisoned, as it were, in a high cravat; his coat closely buttoned up, and constantly taking out his pencil?”\n\n“Oh, do you then know him?” cried Julie, whose eyes sparkled with joy.\n\n“No,” returned Monte Cristo “I only guessed. I knew a Lord Wilmore, who was constantly doing actions of this kind.”\n\n“Without revealing himself?”\n\n“He was an eccentric being, and did not believe in the existence of gratitude.”\n\n“Oh, Heaven,” exclaimed Julie, clasping her hands, “in what did he believe, then?”\n\n30047m\n“He did not credit it at the period which I knew him,” said Monte Cristo, touched to the heart by the accents of Julie’s voice; “but, perhaps, since then he has had proofs that gratitude does exist.”\n\n“And do you know this gentleman, monsieur?” inquired Emmanuel.\n\n“Oh, if you do know him,” cried Julie, “can you tell us where he is—where we can find him? Maximilian—Emmanuel—if we do but discover him, he must believe in the gratitude of the heart!”\n\nMonte Cristo felt tears start into his eyes, and he again walked hastily up and down the room.\n\n“In the name of Heaven,” said Maximilian, “if you know anything of him, tell us what it is.”\n\n“Alas,” cried Monte Cristo, striving to repress his emotion, “if Lord Wilmore was your unknown benefactor, I fear you will never see him again. I parted from him two years ago at Palermo, and he was then on the point of setting out for the most remote regions; so that I fear he will never return.”\n\n“Oh, monsieur, this is cruel of you,” said Julie, much affected; and the young lady’s eyes swam with tears.\n\n“Madame,” replied Monte Cristo gravely, and gazing earnestly on the two liquid pearls that trickled down Julie’s cheeks, “had Lord Wilmore seen what I now see, he would become attached to life, for the tears you shed would reconcile him to mankind;” and he held out his hand to Julie, who gave him hers, carried away by the look and accent of the count.\n\n“But,” continued she, “Lord Wilmore had a family or friends, he must have known someone, can we not——”\n\n“Oh, it is useless to inquire,” returned the count; “perhaps, after all, he was not the man you seek for. He was my friend: he had no secrets from me, and if this had been so he would have confided in me.”\n\n“And he told you nothing?”\n\n“Nothing that would lead you to suppose?”\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“And yet you spoke of him at once.”\n\n“Ah, in such a case one supposes——”\n\n“Sister, sister,” said Maximilian, coming to the count’s aid, “monsieur is quite right. Recollect what our excellent father so often told us, ‘It was no Englishman that thus saved us.’”\n\nMonte Cristo started. “What did your father tell you, M. Morrel?” said he eagerly.\n\n“My father thought that this action had been miraculously performed—he believed that a benefactor had arisen from the grave to save us. Oh, it was a touching superstition, monsieur, and although I did not myself believe it, I would not for the world have destroyed my father’s faith. How often did he muse over it and pronounce the name of a dear friend—a friend lost to him forever; and on his death-bed, when the near approach of eternity seemed to have illumined his mind with supernatural light, this thought, which had until then been but a doubt, became a conviction, and his last words were, ‘Maximilian, it was Edmond Dantès!’”\n\nAt these words the count’s paleness, which had for some time been increasing, became alarming; he could not speak; he looked at his watch like a man who has forgotten the hour, said a few hurried words to Madame Herbault, and pressing the hands of Emmanuel and Maximilian,—“Madame,” said he, “I trust you will allow me to visit you occasionally; I value your friendship, and feel grateful to you for your welcome, for this is the first time for many years that I have thus yielded to my feelings;” and he hastily quitted the apartment.\n\n“This Count of Monte Cristo is a strange man,” said Emmanuel.\n\n“Yes,” answered Maximilian, “but I feel sure he has an excellent heart, and that he likes us.”\n\n“His voice went to my heart,” observed Julie; “and two or three times I fancied that I had heard it before.”\n\n# Chapter 51. Pyramus and Thisbe\n\nAbout two-thirds of the way along the Faubourg Saint-Honoré, and in the rear of one of the most imposing mansions in this rich neighborhood, where the various houses vie with each other for elegance of design and magnificence of construction, extended a large garden, where the wide-spreading chestnut-trees raised their heads high above the walls in a solid rampart, and with the coming of every spring scattered a shower of delicate pink and white blossoms into the large stone vases that stood upon the two square pilasters of a curiously wrought iron gate, that dated from the time of Louis XIII.\n\nThis noble entrance, however, in spite of its striking appearance and the graceful effect of the geraniums planted in the two vases, as they waved their variegated leaves in the wind and charmed the eye with their scarlet bloom, had fallen into utter disuse. The proprietors of the mansion had many years before thought it best to confine themselves to the possession of the house itself, with its thickly planted courtyard, opening into the Faubourg Saint-Honoré, and to the garden shut in by this gate, which formerly communicated with a fine kitchen-garden of about an acre. For the demon of speculation drew a line, or in other words projected a street, at the farther side of the kitchen-garden. The street was laid out, a name was chosen and posted up on an iron plate, but before construction was begun, it occurred to the possessor of the property that a handsome sum might be obtained for the ground then devoted to fruits and vegetables, by building along the line of the proposed street, and so making it a branch of communication with the Faubourg Saint-Honoré itself, one of the most important thoroughfares in the city of Paris.\n\nIn matters of speculation, however, though “man proposes,” yet “money disposes.” From some such difficulty the newly named street died almost in birth, and the purchaser of the kitchen-garden, having paid a high price for it, and being quite unable to find anyone willing to take his bargain off his hands without a considerable loss, yet still clinging to the belief that at some future day he should obtain a sum for it that would repay him, not only for his past outlay, but also the interest upon the capital locked up in his new acquisition, contented himself with letting the ground temporarily to some market-gardeners, at a yearly rental of 500 francs.\n\nAnd so, as we have said, the iron gate leading into the kitchen-garden had been closed up and left to the rust, which bade fair before long to eat off its hinges, while to prevent the ignoble glances of the diggers and delvers of the ground from presuming to sully the aristocratic enclosure belonging to the mansion, the gate had been boarded up to a height of six feet. True, the planks were not so closely adjusted but that a hasty peep might be obtained through their interstices; but the strict decorum and rigid propriety of the inhabitants of the house left no grounds for apprehending that advantage would be taken of that circumstance.\n\nHorticulture seemed, however, to have been abandoned in the deserted kitchen-garden; and where cabbages, carrots, radishes, peas, and melons had once flourished, a scanty crop of lucern alone bore evidence of its being deemed worthy of cultivation. A small, low door gave egress from the walled space we have been describing into the projected street, the ground having been abandoned as unproductive by its various renters, and had now fallen so completely in general estimation as to return not even the one-half per cent it had originally paid. Towards the house the chestnut-trees we have before mentioned rose high above the wall, without in any way affecting the growth of other luxuriant shrubs and flowers that eagerly dressed forward to fill up the vacant spaces, as though asserting their right to enjoy the boon of light and air. At one corner, where the foliage became so thick as almost to shut out day, a large stone bench and sundry rustic seats indicated that this sheltered spot was either in general favor or particular use by some inhabitant of the house, which was faintly discernible through the dense mass of verdure that partially concealed it, though situated but a hundred paces off.\n\nWhoever had selected this retired portion of the grounds as the boundary of a walk, or as a place for meditation, was abundantly justified in the choice by the absence of all glare, the cool, refreshing shade, the screen it afforded from the scorching rays of the sun, that found no entrance there even during the burning days of hottest summer, the incessant and melodious warbling of birds, and the entire removal from either the noise of the street or the bustle of the mansion. On the evening of one of the warmest days spring had yet bestowed on the inhabitants of Paris, might be seen negligently thrown upon the stone bench, a book, a parasol, and a work-basket, from which hung a partly embroidered cambric handkerchief, while at a little distance from these articles was a young woman, standing close to the iron gate, endeavoring to discern something on the other side by means of the openings in the planks,—the earnestness of her attitude and the fixed gaze with which she seemed to seek the object of her wishes, proving how much her feelings were interested in the matter.\n\nAt that instant the little side-gate leading from the waste ground to the street was noiselessly opened, and a tall, powerful young man appeared. He was dressed in a common gray blouse and velvet cap, but his carefully arranged hair, beard and moustache, all of the richest and glossiest black, ill accorded with his plebeian attire. After casting a rapid glance around him, in order to assure himself that he was unobserved, he entered by the small gate, and, carefully closing and securing it after him, proceeded with a hurried step towards the barrier.\n\nAt the sight of him she expected, though probably not in such a costume, the young woman started in terror, and was about to make a hasty retreat. But the eye of love had already seen, even through the narrow chinks of the wooden palisades, the movement of the white robe, and observed the fluttering of the blue sash. Pressing his lips close to the planks, he exclaimed:\n\n“Don’t be alarmed, Valentine—it is I!”\n\nAgain the timid girl found courage to return to the gate, saying, as she did so:\n\n“And why do you come so late today? It is almost dinner-time, and I had to use no little diplomacy to get rid of my watchful stepmother, my too-devoted maid, and my troublesome brother, who is always teasing me about coming to work at my embroidery, which I am in a fair way never to get done. So pray excuse yourself as well as you can for having made me wait, and, after that, tell me why I see you in a dress so singular that at first I did not recognize you.”\n\n“Dearest Valentine,” said the young man, “the difference between our respective stations makes me fear to offend you by speaking of my love, but yet I cannot find myself in your presence without longing to pour forth my soul, and tell you how fondly I adore you. If it be but to carry away with me the recollection of such sweet moments, I could even thank you for chiding me, for it leaves me a gleam of hope, that if you did not expect me (and that indeed would be worse than vanity to suppose), at least I was in your thoughts. You asked me the cause of my being late, and why I come disguised. I will candidly explain the reason of both, and I trust to your goodness to pardon me. I have chosen a trade.”\n\n“A trade? Oh, Maximilian, how can you jest at a time when we have such deep cause for uneasiness?”\n\n“Heaven keep me from jesting with that which is far dearer to me than life itself! But listen to me, Valentine, and I will tell you all about it. I became weary of ranging fields and scaling walls, and seriously alarmed at the idea suggested by you, that if caught hovering about here your father would very likely have me sent to prison as a thief. That would compromise the honor of the French army, to say nothing of the fact that the continual presence of a captain of Spahis in a place where no warlike projects could be supposed to account for it might well create surprise; so I have become a gardener, and, consequently, adopted the costume of my calling.”\n\n“What excessive nonsense you talk, Maximilian!”\n\n“Nonsense? Pray do not call what I consider the wisest action of my life by such a name. Consider, by becoming a gardener I effectually screen our meetings from all suspicion or danger.”\n30053m\n\n“I beseech of you, Maximilian, to cease trifling, and tell me what you really mean.”\n\n“Simply, that having ascertained that the piece of ground on which I stand was to let, I made application for it, was readily accepted by the proprietor, and am now master of this fine crop of lucern. Think of that, Valentine! There is nothing now to prevent my building myself a little hut on my plantation, and residing not twenty yards from you. Only imagine what happiness that would afford me. I can scarcely contain myself at the bare idea. Such felicity seems above all price—as a thing impossible and unattainable. But would you believe that I purchase all this delight, joy, and happiness, for which I would cheerfully have surrendered ten years of my life, at the small cost of 500 francs per annum, paid quarterly? Henceforth we have nothing to fear. I am on my own ground, and have an undoubted right to place a ladder against the wall, and to look over when I please, without having any apprehensions of being taken off by the police as a suspicious character. I may also enjoy the precious privilege of assuring you of my fond, faithful, and unalterable affection, whenever you visit your favorite bower, unless, indeed, it offends your pride to listen to professions of love from the lips of a poor workingman, clad in a blouse and cap.”\n\nA faint cry of mingled pleasure and surprise escaped from the lips of Valentine, who almost instantly said, in a saddened tone, as though some envious cloud darkened the joy which illumined her heart:\n\n“Alas, no, Maximilian, this must not be, for many reasons. We should presume too much on our own strength, and, like others, perhaps, be led astray by our blind confidence in each other’s prudence.”\n\n“How can you for an instant entertain so unworthy a thought, dear Valentine? Have I not, from the first blessed hour of our acquaintance, schooled all my words and actions to your sentiments and ideas? And you have, I am sure, the fullest confidence in my honor. When you spoke to me of experiencing a vague and indefinite sense of coming danger, I placed myself blindly and devotedly at your service, asking no other reward than the pleasure of being useful to you; and have I ever since, by word or look, given you cause of regret for having selected me from the numbers that would willingly have sacrificed their lives for you? You told me, my dear Valentine, that you were engaged to M. d’Épinay, and that your father was resolved upon completing the match, and that from his will there was no appeal, as M. de Villefort was never known to change a determination once formed. I kept in the background, as you wished, and waited, not for the decision of your heart or my own, but hoping that Providence would graciously interpose in our behalf, and order events in our favor. But what cared I for delays or difficulties, Valentine, as long as you confessed that you loved me, and took pity on me? If you will only repeat that avowal now and then, I can endure anything.”\n\n“Ah, Maximilian, that is the very thing that makes you so bold, and which renders me at once so happy and unhappy, that I frequently ask myself whether it is better for me to endure the harshness of my stepmother, and her blind preference for her own child, or to be, as I now am, insensible to any pleasure save such as I find in these meetings, so fraught with danger to both.”\n\n“I will not admit that word,” returned the young man; “it is at once cruel and unjust. Is it possible to find a more submissive slave than myself? You have permitted me to converse with you from time to time, Valentine, but forbidden my ever following you in your walks or elsewhere—have I not obeyed? And since I found means to enter this enclosure to exchange a few words with you through this gate—to be close to you without really seeing you—have I ever asked so much as to touch the hem of your gown or tried to pass this barrier which is but a trifle to one of my youth and strength? Never has a complaint or a murmur escaped me. I have been bound by my promises as rigidly as any knight of olden times. Come, come, dearest Valentine, confess that what I say is true, lest I be tempted to call you unjust.”\n\n30055m\n“It is true,” said Valentine, as she passed the end of her slender fingers through a small opening in the planks, and permitted Maximilian to press his lips to them, “and you are a true and faithful friend; but still you acted from motives of self-interest, my dear Maximilian, for you well knew that from the moment in which you had manifested an opposite spirit all would have been ended between us. You promised to bestow on me the friendly affection of a brother. For I have no friend but yourself upon earth, who am neglected and forgotten by my father, harassed and persecuted by my stepmother, and left to the sole companionship of a paralyzed and speechless old man, whose withered hand can no longer press mine, and who can speak to me with the eye alone, although there still lingers in his heart the warmest tenderness for his poor grandchild. Oh, how bitter a fate is mine, to serve either as a victim or an enemy to all who are stronger than myself, while my only friend and supporter is a living corpse! Indeed, indeed, Maximilian, I am very miserable, and if you love me it must be out of pity.”\n\n“Valentine,” replied the young man, deeply affected, “I will not say you are all I love in the world, for I dearly prize my sister and brother-in-law; but my affection for them is calm and tranquil, in no manner resembling what I feel for you. When I think of you my heart beats fast, the blood burns in my veins, and I can hardly breathe; but I solemnly promise you to restrain all this ardor, this fervor and intensity of feeling, until you yourself shall require me to render them available in serving or assisting you. M. Franz is not expected to return home for a year to come, I am told; in that time many favorable and unforeseen chances may befriend us. Let us, then, hope for the best; hope is so sweet a comforter. Meanwhile, Valentine, while reproaching me with selfishness, think a little what you have been to me—the beautiful but cold resemblance of a marble Venus. What promise of future reward have you made me for all the submission and obedience I have evinced?—none whatever. What granted me?—scarcely more. You tell me of M. Franz d’Épinay, your betrothed lover, and you shrink from the idea of being his wife; but tell me, Valentine, is there no other sorrow in your heart? You see me devoted to you, body and soul, my life and each warm drop that circles round my heart are consecrated to your service; you know full well that my existence is bound up in yours—that were I to lose you I would not outlive the hour of such crushing misery; yet you speak with calmness of the prospect of your being the wife of another! Oh, Valentine, were I in your place, and did I feel conscious, as you do, of being worshipped, adored, with such a love as mine, a hundred times at least should I have passed my hand between these iron bars, and said, ‘Take this hand, dearest Maximilian, and believe that, living or dead, I am yours—yours only, and forever!’”\n\nThe poor girl made no reply, but her lover could plainly hear her sobs and tears. A rapid change took place in the young man’s feelings.\n\n“Dearest, dearest Valentine,” exclaimed he, “forgive me if I have offended you, and forget the words I spoke if they have unwittingly caused you pain.”\n\n“No, Maximilian, I am not offended,” answered she, “but do you not see what a poor, helpless being I am, almost a stranger and an outcast in my father’s house, where even he is seldom seen; whose will has been thwarted, and spirits broken, from the age of ten years, beneath the iron rod so sternly held over me; oppressed, mortified, and persecuted, day by day, hour by hour, minute by minute, no person has cared for, even observed my sufferings, nor have I ever breathed one word on the subject save to yourself. Outwardly and in the eyes of the world, I am surrounded by kindness and affection; but the reverse is the case. The general remark is, ‘Oh, it cannot be expected that one of so stern a character as M. Villefort could lavish the tenderness some fathers do on their daughters. What though she has lost her own mother at a tender age, she has had the happiness to find a second mother in Madame de Villefort.’ The world, however, is mistaken; my father abandons me from utter indifference, while my stepmother detests me with a hatred so much the more terrible because it is veiled beneath a continual smile.”\n\n“Hate you, sweet Valentine,” exclaimed the young man; “how is it possible for anyone to do that?”\n\n“Alas,” replied the weeping girl, “I am obliged to own that my stepmother’s aversion to me arises from a very natural source—her overweening love for her own child, my brother Edward.”\n\n“But why should it?”\n\n“I do not know; but, though unwilling to introduce money matters into our present conversation, I will just say this much—that her extreme dislike to me has its origin there; and I much fear she envies me the fortune I enjoy in right of my mother, and which will be more than doubled at the death of M. and Mme. de Saint-Méran, whose sole heiress I am. Madame de Villefort has nothing of her own, and hates me for being so richly endowed. Alas, how gladly would I exchange the half of this wealth for the happiness of at least sharing my father’s love. God knows, I would prefer sacrificing the whole, so that it would obtain me a happy and affectionate home.”\n\n“Poor Valentine!”\n\n“I seem to myself as though living a life of bondage, yet at the same time am so conscious of my own weakness that I fear to break the restraint in which I am held, lest I fall utterly helpless. Then, too, my father is not a person whose orders may be infringed with impunity; protected as he is by his high position and firmly established reputation for talent and unswerving integrity, no one could oppose him; he is all-powerful even with the king; he would crush you at a word. Dear Maximilian, believe me when I assure you that if I do not attempt to resist my father’s commands it is more on your account than my own.”\n\n“But why, Valentine, do you persist in anticipating the worst,—why picture so gloomy a future?”\n\n“Because I judge it from the past.”\n\n“Still, consider that although I may not be, strictly speaking, what is termed an illustrious match for you, I am, for many reasons, not altogether so much beneath your alliance. The days when such distinctions were so nicely weighed and considered no longer exist in France, and the first families of the monarchy have intermarried with those of the empire. The aristocracy of the lance has allied itself with the nobility of the cannon. Now I belong to this last-named class; and certainly my prospects of military preferment are most encouraging as well as certain. My fortune, though small, is free and unfettered, and the memory of my late father is respected in our country, Valentine, as that of the most upright and honorable merchant of the city; I say our country, because you were born not far from Marseilles.”\n\n“Don’t speak of Marseilles, I beg of you, Maximilian; that one word brings back my mother to my recollection—my angel mother, who died too soon for myself and all who knew her; but who, after watching over her child during the brief period allotted to her in this world, now, I fondly hope, watches from her home in heaven. Oh, if my mother were still living, there would be nothing to fear, Maximilian, for I would tell her that I loved you, and she would protect us.”\n\n“I fear, Valentine,” replied the lover, “that were she living I should never have had the happiness of knowing you; you would then have been too happy to have stooped from your grandeur to bestow a thought on me.”\n\n“Now it is you who are unjust, Maximilian,” cried Valentine; “but there is one thing I wish to know.”\n\n“And what is that?” inquired the young man, perceiving that Valentine hesitated.\n\n“Tell me truly, Maximilian, whether in former days, when our fathers dwelt at Marseilles, there was ever any misunderstanding between them?”\n\n“Not that I am aware of,” replied the young man, “unless, indeed, any ill-feeling might have arisen from their being of opposite parties—your father was, as you know, a zealous partisan of the Bourbons, while mine was wholly devoted to the emperor; there could not possibly be any other difference between them. But why do you ask?”\n\n“I will tell you,” replied the young girl, “for it is but right you should know. Well, on the day when your appointment as an officer of the Legion of Honor was announced in the papers, we were all sitting with my grandfather, M. Noirtier; M. Danglars was there also—you recollect M. Danglars, do you not, Maximilian, the banker, whose horses ran away with my stepmother and little brother, and very nearly killed them? While the rest of the company were discussing the approaching marriage of Mademoiselle Danglars, I was reading the paper to my grandfather; but when I came to the paragraph about you, although I had done nothing else but read it over to myself all the morning (you know you had told me all about it the previous evening), I felt so happy, and yet so nervous, at the idea of speaking your name aloud, and before so many people, that I really think I should have passed it over, but for the fear that my doing so might create suspicions as to the cause of my silence; so I summoned up all my courage, and read it as firmly and as steadily as I could.”\n30059m\n\n“Dear Valentine!”\n\n“Well, would you believe it? directly my father caught the sound of your name he turned round quite hastily, and, like a poor silly thing, I was so persuaded that everyone must be as much affected as myself by the utterance of your name, that I was not surprised to see my father start, and almost tremble; but I even thought (though that surely must have been a mistake) that M. Danglars trembled too.”\n\n“‘Morrel, Morrel,’ cried my father, ‘stop a bit;’ then knitting his brows into a deep frown, he added, ‘surely this cannot be one of the Morrel family who lived at Marseilles, and gave us so much trouble from their violent Bonapartism—I mean about the year 1815.’\n\n“‘Yes,’ replied M. Danglars, ‘I believe he is the son of the old shipowner.’”\n\n“Indeed,” answered Maximilian; “and what did your father say then, Valentine?”\n\n“Oh, such a dreadful thing, that I don’t dare to tell you.”\n\n“Always tell me everything,” said Maximilian with a smile.\n\n“‘Ah,’ continued my father, still frowning, ‘their idolized emperor treated these madmen as they deserved; he called them ‘food for cannon,’ which was precisely all they were good for; and I am delighted to see that the present government have adopted this salutary principle with all its pristine vigor; if Algiers were good for nothing but to furnish the means of carrying so admirable an idea into practice, it would be an acquisition well worthy of struggling to obtain. Though it certainly does cost France somewhat dear to assert her rights in that uncivilized country.’”\n\n“Brutal politics, I must confess.” said Maximilian; “but don’t attach any serious importance, dear, to what your father said. My father was not a bit behind yours in that sort of talk. ‘Why,’ said he, ‘does not the emperor, who has devised so many clever and efficient modes of improving the art of war, organize a regiment of lawyers, judges and legal practitioners, sending them in the hottest fire the enemy could maintain, and using them to save better men?’ You see, my dear, that for picturesque expression and generosity of spirit there is not much to choose between the language of either party. But what did M. Danglars say to this outburst on the part of the procureur?”\n\n“Oh, he laughed, and in that singular manner so peculiar to himself—half-malicious, half-ferocious; he almost immediately got up and took his leave; then, for the first time, I observed the agitation of my grandfather, and I must tell you, Maximilian, that I am the only person capable of discerning emotion in his paralyzed frame. And I suspected that the conversation that had been carried on in his presence (for they always say and do what they like before the dear old man, without the smallest regard for his feelings) had made a strong impression on his mind; for, naturally enough, it must have pained him to hear the emperor he so devotedly loved and served spoken of in that depreciating manner.”\n\n“The name of M. Noirtier,” interposed Maximilian, “is celebrated throughout Europe; he was a statesman of high standing, and you may or may not know, Valentine, that he took a leading part in every Bonapartist conspiracy set on foot during the restoration of the Bourbons.”\n\n“Oh, I have often heard whispers of things that seem to me most strange—the father a Bonapartist, the son a Royalist; what can have been the reason of so singular a difference in parties and politics? But to resume my story; I turned towards my grandfather, as though to question him as to the cause of his emotion; he looked expressively at the newspaper I had been reading. ‘What is the matter, dear grandfather?’ said I, ‘are you pleased?’ He gave me a sign in the affirmative. ‘With what my father said just now?’ He returned a sign in the negative. ‘Perhaps you liked what M. Danglars said?’ Another sign in the negative. ‘Oh, then, you were glad to hear that M. Morrel (I didn’t dare to say Maximilian) had been made an officer of the Legion of Honor?’ He signified assent; only think of the poor old man’s being so pleased to think that you, who were a perfect stranger to him, had been made an officer of the Legion of Honor! Perhaps it was a mere whim on his part, for he is falling, they say, into second childhood, but I love him for showing so much interest in you.”\n\n“How singular,” murmured Maximilian; “your father hates me, while your grandfather, on the contrary—What strange feelings are aroused by politics.”\n\n“Hush,” cried Valentine, suddenly; “someone is coming!” Maximilian leaped at one bound into his crop of lucern, which he began to pull up in the most ruthless way, under the pretext of being occupied in weeding it.\n\n“Mademoiselle, mademoiselle!” exclaimed a voice from behind the trees. “Madame is searching for you everywhere; there is a visitor in the drawing-room.”\n\n“A visitor?” inquired Valentine, much agitated; “who is it?”\n\n“Some grand personage—a prince I believe they said—the Count of Monte Cristo.”\n\n“I will come directly,” cried Valentine aloud.\n\nThe name of Monte Cristo sent an electric shock through the young man on the other side of the iron gate, to whom Valentine’s _“I am coming”_ was the customary signal of farewell.\n\n“Now, then,” said Maximilian, leaning on the handle of his spade, “I would give a good deal to know how it comes about that the Count of Monte Cristo is acquainted with M. de Villefort.”\n\n# Chapter 52. Toxicology\n\nIt was really the Count of Monte Cristo who had just arrived at Madame de Villefort’s for the purpose of returning the procureur’s visit, and at his name, as may be easily imagined, the whole house was in confusion.\n\nMadame de Villefort, who was alone in her drawing-room when the count was announced, desired that her son might be brought thither instantly to renew his thanks to the count; and Edward, who heard this great personage talked of for two whole days, made all possible haste to come to him, not from obedience to his mother, or out of any feeling of gratitude to the count, but from sheer curiosity, and that some chance remark might give him the opportunity for making one of the impertinent speeches which made his mother say:\n\n“Oh, that naughty child! But I can’t be severe with him, he is really _so_ bright.”\n\nAfter the usual civilities, the count inquired after M. de Villefort.\n\n“My husband dines with the chancellor,” replied the young lady; “he has just gone, and I am sure he’ll be exceedingly sorry not to have had the pleasure of seeing you before he went.”\n\nTwo visitors who were there when the count arrived, having gazed at him with all their eyes, retired after that reasonable delay which politeness admits and curiosity requires.\n\n“What is your sister Valentine doing?” inquired Madame de Villefort of Edward; “tell someone to bid her come here, that I may have the honor of introducing her to the count.”\n\n“You have a daughter, then, madame?” inquired the count; “very young, I presume?”\n\n“The daughter of M. de Villefort by his first marriage,” replied the young wife, “a fine well-grown girl.”\n\n“But melancholy,” interrupted Master Edward, snatching the feathers out of the tail of a splendid paroquet that was screaming on its gilded perch, in order to make a plume for his hat.\n\nMadame de Villefort merely cried, “Be still, Edward!” She then added, “This young madcap is, however, very nearly right, and merely re-echoes what he has heard me say with pain a hundred times; for Mademoiselle de Villefort is, in spite of all we can do to rouse her, of a melancholy disposition and taciturn habit, which frequently injure the effect of her beauty. But what detains her? Go, Edward, and see.”\n\n“Because they are looking for her where she is not to be found.”\n\n“And where are they looking for her?”\n\n“With grandpapa Noirtier.”\n\n“And do you think she is not there?”\n\n“No, no, no, no, no, she is not there,” replied Edward, singing his words.\n\n“And where is she, then? If you know, why don’t you tell?”\n\n“She is under the big chestnut-tree,” replied the spoiled brat, as he gave, in spite of his mother’s commands, live flies to the parrot, which seemed keenly to relish such fare.\n\nMadame de Villefort stretched out her hand to ring, intending to direct her waiting-maid to the spot where she would find Valentine, when the young lady herself entered the apartment. She appeared much dejected; and any person who considered her attentively might have observed the traces of recent tears in her eyes.\n\nValentine, whom we have in the rapid march of our narrative presented to our readers without formally introducing her, was a tall and graceful girl of nineteen, with bright chestnut hair, deep blue eyes, and that reposeful air of quiet distinction which characterized her mother. Her white and slender fingers, her pearly neck, her cheeks tinted with varying hues reminded one of the lovely Englishwomen who have been so poetically compared in their manner to the gracefulness of a swan.\n\nShe entered the apartment, and seeing near her stepmother the stranger of whom she had already heard so much, saluted him without any girlish awkwardness, or even lowering her eyes, and with an elegance that redoubled the count’s attention.\n\nHe rose to return the salutation.\n\n“Mademoiselle de Villefort, my step-daughter,” said Madame de Villefort to Monte Cristo, leaning back on her sofa and motioning towards Valentine with her hand.\n\n“And M. de Monte Cristo, King of China, Emperor of Cochin-China,” said the young imp, looking slyly towards his sister.\n\nMadame de Villefort at this really did turn pale, and was very nearly angry with this household plague, who answered to the name of Edward; but the count, on the contrary, smiled, and appeared to look at the boy complacently, which caused the maternal heart to bound again with joy and enthusiasm.\n\n“But, madame,” replied the count, continuing the conversation, and looking by turns at Madame de Villefort and Valentine, “have I not already had the honor of meeting yourself and mademoiselle before? I could not help thinking so just now; the idea came over my mind, and as mademoiselle entered the sight of her was an additional ray of light thrown on a confused remembrance; excuse the remark.”\n\n“I do not think it likely, sir; Mademoiselle de Villefort is not very fond of society, and we very seldom go out,” said the young lady.\n\n“Then it was not in society that I met with mademoiselle or yourself, madame, or this charming little merry boy. Besides, the Parisian world is entirely unknown to me, for, as I believe I told you, I have been in Paris but very few days. No,—but, perhaps, you will permit me to call to mind—stay!”\n\nThe Count placed his hand on his brow as if to collect his thoughts.\n\n“No—it was somewhere—away from here—it was—I do not know—but it appears that this recollection is connected with a lovely sky and some religious _fête_; mademoiselle was holding flowers in her hand, the interesting boy was chasing a beautiful peacock in a garden, and you, madame, were under the trellis of some arbor. Pray come to my aid, madame; do not these circumstances appeal to your memory?”\n\n“No, indeed,” replied Madame de Villefort; “and yet it appears to me, sir, that if I had met you anywhere, the recollection of you must have been imprinted on my memory.”\n\n“Perhaps the count saw us in Italy,” said Valentine timidly.\n\n“Yes, in Italy; it was in Italy most probably,” replied Monte Cristo; “you have travelled then in Italy, mademoiselle?”\n\n“Yes; madame and I were there two years ago. The doctors, anxious for my lungs, had prescribed the air of Naples. We went by Bologna, Perugia, and Rome.”\n\n“Ah, yes—true, mademoiselle,” exclaimed Monte Cristo as if this simple explanation was sufficient to revive the recollection he sought. “It was at Perugia on Corpus Christi Day, in the garden of the Hôtel des Postes, when chance brought us together; you, Madame de Villefort, and her son; I now remember having had the honor of meeting you.”\n\n“I perfectly well remember Perugia, sir, and the Hôtel des Postes, and the festival of which you speak,” said Madame de Villefort, “but in vain do I tax my memory, of whose treachery I am ashamed, for I really do not recall to mind that I ever had the pleasure of seeing you before.”\n\n“It is strange, but neither do I recollect meeting with you,” observed Valentine, raising her beautiful eyes to the count.\n30065m\n\n“But I remember it perfectly,” interposed the darling Edward.\n\n“I will assist your memory, madame,” continued the count; “the day had been burning hot; you were waiting for horses, which were delayed in consequence of the festival. Mademoiselle was walking in the shade of the garden, and your son disappeared in pursuit of the peacock.”\n\n“And I caught it, mamma, don’t you remember?” interposed Edward, “and I pulled three such beautiful feathers out of his tail.”\n\n“You, madame, remained under the arbor; do you not remember, that while you were seated on a stone bench, and while, as I told you, Mademoiselle de Villefort and your young son were absent, you conversed for a considerable time with somebody?”\n\n“Yes, in truth, yes,” answered the young lady, turning very red, “I do remember conversing with a person wrapped in a long woollen mantle; he was a medical man, I think.”\n\n“Precisely so, madame; this man was myself; for a fortnight I had been at that hotel, during which period I had cured my valet de chambre of a fever, and my landlord of the jaundice, so that I really acquired a reputation as a skilful physician. We discoursed a long time, madame, on different subjects; of Perugino, of Raphael, of manners, customs, of the famous _aqua Tofana_, of which they had told you, I think you said, that certain individuals in Perugia had preserved the secret.”\n\n“Yes, true,” replied Madame de Villefort, somewhat uneasily, “I remember now.”\n\n“I do not recollect now all the various subjects of which we discoursed, madame,” continued the count with perfect calmness; “but I perfectly remember that, falling into the error which others had entertained respecting me, you consulted me as to the health of Mademoiselle de Villefort.”\n\n“Yes, really, sir, you were in fact a medical man,” said Madame de Villefort, “since you had cured the sick.”\n\n“Molière or Beaumarchais would reply to you, madame, that it was precisely because I was not, that I had cured my patients; for myself, I am content to say to you that I have studied chemistry and the natural sciences somewhat deeply, but still only as an amateur, you understand.”\n\nAt this moment the clock struck six.\n\n“It is six o’clock,” said Madame de Villefort, evidently agitated. “Valentine, will you not go and see if your grandpapa will have his dinner?”\n\nValentine rose, and saluting the count, left the apartment without speaking.\n\n“Oh, madame,” said the count, when Valentine had left the room, “was it on my account that you sent Mademoiselle de Villefort away?”\n\n“By no means,” replied the young lady quickly; “but this is the hour when we usually give M. Noirtier the unwelcome meal that sustains his pitiful existence. You are aware, sir, of the deplorable condition of my husband’s father?”\n\n“Yes, madame, M. de Villefort spoke of it to me—a paralysis, I think.”\n\n“Alas, yes; the poor old gentleman is entirely helpless; the mind alone is still active in this human machine, and that is faint and flickering, like the light of a lamp about to expire. But excuse me, sir, for talking of our domestic misfortunes; I interrupted you at the moment when you were telling me that you were a skilful chemist.”\n\n“No, madame, I did not say as much as that,” replied the count with a smile; “quite the contrary. I have studied chemistry because, having determined to live in eastern climates I have been desirous of following the example of King Mithridates.”\n\n“_Mithridates, rex Ponticus_,” said the young scamp, as he tore some beautiful portraits out of a splendid album, “the individual who took cream in his cup of poison every morning at breakfast.”\n\n“Edward, you naughty boy,” exclaimed Madame de Villefort, snatching the mutilated book from the urchin’s grasp, “you are positively past bearing; you really disturb the conversation; go, leave us, and join your sister Valentine in dear grandpapa Noirtier’s room.”\n\n“The album,” said Edward sulkily.\n\n“What do you mean?—the album!”\n\n“I want the album.”\n\n“How dare you tear out the drawings?”\n\n“Oh, it amuses me.”\n\n“Go—go at once.”\n\n“I won’t go unless you give me the album,” said the boy, seating himself doggedly in an armchair, according to his habit of never giving way.\n\n“Take it, then, and pray disturb us no longer,” said Madame de Villefort, giving the album to Edward, who then went towards the door, led by his mother. The count followed her with his eyes.\n\n“Let us see if she shuts the door after him,” he muttered.\n\nMadame de Villefort closed the door carefully after the child, the count appearing not to notice her; then casting a scrutinizing glance around the chamber, the young wife returned to her chair, in which she seated herself.\n\n“Allow me to observe, madame,” said the count, with that kind tone he could assume so well, “you are really very severe with that dear clever child.”\n\n“Oh, sometimes severity is quite necessary,” replied Madame de Villefort, with all a mother’s real firmness.\n\n“It was his Cornelius Nepos that Master Edward was repeating when he referred to King Mithridates,” continued the count, “and you interrupted him in a quotation which proves that his tutor has by no means neglected him, for your son is really advanced for his years.”\n\n“The fact is, count,” answered the mother, agreeably flattered, “he has great aptitude, and learns all that is set before him. He has but one fault, he is somewhat wilful; but really, on referring for the moment to what he said, do you truly believe that Mithridates used these precautions, and that these precautions were efficacious?”\n\n“I think so, madame, because I myself have made use of them, that I might not be poisoned at Naples, at Palermo, and at Smyrna—that is to say, on three several occasions when, but for these precautions, I must have lost my life.”\n\n“And your precautions were successful?”\n\n“Completely so.”\n\n“Yes, I remember now your mentioning to me at Perugia something of this sort.”\n\n“Indeed?” said the count with an air of surprise, remarkably well counterfeited; “I really did not remember.”\n\n“I inquired of you if poisons acted equally, and with the same effect, on men of the North as on men of the South; and you answered me that the cold and sluggish habits of the North did not present the same aptitude as the rich and energetic temperaments of the natives of the South.”\n\n“And that is the case,” observed Monte Cristo. “I have seen Russians devour, without being visibly inconvenienced, vegetable substances which would infallibly have killed a Neapolitan or an Arab.”\n\n“And you really believe the result would be still more sure with us than in the East, and in the midst of our fogs and rains a man would habituate himself more easily than in a warm latitude to this progressive absorption of poison?”\n\n“Certainly; it being at the same time perfectly understood that he should have been duly fortified against the poison to which he had not been accustomed.”\n\n“Yes, I understand that; and how would you habituate yourself, for instance, or rather, how did you habituate yourself to it?”\n\n“Oh, very easily. Suppose you knew beforehand the poison that would be made use of against you; suppose the poison was, for instance, brucine——”\n\n“Brucine is extracted from the false angostura8 is it not?” inquired Madame de Villefort.\n\n“Precisely, madame,” replied Monte Cristo; “but I perceive I have not much to teach you. Allow me to compliment you on your knowledge; such learning is very rare among ladies.”\n\n“Oh, I am aware of that,” said Madame de Villefort; “but I have a passion for the occult sciences, which speak to the imagination like poetry, and are reducible to figures, like an algebraic equation; but go on, I beg of you; what you say interests me to the greatest degree.”\n\n30069m\n“Well,” replied Monte Cristo “suppose, then, that this poison was brucine, and you were to take a milligramme the first day, two milligrammes the second day, and so on. Well, at the end of ten days you would have taken a centigramme, at the end of twenty days, increasing another milligramme, you would have taken three hundred centigrammes; that is to say, a dose which you would support without inconvenience, and which would be very dangerous for any other person who had not taken the same precautions as yourself. Well, then, at the end of a month, when drinking water from the same carafe, you would kill the person who drank with you, without your perceiving, otherwise than from slight inconvenience, that there was any poisonous substance mingled with this water.”\n\n“Do you know any other counter-poisons?”\n\n“I do not.”\n\n“I have often read, and read again, the history of Mithridates,” said Madame de Villefort in a tone of reflection, “and had always considered it a fable.”\n\n“No, madame, contrary to most history, it is true; but what you tell me, madame, what you inquire of me, is not the result of a chance query, for two years ago you asked me the same questions, and said then, that for a very long time this history of Mithridates had occupied your mind.”\n\n“True, sir. The two favorite studies of my youth were botany and mineralogy, and subsequently, when I learned that the use of simples frequently explained the whole history of a people, and the entire life of individuals in the East, as flowers betoken and symbolize a love affair, I have regretted that I was not a man, that I might have been a Flamel, a Fontana, or a Cabanis.”\n\n“And the more, madame,” said Monte Cristo, “as the Orientals do not confine themselves, as did Mithridates, to make a cuirass of his poisons, but they also made them a dagger. Science becomes, in their hands, not only a defensive weapon, but still more frequently an offensive one; the one serves against all their physical sufferings, the other against all their enemies. With opium, belladonna, brucea, snake-wood, and the cherry-laurel, they put to sleep all who stand in their way. There is not one of those women, Egyptian, Turkish, or Greek, whom here you call ‘good women,’ who do not know how, by means of chemistry, to stupefy a doctor, and in psychology to amaze a confessor.”\n\n“Really,” said Madame de Villefort, whose eyes sparkled with strange fire at this conversation.\n\n“Oh, yes, indeed, madame,” continued Monte Cristo, “the secret dramas of the East begin with a love philtre and end with a death potion—begin with paradise and end with—hell. There are as many elixirs of every kind as there are caprices and peculiarities in the physical and moral nature of humanity; and I will say further—the art of these chemists is capable with the utmost precision to accommodate and proportion the remedy and the bane to yearnings for love or desires for vengeance.”\n\n“But, sir,” remarked the young woman, “these Eastern societies, in the midst of which you have passed a portion of your existence, are as fantastic as the tales that come from their strange land. A man can easily be put out of the way there, then; it is, indeed, the Bagdad and Bassora of the _Thousand and One Nights_. The sultans and viziers who rule over society there, and who constitute what in France we call the government, are really Haroun-al-Raschids and Giaffars, who not only pardon a poisoner, but even make him a prime minister, if his crime has been an ingenious one, and who, under such circumstances, have the whole story written in letters of gold, to divert their hours of idleness and _ennui_.”\n\n“By no means, madame; the fanciful exists no longer in the East. There, disguised under other names, and concealed under other costumes, are police agents, magistrates, attorneys-general, and bailiffs. They hang, behead, and impale their criminals in the most agreeable possible manner; but some of these, like clever rogues, have contrived to escape human justice, and succeed in their fraudulent enterprises by cunning stratagems. Amongst us a simpleton, possessed by the demon of hate or cupidity, who has an enemy to destroy, or some near relation to dispose of, goes straight to the grocer’s or druggist’s, gives a false name, which leads more easily to his detection than his real one, and under the pretext that the rats prevent him from sleeping, purchases five or six grammes of arsenic—if he is really a cunning fellow, he goes to five or six different druggists or grocers, and thereby becomes only five or six times more easily traced;—then, when he has acquired his specific, he administers duly to his enemy, or near kinsman, a dose of arsenic which would make a mammoth or mastodon burst, and which, without rhyme or reason, makes his victim utter groans which alarm the entire neighborhood. Then arrive a crowd of policemen and constables. They fetch a doctor, who opens the dead body, and collects from the entrails and stomach a quantity of arsenic in a spoon. Next day a hundred newspapers relate the fact, with the names of the victim and the murderer. The same evening the grocer or grocers, druggist or druggists, come and say, ‘It was I who sold the arsenic to the gentleman;’ and rather than not recognize the guilty purchaser, they will recognize twenty. Then the foolish criminal is taken, imprisoned, interrogated, confronted, confounded, condemned, and cut off by hemp or steel; or if she be a woman of any consideration, they lock her up for life. This is the way in which you Northerns understand chemistry, madame. Desrues was, however, I must confess, more skilful.”\n\n“What would you have, sir?” said the lady, laughing; “we do what we can. All the world has not the secret of the Medicis or the Borgias.”\n\n“Now,” replied the count, shrugging his shoulders, “shall I tell you the cause of all these stupidities? It is because, at your theatres, by what at least I could judge by reading the pieces they play, they see persons swallow the contents of a phial, or suck the button of a ring, and fall dead instantly. Five minutes afterwards the curtain falls, and the spectators depart. They are ignorant of the consequences of the murder; they see neither the police commissary with his badge of office, nor the corporal with his four men; and so the poor fools believe that the whole thing is as easy as lying. But go a little way from France—go either to Aleppo or Cairo, or only to Naples or Rome, and you will see people passing by you in the streets—people erect, smiling, and fresh-colored, of whom Asmodeus, if you were holding on by the skirt of his mantle, would say, ‘That man was poisoned three weeks ago; he will be a dead man in a month.’”\n\n“Then,” remarked Madame de Villefort, “they have again discovered the secret of the famous _aqua Tofana_ that they said was lost at Perugia.”\n\n“Ah, but madame, does mankind ever lose anything? The arts change about and make a tour of the world; things take a different name, and the vulgar do not follow them—that is all; but there is always the same result. Poisons act particularly on some organ or another—one on the stomach, another on the brain, another on the intestines. Well, the poison brings on a cough, the cough an inflammation of the lungs, or some other complaint catalogued in the book of science, which, however, by no means precludes it from being decidedly mortal; and if it were not, would be sure to become so, thanks to the remedies applied by foolish doctors, who are generally bad chemists, and which will act in favor of or against the malady, as you please; and then there is a human being killed according to all the rules of art and skill, and of whom justice learns nothing, as was said by a terrible chemist of my acquaintance, the worthy Abbé Adelmonte of Taormina, in Sicily, who has studied these national phenomena very profoundly.”\n\n“It is quite frightful, but deeply interesting,” said the young lady, motionless with attention. “I thought, I must confess, that these tales, were inventions of the Middle Ages.”\n\n“Yes, no doubt, but improved upon by ours. What is the use of time, rewards of merit, medals, crosses, Monthyon prizes, if they do not lead society towards more complete perfection? Yet man will never be perfect until he learns to create and destroy; he does know how to destroy, and that is half the battle.”\n\n“So,” added Madame de Villefort, constantly returning to her object, “the poisons of the Borgias, the Medicis, the Renées, the Ruggieris, and later, probably, that of Baron de Trenck, whose story has been so misused by modern drama and romance——”\n\n“Were objects of art, madame, and nothing more,” replied the count. “Do you suppose that the real _savant_ addresses himself stupidly to the mere individual? By no means. Science loves eccentricities, leaps and bounds, trials of strength, fancies, if I may be allowed so to term them. Thus, for instance, the excellent Abbé Adelmonte, of whom I spoke just now, made in this way some marvellous experiments.”\n\n“Really?”\n\n“Yes; I will mention one to you. He had a remarkably fine garden, full of vegetables, flowers, and fruit. From amongst these vegetables he selected the most simple—a cabbage, for instance. For three days he watered this cabbage with a distillation of arsenic; on the third, the cabbage began to droop and turn yellow. At that moment he cut it. In the eyes of everybody it seemed fit for table, and preserved its wholesome appearance. It was only poisoned to the Abbé Adelmonte. He then took the cabbage to the room where he had rabbits—for the Abbé Adelmonte had a collection of rabbits, cats, and guinea-pigs, fully as fine as his collection of vegetables, flowers, and fruit. Well, the Abbé Adelmonte took a rabbit, and made it eat a leaf of the cabbage. The rabbit died. What magistrate would find, or even venture to insinuate, anything against this? What procureur has ever ventured to draw up an accusation against M. Magendie or M. Flourens, in consequence of the rabbits, cats, and guinea-pigs they have killed?—not one. So, then, the rabbit dies, and justice takes no notice. This rabbit dead, the Abbé Adelmonte has its entrails taken out by his cook and thrown on the dunghill; on this dunghill is a hen, who, pecking these intestines, is in her turn taken ill, and dies next day. At the moment when she is struggling in the convulsions of death, a vulture is flying by (there are a good many vultures in Adelmonte’s country); this bird darts on the dead fowl, and carries it away to a rock, where it dines off its prey. Three days afterwards, this poor vulture, which has been very much indisposed since that dinner, suddenly feels very giddy while flying aloft in the clouds, and falls heavily into a fish-pond. The pike, eels, and carp eat greedily always, as everybody knows—well, they feast on the vulture. Now suppose that next day, one of these eels, or pike, or carp, poisoned at the fourth remove, is served up at your table. Well, then, your guest will be poisoned at the fifth remove, and die, at the end of eight or ten days, of pains in the intestines, sickness, or abscess of the pylorus. The doctors open the body and say with an air of profound learning, ‘The subject has died of a tumor on the liver, or of typhoid fever!’”\n\n“But,” remarked Madame de Villefort, “all these circumstances which you link thus to one another may be broken by the least accident; the vulture may not see the fowl, or may fall a hundred yards from the fish-pond.”\n\n“Ah, that is where the art comes in. To be a great chemist in the East, one must direct chance; and this is to be achieved.”\n\nMadame de Villefort was in deep thought, yet listened attentively.\n\n“But,” she exclaimed, suddenly, “arsenic is indelible, indestructible; in whatsoever way it is absorbed, it will be found again in the body of the victim from the moment when it has been taken in sufficient quantity to cause death.”\n\n“Precisely so,” cried Monte Cristo—“precisely so; and this is what I said to my worthy Adelmonte. He reflected, smiled, and replied to me by a Sicilian proverb, which I believe is also a French proverb, ‘My son, the world was not made in a day—but in seven. Return on Sunday.’ On the Sunday following I did return to him. Instead of having watered his cabbage with arsenic, he had watered it this time with a solution of salts, having their basis in strychnine, _strychnos colubrina_, as the learned term it. Now, the cabbage had not the slightest appearance of disease in the world, and the rabbit had not the smallest distrust; yet, five minutes afterwards, the rabbit was dead. The fowl pecked at the rabbit, and the next day was a dead hen. This time we were the vultures; so we opened the bird, and this time all special symptoms had disappeared, there were only general symptoms. There was no peculiar indication in any organ—an excitement of the nervous system—that was it; a case of cerebral congestion—nothing more. The fowl had not been poisoned—she had died of apoplexy. Apoplexy is a rare disease among fowls, I believe, but very common among men.”\n\nMadame de Villefort appeared more and more thoughtful.\n\n“It is very fortunate,” she observed, “that such substances could only be prepared by chemists; otherwise, all the world would be poisoning each other.”\n\n“By chemists and persons who have a taste for chemistry,” said Monte Cristo carelessly.\n\n“And then,” said Madame de Villefort, endeavoring by a struggle, and with effort, to get away from her thoughts, “however skilfully it is prepared, crime is always crime, and if it avoid human scrutiny, it does not escape the eye of God. The Orientals are stronger than we are in cases of conscience, and, very prudently, have no hell—that is the point.”\n\n30075m\n“Really, madame, this is a scruple which naturally must occur to a pure mind like yours, but which would easily yield before sound reasoning. The bad side of human thought will always be defined by the paradox of Jean Jacques Rousseau,—you remember,—the mandarin who is killed five hundred leagues off by raising the tip of the finger. Man’s whole life passes in doing these things, and his intellect is exhausted by reflecting on them. You will find very few persons who will go and brutally thrust a knife in the heart of a fellow-creature, or will administer to him, in order to remove him from the surface of the globe on which we move with life and animation, that quantity of arsenic of which we just now talked. Such a thing is really out of rule—eccentric or stupid. To attain such a point, the blood must be heated to thirty-six degrees, the pulse be, at least, at ninety, and the feelings excited beyond the ordinary limit. But suppose one pass, as is permissible in philology, from the word itself to its softened synonym, then, instead of committing an ignoble assassination you make an ‘elimination;’ you merely and simply remove from your path the individual who is in your way, and that without shock or violence, without the display of the sufferings which, in the case of becoming a punishment, make a martyr of the victim, and a butcher, in every sense of the word, of him who inflicts them. Then there will be no blood, no groans, no convulsions, and above all, no consciousness of that horrid and compromising moment of accomplishing the act,—then one escapes the clutch of the human law, which says, ‘Do not disturb society!’ This is the mode in which they manage these things, and succeed in Eastern climes, where there are grave and phlegmatic persons who care very little for the questions of time in conjunctures of importance.”\n\n“Yet conscience remains,” remarked Madame de Villefort in an agitated voice, and with a stifled sigh.\n\n“Yes,” answered Monte Cristo “happily, yes, conscience does remain; and if it did not, how wretched we should be! After every action requiring exertion, it is conscience that saves us, for it supplies us with a thousand good excuses, of which we alone are judges; and these reasons, howsoever excellent in producing sleep, would avail us but very little before a tribunal, when we were tried for our lives. Thus Richard III., for instance, was marvellously served by his conscience after the putting away of the two children of Edward IV.; in fact, he could say, ‘These two children of a cruel and persecuting king, who have inherited the vices of their father, which I alone could perceive in their juvenile propensities—these two children are impediments in my way of promoting the happiness of the English people, whose unhappiness they (the children) would infallibly have caused.’ Thus was Lady Macbeth served by her conscience, when she sought to give her son, and not her husband (whatever Shakespeare may say), a throne. Ah, maternal love is a great virtue, a powerful motive—so powerful that it excuses a multitude of things, even if, after Duncan’s death, Lady Macbeth had been at all pricked by her conscience.”\n\nMadame de Villefort listened with avidity to these appalling maxims and horrible paradoxes, delivered by the count with that ironical simplicity which was peculiar to him.\n\nAfter a moment’s silence, the lady inquired:\n\n“Do you know, my dear count,” she said, “that you are a very terrible reasoner, and that you look at the world through a somewhat distempered medium? Have you really measured the world by scrutinies, or through alembics and crucibles? For you must indeed be a great chemist, and the elixir you administered to my son, which recalled him to life almost instantaneously——”\n\n30077m\n“Oh, do not place any reliance on that, madame; _one_ drop of that elixir sufficed to recall life to a dying child, but three drops would have impelled the blood into his lungs in such a way as to have produced most violent palpitations; six would have suspended his respiration, and caused syncope more serious than that in which he was; ten would have destroyed him. You know, madame, how suddenly I snatched him from those phials which he so imprudently touched?”\n\n“Is it then so terrible a poison?”\n\n“Oh, no! In the first place, let us agree that the word poison does not exist, because in medicine use is made of the most violent poisons, which become, according as they are employed, most salutary remedies.”\n\n“What, then, is it?”\n\n“A skilful preparation of my friend’s the worthy Abbé Adelmonte, who taught me the use of it.”\n\n“Oh,” observed Madame de Villefort, “it must be an admirable anti-spasmodic.”\n\n“Perfect, madame, as you have seen,” replied the count; “and I frequently make use of it—with all possible prudence though, be it observed,” he added with a smile of intelligence.\n\n“Most assuredly,” responded Madame de Villefort in the same tone. “As for me, so nervous, and so subject to fainting fits, I should require a Doctor Adelmonte to invent for me some means of breathing freely and tranquillizing my mind, in the fear I have of dying some fine day of suffocation. In the meanwhile, as the thing is difficult to find in France, and your abbé is not probably disposed to make a journey to Paris on my account, I must continue to use Monsieur Planche’s anti-spasmodics; and mint and Hoffman’s drops are among my favorite remedies. Here are some lozenges which I have made up on purpose; they are compounded doubly strong.”\n\nMonte Cristo opened the tortoise-shell box, which the lady presented to him, and inhaled the odor of the lozenges with the air of an amateur who thoroughly appreciated their composition.\n\n“They are indeed exquisite,” he said; “but as they are necessarily submitted to the process of deglutition—a function which it is frequently impossible for a fainting person to accomplish—I prefer my own specific.”\n\n“Undoubtedly, and so should I prefer it, after the effects I have seen produced; but of course it is a secret, and I am not so indiscreet as to ask it of you.”\n\n“But I,” said Monte Cristo, rising as he spoke—“I am gallant enough to offer it you.”\n\n30079m\n“How kind you are.”\n\n“Only remember one thing—a small dose is a remedy, a large one is poison. One drop will restore life, as you have seen; five or six will inevitably kill, and in a way the more terrible inasmuch as, poured into a glass of wine, it would not in the slightest degree affect its flavor. But I say no more, madame; it is really as if I were prescribing for you.”\n\nThe clock struck half-past six, and a lady was announced, a friend of Madame de Villefort, who came to dine with her.\n\n“If I had had the honor of seeing you for the third or fourth time, count, instead of only for the second,” said Madame de Villefort; “if I had had the honor of being your friend, instead of only having the happiness of being under an obligation to you, I should insist on detaining you to dinner, and not allow myself to be daunted by a first refusal.”\n\n“A thousand thanks, madame,” replied Monte Cristo “but I have an engagement which I cannot break. I have promised to escort to the Académie a Greek princess of my acquaintance who has never seen your grand opera, and who relies on me to conduct her thither.”\n\n“Adieu, then, sir, and do not forget the prescription.”\n\n“Ah, in truth, madame, to do that I must forget the hour’s conversation I have had with you, which is indeed impossible.”\n\nMonte Cristo bowed, and left the house. Madame de Villefort remained immersed in thought.\n\n“He is a very strange man,” she said, “and in my opinion is himself the Adelmonte he talks about.”\n\nAs to Monte Cristo the result had surpassed his utmost expectations.\n\n“Good,” said he, as he went away; “this is a fruitful soil, and I feel certain that the seed sown will not be cast on barren ground.”\n\nNext morning, faithful to his promise, he sent the prescription requested.\n\n# Chapter 53. Robert le Diable\n\nThe pretext of an opera engagement was so much the more feasible, as there chanced to be on that very night a more than ordinary attraction at the Académie Royale. Levasseur, who had been suffering under severe illness, made his reappearance in the character of _Bertram_, and, as usual, the announcement of the most admired production of the favorite composer of the day had attracted a brilliant and fashionable audience. Morcerf, like most other young men of rank and fortune, had his orchestra stall, with the certainty of always finding a seat in at least a dozen of the principal boxes occupied by persons of his acquaintance; he had, moreover, his right of entry into the omnibus box. Château-Renaud rented a stall beside his own, while Beauchamp, as a journalist, had unlimited range all over the theatre. It happened that on this particular night the minister’s box was placed at the disposal of Lucien Debray, who offered it to the Comte de Morcerf, who again, upon his rejection of it by Mercédès, sent it to Danglars, with an intimation that he should probably do himself the honor of joining the baroness and her daughter during the evening, in the event of their accepting the box in question. The ladies received the offer with too much pleasure to dream of a refusal. To no class of persons is the presentation of a gratuitous opera-box more acceptable than to the wealthy millionaire, who still hugs economy while boasting of carrying a king’s ransom in his waistcoat pocket.\n\nDanglars had, however, protested against showing himself in a ministerial box, declaring that his political principles, and his parliamentary position as member of the opposition party would not permit him so to commit himself; the baroness had, therefore, despatched a note to Lucien Debray, bidding him call for them, it being wholly impossible for her to go alone with Eugénie to the opera.\n\nThere is no gainsaying the fact that a very unfavorable construction would have been put upon the circumstance if the two women had gone without escort, while the addition of a third, in the person of her mother’s admitted lover, enabled Mademoiselle Danglars to defy malice and ill-nature. One must take the world as one finds it.\n\n30083m\nThe curtain rose, as usual, to an almost empty house, it being one of the absurdities of Parisian fashion never to appear at the opera until after the beginning of the performance, so that the first act is generally played without the slightest attention being paid to it, that part of the audience already assembled being too much occupied in observing the fresh arrivals, while nothing is heard but the noise of opening and shutting doors, and the buzz of conversation.\n\n“Surely,” said Albert, as the door of a box on the first circle opened, “that must be the Countess G——.”\n\n“And who is the Countess G——?” inquired Château-Renaud.\n\n“What a question! Now, do you know, baron, I have a great mind to pick a quarrel with you for asking it; as if all the world did not know who the Countess G—— was.”\n\n“Ah, to be sure,” replied Château-Renaud; “the lovely Venetian, is it not?”\n\n“Herself.” At this moment the countess perceived Albert, and returned his salutation with a smile.\n\n“You know her, it seems?” said Château-Renaud.\n\n“Franz introduced me to her at Rome,” replied Albert.\n\n“Well, then, will you do as much for me in Paris as Franz did for you in Rome?”\n\n“With pleasure.”\n\nThere was a cry of “Shut up!” from the audience. This manifestation on the part of the spectators of their wish to be allowed to hear the music, produced not the slightest effect on the two young men, who continued their conversation.\n\n“The countess was present at the races in the Champ-de-Mars,” said Château-Renaud.\n\n“Today?”\n\n“Yes.”\n“Bless me, I quite forgot the races. Did you bet?”\n\n“Oh, merely a paltry fifty louis.”\n“And who was the winner?”\n\n“Nautilus. I staked on him.”\n\n“But there were three races, were there not?”\n\n“Yes; there was the prize given by the Jockey Club—a gold cup, you know—and a very singular circumstance occurred about that race.”\n\n“What was it?”\n\n“Oh, shut up!” again interposed some of the audience.\n\n“Why, it was won by a horse and rider utterly unknown on the course.”\n\n“Is that possible?”\n\n“True as day. The fact was, nobody had observed a horse entered by the name of Vampa, or that of a jockey styled Job, when, at the last moment, a splendid roan, mounted by a jockey about as big as your fist, presented themselves at the starting-post. They were obliged to stuff at least twenty pounds weight of shot in the small rider’s pockets, to make him weight; but with all that he outstripped Ariel and Barbare, against whom he ran, by at least three whole lengths.”\n\n“And was it not found out at last to whom the horse and jockey belonged?”\n“No.”\n\n“You say that the horse was entered under the name of Vampa?”\n\n“Exactly; that was the title.”\n\n“Then,” answered Albert, “I am better informed than you are, and know who the owner of that horse was.”\n\n“Shut up, there!” cried the pit in chorus. And this time the tone and manner in which the command was given, betokened such growing hostility that the two young men perceived, for the first time, that the mandate was addressed to them. Leisurely turning round, they calmly scrutinized the various countenances around them, as though demanding some one person who would take upon himself the responsibility of what they deemed excessive impertinence; but as no one responded to the challenge, the friends turned again to the front of the theatre, and affected to busy themselves with the stage. At this moment the door of the minister’s box opened, and Madame Danglars, accompanied by her daughter, entered, escorted by Lucien Debray, who assiduously conducted them to their seats.\n\n“Ha, ha,” said Château-Renaud, “here come some friends of yours, viscount! What are you looking at there? don’t you see they are trying to catch your eye?”\n\nAlbert turned round, just in time to receive a gracious wave of the fan from the baroness; as for Mademoiselle Eugénie, she scarcely vouchsafed to waste the glances of her large black eyes even upon the business of the stage.\n\n“I tell you what, my dear fellow,” said Château-Renaud, “I cannot imagine what objection you can possibly have to Mademoiselle Danglars—that is, setting aside her want of ancestry and somewhat inferior rank, which by the way I don’t think you care very much about. Now, barring all that, I mean to say she is a deuced fine girl!”\n\n“Handsome, certainly,” replied Albert, “but not to my taste, which I confess, inclines to something softer, gentler, and more feminine.”\n\n“Ah, well,” exclaimed Château-Renaud, who because he had seen his thirtieth summer fancied himself duly warranted in assuming a sort of paternal air with his more youthful friend, “you young people are never satisfied; why, what would you have more? your parents have chosen you a bride built on the model of Diana, the huntress, and yet you are not content.”\n\n“No, for that very resemblance affrights me; I should have liked something more in the manner of the Venus of Milo or Capua; but this chase-loving Diana, continually surrounded by her nymphs, gives me a sort of alarm lest she should some day bring on me the fate of Actæon.”\n\nAnd, indeed, it required but one glance at Mademoiselle Danglars to comprehend the justness of Morcerf’s remark. She was beautiful, but her beauty was of too marked and decided a character to please a fastidious taste; her hair was raven black, but its natural waves seemed somewhat rebellious; her eyes, of the same color as her hair, were surmounted by well-arched brows, whose great defect, however, consisted in an almost habitual frown, while her whole physiognomy wore that expression of firmness and decision so little in accordance with the gentler attributes of her sex—her nose was precisely what a sculptor would have chosen for a chiselled Juno. Her mouth, which might have been found fault with as too large, displayed teeth of pearly whiteness, rendered still more conspicuous by the brilliant carmine of her lips, contrasting vividly with her naturally pale complexion. But that which completed the almost masculine look Morcerf found so little to his taste, was a dark mole, of much larger dimensions than these freaks of nature generally are, placed just at the corner of her mouth; and the effect tended to increase the expression of self-dependence that characterized her countenance.\n\nThe rest of Mademoiselle Eugénie’s person was in perfect keeping with the head just described; she, indeed, reminded one of Diana, as Château-Renaud observed, but her bearing was more haughty and resolute.\n\nAs regarded her attainments, the only fault to be found with them was the same that a fastidious connoisseur might have found with her beauty, that they were somewhat too erudite and masculine for so young a person. She was a perfect linguist, a first-rate artist, wrote poetry, and composed music; to the study of the latter she professed to be entirely devoted, following it with an indefatigable perseverance, assisted by a schoolfellow,—a young woman without fortune whose talent promised to develop into remarkable powers as a singer. It was rumored that she was an object of almost paternal interest to one of the principal composers of the day, who excited her to spare no pains in the cultivation of her voice, which might hereafter prove a source of wealth and independence. But this counsel effectually decided Mademoiselle Danglars never to commit herself by being seen in public with one destined for a theatrical life; and acting upon this principle, the banker’s daughter, though perfectly willing to allow Mademoiselle Louise d’Armilly (that was the name of the young virtuosa) to practice with her through the day, took especial care not to be seen in her company. Still, though not actually received at the Hôtel Danglars in the light of an acknowledged friend, Louise was treated with far more kindness and consideration than is usually bestowed on a governess.\n\nThe curtain fell almost immediately after the entrance of Madame Danglars into her box, the band quitted the orchestra for the accustomed half-hour’s interval allowed between the acts, and the audience were left at liberty to promenade the salon or lobbies, or to pay and receive visits in their respective boxes.\n\nMorcerf and Château-Renaud were amongst the first to avail themselves of this permission. For an instant the idea struck Madame Danglars that this eagerness on the part of the young viscount arose from his impatience to join her party, and she whispered her expectations to her daughter, that Albert was hurrying to pay his respects to them. Mademoiselle Eugénie, however, merely returned a dissenting movement of the head, while, with a cold smile, she directed the attention of her mother to an opposite box on the first circle, in which sat the Countess G——, and where Morcerf had just made his appearance.\n30087m\n\n“So we meet again, my travelling friend, do we?” cried the countess, extending her hand to him with all the warmth and cordiality of an old acquaintance; “it was really very good of you to recognize me so quickly, and still more so to bestow your first visit on me.”\n\n“Be assured,” replied Albert, “that if I had been aware of your arrival in Paris, and had known your address, I should have paid my respects to you before this. Allow me to introduce my friend, Baron de Château-Renaud, one of the few true gentlemen now to be found in France, and from whom I have just learned that you were a spectator of the races in the Champ-de-Mars, yesterday.”\n\nChâteau-Renaud bowed to the countess.\n\n“So you were at the races, baron?” inquired the countess eagerly.\n\n“Yes, madame.”\n\n“Well, then,” pursued Madame G—— with considerable animation, “you can probably tell me who won the Jockey Club stakes?”\n\n“I am sorry to say I cannot,” replied the baron; “and I was just asking the same question of Albert.”\n\n“Are you very anxious to know, countess?” asked Albert.\n\n“To know what?”\n\n“The name of the owner of the winning horse?”\n\n“Excessively; only imagine—but do tell me, viscount, whether you really are acquainted with it or no?”\n\n“I beg your pardon, madame, but you were about to relate some story, were you not? You said, ‘only imagine,’—and then paused. Pray continue.”\n\n“Well, then, listen. You must know I felt so interested in the splendid roan horse, with his elegant little rider, so tastefully dressed in a pink satin jacket and cap, that I could not help praying for their success with as much earnestness as though the half of my fortune were at stake; and when I saw them outstrip all the others, and come to the winning-post in such gallant style, I actually clapped my hands with joy. Imagine my surprise, when, upon returning home, the first object I met on the staircase was the identical jockey in the pink jacket! I concluded that, by some singular chance, the owner of the winning horse must live in the same hotel as myself; but, as I entered my apartments, I beheld the very gold cup awarded as a prize to the unknown horse and rider. Inside the cup was a small piece of paper, on which were written these words—‘From Lord Ruthven to Countess G——.’”\n\n“Precisely; I was sure of it,” said Morcerf.\n\n“Sure of what?”\n\n“That the owner of the horse was Lord Ruthven himself.”\n\n“What Lord Ruthven do you mean?”\n\n“Why, our Lord Ruthven—the Vampire of the Salle Argentina!”\n\n“Is it possible?” exclaimed the countess; “is he here in Paris?”\n\n“To be sure,—why not?”\n\n“And you visit him?—meet him at your own house and elsewhere?”\n\n“I assure you he is my most intimate friend, and M. de Château-Renaud has also the honor of his acquaintance.”\n\n“But why are you so sure of his being the winner of the Jockey Club prize?”\n\n“Was not the winning horse entered by the name of Vampa?”\n\n“What of that?”\n\n“Why, do you not recollect the name of the celebrated bandit by whom I was made prisoner?”\n\n“Oh, yes.”\n\n“And from whose hands the count extricated me in so wonderful a manner?”\n\n“To be sure, I remember it all now.”\n\n“He called himself Vampa. You see, it’s evident where the count got the name.”\n\n“But what could have been his motive for sending the cup to me?”\n\n“In the first place, because I had spoken much of you to him, as you may believe; and in the second, because he delighted to see a countrywoman take so lively an interest in his success.”\n\n“I trust and hope you never repeated to the count all the foolish remarks we used to make about him?”\n\n“I should not like to affirm upon oath that I have not. Besides, his presenting you the cup under the name of Lord Ruthven——”\n\n“Oh, but that is dreadful! Why, the man must owe me a fearful grudge.”\n\n“Does his action appear like that of an enemy?”\n\n“No; certainly not.”\n\n“Well, then——”\n\n“And so he is in Paris?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“And what effect does he produce?”\n\n“Why,” said Albert, “he was talked about for a week; then the coronation of the queen of England took place, followed by the theft of Mademoiselle Mars’s diamonds; and so people talked of something else.”\n\n“My good fellow,” said Château-Renaud, “the count is your friend and you treat him accordingly. Do not believe what Albert is telling you, countess; so far from the sensation excited in the Parisian circles by the appearance of the Count of Monte Cristo having abated, I take upon myself to declare that it is as strong as ever. His first astounding act upon coming amongst us was to present a pair of horses, worth 32,000 francs, to Madame Danglars; his second, the almost miraculous preservation of Madame de Villefort’s life; now it seems that he has carried off the prize awarded by the Jockey Club. I therefore maintain, in spite of Morcerf, that not only is the count the object of interest at this present moment, but also that he will continue to be so for a month longer if he pleases to exhibit an eccentricity of conduct which, after all, may be his ordinary mode of existence.”\n\n“Perhaps you are right,” said Morcerf; “meanwhile, who is in the Russian ambassador’s box?”\n\n“Which box do you mean?” asked the countess.\n\n“The one between the pillars on the first tier—it seems to have been fitted up entirely afresh.”\n\n“Did you observe anyone during the first act?” asked Château-Renaud.\n\n“Where?”\n“In that box.”\n\n“No,” replied the countess, “it was certainly empty during the first act;” then, resuming the subject of their previous conversation, she said, “And so you really believe it was your mysterious Count of Monte Cristo that gained the prize?”\n\n“I am sure of it.”\n\n“And who afterwards sent the cup to me?”\n\n“Undoubtedly.”\n\n“But I don’t know him,” said the countess; “I have a great mind to return it.”\n\n“Do no such thing, I beg of you; he would only send you another, formed of a magnificent sapphire, or hollowed out of a gigantic ruby. It is his way, and you must take him as you find him.”\n\nAt this moment the bell rang to announce the drawing up of the curtain for the second act. Albert rose to return to his place.\n\n“Shall I see you again?” asked the countess.\n\n“At the end of the next act, with your permission, I will come and inquire whether there is anything I can do for you in Paris?”\n\n“Pray take notice,” said the countess, “that my present residence is 22 Rue de Rivoli, and that I am at home to my friends every Saturday evening. So now, you are both forewarned.”\n\nThe young men bowed, and quitted the box. Upon reaching their stalls, they found the whole of the audience in the parterre standing up and directing their gaze towards the box formerly possessed by the Russian ambassador. A man of from thirty-five to forty years of age, dressed in deep black, had just entered, accompanied by a young woman dressed after the Eastern style. The lady was surpassingly beautiful, while the rich magnificence of her attire drew all eyes upon her.\n\n“Hullo,” said Albert; “it is Monte Cristo and his Greek!”\n\nThe strangers were, indeed, no other than the count and Haydée. In a few moments the young girl had attracted the attention of the whole house, and even the occupants of the boxes leaned forward to scrutinize her magnificent diamonds.\n\nThe second act passed away during one continued buzz of voices—one deep whisper—intimating that some great and universally interesting event had occurred; all eyes, all thoughts, were occupied with the young and beautiful woman, whose gorgeous apparel and splendid jewels made a most extraordinary spectacle.\n\nUpon this occasion an unmistakable sign from Madame Danglars intimated her desire to see Albert in her box directly the curtain fell on the second act, and neither the politeness nor good taste of Morcerf would permit his neglecting an invitation so unequivocally given. At the close of the act he therefore went to the baroness.\n\nHaving bowed to the two ladies, he extended his hand to Debray. By the baroness he was most graciously welcomed, while Eugénie received him with her accustomed coldness.\n\n“My dear fellow,” said Debray, “you have come in the nick of time. There is madame overwhelming me with questions respecting the count; she insists upon it that I can tell her his birth, education, and parentage, where he came from, and whither he is going. Being no disciple of Cagliostro, I was wholly unable to do this; so, by way of getting out of the scrape, I said, ‘Ask Morcerf; he has got the whole history of his beloved Monte Cristo at his fingers’ ends;’ whereupon the baroness signified her desire to see you.”\n\n“Is it not almost incredible,” said Madame Danglars, “that a person having at least half a million of secret-service money at his command, should possess so little information?”\n\n“Let me assure you, madame,” said Lucien, “that had I really the sum you mention at my disposal, I would employ it more profitably than in troubling myself to obtain particulars respecting the Count of Monte Cristo, whose only merit in my eyes consists in his being twice as rich as a nabob. However, I have turned the business over to Morcerf, so pray settle it with him as may be most agreeable to you; for my own part, I care nothing about the count or his mysterious doings.”\n30093m\n\n“I am very sure no nabob would have sent me a pair of horses worth 32,000 francs, wearing on their heads four diamonds valued at 5,000 francs each.”\n\n“He seems to have a mania for diamonds,” said Morcerf, smiling, “and I verily believe that, like Potemkin, he keeps his pockets filled, for the sake of strewing them along the road, as Tom Thumb did his flint stones.”\n\n“Perhaps he has discovered some mine,” said Madame Danglars. “I suppose you know he has an order for unlimited credit on the baron’s banking establishment?”\n\n“I was not aware of it,” replied Albert, “but I can readily believe it.”\n\n“And, further, that he stated to M. Danglars his intention of only staying a year in Paris, during which time he proposed to spend six millions.\n\n“He must be the Shah of Persia, travelling _incog_.”\n\n“Have you noticed the remarkable beauty of the young woman, M. Lucien?” inquired Eugénie.\n\n“I really never met with one woman so ready to do justice to the charms of another as yourself,” responded Lucien, raising his lorgnette to his eye. “A most lovely creature, upon my soul!” was his verdict.\n\n“Who is this young person, M. de Morcerf?” inquired Eugénie; “does anybody know?”\n\n“Mademoiselle,” said Albert, replying to this direct appeal, “I can give you very exact information on that subject, as well as on most points relative to the mysterious person of whom we are now conversing—the young woman is a Greek.”\n\n“So I should suppose by her dress; if you know no more than that, everyone here is as well-informed as yourself.”\n\n“I am extremely sorry you find me so ignorant a _cicerone_,” replied Morcerf, “but I am reluctantly obliged to confess, I have nothing further to communicate—yes, stay, I do know one thing more, namely, that she is a musician, for one day when I chanced to be breakfasting with the count, I heard the sound of a guzla—it is impossible that it could have been touched by any other finger than her own.”\n\n“Then your count entertains visitors, does he?” asked Madame Danglars.\n\n“Indeed he does, and in a most lavish manner, I can assure you.”\n\n“I must try and persuade M. Danglars to invite him to a ball or dinner, or something of the sort, that he may be compelled to ask us in return.”\n\n“What,” said Debray, laughing; “do you really mean you would go to his house?”\n\n“Why not? my husband could accompany me.”\n\n“But do you know this mysterious count is a bachelor?”\n\n“You have ample proof to the contrary, if you look opposite,” said the baroness, as she laughingly pointed to the beautiful Greek.\n\n“No, no!” exclaimed Debray; “that girl is not his wife: he told us himself she was his slave. Do you not recollect, Morcerf, his telling us so at your breakfast?”\n\n“Well, then,” said the baroness, “if slave she be, she has all the air and manner of a princess.”\n\n“Of the ‘Arabian Nights’.”\n\n“If you like; but tell me, my dear Lucien, what it is that constitutes a princess. Why, diamonds—and she is covered with them.”\n\n“To me she seems overloaded,” observed Eugénie; “she would look far better if she wore fewer, and we should then be able to see her finely formed throat and wrists.”\n\n“See how the artist peeps out!” exclaimed Madame Danglars. “My poor Eugénie, you must conceal your passion for the fine arts.”\n\n“I admire all that is beautiful,” returned the young lady.\n\n“What do you think of the count?” inquired Debray; “he is not much amiss, according to my ideas of good looks.”\n\n“The count,” repeated Eugénie, as though it had not occurred to her to observe him sooner; “the count?—oh, he is so dreadfully pale.”\n\n“I quite agree with you,” said Morcerf; “and the secret of that very pallor is what we want to find out. The Countess G—— insists upon it that he is a vampire.”\n\n“Then the Countess G—— has returned to Paris, has she?” inquired the baroness.\n\n“Is that she, mamma?” asked Eugénie; “almost opposite to us, with that profusion of beautiful light hair?”\n\n“Yes,” said Madame Danglars, “that is she. Shall I tell you what you ought to do, Morcerf?”\n\n“Command me, madame.”\n\n“Well, then, you should go and bring your Count of Monte Cristo to us.”\n\n“What for?” asked Eugénie.\n\n“What for? Why, to converse with him, of course. Have you really no desire to meet him?”\n\n“None whatever,” replied Eugénie.\n\n“Strange child,” murmured the baroness.\n\n“He will very probably come of his own accord,” said Morcerf. “There; do you see, madame, he recognizes you, and bows.”\n\nThe baroness returned the salute in the most smiling and graceful manner.\n\n“Well,” said Morcerf, “I may as well be magnanimous, and tear myself away to forward your wishes. Adieu; I will go and try if there are any means of speaking to him.”\n\n“Go straight to his box; that will be the simplest plan.”\n\n“But I have never been presented.”\n\n“Presented to whom?”\n\n“To the beautiful Greek.”\n\n“You say she is only a slave?”\n\n“While you assert that she is a queen, or at least a princess. No; I hope that when he sees me leave you, he will come out.”\n\n“That is possible—go.”\n\n“I am going,” said Albert, as he made his parting bow.\n\nJust as he was passing the count’s box, the door opened, and Monte Cristo came forth. After giving some directions to Ali, who stood in the lobby, the count took Albert’s arm. Carefully closing the box door, Ali placed himself before it, while a crowd of spectators assembled round the Nubian.\n\n“Upon my word,” said Monte Cristo, “Paris is a strange city, and the Parisians a very singular people. See that cluster of persons collected around poor Ali, who is as much astonished as themselves; really one might suppose he was the only Nubian they had ever beheld. Now I can promise you, that a Frenchman might show himself in public, either in Tunis, Constantinople, Bagdad, or Cairo, without being treated in that way.”\n\n“That shows that the Eastern nations have too much good sense to waste their time and attention on objects undeserving of either. However, as far as Ali is concerned, I can assure you, the interest he excites is merely from the circumstance of his being your attendant—you, who are at this moment the most celebrated and fashionable person in Paris.”\n\n“Really? and what has procured me so flattering a distinction?”\n\n“What? why, yourself, to be sure! You give away horses worth a thousand louis; you save the lives of ladies of high rank and beauty; under the name of Major Black you run thoroughbreds ridden by tiny urchins not larger than marmots; then, when you have carried off the golden trophy of victory, instead of setting any value on it, you give it to the first handsome woman you think of!”\n\n“And who has filled your head with all this nonsense?”\n\n“Why, in the first place, I heard it from Madame Danglars, who, by the by, is dying to see you in her box, or to have you seen there by others; secondly, I learned it from Beauchamp’s journal; and thirdly, from my own imagination. Why, if you sought concealment, did you call your horse Vampa?”\n\n“That was an oversight, certainly,” replied the count; “but tell me, does the Count of Morcerf never visit the Opera? I have been looking for him, but without success.”\n\n“He will be here tonight.”\n\n“In what part of the house?”\n\n“In the baroness’s box, I believe.”\n\n“That charming young woman with her is her daughter?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“I congratulate you.”\n\nMorcerf smiled.\n\n“We will discuss that subject at length some future time,” said he. “But what do you think of the music?”\n\n“What music?”\n\n“Why, the music you have been listening to.”\n\n“Oh, it is well enough as the production of a human composer, sung by featherless bipeds, to quote the late Diogenes.”\n\n“From which it would seem, my dear count, that you can at pleasure enjoy the seraphic strains that proceed from the seven choirs of paradise?”\n30097m\n\n“You are right, in some degree; when I wish to listen to sounds more exquisitely attuned to melody than mortal ear ever yet listened to, I go to sleep.”\n\n“Then sleep here, my dear count. The conditions are favorable; what else was opera invented for?”\n\n“No, thank you. Your orchestra is too noisy. To sleep after the manner I speak of, absolute calm and silence are necessary, and then a certain preparation——”\n\n“I know—the famous hashish!”\n\n“Precisely. So, my dear viscount, whenever you wish to be regaled with music come and sup with me.”\n\n“I have already enjoyed that treat when breakfasting with you,” said Morcerf.\n\n“Do you mean at Rome?”\n\n“I do.”\n\n“Ah, then, I suppose you heard Haydée’s guzla; the poor exile frequently beguiles a weary hour in playing over to me the airs of her native land.”\n\nMorcerf did not pursue the subject, and Monte Cristo himself fell into a silent reverie.\n\nThe bell rang at this moment for the rising of the curtain.\n\n“You will excuse my leaving you,” said the count, turning in the direction of his box.\n\n“What? Are you going?”\n\n“Pray, say everything that is kind to Countess G—— on the part of her friend the vampire.”\n\n“And what message shall I convey to the baroness!”\n\n“That, with her permission, I shall do myself the honor of paying my respects in the course of the evening.”\n\nThe third act had begun; and during its progress the Count of Morcerf, according to his promise, made his appearance in the box of Madame Danglars. The Count of Morcerf was not a person to excite either interest or curiosity in a place of public amusement; his presence, therefore, was wholly unnoticed, save by the occupants of the box in which he had just seated himself.\n\nThe quick eye of Monte Cristo however, marked his coming; and a slight though meaning smile passed over his lips. Haydée, whose soul seemed centred in the business of the stage, like all unsophisticated natures, delighted in whatever addressed itself to the eye or ear.\n\n30099m\nThe third act passed off as usual. Mesdemoiselles Noblet, Julia, and Leroux executed the customary pirouettes; Robert duly challenged the Prince of Granada; and the royal father of the princess Isabella, taking his daughter by the hand, swept round the stage with majestic strides, the better to display the rich folds of his velvet robe and mantle. After which the curtain again fell, and the spectators poured forth from the theatre into the lobbies and salon.\n\nThe count left his box, and a moment later was saluting the Baronne Danglars, who could not restrain a cry of mingled pleasure and surprise.\n\n“You are welcome, count!” she exclaimed, as he entered. “I have been most anxious to see you, that I might repeat orally the thanks writing can so ill express.”\n\n“Surely so trifling a circumstance cannot deserve a place in your remembrance. Believe me, madame, I had entirely forgotten it.”\n\n“But it is not so easy to forget, monsieur, that the very next day after your princely gift you saved the life of my dear friend, Madame de Villefort, which was endangered by the very animals your generosity restored to me.”\n30101m\n\n“This time, at least, I do not deserve your thanks. It was Ali, my Nubian slave, who rendered this service to Madame de Villefort.”\n\n“Was it Ali,” asked the Count of Morcerf, “who rescued my son from the hands of bandits?”\n\n“No, count,” replied Monte Cristo taking the hand held out to him by the general; “in this instance I may fairly and freely accept your thanks; but you have already tendered them, and fully discharged your debt—if indeed there existed one—and I feel almost mortified to find you still reverting to the subject. May I beg of you, baroness, to honor me with an introduction to your daughter?”\n\n“Oh, you are no stranger—at least not by name,” replied Madame Danglars, “and the last two or three days we have really talked of nothing but you. Eugénie,” continued the baroness, turning towards her daughter, “this is the Count of Monte Cristo.”\n\nThe count bowed, while Mademoiselle Danglars bent her head slightly.\n\n“You have a charming young person with you tonight, count,” said Eugénie. “Is she your daughter?”\n\n“No, mademoiselle,” said Monte Cristo, astonished at the coolness and freedom of the question. “She is a poor unfortunate Greek left under my care.”\n\n“And what is her name?”\n\n“Haydée,” replied Monte Cristo.\n\n“A Greek?” murmured the Count of Morcerf.\n\n“Yes, indeed, count,” said Madame Danglars; “and tell me, did you ever see at the court of Ali Tepelini, whom you so gloriously and valiantly served, a more exquisite beauty or richer costume?”\n\n“Did I hear rightly, monsieur,” said Monte Cristo “that you served at Yanina?”\n\n“I was inspector-general of the pasha’s troops,” replied Morcerf; “and it is no secret that I owe my fortune, such as it is, to the liberality of the illustrious Albanese chief.”\n\n“But look!” exclaimed Madame Danglars.\n\n“Where?” stammered Morcerf.\n\n“There,” said Monte Cristo placing his arms around the count, and leaning with him over the front of the box, just as Haydée, whose eyes were occupied in examining the theatre in search of her guardian, perceived his pale features close to Morcerf’s face. It was as if the young girl beheld the head of Medusa. She bent forwards as though to assure herself of the reality of what she saw, then, uttering a faint cry, threw herself back in her seat. The sound was heard by the people about Ali, who instantly opened the box-door.\n\n“Why, count,” exclaimed Eugénie, “what has happened to your ward? she seems to have been taken suddenly ill.\n\n“Very probably,” answered the count. “But do not be alarmed on her account. Haydée’s nervous system is delicately organized, and she is peculiarly susceptible to the odors even of flowers—nay, there are some which cause her to faint if brought into her presence. However,” continued Monte Cristo, drawing a small phial from his pocket, “I have an infallible remedy.”\n\nSo saying, he bowed to the baroness and her daughter, exchanged a parting shake of the hand with Debray and the count, and left Madame Danglars’ box. Upon his return to Haydée he found her still very pale. As soon as she saw him she seized his hand; her own hands were moist and icy cold.\n\n“Who was it you were talking with over there?” she asked.\n\n“With the Count of Morcerf,” answered Monte Cristo. “He tells me he served your illustrious father, and that he owes his fortune to him.”\n\n“Wretch!” exclaimed Haydée, her eyes flashing with rage; “he sold my father to the Turks, and the fortune he boasts of was the price of his treachery! Did not you know that, my dear lord?”\n\n“Something of this I heard in Epirus,” said Monte Cristo; “but the particulars are still unknown to me. You shall relate them to me, my child. They are, no doubt, both curious and interesting.”\n\n“Yes, yes; but let us go. I feel as though it would kill me to remain long near that dreadful man.”\n\nSo saying, Haydée arose, and wrapping herself in her burnouse of white cashmere embroidered with pearls and coral, she hastily quitted the box at the moment when the curtain was rising upon the fourth act.\n\n“Do you observe,” said the Countess G—— to Albert, who had returned to her side, “that man does nothing like other people; he listens most devoutly to the third act of _Robert le Diable_, and when the fourth begins, takes his departure.”\n\n# Chapter 54. A Flurry in Stocks\n\nSome days after this meeting, Albert de Morcerf visited the Count of Monte Cristo at his house in the Champs-Élysées, which had already assumed that palace-like appearance which the count’s princely fortune enabled him to give even to his most temporary residences. He came to renew the thanks of Madame Danglars which had been already conveyed to the count through the medium of a letter, signed “Baronne Danglars, _née_ Hermine de Servieux.”\n\nAlbert was accompanied by Lucien Debray, who, joining in his friend’s conversation, added some passing compliments, the source of which the count’s talent for finesse easily enabled him to guess. He was convinced that Lucien’s visit was due to a double feeling of curiosity, the larger half of which sentiment emanated from the Rue de la Chaussée d’Antin. In short, Madame Danglars, not being able personally to examine in detail the domestic economy and household arrangements of a man who gave away horses worth 30,000 francs and who went to the opera with a Greek slave wearing diamonds to the amount of a million of money, had deputed those eyes, by which she was accustomed to see, to give her a faithful account of the mode of life of this incomprehensible person. But the count did not appear to suspect that there could be the slightest connection between Lucien’s visit and the curiosity of the baroness.\n\n“You are in constant communication with the Baron Danglars?” the count inquired of Albert de Morcerf.\n\n“Yes, count, you know what I told you?”\n\n“All remains the same, then, in that quarter?”\n\n“It is more than ever a settled thing,” said Lucien,—and, considering that this remark was all that he was at that time called upon to make, he adjusted the glass to his eye, and biting the top of his gold headed cane, began to make the tour of the apartment, examining the arms and the pictures.\n\n“Ah,” said Monte Cristo “I did not expect that the affair would be so promptly concluded.”\n\n“Oh, things take their course without our assistance. While we are forgetting them, they are falling into their appointed order; and when, again, our attention is directed to them, we are surprised at the progress they have made towards the proposed end. My father and M. Danglars served together in Spain, my father in the army and M. Danglars in the commissariat department. It was there that my father, ruined by the revolution, and M. Danglars, who never had possessed any patrimony, both laid the foundations of their different fortunes.”\n\n“Yes,” said Monte Cristo “I think M. Danglars mentioned that in a visit which I paid him; and,” continued he, casting a side-glance at Lucien, who was turning over the leaves of an album, “Mademoiselle Eugénie is pretty—I think I remember that to be her name.”\n\n“Very pretty, or rather, very beautiful,” replied Albert, “but of that style of beauty which I do not appreciate; I am an ungrateful fellow.”\n\n“You speak as if you were already her husband.”\n\n“Ah,” returned Albert, in his turn looking around to see what Lucien was doing.\n\n“Really,” said Monte Cristo, lowering his voice, “you do not appear to me to be very enthusiastic on the subject of this marriage.”\n\n“Mademoiselle Danglars is too rich for me,” replied Morcerf, “and that frightens me.”\n\n“Bah,” exclaimed Monte Cristo, “that’s a fine reason to give. Are you not rich yourself?”\n\n“My father’s income is about 50,000 francs per annum; and he will give me, perhaps, ten or twelve thousand when I marry.”\n\n“That, perhaps, might not be considered a large sum, in Paris especially,” said the count; “but everything does not depend on wealth, and it is a fine thing to have a good name, and to occupy a high station in society. Your name is celebrated, your position magnificent; and then the Comte de Morcerf is a soldier, and it is pleasing to see the integrity of a Bayard united to the poverty of a Duguesclin; disinterestedness is the brightest ray in which a noble sword can shine. As for me, I consider the union with Mademoiselle Danglars a most suitable one; she will enrich you, and you will ennoble her.”\n\nAlbert shook his head, and looked thoughtful.\n\n“There is still something else,” said he.\n\n“I confess,” observed Monte Cristo, “that I have some difficulty in comprehending your objection to a young lady who is both rich and beautiful.”\n\n“Oh,” said Morcerf, “this repugnance, if repugnance it may be called, is not all on my side.”\n\n“Whence can it arise, then? for you told me your father desired the marriage.”\n\n“It is my mother who dissents; she has a clear and penetrating judgment, and does not smile on the proposed union. I cannot account for it, but she seems to entertain some prejudice against the Danglars.”\n\n30107m\n“Ah,” said the count, in a somewhat forced tone, “that may be easily explained; the Comtesse de Morcerf, who is aristocracy and refinement itself, does not relish the idea of being allied by your marriage with one of ignoble birth; that is natural enough.”\n\n“I do not know if that is her reason,” said Albert, “but one thing I do know, that if this marriage be consummated, it will render her quite miserable. There was to have been a meeting six weeks ago in order to talk over and settle the affair; but I had such a sudden attack of indisposition——”\n\n“Real?” interrupted the count, smiling.\n\n“Oh, real enough, from anxiety doubtless,—at any rate they postponed the matter for two months. There is no hurry, you know. I am not yet twenty-one, and Eugénie is only seventeen; but the two months expire next week. It must be done. My dear count, you cannot imagine how my mind is harassed. How happy you are in being exempt from all this!”\n\n“Well, and why should not you be free, too? What prevents you from being so?”\n\n“Oh, it will be too great a disappointment to my father if I do not marry Mademoiselle Danglars.”\n\n“Marry her then,” said the count, with a significant shrug of the shoulders.\n\n“Yes,” replied Morcerf, “but that will plunge my mother into positive grief.”\n\n“Then do not marry her,” said the count.\n\n“Well, I shall see. I will try and think over what is the best thing to be done; you will give me your advice, will you not, and if possible extricate me from my unpleasant position? I think, rather than give pain to my dear mother, I would run the risk of offending the count.”\n\nMonte Cristo turned away; he seemed moved by this last remark.\n\n“Ah,” said he to Debray, who had thrown himself into an easy-chair at the farthest extremity of the salon, and who held a pencil in his right hand and an account book in his left, “what are you doing there? Are you making a sketch after Poussin?”\n\n“Oh, no,” was the tranquil response; “I am too fond of art to attempt anything of that sort. I am doing a little sum in arithmetic.”\n\n“In arithmetic?”\n\n“Yes; I am calculating—by the way, Morcerf, that indirectly concerns you—I am calculating what the house of Danglars must have gained by the last rise in Haiti bonds; from 206 they have risen to 409 in three days, and the prudent banker had purchased at 206; therefore he must have made 300,000 livres.”\n\n“That is not his biggest scoop,” said Morcerf; “did he not make a million in Spaniards this last year?”\n\n“My dear fellow,” said Lucien, “here is the Count of Monte Cristo, who will say to you, as the Italians do,—\n\n“‘Denaro e santità,\nMetà della metà.’9\n\n“When they tell me such things, I only shrug my shoulders and say nothing.”\n\n“But you were speaking of Haitians?” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“Ah, Haitians,—that is quite another thing! Haitians are the _écarté_ of French stock-jobbing. We may like bouillotte, delight in whist, be enraptured with boston, and yet grow tired of them all; but we always come back to _écarté_—it is not only a game, it is a _hors-d’œuvre_! M. Danglars sold yesterday at 405, and pockets 300,000 francs. Had he but waited till today, the price would have fallen to 205, and instead of gaining 300,000 francs, he would have lost 20 or 25,000.”\n\n“And what has caused the sudden fall from 409 to 206?” asked Monte Cristo. “I am profoundly ignorant of all these stock-jobbing intrigues.”\n\n“Because,” said Albert, laughing, “one piece of news follows another, and there is often great dissimilarity between them.”\n\n“Ah,” said the count, “I see that M. Danglars is accustomed to play at gaining or losing 300,000 francs in a day; he must be enormously rich.”\n\n“It is not he who plays!” exclaimed Lucien; “it is Madame Danglars; she is indeed daring.”\n\n“But you who are a reasonable being, Lucien, and who knows how little dependence is to be placed on the news, since you are at the fountain-head, surely you ought to prevent it,” said Morcerf, with a smile.\n\n“How can I, if her husband fails in controlling her?” asked Lucien; “you know the character of the baroness—no one has any influence with her, and she does precisely what she pleases.”\n\n“Ah, if I were in your place——” said Albert.\n\n“Well?”\n\n“I would reform her; it would be rendering a service to her future son-in-law.”\n\n“How would you set about it?”\n\n“Ah, that would be easy enough—I would give her a lesson.”\n\n“A lesson?”\n\n“Yes. Your position as secretary to the minister renders your authority great on the subject of political news; you never open your mouth but the stockbrokers immediately stenograph your words. Cause her to lose a hundred thousand francs, and that would teach her prudence.”\n\n“I do not understand,” stammered Lucien.\n\n“It is very clear, notwithstanding,” replied the young man, with an artlessness wholly free from affectation; “tell her some fine morning an unheard-of piece of intelligence—some telegraphic despatch, of which you alone are in possession; for instance, that Henri IV. was seen yesterday at Gabrielle’s. That would boom the market; she will buy heavily, and she will certainly lose when Beauchamp announces the following day, in his gazette, ‘The report circulated by some usually well-informed persons that the king was seen yesterday at Gabrielle’s house, is totally without foundation. We can positively assert that his majesty did not quit the Pont-Neuf.’”\n\nLucien half smiled. Monte Cristo, although apparently indifferent, had not lost one word of this conversation, and his penetrating eye had even read a hidden secret in the embarrassed manner of the secretary. This embarrassment had completely escaped Albert, but it caused Lucien to shorten his visit; he was evidently ill at ease. The count, in taking leave of him, said something in a low voice, to which he answered, “Willingly, count; I accept.” The count returned to young Morcerf.\n\n“Do you not think, on reflection,” said he to him, “that you have done wrong in thus speaking of your mother-in-law in the presence of M. Debray?”\n\n“My dear count,” said Morcerf, “I beg of you not to apply that title so prematurely.”\n\n“Now, speaking without any exaggeration, is your mother really so very much averse to this marriage?”\n\n“So much so that the baroness very rarely comes to the house, and my mother, has not, I think, visited Madame Danglars twice in her whole life.”\n\n“Then,” said the count, “I am emboldened to speak openly to you. M. Danglars is my banker; M. de Villefort has overwhelmed me with politeness in return for a service which a casual piece of good fortune enabled me to render him. I predict from all this an avalanche of dinners and routs. Now, in order not to presume on this, and also to be beforehand with them, I have, if agreeable to you, thought of inviting M. and Madame Danglars, and M. and Madame de Villefort, to my country-house at Auteuil. If I were to invite you and the Count and Countess of Morcerf to this dinner, I should give it the appearance of being a matrimonial meeting, or at least Madame de Morcerf would look upon the affair in that light, especially if Baron Danglars did me the honor to bring his daughter. In that case your mother would hold me in aversion, and I do not at all wish that; on the contrary, I desire to stand high in her esteem.”\n\n“Indeed, count,” said Morcerf, “I thank you sincerely for having used so much candor towards me, and I gratefully accept the exclusion which you propose. You say you desire my mother’s good opinion; I assure you it is already yours to a very unusual extent.”\n\n30111m\n“Do you think so?” said Monte Cristo, with interest.\n\n“Oh, I am sure of it; we talked of you an hour after you left us the other day. But to return to what we were saying. If my mother could know of this attention on your part—and I will venture to tell her—I am sure that she will be most grateful to you; it is true that my father will be equally angry.” The count laughed.\n\n“Well,” said he to Morcerf, “but I think your father will not be the only angry one; M. and Madame Danglars will think me a very ill-mannered person. They know that I am intimate with you—that you are, in fact; one of the oldest of my Parisian acquaintances—and they will not find you at my house; they will certainly ask me why I did not invite you. Be sure to provide yourself with some previous engagement which shall have a semblance of probability, and communicate the fact to me by a line in writing. You know that with bankers nothing but a written document will be valid.”\n\n“I will do better than that,” said Albert; “my mother is wishing to go to the sea-side—what day is fixed for your dinner?”\n\n“Saturday.”\n\n“This is Tuesday—well, tomorrow evening we leave, and the day after we shall be at Tréport. Really, count, you have a delightful way of setting people at their ease.”\n\n“Indeed, you give me more credit than I deserve; I only wish to do what will be agreeable to you, that is all.”\n\n“When shall you send your invitations?”\n\n“This very day.”\n\n“Well, I will immediately call on M. Danglars, and tell him that my mother and myself must leave Paris tomorrow. I have not seen you, consequently I know nothing of your dinner.”\n\n“How foolish you are! Have you forgotten that M. Debray has just seen you at my house?”\n\n“Ah, true.”\n\n“Fix it this way. I have seen you, and invited you without any ceremony, when you instantly answered that it would be impossible for you to accept, as you were going to Tréport.”\n\n“Well, then, that is settled; but you will come and call on my mother before tomorrow?”\n\n“Before tomorrow?—that will be a difficult matter to arrange, besides, I shall just be in the way of all the preparations for departure.”\n\n“Well, you can do better. You were only a charming man before, but, if you accede to my proposal, you will be adorable.”\n\n“What must I do to attain such sublimity?”\n\n“You are today free as air—come and dine with me; we shall be a small party—only yourself, my mother, and I. You have scarcely seen my mother; you shall have an opportunity of observing her more closely. She is a remarkable woman, and I only regret that there does not exist another like her, about twenty years younger; in that case, I assure you, there would very soon be a Countess and Viscountess of Morcerf. As to my father, you will not see him; he is officially engaged, and dines with the chief referendary. We will talk over our travels; and you, who have seen the whole world, will relate your adventures—you shall tell us the history of the beautiful Greek who was with you the other night at the Opera, and whom you call your slave, and yet treat like a princess. We will talk Italian and Spanish. Come, accept my invitation, and my mother will thank you.”\n\n“A thousand thanks,” said the count, “your invitation is most gracious, and I regret exceedingly that it is not in my power to accept it. I am not so much at liberty as you suppose; on the contrary, I have a most important engagement.”\n\n“Ah, take care, you were teaching me just now how, in case of an invitation to dinner, one might creditably make an excuse. I require the proof of a pre-engagement. I am not a banker, like M. Danglars, but I am quite as incredulous as he is.”\n\n“I am going to give you a proof,” replied the count, and he rang the bell.\n\n“Humph,” said Morcerf, “this is the second time you have refused to dine with my mother; it is evident that you wish to avoid her.”\n\nMonte Cristo started. “Oh, you do not mean that,” said he; “besides, here comes the confirmation of my assertion.”\n\nBaptistin entered, and remained standing at the door.\n\n“I had no previous knowledge of your visit, had I?”\n\n“Indeed, you are such an extraordinary person, that I would not answer for it.”\n\n“At all events, I could not guess that you would invite me to dinner.”\n\n“Probably not.”\n\n“Well, listen, Baptistin, what did I tell you this morning when I called you into my laboratory?”\n\n“To close the door against visitors as soon as the clock struck five,” replied the valet.\n\n“What then?”\n\n“Ah, my dear count,” said Albert.\n\n“No, no, I wish to do away with that mysterious reputation that you have given me, my dear viscount; it is tiresome to be always acting Manfred. I wish my life to be free and open. Go on, Baptistin.”\n\n“Then to admit no one except Major Bartolomeo Cavalcanti and his son.”\n\n“You hear—Major Bartolomeo Cavalcanti—a man who ranks amongst the most ancient nobility of Italy, whose name Dante has celebrated in the tenth canto of _The Inferno_, you remember it, do you not? Then there is his son, Andrea, a charming young man, about your own age, viscount, bearing the same title as yourself, and who is making his entry into the Parisian world, aided by his father’s millions. The major will bring his son with him this evening, the _contino_, as we say in Italy; he confides him to my care. If he proves himself worthy of it, I will do what I can to advance his interests. You will assist me in the work, will you not?”\n\n“Most undoubtedly. This Major Cavalcanti is an old friend of yours, then?”\n\n“By no means. He is a perfect nobleman, very polite, modest, and agreeable, such as may be found constantly in Italy, descendants of very ancient families. I have met him several times at Florence, Bologna and Lucca, and he has now communicated to me the fact of his arrival in Paris. The acquaintances one makes in travelling have a sort of claim on one; they everywhere expect to receive the same attention which you once paid them by chance, as though the civilities of a passing hour were likely to awaken any lasting interest in favor of the man in whose society you may happen to be thrown in the course of your journey. This good Major Cavalcanti is come to take a second view of Paris, which he only saw in passing through in the time of the Empire, when he was on his way to Moscow. I shall give him a good dinner, he will confide his son to my care, I will promise to watch over him, I shall let him follow in whatever path his folly may lead him, and then I shall have done my part.”\n\n“Certainly; I see you are a model Mentor,” said Albert “Good-bye, we shall return on Sunday. By the way, I have received news of Franz.”\n\n“Have you? Is he still amusing himself in Italy?”\n\n“I believe so; however, he regrets your absence extremely. He says you were the sun of Rome, and that without you all appears dark and cloudy; I do not know if he does not even go so far as to say that it rains.”\n\n“His opinion of me is altered for the better, then?”\n\n“No, he still persists in looking upon you as the most incomprehensible and mysterious of beings.”\n\n“He is a charming young man,” said Monte Cristo “and I felt a lively interest in him the very first evening of my introduction, when I met him in search of a supper, and prevailed upon him to accept a portion of mine. He is, I think, the son of General d’Épinay?”\n\n“He is.”\n\n“The same who was so shamefully assassinated in 1815?”\n\n“By the Bonapartists.”\n\n“Yes. Really I like him extremely; is there not also a matrimonial engagement contemplated for him?”\n\n“Yes, he is to marry Mademoiselle de Villefort.”\n\n“Indeed?”\n\n30115m\n“And you know I am to marry Mademoiselle Danglars,” said Albert, laughing.\n\n“You smile.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Why do you do so?”\n\n“I smile because there appears to me to be about as much inclination for the consummation of the engagement in question as there is for my own. But really, my dear count, we are talking as much of women as they do of us; it is unpardonable.”\n\nAlbert rose.\n\n“Are you going?”\n\n“Really, that is a good idea!—two hours have I been boring you to death with my company, and then you, with the greatest politeness, ask me if I am going. Indeed, count, you are the most polished man in the world. And your servants, too, how very well behaved they are; there is quite a style about them. Monsieur Baptistin especially; I could never get such a man as that. My servants seem to imitate those you sometimes see in a play, who, because they have only a word or two to say, aquit themselves in the most awkward manner possible. Therefore, if you part with M. Baptistin, give me the refusal of him.”\n\n“By all means.”\n\n“That is not all; give my compliments to your illustrious Luccanese, Cavalcante of the Cavalcanti; and if by any chance he should be wishing to establish his son, find him a wife very rich, very noble on her mother’s side at least, and a baroness in right of her father, I will help you in the search.”\n\n“Ah, ha; you will do as much as that, will you?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Well, really, nothing is certain in this world.”\n\n“Oh, count, what a service you might render me! I should like you a hundred times better if, by your intervention, I could manage to remain a bachelor, even were it only for ten years.”\n\n“Nothing is impossible,” gravely replied Monte Cristo; and taking leave of Albert, he returned into the house, and struck the gong three times. Bertuccio appeared.\n\n“Monsieur Bertuccio, you understand that I intend entertaining company on Saturday at Auteuil.” Bertuccio slightly started. “I shall require your services to see that all be properly arranged. It is a beautiful house, or at all events may be made so.”\n\n“There must be a good deal done before it can deserve that title, your excellency, for the tapestried hangings are very old.”\n\n“Let them all be taken away and changed, then, with the exception of the sleeping-chamber which is hung with red damask; you will leave that exactly as it is.” Bertuccio bowed. “You will not touch the garden either; as to the yard, you may do what you please with it; I should prefer that being altered beyond all recognition.”\n\n“I will do everything in my power to carry out your wishes, your excellency. I should be glad, however, to receive your excellency’s commands concerning the dinner.”\n\n“Really, my dear M. Bertuccio,” said the count, “since you have been in Paris, you have become quite nervous, and apparently out of your element; you no longer seem to understand me.”\n\n“But surely your excellency will be so good as to inform me whom you are expecting to receive?”\n\n“I do not yet know myself, neither is it necessary that you should do so. ‘Lucullus dines with Lucullus,’ that is quite sufficient.”\n\nBertuccio bowed, and left the room.\n\n# Chapter 55. Major Cavalcanti\n\nBoth the count and Baptistin had told the truth when they announced to Morcerf the proposed visit of the major, which had served Monte Cristo as a pretext for declining Albert’s invitation. Seven o’clock had just struck, and M. Bertuccio, according to the command which had been given him, had two hours before left for Auteuil, when a cab stopped at the door, and after depositing its occupant at the gate, immediately hurried away, as if ashamed of its employment. The visitor was about fifty-two years of age, dressed in one of the green surtouts, ornamented with black frogs, which have so long maintained their popularity all over Europe. He wore trousers of blue cloth, boots tolerably clean, but not of the brightest polish, and a little too thick in the soles, buckskin gloves, a hat somewhat resembling in shape those usually worn by the gendarmes, and a black cravat striped with white, which, if the proprietor had not worn it of his own free will, might have passed for a halter, so much did it resemble one. Such was the picturesque costume of the person who rang at the gate, and demanded if it was not at No. 30 in the Avenue des Champs-Élysées that the Count of Monte Cristo lived, and who, being answered by the porter in the affirmative, entered, closed the gate after him, and began to ascend the steps.\n\nThe small and angular head of this man, his white hair and thick gray moustaches, caused him to be easily recognized by Baptistin, who had received an exact description of the expected visitor, and who was awaiting him in the hall. Therefore, scarcely had the stranger time to pronounce his name before the count was apprised of his arrival. He was ushered into a simple and elegant drawing-room, and the count rose to meet him with a smiling air.\n\n“Ah, my dear sir, you are most welcome; I was expecting you.”\n\n“Indeed,” said the Italian, “was your excellency then aware of my visit?”\n\n“Yes; I had been told that I should see you today at seven o’clock.”\n\n“Then you have received full information concerning my arrival?”\n\n“Of course.”\n\n“Ah, so much the better, I feared this little precaution might have been forgotten.”\n\n30119m\n“What precaution?”\n\n“That of informing you beforehand of my coming.”\n\n“Oh, no, it has not.”\n\n“But you are sure you are not mistaken.”\n\n“Very sure.”\n\n“It really was I whom your excellency expected at seven o’clock this evening?”\n\n“I will prove it to you beyond a doubt.”\n\n“Oh, no, never mind that,” said the Italian; “it is not worth the trouble.”\n\n“Yes, yes,” said Monte Cristo. His visitor appeared slightly uneasy. “Let me see,” said the count; “are you not the Marquis Bartolomeo Cavalcanti?”\n\n“Bartolomeo Cavalcanti,” joyfully replied the Italian; “yes, I am really he.”\n\n“Ex-major in the Austrian service?”\n\n“Was I a major?” timidly asked the old soldier.\n\n“Yes,” said Monte Cristo “you were a major; that is the title the French give to the post which you filled in Italy.”\n\n“Very good,” said the major, “I do not demand more, you understand——”\n\n“Your visit here today is not of your own suggestion, is it?” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“No, certainly not.”\n\n“You were sent by some other person?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“By the excellent Abbé Busoni?”\n\n“Exactly so,” said the delighted major.\n\n“And you have a letter?”\n\n“Yes, there it is.”\n\n“Give it to me, then.” And Monte Cristo took the letter, which he opened and read. The major looked at the count with his large staring eyes, and then took a survey of the apartment, but his gaze almost immediately reverted to the proprietor of the room.\n\n“Yes, yes, I see. ‘Major Cavalcanti, a worthy patrician of Lucca, a descendant of the Cavalcanti of Florence,’” continued Monte Cristo, reading aloud, “‘possessing an income of half a million.’”\n\nMonte Cristo raised his eyes from the paper, and bowed.\n\n“Half a million,” said he, “magnificent!”\n\n“Half a million, is it?” said the major.\n\n“Yes, in so many words; and it must be so, for the abbé knows correctly the amount of all the largest fortunes in Europe.”\n\n“Be it half a million, then; but on my word of honor, I had no idea that it was so much.”\n\n“Because you are robbed by your steward. You must make some reformation in that quarter.”\n\n“You have opened my eyes,” said the Italian gravely; “I will show the gentlemen the door.”\n\nMonte Cristo resumed the perusal of the letter:\n\n“‘And who only needs one thing more to make him happy.’”\n\n“Yes, indeed but one!” said the major with a sigh.\n\n“‘Which is to recover a lost and adored son.’”\n\n“A lost and adored son!”\n\n“‘Stolen away in his infancy, either by an enemy of his noble family or by the gypsies.’”\n\n“At the age of five years!” said the major with a deep sigh, and raising his eye to heaven.\n\n“Unhappy father,” said Monte Cristo. The count continued:\n\n“‘I have given him renewed life and hope, in the assurance that you have the power of restoring the son whom he has vainly sought for fifteen years.’”\n\nThe major looked at the count with an indescribable expression of anxiety.\n\n“I have the power of so doing,” said Monte Cristo. The major recovered his self-possession.\n\n“So, then,” said he, “the letter was true to the end?”\n\n“Did you doubt it, my dear Monsieur Bartolomeo?”\n\n“No, indeed; certainly not; a good man, a man holding religious office, as does the Abbé Busoni, could not condescend to deceive or play off a joke; but your excellency has not read all.”\n\n“Ah, true,” said Monte Cristo “there is a postscript.”\n\n“Yes, yes,” repeated the major, “yes—there—is—a—postscript.”\n\n“‘In order to save Major Cavalcanti the trouble of drawing on his banker, I send him a draft for 2,000 francs to defray his travelling expenses, and credit on you for the further sum of 48,000 francs, which you still owe me.’”\n\nThe major awaited the conclusion of the postscript, apparently with great anxiety.\n\n“Very good,” said the count.\n\n“He said ‘very good,’” muttered the major, “then—sir——” replied he.\n\n“Then what?” asked Monte Cristo.\n\n“Then the postscript——”\n\n“Well; what of the postscript?”\n\n“Then the postscript is as favorably received by you as the rest of the letter?”\n\n“Certainly; the Abbé Busoni and myself have a small account open between us. I do not remember if it is exactly 48,000 francs, which I am still owing him, but I dare say we shall not dispute the difference. You attached great importance, then, to this postscript, my dear Monsieur Cavalcanti?”\n\n“I must explain to you,” said the major, “that, fully confiding in the signature of the Abbé Busoni, I had not provided myself with any other funds; so that if this resource had failed me, I should have found myself very unpleasantly situated in Paris.”\n\n“Is it possible that a man of your standing should be embarrassed anywhere?” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“Why, really I know no one,” said the major.\n\n“But then you yourself are known to others?”\n\n“Yes, I am known, so that——”\n\n“Proceed, my dear Monsieur Cavalcanti.”\n\n“So that you will remit to me these 48,000 francs?”\n\n“Certainly, at your first request.” The major’s eyes dilated with pleasing astonishment. “But sit down,” said Monte Cristo; “really I do not know what I have been thinking of—I have positively kept you standing for the last quarter of an hour.”\n\n“Don’t mention it.” The major drew an armchair towards him, and proceeded to seat himself.\n\n“Now,” said the count, “what will you take—a glass of sherry, port, or Alicante?”\n\n“Alicante, if you please; it is my favorite wine.”\n\n“I have some that is very good. You will take a biscuit with it, will you not?”\n\n“Yes, I will take a biscuit, as you are so obliging.”\n\nMonte Cristo rang; Baptistin appeared. The count advanced to meet him.\n\n“Well?” said he in a low voice.\n\n“The young man is here,” said the valet de chambre in the same tone.\n\n“Into what room did you take him?”\n\n“Into the blue drawing-room, according to your excellency’s orders.”\n\n“That’s right; now bring the Alicante and some biscuits.”\n\nBaptistin left the room.\n\n“Really,” said the major, “I am quite ashamed of the trouble I am giving you.”\n\n“Pray don’t mention such a thing,” said the count. Baptistin re-entered with glasses, wine, and biscuits. The count filled one glass, but in the other he only poured a few drops of the ruby-colored liquid. The bottle was covered with spiders’ webs, and all the other signs which indicate the age of wine more truly than do wrinkles on a man’s face. The major made a wise choice; he took the full glass and a biscuit. The count told Baptistin to leave the plate within reach of his guest, who began by sipping the Alicante with an expression of great satisfaction, and then delicately steeped his biscuit in the wine.\n30123m\n\n“So, sir, you lived at Lucca, did you? You were rich, noble, held in great esteem—had all that could render a man happy?”\n\n“All,” said the major, hastily swallowing his biscuit, “positively all.”\n\n“And yet there was one thing wanting in order to complete your happiness?”\n\n“Only one thing,” said the Italian.\n\n“And that one thing, your lost child.”\n\n“Ah,” said the major, taking a second biscuit, “that consummation of my happiness was indeed wanting.” The worthy major raised his eyes to heaven and sighed.\n\n“Let me hear, then,” said the count, “who this deeply regretted son was; for I always understood you were a bachelor.”\n\n“That was the general opinion, sir,” said the major, “and I——”\n\n“Yes,” replied the count, “and you confirmed the report. A youthful indiscretion, I suppose, which you were anxious to conceal from the world at large?”\n\nThe major recovered himself, and resumed his usual calm manner, at the same time casting his eyes down, either to give himself time to compose his countenance, or to assist his imagination, all the while giving an under-look at the count, the protracted smile on whose lips still announced the same polite curiosity.\n\n“Yes,” said the major, “I did wish this fault to be hidden from every eye.”\n\n“Not on your own account, surely,” replied Monte Cristo; “for a man is above that sort of thing?”\n\n“Oh, no, certainly not on my own account,” said the major with a smile and a shake of the head.\n\n“But for the sake of the mother?” said the count.\n\n“Yes, for the mother’s sake—his poor mother!” cried the major, taking a third biscuit.\n\n“Take some more wine, my dear Cavalcanti,” said the count, pouring out for him a second glass of Alicante; “your emotion has quite overcome you.”\n\n“His poor mother,” murmured the major, trying to get the lachrymal gland in operation, so as to moisten the corner of his eye with a false tear.\n\n“She belonged to one of the first families in Italy, I think, did she not?”\n\n“She was of a noble family of Fiesole, count.”\n\n“And her name was——”\n\n“Do you desire to know her name——?”\n\n“Oh,” said Monte Cristo “it would be quite superfluous for you to tell me, for I already know it.”\n\n“The count knows everything,” said the Italian, bowing.\n\n“Oliva Corsinari, was it not?”\n\n“Oliva Corsinari!”\n\n“A marchioness?”\n\n“A marchioness!”\n\n“And you married her at last, notwithstanding the opposition of her family?”\n\n“Yes, that was the way it ended.”\n\n“And you have doubtless brought all your papers with you?” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“What papers?”\n\n“The certificate of your marriage with Oliva Corsinari, and the register of your child’s birth.”\n\n“The register of my child’s birth?”\n\n“The register of the birth of Andrea Cavalcanti—of your son; is not his name Andrea?”\n\n“I believe so,” said the major.\n\n“What? You believe so?”\n\n“I dare not positively assert it, as he has been lost for so long a time.”\n\n“Well, then,” said Monte Cristo “you have all the documents with you?”\n\n“Your excellency, I regret to say that, not knowing it was necessary to come provided with these papers, I neglected to bring them.”\n\n“That is unfortunate,” returned Monte Cristo.\n\n“Were they, then, so necessary?”\n\n“They were indispensable.”\n\nThe major passed his hand across his brow. “Ah, _perbacco_, indispensable, were they?”\n\n“Certainly they were; supposing there were to be doubts raised as to the validity of your marriage or the legitimacy of your child?”\n\n“True,” said the major, “there might be doubts raised.”\n\n“In that case your son would be very unpleasantly situated.”\n\n“It would be fatal to his interests.”\n\n“It might cause him to fail in some desirable matrimonial alliance.”\n\n“_O peccato!_”\n\n“You must know that in France they are very particular on these points; it is not sufficient, as in Italy, to go to the priest and say, ‘We love each other, and want you to marry us.’ Marriage is a civil affair in France, and in order to marry in an orthodox manner you must have papers which undeniably establish your identity.”\n\n“That is the misfortune! You see I have not these necessary papers.”\n\n“Fortunately, I have them, though,” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“You?”\n\n“Yes.”\n“You have them?”\n\n“I have them.”\n\n“Ah, indeed?” said the major, who, seeing the object of his journey frustrated by the absence of the papers, feared also that his forgetfulness might give rise to some difficulty concerning the 48,000 francs—“ah, indeed, that is a fortunate circumstance; yes, that really is lucky, for it never occurred to me to bring them.”\n\n“I do not at all wonder at it—one cannot think of everything; but, happily, the Abbé Busoni thought for you.”\n\n“He is an excellent person.”\n\n“He is extremely prudent and thoughtful.”\n\n“He is an admirable man,” said the major; “and he sent them to you?”\n\n“Here they are.”\n\nThe major clasped his hands in token of admiration.\n\n“You married Oliva Corsinari in the church of San Paolo del Monte-Cattini; here is the priest’s certificate.”\n\n“Yes indeed, there it is truly,” said the Italian, looking on with astonishment.\n\n“And here is Andrea Cavalcanti’s baptismal register, given by the curé of Saravezza.”\n\n“All quite correct.”\n\n“Take these documents, then; they do not concern me. You will give them to your son, who will, of course, take great care of them.”\n\n“I should think so, indeed! If he were to lose them——”\n\n“Well, and if he were to lose them?” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“In that case,” replied the major, “it would be necessary to write to the curé for duplicates, and it would be some time before they could be obtained.”\n\n“It would be a difficult matter to arrange,” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“Almost an impossibility,” replied the major.\n\n“I am very glad to see that you understand the value of these papers.”\n\n“I regard them as invaluable.”\n\n“Now,” said Monte Cristo “as to the mother of the young man——”\n\n“As to the mother of the young man——” repeated the Italian, with anxiety.\n\n“As regards the Marchesa Corsinari——”\n\n“Really,” said the major, “difficulties seem to thicken upon us; will she be wanted in any way?”\n\n“No, sir,” replied Monte Cristo; “besides, has she not——”\n\n“Yes, sir,” said the major, “she has——”\n30127m\n\n“Paid the last debt of nature?”\n\n“Alas, yes,” returned the Italian.\n\n“I knew that,” said Monte Cristo; “she has been dead these ten years.”\n\n“And I am still mourning her loss,” exclaimed the major, drawing from his pocket a checked handkerchief, and alternately wiping first the left and then the right eye.\n\n“What would you have?” said Monte Cristo; “we are all mortal. Now, you understand, my dear Monsieur Cavalcanti, that it is useless for you to tell people in France that you have been separated from your son for fifteen years. Stories of gypsies, who steal children, are not at all in vogue in this part of the world, and would not be believed. You sent him for his education to a college in one of the provinces, and now you wish him to complete his education in the Parisian world. That is the reason which has induced you to leave Via Reggio, where you have lived since the death of your wife. That will be sufficient.”\n\n“You think so?”\n\n“Certainly.”\n\n“Very well, then.”\n\n“If they should hear of the separation——”\n\n“Ah, yes; what could I say?”\n\n“That an unfaithful tutor, bought over by the enemies of your family——”\n\n“By the Corsinari?”\n\n“Precisely. Had stolen away this child, in order that your name might become extinct.”\n\n“That is reasonable, since he is an only son.”\n\n“Well, now that all is arranged, do not let these newly awakened remembrances be forgotten. You have, doubtless, already guessed that I was preparing a surprise for you?”\n\n“An agreeable one?” asked the Italian.\n\n“Ah, I see the eye of a father is no more to be deceived than his heart.”\n\n“Hum!” said the major.\n\n“Someone has told you the secret; or, perhaps, you guessed that he was here.”\n\n“That who was here?”\n\n“Your child—your son—your Andrea!”\n\n“I did guess it,” replied the major with the greatest possible coolness. “Then he is here?”\n\n“He is,” said Monte Cristo; “when the valet de chambre came in just now, he told me of his arrival.”\n\n“Ah, very well, very well,” said the major, clutching the buttons of his coat at each exclamation.\n\n“My dear sir,” said Monte Cristo, “I understand your emotion; you must have time to recover yourself. I will, in the meantime, go and prepare the young man for this much-desired interview, for I presume that he is not less impatient for it than yourself.”\n\n“I should quite imagine that to be the case,” said Cavalcanti.\n\n“Well, in a quarter of an hour he shall be with you.”\n\n“You will bring him, then? You carry your goodness so far as even to present him to me yourself?”\n\n“No; I do not wish to come between a father and son. Your interview will be private. But do not be uneasy; even if the powerful voice of nature should be silent, you cannot well mistake him; he will enter by this door. He is a fine young man, of fair complexion—a little too fair, perhaps—pleasing in manners; but you will see and judge for yourself.”\n\n“By the way,” said the major, “you know I have only the 2,000 francs which the Abbé Busoni sent me; this sum I have expended upon travelling expenses, and——”\n\n“And you want money; that is a matter of course, my dear M. Cavalcanti. Well, here are 8,000 francs on account.”\n\nThe major’s eyes sparkled brilliantly.\n\n“It is 40,000 francs which I now owe you,” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“Does your excellency wish for a receipt?” said the major, at the same time slipping the money into the inner pocket of his coat.\n\n“For what?” said the count.\n\n“I thought you might want it to show the Abbé Busoni.”\n\n“Well, when you receive the remaining 40,000, you shall give me a receipt in full. Between honest men such excessive precaution is, I think, quite unnecessary.”\n\n“Yes, so it is, between perfectly upright people.”\n\n“One word more,” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“Say on.”\n\n“You will permit me to make one remark?”\n\n“Certainly; pray do so.”\n\n“Then I should advise you to leave off wearing that style of dress.”\n\n“Indeed,” said the major, regarding himself with an air of complete satisfaction.\n\n“Yes. It may be worn at Via Reggio; but that costume, however elegant in itself, has long been out of fashion in Paris.”\n\n“That’s unfortunate.”\n\n“Oh, if you really are attached to your old mode of dress; you can easily resume it when you leave Paris.”\n\n“But what shall I wear?”\n\n“What you find in your trunks.”\n\n“In my trunks? I have but one portmanteau.”\n\n“I dare say you have nothing else with you. What is the use of boring one’s self with so many things? Besides an old soldier always likes to march with as little baggage as possible.”\n\n“That is just the case—precisely so.”\n\n“But you are a man of foresight and prudence, therefore you sent your luggage on before you. It has arrived at the Hôtel des Princes, Rue de Richelieu. It is there you are to take up your quarters.”\n\n“Then, in these trunks——”\n\n“I presume you have given orders to your valet de chambre to put in all you are likely to need,—your plain clothes and your uniform. On grand occasions you must wear your uniform; that will look very well. Do not forget your crosses. They still laugh at them in France, and yet always wear them, for all that.”\n\n“Very well, very well,” said the major, who was in ecstasy at the attention paid him by the count.\n\n“Now,” said Monte Cristo, “that you have fortified yourself against all painful excitement, prepare yourself, my dear M. Cavalcanti, to meet your lost Andrea.”\n\nSaying which Monte Cristo bowed, and disappeared behind the tapestry, leaving the major fascinated beyond expression with the delightful reception which he had received at the hands of the count.\n\n# Chapter 56. Andrea Cavalcanti\n\nThe Count of Monte Cristo entered the adjoining room, which Baptistin had designated as the drawing-room, and found there a young man, of graceful demeanor and elegant appearance, who had arrived in a cab about half an hour previously. Baptistin had not found any difficulty in recognizing the person who presented himself at the door for admittance. He was certainly the tall young man with light hair, red beard, black eyes, and brilliant complexion, whom his master had so particularly described to him. When the count entered the room the young man was carelessly stretched on a sofa, tapping his boot with the gold-headed cane which he held in his hand. On perceiving the count he rose quickly.\n\n“The Count of Monte Cristo, I believe?” said he.\n\n“Yes, sir, and I think I have the honor of addressing Count Andrea Cavalcanti?”\n\n“Count Andrea Cavalcanti,” repeated the young man, accompanying his words with a bow.\n\n“You are charged with a letter of introduction addressed to me, are you not?” said the count.\n\n“I did not mention that, because the signature seemed to me so strange.”\n\n“The letter signed ‘Sinbad the Sailor,’ is it not?”\n\n“Exactly so. Now, as I have never known any Sinbad, with the exception of the one celebrated in the _Thousand and One Nights_——”\n\n“Well, it is one of his descendants, and a great friend of mine; he is a very rich Englishman, eccentric almost to insanity, and his real name is Lord Wilmore.”\n\n“Ah, indeed? Then that explains everything that is extraordinary,” said Andrea. “He is, then, the same Englishman whom I met—at—ah—yes, indeed. Well, monsieur, I am at your service.”\n\n“If what you say be true,” replied the count, smiling, “perhaps you will be kind enough to give me some account of yourself and your family?”\n\n“Certainly, I will do so,” said the young man, with a quickness which gave proof of his ready invention. “I am (as you have said) the Count Andrea Cavalcanti, son of Major Bartolomeo Cavalcanti, a descendant of the Cavalcanti whose names are inscribed in the golden book at Florence. Our family, although still rich (for my father’s income amounts to half a million), has experienced many misfortunes, and I myself was, at the age of five years, taken away by the treachery of my tutor, so that for fifteen years I have not seen the author of my existence. Since I have arrived at years of discretion and become my own master, I have been constantly seeking him, but all in vain. At length I received this letter from your friend, which states that my father is in Paris, and authorizes me to address myself to you for information respecting him.”\n\n“Really, all you have related to me is exceedingly interesting,” said Monte Cristo, observing the young man with a gloomy satisfaction; “and you have done well to conform in everything to the wishes of my friend Sinbad; for your father is indeed here, and is seeking you.”\n\nThe count from the moment of first entering the drawing-room, had not once lost sight of the expression of the young man’s countenance; he had admired the assurance of his look and the firmness of his voice; but at these words, so natural in themselves, “Your father is indeed here, and is seeking you,” young Andrea started, and exclaimed, “My father? Is my father here?”\n\n“Most undoubtedly,” replied Monte Cristo; “your father, Major Bartolomeo Cavalcanti.” The expression of terror which, for the moment, had overspread the features of the young man, had now disappeared.\n\n“Ah, yes, that is the name, certainly. Major Bartolomeo Cavalcanti. And you really mean to say; monsieur, that my dear father is here?”\n\n“Yes, sir; and I can even add that I have only just left his company. The history which he related to me of his lost son touched me to the quick; indeed, his griefs, hopes, and fears on that subject might furnish material for a most touching and pathetic poem. At length, he one day received a letter, stating that the abductors of his son now offered to restore him, or at least to give notice where he might be found, on condition of receiving a large sum of money, by way of ransom. Your father did not hesitate an instant, and the sum was sent to the frontier of Piedmont, with a passport signed for Italy. You were in the south of France, I think?”\n\n“Yes,” replied Andrea, with an embarrassed air, “I was in the south of France.”\n\n“A carriage was to await you at Nice?”\n\n“Precisely so; and it conveyed me from Nice to Genoa, from Genoa to Turin, from Turin to Chambéry, from Chambéry to Pont-de-Beauvoisin, and from Pont-de-Beauvoisin to Paris.”\n\n30133m\n“Indeed? Then your father ought to have met with you on the road, for it is exactly the same route which he himself took, and that is how we have been able to trace your journey to this place.”\n\n“But,” said Andrea, “if my father had met me, I doubt if he would have recognized me; I must be somewhat altered since he last saw me.”\n\n“Oh, the voice of nature,” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“True,” interrupted the young man, “I had not looked upon it in that light.”\n\n“Now,” replied Monte Cristo “there is only one source of uneasiness left in your father’s mind, which is this—he is anxious to know how you have been employed during your long absence from him, how you have been treated by your persecutors, and if they have conducted themselves towards you with all the deference due to your rank. Finally, he is anxious to see if you have been fortunate enough to escape the bad moral influence to which you have been exposed, and which is infinitely more to be dreaded than any physical suffering; he wishes to discover if the fine abilities with which nature had endowed you have been weakened by want of culture; and, in short, whether you consider yourself capable of resuming and retaining in the world the high position to which your rank entitles you.”\n\n“Sir!” exclaimed the young man, quite astounded, “I hope no false report——”\n\n“As for myself, I first heard you spoken of by my friend Wilmore, the philanthropist. I believe he found you in some unpleasant position, but do not know of what nature, for I did not ask, not being inquisitive. Your misfortunes engaged his sympathies, so you see you must have been interesting. He told me that he was anxious to restore you to the position which you had lost, and that he would seek your father until he found him. He did seek, and has found him, apparently, since he is here now; and, finally, my friend apprised me of your coming, and gave me a few other instructions relative to your future fortune. I am quite aware that my friend Wilmore is peculiar, but he is sincere, and as rich as a gold mine, consequently, he may indulge his eccentricities without any fear of their ruining him, and I have promised to adhere to his instructions. Now, sir, pray do not be offended at the question I am about to put to you, as it comes in the way of my duty as your patron. I would wish to know if the misfortunes which have happened to you—misfortunes entirely beyond your control, and which in no degree diminish my regard for you—I would wish to know if they have not, in some measure, contributed to render you a stranger to the world in which your fortune and your name entitle you to make a conspicuous figure?”\n\n“Sir,” returned the young man, with a reassurance of manner, “make your mind easy on this score. Those who took me from my father, and who always intended, sooner or later, to sell me again to my original proprietor, as they have now done, calculated that, in order to make the most of their bargain, it would be politic to leave me in possession of all my personal and hereditary worth, and even to increase the value, if possible. I have, therefore, received a very good education, and have been treated by these kidnappers very much as the slaves were treated in Asia Minor, whose masters made them grammarians, doctors, and philosophers, in order that they might fetch a higher price in the Roman market.”\n\nMonte Cristo smiled with satisfaction; it appeared as if he had not expected so much from M. Andrea Cavalcanti.\n\n“Besides,” continued the young man, “if there did appear some defect in education, or offence against the established forms of etiquette, I suppose it would be excused, in consideration of the misfortunes which accompanied my birth, and followed me through my youth.”\n\n“Well,” said Monte Cristo in an indifferent tone, “you will do as you please, count, for you are the master of your own actions, and are the person most concerned in the matter, but if I were you, I would not divulge a word of these adventures. Your history is quite a romance, and the world, which delights in romances in yellow covers, strangely mistrusts those which are bound in living parchment, even though they be gilded like yourself. This is the kind of difficulty which I wished to represent to you, my dear count. You would hardly have recited your touching history before it would go forth to the world, and be deemed unlikely and unnatural. You would be no longer a lost child found, but you would be looked upon as an upstart, who had sprung up like a mushroom in the night. You might excite a little curiosity, but it is not everyone who likes to be made the centre of observation and the subject of unpleasant remark.”\n\n“I agree with you, monsieur,” said the young man, turning pale, and, in spite of himself, trembling beneath the scrutinizing look of his companion, “such consequences would be extremely unpleasant.”\n\n“Nevertheless, you must not exaggerate the evil,” said Monte Cristo, “for by endeavoring to avoid one fault you will fall into another. You must resolve upon one simple and single line of conduct, and for a man of your intelligence, this plan is as easy as it is necessary; you must form honorable friendships, and by that means counteract the prejudice which may attach to the obscurity of your former life.”\n\nAndrea visibly changed countenance.\n\n“I would offer myself as your surety and friendly adviser,” said Monte Cristo, “did I not possess a moral distrust of my best friends, and a sort of inclination to lead others to doubt them too; therefore, in departing from this rule, I should (as the actors say) be playing a part quite out of my line, and should, therefore, run the risk of being hissed, which would be an act of folly.”\n\n“However, your excellency,” said Andrea, “in consideration of Lord Wilmore, by whom I was recommended to you——”\n\n“Yes, certainly,” interrupted Monte Cristo; “but Lord Wilmore did not omit to inform me, my dear M. Andrea, that the season of your youth was rather a stormy one. Ah,” said the count, watching Andrea’s countenance, “I do not demand any confession from you; it is precisely to avoid that necessity that your father was sent for from Lucca. You shall soon see him. He is a little stiff and pompous in his manner, and he is disfigured by his uniform; but when it becomes known that he has been for eighteen years in the Austrian service, all that will be pardoned. We are not generally very severe with the Austrians. In short, you will find your father a very presentable person, I assure you.”\n\n“Ah, sir, you have given me confidence; it is so long since we were separated, that I have not the least remembrance of him, and, besides, you know that in the eyes of the world a large fortune covers all defects.”\n\n“He is a millionaire—his income is 500,000 francs.”\n\n“Then,” said the young man, with anxiety, “I shall be sure to be placed in an agreeable position.”\n\n“One of the most agreeable possible, my dear sir; he will allow you an income of 50,000 livres per annum during the whole time of your stay in Paris.”\n\n“Then in that case I shall always choose to remain there.”\n\n“You cannot control circumstances, my dear sir; ‘man proposes, and God disposes.’” Andrea sighed.\n\n“But,” said he, “so long as I do remain in Paris, and nothing forces me to quit it, do you mean to tell me that I may rely on receiving the sum you just now mentioned to me?”\n\n“You may.”\n\n“Shall I receive it from my father?” asked Andrea, with some uneasiness.\n\n“Yes, you will receive it from your father personally, but Lord Wilmore will be the security for the money. He has, at the request of your father, opened an account of 5,000 francs a month at M. Danglars’, which is one of the safest banks in Paris.”\n\n“And does my father mean to remain long in Paris?” asked Andrea.\n\n“Only a few days,” replied Monte Cristo. “His service does not allow him to absent himself more than two or three weeks together.”\n\n“Ah, my dear father!” exclaimed Andrea, evidently charmed with the idea of his speedy departure.\n\n“Therefore,” said Monte Cristo feigning to mistake his meaning—“therefore I will not, for another instant, retard the pleasure of your meeting. Are you prepared to embrace your worthy father?”\n\n“I hope you do not doubt it.”\n\n30137m\n“Go, then, into the drawing-room, my young friend, where you will find your father awaiting you.”\n\nAndrea made a low bow to the count, and entered the adjoining room. Monte Cristo watched him till he disappeared, and then touched a spring in a panel made to look like a picture, which, in sliding partly from the frame, discovered to view a small opening, so cleverly contrived that it revealed all that was passing in the drawing-room now occupied by Cavalcanti and Andrea. The young man closed the door behind him, and advanced towards the major, who had risen when he heard steps approaching him.\n\n“Ah, my dear father!” said Andrea in a loud voice, in order that the count might hear him in the next room, “is it really you?”\n\n“How do you do, my dear son?” said the major gravely.\n\n“After so many years of painful separation,” said Andrea, in the same tone of voice, and glancing towards the door, “what a happiness it is to meet again!”\n\n“Indeed it is, after so long a separation.”\n\n“Will you not embrace me, sir?” said Andrea.\n30139m\n\n“If you wish it, my son,” said the major; and the two men embraced each other after the fashion of actors on the stage; that is to say, each rested his head on the other’s shoulder.\n\n“Then we are once more reunited?” said Andrea.\n\n“Once more,” replied the major.\n\n“Never more to be separated?”\n\n“Why, as to that—I think, my dear son, you must be by this time so accustomed to France as to look upon it almost as a second country.”\n\n“The fact is,” said the young man, “that I should be exceedingly grieved to leave it.”\n\n“As for me, you must know I cannot possibly live out of Lucca; therefore I shall return to Italy as soon as I can.”\n\n“But before you leave France, my dear father, I hope you will put me in possession of the documents which will be necessary to prove my descent.”\n\n“Certainly; I am come expressly on that account; it has cost me much trouble to find you, but I had resolved on giving them into your hands, and if I had to recommence my search, it would occupy all the few remaining years of my life.”\n\n“Where are these papers, then?”\n\n“Here they are.”\n\nAndrea seized the certificate of his father’s marriage and his own baptismal register, and after having opened them with all the eagerness which might be expected under the circumstances, he read them with a facility which proved that he was accustomed to similar documents, and with an expression which plainly denoted an unusual interest in the contents. When he had perused the documents, an indefinable expression of pleasure lighted up his countenance, and looking at the major with a most peculiar smile, he said, in very excellent Tuscan:\n\n“Then there is no longer any such thing, in Italy as being condemned to the galleys?”\n\nThe major drew himself up to his full height.\n\n“Why?—what do you mean by that question?”\n\n“I mean that if there were, it would be impossible to draw up with impunity two such deeds as these. In France, my dear sir, half such a piece of effrontery as that would cause you to be quickly despatched to Toulon for five years, for change of air.”\n\n“Will you be good enough to explain your meaning?” said the major, endeavoring as much as possible to assume an air of the greatest majesty.\n\n“My dear M. Cavalcanti,” said Andrea, taking the major by the arm in a confidential manner, “how much are you paid for being my father?”\n\nThe major was about to speak, when Andrea continued, in a low voice:\n\n“Nonsense, I am going to set you an example of confidence, they give me 50,000 francs a year to be your son; consequently, you can understand that it is not at all likely I shall ever deny my parent.”\n\nThe major looked anxiously around him.\n\n“Make yourself easy, we are quite alone,” said Andrea; “besides, we are conversing in Italian.”\n\n“Well, then,” replied the major, “they paid me 50,000 francs down.”\n\n“Monsieur Cavalcanti,” said Andrea, “do you believe in fairy tales?”\n\n“I used not to do so, but I really feel now almost obliged to have faith in them.”\n\n“You have, then, been induced to alter your opinion; you have had some proofs of their truth?” The major drew from his pocket a handful of gold.\n\n“Most palpable proofs,” said he, “as you may perceive.”\n\n“You think, then, that I may rely on the count’s promises?”\n\n“Certainly I do.”\n\n“You are sure he will keep his word with me?”\n\n“To the letter, but at the same time, remember, we must continue to play our respective parts. I, as a tender father——”\n\n“And I as a dutiful son, as they choose that I shall be descended from you.”\n\n“Whom do you mean by they?”\n\n“_Ma foi_, I can hardly tell, but I was alluding to those who wrote the letter; you received one, did you not?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“From whom?”\n\n“From a certain Abbé Busoni.”\n\n“Have you any knowledge of him?”\n\n“No, I have never seen him.”\n\n“What did he say in the letter?”\n\n“You will promise not to betray me?”\n\n“Rest assured of that; you well know that our interests are the same.”\n\n“Then read for yourself;” and the major gave a letter into the young man’s hand. Andrea read in a low voice:\n\n“‘You are poor; a miserable old age awaits you. Would you like to become rich, or at least independent? Set out immediately for Paris, and demand of the Count of Monte Cristo, Avenue des Champs-Élysées, No. 30, the son whom you had by the Marchesa Corsinari, and who was taken from you at five years of age. This son is named Andrea Cavalcanti. In order that you may not doubt the kind intention of the writer of this letter, you will find enclosed an order for 2,400 francs, payable in Florence, at Signor Gozzi’s; also a letter of introduction to the Count of Monte Cristo, on whom I give you a draft of 48,000 francs. Remember to go to the count on the 26th May at seven o’clock in the evening.\n\n“(Signed) ‘Abbé Busoni.’”\n\n“It is the same.”\n\n“What do you mean?” said the major.\n\n“I was going to say that I received a letter almost to the same effect.”\n\n“You?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“From the Abbé Busoni?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“From whom, then?”\n\n“From an Englishman, called Lord Wilmore, who takes the name of Sinbad the Sailor.”\n\n“And of whom you have no more knowledge than I of the Abbé Busoni?”\n\n“You are mistaken; there I am ahead of you.”\n\n“You have seen him, then?”\n\n“Yes, once.”\n\n“Where?”\n\n“Ah, that is just what I cannot tell you; if I did, I should make you as wise as myself, which it is not my intention to do.”\n\n“And what did the letter contain?”\n\n“Read it.”\n\n“‘You are poor, and your future prospects are dark and gloomy. Do you wish for a name? should you like to be rich, and your own master?’”\n\n“_Parbleu!_” said the young man; “was it possible there could be two answers to such a question?”\n\n“‘Take the post-chaise which you will find waiting at the Porte de Gênes, as you enter Nice; pass through Turin, Chambéry, and Pont-de-Beauvoisin. Go to the Count of Monte Cristo, Avenue des Champs-Élysées, on the 26th of May, at seven o’clock in the evening, and demand of him your father. You are the son of the Marchese Cavalcanti and the Marchesa Oliva Corsinari. The marquis will give you some papers which will certify this fact, and authorize you to appear under that name in the Parisian world. As to your rank, an annual income of 50,000 livres will enable you to support it admirably. I enclose a draft for 5,000 livres, payable on M. Ferrea, banker at Nice, and also a letter of introduction to the Count of Monte Cristo, whom I have directed to supply all your wants.\n\n“‘Sinbad the Sailor.’”\n\n“Humph,” said the major; “very good. You have seen the count, you say?”\n\n“I have only just left him.”\n\n“And has he conformed to all that the letter specified?”\n\n“He has.”\n\n“Do you understand it?”\n\n“Not in the least.”\n\n“There is a dupe somewhere.”\n\n“At all events, it is neither you nor I.”\n\n“Certainly not.”\n\n“Well, then——”\n\n“Why, it does not much concern us, do you think it does?”\n\n“No; I agree with you there. We must play the game to the end, and consent to be blindfolded.”\n\n“Ah, you shall see; I promise you I will sustain my part to admiration.”\n\n“I never once doubted your doing so.” Monte Cristo chose this moment for re-entering the drawing-room. On hearing the sound of his footsteps, the two men threw themselves in each other’s arms, and while they were in the midst of this embrace, the count entered.\n\n“Well, marquis,” said Monte Cristo, “you appear to be in no way disappointed in the son whom your good fortune has restored to you.”\n\n“Ah, your excellency, I am overwhelmed with delight.”\n\n“And what are your feelings?” said Monte Cristo, turning to the young man.\n\n“As for me, my heart is overflowing with happiness.”\n\n“Happy father, happy son!” said the count.\n\n“There is only one thing which grieves me,” observed the major, “and that is the necessity for my leaving Paris so soon.”\n\n“Ah, my dear M. Cavalcanti, I trust you will not leave before I have had the honor of presenting you to some of my friends.”\n\n“I am at your service, sir,” replied the major.\n\n“Now, sir,” said Monte Cristo, addressing Andrea, “make your confession.”\n\n“To whom?”\n\n“Tell M. Cavalcanti something of the state of your finances.”\n\n“_Ma foi!_ monsieur, you have touched upon a tender chord.”\n\n“Do you hear what he says, major?”\n\n“Certainly I do.”\n\n“But do you understand?”\n“I do.”\n\n“Your son says he requires money.”\n\n“Well, what would you have me do?” said the major.\n\n“You should furnish him with some of course,” replied Monte Cristo.\n\n“I?”\n“Yes, you,” said the count, at the same time advancing towards Andrea, and slipping a packet of bank-notes into the young man’s hand.\n\n“What is this?”\n\n“It is from your father.”\n\n“From my father?”\n\n“Yes; did you not tell him just now that you wanted money? Well, then, he deputes me to give you this.”\n\n“Am I to consider this as part of my income on account?”\n\n“No, it is for the first expenses of your settling in Paris.”\n\n“Ah, how good my dear father is!”\n\n“Silence,” said Monte Cristo; “he does not wish you to know that it comes from him.”\n\n“I fully appreciate his delicacy,” said Andrea, cramming the notes hastily into his pocket.\n\n“And now, gentlemen, I wish you good-morning,” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“And when shall we have the honor of seeing you again, your excellency?” asked Cavalcanti.\n\n“Ah,” said Andrea, “when may we hope for that pleasure?”\n\n“On Saturday, if you will—Yes.—Let me see—Saturday—I am to dine at my country house, at Auteuil, on that day, Rue de la Fontaine, No. 28. Several persons are invited, and among others, M. Danglars, your banker. I will introduce you to him, for it will be necessary he should know you, as he is to pay your money.”\n\n“Full dress?” said the major, half aloud.\n\n“Oh, yes, certainly,” said the count; “uniform, cross, knee-breeches.”\n\n“And how shall I be dressed?” demanded Andrea.\n30145m\n\n“Oh, very simply; black trousers, patent leather boots, white waistcoat, either a black or blue coat, and a long cravat. Go to Blin or Véronique for your clothes. Baptistin will tell you where, if you do not know their address. The less pretension there is in your attire, the better will be the effect, as you are a rich man. If you mean to buy any horses, get them of Devedeux, and if you purchase a phaeton, go to Baptiste for it.”\n\n“At what hour shall we come?” asked the young man.\n\n“About half-past six.”\n\n“We will be with you at that time,” said the major. The two Cavalcanti bowed to the count, and left the house. Monte Cristo went to the window, and saw them crossing the street, arm in arm.\n\n“There go two miscreants;” said he, “it is a pity they are not really related!” Then, after an instant of gloomy reflection, “Come, I will go to see the Morrels,” said he; “I think that disgust is even more sickening than hatred.”\n\n# Chapter 57. In the Lucern Patch\n\n30147m\nOur readers must now allow us to transport them again to the enclosure surrounding M. de Villefort’s house, and, behind the gate, half screened from view by the large chestnut-trees, which on all sides spread their luxuriant branches, we shall find some people of our acquaintance. This time Maximilian was the first to arrive. He was intently watching for a shadow to appear among the trees, and awaiting with anxiety the sound of a light step on the gravel walk.\n\nAt length, the long-desired sound was heard, and instead of one figure, as he had expected, he perceived that two were approaching him. The delay had been occasioned by a visit from Madame Danglars and Eugénie, which had been prolonged beyond the time at which Valentine was expected. That she might not appear to fail in her promise to Maximilian, she proposed to Mademoiselle Danglars that they should take a walk in the garden, being anxious to show that the delay, which was doubtless a cause of vexation to him, was not occasioned by any neglect on her part. The young man, with the intuitive perception of a lover, quickly understood the circumstances in which she was involuntarily placed, and he was comforted. Besides, although she avoided coming within speaking distance, Valentine arranged so that Maximilian could see her pass and repass, and each time she went by, she managed, unperceived by her companion, to cast an expressive look at the young man, which seemed to say, “Have patience! You see it is not my fault.”\n\nAnd Maximilian was patient, and employed himself in mentally contrasting the two girls,—one fair, with soft languishing eyes, a figure gracefully bending like a weeping willow; the other a brunette, with a fierce and haughty expression, and as straight as a poplar. It is unnecessary to state that, in the eyes of the young man, Valentine did not suffer by the contrast. In about half an hour the girls went away, and Maximilian understood that Mademoiselle Danglars’ visit had at last come to an end. In a few minutes Valentine re-entered the garden alone. For fear that anyone should be observing her return, she walked slowly; and instead of immediately directing her steps towards the gate, she seated herself on a bench, and, carefully casting her eyes around, to convince herself that she was not watched, she presently arose, and proceeded quickly to join Maximilian.\n\n“Good-evening, Valentine,” said a well-known voice.\n\n“Good-evening, Maximilian; I know I have kept you waiting, but you saw the cause of my delay.”\n\n“Yes, I recognized Mademoiselle Danglars. I was not aware that you were so intimate with her.”\n\n“Who told you we were intimate, Maximilian?”\n\n“No one, but you appeared to be so. From the manner in which you walked and talked together, one would have thought you were two school-girls telling your secrets to each other.”\n\n“We were having a confidential conversation,” returned Valentine; “she was owning to me her repugnance to the marriage with M. de Morcerf; and I, on the other hand, was confessing to her how wretched it made me to think of marrying M. d’Épinay.”\n\n“Dear Valentine!”\n\n“That will account to you for the unreserved manner which you observed between me and Eugénie, as in speaking of the man whom I could not love, my thoughts involuntarily reverted to him on whom my affections were fixed.”\n\n“Ah, how good you are to say so, Valentine! You possess a quality which can never belong to Mademoiselle Danglars. It is that indefinable charm which is to a woman what perfume is to the flower and flavor to the fruit, for the beauty of either is not the only quality we seek.”\n\n“It is your love which makes you look upon everything in that light.”\n\n“No, Valentine, I assure you such is not the case. I was observing you both when you were walking in the garden, and, on my honor, without at all wishing to depreciate the beauty of Mademoiselle Danglars, I cannot understand how any man can really love her.”\n\n“The fact is, Maximilian, that I was there, and my presence had the effect of rendering you unjust in your comparison.”\n\n“No; but tell me—it is a question of simple curiosity, and which was suggested by certain ideas passing in my mind relative to Mademoiselle Danglars——”\n\n“I dare say it is something disparaging which you are going to say. It only proves how little indulgence we may expect from your sex,” interrupted Valentine.\n\n“You cannot, at least, deny that you are very harsh judges of each other.”\n\n“If we are so, it is because we generally judge under the influence of excitement. But return to your question.”\n30151m\n\n“Does Mademoiselle Danglars object to this marriage with M. de Morcerf on account of loving another?”\n\n“I told you I was not on terms of strict intimacy with Eugénie.”\n\n“Yes, but girls tell each other secrets without being particularly intimate; own, now, that you did question her on the subject. Ah, I see you are smiling.”\n\n“If you are already aware of the conversation that passed, the wooden partition which interposed between us and you has proved but a slight security.”\n\n“Come, what did she say?”\n\n“She told me that she loved no one,” said Valentine; “that she disliked the idea of being married; that she would infinitely prefer leading an independent and unfettered life; and that she almost wished her father might lose his fortune, that she might become an artist, like her friend, Mademoiselle Louise d’Armilly.”\n\n“Ah, you see——”\n\n“Well, what does that prove?” asked Valentine.\n\n“Nothing,” replied Maximilian.\n\n“Then why did you smile?”\n\n“Why, you know very well that you are reflecting on yourself, Valentine.”\n\n“Do you want me to go away?”\n\n“Ah, no, no. But do not let us lose time; you are the subject on which I wish to speak.”\n\n“True, we must be quick, for we have scarcely ten minutes more to pass together.”\n\n“_Ma foi!_” said Maximilian, in consternation.\n\n“Yes, you are right; I am but a poor friend to you. What a life I cause you to lead, poor Maximilian, you who are formed for happiness! I bitterly reproach myself, I assure you.”\n\n“Well, what does it signify, Valentine, so long as I am satisfied, and feel that even this long and painful suspense is amply repaid by five minutes of your society, or two words from your lips? And I have also a deep conviction that heaven would not have created two hearts, harmonizing as ours do, and almost miraculously brought us together, to separate us at last.”\n\n“Those are kind and cheering words. You must hope for us both, Maximilian; that will make me at least partly happy.”\n\n“But why must you leave me so soon?”\n\n“I do not know particulars. I can only tell you that Madame de Villefort sent to request my presence, as she had a communication to make on which a part of my fortune depended. Let them take my fortune, I am already too rich; and, perhaps, when they have taken it, they will leave me in peace and quietness. You would love me as much if I were poor, would you not, Maximilian?”\n\n“Oh, I shall always love you. What should I care for either riches or poverty, if my Valentine was near me, and I felt certain that no one could deprive me of her? But do you not fear that this communication may relate to your marriage?”\n\n“I do not think that is the case.”\n\n“However it may be, Valentine, you must not be alarmed. I assure you that, as long as I live, I shall never love anyone else!”\n\n“Do you think to reassure me when you say that, Maximilian?”\n\n“Pardon me, you are right. I am a brute. But I was going to tell you that I met M. de Morcerf the other day.”\n“Well?”\n\n“Monsieur Franz is his friend, you know.”\n\n“What then?”\n\n“Monsieur de Morcerf has received a letter from Franz, announcing his immediate return.” Valentine turned pale, and leaned her hand against the gate.\n\n“Ah heavens, if it were that! But no, the communication would not come through Madame de Villefort.”\n\n“Why not?”\n\n“Because—I scarcely know why—but it has appeared as if Madame de Villefort secretly objected to the marriage, although she did not choose openly to oppose it.”\n\n“Is it so? Then I feel as if I could adore Madame de Villefort.”\n\n“Do not be in such a hurry to do that,” said Valentine, with a sad smile.\n\n“If she objects to your marrying M. d’Épinay, she would be all the more likely to listen to any other proposition.”\n\n“No, Maximilian, it is not suitors to which Madame de Villefort objects, it is marriage itself.”\n\n“Marriage? If she dislikes that so much, why did she ever marry herself?”\n\n“You do not understand me, Maximilian. About a year ago, I talked of retiring to a convent. Madame de Villefort, in spite of all the remarks which she considered it her duty to make, secretly approved of the proposition, my father consented to it at her instigation, and it was only on account of my poor grandfather that I finally abandoned the project. You can form no idea of the expression of that old man’s eye when he looks at me, the only person in the world whom he loves, and, I had almost said, by whom he is beloved in return. When he learned my resolution, I shall never forget the reproachful look which he cast on me, and the tears of utter despair which chased each other down his lifeless cheeks. Ah, Maximilian, I experienced, at that moment, such remorse for my intention, that, throwing myself at his feet, I exclaimed,—‘Forgive me, pray forgive me, my dear grandfather; they may do what they will with me, I will never leave you.’ When I had ceased speaking, he thankfully raised his eyes to heaven, but without uttering a word. Ah, Maximilian, I may have much to suffer, but I feel as if my grandfather’s look at that moment would more than compensate for all.”\n\n“Dear Valentine, you are a perfect angel, and I am sure I do not know what I—sabring right and left among the Bedouins—can have done to merit your being revealed to me, unless, indeed, Heaven took into consideration the fact that the victims of my sword were infidels. But tell me what interest Madame de Villefort can have in your remaining unmarried?”\n\n“Did I not tell you just now that I was rich, Maximilian—too rich? I possess nearly 50,000 livres in right of my mother; my grandfather and my grandmother, the Marquis and Marquise de Saint-Méran, will leave me as much, and M. Noirtier evidently intends making me his heir. My brother Edward, who inherits nothing from his mother, will, therefore, be poor in comparison with me. Now, if I had taken the veil, all this fortune would have descended to my father, and, in reversion, to his son.”\n\n“Ah, how strange it seems that such a young and beautiful woman should be so avaricious.”\n\n“It is not for herself that she is so, but for her son, and what you regard as a vice becomes almost a virtue when looked at in the light of maternal love.”\n\n“But could you not compromise matters, and give up a portion of your fortune to her son?”\n\n“How could I make such a proposition, especially to a woman who always professes to be so entirely disinterested?”\n\n“Valentine, I have always regarded our love in the light of something sacred; consequently, I have covered it with the veil of respect, and hid it in the innermost recesses of my soul. No human being, not even my sister, is aware of its existence. Valentine, will you permit me to make a confidant of a friend and reveal to him the love I bear you?”\n\nValentine started. “A friend, Maximilian; and who is this friend? I tremble to give my permission.”\n\n“Listen, Valentine. Have you never experienced for anyone that sudden and irresistible sympathy which made you feel as if the object of it had been your old and familiar friend, though, in reality, it was the first time you had ever met? Nay, further, have you never endeavored to recall the time, place, and circumstances of your former intercourse, and failing in this attempt, have almost believed that your spirits must have held converse with each other in some state of being anterior to the present, and that you are only now occupied in a reminiscence of the past?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Well, that is precisely the feeling which I experienced when I first saw that extraordinary man.”\n\n“Extraordinary, did you say?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You have known him for some time, then?”\n\n“Scarcely longer than eight or ten days.”\n\n“And do you call a man your friend whom you have only known for eight or ten days? Ah, Maximilian, I had hoped you set a higher value on the title of friend.”\n\n“Your logic is most powerful, Valentine, but say what you will, I can never renounce the sentiment which has instinctively taken possession of my mind. I feel as if it were ordained that this man should be associated with all the good which the future may have in store for me, and sometimes it really seems as if his eye was able to see what was to come, and his hand endowed with the power of directing events according to his own will.”\n\n“He must be a prophet, then,” said Valentine, smiling.\n\n“Indeed,” said Maximilian, “I have often been almost tempted to attribute to him the gift of prophecy; at all events, he has a wonderful power of foretelling any future good.”\n\n“Ah,” said Valentine in a mournful tone, “do let me see this man, Maximilian; he may tell me whether I shall ever be loved sufficiently to make amends for all I have suffered.”\n\n“My poor girl, you know him already.”\n\n“I know him?”\n\n“Yes; it was he who saved the life of your step-mother and her son.”\n\n“The Count of Monte Cristo?”\n\n“The same.”\n\n“Ah,” cried Valentine, “he is too much the friend of Madame de Villefort ever to be mine.”\n\n“The friend of Madame de Villefort! It cannot be; surely, Valentine, you are mistaken?”\n\n“No, indeed, I am not; for I assure you, his power over our household is almost unlimited. Courted by my step-mother, who regards him as the epitome of human wisdom; admired by my father, who says he has never before heard such sublime ideas so eloquently expressed; idolized by Edward, who, notwithstanding his fear of the count’s large black eyes, runs to meet him the moment he arrives, and opens his hand, in which he is sure to find some delightful present,—M. de Monte Cristo appears to exert a mysterious and almost uncontrollable influence over all the members of our family.”\n\n“If such be the case, my dear Valentine, you must yourself have felt, or at all events will soon feel, the effects of his presence. He meets Albert de Morcerf in Italy—it is to rescue him from the hands of the banditti; he introduces himself to Madame Danglars—it is that he may give her a royal present; your step-mother and her son pass before his door—it is that his Nubian may save them from destruction. This man evidently possesses the power of influencing events, both as regards men and things. I never saw more simple tastes united to greater magnificence. His smile is so sweet when he addresses me, that I forget it ever can be bitter to others. Ah, Valentine, tell me, if he ever looked on you with one of those sweet smiles? if so, depend on it, you will be happy.”\n\n“Me?” said the young girl, “he never even glances at me; on the contrary, if I accidentally cross his path, he appears rather to avoid me. Ah, he is not generous, neither does he possess that supernatural penetration which you attribute to him, for if he did, he would have perceived that I was unhappy; and if he had been generous, seeing me sad and solitary, he would have used his influence to my advantage, and since, as you say, he resembles the sun, he would have warmed my heart with one of his life-giving rays. You say he loves you, Maximilian; how do you know that he does? All would pay deference to an officer like you, with a fierce moustache and a long sabre, but they think they may crush a poor weeping girl with impunity.”\n\n“Ah, Valentine, I assure you you are mistaken.”\n\n“If it were otherwise—if he treated me diplomatically—that is to say, like a man who wishes, by some means or other, to obtain a footing in the house, so that he may ultimately gain the power of dictating to its occupants—he would, if it had been but once, have honored me with the smile which you extol so loudly; but no, he saw that I was unhappy, he understood that I could be of no use to him, and therefore paid no attention to me whatever. Who knows but that, in order to please Madame de Villefort and my father, he may not persecute me by every means in his power? It is not just that he should despise me so, without any reason. Ah, forgive me,” said Valentine, perceiving the effect which her words were producing on Maximilian: “I have done wrong, for I have given utterance to thoughts concerning that man which I did not even know existed in my heart. I do not deny the influence of which you speak, or that I have not myself experienced it, but with me it has been productive of evil rather than good.”\n\n“Well, Valentine,” said Morrel with a sigh, “we will not discuss the matter further. I will not make a confidant of him.”\n\n“Alas!” said Valentine, “I see that I have given you pain. I can only say how sincerely I ask pardon for having grieved you. But, indeed, I am not prejudiced beyond the power of conviction. Tell me what this Count of Monte Cristo has done for you.”\n\n30157m\n“I own that your question embarrasses me, Valentine, for I cannot say that the count has rendered me any ostensible service. Still, as I have already told you, I have an instinctive affection for him, the source of which I cannot explain to you. Has the sun done anything for me? No; he warms me with his rays, and it is by his light that I see you—nothing more. Has such and such a perfume done anything for me? No; its odor charms one of my senses—that is all I can say when I am asked why I praise it. My friendship for him is as strange and unaccountable as his for me. A secret voice seems to whisper to me that there must be something more than chance in this unexpected reciprocity of friendship. In his most simple actions, as well as in his most secret thoughts, I find a relation to my own. You will perhaps smile at me when I tell you that, ever since I have known this man, I have involuntarily entertained the idea that all the good fortune which has befallen me originated from him. However, I have managed to live thirty years without this protection, you will say; but I will endeavor a little to illustrate my meaning. He invited me to dine with him on Saturday, which was a very natural thing for him to do. Well, what have I learned since? That your mother and M. de Villefort are both coming to this dinner. I shall meet them there, and who knows what future advantages may result from the interview? This may appear to you to be no unusual combination of circumstances; nevertheless, I perceive some hidden plot in the arrangement—something, in fact, more than is apparent on a casual view of the subject. I believe that this singular man, who appears to fathom the motives of everyone, has purposely arranged for me to meet M. and Madame de Villefort, and sometimes, I confess, I have gone so far as to try to read in his eyes whether he was in possession of the secret of our love.”\n\n“My good friend,” said Valentine, “I should take you for a visionary, and should tremble for your reason, if I were always to hear you talk in a strain similar to this. Is it possible that you can see anything more than the merest chance in this meeting? Pray reflect a little. My father, who never goes out, has several times been on the point of refusing this invitation; Madame de Villefort, on the contrary, is burning with the desire of seeing this extraordinary nabob in his own house, therefore, she has with great difficulty prevailed on my father to accompany her. No, no; it is as I have said, Maximilian,—there is no one in the world of whom I can ask help but yourself and my grandfather, who is little better than a corpse—no support to cling to but my mother in heaven!”\n\n“I see that you are right, logically speaking,” said Maximilian; “but the gentle voice which usually has such power over me fails to convince me today.”\n\n“I feel the same as regards yourself.” said Valentine; “and I own that, if you have no stronger proof to give me——”\n\n“I have another,” replied Maximilian; “but I fear you will deem it even more absurd than the first.”\n\n“So much the worse,” said Valentine, smiling.\n\n“It is, nevertheless, conclusive to my mind. My ten years of service have also confirmed my ideas on the subject of sudden inspirations, for I have several times owed my life to a mysterious impulse which directed me to move at once either to the right or to the left, in order to escape the ball which killed the comrade fighting by my side, while it left me unharmed.”\n\n“Dear Maximilian, why not attribute your escape to my constant prayers for your safety? When you are away, I no longer pray for myself, but for you.”\n30159m\n\n“Yes, since you have known me,” said Morrel, smiling; “but that cannot apply to the time previous to our acquaintance, Valentine.”\n\n“You are very provoking, and will not give me credit for anything; but let me hear this second proof, which you yourself own to be absurd.”\n\n“Well, look through this opening, and you will see the beautiful new horse which I rode here.”\n\n“Ah! what a beautiful creature!” cried Valentine; “why did you not bring him close to the gate, so that I could talk to him and pat him?”\n\n“He is, as you see, a very valuable animal,” said Maximilian. “You know that my means are limited, and that I am what would be designated a man of moderate pretensions. Well, I went to a horse dealer’s, where I saw this magnificent horse, which I have named Médéah. I asked the price; they told me it was 4,500 francs. I was, therefore, obliged to give it up, as you may imagine, but I own I went away with rather a heavy heart, for the horse had looked at me affectionately, had rubbed his head against me and, when I mounted him, had pranced in the most delightful way imaginable, so that I was altogether fascinated with him. The same evening some friends of mine visited me,—M. de Château-Renaud, M. Debray, and five or six other choice spirits, whom you do not know, even by name. They proposed a game of _bouillotte_. I never play, for I am not rich enough to afford to lose, or sufficiently poor to desire to gain. But I was at my own house, you understand, so there was nothing to be done but to send for the cards, which I did.\n\n“Just as they were sitting down to table, M. de Monte Cristo arrived. He took his seat amongst them; they played, and I won. I am almost ashamed to say that my gains amounted to 5,000 francs. We separated at midnight. I could not defer my pleasure, so I took a cabriolet and drove to the horse dealer’s. Feverish and excited, I rang at the door. The person who opened it must have taken me for a madman, for I rushed at once to the stable. Médéah was standing at the rack, eating his hay. I immediately put on the saddle and bridle, to which operation he lent himself with the best grace possible; then, putting the 4,500 francs into the hands of the astonished dealer, I proceeded to fulfil my intention of passing the night in riding in the Champs-Élysées. As I rode by the count’s house I perceived a light in one of the windows, and fancied I saw the shadow of his figure moving behind the curtain. Now, Valentine, I firmly believe that he knew of my wish to possess this horse, and that he lost expressly to give me the means of procuring him.”\n\n“My dear Maximilian, you are really too fanciful; you will not love even me long. A man who accustoms himself to live in such a world of poetry and imagination must find far too little excitement in a common, every-day sort of attachment such as ours. But they are calling me. Do you hear?”\n\n“Ah, Valentine,” said Maximilian, “give me but one finger through this opening in the grating, one finger, the littlest finger of all, that I may have the happiness of kissing it.”\n\n“Maximilian, we said we would be to each other as two voices, two shadows.”\n\n“As you will, Valentine.”\n\n“Shall you be happy if I do what you wish?”\n\n“Oh, yes!”\n\nValentine mounted on a bench, and passed not only her finger but her whole hand through the opening. Maximilian uttered a cry of delight, and, springing forwards, seized the hand extended towards him, and imprinted on it a fervent and impassioned kiss. The little hand was then immediately withdrawn, and the young man saw Valentine hurrying towards the house, as though she were almost terrified at her own sensations.\n\n# Chapter 58. M. Noirtier de Villefort\n\nWe will now relate what was passing in the house of the king’s attorney after the departure of Madame Danglars and her daughter, and during the time of the conversation between Maximilian and Valentine, which we have just detailed.\n\nM. de Villefort entered his father’s room, followed by Madame de Villefort. Both of the visitors, after saluting the old man and speaking to Barrois, a faithful servant, who had been twenty-five years in his service, took their places on either side of the paralytic.\n\nM. Noirtier was sitting in an armchair, which moved upon casters, in which he was wheeled into the room in the morning, and in the same way drawn out again at night. He was placed before a large glass, which reflected the whole apartment, and so, without any attempt to move, which would have been impossible, he could see all who entered the room and everything which was going on around him. M. Noirtier, although almost as immovable as a corpse, looked at the new-comers with a quick and intelligent expression, perceiving at once, by their ceremonious courtesy, that they were come on business of an unexpected and official character.\n\nSight and hearing were the only senses remaining, and they, like two solitary sparks, remained to animate the miserable body which seemed fit for nothing but the grave; it was only, however, by means of one of these senses that he could reveal the thoughts and feelings that still occupied his mind, and the look by which he gave expression to his inner life was like the distant gleam of a candle which a traveller sees by night across some desert place, and knows that a living being dwells beyond the silence and obscurity.\n\nNoirtier’s hair was long and white, and flowed over his shoulders; while in his eyes, shaded by thick black lashes, was concentrated, as it often happens with an organ which is used to the exclusion of the others, all the activity, address, force, and intelligence which were formerly diffused over his whole body; and so although the movement of the arm, the sound of the voice, and the agility of the body, were wanting, the speaking eye sufficed for all. He commanded with it; it was the medium through which his thanks were conveyed. In short, his whole appearance produced on the mind the impression of a corpse with living eyes, and nothing could be more startling than to observe the expression of anger or joy suddenly lighting up these organs, while the rest of the rigid and marble-like features were utterly deprived of the power of participation. Three persons only could understand this language of the poor paralytic; these were Villefort, Valentine, and the old servant of whom we have already spoken. But as Villefort saw his father but seldom, and then only when absolutely obliged, and as he never took any pains to please or gratify him when he was there, all the old man’s happiness was centred in his granddaughter. Valentine, by means of her love, her patience, and her devotion, had learned to read in Noirtier’s look all the varied feelings which were passing in his mind. To this dumb language, which was so unintelligible to others, she answered by throwing her whole soul into the expression of her countenance, and in this manner were the conversations sustained between the blooming girl and the helpless invalid, whose body could scarcely be called a living one, but who, nevertheless, possessed a fund of knowledge and penetration, united with a will as powerful as ever although clogged by a body rendered utterly incapable of obeying its impulses.\n\nValentine had solved the problem, and was able easily to understand his thoughts, and to convey her own in return, and, through her untiring and devoted assiduity, it was seldom that, in the ordinary transactions of every-day life, she failed to anticipate the wishes of the living, thinking mind, or the wants of the almost inanimate body.\n\nAs to the servant, he had, as we have said, been with his master for five-and-twenty years, therefore he knew all his habits, and it was seldom that Noirtier found it necessary to ask for anything, so prompt was he in administering to all the necessities of the invalid.\n\nVillefort did not need the help of either Valentine or the domestic in order to carry on with his father the strange conversation which he was about to begin. As we have said, he perfectly understood the old man’s vocabulary, and if he did not use it more often, it was only indifference and _ennui_ which prevented him from so doing. He therefore allowed Valentine to go into the garden, sent away Barrois, and after having seated himself at his father’s right hand, while Madame de Villefort placed herself on the left, he addressed him thus:\n\n“I trust you will not be displeased, sir, that Valentine has not come with us, or that I dismissed Barrois, for our conference will be one which could not with propriety be carried on in the presence of either. Madame de Villefort and I have a communication to make to you.”\n\nNoirtier’s face remained perfectly passive during this long preamble, while, on the contrary, Villefort’s eye was endeavoring to penetrate into the inmost recesses of the old man’s heart.\n\n“This communication,” continued the procureur, in that cold and decisive tone which seemed at once to preclude all discussion, “will, we are sure, meet with your approbation.”\n\nThe eye of the invalid still retained that vacancy of expression which prevented his son from obtaining any knowledge of the feelings which were passing in his mind; he listened, nothing more.\n\n“Sir,” resumed Villefort, “we are thinking of marrying Valentine.” Had the old man’s face been moulded in wax it could not have shown less emotion at this news than was now to be traced there. “The marriage will take place in less than three months,” said Villefort.\n\nNoirtier’s eye still retained its inanimate expression.\n\nMadame de Villefort now took her part in the conversation and added:\n\n“We thought this news would possess an interest for you, sir, who have always entertained a great affection for Valentine; it therefore only now remains for us to tell you the name of the young man for whom she is destined. It is one of the most desirable connections which could possibly be formed; he possesses fortune, a high rank in society, and every personal qualification likely to render Valentine supremely happy,—his name, moreover, cannot be wholly unknown to you. It is M. Franz de Quesnel, Baron d’Épinay.”\n\nWhile his wife was speaking, Villefort had narrowly watched the old man’s countenance. When Madame de Villefort pronounced the name of Franz, the pupil of M. Noirtier’s eye began to dilate, and his eyelids trembled with the same movement that may be perceived on the lips of an individual about to speak, and he darted a lightning glance at Madame de Villefort and his son. The procureur, who knew the political hatred which had formerly existed between M. Noirtier and the elder d’Épinay, well understood the agitation and anger which the announcement had produced; but, feigning not to perceive either, he immediately resumed the narrative begun by his wife.\n\n“Sir,” said he, “you are aware that Valentine is about to enter her nineteenth year, which renders it important that she should lose no time in forming a suitable alliance. Nevertheless, you have not been forgotten in our plans, and we have fully ascertained beforehand that Valentine’s future husband will consent, not to live in this house, for that might not be pleasant for the young people, but that you should live with them; so that you and Valentine, who are so attached to each other, would not be separated, and you would be able to pursue exactly the same course of life which you have hitherto done, and thus, instead of losing, you will be a gainer by the change, as it will secure to you two children instead of one, to watch over and comfort you.”\n30165m\n\nNoirtier’s look was furious; it was very evident that something desperate was passing in the old man’s mind, for a cry of anger and grief rose in his throat, and not being able to find vent in utterance, appeared almost to choke him, for his face and lips turned quite purple with the struggle. Villefort quietly opened a window, saying, “It is very warm, and the heat affects M. Noirtier.” He then returned to his place, but did not sit down.\n\n“This marriage,” added Madame de Villefort, “is quite agreeable to the wishes of M. d’Épinay and his family; besides, he had no relations nearer than an uncle and aunt, his mother having died at his birth, and his father having been assassinated in 1815, that is to say, when he was but two years old; it naturally followed that the child was permitted to choose his own pursuits, and he has, therefore, seldom acknowledged any other authority but that of his own will.”\n\n“That assassination was a mysterious affair,” said Villefort, “and the perpetrators have hitherto escaped detection, although suspicion has fallen on the head of more than one person.”\n\nNoirtier made such an effort that his lips expanded into a smile.\n\n“Now,” continued Villefort, “those to whom the guilt really belongs, by whom the crime was committed, on whose heads the justice of man may probably descend here, and the certain judgment of God hereafter, would rejoice in the opportunity thus afforded of bestowing such a peace-offering as Valentine on the son of him whose life they so ruthlessly destroyed.” Noirtier had succeeded in mastering his emotion more than could have been deemed possible with such an enfeebled and shattered frame.\n\n“Yes, I understand,” was the reply contained in his look; and this look expressed a feeling of strong indignation, mixed with profound contempt. Villefort fully understood his father’s meaning, and answered by a slight shrug of his shoulders. He then motioned to his wife to take leave.\n\n“Now sir,” said Madame de Villefort, “I must bid you farewell. Would you like me to send Edward to you for a short time?”\n\nIt had been agreed that the old man should express his approbation by closing his eyes, his refusal by winking them several times, and if he had some desire or feeling to express, he raised them to heaven. If he wanted Valentine, he closed his right eye only, and if Barrois, the left. At Madame de Villefort’s proposition he instantly winked his eyes.\n\nProvoked by a complete refusal, she bit her lip and said, “Then shall I send Valentine to you?” The old man closed his eyes eagerly, thereby intimating that such was his wish.\n\nM. and Madame de Villefort bowed and left the room, giving orders that Valentine should be summoned to her grandfather’s presence, and feeling sure that she would have much to do to restore calmness to the perturbed spirit of the invalid. Valentine, with a color still heightened by emotion, entered the room just after her parents had quitted it. One look was sufficient to tell her that her grandfather was suffering, and that there was much on his mind which he was wishing to communicate to her.\n\n“Dear grandpapa,” cried she, “what has happened? They have vexed you, and you are angry?”\n\nThe paralytic closed his eyes in token of assent.\n\n“Who has displeased you? Is it my father?”\n“No.”\n\n“Madame de Villefort?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Me?” The former sign was repeated.\n\n“Are you displeased with me?” cried Valentine in astonishment. M. Noirtier again closed his eyes.\n\n“And what have I done, dear grandpapa, that you should be angry with me?” cried Valentine.\n\nThere was no answer, and she continued:\n\n“I have not seen you all day. Has anyone been speaking to you against me?”\n\n“Yes,” said the old man’s look, with eagerness.\n\n“Let me think a moment. I do assure you, grandpapa—Ah—M. and Madame de Villefort have just left this room, have they not?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“And it was they who told you something which made you angry? What was it then? May I go and ask them, that I may have the opportunity of making my peace with you?”\n\n“No, no,” said Noirtier’s look.\n\n“Ah, you frighten me. What can they have said?” and she again tried to think what it could be.\n\n“Ah, I know,” said she, lowering her voice and going close to the old man. “They have been speaking of my marriage,—have they not?”\n\n“Yes,” replied the angry look.\n\n“I understand; you are displeased at the silence I have preserved on the subject. The reason of it was, that they had insisted on my keeping the matter a secret, and begged me not to tell you anything of it. They did not even acquaint me with their intentions, and I only discovered them by chance, that is why I have been so reserved with you, dear grandpapa. Pray forgive me.”\n\nBut there was no look calculated to reassure her; all it seemed to say was, “It is not only your reserve which afflicts me.”\n\n“What is it, then?” asked the young girl. “Perhaps you think I shall abandon you, dear grandpapa, and that I shall forget you when I am married?”\n“No.”\n\n“They told you, then, that M. d’Épinay consented to our all living together?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Then why are you still vexed and grieved?” The old man’s eyes beamed with an expression of gentle affection.\n\n“Yes, I understand,” said Valentine; “it is because you love me.” The old man assented.\n\n“And you are afraid I shall be unhappy?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You do not like M. Franz?” The eyes repeated several times, “No, no, no.”\n\n“Then you are vexed with the engagement?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Well, listen,” said Valentine, throwing herself on her knees, and putting her arm round her grandfather’s neck, “I am vexed, too, for I do not love M. Franz d’Épinay.”\n\nAn expression of intense joy illumined the old man’s eyes.\n\n“When I wished to retire into a convent, you remember how angry you were with me?” A tear trembled in the eye of the invalid. “Well,” continued Valentine, “the reason of my proposing it was that I might escape this hateful marriage, which drives me to despair.” Noirtier’s breathing came thick and short.\n\n“Then the idea of this marriage really grieves you too? Ah, if you could but help me—if we could both together defeat their plan! But you are unable to oppose them,—you, whose mind is so quick, and whose will is so firm are nevertheless, as weak and unequal to the contest as I am myself. Alas, you, who would have been such a powerful protector to me in the days of your health and strength, can now only sympathize in my joys and sorrows, without being able to take any active part in them. However, this is much, and calls for gratitude and Heaven has not taken away all my blessings when it leaves me your sympathy and kindness.”\n\nAt these words there appeared in Noirtier’s eye an expression of such deep meaning that the young girl thought she could read these words there: “You are mistaken; I can still do much for you.”\n\n“Do you think you can help me, dear grandpapa?” said Valentine.\n\n“Yes.” Noirtier raised his eyes, it was the sign agreed on between him and Valentine when he wanted anything.\n\n“What is it you want, dear grandpapa?” said Valentine, and she endeavored to recall to mind all the things which he would be likely to need; and as the ideas presented themselves to her mind, she repeated them aloud, then,—finding that all her efforts elicited nothing but a constant _“No,”_—she said, “Come, since this plan does not answer, I will have recourse to another.”\n\nShe then recited all the letters of the alphabet from A down to N. When she arrived at that letter the paralytic made her understand that she had spoken the initial letter of the thing he wanted.\n\n“Ah,” said Valentine, “the thing you desire begins with the letter N; it is with N that we have to do, then. Well, let me see, what can you want that begins with N? Na—Ne—Ni—No——”\n\n“Yes, yes, yes,” said the old man’s eye.\n\n“Ah, it is No, then?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nValentine fetched a dictionary, which she placed on a desk before Noirtier; she opened it, and, seeing that the old man’s eye was thoroughly fixed on its pages, she ran her finger quickly up and down the columns. During the six years which had passed since Noirtier first fell into this sad state, Valentine’s powers of invention had been too often put to the test not to render her expert in devising expedients for gaining a knowledge of his wishes, and the constant practice had so perfected her in the art that she guessed the old man’s meaning as quickly as if he himself had been able to seek for what he wanted. At the word _Notary_, Noirtier made a sign to her to stop.\n30169m\n\n“Notary,” said she, “do you want a notary, dear grandpapa?” The old man again signified that it was a notary he desired.\n\n“You would wish a notary to be sent for then?” said Valentine.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Shall my father be informed of your wish?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Do you wish the notary to be sent for immediately?”\n\n“Yes.”\n“Then they shall go for him directly, dear grandpapa. Is that all you want?”\n\n“Yes.” Valentine rang the bell, and ordered the servant to tell Monsieur or Madame de Villefort that they were requested to come to M. Noirtier’s room.\n\n“Are you satisfied now?” inquired Valentine.\n\n“Yes.”\n“I am sure you are; it is not very difficult to discover that.” And the young girl smiled on her grandfather, as if he had been a child. M. de Villefort entered, followed by Barrois.\n\n“What do you want me for, sir?” demanded he of the paralytic.\n\n“Sir,” said Valentine, “my grandfather wishes for a notary.” At this strange and unexpected demand M. de Villefort and his father exchanged looks.\n\n“Yes,” motioned the latter, with a firmness which seemed to declare that with the help of Valentine and his old servant, who both knew what his wishes were, he was quite prepared to maintain the contest.\n\n“Do you wish for a notary?” asked Villefort.\n\n“Yes.”\n“What to do?”\n\nNoirtier made no answer.\n\n“What do you want with a notary?” again repeated Villefort. The invalid’s eye remained fixed, by which expression he intended to intimate that his resolution was unalterable.\n\n“Is it to do us some ill turn? Do you think it is worth while?” said Villefort.\n\n“Still,” said Barrois, with the freedom and fidelity of an old servant, “if M. Noirtier asks for a notary, I suppose he really wishes for a notary; therefore I shall go at once and fetch one.” Barrois acknowledged no master but Noirtier, and never allowed his desires in any way to be contradicted.\n30171m\n\n“Yes, I do want a notary,” motioned the old man, shutting his eyes with a look of defiance, which seemed to say, “and I should like to see the person who dares to refuse my request.”\n\n“You shall have a notary, as you absolutely wish for one, sir,” said Villefort; “but I shall explain to him your state of health, and make excuses for you, for the scene cannot fail of being a most ridiculous one.”\n\n“Never mind that,” said Barrois; “I shall go and fetch a notary, nevertheless.” And the old servant departed triumphantly on his mission.\n\n# Chapter 59. The Will\n\nAs soon as Barrois had left the room, Noirtier looked at Valentine with a malicious expression that said many things. The young girl perfectly understood the look, and so did Villefort, for his countenance became clouded, and he knitted his eyebrows angrily. He took a seat, and quietly awaited the arrival of the notary. Noirtier saw him seat himself with an appearance of perfect indifference, at the same time giving a side look at Valentine, which made her understand that she also was to remain in the room. Three-quarters of an hour after, Barrois returned, bringing the notary with him.\n\n“Sir,” said Villefort, after the first salutations were over, “you were sent for by M. Noirtier, whom you see here. All his limbs have become completely paralysed, he has lost his voice also, and we ourselves find much trouble in endeavoring to catch some fragments of his meaning.”\n\nNoirtier cast an appealing look on Valentine, which look was at once so earnest and imperative, that she answered immediately.\n\n“Sir,” said she, “I perfectly understand my grandfather’s meaning at all times.”\n\n“That is quite true,” said Barrois; “and that is what I told the gentleman as we walked along.”\n\n“Permit me,” said the notary, turning first to Villefort and then to Valentine—“permit me to state that the case in question is just one of those in which a public officer like myself cannot proceed to act without thereby incurring a dangerous responsibility. The first thing necessary to render an act valid is, that the notary should be thoroughly convinced that he has faithfully interpreted the will and wishes of the person dictating the act. Now I cannot be sure of the approbation or disapprobation of a client who cannot speak, and as the object of his desire or his repugnance cannot be clearly proved to me, on account of his want of speech, my services here would be quite useless, and cannot be legally exercised.”\n\nThe notary then prepared to retire. An imperceptible smile of triumph was expressed on the lips of the procureur. Noirtier looked at Valentine with an expression so full of grief, that she arrested the departure of the notary.\n\n“Sir,” said she, “the language which I speak with my grandfather may be easily learnt, and I can teach you in a few minutes, to understand it almost as well as I can myself. Will you tell me what you require, in order to set your conscience quite at ease on the subject?”\n\n“In order to render an act valid, I must be certain of the approbation or disapprobation of my client. Illness of body would not affect the validity of the deed, but sanity of mind is absolutely requisite.”\n\n“Well, sir, by the help of two signs, with which I will acquaint you presently, you may ascertain with perfect certainty that my grandfather is still in the full possession of all his mental faculties. M. Noirtier, being deprived of voice and motion, is accustomed to convey his meaning by closing his eyes when he wishes to signify ‘yes,’ and to wink when he means ‘no.’ You now know quite enough to enable you to converse with M. Noirtier;—try.”\n\nNoirtier gave Valentine such a look of tenderness and gratitude that it was comprehended even by the notary himself.\n\n“You have heard and understood what your granddaughter has been saying, sir, have you?” asked the notary. Noirtier closed his eyes.\n\n“And you approve of what she said—that is to say, you declare that the signs which she mentioned are really those by means of which you are accustomed to convey your thoughts?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“It was you who sent for me?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“To make your will?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“And you do not wish me to go away without fulfilling your original intentions?” The old man winked violently.\n\n“Well, sir,” said the young girl, “do you understand now, and is your conscience perfectly at rest on the subject?”\n\nBut before the notary could answer, Villefort had drawn him aside.\n\n“Sir,” said he, “do you suppose for a moment that a man can sustain a physical shock, such as M. Noirtier has received, without any detriment to his mental faculties?”\n\n“It is not exactly that, sir,” said the notary, “which makes me uneasy, but the difficulty will be in wording his thoughts and intentions, so as to be able to get his answers.”\n\n“You must see that to be an utter impossibility,” said Villefort. Valentine and the old man heard this conversation, and Noirtier fixed his eye so earnestly on Valentine that she felt bound to answer to the look.\n\n“Sir,” said she, “that need not make you uneasy, however difficult it may at first sight appear to be. I can discover and explain to you my grandfather’s thoughts, so as to put an end to all your doubts and fears on the subject. I have now been six years with M. Noirtier, and let him tell you if ever once, during that time, he has entertained a thought which he was unable to make me understand.”\n\n“No,” signed the old man.\n\n“Let us try what we can do, then,” said the notary. “You accept this young lady as your interpreter, M. Noirtier?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Well, sir, what do you require of me, and what document is it that you wish to be drawn up?”\n\nValentine named all the letters of the alphabet until she came to W. At this letter the eloquent eye of Noirtier gave her notice that she was to stop.\n\n“It is very evident that it is the letter W which M. Noirtier wants,” said the notary.\n\n“Wait,” said Valentine; and, turning to her grandfather, she repeated, “Wa—We—Wi——” The old man stopped her at the last syllable. Valentine then took the dictionary, and the notary watched her while she turned over the pages.\n\nShe passed her finger slowly down the columns, and when she came to the word “Will,” M. Noirtier’s eye bade her stop.\n\n“Will,” said the notary; “it is very evident that M. Noirtier is desirous of making his will.”\n\n“Yes, yes, yes,” motioned the invalid.\n\n“Really, sir, you must allow that this is most extraordinary,” said the astonished notary, turning to M. de Villefort.\n\n“Yes,” said the procureur, “and I think the will promises to be yet more extraordinary, for I cannot see how it is to be drawn up without the intervention of Valentine, and she may, perhaps, be considered as too much interested in its contents to allow of her being a suitable interpreter of the obscure and ill-defined wishes of her grandfather.”\n\n“No, no, no,” replied the eye of the paralytic.\n\n“What?” said Villefort, “do you mean to say that Valentine is not interested in your will?”\n\n“No.”\n“Sir,” said the notary, whose interest had been greatly excited, and who had resolved on publishing far and wide the account of this extraordinary and picturesque scene, “what appeared so impossible to me an hour ago, has now become quite easy and practicable, and this may be a perfectly valid will, provided it be read in the presence of seven witnesses, approved by the testator, and sealed by the notary in the presence of the witnesses. As to the time, it will not require very much more than the generality of wills. There are certain forms necessary to be gone through, and which are always the same. As to the details, the greater part will be furnished afterwards by the state in which we find the affairs of the testator, and by yourself, who, having had the management of them, can doubtless give full information on the subject. But besides all this, in order that the instrument may not be contested, I am anxious to give it the greatest possible authenticity, therefore, one of my colleagues will help me, and, contrary to custom, will assist in the dictation of the testament. Are you satisfied, sir?” continued the notary, addressing the old man.\n\n30175m\n“Yes,” looked the invalid, his eye beaming with delight at the ready interpretation of his meaning.\n\n“What is he going to do?” thought Villefort, whose position demanded much reserve, but who was longing to know what his father’s intentions were. He left the room to give orders for another notary to be sent, but Barrois, who had heard all that passed, had guessed his master’s wishes, and had already gone to fetch one. The procureur then told his wife to come up. In the course of a quarter of an hour everyone had assembled in the chamber of the paralytic; the second notary had also arrived.\n\nA few words sufficed for a mutual understanding between the two officers of the law. They read to Noirtier the formal copy of a will, in order to give him an idea of the terms in which such documents are generally couched; then, in order to test the capacity of the testator, the first notary said, turning towards him:\n\n“When an individual makes his will, it is generally in favor or in prejudice of some person.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Have you an exact idea of the amount of your fortune?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“I will name to you several sums which will increase by gradation; you will stop me when I reach the one representing the amount of your own possessions?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThere was a kind of solemnity in this interrogation. Never had the struggle between mind and matter been more apparent than now, and if it was not a sublime, it was, at least, a curious spectacle. They had formed a circle round the invalid; the second notary was sitting at a table, prepared for writing, and his colleague was standing before the testator in the act of interrogating him on the subject to which we have alluded.\n\n“Your fortune exceeds 300,000 francs, does it not?” asked he. Noirtier made a sign that it did.\n\n“Do you possess 400,000 francs?” inquired the notary. Noirtier’s eye remained immovable.\n\n“500,000?” The same expression continued.\n\n“600,000—700,000—800,000—900,000?”\n\nNoirtier stopped him at the last-named sum.\n\n“You are then in possession of 900,000 francs?” asked the notary.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“In landed property?”\n\n“No.”\n“In stock?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“The stock is in your own hands?”\n\nThe look which M. Noirtier cast on Barrois showed that there was something wanting which he knew where to find. The old servant left the room, and presently returned, bringing with him a small casket.\n\n“Do you permit us to open this casket?” asked the notary. Noirtier gave his assent.\n\nThey opened it, and found 900,000 francs in bank scrip. The first notary handed over each note, as he examined it, to his colleague.\n\nThe total amount was found to be as M. Noirtier had stated.\n\n“It is all as he has said; it is very evident that the mind still retains its full force and vigor.” Then, turning towards the paralytic, he said, “You possess, then, 900,000 francs of capital, which, according to the manner in which you have invested it, ought to bring in an income of about 40,000 livres?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“To whom do you desire to leave this fortune?”\n\n“Oh!” said Madame de Villefort, “there is not much doubt on that subject. M. Noirtier tenderly loves his granddaughter, Mademoiselle de Villefort; it is she who has nursed and tended him for six years, and has, by her devoted attention, fully secured the affection, I had almost said the gratitude, of her grandfather, and it is but just that she should reap the fruit of her devotion.”\n\nThe eye of Noirtier clearly showed by its expression that he was not deceived by the false assent given by Madame de Villefort’s words and manner to the motives which she supposed him to entertain.\n\n“Is it, then, to Mademoiselle Valentine de Villefort that you leave these 900,000 francs?” demanded the notary, thinking he had only to insert this clause, but waiting first for the assent of Noirtier, which it was necessary should be given before all the witnesses of this singular scene.\n\nValentine, when her name was made the subject of discussion, had stepped back, to escape unpleasant observation; her eyes were cast down, and she was crying. The old man looked at her for an instant with an expression of the deepest tenderness, then, turning towards the notary, he significantly winked his eye in token of dissent.\n\n“What,” said the notary, “do you not intend making Mademoiselle Valentine de Villefort your residuary legatee?”\n\n“No.”\n“You are not making any mistake, are you?” said the notary; “you really mean to declare that such is not your intention?”\n\n“No,” repeated Noirtier; “No.”\n\nValentine raised her head, struck dumb with astonishment. It was not so much the conviction that she was disinherited that caused her grief, but her total inability to account for the feelings which had provoked her grandfather to such an act. But Noirtier looked at her with so much affectionate tenderness that she exclaimed:\n\n“Oh, grandpapa, I see now that it is only your fortune of which you deprive me; you still leave me the love which I have always enjoyed.”\n\n“Ah, yes, most assuredly,” said the eyes of the paralytic, for he closed them with an expression which Valentine could not mistake.\n\n“Thank you, thank you,” murmured she. The old man’s declaration that Valentine was not the destined inheritor of his fortune had excited the hopes of Madame de Villefort; she gradually approached the invalid, and said:\n\n“Then, doubtless, dear M. Noirtier, you intend leaving your fortune to your grandson, Edward de Villefort?”\n\nThe winking of the eyes which answered this speech was most decided and terrible, and expressed a feeling almost amounting to hatred.\n\n“No?” said the notary; “then, perhaps, it is to your son, M. de Villefort?”\n\n“No.” The two notaries looked at each other in mute astonishment and inquiry as to what were the real intentions of the testator. Villefort and his wife both grew red, one from shame, the other from anger.\n\n“What have we all done, then, dear grandpapa?” said Valentine; “you no longer seem to love any of us?”\n\nThe old man’s eyes passed rapidly from Villefort and his wife, and rested on Valentine with a look of unutterable fondness.\n\n“Well,” said she; “if you love me, grandpapa, try and bring that love to bear upon your actions at this present moment. You know me well enough to be quite sure that I have never thought of your fortune; besides, they say I am already rich in right of my mother—too rich, even. Explain yourself, then.”\n\nNoirtier fixed his intelligent eyes on Valentine’s hand.\n\n“My hand?” said she.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Her hand!” exclaimed everyone.\n\n“Oh, gentlemen, you see it is all useless, and that my father’s mind is really impaired,” said Villefort.\n\n“Ah,” cried Valentine suddenly, “I understand. It is my marriage you mean, is it not, dear grandpapa?”\n\n“Yes, yes, yes,” signed the paralytic, casting on Valentine a look of joyful gratitude for having guessed his meaning.\n30179m\n\n“You are angry with us all on account of this marriage, are you not?”\n\n“Yes?”\n\n“Really, this is too absurd,” said Villefort.\n\n“Excuse me, sir,” replied the notary; “on the contrary, the meaning of M. Noirtier is quite evident to me, and I can quite easily connect the train of ideas passing in his mind.”\n\n“You do not wish me to marry M. Franz d’Épinay?” observed Valentine.\n\n“I do not wish it,” said the eye of her grandfather.\n\n“And you disinherit your granddaughter,” continued the notary, “because she has contracted an engagement contrary to your wishes?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“So that, but for this marriage, she would have been your heir?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThere was a profound silence. The two notaries were holding a consultation as to the best means of proceeding with the affair. Valentine was looking at her grandfather with a smile of intense gratitude, and Villefort was biting his lips with vexation, while Madame de Villefort could not succeed in repressing an inward feeling of joy, which, in spite of herself, appeared in her whole countenance.\n\n“But,” said Villefort, who was the first to break the silence, “I consider that I am the best judge of the propriety of the marriage in question. I am the only person possessing the right to dispose of my daughter’s hand. It is my wish that she should marry M. Franz d’Épinay—and she shall marry him.”\n\nValentine sank weeping into a chair.\n\n“Sir,” said the notary, “how do you intend disposing of your fortune in case Mademoiselle de Villefort still determines on marrying M. Franz?” The old man gave no answer.\n\n“You will, of course, dispose of it in some way or other?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“In favor of some member of your family?”\n\n“No.”\n“Do you intend devoting it to charitable purposes, then?” pursued the notary.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“But,” said the notary, “you are aware that the law does not allow a son to be entirely deprived of his patrimony?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You only intend, then, to dispose of that part of your fortune which the law allows you to subtract from the inheritance of your son?” Noirtier made no answer.\n\n“Do you still wish to dispose of all?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“But they will contest the will after your death?”\n“No.”\n\n“My father knows me,” replied Villefort; “he is quite sure that his wishes will be held sacred by me; besides, he understands that in my position I cannot plead against the poor.” The eye of Noirtier beamed with triumph.\n\n“What do you decide on, sir?” asked the notary of Villefort.\n\n“Nothing, sir; it is a resolution which my father has taken and I know he never alters his mind. I am quite resigned. These 900,000 francs will go out of the family in order to enrich some hospital; but it is ridiculous thus to yield to the caprices of an old man, and I shall, therefore, act according to my conscience.”\n\nHaving said this, Villefort quitted the room with his wife, leaving his father at liberty to do as he pleased. The same day the will was made, the witnesses were brought, it was approved by the old man, sealed in the presence of all and given in charge to M. Deschamps, the family notary.\n\n# Chapter 60. The Telegraph\n\nM. and Madame de Villefort found on their return that the Count of Monte Cristo, who had come to visit them in their absence, had been ushered into the drawing-room, and was still awaiting them there. Madame de Villefort, who had not yet sufficiently recovered from her late emotion to allow of her entertaining visitors so immediately, retired to her bedroom, while the procureur, who could better depend upon himself, proceeded at once to the salon.\n\nAlthough M. de Villefort flattered himself that, to all outward view, he had completely masked the feelings which were passing in his mind, he did not know that the cloud was still lowering on his brow, so much so that the count, whose smile was radiant, immediately noticed his sombre and thoughtful air.\n\n“_Ma foi!_” said Monte Cristo, after the first compliments were over, “what is the matter with you, M. de Villefort? Have I arrived at the moment when you were drawing up an indictment for a capital crime?”\n\nVillefort tried to smile.\n\n“No, count,” he replied, “I am the only victim in this case. It is I who lose my cause, and it is ill-luck, obstinacy, and folly which have caused it to be decided against me.”\n\n“To what do you refer?” said Monte Cristo with well-feigned interest. “Have you really met with some great misfortune?”\n\n“Oh, no, monsieur,” said Villefort with a bitter smile; “it is only a loss of money which I have sustained—nothing worth mentioning, I assure you.”\n\n“True,” said Monte Cristo, “the loss of a sum of money becomes almost immaterial with a fortune such as you possess, and to one of your philosophic spirit.”\n\n“It is not so much the loss of the money that vexes me,” said Villefort, “though, after all, 900,000 francs are worth regretting; but I am the more annoyed with this fate, chance, or whatever you please to call the power which has destroyed my hopes and my fortune, and may blast the prospects of my child also, as it is all occasioned by an old man relapsed into second childhood.”\n30183m\n\n“What do you say?” said the count; “900,000 francs? It is indeed a sum which might be regretted even by a philosopher. And who is the cause of all this annoyance?”\n\n“My father, as I told you.”\n\n“M. Noirtier? But I thought you told me he had become entirely paralyzed, and that all his faculties were completely destroyed?”\n\n“Yes, his bodily faculties, for he can neither move nor speak, nevertheless he thinks, acts, and wills in the manner I have described. I left him about five minutes ago, and he is now occupied in dictating his will to two notaries.”\n\n“But to do this he must have spoken?”\n\n“He has done better than that—he has made himself understood.”\n\n“How was such a thing possible?”\n\n“By the help of his eyes, which are still full of life, and, as you perceive, possess the power of inflicting mortal injury.”\n\n“My dear,” said Madame de Villefort, who had just entered the room, “perhaps you exaggerate the evil.”\n\n“Good-morning, madame,” said the count, bowing.\n\nMadame de Villefort acknowledged the salutation with one of her most gracious smiles.\n\n“What is this that M. de Villefort has been telling me?” demanded Monte Cristo “and what incomprehensible misfortune——”\n\n“Incomprehensible is the word!” interrupted the procureur, shrugging his shoulders. “It is an old man’s caprice!”\n\n“And is there no means of making him revoke his decision?”\n\n“Yes,” said Madame de Villefort; “and it is still entirely in the power of my husband to cause the will, which is now in prejudice of Valentine, to be altered in her favor.”\n\nThe count, who perceived that M. and Madame de Villefort were beginning to speak in parables, appeared to pay no attention to the conversation, and feigned to be busily engaged in watching Edward, who was mischievously pouring some ink into the bird’s water-glass.\n\n“My dear,” said Villefort, in answer to his wife, “you know I have never been accustomed to play the patriarch in my family, nor have I ever considered that the fate of a universe was to be decided by my nod. Nevertheless, it is necessary that my will should be respected in my family, and that the folly of an old man and the caprice of a child should not be allowed to overturn a project which I have entertained for so many years. The Baron d’Épinay was my friend, as you know, and an alliance with his son is the most suitable thing that could possibly be arranged.”\n\n“Do you think,” said Madame de Villefort, “that Valentine is in league with him? She has always been opposed to this marriage, and I should not be at all surprised if what we have just seen and heard is nothing but the execution of a plan concerted between them.”\n\n“Madame,” said Villefort, “believe me, a fortune of 900,000 francs is not so easily renounced.”\n\n“She could, nevertheless, make up her mind to renounce the world, sir, since it is only about a year ago that she herself proposed entering a convent.”\n\n“Never mind,” replied Villefort; “I say that this marriage _shall_ be consummated.”\n\n“Notwithstanding your father’s wishes to the contrary?” said Madame de Villefort, selecting a new point of attack. “That is a serious thing.”\n\nMonte Cristo, who pretended not to be listening, heard however, every word that was said.\n\n30185m\n“Madame,” replied Villefort “I can truly say that I have always entertained a high respect for my father, because, to the natural feeling of relationship was added the consciousness of his moral superiority. The name of father is sacred in two senses; he should be reverenced as the author of our being and as a master whom we ought to obey. But, under the present circumstances, I am justified in doubting the wisdom of an old man who, because he hated the father, vents his anger on the son. It would be ridiculous in me to regulate my conduct by such caprices. I shall still continue to preserve the same respect toward M. Noirtier; I will suffer, without complaint, the pecuniary deprivation to which he has subjected me; but I shall remain firm in my determination, and the world shall see which party has reason on his side. Consequently I shall marry my daughter to the Baron Franz d’Épinay, because I consider it would be a proper and eligible match for her to make, and, in short, because I choose to bestow my daughter’s hand on whomever I please.”\n\n“What?” said the count, the approbation of whose eye Villefort had frequently solicited during this speech. “What? Do you say that M. Noirtier disinherits Mademoiselle de Villefort because she is going to marry M. le Baron Franz d’Épinay?”\n\n“Yes, sir, that is the reason,” said Villefort, shrugging his shoulders.\n\n“The apparent reason, at least,” said Madame de Villefort.\n\n“The _real_ reason, madame, I can assure you; I know my father.”\n\n“But I want to know in what way M. d’Épinay can have displeased your father more than any other person?”\n\n“I believe I know M. Franz d’Épinay,” said the count; “is he not the son of General de Quesnel, who was created Baron d’Épinay by Charles X.?”\n\n“The same,” said Villefort.\n\n“Well, but he is a charming young man, according to my ideas.”\n\n“He is, which makes me believe that it is only an excuse of M. Noirtier to prevent his granddaughter marrying; old men are always so selfish in their affection,” said Madame de Villefort.\n\n“But,” said Monte Cristo “do you not know any cause for this hatred?”\n\n“Ah, _ma foi!_ who is to know?”\n\n“Perhaps it is some political difference?”\n\n“My father and the Baron d’Épinay lived in the stormy times of which I only saw the ending,” said Villefort.\n\n“Was not your father a Bonapartist?” asked Monte Cristo; “I think I remember that you told me something of that kind.”\n\n“My father has been a Jacobin more than anything else,” said Villefort, carried by his emotion beyond the bounds of prudence; “and the senator’s robe, which Napoleon cast on his shoulders, only served to disguise the old man without in any degree changing him. When my father conspired, it was not for the emperor, it was against the Bourbons; for M. Noirtier possessed this peculiarity, he never projected any Utopian schemes which could never be realized, but strove for possibilities, and he applied to the realization of these possibilities the terrible theories of The Mountain,—theories that never shrank from any means that were deemed necessary to bring about the desired result.”\n\n“Well,” said Monte Cristo, “it is just as I thought; it was politics which brought Noirtier and M. d’Épinay into personal contact. Although General d’Épinay served under Napoleon, did he not still retain royalist sentiments? And was he not the person who was assassinated one evening on leaving a Bonapartist meeting to which he had been invited on the supposition that he favored the cause of the emperor?”\n\nVillefort looked at the count almost with terror.\n\n“Am I mistaken, then?” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“No, sir, the facts were precisely what you have stated,” said Madame de Villefort; “and it was to prevent the renewal of old feuds that M. de Villefort formed the idea of uniting in the bonds of affection the two children of these inveterate enemies.”\n\n“It was a sublime and charitable thought,” said Monte Cristo, “and the whole world should applaud it. It would be noble to see Mademoiselle Noirtier de Villefort assuming the title of Madame Franz d’Épinay.”\n\nVillefort shuddered and looked at Monte Cristo as if he wished to read in his countenance the real feelings which had dictated the words he had just uttered. But the count completely baffled the procureur, and prevented him from discovering anything beneath the never-varying smile he was so constantly in the habit of assuming.\n\n“Although,” said Villefort, “it will be a serious thing for Valentine to lose her grandfather’s fortune, I do not think that M. d’Épinay will be frightened at this pecuniary loss. He will, perhaps, hold me in greater esteem than the money itself, seeing that I sacrifice everything in order to keep my word with him. Besides, he knows that Valentine is rich in right of her mother, and that she will, in all probability, inherit the fortune of M. and Madame de Saint-Méran, her mother’s parents, who both love her tenderly.”\n\n“And who are fully as well worth loving and tending as M. Noirtier,” said Madame de Villefort; “besides, they are to come to Paris in about a month, and Valentine, after the affront she has received, need not consider it necessary to continue to bury herself alive by being shut up with M. Noirtier.”\n\nThe count listened with satisfaction to this tale of wounded self-love and defeated ambition.\n\n“But it seems to me,” said Monte Cristo, “and I must begin by asking your pardon for what I am about to say, that if M. Noirtier disinherits Mademoiselle de Villefort because she is going to marry a man whose father he detested, he cannot have the same cause of complaint against this dear Edward.”\n\n“True,” said Madame de Villefort, with an intonation of voice which it is impossible to describe; “is it not unjust—shamefully unjust? Poor Edward is as much M. Noirtier’s grandchild as Valentine, and yet, if she had not been going to marry M. Franz, M. Noirtier would have left her all his money; and supposing Valentine to be disinherited by her grandfather, she will still be three times richer than he.”\n\nThe count listened and said no more.\n\n“Count,” said Villefort, “we will not entertain you any longer with our family misfortunes. It is true that my patrimony will go to endow charitable institutions, and my father will have deprived me of my lawful inheritance without any reason for doing so, but I shall have the satisfaction of knowing that I have acted like a man of sense and feeling. M. d’Épinay, to whom I had promised the interest of this sum, shall receive it, even if I endure the most cruel privations.”\n\n“However,” said Madame de Villefort, returning to the one idea which incessantly occupied her mind, “perhaps it would be better to explain this unlucky affair to M. d’Épinay, in order to give him the opportunity of himself renouncing his claim to the hand of Mademoiselle de Villefort.”\n\n“Ah, that would be a great pity,” said Villefort.\n\n“A great pity,” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“Undoubtedly,” said Villefort, moderating the tones of his voice, “a marriage once concerted and then broken off, throws a sort of discredit on a young lady; then again, the old reports, which I was so anxious to put an end to, will instantly gain ground. No, it will all go well; M. d’Épinay, if he is an honorable man, will consider himself more than ever pledged to Mademoiselle de Villefort, unless he were actuated by a decided feeling of avarice, but that is impossible.”\n\n“I agree with M. de Villefort,” said Monte Cristo, fixing his eyes on Madame de Villefort; “and if I were sufficiently intimate with him to allow of giving my advice, I would persuade him, since I have been told M. d’Épinay is coming back, to settle this affair at once beyond all possibility of revocation. I will answer for the success of a project which will reflect so much honor on M. de Villefort.”\n\nThe procureur arose, delighted with the proposition, but his wife slightly changed color.\n\n“Well, that is all that I wanted, and I will be guided by a counsellor such as you are,” said he, extending his hand to Monte Cristo. “Therefore let everyone here look upon what has passed today as if it had not happened, and as though we had never thought of such a thing as a change in our original plans.”\n\n“Sir,” said the count, “the world, unjust as it is, will be pleased with your resolution; your friends will be proud of you, and M. d’Épinay, even if he took Mademoiselle de Villefort without any dowry, which he will not do, would be delighted with the idea of entering a family which could make such sacrifices in order to keep a promise and fulfil a duty.”\n\nAt the conclusion of these words, the count rose to depart.\n\n“Are you going to leave us, count?” said Madame de Villefort.\n\n“I am sorry to say I must do so, madame, I only came to remind you of your promise for Saturday.”\n\n“Did you fear that we should forget it?”\n\n“You are very good, madame, but M. de Villefort has so many important and urgent occupations.”\n\n“My husband has given me his word, sir,” said Madame de Villefort; “you have just seen him resolve to keep it when he has everything to lose, and surely there is more reason for his doing so where he has everything to gain.”\n\n“And,” said Villefort, “is it at your house in the Champs-Élysées that you receive your visitors?”\n\n“No,” said Monte Cristo, “which is precisely the reason which renders your kindness more meritorious,—it is in the country.”\n\n“In the country?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Where is it, then? Near Paris, is it not?”\n\n“Very near, only half a league from the Barriers,—it is at Auteuil.”\n\n“At Auteuil?” said Villefort; “true, Madame de Villefort told me you lived at Auteuil, since it was to your house that she was taken. And in what part of Auteuil do you reside?”\n\n“Rue de la Fontaine.”\n\n“Rue de la Fontaine!” exclaimed Villefort in an agitated tone; “at what number?”\n\n“No. 28.”\n“Then,” cried Villefort, “was it you who bought M. de Saint-Méran’s house!”\n\n“Did it belong to M. de Saint-Méran?” demanded Monte Cristo.\n\n“Yes,” replied Madame de Villefort; “and, would you believe it, count——”\n\n“Believe what?”\n\n“You think this house pretty, do you not?”\n\n“I think it charming.”\n\n“Well, my husband would never live in it.”\n\n“Indeed?” returned Monte Cristo, “that is a prejudice on your part, M. de Villefort, for which I am quite at a loss to account.”\n\n“I do not like Auteuil, sir,” said the procureur, making an evident effort to appear calm.\n\n“But I hope you will not carry your antipathy so far as to deprive me of the pleasure of your company, sir,” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“No, count,—I hope—I assure you I shall do my best,” stammered Villefort.\n\n“Oh,” said Monte Cristo, “I allow of no excuse. On Saturday, at six o’clock. I shall be expecting you, and if you fail to come, I shall think—for how do I know to the contrary?—that this house, which has remained uninhabited for twenty years, must have some gloomy tradition or dreadful legend connected with it.”\n\n“I will come, count,—I will be sure to come,” said Villefort eagerly.\n\n“Thank you,” said Monte Cristo; “now you must permit me to take my leave of you.”\n\n“You said before that you were obliged to leave us, monsieur,” said Madame de Villefort, “and you were about to tell us why when your attention was called to some other subject.”\n\n“Indeed madame,” said Monte Cristo: “I scarcely know if I dare tell you where I am going.”\n\n“Nonsense; say on.”\n\n“Well, then, it is to see a thing on which I have sometimes mused for hours together.”\n\n“What is it?”\n\n“A telegraph. So now I have told my secret.”\n\n“A telegraph?” repeated Madame de Villefort.\n\n“Yes, a telegraph. I had often seen one placed at the end of a road on a hillock, and in the light of the sun its black arms, bending in every direction, always reminded me of the claws of an immense beetle, and I assure you it was never without emotion that I gazed on it, for I could not help thinking how wonderful it was that these various signs should be made to cleave the air with such precision as to convey to the distance of three hundred leagues the ideas and wishes of a man sitting at a table at one end of the line to another man similarly placed at the opposite extremity, and all this effected by a simple act of volition on the part of the sender of the message. I began to think of genii, sylphs, gnomes, in short, of all the ministers of the occult sciences, until I laughed aloud at the freaks of my own imagination. Now, it never occurred to me to wish for a nearer inspection of these large insects, with their long black claws, for I always feared to find under their stone wings some little human genius fagged to death with cabals, factions, and government intrigues. But one fine day I learned that the mover of this telegraph was only a poor wretch, hired for twelve hundred francs a year, and employed all day, not in studying the heavens like an astronomer, or in gazing on the water like an angler, or even in enjoying the privilege of observing the country around him, but all his monotonous life was passed in watching his white-bellied, black-clawed fellow insect, four or five leagues distant from him. At length I felt a desire to study this living chrysalis more closely, and to endeavor to understand the secret part played by these insect-actors when they occupy themselves simply with pulling different pieces of string.”\n30191m\n\n“And are you going there?”\n“I am.”\n\n“What telegraph do you intend visiting? that of the home department, or of the observatory?”\n\n“Oh, no; I should find there people who would force me to understand things of which I would prefer to remain ignorant, and who would try to explain to me, in spite of myself, a mystery which even they do not understand. _Ma foi!_ I should wish to keep my illusions concerning insects unimpaired; it is quite enough to have those dissipated which I had formed of my fellow-creatures. I shall, therefore, not visit either of these telegraphs, but one in the open country where I shall find a good-natured simpleton, who knows no more than the machine he is employed to work.”\n\n“You are a singular man,” said Villefort.\n\n“What line would you advise me to study?”\n\n“The one that is most in use just at this time.”\n\n“The Spanish one, you mean, I suppose?”\n\n“Yes; should you like a letter to the minister that they might explain to you——”\n\n“No,” said Monte Cristo; “since, as I told you before, I do not wish to comprehend it. The moment I understand it there will no longer exist a telegraph for me; it will be nothing more than a sign from M. Duchâtel, or from M. Montalivet, transmitted to the prefect of Bayonne, mystified by two Greek words, _têle_, _graphein_. It is the insect with black claws, and the awful word which I wish to retain in my imagination in all its purity and all its importance.”\n\n“Go then; for in the course of two hours it will be dark, and you will not be able to see anything.”\n\n“_Ma foi!_ you frighten me. Which is the nearest way? Bayonne?”\n\n“Yes; the road to Bayonne.”\n\n“And afterwards the road to Châtillon?”\n“Yes.”\n\n“By the tower of Montlhéry, you mean?”\n\n“Thank you. Good-bye. On Saturday I will tell you my impressions concerning the telegraph.”\n\nAt the door the count was met by the two notaries, who had just completed the act which was to disinherit Valentine, and who were leaving under the conviction of having done a thing which could not fail of redounding considerably to their credit.\n\n# Chapter 61. How a Gardener May Get Rid of the Dormice that Eat His Peaches\n\nNot on the same night as he had stated, but the next morning, the Count of Monte Cristo went out by the Barrière d’Enfer, taking the road to Orléans. Leaving the village of Linas, without stopping at the telegraph, which flourished its great bony arms as he passed, the count reached the tower of Montlhéry, situated, as everyone knows, upon the highest point of the plain of that name. At the foot of the hill the count dismounted and began to ascend by a little winding path, about eighteen inches wide; when he reached the summit he found himself stopped by a hedge, upon which green fruit had succeeded to red and white flowers.\n\nMonte Cristo looked for the entrance to the enclosure, and was not long in finding a little wooden gate, working on willow hinges, and fastened with a nail and string. The count soon mastered the mechanism, the gate opened, and he then found himself in a little garden, about twenty feet long by twelve wide, bounded on one side by part of the hedge, which contained the ingenious contrivance we have called a gate, and on the other by the old tower, covered with ivy and studded with wall-flowers.\n\nNo one would have thought in looking at this old, weather-beaten, floral-decked tower (which might be likened to an elderly dame dressed up to receive her grandchildren at a birthday feast) that it would have been capable of telling strange things, if,—in addition to the menacing ears which the proverb says all walls are provided with,—it had also a voice.\n\nThe garden was crossed by a path of red gravel, edged by a border of thick box, of many years’ growth, and of a tone and color that would have delighted the heart of Delacroix, our modern Rubens. This path was formed in the shape of the figure of 8, thus, in its windings, making a walk of sixty feet in a garden of only twenty.\n\nNever had Flora, the fresh and smiling goddess of gardeners, been honored with a purer or more scrupulous worship than that which was paid to her in this little enclosure. In fact, of the twenty rose-trees which formed the _parterre_, not one bore the mark of the slug, nor were there evidences anywhere of the clustering aphis which is so destructive to plants growing in a damp soil. And yet it was not because the damp had been excluded from the garden; the earth, black as soot, the thick foliage of the trees betrayed its presence; besides, had natural humidity been wanting, it could have been immediately supplied by artificial means, thanks to a tank of water, sunk in one of the corners of the garden, and upon which were stationed a frog and a toad, who, from antipathy, no doubt, always remained on the two opposite sides of the basin. There was not a blade of grass to be seen in the paths, or a weed in the flower-beds; no fine lady ever trained and watered her geraniums, her cacti, and her rhododendrons, in her porcelain _jardinière_ with more pains than this hitherto unseen gardener bestowed upon his little enclosure.\n\nMonte Cristo stopped after having closed the gate and fastened the string to the nail, and cast a look around.\n\n“The man at the telegraph,” said he, “must either engage a gardener or devote himself passionately to agriculture.”\n\nSuddenly he struck against something crouching behind a wheelbarrow filled with leaves; the something rose, uttering an exclamation of astonishment, and Monte Cristo found himself facing a man about fifty years old, who was plucking strawberries, which he was placing upon grape leaves. He had twelve leaves and about as many strawberries, which, on rising suddenly, he let fall from his hand.\n\n“You are gathering your crop, sir?” said Monte Cristo, smiling.\n\n30193m\n“Excuse me, sir,” replied the man, raising his hand to his cap; “I am not up there, I know, but I have only just come down.”\n\n“Do not let me interfere with you in anything, my friend,” said the count; “gather your strawberries, if, indeed, there are any left.”\n\n“I have ten left,” said the man, “for here are eleven, and I had twenty-one, five more than last year. But I am not surprised; the spring has been warm this year, and strawberries require heat, sir. This is the reason that, instead of the sixteen I had last year, I have this year, you see, eleven, already plucked—twelve, thirteen, fourteen, fifteen, sixteen, seventeen, eighteen. Ah, I miss three, they were here last night, sir—I am sure they were here—I counted them. It must be the son of Mère Simon who has stolen them; I saw him strolling about here this morning. Ah, the young rascal—stealing in a garden—he does not know where that may lead him to.”\n\n“Certainly, it is wrong,” said Monte Cristo, “but you should take into consideration the youth and greediness of the delinquent.”\n\n“Of course,” said the gardener, “but that does not make it the less unpleasant. But, sir, once more I beg pardon; perhaps you are an officer that I am detaining here.” And he glanced timidly at the count’s blue coat.\n\n30197m\n“Calm yourself, my friend,” said the count, with the smile which he made at will either terrible or benevolent, and which now expressed only the kindliest feeling; “I am not an inspector, but a traveller, brought here by a curiosity he half repents of, since he causes you to lose your time.”\n\n“Ah, my time is not valuable,” replied the man with a melancholy smile. “Still it belongs to government, and I ought not to waste it; but, having received the signal that I might rest for an hour” (here he glanced at the sun-dial, for there was everything in the enclosure of Montlhéry, even a sun-dial), “and having ten minutes before me, and my strawberries being ripe, when a day longer—by-the-by, sir, do you think dormice eat them?”\n\n“Indeed, I should think not,” replied Monte Cristo; “dormice are bad neighbors for us who do not eat them preserved, as the Romans did.”\n\n“What? Did the Romans eat them?” said the gardener—“ate dormice?”\n\n“I have read so in Petronius,” said the count.\n\n“Really? They can’t be nice, though they do say ‘as fat as a dormouse.’ It is not a wonder they are fat, sleeping all day, and only waking to eat all night. Listen. Last year I had four apricots—they stole one, I had one nectarine, only one—well, sir, they ate half of it on the wall; a splendid nectarine—I never ate a better.”\n\n“You ate it?”\n\n“That is to say, the half that was left—you understand; it was exquisite, sir. Ah, those gentlemen never choose the worst morsels; like Mère Simon’s son, who has not chosen the worst strawberries. But this year,” continued the horticulturist, “I’ll take care it shall not happen, even if I should be forced to sit by the whole night to watch when the strawberries are ripe.”\n\nMonte Cristo had seen enough. Every man has a devouring passion in his heart, as every fruit has its worm; that of the telegraph man was horticulture. He began gathering the grape-leaves which screened the sun from the grapes, and won the heart of the gardener.\n\n“Did you come here, sir, to see the telegraph?” he said.\n\n“Yes, if it isn’t contrary to the rules.”\n\n“Oh, no,” said the gardener; “not in the least, since there is no danger that anyone can possibly understand what we are saying.”\n\n“I have been told,” said the count, “that you do not always yourselves understand the signals you repeat.”\n\n“That is true, sir, and that is what I like best,” said the man, smiling.\n\n“Why do you like that best?”\n\n“Because then I have no responsibility. I am a machine then, and nothing else, and so long as I work, nothing more is required of me.”\n\n“Is it possible,” said Monte Cristo to himself, “that I can have met with a man that has no ambition? That would spoil my plans.”\n\n“Sir,” said the gardener, glancing at the sun-dial, “the ten minutes are almost up; I must return to my post. Will you go up with me?”\n\n“I follow you.”\n\nMonte Cristo entered the tower, which was divided into three stories. The tower contained implements, such as spades, rakes, watering-pots, hung against the wall; this was all the furniture. The second was the man’s conventional abode, or rather sleeping-place; it contained a few poor articles of household furniture—a bed, a table, two chairs, a stone pitcher—and some dry herbs, hung up to the ceiling, which the count recognized as sweet peas, and of which the good man was preserving the seeds; he had labelled them with as much care as if he had been master botanist in the Jardin des Plantes.\n\n“Does it require much study to learn the art of telegraphing?” asked Monte Cristo.\n\n“The study does not take long; it was acting as a supernumerary that was so tedious.”\n\n“And what is the pay?”\n\n“A thousand francs, sir.”\n\n“It is nothing.”\n\n“No; but then we are lodged, as you perceive.”\n\nMonte Cristo looked at the room. They passed to the third story; it was the telegraph room. Monte Cristo looked in turn at the two iron handles by which the machine was worked. “It is very interesting,” he said, “but it must be very tedious for a lifetime.”\n\n“Yes. At first my neck was cramped with looking at it, but at the end of a year I became used to it; and then we have our hours of recreation, and our holidays.”\n\n“Holidays?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“When?”\n\n“When we have a fog.”\n\n“Ah, to be sure.”\n\n“Those are indeed holidays to me; I go into the garden, I plant, I prune, I trim, I kill the insects all day long.”\n\n“How long have you been here?”\n\n“Ten years, and five as a supernumerary make fifteen.”\n\n“You are——”\n\n“Fifty-five years old.”\n\n“How long must you have served to claim the pension?”\n\n“Oh, sir, twenty-five years.”\n\n“And how much is the pension?”\n\n“A hundred crowns.”\n\n“Poor humanity!” murmured Monte Cristo.\n\n“What did you say, sir?” asked the man.\n\n“I was saying it was very interesting.”\n\n“What was?”\n“All you were showing me. And you really understand none of these signals?”\n\n“None at all.”\n\n“And have you never tried to understand them?”\n\n“Never. Why should I?”\n\n“But still there are some signals only addressed to you.”\n\n“Certainly.”\n\n“And do you understand them?”\n\n“They are always the same.”\n\n“And they mean——”\n\n“‘_Nothing new; You have an hour;_’ or ‘_Tomorrow_.’”\n\n“This is simple enough,” said the count; “but look, is not your correspondent putting itself in motion?”\n\n“Ah, yes; thank you, sir.”\n\n“And what is it saying—anything you understand?”\n\n“Yes; it asks if I am ready.”\n\n“And you reply?”\n\n“By the same sign, which, at the same time, tells my right-hand correspondent that I am ready, while it gives notice to my left-hand correspondent to prepare in his turn.”\n\n“It is very ingenious,” said the count.\n\n“You will see,” said the man proudly; “in five minutes he will speak.”\n\n“I have, then, five minutes,” said Monte Cristo to himself; “it is more time than I require. My dear sir, will you allow me to ask you a question?”\n\n“What is it, sir?”\n\n“You are fond of gardening?”\n\n“Passionately.”\n\n“And you would be pleased to have, instead of this terrace of twenty feet, an enclosure of two acres?”\n\n“Sir, I should make a terrestrial paradise of it.”\n\n“You live badly on your thousand francs?”\n\n“Badly enough; but yet I do live.”\n\n“Yes; but you have a wretchedly small garden.”\n\n“True, the garden is not large.”\n\n“And, then, such as it is, it is filled with dormice, who eat everything.”\n\n“Ah, they are my scourges.”\n\n“Tell me, should you have the misfortune to turn your head while your right-hand correspondent was telegraphing——”\n\n“I should not see him.”\n\n“Then what would happen?”\n\n“I could not repeat the signals.”\n\n“And then?”\n\n“Not having repeated them, through negligence, I should be fined.”\n\n“How much?”\n\n“A hundred francs.”\n\n“The tenth of your income—that would be fine work.”\n\n“Ah!” said the man.\n\n“Has it ever happened to you?” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“Once, sir, when I was grafting a rose-tree.”\n\n“Well, suppose you were to alter a signal, and substitute another?”\n\n“Ah, that is another case; I should be turned off, and lose my pension.”\n\n“Three hundred francs?”\n\n“A hundred crowns, yes, sir; so you see that I am not likely to do any of these things.”\n\n“Not even for fifteen years’ wages? Come, it is worth thinking about?”\n\n“For fifteen thousand francs?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Sir, you alarm me.”\n“Nonsense.”\n\n“Sir, you are tempting me?”\n\n“Just so; fifteen thousand francs, do you understand?”\n\n“Sir, let me see my right-hand correspondent.”\n\n“On the contrary, do not look at him, but at this.”\n\n“What is it?”\n\n“What? Do you not know these bits of paper?”\n\n“Bank-notes!”\n\n“Exactly; there are fifteen of them.”\n\n“And whose are they?”\n\n“Yours, if you like.”\n\n“Mine?” exclaimed the man, half-suffocated.\n\n“Yes; yours—your own property.”\n\n“Sir, my right-hand correspondent is signalling.”\n\n“Let him signal.”\n\n“Sir, you have distracted me; I shall be fined.”\n\n“That will cost you a hundred francs; you see it is your interest to take my bank-notes.”\n\n“Sir, my right-hand correspondent redoubles his signals; he is impatient.”\n\n“Never mind—take these;” and the count placed the packet in the man’s hands. “Now this is not all,” he said; “you cannot live upon your fifteen thousand francs.”\n\n“I shall still have my place.”\n\n“No, you will lose it, for you are going to alter your correspondent’s message.”\n\n“Oh, sir, what are you proposing?”\n\n“A jest.”\n\n“Sir, unless you force me——”\n\n“I think I can effectually force you;” and Monte Cristo drew another packet from his pocket. “Here are ten thousand more francs,” he said, “with the fifteen thousand already in your pocket, they will make twenty-five thousand. With five thousand you can buy a pretty little house with two acres of land; the remaining twenty thousand will bring you in a thousand francs a year.”\n\n“A garden with two acres of land!”\n\n“And a thousand francs a year.”\n\n“Oh, heavens!”\n\n“Come, take them,” and Monte Cristo forced the bank-notes into his hand.\n\n“What am I to do?”\n\n“Nothing very difficult.”\n\n“But what is it?”\n\n“To repeat these signs.” Monte Cristo took a paper from his pocket, upon which were drawn three signs, with numbers to indicate the order in which they were to be worked.\n\n“There, you see it will not take long.”\n\n“Yes; but——”\n\n“Do this, and you will have nectarines and all the rest.”\n\nThe shot told; red with fever, while the large drops fell from his brow, the man executed, one after the other, the three signs given by the count, in spite of the frightful contortions of the right-hand correspondent, who, not understanding the change, began to think the gardener had gone mad. As to the left-hand one, he conscientiously repeated the same signals, which were finally transmitted to the Minister of the Interior.\n\n“Now you are rich,” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“Yes,” replied the man, “but at what a price!”\n\n“Listen, friend,” said Monte Cristo. “I do not wish to cause you any remorse; believe me, then, when I swear to you that you have wronged no man, but on the contrary have benefited mankind.”\n\nThe man looked at the bank-notes, felt them, counted them, turned pale, then red, then rushed into his room to drink a glass of water, but he had no time to reach the water-jug, and fainted in the midst of his dried herbs. Five minutes after the new telegram reached the minister, Debray had the horses put to his carriage, and drove to Danglars’ house.\n\n“Has your husband any Spanish bonds?” he asked of the baroness.\n\n“I think so, indeed! He has six millions’ worth.”\n\n“He must sell them at whatever price.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because Don Carlos has fled from Bourges, and has returned to Spain.”\n30203m\n\n“How do you know?” Debray shrugged his shoulders.\n\n“The idea of asking how I hear the news,” he said.\n\nThe baroness did not wait for a repetition; she ran to her husband, who immediately hastened to his agent, and ordered him to sell at any price. When it was seen that Danglars sold, the Spanish funds fell directly. Danglars lost five hundred thousand francs; but he rid himself of all his Spanish shares. The same evening the following was read in _Le Messager_:\n\n“[By telegraph.] The king, Don Carlos, has escaped the vigilance of his guardians at Bourges, and has returned to Spain by the Catalonian frontier. Barcelona has risen in his favor.”\n\nAll that evening nothing was spoken of but the foresight of Danglars, who had sold his shares, and of the luck of the stock-jobber, who only lost five hundred thousand francs by such a blow. Those who had kept their shares, or bought those of Danglars, looked upon themselves as ruined, and passed a very bad night. Next morning _Le Moniteur_ contained the following:\n\n“It was without any foundation that _Le Messager_ yesterday announced the flight of Don Carlos and the revolt of Barcelona. The king (Don Carlos) has not left Bourges, and the peninsula is in the enjoyment of profound peace. A telegraphic signal, improperly interpreted, owing to the fog, was the cause of this error.”\n\nThe funds rose one per cent higher than before they had fallen. This, reckoning his loss, and what he had missed gaining, made the difference of a million to Danglars.\n\n“Good,” said Monte Cristo to Morrel, who was at his house when the news arrived of the strange reverse of fortune of which Danglars had been the victim, “I have just made a discovery for twenty-five thousand francs, for which I would have paid a hundred thousand.”\n\n“What have you discovered?” asked Morrel.\n\n“I have just discovered how a gardener may get rid of the dormice that eat his peaches.”\n\n# Chapter 62. Ghosts\n\nAt first sight, the exterior of the house at Auteuil gave no indications of splendor, nothing one would expect from the destined residence of the magnificent Count of Monte Cristo; but this simplicity was according to the will of its master, who positively ordered nothing to be altered outside. The splendor was within. Indeed, almost before the door opened, the scene changed.\n\nM. Bertuccio had outdone himself in the taste displayed in furnishing, and in the rapidity with which it was executed. It is told that the Duc d’Antin removed in a single night a whole avenue of trees that annoyed Louis XIV.; in three days M. Bertuccio planted an entirely bare court with poplars, large spreading sycamores to shade the different parts of the house, and in the foreground, instead of the usual paving-stones, half hidden by the grass, there extended a lawn but that morning laid down, and upon which the water was yet glistening. For the rest, the orders had been issued by the count; he himself had given a plan to Bertuccio, marking the spot where each tree was to be planted, and the shape and extent of the lawn which was to take the place of the paving-stones.\n\nThus the house had become unrecognizable, and Bertuccio himself declared that he scarcely knew it, encircled as it was by a framework of trees. The overseer would not have objected, while he was about it, to have made some improvements in the garden, but the count had positively forbidden it to be touched. Bertuccio made amends, however, by loading the antechambers, staircases, and mantle-pieces with flowers.\n\nWhat, above all, manifested the shrewdness of the steward, and the profound science of the master, the one in carrying out the ideas of the other, was that this house which appeared only the night before so sad and gloomy, impregnated with that sickly smell one can almost fancy to be the smell of time, had in a single day acquired the aspect of life, was scented with its master’s favorite perfumes, and had the very light regulated according to his wish. When the count arrived, he had under his touch his books and arms, his eyes rested upon his favorite pictures; his dogs, whose caresses he loved, welcomed him in the antechamber; the birds, whose songs delighted him, cheered him with their music; and the house, awakened from its long sleep, like the sleeping beauty in the wood, lived, sang, and bloomed like the houses we have long cherished, and in which, when we are forced to leave them, we leave a part of our souls.\n\nThe servants passed gayly along the fine courtyard; some, belonging to the kitchens, gliding down the stairs, restored but the previous day, as if they had always inhabited the house; others filling the coach-houses, where the equipages, encased and numbered, appeared to have been installed for the last fifty years; and in the stables the horses replied with neighs to the grooms, who spoke to them with much more respect than many servants pay their masters.\n\nThe library was divided into two parts on either side of the wall, and contained upwards of two thousand volumes; one division was entirely devoted to novels, and even the volume which had been published but the day before was to be seen in its place in all the dignity of its red and gold binding.\n\nOn the other side of the house, to match with the library, was the conservatory, ornamented with rare flowers, that bloomed in china jars; and in the midst of the greenhouse, marvellous alike to sight and smell, was a billiard-table which looked as if it had been abandoned during the past hour by players who had left the balls on the cloth.\n\nOne chamber alone had been respected by the magnificent Bertuccio. Before this room, to which you could ascend by the grand, and go out by the back staircase, the servants passed with curiosity, and Bertuccio with terror.\n\nAt five o’clock precisely, the count arrived before the house at Auteuil, followed by Ali. Bertuccio was awaiting this arrival with impatience, mingled with uneasiness; he hoped for some compliments, while, at the same time, he feared to have frowns. Monte Cristo descended into the courtyard, walked all over the house, without giving any sign of approbation or pleasure, until he entered his bedroom, situated on the opposite side to the closed room; then he approached a little piece of furniture, made of rosewood, which he had noticed at a previous visit.\n\n“That can only be to hold gloves,” he said.\n\n“Will your excellency deign to open it?” said the delighted Bertuccio, “and you will find gloves in it.”\n\nElsewhere the count found everything he required—smelling-bottles, cigars, knick-knacks.\n\n30207m\n“Good,” he said; and M. Bertuccio left enraptured, so great, so powerful, and real was the influence exercised by this man over all who surrounded him.\n\nAt precisely six o’clock the clatter of horses’ hoofs was heard at the entrance door; it was our captain of Spahis, who had arrived on Médéah. “I am sure I am the first,” cried Morrel; “I did it on purpose to have you a minute to myself, before everyone came. Julie and Emmanuel have a thousand things to tell you. Ah, really this is magnificent! But tell me, count, will your people take care of my horse?”\n\n“Do not alarm yourself, my dear Maximilian—they understand.”\n\n“I mean, because he wants petting. If you had seen at what a pace he came—like the wind!”\n\n“I should think so,—a horse that cost 5,000 francs!” said Monte Cristo, in the tone which a father would use towards a son.\n\n“Do you regret them?” asked Morrel, with his open laugh.\n\n“I? Certainly not,” replied the count. “No; I should only regret if the horse had not proved good.”\n\n“It is so good, that I have distanced M. de Château-Renaud, one of the best riders in France, and M. Debray, who both mount the minister’s Arabians; and close on their heels are the horses of Madame Danglars, who always go at six leagues an hour.”\n\n“Then they follow you?” asked Monte Cristo.\n\n“See, they are here.” And at the same minute a carriage with smoking horses, accompanied by two mounted gentlemen, arrived at the gate, which opened before them. The carriage drove round, and stopped at the steps, followed by the horsemen.\n\nThe instant Debray had touched the ground, he was at the carriage-door. He offered his hand to the baroness, who, descending, took it with a peculiarity of manner imperceptible to everyone but Monte Cristo. But nothing escaped the count’s notice, and he observed a little note, passed with the facility that indicates frequent practice, from the hand of Madame Danglars to that of the minister’s secretary.\n\nAfter his wife the banker descended, as pale as though he had issued from his tomb instead of his carriage.\n\nMadame Danglars threw a rapid and inquiring glance which could only be interpreted by Monte Cristo, around the courtyard, over the peristyle, and across the front of the house, then, repressing a slight emotion, which must have been seen on her countenance if she had not kept her color, she ascended the steps, saying to Morrel:\n\n“Sir, if you were a friend of mine, I should ask you if you would sell your horse.”\n\nMorrel smiled with an expression very like a grimace, and then turned round to Monte Cristo, as if to ask him to extricate him from his embarrassment. The count understood him.\n\n“Ah, madame,” he said, “why did you not make that request of me?”\n\n“With you, sir,” replied the baroness, “one can wish for nothing, one is so sure to obtain it. If it were so with M. Morrel——”\n\n“Unfortunately,” replied the count, “I am witness that M. Morrel cannot give up his horse, his honor being engaged in keeping it.”\n\n“How so?”\n\n“He laid a wager he would tame Médéah in the space of six months. You understand now that if he were to get rid of the animal before the time named, he would not only lose his bet, but people would say he was afraid; and a brave captain of Spahis cannot risk this, even to gratify a pretty woman, which is, in my opinion, one of the most sacred obligations in the world.”\n\n“You see my position, madame,” said Morrel, bestowing a grateful smile on Monte Cristo.\n\n“It seems to me,” said Danglars, in his coarse tone, ill-concealed by a forced smile, “that you have already got horses enough.”\n\nMadame Danglars seldom allowed remarks of this kind to pass unnoticed, but, to the surprise of the young people, she pretended not to hear it, and said nothing. Monte Cristo smiled at her unusual humility, and showed her two immense porcelain jars, over which wound marine plants, of a size and delicacy that nature alone could produce. The baroness was astonished.\n\n“Why,” said she, “you could plant one of the chestnut-trees in the Tuileries inside! How can such enormous jars have been manufactured?”\n\n“Ah! madame,” replied Monte Cristo, “you must not ask of us, the manufacturers of fine porcelain, such a question. It is the work of another age, constructed by the genii of earth and water.”\n\n“How so?—at what period can that have been?”\n\n“I do not know; I have only heard that an emperor of China had an oven built expressly, and that in this oven twelve jars like this were successively baked. Two broke, from the heat of the fire; the other ten were sunk three hundred fathoms deep into the sea. The sea, knowing what was required of her, threw over them her weeds, encircled them with coral, and encrusted them with shells; the whole was cemented by two hundred years beneath these almost impervious depths, for a revolution carried away the emperor who wished to make the trial, and only left the documents proving the manufacture of the jars and their descent into the sea. At the end of two hundred years the documents were found, and they thought of bringing up the jars. Divers descended in machines, made expressly on the discovery, into the bay where they were thrown; but of ten three only remained, the rest having been broken by the waves. I am fond of these jars, upon which, perhaps, misshapen, frightful monsters have fixed their cold, dull eyes, and in which myriads of small fish have slept, seeking a refuge from the pursuit of their enemies.”\n\nMeanwhile, Danglars, who had cared little for curiosities, was mechanically tearing off the blossoms of a splendid orange-tree, one after another. When he had finished with the orange-tree, he began at the cactus; but this, not being so easily plucked as the orange-tree, pricked him dreadfully. He shuddered, and rubbed his eyes as though awaking from a dream.\n\n“Sir,” said Monte Cristo to him, “I do not recommend my pictures to you, who possess such splendid paintings; but, nevertheless, here are two by Hobbema, a Paul Potter, a Mieris, two by Gerard Douw, a Raphael, a Van Dyck, a Zurbaran, and two or three by Murillo, worth looking at.”\n\n“Stay,” said Debray; “I recognize this Hobbema.”\n\n“Ah, indeed!”\n\n“Yes; it was proposed for the Museum.”\n\n“Which, I believe, does not contain one?” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“No; and yet they refused to buy it.”\n\n“Why?” said Château-Renaud.\n\n“You pretend not to know,—because government was not rich enough.”\n\n“Ah, pardon me,” said Château-Renaud; “I have heard of these things every day during the last eight years, and I cannot understand them yet.”\n\n“You will, by and by,” said Debray.\n\n“I think not,” replied Château-Renaud.\n\n“Major Bartolomeo Cavalcanti and Count Andrea Cavalcanti,” announced Baptistin.\n\nA black satin stock, fresh from the maker’s hands, gray moustaches, a bold eye, a major’s uniform, ornamented with three medals and five crosses—in fact, the thorough bearing of an old soldier—such was the appearance of Major Bartolomeo Cavalcanti, that tender father with whom we are already acquainted. Close to him, dressed in entirely new clothes, advanced smilingly Count Andrea Cavalcanti, the dutiful son, whom we also know. The three young people were talking together. On the entrance of the new-comers, their eyes glanced from father to son, and then, naturally enough, rested on the latter, whom they began criticising.\n\n“Cavalcanti!” said Debray.\n\n“A fine name,” said Morrel.\n\n“Yes,” said Château-Renaud, “these Italians are well named and badly dressed.”\n\n“You are fastidious, Château-Renaud,” replied Debray; “those clothes are well cut and quite new.”\n\n“That is just what I find fault with. That gentleman appears to be well dressed for the first time in his life.”\n\n“Who are those gentlemen?” asked Danglars of Monte Cristo.\n\n“You heard—Cavalcanti.”\n\n“That tells me their name, and nothing else.”\n\n“Ah! true. You do not know the Italian nobility; the Cavalcanti are all descended from princes.”\n\n“Have they any fortune?”\n\n“An enormous one.”\n\n“What do they do?”\n\n“Try to spend it all. They have some business with you, I think, from what they told me the day before yesterday. I, indeed, invited them here today on your account. I will introduce you to them.”\n\n“But they appear to speak French with a very pure accent,” said Danglars.\n\n“The son has been educated in a college in the south; I believe near Marseilles. You will find him quite enthusiastic.”\n\n“Upon what subject?” asked Madame Danglars.\n\n“The French ladies, madame. He has made up his mind to take a wife from Paris.”\n\n“A fine idea that of his,” said Danglars, shrugging his shoulders. Madame Danglars looked at her husband with an expression which, at any other time, would have indicated a storm, but for the second time she controlled herself.\n\n“The baron appears thoughtful today,” said Monte Cristo to her; “are they going to put him in the ministry?”\n\n“Not yet, I think. More likely he has been speculating on the Bourse, and has lost money.”\n\n“M. and Madame de Villefort,” cried Baptistin.\n\nThey entered. M. de Villefort, notwithstanding his self-control, was visibly affected, and when Monte Cristo touched his hand, he felt it tremble.\n\n“Certainly, women alone know how to dissimulate,” said Monte Cristo to himself, glancing at Madame Danglars, who was smiling on the procureur, and embracing his wife.\n\nAfter a short time, the count saw Bertuccio, who, until then, had been occupied on the other side of the house, glide into an adjoining room. He went to him.\n\n“What do you want, M. Bertuccio?” said he.\n\n“Your excellency has not stated the number of guests.”\n\n“Ah, true.”\n\n“How many covers?”\n\n“Count for yourself.”\n\n“Is everyone here, your excellency?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nBertuccio glanced through the door, which was ajar. The count watched him. “Good heavens!” he exclaimed.\n\n“What is the matter?” said the count.\n\n“That woman—that woman!”\n\n“Which?”\n\n“The one with a white dress and so many diamonds—the fair one.”\n\n“Madame Danglars?”\n\n“I do not know her name; but it is she, sir, it is she!”\n\n“Whom do you mean?”\n\n“The woman of the garden!—she that was _enceinte_—she who was walking while she waited for——”\n\nBertuccio stood at the open door, with his eyes starting and his hair on end.\n\n“Waiting for whom?” Bertuccio, without answering, pointed to Villefort with something of the gesture Macbeth uses to point out Banquo.\n\n“Oh, oh!” he at length muttered, “do you see?”\n\n“What? Who?”\n“Him!”\n\n“Him!—M. de Villefort, the king’s attorney? Certainly I see him.”\n\n“Then I did not kill him?”\n\n“Really, I think you are going mad, good Bertuccio,” said the count.\n\n“Then he is not dead?”\n30213m\n\n“No; you see plainly he is not dead. Instead of striking between the sixth and seventh left ribs, as your countrymen do, you must have struck higher or lower, and life is very tenacious in these lawyers, or rather there is no truth in anything you have told me—it was a fright of the imagination, a dream of your fancy. You went to sleep full of thoughts of vengeance; they weighed heavily upon your stomach; you had the nightmare—that’s all. Come, calm yourself, and reckon them up—M. and Madame de Villefort, two; M. and Madame Danglars, four; M. de Château-Renaud, M. Debray, M. Morrel, seven; Major Bartolomeo Cavalcanti, eight.”\n\n“Eight!” repeated Bertuccio.\n\n“Stop! You are in a shocking hurry to be off—you forget one of my guests. Lean a little to the left. Stay! look at M. Andrea Cavalcanti, the young man in a black coat, looking at Murillo’s ‘Madonna’; now he is turning.”\n\nThis time Bertuccio would have uttered an exclamation, had not a look from Monte Cristo silenced him.\n\n“Benedetto?” he muttered; “fatality!”\n\n“Half-past six o’clock has just struck, M. Bertuccio,” said the count severely; “I ordered dinner at that hour, and I do not like to wait;” and he returned to his guests, while Bertuccio, leaning against the wall, succeeded in reaching the dining-room. Five minutes afterwards the doors of the drawing-room were thrown open, and Bertuccio appearing said, with a violent effort, “The dinner waits.”\n\nThe Count of Monte Cristo offered his arm to Madame de Villefort. “M. de Villefort,” he said, “will you conduct the Baroness Danglars?”\n\nVillefort complied, and they passed on to the dining-room.\n\n# Chapter 63. The Dinner\n\nIt was evident that one sentiment affected all the guests on entering the dining-room. Each one asked what strange influence had brought them to this house, and yet astonished, even uneasy though they were, they still felt that they would not like to be absent. The recent events, the solitary and eccentric position of the count, his enormous, nay, almost incredible fortune, should have made men cautious, and have altogether prevented ladies visiting a house where there was no one of their own sex to receive them; and yet curiosity had been enough to lead them to overleap the bounds of prudence and decorum.\n\nAnd all present, even including Cavalcanti and his son, notwithstanding the stiffness of the one and the carelessness of the other, were thoughtful, on finding themselves assembled at the house of this incomprehensible man. Madame Danglars had started when Villefort, on the count’s invitation, offered his arm; and Villefort felt that his glance was uneasy beneath his gold spectacles, when he felt the arm of the baroness press upon his own. None of this had escaped the count, and even by this mere contact of individuals the scene had already acquired considerable interest for an observer.\n\nM. de Villefort had on the right hand Madame Danglars, on his left Morrel. The count was seated between Madame de Villefort and Danglars; the other seats were filled by Debray, who was placed between the two Cavalcanti, and by Château-Renaud, seated between Madame de Villefort and Morrel.\n\nThe repast was magnificent; Monte Cristo had endeavored completely to overturn the Parisian ideas, and to feed the curiosity as much as the appetite of his guests. It was an Oriental feast that he offered to them, but of such a kind as the Arabian fairies might be supposed to prepare. Every delicious fruit that the four quarters of the globe could provide was heaped in vases from China and jars from Japan. Rare birds, retaining their most brilliant plumage, enormous fish, spread upon massive silver dishes, together with every wine produced in the Archipelago, Asia Minor, or the Cape, sparkling in bottles, whose grotesque shape seemed to give an additional flavor to the draught,—all these, like one of the displays with which Apicius of old gratified his guests, passed in review before the eyes of the astonished Parisians, who understood that it was possible to expend a thousand louis upon a dinner for ten persons, but only on the condition of eating pearls, like Cleopatra, or drinking refined gold, like Lorenzo de’ Medici.\n\nMonte Cristo noticed the general astonishment, and began laughing and joking about it.\n\n“Gentlemen,” he said, “you will admit that, when arrived at a certain degree of fortune, the superfluities of life are all that can be desired; and the ladies will allow that, after having risen to a certain eminence of position, the ideal alone can be more exalted. Now, to follow out this reasoning, what is the marvellous?—that which we do not understand. What is it that we really desire?—that which we cannot obtain. Now, to see things which I cannot understand, to procure impossibilities, these are the study of my life. I gratify my wishes by two means—my will and my money. I take as much interest in the pursuit of some whim as you do, M. Danglars, in promoting a new railway line; you, M. de Villefort, in condemning a culprit to death; you, M. Debray, in pacifying a kingdom; you, M. de Château-Renaud, in pleasing a woman; and you, Morrel, in breaking a horse that no one can ride. For example, you see these two fish; one brought from fifty leagues beyond St. Petersburg, the other five leagues from Naples. Is it not amusing to see them both on the same table?”\n\n“What are the two fish?” asked Danglars.\n\n“M. Château-Renaud, who has lived in Russia, will tell you the name of one, and Major Cavalcanti, who is an Italian, will tell you the name of the other.”\n\n“This one is, I think, a sterlet,” said Château-Renaud.\n\n“And that one, if I mistake not, a lamprey.”\n\n“Just so. Now, M. Danglars, ask these gentlemen where they are caught.”\n\n“Sterlets,” said Château-Renaud, “are only found in the Volga.”\n\n“And,” said Cavalcanti, “I know that Lake Fusaro alone supplies lampreys of that size.”\n\n“Exactly; one comes from the Volga, and the other from Lake Fusaro.”\n\n“Impossible!” cried all the guests simultaneously.\n\n“Well, this is just what amuses me,” said Monte Cristo. “I am like Nero—_cupitor impossibilium_; and that is what is amusing you at this moment. This fish, which seems so exquisite to you, is very likely no better than perch or salmon; but it seemed impossible to procure it, and here it is.”\n\n“But how could you have these fish brought to France?”\n\n“Oh, nothing more easy. Each fish was brought over in a cask—one filled with river herbs and weeds, the other with rushes and lake plants; they were placed in a wagon built on purpose, and thus the sterlet lived twelve days, the lamprey eight, and both were alive when my cook seized them, killing one with milk and the other with wine. You do not believe me, M. Danglars!”\n\n“I cannot help doubting,” answered Danglars with his stupid smile.\n\n“Baptistin,” said the count, “have the other fish brought in—the sterlet and the lamprey which came in the other casks, and which are yet alive.”\n\nDanglars opened his bewildered eyes; the company clapped their hands. Four servants carried in two casks covered with aquatic plants, and in each of which was breathing a fish similar to those on the table.\n\n“But why have two of each sort?” asked Danglars.\n\n“Merely because one might have died,” carelessly answered Monte Cristo.\n\n“You are certainly an extraordinary man,” said Danglars; “and philosophers may well say it is a fine thing to be rich.”\n\n“And to have ideas,” added Madame Danglars.\n\n“Oh, do not give me credit for this, madame; it was done by the Romans, who much esteemed them, and Pliny relates that they sent slaves from Ostia to Rome, who carried on their heads fish which he calls the _mulus_, and which, from the description, must probably be the goldfish. It was also considered a luxury to have them alive, it being an amusing sight to see them die, for, when dying, they change color three or four times, and like the rainbow when it disappears, pass through all the prismatic shades, after which they were sent to the kitchen. Their agony formed part of their merit—if they were not seen alive, they were despised when dead.”\n\n“Yes,” said Debray, “but then Ostia is only a few leagues from Rome.”\n\n“True,” said Monte Cristo; “but what would be the use of living eighteen hundred years after Lucullus, if we can do no better than he could?”\n\nThe two Cavalcanti opened their enormous eyes, but had the good sense not to say anything.\n\n“All this is very extraordinary,” said Château-Renaud; “still, what I admire the most, I confess, is the marvellous promptitude with which your orders are executed. Is it not true that you only bought this house five or six days ago?”\n\n“Certainly not longer.”\n\n“Well, I am sure it is quite transformed since last week. If I remember rightly, it had another entrance, and the courtyard was paved and empty; while today we have a splendid lawn, bordered by trees which appear to be a hundred years old.”\n\n“Why not? I am fond of grass and shade,” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“Yes,” said Madame de Villefort, “the door was towards the road before, and on the day of my miraculous escape you brought me into the house from the road, I remember.”\n\n“Yes, madame,” said Monte Cristo; “but I preferred having an entrance which would allow me to see the Bois de Boulogne over my gate.”\n\n“In four days,” said Morrel; “it is extraordinary!”\n\n“Indeed,” said Château-Renaud, “it seems quite miraculous to make a new house out of an old one; for it was very old, and dull too. I recollect coming for my mother to look at it when M. de Saint-Méran advertised it for sale two or three years ago.”\n\n“M. de Saint-Méran?” said Madame de Villefort; “then this house belonged to M. de Saint-Méran before you bought it?”\n\n“It appears so,” replied Monte Cristo.\n\n“Is it possible that you do not know of whom you purchased it?”\n\n“Quite so; my steward transacts all this business for me.”\n\n“It is certainly ten years since the house had been occupied,” said Château-Renaud, “and it was quite melancholy to look at it, with the blinds closed, the doors locked, and the weeds in the court. Really, if the house had not belonged to the father-in-law of the procureur, one might have thought it some accursed place where a horrible crime had been committed.”\n\nVillefort, who had hitherto not tasted the three or four glasses of rare wine which were placed before him, here took one, and drank it off. Monte Cristo allowed a short time to elapse, and then said:\n\n“It is singular, baron, but the same idea came across me the first time I came here; it looked so gloomy I should never have bought it if my steward had not taken the matter into his own hands. Perhaps the fellow had been bribed by the notary.”\n\n“It is probable,” stammered out Villefort, trying to smile; “but I can assure you that I had nothing to do with any such proceeding. This house is part of Valentine’s marriage-portion, and M. de Saint-Méran wished to sell it; for if it had remained another year or two uninhabited it would have fallen to ruin.”\n\nIt was Morrel’s turn to become pale.\n\n“There was, above all, one room,” continued Monte Cristo, “very plain in appearance, hung with red damask, which, I know not why, appeared to me quite dramatic.”\n\n“Why so?” said Danglars; “why dramatic?”\n\n“Can we account for instinct?” said Monte Cristo. “Are there not some places where we seem to breathe sadness?—why, we cannot tell. It is a chain of recollections—an idea which carries you back to other times, to other places—which, very likely, have no connection with the present time and place. And there is something in this room which reminds me forcibly of the chamber of the Marquise de Ganges10 or Desdemona. Stay, since we have finished dinner, I will show it to you, and then we will take coffee in the garden. After dinner, the play.”\n\nMonte Cristo looked inquiringly at his guests. Madame de Villefort rose, Monte Cristo did the same, and the rest followed their example. Villefort and Madame Danglars remained for a moment, as if rooted to their seats; they questioned each other with vague and stupid glances.\n\n“Did you hear?” said Madame Danglars.\n\n“We must go,” replied Villefort, offering his arm.\n\nThe others, attracted by curiosity, were already scattered in different parts of the house; for they thought the visit would not be limited to the one room, and that, at the same time, they would obtain a view of the rest of the building, of which Monte Cristo had created a palace. Each one went out by the open doors. Monte Cristo waited for the two who remained; then, when they had passed, he brought up the rear, and on his face was a smile, which, if they could have understood it, would have alarmed them much more than a visit to the room they were about to enter. They began by walking through the apartments, many of which were fitted up in the Eastern style, with cushions and divans instead of beds, and pipes instead of furniture. The drawing-rooms were decorated with the rarest pictures by the old masters, the boudoirs hung with draperies from China, of fanciful colors, fantastic design, and wonderful texture. At length they arrived at the famous room. There was nothing particular about it, excepting that, although daylight had disappeared, it was not lighted, and everything in it was old-fashioned, while the rest of the rooms had been redecorated. These two causes were enough to give it a gloomy aspect.\n\n“Oh.” cried Madame de Villefort, “it is really frightful.”\n\nMadame Danglars tried to utter a few words, but was not heard. Many observations were made, the import of which was a unanimous opinion that there was something sinister about the room.\n\n30221m\n“Is it not so?” asked Monte Cristo. “Look at that large clumsy bed, hung with such gloomy, blood-colored drapery! And those two crayon portraits, that have faded from the dampness; do they not seem to say, with their pale lips and staring eyes, ‘We have seen’?”\n\nVillefort became livid; Madame Danglars fell into a long seat placed near the chimney.\n\n“Oh,” said Madame de Villefort, smiling, “are you courageous enough to sit down upon the very seat perhaps upon which the crime was committed?”\n\nMadame Danglars rose suddenly.\n\n“And then,” said Monte Cristo, “this is not all.”\n\n“What is there more?” said Debray, who had not failed to notice the agitation of Madame Danglars.\n\n“Ah, what else is there?” said Danglars; “for, at present, I cannot say that I have seen anything extraordinary. What do you say, M. Cavalcanti?”\n\n“Ah,” said he, “we have at Pisa, Ugolino’s tower; at Ferrara, Tasso’s prison; at Rimini, the room of Francesca and Paolo.”\n\n“Yes, but you have not this little staircase,” said Monte Cristo, opening a door concealed by the drapery. “Look at it, and tell me what you think of it.”\n\n“What a wicked-looking, crooked staircase,” said Château-Renaud with a smile.\n\n“I do not know whether the wine of Chios produces melancholy, but certainly everything appears to me black in this house,” said Debray.\n\nEver since Valentine’s dowry had been mentioned, Morrel had been silent and sad.\n\n“Can you imagine,” said Monte Cristo, “some Othello or Abbé de Ganges, one stormy, dark night, descending these stairs step by step, carrying a load, which he wishes to hide from the sight of man, if not from God?”\n\nMadame Danglars half fainted on the arm of Villefort, who was obliged to support himself against the wall.\n\n“Ah, madame,” cried Debray, “what is the matter with you? how pale you look!”\n\n“It is very evident what is the matter with her,” said Madame de Villefort; “M. de Monte Cristo is relating horrible stories to us, doubtless intending to frighten us to death.”\n\n“Yes,” said Villefort, “really, count, you frighten the ladies.”\n\n“What is the matter?” asked Debray, in a whisper, of Madame Danglars.\n\n“Nothing,” she replied with a violent effort. “I want air, that is all.”\n\n“Will you come into the garden?” said Debray, advancing towards the back staircase.\n\n“No, no,” she answered, “I would rather remain here.”\n\n“Are you really frightened, madame?” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“Oh, no, sir,” said Madame Danglars; “but you suppose scenes in a manner which gives them the appearance of reality.”\n30223m\n\n“Ah, yes,” said Monte Cristo smiling; “it is all a matter of imagination. Why should we not imagine this the apartment of an honest mother? And this bed with red hangings, a bed visited by the goddess Lucina? And that mysterious staircase, the passage through which, not to disturb their sleep, the doctor and nurse pass, or even the father carrying the sleeping child?”\n\nHere Madame Danglars, instead of being calmed by the soft picture, uttered a groan and fainted.\n\n“Madame Danglars is ill,” said Villefort; “it would be better to take her to her carriage.”\n\n“Oh, _mon Dieu!_” said Monte Cristo, “and I have forgotten my smelling-bottle!”\n\n“I have mine,” said Madame de Villefort; and she passed over to Monte Cristo a bottle full of the same kind of red liquid whose good properties the count had tested on Edward.\n\n“Ah,” said Monte Cristo, taking it from her hand.\n\n“Yes,” she said, “at your advice I have made the trial.”\n\n“And have you succeeded?”\n\n“I think so.”\n\nMadame Danglars was carried into the adjoining room; Monte Cristo dropped a very small portion of the red liquid upon her lips; she returned to consciousness.\n\n“Ah,” she cried, “what a frightful dream!”\n\nVillefort pressed her hand to let her know it was not a dream. They looked for M. Danglars, but, as he was not especially interested in poetical ideas, he had gone into the garden, and was talking with Major Cavalcanti on the projected railway from Leghorn to Florence. Monte Cristo seemed in despair. He took the arm of Madame Danglars, and conducted her into the garden, where they found Danglars taking coffee between the Cavalcanti.\n\n“Really, madame,” he said, “did I alarm you much?”\n\n“Oh, no, sir,” she answered; “but you know, things impress us differently, according to the mood of our minds.” Villefort forced a laugh.\n\n“And then, you know,” he said, “an idea, a supposition, is sufficient.”\n\n“Well,” said Monte Cristo, “you may believe me if you like, but it is my opinion that a crime has been committed in this house.”\n\n“Take care,” said Madame de Villefort, “the king’s attorney is here.”\n\n“Ah,” replied Monte Cristo, “since that is the case, I will take advantage of his presence to make my declaration.”\n\n“Your declaration?” said Villefort.\n\n“Yes, before witnesses.”\n\n“Oh, this is very interesting,” said Debray; “if there really has been a crime, we will investigate it.”\n\n“There has been a crime,” said Monte Cristo. “Come this way, gentlemen; come, M. Villefort, for a declaration to be available, should be made before the competent authorities.”\n\nHe then took Villefort’s arm, and, at the same time, holding that of Madame Danglars under his own, he dragged the procureur to the plantain-tree, where the shade was thickest. All the other guests followed.\n\n“Stay,” said Monte Cristo, “here, in this very spot” (and he stamped upon the ground), “I had the earth dug up and fresh mould put in, to refresh these old trees; well, my man, digging, found a box, or rather, the iron-work of a box, in the midst of which was the skeleton of a newly born infant.”\n30225m\n\nMonte Cristo felt the arm of Madame Danglars stiffen, while that of Villefort trembled.\n\n“A newly born infant,” repeated Debray; “this affair becomes serious!”\n\n“Well,” said Château-Renaud, “I was not wrong just now then, when I said that houses had souls and faces like men, and that their exteriors carried the impress of their characters. This house was gloomy because it was remorseful: it was remorseful because it concealed a crime.”\n\n“Who said it was a crime?” asked Villefort, with a last effort.\n\n“How? is it not a crime to bury a living child in a garden?” cried Monte Cristo. “And pray what do you call such an action?”\n\n“But who said it was buried alive?”\n\n“Why bury it there if it were dead? This garden has never been a cemetery.”\n\n“What is done to infanticides in this country?” asked Major Cavalcanti innocently.\n\n“Oh, their heads are soon cut off,” said Danglars.\n\n“Ah, indeed?” said Cavalcanti.\n\n“I think so; am I not right, M. de Villefort?” asked Monte Cristo.\n\n“Yes, count,” replied Villefort, in a voice now scarcely human.\n\nMonte Cristo, seeing that the two persons for whom he had prepared this scene could scarcely endure it, and not wishing to carry it too far, said:\n\n“Come, gentlemen,—some coffee, we seem to have forgotten it,” and he conducted the guests back to the table on the lawn.\n\n“Indeed, count,” said Madame Danglars, “I am ashamed to own it, but all your frightful stories have so upset me, that I must beg you to let me sit down;” and she fell into a chair.\n\nMonte Cristo bowed, and went to Madame de Villefort.\n\n“I think Madame Danglars again requires your bottle,” he said. But before Madame de Villefort could reach her friend, the procureur had found time to whisper to Madame Danglars, “I must speak to you.”\n“When?”\n\n“Tomorrow.”\n\n“Where?”\n\n“In my office, or in the court, if you like,—that is the surest place.”\n\n“I will be there.”\n\nAt this moment Madame de Villefort approached.\n\n“Thanks, my dear friend,” said Madame Danglars, trying to smile; “it is over now, and I am much better.”\n\n# Chapter 64. The Beggar\n\nThe evening passed on; Madame de Villefort expressed a desire to return to Paris, which Madame Danglars had not dared to do, notwithstanding the uneasiness she experienced. On his wife’s request, M. de Villefort was the first to give the signal of departure. He offered a seat in his landau to Madame Danglars, that she might be under the care of his wife. As for M. Danglars, absorbed in an interesting conversation with M. Cavalcanti, he paid no attention to anything that was passing. While Monte Cristo had begged the smelling-bottle of Madame de Villefort, he had noticed the approach of Villefort to Madame Danglars, and he soon guessed all that had passed between them, though the words had been uttered in so low a voice as hardly to be heard by Madame Danglars. Without opposing their arrangements, he allowed Morrel, Château-Renaud, and Debray to leave on horseback, and the ladies in M. de Villefort’s carriage. Danglars, more and more delighted with Major Cavalcanti, had offered him a seat in his carriage. Andrea Cavalcanti found his tilbury waiting at the door; the groom, in every respect a caricature of the English fashion, was standing on tiptoe to hold a large iron-gray horse.\n\nAndrea had spoken very little during dinner; he was an intelligent lad, and he feared to utter some absurdity before so many grand people, amongst whom, with dilating eyes, he saw the king’s attorney. Then he had been seized upon by Danglars, who, with a rapid glance at the stiff-necked old major and his modest son, and taking into consideration the hospitality of the count, made up his mind that he was in the society of some nabob come to Paris to finish the worldly education of his heir. He contemplated with unspeakable delight the large diamond which shone on the major’s little finger; for the major, like a prudent man, in case of any accident happening to his bank-notes, had immediately converted them into an available asset. Then, after dinner, on the pretext of business, he questioned the father and son upon their mode of living; and the father and son, previously informed that it was through Danglars the one was to receive his 48,000 francs and the other 50,000 livres annually, were so full of affability that they would have shaken hands even with the banker’s servants, so much did their gratitude need an object to expend itself upon.\n\nOne thing above all the rest heightened the respect, nay almost the veneration, of Danglars for Cavalcanti. The latter, faithful to the principle of Horace, _nil admirari_, had contented himself with showing his knowledge by declaring in what lake the best lampreys were caught. Then he had eaten some without saying a word more; Danglars, therefore, concluded that such luxuries were common at the table of the illustrious descendant of the Cavalcanti, who most likely in Lucca fed upon trout brought from Switzerland, and lobsters sent from England, by the same means used by the count to bring the lampreys from Lake Fusaro, and the sterlet from the Volga. Thus it was with much politeness of manner that he heard Cavalcanti pronounce these words:\n\n“Tomorrow, sir, I shall have the honor of waiting upon you on business.”\n\n“And I, sir,” said Danglars, “shall be most happy to receive you.”\n\nUpon which he offered to take Cavalcanti in his carriage to the Hôtel des Princes, if it would not be depriving him of the company of his son. To this Cavalcanti replied by saying that for some time past his son had lived independently of him, that he had his own horses and carriages, and that not having come together, it would not be difficult for them to leave separately. The major seated himself, therefore, by the side of Danglars, who was more and more charmed with the ideas of order and economy which ruled this man, and yet who, being able to allow his son 60,000 francs a year, might be supposed to possess a fortune of 500,000 or 600,000 livres.\n\nAs for Andrea, he began, by way of showing off, to scold his groom, who, instead of bringing the tilbury to the steps of the house, had taken it to the outer door, thus giving him the trouble of walking thirty steps to reach it. The groom heard him with humility, took the bit of the impatient animal with his left hand, and with the right held out the reins to Andrea, who, taking them from him, rested his polished boot lightly on the step.\n\nAt that moment a hand touched his shoulder. The young man turned round, thinking that Danglars or Monte Cristo had forgotten something they wished to tell him, and had returned just as they were starting. But instead of either of these, he saw nothing but a strange face, sunburnt, and encircled by a beard, with eyes brilliant as carbuncles, and a smile upon the mouth which displayed a perfect set of white teeth, pointed and sharp as the wolf’s or jackal’s. A red handkerchief encircled his gray head; torn and filthy garments covered his large bony limbs, which seemed as though, like those of a skeleton, they would rattle as he walked; and the hand with which he leaned upon the young man’s shoulder, and which was the first thing Andrea saw, seemed of gigantic size.\n30229m\n\nDid the young man recognize that face by the light of the lantern in his tilbury, or was he merely struck with the horrible appearance of his interrogator? We cannot say; but only relate the fact that he shuddered and stepped back suddenly.\n\n“What do you want of me?” he asked.\n\n“Pardon me, my friend, if I disturb you,” said the man with the red handkerchief, “but I want to speak to you.”\n\n“You have no right to beg at night,” said the groom, endeavoring to rid his master of the troublesome intruder.\n\n“I am not begging, my fine fellow,” said the unknown to the servant, with so ironical an expression of the eye, and so frightful a smile, that he withdrew; “I only wish to say two or three words to your master, who gave me a commission to execute about a fortnight ago.”\n\n“Come,” said Andrea, with sufficient nerve for his servant not to perceive his agitation, “what do you want? Speak quickly, friend.”\n\nThe man said, in a low voice: “I wish—I wish you to spare me the walk back to Paris. I am very tired, and as I have not eaten so good a dinner as you, I can scarcely stand.”\n\nThe young man shuddered at this strange familiarity.\n\n“Tell me,” he said—“tell me what you want?”\n\n“Well, then, I want you to take me up in your fine carriage, and carry me back.” Andrea turned pale, but said nothing.\n\n“Yes,” said the man, thrusting his hands into his pockets, and looking impudently at the youth; “I have taken the whim into my head; do you understand, Master Benedetto?”\n\nAt this name, no doubt, the young man reflected a little, for he went towards his groom, saying:\n\n“This man is right; I did indeed charge him with a commission, the result of which he must tell me; walk to the barrier, there take a cab, that you may not be too late.”\n\nThe surprised groom retired.\n\n“Let me at least reach a shady spot,” said Andrea.\n\n“Oh, as for that, I’ll take you to a splendid place,” said the man with the handkerchief; and taking the horse’s bit he led the tilbury where it was certainly impossible for anyone to witness the honor that Andrea conferred upon him.\n\n“Don’t think I want the glory of riding in your fine carriage,” said he; “oh, no, it’s only because I am tired, and also because I have a little business to talk over with you.”\n\n“Come, step in,” said the young man. It was a pity this scene had not occurred in daylight, for it was curious to see this rascal throwing himself heavily down on the cushion beside the young and elegant driver of the tilbury. Andrea drove past the last house in the village without saying a word to his companion, who smiled complacently, as though well-pleased to find himself travelling in so comfortable a vehicle. Once out of Auteuil, Andrea looked around, in order to assure himself that he could neither be seen nor heard, and then, stopping the horse and crossing his arms before the man, he asked:\n\n“Now, tell me why you come to disturb my tranquillity?”\n\n“Let me ask you why you deceived me?”\n\n“How have I deceived you?”\n\n“‘How,’ do you ask? When we parted at the Pont du Var, you told me you were going to travel through Piedmont and Tuscany; but instead of that, you come to Paris.”\n\n“How does that annoy you?”\n\n“It does not; on the contrary, I think it will answer my purpose.”\n\n“So,” said Andrea, “you are speculating upon me?”\n\n“What fine words he uses!”\n\n“I warn you, Master Caderousse, that you are mistaken.”\n\n“Well, well, don’t be angry, my boy; you know well enough what it is to be unfortunate; and misfortunes make us jealous. I thought you were earning a living in Tuscany or Piedmont by acting as _facchino_ or _cicerone_, and I pitied you sincerely, as I would a child of my own. You know I always did call you my child.”\n\n“Come, come, what then?”\n\n“Patience—patience!”\n\n“I am patient, but go on.”\n\n“All at once I see you pass through the barrier with a groom, a tilbury, and fine new clothes. You must have discovered a mine, or else become a stockbroker.”\n\n“So that, as you confess, you are jealous?”\n\n“No, I am pleased—so pleased that I wished to congratulate you; but as I am not quite properly dressed, I chose my opportunity, that I might not compromise you.”\n\n“Yes, and a fine opportunity you have chosen!” exclaimed Andrea; “you speak to me before my servant.”\n\n“How can I help that, my boy? I speak to you when I can catch you. You have a quick horse, a light tilbury, you are naturally as slippery as an eel; if I had missed you tonight, I might not have had another chance.”\n\n“You see, I do not conceal myself.”\n\n“You are lucky; I wish I could say as much, for I do conceal myself; and then I was afraid you would not recognize me, but you did,” added Caderousse with his unpleasant smile. “It was very polite of you.”\n\n“Come,” said Andrea, “what do you want?”\n\n“You do not speak affectionately to me, Benedetto, my old friend, that is not right—take care, or I may become troublesome.”\n\nThis menace smothered the young man’s passion. He urged the horse again into a trot.\n\n“You should not speak so to an old friend like me, Caderousse, as you said just now; you are a native of Marseilles, I am——”\n\n“Do you know then now what you are?”\n\n“No, but I was brought up in Corsica; you are old and obstinate, I am young and wilful. Between people like us threats are out of place, everything should be amicably arranged. Is it my fault if fortune, which has frowned on you, has been kind to me?”\n\n“Fortune has been kind to you, then? Your tilbury, your groom, your clothes, are not then hired? Good, so much the better,” said Caderousse, his eyes sparkling with avarice.\n\n“Oh, you knew that well enough before speaking to me,” said Andrea, becoming more and more excited. “If I had been wearing a handkerchief like yours on my head, rags on my back, and worn-out shoes on my feet, you would not have known me.”\n\n“You wrong me, my boy; now I have found you, nothing prevents my being as well-dressed as anyone, knowing, as I do, the goodness of your heart. If you have two coats you will give me one of them. I used to divide my soup and beans with you when you were hungry.”\n\n“True,” said Andrea.\n\n“What an appetite you used to have! Is it as good now?”\n\n“Oh, yes,” replied Andrea, laughing.\n\n“How did you come to be dining with that prince whose house you have just left?”\n\n“He is not a prince; simply a count.”\n\n“A count, and a rich one too, eh?”\n\n“Yes; but you had better not have anything to say to him, for he is not a very good-tempered gentleman.”\n\n“Oh, be easy! I have no design upon your count, and you shall have him all to yourself. But,” said Caderousse, again smiling with the disagreeable expression he had before assumed, “you must pay for it—you understand?”\n\n“Well, what do you want?”\n\n“I think that with a hundred francs a month——”\n\n“Well?”\n“I could live——”\n\n“Upon a hundred francs!”\n\n“Come—you understand me; but that with——”\n\n“With?”\n\n“With a hundred and fifty francs I should be quite happy.”\n\n“Here are two hundred,” said Andrea; and he placed ten gold louis in the hand of Caderousse.\n30233m\n\n“Good!” said Caderousse.\n\n“Apply to the steward on the first day of every month, and you will receive the same sum.”\n\n“There now, again you degrade me.”\n\n“How so?”\n\n“By making me apply to the servants, when I want to transact business with you alone.”\n\n“Well, be it so, then. Take it from me then, and so long at least as I receive my income, you shall be paid yours.”\n\n“Come, come; I always said you were a fine fellow, and it is a blessing when good fortune happens to such as you. But tell me all about it?”\n\n“Why do you wish to know?” asked Cavalcanti.\n\n“What? do you again defy me?”\n\n“No; the fact is, I have found my father.”\n\n“What? a real father?”\n\n“Yes, so long as he pays me——”\n\n“You’ll honor and believe him—that’s right. What is his name?”\n\n“Major Cavalcanti.”\n\n“Is he pleased with you?”\n\n“So far I have appeared to answer his purpose.”\n\n“And who found this father for you?”\n\n“The Count of Monte Cristo.”\n\n“The man whose house you have just left?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“I wish you would try and find me a situation with him as grandfather, since he holds the money-chest!”\n\n“Well, I will mention you to him. Meanwhile, what are you going to do?”\n“I?”\n\n“Yes, you.”\n\n“It is very kind of you to trouble yourself about me.”\n\n“Since you interest yourself in my affairs, I think it is now my turn to ask you some questions.”\n\n“Ah, true. Well; I shall rent a room in some respectable house, wear a decent coat, shave every day, and go and read the papers in a café. Then, in the evening, I shall go to the theatre; I shall look like some retired baker. That is what I want.”\n\n“Come, if you will only put this scheme into execution, and be steady, nothing could be better.”\n\n“Do you think so, M. Bossuet? And you—what will you become? A peer of France?”\n\n“Ah,” said Andrea, “who knows?”\n\n“Major Cavalcanti is already one, perhaps; but then, hereditary rank is abolished.”\n\n“No politics, Caderousse. And now that you have all you want, and that we understand each other, jump down from the tilbury and disappear.”\n\n“Not at all, my good friend.”\n\n“How? Not at all?”\n\n“Why, just think for a moment; with this red handkerchief on my head, with scarcely any shoes, no papers, and ten gold napoleons in my pocket, without reckoning what was there before—making in all about two hundred francs,—why, I should certainly be arrested at the barriers. Then, to justify myself, I should say that you gave me the money; this would cause inquiries, it would be found that I left Toulon without giving due notice, and I should then be escorted back to the shores of the Mediterranean. Then I should become simply No. 106, and good-bye to my dream of resembling the retired baker! No, no, my boy; I prefer remaining honorably in the capital.”\n\nAndrea scowled. Certainly, as he had himself owned, the reputed son of Major Cavalcanti was a wilful fellow. He drew up for a minute, threw a rapid glance around him, and then his hand fell instantly into his pocket, where it began playing with a pistol. But, meanwhile, Caderousse, who had never taken his eyes off his companion, passed his hand behind his back, and opened a long Spanish knife, which he always carried with him, to be ready in case of need. The two friends, as we see, were worthy of and understood one another. Andrea’s hand left his pocket inoffensively, and was carried up to the red moustache, which it played with for some time.\n\n“Good Caderousse,” he said, “how happy you will be.”\n\n“I will do my best,” said the innkeeper of the Pont du Gard, shutting up his knife.\n\n“Well, then, we will go into Paris. But how will you pass through the barrier without exciting suspicion? It seems to me that you are in more danger riding than on foot.”\n\n“Wait,” said Caderousse, “we shall see.” He then took the greatcoat with the large collar, which the groom had left behind in the tilbury, and put it on his back; then he took off Cavalcanti’s hat, which he placed upon his own head, and finally he assumed the careless attitude of a servant whose master drives himself.\n\n“But, tell me,” said Andrea, “am I to remain bareheaded?”\n\n“Pooh,” said Caderousse; “it is so windy that your hat can easily appear to have blown off.”\n\n“Come, come; enough of this,” said Cavalcanti.\n\n“What are you waiting for?” said Caderousse. “I hope I am not the cause.”\n\n“Hush,” said Andrea. They passed the barrier without accident. At the first cross street Andrea stopped his horse, and Caderousse leaped out.\n\n“Well!” said Andrea,—“my servant’s coat and my hat?”\n\n“Ah,” said Caderousse, “you would not like me to risk taking cold?”\n\n“But what am I to do?”\n\n“You? Oh, you are young while I am beginning to get old. _Au revoir_, Benedetto;” and running into a court, he disappeared.\n\n“Alas,” said Andrea, sighing, “one cannot be completely happy in this world!”\n\n# Chapter 65. A Conjugal Scene\n\nAt the Place Louis XV. the three young people separated—that is to say, Morrel went to the Boulevards, Château-Renaud to the Pont de la Révolution, and Debray to the Quai. Most probably Morrel and Château-Renaud returned to their “domestic hearths,” as they say in the gallery of the Chamber in well-turned speeches, and in the theatre of the Rue Richelieu in well-written pieces; but it was not the case with Debray. When he reached the wicket of the Louvre, he turned to the left, galloped across the Carrousel, passed through the Rue Saint-Roch, and, issuing from the Rue de la Michodière, he arrived at M. Danglars’ door just at the same time that Villefort’s landau, after having deposited him and his wife at the Faubourg Saint-Honoré, stopped to leave the baroness at her own house.\n\nDebray, with the air of a man familiar with the house, entered first into the court, threw his bridle into the hands of a footman, and returned to the door to receive Madame Danglars, to whom he offered his arm, to conduct her to her apartments. The gate once closed, and Debray and the baroness alone in the court, he asked:\n\n“What was the matter with you, Hermine? and why were you so affected at that story, or rather fable, which the count related?”\n\n“Because I have been in such shocking spirits all the evening, my friend,” said the baroness.\n\n“No, Hermine,” replied Debray; “you cannot make me believe that; on the contrary, you were in excellent spirits when you arrived at the count’s. M. Danglars was disagreeable, certainly, but I know how much you care for his ill-humor. Someone has vexed you; I will allow no one to annoy you.”\n\n“You are deceived, Lucien, I assure you,” replied Madame Danglars; “and what I have told you is really the case, added to the ill-humor you remarked, but which I did not think it worth while to allude to.”\n\nIt was evident that Madame Danglars was suffering from that nervous irritability which women frequently cannot account for even to themselves; or that, as Debray had guessed, she had experienced some secret agitation that she would not acknowledge to anyone. Being a man who knew that the former of these symptoms was one of the inherent penalties of womanhood, he did not then press his inquiries, but waited for a more appropriate opportunity when he should again interrogate her, or receive an avowal _proprio motu_.\n\nAt the door of her apartment the baroness met Mademoiselle Cornélie, her confidential maid.\n\n“What is my daughter doing?” asked Madame Danglars.\n\n“She practiced all the evening, and then went to bed,” replied Mademoiselle Cornélie.\n\n“Yet I think I hear her piano.”\n\n“It is Mademoiselle Louise d’Armilly, who is playing while Mademoiselle Danglars is in bed.”\n\n“Well,” said Madame Danglars, “come and undress me.”\n\nThey entered the bedroom. Debray stretched himself upon a large couch, and Madame Danglars passed into her dressing-room with Mademoiselle Cornélie.\n\n“My dear M. Lucien,” said Madame Danglars through the door, “you are always complaining that Eugénie will not address a word to you.”\n\n“Madame,” said Lucien, playing with a little dog, who, recognizing him as a friend of the house, expected to be caressed, “I am not the only one who makes similar complaints, I think I heard Morcerf say that he could not extract a word from his betrothed.”\n\n“True,” said Madame Danglars; “yet I think this will all pass off, and that you will one day see her enter your study.”\n\n“My study?”\n\n“At least that of the minister.”\n\n“Why so!”\n\n“To ask for an engagement at the Opera. Really, I never saw such an infatuation for music; it is quite ridiculous for a young lady of fashion.”\n\nDebray smiled. “Well,” said he, “let her come, with your consent and that of the baron, and we will try and give her an engagement, though we are very poor to pay such talent as hers.”\n\n“Go, Cornélie,” said Madame Danglars, “I do not require you any longer.”\n\nCornélie obeyed, and the next minute Madame Danglars left her room in a charming loose dress, and came and sat down close to Debray. Then she began thoughtfully to caress the little spaniel. Lucien looked at her for a moment in silence.\n\n“Come, Hermine,” he said, after a short time, “answer candidly,—something vexes you—is it not so?”\n\n30239m\n“Nothing,” answered the baroness.\n\nAnd yet, as she could scarcely breathe, she rose and went towards a looking-glass. “I am frightful tonight,” she said. Debray rose, smiling, and was about to contradict the baroness upon this latter point, when the door opened suddenly. M. Danglars appeared; Debray reseated himself. At the noise of the door Madame Danglars turned round, and looked upon her husband with an astonishment she took no trouble to conceal.\n\n“Good-evening, madame,” said the banker; “good-evening, M. Debray.”\n\nProbably the baroness thought this unexpected visit signified a desire to make up for the sharp words he had uttered during the day. Assuming a dignified air, she turned round to Debray, without answering her husband.\n\n“Read me something, M. Debray,” she said. Debray, who was slightly disturbed at this visit, recovered himself when he saw the calmness of the baroness, and took up a book marked by a mother-of-pearl knife inlaid with gold.\n\n“Excuse me,” said the banker, “but you will tire yourself, baroness, by such late hours, and M. Debray lives some distance from here.”\n\nDebray was petrified, not only to hear Danglars speak so calmly and politely, but because it was apparent that beneath outward politeness there really lurked a determined spirit of opposition to anything his wife might wish to do. The baroness was also surprised, and showed her astonishment by a look which would doubtless have had some effect upon her husband if he had not been intently occupied with the paper, where he was looking to see the closing stock quotations. The result was, that the proud look entirely failed of its purpose.\n\n“M. Lucien,” said the baroness, “I assure you I have no desire to sleep, and that I have a thousand things to tell you this evening, which you must listen to, even though you slept while hearing me.”\n\n“I am at your service, madame,” replied Lucien coldly.\n\n“My dear M. Debray,” said the banker, “do not kill yourself tonight listening to the follies of Madame Danglars, for you can hear them as well tomorrow; but I claim tonight and will devote it, if you will allow me, to talk over some serious matters with my wife.”\n\nThis time the blow was so well aimed, and hit so directly, that Lucien and the baroness were staggered, and they interrogated each other with their eyes, as if to seek help against this aggression, but the irresistible will of the master of the house prevailed, and the husband was victorious.\n\n“Do not think I wish to turn you out, my dear Debray,” continued Danglars; “oh, no, not at all. An unexpected occurrence forces me to ask my wife to have a little conversation with me; it is so rarely I make such a request, I am sure you cannot grudge it to me.”\n\nDebray muttered something, bowed and went out, knocking himself against the edge of the door, like Nathan in _Athalie_.\n\n“It is extraordinary,” he said, when the door was closed behind him, “how easily these husbands, whom we ridicule, gain an advantage over us.”\n30241m\n\nLucien having left, Danglars took his place on the sofa, closed the open book, and placing himself in a dreadfully dictatorial attitude, he began playing with the dog; but the animal, not liking him as well as Debray, and attempting to bite him, Danglars seized him by the skin of his neck and threw him upon a couch on the other side of the room. The animal uttered a cry during the transit, but, arrived at its destination, it crouched behind the cushions, and stupefied at such unusual treatment remained silent and motionless.\n\n“Do you know, sir,” asked the baroness, “that you are improving? Generally you are only rude, but tonight you are brutal.”\n\n“It is because I am in a worse humor than usual,” replied Danglars. Hermine looked at the banker with supreme disdain. These glances frequently exasperated the pride of Danglars, but this evening he took no notice of them.\n\n“And what have I to do with your ill-humor?” said the baroness, irritated at the impassibility of her husband; “do these things concern me? Keep your ill-humor at home in your money boxes, or, since you have clerks whom you pay, vent it upon them.”\n\n“Not so,” replied Danglars; “your advice is wrong, so I shall not follow it. My money boxes are my Pactolus, as, I think, M. Demoustier says, and I will not retard its course, or disturb its calm. My clerks are honest men, who earn my fortune, whom I pay much below their deserts, if I may value them according to what they bring in; therefore I shall not get into a passion with them; those with whom I will be in a passion are those who eat my dinners, mount my horses, and exhaust my fortune.”\n\n“And pray who are the persons who exhaust your fortune? Explain yourself more clearly, I beg, sir.”\n\n“Oh, make yourself easy!—I am not speaking riddles, and you will soon know what I mean. The people who exhaust my fortune are those who draw out 700,000 francs in the course of an hour.”\n\n“I do not understand you, sir,” said the baroness, trying to disguise the agitation of her voice and the flush of her face.\n\n“You understand me perfectly, on the contrary,” said Danglars: “but, if you will persist, I will tell you that I have just lost 700,000 francs upon the Spanish loan.”\n\n“And pray,” asked the baroness, “am I responsible for this loss?”\n“Why not?”\n\n“Is it my fault you have lost 700,000 francs?”\n\n“Certainly it is not mine.”\n\n“Once for all, sir,” replied the baroness sharply, “I tell you I will not hear cash named; it is a style of language I never heard in the house of my parents or in that of my first husband.”\n\n“Oh, I can well believe that, for neither of them was worth a penny.”\n\n“The better reason for my not being conversant with the slang of the bank, which is here dinning in my ears from morning to night; that noise of jingling crowns, which are constantly being counted and re-counted, is odious to me. I only know one thing I dislike more, which is the sound of your voice.”\n\n“Really?” said Danglars. “Well, this surprises me, for I thought you took the liveliest interest in all my affairs!”\n30243m\n\n“I? What could put such an idea into your head?”\n“Yourself.”\n\n“Ah?—what next?”\n\n“Most assuredly.”\n\n“I should like to know upon what occasion?”\n\n“Oh, _mon Dieu!_ that is very easily done. Last February you were the first who told me of the Haitian funds. You had dreamed that a ship had entered the harbor at Le Havre, that this ship brought news that a payment we had looked upon as lost was going to be made. I know how clear-sighted your dreams are; I therefore purchased immediately as many shares as I could of the Haitian debt, and I gained 400,000 francs by it, of which 100,000 have been honestly paid to you. You spent it as you pleased; that was your business. In March there was a question about a grant to a railway. Three companies presented themselves, each offering equal securities. You told me that your instinct,—and although you pretend to know nothing about speculations, I think on the contrary, that your comprehension is very clear upon certain affairs,—well, you told me that your instinct led you to believe the grant would be given to the company called the Southern. I bought two thirds of the shares of that company; as you had foreseen, the shares trebled in value, and I picked up a million, from which 250,000 francs were paid to you for pin-money. How have you spent this 250,000 francs?—it is no business of mine.”\n\n“When are you coming to the point?” cried the baroness, shivering with anger and impatience.\n\n“Patience, madame, I am coming to it.”\n\n“That’s fortunate.”\n\n“In April you went to dine at the minister’s. You heard a private conversation respecting Spanish affairs—on the expulsion of Don Carlos. I bought some Spanish shares. The expulsion took place and I pocketed 600,000 francs the day Charles V. repassed the Bidassoa. Of these 600,000 francs you took 50,000 crowns. They were yours, you disposed of them according to your fancy, and I asked no questions; but it is not the less true that you have this year received 500,000 livres.”\n\n“Well, sir, and what then?”\n\n“Ah, yes, it was just after this that you spoiled everything.”\n\n“Really, your manner of speaking——”\n\n“It expresses my meaning, and that is all I want. Well, three days after that you talked politics with M. Debray, and you fancied from his words that Don Carlos had returned to Spain. Well, I sold my shares, the news got out, and I no longer sold—I gave them away, next day I find the news was false, and by this false report I have lost 700,000 francs.”\n\n“Well?”\n\n“Well, since I gave you a fourth of my gains, I think you owe me a fourth of my losses; the fourth of 700,000 francs is 175,000 francs.”\n\n“What you say is absurd, and I cannot see why M. Debray’s name is mixed up in this affair.”\n\n“Because if you do not possess the 175,000 francs I reclaim, you must have lent them to your friends, and M. Debray is one of your friends.”\n\n30245m\n“For shame!” exclaimed the baroness.\n\n“Oh, let us have no gestures, no screams, no modern drama, or you will oblige me to tell you that I see Debray leave here, pocketing the whole of the 500,000 livres you have handed over to him this year, while he smiles to himself, saying that he has found what the most skilful players have never discovered—that is, a roulette where he wins without playing, and is no loser when he loses.”\n\nThe baroness became enraged.\n\n“Wretch!” she cried, “will you dare to tell me you did not know what you now reproach me with?”\n\n“I do not say that I did know it, and I do not say that I did not know it. I merely tell you to look into my conduct during the last four years that we have ceased to be husband and wife, and see whether it has not always been consistent. Some time after our rupture, you wished to study music, under the celebrated baritone who made such a successful appearance at the Théâtre Italien; at the same time I felt inclined to learn dancing of the _danseuse_ who acquired such a reputation in London. This cost me, on your account and mine, 100,000 francs. I said nothing, for we must have peace in the house; and 100,000 francs for a lady and gentleman to be properly instructed in music and dancing are not too much. Well, you soon become tired of singing, and you take a fancy to study diplomacy with the minister’s secretary. You understand, it signifies nothing to me so long as you pay for your lessons out of your own cash box. But today I find you are drawing on mine, and that your apprenticeship may cost me 700,000 francs per month. Stop there, madame, for this cannot last. Either the diplomatist must give his lessons gratis, and I will tolerate him, or he must never set his foot again in my house;—do you understand, madame?”\n\n“Oh, this is too much,” cried Hermine, choking, “you are worse than despicable.”\n\n“But,” continued Danglars, “I find you did not even pause there——”\n\n“Insults!”\n\n“You are right; let us leave these facts alone, and reason coolly. I have never interfered in your affairs excepting for your good; treat me in the same way. You say you have nothing to do with my cash box. Be it so. Do as you like with your own, but do not fill or empty mine. Besides, how do I know that this was not a political trick, that the minister enraged at seeing me in the opposition, and jealous of the popular sympathy I excite, has not concerted with M. Debray to ruin me?”\n\n“A probable thing!”\n\n“Why not? Who ever heard of such an occurrence as this?—a false telegraphic despatch—it is almost impossible for wrong signals to be made as they were in the last two telegrams. It was done on purpose for me—I am sure of it.”\n\n“Sir,” said the baroness humbly, “are you not aware that the man employed there was dismissed, that they talked of going to law with him, that orders were issued to arrest him and that this order would have been put into execution if he had not escaped by flight, which proves that he was either mad or guilty? It was a mistake.”\n\n“Yes, which made fools laugh, which caused the minister to have a sleepless night, which has caused the minister’s secretaries to blacken several sheets of paper, but which has cost me 700,000 francs.”\n\n“But, sir,” said Hermine suddenly, “if all this is, as you say, caused by M. Debray, why, instead of going direct to him, do you come and tell me of it? Why, to accuse the man, do you address the woman?”\n\n“Do I know M. Debray?—do I wish to know him?—do I wish to know that he gives advice?—do I wish to follow it?—do I speculate? No; you do all this, not I.”\n\n“Still it seems to me, that as you profit by it——”\n\nDanglars shrugged his shoulders. “Foolish creature,” he exclaimed. “Women fancy they have talent because they have managed two or three intrigues without being the talk of Paris! But know that if you had even hidden your irregularities from your husband, who has but the commencement of the art—for generally husbands _will_ not see—you would then have been but a faint imitation of most of your friends among the women of the world. But it has not been so with me,—I see, and always have seen, during the last sixteen years. You may, perhaps, have hidden a thought; but not a step, not an action, not a fault, has escaped me, while you flattered yourself upon your address, and firmly believed you had deceived me. What has been the result?—that, thanks to my pretended ignorance, there is none of your friends, from M. de Villefort to M. Debray, who has not trembled before me. There is not one who has not treated me as the master of the house,—the only title I desire with respect to you; there is not one, in fact, who would have dared to speak of me as I have spoken of them this day. I will allow you to make me hateful, but I will prevent your rendering me ridiculous, and, above all, I forbid you to ruin me.”\n\nThe baroness had been tolerably composed until the name of Villefort had been pronounced; but then she became pale, and, rising, as if touched by a spring, she stretched out her hands as though conjuring an apparition; she then took two or three steps towards her husband, as though to tear the secret from him, of which he was ignorant, or which he withheld from some odious calculation,—odious, as all his calculations were.\n\n“M. de Villefort!—What do you mean?”\n\n“I mean that M. de Nargonne, your first husband, being neither a philosopher nor a banker, or perhaps being both, and seeing there was nothing to be got out of a king’s attorney, died of grief or anger at finding, after an absence of nine months, that you had been _enceinte_ six. I am brutal,—I not only allow it, but boast of it; it is one of the reasons of my success in commercial business. Why did he kill himself instead of you? Because he had no cash to save. My life belongs to my cash. M. Debray has made me lose 700,000 francs; let him bear his share of the loss, and we will go on as before; if not, let him become bankrupt for the 250,000 livres, and do as all bankrupts do—disappear. He is a charming fellow, I allow, when his news is correct; but when it is not, there are fifty others in the world who would do better than he.”\n\nMadame Danglars was rooted to the spot; she made a violent effort to reply to this last attack, but she fell upon a chair thinking of Villefort, of the dinner scene, of the strange series of misfortunes which had taken place in her house during the last few days, and changed the usual calm of her establishment to a scene of scandalous debate.\n\nDanglars did not even look at her, though she did her best to faint. He shut the bedroom door after him, without adding another word, and returned to his apartments; and when Madame Danglars recovered from her half-fainting condition, she could almost believe that she had had a disagreeable dream.\n\n# Chapter 66. Matrimonial Projects\n\nThe day following this scene, at the hour Debray usually chose to pay a visit to Madame Danglars on his way to his office, his _coupé_ did not appear. At this time, that is, about half-past twelve, Madame Danglars ordered her carriage, and went out. Danglars, hidden behind a curtain, watched the departure he had been waiting for. He gave orders that he should be informed as soon as Madame Danglars appeared; but at two o’clock she had not returned. He then called for his horses, drove to the Chamber, and inscribed his name to speak against the budget. From twelve to two o’clock Danglars had remained in his study, unsealing his dispatches, and becoming more and more sad every minute, heaping figure upon figure, and receiving, among other visits, one from Major Cavalcanti, who, as stiff and exact as ever, presented himself precisely at the hour named the night before, to terminate his business with the banker.\n\nOn leaving the Chamber, Danglars, who had shown violent marks of agitation during the sitting, and been more bitter than ever against the ministry, re-entered his carriage, and told the coachman to drive to the Avenue des Champs-Élysées, No. 30.\n\nMonte Cristo was at home; only he was engaged with someone and begged Danglars to wait for a moment in the drawing-room. While the banker was waiting in the anteroom, the door opened, and a man dressed as an abbé and doubtless more familiar with the house than he was, came in and instead of waiting, merely bowed, passed on to the farther apartments, and disappeared.\n\nA minute after the door by which the priest had entered reopened, and Monte Cristo appeared.\n\n“Pardon me,” said he, “my dear baron, but one of my friends, the Abbé Busoni, whom you perhaps saw pass by, has just arrived in Paris; not having seen him for a long time, I could not make up my mind to leave him sooner, so I hope this will be sufficient reason for my having made you wait.”\n\n“Nay,” said Danglars, “it is my fault; I have chosen my visit at a wrong time, and will retire.”\n\n“Not at all; on the contrary, be seated; but what is the matter with you? You look careworn; really, you alarm me. Melancholy in a capitalist, like the appearance of a comet, presages some misfortune to the world.”\n\n“I have been in ill-luck for several days,” said Danglars, “and I have heard nothing but bad news.”\n\n“Ah, indeed?” said Monte Cristo. “Have you had another fall at the Bourse?”\n\n“No; I am safe for a few days at least. I am only annoyed about a bankrupt of Trieste.”\n\n“Really? Does it happen to be Jacopo Manfredi?”\n\n“Exactly so. Imagine a man who has transacted business with me for I don’t know how long, to the amount of 800,000 or 900,000 francs during the year. Never a mistake or delay—a fellow who paid like a prince. Well, I was a million in advance with him, and now my fine Jacopo Manfredi suspends payment!”\n\n“Really?”\n\n“It is an unheard-of fatality. I draw upon him for 600,000 francs, my bills are returned unpaid, and, more than that, I hold bills of exchange signed by him to the value of 400,000 francs, payable at his correspondent’s in Paris at the end of this month. Today is the 30th. I present them; but my correspondent has disappeared. This, with my Spanish affairs, made a pretty end to the month.”\n\n“Then you really lost by that affair in Spain?”\n\n“Yes; only 700,000 francs out of my cash box—nothing more!”\n\n“Why, how could you make such a mistake—such an old stager?”\n\n“Oh, it is all my wife’s fault. She dreamed Don Carlos had returned to Spain; she believes in dreams. It is magnetism, she says, and when she dreams a thing it is sure to happen, she assures me. On this conviction I allow her to speculate, she having her bank and her stockbroker; she speculated and lost. It is true she speculates with her own money, not mine; nevertheless, you can understand that when 700,000 francs leave the wife’s pocket, the husband always finds it out. But do you mean to say you have not heard of this? Why, the thing has made a tremendous noise.”\n\n“Yes, I heard it spoken of, but I did not know the details, and then no one can be more ignorant than I am of the affairs in the Bourse.”\n\n30251m\n“Then you do not speculate?”\n\n“I?—How could I speculate when I already have so much trouble in regulating my income? I should be obliged, besides my steward, to keep a clerk and a boy. But touching these Spanish affairs, I think that the baroness did not dream the whole of the Don Carlos matter. The papers said something about it, did they not?”\n\n“Then you believe the papers?”\n\n“I?—not the least in the world; only I fancied that the honest _Messager_ was an exception to the rule, and that it only announced telegraphic despatches.”\n\n“Well, that’s what puzzles me,” replied Danglars; “the news of the return of Don Carlos was brought by telegraph.”\n\n“So that,” said Monte Cristo, “you have lost nearly 1,700,000 francs this month.”\n\n“Not nearly, indeed; that is exactly my loss.”\n\n“_Diable!_” said Monte Cristo compassionately, “it is a hard blow for a third-rate fortune.”\n\n“Third-rate,” said Danglars, rather humble, “what do you mean by that?”\n\n“Certainly,” continued Monte Cristo, “I make three assortments in fortune—first-rate, second-rate, and third-rate fortunes. I call those first-rate which are composed of treasures one possesses under one’s hand, such as mines, lands, and funded property, in such states as France, Austria, and England, provided these treasures and property form a total of about a hundred millions; I call those second-rate fortunes, that are gained by manufacturing enterprises, joint-stock companies, viceroyalties, and principalities, not drawing more than 1,500,000 francs, the whole forming a capital of about fifty millions; finally, I call those third-rate fortunes, which are composed of a fluctuating capital, dependent upon the will of others, or upon chances which a bankruptcy involves or a false telegram shakes, such as banks, speculations of the day—in fact, all operations under the influence of greater or less mischances, the whole bringing in a real or fictitious capital of about fifteen millions. I think this is about your position, is it not?”\n\n“Confound it, yes!” replied Danglars.\n\n“The result, then, of six more such months as this would be to reduce the third-rate house to despair.”\n\n“Oh,” said Danglars, becoming very pale, how you are running on!”\n\n“Let us imagine seven such months,” continued Monte Cristo, in the same tone. “Tell me, have you ever thought that seven times 1,700,000 francs make nearly twelve millions? No, you have not;—well, you are right, for if you indulged in such reflections, you would never risk your principal, which is to the speculator what the skin is to civilized man. We have our clothes, some more splendid than others,—this is our credit; but when a man dies he has only his skin; in the same way, on retiring from business, you have nothing but your real principal of about five or six millions, at the most; for third-rate fortunes are never more than a fourth of what they appear to be, like the locomotive on a railway, the size of which is magnified by the smoke and steam surrounding it. Well, out of the five or six millions which form your real capital, you have just lost nearly two millions, which must, of course, in the same degree diminish your credit and fictitious fortune; to follow out my simile, your skin has been opened by bleeding, and this if repeated three or four times will cause death—so pay attention to it, my dear Monsieur Danglars. Do you want money? Do you wish me to lend you some?”\n30253m\n\n“What a bad calculator you are!” exclaimed Danglars, calling to his assistance all his philosophy and dissimulation. “I have made money at the same time by speculations which have succeeded. I have made up the loss of blood by nutrition. I lost a battle in Spain, I have been defeated in Trieste, but my naval army in India will have taken some galleons, and my Mexican pioneers will have discovered some mine.”\n\n“Very good, very good! But the wound remains and will reopen at the first loss.”\n\n“No, for I am only embarked in certainties,” replied Danglars, with the air of a mountebank sounding his own praises; “to involve me, three governments must crumble to dust.”\n\n“Well, such things have been.”\n\n“That there should be a famine!”\n\n“Recollect the seven fat and the seven lean kine.”\n\n“Or, that the sea should become dry, as in the days of Pharaoh, and even then my vessels would become caravans.”\n\n“So much the better. I congratulate you, my dear M. Danglars,” said Monte Cristo; “I see I was deceived, and that you belong to the class of second-rate fortunes.”\n\n“I think I may aspire to that honor,” said Danglars with a smile, which reminded Monte Cristo of the sickly moons which bad artists are so fond of daubing into their pictures of ruins. “But, while we are speaking of business,” Danglars added, pleased to find an opportunity of changing the subject, “tell me what I am to do for M. Cavalcanti.”\n\n“Give him money, if he is recommended to you, and the recommendation seems good.”\n\n“Excellent; he presented himself this morning with a bond of 40,000 francs, payable at sight, on you, signed by Busoni, and returned by you to me, with your endorsement—of course, I immediately counted him over the forty bank-notes.”\n\nMonte Cristo nodded his head in token of assent.\n\n“But that is not all,” continued Danglars; “he has opened an account with my house for his son.”\n\n“May I ask how much he allows the young man?”\n\n“Five thousand francs per month.”\n\n“Sixty thousand francs per year. I thought I was right in believing that Cavalcanti to be a stingy fellow. How can a young man live upon 5,000 francs a month?”\n\n“But you understand that if the young man should want a few thousands more——”\n\n“Do not advance it; the father will never repay it. You do not know these ultramontane millionaires; they are regular misers. And by whom were they recommended to you?”\n\n“Oh, by the house of Fenzi, one of the best in Florence.”\n\n“I do not mean to say you will lose, but, nevertheless, mind you hold to the terms of the agreement.”\n\n“Would you not trust the Cavalcanti?”\n\n“I? oh, I would advance ten millions on his signature. I was only speaking in reference to the second-rate fortunes we were mentioning just now.”\n\n“And with all this, how unassuming he is! I should never have taken him for anything more than a mere major.”\n\n“And you would have flattered him, for certainly, as you say, he has no manner. The first time I saw him he appeared to me like an old lieutenant who had grown mouldy under his epaulets. But all the Italians are the same; they are like old Jews when they are not glittering in Oriental splendor.”\n\n“The young man is better,” said Danglars.\n\n“Yes; a little nervous, perhaps, but, upon the whole, he appeared tolerable. I was uneasy about him.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because you met him at my house, just after his introduction into the world, as they told me. He has been travelling with a very severe tutor, and had never been to Paris before.”\n\n“Ah, I believe noblemen marry amongst themselves, do they not?” asked Danglars carelessly; “they like to unite their fortunes.”\n\n“It is usual, certainly; but Cavalcanti is an original who does nothing like other people. I cannot help thinking that he has brought his son to France to choose a wife.”\n\n“Do you think so?”\n\n“I am sure of it.”\n\n“And you have heard his fortune mentioned?”\n\n“Nothing else was talked of; only some said he was worth millions, and others that he did not possess a farthing.”\n\n“And what is your opinion?”\n\n“I ought not to influence you, because it is only my own personal impression.”\n\n“Well, and it is that——”\n\n“My opinion is, that all these old _podestàs_, these ancient _condottieri_,—for the Cavalcanti have commanded armies and governed provinces,—my opinion, I say, is, that they have buried their millions in corners, the secret of which they have transmitted only to their eldest sons, who have done the same from generation to generation; and the proof of this is seen in their yellow and dry appearance, like the florins of the republic, which, from being constantly gazed upon, have become reflected in them.”\n\n“Certainly,” said Danglars, “and this is further supported by the fact of their not possessing an inch of land.”\n\n“Very little, at least; I know of none which Cavalcanti possesses, excepting his palace in Lucca.”\n\n“Ah, he has a palace?” said Danglars, laughing; “come, that is something.”\n\n“Yes; and more than that, he lets it to the Minister of Finance while he lives in a simple house. Oh, as I told you before, I think the old fellow is very close.”\n\n“Come, you do not flatter him.”\n\n“I scarcely know him; I think I have seen him three times in my life; all I know relating to him is through Busoni and himself. He was telling me this morning that, tired of letting his property lie dormant in Italy, which is a dead nation, he wished to find a method, either in France or England, of multiplying his millions, but remember, that though I place great confidence in Busoni, I am not responsible for this.”\n\n“Never mind; accept my thanks for the client you have sent me. It is a fine name to inscribe on my ledgers, and my cashier was quite proud of it when I explained to him who the Cavalcanti were. By the way, this is merely a simple question, when this sort of people marry their sons, do they give them any fortune?”\n\n“Oh, that depends upon circumstances. I know an Italian prince, rich as a gold mine, one of the noblest families in Tuscany, who, when his sons married according to his wish, gave them millions; and when they married against his consent, merely allowed them thirty crowns a month. Should Andrea marry according to his father’s views, he will, perhaps, give him one, two, or three millions. For example, supposing it were the daughter of a banker, he might take an interest in the house of the father-in-law of his son; then again, if he disliked his choice, the major takes the key, double-locks his coffer, and Master Andrea would be obliged to live like the sons of a Parisian family, by shuffling cards or rattling the dice.”\n\n“Ah, that boy will find out some Bavarian or Peruvian princess; he will want a crown, an El Dorado, and Potosí.”\n\n“No; these grand lords on the other side of the Alps frequently marry into plain families; like Jupiter, they like to cross the race. But do you wish to marry Andrea, my dear M. Danglars, that you are asking so many questions?”\n\n“_Ma foi_,” said Danglars, “it would not be a bad speculation, I fancy, and you know I am a speculator.”\n\n“You are not thinking of Mademoiselle Danglars, I hope; you would not like poor Andrea to have his throat cut by Albert?”\n\n“Albert,” repeated Danglars, shrugging his shoulders; “ah, well; he would care very little about it, I think.”\n\n“But he is betrothed to your daughter, I believe?”\n\n“Well, M. de Morcerf and I have talked about this marriage, but Madame de Morcerf and Albert——”\n\n“You do not mean to say that it would not be a good match?”\n\n“Indeed, I imagine that Mademoiselle Danglars is as good as M. de Morcerf.”\n\n“Mademoiselle Danglars’ fortune will be great, no doubt, especially if the telegraph should not make any more mistakes.”\n\n“Oh, I do not mean her fortune only; but tell me——”\n\n“What?”\n\n“Why did you not invite M. and Madame de Morcerf to your dinner?”\n\n“I did so, but he excused himself on account of Madame de Morcerf being obliged to go to Dieppe for the benefit of sea air.”\n\n“Yes, yes,” said Danglars, laughing, “it would do her a great deal of good.”\n\n“Why so?”\n\n“Because it is the air she always breathed in her youth.”\n\nMonte Cristo took no notice of this ill-natured remark.\n\n“But still, if Albert be not so rich as Mademoiselle Danglars,” said the count, “you must allow that he has a fine name?”\n\n“So he has; but I like mine as well.”\n\n“Certainly; your name is popular, and does honor to the title they have adorned it with; but you are too intelligent not to know that according to a prejudice, too firmly rooted to be exterminated, a nobility which dates back five centuries is worth more than one that can only reckon twenty years.”\n\n“And for this very reason,” said Danglars with a smile, which he tried to make sardonic, “I prefer M. Andrea Cavalcanti to M. Albert de Morcerf.”\n\n“Still, I should not think the Morcerfs would yield to the Cavalcanti?”\n\n“The Morcerfs!—Stay, my dear count,” said Danglars; “you are a man of the world, are you not?”\n\n“I think so.”\n\n“And you understand heraldry?”\n\n“A little.”\n\n“Well, look at my coat-of-arms, it is worth more than Morcerf’s.”\n\n“Why so?”\n\n“Because, though I am not a baron by birth, my real name is, at least, Danglars.”\n\n“Well, what then?”\n\n“While his name is not Morcerf.”\n\n“How?—not Morcerf?”\n\n“Not the least in the world.”\n\n“Go on.”\n“I have been made a baron, so that I actually am one; he made himself a count, so that he is not one at all.”\n\n“Impossible!”\n\n“Listen my dear count; M. de Morcerf has been my friend, or rather my acquaintance, during the last thirty years. You know I have made the most of my arms, though I never forgot my origin.”\n\n“A proof of great humility or great pride,” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“Well, when I was a clerk, Morcerf was a mere fisherman.”\n\n“And then he was called——”\n\n“Fernand.”\n\n“Only Fernand?”\n\n“Fernand Mondego.”\n\n“You are sure?”\n\n“_Pardieu!_ I have bought enough fish of him to know his name.”\n\n“Then, why did you think of giving your daughter to him?”\n\n“Because Fernand and Danglars, being both parvenus, both having become noble, both rich, are about equal in worth, excepting that there have been certain things mentioned of him that were never said of me.”\n\n“What?”\n“Oh, nothing!”\n\n“Ah, yes; what you tell me recalls to mind something about the name of Fernand Mondego. I have heard that name in Greece.”\n\n“In conjunction with the affairs of Ali Pasha?”\n\n“Exactly so.”\n\n“This is the mystery,” said Danglars. “I acknowledge I would have given anything to find it out.”\n\n“It would be very easy if you much wished it?”\n\n“How so?”\n\n“Probably you have some correspondent in Greece?”\n\n“I should think so.”\n\n“At Yanina?”\n\n“Everywhere.”\n\n“Well, write to your correspondent in Yanina, and ask him what part was played by a Frenchman named Fernand Mondego in the catastrophe of Ali Tepelini.”\n\n“You are right,” exclaimed Danglars, rising quickly, “I will write today.”\n\n“Do so.”\n\n“I will.”\n30259m\n\n“And if you should hear of anything very scandalous——”\n\n“I will communicate it to you.”\n\n“You will oblige me.”\n\nDanglars rushed out of the room, and made but one leap into his _coupé_.\n\n# Chapter 67. The Office of the King’s Attorney\n\nLet us leave the banker driving his horses at their fullest speed, and follow Madame Danglars in her morning excursion. We have said that at half-past twelve o’clock Madame Danglars had ordered her horses, and had left home in the carriage. She directed her course towards the Faubourg Saint Germain, went down the Rue Mazarine, and stopped at the Passage du Pont-Neuf. She descended, and went through the passage. She was very plainly dressed, as would be the case with a woman of taste walking in the morning. At the Rue Guénégaud she called a cab, and directed the driver to go to the Rue de Harlay. As soon as she was seated in the vehicle, she drew from her pocket a very thick black veil, which she tied on to her straw bonnet. She then replaced the bonnet, and saw with pleasure, in a little pocket-mirror, that her white complexion and brilliant eyes were alone visible. The cab crossed the Pont-Neuf and entered the Rue de Harlay by the Place Dauphine; the driver was paid as the door opened, and stepping lightly up the stairs Madame Danglars soon reached the Salle des Pas-Perdus.\n\nThere was a great deal going on that morning, and many business-like persons at the Palais; business-like persons pay very little attention to women, and Madame Danglars crossed the hall without exciting any more attention than any other woman calling upon her lawyer.\n\nThere was a great press of people in M. de Villefort’s antechamber, but Madame Danglars had no occasion even to pronounce her name. The instant she appeared the door-keeper rose, came to her, and asked her whether she was not the person with whom the procureur had made an appointment; and on her affirmative answer being given, he conducted her by a private passage to M. de Villefort’s office.\n\nThe magistrate was seated in an armchair, writing, with his back towards the door; he did not move as he heard it open, and the door-keeper pronounce the words, “Walk in, madame,” and then reclose it; but no sooner had the man’s footsteps ceased, than he started up, drew the bolts, closed the curtains, and examined every corner of the room. Then, when he had assured himself that he could neither be seen nor heard, and was consequently relieved of doubts, he said:\n\n“Thanks, madame,—thanks for your punctuality;” and he offered a chair to Madame Danglars, which she accepted, for her heart beat so violently that she felt nearly suffocated.\n\n30261m\n“It is a long time, madame,” said the procureur, describing a half-circle with his chair, so as to place himself exactly opposite to Madame Danglars,—“it is a long time since I had the pleasure of speaking alone with you, and I regret that we have only now met to enter upon a painful conversation.”\n\n“Nevertheless, sir, you see I have answered your first appeal, although certainly the conversation must be much more painful for me than for you.” Villefort smiled bitterly.\n\n“It is true, then,” he said, rather uttering his thoughts aloud than addressing his companion,—“it is true, then, that all our actions leave their traces—some sad, others bright—on our paths; it is true that every step in our lives is like the course of an insect on the sands;—it leaves its track! Alas, to many the path is traced by tears.”\n\n“Sir,” said Madame Danglars, “you can feel for my emotion, can you not? Spare me, then, I beseech you. When I look at this room,—whence so many guilty creatures have departed, trembling and ashamed, when I look at that chair before which I now sit trembling and ashamed,—oh, it requires all my reason to convince me that I am not a very guilty woman and you a menacing judge.”\n\nVillefort dropped his head and sighed.\n\n“And I,” he said, “I feel that my place is not in the judge’s seat, but on the prisoner’s bench.”\n30263m\n\n“You?” said Madame Danglars.\n“Yes, I.”\n\n“I think, sir, you exaggerate your situation,” said Madame Danglars, whose beautiful eyes sparkled for a moment. “The paths of which you were just speaking have been traced by all young men of ardent imaginations. Besides the pleasure, there is always remorse from the indulgence of our passions, and, after all, what have you men to fear from all this? the world excuses, and notoriety ennobles you.”\n\n“Madame,” replied Villefort, “you know that I am no hypocrite, or, at least, that I never deceive without a reason. If my brow be severe, it is because many misfortunes have clouded it; if my heart be petrified, it is that it might sustain the blows it has received. I was not so in my youth, I was not so on the night of the betrothal, when we were all seated around a table in the Rue du Cours at Marseilles. But since then everything has changed in and about me; I am accustomed to brave difficulties, and, in the conflict to crush those who, by their own free will, or by chance, voluntarily or involuntarily, interfere with me in my career. It is generally the case that what we most ardently desire is as ardently withheld from us by those who wish to obtain it, or from whom we attempt to snatch it. Thus, the greater number of a man’s errors come before him disguised under the specious form of necessity; then, after error has been committed in a moment of excitement, of delirium, or of fear, we see that we might have avoided and escaped it. The means we might have used, which we in our blindness could not see, then seem simple and easy, and we say, ‘Why did I not do this, instead of that?’ Women, on the contrary, are rarely tormented with remorse; for the decision does not come from you,—your misfortunes are generally imposed upon you, and your faults the results of others’ crimes.”\n\n“In any case, sir, you will allow,” replied Madame Danglars, “that, even if the fault were alone mine, I last night received a severe punishment for it.”\n\n“Poor thing,” said Villefort, pressing her hand, “it was too severe for your strength, for you were twice overwhelmed, and yet——”\n\n“Well?”\n\n“Well, I must tell you. Collect all your courage, for you have not yet heard all.”\n\n“Ah,” exclaimed Madame Danglars, alarmed, “what is there more to hear?”\n\n“You only look back to the past, and it is, indeed, bad enough. Well, picture to yourself a future more gloomy still—certainly frightful, perhaps sanguinary!”\n\nThe baroness knew how calm Villefort naturally was, and his present excitement frightened her so much that she opened her mouth to scream, but the sound died in her throat.\n\n“How has this terrible past been recalled?” cried Villefort; “how is it that it has escaped from the depths of the tomb and the recesses of our hearts, where it was buried, to visit us now, like a phantom, whitening our cheeks and flushing our brows with shame?”\n\n“Alas,” said Hermine, “doubtless it is chance.”\n\n“Chance?” replied Villefort; “No, no, madame, there is no such thing as chance.”\n\n“Oh, yes; has not a fatal chance revealed all this? Was it not by chance the Count of Monte Cristo bought that house? Was it not by chance he caused the earth to be dug up? Is it not by chance that the unfortunate child was disinterred under the trees?—that poor innocent offspring of mine, which I never even kissed, but for whom I wept many, many tears. Ah, my heart clung to the count when he mentioned the dear spoil found beneath the flowers.”\n\n“Well, no, madame,—this is the terrible news I have to tell you,” said Villefort in a hollow voice—“no, nothing was found beneath the flowers; there was no child disinterred—no. You must not weep, no, you must not groan, you must tremble!”\n\n“What can you mean?” asked Madame Danglars, shuddering.\n\n“I mean that M. de Monte Cristo, digging underneath these trees, found neither skeleton nor chest, because neither of them was there!”\n\n“Neither of them there?” repeated Madame Danglars, her staring, wide-open eyes expressing her alarm. “Neither of them there!” she again said, as though striving to impress herself with the meaning of the words which escaped her.\n\n“No,” said Villefort, burying his face in his hands, “no, a hundred times no!”\n\n“Then you did not bury the poor child there, sir? Why did you deceive me? Where did you place it? tell me—where?”\n\n“There! But listen to me—listen—and you will pity me who has for twenty years alone borne the heavy burden of grief I am about to reveal, without casting the least portion upon you.”\n\n“Oh, you frighten me! But speak; I will listen.”\n\n“You recollect that sad night, when you were half-expiring on that bed in the red damask room, while I, scarcely less agitated than you, awaited your delivery. The child was born, was given to me—motionless, breathless, voiceless; we thought it dead.”\n\nMadame Danglars moved rapidly, as though she would spring from her chair, but Villefort stopped, and clasped his hands as if to implore her attention.\n\n“We thought it dead,” he repeated; “I placed it in the chest, which was to take the place of a coffin; I descended to the garden, I dug a hole, and then flung it down in haste. Scarcely had I covered it with earth, when the arm of the Corsican was stretched towards me; I saw a shadow rise, and, at the same time, a flash of light. I felt pain; I wished to cry out, but an icy shiver ran through my veins and stifled my voice; I fell lifeless, and fancied myself killed. Never shall I forget your sublime courage, when, having returned to consciousness, I dragged myself to the foot of the stairs, and you, almost dying yourself, came to meet me. We were obliged to keep silent upon the dreadful catastrophe. You had the fortitude to regain the house, assisted by your nurse. A duel was the pretext for my wound. Though we scarcely expected it, our secret remained in our own keeping alone. I was taken to Versailles; for three months I struggled with death; at last, as I seemed to cling to life, I was ordered to the South. Four men carried me from Paris to Châlons, walking six leagues a day; Madame de Villefort followed the litter in her carriage. At Châlons I was put upon the Saône, thence I passed on to the Rhône, whence I descended, merely with the current, to Arles; at Arles I was again placed on my litter, and continued my journey to Marseilles. My recovery lasted six months. I never heard you mentioned, and I did not dare inquire for you. When I returned to Paris, I learned that you, the widow of M. de Nargonne, had married M. Danglars.\n\n“What was the subject of my thoughts from the time consciousness returned to me? Always the same—always the child’s corpse, coming every night in my dreams, rising from the earth, and hovering over the grave with menacing look and gesture. I inquired immediately on my return to Paris; the house had not been inhabited since we left it, but it had just been let for nine years. I found the tenant. I pretended that I disliked the idea that a house belonging to my wife’s father and mother should pass into the hands of strangers. I offered to pay them for cancelling the lease; they demanded 6,000 francs. I would have given 10,000—I would have given 20,000. I had the money with me; I made the tenant sign the deed of resilition, and when I had obtained what I so much wanted, I galloped to Auteuil. No one had entered the house since I had left it.\n\n“It was five o’clock in the afternoon; I ascended into the red room, and waited for night. There all the thoughts which had disturbed me during my year of constant agony came back with double force. The Corsican, who had declared the vendetta against me, who had followed me from Nîmes to Paris, who had hid himself in the garden, who had struck me, had seen me dig the grave, had seen me inter the child,—he might become acquainted with your person,—nay, he might even then have known it. Would he not one day make you pay for keeping this terrible secret? Would it not be a sweet revenge for him when he found that I had not died from the blow of his dagger? It was therefore necessary, before everything else, and at all risks, that I should cause all traces of the past to disappear—that I should destroy every material vestige; too much reality would always remain in my recollection. It was for this I had annulled the lease—it was for this I had come—it was for this I was waiting.\n\n“Night arrived; I allowed it to become quite dark. I was without a light in that room; when the wind shook all the doors, behind which I continually expected to see some spy concealed, I trembled. I seemed everywhere to hear your moans behind me in the bed, and I dared not turn around. My heart beat so violently that I feared my wound would open. At length, one by one, all the noises in the neighborhood ceased. I understood that I had nothing to fear, that I should neither be seen nor heard, so I decided upon descending to the garden.\n\n“Listen, Hermine; I consider myself as brave as most men, but when I drew from my breast the little key of the staircase, which I had found in my coat—that little key we both used to cherish so much, which you wished to have fastened to a golden ring—when I opened the door, and saw the pale moon shedding a long stream of white light on the spiral staircase like a spectre, I leaned against the wall, and nearly shrieked. I seemed to be going mad. At last I mastered my agitation. I descended the staircase step by step; the only thing I could not conquer was a strange trembling in my knees. I grasped the railings; if I had relaxed my hold for a moment, I should have fallen. I reached the lower door. Outside this door a spade was placed against the wall; I took it, and advanced towards the thicket. I had provided myself with a dark lantern. In the middle of the lawn I stopped to light it, then I continued my path.\n\n“It was the end of November, all the verdure of the garden had disappeared, the trees were nothing more than skeletons with their long bony arms, and the dead leaves sounded on the gravel under my feet. My terror overcame me to such a degree as I approached the thicket, that I took a pistol from my pocket and armed myself. I fancied continually that I saw the figure of the Corsican between the branches. I examined the thicket with my dark lantern; it was empty. I looked carefully around; I was indeed alone,—no noise disturbed the silence but the owl, whose piercing cry seemed to be calling up the phantoms of the night. I tied my lantern to a forked branch I had noticed a year before at the precise spot where I stopped to dig the hole.\n\n“The grass had grown very thickly there during the summer, and when autumn arrived no one had been there to mow it. Still one place where the grass was thin attracted my attention; it evidently was there I had turned up the ground. I went to work. The hour, then, for which I had been waiting during the last year had at length arrived. How I worked, how I hoped, how I struck every piece of turf, thinking to find some resistance to my spade! But no, I found nothing, though I had made a hole twice as large as the first. I thought I had been deceived—had mistaken the spot. I turned around, I looked at the trees, I tried to recall the details which had struck me at the time. A cold, sharp wind whistled through the leafless branches, and yet the drops fell from my forehead. I recollected that I was stabbed just as I was trampling the ground to fill up the hole; while doing so I had leaned against a laburnum; behind me was an artificial rockery, intended to serve as a resting-place for persons walking in the garden; in falling, my hand, relaxing its hold of the laburnum, felt the coldness of the stone. On my right I saw the tree, behind me the rock. I stood in the same attitude, and threw myself down. I rose, and again began digging and enlarging the hole; still I found nothing, nothing—the chest was no longer there!”\n30269m\n\n“The chest no longer there?” murmured Madame Danglars, choking with fear.\n\n“Think not I contented myself with this one effort,” continued Villefort. “No; I searched the whole thicket. I thought the assassin, having discovered the chest, and supposing it to be a treasure, had intended carrying it off, but, perceiving his error, had dug another hole, and deposited it there; but I could find nothing. Then the idea struck me that he had not taken these precautions, and had simply thrown it in a corner. In the last case I must wait for daylight to renew my search. I remained in the room and waited.”\n\n“Oh, Heaven!”\n\n30271m\nWhen daylight dawned I went down again. My first visit was to the thicket. I hoped to find some traces which had escaped me in the darkness. I had turned up the earth over a surface of more than twenty feet square, and a depth of two feet. A laborer would not have done in a day what occupied me an hour. But I could find nothing—absolutely nothing. Then I renewed the search. Supposing it had been thrown aside, it would probably be on the path which led to the little gate; but this examination was as useless as the first, and with a bursting heart I returned to the thicket, which now contained no hope for me.”\n\n“Oh,” cried Madame Danglars, “it was enough to drive you mad!”\n\n“I hoped for a moment that it might,” said Villefort; “but that happiness was denied me. However, recovering my strength and my ideas, ‘Why,’ said I, ‘should that man have carried away the corpse?’”\n\n“But you said,” replied Madame Danglars, “he would require it as a proof.”\n\n“Ah, no, madame, that could not be. Dead bodies are not kept a year; they are shown to a magistrate, and the evidence is taken. Now, nothing of the kind has happened.”\n\n“What then?” asked Hermine, trembling violently.\n\n“Something more terrible, more fatal, more alarming for us—the child was, perhaps, alive, and the assassin may have saved it!”\n\nMadame Danglars uttered a piercing cry, and, seizing Villefort’s hands, exclaimed, “My child was alive?” said she; “you buried my child alive? You were not certain my child was dead, and you buried it? Ah——”\n\nMadame Danglars had risen, and stood before the procureur, whose hands she wrung in her feeble grasp.\n\n“I know not; I merely suppose so, as I might suppose anything else,” replied Villefort with a look so fixed, it indicated that his powerful mind was on the verge of despair and madness.\n\n“Ah, my child, my poor child!” cried the baroness, falling on her chair, and stifling her sobs in her handkerchief. Villefort, becoming somewhat reassured, perceived that to avert the maternal storm gathering over his head, he must inspire Madame Danglars with the terror he felt.\n\n“You understand, then, that if it were so,” said he, rising in his turn, and approaching the baroness, to speak to her in a lower tone, “we are lost. This child lives, and someone knows it lives—someone is in possession of our secret; and since Monte Cristo speaks before us of a child disinterred, when that child could not be found, it is he who is in possession of our secret.”\n\n“Just God, avenging God!” murmured Madame Danglars.\n\nVillefort’s only answer was a stifled groan.\n\n“But the child—the child, sir?” repeated the agitated mother.\n\n“How I have searched for him,” replied Villefort, wringing his hands; “how I have called him in my long sleepless nights; how I have longed for royal wealth to purchase a million of secrets from a million of men, and to find mine among them! At last, one day, when for the hundredth time I took up my spade, I asked myself again and again what the Corsican could have done with the child. A child encumbers a fugitive; perhaps, on perceiving it was still alive, he had thrown it into the river.”\n\n“Impossible!” cried Madame Danglars: “a man may murder another out of revenge, but he would not deliberately drown a child.”\n\n“Perhaps,” continued Villefort, “he had put it in the foundling hospital.”\n\n“Oh, yes, yes,” cried the baroness; “my child is there!”\n\n“I ran to the hospital, and learned that the same night—the night of the 20th of September—a child had been brought there, wrapped in part of a fine linen napkin, purposely torn in half. This portion of the napkin was marked with half a baron’s crown, and the letter H.”\n\n“Truly, truly,” said Madame Danglars, “all my linen is marked thus; Monsieur de Nargonne was a baron, and my name is Hermine. Thank God, my child was not then dead!”\n\n“No, it was not dead.”\n\n“And you can tell me so without fearing to make me die of joy? Where is the child?”\n\nVillefort shrugged his shoulders.\n\n“Do I know?” said he; “and do you believe that if I knew I would relate to you all its trials and all its adventures as would a dramatist or a novel writer? Alas, no, I know not. A woman, about six months after, came to claim it with the other half of the napkin. This woman gave all the requisite particulars, and it was intrusted to her.”\n\n“But you should have inquired for the woman; you should have traced her.”\n\n“And what do you think I did? I feigned a criminal process, and employed all the most acute bloodhounds and skilful agents in search of her. They traced her to Châlons, and there they lost her.”\n\n“They lost her?”\n\n“Yes, forever.”\n\nMadame Danglars had listened to this recital with a sigh, a tear, or a shriek for every detail. “And this is all?” said she; “and you stopped there?”\n\n“Oh, no,” said Villefort; “I never ceased to search and to inquire. However, the last two or three years I had allowed myself some respite. But now I will begin with more perseverance and fury than ever, since fear urges me, not my conscience.”\n\n“But,” replied Madame Danglars, “the Count of Monte Cristo can know nothing, or he would not seek our society as he does.”\n\n“Oh, the wickedness of man is very great,” said Villefort, “since it surpasses the goodness of God. Did you observe that man’s eyes while he was speaking to us?”\n\n“No.”\n“But have you ever watched him carefully?”\n\n“Doubtless he is capricious, but that is all; one thing alone struck me,—of all the exquisite things he placed before us, he touched nothing. I might have suspected he was poisoning us.”\n\n“And you see you would have been deceived.”\n\n“Yes, doubtless.”\n\n“But believe me, that man has other projects. For that reason I wished to see you, to speak to you, to warn you against everyone, but especially against him. Tell me,” cried Villefort, fixing his eyes more steadfastly on her than he had ever done before, “did you ever reveal to anyone our connection?”\n\n“Never, to anyone.”\n\n“You understand me,” replied Villefort, affectionately; “when I say anyone,—pardon my urgency,—to anyone living I mean?”\n\n“Yes, yes, I understand very well,” ejaculated the baroness; “never, I swear to you.”\n\n“Were you ever in the habit of writing in the evening what had transpired in the morning? Do you keep a journal?”\n\n“No, my life has been passed in frivolity; I wish to forget it myself.”\n\n“Do you talk in your sleep?”\n\n30275m\n“I sleep soundly, like a child; do you not remember?”\n\nThe color mounted to the baroness’s face, and Villefort turned awfully pale.\n\n“It is true,” said he, in so low a tone that he could hardly be heard.\n\n“Well?” said the baroness.\n\n“Well, I understand what I now have to do,” replied Villefort. “In less than one week from this time I will ascertain who this M. de Monte Cristo is, whence he comes, where he goes, and why he speaks in our presence of children that have been disinterred in a garden.”\n\nVillefort pronounced these words with an accent which would have made the count shudder had he heard him. Then he pressed the hand the baroness reluctantly gave him, and led her respectfully back to the door. Madame Danglars returned in another cab to the passage, on the other side of which she found her carriage, and her coachman sleeping peacefully on his box while waiting for her.\n\n# Chapter 68. A Summer Ball\n\nThe same day during the interview between Madame Danglars and the procureur, a travelling-carriage entered the Rue du Helder, passed through the gateway of No. 27, and stopped in the yard. In a moment the door was opened, and Madame de Morcerf alighted, leaning on her son’s arm. Albert soon left her, ordered his horses, and having arranged his toilet, drove to the Champs-Élysées, to the house of Monte Cristo.\n\nThe count received him with his habitual smile. It was a strange thing that no one ever appeared to advance a step in that man’s favor. Those who would, as it were, force a passage to his heart, found an impassable barrier. Morcerf, who ran towards him with open arms, was chilled as he drew near, in spite of the friendly smile, and simply held out his hand. Monte Cristo shook it coldly, according to his invariable practice.\n\n“Here I am, dear count.”\n\n“Welcome home again.”\n\n“I arrived an hour since.”\n\n“From Dieppe?”\n\n“No, from Tréport.”\n\n“Indeed?”\n\n“And I have come at once to see you.”\n\n“That is extremely kind of you,” said Monte Cristo with a tone of perfect indifference.\n\n“And what is the news?”\n\n“You should not ask a stranger, a foreigner, for news.”\n\n“I know it, but in asking for news, I mean, have you done anything for me?”\n\n“Had you commissioned me?” said Monte Cristo, feigning uneasiness.\n\n“Come, come,” said Albert, “do not assume so much indifference. It is said, sympathy travels rapidly, and when at Tréport, I felt the electric shock; you have either been working for me or thinking of me.”\n\n“Possibly,” said Monte Cristo, “I have indeed thought of you, but the magnetic wire I was guiding acted, indeed, without my knowledge.”\n\n30277m\n“Indeed! Pray tell me how it happened.”\n\n“Willingly. M. Danglars dined with me.”\n\n“I know it; to avoid meeting him, my mother and I left town.”\n\n“But he met here M. Andrea Cavalcanti.”\n\n“Your Italian prince?”\n\n“Not so fast; M. Andrea only calls himself count.”\n\n“Calls himself, do you say?”\n\n“Yes, calls himself.”\n\n“Is he not a count?”\n\n“What can I know of him? He calls himself so. I, of course, give him the same title, and everyone else does likewise.”\n\n“What a strange man you are! What next? You say M. Danglars dined here?”\n\n“Yes, with Count Cavalcanti, the marquis his father, Madame Danglars, M. and Madame de Villefort,—charming people,—M. Debray, Maximilian Morrel, and M. de Château-Renaud.”\n\n“Did they speak of me?”\n\n“Not a word.”\n\n“So much the worse.”\n“Why so? I thought you wished them to forget you?”\n\n“If they did not speak of me, I am sure they thought about me, and I am in despair.”\n\n“How will that affect you, since Mademoiselle Danglars was not among the number here who thought of you? Truly, she might have thought of you at home.”\n\n“I have no fear of that; or, if she did, it was only in the same way in which I think of her.”\n\n“Touching sympathy! So you hate each other?” said the count.\n\n“Listen,” said Morcerf—“if Mademoiselle Danglars were disposed to take pity on my supposed martyrdom on her account, and would dispense with all matrimonial formalities between our two families, I am ready to agree to the arrangement. In a word, Mademoiselle Danglars would make a charming mistress—but a wife—_diable!_”\n\n“And this,” said Monte Cristo, “is your opinion of your intended spouse?”\n\n“Yes; it is rather unkind, I acknowledge, but it is true. But as this dream cannot be realized, since Mademoiselle Danglars must become my lawful wife, live perpetually with me, sing to me, compose verses and music within ten paces of me, and that for my whole life, it frightens me. One may forsake a mistress, but a wife,—good heavens! There she must always be; and to marry Mademoiselle Danglars would be awful.”\n\n“You are difficult to please, viscount.”\n\n“Yes, for I often wish for what is impossible.”\n\n“What is that?”\n\n“To find such a wife as my father found.”\n\nMonte Cristo turned pale, and looked at Albert, while playing with some magnificent pistols.\n\n“Your father was fortunate, then?” said he.\n\n“You know my opinion of my mother, count; look at her,—still beautiful, witty, more charming than ever. For any other son to have stayed with his mother for four days at Tréport, it would have been a condescension or a martyrdom, while I return, more contented, more peaceful—shall I say more poetic!—than if I had taken Queen Mab or Titania as my companion.”\n30279m\n\n“That is an overwhelming demonstration, and you would make everyone vow to live a single life.”\n\n“Such are my reasons for not liking to marry Mademoiselle Danglars. Have you ever noticed how much a thing is heightened in value when we obtain possession of it? The diamond which glittered in the window at Marlé’s or Fossin’s shines with more splendor when it is our own; but if we are compelled to acknowledge the superiority of another, and still must retain the one that is inferior, do you not know what we have to endure?”\n\n“Worldling,” murmured the count.\n\n“Thus I shall rejoice when Mademoiselle Eugénie perceives I am but a pitiful atom, with scarcely as many hundred thousand francs as she has millions.” Monte Cristo smiled. “One plan occurred to me,” continued Albert; “Franz likes all that is eccentric; I tried to make him fall in love with Mademoiselle Danglars; but in spite of four letters, written in the most alluring style, he invariably answered: ‘My eccentricity may be great, but it will not make me break my promise.’”\n\n“That is what I call devoted friendship, to recommend to another one whom you would not marry yourself.” Albert smiled.\n\n“Apropos,” continued he, “Franz is coming soon, but it will not interest you; you dislike him, I think?”\n\n“I?” said Monte Cristo; “my dear viscount, how have you discovered that I did not like M. Franz! I like everyone.”\n\n“And you include me in the expression everyone—many thanks!”\n\n“Let us not mistake,” said Monte Cristo; “I love everyone as God commands us to love our neighbor, as Christians; but I thoroughly hate but a few. Let us return to M. Franz d’Épinay. Did you say he was coming?”\n\n“Yes; summoned by M. de Villefort, who is apparently as anxious to get Mademoiselle Valentine married as M. Danglars is to see Mademoiselle Eugénie settled. It must be a very irksome office to be the father of a grown-up daughter; it seems to make one feverish, and to raise one’s pulse to ninety beats a minute until the deed is done.”\n\n“But M. d’Épinay, unlike you, bears his misfortune patiently.”\n\n“Still more, he talks seriously about the matter, puts on a white tie, and speaks of his family. He entertains a very high opinion of M. and Madame de Villefort.”\n\n“Which they deserve, do they not?”\n\n“I believe they do. M. de Villefort has always passed for a severe but a just man.”\n\n“There is, then, one,” said Monte Cristo, “whom you do not condemn like poor Danglars?”\n\n“Because I am not compelled to marry his daughter perhaps,” replied Albert, laughing.\n\n“Indeed, my dear sir,” said Monte Cristo, “you are revoltingly foppish.”\n\n“I foppish? how do you mean?”\n\n“Yes; pray take a cigar, and cease to defend yourself, and to struggle to escape marrying Mademoiselle Danglars. Let things take their course; perhaps you may not have to retract.”\n\n“Bah!” said Albert, staring.\n\n“Doubtless, my dear viscount, you will not be taken by force; and seriously, do you wish to break off your engagement?”\n\n“I would give a hundred thousand francs to be able to do so.”\n\n“Then make yourself quite easy. M. Danglars would give double that sum to attain the same end.”\n\n“Am I, indeed, so happy?” said Albert, who still could not prevent an almost imperceptible cloud passing across his brow. “But, my dear count, has M. Danglars any reason?”\n\n“Ah! there is your proud and selfish nature. You would expose the self-love of another with a hatchet, but you shrink if your own is attacked with a needle.”\n\n“But yet, M. Danglars appeared——”\n\n“Delighted with you, was he not? Well, he is a man of bad taste, and is still more enchanted with another. I know not whom; look and judge for yourself.”\n\n“Thank you, I understand. But my mother—no, not my mother; I mistake—my father intends giving a ball.”\n\n“A ball at this season?”\n\n“Summer balls are fashionable.”\n\n“If they were not, the countess has only to wish it, and they would become so.”\n\n“You are right; You know they are select affairs; those who remain in Paris in July must be true Parisians. Will you take charge of our invitation to Messieurs Cavalcanti?”\n\n“When will it take place?”\n\n“On Saturday.”\n\n“M. Cavalcanti’s father will be gone.”\n\n“But the son will be here; will you invite young M. Cavalcanti?”\n\n“I do not know him, viscount.”\n\n“You do not know him?”\n\n“No, I never saw him until a few days since, and am not responsible for him.”\n\n“But you receive him at your house?”\n\n“That is another thing: he was recommended to me by a good abbé, who may be deceived. Give him a direct invitation, but do not ask me to present him. If he were afterwards to marry Mademoiselle Danglars, you would accuse me of intrigue, and would be challenging me,—besides, I may not be there myself.”\n\n“Where?”\n“At your ball.”\n\n“Why should you not be there?”\n\n“Because you have not yet invited me.”\n\n“But I come expressly for that purpose.”\n\n“You are very kind, but I may be prevented.”\n\n“If I tell you one thing, you will be so amiable as to set aside all impediments.”\n\n“Tell me what it is.”\n\n“My mother begs you to come.”\n\n“The Comtesse de Morcerf?” said Monte Cristo, starting.\n\n“Ah, count,” said Albert, “I assure you Madame de Morcerf speaks freely to me, and if you have not felt those sympathetic fibres of which I spoke just now thrill within you, you must be entirely devoid of them, for during the last four days we have spoken of no one else.”\n\n“You have talked of me?”\n\n“Yes, that is the penalty of being a living puzzle!”\n\n“Then I am also a puzzle to your mother? I should have thought her too reasonable to be led by imagination.”\n\n“A problem, my dear count, for everyone—for my mother as well as others; much studied, but not solved, you still remain an enigma, do not fear. My mother is only astonished that you remain so long unsolved. I believe, while the Countess G—— takes you for Lord Ruthven, my mother imagines you to be Cagliostro or the Count Saint-Germain. The first opportunity you have, confirm her in her opinion; it will be easy for you, as you have the philosophy of the one and the wit of the other.”\n\n“I thank you for the warning,” said the count; “I shall endeavor to be prepared for all suppositions.”\n\n“You will, then, come on Saturday?”\n\n“Yes, since Madame de Morcerf invites me.”\n\n“You are very kind.”\n\n“Will M. Danglars be there?”\n\n“He has already been invited by my father. We shall try to persuade the great d’Aguesseau,11 M. de Villefort, to come, but have not much hope of seeing him.”\n\n“‘Never despair of anything,’ says the proverb.”\n\n“Do you dance, count?”\n\n“I dance?”\n\n“Yes, you; it would not be astonishing.”\n\n“That is very well before one is over forty. No, I do not dance, but I like to see others do so. Does Madame de Morcerf dance?”\n\n“Never; you can talk to her, she so delights in your conversation.”\n\n“Indeed?”\n30283m\n\n“Yes, truly; and I assure you. You are the only man of whom I have heard her speak with interest.” Albert rose and took his hat; the count conducted him to the door.\n\n“I have one thing to reproach myself with,” said he, stopping Albert on the steps. “What is it?”\n\n“I have spoken to you indiscreetly about Danglars.”\n\n“On the contrary, speak to me always in the same strain about him.”\n\n“I am glad to be reassured on that point. Apropos, when do you aspect M. d’Épinay?”\n\n“Five or six days hence at the latest.”\n\n“And when is he to be married?”\n\n“Immediately on the arrival of M. and Madame de Saint-Méran.”\n\n“Bring him to see me. Although you say I do not like him, I assure you I shall be happy to see him.”\n\n“I will obey your orders, my lord.”\n\n“Until Saturday, when I may expect you, may I not?”\n\n“Yes, I promised you.” The Count watched Albert, waving his hand to him. When he had mounted his phaeton, Monte Cristo turned, and seeing Bertuccio, “What news?” said he.\n\n“She went to the Palais,” replied the steward.\n\n“Did she stay long there?”\n\n“An hour and a half.”\n\n“Did she return home?”\n\n“Directly.”\n“Well, my dear Bertuccio,” said the count, “I now advise you to go in quest of the little estate I spoke to you of in Normandy.”\n\nBertuccio bowed, and as his wishes were in perfect harmony with the order he had received, he started the same evening.\n\n# Chapter 69. The Inquiry\n\nM. de Villefort kept the promise he had made to Madame Danglars, to endeavor to find out how the Count of Monte Cristo had discovered the history of the house at Auteuil. He wrote the same day for the required information to M. de Boville, who, from having been an inspector of prisons, was promoted to a high office in the police; and the latter begged for two days time to ascertain exactly who would be most likely to give him full particulars. At the end of the second day M. de Villefort received the following note:\n\n“The person called the Count of Monte Cristo is an intimate acquaintance of Lord Wilmore, a rich foreigner, who is sometimes seen in Paris and who is there at this moment; he is also known to the Abbé Busoni, a Sicilian priest, of high repute in the East, where he has done much good.”\n\nM. de Villefort replied by ordering the strictest inquiries to be made respecting these two persons; his orders were executed, and the following evening he received these details:\n\n“The abbé, who was in Paris only for a month, inhabited a small two-storied house behind Saint-Sulpice; there were two rooms on each floor and he was the only tenant. The two lower rooms consisted of a dining-room, with a table, chairs, and side-board of walnut, and a wainscoted parlor, without ornaments, carpet, or timepiece. It was evident that the abbé limited himself to objects of strict necessity. He preferred to use the sitting-room upstairs, which was more library than parlor, and was furnished with theological books and parchments, in which he delighted to bury himself for months at a time, according to his valet de chambre. His valet looked at the visitors through a sort of wicket; and if their faces were unknown to him or displeased him, he replied that the abbé was not in Paris, an answer which satisfied most persons, because the abbé was known to be a great traveller. Besides, whether at home or not, whether in Paris or Cairo, the abbé always left something to give away, which the valet distributed through this wicket in his master’s name. The other room near the library was a bedroom. A bed without curtains, four armchairs, and a couch, covered with yellow Utrecht velvet, composed, with a _prie-Dieu_, all its furniture.\n\n“Lord Wilmore resided in Rue Fontaine-Saint-Georges. He was one of those English tourists who consume a large fortune in travelling. He hired the apartment in which he lived furnished, passed only a few hours in the day there, and rarely slept there. One of his peculiarities was never to speak a word of French, which he however wrote with great facility.”\n\nThe day after this important information had been given to the king’s attorney, a man alighted from a carriage at the corner of the Rue Férou, and rapping at an olive-green door, asked if the Abbé Busoni were within.\n\n“No, he went out early this morning,” replied the valet.\n\n“I might not always be content with that answer,” replied the visitor, “for I come from one to whom everyone must be at home. But have the kindness to give the Abbé Busoni——”\n\n“I told you he was not at home,” repeated the valet.\n\n“Then on his return give him that card and this sealed paper. Will he be at home at eight o’clock this evening?”\n\n“Doubtless, unless he is at work, which is the same as if he were out.”\n\n“I will come again at that time,” replied the visitor, who then retired.\n\nAt the appointed hour the same man returned in the same carriage, which, instead of stopping this time at the end of the Rue Férou, drove up to the green door. He knocked, and it opened immediately to admit him. From the signs of respect the valet paid him, he saw that his note had produced a good effect.\n\n“Is the abbé at home?” asked he.\n\n“Yes; he is at work in his library, but he expects you, sir,” replied the valet. The stranger ascended a rough staircase, and before a table, illumined by a lamp whose light was concentrated by a large shade while the rest of the apartment was in partial darkness, he perceived the abbé in a monk’s dress, with a cowl on his head such as was used by learned men of the Middle Ages.\n\n“Have I the honor of addressing the Abbé Busoni?” asked the visitor.\n\n“Yes, sir,” replied the abbé; “and you are the person whom M. de Boville, formerly an inspector of prisons, sends to me from the prefect of police?”\n\n“Exactly, sir.”\n\n“One of the agents appointed to secure the safety of Paris?”\n\n“Yes, sir” replied the stranger with a slight hesitation, and blushing.\n\nThe abbé replaced the large spectacles, which covered not only his eyes but his temples, and sitting down motioned to his visitor to do the same. “I am at your service, sir,” said the abbé, with a marked Italian accent.\n\n“The mission with which I am charged, sir,” replied the visitor, speaking with hesitation, “is a confidential one on the part of him who fulfils it, and him by whom he is employed.” The abbé bowed. “Your probity,” replied the stranger, “is so well known to the prefect that he wishes as a magistrate to ascertain from you some particulars connected with the public safety, to ascertain which I am deputed to see you. It is hoped that no ties of friendship or humane consideration will induce you to conceal the truth.”\n\n“Provided, sir, the particulars you wish for do not interfere with my scruples or my conscience. I am a priest, sir, and the secrets of confession, for instance, must remain between me and God, and not between me and human justice.”\n\n“Do not alarm yourself, monsieur, we will duly respect your conscience.”\n\nAt this moment the abbé pressed down his side of the shade and so raised it on the other, throwing a bright light on the stranger’s face, while his own remained obscured.\n\n“Excuse me, abbé,” said the envoy of the prefect of the police, “but the light tries my eyes very much.” The abbé lowered the shade.\n\n“Now, sir, I am listening—go on.”\n\n“I will come at once to the point. Do you know the Count of Monte Cristo?”\n\n“You mean Monsieur Zaccone, I presume?”\n\n“Zaccone?—is not his name Monte Cristo?”\n\n“Monte Cristo is the name of an estate, or, rather, of a rock, and not a family name.”\n\n“Well, be it so—let us not dispute about words; and since M. de Monte Cristo and M. Zaccone are the same——”\n\n“Absolutely the same.”\n\n“Let us speak of M. Zaccone.”\n\n“Agreed.”\n\n“I asked you if you knew him?”\n\n“Extremely well.”\n\n“Who is he?”\n\n“The son of a rich shipbuilder in Malta.”\n\n“I know that is the report; but, as you are aware, the police does not content itself with vague reports.”\n\n“However,” replied the abbé, with an affable smile, “when that report is in accordance with the truth, everybody must believe it, the police as well as all the rest.”\n\n“Are you sure of what you assert?”\n\n“What do you mean by that question?”\n\n“Understand, sir, I do not in the least suspect your veracity; I ask if you are certain of it?”\n\n“I knew his father, M. Zaccone.”\n\n“Ah, indeed?”\n\n“And when a child I often played with the son in the timber-yards.”\n\n“But whence does he derive the title of count?”\n\n“You are aware that may be bought.”\n“In Italy?”\n\n“Everywhere.”\n\n“And his immense riches, whence does he procure them?”\n\n“They may not be so very great.”\n\n“How much do you suppose he possesses?”\n\n“From one hundred and fifty to two hundred thousand livres per annum.”\n\n“That is reasonable,” said the visitor; “I have heard he had three or four millions.”\n\n“Two hundred thousand per annum would make four millions of capital.”\n\n“But I was told he had four millions per annum.”\n\n“That is not probable.”\n\n“Do you know this Island of Monte Cristo?”\n\n“Certainly, everyone who has come from Palermo, Naples, or Rome to France by sea must know it, since he has passed close to it and must have seen it.”\n\n“I am told it is a delightful place?”\n\n“It is a rock.”\n\n“And why has the count bought a rock?”\n\n“For the sake of being a count. In Italy one must have territorial possessions to be a count.”\n\n“You have, doubtless, heard the adventures of M. Zaccone’s youth?”\n\n“The father’s?”\n\n“No, the son’s.”\n\n“I know nothing certain; at that period of his life, I lost sight of my young comrade.”\n\n“Was he in the wars?”\n\n“I think he entered the service.”\n\n“In what branch?”\n\n“In the navy.”\n\n“Are you not his confessor?”\n\n“No, sir; I believe he is a Lutheran.”\n\n“A Lutheran?”\n\n“I say, I believe such is the case, I do not affirm it; besides, liberty of conscience is established in France.”\n\n“Doubtless, and we are not now inquiring into his creed, but his actions; in the name of the prefect of police, I ask you what you know of him.\n\n“He passes for a very charitable man. Our holy father, the pope, has made him a knight of Jesus Christ for the services he rendered to the Christians in the East; he has five or six rings as testimonials from Eastern monarchs of his services.”\n\n“Does he wear them?”\n\n“No, but he is proud of them; he is better pleased with rewards given to the benefactors of man than to his destroyers.”\n\n“He is a Quaker then?”\n\n“Exactly, he is a Quaker, with the exception of the peculiar dress.”\n\n“Has he any friends?”\n\n“Yes, everyone who knows him is his friend.”\n\n“But has he any enemies?”\n\n“One only.”\n\n“What is his name?”\n\n“Lord Wilmore.”\n\n“Where is he?”\n\n“He is in Paris just now.”\n\n“Can he give me any particulars?”\n\n“Important ones; he was in India with Zaccone.”\n\n“Do you know his abode?”\n\n“It’s somewhere in the Chaussée d’Antin; but I know neither the street nor the number.”\n30289m\n\n“Are you at variance with the Englishman?”\n\n“I love Zaccone, and he hates him; we are consequently not friends.”\n\n“Do you think the Count of Monte Cristo had ever been in France before he made this visit to Paris?”\n\n“To that question I can answer positively; no, sir, he had not, because he applied to me six months ago for the particulars he required, and as I did not know when I might again come to Paris, I recommended M. Cavalcanti to him.”\n\n“Andrea?”\n“No, Bartolomeo, his father.”\n\n“Now, sir, I have but one question more to ask, and I charge you, in the name of honor, of humanity, and of religion, to answer me candidly.”\n\n“What is it, sir?”\n\n“Do you know with what design M. de Monte Cristo purchased a house at Auteuil?”\n\n“Certainly, for he told me.”\n\n“What is it, sir?”\n\n“To make a lunatic asylum of it, similar to that founded by the Count of Pisani at Palermo. Do you know about that institution?”\n\n“I have heard of it.”\n\n“It is a magnificent charity.” Having said this, the abbé bowed to imply he wished to pursue his studies.\n\nThe visitor either understood the abbé’s meaning, or had no more questions to ask; he arose, and the abbé accompanied him to the door.\n\n“You are a great almsgiver,” said the visitor, “and although you are said to be rich, I will venture to offer you something for your poor people; will you accept my offering?”\n\n“I thank you, sir; I am only jealous in one thing, and that is that the relief I give should be entirely from my own resources.”\n\n“However——”\n\n“My resolution, sir, is unchangeable, but you have only to search for yourself and you will find, alas, but too many objects upon whom to exercise your benevolence.”\n\nThe abbé once more bowed as he opened the door, the stranger bowed and took his leave, and the carriage conveyed him straight to the house of M. de Villefort. An hour afterwards the carriage was again ordered, and this time it went to the Rue Fontaine-Saint-Georges, and stopped at No. 5, where Lord Wilmore lived. The stranger had written to Lord Wilmore, requesting an interview, which the latter had fixed for ten o’clock. As the envoy of the prefect of police arrived ten minutes before ten, he was told that Lord Wilmore, who was precision and punctuality personified, was not yet come in, but that he would be sure to return as the clock struck.\n\nThe visitor was introduced into the drawing-room, which was like all other furnished drawing-rooms. A mantle-piece, with two modern Sèvres vases, a timepiece representing Cupid with his bent bow, a mirror with an engraving on each side—one representing Homer carrying his guide, the other, Belisarius begging—a grayish paper; red and black tapestry—such was the appearance of Lord Wilmore’s drawing-room.\n\nIt was illuminated by lamps with ground-glass shades which gave only a feeble light, as if out of consideration for the envoy’s weak sight. After ten minutes’ expectation the clock struck ten; at the fifth stroke the door opened and Lord Wilmore appeared. He was rather above the middle height, with thin reddish whiskers, light complexion and light hair, turning rather gray. He was dressed with all the English peculiarity, namely, in a blue coat, with gilt buttons and high collar, in the fashion of 1811, a white kerseymere waistcoat, and nankeen pantaloons, three inches too short, but which were prevented by straps from slipping up to the knee. His first remark on entering was:\n\n“You know, sir, I do not speak French?”\n\n“I know you do not like to converse in our language,” replied the envoy.\n\n“But you may use it,” replied Lord Wilmore; “I understand it.”\n\n“And I,” replied the visitor, changing his idiom, “know enough of English to keep up the conversation. Do not put yourself to the slightest inconvenience.”\n\n“Aw?” said Lord Wilmore, with that tone which is only known to natives of Great Britain.\n\nThe envoy presented his letter of introduction, which the latter read with English coolness, and having finished:\n\n“I understand,” said he, “perfectly.”\n30293m\n\nThen began the questions, which were similar to those which had been addressed to the Abbé Busoni. But as Lord Wilmore, in the character of the count’s enemy, was less restrained in his answers, they were more numerous; he described the youth of Monte Cristo, who he said, at ten years of age, entered the service of one of the petty sovereigns of India who make war on the English. It was there Wilmore had first met him and fought against him; and in that war Zaccone had been taken prisoner, sent to England, and consigned to the hulks, whence he had escaped by swimming. Then began his travels, his duels, his caprices; then the insurrection in Greece broke out, and he had served in the Grecian ranks. While in that service he had discovered a silver mine in the mountains of Thessaly, but he had been careful to conceal it from everyone. After the battle of Navarino, when the Greek government was consolidated, he asked of King Otho a mining grant for that district, which was given him. Hence that immense fortune, which, in Lord Wilmore’s opinion, possibly amounted to one or two millions per annum,—a precarious fortune, which might be momentarily lost by the failure of the mine.\n\n“But,” asked the visitor, “do you know why he came to France?”\n\n“He is speculating in railways,” said Lord Wilmore, “and as he is an expert chemist and physicist, he has invented a new system of telegraphy, which he is seeking to bring to perfection.”\n\n“How much does he spend yearly?” asked the prefect.\n\n“Not more than five or six hundred thousand francs,” said Lord Wilmore; “he is a miser.” Hatred evidently inspired the Englishman, who, knowing no other reproach to bring on the count, accused him of avarice.\n\n“Do you know his house at Auteuil?”\n\n“Certainly.”\n\n“What do you know respecting it?”\n\n“Do you wish to know why he bought it?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“The count is a speculator, who will certainly ruin himself in experiments. He supposes there is in the neighborhood of the house he has bought a mineral spring equal to those at Bagnères, Luchon, and Cauterets. He is going to turn his house into a _Badhaus_, as the Germans term it. He has already dug up all the garden two or three times to find the famous spring, and, being unsuccessful, he will soon purchase all the contiguous houses. Now, as I dislike him, and hope his railway, his electric telegraph, or his search for baths, will ruin him, I am watching for his discomfiture, which must soon take place.”\n\n“What was the cause of your quarrel?”\n\n“When he was in England he seduced the wife of one of my friends.”\n\n“Why do you not seek revenge?”\n\n“I have already fought three duels with him,” said the Englishman, “the first with the pistol, the second with the sword, and the third with the sabre.”\n\n“And what was the result of those duels?”\n\n“The first time, he broke my arm; the second, he wounded me in the breast; and the third time, made this large wound.” The Englishman turned down his shirt-collar, and showed a scar, whose redness proved it to be a recent one. “So that, you see, there is a deadly feud between us.”\n\n“But,” said the envoy, “you do not go about it in the right way to kill him, if I understand you correctly.”\n\n“Aw?” said the Englishman, “I practice shooting every day, and every other day Grisier comes to my house.”\n\nThis was all the visitor wished to ascertain, or, rather, all the Englishman appeared to know. The agent arose, and having bowed to Lord Wilmore, who returned his salutation with the stiff politeness of the English, he retired. Lord Wilmore, having heard the door close after him, returned to his bedroom, where with one hand he pulled off his light hair, his red whiskers, his false jaw, and his wound, to resume the black hair, dark complexion, and pearly teeth of the Count of Monte Cristo.\n\nIt was M. de Villefort, and not the prefect, who returned to the house of M. de Villefort. The procureur felt more at ease, although he had learned nothing really satisfactory, and, for the first time since the dinner-party at Auteuil, he slept soundly.\n\n# Chapter 70. The Ball\n\nIt was in the warmest days of July, when in due course of time the Saturday arrived upon which the ball was to take place at M. de Morcerf’s. It was ten o’clock at night; the branches of the great trees in the garden of the count’s house stood out boldly against the azure canopy of heaven, which was studded with golden stars, but where the last fleeting clouds of a vanishing storm yet lingered.\n\nFrom the apartments on the ground floor might be heard the sound of music, with the whirl of the waltz and galop, while brilliant streams of light shone through the openings of the Venetian blinds. At this moment the garden was only occupied by about ten servants, who had just received orders from their mistress to prepare the supper, the serenity of the weather continuing to increase. Until now, it had been undecided whether the supper should take place in the dining-room, or under a long tent erected on the lawn, but the beautiful blue sky, studded with stars, had settled the question in favor of the lawn.\n\nThe gardens were illuminated with colored lanterns, according to the Italian custom, and, as is usual in countries where the luxuries of the table—the rarest of all luxuries in their complete form—are well understood, the supper-table was loaded with wax-lights and flowers.\n\n30297m\nAt the time the Countess of Morcerf returned to the rooms, after giving her orders, many guests were arriving, more attracted by the charming hospitality of the countess than by the distinguished position of the count; for, owing to the good taste of Mercédès, one was sure of finding some devices at her entertainment worthy of describing, or even copying in case of need.\n\nMadame Danglars, in whom the events we have related had caused deep anxiety, had hesitated about going to Madame de Morcerf’s, when during the morning her carriage happened to meet that of Villefort. The latter made a sign, and when the carriages had drawn close together, said:\n\n“You are going to Madame de Morcerf’s, are you not?”\n\n“No,” replied Madame Danglars, “I am too ill.”\n\n“You are wrong,” replied Villefort, significantly; “it is important that you should be seen there.”\n\n“Do you think so?” asked the baroness.\n\n“I do.”\n\n“In that case I will go.”\n\nAnd the two carriages passed on towards their different destinations. Madame Danglars therefore came, not only beautiful in person, but radiant with splendor; she entered by one door at the time when Mercédès appeared at the door. The countess took Albert to meet Madame Danglars. He approached, paid her some well merited compliments on her toilet, and offered his arm to conduct her to a seat. Albert looked around him.\n\n“You are looking for my daughter?” said the baroness, smiling.\n\n“I confess it,” replied Albert. “Could you have been so cruel as not to bring her?”\n\n“Calm yourself. She has met Mademoiselle de Villefort, and has taken her arm; see, they are following us, both in white dresses, one with a bouquet of camellias, the other with one of myosotis. But tell me——”\n\n“Well, what do you wish to know?”\n\n“Will not the Count of Monte Cristo be here tonight?”\n\n“Seventeen!” replied Albert.\n\n“What do you mean?”\n\n“I only mean that the count seems the rage,” replied the viscount, smiling, “and that you are the seventeenth person that has asked me the same question. The count is in fashion; I congratulate him upon it.”\n\n“And have you replied to everyone as you have to me?”\n\n“Ah, to be sure, I have not answered you; be satisfied, we shall have this ‘lion’; we are among the privileged ones.”\n\n“Were you at the Opera yesterday?”\n\n“No.”\n“He was there.”\n\n“Ah, indeed? And did the eccentric person commit any new originality?”\n\n“Can he be seen without doing so? Elssler was dancing in _Le Diable boiteux_; the Greek princess was in ecstasies. After the cachucha he placed a magnificent ring on the stem of a bouquet, and threw it to the charming danseuse, who, in the third act, to do honor to the gift, reappeared with it on her finger. And the Greek princess,—will she be here?”\n\n“No, you will be deprived of that pleasure; her position in the count’s establishment is not sufficiently understood.”\n\n“Wait; leave me here, and go and speak to Madame de Villefort, who is trying to attract your attention.”\n\nAlbert bowed to Madame Danglars, and advanced towards Madame de Villefort, whose lips opened as he approached.\n\n“I wager anything,” said Albert, interrupting her, “that I know what you were about to say.”\n\n“Well, what is it?”\n\n“If I guess rightly, will you confess it?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“On your honor?”\n\n“On my honor.”\n\n“You were going to ask me if the Count of Monte Cristo had arrived, or was expected.”\n\n“Not at all. It is not of him that I am now thinking. I was going to ask you if you had received any news of Monsieur Franz.”\n\n“Yes,—yesterday.”\n\n“What did he tell you?”\n\n“That he was leaving at the same time as his letter.”\n\n“Well, now then, the count?”\n\n“The count will come, of that you may be satisfied.”\n\n“You know that he has another name besides Monte Cristo?”\n\n“No, I did not know it.”\n\n“Monte Cristo is the name of an island, and he has a family name.”\n\n“I never heard it.”\n\n“Well, then, I am better informed than you; his name is Zaccone.”\n\n“It is possible.”\n\n“He is a Maltese.”\n\n“That is also possible.\n\n“The son of a shipowner.”\n\n“Really, you should relate all this aloud, you would have the greatest success.”\n\n“He served in India, discovered a mine in Thessaly, and comes to Paris to establish a mineral water-cure at Auteuil.”\n\n“Well, I’m sure,” said Morcerf, “this is indeed news! Am I allowed to repeat it?”\n\n“Yes, but cautiously, tell one thing at a time, and do not say I told you.”\n“Why so?”\n\n“Because it is a secret just discovered.”\n\n“By whom?”\n\n“The police.”\n\n“Then the news originated——”\n\n“At the prefect’s last night. Paris, you can understand, is astonished at the sight of such unusual splendor, and the police have made inquiries.”\n\n“Well, well! Nothing more is wanting than to arrest the count as a vagabond, on the pretext of his being too rich.”\n\n“Indeed, that doubtless would have happened if his credentials had not been so favorable.”\n\n“Poor count! And is he aware of the danger he has been in?”\n\n“I think not.”\n\n“Then it will be but charitable to inform him. When he arrives, I will not fail to do so.”\n\nJust then, a handsome young man, with bright eyes, black hair, and glossy moustache, respectfully bowed to Madame de Villefort. Albert extended his hand.\n\n“Madame,” said Albert, “allow me to present to you M. Maximilian Morrel, captain of Spahis, one of our best, and, above all, of our bravest officers.”\n\n“I have already had the pleasure of meeting this gentleman at Auteuil, at the house of the Count of Monte Cristo,” replied Madame de Villefort, turning away with marked coldness of manner.\n\nThis answer, and especially the tone in which it was uttered, chilled the heart of poor Morrel. But a recompense was in store for him; turning around, he saw near the door a beautiful fair face, whose large blue eyes were, without any marked expression, fixed upon him, while the bouquet of myosotis was gently raised to her lips.\n\nThe salutation was so well understood that Morrel, with the same expression in his eyes, placed his handkerchief to his mouth; and these two living statues, whose hearts beat so violently under their marble aspect, separated from each other by the whole length of the room, forgot themselves for a moment, or rather forgot the world in their mutual contemplation. They might have remained much longer lost in one another, without anyone noticing their abstraction. The Count of Monte Cristo had just entered.\n\nWe have already said that there was something in the count which attracted universal attention wherever he appeared. It was not the coat, unexceptional in its cut, though simple and unornamented; it was not the plain white waistcoat; it was not the trousers, that displayed the foot so perfectly formed—it was none of these things that attracted the attention,—it was his pale complexion, his waving black hair, his calm and serene expression, his dark and melancholy eye, his mouth, chiselled with such marvellous delicacy, which so easily expressed such high disdain,—these were what fixed the attention of all upon him.\n\nMany men might have been handsomer, but certainly there could be none whose appearance was more _significant_, if the expression may be used. Everything about the count seemed to have its meaning, for the constant habit of thought which he had acquired had given an ease and vigor to the expression of his face, and even to the most trifling gesture, scarcely to be understood. Yet the Parisian world is so strange, that even all this might not have won attention had there not been connected with it a mysterious story gilded by an immense fortune.\n\n30301m\nMeanwhile he advanced through the assemblage of guests under a battery of curious glances towards Madame de Morcerf, who, standing before a mantle-piece ornamented with flowers, had seen his entrance in a looking-glass placed opposite the door, and was prepared to receive him. She turned towards him with a serene smile just at the moment he was bowing to her. No doubt she fancied the count would speak to her, while on his side the count thought she was about to address him; but both remained silent, and after a mere bow, Monte Cristo directed his steps to Albert, who received him cordially.\n\n“Have you seen my mother?” asked Albert.\n\n“I have just had the pleasure,” replied the count; “but I have not seen your father.”\n\n“See, he is down there, talking politics with that little group of great geniuses.”\n\n“Indeed?” said Monte Cristo; “and so those gentlemen down there are men of great talent. I should not have guessed it. And for what kind of talent are they celebrated? You know there are different sorts.”\n\n“That tall, harsh-looking man is very learned, he discovered, in the neighborhood of Rome, a kind of lizard with a vertebra more than lizards usually have, and he immediately laid his discovery before the Institute. The thing was discussed for a long time, but finally decided in his favor. I can assure you the vertebra made a great noise in the learned world, and the gentleman, who was only a knight of the Legion of Honor, was made an officer.”\n\n“Come,” said Monte Cristo, “this cross seems to me to be wisely awarded. I suppose, had he found another additional vertebra, they would have made him a commander.”\n\n“Very likely,” said Albert.\n\n“And who can that person be who has taken it into his head to wrap himself up in a blue coat embroidered with green?”\n\n“Oh, that coat is not his own idea; it is the Republic’s, which deputed David12 to devise a uniform for the Academicians.”\n\n“Indeed?” said Monte Cristo; “so this gentleman is an Academician?”\n\n“Within the last week he has been made one of the learned assembly.”\n\n“And what is his especial talent?”\n\n“His talent? I believe he thrusts pins through the heads of rabbits, he makes fowls eat madder, and punches the spinal marrow out of dogs with whalebone.”\n\n“And he is made a member of the Academy of Sciences for this?”\n\n“No; of the French Academy.”\n\n“But what has the French Academy to do with all this?”\n\n“I was going to tell you. It seems——”\n\n“That his experiments have very considerably advanced the cause of science, doubtless?”\n\n“No; that his style of writing is very good.”\n\n“This must be very flattering to the feelings of the rabbits into whose heads he has thrust pins, to the fowls whose bones he has dyed red, and to the dogs whose spinal marrow he has punched out?”\n\nAlbert laughed.\n\n“And the other one?” demanded the count.\n\n“That one?”\n\n“Yes, the third.”\n\n“The one in the dark blue coat?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“He is a colleague of the count, and one of the most active opponents to the idea of providing the Chamber of Peers with a uniform. He was very successful upon that question. He stood badly with the Liberal papers, but his noble opposition to the wishes of the court is now getting him into favor with the journalists. They talk of making him an ambassador.”\n30303m\n\n“And what are his claims to the peerage?”\n\n“He has composed two or three comic operas, written four or five articles in the _Siècle_, and voted five or six years on the ministerial side.”\n\n“Bravo, viscount,” said Monte Cristo, smiling; “you are a delightful _cicerone_. And now you will do me a favor, will you not?”\n\n“What is it?”\n\n“Do not introduce me to any of these gentlemen; and should they wish it, you will warn me.” Just then the count felt his arm pressed. He turned round; it was Danglars.\n\n“Ah! is it you, baron?” said he.\n\n“Why do you call me baron?” said Danglars; “you know that I care nothing for my title. I am not like you, viscount; you like your title, do you not?”\n\n“Certainly,” replied Albert, “seeing that without my title I should be nothing; while you, sacrificing the baron, would still remain the millionaire.”\n\n“Which seems to me the finest title under the royalty of July,” replied Danglars.\n\n“Unfortunately,” said Monte Cristo, “one’s title to a millionaire does not last for life, like that of baron, peer of France, or academician; for example, the millionaires Franck & Poulmann, of Frankfurt, who have just become bankrupts.”\n\n“Indeed?” said Danglars, becoming pale.\n\n“Yes; I received the news this evening by a courier. I had about a million in their hands, but, warned in time, I withdrew it a month ago.”\n\n“Ah, _mon Dieu!_” exclaimed Danglars, “they have drawn on me for 200,000 francs!”\n\n“Well, you can throw out the draft; their signature is worth five per cent.”\n\n“Yes, but it is too late,” said Danglars, “I have honored their bills.”\n\n“Then,” said Monte Cristo, “here are 200,000 francs gone after——”\n\n“Hush, do not mention these things,” said Danglars; then, approaching Monte Cristo, he added, “especially before young M. Cavalcanti;” after which he smiled, and turned towards the young man in question.\n\nAlbert had left the count to speak to his mother, Danglars to converse with young Cavalcanti; Monte Cristo was for an instant alone. Meanwhile the heat became excessive. The footmen were hastening through the rooms with waiters loaded with ices. Monte Cristo wiped the perspiration from his forehead, but drew back when the waiter was presented to him; he took no refreshment. Madame de Morcerf did not lose sight of Monte Cristo; she saw that he took nothing, and even noticed his gesture of refusal.\n\n“Albert,” she asked, “did you notice that?”\n\n“What, mother?”\n\n“That the count has never been willing to partake of food under the roof of M. de Morcerf.”\n\n“Yes; but then he breakfasted with me—indeed, he made his first appearance in the world on that occasion.”\n\n“But your house is not M. de Morcerf’s,” murmured Mercédès; “and since he has been here I have watched him.”\n\n“Well?”\n“Well, he has taken nothing yet.”\n\n“The count is very temperate.”\n\nMercédès smiled sadly.\n\n“Approach him,” said she, “and when the next waiter passes, insist upon his taking something.”\n\n“But why, mother?”\n\n“Just to please me, Albert,” said Mercédès. Albert kissed his mother’s hand, and drew near the count. Another salver passed, loaded like the preceding ones; she saw Albert attempt to persuade the count, but he obstinately refused. Albert rejoined his mother; she was very pale.\n\n“Well,” said she, “you see he refuses?”\n\n“Yes; but why need this annoy you?”\n\n“You know, Albert, women are singular creatures. I should like to have seen the count take something in my house, if only an ice. Perhaps he cannot reconcile himself to the French style of living, and might prefer something else.”\n\n“Oh, no; I have seen him eat of everything in Italy; no doubt he does not feel inclined this evening.”\n\n“And besides,” said the countess, “accustomed as he is to burning climates, possibly he does not feel the heat as we do.”\n\n“I do not think that, for he has complained of feeling almost suffocated, and asked why the Venetian blinds were not opened as well as the windows.”\n\n“In a word,” said Mercédès, “it was a way of assuring me that his abstinence was intended.”\n\nAnd she left the room.\n\nA minute afterwards the blinds were thrown open, and through the jessamine and clematis that overhung the window one could see the garden ornamented with lanterns, and the supper laid under the tent. Dancers, players, talkers, all uttered an exclamation of joy—everyone inhaled with delight the breeze that floated in. At the same time Mercédès reappeared, paler than before, but with that imperturbable expression of countenance which she sometimes wore. She went straight to the group of which her husband formed the centre.\n\n“Do not detain those gentlemen here, count,” she said; “they would prefer, I should think, to breathe in the garden rather than suffocate here, since they are not playing.”\n\n“Ah,” said a gallant old general, who, in 1809, had sung _Partant pour la Syrie_,—“we will not go alone to the garden.”\n\n“Then,” said Mercédès, “I will lead the way.”\n\nTurning towards Monte Cristo, she added, “count, will you oblige me with your arm?”\n\nThe count almost staggered at these simple words; then he fixed his eyes on Mercédès. It was only a momentary glance, but it seemed to the countess to have lasted for a century, so much was expressed in that one look. He offered his arm to the countess; she took it, or rather just touched it with her little hand, and they together descended the steps, lined with rhododendrons and camellias. Behind them, by another outlet, a group of about twenty persons rushed into the garden with loud exclamations of delight.\n\n# Chapter 71. Bread and Salt\n\nMadame de Morcerf entered an archway of trees with her companion. It led through a grove of lindens to a conservatory.\n\n“It was too warm in the room, was it not, count?” she asked.\n\n“Yes, madame; and it was an excellent idea of yours to open the doors and the blinds.” As he ceased speaking, the count felt the hand of Mercédès tremble. “But you,” he said, “with that light dress, and without anything to cover you but that gauze scarf, perhaps you feel cold?”\n\n“Do you know where I am leading you?” said the countess, without replying to the question.\n\n“No, madame,” replied Monte Cristo; “but you see I make no resistance.”\n\n“We are going to the greenhouse that you see at the other end of the grove.”\n\nThe count looked at Mercédès as if to interrogate her, but she continued to walk on in silence, and he refrained from speaking. They reached the building, ornamented with magnificent fruits, which ripen at the beginning of July in the artificial temperature which takes the place of the sun, so frequently absent in our climate. The countess left the arm of Monte Cristo, and gathered a bunch of Muscatel grapes.\n\n“See, count,” she said, with a smile so sad in its expression that one could almost detect the tears on her eyelids—“see, our French grapes are not to be compared, I know, with yours of Sicily and Cyprus, but you will make allowance for our northern sun.” The count bowed, but stepped back.\n\n“Do you refuse?” said Mercédès, in a tremulous voice.\n\n“Pray excuse me, madame,” replied Monte Cristo, “but I never eat Muscatel grapes.”\n\nMercédès let them fall, and sighed. A magnificent peach was hanging against an adjoining wall, ripened by the same artificial heat. Mercédès drew near, and plucked the fruit.\n\n“Take this peach, then,” she said. The count again refused. “What, again?” she exclaimed, in so plaintive an accent that it seemed to stifle a sob; “really, you pain me.”\n\nA long silence followed; the peach, like the grapes, fell to the ground.\n\n“Count,” added Mercédès with a supplicating glance, “there is a beautiful Arabian custom, which makes eternal friends of those who have together eaten bread and salt under the same roof.”\n\n“I know it, madame,” replied the count; “but we are in France, and not in Arabia, and in France eternal friendships are as rare as the custom of dividing bread and salt with one another.”\n\n“But,” said the countess, breathlessly, with her eyes fixed on Monte Cristo, whose arm she convulsively pressed with both hands, “we are friends, are we not?”\n\nThe count became pale as death, the blood rushed to his heart, and then again rising, dyed his cheeks with crimson; his eyes swam like those of a man suddenly dazzled.\n\n“Certainly, we are friends,” he replied; “why should we not be?”\n\nThe answer was so little like the one Mercédès desired, that she turned away to give vent to a sigh, which sounded more like a groan. “Thank you,” she said. And they walked on again. They went the whole length of the garden without uttering a word.\n\n“Sir,” suddenly exclaimed the countess, after their walk had continued ten minutes in silence, “is it true that you have seen so much, travelled so far, and suffered so deeply?”\n\n“I have suffered deeply, madame,” answered Monte Cristo.\n\n“But now you are happy?”\n\n“Doubtless,” replied the count, “since no one hears me complain.”\n\n“And your present happiness, has it softened your heart?”\n\n“My present happiness equals my past misery,” said the count.\n\n“Are you not married?” asked the countess.\n\n“I, married?” exclaimed Monte Cristo, shuddering; “who could have told you so?”\n\n“No one told me you were, but you have frequently been seen at the Opera with a young and lovely woman.”\n\n“She is a slave whom I bought at Constantinople, madame, the daughter of a prince. I have adopted her as my daughter, having no one else to love in the world.”\n\n“You live alone, then?”\n\n“I do.”\n\n“You have no sister—no son—no father?”\n\n“I have no one.”\n\n“How can you exist thus without anyone to attach you to life?”\n\n“It is not my fault, madame. At Malta, I loved a young girl, was on the point of marrying her, when war came and carried me away. I thought she loved me well enough to wait for me, and even to remain faithful to my memory. When I returned she was married. This is the history of most men who have passed twenty years of age. Perhaps my heart was weaker than the hearts of most men, and I suffered more than they would have done in my place; that is all.”\n\nThe countess stopped for a moment, as if gasping for breath. “Yes,” she said, “and you have still preserved this love in your heart—one can only love once—and did you ever see her again?”\n\n30309m\n“Never.”\n\n“Never?”\n\n“I never returned to the country where she lived.”\n\n“To Malta?”\n\n“Yes; Malta.”\n\n“She is, then, now at Malta?”\n\n“I think so.”\n\n“And have you forgiven her for all she has made you suffer?”\n\n“Her,—yes.”\n“But only her; do you then still hate those who separated you?”\n\n“I hate them? Not at all; why should I?” The countess placed herself before Monte Cristo, still holding in her hand a portion of the perfumed grapes.\n\n“Take some,” she said.\n\n“Madame, I never eat Muscatel grapes,” replied Monte Cristo, as if the subject had not been mentioned before. The countess dashed the grapes into the nearest thicket, with a gesture of despair.\n\n“Inflexible man!” she murmured. Monte Cristo remained as unmoved as if the reproach had not been addressed to him.\n\nAlbert at this moment ran in. “Oh, mother,” he exclaimed, “such a misfortune has happened!”\n\n“What? What has happened?” asked the countess, as though awakening from a sleep to the realities of life; “did you say a misfortune? Indeed, I should expect misfortunes.”\n\n“M. de Villefort is here.”\n\n“Well?”\n\n“He comes to fetch his wife and daughter.”\n\n“Why so?”\n\n“Because Madame de Saint-Méran is just arrived in Paris, bringing the news of M. de Saint-Méran’s death, which took place on the first stage after he left Marseilles. Madame de Villefort, who was in very good spirits, would neither believe nor think of the misfortune, but Mademoiselle Valentine, at the first words, guessed the whole truth, notwithstanding all the precautions of her father; the blow struck her like a thunderbolt, and she fell senseless.”\n\n“And how was M. de Saint-Méran related to Mademoiselle de Villefort?” said the count.\n\n“He was her grandfather on the mother’s side. He was coming here to hasten her marriage with Franz.”\n\n“Ah, indeed!”\n\n“So Franz must wait. Why was not M. de Saint-Méran also grandfather to Mademoiselle Danglars?”\n\n“Albert, Albert,” said Madame de Morcerf, in a tone of mild reproof, “what are you saying? Ah, count, he esteems you so highly, tell him that he has spoken amiss.”\n\nAnd she took two or three steps forward. Monte Cristo watched her with an air so thoughtful, and so full of affectionate admiration, that she turned back and grasped his hand; at the same time she seized that of her son, and joined them together.\n\n“We are friends; are we not?” she asked.\n\n“Oh, madame, I do not presume to call myself your friend, but at all times I am your most respectful servant.” The countess left with an indescribable pang in her heart, and before she had taken ten steps the count saw her raise her handkerchief to her eyes.\n\n“Do not my mother and you agree?” asked Albert, astonished.\n\n“On the contrary,” replied the count, “did you not hear her declare that we were friends?”\n\nThey re-entered the drawing-room, which Valentine and Madame de Villefort had just quitted. It is perhaps needless to add that Morrel departed almost at the same time.\n\n# Chapter 72. Madame de Saint-Méran\n\nA gloomy scene had indeed just passed at the house of M. de Villefort. After the ladies had departed for the ball, whither all the entreaties of Madame de Villefort had failed in persuading him to accompany them, the procureur had shut himself up in his study, according to his custom, with a heap of papers calculated to alarm anyone else, but which generally scarcely satisfied his inordinate desires.\n\nBut this time the papers were a mere matter of form. Villefort had secluded himself, not to study, but to reflect; and with the door locked and orders given that he should not be disturbed excepting for important business, he sat down in his armchair and began to ponder over the events, the remembrance of which had during the last eight days filled his mind with so many gloomy thoughts and bitter recollections.\n\nThen, instead of plunging into the mass of documents piled before him, he opened the drawer of his desk, touched a spring, and drew out a parcel of cherished memoranda, amongst which he had carefully arranged, in characters only known to himself, the names of all those who, either in his political career, in money matters, at the bar, or in his mysterious love affairs, had become his enemies.\n\nTheir number was formidable, now that he had begun to fear, and yet these names, powerful though they were, had often caused him to smile with the same kind of satisfaction experienced by a traveller who from the summit of a mountain beholds at his feet the craggy eminences, the almost impassable paths, and the fearful chasms, through which he has so perilously climbed. When he had run over all these names in his memory, again read and studied them, commenting meanwhile upon his lists, he shook his head.\n\n“No,” he murmured, “none of my enemies would have waited so patiently and laboriously for so long a space of time, that they might now come and crush me with this secret. Sometimes, as Hamlet says:\n\n‘Foul deeds will rise,\n\nThough all the earth o’erwhelm them, to men’s eyes;’\n\nbut, like a phosphoric light, they rise but to mislead. The story has been told by the Corsican to some priest, who in his turn has repeated it. M. de Monte Cristo may have heard it, and to enlighten himself——\n\n“But why should he wish to enlighten himself upon the subject?” asked Villefort, after a moment’s reflection, “what interest can this M. de Monte Cristo or M. Zaccone,—son of a shipowner of Malta, discoverer of a mine in Thessaly, now visiting Paris for the first time,—what interest, I say, can he take in discovering a gloomy, mysterious, and useless fact like this? However, among all the incoherent details given to me by the Abbé Busoni and by Lord Wilmore, by that friend and that enemy, one thing appears certain and clear in my opinion—that in no period, in no case, in no circumstance, could there have been any contact between him and me.”\n\nBut Villefort uttered words which even he himself did not believe. He dreaded not so much the revelation, for he could reply to or deny its truth;—he cared little for that _mene, mene, tekel upharsin_, which appeared suddenly in letters of blood upon the wall;—but what he was really anxious for was to discover whose hand had traced them. While he was endeavoring to calm his fears,—and instead of dwelling upon the political future that had so often been the subject of his ambitious dreams, was imagining a future limited to the enjoyments of home, in fear of awakening the enemy that had so long slept,—the noise of a carriage sounded in the yard, then he heard the steps of an aged person ascending the stairs, followed by tears and lamentations, such as servants always give vent to when they wish to appear interested in their master’s grief.\n\nHe drew back the bolt of his door, and almost directly an old lady entered, unannounced, carrying her shawl on her arm, and her bonnet in her hand. The white hair was thrown back from her yellow forehead, and her eyes, already sunken by the furrows of age, now almost disappeared beneath the eyelids swollen with grief.\n\n“Oh, sir,” she said; “oh, sir, what a misfortune! I shall die of it; oh, yes, I shall certainly die of it!”\n\nAnd then, falling upon the chair nearest the door, she burst into a paroxysm of sobs. The servants, standing in the doorway, not daring to approach nearer, were looking at Noirtier’s old servant, who had heard the noise from his master’s room, and run there also, remaining behind the others. Villefort rose, and ran towards his mother-in-law, for it was she.\n\n“Why, what can have happened?” he exclaimed, “what has thus disturbed you? Is M. de Saint-Méran with you?”\n\n“M. de Saint-Méran is dead,” answered the old marchioness, without preface and without expression; she appeared to be stupefied. Villefort drew back, and clasping his hands together, exclaimed:\n\n“Dead!—so suddenly?”\n\n“A week ago,” continued Madame de Saint-Méran, “we went out together in the carriage after dinner. M. de Saint-Méran had been unwell for some days; still, the idea of seeing our dear Valentine again inspired him with courage, and notwithstanding his illness he would leave. At six leagues from Marseilles, after having eaten some of the lozenges he is accustomed to take, he fell into such a deep sleep, that it appeared to me unnatural; still I hesitated to wake him, although I fancied that his face was flushed, and that the veins of his temples throbbed more violently than usual. However, as it became dark, and I could no longer see, I fell asleep; I was soon aroused by a piercing shriek, as from a person suffering in his dreams, and he suddenly threw his head back violently. I called the valet, I stopped the postilion, I spoke to M. de Saint-Méran, I applied my smelling-salts; but all was over, and I arrived at Aix by the side of a corpse.”\n\nVillefort stood with his mouth half open, quite stupefied.\n\n“Of course you sent for a doctor?”\n\n“Immediately; but, as I have told you, it was too late.”\n\n“Yes; but then he could tell of what complaint the poor marquis had died.”\n\n“Oh, yes, sir, he told me; it appears to have been an apoplectic stroke.”\n\n“And what did you do then?”\n\n“M. de Saint-Méran had always expressed a desire, in case his death happened during his absence from Paris, that his body might be brought to the family vault. I had him put into a leaden coffin, and I am preceding him by a few days.”\n\n“Oh! my poor mother!” said Villefort, “to have such duties to perform at your age after such a blow!”\n\n“God has supported me through all; and then, my dear marquis, he would certainly have done everything for me that I performed for him. It is true that since I left him, I seem to have lost my senses. I cannot cry; at my age they say that we have no more tears,—still I think that when one is in trouble one should have the power of weeping. Where is Valentine, sir? It is on her account I am here; I wish to see Valentine.”\n30315m\n\nVillefort thought it would be terrible to reply that Valentine was at a ball; so he only said that she had gone out with her step-mother, and that she should be fetched. “This instant, sir—this instant, I beseech you!” said the old lady. Villefort placed the arm of Madame de Saint-Méran within his own, and conducted her to his apartment.\n\n“Rest yourself, mother,” he said.\n\nThe marchioness raised her head at this word, and beholding the man who so forcibly reminded her of her deeply-regretted child, who still lived for her in Valentine, she felt touched at the name of mother, and bursting into tears, she fell on her knees before an armchair, where she buried her venerable head. Villefort left her to the care of the women, while old Barrois ran, half-scared, to his master; for nothing frightens old people so much as when death relaxes its vigilance over them for a moment in order to strike some other old person. Then, while Madame de Saint-Méran remained on her knees, praying fervently, Villefort sent for a cab, and went himself to fetch his wife and daughter from Madame de Morcerf’s. He was so pale when he appeared at the door of the ball-room, that Valentine ran to him, saying:\n\n“Oh, father, some misfortune has happened!”\n\n“Your grandmamma has just arrived, Valentine,” said M. de Villefort.\n\n“And grandpapa?” inquired the young girl, trembling with apprehension. M. de Villefort only replied by offering his arm to his daughter. It was just in time, for Valentine’s head swam, and she staggered; Madame de Villefort instantly hastened to her assistance, and aided her husband in dragging her to the carriage, saying:\n\n“What a singular event! Who could have thought it? Ah, yes, it is indeed strange!”\n\nAnd the wretched family departed, leaving a cloud of sadness hanging over the rest of the evening. At the foot of the stairs, Valentine found Barrois awaiting her.\n\n“M. Noirtier wishes to see you tonight, he said, in an undertone.\n\n“Tell him I will come when I leave my dear grandmamma,” she replied, feeling, with true delicacy, that the person to whom she could be of the most service just then was Madame de Saint-Méran.\n\nValentine found her grandmother in bed; silent caresses, heartwrung sobs, broken sighs, burning tears, were all that passed in this sad interview, while Madame de Villefort, leaning on her husband’s arm, maintained all outward forms of respect, at least towards the poor widow. She soon whispered to her husband:\n\n“I think it would be better for me to retire, with your permission, for the sight of me appears still to afflict your mother-in-law.” Madame de Saint-Méran heard her.\n\n“Yes, yes,” she said softly to Valentine, “let her leave; but do you stay.”\n\nMadame de Villefort left, and Valentine remained alone beside the bed, for the procureur, overcome with astonishment at the unexpected death, had followed his wife. Meanwhile, Barrois had returned for the first time to old Noirtier, who having heard the noise in the house, had, as we have said, sent his old servant to inquire the cause; on his return, his quick intelligent eye interrogated the messenger.\n\n“Alas, sir,” exclaimed Barrois, “a great misfortune has happened. Madame de Saint-Méran has arrived, and her husband is dead!”\n\nM. de Saint-Méran and Noirtier had never been on strict terms of friendship; still, the death of one old man always considerably affects another. Noirtier let his head fall upon his chest, apparently overwhelmed and thoughtful; then he closed one eye, in token of inquiry.\n\nBarrois asked, “Mademoiselle Valentine?”\n\nNoirtier nodded his head.\n\n“She is at the ball, as you know, since she came to say good-bye to you in full dress.” Noirtier again closed his left eye.\n\n“Do you wish to see her?” Noirtier again made an affirmative sign.\n\n“Well, they have gone to fetch her, no doubt, from Madame de Morcerf’s; I will await her return, and beg her to come up here. Is that what you wish for?”\n\n“Yes,” replied the invalid.\n\nBarrois, therefore, as we have seen, watched for Valentine, and informed her of her grandfather’s wish. Consequently, Valentine came up to Noirtier, on leaving Madame de Saint-Méran, who in the midst of her grief had at last yielded to fatigue and fallen into a feverish sleep. Within reach of her hand they placed a small table upon which stood a bottle of orangeade, her usual beverage, and a glass. Then, as we have said, the young girl left the bedside to see M. Noirtier.\n\nValentine kissed the old man, who looked at her with such tenderness that her eyes again filled with tears, whose sources he thought must be exhausted. The old gentleman continued to dwell upon her with the same expression.\n\n“Yes, yes,” said Valentine, “you mean that I have yet a kind grandfather left, do you not.” The old man intimated that such was his meaning. “Ah, yes, happily I have,” replied Valentine. “Without that, what would become of me?”\n\nIt was one o’clock in the morning. Barrois, who wished to go to bed himself, observed that after such sad events everyone stood in need of rest. Noirtier would not say that the only rest he needed was to see his child, but wished her good-night, for grief and fatigue had made her appear quite ill.\n\nThe next morning she found her grandmother in bed; the fever had not abated, on the contrary her eyes glistened and she appeared to be suffering from violent nervous irritability.\n\n“Oh, dear grandmamma, are you worse?” exclaimed Valentine, perceiving all these signs of agitation.\n\n“No, my child, no,” said Madame de Saint-Méran; “but I was impatiently waiting for your arrival, that I might send for your father.”\n\n“My father?” inquired Valentine, uneasily.\n\n“Yes, I wish to speak to him.”\n\nValentine durst not oppose her grandmother’s wish, the cause of which she did not know, and an instant afterwards Villefort entered.\n\n“Sir,” said Madame de Saint-Méran, without using any circumlocution, and as if fearing she had no time to lose, “you wrote to me concerning the marriage of this child?”\n\n“Yes, madame,” replied Villefort, “it is not only projected but arranged.”\n\n“Your intended son-in-law is named M. Franz d’Épinay?”\n\n“Yes, madame.”\n\n“Is he not the son of General d’Épinay who was on our side, and who was assassinated some days before the usurper returned from the Island of Elba?”\n“The same.”\n\n“Does he not dislike the idea of marrying the granddaughter of a Jacobin?”\n\n“Our civil dissensions are now happily extinguished, mother,” said Villefort; “M. d’Épinay was quite a child when his father died, he knows very little of M. Noirtier, and will meet him, if not with pleasure, at least with indifference.”\n\n“Is it a suitable match?”\n\n“In every respect.”\n\n“And the young man?”\n\n“Is regarded with universal esteem.”\n\n“You approve of him?”\n\n“He is one of the most well-bred young men I know.”\n\nDuring the whole of this conversation Valentine had remained silent.\n\n“Well, sir,” said Madame de Saint-Méran, after a few minutes’ reflection, “I must hasten the marriage, for I have but a short time to live.”\n\n“You, madame?” “You, dear mamma?” exclaimed M. de Villefort and Valentine at the same time.\n\n“I know what I am saying,” continued the marchioness; “I must hurry you, so that, as she has no mother, she may at least have a grandmother to bless her marriage. I am all that is left to her belonging to my poor Renée, whom you have so soon forgotten, sir.”\n\n“Ah, madame,” said Villefort, “you forget that I was obliged to give a mother to my child.”\n\n“A stepmother is never a mother, sir. But this is not to the purpose,—our business concerns Valentine, let us leave the dead in peace.”\n\nAll this was said with such exceeding rapidity, that there was something in the conversation that seemed like the beginning of delirium.\n\n“It shall be as you wish, madame,” said Villefort; “more especially since your wishes coincide with mine, and as soon as M. d’Épinay arrives in Paris——”\n\n30319m\n“My dear grandmother,” interrupted Valentine, “consider decorum—the recent death. You would not have me marry under such sad auspices?”\n\n“My child,” exclaimed the old lady sharply, “let us hear none of the conventional objections that deter weak minds from preparing for the future. I also was married at the death-bed of my mother, and certainly I have not been less happy on that account.”\n\n“Still that idea of death, madame,” said Villefort.\n\n“Still?—Always! I tell you I am going to die—do you understand? Well, before dying, I wish to see my son-in-law. I wish to tell him to make my child happy; I wish to read in his eyes whether he intends to obey me;—in fact, I will know him—I will!” continued the old lady, with a fearful expression, “that I may rise from the depths of my grave to find him, if he should not fulfil his duty!”\n\n“Madame,” said Villefort, “you must lay aside these exalted ideas, which almost assume the appearance of madness. The dead, once buried in their graves, rise no more.”\n\n“And I tell you, sir, that you are mistaken. This night I have had a fearful sleep. It seemed as though my soul were already hovering over my body, my eyes, which I tried to open, closed against my will, and what will appear impossible above all to you, sir, I saw, with my eyes shut, in the spot where you are now standing, issuing from that corner where there is a door leading into Madame Villefort’s dressing-room—I saw, I tell you, silently enter, a white figure.”\n\nValentine screamed.\n\n“It was the fever that disturbed you, madame,” said Villefort.\n\n30321m\n“Doubt, if you please, but I am sure of what I say. I saw a white figure, and as if to prevent my discrediting the testimony of only one of my senses, I heard my glass removed—the same which is there now on the table.”\n\n“Oh, dear mother, it was a dream.”\n\n“So little was it a dream, that I stretched my hand towards the bell; but when I did so, the shade disappeared; my maid then entered with a light.”\n\n“But she saw no one?”\n\n“Phantoms are visible to those only who ought to see them. It was the soul of my husband!—Well, if my husband’s soul can come to me, why should not my soul reappear to guard my granddaughter? the tie is even more direct, it seems to me.”\n\n“Oh, madame,” said Villefort, deeply affected, in spite of himself, “do not yield to those gloomy thoughts; you will long live with us, happy, loved, and honored, and we will make you forget——”\n\n“Never, never, never,” said the marchioness. “When does M. d’Épinay return?”\n\n“We expect him every moment.”\n\n“It is well. As soon as he arrives inform me. We must be expeditious. And then I also wish to see a notary, that I may be assured that all our property returns to Valentine.”\n\n“Ah, grandmamma,” murmured Valentine, pressing her lips on the burning brow, “do you wish to kill me? Oh, how feverish you are; we must not send for a notary, but for a doctor!”\n\n“A doctor?” said she, shrugging her shoulders, “I am not ill; I am thirsty—that is all.”\n30323m\n\n“What are you drinking, dear grandmamma?”\n\n“The same as usual, my dear, my glass is there on the table—give it to me, Valentine.” Valentine poured the orangeade into a glass and gave it to her grandmother with a certain degree of dread, for it was the same glass she fancied that had been touched by the spectre.\n\nThe marchioness drained the glass at a single draught, and then turned on her pillow, repeating,\n\n“The notary, the notary!”\n\nM. de Villefort left the room, and Valentine seated herself at the bedside of her grandmother. The poor child appeared herself to require the doctor she had recommended to her aged relative. A bright spot burned in either cheek, her respiration was short and difficult, and her pulse beat with feverish excitement. She was thinking of the despair of Maximilian, when he should be informed that Madame de Saint-Méran, instead of being an ally, was unconsciously acting as his enemy.\n\nMore than once she thought of revealing all to her grandmother, and she would not have hesitated a moment, if Maximilian Morrel had been named Albert de Morcerf or Raoul de Château-Renaud; but Morrel was of plebeian extraction, and Valentine knew how the haughty Marquise de Saint-Méran despised all who were not noble. Her secret had each time been repressed when she was about to reveal it, by the sad conviction that it would be useless to do so; for, were it once discovered by her father and mother, all would be lost.\n\nTwo hours passed thus; Madame de Saint-Méran was in a feverish sleep, and the notary had arrived. Though his coming was announced in a very low tone, Madame de Saint-Méran arose from her pillow.\n\n“The notary!” she exclaimed, “let him come in.”\n\nThe notary, who was at the door, immediately entered. “Go, Valentine,” said Madame de Saint-Méran, “and leave me with this gentleman.”\n\n“But, grandmamma——”\n\n“Leave me—go!”\n\nThe young girl kissed her grandmother, and left with her handkerchief to her eyes; at the door she found the valet de chambre, who told her that the doctor was waiting in the dining-room. Valentine instantly ran down. The doctor was a friend of the family, and at the same time one of the cleverest men of the day, and very fond of Valentine, whose birth he had witnessed. He had himself a daughter about her age, but whose life was one continued source of anxiety and fear to him from her mother having been consumptive.\n\n“Oh,” said Valentine, “we have been waiting for you with such impatience, dear M. d’Avrigny. But, first of all, how are Madeleine and Antoinette?”\n\nMadeleine was the daughter of M. d’Avrigny, and Antoinette his niece. M. d’Avrigny smiled sadly.\n\n“Antoinette is very well,” he said, “and Madeleine tolerably so. But you sent for me, my dear child. It is not your father or Madame de Villefort who is ill. As for you, although we doctors cannot divest our patients of nerves, I fancy you have no further need of me than to recommend you not to allow your imagination to take too wide a field.”\n\nValentine colored. M. d’Avrigny carried the science of divination almost to a miraculous extent, for he was one of the physicians who always work upon the body through the mind.\n30325m\n\n“No,” she replied, “it is for my poor grandmother. You know the calamity that has happened to us, do you not?”\n\n“I know nothing.” said M. d’Avrigny.\n\n“Alas,” said Valentine, restraining her tears, “my grandfather is dead.”\n\n“M. de Saint-Méran?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Suddenly?”\n\n“From an apoplectic stroke.”\n\n“An apoplectic stroke?” repeated the doctor.\n\n“Yes, and my poor grandmother fancies that her husband, whom she never left, has called her, and that she must go and join him. Oh, M. d’Avrigny, I beseech you, do something for her!”\n\n“Where is she?”\n\n“In her room with the notary.”\n\n“And M. Noirtier?”\n\n“Just as he was, his mind perfectly clear, but the same incapability of moving or speaking.”\n\n“And the same love for you—eh, my dear child?”\n\n“Yes,” said Valentine, “he was very fond of me.”\n\n“Who does not love you?” Valentine smiled sadly. “What are your grandmother’s symptoms?”\n\n“An extreme nervous excitement and a strangely agitated sleep; she fancied this morning in her sleep that her soul was hovering above her body, which she at the same time watched. It must have been delirium; she fancies, too, that she saw a phantom enter her chamber and even heard the noise it made on touching her glass.”\n\n“It is singular,” said the doctor; “I was not aware that Madame de Saint-Méran was subject to such hallucinations.”\n\n“It is the first time I ever saw her in this condition,” said Valentine; “and this morning she frightened me so that I thought her mad; and my father, who you know is a strong-minded man, himself appeared deeply impressed.”\n\n“We will go and see,” said the doctor; “what you tell me seems very strange.” The notary here descended, and Valentine was informed that her grandmother was alone.\n\n“Go upstairs,” she said to the doctor.\n\n“And you?”\n\n“Oh, I dare not—she forbade my sending for you; and, as you say, I am myself agitated, feverish and out of sorts. I will go and take a turn in the garden to recover myself.”\n\nThe doctor pressed Valentine’s hand, and while he visited her grandmother, she descended the steps. We need not say which portion of the garden was her favorite walk. After remaining for a short time in the parterre surrounding the house, and gathering a rose to place in her waist or hair, she turned into the dark avenue which led to the bench; then from the bench she went to the gate. As usual, Valentine strolled for a short time among her flowers, but without gathering them. The mourning in her heart forbade her assuming this simple ornament, though she had not yet had time to put on the outward semblance of woe.\n30327m\n\nShe then turned towards the avenue. As she advanced she fancied she heard a voice speaking her name. She stopped astonished, then the voice reached her ear more distinctly, and she recognized it to be that of Maximilian.\n\n# Chapter 73. The Promise\n\nIt was indeed Maximilian Morrel, who had passed a wretched existence since the previous day. With the instinct peculiar to lovers he had anticipated after the return of Madame de Saint-Méran and the death of the marquis, that something would occur at M. de Villefort’s in connection with his attachment for Valentine. His presentiments were realized, as we shall see, and his uneasy forebodings had goaded him pale and trembling to the gate under the chestnut-trees.\n\nValentine was ignorant of the cause of this sorrow and anxiety, and as it was not his accustomed hour for visiting her, she had gone to the spot simply by accident or perhaps through sympathy. Morrel called her, and she ran to the gate.\n\n“You here at this hour?” said she.\n\n“Yes, my poor girl,” replied Morrel; “I come to bring and to hear bad tidings.”\n\n“This is, indeed, a house of mourning,” said Valentine; “speak, Maximilian, although the cup of sorrow seems already full.”\n\n“Dear Valentine,” said Morrel, endeavoring to conceal his own emotion, “listen, I entreat you; what I am about to say is very serious. When are you to be married?”\n\n“I will tell you all,” said Valentine; “from you I have nothing to conceal. This morning the subject was introduced, and my dear grandmother, on whom I depended as my only support, not only declared herself favorable to it, but is so anxious for it, that they only await the arrival of M. d’Épinay, and the following day the contract will be signed.”\n\nA deep sigh escaped the young man, who gazed long and mournfully at her he loved.\n\n“Alas,” replied he, “it is dreadful thus to hear my condemnation from your own lips. The sentence is passed, and, in a few hours, will be executed; it must be so, and I will not endeavor to prevent it. But, since you say nothing remains but for M. d’Épinay to arrive that the contract may be signed, and the following day you will be his, tomorrow you will be engaged to M. d’Épinay, for he came this morning to Paris.” Valentine uttered a cry.\n\n“I was at the house of Monte Cristo an hour since,” said Morrel; “we were speaking, he of the sorrow your family had experienced, and I of your grief, when a carriage rolled into the courtyard. Never, till then, had I placed any confidence in presentiments, but now I cannot help believing them, Valentine. At the sound of that carriage I shuddered; soon I heard steps on the staircase, which terrified me as much as the footsteps of the commander did Don Juan. The door at last opened; Albert de Morcerf entered first, and I began to hope my fears were vain, when, after him, another young man advanced, and the count exclaimed: ‘Ah, here is the Baron Franz d’Épinay!’ I summoned all my strength and courage to my support. Perhaps I turned pale and trembled, but certainly I smiled; and five minutes after I left, without having heard one word that had passed.”\n\n“Poor Maximilian!” murmured Valentine.\n\n“Valentine, the time has arrived when you must answer me. And remember my life depends on your answer. What do you intend doing?” Valentine held down her head; she was overwhelmed.\n\n“Listen,” said Morrel; “it is not the first time you have contemplated our present position, which is a serious and urgent one; I do not think it is a moment to give way to useless sorrow; leave that for those who like to suffer at their leisure and indulge their grief in secret. There are such in the world, and God will doubtless reward them in heaven for their resignation on earth, but those who mean to contend must not lose one precious moment, but must return immediately the blow which fortune strikes. Do you intend to struggle against our ill-fortune? Tell me, Valentine for it is that I came to know.”\n\nValentine trembled, and looked at him with amazement. The idea of resisting her father, her grandmother, and all the family, had never occurred to her.\n\n“What do you say, Maximilian?” asked Valentine. “What do you mean by a struggle? Oh, it would be a sacrilege. What? I resist my father’s order, and my dying grandmother’s wish? Impossible!”\n\nMorrel started.\n\n“You are too noble not to understand me, and you understand me so well that you already yield, dear Maximilian. No, no; I shall need all my strength to struggle with myself and support my grief in secret, as you say. But to grieve my father—to disturb my grandmother’s last moments—never!”\n\n“You are right,” said Morrel, calmly.\n\n“In what a tone you speak!” cried Valentine.\n\n“I speak as one who admires you, mademoiselle.”\n\n“Mademoiselle,” cried Valentine; “mademoiselle! Oh, selfish man! he sees me in despair, and pretends he cannot understand me!”\n\n“You mistake—I understand you perfectly. You will not oppose M. Villefort, you will not displease the marchioness, and tomorrow you will sign the contract which will bind you to your husband.”\n\n“But, _mon Dieu!_ tell me, how can I do otherwise?”\n\n“Do not appeal to me, mademoiselle; I shall be a bad judge in such a case; my selfishness will blind me,” replied Morrel, whose low voice and clenched hands announced his growing desperation.\n\n“What would you have proposed, Maximilian, had you found me willing to accede?”\n\n“It is not for me to say.”\n\n“You are wrong; you must advise me what to do.”\n\n“Do you seriously ask my advice, Valentine?”\n\n“Certainly, dear Maximilian, for if it is good, I will follow it; you know my devotion to you.”\n\n“Valentine,” said Morrel pushing aside a loose plank, “give me your hand in token of forgiveness of my anger; my senses are confused, and during the last hour the most extravagant thoughts have passed through my brain. Oh, if you refuse my advice——”\n\n“What do you advise?” said Valentine, raising her eyes to heaven and sighing.\n\n“I am free,” replied Maximilian, “and rich enough to support you. I swear to make you my lawful wife before my lips even shall have approached your forehead.”\n\n“You make me tremble!” said the young girl.\n\n“Follow me,” said Morrel; “I will take you to my sister, who is worthy also to be yours. We will embark for Algiers, for England, for America, or, if you prefer it, retire to the country and only return to Paris when our friends have reconciled your family.”\n\nValentine shook her head.\n\n“I feared it, Maximilian,” said she; “it is the counsel of a madman, and I should be more mad than you, did I not stop you at once with the word ‘Impossible, Morrel, impossible!’”\n\n“You will then submit to what fate decrees for you without even attempting to contend with it?” said Morrel sorrowfully.\n\n“Yes,—if I die!”\n\n“Well, Valentine,” resumed Maximilian, “I can only say again that you are right. Truly, it is I who am mad, and you prove to me that passion blinds the most well-meaning. I appreciate your calm reasoning. It is then understood that tomorrow you will be irrevocably promised to M. Franz d’Épinay, not only by that theatrical formality invented to heighten the effect of a comedy called the signature of the contract, but your own will?”\n\n“Again you drive me to despair, Maximilian,” said Valentine, “again you plunge the dagger into the wound! What would you do, tell me, if your sister listened to such a proposition?”\n\n“Mademoiselle,” replied Morrel with a bitter smile, “I am selfish—you have already said so—and as a selfish man I think not of what others would do in my situation, but of what I intend doing myself. I think only that I have known you not a whole year. From the day I first saw you, all my hopes of happiness have been in securing your affection. One day you acknowledged that you loved me, and since that day my hope of future happiness has rested on obtaining you, for to gain you would be life to me. Now, I think no more; I say only that fortune has turned against me—I had thought to gain heaven, and now I have lost it. It is an every-day occurrence for a gambler to lose not only what he possesses but also what he has not.”\n\nMorrel pronounced these words with perfect calmness; Valentine looked at him a moment with her large, scrutinizing eyes, endeavoring not to let Morrel discover the grief which struggled in her heart.\n\n“But, in a word, what are you going to do?” asked she.\n\n“I am going to have the honor of taking my leave of you, mademoiselle, solemnly assuring you that I wish your life may be so calm, so happy, and so fully occupied, that there may be no place for me even in your memory.”\n\n“Oh!” murmured Valentine.\n\n“Adieu, Valentine, adieu!” said Morrel, bowing.\n\n“Where are you going?” cried the young girl, extending her hand through the opening, and seizing Maximilian by his coat, for she understood from her own agitated feelings that her lover’s calmness could not be real; “where are you going?”\n\n“I am going, that I may not bring fresh trouble into your family: and to set an example which every honest and devoted man, situated as I am, may follow.”\n\n“Before you leave me, tell me what you are going to do, Maximilian.” The young man smiled sorrowfully.\n\n“Speak, speak!” said Valentine; “I entreat you.”\n\n“Has your resolution changed, Valentine?”\n\n“It cannot change, unhappy man; you know it must not!” cried the young girl.\n\n“Then adieu, Valentine!”\n\nValentine shook the gate with a strength of which she could not have been supposed to be possessed, as Morrel was going away, and passing both her hands through the opening, she clasped and wrung them. “I must know what you mean to do!” said she. “Where are you going?”\n\n“Oh, fear not,” said Maximilian, stopping at a short distance, “I do not intend to render another man responsible for the rigorous fate reserved for me. Another might threaten to seek M. Franz, to provoke him, and to fight with him; all that would be folly. What has M. Franz to do with it? He saw me this morning for the first time, and has already forgotten he has seen me. He did not even know I existed when it was arranged by your two families that you should be united. I have no enmity against M. Franz, and promise you the punishment shall not fall on him.”\n\n“On whom, then!—on me?”\n\n“On you? Valentine! Oh, Heaven forbid! Woman is sacred; the woman one loves is holy.”\n\n“On yourself, then, unhappy man; on yourself?”\n\n“I am the only guilty person, am I not?” said Maximilian.\n\n“Maximilian!” said Valentine, “Maximilian, come back, I entreat you!”\n\nHe drew near with his sweet smile, and but for his paleness one might have thought him in his usual happy mood.\n\n“Listen, my dear, my adored Valentine,” said he in his melodious and grave tone; “those who, like us, have never had a thought for which we need blush before the world, such may read each other’s hearts. I never was romantic, and am no melancholy hero. I imitate neither Manfred nor Anthony; but without words, protestations, or vows, my life has entwined itself with yours; you leave me, and you are right in doing so,—I repeat it, you are right; but in losing you, I lose my life. The moment you leave me, Valentine, I am alone in the world. My sister is happily married; her husband is only my brother-in-law, that is, a man whom the ties of social life alone attach to me; no one then longer needs my useless life. This is what I shall do; I will wait until the very moment you are married, for I will not lose the shadow of one of those unexpected chances which are sometimes reserved for us, since M. Franz may, after all, die before that time, a thunderbolt may fall even on the altar as you approach it,—nothing appears impossible to one condemned to die, and miracles appear quite reasonable when his escape from death is concerned. I will, then, wait until the last moment, and when my misery is certain, irremediable, hopeless, I will write a confidential letter to my brother-in-law, another to the prefect of police, to acquaint them with my intention, and at the corner of some wood, on the brink of some abyss, on the bank of some river, I will put an end to my existence, as certainly as I am the son of the most honest man who ever lived in France.”\n30333m\n\nValentine trembled convulsively; she loosened her hold of the gate, her arms fell by her side, and two large tears rolled down her cheeks. The young man stood before her, sorrowful and resolute.\n\n“Oh, for pity’s sake,” said she, “you will live, will you not?”\n\n“No, on my honor,” said Maximilian; “but that will not affect you. You have done your duty, and your conscience will be at rest.”\n\nValentine fell on her knees, and pressed her almost bursting heart. “Maximilian,” said she, “Maximilian, my friend, my brother on earth, my true husband in heaven, I entreat you, do as I do, live in suffering; perhaps we may one day be united.”\n\n“Adieu, Valentine,” repeated Morrel.\n\n“My God,” said Valentine, raising both her hands to heaven with a sublime expression, “I have done my utmost to remain a submissive daughter; I have begged, entreated, implored; he has regarded neither my prayers, my entreaties, nor my tears. It is done,” cried she, wiping away her tears, and resuming her firmness, “I am resolved not to die of remorse, but rather of shame. Live, Maximilian, and I will be yours. Say when shall it be? Speak, command, I will obey.”\n\nMorrel, who had already gone some few steps away, again returned, and pale with joy extended both hands towards Valentine through the opening.\n\n“Valentine,” said he, “dear Valentine, you must not speak thus—rather let me die. Why should I obtain you by violence, if our love is mutual? Is it from mere humanity you bid me live? I would then rather die.”\n\n“Truly,” murmured Valentine, “who on this earth cares for me, if he does not? Who has consoled me in my sorrow but he? On whom do my hopes rest? On whom does my bleeding heart repose? On him, on him, always on him! Yes, you are right, Maximilian, I will follow you. I will leave the paternal home, I will give up all. Oh, ungrateful girl that I am,” cried Valentine, sobbing, “I will give up all, even my dear old grandfather, whom I had nearly forgotten.”\n\n“No,” said Maximilian, “you shall not leave him. M. Noirtier has evinced, you say, a kind feeling towards me. Well, before you leave, tell him all; his consent would be your justification in God’s sight. As soon as we are married, he shall come and live with us, instead of one child, he shall have two. You have told me how you talk to him and how he answers you; I shall very soon learn that language by signs, Valentine, and I promise you solemnly, that instead of despair, it is happiness that awaits us.”\n\n“Oh, see, Maximilian, see the power you have over me, you almost make me believe you; and yet, what you tell me is madness, for my father will curse me—he is inflexible—he will never pardon me. Now listen to me, Maximilian; if by artifice, by entreaty, by accident—in short, if by any means I can delay this marriage, will you wait?”\n\n“Yes, I promise you, as faithfully as you have promised me that this horrible marriage shall not take place, and that if you are dragged before a magistrate or a priest, you will refuse.”\n\n“I promise you by all that is most sacred to me in the world, namely, by my mother.”\n\n“We will wait, then,” said Morrel.\n\n“Yes, we will wait,” replied Valentine, who revived at these words; “there are so many things which may save unhappy beings such as we are.”\n\n“I rely on you, Valentine,” said Morrel; “all you do will be well done; only if they disregard your prayers, if your father and Madame de Saint-Méran insist that M. d’Épinay should be called tomorrow to sign the contract——”\n\n“Then you have my promise, Maximilian.”\n\n“Instead of signing——”\n\n“I will go to you, and we will fly; but from this moment until then, let us not tempt Providence, let us not see each other. It is a miracle, it is a providence that we have not been discovered. If we were surprised, if it were known that we met thus, we should have no further resource.”\n\n“You are right, Valentine; but how shall I ascertain?”\n\n“From the notary, M. Deschamps.”\n\n“I know him.”\n\n“And for myself—I will write to you, depend on me. I dread this marriage, Maximilian, as much as you.”\n\n“Thank you, my adored Valentine, thank you; that is enough. When once I know the hour, I will hasten to this spot, you can easily get over this fence with my assistance, a carriage will await us at the gate, in which you will accompany me to my sister’s; there living, retired or mingling in society, as you wish, we shall be enabled to use our power to resist oppression, and not suffer ourselves to be put to death like sheep, which only defend themselves by sighs.”\n\n“Yes,” said Valentine, “I will now acknowledge you are right, Maximilian; and now are you satisfied with your betrothal?” said the young girl sorrowfully.\n\n“My adored Valentine, words cannot express one half of my satisfaction.”\n\nValentine had approached, or rather, had placed her lips so near the fence, that they nearly touched those of Morrel, which were pressed against the other side of the cold and inexorable barrier.\n\n“Adieu, then, till we meet again,” said Valentine, tearing herself away. “I shall hear from you?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Thanks, thanks, dear love, adieu!”\n\nThe sound of a kiss was heard, and Valentine fled through the avenue. Morrel listened to catch the last sound of her dress brushing the branches, and of her footstep on the gravel, then raised his eyes with an ineffable smile of thankfulness to heaven for being permitted to be thus loved, and then also disappeared.\n\nThe young man returned home and waited all the evening and all the next day without getting any message. It was only on the following day, at about ten o’clock in the morning, as he was starting to call on M. Deschamps, the notary, that he received from the postman a small billet, which he knew to be from Valentine, although he had not before seen her writing. It was to this effect:\n\n“Tears, entreaties, prayers, have availed me nothing. Yesterday, for two hours, I was at the church of Saint-Philippe-du-Roule, and for two hours I prayed most fervently. Heaven is as inflexible as man, and the signature of the contract is fixed for this evening at nine o’clock. I have but one promise and but one heart to give; that promise is pledged to you, that heart is also yours. This evening, then, at a quarter to nine at the gate.\n\n“Your betrothed,\n\n“Valentine de Villefort.”\n\n“P.S.—My poor grandmother gets worse and worse; yesterday her fever amounted to delirium; today her delirium is almost madness. You will be very kind to me, will you not, Morrel, to make me forget my sorrow in leaving her thus? I think it is kept a secret from grandpapa Noirtier, that the contract is to be signed this evening.”\n\nMorrel went also to the notary, who confirmed the news that the contract was to be signed that evening. Then he went to call on Monte Cristo and heard still more. Franz had been to announce the ceremony, and Madame de Villefort had also written to beg the count to excuse her not inviting him; the death of M. de Saint-Méran and the dangerous illness of his widow would cast a gloom over the meeting which she would regret should be shared by the count whom she wished every happiness.\n\nThe day before Franz had been presented to Madame de Saint-Méran, who had left her bed to receive him, but had been obliged to return to it immediately after.\n\nIt is easy to suppose that Morrel’s agitation would not escape the count’s penetrating eye. Monte Cristo was more affectionate than ever,—indeed, his manner was so kind that several times Morrel was on the point of telling him all. But he recalled the promise he had made to Valentine, and kept his secret.\n\nThe young man read Valentine’s letter twenty times in the course of the day. It was her first, and on what an occasion! Each time he read it he renewed his vow to make her happy. How great is the power of a woman who has made so courageous a resolution! What devotion does she deserve from him for whom she has sacrificed everything! How ought she really to be supremely loved! She becomes at once a queen and a wife, and it is impossible to thank and love her sufficiently.\n\nMorrel longed intensely for the moment when he should hear Valentine say, “Here I am, Maximilian; come and help me.” He had arranged everything for her escape; two ladders were hidden in the clover-field; a cabriolet was ordered for Maximilian alone, without a servant, without lights; at the turning of the first street they would light the lamps, as it would be foolish to attract the notice of the police by too many precautions. Occasionally he shuddered; he thought of the moment when, from the top of that wall, he should protect the descent of his dear Valentine, pressing in his arms for the first time her of whom he had yet only kissed the delicate hand.\n\nWhen the afternoon arrived and he felt that the hour was drawing near, he wished for solitude, his agitation was extreme; a simple question from a friend would have irritated him. He shut himself in his room, and tried to read, but his eye glanced over the page without understanding a word, and he threw away the book, and for the second time sat down to sketch his plan, the ladders and the fence.\n\nAt length the hour drew near. Never did a man deeply in love allow the clocks to go on peacefully. Morrel tormented his so effectually that they struck eight at half-past six. He then said, “It is time to start; the signature was indeed fixed to take place at nine o’clock, but perhaps Valentine will not wait for that.” Consequently, Morrel, having left the Rue Meslay at half-past eight by his timepiece, entered the clover-field while the clock of Saint-Philippe-du-Roule was striking eight. The horse and cabriolet were concealed behind a small ruin, where Morrel had often waited.\n\nThe night gradually drew on, and the foliage in the garden assumed a deeper hue. Then Morrel came out from his hiding-place with a beating heart, and looked through the small opening in the gate; there was yet no one to be seen.\n\nThe clock struck half-past eight, and still another half-hour was passed in waiting, while Morrel walked to and fro, and gazed more and more frequently through the opening. The garden became darker still, but in the darkness he looked in vain for the white dress, and in the silence he vainly listened for the sound of footsteps. The house, which was discernible through the trees, remained in darkness, and gave no indication that so important an event as the signature of a marriage-contract was going on. Morrel looked at his watch, which wanted a quarter to ten; but soon the same clock he had already heard strike two or three times rectified the error by striking half-past nine.\n\nThis was already half an hour past the time Valentine had fixed. It was a terrible moment for the young man. The slightest rustling of the foliage, the least whistling of the wind, attracted his attention, and drew the perspiration to his brow; then he tremblingly fixed his ladder, and, not to lose a moment, placed his foot on the first step. Amidst all these alternations of hope and fear, the clock struck ten. “It is impossible,” said Maximilian, “that the signing of a contract should occupy so long a time without unexpected interruptions. I have weighed all the chances, calculated the time required for all the forms; something must have happened.”\n\nAnd then he walked rapidly to and fro, and pressed his burning forehead against the fence. Had Valentine fainted? or had she been discovered and stopped in her flight? These were the only obstacles which appeared possible to the young man.\n\nThe idea that her strength had failed her in attempting to escape, and that she had fainted in one of the paths, was the one that most impressed itself upon his mind. “In that case,” said he, “I should lose her, and by my own fault.” He dwelt on this idea for a moment, then it appeared reality. He even thought he could perceive something on the ground at a distance; he ventured to call, and it seemed to him that the wind wafted back an almost inarticulate sigh.\n\nAt last the half-hour struck. It was impossible to wait longer, his temples throbbed violently, his eyes were growing dim; he passed one leg over the wall, and in a moment leaped down on the other side. He was on Villefort’s premises—had arrived there by scaling the wall. What might be the consequences? However, he had not ventured thus far to draw back. He followed a short distance close under the wall, then crossed a path, hid entered a clump of trees. In a moment he had passed through them, and could see the house distinctly.\n\n30341m\nThen Morrel saw that he had been right in believing that the house was not illuminated. Instead of lights at every window, as is customary on days of ceremony, he saw only a gray mass, which was veiled also by a cloud, which at that moment obscured the moon’s feeble light. A light moved rapidly from time to time past three windows of the second floor. These three windows were in Madame de Saint-Méran’s room. Another remained motionless behind some red curtains which were in Madame de Villefort’s bedroom. Morrel guessed all this. So many times, in order to follow Valentine in thought at every hour in the day, had he made her describe the whole house, that without having seen it he knew it all.\n\nThis darkness and silence alarmed Morrel still more than Valentine’s absence had done. Almost mad with grief, and determined to venture everything in order to see Valentine once more, and be certain of the misfortune he feared, Morrel gained the edge of the clump of trees, and was going to pass as quickly as possible through the flower-garden, when the sound of a voice, still at some distance, but which was borne upon the wind, reached him. At this sound, as he was already partially exposed to view, he stepped back and concealed himself completely, remaining perfectly motionless.\n\nHe had formed his resolution. If it was Valentine alone, he would speak as she passed; if she was accompanied, and he could not speak, still he should see her, and know that she was safe; if they were strangers, he would listen to their conversation, and might understand something of this hitherto incomprehensible mystery.\n\nThe moon had just then escaped from behind the cloud which had concealed it, and Morrel saw Villefort come out upon the steps, followed by a gentleman in black. They descended, and advanced towards the clump of trees, and Morrel soon recognized the other gentleman as Doctor d’Avrigny.\n30339m\n\nThe young man, seeing them approach, drew back mechanically, until he found himself stopped by a sycamore-tree in the centre of the clump; there he was compelled to remain. Soon the two gentlemen stopped also.\n\n“Ah, my dear doctor,” said the procureur, “Heaven declares itself against my house! What a dreadful death—what a blow! Seek not to console me; alas, nothing can alleviate so great a sorrow—the wound is too deep and too fresh! Dead, dead!”\n\nThe cold sweat sprang to the young man’s brow, and his teeth chattered. Who could be dead in that house, which Villefort himself had called accursed?\n\n“My dear M. de Villefort,” replied the doctor, with a tone which redoubled the terror of the young man, “I have not led you here to console you; on the contrary——”\n\n“What can you mean?” asked the procureur, alarmed.\n\n“I mean that behind the misfortune which has just happened to you, there is another, perhaps, still greater.”\n\n“Can it be possible?” murmured Villefort, clasping his hands. “What are you going to tell me?”\n\n“Are we quite alone, my friend?”\n\n“Yes, quite; but why all these precautions?”\n\n“Because I have a terrible secret to communicate to you,” said the doctor. “Let us sit down.”\n\nVillefort fell, rather than seated himself. The doctor stood before him, with one hand placed on his shoulder. Morrel, horrified, supported his head with one hand, and with the other pressed his heart, lest its beatings should be heard. “Dead, dead!” repeated he within himself; and he felt as if he were also dying.\n\n“Speak, doctor—I am listening,” said Villefort; “strike—I am prepared for everything!”\n\n“Madame de Saint-Méran was, doubtless, advancing in years, but she enjoyed excellent health.” Morrel began again to breathe freely, which he had not done during the last ten minutes.\n\n“Grief has consumed her,” said Villefort—“yes, grief, doctor! After living forty years with the marquis——”\n\n“It is not grief, my dear Villefort,” said the doctor; “grief may kill, although it rarely does, and never in a day, never in an hour, never in ten minutes.” Villefort answered nothing, he simply raised his head, which had been cast down before, and looked at the doctor with amazement.\n\n“Were you present during the last struggle?” asked M. d’Avrigny.\n\n“I was,” replied the procureur; “you begged me not to leave.”\n\n“Did you notice the symptoms of the disease to which Madame de Saint-Méran has fallen a victim?”\n\n“I did. Madame de Saint-Méran had three successive attacks, at intervals of some minutes, each one more serious than the former. When you arrived, Madame de Saint-Méran had already been panting for breath some minutes; she then had a fit, which I took to be simply a nervous attack, and it was only when I saw her raise herself in the bed, and her limbs and neck appear stiffened, that I became really alarmed. Then I understood from your countenance there was more to fear than I had thought. This crisis past, I endeavored to catch your eye, but could not. You held her hand—you were feeling her pulse—and the second fit came on before you had turned towards me. This was more terrible than the first; the same nervous movements were repeated, and the mouth contracted and turned purple.”\n\n“And at the third she expired.”\n\n“At the end of the first attack I discovered symptoms of tetanus; you confirmed my opinion.”\n\n“Yes, before others,” replied the doctor; “but now we are alone——”\n\n“What are you going to say? Oh, spare me!”\n\n“That the symptoms of tetanus and poisoning by vegetable substances are the same.”\n\nM. de Villefort started from his seat, then in a moment fell down again, silent and motionless. Morrel knew not if he were dreaming or awake.\n\n“Listen,” said the doctor; “I know the full importance of the statement I have just made, and the disposition of the man to whom I have made it.”\n\n“Do you speak to me as a magistrate or as a friend?” asked Villefort.\n\n“As a friend, and only as a friend, at this moment. The similarity in the symptoms of tetanus and poisoning by vegetable substances is so great, that were I obliged to affirm by oath what I have now stated, I should hesitate; I therefore repeat to you, I speak not to a magistrate, but to a friend. And to that friend I say, ‘During the three-quarters of an hour that the struggle continued, I watched the convulsions and the death of Madame de Saint-Méran, and am thoroughly convinced that not only did her death proceed from poison, but I could also specify the poison.’”\n\n“Can it be possible?”\n\n“The symptoms are marked, do you see?—sleep broken by nervous spasms, excitation of the brain, torpor of the nerve centres. Madame de Saint-Méran succumbed to a powerful dose of brucine or of strychnine, which by some mistake, perhaps, has been given to her.”\n\nVillefort seized the doctor’s hand.\n\n“Oh, it is impossible,” said he, “I must be dreaming! It is frightful to hear such things from such a man as you! Tell me, I entreat you, my dear doctor, that you may be deceived.”\n\n“Doubtless I may, but——”\n\n“But?”\n30345m\n\n“But I do not think so.”\n\n“Have pity on me doctor! So many dreadful things have happened to me lately that I am on the verge of madness.”\n\n“Has anyone besides me seen Madame de Saint-Méran?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Has anything been sent for from a chemist’s that I have not examined?”\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“Had Madame de Saint-Méran any enemies?”\n\n“Not to my knowledge.”\n\n“Would her death affect anyone’s interest?”\n\n“It could not indeed, my daughter is her only heiress—Valentine alone. Oh, if such a thought could present itself, I would stab myself to punish my heart for having for one instant harbored it.”\n\n“Indeed, my dear friend,” said M. d’Avrigny, “I would not accuse anyone; I speak only of an accident, you understand,—of a mistake,—but whether accident or mistake, the fact is there; it is on my conscience and compels me to speak aloud to you. Make inquiry.”\n\n“Of whom?—how?—of what?”\n\n“May not Barrois, the old servant, have made a mistake, and have given Madame de Saint-Méran a dose prepared for his master?”\n\n“For my father?”\n\n“Yes.”\n“But how could a dose prepared for M. Noirtier poison Madame de Saint-Méran?”\n\n“Nothing is more simple. You know poisons become remedies in certain diseases, of which paralysis is one. For instance, having tried every other remedy to restore movement and speech to M. Noirtier, I resolved to try one last means, and for three months I have been giving him brucine; so that in the last dose I ordered for him there were six grains. This quantity, which is perfectly safe to administer to the paralyzed frame of M. Noirtier, which has become gradually accustomed to it, would be sufficient to kill another person.”\n\n“My dear doctor, there is no communication between M. Noirtier’s apartment and that of Madame de Saint-Méran, and Barrois never entered my mother-in-law’s room. In short, doctor although I know you to be the most conscientious man in the world, and although I place the utmost reliance in you, I want, notwithstanding my conviction, to believe this axiom, _errare humanum est_.”\n\n“Is there one of my brethren in whom you have equal confidence with myself?”\n\n“Why do you ask me that?—what do you wish?”\n\n“Send for him; I will tell him what I have seen, and we will consult together, and examine the body.”\n\n“And you will find traces of poison?”\n\n“No, I did not say of poison, but we can prove what was the state of the body; we shall discover the cause of her sudden death, and we shall say, ‘Dear Villefort, if this thing has been caused by negligence, watch over your servants; if from hatred, watch your enemies.’”\n\n“What do you propose to me, d’Avrigny?” said Villefort in despair; “so soon as another is admitted into our secret, an inquest will become necessary; and an inquest in my house—impossible! Still,” continued the procureur, looking at the doctor with uneasiness, “if you wish it—if you demand it, why then it shall be done. But, doctor, you see me already so grieved—how can I introduce into my house so much scandal, after so much sorrow? My wife and my daughter would die of it! And I, doctor—you know a man does not arrive at the post I occupy—one has not been king’s attorney twenty-five years without having amassed a tolerable number of enemies; mine are numerous. Let this affair be talked of, it will be a triumph for them, which will make them rejoice, and cover me with shame. Pardon me, doctor, these worldly ideas; were you a priest I should not dare tell you that, but you are a man, and you know mankind. Doctor, pray recall your words; you have said nothing, have you?”\n\n“My dear M. de Villefort,” replied the doctor, “my first duty is to humanity. I would have saved Madame de Saint-Méran, if science could have done it; but she is dead and my duty regards the living. Let us bury this terrible secret in the deepest recesses of our hearts; I am willing, if anyone should suspect this, that my silence on the subject should be imputed to my ignorance. Meanwhile, sir, watch always—watch carefully, for perhaps the evil may not stop here. And when you have found the culprit, if you find him, I will say to you, ‘You are a magistrate, do as you will!’”\n\n“I thank you, doctor,” said Villefort with indescribable joy; “I never had a better friend than you.” And, as if he feared Doctor d’Avrigny would recall his promise, he hurried him towards the house.\n\nWhen they were gone, Morrel ventured out from under the trees, and the moon shone upon his face, which was so pale it might have been taken for that of a ghost.\n\n“I am manifestly protected in a most wonderful, but most terrible manner,” said he; “but Valentine, poor girl, how will she bear so much sorrow?”\n\nAs he thought thus, he looked alternately at the window with red curtains and the three windows with white curtains. The light had almost disappeared from the former; doubtless Madame de Villefort had just put out her lamp, and the nightlamp alone reflected its dull light on the window. At the extremity of the building, on the contrary, he saw one of the three windows open. A wax-light placed on the mantle-piece threw some of its pale rays without, and a shadow was seen for one moment on the balcony. Morrel shuddered; he thought he heard a sob.\n\nIt cannot be wondered at that his mind, generally so courageous, but now disturbed by the two strongest human passions, love and fear, was weakened even to the indulgence of superstitious thoughts. Although it was impossible that Valentine should see him, hidden as he was, he thought he heard the shadow at the window call him; his disturbed mind told him so. This double error became an irresistible reality, and by one of the incomprehensible transports of youth, he bounded from his hiding-place, and with two strides, at the risk of being seen, at the risk of alarming Valentine, at the risk of being discovered by some exclamation which might escape the young girl, he crossed the flower-garden, which by the light of the moon resembled a large white lake, and having passed the rows of orange-trees which extended in front of the house, he reached the step, ran quickly up and pushed the door, which opened without offering any resistance.\n\nValentine had not seen him. Her eyes, raised towards heaven, were watching a silvery cloud gliding over the azure, its form that of a shadow mounting towards heaven. Her poetic and excited mind pictured it as the soul of her grandmother.\n\nMeanwhile, Morrel had traversed the anteroom and found the staircase, which, being carpeted, prevented his approach being heard, and he had regained that degree of confidence that the presence of M. de Villefort even would not have alarmed him. He was quite prepared for any such encounter. He would at once approach Valentine’s father and acknowledge all, begging Villefort to pardon and sanction the love which united two fond and loving hearts. Morrel was mad.\n\nHappily he did not meet anyone. Now, especially, did he find the description Valentine had given of the interior of the house useful to him; he arrived safely at the top of the staircase, and while he was feeling his way, a sob indicated the direction he was to take. He turned back, a door partly open enabled him to see his road, and to hear the voice of one in sorrow. He pushed the door open and entered. At the other end of the room, under a white sheet which covered it, lay the corpse, still more alarming to Morrel since the account he had so unexpectedly overheard. By its side, on her knees, and with her head buried in the cushion of an easy-chair, was Valentine, trembling and sobbing, her hands extended above her head, clasped and stiff. She had turned from the window, which remained open, and was praying in accents that would have affected the most unfeeling; her words were rapid, incoherent, unintelligible, for the burning weight of grief almost stopped her utterance.\n\nThe moon shining through the open blinds made the lamp appear to burn paler, and cast a sepulchral hue over the whole scene. Morrel could not resist this; he was not exemplary for piety, he was not easily impressed, but Valentine suffering, weeping, wringing her hands before him, was more than he could bear in silence. He sighed, and whispered a name, and the head bathed in tears and pressed on the velvet cushion of the chair—a head like that of a Magdalen by Correggio—was raised and turned towards him. Valentine perceived him without betraying the least surprise. A heart overwhelmed with one great grief is insensible to minor emotions. Morrel held out his hand to her. Valentine, as her only apology for not having met him, pointed to the corpse under the sheet, and began to sob again.\n\nNeither dared for some time to speak in that room. They hesitated to break the silence which death seemed to impose; at length Valentine ventured.\n\n“My friend,” said she, “how came you here? Alas, I would say you are welcome, had not death opened the way for you into this house.”\n\n“Valentine,” said Morrel with a trembling voice, “I had waited since half-past eight, and did not see you come; I became uneasy, leaped the wall, found my way through the garden, when voices conversing about the fatal event——”\n\n“What voices?” asked Valentine. Morrel shuddered as he thought of the conversation of the doctor and M. de Villefort, and he thought he could see through the sheet the extended hands, the stiff neck, and the purple lips.\n\n“Your servants,” said he, “who were repeating the whole of the sorrowful story; from them I learned it all.”\n\n“But it was risking the failure of our plan to come up here, love.”\n\n“Forgive me,” replied Morrel; “I will go away.”\n\n“No,” said Valentine, “you might meet someone; stay.”\n\n“But if anyone should come here——”\n\nThe young girl shook her head. “No one will come,” said she; “do not fear, there is our safeguard,” pointing to the bed.\n\n“But what has become of M. d’Épinay?” replied Morrel.\n\n30349m\n“M. Franz arrived to sign the contract just as my dear grandmother was dying.”\n\n“Alas,” said Morrel with a feeling of selfish joy; for he thought this death would cause the wedding to be postponed indefinitely.\n\n“But what redoubles my sorrow,” continued the young girl, as if this feeling was to receive its immediate punishment, “is that the poor old lady, on her death-bed, requested that the marriage might take place as soon as possible; she also, thinking to protect me, was acting against me.”\n\n“Hark!” said Morrel. They both listened; steps were distinctly heard in the corridor and on the stairs.\n\n“It is my father, who has just left his study.”\n\n“To accompany the doctor to the door,” added Morrel.\n\n“How do you know it is the doctor?” asked Valentine, astonished.\n\n“I imagined it must be,” said Morrel.\n\nValentine looked at the young man; they heard the street door close, then M. de Villefort locked the garden door, and returned upstairs. He stopped a moment in the anteroom, as if hesitating whether to turn to his own apartment or into Madame de Saint-Méran’s; Morrel concealed himself behind a door; Valentine remained motionless, grief seeming to deprive her of all fear. M. de Villefort passed on to his own room.\n\n“Now,” said Valentine, “you can neither go out by the front door nor by the garden.”\n\nMorrel looked at her with astonishment.\n\n“There is but one way left you that is safe,” said she; “it is through my grandfather’s room.” She rose. “Come,” she added.\n\n“Where?” asked Maximilian.\n\n“To my grandfather’s room.”\n\n“I in M. Noirtier’s apartment?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Can you mean it, Valentine?”\n\n“I have long wished it; he is my only remaining friend and we both need his help,—come.”\n\n“Be careful, Valentine,” said Morrel, hesitating to comply with the young girl’s wishes; “I now see my error—I acted like a madman in coming in here. Are you sure you are more reasonable?”\n\n“Yes,” said Valentine; “and I have but one scruple,—that of leaving my dear grandmother’s remains, which I had undertaken to watch.”\n\n“Valentine,” said Morrel, “death is in itself sacred.”\n\n“Yes,” said Valentine; “besides, it will not be for long.”\n\nShe then crossed the corridor, and led the way down a narrow staircase to M. Noirtier’s room; Morrel followed her on tiptoe; at the door they found the old servant.\n\n“Barrois,” said Valentine, “shut the door, and let no one come in.”\n\nShe passed first.\n\nNoirtier, seated in his chair, and listening to every sound, was watching the door; he saw Valentine, and his eye brightened. There was something grave and solemn in the approach of the young girl which struck the old man, and immediately his bright eye began to interrogate.\n\n“Dear grandfather.” said she hurriedly, “you know poor grandmamma died an hour since, and now I have no friend in the world but you.”\n\nHis expressive eyes evinced the greatest tenderness.\n\n“To you alone, then, may I confide my sorrows and my hopes?”\n\nThe paralytic motioned “Yes.”\n\nValentine took Maximilian’s hand.\n\n“Look attentively, then, at this gentleman.”\n\nThe old man fixed his scrutinizing gaze with slight astonishment on Morrel.\n\n“It is M. Maximilian Morrel,” said she; “the son of that good merchant of Marseilles, whom you doubtless recollect.”\n\n“Yes,” said the old man.\n\n“He brings an irreproachable name, which Maximilian is likely to render glorious, since at thirty years of age he is a captain, an officer of the Legion of Honor.”\n\nThe old man signified that he recollected him.\n\n“Well, grandpapa,” said Valentine, kneeling before him, and pointing to Maximilian, “I love him, and will be only his; were I compelled to marry another, I would destroy myself.”\n\nThe eyes of the paralytic expressed a multitude of tumultuous thoughts.\n\n“You like M. Maximilian Morrel, do you not, grandpapa?” asked Valentine.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“And you will protect us, who are your children, against the will of my father?”\n\nNoirtier cast an intelligent glance at Morrel, as if to say, “perhaps I may.”\n\nMaximilian understood him.\n\n“Mademoiselle,” said he, “you have a sacred duty to fulfil in your deceased grandmother’s room, will you allow me the honor of a few minutes’ conversation with M. Noirtier?”\n\n“That is it,” said the old man’s eye. Then he looked anxiously at Valentine.\n\n“Do you fear he will not understand?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Oh, we have so often spoken of you, that he knows exactly how I talk to you.” Then turning to Maximilian, with an adorable smile; although shaded by sorrow,—“He knows everything I know,” said she.\n\nValentine arose, placed a chair for Morrel, requested Barrois not to admit anyone, and having tenderly embraced her grandfather, and sorrowfully taken leave of Morrel, she went away. To prove to Noirtier that he was in Valentine’s confidence and knew all their secrets, Morrel took the dictionary, a pen, and some paper, and placed them all on a table where there was a light.\n\n“But first,” said Morrel, “allow me, sir, to tell you who I am, how much I love Mademoiselle Valentine, and what are my designs respecting her.”\n\nNoirtier made a sign that he would listen.\n\nIt was an imposing sight to witness this old man, apparently a mere useless burden, becoming the sole protector, support, and adviser of the lovers who were both young, beautiful, and strong. His remarkably noble and austere expression struck Morrel, who began his story with trembling. He related the manner in which he had become acquainted with Valentine, and how he had loved her, and that Valentine, in her solitude and her misfortune, had accepted the offer of his devotion. He told him his birth, his position, his fortune, and more than once, when he consulted the look of the paralytic, that look answered, “That is good, proceed.”\n\n“And now,” said Morrel, when he had finished the first part of his recital, “now I have told you of my love and my hopes, may I inform you of my intentions?”\n\n“Yes,” signified the old man.\n\n“This was our resolution; a cabriolet was in waiting at the gate, in which I intended to carry off Valentine to my sister’s house, to marry her, and to wait respectfully M. de Villefort’s pardon.”\n\n“No,” said Noirtier.\n\n“We must not do so?”\n“No.”\n\n“You do not sanction our project?”\n“No.”\n\n“There is another way,” said Morrel. The old man’s interrogative eye said, “Which?”\n\n“I will go,” continued Maximilian, “I will seek M. Franz d’Épinay—I am happy to be able to mention this in Mademoiselle de Villefort’s absence—and will conduct myself toward him so as to compel him to challenge me.” Noirtier’s look continued to interrogate.\n\n“You wish to know what I will do?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“I will find him, as I told you. I will tell him the ties which bind me to Mademoiselle Valentine; if he be a sensible man, he will prove it by renouncing of his own accord the hand of his betrothed, and will secure my friendship, and love until death; if he refuse, either through interest or ridiculous pride, after I have proved to him that he would be forcing my wife from me, that Valentine loves me, and will have no other, I will fight with him, give him every advantage, and I shall kill him, or he will kill me; if I am victorious, he will not marry Valentine, and if I die, I am very sure Valentine will not marry him.”\n\nNoirtier watched, with indescribable pleasure, this noble and sincere countenance, on which every sentiment his tongue uttered was depicted, adding by the expression of his fine features all that coloring adds to a sound and faithful drawing.\n\nStill, when Morrel had finished, he shut his eyes several times, which was his manner of saying “No.”\n\n“No?” said Morrel; “you disapprove of this second project, as you did of the first?”\n\n“I do,” signified the old man.\n\n“But what then must be done?” asked Morrel. “Madame de Saint-Méran’s last request was, that the marriage might not be delayed; must I let things take their course?” Noirtier did not move. “I understand,” said Morrel; “I am to wait.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“But delay may ruin our plan, sir,” replied the young man. “Alone, Valentine has no power; she will be compelled to submit. I am here almost miraculously, and can scarcely hope for so good an opportunity to occur again. Believe me, there are only the two plans I have proposed to you; forgive my vanity, and tell me which you prefer. Do you authorize Mademoiselle Valentine to intrust herself to my honor?”\n\n“No.”\n“Do you prefer I should seek M. d’Épinay?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Whence then will come the help we need—from chance?” resumed Morrel.\n\n“No.”\n“From you?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You thoroughly understand me, sir? Pardon my eagerness, for my life depends on your answer. Will our help come from you?”\n“Yes.”\n\n“You are sure of it?”\n\n“Yes.” There was so much firmness in the look which gave this answer, no one could, at any rate, doubt his will, if they did his power.\n\n“Oh, thank you a thousand times! But how, unless a miracle should restore your speech, your gesture, your movement, how can you, chained to that armchair, dumb and motionless, oppose this marriage?” A smile lit up the old man’s face, a strange smile of the eyes in a paralyzed face.\n\n“Then I must wait?” asked the young man.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“But the contract?” The same smile returned. “Will you assure me it shall not be signed?”\n\n“Yes,” said Noirtier.\n\n“The contract shall not be signed!” cried Morrel. “Oh, pardon me, sir; I can scarcely realize so great a happiness. Will they not sign it?”\n\n“No,” said the paralytic. Notwithstanding that assurance, Morrel still hesitated. This promise of an impotent old man was so strange that, instead of being the result of the power of his will, it might emanate from enfeebled organs. Is it not natural that the madman, ignorant of his folly, should attempt things beyond his power? The weak man talks of burdens he can raise, the timid of giants he can confront, the poor of treasures he spends, the most humble peasant, in the height of his pride, calls himself Jupiter. Whether Noirtier understood the young man’s indecision, or whether he had not full confidence in his docility, he looked uneasily at him.\n\n“What do you wish, sir?” asked Morrel; “that I should renew my promise of remaining tranquil?” Noirtier’s eye remained fixed and firm, as if to imply that a promise did not suffice; then it passed from his face to his hands.\n\n“Shall I swear to you, sir?” asked Maximilian.\n\n“Yes,” said the paralytic with the same solemnity. Morrel understood that the old man attached great importance to an oath. He extended his hand.\n\n“I swear to you, on my honor,” said he, “to await your decision respecting the course I am to pursue with M. d’Épinay.”\n\n“That is right,” said the old man.\n\n“Now,” said Morrel, “do you wish me to retire?”\n“Yes.”\n\n“Without seeing Mademoiselle Valentine?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nMorrel made a sign that he was ready to obey. “But,” said he, “first allow me to embrace you as your daughter did just now.” Noirtier’s expression could not be understood. The young man pressed his lips on the same spot, on the old man’s forehead, where Valentine’s had been. Then he bowed a second time and retired.\n\nHe found outside the door the old servant, to whom Valentine had given directions. Morrel was conducted along a dark passage, which led to a little door opening on the garden, soon found the spot where he had entered, with the assistance of the shrubs gained the top of the wall, and by his ladder was in an instant in the clover-field where his cabriolet was still waiting for him. He got in it, and thoroughly wearied by so many emotions, arrived about midnight in the Rue Meslay, threw himself on his bed and slept soundly.\n40010m\n\n40020m\n\n# Chapter 74. The Villefort Family Vault\n\nTwo days after, a considerable crowd was assembled, towards ten o’clock in the morning, around the door of M. de Villefort’s house, and a long file of mourning-coaches and private carriages extended along the Faubourg Saint-Honoré and the Rue de la Pépinière. Among them was one of a very singular form, which appeared to have come from a distance. It was a kind of covered wagon, painted black, and was one of the first to arrive. Inquiry was made, and it was ascertained that, by a strange coincidence, this carriage contained the corpse of the Marquis de Saint-Méran, and that those who had come thinking to attend one funeral would follow two. Their number was great. The Marquis de Saint-Méran, one of the most zealous and faithful dignitaries of Louis XVIII. and King Charles X., had preserved a great number of friends, and these, added to the personages whom the usages of society gave Villefort a claim on, formed a considerable body.\n\nDue information was given to the authorities, and permission obtained that the two funerals should take place at the same time. A second hearse, decked with the same funereal pomp, was brought to M. de Villefort’s door, and the coffin removed into it from the post-wagon. The two bodies were to be interred in the cemetery of Père-Lachaise, where M. de Villefort had long since had a tomb prepared for the reception of his family. The remains of poor Renée were already deposited there, and now, after ten years of separation, her father and mother were to be reunited with her.\n\nThe Parisians, always curious, always affected by funereal display, looked on with religious silence while the splendid procession accompanied to their last abode two of the number of the old aristocracy—the greatest protectors of commerce and sincere devotees to their principles.\n\nIn one of the mourning-coaches Beauchamp, Debray, and Château-Renaud were talking of the very sudden death of the marchioness.\n\n“I saw Madame de Saint-Méran only last year at Marseilles, when I was coming back from Algiers,” said Château-Renaud; “she looked like a woman destined to live to be a hundred years old, from her apparent sound health and great activity of mind and body. How old was she?”\n\n“Franz assured me,” replied Albert, “that she was sixty-six years old. But she has not died of old age, but of grief; it appears that since the death of the marquis, which affected her very deeply, she has not completely recovered her reason.”\n\n“But of what disease, then, did she die?” asked Debray.\n\n“It is said to have been a congestion of the brain, or apoplexy, which is the same thing, is it not?”\n\n“Nearly.”\n\n“It is difficult to believe that it was apoplexy,” said Beauchamp. “Madame de Saint-Méran, whom I once saw, was short, of slender form, and of a much more nervous than sanguine temperament; grief could hardly produce apoplexy in such a constitution as that of Madame de Saint-Méran.”\n\n“At any rate,” said Albert, “whatever disease or doctor may have killed her, M. de Villefort, or rather, Mademoiselle Valentine,—or, still rather, our friend Franz, inherits a magnificent fortune, amounting, I believe, to 80,000 livres per annum.”\n\n“And this fortune will be doubled at the death of the old Jacobin, Noirtier.”\n\n“That is a tenacious old grandfather,” said Beauchamp. “_Tenacem propositi virum_. I think he must have made an agreement with death to outlive all his heirs, and he appears likely to succeed. He resembles the old Conventionalist of ’93, who said to Napoleon, in 1814, ‘You bend because your empire is a young stem, weakened by rapid growth. Take the Republic for a tutor; let us return with renewed strength to the battle-field, and I promise you 500,000 soldiers, another Marengo, and a second Austerlitz. Ideas do not become extinct, sire; they slumber sometimes, but only revive the stronger before they sleep entirely.’”\n\n“Ideas and men appeared the same to him,” said Albert. “One thing only puzzles me, namely, how Franz d’Épinay will like a grandfather who cannot be separated from his wife. But where is Franz?”\n\n“In the first carriage, with M. de Villefort, who considers him already as one of the family.”\n\n40024m\nSuch was the conversation in almost all the carriages; these two sudden deaths, so quickly following each other, astonished everyone, but no one suspected the terrible secret which M. d’Avrigny had communicated, in his nocturnal walk to M. de Villefort. They arrived in about an hour at the cemetery; the weather was mild, but dull, and in harmony with the funeral ceremony. Among the groups which flocked towards the family vault, Château-Renaud recognized Morrel, who had come alone in a cabriolet, and walked silently along the path bordered with yew-trees.\n\n“You here?” said Château-Renaud, passing his arms through the young captain’s; “are you a friend of Villefort’s? How is it that I have never met you at his house?”\n\n“I am no acquaintance of M. de Villefort’s,” answered Morrel, “but I was of Madame de Saint-Méran.” Albert came up to them at this moment with Franz.\n\n“The time and place are but ill-suited for an introduction.” said Albert; “but we are not superstitious. M. Morrel, allow me to present to you M. Franz d’Épinay, a delightful travelling companion, with whom I made the tour of Italy. My dear Franz, M. Maximilian Morrel, an excellent friend I have acquired in your absence, and whose name you will hear me mention every time I make any allusion to affection, wit, or amiability.”\n\nMorrel hesitated for a moment; he feared it would be hypocritical to accost in a friendly manner the man whom he was tacitly opposing, but his oath and the gravity of the circumstances recurred to his memory; he struggled to conceal his emotion and bowed to Franz.\n\n“Mademoiselle de Villefort is in deep sorrow, is she not?” said Debray to Franz.\n\n“Extremely,” replied he; “she looked so pale this morning, I scarcely knew her.”\n\nThese apparently simple words pierced Morrel to the heart. This man had seen Valentine, and spoken to her! The young and high-spirited officer required all his strength of mind to resist breaking his oath. He took the arm of Château-Renaud, and turned towards the vault, where the attendants had already placed the two coffins.\n\n“This is a magnificent habitation,” said Beauchamp, looking towards the mausoleum; “a summer and winter palace. You will, in turn, enter it, my dear d’Épinay, for you will soon be numbered as one of the family. I, as a philosopher, should like a little country-house, a cottage down there under the trees, without so many free-stones over my poor body. In dying, I will say to those around me what Voltaire wrote to Piron: ‘_Eo rus_, and all will be over.’ But come, Franz, take courage, your wife is an heiress.”\n\n“Indeed, Beauchamp, you are unbearable. Politics has made you laugh at everything, and political men have made you disbelieve everything. But when you have the honor of associating with ordinary men, and the pleasure of leaving politics for a moment, try to find your affectionate heart, which you leave with your stick when you go to the Chamber.”\n\n“But tell me,” said Beauchamp, “what is life? Is it not a halt in Death’s anteroom?”\n40026m\n\n“I am prejudiced against Beauchamp,” said Albert, drawing Franz away, and leaving the former to finish his philosophical dissertation with Debray.\n\nThe Villefort vault formed a square of white stones, about twenty feet high; an interior partition separated the two families, and each apartment had its entrance door. Here were not, as in other tombs, ignoble drawers, one above another, where thrift bestows its dead and labels them like specimens in a museum; all that was visible within the bronze gates was a gloomy-looking room, separated by a wall from the vault itself. The two doors before mentioned were in the middle of this wall, and enclosed the Villefort and Saint-Méran coffins. There grief might freely expend itself without being disturbed by the trifling loungers who came from a picnic party to visit Père-Lachaise, or by lovers who make it their rendezvous.\n\nThe two coffins were placed on trestles previously prepared for their reception in the right-hand crypt belonging to the Saint-Méran family. Villefort, Franz, and a few near relatives alone entered the sanctuary.\n\nAs the religious ceremonies had all been performed at the door, and there was no address given, the party all separated; Château-Renaud, Albert, and Morrel, went one way, and Debray and Beauchamp the other. Franz remained with M. de Villefort; at the gate of the cemetery Morrel made an excuse to wait; he saw Franz and M. de Villefort get into the same mourning-coach, and thought this meeting forboded evil. He then returned to Paris, and although in the same carriage with Château-Renaud and Albert, he did not hear one word of their conversation.\n\nAs Franz was about to take leave of M. de Villefort, “When shall I see you again?” said the latter.\n\n“At what time you please, sir,” replied Franz.\n\n“As soon as possible.”\n\n“I am at your command, sir; shall we return together?”\n\n“If not unpleasant to you.”\n\n“On the contrary, I shall feel much pleasure.”\n\nThus, the future father and son-in-law stepped into the same carriage, and Morrel, seeing them pass, became uneasy. Villefort and Franz returned to the Faubourg Saint-Honoré. The procureur, without going to see either his wife or his daughter, went at once to his study, and, offering the young man a chair:\n\n“M. d’Épinay,” said he, “allow me to remind you at this moment,—which is perhaps not so ill-chosen as at first sight may appear, for obedience to the wishes of the departed is the first offering which should be made at their tomb,—allow me then to remind you of the wish expressed by Madame de Saint-Méran on her death-bed, that Valentine’s wedding might not be deferred. You know the affairs of the deceased are in perfect order, and her will bequeaths to Valentine the entire property of the Saint-Méran family; the notary showed me the documents yesterday, which will enable us to draw up the contract immediately. You may call on the notary, M. Deschamps, Place Beauveau, Faubourg Saint-Honoré, and you have my authority to inspect those deeds.”\n\n“Sir,” replied M. d’Épinay, “it is not, perhaps, the moment for Mademoiselle Valentine, who is in deep distress, to think of a husband; indeed, I fear——”\n\n40028m\n“Valentine will have no greater pleasure than that of fulfilling her grandmother’s last injunctions; there will be no obstacle from that quarter, I assure you.”\n\n“In that case,” replied Franz, “as I shall raise none, you may make arrangements when you please; I have pledged my word, and shall feel pleasure and happiness in adhering to it.”\n\n“Then,” said Villefort, “nothing further is required. The contract was to have been signed three days since; we shall find it all ready, and can sign it today.”\n\n“But the mourning?” said Franz, hesitating.\n\n“Don’t be uneasy on that score,” replied Villefort; “no ceremony will be neglected in my house. Mademoiselle de Villefort may retire during the prescribed three months to her estate of Saint-Méran; I say hers, for she inherits it today. There, after a few days, if you like, the civil marriage shall be celebrated without pomp or ceremony. Madame de Saint-Méran wished her daughter should be married there. When that is over, you, sir, can return to Paris, while your wife passes the time of her mourning with her mother-in-law.”\n\n“As you please, sir,” said Franz.\n\n“Then,” replied M. de Villefort, “have the kindness to wait half an hour; Valentine shall come down into the drawing-room. I will send for M. Deschamps; we will read and sign the contract before we separate, and this evening Madame de Villefort shall accompany Valentine to her estate, where we will rejoin them in a week.”\n\n“Sir,” said Franz, “I have one request to make.”\n\n“What is it?”\n\n“I wish Albert de Morcerf and Raoul de Château-Renaud to be present at this signature; you know they are my witnesses.”\n\n“Half an hour will suffice to apprise them; will you go for them yourself, or shall you send?”\n\n“I prefer going, sir.”\n\n“I shall expect you, then, in half an hour, baron, and Valentine will be ready.”\n\nFranz bowed and left the room. Scarcely had the door closed, when M. de Villefort sent to tell Valentine to be ready in the drawing-room in half an hour, as he expected the notary and M. d’Épinay and his witnesses. The news caused a great sensation throughout the house; Madame de Villefort would not believe it, and Valentine was thunderstruck. She looked around for help, and would have gone down to her grandfather’s room, but on the stairs she met M. de Villefort, who took her arm and led her into the drawing-room. In the anteroom, Valentine met Barrois, and looked despairingly at the old servant. A moment later, Madame de Villefort entered the drawing-room with her little Edward. It was evident that she had shared the grief of the family, for she was pale and looked fatigued. She sat down, took Edward on her knees, and from time to time pressed this child, on whom her affections appeared centred, almost convulsively to her bosom.\n\nTwo carriages were soon heard to enter the courtyard. One was the notary’s; the other, that of Franz and his friends. In a moment the whole party was assembled. Valentine was so pale one might trace the blue veins from her temples, round her eyes and down her cheeks. Franz was deeply affected. Château-Renaud and Albert looked at each other with amazement; the ceremony which was just concluded had not appeared more sorrowful than did that which was about to begin. Madame de Villefort had placed herself in the shadow behind a velvet curtain, and as she constantly bent over her child, it was difficult to read the expression of her face. M. de Villefort was, as usual, unmoved.\n\nThe notary, after having, according to the customary method, arranged the papers on the table, taken his place in an armchair, and raised his spectacles, turned towards Franz:\n\n“Are you M. Franz de Quesnel, baron d’Épinay?” asked he, although he knew it perfectly.\n\n“Yes, sir,” replied Franz. The notary bowed.\n\n“I have, then, to inform you, sir, at the request of M. de Villefort, that your projected marriage with Mademoiselle de Villefort has changed the feeling of M. Noirtier towards his grandchild, and that he disinherits her entirely of the fortune he would have left her. Let me hasten to add,” continued he, “that the testator, having only the right to alienate a part of his fortune, and having alienated it all, the will will not bear scrutiny, and is declared null and void.”\n\n“Yes.” said Villefort; “but I warn M. d’Épinay, that during my life-time my father’s will shall never be questioned, my position forbidding any doubt to be entertained.”\n40032m\n\n“Sir,” said Franz, “I regret much that such a question has been raised in the presence of Mademoiselle Valentine; I have never inquired the amount of her fortune, which, however limited it may be, exceeds mine. My family has sought consideration in this alliance with M. de Villefort; all I seek is happiness.”\n\nValentine imperceptibly thanked him, while two silent tears rolled down her cheeks.\n\n“Besides, sir,” said Villefort, addressing himself to his future son-in-law, “excepting the loss of a portion of your hopes, this unexpected will need not personally wound you; M. Noirtier’s weakness of mind sufficiently explains it. It is not because Mademoiselle Valentine is going to marry you that he is angry, but because she will marry, a union with any other would have caused him the same sorrow. Old age is selfish, sir, and Mademoiselle de Villefort has been a faithful companion to M. Noirtier, which she cannot be when she becomes the Baroness d’Épinay. My father’s melancholy state prevents our speaking to him on any subjects, which the weakness of his mind would incapacitate him from understanding, and I am perfectly convinced that at the present time, although, he knows that his granddaughter is going to be married, M. Noirtier has even forgotten the name of his intended grandson.” M. de Villefort had scarcely said this, when the door opened, and Barrois appeared.\n\n“Gentlemen,” said he, in a tone strangely firm for a servant speaking to his masters under such solemn circumstances,—“gentlemen, M. Noirtier de Villefort wishes to speak immediately to M. Franz de Quesnel, baron d’Épinay.” He, as well as the notary, that there might be no mistake in the person, gave all his titles to the bridegroom elect.\n\nVillefort started, Madame de Villefort let her son slip from her knees, Valentine rose, pale and dumb as a statue. Albert and Château-Renaud exchanged a second look, more full of amazement than the first. The notary looked at Villefort.\n\n“It is impossible,” said the procureur. “M. d’Épinay cannot leave the drawing-room at present.”\n\n“It is at this moment,” replied Barrois with the same firmness, “that M. Noirtier, my master, wishes to speak on important subjects to M. Franz d’Épinay.”\n\n“Grandpapa Noirtier can speak now, then,” said Edward, with his habitual quickness. However, his remark did not make Madame de Villefort even smile, so much was every mind engaged, and so solemn was the situation.\n\n“Tell M. Nortier,” resumed Villefort, “that what he demands is impossible.”\n\n“Then, M. Nortier gives notice to these gentlemen,” replied Barrois, “that he will give orders to be carried to the drawing-room.”\n\nAstonishment was at its height. Something like a smile was perceptible on Madame de Villefort’s countenance. Valentine instinctively raised her eyes, as if to thank heaven.\n\n“Pray go, Valentine,” said; M. de Villefort, “and see what this new fancy of your grandfather’s is.” Valentine rose quickly, and was hastening joyfully towards the door, when M. de Villefort altered his intention.\n\n“Stop,” said he; “I will go with you.”\n\n“Excuse me, sir,” said Franz, “since M. Noirtier sent for me, I am ready to attend to his wish; besides, I shall be happy to pay my respects to him, not having yet had the honor of doing so.”\n\n“Pray, sir,” said Villefort with marked uneasiness, “do not disturb yourself.”\n40032m\n\n“Forgive me, sir,” said Franz in a resolute tone. “I would not lose this opportunity of proving to M. Noirtier how wrong it would be of him to encourage feelings of dislike to me, which I am determined to conquer, whatever they may be, by my devotion.”\n\nAnd without listening to Villefort he arose, and followed Valentine, who was running downstairs with the joy of a shipwrecked mariner who finds a rock to cling to. M. de Villefort followed them. Château-Renaud and Morcerf exchanged a third look of still increasing wonder.\n\n# Chapter 75. A Signed Statement\n\nNoirtier was prepared to receive them, dressed in black, and installed in his armchair. When the three persons he expected had entered, he looked at the door, which his valet immediately closed.\n\n“Listen,” whispered Villefort to Valentine, who could not conceal her joy; “if M. Noirtier wishes to communicate anything which would delay your marriage, I forbid you to understand him.”\n\nValentine blushed, but did not answer. Villefort, approached Noirtier.\n\n“Here is M. Franz d’Épinay,” said he; “you requested to see him. We have all wished for this interview, and I trust it will convince you how ill-formed are your objections to Valentine’s marriage.”\n\nNoirtier answered only by a look which made Villefort’s blood run cold. He motioned to Valentine to approach. In a moment, thanks to her habit of conversing with her grandfather, she understood that he asked for a key. Then his eye was fixed on the drawer of a small chest between the windows. She opened the drawer, and found a key; and, understanding that was what he wanted, again watched his eyes, which turned toward an old secretaire which had been neglected for many years and was supposed to contain nothing but useless documents.\n\n“Shall I open the secretaire?” asked Valentine.\n\n“Yes,” said the old man.\n\n“And the drawers?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Those at the side?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“The middle one?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nValentine opened it and drew out a bundle of papers. “Is that what you wish for?” asked she.\n\n“No.”\n\nShe took successively all the other papers out till the drawer was empty. “But there are no more,” said she. Noirtier’s eye was fixed on the dictionary.\n\n“Yes, I understand, grandfather,” said the young girl.\n\n40036m\nShe pointed to each letter of the alphabet. At the letter S the old man stopped her. She opened, and found the word “secret.”\n\n“Ah! is there a secret spring?” said Valentine.\n\n“Yes,” said Noirtier.\n\n“And who knows it?” Noirtier looked at the door where the servant had gone out.\n\n“Barrois?” said she.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Shall I call him?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nValentine went to the door, and called Barrois. Villefort’s impatience during this scene made the perspiration roll from his forehead, and Franz was stupefied. The old servant came.\n\n“Barrois,” said Valentine, “my grandfather has told me to open that drawer in the secretaire, but there is a secret spring in it, which you know—will you open it?”\n\nBarrois looked at the old man. “Obey,” said Noirtier’s intelligent eye. Barrois touched a spring, the false bottom came out, and they saw a bundle of papers tied with a black string.\n\n“Is that what you wish for?” said Barrois.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Shall I give these papers to M. de Villefort?”\n“No.”\n\n“To Mademoiselle Valentine?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“To M. Franz d’Épinay?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nFranz, astonished, advanced a step. “To me, sir?” said he.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nFranz took them from Barrois and casting a glance at the cover, read:\n\n“‘To be given, after my death, to General Durand, who shall bequeath the packet to his son, with an injunction to preserve it as containing an important document.’\n\n“Well, sir,” asked Franz, “what do you wish me to do with this paper?”\n\n“To preserve it, sealed up as it is, doubtless,” said the procureur.\n\n“No,” replied Noirtier eagerly.\n\n“Do you wish him to read it?” said Valentine.\n\n“Yes,” replied the old man.\n\n“You understand, baron, my grandfather wishes you to read this paper,” said Valentine.\n\n“Then let us sit down,” said Villefort impatiently, “for it will take some time.”\n\n“Sit down,” said the old man. Villefort took a chair, but Valentine remained standing by her father’s side, and Franz before him, holding the mysterious paper in his hand. “Read,” said the old man. Franz untied it, and in the midst of the most profound silence read:\n40038m\n\n“‘_Extract of the report of a meeting of the Bonapartist Club in the Rue Saint-Jacques, held February 5th, 1815_.’”\n\nFranz stopped. “February 5th, 1815!” said he; “it is the day my father was murdered.” Valentine and Villefort were dumb; the eye of the old man alone seemed to say clearly, “Go on.”\n\n“But it was on leaving this club,” said he, “my father disappeared.”\nNoirtier’s eye continued to say, “Read.” He resumed:—\n\n“‘The undersigned Louis-Jacques Beaurepaire, lieutenant-colonel of artillery, Étienne Duchampy, general of brigade, and Claude Lecharpal, keeper of woods and forests, declare, that on the 4th of February, a letter arrived from the Island of Elba, recommending to the kindness and the confidence of the Bonapartist Club, General Flavien de Quesnel, who having served the emperor from 1804 to 1814 was supposed to be devoted to the interests of the Napoleon dynasty, notwithstanding the title of baron which Louis XVIII. had just granted to him with his estate of Épinay.\n\n400340m\n“‘A note was in consequence addressed to General de Quesnel, begging him to be present at the meeting next day, the 5th. The note indicated neither the street nor the number of the house where the meeting was to be held; it bore no signature, but it announced to the general that someone would call for him if he would be ready at nine o’clock. The meetings were always held from that time till midnight. At nine o’clock the president of the club presented himself; the general was ready, the president informed him that one of the conditions of his introduction was that he should be eternally ignorant of the place of meeting, and that he would allow his eyes to be bandaged, swearing that he would not endeavor to take off the bandage. General de Quesnel accepted the condition, and promised on his honor not to seek to discover the road they took. The general’s carriage was ready, but the president told him it was impossible for him to use it, since it was useless to blindfold the master if the coachman knew through what streets he went. “What must be done then?” asked the general.—“I have my carriage here,” said the president.\n\n“‘“Have you, then, so much confidence in your servant that you can intrust him with a secret you will not allow me to know?”\n\n“‘“Our coachman is a member of the club,” said the president; “we shall be driven by a State-Councillor.”\n\n“‘“Then we run another risk,” said the general, laughing, “that of being upset.” We insert this joke to prove that the general was not in the least compelled to attend the meeting, but that he came willingly. When they were seated in the carriage the president reminded the general of his promise to allow his eyes to be bandaged, to which he made no opposition. On the road the president thought he saw the general make an attempt to remove the handkerchief, and reminded him of his oath. “Sure enough,” said the general. The carriage stopped at an alley leading out of the Rue Saint-Jacques. The general alighted, leaning on the arm of the president, of whose dignity he was not aware, considering him simply as a member of the club; they went through the alley, mounted a flight of stairs, and entered the assembly-room.\n\n“‘The deliberations had already begun. The members, apprised of the sort of presentation which was to be made that evening, were all in attendance. When in the middle of the room the general was invited to remove his bandage, he did so immediately, and was surprised to see so many well-known faces in a society of whose existence he had till then been ignorant. They questioned him as to his sentiments, but he contented himself with answering, that the letters from the Island of Elba ought to have informed them——’”\n\nFranz interrupted himself by saying, “My father was a royalist; they need not have asked his sentiments, which were well known.”\n\n“And hence,” said Villefort, “arose my affection for your father, my dear M. Franz. Opinions held in common are a ready bond of union.”\n\n“Read again,” said the old man.\n\nFranz continued:\n\n“‘The president then sought to make him speak more explicitly, but M. de Quesnel replied that he wished first to know what they wanted with him. He was then informed of the contents of the letter from the Island of Elba, in which he was recommended to the club as a man who would be likely to advance the interests of their party. One paragraph spoke of the return of Bonaparte and promised another letter and further details, on the arrival of the _Pharaon_ belonging to the shipbuilder Morrel, of Marseilles, whose captain was entirely devoted to the emperor. During all this time, the general, on whom they thought to have relied as on a brother, manifested evidently signs of discontent and repugnance. When the reading was finished, he remained silent, with knitted brows.\n\n“‘“Well,” asked the president, “what do you say to this letter, general?”\n\n“‘“I say that it is too soon after declaring myself for Louis XVIII. to break my vow in behalf of the ex-emperor.” This answer was too clear to permit of any mistake as to his sentiments. “General,” said the president, “we acknowledge no King Louis XVIII., or an ex-emperor, but his majesty the emperor and king, driven from France, which is his kingdom, by violence and treason.”\n\n“‘“Excuse me, gentlemen,” said the general; “you may not acknowledge Louis XVIII., but I do, as he has made me a baron and a field-marshal, and I shall never forget that for these two titles I am indebted to his happy return to France.”\n\n“‘“Sir,” said the president, rising with gravity, “be careful what you say; your words clearly show us that they are deceived concerning you in the Island of Elba, and have deceived us! The communication has been made to you in consequence of the confidence placed in you, and which does you honor. Now we discover our error; a title and promotion attach you to the government we wish to overturn. We will not constrain you to help us; we enroll no one against his conscience, but we will compel you to act generously, even if you are not disposed to do so.”\n\n“‘“You would call acting generously, knowing your conspiracy and not informing against you, that is what I should call becoming your accomplice. You see I am more candid than you.”’”\n\n“Ah, my father!” said Franz, interrupting himself. “I understand now why they murdered him.” Valentine could not help casting one glance towards the young man, whose filial enthusiasm it was delightful to behold. Villefort walked to and fro behind them. Noirtier watched the expression of each one, and preserved his dignified and commanding attitude. Franz returned to the manuscript, and continued:\n\n“‘“Sir,” said the president, “you have been invited to join this assembly—you were not forced here; it was proposed to you to come blindfolded—you accepted. When you complied with this twofold request you well knew we did not wish to secure the throne of Louis XVIII., or we should not take so much care to avoid the vigilance of the police. It would be conceding too much to allow you to put on a mask to aid you in the discovery of our secret, and then to remove it that you may ruin those who have confided in you. No, no, you must first say if you declare yourself for the king of a day who now reigns, or for his majesty the emperor.”\n\n“‘“I am a royalist,” replied the general; “I have taken the oath of allegiance to Louis XVIII., and I will adhere to it.” These words were followed by a general murmur, and it was evident that several of the members were discussing the propriety of making the general repent of his rashness.\n\n“‘The president again arose, and having imposed silence, said,—“Sir, you are too serious and too sensible a man not to understand the consequences of our present situation, and your candor has already dictated to us the conditions which remain for us to offer you.” The general, putting his hand on his sword, exclaimed,—“If you talk of honor, do not begin by disavowing its laws, and impose nothing by violence.”\n\n“‘“And you, sir,” continued the president, with a calmness still more terrible than the general’s anger, “I advise you not to touch your sword.” The general looked around him with slight uneasiness; however he did not yield, but calling up all his fortitude, said,—“I will not swear.”\n\n“‘“Then you must die,” replied the president calmly. M. d’Épinay became very pale; he looked round him a second time, several members of the club were whispering, and getting their arms from under their cloaks. “General,” said the president, “do not alarm yourself; you are among men of honor who will use every means to convince you before resorting to the last extremity, but as you have said, you are among conspirators, you are in possession of our secret, and you must restore it to us.” A significant silence followed these words, and as the general did not reply,—“Close the doors,” said the president to the door-keeper.\n40042m\n\n“‘The same deadly silence succeeded these words. Then the general advanced, and making a violent effort to control his feelings,—“I have a son,” said he, “and I ought to think of him, finding myself among assassins.”\n\n“‘“General,” said the chief of the assembly, “one man may insult fifty—it is the privilege of weakness. But he does wrong to use his privilege. Follow my advice, swear, and do not insult.” The general, again daunted by the superiority of the chief, hesitated a moment; then advancing to the president’s desk,—“What is the form, said he.\n\n“‘“It is this:—‘I swear by my honor not to reveal to anyone what I have seen and heard on the 5th of February, 1815, between nine and ten o’clock in the evening; and I plead guilty of death should I ever violate this oath.’” The general appeared to be affected by a nervous tremor, which prevented his answering for some moments; then, overcoming his manifest repugnance, he pronounced the required oath, but in so low a tone as to be scarcely audible to the majority of the members, who insisted on his repeating it clearly and distinctly, which he did.\n\n“‘“Now am I at liberty to retire?” said the general. The president rose, appointed three members to accompany him, and got into the carriage with the general after bandaging his eyes. One of those three members was the coachman who had driven them there. The other members silently dispersed. “Where do you wish to be taken?” asked the president.—“Anywhere out of your presence,” replied M. d’Épinay. “Beware, sir,” replied the president, “you are no longer in the assembly, and have only to do with individuals; do not insult them unless you wish to be held responsible.” But instead of listening, M. d’Épinay went on,—“You are still as brave in your carriage as in your assembly because you are still four against one.” The president stopped the coach. They were at that part of the Quai des Ormes where the steps lead down to the river. “Why do you stop here?” asked d’Épinay.\n\n“‘“Because, sir,” said the president, “you have insulted a man, and that man will not go one step farther without demanding honorable reparation.”\n\n“‘“Another method of assassination?” said the general, shrugging his shoulders.\n\n“‘“Make no noise, sir, unless you wish me to consider you as one of the men of whom you spoke just now as cowards, who take their weakness for a shield. You are alone, one alone shall answer you; you have a sword by your side, I have one in my cane; you have no witness, one of these gentlemen will serve you. Now, if you please, remove your bandage.” The general tore the handkerchief from his eyes. “At last,” said he, “I shall know with whom I have to do.” They opened the door and the four men alighted.’”\n\nFranz again interrupted himself, and wiped the cold drops from his brow; there was something awful in hearing the son read aloud in trembling pallor these details of his father’s death, which had hitherto been a mystery. Valentine clasped her hands as if in prayer. Noirtier looked at Villefort with an almost sublime expression of contempt and pride.\n\nFranz continued:\n\n“‘It was, as we said, the fifth of February. For three days the mercury had been five or six degrees below freezing and the steps were covered with ice. The general was stout and tall, the president offered him the side of the railing to assist him in getting down. The two witnesses followed. It was a dark night. The ground from the steps to the river was covered with snow and hoarfrost, the water of the river looked black and deep. One of the seconds went for a lantern in a coal-barge near, and by its light they examined the weapons. The president’s sword, which was simply, as he had said, one he carried in his cane, was five inches shorter than the general’s, and had no guard. The general proposed to cast lots for the swords, but the president said it was he who had given the provocation, and when he had given it he had supposed each would use his own arms. The witnesses endeavored to insist, but the president bade them be silent. The lantern was placed on the ground, the two adversaries took their stations, and the duel began. The light made the two swords appear like flashes of lightning; as for the men, they were scarcely perceptible, the darkness was so great.\n40044m\n\n“‘General d’Épinay passed for one of the best swordsmen in the army, but he was pressed so closely in the onset that he missed his aim and fell. The witnesses thought he was dead, but his adversary, who knew he had not struck him, offered him the assistance of his hand to rise. The circumstance irritated instead of calming the general, and he rushed on his adversary. But his opponent did not allow his guard to be broken. He received him on his sword and three times the general drew back on finding himself too closely engaged, and then returned to the charge. At the third he fell again. They thought he slipped, as at first, and the witnesses, seeing he did not move, approached and endeavored to raise him, but the one who passed his arm around the body found it was moistened with blood. The general, who had almost fainted, revived. “Ah,” said he, “they have sent some fencing-master to fight with me.” The president, without answering, approached the witness who held the lantern, and raising his sleeve, showed him two wounds he had received in his arm; then opening his coat, and unbuttoning his waistcoat, displayed his side, pierced with a third wound. Still he had not even uttered a sigh. General d’Épinay died five minutes after.’”\n\nFranz read these last words in a voice so choked that they were hardly audible, and then stopped, passing his hand over his eyes as if to dispel a cloud; but after a moment’s silence, he continued:\n\n“‘The president went up the steps, after pushing his sword into his cane; a track of blood on the snow marked his course. He had scarcely arrived at the top when he heard a heavy splash in the water—it was the general’s body, which the witnesses had just thrown into the river after ascertaining that he was dead. The general fell, then, in a loyal duel, and not in ambush as it might have been reported. In proof of this we have signed this paper to establish the truth of the facts, lest the moment should arrive when either of the actors in this terrible scene should be accused of premeditated murder or of infringement of the laws of honor.\n\n“‘Signed, Beaurepaire, Duchampy, and Lecharpal.’”\n\nWhen Franz had finished reading this account, so dreadful for a son; when Valentine, pale with emotion, had wiped away a tear; when Villefort, trembling, and crouched in a corner, had endeavored to lessen the storm by supplicating glances at the implacable old man,—\n\n“Sir,” said d’Épinay to Noirtier, “since you are well acquainted with all these details, which are attested by honorable signatures,—since you appear to take some interest in me, although you have only manifested it hitherto by causing me sorrow, refuse me not one final satisfaction—tell me the name of the president of the club, that I may at least know who killed my father.”\n\nVillefort mechanically felt for the handle of the door; Valentine, who understood sooner than anyone her grandfather’s answer, and who had often seen two scars upon his right arm, drew back a few steps.\n\n“Mademoiselle,” said Franz, turning towards Valentine, “unite your efforts with mine to find out the name of the man who made me an orphan at two years of age.” Valentine remained dumb and motionless.\n\n“Hold, sir,” said Villefort, “do not prolong this dreadful scene. The names have been purposely concealed; my father himself does not know who this president was, and if he knows, he cannot tell you; proper names are not in the dictionary.”\n\n“Oh, misery,” cried Franz: “the only hope which sustained me and enabled me to read to the end was that of knowing, at least, the name of him who killed my father! Sir, sir,” cried he, turning to Noirtier, “do what you can—make me understand in some way!”\n\n“Yes,” replied Noirtier.\n\n“Oh, mademoiselle, mademoiselle!” cried Franz, “your grandfather says he can indicate the person. Help me,—lend me your assistance!”\n\nNoirtier looked at the dictionary. Franz took it with a nervous trembling, and repeated the letters of the alphabet successively, until he came to M. At that letter the old man signified “Yes.”\n\n“M,” repeated Franz. The young man’s finger, glided over the words, but at each one Noirtier answered by a negative sign. Valentine hid her head between her hands. At length, Franz arrived at the word MYSELF.\n\n40046m\n\n“Yes!”\n“You!” cried Franz, whose hair stood on end; “you, M. Noirtier—you killed my father?”\n\n“Yes!” replied Noirtier, fixing a majestic look on the young man. Franz fell powerless on a chair; Villefort opened the door and escaped, for the idea had entered his mind to stifle the little remaining life in the heart of this terrible old man.\n\n# Chapter 76. Progress of Cavalcanti the Younger\n\nMeanwhile M. Cavalcanti the elder had returned to his service, not in the army of his majesty the Emperor of Austria, but at the gaming-table of the baths of Lucca, of which he was one of the most assiduous courtiers. He had spent every farthing that had been allowed for his journey as a reward for the majestic and solemn manner in which he had maintained his assumed character of father.\n\nM. Andrea at his departure inherited all the papers which proved that he had indeed the honor of being the son of the Marquis Bartolomeo and the Marchioness Oliva Corsinari. He was now fairly launched in that Parisian society which gives such ready access to foreigners, and treats them, not as they really are, but as they wish to be considered. Besides, what is required of a young man in Paris? To speak its language tolerably, to make a good appearance, to be a good gamester, and to pay in cash. They are certainly less particular with a foreigner than with a Frenchman. Andrea had, then, in a fortnight, attained a very fair position. He was called count, he was said to possess 50,000 livres per annum; and his father’s immense riches, buried in the quarries of Saravezza, were a constant theme. A learned man, before whom the last circumstance was mentioned as a fact, declared he had seen the quarries in question, which gave great weight to assertions hitherto somewhat doubtful, but which now assumed the garb of reality.\n\nSuch was the state of society in Paris at the period we bring before our readers, when Monte Cristo went one evening to pay M. Danglars a visit. M. Danglars was out, but the count was asked to go and see the baroness, and he accepted the invitation. It was never without a nervous shudder, since the dinner at Auteuil, and the events which followed it, that Madame Danglars heard Monte Cristo’s name announced. If he did not come, the painful sensation became most intense; if, on the contrary, he appeared, his noble countenance, his brilliant eyes, his amiability, his polite attention even towards Madame Danglars, soon dispelled every impression of fear. It appeared impossible to the baroness that a man of such delightfully pleasing manners should entertain evil designs against her; besides, the most corrupt minds only suspect evil when it would answer some interested end—useless injury is repugnant to every mind.\n40048m\n\nWhen Monte Cristo entered the boudoir, to which we have already once introduced our readers, and where the baroness was examining some drawings, which her daughter passed to her after having looked at them with M. Cavalcanti, his presence soon produced its usual effect, and it was with smiles that the baroness received the count, although she had been a little disconcerted at the announcement of his name. The latter took in the whole scene at a glance.\n\nThe baroness was partially reclining on a sofa, Eugénie sat near her, and Cavalcanti was standing. Cavalcanti, dressed in black, like one of Goethe’s heroes, with varnished shoes and white silk open-worked stockings, passed a white and tolerably nice-looking hand through his light hair, and so displayed a sparkling diamond, that in spite of Monte Cristo’s advice the vain young man had been unable to resist putting on his little finger. This movement was accompanied by killing glances at Mademoiselle Danglars, and by sighs launched in the same direction.\n\nMademoiselle Danglars was still the same—cold, beautiful, and satirical. Not one of these glances, nor one sigh, was lost on her; they might have been said to fall on the shield of Minerva, which some philosophers assert protected sometimes the breast of Sappho. Eugénie bowed coldly to the count, and availed herself of the first moment when the conversation became earnest to escape to her study, whence very soon two cheerful and noisy voices being heard in connection with occasional notes of the piano assured Monte Cristo that Mademoiselle Danglars preferred to his society and to that of M. Cavalcanti the company of Mademoiselle Louise d’Armilly, her singing teacher.\n\nIt was then, especially while conversing with Madame Danglars, and apparently absorbed by the charm of the conversation, that the count noticed M. Andrea Cavalcanti’s solicitude, his manner of listening to the music at the door he dared not pass, and of manifesting his admiration.\n\nThe banker soon returned. His first look was certainly directed towards Monte Cristo, but the second was for Andrea. As for his wife, he bowed to her, as some husbands do to their wives, but in a way that bachelors will never comprehend, until a very extensive code is published on conjugal life.\n\n“Have not the ladies invited you to join them at the piano?” said Danglars to Andrea.\n\n“Alas, no, sir,” replied Andrea with a sigh, still more remarkable than the former ones. Danglars immediately advanced towards the door and opened it.\n\n40050m\nThe two young ladies were seen seated on the same chair, at the piano, accompanying themselves, each with one hand, a fancy to which they had accustomed themselves, and performed admirably. Mademoiselle d’Armilly, whom they then perceived through the open doorway, formed with Eugénie one of the _tableaux vivants_ of which the Germans are so fond. She was somewhat beautiful, and exquisitely formed—a little fairy-like figure, with large curls falling on her neck, which was rather too long, as Perugino sometimes makes his Virgins, and her eyes dull from fatigue. She was said to have a weak chest, and like Antonia in the _Cremona Violin_, she would die one day while singing.\n\nMonte Cristo cast one rapid and curious glance round this sanctum; it was the first time he had ever seen Mademoiselle d’Armilly, of whom he had heard much.\n\n“Well,” said the banker to his daughter, “are we then all to be excluded?”\n\nHe then led the young man into the study, and either by chance or manœuvre the door was partially closed after Andrea, so that from the place where they sat neither the Count nor the baroness could see anything; but as the banker had accompanied Andrea, Madame Danglars appeared to take no notice of it.\n\nThe count soon heard Andrea’s voice, singing a Corsican song, accompanied by the piano. While the count smiled at hearing this song, which made him lose sight of Andrea in the recollection of Benedetto, Madame Danglars was boasting to Monte Cristo of her husband’s strength of mind, who that very morning had lost three or four hundred thousand francs by a failure at Milan. The praise was well deserved, for had not the count heard it from the baroness, or by one of those means by which he knew everything, the baron’s countenance would not have led him to suspect it.\n\n“Hem,” thought Monte Cristo, “he begins to conceal his losses; a month since he boasted of them.”\n\nThen aloud,—“Oh, madame, M. Danglars is so skilful, he will soon regain at the Bourse what he loses elsewhere.”\n\n“I see that you participate in a prevalent error,” said Madame Danglars.\n\n“What is it?” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“That M. Danglars speculates, whereas he never does.”\n\n“Truly, madame, I recollect M. Debray told me——apropos, what has become of him? I have seen nothing of him the last three or four days.”\n\n“Nor I,” said Madame Danglars; “but you began a sentence, sir, and did not finish.”\n\n“Which?”\n“M. Debray had told you——”\n\n“Ah, yes; he told me it was you who sacrificed to the demon of speculation.”\n\n“I was once very fond of it, but I do not indulge now.”\n\n“Then you are wrong, madame. Fortune is precarious; and if I were a woman and fate had made me a banker’s wife, whatever might be my confidence in my husband’s good fortune, still in speculation you know there is great risk. Well, I would secure for myself a fortune independent of him, even if I acquired it by placing my interests in hands unknown to him.” Madame Danglars blushed, in spite of all her efforts.\n\n“Stay,” said Monte Cristo, as though he had not observed her confusion, “I have heard of a lucky hit that was made yesterday on the Neapolitan bonds.”\n\n“I have none—nor have I ever possessed any; but really we have talked long enough of money, count, we are like two stockbrokers; have you heard how fate is persecuting the poor Villeforts?”\n\n“What has happened?” said the count, simulating total ignorance.\n\n“You know the Marquis of Saint-Méran died a few days after he had set out on his journey to Paris, and the marchioness a few days after her arrival?”\n\n“Yes,” said Monte Cristo, “I have heard that; but, as Claudius said to Hamlet, ‘it is a law of nature; their fathers died before them, and they mourned their loss; they will die before their children, who will, in their turn, grieve for them.’”\n\n“But that is not all.”\n\n“Not all!”\n\n“No; they were going to marry their daughter——”\n\n“To M. Franz d’Épinay. Is it broken off?”\n\n“Yesterday morning, it appears, Franz declined the honor.”\n\n“Indeed? And is the reason known?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“How extraordinary! And how does M. de Villefort bear it?”\n\n“As usual. Like a philosopher.”\n\nDanglars returned at this moment alone.\n\n“Well,” said the baroness, “do you leave M. Cavalcanti with your daughter?”\n\n“And Mademoiselle d’Armilly,” said the banker; “do you consider her no one?” Then, turning to Monte Cristo, he said, “Prince Cavalcanti is a charming young man, is he not? But is he really a prince?”\n\n“I will not answer for it,” said Monte Cristo. “His father was introduced to me as a marquis, so he ought to be a count; but I do not think he has much claim to that title.”\n\n“Why?” said the banker. “If he is a prince, he is wrong not to maintain his rank; I do not like anyone to deny his origin.”\n\n“Oh, you are a thorough democrat,” said Monte Cristo, smiling.\n\n“But do you see to what you are exposing yourself?” said the baroness. “If, perchance, M. de Morcerf came, he would find M. Cavalcanti in that room, where he, the betrothed of Eugénie, has never been admitted.”\n\n“You may well say, perchance,” replied the banker; “for he comes so seldom, it would seem only chance that brings him.”\n\n“But should he come and find that young man with your daughter, he might be displeased.”\n\n“He? You are mistaken. M. Albert would not do us the honor to be jealous; he does not like Eugénie sufficiently. Besides, I care not for his displeasure.”\n\n“Still, situated as we are——”\n\n“Yes, do you know how we are situated? At his mother’s ball he danced once with Eugénie, and M. Cavalcanti three times, and he took no notice of it.”\n\nThe valet announced the Vicomte Albert de Morcerf. The baroness rose hastily, and was going into the study, when Danglars stopped her.\n\n“Let her alone,” said he.\n\nShe looked at him in amazement. Monte Cristo appeared to be unconscious of what passed. Albert entered, looking very handsome and in high spirits. He bowed politely to the baroness, familiarly to Danglars, and affectionately to Monte Cristo. Then turning to the baroness: “May I ask how Mademoiselle Danglars is?” said he.\n\n“She is quite well,” replied Danglars quickly; “she is at the piano with M. Cavalcanti.”\n\nAlbert retained his calm and indifferent manner; he might feel perhaps annoyed, but he knew Monte Cristo’s eye was on him. “M. Cavalcanti has a fine tenor voice,” said he, “and Mademoiselle Eugénie a splendid soprano, and then she plays the piano like Thalberg. The concert must be a delightful one.”\n\n“They suit each other remarkably well,” said Danglars. Albert appeared not to notice this remark, which was, however, so rude that Madame Danglars blushed.\n\n“I, too,” said the young man, “am a musician—at least, my masters used to tell me so; but it is strange that my voice never would suit any other, and a soprano less than any.”\n\nDanglars smiled, and seemed to say, “It is of no consequence.” Then, hoping doubtless to effect his purpose, he said,—“The prince and my daughter were universally admired yesterday. You were not of the party, M. de Morcerf?”\n\n“What prince?” asked Albert.\n\n“Prince Cavalcanti,” said Danglars, who persisted in giving the young man that title.\n\n“Pardon me,” said Albert, “I was not aware that he was a prince. And Prince Cavalcanti sang with Mademoiselle Eugénie yesterday? It must have been charming, indeed. I regret not having heard them. But I was unable to accept your invitation, having promised to accompany my mother to a German concert given by the Baroness of Château-Renaud.”\n\nThis was followed by rather an awkward silence.\n\n“May I also be allowed,” said Morcerf, “to pay my respects to Mademoiselle Danglars?”\n\n“Wait a moment,” said the banker, stopping the young man; “do you hear that delightful cavatina? Ta, ta, ta, ti, ta, ti, ta, ta; it is charming, let them finish—one moment. Bravo, bravi, brava!” The banker was enthusiastic in his applause.\n\n40054m\n“Indeed,” said Albert, “it is exquisite; it is impossible to understand the music of his country better than Prince Cavalcanti does. You said prince, did you not? But he can easily become one, if he is not already; it is no uncommon thing in Italy. But to return to the charming musicians—you should give us a treat, Danglars, without telling them there is a stranger. Ask them to sing one more song; it is so delightful to hear music in the distance, when the musicians are unrestrained by observation.”\n\nDanglars was quite annoyed by the young man’s indifference. He took Monte Cristo aside.\n\n“What do you think of our lover?” said he.\n\n“He appears cool. But, then your word is given.”\n\n“Yes, doubtless I have promised to give my daughter to a man who loves her, but not to one who does not. See him there, cold as marble and proud like his father. If he were rich, if he had Cavalcanti’s fortune, that might be pardoned. _Ma foi_, I haven’t consulted my daughter; but if she has good taste——”\n\n“Oh,” said Monte Cristo, “my fondness may blind me, but I assure you I consider Morcerf a charming young man who will render your daughter happy and will sooner or later attain a certain amount of distinction, and his father’s position is good.”\n\n“Hem,” said Danglars.\n\n“Why do you doubt?”\n\n“The past—that obscurity on the past.”\n\n“But that does not affect the son.”\n\n“Very true.”\n\n“Now, I beg of you, don’t go off your head. It’s a month now that you have been thinking of this marriage, and you must see that it throws some responsibility on me, for it was at my house you met this young Cavalcanti, whom I do not really know at all.”\n\n“But I do.”\n\n“Have you made inquiry?”\n\n“Is there any need of that! Does not his appearance speak for him? And he is very rich.”\n\n“I am not so sure of that.”\n\n“And yet you said he had money.”\n\n“Fifty thousand livres—a mere trifle.”\n\n“He is well educated.”\n\n“Hem,” said Monte Cristo in his turn.\n\n“He is a musician.”\n\n“So are all Italians.”\n\n“Come, count, you do not do that young man justice.”\n\n“Well, I acknowledge it annoys me, knowing your connection with the Morcerf family, to see him throw himself in the way.” Danglars burst out laughing.\n\n“What a Puritan you are!” said he; “that happens every day.”\n\n“But you cannot break it off in this way; the Morcerfs are depending on this union.”\n\n“Indeed.”\n\n“Positively.”\n\n“Then let them explain themselves; you should give the father a hint, you are so intimate with the family.”\n\n“I?—where the devil did you find out that?”\n\n“At their ball; it was apparent enough. Why, did not the countess, the proud Mercédès, the disdainful Catalane, who will scarcely open her lips to her oldest acquaintances, take your arm, lead you into the garden, into the private walks, and remain there for half an hour?”\n\n“Ah, baron, baron,” said Albert, “you are not listening—what barbarism in a megalomaniac like you!”\n\n“Oh, don’t worry about me, Sir Mocker,” said Danglars; then turning to Monte Cristo he said:\n\n“But will you undertake to speak to the father?”\n\n“Willingly, if you wish it.”\n\n“But let it be done explicitly and positively. If he demands my daughter let him fix the day—declare his conditions; in short, let us either understand each other, or quarrel. You understand—no more delay.”\n\n“Yes, sir, I will give my attention to the subject.”\n\n“I do not say that I await with pleasure his decision, but I do await it. A banker must, you know, be a slave to his promise.” And Danglars sighed as M. Cavalcanti had done half an hour before.\n\n“Bravi! bravo! brava!” cried Morcerf, parodying the banker, as the selection came to an end. Danglars began to look suspiciously at Morcerf, when someone came and whispered a few words to him.\n\n“I shall soon return,” said the banker to Monte Cristo; “wait for me. I shall, perhaps, have something to say to you.” And he went out.\n\nThe baroness took advantage of her husband’s absence to push open the door of her daughter’s study, and M. Andrea, who was sitting before the piano with Mademoiselle Eugénie, started up like a jack-in-the-box. Albert bowed with a smile to Mademoiselle Danglars, who did not appear in the least disturbed, and returned his bow with her usual coolness. Cavalcanti was evidently embarrassed; he bowed to Morcerf, who replied with the most impertinent look possible. Then Albert launched out in praise of Mademoiselle Danglars’ voice, and on his regret, after what he had just heard, that he had been unable to be present the previous evening.\n\nCavalcanti, being left alone, turned to Monte Cristo.\n\n“Come,” said Madame Danglars, “leave music and compliments, and let us go and take tea.”\n\n“Come, Louise,” said Mademoiselle Danglars to her friend.\n\nThey passed into the next drawing-room, where tea was prepared. Just as they were beginning, in the English fashion, to leave the spoons in their cups, the door again opened and Danglars entered, visibly agitated. Monte Cristo observed it particularly, and by a look asked the banker for an explanation.\n\n“I have just received my courier from Greece,” said Danglars.\n\n“Ah, yes,” said the count; “that was the reason of your running away from us.”\n\n“Yes.”\n“How is King Otho getting on?” asked Albert in the most sprightly tone.\n\nDanglars cast another suspicious look towards him without answering, and Monte Cristo turned away to conceal the expression of pity which passed over his features, but which was gone in a moment.\n\n“We shall go together, shall we not?” said Albert to the count.\n\n“If you like,” replied the latter.\n\nAlbert could not understand the banker’s look, and turning to Monte Cristo, who understood it perfectly,—“Did you see,” said he, “how he looked at me?”\n\n“Yes,” said the count; “but did you think there was anything particular in his look?”\n\n“Indeed, I did; and what does he mean by his news from Greece?”\n\n“How can I tell you?”\n\n“Because I imagine you have correspondents in that country.”\n\nMonte Cristo smiled significantly.\n\n“Stop,” said Albert, “here he comes. I shall compliment Mademoiselle Danglars on her cameo, while the father talks to you.”\n\n“If you compliment her at all, let it be on her voice, at least,” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“No, everyone would do that.”\n\n“My dear viscount, you are dreadfully impertinent.”\n\nAlbert advanced towards Eugénie, smiling.\n\nMeanwhile, Danglars, stooping to Monte Cristo’s ear, “Your advice was excellent,” said he; “there is a whole history connected with the names Fernand and Yanina.”\n\n“Indeed?” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“Yes, I will tell you all; but take away the young man; I cannot endure his presence.”\n\n“He is going with me. Shall I send the father to you?”\n\n“Immediately.”\n\n“Very well.” The count made a sign to Albert and they bowed to the ladies, and took their leave, Albert perfectly indifferent to Mademoiselle Danglars’ contempt, Monte Cristo reiterating his advice to Madame Danglars on the prudence a banker’s wife should exercise in providing for the future.\n\nM. Cavalcanti remained master of the field.\n\n# Chapter 77. Haydée\n\nScarcely had the count’s horses cleared the angle of the boulevard, when Albert, turning towards the count, burst into a loud fit of laughter—much too loud in fact not to give the idea of its being rather forced and unnatural.\n\n“Well,” said he, “I will ask you the same question which Charles IX. put to Catherine de’ Medici, after the massacre of Saint Bartholomew: ‘How have I played my little part?’”\n\n“To what do you allude?” asked Monte Cristo.\n\n“To the installation of my rival at M. Danglars’.”\n\n“What rival?”\n\n“_Ma foi!_ what rival? Why, your protégé, M. Andrea Cavalcanti!”\n\n“Ah, no joking, viscount, if you please; I do not patronize M. Andrea—at least, not as concerns M. Danglars.”\n\n“And you would be to blame for not assisting him, if the young man really needed your help in that quarter, but, happily for me, he can dispense with it.”\n\n“What, do you think he is paying his addresses?”\n\n“I am certain of it; his languishing looks and modulated tones when addressing Mademoiselle Danglars fully proclaim his intentions. He aspires to the hand of the proud Eugénie.”\n\n“What does that signify, so long as they favor your suit?”\n\n“But it is not the case, my dear count: on the contrary. I am repulsed on all sides.”\n\n“What!”\n\n“It is so indeed; Mademoiselle Eugénie scarcely answers me, and Mademoiselle d’Armilly, her confidant, does not speak to me at all.”\n\n“But the father has the greatest regard possible for you,” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“He? Oh, no, he has plunged a thousand daggers into my heart, tragedy-weapons, I own, which instead of wounding sheathe their points in their own handles, but daggers which he nevertheless believed to be real and deadly.”\n\n“Jealousy indicates affection.”\n\n“True; but I am not jealous.”\n“He is.”\n\n“Of whom?—of Debray?”\n\n“No, of you.”\n\n“Of me? I will engage to say that before a week is past the door will be closed against me.”\n\n“You are mistaken, my dear viscount.”\n\n“Prove it to me.”\n\n“Do you wish me to do so?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Well, I am charged with the commission of endeavoring to induce the Comte de Morcerf to make some definite arrangement with the baron.”\n\n“By whom are you charged?”\n\n“By the baron himself.”\n\n“Oh,” said Albert with all the cajolery of which he was capable. “You surely will not do that, my dear count?”\n\n“Certainly I shall, Albert, as I have promised to do it.”\n\n“Well,” said Albert, with a sigh, “it seems you are determined to marry me.”\n\n“I am determined to try and be on good terms with everybody, at all events,” said Monte Cristo. “But apropos of Debray, how is it that I have not seen him lately at the baron’s house?”\n\n“There has been a misunderstanding.”\n\n“What, with the baroness?”\n\n“No, with the baron.”\n\n“Has he perceived anything?”\n\n“Ah, that is a good joke!”\n\n“Do you think he suspects?” said Monte Cristo with charming artlessness.\n\n“Where have you come from, my dear count?” said Albert.\n\n“From Congo, if you will.”\n\n“It must be farther off than even that.”\n\n“But what do I know of your Parisian husbands?”\n\n“Oh, my dear count, husbands are pretty much the same everywhere; an individual husband of any country is a pretty fair specimen of the whole race.”\n\n“But then, what can have led to the quarrel between Danglars and Debray? They seemed to understand each other so well,” said Monte Cristo with renewed energy.\n\n“Ah, now you are trying to penetrate into the mysteries of Isis, in which I am not initiated. When M. Andrea Cavalcanti has become one of the family, you can ask him that question.”\n\nThe carriage stopped.\n\n“Here we are,” said Monte Cristo; “it is only half-past ten o’clock, come in.”\n\n“Certainly, I will.”\n\n“My carriage shall take you back.”\n\n“No, thank you; I gave orders for my _coupé_ to follow me.”\n\n“There it is, then,” said Monte Cristo, as he stepped out of the carriage. They both went into the house; the drawing-room was lighted up—they went in there. “You will make tea for us, Baptistin,” said the count. Baptistin left the room without waiting to answer, and in two seconds reappeared, bringing on a tray, all that his master had ordered, ready prepared, and appearing to have sprung from the ground, like the repasts which we read of in fairy tales.\n\n“Really, my dear count,” said Morcerf, “what I admire in you is, not so much your riches, for perhaps there are people even wealthier than yourself, nor is it only your wit, for Beaumarchais might have possessed as much,—but it is your manner of being served, without any questions, in a moment, in a second; it is as if they guessed what you wanted by your manner of ringing, and made a point of keeping everything you can possibly desire in constant readiness.”\n\n“What you say is perhaps true; they know my habits. For instance, you shall see; how do you wish to occupy yourself during tea-time?”\n\n“_Ma foi_, I should like to smoke.”\n\nMonte Cristo took the gong and struck it once. In about the space of a second a private door opened, and Ali appeared, bringing two chibouques filled with excellent latakia.\n\n“It is quite wonderful,” said Albert.\n\n“Oh no, it is as simple as possible,” replied Monte Cristo. “Ali knows I generally smoke while I am taking my tea or coffee; he has heard that I ordered tea, and he also knows that I brought you home with me; when I summoned him he naturally guessed the reason of my doing so, and as he comes from a country where hospitality is especially manifested through the medium of smoking, he naturally concludes that we shall smoke in company, and therefore brings two chibouques instead of one—and now the mystery is solved.”\n\n“Certainly you give a most commonplace air to your explanation, but it is not the less true that you——Ah, but what do I hear?” and Morcerf inclined his head towards the door, through which sounds seemed to issue resembling those of a guitar.\n\n“_Ma foi_, my dear viscount, you are fated to hear music this evening; you have only escaped from Mademoiselle Danglars’ piano, to be attacked by Haydée’s guzla.”\n\n“Haydée—what an adorable name! Are there, then, really women who bear the name of Haydée anywhere but in Byron’s poems?”\n\n“Certainly there are. Haydée is a very uncommon name in France, but is common enough in Albania and Epirus; it is as if you said, for example, Chastity, Modesty, Innocence,—it is a kind of baptismal name, as you Parisians call it.”\n\n“Oh, that is charming,” said Albert, “how I should like to hear my countrywomen called Mademoiselle Goodness, Mademoiselle Silence, Mademoiselle Christian Charity! Only think, then, if Mademoiselle Danglars, instead of being called Claire-Marie-Eugénie, had been named Mademoiselle Chastity-Modesty-Innocence Danglars; what a fine effect that would have produced on the announcement of her marriage!”\n\n“Hush,” said the count, “do not joke in so loud a tone; Haydée may hear you, perhaps.”\n\n“And you think she would be angry?”\n\n“No, certainly not,” said the count with a haughty expression.\n\n“She is very amiable, then, is she not?” said Albert.\n\n“It is not to be called amiability, it is her duty; a slave does not dictate to a master.”\n\n“Come; you are joking yourself now. Are there any more slaves to be had who bear this beautiful name?”\n\n“Undoubtedly.”\n\n“Really, count, you do nothing, and have nothing like other people. The slave of the Count of Monte Cristo! Why, it is a rank of itself in France, and from the way in which you lavish money, it is a place that must be worth a hundred thousand francs a year.”\n\n“A hundred thousand francs! The poor girl originally possessed much more than that; she was born to treasures in comparison with which those recorded in the _Thousand and One Nights_ would seem but poverty.”\n\n“She must be a princess then.”\n\n“You are right; and she is one of the greatest in her country too.”\n\n“I thought so. But how did it happen that such a great princess became a slave?”\n\n“How was it that Dionysius the Tyrant became a schoolmaster? The fortune of war, my dear viscount,—the caprice of fortune; that is the way in which these things are to be accounted for.”\n\n“And is her name a secret?”\n\n“As regards the generality of mankind it is; but not for you, my dear viscount, who are one of my most intimate friends, and on whose silence I feel I may rely, if I consider it necessary to enjoin it—may I not do so?”\n\n“Certainly; on my word of honor.”\n\n“You know the history of the Pasha of Yanina, do you not?”\n\n“Of Ali Tepelini?13 Oh, yes; it was in his service that my father made his fortune.”\n\n“True, I had forgotten that.”\n\n40062m\n“Well, what is Haydée to Ali Tepelini?”\n\n“Merely his daughter.”\n\n“What? the daughter of Ali Pasha?”\n\n“Of Ali Pasha and the beautiful Vasiliki.”\n\n“And your slave?”\n\n“_Ma foi_, yes.”\n\n“But how did she become so?”\n\n“Why, simply from the circumstance of my having bought her one day, as I was passing through the market at Constantinople.”\n\n“Wonderful! Really, my dear count, you seem to throw a sort of magic influence over all in which you are concerned; when I listen to you, existence no longer seems reality, but a waking dream. Now, I am perhaps going to make an imprudent and thoughtless request, but——”\n\n“Say on.”\n\n“But, since you go out with Haydée, and sometimes even take her to the Opera——”\n\n“Well?”\n\n“I think I may venture to ask you this favor.”\n\n“You may venture to ask me anything.”\n\n“Well then, my dear count, present me to your princess.”\n\n“I will do so; but on two conditions.”\n\n“I accept them at once.”\n\n“The first is, that you will never tell anyone that I have granted the interview.”\n\n“Very well,” said Albert, extending his hand; “I swear I will not.”\n\n“The second is, that you will not tell her that your father ever served hers.”\n\n“I give you my oath that I will not.”\n\n“Enough, viscount; you will remember those two vows, will you not? But I know you to be a man of honor.”\n\nThe count again struck the gong. Ali reappeared. “Tell Haydée,” said he, “that I will take coffee with her, and give her to understand that I desire permission to present one of my friends to her.”\n\nAli bowed and left the room.\n\n“Now, understand me,” said the count, “no direct questions, my dear Morcerf; if you wish to know anything, tell me, and I will ask her.”\n\n“Agreed.”\n\nAli reappeared for the third time, and drew back the tapestried hanging which concealed the door, to signify to his master and Albert that they were at liberty to pass on.\n\n“Let us go in,” said Monte Cristo.\n\nAlbert passed his hand through his hair, and curled his moustache, then, having satisfied himself as to his personal appearance, followed the count into the room, the latter having previously resumed his hat and gloves. Ali was stationed as a kind of advanced guard, and the door was kept by the three French attendants, commanded by Myrtho.\n\nHaydée was awaiting her visitors in the first room of her apartments, which was the drawing-room. Her large eyes were dilated with surprise and expectation, for it was the first time that any man, except Monte Cristo, had been accorded an entrance into her presence. She was sitting on a sofa placed in an angle of the room, with her legs crossed under her in the Eastern fashion, and seemed to have made for herself, as it were, a kind of nest in the rich Indian silks which enveloped her. Near her was the instrument on which she had just been playing; it was elegantly fashioned, and worthy of its mistress. On perceiving Monte Cristo, she arose and welcomed him with a smile peculiar to herself, expressive at once of the most implicit obedience and also of the deepest love. Monte Cristo advanced towards her and extended his hand, which she as usual raised to her lips.\n40064m\n\nAlbert had proceeded no farther than the door, where he remained rooted to the spot, being completely fascinated by the sight of such surpassing beauty, beheld as it was for the first time, and of which an inhabitant of more northern climes could form no adequate idea.\n\n“Whom do you bring?” asked the young girl in Romaic, of Monte Cristo; “is it a friend, a brother, a simple acquaintance, or an enemy.”\n\n“A friend,” said Monte Cristo in the same language.\n\n“What is his name?”\n\n“Count Albert; it is the same man whom I rescued from the hands of the banditti at Rome.”\n\n“In what language would you like me to converse with him?”\n\nMonte Cristo turned to Albert. “Do you know modern Greek,” asked he.\n\n“Alas! no,” said Albert; “nor even ancient Greek, my dear count; never had Homer or Plato a more unworthy scholar than myself.”\n\n“Then,” said Haydée, proving by her remark that she had quite understood Monte Cristo’s question and Albert’s answer, “then I will speak either in French or Italian, if my lord so wills it.”\n\nMonte Cristo reflected one instant. “You will speak in Italian,” said he.\n\nThen, turning towards Albert,—“It is a pity you do not understand either ancient or modern Greek, both of which Haydée speaks so fluently; the poor child will be obliged to talk to you in Italian, which will give you but a very false idea of her powers of conversation.”\n\nThe count made a sign to Haydée to address his visitor. “Sir,” she said to Morcerf, “you are most welcome as the friend of my lord and master.” This was said in excellent Tuscan, and with that soft Roman accent which makes the language of Dante as sonorous as that of Homer. Then, turning to Ali, she directed him to bring coffee and pipes, and when he had left the room to execute the orders of his young mistress she beckoned Albert to approach nearer to her. Monte Cristo and Morcerf drew their seats towards a small table, on which were arranged music, drawings, and vases of flowers. Ali then entered bringing coffee and chibouques; as to M. Baptistin, this portion of the building was interdicted to him. Albert refused the pipe which the Nubian offered him.\n\n“Oh, take it—take it,” said the count; “Haydée is almost as civilized as a Parisian; the smell of a Havana is disagreeable to her, but the tobacco of the East is a most delicious perfume, you know.”\n\nAli left the room. The cups of coffee were all prepared, with the addition of sugar, which had been brought for Albert. Monte Cristo and Haydée took the beverage in the original Arabian manner, that is to say, without sugar. Haydée took the porcelain cup in her little slender fingers and conveyed it to her mouth with all the innocent artlessness of a child when eating or drinking something which it likes. At this moment two women entered, bringing salvers filled with ices and sherbet, which they placed on two small tables appropriated to that purpose.\n\n“My dear host, and you, signora,” said Albert, in Italian, “excuse my apparent stupidity. I am quite bewildered, and it is natural that it should be so. Here I am in the heart of Paris; but a moment ago I heard the rumbling of the omnibuses and the tinkling of the bells of the lemonade-sellers, and now I feel as if I were suddenly transported to the East; not such as I have seen it, but such as my dreams have painted it. Oh, signora, if I could but speak Greek, your conversation, added to the fairy-scene which surrounds me, would furnish an evening of such delight as it would be impossible for me ever to forget.”\n\n“I speak sufficient Italian to enable me to converse with you, sir,” said Haydée quietly; “and if you like what is Eastern, I will do my best to secure the gratification of your tastes while you are here.”\n\n“On what subject shall I converse with her?” said Albert, in a low tone to Monte Cristo.\n\n“Just what you please; you may speak of her country and of her youthful reminiscences, or if you like it better you can talk of Rome, Naples, or Florence.”\n\n“Oh,” said Albert, “it is of no use to be in the company of a Greek if one converses just in the same style as with a Parisian; let me speak to her of the East.”\n\n“Do so then, for of all themes which you could choose that will be the most agreeable to her taste.”\n\nAlbert turned towards Haydée. “At what age did you leave Greece, signora?” asked he.\n\n“I left it when I was but five years old,” replied Haydée.\n\n“And have you any recollection of your country?”\n\n“When I shut my eyes and think, I seem to see it all again. The mind can see as well as the body. The body forgets sometimes; but the mind always remembers.”\n\n“And how far back into the past do your recollections extend?”\n\n“I could scarcely walk when my mother, who was called Vasiliki, which means royal,” said the young girl, tossing her head proudly, “took me by the hand, and after putting in our purse all the money we possessed, we went out, both covered with veils, to solicit alms for the prisoners, saying, ‘He who giveth to the poor lendeth to the Lord.’ Then when our purse was full we returned to the palace, and without saying a word to my father, we sent it to the convent, where it was divided amongst the prisoners.”\n\n“And how old were you at that time?”\n\n“I was three years old,” said Haydée.\n\n“Then you remember everything that went on about you from the time when you were three years old?” said Albert.\n\n“Everything.”\n\n“Count,” said Albert, in a low tone to Monte Cristo, “do allow the signora to tell me something of her history. You prohibited my mentioning my father’s name to her, but perhaps she will allude to him of her own accord in the course of the recital, and you have no idea how delighted I should be to hear our name pronounced by such beautiful lips.”\n\nMonte Cristo turned to Haydée, and with an expression of countenance which commanded her to pay the most implicit attention to his words, he said in Greek, “Πατρὸς μὲν ἄτην μήζε τὸ ὄνομα προδότου καὶ προδοσίαν εἰπὲ ἡμῖν,”—that is, “Tell us the fate of your father; but neither the name of the traitor nor the treason.” Haydée sighed deeply, and a shade of sadness clouded her beautiful brow.\n\n“What are you saying to her?” said Morcerf in an undertone.\n\n“I again reminded her that you were a friend, and that she need not conceal anything from you.”\n\n“Then,” said Albert, “this pious pilgrimage in behalf of the prisoners was your first remembrance; what is the next?”\n\n“Oh, then I remember as if it were but yesterday sitting under the shade of some sycamore-trees, on the borders of a lake, in the waters of which the trembling foliage was reflected as in a mirror. Under the oldest and thickest of these trees, reclining on cushions, sat my father; my mother was at his feet, and I, childlike, amused myself by playing with his long white beard which descended to his girdle, or with the diamond-hilt of the scimitar attached to his girdle. Then from time to time there came to him an Albanian who said something to which I paid no attention, but which he always answered in the same tone of voice, either ‘Kill,’ or ‘Pardon.’”\n\n“It is very strange,” said Albert, “to hear such words proceed from the mouth of anyone but an actress on the stage, and one needs constantly to be saying to one’s self, ‘This is no fiction, it is all reality,’ in order to believe it. And how does France appear in your eyes, accustomed as they have been to gaze on such enchanted scenes?”\n\n“I think it is a fine country,” said Haydée, “but I see France as it really is, because I look on it with the eyes of a woman; whereas my own country, which I can only judge of from the impression produced on my childish mind, always seems enveloped in a vague atmosphere, which is luminous or otherwise, according as my remembrances of it are sad or joyous.”\n\n“So young,” said Albert, forgetting at the moment the Count’s command that he should ask no questions of the slave herself, “is it possible that you can have known what suffering is except by name?”\n\nHaydée turned her eyes towards Monte Cristo, who, making at the same time some imperceptible sign, murmured:\n\n“Εἰπέ—speak.”\n\n“Nothing is ever so firmly impressed on the mind as the memory of our early childhood, and with the exception of the two scenes I have just described to you, all my earliest reminiscences are fraught with deepest sadness.”\n\n40068m\n“Speak, speak, signora,” said Albert, “I am listening with the most intense delight and interest to all you say.”\n\nHaydée answered his remark with a melancholy smile. “You wish me, then, to relate the history of my past sorrows?” said she.\n\n“I beg you to do so,” replied Albert.\n\n“Well, I was but four years old when one night I was suddenly awakened by my mother. We were in the palace of Yanina; she snatched me from the cushions on which I was sleeping, and on opening my eyes I saw hers filled with tears. She took me away without speaking. When I saw her weeping I began to cry too. ‘Hush, child!’ said she. At other times in spite of maternal endearments or threats, I had with a child’s caprice been accustomed to indulge my feelings of sorrow or anger by crying as much as I felt inclined; but on this occasion there was an intonation of such extreme terror in my mother’s voice when she enjoined me to silence, that I ceased crying as soon as her command was given. She bore me rapidly away.\n\n“I saw then that we were descending a large staircase; around us were all my mother’s servants carrying trunks, bags, ornaments, jewels, purses of gold, with which they were hurrying away in the greatest distraction.\n\n“Behind the women came a guard of twenty men armed with long guns and pistols, and dressed in the costume which the Greeks have assumed since they have again become a nation. You may imagine there was something startling and ominous,” said Haydée, shaking her head and turning pale at the mere remembrance of the scene, “in this long file of slaves and women only half-aroused from sleep, or at least so they appeared to me, who was myself scarcely awake. Here and there on the walls of the staircase, were reflected gigantic shadows, which trembled in the flickering light of the pine-torches till they seemed to reach to the vaulted roof above.\n\n“‘Quick!’ said a voice at the end of the gallery. This voice made everyone bow before it, resembling in its effect the wind passing over a field of wheat, by its superior strength forcing every ear to yield obeisance. As for me, it made me tremble. This voice was that of my father. He came last, clothed in his splendid robes and holding in his hand the carbine which your emperor presented him. He was leaning on the shoulder of his favorite Selim, and he drove us all before him, as a shepherd would his straggling flock. My father,” said Haydée, raising her head, “was that illustrious man known in Europe under the name of Ali Tepelini, pasha of Yanina, and before whom Turkey trembled.”\n\nAlbert, without knowing why, started on hearing these words pronounced with such a haughty and dignified accent; it appeared to him as if there was something supernaturally gloomy and terrible in the expression which gleamed from the brilliant eyes of Haydée at this moment; she appeared like a Pythoness evoking a spectre, as she recalled to his mind the remembrance of the fearful death of this man, to the news of which all Europe had listened with horror.\n\n“Soon,” said Haydée, “we halted on our march, and found ourselves on the borders of a lake. My mother pressed me to her throbbing heart, and at the distance of a few paces I saw my father, who was glancing anxiously around. Four marble steps led down to the water’s edge, and below them was a boat floating on the tide.\n\n40070m\n“From where we stood I could see in the middle of the lake a large blank mass; it was the kiosk to which we were going. This kiosk appeared to me to be at a considerable distance, perhaps on account of the darkness of the night, which prevented any object from being more than partially discerned. We stepped into the boat. I remember well that the oars made no noise whatever in striking the water, and when I leaned over to ascertain the cause I saw that they were muffled with the sashes of our Palikares.14 Besides the rowers, the boat contained only the women, my father, mother, Selim, and myself. The Palikares had remained on the shore of the lake, ready to cover our retreat; they were kneeling on the lowest of the marble steps, and in that manner intended making a rampart of the three others, in case of pursuit. Our bark flew before the wind. ‘Why does the boat go so fast?’ asked I of my mother.\n\n“‘Silence, child! Hush, we are flying!’ I did not understand. Why should my father fly?—he, the all-powerful—he, before whom others were accustomed to fly—he, who had taken for his device,\n‘They hate me; then they fear me!’\n\n“It was, indeed, a flight which my father was trying to effect. I have been told since that the garrison of the castle of Yanina, fatigued with long service——”\n\nHere Haydée cast a significant glance at Monte Cristo, whose eyes had been riveted on her countenance during the whole course of her narrative. The young girl then continued, speaking slowly, like a person who is either inventing or suppressing some feature of the history which he is relating.\n\n“You were saying, signora,” said Albert, who was paying the most implicit attention to the recital, “that the garrison of Yanina, fatigued with long service——”\n\n“Had treated with the Seraskier15 Kourchid, who had been sent by the sultan to gain possession of the person of my father; it was then that Ali Tepelini—after having sent to the sultan a French officer in whom he reposed great confidence—resolved to retire to the asylum which he had long before prepared for himself, and which he called _kataphygion_, or the refuge.”\n\n“And this officer,” asked Albert, “do you remember his name, signora?”\n\nMonte Cristo exchanged a rapid glance with the young girl, which was quite unperceived by Albert.\n\n“No,” said she, “I do not remember it just at this moment; but if it should occur to me presently, I will tell you.”\n\nAlbert was on the point of pronouncing his father’s name, when Monte Cristo gently held up his finger in token of reproach; the young man recollected his promise, and was silent.\n\n“It was towards this kiosk that we were rowing. A ground floor, ornamented with arabesques, bathing its terraces in the water, and another floor, looking on the lake, was all which was visible to the eye. But beneath the ground floor, stretching out into the island, was a large subterranean cavern, to which my mother, myself, and the women were conducted. In this place were together 60,000 pouches and 200 barrels; the pouches contained 25,000,000 of money in gold, and the barrels were filled with 30,000 pounds of gunpowder.\n\n“Near the barrels stood Selim, my father’s favorite, whom I mentioned to you just now. He stood watch day and night with a lance provided with a lighted slowmatch in his hand, and he had orders to blow up everything—kiosk, guards, women, gold, and Ali Tepelini himself—at the first signal given by my father. I remember well that the slaves, convinced of the precarious tenure on which they held their lives, passed whole days and nights in praying, crying, and groaning. As for me, I can never forget the pale complexion and black eyes of the young soldier, and whenever the angel of death summons me to another world, I am quite sure I shall recognize Selim. I cannot tell you how long we remained in this state; at that period I did not even know what time meant. Sometimes, but very rarely, my father summoned me and my mother to the terrace of the palace; these were hours of recreation for me, as I never saw anything in the dismal cavern but the gloomy countenances of the slaves and Selim’s fiery lance. My father was endeavoring to pierce with his eager looks the remotest verge of the horizon, examining attentively every black speck which appeared on the lake, while my mother, reclining by his side, rested her head on his shoulder, and I played at his feet, admiring everything I saw with that unsophisticated innocence of childhood which throws a charm round objects insignificant in themselves, but which in its eyes are invested with the greatest importance. The heights of Pindus towered above us; the castle of Yanina rose white and angular from the blue waters of the lake, and the immense masses of black vegetation which, viewed in the distance, gave the idea of lichens clinging to the rocks, were in reality gigantic fir-trees and myrtles.\n\n“One morning my father sent for us; my mother had been crying all the night, and was very wretched; we found the pasha calm, but paler than usual. ‘Take courage, Vasiliki,’ said he; ‘today arrives the firman of the master, and my fate will be decided. If my pardon be complete, we shall return triumphant to Yanina; if the news be inauspicious, we must fly this night.’—‘But supposing our enemy should not allow us to do so?’ said my mother. ‘Oh, make yourself easy on that head,’ said Ali, smiling; ‘Selim and his flaming lance will settle that matter. They would be glad to see me dead, but they would not like themselves to die with me.’\n\n“My mother only answered by sighs to consolations which she knew did not come from my father’s heart. She prepared the iced water which he was in the habit of constantly drinking,—for since his sojourn at the kiosk he had been parched by the most violent fever,—after which she anointed his white beard with perfumed oil, and lighted his chibouque, which he sometimes smoked for hours together, quietly watching the wreaths of vapor that ascended in spiral clouds and gradually melted away in the surrounding atmosphere. Presently he made such a sudden movement that I was paralyzed with fear. Then, without taking his eyes from the object which had first attracted his attention, he asked for his telescope. My mother gave it him, and as she did so, looked whiter than the marble against which she leaned. I saw my father’s hand tremble. ‘A boat!—two!—three!’ murmured my, father;—‘four!’ He then arose, seizing his arms and priming his pistols. ‘Vasiliki,’ said he to my mother, trembling perceptibly, ‘the instant approaches which will decide everything. In the space of half an hour we shall know the emperor’s answer. Go into the cavern with Haydée.’—‘I will not quit you,’ said Vasiliki; ‘if you die, my lord, I will die with you.’—‘Go to Selim!’ cried my father. ‘Adieu, my lord,’ murmured my mother, determining quietly to await the approach of death. ‘Take away Vasiliki!’ said my father to his Palikares.\n\n“As for me, I had been forgotten in the general confusion; I ran toward Ali Tepelini; he saw me hold out my arms to him, and he stooped down and pressed my forehead with his lips. Oh, how distinctly I remember that kiss!—it was the last he ever gave me, and I feel as if it were still warm on my forehead. On descending, we saw through the lattice-work several boats which were gradually becoming more distinct to our view. At first they appeared like black specks, and now they looked like birds skimming the surface of the waves. During this time, in the kiosk at my father’s feet, were seated twenty Palikares, concealed from view by an angle of the wall and watching with eager eyes the arrival of the boats. They were armed with their long guns inlaid with mother-of-pearl and silver, and cartridges in great numbers were lying scattered on the floor. My father looked at his watch, and paced up and down with a countenance expressive of the greatest anguish. This was the scene which presented itself to my view as I quitted my father after that last kiss.\n\n“My mother and I traversed the gloomy passage leading to the cavern. Selim was still at his post, and smiled sadly on us as we entered. We fetched our cushions from the other end of the cavern, and sat down by Selim. In great dangers the devoted ones cling to each other; and, young as I was, I quite understood that some imminent danger was hanging over our heads.”\n\nAlbert had often heard—not from his father, for he never spoke on the subject, but from strangers—the description of the last moments of the vizier of Yanina; he had read different accounts of his death, but the story seemed to acquire fresh meaning from the voice and expression of the young girl, and her sympathetic accent and the melancholy expression of her countenance at once charmed and horrified him.\n\nAs to Haydée, these terrible reminiscences seemed to have overpowered her for a moment, for she ceased speaking, her head leaning on her hand like a beautiful flower bowing beneath the violence of the storm; and her eyes gazing on vacancy indicated that she was mentally contemplating the green summit of the Pindus and the blue waters of the lake of Yanina, which, like a magic mirror, seemed to reflect the sombre picture which she sketched. Monte Cristo looked at her with an indescribable expression of interest and pity.\n\n“Go on, my child,” said the count in the Romaic language.\n\n40074m\nHaydée looked up abruptly, as if the sonorous tones of Monte Cristo’s voice had awakened her from a dream; and she resumed her narrative.\n\n“It was about four o’clock in the afternoon, and although the day was brilliant out-of-doors, we were enveloped in the gloomy darkness of the cavern. One single, solitary light was burning there, and it appeared like a star set in a heaven of blackness; it was Selim’s flaming lance. My mother was a Christian, and she prayed. Selim repeated from time to time the sacred words: ‘God is great!’ However, my mother had still some hope. As she was coming down, she thought she recognized the French officer who had been sent to Constantinople, and in whom my father placed so much confidence; for he knew that all the soldiers of the French emperor were naturally noble and generous. She advanced some steps towards the staircase, and listened. ‘They are approaching,’ said she; ‘perhaps they bring us peace and liberty!’\n\n“‘What do you fear, Vasiliki?’ said Selim, in a voice at once so gentle and yet so proud. ‘If they do not bring us peace, we will give them war; if they do not bring life, we will give them death.’ And he renewed the flame of his lance with a gesture which made one think of Dionysus of old Crete.16 But I, being only a little child, was terrified by this undaunted courage, which appeared to me both ferocious and senseless, and I recoiled with horror from the idea of the frightful death amidst fire and flames which probably awaited us.\n\n“My mother experienced the same sensations, for I felt her tremble. ‘Mamma, mamma,’ said I, ‘are we really to be killed?’ And at the sound of my voice the slaves redoubled their cries and prayers and lamentations. ‘My child,’ said Vasiliki, ‘may God preserve you from ever wishing for that death which today you so much dread!’ Then, whispering to Selim, she asked what were her master’s orders. ‘If he send me his poniard, it will signify that the emperor’s intentions are not favorable, and I am to set fire to the powder; if, on the contrary, he send me his ring, it will be a sign that the emperor pardons him, and I am to extinguish the match and leave the magazine untouched.’—‘My friend,’ said my mother, ‘when your master’s orders arrive, if it is the poniard which he sends, instead of despatching us by that horrible death which we both so much dread, you will mercifully kill us with this same poniard, will you not?’—‘Yes, Vasiliki,’ replied Selim tranquilly.\n\n“Suddenly we heard loud cries; and, listening, discerned that they were cries of joy. The name of the French officer who had been sent to Constantinople resounded on all sides amongst our Palikares; it was evident that he brought the answer of the emperor, and that it was favorable.”\n\n“And do you not remember the Frenchman’s name?” said Morcerf, quite ready to aid the memory of the narrator. Monte Cristo made a sign to him to be silent.\n\n“I do not recollect it,” said Haydée.\n\n“The noise increased; steps were heard approaching nearer and nearer; they were descending the steps leading to the cavern. Selim made ready his lance. Soon a figure appeared in the gray twilight at the entrance of the cave, formed by the reflection of the few rays of daylight which had found their way into this gloomy retreat. ‘Who are you?’ cried Selim. ‘But whoever you may be, I charge you not to advance another step.’—‘Long live the emperor!’ said the figure. ‘He grants a full pardon to the Vizier Ali, and not only gives him his life, but restores to him his fortune and his possessions.’ My mother uttered a cry of joy, and clasped me to her bosom. ‘Stop,’ said Selim, seeing that she was about to go out; ‘you see I have not yet received the ring,’—‘True,’ said my mother. And she fell on her knees, at the same time holding me up towards heaven, as if she desired, while praying to God in my behalf, to raise me actually to his presence.”\n\nAnd for the second time Haydée stopped, overcome by such violent emotion that the perspiration stood upon her pale brow, and her stifled voice seemed hardly able to find utterance, so parched and dry were her throat and lips.\n40076m\n\nMonte Cristo poured a little iced water into a glass, and presented it to her, saying with a mildness in which was also a shade of command,—“Courage.”\n\nHaydée dried her eyes, and continued:\n\n“By this time our eyes, habituated to the darkness, had recognized the messenger of the pasha,—it was a friend. Selim had also recognized him, but the brave young man only acknowledged one duty, which was to obey. ‘In whose name do you come?’ said he to him. ‘I come in the name of our master, Ali Tepelini.’—‘If you come from Ali himself,’ said Selim, ‘you know what you were charged to remit to me?’—‘Yes,’ said the messenger, ‘and I bring you his ring.’ At these words he raised his hand above his head, to show the token; but it was too far off, and there was not light enough to enable Selim, where he was standing, to distinguish and recognize the object presented to his view. ‘I do not see what you have in your hand,’ said Selim. ‘Approach then,’ said the messenger, ‘or I will come nearer to you, if you prefer it.’—‘I will agree to neither one nor the other,’ replied the young soldier; ‘place the object which I desire to see in the ray of light which shines there, and retire while I examine it.’—‘Be it so,’ said the envoy; and he retired, after having first deposited the token agreed on in the place pointed out to him by Selim.\n\n“Oh, how our hearts palpitated; for it did, indeed, seem to be a ring which was placed there. But was it my father’s ring? that was the question. Selim, still holding in his hand the lighted match, walked towards the opening in the cavern, and, aided by the faint light which streamed in through the mouth of the cave, picked up the token.\n\n“‘It is well,’ said he, kissing it; ‘it is my master’s ring!’ And throwing the match on the ground, he trampled on it and extinguished it. The messenger uttered a cry of joy and clapped his hands. At this signal four soldiers of the Seraskier Kourchid suddenly appeared, and Selim fell, pierced by five blows. Each man had stabbed him separately, and, intoxicated by their crime, though still pale with fear, they sought all over the cavern to discover if there was any fear of fire, after which they amused themselves by rolling on the bags of gold. At this moment my mother seized me in her arms, and hurrying noiselessly along numerous turnings and windings known only to ourselves, she arrived at a private staircase of the kiosk, where was a scene of frightful tumult and confusion. The lower rooms were entirely filled with Kourchid’s troops; that is to say, with our enemies. Just as my mother was on the point of pushing open a small door, we heard the voice of the pasha sounding in a loud and threatening tone. My mother applied her eye to the crack between the boards; I luckily found a small opening which afforded me a view of the apartment and what was passing within. ‘What do you want?’ said my father to some people who were holding a paper inscribed with characters of gold. ‘What we want,’ replied one, ‘is to communicate to you the will of his highness. Do you see this firman?’—‘I do,’ said my father. ‘Well, read it; he demands your head.’\n40078m\n\n“My father answered with a loud laugh, which was more frightful than even threats would have been, and he had not ceased when two reports of a pistol were heard; he had fired them himself, and had killed two men. The Palikares, who were prostrated at my father’s feet, now sprang up and fired, and the room was filled with fire and smoke. At the same instant the firing began on the other side, and the balls penetrated the boards all round us. Oh, how noble did the grand vizier my father look at that moment, in the midst of the flying bullets, his scimitar in his hand, and his face blackened with the powder of his enemies! and how he terrified them, even then, and made them fly before him! ‘Selim, Selim!’ cried he, ‘guardian of the fire, do your duty!’—‘Selim is dead,’ replied a voice which seemed to come from the depths of the earth, ‘and you are lost, Ali!’ At the same moment an explosion was heard, and the flooring of the room in which my father was sitting was suddenly torn up and shivered to atoms—the troops were firing from underneath. Three or four Palikares fell with their bodies literally ploughed with wounds.\n\n“My father howled aloud, plunged his fingers into the holes which the balls had made, and tore up one of the planks entire. But immediately through this opening twenty more shots were fired, and the flame, rushing up like fire from the crater of a volcano, soon reached the tapestry, which it quickly devoured. In the midst of all this frightful tumult and these terrific cries, two reports, fearfully distinct, followed by two shrieks more heartrending than all, froze me with terror. These two shots had mortally wounded my father, and it was he who had given utterance to these frightful cries. However, he remained standing, clinging to a window. My mother tried to force the door, that she might go and die with him, but it was fastened on the inside. All around him were lying the Palikares, writhing in convulsive agonies, while two or three who were only slightly wounded were trying to escape by springing from the windows. At this crisis the whole flooring suddenly gave way, my father fell on one knee, and at the same moment twenty hands were thrust forth, armed with sabres, pistols, and poniards—twenty blows were instantaneously directed against one man, and my father disappeared in a whirlwind of fire and smoke kindled by these demons, and which seemed like hell itself opening beneath his feet. I felt myself fall to the ground, my mother had fainted.”\n\nHaydée’s arms fell by her side, and she uttered a deep groan, at the same time looking towards the count as if to ask if he were satisfied with her obedience to his commands.\n\nMonte Cristo arose and approached her, took her hand, and said to her in Romaic:\n\n“Calm yourself, my dear child, and take courage in remembering that there is a God who will punish traitors.”\n\n“It is a frightful story, count,” said Albert, terrified at the paleness of Haydée’s countenance, “and I reproach myself now for having been so cruel and thoughtless in my request.”\n\n“Oh, it is nothing,” said Monte Cristo. Then, patting the young girl on the head, he continued, “Haydée is very courageous, and she sometimes even finds consolation in the recital of her misfortunes.”\n\n“Because, my lord,” said Haydée eagerly, “my miseries recall to me the remembrance of your goodness.”\n\nAlbert looked at her with curiosity, for she had not yet related what he most desired to know,—how she had become the slave of the count. Haydée saw at a glance the same expression pervading the countenances of her two auditors; she continued:\n\n“When my mother recovered her senses we were before the seraskier. ‘Kill,’ said she, ‘but spare the honor of the widow of Ali.’—‘It is not to me to whom you must address yourself,’ said Kourchid.\n\n“‘To whom, then?’—‘To your new master.’\n\n“‘Who and where is he?’—‘He is here.’\n\n“And Kourchid pointed out one who had more than any contributed to the death of my father,” said Haydée, in a tone of chastened anger.\n\n“Then,” said Albert, “you became the property of this man?”\n40080m\n\n“No,” replied Haydée, “he did not dare to keep us, so we were sold to some slave-merchants who were going to Constantinople. We traversed Greece, and arrived half dead at the imperial gates. They were surrounded by a crowd of people, who opened a way for us to pass, when suddenly my mother, having looked closely at an object which was attracting their attention, uttered a piercing cry and fell to the ground, pointing as she did so to a head which was placed over the gates, and beneath which were inscribed these words:\n\n‘_This is the head of Ali Tepelini, Pasha of Yanina._’\n\n“I cried bitterly, and tried to raise my mother from the earth, but she was dead! I was taken to the slave-market, and was purchased by a rich Armenian. He caused me to be instructed, gave me masters, and when I was thirteen years of age he sold me to the Sultan Mahmoud.”\n\n“Of whom I bought her,” said Monte Cristo, “as I told you, Albert, with the emerald which formed a match to the one I had made into a box for the purpose of holding my hashish pills.”\n\n“Oh, you are good, you are great, my lord!” said Haydée, kissing the count’s hand, “and I am very fortunate in belonging to such a master!”\n\nAlbert remained quite bewildered with all that he had seen and heard.\n\n“Come, finish your cup of coffee,” said Monte Cristo; “the history is ended.”\n\n# Chapter 78. We hear From Yanina\n\nIf Valentine could have seen the trembling step and agitated countenance of Franz when he quitted the chamber of M. Noirtier, even she would have been constrained to pity him. Villefort had only just given utterance to a few incoherent sentences, and then retired to his study, where he received about two hours afterwards the following letter:\n\n“After all the disclosures which were made this morning, M. Noirtier de Villefort must see the utter impossibility of any alliance being formed between his family and that of M. Franz d’Épinay. M. d’Épinay must say that he is shocked and astonished that M. de Villefort, who appeared to be aware of all the circumstances detailed this morning, should not have anticipated him in this announcement.”\n\nNo one who had seen the magistrate at this moment, so thoroughly unnerved by the recent inauspicious combination of circumstances, would have supposed for an instant that he had anticipated the annoyance; although it certainly never had occurred to him that his father would carry candor, or rather rudeness, so far as to relate such a history. And in justice to Villefort, it must be understood that M. Noirtier, who never cared for the opinion of his son on any subject, had always omitted to explain the affair to Villefort, so that he had all his life entertained the belief that General de Quesnel, or the Baron d’Épinay, as he was alternately styled, according as the speaker wished to identify him by his own family name, or by the title which had been conferred on him, fell the victim of assassination, and not that he was killed fairly in a duel. This harsh letter, coming as it did from a man generally so polite and respectful, struck a mortal blow at the pride of Villefort.\n\nHardly had he read the letter, when his wife entered. The sudden departure of Franz, after being summoned by M. Noirtier, had so much astonished everyone, that the position of Madame de Villefort, left alone with the notary and the witnesses, became every moment more embarrassing. Determined to bear it no longer, she arose and left the room; saying she would go and make some inquiries into the cause of his sudden disappearance.\n\nM. de Villefort’s communications on the subject were very limited and concise; he told her, in fact, that an explanation had taken place between M. Noirtier, M. d’Épinay, and himself, and that the marriage of Valentine and Franz would consequently be broken off. This was an awkward and unpleasant thing to have to report to those who were waiting. She therefore contented herself with saying that M. Noirtier having at the commencement of the discussion been attacked by a sort of apoplectic fit, the affair would necessarily be deferred for some days longer. This news, false as it was following so singularly in the train of the two similar misfortunes which had so recently occurred, evidently astonished the auditors, and they retired without a word.\n\nDuring this time Valentine, at once terrified and happy, after having embraced and thanked the feeble old man for thus breaking with a single blow the chain which she had been accustomed to consider as irrefragable, asked leave to retire to her own room, in order to recover her composure. Noirtier looked the permission which she solicited. But instead of going to her own room, Valentine, having once gained her liberty, entered the gallery, and, opening a small door at the end of it, found herself at once in the garden.\n\nIn the midst of all the strange events which had crowded one on the other, an indefinable sentiment of dread had taken possession of Valentine’s mind. She expected every moment that she should see Morrel appear, pale and trembling, to forbid the signing of the contract, like the Laird of Ravenswood in _The Bride of Lammermoor_.\n\nIt was high time for her to make her appearance at the gate, for Maximilian had long awaited her coming. He had half guessed what was going on when he saw Franz quit the cemetery with M. de Villefort. He followed M. d’Épinay, saw him enter, afterwards go out, and then re-enter with Albert and Château-Renaud. He had no longer any doubts as to the nature of the conference; he therefore quickly went to the gate in the clover-patch, prepared to hear the result of the proceedings, and very certain that Valentine would hasten to him the first moment she should be set at liberty. He was not mistaken; peering through the crevices of the wooden partition, he soon discovered the young girl, who cast aside all her usual precautions and walked at once to the barrier. The first glance which Maximilian directed towards her entirely reassured him, and the first words she spoke made his heart bound with delight.\n\n“We are saved!” said Valentine.\n\n“Saved?” repeated Morrel, not being able to conceive such intense happiness; “by whom?”\n\n“By my grandfather. Oh, Morrel, pray love him for all his goodness to us!”\n\nMorrel swore to love him with all his soul; and at that moment he could safely promise to do so, for he felt as though it were not enough to love him merely as a friend or even as a father, he worshiped him as a god.\n\n“But tell me, Valentine, how has it all been effected? What strange means has he used to compass this blessed end?”\n\nValentine was on the point of relating all that had passed, but she suddenly remembered that in doing so she must reveal a terrible secret which concerned others as well as her grandfather, and she said:\n\n“At some future time I will tell you all about it.”\n\n“But when will that be?”\n\n“When I am your wife.”\n\nThe conversation had now turned upon a topic so pleasing to Morrel, that he was ready to accede to anything that Valentine thought fit to propose, and he likewise felt that a piece of intelligence such as he just heard ought to be more than sufficient to content him for one day. However, he would not leave without the promise of seeing Valentine again the next night. Valentine promised all that Morrel required of her, and certainly it was less difficult now for her to believe that she should marry Maximilian than it was an hour ago to assure herself that she should not marry Franz.\n\nDuring the time occupied by the interview we have just detailed, Madame de Villefort had gone to visit M. Noirtier. The old man looked at her with that stern and forbidding expression with which he was accustomed to receive her.\n\n“Sir,” said she, “it is superfluous for me to tell you that Valentine’s marriage is broken off, since it was here that the affair was concluded.”\n\nNoirtier’s countenance remained immovable.\n\n“But one thing I can tell you, of which I do not think you are aware; that is, that I have always been opposed to this marriage, and that the contract was entered into entirely without my consent or approbation.”\n\nNoirtier regarded his daughter-in-law with the look of a man desiring an explanation.\n\n“Now that this marriage, which I know you so much disliked, is done away with, I come to you on an errand which neither M. de Villefort nor Valentine could consistently undertake.”\n\nNoirtier’s eyes demanded the nature of her mission.\n\n“I come to entreat you, sir,” continued Madame de Villefort, “as the only one who has the right of doing so, inasmuch as I am the only one who will receive no personal benefit from the transaction,—I come to entreat you to restore, not your love, for that she has always possessed, but to restore your fortune to your granddaughter.”\n\nThere was a doubtful expression in Noirtier’s eyes; he was evidently trying to discover the motive of this proceeding, and he could not succeed in doing so.\n\n“May I hope, sir,” said Madame de Villefort, “that your intentions accord with my request?”\n\nNoirtier made a sign that they did.\n\n“In that case, sir,” rejoined Madame de Villefort, “I will leave you overwhelmed with gratitude and happiness at your prompt acquiescence to my wishes.” She then bowed to M. Noirtier and retired.\n\nThe next day M. Noirtier sent for the notary; the first will was torn up and a second made, in which he left the whole of his fortune to Valentine, on condition that she should never be separated from him. It was then generally reported that Mademoiselle de Villefort, the heiress of the marquis and marchioness of Saint-Méran, had regained the good graces of her grandfather, and that she would ultimately be in possession of an income of 300,000 livres.\n\nWhile all the proceedings relative to the dissolution of the marriage-contract were being carried on at the house of M. de Villefort, Monte Cristo had paid his visit to the Count of Morcerf, who, in order to lose no time in responding to M. Danglars’ wishes, and at the same time to pay all due deference to his position in society, donned his uniform of lieutenant-general, which he ornamented with all his crosses, and thus attired, ordered his finest horses and drove to the Rue de la Chaussée d’Antin.\n\nDanglars was balancing his monthly accounts, and it was perhaps not the most favorable moment for finding him in his best humor. At the first sight of his old friend, Danglars assumed his majestic air, and settled himself in his easy-chair.\n\nMorcerf, usually so stiff and formal, accosted the banker in an affable and smiling manner, and, feeling sure that the overture he was about to make would be well received, he did not consider it necessary to adopt any manœuvres in order to gain his end, but went at once straight to the point.\n40086m\n\n“Well, baron,” said he, “here I am at last; some time has elapsed since our plans were formed, and they are not yet executed.”\n\nMorcerf paused at these words, quietly waiting till the cloud should have dispersed which had gathered on the brow of Danglars, and which he attributed to his silence; but, on the contrary, to his great surprise, it grew darker and darker.\n\n“To what do you allude, monsieur?” said Danglars; as if he were trying in vain to guess at the possible meaning of the general’s words.\n\n“Ah,” said Morcerf, “I see you are a stickler for forms, my dear sir, and you would remind me that the ceremonial rites should not be omitted. _Ma foi_, I beg your pardon, but as I have but one son, and it is the first time I have ever thought of marrying him, I am still serving my apprenticeship, you know; come, I will reform.”\n\nAnd Morcerf with a forced smile arose, and, making a low bow to M. Danglars, said:\n\n“Baron, I have the honor of asking of you the hand of Mademoiselle Eugénie Danglars for my son, the Vicomte Albert de Morcerf.”\n\nBut Danglars, instead of receiving this address in the favorable manner which Morcerf had expected, knit his brow, and without inviting the count, who was still standing, to take a seat, he said:\n\n“Monsieur, it will be necessary to reflect before I give you an answer.”\n\n“To reflect?” said Morcerf, more and more astonished; “have you not had enough time for reflection during the eight years which have elapsed since this marriage was first discussed between us?”\n\n“Count,” said the banker, “things are constantly occurring in the world to induce us to lay aside our most established opinions, or at all events to cause us to remodel them according to the change of circumstances, which may have placed affairs in a totally different light to that in which we at first viewed them.”\n\n“I do not understand you, baron,” said Morcerf.\n\n“What I mean to say is this, sir,—that during the last fortnight unforeseen circumstances have occurred——”\n\n“Excuse me,” said Morcerf, “but is it a play we are acting?”\n\n“A play?”\n\n“Yes, for it is like one; pray let us come more to the point, and endeavor thoroughly to understand each other.”\n\n“That is quite my desire.”\n\n“You have seen M. de Monte Cristo have you not?”\n\n“I see him very often,” said Danglars, drawing himself up; “he is a particular friend of mine.”\n\n“Well, in one of your late conversations with him, you said that I appeared to be forgetful and irresolute concerning this marriage, did you not?”\n\n“I did say so.”\n\n“Well, here I am, proving at once that I am really neither the one nor the other, by entreating you to keep your promise on that score.”\n\nDanglars did not answer.\n\n“Have you so soon changed your mind,” added Morcerf, “or have you only provoked my request that you may have the pleasure of seeing me humbled?”\n\nDanglars, seeing that if he continued the conversation in the same tone in which he had begun it, the whole thing might turn out to his own disadvantage, turned to Morcerf, and said:\n\n“Count, you must doubtless be surprised at my reserve, and I assure you it costs me much to act in such a manner towards you; but, believe me when I say that imperative necessity has imposed the painful task upon me.”\n\n“These are all so many empty words, my dear sir,” said Morcerf: “they might satisfy a new acquaintance, but the Comte de Morcerf does not rank in that list; and when a man like him comes to another, recalls to him his plighted word, and this man fails to redeem the pledge, he has at least a right to exact from him a good reason for so doing.”\n\nDanglars was a coward, but did not wish to appear so; he was piqued at the tone which Morcerf had just assumed.\n\n“I am not without a good reason for my conduct,” replied the banker.\n\n“What do you mean to say?”\n\n“I mean to say that I have a good reason, but that it is difficult to explain.”\n\n“You must be aware, at all events, that it is impossible for me to understand motives before they are explained to me; but one thing at least is clear, which is, that you decline allying yourself with my family.”\n\n“No, sir,” said Danglars; “I merely suspend my decision, that is all.”\n\n“And do you really flatter yourself that I shall yield to all your caprices, and quietly and humbly await the time of again being received into your good graces?”\n\n“Then, count, if you will not wait, we must look upon these projects as if they had never been entertained.”\n\nThe count bit his lips till the blood almost started, to prevent the ebullition of anger which his proud and irritable temper scarcely allowed him to restrain; understanding, however, that in the present state of things the laugh would decidedly be against him, he turned from the door, towards which he had been directing his steps, and again confronted the banker. A cloud settled on his brow, evincing decided anxiety and uneasiness, instead of the expression of offended pride which had lately reigned there.\n\n“My dear Danglars,” said Morcerf, “we have been acquainted for many years, and consequently we ought to make some allowance for each other’s failings. You owe me an explanation, and really it is but fair that I should know what circumstance has occurred to deprive my son of your favor.”\n\n“It is from no personal ill-feeling towards the viscount, that is all I can say, sir,” replied Danglars, who resumed his insolent manner as soon as he perceived that Morcerf was a little softened and calmed down.\n\n“And towards whom do you bear this personal ill-feeling, then?” said Morcerf, turning pale with anger. The expression of the count’s face had not remained unperceived by the banker; he fixed on him a look of greater assurance than before, and said:\n\n“You may, perhaps, be better satisfied that I should not go farther into particulars.”\n\nA tremor of suppressed rage shook the whole frame of the count, and making a violent effort over himself, he said: “I have a right to insist on your giving me an explanation. Is it Madame de Morcerf who has displeased you? Is it my fortune which you find insufficient? Is it because my opinions differ from yours?”\n\n“Nothing of the kind, sir,” replied Danglars: “if such had been the case, I only should have been to blame, inasmuch as I was aware of all these things when I made the engagement. No, do not seek any longer to discover the reason. I really am quite ashamed to have been the cause of your undergoing such severe self-examination; let us drop the subject, and adopt the middle course of delay, which implies neither a rupture nor an engagement. _Ma foi_, there is no hurry. My daughter is only seventeen years old, and your son twenty-one. While we wait, time will be progressing, events will succeed each other; things which in the evening look dark and obscure, appear but too clearly in the light of morning, and sometimes the utterance of one word, or the lapse of a single day, will reveal the most cruel calumnies.”\n\n“Calumnies, did you say, sir?” cried Morcerf, turning livid with rage. “Does anyone dare to slander me?”\n\n“Monsieur, I told you that I considered it best to avoid all explanation.”\n\n“Then, sir, I am patiently to submit to your refusal?”\n\n“Yes, sir, although I assure you the refusal is as painful for me to give as it is for you to receive, for I had reckoned on the honor of your alliance, and the breaking off of a marriage contract always injures the lady more than the gentleman.”\n\n“Enough, sir,” said Morcerf, “we will speak no more on the subject.”\n\nAnd clutching his gloves in anger, he left the apartment. Danglars observed that during the whole conversation Morcerf had never once dared to ask if it was on his own account that Danglars recalled his word.\n\nThat evening he had a long conference with several friends; and M. Cavalcanti, who had remained in the drawing-room with the ladies, was the last to leave the banker’s house.\n\nThe next morning, as soon as he awoke, Danglars asked for the newspapers; they were brought to him; he laid aside three or four, and at last fixed on _l’Impartial_, the paper of which Beauchamp was the chief editor. He hastily tore off the cover, opened the journal with nervous precipitation, passed contemptuously over the Paris jottings, and arriving at the miscellaneous intelligence, stopped with a malicious smile, at a paragraph headed\n\n_We hear from Yanina._\n\n“Very good,” observed Danglars, after having read the paragraph; “here is a little article on Colonel Fernand, which, if I am not mistaken, would render the explanation which the Comte de Morcerf required of me perfectly unnecessary.”\n\nAt the same moment, that is, at nine o’clock in the morning, Albert de Morcerf, dressed in a black coat buttoned up to his chin, might have been seen walking with a quick and agitated step in the direction of Monte Cristo’s house in the Champs-Élysées. When he presented himself at the gate the porter informed him that the Count had gone out about half an hour previously.\n\n“Did he take Baptistin with him?”\n\n“No, my lord.”\n\n“Call him, then; I wish to speak to him.”\n\nThe concierge went to seek the valet de chambre, and returned with him in an instant.\n\n“My good friend,” said Albert, “I beg pardon for my intrusion, but I was anxious to know from your own mouth if your master was really out or not.”\n\n“He is really out, sir,” replied Baptistin.\n\n“Out, even to me?”\n\n“I know how happy my master always is to receive the vicomte,” said Baptistin; “and I should therefore never think of including him in any general order.”\n\n“You are right; and now I wish to see him on an affair of great importance. Do you think it will be long before he comes in?”\n\n“No, I think not, for he ordered his breakfast at ten o’clock.”\n\n“Well, I will go and take a turn in the Champs-Élysées, and at ten o’clock I will return here; meanwhile, if the count should come in, will you beg him not to go out again without seeing me?”\n\n“You may depend on my doing so, sir,” said Baptistin.\n\nAlbert left the cab in which he had come at the count’s door, intending to take a turn on foot. As he was passing the Allée des Veuves, he thought he saw the count’s horses standing at Gosset’s shooting-gallery; he approached, and soon recognized the coachman.\n\n“Is the count shooting in the gallery?” said Morcerf.\n\n“Yes, sir,” replied the coachman. While he was speaking, Albert had heard the report of two or three pistol-shots. He entered, and on his way met the waiter.\n\n“Excuse me, my lord,” said the lad; “but will you have the kindness to wait a moment?”\n\n“What for, Philip?” asked Albert, who, being a constant visitor there, did not understand this opposition to his entrance.\n\n“Because the person who is now in the gallery prefers being alone, and never practices in the presence of anyone.”\n\n“Not even before you, Philip? Then who loads his pistol?”\n\n“His servant.”\n\n“A Nubian?”\n\n“A negro.”\n\n“It is he, then.”\n\n“Do you know this gentleman?”\n\n“Yes, and I am come to look for him; he is a friend of mine.”\n\n“Oh, that is quite another thing, then. I will go immediately and inform him of your arrival.”\n\nAnd Philip, urged by his own curiosity, entered the gallery; a second afterwards, Monte Cristo appeared on the threshold.\n\n“I ask your pardon, my dear count,” said Albert, “for following you here, and I must first tell you that it was not the fault of your servants that I did so; I alone am to blame for the indiscretion. I went to your house, and they told me you were out, but that they expected you home at ten o’clock to breakfast. I was walking about in order to pass away the time till ten o’clock, when I caught sight of your carriage and horses.”\n\n“What you have just said induces me to hope that you intend breakfasting with me.”\n\n40092m\n“No, thank you, I am thinking of other things besides breakfast just now; perhaps we may take that meal at a later hour and in worse company.”\n\n“What on earth are you talking of?”\n\n“I am to fight today.”\n\n“For what?”\n\n“For the sake of fighting!”\n\n“Yes, I understand that, but what is the quarrel? People fight for all sorts of reasons, you know.”\n\n“I fight in the cause of honor.”\n\n“Ah, that is something serious.”\n\n“So serious, that I come to beg you to render me a service.”\n\n“What is it?”\n\n“To be my second.”\n\n“That is a serious matter, and we will not discuss it here; let us speak of nothing till we get home. Ali, bring me some water.”\n\nThe count turned up his sleeves, and passed into the little vestibule where the gentlemen were accustomed to wash their hands after shooting.\n\n“Come in, my lord,” said Philip in a low tone, “and I will show you something droll.” Morcerf entered, and in place of the usual target, he saw some playing-cards fixed against the wall. At a distance Albert thought it was a complete suit, for he counted from the ace to the ten.\n\n“Ah, ha,” said Albert, “I see you were preparing for a game of cards.”\n\n“No,” said the count, “I was making a suit.”\n\n“How?” said Albert.\n\n“Those are really aces and twos which you see, but my shots have turned them into threes, fives, sevens, eights, nines, and tens.”\n\nAlbert approached. In fact, the bullets had actually pierced the cards in the exact places which the painted signs would otherwise have occupied, the lines and distances being as regularly kept as if they had been ruled with pencil. In going up to the target Morcerf picked up two or three swallows that had been rash enough to come within the range of the count’s pistol.\n\n“_Diable!_” said Morcerf.\n\n“What would you have, my dear viscount?” said Monte Cristo, wiping his hands on the towel which Ali had brought him; “I must occupy my leisure moments in some way or other. But come, I am waiting for you.”\n\nBoth men entered Monte Cristo’s carriage, which in the course of a few minutes deposited them safely at No. 30. Monte Cristo took Albert into his study, and pointing to a seat, placed another for himself. “Now let us talk the matter over quietly,” said the count.\n\n“You see I am perfectly composed,” said Albert.\n\n“With whom are you going to fight?”\n\n“With Beauchamp.”\n\n“One of your friends!”\n\n“Of course; it is always with friends that one fights.”\n\n“I suppose you have some cause of quarrel?”\n\n“I have.”\n\n40094m\n“What has he done to you?”\n\n“There appeared in his journal last night—but wait, and read for yourself.” And Albert handed over the paper to the count, who read as follows:\n\n“A correspondent at Yanina informs us of a fact of which until now we had remained in ignorance. The castle which formed the protection of the town was given up to the Turks by a French officer named Fernand, in whom the grand vizier, Ali Tepelini, had reposed the greatest confidence.”\n\n“Well,” said Monte Cristo, “what do you see in that to annoy you?”\n\n“What do I see in it?”\n\n“Yes; what does it signify to you if the castle of Yanina was given up by a French officer?”\n\n“It signifies to my father, the Count of Morcerf, whose Christian name is Fernand!”\n\n“Did your father serve under Ali Pasha?”\n\n“Yes; that is to say, he fought for the independence of the Greeks, and hence arises the calumny.”\n\n“Oh, my dear viscount, do talk reason!”\n\n“I do not desire to do otherwise.”\n\n“Now, just tell me who the devil should know in France that the officer Fernand and the Count of Morcerf are one and the same person? and who cares now about Yanina, which was taken as long ago as the year 1822 or 1823?”\n\n“That just shows the meanness of this slander. They have allowed all this time to elapse, and then all of a sudden rake up events which have been forgotten to furnish materials for scandal, in order to tarnish the lustre of our high position. I inherit my father’s name, and I do not choose that the shadow of disgrace should darken it. I am going to Beauchamp, in whose journal this paragraph appears, and I shall insist on his retracting the assertion before two witnesses.”\n\n“Beauchamp will never retract.”\n\n“Then we must fight.”\n\n“No you will not, for he will tell you, what is very true, that perhaps there were fifty officers in the Greek army bearing the same name.”\n\n“We will fight, nevertheless. I will efface that blot on my father’s character. My father, who was such a brave soldier, whose career was so brilliant——”\n\n“Oh, well, he will add, ‘We are warranted in believing that this Fernand is not the illustrious Count of Morcerf, who also bears the same Christian name.’”\n\n“I am determined not to be content with anything short of an entire retractation.”\n\n“And you intend to make him do it in the presence of two witnesses, do you?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You do wrong.”\n\n“Which means, I suppose, that you refuse the service which I asked of you?”\n\n“You know my theory regarding duels; I told you my opinion on that subject, if you remember, when we were at Rome.”\n\n“Nevertheless, my dear count, I found you this morning engaged in an occupation but little consistent with the notions you profess to entertain.”\n\n“Because, my dear fellow, you understand one must never be eccentric. If one’s lot is cast among fools, it is necessary to study folly. I shall perhaps find myself one day called out by some harebrained scamp, who has no more real cause of quarrel with me than you have with Beauchamp; he may take me to task for some foolish trifle or other, he will bring his witnesses, or will insult me in some public place, and I am expected to kill him for all that.”\n\n“You admit that you would fight, then? Well, if so, why do you object to my doing so?”\n\n“I do not say that you ought not to fight, I only say that a duel is a serious thing, and ought not to be undertaken without due reflection.”\n\n“Did he reflect before he insulted my father?”\n\n“If he spoke hastily, and owns that he did so, you ought to be satisfied.”\n\n“Ah, my dear count, you are far too indulgent.”\n\n“And you are far too exacting. Supposing, for instance, and do not be angry at what I am going to say——”\n\n“Well.”\n“Supposing the assertion to be really true?”\n\n“A son ought not to submit to such a stain on his father’s honor.”\n\n“_Ma foi!_ we live in times when there is much to which we must submit.”\n\n“That is precisely the fault of the age.”\n\n“And do you undertake to reform it?”\n\n“Yes, as far as I am personally concerned.”\n\n“Well, you are indeed exacting, my dear fellow!”\n\n“Yes, I own it.”\n\n“Are you quite impervious to good advice?”\n\n“Not when it comes from a friend.”\n\n“And do you account me that title?”\n\n“Certainly I do.”\n\n“Well, then, before going to Beauchamp with your witnesses, seek further information on the subject.”\n\n“From whom?”\n\n“From Haydée.”\n\n“Why, what can be the use of mixing a woman up in the affair?—what can she do in it?”\n\n“She can declare to you, for example, that your father had no hand whatever in the defeat and death of the vizier; or if by chance he had, indeed, the misfortune to——”\n\n“I have told you, my dear count, that I would not for one moment admit of such a proposition.”\n\n“You reject this means of information, then?”\n\n“I do—most decidedly.”\n\n“Then let me offer one more word of advice.”\n\n“Do so, then, but let it be the last.”\n\n“You do not wish to hear it, perhaps?”\n\n“On the contrary, I request it.”\n\n“Do not take any witnesses with you when you go to Beauchamp—visit him alone.”\n\n“That would be contrary to all custom.”\n\n“Your case is not an ordinary one.”\n\n“And what is your reason for advising me to go alone?”\n\n“Because then the affair will rest between you and Beauchamp.”\n\n“Explain yourself.”\n\n“I will do so. If Beauchamp be disposed to retract, you ought at least to give him the opportunity of doing it of his own free will,—the satisfaction to you will be the same. If, on the contrary, he refuses to do so, it will then be quite time enough to admit two strangers into your secret.”\n\n“They will not be strangers, they will be friends.”\n\n“Ah, but the friends of today are the enemies of tomorrow; Beauchamp, for instance.”\n\n“So you recommend——”\n\n“I recommend you to be prudent.”\n\n“Then you advise me to go alone to Beauchamp?”\n\n“I do, and I will tell you why. When you wish to obtain some concession from a man’s self-love, you must avoid even the appearance of wishing to wound it.”\n\n“I believe you are right.”\n\n“I am glad of it.”\n\n“Then I will go alone.”\n\n“Go; but you would do better still by not going at all.”\n\n“That is impossible.”\n\n“Do so, then; it will be a wiser plan than the first which you proposed.”\n\n“But if, in spite of all my precautions, I am at last obliged to fight, will you not be my second?”\n\n“My dear viscount,” said Monte Cristo gravely, “you must have seen before today that at all times and in all places I have been at your disposal, but the service which you have just demanded of me is one which it is out of my power to render you.”\n“Why?”\n\n“Perhaps you may know at some future period, and in the mean time I request you to excuse my declining to put you in possession of my reasons.”\n\n“Well, I will have Franz and Château-Renaud; they will be the very men for it.”\n\n“Do so, then.”\n\n“But if I do fight, you will surely not object to giving me a lesson or two in shooting and fencing?”\n\n“That, too, is impossible.”\n\n“What a singular being you are!—you will not interfere in anything.”\n\n“You are right—that is the principle on which I wish to act.”\n\n“We will say no more about it, then. Good-bye, count.”\n\nMorcerf took his hat, and left the room. He found his carriage at the door, and doing his utmost to restrain his anger he went at once to find Beauchamp, who was in his office. It was a gloomy, dusty-looking apartment, such as journalists’ offices have always been from time immemorial. The servant announced M. Albert de Morcerf. Beauchamp repeated the name to himself, as though he could scarcely believe that he had heard aright, and then gave orders for him to be admitted. Albert entered.\n\nBeauchamp uttered an exclamation of surprise on seeing his friend leap over and trample under foot all the newspapers which were strewed about the room.\n\n“This way, this way, my dear Albert!” said he, holding out his hand to the young man. “Are you out of your senses, or do you come peaceably to take breakfast with me? Try and find a seat—there is one by that geranium, which is the only thing in the room to remind me that there are other leaves in the world besides leaves of paper.”\n\n“Beauchamp,” said Albert, “it is of your journal that I come to speak.”\n\n“Indeed? What do you wish to say about it?”\n\n“I desire that a statement contained in it should be rectified.”\n\n“To what do you refer? But pray sit down.”\n\n“Thank you,” said Albert, with a cold and formal bow.\n\n“Will you now have the kindness to explain the nature of the statement which has displeased you?”\n\n“An announcement has been made which implicates the honor of a member of my family.”\n\n“What is it?” said Beauchamp, much surprised; “surely you must be mistaken.”\n\n“The story sent you from Yanina.”\n\n“Yanina?”\n\n“Yes; really you appear to be totally ignorant of the cause which brings me here.”\n\n“Such is really the case, I assure you, upon my honor! Baptiste, give me yesterday’s paper,” cried Beauchamp.\n\n“Here, I have brought mine with me,” replied Albert.\n\nBeauchamp took the paper, and read the article to which Albert pointed in an undertone.\n\n“You see it is a serious annoyance,” said Morcerf, when Beauchamp had finished the perusal of the paragraph.\n\n“Is the officer referred to a relation of yours, then?” demanded the journalist.\n\n“Yes,” said Albert, blushing.\n\n“Well, what do you wish me to do for you?” said Beauchamp mildly.\n\n“My dear Beauchamp, I wish you to contradict this statement.” Beauchamp looked at Albert with a benevolent expression.\n\n“Come,” said he, “this matter will want a good deal of talking over; a retractation is always a serious thing, you know. Sit down, and I will read it again.”\n\nAlbert resumed his seat, and Beauchamp read, with more attention than at first, the lines denounced by his friend.\n\n“Well,” said Albert in a determined tone, “you see that your paper has insulted a member of my family, and I insist on a retractation being made.”\n\n“You insist?”\n\n“Yes, I insist.”\n\n“Permit me to remind you that you are not in the Chamber, my dear viscount.”\n\n“Nor do I wish to be there,” replied the young man, rising. “I repeat that I am determined to have the announcement of yesterday contradicted. You have known me long enough,” continued Albert, biting his lips convulsively, for he saw that Beauchamp’s anger was beginning to rise,—“you have been my friend, and therefore sufficiently intimate with me to be aware that I am likely to maintain my resolution on this point.”\n\n“If I have been your friend, Morcerf, your present manner of speaking would almost lead me to forget that I ever bore that title. But wait a moment, do not let us get angry, or at least not yet. You are irritated and vexed—tell me how this Fernand is related to you?”\n\n“He is merely my father,” said Albert—“M. Fernand Mondego, Count of Morcerf, an old soldier who has fought in twenty battles and whose honorable scars they would denounce as badges of disgrace.”\n\n“Is it your father?” said Beauchamp; “that is quite another thing. Then I can well understand your indignation, my dear Albert. I will look at it again;” and he read the paragraph for the third time, laying a stress on each word as he proceeded. “But the paper nowhere identifies this Fernand with your father.”\n40100m\n\n“No; but the connection will be seen by others, and therefore I will have the article contradicted.”\n\nAt the words _I will_, Beauchamp steadily raised his eyes to Albert’s countenance, and then as gradually lowering them, he remained thoughtful for a few moments.\n\n“You will retract this assertion, will you not, Beauchamp?” said Albert with increased though stifled anger.\n\n“Yes,” replied Beauchamp.\n\n“Immediately?” said Albert.\n\n“When I am convinced that the statement is false.”\n\n“What?”\n\n“The thing is worth looking into, and I will take pains to investigate the matter thoroughly.”\n\n“But what is there to investigate, sir?” said Albert, enraged beyond measure at Beauchamp’s last remark. “If you do not believe that it is my father, say so immediately; and if, on the contrary, you believe it to be him, state your reasons for doing so.”\n\nBeauchamp looked at Albert with the smile which was so peculiar to him, and which in its numerous modifications served to express every varied emotion of his mind.\n\n“Sir,” replied he, “if you came to me with the idea of demanding satisfaction, you should have gone at once to the point, and not have entertained me with the idle conversation to which I have been patiently listening for the last half hour. Am I to put this construction on your visit?”\n\n“Yes, if you will not consent to retract that infamous calumny.”\n\n“Wait a moment—no threats, if you please, M. Fernand Mondego, Vicomte de Morcerf; I never allow them from my enemies, and therefore shall not put up with them from my friends. You insist on my contradicting the article relating to General Fernand, an article with which, I assure you on my word of honor, I had nothing whatever to do?”\n\n“Yes, I insist on it,” said Albert, whose mind was beginning to get bewildered with the excitement of his feelings.\n\n“And if I refuse to retract, you wish to fight, do you?” said Beauchamp in a calm tone.\n\n“Yes,” replied Albert, raising his voice.\n\n“Well,” said Beauchamp, “here is my answer, my dear sir. The article was not inserted by me—I was not even aware of it; but you have, by the step you have taken, called my attention to the paragraph in question, and it will remain until it shall be either contradicted or confirmed by someone who has a right to do so.”\n\n“Sir,” said Albert, rising, “I will do myself the honor of sending my seconds to you, and you will be kind enough to arrange with them the place of meeting and the weapons.”\n\n“Certainly, my dear sir.”\n\n“And this evening, if you please, or tomorrow at the latest, we will meet.”\n\n“No, no, I will be on the ground at the proper time; but in my opinion (and I have a right to dictate the preliminaries, as it is I who have received the provocation)—in my opinion the time ought not to be yet. I know you to be well skilled in the management of the sword, while I am only moderately so; I know, too, that you are a good marksman—there we are about equal. I know that a duel between us two would be a serious affair, because you are brave, and I am brave also. I do not therefore wish either to kill you, or to be killed myself without a cause. Now, I am going to put a question to you, and one very much to the purpose too. Do you insist on this retractation so far as to kill me if I do not make it, although I have repeated more than once, and affirmed on my honor, that I was ignorant of the thing with which you charge me, and although I still declare that it is impossible for anyone but you to recognize the Count of Morcerf under the name of Fernand?”\n\n“I maintain my original resolution.”\n\n“Very well, my dear sir; then I consent to cut throats with you. But I require three weeks’ preparation; at the end of that time I shall come and say to you, ‘The assertion is false, and I retract it,’ or ‘The assertion is true,’ when I shall immediately draw the sword from its sheath, or the pistols from the case, whichever you please.”\n\n“Three weeks!” cried Albert; “they will pass as slowly as three centuries when I am all the time suffering dishonor.”\n\n“Had you continued to remain on amicable terms with me, I should have said, ‘Patience, my friend;’ but you have constituted yourself my enemy, therefore I say, ‘What does that signify to me, sir?’”\n\n“Well, let it be three weeks then,” said Morcerf; “but remember, at the expiration of that time no delay or subterfuge will justify you in——”\n\n“M. Albert de Morcerf,” said Beauchamp, rising in his turn, “I cannot throw you out of window for three weeks—that is to say, for twenty-four days to come—nor have you any right to split my skull open till that time has elapsed. Today is the 29th of August; the 21st of September will, therefore, be the conclusion of the term agreed on, and till that time arrives—and it is the advice of a gentleman which I am about to give you—till then we will refrain from growling and barking like two dogs chained within sight of each other.”\n\nWhen he had concluded his speech, Beauchamp bowed coldly to Albert, turned his back upon him, and went to the press-room. Albert vented his anger on a pile of newspapers, which he sent flying all over the office by switching them violently with his stick; after which ebullition he departed—not, however, without walking several times to the door of the press-room, as if he had half a mind to enter.\n\n40104m\nWhile Albert was lashing the front of his carriage in the same manner that he had the newspapers which were the innocent agents of his discomfiture, as he was crossing the barrier he perceived Morrel, who was walking with a quick step and a bright eye. He was passing the Chinese Baths, and appeared to have come from the direction of the Porte Saint-Martin, and to be going towards the Madeleine.\n\n“Ah,” said Morcerf, “there goes a happy man!” And it so happened Albert was not mistaken in his opinion.\n\n# Chapter 79. The Lemonade\n\nMorrel was, in fact, very happy. M. Noirtier had just sent for him, and he was in such haste to know the reason of his doing so that he had not stopped to take a cab, placing infinitely more dependence on his own two legs than on the four legs of a cab-horse. He had therefore set off at a furious rate from the Rue Meslay, and was hastening with rapid strides in the direction of the Faubourg Saint-Honoré.\n\nMorrel advanced with a firm, manly tread, and poor Barrois followed him as he best might. Morrel was only thirty-one, Barrois was sixty years of age; Morrel was deeply in love, and Barrois was dying with heat and exertion. These two men, thus opposed in age and interests, resembled two parts of a triangle, presenting the extremes of separation, yet nevertheless possessing their point of union. This point of union was Noirtier, and it was he who had just sent for Morrel, with the request that the latter would lose no time in coming to him—a command which Morrel obeyed to the letter, to the great discomfiture of Barrois. On arriving at the house, Morrel was not even out of breath, for love lends wings to our desires; but Barrois, who had long forgotten what it was to love, was sorely fatigued by the expedition he had been constrained to use.\n\nThe old servant introduced Morrel by a private entrance, closed the door of the study, and soon the rustling of a dress announced the arrival of Valentine. She looked marvellously beautiful in her deep mourning dress, and Morrel experienced such intense delight in gazing upon her that he felt as if he could almost have dispensed with the conversation of her grandfather.\n\nBut the easy-chair of the old man was heard rolling along the floor, and he soon made his appearance in the room. Noirtier acknowledged by a look of extreme kindness and benevolence the thanks which Morrel lavished on him for his timely intervention on behalf of Valentine and himself—an intervention which had saved them from despair. Morrel then cast on the invalid an interrogative look as to the new favor which he designed to bestow on him. Valentine was sitting at a little distance from them, timidly awaiting the moment when she should be obliged to speak. Noirtier fixed his eyes on her.\n\n“Am I to say what you told me?” asked Valentine. Noirtier made a sign that she was to do so.\n\n“Monsieur Morrel,” said Valentine to the young man, who was regarding her with the most intense interest, “my grandfather, M. Noirtier, had a thousand things to say, which he told me three days ago; and now, he has sent for you, that I may repeat them to you. I will repeat them, then; and since he has chosen me as his interpreter, I will be faithful to the trust, and will not alter a word of his intentions.”\n\n“Oh, I am listening with the greatest impatience,” replied the young man; “speak, I beg of you.”\n\nValentine cast down her eyes; this was a good omen for Morrel, for he knew that nothing but happiness could have the power of thus overcoming Valentine.\n\n“My grandfather intends leaving this house,” said she, “and Barrois is looking out for suitable apartments for him in another.”\n\n“But you, Mademoiselle de Villefort,—you, who are necessary to M. Noirtier’s happiness——”\n\n“I?” interrupted Valentine; “I shall not leave my grandfather,—that is an understood thing between us. My apartment will be close to his. Now, M. de Villefort must either give his consent to this plan or his refusal; in the first case, I shall leave directly, and in the second, I shall wait till I am of age, which will be in about ten months. Then I shall be free, I shall have an independent fortune, and”—\n\n“And what?” demanded Morrel.\n\n“And with my grandfather’s consent I shall fulfil the promise which I have made you.”\n\nValentine pronounced these last few words in such a low tone, that nothing but Morrel’s intense interest in what she was saying could have enabled him to hear them.\n\n“Have I not explained your wishes, grandpapa?” said Valentine, addressing Noirtier.\n\n“Yes,” looked the old man.\n\n“Once under my grandfather’s roof, M. Morrel can visit me in the presence of my good and worthy protector, if we still feel that the union we contemplated will be likely to insure our future comfort and happiness; in that case I shall expect M. Morrel to come and claim me at my own hands. But, alas, I have heard it said that hearts inflamed by obstacles to their desire grew cold in time of security; I trust we shall never find it so in our experience!”\n\n“Oh,” cried Morrel, almost tempted to throw himself on his knees before Noirtier and Valentine, and to adore them as two superior beings, “what have I ever done in my life to merit such unbounded happiness?”\n\n“Until that time,” continued the young girl in a calm and self-possessed tone of voice, “we will conform to circumstances, and be guided by the wishes of our friends, so long as those wishes do not tend finally to separate us; in a word, and I repeat it, because it expresses all I wish to convey,—we will wait.”\n\n“And I swear to make all the sacrifices which this word imposes, sir,” said Morrel, “not only with resignation, but with cheerfulness.”\n\n“Therefore,” continued Valentine, looking playfully at Maximilian, “no more inconsiderate actions—no more rash projects; for you surely would not wish to compromise one who from this day regards herself as destined, honorably and happily, to bear your name?”\n\nMorrel looked obedience to her commands. Noirtier regarded the lovers with a look of ineffable tenderness, while Barrois, who had remained in the room in the character of a man privileged to know everything that passed, smiled on the youthful couple as he wiped the perspiration from his bald forehead.\n\n“How hot you look, my good Barrois,” said Valentine.\n\n“Ah, I have been running very fast, mademoiselle, but I must do M. Morrel the justice to say that he ran still faster.”\n\nNoirtier directed their attention to a waiter, on which was placed a decanter containing lemonade and a glass. The decanter was nearly full, with the exception of a little, which had been already drunk by M. Noirtier.\n\n“Come, Barrois,” said the young girl, “take some of this lemonade; I see you are coveting a good draught of it.”\n\n“The fact is, mademoiselle,” said Barrois, “I am dying with thirst, and since you are so kind as to offer it me, I cannot say I should at all object to drinking your health in a glass of it.”\n\n“Take some, then, and come back immediately.”\n\nBarrois took away the waiter, and hardly was he outside the door, which in his haste he forgot to shut, than they saw him throw back his head and empty to the very dregs the glass which Valentine had filled. Valentine and Morrel were exchanging their adieux in the presence of Noirtier when a ring was heard at the door-bell. It was the signal of a visit. Valentine looked at her watch.\n\n“It is past noon,” said she, “and today is Saturday; I dare say it is the doctor, grandpapa.”\n\nNoirtier looked his conviction that she was right in her supposition.\n\n“He will come in here, and M. Morrel had better go,—do you not think so, grandpapa?”\n\n“Yes,” signed the old man.\n\n“Barrois,” cried Valentine, “Barrois!”\n\n“I am coming, mademoiselle,” replied he.\n\n“Barrois will open the door for you,” said Valentine, addressing Morrel. “And now remember one thing, Monsieur Officer, that my grandfather commands you not to take any rash or ill-advised step which would be likely to compromise our happiness.”\n\n40108m\n“I promised him to wait,” replied Morrel; “and I will wait.”\n\nAt this moment Barrois entered. “Who rang?” asked Valentine.\n\n“Doctor d’Avrigny,” said Barrois, staggering as if he would fall.\n\n“What is the matter, Barrois?” said Valentine. The old man did not answer, but looked at his master with wild staring eyes, while with his cramped hand he grasped a piece of furniture to enable him to stand upright.\n\n“He is going to fall!” cried Morrel.\n\nThe rigors which had attacked Barrois gradually increased, the features of the face became quite altered, and the convulsive movement of the muscles appeared to indicate the approach of a most serious nervous disorder. Noirtier, seeing Barrois in this pitiable condition, showed by his looks all the various emotions of sorrow and sympathy which can animate the heart of man. Barrois made some steps towards his master.\n\n“Ah, sir,” said he, “tell me what is the matter with me. I am suffering—I cannot see. A thousand fiery darts are piercing my brain. Ah, don’t touch me, pray don’t.”\n\nBy this time his haggard eyes had the appearance of being ready to start from their sockets; his head fell back, and the lower extremities of the body began to stiffen. Valentine uttered a cry of horror; Morrel took her in his arms, as if to defend her from some unknown danger.\n\n“M. d’Avrigny, M. d’Avrigny,” cried she, in a stifled voice. “Help, help!”\n\nBarrois turned round and with a great effort stumbled a few steps, then fell at the feet of Noirtier, and resting his hand on the knee of the invalid, exclaimed:\n\n“My master, my good master!”\n\nAt this moment M. de Villefort, attracted by the noise, appeared on the threshold. Morrel relaxed his hold of Valentine, and retreating to a distant corner of the room remained half hidden behind a curtain. Pale as if he had been gazing on a serpent, he fixed his terrified eye on the agonized sufferer.\n\nNoirtier, burning with impatience and terror, was in despair at his utter inability to help his old domestic, whom he regarded more in the light of a friend than a servant. One might by the fearful swelling of the veins of his forehead and the contraction of the muscles round the eye, trace the terrible conflict which was going on between the living energetic mind and the inanimate and helpless body.\n\nBarrois, his features convulsed, his eyes suffused with blood, and his head thrown back, was lying at full length, beating the floor with his hands, while his legs had become so stiff, that they looked as if they would break rather than bend. A slight appearance of foam was visible around the mouth, and he breathed painfully, and with extreme difficulty.\n\nVillefort seemed stupefied with astonishment, and remained gazing intently on the scene before him without uttering a word. He had not seen Morrel. After a moment of dumb contemplation, during which his face became pale and his hair seemed to stand on end, he sprang towards the door, crying out:\n\n“Doctor, doctor! come instantly, pray come!”\n\n“Madame, madame!” cried Valentine, calling her step-mother, and running upstairs to meet her; “come quick, quick!—and bring your bottle of smelling-salts with you.”\n\n“What is the matter?” said Madame de Villefort in a harsh and constrained tone.\n\n“Oh! come! come!”\n\n“But where is the doctor?” exclaimed Villefort; “where is he?”\n\nMadame de Villefort now deliberately descended the staircase. In one hand she held her handkerchief, with which she appeared to be wiping her face, and in the other a bottle of English smelling-salts. Her first look on entering the room was at Noirtier, whose face, independent of the emotion which such a scene could not fail of producing, proclaimed him to be in possession of his usual health; her second glance was at the dying man. She turned pale, and her eye passed quickly from the servant and rested on the master.\n\n“In the name of heaven, madame,” said Villefort, “where is the doctor? He was with you just now. You see this is a fit of apoplexy, and he might be saved if he could but be bled!”\n\n“Has he eaten anything lately?” asked Madame de Villefort, eluding her husband’s question.\n\n“Madame,” replied Valentine, “he has not even breakfasted. He has been running very fast on an errand with which my grandfather charged him, and when he returned, took nothing but a glass of lemonade.”\n\n“Ah,” said Madame de Villefort, “why did he not take wine? Lemonade was a very bad thing for him.”\n\n“Grandpapa’s bottle of lemonade was standing just by his side; poor Barrois was very thirsty, and was thankful to drink anything he could find.”\n\nMadame de Villefort started. Noirtier looked at her with a glance of the most profound scrutiny.\n\n“He has such a short neck,” said she.\n\n“Madame,” said Villefort, “I ask where is M. d’Avrigny? In God’s name answer me!”\n\n“He is with Edward, who is not quite well,” replied Madame de Villefort, no longer being able to avoid answering.\n\nVillefort rushed upstairs to fetch him.\n\n“Take this,” said Madame de Villefort, giving her smelling-bottle to Valentine. “They will, no doubt, bleed him; therefore I will retire, for I cannot endure the sight of blood;” and she followed her husband upstairs. Morrel now emerged from his hiding-place, where he had remained quite unperceived, so great had been the general confusion.\n\n“Go away as quick as you can, Maximilian,” said Valentine, “and stay till I send for you. Go.”\n\nMorrel looked towards Noirtier for permission to retire. The old man, who had preserved all his usual coolness, made a sign to him to do so. The young man pressed Valentine’s hand to his lips, and then left the house by a back staircase.\n\nAt the same moment that he quitted the room, Villefort and the doctor entered by an opposite door. Barrois was now showing signs of returning consciousness. The crisis seemed past, a low moaning was heard, and he raised himself on one knee. D’Avrigny and Villefort laid him on a couch.\n\n“What do you prescribe, doctor?” demanded Villefort.\n\n“Give me some water and ether. You have some in the house, have you not?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Send for some oil of turpentine and tartar emetic.”\n\nVillefort immediately despatched a messenger. “And now let everyone retire.”\n\n“Must I go too?” asked Valentine timidly.\n\n“Yes, mademoiselle, you especially,” replied the doctor abruptly.\n\nValentine looked at M. d’Avrigny with astonishment, kissed her grandfather on the forehead, and left the room. The doctor closed the door after her with a gloomy air.\n\n“Look, look, doctor,” said Villefort, “he is quite coming round again; I really do not think, after all, it is anything of consequence.”\n\nM. d’Avrigny answered by a melancholy smile.\n\n“How do you feel, Barrois?” asked he.\n\n“A little better, sir.”\n\n“Will you drink some of this ether and water?”\n\n“I will try; but don’t touch me.”\n\n“Why not?”\n\n“Because I feel that if you were only to touch me with the tip of your finger the fit would return.”\n\n“Drink.”\n\nBarrois took the glass, and, raising it to his purple lips, took about half of the liquid offered him.\n\n“Where do you suffer?” asked the doctor.\n\n“Everywhere. I feel cramps over my whole body.”\n\n“Do you find any dazzling sensation before the eyes?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Any noise in the ears?”\n\n“Frightful.”\n40112m\n\n“When did you first feel that?”\n\n“Just now.”\n\n“Suddenly?”\n\n“Yes, like a clap of thunder.”\n\n“Did you feel nothing of it yesterday or the day before?”\n\n“No drowsiness?”\n\n“None.”\n\n“What have you eaten today?”\n\n“I have eaten nothing; I only drank a glass of my master’s lemonade—that’s all.” And Barrois turned towards Noirtier, who, immovably fixed in his armchair, was contemplating this terrible scene without allowing a word or a movement to escape him.\n\n“Where is this lemonade?” asked the doctor eagerly.\n\n“Downstairs in the decanter.”\n\n“Whereabouts downstairs?”\n\n“In the kitchen.”\n\n“Shall I go and fetch it, doctor?” inquired Villefort.\n\n“No, stay here and try to make Barrois drink the rest of this glass of ether and water. I will go myself and fetch the lemonade.”\n\nD’Avrigny bounded towards the door, flew down the back staircase, and almost knocked down Madame de Villefort, in his haste, who was herself going down to the kitchen. She cried out, but d’Avrigny paid no attention to her; possessed with but one idea, he cleared the last four steps with a bound, and rushed into the kitchen, where he saw the decanter about three parts empty still standing on the waiter, where it had been left. He darted upon it as an eagle would seize upon its prey. Panting with loss of breath, he returned to the room he had just left. Madame de Villefort was slowly ascending the steps which led to her room.\n\n“Is this the decanter you spoke of?” asked d’Avrigny.\n\n“Yes, doctor.”\n\n“Is this the same lemonade of which you partook?”\n\n“I believe so.”\n\n“What did it taste like?”\n\n“It had a bitter taste.”\n\nThe doctor poured some drops of the lemonade into the palm of his hand, put his lips to it, and after having rinsed his mouth as a man does when he is tasting wine, he spat the liquor into the fireplace.\n\n“It is no doubt the same,” said he. “Did you drink some too, M. Noirtier?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“And did you also discover a bitter taste?”\n\n“Yes.”\n“Oh, doctor,” cried Barrois, “the fit is coming on again. Oh, do something for me.” The doctor flew to his patient.\n\n“That emetic, Villefort—see if it is coming.”\n\nVillefort sprang into the passage, exclaiming, “The emetic! the emetic!—is it come yet?” No one answered. The most profound terror reigned throughout the house.\n\n“If I had anything by means of which I could inflate the lungs,” said d’Avrigny, looking around him, “perhaps I might prevent suffocation. But there is nothing which would do!—nothing!”\n\n40114m\n“Oh, sir,” cried Barrois, “are you going to let me die without help? Oh, I am dying! Oh, save me!”\n\n“A pen, a pen!” said the doctor. There was one lying on the table; he endeavored to introduce it into the mouth of the patient, who, in the midst of his convulsions, was making vain attempts to vomit; but the jaws were so clenched that the pen could not pass them. This second attack was much more violent than the first, and he had slipped from the couch to the ground, where he was writhing in agony. The doctor left him in this paroxysm, knowing that he could do nothing to alleviate it, and, going up to Noirtier, said abruptly:\n\n“How do you find yourself?—well?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Have you any weight on the chest; or does your stomach feel light and comfortable—eh?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Then you feel pretty much as you generally do after you have had the dose which I am accustomed to give you every Sunday?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Did Barrois make your lemonade?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Was it you who asked him to drink some of it?”\n“No.”\n\n“Was it M. de Villefort?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Madame?”\n“No.”\n\n“It was your granddaughter, then, was it not?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nA groan from Barrois, accompanied by a yawn which seemed to crack the very jawbones, attracted the attention of M. d’Avrigny; he left M. Noirtier, and returned to the sick man.\n\n“Barrois,” said the doctor, “can you speak?” Barrois muttered a few unintelligible words. “Try and make an effort to do so, my good man.” said d’Avrigny. Barrois reopened his bloodshot eyes.\n\n“Who made the lemonade?”\n\n“I did.”\n\n“Did you bring it to your master directly it was made?”\n“No.”\n\n“You left it somewhere, then, in the meantime?”\n\n“Yes; I left it in the pantry, because I was called away.”\n\n“Who brought it into this room, then?”\n\n“Mademoiselle Valentine.” D’Avrigny struck his forehead with his hand.\n\n“Gracious heaven,” exclaimed he.\n\n“Doctor, doctor!” cried Barrois, who felt another fit coming.\n\n“Will they never bring that emetic?” asked the doctor.\n\n“Here is a glass with one already prepared,” said Villefort, entering the room.\n\n“Who prepared it?”\n\n“The chemist who came here with me.”\n40116m\n\n“Drink it,” said the doctor to Barrois.\n\n“Impossible, doctor; it is too late; my throat is closing up. I am choking! Oh, my heart! Ah, my head!—Oh, what agony!—Shall I suffer like this long?”\n\n“No, no, friend,” replied the doctor, “you will soon cease to suffer.”\n\n“Ah, I understand you,” said the unhappy man. “My God, have mercy upon me!” and, uttering a fearful cry, Barrois fell back as if he had been struck by lightning. D’Avrigny put his hand to his heart, and placed a glass before his lips.\n\n“Well?” said Villefort.\n\n“Go to the kitchen and get me some syrup of violets.”\n\nVillefort went immediately.\n\n“Do not be alarmed, M. Noirtier,” said d’Avrigny; “I am going to take my patient into the next room to bleed him; this sort of attack is very frightful to witness.”\n\nAnd taking Barrois under the arms, he dragged him into an adjoining room; but almost immediately he returned to fetch the lemonade. Noirtier closed his right eye.\n\n“You want Valentine, do you not? I will tell them to send her to you.”\n\nVillefort returned, and d’Avrigny met him in the passage.\n\n“Well, how is he now?” asked he.\n\n“Come in here,” said d’Avrigny, and he took him into the chamber where the sick man lay.\n\n“Is he still in a fit?” said the procureur.\n\n“He is dead.”\n\nVillefort drew back a few steps, and, clasping his hands, exclaimed, with real amazement and sympathy, “Dead?—and so soon too!”\n\n“Yes, it is very soon,” said the doctor, looking at the corpse before him; “but that ought not to astonish you; Monsieur and Madame de Saint-Méran died as soon. People die very suddenly in your house, M. de Villefort.”\n\n“What?” cried the magistrate, with an accent of horror and consternation, “are you still harping on that terrible idea?”\n\n“Still, sir; and I shall always do so,” replied d’Avrigny, “for it has never for one instant ceased to retain possession of my mind; and that you may be quite sure I am not mistaken this time, listen well to what I am going to say, M. de Villefort.”\n\nThe magistrate trembled convulsively.\n\n“There is a poison which destroys life almost without leaving any perceptible traces. I know it well; I have studied it in all its forms and in the effects which it produces. I recognized the presence of this poison in the case of poor Barrois as well as in that of Madame de Saint-Méran. There is a way of detecting its presence. It restores the blue color of litmus-paper reddened by an acid, and it turns syrup of violets green. We have no litmus-paper, but, see, here they come with the syrup of violets.”\n\nThe doctor was right; steps were heard in the passage. M. d’Avrigny opened the door, and took from the hands of the chambermaid a cup which contained two or three spoonfuls of the syrup, he then carefully closed the door.\n\n“Look,” said he to the procureur, whose heart beat so loudly that it might almost be heard, “here is in this cup some syrup of violets, and this decanter contains the remainder of the lemonade of which M. Noirtier and Barrois partook. If the lemonade be pure and inoffensive, the syrup will retain its color; if, on the contrary, the lemonade be drugged with poison, the syrup will become green. Look closely!”\n\nThe doctor then slowly poured some drops of the lemonade from the decanter into the cup, and in an instant a light cloudy sediment began to form at the bottom of the cup; this sediment first took a blue shade, then from the color of sapphire it passed to that of opal, and from opal to emerald. Arrived at this last hue, it changed no more. The result of the experiment left no doubt whatever on the mind.\n\n“The unfortunate Barrois has been poisoned,” said d’Avrigny, “and I will maintain this assertion before God and man.”\n\nVillefort said nothing, but he clasped his hands, opened his haggard eyes, and, overcome with his emotion, sank into a chair.\n\n# Chapter 80. The Accusation\n\nM. d’Avrigny soon restored the magistrate to consciousness, who had looked like a second corpse in that chamber of death.\n\n“Oh, death is in my house!” cried Villefort.\n\n“Say, rather, crime!” replied the doctor.\n\n“M. d’Avrigny,” cried Villefort, “I cannot tell you all I feel at this moment,—terror, grief, madness.”\n\n“Yes,” said M. d’Avrigny, with an imposing calmness, “but I think it is now time to act. I think it is time to stop this torrent of mortality. I can no longer bear to be in possession of these secrets without the hope of seeing the victims and society generally revenged.”\n\nVillefort cast a gloomy look around him. “In my house,” murmured he, “in my house!”\n\n“Come, magistrate,” said M. d’Avrigny, “show yourself a man; as an interpreter of the law, do honor to your profession by sacrificing your selfish interests to it.”\n\n“You make me shudder, doctor. Do you talk of a sacrifice?”\n\n“I do.”\n“Do you then suspect anyone?”\n\n“I suspect no one; death raps at your door—it enters—it goes, not blindfolded, but circumspectly, from room to room. Well, I follow its course, I track its passage; I adopt the wisdom of the ancients, and feel my way, for my friendship for your family and my respect for you are as a twofold bandage over my eyes; well——”\n\n“Oh, speak, speak, doctor; I shall have courage.”\n\n“Well, sir, you have in your establishment, or in your family, perhaps, one of the frightful monstrosities of which each century produces only one. Locusta and Agrippina, living at the same time, were an exception, and proved the determination of Providence to effect the entire ruin of the Roman empire, sullied by so many crimes. Brunhilda and Fredegund were the results of the painful struggle of civilization in its infancy, when man was learning to control mind, were it even by an emissary from the realms of darkness. All these women had been, or were, beautiful. The same flower of innocence had flourished, or was still flourishing, on their brow, that is seen on the brow of the culprit in your house.”\n40120m\n\nVillefort shrieked, clasped his hands, and looked at the doctor with a supplicating air. But the latter went on without pity:\n\n“‘Seek whom the crime will profit,’ says an axiom of jurisprudence.”\n\n“Doctor,” cried Villefort, “alas, doctor, how often has man’s justice been deceived by those fatal words. I know not why, but I feel that this crime——”\n\n“You acknowledge, then, the existence of the crime?”\n\n“Yes, I see too plainly that it does exist. But it seems that it is intended to affect me personally. I fear an attack myself, after all these disasters.”\n\n“Oh, man!” murmured d’Avrigny, “the most selfish of all animals, the most personal of all creatures, who believes the earth turns, the sun shines, and death strikes for him alone,—an ant cursing God from the top of a blade of grass! And have those who have lost their lives lost nothing?—M. de Saint-Méran, Madame de Saint-Méran, M. Noirtier——”\n\n“How? M. Noirtier?”\n\n“Yes; think you it was the poor servant’s life was coveted? No, no; like Shakespeare’s Polonius, he died for another. It was Noirtier the lemonade was intended for—it is Noirtier, logically speaking, who drank it. The other drank it only by accident, and, although Barrois is dead, it was Noirtier whose death was wished for.”\n\n“But why did it not kill my father?”\n\n“I told you one evening in the garden after Madame de Saint-Méran’s death—because his system is accustomed to that very poison, and the dose was trifling to him, which would be fatal to another; because no one knows, not even the assassin, that, for the last twelve months, I have given M. Noirtier brucine for his paralytic affection, while the assassin is not ignorant, for he has proved that brucine is a violent poison.”\n\n“Oh, have pity—have pity!” murmured Villefort, wringing his hands.\n\n“Follow the culprit’s steps; he first kills M. de Saint-Méran——”\n\n“Oh, doctor!”\n\n“I would swear to it; what I heard of his symptoms agrees too well with what I have seen in the other cases.” Villefort ceased to contend; he only groaned. “He first kills M. de Saint-Méran,” repeated the doctor, “then Madame de Saint-Méran,—a double fortune to inherit.” Villefort wiped the perspiration from his forehead. “Listen attentively.”\n\n“Alas,” stammered Villefort, “I do not lose a single word.”\n\n“M. Noirtier,” resumed M. d’Avrigny in the same pitiless tone,—“M. Noirtier had once made a will against you—against your family—in favor of the poor, in fact; M. Noirtier is spared, because nothing is expected from him. But he has no sooner destroyed his first will and made a second, than, for fear he should make a third, he is struck down. The will was made the day before yesterday, I believe; you see there has been no time lost.”\n\n“Oh, mercy, M. d’Avrigny!”\n\n“No mercy, sir! The physician has a sacred mission on earth; and to fulfil it he begins at the source of life, and goes down to the mysterious darkness of the tomb. When crime has been committed, and God, doubtless in anger, turns away his face, it is for the physician to bring the culprit to justice.”\n\n40122m\n“Have mercy on my child, sir,” murmured Villefort.\n\n“You see it is yourself who have first named her—you, her father.”\n\n“Have pity on Valentine! Listen, it is impossible. I would as willingly accuse myself! Valentine, whose heart is pure as a diamond or a lily!”\n\n“No pity, procureur; the crime is fragrant. Mademoiselle herself packed all the medicines which were sent to M. de Saint-Méran; and M. de Saint-Méran is dead. Mademoiselle de Villefort prepared all the cooling draughts which Madame de Saint-Méran took, and Madame de Saint-Méran is dead. Mademoiselle de Villefort took from the hands of Barrois, who was sent out, the lemonade which M. Noirtier had every morning, and he has escaped by a miracle. Mademoiselle de Villefort is the culprit—she is the poisoner! To you, as the king’s attorney, I denounce Mademoiselle de Villefort, do your duty.”\n\n“Doctor, I resist no longer—I can no longer defend myself—I believe you; but, for pity’s sake, spare my life, my honor!”\n\n“M. de Villefort,” replied the doctor, with increased vehemence, “there are occasions when I dispense with all foolish human circumspection. If your daughter had committed only one crime, and I saw her meditating another, I would say ‘Warn her, punish her, let her pass the remainder of her life in a convent, weeping and praying.’ If she had committed two crimes, I would say, ‘Here, M. de Villefort, is a poison that the prisoner is not acquainted with,—one that has no known antidote, quick as thought, rapid as lightning, mortal as the thunderbolt; give her that poison, recommending her soul to God, and save your honor and your life, for it is yours she aims at; and I can picture her approaching your pillow with her hypocritical smiles and her sweet exhortations. Woe to you, M. de Villefort, if you do not strike first!’ This is what I would say had she only killed two persons but she has seen three deaths,—has contemplated three murdered persons,—has knelt by three corpses! To the scaffold with the poisoner—to the scaffold! Do you talk of your honor? Do what I tell you, and immortality awaits you!”\n\nVillefort fell on his knees.\n\n“Listen,” said he; “I have not the strength of mind you have, or rather that which you would not have, if instead of my daughter Valentine your daughter Madeleine were concerned.” The doctor turned pale. “Doctor, every son of woman is born to suffer and to die; I am content to suffer and to await death.”\n\n“Beware,” said M. d’Avrigny, “it may come slowly; you will see it approach after having struck your father, your wife, perhaps your son.”\n\nVillefort, suffocating, pressed the doctor’s arm.\n\n40124m\n“Listen,” cried he; “pity me—help me! No, my daughter is not guilty. If you drag us both before a tribunal I will still say, ‘No, my daughter is not guilty;—there is no crime in my house. I will not acknowledge a crime in my house; for when crime enters a dwelling, it is like death—it does not come alone.’ Listen. What does it signify to you if I am murdered? Are you my friend? Are you a man? Have you a heart? No, you are a physician! Well, I tell you I will not drag my daughter before a tribunal, and give her up to the executioner! The bare idea would kill me—would drive me like a madman to dig my heart out with my finger-nails! And if you were mistaken, doctor—if it were not my daughter—if I should come one day, pale as a spectre, and say to you, ‘Assassin, you have killed my child!’—hold—if that should happen, although I am a Christian, M. d’Avrigny, I should kill myself.”\n\n“Well,” said the doctor, after a moment’s silence, “I will wait.”\n\nVillefort looked at him as if he had doubted his words.\n\n“Only,” continued M. d’Avrigny, with a slow and solemn tone, “if anyone falls ill in your house, if you feel yourself attacked, do not send for me, for I will come no more. I will consent to share this dreadful secret with you, but I will not allow shame and remorse to grow and increase in my conscience, as crime and misery will in your house.”\n\n“Then you abandon me, doctor?”\n\n“Yes, for I can follow you no farther, and I only stop at the foot of the scaffold. Some further discovery will be made, which will bring this dreadful tragedy to a close. Adieu.”\n\n“I entreat you, doctor!”\n\n“All the horrors that disturb my thoughts make your house odious and fatal. Adieu, sir.”\n\n“One word—one single word more, doctor! You go, leaving me in all the horror of my situation, after increasing it by what you have revealed to me. But what will be reported of the sudden death of the poor old servant?”\n\n“True,” said M. d’Avrigny; “we will return.”\n\nThe doctor went out first, followed by M. de Villefort. The terrified servants were on the stairs and in the passage where the doctor would pass.\n\n“Sir,” said d’Avrigny to Villefort, so loud that all might hear, “poor Barrois has led too sedentary a life of late; accustomed formerly to ride on horseback, or in the carriage, to the four corners of Europe, the monotonous walk around that armchair has killed him—his blood has thickened. He was stout, had a short, thick neck; he was attacked with apoplexy, and I was called in too late. By the way,” added he in a low tone, “take care to throw away that cup of syrup of violets in the ashes.”\n\nThe doctor, without shaking hands with Villefort, without adding a word to what he had said, went out, amid the tears and lamentations of the whole household. The same evening all Villefort’s servants, who had assembled in the kitchen, and had a long consultation, came to tell Madame de Villefort that they wished to leave. No entreaty, no proposition of increased wages, could induce them to remain; to every argument they replied, “We must go, for death is in this house.”\n\nThey all left, in spite of prayers and entreaties, testifying their regret at leaving so good a master and mistress, and especially Mademoiselle Valentine, so good, so kind, and so gentle.\n\nVillefort looked at Valentine as they said this. She was in tears, and, strange as it was, in spite of the emotions he felt at the sight of these tears, he looked also at Madame de Villefort, and it appeared to him as if a slight gloomy smile had passed over her thin lips, like a meteor seen passing inauspiciously between two clouds in a stormy sky.\n\n# Chapter 81. The Room of the Retired Baker\n\nThe evening of the day on which the Count of Morcerf had left Danglars’ house with feelings of shame and anger at the rejection of the projected alliance, M. Andrea Cavalcanti, with curled hair, moustaches in perfect order, and white gloves which fitted admirably, had entered the courtyard of the banker’s house in Rue de la Chaussée d’Antin. He had not been more than ten minutes in the drawing-room before he drew Danglars aside into the recess of a bow-window, and, after an ingenious preamble, related to him all his anxieties and cares since his noble father’s departure. He acknowledged the extreme kindness which had been shown him by the banker’s family, in which he had been received as a son, and where, besides, his warmest affections had found an object on which to centre in Mademoiselle Danglars.\n\nDanglars listened with the most profound attention; he had expected this declaration for the last two or three days, and when at last it came his eyes glistened as much as they had lowered on listening to Morcerf. He would not, however, yield immediately to the young man’s request, but made a few conscientious objections.\n\n“Are you not rather young, M. Andrea, to think of marrying?”\n\n“I think not, sir,” replied M. Cavalcanti; “in Italy the nobility generally marry young. Life is so uncertain, that we ought to secure happiness while it is within our reach.”\n\n“Well, sir,” said Danglars, “in case your proposals, which do me honor, are accepted by my wife and daughter, by whom shall the preliminary arrangements be settled? So important a negotiation should, I think, be conducted by the respective fathers of the young people.”\n\n“Sir, my father is a man of great foresight and prudence. Thinking that I might wish to settle in France, he left me at his departure, together with the papers establishing my identity, a letter promising, if he approved of my choice, 150,000 livres per annum from the day I was married. So far as I can judge, I suppose this to be a quarter of my father’s revenue.”\n\n“I,” said Danglars, “have always intended giving my daughter 500,000 francs as her dowry; she is, besides, my sole heiress.”\n\n“All would then be easily arranged if the baroness and her daughter are willing. We should command an annuity of 175,000 livres. Supposing, also, I should persuade the marquis to give me my capital, which is not likely, but still is possible, we would place these two or three millions in your hands, whose talent might make it realize ten per cent.”\n\n“I never give more than four per cent, and generally only three and a half; but to my son-in-law I would give five, and we would share the profits.”\n\n“Very good, father-in-law,” said Cavalcanti, yielding to his low-born nature, which would escape sometimes through the aristocratic gloss with which he sought to conceal it. Correcting himself immediately, he said, “Excuse me, sir; hope alone makes me almost mad,—what will not reality do?”\n\n“But,” said Danglars, who, on his part, did not perceive how soon the conversation, which was at first disinterested, was turning to a business transaction, “there is, doubtless, a part of your fortune your father could not refuse you?”\n\n“Which?” asked the young man.\n\n“That you inherit from your mother.”\n\n“Truly, from my mother, Leonora Corsinari.”\n“How much may it amount to?”\n\n“Indeed, sir,” said Andrea, “I assure you I have never given the subject a thought, but I suppose it must have been at least two millions.”\n\nDanglars felt as much overcome with joy as the miser who finds a lost treasure, or as the shipwrecked mariner who feels himself on solid ground instead of in the abyss which he expected would swallow him up.\n\n“Well, sir,” said Andrea, bowing to the banker respectfully, “may I hope?”\n\n“You may not only hope,” said Danglars, “but consider it a settled thing, if no obstacle arises on your part.”\n\n“I am, indeed, rejoiced,” said Andrea.\n\n“But,” said Danglars thoughtfully, “how is it that your patron, M. de Monte Cristo, did not make his proposal for you?”\n\nAndrea blushed imperceptibly.\n\n“I have just left the count, sir,” said he; “he is, doubtless, a delightful man but inconceivably peculiar in his ideas. He esteems me highly. He even told me he had not the slightest doubt that my father would give me the capital instead of the interest of my property. He has promised to use his influence to obtain it for me; but he also declared that he never had taken on himself the responsibility of making proposals for another, and he never would. I must, however, do him the justice to add that he assured me if ever he had regretted the repugnance he felt to such a step it was on this occasion, because he thought the projected union would be a happy and suitable one. Besides, if he will do nothing officially, he will answer any questions you propose to him. And now,” continued he, with one of his most charming smiles, “having finished talking to the father-in-law, I must address myself to the banker.”\n\n“And what may you have to say to him?” said Danglars, laughing in his turn.\n\n“That the day after tomorrow I shall have to draw upon you for about four thousand francs; but the count, expecting my bachelor’s revenue could not suffice for the coming month’s outlay, has offered me a draft for twenty thousand francs. It bears his signature, as you see, which is all-sufficient.”\n\n“Bring me a million such as that,” said Danglars, “I shall be well pleased,” putting the draft in his pocket. “Fix your own hour for tomorrow, and my cashier shall call on you with a check for eighty thousand francs.”\n\n“At ten o’clock then, if you please; I should like it early, as I am going into the country tomorrow.”\n\n“Very well, at ten o’clock; you are still at the Hôtel des Princes?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe following morning, with the banker’s usual punctuality, the eighty thousand francs were placed in the young man’s hands, as he was on the point of starting, after having left two hundred francs for Caderousse. He went out chiefly to avoid this dangerous enemy, and returned as late as possible in the evening.\n\nBut scarcely had he stepped out of his carriage when the porter met him with a parcel in his hand.\n\n“Sir,” said he, “that man has been here.”\n\n“What man?” said Andrea carelessly, apparently forgetting him whom he but too well recollected.\n\n“Him to whom your excellency pays that little annuity.”\n\n“Oh,” said Andrea, “my father’s old servant. Well, you gave him the two hundred francs I had left for him?”\n\n“Yes, your excellency.” Andrea had expressed a wish to be thus addressed. “But,” continued the porter, “he would not take them.”\n\nAndrea turned pale, but as it was dark his pallor was not perceptible. “What? he would not take them?” said he with slight emotion.\n40130m\n\n“No, he wished to speak to your excellency; I told him you were gone out, and after some dispute he believed me and gave me this letter, which he had brought with him already sealed.”\n\n“Give it me,” said Andrea, and he read by the light of his carriage-lamp:\n\n“‘You know where I live; I expect you tomorrow morning at nine o’clock.’”\n\nAndrea examined it carefully, to ascertain if the letter had been opened, or if any indiscreet eyes had seen its contents; but it was so carefully folded, that no one could have read it, and the seal was perfect.\n\n“Very well,” said he. “Poor man, he is a worthy creature.” He left the porter to ponder on these words, not knowing which most to admire, the master or the servant.\n\n“Take out the horses quickly, and come up to me,” said Andrea to his groom. In two seconds the young man had reached his room and burnt Caderousse’s letter. The servant entered just as he had finished.\n\n“You are about my height, Pierre,” said he.\n\n“I have that honor, your excellency.”\n\n“You had a new livery yesterday?”\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\n“I have an engagement with a pretty little girl for this evening, and do not wish to be known; lend me your livery till tomorrow. I may sleep, perhaps, at an inn.”\n\nPierre obeyed. Five minutes after, Andrea left the hotel, completely disguised, took a cabriolet, and ordered the driver to take him to the Cheval Rouge, at Picpus. The next morning he left that inn as he had left the Hôtel des Princes, without being noticed, walked down the Faubourg Saint-Antoine, along the boulevard to Rue Ménilmontant, and stopping at the door of the third house on the left looked for someone of whom to make inquiry in the porter’s absence.\n\n“For whom are you looking, my fine fellow?” asked the fruiteress on the opposite side.\n\n“Monsieur Pailletin, if you please, my good woman,” replied Andrea.\n\n“A retired baker?” asked the fruiteress.\n“Exactly.”\n\n“He lives at the end of the yard, on the left, on the third story.”\n\n40132m\nAndrea went as she directed him, and on the third floor he found a hare’s paw, which, by the hasty ringing of the bell, it was evident he pulled with considerable ill-temper. A moment after Caderousse’s face appeared at the grating in the door.\n\n“Ah! you are punctual,” said he, as he drew back the door.\n\n“Confound you and your punctuality!” said Andrea, throwing himself into a chair in a manner which implied that he would rather have flung it at the head of his host.\n\n“Come, come, my little fellow, don’t be angry. See, I have thought about you—look at the good breakfast we are going to have; nothing but what you are fond of.”\n\nAndrea, indeed, inhaled the scent of something cooking which was not unwelcome to him, hungry as he was; it was that mixture of fat and garlic peculiar to Provençal kitchens of an inferior order, added to that of dried fish, and above all, the pungent smell of musk and cloves. These odors escaped from two deep dishes which were covered and placed on a stove, and from a copper pan placed in an old iron pot. In an adjoining room Andrea saw also a tolerably clean table prepared for two, two bottles of wine sealed, the one with green, the other with yellow, a supply of brandy in a decanter, and a measure of fruit in a cabbage-leaf, cleverly arranged on an earthenware plate.\n\n“What do you think of it, my little fellow?” said Caderousse. “Ay, that smells good! You know I used to be a good cook; do you recollect how you used to lick your fingers? You were among the first who tasted any of my dishes, and I think you relished them tolerably.” While speaking, Caderousse went on peeling a fresh supply of onions.\n\n“But,” said Andrea, ill-temperedly, “by my faith, if it was only to breakfast with you, that you disturbed me, I wish the devil had taken you!”\n\n“My boy,” said Caderousse sententiously, “one can talk while eating. And then, you ungrateful being, you are not pleased to see an old friend? I am weeping with joy.”\n\nHe was truly crying, but it would have been difficult to say whether joy or the onions produced the greatest effect on the lachrymal glands of the old innkeeper of the Pont-du-Gard.\n\n“Hold your tongue, hypocrite,” said Andrea; “you love me!”\n\n“Yes, I do, or may the devil take me. I know it is a weakness,” said Caderousse, “but it overpowers me.”\n\n“And yet it has not prevented your sending for me to play me some trick.”\n\n“Come,” said Caderousse, wiping his large knife on his apron, “if I did not like you, do you think I should endure the wretched life you lead me? Think for a moment. You have your servant’s clothes on—you therefore keep a servant; I have none, and am obliged to prepare my own meals. You abuse my cookery because you dine at the table d’hôte of the Hôtel des Princes, or the Café de Paris. Well, I too could keep a servant; I too could have a tilbury; I too could dine where I like; but why do I not? Because I would not annoy my little Benedetto. Come, just acknowledge that I could, eh?”\n\nThis address was accompanied by a look which was by no means difficult to understand.\n\n“Well,” said Andrea, “admitting your love, why do you want me to breakfast with you?”\n\n“That I may have the pleasure of seeing you, my little fellow.”\n\n“What is the use of seeing me after we have made all our arrangements?”\n\n“Eh, dear friend,” said Caderousse, “are wills ever made without codicils? But you first came to breakfast, did you not? Well, sit down, and let us begin with these pilchards, and this fresh butter; which I have put on some vine-leaves to please you, wicked one. Ah, yes; you look at my room, my four straw chairs, my images, three francs each. But what do you expect? This is not the Hôtel des Princes.”\n\n“Come, you are growing discontented, you are no longer happy; you, who only wish to live like a retired baker.”\n\nCaderousse sighed.\n\n“Well, what have you to say? you have seen your dream realized.”\n\n“I can still say it is a dream; a retired baker, my poor Benedetto, is rich—he has an annuity.”\n\n“Well, you have an annuity.”\n\n“I have?”\n\n40134m\n“Yes, since I bring you your two hundred francs.”\n\nCaderousse shrugged his shoulders.\n\n“It is humiliating,” said he, “thus to receive money given grudgingly,—an uncertain supply which may soon fail. You see I am obliged to economize, in case your prosperity should cease. Well, my friend, fortune is inconstant, as the chaplain of the regiment said. I know your prosperity is great, you rascal; you are to marry the daughter of Danglars.”\n\n“What? of Danglars?”\n\n“Yes, to be sure; must I say Baron Danglars? I might as well say Count Benedetto. He was an old friend of mine and if he had not so bad a memory he ought to invite me to your wedding, seeing he came to mine. Yes, yes, to mine; gad, he was not so proud then,—he was an under-clerk to the good M. Morrel. I have dined many times with him and the Count of Morcerf, so you see I have some high connections and were I to cultivate them a little, we might meet in the same drawing-rooms.”\n\n“Come, your jealousy represents everything to you in the wrong light.”\n\n“That is all very fine, Benedetto mio, but I know what I am saying. Perhaps I may one day put on my best coat, and presenting myself at the great gate, introduce myself. Meanwhile let us sit down and eat.”\n\nCaderousse set the example and attacked the breakfast with good appetite, praising each dish he set before his visitor. The latter seemed to have resigned himself; he drew the corks, and partook largely of the fish with the garlic and fat.\n\n“Ah, mate,” said Caderousse, “you are getting on better terms with your old landlord!”\n\n“Faith, yes,” replied Andrea, whose hunger prevailed over every other feeling.\n\n“So you like it, you rogue?”\n\n“So much that I wonder how a man who can cook thus can complain of hard living.”\n\n“Do you see,” said Caderousse, “all my happiness is marred by one thought?”\n\n“What is that?”\n\n“That I am dependent on another, I who have always gained my own livelihood honestly.”\n\n“Do not let that disturb you, I have enough for two.”\n\n“No, truly; you may believe me if you will; at the end of every month I am tormented by remorse.”\n\n“Good Caderousse!”\n\n“So much so, that yesterday I would not take the two hundred francs.”\n\n“Yes, you wished to speak to me; but was it indeed remorse, tell me?”\n\n“True remorse; and, besides, an idea had struck me.”\n\nAndrea shuddered; he always did so at Caderousse’s ideas.\n\n“It is miserable—do you see?—always to wait till the end of the month.”\n\n“Oh,” said Andrea philosophically, determined to watch his companion narrowly, “does not life pass in waiting? Do I, for instance, fare better? Well, I wait patiently, do I not?”\n\n“Yes; because instead of expecting two hundred wretched francs, you expect five or six thousand, perhaps ten, perhaps even twelve, for you take care not to let anyone know the utmost. Down there, you always had little presents and Christmas-boxes, which you tried to hide from your poor friend Caderousse. Fortunately he is a cunning fellow, that friend Caderousse.”\n\n“There you are beginning again to ramble, to talk again and again of the past! But what is the use of teasing me with going all over that again?”\n\n“Ah, you are only one-and-twenty, and can forget the past; I am fifty, and am obliged to recollect it. But let us return to business.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“I was going to say, if I were in your place——”\n\n“Well.”\n\n“I would realize——”\n\n“How would you realize?”\n\n“I would ask for six months’ in advance, under pretence of being able to purchase a farm, then with my six months I would decamp.”\n\n“Well, well,” said Andrea, “that isn’t a bad idea.”\n\n“My dear friend,” said Caderousse, “eat of my bread, and take my advice; you will be none the worse off, physically or morally.”\n\n“But,” said Andrea, “why do you not act on the advice you gave me? Why do you not realize a six months’, a year’s advance even, and retire to Brussels? Instead of living the retired baker, you might live as a bankrupt, using his privileges; that would be very good.”\n\n“But how the devil would you have me retire on twelve hundred francs?”\n\n“Ah, Caderousse,” said Andrea, “how covetous you are! Two months ago you were dying with hunger.”\n\n“The appetite grows by what it feeds on,” said Caderousse, grinning and showing his teeth, like a monkey laughing or a tiger growling. “And,” added he, biting off with his large white teeth an enormous mouthful of bread, “I have formed a plan.”\n\nCaderousse’s plans alarmed Andrea still more than his ideas; ideas were but the germ, the plan was reality.\n\n“Let me see your plan; I dare say it is a pretty one.”\n\n“Why not? Who formed the plan by which we left the establishment of M——! eh? was it not I? and it was no bad one I believe, since here we are!”\n\n“I do not say,” replied Andrea, “that you never make a good one; but let us see your plan.”\n\n“Well,” pursued Caderousse, “can you without expending one sou, put me in the way of getting fifteen thousand francs? No, fifteen thousand are not enough,—I cannot again become an honest man with less than thirty thousand francs.”\n\n“No,” replied Andrea, dryly, “no, I cannot.”\n\n“I do not think you understand me,” replied Caderousse, calmly; “I said without your laying out a sou.”\n\n“Do you want me to commit a robbery, to spoil all my good fortune—and yours with mine—and both of us to be dragged down there again?”\n\n“It would make very little difference to me,” said Caderousse, “if I were retaken, I am a poor creature to live alone, and sometimes pine for my old comrades; not like you, heartless creature, who would be glad never to see them again.”\n\nAndrea did more than tremble this time, he turned pale.\n\n“Come, Caderousse, no nonsense!” said he.\n\n“Don’t alarm yourself, my little Benedetto, but just point out to me some means of gaining those thirty thousand francs without your assistance, and I will contrive it.”\n\n“Well, I’ll see—I’ll try to contrive some way,” said Andrea.\n\n“Meanwhile you will raise my monthly allowance to five hundred francs, my little fellow? I have a fancy, and mean to get a housekeeper.”\n\n“Well, you shall have your five hundred francs,” said Andrea; “but it is very hard for me, my poor Caderousse—you take advantage——”\n\n“Bah,” said Caderousse, “when you have access to countless stores.”\n\nOne would have said Andrea anticipated his companion’s words, so did his eye flash like lightning, but it was but for a moment.\n\n“True,” he replied, “and my protector is very kind.”\n\n“That dear protector,” said Caderousse; “and how much does he give you monthly?”\n\n“Five thousand francs.”\n\n“As many thousands as you give me hundreds! Truly, it is only bastards who are thus fortunate. Five thousand francs per month! What the devil can you do with all that?”\n\n“Oh, it is no trouble to spend that; and I am like you, I want capital.”\n\n“Capital?—yes—I understand—everyone would like capital.”\n\n“Well, and I shall get it.”\n\n“Who will give it to you—your prince?”\n\n“Yes, my prince. But unfortunately I must wait.”\n40138m\n\n“You must wait for what?” asked Caderousse.\n\n“For his death.”\n\n“The death of your prince?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“How so?”\n\n“Because he has made his will in my favor.”\n\n“Indeed?”\n\n“On my honor.”\n\n“For how much?”\n\n“For five hundred thousand.”\n\n“Only that? It’s little enough.”\n\n“But so it is.”\n\n“No, it cannot be!”\n\n“Are you my friend, Caderousse?”\n\n“Yes, in life or death.”\n\n“Well, I will tell you a secret.”\n\n“What is it?”\n\n“But remember——”\n\n“Ah! _pardieu!_ mute as a carp.”\n\n“Well, I think——”\n\nAndrea stopped and looked around.\n\n“You think? Do not fear; _pardieu!_ we are alone.”\n\n“I think I have discovered my father.”\n\n“Your true father?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Not old Cavalcanti?”\n\n“No, for he has gone again; the true one, as you say.”\n\n“And that father is——”\n\n“Well, Caderousse, it is Monte Cristo.”\n\n“Bah!”\n\n“Yes, you understand, that explains all. He cannot acknowledge me openly, it appears, but he does it through M. Cavalcanti, and gives him fifty thousand francs for it.”\n\n“Fifty thousand francs for being your father? I would have done it for half that, for twenty thousand, for fifteen thousand; why did you not think of me, ungrateful man?”\n\n“Did I know anything about it, when it was all done when I was down there?”\n\n“Ah, truly? And you say that by his will——”\n\n“He leaves me five hundred thousand livres.”\n\n“Are you sure of it?”\n\n“He showed it me; but that is not all—there is a codicil, as I said just now.”\n\n“Probably.”\n\n“And in that codicil he acknowledges me.”\n\n“Oh, the good father, the brave father, the very honest father!” said Caderousse, twirling a plate in the air between his two hands.\n\n“Now, say if I conceal anything from you?”\n\n“No, and your confidence makes you honorable in my opinion; and your princely father, is he rich, very rich?”\n\n“Yes, he is that; he does not himself know the amount of his fortune.”\n\n“Is it possible?”\n\n“It is evident enough to me, who am always at his house. The other day a banker’s clerk brought him fifty thousand francs in a portfolio about the size of your plate; yesterday his banker brought him a hundred thousand francs in gold.”\n\nCaderousse was filled with wonder; the young man’s words sounded to him like metal, and he thought he could hear the rushing of cascades of louis.\n\n“And you go into that house?” cried he briskly.\n\n“When I like.”\n\nCaderousse was thoughtful for a moment. It was easy to perceive he was revolving some unfortunate idea in his mind. Then suddenly,—\n\n“How I should like to see all that,” cried he; “how beautiful it must be!”\n\n“It is, in fact, magnificent,” said Andrea.\n\n“And does he not live in the Champs-Élysées?”\n\n“Yes, No. 30.”\n“Ah,” said Caderousse, “No. 30.”\n\n“Yes, a fine house standing alone, between a courtyard and a garden,—you must know it.”\n\n“Possibly; but it is not the exterior I care for, it is the interior. What beautiful furniture there must be in it!”\n\n“Have you ever seen the Tuileries?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Well, it surpasses that.”\n\n“It must be worth one’s while to stoop, Andrea, when that good M. Monte Cristo lets fall his purse.”\n\n“It is not worthwhile to wait for that,” said Andrea; “money is as plentiful in that house as fruit in an orchard.”\n\n“But you should take me there one day with you.”\n\n“How can I? On what plea?”\n\n“You are right; but you have made my mouth water. I must absolutely see it; I shall find a way.”\n\n“No nonsense, Caderousse!”\n\n“I will offer myself as floor-polisher.”\n\n“The rooms are all carpeted.”\n\n“Well, then, I must be contented to imagine it.”\n\n“That is the best plan, believe me.”\n\n“Try, at least, to give me an idea of what it is.”\n\n“How can I?”\n\n“Nothing is easier. Is it large?”\n\n“Middling.”\n\n“How is it arranged?”\n\n“Faith, I should require pen, ink, and paper to make a plan.”\n\n“They are all here,” said Caderousse, briskly. He fetched from an old secretaire a sheet of white paper and pen and ink. “Here,” said Caderousse, “draw me all that on the paper, my boy.”\n\nAndrea took the pen with an imperceptible smile and began.\n\n“The house, as I said, is between the court and the garden; in this way, do you see?” Andrea drew the garden, the court and the house.\n40142m\n\n“High walls?”\n\n“Not more than eight or ten feet.”\n\n“That is not prudent,” said Caderousse.\n\n“In the court are orange-trees in pots, turf, and clumps of flowers.”\n\n“And no steel-traps?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“The stables?”\n\n“Are on either side of the gate, which you see there.” And Andrea continued his plan.\n\n“Let us see the ground floor,” said Caderousse.\n\n“On the ground floor, dining-room, two drawing-rooms, billiard-room, staircase in the hall, and a little back staircase.”\n\n“Windows?”\n\n“Magnificent windows, so beautiful, so large, that I believe a man of your size should pass through each frame.”\n\n“Why the devil have they any stairs with such windows?”\n\n“Luxury has everything.”\n\n“But shutters?”\n\n“Yes, but they are never used. That Count of Monte Cristo is an original, who loves to look at the sky even at night.”\n\n“And where do the servants sleep?”\n\n“Oh, they have a house to themselves. Picture to yourself a pretty coach-house at the right-hand side where the ladders are kept. Well, over that coach-house are the servants’ rooms, with bells corresponding with the different apartments.”\n\n“Ah, _diable!_ bells did you say?”\n\n“What do you mean?”\n\n“Oh, nothing! I only say they cost a load of money to hang, and what is the use of them, I should like to know?”\n\n“There used to be a dog let loose in the yard at night, but it has been taken to the house at Auteuil, to that you went to, you know.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“I was saying to him only yesterday, ‘You are imprudent, Monsieur Count; for when you go to Auteuil and take your servants the house is left unprotected.’ ‘Well,’ said he, ‘what next?’ ‘Well, next, some day you will be robbed.’”\n\n“What did he answer?”\n\n“He quietly said, ‘What do I care if I am?’”\n\n“Andrea, he has some secretaire with a spring.”\n\n“How do you know?”\n\n“Yes, which catches the thief in a trap and plays a tune. I was told there were such at the last exhibition.”\n\n“He has simply a mahogany secretaire, in which the key is always kept.”\n\n“And he is not robbed?”\n\n“No; his servants are all devoted to him.”\n\n“There ought to be some money in that secretaire?”\n\n“There may be. No one knows what there is.”\n\n“And where is it?”\n\n“On the first floor.”\n\n“Sketch me the plan of that floor, as you have done of the ground floor, my boy.”\n\n“That is very simple.” Andrea took the pen. “On the first story, do you see, there is the anteroom and the drawing-room; to the right of the drawing-room, a library and a study; to the left, a bedroom and a dressing-room. The famous secretaire is in the dressing-room.”\n\n“Is there a window in the dressing-room?”\n\n“Two,—one here and one there.” Andrea sketched two windows in the room, which formed an angle on the plan, and appeared as a small square added to the rectangle of the bedroom. Caderousse became thoughtful.\n\n“Does he often go to Auteuil?” added he.\n\n“Two or three times a week. Tomorrow, for instance, he is going to spend the day and night there.”\n\n“Are you sure of it?”\n\n“He has invited me to dine there.”\n\n“There’s a life for you,” said Caderousse; “a town house and a country house.”\n\n“That is what it is to be rich.”\n\n“And shall you dine there?”\n\n“Probably.”\n\n“When you dine there, do you sleep there?”\n\n“If I like; I am at home there.”\n\nCaderousse looked at the young man, as if to get at the truth from the bottom of his heart. But Andrea drew a cigar-case from his pocket, took a Havana, quietly lit it, and began smoking.\n\n“When do you want your twelve hundred francs?” said he to Caderousse.\n\n“Now, if you have them.” Andrea took five-and-twenty louis from his pocket.\n\n“Yellow boys?” said Caderousse; “no, I thank you.”\n\n“Oh, you despise them.”\n\n“On the contrary, I esteem them, but will not have them.”\n\n“You can change them, idiot; gold is worth five sous.”\n\n“Exactly; and he who changes them will follow friend Caderousse, lay hands on him, and demand what farmers pay him their rent in gold. No nonsense, my good fellow; silver simply, round coins with the head of some monarch or other on them. Anybody may possess a five-franc piece.”\n\n“But do you suppose I carry five hundred francs about with me? I should want a porter.”\n\n“Well, leave them with your porter; he is to be trusted. I will call for them.”\n\n“Today?”\n\n“No, tomorrow; I shall not have time today.”\n\n“Well, tomorrow I will leave them when I go to Auteuil.”\n\n“May I depend on it?”\n\n“Certainly.”\n\n“Because I shall secure my housekeeper on the strength of it.”\n\n“Now see here, will that be all? Eh? And will you not torment me any more?”\n\n“Never.”\n\nCaderousse had become so gloomy that Andrea feared he should be obliged to notice the change. He redoubled his gayety and carelessness.\n\n“How sprightly you are,” said Caderousse; “One would say you were already in possession of your property.”\n\n“No, unfortunately; but when I do obtain it——”\n\n“Well?”\n\n“I shall remember old friends, I can tell you that.”\n\n“Yes, since you have such a good memory.”\n\n“What do you want? It looks as if you were trying to fleece me?”\n\n“I? What an idea! I, who am going to give you another piece of good advice.”\n\n“What is it?”\n\n“To leave behind you the diamond you have on your finger. We shall both get into trouble. You will ruin both yourself and me by your folly.”\n\n“How so?” said Andrea.\n\n“How? You put on a livery, you disguise yourself as a servant, and yet keep a diamond on your finger worth four or five thousand francs.”\n\n“You guess well.”\n\n“I know something of diamonds; I have had some.”\n\n“You do well to boast of it,” said Andrea, who, without becoming angry, as Caderousse feared, at this new extortion, quietly resigned the ring. Caderousse looked so closely at it that Andrea well knew that he was examining to see if all the edges were perfect.\n\n“It is a false diamond,” said Caderousse.\n\n“You are joking now,” replied Andrea.\n\n“Do not be angry, we can try it.” Caderousse went to the window, touched the glass with it, and found it would cut.\n\n“_Confiteor!_” said Caderousse, putting the diamond on his little finger; “I was mistaken; but those thieves of jewellers imitate so well that it is no longer worthwhile to rob a jeweller’s shop—it is another branch of industry paralyzed.”\n\n“Have you finished?” said Andrea,—“do you want anything more?—will you have my waistcoat or my hat? Make free, now you have begun.”\n\n“No; you are, after all, a good companion; I will not detain you, and will try to cure myself of my ambition.”\n\n“But take care the same thing does not happen to you in selling the diamond you feared with the gold.”\n\n“I shall not sell it—do not fear.”\n\n“Not at least till the day after tomorrow,” thought the young man.\n\n“Happy rogue,” said Caderousse; “you are going to find your servants, your horses, your carriage, and your betrothed!”\n\n“Yes,” said Andrea.\n\n“Well, I hope you will make a handsome wedding-present the day you marry Mademoiselle Danglars.”\n\n“I have already told you it is a fancy you have taken in your head.”\n\n“What fortune has she?”\n\n“But I tell you——”\n\n“A million?”\n\nAndrea shrugged his shoulders.\n\n“Let it be a million,” said Caderousse; “you can never have so much as I wish you.”\n\n“Thank you,” said the young man.\n\n“Oh, I wish it you with all my heart!” added Caderousse with his hoarse laugh. “Stop, let me show you the way.”\n\n“It is not worthwhile.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because there is a little secret, a precaution I thought it desirable to take, one of Huret & Fichet’s locks, revised and improved by Gaspard Caderousse; I will manufacture you a similar one when you are a capitalist.”\n\n“Thank you,” said Andrea; “I will let you know a week beforehand.”\n\nThey parted. Caderousse remained on the landing until he had not only seen Andrea go down the three stories, but also cross the court. Then he returned hastily, shut his door carefully, and began to study, like a clever architect, the plan Andrea had left him.\n\n“Dear Benedetto,” said he, “I think he will not be sorry to inherit his fortune, and he who hastens the day when he can touch his five hundred thousand will not be his worst friend.”\n\n# Chapter 82. The Burglary\n\nThe day following that on which the conversation we have related took place, the Count of Monte Cristo set out for Auteuil, accompanied by Ali and several attendants, and also taking with him some horses whose qualities he was desirous of ascertaining. He was induced to undertake this journey, of which the day before he had not even thought and which had not occurred to Andrea either, by the arrival of Bertuccio from Normandy with intelligence respecting the house and sloop. The house was ready, and the sloop which had arrived a week before lay at anchor in a small creek with her crew of six men, who had observed all the requisite formalities and were ready again to put to sea.\n\nThe count praised Bertuccio’s zeal, and ordered him to prepare for a speedy departure, as his stay in France would not be prolonged more than a month.\n\n“Now,” said he, “I may require to go in one night from Paris to Tréport; let eight fresh horses be in readiness on the road, which will enable me to go fifty leagues in ten hours.”\n\n“Your highness had already expressed that wish,” said Bertuccio, “and the horses are ready. I have bought them, and stationed them myself at the most desirable posts, that is, in villages, where no one generally stops.”\n\n“That’s well,” said Monte Cristo; “I remain here a day or two—arrange accordingly.”\n\nAs Bertuccio was leaving the room to give the requisite orders, Baptistin opened the door: he held a letter on a silver waiter.\n\n“What are you doing here?” asked the count, seeing him covered with dust; “I did not send for you, I think?”\n\nBaptistin, without answering, approached the count, and presented the letter. “Important and urgent,” said he.\n\nThe count opened the letter, and read:\n\n“‘M. de Monte Cristo is apprised that this night a man will enter his house in the Champs-Élysées with the intention of carrying off some papers supposed to be in the secretaire in the dressing-room. The count’s well-known courage will render unnecessary the aid of the police, whose interference might seriously affect him who sends this advice. The count, by any opening from the bedroom, or by concealing himself in the dressing-room, would be able to defend his property himself. Many attendants or apparent precautions would prevent the villain from the attempt, and M. de Monte Cristo would lose the opportunity of discovering an enemy whom chance has revealed to him who now sends this warning to the count,—a warning he might not be able to send another time, if this first attempt should fail and another be made.’”\n\nThe count’s first idea was that this was an artifice—a gross deception, to draw his attention from a minor danger in order to expose him to a greater. He was on the point of sending the letter to the commissary of police, notwithstanding the advice of his anonymous friend, or perhaps because of that advice, when suddenly the idea occurred to him that it might be some personal enemy, whom he alone should recognize and over whom, if such were the case, he alone would gain any advantage, as Fiesco17 had done over the Moor who would have killed him. We know the count’s vigorous and daring mind, denying anything to be impossible, with that energy which marks the great man.\n\nFrom his past life, from his resolution to shrink from nothing, the count had acquired an inconceivable relish for the contests in which he had engaged, sometimes against nature, that is to say, against God, and sometimes against the world, that is, against the devil.\n\n“They do not want my papers,” said Monte Cristo, “they want to kill me; they are no robbers, but assassins. I will not allow the prefect of police to interfere with my private affairs. I am rich enough, forsooth, to distribute his authority on this occasion.”\n\nThe count recalled Baptistin, who had left the room after delivering the letter.\n\n“Return to Paris,” said he; “assemble the servants who remain there. I want all my household at Auteuil.”\n\n“But will no one remain in the house, my lord?” asked Baptistin.\n\n“Yes, the porter.”\n\n“My lord will remember that the lodge is at a distance from the house.”\n\n“Well?”\n\n“The house might be stripped without his hearing the least noise.”\n\n“By whom?”\n“By thieves.”\n\n“You are a fool, M. Baptistin. Thieves might strip the house—it would annoy me less than to be disobeyed.” Baptistin bowed.\n\n40150m\n“You understand me?” said the count. “Bring your comrades here, one and all; but let everything remain as usual, only close the shutters of the ground floor.”\n\n“And those of the first floor?”\n\n“You know they are never closed. Go!”\n\nThe count signified his intention of dining alone, and that no one but Ali should attend him. Having dined with his usual tranquillity and moderation, the count, making a signal to Ali to follow him, went out by the side-gate and on reaching the Bois de Boulogne turned, apparently without design towards Paris and at twilight; found himself opposite his house in the Champs-Élysées. All was dark; one solitary, feeble light was burning in the porter’s lodge, about forty paces distant from the house, as Baptistin had said.\n\nMonte Cristo leaned against a tree, and with that scrutinizing glance which was so rarely deceived, looked up and down the avenue, examined the passers-by, and carefully looked down the neighboring streets, to see that no one was concealed. Ten minutes passed thus, and he was convinced that no one was watching him. He hastened to the side-door with Ali, entered hurriedly, and by the servants’ staircase, of which he had the key, gained his bedroom without opening or disarranging a single curtain, without even the porter having the slightest suspicion that the house, which he supposed empty, contained its chief occupant.\n\nArrived in his bedroom, the count motioned to Ali to stop; then he passed into the dressing-room, which he examined. Everything appeared as usual—the precious secretaire in its place, and the key in the secretaire. He double locked it, took the key, returned to the bedroom door, removed the double staple of the bolt, and went in. Meanwhile Ali had procured the arms the count required—namely, a short carbine and a pair of double-barrelled pistols, with which as sure an aim might be taken as with a single-barrelled one. Thus armed, the count held the lives of five men in his hands. It was about half-past nine.\n\nThe count and Ali ate in haste a crust of bread and drank a glass of Spanish wine; then Monte Cristo slipped aside one of the movable panels, which enabled him to see into the adjoining room. He had within his reach his pistols and carbine, and Ali, standing near him, held one of the small Arabian hatchets, whose form has not varied since the Crusades. Through one of the windows of the bedroom, on a line with that in the dressing-room, the count could see into the street.\n\nTwo hours passed thus. It was intensely dark; still Ali, thanks to his wild nature, and the count, thanks doubtless to his long confinement, could distinguish in the darkness the slightest movement of the trees. The little light in the lodge had long been extinct. It might be expected that the attack, if indeed an attack was projected, would be made from the staircase of the ground floor, and not from a window; in Monte Cristo’s opinion, the villains sought his life, not his money. It would be his bedroom they would attack, and they must reach it by the back staircase, or by the window in the dressing-room.\n\nThe clock of the Invalides struck a quarter to twelve; the west wind bore on its moistened gusts the doleful vibration of the three strokes.\n\n40152m\nAs the last stroke died away, the count thought he heard a slight noise in the dressing-room; this first sound, or rather this first grinding, was followed by a second, then a third; at the fourth, the count knew what to expect. A firm and well-practised hand was engaged in cutting the four sides of a pane of glass with a diamond. The count felt his heart beat more rapidly.\n\nInured as men may be to danger, forewarned as they may be of peril, they understand, by the fluttering of the heart and the shuddering of the frame, the enormous difference between a dream and a reality, between the project and the execution. However, Monte Cristo only made a sign to apprise Ali, who, understanding that danger was approaching from the other side, drew nearer to his master. Monte Cristo was eager to ascertain the strength and number of his enemies.\n\nThe window whence the noise proceeded was opposite the opening by which the count could see into the dressing-room. He fixed his eyes on that window—he distinguished a shadow in the darkness; then one of the panes became quite opaque, as if a sheet of paper were stuck on the outside, then the square cracked without falling. Through the opening an arm was passed to find the fastening, then a second; the window turned on its hinges, and a man entered. He was alone.\n\n“That’s a daring rascal,” whispered the count.\n\nAt that moment Ali touched him slightly on the shoulder. He turned; Ali pointed to the window of the room in which they were, facing the street.\n\n“I see!” said he, “there are two of them; one does the work while the other stands guard.” He made a sign to Ali not to lose sight of the man in the street, and turned to the one in the dressing-room.\n\nThe glass-cutter had entered, and was feeling his way, his arms stretched out before him. At last he appeared to have made himself familiar with his surroundings. There were two doors; he bolted them both.\n\nWhen he drew near to the bedroom door, Monte Cristo expected that he was coming in, and raised one of his pistols; but he simply heard the sound of the bolts sliding in their copper rings. It was only a precaution. The nocturnal visitor, ignorant of the fact that the count had removed the staples, might now think himself at home, and pursue his purpose with full security. Alone and free to act as he wished, the man then drew from his pocket something which the count could not discern, placed it on a stand, then went straight to the secretaire, felt the lock, and contrary to his expectation found that the key was missing. But the glass-cutter was a prudent man who had provided for all emergencies. The count soon heard the rattling of a bunch of skeleton keys, such as the locksmith brings when called to force a lock, and which thieves call nightingales, doubtless from the music of their nightly song when they grind against the bolt.\n\n“Ah, ha,” whispered Monte Cristo with a smile of disappointment, “he is only a thief.”\n\nBut the man in the dark could not find the right key. He reached the instrument he had placed on the stand, touched a spring, and immediately a pale light, just bright enough to render objects distinct, was reflected on his hands and countenance.\n\n“By heavens,” exclaimed Monte Cristo, starting back, “it is——”\n\nAli raised his hatchet.\n\n“Don’t stir,” whispered Monte Cristo, “and put down your hatchet; we shall require no arms.”\n\n40154m\nThen he added some words in a low tone, for the exclamation which surprise had drawn from the count, faint as it had been, had startled the man who remained in the pose of the old knife-grinder.\n\nIt was an order the count had just given, for immediately Ali went noiselessly, and returned, bearing a black dress and a three-cornered hat. Meanwhile Monte Cristo had rapidly taken off his greatcoat, waistcoat, and shirt, and one might distinguish by the glimmering through the open panel that he wore a pliant tunic of steel mail, of which the last in France, where daggers are no longer dreaded, was worn by King Louis XVI., who feared the dagger at his breast, and whose head was cleft with a hatchet. The tunic soon disappeared under a long cassock, as did his hair under a priest’s wig; the three-cornered hat over this effectually transformed the count into an abbé.\n\nThe man, hearing nothing more, stood erect, and while Monte Cristo was completing his disguise had advanced straight to the secretaire, whose lock was beginning to crack under his nightingale.\n\n“Try again,” whispered the count, who depended on the secret spring, which was unknown to the picklock, clever as he might be—“try again, you have a few minutes’ work there.”\n\nAnd he advanced to the window. The man whom he had seen seated on a fence had got down, and was still pacing the street; but, strange as it appeared, he cared not for those who might pass from the avenue of the Champs-Élysées or by the Faubourg Saint-Honoré; his attention was engrossed with what was passing at the count’s, and his only aim appeared to be to discern every movement in the dressing-room.\n\nMonte Cristo suddenly struck his finger on his forehead and a smile passed over his lips; then drawing near to Ali, he whispered:\n\n“Remain here, concealed in the dark, and whatever noise you hear, whatever passes, only come in or show yourself if I call you.”\n\nAli bowed in token of strict obedience. Monte Cristo then drew a lighted taper from a closet, and when the thief was deeply engaged with his lock, silently opened the door, taking care that the light should shine directly on his face. The door opened so quietly that the thief heard no sound; but, to his astonishment, the room was suddenly illuminated. He turned.\n\n“Ah, good-evening, my dear M. Caderousse,” said Monte Cristo; “what are you doing here, at such an hour?”\n\n40156m\n“The Abbé Busoni!” exclaimed Caderousse; and, not knowing how this strange apparition could have entered when he had bolted the doors, he let fall his bunch of keys, and remained motionless and stupefied. The count placed himself between Caderousse and the window, thus cutting off from the thief his only chance of retreat.\n\n“The Abbé Busoni!” repeated Caderousse, fixing his haggard gaze on the count.\n\n“Yes, undoubtedly, the Abbé Busoni himself,” replied Monte Cristo. “And I am very glad you recognize me, dear M. Caderousse; it proves you have a good memory, for it must be about ten years since we last met.”\n\nThis calmness of Busoni, combined with his irony and boldness, staggered Caderousse.\n\n“The abbé, the abbé!” murmured he, clenching his fists, and his teeth chattering.\n\n“So you would rob the Count of Monte Cristo?” continued the false abbé.\n\n“Reverend sir,” murmured Caderousse, seeking to regain the window, which the count pitilessly blocked—“reverend sir, I don’t know—believe me—I take my oath——”\n\n“A pane of glass out,” continued the count, “a dark lantern, a bunch of false keys, a secretaire half forced—it is tolerably evident——”\n\nCaderousse was choking; he looked around for some corner to hide in, some way of escape.\n\n“Come, come,” continued the count, “I see you are still the same,—an assassin.”\n\n“Reverend sir, since you know everything, you know it was not I—it was La Carconte; that was proved at the trial, since I was only condemned to the galleys.”\n\n“Is your time, then, expired, since I find you in a fair way to return there?”\n\n“No, reverend sir; I have been liberated by someone.”\n\n“That someone has done society a great kindness.”\n\n“Ah,” said Caderousse, “I had promised——”\n\n“And you are breaking your promise!” interrupted Monte Cristo.\n\n“Alas, yes!” said Caderousse very uneasily.\n\n“A bad relapse, that will lead you, if I mistake not, to the Place de Grève. So much the worse, so much the worse—_diavolo!_ as they say in my country.”\n\n“Reverend sir, I am impelled——”\n\n“Every criminal says the same thing.”\n\n“Poverty——”\n\n“Pshaw!” said Busoni disdainfully; “poverty may make a man beg, steal a loaf of bread at a baker’s door, but not cause him to open a secretaire in a house supposed to be inhabited. And when the jeweller Johannes had just paid you 45,000 francs for the diamond I had given you, and you killed him to get the diamond and the money both, was that also poverty?”\n\n“Pardon, reverend sir,” said Caderousse; “you have saved my life once, save me again!”\n\n“That is but poor encouragement.”\n\n“Are you alone, reverend sir, or have you there soldiers ready to seize me?”\n\n“I am alone,” said the abbé, “and I will again have pity on you, and will let you escape, at the risk of the fresh miseries my weakness may lead to, if you tell me the truth.”\n\n“Ah, reverend sir,” cried Caderousse, clasping his hands, and drawing nearer to Monte Cristo, “I may indeed say you are my deliverer!”\n\n“You mean to say you have been freed from confinement?”\n\n“Yes, that is true, reverend sir.”\n\n“Who was your liberator?”\n\n“An Englishman.”\n\n“What was his name?”\n\n“Lord Wilmore.”\n“I know him; I shall know if you lie.”\n\n“Ah, reverend sir, I tell you the simple truth.”\n\n“Was this Englishman protecting you?”\n\n“No, not me, but a young Corsican, my companion.”\n\n“What was this young Corsican’s name?”\n\n“Benedetto.”\n\n“Is that his Christian name?”\n\n“He had no other; he was a foundling.”\n\n“Then this young man escaped with you?”\n\n“He did.”\n\n“In what way?”\n\n“We were working at Saint-Mandrier, near Toulon. Do you know Saint-Mandrier?”\n\n“I do.”\n\n“In the hour of rest, between noon and one o’clock——”\n\n“Galley-slaves having a nap after dinner! We may well pity the poor fellows!” said the abbé.\n\n“Nay,” said Caderousse, “one can’t always work—one is not a dog.”\n\n“So much the better for the dogs,” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“While the rest slept, then, we went away a short distance; we severed our fetters with a file the Englishman had given us, and swam away.”\n\n“And what is become of this Benedetto?”\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\n“You ought to know.”\n\n“No, in truth; we parted at Hyères.” And, to give more weight to his protestation, Caderousse advanced another step towards the abbé, who remained motionless in his place, as calm as ever, and pursuing his interrogation.\n\n“You lie,” said the Abbé Busoni, with a tone of irresistible authority.\n\n“Reverend sir!”\n\n“You lie! This man is still your friend, and you, perhaps, make use of him as your accomplice.”\n40160m\n\n“Oh, reverend sir!”\n\n“Since you left Toulon what have you lived on? Answer me!”\n\n“On what I could get.”\n\n“You lie,” repeated the abbé a third time, with a still more imperative tone. Caderousse, terrified, looked at the count. “You have lived on the money he has given you.”\n\n“True,” said Caderousse; “Benedetto has become the son of a great lord.”\n\n“How can he be the son of a great lord?”\n\n“A natural son.”\n\n“And what is that great lord’s name?”\n\n“The Count of Monte Cristo, the very same in whose house we are.”\n\n“Benedetto the count’s son?” replied Monte Cristo, astonished in his turn.\n\n“Well, I should think so, since the count has found him a false father—since the count gives him four thousand francs a month, and leaves him 500,000 francs in his will.”\n\n“Ah, yes,” said the factitious abbé, who began to understand; “and what name does the young man bear meanwhile?”\n\n“Andrea Cavalcanti.”\n\n“Is it, then, that young man whom my friend the Count of Monte Cristo has received into his house, and who is going to marry Mademoiselle Danglars?”\n\n“Exactly.”\n“And you suffer that, you wretch!—you, who know his life and his crime?”\n\n“Why should I stand in a comrade’s way?” said Caderousse.\n\n“You are right; it is not you who should apprise M. Danglars, it is I.”\n\n“Do not do so, reverend sir.”\n\n“Why not?”\n\n“Because you would bring us to ruin.”\n\n“And you think that to save such villains as you I will become an abettor of their plot, an accomplice in their crimes?”\n\n“Reverend sir,” said Caderousse, drawing still nearer.\n\n“I will expose all.”\n\n“To whom?”\n\n“To M. Danglars.”\n\n“By Heaven!” cried Caderousse, drawing from his waistcoat an open knife, and striking the count in the breast, “you shall disclose nothing, reverend sir!”\n\nTo Caderousse’s great astonishment, the knife, instead of piercing the count’s breast, flew back blunted. At the same moment the count seized with his left hand the assassin’s wrist, and wrung it with such strength that the knife fell from his stiffened fingers, and Caderousse uttered a cry of pain. But the count, disregarding his cry, continued to wring the bandit’s wrist, until, his arm being dislocated, he fell first on his knees, then flat on the floor.\n\nThe count then placed his foot on his head, saying, “I know not what restrains me from crushing thy skull, rascal.”\n\n“Ah, mercy—mercy!” cried Caderousse.\n\nThe count withdrew his foot.\n\n40162m\n“Rise!” said he. Caderousse rose.\n\n“What a wrist you have, reverend sir!” said Caderousse, stroking his arm, all bruised by the fleshy pincers which had held it; “what a wrist!”\n\n“Silence! God gives me strength to overcome a wild beast like you; in the name of that God I act,—remember that, wretch,—and to spare thee at this moment is still serving him.”\n\n“Oh!” said Caderousse, groaning with pain.\n\n“Take this pen and paper, and write what I dictate.”\n\n“I don’t know how to write, reverend sir.”\n\n“You lie! Take this pen, and write!”\n\nCaderousse, awed by the superior power of the abbé, sat down and wrote:\n\n“Sir,—The man whom you are receiving at your house, and to whom you intend to marry your daughter, is a felon who escaped with me from confinement at Toulon. He was No. 59, and I No. 58. He was called Benedetto, but he is ignorant of his real name, having never known his parents.”\n\n“Sign it!” continued the count.\n\n“But would you ruin me?”\n\n“If I sought your ruin, fool, I should drag you to the first guard-house; besides, when that note is delivered, in all probability you will have no more to fear. Sign it, then!”\n\nCaderousse signed it.\n\n“The address, ‘To monsieur the Baron Danglars, banker, Rue de la Chaussée d’Antin.’”\n\nCaderousse wrote the address. The abbé took the note.\n\n“Now,” said he, “that suffices—begone!”\n\n“Which way?”\n\n“The way you came.”\n\n“You wish me to get out at that window?”\n\n“You got in very well.”\n\n“Oh, you have some design against me, reverend sir.”\n\n“Idiot! what design can I have?”\n\n“Why, then, not let me out by the door?”\n\n“What would be the advantage of waking the porter?”\n\n“Ah, reverend sir, tell me, do you wish me dead?”\n\n“I wish what God wills.”\n\n“But swear that you will not strike me as I go down.”\n\n“Cowardly fool!”\n\n“What do you intend doing with me?”\n\n“I ask you what can I do? I have tried to make you a happy man, and you have turned out a murderer.”\n\n“Oh, monsieur,” said Caderousse, “make one more attempt—try me once more!”\n\n“I will,” said the count. “Listen—you know if I may be relied on.”\n\n“Yes,” said Caderousse.\n\n“If you arrive safely at home——”\n\n“What have I to fear, except from you?”\n\n“If you reach your home safely, leave Paris, leave France, and wherever you may be, so long as you conduct yourself well, I will send you a small annuity; for, if you return home safely, then——”\n\n“Then?” asked Caderousse, shuddering.\n40164m\n\n“Then I shall believe God has forgiven you, and I will forgive you too.”\n\n“As true as I am a Christian,” stammered Caderousse, “you will make me die of fright!”\n\n“Now begone,” said the count, pointing to the window.\n\nCaderousse, scarcely yet relying on this promise, put his legs out of the window and stood on the ladder.\n\n“Now go down,” said the abbé, folding his arms. Understanding he had nothing more to fear from him, Caderousse began to go down. Then the count brought the taper to the window, that it might be seen in the Champs-Élysées that a man was getting out of the window while another held a light.\n\n“What are you doing, reverend sir? Suppose a watchman should pass?” And he blew out the light. He then descended, but it was only when he felt his foot touch the ground that he was satisfied of his safety.\n\nMonte Cristo returned to his bedroom, and, glancing rapidly from the garden to the street, he saw first Caderousse, who after walking to the end of the garden, fixed his ladder against the wall at a different part from where he came in. The count then looking over into the street, saw the man who appeared to be waiting run in the same direction, and place himself against the angle of the wall where Caderousse would come over. Caderousse climbed the ladder slowly, and looked over the coping to see if the street was quiet. No one could be seen or heard. The clock of the Invalides struck one. Then Caderousse sat astride the coping, and drawing up his ladder passed it over the wall; then he began to descend, or rather to slide down by the two stanchions, which he did with an ease which proved how accustomed he was to the exercise. But, once started, he could not stop. In vain did he see a man start from the shadow when he was halfway down—in vain did he see an arm raised as he touched the ground.\n\nBefore he could defend himself that arm struck him so violently in the back that he let go the ladder, crying, “Help!” A second blow struck him almost immediately in the side, and he fell, calling, “Help, murder!” Then, as he rolled on the ground, his adversary seized him by the hair, and struck him a third blow in the chest.\n\nThis time Caderousse endeavored to call again, but he could only utter a groan, and he shuddered as the blood flowed from his three wounds. The assassin, finding that he no longer cried out, lifted his head up by the hair; his eyes were closed, and the mouth was distorted. The murderer, supposing him dead, let fall his head and disappeared.\n\nThen Caderousse, feeling that he was leaving him, raised himself on his elbow, and with a dying voice cried with great effort:\n\n“Murder! I am dying! Help, reverend sir,—help!”\n\nThis mournful appeal pierced the darkness. The door of the back-staircase opened, then the side-gate of the garden, and Ali and his master were on the spot with lights.\n\n# Chapter 83. The Hand of God\n\nCaderousse continued to call piteously, “Help, reverend sir, help!”\n\n“What is the matter?” asked Monte Cristo.\n\n“Help,” cried Caderousse; “I am murdered!”\n\n“We are here;—take courage.”\n\n“Ah, it’s all over! You are come too late—you are come to see me die. What blows, what blood!”\n\nHe fainted. Ali and his master conveyed the wounded man into a room. Monte Cristo motioned to Ali to undress him, and he then examined his dreadful wounds.\n\n“My God!” he exclaimed, “thy vengeance is sometimes delayed, but only that it may fall the more effectually.” Ali looked at his master for further instructions. “Bring here immediately the king’s attorney, M. de Villefort, who lives in the Faubourg Saint-Honoré. As you pass the lodge, wake the porter, and send him for a surgeon.”\n\nAli obeyed, leaving the abbé alone with Caderousse, who had not yet revived.\n\nWhen the wretched man again opened his eyes, the count looked at him with a mournful expression of pity, and his lips moved as if in prayer. “A surgeon, reverend sir—a surgeon!” said Caderousse.\n\n“I have sent for one,” replied the abbé.\n\n“I know he cannot save my life, but he may strengthen me to give my evidence.”\n\n“Against whom?”\n\n“Against my murderer.”\n\n“Did you recognize him?”\n\n“Yes; it was Benedetto.”\n\n“The young Corsican?”\n“Himself.”\n\n“Your comrade?”\n\n“Yes. After giving me the plan of this house, doubtless hoping I should kill the count and he thus become his heir, or that the count would kill me and I should be out of his way, he waylaid me, and has murdered me.”\n\n“I have also sent for the procureur.”\n\n“He will not come in time; I feel my life fast ebbing.”\n\n“Wait a moment,” said Monte Cristo. He left the room, and returned in five minutes with a phial. The dying man’s eyes were all the time riveted on the door, through which he hoped succor would arrive.\n\n“Hasten, reverend sir, hasten! I shall faint again!” Monte Cristo approached, and dropped on his purple lips three or four drops of the contents of the phial. Caderousse drew a deep breath. “Oh,” said he, “that is life to me; more, more!”\n\n“Two drops more would kill you,” replied the abbé.\n\n“Oh, send for someone to whom I can denounce the wretch!”\n\n“Shall I write your deposition? You can sign it.”\n\n“Yes, yes,” said Caderousse; and his eyes glistened at the thought of this posthumous revenge. Monte Cristo wrote:\n\n“I die, murdered by the Corsican Benedetto, my comrade in the galleys at Toulon, No. 59.”\n\n“Quick, quick!” said Caderousse, “or I shall be unable to sign it.”\n\nMonte Cristo gave the pen to Caderousse, who collected all his strength, signed it, and fell back on his bed, saying:\n\n“You will relate all the rest, reverend sir; you will say he calls himself Andrea Cavalcanti. He lodges at the Hôtel des Princes. Oh, I am dying!” He again fainted. The abbé made him smell the contents of the phial, and he again opened his eyes. His desire for revenge had not forsaken him.\n\n“Ah, you will tell all I have said, will you not, reverend sir?”\n\n“Yes, and much more.”\n\n“What more will you say?”\n\n“I will say he had doubtless given you the plan of this house, in the hope the count would kill you. I will say, likewise, he had apprised the count, by a note, of your intention, and, the count being absent, I read the note and sat up to await you.”\n\n“And he will be guillotined, will he not?” said Caderousse. “Promise me that, and I will die with that hope.”\n\n“I will say,” continued the count, “that he followed and watched you the whole time, and when he saw you leave the house, ran to the angle of the wall to conceal himself.”\n\n“Did you see all that?”\n\n“Remember my words: ‘If you return home safely, I shall believe God has forgiven you, and I will forgive you also.’”\n\n“And you did not warn me!” cried Caderousse, raising himself on his elbows. “You knew I should be killed on leaving this house, and did not warn me!”\n\n40168m\n“No; for I saw God’s justice placed in the hands of Benedetto, and should have thought it sacrilege to oppose the designs of Providence.”\n\n“God’s justice! Speak not of it, reverend sir. If God were just, you know how many would be punished who now escape.”\n\n“Patience,” said the abbé, in a tone which made the dying man shudder; “have patience!”\n\nCaderousse looked at him with amazement.\n\n“Besides,” said the abbé, “God is merciful to all, as he has been to you; he is first a father, then a judge.”\n\n“Do you then believe in God?” said Caderousse.\n\n“Had I been so unhappy as not to believe in him until now,” said Monte Cristo, “I must believe on seeing you.”\n\nCaderousse raised his clenched hands towards heaven.\n\n“Listen,” said the abbé, extending his hand over the wounded man, as if to command him to believe; “this is what the God in whom, on your death-bed, you refuse to believe, has done for you—he gave you health, strength, regular employment, even friends—a life, in fact, which a man might enjoy with a calm conscience. Instead of improving these gifts, rarely granted so abundantly, this has been your course—you have given yourself up to sloth and drunkenness, and in a fit of intoxication have ruined your best friend.”\n\n“Help!” cried Caderousse; “I require a surgeon, not a priest; perhaps I am not mortally wounded—I may not die; perhaps they can yet save my life.”\n\n“Your wounds are so far mortal that, without the three drops I gave you, you would now be dead. Listen, then.”\n\n“Ah,” murmured Caderousse, “what a strange priest you are; you drive the dying to despair, instead of consoling them.”\n\n“Listen,” continued the abbé. “When you had betrayed your friend, God began not to strike, but to warn you. Poverty overtook you. You had already passed half your life in coveting that which you might have honorably acquired; and already you contemplated crime under the excuse of want, when God worked a miracle in your behalf, sending you, by my hands, a fortune—brilliant, indeed, for you, who had never possessed any. But this unexpected, unhoped-for, unheard-of fortune sufficed you no longer when you once possessed it; you wished to double it, and how?—by a murder! You succeeded, and then God snatched it from you, and brought you to justice.”\n\n“It was not I who wished to kill the Jew,” said Caderousse; “it was La Carconte.”\n\n“Yes,” said Monte Cristo, “and God,—I cannot say in justice, for his justice would have slain you,—but God, in his mercy, spared your life.”\n\n“_Pardieu!_ to transport me for life, how merciful!”\n\n“You thought it a mercy then, miserable wretch! The coward who feared death rejoiced at perpetual disgrace; for like all galley-slaves, you said, ‘I may escape from prison, I cannot from the grave.’ And you said truly; the way was opened for you unexpectedly. An Englishman visited Toulon, who had vowed to rescue two men from infamy, and his choice fell on you and your companion. You received a second fortune, money and tranquillity were restored to you, and you, who had been condemned to a felon’s life, might live as other men. Then, wretched creature, then you tempted God a third time. ‘I have not enough,’ you said, when you had more than you before possessed, and you committed a third crime, without reason, without excuse. God is wearied; he has punished you.”\n\nCaderousse was fast sinking. “Give me drink,” said he: “I thirst—I burn!” Monte Cristo gave him a glass of water. “And yet that villain, Benedetto, will escape!”\n\n“No one, I tell you, will escape; Benedetto will be punished.”\n\n“Then, you, too, will be punished, for you did not do your duty as a priest—you should have prevented Benedetto from killing me.”\n\n“I?” said the count, with a smile which petrified the dying man, “when you had just broken your knife against the coat of mail which protected my breast! Yet perhaps if I had found you humble and penitent, I might have prevented Benedetto from killing you; but I found you proud and blood-thirsty, and I left you in the hands of God.”\n\n“I do not believe there is a God,” howled Caderousse; “you do not believe it; you lie—you lie!”\n\n“Silence,” said the abbé; “you will force the last drop of blood from your veins. What! you do not believe in God when he is striking you dead? you will not believe in him, who requires but a prayer, a word, a tear, and he will forgive? God, who might have directed the assassin’s dagger so as to end your career in a moment, has given you this quarter of an hour for repentance. Reflect, then, wretched man, and repent.”\n\n“No,” said Caderousse, “no; I will not repent. There is no God; there is no Providence—all comes by chance.”\n\n“There is a Providence; there is a God,” said Monte Cristo, “of whom you are a striking proof, as you lie in utter despair, denying him, while I stand before you, rich, happy, safe and entreating that God in whom you endeavor not to believe, while in your heart you still believe in him.”\n\n“But who are you, then?” asked Caderousse, fixing his dying eyes on the count.\n\n“Look well at me!” said Monte Cristo, putting the light near his face.\n\n“Well, the abbé—the Abbé Busoni.” Monte Cristo took off the wig which disfigured him, and let fall his black hair, which added so much to the beauty of his pallid features.\n\n“Oh?” said Caderousse, thunderstruck, “but for that black hair, I should say you were the Englishman, Lord Wilmore.”\n\n“I am neither the Abbé Busoni nor Lord Wilmore,” said Monte Cristo; “think again,—do you not recollect me?”\n\nThere was a magic effect in the count’s words, which once more revived the exhausted powers of the miserable man.\n\n“Yes, indeed,” said he; “I think I have seen you and known you formerly.”\n\n“Yes, Caderousse, you have seen me; you knew me once.”\n\n“Who, then, are you? and why, if you knew me, do you let me die?”\n\n“Because nothing can save you; your wounds are mortal. Had it been possible to save you, I should have considered it another proof of God’s mercy, and I would again have endeavored to restore you, I swear by my father’s tomb.”\n\n“By your father’s tomb!” said Caderousse, supported by a supernatural power, and half-raising himself to see more distinctly the man who had just taken the oath which all men hold sacred; “who, then, are you?”\n\nThe count had watched the approach of death. He knew this was the last struggle. He approached the dying man, and, leaning over him with a calm and melancholy look, he whispered, “I am—I am——”\n\nAnd his almost closed lips uttered a name so low that the count himself appeared afraid to hear it. Caderousse, who had raised himself on his knees, and stretched out his arm, tried to draw back, then clasping his hands, and raising them with a desperate effort, “Oh, my God, my God!” said he, “pardon me for having denied thee; thou dost exist, thou art indeed man’s father in heaven, and his judge on earth. My God, my Lord, I have long despised thee! Pardon me, my God; receive me, Oh, my Lord!”\n\nCaderousse sighed deeply, and fell back with a groan. The blood no longer flowed from his wounds. He was dead.\n\n“_One!_” said the count mysteriously, his eyes fixed on the corpse, disfigured by so awful a death.\n\nTen minutes afterwards the surgeon and the procureur arrived, the one accompanied by the porter, the other by Ali, and were received by the Abbé Busoni, who was praying by the side of the corpse.\n\n# Chapter 84. Beauchamp\n\nThe daring attempt to rob the count was the topic of conversation throughout Paris for the next fortnight. The dying man had signed a deposition declaring Benedetto to be the assassin. The police had orders to make the strictest search for the murderer. Caderousse’s knife, dark lantern, bunch of keys, and clothing, excepting the waistcoat, which could not be found, were deposited at the registry; the corpse was conveyed to the morgue. The count told everyone that this adventure had happened during his absence at Auteuil, and that he only knew what was related by the Abbé Busoni, who that evening, by mere chance, had requested to pass the night in his house, to examine some valuable books in his library.\n\nBertuccio alone turned pale whenever Benedetto’s name was mentioned in his presence, but there was no reason why anyone should notice his doing so.\n\nVillefort, being called on to prove the crime, was preparing his brief with the same ardor that he was accustomed to exercise when required to speak in criminal cases.\n\nBut three weeks had already passed, and the most diligent search had been unsuccessful; the attempted robbery and the murder of the robber by his comrade were almost forgotten in anticipation of the approaching marriage of Mademoiselle Danglars to the Count Andrea Cavalcanti. It was expected that this wedding would shortly take place, as the young man was received at the banker’s as the betrothed.\n\nLetters had been despatched to M. Cavalcanti, as the count’s father, who highly approved of the union, regretted his inability to leave Parma at that time, and promised a wedding gift of a hundred and fifty thousand livres. It was agreed that the three millions should be intrusted to Danglars to invest; some persons had warned the young man of the circumstances of his future father-in-law, who had of late sustained repeated losses; but with sublime disinterestedness and confidence the young man refused to listen, or to express a single doubt to the baron.\n\nThe baron adored Count Andrea Cavalcanti; not so Mademoiselle Eugénie Danglars. With an instinctive hatred of matrimony, she suffered Andrea’s attentions in order to get rid of Morcerf; but when Andrea urged his suit, she betrayed an entire dislike to him. The baron might possibly have perceived it, but, attributing it to a caprice, feigned ignorance.\n\nThe delay demanded by Beauchamp had nearly expired. Morcerf appreciated the advice of Monte Cristo to let things die away of their own accord. No one had taken up the remark about the general, and no one had recognized in the officer who betrayed the castle of Yanina the noble count in the House of Peers.\n\nAlbert, however, felt no less insulted; the few lines which had irritated him were certainly intended as an insult. Besides, the manner in which Beauchamp had closed the conference left a bitter recollection in his heart. He cherished the thought of the duel, hoping to conceal its true cause even from his seconds. Beauchamp had not been seen since the day he visited Albert, and those of whom the latter inquired always told him he was out on a journey which would detain him some days. Where he was no one knew.\n\nOne morning Albert was awakened by his valet de chambre, who announced Beauchamp. Albert rubbed his eyes, ordered his servant to introduce him into the small smoking-room on the ground floor, dressed himself quickly, and went down.\n\nHe found Beauchamp pacing the room; on perceiving him Beauchamp stopped.\n\n“Your arrival here, without waiting my visit at your house today, looks well, sir,” said Albert. “Tell me, may I shake hands with you, saying, ‘Beauchamp, acknowledge you have injured me, and retain my friendship,’ or must I simply propose to you a choice of arms?”\n\n“Albert,” said Beauchamp, with a look of sorrow which stupefied the young man, “let us first sit down and talk.”\n\n“Rather, sir, before we sit down, I must demand your answer.”\n\n“Albert,” said the journalist, “these are questions which it is difficult to answer.”\n\n“I will facilitate it by repeating the question, ‘Will you, or will you not, retract?’”\n\n“Morcerf, it is not enough to answer ‘yes’ or ‘no’ to questions which concern the honor, the social interest, and the life of such a man as Lieutenant-général the Count of Morcerf, peer of France.”\n\n“What must then be done?”\n40174m\n\n“What I have done, Albert. I reasoned thus—money, time, and fatigue are nothing compared with the reputation and interests of a whole family; probabilities will not suffice, only facts will justify a deadly combat with a friend. If I strike with the sword, or discharge the contents of a pistol at man with whom, for three years, I have been on terms of intimacy, I must, at least, know why I do so; I must meet him with a heart at ease, and that quiet conscience which a man needs when his own arm must save his life.”\n\n“Well,” said Morcerf, impatiently, “what does all this mean?”\n\n“It means that I have just returned from Yanina.”\n\n“From Yanina?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Impossible!”\n\n“Here is my passport; examine the visa—Geneva, Milan, Venice, Trieste, Delvino, Yanina. Will you believe the government of a republic, a kingdom, and an empire?” Albert cast his eyes on the passport, then raised them in astonishment to Beauchamp.\n\n“You have been to Yanina?” said he.\n\n“Albert, had you been a stranger, a foreigner, a simple lord, like that Englishman who came to demand satisfaction three or four months since, and whom I killed to get rid of, I should not have taken this trouble; but I thought this mark of consideration due to you. I took a week to go, another to return, four days of quarantine, and forty-eight hours to stay there; that makes three weeks. I returned last night, and here I am.”\n\n“What circumlocution! How long you are before you tell me what I most wish to know?”\n\n“Because, in truth, Albert——”\n\n“You hesitate?”\n\n“Yes,—I fear.”\n\n“You fear to acknowledge that your correspondent has deceived you? Oh, no self-love, Beauchamp. Acknowledge it, Beauchamp; your courage cannot be doubted.”\n\n“Not so,” murmured the journalist; “on the contrary——”\n\nAlbert turned frightfully pale; he endeavored to speak, but the words died on his lips.\n\n“My friend,” said Beauchamp, in the most affectionate tone, “I should gladly make an apology; but, alas!——”\n\n“The paragraph was correct, my friend.”\n\n“What? That French officer——”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Fernand?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“The traitor who surrendered the castle of the man in whose service he was——”\n\n“Pardon me, my friend, that man was your father!”\n\nAlbert advanced furiously towards Beauchamp, but the latter restrained him more by a mild look than by his extended hand.\n\n“My friend,” said he, “here is a proof of it.”\n\n40176m\nAlbert opened the paper, it was an attestation of four notable inhabitants of Yanina, proving that Colonel Fernand Mondego, in the service of Ali Tepelini, had surrendered the castle for two million crowns. The signatures were perfectly legal. Albert tottered and fell overpowered in a chair. It could no longer be doubted; the family name was fully given. After a moment’s mournful silence, his heart overflowed, and he gave way to a flood of tears. Beauchamp, who had watched with sincere pity the young man’s paroxysm of grief, approached him.\n\n“Now, Albert,” said he, “you understand me—do you not? I wished to see all, and to judge of everything for myself, hoping the explanation would be in your father’s favor, and that I might do him justice. But, on the contrary, the particulars which are given prove that Fernand Mondego, raised by Ali Pasha to the rank of governor-general, is no other than Count Fernand of Morcerf; then, recollecting the honor you had done me, in admitting me to your friendship, I hastened to you.”\n\nAlbert, still extended on the chair, covered his face with both hands, as if to prevent the light from reaching him.\n\n“I hastened to you,” continued Beauchamp, “to tell you, Albert, that in this changing age, the faults of a father cannot revert upon his children. Few have passed through this revolutionary period, in the midst of which we were born, without some stain of infamy or blood to soil the uniform of the soldier, or the gown of the magistrate. Now I have these proofs, Albert, and I am in your confidence, no human power can force me to a duel which your own conscience would reproach you with as criminal, but I come to offer you what you can no longer demand of me. Do you wish these proofs, these attestations, which I alone possess, to be destroyed? Do you wish this frightful secret to remain with us? Confided to me, it shall never escape my lips; say, Albert, my friend, do you wish it?”\n\nAlbert threw himself on Beauchamp’s neck.\n\n“Ah, noble fellow!” cried he.\n\n“Take these,” said Beauchamp, presenting the papers to Albert.\n\nAlbert seized them with a convulsive hand, tore them in pieces, and trembling lest the least vestige should escape and one day appear to confront him, he approached the wax-light, always kept burning for cigars, and burned every fragment.\n\n“Dear, excellent friend,” murmured Albert, still burning the papers.\n\n“Let all be forgotten as a sorrowful dream,” said Beauchamp; “let it vanish as the last sparks from the blackened paper, and disappear as the smoke from those silent ashes.”\n\n“Yes, yes,” said Albert, “and may there remain only the eternal friendship which I promised to my deliverer, which shall be transmitted to our children’s children, and shall always remind me that I owe my life and the honor of my name to you,—for had this been known, oh, Beauchamp, I should have destroyed myself; or,—no, my poor mother! I could not have killed her by the same blow,—I should have fled from my country.”\n\n“Dear Albert,” said Beauchamp. But this sudden and factitious joy soon forsook the young man, and was succeeded by a still greater grief.\n\n“Well,” said Beauchamp, “what still oppresses you, my friend?”\n\n40178m\n“I am broken-hearted,” said Albert. “Listen, Beauchamp! I cannot thus, in a moment relinquish the respect, the confidence, and pride with which a father’s untarnished name inspires a son. Oh, Beauchamp, Beauchamp, how shall I now approach mine? Shall I draw back my forehead from his embrace, or withhold my hand from his? I am the most wretched of men. Ah, my mother, my poor mother!” said Albert, gazing through his tears at his mother’s portrait; “if you know this, how much must you suffer!”\n\n“Come,” said Beauchamp, taking both his hands, “take courage, my friend.”\n\n“But how came that first note to be inserted in your journal? Some unknown enemy—an invisible foe—has done this.”\n\n“The more must you fortify yourself, Albert. Let no trace of emotion be visible on your countenance, bear your grief as the cloud bears within it ruin and death—a fatal secret, known only when the storm bursts. Go, my friend, reserve your strength for the moment when the crash shall come.”\n\n40179m\n“You think, then, all is not over yet?” said Albert, horror-stricken.\n\n“I think nothing, my friend; but all things are possible. By the way——”\n\n“What?” said Albert, seeing that Beauchamp hesitated.\n\n“Are you going to marry Mademoiselle Danglars?”\n\n“Why do you ask me now?”\n\n“Because the rupture or fulfilment of this engagement is connected with the person of whom we were speaking.”\n\n“How?” said Albert, whose brow reddened; “you think M. Danglars——”\n\n“I ask you only how your engagement stands? Pray put no construction on my words I do not mean they should convey, and give them no undue weight.”\n\n“No.” said Albert, “the engagement is broken off.”\n\n“Well,” said Beauchamp. Then, seeing the young man was about to relapse into melancholy, “Let us go out, Albert,” said he; “a ride in the wood in the phaeton, or on horseback, will refresh you; we will then return to breakfast, and you shall attend to your affairs, and I to mine.”\n\n“Willingly,” said Albert; “but let us walk. I think a little exertion would do me good.”\n\nThe two friends walked out on the fortress. When they arrived at the Madeleine:\n\n“Since we are out,” said Beauchamp, “let us call on M. de Monte Cristo; he is admirably adapted to revive one’s spirits, because he never interrogates, and in my opinion those who ask no questions are the best comforters.”\n\n“Gladly,” said Albert; “let us call—I love him.”\n\n# Chapter 85. The Journey\n\nMonte Cristo uttered a joyful exclamation on seeing the young men together. “Ah, ha!” said he, “I hope all is over, explained and settled.”\n\n“Yes,” said Beauchamp; “the absurd reports have died away, and should they be renewed, I would be the first to oppose them; so let us speak no more of it.”\n\n“Albert will tell you,” replied the count “that I gave him the same advice. Look,” added he. “I am finishing the most execrable morning’s work.”\n\n“What is it?” said Albert; “arranging your papers, apparently.”\n\n“My papers, thank God, no,—my papers are all in capital order, because I have none; but M. Cavalcanti’s.”\n\n“M. Cavalcanti’s?” asked Beauchamp.\n\n“Yes; do you not know that this is a young man whom the count is introducing?” said Morcerf.\n\n“Let us not misunderstand each other,” replied Monte Cristo; “I introduce no one, and certainly not M. Cavalcanti.”\n\n“And who,” said Albert with a forced smile, “is to marry Mademoiselle Danglars instead of me, which grieves me cruelly.”\n\n“What? Cavalcanti is going to marry Mademoiselle Danglars?” asked Beauchamp.\n\n“Certainly! do you come from the end of the world?” said Monte Cristo; “you, a journalist, the husband of renown? It is the talk of all Paris.”\n\n“And you, count, have made this match?” asked Beauchamp.\n\n“I? Silence, purveyor of gossip, do not spread that report. I make a match? No, you do not know me; I have done all in my power to oppose it.”\n\n“Ah, I understand,” said Beauchamp, “on our friend Albert’s account.”\n\n“On my account?” said the young man; “oh, no, indeed, the count will do me the justice to assert that I have, on the contrary, always entreated him to break off my engagement, and happily it is ended. The count pretends I have not him to thank;—so be it—I will erect an altar _Deo ignoto_.”\n\n“Listen,” said Monte Cristo; “I have had little to do with it, for I am at variance both with the father-in-law and the young man; there is only Mademoiselle Eugénie, who appears but little charmed with the thoughts of matrimony, and who, seeing how little I was disposed to persuade her to renounce her dear liberty, retains any affection for me.”\n\n“And do you say this wedding is at hand?”\n\n“Oh, yes, in spite of all I could say. I do not know the young man; he is said to be of good family and rich, but I never trust to vague assertions. I have warned M. Danglars of it till I am tired, but he is fascinated with his Luccanese. I have even informed him of a circumstance I consider very serious; the young man was either charmed by his nurse, stolen by gypsies, or lost by his tutor, I scarcely know which. But I do know his father lost sight of him for more than ten years; what he did during these ten years, God only knows. Well, all that was useless. They have commissioned me to write to the major to demand papers, and here they are. I send them, but like Pilate—washing my hands.”\n\n“And what does Mademoiselle d’Armilly say to you for robbing her of her pupil?”\n\n“Oh, well, I don’t know; but I understand that she is going to Italy. Madame Danglars asked me for letters of recommendation for the _impresari_; I gave her a few lines for the director of the Valle Theatre, who is under some obligation to me. But what is the matter, Albert? you look dull; are you, after all, unconsciously in love with Mademoiselle Eugénie?”\n\n“I am not aware of it,” said Albert, smiling sorrowfully. Beauchamp turned to look at some paintings.\n\n“But,” continued Monte Cristo, “you are not in your usual spirits?”\n\n“I have a dreadful headache,” said Albert.\n\n“Well, my dear viscount,” said Monte Cristo, “I have an infallible remedy to propose to you.”\n\n“What is that?” asked the young man.\n\n“Indeed?” said Albert.\n\n“Yes; and as I am just now excessively annoyed, I shall go from home. Shall we go together?”\n\n“You annoyed, count?” said Beauchamp; “and by what?”\n\n“Ah, you think very lightly of it; I should like to see you with a brief preparing in your house.”\n\n“What brief?”\n\n“The one M. de Villefort is preparing against my amiable assassin—some brigand escaped from the gallows apparently.”\n\n“True,” said Beauchamp; “I saw it in the paper. Who is this Caderousse?”\n\n“Some Provençal, it appears. M. de Villefort heard of him at Marseilles, and M. Danglars recollects having seen him. Consequently, the procureur is very active in the affair, and the prefect of police very much interested; and, thanks to that interest, for which I am very grateful, they send me all the robbers of Paris and the neighborhood, under pretence of their being Caderousse’s murderers, so that in three months, if this continues, every robber and assassin in France will have the plan of my house at his fingers’ ends. I am resolved to desert them and go to some remote corner of the earth, and shall be happy if you will accompany me, viscount.”\n\n“Willingly.”\n\n“Then it is settled?”\n\n“Yes, but where?”\n\n“I have told you, where the air is pure, where every sound soothes, where one is sure to be humbled, however proud may be his nature. I love that humiliation, I, who am master of the universe, as was Augustus.”\n\n“But where are you really going?”\n\n“To sea, viscount; you know I am a sailor. I was rocked when an infant in the arms of old Ocean, and on the bosom of the beautiful Amphitrite; I have sported with the green mantle of the one and the azure robe of the other; I love the sea as a mistress, and pine if I do not often see her.”\n\n“Let us go, count.”\n\n“To sea?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You accept my proposal?”\n“I do.”\n\n“Well, viscount, there will be in my courtyard this evening a good travelling britzka, with four post-horses, in which one may rest as in a bed. M. Beauchamp, it holds four very well, will you accompany us?”\n\n“Thank you, I have just returned from sea.”\n\n“What? you have been to sea?”\n\n“Yes; I have just made a little excursion to the Borromean Islands18.”\n\n“What of that? come with us,” said Albert.\n\n“No, dear Morcerf; you know I only refuse when the thing is impossible. Besides, it is important,” added he in a low tone, “that I should remain in Paris just now to watch the paper.”\n\n“Ah, you are a good and an excellent friend,” said Albert; “yes, you are right; watch, watch, Beauchamp, and try to discover the enemy who made this disclosure.”\n\nAlbert and Beauchamp parted, the last pressure of their hands expressing what their tongues could not before a stranger.\n\n“Beauchamp is a worthy fellow,” said Monte Cristo, when the journalist was gone; “is he not, Albert?”\n\n“Yes, and a sincere friend; I love him devotedly. But now we are alone,—although it is immaterial to me,—where are we going?”\n\n“Into Normandy, if you like.”\n\n“Delightful; shall we be quite retired? have no society, no neighbors?”\n\n“Our companions will be riding-horses, dogs to hunt with, and a fishing-boat.”\n\n“Exactly what I wish for; I will apprise my mother of my intention, and return to you.”\n\n“But shall you be allowed to go into Normandy?”\n\n“I may go where I please.”\n\n“Yes, I am aware you may go alone, since I once met you in Italy—but to accompany the mysterious Monte Cristo?”\n\n“You forget, count, that I have often told you of the deep interest my mother takes in you.”\n\n“‘Woman is fickle.’ said Francis I.; ‘woman is like a wave of the sea,’ said Shakespeare; both the great king and the great poet ought to have known woman’s nature well.”\n\n“Woman’s, yes; my mother is not woman, but _a_ woman.”\n\n“As I am only a humble foreigner, you must pardon me if I do not understand all the subtle refinements of your language.”\n\n“What I mean to say is, that my mother is not quick to give her confidence, but when she does she never changes.”\n\n“Ah, yes, indeed,” said Monte Cristo with a sigh; “and do you think she is in the least interested in me?”\n\n“I repeat it, you must really be a very strange and superior man, for my mother is so absorbed by the interest you have excited, that when I am with her she speaks of no one else.”\n\n“And does she try to make you dislike me?”\n\n“On the contrary, she often says, ‘Morcerf, I believe the count has a noble nature; try to gain his esteem.’”\n\n“Indeed?” said Monte Cristo, sighing.\n\n“You see, then,” said Albert, “that instead of opposing, she will encourage me.”\n\n“Adieu, then, until five o’clock; be punctual, and we shall arrive at twelve or one.”\n\n“At Tréport?”\n\n“Yes; or in the neighborhood.”\n\n“But can we travel forty-eight leagues in eight hours?”\n\n“Easily,” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“You are certainly a prodigy; you will soon not only surpass the railway, which would not be very difficult in France, but even the telegraph.”\n\n“But, viscount, since we cannot perform the journey in less than seven or eight hours, do not keep me waiting.”\n\n“Do not fear, I have little to prepare.”\n\nMonte Cristo smiled as he nodded to Albert, then remained a moment absorbed in deep meditation. But passing his hand across his forehead as if to dispel his reverie, he rang the bell twice and Bertuccio entered.\n\n“Bertuccio,” said he, “I intend going this evening to Normandy, instead of tomorrow or the next day. You will have sufficient time before five o’clock; despatch a messenger to apprise the grooms at the first station. M. de Morcerf will accompany me.”\n\nBertuccio obeyed and despatched a courier to Pontoise to say the travelling-carriage would arrive at six o’clock. From Pontoise another express was sent to the next stage, and in six hours all the horses stationed on the road were ready.\n\nBefore his departure, the count went to Haydée’s apartments, told her his intention, and resigned everything to her care.\n\nAlbert was punctual. The journey soon became interesting from its rapidity, of which Morcerf had formed no previous idea.\n\n“Truly,” said Monte Cristo, “with your post-horses going at the rate of two leagues an hour, and that absurd law that one traveller shall not pass another without permission, so that an invalid or ill-tempered traveller may detain those who are well and active, it is impossible to move; I escape this annoyance by travelling with my own postilion and horses; do I not, Ali?”\n\nThe count put his head out of the window and whistled, and the horses appeared to fly. The carriage rolled with a thundering noise over the pavement, and everyone turned to notice the dazzling meteor. Ali, smiling, repeated the sound, grasped the reins with a firm hand, and spurred his horses, whose beautiful manes floated in the breeze. This child of the desert was in his element, and with his black face and sparkling eyes appeared, in the cloud of dust he raised, like the genius of the simoom and the god of the hurricane.\n\n“I never knew till now the delight of speed,” said Morcerf, and the last cloud disappeared from his brow; “but where the devil do you get such horses? Are they made to order?”\n\n“Precisely,” said the count; “six years since I bought a horse in Hungary remarkable for its swiftness. The thirty-two that we shall use tonight are its progeny; they are all entirely black, with the exception of a star upon the forehead.”\n\n“That is perfectly admirable; but what do you do, count, with all these horses?”\n\n“You see, I travel with them.”\n\n“But you are not always travelling.”\n\n“When I no longer require them, Bertuccio will sell them, and he expects to realize thirty or forty thousand francs by the sale.”\n\n“But no monarch in Europe will be wealthy enough to purchase them.”\n\n“Then he will sell them to some Eastern vizier, who will empty his coffers to purchase them, and refill them by applying the bastinado to his subjects.”\n\n“Count, may I suggest one idea to you?”\n\n“Certainly.”\n\n“It is that, next to you, Bertuccio must be the richest gentleman in Europe.”\n\n“You are mistaken, viscount; I believe he has not a franc in his possession.”\n\n“Then he must be a wonder. My dear count, if you tell me many more marvellous things, I warn you I shall not believe them.”\n\n“I countenance nothing that is marvellous, M. Albert. Tell me, why does a steward rob his master?”\n\n“Because, I suppose, it is his nature to do so, for the love of robbing.”\n\n“You are mistaken; it is because he has a wife and family, and ambitious desires for himself and them. Also because he is not sure of always retaining his situation, and wishes to provide for the future. Now, M. Bertuccio is alone in the world; he uses my property without accounting for the use he makes of it; he is sure never to leave my service.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because I should never get a better.”\n\n“Probabilities are deceptive.”\n\n“But I deal in certainties; he is the best servant over whom one has the power of life and death.”\n\n“Do you possess that right over Bertuccio?”\n\n“Yes.”\nThere are words which close a conversation with an iron door; such was the count’s “yes.”\n\nThe whole journey was performed with equal rapidity; the thirty-two horses, dispersed over seven stages, brought them to their destination in eight hours. At midnight they arrived at the gate of a beautiful park. The porter was in attendance; he had been apprised by the groom of the last stage of the count’s approach. At half past two in the morning Morcerf was conducted to his apartments, where a bath and supper were prepared. The servant who had travelled at the back of the carriage waited on him; Baptistin, who rode in front, attended the count.\n\nAlbert bathed, took his supper, and went to bed. All night he was lulled by the melancholy noise of the surf. On rising, he went to his window, which opened on a terrace, having the sea in front, and at the back a pretty park bounded by a small forest.\n\nIn a creek lay a little sloop, with a narrow keel and high masts, bearing on its flag the Monte Cristo arms which were a mountain _or_, on a sea _azure_, with a cross _gules_ in chief which might be an allusion to his name that recalled Calvary, the mount rendered by our Lord’s passion more precious than gold, and to the degrading cross which his blood had rendered holy; or it might be some personal remembrance of suffering and regeneration buried in the night of this mysterious personage’s past life.\n\nAround the schooner lay a number of small fishing-boats belonging to the fishermen of the neighboring village, like humble subjects awaiting orders from their queen. There, as in every spot where Monte Cristo stopped, if but for two days, luxury abounded and life went on with the utmost ease.\n\nAlbert found in his anteroom two guns, with all the accoutrements for hunting; a lofty room on the ground floor containing all the ingenious instruments the English—eminent in piscatory pursuits, since they are patient and sluggish—have invented for fishing. The day passed in pursuing those exercises in which Monte Cristo excelled. They killed a dozen pheasants in the park, as many trout in the stream, dined in a summer-house overlooking the ocean, and took tea in the library.\n\nTowards the evening of the third day. Albert, completely exhausted with the exercise which invigorated Monte Cristo, was sleeping in an armchair near the window, while the count was designing with his architect the plan of a conservatory in his house, when the sound of a horse at full speed on the high road made Albert look up. He was disagreeably surprised to see his own valet de chambre, whom he had not brought, that he might not inconvenience Monte Cristo.\n\n40188m\n“Florentin here!” cried he, starting up; “is my mother ill?” And he hastened to the door. Monte Cristo watched and saw him approach the valet, who drew a small sealed parcel from his pocket, containing a newspaper and a letter.\n\n“From whom is this?” said he eagerly.\n\n“From M. Beauchamp,” replied Florentin.\n\n“Did he send you?”\n\n“Yes, sir; he sent for me to his house, gave me money for my journey, procured a horse, and made me promise not to stop till I had reached you, I have come in fifteen hours.”\n\nAlbert opened the letter with fear, uttered a shriek on reading the first line, and seized the paper. His sight was dimmed, his legs sank under him, and he would have fallen had not Florentin supported him.\n\n“Poor young man,” said Monte Cristo in a low voice; “it is then true that the sin of the father shall fall on the children to the third and fourth generation.”\n\nMeanwhile Albert had revived, and, continuing to read, he threw back his head, saying:\n\n“Florentin, is your horse fit to return immediately?”\n\n“It is a poor, lame post-horse.”\n\n“In what state was the house when you left?”\n\n“All was quiet, but on returning from M. Beauchamp’s, I found madame in tears; she had sent for me to know when you would return. I told her my orders from M. Beauchamp; she first extended her arms to prevent me, but after a moment’s reflection, ‘Yes, go, Florentin,’ said she, ‘and may he come quickly.’”\n\n“Yes, my mother,” said Albert, “I will return, and woe to the infamous wretch! But first of all I must get there.”\n\nHe went back to the room where he had left Monte Cristo. Five minutes had sufficed to make a complete transformation in his appearance. His voice had become rough and hoarse; his face was furrowed with wrinkles; his eyes burned under the blue-veined lids, and he tottered like a drunken man.\n\n“Count,” said he, “I thank you for your hospitality, which I would gladly have enjoyed longer; but I must return to Paris.”\n\n“What has happened?”\n\n“A great misfortune, more important to me than life. Don’t question me, I beg of you, but lend me a horse.”\n\n“My stables are at your command, viscount; but you will kill yourself by riding on horseback. Take a post-chaise or a carriage.”\n\n“No, it would delay me, and I need the fatigue you warn me of; it will do me good.”\n\nAlbert reeled as if he had been shot, and fell on a chair near the door. Monte Cristo did not see this second manifestation of physical exhaustion; he was at the window, calling:\n\n“Ali, a horse for M. de Morcerf—quick! he is in a hurry!”\n\n40190m\nThese words restored Albert; he darted from the room, followed by the count.\n\n“Thank you!” cried he, throwing himself on his horse.\n\n“Return as soon as you can, Florentin. Must I use any password to procure a horse?”\n\n“Only dismount; another will be immediately saddled.”\n\nAlbert hesitated a moment. “You may think my departure strange and foolish,” said the young man; “you do not know how a paragraph in a newspaper may exasperate one. Read that,” said he, “when I am gone, that you may not be witness of my anger.”\n\nWhile the count picked up the paper he put spurs to his horse, which leaped in astonishment at such an unusual stimulus, and shot away with the rapidity of an arrow. The count watched him with a feeling of compassion, and when he had completely disappeared, read as follows:\n\n“The French officer in the service of Ali Pasha of Yanina alluded to three weeks since in _l’Impartial_, who not only surrendered the castle of Yanina, but sold his benefactor to the Turks, styled himself truly at that time Fernand, as our esteemed contemporary states; but he has since added to his Christian name a title of nobility and a family name. He now calls himself the Count of Morcerf, and ranks among the peers.”\n\nThus the terrible secret, which Beauchamp had so generously destroyed, appeared again like an armed phantom; and another paper, deriving its information from some malicious source, had published two days after Albert’s departure for Normandy the few lines which had rendered the unfortunate young man almost crazy.\n\n# Chapter 86. The Trial\n\nAt eight o’clock in the morning Albert had arrived at Beauchamp’s door. The valet de chambre had received orders to usher him in at once. Beauchamp was in his bath.\n\n“Here I am,” Albert said.\n\n“Well, my poor friend,” replied Beauchamp, “I expected you.”\n\n“I need not say I think you are too faithful and too kind to have spoken of that painful circumstance. Your having sent for me is another proof of your affection. So, without losing time, tell me, have you the slightest idea whence this terrible blow proceeds?”\n\n“I think I have some clew.”\n\n“But first tell me all the particulars of this shameful plot.”\n\nBeauchamp proceeded to relate to the young man, who was overwhelmed with shame and grief, the following facts. Two days previously, the article had appeared in another paper besides _ l’Impartial_, and, what was more serious, one that was well known as a government paper. Beauchamp was breakfasting when he read the paragraph. He sent immediately for a cabriolet, and hastened to the publisher’s office. Although professing diametrically opposite principles from those of the editor of the other paper, Beauchamp—as it sometimes, we may say often, happens—was his intimate friend. The editor was reading, with apparent delight, a leading article in the same paper on beet-sugar, probably a composition of his own.\n\n“Ah, _pardieu!_” said Beauchamp, “with the paper in your hand, my friend, I need not tell you the cause of my visit.”\n\n“Are you interested in the sugar question?” asked the editor of the ministerial paper.\n\n“No,” replied Beauchamp, “I have not considered the question; a totally different subject interests me.”\n\n“What is it?”\n\n“The article relative to Morcerf.”\n\n“Indeed? Is it not a curious affair?”\n\n“So curious, that I think you are running a great risk of a prosecution for defamation of character.”\n\n“Not at all; we have received with the information all the requisite proofs, and we are quite sure M. de Morcerf will not raise his voice against us; besides, it is rendering a service to one’s country to denounce these wretched criminals who are unworthy of the honor bestowed on them.”\n\nBeauchamp was thunderstruck.\n\n“Who, then, has so correctly informed you?” asked he; “for my paper, which gave the first information on the subject, has been obliged to stop for want of proof; and yet we are more interested than you in exposing M. de Morcerf, as he is a peer of France, and we are of the opposition.”\n\n“Oh, that is very simple; we have not sought to scandalize. This news was brought to us. A man arrived yesterday from Yanina, bringing a formidable array of documents; and when we hesitated to publish the accusatory article, he told us it should be inserted in some other paper.”\n\nBeauchamp understood that nothing remained but to submit, and left the office to despatch a courier to Morcerf. But he had been unable to send to Albert the following particulars, as the events had transpired after the messenger’s departure; namely, that the same day a great agitation was manifest in the House of Peers among the usually calm members of that dignified assembly. Everyone had arrived almost before the usual hour, and was conversing on the melancholy event which was to attract the attention of the public towards one of their most illustrious colleagues. Some were perusing the article, others making comments and recalling circumstances which substantiated the charges still more.\n\nThe Count of Morcerf was no favorite with his colleagues. Like all upstarts, he had had recourse to a great deal of haughtiness to maintain his position. The true nobility laughed at him, the talented repelled him, and the honorable instinctively despised him. He was, in fact, in the unhappy position of the victim marked for sacrifice; the finger of God once pointed at him, everyone was prepared to raise the hue and cry.\n\nThe Count of Morcerf alone was ignorant of the news. He did not take in the paper containing the defamatory article, and had passed the morning in writing letters and in trying a horse. He arrived at his usual hour, with a proud look and insolent demeanor; he alighted, passed through the corridors, and entered the house without observing the hesitation of the door-keepers or the coolness of his colleagues.\n\n40194m\nBusiness had already been going on for half an hour when he entered. Everyone held the accusing paper, but, as usual, no one liked to take upon himself the responsibility of the attack. At length an honorable peer, Morcerf’s acknowledged enemy, ascended the tribune with that solemnity which announced that the expected moment had arrived. There was an impressive silence; Morcerf alone knew not why such profound attention was given to an orator who was not always listened to with so much complacency.\n\nThe count did not notice the introduction, in which the speaker announced that his communication would be of that vital importance that it demanded the undivided attention of the House; but at the mention of Yanina and Colonel Fernand, he turned so frightfully pale that every member shuddered and fixed his eyes upon him. Moral wounds have this peculiarity,—they may be hidden, but they never close; always painful, always ready to bleed when touched, they remain fresh and open in the heart.\n\nThe article having been read during the painful hush that followed, a universal shudder pervaded the assembly, and immediately the closest attention was given to the orator as he resumed his remarks. He stated his scruples and the difficulties of the case; it was the honor of M. de Morcerf, and that of the whole House, he proposed to defend, by provoking a debate on personal questions, which are always such painful themes of discussion. He concluded by calling for an investigation, which might dispose of the calumnious report before it had time to spread, and restore M. de Morcerf to the position he had long held in public opinion.\n\nMorcerf was so completely overwhelmed by this great and unexpected calamity that he could scarcely stammer a few words as he looked around on the assembly. This timidity, which might proceed from the astonishment of innocence as well as the shame of guilt, conciliated some in his favor; for men who are truly generous are always ready to compassionate when the misfortune of their enemy surpasses the limits of their hatred.\n\nThe president put it to the vote, and it was decided that the investigation should take place. The count was asked what time he required to prepare his defence. Morcerf’s courage had revived when he found himself alive after this horrible blow.\n\n“My lords,” answered he, “it is not by time I could repel the attack made on me by enemies unknown to me, and, doubtless, hidden in obscurity; it is immediately, and by a thunderbolt, that I must repel the flash of lightning which, for a moment, startled me. Oh, that I could, instead of taking up this defence, shed my last drop of blood to prove to my noble colleagues that I am their equal in worth.”\n\nThese words made a favorable impression on behalf of the accused.\n\n“I demand, then, that the examination shall take place as soon as possible, and I will furnish the house with all necessary information.”\n\n“What day do you fix?” asked the president.\n\n“Today I am at your service,” replied the count.\n\nThe president rang the bell. “Does the House approve that the examination should take place today?”\n40196m\n\n“Yes,” was the unanimous answer.\n\nA committee of twelve members was chosen to examine the proofs brought forward by Morcerf. The investigation would begin at eight o’clock that evening in the committee-room, and if postponement were necessary, the proceedings would be resumed each evening at the same hour. Morcerf asked leave to retire; he had to collect the documents he had long been preparing against this storm, which his sagacity had foreseen.\n\nBeauchamp related to the young man all the facts we have just narrated; his story, however, had over ours all the advantage of the animation of living things over the coldness of dead things.\n\nAlbert listened, trembling now with hope, then with anger, and then again with shame, for from Beauchamp’s confidence he knew his father was guilty, and he asked himself how, since he was guilty, he could prove his innocence. Beauchamp hesitated to continue his narrative.\n\n“What next?” asked Albert.\n\n“What next? My friend, you impose a painful task on me. Must you know all?”\n\n“Absolutely; and rather from your lips than another’s.”\n\n“Muster up all your courage, then, for never have you required it more.”\n\nAlbert passed his hand over his forehead, as if to try his strength, as a man who is preparing to defend his life proves his shield and bends his sword. He thought himself strong enough, for he mistook fever for energy. “Go on,” said he.\n\n“The evening arrived; all Paris was in expectation. Many said your father had only to show himself to crush the charge against him; many others said he would not appear; while some asserted that they had seen him start for Brussels; and others went to the police-office to inquire if he had taken out a passport. I used all my influence with one of the committee, a young peer of my acquaintance, to get admission to one of the galleries. He called for me at seven o’clock, and, before anyone had arrived, asked one of the door-keepers to place me in a box. I was concealed by a column, and might witness the whole of the terrible scene which was about to take place. At eight o’clock all were in their places, and M. de Morcerf entered at the last stroke. He held some papers in his hand; his countenance was calm, and his step firm, and he was dressed with great care in his military uniform, which was buttoned completely up to the chin. His presence produced a good effect. The committee was made up of Liberals, several of whom came forward to shake hands with him.”\n\nAlbert felt his heart bursting at these particulars, but gratitude mingled with his sorrow: he would gladly have embraced those who had given his father this proof of esteem at a moment when his honor was so powerfully attacked.\n\n“At this moment one of the door-keepers brought in a letter for the president. ‘You are at liberty to speak, M. de Morcerf,’ said the president, as he unsealed the letter; and the count began his defence, I assure you, Albert, in a most eloquent and skilful manner. He produced documents proving that the Vizier of Yanina had up to the last moment honored him with his entire confidence, since he had interested him with a negotiation of life and death with the emperor. He produced the ring, his mark of authority, with which Ali Pasha generally sealed his letters, and which the latter had given him, that he might, on his return at any hour of the day or night, gain access to the presence, even in the harem. Unfortunately, the negotiation failed, and when he returned to defend his benefactor, he was dead. ‘But,’ said the count, ‘so great was Ali Pasha’s confidence, that on his death-bed he resigned his favorite mistress and her daughter to my care.’”\n\nAlbert started on hearing these words; the history of Haydée recurred to him, and he remembered what she had said of that message and the ring, and the manner in which she had been sold and made a slave.\n\n“And what effect did this discourse produce?” anxiously inquired Albert.\n\n“I acknowledge it affected me, and, indeed, all the committee also,” said Beauchamp.\n\n“Meanwhile, the president carelessly opened the letter which had been brought to him; but the first lines aroused his attention; he read them again and again, and fixing his eyes on M. de Morcerf, ‘Count,’ said he, ‘you have said that the Vizier of Yanina confided his wife and daughter to your care?’—‘Yes, sir,’ replied Morcerf; ‘but in that, like all the rest, misfortune pursued me. On my return, Vasiliki and her daughter Haydée had disappeared.’—‘Did you know them?’—‘My intimacy with the pasha and his unlimited confidence had gained me an introduction to them, and I had seen them above twenty times.’\n\n“‘Have you any idea what became of them?’—‘Yes, sir; I heard they had fallen victims to their sorrow, and, perhaps, to their poverty. I was not rich; my life was in constant danger; I could not seek them, to my great regret.’ The president frowned imperceptibly. ‘Gentlemen,’ said he, ‘you have heard the Comte de Morcerf’s defence. Can you, sir, produce any witnesses to the truth of what you have asserted?’—‘Alas, no, monsieur,’ replied the count; ‘all those who surrounded the vizier, or who knew me at his court, are either dead or gone away, I know not where. I believe that I alone, of all my countrymen, survived that dreadful war. I have only the letters of Ali Tepelini, which I have placed before you; the ring, a token of his good-will, which is here; and, lastly, the most convincing proof I can offer, after an anonymous attack, and that is the absence of any witness against my veracity and the purity of my military life.’\n\n“A murmur of approbation ran through the assembly; and at this moment, Albert, had nothing more transpired, your father’s cause had been gained. It only remained to put it to the vote, when the president resumed: ‘Gentlemen and you, monsieur,—you will not be displeased, I presume, to listen to one who calls himself a very important witness, and who has just presented himself. He is, doubtless, come to prove the perfect innocence of our colleague. Here is a letter I have just received on the subject; shall it be read, or shall it be passed over? and shall we take no notice of this incident?’ M. de Morcerf turned pale, and clenched his hands on the papers he held. The committee decided to hear the letter; the count was thoughtful and silent. The president read:\n\n“‘Mr. President,—I can furnish the committee of inquiry into the conduct of the Lieutenant-General the Count of Morcerf in Epirus and in Macedonia with important particulars.’\n\n“The president paused, and the count turned pale. The president looked at his auditors. ‘Proceed,’ was heard on all sides. The president resumed:\n\n“‘I was on the spot at the death of Ali Pasha. I was present during his last moments. I know what is become of Vasiliki and Haydée. I am at the command of the committee, and even claim the honor of being heard. I shall be in the lobby when this note is delivered to you.’\n\n“‘And who is this witness, or rather this enemy?’ asked the count, in a tone in which there was a visible alteration. ‘We shall know, sir,’ replied the president. ‘Is the committee willing to hear this witness?’—‘Yes, yes,’ they all said at once. The door-keeper was called. ‘Is there anyone in the lobby?’ said the president.\n\n“‘Yes, sir.’—‘Who is it?’—‘A woman, accompanied by a servant.’ Everyone looked at his neighbor. ‘Bring her in,’ said the president. Five minutes after the door-keeper again appeared; all eyes were fixed on the door, and I,” said Beauchamp, “shared the general expectation and anxiety. Behind the door-keeper walked a woman enveloped in a large veil, which completely concealed her. It was evident, from her figure and the perfumes she had about her, that she was young and fastidious in her tastes, but that was all. The president requested her to throw aside her veil, and it was then seen that she was dressed in the Grecian costume, and was remarkably beautiful.”\n\n“Ah,” said Albert, “it was she.”\n“Who?”\n\n“Haydée.”\n\n“Who told you that?”\n\n“Alas, I guess it. But go on, Beauchamp. You see I am calm and strong. And yet we must be drawing near the disclosure.”\n\n“M. de Morcerf,” continued Beauchamp, “looked at this woman with surprise and terror. Her lips were about to pass his sentence of life or death. To the committee the adventure was so extraordinary and curious, that the interest they had felt for the count’s safety became now quite a secondary matter. The president himself advanced to place a seat for the young lady; but she declined availing herself of it. As for the count, he had fallen on his chair; it was evident that his legs refused to support him.\n\n“‘Madame,’ said the president, ‘you have engaged to furnish the committee with some important particulars respecting the affair at Yanina, and you have stated that you were an eyewitness of the event.’—‘I was, indeed,’ said the stranger, with a tone of sweet melancholy, and with the sonorous voice peculiar to the East.\n\n“‘But allow me to say that you must have been very young then.’—‘I was four years old; but as those events deeply concerned me, not a single detail has escaped my memory.’—‘In what manner could these events concern you? and who are you, that they should have made so deep an impression on you?’—‘On them depended my father’s life,’ replied she. ‘I am Haydée, the daughter of Ali Tepelini, pasha of Yanina, and of Vasiliki, his beloved wife.’\n\n40202m\n“The blush of mingled pride and modesty which suddenly suffused the cheeks of the young woman, the brilliancy of her eye, and her highly important communication, produced an indescribable effect on the assembly. As for the count, he could not have been more overwhelmed if a thunderbolt had fallen at his feet and opened an immense gulf before him.\n\n“‘Madame,’ replied the president, bowing with profound respect, ‘allow me to ask one question; it shall be the last: Can you prove the authenticity of what you have now stated?’\n\n“‘I can, sir,’ said Haydée, drawing from under her veil a satin satchel highly perfumed; ‘for here is the register of my birth, signed by my father and his principal officers, and that of my baptism, my father having consented to my being brought up in my mother’s faith,—this latter has been sealed by the grand primate of Macedonia and Epirus; and lastly (and perhaps the most important), the record of the sale of my person and that of my mother to the Armenian merchant El-Kobbir, by the French officer, who, in his infamous bargain with the Porte, had reserved as his part of the booty the wife and daughter of his benefactor, whom he sold for the sum of four hundred thousand francs.’ A greenish pallor spread over the count’s cheeks, and his eyes became bloodshot at these terrible imputations, which were listened to by the assembly with ominous silence.\n\n“Haydée, still calm, but with a calmness more dreadful than the anger of another would have been, handed to the president the record of her sale, written in Arabic. It had been supposed some of the papers might be in the Arabian, Romaic, or Turkish language, and the interpreter of the House was in attendance. One of the noble peers, who was familiar with the Arabic language, having studied it during the famous Egyptian campaign, followed with his eye as the translator read aloud:\n\n“‘I, El-Kobbir, a slave-merchant, and purveyor of the harem of his highness, acknowledge having received for transmission to the sublime emperor, from the French lord, the Count of Monte Cristo, an emerald valued at eight hundred thousand francs; as the ransom of a young Christian slave of eleven years of age, named Haydée, the acknowledged daughter of the late lord Ali Tepelini, pasha of Yanina, and of Vasiliki, his favorite; she having been sold to me seven years previously, with her mother, who had died on arriving at Constantinople, by a French colonel in the service of the Vizier Ali Tepelini, named Fernand Mondego. The above-mentioned purchase was made on his highness’s account, whose mandate I had, for the sum of four hundred thousand francs.\n\n“‘Given at Constantinople, by authority of his highness, in the year 1247 of the Hegira.\n\n“‘Signed, El-Kobbir.’\n\n“‘That this record should have all due authority, it shall bear the imperial seal, which the vendor is bound to have affixed to it.’\n\n“Near the merchant’s signature there was, indeed, the seal of the sublime emperor. A dreadful silence followed the reading of this document; the count could only stare, and his gaze, fixed as if unconsciously on Haydée, seemed one of fire and blood. ‘Madame,’ said the president, ‘may reference be made to the Count of Monte Cristo, who is now, I believe, in Paris?’\n\n“‘Sir,’ replied Haydée, ‘the Count of Monte Cristo, my foster-father, has been in Normandy the last three days.’\n\n“‘Who, then, has counselled you to take this step, one for which the court is deeply indebted to you, and which is perfectly natural, considering your birth and your misfortunes?’—‘Sir,’ replied Haydée, ‘I have been led to take this step from a feeling of respect and grief. Although a Christian, may God forgive me, I have always sought to revenge my illustrious father. Since I set my foot in France, and knew the traitor lived in Paris, I have watched carefully. I live retired in the house of my noble protector, but I do it from choice. I love retirement and silence, because I can live with my thoughts and recollections of past days. But the Count of Monte Cristo surrounds me with every paternal care, and I am ignorant of nothing which passes in the world. I learn all in the silence of my apartments,—for instance, I see all the newspapers, every periodical, as well as every new piece of music; and by thus watching the course of the life of others, I learned what had transpired this morning in the House of Peers, and what was to take place this evening; then I wrote.’\n40204m\n\n“‘Then,’ remarked the president, ‘the Count of Monte Cristo knows nothing of your present proceedings?’—‘He is quite unaware of them, and I have but one fear, which is that he should disapprove of what I have done. But it is a glorious day for me,’ continued the young girl, raising her ardent gaze to heaven, ‘that on which I find at last an opportunity of avenging my father!’\n\n“The count had not uttered one word the whole of this time. His colleagues looked at him, and doubtless pitied his prospects, blighted under the perfumed breath of a woman. His misery was depicted in sinister lines on his countenance. ‘M. de Morcerf,’ said the president, ‘do you recognize this lady as the daughter of Ali Tepelini, pasha of Yanina?’—‘No,’ said Morcerf, attempting to rise, ‘it is a base plot, contrived by my enemies.’ Haydée, whose eyes had been fixed on the door, as if expecting someone, turned hastily, and, seeing the count standing, shrieked, ‘You do not know me?’ said she. ‘Well, I fortunately recognize you! You are Fernand Mondego, the French officer who led the troops of my noble father! It is you who surrendered the castle of Yanina! It is you who, sent by him to Constantinople, to treat with the emperor for the life or death of your benefactor, brought back a false mandate granting full pardon! It is you who, with that mandate, obtained the pasha’s ring, which gave you authority over Selim, the fire-keeper! It is you who stabbed Selim. It is you who sold us, my mother and me, to the merchant, El-Kobbir! Assassin, assassin, assassin, you have still on your brow your master’s blood! Look, gentlemen, all!’\n\n“These words had been pronounced with such enthusiasm and evident truth, that every eye was fixed on the count’s forehead, and he himself passed his hand across it, as if he felt Ali’s blood still lingering there. ‘You positively recognize M. de Morcerf as the officer, Fernand Mondego?’—‘Indeed I do!’ cried Haydée. ‘Oh, my mother, it was you who said, “You were free, you had a beloved father, you were destined to be almost a queen. Look well at that man; it is he who raised your father’s head on the point of a spear; it is he who sold us; it is he who forsook us! Look well at his right hand, on which he has a large wound; if you forgot his features, you would know him by that hand, into which fell, one by one, the gold pieces of the merchant El-Kobbir!” I know him! Ah, let him say now if he does not recognize me!’ Each word fell like a dagger on Morcerf, and deprived him of a portion of his energy; as she uttered the last, he hid his mutilated hand hastily in his bosom, and fell back on his seat, overwhelmed by wretchedness and despair. This scene completely changed the opinion of the assembly respecting the accused count.\n\n“‘Count of Morcerf,’ said the president, ‘do not allow yourself to be cast down; answer. The justice of the court is supreme and impartial as that of God; it will not suffer you to be trampled on by your enemies without giving you an opportunity of defending yourself. Shall further inquiries be made? Shall two members of the House be sent to Yanina? Speak!’ Morcerf did not reply. Then all the members looked at each other with terror. They knew the count’s energetic and violent temper; it must be, indeed, a dreadful blow which would deprive him of courage to defend himself. They expected that his stupefied silence would be followed by a fiery outburst. ‘Well,’ asked the president, ‘what is your decision?’\n40206m\n\n“‘I have no reply to make,’ said the count in a low tone.\n\n“‘Has the daughter of Ali Tepelini spoken the truth?’ said the president. ‘Is she, then, the terrible witness to whose charge you dare not plead “Not guilty”? Have you really committed the crimes of which you are accused?’ The count looked around him with an expression which might have softened tigers, but which could not disarm his judges. Then he raised his eyes towards the ceiling, but withdrew then, immediately, as if he feared the roof would open and reveal to his distressed view that second tribunal called heaven, and that other judge named God. Then, with a hasty movement, he tore open his coat, which seemed to stifle him, and flew from the room like a madman; his footstep was heard one moment in the corridor, then the rattling of his carriage-wheels as he was driven rapidly away. ‘Gentlemen,’ said the president, when silence was restored, ‘is the Count of Morcerf convicted of felony, treason, and conduct unbecoming a member of this House?’—‘Yes,’ replied all the members of the committee of inquiry with a unanimous voice.\n\n“Haydée had remained until the close of the meeting. She heard the count’s sentence pronounced without betraying an expression of joy or pity; then drawing her veil over her face she bowed majestically to the councillors, and left with that dignified step which Virgil attributes to his goddesses.”\n\n# Chapter 87. The Challenge\n\nThen,” continued Beauchamp, “I took advantage of the silence and the darkness to leave the house without being seen. The usher who had introduced me was waiting for me at the door, and he conducted me through the corridors to a private entrance opening into the Rue de Vaugirard. I left with mingled feelings of sorrow and delight. Excuse me, Albert,—sorrow on your account, and delight with that noble girl, thus pursuing paternal vengeance. Yes, Albert, from whatever source the blow may have proceeded—it may be from an enemy, but that enemy is only the agent of Providence.”\n\nAlbert held his head between his hands; he raised his face, red with shame and bathed in tears, and seizing Beauchamp’s arm:\n\n“My friend,” said he, “my life is ended. I cannot calmly say with you, ‘Providence has struck the blow;’ but I must discover who pursues me with this hatred, and when I have found him I shall kill him, or he will kill me. I rely on your friendship to assist me, Beauchamp, if contempt has not banished it from your heart.”\n\n“Contempt, my friend? How does this misfortune affect you? No, happily that unjust prejudice is forgotten which made the son responsible for the father’s actions. Review your life, Albert; although it is only just beginning, did a lovely summer’s day ever dawn with greater purity than has marked the commencement of your career? No, Albert, take my advice. You are young and rich—leave Paris—all is soon forgotten in this great Babylon of excitement and changing tastes. You will return after three or four years with a Russian princess for a bride, and no one will think more of what occurred yesterday than if it had happened sixteen years ago.”\n\n“Thank you, my dear Beauchamp, thank you for the excellent feeling which prompts your advice; but it cannot be. I have told you my wish, or rather my determination. You understand that, interested as I am in this affair, I cannot see it in the same light as you do. What appears to you to emanate from a celestial source, seems to me to proceed from one far less pure. Providence appears to me to have no share in this affair; and happily so, for instead of the invisible, impalpable agent of celestial rewards and punishments, I shall find one both palpable and visible, on whom I shall revenge myself, I assure you, for all I have suffered during the last month. Now, I repeat, Beauchamp, I wish to return to human and material existence, and if you are still the friend you profess to be, help me to discover the hand that struck the blow.”\n\n“Be it so,” said Beauchamp; “if you must have me descend to earth, I submit; and if you will seek your enemy, I will assist you, and I will engage to find him, my honor being almost as deeply interested as yours.”\n\n“Well, then, you understand, Beauchamp, that we begin our search immediately. Each moment’s delay is an eternity for me. The calumniator is not yet punished, and he may hope that he will not be; but, on my honor, if he thinks so, he deceives himself.”\n\n“Well, listen, Morcerf.”\n\n“Ah, Beauchamp, I see you know something already; you will restore me to life.”\n\n“I do not say there is any truth in what I am going to tell you, but it is, at least, a ray of light in a dark night; by following it we may, perhaps, discover something more certain.”\n\n“Tell me; satisfy my impatience.”\n\n“Well, I will tell you what I did not like to mention on my return from Yanina.”\n\n“Say on.”\n\n“I went, of course, to the chief banker of the town to make inquiries. At the first word, before I had even mentioned your father’s name”—\n\n“‘Ah,’ said he. ‘I guess what brings you here.’\n“‘How, and why?’\n\n“‘Because a fortnight since I was questioned on the same subject.’\n\n“‘By whom?’\n\n“‘By a banker of Paris, my correspondent.’\n\n“‘Whose name is——’\n\n“‘Danglars.’”\n\n“He!” cried Albert; “yes, it is indeed he who has so long pursued my father with jealous hatred. He, the man who would be popular, cannot forgive the Count of Morcerf for being created a peer; and this marriage broken off without a reason being assigned—yes, it is all from the same cause.”\n\n“Make inquiries, Albert, but do not be angry without reason; make inquiries, and if it be true——”\n\n“Oh, yes, if it be true,” cried the young man, “he shall pay me all I have suffered.”\n\n“Beware, Morcerf, he is already an old man.”\n\n“I will respect his age as he has respected the honor of my family; if my father had offended him, why did he not attack him personally? Oh, no, he was afraid to encounter him face to face.”\n\n“I do not condemn you, Albert; I only restrain you. Act prudently.”\n\n“Oh, do not fear; besides, you will accompany me. Beauchamp, solemn transactions should be sanctioned by a witness. Before this day closes, if M. Danglars is guilty, he shall cease to live, or I shall die. _Pardieu_, Beauchamp, mine shall be a splendid funeral!”\n\n“When such resolutions are made, Albert, they should be promptly executed. Do you wish to go to M. Danglars? Let us go immediately.”\n\nThey sent for a cabriolet. On entering the banker’s mansion, they perceived the phaeton and servant of M. Andrea Cavalcanti.\n\n“Ah! _parbleu!_ that’s good,” said Albert, with a gloomy tone. “If M. Danglars will not fight with me, I will kill his son-in-law; Cavalcanti will certainly fight.”\n\nThe servant announced the young man; but the banker, recollecting what had transpired the day before, did not wish him admitted. It was, however, too late; Albert had followed the footman, and, hearing the order given, forced the door open, and followed by Beauchamp found himself in the banker’s study.\n\n“Sir,” cried the latter, “am I no longer at liberty to receive whom I choose in my house? You appear to forget yourself sadly.”\n\n“No, sir,” said Albert, coldly; “there are circumstances in which one cannot, except through cowardice,—I offer you that refuge,—refuse to admit certain persons at least.”\n\n“What is your errand, then, with me, sir?”\n\n“I mean,” said Albert, drawing near, and without apparently noticing Cavalcanti, who stood with his back towards the fireplace—“I mean to propose a meeting in some retired corner where no one will interrupt us for ten minutes; that will be sufficient—where two men having met, one of them will remain on the ground.”\n\nDanglars turned pale; Cavalcanti moved a step forward, and Albert turned towards him.\n\n“And you, too,” said he, “come, if you like, monsieur; you have a claim, being almost one of the family, and I will give as many rendezvous of that kind as I can find persons willing to accept them.”\n\nCavalcanti looked at Danglars with a stupefied air, and the latter, making an effort, arose and stepped between the two young men. Albert’s attack on Andrea had placed him on a different footing, and he hoped this visit had another cause than that he had at first supposed.\n\n“Indeed, sir,” said he to Albert, “if you are come to quarrel with this gentleman because I have preferred him to you, I shall resign the case to the king’s attorney.”\n\n“You mistake, sir,” said Morcerf with a gloomy smile; “I am not referring in the least to matrimony, and I only addressed myself to M. Cavalcanti because he appeared disposed to interfere between us. In one respect you are right, for I am ready to quarrel with everyone today; but you have the first claim, M. Danglars.”\n\n40212m\n“Sir,” replied Danglars, pale with anger and fear, “I warn you, when I have the misfortune to meet with a mad dog, I kill it; and far from thinking myself guilty of a crime, I believe I do society a kindness. Now, if you are mad and try to bite me, I will kill you without pity. Is it my fault that your father has dishonored himself?”\n\n“Yes, miserable wretch!” cried Morcerf, “it is your fault.”\n\nDanglars retreated a few steps. “My fault?” said he; “you must be mad! What do I know of the Grecian affair? Have I travelled in that country? Did I advise your father to sell the castle of Yanina—to betray——”\n\n“Silence!” said Albert, with a thundering voice. “No; it is not you who have directly made this exposure and brought this sorrow on us, but you hypocritically provoked it.”\n\n“I?”\n“Yes; you! How came it known?”\n\n“I suppose you read it in the paper in the account from Yanina?”\n\n“Who wrote to Yanina?”\n\n“To Yanina?”\n\n“Yes. Who wrote for particulars concerning my father?”\n\n“I imagine anyone may write to Yanina.”\n\n“But one person only wrote!”\n\n“One only?”\n\n“Yes; and that was you!”\n\n“I, doubtless, wrote. It appears to me that when about to marry your daughter to a young man, it is right to make some inquiries respecting his family; it is not only a right, but a duty.”\n\n“You wrote, sir, knowing what answer you would receive.”\n\n“I, indeed? I assure you,” cried Danglars, with a confidence and security proceeding less from fear than from the interest he really felt for the young man, “I solemnly declare to you, that I should never have thought of writing to Yanina, did I know anything of Ali Pasha’s misfortunes.”\n\n“Who, then, urged you to write? Tell me.”\n\n“_Pardieu!_ it was the most simple thing in the world. I was speaking of your father’s past history. I said the origin of his fortune remained obscure. The person to whom I addressed my scruples asked me where your father had acquired his property? I answered, ‘In Greece.’—‘Then,’ said he, ‘write to Yanina.’”\n\n“And who thus advised you?”\n\n“No other than your friend, Monte Cristo.”\n\n“The Count of Monte Cristo told you to write to Yanina?”\n\n“Yes; and I wrote, and will show you my correspondence, if you like.”\n\nAlbert and Beauchamp looked at each other.\n\n“Sir,” said Beauchamp, who had not yet spoken, “you appear to accuse the count, who is absent from Paris at this moment, and cannot justify himself.”\n\n“I accuse no one, sir,” said Danglars; “I relate, and I will repeat before the count what I have said to you.”\n\n“Does the count know what answer you received?”\n\n“Yes; I showed it to him.”\n\n“Did he know my father’s Christian name was Fernand, and his family name Mondego?”\n\n“Yes, I had told him that long since, and I did only what any other would have done in my circumstances, and perhaps less. When, the day after the arrival of this answer, your father came by the advice of Monte Cristo to ask my daughter’s hand for you, I decidedly refused him, but without any explanation or exposure. In short, why should I have any more to do with the affair? How did the honor or disgrace of M. de Morcerf affect me? It neither increased nor decreased my income.”\n\nAlbert felt the blood mounting to his brow; there was no doubt upon the subject. Danglars defended himself with the baseness, but at the same time with the assurance, of a man who speaks the truth, at least in part, if not wholly—not for conscience’ sake, but through fear. Besides, what was Morcerf seeking? It was not whether Danglars or Monte Cristo was more or less guilty; it was a man who would answer for the offence, whether trifling or serious; it was a man who would fight, and it was evident Danglars would not fight.\n\nIn addition to this, everything forgotten or unperceived before presented itself now to his recollection. Monte Cristo knew everything, as he had bought the daughter of Ali Pasha; and, knowing everything, he had advised Danglars to write to Yanina. The answer known, he had yielded to Albert’s wish to be introduced to Haydée, and allowed the conversation to turn on the death of Ali, and had not opposed Haydée’s recital (but having, doubtless, warned the young girl, in the few Romaic words he spoke to her, not to implicate Morcerf’s father). Besides, had he not begged of Morcerf not to mention his father’s name before Haydée? Lastly, he had taken Albert to Normandy when he knew the final blow was near. There could be no doubt that all had been calculated and previously arranged; Monte Cristo then was in league with his father’s enemies. Albert took Beauchamp aside, and communicated these ideas to him.\n\n“You are right,” said the latter; “M. Danglars has only been a secondary agent in this sad affair, and it is of M. de Monte Cristo that you must demand an explanation.”\n\nAlbert turned.\n\n“Sir,” said he to Danglars, “understand that I do not take a final leave of you; I must ascertain if your insinuations are just, and am going now to inquire of the Count of Monte Cristo.”\n\nHe bowed to the banker, and went out with Beauchamp, without appearing to notice Cavalcanti. Danglars accompanied him to the door, where he again assured Albert that no motive of personal hatred had influenced him against the Count of Morcerf.\n\nAt the banker’s door Beauchamp stopped Morcerf.\n\n“Listen,” said he; “just now I told you it was of M. de Monte Cristo you must demand an explanation.”\n\n“Yes; and we are going to his house.”\n\n“Reflect, Morcerf, one moment before you go.”\n\n“On what shall I reflect?”\n\n“On the importance of the step you are taking.”\n\n“Is it more serious than going to M. Danglars?”\n\n“Yes; M. Danglars is a money-lover, and those who love money, you know, think too much of what they risk to be easily induced to fight a duel. The other is, on the contrary, to all appearance a true nobleman; but do you not fear to find him a bully?”\n\n“I only fear one thing; namely, to find a man who will not fight.”\n\n“Do not be alarmed,” said Beauchamp; “he will meet you. My only fear is that he will be too strong for you.”\n\n“My friend,” said Morcerf, with a sweet smile, “that is what I wish. The happiest thing that could occur to me, would be to die in my father’s stead; that would save us all.”\n\n“Your mother would die of grief.”\n\n“My poor mother!” said Albert, passing his hand across his eyes, “I know she would; but better so than die of shame.”\n\n“Are you quite decided, Albert?”\n\n“Yes; let us go.”\n\n“But do you think we shall find the count at home?”\n\n“He intended returning some hours after me, and doubtless he is now at home.”\n\nThey ordered the driver to take them to No. 30 Champs-Élysées. Beauchamp wished to go in alone, but Albert observed that as this was an unusual circumstance he might be allowed to deviate from the usual etiquette of duels. The cause which the young man espoused was one so sacred that Beauchamp had only to comply with all his wishes; he yielded and contented himself with following Morcerf. Albert sprang from the porter’s lodge to the steps. He was received by Baptistin. The count had, indeed, just arrived, but he was in his bath, and had forbidden that anyone should be admitted.\n\n“But after his bath?” asked Morcerf.\n\n“My master will go to dinner.”\n\n“And after dinner?”\n\n“He will sleep an hour.”\n\n“Then?”\n\n“He is going to the Opera.”\n\n“Are you sure of it?” asked Albert.\n\n“Quite, sir; my master has ordered his horses at eight o’clock precisely.”\n\n“Very good,” replied Albert; “that is all I wished to know.”\n\nThen, turning towards Beauchamp, “If you have anything to attend to, Beauchamp, do it directly; if you have any appointment for this evening, defer it till tomorrow. I depend on you to accompany me to the Opera; and if you can, bring Château-Renaud with you.”\n\nBeauchamp availed himself of Albert’s permission, and left him, promising to call for him at a quarter before eight. On his return home, Albert expressed his wish to Franz Debray, and Morrel, to see them at the Opera that evening. Then he went to see his mother, who since the events of the day before had refused to see anyone, and had kept her room. He found her in bed, overwhelmed with grief at this public humiliation.\n\nThe sight of Albert produced the effect which might naturally be expected on Mercédès; she pressed her son’s hand and sobbed aloud, but her tears relieved her. Albert stood one moment speechless by the side of his mother’s bed. It was evident from his pale face and knit brows that his resolution to revenge himself was growing weaker.\n\n“My dear mother,” said he, “do you know if M. de Morcerf has any enemy?”\n\nMercédès started; she noticed that the young man did not say “my father.”\n\n“My son,” she said, “persons in the count’s situation have many secret enemies. Those who are known are not the most dangerous.”\n\n“I know it, and appeal to your penetration. You are of so superior a mind, nothing escapes you.”\n\n“Why do you say so?”\n\n“Because, for instance, you noticed on the evening of the ball we gave, that M. de Monte Cristo would eat nothing in our house.”\n\nMercédès raised herself on her feverish arm.\n\n“M. de Monte Cristo!” she exclaimed; “and how is he connected with the question you asked me?”\n\n40219m\n“You know, mother, M. de Monte Cristo is almost an Oriental, and it is customary with the Orientals to secure full liberty for revenge by not eating or drinking in the houses of their enemies.”\n\n“Do you say M. de Monte Cristo is our enemy?” replied Mercédès, becoming paler than the sheet which covered her. “Who told you so? Why, you are mad, Albert! M. de Monte Cristo has only shown us kindness. M. de Monte Cristo saved your life; you yourself presented him to us. Oh, I entreat you, my son, if you had entertained such an idea, dispel it; and my counsel to you—nay, my prayer—is to retain his friendship.”\n\n“Mother,” replied the young man, “you have special reasons for telling me to conciliate that man.”\n\n“I?” said Mercédès, blushing as rapidly as she had turned pale, and again becoming paler than ever.\n\n“Yes, doubtless; and is it not that he may never do us any harm?”\n\nMercédès shuddered, and, fixing on her son a scrutinizing gaze, “You speak strangely,” said she to Albert, “and you appear to have some singular prejudices. What has the count done? Three days since you were with him in Normandy; only three days since we looked on him as our best friend.”\n\nAn ironical smile passed over Albert’s lips. Mercédès saw it and with the double instinct of woman and mother guessed all; but as she was prudent and strong-minded she concealed both her sorrows and her fears. Albert was silent; an instant after, the countess resumed:\n\n“You came to inquire after my health; I will candidly acknowledge that I am not well. You should install yourself here, and cheer my solitude. I do not wish to be left alone.”\n\n“Mother,” said the young man, “you know how gladly I would obey your wish, but an urgent and important affair obliges me to leave you for the whole evening.”\n\n“Well,” replied Mercédès, sighing, “go, Albert; I will not make you a slave to your filial piety.”\n\nAlbert pretended he did not hear, bowed to his mother, and quitted her. Scarcely had he shut her door, when Mercédès called a confidential servant, and ordered him to follow Albert wherever he should go that evening, and to come and tell her immediately what he observed. Then she rang for her lady’s maid, and, weak as she was, she dressed, in order to be ready for whatever might happen. The footman’s mission was an easy one. Albert went to his room, and dressed with unusual care. At ten minutes to eight Beauchamp arrived; he had seen Château-Renaud, who had promised to be in the orchestra before the curtain was raised. Both got into Albert’s _coupé_; and, as the young man had no reason to conceal where he was going, he called aloud, “To the Opera.” In his impatience he arrived before the beginning of the performance.\n40221m\n\nChâteau-Renaud was at his post; apprised by Beauchamp of the circumstances, he required no explanation from Albert. The conduct of the son in seeking to avenge his father was so natural that Château-Renaud did not seek to dissuade him, and was content with renewing his assurances of devotion. Debray was not yet come, but Albert knew that he seldom lost a scene at the Opera.\n\nAlbert wandered about the theatre until the curtain was drawn up. He hoped to meet with M. de Monte Cristo either in the lobby or on the stairs. The bell summoned him to his seat, and he entered the orchestra with Château-Renaud and Beauchamp. But his eyes scarcely quitted the box between the columns, which remained obstinately closed during the whole of the first act. At last, as Albert was looking at his watch for about the hundredth time, at the beginning of the second act the door opened, and Monte Cristo entered, dressed in black, and, leaning over the front of the box, looked around the pit. Morrel followed him, and looked also for his sister and brother in-law; he soon discovered them in another box, and kissed his hand to them.\n\nThe count, in his survey of the pit, encountered a pale face and threatening eyes, which evidently sought to gain his attention. He recognized Albert, but thought it better not to notice him, as he looked so angry and discomposed. Without communicating his thoughts to his companion, he sat down, drew out his opera-glass, and looked another way. Although apparently not noticing Albert, he did not, however, lose sight of him, and when the curtain fell at the end of the second act, he saw him leave the orchestra with his two friends. Then his head was seen passing at the back of the boxes, and the count knew that the approaching storm was intended to fall on him. He was at the moment conversing cheerfully with Morrel, but he was well prepared for what might happen.\n\nThe door opened, and Monte Cristo, turning round, saw Albert, pale and trembling, followed by Beauchamp and Château-Renaud.\n\n“Well,” cried he, with that benevolent politeness which distinguished his salutation from the common civilities of the world, “my cavalier has attained his object. Good-evening, M. de Morcerf.”\n\nThe countenance of this man, who possessed such extraordinary control over his feelings, expressed the most perfect cordiality. Morrel only then recollected the letter he had received from the viscount, in which, without assigning any reason, he begged him to go to the Opera, but he understood that something terrible was brooding.\n\n“We are not come here, sir, to exchange hypocritical expressions of politeness, or false professions of friendship,” said Albert, “but to demand an explanation.”\n\nThe young man’s trembling voice was scarcely audible.\n\n“An explanation at the Opera?” said the count, with that calm tone and penetrating eye which characterize the man who knows his cause is good. “Little acquainted as I am with the habits of Parisians, I should not have thought this the place for such a demand.”\n\n“Still, if people will shut themselves up,” said Albert, “and cannot be seen because they are bathing, dining, or asleep, we must avail ourselves of the opportunity whenever they are to be seen.”\n\n“I am not difficult of access, sir; for yesterday, if my memory does not deceive me, you were at my house.”\n\n“Yesterday I was at your house, sir,” said the young man; “because then I knew not who you were.”\n\nIn pronouncing these words Albert had raised his voice so as to be heard by those in the adjoining boxes and in the lobby. Thus the attention of many was attracted by this altercation.\n\n“Where are you come from, sir? “ said Monte Cristo “You do not appear to be in the possession of your senses.”\n\n“Provided I understand your perfidy, sir, and succeed in making you understand that I will be revenged, I shall be reasonable enough,” said Albert furiously.\n\n“I do not understand you, sir,” replied Monte Cristo; “and if I did, your tone is too high. I am at home here, and I alone have a right to raise my voice above another’s. Leave the box, sir!”\n\nMonte Cristo pointed towards the door with the most commanding dignity.\n\n“Ah, I shall know how to make you leave your home!” replied Albert, clasping in his convulsed grasp the glove, which Monte Cristo did not lose sight of.\n\n“Well, well,” said Monte Cristo quietly, “I see you wish to quarrel with me; but I would give you one piece of advice, which you will do well to keep in mind. It is in poor taste to make a display of a challenge. Display is not becoming to everyone, M. de Morcerf.”\n\nAt this name a murmur of astonishment passed around the group of spectators of this scene. They had talked of no one but Morcerf the whole day. Albert understood the allusion in a moment, and was about to throw his glove at the count, when Morrel seized his hand, while Beauchamp and Château-Renaud, fearing the scene would surpass the limits of a challenge, held him back. But Monte Cristo, without rising, and leaning forward in his chair, merely stretched out his arm and, taking the damp, crushed glove from the clenched hand of the young man:\n\n“Sir,” said he in a solemn tone, “I consider your glove thrown, and will return it to you wrapped around a bullet. Now leave me or I will summon my servants to throw you out at the door.”\n\nWild, almost unconscious, and with eyes inflamed, Albert stepped back, and Morrel closed the door. Monte Cristo took up his glass again as if nothing had happened; his face was like marble, and his heart was like bronze. Morrel whispered, “What have you done to him?”\n\n“I? Nothing—at least personally,” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“But there must be some cause for this strange scene.”\n\n“The Count of Morcerf’s adventure exasperates the young man.”\n\n“Have you anything to do with it?”\n\n“It was through Haydée that the Chamber was informed of his father’s treason.”\n\n“Indeed?” said Morrel. “I had been told, but would not credit it, that the Grecian slave I have seen with you here in this very box was the daughter of Ali Pasha.”\n\n“It is true, nevertheless.”\n\n“Then,” said Morrel, “I understand it all, and this scene was premeditated.”\n\n“How so?”\n\n“Yes. Albert wrote to request me to come to the Opera, doubtless that I might be a witness to the insult he meant to offer you.”\n\n“Probably,” said Monte Cristo with his imperturbable tranquillity.\n\n“But what shall you do with him?”\n\n“With whom?”\n\n“With Albert.”\n\n“What shall I do with Albert? As certainly, Maximilian, as I now press your hand, I shall kill him before ten o’clock tomorrow morning.” Morrel, in his turn, took Monte Cristo’s hand in both of his, and he shuddered to feel how cold and steady it was.\n\n“Ah, count,” said he, “his father loves him so much!”\n\n“Do not speak to me of that,” said Monte Cristo, with the first movement of anger he had betrayed; “I will make him suffer.”\n\nMorrel, amazed, let fall Monte Cristo’s hand. “Count, count!” said he.\n\n“Dear Maximilian,” interrupted the count, “listen how adorably Duprez is singing that line,—\n\n‘O Mathilde! idole de mon âme!’\n\n“I was the first to discover Duprez at Naples, and the first to applaud him. Bravo, bravo!”\n\nMorrel saw it was useless to say more, and refrained. The curtain, which had risen at the close of the scene with Albert, again fell, and a rap was heard at the door.\n\n“Come in,” said Monte Cristo with a voice that betrayed not the least emotion; and immediately Beauchamp appeared. “Good-evening, M. Beauchamp,” said Monte Cristo, as if this was the first time he had seen the journalist that evening; “be seated.”\n\nBeauchamp bowed, and, sitting down, “Sir,” said he, “I just now accompanied M. de Morcerf, as you saw.”\n\n“And that means,” replied Monte Cristo, laughing, “that you had, probably, just dined together. I am happy to see, M. Beauchamp, that you are more sober than he was.”\n\n“Sir,” said M. Beauchamp, “Albert was wrong, I acknowledge, to betray so much anger, and I come, on my own account, to apologize for him. And having done so, entirely on my own account, be it understood, I would add that I believe you too gentlemanly to refuse giving him some explanation concerning your connection with Yanina. Then I will add two words about the young Greek girl.”\n\nMonte Cristo motioned him to be silent. “Come,” said he, laughing, “there are all my hopes about to be destroyed.”\n\n“How so?” asked Beauchamp.\n\n“Doubtless you wish to make me appear a very eccentric character. I am, in your opinion, a Lara, a Manfred, a Lord Ruthven; then, just as I am arriving at the climax, you defeat your own end, and seek to make an ordinary man of me. You bring me down to your own level, and demand explanations! Indeed, M. Beauchamp, it is quite laughable.”\n\n“Yet,” replied Beauchamp haughtily, “there are occasions when probity commands——”\n\n“M. Beauchamp,” interposed this strange man, “the Count of Monte Cristo bows to none but the Count of Monte Cristo himself. Say no more, I entreat you. I do what I please, M. Beauchamp, and it is always well done.”\n\n“Sir,” replied the young man, “honest men are not to be paid with such coin. I require honorable guaranties.”\n\n“I am, sir, a living guaranty,” replied Monte Cristo, motionless, but with a threatening look; “we have both blood in our veins which we wish to shed—that is our mutual guaranty. Tell the viscount so, and that tomorrow, before ten o’clock, I shall see what color his is.”\n\n“Then I have only to make arrangements for the duel,” said Beauchamp.\n\n“It is quite immaterial to me,” said Monte Cristo, “and it was very unnecessary to disturb me at the Opera for such a trifle. In France people fight with the sword or pistol, in the colonies with the carbine, in Arabia with the dagger. Tell your client that, although I am the insulted party, in order to carry out my eccentricity, I leave him the choice of arms, and will accept without discussion, without dispute, anything, even combat by drawing lots, which is always stupid, but with me different from other people, as I am sure to gain.”\n\n“Sure to gain!” repeated Beauchamp, looking with amazement at the count.\n\n“Certainly,” said Monte Cristo, slightly shrugging his shoulders; “otherwise I would not fight with M. de Morcerf. I shall kill him—I cannot help it. Only by a single line this evening at my house let me know the arms and the hour; I do not like to be kept waiting.”\n\n“Pistols, then, at eight o’clock, in the Bois de Vincennes,” said Beauchamp, quite disconcerted, not knowing if he was dealing with an arrogant braggadocio or a supernatural being.\n\n“Very well, sir,” said Monte Cristo. “Now all that is settled, do let me see the performance, and tell your friend Albert not to come any more this evening; he will hurt himself with all his ill-chosen barbarisms: let him go home and go to sleep.”\n\nBeauchamp left the box, perfectly amazed.\n\n“Now,” said Monte Cristo, turning towards Morrel, “I may depend upon you, may I not?”\n\n“Certainly,” said Morrel, “I am at your service, count; still——”\n“What?”\n\n“It is desirable I should know the real cause.”\n\n“That is to say, you would rather not?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“The young man himself is acting blindfolded, and knows not the true cause, which is known only to God and to me; but I give you my word, Morrel, that God, who does know it, will be on our side.”\n\n“Enough,” said Morrel; “who is your second witness?”\n\n“I know no one in Paris, Morrel, on whom I could confer that honor besides you and your brother Emmanuel. Do you think Emmanuel would oblige me?”\n\n“I will answer for him, count.”\n\n“Well? that is all I require. Tomorrow morning, at seven o’clock, you will be with me, will you not?”\n\n“We will.”\n\n“Hush, the curtain is rising. Listen! I never lose a note of this opera if I can avoid it; the music of _William Tell_ is so sweet.”\n\n# Chapter 89. The Night\n\nMonte Cristo waited, according to his usual custom, until Duprez had sung his famous “_Suivez-moi!_” then he rose and went out. Morrel took leave of him at the door, renewing his promise to be with him the next morning at seven o’clock, and to bring Emmanuel. Then he stepped into his _coupé_, calm and smiling, and was at home in five minutes. No one who knew the count could mistake his expression when, on entering, he said:\n\n“Ali, bring me my pistols with the ivory cross.”\n\nAli brought the box to his master, who examined the weapons with a solicitude very natural to a man who is about to intrust his life to a little powder and shot. These were pistols of an especial pattern, which Monte Cristo had had made for target practice in his own room. A cap was sufficient to drive out the bullet, and from the adjoining room no one would have suspected that the count was, as sportsmen would say, keeping his hand in.\n\nHe was just taking one up and looking for the point to aim at on a little iron plate which served him as a target, when his study door opened, and Baptistin entered. Before he had spoken a word, the count saw in the next room a veiled woman, who had followed closely after Baptistin, and now, seeing the count with a pistol in his hand and swords on the table, rushed in. Baptistin looked at his master, who made a sign to him, and he went out, closing the door after him.\n\n“Who are you, madame?” said the count to the veiled woman.\n\n40227m\nThe stranger cast one look around her, to be certain that they were quite alone; then bending as if she would have knelt, and joining her hands, she said with an accent of despair:\n\n“Edmond, you will not kill my son!”\n\nThe count retreated a step, uttered a slight exclamation, and let fall the pistol he held.\n\n“What name did you pronounce then, Madame de Morcerf?” said he.\n\n“Yours!” cried she, throwing back her veil,—“yours, which I alone, perhaps, have not forgotten. Edmond, it is not Madame de Morcerf who is come to you, it is Mercédès.”\n\n“Mercédès is dead, madame,” said Monte Cristo; “I know no one now of that name.”\n\n“Mercédès lives, sir, and she remembers, for she alone recognized you when she saw you, and even before she saw you, by your voice, Edmond,—by the simple sound of your voice; and from that moment she has followed your steps, watched you, feared you, and she needs not to inquire what hand has dealt the blow which now strikes M. de Morcerf.”\n\n“Fernand, do you mean?” replied Monte Cristo, with bitter irony; “since we are recalling names, let us remember them all.” Monte Cristo had pronounced the name of Fernand with such an expression of hatred that Mercédès felt a thrill of horror run through every vein.\n\n“You see, Edmond, I am not mistaken, and have cause to say, ‘Spare my son!’”\n\n“And who told you, madame, that I have any hostile intentions against your son?”\n\n“No one, in truth; but a mother has twofold sight. I guessed all; I followed him this evening to the Opera, and, concealed in a parquet box, have seen all.”\n\n“If you have seen all, madame, you know that the son of Fernand has publicly insulted me,” said Monte Cristo with awful calmness.\n\n“Oh, for pity’s sake!”\n\n“You have seen that he would have thrown his glove in my face if Morrel, one of my friends, had not stopped him.”\n\n“Listen to me, my son has also guessed who you are,—he attributes his father’s misfortunes to you.”\n\n“Madame, you are mistaken, they are not misfortunes,—it is a punishment. It is not I who strike M. de Morcerf; it is Providence which punishes him.”\n\n“And why do you represent Providence?” cried Mercédès. “Why do you remember when it forgets? What are Yanina and its vizier to you, Edmond? What injury has Fernand Mondego done you in betraying Ali Tepelini?”\n\n“Ah, madame,” replied Monte Cristo, “all this is an affair between the French captain and the daughter of Vasiliki. It does not concern me, you are right; and if I have sworn to revenge myself, it is not on the French captain, or the Count of Morcerf, but on the fisherman Fernand, the husband of Mercédès the Catalane.”\n\n“Ah, sir!” cried the countess, “how terrible a vengeance for a fault which fatality made me commit!—for I am the only culprit, Edmond, and if you owe revenge to anyone, it is to me, who had not fortitude to bear your absence and my solitude.”\n\n“But,” exclaimed Monte Cristo, “why was I absent? And why were you alone?”\n40231m\n\n“Because you had been arrested, Edmond, and were a prisoner.”\n\n“And why was I arrested? Why was I a prisoner?”\n\n“I do not know,” said Mercédès.\n\n“You do not, madame; at least, I hope not. But I will tell you. I was arrested and became a prisoner because, under the arbor of La Réserve, the day before I was to marry you, a man named Danglars wrote this letter, which the fisherman Fernand himself posted.”\n\nMonte Cristo went to a secretaire, opened a drawer by a spring, from which he took a paper which had lost its original color, and the ink of which had become of a rusty hue—this he placed in the hands of Mercédès. It was Danglars’ letter to the king’s attorney, which the Count of Monte Cristo, disguised as a clerk from the house of Thomson & French, had taken from the file against Edmond Dantès, on the day he had paid the two hundred thousand francs to M. de Boville. Mercédès read with terror the following lines:\n\n“The king’s attorney is informed by a friend to the throne and religion that one Edmond Dantès, second in command on board the _Pharaon_, this day arrived from Smyrna, after having touched at Naples and Porto-Ferrajo, is the bearer of a letter from Murat to the usurper, and of another letter from the usurper to the Bonapartist club in Paris. Ample corroboration of this statement may be obtained by arresting the above-mentioned Edmond Dantès, who either carries the letter for Paris about with him, or has it at his father’s abode. Should it not be found in possession of either father or son, then it will assuredly be discovered in the cabin belonging to the said Dantès on board the _Pharaon_.”\n\n“How dreadful!” said Mercédès, passing her hand across her brow, moist with perspiration; “and that letter——”\n\n“I bought it for two hundred thousand francs, madame,” said Monte Cristo; “but that is a trifle, since it enables me to justify myself to you.”\n\n“And the result of that letter——”\n\n“You well know, madame, was my arrest; but you do not know how long that arrest lasted. You do not know that I remained for fourteen years within a quarter of a league of you, in a dungeon in the Château d’If. You do not know that every day of those fourteen years I renewed the vow of vengeance which I had made the first day; and yet I was not aware that you had married Fernand, my calumniator, and that my father had died of hunger!”\n\n“Can it be?” cried Mercédès, shuddering.\n\n“That is what I heard on leaving my prison fourteen years after I had entered it; and that is why, on account of the living Mercédès and my deceased father, I have sworn to revenge myself on Fernand, and—I have revenged myself.”\n\n“And you are sure the unhappy Fernand did that?”\n\n“I am satisfied, madame, that he did what I have told you; besides, that is not much more odious than that a Frenchman by adoption should pass over to the English; that a Spaniard by birth should have fought against the Spaniards; that a stipendiary of Ali should have betrayed and murdered Ali. Compared with such things, what is the letter you have just read?—a lover’s deception, which the woman who has married that man ought certainly to forgive; but not so the lover who was to have married her. Well, the French did not avenge themselves on the traitor, the Spaniards did not shoot the traitor, Ali in his tomb left the traitor unpunished; but I, betrayed, sacrificed, buried, have risen from my tomb, by the grace of God, to punish that man. He sends me for that purpose, and here I am.”\n\nThe poor woman’s head and arms fell; her legs bent under her, and she fell on her knees.\n\n“Forgive, Edmond, forgive for my sake, who love you still!”\n\nThe dignity of the wife checked the fervor of the lover and the mother. Her forehead almost touched the carpet, when the count sprang forward and raised her. Then seated on a chair, she looked at the manly countenance of Monte Cristo, on which grief and hatred still impressed a threatening expression.\n\n“Not crush that accursed race?” murmured he; “abandon my purpose at the moment of its accomplishment? Impossible, madame, impossible!”\n\n“Edmond,” said the poor mother, who tried every means, “when I call you Edmond, why do you not call me Mercédès?”\n\n“Mercédès!” repeated Monte Cristo; “Mercédès! Well yes, you are right; that name has still its charms, and this is the first time for a long period that I have pronounced it so distinctly. Oh, Mercédès, I have uttered your name with the sigh of melancholy, with the groan of sorrow, with the last effort of despair; I have uttered it when frozen with cold, crouched on the straw in my dungeon; I have uttered it, consumed with heat, rolling on the stone floor of my prison. Mercédès, I must revenge myself, for I suffered fourteen years,—fourteen years I wept, I cursed; now I tell you, Mercédès, I must revenge myself.”\n\nThe count, fearing to yield to the entreaties of her he had so ardently loved, called his sufferings to the assistance of his hatred.\n\n“Revenge yourself, then, Edmond,” cried the poor mother; “but let your vengeance fall on the culprits,—on him, on me, but not on my son!”\n\n“It is written in the good book,” said Monte Cristo, “that the sins of the fathers shall fall upon their children to the third and fourth generation. Since God himself dictated those words to his prophet, why should I seek to make myself better than God?”\n\n“Edmond,” continued Mercédès, with her arms extended towards the count, “since I first knew you, I have adored your name, have respected your memory. Edmond, my friend, do not compel me to tarnish that noble and pure image reflected incessantly on the mirror of my heart. Edmond, if you knew all the prayers I have addressed to God for you while I thought you were living and since I have thought you must be dead! Yes, dead, alas! I imagined your dead body buried at the foot of some gloomy tower, or cast to the bottom of a pit by hateful jailers, and I wept! What could I do for you, Edmond, besides pray and weep? Listen; for ten years I dreamed each night the same dream. I had been told that you had endeavored to escape; that you had taken the place of another prisoner; that you had slipped into the winding sheet of a dead body; that you had been thrown alive from the top of the Château d’If, and that the cry you uttered as you dashed upon the rocks first revealed to your jailers that they were your murderers. Well, Edmond, I swear to you, by the head of that son for whom I entreat your pity,—Edmond, for ten years I saw every night every detail of that frightful tragedy, and for ten years I heard every night the cry which awoke me, shuddering and cold. And I, too, Edmond—oh! believe me—guilty as I was—oh, yes, I, too, have suffered much!”\n\n“Have you known what it is to have your father starve to death in your absence?” cried Monte Cristo, thrusting his hands into his hair; “have you seen the woman you loved giving her hand to your rival, while you were perishing at the bottom of a dungeon?”\n\n“No,” interrupted Mercédès, “but I have seen him whom I loved on the point of murdering my son.”\n\nMercédès uttered these words with such deep anguish, with an accent of such intense despair, that Monte Cristo could not restrain a sob. The lion was daunted; the avenger was conquered.\n\n“What do you ask of me?” said he,—“your son’s life? Well, he shall live!”\n\nMercédès uttered a cry which made the tears start from Monte Cristo’s eyes; but these tears disappeared almost instantaneously, for, doubtless, God had sent some angel to collect them—far more precious were they in his eyes than the richest pearls of Guzerat and Ophir.\n\n“Oh,” said she, seizing the count’s hand and raising it to her lips; “oh, thank you, thank you, Edmond! Now you are exactly what I dreamt you were,—the man I always loved. Oh, now I may say so!”\n\n“So much the better,” replied Monte Cristo; “as that poor Edmond will not have long to be loved by you. Death is about to return to the tomb, the phantom to retire in darkness.”\n\n“What do you say, Edmond?”\n\n“I say, since you command me, Mercédès, I must die.”\n\n“Die? and why so? Who talks of dying? Whence have you these ideas of death?”\n\n“You do not suppose that, publicly outraged in the face of a whole theatre, in the presence of your friends and those of your son—challenged by a boy who will glory in my forgiveness as if it were a victory—you do not suppose that I can for one moment wish to live. What I most loved after you, Mercédès, was myself, my dignity, and that strength which rendered me superior to other men; that strength was my life. With one word you have crushed it, and I die.”\n\n“But the duel will not take place, Edmond, since you forgive?”\n\n“It will take place,” said Monte Cristo, in a most solemn tone; “but instead of your son’s blood to stain the ground, mine will flow.”\n\nMercédès shrieked, and sprang towards Monte Cristo, but, suddenly stopping, “Edmond,” said she, “there is a God above us, since you live and since I have seen you again; I trust to him from my heart. While waiting his assistance I trust to your word; you have said that my son should live, have you not?”\n\n“Yes, madame, he shall live,” said Monte Cristo, surprised that without more emotion Mercédès had accepted the heroic sacrifice he made for her. Mercédès extended her hand to the count.\n\n“Edmond,” said she, and her eyes were wet with tears while looking at him to whom she spoke, “how noble it is of you, how great the action you have just performed, how sublime to have taken pity on a poor woman who appealed to you with every chance against her, Alas, I am grown old with grief more than with years, and cannot now remind my Edmond by a smile, or by a look, of that Mercédès whom he once spent so many hours in contemplating. Ah, believe me, Edmond, as I told you, I too have suffered much; I repeat, it is melancholy to pass one’s life without having one joy to recall, without preserving a single hope; but that proves that all is not yet over. No, it is not finished; I feel it by what remains in my heart. Oh, I repeat it, Edmond; what you have just done is beautiful—it is grand; it is sublime.”\n\n“Do you say so now, Mercédès?—then what would you say if you knew the extent of the sacrifice I make to you? Suppose that the Supreme Being, after having created the world and fertilized chaos, had paused in the work to spare an angel the tears that might one day flow for mortal sins from her immortal eyes; suppose that when everything was in readiness and the moment had come for God to look upon his work and see that it was good—suppose he had snuffed out the sun and tossed the world back into eternal night—then—even then, Mercédès, you could not imagine what I lose in sacrificing my life at this moment.”\n\nMercédès looked at the count in a way which expressed at the same time her astonishment, her admiration, and her gratitude. Monte Cristo pressed his forehead on his burning hands, as if his brain could no longer bear alone the weight of its thoughts.\n\n“Edmond,” said Mercédès, “I have but one word more to say to you.”\n\nThe count smiled bitterly.\n\n“Edmond,” continued she, “you will see that if my face is pale, if my eyes are dull, if my beauty is gone; if Mercédès, in short, no longer resembles her former self in her features, you will see that her heart is still the same. Adieu, then, Edmond; I have nothing more to ask of heaven—I have seen you again, and have found you as noble and as great as formerly you were. Adieu, Edmond, adieu, and thank you.”\n\nBut the count did not answer. Mercédès opened the door of the study and had disappeared before he had recovered from the painful and profound reverie into which his thwarted vengeance had plunged him.\n\nThe clock of the Invalides struck one when the carriage which conveyed Madame de Morcerf rolled away on the pavement of the Champs-Élysées, and made Monte Cristo raise his head.\n\n“What a fool I was,” said he, “not to tear my heart out on the day when I resolved to avenge myself!”\n\n# Chapter 90. The Meeting\n\nAfter Mercédès had left Monte Cristo, he fell into profound gloom. Around him and within him the flight of thought seemed to have stopped; his energetic mind slumbered, as the body does after extreme fatigue.\n\n“What?” said he to himself, while the lamp and the wax lights were nearly burnt out, and the servants were waiting impatiently in the anteroom; “what? this edifice which I have been so long preparing, which I have reared with so much care and toil, is to be crushed by a single touch, a word, a breath! Yes, this self, of whom I thought so much, of whom I was so proud, who had appeared so worthless in the dungeons of the Château d’If, and whom I had succeeded in making so great, will be but a lump of clay tomorrow. Alas, it is not the death of the body I regret; for is not the destruction of the vital principle, the repose to which everything is tending, to which every unhappy being aspires,—is not this the repose of matter after which I so long sighed, and which I was seeking to attain by the painful process of starvation when Faria appeared in my dungeon? What is death for me? One step farther into rest,—two, perhaps, into silence. No, it is not existence, then, that I regret, but the ruin of projects so slowly carried out, so laboriously framed. Providence is now opposed to them, when I most thought it would be propitious. It is not God’s will that they should be accomplished. This burden, almost as heavy as a world, which I had raised, and I had thought to bear to the end, was too great for my strength, and I was compelled to lay it down in the middle of my career. Oh, shall I then, again become a fatalist, whom fourteen years of despair and ten of hope had rendered a believer in Providence?\n\n“And all this—all this, because my heart, which I thought dead, was only sleeping; because it has awakened and has begun to beat again, because I have yielded to the pain of the emotion excited in my breast by a woman’s voice.\n\n“Yet,” continued the count, becoming each moment more absorbed in the anticipation of the dreadful sacrifice for the morrow, which Mercédès had accepted, “yet, it is impossible that so noble-minded a woman should thus through selfishness consent to my death when I am in the prime of life and strength; it is impossible that she can carry to such a point maternal love, or rather delirium. There are virtues which become crimes by exaggeration. No, she must have conceived some pathetic scene; she will come and throw herself between us; and what would be sublime here will there appear ridiculous.”\n\nThe blush of pride mounted to the count’s forehead as this thought passed through his mind.\n\n“Ridiculous?” repeated he; “and the ridicule will fall on me. I ridiculous? No, I would rather die.”\n\nBy thus exaggerating to his own mind the anticipated ill-fortune of the next day, to which he had condemned himself by promising Mercédès to spare her son, the count at last exclaimed:\n\n“Folly, folly, folly!—to carry generosity so far as to put myself up as a mark for that young man to aim at. He will never believe that my death was suicide; and yet it is important for the honor of my memory,—and this surely is not vanity, but a justifiable pride,—it is important the world should know that I have consented, by my free will, to stop my arm, already raised to strike, and that with the arm which has been so powerful against others I have struck myself. It must be; it shall be.”\n\nSeizing a pen, he drew a paper from a secret drawer in his desk, and wrote at the bottom of the document (which was no other than his will, made since his arrival in Paris) a sort of codicil, clearly explaining the nature of his death.\n\n“I do this, Oh, my God,” said he, with his eyes raised to heaven, “as much for thy honor as for mine. I have during ten years considered myself the agent of thy vengeance, and other wretches, like Morcerf, Danglars, Villefort, even Morcerf himself, must not imagine that chance has freed them from their enemy. Let them know, on the contrary, that their punishment, which had been decreed by Providence, is only delayed by my present determination, and although they escape it in this world, it awaits them in another, and that they are only exchanging time for eternity.”\n\nWhile he was thus agitated by gloomy uncertainties,—wretched waking dreams of grief,—the first rays of morning pierced his windows, and shone upon the pale blue paper on which he had just inscribed his justification of Providence.\n\nIt was just five o’clock in the morning when a slight noise like a stifled sigh reached his ear. He turned his head, looked around him, and saw no one; but the sound was repeated distinctly enough to convince him of its reality.\n\nHe arose, and quietly opening the door of the drawing-room, saw Haydée, who had fallen on a chair, with her arms hanging down and her beautiful head thrown back. She had been standing at the door, to prevent his going out without seeing her, until sleep, which the young cannot resist, had overpowered her frame, wearied as she was with watching. The noise of the door did not awaken her, and Monte Cristo gazed at her with affectionate regret.\n\n“She remembered that she had a son,” said he; “and I forgot I had a daughter.” Then, shaking his head sorrowfully, “Poor Haydée,” said he; “she wished to see me, to speak to me; she has feared or guessed something. Oh, I cannot go without taking leave of her; I cannot die without confiding her to someone.”\n\nHe quietly regained his seat, and wrote under the other lines:\n\n“I bequeath to Maximilian Morrel, captain of Spahis,—and son of my former patron, Pierre Morrel, shipowner at Marseilles,—the sum of twenty millions, a part of which may be offered to his sister Julie and brother-in-law Emmanuel, if he does not fear this increase of fortune may mar their happiness. These twenty millions are concealed in my grotto at Monte Cristo, of which Bertuccio knows the secret. If his heart is free, and he will marry Haydée, the daughter of Ali Pasha of Yanina, whom I have brought up with the love of a father, and who has shown the love and tenderness of a daughter for me, he will thus accomplish my last wish. This will has already constituted Haydée heiress of the rest of my fortune, consisting of lands, funds in England, Austria, and Holland, furniture in my different palaces and houses, and which without the twenty millions and the legacies to my servants, may still amount to sixty millions.”\n40241m\n\nHe was finishing the last line when a cry behind him made him start, and the pen fell from his hand.\n\n“Haydée,” said he, “did you read it?”\n\n“Oh, my lord,” said she, “why are you writing thus at such an hour? Why are you bequeathing all your fortune to me? Are you going to leave me?”\n\n“I am going on a journey, dear child,” said Monte Cristo, with an expression of infinite tenderness and melancholy; “and if any misfortune should happen to me——”\n\nThe count stopped.\n\n“Well?” asked the young girl, with an authoritative tone the count had never observed before, and which startled him.\n\n“Well, if any misfortune happen to me,” replied Monte Cristo, “I wish my daughter to be happy.” Haydée smiled sorrowfully, and shook her head.\n\n“Do you think of dying, my lord?” said she.\n\n“The wise man, my child, has said, ‘It is good to think of death.’”\n\n“Well, if you die,” said she, “bequeath your fortune to others, for if you die I shall require nothing;” and, taking the paper, she tore it in four pieces, and threw it into the middle of the room. Then, the effort having exhausted her strength, she fell, not asleep this time, but fainting on the floor.\n\nThe count leaned over her and raised her in his arms; and seeing that sweet pale face, those lovely eyes closed, that beautiful form motionless and to all appearance lifeless, the idea occurred to him for the first time, that perhaps she loved him otherwise than as a daughter loves a father.\n\n“Alas,” murmured he, with intense suffering, “I might, then, have been happy yet.”\n\nThen he carried Haydée to her room, resigned her to the care of her attendants, and returning to his study, which he shut quickly this time, he again copied the destroyed will. As he was finishing, the sound of a cabriolet entering the yard was heard. Monte Cristo approached the window, and saw Maximilian and Emmanuel alight. “Good,” said he; “it was time,”—and he sealed his will with three seals.\n\nA moment afterwards he heard a noise in the drawing-room, and went to open the door himself. Morrel was there; he had come twenty minutes before the time appointed.\n\n“I am perhaps come too soon, count,” said he, “but I frankly acknowledge that I have not closed my eyes all night, nor has anyone in my house. I need to see you strong in your courageous assurance, to recover myself.”\n\nMonte Cristo could not resist this proof of affection; he not only extended his hand to the young man, but flew to him with open arms.\n\n“Morrel,” said he, “it is a happy day for me, to feel that I am beloved by such a man as you. Good-morning, Emmanuel; you will come with me then, Maximilian?”\n\n“Did you doubt it?” said the young captain.\n\n“But if I were wrong——”\n\n“I watched you during the whole scene of that challenge yesterday; I have been thinking of your firmness all night, and I said to myself that justice must be on your side, or man’s countenance is no longer to be relied on.”\n\n“But, Morrel, Albert is your friend?”\n\n“Simply an acquaintance, sir.”\n\n“You met on the same day you first saw me?”\n\n“Yes, that is true; but I should not have recollected it if you had not reminded me.”\n\n“Thank you, Morrel.” Then ringing the bell once, “Look.” said he to Ali, who came immediately, “take that to my solicitor. It is my will, Morrel. When I am dead, you will go and examine it.”\n\n“What?” said Morrel, “you dead?”\n\n“Yes; must I not be prepared for everything, dear friend? But what did you do yesterday after you left me?”\n\n“I went to Tortoni’s, where, as I expected, I found Beauchamp and Château-Renaud. I own I was seeking them.”\n\n“Why, when all was arranged?”\n\n“Listen, count; the affair is serious and unavoidable.”\n\n“Did you doubt it!”\n\n“No; the offence was public, and everyone is already talking of it.”\n\n“Well?”\n\n“Well, I hoped to get an exchange of arms,—to substitute the sword for the pistol; the pistol is blind.”\n\n“Have you succeeded?” asked Monte Cristo quickly, with an imperceptible gleam of hope.\n\n“No; for your skill with the sword is so well known.”\n\n“Ah?—who has betrayed me?”\n\n“The skilful swordsman whom you have conquered.”\n\n“And you failed?”\n\n“They positively refused.”\n\n“Morrel,” said the count, “have you ever seen me fire a pistol?”\n“Never.”\n\n“Well, we have time; look.” Monte Cristo took the pistols he held in his hand when Mercédès entered, and fixing an ace of clubs against the iron plate, with four shots he successively shot off the four sides of the club. At each shot Morrel turned pale. He examined the bullets with which Monte Cristo performed this dexterous feat, and saw that they were no larger than buckshot.\n\n“It is astonishing,” said he. “Look, Emmanuel.” Then turning towards Monte Cristo, “Count,” said he, “in the name of all that is dear to you, I entreat you not to kill Albert!—the unhappy youth has a mother.”\n\n“You are right,” said Monte Cristo; “and I have none.” These words were uttered in a tone which made Morrel shudder.\n\n“You are the offended party, count.”\n\n“Doubtless; what does that imply?”\n\n“That you will fire first.”\n\n“I fire first?”\n\n“Oh, I obtained, or rather claimed that; we had conceded enough for them to yield us that.”\n\n“And at what distance?”\n\n“Twenty paces.” A smile of terrible import passed over the count’s lips.\n\n“Morrel,” said he, “do not forget what you have just seen.”\n\n“The only chance for Albert’s safety, then, will arise from your emotion.”\n\n“I suffer from emotion?” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“Or from your generosity, my friend; to so good a marksman as you are, I may say what would appear absurd to another.”\n\n“What is that?”\n\n“Break his arm—wound him—but do not kill him.”\n\n“I will tell you, Morrel,” said the count, “that I do not need entreating to spare the life of M. de Morcerf; he shall be so well spared, that he will return quietly with his two friends, while I——”\n\n“And you?”\n\n“That will be another thing; I shall be brought home.”\n\n“No, no,” cried Maximilian, quite unable to restrain his feelings.\n\n“As I told you, my dear Morrel, M. de Morcerf will kill me.”\n\nMorrel looked at him in utter amazement. “But what has happened, then, since last evening, count?”\n\n“The same thing that happened to Brutus the night before the battle of Philippi; I have seen a ghost.”\n\n“And that ghost——”\n\n“Told me, Morrel, that I had lived long enough.”\n\nMaximilian and Emmanuel looked at each other. Monte Cristo drew out his watch. “Let us go,” said he; “it is five minutes past seven, and the appointment was for eight o’clock.”\n\nA carriage was in readiness at the door. Monte Cristo stepped into it with his two friends. He had stopped a moment in the passage to listen at a door, and Maximilian and Emmanuel, who had considerately passed forward a few steps, thought they heard him answer by a sigh to a sob from within. As the clock struck eight they drove up to the place of meeting.\n\n“We are first,” said Morrel, looking out of the window.\n\n“Excuse me, sir,” said Baptistin, who had followed his master with indescribable terror, “but I think I see a carriage down there under the trees.”\n\nMonte Cristo sprang lightly from the carriage, and offered his hand to assist Emmanuel and Maximilian. The latter retained the count’s hand between his.\n\n“I like,” said he, “to feel a hand like this, when its owner relies on the goodness of his cause.”\n\n“It seems to me,” said Emmanuel, “that I see two young men down there, who are evidently, waiting.”\n\nMonte Cristo drew Morrel a step or two behind his brother-in-law.\n\n“Maximilian,” said he, “are your affections disengaged?” Morrel looked at Monte Cristo with astonishment. “I do not seek your confidence, my dear friend. I only ask you a simple question; answer it;—that is all I require.”\n\n“I love a young girl, count.”\n\n“Do you love her much?”\n\n“More than my life.”\n\n“Another hope defeated!” said the count. Then, with a sigh, “Poor Haydée!” murmured he.\n\n“To tell the truth, count, if I knew less of you, I should think that you were less brave than you are.”\n\n“Because I sigh when thinking of someone I am leaving? Come, Morrel, it is not like a soldier to be so bad a judge of courage. Do I regret life? What is it to me, who have passed twenty years between life and death? Moreover, do not alarm yourself, Morrel; this weakness, if it is such, is betrayed to you alone. I know the world is a drawing-room, from which we must retire politely and honestly; that is, with a bow, and our debts of honor paid.”\n\n“That is to the purpose. Have you brought your arms?”\n\n“I?—what for? I hope these gentlemen have theirs.”\n\n“I will inquire,” said Morrel.\n\n“Do; but make no treaty—you understand me?”\n\n“You need not fear.” Morrel advanced towards Beauchamp and Château-Renaud, who, seeing his intention, came to meet him. The three young men bowed to each other courteously, if not affably.\n\n“Excuse me, gentlemen,” said Morrel, “but I do not see M. de Morcerf.”\n\n“He sent us word this morning,” replied Château-Renaud, “that he would meet us on the ground.”\n\n“Ah,” said Morrel. Beauchamp pulled out his watch.\n\n“It is only five minutes past eight,” said he to Morrel; “there is not much time lost yet.”\n\n“Oh, I made no allusion of that kind,” replied Morrel.\n\n“There is a carriage coming,” said Château-Renaud. It advanced rapidly along one of the avenues leading towards the open space where they were assembled.\n\n“You are doubtless provided with pistols, gentlemen? M. de Monte Cristo yields his right of using his.”\n\n“We had anticipated this kindness on the part of the count,” said Beauchamp, “and I have brought some weapons which I bought eight or ten days since, thinking to want them on a similar occasion. They are quite new, and have not yet been used. Will you examine them.”\n\n“Oh, M. Beauchamp, if you assure me that M. de Morcerf does not know these pistols, you may readily believe that your word will be quite sufficient.”\n\n“Gentlemen,” said Château-Renaud, “it is not Morcerf coming in that carriage;—faith, it is Franz and Debray!”\n\nThe two young men he announced were indeed approaching. “What chance brings you here, gentlemen?” said Château-Renaud, shaking hands with each of them.\n\n“Because,” said Debray, “Albert sent this morning to request us to come.” Beauchamp and Château-Renaud exchanged looks of astonishment. “I think I understand his reason,” said Morrel.\n\n“What is it?”\n\n“Yesterday afternoon I received a letter from M. de Morcerf, begging me to attend the Opera.”\n\n“And I,” said Debray.\n\n“And I also,” said Franz.\n\n“And we, too,” added Beauchamp and Château-Renaud.\n\n“Having wished you all to witness the challenge, he now wishes you to be present at the combat.”\n\n“Exactly so,” said the young men; “you have probably guessed right.”\n\n“But, after all these arrangements, he does not come himself,” said Château-Renaud. “Albert is ten minutes after time.”\n\n“There he comes,” said Beauchamp, “on horseback, at full gallop, followed by a servant.”\n\n“How imprudent,” said Château-Renaud, “to come on horseback to fight a duel with pistols, after all the instructions I had given him.”\n\n“And besides,” said Beauchamp, “with a collar above his cravat, an open coat and white waistcoat! Why has he not painted a spot upon his heart?—it would have been more simple.”\n\nMeanwhile Albert had arrived within ten paces of the group formed by the five young men. He jumped from his horse, threw the bridle on his servant’s arms, and joined them. He was pale, and his eyes were red and swollen; it was evident that he had not slept. A shade of melancholy gravity overspread his countenance, which was not natural to him.\n\n“I thank you, gentlemen,” said he, “for having complied with my request; I feel extremely grateful for this mark of friendship.” Morrel had stepped back as Morcerf approached, and remained at a short distance. “And to you also, M. Morrel, my thanks are due. Come, there cannot be too many.”\n40248m\n\n“Sir,” said Maximilian, “you are not perhaps aware that I am M. de Monte Cristo’s friend?”\n\n“I was not sure, but I thought it might be so. So much the better; the more honorable men there are here the better I shall be satisfied.”\n\n“M. Morrel,” said Château-Renaud, “will you apprise the Count of Monte Cristo that M. de Morcerf is arrived, and we are at his disposal?”\n\nMorrel was preparing to fulfil his commission. Beauchamp had meanwhile drawn the box of pistols from the carriage.\n\n“Stop, gentlemen,” said Albert; “I have two words to say to the Count of Monte Cristo.”\n\n“In private?” asked Morrel.\n\n“No, sir; before all who are here.”\n\nAlbert’s witnesses looked at each other. Franz and Debray exchanged some words in a whisper, and Morrel, rejoiced at this unexpected incident, went to fetch the count, who was walking in a retired path with Emmanuel.\n\n“What does he want with me?” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“I do not know, but he wishes to speak to you.”\n\n“Ah?” said Monte Cristo, “I trust he is not going to tempt me by some fresh insult!”\n\n“I do not think that such is his intention,” said Morrel.\n\nThe count advanced, accompanied by Maximilian and Emmanuel. His calm and serene look formed a singular contrast to Albert’s grief-stricken face, who approached also, followed by the other four young men.\n\nWhen at three paces distant from each other, Albert and the count stopped.\n\n“Approach, gentlemen,” said Albert; “I wish you not to lose one word of what I am about to have the honor of saying to the Count of Monte Cristo, for it must be repeated by you to all who will listen to it, strange as it may appear to you.”\n\n“Proceed, sir,” said the count.\n\n“Sir,” said Albert, at first with a tremulous voice, but which gradually became firmer, “I reproached you with exposing the conduct of M. de Morcerf in Epirus, for guilty as I knew he was, I thought you had no right to punish him; but I have since learned that you had that right. It is not Fernand Mondego’s treachery towards Ali Pasha which induces me so readily to excuse you, but the treachery of the fisherman Fernand towards you, and the almost unheard-of miseries which were its consequences; and I say, and proclaim it publicly, that you were justified in revenging yourself on my father, and I, his son, thank you for not using greater severity.”\n\nHad a thunderbolt fallen in the midst of the spectators of this unexpected scene, it would not have surprised them more than did Albert’s declaration. As for Monte Cristo, his eyes slowly rose towards heaven with an expression of infinite gratitude. He could not understand how Albert’s fiery nature, of which he had seen so much among the Roman bandits, had suddenly stooped to this humiliation. He recognized the influence of Mercédès, and saw why her noble heart had not opposed the sacrifice she knew beforehand would be useless.\n\n“Now, sir,” said Albert, “if you think my apology sufficient, pray give me your hand. Next to the merit of infallibility which you appear to possess, I rank that of candidly acknowledging a fault. But this confession concerns me only. I acted well as a man, but you have acted better than man. An angel alone could have saved one of us from death—that angel came from heaven, if not to make us friends (which, alas, fatality renders impossible), at least to make us esteem each other.”\n\nMonte Cristo, with moistened eye, heaving breast, and lips half open, extended to Albert a hand which the latter pressed with a sentiment resembling respectful fear.\n\n“Gentlemen,” said he, “M. de Monte Cristo receives my apology. I had acted hastily towards him. Hasty actions are generally bad ones. Now my fault is repaired. I hope the world will not call me cowardly for acting as my conscience dictated. But if anyone should entertain a false opinion of me,” added he, drawing himself up as if he would challenge both friends and enemies, “I shall endeavor to correct his mistake.”\n\n“What happened during the night?” asked Beauchamp of Château-Renaud; “we appear to make a very sorry figure here.”\n\n“In truth, what Albert has just done is either very despicable or very noble,” replied the baron.\n\n“What can it mean?” said Debray to Franz.\n“The Count of Monte Cristo acts dishonorably to M. de Morcerf, and is justified by his son! Had I ten Yaninas in my family, I should only consider myself the more bound to fight ten times.”\n\nAs for Monte Cristo, his head was bent down, his arms were powerless. Bowing under the weight of twenty-four years’ reminiscences, he thought not of Albert, of Beauchamp, of Château-Renaud, or of any of that group; but he thought of that courageous woman who had come to plead for her son’s life, to whom he had offered his, and who had now saved it by the revelation of a dreadful family secret, capable of destroying forever in that young man’s heart every feeling of filial piety.\n\n“Providence still,” murmured he; “now only am I fully convinced of being the emissary of God!”\n\n# Chapter 91. Mother and Son\n\nThe Count of Monte Cristo bowed to the five young men with a melancholy and dignified smile, and got into his carriage with Maximilian and Emmanuel. Albert, Beauchamp, and Château-Renaud remained alone. Albert looked at his two friends, not timidly, but in a way that appeared to ask their opinion of what he had just done.\n\n“Indeed, my dear friend,” said Beauchamp first, who had either the most feeling or the least dissimulation, “allow me to congratulate you; this is a very unhoped-for conclusion of a very disagreeable affair.”\n\nAlbert remained silent and wrapped in thought. Château-Renaud contented himself with tapping his boot with his flexible cane.\n\n“Are we not going?” said he, after this embarrassing silence.\n\n“When you please,” replied Beauchamp; “allow me only to compliment M. de Morcerf, who has given proof today of rare chivalric generosity.”\n\n“Oh, yes,” said Château-Renaud.\n\n“It is magnificent,” continued Beauchamp, “to be able to exercise so much self-control!”\n\n“Assuredly; as for me, I should have been incapable of it,” said Château-Renaud, with most significant coolness.\n\n“Gentlemen,” interrupted Albert, “I think you did not understand that something very serious had passed between M. de Monte Cristo and myself.”\n\n“Possibly, possibly,” said Beauchamp immediately; “but every simpleton would not be able to understand your heroism, and sooner or later you will find yourself compelled to explain it to them more energetically than would be convenient to your bodily health and the duration of your life. May I give you a friendly counsel? Set out for Naples, the Hague, or St. Petersburg—calm countries, where the point of honor is better understood than among our hot-headed Parisians. Seek quietude and oblivion, so that you may return peaceably to France after a few years. Am I not right, M. de Château-Renaud?”\n40252m\n\n“That is quite my opinion,” said the gentleman; “nothing induces serious duels so much as a duel forsworn.”\n\n“Thank you, gentlemen,” replied Albert, with a smile of indifference; “I shall follow your advice—not because you give it, but because I had before intended to quit France. I thank you equally for the service you have rendered me in being my seconds. It is deeply engraved on my heart, and, after what you have just said, I remember that only.”\n\nChâteau-Renaud and Beauchamp looked at each other; the impression was the same on both of them, and the tone in which Morcerf had just expressed his thanks was so determined that the position would have become embarrassing for all if the conversation had continued.\n\n“Good-bye, Albert,” said Beauchamp suddenly, carelessly extending his hand to the young man. The latter did not appear to arouse from his lethargy; in fact, he did not notice the offered hand.\n\n“Good-bye,” said Château-Renaud in his turn, keeping his little cane in his left hand, and saluting with his right.\n\nAlbert’s lips scarcely whispered “Good-bye,” but his look was more explicit; it expressed a whole poem of restrained anger, proud disdain, and generous indignation. He preserved his melancholy and motionless position for some time after his two friends had regained their carriage; then suddenly unfastening his horse from the little tree to which his servant had tied it, he mounted and galloped off in the direction of Paris.\n\nIn a quarter of an hour he was entering the house in the Rue du Helder. As he alighted, he thought he saw his father’s pale face behind the curtain of the count’s bedroom. Albert turned away his head with a sigh, and went to his own apartments. He cast one lingering look on all the luxuries which had rendered life so easy and so happy since his infancy; he looked at the pictures, whose faces seemed to smile, and the landscapes, which appeared painted in brighter colors. Then he took away his mother’s portrait, with its oaken frame, leaving the gilt frame from which he took it black and empty. Then he arranged all his beautiful Turkish arms, his fine English guns, his Japanese china, his cups mounted in silver, his artistic bronzes by Feuchères or Barye; examined the cupboards, and placed the key in each; threw into a drawer of his secretaire, which he left open, all the pocket-money he had about him, and with it the thousand fancy jewels from his vases and his jewel-boxes; then he made an exact inventory of everything, and placed it in the most conspicuous part of the table, after putting aside the books and papers which had collected there.\n\nAt the beginning of this work, his servant, notwithstanding orders to the contrary, came to his room.\n\n“What do you want?” asked he, with a more sorrowful than angry tone.\n\n“Pardon me, sir,” replied the valet; “you had forbidden me to disturb you, but the Count of Morcerf has called me.”\n\n“Well!” said Albert.\n\n“I did not like to go to him without first seeing you.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because the count is doubtless aware that I accompanied you to the meeting this morning.”\n40254m\n\n“It is probable,” said Albert.\n\n“And since he has sent for me, it is doubtless to question me on what happened there. What must I answer?”\n\n“The truth.”\n\n“Then I shall say the duel did not take place?”\n\n“You will say I apologized to the Count of Monte Cristo. Go.”\n\nThe valet bowed and retired, and Albert returned to his inventory. As he was finishing this work, the sound of horses prancing in the yard, and the wheels of a carriage shaking his window, attracted his attention. He approached the window, and saw his father get into it, and drive away. The door was scarcely closed when Albert bent his steps to his mother’s room; and, no one being there to announce him, he advanced to her bedchamber, and distressed by what he saw and guessed, stopped for one moment at the door.\n\nAs if the same idea had animated these two beings, Mercédès was doing the same in her apartments that he had just done in his. Everything was in order,—laces, dresses, jewels, linen, money, all were arranged in the drawers, and the countess was carefully collecting the keys. Albert saw all these preparations and understood them, and exclaiming, “My mother!” he threw his arms around her neck.\n\nThe artist who could have depicted the expression of these two countenances would certainly have made of them a beautiful picture. All these proofs of an energetic resolution, which Albert did not fear on his own account, alarmed him for his mother. “What are you doing?” asked he.\n\n“What were you doing?” replied she.\n\n“Oh, my mother!” exclaimed Albert, so overcome he could scarcely speak; “it is not the same with you and me—you cannot have made the same resolution I have, for I have come to warn you that I bid adieu to your house, and—and to you.”\n\n“I also,” replied Mercédès, “am going, and I acknowledge I had depended on your accompanying me; have I deceived myself?”\n\n“Mother,” said Albert with firmness. “I cannot make you share the fate I have planned for myself. I must live henceforth without rank and fortune, and to begin this hard apprenticeship I must borrow from a friend the loaf I shall eat until I have earned one. So, my dear mother, I am going at once to ask Franz to lend me the small sum I shall require to supply my present wants.”\n\n“You, my poor child, suffer poverty and hunger? Oh, do not say so; it will break my resolutions.”\n\n“But not mine, mother,” replied Albert. “I am young and strong; I believe I am courageous, and since yesterday I have learned the power of will. Alas, my dear mother, some have suffered so much, and yet live, and have raised a new fortune on the ruin of all the promises of happiness which heaven had made them—on the fragments of all the hope which God had given them! I have seen that, mother; I know that from the gulf in which their enemies have plunged them they have risen with so much vigor and glory that in their turn they have ruled their former conquerors, and have punished them. No, mother; from this moment I have done with the past, and accept nothing from it—not even a name, because you can understand that your son cannot bear the name of a man who ought to blush for it before another.”\n\n“Albert, my child,” said Mercédès, “if I had a stronger heart, that is the counsel I would have given you; your conscience has spoken when my voice became too weak; listen to its dictates. You had friends, Albert; break off their acquaintance. But do not despair; you have life before you, my dear Albert, for you are yet scarcely twenty-two years old; and as a pure heart like yours wants a spotless name, take my father’s—it was Herrera. I am sure, my dear Albert, whatever may be your career, you will soon render that name illustrious. Then, my son, return to the world still more brilliant because of your former sorrows; and if I am wrong, still let me cherish these hopes, for I have no future to look forward to. For me the grave opens when I pass the threshold of this house.”\n\n“I will fulfil all your wishes, my dear mother,” said the young man. “Yes, I share your hopes; the anger of Heaven will not pursue us, since you are pure and I am innocent. But, since our resolution is formed, let us act promptly. M. de Morcerf went out about half an hour ago; the opportunity is favorable to avoid an explanation.”\n\n“I am ready, my son,” said Mercédès.\n\nAlbert ran to fetch a carriage. He recollected that there was a small furnished house to let in the Rue des Saints-Pères, where his mother would find a humble but decent lodging, and thither he intended conducting the countess. As the carriage stopped at the door, and Albert was alighting, a man approached and gave him a letter.\n\nAlbert recognized the bearer. “From the count,” said Bertuccio. Albert took the letter, opened, and read it, then looked round for Bertuccio, but he was gone.\n\nHe returned to Mercédès with tears in his eyes and heaving breast, and without uttering a word he gave her the letter. Mercédès read:\n\n“Albert,—While showing you that I have discovered your plans, I hope also to convince you of my delicacy. You are free, you leave the count’s house, and you take your mother to your home; but reflect, Albert, you owe her more than your poor noble heart can pay her. Keep the struggle for yourself, bear all the suffering, but spare her the trial of poverty which must accompany your first efforts; for she deserves not even the shadow of the misfortune which has this day fallen on her, and Providence is not willing that the innocent should suffer for the guilty. I know you are going to leave the Rue du Helder without taking anything with you. Do not seek to know how I discovered it; I know it—that is sufficient.\n\n“Now, listen, Albert. Twenty-four years ago I returned, proud and joyful, to my country. I had a betrothed, Albert, a lovely girl whom I adored, and I was bringing to my betrothed a hundred and fifty louis, painfully amassed by ceaseless toil. This money was for her; I destined it for her, and, knowing the treachery of the sea I buried our treasure in the little garden of the house my father lived in at Marseilles, on the Allées de Meilhan. Your mother, Albert, knows that poor house well. A short time since I passed through Marseilles, and went to see the old place, which revived so many painful recollections; and in the evening I took a spade and dug in the corner of the garden where I had concealed my treasure. The iron box was there—no one had touched it—under a beautiful fig-tree my father had planted the day I was born, which overshadowed the spot. Well, Albert, this money, which was formerly designed to promote the comfort and tranquillity of the woman I adored, may now, through strange and painful circumstances, be devoted to the same purpose.\n\n“Oh, feel for me, who could offer millions to that poor woman, but who return her only the piece of black bread forgotten under my poor roof since the day I was torn from her I loved. You are a generous man, Albert, but perhaps you may be blinded by pride or resentment; if you refuse me, if you ask another for what I have a right to offer you, I will say it is ungenerous of you to refuse the life of your mother at the hands of a man whose father was allowed by your father to die in all the horrors of poverty and despair.”\n\nAlbert stood pale and motionless to hear what his mother would decide after she had finished reading this letter. Mercédès turned her eyes with an ineffable look towards heaven.\n\n“I accept it,” said she; “he has a right to pay the dowry, which I shall take with me to some convent!”\n\nPutting the letter in her bosom, she took her son’s arm, and with a firmer step than she even herself expected she went downstairs.\n\n# Chapter 92. The Suicide\n\nMeanwhile Monte Cristo had also returned to town with Emmanuel and Maximilian. Their return was cheerful. Emmanuel did not conceal his joy at the peaceful termination of the affair, and was loud in his expressions of delight. Morrel, in a corner of the carriage, allowed his brother-in-law’s gayety to expend itself in words, while he felt equal inward joy, which, however, betrayed itself only in his countenance.\n\nAt the Barrière du Trône they met Bertuccio, who was waiting there, motionless as a sentinel at his post. Monte Cristo put his head out of the window, exchanged a few words with him in a low tone, and the steward disappeared.\n\n“Count,” said Emmanuel, when they were at the end of the Place Royale, “put me down at my door, that my wife may not have a single moment of needless anxiety on my account or yours.”\n\n“If it were not ridiculous to make a display of our triumph, said Morrel, I would invite the count to our house; besides that, he doubtless has some trembling heart to comfort. So we will take leave of our friend, and let him hasten home.”\n\n“Stop a moment,” said Monte Cristo; “do not let me lose both my companions. Return, Emmanuel, to your charming wife, and present my best compliments to her; and do you, Morrel, accompany me to the Champs-Élysées.”\n\n“Willingly,” said Maximilian; “particularly as I have business in that quarter.”\n\n“Shall we wait breakfast for you?” asked Emmanuel.\n\n“No,” replied the young man. The door was closed, and the carriage proceeded. “See what good fortune I brought you!” said Morrel, when he was alone with the count. “Have you not thought so?”\n\n“Yes,” said Monte Cristo; “for that reason I wished to keep you near me.”\n\n“It is miraculous!” continued Morrel, answering his own thoughts.\n\n“What?” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“What has just happened.”\n\n“Yes,” said the Count, “you are right—it is miraculous.”\n\n“For Albert is brave,” resumed Morrel.\n\n“Very brave,” said Monte Cristo; “I have seen him sleep with a sword suspended over his head.”\n\n“And I know he has fought two duels,” said Morrel. “How can you reconcile that with his conduct this morning?”\n\n“All owing to your influence,” replied Monte Cristo, smiling.\n\n“It is well for Albert he is not in the army,” said Morrel.\n\n“Why?”\n\n“An apology on the ground!” said the young captain, shaking his head.\n\n“Come,” said the count mildly, “do not entertain the prejudices of ordinary men, Morrel! Acknowledge, that if Albert is brave, he cannot be a coward; he must then have had some reason for acting as he did this morning, and confess that his conduct is more heroic than otherwise.”\n\n“Doubtless, doubtless,” said Morrel; “but I shall say, like the Spaniard, ‘He has not been so brave today as he was yesterday.’”\n\n“You will breakfast with me, will you not, Morrel?” said the count, to turn the conversation.\n\n“No; I must leave you at ten o’clock.”\n\n“Your engagement was for breakfast, then?” said the count.\n\nMorrel smiled, and shook his head.\n\n“Still you must breakfast somewhere.”\n\n“But if I am not hungry?” said the young man.\n\n“Oh,” said the count, “I only know two things which destroy the appetite,—grief—and as I am happy to see you very cheerful, it is not that—and love. Now after what you told me this morning of your heart, I may believe——”\n\n“Well, count,” replied Morrel gayly, “I will not dispute it.”\n\n“But you will not make me your confidant, Maximilian?” said the count, in a tone which showed how gladly he would have been admitted to the secret.\n\n“I showed you this morning that I had a heart, did I not, count?” Monte Cristo only answered by extending his hand to the young man. “Well,” continued the latter, “since that heart is no longer with you in the Bois de Vincennes, it is elsewhere, and I must go and find it.”\n\n“Go,” said the count deliberately; “go, dear friend, but promise me if you meet with any obstacle to remember that I have some power in this world, that I am happy to use that power in the behalf of those I love, and that I love you, Morrel.”\n40260m\n\n“I will remember it,” said the young man, “as selfish children recollect their parents when they want their aid. When I need your assistance, and the moment arrives, I will come to you, count.”\n\n“Well, I rely upon your promise. Good-bye, then.”\n\n“Good-bye, till we meet again.”\n\nThey had arrived in the Champs-Élysées. Monte Cristo opened the carriage-door, Morrel sprang out on the pavement, Bertuccio was waiting on the steps. Morrel disappeared down the Avenue de Marigny, and Monte Cristo hastened to join Bertuccio.\n\n“Well?” asked he.\n\n“She is going to leave her house,” said the steward.\n\n“And her son?”\n\n“Florentin, his valet, thinks he is going to do the same.”\n\n“Come this way.” Monte Cristo took Bertuccio into his study, wrote the letter we have seen, and gave it to the steward. “Go,” said he quickly. “But first, let Haydée be informed that I have returned.”\n\n“Here I am,” said the young girl, who at the sound of the carriage had run downstairs and whose face was radiant with joy at seeing the count return safely. Bertuccio left. Every transport of a daughter finding a father, all the delight of a mistress seeing an adored lover, were felt by Haydée during the first moments of this meeting, which she had so eagerly expected. Doubtless, although less evident, Monte Cristo’s joy was not less intense. Joy to hearts which have suffered long is like the dew on the ground after a long drought; both the heart and the ground absorb that beneficent moisture falling on them, and nothing is outwardly apparent.\n\nMonte Cristo was beginning to think, what he had not for a long time dared to believe, that there were two Mercédès in the world, and he might yet be happy. His eye, elate with happiness, was reading eagerly the tearful gaze of Haydée, when suddenly the door opened. The count knit his brow.\n\n“M. de Morcerf!” said Baptistin, as if that name sufficed for his excuse. In fact, the count’s face brightened.\n\n“Which,” asked he, “the viscount or the count?”\n\n“The count.”\n\n“Oh,” exclaimed Haydée, “is it not yet over?”\n\n“I know not if it is finished, my beloved child,” said Monte Cristo, taking the young girl’s hands; “but I do know you have nothing more to fear.”\n\n“But it is the wretched——”\n\n“That man cannot injure me, Haydée,” said Monte Cristo; “it was his son alone that there was cause to fear.”\n\n“And what I have suffered,” said the young girl, “you shall never know, my lord.”\n\nMonte Cristo smiled. “By my father’s tomb,” said he, extending his hand over the head of the young girl, “I swear to you, Haydée, that if any misfortune happens, it will not be to me.”\n\n“I believe you, my lord, as implicitly as if God had spoken to me,” said the young girl, presenting her forehead to him. Monte Cristo pressed on that pure beautiful forehead a kiss which made two hearts throb at once, the one violently, the other secretly.\n\n“Oh,” murmured the count, “shall I then be permitted to love again? Ask M. de Morcerf into the drawing-room,” said he to Baptistin, while he led the beautiful Greek girl to a private staircase.\n\nWe must explain this visit, which although expected by Monte Cristo, is unexpected to our readers. While Mercédès, as we have said, was making a similar inventory of her property to Albert’s, while she was arranging her jewels, shutting her drawers, collecting her keys, to leave everything in perfect order, she did not perceive a pale and sinister face at a glass door which threw light into the passage, from which everything could be both seen and heard. He who was thus looking, without being heard or seen, probably heard and saw all that passed in Madame de Morcerf’s apartments. From that glass door the pale-faced man went to the count’s bedroom and raised with a constricted hand the curtain of a window overlooking the courtyard. He remained there ten minutes, motionless and dumb, listening to the beating of his own heart. For him those ten minutes were very long. It was then Albert, returning from his meeting with the count, perceived his father watching for his arrival behind a curtain, and turned aside. The count’s eye expanded; he knew Albert had insulted the count dreadfully, and that in every country in the world such an insult would lead to a deadly duel. Albert returned safely—then the count was revenged.\n\nAn indescribable ray of joy illumined that wretched countenance like the last ray of the sun before it disappears behind the clouds which bear the aspect, not of a downy couch, but of a tomb. But as we have said, he waited in vain for his son to come to his apartment with the account of his triumph. He easily understood why his son did not come to see him before he went to avenge his father’s honor; but when that was done, why did not his son come and throw himself into his arms?\n\nIt was then, when the count could not see Albert, that he sent for his servant, who he knew was authorized not to conceal anything from him. Ten minutes afterwards, General Morcerf was seen on the steps in a black coat with a military collar, black pantaloons, and black gloves. He had apparently given previous orders, for as he reached the bottom step his carriage came from the coach-house ready for him. The valet threw into the carriage his military cloak, in which two swords were wrapped, and, shutting the door, he took his seat by the side of the coachman. The coachman stooped down for his orders.\n\n“To the Champs-Élysées,” said the general; “the Count of Monte Cristo’s. Hurry!”\n\nThe horses bounded beneath the whip; and in five minutes they stopped before the count’s door. M. de Morcerf opened the door himself, and as the carriage rolled away he passed up the walk, rang, and entered the open door with his servant.\n\nA moment afterwards, Baptistin announced the Count of Morcerf to Monte Cristo, and the latter, leading Haydée aside, ordered that Morcerf be asked into the drawing-room. The general was pacing the room the third time when, in turning, he perceived Monte Cristo at the door.\n\n“Ah, it is M. de Morcerf,” said Monte Cristo quietly; “I thought I had not heard aright.”\n\n“Yes, it is I,” said the count, whom a frightful contraction of the lips prevented from articulating freely.\n\n“May I know the cause which procures me the pleasure of seeing M. de Morcerf so early?”\n\n“Had you not a meeting with my son this morning?” asked the general.\n\n“I had,” replied the count.\n\n“And I know my son had good reasons to wish to fight with you, and to endeavor to kill you.”\n\n“Yes, sir, he had very good ones; but you see that in spite of them he has not killed me, and did not even fight.”\n\n“Yet he considered you the cause of his father’s dishonor, the cause of the fearful ruin which has fallen on my house.”\n\n“It is true, sir,” said Monte Cristo with his dreadful calmness; “a secondary cause, but not the principal.”\n\n“Doubtless you made, then, some apology or explanation?”\n\n“I explained nothing, and it is he who apologized to me.”\n\n“But to what do you attribute this conduct?”\n\n“To the conviction, probably, that there was one more guilty than I.”\n\n“And who was that?”\n\n“His father.”\n\n“That may be,” said the count, turning pale; “but you know the guilty do not like to find themselves convicted.”\n\n“I know it, and I expected this result.”\n\n“You expected my son would be a coward?” cried the count.\n\n“M. Albert de Morcerf is no coward!” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“A man who holds a sword in his hand, and sees a mortal enemy within reach of that sword, and does not fight, is a coward! Why is he not here that I may tell him so?”\n\n“Sir,” replied Monte Cristo coldly, “I did not expect that you had come here to relate to me your little family affairs. Go and tell M. Albert that, and he may know what to answer you.”\n\n“Oh, no, no,” said the general, smiling faintly, “I did not come for that purpose; you are right. I came to tell you that I also look upon you as my enemy. I came to tell you that I hate you instinctively; that it seems as if I had always known you, and always hated you; and, in short, since the young people of the present day will not fight, it remains for us to do so. Do you think so, sir?”\n\n“Certainly. And when I told you I had foreseen the result, it is the honor of your visit I alluded to.”\n\n“So much the better. Are you prepared?”\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n“You know that we shall fight till one of us is dead,” said the general, whose teeth were clenched with rage.\n\n40262m\n“Until one of us dies,” repeated Monte Cristo, moving his head slightly up and down.\n\n“Let us start, then; we need no witnesses.”\n\n“Very true,” said Monte Cristo; “it is unnecessary, we know each other so well!”\n\n“On the contrary,” said the count, “we know so little of each other.”\n\n“Indeed?” said Monte Cristo, with the same indomitable coolness; “let us see. Are you not the soldier Fernand who deserted on the eve of the battle of Waterloo? Are you not the Lieutenant Fernand who served as guide and spy to the French army in Spain? Are you not the Captain Fernand who betrayed, sold, and murdered his benefactor, Ali? And have not all these Fernands, united, made Lieutenant-General, the Count of Morcerf, peer of France?”\n\n“Oh,” cried the general, as if branded with a hot iron, “wretch,—to reproach me with my shame when about, perhaps, to kill me! No, I did not say I was a stranger to you. I know well, demon, that you have penetrated into the darkness of the past, and that you have read, by the light of what torch I know not, every page of my life; but perhaps I may be more honorable in my shame than you under your pompous coverings. No—no, I am aware you know me; but I know you only as an adventurer sewn up in gold and jewellery. You call yourself, in Paris, the Count of Monte Cristo; in Italy, Sinbad the Sailor; in Malta, I forget what. But it is your real name I want to know, in the midst of your hundred names, that I may pronounce it when we meet to fight, at the moment when I plunge my sword through your heart.”\n\nThe Count of Monte Cristo turned dreadfully pale; his eye seemed to burn with a devouring fire. He leaped towards a dressing-room near his bedroom, and in less than a moment, tearing off his cravat, his coat and waistcoat, he put on a sailor’s jacket and hat, from beneath which rolled his long black hair. He returned thus, formidable and implacable, advancing with his arms crossed on his breast, towards the general, who could not understand why he had disappeared, but who on seeing him again, and feeling his teeth chatter and his legs sink under him, drew back, and only stopped when he found a table to support his clenched hand.\n\n“Fernand,” cried he, “of my hundred names I need only tell you one, to overwhelm you! But you guess it now, do you not?—or, rather, you remember it? For, notwithstanding all my sorrows and my tortures, I show you today a face which the happiness of revenge makes young again—a face you must often have seen in your dreams since your marriage with Mercédès, my betrothed!”\n40268m\n\nThe general, with his head thrown back, hands extended, gaze fixed, looked silently at this dreadful apparition; then seeking the wall to support him, he glided along close to it until he reached the door, through which he went out backwards, uttering this single mournful, lamentable, distressing cry:\n\n“Edmond Dantès!”\n\nThen, with sighs which were unlike any human sound, he dragged himself to the door, reeled across the courtyard, and falling into the arms of his valet, he said in a voice scarcely intelligible,—“Home, home.”\n\nThe fresh air and the shame he felt at having exposed himself before his servants, partly recalled his senses, but the ride was short, and as he drew near his house all his wretchedness revived. He stopped at a short distance from the house and alighted. The door was wide open, a hackney-coach was standing in the middle of the yard—a strange sight before so noble a mansion; the count looked at it with terror, but without daring to inquire its meaning, he rushed towards his apartment.\n\nTwo persons were coming down the stairs; he had only time to creep into an alcove to avoid them. It was Mercédès leaning on her son’s arm and leaving the house. They passed close by the unhappy being, who, concealed behind the damask curtain, almost felt Mercédès dress brush past him, and his son’s warm breath, pronouncing these words:\n\n“Courage, mother! Come, this is no longer our home!”\n\nThe words died away, the steps were lost in the distance. The general drew himself up, clinging to the curtain; he uttered the most dreadful sob which ever escaped from the bosom of a father abandoned at the same time by his wife and son. He soon heard the clatter of the iron step of the hackney-coach, then the coachman’s voice, and then the rolling of the heavy vehicle shook the windows. He darted to his bedroom to see once more all he had loved in the world; but the hackney-coach drove on and the head of neither Mercédès nor her son appeared at the window to take a last look at the house or the deserted father and husband.\n\nAnd at the very moment when the wheels of that coach crossed the gateway a report was heard, and a thick smoke escaped through one of the panes of the window, which was broken by the explosion.\n\n# Chapter 93. Valentine\n\n40270m\nWe may easily conceive where Morrel’s appointment was. On leaving Monte Cristo he walked slowly towards Villefort’s; we say slowly, for Morrel had more than half an hour to spare to go five hundred steps, but he had hastened to take leave of Monte Cristo because he wished to be alone with his thoughts. He knew his time well—the hour when Valentine was giving Noirtier his breakfast, and was sure not to be disturbed in the performance of this pious duty. Noirtier and Valentine had given him leave to go twice a week, and he was now availing himself of that permission.\n\nHe arrived; Valentine was expecting him. Uneasy and almost crazed, she seized his hand and led him to her grandfather. This uneasiness, amounting almost to frenzy, arose from the report Morcerf’s adventure had made in the world, for the affair at the Opera was generally known. No one at Villefort’s doubted that a duel would ensue from it. Valentine, with her woman’s instinct, guessed that Morrel would be Monte Cristo’s second, and from the young man’s well-known courage and his great affection for the count, she feared that he would not content himself with the passive part assigned to him. We may easily understand how eagerly the particulars were asked for, given, and received; and Morrel could read an indescribable joy in the eyes of his beloved, when she knew that the termination of this affair was as happy as it was unexpected.\n\n“Now,” said Valentine, motioning to Morrel to sit down near her grandfather, while she took her seat on his footstool,—“now let us talk about our own affairs. You know, Maximilian, grandpapa once thought of leaving this house, and taking an apartment away from M. de Villefort’s.”\n\n“Yes,” said Maximilian, “I recollect the project, of which I highly approved.”\n\n“Well,” said Valentine, “you may approve again, for grandpapa is again thinking of it.”\n\n“Bravo,” said Maximilian.\n40272m\n\n“And do you know,” said Valentine, “what reason grandpapa gives for leaving this house.” Noirtier looked at Valentine to impose silence, but she did not notice him; her looks, her eyes, her smile, were all for Morrel.\n\n“Oh, whatever may be M. Noirtier’s reason,” answered Morrel, “I can readily believe it to be a good one.”\n\n“An excellent one,” said Valentine. “He pretends the air of the Faubourg Saint-Honoré is not good for me.”\n\n“Indeed?” said Morrel; “in that M. Noirtier may be right; you have not seemed to be well for the last fortnight.”\n\n“Not very,” said Valentine. “And grandpapa has become my physician, and I have the greatest confidence in him, because he knows everything.”\n\n“Do you then really suffer?” asked Morrel quickly.\n\n“Oh, it must not be called suffering; I feel a general uneasiness, that is all. I have lost my appetite, and my stomach feels as if it were struggling to get accustomed to something.” Noirtier did not lose a word of what Valentine said.\n\n“And what treatment do you adopt for this singular complaint?”\n\n“A very simple one,” said Valentine. “I swallow every morning a spoonful of the mixture prepared for my grandfather. When I say one spoonful, I began by one—now I take four. Grandpapa says it is a panacea.” Valentine smiled, but it was evident that she suffered.\n\nMaximilian, in his devotedness, gazed silently at her. She was very beautiful, but her usual pallor had increased; her eyes were more brilliant than ever, and her hands, which were generally white like mother-of-pearl, now more resembled wax, to which time was adding a yellowish hue.\n\nFrom Valentine the young man looked towards Noirtier. The latter watched with strange and deep interest the young girl, absorbed by her affection, and he also, like Morrel, followed those traces of inward suffering which was so little perceptible to a common observer that they escaped the notice of everyone but the grandfather and the lover.\n\n“But,” said Morrel, “I thought this mixture, of which you now take four spoonfuls, was prepared for M. Noirtier?”\n\n“I know it is very bitter,” said Valentine; “so bitter, that all I drink afterwards appears to have the same taste.” Noirtier looked inquiringly at his granddaughter. “Yes, grandpapa,” said Valentine; “it is so. Just now, before I came down to you, I drank a glass of sugared water; I left half, because it seemed so bitter.” Noirtier turned pale, and made a sign that he wished to speak.\n\nValentine rose to fetch the dictionary. Noirtier watched her with evident anguish. In fact, the blood was rushing to the young girl’s head already, her cheeks were becoming red.\n\n“Oh,” cried she, without losing any of her cheerfulness, “this is singular! I can’t see! Did the sun shine in my eyes?” And she leaned against the window.\n\n“The sun is not shining,” said Morrel, more alarmed by Noirtier’s expression than by Valentine’s indisposition. He ran towards her. The young girl smiled.\n\n“Cheer up,” said she to Noirtier. “Do not be alarmed, Maximilian; it is nothing, and has already passed away. But listen! Do I not hear a carriage in the courtyard?” She opened Noirtier’s door, ran to a window in the passage, and returned hastily. “Yes,” said she, “it is Madame Danglars and her daughter, who have come to call on us. Good-bye;—I must run away, for they would send here for me, or, rather, farewell till I see you again. Stay with grandpapa, Maximilian; I promise you not to persuade them to stay.”\n\n40274m\nMorrel watched her as she left the room; he heard her ascend the little staircase which led both to Madame de Villefort’s apartments and to hers. As soon as she was gone, Noirtier made a sign to Morrel to take the dictionary. Morrel obeyed; guided by Valentine, he had learned how to understand the old man quickly. Accustomed, however, as he was to the work, he had to repeat most of the letters of the alphabet and to find every word in the dictionary, so that it was ten minutes before the thought of the old man was translated by these words,\n\n“Fetch the glass of water and the decanter from Valentine’s room.”\n\nMorrel rang immediately for the servant who had taken Barrois’s situation, and in Noirtier’s name gave that order. The servant soon returned. The decanter and the glass were completely empty. Noirtier made a sign that he wished to speak.\n\n“Why are the glass and decanter empty?” asked he; “Valentine said she only drank half the glassful.”\n\nThe translation of this new question occupied another five minutes.\n\n“I do not know,” said the servant, “but the housemaid is in Mademoiselle Valentine’s room: perhaps she has emptied them.”\n\n“Ask her,” said Morrel, translating Noirtier’s thought this time by his look. The servant went out, but returned almost immediately. “Mademoiselle Valentine passed through the room to go to Madame de Villefort’s,” said he; “and in passing, as she was thirsty, she drank what remained in the glass; as for the decanter, Master Edward had emptied that to make a pond for his ducks.”\n\nNoirtier raised his eyes to heaven, as a gambler does who stakes his all on one stroke. From that moment the old man’s eyes were fixed on the door, and did not quit it.\n\nIt was indeed Madame Danglars and her daughter whom Valentine had seen; they had been ushered into Madame de Villefort’s room, who had said she would receive them there. That is why Valentine passed through her room, which was on a level with Valentine’s, and only separated from it by Edward’s. The two ladies entered the drawing-room with that sort of official stiffness which preludes a formal communication. Among worldly people manner is contagious. Madame de Villefort received them with equal solemnity. Valentine entered at this moment, and the formalities were resumed.\n\n“My dear friend,” said the baroness, while the two young people were shaking hands, “I and Eugénie are come to be the first to announce to you the approaching marriage of my daughter with Prince Cavalcanti.” Danglars kept up the title of prince. The popular banker found that it answered better than count.\n\n“Allow me to present you my sincere congratulations,” replied Madame de Villefort. “Prince Cavalcanti appears to be a young man of rare qualities.”\n\n40276m\n“Listen,” said the baroness, smiling; “speaking to you as a friend I can say that the prince does not yet appear all he will be. He has about him a little of that foreign manner by which French persons recognize, at first sight, the Italian or German nobleman. Besides, he gives evidence of great kindness of disposition, much keenness of wit, and as to suitability, M. Danglars assures me that his fortune is majestic—that is his word.”\n\n“And then,” said Eugénie, while turning over the leaves of Madame de Villefort’s album, “add that you have taken a great fancy to the young man.”\n\n“And,” said Madame de Villefort, “I need not ask you if you share that fancy.”\n\n“I?” replied Eugénie with her usual candor. “Oh, not the least in the world, madame! My wish was not to confine myself to domestic cares, or the caprices of any man, but to be an artist, and consequently free in heart, in person, and in thought.”\n\nEugénie pronounced these words with so firm a tone that the color mounted to Valentine’s cheeks. The timid girl could not understand that vigorous nature which appeared to have none of the timidities of woman.\n\n“At any rate,” said she, “since I am to be married whether I will or not, I ought to be thankful to Providence for having released me from my engagement with M. Albert de Morcerf, or I should this day have been the wife of a dishonored man.”\n\n“It is true,” said the baroness, with that strange simplicity sometimes met with among fashionable ladies, and of which plebeian intercourse can never entirely deprive them,—“it is very true that had not the Morcerfs hesitated, my daughter would have married Monsieur Albert. The general depended much on it; he even came to force M. Danglars. We have had a narrow escape.”\n\n“But,” said Valentine, timidly, “does all the father’s shame revert upon the son? Monsieur Albert appears to me quite innocent of the treason charged against the general.”\n\n“Excuse me,” said the implacable young girl, “Monsieur Albert claims and well deserves his share. It appears that after having challenged M. de Monte Cristo at the Opera yesterday, he apologized on the ground today.”\n\n“Impossible,” said Madame de Villefort.\n\n“Ah, my dear friend,” said Madame Danglars, with the same simplicity we before noticed, “it is a fact. I heard it from M. Debray, who was present at the explanation.”\n\nValentine also knew the truth, but she did not answer. A single word had reminded her that Morrel was expecting her in M. Noirtier’s room. Deeply engaged with a sort of inward contemplation, Valentine had ceased for a moment to join in the conversation. She would, indeed, have found it impossible to repeat what had been said the last few minutes, when suddenly Madame Danglars’ hand, pressed on her arm, aroused her from her lethargy.\n\n“What is it?” said she, starting at Madame Danglars’ touch as she would have done from an electric shock.\n\n“It is, my dear Valentine,” said the baroness, “that you are, doubtless, suffering.”\n40280m\n\n“I?” said the young girl, passing her hand across her burning forehead.\n\n“Yes, look at yourself in that glass; you have turned pale and then red successively, three or four times in one minute.”\n\n“Indeed,” cried Eugénie, “you are very pale!”\n\n“Oh, do not be alarmed; I have been so for many days.” Artless as she was, the young girl knew that this was an opportunity to leave, and besides, Madame de Villefort came to her assistance.\n\n“Retire, Valentine,” said she; “you are really suffering, and these ladies will excuse you; drink a glass of pure water, it will restore you.”\n\nValentine kissed Eugénie, bowed to Madame Danglars, who had already risen to take her leave, and went out.\n\n“That poor child,” said Madame de Villefort when Valentine was gone, “she makes me very uneasy, and I should not be astonished if she had some serious illness.”\n\nMeanwhile, Valentine, in a sort of excitement which she could not quite understand, had crossed Edward’s room without noticing some trick of the child, and through her own had reached the little staircase.\n\nShe was within three steps of the bottom; she already heard Morrel’s voice, when suddenly a cloud passed over her eyes, her stiffened foot missed the step, her hands had no power to hold the baluster, and falling against the wall she lost her balance wholly and toppled to the floor. Morrel bounded to the door, opened it, and found Valentine stretched out at the bottom of the stairs. Quick as a flash, he raised her in his arms and placed her in a chair. Valentine opened her eyes.\n\n“Oh, what a clumsy thing I am,” said she with feverish volubility; “I don’t know my way. I forgot there were three more steps before the landing.”\n\n“You have hurt yourself, perhaps,” said Morrel. “What can I do for you, Valentine?”\n\nValentine looked around her; she saw the deepest terror depicted in Noirtier’s eyes.\n\n“Don’t worry, dear grandpapa,” said she, endeavoring to smile; “it is nothing—it is nothing; I was giddy, that is all.”\n\n“Another attack of giddiness,” said Morrel, clasping his hands. “Oh, attend to it, Valentine, I entreat you.”\n\n“But no,” said Valentine,—“no, I tell you it is all past, and it was nothing. Now, let me tell you some news; Eugénie is to be married in a week, and in three days there is to be a grand feast, a betrothal festival. We are all invited, my father, Madame de Villefort, and I—at least, I understood it so.”\n\n“When will it be our turn to think of these things? Oh, Valentine, you who have so much influence over your grandpapa, try to make him answer—Soon.”\n\n“And do you,” said Valentine, “depend on me to stimulate the tardiness and arouse the memory of grandpapa?”\n\n“Yes,” cried Morrel, “make haste. So long as you are not mine, Valentine, I shall always think I may lose you.”\n\n“Oh,” replied Valentine with a convulsive movement, “oh, indeed, Maximilian, you are too timid for an officer, for a soldier who, they say, never knows fear. Ha, ha, ha!”\n\nShe burst into a forced and melancholy laugh, her arms stiffened and twisted, her head fell back on her chair, and she remained motionless. The cry of terror which was stopped on Noirtier’s lips, seemed to start from his eyes. Morrel understood it; he knew he must call assistance. The young man rang the bell violently; the housemaid who had been in Mademoiselle Valentine’s room, and the servant who had replaced Barrois, ran in at the same moment. Valentine was so pale, so cold, so inanimate that without listening to what was said to them they were seized with the fear which pervaded that house, and they flew into the passage crying for help. Madame Danglars and Eugénie were going out at that moment; they heard the cause of the disturbance.\n\n“I told you so!” exclaimed Madame de Villefort. “Poor child!”\n\n# Chapter 94. Maximilian’s Avowal\n\n40278m\nAt the same moment M. de Villefort’s voice was heard calling from his study, “What is the matter?”\n\nMorrel looked at Noirtier who had recovered his self-command, and with a glance indicated the closet where once before under somewhat similar circumstances, he had taken refuge. He had only time to get his hat and throw himself breathless into the closet when the procureur’s footstep was heard in the passage.\n\nVillefort sprang into the room, ran to Valentine, and took her in his arms.\n\n“A physician, a physician,—M. d’Avrigny!” cried Villefort; “or rather I will go for him myself.”\n\nHe flew from the apartment, and Morrel at the same moment darted out at the other door. He had been struck to the heart by a frightful recollection—the conversation he had heard between the doctor and Villefort the night of Madame de Saint-Méran’s death, recurred to him; these symptoms, to a less alarming extent, were the same which had preceded the death of Barrois. At the same time Monte Cristo’s voice seemed to resound in his ear with the words he had heard only two hours before, “Whatever you want, Morrel, come to me; I have great power.”\n\nMore rapidly than thought, he darted down the Rue Matignon, and thence to the Avenue des Champs-Élysées.\n\nMeanwhile M. de Villefort arrived in a hired cabriolet at M. d’Avrigny’s door. He rang so violently that the porter was alarmed. Villefort ran upstairs without saying a word. The porter knew him, and let him pass, only calling to him:\n\n“In his study, Monsieur Procureur—in his study!” Villefort pushed, or rather forced, the door open.\n\n“Ah,” said the doctor, “is it you?”\n\n“Yes,” said Villefort, closing the door after him, “it is I, who am come in my turn to ask you if we are quite alone. Doctor, my house is accursed!”\n\n“What?” said the latter with apparent coolness, but with deep emotion, “have you another invalid?”\n\n“Yes, doctor,” cried Villefort, clutching his hair, “yes!”\n\nD’Avrigny’s look implied, “I told you it would be so.” Then he slowly uttered these words, “Who is now dying in your house? What new victim is going to accuse you of weakness before God?”\n\nA mournful sob burst from Villefort’s heart; he approached the doctor, and seizing his arm,—“Valentine,” said he, “it is Valentine’s turn!”\n40284m\n\n“Your daughter!” cried d’Avrigny with grief and surprise.\n\n“You see you were deceived,” murmured the magistrate; “come and see her, and on her bed of agony entreat her pardon for having suspected her.”\n\n“Each time you have applied to me,” said the doctor, “it has been too late; still I will go. But let us make haste, sir; with the enemies you have to do with there is no time to be lost.”\n\n“Oh, this time, doctor, you shall not have to reproach me with weakness. This time I will know the assassin, and will pursue him.”\n\n“Let us try first to save the victim before we think of revenging her,” said d’Avrigny. “Come.”\n\nThe same cabriolet which had brought Villefort took them back at full speed, and at this moment Morrel rapped at Monte Cristo’s door.\n\nThe count was in his study and was reading with an angry look something which Bertuccio had brought in haste. Hearing the name of Morrel, who had left him only two hours before, the count raised his head, arose, and sprang to meet him.\n\n“What is the matter, Maximilian?” asked he; “you are pale, and the perspiration rolls from your forehead.” Morrel fell into a chair.\n\n“Yes,” said he, “I came quickly; I wanted to speak to you.”\n\n“Are all your family well?” asked the count, with an affectionate benevolence, whose sincerity no one could for a moment doubt.\n\n“Thank you, count—thank you,” said the young man, evidently embarrassed how to begin the conversation; “yes, everyone in my family is well.”\n\n“So much the better; yet you have something to tell me?” replied the count with increased anxiety.\n\n“Yes,” said Morrel, “it is true; I have but now left a house where death has just entered, to run to you.”\n\n“Are you then come from M. de Morcerf’s?” asked Monte Cristo.\n\n“No,” said Morrel; “is someone dead in his house?”\n\n“The general has just blown his brains out,” replied Monte Cristo with great coolness.\n\n“Oh, what a dreadful event!” cried Maximilian.\n\n“Not for the countess, or for Albert,” said Monte Cristo; “a dead father or husband is better than a dishonored one,—blood washes out shame.”\n\n“Poor countess,” said Maximilian, “I pity her very much; she is so noble a woman!”\n\n“Pity Albert also, Maximilian; for believe me he is the worthy son of the countess. But let us return to yourself. You have hastened to me—can I have the happiness of being useful to you?”\n\n40286m\n“Yes, I need your help: that is I thought like a madman that you could lend me your assistance in a case where God alone can succor me.”\n\n“Tell me what it is,” replied Monte Cristo.\n\n“Oh,” said Morrel, “I know not, indeed, if I may reveal this secret to mortal ears, but fatality impels me, necessity constrains me, count——” Morrel hesitated.\n\n“Do you think I love you?” said Monte Cristo, taking the young man’s hand affectionately in his.\n\n“Oh, you encourage me, and something tells me there,” placing his hand on his heart, “that I ought to have no secret from you.”\n\n“You are right, Morrel; God is speaking to your heart, and your heart speaks to you. Tell me what it says.”\n\n“Count, will you allow me to send Baptistin to inquire after someone you know?”\n\n“I am at your service, and still more my servants.”\n\n“Oh, I cannot live if she is not better.”\n\n“Shall I ring for Baptistin?”\n\n“No, I will go and speak to him myself.” Morrel went out, called Baptistin, and whispered a few words to him. The valet ran directly.\n\n“Well, have you sent?” asked Monte Cristo, seeing Morrel return.\n\n“Yes, and now I shall be more calm.”\n\n“You know I am waiting,” said Monte Cristo, smiling.\n\n“Yes, and I will tell you. One evening I was in a garden; a clump of trees concealed me; no one suspected I was there. Two persons passed near me—allow me to conceal their names for the present; they were speaking in an undertone, and yet I was so interested in what they said that I did not lose a single word.”\n\n“This is a gloomy introduction, if I may judge from your pallor and shuddering, Morrel.”\n\n“Oh, yes, very gloomy, my friend. Someone had just died in the house to which that garden belonged. One of the persons whose conversation I overheard was the master of the house; the other, the physician. The former was confiding to the latter his grief and fear, for it was the second time within a month that death had suddenly and unexpectedly entered that house which was apparently destined to destruction by some exterminating angel, as an object of God’s anger.”\n\n“Ah, indeed?” said Monte Cristo, looking earnestly at the young man, and by an imperceptible movement turning his chair, so that he remained in the shade while the light fell full on Maximilian’s face.\n\n“Yes,” continued Morrel, “death had entered that house twice within one month.”\n\n“And what did the doctor answer?” asked Monte Cristo.\n\n“He replied—he replied, that the death was not a natural one, and must be attributed”—\n\n“To what?”\n\n“To poison.”\n\n“Indeed!” said Monte Cristo with a slight cough which in moments of extreme emotion helped him to disguise a blush, or his pallor, or the intense interest with which he listened; “indeed, Maximilian, did you hear that?”\n\n“Yes, my dear count, I heard it; and the doctor added that if another death occurred in a similar way he must appeal to justice.”\n\nMonte Cristo listened, or appeared to do so, with the greatest calmness.\n\n“Well,” said Maximilian, “death came a third time, and neither the master of the house nor the doctor said a word. Death is now, perhaps, striking a fourth blow. Count, what am I bound to do, being in possession of this secret?”\n\n“My dear friend,” said Monte Cristo, “you appear to be relating an adventure which we all know by heart. I know the house where you heard it, or one very similar to it; a house with a garden, a master, a physician, and where there have been three unexpected and sudden deaths. Well, I have not intercepted your confidence, and yet I know all that as well as you, and I have no conscientious scruples. No, it does not concern me. You say an exterminating angel appears to have devoted that house to God’s anger—well, who says your supposition is not reality? Do not notice things which those whose interest it is to see them pass over. If it is God’s justice, instead of his anger, which is walking through that house, Maximilian, turn away your face and let his justice accomplish its purpose.”\n\nMorrel shuddered. There was something mournful, solemn, and terrible in the count’s manner.\n\n“Besides,” continued he, in so changed a tone that no one would have supposed it was the same person speaking—“besides, who says that it will begin again?”\n\n“It has returned, count,” exclaimed Morrel; “that is why I hastened to you.”\n\n“Well, what do you wish me to do? Do you wish me, for instance, to give information to the procureur?” Monte Cristo uttered the last words with so much meaning that Morrel, starting up, cried out:\n\n“You know of whom I speak, count, do you not?”\n\n“Perfectly well, my good friend; and I will prove it to you by putting the dots to the _i_, or rather by naming the persons. You were walking one evening in M. de Villefort’s garden; from what you relate, I suppose it to have been the evening of Madame de Saint-Méran’s death. You heard M. de Villefort talking to M. d’Avrigny about the death of M. de Saint-Méran, and that no less surprising, of the countess. M. d’Avrigny said he believed they both proceeded from poison; and you, honest man, have ever since been asking your heart and sounding your conscience to know if you ought to expose or conceal this secret. We are no longer in the Middle Ages; there is no longer a Vehmgericht, or Free Tribunals; what do you want to ask these people? ‘Conscience, what hast thou to do with me?’ as Sterne said. My dear fellow, let them sleep on, if they are asleep; let them grow pale in their drowsiness, if they are disposed to do so, and pray do you remain in peace, who have no remorse to disturb you.”\n\nDeep grief was depicted on Morrel’s features; he seized Monte Cristo’s hand. “But it is beginning again, I say!”\n\n“Well,” said the Count, astonished at his perseverance, which he could not understand, and looking still more earnestly at Maximilian, “let it begin again,—it is like the house of the Atreidae;19 God has condemned them, and they must submit to their punishment. They will all disappear, like the fabrics children build with cards, and which fall, one by one, under the breath of their builder, even if there are two hundred of them. Three months since it was M. de Saint-Méran; Madame de Saint-Méran two months since; the other day it was Barrois; today, the old Noirtier, or young Valentine.”\n\n“You knew it?” cried Morrel, in such a paroxysm of terror that Monte Cristo started,—he whom the falling heavens would have found unmoved; “you knew it, and said nothing?”\n\n“And what is it to me?” replied Monte Cristo, shrugging his shoulders; “do I know those people? and must I lose the one to save the other? Faith, no, for between the culprit and the victim I have no choice.”\n\n“But I,” cried Morrel, groaning with sorrow, “I love her!”\n\n“You love?—whom?” cried Monte Cristo, starting to his feet, and seizing the two hands which Morrel was raising towards heaven.\n\n“I love most fondly—I love madly—I love as a man who would give his life-blood to spare her a tear—I love Valentine de Villefort, who is being murdered at this moment! Do you understand me? I love her; and I ask God and you how I can save her?”\n\nMonte Cristo uttered a cry which those only can conceive who have heard the roar of a wounded lion. “Unhappy man,” cried he, wringing his hands in his turn; “you love Valentine,—that daughter of an accursed race!”\n\nNever had Morrel witnessed such an expression—never had so terrible an eye flashed before his face—never had the genius of terror he had so often seen, either on the battle-field or in the murderous nights of Algeria, shaken around him more dreadful fire. He drew back terrified.\n\nAs for Monte Cristo, after this ebullition he closed his eyes as if dazzled by internal light. In a moment he restrained himself so powerfully that the tempestuous heaving of his breast subsided, as turbulent and foaming waves yield to the sun’s genial influence when the cloud has passed. This silence, self-control, and struggle lasted about twenty seconds, then the count raised his pallid face.\n\n“See,” said he, “my dear friend, how God punishes the most thoughtless and unfeeling men for their indifference, by presenting dreadful scenes to their view. I, who was looking on, an eager and curious spectator,—I, who was watching the working of this mournful tragedy,—I, who like a wicked angel was laughing at the evil men committed protected by secrecy (a secret is easily kept by the rich and powerful), I am in my turn bitten by the serpent whose tortuous course I was watching, and bitten to the heart!”\n\nMorrel groaned.\n\n“Come, come,” continued the count, “complaints are unavailing, be a man, be strong, be full of hope, for I am here and will watch over you.”\n\nMorrel shook his head sorrowfully.\n\n“I tell you to hope. Do you understand me?” cried Monte Cristo. “Remember that I never uttered a falsehood and am never deceived. It is twelve o’clock, Maximilian; thank heaven that you came at noon rather than in the evening, or tomorrow morning. Listen, Morrel—it is noon; if Valentine is not now dead, she will not die.”\n\n“How so?” cried Morrel, “when I left her dying?”\n\nMonte Cristo pressed his hands to his forehead. What was passing in that brain, so loaded with dreadful secrets? What does the angel of light or the angel of darkness say to that mind, at once implacable and generous? God only knows.\n\nMonte Cristo raised his head once more, and this time he was calm as a child awaking from its sleep.\n\n“Maximilian,” said he, “return home. I command you not to stir—attempt nothing, not to let your countenance betray a thought, and I will send you tidings. Go.”\n\n“Oh, count, you overwhelm me with that coolness. Have you, then, power against death? Are you superhuman? Are you an angel?” And the young man, who had never shrunk from danger, shrank before Monte Cristo with indescribable terror. But Monte Cristo looked at him with so melancholy and sweet a smile, that Maximilian felt the tears filling his eyes.\n\n“I can do much for you, my friend,” replied the count. “Go; I must be alone.”\n\nMorrel, subdued by the extraordinary ascendancy Monte Cristo exercised over everything around him, did not endeavor to resist it. He pressed the count’s hand and left. He stopped one moment at the door for Baptistin, whom he saw in the Rue Matignon, and who was running.\n\nMeanwhile, Villefort and d’Avrigny had made all possible haste, Valentine had not revived from her fainting fit on their arrival, and the doctor examined the invalid with all the care the circumstances demanded, and with an interest which the knowledge of the secret intensified twofold. Villefort, closely watching his countenance and his lips, awaited the result of the examination. Noirtier, paler than even the young girl, more eager than Villefort for the decision, was watching also intently and affectionately.\n\nAt last d’Avrigny slowly uttered these words: “She is still alive!”\n\n“Still?” cried Villefort; “oh, doctor, what a dreadful word is that.”\n\n“Yes,” said the physician, “I repeat it; she is still alive, and I am astonished at it.”\n\n“But is she safe?” asked the father.\n\n“Yes, since she lives.”\n\nAt that moment d’Avrigny’s glance met Noirtier’s eye. It glistened with such extraordinary joy, so rich and full of thought, that the physician was struck. He placed the young girl again on the chair,—her lips were scarcely discernible, they were so pale and white, as well as her whole face,—and remained motionless, looking at Noirtier, who appeared to anticipate and commend all he did.\n\n“Sir,” said d’Avrigny to Villefort, “call Mademoiselle Valentine’s maid, if you please.”\n\nVillefort went himself to find her; and d’Avrigny approached Noirtier.\n\n“Have you something to tell me?” asked he. The old man winked his eyes expressively, which we may remember was his only way of expressing his approval.\n\n“Privately?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Well, I will remain with you.” At this moment Villefort returned, followed by the lady’s maid; and after her came Madame de Villefort.\n\n“What is the matter, then, with this dear child? she has just left me, and she complained of being indisposed, but I did not think seriously of it.”\n\nThe young woman with tears in her eyes and every mark of affection of a true mother, approached Valentine and took her hand. D’Avrigny continued to look at Noirtier; he saw the eyes of the old man dilate and become round, his cheeks turn pale and tremble; the perspiration stood in drops upon his forehead.\n\n“Ah,” said he, involuntarily following Noirtier’s eyes, which were fixed on Madame de Villefort, who repeated:\n\n“This poor child would be better in bed. Come, Fanny, we will put her to bed.”\n\nM. d’Avrigny, who saw that would be a means of his remaining alone with Noirtier, expressed his opinion that it was the best thing that could be done; but he forbade that anything should be given to her except what he ordered.\n\nThey carried Valentine away; she had revived, but could scarcely move or speak, so shaken was her frame by the attack. She had, however, just power to give one parting look to her grandfather, who in losing her seemed to be resigning his very soul. D’Avrigny followed the invalid, wrote a prescription, ordered Villefort to take a cabriolet, go in person to a chemist’s to get the prescribed medicine, bring it himself, and wait for him in his daughter’s room. Then, having renewed his injunction not to give Valentine anything, he went down again to Noirtier, shut the doors carefully, and after convincing himself that no one was listening:\n\n“Do you,” said he, “know anything of this young lady’s illness?”\n\n“Yes,” said the old man.\n\n“We have no time to lose; I will question, and do you answer me.” Noirtier made a sign that he was ready to answer. “Did you anticipate the accident which has happened to your granddaughter?”\n\n“Yes.” D’Avrigny reflected a moment; then approaching Noirtier:\n\n“Pardon what I am going to say,” added he, “but no indication should be neglected in this terrible situation. Did you see poor Barrois die?” Noirtier raised his eyes to heaven.\n\n“Do you know of what he died!” asked d’Avrigny, placing his hand on Noirtier’s shoulder.\n\n“Yes,” replied the old man.\n\n“Do you think he died a natural death?” A sort of smile was discernible on the motionless lips of Noirtier.\n\n“Then you have thought that Barrois was poisoned?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Do you think the poison he fell a victim to was intended for him?”\n\n“No.”\n“Do you think the same hand which unintentionally struck Barrois has now attacked Valentine?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Then will she die too?” asked d’Avrigny, fixing his penetrating gaze on Noirtier. He watched the effect of this question on the old man.\n\n“No,” replied he with an air of triumph which would have puzzled the most clever diviner.\n\n“Then you hope?” said d’Avrigny, with surprise.\n“Yes.”\n\n“What do you hope?” The old man made him understand with his eyes that he could not answer.\n\n“Ah, yes, it is true,” murmured d’Avrigny. Then, turning to Noirtier,—“Do you hope the assassin will be tried?”\n\n“No.”\n“Then you hope the poison will take no effect on Valentine?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“It is no news to you,” added d’Avrigny, “to tell you that an attempt has been made to poison her?” The old man made a sign that he entertained no doubt upon the subject. “Then how do you hope Valentine will escape?”\n\nNoirtier kept his eyes steadfastly fixed on the same spot. D’Avrigny followed the direction and saw that they were fixed on a bottle containing the mixture which he took every morning. “Ah, indeed?” said d’Avrigny, struck with a sudden thought, “has it occurred to you”—Noirtier did not let him finish.\n\n“Yes,” said he.\n\n“To prepare her system to resist poison?”\n“Yes.”\n\n“By accustoming her by degrees——”\n\n“Yes, yes, yes,” said Noirtier, delighted to be understood.\n\n“Of course. I had told you that there was brucine in the mixture I give you.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“And by accustoming her to that poison, you have endeavored to neutralize the effect of a similar poison?” Noirtier’s joy continued. “And you have succeeded,” exclaimed d’Avrigny. “Without that precaution Valentine would have died before assistance could have been procured. The dose has been excessive, but she has only been shaken by it; and this time, at any rate, Valentine will not die.”\n\nA superhuman joy expanded the old man’s eyes, which were raised towards heaven with an expression of infinite gratitude. At this moment Villefort returned.\n\n“Here, doctor,” said he, “is what you sent me for.”\n\n“Was this prepared in your presence?”\n\n“Yes,” replied the procureur.\n\n“Have you not let it go out of your hands?”\n\n“No.”\nD’Avrigny took the bottle, poured some drops of the mixture it contained in the hollow of his hand, and swallowed them.\n\n“Well,” said he, “let us go to Valentine; I will give instructions to everyone, and you, M. de Villefort, will yourself see that no one deviates from them.”\n\n40294m\nAt the moment when d’Avrigny was returning to Valentine’s room, accompanied by Villefort, an Italian priest, of serious demeanor and calm and firm tone, hired for his use the house adjoining the hotel of M. de Villefort. No one knew how the three former tenants of that house left it. About two hours afterwards its foundation was reported to be unsafe; but the report did not prevent the new occupant establishing himself there with his modest furniture the same day at five o’clock. The lease was drawn up for three, six, or nine years by the new tenant, who, according to the rule of the proprietor, paid six months in advance.\n\nThis new tenant, who, as we have said, was an Italian, was called Il Signor Giacomo Busoni. Workmen were immediately called in, and that same night the passengers at the end of the faubourg saw with surprise that carpenters and masons were occupied in repairing the lower part of the tottering house.\n\n# Chapter 95. Father and Daughter\n\nWe saw in a preceding chapter how Madame Danglars went formally to announce to Madame de Villefort the approaching marriage of Eugénie Danglars and M. Andrea Cavalcanti. This formal announcement, which implied or appeared to imply, the approval of all the persons concerned in this momentous affair, had been preceded by a scene to which our readers must be admitted. We beg them to take one step backward, and to transport themselves, the morning of that day of great catastrophes, into the showy, gilded salon we have before shown them, and which was the pride of its owner, Baron Danglars.\n\nIn this room, at about ten o’clock in the morning, the banker himself had been walking to and fro for some minutes thoughtfully and in evident uneasiness, watching both doors, and listening to every sound. When his patience was exhausted, he called his valet.\n\n“Étienne,” said he, “see why Mademoiselle Eugénie has asked me to meet her in the drawing-room, and why she makes me wait so long.”\n\nHaving given this vent to his ill-humor, the baron became more calm; Mademoiselle Danglars had that morning requested an interview with her father, and had fixed on the gilded drawing-room as the spot. The singularity of this step, and above all its formality, had not a little surprised the banker, who had immediately obeyed his daughter by repairing first to the drawing-room. Étienne soon returned from his errand.\n\n“Mademoiselle’s lady’s maid says, sir, that mademoiselle is finishing her toilette, and will be here shortly.”\n\nDanglars nodded, to signify that he was satisfied. To the world and to his servants Danglars assumed the character of the good-natured man and the indulgent father. This was one of his parts in the popular comedy he was performing,—a make-up he had adopted and which suited him about as well as the masks worn on the classic stage by paternal actors, who seen from one side, were the image of geniality, and from the other showed lips drawn down in chronic ill-temper. Let us hasten to say that in private the genial side descended to the level of the other, so that generally the indulgent man disappeared to give place to the brutal husband and domineering father.\n\n“Why the devil does that foolish girl, who pretends to wish to speak to me, not come into my study? and why on earth does she want to speak to me at all?”\n\nHe was turning this thought over in his brain for the twentieth time, when the door opened and Eugénie appeared, attired in a figured black satin dress, her hair dressed and gloves on, as if she were going to the Italian Opera.\n\n“Well, Eugénie, what is it you want with me? and why in this solemn drawing-room when the study is so comfortable?”\n\n“I quite understand why you ask, sir,” said Eugénie, making a sign that her father might be seated, “and in fact your two questions suggest fully the theme of our conversation. I will answer them both, and contrary to the usual method, the last first, because it is the least difficult. I have chosen the drawing-room, sir, as our place of meeting, in order to avoid the disagreeable impressions and influences of a banker’s study. Those gilded cashbooks, drawers locked like gates of fortresses, heaps of bank-bills, come from I know not where, and the quantities of letters from England, Holland, Spain, India, China, and Peru, have generally a strange influence on a father’s mind, and make him forget that there is in the world an interest greater and more sacred than the good opinion of his correspondents. I have, therefore, chosen this drawing-room, where you see, smiling and happy in their magnificent frames, your portrait, mine, my mother’s, and all sorts of rural landscapes and touching pastorals. I rely much on external impressions; perhaps, with regard to you, they are immaterial, but I should be no artist if I had not some fancies.”\n\n“Very well,” replied M. Danglars, who had listened to all this preamble with imperturbable coolness, but without understanding a word, since like every man burdened with thoughts of the past, he was occupied with seeking the thread of his own ideas in those of the speaker.\n\n“There is, then, the second point cleared up, or nearly so,” said Eugénie, without the least confusion, and with that masculine pointedness which distinguished her gesture and her language; “and you appear satisfied with the explanation. Now, let us return to the first. You ask me why I have requested this interview; I will tell you in two words, sir; I will not marry count Andrea Cavalcanti.”\n\nDanglars leaped from his chair and raised his eyes and arms towards heaven.\n\n40298m\n“Yes, indeed, sir,” continued Eugénie, still quite calm; “you are astonished, I see; for since this little affair began, I have not manifested the slightest opposition, and yet I am always sure, when the opportunity arrives, to oppose a determined and absolute will to people who have not consulted me, and things which displease me. However, this time, my tranquillity, or passiveness as philosophers say, proceeded from another source; it proceeded from a wish, like a submissive and devoted daughter” (a slight smile was observable on the purple lips of the young girl), “to practice obedience.”\n\n“Well?” asked Danglars.\n\n“Well, sir,” replied Eugénie, “I have tried to the very last and now that the moment has come, I feel in spite of all my efforts that it is impossible.”\n\n“But,” said Danglars, whose weak mind was at first quite overwhelmed with the weight of this pitiless logic, marking evident premeditation and force of will, “what is your reason for this refusal, Eugénie? what reason do you assign?”\n\n“My reason?” replied the young girl. “Well, it is not that the man is more ugly, more foolish, or more disagreeable than any other; no, M. Andrea Cavalcanti may appear to those who look at men’s faces and figures as a very good specimen of his kind. It is not, either, that my heart is less touched by him than any other; that would be a schoolgirl’s reason, which I consider quite beneath me. I actually love no one, sir; you know it, do you not? I do not then see why, without real necessity, I should encumber my life with a perpetual companion. Has not some sage said, ‘Nothing too much’? and another, ‘I carry all my effects with me’? I have been taught these two aphorisms in Latin and in Greek; one is, I believe, from Phædrus, and the other from Bias. Well, my dear father, in the shipwreck of life—for life is an eternal shipwreck of our hopes—I cast into the sea my useless encumbrance, that is all, and I remain with my own will, disposed to live perfectly alone, and consequently perfectly free.”\n\n“Unhappy girl, unhappy girl!” murmured Danglars, turning pale, for he knew from long experience the solidity of the obstacle he had so suddenly encountered.\n\n“Unhappy girl,” replied Eugénie, “unhappy girl, do you say, sir? No, indeed; the exclamation appears quite theatrical and affected. Happy, on the contrary, for what am I in want of? The world calls me beautiful. It is something to be well received. I like a favorable reception; it expands the countenance, and those around me do not then appear so ugly. I possess a share of wit, and a certain relative sensibility, which enables me to draw from life in general, for the support of mine, all I meet with that is good, like the monkey who cracks the nut to get at its contents. I am rich, for you have one of the first fortunes in France. I am your only daughter, and you are not so exacting as the fathers of the Porte Saint-Martin and Gaîté, who disinherit their daughters for not giving them grandchildren. Besides, the provident law has deprived you of the power to disinherit me, at least entirely, as it has also of the power to compel me to marry Monsieur This or Monsieur That. And so—being, beautiful, witty, somewhat talented, as the comic operas say, and rich—and that is happiness, sir—why do you call me unhappy?”\n\nDanglars, seeing his daughter smiling, and proud even to insolence, could not entirely repress his brutal feelings, but they betrayed themselves only by an exclamation. Under the fixed and inquiring gaze levelled at him from under those beautiful black eyebrows, he prudently turned away, and calmed himself immediately, daunted by the power of a resolute mind.\n\n“Truly, my daughter,” replied he with a smile, “you are all you boast of being, excepting one thing; I will not too hastily tell you which, but would rather leave you to guess it.”\n\nEugénie looked at Danglars, much surprised that one flower of her crown of pride, with which she had so superbly decked herself, should be disputed.\n\n“My daughter,” continued the banker, “you have perfectly explained to me the sentiments which influence a girl like you, who is determined she will not marry; now it remains for me to tell you the motives of a father like me, who has decided that his daughter shall marry.”\n\nEugénie bowed, not as a submissive daughter, but as an adversary prepared for a discussion.\n\n“My daughter,” continued Danglars, “when a father asks his daughter to choose a husband, he has always some reason for wishing her to marry. Some are affected with the mania of which you spoke just now, that of living again in their grandchildren. This is not my weakness, I tell you at once; family joys have no charm for me. I may acknowledge this to a daughter whom I know to be philosophical enough to understand my indifference, and not to impute it to me as a crime.”\n\n“This is not to the purpose,” said Eugénie; “let us speak candidly, sir; I admire candor.”\n\n“Oh,” said Danglars, “I can, when circumstances render it desirable, adopt your system, although it may not be my general practice. I will therefore proceed. I have proposed to you to marry, not for your sake, for indeed I did not think of you in the least at the moment (you admire candor, and will now be satisfied, I hope); but because it suited me to marry you as soon as possible, on account of certain commercial speculations I am desirous of entering into.” Eugénie became uneasy.\n\n40302m\n“It is just as I tell you, I assure you, and you must not be angry with me, for you have sought this disclosure. I do not willingly enter into arithmetical explanations with an artist like you, who fears to enter my study lest she should imbibe disagreeable or anti-poetic impressions and sensations. But in that same banker’s study, where you very willingly presented yourself yesterday to ask for the thousand francs I give you monthly for pocket-money, you must know, my dear young lady, that many things may be learned, useful even to a girl who will not marry. There one may learn, for instance, what, out of regard to your nervous susceptibility, I will inform you of in the drawing-room, namely, that the credit of a banker is his physical and moral life; that credit sustains him as breath animates the body; and M. de Monte Cristo once gave me a lecture on that subject, which I have never forgotten. There we may learn that as credit sinks, the body becomes a corpse, and this is what must happen very soon to the banker who is proud to own so good a logician as you for his daughter.”\n\nBut Eugénie, instead of stooping, drew herself up under the blow. “Ruined?” said she.\n\n“Exactly, my daughter; that is precisely what I mean,” said Danglars, almost digging his nails into his breast, while he preserved on his harsh features the smile of the heartless though clever man; “ruined—yes, that is it.”\n\n“Ah!” said Eugénie.\n\n“Yes, ruined! Now it is revealed, this secret so full of horror, as the tragic poet says. Now, my daughter, learn from my lips how you may alleviate this misfortune, so far as it will affect you.”\n\n“Oh,” cried Eugénie, “you are a bad physiognomist, if you imagine I deplore on my own account the catastrophe of which you warn me. I ruined? and what will that signify to me? Have I not my talent left? Can I not, like Pasta, Malibran, Grisi, acquire for myself what you would never have given me, whatever might have been your fortune, a hundred or a hundred and fifty thousand livres per annum, for which I shall be indebted to no one but myself; and which, instead of being given as you gave me those poor twelve thousand francs, with sour looks and reproaches for my prodigality, will be accompanied with acclamations, with bravos, and with flowers? And if I do not possess that talent, which your smiles prove to me you doubt, should I not still have that ardent love of independence, which will be a substitute for wealth, and which in my mind supersedes even the instinct of self-preservation? No, I grieve not on my own account, I shall always find a resource; my books, my pencils, my piano, all the things which cost but little, and which I shall be able to procure, will remain my own.\n\n“Do you think that I sorrow for Madame Danglars? Undeceive yourself again; either I am greatly mistaken, or she has provided against the catastrophe which threatens you, and, which will pass over without affecting her. She has taken care for herself,—at least I hope so,—for her attention has not been diverted from her projects by watching over me. She has fostered my independence by professedly indulging my love for liberty. Oh, no, sir; from my childhood I have seen too much, and understood too much, of what has passed around me, for misfortune to have an undue power over me. From my earliest recollections, I have been beloved by no one—so much the worse; that has naturally led me to love no one—so much the better—now you have my profession of faith.”\n\n“Then,” said Danglars, pale with anger, which was not at all due to offended paternal love,—“then, mademoiselle, you persist in your determination to accelerate my ruin?”\n\n“Your ruin? I accelerate your ruin? What do you mean? I do not understand you.”\n\n“So much the better, I have a ray of hope left; listen.”\n\n“I am all attention,” said Eugénie, looking so earnestly at her father that it was an effort for the latter to endure her unrelenting gaze.\n\n“M. Cavalcanti,” continued Danglars, “is about to marry you, and will place in my hands his fortune, amounting to three million livres.”\n\n“That is admirable!” said Eugénie with sovereign contempt, smoothing her gloves out one upon the other.\n\n“You think I shall deprive you of those three millions,” said Danglars; “but do not fear it. They are destined to produce at least ten. I and a brother banker have obtained a grant of a railway, the only industrial enterprise which in these days promises to make good the fabulous prospects that Law once held out to the eternally deluded Parisians, in the fantastic Mississippi scheme. As I look at it, a millionth part of a railway is worth fully as much as an acre of waste land on the banks of the Ohio. We make in our case a deposit, on a mortgage, which is an advance, as you see, since we gain at least ten, fifteen, twenty, or a hundred livres’ worth of iron in exchange for our money. Well, within a week I am to deposit four millions for my share; the four millions, I promise you, will produce ten or twelve.”\n\n“But during my visit to you the day before yesterday, sir, which you appear to recollect so well,” replied Eugénie, “I saw you arranging a deposit—is not that the term?—of five millions and a half; you even pointed it out to me in two drafts on the treasury, and you were astonished that so valuable a paper did not dazzle my eyes like lightning.”\n\n“Yes, but those five millions and a half are not mine, and are only a proof of the great confidence placed in me; my title of popular banker has gained me the confidence of charitable institutions, and the five millions and a half belong to them; at any other time I should not have hesitated to make use of them, but the great losses I have recently sustained are well known, and, as I told you, my credit is rather shaken. That deposit may be at any moment withdrawn, and if I had employed it for another purpose, I should bring on me a disgraceful bankruptcy. I do not despise bankruptcies, believe me, but they must be those which enrich, not those which ruin. Now, if you marry M. Cavalcanti, and I get the three millions, or even if it is thought I am going to get them, my credit will be restored, and my fortune, which for the last month or two has been swallowed up in gulfs which have been opened in my path by an inconceivable fatality, will revive. Do you understand me?”\n\n“Perfectly; you pledge me for three millions, do you not?”\n\n“The greater the amount, the more flattering it is to you; it gives you an idea of your value.”\n\n“Thank you. One word more, sir; do you promise me to make what use you can of the report of the fortune M. Cavalcanti will bring without touching the money? This is no act of selfishness, but of delicacy. I am willing to help rebuild your fortune, but I will not be an accomplice in the ruin of others.”\n\n“But since I tell you,” cried Danglars, “that with these three million——”\n\n“Do you expect to recover your position, sir, without touching those three million?”\n\n“I hope so, if the marriage should take place and confirm my credit.”\n\n“Shall you be able to pay M. Cavalcanti the five hundred thousand francs you promise for my dowry?”\n\n“He shall receive them on returning from the mayor’s20.”\n\n“Very well!”\n\n“What next? what more do you want?”\n\n“I wish to know if, in demanding my signature, you leave me entirely free in my person?”\n\n“Absolutely.”\n\n“Then, as I said before, sir,—very well; I am ready to marry M. Cavalcanti.”\n\n“But what are you up to?”\n\n“Ah, that is my affair. What advantage should I have over you, if knowing your secret I were to tell you mine?”\n\nDanglars bit his lips. “Then,” said he, “you are ready to pay the official visits, which are absolutely indispensable?”\n\n“Yes,” replied Eugénie.\n\n“And to sign the contract in three days?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Then, in my turn, I also say, very well!”\n\nDanglars pressed his daughter’s hand in his. But, extraordinary to relate, the father did not say, “Thank you, my child,” nor did the daughter smile at her father.\n\n“Is the conference ended?” asked Eugénie, rising.\n\nDanglars motioned that he had nothing more to say. Five minutes afterwards the piano resounded to the touch of Mademoiselle d’Armilly’s fingers, and Mademoiselle Danglars was singing Brabantio’s malediction on Desdemona. At the end of the piece Étienne entered, and announced to Eugénie that the horses were to the carriage, and that the baroness was waiting for her to pay her visits. We have seen them at Villefort’s; they proceeded then on their course.\n40306m\n\n# Chapter 96. The Contract\n\n50019m\nThree days after the scene we have just described, namely towards five o’clock in the afternoon of the day fixed for the signature of the contract between Mademoiselle Eugénie Danglars and Andrea Cavalcanti, whom the banker persisted in calling prince, a fresh breeze was stirring the leaves in the little garden in front of the Count of Monte Cristo’s house, and the count was preparing to go out. While his horses were impatiently pawing the ground, held in by the coachman, who had been seated a quarter of an hour on his box, the elegant phaeton with which we are familiar rapidly turned the angle of the entrance-gate, and cast out on the doorsteps M. Andrea Cavalcanti, as decked up and gay as if he were going to marry a princess.\n\nHe inquired after the count with his usual familiarity, and ascending lightly to the first story met him at the top of the stairs.\n\nThe count stopped on seeing the young man. As for Andrea, he was launched, and when he was once launched nothing stopped him.\n\n“Ah, good morning, my dear count,” said he.\n\n“Ah, M. Andrea,” said the latter, with his half-jesting tone; “how do you do?”\n\n“Charmingly, as you see. I am come to talk to you about a thousand things; but, first tell me, were you going out or just returned?”\n\n“I was going out, sir.”\n\n“Then, in order not to hinder you, I will get up with you if you please in your carriage, and Tom shall follow with my phaeton in tow.”\n\n“No,” said the count, with an imperceptible smile of contempt, for he had no wish to be seen in the young man’s society,—“no; I prefer listening to you here, my dear M. Andrea; we can chat better in-doors, and there is no coachman to overhear our conversation.”\n\nThe count returned to a small drawing-room on the first floor, sat down, and crossing his legs motioned to the young man to take a seat also. Andrea assumed his gayest manner.\n\n“You know, my dear count,” said he, “the ceremony is to take place this evening. At nine o’clock the contract is to be signed at my father-in-law’s.”\n\n“Ah, indeed?” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“What; is it news to you? Has not M. Danglars informed you of the ceremony?”\n\n“Oh, yes,” said the count; “I received a letter from him yesterday, but I do not think the hour was mentioned.”\n\n“Possibly my father-in-law trusted to its general notoriety.”\n\n“Well,” said Monte Cristo, “you are fortunate, M. Cavalcanti; it is a most suitable alliance you are contracting, and Mademoiselle Danglars is a handsome girl.”\n\n“Yes, indeed she is,” replied Cavalcanti, in a very modest tone.\n\n“Above all, she is very rich,—at least, I believe so,” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“Very rich, do you think?” replied the young man.\n\n“Doubtless; it is said M. Danglars conceals at least half of his fortune.”\n\n“And he acknowledges fifteen or twenty millions,” said Andrea with a look sparkling with joy.\n\n“Without reckoning,” added Monte Cristo, “that he is on the eve of entering into a sort of speculation already in vogue in the United States and in England, but quite novel in France.”\n\n“Yes, yes, I know what you mean,—the railway, of which he has obtained the grant, is it not?”\n\n“Precisely; it is generally believed he will gain ten millions by that affair.”\n\n“Ten millions! Do you think so? It is magnificent!” said Cavalcanti, who was quite confounded at the metallic sound of these golden words.\n\n“Without reckoning,” replied Monte Cristo, “that all his fortune will come to you, and justly too, since Mademoiselle Danglars is an only daughter. Besides, your own fortune, as your father assured me, is almost equal to that of your betrothed. But enough of money matters. Do you know, M. Andrea, I think you have managed this affair rather skilfully?”\n\n“Not badly, by any means,” said the young man; “I was born for a diplomatist.”\n\n“Well, you must become a diplomatist; diplomacy, you know, is something that is not to be acquired; it is instinctive. Have you lost your heart?”\n\n“Indeed, I fear it,” replied Andrea, in the tone in which he had heard Dorante or Valère reply to Alceste21 at the Théâtre Français.\n\n“Is your love returned?”\n\n“I suppose so,” said Andrea with a triumphant smile, “since I am accepted. But I must not forget one grand point.”\n\n“Which?”\n\n“That I have been singularly assisted.”\n\n“Nonsense.”\n\n“I have, indeed.”\n\n“By circumstances?”\n\n“By me? Not at all, prince,” said Monte Cristo laying a marked stress on the title, “what have I done for you? Are not your name, your social position, and your merit sufficient?”\n\n“No,” said Andrea,—“no; it is useless for you to say so, count. I maintain that the position of a man like you has done more than my name, my social position, and my merit.”\n\n“You are completely mistaken, sir,” said Monte Cristo coldly, who felt the perfidious manœuvre of the young man, and understood the bearing of his words; “you only acquired my protection after the influence and fortune of your father had been ascertained; for, after all, who procured for me, who had never seen either you or your illustrious father, the pleasure of your acquaintance?—two of my good friends, Lord Wilmore and the Abbé Busoni. What encouraged me not to become your surety, but to patronize you?—your father’s name, so well known in Italy and so highly honored. Personally, I do not know you.”\n\nThis calm tone and perfect ease made Andrea feel that he was, for the moment, restrained by a more muscular hand than his own, and that the restraint could not be easily broken through.\n\n“Oh, then my father has really a very large fortune, count?”\n\n“It appears so, sir,” replied Monte Cristo.\n\n“Do you know if the marriage settlement he promised me has come?”\n\n“I have been advised of it.”\n\n“But the three millions?”\n\n“The three millions are probably on the road.”\n\n“Then I shall really have them?”\n\n“Oh, well,” said the count, “I do not think you have yet known the want of money.”\n\nAndrea was so surprised that he pondered the matter for a moment. Then, arousing from his reverie:\n\n“Now, sir, I have one request to make to you, which you will understand, even if it should be disagreeable to you.”\n\n“Proceed,” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“I have formed an acquaintance, thanks to my good fortune, with many noted persons, and have, at least for the moment, a crowd of friends. But marrying, as I am about to do, before all Paris, I ought to be supported by an illustrious name, and in the absence of the paternal hand some powerful one ought to lead me to the altar; now, my father is not coming to Paris, is he?”\n\n“He is old, covered with wounds, and suffers dreadfully, he says, in travelling.”\n\n“I understand; well, I am come to ask a favor of you.”\n\n“Of me?”\n\n“Yes, of you.”\n\n“And pray what may it be?”\n\n“Well, to take his part.”\n\n“Ah, my dear sir! What?—after the varied relations I have had the happiness to sustain towards you, can it be that you know me so little as to ask such a thing? Ask me to lend you half a million and, although such a loan is somewhat rare, on my honor, you would annoy me less! Know, then, what I thought I had already told you, that in participation in this world’s affairs, more especially in their moral aspects, the Count of Monte Cristo has never ceased to entertain the scruples and even the superstitions of the East. I, who have a seraglio at Cairo, one at Smyrna, and one at Constantinople, preside at a wedding?—never!”\n\n“Then you refuse me?”\n\n“Decidedly; and were you my son or my brother I would refuse you in the same way.”\n\n“But what must be done?” said Andrea, disappointed.\n\n“You said just now that you had a hundred friends.”\n\n“Very true, but you introduced me at M. Danglars’.”\n\n“Not at all! Let us recall the exact facts. You met him at a dinner party at my house, and you introduced yourself at his house; that is a totally different affair.”\n\n“Yes, but, by my marriage, you have forwarded that.”\n\n“I?—not in the least, I beg you to believe. Recollect what I told you when you asked me to propose you. ‘Oh, I never make matches, my dear prince, it is my settled principle.’” Andrea bit his lips.\n\n50025m\n“But, at least, you will be there?”\n\n“Will all Paris be there?”\n\n“Oh, certainly.”\n\n“Well, like all Paris, I shall be there too,” said the count.\n\n“And will you sign the contract?”\n\n“I see no objection to that; my scruples do not go thus far.”\n\n“Well, since you will grant me no more, I must be content with what you give me. But one word more, count.”\n\n“What is it?”\n\n“Advice.”\n\n“Be careful; advice is worse than a service.”\n\n“Oh, you can give me this without compromising yourself.”\n\n“Tell me what it is.”\n\n“Is my wife’s fortune five hundred thousand livres?”\n\n“That is the sum M. Danglars himself announced.”\n\n“Must I receive it, or leave it in the hands of the notary?”\n\n“This is the way such affairs are generally arranged when it is wished to do them stylishly: Your two solicitors appoint a meeting, when the contract is signed, for the next or the following day; then they exchange the two portions, for which they each give a receipt; then, when the marriage is celebrated, they place the amount at your disposal as the chief member of the alliance.”\n\n“Because,” said Andrea, with a certain ill-concealed uneasiness, “I thought I heard my father-in-law say that he intended embarking our property in that famous railway affair of which you spoke just now.”\n\n“Well,” replied Monte Cristo, “it will be the way, everybody says, of trebling your fortune in twelve months. Baron Danglars is a good father, and knows how to calculate.”\n\n“In that case,” said Andrea, “everything is all right, excepting your refusal, which quite grieves me.”\n\n“You must attribute it only to natural scruples under similar circumstances.”\n\n“Well,” said Andrea, “let it be as you wish. This evening, then, at nine o’clock.”\n\n“Adieu till then.”\n\nNotwithstanding a slight resistance on the part of Monte Cristo, whose lips turned pale, but who preserved his ceremonious smile, Andrea seized the count’s hand, pressed it, jumped into his phaeton, and disappeared.\n\nThe four or five remaining hours before nine o’clock arrived, Andrea employed in riding, paying visits,—designed to induce those of whom he had spoken to appear at the banker’s in their gayest equipages,—dazzling them by promises of shares in schemes which have since turned every brain, and in which Danglars was just taking the initiative.\n\nIn fact, at half-past eight in the evening the grand salon, the gallery adjoining, and the three other drawing-rooms on the same floor, were filled with a perfumed crowd, who sympathized but little in the event, but who all participated in that love of being present wherever there is anything fresh to be seen. An Academician would say that the entertainments of the fashionable world are collections of flowers which attract inconstant butterflies, famished bees, and buzzing drones.\n\n50025m\nNo one could deny that the rooms were splendidly illuminated; the light streamed forth on the gilt mouldings and the silk hangings; and all the bad taste of decorations, which had only their richness to boast of, shone in its splendor. Mademoiselle Eugénie was dressed with elegant simplicity in a figured white silk dress, and a white rose half concealed in her jet black hair was her only ornament, unaccompanied by a single jewel. Her eyes, however, betrayed that perfect confidence which contradicted the girlish simplicity of this modest attire.\n\nMadame Danglars was chatting at a short distance with Debray, Beauchamp, and Château-Renaud. Debray was admitted to the house for this grand ceremony, but on the same plane with everyone else, and without any particular privilege. M. Danglars, surrounded by deputies and men connected with the revenue, was explaining a new theory of taxation which he intended to adopt when the course of events had compelled the government to call him into the ministry. Andrea, on whose arm hung one of the most consummate dandies of the Opera, was explaining to him rather cleverly, since he was obliged to be bold to appear at ease, his future projects, and the new luxuries he meant to introduce to Parisian fashions with his hundred and seventy-five thousand livres per annum.\n\nThe crowd moved to and fro in the rooms like an ebb and flow of turquoises, rubies, emeralds, opals, and diamonds. As usual, the oldest women were the most decorated, and the ugliest the most conspicuous. If there was a beautiful lily, or a sweet rose, you had to search for it, concealed in some corner behind a mother with a turban, or an aunt with a bird-of-paradise.\n\nAt each moment, in the midst of the crowd, the buzzing, and the laughter, the door-keeper’s voice was heard announcing some name well known in the financial department, respected in the army, or illustrious in the literary world, and which was acknowledged by a slight movement in the different groups. But for one whose privilege it was to agitate that ocean of human waves, how many were received with a look of indifference or a sneer of disdain!\n\nAt the moment when the hand of the massive time-piece, representing Endymion asleep, pointed to nine on its golden face, and the hammer, the faithful type of mechanical thought, struck nine times, the name of the Count of Monte Cristo resounded in its turn, and as if by an electric shock all the assembly turned towards the door. The count was dressed in black and with his habitual simplicity; his white waistcoat displayed his expansive noble chest and his black stock was singularly noticeable because of its contrast with the deadly paleness of his face. His only jewellery was a chain, so fine that the slender gold thread was scarcely perceptible on his white waistcoat.\n\nA circle was immediately formed around the door. The count perceived at one glance Madame Danglars at one end of the drawing-room, M. Danglars at the other, and Eugénie in front of him. He first advanced towards the baroness, who was chatting with Madame de Villefort, who had come alone, Valentine being still an invalid; and without turning aside, so clear was the road left for him, he passed from the baroness to Eugénie, whom he complimented in such rapid and measured terms, that the proud artist was quite struck. Near her was Mademoiselle Louise d’Armilly, who thanked the count for the letters of introduction he had so kindly given her for Italy, which she intended immediately to make use of. On leaving these ladies he found himself with Danglars, who had advanced to meet him.\n\nHaving accomplished these three social duties, Monte Cristo stopped, looking around him with that expression peculiar to a certain class, which seems to say, “I have done my duty, now let others do theirs.”\n\nAndrea, who was in an adjoining room, had shared in the sensation caused by the arrival of Monte Cristo, and now came forward to pay his respects to the count. He found him completely surrounded; all were eager to speak to him, as is always the case with those whose words are few and weighty. The solicitors arrived at this moment and arranged their scrawled papers on the velvet cloth embroidered with gold which covered the table prepared for the signature; it was a gilt table supported on lions’ claws. One of the notaries sat down, the other remained standing. They were about to proceed to the reading of the contract, which half Paris assembled was to sign. All took their places, or rather the ladies formed a circle, while the gentlemen (more indifferent to the restraints of what Boileau calls the _style énergique_) commented on the feverish agitation of Andrea, on M. Danglars’ riveted attention, Eugénie’s composure, and the light and sprightly manner in which the baroness treated this important affair.\n\nThe contract was read during a profound silence. But as soon as it was finished, the buzz was redoubled through all the drawing-rooms; the brilliant sums, the rolling millions which were to be at the command of the two young people, and which crowned the display of the wedding presents and the young lady’s diamonds, which had been made in a room entirely appropriated for that purpose, had exercised to the full their delusions over the envious assembly.\n\nMademoiselle Danglars’ charms were heightened in the opinion of the young men, and for the moment seemed to outvie the sun in splendor. As for the ladies, it is needless to say that while they coveted the millions, they thought they did not need them for themselves, as they were beautiful enough without them. Andrea, surrounded by his friends, complimented, flattered, beginning to believe in the reality of his dream, was almost bewildered. The notary solemnly took the pen, flourished it above his head, and said:\n\n“Gentlemen, we are about to sign the contract.”\n\nThe baron was to sign first, then the representative of M. Cavalcanti, senior, then the baroness, afterwards the “future couple,” as they are styled in the abominable phraseology of legal documents.\n\nThe baron took the pen and signed, then the representative. The baroness approached, leaning on Madame de Villefort’s arm.\n\n“My dear,” said she, as she took the pen, “is it not vexatious? An unexpected incident, in the affair of murder and theft at the Count of Monte Cristo’s, in which he nearly fell a victim, deprives us of the pleasure of seeing M. de Villefort.”\n\n“Indeed?” said M. Danglars, in the same tone in which he would have said, “Oh, well, what do I care?”\n\n“As a matter of fact,” said Monte Cristo, approaching, “I am much afraid that I am the involuntary cause of his absence.”\n\n“What, you, count?” said Madame Danglars, signing; “if you are, take care, for I shall never forgive you.”\n\nAndrea pricked up his ears.\n\n“But it is not my fault, as I shall endeavor to prove.”\n\nEveryone listened eagerly; Monte Cristo who so rarely opened his lips, was about to speak.\n\n“You remember,” said the count, during the most profound silence, “that the unhappy wretch who came to rob me died at my house; the supposition is that he was stabbed by his accomplice, on attempting to leave it.”\n\n“Yes,” said Danglars.\n\n“In order that his wounds might be examined he was undressed, and his clothes were thrown into a corner, where the police picked them up, with the exception of the waistcoat, which they overlooked.”\n\nAndrea turned pale, and drew towards the door; he saw a cloud rising in the horizon, which appeared to forebode a coming storm.\n\n“Well, this waistcoat was discovered today, covered with blood, and with a hole over the heart.” The ladies screamed, and two or three prepared to faint. “It was brought to me. No one could guess what the dirty rag could be; I alone suspected that it was the waistcoat of the murdered man. My valet, in examining this mournful relic, felt a paper in the pocket and drew it out; it was a letter addressed to you, baron.”\n\n“To me?” cried Danglars.\n\n“Yes, indeed, to you; I succeeded in deciphering your name under the blood with which the letter was stained,” replied Monte Cristo, amid the general outburst of amazement.\n\n“But,” asked Madame Danglars, looking at her husband with uneasiness, “how could that prevent M. de Villefort——”\n\n“In this simple way, madame,” replied Monte Cristo; “the waistcoat and the letter were both what is termed circumstantial evidence; I therefore sent them to the king’s attorney. You understand, my dear baron, that legal methods are the safest in criminal cases; it was, perhaps, some plot against you.” Andrea looked steadily at Monte Cristo and disappeared in the second drawing-room.\n\n“Possibly,” said Danglars; “was not this murdered man an old galley-slave?”\n50025m\n\n“Yes,” replied the count; “a felon named Caderousse.” Danglars turned slightly pale; Andrea reached the anteroom beyond the little drawing-room.\n\n“But go on signing,” said Monte Cristo; “I perceive that my story has caused a general emotion, and I beg to apologize to you, baroness, and to Mademoiselle Danglars.”\n\nThe baroness, who had signed, returned the pen to the notary.\n\n“Prince Cavalcanti,” said the latter; “Prince Cavalcanti, where are you?”\n\n“Andrea, Andrea,” repeated several young people, who were already on sufficiently intimate terms with him to call him by his Christian name.\n\n“Call the prince; inform him that it is his turn to sign,” cried Danglars to one of the floorkeepers.\n\nBut at the same instant the crowd of guests rushed in alarm into the principal salon as if some frightful monster had entered the apartments, _quærens quem devoret_. There was, indeed, reason to retreat, to be alarmed, and to scream. An officer was placing two soldiers at the door of each drawing-room, and was advancing towards Danglars, preceded by a commissary of police, girded with his scarf. Madame Danglars uttered a scream and fainted. Danglars, who thought himself threatened (certain consciences are never calm),—Danglars even before his guests showed a countenance of abject terror.\n\n“What is the matter, sir?” asked Monte Cristo, advancing to meet the commissioner.\n\n“Which of you gentlemen,” asked the magistrate, without replying to the count, “answers to the name of Andrea Cavalcanti?”\n\nA cry of astonishment was heard from all parts of the room. They searched; they questioned.\n\n“But who then is Andrea Cavalcanti?” asked Danglars in amazement.\n\n“A galley-slave, escaped from confinement at Toulon.”\n\n“And what crime has he committed?”\n\n“He is accused,” said the commissary with his inflexible voice, “of having assassinated the man named Caderousse, his former companion in prison, at the moment he was making his escape from the house of the Count of Monte Cristo.”\n\nMonte Cristo cast a rapid glance around him. Andrea was gone.\n\n# Chapter 97. The Departure for Belgium\n\nA few minutes after the scene of confusion produced in the salons of M. Danglars by the unexpected appearance of the brigade of soldiers, and by the disclosure which had followed, the mansion was deserted with as much rapidity as if a case of plague or of cholera morbus had broken out among the guests.\n\nIn a few minutes, through all the doors, down all the staircases, by every exit, everyone hastened to retire, or rather to fly; for it was a situation where the ordinary condolences,—which even the best friends are so eager to offer in great catastrophes,—were seen to be utterly futile. There remained in the banker’s house only Danglars, closeted in his study, and making his statement to the officer of gendarmes; Madame Danglars, terrified, in the boudoir with which we are acquainted; and Eugénie, who with haughty air and disdainful lip had retired to her room with her inseparable companion, Mademoiselle Louise d’Armilly.\n\nAs for the numerous servants (more numerous that evening than usual, for their number was augmented by cooks and butlers from the Café de Paris), venting on their employers their anger at what they termed the insult to which they had been subjected, they collected in groups in the hall, in the kitchens, or in their rooms, thinking very little of their duty, which was thus naturally interrupted. Of all this household, only two persons deserve our notice; these are Mademoiselle Eugénie Danglars and Mademoiselle Louise d’Armilly.\n\nThe betrothed had retired, as we said, with haughty air, disdainful lip, and the demeanor of an outraged queen, followed by her companion, who was paler and more disturbed than herself. On reaching her room Eugénie locked her door, while Louise fell on a chair.\n\n“Ah, what a dreadful thing,” said the young musician; “who would have suspected it? M. Andrea Cavalcanti a murderer—a galley-slave escaped—a convict!”\n\nAn ironical smile curled the lip of Eugénie. “In truth, I was fated,” said she. “I escaped the Morcerf only to fall into the Cavalcanti.”\n\n“Oh, do not confound the two, Eugénie.”\n\n“Hold your tongue! The men are all infamous, and I am happy to be able now to do more than detest them—I despise them.”\n\n“What shall we do?” asked Louise.\n\n“What shall we do?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Why, the same we had intended doing three days since—set off.”\n\n“What?—although you are not now going to be married, you intend still——”\n\n“Listen, Louise. I hate this life of the fashionable world, always ordered, measured, ruled, like our music-paper. What I have always wished for, desired, and coveted, is the life of an artist, free and independent, relying only on my own resources, and accountable only to myself. Remain here? What for?—that they may try, a month hence, to marry me again; and to whom?—M. Debray, perhaps, as it was once proposed. No, Louise, no! This evening’s adventure will serve for my excuse. I did not seek one, I did not ask for one. God sends me this, and I hail it joyfully!”\n\n“How strong and courageous you are!” said the fair, frail girl to her brunette companion.\n\n“Did you not yet know me? Come, Louise, let us talk of our affairs. The post-chaise——”\n\n“Was happily bought three days since.”\n\n“Have you had it sent where we are to go for it?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Our passport?”\n\n“Here it is.”\n\nAnd Eugénie, with her usual precision, opened a printed paper, and read:\n\n“M. Léon d’Armilly, twenty years of age; profession, artist; hair black, eyes black; travelling with his sister.”\n\n“Capital! How did you get this passport?”\n\n“When I went to ask M. de Monte Cristo for letters to the directors of the theatres at Rome and Naples, I expressed my fears of travelling as a woman; he perfectly understood them, and undertook to procure for me a man’s passport, and two days after I received this, to which I have added with my own hand, ‘travelling with his sister.’”\n50035m\n\n“Well,” said Eugénie cheerfully, “we have then only to pack up our trunks; we shall start the evening of the signing of the contract, instead of the evening of the wedding—that is all.”\n\n“But consider the matter seriously, Eugénie!”\n\n“Oh, I am done with considering! I am tired of hearing only of market reports, of the end of the month, of the rise and fall of Spanish funds, of Haitian bonds. Instead of that, Louise—do you understand?—air, liberty, melody of birds, plains of Lombardy, Venetian canals, Roman palaces, the Bay of Naples. How much have we, Louise?”\n\nThe young girl to whom this question was addressed drew from an inlaid secretaire a small portfolio with a lock, in which she counted twenty-three bank-notes.\n\n“Twenty-three thousand francs,” said she.\n\n“And as much, at least, in pearls, diamonds, and jewels,” said Eugénie. “We are rich. With forty-five thousand francs we can live like princesses for two years, and comfortably for four; but before six months—you with your music, and I with my voice—we shall double our capital. Come, you shall take charge of the money, I of the jewel-box; so that if one of us had the misfortune to lose her treasure, the other would still have hers left. Now, the portmanteau—let us make haste—the portmanteau!”\n\n“Stop!” said Louise, going to listen at Madame Danglars’ door.\n\n“What do you fear?”\n\n“That we may be discovered.”\n\n“The door is locked.”\n\n“They may tell us to open it.”\n\n“They may if they like, but we will not.”\n\n“You are a perfect Amazon, Eugénie!” And the two young girls began to heap into a trunk all the things they thought they should require.\n\n“There now,” said Eugénie, “while I change my costume do you lock the portmanteau.” Louise pressed with all the strength of her little hands on the top of the portmanteau.\n\n“But I cannot,” said she; “I am not strong enough; do you shut it.”\n\n“Ah, you do well to ask,” said Eugénie, laughing; “I forgot that I was Hercules, and you only the pale Omphale!”\n\nAnd the young girl, kneeling on the top, pressed the two parts of the portmanteau together, and Mademoiselle d’Armilly passed the bolt of the padlock through. When this was done, Eugénie opened a drawer, of which she kept the key, and took from it a wadded violet silk travelling cloak.\n\n“Here,” said she, “you see I have thought of everything; with this cloak you will not be cold.”\n\n“But you?”\n\n“Oh, I am never cold, you know! Besides, with these men’s clothes——”\n\n“Will you dress here?”\n\n“Certainly.”\n\n“Shall you have time?”\n\n“Do not be uneasy, you little coward! All our servants are busy, discussing the grand affair. Besides, what is there astonishing, when you think of the grief I ought to be in, that I shut myself up?—tell me!”\n\n“No, truly—you comfort me.”\n“Come and help me.”\n\nFrom the same drawer she took a man’s complete costume, from the boots to the coat, and a provision of linen, where there was nothing superfluous, but every requisite. Then, with a promptitude which indicated that this was not the first time she had amused herself by adopting the garb of the opposite sex, Eugénie drew on the boots and pantaloons, tied her cravat, buttoned her waistcoat up to the throat, and put on a coat which admirably fitted her beautiful figure.\n\n“Oh, that is very good—indeed, it is very good!” said Louise, looking at her with admiration; “but that beautiful black hair, those magnificent braids, which made all the ladies sigh with envy,—will they go under a man’s hat like the one I see down there?”\n\n“You shall see,” said Eugénie. And with her left hand seizing the thick mass, which her long fingers could scarcely grasp, she took in her right hand a pair of long scissors, and soon the steel met through the rich and splendid hair, which fell in a cluster at her feet as she leaned back to keep it from her coat. Then she grasped the front hair, which she also cut off, without expressing the least regret; on the contrary, her eyes sparkled with greater pleasure than usual under her ebony eyebrows.\n50039m\n\n“Oh, the magnificent hair!” said Louise, with regret.\n\n“And am I not a hundred times better thus?” cried Eugénie, smoothing the scattered curls of her hair, which had now quite a masculine appearance; “and do you not think me handsomer so?”\n\n“Oh, you are beautiful—always beautiful!” cried Louise. “Now, where are you going?”\n\n“To Brussels, if you like; it is the nearest frontier. We can go to Brussels, Liège, Aix-la-Chapelle; then up the Rhine to Strasbourg. We will cross Switzerland, and go down into Italy by the Saint-Gothard. Will that do?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“What are you looking at?”\n\n“I am looking at you; indeed you are adorable like that! One would say you were carrying me off.”\n\n“And they would be right, _pardieu!_”\n\n“Oh, I think you swore, Eugénie.”\n\nAnd the two young girls, whom everyone might have thought plunged in grief, the one on her own account, the other from interest in her friend, burst out laughing, as they cleared away every visible trace of the disorder which had naturally accompanied the preparations for their escape. Then, having blown out the lights, the two fugitives, looking and listening eagerly, with outstretched necks, opened the door of a dressing-room which led by a side staircase down to the yard,—Eugénie going first, and holding with one arm the portmanteau, which by the opposite handle Mademoiselle d’Armilly scarcely raised with both hands. The yard was empty; the clock was striking twelve. The porter was not yet gone to bed. Eugénie approached softly, and saw the old man sleeping soundly in an armchair in his lodge. She returned to Louise, took up the portmanteau, which she had placed for a moment on the ground, and they reached the archway under the shadow of the wall.\n\nEugénie concealed Louise in an angle of the gateway, so that if the porter chanced to awake he might see but one person. Then placing herself in the full light of the lamp which lit the yard:\n\n“Gate!” cried she, with her finest contralto voice, and rapping at the window.\n\nThe porter got up as Eugénie expected, and even advanced some steps to recognize the person who was going out, but seeing a young man striking his boot impatiently with his riding-whip, he opened it immediately. Louise slid through the half-open gate like a snake, and bounded lightly forward. Eugénie, apparently calm, although in all probability her heart beat somewhat faster than usual, went out in her turn.\n\nA porter was passing and they gave him the portmanteau; then the two young girls, having told him to take it to No. 36, Rue de la Victoire, walked behind this man, whose presence comforted Louise. As for Eugénie, she was as strong as a Judith or a Delilah. They arrived at the appointed spot. Eugénie ordered the porter to put down the portmanteau, gave him some pieces of money, and having rapped at the shutter sent him away. The shutter where Eugénie had rapped was that of a little laundress, who had been previously warned, and was not yet gone to bed. She opened the door.\n\n“Mademoiselle,” said Eugénie, “let the porter get the post-chaise from the coach-house, and fetch some post-horses from the hotel. Here are five francs for his trouble.”\n\n“Indeed,” said Louise, “I admire you, and I could almost say respect you.” The laundress looked on in astonishment, but as she had been promised twenty louis, she made no remark.\n\nIn a quarter of an hour the porter returned with a post-boy and horses, which were harnessed, and put in the post-chaise in a minute, while the porter fastened the portmanteau on with the assistance of a cord and strap.\n\n“Here is the passport,” said the postilion, “which way are we going, young gentleman?”\n\n“To Fontainebleau,” replied Eugénie with an almost masculine voice.\n\n“What do you say?” said Louise.\n\n“I am giving them the slip,” said Eugénie; “this woman to whom we have given twenty louis may betray us for forty; we will soon alter our direction.”\n\nAnd the young girl jumped into the britzka, which was admirably arranged for sleeping in, without scarcely touching the step.\n\n“You are always right,” said the music teacher, seating herself by the side of her friend.\n\nA quarter of an hour afterwards the postilion, having been put in the right road, passed with a crack of his whip through the gateway of the Barrière Saint-Martin.\n\n“Ah,” said Louise, breathing freely, “here we are out of Paris.”\n\n“Yes, my dear, the abduction is an accomplished fact,” replied Eugénie.\n\n“Yes, and without violence,” said Louise.\n\n“I shall bring that forward as an extenuating circumstance,” replied Eugénie.\n\nThese words were lost in the noise which the carriage made in rolling over the pavement of La Villette. M. Danglars no longer had a daughter.\n\n# Chapter 98. The Bell and Bottle Tavern\n\nAnd now let us leave Mademoiselle Danglars and her friend pursuing their way to Brussels, and return to poor Andrea Cavalcanti, so inopportunely interrupted in his rise to fortune. Notwithstanding his youth, Master Andrea was a very skilful and intelligent boy. We have seen that on the first rumor which reached the salon he had gradually approached the door, and crossing two or three rooms at last disappeared. But we have forgotten to mention one circumstance, which nevertheless ought not to be omitted; in one of the rooms he crossed, the _trousseau_ of the bride-elect was on exhibition. There were caskets of diamonds, cashmere shawls, Valenciennes lace, English veils, and in fact all the tempting things, the bare mention of which makes the hearts of young girls bound with joy, and which is called the _corbeille_.22 Now, in passing through this room, Andrea proved himself not only to be clever and intelligent, but also provident, for he helped himself to the most valuable of the ornaments before him.\n\nFurnished with this plunder, Andrea leaped with a lighter heart from the window, intending to slip through the hands of the gendarmes. Tall and well proportioned as an ancient gladiator, and muscular as a Spartan, he walked for a quarter of an hour without knowing where to direct his steps, actuated by the sole idea of getting away from the spot where if he lingered he knew that he would surely be taken. Having passed through the Rue du Mont-Blanc, guided by the instinct which leads thieves always to take the safest path, he found himself at the end of the Rue La Fayette. There he stopped, breathless and panting. He was quite alone; on one side was the vast wilderness of the Saint-Lazare, on the other, Paris enshrouded in darkness.\n\n“Am I to be captured?” he cried; “no, not if I can use more activity than my enemies. My safety is now a mere question of speed.”\n\nAt this moment he saw a cab at the top of the Faubourg Poissonnière. The dull driver, smoking his pipe, was plodding along toward the limits of the Faubourg Saint-Denis, where no doubt he ordinarily had his station.\n\n“Ho, friend!” said Benedetto.\n\n“What do you want, sir?” asked the driver.\n\n“Is your horse tired?”\n\n“Tired? oh, yes, tired enough—he has done nothing the whole of this blessed day! Four wretched fares, and twenty sous over, making in all seven francs, are all that I have earned, and I ought to take ten to the owner.”\n\n“Will you add these twenty francs to the seven you have?”\n\n“With pleasure, sir; twenty francs are not to be despised. Tell me what I am to do for this.”\n\n“A very easy thing, if your horse isn’t tired.”\n\n“I tell you he’ll go like the wind,—only tell me which way to drive.”\n\n“Towards the Louvres.”\n\n“Ah, I know the way—you get good sweetened rum over there.”\n\n“Exactly so; I merely wish to overtake one of my friends, with whom I am going to hunt tomorrow at Chapelle-en-Serval. He should have waited for me here with a cabriolet till half-past eleven; it is twelve, and, tired of waiting, he must have gone on.”\n\n“It is likely.”\n\n“Well, will you try and overtake him?”\n\n“Nothing I should like better.”\n\n“If you do not overtake him before we reach Bourget you shall have twenty francs; if not before Louvres, thirty.”\n\n“And if we do overtake him?”\n\n“Forty,” said Andrea, after a moment’s hesitation, at the end of which he remembered that he might safely promise.\n\n“That’s all right,” said the man; “hop in, and we’re off! Who-o-o-pla!”\n\nAndrea got into the cab, which passed rapidly through the Faubourg Saint-Denis, along the Faubourg Saint-Martin, crossed the barrier, and threaded its way through the interminable Villette. They never overtook the chimerical friend, yet Andrea frequently inquired of people on foot whom he passed and at the inns which were not yet closed, for a green cabriolet and bay horse; and as there are a great many cabriolets to be seen on the road to the Low Countries, and as nine-tenths of them are green, the inquiries increased at every step. Everyone had just seen it pass; it was only five hundred, two hundred, one hundred steps in advance; at length they reached it, but it was not the friend. Once the cab was also passed by a calash rapidly whirled along by two post-horses.\n\n“Ah,” said Cavalcanti to himself, “if I only had that britzka, those two good post-horses, and above all the passport that carries them on!” And he sighed deeply.\n\nThe calash contained Mademoiselle Danglars and Mademoiselle d’Armilly.\n\n“Hurry, hurry!” said Andrea, “we must overtake him soon.”\n\nAnd the poor horse resumed the desperate gallop it had kept up since leaving the barrier, and arrived steaming at Louvres.\n\n“Certainly,” said Andrea, “I shall not overtake my friend, but I shall kill your horse, therefore I had better stop. Here are thirty francs; I will sleep at the _Cheval Rouge_, and will secure a place in the first coach. Good-night, friend.”\n\nAnd Andrea, after placing six pieces of five francs each in the man’s hand, leaped lightly on to the pathway. The cabman joyfully pocketed the sum, and turned back on his road to Paris. Andrea pretended to go towards the hotel of the _Cheval Rouge_, but after leaning an instant against the door, and hearing the last sound of the cab, which was disappearing from view, he went on his road, and with a lusty stride soon traversed the space of two leagues. Then he rested; he must be near Chapelle-en-Serval, where he pretended to be going.\n\nIt was not fatigue that stayed Andrea here; it was that he might form some resolution, adopt some plan. It would be impossible to make use of a diligence, equally so to engage post-horses; to travel either way a passport was necessary. It was still more impossible to remain in the department of the Oise, one of the most open and strictly guarded in France; this was quite out of the question, especially to a man like Andrea, perfectly conversant with criminal matters.\n\nHe sat down by the side of the moat, buried his face in his hands and reflected. Ten minutes after he raised his head; his resolution was made. He threw some dust over the topcoat, which he had found time to unhook from the antechamber and button over his ball costume, and going to Chapelle-en-Serval he knocked loudly at the door of the only inn in the place.\n\nThe host opened.\n\n“My friend,” said Andrea, “I was coming from Mortefontaine to Senlis, when my horse, which is a troublesome creature, stumbled and threw me. I must reach Compiègne tonight, or I shall cause deep anxiety to my family. Could you let me hire a horse of you?”\n\nAn innkeeper has always a horse to let, whether it be good or bad. The host called the stable-boy, and ordered him to saddle _Le Blanc_ then he awoke his son, a child of seven years, whom he ordered to ride before the gentleman and bring back the horse. Andrea gave the innkeeper twenty francs, and in taking them from his pocket dropped a visiting card. This belonged to one of his friends at the Café de Paris, so that the innkeeper, picking it up after Andrea had left, was convinced that he had let his horse to the Count of Mauléon, 25 Rue Saint-Dominique, that being the name and address on the card.\n\n_Le Blanc_ was not a fast animal, but he kept up an easy, steady pace; in three hours and a half Andrea had traversed the nine leagues which separated him from Compiègne, and four o’clock struck as he reached the place where the coaches stop. There is an excellent tavern at Compiègne, well remembered by those who have ever been there. Andrea, who had often stayed there in his rides about Paris, recollected the Bell and Bottle inn; he turned around, saw the sign by the light of a reflected lamp, and having dismissed the child, giving him all the small coin he had about him, he began knocking at the door, very reasonably concluding that having now three or four hours before him he had best fortify himself against the fatigues of the morrow by a sound sleep and a good supper. A waiter opened the door.\n\n“My friend,” said Andrea, “I have been dining at Saint-Jean-aux-Bois, and expected to catch the coach which passes by at midnight, but like a fool I have lost my way, and have been walking for the last four hours in the forest. Show me into one of those pretty little rooms which overlook the court, and bring me a cold fowl and a bottle of Bordeaux.”\n\nThe waiter had no suspicions; Andrea spoke with perfect composure, he had a cigar in his mouth, and his hands in the pocket of his top coat; his clothes were fashionably made, his chin smooth, his boots irreproachable; he looked merely as if he had stayed out very late, that was all. While the waiter was preparing his room, the hostess arose; Andrea assumed his most charming smile, and asked if he could have No. 3, which he had occupied on his last stay at Compiègne. Unfortunately, No. 3 was engaged by a young man who was travelling with his sister. Andrea appeared in despair, but consoled himself when the hostess assured him that No. 7, prepared for him, was situated precisely the same as No. 3, and while warming his feet and chatting about the last races at Chantilly, he waited until they announced his room to be ready.\n\nAndrea had not spoken without cause of the pretty rooms looking out upon the court of the Bell Hotel, which with its triple galleries like those of a theatre, with the jessamine and clematis twining round the light columns, forms one of the prettiest entrances to an inn that you can imagine. The fowl was tender, the wine old, the fire clear and sparkling, and Andrea was surprised to find himself eating with as good an appetite as though nothing had happened. Then he went to bed and almost immediately fell into that deep sleep which is sure to visit men of twenty years of age, even when they are torn with remorse. Now, here we are obliged to own that Andrea ought to have felt remorse, but that he did not.\n\nThis was the plan which had appealed to him to afford the best chance of his security. Before daybreak he would awake, leave the inn after rigorously paying his bill, and reaching the forest, he would, under pretence of making studies in painting, test the hospitality of some peasants, procure himself the dress of a woodcutter and a hatchet, casting off the lion’s skin to assume that of the woodman; then, with his hands covered with dirt, his hair darkened by means of a leaden comb, his complexion embrowned with a preparation for which one of his old comrades had given him the recipe, he intended, by following the wooded districts, to reach the nearest frontier, walking by night and sleeping in the day in the forests and quarries, and only entering inhabited regions to buy a loaf from time to time.\n\nOnce past the frontier, Andrea proposed making money of his diamonds; and by uniting the proceeds to ten bank-notes he always carried about with him in case of accident, he would then find himself possessor of about 50,000 livres, which he philosophically considered as no very deplorable condition after all. Moreover, he reckoned much on the interest of the Danglars to hush up the rumor of their own misadventures. These were the reasons which, added to the fatigue, caused Andrea to sleep so soundly. In order that he might wake early he did not close the shutters, but contented himself with bolting the door and placing on the table an unclasped and long-pointed knife, whose temper he well knew, and which was never absent from him.\n\nAbout seven in the morning Andrea was awakened by a ray of sunlight, which played, warm and brilliant, upon his face. In all well-organized brains, the predominating idea—and there always is one—is sure to be the last thought before sleeping, and the first upon waking in the morning. Andrea had scarcely opened his eyes when his predominating idea presented itself, and whispered in his ear that he had slept too long. He jumped out of bed and ran to the window. A gendarme was crossing the court. A gendarme is one of the most striking objects in the world, even to a man void of uneasiness; but for one who has a timid conscience, and with good cause too, the yellow, blue, and white uniform is really very alarming.\n\n“Why is that gendarme there?” asked Andrea of himself.\n\nThen, all at once, he replied, with that logic which the reader has, doubtless, remarked in him, “There is nothing astonishing in seeing a gendarme at an inn; instead of being astonished, let me dress myself.” And the youth dressed himself with a facility his valet de chambre had failed to rob him of during the two months of fashionable life he had led in Paris.\n\n“Now then,” said Andrea, while dressing himself, “I’ll wait till he leaves, and then I’ll slip away.”\n\n50047m\nAnd, saying this, Andrea, who had now put on his boots and cravat, stole gently to the window, and a second time lifted up the muslin curtain. Not only was the first gendarme still there, but the young man now perceived a second yellow, blue, and white uniform at the foot of the staircase, the only one by which he could descend, while a third, on horseback, holding a musket in his fist, was posted as a sentinel at the great street-door which alone afforded the means of egress. The appearance of the third gendarme settled the matter, for a crowd of curious loungers was extended before him, effectually blocking the entrance to the hotel.\n\n“They’re after me!” was Andrea’s first thought. “_Diable!_”\n\nA pallor overspread the young man’s forehead, and he looked around him with anxiety. His room, like all those on the same floor, had but one outlet to the gallery in the sight of everybody. “I am lost!” was his second thought; and, indeed, for a man in Andrea’s situation, an arrest meant the assizes, trial, and death,—death without mercy or delay.\n\nFor a moment he convulsively pressed his head within his hands, and during that brief period he became nearly mad with terror; but soon a ray of hope glimmered in the multitude of thoughts which bewildered his mind, and a faint smile played upon his white lips and pallid cheeks. He looked around and saw the objects of his search upon the chimney-piece; they were a pen, ink, and paper. With forced composure he dipped the pen in the ink, and wrote the following lines upon a sheet of paper:\n\n“I have no money to pay my bill, but I am not a dishonest man; I leave behind me as a pledge this pin, worth ten times the amount. I shall be excused for leaving at daybreak, for I was ashamed.”\n\nHe then drew the pin from his cravat and placed it on the paper. This done, instead of leaving the door fastened, he drew back the bolts and even placed the door ajar, as though he had left the room, forgetting to close it, and slipping into the chimney like a man accustomed to that kind of gymnastic exercise, after replacing the chimney-board, which represented Achilles with Deidamia, and effacing the very marks of his feet upon the ashes, he commenced climbing the hollow tunnel, which afforded him the only means of escape left.\n\nAt this precise time, the first gendarme Andrea had noticed walked upstairs, preceded by the commissary of police, and supported by the second gendarme who guarded the staircase and was himself reinforced by the one stationed at the door.\n\nAndrea was indebted for this visit to the following circumstances. At daybreak, the telegraphs were set at work in all directions, and almost immediately the authorities in every district had exerted their utmost endeavors to arrest the murderer of Caderousse. Compiègne, that royal residence and fortified town, is well furnished with authorities, gendarmes, and commissaries of police; they therefore began operations as soon as the telegraphic despatch arrived, and the Bell and Bottle being the best-known hotel in the town, they had naturally directed their first inquiries there.\n\nNow, besides the reports of the sentinels guarding the Hôtel de Ville, which is next door to the Bell and Bottle, it had been stated by others that a number of travellers had arrived during the night. The sentinel who was relieved at six o’clock in the morning, remembered perfectly that, just as he was taking his post a few minutes past four, a young man arrived on horseback, with a little boy before him. The young man, having dismissed the boy and horse, knocked at the door of the hotel, which was opened, and again closed after his entrance. This late arrival had attracted much suspicion, and the young man being no other than Andrea, the commissary and gendarme, who was a brigadier, directed their steps towards his room. They found the door ajar.\n\n“Oh, oh,” said the brigadier, who thoroughly understood the trick; “a bad sign to find the door open! I would rather find it triply bolted.”\n\nAnd, indeed, the little note and pin upon the table confirmed, or rather corroborated, the sad truth. Andrea had fled. We say corroborated, because the brigadier was too experienced to be convinced by a single proof. He glanced around, looked in the bed, shook the curtains, opened the closets, and finally stopped at the chimney. Andrea had taken the precaution to leave no traces of his feet in the ashes, but still it was an outlet, and in this light was not to be passed over without serious investigation.\n\nThe brigadier sent for some sticks and straw, and having filled the chimney with them, set a light to it. The fire crackled, and the smoke ascended like the dull vapor from a volcano; but still no prisoner fell down, as they expected. The fact was, that Andrea, at war with society ever since his youth, was quite as deep as a gendarme, even though he were advanced to the rank of brigadier, and quite prepared for the fire, he had climbed out on the roof and was crouching down against the chimney-pots.\n50049m\n\nAt one time he thought he was saved, for he heard the brigadier exclaim in a loud voice, to the two gendarmes, “He is not here!” But venturing to peep, he perceived that the latter, instead of retiring, as might have been reasonably expected upon this announcement, were watching with increased attention.\n\nIt was now his turn to look about him; the Hôtel de Ville, a massive sixteenth century building, was on his right; anyone could descend from the openings in the tower, and examine every corner of the roof below, and Andrea expected momentarily to see the head of a gendarme appear at one of these openings. If once discovered, he knew he would be lost, for the roof afforded no chance of escape; he therefore resolved to descend, not through the same chimney by which he had come up, but by a similar one conducting to another room.\n\nHe looked around for a chimney from which no smoke issued, and having reached it, he disappeared through the orifice without being seen by anyone. At the same minute, one of the little windows of the Hôtel de Ville was thrown open, and the head of a gendarme appeared. For an instant it remained motionless as one of the stone decorations of the building, then after a long sigh of disappointment the head disappeared. The brigadier, calm and dignified as the law he represented, passed through the crowd, without answering the thousand questions addressed to him, and re-entered the hotel.\n\n“Well?” asked the two gendarmes.\n\n“Well, my boys,” said the brigadier, “the brigand must really have escaped early this morning; but we will send to the Villers-Coterets and Noyon roads, and search the forest, when we shall catch him, no doubt.”\n\nThe honorable functionary had scarcely expressed himself thus, in that intonation which is peculiar to brigadiers of the gendarmerie, when a loud scream, accompanied by the violent ringing of a bell, resounded through the court of the hotel.\n\n“Ah, what is that?” cried the brigadier.\n\n“Some traveller seems impatient,” said the host. “What number was it that rang?”\n“Number 3.”\n\n“Run, waiter!”\n\nAt this moment the screams and ringing were redoubled.\n\n“Aha!” said the brigadier, stopping the servant, “the person who is ringing appears to want something more than a waiter; we will attend upon him with a gendarme. Who occupies Number 3?”\n\n“The little fellow who arrived last night in a post-chaise with his sister, and who asked for an apartment with two beds.”\n\nThe bell here rang for the third time, with another shriek of anguish.\n\n“Follow me, Mr. Commissary!” said the brigadier; “tread in my steps.”\n\n“Wait an instant,” said the host; “Number 3 has two staircases,—inside and outside.”\n\n“Good,” said the brigadier. “I will take charge of the inside one. Are the carbines loaded?”\n\n“Yes, brigadier.”\n\n“Well, you guard the exterior, and if he attempts to fly, fire upon him; he must be a great criminal, from what the telegraph says.”\n\nThe brigadier, followed by the commissary, disappeared by the inside staircase, accompanied by the noise which his assertions respecting Andrea had excited in the crowd.\n\nThis is what had happened: Andrea had very cleverly managed to descend two-thirds of the chimney, but then his foot slipped, and notwithstanding his endeavors, he came into the room with more speed and noise than he intended. It would have signified little had the room been empty, but unfortunately it was occupied. Two ladies, sleeping in one bed, were awakened by the noise, and fixing their eyes upon the spot whence the sound proceeded, they saw a man. One of these ladies, the fair one, uttered those terrible shrieks which resounded through the house, while the other, rushing to the bell-rope, rang with all her strength. Andrea, as we can see, was surrounded by misfortune.\n\n“For pity’s sake,” he cried, pale and bewildered, without seeing whom he was addressing,—“for pity’s sake do not call assistance! Save me!—I will not harm you.”\n\n“Andrea, the murderer!” cried one of the ladies.\n\n“Eugénie! Mademoiselle Danglars!” exclaimed Andrea, stupefied.\n\n“Help, help!” cried Mademoiselle d’Armilly, taking the bell from her companion’s hand, and ringing it yet more violently.\n\n“Save me, I am pursued!” said Andrea, clasping his hands. “For pity, for mercy’s sake do not deliver me up!”\n\n“It is too late, they are coming,” said Eugénie.\n\n“Well, conceal me somewhere; you can say you were needlessly alarmed; you can turn their suspicions and save my life!”\n\n50053m\nThe two ladies, pressing closely to one another, and drawing the bedclothes tightly around them, remained silent to this supplicating voice, repugnance and fear taking possession of their minds.\n\n“Well, be it so,” at length said Eugénie; “return by the same road you came, and we will say nothing about you, unhappy wretch.”\n\n“Here he is, here he is!” cried a voice from the landing; “here he is! I see him!”\n\nThe brigadier had put his eye to the keyhole, and had discovered Andrea in a posture of entreaty. A violent blow from the butt end of the musket burst open the lock, two more forced out the bolts, and the broken door fell in. Andrea ran to the other door, leading to the gallery, ready to rush out; but he was stopped short, and he stood with his body a little thrown back, pale, and with the useless knife in his clenched hand.\n\n“Fly, then!” cried Mademoiselle d’Armilly, whose pity returned as her fears diminished; “fly!”\n\n“Or kill yourself!” said Eugénie (in a tone which a Vestal in the amphitheatre would have used, when urging the victorious gladiator to finish his vanquished adversary). Andrea shuddered, and looked on the young girl with an expression which proved how little he understood such ferocious honor.\n\n“Kill myself?” he cried, throwing down his knife; “why should I do so?”\n\n“Why, you said,” answered Mademoiselle Danglars, “that you would be condemned to die like the worst criminals.”\n\n50055m\n“Bah,” said Cavalcanti, crossing his arms, “one has friends.”\n\nThe brigadier advanced to him, sword in hand.\n\n“Come, come,” said Andrea, “sheathe your sword, my fine fellow; there is no occasion to make such a fuss, since I give myself up;” and he held out his hands to be manacled.\n\nThe two girls looked with horror upon this shameful metamorphosis, the man of the world shaking off his covering and appearing as a galley-slave. Andrea turned towards them, and with an impertinent smile asked, “Have you any message for your father, Mademoiselle Danglars, for in all probability I shall return to Paris?”\n\nEugénie covered her face with her hands.\n\n“Oh, oh!” said Andrea, “you need not be ashamed, even though you did post after me. Was I not nearly your husband?”\n50056m\n\nAnd with this raillery Andrea went out, leaving the two girls a prey to their own feelings of shame, and to the comments of the crowd. An hour after they stepped into their calash, both dressed in feminine attire. The gate of the hotel had been closed to screen them from sight, but they were forced, when the door was open, to pass through a throng of curious glances and whispering voices.\n\nEugénie closed her eyes; but though she could not see, she could hear, and the sneers of the crowd reached her in the carriage.\n\n“Oh, why is not the world a wilderness?” she exclaimed, throwing herself into the arms of Mademoiselle d’Armilly, her eyes sparkling with the same kind of rage which made Nero wish that the Roman world had but one neck, that he might sever it at a single blow.\n\nThe next day they stopped at the Hôtel de Flandre, at Brussels. The same evening Andrea was incarcerated in the Conciergerie.\n\n# Chapter 99. The Law\n\nWe have seen how quietly Mademoiselle Danglars and Mademoiselle d’Armilly accomplished their transformation and flight; the fact being that everyone was too much occupied in his or her own affairs to think of theirs.\n\nWe will leave the banker contemplating the enormous magnitude of his debt before the phantom of bankruptcy, and follow the baroness, who after being momentarily crushed under the weight of the blow which had struck her, had gone to seek her usual adviser, Lucien Debray. The baroness had looked forward to this marriage as a means of ridding her of a guardianship which, over a girl of Eugénie’s character, could not fail to be rather a troublesome undertaking; for in the tacit relations which maintain the bond of family union, the mother, to maintain her ascendancy over her daughter, must never fail to be a model of wisdom and a type of perfection.\n\nNow, Madame Danglars feared Eugénie’s sagacity and the influence of Mademoiselle d’Armilly; she had frequently observed the contemptuous expression with which her daughter looked upon Debray,—an expression which seemed to imply that she understood all her mother’s amorous and pecuniary relationships with the intimate secretary; moreover, she saw that Eugénie detested Debray, not only because he was a source of dissension and scandal under the paternal roof, but because she had at once classed him in that catalogue of bipeds whom Plato endeavors to withdraw from the appellation of men, and whom Diogenes designated as animals upon two legs without feathers.\n\nUnfortunately, in this world of ours, each person views things through a certain medium, and so is prevented from seeing in the same light as others, and Madame Danglars, therefore, very much regretted that the marriage of Eugénie had not taken place, not only because the match was good, and likely to insure the happiness of her child, but because it would also set her at liberty. She ran therefore to Debray, who, after having, like the rest of Paris, witnessed the contract scene and the scandal attending it, had retired in haste to his club, where he was chatting with some friends upon the events which served as a subject of conversation for three-fourths of that city known as the capital of the world.\n\nAt the precise time when Madame Danglars, dressed in black and concealed in a long veil, was ascending the stairs leading to Debray’s apartments, notwithstanding the assurances of the concierge that the young man was not at home, Debray was occupied in repelling the insinuations of a friend, who tried to persuade him that after the terrible scene which had just taken place he ought, as a friend of the family, to marry Mademoiselle Danglars and her two millions. Debray did not defend himself very warmly, for the idea had sometimes crossed his mind; still, when he recollected the independent, proud spirit of Eugénie, he positively rejected it as utterly impossible, though the same thought again continually recurred and found a resting-place in his heart. Tea, play, and the conversation, which had become interesting during the discussion of such serious affairs, lasted till one o’clock in the morning.\n\nMeanwhile Madame Danglars, veiled and uneasy, awaited the return of Debray in the little green room, seated between two baskets of flowers, which she had that morning sent, and which, it must be confessed, Debray had himself arranged and watered with so much care that his absence was half excused in the eyes of the poor woman.\n\nAt twenty minutes to twelve, Madame Danglars, tired of waiting, returned home. Women of a certain grade are like prosperous grisettes in one respect, they seldom return home after twelve o’clock. The baroness returned to the hotel with as much caution as Eugénie used in leaving it; she ran lightly upstairs, and with an aching heart entered her apartment, contiguous, as we know, to that of Eugénie. She was fearful of exciting any remark, and believed firmly in her daughter’s innocence and fidelity to the paternal roof. She listened at Eugénie’s door, and hearing no sound tried to enter, but the bolts were in place. Madame Danglars then concluded that the young girl had been overcome with the terrible excitement of the evening, and had gone to bed and to sleep. She called the maid and questioned her.\n\n“Mademoiselle Eugénie,” said the maid, “retired to her apartment with Mademoiselle d’Armilly; they then took tea together, after which they desired me to leave, saying that they needed me no longer.”\n\nSince then the maid had been below, and like everyone else she thought the young ladies were in their own room; Madame Danglars, therefore, went to bed without a shadow of suspicion, and began to muse over the recent events. In proportion as her memory became clearer, the occurrences of the evening were revealed in their true light; what she had taken for confusion was a tumult; what she had regarded as something distressing, was in reality a disgrace. And then the baroness remembered that she had felt no pity for poor Mercédès, who had been afflicted with as severe a blow through her husband and son.\n\n“Eugénie,” she said to herself, “is lost, and so are we. The affair, as it will be reported, will cover us with shame; for in a society such as ours satire inflicts a painful and incurable wound. How fortunate that Eugénie is possessed of that strange character which has so often made me tremble!”\n\nAnd her glance was turned towards heaven, where a mysterious Providence disposes all things, and out of a fault, nay, even a vice, sometimes produces a blessing. And then her thoughts, cleaving through space like a bird in the air, rested on Cavalcanti. This Andrea was a wretch, a robber, an assassin, and yet his manners showed the effects of a sort of education, if not a complete one; he had been presented to the world with the appearance of an immense fortune, supported by an honorable name. How could she extricate herself from this labyrinth? To whom would she apply to help her out of this painful situation? Debray, to whom she had run, with the first instinct of a woman towards the man she loves, and who yet betrays her,—Debray could but give her advice, she must apply to someone more powerful than he.\n\nThe baroness then thought of M. de Villefort. It was M. de Villefort who had remorselessly brought misfortune into her family, as though they had been strangers. But, no; on reflection, the procureur was not a merciless man; and it was not the magistrate, slave to his duties, but the friend, the loyal friend, who roughly but firmly cut into the very core of the corruption; it was not the executioner, but the surgeon, who wished to withdraw the honor of Danglars from ignominious association with the disgraced young man they had presented to the world as their son-in-law. And since Villefort, the friend of Danglars, had acted in this way, no one could suppose that he had been previously acquainted with, or had lent himself to, any of Andrea’s intrigues. Villefort’s conduct, therefore, upon reflection, appeared to the baroness as if shaped for their mutual advantage. But the inflexibility of the procureur should stop there; she would see him the next day, and if she could not make him fail in his duties as a magistrate, she would, at least, obtain all the indulgence he could allow. She would invoke the past, recall old recollections; she would supplicate him by the remembrance of guilty, yet happy days. M. de Villefort would stifle the affair; he had only to turn his eyes on one side, and allow Andrea to fly, and follow up the crime under that shadow of guilt called contempt of court. And after this reasoning she slept easily.\n\nAt nine o’clock next morning she arose, and without ringing for her maid or giving the least sign of her activity, she dressed herself in the same simple style as on the previous night; then running downstairs, she left the hotel, walked to the Rue de Provence, called a cab, and drove to M. de Villefort’s house.\n\nFor the last month this wretched house had presented the gloomy appearance of a lazaretto infected with the plague. Some of the apartments were closed within and without; the shutters were only opened to admit a minute’s air, showing the scared face of a footman, and immediately afterwards the window would be closed, like a gravestone falling on a sepulchre, and the neighbors would say to each other in a low voice, “Will there be another funeral today at the procureur’s house?”\n\nMadame Danglars involuntarily shuddered at the desolate aspect of the mansion; descending from the cab, she approached the door with trembling knees, and rang the bell. Three times did the bell ring with a dull, heavy sound, seeming to participate, in the general sadness, before the concierge appeared and peeped through the door, which he opened just wide enough to allow his words to be heard. He saw a lady, a fashionable, elegantly dressed lady, and yet the door remained almost closed.\n\n“Do you intend opening the door?” said the baroness.\n\n“First, madame, who are you?”\n\n“Who am I? You know me well enough.”\n\n“We no longer know anyone, madame.”\n\n“You must be mad, my friend,” said the baroness.\n\n“Where do you come from?”\n\n“Oh, this is too much!”\n\n“Madame, these are my orders; excuse me. Your name?”\n\n“The baroness Danglars; you have seen me twenty times.”\n\n“Possibly, madame. And now, what do you want?”\n\n“Oh, how extraordinary! I shall complain to M. de Villefort of the impertinence of his servants.”\n\n“Madame, this is precaution, not impertinence; no one enters here without an order from M. d’Avrigny, or without speaking to the procureur.”\n\n“Well, I have business with the procureur.”\n\n“Is it pressing business?”\n\n“You can imagine so, since I have not even brought my carriage out yet. But enough of this—here is my card, take it to your master.”\n\n“Madame will await my return?”\n“Yes; go.”\n\nThe concierge closed the door, leaving Madame Danglars in the street. She had not long to wait; directly afterwards the door was opened wide enough to admit her, and when she had passed through, it was again shut. Without losing sight of her for an instant, the concierge took a whistle from his pocket as soon as they entered the court, and blew it. The valet de chambre appeared on the door-steps.\n\n“You will excuse this poor fellow, madame,” he said, as he preceded the baroness, “but his orders are precise, and M. de Villefort begged me to tell you that he could not act otherwise.”\n\nIn the court showing his merchandise, was a tradesman who had been admitted with the same precautions. The baroness ascended the steps; she felt herself strongly infected with the sadness which seemed to magnify her own, and still guided by the valet de chambre, who never lost sight of her for an instant, she was introduced to the magistrate’s study.\n\nPreoccupied as Madame Danglars had been with the object of her visit, the treatment she had received from these underlings appeared to her so insulting, that she began by complaining of it. But Villefort, raising his head, bowed down by grief, looked up at her with so sad a smile that her complaints died upon her lips.\n\n“Forgive my servants,” he said, “for a terror I cannot blame them for; from being suspected they have become suspicious.”\n\nMadame Danglars had often heard of the terror to which the magistrate alluded, but without the evidence of her own eyesight she could never have believed that the sentiment had been carried so far.\n\n“You too, then, are unhappy?” she said.\n\n“Yes, madame,” replied the magistrate.\n\n“Then you pity me!”\n\n“And you understand what brings me here?”\n\n“You wish to speak to me about the circumstance which has just happened?”\n\n“Yes, sir,—a fearful misfortune.”\n\n“You mean a mischance.”\n\n“A mischance?” repeated the baroness.\n\n“Alas, madame,” said the procureur with his imperturbable calmness of manner, “I consider those alone misfortunes which are irreparable.”\n\n“And do you suppose this will be forgotten?”\n\n“Everything will be forgotten, madame,” said Villefort. “Your daughter will be married tomorrow, if not today—in a week, if not tomorrow; and I do not think you can regret the intended husband of your daughter.”\n\nMadame Danglars gazed on Villefort, stupefied to find him so almost insultingly calm. “Am I come to a friend?” she asked in a tone full of mournful dignity.\n\n“You know that you are, madame,” said Villefort, whose pale cheeks became slightly flushed as he gave her the assurance. And truly this assurance carried him back to different events from those now occupying the baroness and him.\n\n“Well, then, be more affectionate, my dear Villefort,” said the baroness. “Speak to me not as a magistrate, but as a friend; and when I am in bitter anguish of spirit, do not tell me that I ought to be gay.” Villefort bowed.\n\n“When I hear misfortunes named, madame,” he said, “I have within the last few months contracted the bad habit of thinking of my own, and then I cannot help drawing up an egotistical parallel in my mind. That is the reason that by the side of my misfortunes yours appear to me mere mischances; that is why my dreadful position makes yours appear enviable. But this annoys you; let us change the subject. You were saying, madame——”\n\n“I came to ask you, my friend,” said the baroness, “what will be done with this impostor?”\n\n“Impostor,” repeated Villefort; “certainly, madame, you appear to extenuate some cases, and exaggerate others. Impostor, indeed!—M. Andrea Cavalcanti, or rather M. Benedetto, is nothing more nor less than an assassin!”\n\n“Sir, I do not deny the justice of your correction, but the more severely you arm yourself against that unfortunate man, the more deeply will you strike our family. Come, forget him for a moment, and instead of pursuing him, let him go.”\n\n“You are too late, madame; the orders are issued.”\n\n“Well, should he be arrested—do they think they will arrest him?”\n\n“I hope so.”\n\n“If they should arrest him (I know that sometimes prisons afford means of escape), will you leave him in prison?”\n\nThe procureur shook his head.\n\n“At least keep him there till my daughter be married.”\n\n“Impossible, madame; justice has its formalities.”\n\n“What, even for me?” said the baroness, half jesting, half in earnest.\n\n“For all, even for myself among the rest,” replied Villefort.\n50063m\n\n“Ah!” exclaimed the baroness, without expressing the ideas which the exclamation betrayed. Villefort looked at her with that piercing glance which reads the secrets of the heart.\n\n“Yes, I know what you mean,” he said; “you refer to the terrible rumors spread abroad in the world, that the deaths which have kept me in mourning for the last three months, and from which Valentine has only escaped by a miracle, have not happened by natural means.”\n\n“I was not thinking of that,” replied Madame Danglars quickly.\n\n“Yes, you were thinking of it, and with justice. You could not help thinking of it, and saying to yourself, ‘you, who pursue crime so vindictively, answer now, why are there unpunished crimes in your dwelling?’” The baroness became pale. “You were saying this, were you not?”\n\n“Well, I own it.”\n\n“I will answer you.”\n\nVillefort drew his armchair nearer to Madame Danglars; then resting both hands upon his desk he said in a voice more hollow than usual:\n\n“There are crimes which remain unpunished because the criminals are unknown, and we might strike the innocent instead of the guilty; but when the culprits are discovered” (Villefort here extended his hand toward a large crucifix placed opposite to his desk)—“when they are discovered, I swear to you, by all I hold most sacred, that whoever they may be they shall die. Now, after the oath I have just taken, and which I will keep, madame, dare you ask for mercy for that wretch!”\n\n“But, sir, are you sure he is as guilty as they say?”\n\n“Listen; this is his description: ‘Benedetto, condemned, at the age of sixteen, for five years to the galleys for forgery.’ He promised well, as you see—first a runaway, then an assassin.”\n\n“And who is this wretch?”\n\n“Who can tell?—a vagabond, a Corsican.”\n\n“Has no one owned him?”\n\n“No one; his parents are unknown.”\n\n“But who was the man who brought him from Lucca?”\n\n“Another rascal like himself, perhaps his accomplice.” The baroness clasped her hands.\n\n“Villefort,” she exclaimed in her softest and most captivating manner.\n\n“For Heaven’s sake, madame,” said Villefort, with a firmness of expression not altogether free from harshness—“for Heaven’s sake, do not ask pardon of me for a guilty wretch! What am I?—the law. Has the law any eyes to witness your grief? Has the law ears to be melted by your sweet voice? Has the law a memory for all those soft recollections you endeavor to recall? No, madame; the law has commanded, and when it commands it strikes. You will tell me that I am a living being, and not a code—a man, and not a volume. Look at me, madame—look around me. Has mankind treated me as a brother? Have men loved me? Have they spared me? Has anyone shown the mercy towards me that you now ask at my hands? No, madame, they struck me, always struck me!\n50065m\n\n“Woman, siren that you are, do you persist in fixing on me that fascinating eye, which reminds me that I ought to blush? Well, be it so; let me blush for the faults you know, and perhaps—perhaps for even more than those! But having sinned myself,—it may be more deeply than others,—I never rest till I have torn the disguises from my fellow-creatures, and found out their weaknesses. I have always found them; and more,—I repeat it with joy, with triumph,—I have always found some proof of human perversity or error. Every criminal I condemn seems to me living evidence that I am not a hideous exception to the rest. Alas, alas, alas; all the world is wicked; let us therefore strike at wickedness!”\n\nVillefort pronounced these last words with a feverish rage, which gave a ferocious eloquence to his words.\n\n“But”’ said Madame Danglars, resolving to make a last effort, “this young man, though a murderer, is an orphan, abandoned by everybody.”\n\n“So much the worse, or rather, so much the better; it has been so ordained that he may have none to weep his fate.”\n\n“But this is trampling on the weak, sir.”\n\n“The weakness of a murderer!”\n\n“His dishonor reflects upon us.”\n\n“Is not death in my house?”\n\n“Oh, sir,” exclaimed the baroness, “you are without pity for others, well, then, I tell you they will have no mercy on you!”\n\n“Be it so!” said Villefort, raising his arms to heaven with a threatening gesture.\n\n“At least, delay the trial till the next assizes; we shall then have six months before us.”\n\n“No, madame,” said Villefort; “instructions have been given. There are yet five days left; five days are more than I require. Do you not think that I also long for forgetfulness? While working night and day, I sometimes lose all recollection of the past, and then I experience the same sort of happiness I can imagine the dead feel; still, it is better than suffering.”\n\n“But, sir, he has fled; let him escape—inaction is a pardonable offence.”\n\n“I tell you it is too late; early this morning the telegraph was employed, and at this very minute——”\n\n“Sir,” said the valet de chambre, entering the room, “a dragoon has brought this despatch from the Minister of the Interior.”\n\nVillefort seized the letter, and hastily broke the seal. Madame Danglars trembled with fear; Villefort started with joy.\n\n“Arrested!” he exclaimed; “he was taken at Compiègne, and all is over.”\n\nMadame Danglars rose from her seat, pale and cold.\n\n“Adieu, sir,” she said.\n\n“Adieu, madame,” replied the king’s attorney, as in an almost joyful manner he conducted her to the door. Then, turning to his desk, he said, striking the letter with the back of his right hand:\n\n“Come, I had a forgery, three robberies, and two cases of arson, I only wanted a murder, and here it is. It will be a splendid session!”\n\n# Chapter 100. The Apparition\n\nAs the procureur had told Madame Danglars, Valentine was not yet recovered. Bowed down with fatigue, she was indeed confined to her bed; and it was in her own room, and from the lips of Madame de Villefort, that she heard all the strange events we have related; we mean the flight of Eugénie and the arrest of Andrea Cavalcanti, or rather Benedetto, together with the accusation of murder pronounced against him. But Valentine was so weak that this recital scarcely produced the same effect it would have done had she been in her usual state of health. Indeed, her brain was only the seat of vague ideas, and confused forms, mingled with strange fancies, alone presented themselves before her eyes.\n\nDuring the daytime Valentine’s perceptions remained tolerably clear, owing to the constant presence of M. Noirtier, who caused himself to be carried to his granddaughter’s room, and watched her with his paternal tenderness; Villefort also, on his return from the law courts, frequently passed an hour or two with his father and child.\n\nAt six o’clock Villefort retired to his study, at eight M. d’Avrigny himself arrived, bringing the night draught prepared for the young girl, and then M. Noirtier was carried away. A nurse of the doctor’s choice succeeded them, and never left till about ten or eleven o’clock, when Valentine was asleep. As she went downstairs she gave the keys of Valentine’s room to M. de Villefort, so that no one could reach the sick-room excepting through that of Madame de Villefort and little Edward.\n\nEvery morning Morrel called on Noirtier to receive news of Valentine, and, extraordinary as it seemed, each day found him less uneasy. Certainly, though Valentine still labored under dreadful nervous excitement, she was better; and moreover, Monte Cristo had told him when, half distracted, he had rushed to the count’s house, that if she were not dead in two hours she would be saved. Now four days had elapsed, and Valentine still lived.\n\nThe nervous excitement of which we speak pursued Valentine even in her sleep, or rather in that state of somnolence which succeeded her waking hours; it was then, in the silence of night, in the dim light shed from the alabaster lamp on the chimney-piece, that she saw the shadows pass and repass which hover over the bed of sickness, and fan the fever with their trembling wings. First she fancied she saw her stepmother threatening her, then Morrel stretched his arms towards her; sometimes mere strangers, like the Count of Monte Cristo came to visit her; even the very furniture, in these moments of delirium, seemed to move, and this state lasted till about three o’clock in the morning, when a deep, heavy slumber overcame the young girl, from which she did not awake till daylight.\n\nOn the evening of the day on which Valentine had learned of the flight of Eugénie and the arrest of Benedetto,—Villefort having retired as well as Noirtier and d’Avrigny,—her thoughts wandered in a confused maze, alternately reviewing her own situation and the events she had just heard.\n\nEleven o’clock had struck. The nurse, having placed the beverage prepared by the doctor within reach of the patient, and locked the door, was listening with terror to the comments of the servants in the kitchen, and storing her memory with all the horrible stories which had for some months past amused the occupants of the antechambers in the house of the king’s attorney. Meanwhile an unexpected scene was passing in the room which had been so carefully locked.\n\nTen minutes had elapsed since the nurse had left; Valentine, who for the last hour had been suffering from the fever which returned nightly, incapable of controlling her ideas, was forced to yield to the excitement which exhausted itself in producing and reproducing a succession and recurrence of the same fancies and images. The night-lamp threw out countless rays, each resolving itself into some strange form to her disordered imagination, when suddenly by its flickering light Valentine thought she saw the door of her library, which was in the recess by the chimney-piece, open slowly, though she in vain listened for the sound of the hinges on which it turned.\n\nAt any other time Valentine would have seized the silken bell-pull and summoned assistance, but nothing astonished her in her present situation. Her reason told her that all the visions she beheld were but the children of her imagination, and the conviction was strengthened by the fact that in the morning no traces remained of the nocturnal phantoms, who disappeared with the coming of daylight.\n\nFrom behind the door a human figure appeared, but the girl was too familiar with such apparitions to be alarmed, and therefore only stared, hoping to recognize Morrel. The figure advanced towards the bed and appeared to listen with profound attention. At this moment a ray of light glanced across the face of the midnight visitor.\n\n“It is not he,” she murmured, and waited, in the assurance that this was but a dream, for the man to disappear or assume some other form. Still, she felt her pulse, and finding it throb violently she remembered that the best method of dispelling such illusions was to drink, for a draught of the beverage prepared by the doctor to allay her fever seemed to cause a reaction of the brain, and for a short time she suffered less. Valentine therefore reached her hand towards the glass, but as soon as her trembling arm left the bed the apparition advanced more quickly towards her, and approached the young girl so closely that she fancied she heard his breath, and felt the pressure of his hand.\n\nThis time the illusion, or rather the reality, surpassed anything Valentine had before experienced; she began to believe herself really alive and awake, and the belief that her reason was this time not deceived made her shudder. The pressure she felt was evidently intended to arrest her arm, and she slowly withdrew it. Then the figure, from whom she could not detach her eyes, and who appeared more protecting than menacing, took the glass, and walking towards the night-light held it up, as if to test its transparency. This did not seem sufficient; the man, or rather the ghost—for he trod so softly that no sound was heard—then poured out about a spoonful into the glass, and drank it.\n\nValentine witnessed this scene with a sentiment of stupefaction. Every minute she had expected that it would vanish and give place to another vision; but the man, instead of dissolving like a shadow, again approached her, and said in an agitated voice, “Now you may drink.”\n\nValentine shuddered. It was the first time one of these visions had ever addressed her in a living voice, and she was about to utter an exclamation. The man placed his finger on her lips.\n\n“The Count of Monte Cristo!” she murmured.\n\nIt was easy to see that no doubt now remained in the young girl’s mind as to the reality of the scene; her eyes started with terror, her hands trembled, and she rapidly drew the bedclothes closer to her. Still, the presence of Monte Cristo at such an hour, his mysterious, fanciful, and extraordinary entrance into her room through the wall, might well seem impossibilities to her shattered reason.\n\n“Do not call anyone—do not be alarmed,” said the count; “do not let a shade of suspicion or uneasiness remain in your breast; the man standing before you, Valentine (for this time it is no ghost), is nothing more than the tenderest father and the most respectful friend you could dream of.”\n\nValentine could not reply; the voice which indicated the real presence of a being in the room, alarmed her so much that she feared to utter a syllable; still the expression of her eyes seemed to inquire, “If your intentions are pure, why are you here?” The count’s marvellous sagacity understood all that was passing in the young girl’s mind.\n\n“Listen to me,” he said, “or, rather, look upon me; look at my face, paler even than usual, and my eyes, red with weariness—for four days I have not closed them, for I have been constantly watching you, to protect and preserve you for Maximilian.”\n\nThe blood mounted rapidly to the cheeks of Valentine, for the name just announced by the count dispelled all the fear with which his presence had inspired her.\n\n“Maximilian!” she exclaimed, and so sweet did the sound appear to her, that she repeated it—“Maximilian!—has he then owned all to you?”\n\n“Everything. He told me your life was his, and I have promised him that you shall live.”\n\n“You have promised him that I shall live?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“But, sir, you spoke of vigilance and protection. Are you a doctor?”\n\n“Yes; the best you could have at the present time, believe me.”\n\n“But you say you have watched?” said Valentine uneasily; “where have you been?—I have not seen you.”\n\nThe count extended his hand towards the library.\n\n“I was hidden behind that door,” he said, “which leads into the next house, which I have rented.”\n\nValentine turned her eyes away, and, with an indignant expression of pride and modest fear, exclaimed:\n\n“Sir, I think you have been guilty of an unparalleled intrusion, and that what you call protection is more like an insult.”\n\n“Valentine,” he answered, “during my long watch over you, all I have observed has been what people visited you, what nourishment was prepared, and what beverage was served; then, when the latter appeared dangerous to me, I entered, as I have now done, and substituted, in the place of the poison, a healthful draught; which, instead of producing the death intended, caused life to circulate in your veins.”\n\n“Poison—death!” exclaimed Valentine, half believing herself under the influence of some feverish hallucination; “what are you saying, sir?”\n\n50071m\n“Hush, my child,” said Monte Cristo, again placing his finger upon her lips, “I did say poison and death. But drink some of this;” and the count took a bottle from his pocket, containing a red liquid, of which he poured a few drops into the glass. “Drink this, and then take nothing more tonight.”\n\nValentine stretched out her hand, but scarcely had she touched the glass when she drew back in fear. Monte Cristo took the glass, drank half its contents, and then presented it to Valentine, who smiled and swallowed the rest.\n\n“Oh, yes,” she exclaimed, “I recognize the flavor of my nocturnal beverage which refreshed me so much, and seemed to ease my aching brain. Thank you, sir, thank you!”\n\n“This is how you have lived during the last four nights, Valentine,” said the count. “But, oh, how I passed that time! Oh, the wretched hours I have endured—the torture to which I have submitted when I saw the deadly poison poured into your glass, and how I trembled lest you should drink it before I could find time to throw it away!”\n\n“Sir,” said Valentine, at the height of her terror, “you say you endured tortures when you saw the deadly poison poured into my glass; but if you saw this, you must also have seen the person who poured it?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nValentine raised herself in bed, and drew over her chest, which appeared whiter than snow, the embroidered cambric, still moist with the cold dews of delirium, to which were now added those of terror. “You saw the person?” repeated the young girl.\n\n“Yes,” repeated the count.\n\n“What you tell me is horrible, sir. You wish to make me believe something too dreadful. What?—attempt to murder me in my father’s house, in my room, on my bed of sickness? Oh, leave me, sir; you are tempting me—you make me doubt the goodness of Providence—it is impossible, it cannot be!”\n\n“Are you the first that this hand has stricken? Have you not seen M. de Saint-Méran, Madame de Saint-Méran, Barrois, all fall? Would not M. Noirtier also have fallen a victim, had not the treatment he has been pursuing for the last three years neutralized the effects of the poison?”\n\n“Oh, Heaven,” said Valentine; “is this the reason why grandpapa has made me share all his beverages during the last month?”\n\n“And have they all tasted of a slightly bitter flavor, like that of dried orange-peel?”\n\n“Oh, yes, yes!”\n\n“Then that explains all,” said Monte Cristo. “Your grandfather knows, then, that a poisoner lives here; perhaps he even suspects the person. He has been fortifying you, his beloved child, against the fatal effects of the poison, which has failed because your system was already impregnated with it. But even this would have availed little against a more deadly medium of death employed four days ago, which is generally but too fatal.”\n\n“But who, then, is this assassin, this murderer?”\n\n“Let me also ask you a question. Have you never seen anyone enter your room at night?”\n\n“Oh, yes; I have frequently seen shadows pass close to me, approach, and disappear; but I took them for visions raised by my feverish imagination, and indeed when you entered I thought I was under the influence of delirium.”\n\n“Then you do not know who it is that attempts your life?”\n\n50073m\n“No,” said Valentine; “who could desire my death?”\n\n“You shall know it now, then,” said Monte Cristo, listening.\n\n“How do you mean?” said Valentine, looking anxiously around.\n\n“Because you are not feverish or delirious tonight, but thoroughly awake; midnight is striking, which is the hour murderers choose.”\n\n“Oh, heavens,” exclaimed Valentine, wiping off the drops which ran down her forehead. Midnight struck slowly and sadly; every hour seemed to strike with leaden weight upon the heart of the poor girl.\n\n“Valentine,” said the count, “summon up all your courage; still the beatings of your heart; do not let a sound escape you, and feign to be asleep; then you will see.”\n\nValentine seized the count’s hand. “I think I hear a noise,” she said; “leave me.”\n\n“Good-bye, for the present,” replied the count, walking upon tiptoe towards the library door, and smiling with an expression so sad and paternal that the young girl’s heart was filled with gratitude.\n\nBefore closing the door he turned around once more, and said, “Not a movement—not a word; let them think you asleep, or perhaps you may be killed before I have the power of helping you.”\n\nAnd with this fearful injunction the count disappeared through the door, which noiselessly closed after him.\n\n# Chapter 101. Locusta\n\nValentine was alone; two other clocks, slower than that of Saint-Philippe-du-Roule, struck the hour of midnight from different directions, and excepting the rumbling of a few carriages all was silent. Then Valentine’s attention was engrossed by the clock in her room, which marked the seconds. She began counting them, remarking that they were much slower than the beatings of her heart; and still she doubted,—the inoffensive Valentine could not imagine that anyone should desire her death. Why should they? To what end? What had she done to excite the malice of an enemy?\n\nThere was no fear of her falling asleep. One terrible idea pressed upon her mind,—that someone existed in the world who had attempted to assassinate her, and who was about to endeavor to do so again. Supposing this person, wearied at the inefficacy of the poison, should, as Monte Cristo intimated, have recourse to steel!—What if the count should have no time to run to her rescue!—What if her last moments were approaching, and she should never again see Morrel!\n\nWhen this terrible chain of ideas presented itself, Valentine was nearly persuaded to ring the bell, and call for help. But through the door she fancied she saw the luminous eye of the count—that eye which lived in her memory, and the recollection overwhelmed her with so much shame that she asked herself whether any amount of gratitude could ever repay his adventurous and devoted friendship.\n\nTwenty minutes, twenty tedious minutes, passed thus, then ten more, and at last the clock struck the half-hour.\n\nJust then the sound of finger-nails slightly grating against the door of the library informed Valentine that the count was still watching, and recommended her to do the same; at the same time, on the opposite side, that is towards Edward’s room, Valentine fancied that she heard the creaking of the floor; she listened attentively, holding her breath till she was nearly suffocated; the lock turned, and the door slowly opened. Valentine had raised herself upon her elbow, and had scarcely time to throw herself down on the bed and shade her eyes with her arm; then, trembling, agitated, and her heart beating with indescribable terror, she awaited the event.\n\nSomeone approached the bed and drew back the curtains. Valentine summoned every effort, and breathed with that regular respiration which announces tranquil sleep.\n\n“Valentine!” said a low voice.\n\nThe girl shuddered to the heart but did not reply.\n\n“Valentine,” repeated the same voice.\n\nStill silent: Valentine had promised not to wake. Then everything was still, excepting that Valentine heard the almost noiseless sound of some liquid being poured into the glass she had just emptied. Then she ventured to open her eyelids, and glance over her extended arm. She saw a woman in a white dressing-gown pouring a liquor from a phial into her glass. During this short time Valentine must have held her breath, or moved in some slight degree, for the woman, disturbed, stopped and leaned over the bed, in order the better to ascertain whether Valentine slept: it was Madame de Villefort.\n\nOn recognizing her step-mother, Valentine could not repress a shudder, which caused a vibration in the bed. Madame de Villefort instantly stepped back close to the wall, and there, shaded by the bed-curtains, she silently and attentively watched the slightest movement of Valentine. The latter recollected the terrible caution of Monte Cristo; she fancied that the hand not holding the phial clasped a long sharp knife. Then collecting all her remaining strength, she forced herself to close her eyes; but this simple operation upon the most delicate organs of our frame, generally so easy to accomplish, became almost impossible at this moment, so much did curiosity struggle to retain the eyelid open and learn the truth. Madame de Villefort, however, reassured by the silence, which was alone disturbed by the regular breathing of Valentine, again extended her hand, and half hidden by the curtains succeeded in emptying the contents of the phial into the glass. Then she retired so gently that Valentine did not know she had left the room. She only witnessed the withdrawal of the arm—the fair round arm of a woman but twenty-five years old, and who yet spread death around her.\n50077m\n\nIt is impossible to describe the sensations experienced by Valentine during the minute and a half Madame de Villefort remained in the room.\n\nThe grating against the library-door aroused the young girl from the stupor in which she was plunged, and which almost amounted to insensibility. She raised her head with an effort. The noiseless door again turned on its hinges, and the Count of Monte Cristo reappeared.\n\n“Well,” said he, “do you still doubt?”\n\n“Oh,” murmured the young girl.\n\n“Have you seen?”\n\n“Alas!”\n\n“Did you recognize?” Valentine groaned.\n\n“Oh, yes;” she said, “I saw, but I cannot believe!”\n\n“Would you rather die, then, and cause Maximilian’s death?”\n\n“Oh,” repeated the young girl, almost bewildered, “can I not leave the house?—can I not escape?”\n\n“Valentine, the hand which now threatens you will pursue you everywhere; your servants will be seduced with gold, and death will be offered to you disguised in every shape. You will find it in the water you drink from the spring, in the fruit you pluck from the tree.”\n\n“But did you not say that my kind grandfather’s precaution had neutralized the poison?”\n\n“Yes, but not against a strong dose; the poison will be changed, and the quantity increased.” He took the glass and raised it to his lips. “It is already done,” he said; “brucine is no longer employed, but a simple narcotic! I can recognize the flavor of the alcohol in which it has been dissolved. If you had taken what Madame de Villefort has poured into your glass, Valentine—Valentine—you would have been doomed!”\n\n“But,” exclaimed the young girl, “why am I thus pursued?”\n\n“Why?—are you so kind—so good—so unsuspicious of ill, that you cannot understand, Valentine?”\n\n“No, I have never injured her.”\n\n“But you are rich, Valentine; you have 200,000 livres a year, and you prevent her son from enjoying these 200,000 livres.”\n\n“How so? The fortune is not her gift, but is inherited from my relations.”\n\n“Certainly; and that is why M. and Madame de Saint-Méran have died; that is why M. Noirtier was sentenced the day he made you his heir; that is why you, in your turn, are to die—it is because your father would inherit your property, and your brother, his only son, succeed to his.”\n\n“Edward? Poor child! Are all these crimes committed on his account?”\n\n“Ah, then you at length understand?”\n\n“Heaven grant that this may not be visited upon him!”\n\n“Valentine, you are an angel!”\n\n“But why is my grandfather allowed to live?”\n\n“It was considered, that you dead, the fortune would naturally revert to your brother, unless he were disinherited; and besides, the crime appearing useless, it would be folly to commit it.”\n\n“And is it possible that this frightful combination of crimes has been invented by a woman?”\n\n“Do you recollect in the arbor of the Hôtel des Postes, at Perugia, seeing a man in a brown cloak, whom your stepmother was questioning upon _aqua tofana_? Well, ever since then, the infernal project has been ripening in her brain.”\n\n“Ah, then, indeed, sir,” said the sweet girl, bathed in tears, “I see that I am condemned to die!”\n\n“No, Valentine, for I have foreseen all their plots; no, your enemy is conquered since we know her, and you will live, Valentine—live to be happy yourself, and to confer happiness upon a noble heart; but to insure this you must rely on me.”\n\n“Command me, sir—what am I to do?”\n\n“You must blindly take what I give you.”\n\n“Alas, were it only for my own sake, I should prefer to die!”\n\n“You must not confide in anyone—not even in your father.”\n\n“My father is not engaged in this fearful plot, is he, sir?” asked Valentine, clasping her hands.\n\n“No; and yet your father, a man accustomed to judicial accusations, ought to have known that all these deaths have not happened naturally; it is he who should have watched over you—he should have occupied my place—he should have emptied that glass—he should have risen against the assassin. Spectre against spectre!” he murmured in a low voice, as he concluded his sentence.\n\n“Sir,” said Valentine, “I will do all I can to live, for there are two beings who love me and will die if I die—my grandfather and Maximilian.”\n\n“I will watch over them as I have over you.”\n\n“Well, sir, do as you will with me;” and then she added, in a low voice, “oh, heavens, what will befall me?”\n\n“Whatever may happen, Valentine, do not be alarmed; though you suffer; though you lose sight, hearing, consciousness, fear nothing; though you should awake and be ignorant where you are, still do not fear; even though you should find yourself in a sepulchral vault or coffin. Reassure yourself, then, and say to yourself: ‘At this moment, a friend, a father, who lives for my happiness and that of Maximilian, watches over me!’”\n\n“Alas, alas, what a fearful extremity!”\n\n“Valentine, would you rather denounce your stepmother?”\n\n“I would rather die a hundred times—oh, yes, die!”\n\n“No, you will not die; but will you promise me, whatever happens, that you will not complain, but hope?”\n\n“I will think of Maximilian!”\n\n“You are my own darling child, Valentine! I alone can save you, and I will.”\n\nValentine in the extremity of her terror joined her hands,—for she felt that the moment had arrived to ask for courage,—and began to pray, and while uttering little more than incoherent words, she forgot that her white shoulders had no other covering than her long hair, and that the pulsations of her heart could be seen through the lace of her nightdress. Monte Cristo gently laid his hand on the young girl’s arm, drew the velvet coverlet close to her throat, and said with a paternal smile:\n\n“My child, believe in my devotion to you as you believe in the goodness of Providence and the love of Maximilian.” Valentine gave him a look full of gratitude, and remained as docile as a child.\n\nThen he drew from his waistcoat-pocket the little emerald box, raised the golden lid, and took from it a pastille about the size of a pea, which he placed in her hand. She took it, and looked attentively on the count; there was an expression on the face of her intrepid protector which commanded her veneration. She evidently interrogated him by her look.\n\n“Yes,” said he.\n\nValentine carried the pastille to her mouth, and swallowed it.\n\n“And now, my dear child, adieu for the present. I will try and gain a little sleep, for you are saved.”\n\n“Go,” said Valentine, “whatever happens, I promise you not to fear.”\n\nMonte Cristo for some time kept his eyes fixed on the young girl, who gradually fell asleep, yielding to the effects of the narcotic the count had given her. Then he took the glass, emptied three parts of the contents in the fireplace, that it might be supposed Valentine had taken it, and replaced it on the table; then he disappeared, after throwing a farewell glance on Valentine, who slept with the confidence and innocence of an angel at the feet of the Lord.\n\n# Chapter 102. Valentine\n\nThe night-light continued to burn on the chimney-piece, exhausting the last drops of oil which floated on the surface of the water. The globe of the lamp appeared of a reddish hue, and the flame, brightening before it expired, threw out the last flickerings which in an inanimate object have been so often compared with the convulsions of a human creature in its final agonies. A dull and dismal light was shed over the bedclothes and curtains surrounding the young girl. All noise in the streets had ceased, and the silence was frightful.\n\nIt was then that the door of Edward’s room opened, and a head we have before noticed appeared in the glass opposite; it was Madame de Villefort, who came to witness the effects of the drink she had prepared. She stopped in the doorway, listened for a moment to the flickering of the lamp, the only sound in that deserted room, and then advanced to the table to see if Valentine’s glass were empty. It was still about a quarter full, as we before stated. Madame de Villefort emptied the contents into the ashes, which she disturbed that they might the more readily absorb the liquid; then she carefully rinsed the glass, and wiping it with her handkerchief replaced it on the table.\n\nIf anyone could have looked into the room just then he would have noticed the hesitation with which Madame de Villefort approached the bed and looked fixedly on Valentine. The dim light, the profound silence, and the gloomy thoughts inspired by the hour, and still more by her own conscience, all combined to produce a sensation of fear; the poisoner was terrified at the contemplation of her own work.\n\nAt length she rallied, drew aside the curtain, and leaning over the pillow gazed intently on Valentine. The young girl no longer breathed, no breath issued through the half-closed teeth; the white lips no longer quivered—the eyes were suffused with a bluish vapor, and the long black lashes rested on a cheek white as wax. Madame de Villefort gazed upon the face so expressive even in its stillness; then she ventured to raise the coverlet and press her hand upon the young girl’s heart. It was cold and motionless. She only felt the pulsation in her own fingers, and withdrew her hand with a shudder. One arm was hanging out of the bed; from shoulder to elbow it was moulded after the arms of Germain Pillon’s “Graces,”23 but the fore-arm seemed to be slightly distorted by convulsion, and the hand, so delicately formed, was resting with stiff outstretched fingers on the framework of the bed. The nails, too, were turning blue.\n\nMadame de Villefort had no longer any doubt; all was over—she had consummated the last terrible work she had to accomplish. There was no more to do in the room, so the poisoner retired stealthily, as though fearing to hear the sound of her own footsteps; but as she withdrew she still held aside the curtain, absorbed in the irresistible attraction always exerted by the picture of death, so long as it is merely mysterious and does not excite disgust.\n\nThe minutes passed; Madame de Villefort could not drop the curtain which she held like a funeral pall over the head of Valentine. She was lost in reverie, and the reverie of crime is remorse.\n\nJust then the lamp again flickered; the noise startled Madame de Villefort, who shuddered and dropped the curtain. Immediately afterwards the light expired, and the room was plunged in frightful obscurity, while the clock at that minute struck half-past four.\n\nOverpowered with agitation, the poisoner succeeded in groping her way to the door, and reached her room in an agony of fear. The darkness lasted two hours longer; then by degrees a cold light crept through the Venetian blinds, until at length it revealed the objects in the room.\n\nAbout this time the nurse’s cough was heard on the stairs and the woman entered the room with a cup in her hand. To the tender eye of a father or a lover, the first glance would have sufficed to reveal Valentine’s condition; but to this hireling, Valentine only appeared to sleep.\n\n“Good,” she exclaimed, approaching the table, “she has taken part of her draught; the glass is three-quarters empty.”\n\nThen she went to the fireplace and lit the fire, and although she had just left her bed, she could not resist the temptation offered by Valentine’s sleep, so she threw herself into an armchair to snatch a little more rest. The clock striking eight awoke her. Astonished at the prolonged slumber of the patient, and frightened to see that the arm was still hanging out of the bed, she advanced towards Valentine, and for the first time noticed the white lips. She tried to replace the arm, but it moved with a frightful rigidity which could not deceive a sick-nurse. She screamed aloud; then running to the door exclaimed:\n\n“Help, help!”\n50083m\n\n“What is the matter?” asked M. d’Avrigny, at the foot of the stairs, it being the hour he usually visited her.\n\n“What is it?” asked Villefort, rushing from his room. “Doctor, do you hear them call for help?”\n\n“Yes, yes; let us hasten up; it was in Valentine’s room.”\n\nBut before the doctor and the father could reach the room, the servants who were on the same floor had entered, and seeing Valentine pale and motionless on her bed, they lifted up their hands towards heaven and stood transfixed, as though struck by lightening.\n\n“Call Madame de Villefort!—Wake Madame de Villefort!” cried the procureur from the door of his chamber, which apparently he scarcely dared to leave. But instead of obeying him, the servants stood watching M. d’Avrigny, who ran to Valentine, and raised her in his arms.\n\n“What?—this one, too?” he exclaimed. “Oh, where will be the end?”\n\nVillefort rushed into the room.\n\n“What are you saying, doctor?” he exclaimed, raising his hands to heaven.\n\n“I say that Valentine is dead!” replied d’Avrigny, in a voice terrible in its solemn calmness.\n\n50085m\nM. de Villefort staggered and buried his head in the bed. On the exclamation of the doctor and the cry of the father, the servants all fled with muttered imprecations; they were heard running down the stairs and through the long passages, then there was a rush in the court, afterwards all was still; they had, one and all, deserted the accursed house.\n\nJust then, Madame de Villefort, in the act of slipping on her dressing-gown, threw aside the drapery and for a moment stood motionless, as though interrogating the occupants of the room, while she endeavored to call up some rebellious tears. On a sudden she stepped, or rather bounded, with outstretched arms, towards the table. She saw d’Avrigny curiously examining the glass, which she felt certain of having emptied during the night. It was now a third full, just as it was when she threw the contents into the ashes. The spectre of Valentine rising before the poisoner would have alarmed her less. It was, indeed, the same color as the draught she had poured into the glass, and which Valentine had drunk; it was indeed the poison, which could not deceive M. d’Avrigny, which he now examined so closely; it was doubtless a miracle from heaven, that, notwithstanding her precautions, there should be some trace, some proof remaining to reveal the crime.\n\nWhile Madame de Villefort remained rooted to the spot like a statue of terror, and Villefort, with his head hidden in the bedclothes, saw nothing around him, d’Avrigny approached the window, that he might the better examine the contents of the glass, and dipping the tip of his finger in, tasted it.\n\n“Ah,” he exclaimed, “it is no longer brucine that is used; let me see what it is!”\n\nThen he ran to one of the cupboards in Valentine’s room, which had been transformed into a medicine closet, and taking from its silver case a small bottle of nitric acid, dropped a little of it into the liquor, which immediately changed to a blood-red color.\n\n“Ah,” exclaimed d’Avrigny, in a voice in which the horror of a judge unveiling the truth was mingled with the delight of a student making a discovery.\n\nMadame de Villefort was overpowered; her eyes first flashed and then swam, she staggered towards the door and disappeared. Directly afterwards the distant sound of a heavy weight falling on the ground was heard, but no one paid any attention to it; the nurse was engaged in watching the chemical analysis, and Villefort was still absorbed in grief. M. d’Avrigny alone had followed Madame de Villefort with his eyes, and watched her hurried retreat. He lifted up the drapery over the entrance to Edward’s room, and his eye reaching as far as Madame de Villefort’s apartment, he beheld her extended lifeless on the floor.\n\n“Go to the assistance of Madame de Villefort,” he said to the nurse. “Madame de Villefort is ill.”\n50087m\n\n“But Mademoiselle de Villefort——” stammered the nurse.\n\n“Mademoiselle de Villefort no longer requires help,” said d’Avrigny, “since she is dead.”\n\n“Dead,—dead!” groaned forth Villefort, in a paroxysm of grief, which was the more terrible from the novelty of the sensation in the iron heart of that man.\n\n“Dead!” repeated a third voice. “Who said Valentine was dead?”\n\nThe two men turned round, and saw Morrel standing at the door, pale and terror-stricken. This is what had happened. At the usual time, Morrel had presented himself at the little door leading to Noirtier’s room. Contrary to custom, the door was open, and having no occasion to ring he entered. He waited for a moment in the hall and called for a servant to conduct him to M. Noirtier; but no one answered, the servants having, as we know, deserted the house. Morrel had no particular reason for uneasiness; Monte Cristo had promised him that Valentine should live, and so far he had always fulfilled his word. Every night the count had given him news, which was the next morning confirmed by Noirtier. Still this extraordinary silence appeared strange to him, and he called a second and third time; still no answer. Then he determined to go up. Noirtier’s room was opened, like all the rest. The first thing he saw was the old man sitting in his armchair in his usual place, but his eyes expressed alarm, which was confirmed by the pallor which overspread his features.\n\n“How are you, sir?” asked Morrel, with a sickness of heart.\n\n“Well,” answered the old man, by closing his eyes; but his appearance manifested increasing uneasiness.\n\n“You are thoughtful, sir,” continued Morrel; “you want something; shall I call one of the servants?”\n\n“Yes,” replied Noirtier.\n\nMorrel pulled the bell, but though he nearly broke the cord no one answered. He turned towards Noirtier; the pallor and anguish expressed on his countenance momentarily increased.\n\n“Oh,” exclaimed Morrel, “why do they not come? Is anyone ill in the house?” The eyes of Noirtier seemed as though they would start from their sockets. “What is the matter? You alarm me. Valentine? Valentine?”\n\n“Yes, yes,” signed Noirtier.\n\nMaximilian tried to speak, but he could articulate nothing; he staggered, and supported himself against the wainscot. Then he pointed to the door.\n\n“Yes, yes, yes!” continued the old man.\n50089m\n\nMaximilian rushed up the little staircase, while Noirtier’s eyes seemed to say,—“Quicker, quicker!”\n\nIn a minute the young man darted through several rooms, till at length he reached Valentine’s.\n\nThere was no occasion to push the door, it was wide open. A sob was the only sound he heard. He saw as though in a mist, a black figure kneeling and buried in a confused mass of white drapery. A terrible fear transfixed him. It was then he heard a voice exclaim “Valentine is dead!” and another voice which, like an echo repeated:\n\n“Dead,—dead!”\n50090m\n\n# Chapter 103. Maximilian\n\nVillefort rose, half-ashamed of being surprised in such a paroxysm of grief. The terrible office he had held for twenty-five years had succeeded in making him more or less than man. His glance, at first wandering, fixed itself upon Morrel. “Who are you, sir,” he asked, “that forget that this is not the manner to enter a house stricken with death? Go, sir, go!”\n\nBut Morrel remained motionless; he could not detach his eyes from that disordered bed, and the pale corpse of the young girl who was lying on it.\n\n“Go!—do you hear?” said Villefort, while d’Avrigny advanced to lead Morrel out. Maximilian stared for a moment at the corpse, gazed all around the room, then upon the two men; he opened his mouth to speak, but finding it impossible to give utterance to the innumerable ideas that occupied his brain, he went out, thrusting his hands through his hair in such a manner that Villefort and d’Avrigny, for a moment diverted from the engrossing topic, exchanged glances, which seemed to say,—“He is mad!”\n\nBut in less than five minutes the staircase groaned beneath an extraordinary weight. Morrel was seen carrying, with superhuman strength, the armchair containing Noirtier upstairs. When he reached the landing he placed the armchair on the floor and rapidly rolled it into Valentine’s room. This could only have been accomplished by means of unnatural strength supplied by powerful excitement. But the most fearful spectacle was Noirtier being pushed towards the bed, his face expressing all his meaning, and his eyes supplying the want of every other faculty. That pale face and flaming glance appeared to Villefort like a frightful apparition. Each time he had been brought into contact with his father, something terrible had happened.\n\n“See what they have done!” cried Morrel, with one hand leaning on the back of the chair, and the other extended towards Valentine. “See, my father, see!”\n\nVillefort drew back and looked with astonishment on the young man, who, almost a stranger to him, called Noirtier his father. At this moment the whole soul of the old man seemed centred in his eyes which became bloodshot; the veins of the throat swelled; his cheeks and temples became purple, as though he was struck with epilepsy; nothing was wanting to complete this but the utterance of a cry. And the cry issued from his pores, if we may thus speak—a cry frightful in its silence. D’Avrigny rushed towards the old man and made him inhale a powerful restorative.\n\n“Sir,” cried Morrel, seizing the moist hand of the paralytic, “they ask me who I am, and what right I have to be here. Oh, you know it, tell them, tell them!” And the young man’s voice was choked by sobs.\n\nAs for the old man, his chest heaved with his panting respiration. One could have thought that he was undergoing the agonies preceding death. At length, happier than the young man, who sobbed without weeping, tears glistened in the eyes of Noirtier.\n\n“Tell them,” said Morrel in a hoarse voice, “tell them that I am her betrothed. Tell them she was my beloved, my noble girl, my only blessing in the world. Tell them—oh, tell them, that corpse belongs to me!”\n\nThe young man overwhelmed by the weight of his anguish, fell heavily on his knees before the bed, which his fingers grasped with convulsive energy. D’Avrigny, unable to bear the sight of this touching emotion, turned away; and Villefort, without seeking any further explanation, and attracted towards him by the irresistible magnetism which draws us towards those who have loved the people for whom we mourn, extended his hand towards the young man.\n\nBut Morrel saw nothing; he had grasped the hand of Valentine, and unable to weep vented his agony in groans as he bit the sheets. For some time nothing was heard in that chamber but sobs, exclamations, and prayers. At length Villefort, the most composed of all, spoke:\n\n“Sir,” said he to Maximilian, “you say you loved Valentine, that you were betrothed to her. I knew nothing of this engagement, of this love, yet I, her father, forgive you, for I see that your grief is real and deep; and besides my own sorrow is too great for anger to find a place in my heart. But you see that the angel whom you hoped for has left this earth—she has nothing more to do with the adoration of men. Take a last farewell, sir, of her sad remains; take the hand you expected to possess once more within your own, and then separate yourself from her forever. Valentine now requires only the ministrations of the priest.”\n50093m\n\n“You are mistaken, sir,” exclaimed Morrel, raising himself on one knee, his heart pierced by a more acute pang than any he had yet felt—“you are mistaken; Valentine, dying as she has, not only requires a priest, but an avenger. _You_, M. de Villefort, send for the priest; _I_ will be the avenger.”\n\n“What do you mean, sir?” asked Villefort, trembling at the new idea inspired by the delirium of Morrel.\n\n“I tell you, sir, that two persons exist in you; the father has mourned sufficiently, now let the procureur fulfil his office.”\n\nThe eyes of Noirtier glistened, and d’Avrigny approached.\n\n“Gentlemen,” said Morrel, reading all that passed through the minds of the witnesses to the scene, “I know what I am saying, and you know as well as I do what I am about to say—Valentine has been assassinated!”\n\nVillefort hung his head, d’Avrigny approached nearer, and Noirtier said “Yes” with his eyes.\n\n“Now, sir,” continued Morrel, “in these days no one can disappear by violent means without some inquiries being made as to the cause of her disappearance, even were she not a young, beautiful, and adorable creature like Valentine. Now, M. le Procureur du Roi,” said Morrel with increasing vehemence, “no mercy is allowed; I denounce the crime; it is your place to seek the assassin.”\n\nThe young man’s implacable eyes interrogated Villefort, who, on his side, glanced from Noirtier to d’Avrigny. But instead of finding sympathy in the eyes of the doctor and his father, he only saw an expression as inflexible as that of Maximilian.\n\n“Yes,” indicated the old man.\n\n“Assuredly,” said d’Avrigny.\n\n“Sir,” said Villefort, striving to struggle against this triple force and his own emotion,—“sir, you are deceived; no one commits crimes here. I am stricken by fate. It is horrible, indeed, but no one assassinates.”\n\nThe eyes of Noirtier lighted up with rage, and d’Avrigny prepared to speak. Morrel, however, extended his arm, and commanded silence.\n\n“And I say that murders _are_ committed here,” said Morrel, whose voice, though lower in tone, lost none of its terrible distinctness: “I tell you that this is the fourth victim within the last four months. I tell you, Valentine’s life was attempted by poison four days ago, though she escaped, owing to the precautions of M. Noirtier. I tell you that the dose has been double, the poison changed, and that this time it has succeeded. I tell you that you know these things as well as I do, since this gentleman has forewarned you, both as a doctor and as a friend.”\n\n“Oh, you rave, sir,” exclaimed Villefort, in vain endeavoring to escape the net in which he was taken.\n\n50095m\n“I rave?” said Morrel; “well, then, I appeal to M. d’Avrigny himself. Ask him, sir, if he recollects the words he uttered in the garden of this house on the night of Madame de Saint-Méran’s death. You thought yourselves alone, and talked about that tragical death, and the fatality you mentioned then is the same which has caused the murder of Valentine.” Villefort and d’Avrigny exchanged looks.\n\n“Yes, yes,” continued Morrel; “recall the scene, for the words you thought were only given to silence and solitude fell into my ears. Certainly, after witnessing the culpable indolence manifested by M. de Villefort towards his own relations, I ought to have denounced him to the authorities; then I should not have been an accomplice to thy death, as I now am, sweet, beloved Valentine; but the accomplice shall become the avenger. This fourth murder is apparent to all, and if thy father abandon thee, Valentine, it is I, and I swear it, that shall pursue the assassin.”\n\nAnd this time, as though nature had at least taken compassion on the vigorous frame, nearly bursting with its own strength, the words of Morrel were stifled in his throat; his breast heaved; the tears, so long rebellious, gushed from his eyes; and he threw himself weeping on his knees by the side of the bed.\n\nThen d’Avrigny spoke. “And I, too,” he exclaimed in a low voice, “I unite with M. Morrel in demanding justice for crime; my blood boils at the idea of having encouraged a murderer by my cowardly concession.”\n\n“Oh, merciful Heavens!” murmured Villefort. Morrel raised his head, and reading the eyes of the old man, which gleamed with unnatural lustre,—\n\n“Stay,” he said, “M. Noirtier wishes to speak.”\n\n“Yes,” indicated Noirtier, with an expression the more terrible, from all his faculties being centred in his glance.\n\n“Do you know the assassin?” asked Morrel.\n\n“Yes,” replied Noirtier.\n\n“And will you direct us?” exclaimed the young man. “Listen, M. d’Avrigny, listen!”\n\nNoirtier looked upon Morrel with one of those melancholy smiles which had so often made Valentine happy, and thus fixed his attention. Then, having riveted the eyes of his interlocutor on his own, he glanced towards the door.\n\n“Do you wish me to leave?” said Morrel, sadly.\n\n“Yes,” replied Noirtier.\n\n“Alas, alas, sir, have pity on me!”\n\nThe old man’s eyes remained fixed on the door.\n\n“May I, at least, return?” asked Morrel.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Must I leave alone?”\n“No.”\n\n“Whom am I to take with me? The procureur?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“The doctor?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You wish to remain alone with M. de Villefort?”\n\n“Yes.”\n“But can he understand you?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Oh,” said Villefort, inexpressibly delighted to think that the inquiries were to be made by him alone,—“oh, be satisfied, I can understand my father.” While uttering these words with this expression of joy, his teeth clashed together violently.\n\nD’Avrigny took the young man’s arm, and led him out of the room. A more than deathlike silence then reigned in the house. At the end of a quarter of an hour a faltering footstep was heard, and Villefort appeared at the door of the apartment where d’Avrigny and Morrel had been staying, one absorbed in meditation, the other in grief.\n\n“You can come,” he said, and led them back to Noirtier.\n\nMorrel looked attentively on Villefort. His face was livid, large drops rolled down his face, and in his fingers he held the fragments of a quill pen which he had torn to atoms.\n\n“Gentlemen,” he said in a hoarse voice, “give me your word of honor that this horrible secret shall forever remain buried amongst ourselves!” The two men drew back.\n\n“I entreat you——” continued Villefort.\n\n“But,” said Morrel, “the culprit—the murderer—the assassin.”\n\n“Do not alarm yourself, sir; justice will be done,” said Villefort. “My father has revealed the culprit’s name; my father thirsts for revenge as much as you do, yet even he conjures you as I do to keep this secret. Do you not, father?”\n\n“Yes,” resolutely replied Noirtier. Morrel suffered an exclamation of horror and surprise to escape him.\n\n“Oh, sir,” said Villefort, arresting Maximilian by the arm, “if my father, the inflexible man, makes this request, it is because he knows, be assured, that Valentine will be terribly revenged. Is it not so, father?”\n\nThe old man made a sign in the affirmative. Villefort continued:\n\n“He knows me, and I have pledged my word to him. Rest assured, gentlemen, that within three days, in a less time than justice would demand, the revenge I shall have taken for the murder of my child will be such as to make the boldest heart tremble;” and as he spoke these words he ground his teeth, and grasped the old man’s senseless hand.\n\n“Will this promise be fulfilled, M. Noirtier?” asked Morrel, while d’Avrigny looked inquiringly.\n\n“Yes,” replied Noirtier with an expression of sinister joy.\n\n“Swear, then,” said Villefort, joining the hands of Morrel and d’Avrigny, “swear that you will spare the honor of my house, and leave me to avenge my child.”\n\nD’Avrigny turned round and uttered a very feeble “Yes,” but Morrel, disengaging his hand, rushed to the bed, and after having pressed the cold lips of Valentine with his own, hurriedly left, uttering a long, deep groan of despair and anguish.\n\nWe have before stated that all the servants had fled. M. de Villefort was therefore obliged to request M. d’Avrigny to superintend all the arrangements consequent upon a death in a large city, more especially a death under such suspicious circumstances.\n\nIt was something terrible to witness the silent agony, the mute despair of Noirtier, whose tears silently rolled down his cheeks. Villefort retired to his study, and d’Avrigny left to summon the doctor of the mayoralty, whose office it is to examine bodies after decease, and who is expressly named “the doctor of the dead.” M. Noirtier could not be persuaded to quit his grandchild. At the end of a quarter of an hour M. d’Avrigny returned with his associate; they found the outer gate closed, and not a servant remaining in the house; Villefort himself was obliged to open to them. But he stopped on the landing; he had not the courage to again visit the death chamber. The two doctors, therefore, entered the room alone. Noirtier was near the bed, pale, motionless, and silent as the corpse. The district doctor approached with the indifference of a man accustomed to spend half his time amongst the dead; he then lifted the sheet which was placed over the face, and just unclosed the lips.\n\n“Alas,” said d’Avrigny, “she is indeed dead, poor child!”\n50099m\n\n“Yes,” answered the doctor laconically, dropping the sheet he had raised. Noirtier uttered a kind of hoarse, rattling sound; the old man’s eyes sparkled, and the good doctor understood that he wished to behold his child. He therefore approached the bed, and while his companion was dipping the fingers with which he had touched the lips of the corpse in chloride of lime, he uncovered the calm and pale face, which looked like that of a sleeping angel.\n\nA tear, which appeared in the old man’s eye, expressed his thanks to the doctor. The doctor of the dead then laid his permit on the corner of the table, and having fulfilled his duty, was conducted out by d’Avrigny. Villefort met them at the door of his study; having in a few words thanked the district doctor, he turned to d’Avrigny, and said:\n\n“And now the priest.”\n\n“Is there any particular priest you wish to pray with Valentine?” asked d’Avrigny.\n\n“No.” said Villefort; “fetch the nearest.”\n\n“The nearest,” said the district doctor, “is a good Italian abbé, who lives next door to you. Shall I call on him as I pass?”\n\n“D’Avrigny,” said Villefort, “be so kind, I beseech you, as to accompany this gentleman. Here is the key of the door, so that you can go in and out as you please; you will bring the priest with you, and will oblige me by introducing him into my child’s room.”\n50101m\n\n“Do you wish to see him?”\n\n“I only wish to be alone. You will excuse me, will you not? A priest can understand a father’s grief.”\n\nAnd M. de Villefort, giving the key to d’Avrigny, again bade farewell to the strange doctor, and retired to his study, where he began to work. For some temperaments work is a remedy for all afflictions.\n\nAs the doctors entered the street, they saw a man in a cassock standing on the threshold of the next door.\n\n“This is the abbé of whom I spoke,” said the doctor to d’Avrigny. D’Avrigny accosted the priest.\n\n“Sir,” he said, “are you disposed to confer a great obligation on an unhappy father who has just lost his daughter? I mean M. de Villefort, the king’s attorney.”\n\n“Ah,” said the priest, in a marked Italian accent; “yes, I have heard that death is in that house.”\n\n“Then I need not tell you what kind of service he requires of you.”\n\n“I was about to offer myself, sir,” said the priest; “it is our mission to forestall our duties.”\n\n“It is a young girl.”\n\n“I know it, sir; the servants who fled from the house informed me. I also know that her name is Valentine, and I have already prayed for her.”\n\n“Thank you, sir,” said d’Avrigny; “since you have commenced your sacred office, deign to continue it. Come and watch by the dead, and all the wretched family will be grateful to you.”\n\n“I am going, sir; and I do not hesitate to say that no prayers will be more fervent than mine.”\n\nD’Avrigny took the priest’s hand, and without meeting Villefort, who was engaged in his study, they reached Valentine’s room, which on the following night was to be occupied by the undertakers. On entering the room, Noirtier’s eyes met those of the abbé, and no doubt he read some particular expression in them, for he remained in the room. D’Avrigny recommended the attention of the priest to the living as well as to the dead, and the abbé promised to devote his prayers to Valentine and his attentions to Noirtier.\n\nIn order, doubtless, that he might not be disturbed while fulfilling his sacred mission, the priest rose as soon as d’Avrigny departed, and not only bolted the door through which the doctor had just left, but also that leading to Madame de Villefort’s room.\n\n# Chapter 104. Danglars’ Signature\n\nThe next morning dawned dull and cloudy. During the night the undertakers had executed their melancholy office, and wrapped the corpse in the winding-sheet, which, whatever may be said about the equality of death, is at least a last proof of the luxury so pleasing in life. This winding-sheet was nothing more than a beautiful piece of cambric, which the young girl had bought a fortnight before.\n\nDuring the evening two men, engaged for the purpose, had carried Noirtier from Valentine’s room into his own, and contrary to all expectation there was no difficulty in withdrawing him from his child. The Abbé Busoni had watched till daylight, and then left without calling anyone. D’Avrigny returned about eight o’clock in the morning; he met Villefort on his way to Noirtier’s room, and accompanied him to see how the old man had slept. They found him in the large armchair, which served him for a bed, enjoying a calm, nay, almost a smiling sleep. They both stood in amazement at the door.\n\n“See,” said d’Avrigny to Villefort, “nature knows how to alleviate the deepest sorrow. No one can say that M. Noirtier did not love his child, and yet he sleeps.”\n\n“Yes, you are right,” replied Villefort, surprised; “he sleeps, indeed! And this is the more strange, since the least contradiction keeps him awake all night.”\n\n“Grief has stunned him,” replied d’Avrigny; and they both returned thoughtfully to the procureur’s study.\n\n“See, I have not slept,” said Villefort, showing his undisturbed bed; “grief does not stun me. I have not been in bed for two nights; but then look at my desk; see what I have written during these two days and nights. I have filled those papers, and have made out the accusation against the assassin Benedetto. Oh, work, work,—my passion, my joy, my delight,—it is for thee to alleviate my sorrows!” and he convulsively grasped the hand of d’Avrigny.\n\n“Do you require my services now?” asked d’Avrigny.\n\n“No,” said Villefort; “only return again at eleven o’clock; at twelve the—the—oh, Heavens, my poor, poor child!” and the procureur again becoming a man, lifted up his eyes and groaned.\n\n“Shall you be present in the reception-room?”\n\n“No; I have a cousin who has undertaken this sad office. I shall work, doctor—when I work I forget everything.”\n\nAnd, indeed, no sooner had the doctor left the room, than he was again absorbed in work. On the doorsteps d’Avrigny met the cousin whom Villefort had mentioned, a personage as insignificant in our story as in the world he occupied—one of those beings designed from their birth to make themselves useful to others. He was punctual, dressed in black, with crape around his hat, and presented himself at his cousin’s with a face made up for the occasion, and which he could alter as might be required.\n\nAt eleven o’clock the mourning-coaches rolled into the paved court, and the Rue du Faubourg Saint-Honoré was filled with a crowd of idlers, equally pleased to witness the festivities or the mourning of the rich, and who rush with the same avidity to a funeral procession as to the marriage of a duchess.\n\nGradually the reception-room filled, and some of our old friends made their appearance—we mean Debray, Château-Renaud, and Beauchamp, accompanied by all the leading men of the day at the bar, in literature, or the army, for M. de Villefort moved in the first Parisian circles, less owing to his social position than to his personal merit.\n\nThe cousin standing at the door ushered in the guests, and it was rather a relief to the indifferent to see a person as unmoved as themselves, and who did not exact a mournful face or force tears, as would have been the case with a father, a brother, or a lover. Those who were acquainted soon formed into little groups. One of them was made of Debray, Château-Renaud, and Beauchamp.\n\n“Poor girl,” said Debray, like the rest, paying an involuntary tribute to the sad event,—“poor girl, so young, so rich, so beautiful! Could you have imagined this scene, Château-Renaud, when we saw her, at the most three weeks ago, about to sign that contract?”\n\n“Indeed, no,” said Château-Renaud.”\n\n“Did you know her?”\n\n“I spoke to her once or twice at Madame de Morcerf’s, among the rest; she appeared to me charming, though rather melancholy. Where is her stepmother? Do you know?”\n\n“She is spending the day with the wife of the worthy gentleman who is receiving us.”\n\n50105m\n“Who is he?”\n\n“Whom do you mean?”\n\n“The gentleman who receives us? Is he a deputy?”\n\n“Oh, no. I am condemned to witness those gentlemen every day,” said Beauchamp; “but he is perfectly unknown to me.”\n\n“Have you mentioned this death in your paper?”\n\n“It has been mentioned, but the article is not mine; indeed, I doubt if it will please M. Villefort, for it says that if four successive deaths had happened anywhere else than in the house of the king’s attorney, he would have interested himself somewhat more about it.”\n\n“Still,” said Château-Renaud, “Dr. d’Avrigny, who attends my mother, declares he is in despair about it. But whom are you seeking, Debray?”\n\n“I am seeking the Count of Monte Cristo” said the young man.\n\n“I met him on the boulevard, on my way here,” said Beauchamp. “I think he is about to leave Paris; he was going to his banker.”\n\n“His banker? Danglars is his banker, is he not?” asked Château-Renaud of Debray.\n\n“I believe so,” replied the secretary with slight uneasiness. “But Monte Cristo is not the only one I miss here; I do not see Morrel.”\n\n“Morrel? Do they know him?” asked Château-Renaud. “I think he has only been introduced to Madame de Villefort.”\n\n“Still, he ought to have been here,” said Debray; “I wonder what will be talked about tonight; this funeral is the news of the day. But hush, here comes our minister of justice; he will feel obliged to make some little speech to the cousin,” and the three young men drew near to listen.\n\nBeauchamp told the truth when he said that on his way to the funeral he had met Monte Cristo, who was directing his steps towards the Rue de la Chaussée d’Antin, to M. Danglars’. The banker saw the carriage of the count enter the courtyard, and advanced to meet him with a sad, though affable smile.\n\n“Well,” said he, extending his hand to Monte Cristo, “I suppose you have come to sympathize with me, for indeed misfortune has taken possession of my house. When I perceived you, I was just asking myself whether I had not wished harm towards those poor Morcerfs, which would have justified the proverb of ‘He who wishes misfortunes to happen to others experiences them himself.’ Well, on my word of honor, I answered, ‘No!’ I wished no ill to Morcerf; he was a little proud, perhaps, for a man who like myself has risen from nothing; but we all have our faults. Do you know, count, that persons of our time of life—not that you belong to the class, you are still a young man,—but as I was saying, persons of our time of life have been very unfortunate this year. For example, look at the puritanical procureur, who has just lost his daughter, and in fact nearly all his family, in so singular a manner; Morcerf dishonored and dead; and then myself covered with ridicule through the villany of Benedetto; besides——”\n\n“Besides what?” asked the Count.\n\n“Alas, do you not know?”\n\n“What new calamity?”\n\n“My daughter——”\n\n“Mademoiselle Danglars?”\n\n“Eugénie has left us!”\n\n“Good heavens, what are you telling me?”\n\n“The truth, my dear count. Oh, how happy you must be in not having either wife or children!”\n\n“Do you think so?”\n\n“Indeed I do.”\n\n“And so Mademoiselle Danglars——”\n\n“She could not endure the insult offered to us by that wretch, so she asked permission to travel.”\n\n“And is she gone?”\n\n“The other night she left.”\n\n“With Madame Danglars?”\n\n“No, with a relation. But still, we have quite lost our dear Eugénie; for I doubt whether her pride will ever allow her to return to France.”\n\n“Still, baron,” said Monte Cristo, “family griefs, or indeed any other affliction which would crush a man whose child was his only treasure, are endurable to a millionaire. Philosophers may well say, and practical men will always support the opinion, that money mitigates many trials; and if you admit the efficacy of this sovereign balm, you ought to be very easily consoled—you, the king of finance, the focus of immeasurable power.”\n\nDanglars looked at him askance, as though to ascertain whether he spoke seriously.\n\n“Yes,” he answered, “if a fortune brings consolation, I ought to be consoled; I am rich.”\n\n“So rich, dear sir, that your fortune resembles the pyramids; if you wished to demolish them you could not, and if it were possible, you would not dare!”\n\nDanglars smiled at the good-natured pleasantry of the count. “That reminds me,” he said, “that when you entered I was on the point of signing five little bonds; I have already signed two: will you allow me to do the same to the others?”\n\n“Pray do so.”\n\nThere was a moment’s silence, during which the noise of the banker’s pen was alone heard, while Monte Cristo examined the gilt mouldings on the ceiling.\n\n“Are they Spanish, Haitian, or Neapolitan bonds?” said Monte Cristo.\n\n“No,” said Danglars, smiling, “they are bonds on the bank of France, payable to bearer. Stay, count,” he added, “you, who may be called the emperor, if I claim the title of king of finance, have you many pieces of paper of this size, each worth a million?”\n\nThe count took into his hands the papers, which Danglars had so proudly presented to him, and read:—\n\n“‘To the Governor of the Bank. Please pay to my order, from the fund deposited by me, the sum of a million, and charge the same to my account.\n\n“Baron Danglars.’”\n\n“One, two, three, four, five,” said Monte Cristo; “five millions—why what a Crœsus you are!”\n\n“This is how I transact business,” said Danglars.\n\n“It is really wonderful,” said the count; “above all, if, as I suppose, it is payable at sight.”\n\n“It is, indeed,” said Danglars.\n\n“It is a fine thing to have such credit; really, it is only in France these things are done. Five millions on five little scraps of paper!—it must be seen to be believed.”\n\n“You do not doubt it?”\n\n“You say so with an accent—stay, you shall be convinced; take my clerk to the bank, and you will see him leave it with an order on the Treasury for the same sum.”\n\n“No,” said Monte Cristo folding the five notes, “most decidedly not; the thing is so curious, I will make the experiment myself. I am credited on you for six millions. I have drawn nine hundred thousand francs, you therefore still owe me five millions and a hundred thousand francs. I will take the five scraps of paper that I now hold as bonds, with your signature alone, and here is a receipt in full for the six millions between us. I had prepared it beforehand, for I am much in want of money today.”\n\nAnd Monte Cristo placed the bonds in his pocket with one hand, while with the other he held out the receipt to Danglars. If a thunderbolt had fallen at the banker’s feet, he could not have experienced greater terror.\n\n“What,” he stammered, “do you mean to keep that money? Excuse me, excuse me, but I owe this money to the charity fund,—a deposit which I promised to pay this morning.”\n\n“Oh, well, then,” said Monte Cristo, “I am not particular about these five notes, pay me in a different form; I wished, from curiosity, to take these, that I might be able to say that without any advice or preparation the house of Danglars had paid me five millions without a minute’s delay; it would have been remarkable. But here are your bonds; pay me differently;” and he held the bonds towards Danglars, who seized them like a vulture extending its claws to withhold the food that is being wrested from its grasp.\n\nSuddenly he rallied, made a violent effort to restrain himself, and then a smile gradually widened the features of his disturbed countenance.\n\n50109m\n“Certainly,” he said, “your receipt is money.”\n\n“Oh dear, yes; and if you were at Rome, the house of Thomson & French would make no more difficulty about paying the money on my receipt than you have just done.”\n\n“Pardon me, count, pardon me.”\n\n“Then I may keep this money?”\n\n“Yes,” said Danglars, while the perspiration started from the roots of his hair. “Yes, keep it—keep it.”\n\nMonte Cristo replaced the notes in his pocket with that indescribable expression which seemed to say, “Come, reflect; if you repent there is still time.”\n\n“No,” said Danglars, “no, decidedly no; keep my signatures. But you know none are so formal as bankers in transacting business; I intended this money for the charity fund, and I seemed to be robbing them if I did not pay them with these precise bonds. How absurd—as if one crown were not as good as another. Excuse me;” and he began to laugh loudly, but nervously.\n\n“Certainly, I excuse you,” said Monte Cristo graciously, “and pocket them.” And he placed the bonds in his pocket-book.\n\n“But,” said Danglars, “there is still a sum of one hundred thousand francs?”\n\n“Oh, a mere nothing,” said Monte Cristo. “The balance would come to about that sum; but keep it, and we shall be quits.”\n\n“Count,” said Danglars, “are you speaking seriously?”\n\n“I never joke with bankers,” said Monte Cristo in a freezing manner, which repelled impertinence; and he turned to the door, just as the valet de chambre announced:\n\n“M. de Boville, Receiver-General of the charities.”\n\n“_Ma foi_,” said Monte Cristo; “I think I arrived just in time to obtain your signatures, or they would have been disputed with me.”\n\nDanglars again became pale, and hastened to conduct the count out. Monte Cristo exchanged a ceremonious bow with M. de Boville, who was standing in the waiting-room, and who was introduced into Danglars’ room as soon as the count had left.\n\nThe count’s serious face was illumined by a faint smile, as he noticed the portfolio which the receiver-general held in his hand. At the door he found his carriage, and was immediately driven to the bank. Meanwhile Danglars, repressing all emotion, advanced to meet the receiver-general. We need not say that a smile of condescension was stamped upon his lips.\n\n“Good-morning, creditor,” said he; “for I wager anything it is the creditor who visits me.”\n\n“You are right, baron,” answered M. de Boville; “the charities present themselves to you through me; the widows and orphans depute me to receive alms to the amount of five millions from you.”\n\n“And yet they say orphans are to be pitied,” said Danglars, wishing to prolong the jest. “Poor things!”\n\n“Here I am in their name,” said M. de Boville; “but did you receive my letter yesterday?”\n“Yes.”\n\n“I have brought my receipt.”\n\n50111m\n“My dear M. de Boville, your widows and orphans must oblige me by waiting twenty-four hours, since M. de Monte Cristo whom you just saw leaving here—you did see him, I think?”\n\n“Yes; well?”\n\n“Well, M. de Monte Cristo has just carried off their five millions.”\n“How so?”\n\n“The count has an unlimited credit upon me; a credit opened by Thomson & French, of Rome; he came to demand five millions at once, which I paid him with checks on the bank. My funds are deposited there, and you can understand that if I draw out ten millions on the same day it will appear rather strange to the governor. Two days will be a different thing,” said Danglars, smiling.\n\n“Come,” said Boville, with a tone of entire incredulity, “five millions to that gentleman who just left, and who bowed to me as though he knew me?”\n\n“Perhaps he knows you, though you do not know him; M. de Monte Cristo knows everybody.”\n\n“Five millions!”\n\n“Here is his receipt. Believe your own eyes.” M. de Boville took the paper Danglars presented him, and read:\n\n“Received of Baron Danglars the sum of five million one hundred thousand francs, to be repaid on demand by the house of Thomson & French of Rome.”\n\n“It is really true,” said M. de Boville.\n\n“Do you know the house of Thomson & French?”\n\n“Yes, I once had business to transact with it to the amount of 200,000 francs; but since then I have not heard it mentioned.”\n\n“It is one of the best houses in Europe,” said Danglars, carelessly throwing down the receipt on his desk.\n\n“And he had five millions in your hands alone! Why, this Count of Monte Cristo must be a nabob?”\n\n“Indeed I do not know what he is; he has three unlimited credits—one on me, one on Rothschild, one on Lafitte; and, you see,” he added carelessly, “he has given me the preference, by leaving a balance of 100,000 francs.”\n\nM. de Boville manifested signs of extraordinary admiration.\n\n“I must visit him,” he said, “and obtain some pious grant from him.”\n\n“Oh, you may make sure of him; his charities alone amount to 20,000 francs a month.”\n\n“It is magnificent! I will set before him the example of Madame de Morcerf and her son.”\n\n“What example?”\n\n“They gave all their fortune to the hospitals.”\n\n“What fortune?”\n\n“Their own—M. de Morcerf’s, who is deceased.”\n\n“For what reason?”\n\n“Because they would not spend money so guiltily acquired.”\n\n“And what are they to live upon?”\n\n“The mother retires into the country, and the son enters the army.”\n50113m\n\n“Well, I must confess, these are scruples.”\n\n“I registered their deed of gift yesterday.”\n\n“And how much did they possess?”\n\n“Oh, not much—from twelve to thirteen hundred thousand francs. But to return to our millions.”\n\n“Certainly,” said Danglars, in the most natural tone in the world. “Are you then pressed for this money?”\n\n“Yes; for the examination of our cash takes place tomorrow.”\n\n“Tomorrow? Why did you not tell me so before? Why, it is as good as a century! At what hour does the examination take place?”\n\n“At two o’clock.”\n\n“Send at twelve,” said Danglars, smiling.\n\nM. de Boville said nothing, but nodded his head, and took up the portfolio.\n\n“Now I think of it, you can do better,” said Danglars.\n\n“How do you mean?”\n\n“The receipt of M. de Monte Cristo is as good as money; take it to Rothschild’s or Lafitte’s, and they will take it off your hands at once.”\n\n“What, though payable at Rome?”\n\n“Certainly; it will only cost you a discount of 5,000 or 6,000 francs.”\n\nThe receiver started back.\n\n“_Ma foi!_” he said, “I prefer waiting till tomorrow. What a proposition!”\n\n“I thought, perhaps,” said Danglars with supreme impertinence, “that you had a deficiency to make up?”\n\n“Indeed,” said the receiver.\n\n“And if that were the case it would be worth while to make some sacrifice.”\n\n“Thank you, no, sir.”\n\n“Then it will be tomorrow.”\n\n“Ah, you are laughing at me; send tomorrow at twelve, and the bank shall be notified.”\n\n“I will come myself.”\n\n“Better still, since it will afford me the pleasure of seeing you.” They shook hands.\n\n“By the way,” said M. de Boville, “are you not going to the funeral of poor Mademoiselle de Villefort, which I met on my road here?”\n\n“No,” said the banker; “I have appeared rather ridiculous since that affair of Benedetto, so I remain in the background.”\n\n“Bah, you are wrong. How were you to blame in that affair?”\n\n“Listen—when one bears an irreproachable name, as I do, one is rather sensitive.”\n\n“Everybody pities you, sir; and, above all, Mademoiselle Danglars!”\n\n“Poor Eugénie!” said Danglars; “do you know she is going to embrace a religious life?”\n“No.”\n\n“Alas, it is unhappily but too true. The day after the event, she decided on leaving Paris with a nun of her acquaintance; they are gone to seek a very strict convent in Italy or Spain.”\n\n“Oh, it is terrible!” and M. de Boville retired with this exclamation, after expressing acute sympathy with the father. But he had scarcely left before Danglars, with an energy of action those can alone understand who have seen Robert Macaire represented by Frédérick,24 exclaimed:\n“Fool!”\n\nThen enclosing Monte Cristo’s receipt in a little pocket-book, he added:—“Yes, come at twelve o’clock; I shall then be far away.”\n\nThen he double-locked his door, emptied all his drawers, collected about fifty thousand francs in bank-notes, burned several papers, left others exposed to view, and then commenced writing a letter which he addressed:\n\n“To Madame la Baronne Danglars.”\n\n“I will place it on her table myself tonight,” he murmured. Then taking a passport from his drawer he said,—“Good, it is available for two months longer.”\n\n# Chapter 105. The Cemetery of Père-Lachaise\n\nM. de Boville had indeed met the funeral procession which was taking Valentine to her last home on earth. The weather was dull and stormy, a cold wind shook the few remaining yellow leaves from the boughs of the trees, and scattered them among the crowd which filled the boulevards. M. de Villefort, a true Parisian, considered the cemetery of Père-Lachaise alone worthy of receiving the mortal remains of a Parisian family; there alone the corpses belonging to him would be surrounded by worthy associates. He had therefore purchased a vault, which was quickly occupied by members of his family. On the front of the monument was inscribed: “The families of Saint-Méran and Villefort,” for such had been the last wish expressed by poor Renée, Valentine’s mother. The pompous procession therefore wended its way towards Père-Lachaise from the Faubourg Saint-Honoré. Having crossed Paris, it passed through the Faubourg du Temple, then leaving the exterior boulevards, it reached the cemetery. More than fifty private carriages followed the twenty mourning-coaches, and behind them more than five hundred persons joined in the procession on foot.\n\n50117m\nThese last consisted of all the young people whom Valentine’s death had struck like a thunderbolt, and who, notwithstanding the raw chilliness of the season, could not refrain from paying a last tribute to the memory of the beautiful, chaste, and adorable girl, thus cut off in the flower of her youth.\n\nAs they left Paris, an equipage with four horses, at full speed, was seen to draw up suddenly; it contained Monte Cristo. The count left the carriage and mingled in the crowd who followed on foot. Château-Renaud perceived him and immediately alighting from his _coupé_, joined him; Beauchamp did the same.\n\nThe count looked attentively through every opening in the crowd; he was evidently watching for someone, but his search ended in disappointment.\n\n50119m\n“Where is Morrel?” he asked; “do either of these gentlemen know where he is?”\n\n“We have already asked that question,” said Château-Renaud, “for none of us has seen him.”\n\nThe count was silent, but continued to gaze around him. At length they arrived at the cemetery. The piercing eye of Monte Cristo glanced through clusters of bushes and trees, and was soon relieved from all anxiety, for seeing a shadow glide between the yew-trees, Monte Cristo recognized him whom he sought.\n\nOne funeral is generally very much like another in this magnificent metropolis. Black figures are seen scattered over the long white avenues; the silence of earth and heaven is alone broken by the noise made by the crackling branches of hedges planted around the monuments; then follows the melancholy chant of the priests, mingled now and then with a sob of anguish, escaping from some woman concealed behind a mass of flowers.\n\nThe shadow Monte Cristo had noticed passed rapidly behind the tomb of Abélard and Héloïse, placed itself close to the heads of the horses belonging to the hearse, and following the undertaker’s men, arrived with them at the spot appointed for the burial. Each person’s attention was occupied. Monte Cristo saw nothing but the shadow, which no one else observed. Twice the count left the ranks to see whether the object of his interest had any concealed weapon beneath his clothes. When the procession stopped, this shadow was recognized as Morrel, who, with his coat buttoned up to his throat, his face livid, and convulsively crushing his hat between his fingers, leaned against a tree, situated on an elevation commanding the mausoleum, so that none of the funeral details could escape his observation.\n\nEverything was conducted in the usual manner. A few men, the least impressed of all by the scene, pronounced a discourse, some deploring this premature death, others expatiating on the grief of the father, and one very ingenious person quoting the fact that Valentine had solicited pardon of her father for criminals on whom the arm of justice was ready to fall—until at length they exhausted their stores of metaphor and mournful speeches, elaborate variations on the stanzas of Malherbe to Du Périer.\n\nMonte Cristo heard and saw nothing, or rather he only saw Morrel, whose calmness had a frightful effect on those who knew what was passing in his heart.\n\n“See,” said Beauchamp, pointing out Morrel to Debray. “What is he doing up there?” And they called Château-Renaud’s attention to him.\n\n“How pale he is!” said Château-Renaud, shuddering.\n\n“He is cold,” said Debray.\n\n“Not at all,” said Château-Renaud, slowly; “I think he is violently agitated. He is very susceptible.”\n\n“Bah,” said Debray; “he scarcely knew Mademoiselle de Villefort; you said so yourself.”\n\n“True. Still I remember he danced three times with her at Madame de Morcerf’s. Do you recollect that ball, count, where you produced such an effect?”\n50121m\n\n“No, I do not,” replied Monte Cristo, without even knowing of what or to whom he was speaking, so much was he occupied in watching Morrel, who was holding his breath with emotion.\n\n“The discourse is over; farewell, gentlemen,” said the count, unceremoniously.\n\nAnd he disappeared without anyone seeing whither he went.\n\nThe funeral being over, the guests returned to Paris. Château-Renaud looked for a moment for Morrel; but while they were watching the departure of the count, Morrel had quitted his post, and Château-Renaud, failing in his search, joined Debray and Beauchamp.\n\nMonte Cristo concealed himself behind a large tomb and awaited the arrival of Morrel, who by degrees approached the tomb now abandoned by spectators and workmen. Morrel threw a glance around, but before it reached the spot occupied by Monte Cristo the latter had advanced yet nearer, still unperceived. The young man knelt down. The count, with outstretched neck and glaring eyes, stood in an attitude ready to pounce upon Morrel upon the first occasion. Morrel bent his head till it touched the stone, then clutching the grating with both hands, he murmured:\n\n“Oh, Valentine!”\n\nThe count’s heart was pierced by the utterance of these two words; he stepped forward, and touching the young man’s shoulder, said:\n\n“I was looking for you, my friend.” Monte Cristo expected a burst of passion, but he was deceived, for Morrel turning round, said calmly,—\n\n“You see I was praying.” The scrutinizing glance of the count searched the young man from head to foot. He then seemed more easy.\n\n“Shall I drive you back to Paris?” he asked.\n\n“No, thank you.”\n\n“Do you wish anything?”\n\n“Leave me to pray.”\n\nThe count withdrew without opposition, but it was only to place himself in a situation where he could watch every movement of Morrel, who at length arose, brushed the dust from his knees, and turned towards Paris, without once looking back. He walked slowly down the Rue de la Roquette. The count, dismissing his carriage, followed him about a hundred paces behind. Maximilian crossed the canal and entered the Rue Meslay by the boulevards.\n\nFive minutes after the door had been closed on Morrel’s entrance, it was again opened for the count. Julie was at the entrance of the garden, where she was attentively watching Penelon, who, entering with zeal into his profession of gardener, was very busy grafting some Bengal roses. “Ah, count,” she exclaimed, with the delight manifested by every member of the family whenever he visited the Rue Meslay.\n\n“Maximilian has just returned, has he not, madame?” asked the count.\n\n50123m\n“Yes, I think I saw him pass; but pray, call Emmanuel.”\n\n“Excuse me, madame, but I must go up to Maximilian’s room this instant,” replied Monte Cristo, “I have something of the greatest importance to tell him.”\n\n“Go, then,” she said with a charming smile, which accompanied him until he had disappeared.\n\nMonte Cristo soon ran up the staircase conducting from the ground floor to Maximilian’s room; when he reached the landing he listened attentively, but all was still. Like many old houses occupied by a single family, the room door was panelled with glass; but it was locked, Maximilian was shut in, and it was impossible to see what was passing in the room, because a red curtain was drawn before the glass. The count’s anxiety was manifested by a bright color which seldom appeared on the face of that imperturbable man.\n\n“What shall I do!” he uttered, and reflected for a moment; “shall I ring? No, the sound of a bell, announcing a visitor, will but accelerate the resolution of one in Maximilian’s situation, and then the bell would be followed by a louder noise.”\n\nMonte Cristo trembled from head to foot and as if his determination had been taken with the rapidity of lightning, he struck one of the panes of glass with his elbow; the glass was shivered to atoms, then withdrawing the curtain he saw Morrel, who had been writing at his desk, bound from his seat at the noise of the broken window.\n\n“I beg a thousand pardons,” said the count, “there is nothing the matter, but I slipped down and broke one of your panes of glass with my elbow. Since it is opened, I will take advantage of it to enter your room; do not disturb yourself—do not disturb yourself!”\n\nAnd passing his hand through the broken glass, the count opened the door. Morrel, evidently discomposed, came to meet Monte Cristo less with the intention of receiving him than to exclude his entry.\n\n“_Ma foi_,” said Monte Cristo, rubbing his elbow, “it’s all your servant’s fault; your stairs are so polished, it is like walking on glass.”\n\n“Are you hurt, sir?” coldly asked Morrel.\n\n“I believe not. But what are you about there? You were writing.”\n“I?”\n\n“Your fingers are stained with ink.”\n\n“Ah, true, I was writing. I do sometimes, soldier though I am.”\n\nMonte Cristo advanced into the room; Maximilian was obliged to let him pass, but he followed him.\n\n“You were writing?” said Monte Cristo with a searching look.\n\n“I have already had the honor of telling you I was,” said Morrel.\n\nThe count looked around him.\n\n“Your pistols are beside your desk,” said Monte Cristo, pointing with his finger to the pistols on the table.\n\n“I am on the point of starting on a journey,” replied Morrel disdainfully.\n\n“My friend,” exclaimed Monte Cristo in a tone of exquisite sweetness.\n\n“Sir?”\n\n“My friend, my dear Maximilian, do not make a hasty resolution, I entreat you.”\n\n“I make a hasty resolution?” said Morrel, shrugging his shoulders; “is there anything extraordinary in a journey?”\n\n50125m\n“Maximilian,” said the count, “let us both lay aside the mask we have assumed. You no more deceive me with that false calmness than I impose upon you with my frivolous solicitude. You can understand, can you not, that to have acted as I have done, to have broken that glass, to have intruded on the solitude of a friend—you can understand that, to have done all this, I must have been actuated by real uneasiness, or rather by a terrible conviction. Morrel, you are going to destroy yourself!”\n\n“Indeed, count,” said Morrel, shuddering; “what has put this into your head?”\n\n“I tell you that you are about to destroy yourself,” continued the count, “and here is proof of what I say;” and, approaching the desk, he removed the sheet of paper which Morrel had placed over the letter he had begun, and took the latter in his hands.\n\nMorrel rushed forward to tear it from him, but Monte Cristo perceiving his intention, seized his wrist with his iron grasp.\n\n“You wish to destroy yourself,” said the count; “you have written it.”\n\n“Well,” said Morrel, changing his expression of calmness for one of violence—“well, and if I do intend to turn this pistol against myself, who shall prevent me—who will dare prevent me? All my hopes are blighted, my heart is broken, my life a burden, everything around me is sad and mournful; earth has become distasteful to me, and human voices distract me. It is a mercy to let me die, for if I live I shall lose my reason and become mad. When, sir, I tell you all this with tears of heartfelt anguish, can you reply that I am wrong, can you prevent my putting an end to my miserable existence? Tell me, sir, could you have the courage to do so?”\n\n“Yes, Morrel,” said Monte Cristo, with a calmness which contrasted strangely with the young man’s excitement; “yes, I would do so.”\n\n“You?” exclaimed Morrel, with increasing anger and reproach—“you, who have deceived me with false hopes, who have cheered and soothed me with vain promises, when I might, if not have saved her, at least have seen her die in my arms! You, who pretend to understand everything, even the hidden sources of knowledge,—and who enact the part of a guardian angel upon earth, and could not even find an antidote to a poison administered to a young girl! Ah, sir, indeed you would inspire me with pity, were you not hateful in my eyes.”\n\n“Morrel——”\n\n“Yes; you tell me to lay aside the mask, and I will do so, be satisfied! When you spoke to me at the cemetery, I answered you—my heart was softened; when you arrived here, I allowed you to enter. But since you abuse my confidence, since you have devised a new torture after I thought I had exhausted them all, then, Count of Monte Cristo my pretended benefactor—then, Count of Monte Cristo, the universal guardian, be satisfied, you shall witness the death of your friend;” and Morrel, with a maniacal laugh, again rushed towards the pistols.\n\n“And I again repeat, you shall not commit suicide.”\n\n“Prevent me, then!” replied Morrel, with another struggle, which, like the first, failed in releasing him from the count’s iron grasp.\n\n“I will prevent you.”\n\n50127m\n“And who are you, then, that arrogate to yourself this tyrannical right over free and rational beings?”\n\n“Who am I?” repeated Monte Cristo. “Listen; I am the only man in the world having the right to say to you, ‘Morrel, your father’s son shall not die today;’” and Monte Cristo, with an expression of majesty and sublimity, advanced with arms folded toward the young man, who, involuntarily overcome by the commanding manner of this man, recoiled a step.\n\n“Why do you mention my father?” stammered he; “why do you mingle a recollection of him with the affairs of today?”\n\n“Because I am he who saved your father’s life when he wished to destroy himself, as you do today—because I am the man who sent the purse to your young sister, and the _Pharaon_ to old Morrel—because I am the Edmond Dantès who nursed you, a child, on my knees.”\n\nMorrel made another step back, staggering, breathless, crushed; then all his strength give way, and he fell prostrate at the feet of Monte Cristo. Then his admirable nature underwent a complete and sudden revulsion; he arose, rushed out of the room and to the stairs, exclaiming energetically, “Julie, Julie—Emmanuel, Emmanuel!”\n\nMonte Cristo endeavored also to leave, but Maximilian would have died rather than relax his hold of the handle of the door, which he closed upon the count. Julie, Emmanuel, and some of the servants, ran up in alarm on hearing the cries of Maximilian. Morrel seized their hands, and opening the door exclaimed in a voice choked with sobs:\n\n“On your knees—on your knees—he is our benefactor—the saviour of our father! He is——”\n\nHe would have added “Edmond Dantès,” but the count seized his arm and prevented him.\n\nJulie threw herself into the arms of the count; Emmanuel embraced him as a guardian angel; Morrel again fell on his knees, and struck the ground with his forehead. Then the iron-hearted man felt his heart swell in his breast; a flame seemed to rush from his throat to his eyes, he bent his head and wept. For a while nothing was heard in the room but a succession of sobs, while the incense from their grateful hearts mounted to heaven. Julie had scarcely recovered from her deep emotion when she rushed out of the room, descended to the next floor, ran into the drawing-room with childlike joy and raised the crystal globe which covered the purse given by the unknown of the Allées de Meilhan. Meanwhile, Emmanuel in a broken voice said to the count:\n\n“Oh, count, how could you, hearing us so often speak of our unknown benefactor, seeing us pay such homage of gratitude and adoration to his memory,—how could you continue so long without discovering yourself to us? Oh, it was cruel to us, and—dare I say it?—to you also.”\n\n“Listen, my friends,” said the count—“I may call you so since we have really been friends for the last eleven years—the discovery of this secret has been occasioned by a great event which you must never know. I wished to bury it during my whole life in my own bosom, but your brother Maximilian wrested it from me by a violence he repents of now, I am sure.”\n\nThen turning around, and seeing that Morrel, still on his knees, had thrown himself into an armchair, he added in a low voice, pressing Emmanuel’s hand significantly, “Watch over him.”\n\n“Why so?” asked the young man, surprised.\n\n“I cannot explain myself; but watch over him.” Emmanuel looked around the room and caught sight of the pistols; his eyes rested on the weapons, and he pointed to them. Monte Cristo bent his head. Emmanuel went towards the pistols.\n\n“Leave them,” said Monte Cristo. Then walking towards Morrel, he took his hand; the tumultuous agitation of the young man was succeeded by a profound stupor. Julie returned, holding the silken purse in her hands, while tears of joy rolled down her cheeks, like dewdrops on the rose.\n\n“Here is the relic,” she said; “do not think it will be less dear to us now we are acquainted with our benefactor!”\n\n“My child,” said Monte Cristo, coloring, “allow me to take back that purse? Since you now know my face, I wish to be remembered alone through the affection I hope you will grant me.\n\n“Oh,” said Julie, pressing the purse to her heart, “no, no, I beseech you do not take it, for some unhappy day you will leave us, will you not?”\n\n“You have guessed rightly, madame,” replied Monte Cristo, smiling; “in a week I shall have left this country, where so many persons who merit the vengeance of Heaven lived happily, while my father perished of hunger and grief.”\n\nWhile announcing his departure, the count fixed his eyes on Morrel, and remarked that the words, “I shall have left this country,” had failed to rouse him from his lethargy. He then saw that he must make another struggle against the grief of his friend, and taking the hands of Emmanuel and Julie, which he pressed within his own, he said with the mild authority of a father:\n\n“My kind friends, leave me alone with Maximilian.”\n\nJulie saw the means offered of carrying off her precious relic, which Monte Cristo had forgotten. She drew her husband to the door. “Let us leave them,” she said.\n\nThe count was alone with Morrel, who remained motionless as a statue.\n\n“Come,” said Monte-Cristo, touching his shoulder with his finger, “are you a man again, Maximilian?”\n\n“Yes; for I begin to suffer again.”\n\nThe count frowned, apparently in gloomy hesitation.\n\n“Maximilian, Maximilian,” he said, “the ideas you yield to are unworthy of a Christian.”\n\n“Oh, do not fear, my friend,” said Morrel, raising his head, and smiling with a sweet expression on the count; “I shall no longer attempt my life.”\n\n“Then we are to have no more pistols—no more despair?”\n\n“No; I have found a better remedy for my grief than either a bullet or a knife.”\n\n“Poor fellow, what is it?”\n\n“My grief will kill me of itself.”\n\n“My friend,” said Monte Cristo, with an expression of melancholy equal to his own, “listen to me. One day, in a moment of despair like yours, since it led to a similar resolution, I also wished to kill myself; one day your father, equally desperate, wished to kill himself too. If anyone had said to your father, at the moment he raised the pistol to his head—if anyone had told me, when in my prison I pushed back the food I had not tasted for three days—if anyone had said to either of us then, ‘Live—the day will come when you will be happy, and will bless life!’—no matter whose voice had spoken, we should have heard him with the smile of doubt, or the anguish of incredulity,—and yet how many times has your father blessed life while embracing you—how often have I myself——”\n\n“Ah,” exclaimed Morrel, interrupting the count, “you had only lost your liberty, my father had only lost his fortune, but I have lost Valentine.”\n\n“Look at me,” said Monte Cristo, with that expression which sometimes made him so eloquent and persuasive—“look at me. There are no tears in my eyes, nor is there fever in my veins, yet I see you suffer—you, Maximilian, whom I love as my own son. Well, does not this tell you that in grief, as in life, there is always something to look forward to beyond? Now, if I entreat, if I order you to live, Morrel, it is in the conviction that one day you will thank me for having preserved your life.”\n\n“Oh, heavens,” said the young man, “oh, heavens—what are you saying, count? Take care. But perhaps you have never loved!”\n\n“Child!” replied the count.\n\n“I mean, as I love. You see, I have been a soldier ever since I attained manhood. I reached the age of twenty-nine without loving, for none of the feelings I before then experienced merit the appellation of love. Well, at twenty-nine I saw Valentine; for two years I have loved her, for two years I have seen written in her heart, as in a book, all the virtues of a daughter and wife. Count, to possess Valentine would have been a happiness too infinite, too ecstatic, too complete, too divine for this world, since it has been denied me; but without Valentine the earth is desolate.”\n\n“I have told you to hope,” said the count.\n\n“Then have a care, I repeat, for you seek to persuade me, and if you succeed I should lose my reason, for I should hope that I could again behold Valentine.”\n\nThe count smiled.\n\n“My friend, my father,” said Morrel with excitement, “have a care, I again repeat, for the power you wield over me alarms me. Weigh your words before you speak, for my eyes have already become brighter, and my heart beats strongly; be cautious, or you will make me believe in supernatural agencies. I must obey you, though you bade me call forth the dead or walk upon the water.”\n\n“Hope, my friend,” repeated the count.\n\n“Ah,” said Morrel, falling from the height of excitement to the abyss of despair—“ah, you are playing with me, like those good, or rather selfish mothers who soothe their children with honeyed words, because their screams annoy them. No, my friend, I was wrong to caution you; do not fear, I will bury my grief so deep in my heart, I will disguise it so, that you shall not even care to sympathize with me. Adieu, my friend, adieu!”\n\n“On the contrary,” said the count, “after this time you must live with me—you must not leave me, and in a week we shall have left France behind us.”\n\n“And you still bid me hope?”\n\n“I tell you to hope, because I have a method of curing you.”\n\n“Count, you render me sadder than before, if it be possible. You think the result of this blow has been to produce an ordinary grief, and you would cure it by an ordinary remedy—change of scene.” And Morrel dropped his head with disdainful incredulity.\n\n“What can I say more?” asked Monte Cristo. “I have confidence in the remedy I propose, and only ask you to permit me to assure you of its efficacy.”\n\n“Count, you prolong my agony.”\n\n“Then,” said the count, “your feeble spirit will not even grant me the trial I request? Come—do you know of what the Count of Monte Cristo is capable? do you know that he holds terrestrial beings under his control? nay, that he can almost work a miracle? Well, wait for the miracle I hope to accomplish, or——”\n\n“Or?” repeated Morrel.\n“Or, take care, Morrel, lest I call you ungrateful.”\n\n“Have pity on me, count!”\n\n“I feel so much pity towards you, Maximilian, that—listen to me attentively—if I do not cure you in a month, to the day, to the very hour, mark my words, Morrel, I will place loaded pistols before you, and a cup of the deadliest Italian poison—a poison more sure and prompt than that which has killed Valentine.”\n\n“Will you promise me?”\n\n“Yes; for I am a man, and have suffered like yourself, and also contemplated suicide; indeed, often since misfortune has left me I have longed for the delights of an eternal sleep.”\n\n“But you are sure you will promise me this?” said Morrel, intoxicated.\n\n“I not only promise, but swear it!” said Monte Cristo extending his hand.\n\n“In a month, then, on your honor, if I am not consoled, you will let me take my life into my own hands, and whatever may happen you will not call me ungrateful?”\n\n“In a month, to the day, the very hour and the date is a sacred one, Maximilian. I do not know whether you remember that this is the 5th of September; it is ten years today since I saved your father’s life, who wished to die.”\n\nMorrel seized the count’s hand and kissed it; the count allowed him to pay the homage he felt due to him.\n\n“In a month you will find on the table, at which we shall be then sitting, good pistols and a delicious draught; but, on the other hand, you must promise me not to attempt your life before that time.”\n\n“Oh, I also swear it!”\n\nMonte Cristo drew the young man towards him, and pressed him for some time to his heart. “And now,” he said, “after today, you will come and live with me; you can occupy Haydée’s apartment, and my daughter will at least be replaced by my son.”\n\n“Haydée?” said Morrel, “what has become of her?”\n\n“She departed last night.”\n\n“To leave you?”\n\n“To wait for me. Hold yourself ready then to join me at the Champs-Élysées, and lead me out of this house without anyone seeing my departure.”\n\nMaximilian hung his head, and obeyed with childlike reverence.\n\n# Chapter 106. Dividing the Proceeds\n\nThe apartment on the first floor of the house in the Rue Saint-Germain-des-Prés, where Albert de Morcerf had selected a home for his mother, was let to a very mysterious person. This was a man whose face the concierge himself had never seen, for in the winter his chin was buried in one of the large red handkerchiefs worn by gentlemen’s coachmen on a cold night, and in the summer he made a point of always blowing his nose just as he approached the door. Contrary to custom, this gentleman had not been watched, for as the report ran that he was a person of high rank, and one who would allow no impertinent interference, his _incognito_ was strictly respected.\n\nHis visits were tolerably regular, though occasionally he appeared a little before or after his time, but generally, both in summer and winter, he took possession of his apartment about four o’clock, though he never spent the night there. At half-past three in the winter the fire was lighted by the discreet servant, who had the superintendence of the little apartment, and in the summer ices were placed on the table at the same hour. At four o’clock, as we have already stated, the mysterious personage arrived.\n\nTwenty minutes afterwards a carriage stopped at the house, a lady alighted in a black or dark blue dress, and always thickly veiled; she passed like a shadow through the lodge, and ran upstairs without a sound escaping under the touch of her light foot. No one ever asked her where she was going. Her face, therefore, like that of the gentleman, was perfectly unknown to the two concierges, who were perhaps unequalled throughout the capital for discretion. We need not say she stopped at the first floor. Then she tapped in a peculiar manner at a door, which after being opened to admit her was again fastened, and curiosity penetrated no farther. They used the same precautions in leaving as in entering the house. The lady always left first, and as soon as she had stepped into her carriage, it drove away, sometimes towards the right hand, sometimes to the left; then about twenty minutes afterwards the gentleman would also leave, buried in his cravat or concealed by his handkerchief.\n\nThe day after Monte Cristo had called upon Danglars, the mysterious lodger entered at ten o’clock in the morning instead of four in the afternoon. Almost directly afterwards, without the usual interval of time, a cab arrived, and the veiled lady ran hastily upstairs. The door opened, but before it could be closed, the lady exclaimed:\n\n“Oh, Lucien—oh, my friend!”\n\nThe concierge therefore heard for the first time that the lodger’s name was Lucien; still, as he was the very perfection of a door-keeper, he made up his mind not to tell his wife.\n\n“Well, what is the matter, my dear?” asked the gentleman whose name the lady’s agitation revealed; “tell me what is the matter.”\n\n“Oh, Lucien, can I confide in you?”\n\n“Of course, you know you can do so. But what can be the matter? Your note of this morning has completely bewildered me. This precipitation—this unusual appointment. Come, ease me of my anxiety, or else frighten me at once.”\n\n“Lucien, a great event has happened!” said the lady, glancing inquiringly at Lucien,—“M. Danglars left last night!”\n\n“Left?—M. Danglars left? Where has he gone?”\n\n“I do not know.”\n\n“What do you mean? Has he gone intending not to return?”\n\n“Undoubtedly;—at ten o’clock at night his horses took him to the barrier of Charenton; there a post-chaise was waiting for him—he entered it with his valet de chambre, saying that he was going to Fontainebleau.”\n\n“Then what did you mean——”\n\n“Stay—he left a letter for me.”\n\n“A letter?”\n\n“Yes; read it.”\n\nAnd the baroness took from her pocket a letter which she gave to Debray. Debray paused a moment before reading, as if trying to guess its contents, or perhaps while making up his mind how to act, whatever it might contain. No doubt his ideas were arranged in a few minutes, for he began reading the letter which caused so much uneasiness in the heart of the baroness, and which ran as follows:\n\n“‘Madame and most faithful wife.’”\n\nDebray mechanically stopped and looked at the baroness, whose face became covered with blushes.\n\n“Read,” she said.\n\nDebray continued:\n\n“‘When you receive this, you will no longer have a husband. Oh, you need not be alarmed, you will only have lost him as you have lost your daughter; I mean that I shall be travelling on one of the thirty or forty roads leading out of France. I owe you some explanations for my conduct, and as you are a woman that can perfectly understand me, I will give them. Listen, then. I received this morning five millions which I paid away; almost directly afterwards another demand for the same sum was presented to me; I put this creditor off till tomorrow and I intend leaving today, to escape that tomorrow, which would be rather too unpleasant for me to endure. You understand this, do you not, my most precious wife? I say you understand this, because you are as conversant with my affairs as I am; indeed, I think you understand them better, since I am ignorant of what has become of a considerable portion of my fortune, once very tolerable, while I am sure, madame, that you know perfectly well. For women have infallible instincts; they can even explain the marvellous by an algebraic calculation they have invented; but I, who only understand my own figures, know nothing more than that one day these figures deceived me. Have you admired the rapidity of my fall? Have you been slightly dazzled at the sudden fusion of my ingots? I confess I have seen nothing but the fire; let us hope you have found some gold among the ashes. With this consoling idea, I leave you, madame, and most prudent wife, without any conscientious reproach for abandoning you; you have friends left, and the ashes I have already mentioned, and above all the liberty I hasten to restore to you. And here, madame, I must add another word of explanation. So long as I hoped you were working for the good of our house and for the fortune of our daughter, I philosophically closed my eyes; but as you have transformed that house into a vast ruin I will not be the foundation of another man’s fortune. You were rich when I married you, but little respected. Excuse me for speaking so very candidly, but as this is intended only for ourselves, I do not see why I should weigh my words. I have augmented our fortune, and it has continued to increase during the last fifteen years, till extraordinary and unexpected catastrophes have suddenly overturned it,—without any fault of mine, I can honestly declare. You, madame, have only sought to increase your own, and I am convinced that you have succeeded. I leave you, therefore, as I took you,—rich, but little respected. Adieu! I also intend from this time to work on my own account. Accept my acknowledgments for the example you have set me, and which I intend following.\n\n“‘Your very devoted husband,\n\n“‘Baron Danglars.’”\n\nThe baroness had watched Debray while he read this long and painful letter, and saw him, notwithstanding his self-control, change color once or twice. When he had ended the perusal, he folded the letter and resumed his pensive attitude.\n\n“Well?” asked Madame Danglars, with an anxiety easy to be understood.\n\n“Well, madame?” unhesitatingly repeated Debray.\n\n“With what ideas does that letter inspire you?”\n\n“Oh, it is simple enough, madame; it inspires me with the idea that M. Danglars has left suspiciously.”\n\n“Certainly; but is this all you have to say to me?”\n\n“I do not understand you,” said Debray with freezing coldness.\n\n“He is gone! Gone, never to return!”\n\n“Oh, madame, do not think that!”\n\n“I tell you he will never return. I know his character; he is inflexible in any resolutions formed for his own interests. If he could have made any use of me, he would have taken me with him; he leaves me in Paris, as our separation will conduce to his benefit;—therefore he has gone, and I am free forever,” added Madame Danglars, in the same supplicating tone.\n\nDebray, instead of answering, allowed her to remain in an attitude of nervous inquiry.\n\n“Well?” she said at length, “do you not answer me?”\n\n“I have but one question to ask you,—what do you intend to do?”\n\n“I was going to ask you,” replied the baroness with a beating heart.\n\n“Ah, then, you wish to ask advice of me?”\n\n“Yes; I do wish to ask your advice,” said Madame Danglars with anxious expectation.\n\n“Then if you wish to take my advice,” said the young man coldly, “I would recommend you to travel.”\n\n“To travel!” she murmured.\n\n“Certainly; as M. Danglars says, you are rich, and perfectly free. In my opinion, a withdrawal from Paris is absolutely necessary after the double catastrophe of Mademoiselle Danglars’ broken contract and M. Danglars’ disappearance. The world will think you abandoned and poor, for the wife of a bankrupt would never be forgiven, were she to keep up an appearance of opulence. You have only to remain in Paris for about a fortnight, telling the world you are abandoned, and relating the details of this desertion to your best friends, who will soon spread the report. Then you can quit your house, leaving your jewels and giving up your jointure, and everyone’s mouth will be filled with praises of your disinterestedness. They will know you are deserted, and think you also poor, for I alone know your real financial position, and am quite ready to give up my accounts as an honest partner.”\n\nThe dread with which the pale and motionless baroness listened to this, was equalled by the calm indifference with which Debray had spoken.\n\n“Deserted?” she repeated; “ah, yes, I am, indeed, deserted! You are right, sir, and no one can doubt my position.”\n\nThese were the only words that this proud and violently enamoured woman could utter in response to Debray.\n\n50137m\n“But then you are rich,—very rich, indeed,” continued Debray, taking out some papers from his pocket-book, which he spread upon the table. Madame Danglars did not see them; she was engaged in stilling the beatings of her heart, and restraining the tears which were ready to gush forth. At length a sense of dignity prevailed, and if she did not entirely master her agitation, she at least succeeded in preventing the fall of a single tear.\n\n“Madame,” said Debray, “it is nearly six months since we have been associated. You furnished a principal of 100,000 francs. Our partnership began in the month of April. In May we commenced operations, and in the course of the month gained 450,000 francs. In June the profit amounted to 900,000. In July we added 1,700,000 francs,—it was, you know, the month of the Spanish bonds. In August we lost 300,000 francs at the beginning of the month, but on the 13th we made up for it, and we now find that our accounts, reckoning from the first day of partnership up to yesterday, when I closed them, showed a capital of 2,400,000 francs, that is, 1,200,000 for each of us. Now, madame,” said Debray, delivering up his accounts in the methodical manner of a stockbroker, “there are still 80,000 francs, the interest of this money, in my hands.”\n\n“But,” said the baroness, “I thought you never put the money out to interest.”\n\n“Excuse me, madame,” said Debray coldly, “I had your permission to do so, and I have made use of it. There are, then, 40,000 francs for your share, besides the 100,000 you furnished me to begin with, making in all 1,340,000 francs for your portion. Now, madame, I took the precaution of drawing out your money the day before yesterday; it is not long ago, you see, and I was in continual expectation of being called on to deliver up my accounts. There is your money,—half in bank-notes, the other half in checks payable to bearer. I say _there_, for as I did not consider my house safe enough, or lawyers sufficiently discreet, and as landed property carries evidence with it, and moreover since you have no right to possess anything independent of your husband, I have kept this sum, now your whole fortune, in a chest concealed under that closet, and for greater security I myself concealed it there.\n\n“Now, madame,” continued Debray, first opening the closet, then the chest;—“now, madame, here are 800 notes of 1,000 francs each, resembling, as you see, a large book bound in iron; to this I add a certificate in the funds of 25,000 francs; then, for the odd cash, making I think about 110,000 francs, here is a check upon my banker, who, not being M. Danglars, will pay you the amount, you may rest assured.”\n\nMadame Danglars mechanically took the check, the bond, and the heap of bank-notes. This enormous fortune made no great appearance on the table. Madame Danglars, with tearless eyes, but with her breast heaving with concealed emotion, placed the bank-notes in her bag, put the certificate and check into her pocket-book, and then, standing pale and mute, awaited one kind word of consolation.\n\nBut she waited in vain.\n\n“Now, madame,” said Debray, “you have a splendid fortune, an income of about 60,000 livres a year, which is enormous for a woman who cannot keep an establishment here for a year, at least. You will be able to indulge all your fancies; besides, should you find your income insufficient, you can, for the sake of the past, madame, make use of mine; and I am ready to offer you all I possess, on loan.”\n\n“Thank you, sir—thank you,” replied the baroness; “you forget that what you have just paid me is much more than a poor woman requires, who intends for some time, at least, to retire from the world.”\n\nDebray was, for a moment, surprised, but immediately recovering himself, he bowed with an air which seemed to say, “As you please, madame.”\n\nMadame Danglars had until then, perhaps, hoped for something; but when she saw the careless bow of Debray, and the glance by which it was accompanied, together with his significant silence, she raised her head, and without passion or violence or even hesitation, ran downstairs, disdaining to address a last farewell to one who could thus part from her.\n\n“Bah,” said Debray, when she had left, “these are fine projects! She will remain at home, read novels, and speculate at cards, since she can no longer do so on the Bourse.”\n\nThen taking up his account book, he cancelled with the greatest care all the entries of the amounts he had just paid away.\n\n“I have 1,060,000 francs remaining,” he said. “What a pity Mademoiselle de Villefort is dead! She suited me in every respect, and I would have married her.”\n\nAnd he calmly waited until the twenty minutes had elapsed after Madame Danglars’ departure before he left the house. During this time he occupied himself in making figures, with his watch by his side.\n\nAsmodeus—that diabolical personage, who would have been created by every fertile imagination if Le Sage had not acquired the priority in his great masterpiece—would have enjoyed a singular spectacle, if he had lifted up the roof of the little house in the Rue Saint-Germain-des-Prés, while Debray was casting up his figures.\n\nAbove the room in which Debray had been dividing two millions and a half with Madame Danglars was another, inhabited by persons who have played too prominent a part in the incidents we have related for their appearance not to create some interest.\n\nMercédès and Albert were in that room.\n\nMercédès was much changed within the last few days; not that even in her days of fortune she had ever dressed with the magnificent display which makes us no longer able to recognize a woman when she appears in a plain and simple attire; nor indeed, had she fallen into that state of depression where it is impossible to conceal the garb of misery; no, the change in Mercédès was that her eye no longer sparkled, her lips no longer smiled, and there was now a hesitation in uttering the words which formerly sprang so fluently from her ready wit.\n\nIt was not poverty which had broken her spirit; it was not a want of courage which rendered her poverty burdensome. Mercédès, although deposed from the exalted position she had occupied, lost in the sphere she had now chosen, like a person passing from a room splendidly lighted into utter darkness, appeared like a queen, fallen from her palace to a hovel, and who, reduced to strict necessity, could neither become reconciled to the earthen vessels she was herself forced to place upon the table, nor to the humble pallet which had become her bed.\n\nThe beautiful Catalane and noble countess had lost both her proud glance and charming smile, because she saw nothing but misery around her; the walls were hung with one of the gray papers which economical landlords choose as not likely to show the dirt; the floor was uncarpeted; the furniture attracted the attention to the poor attempt at luxury; indeed, everything offended eyes accustomed to refinement and elegance.\n\nMadame de Morcerf had lived there since leaving her house; the continual silence of the spot oppressed her; still, seeing that Albert continually watched her countenance to judge the state of her feelings, she constrained herself to assume a monotonous smile of the lips alone, which, contrasted with the sweet and beaming expression that usually shone from her eyes, seemed like “moonlight on a statue,”—yielding light without warmth.\n\nAlbert, too, was ill at ease; the remains of luxury prevented him from sinking into his actual position. If he wished to go out without gloves, his hands appeared too white; if he wished to walk through the town, his boots seemed too highly polished. Yet these two noble and intelligent creatures, united by the indissoluble ties of maternal and filial love, had succeeded in tacitly understanding one another, and economizing their stores, and Albert had been able to tell his mother without extorting a change of countenance:\n\n“Mother, we have no more money.”\n50141m\n\nMercédès had never known misery; she had often, in her youth, spoken of poverty, but between want and necessity, those synonymous words, there is a wide difference.\n\nAmongst the Catalans, Mercédès wished for a thousand things, but still she never really wanted any. So long as the nets were good, they caught fish; and so long as they sold their fish, they were able to buy twine for new nets. And then, shut out from friendship, having but one affection, which could not be mixed up with her ordinary pursuits, she thought of herself—of no one but herself. Upon the little she earned she lived as well as she could; now there were two to be supported, and nothing to live upon.\n\nWinter approached. Mercédès had no fire in that cold and naked room—she, who was accustomed to stoves which heated the house from the hall to the boudoir; she had not even one little flower—she whose apartment had been a conservatory of costly exotics. But she had her son. Hitherto the excitement of fulfilling a duty had sustained them. Excitement, like enthusiasm, sometimes renders us unconscious to the things of earth. But the excitement had calmed down, and they felt themselves obliged to descend from dreams to reality; after having exhausted the ideal, they found they must talk of the actual.\n\n“Mother,” exclaimed Albert, just as Madame Danglars was descending the stairs, “let us reckon our riches, if you please; I want capital to build my plans upon.”\n\n“Capital—nothing!” replied Mercédès with a mournful smile.\n\n“No, mother,—capital 3,000 francs. And I have an idea of our leading a delightful life upon this 3,000 francs.”\n\n“Child!” sighed Mercédès.\n\n“Alas, dear mother,” said the young man, “I have unhappily spent too much of your money not to know the value of it. These 3,000 francs are enormous, and I intend building upon this foundation a miraculous certainty for the future.”\n\n“You say this, my dear boy; but do you think we ought to accept these 3,000 francs?” said Mercédès, coloring.\n\n“I think so,” answered Albert in a firm tone. “We will accept them the more readily, since we have them not here; you know they are buried in the garden of the little house in the Allées de Meilhan, at Marseilles. With 200 francs we can reach Marseilles.”\n\n“With 200 francs?—are you sure, Albert?”\n\n“Oh, as for that, I have made inquiries respecting the diligences and steamboats, and my calculations are made. You will take your place in the _coupé_ to Châlons. You see, mother, I treat you handsomely for thirty-five francs.”\n\nAlbert then took a pen, and wrote:\n\nFrs. _Coupé_, thirty-five francs........ .............. ........ 35. From Châlons to Lyons you will go on by the steamboat.. 6. From Lyons to Avignon (still by steamboat).... ......... 16. From Avignon to Marseilles, seven francs........ ....... 7. Expenses on the road, about fifty francs........ ....... 50. Total......... .............. .............. ............ 114 frs.\n\n“Let us put down 120,” added Albert, smiling. “You see I am generous, am I not, mother?”\n\n“But you, my poor child?”\n\n“I? do you not see that I reserve eighty francs for myself? A young man does not require luxuries; besides, I know what travelling is.”\n\n“With a post-chaise and valet de chambre?”\n\n“Any way, mother.”\n\n“Well, be it so. But these 200 francs?”\n\n“Here they are, and 200 more besides. See, I have sold my watch for 100 francs, and the guard and seals for 300. How fortunate that the ornaments were worth more than the watch. Still the same story of superfluities! Now I think we are rich, since instead of the 114 francs we require for the journey we find ourselves in possession of 250.”\n\n“But we owe something in this house?”\n\n“Thirty francs; but I pay that out of my 150 francs,—that is understood,—and as I require only eighty francs for my journey, you see I am overwhelmed with luxury. But that is not all. What do you say to this, mother?”\n\nAnd Albert took out of a little pocket-book with golden clasps, a remnant of his old fancies, or perhaps a tender souvenir from one of the mysterious and veiled ladies who used to knock at his little door,—Albert took out of this pocket-book a note of 1,000 francs.\n\n“What is this?” asked Mercédès.\n\n“A thousand francs.”\n\n“But whence have you obtained them?”\n\n“Listen to me, mother, and do not yield too much to agitation.” And Albert, rising, kissed his mother on both cheeks, then stood looking at her. “You cannot imagine, mother, how beautiful I think you!” said the young man, impressed with a profound feeling of filial love. “You are, indeed, the most beautiful and most noble woman I ever saw!”\n\n“Dear child!” said Mercédès, endeavoring in vain to restrain a tear which glistened in the corner of her eye. “Indeed, you only wanted misfortune to change my love for you to admiration. I am not unhappy while I possess my son!”\n\n“Ah, just so,” said Albert; “here begins the trial. Do you know the decision we have come to, mother?”\n\n“Have we come to any?”\n\n“Yes; it is decided that you are to live at Marseilles, and that I am to leave for Africa, where I will earn for myself the right to use the name I now bear, instead of the one I have thrown aside.” Mercédès sighed. “Well, mother, I yesterday engaged myself as substitute in the Spahis,”25 added the young man, lowering his eyes with a certain feeling of shame, for even he was unconscious of the sublimity of his self-abasement. “I thought my body was my own, and that I might sell it. I yesterday took the place of another. I sold myself for more than I thought I was worth,” he added, attempting to smile; “I fetched 2,000 francs.”\n\n“Then these 1,000 francs——” said Mercédès, shuddering.\n\n“Are the half of the sum, mother; the other will be paid in a year.”\n\nMercédès raised her eyes to heaven with an expression it would be impossible to describe, and tears, which had hitherto been restrained, now yielded to her emotion, and ran down her cheeks.\n\n“The price of his blood!” she murmured.\n\n“Yes, if I am killed,” said Albert, laughing. “But I assure you, mother, I have a strong intention of defending my person, and I never felt half so strong an inclination to live as I do now.”\n\n“Merciful Heavens!”\n\n“Besides, mother, why should you make up your mind that I am to be killed? Has Lamoricière, that Ney of the South, been killed? Has Changarnier been killed? Has Bedeau been killed? Has Morrel, whom we know, been killed? Think of your joy, mother, when you see me return with an embroidered uniform! I declare, I expect to look magnificent in it, and chose that regiment only from vanity.”\n\nMercédès sighed while endeavoring to smile; the devoted mother felt that she ought not to allow the whole weight of the sacrifice to fall upon her son.\n\n“Well, now you understand, mother!” continued Albert; “here are more than 4,000 francs settled on you; upon these you can live at least two years.”\n\n“Do you think so?” said Mercédès.\n\nThese words were uttered in so mournful a tone that their real meaning did not escape Albert; he felt his heart beat, and taking his mother’s hand within his own he said, tenderly:\n\n“Yes, you will live!”\n\n“I shall live!—then you will not leave me, Albert?”\n\n“Mother, I must go,” said Albert in a firm, calm voice; “you love me too well to wish me to remain useless and idle with you; besides, I have signed.”\n50145m\n\n“You will obey your own wish and the will of Heaven!”\n\n“Not my own wish, mother, but reason—necessity. Are we not two despairing creatures? What is life to you?—Nothing. What is life to me?—Very little without you, mother; for believe me, but for you I should have ceased to live on the day I doubted my father and renounced his name. Well, I will live, if you promise me still to hope; and if you grant me the care of your future prospects, you will redouble my strength. Then I will go to the governor of Algeria; he has a royal heart, and is essentially a soldier; I will tell him my gloomy story. I will beg him to turn his eyes now and then towards me, and if he keep his word and interest himself for me, in six months I shall be an officer, or dead. If I am an officer, your fortune is certain, for I shall have money enough for both, and, moreover, a name we shall both be proud of, since it will be our own. If I am killed—well then mother, you can also die, and there will be an end of our misfortunes.”\n\n“It is well,” replied Mercédès, with her eloquent glance; “you are right, my love; let us prove to those who are watching our actions that we are worthy of compassion.”\n\n“But let us not yield to gloomy apprehensions,” said the young man; “I assure you we are, or rather we shall be, very happy. You are a woman at once full of spirit and resignation; I have become simple in my tastes, and am without passion, I hope. Once in service, I shall be rich—once in M. Dantès’ house, you will be at rest. Let us strive, I beseech you,—let us strive to be cheerful.”\n\n“Yes, let us strive, for you ought to live, and to be happy, Albert.”\n\n“And so our division is made, mother,” said the young man, affecting ease of mind. “We can now part; come, I shall engage your passage.”\n\n“And you, my dear boy?”\n\n“I shall stay here for a few days longer; we must accustom ourselves to parting. I want recommendations and some information relative to Africa. I will join you again at Marseilles.”\n\n“Well, be it so—let us part,” said Mercédès, folding around her shoulders the only shawl she had taken away, and which accidentally happened to be a valuable black cashmere. Albert gathered up his papers hastily, rang the bell to pay the thirty francs he owed to the landlord, and offering his arm to his mother, they descended the stairs.\n\nSomeone was walking down before them, and this person, hearing the rustling of a silk dress, turned around. “Debray!” muttered Albert.\n\n“You, Morcerf?” replied the secretary, resting on the stairs. Curiosity had vanquished the desire of preserving his _incognito_, and he was recognized. It was, indeed, strange in this unknown spot to find the young man whose misfortunes had made so much noise in Paris.\n\n“Morcerf!” repeated Debray. Then noticing in the dim light the still youthful and veiled figure of Madame de Morcerf:\n\n“Pardon me,” he added with a smile, “I leave you, Albert.” Albert understood his thoughts.\n\n“Mother,” he said, turning towards Mercédès, “this is M. Debray, secretary of the Minister for the Interior, once a friend of mine.”\n\n“How once?” stammered Debray; “what do you mean?”\n\n“I say so, M. Debray, because I have no friends now, and I ought not to have any. I thank you for having recognized me, sir.” Debray stepped forward, and cordially pressed the hand of his interlocutor.\n\n“Believe me, dear Albert,” he said, with all the emotion he was capable of feeling,—“believe me, I feel deeply for your misfortunes, and if in any way I can serve you, I am yours.”\n\n“Thank you, sir,” said Albert, smiling. “In the midst of our misfortunes, we are still rich enough not to require assistance from anyone. We are leaving Paris, and when our journey is paid, we shall have 5,000 francs left.”\n\nThe blood mounted to the temples of Debray, who held a million in his pocket-book, and unimaginative as he was he could not help reflecting that the same house had contained two women, one of whom, justly dishonored, had left it poor with 1,500,000 francs under her cloak, while the other, unjustly stricken, but sublime in her misfortune, was yet rich with a few deniers. This parallel disturbed his usual politeness, the philosophy he witnessed appalled him, he muttered a few words of general civility and ran downstairs.\n\nThat day the minister’s clerks and the subordinates had a great deal to put up with from his ill-humor. But that same night, he found himself the possessor of a fine house, situated on the Boulevard de la Madeleine, and an income of 50,000 livres.\n\nThe next day, just as Debray was signing the deed, that is about five o’clock in the afternoon, Madame de Morcerf, after having affectionately embraced her son, entered the _coupé_ of the diligence, which closed upon her.\n\nA man was hidden in Lafitte’s banking-house, behind one of the little arched windows which are placed above each desk; he saw Mercédès enter the diligence, and he also saw Albert withdraw. Then he passed his hand across his forehead, which was clouded with doubt.\n\n“Alas,” he exclaimed, “how can I restore the happiness I have taken away from these poor innocent creatures? God help me!”\n\n# Chapter 107. The Lions’ Den\n\nOne division of La Force, in which the most dangerous and desperate prisoners are confined, is called the court of Saint-Bernard. The prisoners, in their expressive language, have named it the “Lions’ Den,” probably because the captives possess teeth which frequently gnaw the bars, and sometimes the keepers also. It is a prison within a prison; the walls are double the thickness of the rest. The gratings are every day carefully examined by jailers, whose herculean proportions and cold pitiless expression prove them to have been chosen to reign over their subjects for their superior activity and intelligence.\n\nThe courtyard of this quarter is enclosed by enormous walls, over which the sun glances obliquely, when it deigns to penetrate into this gulf of moral and physical deformity. On this paved yard are to be seen,—pacing to and fro from morning till night, pale, careworn, and haggard, like so many shadows,—the men whom justice holds beneath the steel she is sharpening. There, crouched against the side of the wall which attracts and retains the most heat, they may be seen sometimes talking to one another, but more frequently alone, watching the door, which sometimes opens to call forth one from the gloomy assemblage, or to throw in another outcast from society.\n\nThe court of Saint-Bernard has its own particular apartment for the reception of guests; it is a long rectangle, divided by two upright gratings placed at a distance of three feet from one another to prevent a visitor from shaking hands with or passing anything to the prisoners. It is a wretched, damp, nay, even horrible spot, more especially when we consider the agonizing conferences which have taken place between those iron bars. And yet, frightful though this spot may be, it is looked upon as a kind of paradise by the men whose days are numbered; it is so rare for them to leave the Lions’ Den for any other place than the barrier Saint-Jacques, the galleys! or solitary confinement.\n\nIn the court which we have attempted to describe, and from which a damp vapor was rising, a young man with his hands in his pockets, who had excited much curiosity among the inhabitants of the “Den,” might be seen walking. The cut of his clothes would have made him pass for an elegant man, if those clothes had not been torn to shreds; still they did not show signs of wear, and the fine cloth, beneath the careful hands of the prisoner, soon recovered its gloss in the parts which were still perfect, for the wearer tried his best to make it assume the appearance of a new coat. He bestowed the same attention upon the cambric front of a shirt, which had considerably changed in color since his entrance into the prison, and he polished his varnished boots with the corner of a handkerchief embroidered with initials surmounted by a coronet.\n\nSome of the inmates of the “Lions’ Den” were watching the operations of the prisoner’s toilet with considerable interest.\n\n“See, the prince is pluming himself,” said one of the thieves.\n\n“He’s a fine looking fellow,” said another; “if he had only a comb and hair-grease, he’d take the shine off the gentlemen in white kids.”\n\n“His coat looks nearly new, and his boots are brilliant. It is pleasant to have such well-dressed brethren; and those gendarmes behaved shamefully. What jealousy; to tear such clothes!”\n\n“He looks like a big-bug,” said another; “dresses in fine style. And, then, to be here so young! Oh, what larks!”\n\nMeanwhile the object of this hideous admiration approached the wicket, against which one of the keepers was leaning.\n\n“Come, sir,” he said, “lend me twenty francs; you will soon be paid; you run no risks with me. Remember, I have relations who possess more millions than you have deniers. Come, I beseech you, lend me twenty francs, so that I may buy a dressing-gown; it is intolerable always to be in a coat and boots! And what a coat, sir, for a prince of the Cavalcanti!”\n\nThe keeper turned his back, and shrugged his shoulders; he did not even laugh at what would have caused anyone else to do so; he had heard so many utter the same things,—indeed, he heard nothing else.\n\n“Come,” said Andrea, “you are a man void of compassion; I’ll have you turned out.”\n\nThis made the keeper turn around, and he burst into a loud laugh. The prisoners then approached and formed a circle.\n\n“I tell you that with that wretched sum,” continued Andrea, “I could obtain a coat, and a room in which to receive the illustrious visitor I am daily expecting.”\n\n“Of course—of course,” said the prisoners;—“anyone can see he’s a gentleman!”\n\n“Well, then, lend him the twenty francs,” said the keeper, leaning on the other shoulder; “surely you will not refuse a comrade!”\n50151m\n\n“I am no comrade of these people,” said the young man, proudly, “you have no right to insult me thus.”\n\nThe thieves looked at one another with low murmurs, and a storm gathered over the head of the aristocratic prisoner, raised less by his own words than by the manner of the keeper. The latter, sure of quelling the tempest when the waves became too violent, allowed them to rise to a certain pitch that he might be revenged on the importunate Andrea, and besides it would afford him some recreation during the long day.\n\nThe thieves had already approached Andrea, some screaming, _“La savate—La savate!”_26 a cruel operation, which consists in cuffing a comrade who may have fallen into disgrace, not with an old shoe, but with an iron-heeled one. Others proposed the _anguille_, another kind of recreation, in which a handkerchief is filled with sand, pebbles, and two-sous pieces, when they have them, which the wretches beat like a flail over the head and shoulders of the unhappy sufferer.\n\n“Let us horsewhip the fine gentleman!” said others.\n\nBut Andrea, turning towards them, winked his eyes, rolled his tongue around his cheeks, and smacked his lips in a manner equivalent to a hundred words among the bandits when forced to be silent. It was a Masonic sign Caderousse had taught him. He was immediately recognized as one of them; the handkerchief was thrown down, and the iron-heeled shoe replaced on the foot of the wretch to whom it belonged.\n\nSome voices were heard to say that the gentleman was right; that he intended to be civil, in his way, and that they would set the example of liberty of conscience,—and the mob retired. The keeper was so stupefied at this scene that he took Andrea by the hands and began examining his person, attributing the sudden submission of the inmates of the Lions’ Den to something more substantial than mere fascination.\n\nAndrea made no resistance, although he protested against it. Suddenly a voice was heard at the wicket.\n\n“Benedetto!” exclaimed an inspector. The keeper relaxed his hold.\n\n“I am called,” said Andrea.\n\n“To the visitors’ room!” said the same voice.\n\n“You see someone pays me a visit. Ah, my dear sir, you will see whether a Cavalcanti is to be treated like a common person!”\n\nAnd Andrea, gliding through the court like a black shadow, rushed out through the wicket, leaving his comrades, and even the keeper, lost in wonder. Certainly a call to the visitors’ room had scarcely astonished Andrea less than themselves, for the wily youth, instead of making use of his privilege of waiting to be claimed on his entry into La Force, had maintained a rigid silence.\n\n50153m\n“Everything,” he said, “proves me to be under the protection of some powerful person,—this sudden fortune, the facility with which I have overcome all obstacles, an unexpected family and an illustrious name awarded to me, gold showered down upon me, and the most splendid alliances about to be entered into. An unhappy lapse of fortune and the absence of my protector have cast me down, certainly, but not forever. The hand which has retreated for a while will be again stretched forth to save me at the very moment when I shall think myself sinking into the abyss. Why should I risk an imprudent step? It might alienate my protector. He has two means of extricating me from this dilemma,—the one by a mysterious escape, managed through bribery; the other by buying off my judges with gold. I will say and do nothing until I am convinced that he has quite abandoned me, and then——”\n\nAndrea had formed a plan which was tolerably clever. The unfortunate youth was intrepid in the attack, and rude in the defence. He had borne with the public prison, and with privations of all sorts; still, by degrees nature, or rather custom, had prevailed, and he suffered from being naked, dirty, and hungry. It was at this moment of discomfort that the inspector’s voice called him to the visiting-room. Andrea felt his heart leap with joy. It was too soon for a visit from the examining magistrate, and too late for one from the director of the prison, or the doctor; it must, then, be the visitor he hoped for. Behind the grating of the room into which Andrea had been led, he saw, while his eyes dilated with surprise, the dark and intelligent face of M. Bertuccio, who was also gazing with sad astonishment upon the iron bars, the bolted doors, and the shadow which moved behind the other grating.\n\n“Ah,” said Andrea, deeply affected.\n\n“Good morning, Benedetto,” said Bertuccio, with his deep, hollow voice.\n\n“You—you?” said the young man, looking fearfully around him.\n\n“Do you not recognize me, unhappy child?”\n\n“Silence,—be silent!” said Andrea, who knew the delicate sense of hearing possessed by the walls; “for Heaven’s sake, do not speak so loud!”\n\n“You wish to speak with me alone, do you not?” said Bertuccio.\n“Oh, yes.”\n\n“That is well.”\n\nAnd Bertuccio, feeling in his pocket, signed to a keeper whom he saw through the window of the wicket.\n\n“Read?” he said.\n\n“What is that?” asked Andrea.\n\n“An order to conduct you to a room, and to leave you there to talk to me.”\n\n“Oh,” cried Andrea, leaping with joy. Then he mentally added,—“Still my unknown protector! I am not forgotten. They wish for secrecy, since we are to converse in a private room. I understand, Bertuccio has been sent by my protector.”\n\nThe keeper spoke for a moment with an official, then opened the iron gates and conducted Andrea to a room on the first floor. The room was whitewashed, as is the custom in prisons, but it looked quite brilliant to a prisoner, though a stove, a bed, a chair, and a table formed the whole of its sumptuous furniture. Bertuccio sat down upon the chair, Andrea threw himself upon the bed; the keeper retired.\n\n“Now,” said the steward, “what have you to tell me?”\n\n“And you?” said Andrea.\n\n“You speak first.”\n\n“Oh, no. You must have much to tell me, since you have come to seek me.”\n\n50155m\n“Well, be it so. You have continued your course of villany; you have robbed—you have assassinated.”\n\n“Well, I should say! If you had me taken to a private room only to tell me this, you might have saved yourself the trouble. I know all these things. But there are some with which, on the contrary, I am not acquainted. Let us talk of those, if you please. Who sent you?”\n\n“Come, come, you are going on quickly, M. Benedetto!”\n\n“Yes, and to the point. Let us dispense with useless words. Who sends you?”\n\n“No one.”\n\n“How did you know I was in prison?”\n\n“I recognized you, some time since, as the insolent dandy who so gracefully mounted his horse in the Champs-Élysées.”\n\n“Oh, the Champs-Élysées? Ah, yes; we burn, as they say at the game of pincette. The Champs-Élysées? Come, let us talk a little about my father.”\n\n“Who, then, am I?”\n\n“You, sir?—you are my adopted father. But it was not you, I presume, who placed at my disposal 100,000 francs, which I spent in four or five months; it was not you who manufactured an Italian gentleman for my father; it was not you who introduced me into the world, and had me invited to a certain dinner at Auteuil, which I fancy I am eating at this moment, in company with the most distinguished people in Paris—amongst the rest with a certain procureur, whose acquaintance I did very wrong not to cultivate, for he would have been very useful to me just now;—it was not you, in fact, who bailed me for one or two millions, when the fatal discovery of my little secret took place. Come, speak, my worthy Corsican, speak!”\n\n“What do you wish me to say?”\n\n“I will help you. You were speaking of the Champs-Élysées just now, worthy foster-father.”\n\n“Well?”\n\n“Well, in the Champs-Élysées there resides a very rich gentleman.”\n\n“At whose house you robbed and murdered, did you not?”\n\n“I believe I did.”\n\n“The Count of Monte Cristo?”\n\n“’Tis you who have named him, as M. Racine says. Well, am I to rush into his arms, and strain him to my heart, crying, ‘My father, my father!’ like Monsieur Pixérécourt.”27\n\n“Do not let us jest,” gravely replied Bertuccio, “and dare not to utter that name again as you have pronounced it.”\n\n“Bah,” said Andrea, a little overcome, by the solemnity of Bertuccio’s manner, “why not?”\n\n“Because the person who bears it is too highly favored by Heaven to be the father of such a wretch as you.”\n\n“Oh, these are fine words.”\n\n“And there will be fine doings, if you do not take care.”\n\n“Menaces—I do not fear them. I will say——”\n\n“Do you think you are engaged with a pygmy like yourself?” said Bertuccio, in so calm a tone, and with so steadfast a look, that Andrea was moved to the very soul. “Do you think you have to do with galley-slaves, or novices in the world? Benedetto, you are fallen into terrible hands; they are ready to open for you—make use of them. Do not play with the thunderbolt they have laid aside for a moment, but which they can take up again instantly, if you attempt to intercept their movements.”\n50157m\n\n“My father—I will know who my father is,” said the obstinate youth; “I will perish if I must, but I _will_ know it. What does scandal signify to me? What possessions, what reputation, what ‘pull,’ as Beauchamp says,—have I? You great people always lose something by scandal, notwithstanding your millions. Come, who is my father?”\n\n“I came to tell you.”\n\n“Ah,” cried Benedetto, his eyes sparkling with joy. Just then the door opened, and the jailer, addressing himself to Bertuccio, said:\n\n“Excuse me, sir, but the examining magistrate is waiting for the prisoner.”\n\n“And so closes our interview,” said Andrea to the worthy steward; “I wish the troublesome fellow were at the devil!”\n\n“I will return tomorrow,” said Bertuccio.\n\n“Good! Gendarmes, I am at your service. Ah, sir, do leave a few crowns for me at the gate that I may have some things I am in need of!”\n\n“It shall be done,” replied Bertuccio.\n\nAndrea extended his hand; Bertuccio kept his own in his pocket, and merely jingled a few pieces of money.\n\n“That’s what I mean,” said Andrea, endeavoring to smile, quite overcome by the strange tranquillity of Bertuccio.\n\n“Can I be deceived?” he murmured, as he stepped into the oblong and grated vehicle which they call “the salad basket.”\n\n“Never mind, we shall see! Tomorrow, then!” he added, turning towards Bertuccio.\n\n“Tomorrow!” replied the steward.\n\n# Chapter 108. The Judge\n\nWe remember that the Abbé Busoni remained alone with Noirtier in the chamber of death, and that the old man and the priest were the sole guardians of the young girl’s body. Perhaps it was the Christian exhortations of the abbé, perhaps his kind charity, perhaps his persuasive words, which had restored the courage of Noirtier, for ever since he had conversed with the priest his violent despair had yielded to a calm resignation which surprised all who knew his excessive affection for Valentine.\n\nM. de Villefort had not seen his father since the morning of the death. The whole establishment had been changed; another valet was engaged for himself, a new servant for Noirtier, two women had entered Madame de Villefort’s service,—in fact, everywhere, to the concierge and coachmen, new faces were presented to the different masters of the house, thus widening the division which had always existed between the members of the same family. The assizes, also, were about to begin, and Villefort, shut up in his room, exerted himself with feverish anxiety in drawing up the case against the murderer of Caderousse. This affair, like all those in which the Count of Monte Cristo had interfered, caused a great sensation in Paris. The proofs were certainly not convincing, since they rested upon a few words written by an escaped galley-slave on his death-bed, and who might have been actuated by hatred or revenge in accusing his companion. But the mind of the procureur was made up; he felt assured that Benedetto was guilty, and he hoped by his skill in conducting this aggravated case to flatter his self-love, which was about the only vulnerable point left in his frozen heart.\n\nThe case was therefore prepared owing to the incessant labor of Villefort, who wished it to be the first on the list in the coming assizes. He had been obliged to seclude himself more than ever, to evade the enormous number of applications presented to him for the purpose of obtaining tickets of admission to the court on the day of trial. And then so short a time had elapsed since the death of poor Valentine, and the gloom which overshadowed the house was so recent, that no one wondered to see the father so absorbed in his professional duties, which were the only means he had of dissipating his grief.\n\nOnce only had Villefort seen his father; it was the day after that upon which Bertuccio had paid his second visit to Benedetto, when the latter was to learn his father’s name. The magistrate, harassed and fatigued, had descended to the garden of his house, and in a gloomy mood, similar to that in which Tarquin lopped off the tallest poppies, he began knocking off with his cane the long and dying branches of the rose-trees, which, placed along the avenue, seemed like the spectres of the brilliant flowers which had bloomed in the past season.\n\nMore than once he had reached that part of the garden where the famous boarded gate stood overlooking the deserted enclosure, always returning by the same path, to begin his walk again, at the same pace and with the same gesture, when he accidentally turned his eyes towards the house, whence he heard the noisy play of his son, who had returned from school to spend the Sunday and Monday with his mother.\n\nWhile doing so, he observed M. Noirtier at one of the open windows, where the old man had been placed that he might enjoy the last rays of the sun which yet yielded some heat, and was now shining upon the dying flowers and red leaves of the creeper which twined around the balcony.\n\nThe eye of the old man was riveted upon a spot which Villefort could scarcely distinguish. His glance was so full of hate, of ferocity, and savage impatience, that Villefort turned out of the path he had been pursuing, to see upon what person this dark look was directed.\n\nThen he saw beneath a thick clump of linden-trees, which were nearly divested of foliage, Madame de Villefort sitting with a book in her hand, the perusal of which she frequently interrupted to smile upon her son, or to throw back his elastic ball, which he obstinately threw from the drawing-room into the garden.\n\nVillefort became pale; he understood the old man’s meaning.\n\nNoirtier continued to look at the same object, but suddenly his glance was transferred from the wife to the husband, and Villefort himself had to submit to the searching investigation of eyes, which, while changing their direction and even their language, had lost none of their menacing expression. Madame de Villefort, unconscious of the passions that exhausted their fire over her head, at that moment held her son’s ball, and was making signs to him to reclaim it with a kiss. Edward begged for a long while, the maternal kiss probably not offering sufficient recompense for the trouble he must take to obtain it; however at length he decided, leaped out of the window into a cluster of heliotropes and daisies, and ran to his mother, his forehead streaming with perspiration. Madame de Villefort wiped his forehead, pressed her lips upon it, and sent him back with the ball in one hand and some bonbons in the other.\n\nVillefort, drawn by an irresistible attraction, like that of the bird to the serpent, walked towards the house. As he approached it, Noirtier’s gaze followed him, and his eyes appeared of such a fiery brightness that Villefort felt them pierce to the depths of his heart. In that earnest look might be read a deep reproach, as well as a terrible menace. Then Noirtier raised his eyes to heaven, as though to remind his son of a forgotten oath.\n\n“It is well, sir,” replied Villefort from below,—“it is well; have patience but one day longer; what I have said I will do.”\n\nNoirtier seemed to be calmed by these words, and turned his eyes with indifference to the other side. Villefort violently unbuttoned his greatcoat, which seemed to strangle him, and passing his livid hand across his forehead, entered his study.\n\nThe night was cold and still; the family had all retired to rest but Villefort, who alone remained up, and worked till five o’clock in the morning, reviewing the last interrogatories made the night before by the examining magistrates, compiling the depositions of the witnesses, and putting the finishing stroke to the deed of accusation, which was one of the most energetic and best conceived of any he had yet delivered.\n\nThe next day, Monday, was the first sitting of the assizes. The morning dawned dull and gloomy, and Villefort saw the dim gray light shine upon the lines he had traced in red ink. The magistrate had slept for a short time while the lamp sent forth its final struggles; its flickerings awoke him, and he found his fingers as damp and purple as though they had been dipped in blood.\n\nHe opened the window; a bright yellow streak crossed the sky, and seemed to divide in half the poplars, which stood out in black relief on the horizon. In the clover-fields beyond the chestnut-trees, a lark was mounting up to heaven, while pouring out her clear morning song. The damps of the dew bathed the head of Villefort, and refreshed his memory.\n\n“Today,” he said with an effort,—“today the man who holds the blade of justice must strike wherever there is guilt.”\n\nInvoluntarily his eyes wandered towards the window of Noirtier’s room, where he had seen him the preceding night. The curtain was drawn, and yet the image of his father was so vivid to his mind that he addressed the closed window as though it had been open, and as if through the opening he had beheld the menacing old man.\n\n“Yes,” he murmured,—“yes, be satisfied.”\n\nHis head dropped upon his chest, and in this position he paced his study; then he threw himself, dressed as he was, upon a sofa, less to sleep than to rest his limbs, cramped with cold and study. By degrees everyone awoke. Villefort, from his study, heard the successive noises which accompany the life of a house,—the opening and shutting of doors, the ringing of Madame de Villefort’s bell, to summon the waiting-maid, mingled with the first shouts of the child, who rose full of the enjoyment of his age. Villefort also rang; his new valet brought him the papers, and with them a cup of chocolate.\n\n“What are you bringing me?” said he.\n\n“A cup of chocolate.”\n\n“I did not ask for it. Who has paid me this attention?”\n\n“My mistress, sir. She said you would have to speak a great deal in the murder case, and that you should take something to keep up your strength;” and the valet placed the cup on the table nearest to the sofa, which was, like all the rest, covered with papers.\n\nThe valet then left the room. Villefort looked for an instant with a gloomy expression, then, suddenly, taking it up with a nervous motion, he swallowed its contents at one draught. It might have been thought that he hoped the beverage would be mortal, and that he sought for death to deliver him from a duty which he would rather die than fulfil. He then rose, and paced his room with a smile it would have been terrible to witness. The chocolate was inoffensive, for M. de Villefort felt no effects.\n\nThe breakfast-hour arrived, but M. de Villefort was not at table. The valet re-entered.\n\n“Madame de Villefort wishes to remind you, sir,” he said, “that eleven o’clock has just struck, and that the trial commences at twelve.”\n\n“Well,” said Villefort, “what then?”\n\n“Madame de Villefort is dressed; she is quite ready, and wishes to know if she is to accompany you, sir?”\n“Where to?”\n\n“To the Palais.”\n\n“What to do?”\n\n“My mistress wishes much to be present at the trial.”\n\n“Ah,” said Villefort, with a startling accent; “does she wish that?”\n\nThe servant drew back and said, “If you wish to go alone, sir, I will go and tell my mistress.”\n\nVillefort remained silent for a moment, and dented his pale cheeks with his nails.\n\n“Tell your mistress,” he at length answered, “that I wish to speak to her, and I beg she will wait for me in her own room.”\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\n“Then come to dress and shave me.”\n“Directly, sir.”\n\nThe valet re-appeared almost instantly, and, having shaved his master, assisted him to dress entirely in black. When he had finished, he said:\n\n“My mistress said she should expect you, sir, as soon as you had finished dressing.”\n\n“I am going to her.”\n\nAnd Villefort, with his papers under his arm and hat in hand, directed his steps toward the apartment of his wife.\n\nAt the door he paused for a moment to wipe his damp, pale brow. He then entered the room. Madame de Villefort was sitting on an ottoman and impatiently turning over the leaves of some newspapers and pamphlets which young Edward, by way of amusing himself, was tearing to pieces before his mother could finish reading them. She was dressed to go out, her bonnet was placed beside her on a chair, and her gloves were on her hands.\n\n“Ah, here you are, monsieur,” she said in her naturally calm voice; “but how pale you are! Have you been working all night? Why did you not come down to breakfast? Well, will you take me, or shall I take Edward?”\n\nMadame de Villefort had multiplied her questions in order to gain one answer, but to all her inquiries M. de Villefort remained mute and cold as a statue.\n\n“Edward,” said Villefort, fixing an imperious glance on the child, “go and play in the drawing-room, my dear; I wish to speak to your mamma.”\n\nMadame de Villefort shuddered at the sight of that cold countenance, that resolute tone, and the awfully strange preliminaries. Edward raised his head, looked at his mother, and then, finding that she did not confirm the order, began cutting off the heads of his leaden soldiers.\n\n“Edward,” cried M. de Villefort, so harshly that the child started up from the floor, “do you hear me?—Go!”\n\nThe child, unaccustomed to such treatment, arose, pale and trembling; it would be difficult to say whether his emotion were caused by fear or passion. His father went up to him, took him in his arms, and kissed his forehead.\n\n“Go,” he said: “go, my child.” Edward ran out.\n\nM. de Villefort went to the door, which he closed behind the child, and bolted.\n\n“Dear me!” said the young woman, endeavoring to read her husband’s inmost thoughts, while a smile passed over her countenance which froze the impassibility of Villefort; “what is the matter?”\n\n“Madame, where do you keep the poison you generally use?” said the magistrate, without any introduction, placing himself between his wife and the door.\n\nMadame de Villefort must have experienced something of the sensation of a bird which, looking up, sees the murderous trap closing over its head.\n\nA hoarse, broken tone, which was neither a cry nor a sigh, escaped from her, while she became deadly pale.\n\n“Monsieur,” she said, “I—I do not understand you.”\n\nAnd, in her first paroxysm of terror, she had raised herself from the sofa, in the next, stronger very likely than the other, she fell down again on the cushions.\n\n“I asked you,” continued Villefort, in a perfectly calm tone, “where you conceal the poison by the aid of which you have killed my father-in-law, M. de Saint-Méran, my mother-in-law, Madame de Saint-Méran, Barrois, and my daughter Valentine.”\n\n“Ah, sir,” exclaimed Madame de Villefort, clasping her hands, “what do you say?”\n\n“It is not for you to interrogate, but to answer.”\n\n“Is it to the judge or to the husband?” stammered Madame de Villefort.\n\n“To the judge—to the judge, madame!” It was terrible to behold the frightful pallor of that woman, the anguish of her look, the trembling of her whole frame.\n\n“Ah, sir,” she muttered, “ah, sir,” and this was all.\n\n“You do not answer, madame!” exclaimed the terrible interrogator. Then he added, with a smile yet more terrible than his anger, “It is true, then; you do not deny it!” She moved forward. “And you cannot deny it!” added Villefort, extending his hand toward her, as though to seize her in the name of justice. “You have accomplished these different crimes with impudent address, but which could only deceive those whose affections for you blinded them. Since the death of Madame de Saint-Méran, I have known that a poisoner lived in my house. M. d’Avrigny warned me of it. After the death of Barrois my suspicions were directed towards an angel,—those suspicions which, even when there is no crime, are always alive in my heart; but after the death of Valentine, there has been no doubt in my mind, madame, and not only in mine, but in those of others; thus your crime, known by two persons, suspected by many, will soon become public, and, as I told you just now, you no longer speak to the husband, but to the judge.”\n50165m\n\nThe young woman hid her face in her hands.\n\n“Oh, sir,” she stammered, “I beseech you, do not believe appearances.”\n\n“Are you, then, a coward?” cried Villefort, in a contemptuous voice. “But I have always observed that poisoners were cowards. Can you be a coward, you, who have had the courage to witness the death of two old men and a young girl murdered by you?”\n\n“Sir! sir!”\n\n“Can you be a coward?” continued Villefort, with increasing excitement, “you, who could count, one by one, the minutes of four death agonies? _You_, who have arranged your infernal plans, and removed the beverages with a talent and precision almost miraculous? Have you, then, who have calculated everything with such nicety, have you forgotten to calculate one thing—I mean where the revelation of your crimes will lead you to? Oh, it is impossible—you must have saved some surer, more subtle and deadly poison than any other, that you might escape the punishment that you deserve. You have done this—I hope so, at least.”\n\nMadame de Villefort stretched out her hands, and fell on her knees.\n\n“I understand,” he said, “you confess; but a confession made to the judges, a confession made at the last moment, extorted when the crime cannot be denied, diminishes not the punishment inflicted on the guilty!”\n\n“The punishment?” exclaimed Madame de Villefort, “the punishment, monsieur? Twice you have pronounced that word!”\n\n“Certainly. Did you hope to escape it because you were four times guilty? Did you think the punishment would be withheld because you are the wife of him who pronounces it?—No, madame, no; the scaffold awaits the poisoner, whoever she may be, unless, as I just said, the poisoner has taken the precaution of keeping for herself a few drops of her deadliest poison.”\n\nMadame de Villefort uttered a wild cry, and a hideous and uncontrollable terror spread over her distorted features.\n\n“Oh, do not fear the scaffold, madame,” said the magistrate; “I will not dishonor you, since that would be dishonor to myself; no, if you have heard me distinctly, you will understand that you are not to die on the scaffold.”\n\n“No, I do not understand; what do you mean?” stammered the unhappy woman, completely overwhelmed.\n\n“I mean that the wife of the first magistrate in the capital shall not, by her infamy, soil an unblemished name; that she shall not, with one blow, dishonor her husband and her child.”\n\n“No, no—oh, no!”\n“Well, madame, it will be a laudable action on your part, and I will thank you for it!”\n\n“You will thank me—for what?”\n\n“For what you have just said.”\n\n“What did I say? Oh, my brain whirls; I no longer understand anything. Oh, my God, my God!”\n\nAnd she rose, with her hair dishevelled, and her lips foaming.\n\n“Have you answered the question I put to you on entering the room?—where do you keep the poison you generally use, madame?”\n\nMadame de Villefort raised her arms to heaven, and convulsively struck one hand against the other.\n\n“No, no,” she vociferated, “no, you cannot wish that!”\n50169m\n\n“What I do not wish, madame, is that you should perish on the scaffold. Do you understand?” asked Villefort.\n\n“Oh, mercy, mercy, monsieur!”\n\n“What I require is, that justice be done. I am on the earth to punish, madame,” he added, with a flaming glance; “any other woman, were it the queen herself, I would send to the executioner; but to you I shall be merciful. To you I will say, ‘Have you not, madame, put aside some of the surest, deadliest, most speedy poison?’”\n\n50170m\n“Oh, pardon me, sir; let me live!”\n\n“She is cowardly,” said Villefort.\n\n“Reflect that I am your wife!”\n\n“You are a poisoner.”\n\n“In the name of Heaven!”\n\n“No!”\n“In the name of the love you once bore me!”\n\n“In the name of our child! Ah, for the sake of our child, let me live!”\n\n50167m\n“No, no, no, I tell you; one day, if I allow you to live, you will perhaps kill him, as you have the others!”\n\n“I?—I kill my boy?” cried the distracted mother, rushing toward Villefort; “I kill my son? Ha, ha, ha!” and a frightful, demoniac laugh finished the sentence, which was lost in a hoarse rattle.\n\nMadame de Villefort fell at her husband’s feet. He approached her.\n\n“Think of it, madame,” he said; “if, on my return, justice has not been satisfied, I will denounce you with my own mouth, and arrest you with my own hands!”\n\nShe listened, panting, overwhelmed, crushed; her eye alone lived, and glared horribly.\n\n“Do you understand me?” he said. “I am going down there to pronounce the sentence of death against a murderer. If I find you alive on my return, you shall sleep tonight in the conciergerie.”\n\nMadame de Villefort sighed; her nerves gave way, and she sunk on the carpet. The king’s attorney seemed to experience a sensation of pity; he looked upon her less severely, and, bowing to her, said slowly:\n\n“Farewell, madame, farewell!”\n\nThat farewell struck Madame de Villefort like the executioner’s knife. She fainted. The procureur went out, after having double-locked the door.\n\n# Chapter 109. The Assizes\n\nThe Benedetto affair, as it was called at the Palais, and by people in general, had produced a tremendous sensation. Frequenting the Café de Paris, the Boulevard de Gand, and the Bois de Boulogne, during his brief career of splendor, the false Cavalcanti had formed a host of acquaintances. The papers had related his various adventures, both as the man of fashion and the galley-slave; and as everyone who had been personally acquainted with Prince Andrea Cavalcanti experienced a lively curiosity in his fate, they all determined to spare no trouble in endeavoring to witness the trial of M. Benedetto for the murder of his comrade in chains.\n\nIn the eyes of many, Benedetto appeared, if not a victim to, at least an instance of, the fallibility of the law. M. Cavalcanti, his father, had been seen in Paris, and it was expected that he would re-appear to claim the illustrious outcast. Many, also, who were not aware of the circumstances attending his withdrawal from Paris, were struck with the worthy appearance, the gentlemanly bearing, and the knowledge of the world displayed by the old patrician, who certainly played the nobleman very well, so long as he said nothing, and made no arithmetical calculations.\n\nAs for the accused himself, many remembered him as being so amiable, so handsome, and so liberal, that they chose to think him the victim of some conspiracy, since in this world large fortunes frequently excite the malevolence and jealousy of some unknown enemy.\n\nEveryone, therefore, ran to the court; some to witness the sight, others to comment upon it. From seven o’clock in the morning a crowd was stationed at the iron gates, and an hour before the trial commenced the hall was full of the privileged. Before the entrance of the magistrates, and indeed frequently afterwards, a court of justice, on days when some especial trial is to take place, resembles a drawing-room where many persons recognize each other and converse if they can do so without losing their seats; or, if they are separated by too great a number of lawyers, communicate by signs.\n\nIt was one of the magnificent autumn days which make amends for a short summer; the clouds which M. de Villefort had perceived at sunrise had all disappeared as if by magic, and one of the softest and most brilliant days of September shone forth in all its splendor.\n\nBeauchamp, one of the kings of the press, and therefore claiming the right of a throne everywhere, was eying everybody through his monocle. He perceived Château-Renaud and Debray, who had just gained the good graces of a sergeant-at-arms, and who had persuaded the latter to let them stand before, instead of behind him, as they ought to have done. The worthy sergeant had recognized the minister’s secretary and the millionnaire, and, by way of paying extra attention to his noble neighbors, promised to keep their places while they paid a visit to Beauchamp.\n\n“Well,” said Beauchamp, “we shall see our friend!”\n\n“Yes, indeed!” replied Debray. “That worthy prince. Deuce take those Italian princes!”\n\n“A man, too, who could boast of Dante for a genealogist, and could reckon back to the _Divina Comedia_.”\n\n“A nobility of the rope!” said Château-Renaud phlegmatically.\n\n“He will be condemned, will he not?” asked Debray of Beauchamp.\n\n“My dear fellow, I think we should ask you that question; you know such news much better than we do. Did you see the president at the minister’s last night?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“What did he say?”\n\n“Something which will surprise you.”\n\n“Oh, make haste and tell me, then; it is a long time since that has happened.”\n\n“Well, he told me that Benedetto, who is considered a serpent of subtlety and a giant of cunning, is really but a very commonplace, silly rascal, and altogether unworthy of the experiments that will be made on his phrenological organs after his death.”\n\n“Bah,” said Beauchamp, “he played the prince very well.”\n\n“Yes, for you who detest those unhappy princes, Beauchamp, and are always delighted to find fault with them; but not for me, who discover a gentleman by instinct, and who scent out an aristocratic family like a very bloodhound of heraldry.”\n\n“Then you never believed in the principality?”\n\n“Yes.—in the principality, but not in the prince.”\n\n“Not so bad,” said Beauchamp; “still, I assure you, he passed very well with many people; I saw him at the ministers’ houses.”\n\n“Ah, yes,” said Château-Renaud. “The idea of thinking ministers understand anything about princes!”\n\n“There is something in what you have just said,” said Beauchamp, laughing.\n\n“But,” said Debray to Beauchamp, “if I spoke to the president, _you_ must have been with the procureur.”\n\n“It was an impossibility; for the last week M. de Villefort has secluded himself. It is natural enough; this strange chain of domestic afflictions, followed by the no less strange death of his daughter——”\n\n“Strange? What do you mean, Beauchamp?”\n\n“Oh, yes; do you pretend that all this has been unobserved at the minister’s?” said Beauchamp, placing his eye-glass in his eye, where he tried to make it remain.\n\n“My dear sir,” said Château-Renaud, “allow me to tell you that you do not understand that manœuvre with the eye-glass half so well as Debray. Give him a lesson, Debray.”\n\n“Stay,” said Beauchamp, “surely I am not deceived.”\n\n“What is it?”\n\n“It is she!”\n\n“Whom do you mean?”\n\n“They said she had left.”\n\n“Mademoiselle Eugénie?” said Château-Renaud; “has she returned?”\n\n“No, but her mother.”\n\n“Madame Danglars? Nonsense! Impossible!” said Château-Renaud; “only ten days after the flight of her daughter, and three days from the bankruptcy of her husband?”\n\nDebray colored slightly, and followed with his eyes the direction of Beauchamp’s glance.\n\n“Come,” he said, “it is only a veiled lady, some foreign princess, perhaps the mother of Cavalcanti. But you were just speaking on a very interesting topic, Beauchamp.”\n“I?”\n\n“Yes; you were telling us about the extraordinary death of Valentine.”\n\n“Ah, yes, so I was. But how is it that Madame de Villefort is not here?”\n\n“Poor, dear woman,” said Debray, “she is no doubt occupied in distilling balm for the hospitals, or in making cosmetics for herself or friends. Do you know she spends two or three thousand crowns a year in this amusement? But I wonder she is not here. I should have been pleased to see her, for I like her very much.”\n\n“And I hate her,” said Château-Renaud.\n\n“Why?”\n\n“I do not know. Why do we love? Why do we hate? I detest her, from antipathy.”\n\n“Or, rather, by instinct.”\n\n“Perhaps so. But to return to what you were saying, Beauchamp.”\n\n“Well, do you know why they die so multitudinously at M. de Villefort’s?”\n\n“‘Multitudinously’ is good,” said Château-Renaud.\n\n“My good fellow, you’ll find the word in Saint-Simon.”\n\n“But the thing itself is at M. de Villefort’s; but let’s get back to the subject.”\n\n“Talking of that,” said Debray, “Madame was making inquiries about that house, which for the last three months has been hung with black.”\n\n“Who is Madame?” asked Château-Renaud.\n\n“The minister’s wife, _pardieu!_”\n\n“Oh, your pardon! I never visit ministers; I leave that to the princes.”\n\n“Really, you were only before sparkling, but now you are brilliant; take compassion on us, or, like Jupiter, you will wither us up.”\n\n“I will not speak again,” said Château-Renaud; “pray have compassion upon me, and do not take up every word I say.”\n\n“Come, let us endeavor to get to the end of our story, Beauchamp; I told you that yesterday Madame made inquiries of me upon the subject; enlighten me, and I will then communicate my information to her.”\n\n“Well, gentlemen, the reason people die so multitudinously (I like the word) at M. de Villefort’s is that there is an assassin in the house!”\n\nThe two young men shuddered, for the same idea had more than once occurred to them.\n\n“And who is the assassin;” they asked together.\n\n“Young Edward!” A burst of laughter from the auditors did not in the least disconcert the speaker, who continued,—“Yes, gentlemen; Edward, the infant phenomenon, who is quite an adept in the art of killing.”\n\n“You are jesting.”\n\n“Not at all. I yesterday engaged a servant, who had just left M. de Villefort—I intend sending him away tomorrow, for he eats so enormously, to make up for the fast imposed upon him by his terror in that house. Well, now listen.”\n\n“We are listening.”\n\n“It appears the dear child has obtained possession of a bottle containing some drug, which he every now and then uses against those who have displeased him. First, M. and Madame de Saint-Méran incurred his displeasure, so he poured out three drops of his elixir—three drops were sufficient; then followed Barrois, the old servant of M. Noirtier, who sometimes rebuffed this little wretch—he therefore received the same quantity of the elixir; the same happened to Valentine, of whom he was jealous; he gave her the same dose as the others, and all was over for her as well as the rest.”\n\n“Why, what nonsense are you telling us?” said Château-Renaud.\n\n“Yes, it is an extraordinary story,” said Beauchamp; “is it not?”\n\n“It is absurd,” said Debray.\n\n“Ah,” said Beauchamp, “you doubt me? Well, you can ask my servant, or rather him who will no longer be my servant tomorrow, it was the talk of the house.”\n\n“And this elixir, where is it? what is it?”\n\n“The child conceals it.”\n\n“But where did he find it?”\n\n“In his mother’s laboratory.”\n\n“Does his mother then, keep poisons in her laboratory?”\n\n“How can I tell? You are questioning me like a king’s attorney. I only repeat what I have been told, and like my informant I can do no more. The poor devil would eat nothing, from fear.”\n\n“It is incredible!”\n\n“No, my dear fellow, it is not at all incredible. You saw the child pass through the Rue Richelieu last year, who amused himself with killing his brothers and sisters by sticking pins in their ears while they slept. The generation who follow us are very precocious.”\n\n“Come, Beauchamp,” said Château-Renaud, “I will bet anything you do not believe a word of all you have been telling us. But I do not see the Count of Monte Cristo here.”\n\n“He is worn out,” said Debray; “besides, he could not well appear in public, since he has been the dupe of the Cavalcanti, who, it appears, presented themselves to him with false letters of credit, and cheated him out of 100,000 francs upon the hypothesis of this principality.”\n\n“By the way, M. de Château-Renaud,” asked Beauchamp, “how is Morrel?”\n\n“_Ma foi_, I have called three times without once seeing him. Still, his sister did not seem uneasy, and told me that though she had not seen him for two or three days, she was sure he was well.”\n\n“Ah, now I think of it, the Count of Monte Cristo cannot appear in the hall,” said Beauchamp.\n\n“Why not?”\n\n“Because he is an actor in the drama.”\n\n“Has he assassinated anyone, then?”\n\n“No, on the contrary, they wished to assassinate him. You know that it was in leaving his house that M. de Caderousse was murdered by his friend Benedetto. You know that the famous waistcoat was found in his house, containing the letter which stopped the signature of the marriage-contract. Do you see the waistcoat? There it is, all blood-stained, on the desk, as a testimony of the crime.”\n\n“Ah, very good.”\n\n“Hush, gentlemen, here is the court; let us go back to our places.”\n\nA noise was heard in the hall; the sergeant called his two patrons with an energetic “hem!” and the door-keeper appearing, called out with that shrill voice peculiar to his order, ever since the days of Beaumarchais:\n\n“The court, gentlemen!”\n\n# Chapter 110. The Indictment\n\nThe judges took their places in the midst of the most profound silence; the jury took their seats; M. de Villefort, the object of unusual attention, and we had almost said of general admiration, sat in the armchair and cast a tranquil glance around him. Everyone looked with astonishment on that grave and severe face, whose calm expression personal griefs had been unable to disturb, and the aspect of a man who was a stranger to all human emotions excited something very like terror.\n\n“Gendarmes,” said the president, “lead in the accused.”\n\nAt these words the public attention became more intense, and all eyes were turned towards the door through which Benedetto was to enter. The door soon opened and the accused appeared.\n\nThe same impression was experienced by all present, and no one was deceived by the expression of his countenance. His features bore no sign of that deep emotion which stops the beating of the heart and blanches the cheek. His hands, gracefully placed, one upon his hat, the other in the opening of his white waistcoat, were not at all tremulous; his eye was calm and even brilliant. Scarcely had he entered the hall when he glanced at the whole body of magistrates and assistants; his eye rested longer on the president, and still more so on the king’s attorney.\n\nBy the side of Andrea was stationed the lawyer who was to conduct his defence, and who had been appointed by the court, for Andrea disdained to pay any attention to those details, to which he appeared to attach no importance. The lawyer was a young man with light hair whose face expressed a hundred times more emotion than that which characterized the prisoner.\n\n50181m\nThe president called for the indictment, revised as we know, by the clever and implacable pen of Villefort. During the reading of this, which was long, the public attention was continually drawn towards Andrea, who bore the inspection with Spartan unconcern. Villefort had never been so concise and eloquent. The crime was depicted in the most vivid colors; the former life of the prisoner, his transformation, a review of his life from the earliest period, were set forth with all the talent that a knowledge of human life could furnish to a mind like that of the procureur. Benedetto was thus forever condemned in public opinion before the sentence of the law could be pronounced.\n\nAndrea paid no attention to the successive charges which were brought against him. M. de Villefort, who examined him attentively, and who no doubt practiced upon him all the psychological studies he was accustomed to use, in vain endeavored to make him lower his eyes, notwithstanding the depth and profundity of his gaze. At length the reading of the indictment was ended.\n\n“Accused,” said the president, “your name and surname?”\n\nAndrea arose.\n\n“Excuse me, Mr. President,” he said, in a clear voice, “but I see you are going to adopt a course of questions through which I cannot follow you. I have an idea, which I will explain by and by, of making an exception to the usual form of accusation. Allow me, then, if you please, to answer in different order, or I will not do so at all.”\n\nThe astonished president looked at the jury, who in turn looked at Villefort. The whole assembly manifested great surprise, but Andrea appeared quite unmoved.\n\n“Your age?” said the president; “will you answer that question?”\n\n“I will answer that question, as well as the rest, Mr. President, but in its turn.”\n\n“Your age?” repeated the president.\n\n“I am twenty-one years old, or rather I shall be in a few days, as I was born the night of the 27th of September, 1817.”\n\nM. de Villefort, who was busy taking down some notes, raised his head at the mention of this date.\n\n“Where were you born?” continued the president.\n\n“At Auteuil, near Paris.”\n\nM. de Villefort a second time raised his head, looked at Benedetto as if he had been gazing at the head of Medusa, and became livid. As for Benedetto, he gracefully wiped his lips with a fine cambric pocket-handkerchief.\n\n“Your profession?”\n\n“First I was a forger,” answered Andrea, as calmly as possible; “then I became a thief, and lately have become an assassin.”\n\nA murmur, or rather storm, of indignation burst from all parts of the assembly. The judges themselves appeared to be stupefied, and the jury manifested tokens of disgust for a cynicism so unexpected in a man of fashion. M. de Villefort pressed his hand upon his brow, which, at first pale, had become red and burning; then he suddenly arose and looked around as though he had lost his senses—he wanted air.\n50183m\n\n“Are you looking for anything, Mr. Procureur?” asked Benedetto, with his most ingratiating smile.\n\nM. de Villefort answered nothing, but sat, or rather threw himself down again upon his chair.\n\n“And now, prisoner, will you consent to tell your name?” said the president. “The brutal affectation with which you have enumerated and classified your crimes calls for a severe reprimand on the part of the court, both in the name of morality, and for the respect due to humanity. You appear to consider this a point of honor, and it may be for this reason, that you have delayed acknowledging your name. You wished it to be preceded by all these titles.”\n\n“It is quite wonderful, Mr. President, how entirely you have read my thoughts,” said Benedetto, in his softest voice and most polite manner. “This is, indeed, the reason why I begged you to alter the order of the questions.”\n\nThe public astonishment had reached its height. There was no longer any deceit or bravado in the manner of the accused. The audience felt that a startling revelation was to follow this ominous prelude.\n\n“Well,” said the president; “your name?”\n\n“I cannot tell you my name, since I do not know it; but I know my father’s, and can tell it to you.”\n\nA painful giddiness overwhelmed Villefort; great drops of acrid sweat fell from his face upon the papers which he held in his convulsed hand.\n\n“Repeat your father’s name,” said the president.\n\nNot a whisper, not a breath, was heard in that vast assembly; everyone waited anxiously.\n\n“My father is king’s attorney,” replied Andrea calmly.\n\n50179m\n“King’s attorney?” said the president, stupefied, and without noticing the agitation which spread over the face of M. de Villefort; “king’s attorney?”\n\n“Yes; and if you wish to know his name, I will tell it,—he is named Villefort.”\n\nThe explosion, which had been so long restrained from a feeling of respect to the court of justice, now burst forth like thunder from the breasts of all present; the court itself did not seek to restrain the feelings of the audience. The exclamations, the insults addressed to Benedetto, who remained perfectly unconcerned, the energetic gestures, the movement of the gendarmes, the sneers of the scum of the crowd always sure to rise to the surface in case of any disturbance—all this lasted five minutes, before the door-keepers and magistrates were able to restore silence. In the midst of this tumult the voice of the president was heard to exclaim:\n\n“Are you playing with justice, accused, and do you dare set your fellow-citizens an example of disorder which even in these times has never been equalled?”\n\nSeveral persons hurried up to M. de Villefort, who sat half bowed over in his chair, offering him consolation, encouragement, and protestations of zeal and sympathy. Order was re-established in the hall, except that a few people still moved about and whispered to one another. A lady, it was said, had just fainted; they had supplied her with a smelling-bottle, and she had recovered. During the scene of tumult, Andrea had turned his smiling face towards the assembly; then, leaning with one hand on the oaken rail of the dock, in the most graceful attitude possible, he said:\n\n“Gentlemen, I assure you I had no idea of insulting the court, or of making a useless disturbance in the presence of this honorable assembly. They ask my age; I tell it. They ask where I was born; I answer. They ask my name, I cannot give it, since my parents abandoned me. But though I cannot give my own name, not possessing one, I can tell them my father’s. Now I repeat, my father is named M. de Villefort, and I am ready to prove it.”\n\nThere was an energy, a conviction, and a sincerity in the manner of the young man, which silenced the tumult. All eyes were turned for a moment towards the procureur, who sat as motionless as though a thunderbolt had changed him into a corpse.\n\n“Gentlemen,” said Andrea, commanding silence by his voice and manner; “I owe you the proofs and explanations of what I have said.”\n\n“But,” said the irritated president, “you called yourself Benedetto, declared yourself an orphan, and claimed Corsica as your country.”\n\n“I said anything I pleased, in order that the solemn declaration I have just made should not be withheld, which otherwise would certainly have been the case. I now repeat that I was born at Auteuil on the night of the 27th of September, 1817, and that I am the son of the procureur, M. de Villefort. Do you wish for any further details? I will give them. I was born in No. 28, Rue de la Fontaine, in a room hung with red damask; my father took me in his arms, telling my mother I was dead, wrapped me in a napkin marked with an H and an N, and carried me into a garden, where he buried me alive.”\n\nA shudder ran through the assembly when they saw that the confidence of the prisoner increased in proportion to the terror of M. de Villefort.\n\n“But how have you become acquainted with all these details?” asked the president.\n\n“I will tell you, Mr. President. A man who had sworn vengeance against my father, and had long watched his opportunity to kill him, had introduced himself that night into the garden in which my father buried me. He was concealed in a thicket; he saw my father bury something in the ground, and stabbed him; then thinking the deposit might contain some treasure he turned up the ground, and found me still living. The man carried me to the foundling asylum, where I was registered under the number 37. Three months afterwards, a woman travelled from Rogliano to Paris to fetch me, and having claimed me as her son, carried me away. Thus, you see, though born in Paris, I was brought up in Corsica.”\n\nThere was a moment’s silence, during which one could have fancied the hall empty, so profound was the stillness.\n\n“Proceed,” said the president.\n\n“Certainly, I might have lived happily amongst those good people, who adored me, but my perverse disposition prevailed over the virtues which my adopted mother endeavored to instil into my heart. I increased in wickedness till I committed crime. One day when I cursed Providence for making me so wicked, and ordaining me to such a fate, my adopted father said to me, ‘Do not blaspheme, unhappy child, the crime is that of your father, not yours,—of your father, who consigned you to hell if you died, and to misery if a miracle preserved you alive.’ After that I ceased to blaspheme, but I cursed my father. That is why I have uttered the words for which you blame me; that is why I have filled this whole assembly with horror. If I have committed an additional crime, punish me, but if you will allow that ever since the day of my birth my fate has been sad, bitter, and lamentable, then pity me.”\n\n“But your mother?” asked the president.\n\n“My mother thought me dead; she is not guilty. I did not even wish to know her name, nor do I know it.”\n\nJust then a piercing cry, ending in a sob, burst from the centre of the crowd, who encircled the lady who had before fainted, and who now fell into a violent fit of hysterics. She was carried out of the hall, the thick veil which concealed her face dropped off, and Madame Danglars was recognized. Notwithstanding his shattered nerves, the ringing sensation in his ears, and the madness which turned his brain, Villefort rose as he perceived her.\n\n“The proofs, the proofs!” said the president; “remember this tissue of horrors must be supported by the clearest proofs.”\n\n“The proofs?” said Benedetto, laughing; “do you want proofs?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Well, then, look at M. de Villefort, and then ask me for proofs.”\n\nEveryone turned towards the procureur, who, unable to bear the universal gaze now riveted on him alone, advanced staggering into the midst of the tribunal, with his hair dishevelled and his face indented with the mark of his nails. The whole assembly uttered a long murmur of astonishment.\n\n“Father,” said Benedetto, “I am asked for proofs, do you wish me to give them?”\n\n“No, no, it is useless,” stammered M. de Villefort in a hoarse voice; “no, it is useless!”\n\n“How useless?” cried the president, “what do you mean?”\n\n“I mean that I feel it impossible to struggle against this deadly weight which crushes me. Gentlemen, I know I am in the hands of an avenging God! We need no proofs; everything relating to this young man is true.”\n\nA dull, gloomy silence, like that which precedes some awful phenomenon of nature, pervaded the assembly, who shuddered in dismay.\n\n“What, M. de Villefort,” cried the president, “do you yield to an hallucination? What, are you no longer in possession of your senses? This strange, unexpected, terrible accusation has disordered your reason. Come, recover.”\n\nThe procureur dropped his head; his teeth chattered like those of a man under a violent attack of fever, and yet he was deadly pale.\n\n“I am in possession of all my senses, sir,” he said; “my body alone suffers, as you may suppose. I acknowledge myself guilty of all the young man has brought against me, and from this hour hold myself under the authority of the procureur who will succeed me.”\n\nAnd as he spoke these words with a hoarse, choking voice, he staggered towards the door, which was mechanically opened by a door-keeper. The whole assembly were dumb with astonishment at the revelation and confession which had produced a catastrophe so different from that which had been expected during the last fortnight by the Parisian world.\n\n“Well,” said Beauchamp, “let them now say that drama is unnatural!”\n\n“_Ma foi!_” said Château-Renaud, “I would rather end my career like M. de Morcerf; a pistol-shot seems quite delightful compared with this catastrophe.”\n\n“And moreover, it kills,” said Beauchamp.\n\n“And to think that I had an idea of marrying his daughter,” said Debray. “She did well to die, poor girl!”\n\n“The sitting is adjourned, gentlemen,” said the president; “fresh inquiries will be made, and the case will be tried next session by another magistrate.”\n\nAs for Andrea, who was calm and more interesting than ever, he left the hall, escorted by gendarmes, who involuntarily paid him some attention.\n\n“Well, what do you think of this, my fine fellow?” asked Debray of the sergeant-at-arms, slipping a louis into his hand.\n\n“There will be extenuating circumstances,” he replied.\n\n# Chapter 111. Expiation\n\nNotwithstanding the density of the crowd, M. de Villefort saw it open before him. There is something so awe-inspiring in great afflictions that even in the worst times the first emotion of a crowd has generally been to sympathize with the sufferer in a great catastrophe. Many people have been assassinated in a tumult, but even criminals have rarely been insulted during trial. Thus Villefort passed through the mass of spectators and officers of the Palais, and withdrew. Though he had acknowledged his guilt, he was protected by his grief. There are some situations which men understand by instinct, but which reason is powerless to explain; in such cases the greatest poet is he who gives utterance to the most natural and vehement outburst of sorrow. Those who hear the bitter cry are as much impressed as if they listened to an entire poem, and when the sufferer is sincere they are right in regarding his outburst as sublime.\n\nIt would be difficult to describe the state of stupor in which Villefort left the Palais. Every pulse beat with feverish excitement, every nerve was strained, every vein swollen, and every part of his body seemed to suffer distinctly from the rest, thus multiplying his agony a thousand-fold. He made his way along the corridors through force of habit; he threw aside his magisterial robe, not out of deference to etiquette, but because it was an unbearable burden, a veritable garb of Nessus, insatiate in torture. Having staggered as far as the Rue Dauphine, he perceived his carriage, awoke his sleeping coachman by opening the door himself, threw himself on the cushions, and pointed towards the Faubourg Saint-Honoré; the carriage drove on.\n\nAll the weight of his fallen fortune seemed suddenly to crush him; he could not foresee the consequences; he could not contemplate the future with the indifference of the hardened criminal who merely faces a contingency already familiar.\n\nGod was still in his heart. “God,” he murmured, not knowing what he said,—“God—God!” Behind the event that had overwhelmed him he saw the hand of God. The carriage rolled rapidly onward. Villefort, while turning restlessly on the cushions, felt something press against him. He put out his hand to remove the object; it was a fan which Madame de Villefort had left in the carriage; this fan awakened a recollection which darted through his mind like lightning. He thought of his wife.\n50189m\n\n“Oh!” he exclaimed, as though a red-hot iron were piercing his heart.\n\nDuring the last hour his own crime had alone been presented to his mind; now another object, not less terrible, suddenly presented itself. His wife! He had just acted the inexorable judge with her, he had condemned her to death, and she, crushed by remorse, struck with terror, covered with the shame inspired by the eloquence of _his_ irreproachable virtue,—she, a poor, weak woman, without help or the power of defending herself against his absolute and supreme will,—she might at that very moment, perhaps, be preparing to die!\n\nAn hour had elapsed since her condemnation; at that moment, doubtless, she was recalling all her crimes to her memory; she was asking pardon for her sins; perhaps she was even writing a letter imploring forgiveness from her virtuous husband—a forgiveness she was purchasing with her death! Villefort again groaned with anguish and despair.\n\n“Ah,” he exclaimed, “that woman became criminal only from associating with me! I carried the infection of crime with me, and she has caught it as she would the typhus fever, the cholera, the plague! And yet I have punished her—I have dared to tell her—_I_ have—‘Repent and die!’ But no, she must not die; she shall live, and with me. We will flee from Paris and go as far as the earth reaches. I told her of the scaffold; oh, Heavens, I forgot that it awaits me also! How could I pronounce that word? Yes, we will fly; I will confess all to her,—I will tell her daily that I also have committed a crime!—Oh, what an alliance—the tiger and the serpent; worthy wife of such as I am! She _must_ live that my infamy may diminish hers.”\n\nAnd Villefort dashed open the window in front of the carriage.\n\n“Faster, faster!” he cried, in a tone which electrified the coachman. The horses, impelled by fear, flew towards the house.\n\n“Yes, yes,” repeated Villefort, as he approached his home—“yes, that woman must live; she must repent, and educate my son, the sole survivor, with the exception of the indestructible old man, of the wreck of my house. She loves him; it was for his sake she has committed these crimes. We ought never to despair of softening the heart of a mother who loves her child. She will repent, and no one will know that she has been guilty. The events which have taken place in my house, though they now occupy the public mind, will be forgotten in time, or if, indeed, a few enemies should persist in remembering them, why then I will add them to my list of crimes. What will it signify if one, two, or three more are added? My wife and child shall escape from this gulf, carrying treasures with them; she will live and may yet be happy, since her child, in whom all her love is centred, will be with her. I shall have performed a good action, and my heart will be lighter.”\n\nAnd the procureur breathed more freely than he had done for some time.\n\n50191m\nThe carriage stopped at the door of the house. Villefort leaped out of the carriage, and saw that his servants were surprised at his early return; he could read no other expression on their features. Neither of them spoke to him; they merely stood aside to let him pass by, as usual, nothing more. As he passed by M. Noirtier’s room, he perceived two figures through the half-open door; but he experienced no curiosity to know who was visiting his father; anxiety carried him on further.\n\n“Come,” he said, as he ascended the stairs leading to his wife’s room, “nothing is changed here.”\n\nHe then closed the door of the landing.\n\n“No one must disturb us,” he said; “I must speak freely to her, accuse myself, and say”—he approached the door, touched the crystal handle, which yielded to his hand. “Not locked,” he cried; “that is well.”\n\nAnd he entered the little room in which Edward slept; for though the child went to school during the day, his mother could not allow him to be separated from her at night. With a single glance Villefort’s eye ran through the room.\n\n“Not here,” he said; “doubtless she is in her bedroom.” He rushed towards the door, found it bolted, and stopped, shuddering.\n\n“Héloïse!” he cried. He fancied he heard the sound of a piece of furniture being removed.\n\n“Héloïse!” he repeated.\n\n“Who is there?” answered the voice of her he sought. He thought that voice more feeble than usual.\n\n“Open the door!” cried Villefort. “Open; it is I.”\n\nBut notwithstanding this request, notwithstanding the tone of anguish in which it was uttered, the door remained closed. Villefort burst it open with a violent blow. At the entrance of the room which led to her boudoir, Madame de Villefort was standing erect, pale, her features contracted, and her eyes glaring horribly.\n\n“Héloïse, Héloïse!” he said, “what is the matter? Speak!” The young woman extended her stiff white hands towards him.\n\n“It is done, monsieur,” she said with a rattling noise which seemed to tear her throat. “What more do you want?” and she fell full length on the floor.\n\nVillefort ran to her and seized her hand, which convulsively clasped a crystal bottle with a golden stopper. Madame de Villefort was dead. Villefort, maddened with horror, stepped back to the threshhold of the door, fixing his eyes on the corpse.\n\n“My son!” he exclaimed suddenly, “where is my son?—Edward, Edward!” and he rushed out of the room, still crying, “Edward, Edward!” The name was pronounced in such a tone of anguish that the servants ran up.\n\n“Where is my son?” asked Villefort; “let him be removed from the house, that he may not see——”\n\n“Master Edward is not downstairs, sir,” replied the valet.\n\n“Then he must be playing in the garden; go and see.”\n\n50193m\n“No, sir; Madame de Villefort sent for him half an hour ago; he went into her room, and has not been downstairs since.”\n\nA cold perspiration burst out on Villefort’s brow; his legs trembled, and his thoughts flew about madly in his brain like the wheels of a disordered watch.\n\n“In Madame de Villefort’s room?” he murmured and slowly returned, with one hand wiping his forehead, and with the other supporting himself against the wall. To enter the room he must again see the body of his unfortunate wife. To call Edward he must reawaken the echo of that room which now appeared like a sepulchre; to speak seemed like violating the silence of the tomb. His tongue was paralyzed in his mouth.\n\n“Edward!” he stammered—“Edward!”\n\nThe child did not answer. Where, then, could he be, if he had entered his mother’s room and not since returned? He stepped forward. The corpse of Madame de Villefort was stretched across the doorway leading to the room in which Edward must be; those glaring eyes seemed to watch over the threshold, and the lips bore the stamp of a terrible and mysterious irony. Through the open door was visible a portion of the boudoir, containing an upright piano and a blue satin couch. Villefort stepped forward two or three paces, and beheld his child lying—no doubt asleep—on the sofa. The unhappy man uttered an exclamation of joy; a ray of light seemed to penetrate the abyss of despair and darkness. He had only to step over the corpse, enter the boudoir, take the child in his arms, and flee far, far away.\n\nVillefort was no longer the civilized man; he was a tiger hurt unto death, gnashing his teeth in his wound. He no longer feared realities, but phantoms. He leaped over the corpse as if it had been a burning brazier. He took the child in his arms, embraced him, shook him, called him, but the child made no response. He pressed his burning lips to the cheeks, but they were icy cold and pale; he felt the stiffened limbs; he pressed his hand upon the heart, but it no longer beat,—the child was dead.\n\nA folded paper fell from Edward’s breast. Villefort, thunderstruck, fell upon his knees; the child dropped from his arms, and rolled on the floor by the side of its mother. He picked up the paper, and, recognizing his wife’s writing, ran his eyes rapidly over its contents; it ran as follows:\n\n“You know that I was a good mother, since it was for my son’s sake I became criminal. A good mother cannot depart without her son.”\n\nVillefort could not believe his eyes,—he could not believe his reason; he dragged himself towards the child’s body, and examined it as a lioness contemplates its dead cub. Then a piercing cry escaped from his breast, and he cried,\n\n“Still the hand of God.”\n\nThe presence of the two victims alarmed him; he could not bear solitude shared only by two corpses. Until then he had been sustained by rage, by his strength of mind, by despair, by the supreme agony which led the Titans to scale the heavens, and Ajax to defy the gods. He now arose, his head bowed beneath the weight of grief, and, shaking his damp, dishevelled hair, he who had never felt compassion for anyone determined to seek his father, that he might have someone to whom he could relate his misfortunes,—someone by whose side he might weep.\n50195m\n\nHe descended the little staircase with which we are acquainted, and entered Noirtier’s room. The old man appeared to be listening attentively and as affectionately as his infirmities would allow to the Abbé Busoni, who looked cold and calm, as usual. Villefort, perceiving the abbé, passed his hand across his brow. The past came to him like one of those waves whose wrath foams fiercer than the others.\n\nHe recollected the call he had made upon him after the dinner at Auteuil, and then the visit the abbé had himself paid to his house on the day of Valentine’s death.\n\n“You here, sir!” he exclaimed; “do you, then, never appear but to act as an escort to death?”\n\nBusoni turned around, and, perceiving the excitement depicted on the magistrate’s face, the savage lustre of his eyes, he understood that the revelation had been made at the assizes; but beyond this he was ignorant.\n\n“I came to pray over the body of your daughter.”\n\n“And now why are you here?”\n\n“I come to tell you that you have sufficiently repaid your debt, and that from this moment I will pray to God to forgive you, as I do.”\n\n“Good heavens!” exclaimed Villefort, stepping back fearfully, “surely that is not the voice of the Abbé Busoni!”\n\n“No!” The abbé threw off his wig, shook his head, and his hair, no longer confined, fell in black masses around his manly face.\n\n“It is the face of the Count of Monte Cristo!” exclaimed the procureur, with a haggard expression.\n\n“You are not exactly right, M. Procureur; you must go farther back.”\n\n“That voice, that voice!—where did I first hear it?”\n\n“You heard it for the first time at Marseilles, twenty-three years ago, the day of your marriage with Mademoiselle de Saint-Méran. Refer to your papers.”\n\n“You are not Busoni?—you are not Monte Cristo? Oh, heavens! you are, then, some secret, implacable, and mortal enemy! I must have wronged you in some way at Marseilles. Oh, woe to me!”\n\n“Yes; you are now on the right path,” said the count, crossing his arms over his broad chest; “search—search!”\n\n“But what have I done to you?” exclaimed Villefort, whose mind was balancing between reason and insanity, in that cloud which is neither a dream nor reality; “what have I done to you? Tell me, then! Speak!”\n\n“You condemned me to a horrible, tedious death; you killed my father; you deprived me of liberty, of love, and happiness.”\n\n“Who are you, then? Who are you?”\n\n“I am the spectre of a wretch you buried in the dungeons of the Château d’If. God gave that spectre the form of the Count of Monte Cristo when he at length issued from his tomb, enriched him with gold and diamonds, and led him to you!”\n\n“Ah, I recognize you—I recognize you!” exclaimed the king’s attorney; “you are——”\n\n“I am Edmond Dantès!”\n\n“You are Edmond Dantès,” cried Villefort, seizing the count by the wrist; “then come here!”\n\nAnd up the stairs he dragged Monte Cristo; who, ignorant of what had happened, followed him in astonishment, foreseeing some new catastrophe.\n\n“There, Edmond Dantès!” he said, pointing to the bodies of his wife and child, “see, are you well avenged?”\n\nMonte Cristo became pale at this horrible sight; he felt that he had passed beyond the bounds of vengeance, and that he could no longer say, “God is for and with me.” With an expression of indescribable anguish he threw himself upon the body of the child, reopened its eyes, felt its pulse, and then rushed with him into Valentine’s room, of which he double-locked the door.\n\n“My child,” cried Villefort, “he carries away the body of my child! Oh, curses, woe, death to you!”\n\nHe tried to follow Monte Cristo; but as though in a dream he was transfixed to the spot,—his eyes glared as though they were starting through the sockets; he griped the flesh on his chest until his nails were stained with blood; the veins of his temples swelled and boiled as though they would burst their narrow boundary, and deluge his brain with living fire. This lasted several minutes, until the frightful overturn of reason was accomplished; then uttering a loud cry followed by a burst of laughter, he rushed down the stairs.\n\nA quarter of an hour afterwards the door of Valentine’s room opened, and Monte Cristo reappeared. Pale, with a dull eye and heavy heart, all the noble features of that face, usually so calm and serene, were overcast by grief. In his arms he held the child, whom no skill had been able to recall to life. Bending on one knee, he placed it reverently by the side of its mother, with its head upon her breast. Then, rising, he went out, and meeting a servant on the stairs, he asked:\n\n“Where is M. de Villefort?”\n\nThe servant, instead of answering, pointed to the garden. Monte Cristo ran down the steps, and advancing towards the spot designated beheld Villefort, encircled by his servants, with a spade in his hand, and digging the earth with fury.\n\n“It is not here!” he cried. “It is not here!”\n50197m\n\nAnd then he moved farther on, and began again to dig.\n\nMonte Cristo approached him, and said in a low voice, with an expression almost humble:\n\n“Sir, you have indeed lost a son; but——”\n\nVillefort interrupted him; he had neither listened nor heard.\n\n“Oh, I _will_ find it,” he cried; “you may pretend he is not here, but I _will_ find him, though I dig forever!”\n\nMonte Cristo drew back in horror.\n\n“Oh,” he said, “he is mad!” And as though he feared that the walls of the accursed house would crumble around him, he rushed into the street, for the first time doubting whether he had the right to do as he had done. “Oh, enough of this,—enough of this,” he cried; “let me save the last.” On entering his house, he met Morrel, who wandered about like a ghost awaiting the heavenly mandate for return to the tomb.\n\n“Prepare yourself, Maximilian,” he said with a smile; “we leave Paris tomorrow.”\n\n“Have you nothing more to do there?” asked Morrel.\n\n“No,” replied Monte Cristo; “God grant I may not have done too much already.”\n\nThe next day they indeed left, accompanied only by Baptistin. Haydée had taken away Ali, and Bertuccio remained with Noirtier.\n\n# Chapter 112. The Departure\n\nThe recent events formed the theme of conversation throughout all Paris. Emmanuel and his wife conversed with natural astonishment in their little apartment in the Rue Meslay upon the three successive, sudden, and most unexpected catastrophes of Morcerf, Danglars, and Villefort. Maximilian, who was paying them a visit, listened to their conversation, or rather was present at it, plunged in his accustomed state of apathy.\n\n“Indeed,” said Julie, “might we not almost fancy, Emmanuel, that those people, so rich, so happy but yesterday, had forgotten in their prosperity that an evil genius—like the wicked fairies in Perrault’s stories who present themselves unbidden at a wedding or baptism—hovered over them, and appeared all at once to revenge himself for their fatal neglect?”\n\n“What a dire misfortune!” said Emmanuel, thinking of Morcerf and Danglars.\n\n“What dreadful sufferings!” said Julie, remembering Valentine, but whom, with a delicacy natural to women, she did not name before her brother.\n\n“If the Supreme Being has directed the fatal blow,” said Emmanuel, “it must be that he in his great goodness has perceived nothing in the past lives of these people to merit mitigation of their awful punishment.”\n\n“Do you not form a very rash judgment, Emmanuel?” said Julie. “When my father, with a pistol in his hand, was once on the point of committing suicide, had anyone then said, ‘This man deserves his misery,’ would not that person have been deceived?”\n\n“Yes; but your father was not allowed to fall. A being was commissioned to arrest the fatal hand of death about to descend on him.”\n\nEmmanuel had scarcely uttered these words when the sound of the bell was heard, the well-known signal given by the porter that a visitor had arrived. Nearly at the same instant the door was opened and the Count of Monte Cristo appeared on the threshold. The young people uttered a cry of joy, while Maximilian raised his head, but let it fall again immediately.\n50201m\n\n“Maximilian,” said the count, without appearing to notice the different impressions which his presence produced on the little circle, “I come to seek you.”\n\n“To seek me?” repeated Morrel, as if awakening from a dream.\n\n“Yes,” said Monte Cristo; “has it not been agreed that I should take you with me, and did I not tell you yesterday to prepare for departure?”\n\n“I am ready,” said Maximilian; “I came expressly to wish them farewell.”\n\n“Whither are you going, count?” asked Julie.\n\n“In the first instance to Marseilles, madame.”\n\n“To Marseilles!” exclaimed the young couple.\n\n“Yes, and I take your brother with me.”\n\n“Oh, count.” said Julie, “will you restore him to us cured of his melancholy?” Morrel turned away to conceal the confusion of his countenance.\n\n“You perceive, then, that he is not happy?” said the count.\n\n“Yes,” replied the young woman; “and fear much that he finds our home but a dull one.”\n\n“I will undertake to divert him,” replied the count.\n\n“I am ready to accompany you, sir,” said Maximilian. “Adieu, my kind friends! Emmanuel—Julie—farewell!”\n\n“How farewell?” exclaimed Julie; “do you leave us thus, so suddenly, without any preparations for your journey, without even a passport?”\n\n“Needless delays but increase the grief of parting,” said Monte Cristo, “and Maximilian has doubtless provided himself with everything requisite; at least, I advised him to do so.”\n\n“I have a passport, and my clothes are ready packed,” said Morrel in his tranquil but mournful manner.\n\n“Good,” said Monte Cristo, smiling; “in these prompt arrangements we recognize the order of a well-disciplined soldier.”\n\n“And you leave us,” said Julie, “at a moment’s warning? you do not give us a day—no, not even an hour before your departure?”\n\n“My carriage is at the door, madame, and I must be in Rome in five days.”\n\n“But does Maximilian go to Rome?” exclaimed Emmanuel.\n\n“I am going wherever it may please the count to take me,” said Morrel, with a smile full of grief; “I am under his orders for the next month.”\n\n“Oh, heavens, how strangely he expresses himself, count!” said Julie.\n\n“Maximilian goes with _me_,” said the count, in his kindest and most persuasive manner; “therefore do not make yourself uneasy on your brother’s account.”\n\n“Once more farewell, my dear sister; Emmanuel, adieu!” Morrel repeated.\n\n“His carelessness and indifference touch me to the heart,” said Julie. “Oh, Maximilian, Maximilian, you are certainly concealing something from us.”\n\n“Pshaw!” said Monte Cristo, “you will see him return to you gay, smiling, and joyful.”\n\nMaximilian cast a look of disdain, almost of anger, on the count.\n\n“We must leave you,” said Monte Cristo.\n50203m\n\n“Before you quit us, count,” said Julie, “will you permit us to express to you all that the other day——”\n\n“Madame,” interrupted the count, taking her two hands in his, “all that you could say in words would never express what I read in your eyes; the thoughts of your heart are fully understood by mine. Like benefactors in romances, I should have left you without seeing you again, but that would have been a virtue beyond my strength, because I am a weak and vain man, fond of the tender, kind, and thankful glances of my fellow-creatures. On the eve of departure I carry my egotism so far as to say, ‘Do not forget me, my kind friends, for probably you will never see me again.’”\n\n“Never see you again?” exclaimed Emmanuel, while two large tears rolled down Julie’s cheeks, “never behold you again? It is not a man, then, but some angel that leaves us, and this angel is on the point of returning to heaven after having appeared on earth to do good.”\n\n“Say not so,” quickly returned Monte Cristo—“say not so, my friends; angels never err, celestial beings remain where they wish to be. Fate is not more powerful than they; it is they who, on the contrary, overcome fate. No, Emmanuel, I am but a man, and your admiration is as unmerited as your words are sacrilegious.”\n\nAnd pressing his lips on the hand of Julie, who rushed into his arms, he extended his other hand to Emmanuel; then tearing himself from this abode of peace and happiness, he made a sign to Maximilian, who followed him passively, with the indifference which had been perceptible in him ever since the death of Valentine had so stunned him.\n\n“Restore my brother to peace and happiness,” whispered Julie to Monte Cristo. And the count pressed her hand in reply, as he had done eleven years before on the staircase leading to Morrel’s study.\n\n“You still confide, then, in Sinbad the Sailor?” asked he, smiling.\n\n“Oh, yes,” was the ready answer.\n\n“Well, then, sleep in peace, and put your trust in the Lord.”\n\nAs we have before said, the post-chaise was waiting; four powerful horses were already pawing the ground with impatience, while Ali, apparently just arrived from a long walk, was standing at the foot of the steps, his face bathed in perspiration.\n\n“Well,” asked the count in Arabic, “have you been to see the old man?” Ali made a sign in the affirmative.\n\n“And have you placed the letter before him, as I ordered you to do?”\n\nThe slave respectfully signalized that he had.\n\n“And what did he say, or rather do?” Ali placed himself in the light, so that his master might see him distinctly, and then imitating in his intelligent manner the countenance of the old man, he closed his eyes, as Noirtier was in the custom of doing when saying “Yes.”\n\n“Good; he accepts,” said Monte Cristo. “Now let us go.”\n50205m\n\nThese words had scarcely escaped him, when the carriage was on its way, and the feet of the horses struck a shower of sparks from the pavement. Maximilian settled himself in his corner without uttering a word. Half an hour had passed when the carriage stopped suddenly; the count had just pulled the silken check-string, which was fastened to Ali’s finger. The Nubian immediately descended and opened the carriage door. It was a lovely starlight night—they had just reached the top of the hill Villejuif, from whence Paris appears like a sombre sea tossing its millions of phosphoric waves into light—waves indeed more noisy, more passionate, more changeable, more furious, more greedy, than those of the tempestuous ocean,—waves which never rest as those of the sea sometimes do,—waves ever dashing, ever foaming, ever ingulfing what falls within their grasp.\n\nThe count stood alone, and at a sign from his hand, the carriage went on for a short distance. With folded arms, he gazed for some time upon the great city. When he had fixed his piercing look on this modern Babylon, which equally engages the contemplation of the religious enthusiast, the materialist, and the scoffer,—\n\n“Great city,” murmured he, inclining his head, and joining his hands as if in prayer, “less than six months have elapsed since first I entered thy gates. I believe that the Spirit of God led my steps to thee and that he also enables me to quit thee in triumph; the secret cause of my presence within thy walls I have confided alone to him who only has had the power to read my heart. God only knows that I retire from thee without pride or hatred, but not without many regrets; he only knows that the power confided to me has never been made subservient to my personal good or to any useless cause. Oh, great city, it is in thy palpitating bosom that I have found that which I sought; like a patient miner, I have dug deep into thy very entrails to root out evil thence. Now my work is accomplished, my mission is terminated, now thou canst neither afford me pain nor pleasure. Adieu, Paris, adieu!”\n\nHis look wandered over the vast plain like that of some genius of the night; he passed his hand over his brow, got into the carriage, the door was closed on him, and the vehicle quickly disappeared down the other side of the hill in a whirlwind of dust and noise.\n\nTen leagues were passed and not a single word was uttered. Morrel was dreaming, and Monte Cristo was looking at the dreamer.\n\n“Morrel,” said the count to him at length, “do you repent having followed me?”\n\n“No, count; but to leave Paris——”\n\n“If I thought happiness might await you in Paris, Morrel, I would have left you there.”\n\n“Valentine reposes within the walls of Paris, and to leave Paris is like losing her a second time.”\n\n“Maximilian,” said the count, “the friends that we have lost do not repose in the bosom of the earth, but are buried deep in our hearts, and it has been thus ordained that we may always be accompanied by them. I have two friends, who in this way never depart from me; the one who gave me being, and the other who conferred knowledge and intelligence on me. Their spirits live in me. I consult them when doubtful, and if I ever do any good, it is due to their beneficent counsels. Listen to the voice of your heart, Morrel, and ask it whether you ought to preserve this melancholy exterior towards me.”\n\n“My friend,” said Maximilian, “the voice of my heart is very sorrowful, and promises me nothing but misfortune.”\n\n“It is the way of weakened minds to see everything through a black cloud. The soul forms its own horizons; your soul is darkened, and consequently the sky of the future appears stormy and unpromising.”\n\n“That may possibly be true,” said Maximilian, and he again subsided into his thoughtful mood.\n\nThe journey was performed with that marvellous rapidity which the unlimited power of the count ever commanded. Towns fled from them like shadows on their path, and trees shaken by the first winds of autumn seemed like giants madly rushing on to meet them, and retreating as rapidly when once reached. The following morning they arrived at Châlons, where the count’s steamboat waited for them. Without the loss of an instant, the carriage was placed on board and the two travellers embarked without delay. The boat was built for speed; her two paddle-wheels were like two wings with which she skimmed the water like a bird.\n\nMorrel was not insensible to that sensation of delight which is generally experienced in passing rapidly through the air, and the wind which occasionally raised the hair from his forehead seemed on the point of dispelling momentarily the clouds collected there.\n\nAs the distance increased between the travellers and Paris, almost superhuman serenity appeared to surround the count; he might have been taken for an exile about to revisit his native land.\n\nEre long Marseilles presented herself to view,—Marseilles, white, fervid, full of life and energy,—Marseilles, the younger sister of Tyre and Carthage, the successor to them in the empire of the Mediterranean,—Marseilles, old, yet always young. Powerful memories were stirred within them by the sight of the round tower, Fort Saint-Nicolas, the City Hall designed by Puget,28 the port with its brick quays, where they had both played in childhood, and it was with one accord that they stopped on the Canebière.\n\nA vessel was setting sail for Algiers, on board of which the bustle usually attending departure prevailed. The passengers and their relations crowded on the deck, friends taking a tender but sorrowful leave of each other, some weeping, others noisy in their grief, the whole forming a spectacle that might be exciting even to those who witnessed similar sights daily, but which had no power to disturb the current of thought that had taken possession of the mind of Maximilian from the moment he had set foot on the broad pavement of the quay.\n\n“Here,” said he, leaning heavily on the arm of Monte Cristo,—“here is the spot where my father stopped, when the _Pharaon_ entered the port; it was here that the good old man, whom you saved from death and dishonor, threw himself into my arms. I yet feel his warm tears on my face, and his were not the only tears shed, for many who witnessed our meeting wept also.”\n\nMonte Cristo gently smiled and said,—“I was there;” at the same time pointing to the corner of a street. As he spoke, and in the very direction he indicated, a groan, expressive of bitter grief, was heard, and a woman was seen waving her hand to a passenger on board the vessel about to sail. Monte Cristo looked at her with an emotion that must have been remarked by Morrel had not his eyes been fixed on the vessel.\n\n“Oh, heavens!” exclaimed Morrel, “I do not deceive myself—that young man who is waving his hat, that youth in the uniform of a lieutenant, is Albert de Morcerf!”\n\n“Yes,” said Monte Cristo, “I recognized him.”\n\n“How so?—you were looking the other way.”\n50209m\n\nThe count smiled, as he was in the habit of doing when he did not want to make any reply, and he again turned towards the veiled woman, who soon disappeared at the corner of the street. Turning to his friend:\n\n“Dear Maximilian,” said the count, “have you nothing to do in this land?”\n\n“I have to weep over the grave of my father,” replied Morrel in a broken voice.\n\n“Well, then, go,—wait for me there, and I will soon join you.”\n\n“You leave me, then?”\n\n“Yes; I also have a pious visit to pay.”\n\nMorrel allowed his hand to fall into that which the count extended to him; then with an inexpressibly sorrowful inclination of the head he quitted the count and bent his steps to the east of the city. Monte Cristo remained on the same spot until Maximilian was out of sight; he then walked slowly towards the Allées de Meilhan to seek out a small house with which our readers were made familiar at the beginning of this story.\n\nIt yet stood, under the shade of the fine avenue of lime-trees, which forms one of the most frequent walks of the idlers of Marseilles, covered by an immense vine, which spreads its aged and blackened branches over the stone front, burnt yellow by the ardent sun of the south. Two stone steps worn away by the friction of many feet led to the door, which was made of three planks; the door had never been painted or varnished, so great cracks yawned in it during the dry season to close again when the rains came on. The house, with all its crumbling antiquity and apparent misery, was yet cheerful and picturesque, and was the same that old Dantès formerly inhabited—the only difference being that the old man occupied merely the garret, while the whole house was now placed at the command of Mercédès by the count.\n\nThe woman whom the count had seen leave the ship with so much regret entered this house; she had scarcely closed the door after her when Monte Cristo appeared at the corner of a street, so that he found and lost her again almost at the same instant. The worn out steps were old acquaintances of his; he knew better than anyone else how to open that weather-beaten door with the large headed nail which served to raise the latch within. He entered without knocking, or giving any other intimation of his presence, as if he had been a friend or the master of the place. At the end of a passage paved with bricks, was a little garden, bathed in sunshine, and rich in warmth and light. In this garden Mercédès had found, at the place indicated by the count, the sum of money which he, through a sense of delicacy, had described as having been placed there twenty-four years previously. The trees of the garden were easily seen from the steps of the street-door.\n\nMonte Cristo, on stepping into the house, heard a sigh that was almost a deep sob; he looked in the direction whence it came, and there under an arbor of Virginia jessamine,29 with its thick foliage and beautiful long purple flowers, he saw Mercédès seated, with her head bowed, and weeping bitterly. She had raised her veil, and with her face hidden by her hands was giving free scope to the sighs and tears which had been so long restrained by the presence of her son.\n\nMonte Cristo advanced a few steps, which were heard on the gravel. Mercédès raised her head, and uttered a cry of terror on beholding a man before her.\n\n50211m\n“Madame,” said the count, “it is no longer in my power to restore you to happiness, but I offer you consolation; will you deign to accept it as coming from a friend?”\n\n“I am, indeed, most wretched,” replied Mercédès. “Alone in the world, I had but my son, and he has left me!”\n\n“He possesses a noble heart, madame,” replied the count, “and he has acted rightly. He feels that every man owes a tribute to his country; some contribute their talents, others their industry; these devote their blood, those their nightly labors, to the same cause. Had he remained with you, his life must have become a hateful burden, nor would he have participated in your griefs. He will increase in strength and honor by struggling with adversity, which he will convert into prosperity. Leave him to build up the future for you, and I venture to say you will confide it to safe hands.”\n\n“Oh,” replied the wretched woman, mournfully shaking her head, “the prosperity of which you speak, and which, from the bottom of my heart, I pray God in his mercy to grant him, I can never enjoy. The bitter cup of adversity has been drained by me to the very dregs, and I feel that the grave is not far distant. You have acted kindly, count, in bringing me back to the place where I have enjoyed so much bliss. I ought to meet death on the same spot where happiness was once all my own.”\n\n“Alas,” said Monte Cristo, “your words sear and embitter my heart, the more so as you have every reason to hate me. I have been the cause of all your misfortunes; but why do you pity, instead of blaming me? You render me still more unhappy——”\n\n“Hate you, blame you—_you_, Edmond! Hate, reproach, the man that has spared my son’s life! For was it not your fatal and sanguinary intention to destroy that son of whom M. de Morcerf was so proud? Oh, look at me closely, and discover, if you can, even the semblance of a reproach in me.”\n\nThe count looked up and fixed his eyes on Mercédès, who arose partly from her seat and extended both her hands towards him.\n\n“Oh, look at me,” continued she, with a feeling of profound melancholy, “my eyes no longer dazzle by their brilliancy, for the time has long fled since I used to smile on Edmond Dantès, who anxiously looked out for me from the window of yonder garret, then inhabited by his old father. Years of grief have created an abyss between those days and the present. I neither reproach you nor hate you, my friend. Oh, no, Edmond, it is myself that I blame, myself that I hate! Oh, miserable creature that I am!” cried she, clasping her hands, and raising her eyes to heaven. “I once possessed piety, innocence, and love, the three ingredients of the happiness of angels, and now what am I?”\n\nMonte Cristo approached her, and silently took her hand.\n\n“No,” said she, withdrawing it gently—“no, my friend, touch me not. You have spared me, yet of all those who have fallen under your vengeance I was the most guilty. They were influenced by hatred, by avarice, and by self-love; but I was base, and for want of courage acted against my judgment. Nay, do not press my hand, Edmond; you are thinking, I am sure, of some kind speech to console me, but do not utter it to me, reserve it for others more worthy of your kindness. See” (and she exposed her face completely to view)—“see, misfortune has silvered my hair, my eyes have shed so many tears that they are encircled by a rim of purple, and my brow is wrinkled. You, Edmond, on the contrary,—you are still young, handsome, dignified; it is because you have had faith; because you have had strength, because you have had trust in God, and God has sustained you. But as for me, I have been a coward; I have denied God and he has abandoned me.”\n50213m\n\nMercédès burst into tears; her woman’s heart was breaking under its load of memories. Monte Cristo took her hand and imprinted a kiss on it; but she herself felt that it was a kiss of no greater warmth than he would have bestowed on the hand of some marble statue of a saint.\n\n“It often happens,” continued she, “that a first fault destroys the prospects of a whole life. I believed you dead; why did I survive you? What good has it done me to mourn for you eternally in the secret recesses of my heart?—only to make a woman of thirty-nine look like a woman of fifty. Why, having recognized you, and I the only one to do so—why was I able to save my son alone? Ought I not also to have rescued the man that I had accepted for a husband, guilty though he were? Yet I let him die! What do I say? Oh, merciful heavens, was I not accessory to his death by my supine insensibility, by my contempt for him, not remembering, or not willing to remember, that it was for my sake he had become a traitor and a perjurer? In what am I benefited by accompanying my son so far, since I now abandon him, and allow him to depart alone to the baneful climate of Africa? Oh, I have been base, cowardly, I tell you; I have abjured my affections, and like all renegades I am of evil omen to those who surround me!”\n\n“No, Mercédès,” said Monte Cristo, “no; you judge yourself with too much severity. You are a noble-minded woman, and it was your grief that disarmed me. Still I was but an agent, led on by an invisible and offended Deity, who chose not to withhold the fatal blow that I was destined to hurl. I take that God to witness, at whose feet I have prostrated myself daily for the last ten years, that I would have sacrificed my life to you, and with my life the projects that were indissolubly linked with it. But—and I say it with some pride, Mercédès—God needed me, and I lived. Examine the past and the present, and endeavor to dive into futurity, and then say whether I am not a divine instrument. The most dreadful misfortunes, the most frightful sufferings, the abandonment of all those who loved me, the persecution of those who did not know me, formed the trials of my youth; when suddenly, from captivity, solitude, misery, I was restored to light and liberty, and became the possessor of a fortune so brilliant, so unbounded, so unheard-of, that I must have been blind not to be conscious that God had endowed me with it to work out his own great designs. From that time I looked upon this fortune as something confided to me for a particular purpose. Not a thought was given to a life which you once, Mercédès, had the power to render blissful; not one hour of peaceful calm was mine; but I felt myself driven on like an exterminating angel. Like adventurous captains about to embark on some enterprise full of danger, I laid in my provisions, I loaded my weapons, I collected every means of attack and defence; I inured my body to the most violent exercises, my soul to the bitterest trials; I taught my arm to slay, my eyes to behold excruciating sufferings, and my mouth to smile at the most horrid spectacles. Good-natured, confiding, and forgiving as I had been, I became revengeful, cunning, and wicked, or rather, immovable as fate. Then I launched out into the path that was opened to me. I overcame every obstacle, and reached the goal; but woe to those who stood in my pathway!”\n\n50215m\n“Enough,” said Mercédès; “enough, Edmond! Believe me, that she who alone recognized you has been the only one to comprehend you; and had she crossed your path, and you had crushed her like glass, still, Edmond, still she must have admired you! Like the gulf between me and the past, there is an abyss between you, Edmond, and the rest of mankind; and I tell you freely that the comparison I draw between you and other men will ever be one of my greatest tortures. No, there is nothing in the world to resemble you in worth and goodness! But we must say farewell, Edmond, and let us part.”\n\n“Before I leave you, Mercédès, have you no request to make?” said the count.\n\n“I desire but one thing in this world, Edmond,—the happiness of my son.”\n\n“Pray to the Almighty to spare his life, and I will take upon myself to promote his happiness.”\n\n“Thank you, Edmond.”\n\n“But have you no request to make for yourself, Mercédès?”\n\n“For myself I want nothing. I live, as it were, between two graves. One is that of Edmond Dantès, lost to me long, long since. He had my love! That word ill becomes my faded lip now, but it is a memory dear to my heart, and one that I would not lose for all that the world contains. The other grave is that of the man who met his death from the hand of Edmond Dantès. I approve of the deed, but I must pray for the dead.”\n\n“Your son shall be happy, Mercédès,” repeated the count.\n\n“Then I shall enjoy as much happiness as this world can possibly confer.”\n\n“But what are your intentions?”\n\nMercédès smiled sadly.\n\n“To say that I shall live here, like the Mercédès of other times, gaining my bread by labor, would not be true, nor would you believe me. I have no longer the strength to do anything but to spend my days in prayer. However, I shall have no occasion to work, for the little sum of money buried by you, and which I found in the place you mentioned, will be sufficient to maintain me. Rumor will probably be busy respecting me, my occupations, my manner of living—that will signify but little, that concerns God, you, and myself.”\n\n“Mercédès,” said the count, “I do not say it to blame you, but you made an unnecessary sacrifice in relinquishing the whole of the fortune amassed by M. de Morcerf; half of it at least by right belonged to you, in virtue of your vigilance and economy.”\n\n“I perceive what you are intending to propose to me; but I cannot accept it, Edmond—my son would not permit it.”\n\n“Nothing shall be done without the full approbation of Albert de Morcerf. I will make myself acquainted with his intentions and will submit to them. But if he be willing to accept my offers, will you oppose them?”\n\n“You well know, Edmond, that I am no longer a reasoning creature; I have no will, unless it be the will never to decide. I have been so overwhelmed by the many storms that have broken over my head, that I am become passive in the hands of the Almighty, like a sparrow in the talons of an eagle. I live, because it is not ordained for me to die. If succor be sent to me, I will accept it.”\n\n“Ah, madame,” said Monte Cristo, “you should not talk thus! It is not so we should evince our resignation to the will of heaven; on the contrary, we are all free agents.”\n\n“Alas!” exclaimed Mercédès, “if it were so, if I possessed free-will, but without the power to render that will efficacious, it would drive me to despair.”\n\nMonte Cristo dropped his head and shrank from the vehemence of her grief.\n\n“Will you not even say you will see me again?” he asked.\n\n“On the contrary, we shall meet again,” said Mercédès, pointing to heaven with solemnity. “I tell you so to prove to you that I still hope.”\n\nAnd after pressing her own trembling hand upon that of the count, Mercédès rushed up the stairs and disappeared. Monte Cristo slowly left the house and turned towards the quay. But Mercédès did not witness his departure, although she was seated at the little window of the room which had been occupied by old Dantès. Her eyes were straining to see the ship which was carrying her son over the vast sea; but still her voice involuntarily murmured softly:\n\n“Edmond, Edmond, Edmond!”\n\n# Chapter 113. The Past\n\nThe count departed with a sad heart from the house in which he had left Mercédès, probably never to behold her again. Since the death of little Edward a great change had taken place in Monte Cristo. Having reached the summit of his vengeance by a long and tortuous path, he saw an abyss of doubt yawning before him. More than this, the conversation which had just taken place between Mercédès and himself had awakened so many recollections in his heart that he felt it necessary to combat with them. A man of the count’s temperament could not long indulge in that melancholy which can exist in common minds, but which destroys superior ones. He thought he must have made an error in his calculations if he now found cause to blame himself.\n\n“I cannot have deceived myself,” he said; “I must look upon the past in a false light. What!” he continued, “can I have been following a false path?—can the end which I proposed be a mistaken end?—can one hour have sufficed to prove to an architect that the work upon which he founded all his hopes was an impossible, if not a sacrilegious, undertaking? I cannot reconcile myself to this idea—it would madden me. The reason why I am now dissatisfied is that I have not a clear appreciation of the past. The past, like the country through which we walk, becomes indistinct as we advance. My position is like that of a person wounded in a dream; he feels the wound, though he cannot recollect when he received it.\n\n“Come, then, thou regenerate man, thou extravagant prodigal, thou awakened sleeper, thou all-powerful visionary, thou invincible millionaire,—once again review thy past life of starvation and wretchedness, revisit the scenes where fate and misfortune conducted, and where despair received thee. Too many diamonds, too much gold and splendor, are now reflected by the mirror in which Monte Cristo seeks to behold Dantès. Hide thy diamonds, bury thy gold, shroud thy splendor, exchange riches for poverty, liberty for a prison, a living body for a corpse!”\n\nAs he thus reasoned, Monte Cristo walked down the Rue de la Caisserie. It was the same through which, twenty-four years ago, he had been conducted by a silent and nocturnal guard; the houses, today so smiling and animated, were on that night dark, mute, and closed.\n\n“And yet they were the same,” murmured Monte Cristo, “only now it is broad daylight instead of night; it is the sun which brightens the place, and makes it appear so cheerful.”\n\nHe proceeded towards the quay by the Rue Saint-Laurent, and advanced to the Consigne; it was the point where he had embarked. A pleasure-boat with striped awning was going by. Monte Cristo called the owner, who immediately rowed up to him with the eagerness of a boatman hoping for a good fare.\n\nThe weather was magnificent, and the excursion a treat. The sun, red and flaming, was sinking into the embrace of the welcoming ocean. The sea, smooth as crystal, was now and then disturbed by the leaping of fish, which were pursued by some unseen enemy and sought for safety in another element; while on the extreme verge of the horizon might be seen the fishermen’s boats, white and graceful as the sea-gull, or the merchant vessels bound for Corsica or Spain.\n\nBut notwithstanding the serene sky, the gracefully formed boats, and the golden light in which the whole scene was bathed, the Count of Monte Cristo, wrapped in his cloak, could think only of this terrible voyage, the details of which were one by one recalled to his memory. The solitary light burning at the Catalans; that first sight of the Château d’If, which told him whither they were leading him; the struggle with the gendarmes when he wished to throw himself overboard; his despair when he found himself vanquished, and the sensation when the muzzle of the carbine touched his forehead—all these were brought before him in vivid and frightful reality.\n\nLike the streams which the heat of the summer has dried up, and which after the autumnal storms gradually begin oozing drop by drop, so did the count feel his heart gradually fill with the bitterness which formerly nearly overwhelmed Edmond Dantès. Clear sky, swift-flitting boats, and brilliant sunshine disappeared; the heavens were hung with black, and the gigantic structure of the Château d’If seemed like the phantom of a mortal enemy. As they reached the shore, the count instinctively shrunk to the extreme end of the boat, and the owner was obliged to call out, in his sweetest tone of voice:\n\n“Sir, we are at the landing.”\n\nMonte Cristo remembered that on that very spot, on the same rock, he had been violently dragged by the guards, who forced him to ascend the slope at the points of their bayonets. The journey had seemed very long to Dantès, but Monte Cristo found it equally short. Each stroke of the oar seemed to awaken a new throng of ideas, which sprang up with the flying spray of the sea.\n50219m\n\nThere had been no prisoners confined in the Château d’If since the revolution of July; it was only inhabited by a guard, kept there for the prevention of smuggling. A concierge waited at the door to exhibit to visitors this monument of curiosity, once a scene of terror.\n\nThe count inquired whether any of the ancient jailers were still there; but they had all been pensioned, or had passed on to some other employment. The concierge who attended him had only been there since 1830. He visited his own dungeon. He again beheld the dull light vainly endeavoring to penetrate the narrow opening. His eyes rested upon the spot where had stood his bed, since then removed, and behind the bed the new stones indicated where the breach made by the Abbé Faria had been. Monte Cristo felt his limbs tremble; he seated himself upon a log of wood.\n\n“Are there any stories connected with this prison besides the one relating to the poisoning of Mirabeau?” asked the count; “are there any traditions respecting these dismal abodes,—in which it is difficult to believe men can ever have imprisoned their fellow-creatures?”\n\n“Yes, sir; indeed, the jailer Antoine told me one connected with this very dungeon.”\n\nMonte Cristo shuddered; Antoine had been his jailer. He had almost forgotten his name and face, but at the mention of the name he recalled his person as he used to see it, the face encircled by a beard, wearing the brown jacket, the bunch of keys, the jingling of which he still seemed to hear. The count turned around, and fancied he saw him in the corridor, rendered still darker by the torch carried by the concierge.\n\n“Would you like to hear the story, sir?”\n\n“Yes; relate it,” said Monte Cristo, pressing his hand to his heart to still its violent beatings; he felt afraid of hearing his own history.\n\n“This dungeon,” said the concierge, “was, it appears, some time ago occupied by a very dangerous prisoner, the more so since he was full of industry. Another person was confined in the Château at the same time, but he was not wicked, he was only a poor mad priest.”\n\n“Ah, indeed?—mad!” repeated Monte Cristo; “and what was his mania?”\n\n“He offered millions to anyone who would set him at liberty.”\n\nMonte Cristo raised his eyes, but he could not see the heavens; there was a stone veil between him and the firmament. He thought that there had been no less thick a veil before the eyes of those to whom Faria offered the treasures.\n\n“Could the prisoners see each other?” he asked.\n\n“Oh, no, sir, it was expressly forbidden; but they eluded the vigilance of the guards, and made a passage from one dungeon to the other.”\n\n“And which of them made this passage?”\n\n“Oh, it must have been the young man, certainly, for he was strong and industrious, while the abbé was aged and weak; besides, his mind was too vacillating to allow him to carry out an idea.”\n\n“Blind fools!” murmured the count.\n\n“However, be that as it may, the young man made a tunnel, how or by what means no one knows; but he made it, and there is the evidence yet remaining of his work. Do you see it?” and the man held the torch to the wall.\n50223m\n\n“Ah, yes; I see,” said the count, in a voice hoarse from emotion.\n\n“The result was that the two men communicated with one another; how long they did so, nobody knows. One day the old man fell ill and died. Now guess what the young one did?”\n\n“Tell me.”\n\n“He carried off the corpse, which he placed in his own bed with its face to the wall; then he entered the empty dungeon, closed the entrance, and slipped into the sack which had contained the dead body. Did you ever hear of such an idea?”\n\nMonte Cristo closed his eyes, and seemed again to experience all the sensations he had felt when the coarse canvas, yet moist with the cold dews of death, had touched his face.\n\nThe jailer continued:\n\n“Now this was his project. He fancied that they buried the dead at the Château d’If, and imagining they would not expend much labor on the grave of a prisoner, he calculated on raising the earth with his shoulders, but unfortunately their arrangements at the Château frustrated his projects. They never buried the dead; they merely attached a heavy cannon-ball to the feet, and then threw them into the sea. This is what was done. The young man was thrown from the top of the rock; the corpse was found on the bed next day, and the whole truth was guessed, for the men who performed the office then mentioned what they had not dared to speak of before, that at the moment the corpse was thrown into the deep, they heard a shriek, which was almost immediately stifled by the water in which it disappeared.”\n\nThe count breathed with difficulty; the cold drops ran down his forehead, and his heart was full of anguish.\n\n“No,” he muttered, “the doubt I felt was but the commencement of forgetfulness; but here the wound reopens, and the heart again thirsts for vengeance. And the prisoner,” he continued aloud, “was he ever heard of afterwards?”\n\n“Oh, no; of course not. You can understand that one of two things must have happened; he must either have fallen flat, in which case the blow, from a height of ninety feet, must have killed him instantly, or he must have fallen upright, and then the weight would have dragged him to the bottom, where he remained—poor fellow!”\n\n“Then you pity him?” said the count.\n\n“_Ma foi_, yes; though he was in his own element.”\n\n“What do you mean?”\n\n“The report was that he had been a naval officer, who had been confined for plotting with the Bonapartists.”\n\n“Great is truth,” muttered the count, “fire cannot burn, nor water drown it! Thus the poor sailor lives in the recollection of those who narrate his history; his terrible story is recited in the chimney-corner, and a shudder is felt at the description of his transit through the air to be swallowed by the deep.” Then, the count added aloud, “Was his name ever known?”\n\n“Oh, yes; but only as No. 34.”\n\n“Oh, Villefort, Villefort,” murmured the count, “this scene must often have haunted thy sleepless hours!”\n\n“Do you wish to see anything more, sir?” said the concierge.\n\n“Yes, especially if you will show me the poor abbé’s room.”\n\n“Ah! No. 27.”\n\n“Yes; No. 27.” repeated the count, who seemed to hear the voice of the abbé answering him in those very words through the wall when asked his name.\n\n“Come, sir.”\n“Wait,” said Monte Cristo, “I wish to take one final glance around this room.”\n\n“This is fortunate,” said the guide; “I have forgotten the other key.”\n\n“Go and fetch it.”\n\n“I will leave you the torch, sir.”\n\n“No, take it away; I can see in the dark.”\n\n“Why, you are like No. 34. They said he was so accustomed to darkness that he could see a pin in the darkest corner of his dungeon.”\n\n“He spent fourteen years to arrive at that,” muttered the count.\n\nThe guide carried away the torch. The count had spoken correctly. Scarcely had a few seconds elapsed, ere he saw everything as distinctly as by daylight. Then he looked around him, and really recognized his dungeon.\n\n“Yes,” he said, “there is the stone upon which I used to sit; there is the impression made by my shoulders on the wall; there is the mark of my blood made when one day I dashed my head against the wall. Oh, those figures, how well I remember them! I made them one day to calculate the age of my father, that I might know whether I should find him still living, and that of Mercédès, to know if I should find her still free. After finishing that calculation, I had a minute’s hope. I did not reckon upon hunger and infidelity!” and a bitter laugh escaped the count.\n\nHe saw in fancy the burial of his father, and the marriage of Mercédès. On the other side of the dungeon he perceived an inscription, the white letters of which were still visible on the green wall:\n\n“‘_Oh, God!_’” he read, “‘_preserve my memory!_’”\n\n“Oh, yes,” he cried, “that was my only prayer at last; I no longer begged for liberty, but memory; I dreaded to become mad and forgetful. Oh, God, thou hast preserved my memory; I thank thee, I thank thee!”\n\nAt this moment the light of the torch was reflected on the wall; the guide was coming; Monte Cristo went to meet him.\n\n“Follow me, sir;” and without ascending the stairs the guide conducted him by a subterraneous passage to another entrance. There, again, Monte Cristo was assailed by a multitude of thoughts. The first thing that met his eye was the meridian, drawn by the abbé on the wall, by which he calculated the time; then he saw the remains of the bed on which the poor prisoner had died. The sight of this, instead of exciting the anguish experienced by the count in the dungeon, filled his heart with a soft and grateful sentiment, and tears fell from his eyes.\n\n“This is where the mad abbé was kept, sir, and that is where the young man entered;” and the guide pointed to the opening, which had remained unclosed. “From the appearance of the stone,” he continued, “a learned gentleman discovered that the prisoners might have communicated together for ten years. Poor things! Those must have been ten weary years.”\n\nDantès took some louis from his pocket, and gave them to the man who had twice unconsciously pitied him. The guide took them, thinking them merely a few pieces of little value; but the light of the torch revealed their true worth.\n\n“Sir,” he said, “you have made a mistake; you have given me gold.”\n\n“I know it.”\n\nThe concierge looked upon the count with surprise.\n\n“Sir,” he cried, scarcely able to believe his good fortune—“sir, I cannot understand your generosity!”\n\n“Oh, it is very simple, my good fellow; I have been a sailor, and your story touched me more than it would others.”\n\n“Then, sir, since you are so liberal, I ought to offer you something.”\n50227m\n\n“What have you to offer to me, my friend? Shells? Straw-work? Thank you!”\n\n“No, sir, neither of those; something connected with this story.”\n\n“Really? What is it?”\n\n“Listen,” said the guide; “I said to myself, ‘Something is always left in a cell inhabited by one prisoner for fifteen years,’ so I began to sound the wall.”\n\n“Ah,” cried Monte Cristo, remembering the abbé’s two hiding-places.\n\n“After some search, I found that the floor gave a hollow sound near the head of the bed, and at the hearth.”\n\n“Yes,” said the count, “yes.”\n\n“I raised the stones, and found——”\n\n“A rope-ladder and some tools?”\n\n“How do you know that?” asked the guide in astonishment.\n\n“I do not know—I only guess it, because that sort of thing is generally found in prisoners’ cells.”\n\n“Yes, sir, a rope-ladder and tools.”\n\n“And have you them yet?”\n\n“No, sir; I sold them to visitors, who considered them great curiosities; but I have still something left.”\n\n“What is it?” asked the count, impatiently.\n\n“A sort of book, written upon strips of cloth.”\n\n“Go and fetch it, my good fellow; and if it be what I hope, you will do well.”\n\n“I will run for it, sir;” and the guide went out.\n\nThen the count knelt down by the side of the bed, which death had converted into an altar.\n\n“Oh, second father,” he exclaimed, “thou who hast given me liberty, knowledge, riches; thou who, like beings of a superior order to ourselves, couldst understand the science of good and evil; if in the depths of the tomb there still remain something within us which can respond to the voice of those who are left on earth; if after death the soul ever revisit the places where we have lived and suffered,—then, noble heart, sublime soul, then I conjure thee by the paternal love thou didst bear me, by the filial obedience I vowed to thee, grant me some sign, some revelation! Remove from me the remains of doubt, which, if it change not to conviction, must become remorse!” The count bowed his head, and clasped his hands together.\n\n“Here, sir,” said a voice behind him.\n\nMonte Cristo shuddered, and arose. The concierge held out the strips of cloth upon which the Abbé Faria had spread the riches of his mind. The manuscript was the great work by the Abbé Faria upon the kingdoms of Italy. The count seized it hastily, his eyes immediately fell upon the epigraph, and he read:\n\n“Thou shalt tear out the dragons’ teeth, and shall trample the lions under foot, saith the Lord.”\n\n“Ah,” he exclaimed, “here is my answer. Thanks, father, thanks.” And feeling in his pocket, he took thence a small pocket-book, which contained ten bank-notes, each of 1,000 francs.\n\n“Here,” he said, “take this pocket-book.”\n\n“Do you give it to me?”\n\n“Yes; but only on condition that you will not open it till I am gone;” and placing in his breast the treasure he had just found, which was more valuable to him than the richest jewel, he rushed out of the corridor, and reaching his boat, cried, “To Marseilles!”\n\nThen, as he departed, he fixed his eyes upon the gloomy prison.\n\n“Woe,” he cried, “to those who confined me in that wretched prison; and woe to those who forgot that I was there!”\n50229m\n\nAs he repassed the Catalans, the count turned around and burying his head in his cloak murmured the name of a woman. The victory was complete; twice he had overcome his doubts. The name he pronounced, in a voice of tenderness, amounting almost to love, was that of Haydée.\n\nOn landing, the count turned towards the cemetery, where he felt sure of finding Morrel. He, too, ten years ago, had piously sought out a tomb, and sought it vainly. He, who returned to France with millions, had been unable to find the grave of his father, who had perished from hunger. Morrel had indeed placed a cross over the spot, but it had fallen down and the grave-digger had burnt it, as he did all the old wood in the churchyard.\n\nThe worthy merchant had been more fortunate. Dying in the arms of his children, he had been by them laid by the side of his wife, who had preceded him in eternity by two years. Two large slabs of marble, on which were inscribed their names, were placed on either side of a little enclosure, railed in, and shaded by four cypress-trees. Morrel was leaning against one of these, mechanically fixing his eyes on the graves. His grief was so profound that he was nearly unconscious.\n50231m\n\n“Maximilian,” said the count, “you should not look on the graves, but there;” and he pointed upwards.\n\n“The dead are everywhere,” said Morrel; “did you not yourself tell me so as we left Paris?”\n\n“Maximilian,” said the count, “you asked me during the journey to allow you to remain some days at Marseilles. Do you still wish to do so?”\n\n“I have no wishes, count; only I fancy I could pass the time less painfully here than anywhere else.”\n\n“So much the better, for I must leave you; but I carry your word with me, do I not?”\n\n“Ah, count, I shall forget it.”\n\n“No, you will not forget it, because you are a man of honor, Morrel, because you have taken an oath, and are about to do so again.”\n\n“Oh, count, have pity upon me. I am so unhappy.”\n\n“I have known a man much more unfortunate than you, Morrel.”\n\n“Impossible!”\n\n“Alas,” said Monte Cristo, “it is the infirmity of our nature always to believe ourselves much more unhappy than those who groan by our sides!”\n\n“What can be more wretched than the man who has lost all he loved and desired in the world?”\n\n“Listen, Morrel, and pay attention to what I am about to tell you. I knew a man who like you had fixed all his hopes of happiness upon a woman. He was young, he had an old father whom he loved, a betrothed bride whom he adored. He was about to marry her, when one of the caprices of fate,—which would almost make us doubt the goodness of Providence, if that Providence did not afterwards reveal itself by proving that all is but a means of conducting to an end,—one of those caprices deprived him of his mistress, of the future of which he had dreamed (for in his blindness he forgot he could only read the present), and cast him into a dungeon.”\n\n“Ah,” said Morrel, “one quits a dungeon in a week, a month, or a year.”\n\n“He remained there fourteen years, Morrel,” said the count, placing his hand on the young man’s shoulder. Maximilian shuddered.\n\n“Fourteen years!” he muttered.\n\n“Fourteen years!” repeated the count. “During that time he had many moments of despair. He also, Morrel, like you, considered himself the unhappiest of men.”\n\n“Well?” asked Morrel.\n\n“Well, at the height of his despair God assisted him through human means. At first, perhaps, he did not recognize the infinite mercy of the Lord, but at last he took patience and waited. One day he miraculously left the prison, transformed, rich, powerful. His first cry was for his father; but that father was dead.”\n\n“My father, too, is dead,” said Morrel.\n\n“Yes; but your father died in your arms, happy, respected, rich, and full of years; his father died poor, despairing, almost doubtful of Providence; and when his son sought his grave ten years afterwards, his tomb had disappeared, and no one could say, ‘There sleeps the father you so well loved.’”\n\n“Oh!” exclaimed Morrel.\n\n“He was, then, a more unhappy son than you, Morrel, for he could not even find his father’s grave.”\n\n“But then he had the woman he loved still remaining?”\n\n“You are deceived, Morrel, that woman——”\n\n“She was dead?”\n\n“Worse than that, she was faithless, and had married one of the persecutors of her betrothed. You see, then, Morrel, that he was a more unhappy lover than you.”\n\n“And has he found consolation?”\n\n“He has at least found peace.”\n\n“And does he ever expect to be happy?”\n\n“He hopes so, Maximilian.”\n\nThe young man’s head fell on his breast.\n\n“You have my promise,” he said, after a minute’s pause, extending his hand to Monte Cristo. “Only remember——”\n\n“On the 5th of October, Morrel, I shall expect you at the Island of Monte Cristo. On the 4th a yacht will wait for you in the port of Bastia, it will be called the _Eurus_. You will give your name to the captain, who will bring you to me. It is understood—is it not?”\n\n“But, count, do you remember that the 5th of October——”\n\n“Child,” replied the count, “not to know the value of a man’s word! I have told you twenty times that if you wish to die on that day, I will assist you. Morrel, farewell!”\n\n“Do you leave me?”\n\n“Yes; I have business in Italy. I leave you alone in your struggle with misfortune—alone with that strong-winged eagle which God sends to bear aloft the elect to his feet. The story of Ganymede, Maximilian, is not a fable, but an allegory.”\n\n“When do you leave?”\n\n“Immediately; the steamer waits, and in an hour I shall be far from you. Will you accompany me to the harbor, Maximilian?”\n\n50233m\n“I am entirely yours, count.”\n\nMorrel accompanied the count to the harbor. The white steam was ascending like a plume of feathers from the black chimney. The steamer soon disappeared, and in an hour afterwards, as the count had said, was scarcely distinguishable in the horizon amidst the fogs of the night.\n\n# Chapter 114. Peppino\n\nAt the same time that the steamer disappeared behind Cape Morgiou, a man travelling post on the road from Florence to Rome had just passed the little town of Aquapendente. He was travelling fast enough to cover a great deal of ground without exciting suspicion. This man was dressed in a greatcoat, or rather a surtout, a little worse for the journey, but which exhibited the ribbon of the Legion of Honor still fresh and brilliant, a decoration which also ornamented the under coat. He might be recognized, not only by these signs, but also from the accent with which he spoke to the postilion, as a Frenchman.\n\nAnother proof that he was a native of the universal country was apparent in the fact of his knowing no other Italian words than the terms used in music, and which like the “goddam” of Figaro, served all possible linguistic requirements. “_Allegro!_” he called out to the postilions at every ascent. “_Moderato!_” he cried as they descended. And heaven knows there are hills enough between Rome and Florence by the way of Aquapendente! These two words greatly amused the men to whom they were addressed. On reaching La Storta, the point from whence Rome is first visible, the traveller evinced none of the enthusiastic curiosity which usually leads strangers to stand up and endeavor to catch sight of the dome of Saint Peter’s, which may be seen long before any other object is distinguishable. No, he merely drew a pocketbook from his pocket, and took from it a paper folded in four, and after having examined it in a manner almost reverential, he said:\n\n“Good! I have it still!”\n\n50235m\nThe carriage entered by the Porta del Popolo, turned to the left, and stopped at the Hôtel d’Espagne. Old Pastrini, our former acquaintance, received the traveller at the door, hat in hand. The traveller alighted, ordered a good dinner, and inquired the address of the house of Thomson & French, which was immediately given to him, as it was one of the most celebrated in Rome. It was situated in the Via dei Banchi, near St. Peter’s.\n\nIn Rome, as everywhere else, the arrival of a post-chaise is an event. Ten young descendants of Marius and the Gracchi, barefooted and out at elbows, with one hand resting on the hip and the other gracefully curved above the head, stared at the traveller, the post-chaise, and the horses; to these were added about fifty little vagabonds from the Papal States, who earned a pittance by diving into the Tiber at high water from the bridge of St. Angelo. Now, as these street Arabs of Rome, more fortunate than those of Paris, understand every language, more especially the French, they heard the traveller order an apartment, a dinner, and finally inquire the way to the house of Thomson & French.\n\nThe result was that when the new-comer left the hotel with the _cicerone_, a man detached himself from the rest of the idlers, and without having been seen by the traveller, and appearing to excite no attention from the guide, followed the stranger with as much skill as a Parisian police agent would have used.\n\nThe Frenchman had been so impatient to reach the house of Thomson & French that he would not wait for the horses to be harnessed, but left word for the carriage to overtake him on the road, or to wait for him at the bankers’ door. He reached it before the carriage arrived. The Frenchman entered, leaving in the anteroom his guide, who immediately entered into conversation with two or three of the industrious idlers who are always to be found in Rome at the doors of banking-houses, churches, museums, or theatres. With the Frenchman, the man who had followed him entered too; the Frenchman knocked at the inner door, and entered the first room; his shadow did the same.\n\n“Messrs. Thomson & French?” inquired the stranger.\n\nAn attendant arose at a sign from a confidential clerk at the first desk.\n\n“Whom shall I announce?” said the attendant.\n\n“Baron Danglars.”\n\n“Follow me,” said the man.\n\nA door opened, through which the attendant and the baron disappeared. The man who had followed Danglars sat down on a bench. The clerk continued to write for the next five minutes; the man preserved profound silence, and remained perfectly motionless. Then the pen of the clerk ceased to move over the paper; he raised his head, and appearing to be perfectly sure of privacy:\n\n“Ah, ha,” he said, “here you are, Peppino!”\n\n“Yes,” was the laconic reply. “You have found out that there is something worth having about this large gentleman?”\n\n“There is no great merit due to me, for we were informed of it.”\n\n“You know his business here, then.”\n\n“_Pardieu_, he has come to draw, but I don’t know how much!”\n\n“You will know presently, my friend.”\n\n“Very well, only do not give me false information as you did the other day.”\n\n“What do you mean?—of whom do you speak? Was it the Englishman who carried off 3,000 crowns from here the other day?”\n\n50237m\n“No; he really had 3,000 crowns, and we found them. I mean the Russian prince, who you said had 30,000 livres, and we only found 22,000.”\n\n“You must have searched badly.”\n\n“Luigi Vampa himself searched.”\n\n“In that case he must either have paid his debts——”\n\n“A Russian do that?”\n\n“Or spent the money?”\n“Possibly, after all.”\n\n“Certainly. But you must let me make my observations, or the Frenchman will transact his business without my knowing the sum.”\n\nPeppino nodded, and taking a rosary from his pocket began to mutter a few prayers while the clerk disappeared through the same door by which Danglars and the attendant had gone out. At the expiration of ten minutes the clerk returned with a beaming countenance.\n\n“Well?” asked Peppino of his friend.\n\n“Joy, joy—the sum is large!”\n\n“Five or six millions, is it not?”\n\n“On the receipt of the Count of Monte Cristo?”\n\n“Why, how came you to be so well acquainted with all this?”\n\n“I told you we were informed beforehand.”\n\n“Then why do you apply to me?”\n\n“That I may be sure I have the right man.”\n\n“Yes, it is indeed he. Five millions—a pretty sum, eh, Peppino?”\n\n“Hush—here is our man!” The clerk seized his pen, and Peppino his beads; one was writing and the other praying when the door opened. Danglars looked radiant with joy; the banker accompanied him to the door. Peppino followed Danglars.\n\nAccording to the arrangements, the carriage was waiting at the door. The guide held the door open. Guides are useful people, who will turn their hands to anything. Danglars leaped into the carriage like a young man of twenty. The _cicerone_ reclosed the door, and sprang up by the side of the coachman. Peppino mounted the seat behind.\n\n“Will your excellency visit Saint Peter’s?” asked the _cicerone_.\n\n“I did not come to Rome to see,” said Danglars aloud; then he added softly, with an avaricious smile, “I came to touch!” and he rapped his pocket-book, in which he had just placed a letter.\n\n“Then your excellency is going——”\n\n“To the hotel.”\n\n“Casa Pastrini!” said the _cicerone_ to the coachman, and the carriage drove rapidly on.\n\n50239m\nTen minutes afterwards the baron entered his apartment, and Peppino stationed himself on the bench outside the door of the hotel, after having whispered something in the ear of one of the descendants of Marius and the Gracchi whom we noticed at the beginning of the chapter, who immediately ran down the road leading to the Capitol at his fullest speed. Danglars was tired and sleepy; he therefore went to bed, placing his pocketbook under his pillow. Peppino had a little spare time, so he had a game of _morra_ with the facchini, lost three crowns, and then to console himself drank a bottle of Orvieto.\n\nThe next morning Danglars awoke late, though he went to bed so early; he had not slept well for five or six nights, even if he had slept at all. He breakfasted heartily, and caring little, as he said, for the beauties of the Eternal City, ordered post-horses at noon. But Danglars had not reckoned upon the formalities of the police and the idleness of the posting-master. The horses only arrived at two o’clock, and the _cicerone_ did not bring the passport till three.\n\nAll these preparations had collected a number of idlers round the door of Signor Pastrini’s; the descendants of Marius and the Gracchi were also not wanting. The baron walked triumphantly through the crowd, who for the sake of gain styled him “your excellency.” As Danglars had hitherto contented himself with being called a baron, he felt rather flattered at the title of excellency, and distributed a dozen silver coins among the beggars, who were ready, for twelve more, to call him “your highness.”\n\n“Which road?” asked the postilion in Italian.\n\n“The Ancona road,” replied the baron. Signor Pastrini interpreted the question and answer, and the horses galloped off.\n\nDanglars intended travelling to Venice, where he would receive one part of his fortune, and then proceeding to Vienna, where he would find the rest, he meant to take up his residence in the latter town, which he had been told was a city of pleasure.\n\nHe had scarcely advanced three leagues out of Rome when daylight began to disappear. Danglars had not intended starting so late, or he would have remained; he put his head out and asked the postilion how long it would be before they reached the next town. “_Non capisco_” (do not understand), was the reply. Danglars bent his head, which he meant to imply, “Very well.” The carriage again moved on.\n\n“I will stop at the first posting-house,” said Danglars to himself.\n\nHe still felt the same self-satisfaction which he had experienced the previous evening, and which had procured him so good a night’s rest. He was luxuriously stretched in a good English calash, with double springs; he was drawn by four good horses, at full gallop; he knew the relay to be at a distance of seven leagues. What subject of meditation could present itself to the banker, so fortunately become bankrupt?\n\nDanglars thought for ten minutes about his wife in Paris; another ten minutes about his daughter travelling with Mademoiselle d’Armilly; the same period was given to his creditors, and the manner in which he intended spending their money; and then, having no subject left for contemplation, he shut his eyes, and fell asleep. Now and then a jolt more violent than the rest caused him to open his eyes; then he felt that he was still being carried with great rapidity over the same country, thickly strewn with broken aqueducts, which looked like granite giants petrified while running a race. But the night was cold, dull, and rainy, and it was much more pleasant for a traveller to remain in the warm carriage than to put his head out of the window to make inquiries of a postilion whose only answer was “_Non capisco_.”\n50241m\n\nDanglars therefore continued to sleep, saying to himself that he would be sure to awake at the posting-house. The carriage stopped. Danglars fancied that they had reached the long-desired point; he opened his eyes and looked through the window, expecting to find himself in the midst of some town, or at least village; but he saw nothing except what seemed like a ruin, where three or four men went and came like shadows.\n\nDanglars waited a moment, expecting the postilion to come and demand payment with the termination of his stage. He intended taking advantage of the opportunity to make fresh inquiries of the new conductor; but the horses were unharnessed, and others put in their places, without anyone claiming money from the traveller. Danglars, astonished, opened the door; but a strong hand pushed him back, and the carriage rolled on. The baron was completely roused.\n\n“Eh?” he said to the postilion, “eh, _mio caro?_”\n\nThis was another little piece of Italian the baron had learned from hearing his daughter sing Italian duets with Cavalcanti. But _mio caro_ did not reply. Danglars then opened the window.\n\n“Come, my friend,” he said, thrusting his hand through the opening, “where are we going?”\n\n“_Dentro la testa!_” answered a solemn and imperious voice, accompanied by a menacing gesture.\n\nDanglars thought _dentro la testa_ meant, “Put in your head!” He was making rapid progress in Italian. He obeyed, not without some uneasiness, which, momentarily increasing, caused his mind, instead of being as unoccupied as it was when he began his journey, to fill with ideas which were very likely to keep a traveller awake, more especially one in such a situation as Danglars. His eyes acquired that quality which in the first moment of strong emotion enables them to see distinctly, and which afterwards fails from being too much taxed. Before we are alarmed, we see correctly; when we are alarmed, we see double; and when we have been alarmed, we see nothing but trouble. Danglars observed a man in a cloak galloping at the right hand of the carriage.\n\n“Some gendarme!” he exclaimed. “Can I have been intercepted by French telegrams to the pontifical authorities?”\n\nHe resolved to end his anxiety. “Where are you taking me?” he asked.\n\n“_Dentro la testa_,” replied the same voice, with the same menacing accent.\n\nDanglars turned to the left; another man on horseback was galloping on that side.\n\n“Decidedly,” said Danglars, with the perspiration on his forehead, “I must be under arrest.” And he threw himself back in the calash, not this time to sleep, but to think.\n\nDirectly afterwards the moon rose. He then saw the great aqueducts, those stone phantoms which he had before remarked, only then they were on the right hand, now they were on the left. He understood that they had described a circle, and were bringing him back to Rome.\n\n“Oh, unfortunate!” he cried, “they must have obtained my arrest.”\n\nThe carriage continued to roll on with frightful speed. An hour of terror elapsed, for every spot they passed showed that they were on the road back. At length he saw a dark mass, against which it seemed as if the carriage was about to dash; but the vehicle turned to one side, leaving the barrier behind and Danglars saw that it was one of the ramparts encircling Rome.\n50243m\n\n“_Mon dieu!_” cried Danglars, “we are not returning to Rome; then it is not justice which is pursuing me! Gracious heavens; another idea presents itself—what if they should be——”\n\nHis hair stood on end. He remembered those interesting stories, so little believed in Paris, respecting Roman bandits; he remembered the adventures that Albert de Morcerf had related when it was intended that he should marry Mademoiselle Eugénie. “They are robbers, perhaps,” he muttered.\n\nJust then the carriage rolled on something harder than gravel road. Danglars hazarded a look on both sides of the road, and perceived monuments of a singular form, and his mind now recalled all the details Morcerf had related, and comparing them with his own situation, he felt sure that he must be on the Appian Way. On the left, in a sort of valley, he perceived a circular excavation. It was Caracalla’s circus. On a word from the man who rode at the side of the carriage, it stopped. At the same time the door was opened. “_Scendi!_” exclaimed a commanding voice.\n\nDanglars instantly descended; although he did not yet speak Italian, he understood it very well. More dead than alive, he looked around him. Four men surrounded him, besides the postilion.\n\n“_Di quà_,” said one of the men, descending a little path leading out of the Appian Way. Danglars followed his guide without opposition, and had no occasion to turn around to see whether the three others were following him. Still it appeared as though they were stationed at equal distances from one another, like sentinels. After walking for about ten minutes, during which Danglars did not exchange a single word with his guide, he found himself between a hillock and a clump of high weeds; three men, standing silent, formed a triangle, of which he was the centre. He wished to speak, but his tongue refused to move.\n\n“_Avanti!_” said the same sharp and imperative voice.\n\nThis time Danglars had double reason to understand, for if the word and gesture had not explained the speaker’s meaning, it was clearly expressed by the man walking behind him, who pushed him so rudely that he struck against the guide. This guide was our friend Peppino, who dashed into the thicket of high weeds, through a path which none but lizards or polecats could have imagined to be an open road.\n\nPeppino stopped before a rock overhung by thick hedges; the rock, half open, afforded a passage to the young man, who disappeared like the evil spirits in the fairy tales. The voice and gesture of the man who followed Danglars ordered him to do the same. There was no longer any doubt, the bankrupt was in the hands of Roman banditti. Danglars acquitted himself like a man placed between two dangerous positions, and who is rendered brave by fear. Notwithstanding his large stomach, certainly not intended to penetrate the fissures of the Campagna, he slid down like Peppino, and closing his eyes fell upon his feet. As he touched the ground, he opened his eyes.\n50245m\n\nThe path was wide, but dark. Peppino, who cared little for being recognized now that he was in his own territories, struck a light and lit a torch. Two other men descended after Danglars forming the rearguard, and pushing Danglars whenever he happened to stop, they came by a gentle declivity to the intersection of two corridors. The walls were hollowed out in sepulchres, one above the other, and which seemed in contrast with the white stones to open their large dark eyes, like those which we see on the faces of the dead. A sentinel struck the rings of his carbine against his left hand.\n\n“Who comes there?” he cried.\n\n“A friend, a friend!” said Peppino; “but where is the captain?”\n\n“There,” said the sentinel, pointing over his shoulder to a spacious crypt, hollowed out of the rock, the lights from which shone into the passage through the large arched openings.\n\n“Fine spoil, captain, fine spoil!” said Peppino in Italian, and taking Danglars by the collar of his coat he dragged him to an opening resembling a door, through which they entered the apartment which the captain appeared to have made his dwelling-place.\n\n“Is this the man?” asked the captain, who was attentively reading Plutarch’s _Life of Alexander_.\n\n“Himself, captain—himself.”\n\n“Very well, show him to me.”\n\nAt this rather impertinent order, Peppino raised his torch to the face of Danglars, who hastily withdrew that he might not have his eyelashes burnt. His agitated features presented the appearance of pale and hideous terror.\n\n“The man is tired,” said the captain, “conduct him to his bed.”\n\n“Oh,” murmured Danglars, “that bed is probably one of the coffins hollowed in the wall, and the sleep I shall enjoy will be death from one of the poniards I see glistening in the darkness.”\n\nFrom their beds of dried leaves or wolf-skins at the back of the chamber now arose the companions of the man who had been found by Albert de Morcerf reading _Cæsar’s Commentaries_, and by Danglars studying the _Life of Alexander_. The banker uttered a groan and followed his guide; he neither supplicated nor exclaimed. He no longer possessed strength, will, power, or feeling; he followed where they led him. At length he found himself at the foot of a staircase, and he mechanically lifted his foot five or six times. Then a low door was opened before him, and bending his head to avoid striking his forehead he entered a small room cut out of the rock. The cell was clean, though empty, and dry, though situated at an immeasurable distance under the earth. A bed of dried grass covered with goat-skins was placed in one corner. Danglars brightened up on beholding it, fancying that it gave some promise of safety.\n\n“Oh, God be praised,” he said; “it is a real bed!”\n\nThis was the second time within the hour that he had invoked the name of God. He had not done so for ten years before.\n\n“_Ecco!_” said the guide, and pushing Danglars into the cell, he closed the door upon him.\n\nA bolt grated and Danglars was a prisoner. If there had been no bolt, it would have been impossible for him to pass through the midst of the garrison who held the catacombs of St. Sebastian, encamped round a master whom our readers must have recognized as the famous Luigi Vampa.\n\nDanglars, too, had recognized the bandit, whose existence he would not believe when Albert de Morcerf mentioned him in Paris; and not only did he recognize him, but the cell in which Albert had been confined, and which was probably kept for the accommodation of strangers. These recollections were dwelt upon with some pleasure by Danglars, and restored him to some degree of tranquillity. Since the bandits had not despatched him at once, he felt that they would not kill him at all. They had arrested him for the purpose of robbery, and as he had only a few louis about him, he doubted not he would be ransomed.\n\nHe remembered that Morcerf had been taxed at 4,000 crowns, and as he considered himself of much greater importance than Morcerf he fixed his own price at 8,000 crowns. Eight thousand crowns amounted to 48,000 livres; he would then have about 5,050,000 francs left. With this sum he could manage to keep out of difficulties. Therefore, tolerably secure in being able to extricate himself from his position, provided he were not rated at the unreasonable sum of 5,050,000 francs, he stretched himself on his bed, and after turning over two or three times, fell asleep with the tranquillity of the hero whose life Luigi Vampa was studying.\n\n# Chapter 115. Luigi Vampa’s Bill of Fare\n\nWe awake from every sleep except the one dreaded by Danglars. He awoke. To a Parisian accustomed to silken curtains, walls hung with velvet drapery, and the soft perfume of burning wood, the white smoke of which diffuses itself in graceful curves around the room, the appearance of the whitewashed cell which greeted his eyes on awakening seemed like the continuation of some disagreeable dream. But in such a situation a single moment suffices to change the strongest doubt into certainty.\n\n“Yes, yes,” he murmured, “I am in the hands of the brigands of whom Albert de Morcerf spoke.” His first idea was to breathe, that he might know whether he was wounded. He borrowed this from _Don Quixote_, the only book he had ever read, but which he still slightly remembered.\n\n“No,” he cried, “they have not wounded, but perhaps they have robbed me!” and he thrust his hands into his pockets. They were untouched; the hundred louis he had reserved for his journey from Rome to Venice were in his trousers pocket, and in that of his greatcoat he found the little note-case containing his letter of credit for 5,050,000 francs.\n\n“Singular bandits!” he exclaimed; “they have left me my purse and pocket-book. As I was saying last night, they intend me to be ransomed. Hello, here is my watch! Let me see what time it is.”\n\nDanglars’ watch, one of Breguet’s repeaters, which he had carefully wound up on the previous night, struck half past five. Without this, Danglars would have been quite ignorant of the time, for daylight did not reach his cell. Should he demand an explanation from the bandits, or should he wait patiently for them to propose it? The last alternative seemed the most prudent, so he waited until twelve o’clock. During all this time a sentinel, who had been relieved at eight o’clock, had been watching his door.\n50249m\n\nDanglars suddenly felt a strong inclination to see the person who kept watch over him. He had noticed that a few rays, not of daylight, but from a lamp, penetrated through the ill-joined planks of the door; he approached just as the brigand was refreshing himself with a mouthful of brandy, which, owing to the leathern bottle containing it, sent forth an odor which was extremely unpleasant to Danglars. “Faugh!” he exclaimed, retreating to the farther corner of his cell.\n\nAt twelve this man was replaced by another functionary, and Danglars, wishing to catch sight of his new guardian, approached the door again.\n\nHe was an athletic, gigantic bandit, with large eyes, thick lips, and a flat nose; his red hair fell in dishevelled masses like snakes around his shoulders.\n\n“Ah, ha,” cried Danglars, “this fellow is more like an ogre than anything else; however, I am rather too old and tough to be very good eating!”\n\nWe see that Danglars was collected enough to jest; at the same time, as though to disprove the ogreish propensities, the man took some black bread, cheese, and onions from his wallet, which he began devouring voraciously.\n\n“May I be hanged,” said Danglars, glancing at the bandit’s dinner through the crevices of the door,—“may I be hanged if I can understand how people can eat such filth!” and he withdrew to seat himself upon his goat-skin, which reminded him of the smell of the brandy.\n\nBut the mysteries of nature are incomprehensible, and there are certain invitations contained in even the coarsest food which appeal very irresistibly to a fasting stomach. Danglars felt his own not to be very well supplied just then, and gradually the man appeared less ugly, the bread less black, and the cheese more fresh, while those dreadful vulgar onions recalled to his mind certain sauces and side-dishes, which his cook prepared in a very superior manner whenever he said, “Monsieur Deniseau, let me have a nice little fricassee today.” He got up and knocked on the door; the bandit raised his head. Danglars knew that he was heard, so he redoubled his blows.\n\n“_Che cosa?_” asked the bandit.\n\n“Come, come,” said Danglars, tapping his fingers against the door, “I think it is quite time to think of giving me something to eat!”\n\nBut whether he did not understand him, or whether he had received no orders respecting the nourishment of Danglars, the giant, without answering, went on with his dinner. Danglars’ feelings were hurt, and not wishing to put himself under obligations to the brute, the banker threw himself down again on his goat-skin and did not breathe another word.\n\nFour hours passed by and the giant was replaced by another bandit. Danglars, who really began to experience sundry gnawings at the stomach, arose softly, again applied his eye to the crack of the door, and recognized the intelligent countenance of his guide. It was, indeed, Peppino who was preparing to mount guard as comfortably as possible by seating himself opposite to the door, and placing between his legs an earthen pan, containing chick-peas stewed with bacon. Near the pan he also placed a pretty little basket of Villetri grapes and a flask of Orvieto. Peppino was decidedly an epicure. Danglars watched these preparations and his mouth watered.\n\n“Come,” he said to himself, “let me try if he will be more tractable than the other;” and he tapped gently at the door.\n\n“_On y va_,” (coming) exclaimed Peppino, who from frequenting the house of Signor Pastrini understood French perfectly in all its idioms.\n\nDanglars immediately recognized him as the man who had called out in such a furious manner, “Put in your head!” But this was not the time for recrimination, so he assumed his most agreeable manner and said with a gracious smile:\n\n“Excuse me, sir, but are they not going to give me any dinner?”\n\n“Does your excellency happen to be hungry?”\n\n“Happen to be hungry,—that’s pretty good, when I haven’t eaten for twenty-four hours!” muttered Danglars. Then he added aloud, “Yes, sir, I am hungry—very hungry.”\n\n“And your excellency wants something to eat?”\n\n“At once, if possible”\n\n“Nothing easier,” said Peppino. “Here you can get anything you want; by paying for it, of course, as among honest folk.”\n\n“Of course!” cried Danglars. “Although, in justice, the people who arrest and imprison you, ought, at least, to feed you.”\n\n“That is not the custom, excellency,” said Peppino.\n\n“A bad reason,” replied Danglars, who reckoned on conciliating his keeper; “but I am content. Let me have some dinner!”\n\n“At once! What would your excellency like?”\n\nAnd Peppino placed his pan on the ground, so that the steam rose directly under the nostrils of Danglars. “Give your orders.”\n\n“Have you kitchens here?”\n\n“Kitchens?—of course—complete ones.”\n\n“And cooks?”\n\n“Excellent!”\n\n“Well, a fowl, fish, game,—it signifies little, so that I eat.”\n\n“As your excellency pleases. You mentioned a fowl, I think?”\n\n“Yes, a fowl.”\n\nPeppino, turning around, shouted, “A fowl for his excellency!” His voice yet echoed in the archway when a handsome, graceful, and half-naked young man appeared, bearing a fowl in a silver dish on his head, without the assistance of his hands.\n\n“I could almost believe myself at the Café de Paris,” murmured Danglars.\n\n“Here, your excellency,” said Peppino, taking the fowl from the young bandit and placing it on the worm-eaten table, which with the stool and the goat-skin bed formed the entire furniture of the cell. Danglars asked for a knife and fork.\n\n“Here, excellency,” said Peppino, offering him a little blunt knife and a boxwood fork. Danglars took the knife in one hand and the fork in the other, and was about to cut up the fowl.\n\n“Pardon me, excellency,” said Peppino, placing his hand on the banker’s shoulder; “people pay here before they eat. They might not be satisfied, and——”\n\n“Ah, ha,” thought Danglars, “this is not so much like Paris, except that I shall probably be skinned! Never mind, I’ll fix that all right. I have always heard how cheap poultry is in Italy; I should think a fowl is worth about twelve sous at Rome.—There,” he said, throwing a louis down.\n\nPeppino picked up the louis, and Danglars again prepared to carve the fowl.\n\n“Stay a moment, your excellency,” said Peppino, rising; “you still owe me something.”\n\n“I said they would skin me,” thought Danglars; but resolving to resist the extortion, he said, “Come, how much do I owe you for this fowl?”\n\n“Your excellency has given me a louis on account.”\n\n“A louis on account for a fowl?”\n\n“Certainly; and your excellency now owes me 4,999 louis.”\n\nDanglars opened his enormous eyes on hearing this gigantic joke.\n\n“Very droll,” he muttered, “very droll indeed,” and he again began to carve the fowl, when Peppino stopped the baron’s right hand with his left, and held out his other hand.\n\n“Come, now,” he said.\n\n“Is it not a joke?” said Danglars.\n\n“We never joke,” replied Peppino, solemn as a Quaker.\n\n“What! A hundred thousand francs for a fowl!”\n\n“Ah, excellency, you cannot imagine how hard it is to rear fowls in these horrible caves!”\n\n“Come, come, this is very droll—very amusing—I allow; but, as I am very hungry, pray allow me to eat. Stay, here is another louis for you.”\n\n“Then that will make only 4,998 louis more,” said Peppino with the same indifference. “I shall get them all in time.”\n\n“Oh, as for that,” said Danglars, angry at this prolongation of the jest,—“as for that you won’t get them at all. Go to the devil! You do not know with whom you have to deal!”\n\n50253m\nPeppino made a sign, and the youth hastily removed the fowl. Danglars threw himself upon his goat-skin, and Peppino, reclosing the door, again began eating his peas and bacon. Though Danglars could not see Peppino, the noise of his teeth allowed no doubt as to his occupation. He was certainly eating, and noisily too, like an ill-bred man. “Brute!” said Danglars. Peppino pretended not to hear him, and without even turning his head continued to eat slowly. Danglars’ stomach felt so empty, that it seemed as if it would be impossible ever to fill it again; still he had patience for another half-hour, which appeared to him like a century. He again arose and went to the door.\n\n“Come, sir, do not keep me starving here any longer, but tell me what they want.”\n\n“Nay, your excellency, it is you who should tell us what you want. Give your orders, and we will execute them.”\n\n“Then open the door directly.” Peppino obeyed. “Now look here, I want something to eat! To eat—do you hear?”\n\n“Are you hungry?”\n\n“Come, you understand me.”\n\n“What would your excellency like to eat?”\n\n“A piece of dry bread, since the fowls are beyond all price in this accursed place.”\n\n“Bread? Very well. Holloa, there, some bread!” he called. The youth brought a small loaf. “How much?” asked Danglars.\n\n“Four thousand nine hundred and ninety-eight louis,” said Peppino; “You have paid two louis in advance.”\n50255m\n\n“What? One hundred thousand francs for a loaf?”\n\n“One hundred thousand francs,” repeated Peppino.\n\n“But you only asked 100,000 francs for a fowl!”\n\n“We have a fixed price for all our provisions. It signifies nothing whether you eat much or little—whether you have ten dishes or one—it is always the same price.”\n\n“What, still keeping up this silly jest? My dear fellow, it is perfectly ridiculous—stupid! You had better tell me at once that you intend starving me to death.”\n\n“Oh, dear, no, your excellency, unless you intend to commit suicide. Pay and eat.”\n\n“And what am I to pay with, brute?” said Danglars, enraged. “Do you suppose I carry 100,000 francs in my pocket?”\n\n“Your excellency has 5,050,000 francs in your pocket; that will be fifty fowls at 100,000 francs apiece, and half a fowl for the 50,000.”\n\nDanglars shuddered. The bandage fell from his eyes, and he understood the joke, which he did not think quite so stupid as he had done just before.\n\n“Come,” he said, “if I pay you the 100,000 francs, will you be satisfied, and allow me to eat at my ease?”\n\n“Certainly,” said Peppino.\n\n“But how can I pay them?”\n\n“Oh, nothing easier; you have an account open with Messrs. Thomson & French, Via dei Banchi, Rome; give me a draft for 4,998 louis on these gentlemen, and our banker shall take it.”\n\nDanglars thought it as well to comply with a good grace, so he took the pen, ink, and paper Peppino offered him, wrote the draft, and signed it.\n\n“Here,” he said, “here is a draft at sight.”\n\n“And here is your fowl.”\n\nDanglars sighed while he carved the fowl; it appeared very thin for the price it had cost. As for Peppino, he examined the paper attentively, put it into his pocket, and continued eating his peas.\n\n# Chapter 116. The Pardon\n\nThe next day Danglars was again hungry; certainly the air of that dungeon was very provocative of appetite. The prisoner expected that he would be at no expense that day, for like an economical man he had concealed half of his fowl and a piece of the bread in the corner of his cell. But he had no sooner eaten than he felt thirsty; he had forgotten that. He struggled against his thirst till his tongue clave to the roof of his mouth; then, no longer able to resist, he called out. The sentinel opened the door; it was a new face. He thought it would be better to transact business with his old acquaintance, so he sent for Peppino.\n\n“Here I am, your excellency,” said Peppino, with an eagerness which Danglars thought favorable to him. “What do you want?”\n\n“Something to drink.”\n\n“Your excellency knows that wine is beyond all price near Rome.”\n\n“Then give me water,” cried Danglars, endeavoring to parry the blow.\n\n“Oh, water is even more scarce than wine, your excellency,—there has been such a drought.”\n\n“Come,” thought Danglars, “it is the same old story.” And while he smiled as he attempted to regard the affair as a joke, he felt his temples get moist with perspiration.\n\n“Come, my friend,” said Danglars, seeing that he made no impression on Peppino, “you will not refuse me a glass of wine?”\n\n“I have already told you that we do not sell at retail.”\n\n“Well, then, let me have a bottle of the least expensive.”\n\n“They are all the same price.”\n\n“And what is that?”\n\n“Twenty-five thousand francs a bottle.”\n\n“Tell me,” cried Danglars, in a tone whose bitterness Harpagon30 alone has been capable of revealing—“tell me that you wish to despoil me of all; it will be sooner over than devouring me piecemeal.”\n\n“It is possible such may be the master’s intention.”\n\n“The master?—who is he?”\n\n“The person to whom you were conducted yesterday.”\n\n“Where is he?”\n\n“Here.”\n“Let me see him.”\n\n“Certainly.”\n\nAnd the next moment Luigi Vampa appeared before Danglars.\n\n“You sent for me?” he said to the prisoner.\n\n“Are you, sir, the chief of the people who brought me here?”\n\n“Yes, your excellency. What then?”\n\n“How much do you require for my ransom?”\n\n“Merely the 5,000,000 you have about you.” Danglars felt a dreadful spasm dart through his heart.\n\n“But this is all I have left in the world,” he said, “out of an immense fortune. If you deprive me of that, take away my life also.”\n\n“We are forbidden to shed your blood.”\n\n“And by whom are you forbidden?”\n\n“By him we obey.”\n\n“You do, then, obey someone?”\n\n“Yes, a chief.”\n\n“I thought you said you were the chief?”\n\n“So I am of these men; but there is another over me.”\n\n“And did your superior order you to treat me in this way?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“But my purse will be exhausted.”\n\n“Probably.”\n\n“Come,” said Danglars, “will you take a million?”\n“No.”\n\n“Two millions?—three?—four? Come, four? I will give them to you on condition that you let me go.”\n\n“Why do you offer me 4,000,000 for what is worth 5,000,000? This is a kind of usury, banker, that I do not understand.”\n\n“Take all, then—take all, I tell you, and kill me!”\n\n“Come, come, calm yourself. You will excite your blood, and that would produce an appetite it would require a million a day to satisfy. Be more economical.”\n\n“But when I have no more money left to pay you?” asked the infuriated Danglars.\n\n“Then you must suffer hunger.”\n\n“Suffer hunger?” said Danglars, becoming pale.\n\n“Most likely,” replied Vampa coolly.\n\n“But you say you do not wish to kill me?”\n“No.”\n\n“And yet you will let me perish with hunger?”\n\n“Ah, that is a different thing.”\n\n“Well, then, wretches,” cried Danglars, “I will defy your infamous calculations—I would rather die at once! You may torture, torment, kill me, but you shall not have my signature again!”\n\n“As your excellency pleases,” said Vampa, as he left the cell.\n\nDanglars, raving, threw himself on the goat-skin. Who could these men be? Who was the invisible chief? What could be his intentions towards him? And why, when everyone else was allowed to be ransomed, might he not also be? Oh, yes; certainly a speedy, violent death would be a fine means of deceiving these remorseless enemies, who appeared to pursue him with such incomprehensible vengeance. But to die? For the first time in his life, Danglars contemplated death with a mixture of dread and desire; the time had come when the implacable spectre, which exists in the mind of every human creature, arrested his attention and called out with every pulsation of his heart, “Thou shalt die!”\n\nDanglars resembled a timid animal excited in the chase; first it flies, then despairs, and at last, by the very force of desperation, sometimes succeeds in eluding its pursuers. Danglars meditated an escape; but the walls were solid rock, a man was sitting reading at the only outlet to the cell, and behind that man shapes armed with guns continually passed. His resolution not to sign lasted two days, after which he offered a million for some food. They sent him a magnificent supper, and took his million.\n\nFrom this time the prisoner resolved to suffer no longer, but to have everything he wanted. At the end of twelve days, after having made a splendid dinner, he reckoned his accounts, and found that he had only 50,000 francs left. Then a strange reaction took place; he who had just abandoned 5,000,000 endeavored to save the 50,000 francs he had left, and sooner than give them up he resolved to enter again upon a life of privation—he was deluded by the hopefulness that is a premonition of madness.\n\nHe, who for so long a time had forgotten God, began to think that miracles were possible—that the accursed cavern might be discovered by the officers of the Papal States, who would release him; that then he would have 50,000 remaining, which would be sufficient to save him from starvation; and finally he prayed that this sum might be preserved to him, and as he prayed he wept. Three days passed thus, during which his prayers were frequent, if not heartfelt. Sometimes he was delirious, and fancied he saw an old man stretched on a pallet; he, also, was dying of hunger.\n\nOn the fourth, he was no longer a man, but a living corpse. He had picked up every crumb that had been left from his former meals, and was beginning to eat the matting which covered the floor of his cell. Then he entreated Peppino, as he would a guardian angel, to give him food; he offered him 1,000 francs for a mouthful of bread. But Peppino did not answer. On the fifth day he dragged himself to the door of the cell.\n\n“Are you not a Christian?” he said, falling on his knees. “Do you wish to assassinate a man who, in the eyes of Heaven, is a brother? Oh, my former friends, my former friends!” he murmured, and fell with his face to the ground. Then rising in despair, he exclaimed, “The chief, the chief!”\n\n“Here I am,” said Vampa, instantly appearing; “what do you want?”\n\n“Take my last gold,” muttered Danglars, holding out his pocket-book, “and let me live here; I ask no more for liberty—I only ask to live!”\n\n“Then you suffer a great deal?”\n\n“Oh, yes, yes, cruelly!”\n\n“Still, there have been men who suffered more than you.”\n\n“I do not think so.”\n\n“Yes; those who have died of hunger.”\n\nDanglars thought of the old man whom, in his hours of delirium, he had seen groaning on his bed. He struck his forehead on the ground and groaned. “Yes,” he said, “there have been some who have suffered more than I have, but then they must have been martyrs at least.”\n\n“Do you repent?” asked a deep, solemn voice, which caused Danglars’ hair to stand on end. His feeble eyes endeavored to distinguish objects, and behind the bandit he saw a man enveloped in a cloak, half lost in the shadow of a stone column.\n\n“Of what must I repent?” stammered Danglars.\n\n“Of the evil you have done,” said the voice.\n\n“Oh, yes; oh, yes, I do indeed repent.” And he struck his breast with his emaciated fist.\n\n“Then I forgive you,” said the man, dropping his cloak, and advancing to the light.\n\n“The Count of Monte Cristo!” said Danglars, more pale from terror than he had been just before from hunger and misery.\n\n“You are mistaken—I am not the Count of Monte Cristo.”\n\n“Then who are you?”\n\n50261m\n“I am he whom you sold and dishonored—I am he whose betrothed you prostituted—I am he upon whom you trampled that you might raise yourself to fortune—I am he whose father you condemned to die of hunger—I am he whom you also condemned to starvation, and who yet forgives you, because he hopes to be forgiven—I am Edmond Dantès!”\n\nDanglars uttered a cry, and fell prostrate.\n\n“Rise,” said the count, “your life is safe; the same good fortune has not happened to your accomplices—one is mad, the other dead. Keep the 50,000 francs you have left—I give them to you. The 5,000,000 you stole from the hospitals has been restored to them by an unknown hand. And now eat and drink; I will entertain you tonight. Vampa, when this man is satisfied, let him be free.”\n\nDanglars remained prostrate while the count withdrew; when he raised his head he saw disappearing down the passage nothing but a shadow, before which the bandits bowed.\n\nAccording to the count’s directions, Danglars was waited on by Vampa, who brought him the best wine and fruits of Italy; then, having conducted him to the road, and pointed to the post-chaise, left him leaning against a tree. He remained there all night, not knowing where he was. When daylight dawned he saw that he was near a stream; he was thirsty, and dragged himself towards it. As he stooped down to drink, he saw that his hair had become entirely white.\n\n# Chapter 117. The Fifth of October\n\nIt was about six o’clock in the evening; an opal-colored light, through which an autumnal sun shed its golden rays, descended on the blue ocean. The heat of the day had gradually decreased, and a light breeze arose, seeming like the respiration of nature on awakening from the burning siesta of the south. A delicious zephyr played along the coasts of the Mediterranean, and wafted from shore to shore the sweet perfume of plants, mingled with the fresh smell of the sea.\n\nA light yacht, chaste and elegant in its form, was gliding amidst the first dews of night over the immense lake, extending from Gibraltar to the Dardanelles, and from Tunis to Venice. The vessel resembled a swan with its wings opened towards the wind, gliding on the water. It advanced swiftly and gracefully, leaving behind it a glittering stretch of foam. By degrees the sun disappeared behind the western horizon; but as though to prove the truth of the fanciful ideas in heathen mythology, its indiscreet rays reappeared on the summit of every wave, as if the god of fire had just sunk upon the bosom of Amphitrite, who in vain endeavored to hide her lover beneath her azure mantle.\n\nThe yacht moved rapidly on, though there did not appear to be sufficient wind to ruffle the curls on the head of a young girl. Standing on the prow was a tall man, of a dark complexion, who saw with dilating eyes that they were approaching a dark mass of land in the shape of a cone, which rose from the midst of the waves like the hat of a Catalan.\n\n“Is that Monte Cristo?” asked the traveller, to whose orders the yacht was for the time submitted, in a melancholy voice.\n\n“Yes, your excellency,” said the captain, “we have reached it.”\n\n“We have reached it!” repeated the traveller in an accent of indescribable sadness.\n\nThen he added, in a low tone, “Yes; that is the haven.”\n\nAnd then he again plunged into a train of thought, the character of which was better revealed by a sad smile, than it would have been by tears. A few minutes afterwards a flash of light, which was extinguished instantly, was seen on the land, and the sound of firearms reached the yacht.\n\n“Your excellency,” said the captain, “that was the land signal, will you answer yourself?”\n\n“What signal?”\n\nThe captain pointed towards the island, up the side of which ascended a volume of smoke, increasing as it rose.\n\n“Ah, yes,” he said, as if awaking from a dream. “Give it to me.”\n\nThe captain gave him a loaded carbine; the traveller slowly raised it, and fired in the air. Ten minutes afterwards, the sails were furled, and they cast anchor about a hundred fathoms from the little harbor. The gig was already lowered, and in it were four oarsmen and a coxswain. The traveller descended, and instead of sitting down at the stern of the boat, which had been decorated with a blue carpet for his accommodation, stood up with his arms crossed. The rowers waited, their oars half lifted out of the water, like birds drying their wings.\n50265m\n\n“Give way,” said the traveller. The eight oars fell into the sea simultaneously without splashing a drop of water, and the boat, yielding to the impulsion, glided forward. In an instant they found themselves in a little harbor, formed in a natural creek; the boat grounded on the fine sand.\n\n“Will your excellency be so good as to mount the shoulders of two of our men, they will carry you ashore?” The young man answered this invitation with a gesture of indifference, and stepped out of the boat; the sea immediately rose to his waist.\n\n“Ah, your excellency,” murmured the pilot, “you should not have done so; our master will scold us for it.”\n\nThe young man continued to advance, following the sailors, who chose a firm footing. Thirty strides brought them to dry land; the young man stamped on the ground to shake off the wet, and looked around for someone to show him his road, for it was quite dark. Just as he turned, a hand rested on his shoulder, and a voice which made him shudder exclaimed:\n\n“Good-evening, Maximilian; you are punctual, thank you!”\n\n“Ah, is it you, count?” said the young man, in an almost joyful accent, pressing Monte Cristo’s hand with both his own.\n\n“Yes; you see I am as exact as you are. But you are dripping, my dear fellow; you must change your clothes, as Calypso said to Telemachus. Come, I have a habitation prepared for you in which you will soon forget fatigue and cold.”\n\nMonte Cristo perceived that the young man had turned around; indeed, Morrel saw with surprise that the men who had brought him had left without being paid, or uttering a word. Already the sound of their oars might be heard as they returned to the yacht.\n\n“Oh, yes,” said the count, “you are looking for the sailors.”\n\n“Yes, I paid them nothing, and yet they are gone.”\n\n“Never mind that, Maximilian,” said Monte Cristo, smiling. “I have made an agreement with the navy, that the access to my island shall be free of all charge. I have made a bargain.”\n\nMorrel looked at the count with surprise. “Count,” he said, “you are not the same here as in Paris.”\n\n“How so?”\n\n“Here you laugh.” The count’s brow became clouded.\n\n“You are right to recall me to myself, Maximilian,” he said; “I was delighted to see you again, and forgot for the moment that all happiness is fleeting.”\n\n“Oh, no, no, count,” cried Maximilian, seizing the count’s hands, “pray laugh; be happy, and prove to me, by your indifference, that life is endurable to sufferers. Oh, how charitable, kind, and good you are; you affect this gayety to inspire me with courage.”\n\n“You are wrong, Morrel; I was really happy.”\n\n“Then you forget me, so much the better.”\n\n“How so?”\n\n“Yes; for as the gladiator said to the emperor, when he entered the arena, ‘He who is about to die salutes you.’”\n\n“Then you are not consoled?” asked the count, surprised.\n\n“Oh,” exclaimed Morrel, with a glance full of bitter reproach, “do you think it possible that I could be?”\n\n“Listen,” said the count. “Do you understand the meaning of my words? You cannot take me for a commonplace man, a mere rattle, emitting a vague and senseless noise. When I ask you if you are consoled, I speak to you as a man for whom the human heart has no secrets. Well, Morrel, let us both examine the depths of your heart. Do you still feel the same feverish impatience of grief which made you start like a wounded lion? Have you still that devouring thirst which can only be appeased in the grave? Are you still actuated by the regret which drags the living to the pursuit of death; or are you only suffering from the prostration of fatigue and the weariness of hope deferred? Has the loss of memory rendered it impossible for you to weep? Oh, my dear friend, if this be the case,—if you can no longer weep, if your frozen heart be dead, if you put all your trust in God, then, Maximilian, you are consoled—do not complain.”\n\n“Count,” said Morrel, in a firm and at the same time soft voice, “listen to me, as to a man whose thoughts are raised to heaven, though he remains on earth; I come to die in the arms of a friend. Certainly, there are people whom I love. I love my sister Julie,—I love her husband Emmanuel; but I require a strong mind to smile on my last moments. My sister would be bathed in tears and fainting; I could not bear to see her suffer. Emmanuel would tear the weapon from my hand, and alarm the house with his cries. You, count, who are more than mortal, will, I am sure, lead me to death by a pleasant path, will you not?”\n50267m\n\n“My friend,” said the count, “I have still one doubt,—are you weak enough to pride yourself upon your sufferings?”\n\n“No, indeed,—I am calm,” said Morrel, giving his hand to the count; “my pulse does not beat slower or faster than usual. No, I feel that I have reached the goal, and I will go no farther. You told me to wait and hope; do you know what you did, unfortunate adviser? I waited a month, or rather I suffered for a month! I did hope (man is a poor wretched creature), I did hope. What I cannot tell,—something wonderful, an absurdity, a miracle,—of what nature he alone can tell who has mingled with our reason that folly we call hope. Yes, I did wait—yes, I did hope, count, and during this quarter of an hour we have been talking together, you have unconsciously wounded, tortured my heart, for every word you have uttered proved that there was no hope for me. Oh, count, I shall sleep calmly, deliciously in the arms of death.”\n\nMorrel uttered these words with an energy which made the count shudder.\n\n“My friend,” continued Morrel, “you named the fifth of October as the end of the period of waiting,—today is the fifth of October,” he took out his watch, “it is now nine o’clock,—I have yet three hours to live.”\n\n“Be it so,” said the count, “come.” Morrel mechanically followed the count, and they had entered the grotto before he perceived it. He felt a carpet under his feet, a door opened, perfumes surrounded him, and a brilliant light dazzled his eyes. Morrel hesitated to advance; he dreaded the enervating effect of all that he saw. Monte Cristo drew him in gently.\n\n“Why should we not spend the last three hours remaining to us of life, like those ancient Romans, who when condemned by Nero, their emperor and heir, sat down at a table covered with flowers, and gently glided into death, amid the perfume of heliotropes and roses?”\n\nMorrel smiled. “As you please,” he said; “death is always death,—that is forgetfulness, repose, exclusion from life, and therefore from grief.”\n\nHe sat down, and Monte Cristo placed himself opposite to him. They were in the marvellous dining-room before described, where the statues had baskets on their heads always filled with fruits and flowers. Morrel had looked carelessly around, and had probably noticed nothing.\n\n“Let us talk like men,” he said, looking at the count.\n\n“Go on!”\n\n“Count,” said Morrel, “you are the epitome of all human knowledge, and you seem like a being descended from a wiser and more advanced world than ours.”\n\n“There is something true in what you say,” said the count, with that smile which made him so handsome; “I have descended from a planet called grief.”\n\n50269m\n“I believe all you tell me without questioning its meaning; for instance, you told me to live, and I did live; you told me to hope, and I almost did so. I am almost inclined to ask you, as though you had experienced death, ‘is it painful to die?’”\n\nMonte Cristo looked upon Morrel with indescribable tenderness. “Yes,” he said, “yes, doubtless it is painful, if you violently break the outer covering which obstinately begs for life. If you plunge a dagger into your flesh, if you insinuate a bullet into your brain, which the least shock disorders,—then certainly, you will suffer pain, and you will repent quitting a life for a repose you have bought at so dear a price.”\n\n“Yes; I know that there is a secret of luxury and pain in death, as well as in life; the only thing is to understand it.”\n\n“You have spoken truly, Maximilian; according to the care we bestow upon it, death is either a friend who rocks us gently as a nurse, or an enemy who violently drags the soul from the body. Some day, when the world is much older, and when mankind will be masters of all the destructive powers in nature, to serve for the general good of humanity; when mankind, as you were just saying, have discovered the secrets of death, then that death will become as sweet and voluptuous as a slumber in the arms of your beloved.”\n\n“And if you wished to die, you would choose this death, count?”\n“Yes.”\n\nMorrel extended his hand. “Now I understand,” he said, “why you had me brought here to this desolate spot, in the midst of the ocean, to this subterranean palace; it was because you loved me, was it not, count? It was because you loved me well enough to give me one of those sweet means of death of which we were speaking; a death without agony, a death which allows me to fade away while pronouncing Valentine’s name and pressing your hand.”\n\n“Yes, you have guessed rightly, Morrel,” said the count, “that is what I intended.”\n\n“Thanks; the idea that tomorrow I shall no longer suffer, is sweet to my heart.”\n\n“Do you then regret nothing?”\n\n“No,” replied Morrel.\n\n“Not even me?” asked the count with deep emotion. Morrel’s clear eye was for the moment clouded, then it shone with unusual lustre, and a large tear rolled down his cheek.\n\n“What,” said the count, “do you still regret anything in the world, and yet die?”\n\n“Oh, I entreat you,” exclaimed Morrel in a low voice, “do not speak another word, count; do not prolong my punishment.”\n\nThe count fancied that he was yielding, and this belief revived the horrible doubt that had overwhelmed him at the Château d’If.\n\n“I am endeavoring,” he thought, “to make this man happy; I look upon this restitution as a weight thrown into the scale to balance the evil I have wrought. Now, supposing I am deceived, supposing this man has not been unhappy enough to merit happiness. Alas, what would become of me who can only atone for evil by doing good?”\n\n50271m\nThen he said aloud: “Listen, Morrel, I see your grief is great, but still you do not like to risk your soul.” Morrel smiled sadly.\n\n“Count,” he said, “I swear to you my soul is no longer my own.”\n\n“Maximilian, you know I have no relation in the world. I have accustomed myself to regard you as my son: well, then, to save my son, I will sacrifice my life, nay, even my fortune.”\n\n“What do you mean?”\n\n“I mean, that you wish to quit life because you do not understand all the enjoyments which are the fruits of a large fortune. Morrel, I possess nearly a hundred millions and I give them to you; with such a fortune you can attain every wish. Are you ambitious? Every career is open to you. Overturn the world, change its character, yield to mad ideas, be even criminal—but live.”\n\n“Count, I have your word,” said Morrel coldly; then taking out his watch, he added, “It is half-past eleven.”\n\n“Morrel, can you intend it in my house, under my very eyes?”\n\n“Then let me go,” said Maximilian, “or I shall think you did not love me for my own sake, but for yours;” and he arose.\n\n“It is well,” said Monte Cristo whose countenance brightened at these words; “you wish it—you are inflexible. Yes, as you said, you are indeed wretched and a miracle alone can cure you. Sit down, Morrel, and wait.”\n\nMorrel obeyed; the count arose, and unlocking a closet with a key suspended from his gold chain, took from it a little silver casket, beautifully carved and chased, the corners of which represented four bending figures, similar to the Caryatides, the forms of women, symbols of the angels aspiring to heaven.\n\nHe placed the casket on the table; then opening it took out a little golden box, the top of which flew open when touched by a secret spring. This box contained an unctuous substance partly solid, of which it was impossible to discover the color, owing to the reflection of the polished gold, sapphires, rubies, emeralds, which ornamented the box. It was a mixed mass of blue, red, and gold.\n\nThe count took out a small quantity of this with a gilt spoon, and offered it to Morrel, fixing a long steadfast glance upon him. It was then observable that the substance was greenish.\n\n“This is what you asked for,” he said, “and what I promised to give you.”\n\n“I thank you from the depths of my heart,” said the young man, taking the spoon from the hands of Monte Cristo. The count took another spoon, and again dipped it into the golden box. “What are you going to do, my friend?” asked Morrel, arresting his hand.\n\n“Well, the fact is, Morrel, I was thinking that I too am weary of life, and since an opportunity presents itself——”\n\n“Stay!” said the young man. “You who love, and are beloved; you, who have faith and hope,—oh, do not follow my example. In your case it would be a crime. Adieu, my noble and generous friend, adieu; I will go and tell Valentine what you have done for me.”\n\nAnd slowly, though without any hesitation, only waiting to press the count’s hand fervently, he swallowed the mysterious substance offered by Monte Cristo. Then they were both silent. Ali, mute and attentive, brought the pipes and coffee, and disappeared. By degrees, the light of the lamps gradually faded in the hands of the marble statues which held them, and the perfumes appeared less powerful to Morrel. Seated opposite to him, Monte Cristo watched him in the shadow, and Morrel saw nothing but the bright eyes of the count. An overpowering sadness took possession of the young man, his hands relaxed their hold, the objects in the room gradually lost their form and color, and his disturbed vision seemed to perceive doors and curtains open in the wall.\n\n50273m\n“Friend,” he cried, “I feel that I am dying; thanks!”\n\nHe made a last effort to extend his hand, but it fell powerless beside him. Then it appeared to him that Monte Cristo smiled, not with the strange and fearful expression which had sometimes revealed to him the secrets of his heart, but with the benevolent kindness of a father for a child. At the same time the count appeared to increase in stature, his form, nearly double its usual height, stood out in relief against the red tapestry, his black hair was thrown back, and he stood in the attitude of an avenging angel. Morrel, overpowered, turned around in the armchair; a delicious torpor permeated every vein. A change of ideas presented themselves to his brain, like a new design on the kaleidoscope. Enervated, prostrate, and breathless, he became unconscious of outward objects; he seemed to be entering that vague delirium preceding death. He wished once again to press the count’s hand, but his own was immovable. He wished to articulate a last farewell, but his tongue lay motionless and heavy in his throat, like a stone at the mouth of a sepulchre. Involuntarily his languid eyes closed, and still through his eyelashes a well-known form seemed to move amid the obscurity with which he thought himself enveloped.\n\nThe count had just opened a door. Immediately a brilliant light from the next room, or rather from the palace adjoining, shone upon the room in which he was gently gliding into his last sleep. Then he saw a woman of marvellous beauty appear on the threshold of the door separating the two rooms. Pale, and sweetly smiling, she looked like an angel of mercy conjuring the angel of vengeance.\n\n“Is it heaven that opens before me?” thought the dying man; “that angel resembles the one I have lost.”\n\nMonte Cristo pointed out Morrel to the young woman, who advanced towards him with clasped hands and a smile upon her lips.\n\n“Valentine, Valentine!” he mentally ejaculated; but his lips uttered no sound, and as though all his strength were centred in that internal emotion, he sighed and closed his eyes. Valentine rushed towards him; his lips again moved.\n\n“He is calling you,” said the count; “he to whom you have confided your destiny—he from whom death would have separated you, calls you to him. Happily, I vanquished death. Henceforth, Valentine, you will never again be separated on earth, since he has rushed into death to find you. Without me, you would both have died. May God accept my atonement in the preservation of these two existences!”\n\nValentine seized the count’s hand, and in her irresistible impulse of joy carried it to her lips.\n\n50275m\n“Oh, thank me again!” said the count; “tell me till you are weary, that I have restored you to happiness; you do not know how much I require this assurance.”\n\n“Oh, yes, yes, I thank you with all my heart,” said Valentine; “and if you doubt the sincerity of my gratitude, oh, then, ask Haydée! ask my beloved sister Haydée, who ever since our departure from France, has caused me to wait patiently for this happy day, while talking to me of you.”\n\n“You then love Haydée?” asked Monte Cristo with an emotion he in vain endeavored to dissimulate.\n\n“Oh, yes, with all my soul.”\n\n“Well, then, listen, Valentine,” said the count; “I have a favor to ask of you.”\n\n“Of me? Oh, am I happy enough for that?”\n\n“Yes; you have called Haydée your sister,—let her become so indeed, Valentine; render her all the gratitude you fancy that you owe to me; protect her, for” (the count’s voice was thick with emotion) “henceforth she will be alone in the world.”\n\n“Alone in the world!” repeated a voice behind the count, “and why?”\n\nMonte Cristo turned around; Haydée was standing pale, motionless, looking at the count with an expression of fearful amazement.\n\n“Because tomorrow, Haydée, you will be free; you will then assume your proper position in society, for I will not allow my destiny to overshadow yours. Daughter of a prince, I restore to you the riches and name of your father.”\n\nHaydée became pale, and lifting her transparent hands to heaven, exclaimed in a voice stifled with tears, “Then you leave me, my lord?”\n\n“Haydée, Haydée, you are young and beautiful; forget even my name, and be happy.”\n\n“It is well,” said Haydée; “your order shall be executed, my lord; I will forget even your name, and be happy.” And she stepped back to retire.\n\n“Oh, heavens,” exclaimed Valentine, who was supporting the head of Morrel on her shoulder, “do you not see how pale she is? Do you not see how she suffers?”\n\nHaydée answered with a heartrending expression,\n\n“Why should he understand this, my sister? He is my master, and I am his slave; he has the right to notice nothing.”\n\nThe count shuddered at the tones of a voice which penetrated the inmost recesses of his heart; his eyes met those of the young girl and he could not bear their brilliancy.\n\n“Oh, heavens,” exclaimed Monte Cristo, “can my suspicions be correct? Haydée, would it please you not to leave me?”\n\n“I am young,” gently replied Haydée; “I love the life you have made so sweet to me, and I should be sorry to die.”\n\n“You mean, then, that if I leave you, Haydée——”\n\n“I should die; yes, my lord.”\n\n“Do you then love me?”\n\n“Oh, Valentine, he asks if I love him. Valentine, tell him if you love Maximilian.”\n\nThe count felt his heart dilate and throb; he opened his arms, and Haydée, uttering a cry, sprang into them.\n\n“Oh, yes,” she cried, “I do love you! I love you as one loves a father, brother, husband! I love you as my life, for you are the best, the noblest of created beings!”\n50277m\n\n“Let it be, then, as you wish, sweet angel; God has sustained me in my struggle with my enemies, and has given me this reward; he will not let me end my triumph in suffering; I wished to punish myself, but he has pardoned me. Love me then, Haydée! Who knows? perhaps your love will make me forget all that I do not wish to remember.”\n\n“What do you mean, my lord?”\n\n“I mean that one word from you has enlightened me more than twenty years of slow experience; I have but you in the world, Haydée; through you I again take hold on life, through you I shall suffer, through you rejoice.”\n\n“Do you hear him, Valentine?” exclaimed Haydée; “he says that through me he will suffer—through _me_, who would yield my life for his.”\n\nThe count withdrew for a moment. “Have I discovered the truth?” he said; “but whether it be for recompense or punishment, I accept my fate. Come, Haydée, come!” and throwing his arm around the young girl’s waist, he pressed the hand of Valentine, and disappeared.\n\n50279m\nAn hour had nearly passed, during which Valentine, breathless and motionless, watched steadfastly over Morrel. At length she felt his heart beat, a faint breath played upon his lips, a slight shudder, announcing the return of life, passed through the young man’s frame. At length his eyes opened, but they were at first fixed and expressionless; then sight returned, and with it feeling and grief.\n\n“Oh,” he cried, in an accent of despair, “the count has deceived me; I am yet living;” and extending his hand towards the table, he seized a knife.\n\n“Dearest,” exclaimed Valentine, with her adorable smile, “awake, and look at me!” Morrel uttered a loud exclamation, and frantic, doubtful, dazzled, as though by a celestial vision, he fell upon his knees.\n\nThe next morning at daybreak, Valentine and Morrel were walking arm-in-arm on the seashore, Valentine relating how Monte Cristo had appeared in her room, explained everything, revealed the crime, and, finally, how he had saved her life by enabling her to simulate death.\n\nThey had found the door of the grotto opened, and gone forth; on the azure dome of heaven still glittered a few remaining stars.\n\nMorrel soon perceived a man standing among the rocks, apparently awaiting a sign from them to advance, and pointed him out to Valentine.\n\n“Ah, it is Jacopo,” she said, “the captain of the yacht;” and she beckoned him towards them.\n\n“Do you wish to speak to us?” asked Morrel.\n\n“I have a letter to give you from the count.”\n\n“From the count!” murmured the two young people.\n\n“Yes; read it.”\n50281m\n\nMorrel opened the letter, and read:\n\n“My Dear Maximilian,\n\n“There is a felucca for you at anchor. Jacopo will carry you to Leghorn, where Monsieur Noirtier awaits his granddaughter, whom he wishes to bless before you lead her to the altar. All that is in this grotto, my friend, my house in the Champs-Élysées, and my château at Tréport, are the marriage gifts bestowed by Edmond Dantès upon the son of his old master, Morrel. Mademoiselle de Villefort will share them with you; for I entreat her to give to the poor the immense fortune reverting to her from her father, now a madman, and her brother who died last September with his mother. Tell the angel who will watch over your future destiny, Morrel, to pray sometimes for a man, who, like Satan, thought himself for an instant equal to God, but who now acknowledges with Christian humility that God alone possesses supreme power and infinite wisdom. Perhaps those prayers may soften the remorse he feels in his heart. As for you, Morrel, this is the secret of my conduct towards you. There is neither happiness nor misery in the world; there is only the comparison of one state with another, nothing more. He who has felt the deepest grief is best able to experience supreme happiness. We must have felt what it is to die, Morrel, that we may appreciate the enjoyments of living.\n\n“Live, then, and be happy, beloved children of my heart, and never forget that until the day when God shall deign to reveal the future to man, all human wisdom is summed up in these two words,—‘_Wait and hope_.’—Your friend,\n\n“Edmond Dantès, _Count of Monte Cristo_.”\n\n50282m\nDuring the perusal of this letter, which informed Valentine for the first time of the madness of her father and the death of her brother, she became pale, a heavy sigh escaped from her bosom, and tears, not the less painful because they were silent, ran down her cheeks; her happiness cost her very dear.\n\nMorrel looked around uneasily.\n\n“But,” he said, “the count’s generosity is too overwhelming; Valentine will be satisfied with my humble fortune. Where is the count, friend? Lead me to him.”\n\nJacopo pointed towards the horizon.\n\n“What do you mean?” asked Valentine. “Where is the count?—where is Haydée?”\n\n“Look!” said Jacopo.\n\nThe eyes of both were fixed upon the spot indicated by the sailor, and on the blue line separating the sky from the Mediterranean Sea, they perceived a large white sail.\n\n“Gone,” said Morrel; “gone!—adieu, my friend—adieu, my father!”\n\n“Gone,” murmured Valentine; “adieu, my sweet Haydée—adieu, my sister!”\n\n“Who can say whether we shall ever see them again?” said Morrel with tearful eyes.\n\n“Darling,” replied Valentine, “has not the count just told us that all human wisdom is summed up in two words:\n\n“‘_Wait and hope_ (Fac et spera)!’”\n\n[1] “The wicked are great drinkers of water; As the flood proved once for all.”\n\n[2] $2,600,000 in 1894.\n\n[3] Knocked on the head.\n\n[4] Beheaded.\n\n[5] Scott, of course: “The son of an ill-fated sire, and the father of a yet more unfortunate family, bore in his looks that cast of inauspicious melancholy by which the physiognomists of that time pretended to distinguish those who were predestined to a violent and unhappy death.”—The Abbot, ch. xxii.\n\n[6] Guillotine.\n\n[7] Dr. Guillotin got the idea of his famous machine from witnessing an execution in Italy.\n\n[8] Brucea ferruginea.\n\n[9] ‘Money and sanctity, Each in a moiety.’\n\n[10] Elisabeth de Rossan, Marquise de Ganges, was one of the famous women of the court of Louis XIV. where she was known as “La Belle Provençale.” She was the widow of the Marquis de Castellane when she married de Ganges, and having the misfortune to excite the enmity of her new brothers-in-law, was forced by them to take poison; and they finished her off with pistol and dagger.—Ed.\n\n[11] Magistrate and orator of great eloquence—chancellor of France under Louis XV.\n\n[12] Jacques-Louis David, a famous French painter (1748-1825).\n\n[13] Ali Pasha, “The Lion,” was born at Tepelini, an Albanian village at the foot of the Klissoura Mountains, in 1741. By diplomacy and success in arms he became almost supreme ruler of Albania, Epirus, and adjacent territory. Having aroused the enmity of the Sultan, he was proscribed and put to death by treachery in 1822, at the age of eighty.—Ed.\n\n[14] Greek militiamen in the war for independence.—Ed.\n\n[15] A Turkish pasha in command of the troops of a province.—Ed.\n\n[16] The god of fruitfulness in Grecian mythology. In Crete he was supposed to be slain in winter with the decay of vegetation and to revive in the spring. Haydée’s learned reference is to the behavior of an actor in the Dionysian festivals.—Ed.\n\n[17] The Genoese conspirator.\n\n[18] Lake Maggiore.\n\n[19] In the old Greek legend the Atreidae, or children of Atreus, were doomed to punishment because of the abominable crime of their father. The _Agamemnon_ of Aeschylus is based on this legend.\n\n[20] The performance of the civil marriage.\n\n[21] In Molière’s comedy, _Le Misanthrope_.\n\n[22] Literally, “the basket,” because wedding gifts were originally brought in such a receptacle.\n\n[23] Germain Pillon was a famous French sculptor (1535-1598). His best known work is “The Three Graces,” now in the Louvre.\n\n[24] Frédérick Lemaître—French actor (1800-1876). Robert Macaire is the hero of two favorite melodramas—“Chien de Montargis” and “Chien d’Aubry”—and the name is applied to bold criminals as a term of derision.\n\n[25] The Spahis are French cavalry reserved for service in Africa.\n\n[26] _Savate_: an old shoe.\n\n[27] Guilbert de Pixérécourt, French dramatist (1773-1844).\n\n[28] Gaspard Puget, the sculptor-architect, was born at Marseilles in 1615.\n\n[29] The Carolina—not Virginia—jessamine, _gelsemium sempervirens_ (properly speaking not a jessamine at all) has yellow blossoms. The reference is no doubt to the _Wistaria frutescens_.—Ed.\n\n[30] The miser in Molière’s comedy of _L’Avare_.—Ed.";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-1184"]="1815년 2월 24일, 노트르담드라가르드 망루의 감시병이 스미르나와 트리에스테, 나폴리를 거쳐 들어오는 삼범선 파라옹호를 발견하고 신호를 보냈다.\n\n여느 때처럼 곧바로 도선사가 작은 배를 띄웠고, 이프성을 돌아 모르지우곶과 리옹섬 사이에서 배에 올랐다.\n\n# Chapter 1. Marseilles—The Arrival\n\n언제나 그렇듯 곧 생장 요새의 성벽이 구경꾼들로 뒤덮였다. 마르세유에서 배 한 척이 입항하는 것은 늘 하나의 사건이었다. 더구나 그 배가 파라옹호처럼 오래된 포세 부두에서 건조되고 의장을 갖추고 짐을 실은 데다, 그 도시 선주의 소유라면 말할 것도 없었다.\n\n배는 점점 다가와 칼라사레뉴섬과 자로스섬 사이에 화산의 충격이 만들어 놓은 해협을 무사히 통과했고, 포메그섬을 돌아 윗돛과 삼각돛, 후장 종범을 펼친 채 항구로 다가왔다. 그러나 그 속도가 너무 느리고 침울해서, 불길한 일을 미리 감지하는 본능을 지닌 구경꾼들은 배에 무슨 변고가 생긴 게 아니냐고 서로 물었다. 그러나 항해에 밝은 사람들은 사고가 있었다 해도 배 자체에 일어난 일은 아니라는 것을 한눈에 알아보았다. 배는 능숙하게 다루어진 흔적이 역력했다. 닻은 내릴 채비가 되어 있었고, 제2사장 받침줄은 이미 풀려 있었으며, 도선사 곁에는 한 젊은이가 서서 파라옹호를 내항의 좁은 입구로 이끌고 있었다. 그는 민첩하고 경계 어린 눈으로 배의 모든 움직임을 살피며 도선사의 지시를 하나하나 복창했다.\n\n구경꾼들 사이에 감도는 막연한 불안이 무리 가운데 한 사람을 어찌나 자극했던지, 그는 배가 항구에 들어오기를 기다리지 못하고 작은 거룻배에 뛰어올라 파라옹호 곁으로 저어 가게 했다. 그는 배가 라레제르브 정박지로 돌아드는 지점에서 배에 닿았다.\n\n배 위의 젊은이는 그 사람이 다가오는 것을 보자 도선사 곁의 자리를 떠나 모자를 손에 쥐고 뱃전 난간 너머로 몸을 기울였다.\n\n그는 열여덟이나 스무 살쯤 된, 잘생기고 키가 크며 호리호리한 젊은이였다. 까만 눈에 머리카락은 까마귀 날개처럼 검었다. 그 모습 전체에서 요람에서부터 위험과 맞서 온 사람 특유의 침착함과 결단력이 묻어났다.\n\n\"아, 자넨가, 당테스?\" 거룻배에 탄 사람이 외쳤다. \"무슨 일인가? 어쩌자고 배 위에서 그렇게 침울한 얼굴을 하고 있나?\"\n\n\"큰 변고가 있었습니다, 모렐 씨.\" 젊은이가 대답했다. \"큰 변고가요, 특히 저에게는요! 치비타베키아 앞바다에서 우리의 용감하신 르클레르 선장님을 잃었습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 화물은?\" 선주가 다급히 물었다.\n\n\"모두 무사합니다, 모렐 씨. 그 점은 만족하실 겁니다. 하지만 가엾은 르클레르 선장님은……\"\n\n\"그분에게 무슨 일이 있었나?\" 선주가 적잖이 체념한 듯한 기색으로 물었다. \"그 훌륭하신 선장에게 무슨 일이 있었어?\"\n\n\"돌아가셨습니다.\"\n\n\"바다에 빠지셨나?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 선주님. 뇌염으로 끔찍한 고통 속에 돌아가셨습니다.\" 그러고는 선원들 쪽으로 돌아서서 말했다. \"거기, 손들 빌려서 돛을 거두게!\"\n\n모두가 명령에 따랐다. 여덟이나 열 명쯤 되는 선원들이 일제히 제자리로 달려갔다. 후장 종범의 졸인줄과 끌어내는 줄, 윗돛 아딧줄과 올림줄, 삼각돛 내림줄, 윗돛 거두는 줄과 묶는 줄로 흩어졌다. 젊은 선원은 자신의 명령이 신속하고 정확하게 이행되는지 한번 살피고는 다시 선주에게로 돌아섰다.\n\n\"그래, 어쩌다 그런 변고가 생겼나?\" 선주가 끊겼던 대화를 이어 물었다.\n\n\"아아, 선주님, 정말 뜻밖의 일이었습니다. 르클레르 선장님은 항만장과 한참 이야기를 나누신 뒤 몹시 심란한 마음으로 나폴리를 떠나셨습니다. 스물네 시간 만에 열병에 걸리셨고, 사흘 뒤에 돌아가셨지요. 우리는 관례대로 수장식을 치렀습니다. 선장님은 엘질리오섬 앞바다에서 머리맡과 발치에 36파운드 포탄을 매단 채 해먹에 꿰매어져 편히 잠드셨습니다. 미망인께는 선장님의 검과 명예 십자훈장을 가져다 드릴 겁니다. 정말이지,\" 젊은이가 서글픈 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다. \"십 년 동안 영국군과 싸우신 분이 결국 다른 사람들처럼 침상에서 돌아가시다니, 그럴 만한 가치가 있었던 일이었습니다.\"\n\n\"이보게, 에드몽,\" 선주가 대답했다. 그는 시간이 갈수록 마음이 놓이는 듯했다. \"우리 모두 죽게 마련이고, 늙은이는 젊은이에게 자리를 내주어야 하는 법이지. 안 그러면 승진이라는 게 어디 있겠나. 게다가 자네가 화물은 모두 무사하다고 장담하니……\"\n\n\"모두 멀쩡합니다, 모렐 씨. 제 말을 믿으셔도 됩니다. 이번 항해의 수익으로 2만 5천 프랑은 받으셔야지, 그보다 적게 받으시면 안 됩니다.\"\n\n그때 마침 라운드 타워를 지나는 참이라, 젊은이가 외쳤다. \"거기, 윗돛과 삼각돛 내릴 준비! 후장 종범 거두어 올려!\"\n\n명령은 군함에서나 볼 법한 신속함으로 이행되었다.\n\n\"풀어라, 그리고 묶어 올려!\" 이 마지막 명령에 모든 돛이 내려졌고, 배는 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 천천히 앞으로 나아갔다.\n\n\"자, 이제 올라오시지요, 모렐 씨.\" 선주의 조바심을 알아챈 당테스가 말했다. \"마침 회계 담당인 당글라르 씨가 선실에서 나오는군요. 그가 자세한 사정을 다 알려 드릴 겁니다. 저는 닻을 내리는 일을 마저 살피고, 배에 조의를 표할 채비를 해야 합니다.\"\n\n선주는 두 번 권할 것도 없이, 당테스가 던져 준 밧줄을 붙잡고 선원에게도 뒤지지 않을 민첩함으로 뱃전을 타고 올라왔다. 그동안 젊은이는 제 일을 보러 가며 대화를 당글라르에게 넘겼고, 당글라르가 선주에게 다가왔다. 그는 스물대여섯 살가량의 사내로, 호감이 가지 않는 인상에, 윗사람에게는 굽실거리고 아랫사람에게는 거만했다. 게다가 선상에서 회계 책임자라는 직책 자체가 늘 선원들의 미움을 사는 자리였으니, 에드몽 당테스가 선원들에게 사랑받는 만큼이나 그는 미움을 받았다.\n\n\"그래, 모렐 씨,\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"저희에게 닥친 변고에 대해 들으셨습니까?\"\n\n\"그래, 그래. 가엾은 르클레르 선장! 용감하고 정직한 사람이었지.\"\n\n\"그리고 일류 선원이기도 했지요. 오랫동안 명예롭게 복무한 분이라, 모렐 부자 상회처럼 중요한 상회의 이익을 맡길 만한 분이었습니다.\" 당글라르가 대답했다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 선주가 자기 배의 닻을 내리는 모습을 지켜보는 당테스 쪽을 흘끗 보며 대답했다. \"내가 보기에는, 당글라르, 자네 말처럼 그렇게 나이가 많아야만 일을 제대로 아는 건 아닌 것 같네. 우리 에드몽만 봐도 일을 훤히 꿰뚫고 있고, 누구한테 가르침을 받을 것도 없어 보이니 말일세.\"\n\n\"네,\" 당글라르가 에드몽에게 증오로 번득이는 눈길을 던지며 말했다. \"네, 그는 젊지요. 젊은이란 늘 자신만만한 법이니까요. 선장님이 숨을 거두기가 무섭게 누구와도 상의하지 않고 지휘권을 잡더니, 마르세유로 곧장 오지 않고 엘바섬에서 하루 반나절이나 허비하게 만들었습니다.\"\n\n\"배의 지휘권을 잡은 것은,\" 모렐이 대답했다. \"일등 항해사로서 마땅한 의무였네. 엘바섬 앞바다에서 하루 반나절을 허비한 것은, 배에 수리가 필요한 게 아니었다면 잘못한 일이지.\"\n\n\"배는 저만큼이나, 또 모렐 씨도 그러시길 바랍니다만 그만큼이나 멀쩡한 상태였습니다. 그 하루 반나절은 순전히 변덕으로, 그저 뭍에 내려 보고 싶다는 즐거움 때문에 허비한 것이었지요. 다른 이유는 없습니다.\"\n\n\"당테스,\" 선주가 젊은이 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다. \"이리로 좀 와 보게!\"\n\n\"곧 가겠습니다, 선주님.\" 당테스가 대답했다. \"잠시면 됩니다.\" 그러고는 선원들에게 외쳤다. \"놓아라!\"\n\n닻이 곧바로 떨어지면서 사슬이 뱃전 구멍을 통해 요란하게 풀려 나갔다. 당테스는 도선사가 곁에 있는데도 이 작업이 끝날 때까지 제자리를 지켰고, 그런 뒤에 덧붙였다. \"깃발을 반기로 내리고, 활대를 가지런히 하라!\"\n\n\"보십시오,\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"정말이지, 벌써 자기가 선장인 줄 안다니까요.\"\n\n\"실제로도 선장이나 다름없지.\" 선주가 말했다.\n\n\"모렐 씨와 동업자분의 서명만 빠졌지요.\"\n\n\"그가 그걸 받지 못할 이유가 있나?\" 선주가 물었다. \"젊긴 하지만, 내가 보기엔 더할 나위 없는 선원이고 경험도 충분해 보이는데.\"\n\n당글라르의 이마에 그늘이 스쳤다.\n\n\"실례합니다, 모렐 씨.\" 당테스가 다가오며 말했다. \"이제 배가 닻을 내리고 정박했으니, 분부만 하십시오. 저를 부르셨지요?\"\n\n당글라르가 한두 걸음 물러섰다. \"왜 엘바섬에 들렀는지 물어보고 싶었네.\"\n\n\"저도 모릅니다, 선주님. 르클레르 선장님의 마지막 분부를 따른 것뿐이었습니다. 선장님이 돌아가시면서 베르트랑 원수께 전할 꾸러미를 제게 맡기셨거든요.\"\n\n\"그래, 원수를 만났나, 에드몽?\"\n\n\"누구를요?\"\n\n\"원수 말일세.\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n모렐이 주위를 둘러보더니 당테스를 한쪽으로 끌어당기고는 불쑥 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 황제께서는 어떠시던가?\"\n\n\"제가 뵌 바로는, 아주 건강하셨습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 자네가 황제를 뵈었단 말인가?\"\n\n\"제가 거기 있는 동안 황제께서 원수의 방으로 들어오셨습니다.\"\n\n\"그래서 말을 나눴나?\"\n\n\"실은 황제께서 먼저 제게 말을 거셨습니다, 선주님.\" 당테스가 미소를 띠며 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 자네에게 뭐라고 하시던가?\"\n\n\"배에 관해 이것저것 물으셨습니다. 마르세유를 언제 떠났는지, 어떤 항로를 택했는지, 화물은 무엇인지 말입니다. 배에 짐이 실리지 않았고 제가 선주였더라면, 황제께서 그 배를 사셨을지도 모릅니다. 하지만 저는 일등 항해사일 뿐이고 배는 모렐 부자 상회의 소유라고 말씀드렸지요. '아, 그렇군.' 하시며 '나도 그들을 안다. 모렐 가문은 대대로 선주였지. 내가 발랑스에 주둔하던 시절, 같은 연대에서 복무한 모렐이라는 자가 있었어.' 하셨습니다.\"\n\n\"이런! 그게 사실일세!\" 선주가 크게 기뻐하며 외쳤다. \"그게 바로 내 삼촌 폴리카르 모렐이야. 나중에 대위가 되셨지. 당테스, 황제께서 삼촌을 기억하시더라고 꼭 전해 드리게. 그러면 그 늙은 군인의 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁 맺히는 걸 보게 될 걸세. 자, 자,\" 그가 에드몽의 어깨를 다정하게 두드리며 말을 이었다. \"당테스, 르클레르 선장님의 분부를 따라 엘바섬에 들른 것은 아주 잘한 일이야. 다만 자네가 원수께 꾸러미를 전하고 황제와 이야기를 나눴다는 사실이 알려지면 곤란해질 수도 있겠네.\"\n\n\"그게 어찌 저를 곤란하게 하겠습니까, 선주님?\" 당테스가 물었다. \"저는 제가 무엇을 전하는지도 몰랐고, 황제께서도 처음 보는 사람에게 으레 던질 법한 질문을 하셨을 뿐인걸요. 그런데 실례합니다, 검역관과 세관 검사관들이 배 곁으로 다가오는군요.\" 젊은이는 현문 쪽으로 걸어갔다. 그가 자리를 뜨자 당글라르가 다가와 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 그가 포르토페라요에 내린 까닭을 흡족하게 설명하던가요?\"\n\n\"그래, 아주 흡족했네, 당글라르.\"\n\n\"음, 그렇다면 다행이지요.\" 회계 담당이 말했다. \"동료가 제 의무를 다하지 않았다고 생각하는 건 유쾌한 일이 아니니까요.\"\n\n\"당테스는 제 의무를 다했네.\" 선주가 대답했다. \"그것도 새삼스러운 말이 아니지. 이번 지체는 르클레르 선장이 명령한 일이었으니까.\"\n\n\"르클레르 선장 이야기가 나왔으니 말인데, 당테스가 선장한테서 받은 편지를 전해 드리지 않던가요?\"\n\n\"내게? 아니, 편지가 있었나?\"\n\n\"제 생각에는, 꾸러미 말고도 르클레르 선장이 그에게 편지 한 통을 맡긴 듯합니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 꾸러미를 말하는 건가, 당글라르?\"\n\n\"아, 당테스가 포르토페라요에 두고 온 그 꾸러미 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그가 포르토페라요에 두고 올 꾸러미가 있었다는 걸 자네가 어떻게 아나?\"\n\n당글라르의 얼굴이 새빨개졌다.\n\n\"선장 선실 문 가까이를 지나가는데 문이 반쯤 열려 있었고, 선장이 당테스에게 꾸러미와 편지를 건네는 걸 보았습니다.\"\n\n\"그가 내게는 그 얘기를 하지 않았네.\" 선주가 대답했다. \"하지만 편지가 있다면 내게 줄 걸세.\"\n\n당글라르가 잠시 생각에 잠겼다. \"그렇다면 모렐 씨, 부탁드립니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"그 일에 대해서는 당테스에게 한마디도 하지 말아 주십시오. 제가 잘못 봤을 수도 있으니까요.\"\n\n바로 그때 젊은이가 돌아왔다. 당글라르는 물러갔다.\n\n\"그래, 당테스, 이제 자유로운 몸인가?\" 선주가 물었다.\n\n\"네, 선주님.\"\n\n\"오래 붙잡혀 있지는 않았군.\"\n\n\"네. 세관원들에게는 선하증권 사본을 한 부 주었습니다. 다른 서류들은 도선사 편에 사람을 딸려 보내, 그 사람에게 넘겼고요.\"\n\n\"그럼 이제 여기서 더 할 일은 없나?\"\n\n\"네, 이제 다 정리됐습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 나와 함께 저녁을 들겠나?\"\n\n\"정말이지 양해해 주셔야겠습니다, 모렐 씨. 제일 먼저 아버지를 찾아뵈어야 하니까요. 베풀어 주신 호의가 고맙지 않아서가 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"옳지, 당테스, 옳은 말이야. 자네가 효성 깊은 아들이라는 건 진작부터 알고 있었지.\"\n\n\"그런데,\" 당테스가 조금 망설이며 물었다. \"혹시 제 아버지가 어떻게 지내시는지 아십니까?\"\n\n\"잘 지내시는 듯하네, 에드몽. 요즘 뵙지는 못했지만.\"\n\n\"네, 아버지는 그 작은 방에 틀어박혀 지내시는 걸 좋아하시지요.\"\n\n\"그건 적어도 자네가 없는 동안에도 아쉬운 것 없이 지내셨다는 증거 아니겠나.\"\n\n당테스가 미소 지었다. \"제 아버지는 자존심이 강하셔서요, 선주님. 끼니가 떨어졌다 해도, 하늘에 비는 것 말고는 누구한테도 아무것도 청하지 않으셨을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 첫 방문을 마치고 나면 우리 쪽도 기대해도 되겠지.\"\n\n\"또 양해를 구해야겠습니다, 모렐 씨. 그 첫 방문을 마치면 꼭 가 봐야 할 또 다른 곳이 있어서요.\"\n\n\"그렇지, 당테스, 내가 깜빡했군. 카탈루냐 마을에 자네 아버지 못지않게 애타게 자네를 기다리는 사람이 있었지. 사랑스러운 메르세데스 말일세.\"\n\n당테스가 얼굴을 붉혔다.\n\n\"하하,\" 선주가 말했다. \"조금도 놀랍지 않네. 그 아가씨가 세 번이나 나를 찾아와 파라옹호 소식이 없느냐고 물었거든. 이런! 에드몽, 자네 아주 어여쁜 연인을 두었더군!\"\n\n\"그녀는 제 연인이 아닙니다.\" 젊은 선원이 진지하게 대답했다. \"제 약혼녀입니다.\"\n\n\"때로는 그게 그거지.\" 모렐이 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"저희에게는 그렇지 않습니다, 선주님.\" 당테스가 대답했다.\n\n\"그래, 그래, 에드몽.\" 선주가 말을 이었다. \"내가 자네를 붙잡지 않으마. 자네가 내 일을 워낙 잘 처리해 주었으니, 자네 자신의 일에도 필요한 만큼 시간을 내 줘야 마땅하지. 돈이 좀 필요한가?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 선주님. 받을 봉급이 그대로 남아 있는걸요. 거의 석 달치 급료입니다.\"\n\n\"자네는 알뜰한 사람이야, 에드몽.\"\n\n\"가난한 아버지를 둔 탓이라고 해 두지요, 선주님.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 자네가 얼마나 효성스러운 아들인지 잘 알지. 자, 어서 아버지를 뵈러 가게. 나도 아들이 하나 있는데, 석 달 항해를 마치고 돌아온 아들을 누가 붙잡아 두기라도 한다면 몹시 화가 날 걸세.\"\n\n\"그럼 가 봐도 되겠습니까, 선주님?\"\n\n\"그래, 내게 더 할 말이 없다면 말이야.\"\n\n\"없습니다.\"\n\n\"르클레르 선장이 죽기 전에, 내게 전할 편지를 자네에게 주지 않았나?\"\n\n\"선장님은 글을 쓰실 수 없는 상태였습니다, 선주님. 아, 그러고 보니 며칠 동안 휴가를 청해야겠습니다.\"\n\n\"결혼하려고?\"\n\n\"네, 그게 우선이고, 그다음엔 파리에 다녀오려고요.\"\n\n\"좋아, 필요한 만큼 시간을 쓰게, 당테스. 화물을 다 부리려면 족히 여섯 주는 걸릴 테고, 그 뒤로도 석 달은 지나야 다시 출항 채비가 될 테니까. 다만 석 달 안에는 돌아오게. 파라옹호는,\" 선주가 젊은 선원의 등을 두드리며 덧붙였다. \"선장 없이는 출항할 수 없으니까.\"\n\n\"선장 없이는요!\" 당테스가 외쳤다. 그의 눈이 생기로 반짝였다. \"부디 말씀을 가려 해 주십시오. 제 마음속 가장 은밀한 소망을 건드리고 계시니까요. 정말로 저를 파라옹호의 선장으로 삼으실 생각이십니까?\"\n\n\"내가 단독 선주라면 지금 당장 악수하고 결정된 일로 칠 텐데 말일세, 당테스. 하지만 내겐 동업자가 있고, 자네도 이탈리아 속담을 알 거야. '키 아 콤파뇨 아 파드로네' — '동업자를 둔 사람은 주인을 둔 셈이다'라는 말이지. 그래도 적어도 절반은 된 일이야. 두 표 중 한 표는 이미 자네 것이니까. 나머지 한 표는 내게 맡기게. 최선을 다해 보겠네.\"\n\n\"아, 모렐 씨,\" 젊은 선원이 눈에 눈물을 글썽이며 선주의 손을 부여잡고 외쳤다. \"모렐 씨, 제 아버지와 메르세데스의 이름으로 감사드립니다.\"\n\n\"됐네, 에드몽. 마땅한 사람을 굽어살피는 섭리가 있는 법이지. 어서 아버지께 가 보게. 메르세데스도 만나고, 그런 뒤에 내게 오게나.\"\n\n\"제가 뭍까지 모셔다 드릴까요?\"\n\n\"아니, 고맙지만 됐네. 나는 남아서 당글라르와 함께 장부를 살펴볼 참이네. 이번 항해에서 그자에게 만족했나?\"\n\n\"그건 선주님이 그 질문에 어떤 뜻을 두시느냐에 달렸습니다. 좋은 동료냐고 물으시는 거라면, 아닙니다. 언젠가 사소한 다툼 끝에 제가 어리석게도 몬테크리스토섬에 십 분만 들러 시비를 가리자고 제안한 적이 있는데, 그날 이후로 그자가 저를 좋아한 적이 없는 것 같으니까요. 제가 그런 제안을 한 것이 잘못이었고, 그가 거절한 것이 옳았지요. 회계 책임자로서 어떠냐고 물으시는 거라면, 흠잡을 데가 없다고 봅니다. 그가 제 의무를 수행한 방식에 만족하실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그런데 말해 보게, 당테스. 자네가 파라옹호를 지휘하게 된다면, 당글라르가 남아 있는 게 좋겠나?\"\n\n\"선장이든 항해사든, 모렐 씨, 저는 선주들의 신임을 받는 사람들을 언제나 가장 존중할 것입니다.\"\n\n\"옳지, 옳아, 당테스! 자네가 정말 훌륭한 사람이라는 걸 알겠네. 더는 붙잡지 않으마. 어서 가게. 자네가 얼마나 조바심이 나는지 다 보이는구먼.\"\n\n\"그럼 가 봐도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"가라니까.\"\n\n\"거룻배를 좀 써도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"물론이지.\"\n\n\"그럼 이만 작별 인사를 드립니다, 모렐 씨. 천 번이라도 감사드립니다!\"\n\n\"곧 다시 보세, 에드몽. 행운을 비네.\"\n\n젊은 선원은 거룻배에 뛰어올라 고물 자리에 앉으며, 라카느비에르에 내려 달라고 일렀다. 두 사공이 노에 몸을 실었고, 작은 배는 항구 어귀에서 오를레앙 부두로 이어지는, 두 줄로 늘어선 배들 사이의 좁은 물길을 가득 메운 수천 척의 배 사이를 최대한 빠르게 미끄러져 갔다.\n\n선주는 미소를 띤 채 그의 모습을 눈으로 좇았다. 그가 부두로 뛰어올라 군중 속으로 사라질 때까지였다. 그 유명한 라카느비에르 거리는 아침 다섯 시부터 밤 아홉 시까지 사람들로 들끓었다. 오늘날의 포세 사람들은 이 거리를 어찌나 자랑스러워하는지, 세상 더없이 진지한 얼굴로, 또 말에 묘한 무게를 싣는 그 특유의 억양으로 이렇게 말한다. \"파리에 라카느비에르가 있다면, 파리도 제2의 마르세유가 될 텐데.\" 선주가 몸을 돌리자, 당글라르가 등 뒤에 서서 겉으로는 지시를 기다리는 듯했으나 실은 그 역시 젊은 선원을 지켜보고 있었다. 그러나 에드몽 당테스의 움직임을 좇는 두 사람의 표정에는 커다란 차이가 있었다.\n\n# Chapter 2. Father and Son\n\n당글라르가 증오의 악령과 씨름하며 선주의 귀에 동료를 헐뜯는 사악한 의심을 슬그머니 불어넣으려 애쓰도록 내버려 두고, 우리는 당테스를 따라가 보자. 그는 라카느비에르 거리를 가로질러 노아유 거리로 접어들었고, 메이앙 가로수길 왼편의 작은 집으로 들어가 한 손으로는 난간을 붙들고 다른 손으로는 쿵쾅대는 심장을 억누르며 어두운 계단을 네 층이나 빠르게 올라갔다. 그리고 반쯤 열린 문 앞에서 걸음을 멈추었다. 그 문틈으로 작은 방 안이 한눈에 들여다보였다.\n\n이 방에는 당테스의 아버지가 살고 있었다. 파라옹호가 들어왔다는 소식은 아직 노인에게 닿지 않았고, 그는 의자에 올라선 채 떨리는 손으로 창가 격자 시렁을 타고 오른 한련화와 으아리 덩굴을 손질하며 시간을 보내고 있었다. 그때 문득 누군가의 팔이 자신의 몸을 감싸 안는 것이 느껴졌고, 등 뒤에서 귀에 익은 목소리가 외쳤다. \"아버지, 아버지!\"\n\n노인이 짧은 소리를 지르며 돌아섰다. 그러고는 아들을 보자 창백하게 떨면서 그 품에 안겼다.\n\n\"무슨 일이세요, 아버지? 어디 편찮으세요?\" 젊은이가 몹시 놀라 물었다.\n\n\"아니다, 아니야, 내 사랑하는 에드몽, 내 아들아! 아니다. 다만 네가 올 줄을 몰랐던 데다, 이렇게 갑자기 너를 보게 된 기쁨과 놀라움에…… 아, 꼭 죽을 것만 같구나.\"\n\n\"자, 자, 기운 차리세요, 아버지! 저예요, 진짜 저라고요! 기쁨은 사람을 해치지 않는다고들 하기에, 미리 기별도 없이 이렇게 찾아왔어요. 자, 그렇게 엄숙한 얼굴로 보지 마시고 어서 웃으세요. 제가 돌아왔으니, 이제 우린 행복하게 지낼 거예요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 내 아들아, 그래야지, 그래야 하고말고.\" 노인이 대답했다. \"한데 어떻게 행복하게 지낸단 말이냐? 이제 다시는 날 떠나지 않을 게냐? 자, 네게 찾아온 그 좋은 일을 다 들려주려무나.\"\n\n\"남의 불행에서 비롯된 행복을 기뻐하는 저를 하느님께서 용서해 주시기를.\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"하지만 하늘이 아시다시피, 저는 이 행운을 바란 적이 없습니다. 그저 일어난 일일 뿐이고, 솔직히 슬픈 척할 수도 없는걸요. 르클레르 선장님이 돌아가셨어요, 아버지. 그래서 아마 모렐 씨의 도움으로 제가 그 자리를 잇게 될 것 같아요. 아시겠어요, 아버지? 스무 살에 선장이 되어 봉급으로 백 루이를 받고, 거기에 이익까지 나눠 받는다고 한번 상상해 보세요! 저 같은 가난한 선원이 감히 바랄 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더한 일 아니겠어요?\"\n\n\"그래, 내 아들아.\" 노인이 대답했다. \"참으로 다행스러운 일이구나.\"\n\n\"그러니까 첫 봉급을 손에 쥐면, 아버지께 작은 집을 한 채 마련해 드릴 거예요. 으아리며 한련화며 인동덩굴을 심을 정원이 딸린 집으로요. 그런데 무슨 일이세요, 아버지? 어디 안 좋으세요?\"\n\n\"아무것도 아니다, 아무것도. 곧 괜찮아질 게야.\" 그렇게 말하면서 노인은 힘이 다해 뒤로 쓰러졌다.\n\n\"이런, 이런.\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"포도주 한 잔이면 기운이 나실 거예요, 아버지. 포도주는 어디 두셨어요?\"\n\n\"아니다, 아니야, 됐다. 찾을 것 없다. 마시고 싶지 않구나.\" 노인이 말했다.\n\n\"아니에요, 아버지, 어디 있는지 말씀하세요.\" 그러고는 찬장을 두세 개 열어 보았다.\n\n\"소용없다.\" 노인이 말했다. \"포도주가 없어.\"\n\n\"네? 포도주가 없다고요?\" 당테스가 창백해지며, 노인의 움푹 꺼진 두 뺨과 텅 빈 찬장을 번갈아 바라보며 말했다. \"포도주가 없다니요? 돈이 떨어지셨던 거예요, 아버지?\"\n\n\"네가 곁에 있으니 이제 아무것도 아쉬울 게 없다.\" 노인이 말했다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 당테스가 이마의 땀을 닦으며 더듬더듬 말했다. \"하지만 제가 석 달 전에 떠나면서 이백 프랑을 드렸잖아요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 에드몽, 그건 맞다. 하지만 너는 그때 우리 이웃 카드루스에게 진 작은 빚을 잊고 있었지. 그가 내게 그 일을 일깨우면서, 너 대신 갚지 않으면 모렐 씨에게서 받아 내겠다고 하더구나. 그러니 알겠지, 그자가 네게 해코지라도 할까 봐……\"\n\"그래서요?\"\n\n\"그래서, 내가 그에게 갚았다.\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 당테스가 외쳤다. \"제가 카드루스에게 진 빚은 백사십 프랑이었는걸요.\"\n\n\"그래.\" 노인이 더듬거리며 말했다.\n\n\"그래서 제가 드린 이백 프랑에서 그 돈을 갚으셨어요?\"\n\n노인이 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"그럼 석 달을 육십 프랑으로 사셨다는 거잖아요.\" 에드몽이 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"내가 얼마나 적게 쓰는지 너도 알지 않느냐.\" 노인이 말했다.\n\n\"하느님, 저를 용서하소서.\" 에드몽이 외치며 아버지 앞에 무릎을 꿇었다.\n\n\"무얼 하는 게냐?\"\n\n\"제 가슴을 후벼 파셨어요.\"\n\n\"마음 쓰지 마라. 이렇게 너를 다시 보지 않느냐.\" 노인이 말했다. \"이제 다 지난 일이다. 모든 게 다시 제자리로 돌아왔어.\"\n\n\"네, 제가 돌아왔어요.\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"앞날도 밝고, 돈도 조금 있고요. 자, 아버지, 여기요!\" 그가 말했다. \"이걸 받으세요, 받으시고 어서 뭐든 사 오라고 사람을 보내세요.\" 그러고는 주머니를 탁자 위에 털어 놓았다. 금화 열두어 닢에, 오 프랑짜리 은화 대여섯 닢, 그리고 잔돈 몇 푼이 쏟아져 나왔다. 늙은 당테스의 얼굴이 환해졌다.\n\n\"이게 다 누구 거냐?\" 노인이 물었다.\n\n\"제 거고, 아버지 거고, 우리 거예요! 받으세요. 먹을 걸 사세요. 마음 편히 지내시고요. 내일이면 더 생길 테니까요.\"\n\n\"천천히, 천천히 하자꾸나.\" 노인이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그리고 네가 허락한다면 네 지갑을 아껴 쓰마. 내가 한꺼번에 너무 많은 걸 사들이는 걸 사람들이 보면, 이걸 살 수 있을 때까지 네가 돌아오기만 기다렸다고들 할 테니까.\"\n\n\"마음대로 하세요. 하지만 무엇보다도, 아버지, 부디 사람을 하나 두세요. 이렇게 오래도록 혼자 지내시게 둘 수는 없어요. 선창의 작은 궤짝에 밀수해 들여온 커피랑 아주 상등품 담배가 있으니, 내일 가져다 드릴게요. 그런데 쉿, 누가 오네요.\"\n\n\"카드루스로구나. 네가 돌아왔다는 소식을 듣고, 무사히 돌아온 걸 축하하러 오는 게 분명하다.\"\n\n\"아, 입으로는 한 가지를 말하면서 속으로는 딴생각을 하는 자라니까요.\" 에드몽이 중얼거렸다. \"하지만 괜찮아요. 한때 우리에게 도움을 준 이웃이니, 반갑게 맞아야지요.\"\n\n에드몽이 말을 멈춘 사이, 검고 수염 난 카드루스의 머리가 문가에 나타났다. 스물대여섯쯤 된 사내였고, 천 조각을 손에 들고 있었다. 재단사인 그는 그것으로 외투 안감을 대려는 참이었다.\n\n\"아니, 에드몽, 자네 돌아왔나?\" 그가 마르세유 사투리가 짙게 밴 말투로, 상아처럼 흰 이를 드러내며 히죽 웃었다.\n\n\"네, 보시다시피요, 카드루스 아저씨. 어떤 일이든 기꺼이 도와 드릴 준비가 돼 있어요.\" 당테스가 대답했지만, 예의라는 겉치레 아래 냉랭함을 잘 감추지는 못했다.\n\n\"고맙네, 고마워. 하지만 다행히 난 아쉬운 게 없어. 오히려 가끔은 나를 필요로 하는 사람들이 있지.\" 당테스가 무슨 몸짓을 했다. \"자네 얘기가 아니야, 이 사람아. 아니지, 아니고말고! 내가 자네한테 돈을 빌려줬고 자네가 갚았으니, 그게 좋은 이웃 사이지. 우린 셈이 끝난 거야.\"\n\n\"우리를 도와준 이들과는 결코 셈이 끝나지 않지요.\" 당테스가 대답했다. \"돈을 갚고 나면, 이번엔 고마운 마음을 빚지게 되니까요.\"\n\n\"그런 얘긴 해서 뭐 하나? 끝난 건 끝난 거지. 자네가 무사히 돌아온 얘기나 하세, 이 사람아. 내가 오디색 천을 맞춰 보려고 부두에 나갔다가 친구 당글라르를 만났지 뭔가. '아니, 자네 마르세유에 있었나?' 하니까 '그래.' 하더군.\n\n\"'난 자네가 스미르나에 있는 줄 알았는데.' 했더니 '있었지. 한데 이제 돌아왔어.' 하더라고.\n\n\"'그럼 그 귀여운 친구, 우리 어린 에드몽은 어디 있나?'\n\n\"'아버지하고 같이 있겠지, 뭐.' 당글라르가 그러더군. 그래서 이렇게,\" 카드루스가 덧붙였다. \"한달음에 달려왔지. 친구하고 악수라도 나누는 기쁨을 누리려고 말이야.\"\n\n\"고마운 카드루스 같으니!\" 노인이 말했다. \"우리한테 정이 참 깊은 사람이야.\"\n\n\"그럼요, 정말 그렇고말고요. 제가 두 분을 사랑하고 존경하는 건, 정직한 사람이 워낙 드물기 때문이지요. 한데 자네, 부자가 되어 돌아온 모양이군.\" 재단사가 당테스가 탁자 위에 던져 놓은 한 줌의 금화와 은화를 곁눈질하며 말을 이었다.\n\n젊은이는 이웃의 검은 눈에 번득이는 탐욕스러운 눈빛을 알아챘다. \"아.\" 그가 무심한 듯 말했다. \"이 돈은 제 게 아니에요. 제가 없는 동안 아버지가 여러모로 궁하셨을까 봐 걱정된다고 했더니, 그게 아니란 걸 보여 주시려고 지갑을 탁자에 털어 놓으신 거예요. 자, 아버지.\" 당테스가 덧붙였다. \"이 돈은 도로 상자에 넣어 두세요. 카드루스 아저씨가 뭔가 필요하지 않으시다면요. 필요하시면 얼마든지 쓰셔도 되고요.\"\n\n\"아니야, 이 사람아, 아니야.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"난 아쉬울 게 없어. 고맙게도 내 형편에 맞춰 살고 있으니까. 그 돈은 자네가 간직하게, 간직하라니까. 돈이란 아무리 많아도 지나친 법이 없지. 하지만 자네가 권해 준 것만으로도, 정말 받아 쓴 것만큼이나 고맙네.\"\n\n\"진심으로 권한 거예요.\" 당테스가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇겠지, 이 사람아, 그렇고말고. 그건 그렇고, 듣자 하니 자네 모렐 씨 눈에 단단히 들었다던데. 이 능청맞은 친구 같으니!\"\n\n\"모렐 씨는 늘 제게 더없이 잘해 주셨어요.\" 당테스가 대답했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 그분과 저녁을 같이 들자는 걸 마다한 건 잘못한 일이지.\"\n\n\"아니, 그분과 저녁 드는 걸 마다했단 말이냐?\" 늙은 당테스가 말했다. \"그분이 너를 저녁 식사에 초대하셨어?\"\n\n\"네, 아버지.\" 에드몽이, 아들에게 베풀어진 과분한 영예에 놀라워하는 아버지를 보며 미소 지으며 대답했다.\n\n\"그런데 왜 마다했느냐, 아들아?\" 노인이 물었다.\n\n\"한시라도 빨리 아버지를 다시 뵙고 싶어서였지요.\" 젊은이가 대답했다. \"아버지가 무척 보고 싶었거든요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그건 모렐 씨를, 그 선량하고 훌륭한 분을 언짢게 했을 게야.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"더구나 선장이 되기를 바라는 마당에, 선주를 거슬리게 한 건 잘못이지.\"\n\n\"하지만 거절한 까닭을 그분께 말씀드렸고,\" 당테스가 대답했다. \"충분히 이해하셨으리라 믿어요.\"\n\n\"그래도 선장이 되려면 윗사람한테 약간의 아첨은 떨어야 하는 법이야.\"\n\n\"그런 것 없이 선장이 되고 싶어요.\" 당테스가 말했다.\n\n\"그럴수록 더 좋지, 더 좋고말고! 자네 옛 친구들 모두에게 그보다 더 큰 기쁨은 없을 거야. 그리고 저기 생니콜라 요새 너머에 그 소식을 들으면 섭섭해하지 않을 사람이 하나 있다는 것도 내가 알지.\"\n\n\"메르세데스 말인가?\" 노인이 말했다.\n\n\"네, 아버지. 아버지가 허락해 주신다면, 이렇게 아버지를 뵙고 건강하신 것도, 필요한 게 다 갖춰진 것도 확인했으니, 이제 카탈루냐 마을에 다녀와도 좋다고 허락해 주세요.\"\n\n\"가거라, 내 아들아.\" 늙은 당테스가 말했다. \"내가 아들 덕에 복을 받았듯, 하늘이 네게 좋은 아내로 복을 내려 주시기를!\"\n\n\"아내라니!\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"아니, 어찌 그리 앞서 가시나, 당테스 영감님. 내가 보기엔 아직 저 사람 아내가 아닌데요.\"\n\n\"아직은 아니지요. 하지만 머잖아 그렇게 될 게 틀림없는걸요.\" 에드몽이 대답했다.\n\n\"그래, 그래.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"하지만 되도록 빨리 돌아온 건 잘한 일이야, 이 사람아.\"\n\n\"왜요?\"\n\n\"메르세데스는 아주 어여쁜 처녀고, 어여쁜 처녀에겐 늘 따라다니는 사내들이 끊이질 않거든. 더구나 그 처녀한테는 떼로 몰려들지.\"\n\n\"정말요?\" 에드몽이, 희미한 불안의 기색이 어린 미소를 띠며 대답했다.\n\n\"아, 그럼.\" 카드루스가 말을 이었다. \"그것도 썩 괜찮은 청혼들이지. 하지만 알다시피 자넨 선장이 될 몸이니, 그땐 누가 자넬 마다하겠나?\"\n\n\"그러니까,\" 당테스가, 속마음을 잘 감추지 못하는 미소를 띠며 대답했다. \"제가 선장이 못 된다면……\"\n\n\"어허, 그게 말이지!\" 카드루스가 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했다.\n\n\"자, 자.\" 선원이 말했다. \"저는 아저씨보다 여자들을, 특히 메르세데스를 더 좋게 봅니다. 그리고 제가 선장이 되든 못 되든, 그녀가 언제까지나 제게 한결같으리란 걸 확신해요.\"\n\n\"그럴수록 더 좋지, 더 좋고말고.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"결혼을 앞둔 사람한테 굳건한 믿음만 한 게 없으니까. 하지만 그런 건 신경 쓰지 말게, 이 사람아. 어서 가서 자네가 돌아온 걸 알리고, 자네의 모든 꿈과 앞날의 일들을 그 처녀에게 들려주게나.\"\n\n\"바로 가야지요.\" 에드몽이 대답했다. 그러고는 아버지를 끌어안고 카드루스에게 가볍게 고개를 까딱한 뒤 방을 나섰다.\n\n카드루스는 잠시 머뭇거리다가, 늙은 당테스에게 작별을 고하고 아래층으로 내려가 세나크 거리 모퉁이에서 기다리던 당글라르에게 다시 합류했다.\n\n\"그래,\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"만나 봤나?\"\n\n\"방금 헤어졌네.\" 카드루스가 대답했다.\n\n\"선장이 되리란 기대에 대해 무슨 말을 하던가?\"\n\n\"이미 정해진 일처럼 말하더군.\"\n\n\"이런!\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"내가 보기엔 너무 서두르는 것 같은데.\"\n\n\"글쎄, 모렐 씨가 그 자리를 약속한 모양이야.\"\n\n\"그래서 잔뜩 우쭐해 있던가?\"\n\n\"우쭐 정도가 아니라, 아주 거만하게 굴더라니까. 무슨 대단한 인물이라도 되는 양 나한테 자기 후원을 받으라고 하질 않나, 무슨 은행가라도 되는 양 돈을 빌려주겠다고 나서질 않나.\"\n\n\"그래서 거절했고?\"\n\n\"두말하면 잔소리지. 마음만 먹었으면 쉽게 받을 수도 있었어. 그 친구가 난생처음 손에 쥔 은화를 쥐여 준 게 바로 나였으니까. 하지만 이제 당테스 씨는 남의 도움 따위 필요 없으시다네. 곧 선장이 되실 몸이니 말이야.\"\n\n\"흥!\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"아직 선장이 된 것도 아니잖아.\"\n\n\"그럼! 차라리 안 되는 편이 나아.\" 카드루스가 대답했다. \"선장이라도 됐다간, 정말이지 말도 못 붙이게 될 테니까.\"\n\n\"우리가 마음만 먹으면,\" 당글라르가 대답했다. \"저 친구는 지금 그대로일 거야. 어쩌면 지금보다 못한 신세가 될지도 모르지.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 소린가?\"\n\n\"아무것도 아니야. 혼잣말이었어. 그래, 저 친구 아직도 그 카탈루냐 처녀한테 푹 빠져 있나?\"\n\n\"머리끝까지 빠졌지. 하지만 내가 크게 잘못 본 게 아니라면, 그쪽에 한바탕 풍파가 일 거야.\"\n\n\"무슨 말인지 좀 풀어 보게.\"\n\n\"내가 왜?\"\n\n\"자네가 생각하는 것보다 중요한 일일지도 몰라. 자네, 당테스를 좋아하지 않지?\"\n\n\"난 벼락출세하는 자들을 좋아한 적이 없어.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 카탈루냐 처녀에 대해 아는 걸 다 말해 보게.\"\n\n\"확실히 아는 건 없네. 다만 아까 말했듯이, 그 미래의 선장 양반이 비에유 앵피르므리 근처에서 골치깨나 썩일 거라고 믿게 할 만한 것들을 봤을 뿐이지.\"\n\n\"뭘 봤는데? 자, 어서 말해 보게!\"\n\n\"음, 메르세데스가 시내로 들어오는 걸 볼 때마다, 늘 키 크고 떡 벌어진 검은 눈의 카탈루냐 사내가 따라붙어 있더라고. 불그레한 낯빛에 가무잡잡한 살갗, 사나운 인상을 한 자였어. 처녀는 그자를 사촌이라고 부르더군.\"\n\n\"그래? 그럼 그 사촌이 처녀한테 마음을 두고 있다고 보는 건가?\"\n\n\"그저 짐작일 뿐이야. 안 그러면 떡대 좋은 스물한 살짜리 사내가 열일곱짜리 어여쁜 처녀한테 무슨 다른 속셈이 있겠나?\"\n\n\"그래서 당테스가 카탈루냐 마을에 갔다는 거지?\"\n\n\"내가 내려오기 전에 벌써 갔어.\"\n\n\"우리도 같은 쪽으로 가세. 라레제르브에 들러 라말그 한 잔 마시면서 소식을 기다리면 되겠군.\"\n\n\"가세.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"대신 술값은 자네가 내게.\"\n\n\"물론이지.\" 당글라르가 대답했다. 둘은 그곳으로 서둘러 가서 포도주 한 병과 잔 두 개를 청했다.\n\n팡필 영감은 채 십 분도 안 돼 당테스가 지나가는 것을 보았다고 했다. 그가 카탈루냐 마을에 있는 게 분명하다고 확신한 두 사람은, 막 움트기 시작한 플라타너스와 시카모어 잎사귀 아래 자리를 잡고 앉았다. 그 가지에서는 새들이 봄의 첫 며칠 가운데 하루를 반기며 지저귀고 있었다.\n\n# Chapter 3. The Catalans\n\n세 친구가 포도주를 마시며 보고 듣던 자리에서 백 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳, 비바람에 닳은 헐벗은 벽 너머에 카탈루냐 마을이 있었다. 오래전 이 수수께끼 같은 무리는 에스파냐를 떠나, 오늘날까지도 그들이 머무는 그 좁은 땅에 정착했다. 어디서 왔는지 아는 사람은 없었고, 그들은 알 수 없는 말을 썼다. 프로방스 말을 할 줄 아는 그들의 우두머리 중 한 사람이 마르세유시 당국에 이 헐벗고 메마른 곶을 달라고 청했다. 옛 뱃사람들처럼 그들이 배를 끌어 올려 둔 곳이었다. 청은 받아들여졌고, 석 달 뒤에는 이 바다 집시들을 실어 온 열두어 척에서 열다섯 척쯤 되는 작은 배들을 둘러싸고 작은 마을이 생겨났다.\n무어풍과 에스파냐풍이 반반씩 섞인 독특하고 그림 같은 모양으로 지어진 이 마을은 지금도 남아 있으며, 처음 건너온 이들의 후손이 살면서 조상의 말을 쓴다. 서너 세기 동안 그들은 한 무리의 바닷새가 내려앉듯 자리 잡은 이 작은 곶 위에 머물렀다. 마르세유 사람들과 섞이지 않고 자기들끼리만 혼인하며, 모국의 언어를 지켜 온 것처럼 본래의 풍습과 옷차림도 고스란히 간직해 왔다.\n\n독자들은 이 작은 마을의 단 하나뿐인 골목길을 따라 우리와 함께 걸어, 어느 집으로 들어서게 될 것이다. 그 집은 햇볕에 그을려 이 고장 건물 특유의, 마른 나뭇잎 같은 아름다운 빛깔을 띠었고, 안쪽은 에스파냐의 여인숙처럼 흰 회칠이 되어 있었다. 칠흑처럼 검은 머리에 영양처럼 부드러운 눈을 가진 젊고 아름다운 처녀가 벽판에 등을 기대고 서 있었다. 가늘고 섬세하게 빚어진 손가락으로 히스 꽃 한 다발을 비벼, 떨어진 꽃잎을 바닥에 흩뿌리고 있었다. 팔꿈치까지 드러난 갈색 팔은 아를의 비너스 상에서 본뜬 듯한 모양이었는데, 안절부절못하는 듯한 조바심으로 움직였다. 그녀가 활처럼 휘고 유연한 발로 땅을 톡톡 두드릴 때마다, 회색과 파란색 무늬가 박힌 붉은 무명 양말 속, 매끈하게 빠진 다리의 순결하고 풍만한 윤곽이 드러났다. 그녀에게서 세 걸음 떨어진 곳, 벌레 먹은 낡은 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 의자를 뒷다리 둘로만 기우뚱하게 세운 채 앉아 있는 사람은 스물에서 스물둘쯤 된 키 큰 청년이었다. 그는 짜증과 불안이 뒤섞인 표정으로 그녀를 바라보았다. 두 눈으로 그녀에게 묻고 있었지만, 처녀의 굳고 흔들림 없는 눈길이 그의 시선을 눌렀다.\n\n\"봐, 메르세데스.\" 청년이 말했다. \"또 부활절이 돌아왔어. 말해 봐, 이제는 결혼할 때가 되지 않았어?\"\n\n\"백 번도 더 대답했잖아, 페르낭. 또 묻는 걸 보면 정말 어지간히 둔하구나.\"\n\n\"그래, 그럼 다시, 다시 말해 줘, 제발. 그래야 나도 마침내 믿을 수 있을 테니까! 백 번째로 말해 줘. 네 어머니까지 허락하셨던 내 사랑을 거절한다고. 네가 내 행복을 가지고 장난치고 있다는 걸, 내가 죽든 살든 너한테는 아무것도 아니라는 걸 한 번에 깨닫게 해 줘. 아, 십 년 동안 네 남편이 되는 꿈을 꿔 왔는데, 메르세데스, 내 삶을 지탱해 준 유일한 희망을 잃다니!\"\n\n\"적어도 그 희망을 부추긴 건 내가 아니야, 페르낭.\" 메르세데스가 대답했다. \"넌 내가 조금이라도 교태를 부렸다고 탓할 수는 없어. 난 늘 너에게 말했잖아. '난 너를 오빠처럼 사랑해. 하지만 누이의 정 이상은 바라지 마. 내 마음은 다른 사람의 것이니까.' 안 그래, 페르낭?\"\n\n\"그래, 정말 그랬지, 메르세데스.\" 청년이 대답했다. \"그래, 넌 잔인할 만큼 솔직했어. 하지만 카탈루냐 사람들끼리 혼인하는 것이 신성한 율법이라는 걸 잊었어?\"\n\n\"틀렸어, 페르낭. 그건 율법이 아니라 그저 관습일 뿐이야. 그러니 부탁인데, 네게 유리하게 그 관습을 들먹이지 마. 넌 징집 대상이잖아, 페르낭. 지금 풀려나 있는 것도 잠시 봐주는 것뿐이고, 언제라도 무기를 들라는 부름을 받을 수 있어. 일단 군인이 되면, 가난한 고아인 나를 어쩌려고? 의지할 데도 없고 재산도 없는, 다 쓰러져 가는 오두막 한 채와 다 해진 그물 몇 개밖에 없는 나를. 그것도 아버지가 어머니께, 어머니가 내게 남긴 초라한 유산일 뿐이야. 어머니가 돌아가신 지 일 년이 됐는데, 페르낭, 너도 알다시피 난 거의 남의 적선으로 연명해 왔어. 넌 가끔 내가 너에게 쓸모가 있는 척하지만, 그건 네가 잡은 물고기를 나와 나누기 위한 핑계일 뿐이지. 그래도 난 그걸 받아, 페르낭. 네가 아버지 형제의 아들이고, 우리가 함께 자랐고, 무엇보다 내가 거절하면 네가 너무나 마음 아파할 테니까. 하지만 내가 가져다 파는 그 물고기, 그걸 판 돈으로 실을 잣는 아마를 사는 그 물고기가 사실은 적선이라는 걸, 페르낭, 난 뼈저리게 느끼고 있어.\"\n\n\"설령 그렇다 해도, 메르세데스, 가난하고 외로운 너라도, 나에게는 마르세유 제일가는 선주의 딸이나 제일 부유한 은행가의 딸만큼이나 어울리는 사람이야! 우리 같은 사람이 바라는 게 좋은 아내와 알뜰한 살림꾼 말고 뭐가 있겠어? 그런 사람을 너 말고 어디서 찾겠어?\"\n\n\"페르낭.\" 메르세데스가 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"여자가 제 남편보다 다른 남자를 더 사랑하면, 살림은 엉망이 되고, 그 여자가 정숙하게 남을지 누가 장담하겠어? 내 우정으로 만족해. 다시 말하지만 그게 내가 약속할 수 있는 전부고, 난 줄 수 있는 것 이상은 약속하지 않을 거야.\"\n\n\"알겠어.\" 페르낭이 대답했다. \"넌 네 비참함은 참고 견딜 수 있어도, 내 비참함을 함께 나누는 건 두려운 거야. 좋아, 메르세데스, 네 사랑을 받는다면 난 운명에 맞서 보겠어. 네가 행운을 가져다줄 테고, 난 부자가 될 거야. 어부 일을 넓혀 나가고, 어느 창고의 사무원 자리를 얻고, 때가 되면 나도 장사꾼이 될 수 있어.\"\n\n\"그런 건 못 해, 페르낭. 넌 군인이야. 네가 카탈루냐에 머물러 있는 건 전쟁이 없기 때문일 뿐이야. 그러니 어부로 남아, 내 우정에 만족해. 그 이상은 줄 수 없으니까.\"\n\n\"좋아, 그럼 더 잘해 보겠어, 메르세데스. 난 뱃사람이 되겠어. 네가 경멸하는 우리 조상의 옷차림 대신, 칠을 한 모자에 줄무늬 셔츠, 단추에 닻이 새겨진 파란 웃옷을 입겠어. 그런 차림이면 네 마음에 들겠어?\"\n\n\"무슨 말이야?\" 메르세데스가 성난 눈길로 물었다. \"무슨 소리야? 무슨 말인지 모르겠어.\"\n\n\"내 말은, 메르세데스, 네가 나한테 이렇게 모질고 잔인한 건, 그런 차림을 한 누군가를 기다리고 있기 때문이라는 거야. 하지만 어쩌면 네가 기다리는 그 사람은 변덕스러운 자일지도 몰라. 설령 그가 그렇지 않더라도, 바다가 변덕스러우니까.\"\n\n\"페르낭!\" 메르세데스가 외쳤다. \"난 네가 착한 사람인 줄 알았는데, 내가 잘못 봤구나! 페르낭, 질투와 하느님의 진노를 끌어들이다니, 너 정말 못됐어! 그래, 부인하지 않겠어. 난 네가 말하는 그 사람을 기다리고 있고, 사랑해. 그리고 만약 그가 돌아오지 않는다 해도, 네가 넌지시 비추는 변덕을 탓하는 대신, 그가 나만을, 오직 나만을 사랑하며 죽었다고 말하겠어.\" 처녀는 분노에 찬 몸짓을 했다. \"네 속을 알겠어, 페르낭. 내가 너를 사랑하지 않으니까 그 사람에게 복수하려는 거지. 네 카탈루냐 칼을 그의 단검과 맞대 보려는 거야. 그래서 뭘 얻겠어? 그가 진다면 넌 내 우정을 잃을 테고, 네가 이긴다면 그 우정이 증오로 바뀌는 걸 보게 될 거야. 내 말을 믿어, 한 남자에게 싸움을 거는 건 그 남자를 사랑하는 여자의 마음을 얻는 데 나쁜 방법이야. 안 돼, 페르낭, 넌 그런 사악한 생각에 굴복하지 않을 거야. 나를 아내로 둘 수 없으니, 누이이자 벗으로 두는 것에 만족할 거야. 그리고,\" 그녀는 눈물로 흐려진 눈을 하고 덧붙였다. \"기다려, 기다려, 페르낭. 방금 네가 바다는 변덕스럽다고 했지. 그가 떠난 지 넉 달이 됐고, 그 넉 달 동안 무서운 폭풍이 몇 번이나 있었어.\"\n\n페르낭은 아무 대답도 하지 않았고, 메르세데스의 두 뺨을 타고 흐르는 눈물을 막으려 하지도 않았다. 그 눈물 한 방울 한 방울을 위해서라면 제 심장의 피라도 흘렸을 그였지만, 그 눈물은 다른 사람을 위한 것이었다. 그는 일어나 오두막 안을 잠시 서성이다가, 불현듯 메르세데스 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 두 눈은 이글거리고 주먹은 움켜쥔 채였다. \"말해 봐, 메르세데스.\" 그가 말했다. \"단 한 번만 묻겠어. 이게 네 마지막 결심이야?\"\n\n\"난 에드몽 당테스를 사랑해.\" 처녀가 차분하게 대답했다. \"그리고 에드몽 말고는 그 누구도 내 남편이 될 수 없어.\"\n\n\"그리고 언제까지나 그를 사랑할 거야?\"\n\n\"내가 살아 있는 한.\"\n\n페르낭은 패배한 사람처럼 고개를 떨구고는 신음 같은 한숨을 내쉬었다. 그러더니 갑자기 이를 악물고 콧구멍을 벌름거리며 그녀의 얼굴을 똑바로 쳐다보고 말했다. \"하지만 그가 죽었다면……\"\n\n\"그가 죽었다면, 나도 죽을 거야.\"\n\n\"그가 너를 잊었다면……\"\n\n\"메르세데스!\" 밖에서 명랑한 목소리가 불렀다. \"메르세데스!\"\n\n\"아.\" 처녀가 기쁨으로 얼굴을 붉히며, 넘쳐흐르는 사랑으로 펄쩍 뛰며 외쳤다. \"봤지, 그는 나를 잊지 않았어. 저기 그가 왔잖아!\" 그러고는 문으로 달려가 문을 열며 말했다. \"여기, 에드몽, 나 여기 있어!\"\n\n페르낭은 뱀을 본 나그네처럼 창백하게 떨며 뒷걸음질 치다가 곁에 있던 의자에 주저앉았다. 에드몽과 메르세데스는 서로의 품에 안겼다. 열린 문으로 쏟아져 들어온 마르세유의 타는 듯한 햇살이 두 사람을 빛의 홍수로 뒤덮었다. 처음에 그들은 주위의 아무것도 보지 못했다. 벅찬 행복이 그들을 세상 모든 것에서 떼어 놓았고, 두 사람은 끊긴 말로만 이야기를 나누었다. 그것은 기쁨이 너무도 지극할 때 나오는 표시여서, 오히려 슬픔을 드러내는 것처럼 보였다. 그때 문득 에드몽은 그늘 속에 윤곽을 드러낸 페르낭의 어둡고 창백하며 위협적인 얼굴을 보았다. 젊은 카탈루냐 사내는 스스로도 거의 까닭을 알 수 없는 동작으로 허리띠에 찬 칼에 손을 얹었다.\n\n\"아, 미안합니다.\" 당테스가 이번에는 그가 눈살을 찌푸리며 말했다. \"여기 우리 셋이 있는 줄은 미처 몰랐군요.\" 그러고는 메르세데스 쪽으로 돌아서서 물었다. \"이분은 누구지?\"\n\n\"당신의 가장 좋은 벗이 될 사람이에요, 당테스. 내 친구이자 사촌이고 오빠니까요. 페르낭이에요. 에드몽, 당신 다음으로 내가 이 세상에서 가장 사랑하는 사람이죠. 기억 안 나세요?\"\n\n\"기억나지!\" 당테스가 말했다. 그는 한 손에 메르세데스의 손을 꼭 쥔 채, 다른 손을 다정하게 카탈루냐 사내에게 내밀었다. 그러나 페르낭은 그 정겨운 몸짓에 응하기는커녕, 입을 다문 채 떨고만 있었다. 에드몽은 어쩔 줄 몰라 당황하는 메르세데스에게, 그리고 다시 어둡고 위협적인 페르낭에게 살피는 듯한 눈길을 던졌다. 그 눈길로 그는 모든 것을 알아차렸고, 분노가 끓어올랐다.\n\n\"이렇게 한달음에 당신에게 달려오면서, 여기서 적을 만나리라고는 생각도 못 했군요.\"\n\n\"적이라뇨!\" 메르세데스가 사촌을 향해 성난 눈길을 던지며 외쳤다. \"내 집에 적이 있다니, 그게 무슨 말이에요, 에드몽! 만약 그렇게 믿었다면, 난 당신 팔에 내 팔을 끼고 마르세유로 떠나, 이 집에 다시는 돌아오지 않았을 거예요.\"\n\n페르낭의 눈에서 번갯불이 일었다. \"그리고 당신에게 무슨 불행한 일이라도 닥친다면, 사랑하는 에드몽.\" 그녀는 조금 전과 같은 차분함으로 말을 이었는데, 그 차분함은 처녀가 페르낭의 음흉한 생각의 가장 깊은 속내까지 읽어 냈다는 것을 그에게 증명해 보였다. \"당신에게 불행한 일이 닥친다면, 난 모르지우곶의 가장 높은 곳에 올라가 거기서 곤두박질쳐 몸을 던지겠어요.\"\n\n페르낭의 얼굴이 죽은 듯이 창백해졌다. \"하지만 당신이 잘못 봤어요, 에드몽.\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"여기 당신의 적은 없어요. 오직 내 오빠 페르낭뿐이고, 그는 헌신적인 벗으로서 당신 손을 잡아 줄 거예요.\"\n\n이렇게 말하며 처녀는 카탈루냐 사내에게 위엄 어린 눈길을 고정시켰다. 그는 그 눈길에 홀린 듯, 천천히 에드몽에게 다가가 손을 내밀었다. 그의 증오는, 사납기는 해도 힘없는 파도처럼, 메르세데스가 그에게 행사하는 강한 지배력에 부딪혀 부서졌다. 그러나 에드몽의 손에 닿기가 무섭게, 그는 자기가 할 수 있는 일은 다 했다고 느끼고는, 황급히 집 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔다.\n\n\"오.\" 그가 미친 듯이 달리며 머리를 쥐어뜯고 외쳤다. \"오, 누가 나를 저 사내한테서 구해 줄까? 비참해, 난 정말 비참해!\"\n\n\"이봐, 카탈루냐 사람! 이봐, 페르낭! 어디로 그렇게 달려가나?\" 한 목소리가 외쳤다.\n\n청년은 우뚝 멈춰 서서 주위를 둘러보았고, 정자 아래 탁자에 당글라르와 함께 앉아 있는 카드루스를 보았다.\n\n\"이봐.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"왜 안 오나? 자네 정말 그렇게 바빠서 친구들하고 잠깐 인사 나눌 틈도 없나?\"\n\n\"더구나 친구들 앞에 술이 가득 든 병이 아직 남아 있는데 말이야.\" 당글라르가 덧붙였다. 페르낭은 멍한 얼굴로 두 사람을 바라보았지만, 한마디도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"넋이 나간 것 같군.\" 당글라르가 무릎으로 카드루스를 쿡 찌르며 말했다. \"우리가 잘못 짚은 건가, 우리가 그렇게 믿었는데도 당테스가 결국 이긴 건가?\"\n\n\"그야 알아봐야지.\" 카드루스가 대답하고는 청년 쪽으로 돌아서서 말했다. \"이봐, 카탈루냐 사람, 마음을 못 정하겠나?\"\n\n페르낭은 이마에 김처럼 솟아오른 땀을 닦고는, 천천히 정자 안으로 들어섰다. 그늘이 다소나마 그의 정신에 평온함을 되돌려 주는 듯했고, 그 서늘함이 지친 몸에 얼마간 생기를 불어넣어 주는 듯했다.\n\n\"안녕들 하신가.\" 그가 말했다. \"나를 불렀지?\" 그러고는 탁자를 둘러싼 의자 중 하나에 앉았다기보다 쓰러지듯 주저앉았다.\n\n\"자네가 미친 사람처럼 달려가기에 불렀지, 바다에라도 뛰어들까 봐 겁이 났거든.\" 카드루스가 웃으며 말했다. \"이봐, 친구가 있다는 건 술 한잔 권하는 것뿐만 아니라, 쓸데없이 물을 서너 됫박씩 들이켜는 걸 막아 주기 위한 것이기도 하다고!\"\n\n페르낭은 흐느낌 같은 신음을 내뱉고는, 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 두 손에 얼굴을 묻었다.\n\n\"이봐, 페르낭, 내 말해야겠는데.\" 카드루스가 말문을 열었다. 호기심이 모든 외교적 수완을 망쳐 버리는, 서민 특유의 그 거친 말투였다. \"자네 영락없이 차인 연인 꼴이로군.\" 그러고는 쉰 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"쳇!\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"저런 체격을 타고난 녀석이 사랑 때문에 불행해질 리가 있나. 자네 저 친구를 놀리는 거지, 카드루스.\"\n\n\"아니야.\" 그가 대답했다. \"저 한숨 소리나 좀 들어 보라고! 이봐, 이봐, 페르낭.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"고개를 들고 대답 좀 해 봐. 친구들이 안부를 묻는데 대꾸도 안 하는 건 예의가 아니지.\"\n\n\"내 안부는 괜찮아.\" 페르낭이 고개도 들지 않고 두 손을 움켜쥐며 말했다.\n\n\"아, 이거 봐, 당글라르.\" 카드루스가 친구에게 눈짓하며 말했다. \"사정이 이렇다네. 여기 이 페르낭은 착하고 용감한 카탈루냐 사람이고, 마르세유 최고의 어부 중 하나야. 그런데 메르세데스라는 아주 어여쁜 처녀에게 반했지. 그런데 불행히도 그 어여쁜 처녀는 파라옹호의 항해사한테 반한 모양이야. 게다가 파라옹호가 오늘 들어왔으니, 그래, 자네도 알 만하지!\"\n\n\"아니, 모르겠는데.\" 당글라르가 말했다.\n\n\"가엾은 페르낭이 차인 거지.\" 카드루스가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그래서, 그게 어쨌단 말인가?\" 페르낭이 고개를 들고, 분풀이할 상대를 찾는 사람처럼 카드루스를 노려보며 말했다. \"메르세데스는 누구한테도 해명할 의무가 없어, 안 그래? 그녀가 누구를 사랑하든 자유 아닌가?\"\n\n\"오, 그런 식으로 받아들인다면야.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"얘기가 다르지. 하지만 난 자네가 카탈루냐 사람인 줄 알았는데. 카탈루냐 사람들은 연적한테 밀려나는 걸 가만히 두고 보는 위인들이 아니라고들 하던데. 특히 페르낭, 자네는 복수가 무섭기로 소문났다고 들었거든.\"\n\n페르낭이 가련하게 웃었다. \"사랑에 빠진 사람은 결코 무섭지 않은 법이지.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"가엾은 친구!\" 당글라르가 진심으로 청년을 동정하는 체하며 말했다. \"이거 봐, 저 친구는 당테스가 이렇게 갑자기 돌아올 줄은 몰랐던 거야. 아마 죽은 줄 알았겠지. 아니면 마음이 변했거나! 이런 일은 갑자기 닥칠수록 더 모질게 느껴지는 법이거든.\"\n\n\"아, 정말이지, 어떤 경우라도 말이야!\" 카드루스가 말하면서 술을 들이켰다. 술기운이 슬슬 오르기 시작한 참이었다. \"어떤 경우라도, 당테스가 운 좋게 돌아온 탓에 곤란해진 사람이 페르낭 혼자만은 아니지, 안 그런가, 당글라르?\"\n\n\"그래, 자네 말이 맞아. 내 보기엔 그게 그자한테 불운을 가져다줄 것 같은데.\"\n\n\"뭐, 아무래도 좋아.\" 카드루스가 페르낭에게 술을 한 잔 따라 주고, 제 잔도 여덟 번째인지 아홉 번째인지 채우며 대답했다. 당글라르는 제 잔을 찔끔 입에 댔을 뿐이었다. \"아무래도 좋아. 어쨌든 그자는 메르세데스와, 그 어여쁜 메르세데스와 결혼할 테니까. 적어도 그러려고 돌아온 거지.\"\n\n그동안 당글라르는 청년에게 날카로운 눈길을 고정하고 있었다. 카드루스의 말은 청년의 가슴에 녹아내린 납처럼 떨어졌다.\n\n\"그래, 결혼은 언제 한답니까?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"오, 아직 정해지지 않았어요!\" 페르낭이 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"아니, 곧 정해지지.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"당테스가 파라옹호의 선장이 될 게 틀림없는 것만큼이나 확실하게 말이야, 안 그런가, 당글라르?\"\n\n당글라르는 이 뜻밖의 일격에 흠칫 놀라, 카드루스 쪽으로 돌아서서 그 얼굴을 살피며 그 한 방이 미리 계산된 것인지 알아내려 했다. 그러나 이미 취기로 둔하고 멍청해진 얼굴에서 시샘 말고는 아무것도 읽어 내지 못했다.\n\n\"좋아.\" 그가 잔을 채우며 말했다. \"아름다운 카탈루냐 처녀의 남편, 에드몽 당테스 선장을 위하여 마시세!\"\n\n카드루스가 흔들리는 손으로 잔을 입에 가져가 단숨에 비웠다. 페르낭은 자기 잔을 땅바닥에 내동댕이쳤다.\n\n\"어, 어, 어!\" 카드루스가 더듬거렸다. \"저기 저 벽 옆, 카탈루냐 마을 쪽에 보이는 게 뭐지? 봐, 페르낭, 자네 눈이 내 눈보다 낫잖아. 내 눈엔 둘로 보이는데. 술이 사람을 속이는 건 알지만, 내 보기엔 연인 두 사람이 나란히, 손을 맞잡고 걸어오는 것 같단 말이야. 맙소사, 우리가 보고 있는 줄도 모르고, 저것들이 정말 끌어안고 있잖아!\"\n\n당글라르는 페르낭이 겪는 괴로움을 하나도 놓치지 않았다.\n\n\"자네 저들을 아나, 페르낭?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"알지.\" 낮은 목소리로 대답이 돌아왔다. \"에드몽과 메르세데스야!\"\n\n\"아, 저것 봐, 정말!\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"난 못 알아봤네! 이봐, 당테스! 이봐, 어여쁜 아가씨! 이리로 와서, 결혼식이 언제인지 좀 알려 주게. 여기 이 페르낭이 어찌나 고집을 부리는지 도통 말을 안 해 주니 말이야.\"\n\n\"입 좀 다물게, 응?\" 당글라르가 카드루스를 말리는 체하며 말했다. 카드루스는 술꾼 특유의 끈질김으로 정자 밖으로 몸을 내밀고 있었다. \"똑바로 좀 서 보게, 그리고 저 연인들이 방해받지 않고 사랑을 속삭이게 내버려 둬. 봐, 페르낭을 보고 본받으라고. 저 친구는 점잖게 굴잖아!\"\n\n페르낭은, 투우사의 작살에 찔린 황소처럼 당글라르에게 들쑤셔진 탓에 아마도 참을 수 없을 만큼 격해진 듯, 막 뛰쳐나가려던 참이었다. 자리에서 일어나, 마음을 다잡고 연적에게 곤두박질쳐 달려들 기세였다. 바로 그때 메르세데스가 미소 띤 우아한 얼굴로 어여쁜 고개를 들어, 맑고 빛나는 눈으로 그들을 바라보았다. 그 순간 페르낭은 에드몽이 죽으면 자기도 죽겠다던 그녀의 위협을 떠올리고는, 다시 의자에 털썩 주저앉았다. 당글라르는 두 사내를, 술에 절어 망가진 자와 사랑에 짓눌린 자를 차례로 번갈아 바라보았다.\n\n'이 멍청이들한테서는 아무것도 얻어 낼 게 없겠군.' 그는 중얼거렸다. '술주정뱅이와 겁쟁이 사이에 끼어 있는 게 영 마음에 안 들어. 분노를 곱씹어야 할 판에 시샘이나 하며 술에 취하는 녀석이 하나 있고, 제가 사랑하는 여자를 코앞에서 빼앗기고도 큰 어린애처럼 구는 멍청이가 하나 있으니. 그래도 이 카탈루냐 녀석은 복수심에 불타는 에스파냐인이나 시칠리아인, 칼라브리아인처럼 번득이는 눈을 가졌고, 저쪽 녀석은 황소를 한 방에 때려눕힐 만한 주먹을 가졌단 말이지. 의심할 여지 없이 에드몽의 별이 떠오르고 있어. 저 멋진 처녀와 결혼하고, 선장도 될 테고, 우리 모두를 비웃겠지. 만일—' 음흉한 미소가 당글라르의 입가를 스쳤다. '만일 내가 이 일에 한 손 거들지 않는다면 말이지.' 그가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"이봐!\" 카드루스가 탁자에 주먹을 짚고 반쯤 몸을 일으키며 계속 외쳤다. \"이봐, 에드몽! 친구들이 안 보이나, 아니면 너무 잘나서 우리한테는 말도 안 거는 건가?\"\n\n\"아니야, 친구!\" 당테스가 대답했다. \"잘나서가 아니라 행복해서 그래. 행복은 자만보다 더 사람의 눈을 멀게 하는 모양이야.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇군, 그거 말 되는 설명이로군!\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"안녕하시오, 당테스 부인?\"\n\n메르세데스가 점잖게 인사하고 말했다. \"그건 제 이름이 아니에요. 우리 고장에서는 처녀를 정혼자가 남편이 되기도 전에 그의 이름으로 부르면 불행을 부른다고들 해요. 그러니 부디 메르세데스라고 불러 주세요.\"\n\n\"이 사람 좋은 우리 이웃을 너그러이 봐줘야겠어, 메르세데스.\" 당테스가 말했다. \"워낙 실수가 잦은 사람이라.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 당테스 씨, 결혼식을 곧 올리시겠군요.\" 당글라르가 젊은 연인에게 고개를 숙이며 말했다.\n\n\"되도록 빨리요, 당글라르 씨. 오늘 아버지 댁에서 모든 준비를 마치고, 내일이나 늦어도 모레는 여기 라레제르브에서 결혼 잔치를 열 겁니다. 제 친구들도 와 주시면 좋겠습니다. 그러니까, 당글라르 씨, 당신도 초대하는 거고, 카드루스, 자네도 초대하는 거야.\"\n\n\"그리고 페르낭도.\" 카드루스가 킬킬거리며 말했다. \"페르낭도 초대받은 거지!\"\n\n\"제 아내의 오빠는 곧 제 형제죠.\" 에드몽이 말했다. \"메르세데스도 저도, 이런 날 그가 빠진다면 무척 서운할 겁니다.\"\n\n페르낭은 대꾸하려고 입을 열었지만, 목소리가 입술에서 잦아들어 한마디도 내뱉을 수 없었다.\n\n\"오늘 준비를 하고, 내일이나 모레 예식이라! 선장님, 어지간히 서두르시는군요!\"\n\n\"당글라르.\" 에드몽이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"방금 메르세데스가 카드루스에게 한 말을 당신에게도 그대로 해야겠군요. '내게 어울리지 않는 호칭은 붙이지 말아 주세요.' 그러다 불운을 부를 수도 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"이거 실례했습니다.\" 당글라르가 대답했다. \"그저 당신이 서두르는 것 같다고 했을 뿐인데요. 시간이야 얼마든지 있지요. 파라옹호는 석 달이 지나야 다시 출항할 수 있을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"행복을 누리는 일에는 늘 마음이 급한 법이지요, 당글라르 씨. 오랫동안 고생을 한 사람은 행운을 좀처럼 믿기 어려운 법이니까요. 하지만 제가 이렇게 서두르는 게 이기심 때문만은 아닙니다. 파리에 다녀와야 하거든요.\"\n\n\"아, 정말요? 파리에요! 거기 가는 게 처음이오, 당테스?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"거기 무슨 볼일이 있소?\"\n\n\"제 일은 아닙니다. 가엾은 르클레르 선장님의 마지막 부탁이지요. 무슨 말인지 아시겠지요, 당글라르. 그건 신성한 일입니다. 게다가 다녀올 만큼의 시간만 쓸 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 알겠소.\" 당글라르가 말했다. 그러고는 낮은 목소리로 덧붙였다. '파리라, 틀림없이 대원수가 맡긴 그 편지를 전하러 가는 거겠지. 아, 이 편지가 한 가지 생각을 떠오르게 하는군. 기막힌 생각이야! 아, 당테스, 이 친구야, 자네는 아직 파라옹호의 일등 선원으로 등록되지 않았어.' 그러고는 멀어져 가는 에드몽 쪽으로 돌아서서 외쳤다. \"즐거운 여행 되시오!\"\n\n\"고맙소.\" 에드몽이 다정하게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. 그리고 두 연인은 마치 하늘이 특별히 점지한 사람들처럼 평온하고 즐겁게 가던 길을 계속 갔다.\n\n# Chapter 4. Conspiracy\n\n당글라르는 에드몽과 메르세데스가 생니콜라 요새의 한 모퉁이 뒤로 사라질 때까지 두 연인을 눈으로 좇았다. 그러고는 몸을 돌려, 의자에 푹 주저앉아 창백하게 떨고 있는 페르낭과, 술 노래 가사를 더듬더듬 흥얼대는 카드루스를 보았다.\n\n\"이보게,\" 당글라르가 페르낭에게 말했다. \"이 결혼이 모두를 행복하게 해 주는 건 아닌 모양이군.\"\n\n\"미칠 것 같습니다.\" 페르낭이 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 자네, 메르세데스를 사랑하나?\"\n\n\"흠모합니다!\"\n\n\"오래됐나?\"\n\n\"그녀를 안 뒤로 줄곧, 늘 그랬습니다.\"\n\n\"한데 자넨 처지를 바로잡을 궁리는 않고, 거기 앉아 머리만 쥐어뜯고 있군. 자네 같은 사람들이 그런 줄은 몰랐는데.\"\n\n\"그럼 어쩌란 말입니까?\" 페르낭이 말했다.\n\n\"내가 그걸 어찌 알겠나? 그게 내 일인가? 난 메르세데스 양을 사랑하지 않아. 하지만 자넨 다르지. 복음서에도 있잖나, 찾으라, 그러면 찾을 것이다.\"\n\n\"이미 찾았습니다.\"\n\n\"무얼?\"\n\n\"그 사내를 칼로 찔러 버리고 싶지만, 그녀는 약혼자에게 무슨 변고라도 생기면 스스로 목숨을 끊겠다고 했습니다.\"\n\n\"흥! 여자들이야 다들 그런 말을 하지만, 정작 실행에 옮기진 않아.\"\n\n\"당신은 메르세데스를 모릅니다. 그녀는 한다고 하면 합니다.\"\n\n\"멍청이 같으니.\" 당글라르가 중얼거렸다. '그녀가 죽든 안 죽든, 당테스가 선장만 안 되면 그만이지.'\n\n\"메르세데스가 죽기 전에,\" 페르낭이 흔들림 없는 결의에 찬 목소리로 대꾸했다. \"제가 먼저 죽을 겁니다!\"\n\n\"그게 바로 내가 말하는 사랑이지!\" 카드루스가 그 어느 때보다도 취한 목소리로 말했다. \"저게 사랑이야, 아니면 난 사랑이 뭔지도 모르는 거고.\"\n\n\"이보게,\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"자넨 괜찮은 사람 같으니, 빌어먹을, 자넬 좀 도와주고 싶네만……\"\n\n\"그래요,\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"근데 어떻게?\"\n\n\"이보게,\" 당글라르가 대꾸했다. \"자넨 거의 다 취했어. 그 병을 비우면 아주 곤드레가 될 걸세. 그러니 마시기나 하고, 우리가 의논하는 일에는 끼어들지 말게. 이건 머리도 맑아야 하고 냉정한 판단력도 필요한 일이거든.\"\n\n\"내가 취했다고!\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"이거 참 우습군! 이런 병이라면 네 병은 더 마실 수 있어. 오드콜로뉴 병만 한 것들인데 뭘. 팡필 영감, 술 더 주쇼!\"\n\n그러고는 카드루스가 잔을 식탁에 탁탁 두드렸다.\n\n\"방금 무슨 말씀을 하시려던 거였죠……\" 페르낭이 끊긴 말의 끝을 몹시 애타게 기다리며 물었다.\n\n\"내가 무슨 말을 했더라? 잊었군. 이 취한 카드루스 때문에 말의 흐름을 놓쳤어.\"\n\n\"취했지, 그러시든가. 술을 두려워하는 자들이나 손해 보는 거야. 그건 마음속에 못된 생각을 품고 있어서, 술이 그걸 끄집어낼까 봐 무서운 거거든.\" 카드루스는 그러더니 그 무렵 크게 유행하던 노래의 마지막 두 행을 부르기 시작했다.\n\n'못된 자들은 모두 물만 마신다네.\n\n대홍수가 그걸 똑똑히 증명했지.'\n\n\"도와주고 싶지만, 하셨잖습니까……\"\n\n\"그래. 그런데 내가 덧붙이길, 자넬 도우려면 당테스가 자네 사랑하는 여인과 결혼만 못 하게 하면 충분하다고 했지. 내 생각엔 그 결혼은 쉽게 깨뜨릴 수 있어. 게다가 당테스가 꼭 죽을 필요도 없고 말이야.\"\n\n\"죽음만이 둘을 갈라놓을 수 있습니다.\" 페르낭이 말했다.\n\n\"자넨 얼간이처럼 말하는군, 친구.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"여기 당글라르는 눈치 빠르고 영리하고 속이 깊은 사람이야. 자네가 틀렸다는 걸 증명해 줄 거라고. 증명해 봐, 당글라르. 내가 자네 대신 장담했으니. 당테스가 죽을 필요는 없다고 말해 봐. 그가 죽는다면 정말이지 안타까운 일이지. 당테스는 좋은 녀석이야. 난 당테스가 좋아. 당테스, 자네 건강을 위하여.\"\n\n페르낭이 조바심을 내며 일어섰다. \"내버려 두게.\" 당글라르가 젊은이를 붙들며 말했다. \"저렇게 취했어도, 하는 말이 그리 틀리진 않아. 떨어져 있는 것도 죽음 못지않게 사람을 갈라놓지. 만약 에드몽과 메르세데스 사이에 감옥의 벽이 가로놓인다면, 그가 묘비 아래 누운 것이나 다름없이 둘은 떼어지는 거야.\"\n\n\"그래요, 하지만 감옥에서는 나오기 마련이죠.\" 남은 정신을 그러모아 대화에 열심히 귀를 기울이던 카드루스가 말했다. \"그리고 일단 나오면, 그것도 이름이 에드몽 당테스라면, 복수를 하려 들 거고……\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 상관이야?\" 페르낭이 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"그리고 대체 왜,\" 카드루스가 끈질기게 물었다. \"당테스를 감옥에 처넣어야 한단 말이야? 그는 훔치지도, 죽이지도, 살해하지도 않았는데.\"\n\n\"입 다물어!\" 당글라르가 말했다.\n\n\"입 안 다물 거야!\" 카드루스가 대꾸했다. \"난 왜 당테스를 감옥에 넣어야 하는지 알고 싶다니까. 난 당테스가 좋아. 당테스, 자네 건강을 위하여!\" 그러고는 술 한 잔을 또 들이켰다.\n\n당글라르는 양복장이의 흐리멍덩한 눈빛에서 취기가 깊어 가는 것을 보고, 페르낭 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다. \"자, 알겠지, 그를 죽일 필요는 없는 거야.\"\n\n\"물론 죽일 필요야 없지요. 방금 말씀하신 대로, 당테스를 체포되게 만들 수단이 있다면 말입니다. 그런 수단이 있습니까?\"\n\n\"찾으면 나오는 법이지. 한데 내가 왜 이 일에 끼어들어야 하나? 내 일도 아닌데.\"\n\n\"왜 끼어드는지는 모르겠습니다.\" 페르낭이 그의 팔을 붙잡으며 말했다. \"하지만 이건 압니다. 당신은 당테스에게 개인적인 원한이 있어요. 스스로 미워하는 사람은 남의 감정도 결코 잘못 읽지 않는 법이니까요.\"\n\n\"내가! 당테스에게 원한이라? 천만에, 맹세코 없네! 자네가 불행해 보였고, 그 불행이 내 관심을 끌었을 뿐이야. 그게 다라고. 한데 내가 내 잇속을 챙기려 그런다고 믿는다면, 그럼 잘 있게, 친구. 이 일은 자네 알아서 풀어 보게나.\" 그러고는 당글라르가 떠나려는 듯 일어섰다.\n\n\"아니, 아닙니다.\" 페르낭이 그를 붙들며 말했다. \"계세요! 따지고 보면, 당신이 당테스에게 악감정이 있든 없든 제겐 별로 중요하지 않습니다. 전 그자를 증오합니다! 드러내 놓고 고백하지요. 당신이 수단을 찾아 주면 제가 실행하겠습니다. 다만 그를 죽이는 일만은 안 됩니다. 당테스가 죽으면 메르세데스가 스스로 목숨을 끊겠다고 했으니까요.\"\n\n식탁에 머리를 떨구고 있던 카드루스가 이제 고개를 들더니, 흐리멍덩하고 멍한 눈으로 페르낭을 바라보며 말했다. \"당테스를 죽인다고! 누가 당테스를 죽인단 말이야? 난 그를 못 죽이게 할 거야, 안 돼! 그는 내 친구야. 오늘 아침에도 내가 그에게 그랬듯, 제 돈을 나랑 나누겠다고 했단 말이야. 난 당테스를 죽이게 두지 않아, 안 돼!\"\n\n\"누가 그를 죽인다고 했나, 이 멍청아?\" 당글라르가 대꾸했다. \"그냥 농담했던 거야. 그의 건강을 위해 마시기나 해.\" 그는 카드루스의 잔을 채우며 덧붙였다. \"그리고 우리 일에 끼어들지 마.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 당테스의 건강을 위하여!\" 카드루스가 잔을 비우며 말했다. \"그의 건강을 위하여! 그의 건강을, 만세!\"\n\n\"한데 수단은, 그 수단 말입니다?\" 페르낭이 말했다.\n\n\"자넨 아무것도 떠오른 게 없나?\" 당글라르가 물었다.\n\n\"없습니다! 당신이 찾아 주겠다고 하셨잖습니까.\"\n\n\"맞아.\" 당글라르가 대꾸했다. \"프랑스인이 에스파냐인보다 나은 점이 바로 이거지. 에스파냐인은 곱씹기만 하지만, 프랑스인은 만들어 내거든.\"\n\n\"그럼 만들어 내 보세요.\" 페르낭이 조바심을 내며 말했다.\n\n\"여보게,\" 당글라르가 종업원을 불렀다. \"펜이랑 잉크, 종이 좀 주게.\"\n\n\"펜, 잉크, 종이라.\" 페르낭이 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"그래. 난 회계 담당 아닌가. 펜, 잉크, 종이가 내 연장이지. 연장이 없으면 난 아무짝에도 쓸모없어.\"\n\n\"펜이랑 잉크, 종이 주게.\" 페르낭이 큰 소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"필요하신 게 저 식탁에 있습니다.\" 종업원이 말했다.\n\n\"이리 가져오게.\" 종업원이 시키는 대로 했다.\n\n\"생각해 보면 말이야,\" 카드루스가 종이 위에 손을 툭 떨어뜨리며 말했다. \"여기 이것들이야말로, 숲 모퉁이에 숨어 누굴 죽이려고 기다리는 것보다 더 확실하게 사람을 죽일 수 있는 물건이라고! 난 늘 칼이나 권총보다도 펜 한 자루, 잉크 한 병, 종이 한 장이 더 무서웠어.\"\n\n\"이 친구, 보기만큼 취하진 않았군.\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"술 좀 더 주게, 페르낭.\" 페르낭이 카드루스의 잔을 채우자, 골수 술꾼답게 카드루스는 종이에서 손을 떼고 잔을 움켜쥐었다.\n\n카탈루냐 사내는, 이 새로운 일격에 정신을 거의 잃다시피 한 카드루스가 잔을 식탁에 내려놓는, 아니 떨구는 것을 지켜보았다.\n\n\"자!\" 카탈루냐 사내는 마지막 한 잔 앞에서 카드루스의 이성이 마지막 불꽃마저 꺼져 가는 것을 보며 다시 입을 열었다.\n\n\"좋아, 그럼 이를테면 이렇게 말해 볼 수 있겠지.\" 당글라르가 말을 이었다. \"당테스가 방금 마친 것 같은 항해, 그러니까 엘바섬에 들렀던 항해를 한 뒤에, 누군가가 그를 보나파르트파 첩자라고 검찰관에게 고발한다면 말이야……\"\n\n\"내가 고발하겠습니다!\" 젊은이가 황급히 외쳤다.\n\n\"그래, 하지만 그러면 자네더러 진술서에 서명하게 하고, 자네가 고발한 자와 대질시킬 걸세. 나야 자네가 그 고발을 뒷받침할 수단을 마련해 주겠지. 그 사실을 잘 알고 있으니까. 하지만 당테스가 영영 감옥에 있을 수는 없는 노릇, 언젠가는 나올 거야. 그리고 그가 나오는 날, 그를 가둔 장본인에게 화가 미칠 걸세!\"\n\n\"오, 그가 와서 나한테 싸움을 걸어 준다면야 더 바랄 게 없죠.\"\n\n\"그래, 그럼 메르세데스는! 그녀가 그토록 사랑하는 에드몽의 살갗에 흠집 하나라도 내는 불행을 저지른다면, 메르세데스가 자넬 증오할 텐데!\"\n\n\"맞습니다!\" 페르낭이 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 아니야.\" 당글라르가 말을 이었다. \"그런 일을 벌이기로 했다면, 차라리 내가 지금 하는 것처럼 이 펜을 들어 잉크에 적시고, 왼손으로(필체를 알아보지 못하게) 우리가 꾸미는 고발장을 쓰는 편이 훨씬 낫지.\" 그러고는 당글라르는 이론에 실천을 곁들여, 왼손으로, 제 평소 필체와는 거꾸로, 전혀 딴판인 글씨로 다음과 같은 몇 줄을 써서 페르낭에게 건넸다. 페르낭은 그것을 낮은 목소리로 읽었다.\n\n\"왕좌와 종교의 벗 된 한 사람이 검찰관 각하께 알리는 바입니다. 파라옹호의 일등 항해사 에드몽 당테스라는 자가, 나폴리와 포르토페라요에 들른 뒤 오늘 아침 스미르나에서 입항하였으며, 뮈라에게서 찬탈자에게 전할 편지를, 또 찬탈자에게서 파리의 보나파르트파 위원회에 전할 편지를 맡았습니다. 이 죄의 증거는 그를 체포하면 드러날 것입니다. 그 편지는 그의 몸에서, 혹은 그의 아버지 집에서, 혹은 파라옹호의 그의 선실에서 발견될 것입니다.\"\n\n\"아주 좋아.\" 당글라르가 말을 이었다. \"이제 자네 복수도 제법 이치에 맞아 보이는군. 어떤 식으로도 자네한테 화살이 돌아오지 않을 테고, 일은 저절로 굴러가게 될 거야. 이제 할 일이라곤 내가 하는 대로 편지를 접어서, 그 위에 '검찰관 앞'이라고 쓰는 것뿐이지. 그럼 다 끝나는 거고.\" 당글라르는 말하면서 수신인을 적었다.\n\n\"그래, 이걸로 다 끝났군!\" 마지막 안간힘으로 정신을 차려 편지 읽는 소리를 따라가던 카드루스가 외쳤다. 그는 이런 고발이 불러올 온갖 참상을 본능적으로 깨달았다. \"그래, 이걸로 다 끝나. 다만 이건 파렴치한 짓이야.\" 그러고는 편지를 잡으려 손을 뻗었다.\n\n\"그렇지.\" 당글라르가 그의 손이 닿지 않는 곳으로 편지를 빼며 말했다. \"내가 말하고 행한 건 다 그저 농담일 뿐이야. 그리고 누구보다도 내가 가장 먼저, 당테스에게, 그 훌륭한 당테스에게 무슨 일이라도 생긴다면 가슴 아파할 사람이지. 자, 보라고!\" 그러고는 편지를 집어 두 손으로 구겨 정자 한구석에 던져 버렸다.\n\n\"좋았어!\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"당테스는 내 친구야. 그를 괴롭히게 두지 않을 거라고.\"\n\n\"누가 그를 괴롭힌단 말인가? 나도 페르낭도 분명 아니지.\" 당글라르가 일어서며, 여전히 앉은 채 한구석에 내던져진 고발장에 눈을 박고 있는 젊은이를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 카드루스가 대꾸했다. \"술이나 더 마시자고. 에드몽과 어여쁜 메르세데스의 건강을 위해 마시고 싶어.\"\n\n\"자넨 이미 너무 많이 마셨어, 이 술꾼아.\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"계속 마시다간 제 다리로 설 수도 없어서 여기서 자게 될 거야.\"\n\n\"내가?\" 카드루스가 술 취한 사람 특유의 모욕당한 위엄을 한껏 세우며 일어섰다. \"내가 다리로 못 선다고? 흥, 내기해도 좋아, 난 아쿨 종탑까지 올라갈 수 있어, 그것도 비틀거리지 않고!\"\n\n\"좋아!\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"그 내기 받지. 하지만 내일 하세. 오늘은 돌아갈 시간이야. 내 팔을 잡게, 가자고.\"\n\n\"좋아, 가자.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"근데 자네 팔 따윈 필요 없어. 자, 페르낭, 우리랑 마르세유로 안 돌아갈 텐가?\"\n\n\"안 갑니다.\" 페르낭이 말했다. \"전 카탈루냐 마을로 돌아갈 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그러면 안 되지. 우리랑 마르세유로 가자고, 어서.\"\n\n\"안 갑니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 소리야? 안 가겠다고? 그래, 좋을 대로 하시게, 우리 왕자님. 누구에게나 자유는 있는 법이니까. 가자고, 당글라르. 이 젊은 양반은 가고 싶다면 카탈루냐 마을로 가게 내버려 두지.\"\n\n당글라르는 그 순간 카드루스의 기분을 틈타, 비틀거리는 그를 데리고 생빅토르 문을 지나 마르세유 쪽으로 데려갔다.\n\n스무 걸음쯤 갔을 때, 당글라르가 뒤를 돌아보니 페르낭이 몸을 굽혀 구겨진 종이를 집어 주머니에 넣고는, 정자에서 뛰쳐나가 피용 쪽으로 달려가는 것이 보였다.\n\n\"이런,\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"원, 새빨간 거짓말을 했군! 카탈루냐 마을로 간다더니, 시내로 가고 있잖아. 어이, 페르낭! 너 길을 잘못 들었어, 이 친구야!\"\n\n\"오, 자네 눈이 비뚤어졌군.\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"저 친구는 비에유앵피르므리 길로 똑바로 갔는데.\"\n\n\"그래.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"난 분명 저 친구가 오른쪽으로 도는 걸 봤다고 맹세할 수도 있었는데. 참, 술이란 게 이렇게 사람을 속이는군!\"\n\n\"좋아, 좋아.\" 당글라르가 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다. '이제 일은 시작됐고, 누구 손도 빌리지 않고 제 갈 길을 갈 거야.'\n\n# Chapter 5. The Marriage Feast\n\n아침 해가 맑고 눈부시게 떠올라, 거품 이는 물결을 루비빛 그물망처럼 물들였다.\n\n잔치는 라레제르브의 이층에 차려졌다. 독자들은 그 정자를 이미 알고 있다. 잔치를 위해 마련된 방은 널찍했고, 여러 개의 창문으로 빛이 들어왔다. 무슨 까닭에서인지 알 수 없지만 창마다 위에는 프랑스 주요 도시들의 이름이 하나씩 금빛 글자로 적혀 있었다. 그 창들 아래로는 나무 발코니가 집 전체 길이만큼 뻗어 있었다. 잔치는 정오로 정해져 있었지만, 그보다 한 시간 전부터 발코니는 조바심 내며 기다리는 손님들로 가득 찼다. 파라옹호 선원들 가운데 특별히 초대받은 이들과 신랑의 다른 가까운 벗들이었다. 모두들 이 자리를 더욱 빛내려고 가장 좋은 옷차림으로 단장하고 나섰다.\n\n파라옹호의 선주들이 혼인 잔치에 참석하기로 약속했다는 소문이 여기저기 떠돌았다. 그러나 그토록 드물고 과분한 호의가 정말로 베풀어지리라는 데에는 모두가 한결같이 의심을 품는 듯했다.\n\n그런데 카드루스를 데리고 나타난 당글라르가 그 소문을 확실하게 못 박았다. 그는 얼마 전 모렐 씨와 이야기를 나누었는데, 모렐 씨가 라레제르브에서 저녁을 들겠다고 직접 장담했다는 것이다.\n\n과연 잠시 뒤 모렐 씨가 모습을 드러냈고, 파라옹호 선원들의 열렬한 박수갈채가 그를 맞았다. 선원들은 선주의 방문을, 그가 이렇게 기꺼이 혼인 잔치를 빛내 주려는 그 사내가 머지않아 배의 첫 지휘권을 맡게 되리라는 확실한 징표로 받아들였다. 당테스는 배에서 두루 사랑받는 사람이었기에, 선원들은 윗사람들의 생각과 선택이 자신들의 것과 이토록 꼭 들어맞는 것을 알고 터져 나오는 기쁨을 조금도 억누르지 않았다.\n\n모렐 씨가 들어오자, 당글라르와 카드루스는 신랑을 찾으러 보내졌다. 그토록 떠들썩한 화제를 불러일으킨 귀빈이 도착했음을 신랑에게 알리고, 서둘러 오라고 청하기 위해서였다.\n\n당글라르와 카드루스는 곧장 심부름을 하러 전속력으로 달려 나갔다. 그러나 몇 걸음 가기도 전에 자기들 쪽으로 다가오는 한 무리를 보았다. 약혼한 한 쌍과 신부를 모시는 처녀들의 행렬이었고, 그 곁으로는 당테스의 아버지가 걸어왔다. 행렬의 맨 뒤를 페르낭이 따랐는데, 그의 입가에는 여느 때처럼 음험한 미소가 걸려 있었다.\n\n메르세데스도 에드몽도 그의 얼굴에 떠오른 그 야릇한 표정을 알아채지 못했다. 두 사람은 너무 행복해서 햇살과 서로의 존재만을 느낄 뿐이었다.\n\n심부름을 마치고 에드몽과 정겹게 손을 맞잡은 뒤, 당글라르와 카드루스는 페르낭과 늙은 당테스 곁에 자리를 잡았다. 늙은 당테스는 모두의 시선을 한 몸에 끌었다.\n\n노인은 반짝이는 물결무늬 비단 양복을 입고 있었는데, 곱게 재단하고 광을 낸 강철 단추로 장식되어 있었다. 가늘지만 다부진 두 다리에는 화려한 자수가 놓인, 한눈에 영국제임을 알 수 있는 줄무늬 양말을 신었고, 삼각모에서는 희고 푸른 리본 매듭이 길게 늘어져 나부꼈다. 이렇게 차려입은 그는 정교하게 조각된 지팡이에 몸을 의지한 채, 늙은 얼굴에 행복이 환하게 피어난 모습으로 다가왔다. 그 모습은 영락없이 1796년 무렵의 늙은 멋쟁이가 막 문을 연 뤽상부르나 튈르리 정원을 으스대며 거니는 것 같았다.\n\n그 곁으로는 카드루스가 슬그머니 따라왔다. 혼인 잔치에 차려진 좋은 음식을 맛보고 싶다는 욕심에, 그는 당테스 부자와 화해하기로 한 터였다. 그래도 그의 마음 한구석에는 지난밤 일에 대한 희미하고 어렴풋한 기억이 남아 있었다. 마치 아침에 잠에서 깬 뒤에도 머릿속에 흐릿하게 어른거리는 꿈의 윤곽처럼.\n\n당글라르는 실연당한 사내에게 다가가며 깊은 뜻이 담긴 눈길을 던졌다. 페르낭은 행복한 한 쌍의 뒤를 천천히 걸어왔다. 두 사람은 자기들만의 순수한 만족에 빠져, 페르낭 같은 존재가 있다는 사실조차 까맣게 잊은 듯했다. 그는 창백한 얼굴로 넋이 나가 있었다. 그러다 이따금 얼굴에 시뻘건 홍조가 번지고, 신경질적인 경련이 이목구비를 일그러뜨렸다. 그럴 때면 그는 불안하고 초조한 눈길로 마르세유 쪽을 흘끔거렸다. 어떤 크고 중대한 일이 일어나기를 기다리거나, 아니면 미리 내다보고 있는 사람처럼.\n\n당테스 자신은 수수하지만 어울리는 차림이었다. 상선 선원 특유의 복장으로, 군복과 평상복의 중간쯤 되는 옷이었다. 기쁨과 행복으로 환히 빛나는 잘생긴 얼굴과 어우러져, 그보다 더 완벽한 남성미의 본보기는 좀처럼 떠올리기 어려울 정도였다.\n\n키프로스나 키오스섬의 그리스 처녀들만큼이나 아리따운 메르세데스는 그들과 똑같이 까만 눈을 반짝였고, 무르익은 둥근 산호빛 입술을 지녔다. 그녀는 아를 여인이나 안달루시아 여인처럼 가볍고 자유로운 걸음으로 움직였다. 대도시의 처세술에 더 능한 여자였다면 베일로 홍조를 가리거나, 적어도 짙은 속눈썹을 내리깔아 생기 넘치는 눈의 촉촉한 광채를 감추었을 것이다. 그러나 정반대로, 기쁨에 찬 처녀는 이렇게 말하는 듯한 미소를 띠고 주위를 둘러보았다. \"당신들이 내 벗이라면, 나와 함께 기뻐해 주세요. 저는 너무나 행복하니까요.\"\n\n혼인 행렬이 라레제르브에 모습을 드러내자마자, 모렐 씨가 내려와 맞으러 나왔다. 그곳에 모인 군인들과 선원들이 그 뒤를 따랐는데, 그는 그들에게 당테스가 작고한 르클레르 선장의 후임이 되리라는, 이미 한 약속을 다시 한번 들려준 터였다. 자신의 후원자가 다가오자, 에드몽은 공손하게 약혼녀의 팔을 모렐 씨의 팔에 끼워 주었다. 모렐 씨는 곧장 그녀를 이끌어 잔치가 마련된 방으로 통하는 나무 계단을 올랐고, 손님들이 흥겹게 그 뒤를 따랐다. 그 묵직한 발걸음에 짓눌려 부실한 계단은 몇 분 동안이나 삐걱대며 신음했다.\n\n\"아버님,\" 메르세데스가 식탁 한가운데에 이르러 걸음을 멈추며 말했다. \"부디 제 오른편에 앉으세요. 왼편에는 늘 저에게 오라버니 같았던 분을 모실게요.\" 그녀는 부드럽고 다정한 미소를 지으며 페르낭을 가리켰다. 그러나 그녀의 말과 눈길은 페르낭에게 더없이 끔찍한 고통을 안기는 듯했다. 그의 입술이 끔찍하게 창백해졌고, 가무잡잡한 얼굴빛 아래에서조차 피가 물러나는 것이 보였다. 마치 갑작스러운 격통이 피를 심장으로 되몰아넣는 것 같았다.\n\n그동안 식탁 맞은편에서는 당테스가 자기 쪽 귀빈들을 비슷하게 자리에 앉히고 있었다. 모렐 씨를 오른편에, 당글라르를 왼편에 앉혔다. 그리고 에드몽이 눈짓을 하자, 나머지 사람들은 저마다 편한 대로 자리를 잡았다.\n\n이윽고 거뭇하고 매콤한 아를식 소시지가 돌려졌고, 눈부신 붉은 갑옷을 두른 바닷가재, 큼지막하고 빛깔 고운 새우, 가시투성이 겉껍질 속에 입에 살살 녹는 살을 품은 성게, 남부의 미식가들이 북부의 굴 못지않은 절묘한 맛으로 친다는 조개도 차례로 돌았다. 한마디로 물결에 밀려 모래밭에 올라오는 온갖 진미였으니, 고마운 어부들이 \"바다의 열매\"라 부르는 것들이었다.\n\n\"참으로 곱기도 한 침묵이로구나!\" 신랑의 늙은 아버지가 말했다. 그는 토파즈처럼 빛깔 곱고 환한 포도주 한 잔을 입가로 가져갔는데, 마침 메르세데스 앞에 놓여 있던 잔이었다. \"자, 이 방에 웃고 춤추며 시간을 보내는 것보다 더 바라는 게 없는, 행복하고 흥겨운 손님들이 모여 있다고 누가 생각이나 하겠나?\"\n\n\"아,\" 카드루스가 한숨지었다. \"결혼을 앞두고 있다고 해서 사람이 늘 행복할 수만은 없는 법이지요.\"\n\n\"사실은,\" 당테스가 대답했다. \"제가 너무 행복해서 떠들썩하게 즐기지 못하는 겁니다. 그게 자네가 말하려던 거라면, 친애하는 친구, 자네 말이 옳아. 기쁨이란 때때로 야릇한 작용을 해서, 슬픔만큼이나 사람을 짓누르는 것 같으니 말이야.\"\n\n당글라르는 페르낭 쪽을 바라보았다. 그 격하기 쉬운 천성은 새로운 인상을 하나하나 받아들이고 그대로 드러냈다.\n\n\"아니, 자넨 무슨 일인가?\" 그가 에드몽에게 물었다. \"무슨 불행이라도 닥칠까 두려운가? 내가 보기엔 지금 이 순간 자네야말로 세상에서 가장 행복한 사람일 텐데.\"\n\n\"바로 그게 저를 두렵게 합니다.\" 당테스가 대답했다. \"사람은 이토록 순수한 행복을 누리도록 만들어진 존재가 아닌 것 같거든요. 행복이란 우리가 어린 시절 이야기로 읽던 마법의 궁전 같은 것입니다. 사납고 불을 뿜는 용이 입구와 길목을 지키고, 온갖 모양과 종류의 괴물들이 버티고 있어, 승리를 거머쥐기 전에 그것들을 모두 물리쳐야 하지요. 솔직히 저는 제가 어울리지 않는다고 여기는 영예, 곧 메르세데스의 남편이 되는 영예로 올라선 것이 그저 놀랍기만 합니다.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니지!\" 카드루스가 웃으며 외쳤다. \"자네는 아직 그 영예에 이르지 못했네. 메르세데스는 아직 자네 아내가 아니야. 어디 한번 남편 같은 말투와 태도를 부려 보게. 그러면 자네 차례가 아직 오지 않았다고 그녀가 일깨워 줄 테니!\"\n\n신부는 얼굴을 붉혔고, 페르낭은 안절부절 불안해하며 무슨 소리가 날 때마다 흠칫 놀라는 듯했다. 그러고는 이따금 이마에 맺힌 굵은 땀방울을 닦아 냈다.\n\n\"뭐, 그건 괜찮네, 이웃 카드루스. 그런 사소한 일로 내 말에 토를 달 것까진 없지. 메르세데스가 아직 정말로 내 아내가 아닌 건 맞아. 하지만,\" 그가 회중시계를 꺼내며 덧붙였다. \"한 시간 반 뒤면 내 아내가 될 걸세.\"\n\n늙은 당테스만 빼고 식탁에 빙 둘러앉은 사람들 사이에서 놀라움의 탄성이 터졌다. 노인은 웃음을 터뜨렸는데, 여전히 완벽하게 고운 큼직하고 새하얀 이가 드러났다. 메르세데스는 흐뭇하고 만족스러운 표정이었고, 페르낭은 경련하듯 칼자루를 부여잡았다.\n\n\"한 시간 뒤라고?\" 당글라르가 창백해지며 물었다. \"그게 어떻게 된 일인가, 친구?\"\n\n\"이렇게 된 거지.\" 당테스가 대답했다. \"아버지 다음으로 제가 누리는 모든 복을 빚지고 있는 모렐 씨의 힘 덕분에, 모든 걸림돌이 치워졌습니다. 으레 거쳐야 하는 대기 기간을 면제받도록 허가를 사 두었거든요. 두 시 반에 마르세유 시장님이 시청에서 저희를 기다리실 겁니다. 자, 벌써 한 시 십오 분을 알리는 종이 울렸으니, 한 시간 삼십 분 뒤면 메르세데스가 당테스 부인이 되어 있으리라고 말해도 지나친 장담은 아니겠지요.\"\n\n페르낭은 두 눈을 감았다. 이마를 타고 타는 듯한 감각이 스쳤고, 그는 의자에서 굴러떨어지지 않으려고 식탁을 짚어 몸을 가눠야 했다. 그러나 아무리 안간힘을 써도 깊은 신음만은 새어 나오는 것을 막을 수 없었다. 다만 그 소리는 떠들썩한 손님들의 축하 속에 묻혀 버렸다.\n\n\"맹세코,\" 노인이 외쳤다. \"자네는 이런 일을 참 후딱 해치우는구먼. 어제 아침에야 도착해서 오늘 세 시에 결혼이라니! 일을 빨리 해치우는 데는 역시 선원이 으뜸이라니까!\"\n\n\"그런데,\" 당글라르가 조심스러운 말투로 물었다. \"다른 절차들은 어떻게 처리했나? 혼인 계약이라든가, 재산 정리 말일세.\"\n\n\"혼인 계약이라,\" 당테스가 웃으며 대답했다. \"그건 정하는 데 오래 걸리지도 않았네. 메르세데스에게는 재산이 없고, 나도 그녀에게 정해 줄 재산이 없으니까. 그러니 알겠나, 서류는 금세 작성됐고, 돈도 그리 많이 들지 않았지.\" 이 농담에 또 한 차례 박수가 터졌다.\n\n\"그렇다면 우리가 그저 약혼 잔치인 줄 알았던 게 사실은 진짜 결혼 만찬이었군!\" 당글라르가 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 아니지.\" 당테스가 대답했다. \"내가 자네들을 그렇게 야박하게 떼어 보내리라고는 생각지 말게. 내일 아침 나는 파리로 떠나네. 가는 데 나흘, 오는 데 나흘, 거기다 내게 맡겨진 심부름을 처리하는 데 하루를 더하면, 그게 내가 자리를 비우는 시간 전부일세. 3월 1일까지는 이곳에 돌아올 테고, 2일에는 진짜 결혼 잔치를 열겠네.\"\n\n새로운 잔치가 또 있으리라는 기대에 손님들의 흥은 한층 더 달아올랐다. 그래서 식사가 시작될 무렵 침묵이 흐른다고 한마디 했던 늙은 당테스는, 이제 사방에서 떠들어대는 목소리들 틈에서 신랑 신부의 건강과 번영을 비는 건배를 위해 잠깐의 고요를 얻기조차 힘들 지경이었다.\n\n당테스는 아버지의 애정 어린 열의를 알아채고, 고마움과 기쁨이 담긴 눈길로 화답했다. 메르세데스는 시계를 흘끗 보고는 에드몽에게 의미심장한 몸짓을 했다.\n\n식탁 주위로는 이런 자리에서 으레 감도는 떠들썩한 흥겨움이 넘쳤다. 사회적 지위의 굴레에 매이지 않을 만큼 자유로운 사람들 사이에서나 볼 수 있는 흥이었다. 식사가 시작될 때 마음에 드는 자리에 앉지 못했던 이들은 거리낌 없이 일어나 더 마음 맞는 짝을 찾아갔다. 모두가 대답을 기다리지도 않고 한꺼번에 떠들어댔으며, 저마다 제 생각을 입 밖에 내는 것만으로 만족하는 듯했다.\n\n페르낭의 창백한 기운이 당글라르에게도 옮은 듯했다. 페르낭 자신으로 말하자면, 그는 지옥에 떨어진 자의 고통을 견디는 것 같았다. 한자리에 가만히 있지 못하고 가장 먼저 식탁을 떠난 그는, 귀가 먹먹할 만큼 요란하게 솟구치는 흥겨운 웃음소리를 피하려는 듯, 완전한 침묵 속에서 홀의 저쪽 끝을 계속 서성였다.\n\n페르낭이 한사코 피하려는 눈치였던 당글라르가 방 한구석에서 그에게 다가가던 바로 그때, 카드루스가 그쪽으로 걸어왔다.\n\n\"맹세코,\" 카드루스가 말했다. 그의 마음속에서는 당테스의 다정한 대접과 실컷 마신 좋은 포도주의 기운이 어우러져, 당테스의 행운에 대한 시샘이나 질투의 감정이 말끔히 지워진 터였다. \"맹세코, 당테스는 정말이지 더없이 좋은 친구야. 곧 자기 아내가 될 저 어여쁜 색시 곁에 앉아 있는 걸 보면, 어제 자네가 꾸미던 그런 장난을 저 친구에게 쳤더라면 정말 못할 짓이었겠다 싶은 생각이 절로 든다니까.\"\n\n\"아, 나쁜 뜻은 없었네.\" 당글라르가 대답했다. \"처음에는 페르낭이 무슨 짓을 저지를까 솔직히 좀 불안하긴 했어. 하지만 그가 제 감정을 완전히 다스려, 연적의 들러리 노릇까지 하는 걸 보고 나니 더는 걱정할 게 없다는 걸 알았지.\" 카드루스가 페르낭을 똑바로 바라보았다. 그는 끔찍하게 창백했다.\n\n\"하긴,\" 당글라르가 말을 이었다. \"신부가 저렇게 아리따우니, 그 희생이 결코 가벼운 건 아니었지. 정말이지, 앞으로 내 선장이 될 저 친구는 운수 좋은 녀석이야! 이런! 그가 나더러 자기 자리를 대신하라고 해 주기만 한다면 좋으련만.\"\n\n\"이제 출발하지 않으실 거예요?\" 메르세데스의 곱고 은쟁반 같은 목소리가 물었다. \"방금 두 시 종이 울렸어요. 다들 아시다시피 십오 분 뒤에 우리를 기다리고 있잖아요.\"\n\n\"그렇고말고! 그렇고말고!\" 당테스가 신이 나서 식탁에서 일어나며 외쳤다. \"어서 갑시다!\"\n\n그의 말은 손님들 전체가 우렁찬 환호로 받아 메아리쳤다.\n\n바로 그 순간, 페르낭의 표정과 태도의 변화를 줄곧 살피고 있던 당글라르는, 그가 비틀거리다가 거의 경련하듯 발작을 일으키며 열린 창문 곁에 놓인 의자에 쓰러지듯 기대는 것을 보았다. 같은 순간 그의 귀에 계단 쪽에서 무언가 어렴풋한 소리가 들렸고, 뒤이어 군화의 박자 맞춘 발소리와 칼, 군장이 부딪치는 쩔그렁 소리가 들려왔다. 그러고는 여럿이 웅성거리는 듯한 소리가 일어, 혼인 잔치의 떠들썩한 흥겨움마저 잦아들게 했다. 손님들 사이에서는 막연한 호기심과 불안이 떠들고 싶은 마음을 모조리 짓눌렀고, 거의 순식간에 죽음 같은 정적이 감돌았다.\n\n소리가 점점 가까워졌다. 문짝을 세 번 두드리는 소리가 났다. 손님들은 서로 얼굴을 바라보며 어쩔 줄 몰라 했다.\n\n\"문을 여시오.\" 방 밖에서 우렁찬 목소리가 말했다. \"법의 이름으로!\" 아무도 막으려 하지 않자, 문이 열리며 관복 띠를 두른 사법관이 모습을 드러냈고, 그 뒤로 병사 넷과 하사 하나가 따랐다. 자리에 있던 사람들의 불안은 이제 더없이 극심한 공포로 바뀌었다.\n\n\"이 뜻밖의 방문이 무슨 까닭인지 여쭤도 되겠습니까?\" 모렐 씨가 사법관에게 물었다. 그는 분명 그를 아는 사이였다. \"필시 쉽게 풀릴 무슨 착오가 있는 것이겠지요.\"\n\n\"만약 그렇다면,\" 사법관이 대답했다. \"남김없이 바로잡힐 거라 믿으셔도 좋습니다. 그동안 저는 체포 명령을 집행하러 왔고, 비록 제게 맡겨진 이 일이 더없이 내키지 않지만, 그래도 이행해야 합니다. 여기 모이신 분들 가운데 에드몽 당테스라는 이름을 쓰는 분이 누구입니까?\"\n\n모두의 시선이 젊은이에게로 쏠렸다. 그는 동요를 느끼지 않을 수 없으면서도 위엄 있게 앞으로 나서며, 굳은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"제가 바로 그 사람입니다. 제게 무슨 볼일이십니까?\"\n\n\"에드몽 당테스,\" 사법관이 대답했다. \"법의 이름으로 당신을 체포합니다!\"\n\n\"저를요!\" 에드몽이 약간 안색이 변하며 되뇌었다. \"대체 무슨 까닭입니까?\"\n\n\"제가 알려 드릴 수는 없습니다. 다만 예심에서 이러한 조치가 필요해진 이유를 정식으로 알게 되실 겁니다.\"\n\n모렐 씨는 더 버티거나 항의해 봐야 소용없음을 깨달았다. 그의 앞에는 법을 집행하도록 위임받은 관리가 있었고, 관복 띠를 두른 사법관에게 동정을 구하는 것은 차디찬 대리석상에 탄원하는 것만큼이나 부질없음을 그는 너무도 잘 알았다. 그러나 늙은 당테스는 앞으로 뛰쳐나갔다. 아버지나 어머니의 마음으로는 도저히 헤아릴 수 없는 상황이라는 게 있는 법이다. 그는 어찌나 절절한 말로 빌고 애원했던지, 관리마저 마음이 움직일 정도였다. 그는 직무에는 단호했지만, 친절하게 말했다. \"친애하는 분, 부디 마음을 가라앉히시지요. 아드님은 아마 화물을 등록하면서 정해진 어떤 형식이나 절차를 빠뜨렸을 겁니다. 선원들의 건강에 관해서든 화물의 가치에 관해서든, 요구되는 정보를 제출하기만 하면 곧바로 풀려날 가능성이 큽니다.\"\n\n\"이게 다 무슨 영문이오?\" 카드루스가 잔뜩 놀란 표정을 지어 보이는 당글라르에게 인상을 찌푸리며 물었다.\n\n\"낸들 어떻게 알겠나?\" 그가 대답했다. \"나도 자네처럼 이게 다 무슨 일인지 도무지 어리둥절할 뿐, 무슨 영문인지 조금도 짚이는 게 없네.\" 그러자 카드루스는 페르낭을 찾아 주위를 둘러보았다. 그러나 그는 사라지고 없었다.\n\n그 순간 지난밤의 장면이 놀랍도록 또렷하게 그의 머릿속으로 되돌아왔다. 방금 목격한 가슴 아픈 변고가, 전날 밤의 취기가 그와 기억 사이에 드리워 놓았던 장막을 완전히 찢어 버린 듯했다.\n\n\"그러니까, 그러니까,\" 그가 쉬고 막힌 목소리로 당글라르에게 말했다. \"이게 결국, 어제 자네가 꾸미던 그 장난의 일부란 말이지? 내가 할 수 있는 말은, 정말 그렇다면 이건 고약한 짓이고, 그런 일을 꾀한 자들에게는 갑절의 화가 내려도 마땅하다는 거야.\"\n\n\"말도 안 되는 소리.\" 당글라르가 대꾸했다. \"다시 말하지만 나는 그 일과 아무 상관도 없네. 게다가 자네도 잘 알다시피, 내가 그 종이를 갈기갈기 찢어 버렸잖나.\"\n\n\"아니, 안 찢었어!\" 카드루스가 대답했다. \"자네는 그냥 그걸 던져 버렸을 뿐이야. 한구석에 떨어져 있는 걸 내가 봤다고.\"\n\n\"입 닥치게, 이 멍청아! 자네가 뭘 안다고 그래? 자넨 그때 취해 있었잖나!\"\n\n\"페르낭은 어디 있나?\" 카드루스가 물었다.\n\n\"낸들 어찌 아나?\" 당글라르가 대답했다. \"신중한 사람이라면 누구나 그러듯, 제 일을 챙기러 갔겠지. 어디 있든 신경 쓰지 말고, 자네하고 나하고 우리 가엾은 친구들을 위해 뭘 할 수 있을지나 보러 가세.\"\n\n이런 대화가 오가는 동안, 당테스는 동정 어린 벗들 모두와 정겹게 손을 맞잡고 나서, 자신을 체포하러 온 관리에게 순순히 몸을 맡겼다. 그저 이렇게 말할 뿐이었다. \"마음들 푹 놓게, 친구들. 풀어야 할 자그마한 착오가 있을 뿐이야, 그게 전부니까 믿어 주게. 어쩌면 그걸 풀려고 감옥까지 갈 것도 없을지 몰라.\"\n\n\"오, 그렇고말고!\" 어느새 무리 곁으로 다가온 당글라르가 거들었다. \"착오일 뿐이야, 나는 확신하네.\"\n\n당테스는 사법관을 앞세우고 병사들을 뒤따르게 한 채 계단을 내려갔다. 문 앞에는 마차가 기다리고 있었다. 그가 올라타자 병사 둘과 사법관이 뒤따라 탔고, 마차는 마르세유를 향해 달려갔다.\n\n\"안녕, 안녕, 사랑하는 에드몽!\" 메르세데스가 발코니에서 두 팔을 그에게 뻗으며 외쳤다.\n\n죄수는 그 외침을 들었다. 그것은 찢긴 가슴이 터뜨리는 흐느낌처럼 들렸다. 그는 마차 밖으로 몸을 내밀고 소리쳤다. \"잘 있어요, 메르세데스. 우리 곧 다시 만납시다!\" 그러고는 마차가 생니콜라 요새의 모퉁이 하나를 돌아 사라졌다.\n\n\"다들 여기서 나를 기다려 주시오!\" 모렐 씨가 외쳤다. \"내가 제일 먼저 눈에 띄는 마차를 잡아타고 마르세유로 서둘러 가서, 일이 어떻게 돌아가는지 소식을 가져오겠소.\"\n\n\"옳습니다!\" 여러 목소리가 한꺼번에 외쳤다. \"어서 다녀오세요, 되도록 빨리요!\"\n\n이 두 번째 출발 뒤로, 남겨진 사람들 사이에는 겁에 질린 침묵이 길고도 무섭게 이어졌다. 늙은 아버지와 메르세데스는 한동안 따로 떨어진 채 저마다 슬픔에 잠겨 있었다. 그러나 마침내 같은 일격에 쓰러진 두 가엾은 희생자는 눈을 들었고, 동시에 북받치는 감정으로 서로의 품에 뛰어들었다.\n\n그사이 페르낭이 모습을 드러내, 떨리는 손으로 물을 한 잔 따랐다. 그러고는 황급히 들이켜고 가장 가까운 빈자리에 가서 앉았는데, 그곳은 우연히도 가엾은 메르세데스가 앉은 자리 바로 옆이었다. 그녀는 늙은 당테스의 따뜻하고 다정한 포옹에서 풀려나자 반쯤 정신을 잃고 그 자리에 쓰러져 있었다. 페르낭은 본능적으로 의자를 뒤로 물렸다.\n\n\"저자가 이 모든 불행의 원흉이야. 나는 그걸 확신하네.\" 페르낭에게서 한순간도 눈을 떼지 않던 카드루스가 당글라르에게 속삭였다.\n\n\"난 그렇게 생각 안 해.\" 상대가 대답했다. \"저 친구는 그런 계략을 짜내기엔 너무 멍청해. 나는 그저 그 짓을 꾸민 자가 누구든 그 머리 위로 화가 떨어지기만 바랄 뿐이야.\"\n\n\"그 일을 거들고 부추긴 자들은 입에 올리지 않는군.\" 카드루스가 말했다.\n\n\"이보게,\" 당글라르가 대답했다. \"허공에 쏘아 올린 화살이 어디로 떨어지든 그 책임을 다 질 수야 없는 노릇 아닌가.\"\n\n\"질 수 있지, 그 화살촉이 아래로 떨어져 누군가의 머리에 꽂힌다면 말이야.\"\n\n그러는 동안 체포 사건은 온갖 형태로 입에 오르내리고 있었다.\n\n\"당글라르, 자네는 이 일을 어떻게 보나?\" 일행 가운데 한 사람이 그를 돌아보며 물었다.\n\n\"글쎄,\" 그가 대답했다. \"내 생각엔 당테스가 배에 여기서 금제품으로 여겨질 만한 자그마한 물건을 싣고 있다가 들켰을 가능성이 있어 보여.\"\n\n\"하지만 자네가 배의 회계 책임자인데, 어떻게 자네 모르게 그런 짓을 할 수 있었겠나, 당글라르?\"\n\n\"뭐, 그거야, 나는 배에 실린 상품에 관해 들은 것만 알 뿐이지. 면화를 실었다는 것, 그 화물을 알렉산드리아에서는 파스트레 창고에서, 스미르나에서는 파스칼 창고에서 실었다는 것, 그게 내가 알아야 할 전부였고, 더 자세한 건 묻지 말아 주길 바라네.\"\n\n\"이제 생각나는군.\" 비탄에 잠긴 늙은 아버지가 말했다. \"내 가엾은 아들이 어제 그러더군, 나한테 줄 자그마한 커피 한 상자랑 담배 한 상자를 가져왔다고!\"\n\n\"거 보세요.\" 당글라르가 외쳤다. \"이제 화근이 드러났군요. 분명 우리가 없는 사이에 세관 사람들이 배를 샅샅이 뒤지다가 가엾은 당테스가 감춰 둔 보물을 찾아낸 겁니다.\"\n\n그러나 메르세데스는 연인의 체포에 대한 이런 설명에 조금도 귀 기울이지 않았다. 그때까지 억누르려 애써 온 그녀의 슬픔이, 이제 격렬한 흐느낌 발작으로 터져 나왔다.\n\n\"자, 자,\" 노인이 말했다. \"기운을 내거라, 가엾은 것아. 아직 희망이 있단다!\"\n\n\"희망이라!\" 당글라르가 되뇌었다.\n\n\"희망이라……\" 페르낭이 희미하게 중얼거렸다. 그러나 그 말은 창백하게 떨리는 입술 위에서 그대로 잦아드는 듯했고, 경련의 발작이 그의 얼굴을 스쳐 지나갔다.\n\n\"좋은 소식이오! 좋은 소식!\" 발코니에서 망을 보던 일행 가운데 한 사람이 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"모렐 씨가 돌아오시는군. 이제 틀림없이 우리 친구가 풀려났다는 소식을 듣게 될 거요!\"\n\n메르세데스와 노인이 선주를 맞으러 달려가, 문간에서 그를 맞았다. 그는 몹시 창백했다.\n\n\"무슨 소식입니까?\" 여러 목소리가 한꺼번에 터져 나왔다.\n\n\"아아, 여러분.\" 모렐 씨가 슬프게 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"내가 생각했던 것보다 일이 더 심각해졌소.\"\n\n\"오, 정말로, 정말로요, 선주님, 그이는 죄가 없어요!\" 메르세데스가 흐느끼며 말했다.\n\n\"나도 그렇게 믿소!\" 모렐 씨가 대답했다. \"하지만 그래도 그는 혐의를 받고 있소……\"\n\n\"무슨 혐의요?\" 늙은 당테스가 물었다.\n\n\"보나파르트파의 첩자라는 혐의요!\" 우리 이야기가 펼쳐지는 시대에 그런 고발이 얼마나 무시무시한 것이었는지, 독자들 가운데 많은 분이 기억할 수 있을 것이다.\n\n메르세데스의 창백한 입술에서 절망의 외침이 새어 나왔다. 노인은 의자에 털썩 주저앉았다.\n\n\"아, 당글라르!\" 카드루스가 속삭였다. \"자네가 나를 속였어. 어젯밤 자네가 말한 그 장난이 결국 벌어진 거야. 하지만 나는 자네 잘못 때문에 가엾은 노인이나 죄 없는 처녀가 슬픔으로 죽어 가는 걸 그냥 두고 볼 수는 없네. 모든 걸 다 털어놓고 말겠어.\"\n\n\"입 닥치게, 이 얼간이야!\" 당글라르가 그의 팔을 움켜쥐며 외쳤다. \"안 그러면 자네 목숨도 내가 책임 못 져. 당테스가 죄가 없는지 있는지 누가 알겠나? 배는 분명 엘바섬에 들렀고, 거기서 당테스가 내려 섬에서 꼬박 하루를 보냈어. 자, 그에게서 무슨 편지나 그 밖에 위험한 문서라도 나온다면, 그를 두둔하는 자들은 모조리 한패로 여겨지지 않겠나?\"\n\n이기심에서 비롯된 빠른 직감으로, 카드루스는 이내 그 논리의 단단함을 알아챘다. 그는 미심쩍은 듯, 안타까운 듯 당글라르를 바라보았다. 그러고는 조심성이 의협심을 밀어냈다.\n\n\"잠시 기다리면서 어떻게 되어 가는지 두고 보세.\" 그가 어리둥절한 눈길로 동료를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇고말고!\" 당글라르가 대답했다. \"무슨 일이 있어도 기다려 보세. 그가 죄가 없다면 당연히 풀려날 테고, 죄가 있다면, 뭐, 우리가 음모에 휘말려 봐야 좋을 게 없지.\"\n\n\"그럼 가세. 나는 더 이상 여기 못 있겠어.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그러지!\" 당글라르가 상대가 이렇게 고분고분한 게 흡족해서 대답했다. \"우리는 슬그머니 빠져나가고, 일은 당분간 흘러가는 대로 내버려 두세.\"\n\n두 사람이 떠난 뒤, 다시 메르세데스의 벗이자 보호자가 된 페르낭은 처녀를 집까지 데려다주었고, 당테스의 벗 몇몇은 거의 송장이나 다름없는 그의 아버지를 메이앙 가로수길까지 모셔 갔다.\n\n에드몽이 보나파르트파 첩자로 체포되었다는 소문은 어느새 도시 전체로 퍼져 나갔다.\n\n\"이런 일이 있으리라고 자네는 짐작이나 했겠나, 친애하는 당글라르?\" 모렐 씨가 물었다. 그는 차장검사 빌포르 씨에게서 당테스에 관한 새 소식을 알아보려고 항구로 돌아오던 길에, 회계 책임자와 카드루스를 따라잡은 참이었다. \"이런 일이 가능하리라고 믿을 수 있었겠나?\"\n\n\"뭐, 제가 말씀드렸잖습니까.\" 당글라르가 대답했다. \"그가 엘바섬에 닻을 내렸다는 게 무척 수상한 정황이라고 여겼다고 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그런데 그 의심을 나 말고 다른 사람에게도 이야기했나?\"\n\n\"천만에요!\" 당글라르가 대답했다. 그러더니 낮은 목소리로 속삭이듯 덧붙였다. \"아시겠지만, 다른 정부 아래에서 복무하셨고 그 일에 대해 자기 생각을 그다지 숨기지 않으시는 백부님, 폴리카르 모렐 씨 때문에, 선주님께서 나폴레옹의 퇴위를 아쉬워하신다는 의심을 단단히 받고 계십니다. 제가 제 우려를 누구한테든 발설했다면, 에드몽과 선주님 두 분 모두에게 해를 끼쳤을까 봐 두려웠던 거지요. 저야 저 같은 아랫사람은 일어나는 모든 일을 선주께 알릴 의무가 있지만, 그 밖의 사람들에게는 더없이 조심해서 감춰야 할 일들이 많다는 걸 너무도 잘 알고 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"잘했네, 당글라르, 잘했어!\" 모렐 씨가 대답했다. \"자네는 쓸 만한 친구야. 가엾은 에드몽이 파라옹호의 선장이 될 경우에 대비해서, 나는 이미 자네 앞날도 생각해 두고 있었네.\"\n\n\"그토록 마음 써 주셨단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"그래, 정말이지. 진작에 당테스에게 자네를 어떻게 생각하는지, 또 자네를 그 자리에 계속 두는 데 무슨 거리낌이라도 있는지 물어봤네. 어쩐지 자네들 사이에 일종의 서먹함이 느껴졌거든.\"\n\n\"그래서 그가 뭐라고 대답하던가요?\"\n\n\"자기가 자세한 내막은 그저 넌지시 언급만 하고 들어가지 않은 어떤 일로 자네 기분을 상하게 한 것 같다고 분명히 인정하더군. 하지만 선주들의 호평과 신임을 받는 사람이라면 누구든 자기도 그를 우선시하겠다고 했네.\"\n\n\"위선자 같으니!\" 당글라르가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"가엾은 당테스!\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"그가 고결한 마음씨를 지닌 젊은이라는 건 누구도 부정 못 하지.\"\n\n\"하지만 그러는 동안,\" 모렐 씨가 말을 이었다. \"파라옹호는 선장이 없게 되었네.\"\n\n\"오,\" 당글라르가 대답했다. \"앞으로 석 달은 이 항구를 떠날 수 없으니, 그 기한이 다하기 전에 당테스가 풀려나기를 바라야지요.\"\n\n\"틀림없이 그래야겠지. 하지만 그동안은?\"\n\n\"저는 전적으로 선주님의 분부를 따르겠습니다, 모렐 씨.\" 당글라르가 대답했다. \"아시다시피 저는 어느 노련한 선장 못지않게 배를 다룰 줄 압니다. 게다가 제 도움을 받아들이시면 이런 이점도 있지요. 에드몽이 감옥에서 풀려나면, 파라옹호에서는 당테스와 제가 각자 본래 자리로 돌아가기만 하면 될 뿐, 더 손볼 게 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"고맙네, 당글라르. 그러면 모든 골칫거리가 매끄럽게 풀리겠군. 자네에게 즉시 파라옹호의 지휘를 맡길 권한을 주겠네. 화물 하역을 빈틈없이 살펴 주게. 개인적인 불행 때문에 일이 어그러지는 일은 결코 있어선 안 되니까.\"\n\n\"그 점은 마음 놓으십시오, 모렐 씨. 그런데 우리가 가엾은 에드몽을 만나 볼 수 있을 것 같습니까?\"\n\n\"빌포르 씨를 만나는 대로 곧 알려 주겠네. 그를 에드몽 편에 서도록 설득해 볼 작정이야. 그가 광적인 왕당파라는 건 나도 알지만, 그래도, 또 국왕의 검사라는 점에도 불구하고, 그도 우리 같은 사람이니, 그리 나쁜 사람은 아닐 거라고 보네.\"\n\n\"아마 아니겠지요.\" 당글라르가 대답했다. \"하지만 듣자 하니 그가 야심가라더군요. 그 점은 좀 마음에 걸립니다.\"\n\n\"뭐, 뭐,\" 모렐 씨가 대꾸했다. \"두고 보면 알겠지. 자, 이제 어서 배로 가게. 나도 머지않아 그리로 가겠네.\"\n\n이렇게 말하고 쓸 만한 선주는 두 동맹자를 떠나 재판소 쪽으로 걸어갔다.\n\n\"보았나,\" 당글라르가 카드루스에게 말했다. \"일이 어떻게 돌아가는지. 자네 아직도 그를 변호하겠다고 나설 마음이 있나?\"\n\n\"손톱만큼도 없네. 하지만 그래도 한낱 장난이 이런 결과를 부른다는 게 끔찍한 일로 여겨지는군.\"\n\n\"그런데 그 장난을 저지른 게 누군지 묻고 싶네만? 자네도 나도 아니고 페르낭이야. 자네도 잘 알다시피 나는 그 종이를 방구석에 던져 버렸어. 사실 나는 그걸 없애 버린 줄로만 알았지.\"\n\n\"오, 아니야.\" 카드루스가 대답했다. \"그건 내가 장담할 수 있어, 자넨 그러지 않았어. 정자 구석에 구겨지고 짓이겨진 채 놓여 있던 그 종이가, 그때 봤던 것처럼 지금도 또렷이 보였으면 좋겠군.\"\n\n\"그래, 그렇다면, 분명 페르낭이 그걸 주워서 베껴 썼거나, 누구한테 베껴 쓰게 했을 거야. 어쩌면 다시 베껴 쓰는 수고조차 안 했을지도 몰라. 그러고 보니, 맙소사, 그 편지 그대로를 보내 버렸을 수도 있겠군! 다행히 나로서는, 필체를 위장해 뒀으니까.\"\n\n\"그럼 자네는 당테스가 음모에 가담했다는 걸 알고 있었단 말인가?\"\n\n\"천만에. 앞서 말했듯이 나는 그게 다 장난, 그 이상도 이하도 아니라고 생각했네. 그런데 보아하니 내가 나도 모르게 우연히 진실을 짚어 버린 모양이야.\"\n\n\"그래도,\" 카드루스가 따졌다. \"이런 일이 아예 일어나지 않았더라면, 아니, 적어도 내가 거기 끼지만 않았더라면, 무엇을 줘서라도 그렇게 됐으면 싶네. 두고 보게, 당글라르, 이건 우리 둘 모두에게 재수 없는 일로 판가름 날 거야.\"\n\n\"말도 안 되는 소리! 무슨 화가 닥친다 해도 그건 죄지은 자에게 떨어져야 마땅하고, 자네도 알다시피 그건 페르낭이야. 우리가 무슨 수로 얽혀들겠나? 우리가 해야 할 일이라곤 입을 꾹 다물고 쥐 죽은 듯 잠자코 있는 것, 살아 있는 누구에게도 한마디 흘리지 않는 것뿐이야. 그러면 두고 보게, 폭풍은 우리를 조금도 건드리지 않고 지나갈 테니.\"\n\n\"아멘!\" 카드루스가 당글라르에게 작별의 표시로 손을 흔들며 대답했다. 그러고는 메이앙 가로수길 쪽으로 발걸음을 옮기며, 머리를 이리저리 흔들고 무언가 중얼거렸다. 하나의 강박에 사로잡혀 머릿속이 가득 찬 사람 같은 모습이었다.\n\n'그러니까 여기까지는,' 당글라르가 속으로 생각했다. '모든 게 내 뜻대로 풀린 셈이군. 나는 당장은 파라옹호의 지휘관이고, 저 멍청한 카드루스가 입을 다물도록 구슬릴 수만 있다면 그 자리를 영영 차지하게 될 게 확실해. 내가 두려운 건 오직 당테스가 풀려날 가능성뿐이야. 하지만, 흥, 그는 사법의 손에 들어갔고,' 그가 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다. '사법은 제 몫을 챙길 테지.' 이렇게 중얼거리며 그는 보트에 뛰어올라, 모렐 씨가 만나기로 한 파라옹호로 저어 가 달라고 일렀다.\n\n# Chapter 6. The Deputy Procureur du Roi\n\n메두사 분수 맞은편, 그랑쿠르 거리에 퓌제가 세운 귀족풍 저택 가운데 한 곳에서, 당테스가 베푼 약혼 잔치와 거의 같은 시각에 또 다른 약혼 잔치가 벌어지고 있었다. 그러나 이 경우에는, 잔치의 명분이야 비슷했어도 모인 사람들은 사뭇 달랐다. 선원과 군인, 그리고 가장 비천한 계급의 사람들이 거칠게 뒤섞이는 대신, 지금 이 자리에는 마르세유 사교계의 정수만이 모여 있었다. 찬탈자의 치세 동안 관직에서 물러난 행정관들, 제국군에서 탈영해 콩데 진영에 합류한 장교들, 그리고 오 년의 유배가 한 사람을 순교자로 만들고 십오 년의 왕정복고가 그를 신의 반열에 올려놓게 될, 바로 그 사람을 증오하고 저주하도록 길러진 가문의 젊은이들이었다.\n\n손님들은 아직 식탁에 둘러앉아 있었고, 그 자리를 지배하던 격하고 열띤 대화는 당시 남부의 주민이라면 누구나 마음속에 품고 있던 격렬하고 앙심에 찬 격정을 고스란히 드러냈다. 안타깝게도 그곳에서는 오백 년 동안 이어진 종교 분쟁이 당파 감정의 폭력에 더한층 쓰라림을 더해 온 터였다.\n\n세계의 절반을 통치하던 황제는, 이제 고작 대여섯 천 명의 백성을 거느린 작은 엘바섬의 왕으로 전락해 있었다. 열 개의 다른 언어로 외쳐 대는 일억 이천만 인류의 \"나폴레옹 만세\" 소리를 듣는 데 익숙했던 그가, 이곳에서는 프랑스와의 새로운 인연이나 왕위에 대한 권리에서 영영 떨어져 나간, 몰락한 사내로 여겨졌다.\n\n행정관들은 거리낌 없이 정치적 견해를 주고받았고, 좌중의 군인들은 모스크바와 라이프치히에 관해 스스럼없이 이야기를 나누었으며, 부인들은 조제핀의 이혼을 두고 입방아를 찧었다. 그들이 기뻐한 것은 한 사람의 몰락이 아니라 나폴레옹 사상의 패배였으며, 거기에서 그들은 되살아난 정치적 삶이라는 밝고 활기찬 앞날을 미리 그려 보았다.\n\n생루이 십자훈장을 단 한 노인이 일어서서 루이 십팔세의 건강을 위해 건배를 청했다. 생메랑 후작이었다. 하트웰에서 참고 견딘 유배 생활과 평화를 사랑하는 프랑스 국왕을 한꺼번에 떠올리게 한 이 건배사는 좌중의 열광을 불러일으켰다. 잔이 영국식으로 허공에 치켜올려졌고, 부인들은 고운 가슴에서 꽃다발을 빼내어 식탁 위에 그 꽃의 보물들을 흩뿌렸다. 한마디로 거의 시적인 열기가 자리를 휘감았다.\n\n\"아,\" 생메랑 후작 부인이 입을 열었다. 그녀는 쉰 줄에 들어섰음에도 여전히 기품 있고 고상한 풍모를 지녔으나, 눈빛만은 매섭고 위압적이었다. \"아, 우리를 영지에서 내쫓고는 공포정치 때 그 땅을 헐값에 사들인 그 혁명파들이 여기 있었다면 인정할 수밖에 없었을 거예요. 진정한 충절은 모두 우리 편에 있었다고요. 우리는 몰락하는 군주의 운명을 기꺼이 따랐지만, 그들은 그와 반대로 떠오르는 태양을 숭배하며 제 잇속을 채웠으니 말이에요. 그래요, 그래, 그들도 인정하지 않을 수 없을 거예요. 우리가 지위와 재산과 신분을 바친 그 임금이야말로 진정 우리의 '사랑받는 루이'였고, 그들의 가련한 찬탈자는 그들에게 악령, 그들의 '저주받은 나폴레옹'이었으며 앞으로도 영영 그러하리라는 것을요. 내 말이 틀렸나요, 빌포르?\"\n\n\"용서하십시오, 부인. 정말이지 너그러이 봐주셔야겠습니다. 실은 제가 대화에 귀를 기울이지 못하고 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"후작 부인, 후작 부인!\" 건배를 청했던 노귀족이 끼어들었다. \"젊은 사람들은 좀 내버려 두구려. 내 말하지만, 혼례를 치르는 날에는 메마른 정치보다 훨씬 즐거운 이야깃거리가 많은 법이오.\"\n\n\"신경 쓰지 마세요, 어머니.\" 연갈색 머리채가 탐스럽고, 두 눈은 맑은 수정 속에 떠 있는 듯한 젊고 어여쁜 아가씨가 말했다. \"제가 빌포르 씨를 붙들고 있어서 어머니 말씀을 못 듣게 한 거예요, 다 제 잘못이지요. 그러니 자, 이제 데려가세요. 원하시는 만큼 어머니 차지예요. 빌포르 씨, 어머니가 말씀하고 계신다는 걸 일러 드릴게요.\"\n\n\"후작 부인께서 제가 제대로 듣지 못한 말씀을 다시 한번 들려주신다면, 기꺼이 대답해 올리겠습니다.\" 빌포르가 말했다.\n\n\"괜찮다, 르네.\" 후작 부인이 대답했다. 메마르고 딱딱한 이목구비와는 어울리지 않는 다정한 눈빛이었다. 여인의 본성에서 다른 모든 감정이 시들어 버린다 해도, 그 마음의 사막에는 늘 밝게 미소 짓는 한 곳이 남아 있게 마련이니, 그것이 바로 모성애의 성소다. \"용서하마. 내가 하던 말은, 빌포르, 보나파르트파에게는 우리 같은 진심도, 열정도, 충절도 없었다는 거예요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그들에게는 그 훌륭한 자질들을 대신할 만한 것이 있었지요.\" 청년이 대답했다. \"바로 광신입니다. 나폴레옹은 서양의 마호메트로, 평범하지만 야심에 찬 추종자들에게 지도자이자 입법자로서만이 아니라 평등의 화신으로 숭배받습니다.\"\n\n\"그가!\" 후작 부인이 외쳤다. \"나폴레옹이 평등의 전형이라니! 그렇다면 제발 묻겠는데, 로베스피에르는 뭐라고 부르겠어요? 자, 자, 로베스피에르에게서 그가 마땅히 누려야 할 권리를 빼앗아 그 코르시카인에게 안겨 주지 말아요. 내가 보기에 그자는 이미 충분히 많은 걸 찬탈했으니까.\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 부인. 저는 이 두 영웅을 각자 마땅한 받침대 위에 세우려는 것입니다. 로베스피에르는 루이 십오세 광장의 단두대 위에, 나폴레옹은 방돔 광장의 기둥 위에 말입니다. 다만 두 사람이 내세운 평등의 성격이 정반대라는 점이 다를 뿐이지요. 하나는 끌어올리는 평등이고, 다른 하나는 끌어내리는 평등입니다. 하나는 왕을 단두대에 닿을 수 있는 데까지 끌어내리고, 다른 하나는 백성을 왕좌와 같은 높이로 끌어올립니다. 보십시오.\" 빌포르가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"저는 이 두 사람이 모두 혁명의 악당이었음을, 또 테르미도르 9일과 1814년 4월 4일이 프랑스에는 다행스러운 날이었으며 군주제와 시민 질서를 사랑하는 이라면 누구나 고맙게 기억할 만한 날이었음을 부인할 생각은 없습니다. 그래서 제가 영영 몰락했으리라 믿는 나폴레옹이 여전히 기생충 같은 추종자들을 거느리고 있다는 사실도 이로써 설명이 되는 것이지요. 하지만 후작 부인, 다른 찬탈자들도 마찬가지였습니다. 가령 크롬웰만 해도 나폴레옹의 절반만큼도 악하지 않았지만, 그에게도 당파와 옹호자들이 있었으니까요.\"\n\n\"빌포르, 당신 지금 끔찍하리만치 혁명적인 어조로 말하고 있다는 걸 알기나 해요? 하지만 봐주겠어요. 지롱드파의 아들에게 그 낡은 효모의 잔재가 한 점도 없기를 바랄 수는 없는 노릇이니까.\" 빌포르의 얼굴이 새빨갛게 물들었다.\n\n\"사실입니다, 부인.\" 그가 대답했다. \"제 아버지가 지롱드파였던 것은 맞습니다. 하지만 국왕의 사형에 찬성표를 던진 자들 축에는 들지 않았습니다. 아버지도 공포정치 동안 부인과 마찬가지로 고초를 겪으셨고, 부인의 아버님이 목숨을 잃으신 바로 그 단두대에서 하마터면 머리를 잃을 뻔하셨지요.\"\n\n\"맞아요.\" 후작 부인이 대답했다. 그렇게 떠오른 비극적 기억에도 그녀는 조금도 움찔하지 않았다. \"하지만 부디 명심하세요. 우리 양가의 부모는 정반대 원칙에서 박해와 추방을 당했답니다. 그 증거로 한마디 하자면, 우리 집안은 망명한 왕족들의 가장 굳건한 지지자로 남았던 반면, 당신 아버지는 지체 없이 새 정부에 가담했지요. 그리고 시민 누아르티에는 지롱드파였지만, 누아르티에 백작은 상원의원이 되었고요.\"\n\n\"어머니,\" 르네가 끼어들었다. \"이런 불쾌한 옛이야기는 영영 묻어 두기로 했잖아요. 잘 아시면서.\"\n\n\"부인, 저 또한 간청드립니다.\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"생메랑 양의 청에 제 간절한 부탁을 보태도록 허락해 주십시오. 부디 망각의 장막으로 과거를 덮어 가려 주십시오. 이미 돌이킬 수 없는 일을 두고 서로 헐뜯어 봤자 무슨 소용이 있겠습니까? 저로 말하자면, 아버지의 이름조차 버렸고, 그분의 정치적 신념을 송두리째 부인하고 있습니다. 아버지는 보나파르트파였고 — 어쩌면 지금도 그러할 테고 — 누아르티에라 불립니다. 저는 그와 반대로 확고한 왕당파이며, 스스로를 드 빌포르라 칭합니다. 혁명의 수액이 무엇이든 남아 있다면 늙은 줄기와 함께 다 소진되어 시들어 사라지게 두십시오. 그리고 어미 나무에서 멀찍이 돋아난 어린 가지만을 굽어살펴 주십시오. 그 가지는 제가 뻗어 나온 그루터기로부터 완전히 떨어져 나갈 힘도, 그럴 뜻도 없는 것입니다.\"\n\n\"옳거니, 빌포르!\" 후작이 외쳤다. \"참으로 훌륭한 말이오! 자, 이제 나는 여러 해 동안 후작 부인에게 약속받으려 애써 온 것을 얻을 수 있겠다는 희망이 생기는구려. 다름 아니라, 과거에 대한 완전한 사면과 망각 말이오.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그러지요.\" 후작 부인이 대답했다. \"과거는 영영 잊기로 해요. 분명히 말하지만, 과거를 다시 들추는 일은 당신만큼이나 내게도 즐겁지 않으니까요. 다만 내가 바라는 건, 빌포르가 앞으로 정치적 신념에서 굳건하고 흔들림이 없어야 한다는 것뿐이에요. 그리고 빌포르, 이것도 잊지 말아요. 우리가 폐하께 당신의 충절과 철저한 충성을 두고 보증을 섰다는 것을, 그리고 우리의 천거로 폐하께서 과거를 잊기로 윤허하셨다는 것을요. 내가 그러하듯이,\" 여기서 그녀는 그에게 손을 내밀었다. \"당신의 간청에 따라 지금 내가 그러하듯이 말이에요. 하지만 명심해요. 만일 정부에 맞서 음모를 꾸민 자가 당신 앞에 나타나거든, 당신이 의심받는 집안 출신임이 알려져 있는 만큼 더더욱 그 죄를 가차 없이 처벌해야 한다는 것을요.\"\n\n\"안타깝지만 부인,\" 빌포르가 대답했다. \"제 직업도, 우리가 사는 시대도 저를 준엄하게 만듭니다. 저는 이미 여러 건의 공소를 성공리에 이끌어 죄인들을 마땅한 벌에 처했습니다. 하지만 그 일이 아직 끝난 것은 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"정말로 그렇게 생각하나요?\" 후작 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"적어도 저는 그것을 두려워하고 있습니다. 엘바섬의 나폴레옹은 프랑스에 너무 가까이 있고, 그 근접함이 추종자들의 희망을 떠받치고 있습니다. 마르세유에는 반액 봉급을 받는 퇴역 장교들이 가득한데, 그들은 날마다 시시한 핑계를 대며 왕당파와 시비를 붙고 있습니다. 그래서 상류층에서는 끊임없이 치명적인 결투가 벌어지고, 하류층에서는 암살이 일어나는 것이지요.\"\n\n\"혹시 들으셨소,\" 생메랑 씨의 가장 오랜 벗이자 아르투아 백작의 시종인 살비외 백작이 말했다. \"신성 동맹이 그를 그곳에서 옮길 작정이라는 이야기를?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 우리가 파리를 떠날 무렵 그 이야기가 오가고 있었지요.\" 생메랑 씨가 말했다. \"그래, 어디로 옮기기로 정해졌소?\"\n\n\"세인트헬레나로요.\"\n\n\"맙소사, 거기가 어디예요?\" 후작 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"적도 너머에 자리한 섬으로, 여기서 적어도 이천 리외는 떨어져 있습니다.\" 백작이 대답했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 다행이군요. 빌포르가 말한 대로, 그런 자를 그가 태어난 코르시카와 그의 매부가 왕으로 있는 나폴리 사이에, 게다가 그가 제 아들을 위해 그 주권을 탐냈던 이탈리아를 마주 보는 자리에 내버려 둔 것은 크나큰 어리석음이었어요.\"\n\n\"안타깝지만,\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"1814년의 조약들이 있는지라, 그 협정을 깨뜨리지 않고서는 나폴레옹을 건드릴 수가 없습니다.\"\n\n\"오, 글쎄, 어떻게든 빠져나갈 길을 찾아내겠지요.\" 살비외 씨가 대꾸했다. \"가엾은 앙기앵 공작을 총살할 때는 조약 따위로 골치를 썩이지 않았으니 말이오.\"\n\n\"자,\" 후작 부인이 말했다. \"보아하니 신성 동맹의 도움으로 우리는 나폴레옹을 떨쳐 낼 수 있을 듯하군요. 그리고 마르세유에서 그 추종자들을 숙청하는 일은 빌포르 씨의 경계심에 맡겨야겠어요. 임금은 임금이거나 임금이 아니거나 둘 중 하나예요. 프랑스의 군주로 인정받는다면, 평화와 안녕 속에서 떠받들어져야 마땅하지요. 그리고 그것은 가장 흔들림 없는 일꾼들을 부려 모든 음모의 시도를 짓밟는 것으로써 가장 잘 이뤄질 수 있어요. 그게 화근을 막는 최선이자 가장 확실한 방법이니까요.\"\n\n\"안타깝지만 부인,\" 빌포르가 대답했다. \"법의 강한 팔은 악이 이미 벌어진 뒤에야 비로소 개입하라는 부름을 받습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그가 할 일은 그것을 바로잡으려 애쓰는 것뿐이겠군요.\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 부인. 법은 그것을 바로잡기에는 무력할 때가 많습니다. 법이 할 수 있는 일이라곤 저질러진 잘못에 복수하는 것뿐이지요.\"\n\n\"오, 빌포르 씨,\" 살비외 백작의 딸이자 생메랑 양의 아끼는 벗인 아름다운 젊은 처녀가 외쳤다. \"우리가 마르세유에 있는 동안 무슨 떠들썩한 재판이라도 한번 열어 주세요. 저는 한 번도 법정에 가 본 적이 없어요. 굉장히 재미있다던데요!\"\n\n\"재미있고말고요.\" 청년이 대답했다. \"극장에서 꾸며 낸 비극을 보며 눈물을 흘리는 것과는 달리, 법정에서는 진짜로, 진정으로 고통받는 사건을, 인생의 한 편 드라마를 목격하게 되니까요. 거기서 보게 되는 죄수는 창백하고 동요하고 겁에 질려 있는데, 비극의 막이 내릴 때처럼 집으로 돌아가 가족과 평온히 저녁을 들고 잠자리에 들어 이튿날 다시 거짓 슬픔을 연기하는 것이 아니라, 그저 다시 감옥으로 끌려가 사형 집행인에게 넘겨지기 위해 당신 눈앞에서 치워질 뿐이지요. 그런 장면을 당신의 신경이 얼마나 견뎌 낼 수 있을지는 당신의 판단에 맡기겠습니다. 다만 이것만은 믿어 두십시오. 적당한 기회가 생긴다면, 그 자리에 함께하실지 말지 그 선택권을 반드시 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n\"부끄러운 줄 아세요, 빌포르 씨!\" 르네가 새파랗게 질려 말했다. \"지금 우리를 얼마나 겁주고 있는지 모르시겠어요? 그러면서 웃기까지 하다니.\"\n\n\"어쩌란 말입니까? 그건 결투와 같은 겁니다. 저는 이미 정치적 음모의 주모자들에게 다섯 번인가 여섯 번 사형을 선고한 바 있습니다. 그러니 얼마나 많은 단검이 날을 갈고서, 그저 좋은 기회만을 기다리며 제 심장에 박힐 채비를 하고 있을지 누가 알겠습니까?\"\n\n\"맙소사, 빌포르 씨,\" 르네가 점점 더 겁에 질려 말했다. \"설마 진심은 아니시겠죠.\"\n\n\"진심이고말고요.\" 젊은 행정관이 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"그리고 저 아가씨가 그토록 보고 싶어 하는 흥미로운 재판이라면, 사정이 한층 더 심각해질 따름입니다. 가령, 죄수가 — 충분히 있을 법한 일입니다만 — 나폴레옹 휘하에서 복무한 자라고 칩시다. 그렇다면 지휘관의 명령 한마디에 적의 총검 속으로 두려움 없이 돌진하던 데 익숙한 자가, 그저 따르도록 명받았다는 이유만으로 제 동족을 살육하던 자가, 제 개인적 원수임을 잘 아는 사람의 심장에 단검을 찔러 넣기를 한순간이라도 망설이리라 기대할 수 있겠습니까? 게다가 충분히 격렬하고 힘 있는 상태로 스스로를 몰아세우려면, 피고의 눈에 미움받는 자가 되는 자극이 필요한 법입니다. 저는 제가 논고하는 상대가 제 말을 비웃듯 미소 짓는 꼴을 보고 싶지 않습니다. 그게 아니라, 제 웅변의 불길에 모든 평정을 잃고 창백하게 동요하는 피고를 보는 것, 그것이 제 긍지입니다.\" 르네가 짓눌린 탄성을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"훌륭하오!\" 손님 가운데 하나가 외쳤다. \"저것이야말로 핵심을 찌르는 말이지.\"\n\n\"지금 같은 때에 꼭 필요한 인물이로군요.\" 또 다른 사람이 말했다.\n\n\"여보게 빌포르, 자네의 그 지난번 사건은 참으로 굉장했네!\" 세 번째 사람이 말했다. \"아비를 살해한 사내를 재판한 일 말일세. 정말이지, 사형 집행인이 손을 대기도 전에 자네가 그자를 죽여 버린 셈이었지.\"\n\n\"오, 아비를 죽인 자나 그런 끔찍한 인간들이라면,\" 르네가 끼어들었다. \"그들에게 무슨 짓을 하든 별 상관이 없어요. 하지만 정치적 음모에 휘말린 것이 유일한 죄인 가엾고 불행한 사람들로 말하자면……\"\n\n\"이런, 그거야말로 그들이 저지를 수 있는 가장 무거운 죄요. 르네, 모르겠소? 임금은 백성의 아버지이고, 삼천이백만 인구의 아버지의 목숨과 안위를 해치려 음모하거나 획책하는 자야말로 무시무시하리만치 거대한 규모의 존속 살해범이 아니겠소?\"\n\n\"그런 건 잘 모르겠어요.\" 르네가 대답했다. \"하지만 빌포르 씨, 제게 약속하셨잖아요. 그렇죠? 제가 변호하는 사람들에게는 늘 자비를 베풀겠다고요.\"\n\n\"그 점은 안심하셔도 됩니다.\" 빌포르가 더없이 다정한 미소를 지으며 대답했다. \"당신과 저는 늘 함께 의논하여 판결을 내릴 테니까요.\"\n\n\"얘야,\" 후작 부인이 말했다. \"너는 비둘기며 애완견이며 자수에나 마음을 쓰고, 알지도 못하는 일에는 끼어들지 마라. 요즈음 군인의 직업은 한물갔고, 법복이야말로 명예의 표지란다. 꼭 들어맞는 라틴 격언이 하나 있지.\"\n\n\"케단트 아르마 토게●.\" 빌포르가 머리를 숙이며 말했다.\n\n\"나는 라틴어를 못 한다오.\" 후작 부인이 대꾸했다.\n\n\"있잖아요,\" 르네가 말했다. \"저는 당신이 지금 하는 일 말고 다른 직업을 택하지 않은 게 아쉬워요. 가령 의사라든가요. 아세요? 저는 '파괴하는' 천사라는 생각만으로도 늘 몸서리가 쳐졌답니다.\"\n\n\"사랑스럽고 착한 르네.\" 빌포르가 그 어여쁜 말상대를 형언할 수 없는 다정함으로 바라보며 속삭였다.\n\n\"바라건대, 얘야,\" 후작이 외쳤다. \"빌포르 씨가 이 지방의 도덕적, 정치적 의사가 되어 주기를 바라자꾸나. 그리한다면 고귀한 과업을 이룬 셈이 될 테니.\"\n\n\"그리고 그 일은 제 아비의 행적에 대한 기억을 지우는 데에도 크게 보탬이 되겠지요.\" 구제 불능의 후작 부인이 덧붙였다.\n\n\"부인,\" 빌포르가 서글픈 미소를 띠며 대답했다. \"이미 말씀드리는 영광을 누렸습니다만, 제 아버지는 — 적어도 저는 그러기를 바랍니다 — 지난날의 과오를 버렸으며, 지금 이 순간 종교와 질서의 굳건하고 열렬한 벗이 되어 있습니다. 어쩌면 그 아들보다 더 나은 왕당파일지도 모르지요. 아버지에게는 지난날의 배신을 속죄해야 할 일이 있는 반면, 제게는 따뜻하고 단호한 선호와 확신 말고는 다른 동기가 없으니 말입니다.\" 이 잘 다듬어진 말을 마친 빌포르는, 마치 공개 법정에서 판사석을 향해 변론이라도 한 듯, 제 웅변의 효과를 가늠하려 좌중을 조심스레 둘러보았다.\n\n\"여보게 빌포르, 알고 있나,\" 살비외 백작이 외쳤다. \"그게 바로 며칠 전 튀일리궁에서 폐하의 수석 시종이 지롱드파의 아들과 콩데 공작 휘하 장교의 딸 사이의 혼인이 기묘하다며 물었을 때, 내가 직접 했던 말과 똑같다는 걸 말이야. 분명히 말하지만, 그는 정치적 차이를 이렇게 화해시키는 방식이 건전하고 훌륭한 원칙에 바탕을 두고 있음을 충분히 이해하는 듯했네. 그러자 우리가 눈치채지 못하는 사이 대화를 엿듣고 계시던 폐하께서 우리 말을 끊고 이렇게 말씀하셨지. '빌포르' — 폐하께서 누아르티에라는 말은 입에 올리지 않으시고, 오히려 빌포르라는 말에 상당히 힘을 주셨음을 눈여겨보게 — '빌포르는,' 폐하께서 말씀하셨네. '판단력과 분별력이 뛰어난 젊은이로, 틀림없이 그 직업에서 두각을 나타낼 것이다. 나는 그를 무척 마음에 들어 하며, 그가 생메랑 후작 부부의 사위가 될 참이라는 소식을 듣고 크게 기뻤다. 고귀한 후작이 내 뜻을 앞질러 윤허를 청하지 않았더라면, 내가 직접 그 혼사를 천거했을 것이다.'\"\n\n\"폐하께서 그토록 저를 호의적으로 말씀하시는 데까지 마음을 베푸셨을 수가 있단 말입니까?\" 황홀해진 빌포르가 물었다.\n\n\"폐하의 말씀을 그대로 옮긴 걸세. 후작이 솔직하시기로 마음먹는다면, 폐하의 말씀이 여섯 달 전 자네와 따님의 혼인 건을 의논하러 갔을 때 폐하께서 그에게 하신 말씀과 완벽히 일치한다는 걸 인정하실 거야.\"\n\n\"그건 사실이오.\" 후작이 대답했다.\n\n\"제가 이 자애로운 군주께 진 빚이 얼마나 큰지요! 이 간절한 감사를 보여 드리기 위해서라면 못 할 일이 무엇이겠습니까!\"\n\n\"옳거니.\" 후작 부인이 외쳤다. \"그렇게 나오는 게 보기 좋군요. 자, 그럼, 음모자가 당신 손에 떨어진다면 더없이 환영받을 거예요.\"\n\n\"저로서는, 어머니,\" 르네가 끼어들었다. \"어머니의 바람이 이뤄지지 않기를 빌어요. 그리고 섭리가 빌포르 씨의 손에 떨어뜨리는 것이 고작 좀도둑이나 가엾은 빚쟁이, 비루한 사기꾼뿐이기를 바라요. 그래야 제 마음이 편할 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그건 의사더러 두통이나 홍역, 벌에 쏘인 상처, 그 밖의 사소한 피부 질환에나 처방을 내리게 해 달라고 비는 거나 마찬가지요. 나를 임금의 검사로 만들고 싶다면, 의사에게 큰 명예를 안겨 주는 그런 격렬하고 위험한 병들을 좀 바라 주어야 합니다.\"\n\n바로 그 순간, 마치 빌포르가 입에 올린 소망이 그것을 이루어 내기에 충분하기라도 했던 듯, 하인 하나가 방에 들어와 그의 귀에 몇 마디를 속삭였다. 빌포르는 곧장 식탁에서 일어나 급한 용무를 핑계로 방을 나섰다. 그러나 이내 돌아왔는데, 온 얼굴이 기쁨으로 환히 빛나고 있었다. 르네는 다정한 애정을 담아 그를 바라보았다. 그 잘생긴 이목구비가 여느 때보다 더한 불꽃과 생기로 환히 빛나는 모습은, 우아하고 총명한 연인을 바라보는 그녀의 순진한 찬탄을 자아내려 빚어진 것만 같았다.\n\n\"방금 당신은,\" 빌포르가 그녀를 향해 말했다. \"제가 변호사 대신 의사였더라면 하고 바라셨지요. 그런데 적어도 한 가지 점에서는 제가 아스클레피오스의 제자들을 닮았답니다(1815년에는 이런 식으로 말하곤 했다). 바로 단 하루도 제 것이라 부를 수 없다는 점이지요. 약혼식 날조차도요.\"\n\n\"그런데 방금 무슨 일로 불려 가셨나요?\" 생메랑 양이 깊은 관심을 담아 물었다.\n\n\"아주 중대한 일입니다. 자칫하면 사형 집행인에게 일거리를 만들어 줄 만한 일이지요.\"\n\n\"끔찍해라!\" 르네가 새파랗게 질리며 외쳤다.\n\n\"그게 정말이오?\" 행정관의 말을 들을 만큼 가까이 있던 이들 모두의 입에서 동시에 터져 나왔다.\n\n\"실은, 제가 받은 정보가 옳다면, 일종의 보나파르트파 음모가 방금 발각되었습니다.\"\n\n\"내 귀를 믿어도 되나요?\" 후작 부인이 외쳤다.\n\n\"적어도 그 고발 내용이 담긴 편지를 읽어 드리지요.\" 빌포르가 말했다.\n\n\"'국왕의 검사께, 왕좌와 조국의 종교 기관을 위하는 한 벗이 알려 드립니다. 오늘 스미르나에서 나폴리와 포르토페라요를 거쳐 입항한 파라옹호의 항해사 에드몽 당테스라는 자가 뮈라가 찬탈자에게 보내는 편지를 전달했으며, 다시 찬탈자가 파리의 보나파르트파 클럽에 보내는 또 다른 편지를 맡았습니다. 위에 언급한 에드몽 당테스를 체포하면 이 진술을 충분히 입증할 수 있을 것입니다. 그자는 파리로 보낼 편지를 몸에 지니고 있거나, 그 아비의 거처에 두었을 것입니다. 만일 부자 어느 쪽에서도 발견되지 않거든, 파라옹호 선상의 당테스 선실에서 반드시 찾아낼 수 있을 것입니다.'\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 르네가 말했다. \"이 편지는, 따지고 보면 익명의 휘갈겨 쓴 쪽지에 지나지 않는 데다, 당신이 아니라 국왕의 검사 앞으로 되어 있잖아요.\"\n\n\"맞습니다. 하지만 그분이 부재중이라, 그 비서가 지시에 따라 편지들을 개봉했는데, 이것이 중요하다고 여겨 저를 부른 것이지요. 그런데 저를 찾지 못하자 스스로 책임지고 피고발인을 체포하라는 필요한 명령을 내렸습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그 죄인은 이미 구금되어 있나요?\" 후작 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 어머니. 피고발인이라고 하셔야지요. 아직 그를 죄인이라 단정할 수는 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"그자는 안전하게 구금되어 있습니다.\" 빌포르가 대답했다. \"그리고 믿으셔도 됩니다, 만일 그 편지가 발견된다면 그자가 다시 바깥세상을 믿고 활보하는 일은 없을 겁니다. 처형인의 특별한 보호를 받으며 나서는 것이 아니라면 말이지요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그 가엾은 자는 어디 있나요?\" 르네가 물었다.\n\n\"제 집에 있습니다.\"\n\n\"자, 자, 여보게.\" 후작 부인이 끼어들었다. \"우리와 머무느라 자네 본분을 소홀히 하지 말게. 자네는 임금의 종복이니, 그 임무가 부르는 곳이라면 어디든 가야 하네.\"\n\n\"오, 빌포르!\" 르네가 두 손을 맞잡고 애처로울 만큼 간절하게 연인을 바라보며 외쳤다. \"우리 약혼식 날이니, 부디 자비를 베풀어 주세요.\"\n\n청년은 어여쁜 탄원자가 앉은 식탁 쪽으로 돌아가, 그녀의 의자에 몸을 기울이며 다정하게 말했다.\n\n\"당신을 기쁘게 하기 위해, 사랑하는 르네, 제 힘이 닿는 한 온갖 관용을 베풀겠다고 약속하지요. 하지만 이 보나파르트파 영웅에게 씌워진 혐의가 사실로 드러난다면, 그땐 정말이지 제가 그자의 목을 치라 명할 수 있도록 허락해 주셔야 합니다.\"\n\n르네는 '친다'는 말에 몸서리를 쳤다. 문제의 그 존재에게는 목이 달려 있었으니 말이다.\n\n\"저 어리석은 아이는 신경 쓰지 말게, 빌포르.\" 후작 부인이 말했다. \"이런 일쯤은 곧 익숙해질 테니.\" 그러면서 생메랑 부인은 메마르고 앙상한 손을 빌포르에게 내밀었다. 그는 사위의 정중한 입맞춤을 그 손에 새기면서 르네를 바라보았는데, 그 눈빛은 마치 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. '마땅히 그래야 했을 당신의 사랑스러운 손에 입 맞추는 거라고 여기려 애써야겠군요.'\n\n\"약혼식에 따르는 징조치고는 음울하기도 하네요.\" 가엾은 르네가 한숨지었다.\n\n\"이런, 얘야!\" 화가 난 후작 부인이 외쳤다. \"네 어리석음이 도를 넘는구나. 도대체 너의 그 병약한 감상과 나랏일 사이에 무슨 관련이 있을 수 있는지 알고 싶구나!\"\n\n\"오, 어머니!\" 르네가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 부인, 부디 이 작은 배반자를 용서해 주십시오. 약속드리지요, 이 아이의 충절 부족을 메우기 위해서라도 저는 더없이 가차 없이 준엄하게 굴겠습니다.\" 그러면서 그는 약혼녀에게 의미심장한 눈길을 던졌는데, 그 눈빛은 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. '걱정 마세요, 사랑하는 당신을 위해 제 정의에는 자비가 깃들 테니까요.' 그리고 그 대가로 달콤하고 흡족해하는 미소를 돌려받은 빌포르는, 마음속에 천국을 품은 채 자리를 떠났다.\n\n# Chapter 7. The Examination\n\n● 케단트 아르마 토게: '무기여 법복에 자리를 내주어라'라는 뜻의 라틴어 격언. —옮긴이\n빌포르는 응접실을 나서기가 무섭게, 사람의 생사를 손에 쥔 자다운 근엄한 표정을 지었다. 그는 노련한 배우처럼 거울 앞에서 그 위엄 있는 얼굴을 미리 갈고닦아 두었지만, 그럼에도 재판관다운 준엄한 기색을 꾸며 내기란 결코 쉬운 일이 아니었다. 자신의 아버지가 택한 정치적 노선에 대한 기억만 아니었다면, 그래서 자칫 큰 신중을 기하지 않으면 그것이 자기 출셋길을 가로막을지 모른다는 우려만 없었다면, 제라르 드 빌포르는 더 바랄 것 없이 행복한 사내였다. 이미 부유한 데다, 겨우 스물일곱의 나이에 높은 관직에 올라 있었다. 그는 사랑하는 젊고 매력적인 여인과 곧 결혼할 참이었는데, 차석 검사답게 열정적으로가 아니라 분별 있게 사랑하는 여인이었다. 그리고 생메랑 양은 그 자체로 빼어난 매력을 지녔을 뿐 아니라, 그 집안이 상당한 정치적 영향력을 지니고 있어 당연히 그것을 그에게 유리하게 행사해 줄 터였다. 아내가 가져올 지참금은 5만 에퀴에 달했고, 게다가 장인이 세상을 떠나면 그 재산이 50만으로 불어나는 것을 지켜볼 전망까지 있었다. 이런 사정들이 빌포르에게 더없이 완벽한 행복감을 안겨 주었으니, 그 행복을 떠올리는 것만으로도 그의 마음은 그야말로 눈이 부실 지경이었다.\n\n문 앞에서 그는 자신을 기다리던 경찰서장과 마주쳤다. 이 관리의 모습은 빌포르를 셋째 하늘에서 다시 땅으로 끌어내렸다. 그는 앞서 묘사한 대로 표정을 가다듬고 말했다. \"편지는 읽었네. 그 사내를 체포한 건 잘한 일이야. 자, 이제 그자와 음모에 관해 알아낸 바를 보고하게.\"\n\n\"음모에 대해서는 아직 아무것도 모릅니다, 검사님. 발견된 서류는 모두 봉인해 검사님 책상에 올려 두었습니다. 죄인의 이름은 에드몽 당테스이고, 삼범선 파라옹호의 일등 항해사로, 알렉산드리아와 스미르나를 오가며 면화를 거래하는 마르세유의 모렐 부자 상회 소속입니다.\"\n\n\"상선에 오르기 전에, 해군에 복무한 적이 있던가?\"\n\n\"아, 아닙니다, 검사님. 아주 젊은 자입니다.\"\n\n\"몇 살인가?\"\n\n\"많아야 열아홉이나 스물입니다.\"\n\n바로 그때, 빌포르가 콩세유 거리 모퉁이에 이르렀을 때, 그를 기다리고 있었던 듯한 한 사내가 다가왔다. 모렐 씨였다.\n\n\"아, 빌포르 씨,\" 그가 외쳤다. \"이렇게 뵙게 되어 정말 다행입니다. 당신 부하들이 참으로 어처구니없는 실수를 저질렀습니다. 방금 제 배의 일등 항해사인 에드몽 당테스를 체포했지 뭡니까.\"\n\n\"알고 있습니다, 모렐 씨.\" 빌포르가 대답했다. \"지금 그자를 신문하러 가는 길입니다.\"\n\n\"아,\" 모렐이 우정에 못 이겨 말했다. \"당신은 그를 모르시지만, 저는 압니다. 그는 세상에서 가장 존경할 만하고 가장 믿음직한 사람이며, 감히 말씀드리건대 상선 통틀어 그보다 나은 선원은 없습니다. 아, 빌포르 씨, 부디 그에게 너그러이 자비를 베풀어 주십시오.\"\n\n이미 보았듯이 빌포르는 마르세유의 귀족파에, 모렐은 평민파에 속했다. 전자는 왕당파였고, 후자는 보나파르트주의자로 의심받았다. 빌포르가 모렐을 경멸 어린 눈으로 바라보며 차갑게 대답했다.\n\n\"아시다시피, 모렐 씨, 사람이 사생활에서는 존경할 만하고 믿음직하며 상선 최고의 선원이라 해도, 정치적으로 말하자면 중죄인일 수 있는 법입니다. 그렇지 않습니까?\"\n\n판사는 마치 이 말을 선주 자신에게 들이대려는 듯 한 마디 한 마디에 힘을 주었고, 그 눈은 남을 위해 변호하면서 정작 제 자신이 자비를 필요로 하는 자의 가슴속을 파고드는 것 같았다. 모렐의 얼굴이 붉어졌다. 정치 문제에 관한 한 그 자신의 양심도 완전히 떳떳하지는 않았던 것이다. 게다가 당테스가 대원수와 만난 일과 황제가 그에게 했다는 말을 들려준 터라, 그것이 그를 거북하게 했다. 그러나 그는 깊은 관심이 묻어나는 어조로 대답했다.\n\n\"간청드립니다, 빌포르 씨. 늘 그러시듯이 부디 친절하고 공정하게 처분하시어, 그를 어서 우리에게 돌려보내 주십시오.\" 이 \"우리에게\"라는 말이 차석 검사의 귀에는 혁명적으로 들렸다.\n\n\"허허,\" 그가 중얼거렸다. \"그럼 당테스가 무슨 카르보나리 결사의 일원이라, 그 비호자가 이렇게 복수형을 쓰는 건가? 내 기억이 맞다면, 그자는 술집에서 수많은 사람들과 어울려 있다가 체포되었지.\" 그러고는 덧붙였다. \"모렐 씨, 안심하셔도 됩니다. 저는 공정하게 제 의무를 다할 것이고, 만약 그가 결백하다면 당신이 저를 찾아온 것이 헛되지 않을 겁니다. 그러나 만약 그가 유죄라면, 지금 같은 시국에 처벌을 면해 주는 것은 위험한 선례가 될 테니, 저는 제 의무를 다해야만 합니다.\"\n\n이제 그는 법원 청사에 맞붙은 자기 집 문 앞에 이르렀으므로, 선주에게 차갑게 인사하고는 안으로 들어갔다. 선주는 빌포르가 떠난 자리에 못 박힌 듯 그대로 서 있었다. 대기실은 경찰 요원과 헌병들로 가득했고, 그 한가운데에 죄인이 삼엄한 감시를 받으면서도 침착하게 미소를 띤 채 서 있었다. 빌포르는 대기실을 가로지르며 당테스에게 곁눈질을 던지고는, 헌병이 건네는 꾸러미를 받아 들고 \"죄인을 들여보내라.\"라고 말하며 사라졌다.\n\n빌포르의 시선은 순식간에 스쳐 갔지만, 자신이 신문하려는 사내가 어떤 인물인지 가늠하기에는 충분했다. 그는 그 훤한 이마에서 총기를, 검은 눈과 찌푸린 눈썹에서 용기를, 진주처럼 가지런한 이를 드러내는 두툼한 입술에서 솔직함을 읽어 냈다. 빌포르의 첫인상은 호의적이었다. 그러나 그는 첫 충동을 믿지 말라는 경고를 하도 자주 들어 온 터라, 그 격언을 첫인상에까지 적용하고 말았다. 충동과 인상이라는 두 말의 차이는 잊어버린 채였다. 그래서 그는 솟아오르는 연민의 감정을 억누르고, 표정을 가다듬은 뒤, 책상 앞에 험상궂고 음울한 얼굴로 앉았다. 잠시 후 당테스가 들어왔다. 그는 창백했지만 침착하고 차분했으며, 편안한 정중함으로 재판관에게 인사하고는, 마치 모렐 씨의 응접실에 와 있기라도 한 듯 앉을 자리를 둘러보았다. 바로 그때 그는 처음으로 빌포르의 시선과 마주쳤다. 남의 생각을 읽어 내는 듯하면서도 정작 제 속은 조금도 드러내지 않는, 판사 특유의 그 시선이었다.\n\n\"이름이 무엇이고 무얼 하는 자인가?\" 빌포르가 물었다. 그는 죄인에 관한 정보가 담긴 서류 뭉치를 뒤적이고 있었다. 그가 들어올 때 경찰 요원이 건네준 것으로, \"피고인\"이 늘 희생양이 되는 부패한 밀정 활동 덕분에 불과 한 시간 만에 방대한 분량으로 불어나 있었다.\n\n\"제 이름은 에드몽 당테스입니다.\" 젊은이가 차분하게 대답했다. \"모렐 부자 상회 소속 파라옹호의 일등 항해사입니다.\"\n\n\"나이는?\" 빌포르가 이어 물었다.\n\n\"열아홉입니다.\" 당테스가 대답했다.\n\n\"체포될 당시에는 무얼 하고 있었나?\"\n\n\"제 결혼 잔치 중이었습니다, 검사님.\" 젊은이가 말했다. 그 목소리는 살짝 떨렸다. 그 행복했던 순간과 지금 겪고 있는 이 괴로운 절차가 너무도 대조적이었고, 빌포르 씨의 음울한 표정과 메르세데스의 빛나는 얼굴이 너무도 대조적이었던 것이다.\n\n\"결혼 잔치 중이었다고?\" 차석 검사가 자기도 모르게 흠칫하며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 검사님. 삼 년 동안 사랑해 온 처녀와 곧 결혼하려던 참이었습니다.\" 빌포르는 무심한 사람이었지만 이 우연의 일치에 충격을 받았다. 행복의 한복판에서 불시에 붙들려 온 당테스의 떨리는 목소리가 그 자신의 가슴속에서도 공명의 현을 울렸다. 그 역시 곧 결혼을 앞두고 있었고, 자신의 행복으로부터 불려 나와 남의 행복을 짓밟으려는 참이었다. '이 철학적인 성찰은,' 그는 생각했다. '생메랑 씨 댁에서 큰 반향을 불러일으키겠군.' 그리고 당테스가 다음 질문을 기다리는 동안, 그는 웅변가들이 종종 달변가라는 명성을 얻는 데 쓰는 그 대조법을 속으로 짜 맞추었다. 이 연설을 다 다듬고 나자 빌포르가 당테스에게로 고개를 돌렸다.\n\n\"계속하게.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"무엇을 말씀드리면 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"아는 바를 모조리 진술하게.\"\n\n\"어느 점에 대해 알고 싶으신지 말씀해 주시면 제가 아는 것을 전부 말씀드리겠습니다. 다만,\" 그가 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다. \"미리 말씀드리지만 제가 아는 것은 거의 없습니다.\"\n\n\"찬탈자 밑에서 복무한 적이 있나?\"\n\n\"그가 몰락했을 때 저는 막 왕립 해군에 편입될 참이었습니다.\"\n\n\"자네의 정치적 견해가 극단적이라는 보고가 있던데.\" 빌포르가 말했다. 그는 그런 말을 들은 적이 전혀 없었지만, 마치 그것이 하나의 혐의라도 되는 양 이렇게 물어보는 것이 싫지 않았다.\n\n\"제 정치적 견해라고요!\" 당테스가 대답했다. \"아아, 검사님, 저는 견해라는 것을 가져 본 적이 없습니다. 저는 겨우 열아홉이고, 아는 것도 없으며, 맡을 역할도 없습니다. 제가 바라는 자리를 얻게 된다면 그건 모렐 씨 덕분이겠지요. 그러니 제 모든 견해는, 공적인 것은 말할 것도 없고 사적인 것까지도, 이 세 가지 감정에 한정됩니다. 저는 아버지를 사랑하고, 모렐 씨를 존경하며, 메르세데스를 흠모합니다. 검사님, 이것이 제가 말씀드릴 수 있는 전부이고, 보시다시피 하나도 흥미로울 게 없습니다.\" 당테스가 말하는 동안 빌포르는 그 천진하고 거리낌 없는 얼굴을 바라보며, 죄인이 누구인지도 모른 채 그를 너그러이 봐 달라고 간청하던 르네의 말을 떠올렸다. 범죄와 범죄자를 잘 아는 차석 검사로서는, 젊은이가 내뱉는 말 한 마디 한 마디가 점점 더 그의 결백을 확신하게 만들었다. 이 젊은이는, 아니 아직 어른이라 하기도 어려운 이 소년은, 단순하고 자연스러웠으며, 애써 구할 때는 결코 찾을 수 없는 그 진심에서 우러난 웅변으로 말했다. 행복한 까닭에, 그리고 행복은 악인마저도 선하게 만드는 까닭에 모두에게 애정이 넘쳤다. 그 애정은 빌포르의 준엄한 눈빛과 단호한 말투에도 불구하고 재판관에게까지 미쳤다. 당테스는 선의로 가득 차 있는 듯했다.\n\n\"이런!\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"고결한 청년이로군. 르네가 내게 처음으로 내린 부탁을 들어주면 그녀의 환심을 쉽게 살 수 있겠어. 적어도 사람들 앞에서는 손을 꼭 쥐어 줄 테고, 단둘이 있을 때는 달콤한 입맞춤을 해 주겠지.\" 이런 생각으로 가득 차자 빌포르의 얼굴이 어찌나 환해졌던지, 그가 당테스에게로 고개를 돌렸을 때, 그 표정의 변화를 지켜보던 당테스 역시 미소를 짓고 있었다.\n\n\"이보게,\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"자네에게 적이 있나? 적어도 자네가 아는 한에서 말일세.\"\n\n\"제게 적이라고요?\" 당테스가 대답했다. \"제 처지는 적을 둘 만큼 높지 않습니다. 성미로 말하자면, 어쩌면 좀 급한 편일지도 모르지만, 그것도 누르려고 애써 왔습니다. 제 밑에 선원이 열에서 열둘쯤 있었는데, 그들에게 물어보시면 저를 사랑하고 존경한다고 할 겁니다. 제가 너무 어리니 아버지로서는 아니더라도, 맏형으로서 말이지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 누군가의 시기를 샀을 수도 있지 않나. 자네는 열아홉에 선장이 되려는 참이니, 이는 높은 자리야. 게다가 자네를 사랑하는 예쁜 처녀와 결혼하려 하고 있지. 이 두 가지 행운이 누군가의 질투를 불러일으켰을 수도 있어.\"\n\n\"옳은 말씀입니다. 검사님은 저보다 사람을 더 잘 아시니, 말씀하신 대로일 수도 있겠지요. 인정합니다. 하지만 그런 자들이 제 지인 가운데 있다면, 저는 차라리 모르는 편이 낫습니다. 알게 되면 그들을 미워할 수밖에 없을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그건 잘못된 생각일세. 사람은 늘 자기 주위를 똑똑히 살피려 애써야 하는 법이야. 자네는 훌륭한 청년으로 보이니, 내 의무의 엄격한 선을 넘어서라도 이 고발을 꾸민 자를 밝혀내도록 도와주겠네. 여기 그 종이가 있네. 이 필적을 아는가?\" 그러면서 빌포르는 주머니에서 편지를 꺼내 당테스에게 내밀었다. 당테스가 그것을 읽었다. 그의 이마에 그늘이 스쳤다. 그가 말했다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 검사님. 이 필적은 모르겠습니다. 그래도 글씨는 제법 또렷하군요. 이걸 쓴 자가 누구든 글솜씨가 좋습니다. 저는 참으로 운이 좋습니다.\" 그가 빌포르를 고마운 눈길로 바라보며 덧붙였다. \"검사님 같은 분에게 신문을 받게 되었으니까요. 이 시기심에 찬 자는 진짜 적이니 말입니다.\" 그리고 젊은이의 눈에서 번뜩 스쳐 간 그 눈빛을 보고, 빌포르는 이 온순함 아래 얼마나 큰 열정이 숨어 있는지를 알아차렸다.\n\n\"자,\" 차석 검사가 말했다. \"이제 솔직하게 대답하게. 죄인이 재판관에게 답하듯이가 아니라, 자네에게 관심을 가진 한 사람에게 답하듯이 말일세. 이 익명의 편지에 담긴 고발에 어느 정도의 진실이 있는가?\" 그러면서 빌포르는 당테스가 방금 돌려준 편지를 경멸하듯 책상 위로 던졌다.\n\n\"전혀 없습니다. 실제 사실을 말씀드리지요. 선원으로서의 제 명예를 걸고, 메르세데스를 향한 제 사랑을 걸고, 제 아버지의 목숨을 걸고 맹세컨대……\"\n\n\"말해 보게.\" 빌포르가 말했다. 그러고는 속으로 생각했다. '르네가 지금 나를 본다면 흡족해할 테고, 더는 나를 목 자르는 사람이라 부르지 않겠지.'\n\n\"그러니까, 저희가 나폴리를 떠났을 때, 르클레르 선장님이 뇌염에 걸리셨습니다. 배에는 의사가 없었고, 선장님은 엘바섬에 도착하기를 어찌나 애타게 바라셨는지 다른 어떤 항구에도 들르려 하지 않으셨습니다. 그래서 병세가 점점 심해져, 사흘째 되던 날 끝에 자신이 죽어 가고 있음을 느끼시고는 저를 부르셨습니다. '여보게 당테스,' 선장님이 말씀하셨습니다. '내가 이제 하는 말을 꼭 지키겠다고 맹세하게. 이건 더없이 중대한 일이니까.'\n\n\"'맹세합니다, 선장님.' 제가 대답했습니다.\n\n\"'좋아, 내가 죽고 나면 일등 항해사인 자네에게 지휘권이 넘어가니, 지휘를 맡아 엘바섬으로 뱃머리를 돌리게. 포르토페라요에 내려 대원수를 찾아가 이 편지를 전하게. 어쩌면 그쪽에서 다른 편지 한 통을 건네며 자네에게 어떤 임무를 맡길지도 모르네. 그러면 자네가 내가 해야 했던 일을 대신 완수하는 것이고, 그 모든 명예와 이익도 자네가 차지하게 될 걸세.'\n\n\"'그리하겠습니다, 선장님. 하지만 어쩌면 선장님 생각만큼 쉽게 대원수를 뵙지 못할 수도 있지 않겠습니까?'\n\n\"'여기 반지가 하나 있네. 이것이면 그분을 알현할 수 있고, 어떤 어려움도 사라질 걸세.' 선장님이 말씀하셨습니다. 이 말과 함께 선장님은 제게 반지를 건네셨습니다. 마침맞은 때였습니다. 두 시간 뒤 선장님은 헛소리를 하기 시작하셨고, 이튿날 돌아가셨으니까요.\"\n\n\"그래서 그다음엔 어떻게 했나?\"\n\n\"제가 마땅히 해야 할 일을, 그리고 제 처지였다면 누구라도 했을 일을 했습니다. 죽어 가는 사람의 마지막 부탁은 어디서나 신성한 법이지만, 선원에게 상관의 마지막 부탁은 곧 명령입니다. 저는 엘바섬을 향해 떠나 이튿날 그곳에 도착했습니다. 모두에게 배에 남아 있으라 이르고 저 혼자 뭍에 내렸습니다. 예상대로 대원수를 만나기는 다소 어려웠지만, 선장님께 받은 반지를 그분께 보내자 곧바로 안으로 들여보내 주었습니다. 그분은 르클레르 선장님의 죽음에 관해 제게 물으셨고, 선장님이 말씀하신 대로 파리에 있는 어떤 사람에게 전할 편지를 제게 주셨습니다. 저는 그 일을 맡았습니다. 선장님이 그리하라 분부하신 일이었으니까요. 이곳에 내려 배에 관한 일을 정리한 뒤, 서둘러 약혼녀를 찾아갔더니 그녀는 그 어느 때보다 사랑스러웠습니다. 모렐 씨 덕분에 모든 절차가 마무리되었고, 한마디로 말씀드린 대로 저는 결혼 잔치 자리에 있었습니다. 한 시간만 더 있었으면 결혼식을 올렸을 테고, 내일은 파리로 떠날 작정이었습니다. 검사님도 저도 부당하다는 것을 이제 아는 이 혐의로 체포되지만 않았다면 말입니다.\"\n\n\"아,\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"내가 보기에도 이게 사실인 것 같군. 자네에게 잘못이 있었다면 그건 경솔함이었고, 그 경솔함도 선장의 명령에 따른 것이었어. 엘바섬에서 가져온 그 편지를 내놓게. 그리고 부르면 출두하겠다고 약조하게. 그런 뒤 친구들에게 돌아가게.\"\n\n\"그럼 저는 자유로운 몸입니까, 검사님?\" 당테스가 기뻐하며 외쳤다.\n\n\"그렇네. 하지만 먼저 그 편지를 내게 주게.\"\n\n\"이미 가지고 계십니다. 다른 것들과 함께 제게서 압수되었는데, 저기 그 꾸러미 안에 보이는군요.\"\n\n\"잠깐,\" 당테스가 모자와 장갑을 집어 들자 차석 검사가 말했다. \"그 편지는 누구 앞으로 된 건가?\"\n\n\"파리 코크에롱 거리의 누아르티에 씨 앞으로 된 것입니다.\" 벼락이 방 안에 떨어졌다 해도 빌포르가 이보다 더 아연실색하지는 못했을 것이다. 그는 의자에 털썩 주저앉아 황급히 꾸러미를 뒤져 그 운명의 편지를 꺼냈고, 공포에 질린 표정으로 그것을 흘끗 보았다.\n\n\"코크에롱 거리 13번지, 누아르티에 씨라.\" 그가 더욱 창백해지며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"네,\" 당테스가 말했다. \"그분을 아십니까?\"\n\n\"아니,\" 빌포르가 대답했다. \"왕의 충실한 신하는 음모자를 알지 못하네.\"\n\n\"그럼 음모란 말입니까?\" 당테스가 물었다. 자유로워졌다고 믿었던 그는 이제 열 배는 더한 두려움에 사로잡히기 시작했다. \"하지만 검사님, 저는 이미 말씀드렸듯이 편지의 내용은 전혀 모릅니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 하지만 자네는 그 편지를 받을 사람의 이름은 알고 있었지.\" 빌포르가 말했다.\n\n\"누구에게 건네야 할지 알려면 받는 사람을 읽을 수밖에 없었습니다.\"\n\n\"이 편지를 누군가에게 보인 적이 있나?\" 빌포르가 더욱 창백해지며 물었다.\n\n\"아무에게도 보이지 않았습니다, 명예를 걸고.\"\n\n\"자네가 엘바섬에서 온, 누아르티에 씨 앞으로 된 편지를 지니고 있다는 사실을 아무도 모른다는 말이지?\"\n\n\"제게 그것을 건넨 분 말고는 아무도 모릅니다.\"\n\n\"그것조차도 너무 많아, 너무도 많지.\" 빌포르가 중얼거렸다. 빌포르의 이마는 점점 더 어두워졌고, 핏기 없는 입술과 앙다문 이가 당테스를 불안하게 했다. 편지를 다 읽고 나서 빌포르는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다.\n\n\"아,\" 당테스가 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"무슨 일이십니까?\" 빌포르는 아무 대답도 하지 않다가, 몇 초 뒤 고개를 들고 다시 편지를 꼼꼼히 읽었다.\n\n\"그러니까 자네는 이 편지의 내용을 모른다는 거지?\"\n\n\"명예를 걸고 맹세합니다, 검사님.\" 당테스가 말했다. \"그런데 무슨 일이십니까? 안색이 안 좋으십니다. 사람을 부를까요? 누구라도 부를까요?\"\n\n\"아니,\" 빌포르가 황급히 일어서며 말했다. \"그 자리에 가만히 있게. 여기서 명령을 내리는 건 나지 자네가 아니야.\"\n\n\"검사님,\" 당테스가 자존심 상한 듯 대답했다. \"그저 도움을 청해 드리려던 것뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"필요 없네. 잠깐 몸이 좋지 않았을 뿐이야. 자네 일이나 신경 쓰고, 내 질문에 답하게.\" 당테스는 질문을 기다렸으나 헛일이었다. 빌포르는 의자에 다시 주저앉아 땀에 젖은 이마를 손으로 쓸고는, 세 번째로 편지를 읽었다.\n\n'아, 이자가 이 편지의 내용을 안다면,' 그가 중얼거렸다. '그리고 누아르티에가 빌포르의 아버지라는 걸 안다면, 나는 끝장이다!' 그러고는 마치 그 생각을 꿰뚫어 보려는 듯 에드몽에게 시선을 고정했다.\n\n\"오, 의심할 여지가 없어.\" 그가 불쑥 외쳤다.\n\n\"제발 부탁드립니다!\" 불행한 젊은이가 외쳤다. \"저를 의심하신다면 신문해 주십시오. 답하겠습니다.\" 빌포르는 안간힘을 쓰며, 애써 단호하게 들리도록 한 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"이보게,\" 그가 말했다. \"유감스럽게도 내가 바랐던 대로 자네를 당장 풀어 줄 수가 없게 되었네. 그러기에 앞서 예심 판사와 상의해야 하니까. 내 마음이 어떤지는 자네도 이미 알 걸세.\"\n\n\"오, 검사님,\" 당테스가 외쳤다. \"검사님은 재판관이라기보다 차라리 벗이셨습니다.\"\n\n\"그래서 자네를 좀 더 붙들어 두어야겠네만, 되도록 짧게 끝내도록 애쓰겠네. 자네에게 걸린 주된 혐의는 이 편지이니, 보게……\" 빌포르는 벽난로로 다가가 편지를 불 속에 던지고는, 그것이 완전히 다 타 버릴 때까지 기다렸다.\n\"보다시피, 내가 이걸 없애 버리지 않나?\"\n\n\"오,\" 당테스가 외쳤다. \"검사님은 그야말로 선의 그 자체이십니다.\"\n\n\"잘 듣게,\" 빌포르가 말을 이었다. \"내가 이렇게까지 했으니 이제 자네도 나를 믿을 수 있겠지.\"\n\n\"오, 명령만 하십시오. 따르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"잘 듣게. 이건 명령이 아니라 내가 자네에게 주는 충고일세.\"\n\n\"말씀하십시오. 충고를 따르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"오늘 저녁까지 자네를 법원 청사에 붙들어 두겠네. 혹시 다른 누군가가 자네를 신문하거든, 내게 말한 대로 말하되, 이 편지에 대해서는 한마디도 입 밖에 내지 말게.\"\n\n\"약속합니다.\" 간청하는 쪽은 빌포르인 듯했고, 안심시키는 쪽은 죄인인 듯했다.\n\n\"보다시피,\" 그가 불꽃 속에서 타다 남은 종잇조각이 나풀거리는 화덕 쪽을 흘끗 보며 말을 이었다. \"편지는 없어졌네. 이것이 있었다는 사실은 자네와 나 둘만 아는 일이야. 그러니 누가 묻거든 그런 건 전혀 모른다고 부인하게. 대담하게 부인하란 말이야. 그러면 자네는 살아날 걸세.\"\n\n\"안심하십시오. 부인하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"편지는 그것 하나뿐이었나?\"\n\n\"하나뿐이었습니다.\" \"맹세하게.\"\n\n\"맹세합니다.\"\n\n\"맹세합니다.\"\n빌포르가 종을 울렸다. 경찰 요원이 들어왔다. 빌포르가 그의 귀에 몇 마디 속삭이자, 요원이 고갯짓으로 대답했다.\n\n\"이 사람을 따라가게.\" 빌포르가 당테스에게 말했다. 당테스는 빌포르에게 인사하고 물러갔다. 문이 닫히기가 무섭게 빌포르는 반쯤 정신을 잃은 채 의자에 몸을 던졌다.\n\n\"아아, 큰일 날 뻔했어.\" 그가 중얼거렸다. \"검사장이 직접 마르세유에 있었더라면 나는 파멸했을 거야. 이 저주받은 편지가 내 모든 희망을 무너뜨렸겠지. 오, 아버지, 아버지의 지난 행적이 언제까지나 제 성공을 가로막아야 한단 말입니까?\" 문득 그의 얼굴에 한 줄기 빛이 스치고, 앙다문 입가에 미소가 어렸으며, 초췌하던 눈이 골똘한 생각에 잠겨 한 점에 멎었다.\n\n\"이거면 되겠어.\" 그가 말했다. \"나를 파멸시킬 뻔했던 이 편지로, 나는 도리어 내 출세의 발판을 마련하겠어. 자, 이제 내가 할 일을 해야지.\" 그러고는 죄인이 떠난 것을 확인한 뒤, 차석 검사는 서둘러 약혼녀의 집으로 향했다.\n\n# Chapter 8. The Château d’If\n\n경찰서장은 대기실을 가로지르며 헌병 두 명에게 신호를 보냈고, 그들은 한 사람은 당테스의 오른쪽에, 다른 한 사람은 왼쪽에 섰다. 재판소로 통하는 문이 열렸고, 일행은 음산한 복도를 한참 지나갔다. 그 모습은 아무리 담대한 사람이라도 몸서리치게 할 만했다. 재판소는 감옥과 이어져 있었다. 음울한 건물이었고, 창살 박힌 창문 너머로 아쿨의 시계탑이 내다보였다. 수없이 굽이진 길을 지난 끝에 당테스는 쇠살문이 달린 문 하나를 보았다. 서장이 쇠망치를 집어 들고 세 번 두드렸는데, 한 번 두드릴 때마다 당테스에게는 그것이 제 심장을 치는 것만 같았다. 문이 열리자 헌병 두 명이 그를 가만히 앞으로 밀어 넣었고, 문은 등 뒤에서 요란한 소리를 내며 닫혔다. 그가 들이마신 공기는 더 이상 맑지 않고 탁하고 역겨웠다. 그는 감옥 안에 있었다.\n\n그는 제법 깨끗한 방으로 끌려갔지만, 창살과 빗장이 둘러쳐져 있었다. 그래서 그 모습은 그를 크게 놀라게 하지는 않았다. 게다가 그토록 자신에게 마음을 써 주는 듯하던 빌포르의 말이 자유를 약속하듯 여전히 귓가에 울렸다. 당테스가 이 방에 갇힌 것은 네 시였다. 앞서 말했듯이 그날은 3월 1일이었고, 죄수는 곧 어둠 속에 묻혔다. 어둠은 그의 청각을 더욱 예민하게 만들었다. 아주 작은 소리에도 그는 벌떡 일어나 문으로 달려갔고, 곧 풀려나리라 확신했다. 그러나 소리는 잦아들었고, 당테스는 다시 제자리에 주저앉았다. 마침내 열 시쯤, 당테스가 절망에 빠지기 시작할 무렵, 복도에서 발소리가 들렸다. 자물쇠에서 열쇠가 돌아갔고, 빗장이 삐걱거렸고, 묵직한 떡갈나무 문이 활짝 열렸다. 횃불 두 개에서 쏟아지는 불빛이 방 안에 가득 찼다.\n\n횃불 빛에 당테스는 헌병 넷의 번득이는 군도와 기병총을 보았다. 그는 처음에 앞으로 나섰으나, 이렇게 무력을 과시하는 광경을 보고 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"저를 데리러 오신 겁니까?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"그렇다.\" 헌병 하나가 대답했다.\n\n\"검사 대리의 명령으로요?\"\n\n\"그런 모양이다.\" 빌포르 씨가 보낸 사람들이라는 확신이 당테스의 모든 불안을 가라앉혔다. 그는 차분히 앞으로 나아가 호송대 한가운데에 섰다. 문 앞에 마차 한 대가 기다리고 있었고, 마부는 마부석에 앉아 있었으며, 그 옆에는 경찰관 한 명이 자리 잡고 있었다.\n\n\"이 마차가 저를 위한 겁니까?\" 당테스가 말했다.\n\n\"너를 위한 것이다.\" 헌병 하나가 대답했다.\n\n당테스가 무어라 말하려 했다. 그러나 앞으로 떠밀리는 데다 저항할 힘도, 그럴 마음도 없었기에 그는 발판을 밟고 올라갔고, 순식간에 헌병 둘 사이에 자리 잡았다. 나머지 둘은 맞은편에 앉았고, 마차는 돌바닥 위를 무겁게 굴러갔다.\n\n죄수는 창문을 흘끗 보았다. 창에는 창살이 박혀 있었다. 그는 감옥 하나를, 자신을 어디론가 데려가는 또 다른 감옥으로 바꾼 셈이었고, 그곳이 어디인지는 알지 못했다. 그래도 창살 너머로 당테스는 일행이 케스리 거리를 지나, 생로랑 거리와 타라미 거리를 거쳐 부두로 향하는 것을 보았다. 곧 라콩시뉴의 불빛이 보였다.\n\n마차가 멈추자 경찰관이 내려 경비소로 다가갔고, 병사 열두 명가량이 나와 대오를 갖추었다. 당테스는 부두의 등불 빛에 그들의 총이 반사되는 것을 보았다.\n\n'이 많은 병력이 정녕 나 하나 때문에 동원된 것인가?' 그는 생각했다.\n\n경찰관이 잠겨 있던 문을 열었고, 한마디 말도 없이 당테스의 물음에 답해 주었다. 병사들이 늘어선 사이로 마차에서 항구까지 통로가 트여 있는 것이 보였기 때문이다. 맞은편에 있던 헌병 둘이 먼저 내렸고, 이어 당테스에게 내리라는 명령이 떨어졌으며, 좌우의 헌병들도 그를 따랐다. 그들은 세관원이 부두 가까이에서 사슬로 붙잡고 있는 작은 배 한 척으로 다가갔다.\n\n병사들은 멍청한 호기심 어린 얼굴로 당테스를 바라보았다. 순식간에 그는 헌병들 사이로 배의 고물 자리에 앉혀졌고, 경찰관은 뱃머리에 자리 잡았다. 한 번 떠밀자 배가 물 위로 미끄러졌고, 건장한 노잡이 넷이 배를 빠르게 필롱 쪽으로 몰아갔다. 배에서 외치는 소리가 들리자 항구 입구를 막고 있던 사슬이 내려졌고, 순식간에 그들은 당테스가 알기로 프리울 해역, 곧 내항 밖으로 나와 있었다.\n\n죄수가 처음 느낀 것은 다시 맑은 공기를 들이마시는 기쁨이었다. 공기란 곧 자유이기 때문이다. 그러나 그는 곧 한숨을 내쉬었다. 그날 아침 그토록 행복했던 라레제르브 앞을 지나는 참이었고, 이제 그 열린 창문으로 무도회의 웃음소리와 흥겨운 소리가 흘러나왔다. 당테스는 두 손을 모으고 하늘을 향해 눈을 들어 간절히 기도했다.\n\n배는 계속 나아갔다. 그들은 테트드모르를 지나, 이제 앙스뒤파로 앞바다에 이르렀고, 포대를 돌아 나가려는 참이었다. 이 항로가 당테스로서는 도무지 이해되지 않았다.\n\n\"저를 어디로 데려가는 겁니까?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"곧 알게 될 것이다.\"\n\n\"하지만 그래도……\"\n\n\"우리는 너에게 아무런 설명도 해 줄 수 없다.\" 규율에 익숙한 당테스는 대답이 금지된 부하들에게 캐묻는 것만큼 어리석은 일은 없음을 알았다. 그래서 그는 입을 다물었다.\n\n가장 막연하고 터무니없는 생각들이 그의 머릿속을 스쳐 갔다. 그들이 탄 배로는 먼 항해를 할 수 없었다. 항구 밖에는 닻을 내린 배가 한 척도 없었다. 그는 어쩌면 자신을 어느 먼 곳에 버려 두려는 것이 아닐까 생각했다. 그는 묶이지도 않았고, 수갑을 채우려는 시도도 없었다. 이것은 좋은 징조처럼 보였다. 게다가 그토록 친절하게 대해 주던 검사 대리가, 그 무시무시한 누아르티에라는 이름만 입에 올리지 않으면 두려워할 것이 없다고 말하지 않았던가? 빌포르가 자기 앞에서 그 치명적인 편지를, 자신에게 불리한 유일한 증거를 없애 버리지 않았던가?\n\n그는 어둠을 꿰뚫어 보려 애쓰며 말없이 기다렸다.\n\n그들은 등대가 서 있는 라토노섬을 오른쪽으로 두고 지났고, 이제 카탈루냐곶 맞은편에 있었다. 죄수에게는 바닷가에서 여인의 형체 하나가 어렴풋이 보이는 듯했다. 메르세데스가 사는 곳이 바로 그곳이었기 때문이다. 어째서 메르세데스에게는 자신의 연인이 삼백 야드도 채 안 되는 곳에 있다는 예감이 들지 않는 것일까?\n\n불빛이라곤 단 하나만 보였다. 당테스는 그것이 메르세데스의 방에서 새어 나오는 것임을 알았다. 그 마을 전체에서 깨어 있는 사람은 메르세데스뿐이었다. 큰 소리로 외치면 그녀에게 들릴 만했다. 그러나 자존심이 그를 가로막았고, 그는 소리치지 않았다. 그가 미친 사람처럼 고함을 지른다면 호위병들이 어떻게 생각하겠는가?\n\n그는 그 불빛에 눈을 고정한 채 잠자코 있었다. 배는 계속 나아갔으나, 죄수는 오직 메르세데스만을 생각했다. 가로막은 육지의 융기가 그 불빛을 가렸다. 당테스가 고개를 돌려 보니, 어느새 그들은 먼바다로 나와 있었다. 그가 생각에 잠겨 있는 동안 그들은 노를 거두고 돛을 올렸고, 배는 이제 바람을 받으며 나아가고 있었다.\n\n호위병들에게 말 거는 것이 내키지 않았지만, 당테스는 가장 가까이 있는 헌병 쪽으로 몸을 돌려 그의 손을 잡았다.\n\n\"여보시오,\" 그가 말했다. \"기독교인이자 군인으로서 청하니, 우리가 어디로 가는지 말해 주시오. 나는 당테스 선장이고, 반역죄로 고발당했으나 충성스러운 프랑스인이오. 나를 어디로 데려가는지 말해 주면, 명예를 걸고 내 운명에 순순히 따르겠다고 약속하겠소.\"\n\n헌병은 머뭇거리며 동료를 바라보았고, 동료는 \"이제 말해 줘도 큰 해는 없을 것 같다\"는 뜻의 신호로 답했다. 그러자 헌병이 대답했다.\n\n\"당신은 마르세유 토박이에 뱃사람이면서, 어디로 가는지 모른단 말이오?\"\n\n\"명예를 걸고, 정말 모르겠소.\"\n\n\"전혀 짐작도 못 하오?\"\n\n\"전혀.\"\n\n\"그럴 리가.\"\n\n\"정말이라고 맹세하오. 제발 말해 주시오.\"\n\n\"하지만 명령이 있어서.\"\n\n\"내가 십 분 뒤, 삼십 분 뒤, 한 시간 뒤면 알게 될 일을 말해 주는 것까지 명령이 금하지는 않을 거요. 보다시피 나는 달아나려 해도 달아날 수가 없소.\"\n\n\"당신이 눈먼 사람이거나, 한 번도 항구 밖에 나와 본 적이 없는 사람이 아니라면, 알 텐데.\"\n\n\"모르겠소.\"\n\n\"그럼 주위를 둘러보시오.\" 당테스가 일어나 앞쪽을 보니, 백 야드도 채 안 되는 곳에 이프성이 서 있는 검고 험상궂은 바위가 솟아오르는 것이 보였다. 삼백 년 넘게 수많은 황당한 전설의 먹잇감이 되어 온 이 음울한 요새가, 당테스에게는 죄인에게 들이닥친 처형대처럼 보였다.\n\n\"이프성?\" 그가 외쳤다. \"거기는 무엇하러 가는 거요?\"\n\n헌병이 빙긋 웃었다.\n\n\"나는 거기 갇히러 가는 게 아니오.\" 당테스가 말했다. \"거기는 정치범들만 가두는 곳이잖소. 나는 아무 죄도 짓지 않았소. 이프성에 치안판사나 재판관이 있기라도 하오?\"\n\n\"거기 있는 거라곤,\" 헌병이 말했다. \"성주 한 명에 수비대, 간수들, 그리고 두툼한 성벽뿐이오. 자, 자, 그렇게 놀란 얼굴 하지 마시오. 안 그러면 내가 친절을 베푼 보답으로 당신이 나를 놀린다고 여길 테니.\"\n\n당테스는 짓이겨 버릴 듯 헌병의 손을 움켜쥐었다.\n\n\"그러니까,\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 이프성에 갇히려고 끌려가는 거라고 생각하는 거요?\"\n\n\"아마 그럴 거요. 하지만 그렇게 세게 쥘 것까지는 없잖소.\"\n\n\"아무런 심문도, 아무런 절차도 없이?\"\n\n\"절차는 다 끝났소. 심문도 이미 마쳤고.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 빌포르 씨의 약속에도 불구하고?\"\n\n\"빌포르 씨가 당신에게 무얼 약속했는지는 모르오.\" 헌병이 말했다. \"하지만 우리가 당신을 이프성으로 데려가고 있다는 건 아오. 그런데, 뭐 하는 거요? 도와줘, 동료들, 도와줘!\"\n\n헌병의 노련한 눈이 알아챈 잽싼 동작으로, 당테스는 바다로 몸을 던지려 앞으로 솟구쳤다. 그러나 발이 배 바닥에서 떨어지는 순간, 억센 팔 넷이 그를 붙들었다. 그는 분노에 차 욕설을 퍼부으며 뒤로 넘어졌다.\n\n\"좋아!\" 헌병이 그의 가슴을 무릎으로 누르며 말했다. \"이게 뱃사람으로서 약속을 지키는 방식이로군! 말솜씨 좋은 양반들 말을 다시 또 믿어 보시지! 잘 들어, 친구, 나는 첫 명령은 어겼지만, 두 번째 명령은 어기지 않을 거야. 움직이면, 네 머리통을 날려 버리겠어.\" 그러고는 기병총을 당테스에게 겨누었고, 당테스는 총구가 자기 관자놀이에 닿는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n한순간 몸부림쳐서 자신을 덮친 이 뜻밖의 재앙을 그렇게 끝내 버릴까 하는 생각이 그의 머리를 스쳤다. 그러나 그는 빌포르 씨의 약속을 떠올렸다. 게다가 배 안에서 헌병의 손에 죽는다는 것은 너무도 끔찍하게 여겨졌다. 그는 꼼짝 않고 있었지만, 이를 갈고 두 손을 비틀어 대며 분노에 떨었다.\n\n이때 배가 거센 충격과 함께 뭍에 닿았다. 뱃사람 하나가 물에 뛰어내렸고, 도르래를 타고 밧줄이 미끄러지며 삐걱거렸다. 당테스는 항해가 끝났으며, 그들이 배를 매고 있다는 것을 짐작했다.\n\n호위병들은 그의 팔과 옷깃을 붙잡아 억지로 일으켜 세웠고, 요새 정문으로 이어지는 계단 쪽으로 끌고 갔다. 그동안 경찰관은 착검한 총을 들고 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n당테스는 저항하지 않았다. 그는 꿈속을 헤매는 사람 같았다. 그는 둑 위에 늘어선 병사들을 보았다. 자신이 계단을 오르고 있다는 것을 어렴풋이 알았다. 문 하나를 지났고, 그 문이 등 뒤에서 닫혔다는 것을 의식했다. 그러나 이 모든 것이 안개 너머로 보듯 흐릿했다. 그는 죄수들이 깊은 절망에 잠겨 바라보는 그 바다, 자유를 가로막는 그 무시무시한 장벽조차 보지 못했다.\n\n그들은 잠시 멈춰 섰고, 그동안 그는 흩어진 생각을 추스르려 애썼다. 그는 주위를 둘러보았다. 그는 높은 성벽으로 둘러싸인 안뜰에 있었다. 보초들의 절도 있는 발소리가 들렸고, 그들이 불빛 앞을 지날 때면 총신이 번쩍이는 것이 보였다.\n\n그들은 십 분 남짓 기다렸다. 당테스가 달아날 수 없음을 확신한 헌병들은 그를 놓아주었다. 그들은 명령을 기다리는 듯했다. 명령이 떨어졌다.\n\n\"죄수는 어디 있나?\" 한 목소리가 말했다.\n\n\"여기 있습니다.\" 헌병들이 대답했다.\n\n\"나를 따라오게 하라. 내가 감방으로 데려가겠다.\"\n\n\"가!\" 헌병들이 당테스를 앞으로 떠밀며 말했다.\n\n죄수는 안내자를 따라갔고, 그는 그를 거의 지하나 다름없는 방으로 이끌었다. 그곳의 헐벗고 축축한 벽은 눈물에 흠뻑 젖어 있는 듯했다. 걸상 위에 놓인 등잔 하나가 방을 희미하게 비추었고, 당테스는 그 빛에 안내자의 얼굴을 보았다. 옷차림이 추레하고 음침한 인상의 하급 간수였다.\n\n\"오늘 밤은 여기가 당신 방이오.\" 그가 말했다. \"시간이 늦어서 성주님은 주무시오. 내일이면 어쩌면 당신을 옮겨 줄지도 모르겠소. 그동안 빵과 물, 그리고 새 짚이 있소. 죄수가 바랄 수 있는 건 그게 다요. 잘 자시오.\" 그러고는 당테스가 입을 열기도 전에, 간수가 빵과 물을 어디에 두었는지 살피기도 전에, 짚이 놓인 구석을 흘끗 보기도 전에, 간수는 등잔을 들고 문을 닫으며 사라져 버렸다. 죄수의 머릿속에는 물방울 떨어지는 지하 감옥 벽의 희미한 잔상만이 남았다.\n\n당테스는 어둠 속에, 정적 속에 홀로 있었다. 그 어둠은 그의 달아오른 이마에 숨을 불어 대는 그림자처럼 차가웠다. 동이 트자마자 간수가 당테스를 그대로 두라는 명령을 받고 돌아왔다. 그는 죄수가 마치 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 똑같은 자세로 있는 것을 보았고, 그 두 눈은 울어서 퉁퉁 부어 있었다. 그는 밤새 선 채로, 잠도 자지 않고 보낸 것이었다. 간수가 다가왔다. 당테스는 그를 알아보지 못하는 듯했다. 간수가 그의 어깨를 건드렸다. 에드몽이 흠칫 놀랐다.\n\n\"잠을 못 잤소?\" 간수가 말했다.\n\n\"모르겠소.\" 당테스가 대답했다. 간수가 빤히 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"배가 고프오?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"모르겠소.\"\n\n\"뭐 필요한 거라도 있소?\"\n\n\"성주를 만나고 싶소.\"\n\n간수는 어깨를 으쓱하고는 방을 나갔다.\n\n당테스는 눈으로 그를 좇으며 열린 문 쪽으로 두 손을 뻗었다. 그러나 문은 닫혔다. 그러자 억눌렸던 온갖 감정이 터져 나왔다. 그는 바닥에 몸을 던지고 비통하게 울며, 대체 무슨 죄를 지었기에 이런 벌을 받는 것인지 자문했다.\n\n하루가 그렇게 흘러갔다. 그는 음식에 거의 손도 대지 않은 채, 우리에 갇힌 들짐승처럼 감방 안을 빙빙 돌았다. 한 가지 생각이 특히 그를 괴롭혔다. 곧 이곳으로 오는 동안 자신이 그토록 가만히 앉아 있었다는 것이었다. 마음만 먹었다면 열두 번도 더 바다로 뛰어들 수 있었고, 그 유명한 수영 솜씨 덕분에 뭍에 닿아, 제노바나 에스파냐 배가 올 때까지 몸을 숨겼다가, 에스파냐나 이탈리아로 달아날 수 있었을 텐데. 그러면 메르세데스와 아버지도 그곳으로 와서 합칠 수 있었을 것이다. 어떻게 먹고살지는 걱정도 되지 않았다. 솜씨 좋은 뱃사람은 어디서나 환영받으니까. 그는 토스카나 사람처럼 이탈리아어를 했고, 카스티야 사람처럼 에스파냐어를 했다. 그랬다면 그는 자유로웠을 것이고, 메르세데스와 아버지와 함께 행복했을 것이다. 그런데 지금 그는 난공불락의 요새 이프성에 갇혀, 아버지와 메르세데스의 앞날이 어찌 될지도 모르는 처지였다. 이 모든 것이 빌포르의 약속을 믿었기 때문이었다. 그 생각은 미칠 듯한 것이었고, 당테스는 분에 못 이겨 짚더미 위에 몸을 내던졌다. 이튿날 아침 같은 시각에 간수가 다시 왔다.\n\n\"자,\" 간수가 말했다. \"오늘은 좀 분별이 생겼소?\" 당테스는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"자, 기운을 내시오. 내가 해 줄 수 있는 일이 뭐 있겠소?\"\n\n\"성주를 만나고 싶소.\"\n\n\"그건 안 된다고 이미 말했잖소.\"\n\n\"어째서요?\"\n\n\"감옥 규칙에 어긋나기 때문이오. 죄수는 그런 걸 청해서도 안 되오.\"\n\n\"그럼 허락되는 건 뭐요?\"\n\n\"값을 치른다면 더 나은 식사, 그리고 책, 또 바깥을 거니는 것 정도요.\"\n\n\"책은 필요 없고, 음식은 이대로 만족하고, 거니는 데도 관심 없소. 다만 성주를 만나고 싶소.\"\n\n\"같은 말만 되풀이해서 나를 성가시게 하면, 더는 먹을 것을 가져다주지 않겠소.\"\n\n\"그럼, 좋소.\" 에드몽이 말했다. \"안 가져다주면 나는 굶어 죽겠지. 그뿐이오.\"\n\n간수는 그 말투로 그가 기꺼이 죽으려 한다는 것을 알아챘다. 그리고 죄수 하나가 간수에게 하루 십 수의 값어치가 있는 터라, 그는 한결 누그러진 목소리로 대답했다.\n\n\"당신이 청하는 건 불가능하오. 하지만 아주 얌전히 굴면 바깥을 거니는 것이 허락될 것이고, 그러다 언젠가 성주를 마주칠지도 모르오. 그때 성주가 대답해 주고 싶다면, 그건 그분 마음이지.\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 당테스가 물었다. \"그게 얼마나 기다려야 하는 거요?\"\n\n\"아, 한 달, 여섯 달, 아니면 일 년.\"\n\n\"너무 긴 시간이오. 당장 만나고 싶소.\"\n\n\"아,\" 간수가 말했다. \"불가능한 일만 곱씹지 마시오. 안 그러면 보름 만에 미쳐 버릴 테니.\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각하오?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 여기 본보기가 하나 있소. 당신보다 먼저 이 방에 있던 어떤 신부가, 자유를 달라며 성주에게 줄곧 백만 프랑을 주겠다고 하다가 미쳐 버렸지.\"\n\n\"그가 여기를 떠난 지 얼마나 됐소?\"\n\n\"이 년.\"\n\n\"그럼 풀려난 거요?\"\n\n\"아니. 지하 감옥에 처넣어졌소.\"\n\n\"잘 들으시오!\" 당테스가 말했다. \"나는 신부가 아니고, 미치지도 않았소. 어쩌면 미칠지도 모르지만, 지금은 안타깝게도 멀쩡하오. 다른 제안을 하나 하겠소.\"\n\n\"그게 뭐요?\"\n\n\"당신에게 백만 프랑을 주겠다는 건 아니오. 내겐 그런 돈이 없으니까. 하지만 다음번 마르세유에 갈 때, 카탈루냐 마을에 사는 메르세데스라는 처녀를 찾아가 내 글 두 줄을 전해 준다면, 백 크라운을 주겠소.\"\n\n\"그걸 가져갔다가 들키면 나는 일자리를 잃을 거요. 일 년에 이천 프랑짜리 자리란 말이오. 그러니 삼백 프랑 때문에 그런 위험을 무릅쓴다면 내가 천하의 바보지.\"\n\n\"좋소,\" 당테스가 말했다. \"그럼 이 말을 새겨들으시오. 적어도 내가 여기 있다는 걸 메르세데스에게 알려 주는 것조차 거절한다면, 언젠가 문 뒤에 숨어 있다가 당신이 들어올 때 이 걸상으로 당신 머리통을 박살 내겠소.\"\n\n\"협박이라니!\" 간수가 뒤로 물러서며 방어 자세를 취하고 외쳤다. \"당신 정말 미쳐 가는군. 그 신부도 당신처럼 시작했지. 사흘이면 당신도 그자처럼 될 거요. 꽁꽁 묶어 둘 만큼 단단히 미칠 테지. 하지만 다행히도 여기엔 지하 감옥이 있소.\"\n\n당테스가 걸상을 머리 위로 휘둘렀다.\n\n\"알았소, 알았어.\" 간수가 말했다. \"알았으니, 정 그렇다면. 성주께 말씀을 전하겠소.\"\n\n\"좋소.\" 당테스가 걸상을 내려놓고는 정말로 미친 사람처럼 그 위에 앉으며 대꾸했다. 간수는 나갔다가, 곧 하사 한 명과 병사 넷을 데리고 돌아왔다.\n\n\"성주의 명령이다.\" 그가 말했다. \"이 죄수를 아래층으로 데려가라.\"\n\n\"그럼 지하 감옥으로군.\" 하사가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다. 미친놈은 미친놈들 틈에 넣어야지.\" 병사들이 당테스를 붙잡았고, 그는 순순히 따라갔다.\n\n그는 열다섯 계단을 내려갔고, 지하 감옥 문이 열리자 그 안으로 떠밀려 들어갔다. 문이 닫혔고, 당테스는 두 손을 앞으로 뻗은 채 벽에 닿을 때까지 나아갔다. 그런 다음 눈이 어둠에 익을 때까지 구석에 앉아 있었다. 간수의 말이 옳았다. 당테스는 완전히 미치기까지 그리 멀지 않았다.\n\n# Chapter 9. The Evening of the Betrothal\n\n빌포르는 앞서 말한 대로 그랑쿠르 광장에 있는 생메랑 부인의 저택으로 서둘러 돌아왔다. 집에 들어서니 식탁에 두고 떠났던 손님들이 살롱에서 커피를 마시고 있었다. 르네는 다른 사람들과 함께 그를 애타게 기다리고 있었고, 그가 들어서자 모두가 일제히 탄성을 질렀다.\n\n\"자, 단두대 집행관, 국가의 수호자, 왕당파, 브루투스 양반, 무슨 일이오?\" 한 사람이 말했다. \"어디 털어놓아 보시오.\"\n\n\"또다시 공포 정치의 위협이라도 닥친 거요?\" 다른 사람이 물었다.\n\n\"코르시카의 식인귀가 풀려나기라도 했소?\" 또 다른 사람이 외쳤다.\n\n\"후작 부인,\" 빌포르가 장차 장모가 될 사람에게 다가가며 말했다. \"이렇게 자리를 비우게 되어 용서를 구합니다. 후작님께 잠시 단둘이 말씀드릴 영광을 베풀어 주실 수 있겠습니까?\"\n\n\"아, 그렇다면 정말 심각한 일이로군?\" 후작이 빌포르의 이마에 드리운 그늘을 알아채고 물었다.\n\n\"며칠 동안 떠나 있어야 할 만큼 심각합니다. 그러니,\" 그가 르네 쪽으로 돌아서며 덧붙였다. \"그것이 중요한 일인지 아닌지는 직접 판단해 보시지요.\"\n\n\"저희를 떠나신다고요?\" 르네가 이 뜻밖의 통보에 동요를 감추지 못하고 외쳤다.\n\n\"아아,\" 빌포르가 대답했다. \"어쩔 수 없습니다!\"\n\n\"그래, 어디로 가시는 겁니까?\" 후작 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"그건, 부인, 공무상의 비밀입니다. 다만 파리에 부탁하실 일이 있으시면, 오늘 밤 제 친구 하나가 그리로 가니 기꺼이 맡아 줄 겁니다.\" 손님들이 서로 얼굴을 마주 보았다.\n\n\"나하고만 이야기하고 싶다는 건가?\" 후작이 말했다.\n\n\"네, 서재로 가시지요.\" 후작이 그의 팔을 잡았고, 두 사람은 살롱을 나섰다.\n\n\"자,\" 단둘이 되자마자 후작이 물었다. \"무슨 일인지 말해 보게.\"\n\n\"더없이 중대한 사안이라, 제가 당장 파리로 가야 합니다. 그런데, 실례를 무릅쓰고 여쭙겠습니다만, 후작님께서는 토지 재산이 있으신지요?\"\n\n\"내 재산은 전부 공채에 들어 있네. 칠팔십만 프랑쯤 되지.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 파십시오. 어서 파십시오, 후작님. 안 그러면 전부 잃으실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 여기서 어떻게 판단 말인가?\"\n\n\"증권 중개인이 있으시지요?\"\n\n\"있네.\"\n\n\"그럼 그에게 편지를 한 통 써 주십시오. 한순간도 지체 말고 팔아 치우라고 하십시오. 어쩌면 지금도 이미 늦었을지 모릅니다.\"\n\n\"이런 변고가!\" 후작이 대답했다. \"그렇다면 한시도 허비할 수 없지!\"\n\n그러고는 자리에 앉아 중개인에게 시세대로 팔아 달라는 편지를 썼다.\n\n\"자, 그럼,\" 빌포르가 편지를 수첩에 넣으며 말했다. \"한 통이 더 필요합니다!\"\n\"누구한테?\"\n\n\"왕께 보낼 편지입니다.\"\n\n\"왕께?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"나는 감히 폐하께 편지를 쓸 수 없네.\"\n\n\"폐하께 직접 쓰시라는 게 아닙니다. 살비외 씨께 부탁해 써 달라고 하십시오. 알현을 청하는 온갖 격식을 거치지 않고도 왕 앞에 나아갈 수 있게 해 줄 편지가 필요합니다. 그런 절차를 밟다가는 귀중한 시간을 잃게 될 테니까요.\"\n\n\"하지만 옥새 상서에게 부탁하게. 그분은 튀일리 궁에 출입할 권한이 있어서, 밤이든 낮이든 언제든 알현을 주선해 줄 수 있네.\"\n\n\"물론입니다. 하지만 제 발견의 영예를 그분과 나눌 까닭이 없지요. 옥새 상서는 저를 뒷전으로 밀어내고 모든 공을 제 것으로 가로챌 겁니다. 분명히 말씀드리지만, 후작님, 제가 튀일리 궁에 가장 먼저 닿기만 한다면 제 출세는 따 놓은 당상입니다. 왕께서는 제가 베푼 봉사를 잊지 않으실 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 어서 가서 채비하게. 내가 살비외를 불러 편지를 쓰게 하겠네.\"\n\n\"되도록 빨리 부탁드립니다. 십오 분 안에 길을 나서야 하니까요.\"\n\n\"마부에게 문 앞에 대기하라고 일러 두게.\"\n\n\"후작 부인과 르네 양께 제 사정을 잘 전해 주십시오. 하필 이런 날 떠나게 되어 더없이 안타깝습니다.\"\n\n\"두 사람 다 여기 있을 테니, 직접 작별 인사를 하면 되네.\"\n\n\"정말 감사합니다. 그럼 이제 편지를 부탁드립니다.\"\n\n후작이 종을 울리자 하인이 들어왔다.\n\n\"살비외 백작께 좀 뵙고 싶다고 전하게.\"\n\n\"자, 그럼 가 보게.\" 후작이 말했다.\n\n\"잠깐이면 돌아오겠습니다.\"\n\n빌포르는 황급히 방을 나섰다. 그러나 차장 검사가 거리를 내달리는 모습이 온 도시를 혼란에 빠뜨릴 수 있다는 데 생각이 미쳐, 평소의 걸음걸이로 돌아갔다. 자기 집 문 앞에 이르자 그림자 속에서 그를 기다리는 듯한 사람의 형체가 보였다. 메르세데스였다. 연인의 소식을 듣지 못한 그녀가 그를 찾아 남몰래 온 것이었다.\n\n빌포르가 다가가자 그녀가 앞으로 나서서 그의 앞에 섰다. 당테스가 메르세데스 이야기를 한 적이 있어, 빌포르는 그녀를 단번에 알아보았다. 그녀의 아름다움과 기품 있는 자태에 그는 놀랐고, 그녀가 자기 연인이 어떻게 되었느냐고 묻자, 마치 그녀가 재판관이고 자기가 피고가 된 듯한 기분이 들었다.\n\n\"그대가 말하는 그 젊은이는,\" 빌포르가 퉁명스럽게 말했다. \"중대한 죄인이오. 나로서는 그를 위해 해 줄 수 있는 일이 없소, 아가씨.\" 메르세데스가 울음을 터뜨렸고, 빌포르가 그녀를 지나치려 하자 다시 말을 걸었다.\n\n\"하지만 적어도 그이가 어디 있는지는 알려 주세요. 살아 있는지 죽었는지라도 알 수 있게요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"나는 모르오. 그는 더 이상 내 손에 있지 않소.\" 빌포르가 대답했다.\n\n그러고는 그 대면을 끝내고 싶은 마음에, 그녀를 밀치고 지나가 마치 자신이 느끼는 고통을 차단하려는 듯 문을 닫았다. 그러나 양심의 가책은 그렇게 쫓아낼 수 있는 것이 아니었다. 베르길리우스가 그린 부상당한 영웅처럼, 그는 화살을 상처에 박은 채로 살롱에 이르렀고, 빌포르는 흐느낌에 가까운 한숨을 내쉬며 의자에 털썩 주저앉았다.\n\n그러자 끝없는 고문의 첫 고통이 그의 가슴을 사로잡았다. 자신의 야망을 위해 제물로 바친 사내, 아비의 죄과를 위해 제단에 바쳐진 그 무고한 희생자가, 창백하고 위협하는 모습으로 약혼녀의 손을 잡아 이끌고 그의 앞에 나타났다. 그리고 그와 함께 양심의 가책을 끌고 왔다. 그것은 고대인들이 그린, 광포하고 끔찍한 모습이 아니라, 죽음의 바로 그 순간까지 시시각각 그 고통이 더해 가는, 더디고 좀먹는 듯한 번민이었다. 그러자 그는 잠시 망설였다. 그는 그동안 죄인들에게 극형을 내려야 한다고 숱하게 주장해 왔고, 거역할 수 없는 그의 웅변 덕에 그들은 유죄 판결을 받았다. 그런데도 양심의 가책이 그 그림자 한 점조차 빌포르의 이마를 흐린 적이 없었다. 그들은 죄가 있었기 때문이다. 적어도 그는 그렇게 믿었다. 하지만 지금 여기 있는 것은 자기가 행복을 짓밟아 버린 무고한 사람이었다. 이번에는 그가 재판관이 아니라 사형 집행인이었다.\n\n그렇게 곱씹는 사이, 그는 앞서 묘사한, 여태껏 그가 알지 못했던 그 감각이 가슴속에서 솟아올라 막연한 불안으로 자신을 채우는 것을 느꼈다. 그것은 부상당한 사람이 상처가 아물 때까지 그 상처로 손가락이 다가오면 본능적으로 떠는 것과 같았다. 그러나 빌포르의 상처는 결코 아물지 않는 부류의 것이었다. 설령 아문다 해도 그것은 전보다 더 고통스럽게 다시 벌어지기 위해 아무는 것일 뿐이었다. 만약 이 순간 르네의 다정한 목소리가 그의 귀에 자비를 청하며 들려왔거나, 아름다운 메르세데스가 들어와 \"하느님의 이름으로 간청하니, 제 약혼자를 돌려주세요.\" 하고 말했더라면, 차갑게 떨리는 그의 두 손은 그의 석방 서류에 서명하고 말았을 것이다. 그러나 그 어떤 목소리도 방 안의 정적을 깨뜨리지 않았고, 문이 열린 것은 그저 여행 마차가 준비되었음을 알리러 온 빌포르의 하인에 의해서였다.\n\n빌포르가 자리에서 일어났다, 아니 벌떡 솟구쳐 올랐다. 그는 황급히 책상 서랍 하나를 열어 안에 든 금화를 전부 호주머니에 쏟아 넣고는, 한 손을 머리에 짚은 채 잠시 꼼짝 않고 서 있다가, 알아들을 수 없는 소리를 몇 마디 중얼거렸다. 그러다 하인이 자기 어깨에 외투를 걸쳐 준 것을 알아채고는 마차 안으로 뛰어들어, 마부에게 생메랑 씨 댁으로 몰라고 명령했다. 가엾은 당테스의 운명은 결정된 것이었다.\n\n후작이 약속한 대로, 빌포르는 후작 부인과 르네가 기다리고 있는 것을 보았다. 르네를 보자 그는 흠칫했다. 그녀가 또다시 당테스를 위해 호소하려는 줄 알았기 때문이다. 그러나 아아, 그녀의 감정은 온전히 자신에 관한 것이었다. 그녀는 오직 빌포르가 떠난다는 사실만을 생각하고 있었다.\n\n그녀는 빌포르를 사랑했고, 그는 막 그녀의 남편이 되려는 순간에 그녀를 두고 떠나는 것이었다. 빌포르는 자신이 언제 돌아올지 알 수 없었고, 르네는 당테스를 위해 호소하기는커녕, 자신을 연인에게서 갈라놓은 그 죄인을 미워했다.\n\n그러는 사이 메르세데스는 어찌 되었는가? 그녀는 로주 거리 모퉁이에서 페르낭과 마주쳤다. 그녀는 카탈루냐 마을로 돌아와 절망에 빠진 채 침상에 몸을 던졌다. 페르낭이 그녀 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 그녀의 손을 잡아 입맞춤을 퍼부었으나, 메르세데스는 그것을 느끼지조차 못했다. 그녀는 그렇게 밤을 지새웠다. 등불은 기름이 떨어져 꺼졌으나 그녀는 어둠에도 아랑곳하지 않았고, 새벽이 왔으나 날이 밝은 줄도 몰랐다. 슬픔이 그녀를 단 하나의 대상, 곧 에드몽 외에는 아무것도 보지 못하게 만든 것이었다.\n\n\"아, 거기 계셨군요.\" 그녀가 마침내 페르낭 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다.\n\n\"어제부터 줄곧 곁을 떠나지 않았어.\" 페르낭이 서글프게 대답했다.\n\n모렐 씨는 호락호락 싸움을 포기하지 않았다. 그는 당테스가 감옥으로 끌려갔다는 것을 알고는, 친구란 친구와 도시의 유력자들을 모조리 찾아다녔다. 그러나 당테스가 보나파르트파 첩자로 체포되었다는 소문이 이미 돌고 있었다. 게다가 가장 낙관적인 사람들조차 나폴레옹이 다시 왕좌에 오르려는 시도는 불가능한 일로 여겼던 터라, 그는 거절만 당하다가 절망에 빠져 집으로 돌아왔고, 사안이 심각해서 더 이상 할 수 있는 일이 없다고 토로했다.\n\n카드루스도 못지않게 안절부절못하며 불안에 시달렸다. 그러나 그는 모렐 씨처럼 당테스를 도우려 하기는커녕, 까막까치밥나무 브랜디 두 병을 들고 틀어박혀 생각을 잊어 보려 했다. 그러나 뜻대로 되지 않았다. 술을 더 가지러 갈 수 없을 만큼은 취했으나, 일어난 일을 잊을 만큼 취하지는 못한 것이다. 그는 두 팔꿈치를 탁자에 괴고 빈 병 두 개 사이에 앉아 있었고, 심지를 다듬지 않은 촛불 빛 속에서 망령들이 춤을 추었다. 호프만이 펀치에 흠뻑 젖은 책장 위에 검은 환상의 먼지처럼 흩뿌려 놓은 그런 망령들이었다.\n\n오직 당글라르만이 흡족하고 즐거웠다. 그는 적을 없앴고, 파라옹호에서 자기 자리를 굳건히 했다. 당글라르는 귀 뒤에 펜을 꽂고, 심장 대신 잉크병을 단 채 태어난 부류의 사람이었다. 그에게는 모든 것이 곱셈 아니면 뺄셈이었다. 한 인간의 목숨도 그에게는 숫자 하나보다 훨씬 하찮은 것이었다. 더구나 그것을 없앰으로써 자신의 욕망을 합산한 총액을 늘릴 수 있을 때라면 더욱 그러했다. 그는 여느 때와 같은 시각에 잠자리에 들었고, 평온하게 잠들었다.\n\n빌포르는 살비외 씨의 편지를 받고 나서 르네를 끌어안고, 후작 부인의 손에 입을 맞추고, 후작과 악수를 나눈 뒤, 엑스 가도를 따라 파리로 출발했다.\n\n늙은 당테스 노인은 에드몽이 어찌 되었는지 알고 싶은 마음에 애가 타 죽을 지경이었다. 그러나 에드몽이 어찌 되었는지는 우리가 잘 알고 있다.\n\n# Chapter 10. The King’s Closet at the Tuileries\n\n우리는 빌포르를 파리로 향하는 길 위에 — 세 배의 요금 덕분에 전속력으로 달리며 — 남겨 두기로 하고, 두세 개의 방을 지나, 튈르리궁에 있는 아치형 창이 달린 작은 방으로 들어가자. 나폴레옹과 루이 18세, 그리고 지금은 루이 필리프가 가장 아끼는 방으로 잘 알려진 곳이다.\n\n그곳, 하트웰에서 가져온 호두나무 탁자 앞에 — 위인들에게 드물지 않은 그런 변덕 가운데 하나로, 그는 그 탁자에 유독 애착이 깊었다 — 국왕 루이 18세가 앉아, 쉰이나 쉰두 살쯤 된 한 사내의 말을 무심히 듣고 있었다. 그 사내는 머리가 희끗하고, 귀족적인 풍채에, 더없이 신사다운 차림새였다. 그러는 동안 국왕은 그리피우스판 호라티우스 시집 — 다소 부정확하지만 무척 찾는 이가 많은 판본 — 의 여백에 주석을 달고 있었는데, 그 판본은 이 철학적인 군주의 명민한 관찰에 적잖이 빚지고 있었다.\n\n\"경의 말씀은……\" 국왕이 말했다.\n\n\"제가 몹시 불안하다는 것입니다, 폐하.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 살진 암소 일곱 마리와 야윈 암소 일곱 마리의 환영이라도 보았소?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 폐하. 그것이라면 우리에게 칠 년의 풍년과 칠 년의 흉년을 뜻할 뿐이니까요. 그리고 폐하처럼 선견지명이 가득하신 군주를 모신 마당에, 흉년 따위는 두려워할 일이 아니지요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 또 무슨 재앙이 두렵단 말이오, 나의 블라카스 경?\"\n\n\"폐하, 저는 남부에 폭풍이 일고 있다고 믿을 만한 근거가 충분합니다.\"\n\n\"이보게, 공작,\" 루이 18세가 대답했다. \"내 생각엔 경이 잘못된 정보를 들은 게요. 오히려 그쪽은 아주 날씨가 좋다는 걸 나는 분명히 알고 있소.\" 유능한 사람답게, 루이 18세는 기분 좋은 익살을 즐겼다.\n\n\"폐하,\" 블라카스 씨가 말을 이었다. \"충직한 신하 하나를 안심시키기 위해서라도, 폐하께서 랑그도크와 프로방스, 도피네에 믿을 만한 사람들을 보내시어, 이 세 지방의 민심에 관해 충실한 보고를 받아 보시는 것이 어떻겠습니까?\"\n\n\"카니무스 수르디스.\" 국왕이 호라티우스에 주석을 계속 달며 대답했다.\n\n\"폐하,\" 신하가 그 인용을 알아듣는 척하려고 웃으며 대답했다. \"프랑스의 선량한 민심을 믿으시는 폐하의 판단이 더없이 옳을지도 모릅니다. 하오나 어떤 무모한 시도를 염려하는 저의 두려움도 아주 틀리지만은 않을 듯합니다.\"\n\n\"누구에 의해서요?\" \"보나파르트, 적어도 그의 추종자들에 의한 것입니다.\"\n\n\"나의 블라카스 경,\" 국왕이 말했다. \"경은 그 불안 타령으로 내가 일을 못 하게 만드는구려.\"\n\n\"그리고 폐하께서는 그 태평하심으로 제가 잠을 이루지 못하게 하십니다.\"\n\n\"기다리시오, 경, 잠깐만 기다리시오. 마침 '파스토르 쿰 트라헤레트' 구절에 대해 아주 흥미로운 주석거리가 떠올랐으니. 기다리시오, 그런 뒤에 경의 말을 듣겠소.\"\n\n잠시 침묵이 흘렀고, 그동안 루이 18세는 호라티우스의 여백에 되도록 잔글씨로 또 하나의 주석을 적었다. 그러고는 남의 생각에 주석을 다는 것뿐이면서 마치 제 나름의 착상이 떠올랐다고 여기는 사람의 표정으로 공작을 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"계속하시오, 공작, 계속하시오 — 듣고 있소.\"\n\n\"폐하,\" 블라카스가 말했다. 그는 한순간 빌포르를 제 잇속을 위해 희생시킬 수 있으리라는 희망을 품었다. \"감히 말씀드리오니, 저를 이토록 불안하게 하는 것은 근거 없는 한낱 풍문 따위가 아닙니다. 제가 전적으로 신뢰하며 남부를 감시하라고 맡긴 진중한 인물이\" (공작은 이 말을 하면서 잠시 머뭇거렸다) \"역마를 달려와, 국왕께 큰 위험이 닥쳐오고 있다고 전했습니다. 그래서 서둘러 폐하께 달려온 것입니다.\"\n\n\"말라 두키스 아비 도뭄.\" 루이 18세가 여전히 주석을 달며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"이 이야기를 그만두기를 바라시옵니까, 폐하?\"\n\n\"천만에, 공작. 다만 손 좀 뻗어 보시오.\"\n\"어느 쪽을요?\"\n\n\"아무 쪽이나 좋소 — 저기 왼쪽에.\"\n\n\"여기 말씀입니까, 폐하?\"\n\"왼쪽이라 했더니 오른쪽을 보고 있구려. 내 왼쪽 말이오 — 그래, 거기. 어제 자 경찰청장 보고서가 있을 게요. 아, 마침 당드레 청장이 직접 왔군.\" 그러자 시종이 알린 대로 당드레 씨가 들어왔다.\n\n\"들어오시오,\" 루이 18세가 웃음을 억누르며 말했다. \"들어와서 공작에게 그대가 아는 것을 모두 말해 주시오 — 보나파르트 씨에 관한 최신 소식 말이오. 아무리 심각한 것이라도 숨기지 마시오. 자, 봅시다, 엘바섬은 화산이니, 그곳에서 화염을 내뿜고 가시 돋친 전쟁이 솟아 나오리라 — 벨라, 호리다 벨라.\"\n\n당드레 씨는 두 손으로 의자 등받이에 아주 공손히 기대며 말했다.\n\n\"폐하께서는 어제 자 보고서를 읽어 보셨습니까?\"\n\n\"읽었소, 읽었소. 허나 아무것도 못 찾는 공작에게 직접 그 보고서의 내용을 말해 주시오 — 그 찬탈자가 제 작은 섬에서 무슨 짓을 하고 있는지 소상히 일러 주시오.\"\n\n\"공작님,\" 남작이 공작에게 말했다. \"폐하의 모든 신하께서 엘바섬에서 온 최신 정보를 반기실 것입니다. 보나파르트는……\"\n\n당드레 씨가 루이 18세를 바라보았으나, 국왕은 주석을 적느라 고개조차 들지 않았다. \"보나파르트는,\" 남작이 말을 이었다. \"지독한 권태에 빠져, 포르토롱고네에서 광부들이 일하는 모습을 지켜보며 날을 통째로 보내고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 심심풀이로 제 몸을 긁어 대지.\" 국왕이 덧붙였다.\n\n\"몸을 긁다니요?\" 공작이 물었다. \"폐하, 무슨 말씀이십니까?\"\n\n\"그렇다오, 공작. 그 위대한 인물, 그 영웅, 그 반신(半神)이 사람을 죽도록 괴롭히는 피부병에 걸렸다는 걸 잊으셨소? 가려움증 말이오, 프루리고.\"\n\n\"게다가 말입니다, 공작님,\" 경찰청장이 말을 이었다. \"머지않아 그 찬탈자가 미쳐 버릴 거라는 게 거의 확실합니다.\"\n\n\"미쳐?\"\n\n\"완전히 광인이 되는 겁니다. 머리가 점점 약해지고 있어요. 어느 때는 비통하게 울고, 어느 때는 떠들썩하게 웃고, 또 어느 때는 몇 시간씩 해변에서 물수제비를 뜬답니다. 그러다 돌멩이가 대여섯 번 튀면, 마치 또 한 번 마렝고나 아우스터리츠에서 승리라도 거둔 양 기뻐한다더군요. 자, 이것이야말로 광기의 확실한 징후라는 데 동의하실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"아니면 지혜의 징후일 수도 있지, 친애하는 남작 — 지혜의 징후 말이오.\" 루이 18세가 웃으며 말했다. \"고대의 가장 위대한 장군들도 바다에 돌멩이를 던지며 즐겼다오 — 플루타르코스가 쓴 스키피오 아프리카누스 전기를 보시오.\"\n\n블라카스 씨는 자신만만한 군주와 진실을 말하는 청장 사이에서 깊은 생각에 잠겼다. 빌포르는 폭로의 이득을 남이 다 거둘까 봐 비밀을 전부 털어놓지는 않았으나, 공작을 더없이 불안하게 만들 만큼은 일러 둔 터였다.\n\n\"좋소, 좋소, 당드레,\" 루이 18세가 말했다. \"블라카스가 아직도 수긍하지 못하니, 그렇다면 그 찬탈자의 개심(改心) 이야기로 넘어갑시다.\" 경찰청장이 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"찬탈자의 개심이라!\" 공작이 국왕과 당드레를 번갈아 바라보며 중얼거렸다. 두 사람이 베르길리우스의 목동들처럼 번갈아 말을 주고받았던 것이다. \"찬탈자가 개심을 했다고!\"\n\n\"분명히 그렇소, 공작.\"\n\n\"어떤 식으로 개심을 했다는 말씀입니까?\"\n\n\"올바른 신념으로 말이오. 남작, 그에게 자초지종을 일러 주시오.\"\n\n\"실은 이렇습니다.\" 청장이 세상에 더없이 진중한 표정으로 말했다. \"나폴레옹이 얼마 전 사열을 했는데, 늙은 노병 두셋이 프랑스로 돌아가고 싶다는 뜻을 비치자, 그들을 제대시키며 '선량하신 국왕을 섬기라'고 권했답니다. 이것이 바로 그자가 직접 한 말입니다. 그 점은 제가 확신합니다.\"\n\n\"자, 블라카스, 이 일을 어찌 생각하시오?\" 국왕이 의기양양하게 물으며, 앞에 놓인 방대한 주석서에서 잠시 손을 멈췄다.\n\n\"폐하, 제 말씀은, 경찰청장이 크게 속고 있거나, 아니면 제가 속고 있다는 것입니다. 그런데 폐하의 안전과 명예를 지키는 일을 맡은 경찰청장이 속았을 리 없으니, 아마도 제가 잘못 안 것이겠지요. 하오나 폐하, 제 의견을 올려도 된다면, 제가 말씀드린 그 인물을 직접 신문해 주시기를 청하옵고, 부디 그자에게 그런 영광을 베풀어 주시기를 폐하께 간곡히 권하옵니다.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그러겠소, 공작. 경이 천거하는 사람이라면 누구든 맞이하겠소. 다만 내가 너무 쉽게 믿으리라 기대하지는 마시오. 남작, 이 2월 20일 자 보고서보다 더 최근의 것은 없소? 오늘이 벌써 3월 3일인데.\"\n\n\"없습니다, 폐하. 하오나 시시각각 도착하기를 기다리고 있습니다. 제가 청을 나선 사이에 이미 도착했을지도 모르지요.\"\n\n\"그리로 가 보시오. 만일 없거든 — 뭐, 좋소,\" 루이 18세가 말을 이었다. \"하나 지어내시오. 그게 으레 하는 방식이지 않소?\" 그러고는 국왕이 익살스럽게 웃었다.\n\n\"오, 폐하,\" 청장이 대답했다. \"굳이 지어낼 필요도 없습니다. 날마다 저희 책상에는 더없이 소상한 밀고가 산더미처럼 쌓이니까요. 자기가 베풀려 하나 베풀지 못하는 공로에 대해 무언가 보답을 바라는 무리에게서 옵니다. 그들은 요행에 기대어, 어떤 뜻밖의 사건이 어떻게든 제 예언을 입증해 주기를 바라는 것이지요.\"\n\n\"좋소, 그럼 가 보시오.\" 루이 18세가 말했다. \"내가 기다리고 있다는 걸 잊지 마시오.\"\n\n\"다녀와서 곧 돌아오겠습니다, 폐하. 십 분이면 돌아오겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 저는, 폐하,\" 블라카스 씨가 말했다. \"제 전령을 데려오겠습니다.\"\n\n\"기다리시오, 경, 기다리시오.\" 루이 18세가 말했다. \"정말이지, 블라카스 경, 내 경의 문장(紋章)을 바꿔 주어야겠소. 날개를 활짝 편 독수리가 발톱에 헛되이 달아나려 발버둥치는 먹이를 움켜쥔 그림에, '테낙스'라는 명구를 붙여 주리다.\"\n\n\"폐하, 듣고 있습니다.\" 드 블라카스가 초조함에 손톱을 깨물며 말했다.\n\n\"이 구절에 대해 경의 의견을 듣고 싶소. '몰리 푸기엔스 안헬리투', 늑대에게서 달아나는 사슴을 두고 한 말인 줄 경도 알 게요. 경은 사냥꾼이자 이름난 늑대 사냥꾼이 아니오? 그러니, 이 '몰리 안헬리투'를 어찌 생각하시오?\"\n\n\"훌륭합니다, 폐하. 하오나 제 전령이야말로 폐하께서 말씀하신 그 사슴 같습니다. 사흘이 채 안 되어 220리외를 역마로 달려왔으니까요.\"\n\n\"그건 큰 피로와 노심초사를 겪은 셈이오, 공작. 우리에게는 서너 시간 만에 소식을 전하는 전신이 있고, 그것도 숨 한 번 가빠지지 않으니 말이오.\"\n\n\"아, 폐하, 폐하께서는 이토록 멀리서, 이토록 열성으로 폐하께 유익한 정보를 바치러 온 가엾은 젊은이에게 너무 박하게 대하십니다. 그를 제게 천거한 드 살비외 씨의 낯을 보아서라도, 부디 그를 너그러이 맞아 주시기를 폐하께 간청하옵니다.\"\n\n\"드 살비외라면, 내 아우의 시종 말이오?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 폐하.\"\n\n\"그는 마르세유에 있소.\"\n\n\"거기서 제게 편지를 보냈습니다.\"\n\n\"그가 경에게 이 음모에 대해 말하던가?\"\n\n\"아닙니다. 다만 드 빌포르 씨를 강력히 천거하며, 그를 폐하께 알현시켜 달라고 청했습니다.\"\n\n\"드 빌포르 씨라니!\" 국왕이 외쳤다. \"그 전령의 이름이 드 빌포르 씨란 말이오?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 폐하.\"\n\n\"그리고 마르세유에서 왔다고?\"\n\n\"직접 말입니다.\"\n\n\"어찌하여 진작 그 이름을 말하지 않았소?\" 국왕이 약간의 불안을 내비치며 대답했다.\n\n\"폐하, 그 이름을 폐하께서 모르시리라 여겼습니다.\"\n\n\"아니오, 아니오, 블라카스. 그는 강하고 고매한 식견을 지닌 자요, 야심도 크지. 게다가 빌어먹을, 경도 그 아비의 이름을 알지 않소!\"\n\n\"그 아비를요?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 누아르티에 말이오.\"\n\n\"지롱드파의 누아르티에 말씀입니까? — 원로원 의원 누아르티에?\"\n\n\"바로 그자요.\"\n\n\"그런 자의 아들을 폐하께서 쓰셨단 말씀입니까?\"\n\n\"블라카스, 친구, 경은 도무지 이해가 짧구려. 내 빌포르가 야심가라 하지 않았소. 그 야심을 이루기 위해서라면 빌포르는 무엇이든, 제 아비조차도 희생할 자요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 폐하, 그를 들여보내도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"당장이오, 공작! 그가 어디 있소?\"\n\n\"아래, 제 마차 안에서 기다리고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"어서 데려오시오.\"\n\n\"서둘러 그리하겠습니다.\"\n\n공작은 젊은이 같은 날렵함으로 어전을 물러났다. 진심에서 우러난 왕당파 충정이 그를 다시 젊게 만든 것이다. 루이 18세는 홀로 남아, 반쯤 펼쳐진 호라티우스로 눈길을 돌리며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"유스툼 에트 테나켐 프로포시티 비룸.\"\n\n드 블라카스 씨는 떠날 때와 똑같이 잰걸음으로 돌아왔으나, 대기실에서 국왕의 권위를 빌려야 했다. 빌포르의 먼지투성이 옷차림, 궁정식 재단과는 거리가 먼 그 의복이 드 브레제 씨의 신경을 곤두서게 했던 것이다. 그는 이 젊은이가 그런 차림으로 감히 국왕 앞에 들겠다는 데 그저 경악할 뿐이었다. 하나 공작은 한마디 말로 모든 난관을 물리쳤으니 — 폐하의 명령이었다. 그리하여 의전관이 제 직분과 신조의 명예를 걸고 항의했음에도, 빌포르는 안으로 들여보내졌다.\n\n국왕은 공작이 그를 떠났던 바로 그 자리에 앉아 있었다. 문을 열자 빌포르는 정면으로 국왕과 마주하게 되었고, 젊은 판사는 첫 충동에 걸음을 멈추었다.\n\n\"들어오시오, 드 빌포르 씨.\" 국왕이 말했다. \"들어오시오.\"\n\n빌포르는 절을 하고, 몇 걸음 나아간 뒤, 국왕이 물어 오기를 기다렸다.\n\n\"드 빌포르 씨,\" 루이 18세가 말했다. \"드 블라카스 공작이 그대가 전할 흥미로운 정보를 가지고 있다고 장담하더군.\"\n\n\"폐하, 공작의 말씀이 옳습니다. 그리고 폐하께서도 그에 못지않게 중대하다 여기시리라 믿습니다.\"\n\n\"무엇보다 먼저 말이오, 그대 생각에도 그 소식이 내가 들은 만큼이나 나쁜 것이오?\"\n\n\"폐하, 제가 보기에는 더없이 긴박합니다. 하오나 제가 서둘러 온 덕에, 돌이킬 수 없는 지경은 아니기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"마음껏 소상히 말해 보시오.\" 국왕이 말했다. 블라카스의 얼굴에 드러났고 빌포르의 목소리에 배어 있던 그 동요가 국왕에게도 번지기 시작했다. \"말해 보시오, 그리고 부디 처음부터 시작하시오. 나는 무슨 일이든 순서가 있는 것을 좋아하오.\"\n\n\"폐하,\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"폐하께 충실히 보고를 올리겠습니다. 다만 제 불안으로 말미암아 말이 다소 분명치 못하더라도 용서를 구하옵니다.\" 이 신중하고 교묘한 서두를 던진 뒤 국왕을 슬쩍 살핀 빌포르는, 자신의 존귀한 청자가 너그러운 마음임을 확인하고 말을 이었다.\n\n\"폐하, 저는 직무를 수행하던 중에, 날마다 하층민이나 군대에서 꾸며지는 흔하고 하찮은 음모가 아니라, 실로 폐하의 옥좌마저 위협하는 폭풍과도 같은 진짜 역모를 발견하였기에, 되도록 빨리 파리로 달려와 이를 폐하께 아뢰는 것입니다. 폐하, 그 찬탈자가 배 세 척을 무장시키고 있습니다. 그는 어떤 계획을 꾸미고 있는데, 아무리 미친 짓이라 해도 어쩌면 무서운 결과를 낳을지 모릅니다. 지금쯤 그는 엘바섬을 떠났을 것입니다. 어디로 향했는지는 알 수 없으나, 분명 나폴리든 토스카나 해안이든, 혹은 프랑스 연안이든 어딘가에 상륙을 꾀하려는 것입니다. 폐하께서도 엘바섬의 주인이 이탈리아 및 프랑스와 관계를 유지해 왔음을 잘 아시지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"알고 있소.\" 국왕이 몹시 동요하여 말했다. \"그리고 최근에는 보나파르트파 모임이 생자크 거리에서 회합을 가졌다는 정보도 들었소. 허나 계속하시오, 부탁이오. 그런 자세한 것들을 어찌 알아냈소?\"\n\n\"폐하, 그것들은 제가 한동안 감시하다가 출발하던 날 체포한 마르세유의 한 사내를 신문하여 얻은 결과입니다. 이 자는 성격이 거친 뱃사람으로, 제가 보나파르트파라고 의심하던 자인데, 몰래 엘바섬에 다녀왔습니다. 그곳에서 그자는 대원수를 만났고, 대원수는 그자에게 파리에 있는 어느 보나파르트파에게 구두로 전할 전갈을 맡겼습니다. 그자에게서 그 사람의 이름은 끝내 캐내지 못했으나, 그 임무란 곧 다가올 복귀에 대비해 민심을 준비시키는 것이었습니다 — 이건 그자가 한 말입니다, 폐하 — 머지않아 일어날 복귀 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 그자는 지금 어디 있소?\"\n\n\"감옥에 있습니다, 폐하.\"\n\n\"그래, 그대가 보기에 사태가 심각한 것 같소?\"\n\n\"심히 심각하여, 폐하, 그 일이 가족 잔치 한복판에서, 그것도 제 약혼 바로 그날 저를 덮쳤을 때, 저는 신부와 벗들을 두고 만사를 미룬 채 달려왔습니다. 저를 사로잡은 두려움과 폐하를 향한 충정을 폐하의 발치에 바치고자 함이었습니다.\"\n\n\"옳지,\" 루이 18세가 말했다. \"그대와 드 생메랑 양 사이에 혼약이 있지 않았소?\"\n\n\"폐하의 더없이 충직한 신하 가운데 한 분의 따님입니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래. 허나 이 음모 이야기를 합시다, 드 빌포르 씨.\"\n\n\"폐하, 두렵건대 이는 음모를 넘어선 것입니다. 역모일까 두렵습니다.\"\n\n\"이런 시국에 역모란,\" 루이 18세가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"꾸미기는 매우 쉬우나 끝까지 밀고 나가기는 더 어려운 법이오. 우리가 조상의 옥좌에 오른 지 얼마 되지 않은 만큼, 과거와 현재, 미래를 한꺼번에 똑똑히 지켜보고 있기 때문이오. 지난 열 달 동안 내 대신들은 지중해 연안을 감시하느라 경계를 배로 강화해 왔소. 보나파르트가 나폴리에 상륙한다면, 그가 피옴비노에 닿기도 전에 연합군 전체가 들고일어날 게요. 토스카나에 상륙한다면 적지에 발을 들이는 셈이고, 프랑스에 상륙한다면 한 줌의 병력으로일 텐데, 그 결과는 뻔하지 않소. 백성이 그토록 그를 증오하니 말이오. 용기를 내시오. 다만 이와 더불어 우리 왕가의 감사도 믿어도 좋소.\"\n\n\"아, 마침 당드레 씨가 왔습니다!\" 드 블라카스가 외쳤다. 바로 그 순간 경찰청장이 문가에 나타났는데, 얼굴이 창백하고 떨고 있어 금방이라도 쓰러질 듯했다. 빌포르가 물러나려 하자, 드 블라카스 씨가 그의 손을 붙잡아 만류했다.\n\n# Chapter 11. The Corsican Ogre\n\n루이 18세는 그 동요하는 모습을 보자 앉아 있던 탁자를 거칠게 밀쳐냈다.\n\n\"무슨 일인가, 남작?\" 그가 소리쳤다. \"몹시 질겁한 얼굴이군. 그대의 불안이 블라카스 씨가 내게 말하고 빌포르 씨가 방금 확인해 준 그 일과 관계가 있는가?\" 블라카스 씨가 불쑥 남작 쪽으로 다가섰으나, 신하의 겁먹은 모습이 정치가의 관용을 청하고 있었다. 게다가 사정이 이러하니, 그로서는 경찰청장을 모욕하기보다 경찰청장이 자기보다 우위에 서게 두는 편이 훨씬 이로웠다.\n\n\"폐하…….\" 남작이 더듬거렸다.\n\n\"그래, 무슨 일이냐고?\" 루이 18세가 물었다. 경찰청장은 절망에 휩쓸린 나머지 루이 18세의 발치에 몸을 던지려 했고, 왕은 한 걸음 물러서며 이맛살을 찌푸렸다.\n\n\"말을 하겠는가?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"오, 폐하, 이 무슨 끔찍한 불행입니까! 정말이지 저는 가엾은 처지입니다. 저 자신을 결코 용서할 수 없을 것입니다!\"\n\n\"경,\" 루이 18세가 말했다. \"내 명하노니, 말하라.\"\n\n\"그러시다면 폐하, 그 찬탈자가 2월 26일에 엘바섬을 떠나 3월 1일에 상륙했습니다.\"\n\n\"어디에? 이탈리아에 말인가?\" 왕이 다급히 물었다.\n\n\"프랑스입니다, 폐하. 앙티브 부근, 쥐앙만의 작은 항구입니다.\"\n\n\"찬탈자가 프랑스에, 앙티브 부근 쥐앙만에, 파리에서 이백오십 리외나 떨어진 곳에 3월 1일에 상륙했는데, 그대는 오늘에야, 3월 3일에야 그 사실을 알았단 말인가! 이보게, 그대가 하는 말은 있을 수 없는 일이야. 잘못된 보고를 받았거나, 아니면 정신이 나간 게지.\"\n\n\"아아, 폐하, 너무도 분명한 사실입니다!\" 루이는 형언할 수 없는 분노와 경악의 몸짓을 보였고, 이내 그 갑작스러운 일격이 가슴과 얼굴을 한꺼번에 강타하기라도 한 듯 몸을 곧추세웠다.\n\n\"프랑스에!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"그 찬탈자가 프랑스에! 그렇다면 아무도 그자를 감시하지 않았던 게로군. 누가 알겠나? 어쩌면 그자와 한통속이었는지도.\"\n\n\"오, 폐하,\" 블라카스 공작이 외쳤다. \"당드레 씨는 반역으로 의심받을 사람이 아닙니다! 폐하, 저희 모두가 눈이 멀었던 것입니다. 경찰청장도 그 보편적인 맹목을 함께 나누었을 뿐, 그것이 전부입니다.\"\n\n\"하지만…….\" 빌포르가 입을 열었다가 문득 자신을 억누르며 침묵했다. 그러더니 다시 말을 이었다. \"용서하십시오, 폐하.\" 그가 고개를 숙이며 말했다. \"저의 충정이 그만 저를 앞서게 했습니다. 폐하께서 너그러이 용서해 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"말하라, 경, 거리낌 없이 말하라.\" 루이가 대답했다. \"이 환란을 미리 알린 사람은 그대뿐이니, 이제 그 해법으로 우리를 도와 보라.\"\n\n\"폐하,\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"그 찬탈자는 남부에서 미움을 받고 있습니다. 제 생각으로는, 그자가 무모하게 남부로 들어선다면 랑그도크와 프로방스를 들고일어나게 하기는 쉬울 것입니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 그건 확실하지.\" 청장이 대답했다. \"그러나 그자는 가프와 시스트롱을 거쳐 진군하고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"진군이라니, 진군한다고!\" 루이 18세가 말했다. \"그렇다면 그자가 파리로 진군한단 말인가?\" 경찰청장은 침묵을 지켰고, 그것은 완전한 시인이나 다름없었다.\n\n\"그럼 도피네는 어떤가, 경?\" 왕이 빌포르에게 물었다. \"프로방스처럼 그곳도 들고일어나게 할 수 있을 것 같은가?\"\n\n\"폐하, 폐하께 가혹한 사실을 아뢰어야 함을 송구하게 여깁니다. 그러나 도피네의 민심은 프로방스나 랑그도크와는 정반대입니다. 그 산악 지방 사람들은 보나파르트파입니다, 폐하.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 루이가 중얼거렸다. \"그자는 정보를 잘 갖추고 있었던 게로군. 그래, 그자가 거느린 병력은 얼마나 되는가?\"\n\n\"모르겠습니다, 폐하.\" 경찰청장이 대답했다.\n\n\"뭐라, 모른다고! 그 점에 대해 정보 얻기를 게을리한 것인가? 하기야 별로 중요한 일도 아니지.\" 그가 매서운 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"폐하, 알아낼 길이 없었습니다. 급보에는 그저 상륙 사실과 찬탈자가 택한 경로만 적혀 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 그 급보는 어떻게 그대에게 닿았는가?\" 왕이 물었다. 청장은 고개를 떨구었고, 뺨에 짙은 홍조가 번지는 가운데 더듬거리며 말했다.\n\n\"전신으로 받았습니다, 폐하.\" 루이 18세는 한 걸음 나아가, 나폴레옹이 그러했을 법한 자세로 가슴 위에 팔짱을 꼈다.\n\n\"그러니까,\" 그가 분노로 창백해지며 외쳤다. \"일곱 나라의 연합군이 힘을 합쳐 그자를 무너뜨렸다. 하늘의 기적이 이십오 년의 망명 끝에 나를 선조의 옥좌에 다시 앉혀 주었다. 그 이십오 년 동안, 나는 프랑스 백성과 내게 맡겨진 이익을 헤아리려 온갖 수고를 아끼지 않았다. 그런데 이제 내 소망의 결실이 거의 손에 잡히려는 순간, 내 손안에 쥔 권력이 터져 나를 산산조각으로 부수어 버리는구나!\"\n\n\"폐하, 이는 운명입니다!\" 청장이 중얼거렸다. 그는 상황의 무게가, 운명에게는 한낱 가벼운 것일지언정, 인간의 힘으로 견디기에는 너무 무겁다는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"우리의 적들이 우리를 두고 하는 말이 결국 옳았던 게로군. 우리는 아무것도 배우지 못했고, 아무것도 잊지 못했다! 만일 내가 그자처럼 배신을 당했다면 차라리 스스로를 위로하겠다. 그러나 내가 손수 영예로운 자리에 올려 준 자들, 제 몸보다 나를 더 충실히 살펴야 마땅한 자들 한가운데에서 이런 일을 당하다니. 나의 운명이 곧 그들의 운명이거늘. 내가 있기 전에 그들은 아무것도 아니었고, 내가 사라지면 다시 아무것도 아닐 것이며, 무능과 무력으로 비참하게 스러질 것이다! 오, 그래, 경, 그대 말이 옳다. 이는 운명이야!\"\n\n청장은 이 빈정거림의 폭발 앞에 몸을 움츠렸다. 블라카스 씨는 이마에 맺힌 땀을 닦았다. 빌포르는 자신의 비중이 커진 것을 느끼며 속으로 미소 지었다.\n\n\"몰락하다니,\" 첫눈에 왕정이 매달려 있는 심연을 가늠한 루이 왕이 말을 이었다. \"몰락하고, 게다가 그 몰락을 전신으로 알게 되다니! 오, 차라리 내 아우 루이 16세의 단두대에 오를지언정, 비웃음에 쫓겨 튀일리궁의 계단을 이렇게 내려가지는 않으리라. 비웃음이라니, 경, 그대는 프랑스에서 그것이 지닌 위력을 모르는 게야. 그러면서도 마땅히 알아야 하거늘!\"\n\n\"폐하, 폐하,\" 청장이 중얼거렸다. \"부디 자비를…….\"\n\n\"이리 오시오, 빌포르 씨.\" 왕이 그 젊은이에게 말을 건네며 다시 입을 열었다. 빌포르는 한 왕국의 운명이 걸린 그 대화에 꼼짝도 하지 않고 숨죽인 채 귀를 기울이고 있었다. \"이리 와서, 그가 알지 못한 모든 것을 미리 알아내는 일이 가능하다고 청장에게 일러 주시오.\"\n\n\"폐하, 그자가 온 세상으로부터 숨긴 비밀을 알아내기란 정말로 불가능한 일이었습니다.\"\n\n\"정말로 불가능이라! 그래, 그것 참 대단한 말이로군, 경. 불행히도 대단한 사람이 있듯 대단한 말도 있는 법, 나는 그것들을 재어 보았다. 청사와 요원과 첩자를 거느리고 비밀 공작 자금으로 백오십만 프랑을 쥔 청장이, 프랑스 해안에서 육십 리외 떨어진 곳에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 알아내기가 정말로 불가능하다니! 그렇다면 자, 보라. 여기 그런 수단을 하나도 갖추지 못한 한 신사가 있다. 일개 평범한 판사에 지나지 않는 신사가, 온갖 경찰을 거느린 그대보다 더 많이 알아냈고, 만일 그대처럼 전신을 부릴 권한이 있었다면 내 왕관을 지켜냈을 것이다.\" 경찰청장의 시선이 응축된 원한을 담아 빌포르에게 향했고, 빌포르는 겸손한 승리감에 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"블라카스, 그대를 두고 하는 말이 아니오.\" 루이 18세가 말을 이었다. \"그대는 아무것도 알아내지 못했을지언정, 적어도 자신의 의심을 끝까지 밀고 나간 분별은 있었으니까. 그대가 아닌 다른 누구였다면 빌포르 씨의 폭로를 하찮게 여기거나, 아니면 매수당한 야심에서 나온 말로 치부했을 것이오.\" 이 말은 한 시간 전에 경찰청장이 그토록 자신만만하게 늘어놓았던 견해를 빗댄 것이었다.\n\n빌포르는 왕의 의도를 알아차렸다. 다른 사람이었다면 이처럼 사람을 도취시키는 찬사의 술잔에 그만 정신을 잃었을지도 모른다. 그러나 그는 당드레가 돌이킬 수 없이 끝장났음을 보면서도, 경찰청장을 자신의 철천지원수로 만드는 일이 두려웠다. 사실 그 청장은, 권세가 절정에 있을 때에도 나폴레옹의 비밀을 캐내지 못한 자였으니, 제 몰락에 절망한 나머지 당테스를 심문하여 빌포르가 꾸민 음모의 동기를 낱낱이 드러낼 수도 있었다. 이를 깨달은 빌포르는 의기소침해진 청장을 짓밟는 데 가세하는 대신 그를 구해 주기로 했다.\n\n\"폐하,\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"이 사건의 갑작스러움이야말로, 그 결말이 섭리의 손에 달려 있음을 폐하께 입증하는 것입니다. 폐하께서 황송하게도 깊은 통찰력으로 돌려 주신 것은 그저 우연에서 비롯된 일이며, 저는 충성스럽고 헌신적인 종복으로서 그 우연을 활용했을 뿐, 그것이 전부입니다. 폐하, 분에 넘치는 공을 제게 돌리지 마십시오. 그래야 폐하께서 처음에 황송하게도 저에 대해 품으신 견해를 거두실 일이 없을 것입니다.\" 경찰청장은 의미심장한 눈길로 그 젊은이에게 고마움을 표했고, 빌포르는 자신의 계획이 성공했음을 알아차렸다. 다시 말해, 왕의 환심을 잃지 않으면서도, 필요할 때면 의지할 수 있는 한 사람을 벗으로 삼은 것이다.\n\n\"좋다.\" 왕이 다시 말했다. \"자, 경들,\" 그가 블라카스 씨와 경찰청장 쪽으로 돌아서며 말을 이었다. \"이제 그대들에게 더 볼일이 없으니 물러가도 좋소. 이제 남은 일은 육군대신의 소관이오.\"\n\n\"다행히도, 폐하,\" 블라카스 씨가 말했다. \"저희는 군대를 믿을 수 있습니다. 모든 보고가 군대의 충성과 결속을 확인해 준다는 것을 폐하께서도 아십니다.\"\n\n\"보고 이야기는 내게 꺼내지 마시오, 공작. 이제 그것을 어디까지 믿어야 할지 잘 알았으니. 그래도 보고 이야기가 나온 김에, 남작, 생자크 거리 사건에 관해서는 무엇을 알아냈는가?\"\n\n\"생자크 거리 사건이라니요!\" 빌포르가 외쳤다. 그는 탄성을 억누르지 못했다. 그러더니 문득 말을 멈추고 덧붙였다. \"용서하십시오, 폐하. 폐하를 향한 저의 충정이 그만 저를 잊게 만들었습니다. 제가 품은 존경심을 잊은 것이 아닙니다. 그것은 제 가슴에 너무도 깊이 새겨져 있으니까요. 다만 예법을 잊었던 것입니다.\"\n\n\"계속하시오, 계속하시오, 경.\" 왕이 대답했다. \"그대는 오늘 이곳에서 물을 자격을 얻었으니까.\"\n\n\"폐하,\" 경찰청장이 끼어들었다. \"조금 전 이 건에 관해 새로 입수한 정보를 폐하께 아뢰러 들어왔습니다만, 그만 만에서 벌어진 그 끔찍한 사건에 폐하의 관심이 쏠리고 말았습니다. 이제 이 일들은 폐하의 흥미를 끌지 못할 듯합니다.\"\n\n\"천만에, 경, 천만에.\" 루이 18세가 말했다. \"이 사건은 내게는 지금 우리의 주의를 사로잡고 있는 일과 분명한 연관이 있어 보이오. 케넬 장군의 죽음이 어쩌면 우리를 중대한 내부 음모의 직접적인 단서로 이끌어 줄지도 모르지.\" 케넬 장군이라는 이름에 빌포르는 몸을 떨었다.\n\n\"모든 정황이 한 결론을 가리키고 있습니다, 폐하.\" 경찰청장이 말했다. \"그 죽음은 처음에 저희가 믿었던 것처럼 자살이 아니라 암살이었습니다. 케넬 장군은 자취를 감추기 직전에 어느 보나파르트파 모임을 막 나섰던 것으로 보입니다. 그날 아침 정체 모를 인물이 그를 찾아와 생자크 거리에서 만나기로 약속을 잡았습니다. 안타깝게도, 그 낯선 자가 들어왔을 때 마침 장군의 머리를 매만지던 시종이 거리 이름은 들었으나 번지는 듣지 못했습니다.\" 경찰청장이 이 이야기를 왕에게 들려주는 동안, 자기 목숨이 그 입술에 매달리기라도 한 듯한 표정의 빌포르는 붉어졌다 창백해지기를 번갈아 했다. 왕이 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"빌포르 씨, 그대도 나와 같은 생각이 아니오? 사람들은 케넬 장군이 찬탈자에게 속한 줄 알았으나 실은 온전히 내게 충성하던 사람이었는데, 그가 보나파르트파의 매복에 희생되어 죽은 것이라고 말이오?\"\n\n\"그럴 법한 일입니다, 폐하.\" 빌포르가 대답했다. \"그런데 알려진 것이 이게 전부입니까?\"\n\n\"그와 만나기로 약속을 잡은 자의 자취를 쫓고 있소.\"\n\n\"그자의 자취를요?\" 빌포르가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소. 시종이 인상착의를 일러 주었지. 쉰에서 쉰둘쯤 된 사내로, 거무스름한 피부에 검은 눈을 짙은 눈썹이 덮고 있고, 콧수염이 텁수룩하다고 하오. 턱까지 단추를 채운 푸른 프록코트를 입었고, 단춧구멍에는 레지옹 도뇌르 장교 약장을 달고 있었다 하오. 어제 이 인상착의에 꼭 들어맞는 자를 미행했으나, 쥐시엔 거리와 코크에롱 거리 모퉁이에서 놓치고 말았소.\" 경찰청장이 말을 이어 가는 동안 빌포르는 안락의자 등받이에 몸을 기댔다. 다리가 후들거리는 것이 느껴졌기 때문이다. 그러나 그 낯선 자가 미행하던 요원의 감시를 따돌렸다는 말을 듣자 다시 숨을 돌렸다.\n\n\"그 사내를 계속 찾으시오, 경.\" 왕이 경찰청장에게 말했다. \"이 순간 우리에게 그토록 요긴했을 케넬 장군이, 내가 거의 확신하는 대로 살해당한 것이라면, 그 암살자들은 보나파르트파든 아니든 가차 없이 벌하겠소.\" 이 왕의 선언이 불러일으킨 공포를 드러내지 않으려면 빌포르의 온갖 침착함이 다 필요했다.\n\n\"참으로 이상하지,\" 왕이 다소 신랄하게 말을 이었다. \"경찰이란 '살인이 저질러졌습니다'라고 말하기만 하면 모든 일을 처리한 것으로 여기고, 더욱이 '그리고 저희는 범인의 자취를 쫓고 있습니다'라고 덧붙일 수 있을 때면 그러하단 말이오.\"\n\n\"폐하, 적어도 이 점에 관해서는 폐하께서 충분히 만족하시리라 믿습니다.\"\n\n\"두고 봅시다. 더는 그대를 붙잡지 않겠소, 빌포르 씨. 그토록 먼 길을 왔으니 지쳤을 것이오. 가서 쉬시오. 물론 부친 댁에 들렀겠지?\" 빌포르는 아찔한 기운에 휩싸였다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 폐하.\" 그가 대답했다. \"투르농 거리의 마드리드 호텔에 묵었습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 부친은 만났겠지?\"\n\n\"폐하, 저는 곧장 블라카스 공작께로 갔습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 만나기는 할 테지?\"\n\n\"그러지 않을 듯합니다, 폐하.\"\n\n\"아, 내가 깜빡했군.\" 루이가 미소를 지으며 말했다. 그 미소는 이 모든 물음이 까닭 없이 던져진 것이 아님을 드러내고 있었다. \"그대와 누아르티에 씨가 그다지 좋은 사이가 아니라는 걸 잊고 있었소. 그 또한 왕가의 대의를 위해 그대가 치른 또 하나의 희생이니, 마땅히 보답받아야 할 일이지.\"\n\n\"폐하, 폐하께서 황송하게도 제게 베풀어 주시는 자애야말로 저의 가장 큰 야심마저 훌쩍 뛰어넘는 보답이오니, 더 청할 것이 없습니다.\"\n\n\"걱정 마시오, 경, 그대를 잊지 않을 것이오. 마음을 놓으시오. 그러는 동안에\"(이때 왕은 평소 푸른 코트 위, 생루이 십자훈장 곁, 노트르담뒤몽카르멜과 생라자르 훈장 위에 달고 다니던 레지옹 도뇌르 십자훈장을 떼어 빌포르에게 건넸다) \"그러는 동안 이 십자훈장을 받아 두시오.\"\n\n\"폐하,\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"폐하께서 잘못 아셨습니다. 이것은 장교용 십자훈장입니다.\"\n\n\"맙소사!\" 루이 18세가 말했다. \"있는 그대로 받아 두시오. 다른 것을 마련해 줄 시간이 없으니. 블라카스, 임명장을 작성해 빌포르 씨에게 보내는 일을 그대가 맡아 주시오.\" 빌포르의 두 눈에 기쁨과 긍지의 눈물이 그렁그렁 맺혔다. 그는 십자훈장을 받아 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"그럼 이제,\" 그가 말했다. \"폐하께서 황송하게도 제게 내리시는 분부가 무엇인지 여쭈어도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"필요한 만큼 쉬도록 하시오. 그리고 여기 파리에서 나를 섬길 수 없거든, 마르세유에서 내게 더없이 큰 도움이 되어 줄 수 있다는 것을 잊지 마시오.\"\n\n\"폐하,\" 빌포르가 고개를 숙이며 대답했다. \"한 시간 안에 파리를 떠나겠습니다.\"\n\n\"가시오, 경.\" 왕이 말했다. \"그리고 혹 내가 그대를 잊거든(왕의 기억이란 짧은 법이오) 주저 말고 그대 자신을 내 기억에 떠올리게 하시오. 남작, 육군대신을 불러오라. 블라카스, 그대는 남으시오.\"\n\n\"아, 경,\" 경찰청장이 튀일리궁을 나서며 빌포르에게 말했다. \"그대는 행운의 문으로 들어섰소. 그대의 출세는 따 놓은 당상이오.\"\n\n\"머지않아 그리되겠지요?\" 빌포르가 중얼거렸다. 그는 이미 경력이 끝장난 청장에게 인사를 건네고는 삯마차를 찾아 주위를 둘러보았다. 마침 한 대가 지나가자 그것을 불러 세웠다. 마부에게 주소를 일러 주고는 안으로 뛰어들어 좌석에 몸을 던졌고, 야심에 찬 몽상에 한껏 빠져들었다.\n\n십 분쯤 뒤 빌포르는 호텔에 다다라, 두 시간 안에 말을 준비해 두라 이르고 아침 식사를 가져오게 했다. 막 식사를 시작하려는데 초인종 소리가 날카롭고 크게 울렸다. 시종이 문을 열었고, 빌포르는 누군가 자기 이름을 말하는 것을 들었다.\n\n\"내가 벌써 여기 와 있다는 걸 누가 안단 말인가?\" 젊은이가 말했다. 시종이 들어왔다.\n\n\"그래,\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"무슨 일인가? 누가 종을 울렸지? 누가 나를 찾던가?\"\n\n\"이름을 밝히지 않으려는 낯선 분입니다.\"\n\n\"이름을 밝히지 않으려는 낯선 자라니! 내게 무슨 볼일이라던가?\"\n\n\"나리와 이야기를 나누고 싶다 하십니다.\"\n\n\"나와?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"내 이름을 대던가?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"어떤 사람인가?\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 나리, 쉰쯤 된 분입니다.\"\n\n\"키는 작던가, 크던가?\"\n\n\"나리와 비슷한 키입니다.\"\n\n\"거무스름하던가, 희던가?\"\n\n\"거무스름합니다. 아주 거무스름하고요. 검은 눈에, 검은 머리, 검은 눈썹입니다.\"\n\n\"옷차림은 어떻던가?\" 빌포르가 다급히 물었다.\n\n\"단추를 단단히 채운 푸른 프록코트에 레지옹 도뇌르 훈장을 달고 계십니다.\"\n\n\"그자로구나!\" 빌포르가 창백해지며 말했다.\n\n\"이런, 빌어먹을!\" 우리가 두 번이나 인상착의를 일러 준 그 인물이 문으로 들어서며 말했다. \"예의도 한참 차리는구나! 마르세유에서는 아들이 제 아비를 대기실에 기다리게 두는 게 풍습이더냐?\"\n\n\"아버지!\" 빌포르가 외쳤다. \"역시 제가 잘못 짚은 게 아니었군요. 아버지일 거라고 확신하고 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"음, 그렇게나 확신했다면,\" 새로 들어선 사람이 지팡이를 한구석에 세워 두고 모자를 의자에 내려놓으며 대답했다. \"이거 한마디 해야겠구나, 내 사랑하는 제라르. 이 아비를 문간에 기다리게 한 건 그리 효성스러운 짓이 아니었어.\"\n\n\"우리만 있게 해 주게, 제르맹.\" 빌포르가 말했다. 하인은 역력한 놀라움의 기색을 띤 채 방을 나갔다.\n\n# Chapter 12. Father and Son\n\n누아르티에 씨는, 방으로 들어온 사람이 정말로 그였는데, 문이 닫힐 때까지 하인의 뒤를 지켜보았다. 그러고는 누군가 대기실에서 엿들을지도 모른다는 걱정에서였는지, 다시 문을 열었다. 그 조심성은 헛되지 않았다. 제르맹이 황급히 물러나는 모습이 드러났기 때문이다. 그것은 우리 최초의 조상을 파멸시킨 죄에서 그 역시 자유롭지 못함을 증명하는 일이었다. 누아르티에 씨는 그제야 수고스럽게 대기실 문을 닫고 빗장을 지른 뒤, 침실 문도 마저 잠그고서, 자신의 모든 동작을 감추지 못한 놀라움으로 좇던 빌포르에게 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"자, 이보게, 사랑하는 제라르.\" 그가 의미심장한 눈길로 젊은이에게 말했다. \"내가 보기엔, 자네가 나를 만난 걸 그리 반기지 않는 것 같군그래.\"\n\n\"사랑하는 아버지.\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"오히려 그 반대로, 무척 기쁩니다. 다만 아버지께서 찾아오시리라고는 전혀 예상치 못했던 터라, 다소 정신이 없을 뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 이 사람아.\" 누아르티에 씨가 자리에 앉으며 대답했다. \"나야말로 자네에게 같은 말을 할 수 있겠네. 2월 28일에 결혼한다고 알려 놓고서, 3월 3일에 여기 파리에 불쑥 나타났으니 말일세.\"\n\n\"제가 오긴 했습니다만, 사랑하는 아버지.\" 제라르가 누아르티에 씨에게 더 가까이 다가서며 말했다. \"탓하지는 마십시오. 아버지를 위해서 온 것이고, 제 여정이 아버지를 구할 테니까요.\"\n\n\"오, 그래?\" 누아르티에 씨가 의자에 느긋하게 몸을 뻗으며 말했다. \"정말이지, 어디 다 들려주게나. 흥미로운 이야기일 게 틀림없으니.\"\n\n\"아버지, 생자크 거리에 있는 어떤 보나파르트파 클럽 이야기를 들어 보셨습니까?\"\n\n\"53번지 말이지. 그래, 내가 거기 부회장일세.\"\n\n\"아버지, 그 태연하심에 소름이 끼칩니다.\"\n\n\"이보게, 산악파에게 추방당하고, 건초 수레에 숨어 파리를 빠져나오고, 로베스피에르의 사냥개들에게 보르도 평원을 가로질러 쫓겨 본 사람이라면, 웬만한 일에는 다 익숙해지는 법이라네. 어쨌든 계속해 보게. 생자크 거리의 클럽이 어쨌다는 건가?\"\n\n\"그자들이 케넬 장군을 그곳으로 꾀어냈는데, 저녁 아홉 시에 집을 나선 케넬 장군이 이튿날 센강에서 발견되었습니다.\"\n\n\"한데 누가 자네에게 그 그럴듯한 이야기를 들려줬나?\"\n\n\"국왕 폐하께서 직접 하셨습니다.\" \"좋아, 그렇다면 자네 이야기에 답하여.\" 누아르티에가 말을 이었다. \"내가 다른 이야기를 하나 들려주지.\"\n\n\"사랑하는 아버지, 제가 보기엔 아버지께서 하시려는 말씀을 이미 알 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"아, 황제께서 상륙하셨다는 소식을 들었나?\"\n\n\"좀 낮춰 말씀해 주십시오, 아버지, 간청드립니다. 아버지를 위해서나 저를 위해서나요. 네, 그 소식은 들었습니다. 아버지보다 먼저 알았지요. 사흘 전에 마르세유에서 파리까지 갖은 속도를 다해 달려왔으니까요. 어쩔 수 없이 지체될 때마다 반쯤 미칠 지경이었습니다.\"\n\n\"사흘 전이라고? 제정신이 아니로군. 사흘 전이면 황제께서 아직 상륙하시기도 전이었네.\"\n\n\"그래도 상관없습니다. 저는 황제의 의도를 알고 있었으니까요.\"\n\n\"그건 어떻게 알았나?\"\n\n\"엘바섬에서 아버지께 보낸 편지 한 통을 통해서요.\"\n\n\"내게?\"\n\n\"아버지께요. 그 편지를 전령의 수첩에서 발견했습니다. 만일 그 편지가 다른 사람의 손에 들어갔다면, 사랑하는 아버지, 아버지는 지금쯤 십중팔구 총살당하셨을 겁니다.\" 빌포르의 아버지가 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"이런, 이런.\" 그가 말했다. \"왕정복고 정권이 그리도 잽싸게 제정의 수법을 따라 하겠나? 총살이라니, 이 사람아? 무슨 그런 생각을! 자네가 말하는 그 편지는 어디 있나? 자네가 그런 걸 그냥 지나칠 사람이 아니라는 건 내가 잘 알지.\"\n\n\"태워 버렸습니다. 한 조각이라도 남을까 두려워서요. 그 편지는 아버지를 단죄하는 빌미가 되었을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그리고 자네 앞날의 전망도 무너뜨렸겠지.\" 누아르티에가 대답했다. \"그래, 충분히 이해하네. 하지만 나를 지켜 줄 자네가 있는 한, 나는 두려울 게 없어.\"\n\n\"저는 그보다 더한 일을 하고 있습니다. 아버지를 구하는 거지요.\"\n\n\"자네가? 이거 참, 점점 더 극적으로 흘러가는군. 어디 설명해 보게.\"\n\n\"다시 생자크 거리의 클럽 이야기로 돌아가야겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그 클럽이 경찰에게는 꽤나 골칫거리인 모양이군. 어째서 더 철저히 뒤지지 않았을까? 그랬다면 찾아냈을 텐데……\"\n\n\"찾아내지는 못했지만, 단서를 쫓고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 늘 그 소리지. 나도 익히 아는 말이야. 경찰이 헛다리를 짚으면, 단서를 쫓고 있다고 떠벌리지. 그러면 정부는 경찰이 머쓱한 낯으로 와서 단서를 놓쳤다고 말하는 날을 끈기 있게 기다리는 거고.\"\n\n\"그렇지만 시신이 발견됐습니다. 장군이 살해된 거고, 어느 나라에서나 그런 건 살인이라고 부르지요.\"\n\n\"살인이라고? 이런, 장군이 살해됐다는 증거는 아무것도 없네. 센강에서는 날마다 사람이 발견돼. 스스로 몸을 던졌거나, 헤엄칠 줄 몰라 익사했거나 해서 말이야.\"\n\n\"아버지, 장군이 절망에 빠져 강에 몸을 던질 사람이 아니라는 걸, 그리고 1월에는 누구도 센강에서 멱을 감지 않는다는 걸 아버지께서도 잘 아시지 않습니까. 아니에요, 아니에요, 속지 마십시오. 이건 어느 모로 보나 살인이었습니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 그걸 그렇게 규정한 게 누군가?\"\n\n\"국왕 폐하께서 직접 하셨습니다.\"\n\n\"폐하가! 정치에는 살인이란 없다고 인정할 만큼은 사려 깊은 분인 줄 알았는데. 이 사람아, 정치에는 자네도 나만큼이나 잘 알다시피, 사람이 아니라 사상이 있을 뿐이고, 감정이 아니라 이해관계가 있을 뿐이라네. 정치에서 우리는 사람을 죽이는 게 아니라, 그저 장애물을 치울 뿐이지, 그게 전부야. 일이 어떻게 진행됐는지 알고 싶나? 좋아, 내가 들려주지. 케넬 장군은 믿을 만하다고들 여겼네. 엘바섬에서 우리에게 천거된 사람이었거든. 우리 중 한 사람이 그를 찾아가, 친구들을 만날 수 있을 거라며 생자크 거리로 초대했지. 그가 그리로 왔고, 엘바섬을 떠나는 계획이며 예정된 상륙이며 하는 것들을 그에게 펼쳐 보였네. 그가 그 모든 것을 남김없이 듣고 이해한 뒤, 자기는 왕당파라고 대답하더군. 그러자 모두가 서로 얼굴을 마주 보았네. 그에게 맹세를 시켰고, 그도 맹세를 했네만, 어찌나 마지못해 하던지 그렇게 맹세하는 건 정말이지 신의 뜻을 시험하는 격이었어. 그런데도 장군은 자유롭게 떠나도록 허락받았네. 완전히 자유롭게 말이야. 그런데도 그는 집으로 돌아가지 않았어. 그게 무슨 뜻이겠나? 이 사람아, 우리한테서 나가다가 길을 잃은 것뿐이야, 그게 전부라고. 살인이라니? 정말이지, 빌포르, 자네에겐 놀랐네. 자네 같은 검사 대리가 그런 빈약한 전제 위에 고발을 세우다니! 자네가 왕당파 노릇을 하면서 우리 편 한 사람의 목을 쳤을 때, 내가 자네한테 '아들아, 너는 살인을 저질렀다'고 말하던가? 아닐세, 나는 이렇게 말했지. '잘했네, 자네가 이겼어. 어쩌면 내일은 우리 차례일지도 모르지.'\"\n\n\"하지만 아버지, 조심하십시오. 우리 차례가 오면, 우리의 복수는 가차 없을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 말인지 모르겠군.\"\n\n\"찬탈자가 돌아오리라 믿고 계시는군요?\"\n\n\"그렇다네.\"\n\n\"틀리셨습니다. 그자는 프랑스 내륙으로 2리외도 못 들어가서 들짐승처럼 쫓기고 추적당하고 붙잡히고 말 겁니다.\"\n\n\"이 사람아, 황제께서는 지금 이 순간 그르노블로 가시는 중일세. 10일이나 12일이면 리옹에, 20일이나 25일이면 파리에 닿으실 거야.\"\n\n\"백성들이 들고일어날 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 그분을 맞으러 일어나겠지.\"\n\n\"황제 곁에는 한 줌의 병력밖에 없고, 그를 치러 군대가 파견될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 그분을 수도까지 호위하러 파견되겠지. 정말이지, 사랑하는 제라르, 자네는 아직 어린애일세. 전신이 상륙 사흘 뒤에야 '찬탈자가 몇 명을 거느리고 칸에 상륙했다. 추격 중이다'라고 전해 주었다고 해서, 자네는 자신이 정통한 줄 알고 있어. 한데 그분은 지금 어디 계신가? 무얼 하고 계신가? 자네는 전혀 모르지. 이런 식이라면 그분을 파리까지 쫓는 동안 방아쇠 한 번 당기지 못할 걸세.\"\n\n\"그르노블과 리옹은 충성스러운 도시라, 그자에게 넘을 수 없는 장벽이 될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그르노블은 환호하며 성문을 열어 줄 것이고, 리옹은 온통 그분을 맞으러 달려 나갈 걸세. 내 말을 믿게, 우리도 자네만큼 정통하고, 우리 정보망도 자네네 것 못지않아. 그 증거를 보고 싶나? 자, 자네는 내게 여정을 숨기려 했지만, 나는 자네가 관문을 지난 지 반 시간 만에 도착 사실을 알았네. 자네는 마부 말고는 아무에게도 행선지를 일러 주지 않았는데, 나는 자네 주소를 알고 있었고, 그 증거로 자네가 막 식탁에 앉으려는 바로 그 순간에 이렇게 와 있지 않은가. 그러니 종을 울려서, 나이프와 포크와 접시를 하나씩 더 가져오라 하게. 함께 저녁을 들지.\"\n\n\"그렇군요!\" 빌포르가 놀라움에 차 아버지를 바라보며 대답했다. \"정말이지 무척 정통하신 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"엉? 간단한 일이야. 권력을 쥔 자네들에게는 돈이 만들어 내는 수단밖에 없지만, 때를 기다리는 우리에게는 헌신이 불러일으키는 수단이 있거든.\"\n\n\"헌신이라니요!\" 빌포르가 비웃듯 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 헌신. 내가 알기로는 그게 희망에 찬 야심을 일컫는 말이거든.\"\n\n그러고는 빌포르의 아버지가 아들이 부르지도 않은 하인을 부르려고 종 줄에 손을 뻗었다. 빌포르가 그 팔을 붙잡았다.\n\n\"잠깐만요, 사랑하는 아버지.\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"한마디만 더 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n\"말해 보게.\"\n\n\"왕당파 경찰이 아무리 멍청하다 해도, 한 가지 끔찍한 사실만은 알고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그게 뭔가?\"\n\n\"케넬 장군이 사라지던 날 아침, 그의 집을 찾아간 남자의 인상착의입니다.\"\n\n\"오, 그 대단하신 경찰이 그걸 알아냈단 말이지? 그래, 그 인상착의가 어떻다던가?\"\n\n\"가무잡잡한 얼굴빛, 검은 머리에 검은 눈썹과 구레나룻, 턱까지 단추를 채운 푸른 프록코트, 단춧구멍에 단 레지옹 도뇌르 장교 표장, 챙 넓은 모자에 지팡이.\"\n\n\"하, 그렇단 말이지?\" 누아르티에가 말했다. \"그렇다면 어째서 아직 그자를 붙잡지 못했단 말인가?\"\n\n\"어제인가 그제, 코케롱 거리 모퉁이에서 그를 놓쳤기 때문이지요.\"\n\n\"자네네 경찰은 아무짝에도 쓸모없다고 내가 말하지 않았나?\"\n\n\"네, 하지만 아직 그를 붙잡을 수도 있지요.\"\n\n\"그렇겠지.\" 누아르티에가 무심하게 주위를 둘러보며 말했다. \"그렇겠지, 만일 그 사람이 지금처럼 경계하고 있지 않다면 말이야.\" 그러고는 미소 지으며 덧붙였다. \"그러니 그 사람은 외모를 좀 바꾸겠지.\" 이 말과 함께 그는 자리에서 일어나 프록코트와 크라바트를 벗고, 아들의 세면도구가 놓인 탁자로 다가가 얼굴에 비누 거품을 칠하고 면도칼을 집어 들더니, 단호한 손놀림으로 의심을 살 만한 구레나룻을 잘라 냈다. 빌포르는 경탄이 섞이지 않았다고는 할 수 없는 불안한 마음으로 그를 지켜보았다.\n\n구레나룻을 잘라 낸 누아르티에는 머리 모양도 한 번 매만지고, 검은 크라바트 대신 열린 여행 가방 맨 위에 놓인 색깔 있는 목수건을 둘렀다. 그리고 푸른색에 단추를 높이 채운 프록코트 대신, 빌포르의 짙은 갈색 코트, 앞이 비스듬히 재단된 것을 걸치고, 아들의 챙 좁은 모자를 거울 앞에서 써 보았는데 그에게 꼭 맞는 듯했다. 그리고 좀 전에 세워 둔 모퉁이에 지팡이를 남겨 둔 채, 가느다란 등나무 단장을 집어 들어 허공을 한두 번 가르고는, 자신의 주된 특징 가운데 하나인 그 느긋하고 거들먹거리는 걸음걸이로 방 안을 거닐었다.\n\n\"자, 어떤가.\" 변장을 마친 그가 어리둥절해하는 아들 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다. \"자, 이제 자네네 경찰이 나를 알아볼 것 같나?\"\n\n\"못 할 겁니다, 아버지.\" 빌포르가 더듬거리며 말했다. \"적어도, 그러기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 이제, 이보게.\" 누아르티에가 말을 이었다. \"내가 자네에게 맡기고 가는 물건들은 자네가 알아서 신중하게 치워 주리라 믿네.\"\n\n\"오, 제게 맡기십시오.\" 빌포르가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 그래. 이제 나도 자네 말이 옳았다고, 자네가 정말로 내 목숨을 구했다고 인정하네. 이 은혜는 훗날 꼭 갚으마, 안심하게.\"\n\n빌포르가 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"아직도 확신이 안 서나?\"\n\n\"적어도 아버지께서 틀리시기를 바랄 뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"국왕을 다시 알현하게 되나?\"\n\n\"아마도요.\"\n\n\"그분 눈에 예언자로 보이고 싶은가?\"\n\n\"불행을 예언하는 자는 궁정에서 환영받지 못합니다, 아버지.\"\n\n\"그렇지, 하지만 언젠가는 그들에게 마땅한 평가를 내려 주는 법이지. 그리고 만일 두 번째 왕정복고가 온다면, 자네는 그때 위인으로 통하게 될 걸세.\"\n\n\"그럼, 국왕께 뭐라고 아뢰어야 합니까?\"\n\n\"이렇게 아뢰게. '폐하, 폐하께서는 프랑스의 민심에 대해서도, 도시들의 여론에 대해서도, 군대의 정서에 대해서도 잘못 알고 계십니다. 파리에서 폐하께서 코르시카의 식인귀라 부르고 느베르에서는 찬탈자라 일컫는 그자가, 리옹에서는 벌써 보나파르트로, 그르노블에서는 황제로 경례를 받고 있습니다. 폐하께서는 그가 추적당하고 쫓기고 사로잡혔다 여기시지만, 그는 제 독수리 깃발만큼이나 빠르게 진군하고 있습니다. 폐하께서 굶주림에 죽어 가고 피로에 지쳐 탈영할 채비가 된 줄로만 아시는 병사들은, 굴러가며 점점 커지는 눈덩이에 들러붙는 눈송이처럼 그에게로 모여들고 있습니다. 폐하, 떠나십시오, 프랑스를 그 진정한 주인에게, 돈으로 사들인 것이 아니라 정복의 권리로 얻은 자에게 넘기십시오. 떠나십시오, 폐하, 무슨 위험을 무릅쓰셔서가 아닙니다. 폐하의 적수는 폐하께 자비를 베풀 만큼 강대하니까요. 다만 성왕 루이의 후손이 아르콜라와 마렝고와 아우스터리츠의 사나이에게 목숨을 빚지는 것은 굴욕스러운 일이기 때문입니다.' 그분께 이렇게 아뢰게, 제라르. 아니, 차라리 아무 말도 하지 말게. 자네 여정은 비밀에 부치게. 파리에 무엇을 하러 왔는지, 무엇을 했는지 떠벌리지 말게. 갖은 속도를 다해 돌아가게. 밤에 마르세유로 들어가, 뒷문으로 집에 들고, 거기 그대로 조용히, 순순히, 은밀히, 그리고 무엇보다 누구의 눈에도 거슬리지 않게 머무르게. 이번에는, 내 자네에게 맹세하는데, 우리는 제 적을 아는 강한 자들답게 움직일 테니까. 가게, 아들아, 가게, 사랑하는 제라르. 아비의 명령에, 아니 자네가 그렇게 부르고 싶다면 정다운 충고에 따른다면, 우리가 자네를 그 자리에 지켜 주겠네. 이 또한,\" 누아르티에가 미소 지으며 덧붙였다. \"언젠가 정치의 저울추가 다시 한번 기울어 자네를 높이 띄우고 나를 내동댕이친다면, 자네가 두 번째로 나를 구할 수 있는 한 가지 방편이 되겠지. 잘 있게, 사랑하는 제라르. 다음번 여정에는 내 집 문 앞에 내리게.\"\n\n말을 마친 누아르티에는, 이 놀랍고도 마음 졸이는 대화 내내 그를 한결같이 감돌던 그 침착함 그대로 방을 나섰다. 창백하게 질려 동요하던 빌포르는 창가로 달려가 커튼을 젖히고, 그가 거리 모퉁이에 서 있던 두세 명의 험상궂은 사내들 곁을 태연하고 침착하게 지나치는 모습을 지켜보았다. 그자들은 어쩌면 검은 구레나룻에 푸른 프록코트, 챙 넓은 모자를 쓴 남자를 체포하려고 거기 있었을지도 몰랐다.\n\n빌포르는 숨죽인 채 서서, 아버지가 뷔시 거리에서 사라질 때까지 지켜보았다. 그러고는 아버지가 남기고 간 갖가지 물건으로 돌아서서, 검은 크라바트와 푸른 프록코트를 여행 가방 맨 밑에 넣고, 모자는 어두운 벽장에 던져 넣고, 지팡이는 자잘하게 부러뜨려 불 속에 던졌다. 그리고 여행용 모자를 쓰고, 시종을 불러 막 쏟아 내려던 수많은 질문을 눈짓 하나로 막은 뒤, 셈을 치르고는 채비된 마차에 뛰어올랐다. 리옹에서 보나파르트가 그르노블에 입성했다는 소식을 듣고, 길마다 들끓는 소란 한가운데를 지나, 마침내 마르세유에 다다랐다. 야심과 그 첫 성공이 사람의 마음속에 불러일으키는 온갖 희망과 두려움에 사로잡힌 채로.\n\n# 제13장 백일천하\n\n누아르티에 씨는 진정한 예언자였고, 그의 말대로 사태는 빠르게 진행되었다. 그 유명한 엘바섬 탈출의 역사를 모르는 사람은 없다. 과거에 전례가 없던 사건이었고, 앞으로도 견줄 만한 일은 아마 없을 것이다.\n\n루이 18세는 이 뜻밖의 일격을 막아 보려 했으나 그 시도는 미약하기 짝이 없었다. 그가 간신히 재건한 왕정은 불안한 토대 위에서 휘청거렸고, 황제가 손짓 한 번 하자 낡은 편견과 새로운 사상이 뒤섞인 그 어색한 구조물은 와르르 무너져 내렸다. 그리하여 빌포르가 얻은 것이라고는 왕의 감사(지금 같은 시국에는 오히려 해가 될 만한 것이었다)와 레지옹 도뇌르 십자훈장뿐이었다. 블라카스 씨가 정식으로 임명장을 보내 주었으나, 빌포르는 신중하게도 그 훈장을 달지 않았다.\n\n나폴레옹은 분명 빌포르의 자리를 빼앗았을 것이다. 궁정에서 절대적인 영향력을 지닌 누아르티에가 없었더라면 말이다. 그리하여 93년의 지롱드파이자 1806년의 원로원 의원이었던 그가, 얼마 전 자신을 보호해 주었던 아들을 이제는 거꾸로 보호하게 되었다. 빌포르가 지닌 모든 영향력을 끌어모아 봐야, 당테스가 하마터면 누설할 뻔했던 비밀을 겨우 덮을 수 있을 정도였다. 자리를 잃은 것은 검찰총장뿐이었는데, 그는 왕당파라는 의심을 받았다.\n\n그러나 제국의 권력이 채 자리 잡기도 전에 — 다시 말해 황제가 튀일리궁에 다시 들어와, 우리가 독자들을 안내했던 그 밀실에서 명령을 내리기 시작하고, 그 방 탁자 위에서 루이 18세가 반쯤 채워 둔 코담뱃갑을 발견하기가 무섭게 — 마르세유는 당국의 만류에도 아랑곳없이 다시 내란의 불길을 지피기 시작했다. 남부에서는 늘 그 불씨가 잿더미 속에 도사리고 있었던 것이다. 왕당파가 밖으로 나서기만 하면 사람들은 고함과 욕설을 퍼부었는데, 그보다 훨씬 더 거센 폭력으로 민중을 들끓게 하는 데에는 그리 많은 것이 필요치 않았다.\n\n이러한 변화 덕분에 그 선량한 선주는 그 순간 — 전능했다고까지는 말하지 않겠다. 모렐은 신중하고 다소 소심한 사람이어서, 보나파르트의 가장 열성적인 추종자들 가운데 상당수가 그를 두고 \"미온적\"이라고 비난할 정도였으니까 — 그래도 당테스를 위해 청원을 넣을 만큼은 영향력을 갖추게 되었다.\n\n빌포르는 자리를 지켰지만, 결혼은 좀 더 유리한 때를 기다리며 미루어졌다. 황제가 왕좌를 지킨다면, 제라르는 출셋길을 위해 다른 혼처를 구해야 했다. 루이 18세가 돌아온다면, 생메랑 씨의 영향력은 그 자신의 영향력과 마찬가지로 크게 불어날 테고, 그 결혼은 한층 더 어울리는 일이 될 터였다. 그리하여 차장검사는 마르세유의 으뜸가는 사법관이 되었는데, 어느 날 아침 그의 방문이 열리며 모렐 씨가 왔다는 전갈이 들어왔다.\n\n다른 사람 같았으면 서둘러 그를 맞이했을 것이다. 그러나 빌포르는 수완가였고, 그것이 약점을 드러내는 짓임을 알았다. 그는 모렐을 대기실에서 기다리게 했다. 함께 있는 사람도 없었지만, 그저 검찰관이란 누구든 늘 기다리게 한다는 단순한 이유에서였다. 그러고는 십오 분 동안 서류를 읽은 뒤에야 모렐 씨를 들이라고 일렀다.\n\n모렐은 빌포르가 풀이 죽어 있으리라 짐작했다. 그러나 그는 육 주 전 보았던 그대로였다. 차분하고 단호하며, 교양 있는 자와 천박한 자를 가르는 가장 넘기 힘든 장벽, 그 얼음장 같은 정중함으로 가득 차 있었다.\n\n모렐은 자신을 보면 사법관이 부들부들 떨리라 기대하며 빌포르의 집무실에 들어섰다. 그러나 정작 그 자신이 온몸에 차가운 전율을 느꼈다. 빌포르가 책상에 팔꿈치를 괴고 손으로 머리를 받친 채 앉아 있는 모습을 보았을 때였다. 모렐은 문간에 멈춰 섰다. 빌포르는 그를 알아보기 어렵다는 듯 빤히 바라보았다. 정직한 선주가 손에 든 모자를 만지작거리는 동안 잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤,\n\n\"모렐 씨, 맞으시지요?\" 빌포르가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"가까이 오시지요.\" 사법관이 거들먹거리는 손짓으로 말했다. \"어인 일로 이렇게 찾아오시는 영광을 베푸셨는지 말씀해 보십시오.\"\n\n\"짐작이 안 가십니까, 검사님?\" 모렐이 물었다.\n\n\"전혀요. 다만 무슨 일로든 도움이 되어 드릴 수 있다면 기쁘겠습니다.\"\n\n\"모든 것이 검사님께 달려 있습니다.\"\n\n\"분명히 말씀해 보시지요.\"\n\n\"검사님,\" 모렐이 말을 이어 가며 차츰 침착함을 되찾았다. \"기억하십니까? 황제 폐하께서 상륙하시기 며칠 전, 제가 한 젊은이를 위해 탄원하러 찾아왔던 일 말입니다. 제 배의 항해사인데, 엘바섬과 내통한 혐의를 받고 있었지요. 며칠 전만 해도 죄였던 것이 오늘은 칭찬받을 자격이 되었습니다. 그때 검사님은 루이 18세를 섬기며 그에게 아무런 호의도 보이지 않으셨습니다 — 그것이 검사님의 의무였으니까요. 오늘 검사님은 나폴레옹을 섬기시니 마땅히 그를 보호하셔야 합니다 — 그 역시 검사님의 의무이지요. 그러니 그가 어떻게 되었는지 여쭈러 왔습니다.\"\n\n빌포르는 안간힘을 다해 자제하려 애썼다. \"그 사람 이름이 무엇입니까?\" 그가 말했다. \"이름을 말씀해 보십시오.\"\n\n\"에드몽 당테스입니다.\"\n\n빌포르는 이 이름을 듣느니 차라리 스물다섯 걸음 앞에서 권총 총구를 마주하는 편이 나았을 것이다. 그러나 그는 낯빛 하나 변하지 않았다.\n\n\"당테스,\" 그가 되뇌었다. \"에드몽 당테스.\"\n\n\"네, 그렇습니다.\" 빌포르는 커다란 등록부를 펼치더니 탁자로 갔다가, 탁자에서 다시 등록부로 돌아왔다. 그러고는 모렐을 향해 돌아서며,\n\n\"잘못 아신 게 아니라고 확신하십니까, 선생?\" 세상에서 가장 자연스러운 어조로 그가 물었다.\n\n모렐이 좀 더 눈치 빠른 사람이거나 이런 일에 밝았더라면, 검찰관이 자신을 교도소장이나 도지사에게 보내지 않고 직접 이런 사안에 답하는 것을 의아하게 여겼을 것이다. 그러나 두려움을 불러일으키리라던 기대가 빗나간 모렐은, 그저 상대의 호의에만 마음을 쓰고 있었다. 빌포르의 계산은 옳았다.\n\n\"아닙니다.\" 모렐이 말했다. \"잘못 안 게 아닙니다. 그를 십 년 동안 알고 지냈고, 그중 최근 사 년은 제 밑에서 일했습니다. 기억나지 않으십니까, 육 주쯤 전에 제가 선처를 호소하러 왔던 일이요. 오늘 정의를 호소하러 온 것처럼 말입니다. 그때 검사님은 저를 몹시 차갑게 맞으셨지요. 아, 그 시절 왕당파는 보나파르트파에게 무척 가혹했으니까요.\"\n\n\"선생,\" 빌포르가 대꾸했다. \"그때 저는 왕당파였습니다. 부르봉가가 왕위의 상속자일 뿐 아니라 국민이 선택한 자들이라 믿었기 때문이지요. 나폴레옹의 기적 같은 귀환이 저를 굴복시켰습니다. 정당한 군주란 백성에게 사랑받는 자이니까요.\"\n\n\"옳은 말씀입니다!\" 모렐이 외쳤다. \"그렇게 말씀하시는 걸 들으니 좋습니다. 에드몽에게도 좋은 징조라 여기겠습니다.\"\n\n\"잠깐만 기다리십시오.\" 빌포르가 등록부의 책장을 넘기며 말했다. \"여기 있군요 — 어느 카탈루냐 처녀와 결혼하려던 선원이라. 이제 기억이 납니다. 아주 중대한 혐의였지요.\"\n\n\"어째서요?\"\n\n\"이곳을 떠나 재판소로 끌려갔다는 건 아시겠지요.\"\n\n\"그래서요?\"\n\n\"제가 파리 당국에 보고서를 올렸고, 일주일 뒤에 그는 이송되었습니다.\"\n\n\"이송이라니!\" 모렐이 말했다. \"그를 어떻게 했단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"아, 페네스트렐이나 피네롤, 아니면 생트마르그리트섬으로 보내졌겠지요. 언젠가 어느 화창한 아침에 돌아와 다시 선생의 배를 지휘하게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"언제 돌아오든, 그 자리는 비워 두겠습니다. 하지만 어째서 아직 돌아오지 않은 겁니까? 제가 보기엔, 정부가 가장 먼저 해야 할 일은 자기를 지지하다 고초를 겪은 이들을 풀어 주는 것일 텐데요.\"\n\n\"너무 서두르지 마십시오, 모렐 씨.\" 빌포르가 대답했다. \"투옥 명령은 높은 곳에서 내려왔으니, 석방 명령도 같은 곳에서 나와야 합니다. 그런데 나폴레옹이 복위한 지 겨우 보름밖에 지나지 않았으니, 아직 서신들이 발송되지 않았을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 모렐이 말했다. \"이 모든 절차를 앞당길 방법은 없습니까 — 그를 구금에서 풀어 줄 방법 말입니다.\"\n\n\"구금된 적이 없습니다.\"\n\n\"네?\"\n\n\"정부로서는 때때로 어떤 사람을 흔적 하나 남기지 않고 사라지게 만드는 것이 긴요할 때가 있지요. 어떤 서류나 문서로도 그 뜻을 거스를 수 없게 말입니다.\"\n\n\"부르봉가 치하에서는 그랬을지 몰라도, 지금은……\"\n\n\"늘 그래 왔습니다, 모렐 씨. 루이 14세 치세 이래로 줄곧 말입니다. 황제는 루이 본인보다도 옥살이 규율에 더 엄격하시고, 이름이 등록부에 오르지 않은 죄수의 수는 헤아릴 수조차 없습니다.\" 모렐에게 어떤 의심이 있었다 한들, 이만한 친절이라면 모두 흩어졌을 것이다.\n\n\"그렇다면, 빌포르 씨, 제가 어떻게 하면 좋겠습니까?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"장관에게 청원하십시오.\"\n\n\"아, 그게 어떤 건지 압니다. 장관은 매일 이백 통의 청원을 받지만 세 통도 읽지 않지요.\"\n\n\"맞습니다. 하지만 제가 부서하여 직접 제출한 청원이라면 읽을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그걸 검사님이 전해 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그러지요. 당테스는 그때는 죄인이었으나 지금은 무고한 사람이니, 그를 풀어 주는 것 또한 그를 단죄했던 것 못지않은 제 의무입니다.\" 이렇게 빌포르는 행여 있을지 모를 조사의 위험을 미리 막았다. 그럴 가능성은 희박했지만, 만약 조사가 이루어진다면 그는 속수무책이 될 터였다.\n\n\"그런데 장관에게 어떻게 써야 합니까?\"\n\n\"거기 앉으십시오.\" 빌포르가 자기 자리를 모렐에게 내주며 말했다. \"제가 부르는 대로 받아 적으시지요.\"\n\n\"그렇게까지 해 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"물론입니다. 다만 지체하지 마십시오. 이미 너무 많은 시간을 허비했으니까요.\"\n\n\"맞습니다. 그 가엾은 친구가 지금 이 순간에도 얼마나 고초를 겪고 있을지 생각하면……\"\n\n빌포르는 그 말에 몸서리쳤다. 그러나 이미 돌이킬 수 없을 만큼 멀리 와 버렸다. 빌포르의 야망을 채우려면 당테스는 짓밟혀야만 했다.\n\n빌포르는 청원서를 받아쓰게 했는데, 분명 더없이 선한 의도에서였겠지만, 당테스의 애국적 공로가 부풀려졌고, 그는 나폴레옹의 귀환을 도운 가장 적극적인 첩자 가운데 하나로 그려졌다. 이 문서를 보면 장관이 그를 즉시 풀어 주리라는 것은 불 보듯 뻔했다. 청원서가 완성되자 빌포르가 소리 내어 읽었다.\n\n\"이만하면 됐습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"나머지는 제게 맡기십시오.\"\n\n\"청원서는 곧 보내집니까?\"\n\n\"오늘 보냅니다.\"\n\n\"검사님이 부서하시고요?\"\n\n\"제가 할 수 있는 최선은 이 청원서 내용이 사실임을 보증하는 것입니다.\" 빌포르는 앉아서 맨 아래에 증명서를 써 넣었다.\n\n\"이제 또 무엇을 하면 됩니까?\"\n\n\"필요한 일은 제가 다 하겠습니다.\" 이 다짐에 모렐은 기뻐했다. 그는 빌포르에게 작별을 고하고, 늙은 당테스에게 곧 아들을 보게 될 거라고 알리러 서둘러 떠났다.\n\n빌포르로 말하자면, 그는 청원서를 파리로 보내기는커녕, 당테스를 무섭게 옭아매는 그 청원서를 조심스레 간직했다. 충분히 일어날 법한 어떤 사건 — 즉 두 번째 왕정복고를 기대하면서 말이다. 당테스는 여전히 죄수로 남았고, 루이 18세의 왕좌가 무너지는 소리도, 그보다 더 비극적인 제국의 멸망 소리도 듣지 못했다.\n\n백일천하 동안 모렐은 두 번 청을 거듭했고, 빌포르는 두 번 다 약속으로 그를 달랬다. 마침내 워털루가 닥쳤고, 모렐은 다시 찾아오지 않았다. 그는 자기가 할 수 있는 일을 다 했고, 새로이 무언가를 시도해 봐야 부질없이 제 처지만 위태롭게 할 뿐이었다.\n\n루이 18세가 다시 왕좌에 올랐다. 마르세유가 회한 어린 기억으로 가득 차 버린 빌포르는 툴루즈의 검찰관 자리를 청하여 얻어 냈고, 보름 뒤 생메랑 양과 결혼했다. 그녀의 아버지는 이제 궁정에서 그 어느 때보다 높은 자리를 차지하고 있었다.\n\n그리하여 당테스는 백일천하가 지나고 워털루가 지난 뒤에도 여전히 지하 감옥에 갇힌 채, 땅에서도 하늘에서도 잊힌 존재로 남았다.\n\n당글라르는 당테스를 짓누른 비참한 운명이 얼마나 가혹한 것인지 속속들이 알았다. 그래서 나폴레옹이 프랑스로 돌아왔을 때, 그는 평범한 인간들이 으레 그러듯 그 우연의 일치를 두고 섭리의 뜻이라 불렀다. 그러나 나폴레옹이 파리로 돌아오자 당글라르는 간담이 서늘해졌고, 당테스가 복수의 사명을 띠고 돌아올까 봐 늘 두려움 속에 살았다. 그리하여 그는 모렐 씨에게 바다를 떠나고 싶다는 뜻을 전했고, 그에게서 어느 스페인 상인에게 보내는 추천장을 받아 냈다. 그는 3월 말, 다시 말해 나폴레옹이 돌아온 지 열흘이나 열이틀쯤 뒤에 그 상인의 밑으로 들어갔다. 그러고는 마드리드로 떠났고, 그 뒤로는 소식이 끊겼다.\n\n페르낭은 당테스가 사라졌다는 것 말고는 아무것도 알지 못했다. 그가 어떻게 되었는지는 알아보려고도 하지 않았다. 다만 연적이 사라져 한숨 돌리게 된 그동안, 그는 한편으로는 그가 사라진 까닭을 메르세데스에게 둘러댈 방도를, 다른 한편으로는 이주와 납치의 계획을 궁리했다. 이따금 그는 마르세유와 카탈루냐 마을이 한눈에 내려다보이는 파로곶 꼭대기에 슬프고도 미동도 없이 앉아, 젊고 잘생긴 사내가 나타나기를 살피곤 했다. 그에게도 그 사내는 복수를 알리는 전령이었던 것이다. 페르낭은 마음을 정했다. 당테스를 쏘아 죽이고, 그런 뒤에 자기도 목숨을 끊으리라. 그러나 페르낭의 생각은 빗나갔다. 그런 기질의 사내는 결코 스스로 목숨을 끊지 않는다. 끊임없이 무언가를 바라기 때문이다.\n\n이 무렵 제국은 마지막 징집을 단행했고, 무기를 들 수 있는 프랑스의 사내란 사내는 모두 황제의 부름에 따르려 달려갔다. 페르낭도 다른 이들과 함께 떠났다. 자기가 떠나 있는 동안 연적이 어쩌면 돌아와 메르세데스와 결혼해 버릴지 모른다는 끔찍한 생각을 품은 채로. 만약 페르낭이 정말로 스스로 죽을 작정이었다면, 메르세데스와 헤어지던 그 순간에 그렇게 했을 것이다. 그의 헌신과, 그녀의 불행을 향해 보인 연민은 고귀한 마음에 으레 불러일으키는 효과를 자아냈다 — 메르세데스는 늘 페르낭에게 진심 어린 정을 품고 있었고, 이제 거기에 고마움까지 더해졌다.\n\n\"오라버니,\" 그녀가 그의 어깨에 배낭을 메어 주며 말했다. \"부디 몸조심하세요. 오라버니마저 죽으면, 저는 이 세상에 홀로 남게 되니까요.\" 이 말은 페르낭의 가슴에 한 줄기 희망의 빛을 던졌다. 당테스가 돌아오지 않는다면, 언젠가 메르세데스가 자기 사람이 될지도 몰랐다.\n\n메르세데스는 홀로 남았다. 한 번도 그토록 황량해 보인 적 없는 드넓은 들판과, 한 번도 그토록 끝없어 보인 적 없는 바다를 마주한 채로. 그녀는 눈물에 젖어 카탈루냐 마을을 헤매고 다녔다. 어떤 때는 조각상처럼 말없이 꼼짝 않고 서서 마르세유 쪽을 바라보았고, 또 어떤 때는 바다를 응시하며, 차라리 저 바다의 심연에 몸을 던져 모든 슬픔을 끝내는 편이 낫지 않을까 고민했다. 그 결심을 행동으로 옮기지 못한 것은 용기가 없어서가 아니었다. 신앙심이 그녀를 도와 구해 준 것이었다.\n\n카드루스도 페르낭처럼 군대에 편입되었으나, 결혼한 데다 여덟 살 더 많았으므로 그저 국경으로 보내졌을 뿐이다. 오로지 희망으로만 버텨 온 늙은 당테스는 나폴레옹이 몰락하자 모든 희망을 잃었다. 아들과 헤어진 지 다섯 달 뒤, 거의 아들이 체포되던 그 시각에, 그는 메르세데스의 품에서 숨을 거두었다. 모렐 씨가 장례 비용과 그 가엾은 노인이 진 자잘한 빚 몇 가지를 갚아 주었다.\n\n이 행동에는 선의 이상의 것이 담겨 있었다. 거기에는 용기가 있었다. 남부 전역이 불타오르고 있었고, 당테스처럼 위험한 보나파르트파의 아비를, 그것도 임종의 자리에서까지 도왔다는 것은 죄로 낙인찍히는 일이었으니까.\n\n# Chapter 14. The Two Prisoners\n\n루이 18세가 복위하고 일 년이 지났을 무렵, 감옥 총감독관이 시찰을 나왔다. 당테스는 자신의 독방에서 시찰 준비로 분주한 소리를 들었다. 그가 갇혀 있는 깊은 곳에서는, 매시간 천장에서 떨어지는 물방울 소리까지 들을 수 있는 죄수의 귀가 아니고서는 도무지 알아챌 수 없는 소리였다. 그는 살아 있는 사람들 사이에 무언가 심상치 않은 일이 벌어지고 있음을 짐작했다. 그러나 너무 오랫동안 세상과 단절되어 있던 터라, 그는 스스로를 죽은 사람이나 다름없다고 여기고 있었다.\n\n감독관은 여러 죄수들의 독방과 지하 감방을 차례로 둘러보았다. 품행이 바르거나 어리석은 까닭에 정부의 관대한 처분을 받을 만한 자들이었다. 그는 식사는 어떤지, 청할 것은 없는지 물었다. 한결같은 대답은, 음식이 형편없으며 풀려나고 싶다는 것이었다.\n\n감독관은 그 밖에 청할 것이 더 없는지 물었다. 죄수들은 고개를 저었다. 자유 말고 그들이 무엇을 바랄 수 있겠는가? 감독관이 미소를 지으며 소장을 돌아보았다.\n\n\"정부가 이런 쓸데없는 시찰에 무슨 명분을 댈 수 있는지 모르겠소. 죄수 한 명을 보면 다 본 거나 마찬가지요. 늘 똑같지. 제대로 먹지도 못하고, 죄도 없다는 거요. 다른 자들은 없소?\"\n\n\"있습니다. 위험한 죄수들과 미친 죄수들이 지하 감방에 있습니다.\"\n\n\"가 봅시다.\" 감독관이 지친 기색으로 말했다. \"기왕 시작한 익살극이니 끝까지 해야지. 지하 감방을 봅시다.\"\n\n\"먼저 병사 둘을 부르는 게 좋겠습니다.\" 소장이 말했다. \"죄수들이 더러는, 삶이 견딜 수 없이 괴로운 나머지, 사형 선고를 받으려고 부질없는 폭력을 휘두르는 일이 있습니다. 그러다 변을 당하실 수도 있습니다.\"\n\n\"필요한 만큼 만반의 대비를 하시오.\" 감독관이 대답했다.\n\n이에 병사 둘을 불러왔고, 감독관은 계단을 따라 내려갔다. 어찌나 더럽고 축축하고 어두운지, 보기에도 냄새 맡기에도 숨쉬기에도 역겨운 곳이었다.\n\n\"오.\" 감독관이 외쳤다. \"여기서 어떻게 사람이 살 수 있단 말이오?\"\n\n\"극히 위험한 음모자입니다. 대담하고 단호한 자라, 가장 철저히 감시하라는 명을 받은 사람입니다.\"\n\n\"혼자 갇혀 있소?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"여기 들어온 지는 얼마나 됐소?\"\n\n\"거의 일 년 됐습니다.\"\n\n\"처음 들어왔을 때부터 여기에 가뒀소?\"\n\n\"아닙니다. 음식을 가져다주던 간수를 죽이려 한 뒤부터입니다.\"\n\n\"간수를 죽이려 했다고?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다. 바로 지금 우리에게 불을 밝혀 주고 있는 저 사람입니다. 그렇지 않나, 앙투안?\" 소장이 물었다.\n\n\"그렇고말고요. 저를 죽이려 했습니다!\" 간수가 대답했다.\n\n\"미친 게 틀림없군.\" 감독관이 말했다.\n\n\"그보다 더합니다. 악마입니다!\" 간수가 대답했다.\n\n\"내가 이자를 고발할까?\" 감독관이 물었다.\n\n\"아, 아닙니다. 그래 봤자 소용없습니다. 게다가 이자는 이제 거의 미쳤고, 일 년만 더 지나면 완전히 미칠 겁니다.\"\n\n\"차라리 잘된 일이지. 그러면 덜 괴로울 테니.\" 감독관이 말했다. 이 말에서 드러나듯, 그는 박애심이 넘치고 모든 면에서 자기 직책에 더없이 어울리는 사람이었다.\n\n\"옳으신 말씀입니다.\" 소장이 대답했다. \"그 말씀을 들으니 이 문제를 깊이 헤아리고 계신 것을 알겠습니다. 자, 여기서 스무 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳에, 또 다른 계단을 내려가야 나오는 지하 감방에 늙은 신부가 하나 있습니다. 한때 이탈리아에서 한 당파의 지도자였던 자로, 1811년부터 이곳에 있었습니다. 1813년에 미쳤는데, 그 변화가 놀랍습니다. 예전에는 울더니 이제는 웃고, 예전에는 비쩍 말랐는데 이제는 살이 오릅니다. 한번 보시는 게 좋을 겁니다. 그자의 광기는 제법 재미있거든요.\"\n\n\"둘 다 보겠소.\" 감독관이 대답했다. \"내 직무를 양심껏 수행해야 하니.\"\n\n이번이 감독관의 첫 시찰이었다. 그는 자신의 권위를 과시하고 싶었다.\n\n\"이자부터 봅시다.\" 그가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"여부가 있겠습니까.\" 소장이 대답하고는 간수에게 문을 열라는 신호를 보냈다. 자물쇠에 열쇠가 돌아가는 소리와 경첩이 삐걱이는 소리에, 위쪽의 좁은 쇠창살 사이로 들어오는 한 줄기 빛이 보이는 감방 한구석에 웅크리고 있던 당테스가 고개를 들었다. 횃불을 든 간수 둘의 호위를 받고 병사 둘을 거느린, 게다가 소장이 모자를 벗고 말을 건네는 낯선 사람을 보자, 당테스는 사정을 단번에 알아채고 상부 권력에 호소할 순간이 왔음을 깨달았다. 그는 두 손을 맞잡고 앞으로 뛰쳐나갔다.\n\n병사들이 그가 감독관을 공격하려는 줄 알고 총검을 들이밀자, 감독관은 두세 걸음 물러섰다. 당테스는 자신이 위험한 자로 비치고 있음을 알아챘다. 그러자 가진 모든 겸손을 두 눈과 목소리에 담아 감독관에게 호소하며 연민을 자아내려 애썼다.\n\n감독관은 주의 깊게 귀 기울이더니, 소장을 돌아보며 말했다. \"이자는 신앙심을 갖게 되겠군. 벌써 한결 온순해졌어. 겁을 먹고 총검 앞에서 물러섰지 않소. 미친 자들은 아무것도 두려워하지 않는 법이오. 내가 샤랑통에서 이에 관해 흥미로운 관찰을 좀 했지.\" 그러고는 죄수를 향해 말했다. \"원하는 게 무엇이오?\"\n\n\"제가 무슨 죄를 지었는지 알고 싶습니다. 재판을 받고 싶습니다. 죄가 있다면 총살을 당하고, 죄가 없다면 풀려나기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"식사는 잘 나오시오?\" 감독관이 물었다.\n\n\"그런 것 같습니다. 모르겠습니다. 그건 중요하지 않습니다. 정말로 중요한 것은, 저뿐 아니라 사법 관리들과 임금님께도 마찬가지인데, 죄 없는 사람이 파렴치한 밀고의 희생양이 되어 감옥에서 시들어 가다가, 자신을 처형한 자들을 저주하며 이곳에서 죽어 간다는 사실입니다.\"\n\n\"오늘은 아주 겸손하구먼.\" 소장이 한마디 했다. \"늘 그런 건 아니지. 이를테면 며칠 전, 간수를 죽이려 들었을 때만 해도 말이야.\"\n\n\"맞습니다. 그리고 그에게 용서를 빕니다. 그는 늘 제게 잘해 주었으니까요. 하지만 그때 저는 제정신이 아니었습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 지금은 제정신이라는 건가?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다. 갇혀 지내면서 저도 누그러졌습니다. 이곳에 너무 오래 있었으니까요.\"\n\n\"너무 오래라니, 그럼 언제 체포됐소?\" 감독관이 물었다.\n\n\"1815년 2월 28일, 오후 두 시 반이었습니다.\"\n\n\"오늘이 1816년 7월 30일이니, 아니, 겨우 열일곱 달밖에 안 됐잖소.\"\n\n\"겨우 열일곱 달이라고요.\" 당테스가 대답했다. \"오, 감옥에서의 열일곱 달이 어떤 것인지 모르시는 말씀입니다! 열일곱 세기라고 하는 편이 낫지요. 특히 저처럼 야망의 정점에 막 다다랐던 사람에게는요. 저처럼 사랑하는 여인과 막 결혼하려던 참이었고, 눈앞에 명예로운 앞길이 열려 있었는데, 그 모든 것을 한순간에 잃어버린 사람에게는요. 자신의 앞날이 무너지는 것을 지켜보면서도, 약혼한 아내가 어찌 되었는지, 늙으신 아버지가 아직 살아 계신지조차 알지 못하는 사람에게는요! 끝없는 바다에 익숙한 뱃사람에게 열일곱 달의 감금이란, 어떤 인간의 죄로도 마땅하지 않을 만큼 가혹한 형벌입니다. 그러니 저를 가엾이 여기시어, 정보가 아니라 재판을 청해 주십시오. 사면이 아니라 판결을, 재판을, 나리, 저는 그저 재판을 청할 뿐입니다. 죄를 추궁당하는 사람에게 그것만은 거절할 수 없는 일 아니겠습니까!\"\n\n\"두고 봅시다.\" 감독관이 말하고는 소장을 돌아보았다. \"정말이지 저 가엾은 자가 마음을 움직이는구려. 저자에 대한 증거를 보여 주시오.\"\n\n\"여부가 있겠습니까. 다만 끔찍한 혐의들을 보시게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"나리.\" 당테스가 말을 이었다. \"저를 풀어 주시는 것이 나리의 권한 밖이라는 것은 압니다. 하지만 저를 위해 변호해 주실 수는 있습니다. 재판을 받게 해 주실 수는 있습니다. 제가 청하는 것은 그것뿐입니다. 제 죄가 무엇인지, 무슨 까닭으로 제가 유죄가 되었는지 알게 해 주십시오. 모르고 있는 것이 그 무엇보다 괴롭습니다.\"\n\n\"불을 들고 가지.\" 감독관이 말했다.\n\n\"나리.\" 당테스가 외쳤다. \"목소리만 들어도 연민을 느끼고 계신 것을 알겠습니다. 적어도 희망을 가져도 좋다고 말씀해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"그렇게는 말해 줄 수 없소.\" 감독관이 대답했다. \"그대의 사정을 살펴보겠다고 약속할 수 있을 뿐이오.\"\n\n\"오, 저는 자유의 몸이 되겠군요. 그렇다면 저는 살았습니다!\"\n\n\"누가 그대를 체포했소?\"\n\n\"빌포르 씨입니다. 그분을 만나, 그분이 하는 말을 들어 보십시오.\"\n\n\"빌포르 씨는 이제 마르세유에 없소. 지금은 툴루즈에 있소.\"\n\n\"제 유일한 보호자가 떠나 버렸으니, 제가 갇혀 있는 것도 이젠 놀랍지 않군요.\" 당테스가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"빌포르 씨가 그대에게 개인적인 반감을 품을 만한 까닭이 있었소?\"\n\n\"전혀 없습니다. 오히려 그분은 제게 무척 친절했습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그가 그대에 관해 남긴 기록을 믿어도 되겠군?\"\n\n\"전적으로요.\"\n\n\"좋소. 그럼 참고 기다리시오.\"\n\n당테스는 무릎을 꿇고 간절히 기도했다. 문이 닫혔다. 그러나 이번에는 당테스 곁에 새로운 동거인이 남겨졌으니, 바로 희망이었다.\n\n\"명부를 지금 바로 보시겠습니까,\" 소장이 물었다. \"아니면 다른 감방으로 가시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"전부 둘러봅시다.\" 감독관이 말했다. \"이 계단을 한번 올라가고 나면, 다시 내려올 엄두가 나지 않을 테니.\"\n\n\"아, 이자는 저자와는 다릅니다. 이자의 광기는 저자가 보여 준 멀쩡한 정신만큼 마음을 흔들지는 않을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 망상에 빠져 있소?\"\n\n\"제가 어마어마한 보물을 가지고 있다고 믿습니다. 첫해에는 풀어 주는 대가로 정부에 백만 프랑을 내놓겠다고 하더니, 이듬해에는 이백만, 그다음 해에는 삼백만, 그렇게 해마다 늘려 갔습니다. 지금 갇힌 지 다섯 해째인데, 나리께 따로 말씀드리고 싶다고 청한 뒤 오백만을 내놓겠다고 할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"참 별나군! 이름이 무엇이오?\"\n\n\"파리아 신부입니다.\"\n\n\"27호로군.\" 감독관이 말했다.\n\n\"여깁니다. 문을 열게, 앙투안.\"\n\n간수가 시키는 대로 하자, 감독관은 사람들이 흔히 부르는 대로 미친 신부의 방 안을 호기심 어린 눈으로 들여다보았다.\n\n감방 한가운데, 벽에서 떨어져 나온 회반죽 조각으로 그린 원 안에 한 남자가 앉아 있었다. 다 해진 옷이 몸을 겨우 가리고 있을 뿐이었다. 그는 그 원 안에 기하학 도형의 선들을 그리고 있었는데, 마르켈루스의 병사가 아르키메데스를 죽일 때 그가 문제에 골몰해 있던 것만큼이나 자신의 문제에 깊이 빠져 있는 듯했다. 그는 문소리에도 꿈쩍하지 않고 계산을 이어 가다가, 횃불의 불빛이 감방의 침침한 벽을 평소와 다른 환한 빛으로 밝히자, 그제야 고개를 들고 그곳에 모인 사람들의 수를 보고 놀랐다. 그는 황급히 침대 덮개를 움켜쥐어 몸에 둘렀다.\n\n\"원하는 게 무엇이오?\" 감독관이 말했다.\n\n\"제가요, 나리?\" 신부가 놀란 기색으로 대답했다. \"저는 아무것도 바라는 게 없습니다.\"\n\n\"잘못 알아들으셨군.\" 감독관이 말을 이었다. \"나는 정부의 명을 받고 감옥을 시찰하며 죄수들의 청을 듣고자 온 사람이오.\"\n\n\"오, 그렇다면 이야기가 다릅니다.\" 신부가 외쳤다. \"우리가 서로 잘 통할 수 있겠군요.\"\n\n\"보십시오.\" 소장이 속삭였다. \"제가 말씀드린 그대로지요.\"\n\n\"나리.\" 죄수가 말을 이었다. \"저는 파리아 신부로, 로마에서 태어났습니다. 이십 년 동안 스파다 추기경의 비서로 일했지요. 1811년 초쯤에 체포되었는데, 무슨 까닭인지는 모릅니다. 그 뒤로 이탈리아 정부와 프랑스 정부에 줄곧 석방을 요구해 왔습니다.\"\n\n\"어찌하여 프랑스 정부에 말이오?\"\n\n\"피옴비노에서 체포되었으니까요. 밀라노나 피렌체처럼, 피옴비노도 어느 프랑스 도(道)의 중심지가 되었으리라 짐작합니다.\"\n\n\"아.\" 감독관이 말했다. \"이탈리아의 최근 소식은 모르시는군.\"\n\n\"제가 아는 소식은 체포되던 날에 멈춰 있습니다.\" 파리아 신부가 대답했다. \"황제께서 갓난 아드님을 위해 로마 왕국을 세우셨으니, 이탈리아를 하나의 통일된 왕국으로 만들겠다는 마키아벨리와 체사레 보르자의 꿈을 이루셨으리라 짐작합니다.\"\n\n\"나리.\" 감독관이 대답했다. \"그대가 그토록 열렬히 옹호하는 그 원대한 계획을, 섭리가 바꾸어 놓았소이다.\"\n\n\"그것이야말로 이탈리아를 강하고 행복하며 독립된 나라로 만드는 유일한 길입니다.\"\n\n\"그럴 수도 있겠지. 다만 나는 정치를 논하러 온 것이 아니라, 그대가 청하거나 불평할 것이 있는지 물으러 온 것이오.\"\n\n\"음식은 다른 감옥과 마찬가지입니다. 다시 말해 아주 형편없지요. 거처는 몹시 건강에 해롭지만, 지하 감방치고는 그런대로 견딜 만합니다. 하지만 제가 말하고 싶은 것은 그게 아니라, 더없이 중대한, 밝혀야 할 비밀입니다.\"\n\n\"이제 본론으로 들어가는군요.\" 소장이 속삭였다.\n\n\"바로 그 때문에 나리를 뵙게 되어 기쁩니다.\" 신부가 말을 이었다. \"비록 나리께서 더없이 중요한 계산을 방해하셨지만 말입니다. 그 계산이 성공한다면 뉴턴의 체계를 뒤바꿀 수도 있을 텐데요. 잠시 따로 말씀을 나눌 수 있게 해 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"제가 뭐라고 했습니까?\" 소장이 말했다.\n\n\"그대가 이자를 잘 알고 있었군.\" 감독관이 미소를 띠며 대답했다.\n\n\"그대가 청하는 것은 불가능하오, 나리.\" 그가 파리아를 향해 말을 이었다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 신부가 말했다. \"오백만에 이르는 막대한 금액에 관한 이야기입니다.\"\n\n\"바로 그 액수로군.\" 이번에는 감독관이 속삭였다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 감독관이 떠나려 하는 것을 본 파리아가 말을 이었다. \"우리 둘만 있어야 하는 건 아닙니다. 소장도 함께 있어도 됩니다.\"\n\n\"안타깝게도,\" 소장이 말했다. \"당신이 무슨 말을 할지 나는 미리 다 알고 있소. 당신의 보물에 관한 이야기 아니오?\" 파리아는 다른 사람이라면 누구라도 그가 멀쩡하다고 믿게 만들 만한 표정으로 소장을 응시했다.\n\n\"물론이오.\" 그가 말했다. \"그것 말고 또 무슨 이야기를 하겠소?\"\n\n\"감독관님.\" 소장이 말을 이었다. \"그 이야기라면 저도 이자만큼 잘 풀어놓을 수 있습니다. 지난 네댓 해 동안 줄곧 제 귀에 못이 박이도록 들어 왔으니까요.\"\n\n\"그것이 바로,\" 신부가 대답했다. \"당신이 성서에 나오는 자들과 같다는 증거요. 눈이 있어도 보지 못하고, 귀가 있어도 듣지 못하는 자들 말이오.\"\n\n\"이보시오, 정부는 부유하여 당신의 보물 따위는 필요로 하지 않소.\" 감독관이 대답했다. \"풀려날 때까지 잘 간직하시구려.\" 신부의 두 눈이 번득였다. 그는 감독관의 손을 붙잡았다.\n\n\"하지만 제가 풀려나지 못한다면 어쩌겠습니까,\" 그가 외쳤다. \"죽을 때까지 여기 갇혀 있다면요? 그러면 이 보물은 사라지고 말 겁니다. 차라리 정부가 그것으로 이득을 보는 편이 낫지 않겠습니까? 육백만을 내놓겠습니다. 자유만 준다면 나머지는 가지고서 만족하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"정말이지,\" 감독관이 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"이자가 미쳤다는 말을 미리 듣지 않았더라면, 저자의 말을 믿었을 거요.\"\n\n\"저는 미치지 않았습니다.\" 파리아가 죄수 특유의 예민한 청각으로 알아듣고 대답했다. \"제가 말하는 보물은 실제로 존재합니다. 나리와 약정을 맺고 서명할 것을 제안합니다. 나리께서 땅을 파게 될 그 장소로 안내하겠다고 약속하지요. 만일 제가 나리를 속인다면, 저를 다시 이리로 데려오십시오. 그 이상은 바라지 않습니다.\"\n\n소장이 웃었다. \"그 장소가 여기서 먼가?\"\n\n\"백 리외입니다.\"\n\n\"제법 그럴듯하게 꾸몄군.\" 소장이 말했다. \"죄수들이 너도나도 백 리외를 여행하겠다고 마음먹고, 간수들이 그걸 따라나서 준다면, 도망칠 절호의 기회가 생기겠지.\"\n\n\"빤한 수법이오.\" 감독관이 말했다. \"이 신부의 계획은 독창성이라는 미덕조차 없소이다.\" 그러고는 파리아를 향해 말했다. \"식사는 잘 나오느냐고 물었소만.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"맹세해 주십시오.\" 파리아가 대답했다. \"제 말이 사실로 드러나면 저를 풀어 주겠다고요. 나리께서 그 장소로 가시는 동안 저는 여기 남아 있겠습니다.\"\n\n\"식사는 잘 나오시오?\" 감독관이 거듭 물었다.\n\n\"나리, 나리께서는 아무 위험도 무릅쓰지 않으십니다. 말씀드렸듯이 저는 여기 남아 있을 테니까요. 그러니 제가 도망칠 가능성은 없습니다.\"\n\n\"내 물음에는 대답을 않는군.\" 감독관이 못마땅한 듯 말했다.\n\n\"나리께서도 제 물음에 답하지 않으십니다.\" 신부가 외쳤다. \"나리는 제 황금을 받지 않으시겠다니, 그것은 제가 가지겠습니다. 나리는 제게 자유를 주지 않으시겠다니, 그것은 하느님이 주실 겁니다.\" 그러고는 신부가 덮개를 내던지고 제자리로 돌아가 계산을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"저자가 거기서 뭘 하는 거요?\" 감독관이 말했다.\n\n\"제 보물을 세고 있는 거지요.\" 소장이 대답했다.\n\n파리아는 이 빈정거림에 깊은 경멸의 눈길로 응답했다. 그들은 밖으로 나왔다. 간수가 그들 뒤로 문을 닫았다.\n\n\"저자가 한때는 부유했을지도 모르겠소?\" 감독관이 말했다.\n\n\"아니면 부유하다고 꿈을 꾸다가, 깨어나 미쳐 버린 거겠지요.\"\n\n\"하긴,\" 감독관이 말했다. \"정말로 부유했더라면 여기 와 있지도 않았겠지.\"\n\n파리아 신부에 관한 일은 그렇게 끝이 났다. 그는 자신의 감방에 그대로 남았고, 이번 시찰은 그가 미쳤다는 믿음을 굳혀 놓았을 뿐이었다.\n\n칼리굴라나 네로, 보물을 좇고 불가능한 것을 탐하던 그 황제들이었다면, 이 가엾은 자에게 그 부와 맞바꾸어 그토록 간절히 빌던 자유를 내주었으리라. 그러나 한낱 개연성의 한계에 매여 있는 근대의 군주들에게는 그럴 용기도 욕망도 없다. 그들은 자신의 명령을 듣는 귀와 자신의 행동을 살피는 눈을 두려워한다. 옛날에는 군주들이 스스로를 유피테르의 후손으로 여기며 그 출신으로 보호받는다 믿었으나, 오늘날에는 그들도 침범당하지 않는 존재가 아니다.\n\n박해의 희생자들이 다시 모습을 드러내게 두는 것은 늘 전제 정권의 방침에 어긋나는 일이었다. 종교 재판소가 사지가 뒤틀리고 살이 고문으로 찢긴 희생자들의 모습이 드러나는 것을 좀처럼 허락하지 않았듯이, 광기는 언제나 감방 안에 감추어진다. 만일 거기서 나간다 해도, 어느 음침한 병원으로 보내질 뿐인데, 그곳의 의사는 간수가 넘겨주는 그 망가진 존재 속에서 인간도 정신도 헤아리지 않는다. 감옥에서 미쳐 버린 파리아 신부의 광기야말로 그를 영영 갇혀 지내도록 만든 셈이었다.\n\n감독관은 당테스와 한 약속을 지켰다. 그는 명부를 살펴보았고, 그에 관한 다음과 같은 기록을 찾아냈다.\n\n에드몽 당테스.\n\n과격한 보나파르트파. 엘바섬에서의 귀환에 적극 가담함.\n\n각별히 엄중하게 감시하고 다룰 것.\n\n이 기록은 다른 부분과는 다른 필체로 쓰여 있었는데, 그가 수감된 뒤에 덧붙여진 것임을 알 수 있었다. 감독관은 이 고발에 맞설 도리가 없었다. 그는 그저 손쓸 것 없음이라고 적었을 뿐이다.\n\n이번 시찰은 당테스에게 새로운 활력을 불어넣었다. 그때까지 그는 날짜를 잊고 있었다. 그러나 이제 회반죽 조각으로 1816년 7월 30일이라는 날짜를 적고, 다시는 셈을 놓치지 않으려고 날마다 표시를 해 나갔다. 며칠이 지나고, 몇 주가 지나고, 이어 몇 달이 흘렀다. 당테스는 여전히 기다렸다. 처음에는 보름 안에 풀려나리라 기대했다. 그 보름이 지나자, 그는 감독관이 파리로 돌아가기 전에는 아무것도 하지 않을 것이고, 시찰을 다 마쳐야 그곳에 닿으리라 판단하여, 석 달로 기한을 잡았다. 석 달이 지나고, 다시 여섯 달이 흘렀다. 마침내 열 달 반이 지나도록 좋은 쪽으로의 변화는 일어나지 않았고, 당테스는 감독관의 시찰이 한낱 꿈, 머릿속의 환상이 아니었나 여기기 시작했다.\n\n일 년이 다 지났을 무렵, 소장이 전임되었다. 그는 앙 요새의 책임을 맡게 되었다. 그는 부하 몇을 데려갔는데, 그중에는 당테스의 간수도 있었다. 새 소장이 부임했다. 죄수들의 이름을 일일이 익히는 것은 너무 번거로운 일이었으므로, 그는 이름 대신 번호를 익혔다. 이 끔찍한 곳에는 감방이 쉰 개 있었다. 그 안에 든 자들은 자기 감방의 번호로 불렸고, 이 불행한 젊은이는 더 이상 에드몽 당테스가 아니었다. 그는 이제 34호였다.\n\n# Chapter 15. Number 34 and Number 27\n\n당테스는 불안 속에 갇힌 죄수들이 으레 겪는 모든 고통의 단계를 차례로 거쳐 갔다. 처음에는 무죄를 확신하는 자긍심이 그를 떠받쳐 주었으니, 그것은 희망에 잇닿은 감정이었다. 그러다 그는 자신의 무죄를 의심하기 시작했는데, 이는 그가 정신이 나갔다는 소장의 믿음을 어느 정도 뒷받침하는 셈이었다. 그러고는 자긍심마저 누그러뜨린 채, 하느님이 아니라 인간에게 탄원하기에 이르렀다. 하느님은 언제나 마지막 의지처다. 하느님부터 찾아야 마땅한 불행한 사람들도, 다른 모든 구원의 수단을 다 써 버리기 전에는 하느님께 아무 희망도 걸지 않는 법이다.\n\n당테스는 지금 갇힌 지하 감방에서 다른 감방으로 옮겨 달라고 청했다. 그곳이 더 어둡고 깊은 곳이라 해도 상관없었다. 아무리 불리한 변화라도 변화는 변화였고, 그것이 그에게 약간의 위안이라도 줄 터였다. 그는 밖을 거닐게 해 달라고, 신선한 공기와 책과 필기도구를 달라고 애원했다. 그의 청은 받아들여지지 않았으나, 그래도 그는 끊임없이 청했다. 그는 새 간수에게 말을 거는 데에도 익숙해졌다. 새 간수는 옛 간수보다 더하면 더했지 덜하지 않을 만큼 말이 없는 사람이었다. 그래도 사람에게 말을 거는 것은, 비록 그가 벙어리나 다름없다 해도, 무언가는 되었다. 당테스는 제 목소리를 듣기 위해 말을 했다. 혼자 있을 때도 말해 보려 했으나, 제 목소리에 그만 소스라쳤다.\n\n갇히기 전, 당테스는 도둑과 부랑자와 살인자로 이루어진 죄수들의 무리를 떠올리며 몸서리치곤 했다. 이제 그는 그들 틈에 끼고 싶었다. 간수의 얼굴 말고 다른 얼굴이라도 보고 싶었기 때문이다. 그는 갤리선을 그리워했다. 그 치욕스러운 죄수복과 쇠사슬, 어깨에 찍힌 낙인까지도. 갤리선의 노예들은 하늘의 신선한 공기를 들이마셨고, 서로의 얼굴을 보았다. 그들은 더없이 행복한 자들이었다.\n\n어느 날 그는 간수에게 동무를 하나 붙여 달라고 간청했다. 그 미친 신부라도 좋다고 했다. 간수는 그토록 많은 고통을 늘 보아 온 탓에 거칠고 무뎌졌으나, 그래도 사람이었다. 마음 깊은 곳에서는 이토록 괴로워하는 가엾은 젊은이에게 종종 연민을 느꼈다. 그래서 그는 34호의 청을 소장에게 전했다. 그러나 소장은 당테스가 음모를 꾸미거나 탈옥을 꾀하려는 것이라고 그럴듯하게 짐작하고는 그 청을 거절했다. 당테스는 인간의 모든 방도를 다 써 버렸고, 그제야 하느님께로 돌아섰다.\n\n그토록 오래 잊고 있던 경건한 생각들이 모두 되살아났다. 그는 어머니가 가르쳐 준 기도를 떠올렸고, 그 한마디 한마디에서 새로운 뜻을 발견했다. 형편이 좋을 때 기도란 그저 말의 뒤섞임일 뿐이지만, 불행이 닥치고서야 비로소 불행한 자는 하늘의 자비를 구하는 그 숭고한 언어의 뜻을 처음으로 깨닫는 법이다. 그는 기도했고, 소리 내어 기도했다. 더는 제 목소리에 소스라치지 않았으니, 일종의 황홀경에 빠져들었기 때문이다. 그는 제 삶의 모든 행적을 전능하신 분 앞에 펼쳐 놓고, 이루어야 할 과업을 다짐했다. 그리고 기도가 끝날 때마다 하느님보다 인간에게 더 자주 던져지는 그 탄원을 덧붙였다. \"저희에게 잘못한 이를 저희가 용서하듯이, 저희 죄를 용서하소서.\" 그러나 그 간절한 기도에도 당테스는 여전히 죄수였다.\n\n이윽고 어둠이 그를 무겁게 짓눌렀다. 당테스는 생각이 지극히 단순하고 교육을 받지 못한 사람이었다. 그러니 지하 감방의 고독 속에서 그는 마음의 눈으로 시대의 역사를 가로지를 수도, 스러진 민족들을 되살릴 수도, 마틴●의 바빌론 그림 속에서 천상의 빛깔로 눈앞에 떠오르는 그 광대하고 경이로운 고대 도시들을 상상 속에 다시 세울 수도 없었다. 그는 그럴 수 없었다. 과거는 너무도 짧았고, 현재는 너무도 우울했으며, 미래는 너무도 불확실했으니까. 영원한 어둠 속에서 곱씹어야 할 빛이라곤 고작 열아홉 해뿐이었다! 어떤 위안도 그를 도우러 올 수 없었다. 그렇게 과거를 되짚으며 드높이 솟구쳤을 그의 활기찬 정신은, 새장에 갇힌 독수리처럼 갇혀 버렸다. 그는 한 가지 생각에 매달렸다. 아무런 분명한 까닭도 없이 전대미문의 운명에 짓밟힌 자신의 행복이라는 생각이었다. 그는 그 생각을 곱씹고 또 곱씹었으며, 말하자면 그것을 게걸스레 뜯어먹었다. 단테의 『지옥편』에서 무자비한 우골리노가 로제 대주교의 두개골을 뜯어먹듯이.\n\n종교적 열정의 자리를 분노가 차지했다. 당테스는 간수가 끔찍해하며 뒷걸음치게 만들 만한 신성모독의 말을 내뱉었고, 감방의 벽에 미친 듯이 제 몸을 부딪쳤으며, 닥치는 대로, 특히 제 자신에게 분풀이를 했다. 그래서 아주 사소한 것에도, 이를테면 모래 한 알, 지푸라기 한 가닥, 거슬리는 한 줄기 바람에도 발작적인 광기에 사로잡혔다. 그러다 빌포르가 보여 준 그 편지가 다시 떠올랐고, 한 줄 한 줄이 벨사살의 메네 메네 데겔 우바르신●●처럼 벽 위에 불타는 글자로 어른거렸다. 그는 자신을 이토록 깊은 비참 속으로 빠뜨린 것이 하늘의 복수가 아니라 인간의 적의였다고 되뇌었다. 그는 정체 모를 박해자들을 상상할 수 있는 가장 끔찍한 고문에 처했지만, 그 어느 것에도 만족하지 못했다. 고문 뒤에는 죽음이 오고, 죽음 뒤에는, 안식까지는 아니더라도 적어도 무의식이라는 은총이 따르기 때문이었다.\n\n평온함이 곧 죽음이고, 만일 형벌이 목적이라면 죽음 말고 다른 고문을 고안해 내야 한다는 생각을 끊임없이 곱씹은 끝에, 그는 자살을 떠올리기 시작했다. 불행하여라, 불행의 벼랑 끝에서 이런 생각에 잠기는 자여!\n\n그의 앞에는 푸르고 잔잔하게 펼쳐진 죽음의 바다가 눈앞에 놓여 있다. 그러나 무심코 그 품으로 뛰어든 자는 자신을 파멸로 끌어내리려는 괴물과 씨름하는 처지가 된다. 일단 그렇게 옭매이면, 하느님의 보호하는 손길이 그를 거기서 낚아채지 않는 한 모든 것이 끝나며, 발버둥 칠수록 파멸만 앞당길 뿐이다. 그러나 이 마음의 고뇌는 그에 앞서는 고통이나 뒤따를지도 모를 형벌보다는 덜 끔찍하다. 입을 떡 벌린 심연, 그 바닥에 어둠과 암흑이 깔린 그 심연을 바라보는 데에는 일종의 위안이 있다.\n\n에드몽은 이런 생각에서 얼마간 위안을 얻었다. 죽음의 천사가 막 들어서려는 듯하자, 그의 모든 슬픔과 모든 고통이, 음울한 망령들을 거느린 채 감방에서 달아났다. 당테스는 침착하게 지난 삶을 돌아보았고, 앞으로의 삶을 두려운 마음으로 내다보며, 자신에게 피난처가 되어 줄 듯한 그 중간의 길을 택했다.\n\n\"이따금,\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 한 사람의 남자로서 다른 사람들을 지휘하며 항해할 때, 하늘이 잔뜩 흐려지고 바다가 미쳐 날뛰며 거품을 일으키고, 폭풍이 일어 괴물 같은 새가 양쪽 수평선을 날개로 후려치는 광경을 보았지. 그럴 때면 나는 내 배가 부질없는 피난처임을, 폭풍 앞에서 떨고 흔들리는 피난처임을 느꼈다. 이내 성난 파도와 날카로운 바위가 죽음의 임박을 알렸고, 그때는 죽음이 나를 두렵게 했지. 그래서 나는 한 사람의 남자로서, 또 뱃사람으로서 가진 모든 솜씨와 지혜를 다해 하느님의 노여움에 맞서 싸웠다. 하지만 그렇게 한 것은 내가 행복했기 때문이고, 죽음을 청하지 않았기 때문이며, 바위와 해초로 된 잠자리에 내던져지는 것이 끔찍했기 때문이고, 하느님을 섬기기 위해 지어진 내가 갈매기와 까마귀의 먹이가 되는 것을 견딜 수 없었기 때문이지. 하지만 이제는 다르다. 나를 삶에 붙들어 매던 모든 것을 잃었고, 죽음이 미소 지으며 안식으로 나를 부르고 있어. 나는 내 방식대로 죽으리라. 감방을 삼천 번 맴돌고 나서 잠드는 것처럼, 기진맥진 넋이 꺾인 채로 죽으리라. 삼천 번이면 삼만 걸음, 그러니까 십 리외쯤 되는 길이지.\"\n\n이 생각이 그를 사로잡자마자, 그는 한결 차분해졌다. 그는 할 수 있는 한 잠자리를 정돈했고, 적게 먹고 더 적게 잤으며, 삶이 거의 견딜 만하다고 느꼈다. 다 낡은 옷처럼 마음 내키면 언제든 벗어 던질 수 있다고 느꼈기 때문이다. 스스로 목숨을 끊는 두 가지 방법이 그에게 있었다. 손수건으로 목을 매어 창살에 매달릴 수도 있었고, 음식을 거부해 굶어 죽을 수도 있었다. 그러나 첫 번째 방법은 그에게 역겨웠다. 당테스는 활대에 매달려 죽는 해적들에게 늘 더없는 혐오를 품어 왔다. 그는 치욕스러워 보이는 그런 죽음으로는 죽지 않으리라 했다. 그는 두 번째 방법을 택하기로 마음먹고, 그날부터 결심을 실행에 옮기기 시작했다.\n\n거의 사 년이 흘렀다. 둘째 해가 끝날 무렵 그는 세월의 흐름을 헤아리기를 그만두었다. 당테스는 \"죽고 싶다\"라고 말했고, 죽음의 방식을 골랐으며, 마음이 바뀔까 두려워 죽겠노라 맹세까지 했다. '아침과 저녁 끼니가 들어오면,' 그는 생각했다. '창밖으로 던져 버리리라. 그러면 저들은 내가 먹은 줄 알겠지.'\n\n그는 약속을 지켰다. 하루에 두 번, 그는 간수가 가져온 음식을 창살 사이로 던져 버렸다. 처음에는 즐거이, 다음에는 신중하게, 마침내는 미련을 품은 채로. 오로지 맹세에 대한 기억만이 그에게 계속할 힘을 주었다. 굶주림은 한때 역겹던 음식을 이제 군침 도는 것으로 만들었다. 그는 한 시간이고 접시를 손에 든 채, 질 나쁜 고기 한 점, 상한 생선, 검고 곰팡이 핀 빵 조각을 골똘히 바라보았다. 그것은 삶에 대한 마지막 갈망이 절망의 결심과 다투는 것이었다. 그럴 때면 지하 감방도 덜 음침해 보였고, 앞날도 덜 절망스러워 보였다. 그는 아직 젊었다. 고작 스물네다섯이었고, 살날이 오십 년 가까이 남아 있었다. 어떤 뜻밖의 일이 일어나 감방 문을 열고 그를 다시 자유의 몸으로 돌려보내 줄지도 모를 일이었다. 그러면 그는, 스스로를 가둔 탄탈로스처럼 제게 금했던 음식을 입가로 가져갔다. 그러나 맹세를 떠올리고는 그것을 깨려 하지 않았다. 그는 버티고 또 버텼고, 마침내는 일어나 저녁밥을 창살 틈으로 내던질 힘조차 남지 않았다. 이튿날 아침 그는 보지도 듣지도 못했다. 간수는 그가 위독한 줄 알고 겁을 냈다. 에드몽은 자기가 죽어 가는 것이기를 바랐다.\n\n그렇게 하루가 흘렀다. 에드몽은 일종의 혼미한 기운이 자신을 덮쳐 오는 것을 느꼈고, 그것은 거의 만족에 가까운 느낌을 함께 가져왔다. 속을 쥐어뜯던 통증은 멎었고, 갈증도 누그러졌다. 눈을 감으면 수많은 불빛이 눈앞에서 춤추는 것이 보였는데, 늪 위에서 노니는 도깨비불 같았다. 그것은 죽음이라 불리는 신비로운 나라의 여명이었다!\n\n그러던 어느 날 저녁 아홉 시쯤, 에드몽은 자신이 기대 누운 벽 안쪽에서 공허한 소리를 들었다.\n\n워낙 많은 흉측한 짐승들이 감옥에 깃들어 살아서, 그놈들의 소리는 대개 그를 깨우지 못했다. 그러나 굶주림이 그의 감각을 예민하게 했는지, 아니면 정말로 그 소리가 여느 때보다 컸는지, 에드몽은 고개를 들고 귀를 기울였다. 그것은 끊임없이 긁는 소리였다. 마치 거대한 발톱이나 강력한 이빨, 혹은 어떤 쇠붙이 연장이 돌을 파고드는 것 같았다.\n\n쇠약해진 몸이었지만, 젊은이의 머릿속에는 모든 죄수를 따라다니는 그 생각이 곧바로 떠올랐다. 자유! 마침내 하늘이 자기를 가엾이 여겨, 바로 심연의 벼랑 끝에서 그를 일깨우려고 이 소리를 보낸 것만 같았다. 어쩌면 그가 그토록 자주 떠올리던 사랑하는 이들 가운데 누군가가 그를 생각하며, 둘 사이를 가르는 거리를 좁히려 애쓰고 있는지도 몰랐다.\n\n아니, 아니, 분명 착각일 것이다. 그저 죽음에 앞서 찾아오는 그런 꿈 중 하나일 뿐이리라!\n\n에드몽은 여전히 그 소리를 들었다. 소리는 거의 세 시간을 이어졌다. 그러다 그는 무언가 떨어지는 소리를 들었고, 모든 것이 고요해졌다.\n\n몇 시간 뒤 소리가 다시 시작되었다. 더 가깝고 더 또렷했다. 에드몽은 온 신경을 곤두세웠다. 갑자기 간수가 들어왔다.\n\n죽기로 결심한 일주일 동안, 그리고 그 결심을 실행에 옮긴 나흘 동안, 에드몽은 간수에게 말을 걸지 않았고, 무슨 일이냐고 물어도 대답하지 않았으며, 간수가 너무 호기심 어린 눈으로 들여다보면 얼굴을 벽 쪽으로 돌렸다. 그러나 이제 간수가 그 소리를 듣고 멈추게 하기라도 하면, 그의 마지막 순간을 달래 주던 그 희망 비슷한 한 줄기 빛마저 사라질 판이었다.\n\n간수가 그에게 아침을 가져왔다. 당테스는 몸을 일으켜 이것저것 떠들기 시작했다. 음식의 질이 나쁘다느니, 감방이 춥다느니, 투덜대고 불평하며 더 크게 말할 핑계를 만들고, 간수의 인내심을 시험했다. 간수는 마음씨가 고운 사람이라 죄수를 위해 수프와 흰 빵을 가져온 터였다.\n\n다행히도 간수는 당테스가 헛소리를 하는 줄로만 알았다. 그는 음식을 흔들거리는 탁자에 놓고 물러갔다. 에드몽은 귀를 기울였고, 소리는 점점 더 또렷해졌다.\n\n'틀림없어.' 그는 생각했다. '자유를 찾으려고 애쓰는 어떤 죄수야. 아, 내가 거기 있어 도울 수만 있다면!'\n\n그러다 또 다른 생각이 그의 마음을 사로잡았다. 불행에 너무 익숙해져 좀처럼 희망을 품지 못하는 그의 마음을 말이다. 소장이 이웃 감방을 수리하라고 시킨 일꾼들이 내는 소리라는 생각이었다.\n\n이를 확인하기는 쉬웠다. 그러나 어떻게 그 위험을 무릅쓰고 물어본단 말인가? 간수에게 그 소리에 주의를 돌리게 하고, 그가 귀 기울이는 동안 표정을 살피기란 쉬운 일이었다. 그러나 그렇게 하다가는 한순간의 호기심을 채우는 보잘것없는 만족보다 훨씬 더 중요한 희망을 무너뜨릴 수도 있지 않은가? 안타깝게도 에드몽의 머리는 여전히 너무 흐릿해서 무엇 하나에도 생각을 집중할 수 없었다. 그는 판단력에 다시 또렷함과 명료함을 되찾을 방법이 단 하나뿐임을 알았다. 그는 간수가 가져온 수프 쪽으로 눈길을 돌렸고, 일어나 비틀비틀 그쪽으로 다가가, 그릇을 입가로 들어 올려 형언할 수 없는 기쁨을 느끼며 내용물을 들이켰다.\n\n그는 거기서 멈추는 결단력을 발휘했다. 그는 난파당한 사람들이 음식을 게걸스레 너무 많이 먹다가 죽었다는 이야기를 자주 들어 왔다. 에드몽은 막 삼키려던 빵을 다시 탁자에 내려놓고 잠자리로 돌아왔다. 그는 죽고 싶지 않았다. 곧 그는 생각이 다시 가다듬어지는 것을 느꼈다. 이제 사고할 수 있었고, 추론으로 생각에 힘을 실을 수 있었다. 그러고는 스스로에게 말했다.\n\n\"이걸 시험해 봐야 해. 하지만 누구도 위험에 빠뜨리지 않으면서. 일꾼이라면 벽을 두드리기만 하면 돼. 그러면 누가 왜 두드리는지 알아보려고 일을 멈추겠지. 하지만 그 일은 소장이 허락한 것이니 곧 다시 시작할 거야. 반대로 죄수라면, 내가 내는 소리에 놀라 멈출 테고, 다들 잠들었다고 여겨질 때까지 다시 시작하지 않겠지.\"\n\n에드몽은 다시 일어났다. 그러나 이번에는 다리가 떨리지 않았고 시야도 또렷했다. 그는 감방 구석으로 가서 돌 하나를 떼어 냈고, 그것으로 소리가 나는 쪽 벽을 두드렸다. 세 번 두드렸다.\n\n첫 번째 타격에 소리가 마치 마법처럼 멎었다.\n\n에드몽은 온 신경을 곤두세워 귀를 기울였다. 한 시간이 흘렀고, 두 시간이 흘렀으나, 벽에서는 아무 소리도 들리지 않았다. 그곳은 온통 고요했다.\n\n희망에 부푼 에드몽은 빵과 물을 몇 입 삼켰고, 타고난 튼튼한 체질 덕분에 거의 회복된 자신을 느꼈다.\n\n그날 하루는 완전한 침묵 속에 지나갔고, 밤이 와도 그 소리는 다시 들리지 않았다.\n\n\"죄수야.\" 에드몽이 기쁨에 차서 말했다. 그의 머리는 불타올랐고, 생기와 기운이 돌아왔다.\n\n밤은 완벽한 고요 속에 지나갔다. 에드몽은 눈을 붙이지 않았다.\n\n아침에 간수가 새 음식을 가져왔다. 그는 전날 것을 이미 게걸스레 먹어 치운 뒤였다. 그는 음식을 먹으면서 그 소리가 다시 나는지 애타게 귀를 기울였고, 감방을 빙빙 돌았으며, 창살을 흔들고, 운동으로 팔다리에 다시 힘과 민첩함을 불어넣어 앞으로의 운명에 대비했다. 틈틈이 그는 소리가 다시 시작되지 않았는지 귀를 기울였고, 자기 못지않게 자유를 갈망하는 죄수가 자기를 방해한 것임을 알아채지 못한 채 너무도 신중하기만 한 그에게 조바심을 냈다.\n\n사흘이 흘렀다. 분 단위로 헤아린 일흔두 시간, 길고 지루한 시간이었다!\n\n마침내 어느 날 저녁, 간수가 그날 밤의 마지막 순회를 도는 참에, 당테스는 백 번째로 벽에 귀를 댄 채 돌들 사이에서 거의 알아챌 수 없는 움직임을 들은 것 같았다. 그는 자리를 옮겨 감방을 오르락내리락하며 생각을 가다듬었고, 그런 뒤 다시 돌아와 귀를 기울였다.\n\n이제는 의심의 여지가 없었다. 벽 저편에서 무언가가 작업 중이었다. 죄수는 위험을 알아채고는 끌 대신 지렛대를 쓰고 있었다.\n\n이 발견에 용기를 얻은 에드몽은 지칠 줄 모르는 그 일꾼을 돕기로 마음먹었다. 그는 우선 침대를 옮기고, 벽을 뚫고 축축한 회반죽을 파고들어 돌 하나를 빼낼 만한 것이 없는지 둘러보았다.\n\n아무것도 눈에 띄지 않았다. 칼도 날카로운 연장도 없었고, 창살은 쇠로 되어 있었지만 그 단단함은 이미 너무도 자주 확인한 터였다. 그의 가구라곤 침대, 의자, 탁자, 양동이, 물주전자가 전부였다. 침대에는 쇠 죔쇠가 달려 있었으나 나무에 나사로 박혀 있어, 그것을 떼어 내려면 드라이버가 필요했다. 탁자와 의자에는 아무것도 없었고, 양동이에는 한때 손잡이가 있었지만 그것마저 떼어 낸 뒤였다.\n\n당테스에게 남은 방도는 단 하나, 물주전자를 깨뜨려 그 날카로운 조각 하나로 벽을 공략하는 것이었다. 그는 물주전자를 바닥에 떨어뜨렸고, 주전자는 산산조각이 났다.\n\n당테스는 가장 날카로운 조각 두세 개를 침대에 감추고 나머지는 바닥에 그대로 두었다. 물주전자가 깨진 것은 너무도 자연스러운 사고라 의심을 사지 않을 터였다. 에드몽에게는 밤새 일할 시간이 있었지만, 어둠 속에서는 별로 손을 쓰지 못했고, 곧 무언가 아주 단단한 것에 부딪혀 일하고 있음을 느꼈다. 그는 침대를 도로 밀어 놓고 날이 밝기를 기다렸다.\n\n밤새 그는 땅속의 일꾼이 굴을 파 나가는 소리를 들었다. 날이 밝자 간수가 들어왔다. 당테스는 물을 마시다 주전자를 손에서 떨어뜨렸다고 둘러댔고, 간수는 투덜대며 새것을 가지러 갔다. 깨진 조각들을 치우는 수고는 하지 않은 채였다. 그는 금세 돌아와 죄수에게 더 조심하라고 이르고는 떠났다.\n\n당테스는 자물쇠에서 열쇠가 돌아가는 소리를 기쁘게 들었다. 그는 발소리가 멀어져 사라질 때까지 귀를 기울였고, 그런 뒤 서둘러 침대를 옮겨, 감방으로 스며드는 희미한 빛에 의지해 깨달았다. 전날 저녁 자신이 돌을 에워싼 회반죽을 걷어 내는 대신 돌 자체를 공략하느라 헛수고를 했다는 것을.\n\n습기 탓에 회반죽은 푸석푸석해져 있었고, 당테스는 그것을 부숴 낼 수 있었다. 비록 작은 조각으로였지만, 반 시간 만에 그는 한 줌을 긁어냈다. 수학자라면, 만일 암반에 부딪히지 않는다고 가정할 때, 이 년이면 길이 스무 자에 너비 두 자짜리 통로를 낼 수 있으리라 계산했을 것이다.\n\n죄수는 부질없는 희망과 기도와 낙담으로 흘려보낸 시간을 이렇게 쓰지 못한 것을 자책했다. 갇혀 지낸 육 년 동안, 그는 무엇인들 이루지 못했겠는가?\n\n이 생각이 그에게 새로운 기운을 불어넣었고, 사흘 만에 그는 더없이 신중하게 회반죽을 걷어 내 돌 쌓은 부분을 드러내는 데 성공했다. 벽은 거친 돌로 쌓여 있었고, 그 사이사이에 구조를 튼튼히 하려고 다듬은 돌덩이들이 박혀 있었다. 그가 드러낸 것은 그 돌덩이 가운데 하나였고, 그것을 박힌 자리에서 빼내야 했다.\n\n당테스는 손톱으로 그 일을 하려 했으나, 손톱은 너무 약했다. 주전자 조각들은 부러졌고, 한 시간의 헛된 노고 끝에 당테스는 이마에 괴로움을 띤 채 손을 멈췄다.\n\n이렇게 첫걸음에서 가로막혀, 동료 일꾼이 제 일을 끝낼 때까지 손 놓고 기다려야 한단 말인가? 갑자기 한 가지 생각이 떠올랐다. 그는 미소를 지었고, 이마의 땀이 말라 갔다.\n\n간수는 늘 쇠로 된 냄비에 당테스의 수프를 담아 왔다. 이 냄비에는 두 죄수 몫의 수프가 들어 있었으니, 당테스는 간수가 자기에게 먼저 주느냐 동무에게 먼저 주느냐에 따라 냄비가 가득 차 있거나 절반쯤 비어 있는 것을 눈여겨봐 둔 터였다.\n\n이 냄비의 손잡이는 쇠로 되어 있었다. 당테스라면 그것과 맞바꾸기 위해 제 목숨의 십 년이라도 내놓았으리라.\n\n간수는 냄비의 내용물을 당테스의 접시에 따라 주곤 했고, 당테스는 나무 숟가락으로 수프를 먹은 뒤 접시를 씻어 날마다 같은 접시를 썼다. 그런데 저녁이 되자 당테스는 접시를 문 가까이 바닥에 놓았다. 간수는 들어오다 그것을 밟아 깨뜨렸다.\n\n이번에는 간수도 당테스를 탓할 수 없었다. 거기에 접시를 둔 것은 당테스의 잘못이었으나, 앞을 살피지 않은 것은 간수의 잘못이었다. 그래서 간수는 그저 투덜대기만 했다. 그러고는 수프를 따를 만한 것을 찾아 두리번거렸다. 당테스의 식기라곤 접시 하나가 전부였으니, 달리 방법이 없었다.\n\n\"냄비를 두고 가시오.\" 당테스가 말했다. \"아침을 가져올 때 도로 가져가면 되잖소.\"\n\n이 충고는 간수의 마음에 들었다. 다시 한 번 걸음할 수고를 덜어 주었기 때문이다. 그는 냄비를 두고 갔다.\n\n당테스는 기쁨에 어쩔 줄 몰랐다. 그는 음식을 후딱 먹어 치웠고, 간수가 마음을 바꿔 되돌아올까 봐 한 시간을 기다린 뒤, 침대를 치우고 냄비 손잡이를 집어 들었다. 그는 그 끝을 다듬은 돌과 거친 돌 사이에 끼워 넣고 지렛대로 썼다. 약간 흔들리는 것을 보고 당테스는 일이 잘되어 가고 있음을 알았다. 한 시간 만에 돌이 벽에서 빠져나왔고, 지름 한 자 반쯤 되는 구멍이 생겼다.\n\n당테스는 회반죽을 조심스레 그러모아 감방 구석으로 날라다 흙으로 덮었다. 그러고는 일할 수단이 있는 동안 시간을 한껏 쓰고 싶어, 쉬지 않고 작업을 이어 갔다. 동틀 무렵 그는 돌을 도로 끼워 넣고, 침대를 벽에 밀어붙인 뒤 누웠다. 아침은 빵 한 조각이었다. 간수가 들어와 빵을 탁자에 놓았다.\n\n\"이런, 접시를 새로 가져다줄 생각은 없소?\" 당테스가 물었다.\n\n\"없소.\" 간수가 대답했다. \"당신은 죄다 부숴 버리잖소. 처음엔 주전자를 깨더니, 이번엔 나더러 접시를 깨게 만들고. 죄수들이 죄다 당신처럼 굴면 나라가 거덜 나겠소. 냄비를 두고 갈 테니 거기다 수프를 따라 먹으시오. 앞으로는 그렇게 살림을 거덜 내지 않기를 바라오.\"\n\n당테스는 하늘로 눈을 들었고, 이불 밑에서 두 손을 맞잡았다. 그는 이 쇳조각 하나를 손에 넣은 것에, 여태껏 그 무엇에도 느껴 본 적 없는 깊은 감사를 느꼈다. 그러나 그는 벽 저편의 죄수가 작업을 멈췄음을 알아챈 터였다. 그래도 상관없었다. 오히려 그것은 계속해야 할 더 큰 이유였다. 이웃이 그에게 오지 않는다면, 그가 이웃에게 가면 될 일이었다. 그는 온종일 지칠 줄 모르고 일했고, 저녁이 되자 회반죽과 돌 부스러기를 열 줌이나 긁어내는 데 성공했다. 간수가 들를 시간이 되자, 당테스는 냄비 손잡이를 할 수 있는 한 곧게 펴서 제자리에 두었다. 간수는 거기에 수프를 따라 주었고, 생선도 곁들였다. 일주일에 세 번은 죄수들에게 고기가 주어지지 않았기 때문이다. 이것은 시간을 헤아리는 방법이 될 수도 있었으나, 당테스는 이미 오래전에 그러기를 그만둔 터였다. 수프를 따라 준 뒤 간수는 물러갔다.\n\n당테스는 이웃이 정말로 작업을 그만두었는지 확인하고 싶었다. 그는 귀를 기울였다. 지난 사흘 내내 그랬듯이 모든 것이 고요했다. 당테스는 한숨을 내쉬었다. 이웃이 자기를 믿지 못하는 것이 분명했다. 그래도 그는 낙담하지 않고 밤새 일을 이어 갔다. 그러나 두세 시간 뒤 장애물에 부딪혔다. 쇠가 아무런 자국도 내지 못한 채 매끈한 표면에 막혔다. 당테스가 만져 보니, 그것은 들보였다. 이 들보가 당테스가 뚫어 놓은 구멍을 가로질러, 아니 가로막고 있었다. 그러니 그 위나 아래를 파야 했다. 가엾은 젊은이는 이런 것까지는 생각하지 못했다.\n\n\"오, 하느님, 하느님!\" 그가 중얼거렸다. \"이토록 간절히 당신께 기도드렸으니, 제 기도가 닿았으리라 믿었습니다. 제게서 자유를 앗아 가시고, 죽음마저 앗아 가시고, 다시 삶으로 불러내셨으니, 하느님, 저를 가엾이 여기시어 절망 속에 죽게 내버려 두지 마소서!\"\n\n\"누가 하느님과 절망을 한꺼번에 입에 올리는가?\" 땅속에서 들려오는 듯한 목소리가 말했다. 거리에 짓눌린 그 소리는 젊은이의 귀에 공허하고 무덤 같은 울림으로 닿았다. 에드몽은 머리카락이 곤두섰고, 무릎을 꿇고 몸을 일으켰다.\n\n\"아.\" 그가 말했다. \"사람의 목소리가 들린다.\" 에드몽은 사오 년 동안 간수 말고는 누구의 말도 들어 본 적이 없었다. 그리고 간수란 죄수에게 사람이 아니다. 그는 살아 있는 문이요, 참나무와 쇠의 구속에 힘을 더하는 살과 피의 장벽일 뿐이다.\n\n\"하늘에 맹세코,\" 당테스가 외쳤다. \"다시 말해 주시오. 당신 목소리가 나를 떨게 할지언정. 당신은 누구요?\"\n\n\"당신은 누구요?\" 목소리가 물었다.\n\n\"불행한 죄수요.\" 당테스가 망설임 없이 대답했다.\n\n\"어느 나라 사람이오?\"\n\n\"프랑스인이오.\"\n\n\"이름은?\"\n\n\"에드몽 당테스요.\"\n\n\"직업은?\"\n\n\"뱃사람이오.\"\n\n\"여기 온 지는 얼마나 됐소?\"\n\n\"1815년 2월 28일부터요.\"\n\n\"죄목은?\"\n\n\"나는 무죄요.\"\n\n\"하지만 무슨 죄로 고발당했소?\"\n\n\"황제의 귀환을 도우려고 음모를 꾸몄다는 죄로요.\"\n\n\"뭐라고! 황제의 귀환을? 그럼 황제는 이제 왕좌에 계시지 않단 말이오?\"\n\n\"1814년 퐁텐블로에서 퇴위하시고 엘바섬으로 보내지셨소. 한데 당신은 여기 온 지 얼마나 됐기에 이 모든 걸 모른단 말이오?\"\n\n\"1811년부터요.\"\n\n당테스는 몸서리쳤다. 이 사람은 자기보다 사 년이나 더 오래 갇혀 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"더는 파지 마시오.\" 목소리가 말했다. \"당신이 판 굴이 얼마나 높은 데 있는지만 말해 주시오.\"\n\n\"바닥과 같은 높이요.\"\n\n\"어떻게 감춰 놨소?\"\n\n\"침대 뒤에요.\"\n\n\"갇힌 뒤로 침대를 옮긴 적이 있소?\"\n\n\"없소.\"\n\n\"당신 방은 어디로 통하오?\"\n\n\"복도로요.\"\n\n\"복도는?\"\n\n\"안뜰로요.\"\n\n\"아아!\" 목소리가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"오, 무슨 일이오?\" 당테스가 외쳤다.\n\n\"설계가 틀려서 실수를 했소. 각도를 잘못 잡아, 의도한 곳에서 열다섯 자나 빗나가 나왔구려. 당신이 파던 그 벽을 요새의 바깥벽으로 여겼던 거요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그렇다면 당신은 바다 가까이에 있는 거 아니오?\"\n\n\"그게 내가 바라던 바였소.\"\n\n\"그래서 만일 성공했다면?\"\n\n\"바다에 몸을 던져, 이 근처 섬 가운데 하나에, 돔섬이나 티불랑섬에 닿았을 거요. 그러면 안전했겠지.\"\n\n\"그렇게 멀리까지 헤엄칠 수 있었겠소?\"\n\n\"하늘이 내게 힘을 주셨겠지. 하지만 이제 모든 게 끝났소.\"\n\n\"모든 게요?\"\n\"그렇소. 당신이 판 굴을 조심스레 막고, 더는 일하지 말고, 내게서 소식이 올 때까지 기다리시오.\"\n\n\"적어도 당신이 누구인지는 말해 주시오.\"\n\n\"나는, 나는 27호요.\"\n\n\"그럼 나를 못 믿는 거구려.\" 당테스가 말했다. 에드몽은 그 깊은 곳에서 씁쓸한 웃음이 울려오는 것을 들은 듯했다.\n\n\"오, 나는 기독교인이오.\" 당테스가 외쳤다. 이 사람이 자기를 저버리려 한다는 것을 본능적으로 알아챘던 것이다. \"우리를 위해 돌아가신 분께 맹세하건대, 그 무엇도 나를 부추겨 간수들에게 한 마디도 흘리게 하지 못할 거요. 하지만 부디 나를 버리지 마시오. 만일 버린다면, 맹세코 말하지만, 나는 이미 기력이 다했으니, 이 벽에 머리를 박살 내 버릴 거요. 그러면 당신은 내 죽음을 두고 스스로를 책망하게 될 거요.\"\n\n\"나이가 몇이오? 목소리를 들으니 젊은 사람 같은데.\"\n\n\"내 나이를 모르겠소. 여기 온 뒤로 햇수를 세지 않았으니까. 다만 아는 건, 1815년 2월 28일에 붙잡혔을 때 막 열아홉이었다는 것뿐이오.\"\n\n\"채 스물여섯도 안 됐군!\" 목소리가 중얼거렸다. \"그 나이라면 배신자일 리 없지.\"\n\n\"오, 아니오, 아니오.\" 당테스가 외쳤다. \"다시 맹세하건대, 당신을 배신하느니 차라리 내 몸이 갈가리 찢기는 편을 택하겠소!\"\n\n\"내게 말을 걸고 도움을 청한 건 잘한 일이오. 나는 막 다른 계획을 세워 당신을 떠나려던 참이었으니까. 하지만 당신의 나이가 나를 안심시키는구려. 당신을 잊지 않으리다. 기다리시오.\"\n\n\"얼마나요?\"\n\n\"우리의 가능성을 따져 봐야 하오. 내가 신호를 보내리다.\"\n\n\"하지만 나를 떠나지는 마시오. 당신이 내게로 오든지, 아니면 내가 당신에게 가게 해 주시오. 우리는 탈출할 거요. 탈출하지 못한다면 이야기라도 나눕시다. 당신은 당신이 사랑하는 이들에 대해, 나는 내가 사랑하는 이들에 대해. 당신도 누군가를 사랑하겠지요?\"\n\n\"아니오, 나는 세상에 혼자요.\"\n\n\"그럼 당신은 나를 사랑하게 될 거요. 당신이 젊다면 내가 당신의 벗이 되겠소. 당신이 늙었다면 내가 당신의 아들이 되겠소. 내겐 아버지가 계신데, 살아 계신다면 일흔이오. 나는 오직 그분과, 메르세데스라는 젊은 처녀만을 사랑하오. 아버지는 아직 나를 잊지 않으셨을 거요, 분명히. 하지만 그녀가 여전히 나를 사랑하는지는 하느님만이 아실 일이오. 나는 아버지를 사랑하듯 당신을 사랑하겠소.\"\n\n\"좋소.\" 목소리가 대답했다. \"내일.\"\n\n이 몇 마디는 그의 진심을 의심할 여지 없는 어조로 발음되었다. 당테스는 일어나 전과 같은 조심성으로 부스러기를 흩어 놓고, 침대를 벽으로 도로 밀어붙였다. 그러고는 행복에 자신을 내맡겼다. 그는 더 이상 혼자가 아니리라. 어쩌면 자유를 되찾으려는 참인지도 몰랐다. 최악의 경우라도 동무가 생길 터였고, 함께 나누는 갇힘이란 절반의 갇힘에 지나지 않았다. 함께 드리는 탄식은 거의 기도나 다름없고, 두셋이 모여 드리는 기도는 하늘의 자비를 불러온다.\n\n당테스는 온종일 감방을 오르락내리락했다. 이따금 침대에 앉아 가슴에 손을 얹었다. 작은 소리에도 그는 문 쪽으로 튀어 갔다. 한두 번은 이미 사랑하게 된 그 미지의 사람과 떨어지게 될지도 모른다는 생각이 머리를 스쳤다. 그러자 그는 마음을 굳혔다. 간수가 침대를 옮기고 몸을 숙여 그 구멍을 살피려 들면, 물주전자로 그를 죽이리라. 그러면 사형을 선고받겠지만, 그는 이 기적 같은 소리가 그를 다시 삶으로 불러내기 전까지는 슬픔과 절망으로 죽어 가던 참이었다.\n\n저녁에 간수가 들어왔다. 당테스는 침대에 누워 있었다. 그렇게 하면 끝나지 않은 구멍을 더 잘 지킬 수 있을 것 같았다. 분명 그의 눈빛에 묘한 기색이 있었던지, 간수가 말했다. \"이봐, 또 정신이 나가려는 거요?\"\n\n당테스는 대답하지 않았다. 목소리에 묻어날 격한 감정이 자신을 드러낼까 두려웠다. 간수는 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 떠났다. 밤이 왔다. 당테스는 이웃이 이 고요를 틈타 자기에게 말을 걸어 주기를 바랐으나, 그것은 착각이었다. 그러나 이튿날 아침, 막 침대를 벽에서 떼어 놓는 참에, 그는 똑똑똑 세 번 두드리는 소리를 들었다. 그는 무릎을 꿇었다.\n\n\"당신이오?\" 그가 말했다. \"여기 있소.\"\n\n\"당신 간수는 갔소?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\" 당테스가 말했다. \"저녁까지는 돌아오지 않을 거요. 그러니 우리에겐 열두 시간이 있소.\"\n\n\"그럼 일해도 되겠소?\" 목소리가 물었다.\n\n\"오, 그럼, 그럼이오. 지금 당장, 부디 부탁하오.\"\n\n순간, 당테스가 머리를 구멍에 들이민 채 무릎을 꿇고 두 손을 짚고 있던 바닥의 그 부분이 갑자기 푹 꺼졌다. 그가 잽싸게 물러서는 사이, 돌과 흙더미가 그 자신이 뚫어 놓은 구멍 아래로 열린 구덩이 속으로 사라졌다. 그러더니 깊이를 가늠할 길 없는 그 통로 밑바닥에서, 먼저 머리가, 다음에 어깨가, 마지막으로 한 사내의 몸이 나타나는 것을 그는 보았다. 사내는 가뿐히 그의 감방 안으로 뛰어들었다.\n\n# Chapter 16. A Learned Italian\n\n오랫동안 간절히 바라던 친구를 두 팔로 끌어안은 당테스는 그를 거의 안다시피 창가로 데려갔다. 쇠창살 사이로 비쳐 드는 희미한 빛에 의지해서나마 그의 얼굴을 좀 더 잘 보기 위해서였다.\n\n오래도록 애타게 바라던 친구를 두 팔로 끌어안은 당테스는, 창살 사이로 비쳐 드는 희미한 빛에 기대어 그 얼굴을 좀 더 잘 보려고 노인을 거의 안다시피 하여 창가로 데려갔다.\n키가 작은 사내였다. 머리카락은 나이보다는 고통과 슬픔으로 하얗게 세어 있었다. 짙은 잿빛 눈썹 아래 거의 파묻히다시피 한 눈은 움푹 들어가 있으면서도 꿰뚫는 듯했고, 길고 여전히 검은 수염은 가슴까지 늘어져 있었다. 근심으로 깊게 골이 팬 야윈 얼굴과 뚜렷하게 새겨진 이목구비의 대담한 윤곽은, 육체의 힘보다 정신의 능력을 단련하는 데 더 익숙한 사람임을 말해 주었다. 지금 그의 이마에는 굵은 땀방울이 맺혀 있었고, 몸에 걸친 옷은 어찌나 누더기인지 본래 어떤 모양으로 지어진 것인지 짐작만 할 수 있을 따름이었다.\n\n낯선 사내는 예순이나 예순다섯쯤 되어 보였다. 그러나 동작에 어딘가 민첩하고 활기찬 데가 있어, 세월보다는 감금 생활 탓에 더 늙어 보이는 것이라 짐작케 했다. 그는 젊은 친구의 열렬한 인사를 분명 기쁜 마음으로 받아들였다. 얼어붙어 있던 그의 애정이, 이토록 따뜻하고 뜨거운 사람과 닿아 다시 불붙고 되살아나는 듯했다. 그는 따뜻한 환대에 고마운 마음을 담아 진심으로 감사를 표했다. 자유를 되찾을 길을 발견하리라 굳게 믿었던 곳에서 또 다른 감방을 발견했으니, 그 순간 그는 분명 쓰라린 고통을 느끼고 있었을 텐데도 그러했다.\n\n\"우선,\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 여기 들어온 흔적을 지울 수 있을지부터 봅시다. 앞으로 우리가 평온하게 지낼 수 있느냐는, 간수들이 이 일을 전혀 모르게 하는 데 달려 있으니까요.\"\n\n그가 구멍 쪽으로 다가가 몸을 굽히더니, 무거운데도 손쉽게 돌을 들어 올렸다. 그러고는 그것을 제자리에 끼워 맞추며 말했다.\n\n\"이 돌을 꽤 허술하게 들어냈군요. 하지만 도와줄 연장이 없었을 테니 그럴 만하지요.\"\n\n\"아니,\" 당테스가 놀라 소리쳤다. \"선생님은 연장을 가지고 계십니까?\"\n\n\"몇 개 만들었지요. 줄칼만 빼면 필요한 건 다 있습니다. 끌과 집게, 그리고 지렛대요.\"\n\n\"아, 선생님의 그 솜씨와 인내가 빚어낸 물건들을 어찌나 보고 싶은지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 우선 이게 내 끌이오.\"\n\n그러면서 그는 너도밤나무로 손잡이를 댄, 날카롭고 튼튼한 날을 보여 주었다.\n\n\"그런데 그걸 무엇으로 만드셨습니까?\" 당테스가 물었다.\n\n\"내 침대 틀의 꺾쇠 하나로 만들었지요. 바로 이 연장 하나로 여기까지 오는 길을 파냈습니다. 한 오십 피트쯤 되는 거리지요.\"\n\n\"오십 피트요!\" 당테스가 거의 겁에 질린 듯 되물었다.\n\n\"그렇게 큰 소리로 말하지 마시오, 젊은이. 큰 소리로 말하면 안 돼요. 이런 국사범 감옥에서는, 죄수들이 나누는 이야기를 엿들으려고 일부러 감방 문밖에 사람을 세워 두는 일이 흔하니까요.\"\n\n\"하지만 사람들은 제가 여기 혼자 갇혀 있다고 알고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그래도 마찬가지요.\"\n\n\"그러니까 여기까지 오시려고 오십 피트나 길을 파셨다는 말씀입니까?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 당신 방과 내 방을 갈라놓은 거리가 그쯤 되지요. 다만 운 나쁘게도 방향을 제대로 굽히지 못했어요. 비례를 가늠할 기하 도구가 없는 탓에, 사십 피트짜리 타원으로 잡았어야 할 것을 오십 피트로 만들고 만 거지요. 아까 말했듯이, 나는 바깥벽까지 닿아 그 벽을 뚫고 바다로 몸을 던질 작정이었소. 그런데 당신 방으로 통하는 복도 밑을 지나가야 했을 것을, 그 복도를 따라 나란히 파 버린 셈이오. 내 노고가 다 헛것이 된 게, 알고 보니 그 복도가 군인들로 가득한 안마당을 면하고 있더란 말입니다.\"\n\n\"맞습니다.\" 당테스가 말했다. \"하지만 선생님이 말씀하시는 그 복도는 제 감방의 한쪽 면만 막고 있을 뿐입니다. 나머지 세 면이 더 있는데, 그 사정에 대해서는 뭔가 아시는 게 있습니까?\"\n\n\"이쪽은 단단한 바위에 기대어 세워졌으니, 노련한 광부 열 명이 제대로 된 연장을 갖추고 달려들어도 뚫는 데 십 년은 걸릴 거요. 이쪽은 소장 관저의 아래층에 잇닿아 있어서, 뚫고 나간다 해도 잠긴 지하 창고 따위로 들어가게 될 테고, 그러면 영락없이 다시 붙잡히고 말지요. 당신 감방의 네 번째, 마지막 면은 어딜 향하고 있느냐 하면, 어딜 향하고 있느냐 하면, 잠깐만, 가만, 그게 어딜 향하고 있더라?\"\n\n그가 말하는 벽은, 방 안으로 빛이 들어오는 총안이 뚫려 있는 그 벽이었다. 이 총안은 바깥으로 갈수록 차츰 좁아져, 끝에 가서는 어린아이도 빠져나갈 수 없을 만한 구멍이 되어 있었다. 게다가 더 확실히 하려고 세 개의 쇠창살까지 박아 두어, 아무리 의심 많은 간수라 해도 죄수가 탈출할 수 있으리라는 염려 따위는 깨끗이 거둘 만했다. 낯선 사내는 그렇게 물으면서 탁자를 창문 밑으로 끌어다 놓았다.\n\n\"올라가 보시오.\" 그가 당테스에게 말했다.\n\n젊은이는 시키는 대로 탁자 위로 올라갔다. 그러고는 동료의 뜻을 헤아려, 등을 벽에 단단히 기대고 두 손을 내밀었다. 아직 당테스가 감방 번호로만 알고 있던 그 낯선 사내는, 그 나이로는 도무지 기대하기 어려운 날렵함으로 뛰어올랐다. 고양이나 도마뱀처럼 가볍고 안정된 몸놀림으로 탁자에서 당테스가 내민 두 손으로, 다시 거기서 그의 어깨로 기어올랐다. 그러고는 천장이 낮아 똑바로 설 수 없었기에 몸을 반으로 굽힌 채, 머리를 창문 위쪽 창살 사이로 밀어 넣어 위에서 아래까지 환히 내다볼 수 있게 했다.\n\n잠시 뒤 그는 황급히 머리를 빼며 \"그럴 줄 알았어!\" 하고는, 올라갈 때처럼 능숙하게 당테스의 어깨에서 미끄러져 내려와 탁자에서 바닥으로 가뿐히 뛰어내렸다.\n\n\"무엇을 그럴 줄 아셨단 말입니까?\" 이번에는 당테스가 탁자에서 내려오며 불안한 듯 물었다.\n\n나이 든 죄수는 그 문제를 곰곰이 따져 보았다. \"그래,\" 마침내 그가 말했다. \"그렇군. 당신 방의 이쪽 면은 일종의 트인 회랑을 향하고 있소. 거기로는 순찰병이 끊임없이 오가고, 보초가 밤낮으로 망을 보지요.\"\n\n\"그게 정말 확실합니까?\"\n\n\"확실하오. 병사의 형체와 머스킷 총 끝이 보였어요. 그래서 그렇게 얼른 머리를 뺀 거요. 그자 역시 나를 볼까 봐 겁이 났으니까.\"\n\n\"그래서요?\" 당테스가 물었다.\n\n\"그러니 당신 감방을 통해 탈출하기란 도저히 불가능하다는 걸 알겠지요?\"\n\n\"그렇다면……\" 젊은이가 다급하게 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 나이 든 죄수가 대답했다. \"하느님의 뜻이 이루어지기를 바랄밖에!\" 노인이 그 말을 천천히 내뱉는 동안, 근심에 찌든 그의 얼굴에는 깊은 체념의 빛이 번졌다. 그토록 오래도록 애타게 키워 온 희망을 이처럼 의연하게 내려놓을 수 있는 사람을, 당테스는 경탄과 놀라움이 뒤섞인 눈으로 바라보았다.\n\n\"부디 말씀해 주십시오, 선생님은 대체 누구시고 무얼 하시던 분입니까?\" 마침내 그가 입을 열었다. \"이제껏 선생님처럼 비범한 분은 만나 본 적이 없습니다.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 말해 드리지요.\" 낯선 사내가 대답했다. \"이제는, 아아, 당신에게 아무런 도움도 줄 수 없게 된 사람에 대해 정말로 궁금한 게 있다면 말이오.\"\n\n\"그렇게 말씀하지 마십시오. 선생님은 그 강인한 정신의 힘으로 저를 위로하고 떠받쳐 주실 수 있습니다. 부디 선생님이 진정 누구신지 알려 주십시오.\"\n\n낯선 사내는 우수에 찬 미소를 지었다. \"그럼 들어 보시오.\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 파리아 신부요. 당신도 알다시피 1811년부터 이 이프성에 갇혀 있었지요. 그 전에는 페네스트렐 요새에 삼 년 동안 갇혀 있었고요. 1811년에 나는 피에몬테에 있다가 프랑스로 이송되었소. 나폴레옹이 품은 온갖 소망에 고분고분 따르는 듯하던 운명이, 그에게 아들 하나를 점지해 주어 요람에서부터 로마 왕이라 불리게 했다는 소식을 들은 게 바로 그 무렵이었지요. 그때만 해도 나는 방금 당신이 알려 준 그 변화를, 그러니까 사 년 뒤에 그 권력의 거인이 무너지리라고는 도무지 예상하지 못했소. 그래, 지금 프랑스를 다스리는 게 누구요, 나폴레옹 2세입니까?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 루이 18세입니다.\"\n\n\"루이 16세의 아우 말이오! 섭리의 길이란 얼마나 헤아릴 수 없는 것인지. 한때 그토록 높이 올랐던 사람을 낮추고, 그토록 낮았던 사람을 들어 올리신 것은, 하늘께서 대체 어떤 크고 신비로운 뜻을 품으셨기에 그러신 것일까?\"\n\n남의 운명에 골몰하느라 자기 자신의 불행마저 잊을 수 있는 이 사람에게, 당테스의 온 신경이 쏠렸다.\n\n\"그래, 그래.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"영국에서 그러했던 것과 똑같이 되겠지요. 찰스 1세 다음에 크롬웰, 크롬웰 다음에 찰스 2세, 그다음엔 제임스 2세, 그러고는 어떤 사위든 친척이든, 어느 오라녜 공이라는 자가, 총독 자리에 있던 자가 왕이 되는 게요. 그러고 나면 백성에게 새로운 양보가 베풀어지고, 이어서 헌법이, 그다음엔 자유가 오는 거지요. 아, 친구여!\" 신부가 당테스를 돌아보며 예언자의 타오르는 눈빛으로 그를 살피며 말했다. \"당신은 젊으니, 이 모든 일이 일어나는 걸 보게 될 거요.\"\n\n\"제가 이 감옥을 빠져나갈 수만 있다면, 아마 그렇겠지요!\"\n\n\"맞는 말이오.\" 파리아가 대답했다. \"우리는 죄수지요. 하지만 나는 가끔 그걸 잊는다오. 심지어 마음의 눈이 나를 이 벽 너머로 데려가서, 내가 자유로운 몸인 양 여겨지는 순간마저 있지요.\"\n\n\"그런데 선생님은 어쩌다 여기 들어오시게 되었습니까?\"\n\n\"1807년에 나는 나폴레옹이 1811년에 실현하려던 바로 그 계획을 꿈꾸었기 때문이오. 마키아벨리처럼, 나는 이탈리아의 정치 지형을 바꾸고 싶었소. 약하거나 폭압적인 군주들이 저마다 차지한 자잘한 공국들로 쪼개진 채 내버려 두는 대신, 하나의 크고 단단하며 강력한 제국을 세우려 했지요. 그리고 마지막으로, 왕관을 쓴 어느 멍청이를 내 체사레 보르자로 점찍었기 때문이오. 그자는 나를 배신하려고 내 뜻에 동조하는 척했던 거였소. 그것은 알렉산데르 6세와 클레멘스 7세의 계획이었지만, 이제는 결코 성공하지 못할 거요. 그들도 헛되이 시도했고, 나폴레옹 역시 제 과업을 끝내지 못했으니. 이탈리아는 불행을 타고난 듯하오.\" 그러고는 노인이 고개를 숙였다.\n\n당테스는 그런 일들 때문에 목숨을 거는 사람을 이해할 수 없었다. 나폴레옹이라면 직접 보고 말까지 나눠 본 터라 어느 정도 알았다. 그러나 클레멘스 7세나 알렉산데르 6세에 대해서는 아무것도 몰랐다.\n\n\"혹시 선생님이,\" 그가 물었다. \"이 이프성에서 다들 좀, 편찮으시다고 여기는 그 신부님 아니십니까?\"\n\n\"미쳤다는 말이지요, 안 그렇소?\"\n\n\"차마 그렇게는 말씀드리기 어려웠습니다.\" 당테스가 미소 지으며 대답했다.\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 파리아가 씁쓸한 미소를 지으며 말을 이었다. \"당신 물음에 빠짐없이 답해 드리리다. 나는 이프성의 가엾은 미친 죄수요. 여러 해 동안 이곳을 찾는 갖가지 방문객들에게 이른바 내 광기로 즐거움을 베푸는 일을 허락받았지요. 그리고 십중팔구는, 아이들에게 웃음거리가 되어 주는 영예로까지 승진했을 거요. 다만 이처럼 고통과 절망에 바쳐진 거처에서 그런 순진한 어린것들을 찾을 수만 있었다면 말이오.\"\n\n당테스는 잠시 말없이 꼼짝도 하지 않고 있었다. 이윽고 그가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"그럼 선생님은 탈출의 희망을 모두 버리셨단 말씀입니까?\"\n\n\"그게 전혀 불가능하다는 걸 알았으니까요. 게다가 전능하신 분께서 분명 허락지 않으시는 일을 굳이 꾀하는 건 불경하다고 여깁니다.\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 낙심하지 마십시오. 첫 시도에 성공하기를 바라는 건 너무 큰 욕심이 아니겠습니까? 이번에 그토록 안타깝게 실패한 방향 말고, 다른 쪽으로 길을 뚫어 보시는 건 어떻습니까?\"\n\n\"아아, 다시 시작하자는 말을 하는 걸 보니, 그토록 뜻밖에 좌절된 일을 이루느라 내가 얼마나 많은 대가를 치렀는지 당신은 거의 짐작도 못 하는구려. 우선, 지금 가진 연장들을 만드는 데 사 년이 걸렸소. 그리고 화강암처럼 단단한 흙을 긁고 파내는 데 이 년을 보냈지요. 그다음엔, 한때는 도무지 떼어 낼 수 없으리라 여겼던 거대한 돌들을 들어내느라 또 얼마나 애쓰고 지쳤는지 모른다오. 그런 엄청난 노고에 며칠씩을 다 바치면서도, 밤이 될 무렵 이 단단하게 굳은 회반죽을, 세월이 흘러 돌만큼이나 굳어 버린 그것을 한 치 사방만큼이라도 떼어 낼 수 있으면 그 수고가 충분히 보상받았다고 여겼지요. 또 파낸 흙과 부스러기 더미를 감추려고 계단 하나를 부수고, 그 노고의 결실을 그 빈 속에 쏟아 넣어야 했소. 그런데 이제 그 구덩이가 어찌나 꽉 들어찼는지, 한 줌의 흙이라도 더 넣었다간 들통이 날 판이오. 게다가 생각해 보시오, 나는 내 과업의 목적과 목표를 다 이루었다고 굳게 믿었던 거요. 그 일이 끝날 때까지 딱 버틸 만큼만 힘을 쓰도록 그토록 정확히 아껴 두었으니 말이오. 그런데 이제 성공을 손꼽아 기다리던 바로 그 순간, 내 희망은 영영 산산이 부서져 버렸소. 아니, 다시 한번 말하지만, 무엇으로도 나를 움직여, 전능하신 분의 뜻에 분명히 어긋나는 시도를 되풀이하게 할 수는 없을 거요.\"\n\n당테스는 동료가 생겼다는 생각에 차오르는 기쁨이, 신부의 계획이 실패한 데 대한 안타까움을 짓누르고 있음을 들킬까 봐 고개를 숙였다.\n\n신부는 에드몽의 침대에 주저앉았고, 에드몽은 그대로 서 있었다. 탈출이라는 생각은 단 한 번도 그의 머리에 떠오른 적이 없었다. 정말이지, 어떤 일들은 너무나 불가능해 보여서 마음이 한순간도 거기에 머물지 않는 법이다. 오십 피트나 땅을 파고 들어가는 것, 성공한다 해도 바다 위로 깎아지른 벼랑으로나 데려다줄 일에 삼 년을 바치는 것, 보초들의 총격을 운 좋게 피했다 쳐도 오십이나 육십, 어쩌면 백 피트나 되는 높이에서 바위에 부딪쳐 산산조각 날 위험을 무릅쓰고 파도 속으로 뛰어드는 것, 그 모든 위험을 다 넘겼다고 가정하더라도 뭍에 닿으려면 적어도 삼 마일은 죽을힘을 다해 헤엄쳐야 하는 것, 이 모든 것이 너무나 아찔하고 엄청난 난관이라, 당테스는 그런 계획을 꿈에도 그려 본 적이 없었고, 차라리 죽음에 자신을 내맡기는 편이었다.\n\n그러나 그토록 필사적인 용기로 삶에 매달리는 노인을 보자, 그의 생각은 새롭게 방향을 틀었고 가슴에는 새로운 용기가 솟았다. 자기보다 나이 많고 힘도 약한 다른 사람이, 자신은 감히 엄두조차 내지 못했던 일을 시도했고, 그것도 단지 계산이 어긋난 탓에 실패했을 뿐이었다. 바로 그 사람이, 거의 믿기 어려운 인내와 끈기로, 이토록 유례없는 시도에 필요한 연장들을 스스로 마련해 냈다. 다른 사람이 이 모든 걸 해냈는데, 그렇다면 어째서 당테스에게는 불가능하단 말인가? 파리아가 오십 피트를 파고 나아갔다면, 당테스는 백 피트라도 팔 것이다. 파리아가 쉰 살의 나이로 그 일에 삼 년을 바쳤다면, 그 절반밖에 안 된 자기는 육 년이라도 바칠 것이다. 신부이자 학자인 파리아가, 도므섬이든 라토노섬이든 르메르섬이든 그 어딘가로 삼 마일을 헤엄쳐 가며 목숨을 거는 일을 마다하지 않았다면, 자기처럼 강인한 뱃사람이자 노련한 잠수부가 그런 일을 두려워해서야 되겠는가. 그저 재미 삼아 그토록 자주 바다 밑바닥까지 잠겨 들어가 빛나는 산호 가지를 건져 올리던 자기가, 같은 계획을 품기를 망설여서야 되겠는가. 한 시간이면 해낼 수 있는 일이었고, 순전히 심심풀이로 그 두 배도 넘게 물속에 머문 적이 또 얼마나 많았던가! 당테스는 곧장 기운찬 동료의 용감한 본보기를 따르기로, 그리고 한 번 이루어진 일은 다시 이루어질 수 있다는 사실을 마음에 새기기로 결심했다.\n\n한동안 깊은 생각에 잠겨 있던 젊은이가 불쑥 외쳤다. \"선생님이 찾으시던 걸 제가 찾아냈습니다!\"\n\n파리아가 흠칫했다. \"정말로 그랬소?\" 그가 재빨리 불안한 기색으로 고개를 들며 소리쳤다. \"부디, 당신이 무얼 알아냈는지 말해 보시오.\"\n\n\"선생님이 지금 계신 감방에서부터 뚫고 오신 그 복도는, 바깥 회랑과 같은 방향으로 뻗어 있지요, 그렇지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"그리고 그 회랑과 십오 피트 이상 떨어져 있지는 않고요?\"\n\n\"그쯤 되지요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 우리가 무얼 해야 할지 말씀드리겠습니다. 복도의 한가운데쯤에 옆으로 구멍을 내서 뚫고 나가는 겁니다. 마치 십자가의 윗부분처럼요. 이번에는 선생님이 계획을 좀 더 정확하게 세우시는 겁니다. 그렇게 해서 선생님이 말씀하신 그 회랑으로 나가, 거길 지키는 보초를 죽이고 탈출하는 거지요. 성공을 거두는 데 필요한 건 오직 용기뿐인데, 그건 선생님이 가지셨고, 또 힘인데, 그건 제게 모자라지 않습니다. 인내로 말하면, 선생님은 이미 넘치도록 증명하셨으니, 이제 제가 그걸 증명하는 걸 보시게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"잠깐만, 친구여.\" 신부가 대답했다. \"당신은 내가 지닌 용기가 어떤 성질의 것인지, 또 내가 내 힘을 어디에 쓰려 하는지를 잘 모르는 게 분명하오. 인내로 말하면, 매일 아침 전날 밤의 일을 다시 시작하고 매일 밤 그날 낮의 일을 다시 이어 가면서, 나는 그걸 넉넉히 발휘했다고 생각하오. 하지만 젊은이여, 부디 내 말에 온전히 귀 기울여 주시오. 그때 나는, 죄 없는 사람 하나를, 아무 잘못도 저지르지 않았고 단죄받을 까닭도 없는 사람 하나를 자유롭게 풀어 주려 애쓰는 일이라면 전능하신 분의 노여움을 살 일은 없으리라 여겼던 거요.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 생각이 바뀌셨습니까?\" 당테스가 몹시 놀라며 물었다. \"저를 만났다고 해서, 이제 그 시도를 하는 일이 더 죄스럽다고 여기시는 건가요?\"\n\n\"아니오. 나 또한 죄를 짓고 싶지는 않소. 지금껏 나는 그저 사람이 아니라 처지와 싸우는 거라 여겨 왔소. 벽을 뚫거나 계단을 부수는 건 죄가 아니라고 생각했지요. 하지만 가슴을 꿰뚫고 목숨을 앗는 일이라면, 그렇게 쉽게 스스로를 납득시킬 수가 없구려.\"\n\n당테스의 입에서 가벼운 놀라움의 기색이 새어 나왔다.\n\n\"그럴 수가 있습니까,\" 그가 말했다. \"선생님의 자유가 걸린 일인데, 그런 거리낌 따위가 그걸 얻는 일을 막도록 내버려 두실 수 있단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"말해 보시오.\" 파리아가 대답했다. \"당신은 침대 틀에서 나뭇조각 하나를 뜯어내 간수를 때려눕히고, 그자의 옷으로 갈아입은 다음 탈출을 꾀할 수도 있었을 텐데, 무엇이 당신을 가로막았소?\"\n\n\"그저 그런 생각이 한 번도 떠오르지 않았다는 사실뿐입니다.\" 당테스가 대답했다.\n\n\"그건,\" 노인이 말했다. \"그런 죄를 저지르는 데 대한 타고난 거부감이 당신이 그런 생각을 하지 못하게 막은 거요. 언제나 그렇소. 단순하고 허락된 일들에 있어서는, 우리의 타고난 본능이 우리가 의무의 엄정한 선에서 벗어나지 않게 붙잡아 주거든요. 피 흘리는 데서 기쁨을 느끼도록 천성을 타고난 호랑이는, 먹이가 닿을 거리 안에 들어왔음을 후각만으로 알아채고, 그 본능을 좇아 제 먹이에게 덮치는 데 필요한 도약의 거리를 가늠할 수 있소. 그러나 사람은 그와 반대로 피라는 것을 끔찍이 싫어하지요. 그건 단지 사회생활의 법도가 목숨을 빼앗는 데 대한 움츠러드는 두려움을 불어넣기 때문만이 아니오. 사람의 타고난 구조와 생리적 형성이……\"\n\n당테스는 자기도 모르게 마음속에서, 아니 영혼 속에서 꿈틀거리고 있던 생각을 이렇게 풀이해 주는 말 앞에서 어리둥절해져 입을 다물었다. 본디 생각에는 뚜렷이 다른 두 종류가 있으니, 머리에서 나오는 것과 가슴에서 우러나오는 것이 그것이다.\n\n\"갇혀 지내는 동안,\" 파리아가 말했다. \"나는 기록에 남은 가장 유명한 탈옥 사례들을 모두 곰곰이 생각해 보았소. 성공한 경우는 드물었지요. 온전한 성공으로 빛난 것들은 오래도록 궁리하고 세심하게 짜여진 것들이었소. 이를테면 보포르 공작이 뱅센성을 빠져나온 일, 뒤뷔쿠아 신부가 포르레베크를 탈출한 일, 라튀드가 바스티유에서 도망친 일이 그렇지요. 그다음으로는 더러 우연이 기회를 마련해 주는 경우가 있는데, 그것이야말로 무엇보다 가장 나은 것이오. 그러니 우리도 어떤 알맞은 순간이 올 때까지 참을성 있게 기다렸다가, 그 순간이 찾아오거든 그것을 잘 살립시다.\"\n\n\"아,\" 당테스가 말했다. \"선생님이야 그 지루한 기다림을 견디실 만했겠지요. 스스로 정해 둔 일에 끊임없이 매달리셨고, 노고에 지치면 다시 기운을 북돋아 주고 격려해 줄 희망이 있었으니까요.\"\n\n\"정말이지,\" 노인이 대답했다. \"나는 기분 전환이나 버팀목을 얻으려고 그 희망이라는 샘에 기댄 게 아니었소.\"\n\n\"그럼 무얼 하셨습니까?\"\n\n\"글을 쓰거나 연구를 했지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 펜과 잉크와 종이를 쓰는 게 허락되었습니까?\"\n\n\"오, 아니오.\" 신부가 대답했다. \"내가 직접 만든 것 말고는 아무것도 없었지요.\"\n\n\"종이와 펜과 잉크를 직접 만드셨다고요?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n당테스는 경탄하며 바라보았지만, 좀처럼 믿기 어려워했다. 파리아는 그것을 알아차렸다.\n\n\"젊은 친구여, 당신이 내 감방을 찾아오거든,\" 그가 말했다. \"온전한 저작 한 편을 보여 주리다. 내 평생의 사색과 성찰이 맺은 열매지요. 그 가운데 많은 것을 로마의 콜로세움 그늘에서, 베네치아의 산마르코 광장 기둥 아래에서, 또 피렌체의 아르노강 기슭에서 곰곰이 궁리했더랬소. 그것들이 언젠가 이프성 담장 안에서 차곡차곡 정리되리라고는 그때는 꿈에도 몰랐지요. 내가 말하는 그 저작은 『이탈리아 통일 군주국 수립 가능성에 관한 논고』라는 것인데, 큰 사절판으로 두툼한 한 권은 될 거요.\"\n\n\"그런데 그 모든 걸 어디에다 쓰셨습니까?\"\n\n\"내 셔츠 두 장에다 썼지요. 아마포를 양피지처럼 매끄럽고 글쓰기 좋게 만드는 처리법을 고안해 냈거든요.\"\n\n\"그럼 선생님은 화학자이십니까?\"\n\n\"조금은 그렇소. 라부아지에를 알고, 카바니스와는 절친한 사이였으니까요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그런 저작을 쓰시려면 책이 있어야 했을 텐데, 책은 좀 가지고 계셨습니까?\"\n\n\"로마에 있는 내 서재에는 책이 오천 권 가까이 있었소. 그런데 그것들을 여러 번 거듭 읽고 나니, 잘 골라낸 백쉰 권만 있으면, 인간 지식 전부를 빠짐없이 간추린 건 아닐지라도, 적어도 사람이 정말로 알아야 할 모든 것은 손에 넣은 셈이라는 걸 깨달았지요. 나는 그 백쉰 권을 읽고 공부하는 데 내 삶의 삼 년을 바쳤고, 거의 외우다시피 했소. 그래서 감옥에 들어온 뒤로는, 아주 가벼운 기억의 노력만으로도 마치 책장이 눈앞에 펼쳐진 듯 그 내용을 술술 떠올릴 수 있다오. 투키디데스, 크세노폰, 플루타르코스, 티투스 리비우스, 타키투스, 스트라다, 요르난데스, 단테, 몽테뉴, 셰익스피어, 스피노자, 마키아벨리, 그리고 보쉬에를 통째로 외워 들려줄 수도 있지요. 가장 중요한 이들만 꼽아도 그렇소.\"\n\n\"그 책들을 다 읽으실 수 있었다니, 선생님은 분명 여러 언어에 정통하시겠군요?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 나는 현대어 다섯 가지를 하지요. 그러니까 독일어, 프랑스어, 이탈리아어, 영어, 그리고 에스파냐어를 말이오. 고대 그리스어의 도움으로 현대 그리스어도 익혔는데, 바라는 만큼 잘하지는 못해서 아직도 갈고닦으려 애쓰는 중이오.\"\n\n\"갈고닦으신다고요!\" 당테스가 되뇌었다. \"아니, 어떻게 그러실 수가 있습니까?\"\n\n\"내가 아는 낱말들로 어휘집을 만들어, 이리저리 뒤집고 또 뒤집고 짜 맞춰서, 그것들을 통해 내 생각을 표현할 수 있게 했지요. 나는 천 개 가까운 낱말을 아는데, 그게 꼭 필요한 전부라오. 사전에는 십만 개 가까운 낱말이 있다지만 말이오. 아주 유창하기를 바랄 수는 없지만, 내가 바라고 원하는 바를 설명하는 데는 분명 아무 어려움이 없을 거요. 그리고 그것이야말로 내가 평생 필요로 할 전부일 테고요.\"\n\n당테스의 경이는 점점 더 커져, 마치 초자연적인 능력을 타고난 사람을 상대하고 있는 듯한 기분마저 들었다. 그래도 이 사람을 보통의 인간들과 같은 수준으로 끌어내릴 만한 무슨 흠이라도 찾아낼까 하는 마음에, 그는 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"그럼 펜도 마련돼 있지 않았는데, 말씀하신 그 저작은 어떻게 쓰셨습니까?\"\n\n\"훌륭한 펜을 몇 자루 만들었지요. 한번 알려지기만 하면 누구나 다른 모든 펜보다 그걸 더 좋아할 만한 것들이라오. 금육일이면 우리에게 어마어마하게 큰 대구가 나오는 걸 당신도 알 거요. 그래서 나는 그 생선 머릿속의 연골을 골라냈는데, 수요일과 금요일과 토요일이 돌아올 때마다 내 펜 재고를 늘릴 수단이 생긴다는 생각에 얼마나 반가워했는지는 당신은 짐작도 못 할 거요. 솔직히 털어놓자면, 그 역사 저술이야말로 내게 가장 큰 위안이자 안식이었으니까. 지난날을 되짚는 동안에는 현재를 잊고, 역사의 길을 마음껏 거니노라면 나 자신이 죄수라는 사실마저 잊게 되거든요.\"\n\n\"하지만 잉크는요.\" 당테스가 말했다. \"잉크는 무엇으로 만드셨습니까?\"\n\n\"내 감방에는 예전에 벽난로가 하나 있었소.\" 파리아가 대답했다. \"내가 이 감옥에 들어오기 한참 전에 막아 버렸지만요. 그래도 오랜 세월 쓰였던 모양인지, 그을음이 두텁게 한 켜 덮여 있었소. 나는 그 그을음을 일요일마다 내게 나오는 포도주에 조금 풀었는데, 정말이지 그보다 더 나은 잉크는 바랄 수 없을 거요. 더 정신을 바짝 차려야 하는 아주 중요한 메모를 할 때는, 손가락 하나를 찔러서 내 피로 썼지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 모든 걸 언제쯤 볼 수 있겠습니까?\" 당테스가 물었다.\n\n\"당신이 바라는 때면 언제든지요.\" 신부가 대답했다.\n\n\"오, 그렇다면 지금 당장 보고 싶습니다!\" 젊은이가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"그럼 나를 따라오시오.\" 신부는 그렇게 말하며 지하 통로로 다시 들어갔고, 이내 그 안으로 사라졌다. 당테스가 그 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n# Chapter 17. The Abbé’s Chamber\n\n지하 통로를 비교적 수월하게 빠져나왔다. 다만 그 통로는 몸을 똑바로 세울 만큼 높지 않았다. 두 친구는 복도 끝에 다다랐고, 그곳에서 신부의 독방으로 이어지는 입구가 열려 있었다. 그 지점부터 통로는 훨씬 좁아져, 손과 무릎으로 겨우 기어 들어갈 만했다. 신부의 독방 바닥은 돌로 포장되어 있었는데, 가장 어두운 구석의 돌 하나를 들어 올리는 것에서부터 파리아는 그 고된 작업을 시작했고, 당테스는 그 작업이 완성되는 광경을 직접 보았다.\n\n친구의 방에 들어서면서 당테스는 기대하던 경이로운 물건들을 찾아 간절하고 탐색하는 눈길을 사방으로 던졌지만, 평범한 것들 말고는 아무것도 눈에 들어오지 않았다.\n\n\"됐네.\" 신부가 말했다. \"우리에게는 몇 시간이 있어. 지금이 막 열두 시 십오 분이니까.\" 당테스는 본능적으로 몸을 돌려, 신부가 대체 어떤 시계로 그렇게 정확하게 시간을 짚어 냈는지 살폈다.\n\n\"내 창문으로 들어오는 저 빛줄기를 보게.\" 신부가 말했다. \"그런 다음 벽에 그어 놓은 선들을 보게나. 자, 지구의 이중 운동과 지구가 태양 둘레로 그리는 타원에 따라 그린 저 선들 덕분에, 나는 시계를 가졌을 때보다 더 세밀하게 정확한 시간을 알아낼 수 있다네. 시계라면 부서지거나 작동이 어긋날 수 있지만, 태양과 지구는 정해진 길에서 결코 벗어나는 법이 없으니까.\"\n\n이 마지막 설명은 당테스에게 전혀 와닿지 않았다. 그는 태양이 산 너머에서 떠올라 지중해로 지는 것을 보아 왔기에, 늘 움직이는 것은 태양이지 지구가 아니라고 여겼던 것이다. 자신이 사는 지구가 이중으로 움직이고 있는데도 그것을 조금도 느끼지 못한다는 사실이 그에게는 도무지 있을 수 없는 일로 보였다. 친구의 입에서 떨어지는 말 한마디 한마디가 학문의 신비로 가득 차 있는 듯했고, 그것은 그가 아주 어린 시절의 항해 중에 들렀던 기억이 어렴풋이 남아 있는 구제라트와 골콘다의 광산에서 나는 금이나 다이아몬드만큼이나 캐낼 가치가 있는 것으로 느껴졌다.\n\n\"자,\" 그가 신부에게 말했다. \"어서 신부님의 보물들을 보고 싶습니다.\"\n\n신부가 미소 지었다. 그러고는 쓰지 않는 벽난로 쪽으로 가서, 끌의 도움을 빌려 긴 돌 하나를 들어 올렸다. 분명 화덕이었을 그 돌 밑에는 제법 깊은 구멍이 있었고, 그것은 당테스에게 말했던 물건들을 안전하게 보관하는 창고 노릇을 하고 있었다.\n\n\"무엇부터 보고 싶은가?\" 신부가 물었다.\n\n\"오, 이탈리아 군주제에 관한 신부님의 대작을요!\"\n\n그러자 파리아는 은닉처에서 아마포 두루마리 서너 개를 꺼냈다. 파피루스를 접어 놓은 것처럼 차곡차곡 포개져 있었다. 이 두루마리들은 폭이 약 4인치, 길이가 18인치쯤 되는 천 조각들로 이루어져 있었다. 모두 꼼꼼히 번호가 매겨져 있었고, 빽빽하게 글씨가 적혀 있었는데, 어찌나 또렷한지 당테스는 어렵지 않게 읽을 수 있었을 뿐 아니라 뜻도 알아챌 수 있었다. 프로방스 사람인 그가 완벽하게 이해하는 이탈리아어로 쓰여 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"여기 있네.\" 그가 말했다. \"이게 완성된 작품일세. 일주일쯤 전에 예순여덟 번째 천 조각 끝에 '피니스'라는 단어를 써넣었지. 이 귀한 페이지들을 마저 채우려고 셔츠 두 벌과 가진 손수건을 죄다 찢었다네. 언젠가 감옥을 나가서, 온 이탈리아를 통틀어 내가 쓴 것을 출판할 만큼 용기 있는 인쇄업자를 만나게 된다면, 내 문인으로서의 명성은 영원히 보장될 걸세.\"\n* 피니스: 라틴어로 '끝'이라는 뜻. —옮긴이\n\n\"알겠습니다.\" 당테스가 대답했다. \"이제 신부님이 이 작품을 쓰신 진기한 펜을 좀 보여 주십시오.\"\n\n\"보게!\" 파리아가 길이 6인치쯤 되는 가느다란 막대기를 젊은이에게 보여 주며 말했다. 가는 그림 붓의 자루만 한 굵기였는데, 그 끝에는 실로 연골 한 조각이 묶여 있었다. 앞서 신부가 당테스에게 말했던 바로 그 연골이었다. 끝은 뾰족하게 다듬어졌고, 보통 펜처럼 펜촉이 갈라져 있었다. 당테스는 깊은 감탄에 잠겨 그것을 살펴보았다. 그러고는 이토록 정확하게 모양을 다듬은 도구가 무엇인지 보려고 주위를 둘러보았다.\n\n\"아, 그래.\" 파리아가 말했다. \"주머니칼 말이군. 그게 내 걸작이야. 저 큰 칼과 마찬가지로, 낡은 쇠 촛대로 만들었지.\" 주머니칼은 면도날처럼 날카롭고 예리했다. 다른 칼은 두 가지 용도로 쓸 수 있어서, 베기도 하고 찌르기도 할 수 있었다.\n\n당테스는 마르세유 상점들에 진열된 진귀한 물건들과 기이한 연장들을 살펴볼 때와 똑같은 주의를 기울여 보여 주는 물건들을 하나하나 살폈다. 그 물건들은 남태평양 미개인들의 작품으로, 여러 무역선이 가져온 것들이었다.\n\n\"잉크로 말하자면,\" 파리아가 말했다. \"내가 어떻게 그걸 구했는지 자네에게 말했지. 그리고 그저 필요할 때마다 이따금 만들어 쓴다네.\"\n\n\"한 가지가 아직도 풀리지 않습니다.\" 당테스가 말했다. \"이 모든 일을 어떻게 대낮에 하셨는지 말입니다.\"\n\n\"밤에도 일했네.\" 파리아가 대답했다.\n\n\"밤에요? 아니, 도대체, 신부님 눈이 고양이 눈이라도 됩니까? 어둠 속에서도 보고 일을 하시다니요?\"\n\n\"그럴 리가 있겠나. 하지만 하느님은 인간에게 지성을 주셔서, 타고난 조건의 한계를 넘어설 수 있게 하셨지. 나는 등불을 마련했다네.\"\n\n\"내게 나오는 고기에서 기름기를 떼어 내 녹여서 기름을 만들었지. 자, 이게 내 등불일세.\" 그렇게 말하며 신부는 거리 축제 때 쓰는 횃불과 아주 비슷한 것을 보여 주었다.\n\n\"하지만 불은 어떻게 붙이십니까?\"\n\n\"오, 여기 부싯돌 두 개와 태운 아마포 조각이 있지 않은가.\"\n\n\"성냥은요?\"\n\n\"피부병에 걸린 척하면서 유황을 조금 달라고 했더니, 선뜻 내주더군.\"\n\n당테스는 보고 있던 물건들을 탁자 위에 내려놓고, 파리아의 정신력과 끈기에 압도된 듯 고개를 가슴께로 떨군 채 서 있었다.\n\n\"자네는 아직 다 보지 못했네.\" 파리아가 말을 이었다. \"내 보물을 전부 한 은닉처에 맡기는 게 현명하다고 생각하지 않았거든. 이건 이제 닫아 두세.\" 두 사람은 돌을 제자리에 도로 놓았다. 신부는 그 위에 먼지를 살짝 뿌려 들어낸 흔적을 감추고, 발로 잘 문질러 다른 돌과 똑같아 보이게 만들었다. 그런 다음 침대 쪽으로 가더니, 침대를 놓여 있던 자리에서 치웠다. 침대 머리맡 뒤에는 어떤 의심도 허락하지 않을 만큼 딱 맞물린 돌에 가려진 빈 공간이 있었고, 그 공간 안에는 길이가 25피트에서 30피트쯤 되는 밧줄 사다리가 들어 있었다. 당테스는 그것을 가까이서 열심히 살폈다. 그것은 어떤 무게도 견딜 만큼 튼튼하고 단단하고 촘촘하게 짜여 있었다.\n\n\"이 놀라운 물건을 만드실 재료는 누가 대 주었습니까?\"\n\n\"페네스트렐에서 삼 년 동안 갇혀 있을 때, 셔츠 여러 벌을 찢고 침대 시트의 솔기를 뜯어냈지. 그리고 이프성으로 옮겨질 때, 그 실밥들을 용케 함께 가져왔다네. 덕분에 여기서 작업을 마칠 수 있었지.\"\n\n\"그런데 시트의 단이 풀린 게 발각되지 않았습니까?\"\n\n\"오, 그렇지 않았네. 필요한 실을 뽑아낸 뒤에는 가장자리를 다시 꿰매 두었으니까.\"\n\n\"무엇으로요?\"\n\n\"이 바늘로.\" 신부가 말하며 누더기 같은 옷을 헤쳐, 길고 뾰족한 생선 뼈를 당테스에게 보여 주었다. 실을 꿸 작은 구멍이 뚫려 있었고, 거기에는 아직 실 한 토막이 남아 있었다.\n\n\"한때는,\" 파리아가 말을 이었다. \"이 쇠창살을 떼어 내고 창문으로 내려갈 생각도 했었네. 보다시피 이 창문이 자네 것보다 조금 넓긴 하지만, 그래도 탈출에 앞서 더 넓혀야 했겠지. 하지만 알고 보니 그저 일종의 안마당으로 떨어질 뿐이더군. 그래서 위험과 모험이 너무 크다고 여겨 그 계획을 통째로 단념했지. 그래도 아까 말한 그런 뜻밖의 기회, 갑작스러운 운이 곧잘 가져다주는 그런 기회를 대비해, 이 사다리만은 소중히 간직해 두었네.\"\n\n당테스는 사다리를 자세히 들여다보는 척했지만, 실은 마음속으로 다른 생각에 골몰해 있었다. 신부처럼 그토록 지혜롭고 영리하며 통찰력 있는 사람이라면, 정작 자신은 아무것도 알아내지 못하는 자기 불행의 어두운 수수께끼를 풀어 줄 수 있을지도 모른다는 생각이었다.\n\n\"무슨 생각을 하고 있나?\" 신부가 미소 지으며 물었다. 그는 손님이 깊은 상념에 빠진 것을, 지나친 경외감과 경이로움 탓으로 여겼다.\n\n\"우선,\" 당테스가 대답했다. \"신부님이 지금 이르신 이 높은 경지에 다다르려면 얼마나 엄청난 지성과 능력을 쏟아부으셨을지를 생각하고 있었습니다. 만약 자유로운 몸이셨다면 무엇인들 못 이루셨겠습니까?\"\n\n\"아마 아무것도 못 이뤘을 걸세. 자유로운 처지였다면 내 머릿속 넘쳐 나는 것들도 아마 천 가지 어리석은 일로 흩어져 버렸겠지. 인간 지성의 보물을 빛으로 끌어내려면 불행이 필요한 법일세. 화약을 터뜨리려면 압축이 필요하듯이 말이야. 갇혀 지내는 처지가 내 정신력을 한곳에 모아 주었네. 자네도 잘 알겠지만, 구름이 부딪치면 전기가 생기고, 전기에서 번개가, 번개에서 빛이 나오지 않는가.\"\n\n\"아닙니다.\" 당테스가 대답했다. \"저는 아무것도 모릅니다. 신부님의 말씀 가운데 더러는 제게 전혀 뜻 없는 소리로 들립니다. 그런 지식을 지니셨으니, 신부님은 참으로 복 받은 분이십니다.\"\n\n신부가 미소 지었다. \"그건 그렇고,\" 그가 말했다. \"자네에겐 또 다른 생각거리가 있지 않았나? 방금 그렇게 말하지 않았어?\"\n\n\"그랬습니다!\"\n\"여태 그중 하나만 말했으니, 나머지 하나도 들려주게.\"\n\n\"이런 것이었습니다. 신부님은 제게 지나온 삶의 자초지종을 모두 들려주셨는데, 정작 제 삶에 대해서는 전혀 모르고 계신다는 것 말입니다.\"\n\n\"젊은 친구, 자네 인생은 아주 중요한 사건들을 두루 겪었다고 하기엔 아직 그리 길지 않잖은가.\"\n\n\"제게 크나큰, 그것도 까닭 없는 불행을 안기기에는 충분히 길었습니다. 저는 그 불행의 근원을 인간에게서 찾고 싶습니다. 그래야 더는 하늘을 원망하지 않을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 자네는 자신에게 씌워진 죄목을 정말 모른다는 건가?\"\n\n\"정말 모릅니다. 이 땅에서 제게 가장 소중한 두 사람, 제 아버지와 메르세데스의 이름을 걸고 맹세합니다.\"\n\n\"자,\" 신부가 은닉처를 닫고 침대를 본래 자리로 밀어 놓으며 말했다. \"자네 이야기를 들려주게.\"\n\n당테스는 시키는 대로, 자신이 일생이라 부른 이야기를 시작했다. 그러나 그것은 인도로의 항해와 레반트로의 두세 차례 항해에 관한 이야기일 뿐이었고, 마침내 마지막 항해 이야기에 이르렀다. 르클레르 선장의 죽음, 자신이 직접 대원수에게 전하라며 받은 꾸러미, 그 인물과의 만남, 그리고 가져간 꾸러미 대신 받은, 누아르티에라는 인물에게 보내는 편지 이야기였다. 마르세유에 도착해 아버지를 만난 일, 메르세데스를 향한 사랑과 결혼 잔치, 체포와 뒤이은 심문, 재판소에서의 일시적 구금, 그리고 마침내 이프성에 갇힌 일까지. 이 지점부터는 당테스에게 모든 것이 백지였다. 그는 그 이상은 아무것도 몰랐고, 자신이 갇혀 있던 기간이 얼마나 되는지조차 알지 못했다. 그의 이야기가 끝나자 신부는 오랫동안 골똘히 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n\"있다네.\" 그가 한참 깊이 생각한 끝에 입을 열었다. \"조금 전에 내가 자네에게 한 말과도 통하는, 영리한 격언이 하나 있지. 본디 타락한 마음에 사악한 생각이 뿌리내리지 않는 한, 인간 본성이란 올바르고 건전한 상태에서는 범죄를 거부한다는 것일세. 그래도 인위적인 문명에서 비롯된 욕망과 악덕과 그릇된 취향이 있어, 그것들이 이따금 너무 강해지면 우리 안의 온갖 선한 감정을 질식시키고 끝내 우리를 죄와 악으로 이끌지. 그러니 이런 관점에서 보면 한 가지 공리가 나오네. 어떤 나쁜 짓의 장본인을 찾으려거든, 먼저 그 나쁜 짓이 저질러져 어떤 식으로든 이득을 볼 사람이 누구인지부터 찾으라는 것이지. 자, 이걸 자네 경우에 적용해 보세. 자네가 사라져서 누가 덕을 볼 수 있었겠나?\"\n\n\"맹세코, 아무도 없습니다! 저는 아주 보잘것없는 사람이었으니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇게 말하지 말게. 자네 대답에는 논리도 철학도 없네. 젊은 친구, 세상 모든 것은 상대적이라네. 후계자의 앞길을 막고 선 왕에서부터, 경쟁자가 자리를 차지하지 못하게 막는 말단 직원에 이르기까지 말이야. 왕이 죽으면 후계자가 왕관을 물려받고, 그 직원이 죽으면 후보가 그 자리를 차지해 연봉 1만 2천 리브르를 받지. 자, 그 1만 2천 리브르는 그에게 왕의 1천 2백만이나 다름없이 긴요한, 그의 생계 목록일세. 가장 높은 자에서 가장 낮은 자에 이르기까지 누구나 사회라는 사다리에서 제 자리를 차지하고 있고, 데카르트의 압력과 추진 이론에서처럼 격렬한 욕망과 상충하는 이해관계에 둘러싸여 있지. 그런데 이 힘들은 위로 올라갈수록 커지기 때문에, 이치를 거슬러 밑바닥이 아니라 꼭대기로 떠받쳐진 나선이 생기는 거야. 자, 이제 자네의 작은 세계로 돌아가 보세. 자네가 파라옹호의 선장이 되기 직전이었다고 했지?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"그리고 젊고 아리따운 아가씨의 남편이 되려던 참이었고?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"자, 이 두 가지가 이루어지는 것을 막아서 이득을 볼 사람이 있었겠나? 하지만 우선 자네가 파라옹호의 선장이 되는 걸 방해해서 누군가 득을 볼 일이 있었는지부터 따져 보세. 어떤가?\"\n\n\"그런 일이 있었다고는 믿기 어렵습니다. 저는 배 안에서 두루 사랑받았고, 선원들이 직접 선장을 뽑을 권리가 있었다면 분명 저를 골랐으리라 확신합니다. 선원들 가운데 제게 악감정을 품은 사람은 딱 한 명뿐이었습니다. 얼마 전에 그와 다툰 적이 있었고, 결투를 신청하기까지 했지만, 그가 거절했지요.\"\n\n\"이제 좀 풀려 가는군. 그 사람 이름이 뭐였나?\"\n\n\"당글라르입니다.\"\n\n\"배에서 무슨 직책을 맡고 있었지?\"\n\n\"회계 담당이었습니다.\"\n\n\"자네가 선장이 되었다면, 그를 그 자리에 그대로 두었겠나?\"\n\n\"제게 선택권이 있었다면 그러지 않았을 겁니다. 그의 장부에서 부정확한 점을 자주 보았으니까요.\"\n\n\"또 좋아! 그럼 말해 보게. 르클레르 선장과 마지막으로 나눈 대화 자리에 누가 있었나?\"\n\n\"아니요. 저희 둘뿐이었습니다.\"\n\n\"누가 그 대화를 엿들을 수도 있었을까?\"\n\n\"그럴 수도 있었겠지요. 선실 문이 열려 있었으니까요. 그리고…… 가만, 이제 생각났습니다. 르클레르 선장님이 제게 대원수에게 전할 꾸러미를 건네던 바로 그때, 당글라르가 곁을 지나갔습니다.\"\n\n\"그게 더 낫군.\" 신부가 외쳤다. \"이제 제대로 냄새를 맡았네. 엘바섬 항구에 들렀을 때 누구를 데려갔나?\"\n\n\"아무도요.\"\n\n\"거기서 누군가 자네 꾸러미를 받고, 그 대신 편지를 한 통 주었지?\"\n\n\"네, 대원수가요.\"\n\n\"그 편지로는 무엇을 했나?\"\n\n\"제 서류첩에 넣었습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 그때 서류첩을 가지고 있었단 말인가? 그런데 선원이 어떻게 주머니에 공문서가 들어갈 만한 큰 서류첩을 넣고 다닐 수 있나?\"\n\n\"맞습니다. 그건 배에 두고 갔었습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 배로 돌아온 뒤에야 편지를 서류첩에 넣은 거로군?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"그럼 포르토페라요에서 배로 돌아오는 동안에는 그 편지를 어떻게 했나?\"\n\n\"손에 들고 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"그러니까 자네가 파라옹호에 올랐을 때, 누구나 자네가 손에 편지를 쥐고 있는 걸 볼 수 있었겠군?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"당글라르도, 다른 사람들과 마찬가지로?\"\n\n\"당글라르도, 다른 사람들과 마찬가지로요.\"\n\n\"자, 내 말을 잘 듣고, 자네 체포에 얽힌 정황을 하나하나 떠올려 보게. 자네를 고발한 밀고장이 어떤 문구로 적혀 있었는지 기억하나?\"\n\n\"오, 그럼요. 세 번이나 거듭 읽어서, 그 문구가 기억에 깊이 박혀 있습니다.\"\n\n\"내게 그대로 말해 보게.\"\n\n당테스는 잠시 멈추었다가 말했다. \"한 글자 한 글자 그대로 옮기면 이렇습니다. '왕좌와 종교를 받드는 한 벗이 검사장께 알려 드립니다. 파라옹호의 일등 항해사 에드몽 당테스라는 자가 나폴리와 포르토페라요를 거쳐 오늘 스미르나에서 입항했는데, 뮈라에게서 찬탈자에게 전할 꾸러미를 맡았고, 다시 그 찬탈자에게서 파리의 보나파르트파 클럽에 전할 편지를 받았습니다. 그자를 즉시 체포하면 이 죄의 증거를 확보할 수 있을 것입니다. 그 편지는 그자의 몸에서든, 그의 아버지 집에서든, 아니면 파라옹호의 그의 선실에서든 발견될 것입니다.'\"\n\n신부가 어깨를 으쓱했다. \"이건 대낮처럼 명백하군.\" 그가 말했다. \"이 일의 출처를 의심하지 않았다니, 자네는 천성이 무척 사람을 잘 믿는 데다 마음씨도 착했던 게 틀림없어.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇게 보십니까? 아, 그렇다면 정말 비열한 짓이로군요.\"\n\n\"당글라르는 평소에 어떻게 썼나?\"\n\n\"멋들어진 흘림체로 썼습니다.\"\n\n\"그 익명의 편지는 어떻게 쓰여 있었지?\"\n\n신부가 다시 미소 지었다. \"꾸민 글씨로군.\"\n\n\"꾸민 글씨치고는 아주 대담하게 쓰여 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"잠깐.\" 신부가 자신의 펜이라 부르던 것을 집어 들고 잉크에 적신 다음, 손질해 둔 아마포 조각에 왼손으로 그 고발장의 첫 두세 단어를 써 보였다. 당테스는 뒷걸음질하며, 거의 공포에 가까운 감정으로 신부를 바라보았다.\n\n\"이럴 수가!\" 그가 마침내 외쳤다. \"아니, 신부님 글씨가 그 고발장 글씨와 똑 닮았습니다.\"\n\n\"그 고발장이 왼손으로 쓰였기 때문일 뿐일세. 내가 알아챈 것이 있는데……\"\n\"무엇입니까?\"\n\n\"오른손으로 쓴 글씨는 사람마다 다르지만, 왼손으로 쓴 글씨는 한결같이 똑같다는 것일세.\"\n\n\"신부님은 정말이지 모든 것을 보고 살피셨군요.\"\n\n\"계속하세.\"\n\n\"네, 네!\"\n\n\"이제 두 번째 문제로 가 보세.\"\n\n\"듣고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"자네와 메르세데스의 결혼을 막아서 이득을 볼 사람이 있었나?\"\n\n\"네, 그녀를 사랑한 젊은이가 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"그 이름은……\"\n\n\"페르낭입니다.\"\n\n\"그건 스페인 이름인 것 같은데?\"\n\n\"카탈루냐 사람이었습니다.\"\n\n\"그가 그 편지를 쓸 만한 사람이라고 보나?\"\n\n\"오, 아닙니다. 그자였다면 차라리 제게 칼을 꽂아 저를 없애려 했을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그게 바로 스페인 사람의 기질에 딱 들어맞지. 살인은 망설임 없이 저질러도, 비겁한 짓은 결코 하지 않으니까.\"\n\n\"게다가,\" 당테스가 말했다. \"편지에 적힌 여러 정황은 그가 전혀 모르는 것들이었습니다.\"\n\n\"자네 입으로 그것들을 누구에게도 말한 적이 없나?\"\n\n\"누구에게도요.\"\n\n\"연인에게조차?\"\n\n\"네, 약혼녀에게조차 말하지 않았습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 당글라르군.\"\n\n\"이제 확신이 듭니다.\"\n\n\"잠깐만. 당글라르가 페르낭을 알고 있었나?\"\n\n\"아니요…… 아니, 알고 있었습니다. 이제 생각이 나는군요.\"\n\n\"무엇이?\"\n\n\"제 결혼식 전날 저녁, 두 사람이 팡필 영감네 정자 아래 탁자에 함께 앉아 있는 걸 본 적이 있습니다. 두 사람은 진지하게 이야기를 나누고 있었지요. 당글라르는 정답게 농을 던지고 있었지만, 페르낭은 창백하고 안절부절못하는 얼굴이었습니다.\"\n\n\"둘뿐이었나?\"\n\n\"제가 아주 잘 아는 세 번째 사람이 함께 있었습니다. 아마 그가 두 사람을 서로 소개했을 겁니다. 카드루스라는 재단사였는데, 몹시 취해 있었지요. 가만! 가만! 어째서 이게 진작 떠오르지 않았을까요! 이제 똑똑히 기억납니다. 그들이 둘러앉아 있던 탁자 위에 펜과 잉크와 종이가 놓여 있었습니다. 오, 그 매정하고 음흉한 악당들!\" 당테스가 욱신거리는 이마에 손을 갖다 대며 부르짖었다.\n\n\"자네 친구들의 악행 말고도, 내가 밝혀내는 걸 도와줄 일이 더 있겠나?\" 신부가 웃으며 물었다.\n\n\"네, 네.\" 당테스가 간절히 대답했다. \"사물의 밑바닥까지 환히 꿰뚫어 보시고, 더없이 큰 수수께끼도 쉬운 수수께끼처럼 푸시는 신부님께 부탁드립니다. 제가 어째서 두 번째 심문을 받지도 않았고, 재판에 회부되지도 않았으며, 무엇보다도 선고 한 번 받지 않고 형을 살게 되었는지, 그 까닭을 설명해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"그건 전혀 다른, 훨씬 더 심각한 문제일세.\" 신부가 대답했다. \"사법의 길이란 너무 어둡고 신비로워서 쉽게 꿰뚫어 보기 어려운 경우가 흔하지. 우리가 여태 이 일에서 한 것은 어린애 장난에 지나지 않아. 더 어려운 부분으로 들어가길 바란다면, 모든 점에 대해 더없이 세세한 정보로 나를 도와야 하네.\"\n\n\"무엇이든 물어보십시오. 사실 신부님은 저 자신보다 제 인생을 더 또렷이 보고 계시니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 우선, 누가 자네를 심문했나? 검사장인가, 그 대리인인가, 아니면 판사인가?\"\n\n\"대리인이었습니다.\"\n\n\"젊었나, 나이가 들었나?\"\n\n\"스물여섯이나 일곱쯤 됐을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그렇군.\" 신부가 대답했다. \"야심을 품을 만큼은 나이가 들었지만, 부패하기에는 너무 젊은 나이로군. 그가 자네를 어떻게 대했나?\"\n\n\"가혹하다기보다는 너그럽게 대했습니다.\"\n\n\"자네 이야기를 전부 했나?\"\n\n\"전부 했습니다.\"\n\"심문하는 동안 그의 태도가 조금이라도 변하던가?\"\n\n\"저를 이 곤경에 빠뜨린 편지를 읽었을 때는 무척 동요하는 듯 보였습니다. 제 불행에 완전히 마음이 무너진 것 같았지요.\"\n\n\"자네의 불행에?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"그럼 자네는 그가 한탄한 게 정말 자네의 불행이라고 확신하나?\"\n\n\"적어도 그는 제게 동정의 큰 증거를 하나 보여 주었습니다.\"\n\n\"그게 뭐지?\"\n\n\"저를 죄에 빠뜨릴 수 있는 유일한 증거를 태워 버렸습니다.\"\n\n\"뭐라고? 그 고발장을?\"\n\n\"아니요, 그 편지를요.\"\n\n\"확실한가?\"\n\n\"제 눈으로 직접 봤습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 사정이 달라지는군. 어쩌면 그자가 자네가 짐작하던 것보다 훨씬 더 큰 악당일지도 모르겠네.\"\n\n\"맹세코,\" 당테스가 말했다. \"신부님 말씀에 몸서리가 쳐집니다. 세상이 호랑이와 악어로 가득 차 있단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"그렇다네. 그리고 명심하게, 두 발 달린 호랑이와 악어가 다른 것들보다 더 위험한 법이야.\"\n\n\"상관없습니다. 계속하시지요.\"\n\n\"기꺼이! 그가 편지를 태웠다고 했지?\"\n\n\"네. 그러면서 동시에 이렇게 말했습니다. '보시오, 이렇게 당신에게 불리한 유일한 증거를 없애 드리는 거요.'\"\n\n\"그 행동은 자연스럽다기에는 너무 숭고하군.\"\n\n\"그렇게 보십니까?\"\n\n\"확신하네. 그 편지는 누구에게 보내는 것이었나?\"\n\n\"파리 코크에롱 거리 13번지의 누아르티에 씨에게요.\"\n\n\"자, 그 영웅적인 대리인이 그 편지를 없애서 얻을 만한 이득이 무엇이었을지 자네는 짐작이 가나?\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 전혀 없다고는 할 수 없습니다. 그는 그 편지에 대해 누구에게도 절대 말하지 말라고 여러 번 다짐을 받았으니까요. 다 저를 위해 하는 충고라고 하면서요. 게다가 그것도 모자라, 그 수신인에 적힌 이름을 절대 입에 올리지 않겠다고 엄숙히 맹세하라고 다그쳤습니다.\"\n\n\"누아르티에라!\" 신부가 되뇌었다. \"누아르티에! 나는 에트루리아 여왕의 궁정에서 그 이름을 가진 사람을 알았네. 혁명 때 지롱드파였던 누아르티에라는 자였지! 자네를 심문한 그 대리인의 이름이 뭐였나?\"\n\n\"드 빌포르였습니다!\" 신부는 와락 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 당테스는 어안이 벙벙해져 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"왜 그러십니까?\" 그가 마침내 물었다.\n\n\"저 햇살이 보이나?\"\n\"보입니다.\"\n\n\"음, 이 모든 일이 내게는 저 햇살이 자네에게 보이는 것보다 더 환하게 보이네. 가엾은 사람! 가엾은 젊은이! 그런데 자네는 그 판사가 자네에게 큰 동정과 연민을 보였다고 했지?\"\n\n\"그랬습니다.\"\n\n\"그 갸륵한 양반이 자네에게 불리한 편지를 없앴고?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"그러고는 누아르티에라는 이름을 절대 입에 올리지 않겠다고 맹세하게 했고?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"이런, 가엾고 눈먼 어수룩한 친구야, 그가 그토록 조심스레 숨기려 했던 그 누아르티에가 누구인지 짐작도 못 하겠나? 그 누아르티에가 바로 그의 아버지였네!\"\n\n벼락이 당테스의 발치에 떨어졌거나 지옥이 그의 앞에 아가리를 쩍 벌렸더라도, 이 뜻밖의 말을 들었을 때보다 더 완전히 공포에 사로잡히지는 않았을 것이다. 벌떡 일어선 그는 머릿속이 터져 나가는 것을 막으려는 듯 두 손으로 머리를 감싸 쥐며 부르짖었다. \"그의 아버지! 그의 아버지라고요!\"\n\n\"그래, 그의 아버지지.\" 신부가 대답했다. \"그자의 본명은 누아르티에 드 빌포르였네.\"\n\n이 순간, 한 줄기 밝은 빛이 당테스의 머릿속을 꿰뚫으며, 여태 어둡고 막연했던 모든 것을 환히 밝혀 주었다. 심문 도중 빌포르에게 일어난 변화, 편지를 없앤 일, 받아 낸 약속, 처벌을 선고한다기보다 차라리 자비를 간청하는 듯하던 그 판사의 애원하는 목소리까지, 모든 것이 충격적인 힘으로 그의 기억에 되살아났다. 그는 비명을 지르며 술 취한 사람처럼 벽에 비틀거리며 기댔다가, 신부의 독방에서 자기 방으로 통하는 입구로 서둘러 가서 말했다. \"혼자 있어야겠습니다. 이 모든 걸 곰곰이 생각해 봐야 해요.\"\n\n자기 감방으로 돌아온 그는 침대에 몸을 던졌고, 저녁 순찰을 돌던 간수가 그를 발견했을 때, 그는 시선을 한곳에 고정한 채 일그러진 얼굴로 앉아 있었다. 동상처럼 말 없고 미동도 없었다. 그에게는 그저 몇 분처럼 느껴진 그 깊은 사색의 시간 동안, 그는 무시무시한 결심을 굳혔고, 엄숙한 맹세로 그것을 반드시 이루리라 스스로 다짐했다.\n\n마침내 당테스는 파리아의 목소리에 상념에서 깨어났다. 파리아 역시 간수의 순찰을 받은 뒤, 함께 고통받는 벗을 저녁 식사에 초대하러 온 것이었다. 비록 해롭지 않고 심지어 재미있는 쪽이긴 했지만, 정신이 나갔다는 평판 덕분에 신부는 보기 드문 특권을 누리고 있었다. 그에게는 여느 감옥 식사보다 곱고 흰 빵이 나왔고, 일요일마다 약간의 포도주까지 곁들여졌다. 마침 그날이 일요일이었고, 신부는 젊은 벗에게 이 호사를 함께 누리자고 청하러 온 것이었다.\n\n당테스는 그를 따라갔다. 그의 얼굴은 더 이상 일그러져 있지 않았고, 이제 평소의 표정을 되찾고 있었다. 하지만 그의 온 모습에는 굳고 절박한 결심에 다다른 사람의 기색이 어려 있었다. 파리아는 꿰뚫는 듯한 눈길을 그에게 던졌다.\n\n\"이제 와 후회가 되는군.\" 그가 말했다. \"조금 전 자네 추궁을 도왔던 것이, 또 내가 그런 정보를 주었던 것이 말일세.\"\n\n\"어째서요?\" 당테스가 물었다.\n\n\"그것이 자네 마음에 새로운 열정을 심어 놓았으니까. 바로 복수라는 열정 말이야.\"\n\n당테스가 미소 지었다. \"다른 이야기를 하시지요.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n신부는 다시 한번 그를 바라보더니, 서글프게 고개를 저었다. 하지만 당테스의 청에 따라 다른 이야기를 꺼내기 시작했다. 이 나이 든 죄수는, 숱한 시련을 겪은 모든 이가 그러하듯, 그의 대화 속에 건전한 지식뿐 아니라 유익하고 중요한 가르침이 가득한 사람이었다. 하지만 그것은 결코 제 자랑이 아니었다. 이 불행한 사람은 자신의 슬픔을 결코 입에 올리지 않았기 때문이다. 당테스는 그가 하는 모든 말을 감탄 어린 주의를 기울여 들었다. 그의 말 가운데 더러는 당테스가 이미 알고 있던 것과 들어맞았고, 더러는 그가 뱃사람 생활로 익힌 지식과 통했다. 그러나 선량한 신부의 말 가운데 일부는 당테스로서는 도무지 이해할 수 없는 것이었다. 그럼에도 그 말들은, 북쪽 바다에서 항해사를 인도하는 오로라처럼, 알고자 하는 청자의 마음에 새로운 전망을 열어 주었다. 그리고 새 지평을 환상적으로 엿보게 해 주어, 파리아처럼 풍성한 재능을 지닌 사람이 마치 제집처럼 노니는 진리의 높은 곳을 따라가는 일에서, 지성을 갖춘 정신이 누릴 기쁨이 어떤 것인지를 그가 제대로 가늠할 수 있게 해 주었다.\n\n\"신부님이 아시는 것의 작은 부분이라도 제게 가르쳐 주십시오.\" 당테스가 말했다. \"신부님이 저를 지겨워하지 않으시도록 말입니다. 신부님처럼 학식 높은 분이라면, 저처럼 무지하고 배움 없는 자와 어울려 시달리느니 차라리 완전한 고독을 택하시리라는 걸 잘 압니다. 제 부탁만 들어주신다면, 다시는 탈출 이야기를 입에 올리지 않겠다고 약속드리겠습니다.\"\n\n신부가 미소 지었다.\n\n\"아아, 얘야,\" 그가 말했다. \"인간의 지식이란 아주 좁은 한계 안에 갇혀 있단다. 자네에게 수학과 물리, 역사, 그리고 내가 아는 서너 가지 현대어를 가르치고 나면, 자네는 나만큼 알게 될 걸세. 그런데 내가 가진 학문의 밑천을 자네에게 전하는 데 채 이 년도 걸리지 않을 거야.\"\n\n\"이 년이라고요!\" 당테스가 외쳤다. \"이 모든 것을 그렇게 짧은 기간에 익힐 수 있다고 정말 믿으십니까?\"\n\n\"그 응용까지야 물론 아니지만, 원리는 익힐 수 있지. 배우는 것이 곧 아는 것은 아니야. 배우는 자가 있고, 깨우친 자가 있지. 기억이 배우는 자를 만들고, 철학이 깨우친 자를 만드는 법일세.\"\n\n\"하지만 철학은 배울 수 없습니까?\"\n\n\"철학은 가르칠 수 있는 것이 아니야. 그것은 학문을 진리에 적용하는 것이지. 메시아가 하늘로 오를 때 타고 간 황금 구름과도 같은 것이라네.\"\n\n\"그럼 좋습니다.\" 당테스가 말했다. \"무엇부터 가르쳐 주시겠습니까? 어서 시작하고 싶습니다. 배우고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"전부 다.\" 신부가 말했다. 그리고 바로 그날 저녁, 두 죄수는 이튿날부터 시작할 학습 계획을 짰다. 당테스는 경이로운 기억력에다 놀라울 만큼 빠르고 민첩한 이해력을 두루 갖추고 있었다. 수학적인 머리 덕분에 온갖 계산에 능했고, 타고난 시적 감수성은 무미건조한 산수 계산이나 딱딱하고 엄정한 기하학 위에 가볍고 즐거운 베일을 드리워 주었다. 그는 이미 이탈리아어를 알았고, 동방으로의 항해 중에 로마이어도 조금 익혀 두었다. 이 두 언어의 도움으로 그는 다른 모든 언어의 짜임새를 쉽게 이해했고, 그리하여 여섯 달이 지날 무렵에는 스페인어, 영어, 독일어를 말하기 시작했다.\n\n신부와 한 약속을 철저히 지켜, 당테스는 더 이상 탈출 이야기를 입에 올리지 않았다. 어쩌면 공부가 안겨 주는 기쁨이 그런 생각이 들어설 자리를 남기지 않았는지도 모른다. 어쩌면 자신이 약속을 했다는 기억이(그는 명예에 대한 감각이 예민했다) 탈출 가능성을 어떤 식으로든 입에 올리지 못하게 막았는지도 모른다. 빠르고 유익한 한 줄기 과정 속에서 며칠, 아니 몇 달이 알지 못하는 사이에 흘러갔다. 일 년이 지날 무렵 당테스는 새사람이 되어 있었다. 그러나 당테스는, 파리아가 자신의 벗이 되어 주는 위안에도 불구하고 날이 갈수록 더 침울해지는 것을 알아챘다. 한 가지 생각이 끊임없이 그의 마음을 들볶고 어지럽히는 듯했다. 이따금 그는 긴 상념에 잠겨 무겁고 저도 모르게 한숨을 내쉬다가, 별안간 일어나 팔짱을 끼고 좁은 감방 안을 서성이곤 했다. 어느 날 그는 갑자기 걸음을 멈추더니 외쳤다.\n\n\"아, 저 보초만 없다면!\"\n\n\"신부님이 원하시는 순간, 보초는 단 일 분도 더 거기 있지 못할 겁니다.\" 당테스가 말했다. 그는 마치 신부의 머릿속이 가장 미세한 작동까지 들여다보일 만큼 투명한 수정으로 감싸여 있기라도 한 듯, 그 생각의 흐름을 정확히 따라가고 있었다.\n\n\"전에도 말했지만,\" 신부가 대답했다. \"나는 피를 흘리게 한다는 생각만으로도 진저리가 나네.\"\n\n\"하지만 그 살인은, 굳이 그렇게 부르신다면, 그저 자기 보존을 위한 조치일 뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"그래도 안 돼! 나는 결코 거기에 동의할 수 없네.\"\n\n\"그래도 그 생각을 해 보신 적은 있지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"끊임없이 했지, 안타깝게도!\" 신부가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그리고 우리가 자유를 되찾을 방법을 찾아내셨지요, 그렇지 않습니까?\" 당테스가 간절히 물었다.\n\n\"찾았네. 우리 너머 회랑에 귀먹고 눈먼 보초를 세워 둘 수만 있다면 말이지.\"\n\n\"그자를 눈멀고 귀먹게 만들겠습니다.\" 젊은이가 대답했다. 그 단호한 태도에 벗은 몸서리를 쳤다.\n\n\"안 돼, 안 돼.\" 신부가 외쳤다. \"그건 안 될 말이야!\"\n\n당테스는 그 이야기를 다시 꺼내려 했지만, 신부는 안 된다는 뜻으로 고개를 저었고, 더는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다. 석 달이 흘렀다.\n\n\"자네, 힘이 센가?\" 어느 날 신부가 당테스에게 물었다. 젊은이는 대답 대신 끌을 집어 들어 말굽 모양으로 구부렸다가, 다시 그만큼 손쉽게 곧게 폈다.\n\n\"그리고 최후의 수단이 아니고서는 보초에게 어떤 해도 끼치지 않겠다고 약속하겠나?\"\n\n\"명예를 걸고 약속합니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 신부가 말했다. \"우리 계획을 실행에 옮길 수 있겠군.\"\n\n\"그 일을 끝내는 데 얼마나 걸릴까요?\"\n\n\"적어도 일 년은.\"\n\n\"그럼 당장 시작할까요?\"\n\n\"당장.\"\n\n\"우리가 일 년을 헛되이 날렸군요!\" 당테스가 외쳤다.\n\n\"지난 열두 달이 헛되이 보낸 것이라 생각하나?\" 신부가 물었다.\n\n\"용서하십시오!\" 에드몽이 얼굴을 새빨갛게 붉히며 외쳤다.\n\n\"쯧쯧!\" 신부가 대답했다. \"인간은 결국 인간일 뿐이고, 자네는 내가 아는 한 그 종족 가운데 가장 훌륭한 본보기일세. 자, 내 계획을 보여 주지.\"\n\n그리하여 신부는 탈출을 위해 그려 둔 도면을 당테스에게 보여 주었다. 그것은 자신의 감방과 당테스의 감방, 그리고 그 둘을 잇는 통로를 그린 도면이었다. 그는 광산에서 하듯이 이 통로에 수평 갱도를 뚫을 작정이었다. 이 갱도는 두 죄수를 보초가 망을 보는 회랑 바로 밑까지 데려다줄 터였다. 일단 거기에 이르면, 큰 구덩이를 파고, 회랑 바닥에 깔린 포석 하나를 완전히 헐겁게 만들어, 원하는 순간에 그것이 병사의 발밑에서 꺼지게 만드는 것이었다. 그러면 떨어지면서 정신을 잃은 병사를, 그가 저항할 새도 없이 당테스가 곧바로 결박하고 재갈을 물릴 참이었다. 그런 다음 두 죄수는 회랑 창문 중 하나로 빠져나가, 신부의 밧줄 사다리를 이용해 바깥벽을 타고 내려갈 것이었다.\n\n당테스의 눈이 기쁨으로 반짝였고, 단순하면서도 보기에는 틀림없이 성공할 듯한 그 계획에 신이 나서 두 손을 비볐다. 바로 그날 광부들은 작업에 착수했고, 오랜 휴식으로 쌓인 기력과 끝내 성공하리라는 희망에 걸맞은 활력과 민첩함으로 임했다. 간수의 순찰에 대비해 각자 자기 감방으로 돌아가야 하는 것 말고는, 아무것도 작업의 진척을 방해하지 않았다. 그들은 간수가 그들의 감방을 향해 내려올 때 들리는, 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 희미한 발소리를 분간하는 법을 익혔고, 다행히 그가 올 때를 대비하지 못한 적이 한 번도 없었다. 이번 작업에서 파낸 새 흙은 옛 통로를 죄다 막아 버렸을 텐데, 파리아나 당테스의 감방 창문 밖으로 조금씩, 더없이 조심스럽게 내버렸다. 그 부스러기 흙을 먼저 아주 곱게 빻아 두어, 밤바람이 그것을 멀리 날려 보내, 아주 작은 흔적조차 남기지 않게 했다.\n\n이 작업에 일 년 넘는 시간이 들었는데, 연장이라고는 끌 하나, 칼 하나, 그리고 나무 지렛대 하나가 전부였다. 그동안에도 파리아는 당테스와 이야기를 나누며 그를 계속 가르쳤다. 어떤 때는 이 언어로, 어떤 때는 저 언어로, 또 어떤 때는 이따금 명성을 얻어 영광의 길을 걸은 민족들과 위인들의 역사를 들려주었다. 신부는 세상 물정에 밝은 사람이었고, 더구나 당대의 일류 사교계에 몸담은 적도 있었다. 그에게는 우수에 잠긴 기품 같은 것이 어려 있었는데, 당테스는 타고난 모방력 덕분에 그것을 쉽게 익혔다. 또한 전에는 갖추지 못했던 외양의 세련됨과 예의범절도 익혔는데, 그것은 높은 가문과 좋은 교양을 지닌 사람들과 끊임없이 어울려 본 자라야 좀처럼 지닐 수 있는 것이었다.\n\n열다섯 달이 지날 무렵 수평 갱도가 완성되었고, 회랑 밑의 구덩이도 다 파였다. 두 일꾼은 보초가 그들의 머리 위를 오가며 내딛는 일정한 발소리를 또렷이 들을 수 있었다. 탈출에 알맞은 어두운 밤을 기다려야 하는 처지였기에, 그 좋은 순간이 올 때까지 마지막 결행을 미룰 수밖에 없었다. 이제 그들의 가장 큰 두려움은, 보초가 떨어지게 되어 있는 그 돌이 제때보다 먼저 무너지지나 않을까 하는 것이었다. 그래서 그들은 작업하며 파고든 벽에서 찾아낸 작은 들보로 그 돌을 받쳐, 어느 정도 대비를 해 두었다. 당테스가 이 나무토막을 손보고 있을 때, 밧줄 사다리를 고정할 못을 깎으려고 에드몽의 감방에 남아 있던 파리아가 큰 고통이 묻어나는 목소리로 그를 부르는 소리를 들었다. 당테스는 서둘러 그의 감방으로 달려갔고, 거기서 방 한가운데 서 있는 그를 발견했다. 죽은 사람처럼 창백하고, 이마에서는 땀이 줄줄 흐르고, 두 손은 꽉 움켜쥐고 있었다.\n\n\"맙소사!\" 당테스가 외쳤다. \"무슨 일입니까? 무슨 일이 있었던 거예요?\"\n\n\"빨리! 빨리!\" 신부가 대답했다. \"내 말을 잘 듣게.\"\n\n당테스는 두려움과 놀라움에 차서 파리아의 새파랗게 질린 얼굴을 들여다보았다. 이미 흐릿하고 움푹 꺼진 그의 두 눈은 자줏빛 테두리에 둘러싸여 있었고, 입술은 시체의 것처럼 하얬으며, 머리카락마저 곤두서는 듯했다.\n\n\"제발, 무엇이 잘못된 건지 말씀해 주세요!\" 당테스가 끌을 바닥에 떨어뜨리며 외쳤다.\n\n\"아아,\" 신부가 더듬더듬 말했다. \"나는 이제 끝났네. 무서운 병에, 어쩌면 목숨을 앗아 갈 병에 사로잡혔어. 발작이 빠르게 다가오는 게 느껴진다네. 갇히기 전해에도 비슷한 발작을 겪었지. 이 병에는 단 하나의 약밖에 없어. 그게 뭔지 말해 주겠네. 되도록 빨리 내 감방으로 가게. 침대를 받치는 다리 하나를 빼내 보게. 그 안이 일부러 비워져 있어서, 거기 작은 약병이 보일 걸세. 불그스름한 액체가 반쯤 차 있지. 그걸 내게 가져오게나. 아니, 그보다는, 안 돼, 안 돼! 여기서 들킬 수도 있어. 그러니 내가 몸을 끌고 갈 힘이 남아 있는 동안 나를 내 방으로 데려가 주게. 무슨 일이 일어날지, 발작이 얼마나 갈지 누가 알겠나?\"\n\n이렇게 갑작스레 자신의 희망이 좌절되는 크나큰 불행 앞에서도 당테스는 침착함을 잃지 않았다. 그는 불행한 벗을 끌며 통로로 내려갔다. 그러고는 반쯤 안고 반쯤 부축해 가까스로 신부의 방에 이르렀고, 곧바로 환자를 침대에 눕혔다.\n\n\"고맙네.\" 가엾은 신부가 핏줄에 얼음이라도 가득 찬 듯 부들부들 떨며 말했다. \"이제 곧 강경증 발작이 일어날 거야. 그게 절정에 이르면, 나는 아마 죽은 사람처럼 미동도 없이 누운 채, 한숨 한 번 신음 한 번 내지 못하겠지. 반대로 증상이 훨씬 더 격렬해서, 무서운 경련을 일으키고 입에 거품을 물고 크게 비명을 지를 수도 있어. 내 비명이 들리지 않도록 조심하게. 만일 들리면, 십중팔구 나는 감옥 다른 곳으로 옮겨질 테고, 우리는 영영 헤어지게 될 테니까. 내가 완전히 미동도 없이, 차갑고 뻣뻣하게 시체처럼 굳어지면, 그때, 그 전에는 절대 안 되네, 이 점을 명심하게, 칼로 내 이를 억지로 벌리고, 약병에 든 약을 여덟에서 열 방울쯤 목구멍에 흘려 넣게. 그러면 어쩌면 내가 다시 깨어날지도 몰라.\"\n\n\"어쩌면이라니요!\" 당테스가 비통한 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"사람 살려! 사람 살려!\" 신부가 부르짖었다. \"나, 나는, 죽는다, 나는……\"\n\n발작이 어찌나 갑작스럽고 격렬한지, 불행한 죄수는 그 문장을 채 끝맺지 못했다. 격렬한 경련이 그의 온몸을 뒤흔들었고, 두 눈이 눈구멍에서 튀어나올 듯했으며, 입은 한쪽으로 비뚤어지고, 두 뺨은 자줏빛으로 물들었다. 그는 발버둥치고, 거품을 물고, 몸을 마구 내던지며, 더없이 끔찍한 비명을 질러 댔다. 그러나 당테스가 담요로 그의 머리를 덮어, 그 소리가 새어 나가지 못하게 막았다. 발작은 두 시간 동안 이어졌다. 그런 뒤 그는 갓난아기보다 더 무력하게, 대리석보다 더 차갑고 창백하게, 발에 짓밟힌 갈대보다 더 으스러지고 부러진 채, 마지막 경련에 몸을 잔뜩 웅크리며 뒤로 쓰러졌다. 그리고 시체처럼 뻣뻣하게 굳어졌다.\n\n에드몽은 벗의 몸에서 생명이 다한 듯 보일 때까지 기다렸다가, 칼을 집어 들고 굳게 다물린 턱을 가까스로 벌렸다. 그러고는 정해진 방울 수만큼 조심스레 약을 흘려 넣고, 애타게 결과를 기다렸다. 한 시간이 지났지만 노인은 다시 살아난다는 기미를 전혀 보이지 않았다. 당테스는 약을 너무 늦게 먹인 것은 아닐까 두려워지기 시작했고, 머리카락에 두 손을 찔러 넣은 채 벗의 죽은 듯한 얼굴을 계속 들여다보았다. 마침내 새파랗던 두 뺨에 옅은 핏기가 돌고, 흐릿하게 뜬 눈동자에 의식이 되돌아왔으며, 입술에서 가느다란 한숨이 새어 나왔다. 그리고 환자는 가까스로 몸을 움직이려 애썼다.\n\n\"살았다! 살았어!\" 당테스가 미칠 듯한 기쁨에 차서 외쳤다.\n\n병자는 아직 말을 할 수 없었지만, 역력한 불안을 담아 문 쪽을 가리켰다. 당테스가 귀를 기울이자, 간수가 다가오는 발소리가 또렷이 들렸다. 그러니 일곱 시가 가까웠던 것이다. 그러나 에드몽은 불안에 사로잡혀 시간 따위는 까맣게 잊고 있었다.\n\n젊은이는 입구로 뛰어가 그 사이로 빠져나간 뒤, 조심스레 돌을 끌어다 구멍을 덮고 서둘러 자기 감방으로 돌아갔다. 그가 그러기 무섭게 문이 열렸고, 간수는 죄수가 여느 때처럼 침대 한쪽에 앉아 있는 것을 보았다. 자물쇠에서 열쇠가 돌아가기가 무섭게, 간수가 거쳐 가야 할 긴 복도에서 그 멀어지는 발소리가 채 잦아들기도 전에, 벗에 대한 들끓는 불안 탓에 가져다준 음식에는 손댈 마음도 없던 당테스는 서둘러 신부의 방으로 돌아갔다. 그러고는 돌에 머리를 들이대 밀어 올리고, 이내 병자의 침상 곁에 다다랐다. 파리아는 이제 의식을 완전히 되찾았지만, 여전히 비참한 침대 위에 무력하고 기진맥진한 채 누워 있었다.\n\n\"자네를 다시 보게 될 줄은 몰랐네.\" 그가 힘없이 당테스에게 말했다.\n\n\"어째서요?\" 젊은이가 물었다. \"신부님이 돌아가시는 줄 아셨습니까?\"\n\n\"아니, 그런 생각은 아니었네. 다만 탈출 준비가 다 되어 있다는 걸 아니까, 자네가 도망쳤을지도 모른다고 생각했지.\"\n\n당테스의 두 뺨에 분노의 진한 홍조가 번졌다.\n\n\"신부님을 두고요? 정말 제가 그럴 사람이라고 생각하셨습니까?\"\n\n\"적어도,\" 신부가 말했다. \"그런 생각이 얼마나 잘못된 것이었는지 이제 알겠네. 아아, 아아! 이번 발작으로 나는 무섭도록 기진하고 쇠약해졌네.\"\n\n\"기운을 내십시오.\" 당테스가 대답했다. \"기력은 되돌아올 겁니다.\" 그렇게 말하며 그는 침대 곁 파리아 옆에 앉아 그의 두 손을 잡았다. 신부가 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"지난번 발작은,\" 그가 말했다. \"겨우 반 시간밖에 가지 않았네. 그러고 나서는 배가 고팠고, 부축 없이도 일어났지. 한데 지금은 오른팔도 오른다리도 움직일 수가 없고, 머릿속이 거북한 게, 뇌에 피가 번졌다는 뜻이야. 세 번째 발작이 오면 나는 죽거나, 아니면 평생 마비된 채로 남을 걸세.\"\n\n\"아니요, 아니요.\" 당테스가 외쳤다. \"잘못 생각하시는 겁니다. 신부님은 죽지 않으실 거예요! 그리고 세 번째 발작이(정말 또 온다 하더라도) 닥칠 때, 신부님은 자유로운 몸이실 겁니다. 이번처럼 우리가 다시 신부님을 구할 거예요. 다만 그때는 더 큰 성공의 가망을 안고서요. 필요한 모든 도움을 마음대로 부를 수 있을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"착한 에드몽,\" 신부가 대답했다. \"착각하지 말게. 방금 지나간 이 발작이 나를 영영 감옥 벽 안에 가두었네. 걷지 못하는 자는 누구도 감옥에서 달아날 수 없는 법이야.\"\n\n\"그럼 기다리지요. 한 주든, 한 달이든, 필요하다면 두 달이든요. 그동안 신부님 기력은 되돌아올 겁니다. 탈출 채비는 다 갖춰져 있으니, 우리가 원하는 때를 고르면 됩니다. 신부님이 헤엄칠 수 있다고 느껴지자마자 떠나면 돼요.\"\n\n\"나는 다시는 헤엄치지 못할 걸세.\" 파리아가 대답했다. \"이 팔은 마비됐어. 한동안이 아니라 영영 말이야. 들어 보게, 내가 잘못 짚은 건지 판단해 보게.\"\n\n젊은이가 그 팔을 들어 올리자, 팔은 제 무게에 못 이겨 도로 툭 떨어졌다. 완전히 생기를 잃고 무력했다. 그의 입에서 한숨이 새어 나왔다.\n\n\"이제 자네도 수긍이 가지, 에드몽, 그렇지 않은가?\" 신부가 물었다. \"내 말을 믿게, 나는 내가 무슨 말을 하는지 알고 있네. 이 병으로 첫 발작을 겪은 뒤로, 나는 끊임없이 그것을 곱씹어 왔어. 사실 나는 이미 예상하고 있었지. 이건 집안 내력이거든. 내 아버지도 할아버지도 세 번째 발작에 돌아가셨으니까. 내가 두 번이나 효험을 본 그 약을 지어 준 의사는 다름 아닌 저 유명한 카바니스였는데, 그도 내게 같은 최후를 예언했다네.\"\n\n\"의사가 틀릴 수도 있지 않습니까!\" 당테스가 외쳤다. \"그리고 그 가엾은 팔이야, 그게 무슨 문제가 되겠습니까? 제가 신부님을 어깨에 짊어지고, 우리 둘 몫을 헤엄치면 됩니다.\"\n\n\"얘야,\" 신부가 말했다. \"뱃사람이자 헤엄꾼인 자네라면 나만큼이나 잘 알 게 아닌가. 그렇게 짐을 진 사람은 채 쉰 번도 팔을 젓기 전에 가라앉고 만다는 걸 말이야. 그러니 자네의 그 갸륵한 마음조차 믿으려 들지 않는 헛된 희망에 더는 자네 자신을 속이지 말게. 나는 풀려날 시간이 올 때까지 여기 남을 걸세. 그리고 그 시간이란, 인간으로서 헤아릴 수 있는 모든 가망으로 보아, 내가 죽는 시간일 테지. 젊고 활기찬 자네는, 나 때문에 지체하지 말고 달아나게, 떠나게. 자네 약속은 내가 도로 거두어 주겠네.\"\n\n\"좋습니다.\" 당테스가 말했다. \"그렇다면 저도 남겠습니다.\" 그러고는 일어나, 엄숙한 기색으로 노인의 머리 위로 손을 뻗으며 천천히 덧붙였다. \"그리스도의 피를 걸고 맹세합니다. 신부님이 살아 계신 한 결코 곁을 떠나지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n파리아는 고결한 마음과 한결같은 마음, 드높은 신념을 지닌 젊은 벗을 애정 어린 눈으로 바라보았고, 그 얼굴에서 헌신의 진심과 결의의 충직함을 넉넉히 확인했다.\n\n\"고맙네.\" 병자가 한 손을 내밀며 나직이 말했다. \"그 마음 받아들이지. 언젠가 자네는 그 사심 없는 헌신의 보답을 거두게 될지도 몰라. 한데 나는 이곳을 떠날 수 없고 자네는 떠나려 하지 않으니, 병사의 회랑 밑에 판 구덩이를 메워야겠네. 그자가 어쩌다 제 발소리가 텅 비게 울리는 걸 듣고, 그 일을 상관에게 알릴지도 모르니까. 그러면 발각될 테고, 그러면 우리는 틀림없이 헤어지게 될 거야. 자, 가서 그 일에 착수하게. 안타깝게도 그 일에는 내가 도움을 줄 수 없네. 필요하다면 밤새 매달리게. 그리고 내일은 간수가 나를 다녀간 뒤에야 이리로 돌아오게. 자네에게 더없이 중요한 이야기를 들려줄 게 있으니.\"\n\n당테스는 신부의 손을 잡아 애정 어린 마음으로 꼭 쥐었다. 파리아가 그에게 격려하듯 미소 지었고, 젊은이는 나이 든 벗에게 보이겠다고 맹세한 순종과 존경의 마음으로 자신의 일을 하러 물러갔다.\n\n# Chapter 18. The Treasure\n\n이튿날 아침, 당테스가 함께 갇혀 있는 동료의 방으로 돌아왔을 때, 파리아는 자리에 앉아 차분한 얼굴을 하고 있었다. 감방의 좁은 창으로 들어오는 한 줄기 빛 속에서, 그는—기억하다시피 그에게 쓸 수 있는 손이라곤 그것뿐인 왼손에—종이 한 장을 펼쳐 들고 있었다. 종이는 작게 둘둘 말려 있던 탓에 원통 모양이 되어 좀처럼 펼친 채로 두기가 어려웠다. 그는 아무 말 없이 종이를 당테스에게 내밀어 보였다.\n\n\"이게 뭡니까?\" 당테스가 물었다.\n\n\"한번 보게.\" 신부가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"있는 힘껏 주의를 기울여 보았습니다.\" 당테스가 말했다. \"하지만 반쯤 타 버린 종이밖에 보이지 않는군요. 거기에 묘한 잉크로 쓴 고딕체 글자의 흔적이 남아 있고요.\"\n\n\"이 종이가 말일세, 친구.\" 파리아가 말했다. \"이제는 자네에게 털어놓아도 되겠지. 자네의 충실함을 확인했으니 말이야. 이 종이가 바로 내 보물일세. 그리고 오늘부터 그 절반은 자네 것이지.\"\n\n당테스의 이마에 땀이 배어났다. 오늘 이날까지—그것도 얼마나 오랜 세월 동안!—그는 보물 이야기를 입에 올리지 않으려 애써 왔다. 바로 그 보물이 신부에게 미치광이라는 누명을 씌운 것이었다. 에드몽은 타고난 섬세함으로 그 아픈 자리를 건드리지 않으려 했고, 파리아 역시 똑같이 입을 다물어 왔다. 에드몽은 노인의 침묵을 이성의 회복으로 여겼다. 그런데 이토록 고통스러운 발작이 지나간 뒤에 파리아가 내뱉은 이 몇 마디 말은, 정신착란으로의 심각한 재발을 암시하는 듯했다.\n\n\"보물이라고요?\" 당테스가 더듬거렸다. 파리아가 미소 지었다.\n\n\"그래.\" 그가 말했다. \"에드몽, 자네는 정말 고결한 천성을 지녔어. 자네의 창백한 얼굴과 동요를 보니 지금 자네 마음속에 무슨 생각이 오가는지 알겠네. 아니야, 안심하게. 나는 미치지 않았어. 이 보물은 실제로 있다네, 당테스. 그리고 내가 그것을 가질 수 없게 된다면, 자네가 갖게 될 걸세. 그래—자네가 말이야. 아무도 내 말을 들어주거나 믿어 주지 않았지. 모두 나를 미친 사람으로 여겼으니까. 하지만 내가 미치지 않았다는 걸 분명히 아는 자네는, 내 말을 들어 보게. 그러고 나서도 그렇게 믿고 싶다면 그때 미쳤다 여기게나.\"\n\n\"아아.\" 에드몽이 속으로 중얼거렸다. '끔찍한 재발이로구나! 이 일격만이 남아 있던 거였어.' 그러고는 소리 내어 말했다. \"친애하는 친구여, 발작 때문에 지치셨을지도 모릅니다. 잠시 쉬시는 게 어떻겠습니까? 원하신다면 내일 그 이야기를 듣겠습니다. 하지만 오늘은 정성껏 간병해 드리고 싶군요. 게다가,\" 그가 말했다. \"보물이라는 건 서둘러야 할 일도 아니지 않습니까.\"\n\n\"오히려 그 반대일세, 에드몽. 이건 더없이 중대한 문제야!\" 노인이 대답했다. \"내일이나 모레, 세 번째 발작이 닥치지 않으리란 법이 어디 있겠나? 그러면 모든 게 끝나 버리는 게 아닌가? 그래, 정말이지 나는 쓰디쓴 기쁨에 잠겨 종종 이런 생각을 했네. 열두 가문을 부유하게 만들 이 재물이, 나를 박해한 자들에게서 영원히 사라지리라고 말이야. 이 생각은 내게 복수와도 같았고, 나는 지하 감옥의 어둠 속에서, 갇힌 자의 절망 속에서 그것을 천천히 음미했네. 하지만 이제 나는 자네를 사랑하는 마음으로 세상을 용서했어. 이제 젊고 앞날이 창창한 자네를 보고 있으니, 이런 사실을 털어놓는 행운으로 자네에게 돌아갈 모든 것을 생각하니, 조금이라도 지체하는 게 두렵네. 자네처럼 마땅한 사람에게 그토록 막대한 숨은 재물의 소유를 보장해 주지 못할까 봐 떨린다네.\"\n\n에드몽은 한숨을 내쉬며 고개를 돌렸다.\n\n\"자네는 끝내 믿지 못하는군, 에드몽.\" 파리아가 말을 이었다. \"내 말이 자네를 설득하지 못한 거야. 증거가 필요하다는 걸 알겠네. 그렇다면 이 종이를 읽어 보게. 지금껏 누구에게도 보여 준 적이 없는 거라네.\"\n\n\"내일 읽겠습니다, 친애하는 친구여.\" 노인의 광기에 굴복하고 싶지 않았던 에드몽이 말했다. \"내일까지는 그 이야기를 하지 않기로 한 줄 알았습니다만.\"\n\n\"그럼 내일까지 이야기는 하지 않세. 하지만 이 종이만은 오늘 읽어 보게.\"\n\n'노인을 자극하진 말아야지.' 에드몽은 그렇게 생각하며, 절반이 사라진—틀림없이 어떤 사고로 타 버렸을—종이를 받아 들고 읽어 내려갔다.\n\n\"이 보물은, 이백만에 이를 수도……\n\n로마 화폐로 가장 먼 곳……\n\n두 번째 입구에서 ……\n\n그에게만 속한다고 선언하……\n\n상속인.\n\n1498년 4월 25일\"\n\n\"어떤가!\" 청년이 다 읽고 나자 파리아가 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄요.\" 당테스가 대답했다. \"끊긴 줄과 이어지지 않는 낱말들밖에 보이지 않습니다. 불에 타서 알아볼 수가 없는걸요.\"\n\n\"그래, 처음 읽는 자네에게는 그렇겠지. 하지만 여러 밤을 그 위에서 창백해지도록 연구하며 모든 구절을 다시 짜 맞추고 모든 생각을 완성한 내게는 그렇지 않다네.\"\n\n\"그래서 숨겨진 뜻을 알아내셨다는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"확실히 알아냈네. 자네가 직접 판단해 보게. 하지만 우선 이 종이의 내력을 들어 보게나.\"\n\n\"쉿!\" 당테스가 외쳤다. \"발소리가 다가옵니다. 가 보겠습니다. 안녕히 계십시오!\"\n\n친구의 정신이 불안정하다는 믿음을 굳혀 줄 게 뻔한 그 내력과 설명에서 벗어나게 된 게 다행스러워, 당테스는 뱀처럼 좁은 통로를 미끄러져 나갔다. 한편 놀란 덕분에 어느 정도 기력을 되찾은 파리아는 발로 돌을 밀어 제자리에 끼워 넣고, 더 확실히 발각을 피하려고 그 위에 거적을 덮었다.\n\n찾아온 이는 소장이었다. 간수에게서 파리아가 아프다는 말을 듣고 직접 보러 온 것이었다.\n\n파리아는 그를 맞으려 몸을 일으키되, 자신을 이미 절반쯤 죽음으로 몰아넣은 마비를 소장에게 감추려고 모든 몸짓을 삼갔다. 소장이 연민에 마음이 움직여 더 나은 거처로 옮기라 명하고, 그리하여 자신을 젊은 동료에게서 떼어 놓을까 두려웠던 것이다. 그러나 다행히 그런 일은 없었고, 소장은 자신이 마음속으로 일종의 애정을 품고 있던 그 가엾은 미치광이가 가벼운 병을 앓을 뿐이라고 확신한 채 그를 떠났다.\n\n그동안 에드몽은 침대에 앉아 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 흩어진 생각들을 가다듬으려 애썼다. 파리아는 처음 알게 된 이래로 모든 점에서 그토록 이성적이고 논리적이며, 실로 놀라울 만큼 명민했다. 그래서 그 모든 분야에 걸친 그토록 깊은 지혜가 어떻게 광기와 한데 어울릴 수 있는지 도무지 이해할 수 없었다. 파리아가 자기 보물에 관해 속고 있는 것인가, 아니면 온 세상이 파리아에 관해 속고 있는 것인가?\n\n당테스는 온종일 감방에 머물며 감히 친구에게 돌아가지 못했다. 그렇게 함으로써, 신부가 미쳤다는 확신을 끝내 굳히게 될 순간을 미루려 한 것이었다. 그런 확신은 너무도 끔찍할 테니까!\n\n그러나 저녁 무렵, 여느 때의 방문 시각이 지났는데도 청년이 나타나지 않자, 파리아는 둘을 갈라놓은 거리를 몸소 움직여 건너 보려 했다. 노인이 몸을 끌고 오느라 애쓰는 고통스러운 소리를 듣고 에드몽은 몸서리쳤다. 노인의 한쪽 다리는 움직이지 못했고, 한쪽 팔도 더는 쓸 수 없었다. 에드몽은 그를 도와줄 수밖에 없었다. 그러지 않으면 당테스의 방으로 통하는 작은 구멍으로 노인은 들어올 수 없었을 테니까.\n\n\"자, 이렇게 나는 끈질기게 자네를 쫓아왔네.\" 노인이 인자한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"내 후한 선물에서 달아날 줄 알았겠지만 헛수고일세. 내 말을 들어 보게.\"\n\n에드몽은 벗어날 길이 없음을 깨닫고, 노인을 침대에 눕힌 뒤 그 곁의 걸상에 앉았다.\n\n\"자네도 알다시피,\" 신부가 말했다. \"나는 그 가문의 마지막 군주였던 스파다 추기경의 비서이자 가까운 벗이었네. 내가 누린 모든 행복은 이 훌륭한 분 덕분이었지. 비록 그분 가문의 재산이 속담이 될 만큼 유명해서 '스파다처럼 부유하다'는 말을 자주 듣긴 했지만, 정작 그분은 부유하지 않으셨네. 그분도, 세상의 소문도 그 부의 명성으로 살아갔던 셈이지. 그분의 궁전은 내겐 천국이었네. 나는 그분의 조카들을 가르치는 가정교사였는데, 그 조카들은 다 세상을 떠났지. 그리고 그분이 세상에 홀로 남으셨을 때, 나는 그분의 뜻에 절대적으로 헌신함으로써, 십 년간 한결같은 친절로 내게 베풀어 주신 모든 것에 보답하려 애썼다네. 추기경 댁에는 내게 비밀이라곤 없었어. 나는 고귀한 후원자께서 옛 책에 주를 달고, 먼지 쌓인 집안 필사본들 사이를 열심히 뒤지시는 모습을 자주 보았네. 어느 날, 내가 그 헛된 탐색을 두고 그분을 나무라며, 그 뒤에 찾아오는 정신의 쇠진을 안타까워하자, 그분은 나를 바라보며 쓸쓸히 미소 짓더니 로마시의 역사에 관한 책 한 권을 펼치셨네. 거기, 교황 알렉산데르 6세의 생애를 다룬 스무 번째 장에 다음과 같은 구절이 있었는데, 나는 그 구절을 결코 잊을 수가 없네.\n\n\"'로마냐의 큰 전쟁들이 끝났다. 정복을 마친 체사레 보르자는 온 이탈리아를 사들이기 위해 돈이 필요했다. 교황 또한 최근의 패배에도 여전히 만만찮았던 프랑스 왕 루이 12세와의 문제를 매듭짓기 위해 돈이 필요했다. 따라서 어떤 수익성 있는 방책에 기대지 않을 수 없었는데, 피폐해진 이탈리아의 궁핍한 형편에서 이는 무척 어려운 일이었다. 교황 성하께 한 가지 묘안이 떠올랐다. 그는 추기경 둘을 임명하기로 했다.'\n\n\"로마의 가장 큰 인물 둘을, 특히 부유한 자들을 고름으로써—바로 이것이 교황 성하께서 노린 보답이었네. 우선 그는 그 추기경들이 이미 가지고 있던 큰 직책과 화려한 관직들을 팔 수 있었지. 그리고 그 밖에도 추기경 모자 두 개를 팔 수 있었고. 또 세 번째 노림수가 있었는데, 그건 나중에 드러날 걸세.\n\n\"교황과 체사레 보르자는 먼저 미래의 추기경 두 사람을 골랐네. 교황청의 최고위직 가운데 넷을 차지하고 있던 조반니 로스피글리오시와, 로마 귀족 중에서도 가장 고귀하고 부유한 자 가운데 하나였던 체사레 스파다였지. 둘 다 교황으로부터 그런 은총을 받는 것을 더없는 영광으로 여겼네. 두 사람은 야심가였고, 체사레 보르자는 곧 그들의 직책을 살 사람들을 찾아냈지. 그 결과 로스피글리오시와 스파다는 추기경이 되기 위해 돈을 치렀고, 다른 여덟 사람은 두 추기경이 승진하기 전에 가지고 있던 관직들을 사려고 돈을 치렀네. 그리하여 팔십만 크라운이 그 투기꾼들의 금고로 들어갔지.\n\n\"이제 이 투기의 마지막 단계로 넘어갈 때가 됐네. 교황은 로스피글리오시와 스파다에게 온갖 호의를 베풀고 추기경의 표장을 내려 주었으며, 그들에게 신변을 정리하고 로마에 거처를 두라고 권했네. 그런 다음 교황과 체사레 보르자는 두 추기경을 만찬에 초대했지. 이건 교황 성하와 그 아들 사이에 의견이 갈린 문제였네. 체사레는 자신이 친구들을 위해 늘 준비해 두던 수단 가운데 하나를 쓸 수 있으리라 여겼지. 우선 그 유명한 열쇠가 있었네. 특정한 사람들에게 그것을 건네며 가서 지정된 찬장을 열어 보라고 청하는 것이었지. 이 열쇠에는 작은 쇠바늘이 달려 있었네. 자물쇠공의 부주의인 척했지만 말이야. 자물쇠가 빡빡한 그 찬장을 열려고 열쇠를 누르면, 그 사람은 그 작은 바늘에 찔렸고 이튿날 죽었지. 그리고 사자 머리가 새겨진 반지도 있었네. 체사레는 친구들을 악수로 맞이하고 싶을 때 그것을 끼었지. 사자는 그 영광스러운 손을 물었고, 스물네 시간이 지나면 그 물린 상처는 치명적이었네.\n\n\"체사레는 추기경들에게 찬장을 열어 보라고 하거나 악수를 청하자고 아버지에게 제안했네. 하지만 알렉산데르 6세는 이렇게 답했지. '저 훌륭한 추기경들, 스파다와 로스피글리오시 말이다, 둘 다 만찬에 청하기로 하자. 어쩐지 그 돈을 되찾을 것 같은 예감이 드는구나. 게다가 너는 잊고 있구나, 체사레. 체기는 곧바로 나타나지만, 바늘이나 물린 상처는 하루 이틀의 지체를 부른다는 걸 말이다.' 체사레는 그토록 설득력 있는 논리 앞에 물러섰고, 그리하여 추기경들은 만찬에 초대되었네.\n\n\"식탁은 산피에르다레나 부근에 있는 교황 소유의 포도밭에 차려졌네. 추기경들도 소문으로 잘 알고 있던 매혹적인 별장이었지. 새 지위에 잔뜩 우쭐해진 로스피글리오시는 좋은 식욕과 더없이 비위 맞추는 태도로 갔네. 신중한 사람인 데다 더없이 촉망받는 젊은 대위인 외동 조카를 무척 아끼던 스파다는, 종이와 펜을 들어 유언장을 작성했네. 그러고는 조카에게 포도밭 근처에서 자신을 기다리라는 전갈을 보냈지. 하지만 하인이 그를 찾지 못한 모양이었네.\n\n\"스파다는 그런 초대가 무엇을 뜻하는지 알고 있었네. 그토록 두드러지게 문명을 일으킨 기독교가 로마에서 진보한 이래로, 폭군의 명을 받아 '체사레가 그대의 죽음을 원한다'는 전갈을 들고 오는 건 더 이상 백인대장이 아니었지. 입가에 미소를 띤 채 와서 교황의 말을 전하는 특사였네. '성하께서 그대에게 함께 식사하기를 청하십니다.'\n\n\"스파다는 두 시쯤 산피에르다레나로 출발했네. 교황이 그를 기다리고 있었지. 스파다의 눈에 가장 먼저 들어온 것은, 성장을 차려입은 그의 조카와, 그에게 더없이 두드러진 호의를 베풀고 있는 체사레 보르자의 모습이었네. 스파다는 새파랗게 질렸지. 체사레가 비웃는 듯한 기색으로 그를 바라보았는데, 그것은 그가 모든 것을 미리 내다보았으며 덫이 잘 펼쳐졌음을 보여 주는 것이었네.\n\n\"만찬이 시작되었고, 스파다는 가까스로 조카에게 자신의 전갈을 받았는지 물을 수 있었네. 조카는 못 받았다고 대답했지. 그 질문의 뜻을 완벽히 알아차린 채로 말이야. 하지만 이미 늦었네. 그가 교황의 집사가 일부러 그를 위해 따라 둔 훌륭한 포도주 한 잔을 이미 마셔 버린 뒤였으니까. 바로 그 순간 스파다는 또 다른 술병이 자기 쪽으로 다가오는 것을 보았고, 맛보라는 권유를 받았네. 한 시간 뒤 의사 하나가 두 사람이 버섯을 먹고 독에 중독되었다고 선언했지. 스파다는 포도밭 문턱에서 숨을 거두었고, 조카는 제집 문간에서 아내가 알아듣지 못하는 손짓을 하며 숨을 거두었네.\n\n\"그러자 체사레와 교황은 죽은 자의 서류를 찾는다는 구실로 서둘러 유산에 손을 댔네. 하지만 그 유산이라는 건 오직 이것뿐이었지. 스파다가 적어 둔 종이쪽지 한 장이었네. '나는 사랑하는 조카에게 내 금고와 내 책들을, 그리고 그 가운데서도 특히 금박 모서리를 두른 내 기도서를 남긴다. 부디 다정한 숙부를 기억하며 그것을 간직해 주기를 바란다.'\n\n\"상속자들은 곳곳을 뒤졌고, 그 기도서에 감탄했으며, 가구에 손을 댔네. 그러고는 부자였던 스파다가 실은 가장 비참한 숙부였다는 사실에 크게 놀랐지. 보물이라곤 없었던 거야—서재와 실험실에 담긴 학문의 보물이라면 또 모를까. 그게 전부였네. 체사레와 그 아버지는 찾고, 살피고, 샅샅이 뒤졌지만 아무것도, 아니 적어도 변변한 건 찾지 못했네. 금붙이로 몇천 크라운, 현금으로 그만한 액수를 넘지 않았지. 하지만 조카는 숨을 거두기 전에 아내에게 이렇게 말할 틈이 있었네. '숙부의 서류를 잘 살펴보시오. 거기 유언장이 있소.'\n\n\"그들은 고귀한 상속자들보다 더 철저히 뒤졌지만 헛수고였네. 팔라티노 언덕 뒤편에 궁전 두 채와 포도밭 하나가 있었지. 하지만 그 시절엔 토지 재산이 그리 값나가지 않았고, 그 두 궁전과 포도밭은 교황과 그 아들의 탐욕을 끌 만한 것이 못 되었기에 가문에 남았네. 달이 가고 해가 흘렀지. 알렉산데르 6세는 독에 중독되어 죽었네—무슨 착오로 그리됐는지는 자네도 알 거야. 같은 때에 독에 중독된 체사레는 뱀처럼 허물을 벗고 살아남았지만, 그 새 허물은 독에 얼룩져 호랑이 가죽처럼 보였지. 그 뒤 로마를 떠나야 했던 그는, 역사에 거의 주목받지도 못한 어느 야간 전투에서 이름도 없이 죽고 말았네.\n\n\"교황이 죽고 그 아들이 추방되자, 사람들은 스파다 가문이 추기경 시절 이전에 누렸던 화려한 지위를 되찾으리라 여겼네. 하지만 그렇지 않았지. 스파다 가문은 그저 그런 형편에 머물렀고, 이 어두운 사건에는 수수께끼가 드리워졌네. 세간의 소문은, 제 아버지보다 더 능란한 정치가였던 체사레가 두 추기경의 재산을 교황에게서 빼돌렸다는 것이었네. 두 추기경이라 말하는 건, 아무런 조심도 하지 않았던 로스피글리오시 추기경은 완전히 털렸기 때문이지.\n\n\"여기까지는,\" 파리아가 이야기의 흐름을 끊으며 말했다. \"자네에겐 분명 아무런 의미도 없게 들렸겠지, 안 그런가?\"\n\n\"오, 친구여,\" 당테스가 외쳤다. \"오히려 반대입니다. 무척 흥미로운 이야기를 읽는 것만 같습니다. 계속해 주십시오, 부탁드립니다.\"\n\n\"그러지. 가문은 그 무명의 처지에 차츰 익숙해졌네. 세월이 흘렀고, 후손들 가운데 더러는 군인, 더러는 외교관이 되었지. 더러는 성직자, 더러는 은행가가 되었고. 부유해진 자도 있고 몰락한 자도 있었네. 이제 나는 가문의 마지막 인물, 곧 내가 비서로 모셨던 스파다 백작에게 이르렀네. 나는 그분이 자신의 지위에 어울리지 않는 재산을 두고 한탄하시는 걸 자주 들었지. 그래서 나는 가진 모든 것을 연금에 넣으시라 권했네. 그분은 그렇게 하셨고, 그리하여 수입이 갑절로 늘었지. 그 유명한 기도서는 가문에 남아 백작의 손에 있었네. 아버지에게서 아들로 대물림되어 온 것이었지. 유일하게 발견된 유언장의 그 기묘한 조항 때문에, 그것은 진짜 유물로 여겨져 미신에 가까운 숭배 속에 가문에 보존되어 왔거든. 아름다운 고딕체 글자로 채색된 책이었고, 금이 어찌나 무겁게 박혀 있었던지, 큰 의식이 있는 날이면 늘 하인 하나가 추기경 앞에서 그것을 받쳐 들고 다녔다네.\n\n\"독살된 추기경에게서 전부 내려온, 가문의 문서고에 보관된 온갖 종류의 서류들—권리증, 계약서, 양피지 문서들을 보며, 이번엔 내가 나설 차례였네. 나보다 앞서 스무 명쯤의 하인과 집사와 비서가 그랬듯이, 그 방대한 문서 뭉치들을 살폈지. 하지만 더없이 철저히 뒤졌는데도 나는 아무것도 찾지 못했네. 그래도 나는 보르자 가문의 정확한 역사를 읽고, 심지어 직접 써 보기까지 했네. 오로지 체사레 스파다 추기경이 죽고 나서 그들에게 재산의 증식이 있었는지 확인하려는 목적에서였지. 하지만 그 불운의 동반자였던 로스피글리오시 추기경의 재산을 손에 넣은 흔적밖에는 찾을 수 없었네.\n\n\"그래서 나는 그 유산이 보르자 가문에도, 스파다 가문에도 득이 되지 않았으며, 마치 정령의 눈앞에서 땅속에 잠들어 있던 아라비안나이트의 보물처럼 임자 없이 남아 있다고 거의 확신하게 되었네. 나는 삼백 년에 걸친 가문의 수입과 지출을 천 번이고 만 번이고 찾고, 뒤지고, 세고, 계산했지. 헛수고였네. 나는 여전히 깜깜했고, 스파다 백작은 여전히 가난했지.\n\n\"내 후원자께서 돌아가셨네. 그분은 연금에서 집안 서류와, 오천 권으로 이루어진 서재와, 그 유명한 기도서를 떼어 두셨네. 그 모든 것을, 가지고 계시던 현금 천 로마 크라운과 함께 내게 남겨 주셨지. 다만 내가 그분의 영혼의 안식을 위해 해마다 추도 미사를 올리고, 그분 가문의 족보와 역사를 작성한다는 조건이었네. 나는 그 모든 것을 빈틈없이 해냈지. 안심하게, 친애하는 에드몽, 이제 결말에 가까워졌으니까.\n\n\"1807년, 내가 체포되기 한 달 전이자 스파다 백작이 돌아가시고 보름 뒤인 12월 25일에—이 날짜가 어떻게 내 기억에 박혔는지는 곧 알게 될 걸세—나는 정리하던 그 서류들을 천 번째로 읽고 있었네. 궁전이 어떤 낯선 사람에게 팔려, 나는 로마를 떠나 피렌체에 정착할 참이었거든. 가진 돈 만 이천 프랑과 내 서재, 그리고 그 유명한 기도서를 가지고 갈 작정이었지. 그런데 같은 일을 끊임없이 하느라 지치고, 거하게 먹은 저녁 탓에 노곤해져, 머리가 두 손 위로 떨어지면서 오후 세 시쯤 잠이 들고 말았네.\n\n\"시계가 여섯 시를 칠 때 나는 깨어났네. 고개를 들어 보니 칠흑 같은 어둠 속이었지. 불을 가져오라고 종을 울렸지만 아무도 오지 않기에, 직접 불을 구하기로 했네. 사실 머지않아 어쩔 수 없이 받아들여야 할 그 소박한 생활방식을 미리 맛본 셈이었지. 나는 한 손에 밀랍 초를 들고, 다른 손으로 종이쪽지 하나를 더듬어 찾았네—성냥갑이 비어 있었거든—잿불 위에 아직 가물거리는 작은 불꽃에서 불을 옮겨 붙일 작정이었지. 하지만 값나가는 종이를 쓸까 봐 잠시 망설였네. 그러다 문득, 곁의 탁자 위에 놓인 그 유명한 기도서 안에서, 세월에 누렇게 바랜 채 수백 년 동안 책갈피 노릇을 해 온, 상속자들의 청에 따라 거기 끼워 둔 오래된 종이 한 장을 본 게 떠올랐네. 나는 그것을 더듬어 찾아 둘둘 비틀어 말고는, 꺼져 가는 불꽃에 갖다 대어 불을 붙였지.\n\n\"그런데 내 손가락 밑에서, 마치 마법처럼, 불길이 타올라 가는 데 따라 누르스름한 글자들이 종이 위에 나타나는 게 보였네. 나는 그것을 손에 움켜쥐고 되도록 빨리 불을 끈 뒤, 바로 그 불에 초를 켜고는, 형언할 수 없는 감격에 휩싸여 구겨진 종이를 펼쳤네. 그러고 나서 알아챘지. 그 글자들이 신비로운 발색 잉크로 쓰여, 불에 쬘 때만 나타난다는 것을. 종이의 거의 삼분의 일이 불에 타 버린 뒤였네. 그게 바로 자네가 오늘 아침 읽은 종이일세. 다시 읽어 보게, 당테스. 그러면 끊긴 낱말과 이어지지 않는 뜻을 자네를 위해 채워 주겠네.\"\n\n파리아는 의기양양한 기색으로 당테스에게 종이를 내밀었고, 이번에 당테스는 녹처럼 불그스름한 잉크로 쓰인 다음 글자들을 읽었다.\n\n\"1498년 4월 25일, 이날에……\n\n알렉산데르 6세, 그리고 ……않을까 두려워……\n\n그가 나의 상속인이 되고자 하여, 그리고 다……\n\n독살된 벤티볼리오를 ……\n\n나의 유일한 상속인에게, 내가 묻……\n\n나와 함께 가 본 곳, 곧……\n\n몬테크리스토섬에, 내가 가진 모든 것……\n\n보석, 다이아몬드, 보물을. 나만이 홀로……\n\n거의 이백만……에 이를 수도……\n\n스무 번째 바위를 들어 올리면 찾……\n\n동쪽 만에서 일직선으로. 두 개의 입……\n\n이 동굴들 안에. 보물은 가장 깊은……\n\n그 보물을 나는 남기며 전부 물려……\n\n나의 유일한 상속인으로서.\n\n1498년 4월 25일.\n\n체사……\"\n\n\"그리고 이제,\" 신부가 말했다. \"이 다른 종이를 읽어 보게.\" 그가 당테스에게 두 번째 장을 내밀었다. 거기에는 토막 난 줄들이 적혀 있었고, 에드몽은 다음과 같이 읽었다.\n\n\"……성하의 만찬에 초대받아\n\n……내게 모자값을 치르게 한 것에 만족하지 않고\n\n……카프라라 추기경의 운명을 내게 마련해 두니\n\n……나의 조카 귀도 스파다에게 선언하노라\n\n……그가 아는 곳에 묻혀\n\n……작은 섬의 동굴들\n\n……주괴, 금, 돈,\n\n……이 보물의 존재를 아는 것은\n\n……로마 크라운으로, 그리고 그가\n\n……작은 ……에서\n\n……입구가 만들어졌으며\n\n……두 번째의 모퉁이에\n\n……그에게 전부 물려\n\n체사 † 스파다.\"\n\n파리아는 흥분한 눈빛으로 그를 지켜보았다.\n\n\"자, 이제,\" 당테스가 마지막 줄을 다 읽은 것을 보고 그가 말했다. \"두 조각을 한데 맞춰 보게. 그리고 자네 스스로 판단해 보게나.\" 당테스는 그대로 했고, 이어 맞춘 조각들은 다음과 같았다.\n\n\"1498년 4월 25일, 이날에……성하의 만찬에 초대받아 알렉산데르 6세, 그리고 ……않을까 두려워……내게 모자값을 치르게 한 것에 만족하지 않고, 그가 나의 상속인이 되고자 하여, 그리고 다……카프라라 추기경과 독살된 벤티볼리오의 운명을 내게 마련해 두니,……나의 조카 귀도 스파다, 나의 유일한 상속인에게 선언하노라. 내가 묻……그가 아는 곳, 나와 함께 가 본 곳, 곧……작은 몬테크리스토섬의 동굴들에, 내가 가진 모든 것……주괴, 금, 돈, 보석, 다이아몬드, 보물을. 나만이 홀로……이 보물의 존재를 아는 것은, 거의 이백만……로마 크라운에 이를 수도 있으며, 그가……작은 만에서 스무 번째 바위를 들어 올리면 동쪽으로 일직선으로 찾……게 될 것이다. 두 개의 입……구가 이 동굴들 안에 만들어졌으며. 보물은 가장 깊은……두 번째의 모퉁이에. 그 보물을 나는 나의 유일한 상속인으로서 그에게 전부 물려……주며 남기노라. 1498년 4월 25일. 체사……체사 † 스파다.\"\n\n\"자, 이제 알겠나?\" 파리아가 물었다.\n\n\"스파다 추기경의 선언이자, 그토록 오래 찾아 헤맨 유언장이군요.\" 여전히 믿지 못하며 에드몽이 대답했다.\n\n\"그래. 천 번이고 그렇다네, 그래!\"\n\n\"그런데 지금처럼 이렇게 완성한 사람은 누굽니까?\"\n\n\"나일세. 남아 있는 조각의 도움을 받아 나머지를 짐작했지. 종이의 길이로 줄의 길이를 가늠하고, 일부 드러난 것을 실마리 삼아 숨겨진 뜻을 헤아렸네. 마치 동굴 속에서 머리 위의 작은 빛줄기에 인도되듯이 말이야.\"\n\n\"그래서 이 결론에 이르렀을 때 어떻게 하셨습니까?\"\n\n\"길을 떠나기로 결심했고, 바로 그 순간 떠났네. 내 위대한 과업, 곧 이탈리아 왕국 통일의 첫 단초를 지니고 말이야. 하지만 한동안 황제의 경찰이—이 무렵 그들은 나폴레옹이 아들을 얻자마자 그토록 바라던 것과는 정반대로, 지방들의 분할을 원하고 있었지—나를 감시하고 있었네. 그리고 그들이 까닭을 짐작하지 못한 내 황급한 출발이 그들의 의심을 불러일으켜, 나는 피옴비노를 떠나려던 바로 그 순간 체포되고 말았지.\n\n\"이제,\" 파리아가 거의 아버지 같은 표정으로 당테스를 향해 말을 이었다. \"이제 자네는 나만큼이나 다 알게 됐네. 우리가 언젠가 함께 탈출한다면 이 보물의 절반은 자네 것일세. 내가 여기서 죽고 자네 혼자 탈출한다면, 그 전부가 자네 것이고.\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 당테스가 머뭇거리며 물었다. \"이 보물에는 우리 말고 더 정당한 임자가 세상에 없는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"없네, 없어. 그 점은 안심하게. 가문은 끊어졌으니까. 게다가 마지막 스파다 백작은 나를 상속인으로 삼아, 이 상징적인 기도서를 내게 남기면서 그 안에 담긴 모든 것을 내게 물려주셨네. 아니, 아니야, 그 점은 마음 놓아도 되네. 우리가 이 재산에 손을 댄다 해도, 양심의 가책 없이 누릴 수 있어.\"\n\n\"그래서 이 보물이 얼마나 된다고 하셨지요……\"\n\n\"이백만 로마 크라운. 우리 돈으로 거의 천삼백만 프랑일세.\"\n\n\"그럴 리가요!\" 당테스가 그 엄청난 액수에 아연실색하며 말했다.\n\n\"그럴 리가 없다고? 어째서 말인가?\" 노인이 물었다. \"스파다 가문은 15세기의 가장 오래되고 강력한 가문 가운데 하나였네. 그리고 그 시절에는 달리 투자할 기회가 없었으니, 그렇게 금과 보석을 쌓아 두는 일이 결코 드물지 않았지. 오늘날에도 한정 상속으로 물려받은, 거의 백만에 이르는 다이아몬드와 보석을 가지고 있으면서도 손을 댈 수 없어 굶어 죽어 가는 로마 가문들이 있다네.\"\n\n에드몽은 꿈을 꾸는 것만 같았다. 그는 불신과 기쁨 사이에서 갈팡질팡했다.\n\n\"내가 이 비밀을 자네에게 이토록 오래 감춰 둔 것은,\" 파리아가 말을 이었다. \"자네의 사람됨을 시험해 본 다음, 자네를 놀라게 해 주려는 마음에서였네. 내가 강경증 발작을 일으키기 전에 우리가 탈출했더라면, 나는 자네를 몬테크리스토섬으로 데려갔을 걸세. 하지만 이제는,\" 그가 한숨을 쉬며 덧붙였다. \"자네가 나를 그리로 데려가게 되겠지. 그래, 당테스, 자네 내게 고맙다는 말도 안 하는군?\"\n\n\"이 보물은 당신 것입니다, 친애하는 친구여.\" 당테스가 대답했다. \"오직 당신만의 것이지요. 저에겐 그것에 대한 아무런 권리도 없습니다. 저는 당신의 친척도 아닌걸요.\"\n\n\"자네는 내 아들일세, 당테스.\" 노인이 외쳤다. \"자네는 내 갇힌 세월이 낳은 자식이야. 내 직분이 나를 독신으로 묶어 두었지. 하느님께서 아버지가 될 수 없었던 사내와, 자유로워질 수 없었던 죄수를 한꺼번에 위로하시려고 자네를 내게 보내 주신 걸세.\"\n\n그러고는 파리아가 자신에게 유일하게 쓸 수 있는 팔을 청년에게 뻗었고, 청년은 그의 목에 몸을 던지며 흐느꼈다.\n\n# Chapter 19. The Third Attack\n\n* 발색 잉크: 열을 가하면 글자가 드러나는 잉크. —옮긴이\n그토록 오랫동안 신부의 명상의 대상이었던 그 보물이 이제 파리아가 친아들처럼 사랑하는 사람의 앞날에 행복을 보장해 줄 수 있게 되자, 그의 눈에 그 가치는 두 배로 불어났다. 그는 날마다 그 액수를 늘어놓으며, 천삼백만이나 천사백만 프랑을 손에 쥔 사람이 요즘 세상에서 벗들에게 얼마나 많은 선을 베풀 수 있는지 당테스에게 설명했다. 그러면 당테스의 얼굴은 어두워졌다. 그가 맹세한 복수의 다짐이 떠올랐기 때문이었고, 요즘 세상에서 천삼백만이나 천사백만을 가진 사람이 자기 원수들에게 얼마나 많은 해악을 끼칠 수 있는지를 곱씹었기 때문이었다.\n\n신부는 몬테크리스토섬을 알지 못했다. 그러나 당테스는 그 섬을 알았고, 여러 번 그 곁을 지난 적이 있었다. 코르시카섬과 엘바섬 사이, 피아노사에서 이십오 마일 떨어진 곳에 자리한 섬이었고, 한 번은 그곳에 닿은 적도 있었다. 이 섬은 늘 완전히 무인도였고, 지금도 그러하다. 거의 원뿔 모양의 바위로, 마치 화산의 힘이 바다 밑바닥에서 수면 위로 밀어 올린 것처럼 보였다. 당테스가 파리아에게 그 섬의 지도를 그려 주자, 파리아는 보물을 되찾기 위해 써야 할 방법에 대해 당테스에게 조언했다. 그러나 당테스는 노인만큼 열정적이지도, 확신에 차 있지도 않았다. 파리아가 미치광이가 아니라는 것은 이제 의심할 여지가 없었고, 그가 광기로 의심받게 만든 그 발견에 이른 과정을 알게 되자 에드몽의 존경심은 더욱 커졌다. 그러나 동시에 당테스는 그 보물이 — 설령 한때 존재했다 하더라도 — 지금까지 그대로 남아 있으리라고는 믿을 수 없었다. 보물이 결코 허황된 이야기는 아니라고 여기면서도, 이제는 그 자리에 없으리라고 생각했던 것이다.\n\n그러나 운명은 이 죄수들에게서 마지막 기회마저 앗아 가, 그들이 영원한 감금형에 처해졌음을 깨닫게 하려는 듯, 새로운 불행을 안겼다. 오랫동안 허물어진 채 방치되어 있던 바다 쪽 회랑이 다시 세워진 것이다. 그들은 그곳을 완전히 보수했고, 당테스가 일부 메워 두었던 구멍을 거대한 돌덩이들로 틀어막았다. 기억하다시피 신부가 에드몽에게 일러 준 그 조심성이 아니었더라면, 불행은 더 컸을 것이다. 그들의 탈출 시도가 발각되었을 테고, 두 사람은 틀림없이 떨어뜨려졌을 테니까. 이렇게 더 새롭고, 더 견고하며, 더 가차 없는 장벽이 가로놓여 그들의 희망이 실현될 길을 끊어 놓았다.\n\n\"보십시오.\" 젊은이가 슬프게 체념한 표정으로 파리아에게 말했다. \"하느님께서는 신부님이 제 헌신이라고 부르시는 것에 대해 제가 어떤 공로도 주장하지 못하도록 하시는 게 옳다고 여기시나 봅니다. 저는 신부님 곁에 영원히 남겠다고 약속드렸고, 이제는 설령 그러고 싶다 해도 그 약속을 깰 수 없게 되었으니까요. 그 보물은 제 것도, 신부님 것도 아니게 될 테고, 우리 둘 다 이 감옥을 떠나지 못할 겁니다. 하지만 제 진짜 보물은 몬테크리스토섬의 음침한 바위 밑에서 저를 기다리는 그것이 아닙니다, 사랑하는 벗이시여. 제 진짜 보물은 신부님이 곁에 계신다는 것, 간수들의 눈을 피해 하루에 대여섯 시간을 함께 살아간다는 것입니다. 신부님이 제 머릿속에서 끌어내 주신 지성의 빛이고, 제 기억 속에 심어 주신 언어들이며, 그것들이 온갖 문헌학적 가지를 뻗으며 거기 뿌리내린 것입니다. 신부님이 그토록 깊이 알고 계신 지식과, 그것을 명료한 원리로 간추려 주신 덕분에 제게 그토록 쉬워진 이 갖가지 학문들 — 이것이 제 보물입니다, 사랑하는 벗이시여. 신부님은 이것으로 저를 부유하고 행복하게 만들어 주셨습니다. 제 말을 믿으시고, 위안을 얻으십시오. 이것이 제게는 수 톤의 금이나 상자에 가득한 다이아몬드보다 낫습니다. 설령 그것들이, 아침에 바다 위를 떠다니는 구름처럼 — 우리가 육지인 줄 알았다가 다가서면 흩어져 사라지는 그 구름처럼 — 허황된 것이 아니라 해도 말입니다. 신부님을 되도록 오래 곁에 두는 것, 신부님의 그 유창한 말씀을 듣는 것 — 그 말씀은 제 정신을 아름답게 하고, 제 영혼을 굳세게 하며, 언젠가 제가 자유로워진다면 위대하고도 무서운 일들을 해낼 수 있는 사람으로 제 온몸을 빚어 줍니다 — 그것이 제 삶 전체를 가득 채워서, 신부님을 알기 직전까지 제가 막 굴복하려던 그 절망이 이제는 저를 조금도 사로잡지 못합니다. 그리고 이것이 — 이것이 제 재산입니다. 허황된 것이 아니라, 실제로 존재하는 것이지요. 저는 신부님께 제 진정한 행복을, 제 지금의 행복을 빚지고 있습니다. 그리고 이 땅의 어떤 군주도, 심지어 체사레 보르자조차도 제게서 이것을 빼앗을 수 없을 겁니다.\"\n\n이렇게, 정녕 행복하다고는 할 수 없어도, 이 두 불운한 사람이 함께 보내는 나날은 빠르게 흘러갔다. 그토록 오랫동안 보물에 대해 입을 다물고 있던 파리아는 이제 끊임없이 그 이야기를 했다. 그가 예언했던 대로, 그는 오른팔과 왼다리가 마비된 채로 지냈고, 자신이 직접 그 보물을 누리리라는 희망은 모두 버린 상태였다. 그러나 그는 자기 젊은 동료가 탈출할 방도를 끊임없이 궁리했고, 당테스가 누릴 즐거움을 미리 그려 보았다. 그 편지가 언젠가 분실되거나 도둑맞을 것을 염려한 그는, 당테스에게 그것을 통째로 외우게 했다. 당테스는 첫 글자부터 마지막 글자까지 그것을 알게 되었다. 그러자 파리아는 편지의 둘째 부분을 없애 버렸다. 설령 첫째 부분이 압수된다 해도 누구도 그 진짜 뜻을 알아낼 수 없으리라 확신했기 때문이다. 파리아가 당테스에게 지시를 내리느라 몇 시간이 통째로 지나가기도 했다 — 당테스가 자유의 몸이 되었을 때 도움이 될 지시들이었다. 그렇게 일단 자유로워지면, 그날 그 시각 그 순간부터, 그는 오직 한 가지 생각만 품어야 했다. 어떻게든 몬테크리스토섬에 닿아, 어떤 의심도 사지 않을 구실을 대고 홀로 그곳에 머물 것. 그리고 일단 그곳에 닿으면, 그 놀라운 동굴들을 찾아내어 정해진 자리를 뒤질 것 — 기억하다시피 그 정해진 자리란, 두 번째로 뚫린 입구의 가장 안쪽 구석이었다.\n\n그러는 동안 시간은, 빠르다고는 못해도 적어도 견딜 만하게 흘러갔다. 앞서 말했듯이 파리아는 손과 발의 기능은 되찾지 못했어도 정신의 명료함은 모두 되찾았고, 우리가 상세히 설명한 도덕적 가르침 외에도, 무에서 무언가를 만들어 내는 법을 배우는 죄수의 인내롭고 숭고한 의무를 자기 젊은 동료에게 차츰 가르쳤다. 그리하여 두 사람은 끊임없이 무언가에 매달렸다. 파리아는 자신이 늙어 가는 것을 보지 않기 위해, 당테스는 이제 밤중에 떠도는 먼 불빛처럼 기억 속에 가물거리는, 거의 꺼져 버린 과거를 떠올릴까 두려워서였다. 그렇게 그들의 삶은, 불행의 희생자가 아닌 사람들에게서, 섭리의 눈길 아래 기계적으로 또 평온하게 흘러가는 사람들에게서 그러하듯 이어졌다.\n\n그러나 이 겉으로 드러난 평온 아래, 젊은이의 가슴속에는, 어쩌면 노인의 가슴속에도, 억눌린 숱한 욕망과 짓눌린 숱한 한숨이 도사리고 있었다. 그것들은 파리아가 홀로 남고 에드몽이 제 감방으로 돌아갔을 때 비로소 터져 나왔다.\n\n어느 날 밤 에드몽은 누군가 자기를 부르는 소리를 들은 것 같아 문득 잠에서 깼다. 그가 눈을 떠 보니 칠흑 같은 어둠뿐이었다. 자기 이름이, 아니 그 이름을 부르려 애쓰는 애처로운 목소리가 그에게 들려왔다. 그는 침대에서 몸을 일으켰고, 이마에 식은땀이 솟았다. 그 부르는 소리는 틀림없이 파리아의 지하 감방에서 나오고 있었다.\n\n\"아아.\" 에드몽이 중얼거렸다. \"설마, 그럴 리가?\"\n\n그는 침대를 옮기고, 돌을 들어 올린 뒤, 통로로 뛰어들어 반대편 끝에 이르렀다. 비밀 입구가 열려 있었다. 앞서 말한 그 초라하고 가물거리는 등불 빛으로, 당테스는 노인이 창백한 얼굴로, 그래도 몸을 세운 채 침대 틀에 매달려 있는 것을 보았다. 그의 얼굴은, 이미 알고 있던, 처음 보았을 때 그를 그토록 심각하게 놀라게 했던 그 끔찍한 증상들로 일그러지고 있었다.\n\n\"아아, 사랑하는 벗이여.\" 파리아가 체념한 어조로 말했다. \"자네는 알아챘겠지, 안 그런가? 굳이 설명할 것도 없겠지?\"\n\n에드몽은 비통한 외침을 토하며, 거의 제정신이 아닌 채로 문을 향해 달려가 소리쳤다. \"도와줘요, 도와줘요!\"\n\n파리아는 그를 가까스로 붙잡을 만큼의 힘은 남아 있었다.\n\n\"조용히 하게.\" 그가 말했다. \"안 그러면 자네는 끝장이야. 이제 우리는 자네만 생각해야 하네, 사랑하는 벗이여. 자네의 옥살이를 견딜 만하게 하든가, 자네의 탈출을 가능하게 하든가, 그렇게 움직여야 해. 내가 여기서 한 일을 다시 하려면 몇 년이 걸릴 테고, 우리가 서로 통했다는 걸 간수들이 알게 되면 그 성과는 당장에 무너지고 말 걸세. 게다가 안심하게, 사랑하는 에드몽. 내가 곧 떠날 이 지하 감방은 오래 비어 있지 않을 거야. 또 다른 불운한 자가 곧 내 자리를 차지할 테고, 그에게 자네는 구원의 천사처럼 보일 걸세. 어쩌면 그자는 자네처럼 젊고, 강하고, 끈질긴 사람이라, 자네의 탈출을 도울 수 있을지도 모르지. 나는 그저 걸림돌이었을 뿐인데 말이야. 자네는 더 이상 자네의 모든 움직임에 족쇄처럼 매달린 반쯤 죽은 몸뚱이를 끌고 다니지 않아도 돼. 마침내 섭리가 자네를 위해 무언가를 해 준 거야. 빼앗아 가는 것보다 더 많은 것을 자네에게 되돌려 주는 셈이고, 내가 죽을 때도 된 거지.\"\n\n에드몽은 그저 두 손을 맞잡고 외칠 따름이었다. \"오, 벗이여, 벗이여, 그런 말씀 마십시오!\" 그러고는 이 충격에 한순간 휘청거렸던 침착함을 모두 되찾고, 노인의 말에 꺾였던 기운을 다시 추슬러 말했다. \"오, 제가 한 번 신부님을 살려 냈으니, 두 번째로도 살려 내겠습니다!\" 그러고는 침대 발치를 들어 올려, 아직 삼분의 일쯤 붉은 액체가 남아 있는 작은 병을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"보십시오.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"이 신비한 물약이 아직 좀 남아 있습니다. 어서요, 어서! 이번에는 제가 무얼 해야 하는지 말씀해 주세요. 새로 일러 주실 게 있습니까? 말씀하세요, 벗이여. 듣고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"희망은 없네.\" 파리아가 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"하지만 상관없어. 하느님은 당신이 지으신 인간이, 또 그 가슴속에 삶에 대한 사랑을 그토록 깊이 심어 놓으신 그 인간이, 제 목숨을 지키기 위해 할 수 있는 모든 일을 다하기를 바라시지. 그 목숨이란, 아무리 고통스러울지언정 언제나 그토록 소중한 법이니까.\"\n\n\"오, 그렇고말고요, 그렇고말고요!\" 당테스가 외쳤다. \"분명히 말씀드리지만, 저는 기어이 신부님을 살려 내겠습니다.\"\n\n\"좋아, 그럼 해 보게. 한기가 점점 더 나를 덮쳐 오는군. 피가 머리로 몰리는 게 느껴져. 이가 딱딱 부딪치게 하고 뼈마디를 어긋나게 하는 것 같은 이 끔찍한 오한이 온몸으로 퍼지기 시작했어. 오 분 뒤면 발작이 절정에 이를 테고, 십오 분 뒤면 내게 남는 건 송장뿐일 걸세.\"\n\n\"오!\" 당테스가 비통함에 가슴이 찢어지는 듯 외쳤다.\n\n\"전에 했던 대로 하게. 다만 그렇게 오래 기다리지는 말게. 이제 내 안의 생명의 샘은 모두 말라 버렸고, 죽음은,\" 그가 마비된 팔과 다리를 바라보며 말을 이었다. \"이미 절반은 일을 끝내 놓았으니까. 열 방울 대신 열두 방울을 삼키게 한 뒤에도 내가 깨어나지 않거든, 그땐 남은 것을 모두 목에 부어 넣게. 자, 이제 나를 침대에 눕혀 주게. 더는 내 몸을 가눌 수가 없어.\"\n\n에드몽은 노인을 두 팔로 안아 침대에 눕혔다.\n\n\"그리고 이제, 사랑하는 벗이여.\" 파리아가 말했다. \"내 비참한 삶의 유일한 위안이었던 사람 — 하늘이 다소 늦게 내게 보내 주었으나 그래도 보내 준, 값을 매길 수 없는 선물, 내가 더없이 감사하는 그 선물이여 — 자네와 영원히 헤어지는 이 순간에, 나는 자네가 마땅히 누릴 모든 행복과 모든 번영을 빌어 주네. 내 아들아, 너를 축복하노라!\"\n\n젊은이는 무릎을 꿇고, 노인의 침대에 머리를 기댔다.\n\n\"이제, 죽어 가는 이 순간에 내가 하는 말을 들어 두게. 스파다 가문의 보물은 실재하네. 하느님이 내게 시간과 공간에 얽매이지 않는 환시의 은총을 베푸셨어. 안쪽 동굴 깊숙한 곳에 있는 그것이 보여. 내 눈이 땅속 가장 깊은 구석까지 꿰뚫고 들어가, 그 엄청난 재물을 보고 눈이 부시는구나. 만일 자네가 탈출하거든, 온 세상이 미치광이라 불렀던 그 가엾은 신부가 실은 미치지 않았다는 것을 기억하게. 어서 몬테크리스토섬으로 가게 — 그 재산을 차지하게 — 자네는 정말이지 충분히, 너무도 오래 고통받았으니까.\"\n\n격렬한 경련이 노인을 덮쳤다. 당테스가 고개를 들어 보니 파리아의 두 눈에 피가 몰려 있었다. 마치 핏줄기가 가슴에서 머리로 거슬러 올라온 듯했다.\n\n\"잘 있게, 잘 있게!\" 노인이 에드몽의 손을 발작적으로 부여잡으며 중얼거렸다. \"잘 있게!\"\n\n\"오, 안 돼요 — 안 돼요, 아직은 안 됩니다.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"저를 버리지 마세요! 오, 이분을 구해 주세요! 도와줘요 — 도와줘요 — 도와줘요!\"\n\n\"쉿! 쉿!\" 죽어 가는 사람이 중얼거렸다. \"자네가 나를 살린다 해도, 저들이 우리를 떼어 놓지 못하게 해야지!\"\n\n\"옳은 말씀입니다. 오, 그래요, 그래요. 안심하세요, 제가 꼭 살려 내겠습니다! 게다가, 신부님이 몹시 괴로워하시긴 해도, 전처럼 그렇게 고통스러워 보이지는 않으시는걸요.\"\n\n\"착각하지 말게! 내가 덜 괴로워하는 건, 견딜 힘이 그만큼 줄었기 때문이야. 자네 나이엔 삶을 믿지. 믿고 바라는 것은 젊음의 특권이지만, 늙은이는 죽음을 더 또렷이 본다네. 오, 여기 왔어 — 여기 왔어 — 끝이야 — 시야가 사라지네 — 감각이 스러져! 자네 손을, 당테스! 잘 있게! 잘 있게!\"\n\n그리고 그는 마지막 안간힘으로, 온 정신을 끌어모아 몸을 일으키며 말했다. \"몬테크리스토, 몬테크리스토를 잊지 말게!\" 그러고는 침대 위로 쓰러졌다.\n\n발작은 끔찍했다. 좀 전까지 그곳에 누워 있던 그 지성적인 존재 대신, 사지가 뒤틀리고 눈꺼풀이 부어오르고 입술에 피거품이 얼룩진 뻣뻣한 형체가 그 고통의 침상에 누워 있었다.\n\n당테스는 등불을 집어 침대 위로 돌출된 돌 위에 올려놓았다. 거기서 떨리는 불빛이 기이하고 환상적인 빛줄기로 일그러진 얼굴과, 움직임 없이 굳어 버린 몸뚱이 위로 떨어졌다. 그는 흔들림 없는 눈길로, 회복약을 먹일 순간을 확신에 차서 기다렸다.\n\n적절한 순간이 왔다고 여겨지자, 그는 칼을 들어, 전보다 덜 버티는 이를 비집어 벌리고, 열두 방울을 하나하나 세어 넣은 뒤 지켜보았다. 병에는 아마 그 두 배쯤 되는 액체가 더 남아 있었을 것이다. 그는 십 분을, 십오 분을, 삼십 분을 기다렸다. 아무런 변화도 없었다. 그는 부들부들 떨며, 머리카락을 곤두세운 채, 이마를 땀으로 적시며, 제 심장 박동으로 초를 세었다. 그러고는 마지막 시도를 할 때라고 생각했다. 그는 작은 병을 파리아의 검푸른 입술에 갖다 댔고, 벌어진 채로 있던 턱을 억지로 벌릴 것도 없이, 남은 액체를 모두 그의 목으로 흘려 넣었다.\n\n물약은 전기 충격 같은 작용을 일으켰다. 격렬한 떨림이 노인의 사지를 훑고 지나갔고, 두 눈이 차마 바라볼 수 없을 만큼 크게 떠졌으며, 그는 비명에 가까운 한숨을 토했다. 그러더니 경련하던 몸뚱이가 차츰 본래의 부동 상태로 돌아갔고, 두 눈은 뜬 채로 남았다.\n\n삼십 분이, 한 시간이, 한 시간 반이 지났다. 이 비통한 시간 동안, 에드몽은 벗 위로 몸을 숙이고 손을 그의 심장에 갖다 댄 채, 몸이 차츰 식어 가는 것을, 그리고 심장의 박동이 점점 더 깊고 둔해지다가 마침내 멎는 것을 느꼈다. 심장의 마지막 움직임이 그쳤고, 얼굴은 납빛이 되었으며, 두 눈은 뜬 채로였으나 눈동자는 흐릿하게 풀려 있었다.\n\n아침 여섯 시였다. 동이 막 트고 있었고, 그 희미한 빛줄기가 지하 감방으로 들어와 등불의 무력한 불빛을 바래게 했다. 기이한 그림자들이 죽은 사람의 얼굴 위로 지나가며, 이따금 그것을 살아 있는 것처럼 보이게 했다. 낮과 밤의 다툼이 계속되는 동안 당테스는 여전히 의심을 품었다. 그러나 햇빛이 우위를 차지하자마자, 그는 자신이 한 구의 시신과 단둘이 남아 있음을 깨달았다. 그러자 걷잡을 수 없는 극도의 공포가 그를 사로잡았다. 그는 침대 밖으로 늘어진 그 손을 다시 쥐어 볼 엄두를 내지 못했고, 그 멍하니 고정된 두 눈을 더는 바라볼 엄두도 내지 못했다. 그는 여러 번 그 눈을 감기려 했으나 헛수고였다. 감기면 이내 다시 떠졌던 것이다. 그는 등불을 끄고, 그것을 조심스레 감춘 뒤, 그곳을 떠났다. 내려가면서 비밀 통로의 입구를 그 큰 돌로 되도록 잘 막아 두었다.\n\n때마침이었다. 간수가 오고 있었던 것이다. 이번에 그는 당테스의 감방부터 순회를 시작했고, 거기서 나오자 파리아의 지하 감방으로 가, 아침 식사와 새 속옷을 가져갔다. 그 사내가 무슨 일이 일어났는지 알고 있다는 기색은 전혀 없었다. 그는 제 갈 길을 갔다.\n\n그러자 당테스는 불운한 벗의 지하 감방에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 알고 싶은, 형언할 수 없는 욕망에 사로잡혔다. 그리하여 그는 지하 회랑을 통해 되돌아갔고, 마침 도움을 청하며 소리치는 간수의 외침을 들을 수 있었다. 다른 간수들이 달려왔고, 이어 병사들의 규칙적인 발소리가 들려왔다. 마지막으로 소장이 왔다.\n\n에드몽은 그들이 시신을 옮길 때 침대가 삐걱대는 소리를 들었고, 죽은 자의 얼굴에 물을 끼얹으라고 지시하는 소장의 목소리를 들었다. 그렇게 했는데도 죄수가 깨어나지 않자, 그들은 의사를 부르러 사람을 보냈다. 그러고 나서 소장이 나갔고, 연민의 말들이, 거친 웃음소리와 뒤섞여 귀 기울이고 있던 당테스의 귀에 들려왔다.\n\n\"흥, 흥.\" 누군가 말했다. \"미치광이가 제 보물을 찾으러 갔구먼. 잘 가라지!\"\n\n\"제깟 수백만을 다 가져도, 제 수의값도 못 치를걸!\" 다른 자가 말했다.\n\n\"아.\" 세 번째 목소리가 덧붙였다. \"이프성의 수의야 비쌀 것도 없지!\"\n\n\"하긴.\" 앞서 말한 자들 중 하나가 말했다. \"성직자였으니, 그자를 위해 좀 돈을 쓸지도 모르지.\"\n\n\"자루의 영예를 베풀어 줄지도.\"\n\n에드몽은 한마디도 놓치지 않았지만, 무슨 말인지는 거의 알아듣지 못했다. 이내 목소리들이 그쳤고, 모두가 감방을 떠난 것처럼 여겨졌다. 그래도 그는 안으로 들어갈 엄두를 내지 못했다. 죽은 자를 지키도록 간수 하나를 남겨 두었을지도 모를 일이었다. 그래서 그는 숨조차 제대로 쉬지 못한 채, 말없이 꼼짝도 않고 있었다. 한 시간이 지나자 희미한 소리가 들려왔고, 점점 커졌다. 의사와 다른 수행원들을 거느리고 돌아온 소장이었다. 잠시 침묵이 흘렀다. 의사가 시신을 살펴보고 있는 게 분명했다. 이내 조사가 시작되었다.\n\n의사는 죄수가 굴복한 병의 증상들을 분석한 뒤, 그가 죽었다고 선언했다. 질문과 대답이 무심한 투로 오갔고, 그것이 당테스를 분노케 했다. 그는 온 세상이 가엾은 신부에게 자신과 똑같은 사랑과 존경을 품어야 마땅하다고 느꼈기 때문이었다.\n\n\"방금 한 말이 참 안됐군.\" 의사의 확언에 답하며 소장이 말했다. \"그 노인이 정말로 죽었다니 말이야. 조용하고 해를 끼치지 않는 죄수였고, 제 광기 속에서 행복했으며, 감시할 필요도 없었는데.\"\n\n\"그렇습죠.\" 간수가 덧붙였다. \"그자는 감시할 까닭이 없었습니다. 장담컨대, 탈출이라곤 시도도 않고 여기서 오십 년이라도 지냈을 위인이었으니까요.\"\n\n\"그래도.\" 소장이 말했다. \"확신하시는 바와는 별개로, 그리고 선생의 의술을 의심해서가 아니라, 내 직무를 다하기 위해서, 죄수가 죽었다는 것을 완벽하게 확인해 두는 게 필요할 듯하오.\"\n\n완전한 침묵이 잠시 흘렀고, 그동안 여전히 귀 기울이고 있던 당테스는 의사가 두 번째로 시신을 살펴보고 있음을 알아챘다.\n\n\"마음 놓으셔도 됩니다.\" 의사가 말했다. \"이 사람은 죽었습니다. 제가 보증하지요.\"\n\n\"아시다시피, 선생.\" 소장이 고집스레 말했다. \"이런 경우에는 그처럼 간단한 검진만으로 만족할 수가 없소. 그러니 겉모습이야 어떻든, 부디 법이 정한 절차를 마저 밟아 직무를 끝내 주시오.\"\n\n\"인두를 달구라고 하시오.\" 의사가 말했다. \"하지만 정말이지 쓸데없는 조치입니다.\"\n\n인두를 달구라는 그 명령에 당테스는 몸서리쳤다. 그는 황급한 발소리와, 문이 삐걱대는 소리와, 사람들이 오가는 소리를 들었고, 몇 분 뒤 간수 하나가 들어오며 말하는 소리를 들었다.\n\n\"불 피운 화로 가져왔습니다.\"\n\n잠시 침묵이 흘렀고, 이어 살이 타들어 가는 자글거리는 소리가 들렸다. 그 독특하고 역겨운 냄새가, 당테스가 공포에 질려 귀 기울이고 있던 벽 뒤까지 스며들었다. 젊은이의 이마에서 땀이 줄줄 흘렀고, 그는 금방이라도 기절할 것만 같았다.\n\n\"보십시오, 선생. 이 사람은 정말로 죽었습니다.\" 의사가 말했다. \"발뒤꿈치의 이 화상이 결정적인 증거지요. 이 가엾은 바보는 제 광기에서 벗어났고, 옥살이에서도 풀려난 겁니다.\"\n\n\"이름이 파리아 아니었소?\" 소장을 수행한 관리들 중 하나가 물었다.\n\n\"그렇소, 선생. 본인 말로는 유서 깊은 이름이라더군. 게다가 무척 박식했고, 제 보물에 관한 것만 아니면 매사에 꽤 분별이 있었지요. 하지만 그 보물 얘기에 들어가면, 정말이지 손쓸 도리가 없었소.\"\n\n\"우리가 편집광이라 부르는 부류의 병입니다.\" 의사가 말했다.\n\n\"불평할 만한 일은 한 번도 없었나?\" 소장이 신부를 맡았던 간수에게 물었다.\n\n\"한 번도 없었습니다, 소장님.\" 간수가 대답했다. \"한 번도요. 오히려 이따금 이야기를 들려줘서 저를 아주 즐겁게 해 줬습죠. 한번은 제 아내가 앓아누웠을 때, 처방을 일러 줘서 아내를 낫게 한 적도 있고요.\"\n\n\"이런, 이런!\" 의사가 말했다. \"내게 경쟁자가 있는 줄은 몰랐는걸. 하지만 소장, 그러니 그자에게 마땅한 예우를 모두 갖춰 주시기 바라오.\"\n\n\"그래요, 그래, 마음 놓으시오. 우리가 구할 수 있는 가장 새것인 자루에 점잖게 묻어 주겠소. 그러면 만족하겠소?\"\n\n\"이 마지막 절차는 소장님이 입회하신 자리에서 치러야 합니까?\" 간수 하나가 물었다.\n\n\"물론이지. 하지만 서두르게 — 나는 온종일 여기 있을 수 없으니.\" 사람들이 오가는 또 다른 발소리가 들렸고, 잠시 후 천이 바스락거리는 소리가 당테스의 귀에 닿았다. 침대가 삐걱대더니, 무게를 들어 올리는 사내의 무거운 발소리가 바닥에 울렸다. 이어 다시 침대가, 그 위에 내려놓인 무게에 눌려 삐걱거렸다.\n\n\"오늘 저녁이오.\" 소장이 말했다.\n\n\"미사는 있습니까?\" 수행원 하나가 물었다.\n\n\"그건 불가능하네.\" 소장이 대답했다. \"성 안의 사제가 어제 내게 와서, 이예르로 일주일간 다녀오겠다며 휴가를 청했지. 자리를 비운 동안 죄수들은 내가 돌보겠다고 했어. 가엾은 신부가 그렇게 서두르지만 않았더라면, 진혼 미사쯤은 받을 수 있었을 텐데.\"\n\n\"흥, 흥.\" 의사가 그 직업의 사람들에게 흔한 불경한 투로 말했다. \"성직자 아닙니까. 하느님께서 그 직분을 존중하셔서, 악마에게 사제를 보내 주는 고약한 즐거움 따위는 안기지 않으실 겁니다.\" 이 거친 농담 뒤로 왁자한 웃음이 터졌다. 그러는 동안 시신을 자루에 넣는 작업이 진행되고 있었다.\n\n\"오늘 저녁이오.\" 작업이 끝나자 소장이 말했다.\n\n\"몇 시에요?\" 간수 하나가 물었다.\n\n\"음, 열 시나 열한 시쯤.\"\n\n\"시신을 지킬까요?\"\n\n\"그래 봐야 무슨 소용인가? 살아 있을 때처럼 감방을 잠가 두게 — 그게 다일세.\"\n\n이윽고 발소리가 멀어졌고, 목소리들이 저 멀리로 잦아들었다. 삐걱대는 돌쩌귀와 빗장이 달린 문의 소리가 그쳤고, 고독의 정적보다 더 음울한 침묵이 뒤따랐다 — 죽음의 침묵이었다. 그것은 사방으로 퍼져 나가, 당테스의 영혼 깊은 곳까지 얼음장 같은 한기를 박아 넣었다.\n\n그러자 그는 머리로 조심스레 포석을 들어 올리고, 방 안을 찬찬히 둘러보았다. 방은 비어 있었고, 당테스는 굴에서 빠져나왔다.\n\n# Chapter 20. The Cemetery of the Château d’If\n\n침대 위에 길게, 창에서 들어오는 희미한 빛에 어렴풋이 드러난 채, 캔버스 자루 하나가 놓여 있었다. 거친 주름 아래로 길고 뻣뻣하게 굳은 형체가 뻗어 있었다. 그것은 파리아의 마지막 수의였다. 간수가 말했듯이 비용이 얼마 들지 않은 수의였다. 모든 것이 준비되어 있었다. 당테스와 그의 오랜 벗 사이에 장벽이 놓인 것이다. 에드몽은 이제 죽음의 신비를 꿰뚫어 보는 듯하던 그 크게 뜬 두 눈을 다시는 들여다볼 수 없었다. 그의 삶을 축복으로 만들어 준 그 손을 다시는 잡을 수 없었다. 그토록 가깝게 지내던 자비롭고 쾌활한 동료 파리아는 더 이상 숨 쉬지 않았다. 그는 그 끔찍한 침대 가장자리에 걸터앉아 우울하고 음울한 상념에 잠겼다.\n\n홀로! 그는 다시 홀로였다! 다시 침묵에 갇히고, 다시 허무와 마주 선 것이다! 홀로라니! 그를 이 땅에 붙들어 매 주던 유일한 사람의 얼굴을 다시는 볼 수 없고, 그 목소리를 다시는 들을 수 없다니! 결국 파리아의 운명이 더 나은 것이 아니었을까. 끔찍한 고통의 위험을 무릅쓰고서라도, 그 근원에서 삶의 문제를 풀어 버린 것이.\n\n벗의 쾌활한 존재가 멀리 쫓아 막아 주던 자살이라는 생각이, 이제 신부의 주검 위로 유령처럼 떠돌았다.\n\n\"내가 죽을 수만 있다면,\" 그가 말했다. \"그가 가는 곳으로 가서, 틀림없이 그를 다시 만날 텐데. 그런데 어떻게 죽는단 말인가? 아주 쉬운 일이지.\" 그가 미소를 띠며 말을 이었다. \"여기 그대로 있다가, 문을 여는 첫 번째 사람에게 달려들어 목을 졸라 버리면, 나를 단두대에 세우겠지.\"\n\n그러나 지나친 슬픔은 바다의 폭풍과도 같아서, 연약한 조각배가 물결의 골에서 마루로 내던져지듯 한다. 당테스는 그토록 치욕스러운 죽음이라는 생각에서 움찔 물러섰고, 절망에서 삶과 자유를 향한 뜨거운 갈망으로 돌연 옮겨 갔다.\n\n\"죽는다고? 아, 아니야.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"이렇게 오래, 이토록 많이 살고 고통받은 끝에 지금 죽다니, 안 될 말이다! 죽는다고? 그래, 몇 해 전에 죽었더라면 모르지. 하지만 지금 죽는다면, 그야말로 운명의 비웃음에 굴복하는 꼴이 아닌가. 안 돼, 나는 살고 싶다. 마지막 순간까지 싸울 것이다. 빼앗긴 행복을 기어이 되찾고 말 것이다. 죽기 전에 잊지 말아야 한다. 내게는 벌해야 할 형리들이 있고, 또 어쩌면, 누가 알겠는가, 보답해야 할 벗들도 있다는 것을. 그런데도 여기 사람들은 나를 잊을 테고, 나는 파리아처럼 이 토굴에서 죽고 말겠지.\"\n\n이렇게 말하다가 그는 입을 다물고, 기이하고 놀라운 생각에 사로잡힌 사람처럼 앞을 똑바로 응시했다. 갑자기 그는 벌떡 일어나, 머리가 어지러운 듯 이마에 손을 갖다 대고는, 토굴 안을 두세 바퀴 돌다가 침대 곁에서 우뚝 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"공정하신 하느님!\" 그가 중얼거렸다. \"이 생각은 어디서 온 것입니까? 당신에게서 온 것입니까? 이 토굴에서 자유로이 나가는 것은 오직 죽은 자뿐이니, 내가 그 죽은 자의 자리를 차지하게 해 주십시오!\"\n\n자신의 결정을 다시 곱씹을 틈도 주지 않고, 또한 그 필사적인 결심에서 마음이 흐트러지지 않도록, 그는 그 끔찍한 수의 위로 몸을 숙여 파리아가 만든 칼로 자루를 갈랐다. 그러고는 주검을 자루에서 끌어내 굴을 따라 자기 방으로 옮겨와 자신의 침상에 눕혔다. 자기가 밤마다 머리에 두르던 헝겊을 주검의 머리에 둘러 주고, 자기 이불로 덮어 주었다. 다시 한번 얼음장처럼 차가운 이마에 입을 맞추고는, 끔찍하게 부릅뜬 채 좀처럼 감기지 않는 두 눈을 감기려 했으나 헛수고였다. 그래서 머리를 벽 쪽으로 돌려놓았으니, 간수가 저녁 식사를 가져왔을 때 그가 평소처럼 잠든 줄로 믿게 하기 위해서였다. 그는 다시 굴로 들어가 침대를 벽에 붙여 놓고, 다른 감방으로 돌아와 은닉처에서 바늘과 실을 꺼냈다. 거친 캔버스 아래로 벌거벗은 살결만 만져지도록 누더기를 벗어 던지고, 자루 안으로 들어가 주검이 놓여 있던 그대로 자세를 잡은 뒤, 안쪽에서 자루 입구를 꿰맸다.\n\n만에 하나 간수가 그 순간 들어오기라도 했다면, 그의 심장 박동 소리에 들통이 났을 것이다. 당테스는 저녁 순찰이 끝날 때까지 기다릴 수도 있었다. 그러나 그는 소장이 마음을 바꿔 주검을 더 일찍 치우라고 명할까 봐 두려웠다. 그렇게 되면 그의 마지막 희망은 부서지고 말 것이었다.\n\n이제 계획은 빈틈없이 짜였고, 그가 하려는 일은 이러했다. 자기를 운반해 나가는 동안 인부들이 죽은 시신이 아니라 산 사람을 나르고 있음을 알아챈다면, 당테스는 그들에게 자신을 알아볼 틈을 주지 않을 작정이었다. 칼로 자루를 위에서 아래까지 단숨에 가르고, 그들이 놀란 틈을 타 달아날 셈이었다. 그들이 붙잡으려 한다면, 칼을 더 요긴하게 쓸 것이었다.\n\n자기를 묘지로 데려가 무덤에 눕힌다면, 그는 흙에 덮이도록 가만히 있을 작정이었다. 그러고는, 때가 밤이니, 인부들이 미처 등을 돌리기도 전에 그는 무른 흙을 헤치고 빠져나가 달아날 수 있을 것이었다. 그는 흙의 무게가 도저히 헤쳐 나올 수 없을 만큼 무겁지는 않기를 바랐다. 만약 이 일이 들통나고 흙이 너무 무거워 빠져나올 수 없다면, 그는 질식하고 말 테고, 그러면 그것대로 잘된 일, 모든 것이 끝나는 셈이었다.\n\n당테스는 전날 저녁 이후로 아무것도 먹지 못했으나, 배고픔은 생각조차 하지 않았고 지금도 마찬가지였다. 처지가 너무도 위태로워, 오직 한 가지 생각 말고는 다른 어떤 것을 헤아릴 겨를조차 없었다.\n\n당테스가 무릅쓴 첫 번째 위험은, 일곱 시에 저녁을 가져오는 간수가 바뀐 것을 알아챌지도 모른다는 것이었다. 다행히도 당테스는 적어도 스무 번은, 사람이 싫어서든 피곤해서든, 침대에 누운 채로 간수를 맞이한 적이 있었다. 그럴 때면 간수는 빵과 수프를 탁자에 놓고 한마디 말도 없이 나가곤 했다. 그러나 이번에는 간수가 평소처럼 잠자코 있지 않고 당테스에게 말을 걸 수도 있었다. 그리고 아무 대답이 없는 것을 보고 침대로 다가가, 그렇게 모든 것을 알아챌 수도 있었다.\n\n일곱 시가 되자, 당테스의 고뇌가 정말로 시작되었다. 심장에 얹은 한 손으로도 그 두근거림을 가라앉힐 수 없었고, 다른 손으로는 관자놀이에 맺힌 땀을 닦아 냈다. 이따금 온몸에 오한이 훑고 지나가며 얼음 같은 손아귀로 심장을 옥죄었다. 그럴 때면 그는 자기가 죽는 줄 알았다. 그러나 별다른 소란 없이 시간이 흘러갔고, 당테스는 첫 번째 위기를 넘겼음을 깨달았다. 좋은 징조였다.\n\n마침내 소장이 정한 시각쯤 되어, 계단에서 발소리가 들렸다. 에드몽은 때가 왔음을 느끼고 온 용기를 끌어모아 숨을 죽였다. 그와 동시에 핏줄의 두근거림까지 억누를 수 있었다면 더없이 좋았을 것이다. 두 사람의 발소리가 문 앞에서 멈췄다. 당테스는 인부 둘이 자기를 데리러 왔음을 짐작했다. 이내 그들이 들것을 내려놓는 소리가 들리자, 그 짐작은 확신으로 바뀌었다.\n\n문이 열렸고, 그를 덮은 거친 자루 너머로 희미한 빛이 당테스의 눈에 닿았다. 두 그림자가 침대로 다가오고, 세 번째 그림자는 횃불을 손에 든 채 문가에 남는 것이 보였다. 두 사내가 침대 양 끝으로 다가와 자루의 끝을 붙잡았다.\n\n\"늙고 비쩍 마른 양반치고는 꽤 무겁네.\" 머리 쪽을 들어 올리던 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"해마다 뼈 무게가 반 파운드씩 늘어난다고들 하지.\" 발치를 들어 올리던 다른 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"매듭은 묶었나?\" 먼저 말한 사내가 물었다.\n\n\"이렇게 무거운 걸 더 무겁게 해서 뭐 하게?\" 대답이 돌아왔다. \"거기 도착해서 묶으면 되지.\"\n\n\"그래, 자네 말이 맞아.\" 동료가 대답했다.\n\n'매듭이라니, 무슨 매듭이지?' 당테스는 생각했다.\n\n그들은 시신으로 짐작되는 것을 들것 위에 내려놓았다. 에드몽은 죽은 사람 노릇을 하려고 몸을 뻣뻣하게 굳혔다. 이윽고 일행은 앞장선 횃불 든 사내의 불빛을 따라 계단을 올랐다. 갑자기 그는 차고 매서운 밤공기를 느꼈고, 미스트랄이 불고 있음을 알아차렸다. 쾌감과 고통이 묘하게 뒤섞인 감각이었다.\n\n운반하는 자들은 스무 걸음쯤 가다가 멈춰 서서 들것을 땅에 내려놓았다. 그중 하나가 자리를 떴고, 당테스는 그의 신발이 포석을 딛는 소리를 들었다.\n\n'내가 지금 어디 있는 거지?' 그는 자문했다.\n\n\"이거 참, 결코 가벼운 짐이 아니라니까!\" 들것 가장자리에 걸터앉으며 다른 사내가 말했다.\n\n당테스는 당장 달아나고 싶은 충동이 일었으나, 다행히 그러지 않았다.\n\n\"불 좀 비춰 줘.\" 다른 사내가 말했다. \"안 그러면 찾는 걸 도무지 못 찾겠어.\"\n\n횃불을 든 사내는, 그리 정중한 부탁은 아니었으나 시키는 대로 했다.\n\n'대체 뭘 찾는 거지?' 에드몽은 생각했다. '아마 삽이겠지.'\n\n만족스러운 외침으로 보아, 인부가 찾던 물건을 찾아낸 모양이었다. \"드디어 찾았네.\" 그가 말했다. \"꽤나 애를 먹었지만.\"\n\n\"그래.\" 대답이 돌아왔다. \"그래도 기다린 보람은 있는 물건이지.\"\n\n이렇게 말하며 사내가 에드몽 쪽으로 다가왔고, 에드몽은 묵직한 쇠붙이가 자기 곁에 놓이는 소리를 들었다. 동시에 어떤 줄이 그의 발에 느닷없이, 아프도록 거칠게 매여졌다.\n\n\"자, 매듭은 묶었나?\" 곁에서 지켜보던 인부가 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 그것도 아주 단단히 묶었지, 장담하네.\" 대답이 돌아왔다.\n\n\"그럼 가자고.\" 그러자 들것이 다시 들렸고, 그들은 걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n그들은 쉰 걸음쯤 더 나아가다가 문을 열려고 멈춰 섰고, 그런 뒤 다시 앞으로 나아갔다. 성이 세워진 바위에 물결이 부딪치는 소리가 걸음을 옮길수록 당테스의 귀에 또렷이 들려왔다.\n\n\"날씨 한번 고약하군!\" 운반하는 자 하나가 말했다. \"바다에 한 번 담그기엔 영 안 좋은 밤이야.\"\n\n\"그러게 말이야, 신부 양반이 흠뻑 젖게 생겼군.\" 다른 자가 말했다. 그러더니 한바탕 거친 웃음이 터졌다.\n\n당테스는 그 농담을 알아듣지 못했으나, 머리카락이 곤두섰다.\n\n\"자, 드디어 다 왔다.\" 그중 하나가 말했다.\n\n\"조금만 더, 조금만 더.\" 다른 자가 말했다. \"지난번 그자는 가다가 걸려서 바위에 부딪혔던 거 잘 알잖아. 다음 날 소장이 우리더러 칠칠치 못한 놈들이라고 했지.\"\n\n그들은 대여섯 계단을 더 올랐다. 그러고 나서 당테스는 그들이 한 사람은 머리를, 다른 사람은 발꿈치를 잡고서 자기를 앞뒤로 흔들어 대는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"하나!\" 인부들이 말했다. \"둘! 셋!\"\n\n그와 동시에 당테스는 상처 입은 새처럼 허공으로 내던져지는 것을 느꼈다. 피가 얼어붙을 만큼 빠른 속도로, 떨어지고 또 떨어졌다. 그 빠른 추락을 재촉하는 묵직한 무게에 아래로 끌려가면서도, 그에게는 그 추락이 한 세기나 이어지는 듯했다. 마침내 그는 끔찍한 물보라와 함께 화살처럼 얼음장 같은 물속으로 곤두박질쳤고, 그 순간 날카로운 비명을 질렀으나, 물결 아래로 잠기면서 곧 잦아들고 말았다.\n\n당테스는 바다로 내던져진 것이었고, 발에 묶인 36파운드 포탄에 끌려 깊은 물속으로 가라앉고 있었다.\n\n바다, 그것이 바로 이프성의 묘지였다.\n\n# Chapter 21. The Island of Tiboulen\n\n당테스는 정신이 멍하고 거의 숨이 막힐 지경이었지만, 그래도 숨을 참을 만큼의 침착함은 잃지 않았다. 어떤 상황에도 대비하고 있던 그의 오른손에는 펼쳐진 칼이 쥐어져 있었다. 그는 재빨리 자루를 찢고 팔을 빼낸 다음 몸을 빼냈다. 그러나 포탄에서 벗어나려는 온갖 몸부림에도 불구하고, 포탄이 자신을 점점 더 깊은 곳으로 끌어내리는 것이 느껴졌다. 그는 몸을 굽혔고, 정말로 목이 졸리는 듯한 바로 그 순간, 필사적인 힘을 쏟아 다리를 묶은 밧줄을 끊어 냈다. 그러고는 힘껏 솟구쳐 바다 표면으로 떠올랐고, 그동안 포탄은 하마터면 그의 수의가 될 뻔했던 자루를 깊은 바닷속으로 끌고 내려갔다.\n\n당테스는 숨을 돌리기만을 기다렸다가, 들키지 않으려고 다시 물속으로 잠수했다. 두 번째로 떠올랐을 때, 그는 처음 가라앉았던 곳에서 쉰 걸음쯤 떨어진 지점에 있었다. 머리 위로는 검고 사나운 하늘이 펼쳐져 있었고, 바람이 구름을 몰아가는 사이로 이따금 반짝이는 별 하나가 모습을 드러냈다. 앞쪽으로는 음울하고 무서운 망망한 바다가 펼쳐졌고, 폭풍이 다가오기라도 하는 듯 파도가 거품을 일으키며 포효했다. 등 뒤로는 바다보다 검고 하늘보다 검은, 유령처럼 우뚝 솟은 거대한 돌 건축물이 있었다. 그 돌출한 바위들은 마치 먹잇감을 움켜쥐려고 뻗은 팔처럼 보였고, 가장 높은 바위 위에는 횃불 하나가 두 사람의 형체를 비추고 있었다.\n\n그는 그 두 형체가 바다를 바라보고 있다고 생각했다. 분명 그 기이한 무덤 파는 자들이 자신의 비명을 들었을 것이다. 당테스는 다시 잠수해 오랫동안 물속에 머물렀다. 그에게는 쉬운 일이었다. 마르세유 등대 앞 만에서 헤엄을 칠 때면 늘 구경꾼들이 떼로 몰려들었고, 그는 항구에서 으뜸가는 수영 선수로 모두에게 인정받았기 때문이다. 다시 떠올랐을 때 횃불은 사라지고 없었다.\n\n이제 방향을 가늠해야 했다. 이프성을 둘러싼 모든 섬 가운데 라토노섬과 포메그섬이 가장 가까웠지만, 라토노섬과 포메그섬에는 사람이 살았고, 돔섬도 마찬가지였다. 그러니 당테스의 모험에 가장 안전한 곳은 티불랑섬과 르메르섬이었다. 티불랑섬과 르메르섬은 이프성에서 1리외쯤 떨어져 있었다. 그래도 당테스는 그쪽으로 가기로 마음먹었다. 그러나 이 캄캄한 밤중에 어떻게 길을 찾는단 말인가?\n\n그 순간 그는 앞쪽에서 별처럼 반짝이는 플라니에 등대의 불빛을 보았다. 이 불빛을 오른쪽에 두면 티불랑섬은 약간 왼쪽에 놓이게 되니, 왼쪽으로 돌면 섬을 찾을 수 있을 터였다. 그러나 앞서 말했듯이, 이프성에서 그 섬까지는 적어도 1리외였다. 감옥에 있을 때 파리아는 그가 빈둥거리며 무기력하게 있는 것을 볼 때면 종종 이렇게 말하곤 했다.\n\n\"당테스, 그렇게 늘어져 있으면 안 되네. 탈출을 시도하다간 물에 빠져 죽고 말 거야. 자네 체력이 제대로 단련되지도, 힘쓸 준비가 되지도 않았으니 말일세.\"\n\n이 말이 파도 밑에서까지 당테스의 귓전에 울렸다. 그는 자신의 힘을 잃지 않았는지 확인하려고 서둘러 물살을 갈랐다. 그는 갇혀 지낸 세월이 자신의 힘을 조금도 앗아 가지 않았으며, 어린 시절 그토록 자주 그 품에서 뛰놀던 이 물이라는 존재를 여전히 자신이 다스리고 있음을 기쁘게 깨달았다.\n\n가차 없이 따라붙는 추격자, 두려움이 당테스의 동작을 더디게 했다. 그는 들릴 만한 소리가 없는지 귀를 기울였고, 파도 마루에 올라설 때마다 수평선을 살피며 어둠을 꿰뚫어 보려 애썼다. 등 뒤의 파도 하나하나가 추격해 오는 배처럼 느껴졌고, 그는 더욱더 힘을 쏟아 성에서 빠르게 멀어졌지만, 그만큼 기력도 소진했다. 그래도 그는 계속 헤엄쳤고, 이미 그 무시무시한 성은 어둠 속으로 사라지고 없었다. 보이지는 않았으나, 그는 그 존재를 느낄 수 있었다.\n\n한 시간이 흘렀고, 그동안 당테스는 자유롭다는 감격에 들떠 계속 파도를 갈랐다.\n\n\"어디 보자.\" 그가 혼잣말을 했다. \"한 시간 넘게 헤엄쳤지만, 바람이 거슬러 부니 속도가 더뎌졌어. 그래도 내 짐작이 틀리지 않다면, 이제 티불랑섬에 가까이 왔을 거야. 그런데 만약 내가 틀렸다면?\"\n\n몸서리가 그를 훑고 지나갔다. 그는 쉬려고 선헤엄을 치려 했지만, 바다가 너무 거칠어 그런 식으로는 기력을 회복할 수 없음을 깨달았다.\n\n\"좋아.\" 그가 말했다. \"기진맥진하거나 쥐가 날 때까지 헤엄치자. 그러면 가라앉겠지.\" 그러고는 절망의 힘을 끌어모아 헤엄쳐 나아갔다.\n\n문득 하늘이 더욱 어둡고 짙어지는 듯했고, 무거운 구름이 그를 향해 내리덮치는 듯했다. 동시에 무릎에 날카로운 통증이 느껴졌다. 그는 한순간 총에 맞은 줄 알고 총성을 기다렸으나, 아무 소리도 들리지 않았다. 그러고는 손을 뻗자 무언가 걸리는 것이 있었고, 한 번 더 손을 젓고 나서야 자신이 뭍에 닿았음을 알았다.\n\n그의 앞에는 기괴한 바위 덩어리가 솟아 있었는데, 그 무엇보다도 가장 맹렬히 타오르던 순간에 그대로 굳어 버린 거대한 불길처럼 보였다. 티불랑섬이었다. 당테스는 일어나 몇 걸음 나아갔고, 간절한 감사의 기도를 올린 뒤 화강암 위에 몸을 뉘었다. 그에게는 그 화강암이 솜털보다 부드럽게 느껴졌다. 그러고는 바람과 비에도 아랑곳없이, 완전히 탈진한 자만이 빠질 수 있는 깊고 달콤한 잠에 빠져들었다. 한 시간이 지났을 무렵, 에드몽은 천둥소리에 잠에서 깼다. 폭풍이 풀려나 그 거대한 날개로 대기를 후려치고 있었다. 이따금 번개가 불뱀처럼 하늘을 가로지르며, 거대하고 무질서한 파도처럼 굽이쳐 가는 구름을 밝혔다.\n\n당테스는 속지 않았다. 그는 두 섬 가운데 첫 번째 섬에 닿았고, 그곳은 과연 티불랑섬이었다. 그는 그 섬이 메마르고 몸을 숨길 곳이 없다는 것을 알았다. 그래서 바다가 좀 더 잔잔해지면 다시 파도 속으로 뛰어들어, 마찬가지로 황량하지만 더 크고 따라서 몸을 숨기기에 더 알맞은 르메르섬으로 헤엄쳐 가기로 마음먹었다.\n\n불쑥 튀어나온 바위 하나가 그에게 임시 피난처가 되어 주었다. 그가 막 그 아래에 몸을 숨기자마자 폭풍이 온 위세를 떨치며 몰아쳤다. 에드몽은 자신이 누운 바위가 떨리는 것을 느꼈다. 파도가 바위에 부딪치며 물보라로 그를 적셨다. 안전하게 몸을 숨기고 있었지만, 그래도 그는 맹렬히 다투는 자연의 힘과 눈부신 번갯불 한가운데서 어지러움을 느꼈다. 섬이 그 밑바닥까지 흔들리는 것 같았고, 닻을 내린 배처럼 닻줄을 끊고 자신을 폭풍의 한복판으로 끌고 가 버릴 것만 같았다.\n\n그제야 그는 스물네 시간 동안 아무것도 먹지도 마시지도 못했다는 사실을 떠올렸다. 그는 두 손을 뻗어, 바위 우묵한 곳에 고인 빗물을 게걸스럽게 들이켰다.\n\n그가 몸을 일으키는데, 하늘 가장 먼 끝까지 갈라놓는 듯한 번개 한 줄기가 어둠을 밝혔다. 그 불빛으로, 르메르섬과 크루아젤곶 사이 4분의 1리외쯤 떨어진 곳에서, 당테스는 바람과 파도의 위세에 떠밀려 유령처럼 빠르게 내달리는 고깃배 한 척을 보았다. 잠시 후, 그는 그 배가 무서운 속도로 다가오는 것을 다시 보았다. 당테스는 위험을 알리려고 목청껏 소리쳤으나, 그들도 이미 위험을 알고 있었다. 또 한 번 번쩍이는 빛에, 그는 네 사람이 부러진 돛대와 삭구에 매달려 있고, 다섯 번째 사람은 부서진 키에 매달려 있는 것을 보았다. 그가 본 사람들도 분명 그를 보았으니, 그들의 외침이 바람을 타고 그의 귀에 실려 왔기 때문이다. 쪼개진 돛대 위에서 갈가리 찢긴 돛 한 폭이 펄럭이고 있었다. 갑자기 그 돛을 붙들고 있던 밧줄이 끊어졌고, 돛은 거대한 바닷새처럼 밤의 어둠 속으로 사라졌다.\n\n그 순간 요란한 충돌 소리와 함께 비명이 들려왔다. 당테스는 바위 위 자리에서 부서진 배와, 그 잔해 사이로 떠다니는 가엾은 선원들의 몸뚱이를 보았다. 그러고는 다시 온통 어두워졌다.\n\n당테스는 제 몸이 산산조각 날 위험을 무릅쓰고 바위를 타고 내려갔다. 귀를 기울이고 더듬어 보았으나, 아무것도 들리지도 보이지도 않았다. 비명은 그쳤고, 폭풍만 여전히 사납게 몰아쳤다. 차츰 바람이 잦아들었고, 거대한 잿빛 구름이 서쪽으로 굴러갔으며, 푸른 하늘이 밝은 별들을 총총히 박은 채 모습을 드러냈다. 이윽고 수평선에 붉은 띠가 어렸고, 파도가 하얗게 부서지더니, 빛이 그 위에서 일렁이며 거품 이는 마루를 황금빛으로 물들였다. 날이 밝은 것이다.\n\n당테스는 이 장엄한 광경 앞에 말없이 꼼짝 않고 서 있었다. 마치 처음 보는 광경인 듯했다. 실제로 이프성에 갇힌 뒤로 그는 이런 장면을 볼 수 있다는 사실조차 잊고 지냈다. 그는 요새 쪽으로 몸을 돌려 바다와 뭍을 둘러보았다. 그 음울한 건물은 위풍당당한 위용으로 바다 한복판에 솟아 온 풍경을 지배하는 듯했다. 다섯 시쯤이었다. 바다는 점점 더 잔잔해지고 있었다.\n\n'두세 시간 안에,' 당테스는 생각했다. '간수가 내 방에 들어와 가엾은 친구의 시신을 발견하고, 그게 누구인지 알아본 다음, 나를 찾으려다 헛수고하고는 경보를 울리겠지. 그러면 굴이 발각될 테고, 나를 바다에 던진 자들, 내가 지른 비명을 분명 들었을 그자들이 추궁을 받겠지. 그런 다음 무장한 병사들을 가득 태운 배들이 가엾은 도망자를 뒤쫓을 테고. 대포가 울려, 벌거벗고 굶주린 채 떠도는 사내에게 누구도 거처를 내주지 말라고 모두에게 경고하겠지. 마르세유 경찰은 뭍에서 경계를 펼 테고, 성주는 바다에서 나를 추격하겠지. 나는 춥고, 배가 고프다. 나를 살려 준 칼마저 잃어버렸어. 오, 하느님, 저는 분명 충분히 고통받았습니다! 부디 저를 가엾이 여기시어, 제 힘으로 할 수 없는 일을 대신해 주소서.'\n\n당테스가 이프성 쪽으로 눈을 돌린 채 이 기도를 올리고 있을 때, 그는 포메그섬의 먼 끄트머리 너머로 작은 배 한 척이 삼각돛을 달고, 먹이를 찾는 갈매기처럼 바다 위를 스치듯 나아가는 것을 보았다. 선원다운 눈으로 그는 그것이 제노바의 타르탄선임을 알아보았다. 그 배는 마르세유 항구를 빠져나와, 날카로운 뱃머리로 파도를 가르며 빠르게 먼바다로 나아가고 있었다.\n\n\"오.\" 에드몽이 외쳤다. \"추궁당하고, 정체가 드러나, 마르세유로 도로 끌려갈 게 두렵지만 않다면, 반 시간 안에 저 배에 닿을 수 있을 텐데! 어떻게 하지? 무슨 이야기를 지어내야 할까? 해안을 따라 장사하는 척하지만 실은 밀수꾼인 저자들은, 선행을 베푸느니 나를 팔아넘기는 편을 택하겠지. 기다려야 해. 하지만 그럴 수 없어. 굶어 죽을 지경이니까. 몇 시간만 지나면 내 힘은 완전히 바닥날 거야. 게다가 어쩌면 요새에서는 아직 내가 사라진 걸 모를지도 몰라. 나는 어젯밤 난파한 선원 중 하나로 행세할 수 있어. 내 이야기를 받아들일 거야. 나를 반박할 사람이 아무도 남지 않았으니까.\"\n\n이렇게 중얼거리며 당테스는 고깃배가 난파한 지점 쪽을 바라보았고, 흠칫 놀랐다. 선원 하나가 쓰던 빨간 모자가 바위 끄트머리에 걸려 있었고, 배의 용골 일부였던 목재 몇 토막이 바위 아래에 떠다니고 있었다. 순식간에 당테스의 계획이 섰다. 그는 그 모자로 헤엄쳐 가 머리에 쓰고, 목재 하나를 붙잡은 다음, 배가 나아가는 항로를 가로지르도록 헤엄쳐 나갔다.\n\n\"나는 살았다!\" 그가 중얼거렸다. 이 확신이 그의 힘을 되살렸다.\n\n이윽고 그는 그 배가 정면으로 바람을 받으며 이프성과 플라니에 탑 사이에서 갈지자로 항해하고 있음을 알아보았다. 한순간 그는 배가 해안에 붙어 있지 않고 먼바다로 나가 버리지 않을까 두려웠다. 그러나 곧 그 배가, 이탈리아로 향하는 대부분의 배들처럼, 자로스섬과 칼라사레뉴섬 사이를 지나가리라는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n그러는 사이 배와 헤엄치는 사람은 자신도 모르게 점점 가까워졌고, 한 번 방향을 트는 사이에 타르탄선은 그에게서 4분의 1마일 안쪽으로 내려왔다. 그는 파도 위로 솟구쳐 조난 신호를 보냈으나, 배 위의 누구도 그를 보지 못했고, 배는 다시 방향을 틀어 나아갔다. 당테스는 소리치고 싶었으나, 바람이 그 목소리를 삼켜 버리리라는 것을 알고 있었다.\n\n그제야 그는 목재를 챙겨 둔 자신의 신중함이 다행스러웠다. 그것이 없었다면 그는 어쩌면 배에 닿지도 못했을 테고, 사람들의 눈길을 끄는 데 실패할 경우 해안으로 돌아오는 것도 분명 불가능했을 것이다.\n\n당테스는 배가 어느 항로를 택할지 거의 확신하면서도, 배가 방향을 틀어 자신에게로 다가올 때까지 마음 졸이며 지켜보았다. 그러고는 앞으로 나아갔으나, 미처 닿기도 전에 배가 또다시 항로를 바꾸었다. 그는 안간힘을 쏟아 물 밖으로 반쯤 솟구쳐 모자를 흔들며, 선원 특유의 우렁찬 고함을 질렀다. 이번에는 그의 모습이 보이고 소리도 들렸는지, 타르탄선이 곧장 그를 향해 키를 돌렸다. 동시에 그는 그들이 보트를 내리려는 것을 보았다.\n\n잠시 후, 두 사람이 노를 젓는 보트가 그를 향해 빠르게 다가왔다. 당테스는 이제 쓸모없어졌다고 여긴 목재를 놓고, 그들을 만나러 힘차게 헤엄쳤다. 그러나 그는 제 힘을 너무 과신했고, 그제야 그 목재가 자신에게 얼마나 큰 도움이 되었는지 깨달았다. 팔이 뻣뻣해지고, 다리가 유연함을 잃었으며, 숨이 거의 막힐 지경이었다.\n\n그가 다시 소리쳤다. 두 선원이 더욱 힘껏 노를 저었고, 그중 한 사람이 이탈리아어로 외쳤다. \"힘내요!\"\n\n그 말이 그의 귀에 닿는 순간, 이제는 넘어설 힘조차 없는 파도 하나가 그의 머리 위를 덮쳤다. 그는 다시 수면으로 떠올라, 물에 빠진 사람의 마지막 필사적인 안간힘으로 발버둥 치며 세 번째 비명을 질렀고, 마치 그 죽음의 포탄이 다시 발에 매달린 것처럼 가라앉는 것을 느꼈다. 물이 그의 머리 위를 넘었고, 하늘이 잿빛으로 변했다. 발작하듯 한 번 몸을 틀자 그는 다시 수면으로 떠올랐다. 누군가 그의 머리채를 붙잡는 것이 느껴졌고, 그다음에는 아무것도 보이지도 들리지도 않았다. 그는 정신을 잃었다.\n\n당테스가 눈을 떴을 때, 그는 타르탄선 갑판 위에 누워 있었다. 그가 가장 먼저 신경 쓴 것은 배가 어느 쪽으로 가고 있는지였다. 배는 이프성을 빠르게 뒤로하고 있었다. 당테스는 너무 기진맥진한 나머지, 그가 내뱉은 기쁨의 탄성이 한숨으로 오해받을 정도였다.\n\n앞서 말했듯이 그는 갑판에 누워 있었다. 한 선원이 모직 천으로 그의 팔다리를 문지르고 있었고, \"힘내요!\"라고 외쳤던 그 사람으로 짐작되는 또 한 선원은 럼주가 가득 든 호리병을 그의 입에 대 주고 있었다. 한편 세 번째 사람, 도선사이자 선장을 겸한 늙은 선원은, 어제 자신은 모면했으나 내일은 자신에게 닥칠지도 모르는 불행에 대해 사람들이 느끼는 그 이기적인 연민의 눈길로 지켜보고 있었다.\n\n럼주 몇 방울이 멎었던 숨을 되살렸고, 팔다리를 문질러 주자 다시 탄력이 돌아왔다.\n\n\"당신은 누구요?\" 도선사가 서툰 프랑스어로 물었다.\n\n\"저는,\" 당테스가 서툰 이탈리아어로 대답했다. \"몰타 선원입니다. 시러큐스에서 곡물을 싣고 오던 길이었지요. 어젯밤 폭풍이 모르지우곶에서 우리를 덮쳤고, 우리는 이 바위에 부딪혀 난파했습니다.\"\n\n\"어디서 오는 길이오?\"\n\n\"제가 운 좋게 매달릴 수 있었던 저 바위에서 옵니다. 우리 선장과 나머지 선원들은 모두 목숨을 잃었지요. 저는 당신네 배를 보았고, 황량한 섬에 버려져 죽을까 두려워, 잔해 한 조각에 의지해 헤엄쳐 나와 당신들 항로를 가로막아 보려 했습니다. 당신들이 제 목숨을 구해 주셨으니, 감사드립니다.\" 당테스가 말을 이었다. \"당신네 선원 한 분이 제 머리채를 붙잡았을 때, 저는 죽은 목숨이었습니다.\"\n\n\"그게 바로 나요.\" 솔직하고 사내다운 인상의 선원이 말했다. \"마침맞은 때였지요. 당신은 가라앉고 있었으니까.\"\n\n\"네.\" 당테스가 손을 내밀며 대답했다. \"다시 한번 감사드립니다.\"\n\n\"그래도 나는 망설일 뻔했소.\" 선원이 대답했다. \"당신은 정직한 사람이라기보다 산적처럼 보였거든. 수염은 여섯 치나 되고, 머리카락은 한 자나 길었으니 말이오.\"\n\n당테스는 이프성에 있는 내내 머리도 수염도 한 번 자르지 않았다는 사실을 떠올렸다.\n\n\"네.\" 그가 말했다. \"동굴의 성모님께 맹세를 했지요. 위급한 순간에 목숨을 구하게 되면 십 년 동안 머리와 수염을 자르지 않겠다고요. 그런데 오늘로 그 맹세가 끝났네요.\"\n\n\"자, 이제 당신을 어떻게 하면 좋겠소?\" 선장이 물었다.\n\n\"아아, 좋으실 대로 하십시오. 제 선장은 죽었고, 저는 가까스로 살아났지요. 하지만 저는 훌륭한 선원입니다. 처음 닿는 항구에 저를 내려 주십시오. 일자리쯤은 틀림없이 찾을 수 있을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"지중해를 잘 아시오?\"\n\n\"어릴 적부터 그 바다를 누볐습니다.\"\n\n\"좋은 항구들을 잘 아시오?\"\n\n\"눈을 가린 채로도 드나들 수 있는 항구가 한둘이 아니지요.\"\n\n\"이보세요, 선장님.\" 당테스에게 \"힘내요!\"라고 외쳤던 선원이 말했다. \"이 사람 말이 사실이라면, 우리와 함께 지내지 못할 이유가 어디 있겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그 말이 사실이라면 말이지.\" 선장이 미심쩍은 듯 말했다. \"하지만 지금 같은 처지라면 무슨 약속이든 하고서, 나중에 지킬지 말지는 운에 맡길 테지.\"\n\n\"저는 약속한 것보다 더 해 보이겠습니다.\" 당테스가 말했다.\n\n\"두고 봅시다.\" 상대가 미소 지으며 대답했다.\n\n\"어디로 가는 길입니까?\" 당테스가 물었다.\n\n\"리보르노로 가오.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 어째서 그렇게 자주 방향을 틀지 않고, 바람을 좀 더 바짝 받으며 가지 않으십니까?\"\n\n\"그랬다간 곧장 리옹섬으로 처박힐 테니까.\"\n\n\"제가 그 섬을 20패덤 차이로 비껴 지나가게 해 보이겠습니다.\"\n\n\"키를 잡아 보시오. 당신 솜씨가 어떤지 봅시다.\"\n\n젊은이는 키를 잡고, 배가 키에 곧바로 반응하는지 가늠해 보았다. 일류 선박은 아니었으나, 그래도 제법 말을 잘 듣는 편이었다.\n\n\"아딧줄을 잡아라.\" 그가 말했다. 선원으로 일하는 네 사람이 명령에 따랐고, 도선사는 지켜보았다. \"팽팽히 당겨라.\"\n\n그들이 따랐다.\n\n\"매어라.\" 이 명령도 그대로 이행되었다. 그리고 배는 당테스가 예언한 대로 바람 부는 쪽으로 20패덤 차이를 두고 지나갔다.\n\n\"브라보!\" 선장이 외쳤다.\n\n\"브라보!\" 선원들이 따라 외쳤다. 그들은 모두 놀란 눈으로 이 사내를 바라보았다. 이제 그의 눈에서는 그들이 예상치 못했던 총기가, 그의 몸에서는 미처 짐작지 못했던 활력이 드러나고 있었다.\n\n\"보십시오.\" 당테스가 키에서 손을 떼며 말했다. \"적어도 항해하는 동안에는 제가 당신들께 도움이 될 겁니다. 리보르노에서 제가 필요 없으시면, 거기 내려 주십시오. 그러면 제 먹은 음식값과 빌려 입은 옷값을, 처음 받는 봉급에서 갚아 드리지요.\"\n\n\"아.\" 선장이 말했다. \"당신이 사리에 밝은 사람이라면, 우리 잘 합의할 수 있겠소.\"\n\n\"다른 사람들에게 주시는 만큼만 주십시오. 그러면 됩니다.\" 당테스가 대답했다.\n\n\"그건 공정하지 않은데요.\" 당테스를 구한 선원이 말했다. \"당신은 우리보다 아는 게 많으니까요.\"\n\n\"그게 자네와 무슨 상관인가, 자코포?\" 선장이 대꾸했다. \"누구든 제가 원하는 만큼 청할 자유는 있는 거지.\"\n\n\"그건 그렇습니다.\" 자코포가 대답했다. \"그저 한마디 한 것뿐인걸요.\"\n\n\"그럴 거면, 가지고 있거든 이 사람에게 윗옷하고 바지나 한 벌 찾아 주는 게 훨씬 나을 걸세.\"\n\n\"윗옷은 없습니다.\" 자코포가 말했다. \"하지만 셔츠 한 장하고 바지 한 벌은 있지요.\"\n\n\"그거면 충분합니다.\" 당테스가 말을 가로챘다. 자코포는 선창으로 뛰어 내려가, 곧 에드몽이 원하던 것을 가지고 돌아왔다.\n\n\"자, 그럼 그 밖에 더 필요한 건 없소?\" 선장이 물었다.\n\n\"빵 한 조각하고, 아까 맛본 그 일품 럼주 한 잔만 더 주십시오. 오랫동안 아무것도 먹지도 마시지도 못했거든요.\" 그는 마흔 시간 동안 음식을 입에 대지 못했다. 빵 한 조각이 건네졌고, 자코포가 호리병을 내밀었다.\n\n\"키를 좌현으로!\" 선장이 키잡이에게 외쳤다. 당테스는 호리병을 입으로 가져가다 그쪽을 흘끗 보았고, 그러고는 손을 허공에 든 채 멈췄다.\n\n\"이런! 이프성에 무슨 일이 생겼나?\" 선장이 말했다.\n\n당테스의 눈길을 끌었던 작고 하얀 구름 한 점이 이프성 능보 꼭대기에 걸려 있었다. 동시에 어렴풋한 포성이 들려왔다. 선원들이 서로를 마주 보았다.\n\n\"이게 무슨 일이지?\" 선장이 물었다.\n\n\"이프성에서 죄수 하나가 탈출해서, 경보 대포를 쏘는 겁니다.\" 당테스가 대답했다. 선장이 그를 흘끗 보았으나, 그가 럼주를 입에 대고 어찌나 태연하게 들이켜는지, 선장에게 의심이 있었더라도 이내 사그라지고 말았다.\n\n\"꽤 독한 럼주로군요!\" 당테스가 소매로 이마를 훔치며 말했다.\n\n'어쨌든,' 선장이 속으로 중얼거렸다. '정말 그렇다면, 더욱 잘된 일이지. 좀처럼 보기 드문 일꾼을 하나 얻은 셈이니까.'\n\n당테스는 피곤하다는 핑계로 키를 잡겠다고 청했다. 키잡이는 짐을 덜어 기뻐하며 선장을 바라보았고, 선장은 새 동료에게 키를 맡겨도 좋다는 신호를 보냈다. 그리하여 당테스는 마르세유에서 눈을 뗄 수 있게 되었다.\n\n\"오늘이 며칠이오?\" 그가 곁에 앉은 자코포에게 물었다.\n\n\"2월 28일이오.\"\n\n\"몇 년도요?\"\n\n\"몇 년도냐니, 그걸 나한테 묻는 거요?\"\n\n\"네.\" 젊은이가 대답했다. \"몇 년도냐고 묻는 겁니다!\"\n\n\"그새 잊어버린 거요?\"\n\n\"어젯밤 어찌나 혼이 났는지,\" 당테스가 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"기억을 거의 잃어버릴 지경이었거든요. 그래서 올해가 몇 년도냐고 묻는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"1829년이지요.\" 자코포가 대답했다.\n\n당테스가 체포된 지, 날짜까지 꼭 들어맞게 14년이 흐른 것이었다. 그가 이프성에 들어갈 때 열아홉이었고, 탈출한 지금은 서른셋이었다. 서글픈 미소가 그의 얼굴을 스쳤다. 그는 분명 자신을 죽은 사람으로 여기고 있을 메르세데스가 어떻게 되었을지 자문했다. 이윽고, 그토록 길고 비참한 옥살이를 안긴 세 사내를 떠올리자 그의 눈이 증오로 번득였다. 그는 지하 감옥에서 했던, 가차 없는 복수의 맹세를 당글라르와 페르낭과 빌포르를 향해 다시금 새겼다.\n\n이 맹세는 더 이상 헛된 위협이 아니었다. 지중해에서 가장 빠른 배라 해도, 돛이란 돛은 죄다 펼친 채 바람을 타고 리보르노로 날아가는 이 작은 타르탄선을 따라잡지는 못할 터였기 때문이다.\n\n# Chapter 22. The Smugglers\n\n당테스는 배에 오른 지 하루도 지나지 않아 자신이 어떤 부류의 사람들 틈에 끼게 되었는지 또렷이 파악했다. 라죈아멜리호—그 제노바산 삼각돛배의 이름이었다—를 부리는 노련한 선장은 파리아 신부의 가르침을 받은 적이 없었지만, 지중해라 불리는 그 거대한 호수의 연안에서 쓰이는 온갖 언어를 아랍어부터 프로방스어까지 얼추 알고 있었다. 덕분에 그는 늘 성가시고 입이 가벼운 통역을 쓸 필요가 없었고, 바다에서 마주치는 배들이나 해안을 따라 항해하는 작은 배들, 또는 이름도 나라도 직업도 없이 항구의 부두에서 늘 어슬렁거리는 사람들과 손쉽게 소통할 수 있었다. 그런 자들은 눈에 보이는 생계 수단이 없으니, 그들이 살아가는 은밀하고 알 수 없는 방편이란 섭리가 직접 내려 준 선물이라 여길 수밖에 없었다. 당테스가 밀수선에 올랐다고 보는 것이 타당했다.\n\n처음에 선장은 당테스를 어느 정도 미심쩍어하며 배에 받아들였다. 그는 해안의 세관원들에게 아주 잘 알려진 인물이었고, 그 위인들과 자기 사이에는 끊임없는 두뇌 싸움이 벌어지고 있었기에, 당테스가 그 부지런한 권리와 의무의 파수꾼들이 보낸 첩자일지도 모른다고 처음에는 생각했다. 그들이 자기 일의 비밀을 캐내려고 이런 교묘한 수단을 쓴 게 아닐까 의심한 것이다. 그러나 당테스가 능숙하게 배를 다루는 솜씨를 보고는 의심을 말끔히 거두었다. 그러다 이프성 보루 위로 떠오르는 가벼운 연기 깃털을 보고 멀리서 들려오는 포성을 들었을 때, 그는 문득 자기 배에 왕의 행차처럼 오가는 길마다 예포가 울리는 인물을 태우고 있다는 생각이 들었다. 솔직히 말해 이런 생각은, 새로 온 자가 세관원으로 드러나는 것보다는 그를 덜 불안하게 했다. 그러나 이 추측 역시 신참이 더할 나위 없이 태연한 모습을 보이자 첫 번째 추측과 마찬가지로 사라져 버렸다.\n\n이리하여 에드몽은 선주가 자신이 누구인지 모르는 채로 선주가 어떤 인물인지를 아는 잇점을 누렸다. 그 늙은 선원과 선원들이 아무리 그의 속을 떠보려 해도 더 이상 아무것도 캐내지 못했다. 그는 마르세유만큼이나 잘 아는 나폴리와 몰타를 정확하게 묘사하며, 처음 한 이야기를 끝까지 굳건히 고수했다. 이렇게 해서 교활한 그 제노바 사람은 에드몽에게 보기 좋게 속아 넘어갔다. 에드몽의 온화한 태도와 항해 솜씨, 그리고 감탄스러울 만큼 능청스러운 위장술이 그에게 유리하게 작용했다. 더구나 그 제노바 사람은 알아야 할 것만 알고 믿어야 할 것만 믿는 약삭빠른 부류 가운데 하나였는지도 모른다.\n\n이런 상호 양해 속에서 그들은 리보르노에 닿았다. 여기서 에드몽은 또 다른 시험을 치러야 했다. 십사 년 동안 제 얼굴을 본 적이 없으니, 자기 자신을 알아볼 수 있을지 확인해야 했던 것이다. 그는 젊은 시절의 자기 모습을 그런대로 잘 기억하고 있었고, 이제 그 사내가 어떻게 변했는지 알아볼 참이었다. 동료들은 그가 서원을 다 마쳤다고 믿었다. 리보르노에는 스무 번도 넘게 들렀던 터라, 그는 생페르디낭 거리의 어느 이발사를 떠올렸다. 수염과 머리를 깎으러 그곳에 갔다. 이발사는 그 길고 숱 많은 검은 머리와 수염을 보고 깜짝 놀랐다. 티치아노의 초상화 속 인물 같은 머리였다. 그 시절에는 그토록 긴 수염과 머리가 유행이 아니었다. 요즘 같으면 이발사는 그런 멋진 장점을 타고난 사내가 제 발로 그것을 없애겠다고 할 때나 놀랐을 것이다. 리보르노의 이발사는 아무 말 없이 일을 시작했다.\n\n작업이 끝나고 에드몽이 턱이 매끈해지고 머리가 평소 길이로 돌아온 것을 느끼자, 그는 거울을 청했다. 앞서 말했듯이 그는 이제 서른세 살이었고, 십사 년의 옥살이는 그의 모습을 크게 바꿔 놓았다.\n\n당테스는 둥글고 환하게 웃는 얼굴로 이프성에 들어갔었다. 인생의 첫 길이 순탄하기만 했고 과거에 걸맞은 미래를 꿈꾸던, 젊고 행복한 사내의 얼굴이었다. 그 모든 것이 이제 달라졌다. 갸름하던 얼굴은 길어졌고, 미소 짓던 입가에는 결단을 드러내는 단단하고 또렷한 선이 패었다. 사색으로 골이 진 이마 아래 눈썹은 활처럼 휘었고, 두 눈에는 우수가 가득했으며, 그 깊은 곳에서 이따금 인간에 대한 혐오와 증오의 음울한 불꽃이 번득였다. 오랫동안 햇빛을 보지 못한 그의 안색은 이제 창백한 빛을 띠었는데, 검은 머리에 둘러싸인 그 얼굴은 북방 사내의 귀족적인 아름다움을 자아냈다. 게다가 그가 쌓아 올린 깊은 학식은 그의 이목구비에 세련된 지성의 표정을 퍼뜨렸다. 또한 본래 늠름한 체격을 타고난 그는, 오랫동안 모든 힘을 제 안에 응축해 온 몸이 지니는 그 강건함을 얻었다.\n\n가냘프고 예민했던 몸의 우아함은 이제 둥글고 근육진 체구의 단단함으로 바뀌어 있었다. 목소리로 말하자면, 기도와 흐느낌과 저주가 그것을 바꿔 놓아, 때로는 유난히 사무치는 부드러움을 띠다가도 때로는 거칠고 거의 쉰 소리가 났다.\n\n게다가 오랫동안 어스름과 어둠 속에 있었던 탓에 그의 눈은 하이에나나 늑대처럼 밤에도 사물을 분간하는 능력을 얻었다. 에드몽은 제 모습을 보고 미소 지었다. 가장 친한 친구가—설령 아직 그에게 친구가 남아 있다 한들—그를 알아보기란 불가능했다. 그 자신조차 자기를 알아보지 못했으니까.\n\n라죈아멜리호의 선장은 에드몽처럼 쓸모 있는 사내를 선원으로 붙들어 두고 싶은 마음이 간절해, 앞으로의 수익에서 미리 자금을 빌려주겠다고 제안했고 에드몽은 그것을 받아들였다. 그를 처음으로 탈바꿈시켜 준 이발소를 나선 뒤, 그가 다음으로 한 일은 가게에 들러 선원 복장 한 벌을 통째로 사는 것이었다. 누구나 알다시피 아주 단순한 차림으로, 흰 바지와 줄무늬 셔츠, 그리고 모자로 이루어진 옷이었다.\n\n에드몽은 이 차림으로, 자코포에게 빌렸던 셔츠와 바지를 돌려주며 그 삼각돛배의 선장 앞에 다시 나타났다. 선장은 그의 이야기를 몇 번이나 거듭 들은 뒤에야 겨우 믿을 수 있었고, 또 그 말끔하고 단정한 선원이 자기가 발가벗긴 채 거의 익사 직전에 건져 올렸던 그 사내—숱이 엉킨 수염에 머리는 해초로 헝클어지고 몸은 바닷물에 절어 있던 그 사내—임을 알아볼 수 있었다. 호감 가는 그의 모습에 끌린 선장은 당테스에게 다시 한번 고용 제안을 했다. 그러나 자기 나름의 계획이 있던 당테스는 석 달 이상은 안 된다고 했다.\n\n라죈아멜리호에는 아주 활기찬 선원들이 있었고, 그들은 시간을 최대한 아끼는 선장에게 매우 순종적이었다. 리보르노에 닿은 지 일주일도 안 되어 배의 선창은 무늬 찍은 모슬린과 밀수 면포, 영국제 화약, 그리고 세금 도장이 깜빡 잊힌 담배로 가득 찼다. 선장은 이 모든 것을 세금 한 푼 안 내고 리보르노에서 빼내, 코르시카 해안에 부려야 했다. 그곳에서 어떤 투기꾼들이 화물을 프랑스로 보내는 일을 맡기로 되어 있었다.\n\n그들은 출항했다. 에드몽은 다시금 젊은 시절 처음 보았던 그 지평선, 감옥에서 그토록 자주 꿈꾸었던 그 쪽빛 바다를 가르고 있었다. 그는 고르고나섬을 오른쪽에, 라피아노자섬을 왼쪽에 두고 파올리와 나폴레옹의 고장을 향해 나아갔다.\n\n이튿날 아침, 늘 그렇듯 이른 시각에 갑판에 오른 선장은 당테스가 뱃전에 기댄 채 화강암 바위 더미를 골똘히 바라보고 있는 것을 발견했다. 떠오르는 해가 장밋빛으로 물들이고 있는 그 바위 더미는 바로 몬테크리스토섬이었다.\n\n라죈아멜리호는 그 섬을 왼쪽으로 4분의 3리외쯤 떨어뜨려 두고 코르시카를 향해 나아갔다. 당테스는 그토록 흥미로운 이름을 지닌 그 섬 곁을 이렇게 가까이 지나며 생각했다. 바다로 뛰어들기만 하면 반 시간 안에 약속의 땅에 닿을 수 있으리라고. 그러나 그러고 나서, 보물을 찾아낼 도구도 없고 제 몸을 지킬 무기도 없으니 무엇을 할 수 있단 말인가? 게다가 선원들이 뭐라고 하겠는가? 선장은 무엇을 떠올리겠는가? 기다려야 했다.\n\n다행히 당테스는 기다리는 법을 배운 사람이었다. 자유를 얻기까지 십사 년을 기다렸으니, 이제 자유로워진 그는 부를 위해서라면 적어도 여섯 달, 아니 일 년쯤은 기다릴 수 있었다. 만약 재물 없는 자유가 주어졌더라도 그는 그것을 받아들이지 않았겠는가? 게다가 그 재물이 한낱 헛것은 아닐까? 가엾은 파리아 신부의 머릿속에서 태어난 그것이, 신부와 함께 죽어 버린 것은 아닐까? 하기야 스파다 추기경의 편지는 유난히 자세했고, 당테스는 그것을 한 글자도 잊지 않았기에 처음부터 끝까지 속으로 되뇌어 보았다.\n\n저녁이 왔다. 에드몽은 그 섬이 황혼의 그늘에 물들었다가, 이윽고 어둠 속으로 모든 이의 눈에서 사라지는 것을 지켜보았다. 다만 그만은 예외였다. 감옥의 어둠에 길든 그의 눈은 그것을 가장 마지막까지 바라볼 수 있었고, 그는 갑판에 홀로 남아 있었으니까. 이튿날 아침은 알레리아 앞바다에서 밝았다. 그들은 하루 종일 해안을 따라 항해했고, 저녁이 되자 뭍에 피워 놓은 불을 보았다. 그 위치는 분명 상륙 신호였으니, 돛대 꼭대기에는 깃발 대신 배의 등불이 내걸렸고, 그들은 해안에서 포 한 발 거리까지 다가갔다. 당테스는 라죈아멜리호의 선장이 뭍에 가까워지자 작은 회전포 두 문을 거치하는 것을 알아챘다. 그 포들은 큰 소리를 내지 않고도 4온스짜리 탄알을 천 보가량 날려 보낼 수 있었다.\n\n그러나 이번만큼은 그 대비가 쓸데없었고, 모든 일이 더할 나위 없이 매끄럽고 정중하게 진행되었다. 작은 거룻배 네 척이 거의 소리도 없이 삼각돛배 곁으로 다가왔고, 배는 그 인사에 답하듯 제 거룻배를 바다에 내렸다. 다섯 척의 배가 어찌나 일을 잘 해냈던지, 새벽 두 시 무렵에는 라죈아멜리호의 화물이 죄다 빠져나가 단단한 땅에 올라 있었다. 라죈아멜리호의 선장은 어찌나 빈틈없는 사내였던지, 바로 그날 밤 수익을 나누었고, 저마다 토스카나 리브르로 백 리브르, 그러니까 약 80프랑씩을 받았다.\n\n그러나 항해는 끝나지 않았다. 그들은 뱃머리를 사르데냐 쪽으로 돌렸다. 그곳에서 부려 낸 짐을 대신할 새 화물을 실을 작정이었다. 두 번째 작업도 첫 번째만큼 성공적이었다. 라죈아멜리호는 운이 좋았다. 이 새 화물은 루카 공국 연안으로 보내질 것이었고, 거의 전부가 아바나산 시가와 셰리주, 말라가 포도주였다.\n\n그곳에서 그들은 세금을 피하느라 작은 충돌을 겪었다. 사실 세관은 라죈아멜리호 선장의 영원한 숙적이었다. 세관원 하나가 쓰러졌고 선원 둘이 다쳤는데, 당테스가 그중 하나였다. 탄알이 그의 왼쪽 어깨를 스친 것이다. 당테스는 이 싸움이 거의 반가웠고, 다친 것조차 거의 기뻤다. 어떤 눈으로 위험을 바라볼 수 있는지, 또 어떤 인내로 고통을 견딜 수 있는지를 가르쳐 준 모진 수업이었으니까. 그는 미소를 띤 채 위험을 마주했고, 부상을 입었을 때는 저 위대한 철학자처럼 외쳤다. \"고통이여, 너는 악이 아니다.\"\n\n게다가 그는 치명상을 입은 세관원을 바라보았는데, 싸움이 끓어오르게 한 격정 탓인지 아니면 인간적 정의 차가워진 탓인지, 그 광경은 그에게 거의 아무런 인상도 남기지 못했다. 당테스는 자기가 가고자 하는 길 위에 있었고, 이루고자 하는 목표를 향해 나아가고 있었다. 그의 가슴속 심장은 차츰 돌처럼 굳어 가고 있었다. 그가 쓰러지는 것을 본 자코포는 그가 죽은 줄로 알고 달려가 그를 일으켜 세웠고, 헌신적인 동료의 온갖 정성으로 그를 돌봐 주었다.\n\n그렇다면 이 세상은 팡글로스 박사가 믿었던 것만큼 좋지도 않았지만, 당테스가 생각하던 것만큼 악하지도 않았다. 동료에게서 기대할 것이라곤 그가 받을 몫의 상금을 물려받는 일뿐인 이 사내가, 그가 쓰러지는 것을 보고 그토록 슬퍼했으니 말이다. 다행히 앞서 말했듯 에드몽은 다치기만 했고, 사르데냐의 노파들이 일정한 철에 캐어 밀수꾼들에게 파는 어떤 약초 덕분에 상처는 곧 아물었다. 그러자 에드몽은 자코포를 시험해 보기로 마음먹고, 보살펴 준 보답으로 자기 몫의 상금을 나눠 주겠다고 했다. 그러나 자코포는 분개하며 그것을 거절했다.\n\n자코포가 처음부터 에드몽에게 쏟은 따뜻한 헌신에 에드몽도 어느 정도의 애정을 품게 되었다. 그러나 자코포에게는 그것으로 충분했다. 그는 에드몽이 더 높은 자리에 설 자격이 있는 사람임을 본능적으로 느꼈으니까. 에드몽이 다른 모든 이에게는 숨겨 온 그 우월함을 말이다. 그리하여 이때부터 에드몽이 보여 주는 친절만으로도 그 용감한 선원에게는 족했다.\n\n그러다 배 위의 긴 나날, 순풍이 돛을 부풀려 준 덕에 배가 쪽빛 바다 위를 안전하게 미끄러져 나가며 키잡이의 손길 외에는 아무런 보살핌도 필요로 하지 않을 때면, 에드몽은 손에 해도를 들고 자코포의 스승이 되었다. 가엾은 파리아 신부가 자신의 스승이었던 것처럼 말이다. 그는 자코포에게 해안의 방위를 일러 주고, 나침반의 편차를 설명해 주었으며, 우리 머리 위에 펼쳐진 저 거대한 책, 사람들이 하늘이라 부르고 신이 다이아몬드 글자로 쪽빛 위에 글을 써 놓은 그 책을 읽는 법을 가르쳐 주었다.\n\n자코포가 그에게 \"나 같은 가난한 선원에게 이런 것들을 다 가르쳐서 무슨 소용이 있습니까?\" 하고 물으면, 에드몽은 이렇게 대답했다. \"누가 알겠나? 자네도 언젠가는 배의 선장이 될지. 자네 동향 사람인 보나파르트도 황제가 되지 않았나.\" 자코포가 코르시카 사람이었다는 사실을 미처 말하지 못했다.\n\n이런 항해로 두 달 반이 흘렀고, 에드몽은 강인한 선원이었던 만큼이나 능숙한 연안 항해사가 되었다. 그는 해안의 밀수꾼들과 죄다 안면을 텄고, 그 반쯤은 해적이나 다름없는 자들이 서로를 알아보는 암호 신호를 모조리 익혔다. 그는 자신의 몬테크리스토섬 곁을 스무 번이나 오갔지만, 그곳에 상륙할 기회는 단 한 번도 잡지 못했다.\n\n그래서 그는 한 가지 결심을 굳혔다. 라죈아멜리호 선장과의 계약이 끝나는 대로, 그는 자기 돈으로 작은 배를 한 척 빌릴 작정이었다. 여러 차례 항해하는 동안 백 피아스터를 모아 둔 터였다. 그러고는 무슨 핑계를 대고 몬테크리스토섬에 내리는 것이다. 그러면 그는 자유롭게 탐색에 나설 수 있을 것이었다. 아주 완전히 자유롭지는 않을지도 모른다. 함께 온 자들이 분명 그를 지켜볼 테니까. 그러나 이 세상에서는 무언가를 걸어야 하는 법이다. 감옥은 에드몽을 신중하게 만들었고, 그는 그 어떤 위험도 감수하고 싶지 않았다. 그러나 아무리 상상력을 짜내 봐도, 그 풍부한 상상력으로도 그는 동행 없이 그 섬에 닿을 방도를 도무지 떠올릴 수 없었다.\n\n당테스가 이런 의심과 소망 사이에서 흔들리고 있을 때였다. 그를 깊이 신뢰하며 곁에 붙들어 두고 싶어 하던 선장이 어느 날 저녁 그의 팔을 붙잡고 비아델롤리오 거리의 한 선술집으로 데려갔다. 리보르노의 내로라하는 밀수꾼들이 모여 자기네 일에 관해 의논하던 곳이었다. 당테스는 이미 이 바다의 거래소에 두세 번 들른 적이 있었다. 거의 이백 리외에 이르는 해안 전역에 물건을 대는 그 강인한 자유 무역상들을 보면서, 그는 자문하곤 했다. 이 제각각 엇갈리는 자들에게 한 사람의 의지로 추진력을 불어넣는 자라면 과연 어떤 권세에 오를 수 있을까 하고. 이번에는 중대한 안건이 논의되고 있었다. 터키 양탄자와 레반트산 직물, 캐시미어를 실은 배에 관한 일이었다. 거래를 치를 중립 지대를 어딘가 찾아낸 다음, 그 물건들을 프랑스 해안에 부려 보려는 것이었다. 만약 이 모험이 성공한다면 이익은 어마어마할 것이었고, 선원 한 사람당 쉰에서 예순 피아스터의 벌이가 될 터였다.\n\n라죈아멜리호의 선장은 상륙지로 몬테크리스토섬을 제안했다. 완전히 인적 없는 섬으로, 병사도 세관원도 없으니, 이교도의 올림포스 시절부터 상인과 도둑의 신 메르쿠리우스가 망망대해 한가운데에 떡하니 놓아둔 것만 같은 곳이었다. 상인과 도둑이라는 두 부류는 오늘날 우리가 구별까지는 아니어도 갈라놓기는 했으나, 고대에는 같은 범주에 넣었던 모양이다.\n\n몬테크리스토라는 이름이 나오자 당테스는 기쁨에 흠칫했다. 그는 감정을 감추려 일어서서, 알려진 세상의 온갖 언어가 공통어로 뒤섞이는 그 연기 자욱한 선술집을 한 바퀴 돌았다.\n\n그가 그 일을 의논하던 두 사람에게 다시 합류했을 때는, 몬테크리스토섬에 들렀다가 이튿날 밤에 출발하기로 결정이 나 있었다. 의견을 묻자 에드몽은, 그 섬이 더할 나위 없이 안전하며, 큰일을 제대로 해내려면 빠르게 해치워야 한다고 말했다.\n\n그리하여 계획은 조금도 바뀌지 않았고, 이튿날 밤에 닻을 올려, 바람과 날씨가 허락한다면 그다음 날에는 그 중립의 섬에 닿으라는 명령이 내려졌다.\n\n# Chapter 23. The Island of Monte Cristo\n\n이리하여 마침내, 오랜 세월 악운의 희생자로 살아온 사람들에게 가끔 찾아드는 뜻밖의 행운 가운데 하나로, 당테스는 그토록 바라던 기회를 손에 넣게 되었다. 단순하고 자연스러운 방법으로, 누구의 의심도 사지 않고 섬에 발을 디딜 수 있게 된 것이다. 하룻밤만 더 지나면 그는 떠날 수 있었다.\n\n그 밤은 열병처럼 어수선한 밤이었고, 시간이 흐르는 동안 좋고 나쁜 온갖 환영이 당테스의 머릿속을 스쳐 갔다. 눈을 감으면 스파다 추기경의 편지가 불꽃 같은 글자로 벽에 적혀 있는 것이 보였다. 잠깐이라도 잠이 들면 더없이 기괴한 꿈이 그의 머리를 휘저었다. 그는 에메랄드로 포장되고 벽판은 루비로 이루어졌으며 천장은 다이아몬드 종유석으로 빛나는 동굴 속으로 올라갔다. 지하수가 동굴에서 스며 나오듯, 진주가 한 방울 한 방울 떨어졌다. 에드몽은 놀라움에 넋을 잃은 채 주머니마다 그 찬란한 보석을 가득 채우고 다시 햇빛 아래로 돌아왔는데, 그제야 자신이 거머쥔 것들이 모두 평범한 조약돌로 변해 버린 것을 알았다. 그는 그 경이로운 동굴로 다시 들어가려 했지만, 동굴은 어느새 멀찍이 물러나 있었고, 이제 길은 미궁이 되었으며, 그러다 입구마저 사라져 버렸다. 그는 아라비아의 어부 앞에서 알리바바의 찬란한 동굴을 열어 준 그 마법의 신비로운 주문을 떠올리려고 헛되이 기억을 쥐어짰다. 모두 소용없었다. 보물은 사라졌고, 잠시나마 그가 가로채려 했던 그 정령들에게 다시 돌아가 버렸다.\n\n마침내 날이 밝았고, 낮 역시 밤만큼이나 열에 들떠 있었지만, 그래도 그 낮은 상상력에 이성을 보태 주었다. 덕분에 당테스는 그때까지 머릿속에서 막연하고 어수선했던 계획을 정리할 수 있었다. 밤이 왔고, 그와 함께 출항 준비가 시작되었으며, 그 준비는 당테스의 동요를 감추는 데 도움이 되었다. 그는 어느새 동료들에게 상당한 권위를 행사하게 되어, 배 위에서 거의 지휘관이나 다름없었다. 그의 명령은 언제나 분명하고 또렷하며 이행하기 쉬웠으므로, 동료들은 기꺼이 신속하게 그에게 복종했다.\n\n늙은 선장은 끼어들지 않았다. 그 역시 당테스가 선원들과 자신보다 한 수 위라는 것을 인정하고 있었던 것이다. 그는 이 젊은이에게서 자신의 당연한 후계자를 보았고, 자신에게 딸이 없어 더 든든한 인연으로 에드몽을 붙잡아 두지 못하는 것을 아쉬워했다. 저녁 일곱 시에 모든 준비가 끝났고, 일곱 시 십 분에 등대의 불빛이 막 켜지는 순간 그들은 등대를 돌아 나갔다. 바다는 잔잔했고, 남동쪽에서 불어오는 산뜻한 바람을 받으며, 그들은 맑고 푸른 하늘 아래로 나아갔다. 그 하늘에도 하느님이 차례차례 등불을 밝혀 두셨으니, 그 하나하나가 곧 하나의 세계였다. 당테스는 모두 들어가 쉬어도 좋다고, 자신이 키를 잡겠다고 말했다. 몰타 사람이(사람들은 당테스를 그렇게 불렀다) 그렇게 말하면, 그것으로 충분했다. 모두 흡족한 마음으로 침상으로 돌아갔다.\n\n이런 일은 자주 있었다. 고독에서 세상으로 내던져진 당테스는, 종종 고독을 향한 거역할 수 없는 갈망을 느꼈다. 밤의 어둠 속, 광막함의 침묵 속, 하늘의 눈길 아래, 바다 위에 외따로 떠 있는 배의 고독보다 더 완전하고 더 시적인 고독이 어디 있겠는가?\n\n이제 이 고독은 그의 상념으로 가득 찼고, 밤은 그의 환상으로 환해졌으며, 침묵은 그의 기대로 생기를 띠었다. 선장이 잠에서 깼을 때, 배는 돛을 모두 펼친 채, 그 돛마다 바람을 가득 머금고 질주하고 있었다. 시속 거의 십 노트로 나아가고 있었다. 몬테크리스토섬이 수평선 위로 크게 떠올랐다. 에드몽은 범선을 선장에게 맡기고 자신의 해먹에 가서 누웠지만, 한숨도 못 잔 밤을 보냈으면서도 잠시도 눈을 붙일 수 없었다.\n\n두 시간 뒤 그는 갑판으로 올라왔다. 배가 막 엘바섬을 돌아 나가려는 참이었다. 그들은 바로 마르치아나 앞에, 평평하지만 푸르른 라피아노사섬 너머에 있었다. 이글거리는 태양에 붉게 물든 몬테크리스토의 봉우리가 짙푸른 하늘을 배경으로 솟아 있었다. 당테스는 키잡이에게 라피아노사를 우현으로 두도록 키를 내리라고 명했다. 그렇게 하면 항로를 이삼 노트 줄일 수 있다는 것을 알았던 것이다. 저녁 다섯 시쯤 되자 섬이 또렷이 보였고, 해질녘 햇살이 빚어내는 특유의 청명한 공기 덕에 섬 위의 모든 것이 선명하게 드러났다.\n\n에드몽은 가장 밝은 분홍빛부터 가장 짙은 푸른빛까지, 황혼의 온갖 빛깔을 뿜어내는 그 바위 더미를 몹시 진지하게 바라보았다. 때때로 그의 두 뺨이 달아올랐고, 이마가 어두워졌으며, 두 눈에 안개가 서렸다. 주사위 한 번에 전 재산을 거는 노름꾼도, 희망의 발작 속에서 에드몽이 느낀 그 고통만큼은 결코 겪어 보지 못했으리라.\n\n밤이 왔고, 열 시에 그들은 닻을 내렸다. 라죈아멜리호가 약속 장소에 가장 먼저 도착했다. 평소 누구보다 자제력이 강한 당테스였지만, 이번에는 자신의 격정을 억누를 수 없었다. 그는 가장 먼저 뭍으로 뛰어내렸다. 감히 그럴 수 있었다면, 그는 루키우스 브루투스처럼 \"어머니 대지에 입을 맞추었을\" 것이다. 어두웠지만, 열한 시가 되자 바다 한가운데서 달이 떠올라 물결마다 은빛으로 물들였고, 그러다 \"높이 솟아오르며\" 이 또 하나의 펠리온산의 바위 언덕 위로 창백한 빛을 쏟아부었다.\n\n이 섬은 라죈아멜리호의 선원들에게 낯익은 곳이었다. 그들이 늘 드나드는 곳 가운데 하나였던 것이다. 당테스로 말하자면, 레반트를 오가는 항해 때 이 섬을 지나친 적은 있어도 발을 디딘 적은 한 번도 없었다. 그는 야코포에게 물었다.\n\n\"오늘 밤은 어디서 지내나?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"그야 배 위에서 지내지요.\" 선원이 대답했다.\n\n\"동굴에서 자는 게 낫지 않겠나?\"\n\n\"무슨 동굴이요?\"\n\n\"그야 동굴 말일세, 이 섬의 동굴들.\"\n\n\"동굴 같은 건 모르겠는데요.\" 야코포가 대답했다.\n\n당테스의 이마에 식은땀이 솟았다.\n\n\"뭐라고, 몬테크리스토에 동굴이 없단 말인가?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"없습니다.\"\n\n당테스는 잠시 말을 잃었다. 그러다, 어쩌면 그 동굴들이 무슨 사고로 메워졌거나, 아니면 더 안전하게 하려고 스파다 추기경이 일부러 막아 버렸을지도 모른다는 생각이 떠올랐다. 그렇다면 문제는 숨겨진 입구를 찾아내는 것이었다. 밤에 뒤지는 것은 소용없는 일이라, 당테스는 모든 탐색을 아침으로 미루었다. 게다가 바다 쪽 반 리외 떨어진 곳에서 신호가 올라왔고, 라죈아멜리호가 같은 신호로 응답한 것을 보면, 거래의 순간이 다가왔다는 뜻이었다.\n\n이제 다가오는 배는, 응답 신호로 모든 일이 잘 풀렸음을 확인한 뒤, 유령처럼 희고 고요하게 곧 모습을 드러냈고, 해안에서 밧줄 길이만큼 떨어진 곳에 닻을 내렸다.\n\n그러고는 짐 부리기가 시작되었다. 당테스는 일을 하면서 생각했다. 가슴속에 한결같이 자리 잡은 그 한 가지 생각을 입 밖에 내기만 하면, 단 한마디로 이 모든 사내들에게서 환호성을 끌어낼 수 있으리라고. 그러나 그 귀한 비밀을 털어놓기는커녕, 그는 자신이 이미 너무 많은 말을 흘린 것은 아닌지, 안절부절못하는 모습과 끊임없는 질문, 자질구레한 관찰과 뻔히 드러나는 골몰함으로 의심을 산 것은 아닌지 거의 두려워했다. 다행히, 적어도 이 점에서는, 그의 고통스러운 과거가 그의 얼굴에 지울 수 없는 슬픔을 새겨 두었고, 그 구름 아래로 언뜻언뜻 비치는 즐거움의 빛은 정말이지 한순간의 것에 지나지 않았다.\n\n아무도 조금도 의심하지 않았다. 이튿날 당테스가 엽총과 화약과 탄환을 챙기며 바위에서 바위로 뛰어다니는 야생 염소 몇 마리를 잡으러 가겠다고 했을 때, 사람들은 그저 사냥을 좋아하거나 혼자 있고 싶어 하는 것쯤으로 여겼다. 그래도 야코포가 따라가겠다고 우겼고, 당테스는 거절하면 불신을 살까 두려워 막지 않았다. 그러나 사반 리외도 채 못 갔을 무렵, 그는 새끼 염소 한 마리를 잡고서 야코포에게 그것을 동료들에게 가져다주고 요리를 부탁해 달라고, 그리고 다 되면 총을 한 발 쏘아 알려 달라고 청했다. 이 염소 고기에 말린 과일 몇 알과 몬테풀치아노 한 병이 그날의 차림표였다.\n\n당테스는 이따금 등 뒤와 사방을 살피며 계속 나아갔다. 어느 바위 꼭대기에 이르자, 천 피트 아래로 동료들이 보였다. 야코포가 합류한 그들은 모두 식사를 준비하느라 분주했는데, 명사수 에드몽의 솜씨 덕에 그 식탁에 근사한 한 접시가 더해진 참이었다.\n\n에드몽은 동료들보다 한 수 위인 사람 특유의 슬프고도 부드러운 미소를 띤 채 잠시 그들을 바라보았다.\n\n\"두 시간만 지나면,\" 그가 말했다. \"저 사람들은 각자 오십 피아스터씩 더 부자가 되어 떠나, 다시 오십을 더 벌어 보겠다고 목숨을 걸겠지. 그러고는 육백 프랑쯤 되는 재산을 안고 돌아와, 그 보물을 어느 도시에서 술탄 같은 거만함과 졸부 같은 오만함으로 탕진해 버릴 테고. 지금 이 순간, 희망이 나로 하여금 저들의 재물을 하찮게 여기게 하니, 그것이 내게는 보잘것없어 보이는구나. 하지만 어쩌면 내일이면 환멸이 나를 휘저어, 마지못해 그토록 하찮은 재물을 더없는 행복으로 여기게 될지도 모르지. 아, 아니야!\" 에드몽이 외쳤다. \"그럴 리는 없어. 지혜롭고 한 치의 오차도 없던 파리아 신부님이 이 한 가지만은 틀렸을 리 없어. 게다가 이 비천하고 가련한 삶을 계속하느니 차라리 죽는 편이 나아.\"\n\n이렇게 석 달 전만 해도 자유 말고는 아무 바람도 없던 당테스는, 이제 자유만으로는 부족해 부를 갈망하고 있었다. 그 원인은 당테스에게 있는 것이 아니라, 인간의 힘은 제한하면서도 그 안에 끝없는 욕망을 가득 채워 넣으신 섭리에 있었다.\n\n그러는 동안, 두 바위 벽 사이의 갈라진 틈을 따라, 급류가 깎아 놓은 길을 따라, 사람의 발이라면 십중팔구 한 번도 밟은 적 없었을 그 길을 따라, 당테스는 동굴이 있으리라 짐작한 지점으로 다가갔다. 해안을 따라가며 아무리 작은 것이라도 진지하게 살피던 그는, 어떤 바위에서 사람 손으로 새긴 자국을 알아볼 수 있을 것 같았다.\n\n모든 물질에 이끼 외투를 씌우고 모든 정신의 일에 망각을 입히는 세월도, 이 표식만큼은 손대지 않은 듯했다. 그것은 어느 정도 규칙적으로, 아마도 분명한 목적을 가지고 새겨진 것이 분명했다. 때때로 그 자국은 꽃을 가득 단 커다란 덤불로 퍼져 나간 도금양 무더기 아래, 혹은 기생 지의류 아래 숨어 있었다. 그래서 에드몽은 길잡이 표식이 어디 있는지 알아보려고 가지를 헤치거나 이끼를 쓸어내야 했다. 그 표식을 보자 에드몽의 더없이 간절한 희망이 되살아났다. 어쩌면 추기경 자신이 가장 먼저 이 자국을 새긴 것은 아닐까. 자신이 미처 그토록 완전하리라고는 내다보지 못했을 재앙이 닥쳤을 때, 조카에게 길잡이가 되도록 말이다. 이 외딴곳은 보물을 묻고자 하는 사람의 요건에 그야말로 안성맞춤이었다. 다만, 정작 그 표식이 본디 새겨진 이가 아닌 다른 눈을 끌어들이지는 않았을까? 그리고 이 어둡고 경이로운 섬은 정말로 그 귀한 비밀을 충실히 지켜 냈을까?\n\n그러나 땅의 굴곡에 가려 동료들의 눈에서 벗어나 있던 에드몽이 보기에, 항구에서 예순 걸음쯤 떨어진 지점에서 표식이 끊겼다. 게다가 그것은 어떤 동굴에서 끝나는 것도 아니었다. 큼직하고 둥근 바위 하나가 밑동을 단단히 박고 자리 잡고 있었는데, 표식이 향하는 곳이라곤 오직 그곳뿐인 듯했다. 에드몽은, 어쩌면 자신이 길의 끝에 다다른 것이 아니라 그 시작만 더듬은 것일지도 모른다고 결론짓고, 몸을 돌려 왔던 길을 되짚어 갔다.\n\n그동안 동료들은 식사를 준비해 두었다. 샘에서 물을 길어 오고, 과일과 빵을 펼쳐 놓고, 염소 고기를 구웠다. 마침 꼬챙이에서 그 먹음직한 짐승을 막 빼내려는 순간, 그들은 에드몽이 영양처럼 대담하게 바위에서 바위로 뛰어다니는 것을 보고는, 약속한 신호로 총을 한 발 쏘았다. 사냥꾼은 즉시 방향을 바꾸어 그들을 향해 재빨리 달려왔다. 그러나 그의 대담한 발걸음을 지켜보던 바로 그때, 에드몽의 발이 미끄러졌고, 그들은 그가 바위 끝에서 비틀거리다 사라지는 것을 보았다. 모두 그에게로 달려갔다. 한 수 위인 그를 다들 사랑했던 것이다. 그래도 야코포가 가장 먼저 그에게 다다랐다.\n\n야코포는 에드몽이 엎어진 채 피를 흘리며 거의 정신을 잃은 것을 발견했다. 그는 열두어 피트 비탈을 굴러떨어진 것이었다. 그들이 그의 목구멍에 럼주를 조금 흘려 넣자, 전에도 그에게 그토록 효험을 보였던 이 처방이 이번에도 똑같은 효과를 냈다. 에드몽이 눈을 뜨더니, 무릎이 몹시 아프고 머리가 무겁고 허리가 심하게 결린다고 호소했다. 그들은 그를 해안으로 옮기려 했지만, 야코포의 지시에 따라 손을 댔는데도, 그는 무겁게 신음하며 도저히 움직일 수가 없다고 했다.\n\n당테스가 지금 저녁밥 생각을 했을 리는 없을 것이다. 그러나 그는 자신처럼 굶을 까닭이 없는 동료들에게는 식사를 하라고 우겼다. 자신으로 말하자면, 잠깐 쉬기만 하면 된다고, 그들이 돌아올 즈음이면 한결 나아져 있을 거라고 했다. 선원들은 굳이 권할 것도 없었다. 다들 배가 고팠고, 구운 염소 고기 냄새가 무척 구수했으며, 뱃사람들이란 격식 같은 건 그다지 차리지 않는 법이다. 한 시간 뒤 그들이 돌아왔다. 에드몽이 할 수 있었던 일이라곤 이끼 낀 바위에 기대려고 열 걸음쯤 몸을 끌고 앞으로 나아간 것뿐이었다.\n\n그러나 당테스의 통증은 가라앉기는커녕 점점 더 심해지는 듯했다. 아침에 니스와 프레쥐스 사이, 피에몬테와 프랑스 국경에 화물을 내리려면 출항해야 했던 늙은 선장은, 당테스에게 일어나 보라고 다그쳤다. 에드몽은 따르려고 무진 애를 썼다. 그러나 애쓸 때마다 신음하며 새파랗게 질려 다시 주저앉았다.\n\n\"갈비뼈가 부러졌군.\" 선장이 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"상관없어. 좋은 친구니, 두고 갈 수는 없지. 어떻게든 배로 옮겨 보세.\"\n\n그러나 당테스는, 조금만 움직여도 겪는 그 고통을 견디느니 차라리 이 자리에서 죽는 편이 낫다고 했다.\n\n\"좋네.\" 선장이 말했다. \"무슨 일이 있어도, 자네 같은 좋은 동료를 버리고 갔다는 소리는 결코 듣지 않을 걸세. 저녁까지는 떠나지 않겠네.\"\n\n이 말에 선원들은 무척 놀랐지만, 누구 하나 반대하지는 않았다. 선장이 워낙 엄격한 사람이라, 그가 일을 포기하거나 그 실행을 미루는 것을 보는 건 이번이 처음이었던 것이다. 당테스는 자기 때문에 정당하고 정해진 규칙을 그렇게 어기는 일은 결코 허락할 수 없다고 했다.\n\n\"아니요, 아닙니다.\" 그가 선장에게 말했다. \"제가 서툴렀으니, 그 서투름의 대가를 치르는 것이 마땅하지요. 비스킷이나 조금 남겨 주십시오. 그리고 염소를 잡거나 만일에 대비해 제 몸을 지킬 총과 화약, 탄알, 그리고 저를 데리러 오시는 게 늦어지면 거처라도 지을 수 있게 곡괭이 한 자루만 주십시오.\"\n\n\"하지만 굶어 죽고 말 텐데.\" 선장이 말했다.\n\n\"그래도 그편이 낫습니다.\" 에드몽이 대답했다. \"조금만 움직여도 겪는 그 말로 다 할 수 없는 고통을 견디느니 말입니다.\"\n\n선장은 작은 항구의 너울에 흔들리는 자기 배 쪽으로 고개를 돌렸다. 돛을 반쯤 펼친 그 배는, 채비를 마치기만 하면 곧 바다로 나설 참이었다.\n\n\"어떻게 해야겠나, 몰타 친구?\" 선장이 물었다. \"이렇게 자네를 여기 두고 갈 수도 없고, 그렇다고 머무를 수도 없으니.\"\n\n\"가십시오, 가세요!\" 당테스가 외쳤다.\n\n\"적어도 일주일은 걸릴 거야.\" 선장이 말했다. \"그러고는 또 항로를 벗어나 여기까지 와서 자네를 데려가야 하고.\"\n\n\"이러면 어떻겠습니까.\" 당테스가 말했다. \"이삼 일 안에 고기잡이배라도 만나거든, 저에게 와 달라고 일러 주십시오. 리보르노까지 데려다주면 뱃삯으로 이십오 피아스터를 내겠습니다. 그런 배를 못 만나거든, 그때 저를 데리러 오시면 됩니다.\" 선장은 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"발디 선장님, 들어 보세요. 이걸 해결할 방법이 하나 있습니다.\" 야코포가 말했다. \"선장님은 떠나시고, 제가 남아 다친 사람을 돌보겠습니다.\"\n\n\"이번 일의 자기 몫까지 포기하고,\" 에드몽이 말했다. \"나와 함께 남겠다고?\"\n\n\"네.\" 야코포가 말했다. \"조금도 망설이지 않고요.\"\n\n\"자넨 참 좋은 사람이고 마음씨 고운 동료야.\" 에드몽이 대답했다. \"자네의 그 너그러운 마음을 하늘이 갚아 주실 걸세. 하지만 나는 누구도 함께 남는 걸 바라지 않아. 하루 이틀 쉬면 나아질 테고, 이 바위들 사이에서 타박상에 더없이 잘 듣는 약초도 좀 찾을 수 있겠지.\"\n\n당테스의 입가에 묘한 미소가 스쳤다. 그는 야코포의 손을 따뜻하게 꼭 쥐었지만, 남겠다는, 그것도 혼자 남겠다는 결심만은 무엇으로도 흔들 수 없었다.\n\n밀수꾼들은 에드몽이 청한 것들을 그의 곁에 두고 돛을 올렸다. 그러면서도 여러 번 뒤를 돌아보았고, 그때마다 다정한 작별의 손짓을 보냈다. 에드몽은 마치 나머지 몸은 꼼짝할 수 없다는 듯, 오직 손만 들어 그 인사에 답했다.\n\n이윽고 그들이 시야에서 사라지자, 그는 미소 지으며 말했다. \"참으로 이상한 일이군. 우정과 헌신의 증거를 바로 저런 사람들한테서 찾게 되다니.\" 그러고는 조심스레 몸을 끌고 바위 꼭대기로 올라갔다. 거기서는 바다가 훤히 내다보였는데, 그곳에서 그는 그 배가 출항 준비를 마치고 닻을 올린 뒤, 날아오르기 직전의 물새처럼 우아하게 몸을 가누고 돛을 펼치는 것을 보았다.\n\n한 시간이 지나자 배는 완전히 시야에서 사라졌다. 적어도 다친 사람이 있던 그 자리에서는 더 이상 배를 볼 수 없었다. 그러자 당테스는 이 거친 바위들의 도금양과 관목 사이의 새끼 염소보다도 더 날쌔고 가뿐하게 몸을 일으켜, 한 손에 총을, 다른 한 손에 곡괭이를 쥐고, 자신이 눈여겨본 표식이 끝나던 그 바위를 향해 서둘러 갔다.\n\n\"자, 이제.\" 그가 파리아 신부에게서 들은 아라비아 어부 이야기를 떠올리며 외쳤다. \"자, 열려라 참깨!\"\n\n# Chapter 24. The Secret Cave\n\n해는 거의 중천에 이르렀고, 타는 듯한 햇살이 바위들 위로 가득 쏟아졌다. 바위들마저 그 열기를 느끼는 듯했다. 덤불 속에 숨은 수천 마리 메뚜기가 단조롭고 둔탁한 소리로 울어 댔다. 미르테와 올리브나무 잎들이 바람결에 흔들리며 바스락거렸다. 에드몽은 한 걸음 옮길 때마다 에메랄드빛으로 반짝이는 도마뱀들을 놀라게 했다. 저 멀리서는 야생 염소들이 바위에서 바위로 뛰어다니는 것이 보였다. 한마디로 그 섬에는 생명이 깃들어 있었다. 그런데도 에드몽은 신의 손에 이끌리는, 홀로 남겨진 자신을 느꼈다.\n\n그는 형언할 수 없는 감각에 사로잡혔다. 그것은 일종의 두려움, 사막 한가운데에서조차 누군가가 지켜보고 감시한다는 불안을 일으키는 그 대낮의 두려움과 비슷한 것이었다. 그 느낌이 어찌나 강했던지, 에드몽은 막 작업을 시작하려던 참에 멈추어 곡괭이를 내려놓고 총을 집어 든 뒤, 가장 높은 바위 꼭대기로 올라가 사방을 둘러보았다.\n\n그러나 그가 바라본 것은 집들까지 분간할 수 있는 코르시카섬도, 사르데냐섬도, 역사적 사연이 깃든 엘바섬도 아니었다. 노련한 선원의 눈에만 보일 듯 말 듯한, 도도한 제노바와 상업의 도시 레그혼의 해안을 드러내는 그 희미한 선도 아니었다. 에드몽이 눈길을 박은 것은 아침에 떠난 쌍돛 범선과 방금 닻을 올린 타르탄선이었다.\n\n앞엣것은 막 보니파시오 해협 속으로 사라지는 참이었고, 뒤엣것은 반대 방향으로 나아가 코르시카섬을 돌아가려는 참이었다.\n\n이 광경에 그는 안심했다. 그런 다음 가까이 있는 것들을 살펴보았다. 자신이 섬에서 가장 높은 지점에, 마치 이 거대한 화강암 받침대 위에 놓인 조각상처럼 서 있다는 것을 알았다. 사람이라곤 그림자도 보이지 않았고, 그저 푸른 바다가 섬의 밑동에 부딪치며 하얀 거품의 띠로 그것을 둘렀다. 그러고는 조심스럽고 느린 걸음으로 내려왔다. 그렇게 능숙하게 꾸며 냈던 사고가 정말로 일어날까 봐 두려웠기 때문이다.\n\n앞서 말했듯이 당테스는 바위를 따라 새겨진 표시들을 더듬어 갔고, 그것들이 어느 옛 요정의 욕탕처럼 숨겨진 작은 후미로 이어진다는 것을 알아챘다. 이 후미는 어귀가 제법 넓고 한가운데가 깊어, 러거형 작은 배 한 척쯤은 들어와 누구의 눈에도 띄지 않게 완벽히 숨길 수 있을 만했다.\n\n그런 뒤 파리아 신부의 손에서 그토록 능란하게 다루어져, 갖가지 추측이 얽힌 미궁을 헤쳐 나가도록 그를 인도해 준 그 실마리를 따라가며, 그는 이렇게 생각했다. 스파다 추기경은 감시당하지 않으려고 이 후미로 들어와 작은 배를 숨기고, 바위에 새긴 홈이 표시한 길을 따라가, 그 끝에 보물을 묻었으리라고. 바로 이 생각이 당테스를 둥근 바위로 다시 데려온 것이었다. 다만 한 가지가 에드몽을 당혹스럽게 하며 그의 추론을 무너뜨렸다. 몇 톤이나 나가는 이 바위가, 어떻게 여러 사람의 힘 없이 이 자리까지 들어 올려질 수 있었단 말인가?\n\n문득 한 가지 생각이 머릿속을 스쳤다. '들어 올린 게 아니라, 끌어 내린 거야.' 그는 이렇게 생각하며, 바위가 예전에 놓여 있던 밑동을 살펴보려고 바위에서 뛰어내렸다.\n\n곧 그는 비탈이 만들어져 있었고, 바위가 그 비탈을 따라 미끄러져 지금의 자리에 멈추었다는 것을 알아챘다. 큰 돌멩이 하나가 쐐기 노릇을 했고, 그 둘레에 부싯돌과 자갈을 끼워 넣어 입구를 가렸다. 이렇게 돌을 쌓은 위에는 흙을 덮었고, 거기에 풀과 잡초가 자랐으며, 돌에는 이끼가 들러붙고 미르테 덤불이 뿌리를 내려, 그 오래된 바위는 마치 땅에 단단히 박힌 것처럼 보였다.\n\n당테스는 조심스럽게 흙을 파헤쳤고, 그 기발한 술책을 알아챘다, 아니 알아챘다고 여겼다. 그는 세월의 손길로 굳어진 이 벽을 곡괭이로 공격했다. 십 분쯤 작업하자 벽이 무너지며, 팔을 넣을 만한 구멍이 뚫렸다.\n\n당테스는 가서 찾을 수 있는 가장 단단한 올리브나무를 잘라 가지를 쳐 낸 뒤, 그것을 구멍에 끼워 지렛대로 썼다. 그러나 바위는 너무 무겁고 너무 단단히 박혀 있어, 설령 헤라클레스라 해도 혼자서는 움직일 수 없을 정도였다. 당테스는 쐐기를 공략해야 한다는 것을 깨달았다. 그런데 어떻게?\n\n그는 주위를 둘러보다가, 친구 자코포가 남겨 준 화약이 가득 든 뿔통을 보았다. 그는 미소 지었다. 그 흉악한 발명품이 이번 목적에 쓸모가 있을 터였다.\n\n당테스는 곡괭이를 써서, 일손을 더는 데 능한 개척자처럼 위쪽 바위와 그것을 받친 바위 사이에 갱도를 파고는 화약을 채웠다. 그런 다음 손수건을 초석에 굴려 도화선을 만들었다. 거기에 불을 붙이고 물러섰다.\n\n곧 폭발이 뒤따랐다. 화약의 무시무시한 힘에 위쪽 바위가 밑동에서 들렸고, 아래쪽 바위는 산산조각이 났다. 앞서 당테스가 뚫어 놓은 틈에서 수천 마리 벌레가 쏟아져 나왔고, 보물을 지키는 수호 악마 같은 커다란 뱀 한 마리가 시커먼 똬리를 풀며 기어가 사라졌다.\n\n당테스는 위쪽 바위로 다가갔다. 이제 받쳐 줄 것이 없어진 그 바위는 바다 쪽으로 기울어 있었다. 겁 없는 보물 사냥꾼은 바위를 빙 돌며, 가장 쉽게 공략할 만한 지점을 골라 그 틈새 하나에 지렛대를 끼우고는, 온 신경을 곤두세워 그 덩어리를 움직이려 했다.\n\n폭발로 이미 흔들린 바위가 밑동에서 휘청거렸다. 당테스는 더욱 힘을 쏟았다. 그는 신들의 아버지를 향해 던지려고 산을 통째로 뽑아 든 옛 티탄족 같았다. 바위는 굴복했고, 굴러떨어지며 이 지점 저 지점을 튕기다가, 마침내 바닷속으로 사라졌다.\n\n바위가 차지하고 있던 자리에는 둥근 공간이 드러났고, 네모난 판석에 박힌 쇠고리가 그 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n당테스는 기쁨과 놀라움의 탄성을 질렀다. 첫 시도가 이보다 더 완벽한 성공으로 마무리된 적은 없었다. 그는 계속하고 싶었지만, 무릎이 떨리고 심장이 어찌나 거칠게 뛰던지, 눈앞이 흐려져 잠시 멈출 수밖에 없었다.\n\n그 느낌은 잠깐뿐이었다. 에드몽은 고리에 지렛대를 끼우고 온 힘을 다했다. 판석이 들리며, 지하 동굴의 어둠 속으로 사라지듯 이어지는 계단이 드러났다.\n\n다른 사람 같으면 기쁨의 함성을 지르며 달려 내려갔을 것이다. 당테스는 새파랗게 질려 머뭇거리며 곰곰이 생각했다.\n\n\"자,\" 그는 혼잣말을 했다. \"사내답게 굴자. 나는 역경에 익숙한 사람이야. 속았다는 걸 알게 됐다고 해서 무너져선 안 돼. 그렇다면 내가 견뎌 온 그 모든 고생이 무슨 소용이란 말인가? 달콤한 희망에 부풀었다가 그 모든 환상이 무너지는 걸 보게 되면, 가슴이 찢어지는 법이지. 파리아가 꿈을 꾼 거야. 스파다 추기경은 여기에 아무 보물도 묻지 않았어. 어쩌면 여기 온 적조차 없을지도 몰라. 설령 왔다 해도, 겁 없는 모험가이자 은밀하고 지칠 줄 모르는 약탈자 체사레 보르자가 그를 뒤따라와 그 흔적을 찾아내, 나처럼 그것을 쫓아 돌을 들어 올리고는, 나보다 먼저 내려가 내게 아무것도 남기지 않았을 수도 있어.\"\n\n그는 발치에 입을 벌린 음울한 구멍에 눈을 박은 채, 움직임 없이 생각에 잠겨 있었다.\n\n\"이제 아무것도 기대하지 않으니, 더는 손톱만큼의 희망도 품지 않으니, 이 모험의 끝은 그저 호기심의 문제일 뿐이야.\" 그리고 그는 다시 움직임 없이 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n\"그래, 그래. 이건 그 왕족 출신 도적의 파란만장한 생애에 한자리를 차지할 만한 모험이지. 이 전설 같은 사건도 길게 이어진 경이의 사슬에서 한 고리에 지나지 않았던 거야. 그래, 보르자가 여기 왔었어. 한 손엔 횃불을, 다른 손엔 칼을 들고서. 그리고 스무 걸음 떨어진 이 바위 밑에는, 어쩌면 보초 둘이 뭍과 바다를 지켜보았겠지. 그동안 그들의 주인은, 내가 지금 막 내려가려는 것처럼, 위풍당당한 발걸음 앞의 어둠을 몰아내며 내려갔을 거야.\"\n\n\"그런데 그렇게 그의 비밀을 알게 된 보초들의 운명은 어찌 되었을까?\" 당테스는 스스로에게 물었다.\n\n\"운명이라,\" 그는 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"알라리크를 묻은 자들이 그 시신과 함께 묻혔듯 되었겠지.\"\n\n'하지만 그가 정말 왔었다면,' 당테스는 생각했다. '보물을 찾아냈겠지. 이탈리아를 잎사귀 하나하나 따 먹을 수 있는 아티초크에 비유했던 그 보르자라면, 시간의 값어치를 너무도 잘 알았을 테니 이 바위를 도로 갖다 놓느라 허비하진 않았을 거야. 내려가 보자.'\n\n그러고는 입가에 미소를 띤 채, 인간 철학의 그 마지막 말을 중얼거리며 내려갔다. \"어쩌면!\"\n\n그러나 당테스가 마주하리라 예상했던 어둠과 짙고 탁한 공기 대신, 흐릿한 푸르스름한 빛이 보였다. 그 빛은 공기와 마찬가지로, 방금 그가 뚫어 놓은 구멍으로만이 아니라 밖에서도 보이던 바위의 틈새와 갈라진 곳들을 통해 들어왔다. 그 틈으로 그는 푸른 하늘과 흔들리는 상록 떡갈나무 가지들, 그리고 바위에서 자라난 덩굴손들을 분간할 수 있었다.\n\n축축하다기보다는 따뜻한 동굴 안에 몇 분쯤 서 있자, 어둠에 익숙해진 당테스의 눈은 다이아몬드처럼 반짝이는 화강암으로 된 동굴의 가장 외진 구석까지도 꿰뚫어 볼 수 있었다.\n\n\"아아,\" 에드몽이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"추기경이 남긴 보물이란 게 이거였군. 그리고 그 선량한 신부는 꿈속에서 이 반짝이는 벽을 보고 헛된 희망을 품었던 거야.\"\n\n그러나 그는 외워 두었던 유언장의 글귀를 떠올렸다. '두 번째 입구의 가장 깊은 구석에.' 추기경의 유언장은 그렇게 적고 있었다. 그는 첫 번째 동굴만 찾아냈을 뿐이었다. 이제 두 번째 것을 찾아야 했다. 당테스는 탐색을 이어 갔다. 그는 이 두 번째 동굴이 섬 안쪽으로 더 깊이 파고들었으리라 생각했다. 그는 돌들을 살피고, 만일을 위해 가려 놓은 입구가 있을 법한 벽 한쪽을 두드려 보았다.\n\n곡괭이가 잠시 둔탁한 소리를 내며 부딪쳤고, 당테스의 이마에서 굵은 땀방울이 솟았다. 마침내 벽 한 부분이 더 텅 비고 깊은 울림을 내는 것 같았다. 그는 다급히 다가가, 죄수만이 지닐 수 있는 예민한 지각으로, 십중팔구 그곳에 입구가 있으리라는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n그러나 그 역시 체사레 보르자처럼 시간의 값어치를 알았다. 그래서 헛수고를 피하려고, 곡괭이로 다른 벽들을 모두 두드려 보고 총 개머리판으로 바닥을 쳐 보았다. 수상쩍은 데가 아무것도 없자, 그는 앞서 위안이 되는 소리를 들었던 그 벽 부분으로 돌아왔다.\n\n그는 다시, 더 큰 힘으로 그곳을 쳤다. 그러자 기이한 일이 벌어졌다. 그가 벽을 치자, 아라베스크 무늬의 바탕에 쓰는 것과 비슷한 회반죽 조각들이 떨어져 나와 부스러기째 바닥으로 떨어지며, 커다란 흰 돌이 드러났다. 바위의 입구를 돌로 막은 뒤, 그 위에 이 회반죽을 발라 화강암처럼 보이게 칠해 놓았던 것이다. 당테스가 곡괭이의 뾰족한 끝으로 치자, 그것이 틈새 사이로 어느 정도 파고들었다.\n\n바로 거기를 파야 했다.\n\n그러나 묘하게도 감정이 엇갈려, 파리아가 속은 것이 아니라는 증거가 점점 더 분명해질수록 그의 마음은 도리어 무너져 갔고, 낙담의 기운이 그를 휘감았다. 이 마지막 증거는 그에게 새로운 힘을 주기는커녕 오히려 힘을 앗아 갔다. 곡괭이가 내려갔다, 아니 떨어졌다. 그는 그것을 바닥에 내려놓고 손으로 이마를 쓸어내린 뒤 계단을 다시 올라갔다. 누가 자신을 지켜보고 있지나 않은지 확인하고 싶어서라고 스스로 핑계를 댔지만, 사실은 정신을 잃을 것만 같았기 때문이었다.\n\n섬은 인적이 없었고, 해는 불타는 눈길로 섬을 뒤덮은 듯했다. 저 멀리, 작은 고깃배 몇 척이 푸른 바다의 품을 점점이 수놓고 있었다.\n\n당테스는 아무것도 먹지 않은 상태였지만, 그런 순간에 허기 따위는 떠오르지 않았다. 그는 럼주 몇 방울을 황급히 들이켜고 다시 동굴로 들어갔다.\n\n그토록 무겁게 느껴지던 곡괭이가 이제는 손안에서 깃털처럼 가벼웠다. 그는 그것을 움켜쥐고 벽을 공격했다. 몇 번 내려치고 나서, 그는 돌들이 회반죽으로 붙여진 것이 아니라 그저 하나하나 포개어 쌓은 뒤 회반죽으로 덮어 놓은 것임을 알아챘다. 그는 곡괭이 끝을 끼워 넣고 자루를 지렛대 삼았다. 그러자 곧, 돌이 마치 경첩에 달린 듯 돌아가 발치에 떨어지는 것을 기쁘게 지켜보았다.\n\n이제 그가 할 일은, 곡괭이의 쇠 이빨로 돌들을 하나씩 자기 쪽으로 끌어내는 것뿐이었다. 구멍은 이미 그가 들어갈 만큼 넓어졌지만, 좀 더 기다리면 여전히 희망에 매달릴 수 있었고, 환멸의 확정을 미룰 수 있었다. 마침내, 거듭 망설인 끝에 당테스는 두 번째 동굴로 들어갔다.\n\n두 번째 동굴은 첫 번째 것보다 낮고 더 음침했다. 새로 뚫은 입구로만 들어올 수 있는 공기에서는, 당테스가 바깥 동굴에서 맡지 못해 놀랐던 그 탁한 냄새가 났다. 그는 맑은 공기가 그 더러운 기운을 밀어낼 때까지 기다렸다가 안으로 나아갔다.\n\n입구 왼쪽에는 어둡고 깊숙한 구석이 있었다. 그러나 당테스의 눈에는 어둠이란 없었다. 그는 이 두 번째 동굴을 둘러보았다. 그곳도 첫 번째와 마찬가지로 텅 비어 있었다.\n\n보물이 존재한다면, 이 구석에 묻혀 있을 터였다. 마침내 그때가 온 것이다. 흙 두 자만 걷어 내면 당테스의 운명이 결판날 것이었다.\n\n그는 그 구석으로 다가가, 온 결심을 그러모아 곡괭이로 바닥을 공략했다. 다섯 번째인가 여섯 번째 내려쳤을 때, 곡괭이가 무언가 쇠붙이에 부딪쳤다. 어떤 장례 종소리도, 어떤 경종 소리도 듣는 이에게 이보다 더한 충격을 준 적은 없었다. 차라리 아무것도 찾지 못했다 해도, 당테스가 이보다 더 송장처럼 새파래질 수는 없었을 것이다.\n\n그는 다시 곡괭이를 흙 속으로 내리쳤고, 같은 저항을 만났으나 소리는 같지 않았다.\n\n'쇠를 두른 나무 상자로군.' 그는 생각했다.\n\n바로 그때, 입구 앞으로 그림자 하나가 빠르게 스쳐 갔다. 당테스는 총을 움켜쥐고 구멍을 빠져나가 계단을 올랐다. 야생 염소 한 마리가 동굴 어귀 앞을 지나가더니, 조금 떨어진 곳에서 풀을 뜯고 있었다. 저녁거리를 확보하기에 더없이 좋은 기회였다. 그러나 당테스는 총소리가 누군가의 주의를 끌까 봐 두려웠다.\n\n그는 잠시 생각하더니, 진이 흐르는 나무에서 가지 하나를 잘라, 밀수꾼들이 아침을 지어 먹던 불에 불을 붙이고는, 그 횃불을 들고 내려갔다.\n\n그는 모든 것을 보고 싶었다. 그는 자신이 파 놓은 구덩이로 다가갔고, 이제 횃불의 도움으로, 곡괭이가 정말로 쇠와 나무에 부딪쳤음을 보았다. 그는 횃불을 바닥에 꽂고 작업을 다시 시작했다.\n\n순식간에 길이 석 자, 너비 두 자의 공간이 드러났고, 당테스는 잘라 낸 강철로 테를 두른 떡갈나무 궤를 볼 수 있었다. 뚜껑 한가운데에는, 아직 빛이 바래지 않은 은판에 스파다 가문의 문장이 새겨져 있었다. 다른 모든 이탈리아 문장처럼 타원형 방패 위에 세로로 놓인 칼 한 자루, 그 위에 추기경의 모자가 얹힌 모양이었다.\n\n당테스는 그것을 단번에 알아보았다. 파리아가 어찌나 자주 그려 주었던지. 이제 더는 의심할 여지가 없었다. 보물은 거기 있었다. 텅 빈 궤를 감추려고 이토록 공을 들였을 리는 없었다. 그는 순식간에 모든 장애물을 치웠고, 자물쇠 두 개 사이에 놓인 자물쇠 하나와 양쪽 끝의 손잡이 둘을 차례로 보았다. 그 모든 것은, 가장 흔한 금속마저 예술이 귀하게 빚어 내던 그 시대의 방식대로 조각되어 있었다.\n\n당테스는 손잡이를 움켜쥐고 궤를 들어 올리려 했다. 불가능했다. 그는 그것을 열어 보려 했다. 자물쇠와 맹꽁이자물쇠가 단단히 채워져 있었다. 이 충직한 수호자들은 맡은 것을 내어 줄 마음이 없는 듯했다. 당테스는 궤와 뚜껑 사이에 곡괭이의 뾰족한 끝을 끼우고, 자루에 온 힘을 실어 눌러 잠금쇠들을 터뜨려 열었다. 경첩들도 차례로 굴복해, 나뭇조각을 여전히 문 채로 떨어져 나갔다. 그렇게 궤가 열렸다.\n\n에드몽은 현기증에 사로잡혔다. 그는 총의 공이를 당겨 곁에 내려놓았다. 그런 다음, 아이들이 제 상상의 찬란한 밤하늘에서 실제 창공보다 더 많은 별을 보려고 그러듯 두 눈을 감았다. 그러고는 다시 눈을 뜨고, 놀라움에 옴짝달싹 못 한 채 서 있었다.\n\n궤는 세 칸으로 나뉘어 있었다. 첫째 칸에는 금화 더미가 번쩍였다. 둘째 칸에는, 그 값어치 말고는 아무 매력도 없는, 다듬지 않은 금괴들이 줄지어 놓여 있었다. 셋째 칸에서 에드몽은 다이아몬드와 진주, 루비를 한 움큼씩 그러쥐었고, 그것들이 서로 부딪쳐 떨어지며 유리에 우박이 떨어지는 듯한 소리를 냈다.\n\n이 보물들을 만지고 더듬고 살피고 난 뒤, 에드몽은 광기에 사로잡힌 사람처럼 동굴을 가로질러 달려 나갔다. 그는 바다를 굽어볼 수 있는 바위 위로 뛰어올랐다. 그는 혼자였다. 이 헤아릴 수 없는, 전대미문의 보물과 단둘이 있었다! 그는 깨어 있는 것인가, 아니면 한낱 꿈인가? 스쳐 가는 환영인가, 아니면 그가 현실과 얼굴을 맞대고 있는 것인가?\n\n그는 제 황금을 바라보고 싶었지만, 그럴 기운조차 없었다. 잠시 그는 정신이 달아나지 못하게 막으려는 듯 두 손에 머리를 묻었다가, 미친 듯이 몬테크리스토섬의 바위들을 헤집고 다니며, 거친 고함과 몸짓으로 야생 염소들을 겁주고 바닷새들을 놀라게 했다. 그러고는 돌아와, 여전히 제 감각이 전하는 증거를 믿지 못한 채 동굴 안으로 뛰어들어, 이 금과 보석의 광맥 앞에 다시 마주 섰다.\n\n이번에는 그가 무릎을 꿇고 두 손을 발작하듯 맞잡은 채, 오직 신만이 알아들을 수 있는 기도를 올렸다. 그는 곧 더 차분해지고 더 행복해졌다. 이제야 비로소 자신의 복락을 실감하기 시작했기 때문이다.\n\n그런 다음 그는 자기 재산을 헤아리는 일에 착수했다. 금괴가 천 개 있었는데, 저마다 무게가 두세 파운드씩 나갔다. 다음으로 그는 이만 오천 닢의 크라운화를 쌓았는데, 우리 돈으로 한 닢에 팔십 프랑쯤 되는 것으로, 알렉산데르 6세와 그 선대들의 초상이 새겨져 있었다. 그리고 그 양이 절반도 비지 않았음을 알았다. 또 그는 진주와 다이아몬드, 그 밖의 보석을 두 손 가득 열 번 그러쥐어 헤아렸는데, 그 가운데 상당수는 가장 이름난 세공사들이 박아 넣은 것이라 본래 값어치 이상으로 귀했다.\n\n당테스는 빛이 차츰 사라지는 것을 보았고, 동굴 안에서 불시에 들킬까 두려워 총을 손에 든 채 그곳을 나섰다. 비스킷 한 조각과 럼주 약간이 그의 저녁이 되었고, 그는 동굴 어귀에 누워 몇 시간의 잠을 청했다.\n\n그것은 기쁨과 공포의 밤이었다. 어마어마한 감정의 소유자인 이 사내가 평생에 두세 번쯤은 이미 겪어 본 그런 밤이었다.\n\n# Chapter 25. The Unknown\n\n에드몽이 두 눈을 부릅뜬 채 그토록 애타게 기다리던 날이 다시 밝았다. 동이 트자마자 당테스는 다시 수색에 나섰다. 그는 전날 저녁에 올랐던 바위 봉우리를 또 한 번 기어올라, 풍경의 작은 특징 하나라도 놓치지 않으려 시야를 곤두세웠다. 그러나 아침 햇살 속에서 본 풍경은 저녁의 스러지는 빛 속에서 살폈을 때와 똑같이 거칠고 메마른 모습이었다.\n\n동굴 안으로 내려간 그는 돌을 들어 올리고 주머니마다 보석을 가득 채운 뒤, 상자를 최대한 단단하고 빈틈없이 다시 맞추어 놓았다. 상자가 있던 자리에는 새 모래를 뿌렸고, 흙을 꼼꼼히 다져 어디나 한결같은 모양이 되게 했다. 그런 다음 동굴을 빠져나와 돌을 제자리에 되돌려 놓고, 그 위에 부서진 바윗덩이며 부스러지는 화강암 조각들을 쌓아 올렸다. 틈새는 흙으로 메우고, 그 흙 속에 야생 도금양과 꽃핀 가시나무처럼 빨리 자라는 식물들을 솜씨 좋게 끼워 넣었다. 그러고는 이 새로 심은 것들에 정성껏 물을 주고, 발자국이라곤 하나도 남기지 않도록 모든 흔적을 세심히 지워, 동굴로 가는 길을 처음 찾았을 때처럼 거칠고 사람의 발길이 닿지 않은 모습으로 만들어 놓았다. 이 일을 마친 그는 일행이 돌아오기를 초조하게 기다렸다. 자기 손에 굴러든 그 헤아릴 수도 없는 재물을 용처럼 지키며 몬테크리스토섬에 눌러앉아 있는 것은 그의 마음의 갈망을 채워 주지 못했다. 그의 마음은 다시 인간 세상으로 돌아가, 부에 늘 따라붙는 지위와 권력과 영향력을 누리기를 갈망했다. 부야말로 인간이 손에 쥘 수 있는 모든 힘 가운데 으뜸가는 가장 큰 힘이었다.\n\n엿새째 되는 날, 밀수꾼들이 돌아왔다. 멀리서도 당테스는 죈아멜리호의 의장과 조종 방식을 알아보았다. 그는 짐짓 힘겨운 척 몸을 끌며 상륙 지점으로 나아갔고, 떠날 때보다는 한결 나아졌으나 지난번 사고로 아직도 몹시 고통스러운 척하며 일행을 맞이했다. 이어 그는 항해가 어떠했는지 물었다. 이 물음에 밀수꾼들은 무사히 화물을 부리는 데까지는 성공했으나, 그러기가 무섭게 툴롱 항구를 막 떠난 경비선 한 척이 그들을 향해 돛을 한껏 펼치고 다가온다는 소식을 받았다고 대답했다. 그래서 적을 따돌리려고 있는 힘껏 속도를 내야 했고, 그때만큼 당테스가 없는 것을 아쉬워한 적이 없었다고 했다. 배를 다루는 그의 뛰어난 솜씨가 있었다면 큰 도움이 되었을 거라는 것이었다. 실제로 추격선이 거의 따라잡았을 무렵 다행히 밤이 내려, 그들은 코르시카곶을 돌아 더 이상의 추격을 따돌릴 수 있었다. 그러나 전체적으로 보면 이번 항해는 관련된 모두를 만족시킬 만큼 충분히 성공적이었다. 선원들, 특히 자코포는 이번 수익을 당테스가 자기들과 똑같이 나눠 갖지 못한 것을 몹시 아쉬워했다. 그 수익은 한 사람당 무려 오십 피아스터에 이르렀다.\n\n에드몽은 더없이 훌륭하게 자신을 다스리며, 만약 섬을 떠날 수 있었다면 얻었을 온갖 이득을 늘어놓는 그 말에도 희미한 미소 한 자락 새어 나오게 하지 않았다. 그러나 죈아멜리호는 그저 그를 데려가려고 몬테크리스토섬에 들른 것이었으므로, 그는 바로 그날 저녁 배에 올라 선장과 함께 레그혼으로 향했다.\n\n레그혼에 도착하자 그는 보석상을 하는 어느 유대인의 집을 찾아가, 가장 작은 다이아몬드 네 개를 개당 오천 프랑씩에 팔았다. 당테스는 자기 같은 가난한 선원의 손에 그런 값진 보석이 들려 있으면 의심을 살까 봐 내심 두려워했다. 그러나 약삭빠른 매입자는 적어도 팔 할의 두둑한 이문을 남기는 거래를 두고, 성가신 질문 따위는 하나도 하지 않았다.\n\n이튿날 당테스는 자코포에게 완전히 새 배 한 척을 선물하고, 거기에 백 피아스터를 얹어 주었다. 알맞은 선원들과 의장에 필요한 그 밖의 물품들을 갖추는 데 쓰라는 것이었다. 다만 곧장 마르세유로 가서, 메이앙 거리에 사는 루이 당테스라는 노인과 카탈루냐 마을에 사는 메르세데스라는 젊은 여인의 소식을 알아본다는 조건이었다.\n\n자코포는 이 굉장한 선물을 받고도 제 감각을 좀처럼 믿지 못했다. 그러자 당테스는 서둘러 사연을 둘러댔다. 자기는 그저 변덕에서, 또 마음대로 쓸 만큼 돈을 주지 않던 가족에게 앙갚음하려고 선원 노릇을 했을 뿐이라고. 그런데 레그혼에 도착하니 어느 삼촌이 남긴 큰 재산이 굴러들어 왔고, 자기가 그 유일한 상속자라는 것이었다. 당테스의 빼어난 학식이 이 이야기에 더없이 그럴듯한 빛을 입혀 주었으므로, 자코포는 그 진위를 의심할 생각조차 하지 않았다.\n\n에드몽이 죈아멜리호에서 일하기로 한 기간이 다 차자, 당테스는 선장에게 작별을 고했다. 선장은 처음엔 온갖 말로 그를 구슬려 선원으로 남게 하려 했으나, 유산 이야기를 듣고는 더 이상 졸라 대지 않았다.\n\n이튿날 아침 자코포는 마르세유를 향해 출항했다. 일을 마치면 몬테크리스토섬에서 합류하라는 당테스의 지시를 받은 채였다.\n\n자코포가 항구를 무사히 빠져나가는 것을 지켜본 뒤, 당테스는 죈아멜리호에 올라 마지막 작별 인사를 나누었다. 선원들에게 후한 사례금을 두루 나누어 주어 모두의 호의와 그에 관한 모든 일에 진심 어린 관심을 사 두었다. 선장에게는 앞으로의 계획이 정해지면 편지를 쓰겠노라 약속했다. 그런 다음 당테스는 제노바로 떠났다.\n\n그가 도착한 바로 그 무렵, 만에서는 작은 요트 한 척이 시험 운항 중이었다. 이 요트는 어느 영국인의 주문으로 건조된 것이었다. 그는 제노바 사람들이 지중해 연안의 다른 어떤 조선공들보다 쾌속선 건조에 뛰어나다는 말을 듣고, 그 솜씨의 본보기 하나를 갖고 싶어 했다. 영국인과 제노바 조선공 사이에 합의된 값은 사만 프랑이었다. 그 작은 배의 아름다움과 성능에 마음을 빼앗긴 당테스는 그 배를 자기에게 넘겨 달라고 주인에게 청하며, 즉시 인도받는다는 조건으로 육만 프랑을 제안했다. 거절하기엔 너무도 유리한 제안이었다. 더구나 그 요트를 받기로 한 사람은 스위스 일주 여행을 떠나, 적어도 서너 주는 지나야 돌아올 터였고, 그동안 조선공은 다른 배 한 척을 완성할 수 있으리라 셈했다. 그리하여 거래가 성사되었다. 당테스는 요트 주인을 어느 유대인의 집으로 데려가, 그와 함께 몇 분간 작은 뒷방으로 물러나 있었다. 두 사람이 돌아오자 유대인은 조선공에게 반짝이는 금화로 육만 프랑을 세어 주었다.\n\n기뻐하던 조선공은 그 작은 배에 알맞은 선원들을 구해 주겠노라 자청했으나, 당테스는 거듭 고마움을 표하며 그 제안을 사양했다. 자기는 늘 혼자서 항해하는 데 익숙하고, 가장 큰 즐거움이 바로 직접 요트를 다루는 데 있다는 것이었다. 다만 조선공이 그를 도와줄 수 있는 한 가지는, 선실 침대 머리맡에 일종의 비밀 벽장을 만들어 주는 것이었다. 그 벽장은 세 칸으로 나뉘어 있되, 자신 말고는 누구의 눈에도 띄지 않도록 만들어야 했다. 조선공은 흔쾌히 그 일을 맡아, 이튿날까지 이 은밀한 공간들을 완성해 놓겠노라 약속했다. 당테스는 그 칸들을 어떤 치수와 도면대로 만들어야 할지 일러 주었다.\n\n두 시간 뒤 당테스는 제노바 항구를 떠났다. 자기 요트를 손수 다루겠다는 그 부유한 스페인 귀족을 구경하려고 호기심에 끌려 모여든 어마어마한 군중이 그 모습을 지켜보았다. 그러나 그들의 놀라움은 이내 감탄으로 바뀌었다. 당테스가 키를 다루는 솜씨가 그토록 완벽했던 것이다. 사실 그 배는 거의 인간의 지성이라도 깃든 듯, 아주 가벼운 손길에도 곧바로 응했다. 당테스는 그 아름다운 배를 잠깐 다루어 보고도, 제노바 사람들이 조선술에서 그토록 드높은 명성을 얻은 것이 까닭 없는 일이 아님을 인정하기에 충분했다.\n\n구경꾼들은 그 작은 배가 보이지 않을 때까지 눈으로 좇았다. 이윽고 그들은 그 배의 행선지를 두고 이런저런 추측을 나누었다. 어떤 이들은 코르시카로 간다고 우겼고, 또 어떤 이들은 엘바섬이라고 했다. 스페인으로 향하는 거라며 얼마든지 내기를 걸겠다는 이들도 있었다. 한편 여러 사람은 아프리카가 그 배의 목적지라고 단언했다. 그러나 몬테크리스토섬을 떠올린 사람은 아무도 없었다.\n\n하지만 당테스가 배를 몰고 간 곳은 바로 그곳이었다. 그는 이튿날 저물녘 몬테크리스토섬에 닿았다. 그의 배는 일류 쾌속선임을 증명해, 제노바에서 그 거리를 서른다섯 시간 만에 주파했다. 당테스는 해안의 전체적인 모습을 눈여겨 새겨 두었던 터라, 늘 쓰던 상륙 지점이 아니라 작은 만에 닻을 내렸다. 섬은 완전히 인적이 끊겨 있었고, 그가 떠난 뒤로 누가 다녀간 흔적이라곤 없었다. 그의 보물은 두고 갔던 그대로였다.\n\n이튿날 이른 아침 그는 재물을 옮기는 일에 착수해, 날이 저물기 전에 그 어마어마한 재산 전부를 비밀 벽장 칸칸이 안전하게 들여놓았다.\n\n한 주가 지나갔다. 당테스는 그동안 요트를 몰아 섬을 빙빙 돌며, 솜씨 좋은 기수가 중요한 일에 쓸 말을 살피듯 배를 연구했다. 그렇게 그 기간이 끝날 무렵에는 배의 장점과 단점을 완벽하게 꿰뚫게 되었다. 장점은 더 키우고, 단점은 손보려는 것이었다.\n\n여드레째 되는 날, 그는 작은 배 한 척이 돛을 한껏 펼치고 몬테크리스토섬으로 다가오는 것을 알아보았다. 가까이 다가오자, 그는 그것이 자코포에게 준 배임을 알아챘다. 그는 곧바로 신호를 보냈다. 신호가 되돌아왔고, 두 시간 뒤 그 새 배는 요트 곁에 닻을 내렸다.\n\n자코포가 알아 온 소식을 두고 에드몽이 애타게 묻는 물음마다, 비통한 답이 그를 기다리고 있었다. 늙은 당테스는 죽었고, 메르세데스는 자취를 감추었다.\n\n당테스는 이 서글픈 소식을 겉으로는 차분하게 들었다. 그러나 가볍게 뭍으로 뛰어내리며, 혼자 있고 싶다는 뜻을 내비쳤다. 두 시간쯤 뒤 그는 돌아왔다. 자코포의 배에서 두 사람이 요트에 올라 항해를 돕기로 했고, 그는 곧장 마르세유로 키를 잡으라 명했다. 아버지의 죽음은 어느 정도 마음의 준비가 되어 있었다. 그러나 메르세데스가 알 수 없이 사라진 일은 도무지 설명할 길이 없었다.\n\n비밀을 털어놓지 않고서는 당테스가 대리인에게 충분히 분명한 지시를 내릴 수 없었다. 게다가 그가 확인하고 싶은 다른 세부 사정들도 있었는데, 그것은 오직 그 자신만이 스스로 만족할 만한 방식으로 캐낼 수 있는 성질의 것이었다. 레그혼에 머무는 동안 거울은 그에게 누가 알아볼 위험은 없다고 일러 주었다. 더구나 이제 그는 마음에 드는 어떤 변장이든 할 수 있는 수단을 갖추고 있었다. 그리하여 어느 화창한 아침, 그의 요트는 작은 고깃배를 뒤에 거느리고 당당하게 마르세유 항구로 들어가, 바로 그 잊을 수 없던 밤 — 이프성으로 떠나던 그날 밤 — 그를 그곳으로 실어 갈 배에 태웠던 바로 그 자리 맞은편에 닻을 내렸다.\n\n그래도 당테스는 헌병 한 명이 다가오는 모습을 몸서리치지 않고는 바라볼 수 없었다. 그 헌병은 요트가 뭍과 교통하도록 허락받기 전에 검역 증명을 요구하러 온 관리들을 따라온 것이었다. 그러나 파리아와 알고 지내는 동안 익힌 그 완벽한 침착함으로, 당테스는 레그혼에서 발급받은 영국 여권을 태연히 내밀었다. 그것이 프랑스 여권으로는 누릴 수 없는 신분을 보장해 준 덕분에, 그는 즉시 상륙하는 데 아무런 걸림돌이 없다는 통보를 받았다.\n\n카느비에르 거리에 내린 당테스의 눈길을 가장 먼저 끈 사람은 파라옹호 선원 가운데 하나였다. 에드몽은 자기 부하 선원이었던 그 사내와의 만남을, 세월이 자기 모습에 일으킨 변화의 정도를 가늠할 확실한 방편으로 반겼다. 그는 곧장 그에게 다가가 이런저런 주제로 온갖 질문을 던지며, 그러는 내내 사내의 얼굴빛을 유심히 살폈다. 그러나 지금 이야기를 나누는 상대를 전에 본 적이 있다는 기색은 단 한마디 말에서도, 단 한 번의 눈빛에서도 비치지 않았다.\n\n당테스는 그 친절에 대한 답례로 사내에게 돈 한 닢을 쥐여 주고는 발걸음을 옮겼다. 그러나 몇 걸음 가기도 전에, 그 사내가 멈추라고 큰 소리로 부르는 것을 들었다.\n\n당테스는 즉시 돌아서서 그를 맞았다.\n\n\"죄송합니다, 나리.\" 정직한 사내가 숨이 턱에 닿도록 다급하게 말했다. \"아무래도 착각하신 것 같습니다. 이 프랑짜리를 주시려던 모양인데, 보세요, 두 배짜리 나폴레옹 금화를 주셨습니다.\"\n\n\"고맙네, 이 사람. 자네 말대로 내가 사소한 실수를 했군. 하지만 자네 정직함에 대한 보답으로, 두 배짜리 나폴레옹 금화를 한 닢 더 주겠네. 내 건강을 위해 한잔하고, 동료들도 불러 함께 마시게나.\"\n\n사내는 어찌나 놀랐던지 에드몽에게 고맙다는 말조차 하지 못한 채, 멀어져 가는 그의 뒷모습을 말없이 멍하니 바라볼 뿐이었다. \"인도에서 온 무슨 큰 부자인가 보군.\" 그가 중얼거렸다.\n\n그러는 동안 당테스는 제 갈 길을 갔다. 한 걸음 내디딜 때마다 새로운 감회가 그의 가슴을 짓눌렀다. 가장 처음의, 가장 지워지지 않는 기억들이 그곳에 있었다. 지나치는 나무 한 그루, 거리 한 자락도 정겹고 소중한 추억으로 가득 차지 않은 것이 없었다. 그렇게 그는 노아유 거리 끝에 이를 때까지 걸어 나갔고, 그곳에서는 메이앙 거리가 한눈에 들어왔다. 자애로운 아버지에 대한 추억으로 가득한 이 자리에서, 그의 심장은 터질 듯이 뛰었고 무릎은 휘청거렸으며 눈앞에는 안개가 어렸다. 나무 한 그루에 매달려 몸을 가누지 않았더라면, 그는 어김없이 땅바닥에 쓰러져 그곳을 끊임없이 오가는 숱한 마차들에 깔리고 말았을 것이다. 그러나 가까스로 정신을 차린 그는 이마의 땀을 닦고는, 아버지가 살던 집 문 앞에 다다를 때까지 다시는 멈추지 않았다.\n\n아버지가 창가에 손수 가꾸기를 즐기던 한련화며 다른 화초들은 집 위층에서 모조리 사라지고 없었다.\n\n나무에 몸을 기댄 채, 그는 한동안 그 초라한 작은 집의 위층들을 생각에 잠겨 바라보았다. 이윽고 그는 문 앞으로 나아가 세놓을 방이 있는지 물었다. 없다는 대답을 들었지만, 그는 오 층 방들을 좀 둘러보게 해 달라고 어찌나 간곡히 청했던지, 거기에 세 든 사람이 있다는 관리인의 거듭된 장담에도 불구하고, 당테스는 끝내 그를 설득해 세입자에게 올라가 어느 신사분이 방을 좀 보아도 될지 허락을 구하게 만들었다.\n\n그 초라한 셋방에 든 사람들은 결혼한 지 채 일주일도 안 된 젊은 부부였다. 그들을 본 당테스는 무겁게 한숨을 내쉬었다. 두 칸짜리 작은 방 어디에도 늙은 당테스가 살던 시절 그대로 남아 있는 것은 없었다. 벽지마저 달라졌고, 에드몽이 살던 때 방을 채우던 낡은 가구들도 죄다 사라지고 없었다. 오직 네 벽만이 그가 떠날 때 그대로 남아 있었다.\n\n지금 사는 사람들의 침대는 이전 방 주인이 늘 두던 자리에 놓여 있었다. 바로 그 자리에서 노인이, 아들을 헛되이 부르며 마지막 숨을 거두었으리라는 생각이 들자, 에드몽은 애써 참으려 했음에도 두 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁 차올랐다.\n\n젊은 부부는 손님이 보이는 감정에 놀라 빤히 바라보며, 평소엔 엄하고 미동도 없을 그의 얼굴 위로 굵은 눈물이 소리 없이 잇따라 흘러내리는 것을 의아하게 여겼다. 그러나 그들은 그의 슬픔이 신성한 것임을 느꼈고, 그 까닭을 캐묻지 않는 친절을 베풀었다. 그러고는 본능적인 섬세함으로, 그가 홀로 슬픔에 잠기도록 자리를 비켜 주었다.\n\n그가 그 고통스러운 추억의 자리에서 물러나오자, 두 사람은 함께 아래층까지 그를 배웅했다. 언제든 마음 내키면 다시 찾아오기 바란다고 거듭 말하며, 자기들의 누추한 집은 언제나 그에게 열려 있을 거라고 다짐해 주었다.\n\n에드몽은 사 층 문 앞을 지나다가, 양복장이 카드루스가 아직 거기 사는지 물어보려 걸음을 멈추었다. 그러나 그 사람은 형편이 어려워져, 지금은 벨가르드에서 보케르로 가는 길목에서 작은 여인숙을 하고 있다는 답이 돌아왔다.\n\n메이앙 거리의 그 집 주인이 누구인지 주소를 알아낸 당테스는, 이번엔 그곳으로 가서 윌모어 경(여권에 적힌 이름과 작위였다)의 이름으로 그 작은 집을 이만 오천 프랑에 사들였다. 적어도 제값보다 만 프랑은 더 얹어 준 셈이었다. 그러나 주인이 오십만 프랑을 불렀더라도 그는 망설임 없이 내주었을 것이다.\n\n바로 그날, 이제 당테스의 소유가 된 그 집 오 층에 사는 사람들은, 증서 양도 따위의 필요한 절차를 처리한 공증인을 통해 정식으로 통보를 받았다. 새 집주인이 그들에게 집 안 어느 방이든 골라 쓰도록 하되 집세는 한 푼도 올리지 않겠다는 것, 다만 지금 살고 있는 두 칸짜리 작은 방을 즉시 비워 준다는 조건이었다.\n\n이 기이한 일은 메이앙 거리 일대에 큰 놀라움과 호기심을 불러일으켰고, 온갖 추측이 떠돌았으나 그 어느 것도 진실 근처에도 미치지 못했다. 그러나 사람들의 놀라움을 절정으로 끌어올리고 모든 억측을 무색하게 만든 것은, 아침에 메이앙 거리를 찾았던 바로 그 낯선 사내가 저녁에는 카탈루냐 마을을 거니는 모습으로 목격되었다는 사실이었다. 게다가 그 뒤로 그가 어느 가난한 어부의 오두막에 들어가, 죽었거나 떠난 지 열대여섯 해도 더 된 사람들의 소식을 묻느라 한 시간 넘게 머물렀다는 것이었다.\n\n그런데 이튿날, 이 모든 사정을 캐물음당했던 그 가족은 완전히 새 고깃배 한 척에 두릿그물 두 벌과 딸린 거룻배까지 곁들인 푸짐한 선물을 받았다.\n\n이 후한 선물을 받고 기뻐 어쩔 줄 모르던 사람들은 너그러운 은인에게 고마움을 쏟아붓고 싶었으나, 그가 오두막을 나서며 어느 선원에게 몇 마디 지시만 내리고는 가볍게 말에 뛰어올라 엑스 문으로 마르세유를 떠나는 것을 보았을 뿐이었다.\n퐁뒤가르 여관\n\n# Chapter 26. The Pont du Gard Inn\n\n프랑스 남부로 도보 여행을 떠나 본 독자라면, 보케르 시와 벨가르드 마을 사이쯤, 그러니까 마을보다는 도시 쪽에 좀 더 가까운 곳에서 길가의 작은 여관 하나를 보았을지도 모른다. 그 정면에는 양철판 하나가 바람에 삐걱거리며 펄럭이고 있었는데, 거기에는 퐁뒤가르●를 우스꽝스럽게 그린 그림이 새겨져 있었다. 이 변변찮은 객점은 역참 도로 왼편에 서서 론강을 등지고 있었다. 또 랑그도크 지방에서 정원이라 부르는 것도 갖추고 있었는데, 손님맞이용 정문 맞은편에 자리한 작은 땅뙈기에 불과했다. 거기서는 거무튀튀한 올리브나무 몇 그루와 발육이 멈춘 무화과나무들이 살겠다고 힘겹게 버티고 있었지만, 시들고 먼지 앉은 잎사귀가 그 싸움이 얼마나 가망 없는 것인지를 넉넉히 증명하고 있었다. 그 병든 관목들 사이에는 마늘과 토마토, 골파가 빈약하게 자라났다. 그리고 한쪽 구석에는 잊힌 보초병처럼 외롭고 쓸쓸하게 키 큰 소나무 한 그루가 우울한 머리를 쳐들고서, 아열대의 태양이 내리쬐는 사나운 열기에 말라 갈라진 낭창한 줄기와 부챗살 모양의 우듬지를 드러내고 있었다.\n\n크든 작든 이 나무들은 모두 미스트랄●●이 불어 가는 쪽으로 휘어 있었다. 미스트랄은 프로방스의 세 가지 재앙 가운데 하나였고, 나머지 둘은 뒤랑스강과 의회였다.\n\n단단한 땅이라기보다 먼지로 뒤덮인 호수에 더 가까운 주변 평야에는 비참한 밀 줄기 몇 가닥이 흩어져 있었다. 이는 분명, 그토록 메마른 지역에서도 곡식을 키우는 일이 과연 가능한지 알아보려는 이 고장 농부들의 별난 욕심이 빚어낸 결과였다. 밀 줄기마다 메뚜기가 한 마리씩 앉아 횃대로 삼고서, 이 이집트 같은 풍경을 지나는 행인들에게 거슬리고 단조로운 울음소리를 들려주었다.\n\n이 작은 선술집은 한 일고여덟 해 동안 한 남자와 그의 아내가 두 하인을 거느리고 꾸려 왔다. 하인은 트리네트라는 객실 하녀와 페코라는 마부였다. 이 단출한 일손으로 모든 일을 감당하기에 충분했다. 보케르와 에그모르트 사이에 운하가 뚫리면서, 짐수레와 역마차 대신 배가 쓰이게 되어 운송 방식에 혁명이 일어났기 때문이다. 그리고 이 번창하는 운하가 가엾은 여관 주인에게 날마다 안기는 고통을 더하기라도 하려는 듯, 운하는 그의 완전한 파멸을 빠르게 이루어 가면서, 제 물줄기의 발원지인 론강과 제가 말려 버린 역참 도로 사이에 놓여 있었다. 우리가 짧지만 충실하게 묘사한 이 여관에서 백 걸음도 떨어지지 않은 곳에 말이다.\n\n여관 주인은 마흔에서 쉰다섯쯤 된 남자로, 키가 크고 다부지며 뼈대가 굵은, 이 남부 위도 지방 토박이의 완벽한 본보기였다. 그는 거무스름하게 반짝이는 움푹한 눈에 매부리코, 그리고 육식 짐승처럼 흰 이를 지녔다. 머리카락은 턱밑에 기른 수염과 마찬가지로 숱이 많고 곱슬곱슬했으며, 나이에 비해 은빛 올이 몇 가닥 섞였을 뿐이었다. 본디 가무잡잡한 그의 얼굴빛은 한층 더 짙은 갈색을 띠게 되었는데, 이 가엾은 사내가 아침부터 저녁까지 제 문턱에 서서 좀처럼 오지 않는 손님을 기다리는 버릇이 든 탓이었다. 그래도 그는 날이면 날마다 그 자리에 서서, 머리에는 스페인 노새몰이꾼들처럼 붉은 손수건을 둘둘 동여맨 것 말고는 아무런 가림막도 없이, 한낮에 타오르는 태양의 직사광선에 몸을 드러내고 있었다. 이 사내가 바로 우리의 옛 지인, 가스파르 카드루스였다.\n\n반면에 그의 아내는, 처녀 적 이름이 마들렌 라델이었는데, 창백하고 야위었으며 병약해 보였다. 아를 근방에서 태어난 그녀는 그곳 여인들 특유의, 소문난 미모를 한때 지녔었다. 그러나 그 아름다움은 에그모르트의 연못과 카마르그의 늪지 주민들 사이에 만연한 만성 열병의 황폐한 영향 아래 차츰 시들어 버렸다. 그녀는 거의 늘 이 층 방에 틀어박혀 의자에 앉아 오들오들 떨거나, 침대에 나른하고 기운 없이 늘어져 있었다. 그동안 남편은 날마다 문간에서 망을 보았는데, 그는 그 일을 한층 기꺼이 해냈다. 그래야 끝없이 이어지는 제 아내의 푸념과 넋두리를 듣지 않아도 되었기 때문이다. 아내는 남편을 볼 때마다 운명을 향해 모진 욕설을 퍼부었고, 그럴 때면 남편은 한결같이 이런 철학적인 말로 차분히 대꾸했다.\n\n\"조용히 해, 라카르콩트. 일이 이리된 건 다 하느님의 뜻이야.\"\n\n마들렌 라델에게 라카르콩트라는 별명이 붙은 것은, 그녀가 살롱과 랑베스크 사이에 있는 그 이름의 마을에서 태어났기 때문이었다. 카드루스가 사는 프랑스의 그 지방에서는 사람마다 저마다 특별하고 남다른 호칭으로 부르는 관습이 있었던 터라, 남편은 마들렌이라는 곱고 듣기 좋은 이름 대신 라카르콩트라는 이름을 붙여 준 것이다. 아마도 그의 거칠고 목쉰 발음으로는 마들렌이라는 이름을 제대로 부르기 어려웠을 것이다.\n\n그렇다고 해서, 섭리의 뜻에 짐짓 순응하는 척하는 이 가엾은 여관 주인이 이중의 비참함 아래 몸부림치지 않았다고 생각해서는 안 된다. 그는 그 얄미운 운하가 손님과 수익을 앗아 가는 것을 보았고, 또 까탈스러운 아내의 푸념과 넋두리를 날마다 견뎌야 했으니 말이다.\n\n남부의 다른 주민들처럼, 그도 술버릇이 검소하고 욕심도 적은 사내였지만, 겉치레를 좋아하고 허영심이 강하며 과시하기를 즐겼다. 형편이 넉넉하던 시절에는, 그와 아내가 구경꾼 축에 끼지 않는 잔치가 한 번도 없었다. 그는 남부 프랑스 주민들이 큰 행사 때 입는 그림 같은 의상을 차려입었는데, 그것은 카탈루냐인과 안달루시아인이 즐겨 입는 양식을 똑같이 닮아 있었다. 한편 라카르콩트는 아를 여인들 사이에 유행하던 매혹적인 옷차림을 뽐냈으니, 그리스와 아라비아에서 두루 빌려 온 복식이었다. 그러나 차츰 시곗줄과 목걸이, 색색의 스카프, 수놓은 보디스, 벨벳 조끼, 정교하게 짠 양말, 줄무늬 각반, 구두에 다는 은 버클이 모조리 사라지고 말았다. 가스파르 카드루스는 예전의 화려한 차림으로 바깥나들이를 할 수 없게 되자, 저와 아내를 위한 온갖 호사와 허영을 더는 누리지 않기로 했다. 그래도 흥겨운 잔치꾼들의 웃음소리와 즐거운 음악 소리가, 그가 수익보다는 비바람을 피할 곳으로 여겨 여태 매달려 있던 이 초라한 객점에까지 들려올 때면, 그의 마음은 시샘 어린 불만으로 쓰라리게 차오르곤 했다.\n\n그러니까 카드루스는 여느 때처럼 문 앞 제 망보는 자리에 서서, 짧게 깎인 풀밭에서 닭 몇 마리가 입맛에 맞는 곡식 낟알이나 벌레를 찾아 부지런히, 그러나 헛되이 헤집는 모습을 무심히 바라보다가, 다시 남북으로 뻗은 그 인적 끊긴 길로 눈길을 옮기고 있었다. 그때 아내의 날카로운 목소리에 정신이 번쩍 든 그는, 투덜거리며 아내의 방으로 올라갔다. 다만 올라가기 전에, 지나갈지도 모르는 어떤 길손이라도 들어오라는 뜻으로, 현관문을 활짝 열어 두는 것은 잊지 않았다.\n\n카드루스가 문 앞에서 보초처럼 지키던 자리를 떠난 순간, 그가 그토록 애타게 눈여겨보던 길은 한낮의 사막처럼 텅 비고 적막했다. 길은 끝없는 먼지와 모래의 한 줄기로 뻗어 나갔고, 양옆으로는 키 크고 앙상한 나무들이 늘어서 있어, 그 모습이 어찌나 삭막한지, 제정신을 가진 사람이라면 누구도, 길 떠날 시간을 제 마음대로 고를 수 있는 어떤 길손이 굳이 이런 무시무시한 사하라 사막에 제 몸을 드러내려 들리라고는 상상조차 할 수 없었다.\n\n그렇지만 카드루스가 몇 분만 더 자리를 지켰더라면, 벨가르드 쪽에서 무언가 다가오는 흐릿한 윤곽을 어렴풋이 보았을 것이다. 그 움직이는 물체가 가까이 다가올수록, 그는 그것이 한 사람과 한 마리 말이며, 둘 사이에 더없이 다정하고 정겨운 교감이 흐르는 듯하다는 것을 어렵잖게 알아보았을 것이다. 말은 헝가리 종으로, 느긋한 걸음으로 천천히 나아가고 있었다. 말 탄 이는 검은 옷을 입고 삼각모를 쓴 사제였다. 한낮 태양의 뜨거운 햇살에도 아랑곳없이, 그 한 쌍은 제법 빠른 속도로 다가오고 있었다.\n\n퐁뒤가르 앞에 이르자 말이 멈춰 섰는데, 제 뜻으로 그런 것인지 주인의 뜻으로 그런 것인지는 가늠하기 어려웠다. 어쨌든 사제는 말에서 내려, 매어 둘 만한 곳을 찾아 고삐를 잡고 말을 끌었다. 반쯤 떨어져 나간 문에 손잡이 하나가 삐죽 튀어나와 있는 것을 발견하고, 그는 그 짐승을 단단히 붙들어 맨 뒤, 주머니에서 붉은 면 손수건을 꺼내 이마에 줄줄 흐르는 땀을 닦았다. 그러고는 문 쪽으로 다가가, 끝에 쇠를 댄 지팡이로 세 번 두드렸다.\n\n이 낯선 소리에, 평소 고요하던 제 거처에 겁도 없이 쳐들어온 침입자를 맞으러 커다란 검은 개 한 마리가 달려 나왔다. 개는 으르렁거리며 날카로운 흰 이를 드러냈는데, 그 단호한 적의는 이 개가 손님 맞는 일에 얼마나 익숙지 않은지를 넉넉히 증명했다. 그때 위층에서 내려오는 나무 계단을 밟는 묵직한 발소리가 들리더니, 퐁뒤가르의 주인이 연신 허리를 굽히고 공손히 웃으며 손님에게 들어오기를 청했다.\n\n\"어서 오십시오, 손님, 정말 잘 오셨습니다!\" 어리둥절해진 카드루스가 거듭 말했다. \"자, 마르고탱,\" 그가 개를 향해 외쳤다. \"좀 조용히 못 하겠니? 부디 신경 쓰지 마십시오, 손님! 짖기만 하지, 물지는 않는 놈입니다. 이렇게 지독하게 더운 날에는 좋은 포도주 한 잔이 반가우시겠지요.\" 그러다가 자기가 모실 길손의 옷차림을 그제야 처음 알아보고는, 카드루스가 황급히 외쳤다. \"천 번 만 번 용서하십시오! 누추한 제 지붕 아래 모시게 된 분이 누구신지 정말 미처 알아뵙지 못했습니다. 신부님, 무엇을 드시겠습니까? 무슨 음식을 올릴까요? 제게 있는 건 무엇이든 다 신부님 것입니다.\"\n\n사제는 자기에게 말을 건네는 사람을 길고 꿰뚫는 듯한 눈길로 바라보았다. 심지어 여관 주인 쪽에서도 자기를 그렇게 살펴봐 주기를 바라는 듯한 기색마저 있었다. 그러나 상대의 얼굴에서, 그토록 정중하게 건넨 물음에 제가 미처 주의를 기울이지 못한 데 대한 극도의 놀라움 말고는 아무런 표정도 읽어 내지 못하자, 그는 이 무언극을 끝내는 편이 낫겠다고 여기고, 강한 이탈리아 억양으로 말했다. \"카드루스 씨가 맞으시지요?\"\n\n\"네, 손님.\" 주인이 대답했는데, 앞선 침묵보다도 이 물음에 한층 더 놀란 기색이었다. \"제가 가스파르 카드루스입니다, 분부만 하십시오.\"\n\n\"가스파르 카드루스.\" 사제가 다시 말했다. \"그렇군요, 세례명과 성이 그대로네요. 전에는 메이앙 가로의 사 층에 사셨지요?\"\n\n\"그랬습니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 재단사 일을 하셨고요?\"\n\n\"맞습니다, 장사가 망하기 전까지는 재단사였지요. 마르세유는 어찌나 더운지, 정말이지 점잖은 주민들이 머잖아 옷이라곤 한 오라기도 안 걸치고 다닐 것만 같습니다. 그건 그렇고, 더운 얘기가 나왔으니 말인데, 뭐 좀 시원하게 드실 것을 올릴까요?\"\n\n\"좋소. 가장 좋은 포도주로 한 병 주시오. 그런 다음, 괜찮다면 아까 끊긴 데서 다시 이야기를 이어 갑시다.\"\n\n\"분부대로 하지요, 손님.\" 카드루스가 말했다. 그는 아직 몇 병 남지 않은 카오르산 포도주 가운데 한 병을 팔 이 좋은 기회를 놓치지 않으려고, 두 사람이 있던 방—응접실 겸 부엌으로 쓰는 곳이었다—의 바닥에 난 들창문을 황급히 들어 올렸다.\n\n오 분 뒤 지하 저장고에서 올라온 그는, 신부가 나무 걸상에 앉아 식탁에 팔꿈치를 괴고 있는 것을 보았다. 길손이 음식을 청한 뜻밖의 일로 적의가 누그러진 듯한 마르고탱은, 어느새 그에게 슬그머니 다가가 두 무릎 사이에 아주 편안히 자리를 잡고 있었다. 길고 비쩍 마른 목을 그의 무릎에 얹은 채, 흐릿한 눈을 길손의 얼굴에 골똘히 박고 있었다.\n\n\"혼자 계신 거요?\" 카드루스가 포도주 병과 잔을 앞에 놓자 손님이 물었다.\n\n\"네, 아주 혼자입니다.\" 사내가 대답했다. \"아니, 적어도 사실상 그런 셈이지요. 이 집에 저 말고 있는 사람이라곤 제 가엾은 아내뿐인데, 병으로 자리에 누워 제게 손톱만큼도 거들지 못하는 형편이니까요, 가엾은 사람!\"\n\n\"그럼 결혼하셨군요?\" 사제가 관심을 보이며 말했다. 말하는 동안 그의 눈길은 방 안의 빈약한 세간을 휘둘러보았다.\n\n\"아아, 손님.\" 카드루스가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"보시다시피 제가 넉넉한 사람이 못 됩니다. 하지만 이 세상에서는 정직하다고 해서 더 잘 풀리는 법이 없지요.\" 사제가 그에게 캐묻는 듯한 날카로운 눈길을 던졌다.\n\n\"네, 정직하다는 것—적어도 제 입으로 그만큼은 떳떳이 말할 수 있습니다.\" 여관 주인이 사제의 응시를 제법 의젓하게 견뎌 내며 말을 이었다. \"저는 정직한 사람이라고 사실대로 자랑할 수 있습니다. 그리고,\" 그가 한 손을 가슴에 얹고 고개를 절레절레 저으며 의미심장하게 덧붙였다. \"그건 요즘 세상에 아무나 할 수 있는 말이 아니지요.\"\n\n\"하신 말씀이 사실이라면, 그건 당신에게 잘된 일이오.\" 사제가 말했다. \"나는 늦든 빠르든 선한 자는 상을 받고 악한 자는 벌을 받는다고 굳게 믿고 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"그런 말씀이야 신부님 직업에 어울리는 것이지요.\" 카드루스가 대답했다. \"그러니 신부님이 거듭 말씀하시는 것도 당연합니다. 하지만,\" 그가 씁쓸한 표정을 지으며 덧붙였다. \"그 말을 믿고 안 믿고는 사람 마음에 달린 거지요.\"\n\n\"그렇게 말하는 건 잘못이오.\" 사제가 말했다. \"그리고 어쩌면 내가 직접, 당신이 얼마나 철저히 틀렸는지 증명해 보일 수 있을지도 모르오.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이신지요?\" 카드루스가 놀란 얼굴로 물었다.\n\n\"우선, 당신이 내가 찾는 그 사람이 맞는지부터 확인해야겠소.\"\n\n\"무슨 증거가 필요하십니까?\"\n\n\"1814년이나 1815년에, 당테스라는 젊은 선원에 대해 무얼 좀 아셨소?\"\n\n\"당테스요? 가엾고 그리운 에드몽을 알았느냐고요? 아니, 에드몽 당테스와 저는 둘도 없는 친구 사이였습니다!\" 카드루스가 외쳤다. 그러나 사제의 꿰뚫는 듯한 눈길이 자기에게 박혀 있는 것을 알아채자 그의 얼굴은 검붉게 달아올랐고, 한편 묻는 이의 맑고 차분한 눈은 열에 들뜬 듯 응시하며 점점 커지는 것 같았다.\n\n\"그러고 보니,\" 신부가 말했다. \"내가 물은 그 젊은이가 에드몽이라는 이름으로 불렸다고 했던 게 떠오르는군요.\"\n\n\"불렸다니요!\" 카드루스가 흥분하여 안달하며 거듭 말했다. \"아니, 제가 가스파르 카드루스라는 이름을 가진 게 분명한 것만큼이나, 그도 분명 그렇게 불렸습니다. 그런데 부디 말씀해 주십시오, 가엾은 에드몽은 어찌 되었습니까? 그를 아셨습니까? 살아서 자유의 몸이 되었나요? 잘되어 행복하게 지냅니까?\"\n\n\"그는 툴롱의 갤리선에서 죗값을 치르는 죄수들보다도 더 비참하고 가망 없이, 가슴이 찢어진 채 죽었소.\"\n\n카드루스의 얼굴에서 붉은빛이 가시고 죽은 사람처럼 핏기가 가셨다. 그는 고개를 돌렸고, 사제는 그가 머리에 둘둘 감은 붉은 손수건 귀퉁이로 눈물을 훔치는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"가엾은 친구, 가엾은 친구!\" 카드루스가 중얼거렸다. \"자, 보십시오, 손님, 이거야말로 착한 사람들은 이 땅에서 결코 보답받지 못하고, 악한 자들만 잘 풀린다는 또 하나의 증거가 아니겠습니까. 아아,\" 카드루스가 남부 특유의 과장된 말투로 말을 이었다. \"세상은 갈수록 험악해집니다. 하느님이 정말로 사람들이 말하듯 악한 자를 미워하신다면, 어째서 유황불을 내려 그것들을 죄다 태워 버리지 않으시는 걸까요?\"\n\n\"이 젊은 당테스를 무척 아꼈던 것처럼 말씀하시는군요.\" 사제가 상대의 격앙된 태도에는 아랑곳없이 말했다.\n\n\"그랬지요.\" 카드루스가 대답했다. \"한때는, 솔직히, 그의 좋은 팔자를 시샘한 적도 있었습니다. 하지만 맹세컨대, 손님, 사람이 소중히 여기는 모든 것을 두고 맹세컨대, 저는 그 뒤로 그의 불행한 운명을 깊고 진심으로 애통해했습니다.\"\n\n잠시 침묵이 흘렀고, 그동안 사제의 못 박힌 듯한 탐색의 눈은 여관 주인의 동요하는 얼굴을 샅샅이 살피고 있었다.\n\n\"그럼 그 가엾은 친구를 아셨던 거군요?\" 카드루스가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그가 죽어 가는 자리에 불려 가, 종교의 위안을 베풀어 주었소.\"\n\n\"무엇 때문에 죽었습니까?\" 카드루스가 목멘 소리로 물었다.\n\n\"젊고 건강한 사람이, 서른도 채 못 넘기고 감옥에서 죽는다면, 그게 옥살이 말고 무엇 때문이겠소?\" 카드루스가 이마에 맺힌 굵은 땀방울을 닦았다.\n\n\"하지만 이 이야기에서 가장 이상한 대목은,\" 사제가 다시 말을 이었다. \"당테스가 죽어 가는 순간에도, 십자가에 못 박히신 구세주를 두고, 자기가 어째서 갇혔는지 그 까닭을 도무지 모른다고 맹세했다는 거요.\"\n\n\"그야 정말 그랬으니까요.\" 카드루스가 중얼거렸다. \"어떻게 알 수가 있었겠습니까? 아아, 손님, 그 가엾은 친구는 신부님께 사실대로 말한 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그래서 그는, 자기가 끝내 풀지 못한 수수께끼를 밝혀 달라고, 그리고 혹시 자기 명예에 더러운 오점이나 얼룩이 묻었다면 그것을 씻어 달라고 내게 간곡히 부탁했소.\"\n\n그러면서 사제의 눈길은 점점 더 못 박힌 듯해지더니, 카드루스의 얼굴에 빠르게 번져 가는 음울한 침울함 위에 좀처럼 감추지 못한 만족감을 띤 채 머무는 듯했다.\n\n\"한 부유한 영국인이,\" 사제가 말을 이었다. \"불행을 함께 겪은 그의 동료였는데, 두 번째 왕정복고 때 감옥에서 풀려났소. 그자는 어마어마한 값어치의 다이아몬드를 지니고 있었지요. 그는 감옥을 떠나면서 그 보석을 당테스에게 주었소. 갇혀 지내는 동안 중병을 앓았을 때, 당테스가 형제처럼 살뜰히 보살펴 준 친절에 대한 고마움의 표시였소. 당테스는 그 다이아몬드를 간수들을 매수하는 데 쓰지 않았소. 그자들은 그것만 챙기고 그를 소장에게 밀고했을지도 모르니까요. 대신 그는 그것을 고이 간직했소. 혹여 감옥에서 나가게 되면 살아갈 밑천으로 삼으려고 말이오. 그런 다이아몬드를 팔면 한밑천 톡톡히 잡기에 충분했을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 카드루스가 눈을 번뜩이며 간절하게 물었다. \"어마어마한 값어치의 보석이었단 말씀이지요?\"\n\n\"그야 다 상대적인 거지요.\" 사제가 대답했다. \"에드몽 같은 처지에 놓인 사람에게야, 그 다이아몬드는 분명 대단한 값어치가 있었소. 오만 프랑은 나가는 것으로 매겨졌으니까요.\"\n\n\"맙소사!\" 카드루스가 외쳤다. \"오만 프랑이라니! 그만한 값이 나가려면 다이아몬드가 호두만큼은 컸겠군요.\"\n\n\"아니오.\" 사제가 대답했다. \"그렇게 큰 건 아니었소. 하지만 직접 보고 판단하시오. 내가 그걸 지니고 있으니까.\"\n\n카드루스의 날카로운 눈길이 그 보물이 어디 있는지 찾아내기라도 하려는 듯, 곧장 사제의 옷자락으로 향했다. 사제는 주머니에서 검은 가죽으로 싼 작은 갑을 차분히 꺼내 열고는, 그 안에 든 반짝이는 보석을 카드루스의 휘둥그레진 눈앞에 드러내 보였다. 보석은 솜씨가 빼어난 반지에 박혀 있었다.\n\n\"그래서 그 다이아몬드가,\" 카드루스가 간절한 감탄에 숨이 막힐 지경으로 외쳤다. \"오만 프랑 값어치가 나간다는 말씀이지요?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 그것도 받침대 값은 빼고 말이오. 받침대도 값나가는 것이지만.\" 사제가 갑을 닫아 다시 주머니에 넣으며 대답했다. 그러나 그 찬란한 빛깔은 넋이 나간 여관 주인의 눈앞에 여전히 어른거리는 듯했다.\n\n\"한데 그 다이아몬드가 어쩌다 신부님 손에 들어왔습니까? 에드몽이 신부님을 상속인으로 삼았나요?\"\n\n\"아니오, 그저 유언 집행인으로 삼았을 뿐이오. '내게는 약혼했던 처녀 말고도, 소중하고 의리 있는 벗이 넷 있었습니다.' 그가 말했소. '그들 모두 내 죽음을 거짓 없이 슬퍼했으리라 믿습니다. 그 네 벗 가운데 하나가 카드루스입니다.'\" 여관 주인이 부르르 떨었다.\n\n\"'그 가운데 또 하나는,'\" 사제가 카드루스의 동요는 알아채지 못한 척하며 말을 이었다. \"'당글라르라고 합니다. 그리고 세 번째는, 내 연적이었음에도, 나에게 아주 진심 어린 애정을 품고 있었지요.'\"\n\n카드루스의 얼굴에 악마 같은 미소가 스쳤다. 그가 사제의 말을 막 가로채려는 참에, 사제가 손을 내저으며 말했다. \"내가 먼저 끝내게 해 주시오. 그런 다음 할 말이 있거든 그때 하면 되오. '내 세 번째 벗은, 비록 연적이었지만, 나를 무척 따랐습니다. 그의 이름은 페르낭이지요. 그리고 내 약혼녀의 이름은—' 잠깐, 잠깐,\" 사제가 말을 이었다. \"그가 그 처녀를 뭐라고 불렀는지 잊어버렸군요.\"\n\n\"메르세데스입니다.\" 카드루스가 다급히 말했다.\n\n\"맞소.\" 사제가 한숨을 억누르며 말했다. \"메르세데스였소.\"\n\n\"계속하십시오.\" 카드루스가 재촉했다.\n\n\"물 한 병만 갖다주시오.\" 사제가 말했다.\n\n카드루스가 얼른 낯선 이의 분부를 따랐다. 사제는 잔에 물을 따라 천천히 들이켜고는, 평소의 차분한 태도를 되찾아, 빈 잔을 식탁에 내려놓으며 말했다.\n\n\"어디까지 했더라?\"\n\n\"에드몽 약혼녀의 이름이 메르세데스라고 하셨지요.\"\n\n\"그렇지. '자네는 마르세유로 가게.' 당테스가 말했소—내가 그의 말을 그가 한 그대로 옮기는 거니 알아들으셔야 하오. 알아듣겠소?\"\n\n\"잘 알겠습니다.\"\n\n\"'자네는 이 다이아몬드를 팔게. 그 돈을 똑같이 다섯 몫으로 나누어, 이 좋은 벗들에게 한 몫씩 똑같이 주게. 이 땅에서 나를 사랑해 준 단 한 사람들이니까.'\"\n\n\"한데 왜 다섯 몫입니까?\" 카드루스가 물었다. \"넷밖에 말씀 안 하셨는데요.\"\n\n\"다섯째는 죽었다고 들었기 때문이오. 에드몽의 유산을 나누어 받을 다섯째는, 바로 그의 아버지였소.\"\n\n\"맞습니다, 정말 그렇지요!\" 카드루스가 밀려드는 갖가지 감정에 숨이 막힐 듯 외쳤다. \"그 가엾은 노인은 정말 돌아가셨지요.\"\n\n\"그건 마르세유에서 들어 알고 있었소.\" 사제가 애써 무심한 척하며 대답했다. \"하지만 노 당테스가 죽은 지 너무 오랜 세월이 흘러서, 그가 어떻게 최후를 맞았는지 자세한 내막은 알아낼 수가 없었소. 그 점에 대해 좀 일러 줄 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"제가 모른다면 알 사람이 또 어디 있겠습니까.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"아니, 저는 그 가엾은 노인과 거의 같은 층에서 살았는걸요. 아아, 그렇지요, 아들이 종적을 감추고 한 해쯤 지나서, 그 가엾은 노인은 돌아가셨습니다.\"\n\n\"무엇 때문에 돌아가셨소?\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 의사들은 그 병을 위장염이라고 불렀던 것 같습니다. 그분을 아는 사람들은 슬픔으로 죽었다고들 하지요. 하지만 그분이 숨을 거두는 순간을 지켜본 저는, 이렇게 말합니다, 그분이 죽은 건—\"\n\n카드루스가 말을 멈췄다.\n\n\"무엇 때문이오?\" 사제가 애타게 다그치며 물었다.\n\n\"굶어서, 그야말로 쫄쫄 굶어서 돌아가셨습니다.\"\n\n\"굶어서라니!\" 사제가 자리에서 벌떡 일어나며 외쳤다. \"아니, 가장 천한 짐승도 그런 식으로 죽게 내버려 두지는 않소. 집도 절도 없이 거리를 떠도는 개조차 빵 한 조각이라도 던져 주는 가엾은 손길을 만나는 법이오. 그런데 사람이, 그것도 그리스도교인이, 스스로를 그리스도교인이라 부르는 다른 사람들 한복판에서 굶어 죽도록 내버려졌다니, 너무 끔찍해서 믿을 수가 없소. 오, 그럴 리가 없소!—절대로 그럴 리가 없소!\"\n\n\"제가 말한 건 말한 그대로입니다.\" 카드루스가 대답했다.\n\n\"그런 일을 입에 올리다니 당신은 멍청이야.\" 계단 위에서 누군가의 목소리가 들렸다. \"당신과 상관도 없는 일에 왜 끼어드는 거예요?\"\n\n두 사람이 홱 돌아보니, 난간 살 사이로 라카르콩트의 병색 짙은 얼굴이 들여다보고 있었다. 말소리에 이끌려, 그녀는 기진한 몸을 계단 아래로 간신히 끌고 내려와, 맨 아래 계단에 머리를 무릎에 묻고 앉아, 앞서 오간 대화를 죄다 듣고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"당신 일이나 신경 써, 마누라.\" 카드루스가 쏘아붙였다. \"이분이 내게 물으시니, 사람의 도리상 대답을 안 할 수가 없는 거지.\"\n\n\"도리는 무슨 도리, 이 멍청한 양반아!\" 라카르콩트가 되받아쳤다. \"당신이 도리하고 무슨 상관이람? 차라리 분별이라는 걸 좀 배우는 게 나을걸. 저 사람이 당신한테서 캐낼 수 있는 건 죄다 캐내려는 속셈이 무엇인지 어떻게 알아요?\"\n\n\"맹세컨대, 부인,\" 사제가 말했다. \"제 의도는 선한 것이며, 바깥양반이 솔직하게만 대답하신다면 어떤 위험도 닥칠 일은 없습니다.\"\n\n\"흥, 그거야 다 듣기 좋은 말이지요.\" 여자가 되받았다. \"걱정할 것 없다고 그럴싸하게 약속하고 다짐하는 거야 누군들 못 하겠어요. 하지만 우리 집 양반 같은 가엾고 어리석은 사람들이 아는 걸 죄다 털어놓고 나면, 그 약속이며 안전하다던 다짐이며 금세 다 잊히는 법이지요. 그러다 아무도 예상치 못한 어느 순간에, 보세요, 온갖 곤경과 비참함과 별별 박해가 그 가엾은 사람들 머리 위로 쏟아지는데, 정작 그들은 그 모든 고난이 어디서 오는지조차 알지도 못한단 말이에요.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니오, 부인, 부디 마음 푹 놓으십시오. 어떤 화가 닥치든, 그것이 내 손으로 말미암은 것은 결코 아닐 것입니다, 엄숙히 약속드리지요.\"\n\n라카르콩트는 알아들을 수 없는 말을 몇 마디 중얼거리더니, 다시 머리를 무릎에 떨구고는 오한에 부르르 떨었다. 그러고는 두 사람이 대화를 다시 잇도록 내버려 두었지만, 그들이 주고받는 말을 한마디도 빠짐없이 들을 수 있는 자리에 그대로 남아 있었다. 사제는 또다시 자기를 압도하려는 감정을 가라앉히려고 물을 한 모금 들이켜야 했다.\n\n마음을 충분히 추스른 뒤, 그가 말했다. \"그렇다면, 당신이 말한 그 가엾은 노인은 모두에게 버림받은 모양이군요. 그렇지 않고서야, 그리 끔찍한 죽음으로 스러지지는 않았을 테니 말이오.\"\n\n\"아니, 아주 버림받은 건 아니었습니다.\" 카드루스가 말을 이었다. \"카탈루냐 처녀 메르세데스와 모렐 씨가 그분께 무척 잘해 드렸으니까요. 다만 어쩐 일인지 그 가엾은 노인은 페르낭을 뼛속 깊이 미워하게 되셨지요. 바로 그 사람,\" 카드루스가 씁쓸한 미소를 지으며 덧붙였다. \"신부님이 방금 당테스의 의리 있고 충실한 벗 가운데 하나로 꼽으신 그 사람 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그가 정말 그런 벗이 아니었단 말이오?\" 사제가 물었다.\n\n\"가스파르, 가스파르!\" 계단에 앉은 여자가 중얼거렸다. \"말조심해요!\"\n\n카드루스는 끼어드는 말에 짜증과 거슬림이 역력했지만, 이 말에는 대꾸하지 않고 사제를 향해 말했다. \"남의 아내를 탐내고 제 것으로 삼고 싶어 하는 자에게, 사람이 어찌 의리를 지킬 수 있겠습니까? 하지만 당테스는 천성이 워낙 곧고 진실해서, 누구든 친구라고 하는 사람의 말을 다 믿었지요. 가엾은 에드몽, 그는 잔인하게 속았습니다. 하지만 그가 끝내 몰랐던 게 차라리 다행이었지요. 안 그랬다면 죽음의 자리에서 원수들을 용서하기가 더 어려웠을 테니까요. 그리고 사람들이 뭐라고 하든,\" 카드루스가 거친 시정이 없지 않은 제 고장 말씨로 말을 이었다. \"저는 산 자의 미움보다 죽은 자의 저주가 더 무섭게 느껴지는 걸 어쩔 수가 없습니다.\"\n\n\"멍청한 양반!\" 라카르콩트가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그렇다면, 페르낭이 어떤 식으로 당테스를 해쳤는지 당신은 알고 있소?\" 사제가 카드루스에게 물었다.\n\n\"제가요? 저보다 더 잘 아는 사람은 없지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 어서 말해 보시오, 그게 무엇이었는지!\"\n\n\"가스파르!\" 라카르콩트가 외쳤다. \"맘대로 해요. 당신이 주인이니까—하지만 내 충고를 듣겠거든 입 다물고 있는 게 좋을걸.\"\n\n\"음, 마누라,\" 카드루스가 대답했다. \"당신 말이 옳을지도 모르겠군!\"\n\n\"그럼 아무 말도 안 하시겠다는 거요?\" 사제가 물었다.\n\n\"그래 봤자 무슨 소용이 있겠습니까?\" 카드루스가 물었다. \"그 가엾은 친구가 살아 있어서, 제게 와서 누가 참된 벗이고 누가 거짓된 벗인지 솔직히 말해 달라고 사정한다면, 글쎄요, 어쩌면 저도 망설이지 않았겠지요. 하지만 신부님 말씀으로는 그가 이미 이 세상 사람이 아니라니, 미움이니 복수니 하는 것과는 아무 상관도 없게 된 거지요. 그러니 그런 감정일랑 죄다 그와 함께 묻어 둡시다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 사제가 말했다. \"당신 말로는 거짓되고 음흉하다는 자들에게, 의리 있는 우정에 돌아갈 보답을 내가 베풀어 주는 편이 낫겠다는 거요?\"\n\n\"그건 정말 맞는 말씀입니다.\" 카드루스가 대답했다. \"옳은 말씀이지요, 가엾은 에드몽의 선물은 페르낭이나 당글라르 같은 배신자들을 위한 게 아니었습니다. 게다가, 그자들에게 그게 무슨 대수겠습니까? 바다에 물 한 방울 떨어뜨리는 거나 다를 바 없지요.\"\n\n\"잊지 마세요,\" 라카르콩트가 끼어들었다. \"그 둘은 당신쯤은 단번에 짓뭉개 버릴 수 있는 사람들이라고요!\"\n\n\"어째서요?\" 사제가 물었다. \"그렇다면 그자들이 그토록 부유하고 권세가 있단 말이오?\"\n\n\"그자들 내력을 모르신단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"모르오. 부디 내게 들려주시오!\"\n\n카드루스는 잠시 생각에 잠기는 듯하더니 말했다. \"아니요, 정말이지, 그러자면 시간이 너무 많이 걸립니다.\"\n\n\"그럼, 이보시오,\" 사제가 자기로서는 전혀 관심 없다는 듯한 어조로 대꾸했다. \"말을 하든 입을 다물든, 그건 당신 마음이오. 나로서는 당신의 망설임을 존중하고 당신의 마음 씀씀이에 감탄할 따름이니, 이 일은 이쯤에서 끝냅시다. 나는 양심껏 내 의무를 다하고, 죽어 가는 이에게 한 약속을 지킬 것이오. 우선 이 다이아몬드를 처분하는 일부터 시작해야겠소.\"\n\n그렇게 말하며 사제는 다시 주머니에서 작은 갑을 꺼내 열고는, 빛이 잘 들도록 요리조리 손에 쥐어, 찬란한 빛깔의 환한 섬광이 카드루스의 휘둥그레진 눈앞을 스쳐 가게 했다.\n\n\"마누라, 마누라!\" 그가 쉰 목소리로 외쳤다. \"이리 좀 와 봐!\"\n\n\"다이아몬드!\" 라카르콩트가 자리에서 일어나 제법 단단한 걸음걸이로 방으로 내려오며 외쳤다. \"무슨 다이아몬드 얘기를 하는 거예요?\"\n\n\"아니, 우리가 한 얘기를 다 듣지 않았어?\" 카드루스가 물었다. \"가엾은 에드몽 당테스가 남긴 아름다운 다이아몬드야. 이걸 팔아서, 그 돈을 그의 아버지와 약혼녀 메르세데스, 페르낭, 당글라르, 그리고 나, 이렇게 나눠 가지라는 거지. 이 보석이 적어도 오만 프랑 값어치가 나간대.\"\n\n\"오, 정말 굉장한 보석이네!\" 깜짝 놀란 여자가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그럼 이 보석에서 나오는 돈의 오분의 일이 우리 몫이라는 거지, 안 그래?\" 카드루스가 물었다.\n\n\"그렇소.\" 사제가 대답했다. \"거기에 노 당테스 몫으로 정해진 부분도 똑같이 나눌 텐데, 나는 그 몫을 살아남은 네 사람에게 고루 나누어도 괜찮다고 생각하오.\"\n\n\"그런데 왜 우리 넷에게 나눠 줍니까?\" 카드루스가 물었다.\n\n\"에드몽이 자기에게 가장 의리 있고 헌신적이었다고 여긴 벗들이니까요.\"\n\n\"배신하고 망쳐 먹는 자들을 친구라고 부를 수는 없지요.\" 이번에는 아내가 낮게 웅얼대는 목소리로 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"물론이지!\" 카드루스가 얼른 맞장구쳤다. \"나도 그렇게 생각해, 방금 이분께 내가 한 말이 바로 그거였어. 나는 배신을, 어쩌면 죄악을 보답하는 게 신성모독이나 다름없다고 생각한다고 말했지.\"\n\n\"잊지 마시오,\" 사제가 보석과 그 갑을 사제복 주머니에 도로 넣으며 차분히 대답했다. \"내가 그러는 건 내 탓이 아니라 당신 탓이오. 에드몽의 마지막 소원을 이룰 수 있도록, 페르낭과 당글라르 두 사람의 주소를 좀 일러 주시오.\"\n\n카드루스의 동요는 극에 달했고, 달아오른 이마에서 굵은 땀방울이 굴러떨어졌다. 사제가 자리에서 일어나, 말이 길을 다시 떠날 만큼 충분히 쉬었는지 확인하려는 듯 문 쪽으로 걸어가자, 카드루스와 그의 아내는 깊은 속뜻이 담긴 눈길을 주고받았다.\n\n\"자, 봐, 마누라,\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"우리가 마음만 먹으면, 이 굉장한 다이아몬드가 죄다 우리 것이 될 수도 있다고!\"\n\n\"그 말을 믿어요?\"\n\n\"아니, 저렇게 거룩한 직업을 가진 분이 설마 우릴 속이겠어!\"\n\n\"그래요,\" 라카르콩트가 대답했다. \"당신 좋을 대로 해요. 나는 이 일에서 손 떼겠어요.\"\n\n그렇게 말하며 그녀는 다시 제 방으로 통하는 계단을 올라갔다. 날씨가 그토록 무더운데도 그녀의 몸은 오한으로 떨렸고, 이가 딱딱 부딪쳤다. 맨 위 계단에 다다르자, 그녀는 돌아서서 경고하는 어조로 남편을 향해 소리쳤다. \"가스파르, 당신이 무슨 짓을 하려는지 잘 생각해 봐요!\"\n\n\"이미 생각도 하고 마음도 정했어.\" 그가 대답했다.\n\n라카르콩트는 그러고서 제 방으로 들어갔다. 무겁고 휘청대는 발걸음에 방바닥이 삐걱거렸고, 그녀는 안락의자 쪽으로 가더니 기진한 듯 의자에 풀썩 주저앉았다.\n\n\"자,\" 아래층 방으로 돌아온 사제가 물었다. \"어떻게 하기로 마음을 정했소?\"\n\n\"제가 아는 걸 죄다 말씀드리지요.\" 그가 대답했다.\n\n\"분명, 그렇게 하시는 게 현명한 처사라고 생각하오.\" 신부가 말했다. \"내게 감추고 싶은 무언가를 알아내고 싶은 마음이 손톱만큼이라도 있어서가 아니라, 그저 당신의 도움으로 유언한 이의 뜻대로 유산을 나눠 줄 수 있다면, 글쎄, 그야말로 더없이 좋은 일이니, 그뿐이오.\"\n\n\"그렇게 되기를 바랍니다.\" 카드루스가 탐욕으로 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했다.\n\n\"귀 기울여 듣겠소.\" 사제가 말했다.\n\n\"잠깐만요.\" 카드루스가 대답했다. \"이야기의 가장 흥미로운 대목에서 방해를 받을 수도 있으니, 그러면 아까울 테고요. 그리고 신부님이 여길 다녀가셨다는 건 우리끼리만 아는 게 좋겠습니다.\"\n\n이렇게 말하며 그는 살그머니 문으로 가서 문을 닫고는, 한층 더 단단히 조심하느라, 밤마다 하던 대로 빗장을 지르고 가로대를 걸었다.\n\n그동안 사제는 마음 편히 이야기를 들을 자리를 골라 두었다. 그는 자기 자리를 방구석으로 옮겨, 자신은 깊은 그늘에 들고 이야기하는 이에게는 빛이 환히 비치도록 했다. 그러고는 고개를 숙이고 두 손을 맞잡은 채—아니, 차라리 꽉 움켜쥔 채—제 맞은편 작은 걸상에 앉은 카드루스에게 온 정신을 쏟을 채비를 했다.\n\n\"이건 내 일이 아니라는 거 잊지 마.\" 라카르콩트가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. 마치 자기 방 마룻바닥 너머로 아래에서 벌어지는 장면을 지켜보기라도 하는 것처럼.\n\n“됐어요, 됐어!” 카드루스가 대답했다. “그 이야기는 그만하시죠. 모든 결과는 제가 책임지겠습니다.” 그러고는 이야기를 시작했다.\n\n“우선, 선생님,” 카드루스가 말했다. “제게 한 가지 약속을 해 주셔야 합니다.”\n\n“무슨 약속이오?” 신부가 물었다.\n\n“그러니까, 제가 지금부터 말씀드리는 이야기를 혹시라도 쓰시게 되더라도, 그 내용을 누구에게 알려 준 사람이 저라는 걸 절대 발설하지 않으시겠다는 약속 말입니다. 제가 이제부터 말할 사람들은 부유하고 권세가 있는 자들이라, 그자들이 손가락 끝만 제게 갖다 대도 저는 유리처럼 산산조각이 나고 말 테니까요.”\n\n“안심하시오, 벗이여.” 신부가 대답했다. “나는 사제이고, 고해는 내 가슴속에서 함께 묻힙니다. 명심하시오, 우리의 유일한 바람은 우리 벗의 마지막 소원을 합당한 방식으로 이루는 것뿐이오. 그러니 거리낌 없이, 또 미움 없이 말하시오. 진실을, 오직 진실만을 말하시오. 나는 그대가 말할 사람들을 알지 못하고, 앞으로도 결코 알 일이 없을 것이오. 게다가 나는 프랑스인이 아니라 이탈리아인이며, 사람이 아니라 하느님께 속한 몸이오. 머지않아 나는 수도원으로 돌아갈 것인데, 임종을 맞는 사람의 마지막 소원을 이루기 위해 잠시 떠나왔을 뿐이오.”\n\n이 분명한 다짐이 카드루스에게 약간의 용기를 주는 듯했다.\n\n“그렇다면, 이런 사정이라면 말이지요,” 카드루스가 말했다. “말씀드리겠습니다, 아니, 마땅히 말씀드려야겠지요. 가엾은 에드몽이 그토록 진실하고 의심할 여지 없다고 믿었던 우정에 대해, 선생님의 오해를 풀어 드려야겠습니다.”\n\n“괜찮다면 그의 아버지 이야기부터 시작해 주시오.” 신부가 말했다. “에드몽은 자기가 가장 깊이 사랑한 그 노인에 대해 내게 무척 많은 이야기를 했소.”\n\n“슬픈 이야기입니다, 선생님.” 카드루스가 고개를 저으며 말했다. “앞부분은 아마 다 알고 계시겠지요?”\n\n“그렇소.” 신부가 대답했다. “에드몽은 마르세유 근처의 작은 선술집에서 체포되던 순간까지의 모든 일을 내게 들려주었소.”\n\n“라레제르브에서였지요! 아, 그렇습니다. 지금도 눈앞에 생생히 보이는 듯합니다.”\n\n“그날이 그의 약혼 잔치 자리가 아니었소?”\n\n“그랬지요. 그토록 흥겹게 시작된 잔치가 아주 슬프게 끝나고 말았습니다. 경찰서장이 병사 넷을 데리고 들어와서, 당테스가 체포되었으니까요.”\n\n“그렇소, 거기까지는 나도 다 알고 있소.” 신부가 말했다. “당테스 자신은 자기 자신에게 직접 관련된 일밖에 알지 못했소. 그는 내가 그대에게 말한 다섯 사람을 다시는 보지 못했고, 그들 누구의 소식도 들은 적이 없으니까.”\n\n“그러니까, 당테스가 체포되자 모렐 씨가 서둘러 자세한 사정을 알아보려 했는데, 알아낸 것은 무척 슬픈 일들뿐이었습니다. 노인은 홀로 집으로 돌아와, 눈물을 글썽이며 아들의 결혼 예복을 접어 놓고는, 종일 방 안을 서성거렸습니다. 잠자리에도 들지 않았지요. 제가 그 아래층에 살았던 터라, 밤새도록 그분이 걸어다니는 소리를 들었으니까요. 저 또한 한숨도 잘 수 없었습니다. 정말이지, 가엾은 아버지의 슬픔이 저를 몹시 괴롭게 했고, 그분이 내딛는 발걸음 하나하나가 마치 제 가슴을 짓밟는 것처럼 가슴에 사무쳤으니까요.\n\n“이튿날 메르세데스가 빌포르 씨의 도움을 청하러 찾아갔습니다. 하지만 도움을 얻지 못했고, 노인을 보러 왔지요. 그녀는 그분이 그토록 비참하고 상심에 잠겨, 한숨도 못 자고 전날부터 음식 한 입 대지 않은 모습을 보고는, 자기가 돌봐 드릴 수 있도록 함께 가자고 했습니다. 하지만 노인은 한사코 거절했습니다. ‘아니다.’ 노인은 이렇게 대답했지요. ‘나는 이 집을 떠나지 않겠다. 내 가엾은 아들은 세상 무엇보다 나를 사랑하니, 감옥에서 풀려나면 제일 먼저 나를 보러 올 게다. 그런데 내가 여기서 그 아이를 기다리지 않는다면, 그 아이가 어찌 생각하겠느냐?’ 저는 이 모든 이야기를 창문 너머로 들었습니다. 메르세데스가 노인을 설득해 데려가 주기를 간절히 바랐으니까요. 밤이고 낮이고 제 머리 위에서 울리는 그분의 발소리가 저를 한시도 편히 쉬게 두지 않았거든요.”\n\n“하지만 그대는 위층으로 올라가 그 가엾은 노인을 위로해 드리려 하지 않았소?” 신부가 물었다.\n\n“아, 선생님,” 카드루스가 대답했다. “위로받기를 마다하는 사람은 위로할 수가 없는 법이지요. 그분이 바로 그런 분이었습니다. 게다가 왜 그런지는 모르겠으나, 그분은 저를 보는 걸 싫어하시는 것 같았습니다. 그래도 어느 날 밤엔 그분의 흐느낌이 들려와, 올라가 보고 싶은 마음을 도저히 참을 수 없었지요. 그런데 막상 문 앞에 이르렀을 때는, 그분이 더 이상 울지 않고 기도를 드리고 계셨습니다. 그분이 쏟아 놓은 그 절절한 말들과 애원의 기도를 지금 그대로 옮길 수는 없습니다, 선생님. 그것은 신앙심 이상이었고, 슬픔 이상이었습니다. 저는 위선자도 아니고 예수회 사람들을 질색하지만, 그때만은 속으로 이렇게 중얼거렸지요. ‘이것 참 대단하구나. 내게 자식이 없는 게 정말 다행이다. 만일 내가 아비가 되어 저 노인처럼 그토록 사무친 슬픔을 겪으면서도, 저분이 지금 하시는 저런 말들을 내 기억이나 가슴속에서 찾아내지 못한다면, 나는 차라리 바다에 몸을 던지고 말 테니까. 도저히 견딜 수 없을 테니까.’”\n\n“가엾은 아버지!” 사제가 나직이 중얼거렸다.\n\n“그렇게 그분은 날이 갈수록 점점 더 외롭게, 홀로 살아가셨습니다. 모렐 씨와 메르세데스가 보러 왔지만 문은 닫혀 있었고, 분명 집에 계시는데도 아무 대답을 하지 않으셨지요. 그러다 어느 날, 평소와 달리 메르세데스를 들이셨는데, 그 가엾은 처녀가 자기 슬픔과 절망에도 불구하고 그분을 위로하려 애쓰자, 그분이 이렇게 말씀하셨습니다. ‘안심하거라, 내 사랑하는 딸아. 그 아이는 죽었다. 우리가 그 아이를 기다리는 게 아니라, 그 아이가 우리를 기다리고 있는 거다. 나는 아주 행복하구나. 내가 가장 늙었으니, 당연히 그 아이를 가장 먼저 보게 될 테니까.’\n\n“아무리 마음씨 좋은 사람이라도, 보십시오, 시간이 지나면 슬픔에 잠긴 사람을 더 이상 찾지 않게 됩니다. 그런 사람을 보면 마음이 울적해지니까요. 그렇게 결국 늙은 당테스는 완전히 홀로 남겨졌고, 저는 이따금 낯선 자들이 그분 집으로 올라갔다가 무언가 꾸러미를 감추려 애쓰며 다시 내려오는 것만 보았을 뿐입니다. 하지만 그 꾸러미가 무엇인지, 그분이 끼니를 잇기 위해 가진 것을 조금씩 팔고 있다는 걸 저는 짐작했지요. 마침내 그 가엾은 노인은 가진 것을 모조리 다 써 버렸습니다. 집세가 세 분기나 밀려, 사람들이 그분을 내쫓겠다고 으름장을 놓았지요. 그분이 일주일만 더 말미를 달라고 청했고, 그것은 허락되었습니다. 제가 이걸 아는 것은, 집주인이 그분 방을 나와 제 방으로 들어왔기 때문입니다.\n\n“처음 사흘 동안은 그분이 평소처럼 걸어다니는 소리가 들렸습니다. 그런데 나흘째 되는 날에는 아무 소리도 들리지 않았지요. 그래서 무슨 일이 있어도 올라가 봐야겠다고 마음먹었습니다. 문은 닫혀 있었지만 열쇠 구멍으로 들여다보니, 그분이 어찌나 창백하고 수척한지, 중병에 걸린 줄로만 알고 모렐 씨에게 달려가 알린 다음 메르세데스에게까지 달려갔습니다. 두 사람이 곧장 왔는데, 모렐 씨는 의사를 데려왔지요. 의사는 장염이라며 음식을 제한하라는 처방을 내렸습니다. 저도 그 자리에 있었는데, 그 처방을 듣고 노인이 짓던 미소를 저는 결코 잊지 못할 겁니다.\n\n“그때부터 그분은 찾아오는 사람을 모두 맞이했습니다. 더 이상 음식을 먹지 않을 핑계가 생긴 셈이었으니까요. 의사가 식사를 제한하라고 했으니 말입니다.”\n\n신부가 일종의 신음 같은 소리를 냈다.\n\n“이 이야기에 마음이 움직이시나 봅니다, 선생님, 그렇지요?” 카드루스가 물었다.\n\n“그렇소.” 신부가 대답했다. “무척 가슴 아픈 이야기요.”\n\n“메르세데스가 다시 찾아왔는데, 그분이 어찌나 달라져 있던지, 전보다 더더욱 자기 집으로 모셔 가고 싶어 했습니다. 모렐 씨의 바람도 그러해서, 그분의 뜻을 거슬러서라도 모셔 가려 했지요. 하지만 노인이 어찌나 거세게 버티고 울부짖던지, 두 사람이 정말로 겁을 먹을 정도였습니다. 그래서 메르세데스는 그분 머리맡에 남았고, 모렐 씨는 자기 지갑을 벽난로 선반에 두고 왔다는 신호를 그 카탈루냐 처녀에게 보내고는 떠났지요. 하지만 노인은 의사의 명령을 핑계 삼아 아무것도 입에 대려 하지 않았습니다. 마침내 (아흐레 동안의 절망과 단식 끝에) 노인은 숨을 거두었습니다. 자기를 그토록 비참하게 만든 자들을 저주하면서, 메르세데스에게 이렇게 말하면서요. ‘혹시라도 내 에드몽을 다시 보거든, 내가 그 아이를 축복하며 죽는다고 전해 다오.’”\n\n신부가 의자에서 일어나 방 안을 두 바퀴 돌더니, 떨리는 손을 바싹 마른 목에 갖다 댔다.\n\n“그렇다면 그대는 그분이 굶어서 돌아가셨다고……”\n\n“굶주려서요, 선생님, 굶주려서지요.” 카드루스가 말했다. “우리 둘이 그리스도교인이라는 것만큼이나 확실합니다.”\n\n신부는 떨리는 손으로 곁에 놓인 반쯤 채워진 물잔을 집어 단숨에 들이켜고는, 벌게진 눈과 핏기 없는 뺨으로 다시 자리에 앉았다.\n\n“참으로 끔찍한 일이었구려.” 그가 쉰 목소리로 말했다.\n\n“하느님이 아니라 사람이 저지른 일이었으니, 더욱 그렇지요, 선생님.”\n\n“그 사람들에 대해 말해 보시오.” 신부가 말했다. “그리고 명심하시오,” 그가 거의 위협하는 듯한 어조로 덧붙였다. “그대는 내게 모든 걸 말하겠다고 약속했소. 그러니 말하시오. 아들은 절망으로, 아버지는 굶주림으로 죽인 그자들이 누구요?”\n\n“그를 시기하던 두 사람입니다, 선생님. 하나는 사랑 때문에, 다른 하나는 야심 때문이었지요. 페르낭과 당글라르입니다.”\n\n“그 시기심이 어떤 식으로 드러났소? 계속 말하시오.”\n\n“그들은 에드몽을 보나파르트파의 첩자라고 밀고했습니다.”\n\n“둘 중 누가 밀고했소? 누가 진짜 죄인이었소?”\n\n“둘 다입니다, 선생님. 하나는 편지를 썼고, 다른 하나는 그것을 부쳤지요.”\n\n“그 편지는 어디서 쓴 것이오?”\n\n“라레제르브에서, 약혼 잔치 전날이었습니다.”\n\n“그랬구나, 그래, 그랬어.” 신부가 중얼거렸다. “아, 파리아, 파리아, 그대는 사람과 세상을 얼마나 정확히 꿰뚫어 보았던가!”\n\n“뭐라고 하셨습니까, 선생님?” 카드루스가 물었다.\n\n“아무것도, 아무것도 아니오.” 사제가 대답했다. “계속하시오.”\n\n“글씨가 알아보이지 않게 하려고 왼손으로 밀고장을 쓴 것이 당글라르였고, 그것을 부친 것이 페르낭이었습니다.”\n\n“하지만,” 신부가 불쑥 외쳤다. “그대도 거기 있었구려.”\n\n“제가요!” 카드루스가 놀라서 말했다. “제가 거기 있었다고 누가 그러던가요?”\n\n신부는 자기가 도를 넘어 너무 앞질러 갔음을 깨닫고는, 얼른 덧붙였다. “아무도 그러지 않았소. 하지만 모든 일을 그토록 소상히 알고 있으려면, 그대가 직접 본 목격자였을 수밖에 없지 않겠소.”\n\n“맞습니다, 맞아요!” 카드루스가 목멘 소리로 말했다. “저도 거기 있었습니다.”\n\n“그런데도 그런 비열한 짓에 항의하지 않았소?” 신부가 물었다. “그러지 않았다면, 그대도 한패였던 셈이오.”\n\n“선생님,” 카드루스가 대답했다. “그자들이 저를 어찌나 취하게 만들었던지, 거의 분별을 잃을 지경이었습니다. 주위에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 흐릿하게밖에 알지 못했지요. 그런 상태의 사람이 할 수 있는 말은 다 했습니다. 하지만 두 사람 다 그저 장난삼아 하는 짓일 뿐, 전혀 해될 게 없다고 제게 장담했습니다.”\n\n“이튿날, 이튿날에는, 선생님, 그자들이 무슨 짓을 저질렀는지 똑똑히 보았을 텐데도, 당테스가 체포될 때 그 자리에 있었으면서도, 아무 말도 하지 않았구려.”\n\n“그렇습니다, 선생님, 저도 거기 있었고, 입을 열고 싶어 안달이 났습니다. 하지만 당글라르가 저를 말렸지요. ‘만일 그가 정말로 죄가 있다면,’ 그가 말하더군요. ‘만일 그가 정말 엘바섬에 들렀다면, 만일 그가 정말 파리의 보나파르트파 위원회에 전할 편지를 지니고 있다가 그 편지가 몸에서 나온다면, 그를 두둔한 자들은 모두 공범으로 몰릴 거야.’ 솔직히 그 무렵의 정치 상황이 그러했던지라, 저도 겁이 나서 입을 다물고 말았습니다. 비겁한 짓이었지요, 인정합니다. 하지만 죄를 지은 건 아니었습니다.”\n\n“알겠소. 그저 일이 되어 가는 대로 내버려 두었다는 거구려.”\n\n“그렇습니다, 선생님.” 카드루스가 대답했다. “그 후로 회한이 밤낮으로 저를 갉아먹고 있습니다. 맹세코, 저는 자주 하느님께 용서를 빕니다. 제 평생 진심으로 자책할 만한 유일한 짓인 그 일이, 틀림없이 지금 이 비참한 처지의 원인일 테니까요. 저는 한순간의 이기심을 속죄하고 있는 셈이지요. 그래서 카르콩트가 불평을 늘어놓을 때마다 늘 이렇게 말합니다. ‘입 다물어, 여편네야. 이게 다 하느님의 뜻이야.’” 그러면서 카드루스는 진심으로 뉘우치는 기색을 온통 내비치며 고개를 떨구었다.\n\n“좋소, 선생.” 신부가 말했다. “그대는 거리낌 없이 털어놓았소. 이렇게 스스로를 탓하는 것은 곧 용서받을 만한 일이오.”\n\n“하지만 안타깝게도 에드몽은 죽었고, 저를 용서해 주지 못했지요.”\n\n“그는 몰랐소.” 신부가 말했다.\n\n“하지만 이제는 다 알 겁니다.” 카드루스가 말을 가로챘다. “죽은 자는 모든 걸 안다고들 하니까요.”\n\n잠시 침묵이 흘렀다. 신부는 일어나 생각에 잠긴 채 방 안을 거닐다가 다시 자리에 앉았다.\n\n“그대가 두세 번 모렐 씨라는 사람을 언급했소.” 그가 말했다. “그가 누구요?”\n\n“파라옹호의 선주이자 당테스의 후원자였습니다.”\n\n“그 사람은 이 슬픈 비극에서 어떤 역할을 했소?” 신부가 물었다.\n\n“용기와 진심 어린 정으로 가득한 정직한 사람의 역할이었지요. 스무 번도 넘게 에드몽을 위해 탄원했습니다. 황제가 돌아왔을 때는 어찌나 열성적으로 편지를 쓰고 애원하고 위협했던지, 두 번째 왕정복고 때 보나파르트파로 몰려 박해를 받았을 정도였습니다. 말씀드렸듯이 그는 열 번이나 당테스의 아버지를 찾아가, 자기 집에 모시겠다고 했지요. 그리고 노인이 돌아가시기 하루 이틀 전에는, 이미 말씀드렸듯이 벽난로 선반에 지갑을 두고 갔고, 그 돈으로 노인의 빚을 갚고 장례를 제대로 치렀습니다. 그렇게 에드몽의 아버지는 평생 누구에게도 해를 끼치지 않고 사셨던 것처럼, 그렇게 돌아가셨습니다. 그 지갑은 지금도 제게 있습니다. 빨간 비단으로 만든 큼직한 지갑이지요.”\n\n“그래,” 신부가 물었다. “모렐 씨는 아직 살아 있소?”\n\n“그렇습니다.” 카드루스가 대답했다.\n\n“그렇다면,” 신부가 말했다. “그는 하느님의 축복을 받은 사람이겠구려. 부유하고, 행복하고.”\n\n카드루스가 씁쓸하게 웃었다. “네, 저만큼이나 행복하지요.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“뭐라고! 모렐 씨가 불행하단 말이오?” 신부가 외쳤다.\n\n“거의 막다른 지경까지 몰렸습니다. 아니, 거의 파산의 불명예 직전에 와 있지요.”\n“어찌하다가?”\n\n“그렇습니다.” 카드루스가 말을 이었다. “사정이 그렇습니다. 이십오 년 동안 일하며 마르세유 상업계에서 더없이 명예로운 이름을 얻었던 모렐 씨가, 완전히 몰락하고 말았습니다. 이태 만에 배 다섯 척을 잃었고, 큰 상회 셋이 파산하는 바람에 손해를 입었지요. 이제 그의 유일한 희망은 바로 가엾은 당테스가 지휘했던 그 파라옹호뿐인데, 코치닐과 인디고를 싣고 인도에서 돌아오기로 되어 있습니다. 만일 이 배마저 다른 배들처럼 가라앉는다면, 그는 끝장난 사람입니다.”\n\n“그 불운한 사람에게 아내나 자식이 있소?” 신부가 물었다.\n\n“네, 아내가 있는데, 어떤 일이 닥쳐도 천사처럼 처신해 온 분입니다. 딸도 하나 있는데, 사랑하는 남자와 결혼하려던 참이었지만, 이제 그 남자의 집안에서 몰락한 사람의 딸을 며느리로 들이지 않으려 합니다. 그 밖에 아들도 하나 있는데, 군대의 중위지요. 짐작하시겠지만, 이 모든 것이 그의 슬픔을 덜기는커녕 오히려 키울 뿐입니다. 만일 그가 이 세상에 혼자였다면, 머리에 총알을 박고 모든 걸 끝내 버렸을 겁니다.”\n\n“끔찍한 일이로군!” 사제가 내뱉었다.\n\n“하늘이 미덕에 보답하는 방식이 이렇습니다, 선생님.” 카드루스가 덧붙였다. “보십시오, 방금 말씀드린 그 한 가지 말고는 평생 나쁜 짓이라곤 한 적이 없는 저는 궁핍 속에 살고 있고, 가엾은 아내는 제 눈앞에서 열병으로 죽어 가는데도, 저는 그녀를 위해 이 세상에서 아무것도 해 줄 수가 없습니다. 저는 늙은 당테스처럼 굶어 죽고 말 텐데, 페르낭과 당글라르는 떵떵거리며 부귀를 누리고 있지요.”\n\n“어찌하여 그렇소?”\n\n“그자들이 저지른 짓이 그들에게는 행운을 가져다주었고, 정직한 사람들은 비참한 처지로 몰렸으니까요.”\n\n“그 일을 사주했으니 가장 죄가 무거운 당글라르는 어떻게 되었소?”\n\n“그가 어떻게 되었느냐고요? 그는 마르세유를 떠나, 그의 죄를 알 리 없는 모렐 씨의 추천으로 어느 스페인 은행에 출납계로 들어갔습니다. 스페인과의 전쟁 동안에는 프랑스군의 군수 부서에서 일하며 한밑천을 잡았지요. 그러고는 그 돈으로 공채에 투자해 자본을 서너 배로 불렸습니다. 처음엔 은행가의 딸과 결혼했는데 그 아내가 죽어 홀아비가 되었고, 두 번째로 어느 미망인과 재혼했지요. 나르곤 부인이라는 여자인데, 궁정에서 큰 총애를 받는 국왕 시종 세르비외 씨의 딸입니다. 그는 백만장자가 되었고, 사람들이 그를 남작으로 만들어 주어, 이제 당글라르 남작이 되었지요. 몽블랑 거리에 으리으리한 저택을 두고, 마구간에는 말이 열 필, 대기실에는 하인이 여섯 명, 그리고 금고에는 몇백만 프랑인지 모를 돈이 들어 있습니다.”\n\n“아!” 신부가 묘한 어조로 말했다. “그는 행복하군.”\n\n“행복하다고요? 누가 그걸 장담할 수 있겠습니까? 행복이든 불행이든, 그건 오직 자기 자신과 벽만이 아는 비밀이지요. 벽에는 귀는 있어도 혀는 없으니까요. 하지만 막대한 재산이 행복을 가져다주는 거라면, 당글라르는 행복한 셈입니다.”\n\n“그럼 페르낭은?”\n\n“페르낭이요? 글쎄요, 거의 똑같은 이야기지요.”\n\n“하지만 교육도 재산도 없던 가난한 카탈루냐 어부 소년이 어떻게 부를 일굴 수 있었소? 솔직히 그건 도무지 이해가 안 가는구려.”\n\n“그건 누구나 다 이해가 안 가는 일입니다. 그의 삶에는 아무도 모르는 어떤 기이한 비밀이 있었던 게 분명합니다.”\n\n“그렇다면 대체 눈에 보이는 어떤 단계들을 거쳐 그런 높은 부와 높은 지위에 이른 것이오?”\n\n“둘 다입니다, 선생님. 그는 부도 지위도 가졌어요. 둘 다 말입니다.”\n\n“그건 있을 수 없는 일이오!”\n\n“그렇게 보이지요. 하지만 들어 보시면 이해가 되실 겁니다. 황제가 돌아오기 며칠 전, 페르낭이 징집되었습니다. 부르봉 왕가는 그를 카탈루냐 마을에 조용히 내버려 두었지만, 나폴레옹이 돌아오면서 특별 징집령이 내려졌고, 페르낭도 군에 들어가야 했지요. 저도 갔습니다. 하지만 저는 페르낭보다 나이가 많았고 막 가엾은 아내와 결혼한 참이라, 해안 경비로만 보내졌지요. 페르낭은 야전군에 편입되어 연대와 함께 국경으로 갔고, 리니 전투에 참가했습니다. 그 전투가 끝난 날 밤, 그는 적과 은밀히 내통하던 어느 장군의 막사 문 앞에서 보초를 서고 있었지요. 바로 그날 밤 그 장군은 영국군 쪽으로 넘어가기로 되어 있었습니다. 장군이 페르낭에게 함께 가자고 제안했고, 페르낭은 그러기로 하고는 초소를 버리고 장군을 따라갔습니다.\n\n“만일 나폴레옹이 왕위에 남아 있었다면 페르낭은 군법 회의에 회부되었겠지만, 그의 행동은 부르봉 왕가에 의해 보상받았습니다. 그는 소위 견장을 달고 프랑스로 돌아왔고, 궁정에서 최고의 총애를 받는 그 장군의 비호를 받게 되어, 스페인 전쟁이 벌어진 1823년에는 대위가 되었지요. 다시 말해, 당글라르가 처음 투자에 손을 댄 바로 그 무렵 말입니다. 페르낭은 스페인 사람이었던 터라, 동포들의 심중을 살피라는 임무를 띠고 스페인으로 파견되었는데, 거기서 당글라르를 만나 무척 가까운 사이가 되었습니다. 그는 수도와 지방의 왕당파의 지지를 끌어내고, 약속을 받아 내고 또 자기 쪽에서도 약속을 했으며, 왕당파가 장악한 산악 협곡들을 자기만 아는 길로 연대를 이끌고 통과했지요. 사실, 이 짧은 원정에서 어찌나 큰 공을 세웠던지, 트로카데로를 함락한 뒤 대령으로 진급했고, 백작 작위와 레지옹 도뇌르 오피시에 십자훈장을 받았습니다.”\n\n“운명이로다! 운명이야!” 신부가 중얼거렸다.\n\n“네, 하지만 들어 보십시오. 그게 전부가 아닙니다. 스페인 전쟁이 끝나자, 유럽 전역에 오래도록 이어질 듯한 긴 평화 때문에 페르낭의 출셋길이 막히고 말았습니다. 오직 그리스만이 터키에 맞서 일어나 독립 전쟁을 시작했고, 모든 눈길이 아테네로 쏠렸지요. 그리스인을 동정하고 지원하는 것이 유행이었으니까요. 아시다시피 프랑스 정부는 그리스를 드러내 놓고 보호하지는 않았지만, 의용군의 지원은 묵인했습니다. 페르낭은 그리스에 가서 복무하게 해 달라고 청해 허락을 받았는데, 여전히 자기 이름은 군적에 남겨 둔 채였지요.\n\n“얼마 후, 모르세르 백작(이것이 그가 지닌 이름이었습니다)이 교관 총감 직위로 알리 파샤를 섬기게 되었다는 소문이 돌았습니다. 아시다시피 알리 파샤는 살해되었지만, 죽기 전에 페르낭의 공로에 보답하여 상당한 금액을 남겨 주었지요. 페르낭은 그 돈을 가지고 프랑스로 돌아왔고, 관보에 중장으로 발표되었습니다.”\n\n“그래서 지금은……?” 신부가 물었다.\n\n“그래서 지금은,” 카드루스가 말을 이었다. “파리의 엘데르 거리 27번지에 으리으리한 저택을 가지고 있지요.”\n\n신부가 입을 열다가 잠시 머뭇거리더니, 애써 마음을 다잡고는 말했다. “그럼 메르세데스는? 듣자 하니 그녀가 자취를 감추었다던데?”\n\n“자취를 감추었지요.” 카드루스가 말했다. “네, 해가 사라지는 것처럼요. 이튿날 더욱 찬란하게 떠오르려고 말입니다.”\n\n“그녀도 부를 일구었소?” 신부가 빈정대는 미소를 띠며 물었다.\n\n“메르세데스는 지금 파리에서 가장 지체 높은 귀부인 가운데 하나입니다.” 카드루스가 대답했다.\n\n“계속하시오.” 신부가 말했다. “꿈 이야기를 듣고 있는 것만 같구려. 하지만 나는 워낙 기이한 일들을 많이 보아 온 터라, 그대가 들려주는 이야기가 다른 때만큼 놀랍지는 않소.”\n\n“메르세데스는 처음엔 에드몽을 앗아 간 그 타격에 깊은 절망에 빠져 있었습니다. 빌포르 씨의 마음을 돌려 보려 애쓴 일이며, 늙은 당테스에게 정성을 다한 일은 이미 말씀드렸지요. 그 절망의 한가운데서 또 다른 고통이 그녀를 덮쳤습니다. 페르낭이 떠난 일이었지요. 그녀는 페르낭의 죄를 알지 못했고, 그를 친오빠처럼 여겼으니까요. 페르낭이 떠나고, 메르세데스는 홀로 남았습니다.\n\n“석 달이 지나도록 그녀는 여전히 울었습니다. 에드몽의 소식도, 페르낭의 소식도 없었고, 절망 속에 죽어 가는 노인 하나 말고는 곁에 아무도 없었지요. 어느 날 저녁, 카탈루냐 마을에서 마르세유로 이어지는 두 갈래 길이 만나는 모퉁이에서 여느 때처럼 하루 종일 지켜본 끝에, 그녀는 그 어느 때보다 풀이 죽어 집으로 돌아왔습니다. 그때 문득 귀에 익은 발소리가 들렸고, 그녀가 불안하게 돌아보는데 문이 열리더니, 소위 군복을 입은 페르낭이 그녀 앞에 서 있었습니다.\n\n“그녀가 가장 간절히 바라던 사람은 아니었지만, 마치 지나간 삶의 한 조각이 되돌아온 듯했지요.\n\n“메르세데스는 페르낭의 두 손을 와락 붙잡았는데, 그는 그것을 사랑이라 여겼지만, 사실은 더 이상 이 세상에 혼자가 아니라는 기쁨, 오랜 시간 홀로 슬퍼하던 끝에 마침내 벗 하나를 보게 된 기쁨일 뿐이었습니다. 게다가 솔직히 말하자면, 페르낭이 미움을 받은 적은 한 번도 없었지요. 다만 딱히 사랑받지 못했을 뿐입니다. 메르세데스의 마음은 온통 다른 사람의 차지였으니까요. 그 다른 사람은 곁에 없었고, 사라졌고, 어쩌면 죽었는지도 몰랐습니다. 이 마지막 생각에 메르세데스는 왈칵 눈물을 쏟으며 고통스레 두 손을 쥐어짰지요. 하지만 전에는 남이 넌지시 일러 줄 때마다 늘 물리쳤던 그 생각이, 이제는 온전한 힘으로 그녀의 마음을 덮쳐 왔습니다. 게다가 늙은 당테스도 끊임없이 그녀에게 이렇게 말했지요. ‘우리 에드몽은 죽었다. 살아 있다면 우리에게 돌아왔을 게다.’\n\n“말씀드렸듯이 노인은 죽었습니다. 만일 그분이 살아 계셨더라면, 어쩌면 메르세데스는 다른 사람의 아내가 되지 않았을지도 모릅니다. 그분이 그 자리에 계셔서 그녀의 변심을 나무랐을 테니까요. 페르낭도 이것을 알았기에, 노인이 죽었다는 소식을 듣고는 돌아왔습니다. 그때 그는 중위가 되어 있었지요. 처음 왔을 때는 메르세데스에게 사랑한다는 말을 한마디도 하지 않았지만, 두 번째 왔을 때는 자기가 그녀를 사랑한다는 것을 일깨웠습니다.\n\n“메르세데스는 에드몽을 기다리며 애도할 시간을 여섯 달만 더 달라고 청했지요.”\n\n“그러니까,” 신부가 씁쓸한 미소를 지으며 말했다. “다 합쳐 열여덟 달이로군. 아무리 헌신적인 연인이라 한들 그 이상을 바랄 수 있겠소?” 그러더니 영국 시인의 말을 나직이 읊조렸다. “‘약한 자여, 그대 이름은 여자로다.’”\n\n“여섯 달 뒤,” 카드루스가 말을 이었다. “결혼식이 아쿨 성당에서 거행되었습니다.”\n\n“바로 그녀가 에드몽과 결혼하기로 했던 그 성당이로군.” 사제가 중얼거렸다. “신랑만 바뀐 셈이오.”\n\n“어쨌든 메르세데스는 결혼했습니다.” 카드루스가 계속했다. “하지만 세상 사람들 눈에는 차분해 보였어도, 라레제르브를 지날 때는 거의 정신을 잃을 뻔했지요. 열여덟 달 전 바로 그곳에서, 자기 마음 밑바닥을 들여다보았다면 여전히 사랑하고 있음을 알았을 그 사람과 약혼 잔치를 벌였으니까요. 페르낭은 더 행복했지만, 그렇다고 마음이 더 편한 것도 아니었습니다. 그 무렵 그가 에드몽이 돌아오지 않을까 끊임없이 두려워하는 걸 제가 보았으니까요. 페르낭은 아내를 데리고 떠나고, 자기도 떠나려고 무척 애를 썼습니다. 카탈루냐 마을에는 불편한 일이 너무 많이 얽혀 있었으니까요. 결혼하고 여드레 뒤, 두 사람은 마르세유를 떠났습니다.”\n\n“그 후로 메르세데스를 다시 본 적이 있소?” 사제가 물었다.\n\n“네, 스페인 전쟁 때, 페르낭이 그녀를 두고 간 페르피냥에서요. 그녀는 아들의 교육에 정성을 쏟고 있었습니다.”\n\n신부가 흠칫했다. “아들이라?” 그가 말했다.\n\n“네.” 카드루스가 대답했다. “어린 알베르 말입니다.”\n\n“하지만 그렇다면 자기 아이를 가르칠 수 있으려면,” 신부가 말을 이었다. “그녀 자신이 교육을 받았어야 할 텐데. 에드몽한테 듣기로는 그녀가 그저 평범한 어부의 딸로, 아름답지만 못 배운 처녀라고 했소.”\n\n“오,” 카드루스가 대답했다. “그가 그 사랑스러운 약혼녀를 그렇게 몰랐단 말입니까? 가장 아름답고 가장 영리한 사람의 머리에 왕관을 씌운다면, 메르세데스는 왕비가 되고도 남았을 겁니다, 선생님. 페르낭의 재산이 이미 불어나고 있었고, 그녀도 그 불어나는 재산과 더불어 함께 성장했지요. 그림이며 음악이며, 온갖 것을 다 배웠습니다. 게다가 우리끼리 얘기지만, 제 생각엔 마음을 다른 데로 돌려 잊어버리려고 그렇게 한 것 같습니다. 가슴을 짓누르는 무게를 덜어 보려고 머릿속을 채운 것이지요. 하지만 이제 그녀의 처지는 안정되었습니다.” 카드루스가 말을 이었다. “재산과 영예가 그녀를 위로해 준 게 분명하지요. 그녀는 부유하고, 백작 부인이고, 그런데도……”\n\n카드루스가 말을 멈추었다.\n\n“그런데도 뭐요?” 신부가 물었다.\n\n“그런데도, 분명 그녀는 행복하지 않습니다.” 카드루스가 말했다.\n\n“어찌하여 그렇게 생각하오?”\n\n“그러니까, 제가 완전히 빈털터리가 되었을 때, 옛 친구들이 어쩌면 저를 도와줄지도 모른다고 생각했지요. 그래서 당글라르를 찾아갔는데, 그는 저를 만나 주지도 않았습니다. 페르낭을 찾아가니, 자기 시종을 시켜 백 프랑을 보내왔지요.”\n\n“그럼 두 사람 다 만나지 못했단 말이오?”\n\n“네, 하지만 모르세르 부인은 저를 보았습니다.”\n\n“그건 또 어찌 된 일이오?”\n\n“제가 돌아서는데 발치에 지갑 하나가 떨어졌습니다. 그 안에는 스물다섯 루이가 들어 있었지요. 얼른 고개를 들어 보니 메르세데스가 보였는데, 곧바로 덧창을 닫아 버리더군요.”\n\n“그럼 빌포르 씨는?” 신부가 물었다.\n\n“오, 그는 한 번도 제 친구였던 적이 없습니다. 저는 그를 알지도 못했고, 그에게 청할 것도 없었지요.”\n\n“그가 어찌 되었는지, 에드몽의 불행에 그가 어떤 몫을 했는지 모르오?”\n\n“모릅니다. 다만 에드몽이 체포되고 얼마 후에 그가 생메랑 양과 결혼했고, 곧 마르세유를 떠났다는 것만 압니다. 틀림없이 그도 다른 자들처럼 운이 좋았겠지요. 틀림없이 당글라르만큼 부유하고, 페르낭만큼 높은 지위에 올랐을 겁니다. 보시다시피 저 혼자만 가난하고 비참하게, 잊힌 채 남았지요.”\n\n“그건 그대가 잘못 생각하는 거요, 벗이여.” 신부가 대답했다. “하느님은 때로 그분의 정의가 잠시 쉬는 동안 한동안 잊으시는 듯 보이지만, 언제나 그분이 다시 기억하시는 순간이 찾아오는 법이오. 자, 보시오, 그 증거가 여기 있소!”\n\n이렇게 말하면서 신부는 주머니에서 다이아몬드를 꺼내 카드루스에게 건네며 말했다. “자, 벗이여, 이 다이아몬드를 받으시오. 이건 그대의 것이오.”\n\n“뭐라고요, 저 혼자만요?” 카드루스가 외쳤다. “아, 선생님, 저를 놀리지 마십시오!”\n\n“이 다이아몬드는 본래 그의 벗들에게 나누어 주려던 것이었소. 그런데 에드몽에게는 벗이 오직 하나뿐이었으니, 나눌 수가 없게 되었구려. 그러니 이 다이아몬드를 받아 파시오. 오만 프랑은 나갈 것이오. 거듭 말하지만, 이 돈이 그대를 비참한 처지에서 건져 내기에 충분하기를 바라오.”\n\n“오, 선생님,” 카드루스가 한 손은 머뭇거리며 내밀고 다른 손으로는 이마에 맺힌 땀을 닦으면서 말했다. “오, 선생님, 한 사람의 행복과 절망을 가지고 농담하지 마십시오.”\n\n“나는 행복이 무엇이고 절망이 무엇인지 잘 아오. 그런 감정을 가지고는 결코 농담하지 않소. 그러니 받으시오. 다만 그 대신에……”\n\n다이아몬드를 만지작거리던 카드루스가 손을 거두었다.\n\n신부가 미소 지었다.\n\n“그 대신에,” 그가 말을 이었다. “모렐 씨가 늙은 당테스의 벽난로 선반에 두고 갔다는, 그래서 아직 그대 손에 있다고 한 그 빨간 비단 지갑을 내게 주시오.”\n\n카드루스는 점점 더 어리둥절해하며 커다란 떡갈나무 찬장으로 가서 문을 열고, 빛바랜 빨간 비단으로 만든 긴 지갑을 신부에게 건넸다. 한때 도금했던 구리 고리 두 개가 지갑을 빙 두르고 있었다. 신부는 그것을 받고, 그 대신 카드루스에게 다이아몬드를 주었다.\n\n“오, 선생님은 하느님의 사람이십니다.” 카드루스가 외쳤다. “에드몽이 선생님께 이 다이아몬드를 주었다는 걸 아무도 몰랐으니, 그냥 가지셔도 됐을 텐데요.”\n\n‘바로 그대라면 그렇게 했겠지.’ 신부가 속으로 중얼거렸다. 신부는 일어나 모자와 장갑을 집어 들었다. “자,” 그가 말했다. “그럼 그대가 들려준 이야기는 모두 빈틈없는 사실이고, 나는 그 모든 세세한 부분까지 믿어도 되는 것이오.”\n\n“보십시오, 선생님.” 카드루스가 대답했다. “저 모퉁이에 성스러운 나무로 만든 십자가가 있고, 이 선반 위에는 제 아내의 성경책이 있습니다. 이 책을 펴 주십시오. 제가 한 손을 십자가에 얹고 그 위에 대고 맹세하겠습니다. 제 영혼의 구원과 그리스도교인으로서의 믿음을 걸고 맹세하건대, 저는 일어난 그대로 모든 것을 선생님께 말씀드렸습니다. 최후 심판의 날에 기록의 천사가 하느님의 귀에 고할 그대로 말입니다!”\n\n“좋소.” 카드루스의 태도와 말투에서 그가 진실을 말하고 있음을 확신한 신부가 말했다. “좋소, 부디 이 돈이 그대에게 보탬이 되기를! 그럼 안녕히. 나는 이렇게 서로를 모질게 해치는 사람들에게서 멀리 떠나가오.”\n\n신부는 카드루스의 격렬한 감사 인사에서 간신히 벗어나, 직접 문을 열고 나가 말에 올랐다. 그러고는 큰 소리로 작별 인사를 연거푸 외쳐 대는 여관 주인에게 다시 한번 인사를 건넨 뒤, 올 때 지나왔던 길로 되돌아갔다.\n\n카드루스가 돌아서자, 뒤에 그 어느 때보다 핏기 없이 떨고 있는 카르콩트가 보였다.\n\n“그럼 내가 들은 게 정말 다 사실이에요?” 그녀가 물었다.\n\n“뭐가? 그 사람이 우리한테만 다이아몬드를 주었다는 거?” 기쁨에 반쯤 정신이 나간 카드루스가 물었다. “그래, 더없이 사실이야! 자, 여기 있잖아.”\n\n여자는 잠시 그것을 뚫어지게 바라보더니, 음울한 목소리로 말했다. “가짜면 어쩌려고요?”\n\n카드루스가 흠칫하며 얼굴이 하얘졌다.\n\n“가짜라고!” 그가 중얼거렸다. “가짜라니! 그 사람이 뭐 하러 내게 가짜 다이아몬드를 주겠어?”\n\n“값도 치르지 않고 당신 비밀을 캐내려고, 이 멍청아!”\n\n카드루스는 그런 생각의 무게에 짓눌려 한순간 망연자실했다.\n\n“오!” 그가 모자를 집어 머리에 동여맨 빨간 손수건 위에 얹으며 말했다. “곧 알아낼 수 있지.”\n\n“어떻게요?”\n\n“보케르에서 장이 서고 있잖아. 거기엔 늘 파리에서 온 보석상들이 있으니, 그 사람들한테 보여 주면 돼. 여보, 집 잘 보고 있어. 두 시간 안에 돌아올 테니까.” 그러고는 카드루스가 황급히 집을 나서더니, 사제가 간 길과 반대 방향으로 쏜살같이 달려갔다.\n\n“오만 프랑이라!” 홀로 남은 카르콩트가 중얼거렸다. “큰돈이긴 하지만, 재산이라고 할 정도는 아니지.”\n\n# Chapter 28. The Prison Register\n\n벨가르드와 보케르 사이의 길에서 방금 우리가 묘사한 장면이 벌어진 그다음 날, 서른이나 서른두 살쯤 된 한 남자가 산뜻한 푸른색 프록코트에 난징목 바지, 흰 조끼 차림으로, 영국인 특유의 풍모와 말씨를 풍기며 마르세유 시장 앞에 나타났다.\n\n\"시장님,\" 그가 말했다. \"저는 로마에 있는 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회의 수석 사무원입니다. 저희는 지난 십 년 동안 마르세유의 모렐 부자 상회와 거래를 이어 왔습니다. 그 상회의 증권을 담보로 십만 프랑가량을 대출해 주었는데, 그 회사가 파산 직전에 몰려 있다는 소문이 들려와 마음이 좀 불안합니다. 그래서 정보를 여쭤보려고 로마에서 일부러 찾아왔습니다.\"\n\n\"선생,\" 시장이 대답했다. \"내가 잘 아는 바로는, 지난 사오 년 동안 불운이 모렐 씨를 따라다닌 듯합니다. 배를 네댓 척 잃었고, 서너 차례 파산의 피해를 보았지요. 하지만 나 자신도 만 프랑의 채권자이긴 하나, 그분의 재정 상태에 대해 무어라 정보를 드릴 처지는 못 됩니다. 시장으로서 모렐 씨를 어떻게 보느냐고 물으신다면, 더없이 명예로운 사람이며 지금까지 모든 약속을 한 치의 어김도 없이 지켜 온 분이라고 말하겠습니다. 내가 할 수 있는 말은 이것뿐입니다, 선생. 더 알고 싶으시거든, 누아유 거리 15번지의 교도소 감찰관 드 보빌 씨를 찾아가 보십시오. 내가 알기로 그분은 모렐 씨에게 이십만 프랑을 맡겨 두었으니, 만약 우려할 만한 근거가 있다면, 액수가 나보다 큰 만큼 나보다 사정을 더 잘 알고 있을 것입니다.\"\n\n영국인은 이 극진한 신중함을 높이 사는 듯, 가볍게 고개를 숙이고는 영국인 특유의 성큼성큼한 걸음으로 일러 준 거리를 향해 떠나갔다.\n\n드 보빌 씨는 집무실에 있었다. 영국인은 그를 보자 놀란 듯한 몸짓을 했는데, 마치 그 앞에 선 것이 처음이 아니라는 듯한 기색이었다. 한편 드 보빌 씨로 말하자면, 어찌나 절망에 빠져 있던지, 그 순간 자기를 사로잡은 생각에 온 정신이 빨려 들어가, 기억이든 상상이든 과거로 거슬러 올라갈 여유가 전혀 없어 보였다.\n\n영국인은 제 나라 사람다운 냉정함으로, 마르세유 시장에게 건넸던 것과 거의 비슷한 말로 그에게 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"오, 선생,\" 드 보빌 씨가 외쳤다. \"불행히도 당신의 우려는 너무나 잘 들어맞았고, 지금 당신 앞에는 절망에 빠진 사람이 있소이다. 나는 이십만 프랑을 모렐 부자 상회에 맡겨 두었소. 그 이십만 프랑은 보름 뒤에 결혼할 내 딸의 지참금이었소. 그 돈은 절반은 이달 15일에, 나머지 절반은 다음 달 15일에 받기로 되어 있었지요. 나는 그 지급을 제때에 받고 싶다는 뜻을 모렐 씨에게 알렸는데, 바로 반 시간 전에 그가 여기 와서, 자기 배 파라옹호가 15일까지 입항하지 못하면 도저히 이 돈을 치를 수 없을 거라고 하지 뭐요.\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 영국인이 말했다. \"그건 지급 정지와 다름없어 보입니다.\"\n\n\"파산에 더 가깝지요!\" 드 보빌 씨가 절망에 차서 외쳤다.\n\n영국인은 잠시 생각에 잠기는 듯하더니 입을 열었다. \"그렇다면, 선생, 이 채권이 당신께 상당한 불안을 안겨 주는 모양이군요?\"\n\n\"솔직히 말하면, 잃은 돈으로 치고 있소.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 제가 그것을 사겠습니다!\"\n\"당신이?\"\n\n\"네, 제가요!\"\n\n\"하지만 엄청나게 깎아서 사려는 거겠지요?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 이십만 프랑 그대로요. 저희 상회는,\" 영국인이 웃으며 덧붙였다. \"그런 식으로 일을 처리하지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 지급은……\"\n\n\"현금으로요.\"\n\n영국인은 주머니에서 지폐 뭉치를 꺼냈는데, 드 보빌 씨가 잃을까 두려워하던 액수의 두 배는 됨 직했다. 드 보빌 씨의 얼굴에 기쁨의 빛이 스쳤으나, 그는 애써 자제하며 말했다.\n\n\"선생, 한 가지 말해 두어야겠소. 십중팔구 이 돈의 육 퍼센트도 건지지 못할 거요.\"\n\n\"그건 제가 알 바 아닙니다.\" 영국인이 대답했다. \"그건 제가 대리하는 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회의 일이지요. 어쩌면 그쪽은 경쟁 회사의 몰락을 앞당기려는 속셈이 있는지도 모릅니다. 하지만 제가 아는 것이라곤, 채권의 양도를 받는 대가로 이 돈을 당신께 넘겨 드릴 채비가 되어 있다는 것뿐입니다. 다만 중개 수수료는 하나 청하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그야 당연히 지극히 정당한 일이지요.\" 드 보빌 씨가 외쳤다. \"수수료는 보통 일 점 오 퍼센트요. 이 퍼센트, 삼 퍼센트, 오 퍼센트, 아니 그 이상이라도 좋소? 말씀만 하시오.\"\n\n\"선생,\" 영국인이 웃으며 대답했다. \"저는 저희 상회와 같아서, 그런 식으로는 하지 않습니다. 아니, 제가 청하는 수수료는 전혀 다른 것입니다.\"\n\n\"부디 말씀해 보시오, 선생.\"\n\n\"당신은 교도소 감찰관이시지요?\"\n\n\"십사 년째 그 일을 하고 있소.\"\n\n\"입소와 퇴소 기록부를 관리하시고요?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"그 기록부에는 수감자에 관한 기록도 덧붙여 있겠지요?\"\n\n\"수감자마다 특별 보고서가 있소.\"\n\n\"그런데, 선생, 저는 로마에서 한 가엾은 신부에게 교육을 받았는데, 그분이 어느 날 갑자기 종적을 감추었습니다. 그 뒤에 그분이 이프성에 갇혀 있었다는 사실을 알게 되었지요. 그분이 어떻게 돌아가셨는지 자세한 내막을 좀 알고 싶습니다.\"\n\n\"이름이 무엇이었소?\"\n\n\"파리아 신부입니다.\"\n\n\"오, 그 사람이라면 똑똑히 기억하지요.\" 드 보빌 씨가 외쳤다. \"미친 사람이었소.\"\n\n\"그렇게들 말하더군요.\"\n\n\"오, 틀림없이 그랬소.\"\n\n\"그럴 수도 있겠지요. 하지만 어떤 광기였습니까?\"\n\n\"엄청난 보물의 소재를 안다고 주장하면서, 자기를 풀어 주면 정부에 막대한 돈을 내놓겠다고 했소.\"\n\n\"가엾은 양반!…… 그래서 돌아가셨습니까?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 선생. 대여섯 달 전, 지난 이월에요.\"\n\n\"날짜까지 그리 잘 기억하시다니, 기억력이 대단하시군요.\"\n\n\"이건 기억하지요. 그 가엾은 양반의 죽음에 기이한 사건이 뒤따랐으니까요.\"\n\n\"무슨 일이었는지 여쭈어도 되겠습니까?\" 영국인이 호기심 어린 표정으로 말했는데, 그의 무덤덤한 얼굴에서 그런 기색을 발견했다면 세심한 관찰자는 적잖이 놀랐을 것이다.\n\n\"오, 그러지요, 선생. 그 신부의 지하 감방은 보나파르트의 밀사 가운데 한 명의 감방에서 사오십 피트쯤 떨어져 있었소. 1815년 그 찬탈자의 복귀에 누구보다 크게 기여한 자였는데, 아주 결단력 있고 위험한 사내였지요.\"\n\n\"저런!\" 영국인이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소,\" 드 보빌 씨가 대답했다. \"나도 1816년인가 1817년인가에 그 사내를 볼 일이 있었는데, 병사들을 한 줄로 늘어세우지 않고는 그 감방에 들어갈 수가 없었소. 그 사내는 내게 깊은 인상을 남겼소. 그 얼굴은 결코 잊지 못할 거요!\"\n\n영국인은 눈에 띄지 않을 만큼 살며시 미소 지었다.\n\n\"그러니까 선생,\" 그가 끼어들었다. \"그 두 감방이……\"\n\n\"오십 피트 거리로 떨어져 있었지요. 그런데 그 에드몽 당테스라는 자가……\"\n\n\"그 위험한 사내의 이름이……\"\n\n\"에드몽 당테스였소. 그런데 선생, 그 에드몽 당테스라는 자가 연장을 구했거나 직접 만든 모양이오. 두 죄수가 서로 연락을 주고받던 굴이 발견되었으니 말이오.\"\n\n\"그 굴은 분명 탈옥할 작정으로 판 것이었겠지요?\"\n\n\"의심할 여지가 없소. 하지만 죄수들로서는 불행하게도, 파리아 신부가 강경증 발작을 일으켜 죽고 말았소.\"\n\n\"그래서 탈옥 계획이 중단되고 말았겠군요.\"\n\n\"죽은 자에게는 그랬지요.\" 드 보빌 씨가 대답했다. \"하지만 살아남은 자에게는 아니었소. 오히려 그 당테스라는 자는 탈옥을 앞당길 방도를 본 거요. 그자는 분명, 이프성에서 죽은 죄수는 보통 묘지에 묻힌다고 여기고는, 죽은 자를 제 감방으로 옮겨 와, 시체를 꿰매 넣은 자루 속에 대신 들어가 매장될 순간을 기다린 거요.\"\n\n\"대담한 짓이었고, 어지간한 배짱이 아니고서는 못 할 일이었군요.\" 영국인이 말했다.\n\n\"이미 말씀드렸다시피, 선생, 그자는 아주 위험한 사내였소. 그런데 다행히도, 제 손으로 한 짓 덕분에 정부는 그자 때문에 품고 있던 두려움을 덜게 되었지요.\"\n\n\"어떻게 그리되었습니까?\"\n\n\"어떻게라니? 모르시겠소?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"이프성에는 묘지가 없소. 그래서 죽은 자의 발에 삼십육 파운드짜리 포탄을 매달아 그대로 바다에 던져 버린다오.\"\n\n\"그래서요?\" 영국인이 마치 알아듣는 데 더딘 사람처럼 물었다.\n\n\"그래서, 그자의 발에 삼십육 파운드짜리 포탄을 매달아 바다에 던졌단 말이오.\"\n\n\"정말입니까!\" 영국인이 외쳤다.\n\n\"그렇소, 선생.\" 교도소 감찰관이 말을 이었다. \"바위 너머로 곤두박질쳤을 때 그 도망자가 얼마나 기겁했을지 상상이 가시오? 그 순간 그자의 얼굴을 봤더라면 좋았을 텐데 말이오.\"\n\n\"그건 어려웠겠지요.\"\n\n\"상관없소.\" 드 보빌은 이십만 프랑을 되찾게 되리라는 확신에 더없이 기분이 좋아져서 대꾸했다. \"상관없어요, 머릿속으로 그려 볼 수 있으니까.\" 그러고는 큰 소리로 웃어 댔다.\n\n\"저도 그렇습니다.\" 영국인이 말하며 함께 웃었다. 하지만 그 웃음은 영국인답게 \"이 끝으로만 짓는\" 웃음이었다.\n\n\"그래서,\" 먼저 평정을 되찾은 영국인이 말을 이었다. \"그자는 물에 빠져 죽은 겁니까?\"\n\n\"의심할 여지가 없소.\"\n\n\"그러니까 소장은 위험한 죄수와 미친 죄수를 한꺼번에 처치한 셈이군요?\"\n\n\"바로 그렇소.\"\n\n\"하지만 이 일에 대해 무슨 공식 문서가 작성되었겠지요?\" 영국인이 물었다.\n\n\"그렇소, 그렇소. 사망 진술서 말이오. 아시겠지만, 당테스에게 친척이 있다면, 그자가 죽었는지 살았는지 아는 데 이해관계가 걸려 있을 수도 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"그러니까 이제 그자에게서 물려받을 것이 있다면, 마음 편히 상속받을 수 있겠군요. 그자는 죽었고, 거기엔 의심의 여지가 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"오, 그렇소. 원할 때면 언제든 그 사실을 입증할 수 있소.\"\n\n\"그렇군요.\" 영국인이 말했다. \"하지만 그 기록부 이야기로 돌아갑시다.\"\n\n\"맞소, 이 이야기 때문에 우리 정신이 거기서 벗어났구려. 미안하오.\"\n\n\"미안하다니요? 그 이야기 때문에요? 천만에요. 정말이지 무척 흥미롭던걸요.\"\n\n\"그렇소, 정말 그렇지요. 자, 선생, 그 가엾은 신부에 관한 것을 모두 보고 싶으시다는 거지요. 정말이지 온화함 그 자체인 분이었는데.\"\n\n\"네, 그렇게 해 주시면 정말 고맙겠습니다.\"\n\n\"여기 내 서재로 들어오시오. 보여 드리리다.\"\n\n그리하여 두 사람은 드 보빌 씨의 서재로 들어갔다. 이곳은 모든 것이 완벽한 질서를 갖추고 정돈되어 있었다. 기록부마다 번호가 매겨져 있었고, 서류철마다 제자리가 정해져 있었다. 감찰관은 영국인에게 안락의자에 앉기를 권하고는, 그 앞에 이프성에 관한 기록부와 문서들을 펼쳐 놓으며 살펴볼 시간을 얼마든지 가지라고 했다. 그러는 동안 드 보빌은 구석에 자리를 잡고 신문을 읽기 시작했다. 영국인은 파리아 신부에 관한 기록을 손쉽게 찾아냈다. 그러나 감찰관이 들려준 이야기가 그의 흥미를 크게 돋운 모양인지, 첫 문서들을 죽 훑어본 뒤에는 장을 넘겨 에드몽 당테스에 관한 진술서가 나올 때까지 거슬러 갔다. 거기에는 모든 것이 차례대로 정돈되어 있었다. 고발장, 심문 기록, 모렐의 청원서, 드 빌포르 씨의 여백 주석까지. 그는 고발장을 조용히 접어, 그만큼이나 조용히 주머니에 넣었다. 심문 기록을 읽고는 거기에 누아르티에라는 이름이 언급되지 않았음을 확인했다. 또한 1815년 4월 10일 자 신청서도 죽 훑어보았는데, 거기서 모렐은 검사대리의 조언에 따라, 더없이 선의로(당시는 나폴레옹이 제위에 있었으므로) 당테스가 제국의 대의에 바친 공로를 부풀려 적어 두었다. 그 공로는 빌포르의 증명서들로 인해 반박할 수 없는 것이 되어 있었다. 그제야 그는 모든 내막을 꿰뚫어 보았다. 빌포르가 가로챈 이 나폴레옹에게 보내는 청원서가, 제2차 왕정복고 아래에서는 왕의 검사 손에서 그를 겨누는 무시무시한 무기로 둔갑한 것이었다. 그는 기록부를 계속 뒤지다가 당테스의 이름 옆 괄호 안에 적힌 이 주석을 발견하고도 더는 놀라지 않았다.\n\n에드몽 당테스.\n\n골수 보나파르트파. 1815년 엘바섬에서의 복귀에 적극 가담함.\n\n엄중한 독방에 수감하고, 면밀히 감시·감금할 것.\n\n이 글줄 아래에는 다른 필체로 이렇게 적혀 있었다. \"위 주석 참조 — 어찌해 볼 도리가 없음.\"\n\n그는 괄호 안의 필체를 모렐의 청원서 아래에 붙은 증명서의 필체와 견주어 보았고, 괄호 안 주석이 증명서와 같은 필체임을, 다시 말해 빌포르의 글씨임을 알아냈다.\n\n이에 딸린 주석으로 말하자면, 영국인은 그것이 한때 당테스의 처지에 잠시 관심을 가졌던 어느 감찰관이 덧붙인 것이되, 앞서 인용한 글귀 때문에 그 관심을 어떻게도 실행에 옮길 수 없음을 깨달았으리라는 것을 알아차렸다.\n\n앞서 말했듯이, 감찰관은 신중을 기하느라, 또 파리아 신부의 제자가 조사하는 것을 방해하지 않으려고 구석에 자리를 잡고 「르 드라포 블랑」을 읽고 있었다. 그래서 영국인이 라레제르브의 정자 아래에서 당글라르가 쓴 고발장을 접어 주머니에 넣는 것을 보지 못했다. 그 고발장에는 \"마르세유, 2월 27일, 오후 6시 배달\"이라는 소인이 찍혀 있었다.\n\n그러나 한 가지 말해 두어야 할 것은, 설령 그가 그 광경을 보았다 하더라도, 그 종잇조각을 워낙 대수롭지 않게 여기고 자신의 이십만 프랑을 워낙 중하게 여겼던 터라, 영국인이 무슨 짓을 하든, 그것이 아무리 규정에 어긋나든 막아서지 않았으리라는 점이다.\n\n\"고맙습니다.\" 영국인이 기록부를 탁 덮으며 말했다. \"원하던 것은 다 얻었습니다. 이제 제 약속을 지킬 차례군요. 채권 양도서를 간단히 한 장 써 주십시오. 거기에 현금을 받았다는 사실을 적어 주시면, 제가 돈을 넘겨 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n그는 일어나 드 보빌 씨에게 자리를 내주었고, 드 보빌 씨는 사양 없이 자리에 앉아 재빨리 양도서를 작성했다. 그러는 동안 영국인은 책상 맞은편에서 지폐를 헤아렸다.\n\n# 제28장. 감옥 기록부\n\n몇 해 전 마르세유를 떠나면서 모렐 상회의 내부 사정을 훤히 꿰뚫고 있던 사람이 지금 다시 돌아온다면, 큰 변화를 발견할 것이다. 번창하고 잘나가는 사업체에 감돌던 생기와 풍요와 행복의 분위기 대신, 창가의 명랑한 얼굴들 대신, 긴 복도를 분주히 오가던 직원들 대신, 짐 꾸러미로 가득 차 짐꾼들의 외침과 농담이 메아리치던 마당 대신, 누구든 곧바로 슬픔과 우울의 기색만을 알아챘을 것이다. 한때 복도와 사무실을 가득 메우던 그 많은 직원들 중 이제 단 둘만 남아 있었다. 한 사람은 스물서너 살쯤 된 젊은이로, 모렐 씨의 딸을 사랑하여, 손을 떼라는 친구들의 갖은 설득에도 그의 곁에 남아 있었다. 다른 한 사람은 외눈박이 늙은 회계원으로, \"코클레스\" 혹은 \"외눈\"이라 불렸다. 이제는 거의 텅 비어 버린 이 거대한 벌집 같은 상회를 한때 가득 채우던 젊은이들이 붙여 준 별명이었는데, 그 별명이 그의 진짜 이름을 어찌나 완전히 대신해 버렸는지, 누가 본명으로 그를 불렀다면 십중팔구 대답조차 하지 않았을 것이다.\n\n코클레스는 모렐 씨 밑에 그대로 남아 있었고, 그의 처지에는 아주 기묘한 변화가 일어나 있었다. 그는 회계원의 지위로 올라서는 동시에 하인의 지위로 떨어진 셈이었다. 그래도 그는 여전히 코클레스, 착하고 참을성 있고 헌신적인 사람이었다. 다만 셈에 관해서만은 굽힐 줄 몰라서, 그 한 가지 일에서만큼은 온 세상을 상대로, 심지어 모렐 씨를 상대로도 뜻을 굽히지 않았을 것이다. 또한 손바닥 보듯 외고 있는 구구단에는 어찌나 강했던지, 어떤 술책을 부리고 어떤 함정을 파 놓아도 그를 걸려들게 할 수 없었다.\n\n상회에 닥친 재난들 한가운데서, 코클레스만이 유일하게 흔들리지 않았다. 그러나 이는 애정이 부족해서가 아니었다. 오히려 그 반대로, 확고한 신념에서 비롯된 것이었다. 가라앉을 운명의 배에서, 닻을 올리기도 전에 한 마리씩 떠나가는 쥐들처럼, 그 많던 직원들도 차츰차츰 사무실과 창고를 버리고 떠났다. 코클레스는 그들이 떠나는 것을 지켜보면서도 그 이유를 물어볼 생각조차 하지 않았다. 앞서 말했듯이 코클레스에게는 모든 것이 셈의 문제였고, 이십 년 동안 그는 모든 대금이 어찌나 정확하게 치러지는지를 늘 보아 온 터라, 이 상회가 지급을 멈춘다는 것은, 물방앗간 주인에게 그토록 오래 물레방아를 돌려 온 강물이 흐르기를 멈춘다는 것만큼이나 있을 수 없는 일로 여겨졌다.\n\n아직 코클레스의 믿음을 흔들 만한 일은 아무것도 일어나지 않았다. 지난달 지급도 더없이 꼼꼼하고 정확하게 이루어졌다. 코클레스는 자기 금고에서 십사 수가 남는 것을 발견하고는, 그날 저녁 그것을 모렐 씨에게 가져갔다. 모렐 씨는 서글픈 미소를 지으며 거의 텅 빈 서랍에 그것을 던져 넣고는 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"고맙네, 코클레스. 자네는 회계원 중의 진주야.\"\n\n코클레스는 더없이 흡족한 마음으로 물러갔다. 마르세유의 정직한 사람들 중에서도 진주와 같은 모렐 씨가 이렇게 칭찬해 주니, 그로서는 오십 크라운을 선물 받는 것보다 더 흐뭇했던 것이다. 그러나 그달 말부터 모렐 씨는 불안에 떠는 시간을 숱하게 보내고 있었다.\n\n당시 만기가 닥친 대금을 치르기 위해, 그는 모든 자금을 끌어모았다. 그리고 자신이 그토록 막다른 지경에 몰렸다는 사실이 알려지면 곤경에 처했다는 소문이 마르세유에 퍼질까 두려워, 그는 보케르 시장으로 가서 아내와 딸의 보석, 그리고 은식기 일부를 팔았다. 이렇게 해서 그달 말은 넘겼지만, 이제 자금은 바닥나 버렸다. 떠도는 소문 탓에 신용은 더 이상 얻을 수 없었다. 이달 15일에 만기가 돌아오는 십만 프랑과, 다음 달 15일에 보빌 씨에게 갚아야 할 십만 프랑을 마련하기 위해, 모렐 씨에게 남은 희망이라고는 사실상 파라옹호의 귀환뿐이었다. 그는 같은 시기에 닻을 올려 이미 항구에 들어와 있는 어느 배로부터 파라옹호가 출항했다는 소식을 들었던 것이다.\n\n그러나 파라옹호처럼 캘커타에서 온 그 배가 입항한 지 보름이 지나도록, 파라옹호에 관해서는 아무 소식도 들려오지 않았다.\n\n보빌 씨와 면담을 가진 다음 날, 로마의 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회의 신임 받는 직원이 모렐 씨를 찾아왔을 때, 사정은 이러했다.\n\n에마뉘엘이 그를 맞았다. 이 젊은이는 새로운 얼굴이 나타날 때마다 가슴이 철렁했다. 새로운 얼굴이란 곧 새로운 채권자, 불안에 차서 상회의 주인을 따지러 온 사람일지도 몰랐기 때문이다. 젊은이는 고용주에게 이 면담의 고통을 덜어 주고자 찾아온 이에게 용건을 물었다. 그러나 낯선 손님은 에마뉘엘 씨에게는 할 말이 없으며, 자기 용건은 모렐 씨 본인과 직접 처리할 일이라고 잘라 말했다.\n\n에마뉘엘은 한숨을 내쉬며 코클레스를 불렀다. 코클레스가 나타나자, 젊은이는 그에게 손님을 모렐 씨의 방으로 안내하라고 일렀다. 코클레스가 앞장서고 낯선 손님이 그 뒤를 따랐다. 계단에서 그들은 열여섯이나 열일곱쯤 된 아름다운 아가씨와 마주쳤는데, 그녀는 불안한 눈빛으로 손님을 바라보았다.\n\n\"모렐 씨는 방에 계시지요, 쥘리 아가씨?\" 회계원이 물었다.\n\n\"네, 적어도 그러실 거예요.\" 아가씨가 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"가서 보세요, 코클레스. 아버지가 계시면 이분이 오셨다고 알려 드리고요.\"\n\n\"제 이름을 알릴 필요는 없습니다, 아가씨.\" 영국인이 대답했다. \"모렐 씨는 제 이름을 모르십니다. 이분께서는 그저 아버님과 거래하시는 로마의 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회에서 신임 받는 직원이 왔다고만 알려 주시면 됩니다.\"\n\n아가씨는 얼굴이 창백해지더니 계단을 마저 내려갔고, 낯선 손님과 코클레스는 계단을 계속 올라갔다. 그녀는 에마뉘엘이 있는 사무실로 들어갔다. 그동안 코클레스는 자기가 가진 열쇠로 두 번째 계단의 층계참 한구석에 있는 문을 열어, 손님을 대기실로 안내한 다음, 두 번째 문을 열고 그 문을 뒤로 닫고는, 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회의 직원을 홀로 남겨 두고 갔다가, 다시 돌아와 들어가도 좋다는 신호를 보냈다.\n\n영국인이 들어서니, 모렐 씨가 탁자 앞에 앉아 자기 부채 목록이 적힌 장부의 그 어마어마한 숫자 행렬을 넘겨 보고 있었다. 낯선 손님을 보자 모렐 씨는 장부를 덮고 일어나 손님에게 자리를 권했고, 손님이 앉는 것을 보고 나서 자기도 다시 의자에 앉았다. 십사 년의 세월은 이 선량한 상인을 바꾸어 놓았다. 이 이야기가 시작될 무렵 서른여섯이던 그는 이제 쉰이 되어 있었다. 머리는 하얗게 세었고, 세월과 슬픔이 그의 이마에 깊은 고랑을 파 놓았으며, 한때 그토록 굳세고 꿰뚫어 보던 눈빛은 이제 어떤 생각이나 사람에게 억지로 시선을 붙잡혀야 할까 봐 두려워하는 듯 흔들리며 갈피를 잡지 못했다.\n\n영국인은 호기심 어린, 그러면서도 분명 관심이 섞인 표정으로 그를 바라보았다. \"선생,\" 이 시선에 불안이 더해진 모렐이 말했다. \"제게 하실 말씀이 있으신지요?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 선생. 제가 어디서 왔는지 아시겠지요?\"\n\n\"톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회. 적어도 제 회계원이 그렇게 전하더군요.\"\n\n\"맞게 전했습니다. 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회는 이달에 프랑스에서 삼사십만 프랑을 치러야 했습니다. 그래서 선생의 철저한 신용을 알기에, 선생의 서명이 든 어음을 모두 거두어들였고, 만기가 되는 대로 제게 그것을 제시하고 그 돈을 달리 쓰라는 임무를 맡겼습니다.\"\n\n모렐은 깊은 한숨을 쉬며 땀으로 뒤덮인 이마를 손으로 훑었다.\n\n\"그러니까, 선생,\" 모렐이 말했다. \"제 어음을 가지고 계시다는 말씀이군요?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다. 그것도 상당한 액수입니다.\"\n\n\"얼마나 됩니까?\" 모렐이 애써 침착하게 들리도록 목소리를 가다듬으며 물었다.\n\n\"여기,\" 영국인이 주머니에서 한 뭉치의 서류를 꺼내며 말했다. \"교도소 감독관인 보빌 씨가 저희 상회로 양도한 이십만 프랑짜리 채권이 있습니다. 그분께 갚으셔야 할 돈이지요. 물론 이 금액을 그분께 빚지고 계심을 인정하시지요?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다. 거의 오 년 전에 그분이 연 사 푼 오 리로 제게 그 돈을 맡기셨습니다.\"\n\n\"언제 갚으셔야 합니까?\"\n\n\"절반은 이달 15일, 절반은 다음 달 15일입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇군요. 그리고 여기, 곧 만기가 돌아오는 삼만 이천오백 프랑짜리가 있습니다. 모두 선생이 서명한 것으로, 소지인들이 저희 상회에 양도한 것입니다.\"\n\n\"제 것이 맞습니다.\" 모렐이 말했다. 그의 얼굴은 벌겋게 달아올랐다. 평생 처음으로 자신의 서명을 이행하지 못하게 되리라는 생각이 든 것이다. \"이게 전부입니까?\"\n\n\"아닙니다. 월말 만기로, 마르세유의 파스칼 상회와 와일드 앤드 터너 상회가 저희에게 양도한 어음들이 있는데, 거의 오만 오천 프랑에 이릅니다. 다 합치면 이십팔만 칠천오백 프랑입니다.\"\n\n이 금액들을 하나하나 읊는 동안 모렐이 어떤 고통을 겪었는지는 이루 다 말할 수 없다. \"이십팔만 칠천오백 프랑.\" 그가 되뇌었다.\n\n\"그렇습니다, 선생.\" 영국인이 대답했다. \"숨기지 않고 말씀드리겠습니다.\" 그가 잠시 침묵한 뒤 말을 이었다. \"지금까지 선생의 정직함과 정확함은 누구나 인정하는 바이지만, 선생이 부채를 감당하지 못하리라는 소문이 마르세유에 떠돌고 있습니다.\"\n\n거의 무례하다 할 이 말에 모렐의 얼굴은 죽은 사람처럼 창백해졌다.\n\n\"선생,\" 그가 말했다. \"지금까지, 선친에게서 이 상회의 경영을 물려받은 지 이제 이십사 년이 넘었고 선친께서도 그전에 삼십오 년을 이끌어 오셨습니다만, 모렐 부자 상회의 서명이 든 것이 부도난 적은 한 번도 없었습니다.\"\n\n\"압니다.\" 영국인이 대답했다. \"하지만 명예를 아는 사람이 다른 명예로운 사람에게 답하듯, 솔직히 말씀해 주십시오. 이것들도 그 같은 신용으로 치르실 수 있겠습니까?\"\n\n모렐은 몸을 떨며 그 사람을 바라보았다. 그는 지금까지보다 한층 확신에 찬 어조로 말하고 있었다.\n\n\"솔직하게 묻는 말에는,\" 모렐이 말했다. \"솔직하게 답하는 것이 도리겠지요. 그렇습니다, 갚을 것입니다. 바라는 대로 제 배가 무사히 도착한다면 말입니다. 그 배가 도착하면, 제가 거듭 당해 온 그 숱한 사고들로 잃어버린 신용을 다시 얻을 수 있을 테니까요. 하지만 파라옹호가 침몰하고, 이 마지막 방편마저 사라진다면……\"\n\n가엾은 이의 눈에 눈물이 차올랐다.\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 상대가 말했다. \"이 마지막 방편마저 어그러진다면 어떻게 하시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 모렐이 대답했다. \"입에 담기 잔인한 말이지만, 이미 불행에 익숙해진 몸이니 치욕에도 저를 길들여야겠지요. 지급을 중단할 수밖에 없을 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"도와줄 친구는 없으십니까?\"\n\n모렐은 서글프게 미소 지었다.\n\n\"사업에는, 선생,\" 그가 말했다. \"친구라는 게 없습니다. 거래처가 있을 뿐이지요.\"\n\n\"맞는 말씀입니다.\" 영국인이 중얼거렸다. \"그렇다면 희망은 단 하나뿐이군요.\"\n\"단 하나입니다.\"\n\n\"그 마지막 희망 말입니까?\"\n\n\"마지막 희망이지요.\"\n\n\"그것이 어그러진다면……\"\n\n\"저는 파산입니다. 완전한 파산이지요!\"\n\n\"제가 이리로 오는 길에, 배 한 척이 항구로 들어오고 있더군요.\"\n\n\"압니다, 선생. 몰락해 가는 제 신세를 아직도 저버리지 않은 한 젊은이가, 좋은 소식을 제일 먼저 제게 전하고 싶은 마음에 집 꼭대기 전망대에서 시간을 보내곤 합니다. 그가 그 배가 들어왔다고 알려 주었습니다.\"\n\n\"그게 선생의 배는 아니고요?\"\n\n\"네, 그것은 보르도 선적의 라지롱드호입니다. 그 배도 인도에서 왔지만, 제 배는 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"혹시 파라옹호와 교신해서, 선생께 무슨 소식을 가져왔을지도 모르지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"솔직히 한 가지 말씀드릴까요, 선생? 저는 제 배의 소식을 듣는 것이, 모르는 채로 남아 있는 것만큼이나 두렵습니다. 불확실함은 그래도 희망이니까요.\" 그러고는 낮은 목소리로 모렐이 덧붙였다. \"이렇게 늦는 건 정상이 아닙니다. 파라옹호는 2월 5일에 캘커타를 떠났습니다. 한 달 전에는 이곳에 도착했어야 합니다.\"\n\n\"저게 무슨 소리지요?\" 영국인이 말했다. \"저 소란은 무슨 영문입니까?\"\n\n\"오, 맙소사!\" 모렐이 창백해지며 외쳤다. \"무슨 일이지?\"\n\n계단에서 사람들이 황급히 움직이는 요란한 소리와, 반쯤 삼킨 흐느낌이 들려왔다. 모렐은 일어나 문 쪽으로 나아갔다. 그러나 힘이 빠져 그는 의자에 주저앉고 말았다. 두 사람은 서로 마주한 채로 있었다. 모렐은 온몸을 부들부들 떨었고, 낯선 손님은 깊은 연민의 표정으로 그를 바라보았다. 소란은 그쳤다. 그러나 모렐은 무언가를 기다리는 듯했다. 무언가가 그 소란을 일으켰고, 무언가가 뒤따를 것이었다. 낯선 손님은 계단에서 발소리가 들리는 것 같았다. 여러 사람의 것인 그 발소리가 문 앞에서 멈추는 듯했다. 첫 번째 문의 자물쇠에 열쇠가 꽂히고, 경첩이 삐걱대는 소리가 들렸다.\n\n\"저 문의 열쇠를 가진 사람은 둘뿐인데.\" 모렐이 중얼거렸다. \"코클레스와 쥘리.\"\n\n바로 그 순간 두 번째 문이 열리고, 눈물에 젖은 눈을 한 아가씨가 나타났다. 모렐은 떨리는 몸으로 의자의 팔걸이에 의지해 일어섰다. 그는 말을 하려 했으나 목소리가 나오지 않았다.\n\n\"오, 아버지!\" 그녀가 두 손을 맞잡으며 말했다. \"나쁜 소식을 전하는 딸을 용서하세요.\"\n\n모렐의 안색이 다시 변했다. 쥘리는 그의 품으로 뛰어들었다.\n\n\"오, 아버지, 아버지!\" 그녀가 흐느꼈다. \"힘을 내세요!\"\n\n\"그렇다면 파라옹호가 가라앉았단 말이냐?\" 모렐이 쉰 목소리로 말했다. 아가씨는 말을 하지 못했다. 그러나 아버지의 가슴에 얼굴을 묻은 채 고개를 끄덕여 그렇다는 표시를 했다.\n\n\"선원들은?\" 모렐이 물었다.\n\n\"무사해요.\" 아가씨가 말했다. \"방금 항구에 들어온 배의 선원들이 구해 주었대요.\"\n\n모렐은 체념과 숭고한 감사가 어린 표정으로 두 손을 하늘로 들어 올렸다.\n\n\"감사합니다, 하느님.\" 그가 말했다. \"적어도 저 하나만 치셨으니까요.\"\n\n냉정한 영국인의 눈가에 눈물이 어렸다.\n\n\"들어오시오, 들어와요.\" 모렐이 말했다. \"다들 문 앞에 와 있을 테니 말이오.\"\n\n이 말이 떨어지기가 무섭게 모렐 부인이 비통하게 울며 들어왔다. 에마뉘엘이 그 뒤를 따랐고, 대기실에는 반쯤 헐벗은 일고여덟 명 선원들의 거친 얼굴이 보였다. 이 사람들을 보자 영국인은 흠칫하며 한 걸음 나섰다가, 이내 스스로를 억누르고는 방의 가장 멀고 가장 어두운 구석으로 물러났다. 모렐 부인은 남편 곁에 앉아 그의 한 손을 자기 두 손으로 감쌌고, 쥘리는 여전히 아버지의 어깨에 머리를 기댄 채 있었으며, 에마뉘엘은 방 한가운데에 서서 모렐의 가족과 문 앞의 선원들 사이를 잇는 연결 고리처럼 보였다.\n\n\"어쩌다 이런 일이 일어났는가?\" 모렐이 말했다.\n\n\"가까이 오게, 페늘롱.\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"그리고 자초지종을 다 말해 주게.\"\n\n열대의 태양에 검게 그을린 늙은 뱃사람이 망가진 모자를 두 손으로 빙빙 돌리며 앞으로 나섰다.\n\n\"안녕하십니까, 모렐 씨.\" 그가 말했다. 마치 전날 저녁에 마르세유를 떠났다가 엑스나 툴롱에서 막 돌아온 사람처럼.\n\n\"잘 있었나, 페늘롱.\" 모렐이 대답했다. 그는 눈물을 흘리면서도 미소를 참지 못했다. \"선장은 어디 있나?\"\n\n\"선장님은요, 모렐 씨. 팔마에서 병이 나 뒤에 남으셨습니다. 하지만 하느님 덕분에 큰 탈은 아닐 테니, 며칠 후면 멀쩡하게 살아 돌아온 선장님을 보시게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"자, 이제 자네 이야기를 해 보게, 페늘롱.\"\n\n페늘롱은 입안의 씹는담배를 한쪽 볼로 굴리고, 손을 입 앞에 갖다 대고, 고개를 돌려 대기실 쪽으로 담뱃진 한 줄기를 길게 내뱉고는, 한 발을 앞으로 내밀어 몸의 균형을 잡고 이야기를 시작했다.\n\n\"그러니까 말입니다, 모렐 씨.\" 그가 말했다. \"저희는 블랑곶과 보야도르곶 사이 어디쯤에 있었습죠. 일주일간 바람이 잔잔하다가 남남서풍 순풍을 받아 잘 나가던 참이었는데, 고마르 선장님이 제게 다가오시더군요. 그때 제가 키를 잡고 있었다는 걸 말씀드려야겠네요. 선장님이 그러시는 겁니다. '페늘롱, 저기 몰려오는 저 구름 어떻게 보나?' 마침 저도 그걸 보고 있던 참이었지요. '제가 어떻게 보냐고요, 선장님? 글쎄요, 제가 보기엔 저것들이 분수에 넘치게 빨리 솟아오르고 있고, 저 정도로 시커먼 걸 보면 분명 심술을 부릴 작정인 것 같습니다.' 그랬더니 '내 생각도 그래.' 하시며 선장님이 말씀하셨죠. '그러니 그에 맞춰 대비를 해야겠어. 돛을 너무 많이 달았어. 자, 거기, 모두 손 빌리세! 보조돛을 거두고 비행 삼각돛을 접게.' 때를 잘 맞춘 거였습죠. 돌풍이 들이닥치자 배가 기울기 시작했으니까요. '아,' 선장님이 말씀하셨죠. '아직도 돛을 너무 많이 달았어. 모두 큰돛을 내려!' 오 분 후에 큰돛이 내려갔고, 저희는 뒷돛대 윗돛과 큰돛대 맨 위 돛만 달고 나아갔습니다. '그래, 페늘롱,' 선장님이 말씀하셨죠. '왜 고개를 젓나?' '글쎄요,' 제가 말했죠. '아직도 너무 많이 단 것 같아서요.' '자네 말이 맞는 것 같군.' 선장님이 대답하셨죠. '강풍이 닥치겠어.' '강풍이요? 그 정도가 아니라, 폭풍이 닥칠 겁니다. 안 그러면 제가 뭘 모르는 거고요.' 몽트르동에 모래바람 일듯 바람이 몰려오는 게 보이더군요. 다행히 선장님은 자기 일을 잘 아는 분이셨죠. '윗돛에 두 단 줄이게!' 선장님이 외치셨습니다. '바람줄을 늦추고, 활대줄을 당기고, 맨 위 돛을 내리고, 활대의 줄임줄을 빼게.'\"\n\n\"그 위도에서는 그것으로 부족했습니다.\" 영국인이 말했다. \"나라면 윗돛에 네 단을 줄이고 후장 종범을 접었을 겁니다.\"\n\n그의 굳건하고 낭랑하며 느닷없는 목소리에 모두가 흠칫했다. 페늘롱은 손을 눈 위에 갖다 대고는, 자기 선장의 조처를 이렇게 비평하는 사람을 빤히 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"저희는 그보다 더한 것을 했습니다, 선생.\" 늙은 뱃사람이 정중하게 말했다. \"키를 올려 폭풍 앞으로 달아나게 했지요. 십 분 후에는 윗돛을 거두고 맨 돛대만으로 질주했습니다.\"\n\n\"그러기엔 배가 너무 낡았는데요.\" 영국인이 말했다.\n\n\"에, 그게 바로 화근이 됐습죠. 열두 시간을 호되게 곤두박질치다가 물이 새기 시작했으니까요. '페늘롱,' 선장님이 말씀하셨죠. '아무래도 가라앉는 것 같아. 키를 내게 주고, 자넨 선창으로 내려가 보게.' 저는 선장님께 키를 넘기고 내려갔습니다. 이미 물이 석 자나 차 있더군요. '모두 펌프로!' 제가 소리쳤죠. 하지만 너무 늦었고, 퍼낼수록 더 차오르는 것 같았습니다. '아,' 네 시간을 퍼낸 뒤 제가 말했죠. '어차피 가라앉을 거라면, 그냥 가라앉읍시다. 사람은 한 번밖에 못 죽으니까요.' '그게 자네가 보이는 본보긴가, 페늘롱?' 선장님이 외치셨죠. '좋아, 잠깐 기다려.' 선장님은 선실로 들어가 권총 두 자루를 들고 나오셨습니다. '펌프를 떠나는 첫 번째 놈은 머리통을 날려 버리겠다.' 그러시더군요.\"\n\n\"잘하셨군!\" 영국인이 말했다.\n\n\"그럴듯한 이유만큼 용기를 북돋아 주는 것도 없죠.\" 뱃사람이 말을 이었다. \"그러는 사이 바람이 잦아들고 파도도 가라앉았지만, 물은 계속 차올랐습니다. 많이는 아니고 한 시간에 두 치씩, 그래도 차오르긴 했지요. 한 시간에 두 치는 별것 아닌 것 같아도, 열두 시간이면 두 자가 됩니다. 거기에 먼저 차 있던 석 자를 더하면 다섯 자가 되는 거죠. '자,' 선장님이 말씀하셨죠. '우리는 할 수 있는 건 다 했고, 모렐 씨도 우릴 탓하실 일은 없을 거야. 배를 구하려고 애썼으니, 이제 우리 목숨이나 건지자. 보트로, 다들 어서, 서둘러.' 그런데 말입니다,\" 페늘롱이 말을 이었다. \"보시다시피, 모렐 씨, 뱃사람은 자기 배에 정이 들기 마련이지만, 그래도 제 목숨에는 더 정이 드는 법이죠. 그래서 저희는 두 번 말할 것도 없었습니다. 더구나 배가 발밑에서 가라앉으면서 '어서 가라, 목숨을 건져라.' 하고 말하는 것 같았으니까요. 저희는 곧 보트를 내렸고, 여덟이 다 거기 올라탔습니다. 선장님이 마지막에 내려오셨죠. 아니, 내려오신 게 아니라, 도통 배를 떠나려 하지 않으셨습니다. 그래서 제가 선장님 허리를 안아 보트로 던지고, 그 뒤를 따라 뛰어내렸습니다. 때를 잘 맞춘 거였죠. 제가 뛰어내리는 바로 그 순간, 군함의 일제 포격 같은 소리를 내며 갑판이 터져 버렸으니까요. 십 분 후에 배는 앞으로 곤두박질치더니, 다시 반대쪽으로, 빙글빙글 돌다가, 그렇게 파라옹호와는 작별이었습니다. 저희로 말하자면, 사흘을 먹지도 마시지도 못했습니다. 그래서 누가 나머지의 양식이 될지 제비를 뽑을까 생각하던 차에, 라지롱드호를 본 거죠. 저희가 조난 신호를 보내자, 그쪽이 저희를 알아보고 다가와 모두 배에 태워 주었습니다. 자, 이제, 모렐 씨, 이게 뱃사람의 명예를 걸고 말씀드리는 사실 그대로입니다. 안 그런가, 거기 자네들?\" 일제히 터져 나온 동의의 웅성거림은 이야기꾼이 그들의 불행과 고초를 빠짐없이 충실하게 전했음을 보여 주었다.\n\n\"좋아, 좋아.\" 모렐 씨가 말했다. \"잘못한 사람은 아무도 없고 다만 운명이 그랬다는 걸 나도 아네. 이리되는 것이 하느님의 뜻이었으니, 그분의 이름에 축복이 있기를. 자네들에게 줄 품삯이 얼마인가?\"\n\n\"오, 그 얘긴 그만두시지요, 모렐 씨.\"\n\n\"아니, 그 얘기를 해야 하네.\"\n\n\"그럼, 석 달 치입니다.\" 페늘롱이 말했다.\n\n\"코클레스, 이 착한 친구들에게 한 사람당 이백 프랑씩 지급하게.\" 모렐이 말했다. \"다른 때 같으면,\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"여기에 이백 프랑씩 더 얹어 선물로 주라고 했을 걸세. 하지만 시절이 변했고, 내게 남은 얼마 안 되는 돈도 내 것이 아니니, 이 일로 나를 인색하다 여기지는 말게.\"\n\n페늘롱은 동료들 쪽으로 돌아서서 몇 마디 주고받았다.\n\n\"그게 말입니다, 모렐 씨,\" 그가 다시 씹는담배를 굴리며 말했다. \"그게 말이죠……\"\n\n\"그게 뭔가?\"\n\n\"그 돈 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그래서……\"\n\"그러니까, 저희 모두 지금은 오십 프랑이면 충분하고, 나머지는 기다리겠다는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"고맙네, 친구들, 고마워!\" 모렐이 감격하여 외쳤다. \"받게, 받아. 그리고 다른 고용주를 찾을 수 있거든, 그 사람 밑으로 들어가게. 그래도 좋아.\"\n\n이 마지막 말은 뱃사람에게 엄청난 충격을 주었다. 페늘롱은 씹는담배를 하마터면 삼킬 뻔했으나, 다행히 가까스로 추슬렀다.\n\n\"아니, 모렐 씨!\" 그가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"저희를 내치시는 겁니까? 그럼 저희에게 화가 나신 거고요!\"\n\n\"아니, 아니야.\" 모렐 씨가 말했다. \"화가 난 게 아니라, 오히려 그 반대일세. 자네들을 내치는 것도 아니고. 하지만 내겐 더 이상 배가 없으니, 뱃사람도 필요가 없는 게야.\"\n\n\"배가 더 없으시다고요!\" 페늘롱이 대꾸했다. \"그럼, 새로 지으시면 되죠. 저희가 기다리겠습니다.\"\n\n\"배를 지을 돈이 없네, 페늘롱.\" 가엾은 선주가 서글프게 말했다. \"그러니 자네들의 고마운 제안을 받아들일 수가 없구먼.\"\n\n\"돈이 더 없으시다고요? 그럼 저희한테 품삯을 안 주시면 됩니다. 저희도 파라옹호처럼 맨 돛대로 질주할 수 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"그만, 그만!\" 모렐이 거의 무너질 듯 외쳤다. \"제발, 나를 두고 가 주게. 더 좋은 시절에 우리 다시 만나세. 에마뉘엘, 이들과 함께 가서 내 지시가 이행되는지 살피게.\"\n\n\"적어도, 저희가 다시 뵐 수는 있는 거지요, 모렐 씨?\" 페늘롱이 물었다.\n\n\"그래. 적어도 그러기를 바라네. 자, 이제 가게.\" 그가 코클레스에게 신호를 보내자 코클레스가 앞장섰고, 뱃사람들이 그 뒤를 따랐으며 에마뉘엘이 맨 뒤를 맡았다. \"자,\" 선주가 아내와 딸에게 말했다. \"나를 좀 두고 나가 주오. 이 신사분과 이야기를 나눠야겠소.\"\n\n그러고는 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회의 직원 쪽으로 눈길을 던졌다. 그는 이 장면 내내 한구석에서 미동도 없이, 앞서 말한 몇 마디 외에는 아무런 관여도 하지 않은 채 있었다. 두 여인은 그 존재를 까맣게 잊고 있던 이 사람을 바라보고는 물러갔다. 그러나 쥘리는 방을 나서면서 낯선 손님에게 애원하는 눈길을 보냈고, 그는 미소로 답했다. 무심한 구경꾼이라면 그 근엄한 얼굴에 떠오른 그런 미소를 보고 놀랐을 것이다. 두 남자만 남았다. \"자, 선생.\" 모렐이 의자에 주저앉으며 말했다. \"다 들으셨으니, 제가 더 드릴 말씀은 없습니다.\"\n\n\"보아하니,\" 영국인이 대답했다. \"새롭고도 부당한 불행이 선생을 덮친 것 같군요. 이것이 선생을 돕고 싶은 제 마음을 더욱 키울 뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"오, 선생!\" 모렐이 외쳤다.\n\n\"어디 봅시다.\" 낯선 손님이 말을 이었다. \"저는 선생의 가장 큰 채권자 중 하나입니다.\"\n\n\"적어도 선생의 어음이 가장 먼저 만기가 돌아오지요.\"\n\n\"갚으실 시간이 필요하십니까?\"\n\n\"기한을 늦춰 주신다면 제 명예를, 따라서 제 목숨을 구하는 셈이 될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"얼마나 늦춰 드리면 되겠습니까?\"\n\n모렐은 곰곰이 생각했다. \"두 달입니다.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"석 달을 드리지요.\" 낯선 손님이 대답했다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 모렐이 물었다. \"톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회가 동의하겠습니까?\"\n\n\"오, 모든 것을 제가 책임지겠습니다. 오늘이 6월 5일이지요.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\"\n\"좋습니다. 그럼 이 어음들을 9월 5일까지로 갱신하십시오. 그리고 9월 5일 열한 시에 (시계 바늘이 열한 시를 가리키고 있었다), 제가 돈을 받으러 오겠습니다.\"\n\n\"기다리고 있겠습니다.\" 모렐이 대답했다. \"그리고 갚아 드리겠습니다. 아니면 저는 죽은 목숨일 테고요.\" 이 마지막 말은 어찌나 낮은 목소리로 내뱉었던지 낯선 손님은 알아듣지 못했다. 어음들이 갱신되고, 옛 어음들이 폐기되니, 가엾은 선주는 자금을 모을 석 달의 말미를 손에 쥐게 되었다. 영국인은 그 나라 사람 특유의 냉정함으로 그의 감사를 받았다. 모렐은 감사의 축복을 쏟아부으며 그를 계단까지 배웅했다. 낯선 손님은 계단에서 쥘리와 마주쳤다. 그녀는 내려가는 척했지만, 사실은 그를 기다리고 있었던 것이다. \"오, 선생……\" 그녀가 두 손을 맞잡으며 말했다.\n\n\"아가씨,\" 낯선 손님이 말했다. \"언젠가 '뱃사람 신드바드'라고 서명된 편지를 한 통 받게 될 겁니다. 그 편지가 아무리 이상하게 보이더라도, 시키는 대로 정확히 따르세요.\"\n\n\"네, 선생.\" 쥘리가 대답했다.\n\n\"약속하시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"맹세코 그렇게 하겠어요.\"\n\n\"좋습니다. 그럼 안녕히, 아가씨. 지금처럼 착하고 다정한 아가씨로 계세요. 그러면 하늘이 에마뉘엘을 남편으로 주어 아가씨에게 보답하리라고 저는 크게 기대하고 있습니다.\"\n\n쥘리는 가냘픈 탄성을 지르며 장미처럼 얼굴을 붉히고 난간에 몸을 기댔다. 낯선 손님은 손을 흔들고는 계단을 계속 내려갔다. 마당에서 그는 페늘롱과 마주쳤는데, 페늘롱은 양손에 백 프랑짜리 돈뭉치를 하나씩 들고서도 그것을 가져야 할지 마음을 정하지 못하는 듯했다. \"나와 함께 가지, 친구.\" 영국인이 말했다. \"자네에게 할 말이 있네.\"\n\n# Chapter 30. The Fifth of September\n\n톰슨앤드프렌치 상회의 대리인이 내준 유예는, 모렐이 전혀 기대하지 않던 순간에 찾아온 것이라, 가엾은 선주에게는 운명이 마침내 자신을 향한 악의를 거두는 데 지쳤다고 믿어도 좋을 만큼 분명한 행운의 손길이었다. 그날 그는 아내와 에마뉘엘, 딸에게 그동안 일어난 모든 일을 들려주었다. 그러자 평온까지는 아니어도 희망의 빛 한 줄기가 가족에게 되돌아왔다. 그러나 불행히도 모렐은 그토록 사려 깊게 대해 준 톰슨앤드프렌치 상회하고만 거래가 있는 것이 아니었다. 그의 말처럼, 사업에는 거래처가 있을 뿐 친구는 없었다. 그는 곰곰이 생각해 보아도 톰슨앤드프렌치 상회가 자기에게 보여 준 그 너그러운 처사를 도무지 이해할 수 없었다. 다만 이런 식의 타산적인 계산 탓이려니 짐작할 뿐이었다. \"우리에게 30만 프랑 가까이 빚진 사람을 도와 석 달 뒤에 그 30만 프랑을 온전히 받는 편이, 그를 서둘러 파산시켜 빌려준 돈의 육 퍼센트나 팔 퍼센트밖에 못 건지는 것보다 낫다.\"\n\n그러나 불행히도, 시기심 때문이든 어리석음 때문이든, 모렐의 거래처들이 모두 이런 견해를 가진 것은 아니었다. 더러는 정반대의 결정을 내리기도 했다. 모렐이 서명한 어음들은 한 치의 오차도 없이 정확하게 그의 사무실로 제시되었고, 영국인이 준 유예 덕분에 코클레스가 그만큼 한 치의 오차도 없이 그것들을 지불했다. 코클레스는 그렇게 평소의 평온을 유지했다. 다만 모렐 혼자만이 불안에 떨며 기억하고 있었다. 15일에 보빌 씨에게 5만 프랑을 갚아야 하고, 30일에는 어음 3만 2천 5백 프랑을 갚아야 한다는 것을, 그리고 이 어음과 형무소 감독관에게 진 빚 모두 유예를 받은 것일 뿐이니, 그것을 갚지 못하면 그는 끝장난 사람이라는 것을.\n\n모든 상인의 의견은 한결같았다. 연이어 모렐을 짓눌러 온 불운을 보건대 그가 지불 능력을 유지하기란 불가능하다는 것이었다. 그러니 월말에 그가 여느 때처럼 정확하게 모든 채무를 청산했을 때 사람들의 놀라움은 컸다. 그래도 모두의 마음에 신뢰가 회복되지는 않았다. 이 불운한 선주의 완전한 파산은 그저 월말까지 미루어진 것뿐이라는 게 일반적인 견해였다.\n\n한 달이 지나는 동안 모렐은 모든 자금을 끌어모으려고 갖은 애를 썼다. 예전에는 그의 어음이라면 어떤 만기든 신용을 받았고, 심지어 서로 사려고 들 정도였다. 그런데 지금은 모렐이 만기 구십 일짜리 어음만이라도 융통해 보려 했지만, 어느 은행도 그에게 신용을 내주지 않았다. 다행히 모렐에게는 기댈 만한 수입이 얼마간 들어올 예정이었다. 그 돈이 들어오자, 그는 7월 말이 왔을 때 채무를 감당할 수 있는 처지가 되었다.\n\n톰슨앤드프렌치 상회의 대리인은 마르세유에서 다시 모습을 보이지 않았다. 모렐을 방문한 다음 날, 혹은 이틀 뒤에 그는 자취를 감추었다. 그 도시에서 그가 상대한 사람이라고는 시장과 형무소 감독관, 그리고 모렐 씨뿐이었으니, 그가 떠난 자리에는 이 세 사람의 기억 말고는 아무런 흔적도 남지 않았다. 파라옹호 선원들로 말하자면, 그들도 자취를 감춘 것으로 보아 어딘가 다른 곳에서 아늑한 일자리를 찾았음이 분명했다.\n\n병에서 회복된 고마르 선장은 팔마에서 돌아왔다. 그는 모렐을 찾아가기를 미루었지만, 선주는 그가 도착했다는 소식을 듣고 그를 만나러 갔다. 마음씨 좋은 선주는 페늘롱의 이야기를 통해 폭풍 속에서 선장이 보여 준 용감한 행동을 알고 있던 터라, 그를 위로하려 했다. 또한 고마르 선장이 감히 청하지 못하던 봉급도 가져다주었다.\n\n계단을 내려오던 모렐은 올라오던 페늘롱과 마주쳤다. 페늘롱은 자기 돈을 잘 쓴 모양인지 새 옷을 차려입고 있었다. 고용주를 보자 마음씨 좋은 이 뱃사람은 몹시 당황한 듯 계단참 구석으로 비켜서더니, 씹던 담배를 이쪽 볼에서 저쪽 볼로 옮기며 커다란 두 눈으로 멍하니 쳐다보았다. 모렐이 여느 때처럼 손을 잡아 주었지만, 그저 가볍게 마주 쥐는 것으로 답할 뿐이었다. 모렐은 페늘롱이 당황한 것을 그 말끔한 옷차림 탓으로 여겼다. 이 선량한 친구가 제 돈으로 그만한 사치를 부렸을 리 없으니, 분명 다른 배에 일자리를 얻은 것이고, 그래서 말하자면 파라옹호의 상을 더 오래 입지 못한 것이 부끄러웠으리라 생각한 것이다. 어쩌면 고마르 선장에게 자신의 행운을 알리고, 새 주인 밑의 일자리를 권하러 온 것인지도 몰랐다.\n\n\"착한 사람들 같으니!\" 모렐은 자리를 떠나며 말했다. \"그대들의 새 주인이 내가 그대들을 아꼈던 만큼 아껴 주기를, 그리고 나보다 더 운이 좋기를!\"\n\n8월은 신용을 새로 트거나 옛 신용을 되살리려는 모렐의 끊임없는 노력 속에 흘러갔다. 8월 20일, 마르세유에는 그가 역마차를 타고 마을을 떠났다는 소식이 돌았다. 그러자 월말이면 어음들이 부도가 날 것이고, 모렐은 채권자들을 상대하라며 수석 서기 에마뉘엘과 출납계 코클레스만 남겨 두고 떠나 버렸다는 말이 나돌았다. 그러나 모두의 예상과는 달리, 8월 31일이 되자 상회는 여느 때처럼 문을 열었고, 코클레스는 계산대 창살 뒤에 나타나 제시되는 어음을 모두 평소대로 꼼꼼히 살펴보고는, 처음부터 끝까지 전부 평소처럼 정확하게 지불했다. 게다가 모렐 씨가 충분히 예상하고 있던 두 건의 환어음까지 들어왔고, 코클레스는 그것들도 선주가 인수한 어음들만큼 정확하게 지불했다. 이 모든 것이 도무지 이해되지 않았고, 그러자 불길한 소식을 전하는 예언자 특유의 끈질김으로, 파산은 9월 말로 미루어졌다.\n\n9월 1일, 모렐이 돌아왔다. 가족은 극도의 불안 속에 그를 기다리고 있었다. 그 파리행에 가족은 큰 기대를 걸고 있었던 것이다. 모렐은 당글라르를 생각하고 있었다. 당글라르는 이제 엄청난 부자가 되어 있었고, 예전에 모렐에게 큰 신세를 진 적이 있었다. 당글라르가 스페인 은행가의 밑에 들어가 막대한 부의 기초를 닦을 수 있었던 것이 바로 모렐 덕분이었기 때문이다. 당시 당글라르는 6백만에서 8백만 프랑의 재산가이며 무한한 신용을 누리고 있다는 말이 돌았다. 그러니 당글라르는 제 주머니에서 동전 한 닢 꺼내지 않고도 모렐을 구할 수 있었다. 대출에 보증만 서 주면 모렐은 살아나는 것이었다. 모렐은 오래전부터 당글라르를 떠올리고 있었지만, 어떤 본능적인 까닭에 그를 멀리해 왔고, 이 마지막 방편에 기대는 일을 가능한 한 미루어 왔다. 그리고 모렐의 판단은 옳았다. 그는 거절당한 굴욕에 짓눌린 채 집으로 돌아왔으니까.\n\n그러나 모렐은 돌아와서도 불평 한마디, 모진 말 한마디 하지 않았다. 그는 흐느끼는 아내와 딸을 끌어안고, 에마뉘엘의 손을 다정하고 따뜻하게 마주 잡았다. 그러고는 이층에 있는 자기 방으로 올라가 코클레스를 불렀다.\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 두 여인이 에마뉘엘에게 말했다. \"우리는 정말 파산하는 거예요.\"\n\n그들끼리 짧게 의논한 끝에, 쥘리가 님에 주둔 중인 오빠에게 편지를 써서 되도록 빨리 와 달라고 청하기로 했다. 가엾은 두 여인은 닥쳐오는 타격을 견디려면 온 힘을 다 끌어모아야 한다는 것을 본능적으로 느끼고 있었다. 게다가 막시밀리앙 모렐은 이제 겨우 스물두 살이었지만 아버지에게 큰 영향력을 지니고 있었다.\n\n그는 의지가 굳고 올곧은 청년이었다. 그가 자기 직업을 정할 때, 아버지는 아들 대신 골라 주려 하지 않고 젊은 막시밀리앙의 뜻을 물었다. 막시밀리앙은 곧바로 군인의 길을 택했고, 그리하여 열심히 공부해 우수한 성적으로 종합기술학교를 졸업한 뒤 보병 제53연대 소위로 임관했다. 그는 일 년째 그 계급에 머물러 있었고, 자리가 나는 대로 진급하기를 기다리고 있었다. 막시밀리앙 모렐은 연대에서 군인에게 지워진 의무뿐 아니라 한 인간으로서의 도리까지 엄격히 지키기로 이름이 나 있었고, 그리하여 \"금욕주의자\"라는 별명을 얻었다. 그에게 이 별명을 붙인 자들 가운데 많은 수가 그저 남이 하는 말을 따라 했을 뿐, 그게 무슨 뜻인지조차 모른 채 되뇌었다는 것은 굳이 말할 필요도 없으리라.\n\n어머니와 누이가 곧 닥쳐올 거라 느끼는 그 혹독한 시련을 견뎌 낼 힘을 얻고자 불러들인 청년이 바로 이런 사람이었다. 그들은 이 사건의 심각함을 잘못 짚은 것이 아니었다. 모렐이 코클레스와 함께 자기 방으로 들어간 직후, 쥘리는 코클레스가 새파랗게 질려 부들부들 떨며, 얼굴에 더할 나위 없는 경악을 드러낸 채 그 방에서 나오는 것을 보았기 때문이다. 쥘리는 그가 곁을 지날 때 물어보려 했지만, 선량한 이 사람은 평소와 달리 황급히 계단을 내려가며 두 손을 하늘로 치켜들고는 외칠 뿐이었다.\n\n\"아아, 아가씨, 아가씨, 이런 끔찍한 변고가 있나요! 누가 이런 일을 믿을 수 있었겠습니까!\"\n\n잠시 후 쥘리는 그가 묵직한 장부 두세 권과 서류철 하나, 돈자루 하나를 들고 다시 올라가는 것을 보았다.\n\n모렐은 장부를 살펴보고, 서류철을 펼치고, 돈을 세었다. 그의 자금을 모두 합치면 6천에서 8천 프랑, 5일까지 받을 어음이 4천에서 5천 프랑이었으니, 아무리 좋게 쳐도 28만 7천 5백 프랑에 이르는 빚을 갚는 데 1만 4천 프랑이 전부였다. 일부라도 변제할 만한 가능성조차 없었다.\n\n그런데도 모렐은 저녁을 먹으러 내려올 때 무척 차분해 보였다. 이 차분함이 두 여인에게는 더없이 깊은 절망보다 더 불안하게 다가왔다. 저녁 식사 후면 모렐은 으레 밖으로 나가 포세앵 클럽에서 커피를 마시며 「세마포르」를 읽곤 했다. 그런데 이날은 집을 나서지 않고 다시 자기 방으로 돌아갔다.\n\n코클레스로 말하자면, 완전히 넋이 나간 듯했다. 그는 한낮의 한동안을 안마당으로 나가 맨머리로 이글거리는 햇볕 아래 돌 위에 앉아 있었다. 에마뉘엘은 두 여인을 위로하려 했지만 그의 말은 자꾸 막혔다. 청년은 상회의 사정을 너무나 잘 알았기에, 모렐 가문에 큰 재앙이 드리워져 있음을 느끼지 않을 수 없었다. 밤이 왔고, 두 여인은 모렐이 방에서 나오면 자기들에게 와 주리라 기대하며 깨어 있었다. 그러나 그들은 그가 발소리를 죽이려 애쓰며 자기들 방문 앞을 지나가는 소리를 들었을 뿐이었다. 두 사람은 귀를 기울였다. 그는 침실로 들어가 안에서 문을 잠갔다. 모렐 부인은 딸을 잠자리에 들게 했고, 쥘리가 물러간 지 삼십 분쯤 지나자 자리에서 일어나 신발을 벗고 살금살금 복도를 따라가, 열쇠 구멍으로 남편이 무엇을 하고 있는지 들여다보려 했다.\n\n복도에서 부인은 물러나는 그림자 하나를 보았다. 쥘리였다. 자신도 불안했던 쥘리가 어머니보다 먼저 와 있었던 것이다. 딸이 모렐 부인 쪽으로 다가왔다.\n\n\"아버지가 글을 쓰고 계세요.\" 쥘리가 말했다.\n\n두 사람은 말없이도 서로의 마음을 알아차렸다. 모렐 부인이 다시 열쇠 구멍으로 들여다보았다. 모렐은 글을 쓰고 있었다. 그러나 모렐 부인은 딸이 알아채지 못한 것을 알아보았다. 남편이 인지가 찍힌 종이에 글을 쓰고 있었던 것이다. 그가 유언장을 쓰고 있다는 끔찍한 생각이 머릿속을 스쳤다. 부인은 몸서리를 쳤지만, 차마 한마디도 입 밖에 낼 힘이 없었다.\n\n이튿날 모렐 씨는 여느 때처럼 차분해 보였고, 평소대로 사무실에 들어갔으며, 정확한 시간에 아침을 들었다. 그러고는 저녁 식사 후 딸을 곁에 앉히고 두 팔로 딸의 머리를 감싸 안아 오래도록 가슴에 꼭 끌어안았다. 그날 저녁 쥘리는 어머니에게, 아버지가 겉으로는 그토록 차분해 보여도 가슴이 거세게 뛰는 것을 느꼈다고 말했다.\n\n그다음 이틀도 거의 같은 식으로 지나갔다. 9월 4일 저녁, 모렐 씨는 딸에게 서재 열쇠를 달라고 했다. 쥘리는 이 부탁에 몸을 떨었다. 불길한 징조처럼 느껴졌던 것이다. 늘 자신이 간직하고 있던, 어린 시절 벌을 받을 때나 빼앗기던 이 열쇠를 어째서 아버지가 달라고 하는 걸까? 쥘리는 모렐을 바라보았다.\n\n\"제가 무슨 잘못을 했기에,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"아버지가 이 열쇠를 가져가시려는 거예요?\"\n\n\"아무 잘못도 없단다, 얘야.\" 이 단순한 물음에 눈에 눈물이 핑 돌며 불행한 사내가 대답했다. \"아무 잘못도 없어. 다만 그게 필요할 뿐이야.\"\n\n쥘리는 열쇠를 찾는 척했다. \"방에 두고 온 모양이에요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n그러고는 밖으로 나갔지만, 자기 방으로 가는 대신 에마뉘엘에게 의논하러 서둘러 갔다.\n\n\"그 열쇠를 아버님께 드리지 마세요.\" 그가 말했다. \"그리고 내일 아침에는, 되도록 한순간도 아버님 곁을 떠나지 마시고요.\"\n\n그녀는 에마뉘엘에게 캐물었지만, 그는 아무것도 몰랐거나, 아니면 아는 것을 말하려 하지 않았다.\n\n9월 4일에서 5일로 넘어가는 밤, 모렐 부인은 작은 소리 하나에도 귀를 곤두세운 채 깨어 있었고, 새벽 세 시까지 남편이 몹시 흥분한 채 방 안을 서성이는 소리를 들었다. 그가 침대에 몸을 던진 것은 세 시였다. 어머니와 딸은 함께 밤을 지새웠다. 그들은 전날 저녁부터 막시밀리앙을 기다리고 있었다. 아침 여덟 시에 모렐이 두 사람의 방으로 들어왔다. 그는 차분했다. 그러나 간밤의 동요가 그 창백하고 수척한 얼굴에 또렷이 드러나 있었다. 두 사람은 차마 그에게 잠은 잘 잤느냐고 묻지 못했다. 모렐은 그 어느 때보다 아내에게 다정했고, 딸에게 살가웠다. 그는 사랑스러운 딸을 바라보고 입맞추기를 멈추지 못했다. 쥘리는 에마뉘엘의 부탁을 떠올리며, 아버지가 방을 나설 때 뒤따르려 했다. 그러나 그가 황급히 말했다.\n\n\"어머니 곁에 있거라, 얘야.\" 쥘리는 따라가려 했다. \"그렇게 해 주었으면 좋겠구나.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n모렐이 이런 식으로 말한 것은 처음이었지만, 그 어조에는 아버지다운 다정함이 배어 있었기에 쥘리는 차마 거역하지 못했다. 그녀는 그 자리에 선 채 말없이 꼼짝 않고 서 있었다. 잠시 후 문이 열리더니, 두 팔이 자신을 감싸안고 어떤 입술이 이마에 닿는 것이 느껴졌다. 그녀는 고개를 들고 기쁨의 탄성을 질렀다.\n\n\"막시밀리앙, 사랑하는 오빠!\" 그녀가 외쳤다.\n\n이 말에 모렐 부인이 일어나 아들의 품으로 뛰어들었다.\n\n\"어머니,\" 청년이 모렐 부인과 누이를 번갈아 보며 말했다. \"무슨 일이에요? 무슨 일이 일어난 거예요? 누이 편지에 깜짝 놀라서 한달음에 달려왔어요.\"\n\n\"쥘리,\" 모렐 부인이 아들에게 눈짓을 하며 말했다. \"가서 막시밀리앙이 막 도착했다고 아버지께 전하렴.\"\n\n쥘리는 방에서 뛰쳐나갔지만, 계단 첫 단에서 편지 한 통을 손에 든 한 남자와 마주쳤다.\n\n\"쥘리 모렐 아가씨 아니신가요?\" 남자가 강한 이탈리아 억양으로 물었다.\n\n\"네, 그런데요.\" 쥘리가 머뭇거리며 대답했다. \"무슨 일이시죠? 저는 당신을 모르는데요.\"\n\n\"이 편지를 읽어 보십시오.\" 그가 편지를 건네며 말했다. 쥘리는 머뭇거렸다. \"아가씨 아버님의 가장 중대한 이익에 관한 일입니다.\" 심부름꾼이 말했다.\n\n쥘리는 황급히 그에게서 편지를 받아 들었다. 얼른 봉투를 뜯어 읽어 내려갔다.\n\n\"지금 당장 메이앙 가로수길로 가서 15번지 집으로 들어가, 문지기에게 육층 방 열쇠를 청하시오. 그 방에 들어가 벽난로 선반 모퉁이에서 붉은 비단으로 짠 지갑 하나를 집어 아버님께 드리시오. 아버님이 그것을 열한 시 전에 받는 것이 중요하오. 당신은 내게 무조건 따르겠다고 약속했소. 그 맹세를 잊지 마시오.\n\n뱃사람 신드바드.\"\n\n쥘리는 기쁨의 탄성을 지르고 눈을 들어 심부름꾼에게 물으려고 주위를 둘러보았다. 그러나 그는 사라지고 없었다. 그녀는 다시 한번 읽어 보려고 쪽지로 눈을 돌렸다가, 추신이 있는 것을 보았다. 그녀는 읽었다.\n\n\"이 일은 반드시 당신이 직접, 혼자 해내야 하오. 다른 사람과 함께 가거나, 당신 대신 다른 누가 간다면, 문지기는 그런 일은 전혀 모른다고 답할 것이오.\"\n\n이 추신은 쥘리의 기쁨을 크게 줄여 놓았다. 정말 두려워할 것이 없을까? 자신에게 무슨 덫을 놓은 것은 아닐까? 그녀는 순진했던 탓에, 자기 또래 처녀에게 닥칠 수 있는 위험을 알지 못했다. 그러나 위험을 두려워하는 데 굳이 그것을 알 필요는 없는 법이다. 오히려 가장 큰 공포를 불러일으키는 것은 대개 알 수 없는 위험이라는 점을 짚어 둘 만하다.\n\n쥘리는 머뭇거리다가 누군가에게 의논하기로 마음먹었다. 그런데 묘한 충동이 일어, 그녀가 의논하러 간 것은 어머니도 오빠도 아닌 에마뉘엘이었다. 그녀는 서둘러 내려가, 톰슨앤드프렌치 상회의 대리인이 아버지를 찾아왔던 날 있었던 일을 그에게 들려주고, 계단에서 있었던 일을 이야기하고, 자기가 한 약속을 되뇌고는, 편지를 보여 주었다.\n\n\"그렇다면 가셔야 합니다, 아가씨.\" 에마뉘엘이 말했다.\n\n\"거길 가라고요?\" 쥘리가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"네. 제가 함께 가 드리지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 저 혼자 가야 한다고 쓰여 있는 걸 못 읽으셨어요?\" 쥘리가 말했다.\n\n\"혼자 가시게 될 겁니다.\" 청년이 대답했다. \"저는 뮈제 거리 모퉁이에서 기다리겠습니다. 그리고 아가씨가 너무 오래 돌아오지 않아 제가 불안해지면, 한달음에 달려가 다시 아가씨 곁에 서겠습니다. 아가씨가 제게 하소연할 일을 만든 자가 있다면, 그자에게 화가 미칠 겁니다!\"\n\n\"그럼, 에마뉘엘,\" 쥘리가 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"이 청에 따르는 게 좋겠다는 말씀이시죠?\"\n\n\"네. 아가씨 아버님의 안위가 거기 달려 있다고 심부름꾼이 말하지 않았습니까?\"\n\n\"하지만 도대체 무슨 위험이 아버지를 위협하고 있는 거죠, 에마뉘엘?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n에마뉘엘은 잠시 망설였지만, 쥘리를 당장 결심하게 만들고 싶은 마음에 이렇게 대답했다.\n\n\"들어 보세요.\" 그가 말했다. \"오늘이 9월 5일 맞지요?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"그럼 오늘 열한 시에, 아버님이 갚으셔야 할 돈이 거의 30만 프랑입니다.\"\n\n\"네, 그건 우리도 알아요.\"\n\n\"그런데,\" 에마뉘엘이 말을 이었다. \"이 집에는 1만 5천 프랑도 없습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 어떻게 되는 거죠?\"\n\n\"오늘 열한 시 전에 아버님을 도와줄 사람을 찾지 못하면, 정오에는 파산을 선언할 수밖에 없게 됩니다.\"\n\n\"아, 그럼 어서, 어서 가요!\" 그녀가 외치며 청년과 함께 서둘러 떠났다.\n\n그동안 모렐 부인은 아들에게 모든 것을 이야기했다. 청년은 아버지에게 연이어 닥친 불운 끝에 생활과 살림의 방식이 크게 달라졌다는 것을 잘 알고 있었지만, 사태가 이 지경에 이른 줄은 몰랐다. 그는 벼락을 맞은 듯했다. 그러더니 황급히 방을 뛰쳐나가 위층으로 달려갔다. 아버지가 서재에 있으리라 여기고 문을 두드렸지만 헛일이었다.\n\n서재 문 앞에 서 있는데 침실 문이 열리는 소리가 들렸다. 그가 돌아보니 아버지였다. 모렐 씨는 서재로 곧장 가는 대신 침실로 돌아갔다가, 이제 막 거기서 나오는 참이었다. 모렐은 도착한 줄도 몰랐던 아들을 보고 놀라움의 외마디를 질렀다. 그는 그 자리에 꼼짝 않고 선 채, 왼손으로 윗옷 안에 감춘 무언가를 누르고 있었다. 막시밀리앙은 계단을 뛰어 내려와 아버지의 목을 두 팔로 끌어안았다. 그러나 그는 별안간 움찔 물러서며 오른손을 모렐의 가슴에 갖다 댔다.\n\n\"아버지,\" 그가 죽은 사람처럼 새파랗게 질려 외쳤다. \"윗옷 안의 그 권총 두 자루로 뭘 하시려는 거예요?\"\n\n\"오, 내가 두려워하던 게 바로 이거였다!\" 모렐이 말했다.\n\n\"아버지, 아버지, 제발,\" 청년이 외쳤다. \"이 총들은 뭐에 쓰시려는 거예요?\"\n\n\"막시밀리앙,\" 모렐이 아들을 똑바로 바라보며 대답했다. \"너는 사내이고, 명예를 아는 사내다. 따라오너라, 설명해 주마.\"\n\n그러고는 모렐이 단호한 걸음으로 서재로 올라갔고, 막시밀리앙은 떨면서 그 뒤를 따랐다. 모렐은 문을 열고 아들을 들인 뒤 문을 닫았다. 그러고는 대기실을 가로질러 책상으로 가서 권총을 내려놓고, 손가락으로 펼쳐진 장부 하나를 가리켰다. 이 장부에는 그의 사업 형편을 정확히 적은 대차대조표가 작성되어 있었다. 모렐은 삼십 분 안에 28만 7천 5백 프랑을 갚아야 했다. 그가 가진 전부는 1만 5천 2백 57프랑이었다.\n\n\"읽어 보아라!\" 모렐이 말했다.\n\n청년은 그것을 읽으며 망연자실했다. 모렐은 한마디도 하지 않았다. 무슨 말을 할 수 있었겠는가? 이토록 절망적인 숫자의 증거에 무슨 말을 보탤 수 있었겠는가?\n\n\"이 참혹한 결과를 막으려고 할 수 있는 일은 다 해 보셨나요, 아버지?\" 잠시 침묵하던 청년이 물었다.\n\n\"다 해 보았다.\" 모렐이 대답했다.\n\n\"기댈 만한 수입이 들어올 데도 없고요?\"\n\n\"없다.\"\n\n\"방편이란 방편은 다 써 보셨고요?\"\n\n\"전부.\"\n\n\"그럼 삼십 분 후에는,\" 막시밀리앙이 침울한 목소리로 말했다. \"우리 가문의 이름이 더럽혀지는군요!\"\n\n\"피는 불명예를 씻어 낸다.\" 모렐이 말했다.\n\n\"옳으신 말씀이에요, 아버지. 무슨 뜻인지 알겠어요.\" 그러고는 권총 하나를 향해 손을 뻗으며 말했다. \"하나는 아버지 것, 하나는 제 것이네요. 고맙습니다!\"\n\n모렐이 그의 손을 붙잡았다. \"네 어머니는! 네 누이는! 누가 그들을 돌보겠느냐?\"\n\n청년의 온몸에 전율이 일었다. \"아버지,\" 그가 말했다. \"지금 제게 살아 있으라고 명하시는 거예요?\"\n\n\"그래, 그렇게 명한다.\" 모렐이 대답했다. \"그게 네 의무다. 너는 차분하고 굳센 정신을 지녔다, 막시밀리앙. 막시밀리앙, 너는 예사 사내가 아니다. 나는 부탁도 명령도 하지 않으마. 다만 내 처지를 네 처지인 것처럼 헤아려 보고, 스스로 판단해 보라는 것뿐이다.\"\n\n청년은 잠시 곰곰이 생각했다. 그러자 그의 눈에 숭고한 체념의 빛이 떠올랐고, 그는 느리고 서글픈 몸짓으로 자기 계급의 표장인 견장 두 개를 떼어 냈다.\n\n\"그럼 그렇게 하겠습니다, 아버지.\" 그가 모렐에게 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"편히 눈감으세요, 아버지. 저는 살겠습니다.\"\n\n모렐이 아들 앞에 무릎을 꿇으려 했지만, 막시밀리앙이 그를 두 팔로 붙들었고, 그 고결한 두 마음은 한순간 서로를 부둥켜안았다.\n\n\"이게 내 잘못이 아니라는 걸 너도 알지.\" 모렐이 말했다.\n\n막시밀리앙이 미소 지었다. \"알아요, 아버지. 제가 아는 가장 명예로운 분이세요.\"\n\n\"착하구나, 아들아. 이제 더 할 말은 없다. 어머니와 누이에게 가 보아라.\"\n\n\"아버지,\" 청년이 한쪽 무릎을 꿇으며 말했다. \"축복해 주세요!\" 모렐은 두 손으로 아들의 머리를 감싸 끌어당기더니, 이마에 여러 번 입맞추며 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그래, 그래, 내 이름으로, 그리고 나를 통해 이렇게 말하는 흠 없는 삼대 조상의 이름으로 너를 축복한다. '불운이 무너뜨린 집을, 섭리는 다시 세울 수 있으리라.' 내가 이런 죽음을 맞는 것을 보면, 아무리 가차 없는 자라도 너를 가엾이 여길 것이다. 그들은 어쩌면 내게 거절했던 시간을 네게는 허락해 줄지도 모른다. 그러니 우리 이름을 불명예에서 지켜 내도록 최선을 다하거라. 일하고, 애쓰고, 뜨겁고 용감하게 분투하거라. 너 자신도, 네 어머니도, 네 누이도 더없이 엄격하게 절약하며 살아서, 내가 네 손에 맡기고 가는 이들의 재산이 날마다 불어나고 열매 맺게 하거라. 완전히 회복되는 그날이 얼마나 영광스럽고, 얼마나 위대하고, 얼마나 엄숙할지 헤아려 보아라. 바로 이 사무실에서 네가 이렇게 말할 그날 말이다. '내 아버지는 오늘 내가 해낸 일을 하지 못해 돌아가셨다. 그러나 돌아가시면서 내가 무엇을 하리라는 것을 아셨기에, 차분하고 평온하게 눈감으셨다.'\"\n\n\"아버지, 아버지!\" 청년이 외쳤다. \"어째서 살아 계시면 안 되는 거예요?\"\n\n\"내가 살아 있으면 모든 게 달라진다. 내가 살아 있으면 호의는 의심으로, 동정은 적의로 변할 거다. 내가 살아 있으면 나는 그저 약속을 어기고 의무를 저버린 사내, 한낱 파산자일 뿐이다. 그러나 반대로 내가 죽으면, 기억해 두어라, 막시밀리앙, 내 주검은 정직하지만 불운했던 사내의 주검이 되는 거다. 살아 있으면 내 가장 가까운 친구들도 내 집을 피할 테지만, 죽으면 온 마르세유가 눈물을 흘리며 나를 마지막 안식처까지 배웅할 거다. 살아 있으면 너는 내 이름을 부끄러워하겠지만, 죽으면 너는 고개를 들고 이렇게 말할 수 있을 거다. '나는 당신들이 죽인 사람의 아들이오. 그는 난생처음으로 약속을 어길 수밖에 없었기에 그리된 것이오.'\"\n\n청년은 신음을 토했지만, 체념한 듯했다.\n\n\"이제,\" 모렐이 말했다. \"나를 혼자 있게 해 다오. 그리고 네 어머니와 누이를 떼어 놓도록 애써 보아라.\"\n\n\"누이를 한 번 더 보지 않으시겠어요?\" 막시밀리앙이 물었다. 청년은 이 만남이 가져올 효과에 마지막 희망을, 그러나 최후의 희망을 걸고 그렇게 제안한 것이었다. 모렐이 고개를 저었다. \"오늘 아침에 그 애를 보았고, 작별 인사도 했다.\"\n\n\"제게 따로 남기실 분부는 없으세요, 아버지?\" 막시밀리앙이 떨리는 목소리로 물었다.\n\n\"있다, 아들아. 그것도 신성한 분부다.\"\n\n\"말씀해 주세요, 아버지.\"\n\n\"톰슨앤드프렌치 상회는, 인정에서였든 아니면 타산에서였든 — 사람의 마음속을 읽는 것은 내가 할 일이 아니다만 — 유일하게 나를 가엾이 여겨 준 곳이다. 그 대리인이 십 분 후에 28만 7천 5백 프랑짜리 어음의 대금을 받으러 올 텐데, 그가 내게 석 달을 내주었다고는 하지 않으마, 다만 먼저 제안했다고 해 두자. 그 상회에 가장 먼저 빚을 갚아라, 아들아. 그리고 그 사람을 공경하거라.\"\n\n\"아버지, 그렇게 하겠습니다.\" 막시밀리앙이 말했다.\n\n\"이제 다시 한번, 잘 있거라.\" 모렐이 말했다. \"가거라, 나를 두고 가거라. 혼자 있고 싶구나. 내 유언장은 침실 책상 서랍에 있을 거다.\"\n\n청년은 의지의 힘은 있으되 그것을 실행할 힘은 없는 채로, 선 자리에 꼼짝 않고 서 있었다.\n\n\"내 말을 들어라, 막시밀리앙.\" 아버지가 말했다. \"내가 너처럼 군인이고, 어떤 보루를 점령하라는 명령을 받았다고 치자. 그리고 네가 내가 그 공격에서 죽으리라는 걸 안다면, 너는 방금 했던 대로 내게 이렇게 말하지 않겠느냐. '가세요, 아버지. 지체하면 아버지는 불명예를 입게 되고, 죽음이 치욕보다 나으니까요!'\"\n\n\"네, 네.\" 청년이 말했다. \"네.\" 그러고는 다시 한번 발작하듯 아버지를 으스러져라 끌어안으며 말했다. \"그럼 그렇게 하세요, 아버지.\"\n\n그러고는 서재를 뛰쳐나갔다. 아들이 떠나자 모렐은 한순간 눈을 문에 고정한 채 서 있었다. 그러고는 팔을 뻗어 종을 당겼다. 잠시 후 코클레스가 나타났다.\n\n이제 그는 더 이상 예전의 그 사람이 아니었다. 지난 사흘간의 끔찍한 진상이 그를 짓이겨 놓았다. 모렐 상회가 곧 지불을 멈추리라는 이 생각이, 이십 년 세월이 했을 것보다 더 그를 땅바닥으로 짓눌렀다.\n\n\"착한 코클레스,\" 모렐이 형언할 수 없는 어조로 말했다. \"대기실에 있게나. 석 달 전에 왔던 그 양반, 톰슨앤드프렌치 상회 대리인이 오거든, 도착했다고 내게 알려 주게.\"\n\n코클레스는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다. 그는 고개로만 알았다는 시늉을 하고는 대기실로 들어가 앉았다. 모렐은 의자에 등을 기댄 채 눈을 시계에 고정했다. 남은 시간은 칠 분, 그게 전부였다. 시곗바늘은 믿기 힘든 속도로 움직였고, 그는 그 움직임이 눈에 보이는 것만 같았다.\n\n극도의 고뇌가 정점에 이른 그 순간 이 사내의 마음속에 무엇이 스쳐 갔는지는 말로 다 할 수 없다. 그는 아직 비교적 젊었고, 헌신적인 가족의 사랑 어린 보살핌에 둘러싸여 있었다. 그러나 그는 어쩌면 비논리적일지언정 분명히 그럴듯한 일련의 추론을 통해, 이 세상에서 자신이 아끼는 모든 것과, 심지어 목숨까지도 떼어 내야 한다고 스스로를 납득시켜 놓은 터였다. 그의 심정을 조금이나마 헤아리려면, 억지로 짜낸 체념의 표정과 눈물에 젖어 하늘을 향해 들린 그 눈을 띤 얼굴을 보았어야만 한다. 분침이 나아갔다. 권총에는 장전이 되어 있었다. 그는 손을 뻗어 하나를 집어 들고 딸의 이름을 중얼거렸다. 그러고는 그것을 내려놓고 펜을 쥐어 몇 마디를 적었다. 사랑하는 딸에게 충분히 작별을 고하지 못한 것 같았던 것이다. 그러고는 다시 시계로 눈을 돌려, 이제는 분이 아니라 초로 시간을 헤아렸다.\n\n그는 다시 그 죽음의 무기를 집어 들었다. 입술은 벌어졌고 눈은 시계에 고정되어 있었다. 그러고는 공이치기를 당기자 방아쇠가 철컥하는 소리에 몸서리쳤다. 죽음 같은 이 고뇌의 순간, 식은땀이 이마에 배어 나왔고, 죽음보다 더 모진 통증이 그의 심장의 끈을 움켜쥐었다. 그는 계단 쪽 문이 경첩에서 삐걱이는 소리를 들었다. 시계가 열한 시를 알리는 예고음을 냈다. 서재 문이 열렸다. 모렐은 돌아보지 않았다. 그는 코클레스의 이 말을 기다리고 있었다. \"톰슨앤드프렌치 상회 대리인입니다.\"\n\n그는 권총 총구를 이 사이에 물었다. 그때 갑자기 외침이 들렸다. 딸의 목소리였다. 그가 돌아보니 쥘리였다. 권총이 손에서 떨어졌다.\n\n\"아버지!\" 처녀가 숨을 헐떡이며, 기쁨에 거의 넋이 나간 채 외쳤다. \"살았어요, 아버지가 살았어요!\" 그러고는 붉은 그물 비단 지갑 하나를 뻗은 손에 든 채 아버지의 품으로 뛰어들었다.\n\n\"살았다니, 얘야?\" 모렐이 말했다. \"그게 무슨 소리냐?\"\n\n\"네, 살았어요, 살았다고요! 보세요, 보세요!\" 처녀가 말했다.\n\n모렐은 지갑을 받아 들었고, 그 순간 흠칫했다. 어렴풋한 기억이, 그것이 한때 자기 것이었음을 일깨워 주었기 때문이다. 한쪽 끝에는 28만 7천 프랑짜리 어음의 영수증이 들어 있었고, 다른 쪽 끝에는 개암만 한 크기의 다이아몬드 하나가, 작은 양피지 조각에 이런 글귀와 함께 들어 있었다. \"쥘리의 지참금.\"\n\n모렐은 손으로 이마를 훔쳤다. 꿈만 같았다. 그 순간 시계가 열한 시를 쳤다. 그는 망치질하는 소리 하나하나가 자기 심장 위로 떨어지는 것만 같았다.\n\n\"설명해 다오, 얘야.\" 그가 말했다. \"설명해 다오, 얘야. 이 지갑을 어디서 찾았느냐?\"\n\n\"메이앙 가로수길 15번지 집, 오층 작은 방의 벽난로 선반 모퉁이에서요.\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 모렐이 외쳤다. \"이 지갑은 네 것이 아니잖느냐!\" 쥘리가 아침에 받은 편지를 아버지에게 건넸다.\n\n\"그럼 혼자 갔단 말이냐?\" 편지를 읽고 난 모렐이 물었다.\n\n\"에마뉘엘이 함께 갔어요, 아버지. 뮈제 거리 모퉁이에서 저를 기다리기로 했는데, 이상하게도 제가 돌아왔을 땐 거기 없었어요.\"\n\n\"모렐 씨!\" 계단에서 어떤 목소리가 외쳤다. \"모렐 씨!\"\n\n\"저 사람 목소리예요!\" 쥘리가 말했다. 그 순간 에마뉘엘이 들어왔다. 그의 얼굴은 생기와 기쁨으로 가득했다.\n\n\"파라옹호예요!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"파라옹호!\"\n\n\"뭐라고! 뭐! 파라옹호라니! 미쳤나, 에마뉘엘? 그 배가 침몰한 걸 자네도 알잖나.\"\n\n\"파라옹호예요, 선주님. 사람들이 파라옹호 신호를 보내고 있어요! 파라옹호가 항구로 들어오고 있다고요!\"\n\n모렐은 의자에 등을 기댄 채 쓰러지듯 주저앉았다. 기력이 다해 가고 있었다. 이런 사건들로 약해진 그의 머리는, 이토록 믿기 힘들고, 들어 본 적 없고, 허황된 사실을 받아들이기를 거부했다. 그러나 그의 아들이 들어왔다.\n\n\"아버지,\" 막시밀리앙이 외쳤다. \"어째서 파라옹호가 침몰했다고 하셨어요? 망루에서 그 배 신호를 보냈고, 지금 항구로 들어오고 있대요.\"\n\n\"얘들아,\" 모렐이 말했다. \"이게 사실이라면, 이건 하늘의 기적일 게다! 그럴 리가 없어, 그럴 리가!\"\n\n그러나 못지않게 믿기 힘들면서도 분명히 실재하는 것이, 그의 손에 들린 지갑이었다. 영수된 어음과, 그 눈부신 다이아몬드 말이다.\n\n\"아, 선주님,\" 코클레스가 외쳤다. \"이게 무슨 영문일까요? 파라옹호라니요?\"\n\n\"가자, 얘들아.\" 모렐이 자리에서 일어서며 말했다. \"가서 보자꾸나. 그리고 만약 잘못된 소식이라면, 하늘이 우리를 가엾이 여겨 주시기를!\"\n\n그들 모두 밖으로 나갔고, 계단에서 서재로 올라오기를 두려워하던 모렐 부인과 마주쳤다. 이내 그들은 카느비에르 거리에 다다랐다. 부두에는 군중이 몰려 있었다. 모든 군중이 모렐 앞에서 길을 비켜 주었다. \"파라옹호다! 파라옹호!\" 사람마다 외쳤다.\n\n그리고 놀랍게도, 생장 요새 앞에 배 한 척이 있었다. 그 고물에는 흰 글씨로 이런 글귀가 적혀 있었다. \"파라옹호, 마르세유의 모렐 부자 상회.\" 그것은 또 다른 파라옹호와 똑같이 빼닮은 배였고, 예전의 그 배가 그랬듯 연지벌레와 쪽으로 짐을 가득 싣고 있었다. 배는 닻을 내리고 돛을 거두어 올렸으며, 갑판에서는 고마르 선장이 명령을 내리고 있었고, 마음씨 좋은 늙은 페늘롱이 모렐 씨에게 신호를 보내고 있었다. 더 의심할 수는 없었다. 두 눈으로 보는 증거가 있었고, 만 명에 이르는 사람들이 그 증거를 뒷받침하러 모여들었다.\n\n모렐과 그의 아들이 부두 끝에서, 이 광경을 지켜보는 온 도시 사람들 앞에서, 그 갈채 속에 서로를 끌어안고 있을 때, 검은 수염으로 얼굴을 반쯤 가린 한 남자가 초소 뒤에 몸을 숨긴 채 흐뭇하게 그 광경을 지켜보며 낮은 목소리로 이렇게 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"행복하시오, 고결한 분이여. 당신이 베푼, 그리고 앞으로 베풀 모든 선행으로 복받으시오. 그리고 내 감사하는 마음은 당신의 선행처럼 어둠 속에 묻어 두겠소.\"\n\n그러고는 더없는 만족을 담은 미소를 지으며 숨어 있던 곳을 떠났다. 그는 아무에게도 눈에 띄지 않고 하선용으로 마련된 계단 한쪽을 내려가, 세 번 소리쳐 불렀다. \"자코포, 자코포, 자코포!\"\n\n그러자 거룻배 한 척이 물가로 다가와 그를 태우고는, 호화롭게 꾸민 요트로 데려갔다. 그는 뱃사람다운 날렵함으로 그 갑판에 뛰어올랐다. 그러고는 다시 한번 모렐 쪽을 바라보았다. 모렐은 기쁨에 눈물을 흘리며 주위의 모든 사람과 더없이 다정하게 악수를 나누고 있었고, 눈길로는 하늘에서 찾는 듯한 그 미지의 은인에게 감사를 보내고 있었다.\n\n\"이제,\" 그 미지의 사내가 말했다. \"친절이여, 인정이여, 감사하는 마음이여, 잘 있거라! 마음을 부풀게 하는 그 모든 감정이여, 잘 있거라! 나는 선한 자에게 보답하는 하늘의 대리인이었다. 이제는 복수의 신이 악한 자를 벌하는 그 권능을 내게 넘겨주는 것이다!\"\n\n이 말과 함께 그가 신호를 보내자, 마치 이 신호만을 기다리고 있었던 듯 요트가 곧장 바다로 나아갔다.\n\n# Chapter 31. Italy: Sinbad the Sailor\n\n1838년 초, 파리 상류 사회에 속한 두 젊은이가 피렌체에 있었다. 모르세르 자작 알베르와 남작 프란츠 데피네였다. 두 사람은 그해 로마에서 카니발을 함께 보기로 약속했고, 지난 서너 해 동안 이탈리아에서 지내 온 프란츠가 알베르의 안내자 노릇을 맡기로 했다.\n\n로마에서 카니발을 보낸다는 것은 결코 만만한 일이 아니었다. 더구나 포폴로 광장이나 캄포 바치노에서 노숙할 마음이 없다면 더욱 그랬다. 그래서 두 사람은 스페인 광장에 있는 런던 호텔의 주인 파스트리니 씨에게 편지를 보내 안락한 방을 예약해 달라고 부탁했다. 파스트리니 씨는 4층에 방 두 개와 거실 하나가 있을 뿐이라고 답하며, 하루 1루이라는 싼값에 그것을 내주겠다고 했다. 두 사람은 그 제안을 받아들였다. 그러나 남은 시간을 알차게 쓰고 싶었던 알베르는 나폴리로 떠났다. 프란츠는 피렌체에 남아, 카시네의 낙원을 거니는 데 며칠을 보내고 피렌체 귀족들의 저택에서 두세 차례 저녁을 보낸 뒤, 문득 한 가지 생각에 사로잡혔다. 이미 보나파르트의 요람인 코르시카를 다녀온 그는, 나폴레옹이 기다리며 지냈던 곳인 엘바섬을 가 보고 싶어진 것이었다.\n\n어느 날 저녁, 그는 리보르노 부두의 쇠고리에 매여 있던 돛단배의 밧줄을 풀어내고는, 외투로 몸을 감싼 채 누워 선원들에게 말했다. \"엘바섬으로!\"\n\n배는 새처럼 항구를 빠져나갔고, 이튿날 아침 프란츠는 포르토페라요에 내렸다. 그는 그 거인이 발자취를 남긴 흔적을 따라 섬을 가로지른 뒤, 다시 배에 올라 마르치아나로 향했다.\n\n두 시간 뒤 그는 다시 피아노사에 내렸다. 그곳에는 붉은자고새가 득시글하다는 말을 들었던 것이다. 그러나 사냥은 신통치 않았다. 프란츠는 자고새 몇 마리를 잡는 데 그쳤고, 사냥에 실패한 사람이 으레 그렇듯 잔뜩 언짢은 얼굴로 배로 돌아왔다.\n\n\"아, 나리께서 마음만 있으시다면,\" 선장이 말했다. \"굉장한 사냥을 즐기실 수 있습니다요.\"\n\n\"어디서?\"\n\n\"저 섬이 보이십니까?\" 선장이 쪽빛 바다 위로 솟아오른 원뿔 모양의 돌무더기를 가리키며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"저 섬이 뭔데?\"\n\n\"몬테크리스토섬입니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 난 저 섬에서 사냥할 허가가 없네.\"\n\n\"나리껜 허가가 필요 없습니다. 저 섬에는 사람이 살지 않으니까요.\"\n\n\"아, 그래!\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"지중해 한복판의 무인도라니, 그것참 구경거리겠군.\"\n\n\"당연한 일입니다요. 저 섬은 바위투성이라, 농사지을 만한 땅이라곤 한 뙈기도 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"저 섬은 누구 소유인가?\"\n\n\"토스카나의 것입니다.\"\n\n\"거기서 무슨 사냥감을 잡을 수 있겠나?\"\n\n\"수천 마리의 들염소를 잡으실 수 있습니다요.\"\n\n\"돌이나 뜯어 먹고 사는 모양이지.\" 프란츠가 못 믿겠다는 듯 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"아닙니다요, 바위틈에서 자라난 덤불과 나무를 뜯어 먹고 삽니다.\"\n\n\"잠은 어디서 자나?\"\n\n\"뭍의 동굴에서 주무시거나, 배 위에서 외투를 덮고 주무시면 됩니다. 게다가 나리만 좋으시다면 언제든 떠날 수 있습니다요. 우린 밤에도 낮 못지않게 항해할 수 있고, 바람이 잦아들면 노를 저으면 되니까요.\"\n\n프란츠는 시간이 넉넉했고, 로마의 숙소도 아직 쓸 수 없는 형편이라 그 제안을 받아들였다. 그가 그러자고 대답하자, 선원들이 자기들끼리 낮은 목소리로 몇 마디를 주고받았다. \"왜 그러나?\" 그가 물었다. \"이번엔 또 뭔가? 무슨 곤란한 일이라도 있어?\"\n\n\"아닙니다.\" 선장이 대답했다. \"다만 나리께 미리 일러 두어야 할 게 있습니다. 저 섬은 오염된 항구라는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 말인가?\"\n\n\"몬테크리스토는 사람이 살지 않지만, 이따금 코르시카며 사르데냐며 아프리카에서 오는 밀수꾼들과 해적들의 피난처 노릇을 합니다요. 우리가 거기 다녀왔다는 게 알려지면, 리보르노로 돌아와서 엿새 동안 검역을 받아야 합니다.\"\n\n\"이런! 그러면 얘기가 달라지는데. 엿새라니! 아니, 그건 전능하신 하느님께서 세상을 만드신 시간만큼이나 길지 않은가! 너무 길어, 너무 길다고.\"\n\n\"하지만 나리께서 몬테크리스토에 다녀왔다는 걸 누가 말하겠습니까요?\"\n\n\"오, 난 안 하지.\" 프란츠가 외쳤다.\n\n\"저희도 안 합니다요, 저희도요.\" 선원들이 입을 모았다.\n\n\"그럼 몬테크리스토로 키를 잡게.\"\n\n선장이 명령을 내리자 키가 돌아갔고, 배는 이내 섬을 향해 나아갔다. 프란츠는 만사가 정돈되기를 기다렸다. 돛이 바람을 머금고, 선원 넷이 제자리를 잡자 — 셋은 뱃머리에, 하나는 키에 — 그는 다시 이야기를 꺼냈다. \"가에타노,\" 그가 선장에게 말했다. \"몬테크리스토가 해적들의 피난처라고 했는데, 그자들은 내가 보기에 염소와는 영 다른 종류의 사냥감 같은데.\"\n\n\"네, 나리, 그렇습니다요.\"\n\n\"밀수꾼이 있다는 건 알았지만, 알제가 함락되고 섭정 정권이 무너진 뒤로 해적이란 건 쿠퍼나 매리엇 선장의 소설 속에나 있는 줄 알았네.\"\n\n\"나리께서 잘못 아신 겁니다요. 해적은 있습니다. 교황 레오 12세께서 진작에 소탕했다고들 믿는 산적들도 여태 날마다 로마 성문 앞에서 여행자들을 털고 있지요. 반년 전에 프랑스 대리공사가 벨레트리에서 오백 걸음도 안 되는 데서 강도를 당했다는 얘기, 나리께선 못 들으셨습니까요?\"\n\n\"오, 그래, 그 얘긴 들었지.\"\n\n\"음, 그렇다면, 나리께서 저희처럼 리보르노에 사셨다면, 이따금 이런 소문을 들으셨을 겁니다요. 바스티아나 포르토페라요, 치비타베키아에 도착하기로 되어 있던 작은 상선이나 영국 요트가 도착하지 않았다, 아무도 그 배가 어찌 됐는지 모른다, 하지만 분명 암초에 부딪혀 가라앉았을 거다, 하고요. 그런데 그 배가 부딪힌 암초란 게 바로 여섯이나 여덟 사람이 탄 길고 좁다란 배였던 겁니다. 그자들이 깜깜하고 폭풍우 치는 밤에, 인적 없고 음산한 섬 근처에서 그 배를 덮쳐 약탈한 거지요. 마치 산적들이 숲속 깊은 곳에서 마차를 터는 것처럼요.\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 외투로 몸을 감싼 채 배 바닥에 누운 프란츠가 물었다. \"약탈당한 자들은 왜 프랑스나 사르데냐, 토스카나 정부에 하소연하지 않는 건가?\"\n\n\"왜냐고요?\" 가에타노가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 왜?\"\n\n\"왜냐하면, 우선 그자들은 배에서 값나가 보이는 건 죄다 자기네 배로 옮깁니다. 그런 다음 선원들의 손발을 묶고, 저마다 목에 24파운드짜리 포탄을 매답니다. 그리고 배 밑바닥에 큰 구멍을 뚫고는 떠나 버리지요. 십 분쯤 지나면 배가 심하게 흔들리며 가라앉기 시작합니다. 먼저 한쪽 뱃전이 물에 잠기고, 그다음 반대쪽이 잠기지요. 그러다 다시 솟구쳤다 가라앉기를 반복하더니, 양쪽이 한꺼번에 물에 잠깁니다. 그러다 별안간 대포 소리 같은 굉음이 납니다 — 그건 갑판을 뚫고 터져 나오는 공기 소리지요. 곧 배수구로 고래가 물을 뿜듯 물이 쏟아져 나오고, 배는 마지막 신음을 토하며 빙글빙글 돌다가 바다 위에 거대한 소용돌이를 남기고 사라집니다. 그러면 모든 게 끝난 겁니다. 그렇게 오 분이면, 바다 밑바닥에 가라앉은 그 배를 볼 수 있는 건 하느님의 눈밖에 없게 되지요. 이제 아시겠습니까,\" 선장이 말했다. \"왜 정부에 하소연 따위가 없고, 왜 그 배가 끝내 항구에 닿지 못하는지를요?\"\n\n가에타노가 이 이야기를 원정을 제안하기 전에 했더라면, 프란츠는 아마 망설였을 것이다. 그러나 이미 출발한 마당에, 그는 이제 와서 물러서는 것은 비겁한 일이라고 여겼다. 그는 무모하게 위험을 자초하는 사람은 아니었지만, 위험이 닥치면 흔들림 없는 냉정함으로 맞서는 부류의 사람이었다. 차분하고 결단력 있는 그는, 어떤 위험이든 결투의 상대를 다루듯 다루었다. 위험이 다가올 법한 방식을 가늠하고, 물러서더라도 그것은 비겁함이 아니라 전략의 일환으로 물러섰으며, 공격할 빈틈을 재빨리 포착해 단번에 찔러 승리를 거두는 사람이었다.\n\n\"흥!\" 그가 말했다. \"난 시칠리아며 칼라브리아를 두루 다녔고, 다도해에서 두 달을 항해했지만, 산적이나 해적의 그림자조차 본 적이 없네.\"\n\n\"제가 이런 얘기를 한 건 나리의 계획을 단념시키려는 게 아닙니다요.\" 가에타노가 대답했다. \"나리께서 물으시기에 답한 것뿐입니다, 그게 다입니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 자네 얘긴 무척 흥미롭군. 그러니 가능한 한 오래 그 얘길 즐기고 싶으니, 몬테크리스토로 키를 잡게.\"\n\n바람이 거세게 불어 배는 시속 6노트에서 7노트로 나아갔고, 두 사람은 빠르게 항해의 끝에 다다르고 있었다. 가까이 다가가자 섬은 바다에서 솟아오르는 듯 보였고, 공기가 어찌나 맑은지 무기고에 쌓인 포탄처럼 차곡차곡 포개진 바위들과, 그 틈새에서 자라난 푸른 덤불과 나무까지 벌써 또렷이 분간할 수 있었다. 선원들로 말하자면, 겉으로는 더없이 태평해 보였지만, 그들이 바짝 경계하고 있다는 것은 분명했다. 그들은 흰 돛을 단 고깃배 몇 척만이 드문드문 보이는, 거울처럼 잔잔한 수면을 조심스레 살피고 있었다.\n\n몬테크리스토까지 15마일쯤 남았을 때, 해가 코르시카 너머로 지기 시작했다. 코르시카의 산들은 하늘을 배경으로 거친 봉우리를 또렷이 드러내며 솟아 있었다. 거인 아다마스토르 같은 그 바위 덩어리가 정면으로 우뚝 솟아 무시무시한 장벽을 이루었고, 그 육중한 봉우리를 황금빛으로 물들이던 햇빛을 가로막은 탓에 항해자들은 그늘 속에 들었다. 그늘은 조금씩 높이 차올라, 마치 저물어 가는 하루의 마지막 햇살을 앞으로 몰아내는 듯했다. 마침내 그 반사광이 산꼭대기에 머물러, 화산의 불타는 봉우리처럼 한순간 멈춰 섰다가, 어둠이 산기슭을 뒤덮었듯 차츰 꼭대기를 뒤덮었다. 이제 섬은 점점 더 어두워져 가는 잿빛 산으로만 보일 뿐이었고, 반 시간 뒤에는 밤이 완전히 깜깜해졌다.\n\n다행히 뱃사람들은 이 위도에 익숙했고, 토스카나 다도해의 바위란 바위는 죄다 꿰고 있었다. 이 어둠 속에서 프란츠는 불안하지 않을 수 없었다. 코르시카는 이미 사라진 지 오래였고, 몬테크리스토 자체도 보이지 않았다. 그러나 선원들은 스라소니처럼 어둠 속을 꿰뚫어 보는 듯했고, 키를 잡은 도선사는 한 치의 망설임도 보이지 않았다.\n\n해가 진 지 한 시간이 지났을 무렵, 프란츠는 왼편으로 사분의 일 마일쯤 떨어진 곳에 검은 덩어리가 보이는 것 같았다. 그러나 그게 정확히 무엇인지 분간할 수 없었고, 떠다니는 구름을 뭍으로 잘못 본 것이라며 선원들의 웃음거리가 될까 두려워 잠자코 있었다. 그때 별안간 물가에 커다란 불빛이 나타났다. 뭍이라면 구름으로 보일 수도 있겠지만, 불은 유성이 아니었다.\n\n\"저 불빛은 뭔가?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"쉿!\" 선장이 말했다. \"불입니다요.\"\n\n\"하지만 자넨 저 섬에 사람이 안 산다고 하지 않았나?\"\n\n\"고정된 거처가 없다고 했지요. 하지만 이따금 밀수꾼들의 항구 노릇을 한다고도 말씀드렸습니다.\"\n\"해적들의 항구 노릇도 한다고 했지?\"\n\n\"해적들의 항구 노릇도 합니다요.\" 가에타노가 프란츠의 말을 되받아 말했다. \"그래서 제가 섬을 지나쳐 가라고 명령한 겁니다. 보시다시피 불이 우리 뒤편에 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"하지만 저 불 말일세.\" 프란츠가 말을 이었다. \"내가 보기엔 오히려 안심이 되는데. 들키고 싶지 않은 자들이라면 불을 피우지 않았을 테니까.\"\n\n\"오, 그건 아무것도 아닙니다요.\" 가에타노가 말했다. \"어둠 속에서도 섬의 위치를 헤아릴 수 있으시다면, 저 불이 옆쪽에서나 피아노사에서는 보이지 않고, 오직 바다에서만 보인다는 걸 아실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그러니까 자네 생각엔, 저 불이 달갑잖은 이웃이 있다는 표시란 말인가?\"\n\n\"그건 알아봐야지요.\" 가에타노가 그 지상의 별에 눈길을 박은 채 대답했다.\n\n\"어떻게 알아본단 말인가?\"\n\n\"보시면 압니다요.\"\n\n가에타노가 동료들과 의논하더니, 오 분쯤 이야기를 나눈 끝에 배를 돌리는 조작이 이루어졌다. 그들은 왔던 길을 되짚어 갔고, 몇 분 만에 불은 솟아오른 지형에 가려 보이지 않게 되었다. 도선사가 다시 배의 항로를 바꾸자 배는 빠르게 섬으로 다가가, 이내 오십 걸음 거리에 이르렀다. 가에타노가 돛을 내리자 배가 멈춰 섰다. 이 모든 일이 침묵 속에 이루어졌고, 항로를 바꾼 순간부터는 한마디 말도 오가지 않았다.\n\n원정을 제안한 가에타노는 모든 책임을 자기 어깨에 짊어졌다. 선원 넷은 노를 꺼내 들고 언제든 저어 달아날 채비를 한 채 그에게 눈을 박았다. 어둠 덕분에 달아나기란 어렵지 않을 터였다. 프란츠로 말하자면, 더없이 침착하게 자신의 무기를 점검했다. 그에게는 쌍발총 두 자루와 소총 한 자루가 있었다. 그는 총에 탄을 재고 점화약을 살핀 뒤 조용히 기다렸다.\n\n그동안 선장은 조끼와 셔츠를 벗어 던지고 바지를 허리에 단단히 동여맸다. 그의 발은 맨발이었으니 벗어야 할 신발이나 양말도 없었다. 이런 채비를 마친 그는 손가락을 입술에 대고는 소리 없이 바닷속으로 미끄러져 들어가, 작은 소리 하나 들리지 않을 만큼 조심스럽게 물가를 향해 헤엄쳐 갔다. 그를 좇을 수 있는 것이라곤 그가 지나간 자리에 남은 인광의 선뿐이었다. 그 자취마저 곧 사라졌다. 그가 물가에 닿은 게 분명했다.\n\n배에 탄 모두가 반 시간 동안 꼼짝 않고 있었다. 그러자 같은 빛의 자취가 다시 보였고, 헤엄치던 자는 이내 배 위로 돌아왔다.\n\n\"어땠나?\" 프란츠와 선원들이 한목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"스페인 밀수꾼들입니다요.\" 그가 말했다. \"코르시카 산적 둘을 데리고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그 코르시카 산적들이 스페인 밀수꾼들과 함께 여기서 뭘 하고 있나?\"\n\n\"아, 그게 말입니다.\" 선장이 더없이 깊은 연민이 어린 어조로 대답했다. \"사람이란 늘 서로 도와야 하는 법이지요. 산적들은 헌병이나 카르비니에리에게 바짝 쫓기는 일이 흔합니다. 그러다 배 한 척이 눈에 띄고, 그 위에 저희 같은 좋은 사람들이 타고 있으면 다가와 잠자리를 청하지요. 쫓기는 가엾은 놈을 어찌 안 도와줍니까. 받아 주는 거지요. 그리고 더 안전하라고 먼바다로 나갑니다. 이건 저희한테 손해 볼 것도 없고, 한 동포의 목숨이나 적어도 자유를 구해 주는 일입니다. 그러면 그자도 첫 기회가 오면, 우리가 방해받지 않고 물건을 부릴 만한 안전한 곳을 일러 주며 그 은혜를 갚지요.\"\n\n\"아!\" 프란츠가 말했다. \"그럼 자네도 이따금 밀수꾼 노릇을 하는 거로군, 가에타노?\"\n\n\"나리, 사람은 어떻게든 먹고살아야 하지 않습니까요.\" 그 사내가 속내를 알 수 없는 미소를 지으며 대꾸했다.\n\n\"그럼 지금 몬테크리스토에 있는 자들을 자네가 안다는 말인가?\"\n\n\"오, 그럼요, 저희 뱃사람들은 프리메이슨 같은 거라, 서로 신호로 알아봅니다요.\"\n\n\"그럼 우리가 뭍에 내려도 두려워할 게 없다고 보나?\"\n\n\"전혀 없습니다요. 밀수꾼은 도둑이 아니니까요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그 코르시카 산적 둘은?\" 프란츠가 위험의 확률을 가늠하며 말했다.\n\n\"그자들이 산적이 된 건 그자들 탓이 아니라, 관청 탓입니다요.\"\n\"어째서 그런가?\"\n\n\"한 놈 해치웠다고 쫓기는 거니까요. 마치 코르시카 사람의 천성에 복수가 없기라도 한 것처럼 말입니다.\"\n\n\"한 놈 해치웠다는 게 무슨 말인가? 사람을 죽였단 말인가?\" 프란츠가 캐묻기를 이어 갔다.\n\n\"적을 하나 죽였다는 겁니다요. 그건 전혀 다른 일이지요.\" 선장이 대답했다.\n\n\"좋아.\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"그 밀수꾼들과 산적들에게 잠자리를 청해 보세. 그자들이 응할 것 같나?\"\n\n\"틀림없이 응할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"몇 명이나 되나?\"\n\n\"넷에다 산적 둘을 더하면 여섯입니다요.\"\n\n\"마침 우리 수와 같군. 그러니 그자들이 말썽을 부린다면 우리도 충분히 제압할 수 있어. 그러니 마지막으로 다시 한번, 몬테크리스토로 키를 잡게.\"\n\n\"네, 다만 나리께서 저희가 마땅한 경계를 다 갖추도록 허락해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"얼마든지. 네스토르처럼 슬기롭고 율리시스처럼 신중하게 하게. 난 허락하는 정도가 아니라, 그러기를 권하는 바일세.\"\n\n\"그럼 조용히!\" 가에타노가 말했다.\n\n모두가 따랐다. 프란츠처럼 자신의 처지를 똑바로 바라보는 사람에게는, 그것은 심각한 상황이었다. 그는 알지도 못하는, 자기에게 충실할 까닭이라곤 없는 선원들과 어둠 속에 단둘이 있었다. 그자들은 그의 허리띠에 수천 프랑이 들어 있다는 것을 알았고, 그의 무기를 — 매우 아름다운 무기를 — 부러움까지는 아니더라도 적어도 호기심 어린 눈으로 여러 번 살펴본 자들이었다. 한편 그는 이 사내들 말고는 아무런 호위도 없이 한 섬에 내리려는 참이었다. 그 섬은 과연 더없이 경건한 이름을 지녔으나, 밀수꾼과 산적들 덕분에 프란츠에게 그리 후한 대접을 베풀 것 같지는 않았다. 낮에는 도무지 믿기지 않던 침몰선 이야기가 밤이 되자 무척 그럴듯하게 여겨졌다. 두 가지 위험의 가능성 사이에 놓인 그는, 선원들에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 총을 손에 쥐고 있었다.\n\n선원들이 다시 돛을 올리자 배는 또 한 번 물결을 갈랐다. 어둠에 어느새 눈이 익은 프란츠는, 배가 따라 항해하는 어슴푸레한 물가를 볼 수 있었다. 그러다 바위 곶을 돌아 나오자, 그는 그 어느 때보다 환히 타오르는 불과 그 주위에 둘러앉은 대여섯 사람을 보았다. 불길이 사방 백 걸음의 바다를 밝히고 있었다. 가에타노는 배를 줄곧 그늘 속에 두며 조심스럽게 불빛을 비껴 갔다. 그러다 불과 정면으로 마주한 지점에서, 그는 뱃노래를 부르며 그 원의 한가운데로 키를 틀었다. 노래의 후렴은 동료들이 받아 불렀다.\n\n노래의 첫 소절이 들리자 불가에 둘러앉아 있던 사내들이 일어나 상륙 지점으로 다가왔다. 그들은 배에 눈을 박은 채, 새로 온 자들이 누구이며 무슨 속셈인지 알아내려는 기색이 역력했다. 곧 그들은 만족한 듯 (한 사람만 물가에 남고) 불가로 돌아갔다. 불 위에서는 염소 한 마리가 통째로 구워지고 있었다. 배가 물가에서 스무 걸음 거리에 이르자, 카빈총을 든 물가의 사내가 보초처럼 받들어총 자세를 취하며 사르데냐 말로 외쳤다. \"누구냐?\"\n\n프란츠는 침착하게 두 총신의 공이치기를 당겼다. 그러자 가에타노가 그 사내와 몇 마디를 주고받았다. 여행자는 그 말을 알아듣지 못했으나, 분명 자기에 관한 이야기였다.\n\n\"나리, 성함을 밝히시겠습니까, 아니면 익명으로 남으시겠습니까?\" 선장이 물었다.\n\n\"내 이름은 알리지 말게.\" 프란츠가 대답했다. \"그저 즐기러 여행하는 프랑스 사람이라고만 하게.\"\n\n가에타노가 이 대답을 전하자, 보초는 불가에 둘러앉은 자들 중 하나에게 명령을 내렸다. 그 사람은 일어나 바위 사이로 사라졌다. 아무도 말을 하지 않았고, 저마다 제 일에 몰두한 듯했다. 프란츠는 상륙 채비에, 선원들은 돛에, 밀수꾼들은 염소에. 그러나 이 태연함 속에서도, 그들이 서로를 살피고 있다는 것은 분명했다.\n\n사라졌던 사내가 떠날 때와는 반대편에서 별안간 돌아왔다. 그가 보초에게 머리로 신호를 보내자, 보초는 배 쪽으로 돌아서서 말했다. \"사코모디.\" 이탈리아어 사코모디는 번역할 수 없는 말이다. 그것은 \"오시오, 들어오시오, 환영하오, 편히 계시오, 당신이 주인이오\"라는 뜻을 한꺼번에 담고 있다. 그것은 몰리에르의 작품에 나오는, 그 한마디에 담긴 숱한 의미로 서민 출신 귀족을 어리둥절하게 했던 터키어 문구와도 같다.\n\n선원들은 두 번 청할 것도 없이, 노를 네 번 저어 뭍에 닿았다. 가에타노가 물가로 뛰어내려 보초와 몇 마디를 나눈 뒤 동료들이 내렸고, 마지막으로 프란츠가 내렸다. 그의 총 한 자루는 어깨에 메여 있었고, 다른 한 자루는 가에타노가, 소총은 한 선원이 들고 있었다. 반은 예술가풍, 반은 멋쟁이풍인 그의 옷차림은 아무런 의심도, 따라서 아무런 불안도 자아내지 않았다. 배를 물가에 매어 놓고 그들은 편안한 야영지를 찾아 몇 걸음 나아갔다. 그러나 그들이 고른 자리가 보초 노릇을 하던 밀수꾼의 마음에 들지 않았던 게 분명했다. 그가 외쳤기 때문이다.\n\n\"미안하지만, 그쪽은 안 됩니다.\"\n\n가에타노가 우물쭈물 변명을 늘어놓으며 반대편으로 나아갔고, 그동안 선원 둘이 불에서 횃불을 붙여 길을 밝혔다.\n\n그들은 서른 걸음쯤 나아가다가, 바위에 둘러싸인 작은 공터에서 멈춰 섰다. 바위에는 보초막과 다름없는 자리들이 깎여 있었다. 그 둘레의 바위틈에는 작달막한 떡갈나무 몇 그루와 무성한 도금양 덤불이 자라 있었다. 프란츠가 횃불을 낮춰 보니, 수북이 쌓인 재 더미로 미루어 이 은신처를 발견한 사람이 자기가 처음이 아니라는 것을 알 수 있었다. 이곳은 분명 몬테크리스토를 떠도는 방문객들의 쉼터 가운데 하나였다.\n\n의심으로 말하자면, 일단 단단한 땅을 밟고, 적대적이지는 않을지언정 무심한 주인들의 태도를 보고 나자, 그의 불안은 깡그리 사라졌다. 아니, 오히려 염소를 보자 식욕으로 바뀌어 버렸다. 그가 이를 가에타노에게 말하자, 가에타노는 배 안에 빵과 포도주, 자고새 여섯 마리와 그것을 구울 좋은 불이 있으니 저녁상을 차리는 것쯤은 더없이 쉬운 일이라고 대답했다.\n\n\"게다가,\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"저들이 굽는 고기 냄새에 군침이 도신다면, 제가 가서 우리 새 두 마리를 줄 테니 한 점 달라고 해 보겠습니다요.\"\n\n\"자넨 타고난 외교관이로군.\" 프란츠가 대꾸했다. \"가서 해 보게.\"\n\n그동안 선원들은 마른 나뭇가지와 잔가지를 모아 불을 피웠다. 프란츠는 구운 고기 냄새를 들이마시며 애가 타게 기다렸다. 그때 선장이 묘한 기색으로 돌아왔다.\n\n\"어떤가,\" 프란츠가 말했다. \"뭐 새로운 소식이라도? 거절하던가?\"\n\n\"오히려 그 반대입니다요.\" 가에타노가 대답했다. \"젊은 프랑스 분이라는 얘기를 들은 두목이, 나리께 함께 저녁을 들자고 청합니다.\"\n\n\"음,\" 프란츠가 말했다. \"그 두목, 무척 예의 바르군. 안 될 것 없지. 더구나 내가 저녁상에 내 몫을 보태는 셈이니까.\"\n\n\"오, 그게 아닙니다요. 저녁거리는 그분께 넘치도록 있습니다. 다만 나리를 댁으로 들이기 전에 한 가지 조건을, 그것도 좀 별난 조건을 내거셨습니다.\"\n\n\"댁이라니? 그럼 여기에 집이라도 지었단 말인가?\"\n\n\"아닙니다요. 그래도 무척 안락한 집이 있다고들 합니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 자넨 그 두목을 아는가?\"\n\n\"소문은 들었습니다요.\"\n\n\"좋은 소문인가, 나쁜 소문인가?\"\n\n\"둘 다입니다요.\"\n\"이런! 그래, 그 조건이란 게 뭔가?\"\n\n\"눈을 가리시고, 그분이 직접 풀라고 하시기 전까지는 그 붕대를 벗지 않으셔야 한다는 겁니다.\"\n\n프란츠는 가에타노가 이 제안을 어떻게 생각하는지 알아내려고 그를 바라보았다. \"아,\" 프란츠의 속내를 짐작한 가에타노가 대답했다. \"저도 이게 심상찮은 일이라는 건 압니다요.\"\n\n\"내 처지라면 자넨 어떻게 하겠나?\"\n\n\"잃을 게 없는 저라면, 가겠습니다요.\"\n\n\"받아들이겠다고?\"\n\n\"네, 호기심 때문에라도요.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 두목에게 뭔가 무척 별난 구석이 있다는 말인가?\"\n\n\"들어 보십시오.\" 가에타노가 목소리를 낮추며 말했다. \"그게 사실인지는 저도 모릅니다만……\" 그가 누가 가까이 있는지 살피려고 말을 멈췄다.\n\n\"뭐라고들 하던가?\"\n\n\"그 두목이 동굴에 산다는데, 피티 궁전도 거기 비하면 아무것도 아니랍니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 헛소리를!\" 프란츠가 다시 자리에 앉으며 말했다.\n\n\"헛소리가 아닙니다요, 정말입니다. 생페르디낭호의 도선사 카마가 한번 들어가 봤는데, 그런 보물은 동화 속에서나 들을 법한 것이더라며 넋이 나가서 돌아왔습니다요.\"\n\n\"이보게,\" 프란츠가 말했다. \"그런 이야기를 들으니 알리바바의 마법 동굴이 떠오르는군.\"\n\n\"저야 들은 대로 말씀드리는 겁니다요.\"\n\n\"그럼 받아들이라고 권하는 건가?\"\n\n\"오, 그렇게는 말씀 못 드리지요. 나리 좋으실 대로 하십시오. 이 일에 관해서는 권해 드리기가 송구합니다요.\"\n\n프란츠는 잠시 그 일을 곰곰이 따져 보았다. 그러고는 그토록 부유한 사람이 자기 같은 사람의 보잘것없는 재물을 약탈할 마음이야 없으리라 결론짓고, 또 그저 잘 차린 저녁상만 떠올리며 받아들이기로 했다. 가에타노가 그 대답을 가지고 떠났다. 신중한 프란츠는 주인에 관해 알아낼 수 있는 모든 것을 알아내고 싶었다. 그는 한 선원에게로 몸을 돌렸다. 그 선원은 이 대화가 오가는 동안, 제 일에 자부심을 느끼는 사람 특유의 태도로 진지하게 자고새 털을 뽑으며 앉아 있었다. 프란츠는 그에게 배라곤 어디에도 보이지 않는데 그자들이 어떻게 이 섬에 내렸는지 물었다.\n\n\"그건 신경 쓰지 마십시오.\" 선원이 대꾸했다. \"저는 그자들 배를 압니다요.\"\n\n\"무척 아름다운 배인가?\"\n\n\"세계 일주를 한다 해도 그보다 나은 배는 바라지 않을 겁니다요.\"\n\n\"적재량은 얼마나 되나?\"\n\n\"백 톤쯤 됩니다요. 하지만 어떤 날씨에도 끄떡없게 지은 배지요. 영국 사람들이 요트라고 부르는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"어디서 지은 배인가?\"\n\n\"모르겠습니다요. 하지만 제 생각엔 제노바산 같습니다.\"\n\n\"그런데 어떻게 밀수꾼 두목이,\" 프란츠가 말을 이었다. \"그런 용도의 배를 감히 제노바에서 짓는단 말인가?\"\n\n\"제가 언제 그 주인이 밀수꾼이라고 했습니까요.\" 선원이 대답했다.\n\n\"아니, 가에타노가 그랬던 것 같은데.\"\n\n\"가에타노는 그 배를 멀리서 봤을 뿐, 그때는 아무하고도 말을 안 나눴습니다요.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 사람이 밀수꾼이 아니라면, 누구란 말인가?\"\n\n\"즐기러 여행 다니는 부유한 양반이지요.\"\n\n'이거,' 프란츠는 생각했다. '두 사람 얘기가 안 맞으니, 더욱 수상쩍군.'\n\n\"그 사람 이름이 뭔가?\"\n\n\"물어보시면 신드바드라고 하지만, 그게 진짜 이름인지는 모르겠습니다요.\"\n\n\"뱃사람 신드바드?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"바다에서 삽니다요.\"\n\n\"어느 나라 사람인가?\"\n\n\"모릅니다요.\"\n\n\"본 적은 있나?\"\n\n\"이따금요.\"\n\n\"어떤 사람인가?\"\n\n\"나리께서 직접 판단하실 겁니다요.\"\n\n\"날 어디서 맞아 줄 텐가?\"\n\n\"틀림없이 가에타노가 말씀드린 그 지하 궁전에서겠지요.\"\n\n\"자넨 뭍에 내려 이 섬이 텅 비어 있는 걸 보고도, 그 마법 궁전을 찾아보고 싶은 호기심이 든 적이 한 번도 없었나?\"\n\n\"오, 있고말고요, 한두 번이 아닙니다요. 하지만 늘 헛수고였지요. 동굴을 샅샅이 살펴봤지만, 입구의 흔적이라곤 조금도 찾을 수 없었습니다. 그 문은 열쇠가 아니라 주문으로 열린다고들 합니다요.\"\n\n\"이건 영락없이,\" 프란츠가 중얼거렸다. \"천일야화의 모험이로군.\"\n\n\"나리께서 기다리십니다.\" 한 목소리가 말했다. 프란츠는 그것이 보초의 목소리임을 알아챘다. 그는 요트 선원 둘과 함께였다.\n\n프란츠는 주머니에서 손수건을 꺼내 자기에게 말을 건 사내에게 건넸다. 그들은 한마디 말도 없이 그의 눈을 가렸는데, 그가 무슨 경솔한 짓이라도 할까 봐 염려하는 기색이 역력한 정성스러운 손길이었다. 그러고는 그에게 붕대를 들춰 보려는 시도를 조금도 하지 않겠다는 약속을 받아 냈다. 그는 약속했다.\n\n이윽고 두 안내인이 그의 양팔을 붙잡았고, 그는 보초를 앞세우고 그들에게 이끌려 걸어갔다. 서른 걸음쯤 가자 구워지는 새끼 염소의 군침 도는 냄새가 풍겨, 그는 자기가 야영지를 지나고 있음을 알았다. 그런 다음 그들은 다시 오십 걸음쯤 더 그를 이끌고 갔는데, 분명 그들이 가에타노에게는 가지 못하게 막았던 그 물가 쪽으로 나아가고 있었다. 이제야 그는 그 거절의 까닭을 헤아릴 수 있었다.\n\n이윽고 공기의 변화로, 그는 자기가 동굴에 들어서고 있음을 알았다. 몇 초 더 나아가자 무언가 타닥거리는 소리가 들렸고, 공기가 다시 바뀌어 향긋하고 그윽해지는 것 같았다. 마침내 그의 발이 두껍고 부드러운 양탄자에 닿았고, 안내인들이 그를 놓아주었다. 한순간 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 외국 억양이 섞이긴 했으나 더없이 훌륭한 프랑스어로 한 목소리가 말했다.\n\n\"어서 오십시오, 선생. 부디 붕대를 벗으시지요.\"\n\n말할 것도 없이, 프란츠는 이 허락이 되풀이되기를 기다리지 않고 손수건을 벗었다. 그러자 서른여덟에서 마흔쯤 된 한 사내가 눈앞에 있었다. 사내는 튀니지풍 옷차림이었다. 다시 말해, 긴 푸른 비단 술이 달린 붉은 모자에, 금실 자수를 놓은 검은 천 조끼, 짙은 붉은색 통 넓은 바지, 조끼처럼 금실 자수를 놓은 같은 색의 크고 헐렁한 각반, 그리고 노란 슬리퍼를 신고 있었다. 허리에는 화려한 캐시미어 천을 둘렀고, 허리띠에는 작고 날카로운 곡선 모양의 단검이 꽂혀 있었다.\n\n거의 송장 같은 창백함을 띠긴 했으나, 이 사내의 얼굴은 놀랍도록 잘생긴 것이었다. 그의 눈은 꿰뚫는 듯 번득였고, 이마에서 곧장 뻗어 나온 반듯한 코는 순수한 그리스풍이었으며, 진주처럼 흰 이는 그것을 두른 검은 콧수염 덕분에 한층 돋보였다.\n\n그의 창백함은 어찌나 유별난지, 마치 오래 무덤에 묻혀 있어 살아 있는 자의 건강한 혈색과 윤기를 되찾을 수 없게 된 사람의 것 같았다. 그는 키가 특별히 크지는 않았으나 무척 균형 잡힌 몸매였고, 남부 사람들이 그렇듯 손발이 작았다. 그러나 가에타노의 묘사를 한낱 꾸며 낸 이야기로 여겼던 프란츠를 놀라게 한 것은, 자신이 들어선 방의 호화로움이었다.\n\n방 전체가 황금빛 꽃무늬를 넣어 짠 진홍색 비단으로 둘러져 있었다. 움푹 들어간 곳에는 일종의 긴 의자가 놓여 있었고, 그 위에는 은 칼집에 든 아라비아 검들을 꽂은 받침대가 있었는데, 손잡이마다 보석이 찬란히 빛났다. 천장에는 아름다운 모양과 빛깔의 베네치아 유리 등이 매달려 있었고, 발은 튀르키예산 양탄자에 푹 잠겨 발등까지 묻혔다. 프란츠가 들어온 문 앞에는 휘장이 드리워져 있었고, 또 다른 문 앞에도 마찬가지였다. 그 문은 휘황하게 밝혀진 듯한 두 번째 방으로 이어져 있었다.\n\n주인은 프란츠가 놀라움에서 헤어나도록 시간을 주었다. 게다가 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않고, 시선에는 시선으로 응했다.\n\n\"선생,\" 그가 잠시 뒤에 말했다. \"이리로 모시면서 취한 조심성에 대해 천 번이라도 양해를 구합니다. 하지만 일 년의 대부분 동안 이 섬은 텅 비어 있는지라, 만약 이 거처의 비밀이 새어 나간다면, 돌아왔을 때 제 임시 은신처가 엉망진창이 되어 있는 것을 보게 될 게 분명합니다. 그건 무척 곤혹스러운 일이지요. 그것이 제게 끼치는 손실 때문이 아니라, 제가 지금 누리고 있는 확신, 즉 내키는 대로 다른 모든 인간들로부터 떨어져 지낼 수 있다는 확신을 잃게 되기 때문입니다. 자, 이제 이 잠깐의 불쾌함을 잊으시도록 애써 보겠습니다. 그리고 선생께서 여기서 찾으리라곤 기대치 못하셨을 것을 대접해 드리지요. 다시 말해, 그럭저럭 먹을 만한 저녁상과 꽤 안락한 잠자리를 말입니다.\"\n\n\"맹세코, 선생,\" 프란츠가 대답했다. \"사과하실 것 없습니다. 마법 궁전에 들어서는 사람들의 눈을 가린다는 건, 저도 늘 보아 온 일입니다. 가령 「위그노 교도들」의 라울 같은 경우 말이지요. 정말이지 저는 불평할 게 조금도 없습니다. 지금 제 눈앞의 광경은 「천일야화」의 경이로움을 떠올리게 하니까요.\"\n\n\"아아! 루쿨루스의 말마따나, 선생의 방문이라는 영광을 미리 알았더라면 그에 걸맞은 채비를 해 두었을 것을. 하지만 이 보잘것없는 은둔처나마 선생께 내어 드리겠습니다. 이 변변찮은 저녁상이나마, 원하신다면 함께 드시지요. 알리, 저녁상은 다 차려졌느냐?\"\n\n이 순간 휘장이 옆으로 젖혀지더니, 흑단처럼 검고 수수한 흰 튜닉을 걸친 누비아인이 식당에 모든 준비가 끝났다는 신호를 주인에게 보냈다.\n\n\"자,\" 낯선 사내가 프란츠에게 말했다. \"선생도 같은 생각이신지 모르겠으나, 저는 두세 시간을 함께 있으면서도 서로를 무어라 부를지 이름도 호칭도 모르는 것만큼 거북한 일은 없다고 봅니다. 부디 알아주십시오, 저는 손님을 대접하는 법도를 너무도 존중하기에 선생의 성함이나 직함을 묻지 않겠습니다. 다만 제가 기꺼이 선생을 부를 수 있도록 호칭 하나만 일러 주시기를 청합니다. 제 쪽으로 말하자면, 선생을 편안케 해 드리고자 미리 말씀드리는데, 저는 흔히 '뱃사람 신드바드'라고 불립니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 저도,\" 프란츠가 대답했다. \"말씀드리지요. 저는 그 놀라운 램프만 있으면 영락없이 알라딘이 될 사람이니, 지금 이 순간 저를 알라딘이라 부르지 못할 까닭이 없겠군요. 그러면 어떤 선한 정령에게 실려 온 듯한 이 동방에서 벗어나지 않아도 될 테고요.\"\n\n\"좋습니다, 그럼 알라딘 나리,\" 이 별난 주인이 대답했다. \"식사 준비가 됐다는 말을 들으셨으니, 이제 식당으로 드시지요. 보잘것없는 종인 제가 앞장서서 길을 안내하겠습니다.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 신드바드는 휘장을 옆으로 젖히며 손님을 앞서 갔다. 프란츠는 이제 또 다른 마법의 광경을 바라보았다. 식탁은 휘황찬란하게 차려져 있었고, 이 중요한 점을 확인하고 나자 그는 사방을 둘러보았다. 식당은 방금 떠나온 방 못지않게 눈부셨다. 온통 대리석으로 되어 있었고, 값을 매길 수 없는 고풍스러운 부조들이 걸려 있었다. 직사각형인 이 방의 네 귀퉁이에는 손에 바구니를 든 웅장한 조각상 넷이 서 있었다. 그 바구니들에는 더없이 화려한 과일이 네 개의 피라미드처럼 담겨 있었다. 시칠리아산 파인애플, 말라가산 석류, 발레아레스제도산 오렌지, 프랑스산 복숭아, 그리고 튀니스산 대추야자였다.\n\n저녁상에는 코르시카산 검은지빠귀로 곁들인 꿩구이, 젤리를 얹은 멧돼지 다리살, 타르타르소스를 곁들인 새끼 염소 사분지 한 마리, 훌륭한 가자미 한 마리, 그리고 거대한 바닷가재 한 마리가 올랐다. 이 큰 요리들 사이에는 갖가지 별미를 담은 작은 접시들이 놓여 있었다. 그릇은 은제였고, 접시는 일본 도자기였다.\n\n프란츠는 이것이 꿈이 아닌지 확인하려고 눈을 비볐다. 식탁 시중은 알리 혼자서 들었는데, 어찌나 훌륭하게 해내는지 손님이 주인에게 그 점을 칭찬할 정도였다.\n\n\"네,\" 그가 더없이 편안하고 우아하게 저녁상을 대접하며 대답했다. \"네, 그는 제게 무척 헌신적인 가엾은 친구로, 그것을 증명하려고 할 수 있는 모든 일을 다 합니다. 제가 제 목숨을 구해 준 것을 기억하지요. 그리고 자기 머리를 소중히 여기는지라, 그것을 어깨 위에 붙어 있게 해 준 데 대해 제게 얼마간 고마움을 느끼는 겁니다.\"\n\n알리가 주인에게 다가가 그의 손을 잡고 입을 맞췄다.\n\n\"신드바드 나리,\" 프란츠가 말했다. \"이 친절에 얽힌 사연을 여쭤본다면 무례한 일이 될까요?\"\n\n\"오, 아주 간단한 이야기입니다.\" 주인이 대답했다. \"이 친구가 튀니스 태수의 후궁 근처를, 제 피부색을 가진 사람에게 허락된 예법보다 더 가까이 어슬렁거리다가 붙잡힌 모양입니다. 태수는 그에게 혀를 자르고 손과 머리를 자르라는 형을 내렸지요. 첫날은 혀, 이튿날은 손, 사흗날은 머리를 말입니다. 저는 늘 말 못 하는 하인을 두고 싶었던지라, 그의 혀가 잘리는 날을 알아내고는 태수를 찾아가 알리를 주는 대가로 태수가 무척 갖고 싶어 하던 줄 아는 훌륭한 쌍발총을 주겠다고 제안했지요. 태수는 잠시 망설였습니다. 그 가엾은 친구의 형벌을 끝까지 마치고 싶은 마음이 워낙 컸으니까요. 하지만 제가 태수의 언월도를 산산조각 낸 적이 있는 영국제 단검을 총에 얹어 주자, 태수는 마음을 누그러뜨려 손과 머리는 용서하기로 했습니다. 다만 그 가엾은 친구가 두 번 다시 튀니스에 발을 들이지 않는다는 조건이었지요. 사실 그건 거래에서 쓸데없는 조항이었습니다. 그 겁쟁이는 아프리카 해안이 어렴풋이 보이기만 해도 아래로 달려 내려가, 우리가 지구의 그 구역을 벗어나야만 겨우 다시 모습을 드러내니까요.\"\n\n프란츠는 잠시 말없이 생각에 잠겨, 주인이 이 짤막한 이야기를 들려준 그 반은 다정하고 반은 잔혹한 태도를 어떻게 받아들여야 할지 알 수 없었다.\n\n\"그리고 선생이 그 이름을 빌려 온 저 유명한 뱃사람처럼,\" 그가 화제를 바꾸려고 말했다. \"선생도 여행하며 생을 보내시는군요?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다. 언젠가 그것을 이룰 수 있으리라곤 거의 생각지도 못했던 시절에 저는 한 가지 맹세를 했지요.\" 낯선 사내가 묘한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"그리고 다른 몇 가지 맹세도 했는데, 때가 되면 이루어지기를 바라고 있습니다.\"\n\n신드바드는 이 말을 무척 차분하게 내뱉었으나, 그의 눈에서는 예사롭지 않은 사나움의 섬광이 번득였다.\n\n\"선생, 많이 고생하셨군요?\" 프란츠가 캐묻듯 말했다.\n\n신드바드가 움찔하더니 그를 뚫어지게 바라보며 대답했다. \"어찌 그리 생각하십니까?\"\n\n\"모든 게 그렇습니다.\" 프란츠가 대답했다. \"선생의 목소리, 눈빛, 창백한 안색, 게다가 선생이 보내는 삶까지도요.\"\n\n\"제가요? 저는 더없이 행복한 삶을, 진짜 파샤의 삶을 살고 있습니다. 저는 만물의 왕입니다. 한곳이 마음에 들면 거기 머물고, 싫증이 나면 떠나지요. 저는 새처럼 자유롭고, 새처럼 날개도 있습니다. 제 시중꾼들은 제 사소한 바람에도 복종합니다. 이따금 저는 산적이나 죄인을 법의 굴레에서 풀어 주며 즐기기도 하지요. 그리고 제게는 정의를 베푸는 저만의 방식이 있는데, 그것은 조용하고 확실하며, 유예도 항소도 없이 죄를 묻거나 사면하고, 아무도 그것을 보지 못합니다. 아, 선생도 제 삶을 맛보셨더라면, 다른 어떤 삶도 바라지 않으셨을 겁니다. 그리고 그곳에서 이뤄야 할 큰 계획이 있지 않은 한, 결코 세상으로 돌아가지 않으셨을 테고요.\"\n\n\"이를테면 복수 같은 것 말이지요!\" 프란츠가 한마디 했다.\n\n낯선 사내는 마음과 생각의 밑바닥까지 꿰뚫어 보는 듯한 그런 눈길 가운데 하나를 젊은이에게 박았다. \"한데 왜 복수입니까?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"왜냐하면,\" 프란츠가 대답했다. \"선생은 제게, 사회에 박해받아 사회와 무시무시한 셈을 치러야 하는 사람처럼 보이니까요.\"\n\n\"아!\" 신드바드가 흰 송곳니를 드러내며 그 특유의 미소로 웃으며 대꾸했다. \"잘못 짚으셨군요. 보시다시피 저는 일종의 철학자입니다. 그리고 언젠가는 어쩌면 파리로 가서 아페르 씨와, 그 작은 파란 외투를 걸친 사내와 겨뤄 볼지도 모르지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 그게 그 여행을 처음 하시는 게 됩니까?\"\n\n\"네, 그렇습니다. 선생께는 제가 도무지 호기심이 없는 사람처럼 보이겠지요. 하지만 그토록 오래 미뤄 온 게 제 탓이 아니라는 건 분명히 말씀드립니다. 언젠가는 이뤄질 일입니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 여행을 머지않아 떠나실 생각이십니까?\"\n\n\"모르겠습니다. 이런저런 조정들에 달린 상황에 따라 정해질 일이지요.\"\n\n\"선생께서 오실 때 저도 그곳에 있고 싶습니다. 몬테크리스토에서 베풀어 주신 후한 환대에, 제 힘닿는 데까지 보답하도록 애써 보겠습니다.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 선생의 제안을 받아들이고 싶군요.\" 주인이 대답했다. \"하지만 안타깝게도, 제가 그곳에 간다면 십중팔구 익명으로 갈 겁니다.\"\n\n저녁상은 오로지 프란츠를 위해 차려진 것처럼 보였다. 낯선 사내는 손님이 흠뻑 즐긴 그 호화로운 만찬에서 요리 한두 가지를 겨우 건드릴 뿐이었다. 이윽고 알리가 후식을 들여왔다. 아니, 차라리 조각상들의 손에서 바구니를 내려 식탁 위에 놓았다고 해야겠다. 그리고 두 바구니 사이에 은 뚜껑이 덮인 작은 은잔 하나를 놓았다. 알리가 이 잔을 식탁에 놓는 정성스러운 손길에 프란츠의 호기심이 일었다. 그가 뚜껑을 들춰 보니, 절인 안젤리카 비슷한 푸르스름한 반죽 같은 것이 들어 있었다. 그러나 그로서는 도무지 알 수 없는 것이었다. 그는 들여다보기 전과 마찬가지로 잔 속에 든 것이 무엇인지 모른 채 뚜껑을 다시 덮었다. 그러고는 주인 쪽으로 눈길을 던지니, 주인이 그의 실망스러운 표정을 보고 미소 짓고 있었다.\n\n\"맞혀 보실 수 없겠지요,\" 그가 말했다. \"저 작은 단지 안에 무엇이 들었는지 말입니다.\"\n\n\"네, 정말 모르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"음, 그렇다면, 저 초록빛 보존식이 바로 헤베가 유피테르의 식탁에서 시중들던 신들의 음식, 암브로시아랍니다.\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 프란츠가 대답했다. \"이 암브로시아라는 것도, 분명 인간의 손을 거치는 동안 그 천상의 이름을 잃고 인간의 이름을 얻었겠지요. 속된 말로, 이 혼합물을 뭐라고 부르십니까? 솔직히 말씀드리면, 그다지 끌리지는 않습니다만.\"\n\n\"아, 바로 그렇게 우리의 물질적인 뿌리가 드러나는 겁니다.\" 신드바드가 외쳤다. \"우리는 흔히 행복 곁을 그토록 가까이 지나치면서도 그것을 보지 못하고, 헤아리지 못합니다. 설령 보고 헤아린다 해도 알아보지 못하지요. 선생은 실속을 따지는 분이십니까, 황금이 선생의 신입니까? 이것을 맛보십시오, 그러면 페루와 구자라트와 골콘다의 광맥이 선생께 열립니다. 선생은 상상력의 사람, 시인이십니까? 이것을 맛보십시오, 그러면 가능성의 경계가 사라지고, 무한한 공간의 들판이 선생께 펼쳐집니다. 선생은 마음도 자유로이, 정신도 자유로이, 거침없는 몽상의 끝없는 영역으로 나아가게 되지요. 선생은 야심가이십니까, 이 땅의 위대함을 좇으십니까? 이것을 맛보십시오, 그러면 한 시간 안에 선생은 왕이 됩니다. 프랑스나 스페인, 영국처럼 유럽 한구석에 숨은 보잘것없는 왕국의 왕이 아니라, 세상의 왕, 우주의 왕, 만물의 왕이 되는 겁니다. 사탄의 발치에 굽실거리지 않고도, 선생은 이 땅의 모든 왕국의 왕이자 주인이 되는 거지요. 제가 선생께 권하는 것이 솔깃하지 않습니까? 게다가 이렇게 하기만 하면 되니, 쉬운 일이 아닙니까? 보십시오!\"\n\n이 말과 함께 그는 그토록 찬양해 마지않던 그 물질이 담긴 작은 잔의 뚜껑을 벗기고, 그 마법의 단것을 한 찻숟갈 떠서 입술로 가져가더니, 눈을 반쯤 감고 고개를 뒤로 젖힌 채 천천히 삼켰다. 프란츠는 그가 좋아하는 단것을 음미하는 동안 그를 방해하지 않았으나, 그가 다 삼키고 나자 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 이 귀한 물건이 대체 무엇입니까?\"\n\n\"혹시 들어 보셨습니까,\" 그가 대답했다. \"필리프 오귀스트를 암살하려 했던 산상 노인의 이야기를요?\"\n\n\"물론 들어 봤지요.\"\n\n\"음, 아시겠지만 그는 풍요로운 골짜기를 다스렸는데, 그 골짜기는 그에게 그림 같은 이름을 안겨 준 산이 굽어보고 있었지요. 이 골짜기에는 하산벤사바가 가꾼 웅장한 정원들이 있었고, 그 정원들에는 외딴 누각들이 있었습니다. 마르코 폴로의 말에 따르면, 그는 이 누각들로 선택받은 자들을 들여, 거기서 그들에게 어떤 풀을 먹였다고 합니다. 그 풀이 그들을 낙원으로, 영원히 꽃피는 관목과 영원히 익은 과일과 영원히 어여쁜 처녀들 한가운데로 데려다주었다지요. 이 행복한 자들이 현실로 여긴 것은 한낱 꿈이었습니다. 그러나 그것은 어찌나 달콤하고, 어찌나 관능적이며, 어찌나 사람을 사로잡는 꿈이었던지, 그들은 그것을 준 자에게 몸과 영혼을 팔아넘기고, 그의 명령을 신의 명령처럼 받들어, 지목된 희생자를 베어 넘기고, 신음 한마디 없이 고문 속에 죽어 갔습니다. 자기들이 겪는 죽음이란 그저, 지금 선생 앞에 놓인 저 거룩한 풀이 살짝 맛보여 준 그 환락의 삶으로 건너가는 빠른 길목일 뿐이라 믿으면서 말이지요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 프란츠가 외쳤다. \"이건 해시시로군요! 적어도 이름만은 압니다.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다, 알라딘 나리. 이건 해시시입니다. 알렉산드리아의 가장 순수하고 가장 섞임 없는 해시시, 저 유명한 제조가 아부고르의 해시시지요. 그에게는 궁전을 지어 이런 글귀를 새겨 마땅한, 그런 유일한 사람입니다. '행복을 파는 자에게, 고마운 세상이 바친다.'\"\n\n\"이보십시오,\" 프란츠가 말했다. \"선생의 그 찬사가 사실인지 과장인지, 제가 직접 판단해 보고 싶은 마음이 무척 큽니다.\"\n\n\"직접 판단해 보십시오, 알라딘 나리. 판단해 보시되, 한 번의 시도로 그치지는 마십시오. 다른 모든 것이 그렇듯, 우리는 감각을 새로운 인상에 — 그것이 부드럽든 거칠든, 슬프든 즐겁든 — 길들여야 하니까요. 자연 속에는 이 신성한 물질에 맞서는 싸움이 있습니다. 기쁨을 위해 만들어지지 않고 고통에 매달리는 자연 속에 말이지요. 굴복한 자연은 그 싸움에서 물러설 수밖에 없고, 꿈이 현실을 대신하게 되며, 그러면 꿈이 더없이 군림합니다. 그러면 꿈이 삶이 되고, 삶이 꿈이 되지요. 한데 어떤 변화가 일어나는지! 오로지 실제 존재의 고통과 가상 존재의 기쁨을 견주어 봐야만, 선생은 더는 살고 싶지 않고 오직 영원히 그렇게 꿈꾸기만을 바라게 됩니다. 그 환상의 세계에서 이 속세로 돌아올 때면, 선생은 마치 나폴리의 봄을 떠나 라플란드의 겨울로, 낙원을 떠나 땅으로, 천국을 떠나 지옥으로 들어서는 듯할 겁니다. 해시시를 맛보십시오, 손님이여, 해시시를 맛보십시오.\"\n\n프란츠의 유일한 대답은, 주인이 먹은 것과 거의 같은 양으로 그 경이로운 조제품을 한 찻숟갈 떠서 입으로 가져가는 것이었다.\n\n\"제기랄!\" 그가 그 신성한 보존식을 삼킨 뒤에 말했다. \"결과가 선생 말씀처럼 흡족할지는 모르겠지만, 적어도 맛은 선생 말씀처럼 좋아 보이진 않는군요.\"\n\n\"선생의 입맛이 아직 그것이 맛을 내는 물질들의 숭고함에 길들지 않아서입니다. 말씀해 보십시오, 굴이며 차며 흑맥주며 송로버섯이며, 지금은 그토록 좋아하시는 갖가지 별미를 처음 맛보셨을 때, 좋으셨습니까? 로마인들이 꿩 속에 아위를 채워 넣고, 중국인들이 제비집을 먹는 것을 이해하실 수 있었습니까? 네? 못 하셨겠지요! 음, 해시시도 마찬가집니다. 일주일만 드셔 보십시오, 그러면 지금은 밋밋하고 역겹게 느껴지는 그 맛의 정묘함에 견줄 만한 것이 세상에 아무것도 없게 됩니다. 자, 이제 옆방으로 갑시다. 그곳이 선생의 거처입니다. 알리가 커피와 담뱃대를 가져다줄 겁니다.\"\n\n두 사람은 일어섰다. 그리고 스스로를 신드바드라 칭한 사내가 — 우리도 그의 손님처럼 그를 구별할 호칭이 있어야 했기에 이따금 그렇게 불러 온 그 사내가 — 하인에게 몇 가지 분부를 내리는 동안, 프란츠는 또 다른 방으로 들어섰다.\n\n그곳은 수수하면서도 호화롭게 꾸며져 있었다. 둥근 방이었고, 커다란 긴 의자가 빙 둘러 놓여 있었다. 긴 의자며 벽이며 천장이며 바닥이 죄다 더없이 값진 양탄자처럼 부드럽고 보드라운 화려한 짐승 가죽으로 덮여 있었다. 아틀라스산 갈기 무성한 사자 가죽, 벵골산 줄무늬 호랑이 가죽, 단테의 눈앞에 나타났던 것처럼 아름다운 반점이 박힌 케이프산 표범 가죽, 시베리아산 곰 가죽, 노르웨이산 여우 가죽, 그 밖에도 갖가지 가죽이 있었다. 이 모든 가죽이 겹겹이 수북하게 깔려 있어, 마치 더없이 이끼 낀 풀밭 위를 걷거나 더없이 호사스러운 침대에 기대 눕는 것 같았다.\n\n두 사람 모두 긴 의자에 몸을 뉘었다. 손이 닿는 곳에는 재스민 대롱과 호박 물부리가 달린 치부크가 놓여 있었는데, 같은 담뱃대를 두 번 피울 필요가 없도록 모두 준비되어 있었다. 둘은 저마다 하나씩 집어 들었고, 알리가 불을 붙여 준 뒤 커피를 준비하러 물러갔다.\n\n한순간 침묵이 흘렀다. 그동안 신드바드는, 이야기를 나누는 와중에도 끊임없이 그를 사로잡는 듯한 상념에 잠겼다. 그리고 프란츠는, 훌륭한 담배를 피울 때면 늘 빠져드는 그 말 없는 몽상에 자신을 내맡겼다. 그 담배는 그 연기로 마음의 온갖 시름을 거두어 가고, 그 대신 영혼의 온갖 환영을 피우는 이에게 건네주는 듯했다. 알리가 커피를 들여왔다.\n\n\"어떻게 드시겠습니까?\" 낯선 사내가 물었다. \"프랑스식으로, 아니면 터키식으로, 진하게 아니면 묽게, 설탕을 넣을까요 말까요, 식혀서 아니면 끓는 채로? 좋으실 대로 하십시오. 어떻게든 준비되어 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"터키식으로 들겠습니다.\" 프란츠가 대답했다.\n\n\"잘 고르셨습니다.\" 주인이 말했다. \"선생께 동방의 삶에 기우는 기질이 있다는 걸 보여 주는군요. 아, 그 동방 사람들이라니. 사는 법을 아는 건 그들뿐입니다. 저로 말하면,\" 그가 젊은이의 눈을 피해 가지 못한 그 묘한 미소를 또 한 번 지으며 덧붙였다. \"파리에서 제 일을 마치고 나면, 동방으로 가서 죽을 겁니다. 그러니 저를 다시 보고 싶으시거든, 카이로나 바그다드나 이스파한에서 찾으셔야 할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"맹세코,\" 프란츠가 말했다. \"그건 세상에서 가장 쉬운 일이겠군요. 제 어깨에서 독수리의 날개가 돋아나는 것 같으니, 그 날개로 스물네 시간 만에 세계를 한 바퀴 돌 수 있을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇지요, 해시시가 효력을 부리기 시작하는 겁니다. 자, 날개를 펼치고 인간을 넘어선 세계로 날아오르십시오. 아무것도 두려워 마십시오, 선생을 지켜보는 이가 있으니까요. 그리고 선생의 날개가 이카로스의 날개처럼 태양 앞에서 녹아내린다 해도, 우리가 여기서 선생의 추락을 가벼이 받아 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n그러고는 그가 알리에게 아라비아어로 무언가를 말했고, 알리는 복종의 표시를 하고 물러갔다. 그러나 멀리 가지는 않았다.\n\n프란츠에게는 기이한 변화가 일어났다. 하루 동안의 온갖 육체적 피로와, 저녁의 사건들이 가져온 온갖 마음속 근심이, 잠이 처음 다가올 때처럼 사라져 버렸다. 아직 잠이 오는 것을 알아챌 만큼은 의식이 또렷한 그 순간처럼 말이다. 그의 몸은 공기처럼 가벼워지는 듯했고, 지각은 놀랍도록 또렷해졌으며, 감각은 그 힘이 갑절로 늘어나는 것 같았고, 지평선은 계속 넓어져 갔다. 그러나 그것은 잠들기 전에 보았던, 막연한 두려움으로 가득한 음울한 지평선이 아니었다. 그것은 푸르고 투명하며 끝없는 지평선이었다. 바다의 온갖 푸름과, 태양의 온갖 반짝임과, 여름 산들바람의 온갖 향기를 머금은 지평선이었다. 그러다 자기 선원들의 노랫소리 한가운데서 — 그 음을 받아 적기만 했더라면 신성한 화음이 되었을 만큼 맑고 낭랑한 그 노랫소리 한가운데서 — 그는 몬테크리스토섬을 보았다. 이제는 물결 한가운데 위협적으로 솟은 바위가 아니라, 사막 가운데의 오아시스 같은 섬이었다. 그러다 배가 가까이 다가가자 노랫소리는 더욱 커졌으니, 황홀하고 신비로운 화음이 하늘로 솟아올랐던 것이다. 마치 어떤 로렐라이가 영혼을 그곳으로 끌어들이기로 작정한 듯, 혹은 마법사 암피온이 거기에 도시를 세우려는 듯.\n\n이윽고 배는 닿았다. 그러나 애쓰지도, 부딪히지도 않고, 마치 입술이 입술에 닿듯 그렇게 닿았다. 그리고 그는 더없이 감미로운 가락이 끊임없이 흐르는 가운데 동굴로 들어섰다. 그는 신선하고 향긋한 공기를 들이마시며 몇 계단을 내려갔다. 아니, 내려가는 것 같았다. 마음을 몽상에 잠기게 하는 향기와 감각마저 태워 버리는 불꽃으로 이루어진, 키르케의 동굴 둘레에 감돌 법한 그런 공기였다. 그리고 그는 잠들기 전에 보았던 모든 것을 다시 보았다. 별난 주인 신드바드부터 말 못 하는 시중꾼 알리까지. 그러다 모든 것이 눈앞에서 차츰 흐려지고 뒤엉키더니, 마치 꺼지기 직전의 환등기에 비친 마지막 그림자들처럼 되었고, 그는 다시 조각상들의 방에 와 있었다. 죽은 듯한 한밤중에 쾌락의 잠을 지키는, 그 창백하고 고풍스러운 등불 가운데 하나만이 방을 밝히고 있었다.\n\n그것은 같은 조각상들이었다. 형태와 매혹과 시정이 넘치고, 사람을 홀리는 눈과 사랑의 미소와 빛나며 흘러내리는 머리채를 지닌 조각상들. 그것은 프리네, 클레오파트라, 메살리나, 저 유명한 세 명의 고급 창부였다. 그러다 그들 사이로, 한 줄기 순수한 빛처럼, 올림포스 한가운데의 그리스도교 천사처럼, 정결한 자태 하나가 미끄러져 들어왔다. 그 고요한 그림자, 그 부드러운 환영은, 이 대리석 탕녀들 앞에서 제 처녀다운 이마를 가리려는 듯했다.\n\n이윽고 세 조각상이 사랑의 눈길을 띠고 그를 향해 다가와, 그가 쉬고 있는 긴 의자로 다가왔다. 발은 긴 흰 튜닉 속에 감추고, 목은 드러내고, 머리채는 물결처럼 흘러내린 채로. 그들은 신들도 거역할 수 없으나 성인들은 견뎌 낸 그런 자태를 취했고, 뱀이 새를 홀릴 때의 그것처럼 굽힐 줄 모르는 뜨거운 눈길을 보냈다. 그러자 그는, 그를 고문하듯 옭아매면서도 관능적인 입맞춤처럼 그의 감각을 황홀케 하는 그 눈길 앞에 무너졌다.\n\n프란츠는 눈을 감은 것 같았고, 마지막으로 사방을 둘러보다가 정결의 환영이 완전히 가려지는 것을 보았다. 그리고 예언자가 선택받은 자들에게 약속한 것 같은 정념의 꿈이 뒤따랐다. 돌의 입술이 불꽃으로 바뀌고, 얼음의 가슴이 뜨거운 용암처럼 되었으니, 처음으로 그 약물의 지배에 굴복한 프란츠에게 사랑은 슬픔이요 관능은 고문이었다. 불타는 입들이 그의 목마른 입술에 짓눌리고, 그는 뱀처럼 서늘한 포옹에 휘감겼다. 이 부정한 정념에 맞서 몸부림칠수록 그의 감각은 더욱 그 굴레에 굴복해 들어갔고, 마침내 제 영혼마저 짜내는 그 싸움에 지친 그는, 무너져 내려, 이 대리석 여신들의 입맞춤과 그 경이로운 꿈의 마법 아래 숨을 헐떡이며 기진맥진한 채 쓰러졌다.\n\n# Chapter 32. The Waking\n\n프란츠가 정신을 차렸을 때, 그는 여전히 꿈속에 있는 듯했다. 자신이 무덤 속에 있다고, 그 무덤에는 햇살 한 줄기가 가엾이 여겨 겨우 스며들 뿐이라고 여겼다. 그는 손을 뻗어 돌을 만졌다. 몸을 일으켜 앉으니, 자신이 부르누스를 깔고 마른 히스 풀로 된 잠자리에 누워 있었다. 그 잠자리는 무척 부드럽고 향기로웠다. 환영은 사라져 버렸다. 마치 조각상들이 무덤에서 나온 그림자에 지나지 않았던 듯, 그가 깨어남과 동시에 자취를 감추고 만 것이었다.\n\n그는 빛이 들어오는 쪽으로 몇 걸음 나아갔다. 꿈이 안겨 준 온갖 흥분이 가시고 그 자리에 현실의 고요함이 들어섰다. 그는 자신이 동굴 안에 있다는 것을 알아차렸다. 입구 쪽으로 다가가니, 부채꼴 채광창 같은 틈으로 푸른 바다와 쪽빛 하늘이 보였다. 공기와 물결이 아침 햇살 속에서 반짝였다. 물가에는 선원들이 앉아 잡담을 나누며 웃고 있었고, 그들에게서 열 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳에는 작은 배가 닻을 내린 채 물 위에서 우아하게 일렁이고 있었다.\n\n그는 한동안 이마를 스치는 산뜻한 바닷바람을 즐겼고, 모래밭에 부딪치는 파도 소리에 귀를 기울였다. 그 파도는 바위에 은처럼 새하얀 거품 레이스를 남겨 놓았다. 한동안 그는 자연 속에 깃든 신성한 매혹을, 특히 환상적인 꿈을 꾼 뒤의 그 매혹을 아무 생각 없이 느꼈다. 그러다 차츰, 이토록 고요하고 맑고 웅장한 바깥세상의 풍경이 그에게 자신이 본 환영의 헛됨을 일깨웠고, 다시 한번 기억을 되살려 주었다. 그는 이 섬에 도착했던 일, 밀수업자 두목에게 소개되었던 일, 호화로움이 넘치던 지하 궁전, 더없이 훌륭한 만찬, 그리고 한 숟갈의 대마를 떠올렸다.\n\n그러나 환한 대낮인데도, 이 모든 일이 일어난 지 적어도 일 년은 흐른 것만 같았다. 꿈이 그의 마음에 남긴 인상이 그토록 깊었고, 그의 상상력을 그토록 강하게 사로잡고 있었던 것이다. 그래서 이따금 그는 선원들 사이에서, 바위에 앉아 있는 어떤 형상이나 배 위에서 일렁이는 어떤 그림자에서, 눈길과 입맞춤으로 자신의 꿈을 함께했던 그림자 가운데 하나를 본 듯한 착각에 빠졌다. 그 점만 빼면 그의 머리는 더없이 맑았고 몸은 개운했다. 두통이라곤 조금도 없었다. 오히려 어떤 가벼움이, 맑은 공기를 들이마시고 밝은 햇살을 그 어느 때보다 생생하게 즐길 수 있는 능력이 느껴졌다.\n\n그는 활기차게 선원들에게 다가갔다. 선원들은 그를 보자마자 자리에서 일어섰다. 선장이 다가와 말했다.\n\n\"신드바드 나리께서 각하께 인사를 남기셨습니다. 직접 작별 인사를 드리지 못하는 것을 몹시 아쉬워하셨지요. 하지만 매우 중요한 용무가 있어 말라가로 가셔야 하니, 너그러이 양해해 주실 거라 믿는다고 하셨습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 가에타노,\" 프란츠가 말했다. \"이게 다 정말 현실이란 말인가? 이 섬에서 나를 맞아 주고, 임금처럼 융숭히 대접해 주고는, 내가 잠든 사이에 떠나 버린 사람이 실제로 있다는 건가?\"\n\n\"각하께서 돛을 모두 펼친 저 작은 요트를 보실 수 있는 것만큼이나 분명히 실재하는 분입니다. 망원경을 써 보시면, 십중팔구 선원들 가운데서 그 주인장을 알아보실 수 있을 겁니다.\"\n\n이렇게 말하며 가에타노는 작은 배 한 척이 코르시카 남단을 향해 돛을 올리고 나아가는 쪽을 가리켰다. 프란츠는 망원경의 초점을 맞춰 요트 쪽으로 겨누었다. 가에타노의 말은 틀리지 않았다. 고물에는 그 신비로운 이방인이 일어선 채 물가 쪽을 바라보며 손에 망원경을 들고 있었다. 그는 전날 저녁과 똑같은 차림이었고, 손수건을 흔들어 손님에게 작별을 알렸다. 프란츠도 신호를 주고받듯 손수건을 흔들어 답례했다. 잠시 후, 배의 고물에서 옅은 연기구름이 일더니 공중으로 퍼지며 우아하게 솟아올랐다. 이어 프란츠는 가벼운 포성을 들었다.\n\n\"자, 들리십니까?\" 가에타노가 말했다. \"각하께 작별을 고하시는 겁니다.\"\n\n청년은 카빈총을 집어 들어 공중에 발사했다. 요트와 물가를 갈라놓은 거리만큼 멀리까지 그 소리가 들릴 거라는 생각은 없었지만 말이다.\n\n\"각하, 분부하실 일이 있으십니까?\" 가에타노가 물었다.\n\n\"우선, 횃불을 하나 밝혀 주게.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇지요, 알겠습니다.\" 선장이 대답했다. \"그 마법의 거처로 들어가는 입구를 찾으시려는 거군요. 재미 삼아 그러시려는 거라면 기꺼이 도와드리겠습니다, 각하. 청하신 횃불을 가져다 드리지요. 사실 저도 각하와 똑같은 생각을 한 적이 있습니다. 두세 번쯤 같은 충동에 사로잡혔지요. 하지만 그때마다 단념했습니다. 조반니, 횃불을 밝혀라.\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"그래서 각하께 드려라.\"\n\n조반니가 시키는 대로 했다. 프란츠는 등불을 받아 들고 지하 동굴로 들어섰고, 가에타노가 뒤를 따랐다. 그는 거기 놓인 히스 풀 잠자리를 보고 자신이 깨어났던 자리를 알아보았다. 하지만 횃불을 들고 동굴 바깥쪽 표면을 아무리 샅샅이 비춰 봐도 헛수고였다. 보이는 것이라곤, 연기 그을린 자국으로 미루어 자기보다 앞서 누군가가 같은 시도를 했고 자기처럼 헛수고했다는 사실뿐이었다. 그래도 그는 미래만큼이나 뚫고 들 수 없는 그 화강암 벽을, 한 자 한 자 빠짐없이 면밀히 살폈다. 갈라진 틈이 보이면 사냥칼 칼날을 찔러 넣어 보았고, 튀어나온 모서리가 보이면 무너지기를 바라며 기대어 눌러 보았다. 모두 헛수고였다. 그는 그렇게 두 시간을 허비했지만, 결국 아무 소용이 없었다. 그 끝에 그는 탐색을 단념했고, 가에타노는 빙그레 웃었다.\n\n프란츠가 다시 물가에 모습을 드러냈을 때, 요트는 수평선 위의 작고 하얀 점 하나로밖에 보이지 않았다. 그는 다시 망원경으로 들여다보았지만, 그때조차 아무것도 분간할 수 없었다.\n\n가에타노는 그가 염소를 사냥하러 왔다는 사실을 일깨워 주었다. 그는 그 일을 까맣게 잊고 있었다. 그는 엽총을 집어 들고, 즐거움을 누리는 사람이라기보다 의무를 다하는 사람 같은 태도로 섬 곳곳을 누비며 사냥에 나섰다. 십오 분쯤 지나자 그는 어미 염소 한 마리와 새끼 두 마리를 잡았다. 이 짐승들은 영양처럼 사납고 날쌨지만, 집에서 기르는 염소와 너무 닮아 있어서 프란츠는 그것들을 사냥감으로 여길 수가 없었다. 게다가 훨씬 더 마음을 사로잡는 다른 생각들이 그의 머릿속을 차지하고 있었다. 전날 저녁 이후로 그는 정말이지 「천일야화」 속 이야기의 주인공이 된 것 같았고, 그 동굴에 거역할 수 없이 이끌렸다.\n\n그래서 첫 탐색이 실패로 끝났는데도, 그는 가에타노에게 새끼 염소 두 마리 가운데 하나를 구워 달라고 일러두고 두 번째 탐색에 나섰다. 이 두 번째 탐색은 꽤 오래 걸렸고, 그가 돌아왔을 때는 새끼 염소가 다 구워져 식사가 차려져 있었다. 프란츠는 전날 저녁, 그 신비로운 주인장이 만찬에 초대했던 바로 그 자리에 앉았다. 그리고 이제 파도 위의 갈매기처럼 보이는 작은 요트가 코르시카를 향해 계속 날아가는 것을 바라보았다.\n\n\"이보게,\" 그가 가에타노에게 말했다. \"자네는 신드바드 나리가 말라가로 가신다고 했는데, 보아하니 포르토베키오 쪽으로 가시는 것 같군.\"\n\n\"기억 안 나십니까?\" 선장이 말했다. \"제가 그 선원들 가운데 코르시카 출신 산적이 둘 있다고 말씀드렸잖습니까.\"\n\n\"그렇지. 그러니까 그들을 내려 주려는 거로군.\" 프란츠가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다.\" 가에타노가 대답했다. \"아, 그분으로 말할 것 같으면 하느님도 악마도 두려워하지 않는 분이라고들 하지요. 가엾은 사람 하나 돕자고 언제든 항로를 오십 리외나 벗어날 분입니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 그런 식의 선행을 베풀다가는, 그가 그런 박애를 펼치는 나라의 당국과 얽힐 수도 있겠군.\" 프란츠가 말했다.\n\n\"그분이 그런 걸 신경이나 쓰겠습니까?\" 가에타노가 웃으며 대답했다. \"어느 나라 당국이든 코웃음을 치실 분입니다. 잡을 테면 잡아 보라지요! 우선 그 요트는 배가 아니라 새거든요. 어떤 군함이든 9노트에 3노트는 따돌려 버릴 겁니다. 게다가 뭍에 올라야 한다 해도, 어디서든 도와줄 친구를 만나는 건 떼어 놓은 당상 아니겠습니까?\"\n\n프란츠의 주인장인 신드바드 나리가 지중해 연안 전역의 밀수업자며 산적들과 더없이 좋은 사이라는 영예를 누리고 있고, 그래서 남다른 특권을 누린다는 것은 더없이 분명했다. 프란츠로 말하자면, 이제 더는 몬테크리스토섬에 머무를 이유가 없었다. 그는 동굴의 비밀을 밝혀내겠다는 희망을 모조리 잃어버렸다. 그래서 아침 식사를 서둘러 마쳤고, 배가 준비되자 부랴부랴 올라탔다. 이윽고 배가 출발했다. 배가 항로에 들어선 순간, 그들은 요트를 시야에서 놓쳤다. 요트가 포르토베키오만으로 사라져 버린 것이다. 그와 함께 지난밤의 마지막 흔적도 지워졌다. 그러자 만찬도, 신드바드도, 대마도, 조각상들도, 그 모든 것이 프란츠에게는 한낱 꿈이 되었다.\n\n배는 온종일, 밤새도록 나아갔고, 이튿날 아침 해가 떠올랐을 때는 몬테크리스토섬을 시야에서 놓친 뒤였다.\n\n프란츠는 다시 한번 뭍에 발을 디디자, 적어도 그 순간만큼은 방금 겪은 일들을 잊었다. 그는 피렌체에서 즐기던 일들을 마무리지었고, 그런 뒤로는 로마에서 자신을 기다리고 있는 동행과 어떻게 다시 만날까 하는 생각뿐이었다.\n\n그는 길을 나섰고, 토요일 저녁에 우편 마차를 타고 두아나 광장에 도착했다. 앞서 말했듯이 숙소는 미리 잡아 두었으니, 그는 파스트리니 씨의 호텔로 가기만 하면 되었다. 하지만 그것은 그리 쉬운 일이 아니었다. 거리가 사람들로 북적였고, 로마는 이미 온갖 큰 행사에 앞서 일렁이는 그 낮고 들뜬 웅성거림에 사로잡혀 있었기 때문이다. 로마에는 해마다 네 가지 큰 행사가 있다. 사육제, 성주간, 성체 축일, 그리고 성 베드로 축일이다.\n\n그 밖의 시기에는 도시가 삶과 죽음 사이의, 저 따분하고 무기력한 상태에 잠겨 있다. 그래서 도시는 이승과 저승 사이의 어떤 정거장 같아 보인다. 시적 정취와 개성이 넘치는 숭고한 곳, 휴식처 같은 곳이다. 프란츠는 이미 그곳에 대여섯 번 머문 적이 있었고, 그때마다 그곳이 더욱 경이롭고 인상 깊게 느껴졌다.\n\n마침내 그는 끊임없이 불어나고 점점 더 소란스러워지는 군중을 헤치고 호텔에 다다랐다. 처음 물었을 때, 그는 집이 만원인 마차꾼이나 여관 주인 특유의 무례한 태도로, 런던 호텔에는 그가 묵을 방이 없다는 대답을 들었다. 그래서 그는 파스트리니 씨에게 명함을 들여보내며 알베르 드 모르세르를 찾았다. 이 방법이 통했다. 파스트리니 씨가 직접 그에게 달려와, 각하를 기다리게 했다며 양해를 구하고 종업원들을 나무라더니, 손님에게 달려들어 알베르에게 안내하려던 짐꾼에게서 촛대를 빼앗았다. 그때 마침 모르세르가 직접 나타났다.\n\n숙소는 작은 방 두 개와 응접실 하나로 이루어져 있었다. 두 방은 거리 쪽으로 나 있었는데, 파스트리니 씨는 이를 두고 더없이 값진 이점이라고 떠벌렸다. 그 층의 나머지는 어느 무척 부유한 신사가 빌려 쓰고 있었다. 사람들은 그가 시칠리아 사람이거나 몰타 사람일 거라고 했지만, 호텔 주인은 그 손님이 두 나라 가운데 어느 쪽 사람인지 가려내지 못했다.\n\n\"아주 좋네, 파스트리니 씨.\" 프란츠가 말했다. \"그런데 당장 저녁 식사를 좀 차려 줘야겠고, 내일과 그 뒤 며칠 동안 쓸 마차도 한 대 필요하네.\"\n\n\"저녁 식사라면,\" 주인이 대답했다. \"지금 바로 차려 드리겠습니다. 그런데 마차로 말씀드리자면……\"\n\n\"마차가 어쨌다는 거요?\" 알베르가 소리쳤다. \"이보시오, 이보시오, 파스트리니 씨, 농담은 그만두시오. 우리는 마차가 꼭 필요하단 말이오.\"\n\n\"손님,\" 주인이 대답했다. \"한 대 구해 드리려고 저희가 할 수 있는 건 다 해 보겠습니다. 제가 드릴 수 있는 말씀은 그게 전부입니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 언제쯤 알 수 있겠소?\" 프란츠가 물었다.\n\n\"내일 아침입니다.\" 여관 주인이 대답했다.\n\n\"오, 제기랄! 그러면 값을 더 치르면 될 일 아닌가, 빤히 보이는군. 드레이크네나 아론네서는 평일에 25리라를 받고, 일요일과 축일에는 하루에 30리라나 35리라를 더 받지. 추가 비용으로 하루에 5리라를 더 보태면 40리라가 될 테니, 그걸로 끝나는 거야.\"\n\n\"그 두 배를 부른다 해도 마차를 구하지 못할까 봐 걱정입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 내 마차에 말을 매면 되지. 여행에는 좀 못하겠지만, 그쯤이야 상관없네.\"\n\n\"말이 없습니다.\"\n\n알베르는 도무지 알아들을 수 없는 대답을 들은 사람처럼 프란츠를 바라보았다.\n\n\"이보게, 프란츠, 이 말 알아듣겠나? 말이 없다는군.\" 그가 말했다. \"그래도 역마라도 빌릴 수 있지 않겠나?\"\n\n\"역마는 지난 두 주 동안 죄다 예약되어, 우편 업무에 반드시 필요한 것 말고는 한 마리도 남지 않았습니다.\"\n\n\"이걸 두고 우리가 뭐라고 해야 하나?\" 프란츠가 물었다.\n\n\"내 생각은 이러네. 어떤 일이 도무지 내 이해를 넘어설 때면, 나는 그 일에 매달리지 않고 다른 일로 넘어가는 버릇이 있지. 저녁 식사는 준비됐소, 파스트리니 씨?\"\n\n\"네, 각하.\"\n\n\"좋아, 그럼 식사나 합시다.\"\n\n\"하지만 마차와 말은?\" 프란츠가 말했다.\n\n\"마음 놓게, 이 친구야. 때가 되면 다 올 걸세. 결국 값을 얼마나 치르느냐의 문제일 뿐이니까.\" 그러고는 모르세르는, 두둑한 지갑이나 빵빵한 지갑에는 불가능한 일이 없다고 믿는 그 흐뭇한 철학으로, 저녁을 먹고 잠자리에 들어 곤히 잠들었다. 그리고 여섯 마리 말이 끄는 마차를 타고 사육제 기간의 로마를 누비며 질주하는 꿈을 꾸었다.\n\n# Chapter 33. Roman Bandits\n\n이튿날 아침 프란츠가 먼저 깨어나 곧바로 종을 울렸다. 종소리가 채 잦아들기도 전에 파스트리니 씨가 직접 들어왔다.\n\n\"그렇습니다, 나리.\" 여관 주인이 의기양양하게, 프란츠가 묻기를 기다리지도 않고 입을 열었다. \"어제 제가 아무것도 약속드리지 못했을 때, 나리께서 너무 늦으신 게 아닌가 걱정했지요. 마차가 한 대도 남아 있질 않습니다. 그러니까, 마지막 사흘 동안은 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇지.\" 프란츠가 대답했다. \"하필 가장 필요한 그 사흘이라니.\"\n\n\"무슨 일인데요?\" 알베르가 들어오며 물었다. \"마차를 못 구한다고요?\"\n\n\"바로 그렇다네.\" 프란츠가 대답했다. \"자네가 알아맞혔어.\"\n\n\"이런, 자네의 영원한 도시라는 데가 참 그럴듯한 곳이로군.\"\n\n\"그러니까 말씀드리자면, 나리.\" 손님 앞에서 그리스도교 세계 수도의 위엄을 지키려는 마음에 파스트리니가 대답했다. \"일요일부터 화요일 저녁까지는 마차를 구할 수 없습니다만, 지금부터 일요일까지라면 원하시는 대로 쉰 대라도 구할 수 있습니다.\"\n\n\"아, 그거 다행이군.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"오늘이 목요일이니, 지금부터 일요일 사이에 무슨 일이 생길지 누가 알겠나?\"\n\n\"여행객이 만 명에서 만 이천 명쯤 도착할 걸세.\" 프란츠가 대답했다. \"그러면 일이 더 어려워지겠지.\"\n\n\"이보게.\" 모르세르가 말했다. \"미래를 두고 음울하게 점치지 말고 현재나 즐기세.\"\n\n\"적어도 창문 하나쯤은 얻을 수 있겠지?\"\n\n\"어디에?\"\n\n\"코르소 거리에.\"\n\n\"아, 창문이요!\" 파스트리니 씨가 소리쳤다. \"그건 도무지 불가능합니다. 도리아 궁전 오 층에 딱 하나 남아 있었는데, 그것도 어느 러시아 공작에게 하루 이십 제키노에 빌려주었습니다.\"\n\n두 젊은이는 어이없다는 표정으로 서로를 마주 보았다.\n\n\"이보게.\" 프란츠가 알베르에게 말했다. \"우리가 할 수 있는 최선이 뭔지 아나? 베네치아에서 카니발을 보내는 거야. 거기서는 마차는 못 구해도 곤돌라만큼은 틀림없이 구할 수 있지.\"\n\n\"아, 무슨 소리, 안 돼.\" 알베르가 외쳤다. \"나는 카니발을 보려고 로마에 왔어. 죽마를 타고서라도 기어이 보고 말겠네.\"\n\n\"브라보! 훌륭한 생각이야. 우리가 괴물 같은 풀치넬라나 랑드 지방 양치기로 변장하면 대성공을 거두겠군.\"\n\n\"그래서 나리들께서는 지금부터 일요일 아침까지 쓰실 마차를 여전히 원하십니까?\"\n\n\"이런 제기랄!\" 알베르가 말했다. \"우리가 변호사 사무실 서기처럼 로마 거리를 두 발로 뛰어다닐 줄 알았나?\"\n\n\"나리들의 분부를 서둘러 받들겠습니다. 다만 미리 말씀드리는데, 마차는 하루에 육 피아스터입니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 나는 옆방의 그 신사 양반처럼 백만장자가 아닐세.\" 프란츠가 말했다. \"미리 말해 두는데, 나는 로마에 이번이 네 번째라 마차 값을 죄다 꿰고 있어. 오늘, 내일, 모레, 이렇게 사흘에 십이 피아스터를 줄 테니, 그래도 자네는 충분히 남는 장사일 거야.\"\n\n\"하지만 나리……\" 파스트리니가 여전히 제 뜻을 관철하려 애쓰며 말했다.\n\n\"이제 가 보게.\" 프란츠가 대답했다. \"안 그러면 내가 직접 자네의 마부에게 가서 흥정하겠어. 그자는 내 마부이기도 하니까. 그는 내 오랜 친구로, 이미 나한테서 톡톡히 등쳐 먹은 자야. 그러니 나한테서 더 뜯어낼 욕심에, 내가 자네에게 제시한 값보다 더 싸게 받을 걸세. 그러면 자네는 우선권을 잃을 텐데, 그건 다 자네 탓일세.\"\n\n\"그렇게 수고하실 것 없습니다, 나리.\" 파스트리니 씨가 패배를 인정할 때 이탈리아 장사꾼 특유의 미소를 띠며 대답했다. \"제가 할 수 있는 일은 다 하겠습니다. 만족하시리라 믿습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 이제 서로 이해한 셈이군.\"\n\n\"마차가 언제 와 있기를 바라십니까?\"\n\n\"한 시간 안에.\"\n\n\"한 시간 안에 문 앞에 대 놓겠습니다.\"\n\n한 시간 뒤 마차가 문 앞에 와 있었다. 이번 일을 기리느라 자가용 마차의 반열에 올려놓은 삯마차에 불과했지만, 그 초라한 겉모습에도 불구하고 두 젊은이는 카니발의 마지막 사흘 동안 그것을 확보한 것만으로도 다행이라 여길 만했다.\n\n\"나리.\" 프란츠가 창가로 다가오는 것을 보고 안내인이 외쳤다. \"마차를 궁전 가까이로 대 올릴까요?\"\n\n이탈리아식 말투에 익숙한 프란츠였지만, 처음에는 무심코 주위를 둘러보았다. 그러나 그 말은 자신에게 한 것이었다. 프란츠가 '나리'였고, 그 탈것이 '마차'였으며, 런던 호텔이 '궁전'이었던 것이다. 이 종족 특유의 찬사의 재능이 그 한마디에 고스란히 담겨 있었다.\n\n프란츠와 알베르가 내려갔고, 마차가 궁전으로 다가왔다. 두 나리는 좌석 위로 다리를 쭉 뻗었고, 안내인은 뒷자리로 훌쩍 올라탔다.\n\n\"나리들께서는 어디로 가시렵니까?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"우선 성 베드로 대성당으로, 그다음에는 콜로세움으로.\" 알베르가 대답했다.\n그러나 알베르는 성 베드로 대성당을 둘러보는 데 하루가 걸리고, 제대로 공부하려면 한 달이 걸린다는 것을 몰랐다. 그날 하루는 성 베드로 대성당 한 곳에서 다 지나갔다.\n\n이윽고 햇빛이 사그라지기 시작했다. 프란츠가 시계를 꺼내 보니 네 시 반이었다. 두 사람은 호텔로 돌아왔다. 문 앞에서 프란츠는 마부에게 여덟 시에 채비를 갖추고 있으라고 일렀다. 그는 낮의 햇빛 아래 알베르에게 성 베드로 대성당을 보여 주었듯, 달빛 아래 콜로세움을 보여 주고 싶었다. 이미 가 본 도시를 친구에게 보여 줄 때면, 우리는 한때 연인이었던 여자를 가리켜 보일 때와 똑같은 자부심을 느낀다.\n\n그는 포폴로 문으로 도시를 빠져나가 바깥 성벽을 따라 돌다가 산조반니 문으로 되돌아 들어올 작정이었다. 그러면 카피톨리노 언덕과 포룸, 셉티미우스 세베루스 개선문, 안토니누스와 파우스티나 신전, 그리고 비아 사크라를 먼저 보느라 인상이 무뎌지는 일 없이 곧장 콜로세움을 마주할 수 있을 터였다.\n\n두 사람은 저녁 식사 자리에 앉았다. 파스트리니 씨는 성대한 잔칫상을 약속했는데, 그런대로 먹을 만한 식사를 차려 냈다. 식사가 끝날 무렵 그가 몸소 들어왔다. 프란츠는 음식 칭찬을 들으러 온 줄로 알고 그렇게 입을 떼었으나, 첫마디부터 말을 가로막혔다.\n\n\"나리.\" 파스트리니가 말했다. \"흡족해하시니 기쁩니다만, 제가 온 것은 그 때문이 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"마차를 구했다고 알려 주러 온 건가?\" 알베르가 시가에 불을 붙이며 물었다.\n\n\"아닙니다. 그리고 나리들께서는 그 일은 더 이상 생각하지 않으시는 게 좋겠습니다. 로마에서는 되는 일이 있고 안 되는 일이 있는데, 안 된다는 말을 들으면 그걸로 끝입니다.\"\n\n\"파리에서는 훨씬 편리하지. 안 되는 일이 있으면 돈을 두 배로 내고, 그러면 곧장 처리되거든.\"\n\n\"프랑스 사람들은 다들 그렇게 말합니다.\" 파스트리니 씨가 다소 발끈하며 대답했다. \"그러니 도대체 왜 여행을 다니시는지 모르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만.\" 알베르가 연기를 한 모금 내뿜고 의자를 뒷다리로 까딱거리며 말했다. \"여행이란 건 미치광이나, 아니면 우리 같은 멍청이나 다니는 거지. 제정신인 사람은 엘데르 거리의 호텔과 강 대로의 산책길과 카페 드 파리를 떠나지 않는 법이야.\"\n\n물론 알베르는 앞서 말한 그 거리에 살았고, 그 멋쟁이들의 산책길에 날마다 모습을 드러냈으며, 정말로 제대로 식사할 수 있는 유일한 식당에서 자주 끼니를 들었다. 단, 그 식당 종업원들과 사이가 좋아야 한다는 조건에서 말이다.\n\n파스트리니 씨는 잠시 입을 다물었다. 그다지 분명치 않게 들리는 그 대답을 곰곰이 곱씹는 게 분명했다.\n\n\"그런데.\" 이번엔 프란츠가 여관 주인의 사색을 가로막으며 말했다. \"여기 온 데는 무슨 까닭이 있었을 텐데, 그게 무엇인지 물어봐도 되겠나?\"\n\n\"아, 그렇지요. 마차를 정확히 여덟 시에 부르셨지요?\"\n\n\"그랬네.\"\n\n\"일 콜로세오를 둘러보실 작정이시고요.\"\n\n\"콜로세움 말인가?\"\n\n\"같은 겁니다. 마부에게 포폴로 문으로 도시를 나가 성벽을 빙 돌고 산조반니 문으로 되돌아 들어오라고 이르셨지요?\"\n\n\"내가 한 말 그대로일세.\"\n\n\"그런데 그 길은 불가능합니다.\"\n\n\"불가능하다고!\"\n\n\"적어도 몹시 위험하지요.\"\n\n\"위험하다니, 어째서?\"\n\n\"그 유명한 루이지 밤파 때문입니다.\"\n\n\"부디 묻건대, 그 유명한 루이지 밤파가 누구인가?\" 알베르가 물었다. \"로마에서야 아주 유명할지 모르나, 장담하건대 파리에서는 전혀 알려지지 않은 자야.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요! 그를 모르신단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"그런 영광은 누리지 못했네.\"\n\n\"그 이름을 한 번도 들어 보신 적이 없다고요?\"\n\n\"한 번도.\"\n\n\"음, 그렇다면 그는 산적입니다. 그에 비하면 데체사리와 가스파로네는 그저 어린애에 불과했지요.\"\n\n\"자, 알베르.\" 프란츠가 외쳤다. \"드디어 자네가 바라던 산적이 나타났군.\"\n\n\"미리 말해 두겠네, 파스트리니 씨. 자네가 우리에게 들려줄 이야기는 한마디도 믿지 않겠어. 이렇게 말해 두었으니 시작하게. '옛날 옛적에……' 자, 어서 해 보게.\"\n\n파스트리니 씨는 둘 중 더 분별 있어 보이는 프란츠 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다. 공정하게 말하자면, 그는 제 집에 수많은 프랑스 사람을 들였지만 한 번도 그들을 이해해 본 적이 없었다.\n\n\"나리.\" 그가 프란츠를 향해 진지하게 말했다. \"저를 거짓말쟁이로 여기신다면, 제가 무슨 말을 해도 소용이 없겠지요. 다 나리들을 위해서 드린 말씀인데……\"\n\n\"알베르는 자네를 거짓말쟁이라고 한 게 아닐세, 파스트리니 씨.\" 프란츠가 말했다. \"다만 자네가 들려줄 이야기를 믿지 않겠다는 거지. 하지만 나는 자네 말을 죄다 믿겠네. 그러니 계속하게.\"\n\n\"하지만 나리께서 제 진실성을 의심하신다면……\"\n\n\"파스트리니 씨.\" 프란츠가 대답했다. \"자네는 카산드라보다 더 예민하구먼. 그 여자는 예언자였는데도 아무도 그 말을 믿지 않았지. 그런데 자네는 적어도 청중의 절반에게는 믿음을 얻고 있질 않나. 자, 앉아서 그 밤파 씨 이야기를 죄다 들려주게.\"\n\n\"말씀드렸듯이 그는 마스트릴라 시대 이래 가장 유명한 산적입니다.\"\n\n\"좋아, 그래서 그 산적이 내가 마부에게 포폴로 문으로 도시를 나가 산조반니 문으로 되돌아 들어오라고 한 명령과 무슨 상관인가?\"\n\n\"이런 상관이 있습니다.\" 파스트리니 씨가 대답했다. \"한쪽 문으로 나가시기는 할 텐데, 다른 쪽 문으로 돌아오실지는 심히 의심스럽습니다.\"\n\n\"어째서?\" 프란츠가 물었다.\n\n\"해가 지고 나면 성문에서 오십 미터만 떨어져도 안전하지 않으니까요.\"\n\n\"명예를 걸고, 그게 사실인가?\" 알베르가 외쳤다.\n\n\"백작 나리.\" 알베르가 거듭 제 말의 진위를 의심하자 마음이 상한 파스트리니 씨가 대답했다. \"이건 나리께 드리는 말씀이 아니라, 로마를 잘 아시고 또 이런 일을 웃어넘길 게 아니라는 것도 아시는 일행분께 드리는 말씀입니다.\"\n\n\"이보게.\" 알베르가 프란츠 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다. \"이거 멋진 모험거리로군. 마차에 권총이며 나팔총이며 쌍열총을 가득 싣는 거야. 루이지 밤파가 우리를 잡으러 오면, 우리가 그를 잡는 거지. 그를 로마로 끌고 와 교황 성하께 바치면, 성하께서 이토록 큰 공을 어떻게 갚으면 좋겠냐고 물으실 테고, 그러면 우리는 그저 마차 한 대와 말 두 필만 청하면 되네. 그러고는 마차를 타고 카니발을 구경하는 거야. 그러면 틀림없이 로마 시민들이 카피톨리노 언덕에서 우리에게 월계관을 씌우고, 쿠르티우스와 호라티우스 코클레스처럼 나라를 구한 영웅이라 칭송하겠지.\"\n\n알베르가 이 계획을 늘어놓는 동안, 파스트리니 씨의 얼굴에는 형언할 수 없는 표정이 떠올랐다.\n\n\"그래, 한데 묻건대.\" 프란츠가 물었다. \"자네가 마차에 가득 채우겠다는 그 권총이며 나팔총이며 다른 흉기들은 어디 있나?\"\n\n\"내 무기고에는 없지. 테라치나에서 사냥칼까지 죄다 털렸으니 말이야. 자네는?\"\n\n\"나도 아콰펜덴테에서 같은 신세였지.\"\n\n\"이보게, 파스트리니 씨.\" 알베르가 첫 시가의 불에 두 번째 시가를 붙이며 말했다. \"이런 관행은 산적들에게 아주 편리하겠어. 게다가 그자들끼리 짜고 하는 일인 것 같단 말이지.\"\n\n파스트리니 씨는 이 농담이 자신을 곤란하게 만든다고 여긴 모양이었다. 질문의 절반에만 답하고는, 그나마 주의 깊게 들어 줄 듯한 프란츠에게 말을 건넸기 때문이다. \"나리께서도 아시다시피, 산적들에게 습격당했을 때는 저항하지 않는 것이 관례입니다.\"\n\n\"뭐라고!\" 순순히 털리는 꼴을 생각하니 용기가 끓어오른 알베르가 외쳤다. \"아무 저항도 하지 말라고!\"\n\n\"네, 소용없는 짓이니까요. 구덩이나 폐허, 혹은 수도교에서 열두 명이나 되는 산적이 튀어나와 총을 겨누면, 무엇을 할 수 있겠습니까?\"\n\n\"이런 제기랄, 그럼 나를 죽이라고 하지.\"\n\n여관 주인은 \"자네 친구는 영락없이 미쳤군\" 하고 말하는 듯한 표정으로 프란츠를 돌아보았다.\n\n\"이보게, 알베르.\" 프란츠가 대답했다. \"자네 대답은 숭고하기 그지없어. 코르네유의 그 '죽게 내버려 두라'에 견줄 만하군. 다만 호라티우스가 그 대답을 했을 때는 로마의 안위가 걸려 있었지. 하지만 우리로 말하자면 한낱 변덕을 채우자는 것뿐인데, 그런 어리석은 까닭으로 목숨을 거는 건 우스운 일이야.\"\n\n알베르는 라크리마 크리스티 한 잔을 따라 이따금 홀짝거리며 알아들을 수 없는 말을 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"자, 파스트리니 씨.\" 프란츠가 말했다. \"이제 내 일행도 잠잠해졌고, 내 의도가 얼마나 평화로운지도 보았을 테니, 그 루이지 밤파가 누구인지 말해 주게. 양치기인가, 귀족인가? 젊은가, 늙었나? 키가 큰가, 작은가? 그를 묘사해 주게. 그래야 행여 장 스보가르나 라라처럼 우연히 마주치더라도 알아볼 수 있지 않겠나.\"\n\n\"이 모든 점에 관해서라면 저보다 더 잘 알려 드릴 사람을 찾기 어려우실 겁니다. 저는 그가 어릴 적부터 그를 알았고, 한번은 페렌티노에서 알라트리로 가다가 그의 손에 떨어진 적이 있는데, 다행히 그가 저를 기억하고는 풀어 주었지요. 몸값을 받지 않았을뿐더러 아주 훌륭한 시계까지 선물로 주고, 제게 자기 내력을 들려주었답니다.\"\n\n\"그 시계 좀 봅시다.\" 알베르가 말했다.\n\n파스트리니 씨가 회중시계 주머니에서 멋들어진 브레게 시계를 꺼냈다. 제작자의 이름이 새겨진 파리제 시계로, 백작의 관(冠) 문장이 박혀 있었다.\n\n\"여기 있습니다.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"이런!\" 알베르가 대꾸했다. \"축하하네. 나도 그것과 짝이 되는 걸 가지고 있지.\" 그는 조끼 주머니에서 제 시계를 꺼냈다. \"이건 삼천 프랑이나 주고 산 거야.\"\n\n\"이야기를 들어 봅시다.\" 프란츠가 파스트리니 씨에게 앉으라고 손짓하며 말했다.\n\n\"나리들께서 허락하시겠습니까?\" 주인이 물었다.\n\n\"이런 맙소사!\" 알베르가 외쳤다. \"자네가 무슨 설교자라고 선 채로 있나!\"\n\n주인은 두 사람에게 각각 공손히 절을 하고서 자리에 앉았다. 그 절은 루이지 밤파에 관해 두 사람이 알고 싶어 하는 것을 죄다 들려줄 채비가 되었다는 뜻이었다.\n\n\"자네 말이.\" 파스트리니 씨가 막 입을 열려는 찰나에 프란츠가 말했다. \"루이지 밤파가 어릴 적부터 그를 알았다고 했지. 그럼 그가 아직 젊은이라는 말이군?\"\n\n\"젊은이냐고요? 이제 겨우 스물둘입니다. 앞으로 이름깨나 떨칠 자지요.\"\n\n\"어떻게 생각하나, 알베르? 스물둘에 그토록 유명하다니?\"\n\n\"그래, 세상에 적잖이 이름을 떨친 알렉산드로스며 카이사르며 나폴레옹도 그 나이에는 한참 그에게 못 미쳤지.\"\n\n\"그러니까.\" 프란츠가 말을 이었다. \"이 이야기의 주인공이 겨우 스물둘이란 말이지?\"\n\n\"그것도 채 안 됩니다.\"\n\n\"키가 큰가, 작은가?\"\n\n\"중키입니다. 저기 나리와 비슷한 키지요.\" 주인이 알베르를 가리키며 대답했다.\n\n\"비유해 줘서 고맙군.\" 알베르가 가볍게 인사하며 말했다.\n\n\"계속하게, 파스트리니 씨.\" 친구의 예민함에 미소 지으며 프란츠가 말을 이었다. \"그는 어느 계층 출신인가?\"\n\n\"그는 산펠리체 백작의 농장에 딸린 양치기 소년이었습니다. 그 농장은 팔레스트리나와 가브리 호수 사이에 있었지요. 그는 팜피나라에서 태어나 다섯 살에 백작의 집에 들어갔습니다. 그의 아버지 역시 양치기로, 작은 양 떼를 거느리고 그 양털과 젖을 로마에 내다 팔아 생계를 꾸렸지요. 어린 밤파는 아주 어릴 때부터 비범한 조숙함을 드러냈습니다. 일곱 살 되던 어느 날, 그는 팔레스트리나의 사제를 찾아가 글을 가르쳐 달라고 청했습니다. 쉽지 않은 일이었지요. 양 떼를 떠날 수 없었으니까요. 하지만 그 마음씨 좋은 사제는 사제를 둘 형편이 못 되어 그저 보르고라고만 불리는, 너무 가난해서 다른 이름조차 없는 작은 마을에 날마다 미사를 드리러 갔습니다. 사제는 루이지에게, 자기가 돌아오는 길에 만나면 그때 글을 가르쳐 주겠노라고 했습니다. 다만 시간이 짧을 테니 되도록 그 시간을 최대한 활용해야 한다고 일러 두었지요. 아이는 기쁘게 받아들였습니다. 날마다 루이지는 팔레스트리나에서 보르고로 가는 길에 양 떼를 풀어놓고 풀을 뜯게 했습니다. 날마다 아침 아홉 시면 사제와 소년은 길가 둑에 앉았고, 어린 양치기는 사제의 성무일도서로 글을 익혔습니다. 석 달이 지나자 그는 글을 읽을 줄 알게 되었지요. 그것으로는 부족했습니다. 이제 쓰는 법을 배워야 했지요. 사제는 로마의 서예 선생에게 부탁해 큰 것, 중간 것, 작은 것, 이렇게 세 가지 글자판을 만들어 오게 했습니다. 그리고 끝이 뾰족한 도구로 석판에 글자를 그으면 쓰는 법을 익힐 수 있다고 일러 주었지요. 그날 저녁, 양 떼를 무사히 농장에 들여놓자마자 어린 루이지는 팔레스트리나의 대장장이에게 달려가 커다란 못을 하나 얻어 와, 그것을 달구고 갈아서 일종의 철필을 만들었습니다. 이튿날 아침 그는 석판 조각을 한 아름 그러모아 시작했지요. 석 달이 지나자 그는 글을 쓸 줄 알게 되었습니다. 그의 명민함과 영리함에 놀란 사제는 그에게 펜과 종이, 그리고 주머니칼을 선물했습니다. 이것은 또 새로운 노력을 요구했지만, 처음에 비할 바는 못 되었지요. 일주일 만에 그는 철필로 쓸 때만큼 펜으로도 잘 쓰게 되었습니다. 사제가 이 일을 산펠리체 백작에게 들려주자, 백작은 어린 양치기를 불러 자기 앞에서 글을 읽고 쓰게 해 보았습니다. 그러고는 시종에게 일러 그를 하인들과 함께 식사하게 하고, 달마다 두 피아스터를 주도록 했지요. 루이지는 그 돈으로 책과 연필을 샀습니다. 그는 무엇에나 모방의 재능을 발휘했고, 어린 시절의 조토처럼 석판에 양과 집과 나무를 그렸습니다. 그러다 칼을 들고 나무에 온갖 물건을 새기기 시작했지요. 그 유명한 조각가 피넬리도 바로 그렇게 시작했답니다.\n\n\"여섯이나 일곱 살쯤 된, 그러니까 밤파보다 조금 어린 소녀가 팔레스트리나 근처 어느 농장에서 양을 치고 있었습니다. 발몬토네에서 태어난 고아였는데, 이름은 테레사였지요. 두 아이는 만나서 나란히 앉아 양 떼를 한데 섞이게 두고는 함께 놀고, 함께 웃고, 함께 이야기를 나누었습니다. 저녁이면 산펠리체 백작의 양 떼와 체르베트리 남작의 양 떼를 갈라놓고, 아이들은 이튿날 아침 다시 만나기로 약속하며 각자의 농장으로 돌아갔지요. 이튿날 아이들은 약속을 지켰고, 그렇게 함께 자랐습니다. 밤파는 열둘, 테레사는 열한 살이 되었지요. 그런데 그들의 타고난 기질이 드러나기 시작했습니다. 루이지는 고독 속에서 갈 수 있는 데까지 밀고 나간 예술적 취향 외에도, 슬픔과 열정의 발작을 번갈아 겪었고, 자주 화를 냈으며 변덕스럽고 늘 빈정거렸지요. 팜피나라며 팔레스트리나, 발몬토네의 어느 사내아이도 그에게 영향을 미치거나 그의 벗이 되지 못했습니다. 그의 기질은 (양보를 하기보다는 늘 양보를 받아 내려는 성미였지요) 그를 모든 우정에서 멀어지게 했습니다. 오직 테레사만이 눈짓 하나, 말 한마디, 몸짓 하나로 이 격렬한 성격을 다스렸지요. 여자의 손길 아래서는 굽혔지만, 남자의 손길 아래서는 부러질지언정 결코 휘어지지 않을 성격이었습니다. 테레사는 발랄하고 명랑했으나, 지나치게 교태를 부렸습니다. 루이지가 달마다 산펠리체 백작의 집사에게서 받는 두 피아스터며, 로마에 내다 판 자잘한 나무 조각의 값은 모두 귀고리며 목걸이며 금 머리핀에 쓰였지요. 그리하여 친구의 너그러움 덕분에 테레사는 로마 인근에서 가장 아름답고 가장 잘 차려입은 시골 처녀가 되었습니다.\n\n\"두 아이는 함께 자라며 모든 시간을 같이 보냈고, 서로 다른 기질에서 비롯된 거친 공상에 빠져들었습니다. 그리하여 온갖 꿈과 소망과 대화 속에서 밤파는 자신이 배의 선장이나 군대의 장군, 혹은 어느 지방의 총독이 된 모습을 그렸습니다. 테레사는 자신이 부유하고 화려하게 차려입은 채, 제복 입은 하인들의 시중을 받는 모습을 그렸지요. 그렇게 하루 종일 허공에 누각을 짓고 나면, 두 사람은 양 떼를 갈라놓고 꿈의 높이에서 내려와 초라한 처지의 현실로 돌아왔습니다.\n\n\"어느 날 어린 양치기는 백작의 집사에게, 사비니 산맥에서 늑대 한 마리가 내려와 양 떼 주위를 어슬렁거리는 것을 보았다고 말했습니다. 집사가 그에게 총을 한 자루 주었지요. 밤파가 그토록 바라던 것이었습니다. 그 총은 브레시아에서 만든 명품 총신을 지녀, 영국제 소총처럼 정확하게 탄알을 날렸지요. 그런데 어느 날 백작이 개머리판을 부러뜨렸고, 그 뒤로 총을 한쪽에 처박아 두었습니다. 하지만 밤파 같은 조각가에게 그쯤은 아무것도 아니었지요. 그는 부러진 개머리판을 살펴보고, 총을 제 어깨에 맞추려면 어떤 손질이 필요한지 가늠하고는 새 개머리판을 만들었습니다. 어찌나 아름답게 새겼던지, 마음만 먹고 내다 팔았다면 열다섯이나 스무 피아스터는 받았을 겁니다. 하지만 그건 그의 생각과는 거리가 멀었지요.\n\n\"오래전부터 총은 그 젊은이의 가장 큰 야망이었습니다. 자유 대신 독립이 자리 잡은 모든 나라에서, 사내다운 마음이 처음으로 품는 소망은 무기를 갖는 것입니다. 무기는 그를 단번에 방어할 수 있고 공격할 수도 있게 해 주며, 그 주인을 무섭게 만들어 종종 두려움의 대상이 되게 하니까요. 이때부터 밤파는 모든 여가를 이 귀중한 무기를 능숙하게 다루는 데 바쳤습니다. 화약과 탄알을 사들였고, 무엇이든 과녁이 되었지요. 사비니 산맥에 자란, 이끼 낀 늙은 올리브 나무 둥치며, 무언가를 노략질하러 굴을 나서는 여우며, 머리 위로 날아오르는 독수리까지 말입니다. 그리하여 그는 곧 빼어난 솜씨를 갖추게 되었고, 처음에는 총소리에 겁을 먹던 테레사도 그 두려움을 이겨 내고는, 그가 손으로 직접 갖다 놓기라도 한 듯 정확하게 탄알을 원하는 곳에 박아 넣는 모습을 지켜보며 즐거워했습니다.\n\n\"어느 날 저녁, 두 사람이 늘 머물던 곳 근처의 소나무 숲에서 늑대 한 마리가 나타났습니다. 그런데 그 늑대는 채 열 걸음도 나오기 전에 죽고 말았지요. 이 위업을 자랑스러워하며 밤파는 죽은 짐승을 어깨에 둘러메고 농장으로 가져갔습니다. 이런 무용담은 루이지에게 적잖은 명성을 안겨 주었지요. 뛰어난 능력을 지닌 사람은 어디를 가든 늘 찬미하는 이를 얻기 마련입니다. 사람들은 그를 사방 십 리에서 가장 날쌔고 힘세고 용감한 시골 청년이라고 입에 올렸습니다. 그리고 테레사가 사비니 일대에서 가장 아름다운 처녀라는 데 누구나 동의했지만, 아무도 그녀에게 사랑을 고백하지 않았지요. 그녀가 밤파의 사랑을 받고 있다는 걸 알았으니까요. 그런데도 두 젊은이는 한 번도 서로의 애정을 말로 드러낸 적이 없었습니다. 두 사람은 뿌리가 한데 얽히고 가지가 서로 휘감기며, 뒤섞인 향기가 하늘로 피어오르는 두 그루 나무처럼 함께 자랐지요. 다만 서로를 보고 싶다는 바람이 어느새 없어서는 안 될 일이 되어, 하루를 떨어져 지내느니 차라리 죽음을 택했을 겁니다.\n\n\"테레사는 열여섯, 밤파는 열일곱이 되었습니다. 무렵에 레피니 산맥에 자리 잡은 한 도적 떼의 소문이 자자해지기 시작했지요. 로마 인근에서 도적은 한 번도 완전히 뿌리 뽑힌 적이 없었습니다. 이따금 두목이 모자랄 뿐, 두목이 나타나기만 하면 추종자 무리를 모으는 데는 좀처럼 오래 걸리지 않았지요.\n\n\"그 유명한 쿠쿠메토는 아브루초에서 쫓기고, 정규전을 벌이던 나폴리 왕국에서 쫓겨난 뒤, 만프레디처럼 가릴리아노강을 건너 손니노와 유페르노 사이 아마시네강 기슭으로 피신했습니다. 그는 추종자 무리를 모으려 애쓰며, 자기가 능가하기를 바랐던 데체사리와 가스파로네의 발자취를 좇았지요. 팔레스트리나며 프라스카티며 팜피나라의 젊은이들이 여럿 사라졌습니다. 처음에는 그 실종이 큰 불안을 일으켰지만, 그들이 쿠쿠메토에게 가담했다는 사실이 곧 알려졌지요. 얼마 뒤 쿠쿠메토는 모든 이의 이목을 끄는 인물이 되었습니다. 그의 잔혹하고 대담무쌍한 행적과 야만성에 관한 더없이 기이한 이야기들이 떠돌았지요.\n\n\"어느 날 그는 프로시노네의 어느 측량 기사의 딸인 젊은 처녀를 납치했습니다. 도적의 율법은 단호하지요. 젊은 처녀는 우선 그녀를 납치한 자의 것이 되고, 그다음에는 나머지가 제비를 뽑아 그녀를 차지하며, 죽음이 그 고통을 끝내 줄 때까지 그들의 잔학함에 내맡겨집니다. 부모가 몸값을 치를 만큼 넉넉하면 흥정하러 사자를 보내는데, 그 사자의 안전을 담보하는 것이 바로 포로지요. 몸값을 거절당하면, 포로는 돌이킬 수 없이 끝장이 납니다. 그 젊은 처녀의 연인이 쿠쿠메토의 무리에 있었습니다. 이름은 카를리니였지요. 가엾은 처녀는 연인을 알아보자 두 팔을 뻗으며 이제 살았다고 믿었습니다. 하지만 카를리니는 가슴이 내려앉았지요. 그녀를 기다리는 운명을 너무도 잘 알았으니까요. 그러나 그는 쿠쿠메토의 총애를 받았고, 삼 년 동안 충실히 섬겼으며, 두목을 베려던 기병을 쏘아 그의 목숨을 구한 적도 있었던지라, 두목이 자기를 가엾이 여겨 주기를 바랐습니다. 그는 쿠쿠메토를 한쪽으로 데려갔습니다. 그동안 젊은 처녀는 숲 한가운데 선 거대한 소나무 밑동에 앉아, 그림처럼 아름다운 머리 장식으로 베일을 만들어 음탕한 도적들의 눈길에서 얼굴을 가렸지요. 그곳에서 그는 두목에게 모든 것을 털어놓았습니다. 포로에 대한 자신의 애정, 서로 굳게 맺은 정절의 약속, 그리고 가까이 온 뒤로 밤마다 인근 폐허에서 만나 왔다는 것까지요.\n\n\"하필 그날 밤 쿠쿠메토가 카를리니를 어느 마을로 보낸 탓에, 그는 만남의 장소에 나갈 수 없었습니다. 그런데 쿠쿠메토가 우연히 그곳에 갔다가, 본인 말로는, 그 처녀를 납치해 왔다는 것이었지요. 카를리니는 두목에게 리타만은 예외로 해 달라고 간청했습니다. 그녀의 아버지가 부유해서 두둑한 몸값을 치를 수 있다면서요. 쿠쿠메토는 친구의 간청에 못 이기는 척하며, 양치기를 하나 구해 프로시노네의 리타 아버지에게 보내라고 일렀습니다.\n\n\"카를리니는 기쁨에 차 리타에게 날아가, 이제 살았다고 말하고는 아버지에게 편지를 써서 그동안의 일을 알리고 몸값이 삼백 피아스터로 정해졌다는 것도 전하라고 했습니다. 허락된 말미는 단 열두 시간, 그러니까 이튿날 아침 아홉 시까지였지요. 편지가 다 쓰이자마자 카를리니는 그것을 움켜쥐고 사자를 찾으러 들판으로 달려갔습니다. 그는 양 떼를 지키는 젊은 양치기를 발견했지요. 도적들의 천연 전령은 바로 도시와 산 사이, 문명과 야만의 삶 사이에 사는 양치기들입니다. 소년은 그 심부름을 맡아, 한 시간도 안 돼 프로시노네에 닿겠노라고 약속했습니다. 카를리니는 연인을 보고 기쁜 소식을 전하고 싶은 마음에 서둘러 돌아왔지요. 그는 무리가 빈터에서, 농부들에게 공물로 뜯어낸 식량으로 저녁을 먹고 있는 것을 보았습니다. 하지만 아무리 둘러보아도 그들 가운데 리타와 쿠쿠메토는 없었지요.\n\n\"그가 두 사람이 어디 있느냐고 묻자, 한바탕 웃음이 답으로 돌아왔습니다. 온몸의 땀구멍에서 식은땀이 솟고, 머리카락이 곤두섰지요. 그가 다시 물었습니다. 도적 하나가 일어나, 오르비에토를 가득 채운 잔을 내밀며 말했습니다. '용감한 쿠쿠메토와 어여쁜 리타의 건강을 위하여.' 그 순간 카를리니는 여자의 비명을 들었습니다. 그는 진상을 알아챘지요. 잔을 낚아채 그것을 내민 자의 얼굴에 박살을 내고는, 비명이 들려온 쪽으로 내달렸습니다. 백 걸음쯤 가서 덤불 모퉁이를 돌자, 쿠쿠메토의 품에 정신을 잃고 늘어진 리타가 보였습니다. 카를리니를 보자 쿠쿠메토는 양손에 권총을 한 자루씩 들고 일어섰지요. 두 도적은 잠시 서로를 노려보았습니다. 한 사람은 입가에 음탕한 미소를 띠고, 다른 한 사람은 이마에 죽음 같은 창백함을 띠고서 말입니다. 두 사내 사이에 끔찍한 격투가 임박한 듯했지요. 그러나 차츰 카를리니의 표정이 풀어지더니, 허리띠의 권총 한 자루를 움켜쥐었던 손이 옆으로 떨어졌습니다. 리타가 두 사람 사이에 누워 있었습니다. 달빛이 그 무리를 비추었지요.\n\n\"'그래.' 쿠쿠메토가 말했다. '심부름은 마쳤나?'\n\n\"'네, 두목.' 카를리니가 대답했다. '내일 아홉 시면 리타의 아버지가 돈을 가지고 이리로 올 겁니다.'\n\n\"'좋아. 그동안 우리는 즐거운 밤을 보내야지. 이 처녀는 매력적이로군. 자네 취향을 칭찬할 만해. 자, 나는 이기적인 사람이 아니니, 동료들에게 돌아가 이 여자를 두고 제비를 뽑세.'\n\n\"'그럼 그녀를 공동의 율법에 내맡기기로 하셨군요?' 카를리니가 말했다.\n\n\"'어째서 그녀에게만 예외를 두어야 하나?'\n\n\"'제 간청이라면……'\n\n\"'네가 다른 자들보다 무슨 권리로 예외를 청하나?'\n\n\"'옳은 말씀입니다.'\n\n\"'하지만 걱정 말게.' 쿠쿠메토가 웃으며 말을 이었다. '머잖아 자네 차례도 올 테니.' 카를리니의 이가 발작하듯 악물렸다.\n\n\"'자, 그럼.' 쿠쿠메토가 다른 도적들 쪽으로 다가가며 말했다. '오겠나?'\n\n\"'따라가겠습니다.'\n\n\"쿠쿠메토는 카를리니에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 떠났습니다. 분명 그가 불시에 자신을 칠까 두려웠던 게지요. 하지만 카를리니에게서는 적의를 드러내는 기색이라곤 없었습니다. 그는 여전히 정신을 잃은 채 누워 있는 리타 곁에 팔짱을 끼고 서 있었지요. 쿠쿠메토는 한순간, 저 젊은이가 그녀를 품에 안고 달아나려는 게 아닐까 싶었습니다. 하지만 리타가 이미 자기 것이 된 마당에 그쯤은 이제 그에게 별일도 아니었지요. 돈으로 말하자면, 무리에게 나누어 줄 삼백 피아스터쯤은 하잘것없는 액수라 그는 거의 신경 쓰지 않았습니다. 그는 계속 빈터로 가는 길을 따라갔지요. 그런데 놀랍게도 카를리니가 그와 거의 동시에 도착했습니다.\n\n\"'제비를 뽑자! 제비를 뽑자!' 두목이 나타나는 것을 보자 도적들이 일제히 외쳤습니다.\n\n\"그들의 요구는 정당했고, 두목은 승낙의 표시로 고개를 끄덕였습니다. 모두의 눈이 그 요구를 하며 사납게 번득였고, 모닥불의 붉은 빛이 그들을 악마처럼 보이게 했지요. 카를리니를 포함한 모두의 이름이 모자 속에 담겼고, 무리에서 가장 어린 자가 표를 하나 뽑았습니다. 표에는 디아볼라치오라는 이름이 적혀 있었지요. 바로 카를리니에게 두목의 건강을 위해 건배하자고 잔을 내밀었다가, 카를리니가 그 잔으로 얼굴을 박살 내며 답했던 그 사내였습니다. 관자놀이에서 입까지 길게 이어진 커다란 상처에서 피가 철철 흐르고 있었지요. 디아볼라치오는 행운이 제게 이렇게 미소 짓는 것을 보고 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸습니다.\n\n\"'두목.' 그가 말했다. '방금 카를리니는 제가 권하는데도 두목의 건강을 위해 마시지 않았습니다. 그에게 제 건강을 위해 마시라고 권해 보십시오. 제게보다 두목에게는 더 너그러운지 봅시다.'\n\n\"모두가 카를리니의 분노가 터지리라 예상했지요. 하지만 놀랍게도 그는 한 손에 잔을, 다른 손에 술병을 들고 잔을 채웠습니다.\n\n\"'자네의 건강을 위해, 디아볼라치오.' 그가 침착하게 말하고는, 손을 조금도 떨지 않고 단숨에 들이켰습니다. 그러고는 모닥불 옆에 앉으며 말했지요. '저녁을 다오. 다녀왔더니 입맛이 도는군.'\n\n\"'잘했어, 카를리니!' 도적들이 외쳤습니다. '사내답게 구는군.' 그러고는 모두 모닥불을 빙 둘러앉았고, 디아볼라치오는 자취를 감추었습니다.\n\n\"카를리니는 아무 일도 없었다는 듯 먹고 마셨습니다. 도적들은 그 기이한 행동을 의아하게 바라보다가, 발소리를 들었지요. 돌아보니 디아볼라치오가 젊은 처녀를 품에 안고 오고 있었습니다. 그녀의 머리는 뒤로 늘어졌고, 긴 머리카락이 땅을 쓸었지요. 그들이 둘러앉은 자리로 들어서자, 도적들은 모닥불 빛에 젊은 처녀와 디아볼라치오의 시신 같은 창백함을 알아볼 수 있었습니다. 이 출현이 어찌나 기이하고 엄숙했던지, 카를리니만 빼고 모두가 일어섰지요. 카를리니는 앉은 채로 태연히 먹고 마실 뿐이었습니다. 디아볼라치오는 더없이 깊은 침묵 속에서 다가와, 리타를 두목의 발치에 내려놓았습니다. 그제야 모두가 젊은 처녀와 도적의 시신 같은 창백함의 까닭을 알 수 있었지요. 칼 한 자루가 자루까지 깊이 리타의 왼쪽 가슴에 박혀 있었습니다. 모두가 카를리니를 바라보았지요. 그의 허리띠에 매인 칼집은 비어 있었습니다.\n\n\"'아하, 아하.' 두목이 말했다. '이제 알겠군. 카를리니가 왜 뒤에 남았는지.'\n\n\"야만적인 본성을 지닌 자들은 모두 결사적인 행위를 높이 삽니다. 다른 도적이라면 아마 똑같이 하지 못했겠지요. 하지만 그들 모두 카를리니가 한 일을 이해했습니다.\n\n\"'자, 그럼.' 카를리니가 이번에는 자기가 일어서서 시신에 다가가며, 권총 한 자루의 손잡이에 손을 얹고 외쳤습니다. '이 여자를 두고 나와 다툴 자가 있나?'\n\n\"'없다.' 두목이 대답했다. '그 여자는 네 것이다.'\n\n\"카를리니는 그녀를 품에 안고 모닥불 빛이 닿는 원 밖으로 나갔습니다. 쿠쿠메토는 밤을 위해 보초들을 세웠고, 도적들은 외투를 둘러쓰고 모닥불 앞에 누웠지요. 자정 무렵 보초가 경보를 울리자, 순식간에 모두가 경계 태세에 들어갔습니다. 리타의 아버지가 직접 딸의 몸값을 가지고 온 것이었지요.\n\n\"'자.' 그가 쿠쿠메토에게 말했다. '여기 삼백 피아스터가 있소. 내 자식을 돌려주시오.'\n\n\"하지만 두목은 돈을 받지 않고, 그에게 따라오라고 손짓했습니다. 노인은 따랐지요. 두 사람은 가지 사이로 달빛이 쏟아져 내리는 나무들 밑으로 나아갔습니다. 쿠쿠메토는 마침내 걸음을 멈추고, 어느 나무 밑동에 모여 있는 두 사람을 가리켰지요.\n\n\"'저기.' 그가 말했다. '카를리니에게 자네 딸을 내놓으라고 하게. 그가 딸이 어찌 되었는지 말해 줄 테니.' 그러고는 동료들에게로 돌아갔습니다.\n\n\"노인은 꼼짝 않고 서 있었습니다. 그는 어떤 크고 예기치 못한 불행이 제 머리 위에 드리워져 있음을 느꼈지요. 마침내 그는 그 무리 쪽으로 다가갔습니다. 그 광경의 뜻을 헤아릴 수 없었지요. 그가 다가가자 카를리니가 고개를 들었고, 노인의 눈에 두 사람의 형상이 또렷이 드러났습니다. 한 여자가 땅에 누워 있었고, 그 머리는 곁에 앉은 사내의 무릎에 얹혀 있었지요. 사내가 고개를 들자, 여자의 얼굴이 드러났습니다. 노인은 제 자식을 알아보았고, 카를리니는 노인을 알아보았지요.\n\n\"'당신을 기다리고 있었습니다.' 도적이 리타의 아버지에게 말했다.\n\n\"'이 악당아!' 노인이 대답했다. '네놈이 무슨 짓을 한 거냐?' 그러고는 창백하게 피투성이가 된 채 가슴에 칼이 박힌 리타를 공포에 질려 바라보았습니다. 한 줄기 달빛이 나무들 사이로 쏟아져 죽은 자의 얼굴을 비추었지요.\n\n\"'쿠쿠메토가 당신 딸을 욕보였습니다.' 도적이 말했다. '나는 그녀를 사랑했기에 죽였습니다. 안 그랬으면 그녀는 무리 전체의 노리개가 되었을 테니까요.' 노인은 아무 말도 못 한 채 죽음처럼 창백해졌습니다. '자.' 카를리니가 말을 이었다. '내가 잘못했다면, 그녀의 원수를 갚으시오.' 그러고는 리타의 가슴에 박힌 칼을 뽑아, 한 손으로 노인에게 내밀고 다른 손으로는 제 조끼를 활짝 열어젖혔습니다.\n\n\"'자네는 옳은 일을 했네!' 노인이 쉰 목소리로 대답했다. '나를 안아 주게, 내 아들아.'\n\n\"카를리니는 어린아이처럼 흐느끼며 연인의 아버지의 품에 몸을 던졌습니다. 그것은 그 피의 사내가 난생처음 흘려 본 눈물이었지요.\n\n\"'자.' 노인이 말했다. '내 자식을 묻는 것을 도와주게.' 카를리니가 곡괭이 두 자루를 가져왔습니다. 아버지와 연인은 거대한 떡갈나무 밑동을 파기 시작했지요. 그 아래에 젊은 처녀를 뉘기로 한 것입니다. 무덤이 다 만들어지자, 아버지가 먼저 그녀를 끌어안았고, 그다음 연인이 끌어안았습니다. 그런 뒤 한 사람은 머리를, 다른 한 사람은 발을 들어 그녀를 무덤에 뉘었지요. 그러고는 무덤 양쪽에 무릎을 꿇고 죽은 이를 위한 기도를 올렸습니다. 기도를 마치자, 무덤이 가득 찰 때까지 시신 위에 흙을 덮었지요. 그런 다음 노인은 손을 내밀며 말했습니다. '고맙네, 내 아들아. 이제 나를 혼자 있게 해 주게.'\n\n\"'하지만……' 카를리니가 대답했다.\n\n\"'나를 두고 가게, 명령일세.'\n\n\"카를리니는 따랐습니다. 동료들에게 돌아가 외투를 둘러쓰고는, 곧 다른 자들처럼 깊이 잠든 듯이 보였지요. 전날 밤 그들은 야영지를 옮기기로 정해 둔 터였습니다. 동트기 한 시간 전, 쿠쿠메토는 부하들을 깨우고 행군 명령을 내렸지요. 하지만 카를리니는 리타의 아버지가 어찌 되었는지 알지 못한 채로는 숲을 떠나려 하지 않았습니다. 그는 노인을 두고 온 자리로 갔지요. 노인은 제 딸의 무덤에 그늘을 드리운 떡갈나무 가지 하나에 목을 맨 채였습니다. 그러자 그는 한 사람의 시신과 다른 한 사람의 무덤 위에서 쓰라린 복수의 맹세를 했지요. 하지만 그는 그 맹세를 끝맺지 못했습니다. 이틀 뒤 로마 기병들과의 충돌에서 카를리니가 죽었으니까요. 그런데 한 가지 의아한 것은, 그가 적을 향해 얼굴을 두고 있었는데도 어깨 사이에 총알을 맞았다는 점이었습니다. 그 놀라움은, 카를리니가 쓰러질 때 쿠쿠메토가 그의 뒤 열 걸음 자리에 있었다고 한 도적이 동료들에게 일러 주자 가셨지요. 프로시노네 숲을 떠나던 날 아침, 그는 어둠 속에서 카를리니를 뒤따라가 그 복수의 맹세를 들었고, 영악한 자답게 미리 손을 쓴 것이었습니다.\n\n\"사람들은 이 도적 두목에 관해 또 다른 열 가지 이야기를 들려주었는데, 하나같이 더 기이했지요. 그리하여 폰디에서 페루자에 이르기까지 모두가 쿠쿠메토라는 이름에 벌벌 떨었습니다.\n\n\"이런 이야기들은 곧잘 루이지와 테레사의 대화 주제가 되었습니다. 젊은 처녀는 그 이야기를 들으며 몹시 떨었지만, 밤파는 탄알을 그토록 잘 날려 보내는 자신의 멋진 사냥총 개머리판을 톡톡 치며 미소로 그녀를 안심시켰지요. 그래도 그녀의 용기가 살아나지 않으면, 그는 죽은 나뭇가지에 앉은 까마귀를 가리켜 겨눈 뒤 방아쇠를 당겼고, 그러면 새는 나무 밑동에 떨어져 죽었습니다. 세월이 흘렀고, 두 젊은이는 밤파가 스무 살, 테레사가 열아홉 살이 되면 결혼하기로 약속했습니다. 둘 다 고아라, 청해야 할 것은 고용주들의 허락뿐이었는데, 그것도 이미 청하여 얻어 둔 터였지요. 어느 날 두 사람이 미래의 계획을 의논하고 있는데, 총소리가 두세 발 들리더니 갑자기 한 사내가, 두 젊은이가 늘 양 떼를 풀어놓던 숲 근처에서 튀어나와 그들 쪽으로 달려왔습니다. 그는 목소리가 닿을 만큼 가까워지자 외쳤지요. '쫓기고 있소. 나를 숨겨 주겠소?'\n\n\"두 사람은 이 도망자가 도적임을 잘 알았습니다. 하지만 로마의 산적과 로마의 농민 사이에는 타고난 동질감이 있어, 후자는 늘 전자를 기꺼이 도왔지요. 밤파는 한마디 말도 없이, 자기들 동굴 입구를 막은 돌로 달려가 그것을 치우고는, 도망자에게 아무도 모르는 그 은신처에 몸을 숨기라고 손짓했습니다. 그러고는 다시 돌로 입구를 막고, 가서 테레사 곁에 도로 앉았지요. 곧이어 말을 탄 기병 넷이 숲 가장자리에 나타났습니다. 그중 셋은 도망자를 찾는 듯했고, 넷째는 도적 하나를 포로로 목덜미를 잡아끌고 있었지요. 세 기병은 사방을 주의 깊게 둘러보다가 젊은 농민들을 발견하고는, 말을 달려 와 캐묻기 시작했습니다. 두 사람은 아무도 보지 못했다고 했지요.\n\n\"'그것 참 곤란하군.' 분대장이 말했다. '우리가 찾는 자가 바로 두목인데.'\n\n\"'쿠쿠메토요?' 루이지와 테레사가 동시에 외쳤습니다.\n\n\"'그렇다.' 분대장이 대답했다. '그자의 목에 로마 크라운 천 냥이 걸려 있으니, 너희가 그를 잡는 걸 도왔다면 오백 냥은 받았을 텐데.' 두 젊은이는 서로 눈길을 주고받았습니다. 분대장은 한순간 희망을 품었지요. 로마 크라운 오백 냥은 삼천 리라이고, 삼천 리라면 결혼을 앞둔 가난한 두 고아에게는 한밑천이었으니까요.\n\n\"'그래요, 정말 곤란하군요.' 밤파가 말했다. '하지만 우리는 그를 보지 못했습니다.'\n\n\"그러자 기병들은 사방으로 흩어져 일대를 샅샅이 뒤졌지만 허사였지요. 그러다 얼마 뒤 그들은 사라졌습니다. 밤파는 그제야 돌을 치웠고, 쿠쿠메토가 나왔지요. 화강암 틈으로 그는 두 젊은 농민이 기병들과 이야기하는 것을 보았고, 그 담판의 내용을 짐작했습니다. 그는 루이지와 테레사의 얼굴에서 자기를 넘기지 않겠다는 확고한 결의를 읽고는, 주머니에서 금화가 가득 든 지갑을 꺼내 두 사람에게 내밀었습니다. 하지만 밤파는 도도하게 고개를 쳐들었지요. 테레사로 말하자면, 이 금화 지갑으로 살 수 있는 온갖 고운 드레스며 화사한 보석을 떠올리자 두 눈이 반짝였습니다.\n\n\"쿠쿠메토는 교활한 악마라, 뱀 대신 도적의 모습을 빌려 나타난 자였지요. 테레사의 그 눈빛을 보고 그는 그녀가 이브의 어엿한 딸임을 알아챘습니다. 그는 가는 길에 몇 번이나 걸음을 멈추고는, 자기를 지켜 준 이들에게 인사를 한다는 핑계로 뒤를 돌아보며 숲으로 돌아갔습니다.\n\n\"며칠이 흘렀지만, 두 사람은 쿠쿠메토를 보지도 그 소식을 듣지도 못했습니다. 카니발 철이 다가왔지요. 산펠리체 백작이 성대한 가면무도회를 연다고 알렸는데, 로마의 내로라하는 인사들이 모두 초대받았습니다. 테레사는 이 무도회를 몹시 보고 싶어 했지요. 루이지는 자기 후견인인 집사에게, 자기와 그녀가 그 집 하인들 틈에 끼어 무도회에 참석해도 되겠냐고 청했습니다. 허락이 떨어졌지요. 무도회는 백작이 끔찍이 아끼는 딸 카르멜라를 특별히 기쁘게 해 주려고 마련한 것이었습니다. 카르멜라는 나이도 몸매도 테레사와 꼭 같았고, 테레사는 카르멜라만큼이나 아름다웠지요. 무도회가 열리던 날 저녁, 테레사는 가장 좋은 옷을 차려입고, 머리에 가장 화려한 장식과 가장 화사한 유리구슬을 달았습니다. 프라스카티 여인들의 의상이었지요. 루이지는 명절 때 입는 로마 농민의 더없이 그림 같은 옷차림이었습니다. 두 사람은 허락받은 대로 하인들과 농민들 틈에 섞여 들었지요.\n\n\"잔치는 장려했습니다. 별장이 휘황하게 불을 밝혔을 뿐 아니라, 정원의 나무들에는 수천 개의 색등이 매달렸지요. 이내 사람들이 궁전에서 테라스로, 테라스에서 정원의 산책로로 넘쳐흘렀습니다. 갈림길마다 악단이 있었고, 다과를 차린 탁자가 놓여 있었지요. 손님들은 걸음을 멈추고 카드리유를 짜서, 마음에 드는 어디서든 춤을 추었습니다. 카르멜라는 손니노 여인처럼 차려입었지요. 모자는 진주로 수놓아졌고, 머리에 꽂은 핀은 금과 다이아몬드였으며, 허리띠는 큼직한 꽃이 수놓인 터키 비단이었습니다. 보디스와 치마는 캐시미어, 앞치마는 인도산 모슬린이었고, 코르셋 단추는 보석이었지요. 동행한 그녀의 두 동무는 하나는 네투노 여인으로, 다른 하나는 라리차 여인으로 차려입었습니다. 로마에서 가장 부유하고 가장 고귀한 가문의 젊은이 넷이 그들을 수행했는데, 세계 어느 나라에서도 견줄 데 없는 이탈리아식 자유분방함이 묻어났지요. 그들은 알바노, 벨레트리, 치비타카스텔라나, 소라의 농민으로 차려입었습니다. 굳이 덧붙일 것도 없지만, 이 농민 의상들은 젊은 처녀들의 의상처럼 금과 보석으로 휘황찬란했지요.\n\n\"카르멜라는 카드리유를 한 조 짜고 싶었지만, 여자 한 명이 모자랐습니다. 카르멜라는 사방을 둘러보았으나, 손님들 가운데 자기나 자기 동무들과 비슷한 의상을 한 사람은 하나도 없었지요. 산펠리체 백작이, 농민 무리 틈에서 루이지의 팔에 매달려 있는 테레사를 가리켰습니다.\n\n\"'허락해 주시겠어요, 아버지?' 카르멜라가 말했다.\n\n\"'그럼.' 백작이 대답했다. '지금이 카니발 철이 아니냐?'\n\n\"카르멜라는 자기와 이야기하던 젊은이 쪽으로 돌아서서 몇 마디 건네고는, 손가락으로 테레사를 가리켰습니다. 젊은이는 바라보더니 순순히 고개를 숙였고, 테레사에게 가서 백작 딸이 이끄는 카드리유에서 함께 춤추자고 청했지요. 테레사는 얼굴이 화끈 달아오르는 것을 느꼈습니다. 그녀는 루이지를 바라보았지만, 그도 동의하지 않을 수 없었지요. 루이지는 제 팔 밑에 끼고 있던 테레사의 팔을 천천히 놓아 주었고, 테레사는 그 우아한 기사와 함께 잔뜩 떨리는 마음으로 귀족들의 카드리유에서 정해진 자리에 섰습니다. 분명 예술가의 눈으로 보자면, 정확하고 엄정한 테레사의 의상은 카르멜라며 그 동무들의 의상과는 사뭇 다른 멋을 지녔지요. 하지만 테레사는 경박하고 교태가 심한 처녀라, 그 자수와 모슬린, 캐시미어 허리띠가 그녀의 눈을 죄다 부시게 했고, 사파이어와 다이아몬드의 광채에 가벼운 머리가 거의 어질어질해졌습니다.\n\n\"루이지는 여태껏 알지 못하던 어떤 감각이 마음속에서 솟아오르는 것을 느꼈습니다. 그것은 심장을 갉아먹다가 온몸으로 전율처럼 퍼지는 날카로운 통증 같았지요. 그는 테레사와 그 기사의 움직임 하나하나를 눈으로 좇았습니다. 두 사람의 손이 닿을 때면 그는 정신을 잃을 것만 같았고, 맥박이 마구 뛰었으며, 귓속에서 종이 울리는 듯했지요. 두 사람이 이야기를 나눌 때면, 테레사는 그 기사의 말에 수줍게 눈을 내리깔고 귀를 기울였지만, 루이지는 그 잘생긴 젊은이의 뜨거운 눈빛에서 그가 찬사를 늘어놓고 있음을 읽을 수 있었습니다. 그러면 온 세상이 자기와 함께 빙빙 도는 듯했고, 지옥의 온갖 목소리가 살인과 암살의 생각을 귓가에 속삭이는 듯했지요. 발작이 자신을 집어삼킬까 두려워진 그는 한 손으로 기대고 선 나뭇가지를 움켜쥐고, 다른 손으로는 허리띠에 꽂힌, 손잡이를 새긴 단검을 발작하듯 거머쥐었습니다. 그러고는 자기도 모르게 이따금 그것을 칼집에서 뽑아 들곤 했지요.\n\n\"루이지는 질투에 사로잡혔던 것입니다!\n\n\"그는, 야심과 교태 어린 기질에 휘둘려 테레사가 자기에게서 달아날지도 모른다는 것을 느꼈습니다.\n\n\"처음에는 수줍고 겁먹었던 젊은 시골 처녀는 곧 평정을 되찾았습니다. 앞서 테레사가 아름답다고 했지만, 그게 다가 아니었지요. 테레사는 우리의 꾸미고 다듬은 우아함보다 훨씬 더 강렬한, 온갖 야성적인 매력을 타고났습니다. 카드리유의 영예는 거의 그녀의 차지였고, 그녀가 산펠리체 백작 딸을 부러워했다면, 카르멜라 역시 그녀를 질투하지 않았다고는 장담하지 못하겠습니다. 잘생긴 기사는 넘치도록 찬사를 늘어놓으며 그녀를 데려왔던 자리, 루이지가 기다리고 있는 그곳으로 다시 데려다주었습니다. 춤추는 동안 두세 번 젊은 처녀는 루이지를 흘끗 보았는데, 그때마다 그가 창백하고 표정이 일그러져 있는 것을 보았지요. 한번은 칼집에서 반쯤 뽑힌 단검의 날이 불길한 빛으로 그녀의 눈을 부시게 하기도 했습니다. 그리하여 그녀는 거의 떨면서 연인의 팔을 다시 잡았지요. 카드리유는 더없이 완벽했고, 다시 한번 추자는 요청이 빗발친 게 분명했지만, 카르멜라만은 반대했습니다. 그러나 산펠리체 백작이 어찌나 간곡히 청하던지, 딸은 결국 응했지요.\n\n\"그러자 기사 하나가 서둘러 테레사를 청하러 나섰습니다. 그녀가 없으면 카드리유를 짤 수 없었으니까요. 하지만 젊은 처녀는 사라지고 없었습니다.\n\n\"실은 루이지가 그런 시련을 한 번 더 견딜 힘이 없어, 반은 설득으로 반은 완력으로 테레사를 정원의 다른 쪽으로 데려간 것이었습니다. 테레사는 마지못해 따라갔지만, 그 젊은이의 일그러진 얼굴을 보자, 그의 침묵과 떨리는 목소리에서 무언가 심상찮은 일이 그의 안에서 일어나고 있음을 알아챘지요. 그녀 자신도 속으로 동요가 없지 않았고, 아무 잘못도 저지르지 않았으면서도, 루이지가 자기를 나무라는 게 옳다는 것을 충분히 헤아렸습니다. 왜인지는 알 수 없었지만, 그래도 그 책망이 마땅하다는 것만은 못지않게 느꼈지요.\n\n\"그런데 테레사로서는 몹시 놀랍게도, 루이지는 입을 다문 채 그날 저녁 내내 한마디도 내뱉지 않았습니다. 밤기운이 손님들을 정원에서 몰아내고, 실내의 잔치를 위해 별장의 문이 그들 뒤로 닫히자, 그는 테레사를 멀찍이 데리고 나왔지요. 그리고 그녀를 집에 데려다주며 말했습니다.\n\n\"'테레사, 너는 젊은 산펠리체 백작 영애와 마주 보고 춤추면서 무슨 생각을 했지?'\n\n\"'생각했어요.' 젊은 처녀가 타고난 솔직함으로 대답했다. '저 사람이 입은 것 같은 옷을 가질 수 있다면 내 목숨의 절반이라도 내놓겠다고요.'\n\n\"'그래, 그 기사는 너한테 뭐라고 했지?'\n\n\"'그건 나한테 달린 일이고, 한마디만 하면 된다고 했어요.'\n\n\"'그자 말이 옳았어.' 루이지가 말했다. '네 말처럼 그렇게 간절히 그걸 원하나?'\n\n\"'네.'\n\n\"'좋아, 그럼 너는 그걸 갖게 될 거야!'\n\n\"젊은 처녀는 몹시 놀라 그를 보려고 고개를 들었지만, 그의 얼굴이 어찌나 음울하고 무섭던지 입가에서 말이 얼어붙었습니다. 루이지는 그렇게 말하고 그녀를 떠났지요. 테레사는 어둠 속으로 사라지는 그를 보일 때까지 눈으로 좇았고, 그가 완전히 자취를 감추자 한숨을 내쉬며 집으로 들어갔습니다.\n\n\"그날 밤 잊지 못할 사건이 벌어졌습니다. 틀림없이 불을 끄는 것을 소홀히 한 어느 하인의 부주의 탓이었지요. 산펠리체 별장은 어여쁜 카르멜라의 방에 바로 잇닿은 방들에서 불이 났습니다. 한밤중 불길의 빛에 잠을 깬 그녀는 침대에서 뛰쳐나와 실내복을 걸치고 문으로 빠져나가려 했지만, 달아나려던 복도는 이미 불길에 휩싸여 있었지요. 그녀는 다시 방으로 돌아와 있는 힘껏 도움을 청했습니다. 그때 갑자기 땅에서 이십 자쯤 떨어진 창문이 열리더니, 젊은 농민 하나가 방 안으로 뛰어들어 그녀를 품에 안고는, 초인적인 솜씨와 힘으로 잔디밭 위로 그녀를 옮겼지요. 그곳에서 그녀는 정신을 잃었습니다. 정신을 차렸을 때, 곁에는 아버지가 있었지요. 하인들이 모두 그녀를 둘러싸고 거들겠다고 나섰습니다. 별장의 한쪽 동이 통째로 타 버렸지만, 카르멜라가 무사하고 다치지 않았으니 그쯤이야 무슨 대수겠습니까?\n\n\"사람들이 사방으로 그녀의 구원자를 찾았지만, 그는 나타나지 않았습니다. 그를 수소문했으나, 아무도 그를 본 사람이 없었지요. 카르멜라는 그를 알아보지 못한 것이 몹시 마음에 걸렸습니다.\n\n\"백작은 엄청난 부자라, 카르멜라가 겪은 위험만 빼면, 그것도 그녀가 기적처럼 벗어난 방식 때문에 그에게는 차라리 진짜 불행이라기보다 섭리의 은총처럼 여겨졌으니, 화재로 입은 손실쯤은 그에게 하잘것없었지요.\n\n\"이튿날 여느 때처럼 두 젊은 농민은 숲 어귀에 나왔습니다. 루이지가 먼저 도착했지요. 그는 들뜬 기색으로 테레사에게 다가왔는데, 전날 저녁의 일은 까맣게 잊은 듯했습니다. 젊은 처녀는 몹시 시름에 잠겨 있었지만, 루이지가 저토록 쾌활한 것을 보자, 그녀도 흥분하거나 화나지 않을 때면 자연스레 짓던 미소 띤 표정을 지었지요.\n\n\"루이지는 그녀의 팔을 제 팔에 끼고 동굴 입구로 이끌었습니다. 그러고는 걸음을 멈추었지요. 무언가 심상찮은 일이 있음을 알아챈 젊은 처녀가 그를 뚫어지게 바라보았습니다.\n\n\"'테레사.' 루이지가 말했다. '어제저녁 너는 백작 딸이 입은 것 같은 옷을 가질 수만 있다면 세상의 무엇이라도 내놓겠다고 했지.'\n\n\"'네.' 테레사가 놀라며 대답했다. '하지만 그런 소원을 입에 담다니, 내가 정신이 나갔던 거예요.'\n\n\"'그래서 나는 대답했어. 「좋아, 너는 그걸 갖게 될 거야」라고.'\n\n\"'네.' 루이지가 한마디 할 때마다 놀라움이 더해 가는 젊은 처녀가 대답했다. '하지만 물론 당신 대답은 그저 나를 기쁘게 해 주려던 거였잖아요.'\n\n\"'나는 너에게 준 것 이상으로 약속한 적이 없어, 테레사.' 루이지가 도도하게 말했다. '동굴 안으로 들어가 옷을 갈아입어.'\n\n\"이렇게 말하며 그는 돌을 치우고, 테레사에게 동굴을 보여 주었습니다. 동굴은 멋진 거울 양옆에서 타오르는 밀랍 초 두 자루로 환히 밝혀져 있었지요. 루이지가 만든 투박한 탁자 위에는 진주 목걸이와 다이아몬드 핀이 펼쳐져 있었고, 옆 의자에는 나머지 의상이 놓여 있었습니다.\n\n\"테레사는 기쁨의 탄성을 질렀고, 이 차림새가 어디서 났는지 묻지도, 루이지에게 고맙다는 인사조차 하지 않은 채, 탈의실로 변한 동굴 안으로 뛰어들었지요.\n\n\"루이지는 그녀의 뒤로 돌을 밀어 닫았습니다. 팔레스트리나 쪽 시야를 가로막는 인근의 자그마한 언덕 마루에, 말을 탄 길손 하나가 마치 제 길을 몰라 망설이듯 잠시 멈춰 선 게 보였기 때문이지요. 그렇게 그는 푸른 하늘을 배경으로, 남쪽 지방의 먼 사물 특유의 완벽한 윤곽을 드러내고 있었습니다. 루이지를 보자 길손은 말에 박차를 가해 그에게 다가왔지요.\n\n\"루이지의 짐작은 틀리지 않았습니다. 팔레스트리나에서 티볼리로 가던 길손이 길을 잘못 든 것이었지요. 젊은이가 길을 일러 주었습니다. 하지만 사 분의 일 마일쯤 가면 길이 다시 세 갈래로 갈리는데, 거기에 이르면 길손이 또 길을 잘못 들 수도 있었던지라, 그는 루이지에게 길잡이가 되어 달라고 청했습니다.\n\n\"루이지는 외투를 땅에 벗어 던지고 소총을 어깨에 멘 뒤, 무거운 겉옷에서 벗어나 산사람 특유의 날랜 걸음으로 길손을 앞장섰지요. 말이 좀처럼 따라잡기 힘든 걸음이었습니다. 십 분 만에 루이지와 길손은 갈림길에 닿았지요. 거기 이르자 그는 황제처럼 위풍당당한 태도로, 길손이 가야 할 그 길을 향해 손을 뻗었습니다.\n\n\"'저것이 나리께서 가실 길입니다. 이제 다시는 잘못 드실 일이 없을 겁니다.'\n\n\"'그럼 이건 자네 사례일세.' 길손이 어린 목동에게 잔돈 몇 닢을 내밀며 말했다.\n\n\"'고맙습니다만.' 루이지가 손을 거두며 말했다. '저는 도움을 베푸는 것이지, 팔지는 않습니다.'\n\n\"'그렇군.' 도시 사람의 비굴함과 산사람의 자존심 사이의 이런 차이에 익숙한 듯한 길손이 대답했다. '품삯은 마다하더라도, 선물이라면 아마 받겠지.'\n\n\"'아, 네, 그건 다른 이야기지요.'\n\n\"'그럼.' 길손이 말했다. '이 베네치아 제키노 두 닢을 받아, 신부에게 주어 귀고리 한 쌍을 장만하게 하게.'\n\n\"'그럼 나리께서는 이 단검을 받으십시오.' 어린 목동이 말했다. '알바노에서 치비타카스텔라나에 이르도록 이보다 더 잘 새긴 것은 찾지 못하실 겁니다.'\n\n\"'받겠네.' 길손이 대답했다. '하지만 그러면 빚은 내 쪽이 지는 셈이군. 이 단검은 제키노 두 닢보다 값이 더 나가니 말일세.'\n\n\"'장사꾼한테야 그렇겠지요. 하지만 제 손으로 직접 새긴 저에게는 한 피아스터도 못 되는 물건입니다.'\n\n\"'자네 이름이 무엇인가?' 길손이 물었다.\n\n\"'루이지 밤파입니다.' 목동이, 마케도니아 왕 알렉산드로스라고 대답하듯 그런 태도로 대답했다. '나리는요?'\n\n\"'나는.' 길손이 말했다. '뱃사람 신드바드라 하네.'\"\n\n프란츠 데피네가 깜짝 놀라며 움찔했다.\n\n\"뱃사람 신드바드라고?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"네.\" 이야기꾼이 대답했다. \"그것이 길손이 밤파에게 일러 준 자기 이름이었습니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 그 이름에 대해 자네는 무슨 할 말이라도 있나?\" 알베르가 물었다. \"아주 예쁜 이름이군. 솔직히 말해, 그 이름의 신사가 펼친 모험은 내 어린 시절에 나를 무척 즐겁게 해 주었지.\"\n\n프란츠는 더 말하지 않았다. 능히 짐작하겠지만, 뱃사람 신드바드라는 이름은, 전날 저녁의 몬테크리스토 백작이라는 이름이 그러했듯, 그의 마음속에 온갖 기억의 세계를 일깨웠던 것이다.\n\n\"계속하게!\" 그가 주인에게 말했다.\n\n\"밤파는 제키노 두 닢을 거만하게 주머니에 넣고, 왔던 길을 천천히 되돌아갔습니다. 동굴에서 이삼백 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳에 이르렀을 때, 그는 비명 소리를 들은 듯했지요. 그 소리가 어디서 나는지 알아내려고 귀를 기울였습니다. 잠시 뒤 자기 이름이 또렷이 불리는 것을 들은 듯했지요.\n\n\"비명은 동굴에서 나고 있었습니다. 그는 영양처럼 펄쩍 뛰어, 달려가며 소총의 공이를 당겼고, 순식간에 아까 그 길손을 발견했던 언덕 맞은편 언덕 꼭대기에 이르렀지요. 도와 달라는 세 번의 외침이 더 또렷이 그의 귀에 닿았습니다. 그가 주위를 둘러보자, 한 사내가, 켄타우로스 네소스가 데이아네이라를 채어 가듯 테레사를 채어 가는 것이 보였지요.\n\n\"숲 쪽으로 서둘러 가는 이 사내는, 동굴에서 숲으로 가는 길의 사 분의 삼쯤을 이미 와 있었습니다. 밤파는 거리를 가늠했지요. 사내는 적어도 이백 걸음은 앞서 있어, 따라잡을 가망이 없었습니다. 어린 양치기는 두 발이 땅에 뿌리박힌 듯 멈춰 섰지요. 그러고는 소총 개머리판을 어깨에 대고 그 납치범을 겨눈 뒤, 일 초 동안 그의 자취를 좇다가 방아쇠를 당겼습니다.\n\n\"납치범이 우뚝 멈춰 서더니, 무릎이 꺾이며 테레사를 품에 안은 채 쓰러졌지요. 젊은 처녀는 곧바로 일어섰지만, 사내는 죽음의 고통에 몸부림치며 땅바닥에 누워 있었습니다. 그러자 밤파는 테레사 쪽으로 내달렸지요. 죽어 가는 사내에게서 열 걸음 떨어진 곳에서 그녀는 다리에 힘이 풀려 무릎을 꿇고 주저앉아 있었고, 젊은이는 두려웠습니다.\n그 총탄이 자기 원수를 쓰러뜨렸을 뿐만 아니라 약혼녀까지 다치게 한 것은 아닐까 두려웠던 것이다.\n\n“다행히 그녀는 상처 하나 없었고, 테레사를 쓰러뜨린 것은 오직 공포뿐이었습니다. 그녀가 무사하고 다친 데 없음을 확인한 루이지는 부상당한 사내 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸지요. 그자는 막 숨을 거둔 참이었습니다. 두 손은 움켜쥔 채였고, 입은 단말마의 경련으로 일그러졌으며, 머리카락은 죽음의 식은땀에 곤두서 있었지요. 두 눈은 위협하듯 뜬 채였습니다. 밤파가 시체에 다가가 보니, 쿠쿠메토였습니다.\n\n“그 산적은 두 젊은 농부에게 목숨을 구원받은 그날부터 테레사에게 반해, 그녀를 제 것으로 만들겠다고 맹세했습니다. 그때부터 두 사람을 지켜봐 오다가, 연인이 그녀를 혼자 두고 떠난 순간을 노려 그녀를 납치했고, 마침내 손아귀에 넣었다고 믿었습니다. 바로 그 순간, 젊은 목동의 빗나감 없는 솜씨로 날아든 총탄이 그자의 심장을 꿰뚫은 것이지요. 밤파는 잠시 동안 추호의 동요도 없이 그를 바라보았습니다. 반면 테레사는 온몸을 떨며 살해된 악당에게 차마 다가가지 못한 채 조금씩 발을 옮겼고, 연인의 어깨 너머로 시신을 향해 머뭇거리는 눈길을 던졌습니다. 갑자기 밤파가 자기 연인 쪽으로 돌아섰습니다.\n\n“‘아,’ 그가 말했습니다. ‘됐어, 됐어! 너는 옷을 차려입었으니, 이제 내가 차려입을 차례군.’\n\n“테레사는 머리부터 발끝까지 산펠리체 백작 딸의 차림새를 하고 있었습니다. 밤파는 쿠쿠메토의 시체를 두 팔로 안아 동굴로 옮겼고, 이번에는 테레사가 밖에 남았습니다. 만약 또 다른 길손이 그곳을 지났더라면, 기이한 광경을 보았을 겁니다. 양 떼를 지키는 양치기 처녀가 캐시미어 가운을 걸치고, 진주 귀고리와 목걸이, 다이아몬드 핀, 사파이어와 에메랄드와 루비 단추를 단 모습이었으니까요. 그 길손은 분명 플로리앙의 시대로 되돌아갔다고 믿었을 테고, 파리에 도착해서는 사비니 언덕 기슭에 앉은 알프스의 양치기 처녀를 만났노라 장담했을 겁니다.\n\n“십오 분쯤 지나자 밤파가 동굴에서 나왔는데, 그의 차림새도 테레사 못지않게 우아했습니다. 그는 금빛으로 깎아 만든 단추가 달린 석류빛 벨벳 조끼를 입었고, 그 위에 자수를 가득 놓은 비단 조끼를 덧입었습니다. 목에는 로마식 스카프를 둘렀고, 금실과 붉은빛, 초록빛 비단으로 수놓은 탄약통을 찼습니다. 하늘빛 벨벳 반바지는 무릎 위에서 다이아몬드 죔쇠로 여몄고, 사슴 가죽으로 만든 대님에는 수천 가지 당초무늬가 새겨져 있었습니다. 모자에는 온갖 빛깔의 리본이 드리워져 있었고, 허리띠에는 시계 두 개가 매달렸으며, 띠에는 화려한 단검이 꽂혀 있었지요.\n\n“테레사가 감탄의 탄성을 질렀습니다. 그런 차림새의 밤파는 레오폴 로베르나 슈네츠의 그림 속 인물 같았습니다. 그는 쿠쿠메토의 차림새를 그대로 갖추었던 것이지요. 젊은이는 약혼녀에게 자아낸 효과를 알아챘고, 그의 입가에 자랑스러운 미소가 스쳤습니다.\n\n“‘자,’ 그가 테레사에게 말했습니다. ‘무엇이 되었든 내 운명을 함께할 준비가 되었나?’\n\n“‘오, 그럼요!’ 처녀가 열렬히 외쳤습니다.\n\n“‘내가 어디로 가든 따라오겠나?’\n\n“‘세상 끝까지라도요.’\n\n“‘그럼 내 팔을 잡고, 가자. 지체할 시간이 없어.’\n\n“처녀는 자기를 어디로 데려가는지 연인에게 묻지도 않고 그대로 따랐습니다. 이 순간 그가 신처럼 잘생기고 당당하며 강인해 보였기 때문이지요. 두 사람은 숲을 향해 걸었고, 곧 그 안으로 들어섰습니다.\n\n“밤파가 산속의 모든 길을 훤히 꿰뚫고 있었다는 건 굳이 말할 필요도 없겠지요. 그래서 다져진 길이 전혀 없는데도 한순간의 망설임도 없이 앞으로 나아갔습니다. 그는 나무와 덤불을 보고 길을 알아냈고, 그렇게 두 사람은 거의 한 시간 반 동안 계속 걸었습니다. 그 끝에 이르렀을 때, 그들은 숲에서 가장 우거진 곳에 다다랐습니다. 물이 말라 버린 골짜기 하나가 깊은 협곡으로 이어졌지요. 밤파는 이 험한 길로 들어섰습니다. 양쪽 능선 사이에 끼어 있고 빽빽한 소나무 그늘에 덮인 그 길은, 내려가기가 험난하다는 점만 빼면, 베르길리우스가 말한 아베르누스로 가는 길 같았습니다. 테레사는 주위 들판의 거칠고 황량한 모습에 겁을 먹고 길잡이에게 바싹 붙어 한마디도 내지 못했지만, 그가 한결같은 걸음과 차분한 얼굴로 나아가는 것을 보고는 두려움을 억누르려 애썼습니다.\n\n“갑자기 그들에게서 열 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳에서 한 사내가 나무 뒤에서 나오더니 밤파를 겨누었습니다.\n\n“‘한 발짝도 더 움직이지 마라.’ 그가 말했습니다. ‘그러지 않으면 죽은 목숨이다.’\n\n“‘이런,’ 밤파가 경멸의 몸짓으로 손을 들어 올리며 말했습니다. 그러는 사이 테레사는 더 이상 두려움을 누르지 못하고 그에게 바싹 매달렸지요. ‘늑대끼리 서로 물어뜯기라도 한다더냐?’\n\n“‘너는 누구냐?’ 보초가 물었습니다.\n\n“‘나는 산펠리체 농장의 목동, 루이지 밤파다.’\n\n“‘무엇을 원하느냐?’\n\n“‘로카비안카 빈터에 있는 네 동료들과 이야기를 나누고 싶다.’\n\n“‘그렇다면 나를 따라오너라.’ 보초가 말했습니다. ‘아니, 길을 안다면 네가 앞장서거라.’\n\n“밤파는 산적의 그런 경계심에 경멸하듯 미소 짓고는, 테레사보다 앞서 나가 아까처럼 굳건하고 거침없는 걸음으로 계속 나아갔습니다. 십 분쯤 지나자 산적이 멈추라는 신호를 보냈지요. 두 젊은이는 그대로 따랐습니다. 그러자 산적이 세 차례 까마귀 울음소리를 흉내 냈고, 그 신호에 까옥 하는 소리가 답했습니다.\n\n“‘좋다!’ 보초가 말했습니다. ‘이제 가도 된다.’\n\n“루이지와 테레사는 다시 발걸음을 옮겼습니다. 나아가는 동안 테레사는 나무 사이로 무기들과 번뜩이는 기총들을 보고 떨면서 연인에게 매달렸지요. 로카비안카의 은신처는 작은 산꼭대기에 있었는데, 그 산은 분명 오래전에는 화산이었을 겁니다. 레무스와 로물루스가 알바를 버리고 로마라는 도시를 세우러 오기 전에 이미 불이 꺼진 화산이었지요.\n\n“테레사와 루이지가 정상에 다다르자, 별안간 산적 스무 명과 마주하게 되었습니다.\n\n“‘여기 당신들을 찾아와 이야기하고 싶다는 젊은이가 있소.’ 보초가 말했습니다.\n\n“‘무슨 할 말이 있다는 거냐?’ 두목이 자리를 비운 사이 지휘를 맡고 있던 젊은이가 물었습니다.\n\n“‘목동의 삶이 지긋지긋해졌다는 말이오.’ 밤파가 대답했습니다.\n\n“‘아, 알겠군.’ 부두목이 말했습니다. ‘그래서 우리 무리에 들고 싶다는 거냐?’\n\n“‘잘 왔네!’ 페루시노와 팜피나라, 아나니 출신의 산적 몇이 루이지 밤파를 알아보고 외쳤습니다.\n\n“‘그렇소, 하지만 나는 당신들의 동료가 되는 것 말고 더 청할 게 있어 왔소.’\n\n“‘그게 무엇이냐?’ 산적들이 놀라며 물었습니다.\n\n“‘나는 당신들의 두목이 되겠다고 청하러 왔소.’ 젊은이가 말했습니다.\n\n“산적들은 폭소를 터뜨렸습니다.\n\n“‘그런 영예를 바랄 만큼 네가 무슨 일을 했단 말이냐?’ 부두목이 따져 물었습니다.\n\n“‘나는 당신들의 두목 쿠쿠메토를 죽였고, 지금 그 옷을 입고 있소. 그리고 내 약혼녀에게 줄 혼례복을 마련하려고 산펠리체 별장에 불을 질렀소.’\n\n“한 시간 뒤, 루이지 밤파는 고인이 된 쿠쿠메토를 대신할 두목으로 뽑혔습니다.”\n\n“자, 친애하는 알베르,” 프란츠가 친구 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다. “시민 루이지 밤파에 대해 어떻게 생각하나?”\n\n“지어낸 이야기라고 봐.” 알베르가 대답했다. “애초에 그런 인물은 있지도 않았어.”\n\n“지어낸 이야기라니, 그게 무슨 뜻입니까?” 파스트리니가 물었다.\n\n“설명하자면 너무 길어요, 주인장.” 프란츠가 대답했다. “그럼 그 밤파 선생이 지금 이 순간에도 로마 근교에서 그 일을 벌이고 있다는 거요?”\n\n“그것도 이전의 어떤 산적도 본보기를 보인 적 없는 대담함으로 말입니다.”\n\n“그렇다면 경찰이 그를 붙잡으려고 애써 봤지만 헛수고였다는 거요?”\n\n“글쎄요, 보시다시피 그는 들판의 목동들, 티베르강의 어부들, 해안의 밀수꾼들과 두루 통해 있습니다. 산속에서 찾으면 그는 물 위에 있고, 물 위로 쫓으면 먼바다에 나가 있지요. 그래서 추격하면 별안간 섬으로, 질리오나 잔누트리, 혹은 몬테크리스토로 피신해 버립니다. 그리고 거기서 그를 사냥하려 들면, 별안간 알바노나 티볼리, 라리차에 다시 나타나는 거지요.”\n\n“그럼 여행자들에게는 어떻게 대하오?”\n\n“아아! 그 방식은 아주 단순합니다. 도시에서 떨어진 거리에 따라, 몸값을 치르도록 여덟 시간이나 열두 시간, 혹은 하루를 주지요. 그 시간이 지나면 한 시간을 더 봐줍니다. 그 한 시간의 예순 번째 분이 되도록 돈이 마련되지 않으면, 권총 한 방으로 포로의 머리통을 날려 버리거나 심장에 단검을 꽂아 버립니다. 그것으로 셈이 끝나는 거지요.”\n\n“자, 알베르,” 프란츠가 친구에게 물었다. “아직도 바깥 성벽을 따라 콜로세움에 갈 생각인가?”\n\n“물론이지.” 알베르가 말했다. “길이 그림처럼 멋지기만 하다면 말이야.”\n\n문이 열리며 시계가 아홉 시를 알릴 때, 마부가 나타났다.\n\n“나리들,” 그가 말했다. “마차 준비가 되었습니다.”\n\n“그럼,” 프란츠가 말했다. “콜로세움으로 가지.”\n\n“포폴로 문으로 가시겠습니까, 아니면 시가지를 거쳐 가시겠습니까, 나리들?”\n\n“시가지로, 빌어먹을! 시가지로 가!” 프란츠가 외쳤다.\n\n“이런, 친구,” 알베르가 일어나며 세 번째 시가에 불을 붙이고는 말했다. “솔직히 자네가 좀 더 배짱 있는 사람인 줄 알았는데.”\n\n이렇게 말하며 두 젊은이는 계단을 내려가 마차에 올랐다.\n\n# Chapter 34. The Colosseum\n\n프란츠는 콜로세움으로 가는 길을 미리 잘 짚어 둔 덕분에, 마차를 타고 가는 동안 단 하나의 고대 유적도 지나치지 않았다. 그래서 그들이 보러 가는 그 거대한 건축물의 어마어마한 규모를 미리 본 인상이 무디게 만드는 일도 없었다. 그가 고른 길은 시스티나 거리의 연장선이었다. 그런 다음 산타 마리아 마조레 성당이 서 있는 거리의 직각 모퉁이를 가로질러, 우르바나 거리와 산 피에트로 인 빈콜리를 지나면, 여행자들은 곧장 콜로세움과 마주하게 되는 것이었다.\n\n이 경로에는 또 하나의 큰 이점이 있었다. 파스트리니 씨의 이야기를 마음껏 곱씹을 수 있도록 프란츠를 완전히 자유롭게 내버려 둔다는 점이었다. 그 이야기에는 몬테 크리스토의 수수께끼 같은 그 집주인이 어찌나 기묘하게 얽혀 있었던지. 그는 마차 한구석에 팔짱을 끼고 앉아, 조금 전에 들은 그 기이한 이야기를 끊임없이 되새기고, 그 갖가지 정황을 둘러싼 무수한 질문을 스스로에게 던졌으나, 어느 하나에도 만족스러운 답에는 이르지 못했다.\n\n다른 무엇보다도 한 가지 사실이 그의 벗 '뱃사람 신드바드'를 다시 떠올리게 했다. 그것은 산적들과 뱃사람들 사이에 존재하는 듯 보이는 그 묘한 친분이었다. 그리고 밤파가 밀수꾼과 어부들의 배에 몸을 숨겼다는 파스트리니의 이야기는, 프란츠에게 작은 요트의 선원들과 그토록 다정하게 저녁을 들던 두 코르시카 산적을 떠올리게 했다. 그 요트는 오직 그들을 내려놓을 목적으로 항로를 벗어나 포르토베키오에 들르기까지 했었다. 몬테 크리스토의 그 집주인이 스스로 댄 이름, 그리고 런던 호텔 주인이 다시 입에 올린 그 이름은, 그의 섬 친구가 코르시카와 토스카나, 에스파냐의 해안에서뿐 아니라 피옴비노와 치비타 베키아, 오스티아, 가에타의 해안에서도 박애주의자 노릇을 하고 있음을 충분히 입증해 주었다. 게다가 프란츠는 그 기이한 접대자가 튀니스와 팔레르모를 함께 언급하던 것을 떠올렸고, 그것은 그의 교제 범위가 얼마나 넓은지를 증명하는 셈이었다.\n\n그러나 젊은이의 마음이 이런 상념에 아무리 깊이 잠겨 있었다 한들, 그 상념은 어마어마한 콜로세움의 어둡고 음울한 폐허가 눈앞에 나타나는 순간 단번에 흩어졌다. 그 여러 틈새로 창백한 달빛이 비쳐 들며 일렁이는 모습은, 마치 떠도는 망자의 눈에서 새어 나오는 이 세상 것 아닌 빛 같았다. 마차가 메타 수단스 근처에서 멈추었다. 문이 열리고, 두 젊은이가 서둘러 내리자, 안내인 하나와 마주쳤다. 그 등장이 어찌나 뜻밖이었던지 땅에서 솟아난 것만 같았다. 호텔에서 따라온 평소의 안내인까지 뒤에 있었으니, 그들은 두 명의 안내인에게 삯을 치른 셈이었다. 로마에서는 이렇게 넘쳐 나는 안내인들을 피할 도리가 없다. 호텔에 발을 들이는 순간 들러붙어 도시에 머무는 내내 떨어지지 않는 평범한 안내인 말고도, 각 기념물마다, 아니 거의 기념물의 부분 부분마다 따로 딸린 전담 안내인이 있다. 그러니 모든 시대의 경이라 불리는 콜로세움에 안내인이 모자랄 리 없다는 것은 쉽게 짐작할 수 있다. 마르티알리스는 그곳을 이렇게 찬미했다.\n\n\"멤피스로 하여금 그 야만스러운 피라미드의 기적을 자랑하기를 멈추게 하라, 바빌론의 경이도 우리 사이에서 더는 입에 오르지 않게 하라. 모두가 카이사르들의 거대한 노역이 지닌 우월함 앞에 머리를 숙여야 하리니, 명성의 숱한 목소리가 이 비할 데 없는 기념물의 빼어난 공덕을 멀리멀리 퍼뜨리리라.\"\n\n알베르와 프란츠로 말하자면, 그들은 안내인 폭군들에게서 달아나려 들지 않았다. 게다가 그 속박을 끊기란 그만큼 더 어려운 일이었으니, 손에 횃불을 들고 이 기념물들을 둘러볼 수 있는 것은 오직 안내인뿐이었던 것이다. 그리하여 두 젊은이는 저항할 엄두도 내지 않고, 그저 무턱대고 믿음직스럽게 안내인들의 보호와 손길에 자신을 내맡겼다. 프란츠는 이미 일고여덟 번이나 콜로세움을 비슷하게 둘러본 적이 있었던 반면, 그보다 운이 덜 따른 그의 동행은 난생처음으로 플라비우스 베스파시아누스의 기념물을 이루는 그 고전의 땅을 밟는 것이었다. 그리고 그를 위해 한마디 해 두자면, 안내인들의 매끄러운 수다 한가운데서도 그의 마음은 눈앞에 펼쳐진 모든 것에 마땅하고도 깊은 경외와 열렬한 감탄으로 물들었다. 이 어마어마한 폐허에 대한 합당한 관념은 직접 가 본 사람, 더구나 달빛 아래에서 본 사람이 아니고서는 도저히 가질 수 없는 것이다. 그 시각이면 건물의 광대한 규모는 남국의 달빛 어린 하늘이 뿜는 신비로운 빛줄기 아래에서 두 배는 더 커 보인다. 그 빛은 서녘의 부드러운 황혼에 견줄 만한 빛으로 지평선을 물들일 만큼 맑고 또렷하다.\n\n그러니 사색에 잠긴 프란츠가 폐허의 안쪽 주랑 아래로 백 걸음도 채 걷기 전에, 그는 알베르를 안내인들 손에 내맡겨 버렸다. (안내인들은 자기들이 으레 정해 놓고 그대로 따르는 순서대로 희생양을 끌고 다닐 관습적 권리를 결코 양보하려 들지 않았고, 아무것도 모르는 방문객을 항변의 여지조차 없는 끈질김으로 이런저런 볼거리 앞으로 끌고 다녔다. 으레 그렇듯 '사자 우리'에서 시작해 '검투사의 방'을 거쳐 '카이사르의 귀빈석'에서 마무리하는 식이었다.) 자신을 둘러싼 경이들에 대한 그 횡설수설과 기계적인 둘러보기에서 벗어나기 위해, 프란츠는 반쯤 허물어진 계단을 올랐다. 그러고는 안내인들이 그 단조로운 순회를 계속하도록 내버려 둔 채, 기둥 하나의 밑동에 자리 잡고 앉았다. 그 맞은편에는 커다란 틈이 나 있어, 그는 그 장엄한 폐허의 거대한 규모를 온전하고도 거침없이 바라볼 수 있었다.\n\n프란츠는 자기가 발견한 쉼터인 그 거대한 기둥의 밑동, 그 그늘에 완전히 몸을 숨긴 채 거의 십오 분 가까이 머물러 있었다. 거기서 그의 눈은 알베르와 그 안내인들의 움직임을 좇았다. 그들은 손에 횃불을 들고 콜로세움 반대편 끝의 한 출입구에서 나타났다가, 다시 베스타 처녀들에게 마련되었던 좌석으로 통하는 계단을 따라 사라졌다. 미끄러지듯 나아가는 그 모습은, 마치 도깨비불의 일렁이는 불빛을 뒤쫓는 안절부절못하는 망령들 같았다. 문득 그의 귀에 어떤 소리가 들렸다. 그가 올라온 계단의 맞은편 계단을 따라 돌멩이 하나가 굴러떨어지는 듯한 소리였다. 화강암 조각이 떨어져 나가 아래로 무겁게 굴러내리는 일에는 별달리 이상할 게 없었다. 하지만 그에게는 떨어진 그 물체가 어떤 발의 무게에 눌려 굴러떨어진 것처럼 느껴졌고, 또 누군가가 발소리를 들키지 않으려 한껏 애쓰면서 그가 앉아 있는 곳으로 다가오고 있는 것만 같았다.\n\n짐작은 곧 확신이 되었다. 한 남자의 형체가 프란츠의 눈에 또렷이 들어왔던 것이다. 맞은편 계단에서 차츰 모습을 드러내는 그 위로, 그 순간 달이 은빛 광채를 가득 쏟아붓고 있었다.\n\n이렇게 모습을 드러낸 이방인은 아마도 프란츠처럼, 안내인들의 시시한 수다보다는 고독과 자기 생각을 즐기기를 더 좋아하는 사람인 듯했다. 그의 모습에는 별난 데가 없었다. 그러나 한 걸음 한 걸음 내디딜 때마다 멈춰 서서 불안한 주의를 기울여 귀 기울이는 그 망설임으로 미루어, 프란츠는 그가 누군가의 도착을 기다리고 있음을 확신했다.\n\n본능적인 충동에 이끌려 프란츠는 기둥 뒤로 한껏 몸을 물렸다.\n\n그와 이방인이 있는 곳에서 열 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳은 지붕이 내려앉아, 둥글고 커다란 구멍이 나 있었다. 그 너머로는 별들이 촘촘히 박힌 푸른 하늘의 천장이 보였다.\n\n이 구멍 둘레에는, 어쩌면 오랜 세월 동안 지금 저 거대한 더미를 비추는 찬란한 달빛이 자유로이 들어오도록 길을 내주었을 그 구멍 둘레에는, 덩굴식물이 무성하게 자라 있었다. 그 가녀린 초록 가지들이 맑고 푸른 창공을 배경으로 또렷이 도드라졌고, 한편 굵고 질긴 섬유질 새순 덩어리들이 갈라진 틈을 비집고 나와 이리저리 흔들리며 늘어져, 마치 출렁이는 끈 가닥들 같았다.\n\n수수께끼 같은 등장으로 프란츠의 주의를 끈 그 사람은 일종의 어스름 속에 서 있어, 옷차림은 쉽게 알아볼 수 있었으나 얼굴은 도무지 분간할 수 없었다. 그는 커다란 갈색 망토를 두르고 있었는데, 그 한 자락을 왼쪽 어깨에 걸쳐 얼굴 아랫부분까지 가리고 있었고, 위쪽은 챙 넓은 모자에 완전히 가려져 있었다. 옷차림의 아랫부분은 부서진 천장을 통해 들어온 환한 달빛 덕분에 더 또렷이 보였다. 그 눈부신 빛줄기가 윤이 나는 가죽으로 멋들어지게 지은 장화에 싸인 발을 비추었고, 그 위로는 검은 천으로 유행에 맞게 재단한 바지가 내려와 있었다.\n\n프란츠가 가진 불완전한 판단의 수단으로는 단 하나의 결론에 이를 수밖에 없었다. 이렇게 지켜보는 인물이 결코 비천한 신분에 속한 사람은 아니라는 것이었다.\n\n몇 분이 흘렀고, 이방인이 뚜렷이 초조한 기색을 보이기 시작할 무렵, 지붕의 그 틈 바깥에서 희미한 소리가 들렸다. 그러더니 거의 곧바로 어두운 그림자 하나가 그리로 들어오던 빛의 물결을 가로막는 듯했고, 한 남자의 형체가 아래의 그 광대한 공간을 열심히 살피며 내려다보는 모습이 또렷이 보였다. 이윽고 그의 눈이 망토를 두른 사내를 알아채자, 그는 빽빽이 엉킨 가지 덩어리를 움켜쥐고 그것을 의지해 땅에서 서너 발쯤 되는 높이까지 미끄러져 내려오더니, 가볍게 발을 디디고 뛰어내렸다. 이토록 대담한 짓을 그토록 태연하게 해낸 그 남자는 트라스테베레 차림을 하고 있었다.\n\n\"각하를 기다리시게 해 죄송합니다.\" 사내가 로마 사투리로 말했다. \"그래도 약속 시간에서 몇 분쯤밖에 늦지 않았다고 봅니다. 산 조반니 인 라테라노 종이 방금 열 시를 쳤으니까요.\"\n\n\"늦었다는 말은 한마디도 마라.\" 이방인이 더없이 순수한 토스카나 말로 대답했다. \"내가 너무 일찍 온 게다. 설령 네가 나를 잠시 기다리게 했다 한들, 그 지체가 네 잘못 때문이 아니라는 것은 충분히 확신했을 게다.\"\n\n\"각하께서 그리 생각하시니 참으로 옳으십니다.\" 사내가 말했다. \"저는 산탄젤로성에서 곧장 이리로 왔습니다만, 베포와 말 한마디 나눌 틈을 얻기까지 엄청나게 애를 먹었습니다.\"\n\n\"베포가 누구냐?\"\n\n\"아, 베포는 감옥에서 일하는 자입니다. 제가 일 년에 얼마씩 쥐여 주고 교황 성하의 성안에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 알려 달라고 합니다.\"\n\n\"그래! 보아하니 너는 앞날을 챙기는 사람이로구나.\"\n\n\"그게, 아시다시피 무슨 일이 닥칠지 누가 알겠습니까. 어쩌면 어느 날 가엾은 페피노처럼 저도 덫에 걸릴지 모르고, 그러면 제 그물코를 갉아 저를 감옥에서 꺼내 줄 자그마한 생쥐가 있다는 게 무척 반가울지도 모르지요.\"\n\n\"간단히 말해, 무엇을 알아냈느냐?\"\n\n\"적잖이 흥미로운 처형 두 건이 모레 두 시에 거행된다는 겁니다. 로마에서 큰 축제가 시작될 때면 으레 그러하듯이요. 죄인 하나는 마촐라토●를 당할 겁니다. 그자는 자기를 길러 준 신부를 죽인 흉악한 악당이니 손톱만큼의 동정도 받을 자격이 없지요. 다른 하나는 데카피타토●●형을 선고받았는데, 각하, 그자가 바로 가엾은 페피노입니다.\"\n\n* 마촐라토: 둔기로 머리를 쳐 죽이는 처형. ●● 데카피타토: 참수형.—옮긴이\n\"사실, 너는 교황청 정부뿐 아니라 인근의 여러 나라에까지 어찌나 극심한 두려움을 심어 놓았는지, 그들은 본보기를 보일 기회라면 무엇이든 반기는 게다.\"\n\n\"하지만 페피노는 제 패거리에 속하지도 않았습니다. 그저 우리에게 식량을 대 준 것이 유일한 죄인 가난한 양치기였을 뿐이지요.\"\n\n\"바로 그 점이 어느 모로 보나 그를 네 공범으로 만드는 게다. 하지만 그가 받는 대접의 차이를 잘 보아라. 그들이 너를 한번 붙잡으면 머리를 쳐 죽일 텐데, 그자는 그저 단두대형을 선고받았을 뿐이다. 게다가 그렇게 함으로써 그날의 구경거리도 다채로워지고, 어느 구경꾼이든 만족시킬 볼거리가 생기는 셈이지.\"\n\n\"제가 그들을 놀래 주려고 준비하고 있는, 전혀 뜻밖의 구경거리는 셈에 넣지 않으셔도요.\"\n\n\"여보게.\" 망토를 두른 사내가 말했다. \"이런 말 해서 미안하네만, 자네는 지금 무슨 무모하거나 터무니없는 짓을 저지를 기분에 딱 사로잡혀 있는 것 같군.\"\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지요. 하지만 한 가지 결심한 게 있습니다. 오직 저를 위해 일하다 이 곤경에 빠진 가엾은 자를 풀어 주기 위해서라면 무슨 짓이든 마다하지 않겠다는 것입니다. 이 다급한 처지에 놓인 그 용감한 친구를 저버린다면, 저는 비겁자인 제 자신을 미워하고 경멸하게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그래서 어쩔 작정인가?\"\n\n\"제 부하 가운데 가장 뛰어난 스무 명으로 처형대를 에워싸려 합니다. 그들은 제 신호에 맞추어, 페피노가 처형장으로 끌려 나오는 즉시 앞으로 달려들어, 단검의 힘을 빌려 호위병들을 밀어내고 죄수를 빼낼 겁니다.\"\n\n\"내가 보기에 그건 위험천만한 데다 불확실하기까지 해서, 내 계획이 자네 것보다 훨씬 낫다는 확신만 들게 하는군.\"\n\n\"각하의 계획은 무엇입니까?\"\n\n\"바로 이것이네. 내가 2천 피아스트르를 아주 요긴하게 쓰면, 그 돈을 받은 자가 페피노에게 내년까지 형 집행 유예를 얻어 줄 걸세. 그리고 그 한 해 동안, 솜씨 좋게 또 한 번 1천 피아스트르를 쓰면 그가 감옥에서 빠져나올 방도가 마련될 게야.\"\n\n\"그래서 성공을 확신하십니까?\"\n\n\"파르디외!\" 망토를 두른 사내가 별안간 프랑스어로 내뱉었다.\n\n\"각하, 무어라 하셨습니까?\" 상대가 물었다.\n\n\"이런 말을 했네, 여보게. 자네와 자네의 온 패거리가 단검에 권총, 기병총, 나팔총까지 다 동원해 해낼 수 있는 것보다, 내가 황금의 힘으로 혼자서 더 많은 일을 해내겠다고 말이야. 그러니 일은 내게 맡기고, 결과는 걱정 말게.\"\n\n\"적어도 각하께서 실패하실 경우에 대비해, 저와 제 패거리가 채비를 갖추고 있는 것은 해될 게 없겠지요.\"\n\n\"전혀 해될 게 없네. 그렇게 하는 게 자네 마음이 놓인다면, 좋을 대로 대비책을 마련해 두게. 하지만 내가 구하려는 그 유예는 반드시 얻어 낼 테니 믿게나.\"\n\n\"처형이 모레로 잡혀 있고, 각하께 주어진 시간이 하루뿐이라는 걸 잊지 마십시오.\"\n\n\"그게 어떻단 말인가? 하루는 스물네 시간으로 나뉘고, 한 시간은 육십 분으로, 또 일 분은 육십 초로 잘게 나뉘지 않나? 8만 6천 4백 초면 참으로 숱한 일을 해낼 수 있다네.\"\n\n\"각하께서 성공하셨는지 아닌지는 제가 어떻게 알 수 있습니까?\"\n\n\"아, 그건 아주 쉽게 일러 둘 수 있네. 내가 카페 로스폴리의 아래층 창문 셋을 빌려 두었네. 페피노에게 필요한 사면을 얻어 냈다면, 바깥쪽 창 둘에는 노란 다마스크 천을 걸고, 가운데 창에는 흰 천에 커다란 붉은 십자가를 그려서 걸 걸세.\"\n\n\"그럼 처형을 지휘하는 관리에게 그 유예장을 누구를 시켜 전하실 작정이십니까?\"\n\n\"자네 부하 하나를 참회 수사로 변장시켜 보내게. 그러면 내가 그에게 그것을 건네지. 그 옷차림 덕에 그는 처형대 바로 곁까지 다가갈 수 있을 테고, 공식 명령서를 관리에게 전하면, 관리는 다시 그것을 사형 집행인에게 넘기겠지. 그동안 페피노에게도 우리가 결정한 바를 알려 두는 게 좋겠네. 적어도 그가 겁에 질려 죽거나 정신을 잃지 않게는 해야지. 어느 쪽이든 그렇게 되면 아주 쓸데없는 돈을 쓴 셈이 될 테니까.\"\n\n\"각하.\" 사내가 말했다. \"제가 각하께 온전히 헌신하고 있다는 것을 충분히 믿으시지요, 그렇지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"암, 그 점은 의심의 여지가 없다고 자부하네.\" 망토를 두른 그 신사가 대답했다.\n\n\"그러시다면, 페피노를 구해 주시겠다는 약속만 지켜 주십시오. 그러면 이제부터 각하께서는 저와 제 부하들로부터 헌신뿐만 아니라, 한 인간이 다른 인간에게 바칠 수 있는 가장 절대적인 복종까지 받으실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"섣불리 약속을 다짐하지 말게, 여보게. 어쩌면 그리 멀지 않은 어느 날, 이번엔 내가 자네의 도움과 영향력을 필요로 할 때, 그 약속을 상기시킬지도 모르니까.\"\n\n\"그날이 이르건 늦건, 각하께서는 이 무거운 곤경 속에서 제가 각하를 본 그대로의 각하를 보시게 될 겁니다. 세상 저 끝에서라도 이러저러한 일을 하라고 한마디만 적어 보내시면, 그건 다 된 일로 여기셔도 좋습니다. 반드시 해낼 테니까요. 제 이름과 신의를 걸고——\"\n\n\"쉿!\" 이방인이 말을 가로막았다. \"무슨 소리가 들리는군.\"\n\n\"횃불을 들고 콜로세움을 구경하는 여행객들일 겁니다.\"\n\n\"우리가 함께 있는 모습을 들키지 않는 게 좋겠네. 저 안내인들은 첩자나 다름없어서, 어쩌면 자네를 알아볼지도 모르니까. 여보게, 자네의 우정으로 내가 아무리 영광스럽다 한들, 우리의 친분이 얼마나 두터운지 한번 알려지는 날이면, 내 평판도 신용도 그로 인해 몹시 상하지 않을까 심히 두렵네.\"\n\n\"그럼 유예를 얻어 내시면요?\"\n\n\"카페 로스폴리의 가운데 창에 붉은 십자가를 새긴 흰 다마스크 천을 걸겠네.\"\n\n\"실패하시면요?\"\n\n\"그럼 창 셋 모두에 노란 휘장을 걸지.\"\n\n\"그러면요?\"\n\n\"그러면, 여보게, 자네 좋을 대로 단검을 쓰게. 그리고 한 가지 더 약속하지. 내가 그 자리에 자네의 무용을 지켜보는 구경꾼으로 가 있겠다고 말일세.\"\n\n\"그럼 우리는 서로를 완벽히 이해한 셈이군요. 안녕히 가십시오, 각하. 제가 각하를 믿는 만큼 굳게 저를 믿어 주십시오.\"\n\n이 말을 하고서 트라스테베레 사내는 계단 아래로 사라졌고, 그의 동행은 전보다 더 단단히 망토 자락에 얼굴을 파묻고 프란츠의 거의 코앞을 스쳐 지나, 바깥쪽 계단을 따라 원형 경기장으로 내려갔다. 다음 순간 프란츠는 알베르가 자기를 부르는 소리를 들었다. 그 드높은 건물이 벗의 이름을 메아리치게 울리고 있었다. 그러나 프란츠는 자기가 엿들은 대화의 두 남자가 내려가는 길에 마주치지 않을 만큼 충분히 멀어졌다고 확신할 때까지 그 부름에 응하지 않았다. 이방인들이 떠난 지 십 분 뒤, 프란츠는 스페인 광장으로 가는 길에 올라, 알베르가 플리니우스와 칼푸르니우스의 방식을 따라 늘어놓는 학식 어린 강론에 짐짓 무심한 척 귀를 기울이고 있었다. 사나운 짐승들이 구경꾼들에게 달려드는 것을 막으려고 쓰던 쇠창 박힌 그물에 관한 이야기였다.\n\n프란츠는 그가 끊임없이 떠들도록 내버려 두었으나, 실은 그 말을 한마디도 듣고 있지 않았다. 그는 혼자가 되어, 방금 일어난 모든 일을 마음껏 곱씹고 싶었다. 콜로세움에서 우연히 목격한 그 수수께끼 같은 만남의 두 남자 가운데 하나는 그에게 전혀 낯선 사람이었으나, 다른 하나는 그렇지 않았다. 비록 프란츠는 그 얼굴을 분간할 수 없었지만—그가 망토에 싸여 있었거나 그늘에 가려 있었던 탓이었다—그 목소리의 음색만큼은 처음 들었을 때부터 그에게 너무도 강렬한 인상을 남긴 터라, 언제 어디서 다시 듣든 결코 잊을 수 없을 것이었다. 특히 그 남자가 반쯤은 농담조로, 반쯤은 쓰디쓴 어조로 말할 때면, 프란츠의 귀는 몬테 크리스토의 동굴에서 자기에게 말을 건넸던 그 깊고 낭랑하면서도 잘 다듬어진 목소리를 가장 또렷이 떠올렸다. 그리고 이제 그 목소리를 콜로세움의 어둠과 폐허가 된 장엄함 한가운데서 두 번째로 들은 것이었다. 그리고 생각하면 할수록, 망토를 두른 그 사람이 다름 아닌 자신의 옛 집주인이자 접대자였던 '뱃사람 신드바드'라는 확신은 더욱 굳어졌다.\n\n다른 어떤 상황에서였다면, 프란츠는 그토록 기이한 인물에 대해 더 알고 싶은 극심한 호기심을 도저히 억누르지 못하고, 그 짧은 친분을 다시 잇고자 했을 것이다. 하지만 지금 이 경우에는, 자기가 엿들은 대화의 은밀한 성격으로 미루어, 이런 때에 자기가 나타나는 것은 결코 달가운 일이 아니리라고 분별 있게 판단했다. 그리하여 앞서 보았듯이, 그는 자기 옛 집주인이 알은체도 하지 않고 물러가도록 내버려 두었으나, 만약 우연히 또 한 번 기회가 주어진다면 지금 이 자제에 대해 톡톡히 보상받으리라고 단단히 다짐했다.\n\n프란츠는 자기를 덮쳐 오는 그 숱한 어지러운 생각들을 떨쳐 내려 했으나 헛수고였고, 잠의 안식을 청해 보았으나 그 또한 부질없었다. 잠은 그의 눈꺼풀을 찾아오기를 마다했고, 밤은 콜로세움의 그 수수께끼 같은 방문객과 몬테 크리스토 동굴의 거주자가 동일 인물임을 입증하는 일련의 정황을 열에 들떠 곱씹는 가운데 흘러갔다. 그리고 생각하면 할수록 그 문제에 대한 그의 의견은 더욱 확고해졌다.\n\n마침내 기진맥진한 그는 동틀 무렵에야 잠이 들어, 늦도록 깨어나지 못했다. 천생 프랑스 사람인 알베르는 그동안 저녁의 놀이를 마련하느라 시간을 썼다. 그는 사람을 보내 아르젠티나 극장의 특별석을 잡아 두었다. 그리고 프란츠는 써야 할 편지가 여러 통 있던 터라, 하루 종일 마차를 알베르에게 양보했다.\n\n다섯 시에 알베르가 하루 일에 흡족해하며 돌아왔다. 그는 소개장들을 돌리며 시간을 보냈고, 그 답례로 도저히 다 받아들일 수 없을 만큼 많은 무도회와 야회 초대를 받았다. 그뿐 아니라 그는 (그의 표현을 빌리자면) 로마의 볼 만한 명소를 죄다 보고 왔다. 그렇다, 그는 단 하루 만에, 더 진중한 그의 동행이라면 몇 주는 걸렸을 일을 해치운 것이다. 또한 그는 그날 밤 아르젠티나 극장에서 공연될 작품의 이름과, 거기 출연하는 배우들이 누구인지 알아내는 것도 빠뜨리지 않았다. 오페라 〈파리지나〉의 상연이 예고되어 있었고, 주역 배우들은 코셀리와 모리아니, 라 스페키아였다.\n\n그러니 두 젊은이는 이탈리아에서 가장 이름난 성악가 셋의 뒷받침을 받아 〈람메르무어의 루치아〉를 작곡한 그 작곡가의 가장 빼어난 작품 가운데 하나를 들을 기회를 얻은 자신들을 운 좋다고 여길 만했다.\n\n알베르는 도무지 이탈리아 극장을 견뎌 내질 못했다. 무대가 보이지 않는 위치의 관현악단이며, 발코니나 트인 특별석이 없는 것이며—이 모든 결점이, 부프 극장에 자기 좌석을 두고 오페라 극장의 아래층 특별석을 함께 쓰던 사내에게는 견디기 힘들었다. 그래도 이런데도 불구하고, 알베르는 극장에 갈 때마다 자기의 가장 눈부시고 효과적인 의상을 뽐냈다. 그러나 아아, 그의 우아한 차림새는 온전히 헛수고였고, 파리 멋쟁이의 가장 훌륭한 본보기 가운데 하나는 단 한 번의 모험도 만나지 못한 채 이탈리아를 거의 다 돌아 버렸다는 굴욕적인 상념을 떠안아야 했다.\n\n이따금 알베르는 자기의 성공 없음을 짐짓 농담거리로 삼곤 했다. 하지만 속으로는 깊이 상처를 입었고, 당대 어느 젊은이보다 사랑받고 쫓김을 받던 알베르 드 모르세르가 이렇게 외면당하고 그저 헛수고만 했다는 생각에 그의 자존심은 몹시 짓밟혔다. 게다가 그 일이 더더욱 분했던 까닭은, 프랑스 사람 특유의 그 겸손함에 따라, 알베르는 이탈리아에서는 자기가 모습을 드러내기만 하면 모든 것을 휩쓸 것이며, 돌아가면 자신의 숱한 연애담을 늘어놓아 파리 사교계를 놀라게 하리라고 굳게 확신한 채 파리를 떠나왔기 때문이었다.\n\n아아, 가엾은 알베르! 그 흥미진진한 모험들 가운데 어느 하나도 그의 앞길에 떨어지지 않았다. 어여쁜 제노바와 피렌체, 나폴리의 여인들은, 남편에게는 아닐지언정 적어도 정부에게는 모두 충실했고, 알베르 드 모르세르의 그 화려한 모습을 보고서도 상대를 바꿀 생각은 하지 않았다. 그가 얻은 것이라곤, 이탈리아 여인들이 프랑스 여인들보다 한 가지 점에서 낫다는 쓰라린 확신뿐이었다. 그들은 부정을 저지를 때조차 충실하다는 것이었다.\n\n그래도 그는 이탈리아에서도 다른 곳에서처럼, 그 일반적인 법칙에 예외가 있을지 모른다는 희망만은 억누를 수 없었다.\n\n알베르는 우아하고 잘생긴 젊은이일 뿐 아니라, 상당한 재능과 능력도 갖추고 있었다. 게다가 그는 자작이었다—물론 최근에 작위를 받은 자작이긴 했지만, 오늘날에는 가문의 내력을 노아까지 거슬러 올라가 따질 필요가 없으며, 가계도란 1399년에서 시작되었건 그저 1815년에서 시작되었건 똑같이 높이 쳐주는 법이다. 그러나 이 모든 이점에 화룡점정을 더하자면, 알베르 드 모르세르는 5만 리브르의 수입을 거느리고 있었으니, 파리에서 그를 상당히 중요한 인물로 만들기에 차고도 넘치는 액수였다. 그러니 이탈리아의 주요 도시 대부분을 둘러보고도 가장 사소한 눈길조차 끌지 못했다는 것은 그에게 적잖은 굴욕이었다.\n\n그러나 알베르는 사육제 동안에 이 모든 무시와 냉대를 보상받으리라 기대하고 있었다. 이 축제가 거행되는 여러 나라와 왕국들 가운데, 가장 지혜롭고 진중한 이들조차 평소의 그 엄격함을 벗어던지고 기꺼이 이 자유와 방종의 시절의 흥청거림에 끼어드는 곳이 바로 로마라는 것을 그는 잘 알고 있었던 것이다. 사육제는 이튿날 시작될 참이었으므로, 알베르로서는 자신의 희망과 기대, 그리고 주목받을 권리의 계획표를 펼쳐 놓는 데 한순간도 허비할 수 없었다.\n\n이런 속셈으로 그는 극장에서 가장 눈에 잘 띄는 자리에 특별석을 잡았고, 더없이 화려하고 공들인 차림새의 힘을 빌려 자신의 매력을 한껏 돋보이게 하려고 애썼다. 알베르가 잡은 특별석은 첫째 층에 있었다. 세 층의 특별석 모두가 똑같이 귀족적인 것으로 여겨져, 그 때문에 흔히 '귀족석'이라 불리긴 하지만, 그리고 두 벗을 위해 잡은 그 특별석이 적어도 열두 명은 너끈히 들어갈 만큼 넓었지만, 그 값은 프랑스의 어느 극장에서 고작 네 사람 드는 자리에 치를 값보다도 적게 들었다.\n\n알베르가 자리를 고르는 데에는 또 다른 동기도 작용했다. 이렇게 좋은 위치에 자리 잡으면, 어느 어여쁜 로마 여인의 눈길을 정말로 끌게 될지, 그래서 소개로 이어져 마차 한자리나 어느 귀족의 발코니 한 자리를 권유받게 되어, 거기서 사육제의 흥겨운 광경을 굽어보게 될지 누가 알겠는가? 이 여러 가지 생각이 한데 어우러져, 알베르는 여태껏보다도 더 활기차고 환심을 사려 안달이 났다. 무대 위의 일은 아예 거들떠보지도 않고, 그는 특별석에서 몸을 내밀어 강력한 오페라글라스의 도움을 받아 가며 예쁜 여인 하나하나의 미모를 열심히 뜯어보기 시작했다. 그러나 아아, 눈길을 끌어 보려던 이 시도는 완전히 실패했다. 호기심조차 동하지 않았으니, 그가 그 환심 안으로 슬며시 비집고 들고 싶어 한 그 사랑스러운 여인들은 하나같이 자기 자신이나 자기 정부, 혹은 자기 생각에 어찌나 골몰해 있던지, 그도, 그가 안경을 만지작거리는 모습도 거들떠보지 않았다는 것이 너무도 분명했다.\n\n사실인즉, 사육제와 그 뒤를 이을 '성주간'의 기대되는 즐거움이 어여쁜 여인들의 가슴마다 어찌나 가득 차 있었던지, 무대 위의 일에조차 조금의 관심도 기울이지 못하게 만든 것이었다. 배우들은 아무도 알아채거나 마음 쓰는 이 없이 등장하고 퇴장했다. 다만 어떤 관습적인 순간이 되면, 관객들은 갑자기 대화를 멈추거나 상념에서 깨어나, 모리아니의 어떤 빼어난 열창이나 코셀리의 잘 다듬어진 서창에 귀를 기울이거나, 라 스페키아의 놀라운 기량에 우레와 같은 갈채를 함께 보내곤 했다. 그러나 그 잠깐의 흥분이 가시면, 그들은 이내 다시금 본래의 그 골몰함이나 흥미로운 대화에 빠져들었다.\n\n1막이 끝나갈 무렵, 여태 비어 있던 한 특별석의 문이 열렸다. 한 부인이 들어왔는데, 프란츠가 파리에서 소개받은 적이 있는 사람이었다. 실은 그는 그녀가 아직 거기 있으리라 여기고 있었다. 알베르의 날카로운 눈은 벗이 그 새 도착한 이를 보고 저도 모르게 흠칫하는 모습을 놓치지 않았다. 그래서 그쪽으로 돌아서며 다급히 말했다.\n\n\"방금 저 특별석에 들어온 여인을 아나?\"\n\n\"그래. 어떻게 생각하나?\"\n\n\"오, 더없이 사랑스럽군—저 피부 좀 보게! 게다가 저 멋진 머리카락이라니! 프랑스 사람인가?\"\n\n\"아니, 베네치아 사람이야.\"\n\n\"그럼 이름이——\"\n\n\"G백작 부인이지.\"\n\n\"아, 이름은 알고 있네!\" 알베르가 외쳤다. \"미모만큼이나 재치와 영민함을 갖춘 분이라고들 하더군. 빌포르 부인의 무도회에서 그분을 만났을 때 소개받기로 되어 있었지.\"\n\n\"자네의 그 소홀함을 바로잡아 주는 일을 내가 도와줄까?\" 프란츠가 물었다.\n\n\"이보게, 자네 정말 저분과 그렇게 가까운 사이라서 나를 저분의 특별석으로 데려갈 엄두까지 낼 수 있는 건가?\"\n\n\"뭐, 내 평생 저분과 같은 자리에 있으면서 이야기를 나눈 영광을 누린 게 서너 번뿐이긴 하네만, 자네도 알다시피 그 정도 친분이라도 자네가 청하는 일을 할 명분은 될 테지.\"\n\n바로 그 순간, 백작 부인이 프란츠를 알아보고는 우아하게 손을 흔들어 보였고, 그는 정중하게 고개를 숙여 답했다. \"정말이지,\" 알베르가 말했다. \"자네는 저 아름다운 백작 부인과 더없이 좋은 사이인 것 같군.\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각한다면 자네가 틀린 걸세.\" 프란츠가 차분히 대꾸했다. \"자네는 그저 우리 동포들 가운데 그토록 많은 이가 어처구니없는 실수를 저지르게 만드는 그 똑같은 오류에 빠져 있을 뿐이야—이탈리아와 에스파냐의 습속과 관례를 우리 파리식 관념으로 재단하는 오류 말이지. 내 말을 믿게, 사람들이 격의 없는 말투를 주고받는다고 해서 그들 사이에 있으리라 짐작되는 친분의 정도를 가늠하는 것만큼 그릇된 일도 없네. 지금 이 순간 우리와 백작 부인 사이에는 어떤 감정의 닮음이 있을 뿐이야—그 이상은 아무것도 없네.\"\n\n\"정말 그런가, 이 사람아? 어디 말해 보게, 그게 마음의 공감인가?\"\n\n\"아니, 취향의 공감이지.\" 프란츠가 진지하게 말을 이었다.\n\n\"한데 그 마음의 어울림이라는 게 어떤 식으로 드러났나?\"\n\n\"백작 부인이 콜로세움을 찾았는데, 우리가 어젯밤 그랬듯이 달빛 아래 거의 혼자서 갔다는 걸세.\"\n\n\"그럼 자네가 그분과 함께 있었나?\"\n\n\"그랬지.\"\n\n\"그래, 그분께 무슨 말을 했나?\"\n\n\"오, 그 장엄한 폐허가 영광스러운 기념물로 남은, 그 이름난 망자들에 대해 이야기했지!\"\n\n\"정말이지,\" 알베르가 외쳤다. \"콜로세움 같은 그토록 감상적인 장소에서 아름다운 여인과 단둘이, 아니 거의 단둘이 있으면서, 고작 망자 이야기 말고는 더 나은 화제를 찾지 못했다니, 자네 참 흥겨운 동행이었겠군! 내가 할 수 있는 말이라곤, 나라면 만약 그런 기회를 얻는다면, 살아 있는 사람을 화제로 삼았을 거라는 것뿐이야.\"\n\n\"그리고 자네는 아마 그 화제를 잘못 골랐다는 걸 알게 될 걸세.\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 알베르가 그의 말허리를 자르며 말했다. \"지난 일은 신경 쓰지 말고, 현재만 기억하세. 우리가 이야기한 그 어여쁜 여인에게 나를 소개해 주겠다던 약속을 지킬 셈인가, 안인가?\"\n\n\"물론이지, 무대의 막이 내리는 즉시 말이야.\"\n\n\"이 1막은 어찌 이리도 지긋지긋하게 긴지. 정말이지, 끝낼 생각이 도무지 없는 것 같군.\"\n\n\"오, 아닐세, 끝낼 거야. 저 매혹적인 피날레나 좀 들어 보게. 코셀리가 자기 배역을 얼마나 절묘하게 노래하는지.\"\n\n\"하지만 저자는 어찌 저리 어설프고 볼품없는 사내인지.\"\n\n\"그래, 그럼 라 스페키아는 어떤가? 저보다 더 완벽한 연기를 본 적 있나?\"\n\n\"이보게, 알다시피 한번 말리브랑이나 존타크에 익숙해진 사람한테는, 저런 가수들이 다른 이들에게 줄지 모를 인상을 똑같이 주지는 못하는 법이라네.\"\n\n\"적어도 모리아니의 양식과 가창만큼은 감탄해야 할 걸세.\"\n\n\"저렇게 거무튀튀하고 둔중하게 생긴 사내가 여자 같은 목소리로 노래하는 건 도무지 상상이 안 가서 말이야.\"\n\n\"이 사람아,\" 프란츠가 그에게로 돌아서며 말했다. 그러는 동안에도 알베르는 극장의 특별석마다 자기 안경을 겨누고 있었다. \"자네는 무엇 하나 마음에 들어 하지 않기로 작정한 모양이군. 자네는 정말이지 비위 맞추기가 너무 까다로워.\"\n\n마침내 공연의 막이 내렸고, 모르세르 자작은 더없이 흡족해했다. 그는 모자를 집어 들고, 머리카락을 손가락으로 잽싸게 쓸어 넘기고, 크라바트와 소맷동을 매만지고는, 프란츠에게 앞장서 줄 것을 기다리고 있다는 뜻을 내비쳤다.\n\n프란츠는 눈짓으로 백작 부인에게 물어 그녀로부터 환영한다는 표시로 우아한 미소를 받은 터라, 알베르의 안달 난 조바심을 채워 주는 일을 미루려 하지 않았다. 그는 곧장 객석을 돌기 시작했고, 알베르가 바짝 뒤따랐다. 알베르는 극장 반대편에 이르기까지의 그 짧은 몇 분을 이용해 옷깃의 높이와 매끄러움을 가다듬고 윗옷 자락을 정돈했다. 백작 부인의 특별석에 다다랐을 때, 이 중대한 작업은 막 끝난 참이었다.\n\n노크 소리에 문이 곧바로 열렸고, 이탈리아 관습에 따라 백작 부인 곁에 앉아 있던 젊은이가 즉시 일어나 손님들에게 자리를 내주었다. 그 손님들 또한 다른 방문객이 오면 물러가야 할 처지였다. 프란츠는 알베르를, 사교계에서의 지위로 보나 비범한 재능으로 보나 당대 가장 뛰어난 젊은이의 한 사람으로 소개했다. 그리고 그가 한 말은 사실 이상도 아니었으니, 파리와 자작이 드나드는 그 사교계에서 그는 완벽함의 본보기로 여겨지고 거론되었던 것이다. 프란츠는 덧붙이기를, 자기 동행이 백작 부인이 파리에 머무는 동안 소개받는 영광을 누리지 못한 것을 몹시 애석해하던 차에, 그것을 만회하고 싶어 안달이 나, 자기(프란츠)에게 그를 부인의 특별석으로 안내함으로써 지난 불운을 바로잡아 달라고 청했노라고 했다. 그러고는 주제넘게 그 일을 떠맡은 데 대해 용서를 구하며 말을 맺었다.\n\n백작 부인은 그 답으로 알베르에게 우아하게 고개 숙여 인사하고는, 프란츠에게는 다정한 친절을 담아 손을 내밀었다. 그런 다음 알베르에게 자기 곁의 빈자리에 앉기를 권하고, 프란츠에게는 발레를 보고 싶다면 그다음으로 좋은 자리에 앉으라며 자기 의자 뒤쪽의 자리를 가리켰다.\n\n알베르는 이내 파리와 파리의 이런저런 일들을 논하는 데 깊이 빠져들어, 두 사람이 그곳에서 함께 아는 여러 인물에 대해 백작 부인에게 이야기했다. 프란츠는 그가 얼마나 완전히 제 세상을 만났는지를 알아챘다. 그래서 그가 그토록 분명히 누리는 즐거움을 방해하고 싶지 않아, 알베르의 안경을 집어 들고 이번에는 자기 차례로 관객석을 둘러보기 시작했다.\n\n바로 맞은편이지만 셋째 줄에 자리 잡은 한 특별석 앞쪽에, 한 여인이 혼자 앉아 있었다. 그리스풍 의상을 차려입은 더없이 아리따운 여인이었는데, 그것을 걸친 자연스러움과 우아함으로 미루어 그 옷이 그녀의 민족 의상임이 분명했다. 그녀 뒤로, 그러나 짙은 그늘 속에, 어떤 남자의 윤곽이 보였으나, 그 인물의 얼굴은 도무지 분간할 수 없었다. 프란츠는 백작 부인과 알베르 사이에 한창 오가는 듯한 흥미로운 대화에 끼어들지 않을 수 없었다. 부인에게 저 맞은편의 아름다운 알바니아 여인이 누구인지 아느냐고 물은 것이었다. 그녀 같은 미모라면 남녀를 막론하고 눈여겨볼 만한 가치가 충분했으니 말이다.\n\n\"저 여인에 대해 제가 말씀드릴 수 있는 거라곤,\" 백작 부인이 대답했다. \"철 초부터 로마에 와 있다는 것뿐이에요. 시즌 첫날 밤에 지금 저 앉아 있는 자리에서 저 여인을 보았는데, 그 뒤로 한 번도 공연을 거른 적이 없거든요. 어떤 때는 지금 함께 있는 저 사람과 같이 오고, 어떤 때는 그저 흑인 하인 하나만 거느리고 오지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 저 여인의 용모는 어떻게 생각하십니까?\"\n\n\"오, 더없이 사랑스럽다고 봐요—제가 생각하는 메도라의 모습이 꼭 저럴 거예요.\"\n\n프란츠와 백작 부인은 서로 미소를 주고받았고, 이어 부인은 다시 알베르와의 대화를 이어 갔다. 그동안 프란츠는 다시 아까 하던 대로 객석과 관객들을 둘러보았다. 발레의 막이 올랐는데, 이탈리아 유파의 그 빼어난 본보기 가운데 하나로, 앙리가 훌륭하게 안무하고 무대에 올린 것이었다. 앙리는 무용 예술에서의 안목과 솜씨로 이탈리아 전역에 큰 명성을 얻은 인물이었다. 그것은 우아함과 짜임새, 기품이 어우러진 거장다운 작품의 하나로, 주역 무용수에서부터 가장 하찮은 단역에 이르기까지 군무단 전체가 무대 위에 한꺼번에 나서는 그런 작품이었다. 백쉰 명에 이르는 사람들이 같은 자세를 취하거나, 같은 팔이나 다리를 일제히 들어 올리는 광경을 볼 수 있었으니, 그 움직이는 무리 전체를 단 하나의 정신, 단 하나의 의지가 좌우하고 있다고 여기게 할 정도였다.\n\n그 발레의 제목은 〈폴리스카〉였다.\n\n발레가 아무리 그의 주의를 끌 만했다 한들, 프란츠는 그 아름다운 그리스 여인에게 너무도 깊이 빠져 있어 발레에는 도통 눈길도 주지 않았다. 한편 그녀는 발레를 지켜보며 거의 어린아이 같은 기쁨을 맛보는 듯했고, 그 열렬하고 생기 넘치는 표정은 그녀의 동행이 보이는 그 완전한 무관심과 강렬하게 대비되었다. 동행은 작품이 이어지는 내내 꼼짝도 하지 않았으니, 관현악단에서 가장 사납고 우레 같은 소리가 터져 나올 때조차 그러했다. 그는 그런 것에는 아랑곳하지 않고, 겉모습을 믿을 수 있다면, 그저 포근한 안식과 환한 천상의 꿈을 누리고 있는 듯했다.\n\n마침내 발레가 끝나고, 열광하며 황홀해하는 관객들의 우레 같은 만장일치의 갈채 속에 막이 내렸다. 오페라의 두 막 사이를 발레로 가르는 그 매우 현명한 방식 덕분에, 공연 사이의 휴식은 매우 짧다. 무용수들이 회전과 우아한 발놀림을 펼치는 동안 오페라의 가수들은 쉬면서 필요하면 의상을 갈아입을 시간을 갖는 것이다.\n\n2막의 서곡이 시작되었다. 그리고 지휘자의 활이 바이올린 위를 처음 긋는 소리가 나자, 프란츠는 그 잠자던 사내가 천천히 일어나 그리스 여인에게 다가가는 것을 보았다. 여인은 몸을 돌려 그에게 몇 마디 건네고는, 다시 특별석 난간 위로 몸을 기울여 무대에서 벌어지는 일에 아까처럼 빠져들었다.\n\n그녀에게 말을 건 그 사람의 얼굴은 너무도 완전히 그늘에 가려 있어, 프란츠가 아무리 애써도 단 하나의 이목구비조차 분간할 수 없었다. 막이 올랐고, 프란츠의 주의는 배우들에게 끌렸다. 그의 눈은 그리스 여인과 그 기이한 동행이 있는 특별석에서 벗어나 무대 위의 일을 지켜보았다.\n\n내 독자들 대부분은 〈파리지나〉의 2막이 그 유명하고 인상적인 이중창으로 막을 연다는 것을 알 것이다. 파리지나가 잠결에 우고에 대한 자신의 사랑을 아초에게 무심코 누설하는 장면이다. 모욕당한 남편은 질투의 온갖 감정을 거치다가 마침내 확신에 사로잡히고, 이윽고 분노와 격분에 미쳐 날뛰며 죄지은 아내를 깨워, 그녀의 죄를 안다고 말하며 복수하겠다고 위협한다.\n\n이 이중창은 도니체티의 그 풍부한 펜 끝에서 흘러나온 가장 아름답고 표현력 넘치며 무시무시한 착상 가운데 하나다. 프란츠는 이제 그것을 세 번째로 듣는 것이었다. 그런데도 가련한 남편과 아내가 저마다의 슬픔과 격정을 토해 내는 그 음표들, 그토록 애틋하게 표현력 넘치고 무섭도록 장엄한 그 음표들은, 처음 들었을 때의 감동에 못지않은 효과로 프란츠의 영혼을 전율케 했다. 평소의 침착함을 넘어 흥분한 프란츠는 관객들과 함께 일어서서, 뒤따른 그 우레 같고 열광적인 갈채에 막 합세하려던 참이었다. 그러나 별안간 그의 뜻은 가로막혔고, 두 손은 옆구리로 툭 떨어졌으며, 반쯤 내뱉던 \"브라보\" 소리가 입술 위에서 사그라졌다.\n\n그리스 여인이 앉아 있던 특별석의 그 사내가 그곳에 감도는 만인의 감탄에 함께하는 듯했다. 그가 자리에서 일어나 앞쪽에 섰던 것이다. 그리하여 그의 얼굴이 온전히 드러나자, 프란츠는 그를 몬테 크리스토의 수수께끼 같은 거주자로, 그리고 바로 전날 저녁 콜로세움의 폐허에서 마주쳤던, 그 목소리와 형체가 그토록 낯익게 느껴졌던 바로 그 인물로 어렵잖게 알아보았다.\n\n이제 그의 정체에 대한 모든 의심은 끝이 났다. 그 기이한 집주인은 분명 로마에 살고 있었다. 프란츠의 앞선 의심이 이렇게 온전히 확인되면서 일어난 놀라움과 동요가 틀림없이 그의 얼굴에도 그에 상응하는 표정을 새겨 놓았던 모양이다. 백작 부인이 어리둥절한 눈길로 그의 얼굴을 한참 들여다보더니 웃음을 터뜨리며 무슨 일이냐고 알려 달라고 청했으니 말이다.\n\n\"백작 부인,\" 프란츠가 그녀의 놀림에는 아랑곳없이 대꾸했다. \"조금 전에 제가 부인께 저 맞은편의 알바니아 여인에 대해 무언가 아시는 게 있느냐고 여쭈었지요. 이제는 부인께 간청드려야겠습니다. 그녀의 남편이 누구이며 어떤 사람인지 좀 알려 주십시오.\"\n\n\"이런,\" 백작 부인이 대답했다. \"그 사람에 대해서는 저도 당신만큼이나 아는 게 없답니다.\"\n\n\"전에 한 번도 그를 눈여겨보신 적이 없는 모양이군요?\"\n\n\"그런 질문을 하다니—참으로 프랑스 사람답네요! 우리 이탈리아 여자들은 오직 사랑하는 남자만 눈에 들어온다는 걸 모르세요?\"\n\n\"맞는 말씀입니다.\" 프란츠가 대답했다.\n\n\"제가 말씀드릴 수 있는 거라곤,\" 백작 부인이 오페라글라스를 집어 들어 문제의 특별석 쪽으로 겨누며 말을 이었다. \"내력을 알려 드릴 수 없는 저 신사가, 제 눈에는 마치 방금 무덤에서 파헤쳐진 사람 같다는 것뿐이에요. 저이는 인간이라기보다는, 어느 마음씨 좋은 무덤지기가 잠시 무덤을 떠나 우리 이 땅을 다시 찾도록 허락해 준 시신 같아 보이는걸요. 저 끔찍하도록 창백한 얼굴 좀 봐요!\"\n\n\"오, 그는 늘 지금 보시는 것처럼 핏기가 없습니다.\" 프란츠가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 그를 아세요?\" 백작 부인이 거의 비명을 지르다시피 했다. \"오, 제발, 부디 저희에게 그에 대해 모두 말씀해 주세요—그는 흡혈귀인가요, 되살아난 시신인가요, 아니면 대체 무엇인가요?\"\n\n\"전에 그를 본 적이 있는 것 같습니다. 그리고 그가 저를 알아보는 것 같기까지 하군요.\"\n\n\"그리고 충분히 이해가 가요,\" 백작 부인이 그 아름다운 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다. 마치 저도 모르게 핏줄을 타고 전율이 스쳐 가기라도 한 듯이. \"저 남자를 한번 본 사람이라면 결코 잊을 수 없으리라는 게 말이에요.\"\n\n프란츠가 느낀 그 감각은 분명 그만의 것이 아니었다. 그와는 전혀 무관한 다른 한 사람도, 똑같이 까닭 모를 외경과 불안을 느낀 것이다.\n\n\"그래,\" 프란츠가 물었다. 백작 부인이 또 한 번 오페라글라스를 그 특별석으로 겨눈 뒤였다. \"우리 맞은편 이웃을 어떻게 생각하십니까?\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 살아 있는 모습을 한 러스벤 경 바로 그 사람이 아닐까 싶네요.\"\n\n바이런을 다시 한번 빗댄 이 말에 프란츠의 얼굴에는 미소가 떠올랐다. 그래도 그는, 만약 흡혈귀의 존재를 믿게 만들 만한 것이 있다면, 그것은 바로 눈앞의 저 수수께끼 같은 인물 같은 사내의 존재이리라는 것만은 인정하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"기어이 저자가 누구이며 어떤 사람인지 알아내야겠습니다.\" 프란츠가 자리에서 일어서며 말했다.\n\n\"안 돼요, 안 돼요.\" 백작 부인이 외쳤다. \"절 떠나시면 안 돼요. 집까지 바래다주실 걸 믿고 있는걸요. 오, 정말이지, 가시도록 둘 수 없어요.\"\n\n\"설마,\" 프란츠가 속삭였다. \"두려움이라도 품고 계신 겁니까?\"\n\n\"말씀드리지요.\" 백작 부인이 대답했다. \"바이런은 흡혈귀의 존재를 더없이 완벽하게 믿었어요. 심지어 자기가 그것들을 본 적이 있다고 제게 장담하기까지 했지요. 그가 제게 들려준 묘사가 눈앞의 저 남자의 이목구비며 성격과 완벽하게 들어맞아요. 오, 저이는 제가 들어 온 그 모습을 그대로 본떠 놓은 것 같아요! 새카만 머리카락, 크고 환하게 번득이는 두 눈, 그 속에서는 사납고 이 세상 것 아닌 불길이 타오르는 것 같아요—그 똑같이 끔찍한 창백함이라니. 그리고 또 보세요, 저이와 함께 있는 여자도 다른 여느 여인과는 전혀 달라요. 저 여자는 이방인이에요—낯선 사람이지요. 저 여자가 누구인지, 어디서 왔는지 아무도 몰라요. 틀림없이 저이와 똑같은 그 끔찍한 족속에 속한 자일 테고, 저이처럼 마법을 다루는 자일 거예요. 부디 저자 가까이 가지 말아 주세요—적어도 오늘 밤은요. 만약 내일도 당신의 호기심이 여전히 그토록 크다면, 좋으실 대로 탐문해 보세요. 하지만 오늘 밤은 안 되고, 그래서도 안 됩니다. 그러려고 저는 당신을 온전히 제 곁에 붙잡아 둘 작정이에요.\"\n\n프란츠는 여러 가지 이유로 그 추적을 이튿날까지 미룰 수 없노라고 항변했다.\n\n\"제 말을 들어 보세요.\" 백작 부인이 말했다. \"그렇게 고집부리지 마시고요. 저는 집에 갑니다. 오늘 밤 저희 집에서 모임이 있어서, 도저히 오페라 끝까지 남아 있을 수가 없어요. 그런데 한 부인이 이렇게 몸소 청하는데도 호위를 거절할 만큼 당신이 신사도가 없는 사람이라고는 한순간도 믿을 수 없는걸요.\"\n\n프란츠로서는 모자를 집어 들고 특별석 문을 열어 백작 부인에게 팔을 내미는 것 말고는 달리 할 일이 없었다. 그녀의 태도로 미루어, 그 불안이 거짓이 아님은 너무도 분명했다. 그리고 프란츠 자신도 미신적인 두려움을 떨칠 수 없었다—그에게는 그 두려움이 한층 더 강렬했으니, 그것은 그것을 뒷받침하는 갖가지 기억에서 비롯된 것이었기 때문이다. 반면 백작 부인의 공포는, 본래 들어 온 무시무시한 이야기들을 끝내 진실로 믿게 되면서 그녀의 마음에 처음 생겨난 본능적인 믿음에서 솟아난 것이었다. 프란츠는 그녀를 부축해 마차에 태우면서 그녀의 팔이 떨리는 것까지 느낄 수 있었다. 그녀의 호텔에 다다랐을 때, 프란츠는 그녀가 손님을 기다린다고 한 말이 자기를 속인 것임을 알아차렸다. 오히려 정해진 시각보다 일찍 돌아온 그녀의 모습에 하인들이 몹시 놀라는 듯했으니 말이다.\n\n\"제 자그마한 핑계를 용서하세요.\" 백작 부인이 그 일에 대한 동행의 반쯤 나무라는 듯한 지적에 답하여 말했다. \"하지만 그 끔찍한 남자가 저를 어찌나 불편하게 만들던지, 혼자가 되어 놀란 마음을 가라앉히고 싶었어요.\"\n\n프란츠는 미소 지어 보이려 했다.\n\n\"이런,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"웃지 마세요. 그건 당신 얼굴 표정과도 어울리지 않고, 또 분명 당신 마음에서 우러난 것도 아니잖아요. 그래도 한 가지만 약속해 주세요.\"\n\n\"무엇을요?\"\n\n\"약속해 달라니까요.\"\n\n\"이 남자가 누구인지 알아내겠다는 제 결심을 버리는 일만 아니라면, 무엇이든 원하시는 대로 하지요. 저는 그가 누구인지, 어디서 왔는지, 어디로 가는지 알고 싶은, 부인께서 상상하실 수 있는 것보다 더 많은 이유가 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그가 어디서 왔는지는 저도 모르지만, 어디로 가는지는 기꺼이 말씀드릴 수 있어요. 두말할 것 없이 저 아래, 지옥이지요.\"\n\n\"부인께서 제게 청하시려던 그 약속에 대해서만 이야기합시다.\" 프란츠가 말했다.\n\n\"좋아요, 그럼 곧장 호텔로 돌아가서, 오늘 밤은 이 남자를 뒤쫓으려는 어떤 시도도 하지 않겠다고 약속해 주세요. 우리가 헤어진 사람들과 그 뒤에 만나는 사람들 사이에는 어떤 끌림 같은 게 있는 법이에요. 제발, 저 남자와 저 사이의 매개자 노릇은 하지 말아 주세요. 내일은 마음껏 그를 뒤쫓으세요. 하지만 저를 두려움에 떨다 죽는 꼴로 보고 싶지 않으시거든, 결코 그를 제 가까이로 데려오지는 마세요. 자, 그럼 안녕히 주무세요. 방으로 가서, 오늘 밤의 기억일랑 모두 잠으로 잊어 버리세요. 저로 말하자면, 도무지 눈을 붙일 수 없으리란 걸 잘 알고 있지만요.\"\n\n이 말을 하고서 백작 부인은 프란츠를 떠났고, 그는 그녀가 그저 자기를 가지고 놀고 있는 것인지, 아니면 그 두려움과 동요가 진짜인지 도무지 가늠할 수 없었다.\n\n호텔로 돌아온 프란츠는 알베르가 실내복에 슬리퍼 차림으로 소파에 늘어진 채 시가를 피우고 있는 것을 발견했다.\n\n\"이 사람아!\" 그가 벌떡 일어나며 외쳤다. \"정말 자넨가? 아니, 내일이나 되어야 볼 줄 알았는데.\"\n\n\"이보게 알베르,\" 프란츠가 대답했다. \"이참에 자네에게 단단히, 그리고 영영 일러 두게 되어 기쁘군. 자네는 이탈리아 여인들에 대해 더없이 그릇된 관념을 품고 있어. 자네가 그동안 자네 연애마다 줄곧 겪어 온 그 실패들이, 이쯤 되면 자네에게 더 나은 걸 가르쳐 줬을 법도 한데 말이지.\"\n\n\"맹세코, 이 여자들은 악마라도 제대로 읽어 내려면 쩔쩔맬 걸세. 자, 보게나—손을 내밀어 주고—이쪽이 잡으면 마주 쥐어 주고—소곤소곤 대화를 이어 가고—집까지 바래다주게 해 주고. 자, 파리 여자가 이런 호의의 표시를 사분의 일이라도 누리게 했다가는, 그 평판은 영영 끝장날 걸세.\"\n\n\"그리고 단테가 적었듯이 '시(sì) 소리가 울리는' 이 아름다운 나라의 여인들이 자기 말과 행동에 그토록 거리낌이 없는 바로 그 까닭은, 그들이 그만큼 사람들 앞에 드러나 살아가고, 실은 감출 게 아무것도 없기 때문이라네. 게다가 자네도 보았겠지, 백작 부인이 정말로 겁에 질려 있던 걸.\"\n\n\"무엇에 말인가? 그 아름다운 그리스 여인과 같은 특별석에 마주 앉아 있던 그 점잖은 신사를 보고서? 그런데, 나로 말하자면, 공연이 끝난 뒤 로비에서 그들과 마주쳤네. 그리고 내 목을 걸어도 좋아, 자네가 어디서 그런 저세상 이야기 같은 생각을 끌어왔는지 도무지 모르겠군. 단언컨대 자네의 그 도깨비란 자는 빌어먹게 잘생긴 사내인 데다, 옷차림도 감탄스러웠어. 사실 그 옷 재단으로 보아, 일류 파리 재단사가—아마 블랭 아니면 위만이—지은 게 틀림없다고 확신하네. 좀 지나치게 창백하긴 했지, 분명히. 하지만 자네도 알다시피, 창백함이란 늘 고귀한 혈통과 빼어난 가문의 강력한 증거로 여겨지는 법 아닌가.\"\n\n프란츠는 미소 지었다. 알베르가 자기 안색에 핏기라곤 도무지 없다는 점을 유난히 자랑스러워한다는 것을 잘 기억하고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"그래, 그건 내 생각을 뒷받침해 주는군.\" 프란츠가 말했다. \"백작 부인의 의심이라는 게 분별도 이치도 없는 것이라는 내 생각 말일세. 그가 자네가 들을 수 있는 데서 말을 하던가? 그리고 자네가 그 말을 좀 알아들었나?\"\n\n\"알아들었네. 하지만 로마이크 방언으로 한 말이었어. 섞여 있는 그리스어 낱말들로 그걸 알았지. 내가 대학 시절에 그리스어를 좀—꽤 잘했다는 걸 자네에게 말한 적이 있는지 모르겠군.\"\n\n\"그가 로마이크어로 말했단 말이지?\"\n\n\"그런 것 같네.\"\n\n\"그걸로 결판났군.\" 프란츠가 중얼거렸다. \"그자야, 의심할 여지없이.\"\n\n\"무어라 했나?\"\n\n\"아무것도, 아무것도 아닐세. 그런데 말해 보게, 내가 들어왔을 때 무슨 생각을 하고 있던 건가?\"\n\n\"오, 자네를 위해 자그마한 깜짝 선물을 마련하던 중이었지.\"\n\n\"그래? 어떤 종류의?\"\n\n\"그게, 자네도 알다시피 마차를 구하기란 도무지 불가능하잖나.\"\n\n\"물론이지. 게다가 우리가 한 대 구해 보려고 인간으로서 할 수 있는 일은 다 했다는 것도 알고 있네.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래서 이 난관에 부닥쳐 내 머리에 번뜩이는 묘안이 하나 떠올랐다네.\"\n\n프란츠는 그의 상상력이 내놓는 제안에 그다지 신뢰가 가지 않는다는 듯이 알베르를 바라보았다.\n\n\"이보게 프란츠 군,\" 알베르가 외쳤다. \"자네가 방금 내게 보내 준 그런 미심쩍고 못 믿겠다는 눈초리에는, 결투를 신청받아 마땅하네.\"\n\n\"그럼 자네 계획이 자네 장담대로 그렇게 기발하게 드러난다면, 신사로서 자네 분풀이에 응해 주겠다고 약속하지.\"\n\n\"좋아, 그럼 들어 보게.\"\n\n\"듣고 있네.\"\n\n\"자네도 동의하지, 마차를 구하는 건 어림없는 일이라는 데 말이야?\"\n\n\"동의하네.\"\n\"말도 구할 수 없고?\"\n\n\"맞아. 얼마든 값을 부르겠다 했지만 실패했지.\"\n\n\"그래, 그럼 짐수레는 어떤가? 그런 거라면 구할 수 있을 법한데.\"\n\n\"충분히 가능하지.\"\n\n\"그리고 황소 두 마리는?\"\n\n\"짐수레만큼이나 쉽게 구할 수 있겠지.\"\n\n\"그럼 보게, 이 사람아, 짐수레 한 대와 황소 두 마리만 있으면 우리 일은 해결되네. 짐수레는 맵시 있게 꾸미는 거야. 그리고 자네와 내가 나폴리 추수꾼 차림을 하면, 저 레오폴 로베르의 그 멋들어진 그림을 본떠 인상적인 활인화를 꾸밀 수 있지. 게다가 백작 부인이 포추올리나 소렌토 농촌 아낙 차림으로 우리와 함께해 준다면 효과가 한결 더할 걸세. 그러면 우리 무리는 완전무결해지지. 더욱이 백작 부인은 성모 마리아를 표현하기에 충분할 만큼 아름다우니까.\"\n\n\"그래,\" 프란츠가 말했다. \"이번만큼은 알베르 군, 자네가 더없이 멋진 묘안을 짚어 냈다고 인정하지 않을 수 없군.\"\n\n\"게다가 아주 자국(自國)다운 묘안이기도 하지.\" 알베르가 흡족한 자부심에 차 대답했다. \"우리네 축제에서 그대로 빌려 온 가장무도회일 뿐이거든. 하하, 너희 로마인들아! 너희는 너희 거지 같은 도시에 마차도 말도 구할 수 없다는 이유로, 우리 가엾은 이방인들을 라차로니●처럼 너희 행렬 꽁무니나 졸졸 따라다니게 만들 작정이었겠지. 하지만 너희는 우리를 모른다. 한 가지를 가질 수 없으면 우리는 다른 걸 지어내는 법이야.\"\n\n* 라차로니: 나폴리의 빈민·부랑자를 가리키던 말.—옮긴이\n\n\"그래서 자네의 그 의기양양한 묘안을 누구에게 알리기라도 했나?\"\n\"우리 주인장한테만 했네. 집에 돌아오자마자 그를 불러서, 내가 무엇을 구하고 싶은지 설명했지. 그가 장담하기를, 내가 바라는 걸 다 마련하는 일쯤은 더없이 쉽다더군. 한 가지 아쉬운 게 있었네. 내가 황소 뿔에 금박을 입히라고 했더니, 그러려면 사흘은 걸린다며 그럴 시간이 없다지 뭔가. 그러니 이 자그마한 사치는 없이 해야겠어.\"\n\n\"그럼 그는 지금 어디 있나?\"\n\n\"누구 말인가?\"\n\n\"우리 주인장 말일세.\"\n\"우리 탈것을 구하러 나갔지. 내일이면 너무 늦을지도 모르니까.\"\n\n\"그럼 오늘 밤 우리에게 답을 줄 수 있겠군.\"\n\n\"오, 곧 돌아올 걸세.\"\n\n바로 그 순간 문이 열리고 파스트리니 씨의 머리가 나타났다. \"들어가도 되겠습니까(페르메소)?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"물론이지요, 물론이지요.\" 프란츠가 외쳤다. \"들어오세요, 주인장.\"\n\n\"자, 그래서,\" 알베르가 다급히 물었다. \"그 바라던 짐수레와 황소는 구했나요?\"\n\n\"그보다 더 나은 것을요!\" 파스트리니 씨가 스스로에게 더없이 흡족해하는 사람의 기색으로 대답했다.\n\n\"조심하시오, 우리 훌륭한 주인장.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"'더 나은 것'이란 '좋은 것'의 확실한 적이니까.\"\n\n\"그저 그 일은 제게 맡겨만 주십시오, 나리들.\" 파스트리니 씨가 한없는 자신감을 드러내는 어조로 대꾸했다.\n\n\"한데 대체 무엇을 했단 말이오?\" 프란츠가 물었다. \"어서 말해 보시오, 우리 양반.\"\n\n\"나리들께서도 아시다시피,\" 주인장이 거드름을 부풀리며 대답했다. \"몬테 크리스토 백작께서 나리들과 같은 층에 묵고 계시지 않습니까!\"\n\n\"그야 우리도 잘 알지요.\" 알베르가 외쳤다. \"바로 그 사정 때문에 우리가 파리 뒷골목의 가난한 학생 둘처럼 이 비좁은 방에 처박혀 있는 거니까요.\"\n\n\"그런데 말입니다, 몬테 크리스토 백작께서 나리들이 처하신 그 곤경을 전해 들으시고, 자신의 마차에 자리를 내드리고, 또 로스폴리 궁의 자기 창가에 두 자리를 내드리겠다고 사람을 보내 청하셨습니다.\" 두 벗은 이루 말할 수 없는 놀라움으로 서로를 마주 보았다.\n\n\"하지만 자네 생각엔,\" 알베르가 물었다. \"우리가 생판 모르는 사람한테서 그런 제안을 받아들여도 되겠나?\"\n\n\"이 몬테 크리스토 백작이라는 사람은 어떤 인물이오?\" 프란츠가 주인장에게 물었다.\n\n\"아주 대단한 귀족이십니다만, 몰타 사람인지 시칠리아 사람인지는 정확히 말씀드릴 수 없습니다. 다만 이것만은 압니다. 보르게세 가문 못지않게 고귀하시고, 금광만큼이나 부유하시다는 것을요.\"\n\n\"내 생각엔,\" 프란츠가 알베르에게 나직이 말했다. \"만약 이 사람이 우리 주인장의 그 거창한 찬사를 받을 만한 인물이라면, 자기 초대를 다른 경로로 전했을 테고, 이런 격식 없는 방식으로 우리에게 전달되게 두지는 않았을 거야. 글로 써서 보냈거나—아니면——\"\n\n바로 그 순간 누군가 문을 두드렸다.\n\n\"들어오시오.\" 프란츠가 말했다.\n\n상당히 멋스럽고 화려한 제복을 입은 하인이 문지방에 나타났다. 그는 카드 두 장을 주인장의 손에 건넸고, 주인장은 그것을 곧장 두 젊은이에게 내밀었다. 하인이 말했다.\n\n\"몬테 크리스토 백작께서 알베르 드 모르세르 자작님과 프란츠 데피네 님께 이것을 전하라 하셨습니다. 몬테 크리스토 백작께서는,\" 하인이 말을 이었다. \"이웃으로서 두 분을 찾아뵙고자 허락을 청하시며, 두 분께서 몇 시에 자신을 맞아 주실지 알려 주시면 영광이겠다 하셨습니다.\"\n\n\"이거 참, 프란츠,\" 알베르가 속삭였다. \"여기엔 흠잡을 게 별로 없군.\"\n\n\"백작께 전하시오,\" 프란츠가 대답했다. \"우리가 기꺼이 그분을 찾아뵙겠노라고 말이오.\"\n\n하인이 고개를 숙이고 물러갔다.\n\n\"저게 바로 내가 우아한 공략법이라 부르는 걸세.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"주인장, 당신 말이 아주 옳았소. 몬테 크리스토 백작은 의심할 여지없이 일류 가문 출신에 세상 물정에 밝은 분이오.\"\n\n\"그럼 그분의 제안을 받아들이시는 겁니까?\" 주인장이 말했다.\n\n\"물론 받아들이고말고요.\" 알베르가 대답했다. \"다만 그 짐수레와 추수꾼 무리를 포기해야 하는 건 솔직히 아쉽군요—그게 얼마나 큰 효과를 냈을지! 그리고 우리의 그 멋진 계획을 잃는 데 대한 보상으로 로스폴리 궁의 창문이 없었다면, 나는 어쩌면 본래 계획을 끝까지 밀고 나갔을지도 모르겠어. 자네 생각은 어떤가, 프란츠?\"\n\n\"오, 나도 자네와 같은 생각일세. 로스폴리 궁의 그 창문이야말로 나를 결심하게 한 거였으니까.\"\n\n사실인즉, 로스폴리 궁의 두 자리 이야기가, 프란츠로 하여금 바로 전날 저녁 콜로세움의 폐허에서 그 수수께끼 같은 낯선 이와 또 다른 자 사이에 오가는 것을 엿들은 그 대화를 떠올리게 했던 것이다\n망토를 두른 낯선 사내가 사형수 한 명을 풀어 주겠노라 장담하던 트라스테베레 사람이 떠올랐다. 그리고 그 얼굴을 감춘 사내가 (프란츠가 확신하듯) 방금 아르헨티나 극장에서 본 그 인물과 같은 사람이라면, 그의 정체를 밝힐 수 있을 뿐 아니라, 그에 관한 조사도 더없이 수월하고 자유롭게 이어 갈 수 있으리라.\n\n프란츠는 그 정체 모를 훼방꾼과 이미 두 번이나 마주쳤던 일을 어수선한 꿈속에서 곱씹으며 밤을 보냈고, 잠에서 깨어서는 이튿날이 무엇을 가져다줄지 이런저런 추측에 빠졌다. 다음 날이면 모든 의문이 풀릴 터였다. 가까운 이웃이자 친구가 되고 싶어 하는 그 몬테 크리스토 백작이 기게스의 반지를 지녀 그 힘으로 제 몸을 보이지 않게 만들지 않는 한, 이번에는 결코 빠져나가지 못하리라는 것만은 분명했다.\n\n여덟 시가 되자 프란츠는 자리에서 일어나 옷을 갖춰 입었다. 일찍 일어날 까닭이 없는 알베르는 여전히 곤히 잠들어 있었다. 프란츠가 가장 먼저 한 일은 여관 주인을 부르는 것이었고, 주인은 여느 때처럼 굽실거리며 나타났다.\n\n\"파스트리니 씨,\" 프란츠가 물었다. \"오늘 사형 집행이 있다던데, 맞소?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 나리. 하지만 구경할 창문을 잡으시려고 물으시는 거라면, 이미 너무 늦으셨습니다.\"\n\n\"아니, 그게 아니오.\" 프란츠가 대답했다. \"그럴 생각은 없었소. 설령 그 광경을 보고 싶었다 한들, 핀초 언덕에서도 볼 수 있지 않겠소?\"\n\n\"아!\" 주인이 외쳤다. \"나리께서 그 언덕에 늘 모여드는 부랑배들 틈에 끼고 싶어 하실 줄은 몰랐습니다. 사실 그자들은 그 언덕을 제 것이라도 되는 양 여기지요.\"\n\n\"안 갈 수도 있소.\" 프란츠가 대답했다. \"하지만 혹시 마음이 동할 수도 있으니, 오늘 집행에 대해 자세히 좀 일러 주시오.\"\n\n\"어떤 점을 알고 싶으십니까, 나리?\"\n\n\"사형을 당할 사람의 수, 이름, 그리고 어떤 죽음을 맞게 되는지 말이오.\"\n\n\"마침 운이 좋으십니다, 나리! 불과 몇 분 전에 타볼레타를 가져다주었거든요.\"\n\n\"그게 뭐요?\"\n\n\"사형 집행 전날 저녁에 거리 모퉁이마다 걸어 두는 일종의 나무 판입니다. 거기에 사형수들의 이름과 죄목, 처형 방식이 적힌 종이를 붙여 놓지요. 이렇게 공공연히 알리는 까닭은, 선량하고 독실한 가톨릭 신자들이 그 불운한 죄인들을 위해 기도를 올리고, 무엇보다 하늘에 진심 어린 회개를 내려 주십사 빌 수 있게 하려는 것입니다.\"\n\n\"그래서 그 판을 댁한테 가져다주니, 댁도 신자들과 함께 기도를 올리라는 게요?\" 프란츠가 다소 미심쩍은 듯 물었다.\n\n\"오, 천만에요, 나리! 저는 제 일과 귀하신 손님들 일 말고는 남의 일에 신경 쓸 겨를이 없습니다. 다만 종이를 붙이는 사람과 약조를 해 두었지요. 그자가 연극 전단처럼 그것을 제게 가져다주는 겁니다. 혹시 제 여관에 묵는 손님 중에 사형 집행을 보고 싶어 하는 분이 계시면, 시간이며 장소 같은 데 필요한 정보를 빠짐없이 알려 드릴 수 있도록 말입니다.\"\n\n\"이거 참, 자네답게 세심한 배려로군, 파스트리니 씨.\" 프란츠가 외쳤다.\n\n\"아이고, 나리,\" 주인이 더없이 흡족한 듯 낄낄대며 두 손을 비볐다. \"이 보잘것없는 여관을 찾아 주시는 귀하신 손님들의 후원과 보살핌을 받을 자격을 얻으려고, 소홀히 하는 일이 없다고 감히 말씀드릴 수 있습니다.\"\n\n\"정말 잘 알겠소, 더없이 훌륭한 주인장. 손님을 향한 이토록 놀라운 정성의 증거를 내가 어디를 가든 널리 알리겠다고 약속하리다. 그런데 우선 그 타볼레타라는 것을 한번 보여 주시오.\"\n\n\"나리의 분부를 따르는 것보다 쉬운 일은 없지요.\" 주인이 방문을 열며 말했다. \"마침 한 장을 나리 방 바로 옆 층계참에 걸어 두었습니다.\"\n\n그러고는 벽에서 그 판을 떼어 프란츠에게 건넸다. 프란츠는 다음과 같이 읽었다.\n\n\"'2월 23일 수요일, 카니발 첫날에 로타 법정의 명에 따라 포폴로 광장에서 두 사람에 대한 사형이 집행됨을 공지하는 바이다. 그 이름은 안드레아 론돌로와, 일명 로카 프리오리라 불리는 페피노이다. 전자는 산조반니인라테라노 성당의 참사회원이며 덕망 높고 모범적인 사제 돈 체사레 토를리니를 살해한 죄로, 후자는 잔혹하고 피에 굶주린 산적 루이지 밤파와 그 무리의 공범인 죄로 유죄를 선고받았다. 첫 번째 죄인은 마촐라토 형에, 두 번째 죄인은 데카피타토 형에 처해질 것이다.\n\n\"'모든 선량한 그리스도교 신자들은 이 불운한 자들을 위해 기도하기를 간청하노니, 하느님께서 부디 그들이 제 죄를 깨닫게 하시고, 그 죄에 대해 진심 어린 참회를 내려 주시기를 바라노라.'\"\n\n이것은 프란츠가 전날 저녁 콜로세움 폐허에서 들었던 바로 그 내용이었다. 어느 한 대목도 다르지 않았다. 사형수들의 이름, 그들의 죄목, 처형 방식, 모든 것이 그가 미리 들었던 정보와 일치했다. 그렇다면 십중팔구 그 트라스테베레 사람은 다름 아닌 산적 루이지 밤파 본인이며, 망토를 두른 사내는 그가 \"뱃사람 신드바드\"로 알고 있던 바로 그 인물일 터였다. 그는 포르토베키오와 튀니스에서 이미 그러했듯, 로마에서도 여전히 자선 행각을 벌이고 있는 것이 틀림없었다.\n\n그러나 시간이 흘러가고 있었으므로, 프란츠는 알베르를 깨우는 편이 좋겠다고 여겼다. 그런데 막 친구의 방으로 가려는 순간, 알베르가 그날에 어울리는 복장을 완벽하게 갖춰 입고 방으로 들어섰다. 카니발의 즐거움에 대한 기대가 머릿속을 어찌나 가득 채웠던지, 그는 평소보다 훨씬 일찍 잠자리를 박차고 나온 것이었다.\n\n\"자, 더없이 훌륭한 파스트리니 씨,\" 프란츠가 주인에게 말했다. \"우리 둘 다 채비가 되었으니, 지금 곧장 몬테 크리스토 백작을 찾아뵈어도 괜찮겠소?\"\n\n\"여부가 있겠습니까.\" 주인이 대답했다. \"몬테 크리스토 백작님은 늘 일찍 일어나시지요. 벌써 두 시간 전부터 일어나 계신 것은 제가 보증합니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 지금 바로 인사를 드리러 가도 폐가 되지 않으리라 정말 생각하시오?\"\n\n\"오, 확실합니다. 만약 제가 나리를 잘못 이끈 것이라면, 그 책임은 모두 제가 지겠습니다.\"\n\n\"좋소, 그렇다면, 알베르, 자네 준비됐나?\"\n\n\"완벽하네.\"\n\n\"가서 그분의 호의에 진심으로 감사를 드리세.\"\n\n\"그래, 그러세.\"\n\n주인은 두 친구를 앞장서서 층계참을 가로질러 안내했다. 백작의 거처와 그들 사이를 가르는 것이라곤 그 층계참뿐이었다. 그는 초인종을 울렸고, 하인이 문을 열자 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"프랑스 신사 두 분이십니다.\"\n\n하인이 정중하게 고개를 숙이고는 그들을 안으로 청했다. 두 사람은 파스트리니 씨의 지붕 아래에서 보리라고는 예상치 못한 호화로운 방 두 개를 지나, 우아하게 꾸며진 응접실로 안내되었다. 더없이 값진 터키산 양탄자가 바닥을 덮고 있었고, 더없이 푹신하고 안락한 긴 의자와 안락의자, 소파가 휴식이나 다과를 원하는 이들에게 두툼하고 폭신한 방석을 내밀고 있었다. 일류 거장들이 그린 빼어난 그림들이 벽을 따라 늘어서 있었고, 그 사이사이에 화려한 전리품들이 섞여 있었으며, 방의 여러 문 앞에는 값비싼 태피스트리로 만든 묵직한 휘장이 드리워져 있었다.\n\n\"나리들께서 앉아 계시면,\" 하인이 말했다. \"백작님께 두 분이 와 계신다고 알려 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 그는 태피스트리 휘장 하나 뒤로 사라졌다. 문이 열리는 순간 구즐라 소리가 두 젊은이의 귀에 닿았으나, 곧바로 잦아들고 말았다. 문이 재빨리 닫히는 바람에 풍성한 선율 한 자락만이 흘러들었던 것이다. 프란츠와 알베르는 서로를 의아한 눈빛으로 바라보다가, 이내 방 안의 화려한 세간으로 눈길을 돌렸다. 모든 것이 처음 휙 둘러보았을 때보다 두 번째로 보니 한층 더 웅장해 보였다.\n\n\"그래,\" 프란츠가 친구에게 말했다. \"이 모든 게 어떤가?\"\n\n\"이거 참, 솔직히 말하자면, 이보게, 내 보기엔 이 우아하고 세심한 우리 이웃은 에스파냐 공채 하락에 투기를 걸어 한몫 잡은 증권 거간꾼이거나, 아니면 신분을 감추고 여행하는 어느 왕족일 것 같네.\"\n\n\"쉿, 쉿!\" 프란츠가 대답했다. \"그가 누구이며 어떤 자인지 곧 알게 될 걸세. 오는군!\"\n\n프란츠가 말하는 사이, 문이 경첩을 따라 돌아가는 소리가 들렸고, 거의 곧바로 태피스트리가 옆으로 젖혀지며 이 모든 부의 주인이 두 젊은이 앞에 모습을 드러냈다. 알베르는 즉시 일어나 그를 맞으러 갔으나, 프란츠는 마치 홀린 듯 의자에 붙박인 채 있었다. 방금 들어선 그 사람에게서, 콜로세움의 정체 모를 방문객이자 아르헨티나 극장 특별석의 주인일 뿐 아니라, 몬테 크리스토에서 만났던 그 비범한 주인의 모습까지 알아보았기 때문이다.\n\n# Chapter 35. La Mazzolata\n\n“여러분, 이렇게 미리 찾아온 것을 부디 양해해 주십시오.” 몬테크리스토 백작이 들어서며 말했다. “좀 더 일찍 두 분 방으로 찾아뵈었다가 폐가 될까 염려스러웠습니다. 게다가 저를 보러 오시겠다는 전갈을 주셨기에, 저는 두 분을 기다리며 채비를 하고 있었지요.”\n\n“백작님, 프란츠와 저는 천 번이라도 감사드려야 할 처지입니다.” 알베르가 대꾸했다. “저희를 큰 곤경에서 구해 주셨으니까요. 친절한 초대장을 받았을 때, 저희는 막 기상천외한 탈것을 하나 궁리해 내려던 참이었답니다.”\n\n“그러셨군요.” 백작이 두 젊은이에게 앉으라고 손짓하며 대꾸했다. “좀 더 일찍 곤경에 처한 두 분을 돕지 못한 것은 다 그 멍청한 파스트리니 탓입니다. 저는 혈혈단신 외톨이로 지내는 처지라 이웃과 사귈 기회라면 무엇이든 마다하지 않는데, 그 사람은 그 사정을 잘 알면서도 두 분이 난처해하신다는 말은 한마디도 꺼내지 않았습니다. 제가 무슨 도움이라도 드릴 수 있다는 걸 알자마자, 저는 서둘러 그 기회를 붙잡아 제 도움을 제안했던 것이지요.”\n\n두 젊은이가 고개를 숙였다. 프란츠는 아직 무슨 말을 해야 할지 찾지 못했다. 마음을 정하지도 못했고, 백작의 태도 어디에도 자신을 알아봐 주기를 바라는 기색이 없었기에, 지난 일을 넌지시 들춰야 할지 아니면 더 확실한 증거가 잡힐 때까지 기다려야 할지 알 수 없었다. 게다가 전날 저녁 칸막이 좌석에 있던 사람이 바로 이 사람이라는 것은 확신했지만, 콜로세움에서 본 그 사내가 맞는지는 그만큼 단언할 수 없었다. 그래서 그는 백작에게 직접 운을 떼지 않고 일이 흘러가는 대로 두기로 마음먹었다. 더구나 그에게는 유리한 점이 하나 있었다. 자신은 백작의 비밀을 쥐고 있는데, 정작 백작은 숨길 것이 하나도 없는 프란츠에게 어떤 약점도 잡을 수 없다는 것이었다. 그래도 그는 의혹을 풀어 줄 만한 화제로 대화를 이끌어 보기로 했다.\n\n“백작님,” 그가 말했다. “저희에게 마차의 자리와 로스폴리 궁전 창가의 자리를 내주신다고 하셨지요. 그런데 포폴로 광장은 어디서 보면 좋을지 알려 주실 수 있겠습니까?”\n\n“아,” 백작이 무심한 듯 모르세르를 유심히 바라보며 말했다. “포폴로 광장에서 무슨 처형 같은 게 있지 않습니까?”\n\n“네.” 프란츠는 백작이 자신이 바라던 화제로 들어서는 것을 보며 대답했다.\n\n“가만, 어제 집사에게 그 일을 챙기라고 일러 둔 것 같군요. 어쩌면 그 사소한 일도 도와드릴 수 있겠습니다.”\n\n그는 손을 뻗어 종을 세 번 울렸다.\n\n“시간을 효율적으로 쓰고 하인을 부르는 일을 간단하게 만드는 방법을 궁리해 보신 적이 있습니까?” 그가 프란츠에게 말했다. “저는 해 봤지요. 한 번 울리면 시종, 두 번 울리면 집사장, 세 번 울리면 집사를 부르는 겁니다. 이렇게 하면 단 일 분도, 단 한마디도 허비하지 않지요. 자, 왔군요.”\n\n마흔다섯이나 쉰쯤 되어 보이는 사내가 들어왔다. 프란츠를 동굴로 안내했던 그 밀수꾼과 똑같이 닮은 사람이었다. 그러나 그는 프란츠를 알아보는 기색이 없었다. 미리 지시를 받았음이 분명했다.\n\n“베르투치오,” 백작이 말했다. “어제 일러 둔 대로 포폴로 광장이 내려다보이는 창을 마련해 두었나?”\n\n“네, 각하.” 집사가 대답했다. “하지만 너무 늦은 시각이었습니다.”\n\n“하나 필요하다고 일러 두지 않았나?” 백작이 미간을 찌푸리며 대꾸했다.\n\n“그래서 각하께서는 창을 하나 얻으셨습니다. 로바니에프 공께 빌려 드렸던 자리인데, 백 닢이나 치러야 했고······.”\n\n“됐네, 됐어, 베르투치오. 그런 집안 사정으로 손님들을 번거롭게 하지 말게. 창이 있다니 그걸로 충분해. 마부에게 지시를 내리고, 계단에서 채비하고 있다가 우리를 안내하게.”\n\n집사가 고개를 숙이고 방을 나서려 했다.\n\n“아!” 백작이 말을 이었다. “파스트리니에게 타볼레타를 받았는지, 처형 안내문을 우리에게 보내 줄 수 있는지 물어봐 주게.”\n\n“그러실 필요 없습니다.” 프란츠가 수첩을 꺼내며 말했다. “제가 그 안내문을 보고 옮겨 적어 두었거든요.”\n\n“좋습니다, 그럼 물러가도 되네, 베르투치오. 더는 자네가 필요 없으니. 아침 식사 준비가 되면 알려 주게. 두 분께서는,” 그가 두 친구에게로 돌아서며 덧붙였다. “저와 함께 아침을 드는 영광을 베풀어 주시겠지요?”\n\n“하지만 백작님,” 알베르가 말했다. “그러면 호의에 너무 기대는 셈이 됩니다.”\n\n“천만에요. 오히려 제게는 큰 기쁨입니다. 두 분 중 한 분이, 어쩌면 두 분 모두, 파리에서 제게 답례해 주시면 되니까요. 베르투치오, 세 사람 분으로 상을 차리게.”\n\n그러고 나서 그는 프란츠의 손에서 수첩을 받아 들었다. “‘알린다.’” 그는 마치 신문이라도 읽듯이 같은 어조로 읽어 내려갔다. “‘오늘 2월 23일, 안드레아 론돌로의 처형이 집행된다. 그는 성 요한 라테란 성당의 참사회원이며 존경받고 추앙받던 돈 체사르 토를리니를 살해한 죄를 지었다. 또한 로카 프리오리라 불리는 페피노는 흉악한 산적 루이지 밤파 및 그 일당과 공모한 죄로 유죄가 확정되었다.’\n\n흠! ‘첫 번째 죄인은 마촐라토 형에, 두 번째 죄인은 데카피타토 형에 처해진다.’ 그래요,” 백작이 말을 이었다. “처음에는 이렇게 정해져 있었지요. 하지만 어제부터 의식의 순서에 무슨 변동이 생긴 듯합니다.”\n\n“정말입니까?” 프란츠가 물었다.\n\n“그렇습니다. 어제저녁 로스필리오시 추기경 댁에서 시간을 보냈는데, 거기서 두 사람 중 하나에게 사면 같은 게 내려졌다는 이야기가 나왔거든요.”\n\n“안드레아 론돌로에게 말입니까?” 프란츠가 물었다.\n\n“아니요.” 백작이 무심히 대답했다. “다른 자에게요.(그는 이름을 떠올리려는 듯 수첩을 흘끗 보았다.) 로카 프리오리라 불리는 페피노에게 말입니다. 그러니 두 분은 단두대에서 목이 떨어지는 사내를 보지는 못하게 되었군요. 하지만 마촐라타 형은 여전히 남아 있습니다. 그건 처음 볼 때, 아니 두 번째로 볼 때까지도 아주 진기한 형벌이지요. 반면 다른 쪽은, 두 분도 아시겠지만 너무나 단순합니다. 만다이아는 절대 빗나가지 않고, 떨리지도 않으며, 샬레 백작의 목을 친 그 병사처럼 서른 번을 헛쳐 대는 일도 없습니다. 그 사형수는 리슐리외가 틀림없이 그 병사의 자비로운 솜씨에 맡겨 버렸겠지요. 아,” 백작이 경멸하는 어조로 덧붙였다. “유럽의 형벌 이야기는 제게 꺼내지 마십시오. 그건 잔혹함의 유아기, 아니 차라리 노년기에 머물러 있으니까요.”\n* 만다이아: 로마에서 쓰던 단두대의 일종으로, 칼날이 초승달 모양이다. —옮긴이\n\n“정말이지 백작님은,” 프란츠가 대답했다. “세상 온갖 나라의 갖가지 고문을 연구하기라도 하신 것 같습니다.”\n\n“적어도 못 본 것이 거의 없기는 합니다.” 백작이 차갑게 말했다.\n\n“그럼 그런 끔찍한 광경을 보며 즐거워하셨다는 말씀입니까?”\n\n“처음엔 공포였고, 두 번째엔 무관심이었고, 세 번째엔 호기심이었지요.”\n\n“호기심이라니, 그건 무서운 말이군요.”\n\n“어째서요? 삶에서 우리의 가장 큰 관심사는 죽음입니다. 그러니 영혼과 육체가 갈라서는 갖가지 방식을 연구하는 것, 또 저마다의 성격과 기질에 따라, 심지어 나라마다 다른 풍습에 따라, 사람들이 삶에서 죽음으로, 존재에서 소멸로 건너가는 그 순간을 어떻게 견뎌 내는지 살펴보는 것이 흥미롭지 않겠습니까? 저로 말하자면, 한 가지는 장담할 수 있습니다. 사람이 죽는 것을 많이 볼수록, 제 자신이 죽는 일도 한결 쉬워진다는 것입니다. 그리고 제 생각으로는, 죽음이 고문일 수는 있어도 속죄일 수는 없습니다.”\n\n“잘 이해가 가지 않습니다.” 프란츠가 대답했다. “부디 그 뜻을 설명해 주십시오. 제 호기심을 더할 나위 없이 자극하시는군요.”\n\n“들어 보십시오.” 백작이 말했다. 그러자 그의 얼굴 위로 깊은 증오가 솟구쳤다. 다른 사람 같으면 얼굴에 피가 솟구쳤을 그 자리에. “만일 어떤 자가 듣도 보도 못한 잔혹한 고문으로 당신의 아버지를, 어머니를, 약혼녀를, 그러니까 당신에게서 떼어 내면 결코 아물지 않는 황폐함과 상처를 가슴에 남길 그런 존재를 짓밟아 버렸다고 합시다. 그런데 사회가 당신에게 해 주는 보상이라는 것이, 그 살인자의 뒤통수와 목덜미 근육 사이에 단두대의 칼날을 들이미는 것뿐이라면, 그리고 우리에게 수년간 정신적 고통을 안긴 자가 단 몇 순간의 육체적 고통으로 빠져나가게 내버려 둔다면, 당신은 그것으로 충분하다고 보십니까?”\n\n“네, 압니다.” 프란츠가 말했다. “인간의 정의는 우리를 위로하기에는 모자라지요. 피에는 피로 갚아 줄 뿐, 그게 전부니까요. 하지만 그 정의에는 그것이 베풀 수 있는 것만을 요구해야 하지 않겠습니까.”\n\n“다른 경우를 하나 들어 보지요.” 백작이 말을 이었다. “어떤 사람이 죽임을 당해 사회가 침해받았을 때, 사회는 죽음을 죽음으로 갚습니다. 하지만 사회가 전혀 인지하지도 못한 채, 방금 우리가 말한 그 불충분한 복수의 수단조차 주지 않은 채, 한 사람이 겪을 수 있는 고문이 수천 가지나 있지 않습니까? 터키의 말뚝형도, 페르시아의 송곳도, 이로쿼이 인디언의 화형주와 인두도 모자란 그런 죄, 그러면서도 사회는 벌하지 못하는 그런 죄가 있지 않습니까? 대답해 보십시오. 그런 죄가 존재하지 않습니까?”\n\n“네.” 프란츠가 대답했다. “바로 그런 죄를 벌하려고 결투가 용인되는 것이지요.”\n\n“아, 결투라!” 백작이 외쳤다. “맹세코, 목적이 복수일 때 그 목적에 다다르는 참으로 유쾌한 방법이지요! 어떤 자가 당신의 정부를 빼앗고, 어떤 자가 당신의 아내를 유혹하고, 어떤 자가 당신의 딸을 욕보였다고 합시다. 신께서 모든 피조물에게 약속하신 그 몫의 행복을 하늘에서 받으리라 기대할 권리가 있던 한 사람의 평생을, 그자는 비참과 치욕의 삶으로 만들어 버렸습니다. 그런데 당신의 머릿속에 광기를, 가슴속에 절망을 심어 놓은 그 사내의 머리통에 총알을 박거나 가슴에 칼을 찔러 넣었다고 해서, 당신은 복수를 했다고 여깁니까? 게다가 그 싸움에서 이겨 세상의 눈에 모든 죄를 사면받고 나오는 쪽이 오히려 그자인 경우도 많다는 것을 기억하십시오. 아닙니다, 아니에요.” 백작이 말을 이었다. “제가 복수를 해야 한다면, 저는 그런 식으로 하지는 않을 겁니다.”\n\n“그럼 결투에는 찬성하지 않으신다는 겁니까? 백작님은 결투를 하지 않으시겠다는 말씀이십니까?” 이번에는 알베르가 그 기이한 논리에 놀라 물었다.\n\n“오, 천만에요.” 백작이 대답했다. “제 말을 오해하지 마십시오. 저는 사소한 일로도, 모욕 한 번으로도, 한 대 맞은 일로도 결투를 할 겁니다. 더구나 온갖 신체 단련에 능한 솜씨와 위험 앞에서 차츰 길러 온 무심함 덕분에, 저는 거의 틀림없이 상대를 죽일 수 있을 테니까요. 오, 그런 일로는 싸우겠습니다. 하지만 더디고, 깊고, 영원한 고문에 대해서라면, 가능한 한 똑같이 되갚아 줄 겁니다. 동방인들이 말하듯, 눈에는 눈, 이에는 이로 말이지요. 그들은 모든 면에서 우리의 스승입니다. 스스로 꿈의 삶과 현실의 낙원을 만들어 낸 그 축복받은 사람들이지요.”\n\n“하지만 백작님,” 프란츠가 백작에게 말했다. “자신을 자기 사건의 재판관이자 동시에 처형인으로 만드는 그 논리대로라면, 영영 법의 손아귀에 떨어지지 않을 길을 택하기란 어려울 겁니다. 증오는 눈이 멀게 하고, 분노는 사람을 휩쓸어 가지요. 복수를 쏟아붓는 자는 쓰디쓴 잔을 들이켜게 될 위험을 무릅쓰는 셈입니다.”\n\n“가난하고 미숙한 자라면 그렇겠지요. 하지만 부유하고 노련한 자라면 그렇지 않습니다. 게다가 그에게 닥칠 최악의 일이라야 우리가 이미 이야기한 그 형벌, 박애를 내세운 프랑스 혁명이 사지를 말에 묶어 찢거나 수레바퀴에 매달아 부수던 형벌 대신 들여놓은 그 형벌일 뿐입니다. 복수만 이룬다면 그깟 형벌이 무슨 대수겠습니까? 솔직히 말해, 십중팔구 저 가엾은 페피노의 목이 떨어지지 않게 되어 거의 아쉬울 지경입니다. 그랬다면 두 분께서도 그 형벌이 얼마나 짧게 끝나는지, 입에 올릴 가치나 있는지 직접 보실 기회가 있었을 텐데 말이지요. 그건 그렇고, 사육제에 어울리지 않는 참 묘한 대화로군요, 여러분. 어쩌다 이런 이야기가 나왔을까요? 아, 생각났습니다. 두 분께서 제 창가의 자리를 청하셨지요. 자리는 드리겠습니다. 하지만 우선 식탁에 앉읍시다. 마침 아침 식사가 준비됐다고 알리러 하인이 오는군요.”\n\n그가 말하는 사이, 하인이 방에 난 네 개의 문 가운데 하나를 열며 말했다.\n\n“알 수오 코모도!”\n\n두 젊은이가 일어나 식당으로 들어갔다.\n\n훌륭하고 흠잡을 데 없이 차려진 식사 동안, 프란츠는 거듭 알베르를 바라보며 주인의 말이 그에게 어떤 인상을 남겼는지 살피려 했다. 그런 인상을 남기지 않았을 리 없다고 여겼던 것이다. 그러나 알베르가 늘 그렇듯 무심해서 별로 귀담아듣지 않았는지, 결투에 관한 몬테크리스토 백작의 해명에 만족했는지, 아니면 프란츠만이 알고 있는 그 사건들이 프란츠에게만 영향을 미쳤는지는 알 수 없었으나, 어쨌든 그가 보기에 동행은 그 말들에 조금도 마음을 두지 않았다. 오히려 지난 너덧 달 동안 이탈리아 요리를, 그러니까 세상에서 가장 형편없는 요리를 먹으라는 선고라도 받았던 사람처럼 게걸스레 먹어 댔다.\n\n백작으로 말하자면, 음식에 거의 손을 대지 않았다. 손님들과 함께 식탁에 앉아 주인의 도리를 다하는 것처럼 보였고, 손님들이 떠난 뒤에야 무언가 색다르거나 더 정교한 음식을 들려는 듯했다. 이 광경은 프란츠의 의지와 상관없이, 백작이 G백작 부인에게 불러일으켰던 그 공포, 그리고 맞은편 좌석의 사내가 흡혈귀라던 부인의 확고한 믿음을 다시 떠올리게 했다.\n\n식사가 끝나 갈 무렵 프란츠가 시계를 꺼냈다.\n\n“이런,” 백작이 말했다. “뭘 하시는 겁니까?”\n\n“양해해 주십시오, 백작님.” 프란츠가 대꾸했다. “하지만 저희는 아직 할 일이 많아서요.”\n\n“그게 무엇입니까?”\n\n“가면이 없습니다. 그것을 반드시 마련해야 하거든요.”\n\n“그 일이라면 염려 마십시오. 제 생각에 포폴로 광장에 저희가 쓸 별실이 하나 있습니다. 두 분이 고르시는 의상은 무엇이든 그리로 가져다 두게 할 테니, 거기서 갈아입으시면 됩니다.”\n\n“처형이 끝난 뒤에 말입니까?” 프란츠가 외쳤다.\n\n“그 전이든 후든, 원하시는 대로요.”\n\n“처형대를 마주 보고서요?”\n\n“처형대도 축제의 한 부분이니까요.”\n\n“백작님, 곰곰이 생각해 보았습니다.” 프란츠가 말했다. “호의는 감사하지만, 저는 마차의 자리와 로스폴리 궁전 창가의 자리만 받아들이는 것으로 만족하겠습니다. 포폴로 광장의 제 자리는 백작님께서 마음대로 처분하셔도 좋습니다.”\n\n“하지만 미리 말씀드리는데, 아주 진기한 구경거리를 놓치시는 겁니다.” 백작이 대꾸했다.\n\n“그건 백작님께서 제게 들려주시면 됩니다.” 프란츠가 대답했다. “백작님 입에서 나온 이야기라면, 제가 직접 본 것이나 다름없이 큰 인상을 남길 테니까요. 저는 한두 번 처형을 직접 볼까 마음먹은 적이 있지만, 끝내 결심이 서지 않았습니다. 자네는 어떤가, 알베르?”\n\n“나는,” 자작이 대답했다. “나는 카스탱이 처형되는 걸 봤지. 하지만 그날은 좀 취해 있었던 것 같아. 그날 아침에 막 학교를 나왔고, 전날 밤을 선술집에서 보냈거든.”\n\n“게다가 파리에서 처형을 보지 못했다고 해서 다른 데서도 보지 말란 법은 없지요. 여행이란 모든 것을 보러 다니는 것이니까요. 누가 ‘로마에서는 어떻게 처형하던가?’ 하고 물었을 때 ‘모르겠습니다!’ 하고 대답한다면, 그 꼴이 어떻겠습니까. 게다가 듣자 하니 그 죄인은 자기를 친자식처럼 길러 준 훌륭한 참사회원을 장작개비로 때려죽인 파렴치한 악당이랍니다. 빌어먹을! 성직자를 죽일 거라면 적어도 장작개비가 아닌 다른 무기를 써야지요. 더구나 그자가 아버지처럼 대해 준 분이었다면 말입니다. 스페인에 가면 투우를 보지 않겠습니까? 자, 이것도 투우를 보러 간다고 치십시오. 옛 로마 원형 경기장의 로마인들을, 사자 삼백 마리와 사람 백 명을 죽이던 그 구경거리를 떠올려 보십시오. 환호하던 팔만 명의 관중을, 딸들을 데리고 오던 점잖은 부인들을, 그리고 그 하얀 손의 엄지로 ‘자, 죽어 가는 자를 끝장내라.’라는 치명적인 신호를 보내던 매혹적인 베스타 무녀들을 떠올려 보십시오.”\n\n“그럼 자네도 가겠나, 알베르?” 프란츠가 물었다.\n\n“맙소사, 가야지. 나도 자네처럼 망설였지만, 백작님의 능변에 마음을 정했네.”\n\n“그럼 가세.” 프란츠가 말했다. “자네가 원하니 말이야. 하지만 포폴로 광장으로 가는 길에 코르소 거리를 지나고 싶군요. 그럴 수 있겠습니까, 백작님?”\n\n“걸어서라면 됩니다만, 마차로는 안 됩니다.”\n\n“그럼 걸어서 가겠습니다.”\n\n“꼭 그쪽 길로 가셔야 합니까?”\n\n“네, 보고 싶은 게 있어서요.”\n\n“좋습니다, 그럼 코르소를 거쳐 가지요. 마차는 바부이노 거리를 통해 포폴로 광장에서 기다리도록 보내 두겠습니다. 저도 제가 내려 둔 몇 가지 지시가 제대로 이행됐는지 보러 코르소를 직접 지나가면 좋겠으니까요.”\n\n“각하,” 하인이 문을 열며 말했다. “고행자 옷차림을 한 사내가 각하를 뵙고자 합니다.”\n\n“아! 그래.” 백작이 대꾸했다. “누군지 알겠군. 여러분, 응접실로 돌아가 계시겠습니까? 가운데 탁자에 좋은 시가가 있을 겁니다. 곧 가지요.”\n\n두 젊은이가 일어나 응접실로 돌아갔고, 백작은 다시 한번 양해를 구하고는 다른 문으로 나갔다. 대단한 애연가였던 알베르는 파리 카페의 시가를 못 피우는 것을 적잖은 희생으로 여기던 터라, 탁자로 다가갔다가 진짜 푸로스 시가를 발견하고는 기쁨의 탄성을 질렀다.\n\n“그래,” 프란츠가 물었다. “몬테크리스토 백작을 어떻게 생각하나?”\n\n“어떻게 생각하느냐고?” 알베르는 동행이 그런 질문을 하는 것에 적잖이 놀란 기색이었다. “더할 나위 없이 유쾌한 사람이라고 생각하네. 식탁의 주인 노릇을 훌륭히 해내고, 여행도 많이 했고, 책도 많이 읽었고, 브루투스처럼 스토아학파에 속한 사람이지. 게다가,” 그가 천장을 향해 연기를 한 모금 내뿜으며 덧붙였다. “최고급 시가를 갖고 있고 말일세.”\n\n이것이 백작에 대한 알베르의 견해였다. 알베르가 오랜 숙고 끝에야 비로소 견해를 내놓는다고 공언하는 사람임을 잘 아는 프란츠는, 그 생각을 굳이 바꾸려 들지 않았다.\n\n“그런데,” 그가 말했다. “아주 묘한 점 하나를 눈치챘나?”\n\n“뭘 말인가?”\n\n“백작이 얼마나 자네를 유심히 보던지.”\n\n“나를?”\n\n“그래.”\n\n알베르가 곰곰이 생각했다. “아,” 그가 한숨을 쉬며 대답했다. “그건 그리 놀랄 일이 아니야. 내가 파리를 떠나온 지 일 년이 넘었으니, 옷차림이 한물간 구식이지 않은가. 백작은 나를 시골뜨기로 본 거야. 기회가 닿거든 부디 그 오해를 풀어 주게. 나는 결코 그런 사람이 아니라고 말일세.”\n\n프란츠가 미소 지었다. 잠시 후 백작이 들어왔다.\n\n“이제 두 분 분부대로 따르겠습니다, 여러분.” 그가 말했다. “마차는 한쪽 길로 포폴로 광장으로 가고, 우리는 다른 길로, 괜찮으시다면 코르소를 거쳐 가지요. 시가를 좀 더 챙기십시오, 모르세르 씨.”\n\n“기꺼이 그러지요.” 알베르가 대꾸했다. “이탈리아 시가는 끔찍하거든요. 파리에 오시면 이 모든 것을 갚아 드리겠습니다.”\n\n“사양하지 않겠습니다. 곧 그리로 갈 작정이니, 허락해 주신다면 댁으로 찾아뵙지요. 자, 지체할 시간이 없습니다. 벌써 열두 시 반이군요. 출발합시다.”\n\n세 사람이 모두 아래로 내려갔다. 마부는 주인의 지시를 받고 바부이노 거리로 마차를 몰았다. 세 신사가 스페인 광장과 프라티나 거리를 걷는 동안, 그 길은 피아노 궁전과 로스폴리 궁전 사이로 곧장 이어졌는데, 프란츠의 시선은 그 마지막 궁전의 창들로 향했다. 망토를 두른 사내와 트라스테베레의 농부 사이에 정해 두었던 그 신호를 잊지 않고 있었던 것이다.\n\n“백작님 창은 어느 것입니까?” 그가 애써 무심한 척하며 백작에게 물었다.\n\n“마지막 세 개입니다.” 백작이 대답했다. 무심한 태도는 전혀 꾸민 것이 아니었다. 그 질문에 어떤 속셈이 있는지 짐작조차 할 수 없었으니까.\n\n프란츠는 재빨리 그 세 창으로 눈길을 던졌다. 양옆 창에는 노란 다마스크 천이 드리워져 있었고, 가운데 창에는 흰 다마스크 천에 붉은 십자가가 새겨져 있었다. 망토를 두른 사내는 트라스테베레의 농부에게 한 약속을 지켰던 것이다. 이제 그가 백작이라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없었다.\n\n세 창은 여전히 비어 있었다. 사방에서 준비가 한창이었다. 의자가 놓이고, 관람대가 세워지고, 창마다 깃발이 드리워졌다. 가면을 쓴 사람들은 아직 나설 수 없었고, 마차도 돌아다닐 수 없었다. 그러나 창과 마차와 문 뒤로는 가면을 쓴 사람들이 보였다.\n\n프란츠와 알베르, 그리고 백작은 코르소를 따라 계속 내려갔다. 포폴로 광장에 가까워질수록 군중은 더욱 빽빽해졌고, 무리의 머리 위로 두 개의 물체가 보였다. 하나는 광장 한복판을 표시하는, 꼭대기에 십자가가 얹힌 오벨리스크였다. 또 하나는 그 오벨리스크 앞, 바부이노와 코르소와 리페타, 세 거리가 만나는 지점에 선 처형대의 두 기둥이었다. 그 사이로 만다이아의 굽은 칼날이 번득였다.\n\n거리 모퉁이에서 그들은 주인을 기다리던 백작의 집사를 만났다. 백작이 분명 손님들에게 숨기고 싶어 했을, 터무니없는 값에 빌린 그 창은 바부이노 거리와 핀초 언덕 사이에 자리한 큰 궁전의 이 층에 있었다. 앞서 말했듯이 그곳은 침실로 통하는 작은 화장실이었고, 사이 문을 닫으면 그 안의 사람들은 완전히 단둘이 있게 되었다. 의자 위에는 파랑과 흰빛 새틴으로 지은 우아한 가장무도회 의상이 놓여 있었다.\n\n“두 분께서 의상 고르는 일을 제게 맡기셨으니,” 백작이 두 친구에게 말했다. “올해 가장 많이들 입을 이걸 가져다 두라고 했습니다. 게다가 콘페티, 그러니까 사탕 알갱이 때문에라도 이게 가장 알맞지요. 밀가루가 묻어도 표가 나지 않으니까요.”\n\n프란츠는 백작의 말을 어렴풋이 들었을 뿐, 자신들의 바람을 또 한 번 헤아려 준 이 배려를 어쩌면 제대로 음미하지 못했다. 그는 포폴로 광장이 펼쳐 보이는 광경과, 그 한복판에 놓인 무시무시한 기계에 온통 사로잡혀 있었다.\n\n프란츠가 단두대를 본 것은 그때가 처음이었다. 단두대라고 말하는 것은, 로마의 만다이아가 프랑스의 기계와 거의 같은 본을 따 만든 것이기 때문이다. 초승달 모양으로 굽은 칼날이 볼록한 쪽으로 베어 내린다는 점, 그리고 떨어지는 높이가 좀 더 낮다는 점, 차이라고는 그것뿐이었다.\n\n희생자를 눕히는 그 움직이는 널빤지 위에 두 사내가 걸터앉아 죄인을 기다리며 아침을 먹고 있었다. 그들의 식사는 보아하니 빵과 소시지였다. 그중 한 사람이 널빤지를 들어 올려 포도주 병을 꺼내더니 한 모금 마시고는 동료에게 건넸다. 이 두 사람은 처형인의 조수였다.\n\n이 광경에 프란츠는 이마에 땀이 솟는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n전날 저녁 카르체리 누오베에서 산타 마리아 델 포폴로 작은 성당으로 옮겨진 죄수들은, 저마다 사제 두 명을 거느린 채 쇠창살로 닫힌 예배당에서 밤을 보냈다. 그 앞에는 보초 두 사람이 서서 번갈아 교대하고 있었다. 성당 문 양옆으로 늘어선 두 줄의 기마총병이 처형대까지 이어져 그 둘레를 에워쌌고, 폭 십 피트가량의 길을 터놓았으며, 단두대 둘레로는 백 피트 가까운 공간을 비워 두었다.\n\n광장의 나머지는 온통 사람 머리로 메워져 있었다. 많은 여인이 어깨 위에 갓난아기를 얹고 있어서, 아이들이 가장 잘 볼 수 있었다. 핀초 언덕은 구경꾼으로 가득 찬 거대한 원형 경기장 같았다. 바부이노 거리와 리페타 거리 모퉁이에 선 두 성당의 발코니도 꽉 들어찼다. 심지어 계단마저 현관 쪽으로 밀려가는 알록달록한 바다처럼 보였다. 담벼락의 움푹한 곳마다 살아 있는 조각상을 하나씩 품고 있었다. 백작의 말은 사실이었다. 삶에서 가장 진기한 구경거리는 죽음의 광경이었다.\n\n그런데도 그 자리에 마땅히 깃들어야 할 침묵과 엄숙함 대신, 군중에게서는 웃음과 농담이 터져 나왔다. 사람들의 눈에 그 처형이 그저 사육제의 시작에 불과하다는 것이 분명했다.\n\n별안간, 마법에라도 걸린 듯 소란이 그쳤고, 성당 문이 열렸다. 머리부터 발끝까지 잿빛 거친 삼베 옷을 두르고 눈 부분에 구멍을 뚫은, 손에는 불 켜진 양초를 든 고행자 단체가 가장 먼저 모습을 드러냈다. 그 우두머리가 맨 앞에서 행진했다.\n\n고행자들 뒤로 거대한 체구와 풍채를 지닌 사내가 따라 나왔다. 그는 왼쪽에 칼집에 든 큼직한 칼을 매단 천 바지 외에는 아무것도 걸치지 않은 알몸이었고, 오른쪽 어깨에는 묵직한 쇠메를 짊어지고 있었다.\n\n이 사내가 처형인이었다.\n\n그는 또 끈으로 동여맨 샌들을 발에 신고 있었다.\n\n처형인 뒤로는 죽을 순서대로, 먼저 페피노가, 그다음에 안드레아가 따라왔다. 둘 다 사제 두 명을 거느리고 있었다. 어느 쪽도 눈을 가리지 않았다.\n\n페피노는 자신을 기다리는 운명을 분명 알고 있는 듯, 흔들림 없는 걸음으로 걸었다. 안드레아는 사제 두 사람의 부축을 받았다. 두 죄인은 이따금 고해 신부가 내미는 십자가상에 입을 맞추었다.\n\n이 광경을 보는 것만으로도 프란츠는 다리가 후들거리는 것을 느꼈다. 그는 알베르를 보았다. 알베르는 셔츠처럼 새하얗게 질려, 채 반도 피우지 않은 시가를 무의식중에 던져 버렸다. 오직 백작만이 동요하지 않은 듯 보였다. 아니, 그뿐 아니라 그 창백한 두 뺨에는 옅은 핏기가 돌려고 안간힘을 쓰는 듯했다. 콧구멍은 먹잇감의 냄새를 맡은 야수처럼 벌름거렸고, 반쯤 벌어진 입술 사이로는 자칼의 것처럼 작고 날카로운 흰 이가 드러났다. 그러면서도 그의 표정에는 프란츠가 일찍이 그에게서 본 적 없는, 미소 띤 다정함이 어려 있었다. 특히 그 검은 두 눈은 친절과 연민으로 가득했다.\n\n그러는 사이 두 죄인이 다가왔고, 가까워질수록 그 얼굴이 또렷이 보였다. 페피노는 햇볕에 그을린, 스물네댓 살쯤 된 잘생긴 청년이었다. 그는 고개를 꼿꼿이 쳐들고, 자신을 구해 줄 사람이 어느 쪽에서 나타날지 살피려는 듯 경계하는 눈빛이었다. 안드레아는 키가 작고 뚱뚱했다. 잔혹함이 짐승처럼 박혀 있는 그 얼굴로는 나이를 가늠할 수 없었다. 서른쯤 되었을까. 감옥에서 수염을 길게 길렀고, 머리는 어깨 위로 떨궈져 있었으며, 두 다리는 휘청거리고, 그 몸짓은 보아하니 의식 없는 자동인형 같았다.\n\n“저는,” 프란츠가 백작에게 말했다. “처형이 한 건뿐일 거라고 말씀하셨던 걸로 압니다만.”\n\n“사실대로 말씀드린 겁니다.” 백작이 차갑게 대답했다.\n\n“그런데 죄인이 둘이지 않습니까.”\n\n“그렇지요. 하지만 이 둘 중 한 사람만 죽게 됩니다. 다른 하나는 앞으로 여러 해를 더 살 거고요.”\n\n“사면이 내려질 거라면, 지체할 시간이 없겠군요.”\n\n“보십시오, 저기 오고 있습니다.” 백작이 말했다. 페피노가 만다이아 발치에 다다른 바로 그 순간, 한 사제가 서둘러 달려와 병사들 사이를 헤치고 나아가더니, 고행자 단체의 우두머리에게 다가가 접힌 종이 한 장을 건넸다. 페피노의 날카로운 눈은 그 모든 것을 놓치지 않았다. 우두머리는 종이를 받아 펼치고는 손을 들어 올리며 큰 소리로 외쳤다. “하늘과 교황 성하께 영광을! 여기 죄수 한 사람에 대한 사면장이 있노라!”\n\n“사면이다!” 군중이 한목소리로 외쳤다. “사면이야!”\n\n이 외침에 안드레아가 고개를 들었다.\n\n“누구에 대한 사면이오?” 그가 외쳤다.\n\n페피노는 숨을 죽인 채 가만히 있었다.\n\n“로카 프리오리라 불리는 페피노에 대한 사면이오.” 우두머리 수도사가 말했다. 그러고는 기마총병을 지휘하는 장교에게 종이를 건넸고, 장교는 그것을 읽고 다시 돌려주었다.\n\n“페피노에게라고!” 빠져 있던 마비에서 깨어난 듯 안드레아가 외쳤다. “어째서 저놈에게는 사면이고 내게는 아니란 말이오? 우리는 함께 죽어야 마땅하오. 저놈도 나와 함께 죽기로 약속받았소. 당신들에겐 나 혼자만 죽일 권리가 없소. 나 혼자는 죽지 않겠소. 절대 안 돼!”\n\n그러고는 야수처럼 발버둥치고 미쳐 날뛰며 사제들을 뿌리치고, 손을 묶은 밧줄을 끊어 보려고 필사적으로 몸부림쳤다. 처형인이 신호를 보내자 두 조수가 처형대에서 뛰어내려 그를 붙잡았다.\n\n“무슨 일입니까?” 프란츠가 백작에게 물었다. 오가는 말이 모두 로마 사투리였던 탓에 제대로 알아듣지 못했던 것이다.\n\n“보이지 않으십니까?” 백작이 대꾸했다. “죽음을 앞둔 저 인간이, 함께 형을 받던 자가 자기와 같이 죽지 않는다고 길길이 날뛰는 게 말입니다. 할 수만 있다면 저자는, 제가 곧 잃게 될 그 목숨을 동료가 누리게 두느니 차라리 이빨과 손톱으로 그를 갈가리 찢어 놓고 말 겁니다. 오, 인간이여, 인간이여, 악어의 족속이여.” 백작이 군중을 향해 꽉 쥔 두 주먹을 내뻗으며 외쳤다. “나는 저기서 너희를 똑똑히 알아보겠노라. 너희가 언제나 너희다움을 잃지 않음을!”\n\n그러는 동안 안드레아와 두 처형인은 땅바닥에서 뒤엉켜 싸웠고, 그는 연신 부르짖었다. “저놈도 죽어야 해! 저놈도 죽일 거야! 나 혼자는 죽지 않아!”\n\n“보십시오, 보세요.” 백작이 두 젊은이의 손을 붙잡으며 외쳤다. “보십시오, 맹세코 흥미로운 일이니까요. 여기 자기 운명을 체념하고 받아들였던 사내가, 처형대로 죽으러 가던 사내가 있습니다. 비겁한 꼴이긴 했어도, 어쨌든 저항 없이 죽으려던 참이었지요. 무엇이 그에게 그런 힘을 주었는지 아십니까? 무엇이 그를 위로했는지 아십니까? 또 다른 자가 그의 형벌을 함께 나누고, 또 다른 자가 그의 고통을 함께 나누며, 또 다른 자가 그보다 먼저 죽는다는 것이었습니다! 양 두 마리를 도살장으로 끌고 가고, 소 두 마리를 도축장으로 끌고 가서, 그중 하나에게 동료는 죽지 않는다고 알아듣게 해 보십시오. 양은 기뻐서 매애 울고, 소는 즐거워서 음매 울 겁니다. 그러나 인간은, 신이 자신의 형상대로 지으신 인간은, 신이 가장 으뜸가고 유일한 계명, 곧 네 이웃을 사랑하라는 계명을 내리신 그 인간은, 신이 제 생각을 표현하라고 목소리를 주신 그 인간은, 제 동료가 살아났다는 말을 들었을 때 가장 먼저 무엇을 외칩니까? 저주입니다. 인간에게 영광을, 이 자연의 걸작에게, 이 만물의 왕에게 영광을!”\n\n그러더니 백작은 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 끔찍한 웃음이었다. 저토록 웃을 수 있으려면 그가 얼마나 처참하게 고통받았을지를 짐작하게 하는 웃음이었다.\n\n그러는 동안에도 싸움은 계속되었고, 차마 지켜보기 끔찍했다. 두 조수가 안드레아를 처형대 위로 끌어 올렸다. 사람들은 모두 안드레아에게 등을 돌렸고, 이만 명의 목소리가 외쳤다. “저놈을 죽여라! 죽여 버려라!”\n\n프란츠가 흠칫 물러섰으나, 백작이 그의 팔을 붙잡아 창 앞에 붙들어 세웠다.\n\n“뭘 하시는 겁니까?” 그가 말했다. “저자가 가엾으십니까? ‘미친개다!’ 하는 외침을 들으면, 당신은 총을 집어 들 겁니다. 따지고 보면 다른 개에게 물린 죄밖에 없는 그 가엾은 짐승을, 당신은 망설임 없이 쏘아 버리겠지요. 그러면서 같은 족속에게 물린 적도 없이 제 은인을 죽인 사내를, 이제는 손이 묶여 누구도 죽일 수 없게 되자 옥에 갇힌 동료가 죽기를 바라는 그 사내를 가엾게 여기십니까? 아닙니다, 아니에요. 보십시오, 보세요!”\n\n그런 권유는 부질없었다. 프란츠는 그 끔찍한 광경에 넋이 나가 있었다.\n\n두 조수가 안드레아를 처형대로 끌어다 놓고는, 그의 발버둥과 깨물기와 비명에도 아랑곳없이 무릎을 꿇려 놓았다. 그사이 처형인은 쇠메를 들어 올리고는 조수들에게 비키라고 신호했다. 죄인이 일어서려고 안간힘을 썼으나, 미처 그럴 틈도 없이 쇠메가 그의 왼쪽 관자놀이에 떨어졌다. 둔하고 묵직한 소리가 울렸고, 사내는 소처럼 얼굴부터 고꾸라졌다가 다시 벌렁 나자빠졌다.\n\n처형인은 쇠메를 떨구고 칼을 뽑아 단칼에 그의 목을 갈랐다. 그러고는 그의 배 위로 올라타 두 발로 사납게 짓밟았다. 짓밟을 때마다 상처에서 핏줄기가 솟구쳤다.\n\n이번에는 프란츠도 더 이상 견디지 못하고, 반쯤 정신을 잃은 채 의자에 주저앉았다.\n\n알베르는 두 눈을 감은 채 창문 커튼을 움켜쥐고 서 있었다.\n\n백작은 복수의 천사처럼, 꼿꼿이 서서 의기양양해 있었다!\n\n# Chapter 36. The Carnival at Rome\n\n프란츠가 정신을 차렸을 때, 알베르는 물 한 잔을 마시고 있었다. 그 창백한 얼굴로 보아 물이 몹시 필요해 보였다. 백작은 가장무도회 의상을 걸치고 있었다. 프란츠는 무심코 광장 쪽을 바라보았다. 풍경은 완전히 달라져 있었다. 처형대도, 사형 집행인들도, 사형수도 모두 사라지고 없었다. 오직 사람들만 남아 떠들썩하게 들끓고 있었다. 교황의 서거와 사육제의 개막 때만 울리는 몬테 치토리오의 종이 흥겨운 가락으로 울려 퍼지고 있었다.\n\n\"그래서,\" 그가 백작에게 물었다. \"대체 무슨 일이 있었던 겁니까?\"\n\n\"아무 일도 없었습니다.\" 백작이 대답했다. \"보시다시피 사육제가 시작되었을 뿐이지요. 어서 옷을 갈아입으십시오.\"\n\n\"사실,\" 프란츠가 말했다. \"그 끔찍한 광경이 꿈처럼 지나가 버렸군요.\"\n\n\"당신을 어지럽힌 건 한낱 꿈, 악몽일 뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇지요, 제가 시달린 것은요. 그런데 그 죄인은요?\"\n\n\"그것도 꿈입니다. 다만 그는 여전히 잠들어 있고, 당신은 깨어났을 뿐이지요. 둘 중 누가 더 운이 좋은지 누가 알겠습니까?\"\n\n\"하지만 페피노는요. 그는 어떻게 됐습니까?\"\n\n\"페피노는 분별 있는 젊은이지요. 대개의 사람들은 주목받는 만큼 행복해하지만, 그는 달랐습니다. 사람들의 관심이 온통 자기 동료에게 쏠리는 걸 보고 기뻐했지요. 그 틈을 타 군중 속으로 슬쩍 빠져나갔습니다. 그를 따라온 갸륵한 신부들에게 고맙다는 인사조차 없이 말입니다. 정말이지 인간이란 배은망덕하고 이기적인 동물입니다. 자, 옷을 갈아입으시지요. 보십시오, 모르세르 씨가 본을 보이고 있지 않습니까.\"\n\n알베르는 검정 바지와 에나멜 장화 위로 새틴 판탈롱을 끌어올리고 있었다.\n\n\"그래, 알베르.\" 프란츠가 말했다. \"흥겨운 잔치에 끼어들고 싶은 마음이 좀 드나? 어디, 솔직히 말해 보게.\"\n\n\"맹세코 그렇지는 않아.\" 알베르가 대꾸했다. \"하지만 그런 광경을 본 건 정말 다행이라고 생각해. 그리고 백작이 한 말이 이제 이해가 가. 그런 구경거리에 한번 익숙해지고 나면, 그것만이 사람을 동요시키는 유일한 광경이 된다는 말 말이야.\"\n\n\"이 순간이야말로 인간의 본성을 들여다볼 수 있는 유일한 때라는 건 생각지 못하셨군요.\" 백작이 말했다. \"처형대 계단에서 죽음은 평생 써 온 가면을 벗겨 내고, 진짜 얼굴을 드러냅니다. 안드레아가 그리 잘생긴 얼굴이 아니었다는 건 인정해야겠지요, 그 흉측한 악당 말입니다! 자, 어서 옷을 입으세요, 여러분, 어서요.\"\n\n프란츠는 두 동행을 따르지 않으면 우스워 보이리라는 것을 깨달았다. 그는 의상을 걸치고, 자신의 얼굴빛에 거의 미치지 못할 만큼 창백한 가면을 썼다. 단장을 마치고 그들은 아래로 내려갔다. 마차가 문 앞에서 그들을 기다리고 있었는데, 사탕과 꽃다발로 가득 차 있었다. 그들은 마차들의 행렬 속으로 끼어들었다.\n\n이 완벽한 변화를 머릿속에 그려 보기란 쉽지 않다. 음울하고 적막한 죽음의 광경 대신, 포폴로 광장은 흥겹고 떠들썩한 환락과 잔치의 무대로 바뀌어 있었다. 가면을 쓴 무리가 사방에서 쏟아져 나와, 문을 통해 흘러나오고 창문에서 내려왔다. 거리마다 모퉁이마다 마차들이 달려 나왔다. 그 안에는 어릿광대, 아를레키노, 도미노 가면을 쓴 자들, 무언극 배우들, 판토마임 연기자들, 트라스테베레 사람들, 기사들, 농부들이 가득 타고 있었다. 그들은 소리를 지르고, 싸우고, 손짓을 해 대며, 밀가루를 채운 달걀과 콘페티, 꽃다발을 던졌다. 친구든 적이든, 동료든 낯선 사람이든 가리지 않고 비아냥과 투척물로 공격했지만, 아무도 화내지 않고 그저 웃기만 했다.\n\n프란츠와 알베르는 격렬한 슬픔을 떨쳐 내려고 술에 기대는 사람들 같았다. 마시고 또 마시며 취해 갈수록, 과거와 현재 사이에 두꺼운 장막이 드리워지는 것을 느끼는 사람들 말이다. 그들은 자신들이 목격한 장면을 보았다, 아니 계속 보고 있었다. 하지만 차츰차츰 전반적인 현기증이 그들을 사로잡았고, 그들은 어쩔 수 없이 그 소란과 혼돈에 끼어들지 않을 수 없게 되었다.\n\n옆을 지나던 마차에서 날아온 콘페티 한 줌이 모르세르와 두 동행을 먼지로 뒤덮었다. 그러면서 가면으로 가리지 못한 그의 목과 얼굴 일부를 바늘 백 개로 찌르듯 따끔하게 만들었다. 이에 자극받은 그는 주위의 가면 쓴 사람들이 모두 벌이고 있는 전반적인 싸움에 끼어들었다. 그도 자리에서 일어나 마차에 가득한 콘페티와 사탕을 한 움큼씩 집어, 자신이 부릴 수 있는 온 힘과 솜씨로 던졌다.\n\n싸움이 본격적으로 시작되었다. 반 시간 전에 본 광경의 기억은 두 젊은이의 머릿속에서 차츰 지워져 갔다. 지금 눈앞에 펼쳐진 흥겹고 화려한 행렬에 그만큼 정신이 팔려 있었기 때문이다.\n\n몬테크리스토 백작으로 말하자면, 그는 단 한순간도 동요한 기색을 내비치지 않았다. 한쪽 끝에서 다른 끝까지 높다란 궁전들이 늘어선, 크고 화려한 코르소 대로를 상상해 보라. 발코니에는 양탄자가 걸려 있고, 창문에는 깃발이 나부낀다. 그 발코니에는 삼십만 명의 구경꾼이 있다. 로마 사람들, 이탈리아 사람들, 세계 각지에서 온 이방인들, 혈통과 부와 재능을 두루 갖춘 귀족들이 한데 모여 있다. 사랑스러운 여인들이 그 분위기에 이끌려 발코니 너머로 몸을 기울이거나 창밖으로 상체를 내밀고 콘페티를 흩뿌리면, 꽃다발이 되돌아온다. 떨어지는 콘페티와 날아다니는 꽃으로 하늘이 어두워진 듯하다. 거리에서는 활기찬 군중이 더없이 기상천외한 의상을 입고 있다. 거대한 양배추가 점잖게 걸어 다니고, 물소 머리가 사람들의 어깨 위에서 울부짖으며, 개가 뒷다리로 걷는다. 이 모든 것 한가운데서 가면이 들어 올려지면, 칼로의 「성 안토니우스의 유혹」에서처럼 사랑스러운 얼굴이 드러난다. 우리는 그 뒤를 따르고 싶지만, 악귀들의 무리에 가로막혀 떨어지고 만다. 이것으로 로마 사육제의 어렴풋한 인상이나마 짐작할 수 있으리라.\n\n두 번째 모퉁이에서 백작은 마차를 세우고, 자리를 뜨게 해 달라고 청하면서 마차는 두 사람이 마음대로 쓰라고 했다. 프란츠가 고개를 들어 보니, 그들은 로스폴리 궁전 맞은편에 있었다. 한가운데 창문, 붉은 십자가가 그려진 흰색 다마스크 천을 두른 그 창문에 푸른 도미노가 보였다. 프란츠는 그 가면 아래에 아르헨티나 극장의 아름다운 그리스 여인을 손쉽게 떠올렸다.\n\n\"여러분,\" 백작이 마차에서 뛰어내리며 말했다. \"배우 노릇에 지쳐 이 광경의 구경꾼이 되고 싶어지거든, 제 창문에 자리가 마련되어 있다는 걸 기억하십시오. 그동안 제 마부와 마차, 하인들을 마음껏 부리시고요.\"\n\n미처 말하지 못했는데, 백작의 마부는 「곰과 파샤」에 나오는 오드리와 똑같이 곰 가죽을 뒤집어쓰고 있었다. 뒤에 선 두 하인은 초록빛 원숭이로 분장하고, 용수철 달린 가면을 쓴 채 지나가는 사람마다 우스꽝스러운 표정을 지어 보였다.\n\n프란츠는 백작의 배려에 고마움을 표했다. 알베르는 가까이 지나가는, 로마 농부들로 가득 찬 마차에 꽃다발을 던지느라 여념이 없었다. 그에게는 불운하게도, 마차 행렬이 다시 움직이기 시작했다. 그가 포폴로 광장으로 내려가는 동안, 다른 마차는 베네치아 궁전 쪽으로 올라가 버렸다.\n\n\"아, 이보게.\" 그가 프란츠에게 말했다. \"자네 못 봤나?\"\n\n\"뭘?\"\n\n\"저기, 로마 농부들이 가득 탄 저 사륜마차 말이야.\"\n\n\"못 봤네.\"\n\"음, 분명히 다들 매력적인 여자들일 거야.\"\n\n\"가면을 쓰고 있었으니 안됐군, 알베르.\" 프란츠가 말했다. \"지난번 실망을 만회할 기회였는데 말이야.\"\n\n\"오.\" 그가 반은 웃으며 반은 진지하게 대꾸했다. \"이번 사육제가 이런저런 보상 한 번 없이 지나가지는 않을 거라고 믿네.\"\n\n하지만 알베르의 바람과 달리, 그날은 로마 농부들로 가득 찬 그 마차와 두세 번 마주친 것 말고는 아무 일도 없이 지나갔다. 그렇게 마주치던 어느 순간, 우연인지 일부러인지 알베르의 가면이 벗겨졌다. 그는 즉시 일어나 남은 꽃다발을 그 마차 안으로 던졌다. 알베르가 그 교태 어린 변장 아래로 알아본 매력적인 여인들 중 하나가 그의 호의에 마음이 움직인 게 분명했다. 두 친구의 마차가 그녀 곁을 지날 때, 그녀가 제비꽃 한 다발을 던졌으니까. 알베르가 그것을 붙잡았다. 프란츠는 그것이 자기에게 던진 것이라고 여길 이유가 없었으므로, 알베르가 그것을 간직하도록 내버려 두었다. 알베르는 그것을 단춧구멍에 꽂았고, 마차는 의기양양하게 나아갔다.\n\n\"그래.\" 프란츠가 그에게 말했다. \"모험의 시작이로군.\"\n\n\"비웃고 싶으면 비웃게. 나는 정말 그렇다고 생각하니까. 그러니 이 꽃다발을 버리지 않겠어.\"\n\n\"이런.\" 프란츠가 웃으며 대꾸했다. \"자네 배은망덕의 증표로군.\"\n\n그러나 그 농담은 곧 진담이 되는 듯했다. 알베르와 프란츠가 그 콘타디니가 탄 마차와 다시 마주쳤을 때, 알베르에게 제비꽃을 던진 여인이 그의 단춧구멍에 꽂힌 그 꽃을 보고는 손뼉을 쳤기 때문이다.\n\n\"브라보, 브라보.\" 프란츠가 말했다. \"일이 놀랍게 풀리는군. 내가 자리를 비켜 줄까? 혼자 있는 편이 낫겠나?\"\n\n\"아니.\" 그가 대답했다. \"오페라 무도회에서들 하는 말마따나, 첫 만남에 시계탑 아래 밀회 약속에나 넘어가는 바보처럼 굴지는 않겠어. 저 어여쁜 농부 아가씨가 일을 더 진전시키고 싶다면, 우리가 그녀를 찾아내겠지. 아니, 내일은 그녀가 우리를 찾아낼 거야. 그러면 그녀가 무슨 신호든 보내올 테고, 나는 내가 뭘 해야 할지 알게 되겠지.\"\n\n\"맹세코,\" 프란츠가 말했다. \"자네는 네스토르만큼 현명하고 율리시스만큼 신중하군. 자네의 그 어여쁜 키르케가 자네를 무슨 짐승으로든 둔갑시키려면, 어지간히 솜씨가 좋거나 어지간히 위력이 세야 할걸세.\"\n\n알베르의 말이 옳았다. 그 어여쁜 미지의 여인은 분명 이 밀회를 더는 진전시키지 않기로 마음먹은 듯했다. 두 젊은이가 몇 바퀴를 더 돌았지만, 그 사륜마차를 다시 보지 못했으니까. 마차는 근처 골목 중 하나로 접어들어 버렸다. 그들은 다시 로스폴리 궁전으로 돌아왔다. 하지만 백작도 푸른 도미노도 사라지고 없었다. 노란 다마스크 천을 두른 두 창문에는 백작이 초대한 사람들이 여전히 자리하고 있었다.\n\n이때, 가장행렬의 시작을 알렸던 바로 그 종이 퇴장을 알렸다. 코르소의 마차 행렬이 대열을 풀었고, 순식간에 모든 마차가 사라졌다. 프란츠와 알베르는 무라테 거리 맞은편에 있었다. 마부는 한마디도 없이 그 길을 올라가, 스페인 광장과 로스폴리 궁전을 지나 호텔 문 앞에 멈췄다. 파스트리니 씨가 손님들을 맞으러 문으로 나왔다.\n\n프란츠는 서둘러 백작의 안부를 물으며, 백작이 제때 돌아오지 못한 것을 안타까워했다. 하지만 파스트리니는 몬테크리스토 백작이 자신을 위해 두 번째 마차를 따로 마련해 두었고, 그것이 네 시에 로스폴리 궁전으로 그를 데리러 갔다고 안심시켰다.\n\n게다가 백작은 그에게 두 친구에게 아르헨티나 극장의 자기 특별석 열쇠를 건네 달라고 부탁해 두었다. 프란츠가 알베르에게 어쩔 셈이냐고 물었다. 하지만 알베르는 극장에 가기 전에 실행에 옮길 큰 계획이 있었다. 그는 대답 대신, 파스트리니 씨가 재단사를 구해 줄 수 있겠느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"재단사라뇨.\" 주인이 말했다. \"무엇 때문에요?\"\n\n\"지금부터 내일까지 우리 둘이 입을 로마 농부 의상 두 벌을 만들어야 해서요.\" 알베르가 대꾸했다.\n\n주인이 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"지금부터 내일까지 두 벌을 만들라굽쇼? 나리들, 송구하오나 그건 영락없이 프랑스식 요구이십니다요. 앞으로 일주일간은, 단추 하나에 한 닢씩을 쳐주신대도 조끼에 단추 여섯 개를 달아 주려 할 재단사를 단 한 명도 못 찾으실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 이 생각은 포기해야겠군요?\"\n\n\"아닙니다요. 이미 다 만들어진 게 있습죠. 다 제게 맡기십시오. 내일 아침 잠에서 깨시면, 만족하실 만한 의상들이 한 벌 갖춰져 있을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"이보게 알베르.\" 프란츠가 말했다. \"다 주인에게 맡기게. 이 사람은 이미 수완이 좋다는 걸 충분히 증명했으니. 우리는 조용히 저녁이나 들고, 그런 다음 「알제의 이탈리아 여인」을 보러 가세!\"\n\n\"좋아.\" 알베르가 대꾸했다. \"다만 파스트리니 씨, 내 친구와 나 둘 다 부탁한 의상을 내일 받는 일을 무엇보다 중하게 여긴다는 걸 잊지 마시오.\"\n\n주인은 자신을 믿어도 좋으며 두 분의 바람을 꼭 챙기겠다고 거듭 다짐했다. 그 말에 프란츠와 알베르는 각자의 방으로 올라가, 의상을 벗기 시작했다. 알베르는 옷을 벗으면서 제비꽃 다발을 조심스레 간직했다. 그것은 내일을 위해 남겨 둔 그의 증표였다.\n\n두 친구는 식탁에 앉았다. 하지만 몬테크리스토 백작의 식탁과 파스트리니 씨의 식탁 사이의 차이를 언급하지 않을 수 없었다. 프란츠는 백작에게 품게 된 듯한 거부감에도 불구하고, 진실 앞에서 파스트리니 쪽이 우세하지 않다는 것을 인정할 수밖에 없었다. 후식을 드는 동안, 하인이 몇 시에 마차를 부르실 거냐고 물었다. 알베르와 프란츠는 백작의 호의를 정말로 남용하는 게 아닐까 염려하며 서로를 바라보았다. 하인이 그 마음을 알아챘다.\n\n\"몬테크리스토 백작 각하께서는,\" 그가 말했다. \"마차를 종일 두 나리의 분부에 두라고 분명히 명하셨습니다. 그러니 조금도 폐가 될까 염려치 마시고 마음껏 쓰셔도 됩니다.\"\n\n그들은 백작의 호의를 누리기로 마음먹고 말에 마구를 채우라고 일렀다. 그동안 그들은 입고 있던 옷을 벗고 야회복으로 갈아입었다. 입고 있던 옷은 그동안 치른 숱한 싸움으로 다소 망가진 상태였다.\n\n이렇게 채비를 마치고 그들은 극장으로 가, 백작의 특별석에 자리를 잡았다. 첫 막이 진행되는 동안, G 백작 부인이 들어왔다. 그녀의 첫 시선은 전날 저녁 백작을 보았던 그 특별석으로 향했다. 그래서 그녀는, 자신이 프란츠에게 그토록 기이한 견해를 밝혔던 바로 그 인물 대신 프란츠와 알베르가 앉아 있는 것을 알아챘다. 그녀의 오페라 안경이 어찌나 그들 쪽에 고정되어 있던지, 프란츠는 그녀의 호기심을 채워 주지 않으면 매정한 일이 되겠다고 느꼈다. 이탈리아 극장 관객들은 특별석을 응접실 삼아 손님을 맞곤 하는데, 두 친구는 그 특권 중 하나를 빌려 백작 부인에게 인사를 하러 갔다. 그들이 들어서기가 무섭게, 그녀는 프란츠에게 상석에 앉으라고 손짓했다. 알베르는 그 뒤에 앉았다.\n\n\"그래.\" 그녀가 프란츠에게 앉을 새도 거의 주지 않고 말했다. \"이 새로운 러스벤 경과 친분을 트는 일보다 더 나은 일이 없으셨나 봐요. 벌써 둘도 없는 친구 사이가 되셨다지요.\"\n\n\"거기까지 나아간 건 아닙니다만, 친애하는 백작 부인.\" 프란츠가 대꾸했다. \"온종일 그의 호의를 남용한 건 부인할 수 없겠군요.\"\n\n\"온종일이요?\"\n\n\"네. 오늘 아침엔 그와 함께 아침을 들었고, 종일 그의 마차를 탔으며, 이제는 그의 특별석까지 차지하고 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"그럼 그를 아세요?\"\n\n\"알기도 하고, 모르기도 합니다.\"\n\n\"그건 긴 이야기입니다.\"\n\n\"들려주세요.\"\n\n\"너무 놀라실 텐데요.\"\n\n\"그러니 더욱 듣고 싶군요.\"\n\n\"적어도 이야기가 결말이 날 때까지는 기다리시지요.\"\n\n\"좋아요. 저는 완결된 이야기를 더 좋아하니까요. 하지만 그와 어떻게 알게 되셨는지는 말씀해 주세요. 누가 그를 소개해 주던가요?\"\n\n\"아니요. 그가 먼저 우리에게 자신을 소개했습니다.\"\n\n\"언제요?\"\n\n\"어젯밤, 우리가 부인 곁을 떠난 뒤에요.\"\n\n\"무슨 인연으로요?\"\n\n\"우리 호텔 주인이라는 더없이 평범한 매개를 통해서지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 그가 당신들과 같은 런던 호텔에 묵고 있나요?\"\n\n\"같은 호텔일 뿐 아니라, 같은 층에 묵고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"이름이 뭐죠? 물론 아실 테죠?\"\n\n\"몬테크리스토 백작입니다.\"\n\n\"가문 이름은 아니겠지요?\"\n\n\"네. 그가 사들인 섬의 이름입니다.\"\n\n\"그래서 백작이라고요?\"\n\n\"토스카나의 백작이지요.\"\n\n\"음, 그건 그러려니 해야겠네요.\" 백작 부인이 말했다. 그녀 자신이 베네치아의 가장 오래된 가문 출신이었다. \"어떤 사람인가요?\"\n\n\"모르세르 자작에게 물어보시지요.\"\n\n\"들으셨죠, 모르세르 씨, 저를 당신에게 넘기시는군요.\" 백작 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그를 유쾌한 사람이라 여기지 않는다면 우리가 어지간히 까다로운 사람일 겁니다, 부인.\" 알베르가 대꾸했다. \"십 년 지기라도 그보다 더, 또 그보다 더 완벽한 예의로 우리에게 잘해 주지는 못했을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"이런.\" 백작 부인이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"제 흡혈귀가 알고 보니 그저 어느 백만장자였나 보군요. 로스차일드 씨와 혼동되지 않으려고 라라의 모습을 빌린 거겠지요. 그래, 그녀는 보셨나요?\"\n\n\"그녀라뇨?\"\n\n\"어제 그 아름다운 그리스 여인 말이에요.\"\n\n\"아니요. 그녀의 구즐라 소리는 들은 것 같은데, 정작 그녀는 끝내 모습을 보이지 않았습니다.\"\n\n\"보이지 않았다고 하는 건,\" 알베르가 끼어들었다. \"그저 신비를 지키려는 말일 뿐입니다. 흰 커튼이 드리운 그 창가의 푸른 도미노를 부인은 누구라고 여기시는지요?\"\n\n\"흰 휘장을 두른 그 창문이 어디 있었지요?\" 백작 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"로스폴리 궁전에요.\"\n\n\"백작이 로스폴리 궁전에 창문을 세 개나 가지고 있었어요?\"\n\n\"네. 코르소를 지나오셨습니까?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"그럼 노란 다마스크 천을 두른 창문 둘과, 붉은 십자가가 그려진 흰색 다마스크 천을 두른 창문 하나를 보셨겠군요? 그게 백작의 창문이었습니다.\"\n\n\"아니, 그 사람은 부호임에 틀림없군요. 그 창문 세 개가 얼마짜리인지 아세요?\"\n\n\"로마 크라운으로 이삼백 정도?\"\n\n\"이삼천이에요.\"\n\n\"맙소사!\"\n\n\"그의 섬이 그에게 그런 수입을 안겨 주나요?\"\n\n\"한 푼도 안 가져다줍니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 왜 그 섬을 샀대요?\"\n\n\"변덕으로요.\"\n\n\"그럼 별난 사람이군요?\"\n\n\"사실,\" 알베르가 한마디 했다. \"제가 보기에도 좀 별난 데가 있더군요. 만약 그가 파리에 살면서 극장을 드나드는 사람이라면, 말 그대로 정신 나간 가엾은 작자라고 했을 겁니다. 오늘 아침에도 디디에나 안토니에게나 어울릴 법한 퇴장을 두세 번이나 해 보였거든요.\"\n\n이때 새로운 손님이 들어왔고, 관례에 따라 프란츠는 그에게 자리를 내주었다. 게다가 이 일은 화제를 바꾸는 효과까지 가져왔다. 한 시간 뒤 두 친구는 호텔로 돌아왔다.\n\n파스트리니 씨는 이미 이튿날 쓸 변장 의상을 마련하느라 분주했다. 그는 두 사람이 완전히 만족할 거라고 장담했다. 이튿날 아침 아홉 시, 그는 로마 농부 의상을 여덟아홉 벌쯤 팔에 걸친 재단사를 데리고 프란츠의 방으로 들어왔다. 두 사람은 똑같은 것으로 두 벌을 골랐다. 그러고는 재단사에게 각자의 모자에 리본을 스무 야드쯤 달고, 서민들이 축제 때 몸을 치장하는 색색의 긴 비단 띠를 두 개 구해 오라고 일렀다.\n\n알베르는 새 옷을 입은 자기 모습이 어떨지 보고 싶어 안달이었다. 푸른 벨벳 재킷과 반바지, 무늬가 짜인 비단 양말, 버클 달린 구두, 그리고 비단 조끼. 이 그림 같은 차림새는 그를 한층 돋보이게 했다. 허리에 띠를 두르고, 모자를 멋스럽게 한쪽으로 비스듬히 얹어 어깨로 리본 자락을 흘러내리게 하자, 프란츠는 인정하지 않을 수 없었다. 우리가 어떤 민족에게 부여하는 외양의 우월함에 의상이 큰 몫을 한다는 사실을 말이다. 터키 사람들은 길고 펄럭이는 옷을 입었을 때 그토록 그림 같았는데, 지금은 턱까지 단추를 채운 푸른 윗도리에 붉은 모자를 쓰고 있으니, 붉은 봉인이 찍힌 포도주 병처럼 보여 흉하지 않은가? 프란츠가 알베르를 칭찬하자, 알베르는 만족스러운 미소를 숨김없이 띠며 거울에 비친 자기 모습을 들여다보았다. 그들이 그러고 있을 때 몬테크리스토 백작이 들어왔다.\n\n\"여러분.\" 그가 말했다. \"동행이 즐거운 일이긴 하나, 완전한 자유가 때로는 그보다 더 즐거운 법이지요. 오늘부터 사육제가 끝날 때까지 마차를 온전히 두 분께 맡기겠다는 말을 하러 왔습니다. 주인이 말해 주겠지만, 제게는 마차가 서너 대 더 있으니 조금도 제게 폐가 되지 않습니다. 부디 즐기시든 일을 보시든 마음껏 쓰십시오.\"\n\n두 젊은이는 사양하려 했지만, 그토록 흡족한 제안을 거절할 그럴듯한 이유를 찾지 못했다. 몬테크리스토 백작은 그들과 십오 분쯤 머물며 온갖 주제에 대해 더없이 편안하게 이야기를 나누었다. 앞서 말했듯 그는 모든 나라의 문학에 통달해 있었다. 그의 살롱 벽을 한번 둘러보는 것만으로도, 프란츠와 알베르는 그가 그림 감식가라는 것을 알 수 있었다. 그가 무심코 던지는 몇 마디에서, 그들은 그가 과학에도 문외한이 아니며, 특히 화학에 깊이 빠져 있는 듯하다는 것을 알아챘다. 두 친구는 백작이 베푼 아침 식사를 답례로 대접할 엄두를 내지 못했다. 그의 훌륭한 식탁에 견주어 파스트리니 씨의 형편없는 식탁을 내놓는 것은 너무도 우스운 일이었을 테니까. 그들은 그 사정을 솔직히 털어놓았고, 백작은 그들의 섬세함을 헤아리는 사람다운 태도로 그 변명을 받아들였다. 알베르는 백작의 태도에 매료되었다. 그가 백작을 완벽한 신사로 인정하는 데 걸림돌이 된 것이 있다면, 오직 그의 다채로운 지식뿐이었다.\n\n마차를 마음대로 써도 좋다는 허락이 무엇보다 그를 기쁘게 했다. 전날 저녁 그 어여쁜 농부 아가씨들이 더없이 우아한 마차를 타고 나타났던 터라, 알베르는 그들과 대등한 처지가 되는 것이 싫지 않았다. 한 시 반에 그들은 아래로 내려갔다. 마부와 하인은 변장 위에 정복을 걸치고 있어, 그 어느 때보다 우스꽝스러워 보였고, 프란츠와 알베르의 갈채를 받았다. 알베르는 시든 제비꽃 다발을 단춧구멍에 꽂았다. 종이 처음 울리자 그들은 비토리아 거리를 통해 코르소로 서둘러 나아갔다.\n\n두 번째 모퉁이에서, 아를레키노들로 가득 찬 마차에서 던져진 싱싱한 제비꽃 다발이 알베르에게 알려 주었다. 자신과 친구처럼, 그 농부 아가씨들도 의상을 바꿔 입었다는 것을. 우연의 결과였는지, 아니면 두 사람 다 같은 마음에 사로잡힌 탓이었는지, 그가 그들의 의상을 걸치는 동안 그들은 그의 의상을 걸친 것이었다.\n\n알베르는 싱싱한 꽃다발을 단춧구멍에 꽂았지만, 시든 것은 손에 쥐고 있었다. 그리고 그 사륜마차를 다시 만났을 때, 그것을 입술로 가져갔다. 그 행동은 꽃을 던진 어여쁜 여인뿐 아니라 그녀의 흥겨운 동행들까지 몹시 즐겁게 한 듯했다. 그날은 전날 못지않게 흥겨웠고, 어쩌면 더 활기차고 떠들썩했다. 백작이 잠깐 창가에 모습을 보였지만, 그들이 다시 지나갈 때는 사라지고 없었다. 알베르와 그 어여쁜 농부 아가씨 사이의 시시덕거림이 온종일 이어졌다는 것은 굳이 말할 필요도 없으리라.\n\n저녁에 돌아오니, 프란츠는 대사관에서 온 편지 한 통을 발견했다. 이튿날 교황 성하를 알현하는 영광을 누리게 되리라는 내용이었다. 그는 로마를 찾을 때마다 매번 같은 은혜를 청하고 또 누려 왔다. 종교적 감정에서나 감사하는 마음에서나, 그는 온갖 미덕의 보기 드문 본보기를 보여 준 성 베드로의 후계자 한 사람의 발치에 정중한 경의를 바치지 않고서는 그리스도교 세계의 수도를 떠나고 싶지 않았다. 그러자 사육제 생각은 사라졌다. 그레고리우스 16세라 불리는 그 존엄하고 고결한 노인은, 그 겸허함과 가슴 뭉클한 자애로움에도 불구하고, 누구든 그 앞에서 경외감 없이는 고개를 숙일 수 없는 분이었기 때문이다.\n\n바티칸에서 돌아오는 길에, 프란츠는 코르소를 일부러 피했다. 그는 경건한 상념의 보물을 가슴에 품고 나왔다. 가면 쓴 자들의 광적인 흥겨움은 그 상념을 모독하는 일이 되었을 테니까.\n\n다섯 시 십 분에 알베르가 몹시 기뻐하며 들어왔다. 그 아를레키노가 다시 농부 의상을 입었고, 곁을 지날 때 가면을 들어 올렸다는 것이다. 그녀는 매혹적이었다. 프란츠가 알베르를 축하했고, 알베르는 마땅히 받을 만한 축하라는 것을 아는 사람다운 태도로 그것을 받았다. 그는 몇 가지 틀림없는 징표로, 그 어여쁜 미지의 여인이 귀족 계급에 속한다는 것을 알아챘다. 그는 이튿날 그녀에게 편지를 쓰기로 마음먹고 있었다.\n\n이 자초지종을 늘어놓는 동안, 프란츠는 알베르가 자기에게 뭔가 청하고 싶어 하면서도 차마 청하지 못하는 듯하다는 것을 눈치챘다. 그는 어떤 희생이든 알베르가 바라는 대로 치를 용의가 있다고 미리 못 박으며 채근했다.\n\n알베르는 우정이 요구하는 만큼만 채근을 받다가, 이튿날 마차를 혼자 쓰게 해 준다면 큰 호의를 베푸는 셈이 되겠다고 프란츠에게 털어놓았다. 알베르는 그 어여쁜 농부 아가씨가 가면을 들어 올린 그 각별한 호의를, 프란츠가 자리에 없었던 덕으로 돌렸다. 프란츠는 친구의 호기심을 그토록 흡족하게 채워 주고 허영심을 그토록 우쭐하게 만들어 줄 모험을 한가운데서 가로막을 만큼 이기적인 사람이 아니었다. 그는 친구의 빈틈없는 입싼 성미가 일어난 일을 죄다 일러 주리라 확신했다. 게다가 그는 이탈리아 전역을 떠돈 삼 년 동안 그런 종류의 행운을 단 한 번도 누려 본 적이 없었으니, 그런 상황에서 어떻게 처신하는지 알게 되는 것이 결코 싫지 않았다. 그리하여 그는 이튿날 로스폴리 궁전의 창문에서 사육제를 구경하는 것으로 만족하겠다고 알베르에게 약속했다.\n\n이튿날 아침, 그는 알베르가 거대한 꽃다발을 들고 오가는 것을 보았다. 그것은 분명 그의 연애편지를 전할 매개로 삼으려는 것이었다. 이 짐작은 확신으로 바뀌었다. 프란츠가 그 꽃다발을, 흰 동백꽃 테두리로 눈에 띄던 그것을, 장밋빛 새틴을 차려입은 매혹적인 아를레키노의 손에서 보았을 때 말이다.\n\n저녁은 더 이상 흥겨움이 아니라 도취였다. 알베르는 그 어여쁜 미지의 여인이 같은 방식으로 답하리라는 것을 조금도 의심하지 않았다. 프란츠는 소란이 자기를 피곤하게 하니 이튿날은 글을 쓰고 일기를 들여다보며 보내겠다고 말하며 그의 바람을 앞질러 주었다. 알베르는 속지 않았다. 이튿날 저녁, 프란츠는 그가 접힌 종이 한 장을 한쪽 귀퉁이로 쥐고 흔들며 의기양양하게 들어오는 것을 보았으니까.\n\n\"그래.\" 그가 말했다. \"내 짐작이 틀렸던가?\"\n\n\"그녀가 답을 보냈군!\" 프란츠가 외쳤다.\n\n\"읽어 보게.\"\n이 말은 뭐라 형용할 수 없는 어조로 내뱉어졌다. 프란츠는 편지를 받아 들고 읽었다.\n\n\"화요일 저녁 일곱 시, 폰테피치 거리 맞은편에서 마차를 내려, 당신의 횃불을 낚아채는 로마 농부 여인을 따라오세요. 산 자코모 성당의 첫 계단에 이르거든, 알아볼 수 있도록 당신의 아를레키노 의상 어깨에 장밋빛 리본 매듭을 꼭 매어 두세요. 그때까지 당신은 저를 볼 수 없을 거예요. ─한결같음과 신중함.\"\n\n\"그래.\" 프란츠가 다 읽자 그가 물었다. \"어떻게 생각하나?\"\n\n\"이 모험이 아주 흡족한 모양새를 갖춰 가는 것 같군.\"\n\n\"나도 그렇게 생각해.\" 알베르가 대답했다. \"그리고 자네가 브라차노 공작의 무도회에 혼자 가게 될까 봐 무척 걱정스럽네.\"\n\n그날 아침 프란츠와 알베르는 그 유명한 로마 은행가에게서 초대를 받았다.\n\n\"조심하게, 알베르.\" 프란츠가 말했다. \"로마의 귀족이란 귀족은 다 참석할 텐데, 자네의 그 어여쁜 미지의 여인이 상류 사회에 속한다면 그녀도 거기 가야 할 테니까.\"\n\n\"그녀가 가든 안 가든, 내 생각은 변함없네.\" 알베르가 대꾸했다. \"그 편지를 읽었지?\"\n\n\"읽었네.\"\n\n\"이탈리아에서 메초 치토 여인들이 얼마나 형편없는 교육을 받는지 아나?\" (이것은 하층 계급을 일컫는 이름이다.)\n\n\"알지.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 편지를 다시 읽어 보게. 필체를 보고, 할 수 있다면 그 말투나 철자에서 흠을 하나라도 찾아보게.\" 필체는 정말로 매혹적이었고, 철자는 나무랄 데가 없었다.\n\n\"자네는 행운을 타고났군.\" 프란츠가 편지를 돌려주며 말했다.\n\n\"실컷 비웃게.\" 알베르가 대꾸했다. \"나는 사랑에 빠졌으니까.\"\n\n\"자네 때문에 겁이 나는군.\" 프란츠가 외쳤다. \"내가 브라차노 공작의 무도회에 혼자 갈 뿐 아니라, 피렌체로도 혼자 돌아가게 생겼어.\"\n\n\"내 미지의 여인이 아름다운 만큼 상냥하기까지 하다면,\" 알베르가 말했다. \"나는 적어도 여섯 주는 로마에 눌러앉을 거야. 나는 로마를 흠모하고, 늘 고고학에 큰 취미가 있었으니까.\"\n\n\"이런, 그런 모험을 두세 번만 더 하면, 자네가 학사원 회원이 되는 것도 보겠는걸.\"\n\n알베르가 학사원 의자에 앉을 자기 자격을 진지하게 논하려 들 참인데, 저녁 식사가 준비되었다는 전갈이 왔다. 알베르의 사랑도 그의 식욕을 앗아 가지는 못했다. 그는 프란츠와 함께 서둘러 자리에 앉았다. 식후에 논의를 다시 이어 가면 될 일이었다. 식사를 마치자 몬테크리스토 백작이 왔다는 전갈이 왔다. 그들은 이틀 동안 그를 보지 못한 터였다. 파스트리니 씨는 일 때문에 그가 치비타베키아에 다녀왔다고 알려 주었다. 그는 전날 저녁에 떠났다가 한 시간 전에야 막 돌아온 것이었다. 그는 매력적이었다. 스스로를 단속한 것인지, 아니면 우연히 다른 상황에서 건드렸던 그 신랄한 가락을 울리지 않은 것인지, 그날 밤 그는 여느 사람과 다를 바 없었다.\n\n이 사내는 프란츠에게 수수께끼였다. 백작은 프란츠가 자신을 알아보았다는 것을 분명 확신하고 있을 텐데, 두 사람 사이에 전부터 안면이 있었음을 내비치는 말은 단 한마디도 흘리지 않았다. 한편 프란츠로서는 지난번 만남을 넌지시 언급하고 싶은 마음이 아무리 컸어도, 자신과 친구에게 호의를 잔뜩 베푼 사람의 비위를 거스를까 두려워 그것을 입에 올리지 못했다.\n\n백작은 두 친구가 아르헨티나 극장의 특별석을 잡으려고 사람을 보냈으나 모두 동이 났다는 답을 들었다는 사실을 알게 되었다. 그래서 그는 자기 특별석 열쇠를 그들에게 가져왔다. 적어도 그것이 그가 찾아온 표면상의 이유였다. 프란츠와 알베르는 그에게서 특별석을 빼앗을까 염려된다며 다소 난색을 표했다. 하지만 백작은 자신은 팔리 극장에 갈 참이라, 두 사람이 쓰지 않으면 아르헨티나 극장의 특별석은 헛것이 될 거라고 대답했다. 이 말에 두 친구는 그것을 받아들이기로 마음을 정했다.\n\n프란츠는 백작의 창백함에 차츰 익숙해져 있었다. 처음 만났을 때 그토록 강렬하게 그를 사로잡았던 그 창백함 말이다. 그는 백작 이목구비의 준엄한 아름다움을 감탄하지 않을 수 없었는데, 그 유일한 결점, 아니 차라리 주된 특질이 바로 그 창백함이었다. 정말이지 바이런풍의 영웅이었다! 프란츠는 그를 본다고는 차마 못 하겠으나, 그를 떠올리는 것만으로도 그 준엄한 머리가 만프레드의 어깨 위에 얹히거나 라라의 투구 아래 놓인 모습을 상상하지 않을 수 없었다. 그의 이마에는 쓰디쓴 상념이 끊임없이 자리하고 있음을 알려 주는 선이 새겨져 있었다. 영혼의 밑바닥까지 꿰뚫는 듯한 불타는 눈, 그리고 거기서 나오는 말에 독특한 성격을 부여해 듣는 이의 마음에 그 말을 아로새기는, 오만하고 경멸 어린 윗입술을 그는 지니고 있었다.\n\n백작은 더 이상 젊지 않았다. 적어도 마흔은 되었다. 그런데도 그가 지금 함께 어울리는 젊은이들을 다스리도록 타고난 사람이라는 것은 쉽게 알 수 있었다. 그리고 그 영국 시인의 환상적인 영웅들과의 닮음을 완성하기라도 하듯, 백작은 사람을 매혹하는 힘을 지닌 듯했다. 알베르는 이런 사람을 만난 행운에 대해 끊임없이 떠들어 댔다. 프란츠는 그만큼 열광적이지는 않았다. 하지만 강한 정신이 덜 위압적인 정신에 대해 늘 얻게 마련인 그 우위를, 백작은 그에게도 행사하고 있었다. 그는 백작이 파리를 방문하려는 계획을 여러 차례 떠올렸다. 그리고 백작의 별난 성격과 독특한 얼굴, 막대한 재산이라면 거기서 큰 반향을 일으키리라는 것을 의심치 않았다. 그러면서도 그는 백작이 파리에 있을 때 자신은 파리에 있고 싶지 않았다.\n\n저녁은 이탈리아 극장에서 저녁이 으레 흘러가는 대로 흘러갔다. 다시 말해, 음악을 듣는 것이 아니라 인사를 다니고 담소를 나누는 것으로 말이다. G 백작 부인이 백작 이야기를 다시 꺼내고 싶어 했지만, 프란츠는 훨씬 더 새로운 이야깃거리가 있다고 알렸다. 그리고 알베르가 거짓 겸손을 부리는 것에도 아랑곳없이, 지난 사흘간 두 사람의 마음을 사로잡았던 그 큰 사건을 백작 부인에게 들려주었다. 여행자들의 말을 믿는다면 이런 밀회는 이탈리아에서 드문 일이 아니었으므로, 백작 부인은 조금도 미심쩍어하는 기색 없이 알베르의 성공을 축하해 주었다. 헤어지면서 그들은 온 로마가 초대받은 브라차노 공작의 무도회에서 만나기로 약속했다.\n\n꽃다발의 여주인공은 약속을 지켰다. 이튿날도, 그다음 날도 그녀는 알베르에게 존재의 기척조차 보이지 않았다.\n\n마침내 화요일이 왔다. 사육제의 마지막이자 가장 떠들썩한 날이었다. 화요일이면 극장은 아침 열 시에 문을 연다. 사순절이 밤 여덟 시 이후에 시작되기 때문이다. 화요일이면, 돈이 없어서든 시간이 없어서든 흥이 나지 않아서든 그전에 사육제를 보지 못한 사람들이 모두 그 흥겨움 속에 뒤섞여 소란과 열기에 한몫을 보탠다. 두 시부터 다섯 시까지 프란츠와 알베르는 축제를 따라다니며, 다른 마차들과 보행자들과 콘페티를 한 줌씩 주고받았다. 보행자들은 말발굽 사이와 마차 바퀴 틈으로 몰려들었지만, 사고도, 다툼도, 싸움도 단 한 건 없었다.\n\n축제는 이탈리아 사람들에게 진정한 즐거움의 날이다. 이탈리아에서 대여섯 해를 지낸 이 이야기의 글쓴이는, 다른 나라에서는 그토록 흔한 그런 사건으로 행사가 중단되는 것을 단 한 번도 본 기억이 없다. 알베르는 아를레키노 의상을 입고 의기양양했다. 장밋빛 리본 매듭이 그의 어깨에서 거의 땅에 닿을 듯이 늘어져 있었다. 혼동이 없도록 프란츠는 농부 의상을 입었다.\n\n날이 깊어 갈수록 소란은 더해 갔다. 포도에서도, 마차에서도, 창문에서도 침묵하는 혀가 하나도 없었고, 움직이지 않는 팔이 하나도 없었다. 그것은 함성의 천둥과, 사탕과 꽃과 달걀과 오렌지와 꽃다발의 우박으로 이루어진 인간의 폭풍우였다.\n\n세 시에, 포폴로 광장과 베네치아 광장에서 터진 불꽃놀이 소리가 (그 소란과 혼란 속에서는 알아듣기 어려웠지만) 경마가 곧 시작되리라는 것을 알렸다.\n\n경마는, 모콜리와 마찬가지로, 사육제 마지막 날들에 특유한 행사 중 하나다. 불꽃놀이 소리가 나자 마차들은 즉시 대열을 풀고 인근 골목으로 물러났다. 이 모든 동작은 경찰이 끼어들 것도 없이, 상상할 수 없을 만큼 능란하고 놀랍도록 신속하게 이루어진다. 보행자들은 담벼락에 바싹 붙어 늘어섰다. 그러자 말발굽 소리와 쇠붙이 부딪치는 소리가 들려왔다. 카르비니에리 한 무리가 열다섯씩 나란히, 바르베리가 달릴 길을 터 주려고 코르소를 질주해 올라갔다. 그 무리가 베네치아 광장에 이르자, 길이 비었음을 알리는 두 번째 불꽃놀이가 터졌다.\n\n거의 곧바로, 엄청난 함성이 사방에서 터지는 가운데, 삼십만 구경꾼의 고함에 흥분한 말 일고여덟 마리가 번개처럼 지나갔다. 그러자 산탄젤로 성에서 대포 세 발을 쏘아, 삼번 말이 이겼음을 알렸다.\n\n곧바로, 다른 신호도 없이 마차들이 다시 움직이며 코르소를 향해, 모든 골목을 따라 흘러나왔다. 한동안 갇혀 있다가 다시 본류로 흘러드는 급류처럼 말이다. 그리고 그 거대한 물줄기는 두 화강암 둑 사이로 다시 제 길을 이어 갔다.\n\n새로운 소음과 움직임의 원천이 군중에 더해졌다. 모콜레티 장수들이 무대에 등장한 것이다. 모콜리, 또는 모콜레티는 부활절 초부터 골풀 심지 양초까지 크기가 제각각인 양초로, 사육제 대단원의 위대한 마지막 장면에 나서는 모든 이에게 두 가지 심각한 과제를 던진다. 첫째는 자기 모콜레토의 불을 어떻게 꺼지지 않게 지키느냐, 둘째는 남들의 모콜레토를 어떻게 끄느냐다. 모콜레토는 생명과 같다. 인간은 그것을 전하는 방법을 단 하나만 찾아냈고, 그 하나는 신에게서 나온다. 하지만 그것을 앗아 가는 방법은 천 가지나 발견해 냈으며, 거기엔 악마도 얼마간 거들었다. 모콜레토는 불꽃에 가까이 대어 켠다. 하지만 모콜레토를 끄는 천 가지 방법을 누가 다 묘사할 수 있으랴? 거대한 풀무, 괴물 같은 소화기, 초인적인 부채들. 모두가 다투어 모콜레티를 사들였다. 프란츠와 알베르도 그중에 끼었다.\n\n밤이 빠르게 다가오고 있었다. 천 명의 장수들이 째지는 목소리로 거듭 외쳐 대는 \"모콜레티!\" 소리에 맞춰, 어느새 군중 사이로 두세 개의 별이 타오르기 시작했다. 그것은 신호였다. 십 분이 지나자 오만 개의 불빛이 반짝이며, 베네치아 궁전에서 포폴로 광장으로 내려가고, 포폴로 광장에서 베네치아 궁전으로 올라갔다. 마치 도깨비불의 축제 같았다.\n\n직접 보지 않고서는 그것을 머릿속에 그려 볼 수 없다. 모든 별이 하늘에서 내려와 지상에서 광란의 춤을 추며 뒤섞이는 광경을 상상해 보라. 세상 그 어느 곳에서도 들어 본 적 없는 함성이 그 모든 것에 곁들여진다. 짐꾼이 귀족을 쫓고, 트라스테베레 사람이 시민을 쫓으며, 저마다 불을 불어 끄고 다시 붙인다. 만약 이 순간 늙은 아이올로스가 나타났더라면, 그는 모콜리의 왕으로 추대되었을 테고, 아퀼로는 왕위 계승자가 되었으리라.\n\n이 광기와 불꽃의 싸움은 두 시간이나 이어졌다. 코르소는 대낮처럼 환했다. 삼층, 사층에 있는 구경꾼들의 이목구비까지 보일 정도였다.\n\n알베르는 오 분마다 시계를 꺼내 보았다. 마침내 시곗바늘이 일곱 시를 가리켰다. 두 친구는 폰테피치 거리에 있었다. 알베르가 모콜레토를 손에 쥐고 마차에서 뛰어내렸다. 가면 쓴 자 두셋이 그의 모콜레토를 손에서 쳐 내려고 덤볐지만, 일류 권투 선수인 알베르는 그들을 하나씩 차례로 길바닥에 나뒹굴게 하고는 산 자코모 성당을 향해 가던 길을 계속 갔다.\n\n계단은 서로의 횃불을 낚아채려는 가면 쓴 자들로 빼곡했다. 프란츠는 알베르를 눈으로 좇으며, 그가 첫 계단을 오르는 것을 보았다.\n\n곧바로, 농부 여인의 그 낯익은 의상을 입은 가면 하나가 알베르의 모콜레토를 낚아챘다. 알베르는 아무런 저항도 하지 않았다. 프란츠는 너무 멀리 있어 그들이 무슨 말을 하는지 들을 수 없었다. 하지만 적의가 오간 일은 분명 없었으니, 알베르가 그 농부 아가씨와 팔짱을 끼고 사라지는 것을 보았기 때문이다. 그는 두 사람이 군중 사이를 헤치고 지나가는 것을 한동안 지켜보았지만, 결국 마첼로 거리에서 그들을 시야에서 놓치고 말았다.\n\n갑자기 사육제의 끝을 알리는 종이 울렸고, 그와 동시에 마치 마법에라도 걸린 듯 모든 모콜레티가 꺼졌다. 거대한 한 줄기 바람이 불어 와 모든 불을 꺼 버린 것만 같았다.\n\n프란츠는 칠흑 같은 어둠 속에 홀로 남았다. 가면 쓴 자들을 집으로 실어 나르는 마차 소리 말고는 아무 소리도 들리지 않았다. 창문 너머로 타오르는 몇 점의 불빛 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않았다.\n\n사육제는 끝났다.\n\n# Chapter 37. The Catacombs of Saint Sebastian\n\n그날 그 순간만큼, 어쩌면 프란츠는 평생 그토록 갑작스러운 인상을, 들뜬 기분에서 슬픔으로의 그토록 빠른 전환을 느껴 본 적이 없었다. 마치 로마가 어떤 밤의 악마가 마법의 입김을 불어넣기라도 한 듯, 별안간 거대한 무덤으로 변해 버린 것 같았다. 어둠을 한층 더 짙게 만든 우연이 겹쳤다. 이지러져 가던 달은 열한 시가 되어서야 떠올랐고, 그래서 젊은이가 지나는 거리는 칠흑 같은 어둠 속에 잠겨 있었다.\n\n거리는 짧았다. 십 분쯤 지나자 그의 마차, 아니 정확히는 백작의 마차가 런던 호텔 앞에 멈춰 섰다.\n\n저녁상이 차려져 있었지만, 알베르가 그렇게 일찍 돌아오지 않을 거라고 말해 두었던 터라 프란츠는 혼자 식탁에 앉았다. 두 사람이 늘 함께 식사하는 데 익숙했던 파스트리니가 알베르가 없는 까닭을 물었으나, 프란츠는 그저 알베르가 전날 저녁 받은 초대에 응했다고만 대답했다.\n\n모콜레티가 별안간 꺼지고, 빛을 대신해 어둠이 깔리고, 소란을 이어 정적이 들어선 일은 프란츠의 마음에 어떤 침울함을 남겼는데, 그 침울함에는 불안이 섞여 있었다. 그래서 그는 무엇이 필요하지 않으냐고 두세 번이나 와서 묻는 주인의 살뜰한 시중에도 불구하고, 아주 말없이 식사를 마쳤다.\n* 모콜레티: 로마 사육제 마지막 날 밤, 사람들이 들고 다니던 작은 양초.—옮긴이\n\n프란츠는 알베르를 되도록 늦게까지 기다려 보기로 했다. 그래서 마차를 열한 시에 대기시키도록 일러 두고, 알베르가 호텔에 돌아오는 즉시 알려 달라고 파스트리니에게 부탁했다.\n\n열한 시가 되어도 알베르는 돌아오지 않았다. 프란츠는 옷을 갖춰 입고 나서며, 브라차노 공작 댁에서 밤을 보내겠다고 주인에게 일러 두었다. 브라차노 공작의 저택은 로마에서 가장 멋진 집 가운데 하나였고, 콜론나 가문의 마지막 상속녀 가운데 한 사람인 공작 부인이 더없이 세련된 품위로 손님을 맞았으니, 그 댁의 연회는 유럽 전역에 이름이 높았다.\n\n프란츠와 알베르는 그 댁에 보낼 소개장을 들고 로마에 왔던 터라, 프란츠가 도착하자 사람들이 가장 먼저 물은 것은 그의 여행 동무가 어디에 있느냐는 것이었다. 프란츠는 모콜리를 끄려던 순간에 그와 헤어졌고, 마첼로 거리에서 그를 놓쳐 버렸다고 대답했다.\n\n\"그래, 아직 돌아오지 않았단 말이오?\" 공작이 물었다.\n\n\"이 시각까지 그를 기다렸습니다.\" 프란츠가 대답했다.\n\n\"그가 어디로 갔는지는 아시오?\"\n\n\"아니요, 정확히는 모릅니다. 다만 밀회 같은 것이 아니었나 싶습니다.\"\n\n\"이런!\" 공작이 말했다. \"오늘은, 아니 오늘 밤은 늦게까지 밖에 나돌기에 나쁜 날이지요. 안 그렇소, 백작 부인?\"\n\n이 말은 막 도착해 공작의 동생인 토를로니아의 팔에 기대고 있던 G 백작 부인을 향한 것이었다.\n\n\"제 생각엔 오히려 더없이 아름다운 밤이에요.\" 백작 부인이 대답했다. \"여기 모인 분들이 불평할 거라곤 단 한 가지, 이 밤이 너무 빨리 지나간다는 것뿐일 거예요.\"\n\n\"제 말은,\" 공작이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"여기 계신 분들 이야기가 아니랍니다. 남자들이야 부인께 반하는 위험밖에 없고, 여자들이야 그토록 어여쁜 부인을 보며 질투로 병이 날 위험밖에 없지요. 제가 말한 건 로마 거리에 나가 있는 사람들 얘깁니다.\"\n\n\"아,\" 백작 부인이 물었다. \"이 시각에 무도회에 가는 게 아니라면, 대체 누가 로마 거리에 나가 있겠어요?\"\n\n\"우리 친구 알베르 드 모르세르입니다, 부인.\" 프란츠가 말했다. \"오늘 저녁 일곱 시쯤 정체 모를 여인을 쫓아가는 걸 보고는, 그 뒤로 보지 못했지요.\"\n\n\"그가 어디 있는지 모르세요?\"\n\n\"전혀 모릅니다.\"\n\n\"무기는 지녔나요?\"\n\n\"가장무도회 차림입니다.\"\n\n\"그를 보내지 말았어야 했소.\" 공작이 프란츠에게 말했다. \"그대는 그 친구보다 로마를 더 잘 알지 않소.\"\n\n\"오늘 경주에서 우승한 바르베리 삼 번 말을 멈춰 세우려는 거나 매한가지였을 겁니다.\" 프란츠가 대답했다. \"게다가 그에게 무슨 일이 일어나기야 하겠습니까?\"\n\n\"누가 알겠소? 밤은 음침하고, 티베르강은 마첼로 거리에서 아주 가깝지.\" 공작과 백작 부인의 심정이 자신의 불안과 그토록 한 가닥으로 맞물리는 것을 보며, 프란츠는 핏줄을 타고 전율이 스치는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"호텔에는 제가 오늘 밤 여기서 묵는 영광을 누린다고 일러 두었습니다, 공작님.\" 프란츠가 말했다. \"그가 돌아오면 이리로 와서 알려 달라고도 했고요.\"\n\n\"아,\" 공작이 대답했다. \"마침 저기, 그대를 찾는 제 하인 하나가 오는 것 같소.\"\n\n공작의 짐작은 틀리지 않았다. 하인은 프란츠를 보자 곧장 그에게 다가왔다.\n\n\"각하,\" 그가 말했다. \"런던 호텔 주인이 사람을 보내, 모르세르 자작님의 편지를 든 사람이 각하를 기다리고 있다고 알려 왔습니다.\"\n\n\"자작의 편지라니!\" 프란츠가 외쳤다.\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"그 사람은 누구냐?\"\n\n\"모르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"왜 그 편지를 여기로 가져오지 않았느냐?\"\n\n\"심부름꾼은 그 까닭을 말하지 않았습니다.\"\n\n\"그 심부름꾼은 어디 있느냐?\"\n\n\"제가 각하를 찾으러 무도회장에 들어서는 걸 보더니 곧장 가 버렸습니다.\"\n\n\"오,\" 백작 부인이 프란츠에게 말했다. \"어서 서둘러 가 보세요. 가엾은 젊은이! 무슨 사고라도 났는지 몰라요.\"\n\n\"서두르겠습니다.\" 프란츠가 대답했다.\n\n\"무슨 일인지 알려 주러 다시 와 주시겠어요?\" 백작 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"네, 큰일이 아니라면요. 큰일이라면, 제가 어떻게 될지 장담할 수 없습니다.\"\n\n\"어쨌든 조심하세요.\" 백작 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"오! 그 점은 안심하십시오.\"\n\n프란츠는 모자를 집어 들고 부랴부랴 떠났다. 마차는 두 시에 데리러 오라는 분부와 함께 돌려보낸 터였다. 다행히 한쪽은 코르소 거리에, 다른 한쪽은 거룩한 사도 광장에 면한 브라차노 궁은 런던 호텔에서 걸어서 십 분도 채 걸리지 않았다.\n\n호텔 가까이 이르렀을 때, 프란츠는 길 한복판에 서 있는 한 사내를 보았다. 알베르가 보낸 심부름꾼이 틀림없었다. 사내는 커다란 망토로 몸을 휘감고 있었다. 프란츠가 다가가자, 놀랍게도 낯선 사내가 먼저 말을 걸어왔다.\n\n\"각하께서는 제게 무슨 볼일이 있으십니까?\" 사내가 경계하듯 한두 걸음 물러서며 물었다.\n\n\"모르세르 자작에게서,\" 프란츠가 물었다. \"내게 편지를 가져온 사람이 그대 아니오?\"\n\n\"각하께서는 파스트리니 호텔에 묵고 계십니까?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"각하께서는 자작님의 여행 동무이십니까?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"각하의 성함은……\"\n\n\"프란츠 데피네 남작이오.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 이 편지는 각하께 보내는 것이군요.\"\n\n\"답이 필요하오?\" 프란츠가 편지를 받아 들며 물었다.\n\n\"네. 적어도 각하의 친구분은 그러길 바라십니다.\"\n\n\"나와 함께 위층으로 올라갑시다. 거기서 답을 주겠소.\"\n\n\"저는 여기서 기다리는 편이 좋습니다.\" 심부름꾼이 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"어째서요?\"\n\n\"편지를 읽으시면 각하께서도 아시게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 그대를 여기서 찾을 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"물론입니다.\"\n\n프란츠는 호텔로 들어섰다. 계단에서 파스트리니와 마주쳤다.\n\"어떻습니까?\" 주인이 물었다.\n\n\"어떻다니, 무슨 말이오?\" 프란츠가 되물었다.\n\n\"친구분이 보내서 각하를 뵙겠다던 그 사람을 만나셨습니까?\" 그가 프란츠에게 물었다.\n\n\"그렇소, 만났소.\" 그가 대답했다. \"그리고 이 편지를 내게 건넸지. 부탁이니 내 방에 촛불을 좀 켜 주시오.\"\n\n여관 주인은 하인에게 불을 들고 프란츠 앞에 서라고 일렀다. 젊은이는 파스트리니가 몹시 겁먹은 얼굴을 하고 있는 것을 보았고, 그 때문에 알베르의 편지를 읽고 싶은 마음이 더욱 다급해졌다. 그래서 그는 곧장 촛불 쪽으로 다가가 편지를 펼쳤다. 알베르가 쓰고 서명한 것이었다. 프란츠는 그 내용을 알아차리기까지 두 번을 읽어야 했다. 편지에는 이렇게 적혀 있었다.\n\n\"친애하는 벗에게,\n\n이 편지를 받는 즉시, 부디 내 수첩에서 신용장을 꺼내 주게. 그건 책상의 네모난 서랍 안에 있을 거야. 그것으로 부족하면 자네 것도 보태 주고. 토를로니아에게 달려가서 당장 사천 피아스트르를 받아, 이 편지를 가져간 사람에게 건네주게. 지체 없이 이 돈을 마련해야 하네. 더는 말하지 않겠네. 내가 자네를 믿듯, 자네도 나를 믿어 주기를 바라며.\n\n자네의 벗,\n\n알베르 드 모르세르.\n\n추신—이제 나는 이탈리아 산적의 존재를 믿네.\"\n\n이 글 아래로, 낯선 필체로 이탈리아어가 다음과 같이 적혀 있었다.\n\n\"세 알레 세이 델라 마티나 레 콰트로 밀레 피아스트레 논 소노 넬레 미에 마니, 알라 세테 일 콘테 알베르토 아브라 체사토 디 비베레.\n\n루이지 밤파.\"\n\n\"내일 아침 여섯 시까지 사천 피아스트르가 내 손에 들어오지 않으면, 일곱 시에 알베르 백작은 목숨을 잃게 될 것이다.\"\n\n이 두 번째 서명은 프란츠에게 모든 것을 설명해 주었다. 그는 이제 심부름꾼이 방으로 올라오기를 거부한 까닭을 알아차렸다. 그에게는 거리가 더 안전했던 것이다. 그러니까 알베르는 그 이름난 산적 두목의 손아귀에 떨어진 것이었다. 그 두목의 존재를 그토록 오래 믿으려 들지 않았던 알베르가 말이다.\n\n지체할 시간이 없었다. 그는 서둘러 책상을 열고 서랍 안에서 수첩을 찾았으며, 그 속에서 신용장을 발견했다. 모두 합쳐 육천 피아스트르였으나, 그 육천 가운데 알베르가 이미 삼천을 써 버린 터였다.\n\n프란츠로 말하자면, 그는 신용장이 없었다. 피렌체에 살고 있었고 로마에는 이레나 여드레쯤 머물 작정으로 왔기에, 가져온 것이라곤 백 루이뿐이었다. 그 가운데 남은 것은 오십 루이도 채 되지 않았다. 그러니 알베르가 요구한 금액을 채우려면 두 사람 몫을 합쳐도 칠팔백 피아스트르가 모자랐다. 물론 그런 경우라면 토를로니아의 호의에 기댈 수도 있었다. 그래서 그는 한시도 지체하지 않고 브라차노 궁으로 돌아가려던 참이었는데, 문득 머릿속에 한 줄기 빛 같은 생각이 스쳤다.\n\n그는 몬테크리스토 백작을 떠올렸다. 프란츠가 파스트리니를 부르려고 종을 울리려는 찰나, 마침 그 친절한 사내가 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n\"이보시오,\" 그가 다급히 말했다. \"백작이 안에 계신지 아시오?\"\n\n\"네, 각하. 방금 막 돌아오셨습니다.\"\n\n\"잠자리에 드셨소?\"\n\n\"아닐 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 부탁이니 그분 방에 가서 종을 울리고, 부디 나를 좀 만나 주십사 청해 주시오.\"\n\n파스트리니는 시키는 대로 했고, 오 분 뒤에 돌아와 말했다.\n\n\"백작께서 각하를 기다리고 계십니다.\"\n\n프란츠는 복도를 따라갔고, 하인이 그를 백작에게 안내했다. 백작은 프란츠가 아직 본 적 없는 작은 방에 있었는데, 그 방은 빙 둘러 긴 의자가 놓여 있었다. 백작이 그를 향해 다가왔다.\n\n\"자, 이 시각에 무슨 좋은 바람이 불어 이리로 오셨소?\" 그가 말했다. \"나와 함께 야식을 드시러 오셨소? 그렇다면 참으로 반가운 일이오.\"\n\n\"아닙니다. 아주 중대한 일로 말씀드리러 왔습니다.\"\n\n\"중대한 일이라.\" 백작이 늘 그렇듯 진지한 눈빛으로 프란츠를 바라보며 말했다. \"그게 무엇이오?\"\n\n\"우리 둘뿐입니까?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\" 백작이 문가로 가서 확인하고 돌아오며 대답했다. 프란츠는 그에게 알베르의 편지를 건넸다.\n\n\"이걸 읽어 보십시오.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n백작이 편지를 읽었다.\n\n\"이런, 이런!\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"추신을 보셨습니까?\"\n\n\"보았소, 분명히.\n\n'세 알레 세이 델라 마티나 레 콰트로 밀레 피아스트레 논 소노 넬레 미에 마니, 알라 세테 일 콘테 알베르토 아브라 체사토 디 비베레.\n\n루이지 밤파.'\"\n\n\"어떻게 생각하십니까?\" 프란츠가 물었다.\n\n\"그가 요구한 돈은 지니고 계시오?\"\n\n\"네, 팔백 피아스트르만 빼고 다요.\"\n\n백작은 책상으로 가서 그것을 열고, 금화가 가득한 서랍을 꺼내며 프란츠에게 말했다. \"나 말고 다른 사람에게 손을 벌려 나를 서운하게 하지는 않으시겠지요.\"\n\n\"보시다시피, 오히려 저는 가장 먼저, 곧장 백작께 온 겁니다.\" 프란츠가 대답했다.\n\n\"고맙소. 원하는 만큼 가져가시오.\" 그러고는 마음껏 집으라는 손짓을 했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 정말로 그 돈을 루이지 밤파에게 보내야 하는 걸까요?\" 젊은이가 이번에는 자기 쪽에서 백작을 뚫어지게 바라보며 물었다.\n\n\"직접 판단해 보시오.\" 그가 대답했다. \"추신은 명백하지 않소.\"\n\n\"백작께서 조금만 곰곰이 생각해 주신다면, 이 협상을 더 간단히 풀 길을 찾으실 수 있을 것 같습니다.\" 프란츠가 말했다.\n\n\"어떻게 말이오?\" 백작이 놀라며 되물었다.\n\n\"우리가 함께 루이지 밤파를 찾아간다면, 그가 백작께 알베르를 풀어 주기를 거절하지는 못할 거라고 확신합니다.\"\n\n\"내가 산적에게 무슨 영향력을 가질 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"방금 그에게 결코 잊지 못할 은혜를 베풀지 않으셨습니까?\"\n\n\"그게 무엇이오?\"\n\n\"페피노의 목숨을 구해 주시지 않았습니까?\"\n\n\"이런, 이런.\" 백작이 말했다. \"그건 누구한테 들으셨소?\"\n\n\"상관없습니다. 저는 알고 있으니까요.\" 백작은 이마를 찌푸리며 잠시 말이 없었다.\n\n\"내가 밤파를 찾아간다면, 그대도 함께 가시겠소?\"\n\n\"제가 폐가 되지 않는다면요.\"\n\n\"그럼 그렇게 합시다. 아름다운 밤이오. 로마 바깥을 걷는 것은 우리 둘 모두에게 좋을 거요.\"\n\n\"무기를 챙겨야 할까요?\"\n\n\"무엇 하러요?\"\n\n\"돈은요?\"\n\n\"필요 없소. 편지를 가져온 사람은 어디 있소?\"\n\n\"거리에요.\"\n\n\"답을 기다리고 있소?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"우리가 어디로 가야 할지 알아야겠소. 그를 이리로 부르겠소.\"\n\n\"소용없습니다. 올라오려 하지 않을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그대 방에는 그러겠지요. 하지만 내 방에 드는 것은 마다하지 않을 거요.\"\n\n백작은 거리로 면한 방의 창가로 가서 독특한 방식으로 휘파람을 불었다. 망토를 두른 사내가 벽에서 떨어져 길 한복판으로 나섰다. \"살리테!\" 백작이 마치 하인에게 명령을 내리듯 그런 어조로 말했다. 심부름꾼은 조금도 망설이지 않고, 오히려 잽싸게 따랐다. 단숨에 계단을 뛰어올라 호텔로 들어섰고, 오 초 만에 방문 앞에 이르렀다.\n\n\"아, 자네였군, 페피노.\" 백작이 말했다. 그러나 페피노는 대답 대신 무릎을 꿇고 백작의 손을 붙잡아 입맞춤을 퍼부었다. \"아,\" 백작이 말했다. \"그러니까 자네는 내가 목숨을 구해 준 것을 잊지 않았군. 일주일이나 지난 일인데, 거참 신기하구먼.\"\n\n\"네, 각하. 결코 잊지 않겠습니다.\" 페피노가 깊은 감사의 어조로 대답했다.\n\n\"결코라니? 그건 긴 시간이지. 하지만 자네가 그렇게 믿는다는 것만으로도 대단한 일일세. 일어나서 대답해 보게.\"\n\n페피노가 불안한 눈길로 프란츠를 흘끗 보았다.\n\n\"오, 이분 앞에서 말해도 되네.\" 백작이 말했다. \"이분은 내 벗 가운데 한 사람이니까. 이런 칭호를 드려도 괜찮겠지요?\" 백작은 프랑스어로 말을 이었다. \"이 사람의 신뢰를 끌어내려면 그래야 합니다.\"\n\n\"내 앞에서 말해도 좋소.\" 프란츠가 말했다. \"나는 백작의 친구요.\"\n\n\"좋습니다!\" 페피노가 대답했다. \"각하께서 무엇을 물으시든 답할 준비가 되어 있습니다.\"\n\n\"알베르 자작이 어쩌다 루이지의 손에 떨어졌나?\"\n\n\"각하, 그 프랑스인의 마차가 테레사가 탄 마차를 여러 번 스쳐 지나갔습니다.\"\n\n\"두목의 정부 말인가?\"\n\n\"네. 그 프랑스인이 테레사에게 꽃다발을 던졌고, 테레사가 그걸 되던졌지요. 이 모든 게 마차에 타고 있던 두목의 허락 아래 이루어진 일입니다.\"\n\n\"뭐라고?\" 프란츠가 외쳤다. \"루이지 밤파가 로마 농부 차림을 한 사람들 틈에 마차에 타고 있었단 말인가?\"\n\n\"마부로 변장해 마차를 몬 게 바로 그였습니다.\" 페피노가 대답했다.\n\n\"그래서?\" 백작이 말했다.\n\n\"그러니까, 그 프랑스인이 가면을 벗었고, 테레사도 두목의 허락 아래 똑같이 가면을 벗었습니다. 프랑스인이 밀회를 청했고, 테레사가 약속을 잡아 주었지요. 다만, 산자코모 성당 계단에 있던 건 테레사가 아니라 베포였습니다.\"\n\n\"뭐라고!\" 프란츠가 외쳤다. \"그 사람한테서 모콜레토를 낚아챈 농촌 처녀가……\"\n\n\"열다섯 살 먹은 사내아이였습니다.\" 페피노가 대답했다. \"하지만 속아 넘어갔다 해서 자작님께 부끄러울 일은 없습니다. 베포한테 넘어간 사람이 어디 한둘이어야 말이지요.\"\n\n\"그래서 베포가 그를 성벽 밖으로 데려갔나?\" 백작이 말했다.\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다. 마첼로 거리 끝에 마차 한 대가 기다리고 있었지요. 베포가 올라타며 프랑스인에게 따라오라고 했고, 그는 두 번 청할 것도 없이 따랐습니다. 그는 정중하게 오른쪽 자리를 베포에게 내주고 그 옆에 앉았지요. 베포가 로마에서 십 리쯤 떨어진 별장으로 데려가겠다고 하자, 프랑스인은 세상 끝까지라도 따라가겠다고 했습니다. 마부는 리페타 거리를 거쳐 산파올로 성문으로 올라갔습니다. 그리고 성문 밖으로 이백 미터쯤 나왔을 때, 프랑스인이 좀 지나치게 들이대자 베포가 그의 머리에 권총 두 자루를 들이댔고, 마부도 마차를 세우고 똑같이 했습니다. 그와 동시에 알모 강둑에 숨어 있던 패거리 네 명이 마차를 에워쌌지요. 프랑스인은 얼마간 저항하며 베포의 목을 거의 졸라 죽일 뻔했지만, 무장한 다섯 명을 당해 낼 수는 없어 굴복할 수밖에 없었습니다. 그들은 그를 마차에서 끌어내 강둑을 따라 걷게 한 뒤, 성세바스티아노 지하 묘지에서 기다리던 테레사와 루이지에게 데려갔습니다.\"\n\n\"자,\" 백작이 프란츠를 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"내 보기엔 아주 그럴듯한 이야기 같소. 그대는 어떻게 생각하시오?\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 가엾은 알베르가 아닌 다른 사람에게 일어난 일이었다면 무척 재미있었을 거라고 생각합니다.\" 프란츠가 대답했다.\n\n\"사실, 그대가 여기서 나를 만나지 못했더라면,\" 백작이 말했다. \"그건 그대 친구에게 비싼 대가를 치르게 할 호기로운 모험이 되었을지도 모르오. 하지만 이제 안심하시오. 그가 겪을 유일한 심각한 후유증이라곤 놀란 가슴뿐일 테니.\"\n\n\"그럼 그를 찾으러 가는 겁니까?\" 프란츠가 물었다.\n\n\"오, 물론이오. 그는 아주 그림 같은 곳에 있소. 그대는 성세바스티아노 지하 묘지를 아시오?\"\n\n\"들어가 본 적은 없습니다만, 가 보리라 마음먹은 적은 여러 번 있었지요.\"\n\n\"자, 그렇다면 손쉽게 굴러든 기회로구려. 이보다 더 좋은 기회를 꾸미기도 어려울 거요. 마차는 있소?\"\n\"없습니다.\"\n\n\"그건 상관없소. 나는 늘 한 대를 준비해 두니까, 밤이든 낮이든.\"\n\n\"늘 준비해 두신다고요?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 나는 변덕이 무척 심한 사람이라, 이런 말을 해 두어야겠소만, 이따금 자리에서 일어났을 때나 식사를 마친 뒤나 한밤중에, 문득 어딘가로 떠나야겠다고 마음먹으면 그대로 떠나 버린다오.\"\n\n백작이 종을 울리자 하인이 나타났다.\n\n\"마차를 대령하라.\" 그가 말했다. \"그리고 권총집에 든 권총은 빼 놓아라. 마부는 깨울 것 없다. 알리가 몰 테니.\"\n\n이내 바퀴 소리가 들리더니, 마차가 문 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 백작이 회중시계를 꺼냈다.\n\n\"열두 시 반이군.\" 그가 말했다. \"다섯 시에 출발해도 시간은 맞겠지만, 그렇게 미루면 그대 친구가 불안한 밤을 보내게 될 거요. 그러니 서둘러 가서 그를 이교도의 손아귀에서 빼내는 편이 낫겠소. 아직도 나와 함께 갈 작정이오?\"\n\n\"그 어느 때보다 굳게요.\"\n\n\"그럼 갑시다.\"\n\n프란츠와 백작은 페피노와 함께 아래층으로 내려갔다. 문 앞에 마차가 있었다. 알리가 마부석에 앉아 있었는데, 프란츠는 그가 몬테크리스토섬 동굴에 있던 그 벙어리 노예임을 알아보았다. 프란츠와 백작이 마차에 올랐다. 페피노는 알리 옆에 자리를 잡았고, 마차는 빠른 속도로 출발했다. 미리 지시를 받은 알리는 코르소 거리를 따라 내려가 캄포 바치노를 가로지른 뒤, 산그레고리오 거리를 올라가 성세바스티아노 성문에 이르렀다. 문지기가 이런저런 트집을 잡았으나, 몬테크리스토 백작이 로마 총독이 발급한 통행증을 내보였다. 밤낮 어느 시각에든 도성을 드나들 수 있게 허락한 증서였다. 그러자 쇠살문이 올라갔고, 문지기는 수고비로 일 루이를 받았으며, 그들은 가던 길을 계속 갔다.\n\n이제 마차가 지나는 길은 옛 아피아 가도였는데, 양옆으로 무덤이 늘어서 있었다. 떠오르기 시작한 달빛 아래, 프란츠는 폐허 곳곳에서 보초 같은 무엇이 나타났다가 페피노의 신호에 따라 별안간 어둠 속으로 물러서는 것을 본 듯한 느낌이 이따금 들었다.\n\n카라칼라 욕장에 다다르기 조금 전에 마차가 멈췄다. 페피노가 문을 열었고, 백작과 프란츠가 내렸다.\n\n\"십 분 뒤면,\" 백작이 동행에게 말했다. \"우리는 그곳에 도착할 거요.\"\n\n그러고는 페피노를 한쪽으로 데려가 낮은 목소리로 무언가를 지시했고, 페피노는 마차에서 가져온 횃불을 들고 떠났다. 오 분이 흐르는 동안, 프란츠는 그 양치기가 캄파냐의 울퉁불퉁하고 부서진 지면 위로 난 좁은 길을 따라가는 것을 지켜보았다. 마침내 그는 키 큰 붉은 풀숲 한가운데로 사라졌는데, 그 풀숲은 거대한 사자의 곤두선 갈기처럼 보였다.\n\n\"자,\" 백작이 말했다. \"그를 따라갑시다.\"\n\n이번에는 프란츠와 백작이 같은 길을 따라 나아갔다. 백 걸음쯤 가니 길은 비탈을 지나 작은 골짜기 바닥으로 이어졌다. 그곳에서 두 사람은 어둠 속에서 이야기를 나누는 두 사내를 보았다.\n\n\"계속 가야 할까요?\" 프란츠가 백작에게 물었다. \"아니면 멈춰야 합니까?\"\n\n\"계속 갑시다. 페피노가 우리가 온다고 보초에게 알려 두었을 거요.\"\n\n두 사내 가운데 하나는 페피노였고, 다른 하나는 망을 보던 산적이었다. 프란츠와 백작이 다가가자, 산적이 그들에게 경례했다.\n\n\"각하,\" 페피노가 백작을 향해 말했다. \"저를 따라오시면, 지하 묘지 입구가 바로 코앞입니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 앞장서게.\" 백작이 대답했다. 그들은 덤불 더미 뒤, 바위가 쌓인 한가운데에 난 입구에 이르렀는데, 사람이 간신히 지날 만한 틈이었다. 페피노가 먼저 그 틈으로 미끄러져 들어갔다. 몇 걸음 나아가자 통로가 넓어졌다. 페피노는 안으로 들어가 횃불을 켜고는, 그들이 뒤따라오는지 돌아보았다. 백작이 먼저 트인 공간에 이르렀고, 프란츠가 바짝 뒤따랐다. 통로는 완만한 내리막으로 기울며 갈수록 넓어졌으나, 그래도 프란츠와 백작은 허리를 굽힌 채 나아가야 했고, 둘이 나란히 걷기도 어려웠다. 그렇게 백오십 걸음쯤 갔을 때, \"누구냐?\" 하는 소리에 발이 멈췄다. 그와 동시에 카빈총 총신에 비친 횃불의 반사광이 보였다.\n\n\"친구다!\" 페피노가 대답했다. 그러고는 혼자 보초를 향해 나아가 낮은 목소리로 몇 마디 건넸다. 그러자 보초도 앞서의 산적처럼 한밤의 손님들에게 경례하며, 들어가도 좋다는 신호를 보냈다.\n\n보초 뒤에는 스무 계단짜리 층계가 있었다. 프란츠와 백작이 그것을 내려가자, 시신을 안치하는 방에 이르렀다. 다섯 갈래 복도가 별의 빛살처럼 뻗어 있었고, 벽은 관 모양으로 위아래 차곡차곡 배열된 벽감들이 파여 있어, 마침내 그들이 지하 묘지에 들어섰음을 알려 주었다. 그 가운데 한 복도, 그 길이를 가늠할 수 없는 복도 끝에서 한 줄기 빛이 보였다. 백작이 프란츠의 어깨에 손을 얹었다.\n\n\"쉬고 있는 산적들의 진영을 보고 싶소?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"무척이나요.\" 프란츠가 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 나를 따라오시오. 페피노, 횃불을 꺼라.\" 페피노가 시키는 대로 하자, 프란츠와 백작은 칠흑 같은 어둠 속에 잠겼다. 다만 오십 걸음쯤 앞에서 불그스름한 빛이 벽을 따라 보였는데, 페피노가 횃불을 끄자 그 빛은 한층 또렷해졌다.\n\n그들은 말없이 나아갔다. 백작은 어둠 속에서도 볼 줄 아는 기이한 능력이라도 지닌 듯 프란츠를 이끌었다. 그러나 프란츠 자신도 길잡이 노릇을 하는 그 빛을 향해 갈수록 길이 점점 또렷이 보였다. 그들 앞에 아치 셋이 있었는데, 가운데 것이 문 노릇을 했다. 이 아치들은 한쪽으로는 백작과 프란츠가 있는 복도로, 다른 쪽으로는 앞서 말한 것과 똑같은 벽감들로 빙 둘러싸인 커다란 네모진 방으로 열려 있었다.\n\n이 방 한가운데에 돌 넷이 놓여 있었는데, 그 위에 아직도 십자가가 얹혀 있는 것으로 보아 한때 제단으로 쓰였던 것이 분명했다. 기둥 밑에 놓인 등잔 하나가 창백하게 일렁이는 불꽃으로, 그늘에 숨은 두 방문객의 눈앞에 펼쳐진 기이한 광경을 비추고 있었다.\n\n한 사내가 기둥에 팔꿈치를 괴고 앉아, 아치들에 등을 돌린 채 책을 읽고 있었다. 새로 들어온 이들은 그 아치 틈으로 그를 바라보았다. 그가 바로 패거리의 두목, 루이지 밤파였다. 그의 주위로는, 저마다 마음 내키는 대로 무리 지어, 망토를 두르고 누워 있거나, 납골당을 빙 두른 일종의 돌 의자에 등을 기댄 채, 스무 명 남짓한 산적들이 보였다. 저마다 손 닿는 곳에 카빈총을 두고 있었다. 방 저쪽 끝에는, 말없이 거의 보이지 않는 그림자 같은 보초 하나가, 동굴 하나 앞을 오락가락하고 있었다. 그 동굴은 그곳의 어둠이 다른 데보다 한층 짙어 보이는 까닭에만 겨우 분간이 되었다.\n\n백작은 프란츠가 이 그림 같은 광경을 충분히 들여다보았다 싶자, 손가락을 입술에 갖다 대어 조용히 하라는 신호를 보냈다. 그러고는 납골당의 복도로 이어지는 세 계단을 올라 가운데 아치를 통해 방으로 들어가, 밤파를 향해 나아갔다. 밤파는 눈앞의 책에 어찌나 골몰해 있던지 그의 발소리를 듣지 못했다.\n\n\"누구냐?\" 보초가 외쳤다. 그는 정신이 덜 팔려 있던 터라, 등잔불에 비친 그림자가 두목에게 다가오는 것을 본 것이었다. 이 외침에 밤파가 벌떡 일어나, 동시에 허리띠에서 권총을 뽑아 들었다. 순식간에 산적들이 모두 일어났고, 스무 자루의 카빈총이 백작을 겨누었다.\n\n\"이런,\" 그가 더없이 침착한 목소리로, 얼굴 근육 하나 흔들리지 않은 채 말했다. \"이런, 친애하는 밤파, 친구를 맞이하는데 격식이 어지간히도 거창하구먼.\"\n\n\"무기를 내려라!\" 두목이 한 손으로 단호한 손짓을 하며 외쳤다. 다른 손으로는 정중하게 모자를 벗었다. 그러고는 이 소동을 일으킨 그 기이한 인물을 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"용서하십시오, 각하. 방문해 주시는 영광을 누릴 줄은 전혀 생각지 못한 터라, 차마 알아뵙지 못했습니다.\"\n\n\"자네는 무엇에든 기억이 한결같이 짧은 모양이군, 밤파.\" 백작이 말했다. \"사람들 얼굴만 잊는 게 아니라, 그들과 맺은 약조까지도 잊으니 말이야.\"\n\n\"제가 무슨 약조를 잊었습니까, 각하?\" 산적이, 잘못을 저지르고 그걸 만회하고 싶어 안달하는 사람의 기색으로 물었다.\n\n\"내 한 몸뿐 아니라 내 벗들의 몸까지 자네가 존중하기로 하지 않았던가?\" 백작이 물었다.\n\n\"제가 그 약조를 어떻게 어겼다는 말씀입니까, 각하?\"\n\n\"자네는 오늘 저녁 알베르 드 모르세르 자작을 납치해 이리로 끌고 왔어. 그런데,\" 백작이 프란츠를 몸서리치게 하는 어조로 말을 이었다. \"이 젊은 신사는 내 벗 가운데 하나야. 이 젊은 신사는 나와 같은 호텔에 묵고 있어. 이 젊은 신사는 여덟 시간 동안이나 내 개인 마차를 타고 코르소 거리를 오르내렸지. 그런데도 다시 말하건대, 자네는 그를 납치해 이리로 끌고 왔고,\" 백작이 주머니에서 편지를 꺼내며 덧붙였다. \"마치 생판 모르는 남에게 그러듯 그에게 몸값을 매겼어.\"\n\n\"왜 너희는 이 모든 걸 나에게 말하지 않았느냐, 너희 말이다?\" 산적 두목이 부하들을 향해 돌아서며 물었고, 그들은 모두 그 눈빛 앞에서 뒷걸음질 쳤다. \"어찌하여 너희는 우리 모두의 목숨을 손에 쥐신 백작 같은 신사분께 내가 약속을 어기게 만들었느냐? 맹세코, 너희 가운데 누구라도 그 젊은 신사가 각하의 벗인 줄 알고 있었다면, 내 손으로 그놈의 머리통을 날려 버렸을 것이다!\"\n\n\"자,\" 백작이 프란츠를 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"내가 여기엔 무슨 착오가 있다고 하지 않았소.\"\n\n\"혼자 오신 게 아닙니까?\" 밤파가 불안한 듯 물었다.\n\n\"이 편지를 받은 사람과 함께 왔지. 그에게 루이지 밤파가 약속을 지키는 사내임을 증명해 보이고 싶었거든. 자, 각하,\" 백작이 프란츠를 돌아보며 덧붙였다. \"여기 루이지 밤파가, 자신이 저지른 착오에 대해 깊은 유감을 직접 표할 거요.\"\n\n프란츠가 다가서자, 두목이 그를 맞으러 몇 걸음 나아왔다.\n\n\"환영합니다, 각하.\" 그가 프란츠에게 말했다. \"방금 백작께서 하신 말씀과 제 대답을 들으셨겠지요. 한마디만 덧붙이자면, 제가 각하의 친구분 몸값으로 매긴 사천 피아스트르를 준다 해도, 이런 일이 일어나기를 바라지는 않았을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"한데,\" 프란츠가 불안한 듯 주위를 둘러보며 말했다. \"자작은 어디 있소? 보이지 않는구려.\"\n\n\"그에게 무슨 일이라도 생긴 게 아니길 바라네.\" 백작이 이맛살을 찌푸리며 말했다.\n\n\"포로는 저기 있습니다.\" 밤파가 산적이 망을 보고 있는 앞쪽의 우묵한 공간을 가리키며 대답했다. \"제가 직접 가서 그가 자유의 몸이 되었다고 일러 주겠습니다.\"\n\n두목은 알베르의 감옥이라고 가리킨 그 자리로 향했고, 프란츠와 백작이 그 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n\"포로는 무엇을 하고 있느냐?\" 밤파가 보초에게 물었다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 대장.\" 보초가 대답했다. \"모르겠습니다. 지난 한 시간 동안 꼼짝하는 소리도 못 들었으니까요.\"\n\n\"드시지요, 각하.\" 밤파가 말했다. 백작과 프란츠는 두목을 따라 일고여덟 계단을 올랐고, 두목이 빗장을 빼고 문을 열었다. 그러자 납골당을 밝히던 것과 비슷한 등잔불 아래, 알베르가 산적 하나가 빌려 준 망토를 두른 채 한쪽 구석에 누워 깊이 잠들어 있는 모습이 보였다.\n\n\"이런,\" 백작이 그 특유의 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"내일 아침 일곱 시에 총살당할 사람치고는 제법인걸.\"\n\n밤파는 일종의 감탄 어린 눈으로 알베르를 바라보았다. 그런 용기의 증거에 무감각할 사람이 아니었던 것이다.\n\n\"옳은 말씀입니다, 각하.\" 그가 말했다. \"이분은 틀림없이 각하의 벗 가운데 한 분이로군요.\"\n\n그러고는 알베르에게 다가가 어깨를 건드리며 말했다. \"각하, 좀 일어나 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n알베르는 두 팔을 쭉 뻗고 눈꺼풀을 비비더니 눈을 떴다.\n\n\"오,\" 그가 말했다. \"당신이오, 대장? 그냥 자게 내버려 두지 그랬소. 정말 근사한 꿈을 꾸고 있었는데. 토를로니아 댁에서 G 백작 부인과 갤럽을 추고 있었거든.\" 그러고는 시간이 얼마나 흘렀는지 보려고 주머니에서 시계를 꺼냈다.\n\n\"겨우 한 시 반인데?\" 그가 말했다. \"대체 이 시각에 왜 나를 깨우는 거요?\"\n\n\"각하께서 자유의 몸이 되셨다는 걸 알려 드리려고요.\"\n\n\"이보게,\" 알베르가 더없이 태평한 마음으로 대답했다. \"앞으로는 나폴레옹의 격언을 잊지 말게. '나쁜 소식이 아니거든 절대 나를 깨우지 마라.' 자게 내버려 두었더라면, 갤럽을 다 추고서 평생 자네에게 고마워했을 텐데. 그래, 그러니까 누가 내 몸값을 치렀단 말인가?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 각하.\"\n\n\"그럼 내가 어떻게 풀려난다는 거지?\"\n\n\"제가 무엇도 거절할 수 없는 분이 각하를 데려가겠다고 오셨습니다.\"\n\n\"여기로 오셨다고?\"\n\n\"네, 여기로요.\"\n\n\"정말로? 그렇다면 그분은 더없이 사랑스러운 분이로군.\"\n\n알베르가 주위를 둘러보다 프란츠를 발견했다. \"아니,\" 그가 말했다. \"자네였군, 친애하는 프란츠. 자네의 헌신과 우정이 이렇게 드러나는구먼.\"\n\n\"아니, 나는 아닐세.\" 프란츠가 대답했다. \"우리 이웃이신 몬테크리스토 백작이시네.\"\n\n\"오, 친애하는 백작님.\" 알베르가 즐겁게 말하며 넥타이와 소맷부리를 매만졌다. \"백작님은 정말이지 더없이 친절한 분이십니다. 부디 제가 영원히 빚을 진 사람으로 여겨 주십시오. 우선은 그 마차 때문에, 다음으로는 이 방문 때문에요.\" 그러고는 백작에게 손을 내밀었다. 백작은 자기 손을 내밀며 몸서리를 쳤으나, 그래도 손을 내밀어 주었다.\n\n산적은 이 광경을 어리둥절한 눈으로 바라보았다. 그는 자기 앞에서 포로들이 벌벌 떠는 것을 보는 데 익숙했던 것이 분명했는데, 여기 그 쾌활한 성정이 한순간도 흐트러지지 않는 자가 있었던 것이다. 프란츠로 말하자면, 알베르가 산적 앞에서 조국의 명예를 지켜 낸 그 태도에 매료되었다.\n\n\"이보게, 알베르,\" 그가 말했다. \"자네가 서둘러 준다면, 아직 토를로니아 댁에서 밤을 마저 보낼 시간이 있을 걸세. 끊겼던 갤럽을 마저 출 수도 있을 테니, 사실 이번 일 내내 신사답게 처신한 루이지 씨에게 원한을 품을 일도 없을 거고.\"\n\n\"자네 말이 전적으로 옳네. 두 시면 궁에 닿을 수 있겠어. 루이지 씨,\" 알베르가 말을 이었다. \"각하와 작별하기 전에 치러야 할 절차가 있소?\"\n\n\"없습니다, 자작님.\" 산적이 대답했다. \"자작님은 바람처럼 자유로운 몸입니다.\"\n\n\"그럼, 행복하고 즐거운 삶을 누리시길. 자, 여러분, 갑시다.\"\n\n알베르는 프란츠와 백작을 거느리고 층계를 내려가, 산적들이 모두 모자를 손에 든 채 서 있는 네모진 방을 가로질렀다.\n\n\"페피노,\" 산적 두목이 말했다. \"횃불을 다오.\"\n\n\"무얼 하려고?\" 백작이 물었다.\n\n\"제가 직접 돌아가는 길을 안내해 드리겠습니다.\" 대장이 말했다. \"그게 각하께 바칠 수 있는 최소한의 예의니까요.\"\n\n그러고는 양치기의 손에서 불붙은 횃불을 받아 들고 손님들 앞에 섰는데, 그 모습은 예를 차리는 하인이 아니라 사절들을 거느리고 앞서는 임금 같았다. 문에 이르자 그가 허리를 굽혔다.\n\n\"그럼 이제, 각하,\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"다시 한번 제 사과를 받아 주시고, 부디 오늘 벌어진 일에 어떤 앙심도 품지 말아 주시길 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"앙심이라니, 친애하는 밤파.\" 백작이 대답했다. \"게다가 자네가 그토록 신사다운 방식으로 잘못을 갚으니, 차라리 그 잘못을 저질러 준 것이 고마울 지경일세.\"\n\n\"여러분,\" 두목이 두 젊은이를 향해 돌아서며 덧붙였다. \"이 제안이 그다지 솔깃하게 들리지 않을지도 모릅니다만, 혹여 언제고 다시 한번 저를 찾아 주실 마음이 드신다면, 제가 어디에 있든 여러분을 환영하겠습니다.\"\n\n프란츠와 알베르가 허리를 굽혀 인사했다. 백작이 먼저 나갔고, 그다음 알베르가 나갔다. 프란츠는 잠시 걸음을 멈췄다.\n\n\"각하, 제게 물으실 게 있으십니까?\" 밤파가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소.\" 프란츠가 대답했다. \"우리가 들어왔을 때 당신이 그토록 골똘히 읽고 있던 책이 무엇인지 궁금하구려.\"\n\n\"카이사르의 『갈리아 전기』입니다.\" 산적이 말했다. \"제가 가장 좋아하는 책이지요.\"\n\n\"이보게, 안 오나?\" 알베르가 물었다.\n\n\"가네.\" 프란츠가 대답했다. \"여기 가네.\" 그러고는 그도 이번에는 동굴을 떠났다. 그들은 평지로 나아갔다.\n\n\"아, 실례하오.\" 알베르가 돌아서며 말했다. \"대장, 잠깐 괜찮겠소?\"\n\n그러고는 밤파의 횃불에 자기 시가에 불을 붙였다.\n\n\"자, 친애하는 백작님.\" 그가 말했다. \"되도록 서둘러 갑시다. 브라차노 공작 댁에서 밤을 마저 보내고 싶어 안달이 나서요.\"\n\n그들은 마차를 세워 두었던 자리에서 마차를 찾았다. 백작이 알리에게 아랍어로 한마디 건네자, 말들이 쏜살같이 달려 나갔다.\n\n두 친구가 무도회장으로 들어섰을 때, 알베르의 시계로 꼭 두 시였다. 그들의 귀환은 그야말로 하나의 사건이었으나, 둘이 함께 들어선 덕에 알베르를 둘러싼 모든 불안은 순식간에 가셨다.\n\n\"부인,\" 모르세르 자작이 백작 부인을 향해 나아가며 말했다. \"어제 부인께서 황송하게도 제게 갤럽 한 곡을 약속해 주셨지요. 이 고마운 약속을 청하기에 좀 늦은 감이 있습니다만, 여기 제 친구가 있습니다. 부인께서도 그의 진실됨을 잘 아시지요. 이 지체가 결코 제 탓이 아니었음을 그가 증언해 줄 겁니다.\"\n\n그리고 마침 그때 악단이 왈츠의 신호를 울리자, 알베르는 백작 부인의 허리에 팔을 두르고 춤추는 무리의 소용돌이 속으로 그녀와 함께 사라졌다.\n\n그동안 프란츠는, 어떤 의미로 떠밀리다시피 알베르에게 손을 내밀어야 했던 그 순간 몬테크리스토 백작을 스치고 지나간 그 기이한 전율에 대해 곰곰이 생각하고 있었다.\n\n# Chapter 38. The Rendezvous\n\n다음 날 아침, 알베르가 친구에게 건넨 첫마디는 백작을 찾아가는 길에 함께 가 달라는 부탁이었다. 사실 그는 전날 저녁에도 백작에게 따뜻하고 열렬하게 감사를 표한 터였다. 하지만 백작이 베푼 정도의 도움이라면 아무리 거듭 사례해도 지나치지 않았다. 프란츠는 어떤 보이지 않는 힘에 이끌리듯 백작에게 끌렸는데, 그 끌림에는 묘하게도 공포가 뒤섞여 있었다. 그래서 그는 이 신비로운 인물이 친구에게 행사하는 듯한 야릇한 매혹 앞에 알베르를 홀로 내버려 두는 것이 몹시 꺼림칙했다. 결국 알베르의 부탁에 토를 달지 않고 곧장 약속한 장소로 함께 갔고, 잠시 기다리자 백작이 객실로 나와 그들과 합류했다.\n\n\"친애하는 백작님,\" 알베르가 그를 맞으러 다가서며 말했다. \"어젯밤 드린 변변찮은 감사를 다시 한번 되풀이하게 해 주십시오. 그리고 백작님께 진 빚의 기억은 결코 제 머릿속에서 지워지지 않으리라는 것도 분명히 말씀드리고 싶습니다. 믿어 주십시오, 살아 있는 한 저는 백작님이 베풀어 주신 그 신속하고 막중한 도움을 감사한 마음으로 늘 되새길 것입니다. 게다가 제 목숨까지 백작님께 빚지고 있다는 사실도 잊지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n\"내 더없이 좋은 벗이자 훌륭한 이웃이여,\" 백작이 미소를 지으며 대답했다. \"정말이지 그대는 내 사소한 수고를 너무 부풀려 말하는군요. 그대가 내게 진 것이라곤 여행 경비에서 아낀 2만 프랑 남짓한 푼돈뿐이니, 우리 사이에 셈할 거리도 그리 많지 않습니다. 다만 그대가 제 운명에 어찌나 태연하고 무심하게 몸을 맡겼는지, 또 사태가 어떻게 흘러가든 더없이 무심한 태도를 보였는지, 그 점만큼은 꼭 축하해 드려야겠습니다.\"\n\n\"솔직히 말해,\" 알베르가 말했다. \"어쩔 수 없는 일에 대해서는 제가 칭찬받을 까닭이 없지요. 다시 말해 모든 일을 있는 그대로 받아들이기로 마음먹은 것, 그리고 그 도적들에게 보여 주려 한 것뿐입니다. 세상 어디서든 사람은 곤란한 처지에 빠지게 마련이지만, 음산한 죽음의 신 앞에서조차 미소 지을 수 있는 민족은 프랑스인밖에 없다는 것을요. 하지만 그런 건 백작님께 진 빚과는 아무 상관이 없습니다. 이제 여쭙고 싶은 건, 제 한 몸으로든, 제 가문으로든, 제 인맥으로든, 어떤 식으로든 백작님께 도움이 될 길이 있겠느냐는 것입니다. 제 아버지 모르세르 백작께서는 에스파냐 혈통이긴 하지만 프랑스 궁정과 마드리드 궁정 양쪽에 상당한 영향력을 지니고 계십니다. 저 자신은 물론, 제가 아끼는 모든 이의 도움을 망설임 없이 백작님께 내드리겠습니다.\"\n\n\"모르세르 씨,\" 백작이 대답했다. \"그대의 제안은 나를 놀라게 하기는커녕, 바로 내가 그대에게서 기대하던 바입니다. 그러니 그 제안이 베풀어진 것과 똑같이 진심 어린 마음으로 받아들이겠습니다. 아니, 한 걸음 더 나아가 말하지요. 실은 나도 그대에게 큰 부탁 하나를 청하려고 진작부터 마음먹고 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"오, 부디 말씀하십시오.\"\n\n\"나는 파리에 대해 전혀 모르는 이방인입니다. 아직 한 번도 본 적이 없는 도시지요.\"\n\n\"그게 가능한 일입니까?\" 알베르가 외쳤다. \"이 나이가 되도록 세상에서 가장 아름다운 수도를 한 번도 가 보지 않으셨다니요? 도무지 믿기지가 않습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇지만 정말 사실입니다. 그래도 유럽 제일의 도시에 대한 이 무지가 어느 모로 보나 내게는 부끄러운 일이며, 당장 바로잡아야 할 문제라는 점에서는 그대와 생각이 같습니다. 하지만 그대들이 자랑할 만한 그 수도의 경이와 아름다움을 익히는 일, 그토록 중대하고 꼭 필요한 의무라면 진작에 해냈을 겁니다. 나를 사교계에 소개해 줄 만한 사람을 한 명이라도 알았다면 말입니다. 그런데 불행히도 그곳에는 아는 이가 하나도 없었고, 어쩔 수 없이 그 생각을 접어야 했지요.\"\n\n\"백작님처럼 출중하신 분이라면,\" 알베르가 외쳤다. \"소개 같은 건 거의 필요 없으셨을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"참으로 친절하시군요. 하지만 내게 무슨 내세울 만한 점이 있는지 모르겠습니다. 굳이 꼽자면 백만장자이니 아구아도 씨나 로트실트 씨의 투기 사업에 동업자로 끼어들 수야 있겠지요. 하지만 내가 그대들의 수도를 찾는 까닭이 주식 놀음의 재미 때문은 아닐 테니, 내 소망을 이룰 만한 좋은 기회가 나타날 때까지 그저 발길을 미루어 두었던 겁니다. 그런데 그대의 제안이 그 모든 난관을 말끔히 풀어 주는군요. 그러니 친애하는 모르세르 씨,\" 이 말에는 더없이 묘한 미소가 곁들여졌다. \"이제 한 가지만 묻겠습니다. 내가 프랑스에 닿거든, 내게는 휴런족이나 코친차이나 토박이만큼이나 낯선 그 사교계의 문을 열어 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"오, 물론이지요, 그것도 더없이 기쁜 마음으로요.\" 알베르가 대답했다. \"마침 오늘 아침 아버지께서 보낸 편지가 저를 파리로 부르고 있으니 더욱 흔쾌히 응할 수 있습니다. 어느 명문가와의 혼담 때문인데요(친애하는 프란츠, 제발 웃지 좀 말게), 파리 사교계의 최상류층과 연이 닿는 집안입니다.\"\n\n\"혼인으로 연이 닿는 집안이라는 말이겠지.\" 프란츠가 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"뭐, 어떻든 상관없네.\" 알베르가 대답했다. \"결국 다 그게 그거니까. 어쩌면 자네가 파리로 돌아올 무렵에는 내가 진중하고 점잖은 한 집안의 가장이 되어 있을지도 모르지! 온갖 가정의 미덕을 더없이 교화적으로 보여 주는 본보기가 되어 있을 거야. 그렇게 생각지 않나? 아무튼 친애하는 백작님, 우리 멋진 도시를 보고 싶으시다는 그 소망에 관해서는 이렇게만 말씀드리겠습니다. 저와 제 식구들을 마음껏 부리셔도 좋습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 정해진 거로군요.\" 백작이 말했다. \"내 엄숙히 장담하건대, 오랫동안 곱씹어 온 계획을 실현할 기회로 바로 이런 기회를 기다려 왔습니다.\"\n\n프란츠는 이 계획이 백작이 몬테크리스토섬 동굴에서 몇 마디 흘린 적 있던 바로 그 계획이리라는 것을 의심치 않았다. 백작이 말하는 동안 젊은이는 그 얼굴에서 속내를 조금이라도 읽어 내기를 바라며 그를 유심히 살폈다. 하지만 그의 표정은 헤아릴 수 없었다. 특히 지금처럼 스핑크스 같은 미소에 가려져 있을 때는 더욱 그러했다.\n\n\"그런데 말씀해 주십시오, 백작님,\" 몬테크리스토처럼 출중한 인물의 안내자 노릇을 하게 되었다는 생각에 들뜬 알베르가 외쳤다. \"정말 진심으로 하시는 말씀인지, 아니면 이 파리 방문 계획이 우리가 살면서 숱하게 짓는 그 허황되고 막연한 공중누각 가운데 하나일 뿐인지, 솔직히 말씀해 주십시오. 모래 위에 지은 집처럼 첫 바람 한 줄기에 날아가 버릴 그런 것 말입니다.\"\n\n\"내 명예를 걸고 맹세하지요.\" 백작이 대답했다. \"말한 그대로 실행할 작정입니다. 내키는 마음에서나, 분명한 필요에서나, 나는 파리에 가야만 합니다.\"\n\n\"언제쯤 가실 생각이십니까?\"\n\n\"그대는 언제 그곳에 가 있을지 마음을 정했습니까?\"\n\n\"물론 정했지요. 두 주나 세 주 안에요. 다시 말해 갈 수 있는 한 빨리요!\"\n\n\"아니지요.\" 백작이 말했다. \"그대와 합류하기까지 석 달의 말미를 두겠습니다. 보다시피 온갖 지체와 난관을 넉넉히 감안해 두는 거지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 석 달 뒤에는,\" 알베르가 말했다. \"제 집으로 오시는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"특정한 날과 시각을 딱 잡아 약속을 정해 둘까요?\" 백작이 물었다. \"다만 한 가지 일러 두자면, 나는 약속을 빈틈없이 지키기로 정평이 난 사람입니다.\"\n\n\"날은 날대로, 시각은 시각대로 정하지요.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"그게 제게는 딱 좋습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 그렇게 합시다.\" 백작이 대답하며 벽난로 곁에 걸린 달력 쪽으로 손을 뻗었다. \"오늘이 2월 21일이군요.\" 그러고는 회중시계를 꺼내며 덧붙였다. \"지금이 정확히 열 시 반입니다. 자, 이걸 기억해 두겠다고 약속하십시오. 5월 21일, 오전 같은 시각에 나를 기다리시면 됩니다.\"\n\n\"더할 나위 없군요!\" 알베르가 외쳤다. \"아침 식사를 준비해 두고 기다리겠습니다.\"\n\n\"어디에 사십니까?\"\n\n\"엘데르가 27번지입니다.\"\n\n\"거기 독신자용 거처가 따로 있습니까? 제가 가는 것이 그대에게 불편을 끼치지 않았으면 합니다만.\"\n\n\"저는 아버지 댁에서 지내지만, 안마당 저쪽 끝에 본채와 완전히 떨어진 별채를 쓰고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그거면 충분하지요.\" 백작이 대답하며 수첩을 꺼내 적어 넣었다. \"엘데르가 27번지, 5월 21일, 오전 열 시 반.\"\n\n\"자, 그럼,\" 백작이 수첩을 다시 주머니에 넣으며 말했다. \"마음 푹 놓으십시오. 그대의 시계 바늘도 나만큼 정확하게 시각을 가리키지는 못할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"떠나기 전에 다시 뵐 수 있을까요?\" 알베르가 물었다.\n\n\"그건 사정에 따라 다르지요. 언제 떠납니까?\"\n\n\"내일 저녁 다섯 시입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 작별 인사를 드려야겠군요. 나는 나폴리에 가야 하는데, 토요일 저녁이나 일요일 아침이 되어야 이리로 돌아올 테니까요. 그리고 남작,\" 백작이 프란츠에게로 말을 돌리며 이었다. \"그대도 내일 떠납니까?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"프랑스로요?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 베네치아로 갑니다. 한두 해는 더 이탈리아에 머물 작정입니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 우리는 파리에서 만나지 못하겠군요?\"\n\n\"안타깝게도 그런 영광은 누리지 못할 듯합니다.\"\n\n\"자, 헤어져야 한다니,\" 백작이 두 젊은이에게 각각 한 손씩을 내밀며 말했다. \"두 분 모두 무사하고 즐거운 여행이 되기를 빌겠습니다.\"\n\n프란츠의 손이 눈앞의 이 신비로운 인물의 손에 닿은 것은 그때가 처음이었다. 그는 그 손길에 자기도 모르게 몸을 떨었다. 시신의 손처럼 차갑고 얼음장 같았던 것이다.\n\n\"서로 분명히 해 두지요.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"정한 거지요? 5월 21일 오전 열 시 반에 엘데르가 27번지에 와 주시고, 시간을 어기지 않겠다고 명예를 걸어 약속하신 거고요?\"\n\n\"5월 21일 오전 열 시 반, 엘데르가 27번지.\" 백작이 대답했다.\n\n그러자 두 젊은이는 자리에서 일어나 백작에게 인사하고 방을 나섰다.\n\n\"무슨 일인가?\" 자기들 거처로 돌아오자 알베르가 프란츠에게 물었다. \"자네 평소보다 생각이 많은 얼굴인데.\"\n\n\"솔직히 말하겠네, 알베르.\" 프란츠가 대답했다. \"백작은 아주 기이한 사람이야. 자네가 파리에서 그를 만나기로 한 그 약속이 나를 천 가지 불안으로 가득 채우는군.\"\n\n\"이 사람아,\" 알베르가 외쳤다. \"그게 뭐 불안해할 거리라도 된단 말인가? 자네 제정신이 아닌 모양일세.\"\n\n\"내가 제정신이든 아니든,\" 프란츠가 대답했다. \"내 느낌이 그렇다는 거야.\"\n\n\"내 말 좀 들어 보게, 프란츠.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"마침 이런 말을 할 기회가 생겨서 다행이군. 자네가 백작을 대하는 태도가 어찌나 냉랭한지 진작부터 눈여겨봤거든. 반대로 그쪽은 우리에게 늘 깍듯하기 그지없었는데 말이야. 그에게 무슨 특별히 거슬리는 점이라도 있나?\"\n\n\"어쩌면.\"\n\n\"여기 오기 전에 그를 만난 적이 있었나?\"\n\n\"있어.\"\n\n\"어디서?\"\n\n\"내가 지금부터 하는 말을 한마디도 입 밖에 내지 않겠다고 약속하겠나?\"\n\n\"약속하지.\"\n\n\"명예를 걸고?\"\n\n\"명예를 걸고.\"\n\n\"그럼 들어 보게.\"\n\n이어서 프란츠는 몬테크리스토섬으로 나들이를 갔던 이야기, 거기서 밀수업자 무리와 그들과 함께 있던 코르시카 도적 둘을 만난 일을 친구에게 들려주었다. 그는 백작에게서 받은 거의 마법 같던 환대와, 천일야화의 동굴에서 누린 그 호화로운 접대를 상당한 힘과 열의를 실어 자세히 이야기했다.\n\n만찬, 대마초, 조각상들, 꿈, 그리고 눈을 떴을 때 그 모든 일의 증거나 흔적이라곤 멀리 수평선 위에서 돛을 활짝 펴고 포르토베키오 쪽으로 내달리던 작은 요트밖에 남아 있지 않았다는 것까지, 그는 빠짐없이 정확하게 자초지종을 늘어놓았다.\n\n이어 그는 콜로세움에서 우연히 엿들은 백작과 밤파 사이의 대화를 자세히 전했다. 그 대화에서 백작은 도적 페피노를 풀려나게 해 주겠다고 약속했고, 독자들도 알다시피 그 약속을 더없이 충실하게 지켰다.\n\n마침내 그는 전날 밤의 일에 다다랐다. 필요한 액수를 채우기에 육칠백 피아스트르가 모자라 현금이 부족했던 곤혹스러운 처지, 그리고 끝내 백작에게 도움을 청했던 일과 그 뒤에 따라온 인상적이고도 흡족한 결말까지 이야기했다. 알베르는 더없이 깊은 주의를 기울여 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"그래,\" 프란츠가 이야기를 마치자 그가 말했다. \"자네가 지금까지 늘어놓은 그 모든 이야기에서 대체 무엇이 거슬린다는 건가? 백작은 여행을 좋아하고, 부자이니 자기 배도 한 척 가지고 있는 거지. 포츠머스나 사우샘프턴에 한번 가 보게. 그 비용을 감당할 만하고 이런 유희를 똑같이 즐기는 영국인들의 요트로 항구가 빼곡할 테니까. 그러니까 나들이를 다니는 동안 쉴 곳을 마련하려고, 또 지난 넉 달 동안 나를 어떻게든 독살하려 들던 그 형편없는 요리를 피하려고 말이야. 자네는 그걸 그만큼 여러 해를 사내답게 견뎌 냈지만 말이지. 아무튼 잠이라도 청할 만한 침대를 얻으려고 몬테크리스토는 자네가 그를 처음 발견한 그곳에 임시 거처를 꾸민 거야. 다만 토스카나 정부가 그 마법의 궁전을 탐내서, 그렇게 막대한 자본을 쏟아부은 데서 으레 기대할 만한 이득을 빼앗아 갈 가능성을 막으려고, 그는 아주 현명하게도 그 섬을 사들이고 그 이름까지 취한 거지. 자, 곰곰이 생각해 보게, 이 사람아. 우리가 아는 이들 중에도 평생 한 번도 주인이었던 적 없는 땅이며 영지의 이름을 갖다 쓰는 사람이 한둘이 아니지 않나?\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 프란츠가 말했다. \"그 배의 선원들 틈에 끼어 있던 코르시카 도적들은 어쩌고?\"\n\n\"아니, 정말이지 그건 내가 보기엔 아주 간단한 일일세. 코르시카의 도적들이 악당이나 도둑이 아니라는 건 누구보다 자네가 잘 알지 않나. 그들은 그저 어떤 불길한 사정에 떠밀려 고향 마을에서 쫓겨난 도망자일 뿐이고, 그들과 어울린다고 해서 망신이나 오명이 따라붙는 것도 아니야. 나로 말하자면, 단언컨대 언젠가 코르시카에 가게 된다면 시장이나 도지사를 찾아뵙기에 앞서 제일 먼저 콜롱바의 도적들부터 만나러 갈 걸세. 어떻게든 그들을 찾아낼 수만 있다면 말이야. 정말이지 그들은 내가 크게 흠모하는 부류의 사람들이거든.\"\n\n\"그래도,\" 프란츠가 굽히지 않고 말했다. \"밤파와 그 패거리 같은 자들은 영락없는 악당들이라는 건 자네도 인정하겠지. 사람을 붙잡아 두는 데 약탈 말고는 다른 동기가 없는 자들이잖나. 그런데 백작이 그 불한당들에게 분명히 행사한 그 영향력은 자네 대체 어떻게 설명할 텐가?\"\n\n\"이 사람아, 십중팔구 내가 지금 무사한 것이 바로 그 영향력 덕분일 텐데, 그 출처를 너무 깊이 캐는 건 나로서는 도리가 아니지. 그러니 그가 무법자들과 가까이 지낸다고 비난할 게 아니라, 그런 교제에 다소 떳떳지 못한 구석이 있더라도 너그러이 봐줄 수 있게 해 주게. 꼭 내 목숨을 건져 줘서만은 아니야. 내 목숨이 그리 큰 위험에 처했던 적은 없었다는 게 내 생각이니까. 하지만 적어도 내게서 4천 피아스트르를 절약해 준 건 분명하지. 그걸 환산하면 우리 돈으로 딱 2만 4천 리브르야. 단언컨대 프랑스에서라면 내 몸값이 결코 그만큼으로 매겨지지는 않았을 테고,\" 알베르가 웃으며 덧붙였다. \"이건 어떤 예언자도 제 고향에서는 대접받지 못한다는 걸 더없이 명백하게 증명해 주는 셈이지.\"\n\n\"나라 이야기가 나왔으니 말인데,\" 프란츠가 대꾸했다. \"백작은 대체 어느 나라 사람이며, 모국어는 무엇이고, 그 막대한 재산은 어디서 나온 것이며, 그토록 어둡고 음울한 인간 혐오로 이후의 세월을 물들여 놓은 그 젊은 날의 사건들은 무엇이었을까? 알려진 바 없으면서도 경이롭기 그지없는 그 삶 말이야. 정말이지 자네 처지라면 나는 이런 물음들에 답을 듣고 싶을 텐데.\"\n\n\"친애하는 프란츠,\" 알베르가 대답했다. \"내 편지를 받고 백작의 도움을 청할 필요가 있다 싶었을 때, 자네는 곧장 그에게 달려가 이렇게 말했지. '제 친구 알베르 드 모르세르가 위험에 처했습니다. 그를 구해 내도록 도와주십시오.' 자네가 한 말이 대충 그런 거였잖나?\"\n\n\"그랬지.\"\n\n\"자, 그럼 그가 자네에게 이렇게 물었나? '알베르 드 모르세르 씨가 누구요? 그 이름은, 그 재산은 어떻게 얻은 것이오? 무얼로 먹고사는 사람이오? 태생은 어디고, 어느 나라 사람이오?' 말해 보게, 그가 자네에게 이런 질문들을 죄다 던지던가?\"\n\n\"고백하건대 아무것도 묻지 않았네.\"\n\n\"그렇지. 그는 그저 와서 나를 밤파 나리의 손아귀에서 풀어 줬을 뿐이야. 장담하지만, 겉으로 아무리 태연하고 무심한 척했어도 나는 거기 더 붙잡혀 있고 싶은 마음이 조금도 없었거든. 자, 그러니 프란츠, 그토록 신속하고 망설임 없이 도움을 베푼 대가로 그가 내게 청한 거라곤, 파리를 지나는 러시아 공작이나 이탈리아 귀족을 위해 날마다 하는 그 일, 그러니까 그저 사교계에 소개해 달라는 것뿐인데, 자네라면 그걸 거절하겠나? 이 사람아, 내가 그토록 냉혈한 같은 잇속을 차리며 굴 수 있으리라 생각하다니, 자네 제정신이 아닌 게 분명하네.\"\n\n그리고 이번만큼은 인정해야 했다. 두 젊은이 사이의 논쟁에서 으레 벌어지던 상황과는 반대로, 설득력 있는 논거는 모조리 알베르 쪽에 있었다.\n\n\"좋네.\" 프란츠가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"마음대로 하게, 친애하는 자작. 자네 논거는 내가 반박할 수 있는 범위를 넘어섰으니까. 그래도 무엇보다, 이 몬테크리스토 백작이 더없이 기이한 인물이라는 것만큼은 자네도 인정해야 하네.\"\n\n\"그는 박애주의자야.\" 상대가 대답했다. \"그가 파리에 가는 동기도 분명 몽티옹 상을 노려서일 테지. 자네도 알다시피 미덕과 인류의 이익을 가장 실질적으로 증진시켰다고 입증된 사람에게 주는 상 말이야. 내 한 표와 영향력으로 그가 그 상을 받을 수 있다면, 나는 기꺼이 한 표를 던지고 영향력도 약속하겠네. 자, 친애하는 프란츠, 이제 다른 이야기를 하세. 어떤가, 점심이나 들고 나서 성 베드로 성당에 마지막으로 한 번 가 볼까?\"\n\n프란츠는 말없이 동의했다. 그리고 이튿날 오후 다섯 시 반, 두 젊은이는 헤어졌다. 알베르 드 모르세르는 파리로 돌아가고, 프란츠 데피네는 베네치아에서 두 주를 보내기 위해서였다.\n\n다만 여행 마차에 오르기 전, 알베르는 자기가 기다릴 손님이 맺어 둔 약속을 잊을까 염려되어, 호텔의 종업원에게 명함 한 장을 맡기며 몬테크리스토 백작에게 전해 달라고 부탁했다. 그 명함에는 알베르 드 모르세르 자작이라는 이름 아래 연필로 이렇게 적혀 있었다.\n\n\"엘데르가 27번지, 5월 21일 오전 열 시 반.\"\n\n# Chapter 39. The Guests\n\n알베르가 몬테크리스토 백작을 초대한 엘데르가의 저택에서는, 5월 21일 아침부터 그 자리를 빛내기 위한 만반의 준비가 갖추어지고 있었다. 알베르 드 모르세르는 넓은 안뜰 한 모퉁이에 자리한 별채에 살았는데, 그 맞은편에는 하인들의 거처가 있는 또 다른 건물이 곧장 마주 서 있었다. 별채의 창문 가운데 거리를 향한 것은 둘뿐이었고, 셋은 안뜰을, 뒤편의 둘은 정원을 내다보고 있었다.\n\n안뜰과 정원 사이에는, 제정 양식의 묵직한 풍취로 지어진 모르세르 백작 부부의 크고 화려한 저택이 서 있었다.\n\n높은 담장이 저택 전체를 둘러싸고 있었고, 군데군데 꽃을 가득 담은 화분들이 그 위에 얹혀 있었으며, 한가운데는 금빛 철문이 담을 갈라 마차 출입구 노릇을 했다. 문지기 막사 가까이에는 작은 문이 하나 더 있어, 걸어서 드나드는 하인들과 주인들이 그리로 나고 들었다.\n\n이 거처를 알베르에게 골라 준 것은, 아들과 떨어지기를 원치 않으면서도 자작 또래의 젊은이라면 자유를 한껏 누려야 한다는 것을 아는 어머니의 섬세한 배려였음을 어렵지 않게 짐작할 수 있었다. 그러나 외아들의 나른하고 거리낌 없는 삶에 매료되어, 말하자면 황금 새장 속에서 살아가는 젊은이의 이른바 영리한 이기심을 보여 주는 흔적도 적지 않았다. 거리를 내다보는 두 창문을 통해 알베르는 바깥에서 벌어지는 모든 일을 볼 수 있었다. 무슨 일이 일어나는지 지켜보는 것은 젊은이들에게 꼭 필요한 일이다. 그 지평선이 한낱 공공 대로에 지나지 않더라도, 그들은 늘 세상이 제 시야를 가로질러 가는 모습을 보고 싶어 하니까. 그러다 좀 더 면밀히 살펴볼 만한 것이 나타나면, 알베르 드 모르세르는 문지기 옆문과 비슷한 작은 쪽문을 통해 그 탐색을 이어 갈 수 있었는데, 그 문은 따로 설명해 둘 만하다.\n\n그것은 집이 지어진 이래로 한 번도 열린 적이 없는 듯한 작은 문이었다. 먼지와 때가 어찌나 빈틈없이 뒤덮고 있었던지 말이다. 그러나 기름이 잘 칠해진 경첩과 자물쇠는 전혀 다른 이야기를 들려주었다. 이 문은 문지기를 우롱하는 존재였다. 그의 감시와 관할에서 벗어나 있었으니까. 그리고 알리바바의 \"열려라 참깨\"라는 주문에 열리던 그 유명한 「천일야화」 속의 문처럼, 세상에서 가장 감미로운 목소리나 가장 새하얀 손가락이 바깥에서 보내는 은밀한 주문이나 약속된 두드림에 맞추어 안쪽으로 스르르 열리곤 했다.\n\n그 문과 통하며 응접실 노릇을 하는 긴 복도 끝에는, 오른쪽으로 안뜰을 내다보는 알베르의 식당이 있었고, 왼쪽으로는 정원을 향한 살롱이 있었다. 관목과 덩굴식물이 창문을 뒤덮어, 정원과 안뜰로부터 이 두 방을 가려 주었다. 1층에 있는 까닭에 호기심 많은 이들의 엿보는 눈길이 들여다볼 수 있는 방은 오직 이 둘뿐이었다.\n\n위층에도 비슷한 방들이 있었는데, 거기에 응접실을 터서 만든 세 번째 방이 더해져 있었다. 이 세 방은 살롱과 부두아르와 침실이었다. 아래층 살롱은 흡연객들을 위한 알제리식 긴 의자 하나가 전부였다. 위층 부두아르는 계단 쪽으로 난 보이지 않는 문을 통해 침실과 통했다. 모든 경계가 빈틈없이 갖추어져 있음이 분명했다. 이 층 위에는 칸막이를 헐어 넓힌 큰 화실이 있었으니, 예술가와 멋쟁이가 서로 우위를 다투는 아수라장이었다.\n\n거기에는 알베르의 변덕이 차례로 거쳐 간 물건들이 죄다 모여 쌓여 있었다. 사냥용 뿔피리, 베이스비올, 플루트 등 오케스트라 한 무리가 다 있었으니, 알베르가 음악에 진정한 취미라기보다는 한때의 변덕을 품었던 까닭이었다. 이젤과 팔레트, 붓과 연필도 있었으니, 음악의 뒤를 그림이 이었던 것이다. 펜싱 검과 권투 장갑, 폭이 넓은 검과 단봉도 있었으니, 당대 멋쟁이 젊은이들의 본을 따라 알베르 드 모르세르가 음악이나 그림보다 훨씬 끈기 있게 갈고닦은, 멋쟁이의 교양을 완성하는 세 가지 기예, 곧 펜싱과 권투와 단봉술 때문이었다. 그리고 그가 그리지에, 쿡스, 샤를 르부셰를 맞이하던 곳이 바로 여기였다.\n\n이 특별한 방의 나머지 가구로는, 중국 도자기와 일본 화병, 루카 델라 로비아의 도기와 팔리시의 접시로 가득 찬 낡은 장식장들이 있었다. 그리고 어쩌면 앙리 4세나 쉴리, 루이 13세나 리슐리외가 앉았을지도 모를 낡은 안락의자들이 있었다. 그중 두 개는 푸른 바탕에 프랑스 왕가의 백합 문장이 새겨진 방패꼴 장식으로 꾸며져 있었는데, 분명 루브르궁이나 적어도 어느 왕실 거처에서 나온 것이었다.\n\n이 어둡고 침침한 의자들 위에는, 페르시아의 햇살 아래 물들였거나 캘커타나 샹데르나고르 여인들의 손길로 짠 화려한 직물들이 걸쳐져 있었다. 이 직물들이 거기서 무엇을 하고 있는지는 도무지 알 수 없었다. 그것들은 눈을 즐겁게 하면서도, 주인 자신조차 알지 못하는 용처를 기다리고 있었다. 그러는 동안 그것들은 금빛과 비단빛 광채로 방 안을 가득 채우고 있었다.\n\n방 한가운데에는 자단으로 만든 롤러앤블랑셰 사의 작은 그랜드피아노가 놓여 있었다. 비좁으면서도 울림 좋은 그 공동 안에 오케스트라의 모든 가능성을 품은 채, 베토벤과 베버, 모차르트와 하이든, 그레트리와 포르포라의 걸작들 무게에 짓눌려 신음하고 있었다.\n\n벽에도, 문 위에도, 천장에도 검과 단검, 말레이의 크리스 단도, 철퇴와 전투용 도끼가 걸려 있었다. 금박을 입히고 다마스쿠스식으로 무늬를 새기고 상감 세공을 한 갑옷들이 있었고, 말린 식물과 광물, 그리고 박제된 새들이 있었다. 불꽃 빛깔의 날개를 활짝 편 채 미동 없는 날갯짓을 멈추고, 부리는 영원히 벌린 모습이었다. 이곳이 알베르가 가장 좋아하는 휴식처였다.\n\n그러나 약속이 있는 그날 아침, 젊은이는 아래층의 작은 살롱에 자리를 잡았다. 그곳에는 탁자 하나가 있었고, 그 둘레로 조금 떨어져 크고 호화로운 긴 의자가 놓여 있었다. 탁자 위에는 알려진 온갖 종류의 담배가, 페테르부르크의 노란 담배에서 시나이의 검은 담배에 이르기까지, 또 메릴랜드와 푸에르토리코를 거쳐 라타키아에 이르기까지 죽 펼쳐져 있었다. 네덜란드인들이 그토록 좋아하는 잔금 간 도기 항아리들에 담긴 채였다. 그 곁에는 향기로운 나무로 만든 상자들 속에 푸로, 레갈리아, 아바나, 마닐라 시가가 크기와 품질에 따라 가지런히 정리되어 있었다. 그리고 열린 장식장 안에는 독일제 파이프와, 호박 물부리에 산호 장식을 단 치부크, 그리고 모로코가죽으로 만든 긴 관이 달린 물담뱃대들이 한데 모여, 흡연객들의 변덕이나 취향을 기다리고 있었다.\n\n알베르는 이 배치, 아니 차라리 대칭을 이룬 흐트러뜨림을 직접 주관했는데, 요즘 사람들은 아침 식사 자리에서 커피를 마신 뒤 이 풍경을, 입에서 새어 나와 길고 환상적인 고리를 그리며 천장으로 피어오르는 연기 사이로 바라보기를 즐겼다.\n\n10시 15분 전, 하인 하나가 들어왔다. 그는 영어밖에 할 줄 모르는 존이라는 어린 마부와 더불어 알베르가 부리는 사람의 전부였다. 물론 호텔 요리사가 늘 그의 시중을 들 채비가 되어 있었고, 큰 행사가 있을 때는 백작의 사냥꾼까지 거들기는 했지만 말이다. 제르맹이라는 이름의 이 하인은 젊은 주인의 두터운 신임을 받고 있었는데, 한 손에는 신문 몇 부를, 다른 손에는 편지 한 묶음을 들고 와 알베르에게 건넸다. 알베르는 여러 통의 서신을 무심히 훑어보다가, 작고 섬세한 필체로 쓰여 향내 나는 봉투에 담긴 두 통을 골라내, 그것들을 뜯어 사뭇 주의 깊게 내용을 읽었다.\n\n\"이 편지들은 어떻게 왔지?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"한 통은 우편으로 왔고, 다른 한 통은 당글라르 부인의 하인이 두고 갔습니다.\"\n\n\"당글라르 부인께, 마련해 주신 관람석 자리를 받겠다고 전해라. 잠깐, 그리고 낮 동안에 로자에게, 오페라가 끝나면 그녀가 바라는 대로 함께 밤참을 들겠다고 일러라. 그녀에게 서로 다른 포도주 여섯 병을 가져가라. 키프로스, 셰리, 말라가 술로, 그리고 오스텐드 굴 한 통도. 보렐 가게에서 사고, 내 것이라고 꼭 말하도록 해라.\"\n\n\"식사는 몇 시에 하시겠습니까, 도련님?\"\n\n\"지금 몇 시지?\"\n\n\"10시 15분 전입니다.\"\n\n\"좋아, 10시 반에 하겠다. 드브레는 어쩌면 장관에게 가 봐야 할지도 모르니까. 게다가,\" 알베르가 수첩을 들여다보며 말했다. \"내가 백작에게 말해 둔 시각이 5월 21일 10시 반이고, 그의 약속을 그리 믿지는 않지만 나는 시간을 지키고 싶거든. 백작 부인은 일어나셨느냐?\"\n\n\"원하신다면 여쭙고 오겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 그분의 리큐어 술상자 하나를 빌려 달라고 여쭤라. 내 것은 다 갖춰지지 않았으니. 그리고 3시쯤 찾아뵙는 영광을 누리겠다고, 또 어떤 분을 소개해 드리고 싶으니 허락을 구한다고 전해라.\"\n\n하인이 방을 나갔다. 알베르는 긴 의자에 몸을 던지고, 신문 두세 부의 띠지를 뜯어내 극장 광고를 들여다보더니, 발레가 아니라 오페라를 한다는 것을 보고 얼굴을 찡그렸다. 광고 사이를 뒤져 소문으로 들은 새 치약을 찾았으나 헛수고였고, 파리의 주요 신문 세 부를 하나씩 차례로 내던지며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"이 신문들, 날이 갈수록 점점 더 멍청해지는군.\"\n\n잠시 후 마차 한 대가 문 앞에 멈추었고, 하인이 뤼시앵 드브레 씨가 왔음을 알렸다. 옅은 머리칼에 맑은 잿빛 눈, 얇고 꼭 다문 입술을 한 키 큰 젊은이가 들어왔다. 아름답게 새긴 금단추가 달린 푸른 코트에 흰 넥타이를 매고, 비단실에 매단 거북등딱지 안경을 눈썹과 광대 근육의 힘을 빌려 눈에 끼운 채였다. 그는 반쯤 공무를 보는 듯한 태도로, 웃지도 말하지도 않고 들어섰다.\n\n\"안녕한가, 뤼시앵, 안녕.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"자네의 시간 엄수에 정말 깜짝 놀랐네. 무슨 소릴 하는 거지? 시간 엄수라니! 가장 늦게 올 줄 알았던 자네가, 정해진 시각이 10시 반인데 10시 5분 전에 도착하다니! 내각이 사퇴라도 했나?\"\n\n\"아닐세, 친구.\" 젊은이가 긴 의자에 앉으며 대답했다. \"안심하게. 우리는 늘 휘청거리지만 결코 쓰러지지는 않으니까. 나는 이제 우리가 부동의 상태로 접어들 것이고, 그러면 이베리아반도 문제가 우리를 완전히 굳건히 다져 줄 거라고 믿기 시작했네.\"\n\n\"아, 맞아. 자네들이 돈 카를로스를 에스파냐에서 쫓아내는군.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니, 친구, 우리 계획을 헷갈리지 말게. 우리는 그를 프랑스 국경 너머로 데려가, 부르주에서 환대를 베풀 거라네.\"\n\n\"부르주에서?\"\n\n\"그래, 그는 불평할 게 별로 없어. 부르주는 샤를 7세의 수도였으니까. 어제 파리 전체가 그 소식을 알았고, 그 전날 이미 증권가에 새어 나갔으며, 당글라르 씨가(그자가 무슨 수로 우리만큼이나 빨리 정보를 손에 넣는지는 모르겠네만) 백만을 벌었다는 걸 자네는 모르나?\"\n\n\"그리고 자네는 훈장 하나를 더 받았고. 단춧구멍에 푸른 리본을 단 게 보이니 말일세.\"\n\n\"그래, 샤를 3세 훈장을 보내왔더군.\" 드브레가 무심하게 대답했다.\n\n\"이봐, 무관심한 척하지 말고, 받아서 기뻤다고 털어놓게.\"\n\n\"오, 차림새의 마무리로는 아주 그만이지. 단추를 채운 검은 코트에 무척 단정하게 어울리거든.\"\n\n\"그리고 자네를 웨일스 공이나 라이히슈타트 공작처럼 보이게 해 주고.\"\n\n\"내가 이렇게 일찍 온 게 바로 그 때문일세.\"\n\n\"샤를 3세 훈장을 받았으니, 내게 그 기쁜 소식을 알리고 싶어서?\"\n\n\"아니, 밤새도록 편지를 쓰느라 그랬네. 전보를 스물다섯 통이나 보냈지. 동틀 녘에 집에 돌아와 자려 했지만 머리가 지끈거려서, 일어나 한 시간쯤 말을 타러 나갔네. 불로뉴 숲에서 권태와 허기가 한꺼번에 나를 덮쳤지. 좀처럼 함께 다니지 않는 두 적이건만, 나를 상대로 손을 잡았으니, 일종의 카를로스파와 공화파의 동맹이라고나 할까. 그러다 자네가 오늘 아침 식사를 차린다는 게 떠올라 이렇게 온 걸세. 나는 배고프니 먹여 주게. 나는 따분하니 즐겁게 해 주고.\"\n\n\"주인 된 도리지.\" 알베르가 종을 울리며 대답했다. 그동안 뤼시앵은 금 손잡이 단장으로 탁자 위에 놓인 신문들을 뒤적였다. \"제르맹, 셰리 한 잔과 비스킷 좀 가져오너라. 그동안, 친애하는 뤼시앵, 여기 시가가 있네. 물론 밀수품이지. 한번 피워 보고, 우리를 양배추 잎으로 중독시키는 대신 이런 걸 팔도록 장관을 설득해 보게.\"\n\n\"맙소사! 그런 짓은 절대 안 할 걸세. 정부에서 나오는 순간 자네는 그것들이 형편없다고 느낄 테니까. 게다가 그건 내무부가 아니라 재무부 소관일세. 위만 씨에게 가서 말하게. 간접세 부서, A 복도 26호일세.\"\n\n\"이런,\" 알베르가 말했다. \"자네 박식함에 놀랐네. 시가나 한 대 들게.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 친애하는 알베르,\" 뤼시앵이 아름다운 법랑 받침대에서 타오르는 장밋빛 양초로 마닐라 시가에 불을 붙이며 대답했다. \"할 일이 없다는 게 자네는 얼마나 행복한지 모를 걸세. 자네는 제 행운을 모르고 있어!\"\n\n\"그럼 자네는 어쩔 텐가, 친애하는 외교관 양반,\" 모르세르가 목소리에 약간의 비꼼을 담아 대꾸했다. \"할 일이 없다면 말일세. 뭐라고? 장관의 개인 비서로 유럽의 음모와 파리의 술책에 단번에 뛰어들고, 왕들을, 아니 그보다 더한 왕비들을 비호하고, 당파를 결속시키고, 선거를 지휘하고, 펜과 전보로 집무실에서 부려 먹는 것이 나폴레옹이 검과 승리로 전장에서 부린 것보다 더하며, 봉급 말고도 연 2만 5천 프랑을 가졌고, 샤토르노가 4백 루이를 주겠다고 했어도 내놓지 않은 말을 가졌고, 자네를 결코 실망시키지 않는 재단사를 두었고, 오페라와 자키 클럽과 그 밖의 오락거리까지 있는데, 자네는 즐길 줄을 모른단 말인가? 좋아, 내가 즐겁게 해 주지.\"\n\n\"어떻게?\"\n\n\"새로운 사람을 한 명 소개해 줌으로써 말이야.\"\n\n\"남자인가, 여자인가?\"\n\n\"남자일세.\"\n\n\"남자라면 이미 너무 많이 알고 있는데.\"\n\n\"하지만 이 사람은 모를 걸세.\"\n\n\"그자는 어디서 왔나? 세상 끝에서?\"\n\n\"어쩌면 그보다 더 먼 곳에서.\"\n\n\"이런! 그가 우리 아침거리를 함께 가져오지는 않았으면 좋겠군.\"\n\n\"오, 아닐세. 우리 아침은 아버지 댁 주방에서 오니까. 시장한가?\"\n\n\"이런 고백을 하자니 부끄럽네만, 그래. 어제 빌포르 씨 댁에서 저녁을 먹었는데, 법조인들은 늘 형편없는 만찬을 차리거든. 마치 무슨 가책이라도 느끼는가 싶어. 자네도 그런 걸 눈치챈 적 있나?\"\n\n\"아, 남의 만찬을 깎아내리는군. 자네들 장관 양반들은 그토록 훌륭한 걸 차린다 이거지.\"\n\n\"그래, 하지만 우리는 멋쟁이들을 초대하지는 않네. 시골뜨기 한 무리가 우리와 같은 생각으로 우리에게 표를 준다는 이유로 그들을 대접해야 하는 처지만 아니라면, 집에서 만찬을 차릴 생각은 꿈에도 안 할 걸세, 정말이야.\"\n\n\"자, 셰리 한 잔 더, 비스킷도 하나 더 들게.\"\n\n\"기꺼이. 자네의 에스파냐 포도주는 일품이군. 보게, 우리가 그 나라를 평정한 게 아주 옳은 일이었다니까.\"\n\n\"그래, 그런데 돈 카를로스는?\"\n\n\"흠, 돈 카를로스는 보르도 술을 마실 테고, 십 년 뒤에는 그의 아들을 어린 왕비와 결혼시킬 걸세.\"\n\n\"그러면 자네가 아직 내각에 있다면 황금양모 훈장을 받겠군.\"\n\n\"알베르, 자네 오늘 아침 나를 연기로 배 채워 주기로 작정한 모양이군.\"\n\n\"뭐, 그게 위장에는 제일 좋은 거라고 인정하게. 그건 그렇고, 옆방에서 보샹 목소리가 들리는군. 자네들끼리 논쟁이나 하게. 그러면 시간이 잘 갈 테니.\"\n\n\"무엇에 대해서?\"\n\n\"신문에 대해서지.\"\n\n\"이보게,\" 뤼시앵이 더없이 도도한 경멸의 기색으로 말했다. \"내가 신문 따위를 읽을 성싶은가?\"\n\n\"그렇다면 더더욱 논쟁이 붙겠군.\"\n\n\"보샹 씨 오셨습니다.\" 하인이 알렸다. \"들어오게, 들어와.\" 알베르가 일어나 젊은이를 맞으러 나아가며 말했다. \"여기 드브레가 있네. 읽지도 않으면서 자네를 몹시 싫어한다는군. 본인 말로는 말이야.\"\n\n\"지당한 말일세.\" 보샹이 대답했다. \"나도 그가 무슨 일을 하는지 모르면서 그를 비판하니까. 안녕하신가, 사령관!\"\n\n\"아, 자네 벌써 알았군.\" 개인 비서가 미소를 지으며 그와 악수했다.\n\n\"당연하지!\"\n\n\"그래, 세상에선 그걸 두고 뭐라던가?\"\n\n\"어느 세상 말인가? 1838년이라는 해에 우리에겐 세상이 어찌나 많은지.\"\n\n\"자네가 지도자 중 하나인 정계 전체 말일세.\"\n\n\"아주 공정한 일이라더군. 그렇게 붉은 것을 잔뜩 뿌렸으니, 푸른 것을 조금쯤 거둘 만하다고 말이야.\"\n\n\"이런, 이런, 제법인걸!\" 뤼시앵이 말했다. \"어째서 우리 당에 들어오지 않나, 친애하는 보샹? 자네의 재능이라면 삼사 년 안에 한밑천 잡을 텐데.\"\n\n\"자네 충고를 따르기 전에 딱 한 가지만 기다리고 있다네. 바로 여섯 달은 자리를 지킬 장관일세. 친애하는 알베르, 한마디만 하지. 가엾은 뤼시앵을 좀 쉬게 해 줘야겠으니. 우리 아침을 먹나, 저녁을 먹나? 나는 의회에 가 봐야 하네. 우리 삶이라는 게 한가하지가 않아서 말일세.\"\n\n\"자네는 아침만 들게. 두 사람을 더 기다리는 중인데, 그들이 도착하는 즉시 식탁에 앉을 걸세.\"\n\n# Chapter 40. The Breakfast\n\n\"그래, 어떤 사람들이 아침을 먹으러 온다는 건가?\" 보샹이 물었다.\n\n\"신사 한 사람과 외교관 한 사람일세.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 신사는 두 시간, 외교관은 세 시간을 기다려야겠군. 나는 디저트 시간에 다시 오겠네. 딸기와 커피, 시가를 좀 남겨 두게. 의회로 가는 길에 커틀릿이나 하나 먹고 가지.\"\n\n\"그러지 말게. 신사가 몽모랑시 가문 사람이고 외교관이 메테르니히라 해도, 우리는 열한 시에는 식사를 할 테니까. 그때까지는 드브레를 본받아서 셰리주 한 잔에 비스킷이나 들게나.\"\n\n\"좋아, 그럼 남겠네. 머릿속 생각을 떨쳐 낼 일이라도 좀 해야지.\"\n\n\"자네도 드브레와 똑같군. 그런데 내가 보기엔, 장관이 풀이 죽어 있을 때면 야당은 기뻐해야 마땅한 거 아닌가.\"\n\n\"아, 자네는 내가 어떤 협박에 시달리는지 모르는군. 오늘 아침에는 당글라르 씨가 하원에서 연설을 한다는 얘기를 들어야 하고, 저녁에는 그의 부인 댁에서 프랑스 상원 의원의 비극을 들어야 하네. 입헌 정부라는 게 악마한테나 잡혀가라지. 그리고 말마따나 우리가 직접 골랐다는데, 어떻게 그런 걸 고를 수 있었던 건지 원.\"\n\n\"무슨 말인지 알겠네. 자네는 웃을 거리를 잔뜩 비축해 둬야겠어.\"\n\n\"당글라르 씨의 연설을 깎아내리지 말게.\" 드브레가 말했다. \"그는 자네 편에 표를 던지지. 야당 소속이니까.\"\n\n\"제기랄, 그게 바로 무엇보다 고약한 점이라네. 마음 편히 웃으려면, 자네가 그를 뤽상부르 궁으로 보내 연설하게 만들 때까지 기다려야 한다는 게.\"\n\n\"이보게,\" 알베르가 보샹에게 말했다. \"에스파냐 문제가 정리됐다는 게 분명하군. 오늘 아침 자네 기분이 그렇게 험악한 걸 보면 말일세. 파리의 입방아꾼들이 나와 외제니 당글라르 양의 결혼 이야기를 떠들었다는 걸 잊지 말게. 그러니 양심상 나는 자네가 어느 날 내게 '자작, 자네도 알다시피 내 딸에게 2백만을 주겠네'라고 말할 사람의 연설을 깎아내리도록 내버려 둘 수가 없네.\"\n\n\"아, 그 결혼은 결코 성사되지 않을 거야.\" 보샹이 말했다. \"왕은 그를 남작으로 만들었고, 상원 의원으로도 만들 수 있겠지. 하지만 신사로 만들 수는 없네. 그리고 모르세르 백작은 너무 귀족적이라 고작 2백만 프랑이라는 하찮은 돈 때문에 격에 안 맞는 결혼에 동의하지 않을 걸세. 모르세르 자작은 후작 부인급하고나 결혼할 수 있어.\"\n\n\"하지만 2백만 프랑이면 제법 쏠쏠한 액수지.\" 모르세르가 대꾸했다.\n\n\"그건 큰길가에 극장 하나를 세울 자본금이거나, 식물원에서 라라페까지 철도를 놓을 돈이야.\"\n\n\"저 친구 말은 신경 쓰지 말게, 모르세르.\" 드브레가 말했다. \"그냥 그녀와 결혼하게. 돈주머니에 붙은 상표와 결혼하는 셈인 건 사실이지. 하지만 그게 무슨 상관인가? 문장(紋章) 하나가 줄고 그 위에 숫자 하나가 느는 게 더 낫지. 자네 가문 문장에는 제비가 일곱 마리 있잖나. 그중 셋을 아내에게 주게. 그래도 자네에겐 넷이 남아. 그건 기즈 공보다 하나 많은 거야. 그는 하마터면 프랑스 왕이 될 뻔했고, 그 사촌은 독일 황제였지.\"\n\n\"이런, 자네 말이 맞는 것 같군, 뤼시앵.\" 알베르가 멍하니 말했다.\n\n\"물론이지. 게다가 백만장자라면 누구나 사생아만큼이나 고귀하지. 그러니까, 고귀해질 수 있다는 말일세.\"\n\n\"그런 말은 하지 말게, 드브레.\" 보샹이 웃으며 대꾸했다. \"여기 샤토르노가 있으니, 자네의 그 역설 중독을 고쳐 주려고 제 조상 르노 드 몽토방의 검으로 자네 몸을 꿰뚫을 테니까.\"\n\n\"그러면 그 검을 더럽히는 셈이지.\" 뤼시앵이 대꾸했다. \"나는 비천한 몸이니까. 아주 비천하지.\"\n\n\"이런, 맙소사.\" 보샹이 외쳤다. \"장관이 베랑제를 인용하다니, 다음엔 또 무슨 일이 벌어질까?\"\n\n\"샤토르노 백작님, 그리고 막시밀리앙 모렐 대위님이십니다.\" 하인이 새 손님 두 사람을 알리며 말했다.\n\n\"자, 그럼 이제 아침을 들지.\" 보샹이 말했다. \"내 기억이 맞다면, 알베르, 자네는 손님이 둘뿐이라고 했잖나.\"\n\n\"모렐이라.\" 알베르가 중얼거렸다. \"모렐이라니, 그게 누구지?\"\n\n그러나 그가 말을 채 끝내기도 전에, 서른 살가량의 잘생긴 청년이자 머리끝부터 발끝까지 신사인 샤토르노가, 그러니까 기슈의 풍채에 모르트마르의 재치를 지닌 사내가 알베르의 손을 잡았다.\n\n\"친애하는 알베르,\" 그가 말했다. \"막시밀리앙 모렐 대위를 소개하지. 스파이 기병 대위이자 내 친구일세. 그리고 그 이상이지. 본인 입으로 직접 말하긴 뭣하지만, 내 생명의 은인이라네. 내 영웅에게 인사하게, 자작.\"\n\n그러고는 한쪽으로 비켜서며 한 청년에게 자리를 내주었다. 세련되고 위엄 있는 자태에, 넓고 트인 이마와 꿰뚫는 듯한 눈, 검은 콧수염을 지닌 사내였다. 우리 독자들은 마르세유에서, 잊을 수 없을 만큼 극적인 상황 속에서 이미 그를 본 적이 있다. 반은 프랑스풍, 반은 동방풍의 화려한 제복이 그의 우아하고 다부진 몸매를 돋보이게 했고, 떡 벌어진 가슴에는 레지옹 도뇌르 훈장이 달려 있었다. 젊은 장교는 편안하면서도 우아한 예의로 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"선생,\" 알베르가 다정한 정중함을 담아 말했다. \"샤토르노 백작은 이 소개가 제게 얼마나 큰 기쁨을 줄지 잘 알고 있었습니다. 당신은 그의 친구이니, 우리의 친구도 되어 주십시오.\"\n\n\"옳은 말이야.\" 샤토르노가 끼어들었다. \"그리고 혹시 자네가 언젠가 비슷한 곤경에 처한다면, 그가 내게 해 준 만큼 자네에게도 해 주기를 기도하게.\"\n\n\"그가 무슨 일을 했나?\" 알베르가 물었다.\n\n\"오, 입에 올릴 만한 일도 아닙니다.\" 모렐이 말했다. \"샤토르노 씨가 과장하는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"입에 올릴 만한 일이 아니라고?\" 샤토르노가 외쳤다. \"사람 목숨이 입에 올릴 만한 일이 아니라니! 정말이지 그건 너무 철학적인 소리야, 모렐. 날마다 목숨을 거는 자네한테야 괜찮은 말이겠지만, 평생에 단 한 번 그랬던 나로서는……\"\n\n\"이 모든 얘기로 미루어 보면, 남작, 모렐 대위가 자네 목숨을 구했다는 거군.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇다네.\"\n\n\"어떤 상황에서?\" 보샹이 물었다.\n\n\"보샹, 이 사람아, 내가 배가 고파 죽을 지경인 거 자네도 알잖나.\" 드브레가 말했다. \"긴 이야기를 늘어놓게 만들지 말게.\"\n\n\"음, 식탁에 앉는 걸 막진 않겠네.\" 보샹이 대꾸했다. \"샤토르노가 아침을 먹는 동안 이야기해 줄 수 있을 테니까.\"\n\n\"여러분,\" 모르세르가 말했다. \"이제 겨우 열 시 십오 분입니다. 한 사람을 더 기다리고 있어요.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇지, 외교관!\" 드브레가 한마디 했다.\n\n\"외교관인지 아닌지는 모르겠네. 다만 내가 아는 건, 그가 나를 위해 어떤 임무를 떠맡았고, 그걸 어찌나 만족스럽게 끝내 주었는지, 내가 왕이었다면 그 자리에서 내 모든 훈장의 기사로 임명했을 거라는 점이야. 황금양모 훈장이든 가터 훈장이든 줄 수만 있었다면 다 줬을 걸세.\"\n\n\"음, 어차피 식탁에 앉지 않을 거라면,\" 드브레가 말했다. \"셰리주나 한 잔 들면서 전부 얘기해 보게.\"\n\n\"내가 아프리카에 가고 싶다는 변덕이 들었다는 건 다들 알고 있겠지.\"\n\n\"그건 자네 조상들이 자네를 위해 닦아 놓은 길이지.\" 알베르가 정중하게 말했다.\n\n\"그런가? 하지만 자네 목적이 그들의 목적, 그러니까 성묘를 구하는 것과 같았는지는 의심스럽군.\"\n\n\"자네 말이 옳네, 보샹.\" 젊은 귀족이 한마디 했다. \"그저 아마추어로서 싸워 보려는 것뿐이었어. 나는 결투를 견딜 수가 없게 됐거든. 언젠가 어떤 일을 정리하려고 입회인 둘을 뽑았는데, 그들이 내게 내 가장 친한 친구 중 하나의 팔을 부러뜨리게 했지. 자네들도 다 아는 친구, 가엾은 프란츠 데피네 말일세.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇지.\" 드브레가 말했다. \"자네가 얼마 전에 결투를 했었지. 무슨 일로?\"\n\n\"빌어먹을, 기억이 나면 다행이게.\" 샤토르노가 대꾸했다. \"하지만 한 가지는 똑똑히 기억하네. 나 같은 재능을 썩혀 두기 싫어서, 누가 내게 선물한 새 권총들을 아랍인들에게 시험해 보고 싶었다는 거야. 그래서 오랑으로 떠나, 거기서 콩스탕틴으로 갔지. 마침 포위가 풀리는 걸 목격할 만한 때에 도착했어. 나는 다른 이들과 함께 마흔여덟 시간 동안 퇴각했네. 낮에는 비를, 밤에는 추위를 그럭저럭 견뎌 냈지만, 사흘째 아침에 내 말이 추위로 죽고 말았네. 가엾은 짐승 같으니. 늘 덮개를 두르고 마구간에 난로를 둔 채 지내던 아라비아산 말이라, 정작 아라비아에서 10도의 추위도 견디지 못한 거지.\"\n\n\"그래서 자네가 내 영국산 말을 사고 싶어 하는 거군.\" 드브레가 말했다. \"그놈은 추위를 더 잘 견디리라 생각하는 거야.\"\n\n\"오해일세. 나는 두 번 다시 아프리카에 가지 않기로 맹세했으니까.\"\n\n\"그럼 몹시 겁을 먹었던 모양이군?\" 보샹이 물었다.\n\n\"음, 그래. 그럴 만한 이유가 충분했지.\" 샤토르노가 대꾸했다. \"말이 죽어서 나는 걸어서 퇴각하고 있었네. 그때 아랍인 여섯 명이 내 목을 베려고 전속력으로 달려왔지. 쌍총신 총으로 둘을 쏘고, 권총으로 둘을 더 쐈네. 하지만 그러고 나니 무기가 다 떨어졌는데, 아직 둘이 남아 있었어. 하나는 내 머리채를 움켜잡았고—그래서 지금 내가 머리를 이렇게 짧게 깎고 다니는 거야, 무슨 일이 생길지 모르니까—다른 하나는 언월도를 휘둘렀지. 차가운 칼날이 이미 내 목에 느껴지던 그 순간, 여기 보이는 이분이 그들에게 돌격해, 내 머리채를 잡은 자를 쏘아 죽이고, 다른 자의 두개골을 검으로 쪼개 버렸네. 그분은 그날 한 사람의 목숨을 구하기로 스스로 정해 두었던 거야. 우연히 그 사람이 바로 나였던 거지. 내가 부자가 되면 클라그만이나 마로케티한테 '우연'의 동상을 주문할 걸세.\"\n\n\"네.\" 모렐이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그날은 9월 5일, 제 아버지가 기적처럼 목숨을 구하신 날의 기념일이었습니다. 그래서 저는 제 힘이 닿는 한, 그날을 어떤……으로 기리려 애쓰지요.\"\n\n\"영웅적인 행동으로.\" 샤토르노가 말을 가로챘다. \"그래서 내가 선택받은 거야. 하지만 그게 다가 아니라네. 검에서 나를 구해 준 뒤, 그는 추위에서도 나를 구해 줬어. 성 마르탱처럼 외투를 반으로 나눠 준 게 아니라, 통째로 줬지. 그다음엔 굶주림에서 구해 줬는데, 그러니까 나와 함께 나눠 먹은 게—무엇이었을 것 같나?\"\n\n\"스트라스부르 파이?\" 보샹이 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 자기 말이었지. 우리 둘이서 한 점씩 아주 맛있게 먹었어. 굉장히 질겼지만.\"\n\n\"말이?\" 모르세르가 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 희생이.\" 샤토르노가 대꾸했다. \"드브레한테 물어보게. 낯선 사람을 위해 자기 영국산 말을 희생할 수 있겠는지?\"\n\n\"낯선 사람을 위해서는 안 되지.\" 드브레가 말했다. \"하지만 친구를 위해서라면, 어쩌면 그럴지도.\"\n\n\"저는 당신이 제 친구가 되리라는 걸 짐작했습니다, 백작님.\" 모렐이 대꾸했다. \"게다가, 영광스럽게도 말씀드린 대로, 영웅적이든 아니든, 희생이든 아니든, 그날 저는 다른 날들에 행운이 베풀어 준 호의에 대한 보답으로 불운에 제물 하나를 바쳐야 할 빚이 있었던 겁니다.\"\n\n\"모렐 씨가 넌지시 말하는 사연은,\" 샤토르노가 말을 이었다. \"참으로 감탄할 만한 이야기로, 여러분이 그와 좀 더 가까워지면 언젠가 그가 들려줄 겁니다. 오늘은 기억이 아니라 배를 채웁시다. 알베르, 자네는 몇 시에 아침을 드나?\"\n\n\"열 시 반에.\"\n\n\"정확히?\" 드브레가 시계를 꺼내며 물었다.\n\n\"오, 자네는 내게 5분쯤 너그러이 봐줄 거잖나.\" 모르세르가 대꾸했다. \"나 역시 생명의 은인 한 사람을 기다리고 있거든.\"\n\n\"누구의?\"\n\n\"내 생명의 은인이지.\" 모르세르가 외쳤다. \"제기랄! 자네들은 내가 남들처럼 목숨을 구원받을 수 없다고 생각하나? 목을 베는 게 아랍인들밖에 없다고 여기는 건가? 우리의 아침 식사는 박애주의적인 자리야. 식탁에—적어도 그러길 바라는데—인류의 은인 두 사람을 모시게 될 거라네.\"\n\n\"그럼 우린 어쩌지?\" 드브레가 말했다. \"몽티옹 상은 하나뿐인데.\"\n\n\"음, 그건 그걸 받을 만한 일을 전혀 하지 않은 누군가에게 돌아가겠지.\" 보샹이 말했다. \"아카데미가 그 딜레마에서 벗어나는 방식이 대개 그렇잖나.\"\n\n\"그래서 그는 어디서 오는 건가?\" 드브레가 물었다. \"이미 한 번 답했지만 어찌나 막연하던지, 한 번 더 물어보려는 거야.\"\n\n\"솔직히,\" 알베르가 말했다. \"나도 모르네. 석 달 전에 그를 초대했을 때는 로마에 있었지. 하지만 그 뒤로 어디로 갔는지 누가 알겠나?\"\n\n\"그가 시간을 정확히 지킬 만한 사람이라고 생각하나?\" 드브레가 따져 물었다.\n\n\"나는 그가 무슨 일이든 할 수 있는 사람이라고 생각하네.\"\n\n\"음, 5분 너그러이 봐주기로 한 시간 중에 이제 10분밖에 안 남았군.\"\n\n\"그 시간을 이용해서 내 손님 이야기를 좀 해 주지.\"\n\n\"미안하지만 묻겠네.\" 보샹이 끼어들었다. \"지금 자네가 들려줄 이야기 속에 기삿거리가 될 만한 재료가 있나?\"\n\n\"그래, 그것도 아주 진기한 기삿거리지.\"\n\n\"그럼 어서 말해 보게. 오늘 아침엔 의회에 못 갈 것 같으니, 그걸 벌충해야겠어.\"\n\n\"나는 지난 사육제 동안 로마에 있었네.\"\n\n\"그건 우리도 알아.\" 보샹이 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 하지만 자네들이 모르는 건, 내가 산적들에게 납치당했다는 거야.\"\n\n\"산적이 어디 있나.\" 드브레가 외쳤다.\n\n\"있다네. 그것도 아주 흉측한, 아니, 아주 감탄스러운 자들이지. 나는 그들이 나를 겁먹게 할 만큼 흉측하다는 걸 알았으니까.\"\n\n\"이보게, 친애하는 알베르.\" 드브레가 말했다. \"솔직히 인정하게. 자네 요리사가 일이 늦었고, 굴이 오스텐데나 마렌에서 도착하지 않았고, 그래서 맹트농 부인처럼 요리 대신 이야기로 때우려는 거라고. 어서 그렇게 말하게. 우리는 충분히 교양 있는 사람들이니 자네를 너그러이 봐주고, 황당무계할 게 뻔한 자네 이야기를 들어 주겠네.\"\n\n\"그리고 자네에게 말하는데, 황당무계해 보일지언정, 나는 처음부터 끝까지 진실로서 이야기하는 거야. 도적들이 나를 납치해, 성 세바스티아노 카타콤이라는 음침한 곳으로 끌고 갔네.\"\n\n\"나도 거기를 아네.\" 샤토르노가 말했다. \"거기서 하마터면 열병에 걸릴 뻔했지.\"\n\n\"나는 그보다 더했네.\" 모르세르가 대꾸했다. \"정말로 열병에 걸렸으니까. 나는 로마 크라운으로 4천 닢, 그러니까 2만 4천 프랑쯤 되는 돈을 낼 때까지 갇혀 있어야 한다는 통고를 받았어. 안타깝게도 내겐 1천 5백 닢밖에 없었네. 여행도 끝물이었고 신용도 바닥이었지. 그래서 프란츠에게 편지를 썼네—그가 여기 있다면 한마디 한마디 다 확인해 줄 거야—프란츠에게 편지를 써서, 만약 여섯 시 전에 4천 크라운을 가지고 오지 않으면, 십 분 후에는 내가 영광스럽게도 함께 지내고 있던 그 복된 성인들과 영광스러운 순교자들의 무리에 끼게 될 거라고 적었지. 그리고 루이지 밤파라는 자가—그게 이 도적들 두목의 이름이었네—틀림없이 약속을 지켰을 걸세.\"\n\n\"하지만 프란츠가 4천 크라운을 가지고 오긴 했군.\" 샤토르노가 말했다. \"프란츠 데피네나 알베르 드 모르세르 같은 이름을 가진 사람이라면 그 정도 마련하는 게 그리 어렵지 않을 테니까.\"\n\n\"아니, 그는 그저 내가 지금 자네들에게 소개하려는 손님만 데리고 왔네.\"\n\n\"아, 그럼 이분은 카쿠스를 죽인 헤라클레스, 안드로메다를 구한 페르세우스 같은 분이군.\"\n\n\"아니, 나하고 키가 엇비슷한 사람이야.\"\n\n\"이로 무장하고 있었나?\"\n\n\"뜨개바늘 하나도 없었네.\"\n\n\"하지만 자네 몸값을 치렀잖나?\"\n\n\"그는 두목에게 두 마디를 했고, 그러자 나는 풀려났네.\"\n\n\"그리고 그들이 자네를 납치한 것에 대해 그에게 사과까지 했나?\" 보샹이 물었다.\n\n\"바로 그랬네.\"\n\n\"이런, 그는 제2의 아리오스토로군.\"\n\n\"아니, 그의 이름은 몬테크리스토 백작이야.\"\n\n\"몬테크리스토 백작이라는 사람은 없네.\" 드브레가 말했다.\n\n\"나도 그렇게 생각하네.\" 유럽 귀족 전체를 완벽하게 꿰뚫고 있는 사람 같은 태도로 샤토르노가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"누구든 몬테크리스토 백작에 대해 뭔가 아는 사람 있나?\"\n\n\"어쩌면 성지에서 온 사람일지도 모르지. 모르트마르 가문이 사해를 소유했듯, 그의 조상 중 하나가 갈보리를 소유했을 수도 있고.\"\n\n\"내가 자네들 조사에 도움이 될 것 같네.\" 막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"몬테크리스토는 작은 섬으로, 아버지가 고용했던 늙은 선원들에게서 여러 번 들었습니다. 지중해 한복판의 모래알 하나, 무한 속의 티끌 하나지요.\"\n\n\"바로 그거야!\" 알베르가 외쳤다. \"음, 내가 말하는 그 사람은 바로 그 모래알, 그 티끌의 영주이자 주인이라네. 그는 토스카나 어딘가에서 백작 작위를 사들였지.\"\n\n\"그럼 부자인가?\"\n\n\"그렇다고 보네.\"\n\n\"하지만 그건 눈에 보여야 하지 않나.\"\n\n\"바로 그 점이 자네를 속이는 걸세, 드브레.\"\n\n\"무슨 말인지 모르겠군.\"\n\n\"자네 『아라비안나이트』를 읽어 봤나?\"\n\n\"별걸 다 묻는군!\"\n\n\"음, 거기 나오는 사람들이 부자인지 가난한지 자네가 알겠나? 그들의 밀자루가 루비나 다이아몬드가 아니라고 어떻게 알겠어? 그들은 가난한 어부처럼 보이다가, 갑자기 인도의 부가 가득한 어떤 신비로운 동굴을 열어젖히지.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 뜻인가?\"\n\n\"내 몬테크리스토 백작이 바로 그런 어부 중 하나라는 뜻이야. 심지어 그는 그 책에서 따온 이름까지 갖고 있어. 자기를 뱃사람 신드바드라 부르고, 황금으로 가득한 동굴을 가지고 있거든.\"\n\n\"그래서 자네가 그 동굴을 봤나, 모르세르?\" 보샹이 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 하지만 프란츠는 봤네. 제발 부탁이니, 그 앞에서는 이 얘기를 한마디도 하지 말게. 프란츠는 눈을 가린 채 들어갔고, 벙어리들과, 클레오파트라쯤은 칠해 놓은 매춘부로 보일 만큼 아름다운 여인들의 시중을 받았지. 다만 그 여인들에 대해서는 그도 확신하지 못해. 그가 해시시를 복용한 후에야 들어왔거든. 그래서 그가 여인들이라 여긴 것이 실은 한 줄로 늘어선 조각상들이었을 수도 있다는 거야.\"\n\n두 젊은이는 마치 \"자네 미친 건가, 아니면 우리를 놀리는 건가?\"라고 묻는 듯한 눈으로 모르세르를 바라보았다.\n\n\"저 역시,\" 모렐이 생각에 잠긴 채 말했다. \"이와 비슷한 이야기를 페늘롱이라는 늙은 선원에게서 들은 적이 있습니다.\"\n\n\"아!\" 알베르가 외쳤다. \"모렐 씨가 나를 도우러 와 주다니 정말 다행이군. 그가 이렇게 미궁의 실마리를 던져 주니 자네들 속이 상하지, 안 그래?\"\n\n\"이보게 알베르,\" 드브레가 말했다. \"자네가 들려주는 이야기는 너무나 터무니없어.\"\n\n\"아, 그건 자네네 대사들과 영사들이 자네들에게 그런 얘기를 안 해 줘서야. 그들은 그럴 시간이 없거든. 여행 다니는 제 나라 사람들 일에 간섭하느라 너무 바쁘니까.\"\n\n\"이제 자네가 화를 내며 우리 가엾은 관리들을 공격하는군. 그들이 어떻게 자네를 지켜 줄 수 있겠나? 의회가 날마다 그들의 봉급을 깎아 대니, 지금은 거의 한 푼도 못 받을 지경이야. 자네가 대사가 되겠나, 알베르? 내가 자네를 콘스탄티노플로 보내 주지.\"\n\n\"싫네. 메헤메트 알리를 옹호하는 시위를 처음 벌이는 순간, 술탄이 내게 활시위를 보내 내 비서들 손에 나를 목 졸라 죽이게 할 테니까.\"\n\n\"지당한 말이군.\" 드브레가 응수했다.\n\n\"그래.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"하지만 이건 몬테크리스토 백작의 존재와는 아무 상관이 없어.\"\n\n\"제기랄! 누구나 다 존재하잖나.\"\n\n\"틀림없이 그렇지. 하지만 같은 방식으로는 아니야. 누구나 다 흑인 노예들과 군주 같은 수행단을 거느리고, 아라비아 요새도 부끄럽지 않을 무기고를 갖추고, 한 마리에 6천 프랑짜리 말들과 그리스인 정부를 두지는 않으니까.\"\n\n\"자네 그 그리스인 정부를 본 적 있나?\"\n\n\"보기도 했고 목소리도 들었네. 극장에서 그녀를 봤고, 백작과 아침을 먹던 어느 날 아침에 목소리를 들었지.\"\n\n\"그래. 하지만 어찌나 조금 먹는지, 먹는다고 하기도 어려울 지경이야.\"\n\n\"그자는 흡혈귀가 틀림없군.\"\n\n\"웃고 싶으면 웃게. 러스벤 경을 알았던 G 백작 부인이, 그 백작은 흡혈귀라고 단언했으니까.\"\n\n\"아, 멋지군.\" 보샹이 말했다. \"신문 일과 무관한 사람이 했다기엔, 이건 『콩스티튀시오넬』지의 그 유명한 바다뱀에 견줄 만한 짝패로군.\"\n\n\"마음대로 수축하고 팽창하는 홍채를 가진 사나운 눈.\" 드브레가 말했다. \"강하게 발달한 안면각, 멋들어진 이마, 납빛 안색, 검은 수염, 날카롭고 새하얀 이, 흠잡을 데 없는 정중함.\"\n\n\"바로 그래, 뤼시앵.\" 모르세르가 대꾸했다. \"자네가 그를 이목구비 하나하나 그대로 묘사했어. 그래, 날카롭고 베일 듯한 정중함. 그 사람 때문에 나는 종종 몸서리를 쳤네. 그리고 우리가 함께 사형 집행을 구경하던 어느 날엔, 나는 까무러칠 것만 같았어. 사형 집행인과 죄인의 모습보다도, 그가 온갖 종류의 고문에 대해 이야기하던 그 차갑고 침착한 말투를 듣고 말이야.\"\n\n\"그가 자네를 콜로세움 폐허로 데려가 자네 피를 빨아먹지는 않았나?\" 보샹이 물었다.\n\n\"아니면 자네를 풀어 준 뒤, 에서가 장자권을 넘기듯 자네 영혼을 그에게 넘긴다는 불타는 양피지에 서명하게 만들지는 않았고?\"\n\n\"마음껏 비웃게, 여러분.\" 모르세르가 다소 발끈해서 말했다. \"강 거리나 불로뉴 숲을 어슬렁대는 자네들 파리지앵을 보고 이 사람을 떠올리면, 우리가 도무지 같은 종족이 아닌 것 같단 말이야.\"\n\n\"대단히 영광이군.\" 보샹이 응수했다.\n\n\"그렇다 해도,\" 샤토르노가 덧붙였다. \"자네의 그 몬테크리스토 백작은 아주 훌륭한 친구야. 이탈리아 산적들과의 그 사소한 거래만 빼면 말이지.\"\n\n\"이탈리아 산적은 없네.\" 드브레가 말했다.\n\n\"흡혈귀도 없고.\" 보샹이 외쳤다.\n\n\"몬테크리스토 백작도 없지.\" 드브레가 덧붙였다. \"열 시 반을 치는군, 알베르.\"\n\n\"이건 자네가 꿈에서 본 거라고 인정하고, 어서 아침 식탁에 앉자고.\" 보샹이 말을 이었다.\n\n그러나 시계 소리가 채 잦아들기도 전에 제르맹이 알렸다. \"몬테크리스토 백작 각하이십니다.\" 다들 저도 모르게 흠칫하는 모습이, 모르세르의 이야기가 그들에게 얼마나 깊은 인상을 남겼는지를 증명했다. 알베르 자신도 갑작스러운 동요를 완전히 감추지는 못했다. 그는 거리에서 마차가 멈추는 소리도, 대기실에서 발걸음 소리도 듣지 못했다. 문은 소리 없이 저절로 열렸다. 백작이 나타났는데, 더할 나위 없이 소박한 차림이었지만, 아무리 까다로운 멋쟁이라도 그의 옷차림에서 트집 잡을 거리를 찾지 못했을 것이다. 모자, 외투, 장갑, 부츠 등 의복의 모든 품목이 일류 제작자의 솜씨였다. 그는 서른다섯 살이 채 안 되어 보였다. 그러나 모두를 놀라게 한 것은, 그가 드브레가 묘사한 초상과 극도로 닮았다는 점이었다. 백작은 미소를 지으며 방 한가운데로 나아가 알베르에게 다가갔고, 알베르는 격식을 갖춘 태도로 손을 내밀며 그에게 서둘러 다가갔다.\n\n\"시간을 지키는 것은,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"내가 알기로 당신네 어느 군주가 말했듯, 왕의 예의라지요. 하지만 여행자에게는 사정이 다릅니다. 그래도 두세 초 늦은 것을 양해해 주시리라 믿습니다. 5백 리외를 별 탈 없이 주파한다는 건 어려운 일이거든요. 더구나 프랑스에서는, 듣자 하니 역마차 마부를 채찍질하는 게 금지되어 있다더군요.\"\n\n\"친애하는 백작님,\" 알베르가 대답했다. \"마침 친구 몇 사람에게 당신의 방문을 알리던 참이었습니다. 영광스럽게도 당신이 제게 해 주신 약속에 따라 초대한 이들이지요. 이제 그들을 소개하겠습니다. 이쪽은 샤토르노 백작으로, 그의 가문은 열두 중신(重臣)까지 거슬러 올라가고, 그 조상들은 원탁에 자리를 차지했습니다. 이쪽은 뤼시앵 드브레 씨로, 내무부 장관의 개인 비서지요. 이쪽은 보샹 씨로, 신문 편집인이자 프랑스 정부의 공포의 대상입니다. 다만 국내에서는 그토록 유명한데도, 당신은 이탈리아에서는 그에 대해 못 들으셨을 겁니다. 그의 신문은 거기서 금지되어 있으니까요. 그리고 이쪽은 막시밀리앙 모렐 씨로, 스파이 기병 대위입니다.\"\n\n이 이름이 나오자, 그때까지 모두에게 정중하면서도 차갑고 격식 차린 태도로 인사하던 백작이 한 발짝 앞으로 나섰고, 그의 창백한 뺨에 옅은 홍조가 스쳤다.\n\n\"당신은 새로운 프랑스 정복자들의 제복을 입고 계시는군요.\" 그가 말했다. \"멋진 제복입니다.\"\n\n무엇이 백작의 목소리를 그토록 깊이 떨리게 했는지, 또 무엇이, 그가 원하기만 하면 대개 그토록 맑고 빛나며 투명한 그의 눈을 그렇게 번득이게 했는지는 아무도 알 수 없었다.\n\n\"백작님은 저희 아프리카 부대원을 본 적이 없으십니까?\" 알베르가 물었다.\n\n\"한 번도 없습니다.\" 이때쯤에는 다시 완벽하게 평정을 되찾은 백작이 대답했다.\n\n\"음, 이 제복 아래에는 군 전체에서 가장 용감하고 가장 고귀한 심장 중 하나가 뛰고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"오, 모르세르 씨.\" 모렐이 끼어들었다.\n\n\"말을 마저 하게 해 주십시오, 대위.\" 알베르가 말을 이었다. \"그리고 우리는 방금, 그의 새로운 무용담을 들었습니다. 어찌나 영웅적인지, 비록 오늘 처음 그를 만났지만, 저는 그를 제 친구로 소개하도록 허락해 주시길 청합니다.\"\n\n이 말에, 몬테크리스토에게서는 여전히 응축된 시선과 변하는 안색, 감정을 드러내는 눈꺼풀의 가벼운 떨림을 관찰할 수 있었다.\n\n\"아, 당신은 고귀한 마음을 가지셨군요.\" 백작이 말했다. \"그렇다면 더욱 좋습니다.\"\n\n알베르가 한 말보다는 백작 자신의 생각에 들어맞는 이 외침이 모두를, 특히 모렐을 놀라게 했고, 그는 경이로운 눈으로 몬테크리스토를 바라보았다. 그러나 동시에 그 억양이 어찌나 부드러웠던지, 그 말이 아무리 이상하게 들릴지라도 거기에 기분이 상한다는 것은 불가능했다.\n\n\"그가 왜 그걸 의심하겠나?\" 보샹이 샤토르노에게 말했다.\n\n\"사실,\" 후자가 대꾸했다. 그는 귀족다운 안목과 세상에 대한 식견으로 몬테크리스토에게서 간파할 수 있는 모든 것을 단번에 꿰뚫어 본 터였다. \"알베르가 우리를 속인 게 아니었어. 백작은 참으로 독특한 존재로군. 자네 생각은 어떤가, 모렐!\"\n\n\"솔직히, 저에 대해 그런 독특한 말을 하긴 했지만, 그분에게는 마음에 드는 솔직한 구석이 있습니다.\"\n\n\"여러분,\" 알베르가 말했다. \"제르맹이 아침 식사가 준비됐다고 알리는군요. 친애하는 백작님, 길을 안내하도록 허락해 주십시오.\" 그들은 말없이 식당으로 들어가, 저마다 자리에 앉았다.\n\n\"여러분,\" 백작이 자리에 앉으며 말했다. \"제가 저지를지도 모를 어떤 결례에 대한 변명이 될 고백 하나를 하도록 허락해 주십시오. 저는 이방인입니다. 그것도 어찌나 낯선 이방인인지, 파리에 와 본 것이 이번이 처음입니다. 프랑스식 생활 방식을 저는 전혀 모르고, 지금까지는 줄곧 동방의 관습을 따라왔습니다. 그건 파리의 관습과는 완전히 상반되지요. 그러니 제게서 너무 튀르크적이거나, 너무 이탈리아적이거나, 너무 아라비아적인 면을 발견하시더라도 너그러이 봐주시길 청합니다. 자, 그럼, 아침을 듭시다.\"\n\n\"이 모든 말을 어찌나 멋진 태도로 하는지.\" 보샹이 중얼거렸다. \"확실히 대단한 인물이야.\"\n\n\"제 나라에서나 대단한 인물이겠지.\" 드브레가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"어느 나라에서든 대단한 인물입니다, 드브레 씨.\" 샤토르노가 말했다.\n\n기억하다시피 백작은 더없이 소식하는 손님이었다. 알베르는 이를 알아채고는, 처음부터 파리의 생활 방식이 가장 본질적인 점에서 이 여행자의 마음에 들지 않을까 봐 걱정스럽다고 말했다.\n\n\"친애하는 백작님,\" 그가 말했다. \"한 가지 두려운 게 있습니다. 엘데르 거리의 음식이 에스파냐 광장의 음식만큼 입맛에 맞지 않을까 봐서요. 그 점에 대해 미리 여쭤보고, 특별히 몇 가지 요리를 준비했어야 했는데 말입니다.\"\n\n\"저를 좀 더 잘 아신다면,\" 백작이 미소 지으며 대꾸했다. \"저 같은 여행자에 대해서는 그런 걸 조금도 신경 쓰지 않으실 겁니다. 저는 나폴리에서 마카로니를, 밀라노에서 폴렌타를, 발렌시아에서 올라 포드리다를, 콘스탄티노플에서 필라프를, 인도에서 카레를, 중국에서 제비집을 차례로 먹으며 살아온 사람이니까요. 저는 어디서든, 무엇이든 먹습니다. 다만 아주 조금 먹을 뿐이지요. 그리고 당신이 제 입맛 없는 걸 나무라는 오늘이, 사실은 제 식욕이 도는 날입니다. 어제 아침부터 아무것도 먹지 않았거든요.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요!\" 손님들이 일제히 외쳤다. \"스물네 시간 동안 아무것도 안 드셨다고요?\"\n\n\"네.\" 백작이 대답했다. \"님 근처에서 무슨 정보를 얻느라 길을 벗어나야 했고, 그래서 좀 늦은 탓에 멈추지 않기로 한 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 마차 안에서 드신 겁니까?\" 모르세르가 물었다.\n\n\"아니요, 잤습니다. 굳이 즐길 마음이 없는데 지칠 때나, 먹고 싶은 마음이 없는데 배가 고플 때면 대개 그러지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 원하실 때 잠을 잘 수 있으십니까, 선생?\" 모렐이 물었다.\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"무슨 비법이라도 있으신가요?\"\n\n\"실패하는 법이 없는 비법이지요.\"\n\n\"그건 우리 아프리카 사람들에게 더없이 값진 것이겠군요. 우리는 늘 먹을 게 있는 것도 아니고, 마실 건 좀처럼 없거든요.\"\n\n\"네.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"하지만 안타깝게도, 저 같은 사람에게는 훌륭한 비법이 군대에 적용되면 매우 위험할 겁니다. 필요할 때 깨어나지 못할 수도 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"그 비법이 무엇인지 여쭤봐도 되겠습니까?\" 드브레가 물었다.\n\n\"오, 물론이지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 대꾸했다. \"비밀로 할 것도 없습니다. 그건 순수하게 얻으려고 제가 직접 광둥에서 가져온 최상급 아편과, 동방에서 자라는 최고급 해시시, 그러니까 티그리스강과 유프라테스강 사이에서 나는 것을 섞은 겁니다. 이 두 재료를 같은 비율로 섞어 알약으로 빚지요. 하나를 복용하고 십 분이 지나면 효과가 나타납니다. 프란츠 데피네 남작에게 물어보십시오. 언젠가 그가 맛본 것 같으니까요.\"\n\n\"네.\" 모르세르가 대꾸했다. \"그가 제게 그 얘기를 했었지요.\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 언론인답게 몹시 의심이 많은 보샹이 말했다. \"그 약을 늘 몸에 지니고 다니십니까?\"\n\"늘 지니고 다닙니다.\"\n\n\"그 귀한 알약을 좀 보여 달라고 청하면 무례한 일일까요?\" 보샹이 그를 곤란하게 만들 수 있기를 바라며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"아니요, 선생.\" 백작이 대꾸했다. 그러고는 주머니에서 놀라운 작은 함을 꺼냈다. 에메랄드 하나를 통째로 깎아 만든 것으로, 돌려서 여는 황금 뚜껑이 닫혀 있었다. 뚜껑을 열자 완두콩만 한 크기의 초록빛 작은 알갱이가 나왔다. 그 알약에서는 톡 쏘는 듯한 향이 코를 찔렀다. 에메랄드 안에는 네다섯 알이 더 들어 있었는데, 열두 알쯤은 담길 만했다. 함이 식탁을 돌았지만, 그것이 손에서 손으로 건네진 것은 알약을 보려는 것보다는 그 감탄스러운 에메랄드를 살펴보려는 마음에서였다.\n\n\"그럼 이 알약을 만드는 사람이 백작님의 요리사입니까?\" 보샹이 물었다.\n\n\"오, 아닙니다, 선생.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"저는 제 즐거움을 그렇게 천박한 자들에게 내맡기지 않습니다. 저는 그럭저럭 화학을 좀 하는 사람이라, 제 알약은 직접 만들지요.\"\n\n\"이건 굉장한 에메랄드로군요. 제가 본 것 중에 제일 큽니다.\" 샤토르노가 말했다. \"저희 어머니께서 가문 대대로 내려오는 보석을 몇 개 가지고 계시긴 합니다만.\"\n\n\"제겐 이와 비슷한 것이 세 개 있었지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 대꾸했다. \"하나는 술탄에게 드렸는데, 그가 자기 검에 박았습니다. 또 하나는 우리의 성부(聖父)이신 교황께 드렸는데, 그분이 삼중관에 박으셨지요. 그 맞은편에는 거의 같은 크기의, 그렇게 곱지는 않은 에메랄드가 박혀 있었습니다. 나폴레옹 황제가 선임 교황 비오 7세께 드린 것이었지요. 세 번째는 제가 가졌는데, 속을 파내게 했습니다. 그래서 값어치는 떨어졌지만, 제가 의도한 용도에는 더 편리해졌지요.\"\n\n모두가 놀라움에 차서 몬테크리스토를 바라보았다. 그는 어찌나 담담하게 말하던지, 그가 진실을 말하고 있거나, 아니면 미친 것이 분명해 보였다. 그러나 에메랄드를 본 터라 자연히 전자의 믿음 쪽으로 기울었다.\n\n\"그래서 그 두 군주는 이 훌륭한 선물에 대한 답례로 백작님께 무엇을 주었습니까?\" 드브레가 물었다.\n\n\"술탄은 한 여인의 자유를.\" 백작이 대답했다. \"교황은 한 남자의 목숨을 주었지요. 그래서 제 생애에 단 한 번, 마치 하늘이 저를 옥좌의 계단 위에 태어나게 한 것처럼 강력한 권세를 누려 본 셈입니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 백작님이 구하신 게 페피노였군요, 그렇지 않습니까?\" 모르세르가 외쳤다. \"백작님이 그를 위해 사면을 얻어 내신 거지요?\"\n\n\"어쩌면요.\" 백작이 미소 지으며 대꾸했다.\n\n\"친애하는 백작님, 이렇게 말씀하시는 걸 들으니 제가 얼마나 기쁜지 모르실 겁니다.\" 모르세르가 말했다. \"저는 미리 친구들에게 당신을 『아라비안나이트』의 마법사, 중세의 마술사라고 소개했습니다. 그런데 파리지앵들은 역설에 어찌나 능한지, 의심할 여지 없는 진실이라도 그것이 자기들 일상의 일부가 아니면 그저 상상의 변덕으로 치부해 버리지요. 예를 들어, 여기 드브레는 읽고 보샹은 인쇄하는데, 날마다 '조키 클럽 회원 한 명이 큰길에서 붙잡혀 강도를 당했다', '생드니 거리'나 '생제르맹 교외에서 네 사람이 살해됐다', '탕플 큰길의 어느 카페나 쥘리앵 욕장에서 도둑 열, 열다섯, 스무 명이 체포됐다'고 하지요. 그러면서도 이 같은 사람들이 마렘마나 로마 평원, 폰티노 습지에 산적들이 존재한다는 건 부정합니다. 제가 산적들에게 붙잡혔다는 것, 그리고 당신의 너그러운 중재가 없었다면 지금쯤 엘데르 거리의 누추한 제 거처에서 이들을 맞이하는 대신 성 세바스티아노 카타콤에서 잠들어 있었으리라는 걸, 부디 직접 말씀해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"아.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"당신은 그 일을 절대 입에 올리지 않겠다고 제게 약속했습니다.\"\n\n\"그런 약속을 한 건 제가 아닙니다.\" 모르세르가 외쳤다. \"같은 방식으로 구해 주셨다가 잊어버리신 다른 누군가였겠지요. 부디 그 얘기를 해 주십시오. 제가 아는 것 몇 가지뿐 아니라, 제가 모르는 것도 잔뜩 이야기해 드릴 테니까요.\"\n\n\"제가 보기엔,\" 백작이 미소 지으며 대꾸했다. \"당신이 꽤 중요한 역할을 했으니, 무슨 일이 있었는지 저만큼이나 잘 알고 계실 텐데요.\"\n\n\"좋습니다, 제가 아는 걸 전부 이야기하면, 당신도 이번엔 제가 모르는 걸 전부 이야기해 주시겠다고 약속하시는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"그거야 공평한 일이지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다.\n\n\"좋습니다.\" 모르세르가 말했다. \"사흘 동안 저는 어떤 가면을 쓴 여인이 제게 마음을 두고 있다고 믿었습니다. 저는 그녀를 툴리아나 포파이아의 후손쯤으로 여겼는데, 실은 그저 콘타디나가 제게 마음을 둔 것이었지요. 시골 처녀라는 말을 피하려고 콘타디나라고 하겠습니다. 제가 아는 거라곤, 제가 바보같이, 그것도 방금 말한 그 친구보다 더한 바보같이, 이 시골 처녀를 열대여섯 살쯤 된 어린 산적으로 잘못 봤다는 겁니다. 턱에는 수염도 안 났고 허리는 호리호리한 녀석이었지요. 제가 그 입술에 정숙한 입맞춤을 막 새기려는 찰나, 그가 제 머리에 권총을 들이대더니, 일고여덟 명의 패거리와 함께 저를 성 세바스티아노 카타콤으로 끌고, 아니 질질 끌고 갔습니다. 거기서 저는 카이사르의 『갈리아 전기』를 정독하고 있는, 대단히 교양 있는 산적 두목을 만났지요. 그는 황송하게도 책 읽기를 멈추고 제게 알려 주더군요. 이튿날 아침 여섯 시 전까지 4천 피아스트르가 그의 은행 계좌로 입금되지 않으면, 여섯 시 십오 분에는 제가 존재하기를 그칠 거라고요. 그 편지는 지금도 볼 수 있습니다. 프란츠 데피네가 가지고 있는데, 제 서명이 들어 있고 루이지 밤파 씨의 추신이 달려 있지요. 제가 아는 건 이게 전부입니다. 다만 백작님, 평소 무엇 하나 좀처럼 존중하는 법이 없는 로마의 산적들에게 어떻게 그토록 큰 존경심을 불어넣으셨는지는 모르겠습니다. 정말이지 프란츠와 저는 감탄을 금치 못했습니다.\"\n\n\"더없이 간단한 일입니다.\" 백작이 대꾸했다. \"저는 그 유명한 밤파를 십 년도 더 전부터 알고 있었습니다. 그가 아직 어린아이로 양치기에 불과했을 때, 길을 가르쳐 준 대가로 제가 금화 몇 닢을 주었지요. 그러자 그는 그 보답으로 제게 단검 하나를 주었는데, 손잡이를 제 손으로—아니, 자기 손으로 직접 새긴 것이었습니다. 제 무기 수집품 중에서 보셨을지도 모르겠군요. 세월이 흐른 뒤, 우리 우정을 굳혔어야 할 그 선물 교환을 잊었는지, 아니면 저를 기억하지 못했는지, 그가 저를 붙잡으려 했지요. 그런데 오히려 제가 그와 그의 패거리 열두 명을 사로잡았습니다. 저는 그를 로마 사법 당국에 넘길 수도 있었지요. 그쪽은 일 처리가 꽤 신속하고, 특히 그에게라면 더더욱 그랬을 겁니다. 하지만 저는 그러지 않았습니다. 그와 그의 패거리가 떠나도록 내버려 두었지요.\"\n\n\"두 번 다시 죄를 짓지 않는다는 조건으로 말이지요.\" 보샹이 웃으며 말했다. \"보아하니 그들이 약속을 지킨 모양이군요.\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 선생.\" 몬테크리스토가 대꾸했다. \"그저 저와 제 친구들을 존중한다는 단순한 조건이었습니다. 어쩌면 제가 지금 하려는 말이, 박애를 떠벌리고 이웃에 대한 의무를 자랑하는 사회주의자인 여러분에게는 이상하게 들릴지도 모르겠습니다. 하지만 저는 저를 보호해 주지 않는 사회를 결코 보호하려 들지 않습니다. 게다가 그 사회는, 굳이 말하자면, 대체로 저를 해치기 위해서만 저에게 신경을 쓰지요. 그래서 그 사회를 제 평가에서 낮은 자리에 두고 그것에 중립을 지킴으로써, 오히려 사회와 제 이웃이 제게 빚을 지는 셈입니다.\"\n\n\"브라보!\" 샤토르노가 외쳤다. \"이기주의를 설파할 만큼 용감한 사람은 제 평생 처음 봅니다. 브라보, 백작님, 브라보!\"\n\n\"적어도 솔직하긴 합니다.\" 모렐이 말했다. \"하지만 백작님이 그토록 대담하게 천명하신 원칙에서 한 번 벗어난 것을 후회하지 않으시리라 확신합니다.\"\n\n\"제가 그 원칙에서 어떻게 벗어났다는 겁니까, 선생?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다. 그는 모렐을 어찌나 강렬하게 바라보던지, 그 청년이 두세 번이나 그 맑고 꿰뚫는 듯한 시선을 견디지 못할 정도였다.\n\n\"그게, 제가 보기엔,\" 모렐이 대답했다. \"알지도 못하는 모르세르 씨를 구해 주심으로써, 당신은 이웃과 사회에 선행을 베푸신 거니까요.\"\n\n\"그가 바로 그 사회의 가장 빛나는 장식이지요.\" 보샹이 샴페인 한 잔을 들이켜며 말했다.\n\n\"친애하는 백작님,\" 모르세르가 외쳤다. \"당신이 틀렸습니다. 제가 아는 가장 가공할 논리가 중 한 분이신 당신이 말입니다. 그리고 당신은 이기주의자가 아니라 박애주의자라는 게 명백히 입증되는 걸 똑똑히 보셔야 합니다. 아, 당신은 자신을 동방인이니 레반트인이니 몰타인이니 인도인이니 중국인이니 하시고, 성(姓)은 몬테크리스토에, 세례명은 뱃사람 신드바드라 하십니다. 그러면서도 파리에 발을 들인 첫날부터, 우리 별난 파리지앵들의 가장 큰 미덕, 아니 가장 큰 결점을 본능적으로 드러내시는군요. 그러니까 갖지도 않은 악덕은 짐짓 꾸미고, 지닌 미덕은 감추시는 것 말입니다.\"\n\n\"친애하는 자작,\" 몬테크리스토가 대꾸했다. \"제가 한 모든 일에서, 당신이나 이분들에게서 받은 그 거창한 찬사를 받을 만한 점은 하나도 보이지 않습니다. 당신은 제게 낯선 사람이 아니었지요. 저는 당신에게 방 두 개를 내주고, 함께 아침을 들자고 청하고, 제 마차 한 대를 빌려주고, 당신과 함께 사육제를 구경하고, 포폴로 광장의 어느 창가에서 당신과 함께 그 사형 집행을 보았던 그때부터 당신을 알았으니까요. 당신은 그 광경에 어찌나 마음이 흔들렸던지 거의 까무러칠 뻔했지요. 이분들 중 누구에게라도 여쭤보겠습니다. 제가 손님을, 당신 표현대로 그 흉측한 산적의 손에 내버려 둘 수 있었겠습니까? 게다가 아시다시피, 저는 프랑스에 오면 당신이 저를 파리의 살롱 몇 군데에 소개해 줄 수 있으리라는 생각을 품고 있었습니다. 얼마 전만 해도 당신은 이 결심을 막연한 계획쯤으로 여겼을지 모르지만, 오늘 보시다시피 그것은 현실이었지요. 그러니 약속을 어겼다는 벌을 받지 않으려면 당신은 이에 응하셔야 합니다.\"\n\n\"지키겠습니다.\" 모르세르가 대꾸했다. \"다만 그림 같은 사건들과 환상적인 지평선에 익숙하신 당신이 크게 실망하실까 봐 걱정입니다. 우리 사이에서는 당신의 모험적인 삶이 그토록 친숙하게 만들어 놓은 그런 일화들을 하나도 만나지 못하실 겁니다. 우리의 침보라소는 몽마르트르고, 우리의 히말라야는 발레리앵산이며, 우리의 대사막은 그르넬 평원입니다. 거기서는 지금 대상(隊商)들에게 물을 대려고 자분정을 파고 있지요. 도둑이야 넘쳐 나지만, 말들 만큼 많지는 않습니다. 그리고 이 도둑들은 영주보다 경찰관을 훨씬 더 무서워하지요. 프랑스는 너무나 산문적이고, 파리는 너무나 문명화된 도시라, 그 여든다섯 개 주(州)에서—코르시카는 빼니까 여든다섯이라고 하는 겁니다—그러니까 이 여든다섯 개 주에서, 전신주가 없는 언덕 하나, 경찰서장이 가스등을 달아 놓지 않은 동굴 하나 찾지 못하실 겁니다. 제가 당신께 해 드릴 수 있는 봉사는 단 하나뿐이고, 그 일이라면 전적으로 당신 분부를 따르겠습니다. 그러니까 어디서든 당신을 소개하거나, 제 친구들이 소개하도록 하는 것이지요. 사실 당신은 누가 소개해 줄 필요도 없습니다. 당신의 이름과 재산과 재능이라면\" (몬테크리스토가 다소 빈정거리는 미소를 띠며 고개를 숙였다) \"어디든 스스로 나설 수 있고, 환대받으실 테니까요. 제가 쓸모 있는 길은 한 가지뿐입니다. 파리의 관습이나, 편히 지내는 방법이나, 시장에 관한 지식이 도움이 된다면, 여기서 당신께 알맞은 거처를 찾아 드리는 일은 저를 믿으셔도 됩니다. 제가 로마에서 당신의 방을 함께 썼던 것처럼 감히 제 거처를 함께 쓰자고 청하지는 못하겠습니다. 이기주의를 표방하지는 않지만, 그럼에도 더없는 이기주의자인 제가 말입니다. 저 말고는, 그 방에 그림자 하나 더 들어설 자리가 없거든요. 그 그림자가 여성의 것이 아니라면요.\"\n\n\"아.\" 백작이 말했다. \"그건 참으로 부부다운 단서로군요. 로마에서 당신이 결혼 계획 같은 걸 말씀하셨던 게 기억납니다. 축하드려도 될까요?\"\n\n\"아직 계획 단계입니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 '계획 단계'라고 말하는 사람은 이미 결정됐다는 뜻이지요.\" 드브레가 말했다.\n\n\"아니요.\" 모르세르가 대꾸했다. \"아버지가 그 일에 무척 신경을 쓰고 계시지요. 머지않아 당신께, 제 아내까지는 아니어도 적어도 약혼녀인 외제니 당글라르 양을 소개할 수 있기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"외제니 당글라르라.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"말해 보십시오, 그녀의 아버지가 당글라르 남작 아닙니까?\"\n\n\"맞습니다.\" 모르세르가 대꾸했다. \"새로 작위를 받은 남작이지요.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 상관입니까,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그가 이 영예를 받을 만한 봉사를 국가에 했다면요?\"\n\n\"엄청난 봉사를 했지요.\" 보샹이 대답했다. \"사실은 자유주의자면서도, 1829년에 샤를 10세를 위해 6백만 차관을 협상해 냈거든요. 그 덕에 그를 남작이자 레지옹 도뇌르 훈장 기사로 만들어 주었지요. 그래서 그는 그 훈장 리본을, 당신이 생각하듯 조끼 주머니에 넣고 다니는 게 아니라, 단춧구멍에 달고 다닙니다.\"\n\n\"아.\" 모르세르가 웃으며 끼어들었다. \"보샹, 보샹, 그런 얘기는 『코르세르』나 『샤리바리』에나 써먹고, 내 앞에서 내 장래의 장인은 좀 봐주게.\" 그러고는 몬테크리스토를 돌아보며 말했다. \"방금 그 이름을 마치 남작을 아시는 것처럼 말씀하시던데요?\"\n\n\"그를 알지는 못합니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 대꾸했다. \"하지만 머지않아 알게 될 것 같군요. 런던의 리처드 앤드 블런트 상회, 빈의 아르슈타인 앤드 에스켈레스 상회, 그리고 로마의 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회를 통해 그에게 신용 거래가 개설되어 있으니까요.\" 마지막 두 이름을 발음할 때, 백작은 막시밀리앙 모렐을 흘끗 보았다. 이방인이 모렐에게 어떤 반응을 끌어내려 했다면, 그는 빗나가지 않았다. 막시밀리앙이 마치 전기에 감전된 듯 흠칫했다.\n\n\"톰슨 앤드 프렌치라.\" 그가 말했다. \"이 상회를 아십니까, 선생?\"\n\n\"그들은 기독교 세계의 수도에 있는 제 거래 은행입니다.\" 백작이 차분히 대꾸했다. \"그들에 대한 제 영향력이 당신께 어떤 도움이 될 수 있을까요?\"\n\n\"오, 백작님, 어쩌면 지금까지 헛수고로 끝났던 어떤 조사에 도움을 주실 수 있을지도 모르겠습니다. 그 상회는 지난날 저희 상회에 큰 도움을 베풀었는데, 무슨 까닭에서인지 그 도움을 베풀었다는 사실을 줄곧 부인해 왔거든요.\"\n\n\"분부대로 하겠습니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 고개를 숙이며 말했다.\n\n\"그런데,\" 모르세르가 말을 이었다. \"당글라르 얘기가 나온 김에 말하자면, 우리가 주제에서 참 멀리도 벗어났군요. 우리는 몬테크리스토 백작께 알맞은 거처를 이야기하던 중이었지요. 자, 여러분, 다들 어디 한 군데씩 제안해 봅시다. 이 새 손님을 우리 대도시 어디에 모실까요?\"\n\n\"생제르맹 교외.\" 샤토르노가 말했다. \"백작께서는 거기서 안뜰과 정원이 딸린 멋진 저택을 찾으실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"흥! 샤토르노,\" 드브레가 대꾸했다. \"자네는 그 따분하고 음침한 생제르맹 교외밖에 모르지. 백작님, 저 친구 말엔 신경 쓰지 마십시오. 쇼세 당탱에 사십시오. 거기가 진짜 파리의 중심입니다.\"\n\n\"오페라 큰길.\" 보샹이 말했다. \"발코니가 딸린 집의 2층이지요. 백작께서는 거기로 은직물 방석을 가져다 놓으시고, 치부크를 피우며 온 파리가 눈앞으로 지나가는 걸 보실 수 있을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 자네는 생각이 없나, 모렐?\" 샤토르노가 물었다. \"아무것도 제안하지 않는군.\"\n\n\"오, 아닙니다.\" 청년이 미소 지으며 대꾸했다. \"오히려 하나 있습니다. 다만 백작께서 다른 분들의 그 멋진 제안 중 하나에 마음이 끌리시리라 생각해서요. 그런데 어느 것에도 답하지 않으시니, 감히 한 가지 제안을 드려 보겠습니다. 멜레 거리에 있는 매력적인 저택의 폼파두르 양식 방들인데, 제 누이가 일 년째 살고 있는 곳이지요.\"\n\n\"누이가 있으십니까?\" 백작이 물었다.\n\n\"네, 선생, 더없이 훌륭한 누이지요.\"\n\n\"결혼은 하셨고요?\"\n\n\"거의 구 년째입니다.\"\n\n\"행복하신가요?\" 백작이 다시 물었다.\n\n\"인간이라는 존재에게 허락된 만큼 행복하지요.\" 막시밀리앙이 대답했다. \"누이는 사랑하는 사람과 결혼했습니다. 저희 집안이 몰락했을 때도 끝까지 우리에게 의리를 지켜 준 사람, 에마뉘엘 에르보 말입니다.\"\n\n몬테크리스토가 알아챌 듯 말 듯 미소 지었다.\n\n\"저는 휴가 중에 거기서 지냅니다.\" 막시밀리앙이 말을 이었다. \"그리고 백작께서 언제든 저희를 찾아 주신다면, 매부 에마뉘엘과 함께 백작님의 분부를 따르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"잠깐만.\" 몬테크리스토에게 대답할 틈을 주지 않고 알베르가 외쳤다. \"조심하게. 자네는 여행자를, 뱃사람 신드바드를, 파리를 구경하러 온 사람을 가둬 두려는 거야. 그를 가부장 같은 사람으로 만들 셈인가.\"\n\n\"오, 아닐세.\" 모렐이 말했다. \"제 누이는 스물다섯이고 매부는 서른인데, 둘 다 명랑하고 젊고 행복하다네. 게다가 백작께서는 자기 집에 계시는 셈이라, 마음 내키실 때만 그들을 보시면 되지.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 선생.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"당신이 영광스럽게도 소개해 주신다면, 저는 당신의 누이와 그 남편께 인사드리는 것으로 만족하겠습니다. 하지만 이분들 중 누구의 제안도 받아들일 수가 없군요. 제 거처는 이미 마련되어 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요!\" 모르세르가 외쳤다. \"그럼 호텔로 가시는 겁니까? 그건 무척 따분하실 텐데요.\"\n\n\"제가 로마에서 그렇게 형편없이 묵었던가요?\" 몬테크리스토가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"제기랄! 로마에서야 방을 꾸미는 데 5만 피아스트르를 쓰셨지요. 하지만 날마다 그만한 돈을 쓸 마음은 없으시겠지요.\"\n\n\"저를 망설이게 한 건 그게 아닙니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 대꾸했다. \"다만 저는 제 집을 따로 갖기로 정하고, 제 시종을 먼저 보냈습니다. 그라면 지금쯤 집을 사서 가구까지 들여놓았을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 파리를 아는 시종이 있으시군요?\" 보샹이 말했다.\n\n\"그가 파리에 와 본 건 이번이 처음입니다. 그는 흑인이고, 말을 못 하지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 대꾸했다.\n\n\"알리로군요!\" 일동의 놀라움 속에서 알베르가 외쳤다.\n\n\"네, 바로 그 알리, 제 누비아인 벙어리지요. 로마에서 보셨을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"물론입니다.\" 모르세르가 말했다. \"그를 똑똑히 기억합니다. 하지만 어떻게 누비아인에게 집을 사오라 맡기고, 벙어리에게 가구를 들이라 맡기실 수 있습니까? 모든 걸 엉망으로 해 놓을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"오해십니다, 선생.\" 몬테크리스토가 대꾸했다. \"저는 오히려, 그가 모든 걸 제 뜻대로 골라 놓으리라 확신합니다. 그는 제 취향과 변덕과 필요를 다 알지요. 그는 일주일 전부터 여기 와서, 사냥개 같은 본능으로 혼자 돌아다니며 찾아냈습니다. 그가 저를 위해 모든 걸 정돈해 놓을 겁니다. 제가 오늘 열 시에 도착하리라는 걸 알고, 아홉 시에 퐁텐블로 관문에서 저를 기다리고 있었지요. 그가 제게 이 종이를 건넸습니다. 제 새 거처의 번지가 적혀 있는데, 직접 읽어 보십시오.\" 그러고는 몬테크리스토가 종이 한 장을 알베르에게 건넸다.\n\n\"아, 정말 독창적이군요.\" 보샹이 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 매우 군주답고요.\" 샤토르노가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"뭐라고요, 당신 집을 모르신단 말입니까?\" 드브레가 물었다.\n\n\"모릅니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"말씀드렸다시피, 저는 늦고 싶지 않았습니다. 마차 안에서 옷을 차려입고, 자작의 문 앞에서 내렸지요.\" 젊은이들은 서로를 마주 보았다. 그들은 몬테크리스토가 한 편의 희극을 연기하고 있는 것인지 알 수 없었지만, 그가 내뱉는 말마다 어찌나 소박한 기운이 감돌던지, 그의 말이 거짓이라고는 도무지 생각할 수 없었다. 게다가 그가 무엇 때문에 거짓말을 하겠는가?\n\n\"그렇다면 우리는,\" 보샹이 말했다. \"우리 힘이 닿는 자잘한 도움이나마 백작께 다 해 드리는 것으로 만족해야겠군요. 저는 언론인의 자격으로, 모든 극장을 백작께 열어 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 선생.\" 몬테크리스토가 대꾸했다. \"제 집사가 극장마다 특별석을 하나씩 잡아 두라는 지시를 받았습니다.\"\n\n\"집사도 누비아인입니까?\" 드브레가 물었다.\n\n\"아니요, 그는 당신네 동포입니다. 코르시카 사람이 누군가의 동포일 수 있다면 말이지요. 그런데 당신은 그를 아십니다, 모르세르 씨.\"\n\n\"창문 빌리는 일을 그토록 잘 아는, 그 훌륭한 베르투치오 씨 말씀입니까?\"\n\n\"네, 제가 영광스럽게도 당신을 맞이하던 날 보셨지요. 그는 군인이었고, 밀수꾼이었고, 사실상 안 해 본 게 없는 사람입니다. 어쩌면 사소한 일로—이를테면 칼부림 같은 것으로 경찰과 얽힌 적이 없다고는 장담 못 하겠군요.\"\n\n\"그런 정직한 시민을 집사로 고르셨군요.\" 드브레가 말했다. \"해마다 당신에게서 얼마나 훔쳐 갑니까?\"\n\n\"솔직히,\" 백작이 대꾸했다. \"다른 자들보다 더 많이는 아닙니다. 그가 제 목적에 맞고, 불가능이란 걸 모른다고 확신하기에, 그래서 데리고 있는 거지요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 샤토르노가 말을 이었다. \"이미 살림과 집사와 샹젤리제의 저택까지 갖추셨으니, 이제 정부(情婦)만 있으면 되겠군요.\" 알베르가 미소 지었다. 그는 아르헨티나 극장과 발레 극장의 백작 특별석에서 보았던 그 아름다운 그리스 여인을 떠올렸다.\n\n\"저는 그보다 더 나은 걸 가지고 있습니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"저에겐 노예가 있지요. 당신들은 오페라극장이나 보드빌극장, 바리에테극장에서 정부를 구하지만, 저는 콘스탄티노플에서 제 노예를 샀습니다. 값은 더 비쌌지만, 저는 두려워할 게 없지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 잊으셨군요.\" 드브레가 웃으며 대꾸했다. \"샤를 왕이 말했듯이 우리는 이름으로도 프랑크인이고 천성으로도 자유로운 사람들이라, 당신의 노예가 프랑스 땅에 발을 들이는 순간 자유의 몸이 된다는 걸요.\"\n\n\"누가 그녀에게 그걸 일러 주겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그녀를 보는 첫 번째 사람이지요.\"\n\n\"그녀는 로마이크어밖에 못 합니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 얘기가 다르군요.\"\n\n\"하지만 적어도 우리가 그녀를 보긴 보겠지요?\" 보샹이 말했다. \"아니면 벙어리뿐 아니라 환관들도 두십니까?\"\n\n\"오, 아닙니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 대꾸했다. \"저는 야만을 그 정도까지 밀고 가지는 않습니다. 제 주위를 둘러싼 이들은 누구나 저를 떠날 자유가 있고, 떠나고 나면 저나 다른 누구도 더는 필요로 하지 않게 되지요. 어쩌면 그래서 그들이 저를 떠나지 않는 건지도 모르겠습니다.\"\n\n그들은 진작에 디저트와 시가로 넘어가 있었다.\n\n\"친애하는 알베르,\" 드브레가 일어서며 말했다. \"두 시 반일세. 자네 손님은 매력적이지만, 가끔은 가장 좋은 자리를 떠나 가장 형편없는 자리로 가야 할 때가 있는 법이지. 나는 장관께 돌아가야겠네. 백작에 대해 말씀드리면, 우리도 곧 그가 누구인지 알게 될 걸세.\"\n\n\"조심하게.\" 알베르가 대꾸했다. \"그 일을 해낸 사람은 아무도 없었으니까.\"\n\n\"오, 우리 경찰에는 3백만이 있거든. 늘 미리 다 써 버리는 게 사실이긴 하지만, 그래도 상관없어. 이 일에 쓸 5만 프랑쯤은 여전히 남아 있을 테니까.\"\n\n\"그럼 알게 되면 내게 말해 주겠나?\"\n\n\"약속하지. 잘 있게, 알베르. 여러분, 안녕히.\"\n\n방을 나서며 드브레가 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"내 마차!\"\n\n\"브라보.\" 보샹이 알베르에게 말했다. \"나는 의회엔 안 가겠네. 하지만 당글라르 씨의 연설보다 내 독자들에게 내놓을 더 나은 거리가 생겼으니까.\"\n\n\"제발 부탁이네, 보샹.\" 모르세르가 대꾸했다. \"그를 어디든 소개하는 공로를 내게서 빼앗지는 말아 주게. 그가 별난 사람 아닌가?\"\n\n\"별난 정도가 아니지.\" 샤토르노가 대꾸했다. \"내 평생 본 사람 중에 가장 비범한 인물 중 하나야. 자네 가려나, 모렐?\"\n\n\"백작께 명함을 드리고 곧 가겠습니다. 멜레 거리 14번지로 저희를 찾아오시겠다고 약속하셨거든요.\"\n\n\"꼭 들르겠습니다.\" 백작이 고개를 숙이며 대꾸했다.\n\n그리하여 막시밀리앙 모렐은 샤토르노 남작과 함께 방을 나섰고, 몬테크리스토는 모르세르와 단둘이 남았다.\n\n# Chapter 41. The Presentation\n\n알베르가 몬테크리스토 백작과 단둘이 남게 되자 입을 열었다. \"백작님,\" 그가 말했다. \"안내자로서 제 임무를 시작하도록 허락해 주십시오. 우선 독신 남자의 거처가 어떤 것인지 보여 드리지요. 이탈리아의 궁전들에 익숙하신 분이니, 파리에서 가장 못한 축에 들지는 않는 한 젊은이가 대체 몇 평방피트 안에서 살아가는지 가늠해 보시는 것도 재미있으실 겁니다. 방에서 방으로 옮겨 갈 때마다 창문을 열어 숨 좀 돌리시게 해 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 이미 일층의 식당과 응접실을 보았다. 알베르는 먼저 그를 자신의 화실로 데려갔다. 앞서 말했듯이 그가 가장 아끼는 방이었다. 몬테크리스토는 알베르가 이곳에 모아 둔 것들을 단숨에 알아보았다. 오래된 장식장, 일본 자기, 동방의 직물, 베네치아 유리, 세계 곳곳에서 가져온 무기들. 모든 것이 그에게 익숙했다. 한눈에 그것들의 연대와 출신지와 내력을 알아맞혔다.\n\n모르세르는 자신이 안내자 노릇을 하리라 기대했다. 그런데 오히려 그 자신이 백작의 안내를 받으며 고고학과 광물학, 박물학 강의를 듣는 처지가 되었다.\n\n두 사람은 이층으로 내려갔다. 알베르는 손님을 응접실로 이끌었다. 응접실은 근대 화가들의 작품으로 가득했다. 뒤프레의 풍경화에는 긴 갈대와 키 큰 나무들, 음매 우는 소들, 그리고 경이로운 하늘이 담겨 있었다. 들라크루아의 아라비아 기병들은 길고 흰 부르누스를 걸치고 번쩍이는 허리띠를 두르고 다마스 무늬가 새겨진 무기를 들었으며, 그 말들은 서로 이빨로 물어뜯고 기수들은 철퇴를 휘두르며 맹렬히 싸우고 있었다. 불랑제의 수채화는 파리 노트르담을 화가로 하여금 시인의 맞수가 되게 할 만한 박력으로 그려 냈다. 디아스의 그림들은 꽃을 실제 꽃보다 더 아름답게, 태양을 실제 태양보다 더 찬란하게 그렸다. 드캉의 데생은 살바토르 로사의 그것만큼이나 색채가 선명하면서도 한결 시적이었다. 지로와 뮐러의 파스텔화는 천사 같은 아이들과 성모의 이목구비를 지닌 여인들을 담았다. 도자의 화집 『동방 여행』에서 뜯어낸 스케치들은 낙타의 안장 위에서, 혹은 모스크의 둥근 지붕 아래에서 단 몇 초 만에 그려진 것이었다. 한마디로 그것은 오래전 시대와 더불어 사라져 버린 옛 예술을 대신하고 보상하기 위해 근대 예술이 내놓을 수 있는 모든 것이었다.\n\n알베르는 이번만큼은 이 여행자에게 새로운 무언가를 보여 줄 수 있으리라 기대했다. 그러나 놀랍게도 백작은 서명을 찾아보지도 않은 채—사실 그 서명들 가운데 다수는 머리글자뿐이었다—그림마다 화가의 이름을 곧바로 댔다. 그 솜씨를 보면 이름 하나하나를 알고 있을 뿐 아니라 거기 결부된 화풍을 두루 음미하고 연구해 왔음이 쉽게 드러났다. 응접실에서 두 사람은 침실로 옮겨 갔다. 침실은 고상한 취향과 소박한 우아함의 본보기였다. 레오폴 로베르의 서명이 들어간 초상화 한 점이 정교하게 조각하고 금박을 입힌 액자 속에서 빛나고 있었다. 그 초상화가 몬테크리스토 백작의 시선을 끌었다. 그는 방 안으로 빠르게 세 걸음을 내딛더니, 그림 앞에서 우뚝 멈춰 섰다.\n\n스물대여섯 살쯤 된 젊은 여인의 초상이었다. 가무잡잡한 피부에, 길고 짙은 속눈썹 아래 맑고 윤기 어린 눈동자가 자리하고 있었다. 여인은 카탈루냐 어부 아낙네들의 그림 같은 옷차림을 하고 있었다. 붉은빛과 검은빛이 어우러진 보디스에, 머리에는 황금 핀을 꽂았다. 여인은 바다를 바라보고 있었고, 그 자태가 푸른 대양과 하늘을 배경으로 또렷이 떠올라 있었다. 방 안이 어찌나 어두침침했던지 알베르는 백작의 얼굴에 퍼지는 창백함도, 가슴과 어깨가 신경질적으로 들썩이는 것도 알아채지 못했다. 한순간 침묵이 흘렀고, 그동안 몬테크리스토는 그림을 뚫어지게 응시했다.\n\n\"참으로 매력적인 연인을 두셨군요, 자작님.\" 백작이 더없이 차분한 어조로 말했다. \"그리고 이 옷차림은—틀림없이 무도회 의상일 텐데—썩 잘 어울립니다.\"\n\n\"아, 백작님.\" 알베르가 대꾸했다. \"이 곁에 다른 그림이 있는 것을 보셨다면 그 오해를 결코 용서하지 않았을 겁니다. 제 어머니를 모르시는군요. 여기 보시는 분이 바로 제 어머니입니다. 육칠 년쯤 전에 이렇게 초상을 그리게 하셨지요. 이 의상은 그저 멋을 부린 것인 듯합니다만, 어찌나 닮았는지 1830년 무렵의 제 어머니 모습을 지금도 보고 있는 것만 같습니다. 백작께서 자리를 비우신 동안 어머니가 이 초상을 그리게 하셨습니다. 분명 아버지께 기쁜 놀라움을 안겨 드리려는 뜻이었겠지요. 그런데 이상하게도 이 초상은 아버지의 마음에 들지 않는 듯했습니다. 보시다시피 레오폴 로베르의 가장 뛰어난 작품 가운데 하나라는 그림의 값어치로도 아버지의 거부감을 누그러뜨리지는 못했지요. 사실 우리끼리 하는 말이지만, 모르세르 백작께서는 뤽상부르에서 가장 부지런한 귀족원 의원 가운데 한 분이고 이론으로 이름난 장군이시지만, 예술 애호가로서는 더없이 평범한 분입니다. 어머니는 다릅니다. 그림 솜씨가 매우 빼어나시지요. 그래서 이토록 값진 그림을 손에서 놓고 싶지 않으셨던 어머니가, 그것을 여기 걸어 두라고 제게 주셨습니다. 그래야 모르세르 백작의 심기를 덜 거스를 테니까요. 아버지의 초상은 그로가 그린 것인데, 그것도 보여 드리겠습니다. 집안일을 늘어놓아 죄송합니다만, 제가 백작님을 아버지께 소개하는 영광을 누릴 터라, 혹여 이 그림에 대해 어떤 말씀이라도 꺼내실까 미리 말씀드리는 겁니다. 이 그림에는 무언가 해로운 기운이 깃든 듯합니다. 어머니는 이곳에 오시면 거의 빠짐없이 이 그림을 바라보시고, 바라보실 때면 거의 빠짐없이 눈물을 흘리시니까요. 이 불화는 백작과 백작 부인 사이에 일어난 유일한 다툼입니다. 결혼하신 지 이십 년이 넘었지만, 두 분은 혼례를 올린 첫날과 다름없이 여전히 깊이 맺어져 계십니다.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 그 말 속에 숨은 뜻이라도 찾으려는 듯 알베르를 흘끗 보았다. 그러나 젊은이가 그 말을 진심에서 우러난 순박함으로 내뱉었다는 것은 분명했다.\n\n\"자,\" 알베르가 말했다. \"이제 제 보물을 다 보셨으니, 변변찮은 것들이나마 백작님께 바치도록 허락해 주십시오. 부디 내 집처럼 편히 여기십시오. 그리고 더 마음 편히 계시도록, 모르세르 백작의 거처로 저와 함께 가 주시지요. 로마에서 백작님이 제게 베풀어 주신 도움을 편지로 아버지께 알리고, 약속하신 방문도 미리 전해 두었습니다. 분명히 말씀드리지만, 아버지도 어머니도 직접 감사를 드리고 싶어 애태우고 계십니다. 백작님이 어지간한 일에는 시들해지신 분이라는 걸 압니다. 숱한 가족 상봉의 장면을 보아 온 신드바드에게 이런 광경이 무슨 큰 감흥이 있겠습니까. 그래도 제가 권하는 이 일을 파리 생활로 들어서는 입문 의식으로 여기고 받아들여 주십시오. 예의와 방문과 소개로 이루어진 생활 말입니다.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 아무 대답 없이 고개를 숙였다. 그는 신사라면 누구나 의무로 여기는 사교계의 관례 가운데 하나로서, 그 제안을 열의도 없이, 그렇다고 마지못한 기색도 없이 받아들였다. 알베르는 하인을 불러, 모르세르 백작 부부에게 몬테크리스토 백작이 도착했음을 알리라고 일렀다. 알베르는 백작과 함께 하인의 뒤를 따랐다. 대기실에 이르자 문 위에 방패꼴 문장이 보였다. 그 화려한 장식과 나머지 가구들과의 조화로 보아, 집주인이 이 문장에 얼마나 큰 의미를 두는지 알 수 있었다. 몬테크리스토는 걸음을 멈추고 그것을 유심히 살폈다.\n\n\"하늘빛 바탕에 금빛 제비 일곱 마리를 비스듬히 배치했군요.\" 그가 말했다. \"이것이 댁의 가문 문장이겠지요? 문장학 지식이 있어 이걸 풀이할 수 있다는 것 말고는, 저는 문장학에 대해 아주 무지합니다. 저야 새로 만들어진 백작, 그것도 성 스테파노 기사단의 도움을 빌려 토스카나에서 꾸며 낸 백작에 지나지 않으니까요. 여행을 많이 다니려면 그런 게 필요하다는 말을 듣지 않았더라면, 그런 수고조차 하지 않았을 겁니다. 게다가 세관원에게 검문당하지 않으려면 마차 옆판에 무언가 새겨 두어야 하지요. 이런 질문을 드리는 걸 양해해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"무례한 질문이 아닙니다.\" 모르세르가 확신에 찬 순박함으로 대답했다. \"정확히 알아맞히셨습니다. 이것이 우리 가문의 문장입니다. 정확히는 아버지 가문의 것이지요. 그런데 보시다시피 다른 방패꼴 문장과 합쳐져 있습니다. 붉은 바탕에 은빛 탑이 있는 것이 어머니 가문의 문장입니다. 어머니 쪽으로는 제가 에스파냐 사람이지만, 모르세르 가문은 프랑스 가문이고, 듣자 하니 프랑스 남부에서 가장 오래된 가문 가운데 하나라고 합니다.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"이 문장들이 그것을 증명하는군요. 성지로 떠난 무장 순례자들은 거의 모두 자기 사명을 기리는 뜻으로 십자가를, 아니면 앞으로 떠날 머나먼 여정을 나타내는 뜻으로 철새를 문장에 새겼습니다. 믿음의 날개를 타고 그 여정을 이루기를 바라면서요. 댁의 선조 가운데 한 분이 십자군에 참여하셨던 거지요. 설령 그것이 성왕 루이의 십자군에 지나지 않았다 해도, 그것만으로 13세기까지 거슬러 올라가는 셈이니 제법 오래된 가문입니다.\"\n\n\"그럴 수도 있겠지요.\" 모르세르가 말했다. \"아버지 서재에 가계도가 하나 있는데, 그것을 보면 다 알 수 있을 겁니다. 제가 거기에 주석을 달아 두었는데, 도지에와 조쿠르●도 크게 감탄했을 정도지요. 요즘은 더 이상 그런 것에 마음을 두지 않습니다. 그래도 말씀드려야겠는데, 우리는 이 민중의 정부 아래에서 다시금 그런 것들에 부쩍 관심을 쏟기 시작했습니다.\"\n* 도지에와 조쿠르: 둘 다 프랑스의 이름난 가문·문장 연구가다. —옮긴이\n\n\"그렇다면 댁의 정부도 과거에서 무언가 골라 쓰려거든, 제가 댁의 기념물들에서 본 것들보다는 더 나은 것을 고르는 편이 좋겠군요. 그것들은 문장학적으로 아무 의미도 없으니 말입니다. 그런데 자작님은,\" 몬테크리스토가 모르세르를 향해 말을 이었다. \"정부보다 운이 좋으십니다. 자작님의 문장은 정말로 아름답고, 상상력을 자극하니까요. 그렇지요, 자작님은 프로방스와 에스파냐 양쪽에서 비롯된 분입니다. 아까 보여 주신 초상이 실물을 닮은 것이라면, 그 고귀한 카탈루냐 여인의 얼굴에서 제가 그토록 감탄했던 가무잡잡한 빛깔도 그래서 설명이 되는군요.\"\n\n겉으로는 더없이 정중하게 내뱉은 이 말 속에 백작이 감추어 둔 빈정거림을 알아채려면 오이디푸스나 스핑크스의 통찰력이 있어야 했을 것이다. 모르세르는 미소로 고마움을 표하고, 자기 가문의 문장이 걸린 문을 밀어 열었다. 앞서 말했듯이 그 문은 응접실로 통했다. 응접실에서 가장 눈에 잘 띄는 자리에 또 다른 초상화가 걸려 있었다. 서른대여섯에서 서른여덟쯤 된 남자의 초상이었다. 그는 장군 정복 차림으로, 높은 지위를 나타내는 굵은 금실 견장을 양어깨에 달고 있었다. 목에는 레지옹 도뇌르 훈장 끈을 둘러 그가 사령관급임을 드러냈고, 오른쪽 가슴에는 구세주 기사단 일등 훈장의 별이, 왼쪽에는 카를로스 3세 대십자 훈장의 별이 달려 있었다. 이는 초상화 속 인물이 그리스와 에스파냐의 전쟁에 종군했거나, 훈장에 관한 한 마찬가지인 일이지만 두 나라에서 어떤 외교 임무를 수행했음을 입증해 주었다.\n\n몬테크리스토는 앞서 다른 초상화에 쏟았던 것 못지않은 주의를 기울여 이 초상화를 살피고 있었다. 그때 또 다른 문이 열렸고, 그는 모르세르 백작 본인과 마주 서게 되었다.\n\n마흔에서 마흔다섯 살쯤 된 남자였으나, 적어도 쉰은 되어 보였다. 검은 콧수염과 눈썹이 군인답게 짧게 깎은 거의 새하얀 머리카락과 기묘한 대조를 이루고 있었다. 그는 평복 차림이었고, 단춧구멍에 자신이 속한 여러 훈장의 약장을 달고 있었다.\n\n그는 제법 위엄 있는 걸음걸이로, 약간 서두르는 기색으로 들어왔다. 몬테크리스토는 한 걸음도 떼지 않은 채 그가 자기 쪽으로 다가오는 것을 바라보았다. 두 발이 땅에 박힌 듯, 두 눈은 모르세르 백작에게 못 박힌 듯했다.\n\n\"아버지.\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"몬테크리스토 백작님을 소개하는 영광을 누리게 되었습니다. 제가 말씀드렸던 그 위급한 처지에서 다행히 만나게 된, 너그러우신 친구분입니다.\"\n\n\"참으로 환영합니다, 백작.\" 모르세르 백작이 미소를 지으며 몬테크리스토에게 인사를 건넸다. \"백작께서는 우리 집안의 하나뿐인 후계자를 지켜 주심으로써, 우리가 영원히 감사할 수밖에 없는 은혜를 베풀어 주셨습니다.\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 모르세르 백작은 의자 하나를 가리켰고, 자신은 창 맞은편의 다른 의자에 앉았다.\n\n몬테크리스토는 모르세르가 권한 자리에 앉으면서, 커다란 벨벳 커튼의 그늘에 몸이 가려지도록 자세를 잡았다. 그러고는 백작의 근심에 찌든 핏기 없는 얼굴에서, 세월이 새겨 놓은 주름 하나하나에 적힌 은밀한 슬픔의 내력을 통째로 읽어 냈다.\n\n\"백작 부인은,\" 모르세르가 말했다. \"맞이할 손님이 온다는 전갈을 받았을 때 마침 몸단장을 하고 있었습니다. 그래도 십 분이면 응접실로 나올 겁니다.\"\n\n\"제게는 더없는 영광입니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"파리에 도착한 첫날에, 명성에 걸맞은 공적을 지니신 분, 운명이 모처럼 공정하게 대해 준 분과 이렇게 마주하게 되었으니 말입니다. 그런데 운명은 미티자 평원이나 아틀라스 산맥에서 아직 원수의 지휘봉 하나쯤 더 내어 줄 여지가 남아 있지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"오.\" 모르세르가 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했다. \"저는 군에서 물러났습니다, 백작. 왕정복고 때 귀족원 의원이 되었고, 첫 원정에서는 부르몽 원수의 지휘 아래 복무했지요. 그러니 더 높은 지위를 바랄 수도 있었고, 만약 부르봉 본가가 왕좌에 그대로 남아 있었다면 무슨 일이 일어났을지 누가 알겠습니까? 그러나 7월 혁명은 보아하니 배은망덕해도 될 만큼 충분히 영광스러웠던 모양이고, 제정 시대까지 거슬러 올라가지 않는 모든 공적에 대해서는 실제로 그렇게 굴었습니다. 저는 사직서를 냈습니다. 전쟁터에서 견장을 따낸 사람은 응접실의 미끄러운 바닥에서 어찌 처신해야 할지 모르는 법이니까요. 저는 칼을 걸어 두고 정치에 뛰어들었습니다. 산업에 몸을 바쳤지요. 실용적인 기술을 연구합니다. 복무한 이십 년 동안 줄곧 그러고 싶었지만, 시간이 없었습니다.\"\n\n\"바로 그런 생각이 귀국을 다른 어느 나라보다 뛰어나게 만드는 것이지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"높은 가문에서 태어나 넉넉한 재산을 지닌 분이, 이름 없는 병사처럼 한 계단 한 계단 승진을 일구는 데 동의하셨다니, 이는 흔치 않은 일입니다. 그러고는 장군이 되고, 프랑스 귀족원 의원이 되고, 레지옹 도뇌르 사령관이 되신 분이, 언젠가 동포에게 쓸모 있는 사람이 되리라는 것 외에 다른 어떤 희망도 욕심도 없이 또다시 두 번째 수련을 시작하기로 하셨군요. 이는 참으로 칭송할 만한 일입니다. 아니, 그 이상이지요. 숭고한 일입니다.\"\n\n알베르는 놀란 얼굴로 지켜보며 귀를 기울였다. 그는 몬테크리스토가 이렇게 열정을 터뜨리는 모습을 본 적이 없었다.\n\n\"아아.\" 이방인이 말을 이었다. 분명 모르세르의 이마에 드리운 옅은 그늘을 걷어 내려는 뜻이었으리라. \"이탈리아에서는 우리가 이렇게 행동하지 않습니다. 우리는 혈통과 종에 따라 자라고, 평생토록 같은 길을, 흔히 같은 무용함을 좇을 따름이지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 백작,\" 모르세르 백작이 말했다. \"백작 같은 공적을 지닌 분에게 이탈리아는 나라라고 할 수 없습니다. 프랑스는 두 팔을 벌려 백작을 맞이할 겁니다. 그 부름에 응해 주십시오. 프랑스가 언제까지나 배은망덕하지는 않을 겁니다. 자기 자식들은 박대하지만, 이방인은 늘 환대하는 나라니까요.\"\n\n\"아, 아버지.\" 알베르가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"아버지가 몬테크리스토 백작님을 모르신다는 게 분명하군요. 백작님은 온갖 명예를 경멸하시고, 여권에 적힌 명예 하나로 만족하시는 분입니다.\"\n\n\"제 자신에 관해 들어 본 말 가운데,\" 이방인이 대답했다. \"가장 옳은 평이군요.\"\n\n\"백작께서는 자유로이 길을 선택하실 수 있었군요.\" 모르세르 백작이 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"그리고 꽃이 흩뿌려진 길을 고르셨고요.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다, 백작.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. 화가도 결코 그려 낼 수 없고 생리학자도 분석해 낼 수 없을 그런 미소를 띤 채였다.\n\n\"피곤하게 해 드릴까 염려되지 않는다면,\" 장군이 말했다. 백작의 처신에 분명 매료된 기색이었다. \"의사당으로 모시고 싶습니다. 오늘 우리 근대 상원 의원들을 낯설어하는 분들에게는 무척 흥미로운 토론이 있거든요.\"\n\n\"훗날 언젠가 그 제안을 다시 해 주신다면 더없이 감사하겠습니다, 백작. 하지만 저는 백작 부인께 소개받으리라는 기대에 마음이 부풀어 있으니, 그때까지 기다리겠습니다.\"\n\n\"아, 어머니가 오셨군요.\" 자작이 외쳤다.\n\n몬테크리스토가 황급히 돌아보니, 남편이 들어온 문 맞은편 문, 응접실 입구에 모르세르 부인이 창백하게 굳은 채 서 있었다. 몬테크리스토가 돌아보자, 그녀는 까닭 모를 이유로 금박 입힌 문기둥에 기대고 있던 팔을 떨구었다. 그녀는 얼마간 그곳에 서서, 손님이 한 마지막 말을 듣고 있었던 것이다. 백작이 일어나 부인에게 인사를 건넸고, 부인은 아무 말 없이 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"아! 이런, 부인.\" 백작이 말했다. \"어디 편찮으신지요, 아니면 방 안의 열기 때문에 그러신지요?\"\n\n\"편찮으세요, 어머니?\" 자작이 어머니에게 달려가며 외쳤다.\n\n그녀는 미소로 두 사람에게 고마움을 표했다.\n\n\"아니다.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"다만 당신의 도움이 없었더라면 우리가 눈물과 비탄에 잠겼을, 그분을 처음으로 뵙게 되니 마음이 좀 벅차오르는구나. 백작님.\" 부인이 여왕 같은 위엄으로 다가서며 말을 이었다. \"저는 백작님께 제 아들의 목숨을 빚졌고, 그래서 백작님께 축복을 빕니다. 이제 마음 깊은 곳에서부터, 축복을 빌었던 것처럼 감사를 드릴 기회를 이렇게 마련해 주시니, 그 기쁨에 감사드립니다.\"\n\n백작이 다시 고개를 숙였다. 이번에는 아까보다 더 깊이 숙였다. 그의 얼굴은 메르세데스보다도 더 창백했다.\n\n\"부인.\" 그가 말했다. \"백작님과 부인께서는 보잘것없는 행동에 너무도 후한 보답을 해 주십니다. 한 사람의 목숨을 구하고, 한 아버지의 마음과 한 어머니의 애틋함을 지켜 드린 일은, 선행이라 할 것도 못 되고 그저 인간으로서의 도리일 뿐입니다.\"\n\n더없이 그윽한 다정함과 정중함으로 내뱉은 이 말에, 모르세르 부인이 대답했다.\n\n\"제 아들이 그런 친구분을 만났다니 참으로 다행입니다, 백작님. 일이 이렇게 되도록 해 주신 하느님께 감사드립니다.\"\n\n그러면서 메르세데스는 어찌나 간절한 감사의 표정으로 아름다운 눈을 하늘로 들어 올렸던지, 백작은 그 눈에 눈물이 어린 것을 본 듯했다. 모르세르 백작이 부인에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"부인.\" 그가 말했다. \"백작께 자리를 떠야 한다고 이미 양해를 구했으니, 당신도 부디 양해해 주오. 회의가 두 시에 시작되는데 지금 세 시요. 게다가 내가 발언을 해야 하오.\"\n\n\"그럼 다녀오세요. 백작님과 저는 당신이 없는 빈자리를 잊으려 애써 보겠어요.\" 부인이 여전히 깊은 감정이 어린 어조로 대답했다. \"백작님.\" 그녀가 몬테크리스토를 향해 말을 이었다. \"남은 하루를 저희와 함께 보내 주시는 영광을 베풀어 주시겠어요?\"\n\n\"믿어 주십시오, 부인. 그 친절에 더없이 감사드립니다. 하지만 오늘 아침 댁의 문 앞에서 여행 마차에서 내린 터라, 거의 알지도 못하는 이 파리에서 제가 어떻게 자리를 잡았는지조차 모릅니다. 사소한 걱정거리라는 건 압니다만, 헤아려 주실 만한 일이기도 하지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 다음 기회에 그 기쁨을 누리겠습니다.\" 부인이 말했다. \"약속해 주시는 거지요?\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 대답 없이 고개를 숙였으나, 그 몸짓은 승낙으로 받아들일 만했다.\n\n\"붙들지 않겠습니다, 백작님.\" 부인이 말을 이었다. \"저희의 감사가 무례하거나 성가신 것이 되기를 바라지 않으니까요.\"\n\n\"백작님.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"로마에서 받은 친절에 보답하고자, 백작님의 마차가 마련될 때까지 제 이륜마차를 마음껏 쓰시도록 내어 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n\"친절은 정말 고맙습니다, 자작님.\" 몬테크리스토 백작이 대답했다. \"하지만 베르투치오가 제가 준 네 시간 반을 알맞게 썼을 테니, 문 앞에 마차 한 대쯤은 채비되어 있으리라 봅니다.\"\n\n알베르는 백작의 일 처리 방식에 익숙했다. 그는 백작이 네로처럼 불가능한 것을 찾아 나서는 사람임을 알았기에 무엇에도 놀라지 않았다. 다만 백작의 지시가 얼마나 이행되었는지 제 눈으로 가늠해 보고 싶어, 집 문간까지 백작을 배웅했다. 몬테크리스토의 짐작은 빗나가지 않았다. 그가 모르세르 백작의 대기실에 모습을 드러내자마자, 로마에서 두 젊은이에게 백작의 명함을 가져다주며 그의 방문을 알렸던 바로 그 하인이 현관으로 달려 나갔다. 그리하여 이 저명한 여행자가 문에 이르렀을 때, 마차가 그를 기다리고 있었다. 콜러가 만든 이륜마차였고, 거기 매인 말과 마구로 말하자면, 어제 드레이크가 칠백 기니를 거절했다는 사실을 파리의 멋쟁이라면 누구나 알고 있는 것이었다.\n\n\"자작님.\" 백작이 알베르에게 말했다. \"제 집까지 함께 가자고 청하지는 않겠습니다. 급하게 꾸민 거처밖에 보여 드릴 게 없으니까요. 게다가 아시다시피 저는 누구에게도 허를 잡히지 않는다는 평판을 지켜야 하는 몸입니다. 그러니 자작님을 초대하기 전에 하루만 더 주십시오. 그러면 손님 대접에 빈틈이 없으리라 확신할 수 있을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"하루를 청하신다면, 백작님, 무엇을 기대해야 할지 알 만합니다. 제가 보게 될 것은 집이 아니라 궁전이겠지요. 백작님은 분명 마음대로 부리는 무슨 천재라도 거느리고 계신 모양입니다.\"\n\n\"맙소사, 그 소문이나 좀 퍼뜨려 주시지요.\" 몬테크리스토 백작이 화려한 마차의 벨벳을 댄 발판에 발을 올리며 대답했다. \"그러면 부인네들 사이에서 제법 득이 될 테니까요.\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 그는 마차에 훌쩍 올랐고, 문이 닫혔다. 그러나 그리 빠르지는 않아서, 몬테크리스토는 자신이 모르세르 부인을 남겨 두고 온 그 방의 커튼이 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 미세하게 흔들리는 것을 놓치지 않았다.\n\n알베르가 어머니에게 돌아가 보니, 그녀는 규방의 커다란 벨벳 안락의자에 기대어 있었다. 방 안이 어찌나 어두침침했던지, 휘장 여기저기에 박힌 반짝이 장식과 그림의 금박 액자 모서리만이 어둠 속에서 어느 정도 빛을 내고 있을 뿐이었다. 알베르는 어머니의 얼굴을 볼 수 없었다. 머리에 쓴 얇은 베일이 안개처럼 주름지며 이목구비를 덮고 있었기 때문이다. 그래도 어머니의 목소리가 달라진 것만은 느껴졌다. 그는 화분대에 놓인 장미와 헬리오트로프의 향내 가운데서 휘발성 소금의 톡 쏘는 향긋한 냄새를 가려낼 수 있었다. 또 벽난로 위에 놓인 무늬 새긴 잔 하나에, 가죽 상자에서 꺼낸 어머니의 각성제 병이 담겨 있는 것을 알아챘다. 그는 방에 들어서며 불안한 어조로 외쳤다.\n\n\"어머니, 제가 없는 동안 편찮으셨어요?\"\n\n\"아니다, 아니야, 알베르. 하지만 이 장미며 월하향이며 오렌지꽃이라는 게, 익숙해지기 전에는 처음엔 향이 어찌나 독한지 알잖니.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 어머니.\" 알베르가 종에 손을 가져가며 말했다. \"이것들을 대기실로 내가야겠어요. 정말로 편찮으신 거예요. 방금 들어오실 때도 그렇게 창백하셨으니까요.\"\n\n\"내가 창백했니, 알베르?\"\n\n\"네. 어머니께 더없이 잘 어울리는 창백함이었지만, 그래도 아버지와 저를 적잖이 놀라게 한 창백함이었어요.\"\n\n\"네 아버지가 그 얘기를 하시더냐?\" 메르세데스가 다급히 물었다.\n\n\"아니요, 어머니. 그래도 아버지가 어머니께 그 얘기를 하셨던 걸 기억하지 못하세요?\"\n\n\"그래, 기억나는구나.\" 부인이 대답했다.\n\n알베르가 종을 울리자 하인이 들어왔다.\n\n\"이 꽃들을 대기실이나 화장실로 옮기게.\" 자작이 말했다. \"이것 때문에 백작 부인이 편찮으시니.\"\n\n하인이 분부에 따랐다. 꽃이 모두 치워질 때까지 긴 침묵이 이어졌다.\n\n\"몬테크리스토라는 이름은 무슨 뜻이니?\" 하인이 마지막 꽃병을 들고 나가자 부인이 물었다. \"가문의 성이냐, 아니면 영지의 이름이냐, 그도 아니면 그저 칭호일 뿐이냐?\"\n\n\"제 생각엔, 어머니, 그저 칭호인 듯합니다. 백작은 토스카나 군도의 섬 하나를 사들였고, 오늘 어머니께도 말씀드렸듯이 기사단을 하나 세웠지요. 피렌체의 성 스테파노 기사단이나, 파르마의 성 게오르기우스 콘스탄티누스 기사단, 심지어 몰타 기사단을 위해서도 같은 일이 이루어졌다는 걸 아실 겁니다. 이것 말고는 귀족 행세를 할 만한 게 전혀 없어서, 스스로를 우연히 된 백작이라고 부른답니다. 그래도 로마에서는 백작이 대단히 지체 높은 인물이라는 게 중론이지요.\"\n\n\"몸가짐이 훌륭하더구나.\" 부인이 말했다. \"적어도 잠깐 머무는 동안 내가 본 바로는 말이다.\"\n\n\"완벽하지요, 어머니. 어찌나 완벽한지, 유럽에서 가장 자존심 강한 세 귀족 집단—영국과 에스파냐와 독일 귀족—의 최상류층에서 제가 보아 온 누구도 그를 한참 따라가지 못할 정도입니다.\"\n\n부인은 잠시 말을 멈췄다. 그러더니 약간 머뭇거린 뒤 다시 입을 열었다.\n\n\"알베르, 네가 봤잖니—어미로서 묻는 거란다—너는 몬테크리스토 백작의 집에서 그를 봤고, 너는 눈썰미가 좋고 세상 물정에도 밝으며, 나이에 비해 보기 드물게 분별이 있는 아이지. 너는 그 백작이 정말로 겉보기 그대로의 사람이라고 생각하니?\"\n\n\"겉보기에는 어떤 사람인데요?\"\n\n\"아니, 방금 네가 말하지 않았니. 지체 높은 인물이라고.\"\n\n\"그렇게 평가받는다고 말씀드린 거지요, 어머니.\"\n\n\"그럼 네 생각은 어떠니, 알베르?\"\n\n\"솔직히 말씀드리면 그에 관해서는 아직 확실한 생각을 정하지 못했습니다. 다만 몰타 사람이 아닐까 합니다.\"\n\n\"출신을 묻는 게 아니라, 어떤 사람이냐고 묻는 거란다.\"\n\n\"아! 어떤 사람이냐, 그건 또 전혀 다른 이야기지요. 그에게서 워낙 비범한 점을 많이 보아서, 제 생각을 정말로 말씀드리자면, 저는 그가 바이런이 그린 영웅들 가운데 하나라고 봅니다. 불운이 치명적인 낙인을 찍어 놓은 인물 말입니다. 어떤 만프레드, 어떤 라라, 어떤 베르너 같은. 말하자면 어느 오래된 가문의 잔해 같은 사람이지요. 물려받을 유산을 빼앗겼지만, 모험을 좇는 천재성의 힘으로 스스로 유산을 일구어 내어 사회의 법 위에 올라선 그런 사람 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그러니까 네 말은—\"\n\"제 말은, 몬테크리스토가 지중해 한복판에 있는 섬이라는 겁니다. 주민도 수비대도 없고, 온갖 나라의 밀수꾼과 온갖 깃발을 단 해적들이 드나드는 곳이지요. 그 부지런한 작자들이 자기들을 지켜 주는 대가로 봉건 영주에게 무슨 세금이라도 바치고 있는지 누가 알겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그럴 수도 있겠구나.\" 부인이 생각에 잠겨 말했다.\n\n\"아무튼.\" 젊은이가 말을 이었다. \"밀수꾼이든 아니든, 직접 보셨으니 어머니도 인정하실 겁니다. 몬테크리스토 백작은 비범한 사람이고, 파리 사교계에서 큰 성공을 거둘 거예요. 아니, 바로 오늘 아침에도 제 방에서, 그가 우리 사이에 등장하면서 사람들을 하나같이 경탄케 했지요. 샤토르노조차 예외가 아니었습니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 백작의 나이가 몇쯤이라고 생각하니?\" 메르세데스가 물었다. 이 질문에 큰 비중을 두는 것이 분명했다.\n\n\"서른다섯이나 서른여섯쯤요, 어머니.\"\n\n\"그렇게 젊다니—그럴 리가 없다.\" 메르세데스가 말했다. 알베르의 말에 대한 대답이면서 동시에 자신의 속생각에 대한 대답이기도 했다.\n\n\"하지만 사실인걸요. 그가 서너 번이나 제게 말했어요. 분명 아무 꿍꿍이 없이요. '그 무렵 나는 다섯 살이었지', '또 그 무렵엔 열 살', '또 그 무렵엔 열두 살'이라고요. 저는 그런 사소한 것에 호기심이 일어 자꾸 마음이 쏠렸고, 그 날짜들을 맞춰 보았는데 한 번도 어긋난 적이 없었습니다. 나이를 가늠할 수 없는 이 묘한 사람의 나이는, 그러니까 저는 서른다섯이 틀림없다고 봅니다. 게다가 어머니, 그 눈이 얼마나 형형한지, 머리카락이 얼마나 까마귀처럼 새카만지 보세요. 그토록 창백한데도 이마에는 주름 하나 없으니, 정정할 뿐 아니라 젊기까지 한 사람입니다.\"\n\n부인은 쓰디쓴 상념의 물결에 짓눌리기라도 한 듯 고개를 떨구었다.\n\n\"그래서 그 사람이 네게 우정을 보이더냐, 알베르?\" 그녀가 신경질적으로 몸을 떨며 물었다.\n\n\"그런 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼—너는—그를—좋아하니?\"\n\n\"네, 그가 마음에 듭니다. 그를 저세상에서 돌아온 존재라고 저더러 믿게 하려는 프란츠 데피네에도 불구하고 말이지요.\"\n\n부인이 흠칫 몸을 떨었다.\n\n\"알베르.\" 그녀가 감정에 겨워 달라진 목소리로 말했다. \"나는 늘 너에게 새로 사귄 사람을 조심하라고 일러 왔다. 이제 너는 어른이 되었고 도리어 나에게 조언을 해 줄 수 있는 나이지. 그래도 다시 한번 당부하마, 알베르. 신중해야 한다.\"\n\n\"하지만 어머니, 어머니의 조언이 쓸모가 있으려면, 제가 무엇을 경계해야 하는지를 미리 알아야 하지 않겠습니까. 백작은 노름을 전혀 하지 않고, 셰리주를 살짝 탄 맹물만 마시며, 어찌나 부자인지 저를 비웃을 작정이 아니고서야 제게 돈을 빌리려 들 리도 없습니다. 그러니 제가 그에게서 무엇을 두려워해야 하지요?\"\n\n\"네 말이 옳다.\" 부인이 말했다. \"내 두려움은 부질없는 약한 마음일 뿐이지. 더구나 네 목숨을 구해 준 사람을 두고 그러다니. 알베르, 네 아버지는 그를 어떻게 맞으셨니? 우리는 백작에게 그저 정중한 것 이상으로 대해야 한다. 모르세르 백작은 가끔 일에 골몰하시고, 사업 때문에 생각에 잠기곤 하시니, 본의 아니게—\"\n\n\"아버지의 처신은 더없이 흠잡을 데 없었습니다, 어머니.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"아니, 그 이상이었지요. 백작이 아주 능숙하고 기분 좋게 두세 마디 칭찬을 건넸는데, 마치 삼십 년이나 알고 지낸 사이처럼 거침이 없더군요. 아버지가 무척 우쭐해하시는 듯했습니다. 그 간질간질한 작은 화살들이 하나하나 아버지의 마음을 흡족하게 했을 거예요.\" 알베르가 웃으며 덧붙였다. \"그렇게 두 분은 더없이 좋은 친구가 되어 헤어졌고, 모르세르 백작은 연설을 들으러 의사당으로 그를 데려가려고까지 하셨답니다.\"\n\n부인은 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그녀는 어찌나 깊은 상념에 빠져들었던지 눈이 차츰 감겼다. 젊은이는 어머니 앞에 선 채로, 아직 젊고 아름다운 어머니를 둔 자식만이 품는 그 애틋하고 다정한 효심으로 어머니를 바라보았다. 그러고는 어머니의 눈이 감기고 숨소리가 잔잔해지는 것을 보고 잠이 든 줄로 여겨, 발끝으로 살그머니 방을 나섰다. 그러고는 더없이 조심스럽게 문을 닫았다.\n\n\"이 빌어먹을 사내 같으니.\" 그가 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 중얼거렸다. \"그때 내가 말했지, 그가 여기서 돌풍을 일으킬 거라고. 그 효과를 나는 어김없는 온도계로 가늠하는 거야. 어머니가 그를 주목하셨으니, 그는 어쩔 수 없이 비범한 사람일 수밖에 없지.\"\n\n그는 마구간으로 내려갔다. 몬테크리스토 백작이 손에 넣은 그 마차 한 벌 때문에, 감식가들의 평가에서 자신의 밤색 말들이 이류로 밀려났다는 사실이 떠올라 약간의 언짢음이 없지 않았다.\n\n\"확실히,\" 그가 말했다. \"사람은 평등하지 않아. 아버지께 이 정리를 귀족원에서 한번 펼쳐 보시라고 청해야겠군.\"\n\n# Chapter 42. Monsieur Bertuccio\n\n그사이 백작은 자기 집에 도착했다. 거리를 지나는 데 6분이 걸렸지만, 그 6분만으로도 직접 마차를 사들일 형편이 못 되었던 젊은이 스무 명이 말을 전속력으로 몰아, 한 필에 2만 프랑씩 주고 말을 살 수 있는 그 부유한 이방인을 보려고 따라붙기에 충분했다.\n\n알리가 골라, 몬테크리스토의 시내 거처가 될 그 집은 샹젤리제를 올라가다 보면 오른편에 자리하고 있었다. 한가운데에는 나무와 관목이 빽빽이 우거져 정면의 일부를 가렸다. 이 수풀을 둘러싸고 두 갈래 길이 마치 두 팔처럼 좌우로 뻗어, 철문에서부터 이중 현관에 이르는 마찻길을 이루었으며, 그 계단마다 꽃이 가득 담긴 도자기 화병이 놓여 있었다. 다른 집들과 동떨어진 이 저택은 정문 외에도 퐁티외 거리 쪽으로 또 하나의 출입구가 있었다.\n마부가 문지기를 부르기도 전에 육중한 대문이 경첩을 돌며 활짝 열렸다. 백작이 오는 것을 미리 본 것이었다. 파리에서도, 다른 어디서나 그렇듯, 그는 번개 같은 신속함으로 시중을 받았다. 마부는 속도를 늦추지 않고 반원형 진입로를 가로질러 들어갔고, 바퀴가 자갈 위에서 멎기도 전에 대문이 닫혔다. 마차가 현관 왼편에 멈추자 두 사람이 마차 창가로 다가왔다. 한 사람은 알리였다. 그는 더없이 진심 어린 기쁨이 어린 미소를 지었는데, 몬테크리스토가 한번 눈길을 던지는 것만으로도 충분히 보상받은 듯했다. 다른 한 사람은 공손히 허리를 숙이고는 백작이 내리는 것을 돕기 위해 팔을 내밀었다.\n\n\"고맙네, 베르투치오 씨.\" 백작이 현관 계단 세 개를 가볍게 뛰어오르며 말했다. \"공증인은?\"\n\n\"작은 응접실에 계십니다, 각하.\" 베르투치오가 대답했다.\n\n\"집 번지가 정해지는 대로 새기라고 일러 둔 명함은?\"\n\n\"각하, 이미 다 되었습니다. 제가 직접 팔레루아얄 최고의 각인사를 찾아가, 제 눈앞에서 동판을 새기게 했습니다. 분부하신 대로 가장 먼저 찍어 낸 명함은 쇼세당탱 거리 7번지의 당글라르 남작께 보냈고, 나머지는 각하의 침실 벽난로 위에 놓여 있습니다.\"\n\n\"좋아. 지금 몇 시인가?\"\n\n\"네 시입니다.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 모르세르 백작 댁에서 마차를 불러 주었던 그 프랑스인 하인에게 모자와 지팡이, 장갑을 건네고는, 길을 안내하는 베르투치오를 앞세워 작은 응접실로 들어갔다.\n\n\"이 대기실의 대리석은 영 변변치가 않군.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"이것들도 곧 다 치웠으면 좋겠네.\"\n\n베르투치오가 허리를 숙였다. 집사가 말한 대로 공증인은 작은 응접실에서 그를 기다리고 있었다. 그는 시골 대서인이라는 분에 넘치는 자리에까지 오른, 어디서나 볼 법한 평범한 법률 사무원이었다.\n\n\"내가 사려는 시골집을 매각하도록 위임받은 공증인이 그대인가?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다.\n\n\"그렇습니다, 백작님.\" 공증인이 대답했다.\n\n\"매매 증서는 준비됐나?\"\n\n\"네, 백작님.\"\n\n\"가져왔나?\"\n\n\"여기 있습니다.\"\n\n\"좋아. 그래, 내가 사는 그 집이 어디에 있지?\" 백작이 무심하게 물었다. 절반은 베르투치오에게, 절반은 공증인에게 던지는 말이었다. 집사는 \"저는 모릅니다\"라는 뜻의 몸짓을 했다. 공증인이 놀란 눈으로 백작을 바라보았다.\n\n\"아니!\" 그가 말했다. \"백작님은 사들이시는 집이 어디 있는지도 모르신단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"모르네.\" 백작이 대답했다.\n\n\"백작님이 모르신다고요?\"\n\n\"내가 어찌 알겠나? 오늘 아침에 카디스에서 막 왔네. 파리에는 처음이고, 프랑스 땅에 발을 디딘 것조차 이번이 처음일세.\"\n\n\"아, 그러시다면 사정이 다르군요. 사들이시는 집은 오퇴유에 있습니다.\"\n\n그 말에 베르투치오의 얼굴이 새파랗게 질렸다.\n\n\"오퇴유는 어디 있나?\" 백작이 물었다.\n\n\"바로 이 근처입니다, 백작님.\" 공증인이 대답했다. \"파시를 조금 지나서, 불로뉴 숲 한복판에 자리한 더없이 운치 있는 곳입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇게 가까운가?\" 백작이 말했다. \"그럼 시골이라고 할 수 없지 않나. 무슨 생각으로 파리 코앞에 있는 집을 고른 건가, 베르투치오 씨?\"\n\n\"제가요.\" 집사가 묘한 표정으로 외쳤다. \"이 집을 사라고 분부하신 적은 없으십니다. 각하께서 떠올려 보시면, 곰곰이 생각해 보시면……\"\n\n\"아, 그렇지.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"이제 기억나는군. 어느 신문에서 광고를 읽었는데, '시골집'이라는 그릇된 표제에 끌렸던 거야.\"\n\n\"아직 늦지 않았습니다.\" 베르투치오가 간절하게 외쳤다. \"각하께서 제게 일을 맡겨 주신다면, 앙기앵이나 퐁트네오로즈, 벨뷔에서 더 나은 집을 찾아 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n\"아니, 됐네.\" 몬테크리스토가 대수롭지 않게 대꾸했다. \"이미 이걸 샀으니, 그냥 갖겠네.\"\n\n\"옳은 말씀이십니다.\" 수수료를 잃을까 봐 마음을 졸이던 공증인이 말했다. \"샘물이 풍부하고 나무도 훌륭한, 더없이 운치 있는 곳입니다. 오래 비워 두긴 했어도 살기에 편안한 집이지요. 게다가 가구도 따로 셈해야 합니다. 오래된 것들이긴 하나, 요즘처럼 골동품을 그토록 찾는 시절에는 값진 물건들이지요. 백작님께서도 요즘 취향을 가지고 계시리라 봅니다만?\"\n\n\"물론이지.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"그래, 살기에 아주 편한가?\"\n\n\"편한 정도가 아니라, 더없이 훌륭합니다.\"\n\n\"이런! 그런 기회를 놓칠 수야 없지.\" 몬테크리스토가 대꾸했다. \"증서를 주게, 공증인.\"\n\n그러고는 집의 위치와 소유주들의 이름이 적힌 부분을 먼저 한번 훑어본 뒤, 빠르게 서명했다.\n\n\"베르투치오.\" 그가 말했다. \"이분께 5만 5천 프랑을 드리게.\"\n\n집사는 비틀거리는 걸음으로 방을 나갔다가 지폐 한 다발을 들고 돌아왔고, 공증인은 돈이 모두 거기 있다는 걸 확인하기 전에는 결코 영수증을 써 주지 않는 사람답게 그것을 세었다.\n\n\"자, 이제.\" 백작이 물었다. \"모든 절차가 다 끝났나?\"\n\n\"전부 끝났습니다.\"\n\n\"열쇠는 가지고 있나?\"\n\n\"집을 관리하는 문지기가 가지고 있습니다만, 여기 백작님을 새 거처에 맞아들이도록 그에게 내린 지시서가 있습니다.\"\n\n\"좋아.\" 그러고는 몬테크리스토가 공증인에게 손짓을 했는데, \"이제 그대에게 볼일이 없으니 가도 좋다\"는 뜻이었다.\n\n\"그런데,\" 정직한 공증인이 말했다. \"백작님께서 착각하신 듯합니다. 모든 걸 포함해 5만 프랑입니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 그대의 수수료는?\"\n\n\"그 금액에 포함되어 있습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 그대가 오퇴유에서 여기까지 오지 않았나?\"\n\n\"네, 그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 시간을 들이고 수고한 값을 받는 것이 마땅하지.\" 백작이 말하고는, 정중하게 물러가라는 몸짓을 했다.\n\n공증인은 뒷걸음질로, 땅에 닿을 듯 허리를 숙이며 방을 나갔다. 이런 손님을 만난 것은 그로서도 난생처음이었다.\n\n\"이분을 배웅해 드리게.\" 백작이 베르투치오에게 말했다. 그러자 집사가 공증인을 따라 방을 나갔다.\n\n혼자 남게 되자마자 백작은 자물쇠로 잠긴 수첩을 호주머니에서 꺼내, 목에 걸고 다니며 한시도 몸에서 떼어 놓지 않는 열쇠로 그것을 열었다. 잠시 뒤적이던 그는 여러 메모가 적힌 한 장에서 멈추어, 탁자 위에 놓인 매매 증서와 그것을 견주어 보며 옛 기억을 더듬었다.\n\n\"'오퇴유, 라퐁텐 거리 28번지.' 과연 같은 곳이군.\" 그가 말했다. \"그런데 종교적 두려움이나 육체적 공포 때문에 토해 낸 고백을 과연 믿어도 될까? 어쨌든 한 시간 안에 모든 걸 알게 되겠지. 베르투치오!\" 그가 외치며, 작은 징을 낭창낭창한 자루가 달린 가벼운 망치로 두드렸다. \"베르투치오!\"\n\n집사가 문가에 나타났다.\n\n\"베르투치오 씨.\" 백작이 말했다. \"전에 프랑스를 여행한 적이 있다고 하지 않았나?\"\n\n\"프랑스 몇몇 지방은요, 네, 각하.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 파리 근교도 알겠군?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 각하, 모릅니다.\" 집사가 일종의 신경질적인 떨림과 함께 대답했다. 온갖 감정을 꿰뚫어 보는 데 일가견이 있는 몬테크리스토는 그것이 깊은 불안에서 나온 것임을 정확히 알아챘다.\n\n\"안타깝군.\" 그가 대꾸했다. \"자네가 근교에 가 본 적이 없다니 말이야. 오늘 저녁에 내 새 영지를 보러 갈 참인데, 자네가 함께 갔더라면 쓸 만한 정보를 좀 일러 줄 수 있었을 텐데.\"\n\n\"오퇴유라니요!\" 베르투치오가 외쳤다. 그 구릿빛 얼굴이 납빛으로 질렸다. \"제가 오퇴유에 간다고요?\"\n\n\"그게 뭐가 그리 놀랄 일인가? 내가 오퇴유에 살게 되면, 자네는 내 밑에서 일하는 사람이니 그곳에 와야지.\"\n\n베르투치오는 주인의 위압적인 눈빛 앞에서 고개를 떨군 채, 아무 대답도 없이 꼼짝 않고 서 있었다.\n\n\"왜 그러나, 무슨 일이 있나? 내가 마차를 두 번 부르게 만들 작정인가?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다. 루이 14세가 그 유명한 \"나는 하마터면 기다릴 뻔했다\"라는 말을 내뱉던 바로 그런 어조였다. 베르투치오는 단숨에 대기실로 뛰쳐나가 쉰 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"각하의 말들을 대령하라!\"\n\n몬테크리스토가 편지 두세 통을 쓰고 마지막 편지를 봉인하고 있을 때, 집사가 다시 나타났다.\n\n\"각하의 마차가 문 앞에 대령했습니다.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"좋아, 그럼 모자와 장갑을 챙기게.\" 몬테크리스토가 대꾸했다.\n\n\"제가 모시고 가야 합니까, 각하?\" 베르투치오가 외쳤다.\n\n\"물론이지, 자네가 지시를 내려야 하지 않나. 나는 그 집에서 지낼 작정이니까.\"\n\n백작의 명을 감히 거역하는 하인이란 일찍이 없던 일이었다. 그래서 집사는 한마디도 없이 주인을 따랐다. 주인은 마차에 올라 그에게 따라오라고 손짓했고, 그는 공손히 앞쪽 자리에 올라앉았다.\n\n# Chapter 43. The House at Auteuil\n\n몬테크리스토는 계단을 내려가면서 베르투치오가 코르시카식으로 성호를 긋는 것을 알아챘다. 엄지손가락으로 허공에 십자를 그리는 그 방식으로, 마차에 올라앉으면서도 짤막한 기도를 중얼거렸다. 끝없는 지식욕에 사로잡힌 사람이 아니었다면, 백작이 성 밖으로 마차를 몰겠다는 계획에 집사가 보이는 유별난 거부감을 보고 측은한 마음이 들었을 것이다. 그러나 백작은 너무도 호기심이 많아 이 자그마한 여정에서 베르투치오를 놓아줄 생각이 없었다. 이십 분 만에 그들은 오퇴유에 도착했다. 마을로 들어서는 동안 집사의 동요는 점점 더 커져 갔다. 마차 구석에 웅크린 베르투치오는 지나치는 집들을 하나하나 열에 들뜬 불안으로 살피기 시작했다.\n\n\"퐁텐가 28번지에 세우라고 일러라.\" 백작이 집사에게 시선을 고정한 채 이렇게 명령했다.\n\n베르투치오의 이마는 땀으로 뒤덮였다. 그래도 그는 명령에 따라 창밖으로 몸을 내밀고 마부에게 외쳤다. \"퐁텐가 28번지로!\" 28번지는 마을 끝자락에 자리하고 있었다. 마차를 달리는 동안 밤이 내려앉았고, 어둠은 주변 풍경에 무대 위 한 장면 같은 인위적인 모습을 입혔다. 마차가 멈추자 하인이 마부석에서 뛰어내려 문을 열었다.\n\n\"이런,\" 백작이 말했다. \"내리지 않는 건가, 베르투치오 씨. 그래, 마차 안에 그대로 있을 셈인가? 오늘 저녁 대체 무슨 생각을 하고 있는 거지?\"\n\n베르투치오는 뛰어내려 백작에게 어깨를 내밀었고, 이번에는 백작이 그 어깨에 기대어 마차의 세 계단을 내려왔다.\n\n\"문을 두드리고,\" 백작이 말했다. \"내가 왔다고 알려라.\"\n\n베르투치오가 문을 두드리자 문이 열리고 문지기가 나타났다.\n\n\"무슨 일이오?\" 문지기가 물었다.\n\n\"당신의 새 주인이시오, 친구.\" 하인이 말했다. 그러고는 공증인의 서류를 문지기에게 내밀었다.\n\n\"그럼 집이 팔렸단 말이오?\" 문지기가 물었다. \"그리고 이 양반이 여기 와서 사신다는 거고?\"\n\n\"그렇다네, 친구.\" 백작이 대답했다. \"옛 주인을 그리워할 일이 없도록 애써 보겠네.\"\n\n\"아, 나리,\" 문지기가 말했다. \"그분을 그리워할 일은 별로 없을 겁니다. 워낙 이곳에 드물게 오셨으니까요. 마지막으로 오신 지가 오 년이나 됐는데, 집을 파신 건 잘하신 일이지요. 아무런 수입도 나지 않는 집이었으니까요.\"\n\n\"옛 주인의 이름이 뭐였나?\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"생메랑 후작이십니다. 아, 사신 값보다 못 받고 파셨을 게 분명합니다.\"\n\n\"생메랑 후작이라!\" 백작이 대꾸했다. \"내가 모르는 이름이 아니로군. 생메랑 후작!\" 그러고는 무언가 생각에 잠긴 듯했다.\n\n\"연로하신 어른이셨지요.\" 문지기가 말을 이었다. \"부르봉 왕가의 충직한 지지자셨고요. 외동딸이 하나 있었는데, 빌포르 씨와 결혼했습니다. 님에서, 나중에는 베르사유에서 검사를 지내신 분이지요.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 베르투치오를 흘끗 보았다. 그는 쓰러지지 않으려 벽에 기대고 있었는데, 그 벽보다 더 하얗게 질려 있었다.\n\n\"그 딸은 이미 죽지 않았나?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다. \"그렇게 들은 것 같은데.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 나리. 이십일 년 전에요. 그 후로는 가엾은 후작님을 세 번도 채 뵙지 못했습니다.\"\n\n\"고맙네, 고마워.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. 집사가 완전히 기진맥진한 것을 보고, 줄을 더 당겼다가는 끊어질 위험이 있다고 판단한 것이다. \"불을 좀 밝혀 주게.\"\n\n\"제가 모셔다 드릴까요, 나리?\"\n\n\"아니, 그럴 것 없네. 베르투치오가 불을 비춰 줄 테니.\"\n\n그러면서 몬테크리스토는 이 말에 금화 두 닢을 곁들였고, 그것은 문지기에게서 감사와 축복의 말을 줄줄이 쏟아져 나오게 했다.\n\n\"아, 나리,\" 문지기가 벽난로 선반과 시렁을 헛되이 뒤져 본 뒤에 말했다. \"초가 한 자루도 없습니다.\"\n\n\"마차 등을 하나 들게, 베르투치오.\" 백작이 말했다. \"그리고 방들을 안내해라.\"\n\n집사는 말없이 명령에 따랐다. 그러나 불을 든 손이 떨리는 모습만 보아도 그가 얼마나 큰 대가를 치르며 복종하는지 쉽게 알 수 있었다. 그들은 제법 넓은 일 층을 둘러보았다. 이 층에는 살롱 하나와 욕실 하나, 침실 둘이 있었다. 침실 하나 가까이에 정원으로 내려가는 나선형 계단이 보였다.\n\n\"아, 여기 따로 계단이 있군.\" 백작이 말했다. \"편리하겠어. 불을 비춰라, 베르투치오 씨, 그리고 앞장서게. 어디로 통하는지 보세.\"\n\n\"나리,\" 베르투치오가 대답했다. \"정원으로 통합니다.\"\n\n\"한데 그걸 어떻게 아나?\"\n\n\"적어도 그래야 마땅하니까요.\"\n\n\"좋아, 그럼 확인해 보세.\"\n\n베르투치오가 한숨을 내쉬며 앞장섰다. 과연 계단은 정원으로 이어졌다. 바깥문 앞에서 집사가 걸음을 멈추었다.\n\n\"계속 가게, 베르투치오 씨.\" 백작이 말했다.\n\n그러나 그 말을 들은 사내는 그 자리에 우두커니 서 있었다. 망연자실하여 넋이 나간 채였다. 초췌한 두 눈은 끔찍한 사건의 흔적이라도 찾는 듯 주위를 두리번거렸고, 움켜쥔 두 손으로는 무시무시한 기억을 떨쳐 내려 안간힘을 쓰는 듯했다.\n\n\"왜 그러나!\" 백작이 재촉했다.\n\n\"아니, 안 됩니다.\" 베르투치오가 안쪽 벽 모퉁이에 등을 내려놓으며 외쳤다. \"안 됩니다, 나리, 이건 불가능합니다. 더는 못 가겠습니다.\"\n\n\"이게 무슨 뜻이지?\" 몬테크리스토의 거역할 수 없는 목소리가 물었다.\n\n\"이걸 보십시오, 나리.\" 집사가 외쳤다. \"이건 예사로운 일이 아닙니다. 집을 사시는데 하필 오퇴유에서 사시고, 오퇴유에서 사시는데 그 집이 하필 퐁텐가 28번지라니요. 아, 어째서 제가 다 말씀드리지 않았을까요? 그랬다면 절 억지로 여기 오게 하지는 않으셨을 텐데. 저는 나리의 집이 이 집이 아닌 다른 집이기를 바랐습니다. 마치 오퇴유에 그 살인이 벌어진 집 말고는 다른 집이 없기라도 한 것처럼요!\"\n\n\"뭐라고, 뭐?\" 몬테크리스토가 갑자기 멈춰 서며 외쳤다. \"지금 무슨 말을 하는 건가? 별난 인간이로군. 역시 코르시카 사람이라 이건가. 늘 알 수 없는 일 아니면 미신 타령이지. 자, 등을 들게, 정원을 둘러보세. 나와 함께라면 유령 따위는 두렵지 않겠지?\"\n\n베르투치오는 등을 들어 올리고 명령에 따랐다. 문이 열리자 음울한 하늘이 드러났다. 달은 구름의 바다를 뚫고 나오려 헛되이 애쓰고 있었다. 구름은 안개의 물결로 달을 뒤덮었고, 달은 한순간 그것을 비추다가 다시 어둠 속으로 가라앉을 뿐이었다. 집사가 왼쪽으로 방향을 틀려 했다.\n\n\"아니, 아니야, 베르투치오.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"오솔길을 따라가서 뭐 하겠나? 여기 아름다운 잔디밭이 있으니 곧장 앞으로 가세.\"\n\n베르투치오는 이마의 땀을 닦으면서도 명령에 따랐다. 그러나 그는 여전히 왼쪽 길을 택했다. 반면 몬테크리스토는 오른쪽 길로 들어섰고, 한 무리의 나무들 가까이에 이르러 걸음을 멈추었다. 집사는 더는 자제하지 못했다.\n\n\"비켜 주십시오, 나리. 거기서 물러나 주십시오, 간청드립니다. 나리는 바로 그 자리에 서 계십니다!\"\n\n\"무슨 자리 말인가?\"\n\n\"그자가 쓰러진 자리요.\"\n\"이보게, 베르투치오 씨.\" 몬테크리스토가 웃으며 말했다. \"정신 좀 차리게. 여기는 사르테네도 코르테도 아니야. 코르시카의 덤불숲이 아니라 영국식 정원이라고. 손질이 엉망인 건 인정하네만, 그렇다고 험담할 것까지는 없지.\"\n\n\"나리, 제발 거기 서 계시지 마십시오!\"\n\n\"내 보기엔 자네가 미쳐 가는 것 같군, 베르투치오.\" 백작이 차갑게 말했다. \"정말 그렇다면 미리 일러두는데, 자네를 정신병원에 처넣을 걸세.\"\n\n\"아아! 나리.\" 베르투치오가 두 손을 맞잡고 머리를 흔들며 대답했다. 그 모습은 백작의 웃음을 자아냈을 법했으나, 백작의 머릿속은 더 큰 관심사로 가득 차 있어, 이 겁먹은 양심이 흘리는 사소한 단서 하나에도 귀를 곤두세우고 있었다. \"아아! 나리, 기어이 화가 닥치고 말았습니다!\"\n\n\"베르투치오 씨.\" 백작이 말했다. \"기꺼이 말해 주지. 자네는 손짓 발짓을 하며 두 손을 비틀고 눈을 굴리는 꼴이 마치 떨어지려 하지 않는 악마에 들린 사람 같아. 그리고 내가 늘 지켜본 바로는, 가장 끈질기게 떨어지지 않는 악마가 바로 비밀이라네. 나는 자네가 코르시카 사람인 걸 알았어. 음울하고 늘 무슨 해묵은 복수극 따위를 곱씹고 있다는 것도 알았지. 하지만 이탈리아에서는 그런 걸 눈감아 주었어. 이탈리아에서는 그런 일을 대수롭지 않게 여기니까. 그러나 프랑스에서는 아주 천박한 취미로 본다네. 그런 일에 매달리는 헌병들이 있고, 단죄하는 판사들이 있고, 복수해 주는 단두대가 있지.\"\n\n베르투치오는 두 손을 움켜쥐었다. 그 모든 몸부림 속에서도 등은 떨어뜨리지 않았기에, 그 불빛이 창백하게 일그러진 그의 얼굴을 드러냈다. 몬테크리스토는 로마에서 안드레아의 처형을 지켜보던 바로 그 눈빛으로 그를 살폈고, 이윽고 가엾은 집사의 핏줄에 전율이 흐르게 하는 어조로 입을 열었다.\n\n\"그렇다면 부소니 신부가 내게 거짓을 말한 셈이군.\" 그가 말했다. \"1829년에 프랑스를 여행한 뒤, 자네의 온갖 훌륭한 자질을 늘어놓은 추천서와 함께 자네를 내게 보냈을 때 말이야. 좋아, 신부에게 편지를 쓰겠네. 자기가 천거한 자의 비행에 대해 책임을 지우고, 이 살인 사건의 전말을 곧 다 알아내겠어. 다만 미리 일러두는데, 나는 어떤 나라에 머물면 그 나라의 모든 법을 따르네. 자네 때문에 프랑스 법의 손아귀에 내 몸을 들이밀 생각은 추호도 없어.\"\n\n\"오, 그러지 마십시오, 나리. 저는 늘 충실히 모셔 왔습니다.\" 베르투치오가 절망하여 외쳤다. \"저는 늘 정직한 사람이었고, 제 힘이 닿는 한 선을 행해 왔습니다.\"\n\n\"부정하지는 않겠네.\" 백작이 대답했다. \"하지만 어째서 이리 동요하는 건가? 좋지 않은 징조야. 떳떳한 양심이라면 사람의 뺨을 이토록 창백하게 만들지도, 두 손을 이토록 열에 들뜨게 하지도 않는 법이지.\"\n\n\"하지만, 나리,\" 베르투치오가 머뭇거리며 대답했다. \"님 감옥에서 제 고해를 들으셨던 부소니 신부님이, 제 양심에 무거운 짐이 얹혀 있다고 말씀드리지 않았습니까?\"\n\n\"그래. 하지만 신부가 자네를 훌륭한 집사가 될 사람이라고 했기에, 나는 자네가 도둑질을 한 모양이라고만 여겼지. 그게 다였어.\"\n\n\"오, 나리!\" 베르투치오가 깊은 모멸감을 드러내며 대답했다.\n\n\"아니면 자네가 코르시카 사람이니, 자네들 말로 누구 하나 '뻣뻣하게 만들고' 싶은 욕망을 못 참았으려니 했지.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 나리.\" 베르투치오가 백작의 발치에 몸을 던지며 외쳤다. \"그건 그저 복수였습니다. 그 외에는 아무것도 아니었습니다.\"\n\n\"그건 알겠어. 하지만 무엇이 자네를 이토록 발작하게 하는지는 모르겠군.\"\n\n\"하지만 나리, 그건 너무도 당연한 일입니다.\" 베르투치오가 대답했다. \"제 복수가 이루어진 곳이 바로 이 집이니까요.\"\n\n\"뭐라고! 내 집이?\"\n\n\"오, 나리, 그땐 나리의 집이 아니었지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 누구 집이었나? 문지기 말로는 생메랑 후작의 집이었다던데. 생메랑 후작에게 무슨 복수를 했단 말인가?\"\n\n\"오, 그분에게 한 게 아닙니다, 나리. 다른 사람에게 한 일이었습니다.\"\n\n\"이상한 일이군.\" 몬테크리스토가 자신의 상념에 잠긴 듯 대꾸했다. \"자네가 그토록 큰 회한을 안겨 준 사건이 벌어진 집에, 아무런 마음의 준비도 없이 들어서게 되다니 말이야.\"\n\n\"나리,\" 집사가 말했다. \"이건 운명입니다, 분명합니다. 우선 나리는 오퇴유에 집을 사셨는데, 이 집이 바로 제가 살인을 저지른 집입니다. 나리는 그자가 내려간 바로 그 계단으로 정원에 내려오셨고, 그자가 칼을 맞은 바로 그 자리에 멈춰 서셨습니다. 그리고 거기서 두 걸음 떨어진 곳에, 그자가 막 제 아이를 묻은 무덤이 있습니다. 이건 우연이 아닙니다. 이런 경우의 우연이란 섭리와 너무도 닮았으니까요.\"\n\n\"좋아, 친애하는 코르시카 양반, 그게 섭리라고 치세. 나는 늘 사람들이 원하는 건 무엇이든 그렇다고 쳐 주거든. 게다가 병든 마음에는 얼마쯤 양보해 줘야 하는 법이지. 자, 정신을 차리고, 내게 모두 말해 보게.\"\n\n\"저는 이 이야기를 단 한 번 했을 뿐입니다. 그것도 부소니 신부님께요. 그런 일은,\" 베르투치오가 머리를 흔들며 말을 이었다. \"고해의 봉인 아래에서만 털어놓는 법입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 백작이 말했다. \"자네를 고해 신부에게 보내 주지. 카르투지오회나 트라피스트회 수도사가 되어 자네 비밀을 털어놓게나. 하지만 나로 말하자면, 그런 헛것에 겁먹는 자는 좋아하지 않아. 그리고 내 하인들이 저녁에 정원을 거니는 것조차 두려워하는 꼴은 보고 싶지 않네. 솔직히 나는 경찰서장의 방문 따위는 별로 반갑지 않아. 이탈리아에서는 정의가 침묵할 때만 값을 치르지만, 프랑스에서는 입을 열 때만 값을 치르는 법이니까. 제기랄! 나는 자네가 코르시카 사람 기질이 좀 있고, 밀수꾼 솜씨가 뛰어나며, 훌륭한 집사라고만 여겼지. 한데 보아하니 자네 활에는 다른 줄도 걸려 있었군. 자네는 이제 내 사람이 아닐세, 베르투치오 씨.\"\n\n\"오, 나리, 나리!\" 그 위협에 공포로 얼어붙은 집사가 외쳤다. \"그것만이 제가 나리를 더 모실 수 없는 이유라면, 모두 말씀드리겠습니다. 나리를 떠나는 건 곧 단두대로 가는 길일 뿐이니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 얘기가 다르지.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"하지만 거짓을 말할 작정이라면, 차라리 입을 다무는 편이 낫다는 걸 명심하게.\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 나리. 구원의 소망을 걸고 맹세하건대, 모두 말씀드리겠습니다. 부소니 신부님조차도 제 비밀의 일부만 아셨으니까요. 하지만 부탁드립니다, 저 플라타너스 나무에서 물러나 주십시오. 마침 달이 구름을 뚫고 나오는데, 나리께서 거기 그렇게 서서 몸을 가리는 그 망토를 두르고 계시니, 빌포르 씨가 떠오릅니다.\"\n\n\"뭐라고!\" 몬테크리스토가 외쳤다. \"빌포르 씨였단 말인가?\"\n\n\"나리께서 그분을 아십니까?\"\n\n\"님의 전직 검사 말인가?\"\n\"그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"생메랑 후작의 딸과 결혼한?\"\n\"그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"가장 엄정하고, 가장 강직하며, 가장 추상같은 법관이라는 평판을 누리던 그 사람?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 나리.\" 베르투치오가 말했다. \"그 흠 하나 없는 평판을 지닌 사내가……\"\n\n\"그래서?\"\n\n\"악당이었습니다.\"\n\n\"쳇,\" 몬테크리스토가 대꾸했다. \"그럴 리가!\"\n\n\"제 말 그대로입니다.\"\n\n\"아, 정말로.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그 증거가 있나?\"\n\n\"있었습니다.\"\n\n\"한데 그걸 잃어버렸다고? 어리석기는!\"\n\n\"그렇습니다. 하지만 잘 찾아보면 되찾을 수 있을지도 모릅니다.\"\n\n\"정말이지,\" 백작이 대꾸했다. \"내게 들려주게. 슬슬 흥미가 동하기 시작하는군.\"\n\n그러고는 백작이 「람메르무어의 루치아」의 한 가락을 흥얼거리며 벤치에 가서 앉았고, 베르투치오는 생각을 가다듬으며 그를 따랐다. 베르투치오는 그의 앞에 선 채로 있었다.\n\n# Chapter 44. The Vendetta\n\n“이야기를 어디서부터 시작할까요, 백작님?” 베르투치오가 물었다.\n\n“자네가 좋은 데서 시작하게. 난 아무것도 모르니까.” 몬테크리스토가 대답했다.\n\n“부소니 신부님께서 백작님께 말씀드린 줄 알았습니다.”\n\n“몇 가지 사정은 들었지. 하지만 칠팔 년 전 일이라 다 잊어버렸네.”\n\n“그렇다면 백작님을 지루하게 할 걱정 없이 말씀드릴 수 있겠군요.”\n\n“계속하게, 베르투치오. 자네가 저녁 신문을 대신해 주는 셈이지.”\n\n“이야기는 1815년에 시작됩니다.”\n\n“아,” 몬테크리스토가 말했다. “1815년이라면 어제오늘 일이 아니로군.”\n\n“네, 백작님. 그런데도 모든 일이 바로 그때 일어난 것처럼 또렷이 기억납니다. 제게는 형이 하나 있었습니다. 손위 형이었지요. 황제 폐하 밑에서 복무했고, 코르시카 사람들로만 이루어진 연대의 중위가 되어 있었습니다. 그 형은 제 유일한 벗이었습니다. 우리는 고아가 되었는데, 저는 다섯 살, 형은 열여덟 살이었지요. 형은 저를 친아들처럼 길러 주었고, 1814년에 결혼했습니다. 황제 폐하가 엘바섬에서 돌아오시자 형은 곧장 군에 합류했고, 워털루에서 가볍게 부상을 입은 뒤 군대를 따라 루아르강 너머로 물러났습니다.”\n\n“하지만 그건 백일천하 이야기 아닌가, 베르투치오.” 백작이 말했다. “내가 틀리지 않았다면, 그건 이미 다 기록되어 있는 일일세.”\n\n“송구합니다, 백작님. 하지만 이런 자세한 사정이 꼭 필요하고, 또 인내해 주시겠다고 약속하셨지 않습니까.”\n\n“계속하게. 약속은 지키지.”\n\n“어느 날 우리는 편지 한 통을 받았습니다. 말씀드리자면 우리는 코르시카곶 끝자락에 있는 로글리아노라는 작은 마을에 살고 있었습니다. 그 편지는 형이 보낸 것이었습니다. 형은 군대가 해산되었으며, 샤토루와 클레르몽페랑, 르퓌, 님을 거쳐 돌아오겠다고 적었습니다. 그리고 만약 제게 돈이 있거든 님에서 제가 거래하던 어느 여관 주인에게 맡겨 두라고 부탁했습니다.”\n\n“밀수 거래 말인가?” 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n“네, 백작님. 사람은 누구나 먹고살아야 하지 않습니까.”\n\n“그렇지. 계속하게.”\n\n“말씀드렸듯이 저는 형을 끔찍이 아꼈습니다. 그래서 돈을 부치는 대신 직접 가져다주기로 마음먹었습니다. 제게는 천 프랑이 있었지요. 그중 오백 프랑은 형수 아순타에게 남겨 두고, 나머지 오백 프랑을 들고 님으로 떠났습니다. 그건 어려운 일이 아니었습니다. 제게는 배가 있었고 바다에서 실어 올 짐도 있었으니, 모든 게 제 계획에 들어맞았지요. 그런데 화물을 다 실은 뒤로 바람이 거꾸로 불어, 나흘이고 닷새고 론강에 들어가지 못했습니다. 하지만 마침내 성공해서 아를까지 거슬러 올라갔습니다. 저는 벨가르드와 보케르 사이에 배를 두고, 님으로 가는 길에 올랐습니다.”\n\n“이제 본 이야기로 들어가는 건가?”\n\n“네, 백작님. 송구합니다만, 보시다시피 저는 꼭 필요한 것만 말씀드리고 있습니다. 마침 그 무렵 프랑스 남부에서 그 악명 높은 학살이 벌어졌습니다. 트레스타용, 트뤼페미, 그라팡이라는 세 악당이 보나파르트파라고 의심되는 사람은 누구든 공공연히 죽였습니다. 백작님께서도 분명 그 학살에 대해 들으셨겠지요?”\n\n“어렴풋이. 그 무렵 나는 프랑스에서 멀리 떨어져 있었네. 계속하게.”\n\n“님에 들어서자 저는 그야말로 피바다 속을 헤치고 다니는 형국이었습니다. 발을 옮길 때마다 시체와, 죽이고 약탈하고 불태우는 살인자 무리와 마주쳤습니다. 그 살육과 참상을 보며 저는 두려움에 휩싸였습니다. 제 자신을 위해서가 아니었지요. 저야 한낱 코르시카 어부에 불과하니 두려울 게 없었고, 오히려 그런 시절은 우리 밀수꾼들에게는 더없이 유리했으니까요. 하지만 형은 제국의 군인이었습니다. 루아르 군대에서 돌아오는 길이었고, 군복과 견장까지 갖추고 있었으니, 모든 게 염려스러웠습니다. 저는 서둘러 여관 주인을 찾아갔습니다. 제 불길한 예감은 너무도 정확했습니다. 형은 전날 저녁 님에 도착했고, 묵을 곳을 청하려던 바로 그 집 문 앞에서 살해당했던 것입니다. 저는 살인자들을 찾아내려고 온갖 수를 다 썼지만, 그들이 어찌나 두려운 존재였던지 누구도 감히 그 이름을 말해 주지 않았습니다. 그래서 저는 그토록 많이 들어 왔던, 아무것도 두려워하지 않는다는 그 프랑스 사법을 떠올리고, 검사를 찾아갔습니다.”\n\n“그런데 그 검사의 이름이 빌포르였나?” 몬테크리스토가 무심한 듯 물었다.\n\n“네, 백작님. 그는 마르세유 출신으로, 그곳에서 검사 대리를 지냈지요. 그 열성 덕에 승진했고, 엘바섬에서의 탈출을 정부에 가장 먼저 알린 사람 중 하나라는 말이 있었습니다.”\n\n“그래서,” 몬테크리스토가 말했다. “그를 찾아갔단 말이지?”\n\n“‘검사님, 제 형이 어제 님 거리에서 살해당했습니다. 누구의 손에 죽었는지는 모르지만, 그것을 밝혀내는 것이 당신의 의무입니다. 당신은 이곳 정의의 대리자이고, 정의가 미처 지켜 주지 못한 자들의 원수를 갚는 것이 바로 정의의 몫이니까요.’ 제가 말했습니다.\n\n“‘당신 형이 누구였소?’ 그가 물었습니다.\n\n“‘코르시카 대대의 중위였습니다.’\n\n“‘그럼 찬탈자의 군인이었군?’\n\n“‘프랑스 군대의 군인이었습니다.’\n\n“‘흠,’ 그가 대답했습니다. ‘칼로 친 자는 칼로 망하는 법이오.’\n\n“‘잘못 아셨습니다, 검사님.’ 제가 대꾸했습니다. ‘제 형은 단검에 찔려 죽었습니다.’\n\n“‘그래서 내가 뭘 해 주길 바라는 거요?’ 그 판사가 물었습니다.\n\n“‘이미 말씀드렸습니다. 원수를 갚아 주십시오.’\n\n“‘누구에게?’\n\n“‘형을 죽인 자들에게요.’\n\n“‘그자들이 누군지 내가 어찌 안단 말이오?’\n\n“‘찾으라고 명령하시면 됩니다.’\n\n“‘이보시오, 당신 형은 싸움에 휘말려 결투로 죽은 거요. 그 늙은 군인들이란 죄다 황제 시절에는 묵인되던 행패를 부리는데, 지금은 그게 용납되지 않소. 이곳 사람들은 그런 난폭한 군인들을 좋아하지 않으니까.’\n\n“‘검사님,’ 제가 대답했습니다. ‘제가 개입을 청하는 것은 저 자신을 위해서가 아닙니다. 저야 형을 위해 슬퍼하거나 복수하면 그만이지요. 하지만 가엾은 제 형에게는 아내가 있고, 만약 제게 무슨 일이 생기면 그 가엾은 여인은 굶어 죽고 말 겁니다. 형의 봉급만으로 살아가던 사람이거든요. 부디 그 여인에게 정부의 작은 연금이라도 받게 해 주십시오.’\n\n“‘혁명에는 으레 참화가 따르는 법이오.’ 빌포르 씨가 대답했습니다. ‘당신 형은 그 희생자가 된 거요. 불행한 일이지만, 정부가 그 가족에게 빚진 것은 없소. 찬탈자의 추종자들이 권력을 쥐었을 때 왕당파를 향해 휘두른 그 모든 복수를 기준으로 따진다면, 당신 형은 아마 오늘쯤 사형을 선고받았을 거요. 일어난 일은 지극히 자연스럽고, 보복의 법칙에도 들어맞소.’\n\n“‘아니,’ 제가 소리쳤습니다. ‘판사라는 분이 제게 그런 식으로 말씀하십니까?’\n\n“‘이 코르시카 놈들은 하나같이 미쳤어, 정말이지.’ 빌포르 씨가 대꾸했습니다. ‘제 고향 사람이 아직도 황제인 줄 아는군. 당신은 때를 잘못 짚었소. 이런 얘기는 두 달 전에 했어야지, 이젠 너무 늦었소. 어서 나가시오, 당장. 안 그러면 끌어내게 할 테니.’\n\n“저는 더 애원해 봤자 기대할 것이 있는지 가늠하려고 한순간 그를 바라보았습니다. 하지만 그는 돌로 된 사람이었습니다. 저는 그에게 다가가 낮은 목소리로 말했습니다. ‘좋습니다. 코르시카 사람을 그토록 잘 아신다니, 그들이 언제나 한 말은 지킨다는 것도 아시겠지요. 당신은 왕당파라서, 보나파르트파였던 제 형을 죽인 것이 잘한 일이라 여기는군요. 그렇다면 저 역시 보나파르트파인 사람으로서 한 가지를 분명히 말해 두겠습니다. 저는 당신을 죽일 겁니다. 이 순간부터 저는 당신에게 방데타를 선언합니다. 그러니 할 수 있는 한 몸조심하시지요. 다음에 우리가 만날 때가 당신의 마지막 순간이 될 테니까요.’ 그리고 그가 놀라움에서 채 깨어나기도 전에, 저는 문을 열고 방을 나섰습니다.”\n\n“허허,” 몬테크리스토가 말했다. “자네처럼 순해 보이는 사람이 그런 일을 저지르다니, 그것도 검사를 상대로 말이야! 그런데 그자는 ‘방데타’라는 그 무서운 말이 무슨 뜻인지 알았나?”\n\n“어찌나 잘 알았던지, 그 순간부터 집 안에 틀어박혀 누군가를 대동하지 않고는 절대 밖에 나가지 않았고, 사방으로 저를 찾아다녔습니다. 다행히 저는 워낙 잘 숨어 있어서 그가 찾아낼 수 없었지요. 그러자 그는 겁을 먹고 더는 님에 머물 엄두를 내지 못했습니다. 그래서 임지를 옮겨 달라고 청원했고, 실제로 영향력이 큰 사람이었던지라 베르사유로 발령받았습니다. 하지만 백작님도 아시다시피, 복수를 맹세한 코르시카 사람에게는 거리 따위는 문제가 되지 않습니다. 그러니 그의 마차가 아무리 빨리 달려도, 걸어서 뒤를 쫓는 저보다 반나절 이상 앞선 적이 없었습니다. 가장 중요한 것은 단지 그를 죽이는 것이 아니라 — 죽일 기회야 백 번도 더 있었으니까요 — 들키지 않고, 적어도 붙잡히지 않고 죽이는 것이었습니다. 이제 저는 제 한 몸만의 사람이 아니었으니까요. 제게는 보살피고 부양해야 할 형수가 있었습니다.\n\n“석 달 동안 저는 빌포르 씨를 감시했고, 석 달 동안 그는 한 걸음도 밖으로 내딛지 못한 채 제가 그 뒤를 따랐습니다. 마침내 저는 그가 은밀히 오퇴유로 드나든다는 것을 알아냈습니다. 저는 그를 그곳까지 따라갔고, 지금 우리가 있는 바로 이 집으로 그가 들어가는 것을 보았습니다. 다만 거리 쪽으로 난 큰 문으로 들어가는 대신, 그는 말을 타거나 마차를 타고 와서는 그 말이나 마차를 작은 여관에 맡기고, 저기 보이는 저 쪽문으로 들어갔습니다.”\n\n몬테크리스토는 베르투치오가 가리키는 문을 어둠 속에서도 알아볼 수 있다는 뜻으로 고개를 끄덕여 보였다.\n\n“이제 베르사유에서는 더 할 일이 없었으므로, 저는 오퇴유로 가서 알아낼 수 있는 정보를 죄다 캐냈습니다. 그를 기습하려면 바로 이곳이 매복하기에 적당한 곳임이 분명했지요. 그 집은, 문지기가 백작님께 말씀드렸듯이, 빌포르의 장인인 생메랑 씨의 소유였습니다. 생메랑 씨는 마르세유에 살았으므로 이 시골 별장은 그에게 쓸모가 없었고, 오로지 ‘남작 부인’이라는 이름으로만 알려진 어느 젊은 과부에게 세를 놓았다는 소문이 돌았습니다.\n\n“어느 저녁, 제가 담장 너머를 살펴보고 있는데, 젊고 아름다운 여인이 그 정원을 홀로 거니는 것이 보였습니다. 그 정원은 어느 창에서도 내려다보이지 않는 곳이었고, 저는 그 여인이 빌포르 씨를 기다리고 있다고 짐작했습니다. 여인이 이목구비를 알아볼 수 있을 만큼 가까이 다가왔을 때, 저는 그녀가 열여덟이나 열아홉쯤 되고, 키가 크며 살결이 무척 흰 여인임을 보았습니다. 헐렁한 모슬린 드레스를 입고 있어 몸매가 전혀 가려지지 않았던 까닭에, 저는 그녀가 머지않아 어머니가 되리라는 것을 알 수 있었습니다. 잠시 후 쪽문이 열리고 한 남자가 들어왔습니다. 젊은 여인이 서둘러 그를 맞으러 갔습니다. 두 사람은 서로의 품에 뛰어들어 다정하게 끌어안고는 함께 집 안으로 들어갔습니다. 그 남자가 바로 빌포르 씨였습니다. 저는 그가 밤에 나올 때면 그 넓은 정원 전체를 홀로 가로질러야 하리라고 굳게 믿었습니다.”\n\n“그런데,” 백작이 물었다. “그 여인의 이름은 알아냈나?”\n\n“아니요, 백작님.” 베르투치오가 대답했다. “보시면 아시겠지만, 그것을 알아낼 겨를이 없었습니다.”\n\n“계속하게.”\n\n“그날 저녁,” 베르투치오가 말을 이었다. “저는 그 검사를 죽일 수도 있었습니다. 하지만 그 일대를 충분히 알지 못해, 단번에 죽이지 못할까 봐, 그래서 그의 비명이 누군가에게 들려 제가 붙잡힐까 봐 두려웠습니다. 그래서 다음 기회로 미루었고, 무엇 하나 놓치지 않으려고 정원 담장이 면한 거리 쪽으로 난 방 하나를 빌렸습니다. 사흘 뒤, 저녁 일곱 시쯤, 하인 하나가 말을 타고 전속력으로 집을 나서서 세브르 쪽으로 길을 잡는 것을 보았습니다. 저는 그가 베르사유로 가는 것이라고 짐작했고, 제 짐작은 틀리지 않았습니다. 세 시간 뒤 그 하인은 먼지를 뒤집어쓴 채 돌아왔습니다. 심부름을 마친 것이지요. 그리고 이 분 뒤, 망토를 두른 또 다른 남자가 걸어와 정원 쪽문을 열고 들어선 다음 문을 닫았습니다. 저는 재빨리 내려갔습니다. 빌포르의 얼굴은 보지 못했지만, 가슴이 뛰는 것으로 그를 알아차렸습니다. 저는 거리를 가로질러, 담장 모퉁이에 세워진 말뚝 곁에 멈춰 섰습니다. 전에도 한 번 그것을 딛고 정원 안을 들여다본 적이 있었지요.\n\n“이번에는 그저 들여다보는 것으로 만족하지 않고, 주머니에서 칼을 꺼내 칼끝이 날카로운지 만져 본 다음 담장을 뛰어넘었습니다. 제일 먼저 한 일은 쪽문으로 달려가는 것이었습니다. 그는 자물쇠에 열쇠를 그대로 꽂아 둔 채, 그저 두 번 돌려 잠그는 정도의 단순한 조심만 해 두었더군요. 그러니 그 문으로 달아나는 데에는 아무 걸림돌이 없는 셈이어서, 저는 정원을 살폈습니다. 정원은 길고 좁았습니다. 한가운데로는 매끄러운 잔디밭이 길게 뻗어 있었고, 모퉁이마다 잎이 두껍고 무성한 나무들이 무리 지어 관목과 꽃들의 배경을 이루고 있었습니다. 쪽문에서 집으로, 또는 집에서 쪽문으로 가려면 빌포르 씨는 이 나무 덤불 가운데 하나를 반드시 지나야 했습니다.\n\n“때는 9월 말이었고, 바람이 사납게 불었습니다. 하늘을 휩쓸고 지나가는 검은 구름 떼에 이따금 가려지는 창백한 달의 희미한 빛이 집으로 이어지는 자갈길을 하얗게 비추었지만, 사람이 들킬 염려 없이 몸을 숨길 수 있는 그 무성한 덤불의 어둠만은 꿰뚫지 못했습니다. 저는 빌포르가 반드시 지날 길과 가장 가까운 덤불 속에 몸을 숨겼습니다. 그런데 거기 들어서자마자, 휘몰아치는 바람 소리 사이로 신음이 들리는 것 같았습니다. 하지만 백작님께서도 아시겠지요, 아니 오히려 모르시겠지요. 사람을 죽이려는 자에게는 낮은 비명이 줄곧 귓가에 울리는 듯이 느껴지는 법입니다. 그렇게 두 시간이 흘렀고, 그동안 저는 거듭 신음 소리를 듣는 것만 같았습니다. 자정을 알리는 종이 울렸습니다. 마지막 종소리가 잦아드는 순간, 우리가 방금 내려온 그 비밀 계단의 창문으로 희미한 불빛이 새어 나오는 것이 보였습니다. 문이 열리고, 망토를 두른 남자가 다시 모습을 드러냈습니다.\n\n“그 끔찍한 순간이 다가왔습니다. 하지만 저는 오래도록 그 순간을 준비해 온 터라 가슴이 조금도 떨리지 않았습니다. 저는 다시 주머니에서 칼을 꺼내 펼치고, 찌를 채비를 했습니다. 망토를 두른 남자가 제 쪽으로 다가왔는데, 가까워지면서 보니 그의 손에 무기가 들려 있었습니다. 저는 두려웠습니다. 다툼이 두려운 것이 아니라, 실패가 두려웠던 것이지요. 그가 제게서 몇 걸음밖에 떨어지지 않았을 때, 제가 무기로 여겼던 것이 실은 삽에 지나지 않음을 알았습니다. 빌포르 씨가 왜 그 삽을 손에 들고 있는지 여전히 짐작하지 못하고 있는데, 그가 제가 숨은 덤불 바로 곁에 멈춰 서서 사방을 둘러보더니 땅에 구덩이를 파기 시작했습니다. 그제야 저는 그가 망토 밑에 무언가를 감추고 있다는 것을 알아챘습니다. 그는 더 편히 파려고 그것을 잔디 위에 내려놓았지요. 그러자, 고백하건대, 증오에 호기심이 뒤섞였습니다. 빌포르가 거기서 무엇을 하려는지 보고 싶어진 저는 숨을 죽인 채 꼼짝 않고 있었습니다. 그때 한 가지 생각이 머릿속을 스쳤고, 그 검사가 망토 밑에서 길이 두 자에 깊이가 예닐곱 치쯤 되는 상자를 들어 올리는 것을 보자 그 생각이 확실해졌습니다. 저는 그가 파 놓은 구덩이에 상자를 내려놓도록 내버려 두었습니다. 그런 다음, 그가 제 작업의 흔적을 모조리 없애려고 발로 흙을 다지는 사이, 저는 그에게 달려들어 가슴에 칼을 깊숙이 꽂으며 외쳤습니다.\n\n“‘나는 조반니 베르투치오다. 내 형의 목숨값으로 네 목숨을, 그의 미망인을 위해 네 보물을 가져가겠다. 보아라, 내 복수는 내가 바라던 것보다도 완벽하구나.’\n\n“그가 이 말을 들었는지는 모르겠습니다. 못 들었으리라 생각합니다. 비명 한마디 없이 쓰러졌으니까요. 그의 피가 제 얼굴 위로 뿜어져 오르는 것이 느껴졌지만, 저는 취해 있었고, 광란에 빠져 있었으며, 그 피는 저를 태우기는커녕 오히려 시원하게 식혀 주었습니다. 순식간에 저는 상자를 파냈습니다. 그런 다음 제가 그렇게 한 것을 들키지 않으려고 구덩이를 도로 메우고, 삽을 담장 너머로 던져 버린 뒤, 쪽문으로 빠져나가 이중으로 잠그고 열쇠를 가지고 갔습니다.”\n\n“흠,” 몬테크리스토가 말했다. “내가 보기엔 살인에 강도질이 더해진 것에 지나지 않는 것 같은데.”\n\n“아닙니다, 백작님.” 베르투치오가 대답했다. “방데타에 뒤이은 반환이었습니다.”\n\n“그래, 그 액수가 컸나?”\n\n“돈이 아니었습니다.”\n\n“아, 생각나는군.” 백작이 대답했다. “자네가 갓난아이에 대해 무슨 말을 하지 않았나?”\n\n“네, 백작님. 저는 강가로 서둘러 가서 둑에 앉아, 칼로 상자의 자물쇠를 비틀어 열었습니다. 고운 아마포 안에 갓 태어난 아기가 싸여 있었습니다. 시퍼런 얼굴과 보랏빛 손은 그 아기가 질식해 죽었음을 말해 주고 있었지만, 아직 차갑지 않았던 까닭에 저는 발치에 흐르는 물속으로 그것을 던져 버리기를 망설였습니다. 잠시 후 저는 심장이 희미하게 뛰는 것을 느낀 것만 같았고, 바스티아 병원에서 조수로 일한 적이 있던 터라 의사라면 했을 법한 일을 했습니다. 입으로 공기를 불어넣어 폐를 부풀린 것이지요. 그러자 십오 분쯤 지나 아기는 숨을 쉬기 시작했고, 가냘프게 울음을 터뜨렸습니다. 이번에는 제가 비명을 질렀습니다. 그러나 기쁨의 비명이었지요.\n\n“‘그렇다면 하느님께서 저를 저주하신 것이 아니로구나.’ 저는 외쳤습니다. ‘제가 빼앗은 목숨 대신 한 인간의 목숨을 구하도록 허락하셨으니.’”\n\n“그래, 그 아이는 어떻게 했나?” 몬테크리스토가 물었다. “도망치려는 사람에게는 거추장스러운 짐이었을 텐데.”\n\n“그 아이를 데리고 있을 생각은 한순간도 하지 않았습니다. 다만 파리에 그런 아이들을 받아 주는 보호소가 있다는 것을 알고 있었지요. 저는 성문을 지나며 길에서 아이를 주웠다고 말하고, 그 보호소가 어디인지 물었습니다. 상자가 제 말을 뒷받침해 주었고, 아마포는 아기가 부유한 부모의 자식임을 입증해 주었으며, 제 몸을 뒤덮은 피는 다른 누구의 것이 아니라 아기의 것일 수도 있었습니다. 아무도 이의를 달지 않았고, 그저 앙페르가 위쪽 끝에 있는 보호소를 알려 주었습니다. 그리고 저는 아마포를 둘로 잘라, 거기 새겨진 두 글자 중 하나는 아기를 감싼 천에 남고 다른 하나는 제 손에 남도록 미리 조처해 둔 다음, 종을 울리고 있는 힘껏 달아났습니다. 보름 뒤 저는 로글리아노에 있었고, 아순타에게 말했습니다.\n\n“‘마음을 추스르시오, 형수. 이스라엘은 죽었지만, 원수는 갚았소.’\n\n“형수가 무슨 뜻이냐고 물었고, 제가 모든 것을 이야기하자, ‘조반니,’ 형수가 말했습니다. ‘그 아이를 데려왔어야지요. 그 아이가 잃은 부모를 우리가 대신해 주고, 베네데토라 이름 짓고, 그 선행 덕분에 하느님께서 우리를 축복해 주셨을 텐데요.’ 저는 대답 대신, 우리가 부유해지면 그 아이를 도로 찾을 수 있도록 간직해 둔 아마포 반쪽을 형수에게 건넸습니다.”\n\n“그 아마포에는 무슨 글자가 새겨져 있었나?” 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n“H와 N이었습니다. 그 위에 남작의 관(冠)이 얹혀 있었지요.”\n\n“이런, 베르투치오, 자네 문장학 용어를 쓰는군. 문장학은 어디서 배웠나?”\n\n“백작님 밑에서 배웠습니다. 거기서는 무엇이든 배우게 되니까요.”\n\n“계속하게. 나는 두 가지가 궁금하군.”\n\n“그게 무엇입니까, 백작님?”\n\n“그 사내아이는 어떻게 되었나? 자네가 사내아이라고 말한 것 같은데, 베르투치오.”\n\n“아닙니다, 백작님. 제가 그렇게 말씀드린 기억이 없습니다.”\n\n“그런 줄 알았는데. 내가 잘못 알았나 보군.”\n\n“아니요, 잘못 아신 게 아닙니다. 실제로 사내아이였으니까요. 그런데 백작님께서 두 가지가 궁금하다고 하셨지요. 두 번째는 무엇입니까?”\n\n“두 번째는, 자네가 고해 신부를 청해 부소니 신부가 자네의 요청대로 님 감옥으로 자네를 찾아왔을 때, 자네가 받고 있던 그 죄목일세.”\n\n“그 이야기는 무척 길 것입니다, 백작님.”\n\n“그게 무슨 상관인가? 알다시피 나는 잠이 별로 없고, 자네도 그다지 잠이 오는 것 같지는 않으니 말일세.” 베르투치오는 고개를 숙이고 다시 이야기를 시작했다.\n\n“한편으로는 저를 따라다니는 지난날의 기억을 지우려고, 또 한편으로는 가난한 미망인의 필요를 채우려고, 저는 다시 열심히 밀수업으로 돌아갔습니다. 혁명 뒤에는 으레 법이 느슨해지게 마련이라 그 일도 한결 수월해져 있었지요. 특히 남부 지방은 아비뇽이며 님이며 위제스에서 끊임없이 소요가 터지는 통에 단속이 허술했습니다. 우리는 정부의 이 한숨 돌릴 틈을 틈타 곳곳에 친구를 만들어 두었습니다. 형이 님 거리에서 살해당한 뒤로 저는 한 번도 그 도시에 발을 들이지 않았습니다. 그 결과, 우리와 거래하던 그 여관 주인은 우리가 더는 자기를 찾아오지 않는 것을 보고 어쩔 수 없이 우리 쪽으로 와야 했고, 벨가르드에서 보케르로 가는 길에 ‘퐁뒤가르’라는 간판을 걸고 분점을 차렸습니다. 그렇게 해서 우리는 에그모르트며 마르티그며 부크에, 물건을 맡겨 두는 곳을, 그리고 필요할 때 헌병이나 세관원들을 피해 숨는 곳을 열두 군데쯤 두게 되었습니다. 밀수란 어느 정도 담력과 영리함을 발휘하면 짭짤한 장사입니다. 저로 말하자면, 산속에서 자란 몸이라 헌병이며 세관원을 두려워할 이유가 두 배였습니다. 제가 판사 앞에 서게 되면 조사가 시작될 테고, 조사란 언제나 과거를 캐는 법이니까요. 그리고 제 지난 삶 속에서는 밀수 담배나 허가 없는 브랜디 통을 판 것보다 훨씬 더 무거운 무언가가 드러날 수도 있었습니다. 그래서 저는 붙잡히느니 죽음을 택하기로 하고, 참으로 놀라운 일들을 해냈습니다. 그러면서 한두 번이 아니라 거듭 깨달았지요. 제 몸을 지나치게 아끼는 것이야말로, 빠른 결단과 힘차고 단호한 실행을 요하는 그런 계획들의 성공을 가로막는 유일한 걸림돌이라는 것을요. 사실 일단 자기 사업에 목숨을 내건 사람은 더 이상 다른 사람들과 대등하지 않습니다. 아니, 오히려 다른 사람들이 더 이상 그와 대등하지 못하게 되지요. 그런 결심을 한 자는 누구나 자신의 힘과 수완이 두 배로 늘어나는 것을 느끼게 됩니다.”\n\n“철학이로군, 베르투치오.” 백작이 말을 끊었다. “자네는 살면서 안 해 본 일이 없구먼.”\n\n“오, 백작님!”\n\n“아니, 아닐세. 다만 밤 열 시 반에 철학이라니 좀 늦은 감이 있군. 그래도 달리 할 말은 없네. 자네 말이 옳으니까. 그건 그 어떤 철학에 대해서도 하기 어려운 칭찬이지.”\n\n“제 항해는 점점 더 멀어지고 점점 더 짭짤해졌습니다. 아순타가 모든 것을 챙겼고, 우리의 작은 재산은 불어났습니다. 어느 날 제가 한 차례 원정을 떠나려는데, ‘다녀오세요.’ 형수가 말했습니다. ‘돌아오시면 깜짝 놀랄 일을 보여 드릴게요.’ 제가 캐물었지만 소용없었습니다. 형수는 아무것도 말해 주지 않았고, 저는 떠났습니다. 그 원정은 거의 여섯 주가 걸렸습니다. 우리는 루카로 가서 기름을 싣고, 리보르노로 가서 영국산 면직물을 실었으며, 아무 방해 없이 화물을 부리고는 기쁨에 차서 집으로 돌아왔습니다. 집에 들어서자 제일 먼저 눈에 들어온 것은, 아순타의 방 한가운데 놓인 요람이었습니다. 나머지 가구에 견주면 호화롭다 할 만한 요람이었고, 그 안에 일고여덟 달 된 아기가 있었습니다. 저는 기쁨의 탄성을 질렀습니다. 그 검사를 죽인 뒤로 제가 겪은 슬픔의 순간이라고는, 그 아이를 버렸다는 기억에서 비롯된 것뿐이었으니까요. 살인 그 자체에 대해서는 한 번도 가책을 느낀 적이 없었습니다. 가엾은 아순타는 모든 것을 짐작하고 있었습니다. 형수는 제가 없는 틈을 타, 제가 간직해 둔 아마포 반쪽을 챙기고, 제가 아이를 보호소에 맡긴 날짜와 시각을 적어 둔 다음, 파리로 떠나 그 아이를 도로 찾아온 것이었습니다. 아무도 이의를 달지 않았고, 아기는 형수에게 넘겨졌습니다. 아, 고백하건대, 백작님, 그 가엾은 것이 요람 속에서 평화롭게 잠든 것을 보았을 때, 저는 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁 차오르는 것을 느꼈습니다. ‘아, 아순타,’ 저는 외쳤습니다. ‘당신은 훌륭한 여자요. 하늘이 당신을 축복할 거요.’”\n\n“그건,” 몬테크리스토가 말했다. “자네 철학보다 덜 정확하군. 그저 믿음일 뿐이야.”\n\n“아아, 백작님 말씀이 옳습니다.” 베르투치오가 대답했다. “하느님은 그 아이를 우리를 벌하는 도구로 삼으셨으니까요. 그토록 일찍부터 비뚤어진 천성을 드러낸 아이는 일찍이 없었습니다. 그렇다고 양육에 무슨 잘못이 있었던 것도 아니었지요. 그 아이는 더없이 사랑스러운 아이였습니다. 큰 푸른 눈은 금발의 살결과 그렇게도 잘 어울리는 깊은 빛깔이었습니다. 다만 머리카락만은 지나치게 옅어서, 그 얼굴에 무척이나 묘한 인상을 더해 주었고, 눈빛의 생기와 미소에 깃든 악의를 한층 돋보이게 했습니다.\n\n“불행히도, ‘붉은 것은 더없이 좋거나 더없이 나쁘거나 둘 중 하나다’라는 속담이 있습니다. 그 속담은 베네데토에게는 너무도 잘 들어맞았고, 어린 시절부터 그 아이는 가장 고약한 성품을 드러냈습니다. 물론 양어머니의 응석받이가 그것을 부추긴 것은 사실입니다. 제 가엾은 형수가 그 아이를 위해 대여섯 리외나 떨어진 도시까지 가서 가장 이른 과일과 가장 군침 도는 사탕을 사다 주었건만, 그 아이는 팔마 포도나 제노바산 설탕절임보다도 이웃집 과수원에서 훔친 밤이나 다락의 말린 사과를 더 좋아했습니다. 제 정원에서 자란 호두며 사과를 얼마든지 먹을 수 있었는데도 말이지요.\n\n“베네데토가 대여섯 살쯤 되던 어느 날, 이웃 와실리오가 — 그는 그 고장 풍습대로 지갑이나 귀중품을 결코 잠가 두는 법이 없었습니다. 백작님께서도 아시다시피 코르시카에는 도둑이 없으니까요 — 지갑에서 1루이가 없어졌다고 하소연했습니다. 우리는 그가 돈을 세다가 착각한 것이려니 했지만, 그는 자기 말이 정확하다고 우겼습니다. 어느 날, 아침부터 집을 나가 우리를 몹시 애태우던 베네데토가 저녁 늦게야 돌아왔는데, 원숭이 한 마리를 끌고 왔습니다. 나무 밑동에 사슬로 묶여 있던 것을 발견했다는 것이었습니다. 한 달이 넘도록 그 개구쟁이는 무엇을 갖고 싶은지도 모르면서 원숭이를 갖고 싶다는 생각에 사로잡혀 있었습니다. 로글리아노를 지나가던 어느 뱃사람이 그런 짐승을 여러 마리 데리고 있었는데, 그 재주가 아이를 무척 즐겁게 해 준 모양이었으니, 분명 거기서 그런 생각이 싹텄을 것입니다. ‘우리 숲에는 나무에 사슬로 묶인 원숭이 따위는 없다.’ 제가 말했습니다. ‘이 짐승을 어떻게 손에 넣었는지 바른대로 말해 봐라.’ 베네데토는 자기 말이 사실이라고 우기며, 그 진실성보다는 상상력에 더 어울리는 자질구레한 이야기를 덧붙였습니다. 저는 화가 났습니다. 그러자 아이가 웃기 시작했고, 제가 때리겠다고 으름장을 놓자 두 걸음 뒤로 물러섰습니다. ‘날 때릴 수는 없어.’ 아이가 말했습니다. ‘그럴 권리가 없잖아. 당신은 내 아버지가 아니니까.’\n\n“우리가 그토록 조심스레 감춰 온 그 끔찍한 비밀을 누가 그 아이에게 일러 주었는지는 끝내 알 수 없었습니다. 어쨌든, 그 아이의 본성이 통째로 드러난 이 대꾸가 저를 거의 질리게 만들었고, 제 팔은 그를 건드리지도 못한 채 툭 떨어지고 말았습니다.\n\n“아이가 의기양양해졌고, 이 승리가 그를 어찌나 대담하게 만들었던지, 그 아이가 그럴 자격이 없어질수록 그를 향한 애정은 오히려 깊어만 가는 듯하던 아순타의 돈은, 그녀가 도무지 맞설 줄 모르는 변덕과, 막을 용기를 내지 못하는 어리석은 짓들에 죄다 탕진되었습니다. 제가 로글리아노에 있을 때는 모든 것이 제대로 굴러갔지만, 제가 등을 돌리기가 무섭게 베네데토가 주인 노릇을 하며 모든 것이 엉망이 되었습니다. 겨우 열한 살 때 그 아이는 열여덟이나 스무 살 된 젊은 패거리 중에서 동무를 골랐는데, 바스티아에서, 아니 코르시카 전체에서 가장 악질인 자들이었습니다. 그들은 벌써 몇몇 못된 장난 때문에 여러 차례 기소 위협을 받은 처지였지요. 저는 겁이 났습니다. 어떤 기소든 심각한 결과를 불러올 수 있었으니까요. 마침 그 무렵 저는 중요한 원정 때문에 코르시카를 떠나야 했습니다. 저는 오랫동안 곰곰이 생각한 끝에, 닥쳐오는 어떤 불행을 막아 보려는 바람으로, 베네데토를 데려가기로 마음먹었습니다.\n\n“저는 밀수꾼의 활기차고 고된 생활과 배 위의 엄격한 규율이, 이제 거의 — 완전히는 아니더라도 — 망가져 가는 그 아이의 성품에 좋은 영향을 주리라 기대했습니다. 저는 베네데토와 단둘이 이야기하며 함께 가자고 권했고, 열두 살 아이의 상상력을 현혹할 만한 온갖 약속으로 그를 꾀어 보려 했습니다. 아이는 참을성 있게 듣더니, 제 말이 끝나자 웃음을 터뜨렸습니다.\n\n“‘삼촌, 미쳤어요?’ (기분이 좋을 때면 그 아이는 저를 이렇게 불렀습니다.) ‘내가 지금 누리는 삶을 삼촌의 삶과 바꿀 줄 알아요? 이 편안한 게으름을, 밤이면 살을 에는 서리에, 낮이면 타는 듯한 더위에 시달리고, 몸을 숨겨야 하고, 들키면 총알 세례를 받아 가며, 그 보잘것없는 푼돈을 벌려고 삼촌이 스스로에게 지우는 그 고되고 위태로운 노역과요? 아니, 나는 갖고 싶은 만큼 돈이 있는걸요. 아순타 엄마는 내가 달라고 하면 언제든 줘요! 보세요, 삼촌 제안을 받아들이면 내가 바보지.’\n\n“그 말들이, 그리고 그 뻔뻔함이 저를 완전히 얼어붙게 했습니다. 베네데토는 제 패거리에게 돌아갔고, 저는 그가 멀리서 저를 바보라고 가리켜 보이는 것을 보았습니다.”\n\n“참으로 귀여운 아이로군.” 몬테크리스토가 중얼거렸다.\n\n“오, 그 아이가 제 친아들이었거나,” 베르투치오가 대답했다. “하다못해 친조카이기만 했어도, 저는 그를 바른길로 되돌려 놓았을 겁니다. 자기가 마땅한 도리를 다하고 있다는 자각은 사람에게 힘을 주니까요. 하지만 제가 그 아비를 죽인 아이를 때리고 있다는 생각이, 그를 도무지 벌할 수 없게 만들었습니다. 저는 그 가엾은 아이를 줄곧 감싸고도는 형수에게 좋은 충고를 해 주었습니다. 그리고 형수가 여러 번 적잖은 돈이 없어진 적이 있다고 털어놓기에, 앞으로 우리의 작은 재산을 숨겨 둘 안전한 곳을 일러 주었습니다. 제 마음은 이미 정해져 있었습니다. 베네데토는 읽기와 쓰기, 셈을 완벽하게 할 줄 알았습니다. 마음이 동할 때면 남들이 일주일에 배울 것을 하루에 익히는 아이였으니까요. 제 생각은 그를 어느 배에 사환으로 들여보내고, 제 계획은 전혀 눈치채지 못하게 한 채 어느 날 아침 배에 태워 버리는 것이었습니다. 그렇게 하면 앞으로 그가 어떤 대접을 받느냐는 오로지 제 행실에 달리게 되는 셈이었지요. 저는 그 계획을 굳히고 나서 프랑스로 떠났습니다. 우리 화물은 리옹만에 부려야 했는데, 그것은 쉽지 않은 일이었습니다. 때가 1829년이었기 때문이지요. 더없는 평온이 회복되어 세관원들의 경계가 두 배로 강화되어 있었고, 마침 보케르 장이 열리는 통에 단속도 한층 엄해져 있었습니다.\n\n“우리 원정은 순조롭게 출발했습니다. 우리는 물건을 숨겨 둔 이중 선창이 달린 배를, 보케르에서 아를까지 론강 기슭을 메운 수많은 다른 배들 틈에 정박시켰습니다. 도착하자 우리는 밤을 틈타 화물을 내리고, 우리와 거래하던 그 여관 주인의 도움을 받아 그것을 시내로 날랐습니다.\n\n“성공에 우리가 방심한 탓인지, 아니면 누가 우리를 밀고한 탓인지 모르겠습니다만, 어느 저녁 다섯 시쯤, 어린 사환이 숨을 헐떡이며 달려와 세관원 일대(一隊)가 우리 쪽으로 다가오고 있다고 알렸습니다. 우리를 불안하게 한 것은 그들이 가까이 왔다는 사실이 아니었습니다. 론강 기슭에는 늘 무리 지어 순찰을 도는 자들이 있었으니까요. 다만 사환의 말로는, 그들이 눈에 띄지 않으려고 무척 조심하더라는 것이었습니다. 순식간에 우리는 경계 태세에 들어갔지만, 이미 늦었습니다. 우리 배는 포위되었고, 세관원들 가운데 헌병도 몇 보였습니다. 다른 무엇 앞에서는 그토록 용감하던 제가 그 제복을 보자 겁에 질려, 저는 선창으로 뛰어들어 현창(舷窓)을 열고 강물 속으로 떨어졌습니다. 물속으로 잠수해, 숨을 쉬려고 이따금씩만 떠오르면서, 얼마 전 론강에서 보케르와 에그모르트를 잇는 운하로 새로 판 도랑에 이를 때까지 헤엄쳤습니다. 이제 저는 안전했습니다. 그 도랑을 따라가면 들키지 않고 헤엄칠 수 있었으니까요. 저는 무사히 운하에 다다랐습니다. 저는 일부러 그쪽 방향을 택한 것이었습니다. 제가 벨가르드에서 보케르로 가는 길에 작은 선술집을 차린 님 출신 여관 주인 이야기를 이미 백작님께 말씀드렸지요.”\n\n“그래,” 몬테크리스토가 말했다. “그 사람은 똑똑히 기억나는군. 자네 동료였던 것 같은데.”\n\n“바로 그렇습니다.” 베르투치오가 대답했다. “하지만 그는 이 무렵보다 칠팔 년 전에 자기 가게를 마르세유의 어느 재단사에게 팔았습니다. 그 재단사는 본래 하던 일로 거의 빈털터리가 된 터라, 다른 일로 한밑천 잡아 보려던 사람이었지요. 물론 우리는 새 주인과도 옛 주인과 맺었던 것과 똑같은 약조를 맺었습니다. 그리고 제가 몸을 숨길 곳을 청하려던 상대가 바로 이 사람이었습니다.”\n\n“그자의 이름이 무엇이었나?” 백작이 물었다. 그는 베르투치오의 이야기에 다소 흥미가 동한 듯했다.\n\n“가스파르 카드루스였습니다. 그는 카르콩트 마을 출신 여자와 결혼했는데, 우리는 그 여자를 그 마을 이름 말고는 달리 부를 줄을 몰랐습니다. 그 여자는 학질에 시달리며 조금씩 죽어 가는 듯했습니다. 남편으로 말하면, 마흔이나 마흔다섯쯤 된 건장한 사내로, 위험한 순간이면 한두 번이 아니라 거듭 침착함과 용기를 충분히 보여 준 자였습니다.”\n\n“그래서 자네 말은,” 몬테크리스토가 말을 끊었다. “이 일이 몇 년쯤에 일어났다고 했지?”\n\n“1829년입니다, 백작님.”\n\n“몇 월에?”\n\n“초인가, 말인가?”\n\n“3일 저녁이었습니다.”\n\n“아,” 몬테크리스토가 말했다. “1829년 6월 3일 저녁이라. 계속하게.”\n\n“제가 몸을 숨길 곳을 청하려던 상대가 바로 카드루스였습니다. 우리는 길로 난 문으로는 결코 들어가는 법이 없었으므로, 저는 그 규칙을 깨지 않기로 하고, 정원 울타리를 넘어 올리브나무와 야생 무화과나무 사이로 기어들었습니다. 그리고 카드루스에게 손님이 있을지도 모른다는 생각에, 제가 곧잘 밤을 지새우던 일종의 헛간으로 들어갔습니다. 그곳은 칸막이 하나로만 여관과 나뉘어 있었는데, 그 칸막이에는 우리가 안을 살피며 기척을 알릴 틈을 노릴 수 있도록 구멍이 뚫려 있었습니다.\n\n“제 생각은, 카드루스가 혼자 있거든 그에게 제가 온 것을 알리고, 세관원들 때문에 중단됐던 식사를 마저 한 다음, 막 몰아칠 듯한 폭풍우를 틈타 론강으로 돌아가 우리 배와 선원들의 형편을 알아보자는 것이었습니다. 저는 헛간으로 들어섰는데, 마침 그러기를 잘한 것이, 바로 그 순간 카드루스가 웬 낯선 사람과 함께 들어왔기 때문입니다.\n\n“저는 그들이 하는 말을 엿들으려고가 아니라, 달리 어쩔 도리가 없어서 참을성 있게 기다렸습니다. 게다가 그런 일은 전에도 자주 있었으니까요. 카드루스와 함께 온 사내는 분명 프랑스 남부 사람이 아니었습니다. 그는 보케르 장에 보석을 팔러 오는 상인들 가운데 하나로, 장이 서는 한 달 동안, 유럽 각지에서 상인과 손님이 그토록 밀려드는 그동안에, 흔히 십만에서 십오만 프랑어치 거래를 하는 자들이었습니다. 카드루스가 급히 들어왔습니다. 그러고는 방이 여느 때처럼 비어 있고 개 한 마리만 지키고 있는 것을 보자, 아내를 불렀습니다. ‘여보, 카르콩트,’ 그가 말했습니다. ‘그 양반 신부님 말이 거짓이 아니었어. 그 다이아몬드는 진짜였다고.’\n\n“기쁨의 탄성이 들리더니, 계단이 가냘픈 발걸음 아래서 삐걱거렸습니다. ‘무슨 말이에요?’ 그의 아내가 죽은 사람처럼 창백한 얼굴로 물었습니다.\n\n“‘이 다이아몬드가 진짜라고. 그리고 파리 일류 보석상인 이분이 이걸 5만 프랑에 사 주시겠다는 거야. 다만, 이게 정말로 우리 것이 맞는지 확인하시려고, 이 다이아몬드가 어떻게 우리 손에 들어오게 됐는지 그 기적 같은 사연을, 내가 이미 말씀드린 대로, 당신도 들려 드렸으면 하셔. 그러니 그동안, 선생님, 부디 앉으시지요. 제가 마실 것 좀 가져오겠습니다.’\n\n“보석상은 여관 내부와, 왕가의 보석함에서나 나왔을 법한 다이아몬드를 팔려는 이 사람들의 한눈에 보이는 가난을 유심히 살펴보았습니다.\n\n“‘부인, 사연을 들려주시지요.’ 그가 말했습니다. 분명 남편이 없는 틈을 타, 남편이 아내의 이야기에 영향을 주지 못하게 해서, 두 사람의 이야기가 들어맞는지 보려는 속셈이었지요.\n\n“‘오,’ 여자가 대답했습니다. ‘그건 하늘이 내린 선물이었답니다. 제 남편은 1814년인가 1815년인가, 에드몽 당테스라는 뱃사람과 둘도 없는 친구였어요. 카드루스가 까맣게 잊고 지내던 그 가엾은 사람은 정작 남편을 잊지 않았고, 죽으면서 이 다이아몬드를 남편에게 남겼지요.’\n\n“‘그런데 그 사람은 이걸 어떻게 손에 넣었답니까?’ 보석상이 물었습니다. ‘투옥되기 전부터 가지고 있었나요?’\n\n“‘아니요, 선생님. 하지만 감옥에서 어느 부유한 영국 사람과 알게 된 모양이에요. 거기서 그 영국 사람이 병이 들었는데, 당테스가 친형제처럼 간호해 주었답니다. 그래서 그 영국 사람이 풀려나면서 이 보석을 당테스에게 주었고, 운이 덜 좋았던 당테스는 죽으면서 다시 우리에게 남기고, 오늘 아침 여기 왔던 그 훌륭한 신부님께 전해 달라고 부탁한 거지요.’\n\n“‘똑같은 이야기로군.’ 보석상이 중얼거렸습니다. ‘처음엔 그럴듯하지 않게 들렸지만, 사실일 수도 있겠어. 다만 값만큼은 우리가 아직 합의를 못 봤지.’\n\n“‘합의를 못 보다니요?’ 카드루스가 말했습니다. ‘내가 부른 값에 합의한 줄 알았는데요.’\n\n“‘그러니까,’ 보석상이 대답했습니다. ‘나는 4만 프랑을 불렀던 거요.’\n\n“‘4만이라니,’ 카르콩트가 소리쳤습니다. ‘그 값에는 못 내놔요. 신부님이 그러는데, 받침을 빼고도 5만 프랑은 나간다고 했단 말이에요.’\n\n“‘그 신부 이름이 무엇이었소?’ 지칠 줄 모르는 그 질문꾼이 물었습니다.\n\n“‘부소니 신부님이오.’ 카르콩트가 말했습니다.\n\n“‘외국 사람이었나요?’\n\n“‘만토바 근방 출신 이탈리아 사람인 것 같았어요.’\n\n“‘이 다이아몬드를 다시 한번 봅시다.’ 보석상이 말했습니다. ‘처음에는 돌의 값어치를 잘못 보는 일이 흔하니까요.’\n\n“카드루스가 주머니에서 검은 상어가죽 작은 갑을 꺼내 열어, 보석상에게 건넸습니다. 개암만큼 큰 그 다이아몬드를 보자, 카르콩트의 눈이 탐욕으로 번쩍였습니다.”\n\n“그래, 자넨 그 그럴듯한 이야기를 어떻게 생각했나, 이 엿듣던 양반아?” 몬테크리스토가 말했다. “그 말을 믿었나?”\n\n“네, 백작님. 저는 카드루스를 나쁜 사람으로 보지 않았고, 그가 범죄는커녕 도둑질조차 저지를 위인이 못 된다고 여겼습니다.”\n\n“그건 자네 경험보다는 자네 마음을 돋보이게 하는 말이로군, 베르투치오. 그들이 입에 올린 그 에드몽 당테스라는 자를 자넨 알고 있었나?”\n\n“아니요, 백작님. 그전에는 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없었고, 그 뒤로도 딱 한 번 들었을 뿐인데, 그것도 부소니 신부님께 직접 들은 것입니다. 님 감옥에서 그분을 뵈었을 때였지요.”\n\n“계속하게.”\n\n“보석상은 반지를 집어 들고, 주머니에서 강철 집게와 작은 구리 저울 한 벌을 꺼내, 받침에서 돌을 빼내 정성스레 무게를 달았습니다.\n\n“‘4만 5천을 드리리다.’ 그가 말했습니다. ‘하지만 한 푼도 더는 안 됩니다. 게다가 그게 이 돌의 정확한 값이라, 마침 딱 그만큼만 가지고 왔거든요.’\n\n“‘오, 그건 문제없습니다.’ 카드루스가 대답했습니다. ‘나머지 5천 프랑은 내가 당신과 함께 가서 받아 오면 되니까요.’\n\n“‘아니요.’ 보석상이 다이아몬드와 반지를 카드루스에게 돌려주며 대답했습니다. ‘아니, 이건 그 이상은 못 됩니다. 내가 그렇게 많이 부른 게 후회될 지경이오. 이 돌에는 미처 못 봤던 흠이 있어요. 그래도 한 말을 되돌리지는 않겠소. 4만 5천을 드리리다.’\n\n“‘적어도 돌은 반지에 도로 끼워 주세요.’ 카르콩트가 쏘아붙였습니다.\n\n“‘아, 그렇지.’ 보석상이 대답하고는 돌을 도로 끼웠습니다.\n\n“‘상관없소.’ 카드루스가 갑을 다시 주머니에 넣으며 말했습니다. ‘다른 사람이 사겠지요.’\n\n“‘그렇겠지요.’ 보석상이 말을 이었습니다. ‘하지만 다른 사람은 나만큼 너그럽지 않을 거고, 그 똑같은 이야기로 만족하지도 않을 거요. 당신 같은 사람이 이런 다이아몬드를 가지고 있다는 게 당연한 일이 아니거든. 그자는 당신을 고발할 거요. 그러면 당신은 부소니 신부를 찾아내야 할 텐데, 2천 루이짜리 다이아몬드를 거저 주는 신부가 흔하겠소? 법이 그걸 압수해서 당신을 감옥에 처넣을 거고, 서너 달 뒤에 풀려난다 해도, 반지는 사라지고 없거나, 5만, 어쩌면 5만 5천 프랑짜리 다이아몬드 대신 3프랑짜리 가짜 돌이 손에 쥐어질 거요. 그러니 이걸 사는 데에도 상당한 위험이 따른다는 걸 인정하셔야지.’\n\n“카드루스와 그의 아내는 서로를 간절히 마주 보았습니다.\n\n“‘아니요.’ 카드루스가 말했습니다. ‘우린 5천 프랑을 잃을 만큼 넉넉한 형편이 못 됩니다.’\n\n“‘좋으실 대로 하시지요, 선생.’ 보석상이 말했습니다. ‘하지만 보다시피, 나는 번쩍이는 돈으로 값을 가져왔소이다.’ 그러고는 주머니에서 금화 한 줌을 꺼내, 눈이 휘둥그레진 여관 주인 앞에 반짝이게 펼쳐 보였고, 다른 한 손에는 지폐 한 뭉치를 들고 있었습니다.\n\n“카드루스의 마음속에서 격렬한 갈등이 이는 것이 분명했습니다. 그가 손에서 이리저리 굴리는 그 작은 상어가죽 갑이, 그의 시선을 사로잡은 그 엄청난 액수에 견줄 만한 값어치로는 여겨지지 않는 게 뻔했지요. 그는 아내 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸습니다.\n\n“‘당신 생각은 어때?’ 그가 낮은 목소리로 물었습니다.\n\n“‘넘겨요, 넘기라고요.’ 여자가 말했습니다. ‘저 사람이 다이아몬드 없이 보케르로 돌아가면 우릴 고발할 거예요. 게다가 저 사람 말처럼, 우리가 부소니 신부님을 다시 볼 수 있을지 누가 알아요? 십중팔구 다시는 못 볼 거라고요.’\n\n“‘좋아, 그럼 그렇게 하지!’ 카드루스가 말했습니다. ‘이 다이아몬드를 4만 5천 프랑에 가져가시오. 다만 내 아내는 금목걸이를 갖고 싶어 하고, 나는 은 버클 한 쌍이 갖고 싶소.’\n\n“보석상이 주머니에서 길쭉하고 납작한 갑을 꺼냈는데, 그 안에는 요구받은 물건들의 견본이 여럿 들어 있었습니다. ‘자,’ 그가 말했습니다. ‘나는 거래가 아주 깔끔한 사람이오. 마음에 드는 걸 고르시구려.’\n\n“여자는 5루이쯤 나가는 금목걸이를 골랐고, 남편은 어쩌면 15프랑쯤 할 버클 한 쌍을 골랐습니다.\n\n“‘이제는 불평 없으시겠지요?’ 보석상이 말했습니다.\n\n“‘신부님은 이게 5만 프랑짜리라고 했단 말이오.’ 카드루스가 중얼거렸습니다.\n\n“‘이런, 이런, 어서 이리 주시오! 별난 양반일세.’ 보석상이 그의 손에서 다이아몬드를 받으며 말했습니다. ‘내가 4만 5천 프랑을, 그러니까 연수입 2천 5백 리브르를 드리는 거요. 나라도 갖고 싶은 그런 재산을 말이오. 그런데도 만족을 못 하시다니!’\n\n“‘그래서 그 4만 5천 프랑은,’ 카드루스가 쉰 목소리로 물었습니다. ‘어디 있소? 자, 어디 좀 봅시다.’\n\n“‘여기 있소.’ 보석상이 대답하고는 탁자 위에 금화로 1만 5천 프랑, 지폐로 3만 프랑을 세어 놓았습니다.\n\n“‘등불을 켤 테니 기다려요.’ 카르콩트가 말했습니다. ‘날이 어두워지고 있어서, 잘못 셀 수도 있으니까요.’ 사실 이 대화가 오가는 동안 밤이 깃들었고, 밤과 함께 반 시간 전부터 몰아칠 듯하던 폭풍우도 닥쳐왔습니다. 천둥이 멀리서 으르렁거렸지만, 보석상도 카드루스도 카르콩트도 셋 다 탐욕의 악마에 사로잡혀 있던 터라 그 소리를 듣지 못한 모양이었습니다. 저 역시 그 모든 금화와 지폐를 보고 있노라니 묘한 황홀감에 빠져들었습니다. 마치 꿈을 꾸는 것만 같았고, 꿈에서 으레 그렇듯, 저는 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 꼼짝할 수 없었습니다. 카드루스가 금화와 지폐를 세고 또 세더니, 그것을 아내에게 건넸고, 아내가 이번에는 또다시 세고 또 셌습니다. 그동안 보석상은 등불 빛에 다이아몬드를 이리저리 비춰 반짝이게 했고, 그 보석이 뿜어내는 빛줄기에 정신이 팔려, 폭풍우의 전조로 창문 너머에서 번쩍이기 시작한 또 다른 빛은 알아채지 못했습니다.\n\n“‘자,’ 보석상이 물었습니다. ‘돈은 다 맞소?’\n\n“‘맞아요.’ 카드루스가 말했습니다. ‘지갑 좀 줘, 카르콩트. 그리고 어디 자루도 하나 찾아봐.’\n\n“카르콩트가 찬장으로 가더니 낡은 가죽 지갑과 자루 하나를 가지고 돌아왔습니다. 지갑에서는 기름때 묻은 편지 몇 장을 꺼내고 그 자리에 지폐를 넣었으며, 자루에서는 각각 6리브르짜리 은화 두세 닢을 꺼냈는데, 그것이 십중팔구 이 가련한 부부의 전 재산이었을 것입니다.\n\n“‘자,’ 카드루스가 말했습니다. ‘이제, 비록 당신이 우리한테서 1만 프랑쯤 우려내긴 했지만, 우리와 함께 저녁이나 드시지 않겠소? 진심으로 청하는 거요.’\n\n“‘고맙소.’ 보석상이 대답했습니다. ‘이제 시간이 꽤 늦은 모양이니, 보케르로 돌아가야겠소. 안 그러면 아내가 걱정할 테니.’ 그는 회중시계를 꺼내 들고 외쳤습니다. ‘이런, 거의 아홉 시로군. 자정 전에는 보케르에 못 닿겠어! 잘들 지내시오, 친구들. 혹시라도 부소니 신부가 돌아오거든, 나를 떠올려 주시오.’\n\n“‘일주일이면 당신도 보케르를 떠나 있을 텐데요.’ 카드루스가 말했습니다. ‘며칠 안에 장이 끝나니까요.’\n\n“‘그렇긴 하지만, 그건 상관없소. 파리로 편지를 보내시오. 팔레루아얄, 피에르 회랑 45번지, 조아네스 앞으로. 그럴 만한 값어치가 있다면, 일부러라도 그를 보러 한걸음에 달려오겠소.’\n\n“바로 그때 어마어마한 천둥소리가 울리고, 등불 빛을 무색하게 할 만큼 눈부신 번개가 번쩍였습니다.\n\n“‘이것 좀 보세요.’ 카드루스가 외쳤습니다. ‘이런 날씨에 나가실 생각은 마세요.’\n\n“‘오, 나는 천둥 따위는 무섭지 않소.’ 보석상이 말했습니다.\n\n“‘게다가 도둑들도 있고요.’ 카르콩트가 말했습니다. ‘장이 서는 동안에는 길이 도통 안전하질 않거든요.’\n\n“‘오, 도둑이라면,’ 조아네스가 말했습니다. ‘여기 그들을 위한 게 있소.’ 그러고는 주머니에서 총구까지 가득 장전된 작은 권총 한 쌍을 꺼냈습니다. ‘자,’ 그가 말했습니다. ‘이놈들은 짖으면서 동시에 무는 개들이오. 당신네 다이아몬드를 탐내는 첫 두 놈을 위한 거지요, 카드루스 친구.’\n\n“카드루스와 그의 아내가 다시 의미심장한 눈빛을 주고받았습니다. 마치 두 사람이 동시에 어떤 끔찍한 생각에 사로잡힌 듯했습니다. ‘그럼, 잘 가시오.’ 카드루스가 말했습니다.\n\n“‘고맙소.’ 보석상이 대답했습니다. 그러고는 낡은 찬장에 세워 두었던 지팡이를 집어 들고 밖으로 나갔습니다. 그가 문을 여는 순간, 어찌나 거센 바람이 불어들었던지 등불이 꺼질 뻔했습니다. ‘오,’ 그가 말했습니다. ‘날씨 한번 고약하구먼. 이런 폭풍 속을 두 리외나 가야 하다니.’\n\n“‘여기 머무시오.’ 카드루스가 말했습니다. ‘여기서 주무시면 되잖소.’\n\n“‘네, 머무세요.’ 카르콩트가 떨리는 목소리로 거들었습니다. ‘저희가 정성껏 모시겠어요.’\n\n“‘아니, 나는 보케르에서 자야 하오. 그러니, 다시 한번, 잘들 지내시오.’ 카드루스가 천천히 문턱까지 그를 따라갔습니다. ‘하늘도 땅도 분간이 안 되는군.’ 문밖으로 나선 보석상이 말했습니다. ‘오른쪽으로 가야 하오, 왼쪽으로 가야 하오?’\n\n“‘오른쪽이오.’ 카드루스가 말했습니다. ‘길 양쪽으로 나무가 늘어서 있으니 잘못 들 일은 없을 거요.’\n\n“‘좋소, 알았소.’ 거의 멀리서 사라지는 목소리가 들렸습니다.\n\n“‘문 닫아요.’ 카르콩트가 말했습니다. ‘난 천둥 칠 때 문 열려 있는 게 싫어요.’\n\n“‘집 안에 돈이 있을 때는 더더욱 그렇겠지, 안 그래?’ 카드루스가 문을 이중으로 잠그며 대꾸했습니다.\n\n“그는 방으로 들어와 찬장으로 가서 자루와 지갑을 꺼냈고, 두 사람은 세 번째로 금화와 지폐를 세기 시작했습니다. 깜박이는 등불이 그 두 얼굴에 드러내는 그런 탐욕의 표정은 저도 일찍이 본 적이 없었습니다. 특히 여자가 흉측했습니다. 평소의 열에 들뜬 떨림이 더 심해졌고, 낯빛은 납빛이 되었으며, 두 눈은 타오르는 숯덩이 같았습니다.\n\n“‘그런데 왜,’ 여자가 쉰 목소리로 물었습니다. ‘오늘 밤 저자더러 여기서 자라고 권한 거예요?’\n\n“‘왜냐고?’ 카드루스가 몸서리치며 말했습니다. ‘저 사람이 보케르까지 돌아가는 수고를 안 해도 되라고 그런 거지.’\n\n“‘아,’ 여자가 뭐라 형용할 수 없는 표정으로 대꾸했습니다. ‘난 또 다른 이유가 있는 줄 알았네.’\n\n“‘이 사람아, 이 사람아, 어쩌자고 그런 생각을 하는 거야?’ 카드루스가 소리쳤습니다. ‘설령 그런 생각이 든다 해도, 왜 혼자 속으로만 담아 두지 못하는 거냐고?’\n\n“‘흥,’ 카르콩트가 잠시 뜸을 들이고 말했습니다. ‘당신은 사내도 아니야.’\n\n“‘그게 무슨 소리야?’ 카드루스가 받아쳤습니다.\n\n“‘당신이 사내였다면, 저자를 여기서 그냥 보내지 않았을 거란 말이지.’\n\n“‘이 사람이!’\n\n“‘아니면 적어도 저자가 보케르까지 닿지는 못했을 거고.’\n\n“‘이 사람이!’\n\n“‘길은 굽이를 도니까 저자는 어쩔 수 없이 그 길을 따라가야 해. 그런데 운하를 끼고 가면 더 가까운 지름길이 있단 말이야.’\n\n“‘이 사람아! 하느님께 죄짓는 소리야. 저것 봐, 들어 봐!’\n\n“바로 그 순간 어마어마한 천둥소리가 울리고, 납빛 번개가 방 안을 환히 밝혔습니다. 그리고 천둥은 멀리 굴러가며, 이 저주받은 거처에서 마지못해 물러나는 것만 같았습니다. ‘주여, 자비를!’ 카드루스가 성호를 그으며 말했습니다.\n\n“바로 그 순간, 천둥소리 뒤에 으레 따르는 그 무시무시한 정적 한가운데서, 두 사람은 문 두드리는 소리를 들었습니다. 카드루스와 그의 아내는 흠칫 놀라 넋이 나간 얼굴로 서로를 바라보았습니다.\n\n“‘누구요?’ 카드루스가 자리에서 일어나며 외쳤습니다. 그러고는 탁자 위에 흩어진 금화와 지폐를 한데 그러모아 두 손으로 덮었습니다.\n\n“‘나요.’ 한 목소리가 소리쳤습니다.\n\n“‘당신이 누구요?’\n\n“‘에이, 이런! 보석상 조아네스요.’\n\n“‘봐요, 당신이 나더러 하느님께 죄짓는 소리라더니.’ 카르콩트가 끔찍한 미소를 지으며 말했습니다. ‘하느님이 저자를 도로 보내 주셨잖아요.’ 카드루스는 창백해진 채 숨도 못 쉬고 의자에 털썩 주저앉았습니다.\n\n“반대로 카르콩트는 일어나, 단호한 걸음으로 문으로 다가가 그것을 열며 말했습니다.\n\n“‘어서 오세요, 조아네스 선생님.’\n\n“‘이런,’ 비에 흠뻑 젖은 보석상이 말했습니다. ‘오늘 밤은 보케르로 돌아갈 팔자가 아닌가 보오. 어리석은 짓도 짧은 게 상책이라 하지 않소, 카드루스 친구. 당신이 묵어 가라고 청했으니, 그 호의를 받아들여 이 정다운 지붕 아래서 자려고 돌아왔소이다.’\n\n“카드루스는 이마에 솟은 땀을 닦으며 무어라 더듬거렸습니다. 카르콩트는 보석상의 등 뒤로 문을 이중으로 잠갔습니다.”\n\n# Chapter 45. The Rain of Blood\n\n보석상이 방으로 돌아오면서 주위를 날카롭게 훑어보았다. 그러나 의심을 불러일으킬 만한 것도, 이미 깨어난 의심을 굳혀 줄 만한 것도 없었다. 카드루스의 두 손은 여전히 금화와 지폐를 움켜쥐고 있었고, 카르콩트는 다시 나타난 손님을 맞으며 더없이 다정한 미소를 지어 보였다.\n\n\"이런, 이런.\" 보석상이 말했다. \"두 분, 제가 자리를 비우자마자 그렇게 꼼꼼하게 돈을 세시는 걸 보니, 받으신 돈이 정확한지 영 미덥지 않으셨던 모양입니다.\"\n\n\"아, 그게 아닙니다.\" 카드루스가 대답했다. \"분명히 말씀드리지만 그런 이유가 아닙니다. 이 재물이 손에 들어온 사정이 너무도 뜻밖이라 우리 행운을 도무지 믿을 수가 없어서, 이렇게 재물을 눈앞에 두고 직접 확인해야만 이 모든 일이 꿈이 아니라고 스스로를 납득시킬 수 있는 겁니다.\"\n\n보석상이 미소 지었다. \"이 집에 다른 손님은 없습니까?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"우리 둘뿐입니다.\" 카드루스가 대답했다. \"사실 우리는 나그네를 묵게 하지 않습니다. 게다가 이 술집이 마을과 너무 가까워서, 여기서 묵을 생각을 하는 사람은 아무도 없지요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 제가 무척 폐를 끼치게 되는 게 아닐까 걱정입니다.\"\n\n\"폐라니요? 천만의 말씀입니다, 손님.\" 카르콩트가 더없이 상냥하게 말했다. \"조금도 폐가 되지 않으니 안심하세요.\"\n\n\"하지만 저를 어디에 재우시려고요?\"\n\n\"위층 방에요.\"\n\n\"거긴 두 분이 주무시는 곳이 아닙니까?\"\n\n\"신경 쓰지 마세요. 옆방에 침대가 하나 더 있으니까요.\"\n\n카드루스가 깜짝 놀라 아내를 빤히 쳐다보았다.\n\n그러는 동안 보석상은 노래를 흥얼거리며 카르콩트가 손님의 젖은 옷을 말리려고 피워 놓은 불에 등을 쬐고 있었다. 카르콩트는 그러고 나서 손님의 저녁상을 차리는 데 매달렸다. 식탁 끝에 냅킨을 펴고 점심에 남은 변변찮은 음식을 차린 다음, 갓 낳은 달걀 서너 개를 더했다. 카드루스는 또 한 번 자기 보물과 헤어졌다. 지폐는 다시 지갑에 넣고, 금화는 자루에 도로 담아, 모두 찬장에 조심스레 넣고 잠갔다. 그러고는 생각에 잠긴 침울한 얼굴로 방 안을 거닐기 시작했다. 이따금 보석상 쪽을 흘끔거렸는데, 보석상은 젖은 옷에서 김을 무럭무럭 피워 올리며 옷이 골고루 마르도록 따뜻한 화롯가에서 자리만 옮겨 가며 서 있었다.\n\n\"자.\" 카르콩트가 식탁에 포도주 한 병을 놓으며 말했다. \"준비되시면 언제든 드세요.\"\n\n\"두 분은요?\" 조아네스가 물었다.\n\n\"난 저녁 생각 없소.\" 카드루스가 말했다.\n\n\"우리는 점심을 아주 늦게 먹었거든요.\" 카르콩트가 황급히 끼어들었다.\n\n\"그럼 저 혼자 먹어야 하는 모양이군요.\" 보석상이 말했다.\n\n\"오, 곁에서 시중드는 즐거움을 누리겠습니다.\" 카르콩트가 대답했다. 돈을 치르는 손님에게조차 좀처럼 보이지 않던 살뜰한 정성이었다.\n\n카드루스는 이따금 아내에게 날카롭게 캐묻는 듯한 눈길을 던졌으나, 그것은 번갯불처럼 순식간이었다. 폭풍은 여전히 계속되고 있었다.\n\n\"저것 봐요, 저것.\" 카르콩트가 말했다. \"저 소리 들리세요? 정말이지 돌아오시길 잘했어요.\"\n\n\"그래도,\" 보석상이 대답했다. \"저녁을 다 먹을 때쯤 폭풍이 좀 잦아들면, 다시 길을 나설 생각입니다.\"\n\n\"미스트랄이오.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"내일 아침까지는 틀림없이 그칠 줄 모를 거요.\" 그가 무겁게 한숨을 내쉬었다.\n\n\"뭐,\" 보석상이 식탁에 앉으며 말했다. \"이런 날 바깥에 나도는 사람들은 더 딱하게 됐다, 그 말밖에 더 하겠습니까.\"\n\n\"그러게요.\" 카르콩트가 맞장구쳤다. \"그 사람들은 끔찍한 밤을 보내겠지요.\"\n\n보석상이 저녁을 먹기 시작했다. 평소에는 다가오는 누구에게나 투덜대고 무심하던 그 여자가 별안간 더없이 환한 미소를 띤 살가운 안주인으로 탈바꿈했다. 그녀가 정성을 쏟아붓는 불행한 사내가 만일 그녀를 전부터 알았더라면, 그토록 갑작스러운 변화에 의심을 품었거나, 적어도 크게 놀랐을 것이다. 그러는 동안 카드루스는 침울하게 입을 다문 채 계속 방 안을 거닐며, 손님의 모습을 한사코 외면했다. 그러나 낯선 손님이 식사를 마치자마자, 동요하던 주막 주인은 부리나케 문으로 가서 문을 열었다.\n\n\"폭풍이 그친 것 같소.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n그러나 그 말을 반박하기라도 하듯, 바로 그 순간 사나운 천둥소리가 집 밑동까지 뒤흔드는 듯했고, 비를 머금은 돌풍이 별안간 몰아쳐 그가 손에 든 등불을 꺼뜨렸다.\n\n카드루스는 벌벌 떨며 겁에 질려 황급히 문을 닫고 손님에게로 돌아왔고, 카르콩트는 화롯가에 가물거리는 잿불에 초에 불을 붙였다.\n\n\"피곤하시겠어요.\" 카르콩트가 보석상에게 말했다. \"침대에 흰 시트 한 벌을 깔아 두었으니, 준비되시면 올라가셔서 푹 주무세요.\"\n\n조아네스는 잠시 머무르며 폭풍의 기세가 잦아드는지 살폈으나, 비와 천둥이 누그러지기는커녕 순간순간 거세지고 있다는 것을 깨닫는 데에는 잠깐이면 충분했다. 그래서 그는 피할 수 없는 일에 체념한 채 주인에게 잘 자라는 인사를 하고 계단을 올라갔다. 그가 내 머리 위를 지나가자, 발걸음에 마룻바닥이 삐걱대는 소리가 들렸다. 그가 올라가는 동안 카르콩트의 날쌔고 탐욕스러운 눈길이 그를 좇았으나, 카드루스는 오히려 등을 돌린 채 그를 흘끗 쳐다보는 것조차 애써 피하는 듯했다.\n\n이 모든 정황이 그때는 나중만큼 마음을 아프게 짓누르지는 않았다. 사실 그때까지 벌어진 일이라면(분명 그럴듯하지 않은 구석이 있던 다이아몬드 이야기만 빼면) 더없이 자연스러워 보였고, 두려워하거나 미심쩍어할 까닭이 없었다. 다만 나는 피로에 지칠 대로 지쳐 있었고, 폭풍이 잦아들면 곧장 길을 떠날 작정이었으므로, 몇 시간만이라도 눈을 붙이기로 했다. 머리 위에서는 보석상의 움직임을 하나하나 또렷이 분간할 수 있었다. 그는 편한 밤을 보내려고 나름대로 잘 채비를 해 두고는 침대에 몸을 던졌고, 나는 그 무게에 침대가 삐걱대고 끙끙대는 소리를 들을 수 있었다.\n\n나도 모르게 눈꺼풀이 무거워지고, 깊은 잠이 슬며시 밀려왔다. 무엇 하나 잘못됐으리라고는 의심하지 않았으므로, 나는 그 잠을 떨쳐 내려 하지 않았다. 한 번 더 부엌을 들여다보니, 카드루스가 긴 식탁 옆 나지막한 나무 걸상에 앉아 있는 것이 보였다. 시골에서 흔히 의자 대신 쓰는 그런 걸상이었다. 그가 내게 등을 돌리고 있어서 표정은 볼 수 없었는데, 설령 다르게 앉아 있었더라도 마찬가지였을 것이다. 두 손에 머리를 파묻고 있었으니까. 카르콩트는 한동안 그를 바라보다가 어깨를 으쓱하고는 그 맞은편에 가서 앉았다.\n\n그 순간 가까이 놓인 장작 한 토막에 불이 붙으면서 꺼져 가던 잿불이 다시 화르르 타올랐고, 밝은 빛이 방 안을 환히 비추었다. 카르콩트는 여전히 남편에게서 눈을 떼지 않았는데, 그가 도무지 자세를 바꿀 기미를 보이지 않자 굳고 앙상한 손을 뻗어 그의 이마를 건드렸다.\n\n카드루스가 흠칫 몸을 떨었다. 여자의 입술이 무슨 말을 하려는 듯 달싹이는 것 같았으나, 그저 낮은 목소리로 말했거나 졸음에 감각이 무뎌진 탓에, 나는 그녀가 내뱉는 말을 한마디도 알아듣지 못했다. 흐릿한 영상과 소리가 눈앞을 떠다니는 듯하더니, 나는 차츰 깊고 무거운 잠에 빠져들었다. 그 정신 없는 상태로 얼마나 있었는지 알 수 없으나, 별안간 권총 소리에 이어 무시무시한 비명이 울리며 나는 화들짝 깨어났다. 위층 방을 가로지르는 비틀거리는 힘없는 발소리가 울려 퍼졌고, 다음 순간 둔하고 무거운 무엇인가가 맥없이 계단으로 떨어지는 듯했다. 미처 정신을 다 차리기도 전에, 다시 신음 소리가 들렸다. 반쯤 짓눌린 비명이 뒤섞인 것이, 마치 목숨을 걸고 격투를 벌이는 사람들이 내는 소리 같았다. 다른 것들보다 길게 이어지다가 거푸 신음으로 잦아드는 비명이 결국 내 졸음의 무력함을 떨쳐 냈다. 나는 황급히 한쪽 팔을 짚고 몸을 일으켜 주위를 둘러보았으나 사방이 캄캄했다. 위층 방 마루를 뚫고 비가 새어 든 게 틀림없는 듯했다. 무슨 물기가 한 방울 한 방울 내 이마에 떨어지는 듯했고, 손으로 이마를 훔쳐 보니 축축하고 끈적했다.\n\n나를 깨운 그 무서운 소리들 뒤에는 더없이 완벽한 정적이 이어졌다. 위층 방에서 한 사내가 서성이는 발소리만이 그 정적을 깨뜨렸다. 계단이 삐걱대더니, 그가 아래층 방으로 내려와 불 쪽으로 다가가 초에 불을 붙였다.\n\n그 사내는 카드루스였다. 그는 핏기가 가셨고, 셔츠는 온통 피투성이였다. 불을 밝힌 그는 다시 황급히 위층으로 올라갔고, 나는 또 한 번 그의 빠르고 불안한 발소리를 들었다.\n\n잠시 뒤 그가 다시 내려왔다. 손에 작은 가죽 상자를 들고 있었는데, 그것을 열어 다이아몬드가 들어 있는지 확인했다. 그러고는 어느 주머니에 넣을지 망설이는 듯하더니, 어느 쪽 주머니도 미덥지 않은 듯 빨간 손수건에 싸서 머리에 조심스레 둘둘 동여맸다.\n\n그러고 나서 그는 찬장에 넣어 두었던 지폐와 금화를 꺼내, 한쪽은 바지 주머니에, 다른 한쪽은 조끼 주머니에 쑤셔 넣고, 황급히 옷가지 한 보따리를 묶고는 문으로 달려가 어두운 밤 속으로 사라졌다.\n\n그러자 모든 것이 또렷하고 분명해졌고, 나는 마치 그 죄를 내 손으로 저지른 것처럼 벌어진 일을 자책했다. 아직도 희미한 신음 소리가 들리는 듯했고, 불운한 보석상이 아주 죽지는 않았을지도 모른다는 생각에, 그를 구하러 가기로 마음먹었다. 내가 저지른 죄가 아니라, 막으려 애쓰지 않은 그 죄를 조금이나마 속죄하기 위해서였다. 그러기 위해 나는 가진 힘을 다 짜내, 내가 웅크리고 있던 비좁은 자리에서 옆방으로 비집고 들어갔다. 나와 옆방을 가르던 허술하게 박힌 판자가 내 힘에 못 이겨 떨어져 나갔고, 나는 집 안으로 들어섰다. 황급히 켜져 있는 초를 집어 들고 계단으로 달려갔다. 계단 중간쯤에 시신 하나가 계단을 가로질러 누워 있었다. 카르콩트의 시신이었다. 내가 들었던 그 권총은 분명 그녀를 향해 발사된 것이었다. 총탄에 목이 끔찍하게 찢겨, 입에서뿐 아니라 쩍 벌어진 두 군데 상처에서도 피가 콸콸 쏟아지고 있었다. 그녀는 완전히 숨이 끊겨 있었다. 나는 그녀를 성큼 넘어 침실로 올라갔다. 그곳은 더없이 어지럽게 난장판이 되어 있었다. 거기서 벌어진 사투에 가구가 나뒹굴었고, 불운한 보석상이 분명 매달렸을 시트는 방을 가로질러 끌려가 있었다. 살해된 사내는 마룻바닥에 머리를 벽에 기댄 채 쓰러져 있었고, 그 주위로는 가슴의 커다란 상처 세 군데에서 쏟아진 피가 흥건히 고여 있었다. 게다가 네 번째 자상이 있었는데, 거기에는 긴 식칼이 손잡이까지 박혀 있었다.\n\n나는 무언가에 발이 걸려 휘청했다. 몸을 굽혀 살펴보니 두 번째 권총이었다. 발사되지 않은 것을 보면, 아마 화약이 젖은 탓인 듯했다. 나는 보석상에게 다가갔다. 그는 아주 죽지는 않았는데, 내 발소리와 마루 삐걱대는 소리에 눈을 떠 불안하게 캐묻는 눈길로 나를 응시하더니, 입술을 달싹여 무슨 말을 하려다 그 힘에 부쳐 도로 쓰러져 숨을 거두었다.\n\n이 소름 끼치는 광경에 나는 거의 제정신을 잃었고, 더는 이 집의 누구에게도 도움이 될 수 없음을 깨닫자, 오직 달아나고 싶은 마음뿐이었다. 나는 머리를 쥐어뜯으며 공포의 신음을 내뱉고 계단으로 달려갔다.\n\n아래층 방에 이르렀을 때, 거기에는 세관원이 대여섯 명, 헌병이 두세 명 있었다. 모두 단단히 무장한 상태였다. 그들이 내게 달려들었다. 나는 저항하지 않았다. 더는 제정신이 아니었다. 말을 하려 해도 입에서 알아들을 수 없는 소리 몇 마디만 새어 나올 뿐이었다.\n\n그들 모두가 내 피투성이 옷을 의미심장하게 가리키는 것을 알아채고, 나는 무심결에 내 몸을 살펴보았다. 그러고는 계단 밑에 누워 있을 때 나를 그토록 적셨던 그 따뜻하고 짙은 핏방울이 카르콩트의 피였음을 깨달았다. 나는 내가 숨어 있던 자리를 가리켰다.\n\n\"저자가 무슨 말을 하려는 거지?\" 한 헌병이 물었다.\n\n세관원 하나가 내가 가리킨 곳으로 갔다.\n\n\"저자 말은,\" 돌아온 그가 대답했다. \"이리로 들어왔다는 겁니다.\" 그러면서 내가 뚫고 들어오느라 낸 구멍을 보여 주었다.\n\n그제야 나는 그들이 나를 살인자로 여긴다는 것을 알았다. 나는 나를 붙든 자들의 손에서 벗어날 만큼의 기운과 힘을 되찾으며, 더듬더듬 외쳤다.\n\n\"내가 한 게 아니오! 정말로, 정말로 내가 한 게 아니란 말이오!\"\n\n헌병 둘이 기총 총구를 내 가슴에 들이댔다.\n\n\"한 발짝이라도 움직이면,\" 그들이 말했다. \"넌 죽은 목숨이다.\"\n\n\"이미 결백을 밝혔는데,\" 내가 외쳤다. \"왜 나를 죽이겠다고 으름장을 놓는 거요?\"\n\n\"쳇, 쳇.\" 사내들이 외쳤다. \"그 결백 타령은 님 재판관 앞에 가서나 늘어놓아라. 그때까진 순순히 우리를 따라오는 게 네게 해 줄 수 있는 최선의 충고다.\"\n\n아아, 저항이라니 나로서는 꿈도 못 꿀 일이었다. 나는 놀라움과 공포에 완전히 짓눌려, 군말 없이 수갑을 차고 말 꼬리에 묶여 끌려갔다. 그렇게 그들은 나를 님으로 데려갔다.\n\n한 세관원이 주막 근처에서 나를 놓치고는 내 뒤를 쫓고 있었던 것이다. 내가 거기서 하룻밤 묵으려 한다고 확신한 그는 동료들을 부르러 돌아갔고, 그들은 마침 권총 소리를 듣기에 딱 맞춰 도착했다. 그렇게 그들은 내 죄를 입증하는 정황 증거가 빼곡한 한복판에서 나를 붙잡았으니, 결백을 증명할 희망 따위는 죄다 부질없었다. 내게 남은 단 한 가닥 기회는, 끌려간 곳의 치안판사에게 부소니 사제를 백방으로 수소문해 달라고 애원하는 것뿐이었다. 그 사제는 그날 아침 퐁뒤가르의 여관에 들렀던 사람이었다.\n\n만일 카드루스가 다이아몬드에 얽힌 이야기를 지어낸 것이고, 부소니 사제라는 사람이 애당초 존재하지 않는다면, 나는 정말로 돌이킬 수 없이 끝장난 셈이었다. 아니면 적어도 내 목숨은 카드루스 본인이 붙잡혀 모든 진실을 자백하리라는 가냘픈 가능성에 매달려 있는 셈이었다.\n\n내 쪽에서는 가망 없는 기다림 속에 두 달이 흘렀다. 그래도 치안판사를 두고 공정하게 말하자면, 그는 내가 결백을 밝혀 줄 수 있다고 주장한 그 사람의 행방을 알아내려고 온갖 수단을 동원했다. 카드루스는 여전히 추적을 따돌렸고, 나는 피할 수 없어 보이는 운명에 체념해 있었다. 내 재판은 다가오는 순회재판에서 열릴 예정이었다. 그런데 9월 8일, 그러니까 내 목숨을 위태롭게 한 사건이 있은 지 정확히 석 달 닷새째 되던 날, 다시 보리라고는 감히 믿지도 못했던 부소니 사제가 감옥 문 앞에 나타났다. 그는 죄수 가운데 자기와 이야기하고 싶어 하는 사람이 있다고 들었다고 했다. 그러면서, 마르세유에서 내 투옥에 얽힌 자초지종을 알게 되어 내 바람에 응하려고 서둘러 왔다고 덧붙였다.\n\n내가 얼마나 간절히 그를 맞았는지, 그리고 내가 보고 들은 모든 것을 얼마나 낱낱이 들려주었는지는 쉬이 짐작하실 겁니다. 다이아몬드 이야기를 꺼낼 때는 다소 마음이 떨렸으나, 이루 말할 수 없이 놀랍게도 그는 그 이야기를 하나하나 다 사실로 확인해 주었다. 마찬가지로 놀랍게도, 그는 내가 한 모든 말을 온전히 믿는 듯했다.\n\n그러자 나는 그의 온화한 자비에 마음이 끌렸고, 그가 내 고향의 온갖 풍습과 관습을 훤히 알고 있음을 보았으며, 또 내가 진정 죄를 지은 단 하나의 범행에 대한 용서가 그토록 자애롭고 다정한 입술에서라면 두 배의 힘으로 내려질 수 있으리라 여겼다. 그래서 나는 그에게 내 고해를 받아 달라고 간청했고, 고해의 봉인 아래 오퇴유 사건의 자초지종은 물론, 내 삶의 다른 모든 일까지 낱낱이 털어놓았다. 가장 선한 마음에 이끌려 한 일이, 마치 계산속에서 나온 일과 똑같은 결과를 낳았다. 내가 오퇴유의 살인을 자진해 고백한 것이, 정작 내가 고발당한 범행은 저지르지 않았다는 증거가 된 것이다. 그는 나를 떠나며 부디 용기를 내라고, 그리고 재판관들에게 내 결백을 납득시키기 위해 자기가 할 수 있는 모든 일을 하리라 믿어도 된다고 일렀다.\n\n이 훌륭한 사제가 나를 위해 애쓰고 있다는 증거는 곧바로 나타났다. 옥살이의 가혹함이 사소하나마 고마운 여러 배려로 누그러졌고, 내 재판이 지금 열리고 있는 순회재판 다음 회기로 미뤄졌다는 말을 들었으니 말이다.\n\n그사이 신의 섭리는 카드루스를 붙잡게 했다. 그는 멀리 떨어진 어느 나라에서 발각되어 프랑스로 압송되었고, 거기서 모든 것을 자백했다. 그는 아내가 살인을 부추기고 꾸몄다는 사실을 제 죄의 핑계로 삼기를 한사코 거부했다. 그 가련한 사내는 무기 도형을 선고받았고, 나는 곧바로 풀려났다.\"\n\n\"그래서 그때,\" 몬테 크리스토가 말했다. \"자네가 부소니 사제의 편지를 들고 나를 찾아온 것이로군?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 나리. 그 자애로운 사제는 저와 관련된 모든 일에 분명한 관심을 보였습니다.\n\n'밀수꾼으로 살아가는 자네의 생활은,' 어느 날 그가 제게 말했습니다. '결국 자네를 파멸시킬 걸세. 풀려나거든 다시는 그 일에 손대지 말게.'\n\n'하지만 어떻게,' 제가 물었습니다. '저 자신과 가엾은 누이를 건사하란 말씀이십니까?'\n\n'내가 고해를 맡고 있고,' 그가 대답했습니다. '나를 무척 존중하는 어떤 사람이 얼마 전 내게 믿을 만한 하인 하나를 구해 달라고 부탁했네. 그런 자리라면 마음에 드는가? 그렇다면 그에게 보낼 소개장을 써 주겠네.'\n\n'오, 신부님,' 제가 외쳤습니다. '참으로 인자하십니다.'\n\n'하지만 내 추천을 후회할 일은 결코 없으리라고 엄숙히 맹세해야 하네.'\n\n저는 손을 내밀어 그가 부르는 대로 무슨 약속이든 하려 했으나, 그가 저를 막았습니다.\n\n'무슨 맹세로 스스로를 옭아맬 것 없네,' 그가 말했습니다. '나는 코르시카 사람의 천성을 너무도 잘 알고 또 높이 사고 있으니 자네를 두려워하지 않네. 자, 이걸 받게.' 그러고는 제가 나리께 가져다드린 그 몇 줄을 재빨리 써 주었습니다. 그 편지를 받으시고 나리께서는 저를 거두어 일을 맡겨 주셨지요. 그래서 떳떳이 여쭙건대, 나리께서는 그렇게 하신 것을 후회하실 일이 한 번이라도 있으셨습니까?\"\n\n\"없네.\" 백작이 대답했다. \"자네가 나를 충직하게 섬겼다는 말은 기꺼이 해 주지, 베르투치오. 다만 자네는 내게 좀 더 마음을 터놓을 수도 있었을 거야.\"\n\n\"제가요, 나리?\"\n\n\"그래, 자네 말일세. 누이와 양아들이 둘 다 있으면서, 어찌 그 어느 쪽 이야기도 내게 한 번도 하지 않았나?\"\n\n\"아아, 제 삶에서 가장 비통한 시기를 아직 들려드려야겠군요. 짐작하시겠지만, 저는 사랑하는 누이를 만나 위로하고 싶은 마음에 한시도 지체 않고 코르시카로 달려갔습니다. 그러나 롤리아노에 다다랐을 때 저는 초상집을 보았습니다. 어찌나 끔찍한 일이 벌어졌던지, 동네 사람들이 오늘까지도 기억하며 입에 올리는 그런 참극의 결과였습니다. 제 충고에 따라 가엾은 누이는 베네데토의 터무니없는 요구를 들어주기를 거절했습니다. 그놈은 누이에게 한 푼이라도 남아 있다고 여기는 한 끊임없이 돈을 뜯어내려 들들 볶았으니까요. 어느 날 아침 그놈은 자기가 원하는 것을 내놓지 않으면 가장 끔찍한 꼴을 보게 될 거라고 누이를 협박하고는 자취를 감추어 온종일 돌아오지 않았습니다. 그놈을 제 자식처럼 사랑하던 마음씨 고운 아순타는 그 행실을 두고 눈물 흘리며 부재를 슬퍼할 뿐이었지요. 저녁이 되었어도, 누이는 어머니다운 끈질긴 애정으로 그놈이 돌아오기를 기다리고 있었습니다.\n\n밤 열한 시를 알리는 종이 울리자, 그놈이 거들먹거리며 들어왔습니다. 가장 타락하고 무모한 술친구 둘을 데리고서요. 누이가 그놈에게 두 팔을 벌렸으나, 그들이 누이를 붙들었고, 셋 중 하나가, 다름 아닌 그 저주받을 베네데토가 외쳤습니다.\n\n'이 여자를 고문해라. 그럼 돈을 어디 숨겼는지 곧 불 거다.'\n\n공교롭게도 그때 이웃인 바실리오가 바스티아에 가 있어서, 그 집에는 그의 아내 말고는 아무도 없었습니다. 우리 집에서 벌어지는 일을 듣거나 볼 수 있는 사람은 달리 아무도 없었지요. 둘이서 가엾은 아순타를 붙들었습니다. 누이는 자기에게 해를 끼치려 한다고는 상상조차 못 하고, 머잖아 제 처형인이 될 자들의 얼굴에 대고 미소 지었습니다. 세 번째 놈은 문과 창문을 막아 버리고 돌아왔습니다. 그러고는 셋이 함께, 그 채비를 보고 겁에 질려 터져 나오는 누이의 비명을 틀어막고는, 아순타의 발을 먼저 끌어 화로 쪽으로 잡아당겼습니다. 숨겨 두었으리라 여긴 보물이 어디 있는지 실토하게 하려는 속셈이었지요. 그 와중에 누이의 옷에 불이 붙었고, 그들은 같은 꼴을 당하지 않으려고 손을 놓을 수밖에 없었습니다. 불길에 휩싸인 아순타는 미친 듯이 문으로 달려갔으나 문은 잠겨 있었습니다. 창문으로 날아갔으나 그것 또한 막혀 있었지요. 그제야 이웃들은 무시무시한 비명을 들었습니다. 도와 달라고 외치는 아순타의 소리였습니다. 그 외침은 신음 속에 잦아들었고, 이튿날 아침 바실리오의 아내가 겨우 용기를 내어 밖으로 나설 수 있게 되자, 그녀는 관청에 알려 우리 집 문을 열게 했습니다. 그때 아순타는 끔찍하게 불에 탔으나 아직 숨이 붙어 있는 채로 발견되었습니다. 집 안의 서랍과 벽장이란 벽장은 모조리 부서져 열려 있었고, 돈은 도둑맞았습니다. 베네데토는 그 뒤로 다시는 롤리아노에 나타나지 않았고, 저도 그날 이후로 그놈에 대해 보거나 들은 것이 전혀 없습니다.\n\n제가 나리를 모시게 된 것은 이 끔찍한 일들이 있은 뒤였습니다. 나리께 베네데토 이야기를 꺼내 봐야 어리석은 짓이었지요. 그놈의 자취는 완전히 끊겨 버린 듯했으니까요. 또 누이 이야기를 꺼낼 것도 없었습니다. 누이는 이미 죽었으니까요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그 일을 자네는 어떻게 보았나?\" 몬테 크리스토가 물었다.\n\n\"제가 저지른 죄에 대한 벌이라고요.\" 베르투치오가 대답했다. \"아, 그 빌포르 가문은 저주받은 종자들입니다!\"\n\n\"정말 그렇지.\" 백작이 음울한 어조로 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"그러니,\" 베르투치오가 말을 이었다. \"나리께서는 이제 어쩌면 헤아리실 수 있을 겁니다. 제가 처음으로 다시 찾은 이곳, 바로 제 범행의 현장인 이 정원이 어찌하여 그리 유쾌하지 못한 생각들을 불러일으켰는지, 그리고 어찌하여 나리께서 알아채시고 그 까닭을 알고 싶다 하셨던 그 침울함과 의기소침을 자아냈는지를요. 이 순간에도 저는 몸서리가 칩니다. 어쩌면 제가 지금 빌포르 씨가 누워 있는 바로 그 무덤 위에 서 있을지도 모른다는 생각에 말입니다. 자기 자식의 시신을 묻으려고 손수 땅을 팠던 바로 그 사내 말입니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 일이든 있을 수 있지.\" 몬테 크리스토가 앉아 있던 벤치에서 일어서며 말했다. \"심지어,\" 그가 들릴 듯 말 듯한 목소리로 덧붙였다. \"그 검사가 죽지 않았다는 것조차도. 부소니 사제는 자네를 내게 보낸 게 옳았어.\" 그가 다시 평소의 어조로 말을 이었다. \"그리고 자네는 자기 이야기를 전부 들려준 게 잘한 일이야. 앞으로 자네에 대해 내가 그릇된 판단을 내리지 않게 해 줄 테니까. 그 이름을 그토록 욕보인 베네데토 말인데, 그놈이 어디로 갔는지, 어찌 되었는지 알아내려고 애써 본 적은 한 번도 없나?\"\n\n\"없습니다. 그놈이 어디로 갔는지 알고 싶기는커녕, 저는 맹수를 피하듯 그놈과 마주칠 가능성마저 피하려 들 겁니다. 다행히 저는 누구한테서도 그놈의 이름이 입에 오르는 것을 들은 적이 없고, 그놈이 죽었기를 바라며 또 그렇게 믿습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각지 말게, 베르투치오.\" 백작이 대답했다. \"악인이란 그리 쉽사리 없어지지 않는 법이니까. 신께서는 그들을 당신 복수의 도구로 쓰시려고 특별히 굽어살피시는 듯하거든.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그런 거겠지요.\" 베르투치오가 대답했다. \"제가 하늘에 바라는 것이라곤 다시는 그놈을 보지 않는 것뿐입니다. 그리고 이제 나리,\" 그가 고개를 숙이며 덧붙였다. \"나리께서는 모든 것을 아십니다. 전능하신 분이 하늘에서 그러하시듯, 나리는 이 땅에서 저를 심판하시는 분입니다. 저를 위한 위로의 말씀은 없으신지요?\"\n\n\"이보게, 나는 부소니 사제가 자네에게 한 말을 되풀이할 수 있을 뿐이네. 빌포르는 자네에게, 그리고 어쩌면 다른 이들에게도 저지른 일로 마땅히 벌을 받아야 했지. 베네데토는 아직 살아 있다면 어떤 식으로든 신의 응징의 도구가 될 것이고, 그런 다음 제 차례가 되어 응당한 벌을 받을 걸세. 자네 자신으로 말하자면, 자네가 정말로 죄를 지은 대목은 단 하나밖에 보이지 않네. 스스로에게 물어보게. 그 갓난아이를 산 무덤에서 구해 낸 뒤, 어찌하여 그 어미에게 돌려주지 않았는가를. 거기에 죄가 있었네, 베르투치오. 바로 거기서 자네는 정말로 죄인이 된 거야.\"\n\n\"옳은 말씀입니다, 나리. 그것이 죄였습니다. 진짜 죄였지요. 거기서 저는 비겁자처럼 굴었으니까요. 갓난아이를 되살려 내는 데 성공한 그 즉시, 제 첫 번째 의무는 그 아이를 어미에게 돌려주는 것이었습니다. 그러나 그러자면 면밀하고 조심스러운 수소문을 거쳐야 했을 테고, 그러다 보면 십중팔구 제가 붙잡히는 결과로 이어졌을 겁니다. 게다가 저는 목숨에 매달려 있었습니다. 한편으로는 누이 때문이었고, 또 한편으로는 우리 마음속에 타고난 그 자존심 때문이었지요. 복수를 마치고도 털끝 하나 다치지 않은 채 의기양양하게 살아남고 싶다는 그 마음 말입니다. 또 어쩌면 누구에게나 타고난 본능적인 삶의 애착이 제 목숨을 위태롭게 하기를 꺼리게 했는지도 모릅니다. 그리고 거기다, 저는 제 가엾은 형만큼 용감하고 담대하지도 못했고요.\"\n\n베르투치오는 이 말을 내뱉으며 두 손에 얼굴을 묻었고, 몬테 크리스토는 헤아릴 수 없는 의미를 담은 눈길로 그를 응시했다. 시각과 장소 탓에 한층 엄숙해진 짧은 침묵 뒤에, 백작이 평소와는 사뭇 다른 우수에 잠긴 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"이 대화를 알맞게 매듭짓기 위해(이 일을 두고 나누는 마지막 대화가 될 테니), 부소니 사제의 입에서 들은 몇 마디를 자네에게 되풀이해 주겠네. 모든 불행에는 두 가지 약이 있다. 시간과 침묵이지. 자, 베르투치오 씨, 이제 나 혼자 이 정원을 거닐게 두고 물러가게. 여기서 벌어진 비극의 장본인인 자네에게 그토록 고통스러운 감정을 안기는 바로 그 정황들이, 내게는 도리어 일종의 만족과도 같은 것의 원천이 되고, 이 집의 가치를 내 눈에 더욱 높여 줄 뿐일세. 나무의 으뜸가는 아름다움은 그 우거진 가지가 드리우는 짙은 그늘에 있는 법이고, 그러는 동안 상상은 그 그늘 아래로 어른거리며 스쳐 지나가는 갖가지 형상과 모습의 무리를 그려 내는 법이지. 여기 내게는 상상이 한껏 펼쳐질 수 있도록 꾸며진 정원이 있고, 빽빽이 우거진 나무들이 들어찼으니, 나 같은 몽상가는 그 잎의 장막 아래서 마음껏 환영을 불러낼 수 있다네. 곧은 담장으로 둘러싸인 텅 빈 마당이나 보게 되리라 예상했던 내게는, 분명히 말하지만 더없이 흐뭇한 뜻밖의 선물일세. 나는 유령을 두려워하지 않거니와, 죽은 자들이 육천 년 동안 끼친 해악이 산 자들이 단 하루에 저지르는 해악만큼 크다는 말은 들어 본 적이 없네. 안으로 들어가게, 베르투치오, 그리고 마음을 가라앉히게. 임종의 순간에 자네의 고해 신부가 부소니 사제만큼 자네에게 너그럽지 못하거든, 내가 아직 이 땅에 있거든 나를 부르게. 그러면 자네의 떠나는 영혼이 영원이라는 대양을 가로질러 나아가기에 앞서, 그것을 어김없이 잠재우고 달래 줄 말로 자네의 귀를 어루만져 주겠네.\"\n\n베르투치오는 공손히 절하고는 무겁게 한숨지으며 돌아섰다. 홀로 남은 몬테 크리스토는 서너 걸음 앞으로 나아가며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"여기, 이 플라타너스 아래가 그 갓난아이의 무덤을 팠던 자리가 틀림없어. 저기 정원으로 통하는 작은 문이 있고. 이 모퉁이에는 침실로 이어지는 은밀한 계단이 있지. 이런 자질구레한 것들을 굳이 적어 둘 필요는 없겠군. 저기, 내 눈앞에, 내 발밑에, 내 주위 사방에, 그 도면이 살아 있는 진실 그대로 그려져 있으니까.\"\n\n정원을 한 차례 더 돌아본 뒤, 백작은 다시 마차에 올랐다. 주인의 표정에 어린 깊은 상념을 알아챈 베르투치오는 한마디도 하지 않고 마부 옆자리에 앉았다. 마차는 파리를 향해 빠르게 나아갔다.\n\n그날 저녁, 샹젤리제의 거처에 다다른 몬테 크리스토 백작은 마치 구석구석을 오래전부터 훤히 알고 있는 사람의 태도로 건물 전체를 둘러보았다. 일행보다 앞장서 걸으면서도, 그는 한 번도 문을 헷갈리거나, 가고자 하는 방이나 방들이 늘어선 곳으로 데려다줄 복도며 계단을 고를 때 사소한 실수 하나 저지르지 않았다. 이 야간 점검 동안 그의 곁을 시중든 것은 주로 알리였다. 백작은 집에 하고 싶은 개수와 변경에 관해 베르투치오에게 이런저런 지시를 내린 다음, 회중시계를 꺼내 들고 곁에서 대기 중인 누비아인에게 말했다.\n\n\"열한 시 반이로군. 하이데가 곧 도착할 게다. 프랑스인 시중꾼들에게 마중 나와 기다리라고 일렀느냐?\"\n\n알리가 아름다운 그리스 여인을 위해 마련된 방들 쪽으로 두 손을 뻗었다. 그 방들은 융단을 드리운 입구로 어찌나 감쪽같이 가려져 있던지, 아무리 호기심 많은 사람이라도 그 존재를 알아채기 어려웠을 것이다. 그 방들을 가리킨 알리는 오른손 손가락 셋을 펴 보이고는, 그 손을 머리 밑에 받친 채 눈을 감고 자는 시늉을 했다.\n\n\"알겠다.\" 알리의 손짓에 익숙한 몬테 크리스토가 말했다. \"시중드는 여인 셋이 침실에서 새 안주인을 기다리고 있다는 말이구나.\"\n\n알리가 자못 활기차게 그렇다는 시늉을 했다.\n\n\"마님은 오늘 밤 피곤할 게다.\" 몬테 크리스토가 말을 이었다. \"분명 쉬고 싶어 할 테고. 프랑스인 시중꾼들에게 질문 따위로 마님을 성가시게 하지 말고, 그저 공손히 예를 갖춘 다음 물러가라고 일러라. 그리고 그리스인 하인들이 이 나라 하인들과는 일절 왕래하지 않도록 살피거라.\"\n\n알리가 절을 했다. 바로 그때 문지기를 부르는 목소리가 들렸다. 대문이 열리고, 마차 한 대가 가로수 길을 굴러 내려와 계단 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 백작이 황급히 내려가, 이미 열린 마차 문 앞에 나서서 한 젊은 여인에게 손을 내밀었다. 그녀는 금실로 화려하게 수놓은 초록빛 비단 외투에 온몸을 폭 감싸고 있었다. 그녀는 자기 쪽으로 내밀어진 손을 들어 입술로 가져가, 사랑과 존경이 뒤섞인 입맞춤을 했다. 호메로스가 신들에게 나누게 한 그 낭랑한 언어로 두 사람 사이에 몇 마디가 오갔다. 젊은 여인은 깊은 다정함이 어린 어조로 말했고, 백작은 부드럽되 진중한 태도로 대답했다.\n\n장밋빛 횃불을 손에 든 알리를 앞세우고, 그 젊은 여인은 방으로 안내되었다. 그녀는 다름 아닌, 이탈리아에서 몬테 크리스토와 함께 다니던 그 사랑스러운 그리스 여인이었다. 백작은 자기를 위해 마련된 별채로 물러갔다. 한 시간 뒤에는 집 안의 모든 불이 꺼졌고, 그 안의 모든 사람이 잠든 것이라 여겨도 좋았을 것이다.\n\n# Chapter 46. Unlimited Credit\n\n이튿날 두 시쯤, 멋진 영국산 말 두 필이 끄는 사륜마차 한 대가 몬테크리스토 저택 문 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 마차에서 한 사내가 마차 문 너머로 몸을 내밀었다. 그는 같은 색 단추가 달린 파란 외투를 걸쳤고, 흰 조끼 위로 묵직한 금사슬을 늘어뜨렸으며, 갈색 바지를 입고 있었다. 검은 머리카락이 눈썹 위까지 어찌나 낮게 내려와 있는지, 그 칠흑 같은 윤기가 얼굴에 깊이 패인 주름과 너무도 어울리지 않아 가발이 아닐까 싶을 정도였다. 한마디로, 분명 쉰은 넘었으면서도 마흔밖에 안 돼 보이기를 바라는 사내였다. 마차 문짝에는 남작의 문장이 새겨져 있었다. 그는 마부에게, 수위실에 들러 몬테크리스토 백작이 여기 사는지, 또 지금 안에 있는지 물어보라고 일렀다.\n\n기다리는 동안 마차 안의 사내는 저택과, 눈에 들어오는 데까지의 정원과, 이리저리 오가는 하인들의 제복을 다소 무례하다 싶을 만큼 빈틈없이 살펴보았다. 그의 눈길은 날카로웠으나 영민함보다는 교활함이 묻어났다. 입술은 일자로 가늘어, 다물면 이 위로 말려 들어갔다. 광대뼈는 넓고 튀어나와 있었는데, 이는 대담함과 음흉함의 어김없는 증표였다. 게다가 이마는 납작하고 뒤통수는 불룩 솟아, 크고 거칠게 생긴 귀보다도 훨씬 높았다. 이 모든 것이 어우러져, 도무지 호감이 가지 않는 인상을 이루었다. 다만 이렇게 으리으리한 마차를 가진 사람이라면 무엇이든 감탄스럽고 부러울 만한 인물이 틀림없다고 여기는 부류의 눈에는 달랐다. 더구나 그의 셔츠에서 번쩍이는 거대한 다이아몬드와 단춧구멍에 늘어뜨린 붉은 리본을 보면 더욱 그러했다.\n\n마부는 지시에 따라 수위실 창을 두드리며 말했다.\n\n\"실례합니다, 여기가 몬테크리스토 백작님 댁입니까?\"\n\n\"각하께서는 여기 사십니다.\" 수위가 대답했다. \"하지만……\" 그러고는 알리에게 묻는 듯한 눈길을 던졌다. 알리는 안 된다는 표시로 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"하지만 뭡니까?\" 마부가 물었다.\n\n\"각하께서는 오늘 손님을 받지 않으십니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 여기 제 주인님 명함을 두고 가겠습니다. 당글라르 남작이십니다. 백작님께 이 명함을 전하면서, 주인님이 의회에 가시는 길에도 일부러 들러 백작님을 뵙는 영광을 누리고자 하셨다고 전해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"저는 각하께 직접 말씀을 올리지 않습니다.\" 수위가 대답했다. \"시종이 전갈을 전할 겁니다.\"\n\n“그래서?” 당글라르가 물었다.\n\n\"어떻게 됐나?\" 당글라르가 물었다.\n핀잔을 들어 다소 풀이 죽은 사내는 수위가 한 말을 그대로 옮겼다.\n\n\"이런.\" 당글라르 남작이 중얼거렸다. \"백작을 두고 '각하'라 부르고, 감히 시종을 통해서만 말을 전할 수 있다니, 이건 백작이 아니라 분명 무슨 왕족이 틀림없군. 하지만 상관없어. 그는 내게 신용장을 가지고 있으니, 돈이 필요하면 결국 나를 만나야 할 테지.\"\n\n그러고는 마차에 몸을 깊이 기대며, 길 건너까지 들릴 만한 소리로 마부에게 외쳤다. \"하원으로.\"\n\n자신을 찾아온 손님을 미리 전해 들은 몬테크리스토는 별채의 덧창 뒤에서 훌륭한 오페라글라스로 남작을 샅샅이 살펴보았다. 당글라르가 저택과 정원과 하인들을 뜯어본 것과 꼭 마찬가지였다.\n\n\"저 작자, 얼굴이 영 좋지 않군.\" 백작이 오페라글라스를 상아 갑에 도로 넣으며 역겹다는 듯이 말했다. \"저 납작하게 들어간 뱀 같은 이마, 둥글둥글한 독수리 머리통, 매부리처럼 끝이 뾰족하게 휘어진 코를 보고도 다들 질겁해 달아나지 않다니 어찌 된 노릇인가? 알리.\" 그러면서 동시에 놋쇠 징을 쳤다. 알리가 나타났다. \"베르투치오를 불러라.\" 백작이 말했다. 거의 곧바로 베르투치오가 방으로 들어왔다.\n\n\"부르셨습니까, 각하?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"불렀네.\" 백작이 대답했다. \"조금 전 문 앞에 서 있던 말들을 보았겠지?\"\n\n\"물론입니다, 각하. 워낙 빼어나게 아름다워 눈여겨보았습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 어찌 된 일인가.\" 몬테크리스토가 이맛살을 찌푸리며 말했다. \"파리에서 구할 수 있는 가장 훌륭한 한 쌍을 사 오라고 일렀건만, 내 것 못지않게 빼어난 또 한 쌍이 내 마구간에 없으니 말일세?\"\n\n백작의 노기 띤 말투에 더해 못마땅해하는 기색이 드러나자, 알리는 얼굴이 새파래져 고개를 떨구었다.\n\n\"네 잘못이 아니다, 착한 알리야.\" 백작이 아랍어로, 그 목소리나 표정에서 누구도 그가 보일 수 있으리라 짐작 못 할 다정함을 담아 말했다. \"네 잘못이 아니야. 너는 영국 말을 보는 안목이 없으니까.\"\n\n가엾은 알리의 얼굴에 다시 평온이 돌아왔다.\n\n\"각하께 한 말씀 올리겠습니다.\" 베르투치오가 말했다. \"제가 각하의 말을 살 때, 말씀하신 그 말들은 팔려고 내놓은 것이 아니었습니다.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 어깨를 으쓱했다. \"이보게 집사.\" 그가 말했다. \"값을 치를 마음만 있다면 세상 모든 것은 살 수 있는 법인데, 자네는 아직 그걸 모르는 모양이군.\"\n\n\"혹시 당글라르 씨가 그 말들에 1만 6천 프랑을 치른 것을 각하께서 모르시는지요?\"\n\n\"좋아. 그럼 그 두 배를 부르게. 은행가란 제 자본을 곱절로 불릴 기회를 결코 놓치지 않는 법이지.\"\n\n\"각하, 정말로 진심이십니까?\" 집사가 물었다.\n\n몬테크리스토는 감히 제 말을 의심하려 드는 자를, 놀라움과 불쾌함이 뒤섞인 눈빛으로 바라보았다.\n\n\"오늘 저녁에 방문할 데가 있네.\" 그가 대답했다. \"그 말들에 완전히 새 마구를 채워, 내 마차와 함께 문 앞에 대 놓도록 하게.\"\n\n베르투치오는 절을 하고 물러가려 했다. 그러나 문에 이르자 걸음을 멈추더니 말했다. \"마차와 말은 몇 시까지 채비를 해 두면 되겠습니까, 각하?\"\n\n\"다섯 시.\" 백작이 대답했다.\n\n\"송구하오나, 각하.\" 집사가 조심스러운 투로 끼어들었다. \"감히 말씀드리자면, 지금 벌써 두 시입니다.\"\n\n\"나도 그쯤은 잘 알고 있네.\" 몬테크리스토가 차분히 대답했다. 그러고는 알리 쪽으로 돌아서서 말했다. \"마구간에 있는 말을 모조리 그 아가씨의 창 앞으로 끌고 가, 마차에 매고 싶은 말을 직접 고르게 하라. 또 나와 함께 저녁을 들겠는지도 물어봐 다오. 그러겠다면 그녀의 방으로 저녁상을 들이고. 자, 이제 물러가고, 내 시종을 이리로 들라 일러라.\"\n\n알리가 사라지기 무섭게 시종이 방으로 들어왔다.\n\n\"바티스탱 군.\" 백작이 말했다. \"자네가 내 밑에서 일한 지 일 년이 되었네. 내가 주위 사람의 됨됨이를 가늠하는 데 으레 두는 기간이지. 자네는 내 마음에 썩 든다네.\"\n\n바티스탱이 깊이 절했다.\n\n\"이제 남은 건, 나 또한 자네 마음에 드는지 아는 것뿐이군.\"\n\n\"오, 각하!\" 바티스탱이 다급히 외쳤다.\n\n\"부디 내 말이 끝날 때까지 들어 주게.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"자네는 여기서 일한 대가로 연 1천5백 프랑을 받지. 조국을 위해 끊임없이 목숨을 거는 어지간한 용감한 하급 장교가 받는 것보다도 많은 액수일세. 자네는 자네보다 열 배는 고되게 일하고도 그만한 돈을 받는 숱한 사무원들보다 훨씬 낫게 살고 있어. 게다가 자네 자신이 하인이면서도, 시중을 들고 옷을 챙기며 자네 속옷을 제때 마련해 주는 다른 하인들까지 거느리고 있지. 또한 자네는 내 몸치장에 쓸 물건을 사 올 때마다 이문을 남기는데, 일 년이면 자네 봉급에 맞먹는 액수가 되더군.\"\n\n\"아니, 정말로, 각하……\"\n\n\"내가 그 일로 자네를 나무라는 건 아니네, 바티스탱 군. 다만 이문은 거기서 그치게. 자네가 지금 누리는 이 자리만큼 짭짤한 일자리는 좀처럼 다시 못 찾을 테니까. 나는 말로든 행동으로든 하인을 함부로 대하거나 학대하지 않아. 실수는 기꺼이 용서하지만, 고의로 게으름을 부리거나 잊어버리는 것은 결코 용서치 않네. 내 명령은 으레 짧고 분명하며 정확하지. 그리고 내 말이 잘못 전해지느니, 차라리 두 번, 세 번 거듭 말하는 편이 낫다고 여긴다네. 나는 알고자 하는 것은 무엇이든 알아낼 만큼 부유하고, 호기심도 적잖다는 걸 약속하지. 그러니 만약 자네가 나를 두고 누구에게든 좋게든 나쁘게든 떠벌리거나, 내 행동을 입에 올리거나, 내 처신을 엿보았다는 걸 알게 되면, 그 즉시 자네는 내 곁을 떠나게 될 걸세. 이제 물러가도 좋네. 나는 하인에게 두 번 다시 경고하지 않으니, 그 점을 명심하게.\"\n\n바티스탱이 절을 하고 문 쪽으로 걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n\"한 가지 일러두는 걸 깜빡했군.\" 백작이 말했다. \"나는 해마다 내 집의 하인 하나하나를 위해 일정한 돈을 따로 떼어 두네. 부득이 내보내야 하는 자는 당연히 그 돈에 대한 몫을 모두 잃고, 그 몫은 내 곁에 남는 하인들을 위해 쌓이는 기금으로 들어가지. 그래서 내가 죽으면 그들 사이에 나뉠 걸세. 자네는 내 밑에서 일한 지 일 년이 되어 자네 몫의 기금이 이미 쌓이기 시작했으니, 그대로 계속 불려 가게나.\"\n\n이 말은 알리가 보는 앞에서 이루어졌다. 알리는 그 말이 오간 언어를 한마디도 알아듣지 못해 미동도 없이 서 있었다. 그러나 바티스탱에게는, 프랑스 하인의 성정과 기질을 살펴본 적 있는 자라야 짐작할 만한 효과를 일으켰다.\n\n\"각하께 약속드리겠습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"적어도 매사에 각하의 인정을 받도록 힘쓰겠으며, 알리 씨를 본보기로 삼겠습니다.\"\n\n\"천만에.\" 백작이 더없이 차가운 어조로 대답했다. \"알리는 더없이 빼어난 자질에 못지않게 결점도 많은 자일세. 자네에게 처신의 본보기가 될 수 없지. 그는 자네처럼 봉급을 받는 하인이 아니라 한낱 노예니까. 한 마리 개일세. 만약 그가 내게 주어진 임무를 저버린다면, 나는 그를 내쫓는 게 아니라 죽일 걸세.\"\n\n바티스탱은 놀라 눈을 휘둥그레 떴다.\n\n\"못 믿겠다는 얼굴이군.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. 그러고는 방금 프랑스어로 바티스탱에게 한 말을 알리에게 아랍어로 되풀이해 일러 주었다.\n\n누비아인은 주인의 말에 동의하듯 미소 지었다. 그러더니 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고 백작의 손에 공손히 입을 맞추었다. 방금 들은 그 말을 이렇게 뒷받침하는 광경은, 바티스탱의 경악과 어리둥절함에 마지막 쐐기를 박았다. 백작은 시종에게 물러가라는 손짓을 하고, 알리에게는 서재로 따라오라 일렀다. 두 사람은 그곳에서 오래도록 진지하게 이야기를 나누었다. 시곗바늘이 다섯 시를 가리키자 백작은 징을 세 번 쳤다. 알리를 부를 때는 한 번, 바티스탱은 두 번, 베르투치오는 세 번 치는 것이었다. 집사가 들어왔다.\n\n\"내 말은.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"각하께서 분부하신 대로 마차에 매여 문 앞에 대 놓았습니다. 제가 모시고 갈까요, 각하?\"\n\n\"아니, 마부와 알리, 바티스탱이 갈 걸세.\"\n\n백작은 저택 문 앞으로 내려가, 아침에 당글라르의 것이라며 그토록 감탄해 마지않던 바로 그 한 쌍의 말이 끄는 마차를 보았다. 그 곁을 지나며 그가 말했다.\n\n\"확실히 더없이 잘생긴 놈들이군. 자네가 잘 사 왔어. 다만 좀 더 일찍 손에 넣지 못한 것은 다소 굼떴다 하겠네.\"\n\n\"실은, 각하, 이 말들을 손에 넣느라 적잖이 애를 먹었습니다. 그러고도 엄청난 값을 치렀고요.\"\n\n\"자네가 치른 그 값 때문에 이 짐승들이 덜 아름다워지기라도 하나?\" 백작이 어깨를 으쓱하며 물었다.\n\n\"아닙니다. 각하께서 흡족하시다면 더 바랄 게 없습니다. 어디로 모실까요, 각하?\"\n\n\"당글라르 남작 댁으로. 쇼세당탱가일세.\"\n\n이 대화는 두 사람이 테라스에 서 있는 동안 오간 것이었다. 테라스에서 돌계단을 내려가면 마찻길이 나 있었다. 베르투치오가 공손히 절하고 물러서려는데, 백작이 그를 다시 불렀다.\n\n\"자네에게 맡길 일이 또 하나 있네, 베르투치오.\" 그가 말했다. \"노르망디 바닷가에 영지를 하나 가지고 싶군. 이를테면 르아브르와 불로뉴 사이쯤이면 좋겠어. 보다시피 자네에게 폭넓게 맡기는 거야. 다만 자네가 고르는 곳에는 반드시 작은 항구나 후미, 만이 있어서, 내 코르벳함이 들어와 닻을 내릴 수 있어야 하네. 흘수가 15피트밖에 안 되는 배일세. 내가 적당하다 싶어 신호를 보내면 곧장 출항할 수 있도록 늘 채비를 갖춰 두어야 해. 그런 곳을 찾도록 필요한 데를 두루 알아보고, 마땅한 자리를 만나거든 직접 가 보게. 바라던 이점이 갖춰져 있거든 곧바로 자네 이름으로 사들이게나. 코르벳함은 지금쯤 페캉으로 가고 있을 텐데, 그렇지 않나?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 각하. 우리가 마르세유를 떠난 바로 그날 저녁 배가 바다로 나가는 걸 제가 보았습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 요트는?\"\n\n\"마르티그에 머물도록 일러 두었습니다.\"\n\n\"잘했네. 자네가 이따금 그 두 배의 선장에게 편지를 보내, 늘 긴장을 풀지 않게 해 주었으면 하네.\"\n\n\"증기선은요?\"\n\n\"샬롱에 있나?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"그 배에도 두 범선과 같은 지시를 내리게.\"\n\n\"알겠습니다.\"\n\n\"내가 바라는 영지를 사들이거든, 북부와 남부 도로를 따라 10리외마다 갈아탈 말을 늘 마련해 두게.\"\n\n\"각하, 제게 믿고 맡기십시오.\"\n\n백작은 흡족하다는 몸짓을 하고는 테라스 계단을 내려가 마차에 뛰어올랐다. 마차는 은행가의 집을 향해 쏜살같이 내달렸다.\n\n당글라르는 그때 마침 철도 위원회를 주재하고 있었다. 그러나 손님의 이름이 알려졌을 때 회의는 거의 끝나 가던 참이었다. 백작의 칭호가 귀에 들리자 그는 자리에서 일어나, 이 의회 저 의회의 의원인 동료들을 향해 말했다.\n\n\"여러분, 이렇게 불쑥 자리를 뜨는 걸 용서하시오. 한데 우스꽝스럽기 짝이 없는 일이 생겨서 말이오. 로마의 은행 톰슨 앤드 프렌치가 몬테크리스토 백작이라는 자를 내게 보내, 우리 은행에서 무제한 신용 거래를 할 수 있게 해 주었지 뭡니까. 솔직히 내가 폭넓게 해외 거래를 해 오면서 이렇게 익살맞은 일은 처음이라, 여러분도 짐작하시겠지만 호기심이 잔뜩 동했소이다. 오늘 아침 일부러 그 자칭 백작을 찾아갔지요. 정말 백작이라면 그렇게 부유할 리가 없을 텐데 말이오. 그런데 글쎄, 믿어지십니까, '손님을 받지 않는다'더군요. 그러니 이 몬테크리스토의 주인이라는 자는 대단한 백만장자나 변덕스러운 미인이나 부릴 법한 거드름을 피우는 셈이오. 알아보니 샹젤리제의 그 저택은 그자 소유고, 분명 꽤 그럴듯하게 관리되고 있더군요. 하지만,\" 당글라르가 음흉한 미소를 띠며 말을 이었다. \"무제한 신용 거래를 허락하는 지시서라는 건, 그걸 받은 은행가로서는 어느 정도 조심하지 않을 수 없는 일이지요. 나는 이 사내를 몹시 보고 싶소이다. 무슨 속임수를 꾸미는 게 아닌가 싶지만, 그걸 꾸민 자들은 자기네가 누굴 상대하는지 까맣게 몰랐던 게요. '마지막에 웃는 자가 진짜 웃는 자'라 하지 않습니까!\"\n\n남작은 거의 숨이 찰 만큼 기세 좋게 이 거창한 연설을 늘어놓고는, 모인 이들에게 절하고 응접실로 물러났다. 흰색과 금색으로 호화롭게 꾸민 그 응접실은 쇼세당탱에서 한바탕 화제가 되었던 곳이다. 그가 손님을 이 방으로 들이라 한 것은, 그 으리으리한 사치에 압도시킬 셈이었다. 와서 보니 백작은 알바노와 파토레의 모사화 몇 점 앞에 서 있었다. 그것들은 원작이라며 은행가에게 떠넘겨진 것이었지만, 한낱 모사화일 뿐이어서, 천장을 뒤덮은 야한 색채와 나란히 놓이자 제 신세의 초라함을 느끼는 듯했다.\n\n백작은 당글라르가 방으로 들어오는 소리를 듣고 돌아섰다. 당글라르는 고개를 살짝 숙이며 백작에게 앉으라는 표시를 했고, 금실로 수놓은 흰 공단을 씌운 도금 안락의자를 의미심장하게 가리켰다. 백작이 앉았다.\n\n\"이렇게 뵙는 영광을 누리는 분이 몬테크리스토 백작님이시겠지요.\"\n\n백작이 절했다.\n\n\"그리고 제가 말씀을 나누는 분은 레지옹 도뇌르 슈발리에이자 하원 의원이신 당글라르 남작이시고요?\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 남작의 명함에서 읽은 칭호를 죄다 되풀이했다.\n\n당글라르는 그 비꼼을 알아채고 입술을 꾹 다물었다.\n\n\"처음 말을 건넬 때 칭호로 부르지 못한 것을 너그러이 봐주시기 바랍니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 아시다시피 우리는 민중을 위한 정부 아래 살고 있고, 저 자신이 인민의 자유를 대변하는 사람인지라 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇군요.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"그래서 자신은 남작이라 부르면서도, 남은 백작이라 불러 주기를 꺼리시는 거로군요.\"\n\n\"이거 참, 백작님.\" 당글라르가 짐짓 무심한 척하며 말했다. \"저는 그런 헛된 칭호 따위에는 아무런 가치도 두지 않습니다. 다만 사실인즉, 저는 공로를 인정받아 남작이 되었고 레지옹 도뇌르 슈발리에도 되었지요. 하지만……\"\n\n\"하지만 몽모랑시 씨나 라파예트 씨가 보인 본을 좇아 칭호를 버리셨다, 그 말씀이군요? 따를 만한 고귀한 본보기였지요.\"\n\n\"글쎄요.\" 당글라르가 대답했다. \"꼭 그렇지만도 않습니다. 하인들 앞에서는…… 무슨 말인지 아시겠지요.\"\n\n\"알겠습니다. 하인들에게는 '나리'이시고, 기자들은 '므슈'라 부르며, 유권자들은 '시민'이라 일컫는 거지요. 입헌 정부 아래서는 더없이 어울리는 구별입니다. 잘 알겠습니다.\"\n\n당글라르는 또 한 번 입술을 깨물었다. 이런 식의 말씨름으로는 도저히 몬테크리스토의 적수가 못 된다는 걸 알아챈 그는, 서둘러 좀 더 자기 입맛에 맞는 화제로 옮겨 갔다.\n\n\"한 가지 알려 드리지요, 백작님.\" 그가 절하며 말했다. \"로마의 톰슨 앤드 프렌치로부터 통지 서신을 받았습니다.\"\n\n\"반가운 소식이군요, 남작. 저야 당신 하인들 방식대로 당신을 부르는 특권을 누려야겠지만요. 저는 출생만으로도 여전히 남작이 남작인 나라에서 살다 보니, 사람들을 칭호로 부르는 못된 버릇이 들었거든요. 어쨌든 그 통지 서신이 당신께 닿았다니 더없이 기쁩니다. 덕분에 제가 직접 돈을 받으러 당신을 찾아오는, 번거롭고 떨떠름한 수고를 덜게 됐으니까요. 정식 통지 서신을 받으셨군요?\"\n\n\"네.\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"하지만 솔직히 그 뜻을 잘 모르겠더군요.\"\n\n\"그래요?\"\n\n\"그래서 제가 직접 찾아뵙는 영광을 누린 겁니다. 설명을 좀 부탁드리려고요.\"\n\n\"말씀하시지요. 자, 여기 있으니 무엇이든 원하는 대로 설명해 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그게 말입니다.\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"그 서신에…… 제가 지금 가지고 있을 텐데요.\" 그러면서 그는 가슴 주머니를 더듬었다. \"그래, 여기 있군요. 자, 이 서신에는 몬테크리스토 백작께서 우리 은행에서 무제한으로 신용 거래를 할 수 있다고 적혀 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그게 어디 이해하기 어려운 대목입니까, 남작?\"\n\n\"그저 무제한이라는 말, 그것뿐입니다, 확실히.\"\n\n\"프랑스에서는 그 말을 모릅니까? 아시다시피 그걸 쓴 사람들은 영국계 독일인이라서요.\"\n\n\"오, 서신의 문장이야 흠잡을 데 없습니다. 다만 그 문서가 효력을 지니는지에 대해서는 분명 의문이 듭니다.\"\n\n\"그럴 수가.\" 백작이 더없이 천진하고 솔직한 기색과 어조를 꾸미며 말했다. \"톰슨 앤드 프렌치가 안전하고 지급 능력 있는 은행으로 여겨지지 않는다는 말씀입니까? 어떻게 생각하시는지 말씀해 주십시오, 남작. 솔직히 저도 그쪽에 적잖은 재산을 맡겨 둔 터라 마음이 편치 않으니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"톰슨 앤드 프렌치야 더할 나위 없이 지급 능력이 있지요.\" 당글라르가 거의 비웃는 듯한 미소를 띠며 대답했다. \"다만 무제한이라는 말은, 금융 거래에서는 너무도 모호합니다.\"\n\n\"사실 무제한이지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"바로 제가 하려던 말씀이 그겁니다.\" 당글라르가 외쳤다. \"한데 모호한 것은 미심쩍은 법이지요. 그래서 어느 현명한 사람이 '의심스럽거든 발을 들이지 마라'고 했고요.\"\n\n\"그러니까,\" 몬테크리스토가 받았다. \"톰슨 앤드 프렌치가 아무리 경솔하고 어리석은 짓을 저지를 작정이라 해도, 당글라르 남작께서는 그 본을 따를 마음이 없으시다, 그 말씀이군요.\"\n\n\"그렇지요.\"\n\n\"분명하군요. 톰슨 앤드 프렌치는 자기네 약정에 한계를 두지 않는 반면, 당글라르 씨의 약정에는 한계가 있다. 자기 말마따나 그분은 현명한 사람이다, 이거로군요.\"\n\n\"백작님.\" 은행가가 거만한 기색으로 몸을 곧추세우며 대답했다. \"제 자산의 규모는 여태껏 단 한 번도 의심받은 적이 없습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 제가 그걸 처음으로 의심하는 사람이 되도록 정해진 모양이군요.\" 몬테크리스토가 차갑게 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 권리로요, 백작?\"\n\n\"당신이 제기한 이의와, 당신이 요구한 설명을 권리 삼아서지요. 그런 데에는 분명 무슨 까닭이 있을 테니까요.\"\n\n당글라르는 다시 한 번 입술을 깨물었다. 그가 밀린 것은 이번이 두 번째였고, 이번에는 자기 안방에서였다. 억지로 차린 정중함이 어색하게 들러붙어, 거의 무례함에 가까워질 지경이었다. 반면 몬테크리스토는 우아하고 부드러운 태도를 잃지 않았고, 마음만 먹으면 꾸며 낼 수 있는 어떤 천진함까지 곁들여 우위를 점했다.\n\n\"그럼, 백작님.\" 당글라르가 잠시 침묵하다 다시 입을 열었다. \"제 뜻이 통하도록 해 보겠습니다. 한 가지 여쭙지요. 제게 얼마나 되는 금액을 인출하실 작정이십니까?\"\n\n\"실은 말입니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 자기가 차지한 자리를 한 치도 내주지 않으려 작정하고 대답했다. \"제가 무제한 신용을 바란 까닭이 바로, 제가 돈이 얼마나 필요할지 몰랐기 때문입니다.\"\n\n은행가는 이제 자기가 주도권을 쥘 때가 왔다고 여겼다. 그래서 안락의자에 몸을 기대며, 오만하고 돈푼깨나 있다는 기색으로 말했다.\n\n\"부디 바라시는 바를 말씀하시는 데 망설이지 마십시오. 그러면 아무리 한정되어 있다 해도 당글라르 은행의 자산이 가장 큰 요구에도 능히 응할 수 있다는 걸 아시게 될 겁니다. 설령 백작께서 백만 프랑을 요구하신다 해도……\"\n\n\"실례합니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"백만이라 했습니다.\" 당글라르가 무지에서 비롯된 자신감으로 대답했다.\n\n\"제가 백만으로 뭘 한단 말입니까?\" 백작이 되받았다. \"이보십시오, 그깟 푼돈으로 충분할 정도라면, 애초에 계좌를 트는 수고 따위 하지도 않았겠지요. 백만이라고요? 제가 으레 수첩이나 화장 상자에 넣고 다니는 액수를 두고 말씀하시니, 웃음이 나는 걸 용서하십시오.\"\n\n이렇게 말하며 몬테크리스토는 호주머니에서 명함을 넣어 두는 작은 갑을 꺼내, 각각 50만 프랑짜리 국고 어음 두 장을 뽑아 들었다. 소지자에게 일람불로 지급되는 어음이었다. 당글라르 같은 사내에게는 그보다 온화한 방식의 교정이란 전혀 통하지 않는 법이다. 지금 눈앞에 드러난 사실의 효과는 가히 충격적이었다. 그는 부들부들 떨었고, 거의 졸중을 일으킬 지경이었다. 몬테크리스토를 바라보는 그의 눈동자가 끔찍하게 커졌다.\n\n\"자, 자.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"솔직히 인정하시지요. 당신은 톰슨 앤드 프렌치를 온전히 믿지 못하고 있는 겁니다. 이해합니다. 저는 그럴 수도 있으리라 미리 헤아려, 거래에 어두운 사람이긴 해도 나름의 대비를 해 두었지요. 보십시오, 당신이 직접 받은 것과 똑같은 서신이 여기 두 통 더 있습니다. 하나는 빈의 아른슈타인 앤드 에스켈레스 은행이 로스차일드 남작에게 보낸 것이고, 다른 하나는 런던의 베어링이 라피트 씨 앞으로 발행한 것입니다. 자, 한마디만 하시면 됩니다. 그러면 제가 이 신용장을 이 두 은행 중 한 곳에 제시해, 당신의 모든 근심을 덜어 드리지요.\"\n\n일격이 정통으로 들어맞았고, 당글라르는 완전히 무릎을 꿇었다. 그는 떨리는 손으로 백작에게서 두 통의 편지를 받아 들었다. 백작은 그것을 엄지와 검지 사이에 무심히 끼우고 있었다. 그러고는 서명을 어찌나 꼼꼼히 뜯어보는지, 만약 지금 은행가를 속이는 것이 백작의 목적에 맞지 않았더라면 모욕으로 여겼을 법했다.\n\n\"오, 백작님.\" 손에 든 문서가 진짜임을 확인한 당글라르가 말했다. 그러고는 눈앞의 사내로 구현된 황금의 위력에 경의를 표하듯 자리에서 일어섰다. \"무제한 신용장이 세 통이라니! 더는 의심할 수가 없군요. 하지만 친애하는 백작님, 제가 어느 정도 놀란 것은 부디 너그러이 봐주십시오.\"\n\n\"천만에요.\" 몬테크리스토가 더없이 신사다운 태도로 대답했다. \"이까짓 하찮은 액수로 당신의 은행을 번거롭게 할 일은 아니지요. 자, 그럼 제게 돈을 좀 내주실 수 있겠지요?\"\n\n\"말씀만 하십시오, 친애하는 백작님. 분부대로 따르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼,\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"우리가 서로 이해하게 되었으니 말입니다. 그런 줄로 알겠습니다만?\" 당글라르가 동의하듯 절했다. \"마음속에 도사린 의심이나 미심쩍음이 한 점도 남지 않은 게 확실하지요?\"\n\n\"오, 친애하는 백작님.\" 당글라르가 외쳤다. \"저는 단 한순간도 당신께 그런 감정을 품은 적이 없습니다.\"\n\n\"그래요, 당신은 그저 확인하고 싶었을 뿐이지요, 그뿐입니다. 한데 이제 이렇게 분명히 서로를 이해하게 되었고, 온갖 불신과 의심도 가라앉았으니, 첫해의 예상 지출액을 어림잡아 정해 두는 것도 좋겠군요. 가령 6백만 프랑쯤이라 하면……\"\n\n\"6백만이라고요!\" 당글라르가 숨을 헐떡였다. \"그렇게 하시지요.\"\n\n\"그러다 더 필요하면,\" 몬테크리스토가 무심한 투로 말을 이었다. \"물론 당신께 더 인출하겠지요. 하지만 지금 제 생각으로는 프랑스에 일 년 넘게 머물 작정은 없고, 그동안 방금 말한 액수를 넘기는 일도 거의 없을 듯합니다. 어쨌든 두고 보지요. 그럼 친절을 베푸시어, 내일 제게 50만 프랑을 보내 주십시오. 정오까지는 집에 있을 테고, 혹 없으면 영수증을 집사에게 맡겨 두겠습니다.\"\n\n\"바라시는 돈은 내일 아침 열 시까지 댁으로 보내 드리겠습니다, 친애하는 백작님.\" 당글라르가 대답했다. \"어떻게 받으시겠습니까? 금화로, 은화로, 아니면 지폐로요?\"\n\n\"절반은 금화로, 나머지 절반은 은행권으로 부탁드립니다.\" 백작이 자리에서 일어서며 말했다.\n\n\"솔직히 말씀드리지요, 백작님.\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"저는 여태껏 유럽의 큰 재산이라면 그 규모를 죄다 꿰고 있다고 자부해 왔는데, 백작님 같은 재산은 도무지 들어 본 적이 없습니다. 외람되오나, 그 재산을 가지신 지 오래되셨는지 여쭈어도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"우리 집안에 아주 오랫동안 내려온 것입니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"일정한 햇수 동안은 절대 손대지 못하도록 정해진 일종의 보물이었지요. 그동안 쌓인 이자가 원금을 곱절로 불려 놓았고요. 유언자가 이 재산을 처분하도록 정한 시기가 온 게 바로 얼마 전이라, 저도 최근 몇 해 사이에야 비로소 쓰기 시작했습니다. 그러니 당신이 그걸 모르셨던 것도 무리는 아니지요. 어쨌든 머지않아 저와 제 재산에 대해 더 잘 아시게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n이 마지막 말을 하면서 백작은, 가엾은 프란츠 데피네의 가슴에 늘 공포를 박아 넣곤 하던 그 섬뜩한 미소 가운데 하나를 곁들였다.\n\n\"백작님의 취향과, 그걸 만족시킬 재력이라면,\" 당글라르가 말을 이었다. \"우리 같은 가련한 백만장자 따위는 완전히 그늘에 가려 버릴 만큼 화려함을 뽐내시겠지요. 제가 잘못 본 게 아니라면 백작님은 그림 애호가이신 듯합니다. 적어도 제가 방에 들어왔을 때 제 그림에 쏟으시던 눈길을 보고 그리 짐작했지요. 괜찮으시다면 제 화랑을 기꺼이 보여 드리고 싶군요. 죄다 옛 거장들의 작품으로만 이루어져 있고, 그건 보증할 수 있습니다. 그중에 현대 그림은 한 점도 없습니다. 저는 현대 화풍을 도무지 견딜 수가 없어서요.\"\n\n\"그것들을 못마땅해하시는 건 지극히 옳습니다. 그것들에게는 큰 흠이 하나 있으니까요. 아직 오래될 시간을 갖지 못했다는 흠 말입니다.\"\n\n\"아니면 토르발센과 바르톨리니, 카노바의 빼어난 조각상 몇 점을 보여 드릴까요? 모두 외국 예술가들이지요. 보시다시피 저는 우리 프랑스 조각가들은 영 대수롭잖게 여기거든요.\"\n\n\"그들에게 박하게 구실 권리가 있으시지요, 므슈. 당신 동포들이니까요.\"\n\n\"하지만 이런 건 다 나중에, 우리가 서로를 더 잘 알게 되었을 때 해도 될 일이지요. 지금은, 괜찮으시다면, 당글라르 남작 부인을 소개하는 것으로 그치겠습니다. 제 조급함을 용서하십시오, 친애하는 백작님. 하지만 당신 같은 고객은 거의 한 식구나 다름없으니까요.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 베푸는 영광을 받아들이겠다는 표시로 절했다. 당글라르가 종을 울리자, 화려한 제복 차림의 하인이 응답했다.\n\n\"마님은 댁에 계신가?\" 당글라르가 물었다.\n\n\"네, 나리.\" 하인이 대답했다.\n\n\"혼자 계시고?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 나리. 마님께 손님이 와 계십니다.\"\n\n\"마님과 함께 있는 손님을 만나도 괜찮으시겠습니까, 아니면 끝까지 신분을 숨기고 싶으십니까?\"\n\n\"천만에요.\" 몬테크리스토가 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"제게 그럴 권리가 있다고 우길 생각은 없습니다.\"\n\n\"마님과 함께 계신 분이 누구냐? 드브레 씨인가?\" 당글라르가 물었다. 그 너그럽고 선량한 기색에, 은행가의 집안 사정을 속속들이 아는 몬테크리스토는 빙긋 웃음이 났다.\n\n\"네, 나리.\" 하인이 대답했다. \"드브레 씨가 마님과 함께 계십니다.\"\n\n당글라르가 고개를 끄덕였다. 그러고는 몬테크리스토를 돌아보며 말했다. \"뤼시앵 드브레 씨는 우리의 오랜 벗이자 내무부 장관의 비서관입니다. 제 아내로 말하자면, 솔직히 말해 저와 결혼함으로써 신분을 낮춘 셈이지요. 프랑스에서 가장 유서 깊은 가문 중 하나 출신이거든요. 처녀 적 이름이 드세르비에르였고, 첫 남편은 나르곤 후작인 대령이었습니다.\"\n\n\"당글라르 부인을 알고 지내는 영광은 없습니다만, 뤼시앵 드브레 씨라면 이미 만난 적이 있습니다.\"\n\n\"아, 그래요?\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"어디서 말입니까?\"\n\n\"모르세르 씨 댁에서요.\"\n\n\"아! 그럼 그 젊은 자작과 아는 사이로군요?\"\n\n\"로마에서 카니발 기간에 꽤 자주 어울렸지요.\"\n\n\"그렇지, 그렇지.\" 당글라르가 외쳤다. \"가만, 폐허에 숨어 있던 산적이며 도둑들과 얽힌 무슨 기이한 모험 이야기를, 그리고 그가 기적처럼 빠져나왔다는 이야기를 들은 적이 있지 않던가요? 어떻게였는지는 잊었지만, 그가 이탈리아에서 돌아온 뒤 제 아내와 딸에게 그 이야기를 들려주며 곧잘 즐겁게 해 주곤 했지요.\"\n\n\"마님께서 두 분을 맞이하려고 기다리고 계십니다.\" 안주인의 뜻을 여쭈러 갔던 하인이 말했다.\n\n\"실례지만,\" 당글라르가 절하며 말했다. \"제가 앞장서서 길을 안내하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그러시지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"뒤따르겠습니다.\"\n\n# Chapter 47. The Dappled Grays\n\n남작은 백작을 뒤에 거느리고 여러 개의 방을 차례로 지나갔다. 그 방들을 지배하는 분위기는 묵직한 호화로움과 부를 과시하는 요란함이었다. 마침내 그는 당글라르 부인의 규방에 이르렀다. 작은 팔각형의 방으로, 벽에는 분홍빛 새틴이 드리워지고 그 위에 흰 인도산 모슬린이 덮여 있었다. 의자들은 고풍스러운 솜씨와 재료로 만든 것이었고, 문 위에는 부셰의 화풍을 본떠 그린 목동과 양치기 처녀의 스케치가 걸려 있었으며, 양옆에는 크레용으로 그린 예쁜 원형 장식이 이 매혹적인 방의 가구들과 잘 어우러졌다. 이 큰 저택을 통틀어 독자적인 취향이 살아 있는 곳은 이 방뿐이었다. 사실 이 방은 당글라르 씨와 그의 건축가가 짜고 따른 설계도에서 완전히 빠져 있었다. 그 건축가는 당대 가장 유행을 타고 가장 이름난 실내장식가라는 이유 하나만으로 남작의 대대적인 개조 작업을 돕도록 뽑힌 사람이었다. 그래서 규방의 장식은 전적으로 당글라르 부인과 뤼시앵 드브레의 손에 맡겨졌다. 당글라르 씨는 총재정부 시대에 이해되던 식의 고가구를 무척 흠모했으나, 아내가 가장 아끼는 이 거실의 소박한 우아함에는 더없는 경멸을 품고 있었다. 덧붙이자면 그는 이 방에 들어오는 것을 결코 허락받지 못했고, 다만 자신보다 더 반가운 손님을 안내해 들일 때만 자신의 출현이 양해되었다. 그런 때에도 그는 다른 사람을 소개하는 사람이라기보다 자신이 소개받는 사람 같은 분위기와 태도를 풍겼으며, 그가 데려온 사람이 남작 부인의 마음에 드느냐 들지 않느냐에 따라 그 환대는 따뜻하기도 하고 싸늘하기도 했다.\n\n당글라르 부인은 (비록 젊음의 첫 만개는 지났으나 여전히 눈에 띄게 아름다웠다) 지금 피아노 앞에 앉아 있었다. 무척 정교한 세공과 상감 솜씨가 들어간 악기였다. 한편 뤼시앵 드브레는 작은 작업대 앞에 서서 화첩의 책장을 넘기고 있었다.\n\n뤼시앵은 백작이 도착하기 전에 그에 관한 많은 사정을 당글라르 부인에게 이야기해 둘 시간이 있었다. 알베르 드 모르세르가 베푼 아침 식사 자리에 모인 모든 사람의 마음에 몬테크리스토가 강렬한 인상을 남겼다는 점을 기억할 것이다. 드브레는 그런 감정에 좀처럼 휘둘리는 사람이 아니었는데도, 백작의 인상적인 눈빛과 태도가 그의 마음에 불러일으킨 강한 영향을 끝내 떨쳐 내지 못했다. 그러니 뤼시앵이 남작 부인에게 들려준 묘사가 그 자신의 달아오른 상상력으로 한껏 윤색되어 있었던 것도 당연했다. 모르세르가 들려준 백작에 관한 놀라운 이야기들로 이미 들떠 있던 당글라르 부인이 드브레가 덧붙여 늘어놓는 온갖 사정에 열심히 귀 기울이고 또 그것을 고스란히 믿어 버린 것도 이상한 일이 아니었다. 피아노 앞에서 자세를 잡고 화첩을 들여다보던 것은 만일을 위한 작은 술책에 지나지 않았다. 당글라르 씨는 더없이 정중한 환영과 평소답지 않은 미소를 받았고, 백작은 신사다운 인사에 대한 답례로 격식을 갖춘 우아한 목례를 받았다. 한편 뤼시앵은 백작과 어딘가 서먹한 알은체를 주고받았고, 당글라르와는 허물없고 가벼운 끄덕임을 나누었다.\n\n\"부인,\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"몬테크리스토 백작을 소개해 드리겠습니다. 로마에 있는 제 거래처에서 더없이 따뜻하게 추천해 준 분이지요. 파리의 모든 부인이 그분의 눈길을 끌려고 다툴 만한 사실 하나만 말씀드리겠습니다. 그분은 일 년 동안 파리에 머무를 작정으로 오셨는데, 그 짧은 동안에 무려 육백만 프랑을 쓰실 계획이라는 겁니다. 그건 곧 끝없는 무도회와 만찬, 정원 잔치를 뜻하지요. 그 모든 자리에서 백작께서 저희를 잊지 않으시길 바랍니다. 물론 저희도 변변치 않은 자리에나마 백작을 꼭 모실 테니까요.\"\n\n이 노골적인 아첨과 거친 말투에도 불구하고, 당글라르 부인은 열두 달에 육백만 프랑을 쓸 수 있는 데다 그 호사스러운 낭비의 무대로 파리를 골랐다는 남자를 적잖은 흥미로 바라보지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"언제 이곳에 도착하셨나요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"어제 아침입니다, 부인.\"\n\n\"여느 때처럼 지구 끝에서 오셨겠지요? 실례합니다만, 적어도 그게 백작님의 습관이라고 들었거든요.\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 부인. 이번에는 그저 카디스에서 왔을 뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"첫 방문으로는 더없이 나쁜 때를 고르셨군요. 파리는 여름이면 끔찍한 곳이랍니다. 무도회도 모임도 축제도 다 끝났고, 이탈리아 오페라단은 런던에 가 있고, 프랑스 오페라단은 파리만 빼고 어디에나 있지요. 프랑세 극장으로 말하자면, 백작님도 물론 아시겠지만 있으나 마나 한 형편이고요. 저희에게 남은 유일한 즐길 거리라고는 샹드마르스와 사토리에서 열리는 시들한 경마뿐입니다. 백작님께서는 그 경마 중 어느 한쪽에라도 말을 출전시키실 생각이 있으신가요?\"\n\n\"파리에서 사람들이 하는 일이라면 저도 무엇이든 하겠습니다, 부인. 여기서 유행하는 오락의 관념을 제게 일러 줄 사람을 다행히 만나기만 한다면 말이지요.\"\n\n\"백작님은 말을 좋아하시나요?\"\n\n\"제 삶의 상당 부분을 동방에서 보냈습니다, 부인. 그리고 동방 사람들이 단 두 가지만을 귀하게 여긴다는 것은 부인께서도 분명 아실 겁니다. 좋은 혈통의 말과 여인의 아름다움이지요.\"\n\n\"어머, 백작님,\" 남작 부인이 말했다. \"여인을 먼저 두셨더라면 좀 더 기사다웠을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"보십시오, 부인. 이곳에서의 제 모든 말과 행동을 이끌어 줄 선생이 필요하다고 한 제 말이 얼마나 옳았는지 말입니다.\"\n\n이때 당글라르 부인이 총애하는 시녀가 규방으로 들어왔다. 시녀는 안주인에게 다가가 낮은 목소리로 몇 마디를 속삭였다. 당글라르 부인의 얼굴이 새파랗게 질리더니, 이내 외쳤다.\n\n\"믿을 수 없구나. 그런 일이 있을 리 없어.\"\n\n\"정말입니다, 마님.\" 시녀가 대답했다. \"제가 말씀드린 그대로입니다.\"\n\n당글라르 부인은 조바심을 내며 남편 쪽으로 돌아서더니 다그쳤다. \"이게 사실인가요?\"\n\n\"무엇이 사실이냐는 말이오, 부인?\" 당글라르가 눈에 띄게 동요하며 물었다.\n\n\"제 하녀가 방금 한 말이요.\"\n\n\"그런데 하녀가 무슨 말을 했소?\"\n\n\"마부가 제 마차에 말을 매려고 했더니, 자기도 모르는 새 말들이 마구간에서 사라졌더라는군요. 이게 대체 무슨 영문인지 알고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"부인, 부디 내 말을 좀 들어 주시오.\" 당글라르가 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그럼요. 듣고말고요. 무슨 변명을 늘어놓으실지 무척이나 궁금하니까요. 이 두 분이 우리 사이를 판가름해 주실 거예요. 다만 먼저 제가 두 분께 사정을 말씀드리지요. 여러분,\" 남작 부인이 말을 이었다. \"당글라르 남작의 마구간에 있는 열 마리 말 가운데 두 마리는 오로지 제 것이랍니다. 파리에서 찾아볼 수 있는 가장 아름답고 가장 기운 넘치는 한 쌍이지요. 하지만 적어도 드브레 씨께는 더 설명을 드릴 필요가 없겠네요. 제 아름다운 얼룩 회색 말 한 쌍을 잘 아실 테니까요. 그런데 제가 빌포르 부인께 내일 불로뉴 숲으로 나들이를 가시도록 제 마차를 빌려 드리기로 약속했답니다. 그런데 마부가 마구간으로 그 회색 말들을 데리러 가니, 글쎄 사라지고 없더라는 거예요. 감쪽같이 사라진 거지요. 당글라르 씨가 보잘것없는 몇천 프랑을 벌겠다는 이기적인 속셈으로 그 말들을 팔아 치운 게 틀림없어요. 오, 정말 가증스러운 무리들이라니까요. 돈에 환장한 이 투기꾼들 말이에요!\"\n\n\"부인,\" 당글라르가 대답했다. \"그 말들은 당신이 타기에는 그리 얌전하지가 못했소. 겨우 네 살배기라, 당신을 생각하면 나는 마음이 몹시 불안했단 말이오.\"\n\n\"말도 안 되는 소리예요.\" 남작 부인이 쏘아붙였다. \"그 일로 불안해하셨을 리가 없잖아요. 제가 한 달 전부터 파리 최고의 마부를 부리고 있다는 걸 누구보다 잘 아시면서요. 아니면 혹시 말들과 함께 그 마부까지 처분하셨나요?\"\n\n\"여보, 제발 그 말들 얘기는 그만합시다. 겉모습이 똑같으면서도 더 얌전하고 차분한 놈들로 한 쌍 구해 주리다.\"\n\n남작 부인은 이루 말할 수 없는 경멸의 기색으로 어깨를 으쓱했다. 그러는 동안 남편은 이 부부답지 않은 몸짓을 못 본 척하며 몬테크리스토 쪽으로 돌아서서 말했다. \"정말이지 백작님, 좀 더 일찍 뵙지 못한 게 무척이나 아쉽군요. 백작님께서는 물론 살림을 꾸리고 계시겠지요?\"\n\n\"네, 그렇습니다.\" 백작이 대답했다.\n\n\"그 말들을 백작님께 권해 드릴 수 있었을 텐데요. 이미 거저 주다시피 한 셈이지만요. 그래도 앞서 말씀드렸듯이, 저는 어떤 조건으로든 그놈들을 처분하고 싶었습니다. 젊은이에게나 어울리는 말들이었거든요.\"\n\n\"제게 보여 주신 호의에 깊이 감사드립니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"하지만 오늘 아침에 아주 훌륭한 마차용 말 한 쌍을 샀습니다. 그리 비싸지도 않았던 것 같고요. 저기 있군요. 자, 드브레 씨, 듣자 하니 안목이 높으시다던데, 저 말들에 대한 의견을 좀 들려주시지요.\"\n\n드브레가 창가로 걸어가는 동안, 당글라르가 아내에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"다른 사람들 앞에서는 말할 수 없었지만,\" 그가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"내가 그 말들을 처분한 이유가 있소. 오늘 아침에 누가 엄청난 값을 부르더란 말이오. 제 몸 망치기를 어떻게든 서두르려 드는 어떤 미치광이인지 멍청이인지가, 실제로 제 집사를 보내 값이 얼마든 좋으니 그 말들을 사 오라고 한 거요. 그래서 사실 나는 그 거래로 만 육천 프랑을 벌었소. 자, 그렇게 화난 얼굴 좀 풀어요. 그 돈에서 사천 프랑을 줄 테니 당신 마음대로 쓰고, 외제니에게는 이천 프랑을 줍시다. 자, 이제 어떻소? 내가 그 말들을 처분한 게 옳지 않았소?\"\n\n당글라르 부인은 몸서리쳐지는 경멸의 눈길로 남편을 훑어보았다.\n\n\"맙소사?\" 드브레가 갑자기 외쳤다.\n\n\"무슨 일이에요?\" 남작 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"제가 잘못 봤을 리 없습니다. 부인의 말들입니다! 우리가 방금 이야기하던 바로 그 말들이, 백작님의 마차에 매여 있어요!\"\n\n\"제 얼룩 회색 말들이라고요?\" 남작 부인이 창가로 달려가며 다그쳤다. \"정말 그 애들이네요!\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n당글라르는 완전히 얼이 빠진 얼굴이었다.\n\n\"이거 참 묘한 일이군요.\" 몬테크리스토가 그럴듯하게 꾸민 놀라움으로 외쳤다.\n\n\"믿을 수가 없군.\" 은행가가 중얼거렸다. 당글라르 부인이 드브레의 귀에 몇 마디를 속삭였고, 드브레가 몬테크리스토에게 다가가 말했다. \"부인께서 백작님이 저 말들을 자기 남편에게서 얼마에 사셨는지 알고 싶어 하십니다.\"\n\n\"글쎄, 잘 모르겠습니다.\" 백작이 대답했다. \"제 집사가 저를 깜짝 놀라게 해 주려고 마련한 일이라서요. 든 값은, 글쎄요, 삼만 프랑쯤 됐을 겁니다.\"\n\n드브레가 백작의 대답을 남작 부인에게 전했다. 가엾은 당글라르는 어찌나 풀이 죽고 낭패한 얼굴인지, 몬테크리스토는 그에게 측은해하는 기색을 지어 보였다.\n\n\"보십시오.\" 백작이 말했다. \"여인이란 얼마나 은혜를 모르는지요. 그 말들을 처분해 남작 부인의 안전을 지켜 드리려 한 당신의 친절한 배려가 부인께는 조금도 와닿지 않은 모양입니다. 하지만 으레 그런 법이지요. 여자란 흔히 한갓 고집 때문에 안전한 것보다 위험한 것을 더 좋아하니까요. 그러니 제 생각에는, 친애하는 남작님, 가장 좋고 가장 손쉬운 길은 그저 여자들의 변덕에 맡겨 두고 제멋대로 하게 내버려 두는 겁니다. 그러다가 무슨 탈이 나면, 적어도 탓할 사람이 자기 자신밖에 없을 테니까요.\"\n\n당글라르는 아무런 대꾸도 하지 않았다. 그는 자신과 남작 부인 사이에 곧 벌어질 장면을 머릿속으로 그려 보느라 정신이 없었다. 잔뜩 찌푸린 부인의 이마는 올림포스의 제우스처럼 폭풍을 예고하고 있었다. 먹구름이 몰려오는 것을 알아챈 드브레는 당글라르 부인의 분노가 터지는 광경을 지켜볼 마음이 없어, 문득 약속이 있는 것을 떠올리고는 자리를 떴다. 한편 몬테크리스토는 머무름을 길게 끌어 얻고자 하는 이점을 그르치고 싶지 않아, 작별 인사로 목례를 하고 떠났다. 당글라르는 아내의 성난 질책을 고스란히 견뎌야 할 처지로 남겨졌다.\n\n\"훌륭하군.\" 몬테크리스토는 그곳을 나오며 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다. \"모든 일이 내 바람대로 흘러갔어. 이제 이 집안의 가정 평화는 내 손안에 있는 셈이지. 자, 그럼 이번에는 또 다른 묘수를 써서 남편과 아내 양쪽의 마음을 다 사 보자고. 정말 즐거운 일이야! 그래도,\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"이 와중에 아직 외제니 당글라르 양에게는 소개를 받지 못했군. 기꺼이 알고 지내고 싶은 사람인데. 하지만,\" 그는 특유의 미소를 지으며 말을 이었다. \"나는 지금 파리에 있고, 시간은 넉넉하니까. 그 일이야 차차 하면 되지.\"\n\n이런 생각을 하며 그는 마차에 올라 집으로 돌아갔다. 두 시간 뒤, 당글라르 부인은 백작에게서 더없이 찬사가 넘치는 편지 한 통을 받았다. 편지에서 백작은 그녀가 가장 아끼는 \"얼룩 회색 말들\"을 도로 받아 달라고 간청하며, 그 멋진 마차가 한 사랑스러운 여인의 아쉬움을 대가로 얻어진 것임을 알면서 파리 사교계에 발을 들인다는 생각은 도저히 견딜 수 없노라고 호소했다. 말들은 그녀가 아침에 보았던 것과 똑같은 마구를 차고 돌려보내졌다. 다만 백작의 명령으로, 말들의 머리 양쪽을 장식한 매듭 장미 한가운데마다 커다란 다이아몬드 하나씩이 박혀 있었다.\n\n몬테크리스토는 당글라르에게도 편지를 써서, 변덕스러운 백만장자의 별난 선물을 너그러이 받아 주기를 청하고, 말들을 돌려보내며 취한 동방식 방식을 남작 부인께서 용서해 주시도록 부탁드린다고 했다.\n\n저녁 무렵, 몬테크리스토는 알리를 데리고 파리를 떠나 오퇴유로 향했다. 이튿날 세 시쯤, 징을 한 번 울리는 소리가 알리를 백작 앞으로 불러들였다.\n\n\"알리,\" 누비아인이 방으로 들어서자 주인이 말을 꺼냈다. \"너는 올가미를 던지는 데 남달리 능숙하다고 내게 여러 번 말한 적이 있지, 그렇지 않으냐?\"\n\n알리는 자랑스럽게 몸을 곧추세우더니, 그렇다는 표시를 해 보였다.\n\n\"내가 잘못 안 게 아니라고 생각했다. 그 올가미로 황소를 멈춰 세울 수 있겠느냐?\"\n\n알리는 다시 한번 그렇다는 몸짓을 되풀이했다.\n\n\"호랑이는 어떠냐?\"\n\n알리는 고개를 숙여 그렇다는 뜻을 보였다.\n\n\"사자까지도?\"\n\n알리는 앞으로 뛰쳐나가더니, 올가미를 던지는 동작에 이어 목 졸린 사자의 시늉을 해 보였다.\n\n\"알겠다.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"사자를 사냥한 적이 있다는 말이로구나?\"\n\n알리는 사자를 여러 마리 뒤쫓아 사로잡아 보았노라는 뜻을 비치며, 의기양양한 자부심으로 미소 지었다.\n\n\"하지만 미친 듯이 날뛰며 앞으로 내달리는 말 두 마리를 멈춰 세울 수 있겠느냐?\"\n\n누비아인이 빙긋 웃었다.\n\n\"좋다.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그럼 내 말을 잘 들어라. 머지않아 마차 한 대가 이 앞을 쏜살같이 지나갈 것이다. 어제 나와 함께 있던 그 얼룩 회색 말 한 쌍이 끄는 마차다. 자, 네 목숨을 걸고서라도 그 말들을 내 집 문 앞에서 멈춰 세워야 한다.\"\n\n알리는 거리로 내려가, 집 입구 바로 앞 포장도로에 곧게 선 하나를 그었다. 그러고는 자기를 지켜보던 백작에게 그어 놓은 선을 가리켜 보였다. 백작은 알리의 어깨를 가볍게 두드렸다. 알리를 칭찬하는 그의 늘 하는 방식이었다. 맡은 임무가 흡족하고 기뻤던 알리는 거리와 집의 모서리를 이루는 튀어나온 돌 쪽으로 차분히 걸어가, 그 위에 앉아 물담뱃대를 피우기 시작했다. 한편 몬테크리스토는 자기 계획의 성공을 굳게 확신하며 집 안으로 다시 들어갔다.\n\n그래도 다섯 시가 가까워지고, 마차가 곧 들이닥치리라 백작이 시시각각 기다리는 동안, 그의 태도에서는 예사롭지 않은 조바심과 불안의 기색이 엿보였다. 그는 거리가 내려다보이는 방에 자리를 잡고, 잠시도 가만있지 못하는 발걸음으로 방 안을 서성였다. 이따금 다가오는 바퀴 소리에 귀를 기울이느라 멈춰 섰다가, 다시 알리에게 초조한 눈길을 던졌다. 그러나 누비아인이 한결같은 박자로 물담뱃대 연기를 뿜어 대는 것으로 보아, 적어도 그만은 가장 좋아하는 일에 흠뻑 빠져 있는 게 분명했다.\n\n문득 멀리서 바퀴가 빠르게 굴러오는 소리가 들리더니, 거의 곧바로 마차 한 대가 나타났다. 사납고 걷잡을 수 없는 말 한 쌍이 끄는 마차였다. 겁에 질린 마부는 그 미친 듯한 속도를 억누르려 애썼으나 헛일이었다.\n\n마차 안에는 젊은 여인과 일고여덟 살쯤 된 아이가 서로를 끌어안고 있었다. 공포가 그들에게서 비명을 지를 힘마저 앗아 간 듯했다. 마차는 우툴두툴한 돌길 위를 내달리며 삐걱대고 덜컹거렸고, 바퀴 밑에 자그마한 장애물 하나만 걸려도 큰일이 날 판이었다. 그러나 마차는 길 한복판을 따라 계속 달려갔고, 그것이 지나가는 것을 본 사람들은 공포에 찬 비명을 질렀다.\n\n알리는 별안간 물담뱃대를 내던지고 주머니에서 올가미를 꺼내, 가까운 쪽 말의 앞다리를 세 겹으로 옭아맬 만큼 능숙하게 던졌다. 그러고는 충격의 거센 힘에 몇 걸음 끌려가도록 몸을 내맡겼다. 그러자 그 말이 끌채 위로 고꾸라지면서 끌채가 부러졌고, 그 바람에 다른 말도 더는 내달리지 못하게 되었다. 마부는 이 틈을 반갑게 이용해 마부석에서 뛰어내렸다. 그러나 알리는 이미 두 번째 말의 콧구멍을 잽싸게 움켜쥐고 쇠집게 같은 손아귀에 단단히 붙들고 있었고, 마침내 그 짐승은 고통으로 콧김을 내뿜으며 동료 곁에 주저앉고 말았다.\n\n이 모든 일은 이야기로 옮기는 데 걸리는 시간보다 훨씬 짧은 동안에 이루어졌다. 그러나 그 짧은 틈은, 사고가 일어난 집에서 한 남자가 하인 여럿을 거느리고 뛰쳐나오기에 충분했다. 마부가 마차 문을 열자, 그는 한 손으로 방석을 발작적으로 움켜쥐고 다른 손으로는 정신을 잃은 어린 사내아이를 가슴에 끌어안은 부인을 마차에서 안아 내렸다. 몬테크리스토는 두 사람을 살롱으로 안아 들고 가, 소파에 뉘었다.\n\n\"마음을 가라앉히십시오, 부인.\" 그가 말했다. \"위험은 다 지나갔습니다.\" 그 말에 부인이 고개를 들었다. 그러고는 어떤 간청보다 훨씬 더 절절한 눈길로, 여전히 의식이 없는 자기 아이를 가리켰다. \"부인의 두려움이 어떤 것인지 잘 압니다.\" 백작이 아이를 조심스레 살피며 말했다. \"하지만 불안해하실 까닭이 조금도 없다고 장담합니다. 이 아이는 어디 하나 다친 데가 없습니다. 정신을 잃은 것은 그저 공포 때문이니, 곧 깨어날 겁니다.\"\n\n\"제 두려움을 달래시려고 하시는 말씀은 아니신가요? 아이가 어찌나 새파랗게 질렸는지 보세요! 내 아가, 사랑하는 에두아르, 엄마한테 말 좀 해 보렴. 그 예쁜 눈 좀 뜨고 한 번만 더 엄마를 봐 다오! 오, 선생님, 제발 의사를 좀 불러 주세요. 제 아이만 살릴 수 있다면 제 전 재산도 아깝지 않아요.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 차분한 미소와 부드러운 손짓으로, 정신없이 애태우는 어머니에게 걱정을 내려놓으라는 뜻을 비쳤다. 그러고는 곁에 놓인 작은 함을 열어, 금으로 상감 세공을 한 보헤미아 유리병 하나를 꺼냈다. 피처럼 붉은 빛깔의 액체가 담긴 병이었다. 그는 그 액체를 아이의 입술에 딱 한 방울 떨어뜨렸다. 그것이 입술에 닿기가 무섭게, 아이는 여전히 대리석처럼 창백한 채로 눈을 뜨더니 주위를 두리번거렸다. 그 모습에 어머니의 기쁨은 거의 광란에 가까울 지경이었다.\n\n\"제가 어디 있는 거죠?\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"그리고 그토록 끔찍했던 두려움이 이렇게 다행스럽게 끝난 것은 누구 덕분인가요?\"\n\n\"부인,\" 백작이 대답했다. \"부인을 더 큰 고통에서 구해 드릴 수 있었던 것을 더없는 행운으로 여기는 사람의 지붕 아래 계십니다.\"\n\n\"이 모든 일이 다 제 못된 호기심 탓이에요.\" 부인이 말을 이었다. \"온 파리가 당글라르 부인의 아름다운 말들을 입에 침이 마르도록 칭찬하기에, 그 말들이 정말로 그런 극찬을 받을 만한지 알고 싶다는 어리석은 생각을 했던 거지요.\"\n\n\"그게 가능한 일입니까?\" 백작이 그럴듯하게 꾸민 놀라움으로 외쳤다. \"그 말들이 남작 부인의 것이라니요?\"\n\n\"정말 그렇답니다. 그런데 혹시 당글라르 부인을 아시는지 여쭤봐도 될까요?\"\n\n\"그분을 아는 영광을 누리고 있습니다. 부인을 위협한 위험에서 부인이 벗어나신 데 대한 제 기쁨은, 부인이 겪으신 그 모든 위험의 본의 아닌, 또 의도치 않은 원인이 바로 저였다는 것을 깨닫고 나니 곱절이 됩니다. 어제 제가 남작에게서 그 말들을 샀는데, 남작 부인이 말들과 헤어지기를 못내 아쉬워하시는 게 분명하기에, 제 손에서 그것들을 받아 주십사 청하며 도로 보내 드렸던 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 분명 에르민이 제게 그토록 많은 이야기를 해 준 그 몬테크리스토 백작님이시군요?\"\n\n\"바로 맞히셨습니다, 부인.\" 백작이 대답했다.\n\n\"저는 엘로이즈 드 빌포르랍니다.\"\n\n백작은 처음 듣는 이름이라는 듯한 태도로 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"빌포르 씨께서 백작님의 그 모든 친절에 얼마나 감사해하실까요. 자신의 아내와 아이가 살아 있는 것이 오직 백작님 덕분임을 얼마나 고마운 마음으로 인정하실까요! 백작님의 용감한 하인이 그토록 재빨리 도와주지 않았더라면, 이 가엾은 아이와 저는 분명 둘 다 목숨을 잃었을 거예요.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 부인이 처하셨던 그 무시무시한 위험을 생각하면 저도 여태 몸서리가 쳐집니다.\"\n\n\"백작님의 하인이 보여 준 그 헌신에 마땅한 보답을 할 수 있도록 허락해 주시기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"간청드립니다, 부인.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"지나친 칭찬이나 보상으로 알리를 버릇 들이지 말아 주십시오. 사소한 일을 할 때마다 보답을 바라는 버릇을 들이게 둘 수는 없으니까요. 알리는 제 노예이고, 부인의 목숨을 구한 것은 제게 대한 자기 의무를 다한 것에 지나지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"아니에요,\" 백작이 취한 위엄 어린 말투에 깊은 인상을 받은 빌포르 부인이 끼어들었다. \"아니에요, 그래도 제 목숨을 구하려고 자기 목숨을 걸었다는 걸 생각해 보세요.\"\n\n\"그의 목숨은 그의 것이 아닙니다, 부인. 제가 그를 죽음에서 구해 준 대가로 받은 제 것이지요.\"\n\n빌포르 부인은 더는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다. 그녀의 마음은 처음 본 순간부터 그토록 강렬한 인상을 안겨 준 그 사람을 가만히 바라보는 데 온통 빠져 있었다.\n\n빌포르 부인이 이렇게 넋을 놓고 있는 동안, 몬테크리스토는 그녀가 두 팔로 끌어안고 더없이 다정한 애무를 퍼붓는 아이의 이목구비와 생김새를 찬찬히 뜯어보았다. 아이는 나이에 비해 몸집이 작고, 부자연스러울 만큼 창백했다. 빗질이나 곱슬로 다듬으려는 온갖 시도를 거부하는 뻣뻣한 검은 머리카락이 툭 튀어나온 이마 위로 흘러내려 어깨까지 닿았고, 그 때문에 이미 짓궂은 장난을 좋아하는 어린아이다운 기운과 온갖 금지된 즐거움을 탐하는 빛으로 반짝이던 두 눈이 한층 더 생기를 띠었다. 입은 컸고, 아직 핏기가 돌아오지 않은 입술은 유난히 얇았다. 사실 그 아이의 얼굴을 지배하는 깊고 교활한 표정은, 그렇게 어린아이라기보다 차라리 열두어 살이나 열네 살쯤 된 소년에게나 어울릴 법한 것이었다. 아이가 맨 처음 한 짓은 자기를 감싼 어머니의 두 팔을 사납게 밀쳐 내고 빠져나가, 백작이 영약이 든 병을 꺼냈던 그 함으로 달려가는 것이었다. 그러고는 누구의 허락도 구하지 않고, 변덕이든 어리광이든 도무지 억제할 줄 모르는 응석받이 아이 특유의 고집으로, 모든 병의 마개를 죄다 뽑아내기 시작했다.\n\n\"아무것도 만지지 마라, 꼬마 친구야.\" 백작이 다급히 외쳤다. \"그 액체들 중에는 맛보는 것은 물론이고 들이마시기만 해도 위험한 것이 있단다.\"\n\n빌포르 부인이 새파랗게 질려 아들의 팔을 붙잡고 안타까이 제 쪽으로 끌어당겼다. 그러나 아이가 무사한 것을 확인하고 나자, 그녀는 그 함에도 짧지만 의미심장한 눈길을 던졌고, 그것은 백작의 눈을 비껴가지 못했다. 이때 알리가 들어왔다. 그를 보자 빌포르 부인은 반가운 소리를 내며 아이를 한층 더 가까이 끌어안고 말했다.\n\n\"에두아르, 우리 아가, 저 착한 분이 보이니? 저분은 아주 큰 용기와 결단을 보여 주셨단다. 우리를 태우고 달아나던 말들을, 분명 마차를 산산조각 냈을 그 말들을 멈춰 세우려고 자기 목숨까지 거셨거든. 그러니 저분께 감사드리렴, 우리 아가, 네가 할 수 있는 가장 예쁜 말로 말이야. 저분이 우리를 도우러 와 주시지 않았다면, 너도 나도 이렇게 살아서 감사 인사를 드리지 못했을 테니까.\"\n\n아이는 입을 삐죽 내밀고 멸시하듯 고개를 돌리며 말했다. \"너무 못생겼는걸요.\"\n\n백작은 그 아이가 자신의 기대를 그대로 이루어 줄 것 같다는 듯 미소 지었다. 한편 빌포르 부인은 무슨 잘못을 저질렀다는 생각이라고는 조금도 들지 않을 만큼 부드럽고 누그러진 태도로 아들을 나무랐다.\n\n\"이 부인께서는,\" 백작이 아랍어로 알리에게 말했다. \"아들이 너에게 두 사람의 목숨을 구해 준 데 대해 감사하기를 바라신다. 그런데 아이는 네가 너무 못생겼다며 거절하는구나.\"\n\n알리는 영민한 얼굴을 아이 쪽으로 돌려, 겉으로는 아무런 감정도 없이 아이를 바라보았다. 그러나 콧구멍이 경련하듯 실룩이는 것을 보고, 몬테크리스토의 노련한 눈은 그 아랍인이 가슴 깊이 상처받았음을 알아챘다.\n\n\"여쭤봐도 될까요?\" 빌포르 부인이 작별을 고하려 일어서며 말했다. \"백작님은 보통 이곳에 머무시나요?\"\n\n\"아닙니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"이건 제가 아주 최근에 사들인 작은 곳입니다. 제가 사는 곳은 샹젤리제 거리 30번지지요. 그런데 부인께서는 놀라셨던 것에서 완전히 회복하신 모양이고, 분명 댁으로 돌아가고 싶으시겠지요. 부인의 뜻을 미리 헤아려, 부인이 타고 오셨던 그 말들을 제 마차 한 대에 매어 두라고 일러 두었습니다. 그리고 부인께서 그토록 못생겼다고 여기시는 알리가,\" 그는 미소 띤 얼굴로 아이에게 말을 건네며 말을 이었다. \"부인을 댁까지 모셔다 드리는 영광을 누릴 겁니다. 부인의 마부는 여기 남아 마차의 필요한 수리를 맡고요. 그 중요한 일이 끝나는 대로, 제 말 한 쌍을 매어 그 마차를 곧장 당글라르 부인께 보내 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그 끔찍한 말들을 타고 돌아갈 엄두가 나지 않아요.\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"보시면 아실 겁니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"알리의 손에 맡겨지면 그 말들이 더없이 딴판이 된다는 것을요. 그의 손에서는 양처럼 순하고 고분고분해질 테니까요.\"\n\n과연 알리는 그것을 증명해 보였다. 그는 가까스로 일으켜 세운 말들에게 다가가, 향긋한 식초를 적신 해면으로 말들의 이마와 콧구멍을 문지르고, 입을 뒤덮은 땀과 거품을 닦아 냈다. 그런 다음 큰 소리로 휘파람을 불기 시작하면서, 몇 분 동안 말들의 온몸을 구석구석 잘 문질러 주었다. 그러고는 부서진 마차 둘레에 모여든 소란스러운 군중에도 아랑곳없이, 알리는 잠잠해진 말들을 백작의 마차에 차분히 매고 고삐를 손에 쥔 채 마부석에 올랐다. 그러자 바로 그 말들의 걷잡을 수 없는 사나움과 미친 듯한 질주를 목격했던 사람들이 깜짝 놀라게도, 그는 채찍을 자못 매섭게 휘두른 뒤에야 비로소 말들을 움직이게 할 수 있었다. 게다가 그러고도 이제 둔하고 굼뜨고 멍청한 두 마리 짐승으로 변해 버린 그 이름난 \"얼룩 회색 말들\"에게서 끌어낼 수 있었던 것이라고는, 어찌나 힘겹게 유지되는지 느릿느릿 굼뜬 걸음뿐이었다. 그 탓에 빌포르 부인은 생토노레 거리의 자기 집으로 돌아가는 데 두 시간이 넘게 걸렸다.\n\n기적 같은 탈출에 대한 첫 축하 인사가 채 끝나기도 전에, 그녀는 당글라르 부인에게 다음과 같은 편지를 썼다.\n\n\"친애하는 에르민, 저는 방금 더없이 급박한 위험에서 놀랍게 벗어났답니다. 그리고 제 무사함은 바로 어제 우리가 이야기하던 그 몬테크리스토 백작님 덕분이지요. 오늘 그분을 뵙게 되리라고는 전혀 생각지 못했는데 말이에요. 제가 당신의 그 칭찬을 과장된 미사여구라 여기고 얼마나 매정하게 비웃었는지 기억나요. 하지만 이제 저는 그 놀라운 분에 대한 당신의 열렬한 묘사가 그분의 진가에 한참 못 미쳤음을 인정할 충분한 이유가 생겼답니다. 당신의 말들이 라넬라까지 갔을 때, 미친 것들처럼 앞으로 내달리더니 어찌나 무시무시한 속도로 질주하던지, 저와 가엾은 에두아르에게는 앞을 가로막는 첫 번째 물체에 부딪혀 산산조각 나는 것 말고는 다른 앞날이 없어 보였어요. 바로 그때 이상하게 생긴 한 남자가, 아랍인인지 흑인인지 누비아인인지, 적어도 어느 나라 사람이든 검은 피부의 사내가, 자기 주인인 백작의 신호 한 번에 자칫 짓밟혀 죽을 위험까지 무릅쓰고 별안간 그 광란하는 짐승들을 붙들어 멈춰 세웠답니다. 그 사내야말로 정말 기적적으로 목숨을 건진 게 틀림없어요. 그러자 백작님이 우리에게 황급히 달려와 우리를 집 안으로 데려가셨고, 거기서 가엾은 에두아르를 금세 되살려 내셨지요. 그분은 자신의 마차로 우리를 집까지 보내 주셨어요. 당신의 마차는 내일 돌아갈 거예요. 당신의 말들은 이 사고의 여파로 상태가 말이 아닐 거예요. 사람에게 정복당한 게 분하고 토라진 듯, 완전히 얼이 빠진 모양이더군요. 그래도 백작님이 제게 단단히 일러 주시기를, 이삼일만 쉬게 하면서 그동안 보리만 잔뜩 먹이면 어제처럼 멋진, 그러니까 어제처럼 무시무시한 상태로 돌아갈 거라고 하셨답니다.\n\n그럼 이만! 어제의 나들이에 대해서는 그다지 감사를 전할 수가 없네요. 하지만 따지고 보면, 당신 말들의 못된 짓을 당신 탓으로 돌릴 수는 없겠지요. 더구나 그 덕분에 몬테크리스토 백작님을 소개받는 기쁨까지 얻었으니까요. 그리고 정말이지 그 명망 높은 분은, 그토록 처분하고 싶어 안달이라는 그 수백만 프랑을 차치하고라도, 제가 어떤 위험을 무릅쓰고서라도 풀어 보기를 즐기는 그 묘하게 흥미로운 수수께끼 같은 분들 중 하나로 보였어요. 설령 당신 말들 뒤에 매여 불로뉴 숲으로 또 한 번 나들이를 가야 한다 해도 말이지요.\n\n에두아르는 그 사고를 기적 같은 용기로 견뎌 냈답니다. 비명 한 번 지르지 않고 정신을 잃은 채 제 품으로 쓰러졌고, 일이 다 끝난 뒤에도 눈물 한 방울 흘리지 않았어요. 당신은 이게 다 눈먼 모정에서 나온 칭찬이라 여기시겠지만, 그 가냘프고 여린 몸 안에는 쇠 같은 영혼이 깃들어 있답니다. 발랑틴이 당신의 사랑스러운 외제니에게 다정한 안부를 전해요. 온 마음을 다해 당신을 껴안습니다.\n\n엘로이즈 드 빌포르.\n\n추신. 부디 제가 당신 댁에서 몬테크리스토 백작님을 만날 방도를 좀 마련해 주세요. 저는 그분을 꼭 다시 봐야겠고, 또 보고야 말 거예요. 방금 빌포르 씨에게 그분을 찾아뵙겠다는 약속을 받아 두었으니, 그 답방도 있기를 바라고 있어요.\"\n\n그날 밤, 오퇴유에서 일어난 그 사건은 어디서나 화젯거리가 되었다. 알베르는 그것을 어머니에게 이야기했고, 샤토르노는 조키 클럽에서 그 일을 떠벌렸으며, 드브레는 장관의 살롱에서 그것을 길게 늘어놓았다. 보샹조차 백작의 용기와 호기로움을 다룬 이야기에 자기 신문의 스무 줄을 할애해, 귀족 사회의 모든 여성 독자들 눈에 그를 그날의 가장 위대한 영웅으로 기렸다.\n\n빌포르 부인의 집에는 방문객과 궁금증에 찬 벗들이 떼를 지어 몰려와 이름을 남기고 갔다. 적당한 때를 골라 다시 찾아와, 이 더없이 낭만적인 사건의 흥미로운 사정을 부인의 입을 통해 직접 들으려는 속셈이었다.\n\n빌포르 씨로 말하자면, 그는 엘로이즈의 예언을 한 자 한 자 그대로 이루어 냈다. 예복을 차려입고 흰 장갑을 끼고, 하인들에게는 정장 제복을 입고 마차를 수행하라 이른 뒤, 바로 그날 밤 샹젤리제 거리 30번지로 마차를 몰았다.\n\n# Chapter 48. Ideology\n\n몬테크리스토 백작이 파리 사교계의 방식에 오랫동안 익숙했더라면, 빌포르 씨가 취한 행동의 의미를 더 잘 헤아렸을 것이다. 군주가 왕가의 큰집 출신이든 작은집 출신이든, 정부가 교조적 자유주의이든 보수주의이든, 그는 늘 궁정에서 입지가 탄탄했다. 정치적 좌절을 한 번도 겪어 보지 못한 사람은 으레 유능하다고 여겨지는 법이니, 그 역시 누구에게나 재능 있는 인물로 통했다. 많은 이의 미움을 사면서도 또 어떤 이들로부터는 열렬한 지지를 받았으나, 정작 누구에게도 진심으로 사랑받지는 못했다. 그런 빌포르 씨는 사법계에서 높은 자리를 차지하고서 아를레나 몰레처럼 그 위엄을 지켰다. 젊은 아내와 첫 결혼에서 얻은 이제 겨우 열여덟 살 난 딸의 생기 있는 손길 덕분에, 그의 응접실은 여전히 파리에서 손꼽히는 절도 있는 살롱 가운데 하나였고, 그곳에서는 전통 관습에 대한 숭배와 엄격한 예법의 준수가 정성스레 지켜졌다. 얼음장 같은 정중함, 정부 원칙에 대한 철저한 충실, 이론과 이론가를 향한 깊은 경멸, 이상주의에 대한 뿌리 깊은 증오, 이런 것들이 빌포르 씨가 사생활과 공적 생활에서 드러내 보이는 요소들이었다.\n\n빌포르 씨는 사법관일 뿐 아니라 거의 외교관이기도 했다. 그가 언제나 위엄과 경의를 담아 이야기하던 옛 궁정과의 관계 덕분에 그는 새 궁정에서도 존경을 받았다. 그는 너무도 많은 것을 알고 있어서, 늘 신중한 대우를 받을 뿐 아니라 때로는 자문을 구하는 대상이 되기도 했다. 빌포르 씨를 제거할 수만 있었다면 사정은 달랐을지도 모른다. 그러나 군주에게 반기를 든 봉건 영주들처럼, 그는 난공불락의 요새 안에 들어앉아 있었다. 그 요새란 검사라는 자리였고, 그는 그 자리의 온갖 이점을 놀라운 솜씨로 활용했다. 그가 그 자리를 내놓을 일이라곤, 대의원이 되어 중립을 야당의 위치로 바꾸는 경우뿐이었다.\n\n빌포르 씨는 평소 거의 방문을 하지도, 받지도 않았다. 그의 아내가 그를 대신해 방문을 다녔고, 사교계에서는 이것이 당연한 일로 받아들여졌다. 사람들은 사법관의 무겁고도 잡다한 업무를 핑계로 인정해 주었으나, 그것은 사실 계산된 자존심에 지나지 않았으며, 공언된 우월감의 표현이었다. 결국 \"스스로를 높이 여기는 척하라, 그러면 세상이 그대를 높이 여길 것이다\"라는 격언을 실천하는 셈이었다. 이 격언은 오늘날 사교계에서 그리스인들의 \"너 자신을 알라\"보다 백 배는 더 쓸모가 있다. 우리 시대에 와서 그 자기 인식은 더 손쉽고 더 이로운 학문, 곧 \"남을 아는\" 기술로 대체되었으니 말이다.\n\n빌포르 씨는 친구들에게는 든든한 보호자였고, 적들에게는 말이 없으나 모진 적수였다. 둘 중 어느 쪽도 아닌 사람들에게 그는 법으로 빚어진 인간의 조각상이었다. 그는 거만한 태도에, 한결같고 속을 알 수 없거나 아니면 무례할 만큼 꿰뚫어 보며 캐묻는 듯한 눈빛을 지녔다. 네 차례에 걸친 혁명이 그의 출세가 딛고 선 받침대를 쌓아 올리고 굳혀 주었다.\n\n빌포르 씨는 프랑스에서 가장 호기심 없고 가장 따분한 사람이라는 평판을 얻고 있었다. 그는 해마다 무도회를 한 번씩 열었으나, 그 자리에 십오 분만 모습을 드러낼 뿐이었다. 다시 말해 왕이 무도회에서 모습을 보이는 시간보다 사십오 분이나 짧았다. 그는 극장에서도, 음악회에서도, 사람들이 모이는 그 어떤 자리에서도 결코 눈에 띄지 않았다. 어쩌다, 그것도 아주 드물게 휘스트 놀이를 했는데, 그럴 때면 그에 걸맞은 상대를 고르도록 세심히 마음을 썼다. 그 상대는 때로 대사들이었고, 때로 대주교들이었으며, 때로는 어느 공작이거나 의장이거나 미망인이 된 공작 부인이었다.\n\n방금 몬테크리스토 백작의 저택 문 앞에 마차를 세운 사람이 바로 그런 인물이었다. 백작이 커다란 탁자 위로 몸을 숙인 채 지도에 상트페테르부르크에서 중국에 이르는 경로를 그려 넣고 있을 때, 시종이 빌포르 씨의 방문을 알렸다.\n\n검사는 법정에 들어설 때와 똑같이 근엄하고 절도 있는 걸음걸이로 들어왔다. 그는 우리가 앞서 마르세유에서 검사보로 보았던 바로 그 인물이었다. 아니, 그 인물이 발전한 모습이라 해야 옳았다. 자연은 늘 그렇듯, 그가 스스로 그어 놓은 길에서 한 치도 벗어나게 하지 않았다. 호리호리하던 몸은 이제 비쩍 말랐고, 한때 창백하던 낯빛은 이제 누렇게 떴다. 깊이 박힌 두 눈은 움푹 꺼졌고, 그 눈을 가린 금테 안경은 이제 그의 얼굴에서 떼어 낼 수 없는 일부처럼 보였다. 그는 흰 넥타이만 빼면 온통 검은 옷차림이었다. 단춧구멍을 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 가느다랗게 가로지른 붉은 리본만이 그 장례식 같은 모습을 조금 누그러뜨릴 뿐이었는데, 그 리본은 마치 섬세한 붓끝으로 그어 놓은 한 줄기 핏자국 같았다.\n\n몬테크리스토는 스스로를 다스리는 사람이었으나, 인사를 받으며 답례한 그 사법관을 억누를 수 없는 호기심으로 뜯어보았다. 빌포르는 천성이 의심 많은 데다, 특히 사교계의 진귀한 인물에 대해서는 좀처럼 믿으려 들지 않았다. 그래서 그는 이미 \"고귀한 이방인\"이라 불리던 몬테크리스토를, 교황청의 왕자나 천일야화 속 술탄이라기보다는 새로운 사냥터를 찾아 헤매는 모험가, 아니면 탈옥한 죄인쯤으로 여겨 더욱 깔보는 눈으로 바라보았다.\n\n\"백작님,\" 빌포르가 입을 열었다. 사법관들이 연설조로 말할 때 곧잘 짓는, 사교 자리에서도 떨쳐 내지 못하거나 떨쳐 내려 하지 않는 그 째지는 듯한 어조였다. \"백작님, 어제 제 아내와 아들에게 베풀어 주신 더없이 귀한 도움 때문에, 저로서는 마땅히 감사의 말씀을 드려야 할 의무가 생겼습니다. 그래서 그 의무를 다하고, 제 벅찬 고마움을 전해 드리고자 이렇게 찾아왔습니다.\"\n\n이렇게 말하는 동안에도 사법관의 \"준엄한 눈\"은 여느 때의 오만함을 조금도 잃지 않았다. 그는 검사장의 목소리로, 목과 어깨를 뻣뻣하게 굳힌 채 말했는데, 바로 그 모습 때문에 그의 아첨꾼들이 (앞서 보았듯이) 그를 살아 있는 법의 조각상이라 일컫는 것이었다.\n\n\"검사님,\" 백작이 싸늘한 기색으로 대답했다. \"어머니에게 아들을 지켜 드리는 도구가 되었다니 저로서는 더없이 기쁩니다. 모성애야말로 세상에서 가장 거룩한 감정이라고들 하니까요. 그런데 검사님, 제게 찾아온 그 행운 덕분에 검사님께서는 굳이 이런 의무를 치르지 않으셔도 되었을 겁니다. 물론 그 의무를 다하시는 일이 제게 더없이 큰 영광을 안겨 주기는 합니다만 말입니다. 빌포르 씨께서 지금 제게 베풀어 주시는 이런 호의를 평소에는 좀처럼 후하게 내주지 않으신다는 것을 저도 알고 있으니까요. 하지만 그 호의가 아무리 값진 것이라 해도, 제 양심 속에서 느끼는 만족에는 미치지 못합니다.\"\n\n빌포르는 전혀 예상하지 못한 이 대답에 깜짝 놀라, 갑옷 위로 날아드는 일격을 느낀 병사처럼 움찔했다. 그리고 멸시하듯 입술 한쪽을 비트는 그 모습은, 바로 그 순간부터 그가 머릿속 수첩에 몬테크리스토 백작은 결코 교양 있는 신사가 아니라고 적어 넣었음을 드러내고 있었다.\n\n그는 대화를 이어 갈 만한 무언가를 붙잡으려고 주위를 둘러보았고, 이내 손쉽게 화제를 찾은 듯했다. 그는 자기가 들어왔을 때 몬테크리스토가 들여다보고 있던 지도를 보고는 말했다.\n\n\"지리에 몰두하고 계신 모양이군요, 백작님? 듣자 하니 이 지도에 그려진 만큼이나 많은 나라를 두루 보셨다는 백작님께는 풍성한 공부거리이겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 검사님.\" 백작이 대답했다. \"저는 인류 전체를 한 덩어리로 놓고, 검사님께서 날마다 개개인을 두고 하시는 일, 곧 생리학적 연구를 해 보려 했습니다. 부분에서 전체로 올라가기보다 전체에서 부분으로 내려가는 편이 훨씬 쉽다고 믿었지요. 이는 미지수에서 기지수로가 아니라 기지수에서 미지수로 나아가게 하는 대수학의 공리이기도 합니다. 그건 그렇고, 앉으시지요, 검사님. 부탁드립니다.\"\n\n몬테크리스토가 의자를 가리켰는데, 검사는 손수 그 의자를 앞으로 끌어와야 하는 수고를 치러야 했다. 반면 백작은 빌포르 씨가 들어왔을 때 무릎을 꿇고 기대 있던 제 의자에 그저 털썩 몸을 묻을 뿐이었다. 그리하여 백작은 창을 등진 채 방문객 쪽으로 반쯤 돌아앉아, 한쪽 팔꿈치를 잠시나마 대화의 주제가 되어 준 지도에 괴고 있었다. 그리고 그 대화는, 당글라르나 모르세르와의 면담 때처럼, 상황까지는 아니더라도 사람에 어울리는 방향으로 흘러갔다.\n\n\"아, 철학을 하시는군요.\" 빌포르가 잠시 침묵한 뒤에 대답했다. 그 침묵 동안 그는 강력한 적수와 맞붙은 씨름꾼처럼 숨을 골랐다. \"그런데 백작님, 정말이지 저도 백작님처럼 달리 할 일이 없다면, 좀 더 재미있는 소일거리를 찾아볼 것 같군요.\"\n\n\"실은요, 검사님,\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"태양 현미경으로 인간을 들여다보는 사람에게 인간이란 그저 흉측한 애벌레에 지나지 않습니다. 그런데 제게 달리 할 일이 없다고 말씀하신 것 같군요. 그렇다면 제가 한번 여쭙겠습니다, 검사님. 검사님께서는 하실 일이 있으십니까? 하실 일이 있다고 정말 믿으십니까? 더 노골적으로 말해, 검사님께서 하시는 일이 정말 무어라 불릴 만한 가치가 있다고 생각하십니까?\"\n\n이 기이한 적수가 이토록 힘차게 찔러 넣은 두 번째 일격에 빌포르의 놀라움은 곱절이 되었다. 이 사법관이 이토록 강력한 역설을 들어 본 것은 참으로 오랜만이었다. 아니, 더 정확히 말하면, 그가 그런 말을 들어 본 것은 난생처음이었다. 검사는 안간힘을 다해 대꾸했다.\n\n\"백작님,\" 그가 응수했다. \"백작님은 이방인이시고, 또 본인 입으로 인생의 한 시절을 동방의 여러 나라에서 보내셨다 하셨으니, 야만의 나라에서는 그토록 신속한 인간의 정의가 우리나라에서는 얼마나 신중하고 면밀하게 따져진 절차를 밟는지 모르실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"아, 압니다, 알고말고요, 검사님. 그건 옛사람들이 말하던 '페데 클라우도', 곧 절뚝거리는 걸음이지요. 저는 그 모든 것을 압니다. 제가 특히 몰두해 온 것이 바로 모든 나라의 정의였으니까요. 저는 자연의 정의를 모든 민족의 형사 절차와 견주어 왔습니다. 그리고 말씀드리건대, 검사님, 제가 하느님의 법에 가장 자주 부합한다고 본 것은 원시 민족들의 법, 곧 보복의 법이었습니다.\"\n\n\"그 법이 채택된다면, 백작님,\" 검사가 말했다. \"우리 법전이 무척 간단해질 테고, 그렇게 되면 사법관들은 (방금 백작님이 지적하신 대로) 할 일이 그리 많지 않겠지요.\"\n\n\"어쩌면 시간이 흐르면 그렇게 될지도 모릅니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"인간의 발명이란 복잡한 데서 단순한 데로 나아가는 법이고, 단순함이란 언제나 완성이니까요.\"\n\n\"그러나 그동안에는,\" 사법관이 말을 이었다. \"우리 법전은 갈리아의 관습과 로마의 법, 프랑크족의 관례에서 비롯된 온갖 모순된 조항을 안은 채 그대로 효력을 발휘하고 있습니다. 백작님도 동의하시겠지만, 그 모든 것을 익히려면 오랜 수고가 필요합니다. 이 지식을 얻으려면 지루한 공부가 필요하고, 일단 얻고 나면 그것을 머릿속에 붙들어 둘 강한 기억력이 필요하지요.\"\n\n\"전적으로 동의합니다, 검사님. 그러나 검사님께서 프랑스 법전에 관해 아시는 그 모든 것을, 저는 그 법전뿐 아니라 모든 나라의 법전에 관해서도 알고 있습니다. 영국의 법, 터키의 법, 일본의 법, 힌두의 법이 제게는 프랑스 법만큼이나 익숙합니다. 그러니 제가 검사님께 상대적으로 (검사님도 아시다시피, 만물은 상대적이지요) 말씀드린 것이 옳았던 셈입니다. 즉 제가 해 온 일에 견주면 검사님께서 하실 일은 아주 적습니다. 그러나 제가 배운 모든 것에 견주면, 검사님께서는 아직 배우셔야 할 것이 무척 많습니다.\"\n\n\"그런데 무슨 동기로 그 모든 것을 배우셨습니까?\" 빌포르가 놀라워하며 물었다.\n\n몬테크리스토가 미소 지었다.\n\n\"정말이지, 검사님,\" 그가 말했다. \"남달리 뛰어난 분이라는 평판을 얻으셨는데도, 검사님께서는 모든 것을 사회의 물질적이고 속된 관점에서, 곧 인간에서 시작해 인간에서 끝나는 관점에서 바라보시는군요. 다시 말해 인간의 지성이 품을 수 있는 가장 제한되고 가장 좁은 시야로 말입니다.\"\n\n\"제발, 백작님, 좀 더 풀어서 설명해 주십시오.\" 빌포르가 점점 더 놀라워하며 말했다. \"저는 정말 백작님 말씀을 온전히 이해하지 못하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"제 말은 이렇습니다, 검사님. 검사님께서는 여러 나라의 사회 조직에 눈을 고정한 채, 기계의 태엽만 볼 뿐 그것을 움직이게 하는 숭고한 장인은 보지 못하신다는 겁니다. 또 검사님 앞과 주위에서, 장관이나 왕이 임명장에 서명해 준 관리들 말고는 아무도 알아보지 못하신다는 겁니다. 하느님께서 그 관리들과 장관들, 왕들 위에 두신 사람들, 곧 채워야 할 자리가 아니라 완수해야 할 사명을 부여받은 사람들은 검사님의 좁고 한정된 관찰의 영역에서 빠져나가 버립니다. 인간의 나약함이 이렇게 실패하는 것은, 그 기관이 쇠약하고 불완전하기 때문이지요. 토비트는 자신에게 빛을 되찾아 준 천사를 평범한 젊은이로 알았습니다. 여러 민족은 자기들을 멸망시킬 운명을 타고난 아틸라를 여느 정복자와 다름없는 정복자로 알았지요. 그래서 둘 다 자기 사명을 밝혀야만, 비로소 알려지고 인정받을 수 있었습니다. 한쪽은 '나는 주님의 천사다'라고 말해야 했고, 다른 쪽은 '나는 하느님의 망치다'라고 말해야 했습니다. 그래야 그 둘 안에 깃든 신성한 본질이 드러날 수 있었던 것입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 빌포르가 점점 더 어리둥절해하며, 자기가 신비주의자나 미치광이와 이야기를 나누고 있다고 정말로 여기며 말했다. \"백작님께서는 스스로를 방금 말씀하신 그 비범한 존재들 가운데 하나로 여기시는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"못 될 것도 없지요?\" 몬테크리스토가 싸늘하게 말했다.\n\n\"용서하십시오, 백작님.\" 빌포르가 완전히 어안이 벙벙해져서 대답했다. \"백작님을 찾아뵈었을 때, 보통 사람들의 지식과 이해를 이토록 멀리 뛰어넘는 지식과 이해를 갖춘 분을 만나리라고는 미처 몰랐던 점을 너그러이 봐주십시오. 우리처럼 문명에 찌든 가련한 자들 사이에서는, 백작님같이 막대한 재산을 지니신 (적어도 그렇다고들 합니다만) 신사를 만나는 일이 흔치 않습니다. 그리고 부디 알아주십시오, 저는 캐묻는 것이 아니라 그저 들은 말을 옮길 뿐입니다. 말씀드리거니와, 그런 특권을 누리는 부유한 분들이 사회의 상태를 두고 사색에 잠기거나, 기껏해야 운명에게 이 세상의 재물을 빼앗긴 자들이나 위로할 만한 철학적 공상에 시간을 허비하는 일은 흔치 않습니다.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 검사님,\" 백작이 응수했다. \"검사님께서는 지금의 그 높은 지위에 오르시면서, 예외라는 것을 한 번도 인정하지 않으시거나, 단 한 번도 마주치지 않으셨단 말입니까? 그토록 섬세하고 확실해졌을 그 두 눈을, 검사님은 한 번도 쓰지 않으십니까? 마주한 상대가 어떤 부류의 인간인지 한눈에 꿰뚫어 보는 데 말입니다. 사법관이라면 마땅히 법을 가장 잘 집행하는 사람일 뿐 아니라, 그 직업의 술수를 가장 교묘하게 풀어내는 사람이어야 하지 않겠습니까? 마음을 더듬는 강철 탐침, 영혼마다 많든 적든 불순물이 섞여 있는 그 금을 시험하는 시금석이어야 하지 않겠습니까?\"\n\n\"백작님,\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"맹세코, 백작님께 손을 들었습니다. 저는 정말로 백작님처럼 말하는 사람을 한 번도 만난 적이 없습니다.\"\n\n\"그건 검사님께서 늘 평범한 조건들의 테두리 안에 갇혀 계실 뿐, 하느님께서 보이지 않거나 예외적인 존재들로 채워 놓으신 저 높은 영역으로 날개를 펼칠 엄두를 한 번도 내 보지 않으셨기 때문이지요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 백작님, 그런 영역이 존재하고, 그 표지를 지닌 보이지 않는 존재들이 우리 사이에 섞여 있다고 인정하시는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"못 될 것도 없지요? 검사님은 지금 숨 쉬는 공기가 보이십니까? 그 공기가 없으면 한순간도 살 수 없는데도 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 우리는 백작님께서 말씀하시는 그 존재들을 보지 못하는 것이군요?\"\n\n\"아니요, 우리는 그들을 봅니다. 하느님께서 그들에게 물질의 형태를 입도록 허락하실 때면 언제든 검사님은 그들을 보게 됩니다. 검사님은 그들을 만지고, 그들과 부딪치고, 그들에게 말을 걸고, 그러면 그들도 검사님께 대답하지요.\"\n\n\"아,\" 빌포르가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"솔직히 그런 존재 가운데 하나가 제 곁에 닿아 있을 때라면 미리 귀띔이라도 받고 싶군요.\"\n\n\"바라시는 대로 이미 받으셨습니다, 검사님. 방금 귀띔을 받으셨고, 지금 제가 다시 한번 귀띔을 드리니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 백작님 자신이 그 표지를 지닌 존재들 가운데 하나라는 말씀입니까?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 검사님. 저는 그렇게 믿습니다. 지금까지 그 누구도 저와 같은 처지에 놓인 적이 없으니까요. 왕들의 영토는 산이나 강으로, 또는 풍속의 변화나 언어의 차이로 경계가 그어집니다. 그러나 제 왕국의 경계는 오직 이 세계뿐입니다. 저는 이탈리아인도, 프랑스인도, 힌두인도, 미국인도, 에스파냐인도 아니기 때문입니다. 저는 세계인입니다. 그 어느 나라도 제가 태어나는 것을 보았다고 말할 수 없습니다. 제가 어느 나라에서 죽을지는 오직 하느님만이 아십니다. 저는 모든 관습을 받아들이고, 모든 언어를 구사합니다. 검사님은 제가 프랑스어를 검사님만큼이나 막힘없이 또 정확하게 구사하니 저를 프랑스인이라 믿으십니다. 그런데 제 누비아인 알리는 저를 아랍인으로 믿고, 제 집사 베르투치오는 저를 로마인으로 여기며, 제 노예 하이데는 저를 그리스인이라 생각합니다. 그러니 검사님도 헤아리실 겁니다. 어느 나라에도 속하지 않고, 어느 정부에도 보호를 청하지 않으며, 그 누구도 형제로 인정하지 않는 저는, 강한 자들을 붙드는 거리낌도, 약한 자들을 마비시키는 장애도, 그 어느 것 하나 저를 마비시키거나 붙들지 못합니다. 제게는 적이 둘뿐입니다. 정복자라고는 하지 않겠습니다. 끈기를 가지면 그 둘마저 굴복시키니까요. 그 둘이란 바로 시간과 거리입니다. 그리고 세 번째이자 가장 무서운 적이 있으니, 그것은 죽음을 면치 못하는 인간이라는 제 처지입니다. 제가 겨누는 목표에 이르기도 전에 저를 가로막을 수 있는 것은 오직 이것뿐입니다. 나머지는 모두 수학의 항으로 환원해 놓았으니까요. 사람들이 운명의 우연이라 부르는 것, 곧 파멸이며 변화며 정황이며 하는 것들을 저는 모조리 예견해 두었습니다. 설령 그중 어느 하나가 저를 덮친다 해도, 저를 짓누르지는 못할 겁니다. 죽지 않는 한, 저는 언제나 지금의 저일 것입니다. 그러기에 저는 검사님께서 한 번도 들어 보지 못한 것들을, 왕들의 입에서조차 들어 보지 못한 것들을 말씀드리는 겁니다. 왕들은 검사님을 필요로 하고, 그 밖의 사람들은 검사님을 두려워하니까요. 우리처럼 어처구니없게 짜인 사회에서 '언젠가 나도 검사와 얽히게 될지 모른다'고 속으로 되뇌지 않는 자가 어디 있겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그러나 백작님께서도 그렇게 말씀하실 수 있지 않습니까? 프랑스의 주민이 되시는 순간, 백작님께서도 당연히 프랑스 법의 지배를 받게 되시니까요.\"\n\n\"압니다, 검사님.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"그러나 저는 어느 나라를 방문하면, 동원할 수 있는 모든 수단을 써서, 제가 무언가를 기대하거나 두려워할 만한 사람들을 연구하기 시작합니다. 그들이 스스로를 아는 것만큼, 어쩌면 그 이상으로 그들을 알게 될 때까지 말입니다. 그러니 제가 상대해야 할 검사가, 그가 누구이든 간에, 저보다 훨씬 더 난처해지리라는 것은 자명한 일이지요.\"\n\n\"그 말씀인즉,\" 빌포르가 머뭇거리며 대답했다. \"인간의 본성이 나약하니, 백작님의 신조대로라면 사람은 누구나 잘못을 저질렀다는 것이군요.\"\n\n\"잘못이든 죄든 말입니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 무심한 기색으로 응수했다.\n\n\"그리고 형제로 인정하지 않는다고 손수 말씀하신 그 사람들 가운데, 오직 백작님 한 분만이,\" 빌포르가 다소 떨리는 어조로 말했다. \"백작님 한 분만이 완벽하시다는 것이군요.\"\n\n\"아니요, 완벽한 것은 아닙니다.\" 백작이 대답했다. \"그저 속을 알 수 없을 뿐이지요. 그게 다입니다. 그러나 이런 가락이 검사님께 거슬리신다면, 이쯤에서 그만두시지요. 저는 검사님의 정의에 동요하지 않습니다. 검사님께서 제 천리안에 동요하지 않으시는 것처럼 말입니다.\"\n\n\"아니요, 아닙니다, 결코요.\" 자기 입지를 포기하는 것처럼 보일까 두려워하며 빌포르가 말했다. \"아닙니다. 백작님의 눈부시고 거의 숭고하기까지 한 대화로, 백작님은 저를 평범한 차원 위로 끌어올려 주셨습니다. 우리는 이제 한낱 이야기를 나누는 것이 아니라 논설의 차원으로 올라선 셈입니다. 그러나 검사님도 아시다시피, 강단에 선 신학자들과 논쟁에 빠진 철학자들은 이따금 잔혹한 진실을 말하지요. 그러니 잠시 우리가 사교적인 방식으로, 아니 철학적인 방식으로 신학을 논하고 있다 칩시다. 그러면 제가 무례하게 들릴지언정 백작님께 이렇게 말씀드리겠습니다. '형제여, 그대는 자존심에 너무 많은 것을 바치고 있소. 그대가 남들 위에 있을지는 모르나, 그대 위에는 하느님이 계시오.'\"\n\n\"우리 모두의 위에 계시지요, 검사님.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. 그 어조와 강조가 어찌나 깊었던지, 빌포르는 자기도 모르게 몸서리를 쳤다. \"저는 사람들에 대해서는 자존심을 품습니다. 사람들이란 짓밟지 않고 지나가려는 이라면 누구든 위협할 채비가 되어 있는 뱀들이니까요. 그러나 하느님 앞에서는 그 자존심을 내려놓습니다. 하느님께서 저를 무에서 끌어내어 지금의 저로 만들어 주셨으니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 백작님, 저는 백작님을 존경합니다.\" 빌포르가 말했다. 이 기이한 대화에서 처음으로 그는, 지금까지 그저 '검사님'이라고만 불러 온 이 낯선 인물에게 귀족을 부르는 호칭을 썼다. \"그렇습니다, 그리고 백작님께 말씀드리겠습니다. 백작님이 정말로 강하고, 정말로 우월하며, 정말로 경건하거나 속을 알 수 없으시다면, 또 그 둘이 같은 것이라 하신 백작님 말씀이 옳다면, 그렇다면 자긍심을 품으십시오, 백작님. 그것이야말로 우월함의 특징이니까요. 그런데 백작님께서는 분명 어떤 야심을 품고 계시겠지요.\"\n\n\"품고 있습니다, 검사님.\"\n\n\"그게 무엇입니까?\"\n\n\"저 역시 사람이라면 누구나 일생에 한 번은 겪는 일을 겪었습니다. 사탄이 저를 이 땅에서 가장 높은 산으로 데려가더니, 그곳에서 세상의 모든 왕국을 보여 주었지요. 그리고 예전에 그가 말했던 것처럼, 제게도 이렇게 말했습니다. '땅의 자식이여, 나를 숭배하려면 무엇을 갖고 싶으냐?' 저는 오래 생각했습니다. 갉아먹는 듯한 야심이 오랫동안 저를 괴롭혀 왔으니까요. 그러고는 이렇게 대답했습니다. '들어라. 나는 늘 섭리에 관한 이야기를 들어 왔으나, 한 번도 그를 본 적이 없다. 그와 닮은 것도, 그가 존재한다고 믿게 할 만한 그 무엇도 본 적이 없다. 나는 나 자신이 섭리가 되고 싶다. 세상에서 가장 아름답고 가장 고귀하며 가장 숭고한 일은 상을 주고 벌을 내리는 것이라고 느끼니까.' 사탄은 고개를 숙이며 신음했습니다. '네가 잘못 알고 있구나.' 그가 말했습니다. '섭리는 분명 존재한다. 다만 네가 한 번도 본 적이 없을 뿐이지. 하느님의 자식은 그 아버지만큼이나 보이지 않으니까. 네가 그와 닮은 것을 본 적이 없는 까닭은, 그가 은밀한 태엽으로 일을 하고, 숨겨진 길로 움직이기 때문이다. 내가 너를 위해 해 줄 수 있는 것이라곤, 너를 그 섭리의 대행자 가운데 하나로 만들어 주는 것뿐이다.' 그렇게 거래가 맺어졌습니다. 어쩌면 제 영혼을 바쳐야 할지도 모르지요. 그러나 그게 무슨 대수겠습니까?\" 몬테크리스토가 덧붙였다. \"다시 그 일을 하게 된다 해도, 저는 또다시 그렇게 할 것입니다.\"\n\n빌포르는 더없이 놀라워하며 몬테크리스토를 바라보았다.\n\n\"백작님,\" 그가 물었다. \"친척이 있으십니까?\"\n\n\"없습니다, 검사님. 저는 이 세상에 홀로입니다.\"\n\n\"그것참 안된 일이군요.\"\n\n\"어째서요?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다.\n\n\"그렇지 않다면, 백작님의 그 자긍심을 무너뜨리고도 남을 광경을 보실 수도 있었을 테니까요. 백작님은 죽음 말고는 아무것도 두렵지 않다고 하셨지요?\"\n\n\"두렵다고는 하지 않았습니다. 그저 죽음만이 제 계획의 실행을 가로막을 수 있다고 말했을 뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"노년은요?\"\n\n\"저는 늙기 전에 제 목적을 이룰 것입니다.\"\n\n\"광기는요?\"\n\n\"저는 거의 미칠 뻔한 적이 있습니다. 그리고 검사님도 '논 비스 인 이뎀'이라는 격언을 아시지요. 같은 일로 두 번 벌하지 않는다는 형법의 격언이니, 검사님께서는 그 뜻을 온전히 헤아리실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"백작님,\" 빌포르가 말을 이었다. \"죽음과 노년과 광기 말고도 두려워할 것이 있습니다. 이를테면 졸중이 있지요. 그 벼락 같은 일격은 사람을 내리치되 죽이지는 않으면서도, 모든 것을 끝장내 버립니다. 백작님은 지금처럼 여전히 백작님 자신이면서도, 더는 백작님 자신이 아니게 됩니다. 아리엘처럼 천사에 가깝던 백작님이, 캘리밴처럼 짐승에 가까운 한낱 무력한 덩어리가 되어 버리는 겁니다. 그리고 인간의 말로 이것을 부르는 이름은, 말씀드렸다시피, 다름 아닌 졸중입니다. 자, 백작님께서 원하신다면, 언제든 저희 집에 오셔서 이 대화를 이어 가시지요. 백작님을 이해할 줄도 알고 또 기꺼이 반박하고 싶어 하는 적수를 만나고 싶으신 날이라면 언제든 말입니다. 그러면 제 아버지 누아르티에 드 빌포르 씨를 보여 드리겠습니다. 프랑스 혁명 때 가장 격렬했던 자코뱅 당원 가운데 한 분이지요. 다시 말해, 더없이 강인한 체질을 바탕으로 더없이 놀라운 대담함을 지녔던 분입니다. 어쩌면 백작님처럼 이 땅의 모든 왕국을 다 보지는 못하셨겠지만, 그 가운데 가장 위대한 나라 하나를 뒤엎는 데 손을 보탠 분입니다. 실로 백작님처럼 자신을, 하느님이 아니라 어느 지고한 존재의 사절로, 섭리가 아니라 운명의 사절로 믿었던 분입니다. 그런데 검사님, 뇌의 한 부분에서 혈관 하나가 터지자 이 모든 것이 무너지고 말았습니다. 하루 만에도, 한 시간 만에도 아닌, 단 일 초 만에 말입니다. 전날 밤만 해도 단두대와 대포와 비수를 비웃던 늙은 자코뱅, 늙은 원로원 의원, 늙은 카르보나리 당원이던 누아르티에 씨, 혁명을 가지고 놀던 누아르티에 씨, 프랑스를 하나의 거대한 장기판으로 여겨, 그 위에서 졸과 차와 마와 비숍과 퀸을 차례로 사라지게 하여 끝내 왕을 외통수에 몰아넣으려던 누아르티에 씨, 그토록 가공할 누아르티에 씨가, 이튿날 아침에는 '가엾은 누아르티에 씨', 곧 집안에서 가장 약한 존재인 손녀 발랑틴의 자비에 매달리는 무력한 노인이 되어 버렸습니다. 사실상 말 못 하고 얼어붙은 송장이 되어, 자기 몸이 썩어 가는 것조차 의식하지 못한 채, 그 몸이 무너져 갈 시간을 벌기 위해 고통 없이 목숨만 이어 가고 있는 것입니다.\"\n\n\"아아, 검사님,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그 광경은 제 눈에도, 제 생각에도 낯선 것이 아닙니다. 저도 의술을 조금 아는 사람이라, 제 동료들처럼 살아 있는 물질에서도, 죽은 물질에서도 영혼을 한두 번 찾아보았습니다. 그러나 섭리와 마찬가지로, 영혼은 제 마음에는 깃들어 있으면서도 제 눈에는 보이지 않은 채로 남았지요. 소크라테스와 세네카, 성 아우구스티누스, 그리고 갈 이래로 수백 명의 저술가가 운문으로, 또 산문으로 검사님이 방금 하신 그 비유를 해 왔습니다. 그렇지만 아버지의 고통이 아들의 마음에 큰 변화를 일으킬 수 있다는 것을, 저도 충분히 이해합니다. 검사님, 찾아뵙겠습니다. 검사님께서 제 자긍심을 위해 그 끔찍한 광경을 한번 보라고 권하시니까요. 그것은 검사님 가족에게 틀림없이 크나큰 슬픔의 원천이었을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"하느님께서 제게 그토록 큰 보상을 내려 주시지 않았더라면, 의심할 바 없이 그러했겠지요. 무덤을 향해 발을 끌며 나아가는 그 노인과는 대조적으로, 이제 막 인생에 들어선 두 아이가 있으니까요. 제 첫 아내, 르네 드 생메랑 양에게서 얻은 딸 발랑틴과, 오늘 백작님께서 목숨을 구해 주신 사내아이 에두아르 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그 보상에서 검사님은 어떤 결론을 끌어내십니까, 검사님?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다.\n\n\"제가 끌어내는 결론은 이렇습니다.\" 빌포르가 대답했다. \"제 아버지는 정념에 휩쓸려, 인간의 정의는 알지 못하나 하느님의 정의가 똑똑히 새겨 둔 어떤 잘못을 저지르셨다는 겁니다. 그 하느님께서 자비로이 단 한 사람만을 벌하고자 하시어, 이 정의를 아버지 한 분께만 내리셨다는 것이지요.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 입가에 미소를 띤 채, 빌포르가 들었더라면 혼비백산하여 달아났을 신음을 영혼 깊은 곳에서 토해 냈다.\n\n\"그럼 이만 가 보겠습니다, 백작님.\" 자리에서 일어선 사법관이 말했다. \"백작님에 대한 기억을, 존경의 기억을 안고 떠나겠습니다. 저를 더 잘 알게 되시면, 그 기억이 백작님께 불쾌하지 않으시기를 바랍니다. 저는 친구를 따분하게 만드는 사람이 아니니까요. 차차 아시게 될 겁니다. 게다가 백작님께서는 빌포르 부인을 영원한 벗으로 얻으셨습니다.\"\n\n백작은 가볍게 고개를 숙였고, 빌포르를 서재 문까지만 배웅하는 데 그쳤다. 검사는 두 하인의 호위를 받으며 마차로 향했는데, 하인들은 주인의 신호 한 번에 더없이 깍듯한 태도로 그를 뒤따랐다. 빌포르가 떠나자 몬테크리스토는 깊은 한숨을 내쉬고는 말했다.\n\n\"이 독은 이쯤 마셨으니, 이제 해독제를 찾아봐야겠군.\"\n\n그러고는 종을 울려, 들어온 알리에게 말했다.\n\n\"부인의 방으로 갈 것이다. 한 시에 마차를 대기시켜 두어라.\"\n\n# Chapter 49. Haydée\n\n몬테크리스토 백작의 새로운, 아니 차라리 오래된 지인이라 해야 할 사람들, 메슬레 거리에 사는 그 사람들이 다름 아닌 막시밀리앙과 쥘리, 그리고 에마뉘엘이라는 것을 독자들은 기억할 것이다.\n\n곧 다가올 방문에서 누릴 기쁨에 대한 예감, 스스로 뛰어든 그 거의 목숨을 건 싸움 위로 천상의 행복이 던지는 밝고 순수한 광채가, 빌포르가 떠난 직후 그의 마음을 비추었다. 적어도 사납고 폭풍 같은 마음의 격정에서 잠시나마 벗어나 쉴 수 있으리라는 위안의 전망으로 그의 생각이 되돌아가자, 그의 얼굴 전체가 형언할 수 없는 기쁨과 평온의 빛으로 환해졌다. 백작의 부름에 서둘러 달려왔던 알리조차도, 평소 그토록 엄하고 차가운 얼굴에 드물게 떠오른 생기와 즐거움에 매혹된 듯 놀라며 주인 앞을 물러났다. 그리고 충직한 누비아인은 그것이 무엇이든 간에 주인의 사색 위를 맴도는 유쾌한 상념을 쫓아낼까 두려워하는 듯, 그 행복한 몽상을 흩어 버릴 미세한 소리조차 내지 않으려 숨을 죽인 채 발끝으로 문을 향해 걸어갔다.\n\n정오였다. 몬테크리스토는 한 시간을 따로 떼어 두어 하이데의 거처에서 보내기로 했다. 마치 짓눌린 그의 영혼이 순수하고 섞임 없는 기쁨의 감정을 단번에 받아들이지는 못하고, 완전하고 온전한 행복을 받아들일 마음의 준비를 위해 잔잔하고 부드러운 감정의 점진적 흐름을 필요로 하는 것 같았다. 평범한 본성을 지닌 이들이 강하거나 격렬한 감각을 받아들이려면 차츰차츰 길들여져야 하듯이 말이다.\n\n이미 말했듯이 그 젊은 그리스 여인은 백작의 거처와는 완전히 동떨어진 방들을 쓰고 있었다. 방들은 동방의 취향에 철저히 맞추어 꾸며져 있었다. 바닥에는 터키가 만들어 낼 수 있는 가장 값진 양탄자가 깔려 있었고, 벽에는 가장 화려한 무늬와 질감의 비단 비단 직물이 드리워져 있었다. 방마다 둘레에는 호화로운 긴 의자가 놓이고 부드럽게 푹 꺼지는 방석이 높이 쌓여 있어, 쉬려는 이의 기분이나 편의에 맞추어 정돈하기만 하면 되었다.\n\n하이데에게는 프랑스인 하녀 셋과 그리스인 하녀 하나가 있었다. 프랑스인 하녀 셋은 늘 작은 대기실에 머물면서, 작은 금종이 울리거나 로마이크어를 쓰는 노예가 명을 전하면 곧장 따를 채비를 하고 있었다. 그 그리스인 노예는 자기 안주인의 뜻을 다른 세 시녀에게 전달할 만큼의 프랑스어는 알고 있었다. 그 세 시녀는 하이데를 여왕에게 보일 법한 예우를 다해 모시라는 몬테크리스토의 지극히 엄한 지시를 받은 터였다.\n\n그 젊은 처녀 자신은 대개 거처의 맨 안쪽 방에서 시간을 보냈다. 그곳은 일종의 규방으로, 원형이었고 오로지 천장으로만 빛이 들었는데, 천장은 장밋빛 유리로 되어 있었다. 하이데는 은빛 점이 박힌 푸른 새틴으로 덮인 부드러운 솜털 방석에 기대 누워 있었다. 더없이 곱게 빚어진 한쪽 팔로 머리를 받쳐 바로 뒤의 긴 의자에 기댄 채, 다른 손으로는 값진 물담뱃대의 산호 물부리를 입술에 가져다 대고 있었다. 향수를 푼 물을 지나며 향기를 머금은 연기가 그 잘 휘는 관을 통해 빨려 들었다. 동방의 여인에게는 더없이 자연스러운 그 자세도, 유럽인이었다면 효과를 노린 지나친 교태로 여겨졌을 것이다.\n\n에피루스 여인들의 차림인 그녀의 옷은, 분홍 장미가 수놓인 흰 새틴 바지로 이루어져 있었다. 그 사이로 드러난 발은 어찌나 곱게 생기고 어찌나 희고 섬세한지, 끝이 위로 들린 작은 슬리퍼를 들락거리며 끊임없이 움직이는 모습이 눈을 일깨워 주지 않았더라면 파로스 대리석으로 착각했을 만했다. 슬리퍼는 금과 진주로 아름답게 장식되어 있었다. 그녀는 파란색과 흰색 줄무늬의 조끼를 입었는데, 길게 트인 소매에는 은빛 고리와 진주 단추가 달려 있었다. 그리고 일종의 보디스를 걸쳤는데, 가운데에서 허리까지만 여며져 있어 상아 같은 목 전체와 가슴 윗부분이 드러났다. 그것은 세 개의 화려한 다이아몬드 걸쇠로 채워져 있었다. 보디스와 바지가 만나는 부분은 갖가지 빛깔의 스카프 하나로 완전히 가려져 있었는데, 그 찬란한 색조와 풍성한 비단 술 장식은 파리 미인들의 눈에 그토록 귀하게 비치는 것이었다.\n\n머리 한쪽에 비스듬히, 진주가 수놓인 황금빛 비단의 작은 모자를 얹었고, 다른 한쪽에는 자줏빛 장미가 그 타오르는 빛깔을 풍성한 머릿단과 뒤섞고 있었다. 머리카락은 어찌나 새카만지 푸른빛이 감돌 정도였다.\n\n옷이 더 보태려는 부질없는 시도를 비웃듯 사랑스럽게 빛나는 그 얼굴의 극치의 아름다움은, 유난히도 순수한 그리스의 것이었다. 그녀의 종족과 나라가 지닌 크고 검고 그윽한 눈, 곱게 빚어진 코, 산호 같은 입술, 진주 같은 이가 거기 있었다.\n\n그리고 모든 것을 완성하듯, 하이데는 젊음의 매력이 한창 피어나는 봄날에 있었다. 그녀는 아직 열아홉이나 스무 번의 여름밖에 헤아리지 못했다.\n\n몬테크리스토는 그리스인 시녀를 불러, 안주인이 자신의 방문을 받아 줄 만한지 여쭈어 보라고 일렀다. 하이데의 유일한 대답은, 손짓으로 시녀에게 규방 문 앞에 드리운 융단 휘장을 걷으라고 이른 것이었다. 그렇게 열린 문틀이 일종의 테두리가 되어, 젊은 처녀의 그림 같은 자세와 자태가 빚어내는 우아한 정경을 감싸 안았다.\n\n몬테크리스토가 다가가자 그녀는 물담뱃대를 든 팔의 팔꿈치에 몸을 기대고, 다른 손을 그에게 내밀며, 아테네와 스파르타의 여인들이 쓰는 낭랑한 언어로, 사람을 사로잡는 달콤한 미소를 띤 채 말했다.\n\n\"들어오시기 전에 어찌 허락을 구하세요? 더는 제 주인이 아니신가요, 아니면 제가 더는 당신의 노예가 아닌가요?\"\n\n몬테크리스토도 미소로 답했다.\n\n\"하이데,\" 그가 말했다. \"너도 잘 알지 않느냐.\"\n\n\"어찌 그리 차갑게, 그리 멀게 제게 말씀하세요?\" 젊은 그리스 여인이 물었다. \"제가 무슨 일로든 당신을 언짢게 했나요? 오, 그렇다면 마음대로 벌하셔요. 하지만 부디, 부디 그렇게 격식 차리고 거북한 말투와 태도로 제게 말하지는 마셔요.\"\n\n\"하이데,\" 백작이 대답했다. \"너는 지금 프랑스에 있고, 자유의 몸이라는 걸 알지 않느냐.\"\n\n\"무엇을 할 자유 말씀이세요?\" 젊은 처녀가 물었다.\n\n\"나를 떠날 자유 말이다.\"\n\n\"당신을 떠난다고요? 제가 왜 당신을 떠나야 하나요?\"\n\n\"그건 내가 말할 일이 아니다. 하지만 우리는 이제 사교계에 섞여, 남을 찾아가고 또 찾아오는 손님을 맞이하게 될 것이다.\"\n\n\"저는 당신 말고는 누구도 보고 싶지 않아요.\"\n\n\"그러다 네가 더 마음에 두게 될 사람을 만나거든, 나도 그렇게 부당하게 굴지는……\"\n\n\"저는 당신보다 더 마음에 두는 사람을 본 적이 없고, 당신과 제 아버지 말고는 누구도 사랑한 적이 없어요.\"\n\n\"가엾은 아이로구나,\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"그건 그저 네 아버지와 나만이 너에게 말을 건넨 유일한 남자들이기 때문이란다.\"\n\n\"저는 다른 누구도 제게 말 거는 걸 원치 않아요. 제 아버지는 저를 당신의 '기쁨'이라 부르셨고, 당신은 저를 당신의 '사랑'이라 부르시지요. 그리고 두 분 다 저를 '내 아이'라 부르셨어요.\"\n\n\"네 아버지를 기억하느냐, 하이데?\"\n\n젊은 그리스 여인이 미소 지었다.\n\n\"아버지는 여기, 그리고 여기에 계세요.\" 그녀가 두 눈과 가슴을 가리키며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 나는 어디에 있느냐?\" 몬테크리스토가 웃으며 물었다.\n\n\"당신이요?\" 그녀가 가슴 떨리는 다정함이 어린 목소리로 외쳤다. \"당신은 어디에나 계세요!\" 몬테크리스토는 젊은 처녀의 가녀린 손을 자기 손에 쥐고 입술로 가져가려 했으나, 천진한 자연의 아이는 황급히 손을 거두고 뺨을 내밀었다.\n\n\"이제 알겠지, 하이데,\" 백작이 말했다. \"이 순간부터 너는 완전히 자유의 몸이며, 여기서는 무엇이든 마음껏 할 수 있고, 네 나라의 옷차림을 벗어 두든 그대로 두든 네 마음대로 할 수 있다. 이 저택 안에서 너는 네 행동의 절대적인 주인이며, 가장 마음에 드는 대로 바깥출입을 하든 네 방에 머물든 자유다. 마차는 네 명령을 기다리고 있고, 알리와 미르토가 네가 가고자 하는 어디든 함께 갈 것이다. 다만 너에게 한 가지 청하고 싶은 것이 있다.\"\n\n\"말씀하셔요.\"\n\n\"네 출생의 비밀을 부디 잘 지켜다오. 지난날에 대해서는 한마디도 비치지 말고, 어떤 경우에도 너의 고귀한 아버지나 불운한 어머니의 이름을 입에 올리는 일이 없도록 해라.\"\n\n\"이미 말씀드렸듯이, 주인님, 저는 아무도 만나지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"하이데, 그렇게 완벽한 칩거는 동방의 습속과 관습에는 맞을지 모르나, 파리에서는 가능하지 않을 수도 있다. 그러니 로마와 피렌체, 밀라노, 마드리드의 방식에 익숙해졌듯이, 이 북녘 땅에서의 우리 생활 방식에도 익숙해지도록 애써 보아라. 네가 여기 머물든 동방으로 돌아가든, 언젠가는 그것이 너에게 쓸모가 있을 것이다.\"\n\n젊은 처녀는 눈물 어린 눈을 몬테크리스토에게로 들며, 가슴 저미는 간절함으로 말했다. \"우리가 동방으로 돌아갈 때 말씀이시지요, 주인님, 그렇지 않나요?\"\n\n\"내 아이야,\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"우리가 헤어지게 된다면 그건 결코 내 잘못도 내 바람도 아니라는 것을 너는 잘 알지 않느냐. 나무가 꽃을 버리는 것이 아니라, 꽃이 나무에서 떨어지는 것이란다.\"\n\n\"주인님,\" 하이데가 대답했다. \"저는 결코 당신을 떠나지 않을 거예요. 당신 없이는 살아갈 수 없으리라는 걸 잘 아니까요.\"\n\n\"가엾은 아이야, 십 년 뒤면 나는 늙어 있을 테고, 너는 여전히 젊을 것이다.\"\n\n\"제 아버지는 길고 흰 수염을 기르셨지만, 저는 아버지를 사랑했어요. 아버지는 예순이셨지만, 제게는 제가 본 그 어떤 잘생긴 젊은이들보다 더 멋지셨어요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 말해 보아라, 하이데. 우리의 지금 생활 방식에 익숙해질 수 있을 것 같으냐?\"\n\n\"당신을 뵐 수 있나요?\"\n\n\"날마다.\"\n\n\"그럼 무엇을 두려워하시나요, 주인님?\"\n\n\"네가 지루해할까 봐 그런다.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 주인님. 아침이면 당신이 오시리라는 기대로 기뻐할 것이고, 저녁이면 당신 곁에서 누린 행복을 떠올리며 즐거워할 거예요. 게다가 혼자 있을 때면 지난날의 웅대한 그림들을 불러낼 수 있고, 핀두스와 올림푸스의 우뚝 솟은 산봉우리로만 가로막힌 드넓은 지평선을 볼 수 있어요. 오, 믿어 주세요. 슬픔과 사랑과 감사라는 세 가지 큰 격정이 가슴을 가득 채우고 있을 때, 권태가 들어설 자리는 없답니다.\"\n\n\"너는 에피루스의 자랑스러운 딸이로구나, 하이데. 너의 매혹적이고 시적인 생각은, 네 나라를 고향이라 일컫는 그 여신들의 혈통에서 네가 나왔음을 잘 보여 준다. 너의 젊음이 시들거나 쓸쓸한 고독 속에 흘러가 버리지 않도록 내가 보살피마, 그것은 믿어도 좋다. 그리고 이것만은 분명히 알아 두어라. 네가 나를 아버지처럼 사랑한다면, 나는 너를 자식처럼 사랑한다는 것을.\"\n\n\"틀리셨어요, 주인님. 제가 당신께 품은 사랑은 제 아버지께 품었던 사랑과는 아주 달라요. 제 아버지는 돌아가셨지만, 저는 죽지 않았어요. 하지만 당신이 돌아가신다면, 저도 함께 죽을 거예요.\"\n\n백작은 깊은 다정함이 어린 미소를 띠고 손을 내밀었고, 그녀는 그 손을 입술로 가져갔다.\n\n이렇게 모렐과 그 가족과 나누려던 만남에 어울리는 마음 상태가 된 몬테크리스토는, 핀다로스의 이런 시구를 읊조리며 걸어 나갔다. \"젊음은 꽃이요 사랑은 그 열매라. 그 고요한 자람을 지켜본 끝에 그것을 거두어 제 것이라 부를 수 있는 자는 복되도다.\" 마차는 명령대로 준비되어 있었고, 백작은 가볍게 올라타 여느 때처럼 빠른 속도로 달려갔다.\n\n# Chapter 50. The Morrel Family\n\n몇 분도 채 지나지 않아 백작은 멜레가 7번지에 다다랐다. 흰 돌로 지은 집이었고, 그 앞 작은 안뜰에는 아름다운 꽃이 가득한 작은 화단 두 개가 놓여 있었다. 문을 열어 준 문지기에게서 백작은 코클레스를 알아보았다. 그러나 코클레스는 한쪽 눈만 남은 데다, 그 한 눈마저 구 년 세월에 다소 흐릿해진 탓에 백작을 알아보지 못했다.\n\n현관 앞까지 들어온 마차들은 바위로 꾸민 수반에서 솟구치는 분수를 피하느라 방향을 틀어야 했다. 이 장식은 동네 전체의 부러움을 사, 이곳에 \"작은 베르사유\"라는 별명을 안겨 준 터였다. 수반 안에 금붕어와 은붕어가 헤엄치고 있었음은 굳이 덧붙일 필요도 없으리라. 집은 아래에 부엌과 지하 저장고를 두고, 일층 위로 두 개 층과 다락이 더 있었다. 거대한 작업장과 정원 끝의 별채 두 채, 그리고 정원 자체로 이루어진 이 부지 전체를 에마뉘엘이 사들였는데, 그는 한눈에 이것을 수지맞는 거래로 만들 수 있음을 알아보았다. 그는 집과 정원의 절반을 자기 몫으로 남기고, 정원과 작업장 사이에 담을 쌓은 뒤 정원 끝 별채와 함께 임대했다. 그리하여 그는 푼돈으로도, 생제르맹 거리의 가장 훌륭한 저택에 사는 이들 못지않게 안락하고 또 남의 눈에 띄지 않는 거처를 누렸다.\n\n아침 식당은 떡갈나무로, 응접실은 마호가니로 마감했고, 가구는 푸른 벨벳이었다. 침실은 시트론 나무와 초록빛 다마스크로 꾸며져 있었다. 한 번도 공부한 적 없는 에마뉘엘에게는 서재가, 한 번도 연주한 적 없는 쥘리에게는 음악실이 있었다. 이층 전체는 막시밀리앙의 차지였다. 그의 거처는 누이의 것과 거의 똑같았으나, 아침 식당 대신 친구들을 맞이하는 당구실이 있다는 점만 달랐다. 백작의 마차가 대문 앞에 멈춰 섰을 때, 그는 정원 입구에서 시가를 피우며 자기 말을 손질하는 모습을 지켜보고 있었다.\n\n코클레스가 대문을 열자, 마부석에서 뛰어내린 바티스탱이 에르보 부부와 막시밀리앙 모렐 씨가 몬테크리스토 백작 각하를 만나 주실 수 있겠느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"몬테크리스토 백작이라고?\" 모렐이 시가를 던지고 마차 쪽으로 달려오며 외쳤다. \"당연히 뵙고말고요. 아, 정말 고맙습니다, 백작님. 약속을 잊지 않고 와 주셨군요.\"\n\n그러고서 젊은 장교는 어찌나 뜨겁게 백작의 손을 잡고 흔들었던지, 몬테크리스토는 그 기쁨이 진심임을 의심할 수 없었다. 자신이 애타게 기다려졌고 또 반갑게 맞아들여지고 있음을 그는 알아차렸다.\n\n\"자, 자,\" 막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"제가 길잡이가 되어 드리지요. 백작님 같은 분을 하인 손에 맡길 수야 없지요. 누이는 정원에서 시든 장미를 따고 있고, 매형은 그녀에게서 여섯 걸음도 안 되는 곳에서 신문 두 가지, 라 프레스와 레 데바를 읽고 있을 겁니다. 에르보 부인이 보이는 곳이라면, 반경 사 미터 안을 살피기만 하면 에마뉘엘 씨가 있거든요. 또 그 '역도 성립한다'고, 폴리테크니크 학교에서들 말하듯 말이지요.\"\n\n발소리에 스물에서 스물다섯 살쯤 된 젊은 여인이 고개를 들었다. 비단 실내복 차림으로 노아제트 장미나무의 시든 잎을 부지런히 떼어 내던 참이었다. 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회 직원이 예언한 그대로, 에마뉘엘 에르보 부인이 된 쥘리였다. 그녀는 낯선 사람을 보고 놀란 소리를 냈고, 막시밀리앙은 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"신경 쓰지 마, 쥘리.\" 그가 말했다. \"백작님은 파리에 오신 지 이삼 일밖에 안 됐지만, 마레의 멋쟁이 부인이 어떤지는 벌써 다 알고 계셔. 혹 모르신다면, 네가 직접 보여 드리면 되고.\"\n\n\"아, 백작님,\" 쥘리가 대답했다. \"이렇게 백작님을 모시고 오다니, 제 동생의 횡포랍니다. 이 애는 가엾은 누이를 한 번도 배려한 적이 없어요. 페늘롱, 페늘롱!\"\n\n화단 한 곳을 부지런히 파고 있던 노인이 삽을 흙에 꽂아 두고 다가왔다. 모자를 손에 쥐고서, 방금 뺨 안쪽에 밀어 넣은 씹는담배를 감추려 애쓰고 있었다. 여전히 숱 많고 헝클어진 머리칼에 희끗한 가닥이 섞여 있었고, 햇볕에 그을린 얼굴과 단호한 눈빛은 적도의 열기와 열대의 폭풍을 견뎌 온 늙은 선원에게 잘 어울렸다.\n\n\"부르셨습니까, 쥘리 아가씨?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n페늘롱은 여전히 주인의 딸을 \"쥘리 아가씨\"라고 부르는 버릇을 간직하고 있었고, 그 호칭을 에르보 부인으로 바꾸지 못했다.\n\n\"페늘롱,\" 쥘리가 대답했다. \"에마뉘엘 씨께 이분이 오셨다고 알려 드려요. 그리고 막시밀리앙, 백작님을 응접실로 모셔 드려.\"\n\n그러고는 몬테크리스토를 돌아보며 말을 이었다. \"잠시 자리를 비우는 것을 양해해 주시기 바랍니다.\" 그녀는 대답도 기다리지 않고 나무 덤불 뒤로 사라져, 옆길로 집 안으로 들어가 버렸다.\n\n\"제가 댁에 적잖은 소란을 일으킨 모양입니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 모렐에게 말했다.\n\n\"저기 보십시오.\" 막시밀리앙이 웃으며 말했다. \"매형이 작업복을 정장으로 갈아입고 있군요. 정말이지, 멜레가에서 백작님은 모르는 사람이 없답니다.\"\n\n\"댁의 가족은 무척 행복한 분들 같군요.\" 백작이 혼잣말하듯 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그럼요, 백작님. 행복에 필요한 것은 아무것도 부족하지 않습니다. 두 사람은 젊고 쾌활하며 서로 깊이 사랑하지요. 연 이만 오천 프랑이면 자기들이 로스차일드만큼이나 부유하다고 여긴답니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 이만 오천 프랑은 큰돈이 아닌데요.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. 어찌나 부드럽고 다정한 목소리였던지, 막시밀리앙의 가슴에는 마치 아버지의 음성처럼 와닿았다. \"하지만 두 분은 그것으로 만족하지 못할 겁니다. 매형 되시는 분은 변호사입니까? 의사입니까?\"\n\n\"상인이었습니다, 백작님. 제 가엾은 아버지의 사업을 이어받았지요. 모렐 씨는 돌아가시면서 오십만 프랑을 남기셨고, 그것이 누이와 제게 나뉘었습니다. 우리가 유일한 자식이었으니까요. 매형은 누이와 결혼할 때 고결한 정직함과 일류의 능력, 흠 없는 평판 말고는 아무 재산도 없었지만, 아내만큼의 재산을 갖고 싶어 했습니다. 그는 일하고 또 애써, 마침내 이십오만 프랑을 모았지요. 이 목표를 이루는 데 육 년이면 충분했습니다. 오, 정말이지, 백작님, 재능으로 보아 더 높은 자리에 오를 운명이었던 이 젊은 부부가 함께 애쓰는 모습은 가슴 뭉클한 광경이었습니다. 두 사람은 아버지 집안의 관습을 조금도 바꾸려 하지 않았던 탓에, 양심이 무딘 사람들이라면 두세 해면 이뤘을 일을 육 년 걸려 이뤄 냈지요. 마르세유는 두 사람의 정당한 칭송으로 떠들썩했습니다. 마침내 어느 날, 에마뉘엘이 막 장부 정리를 마친 아내에게 찾아왔습니다.\n\n\"'쥘리,' 그가 그녀에게 말했습니다. '방금 코클레스가 마지막 백 프랑짜리 다발을 내게 가져왔소. 이것으로 우리가 한도로 정해 둔 이십오만 프랑이 다 채워졌소. 앞으로 우리가 가지게 될 이 소박한 재산에 당신은 만족할 수 있겠소? 내 말을 들어 보오. 우리 상회는 연간 백만 프랑어치 거래를 하고, 거기서 사만 프랑의 수입을 얻고 있소. 원한다면 한 시간 안에 사업을 처분할 수도 있소. 들로네 씨에게서 편지를 받았는데, 우리 상회의 영업권을 자기 것과 합치려고 삼십만 프랑에 사겠다고 했거든. 어떻게 하면 좋을지 조언해 주오.'\n\n\"'에마뉘엘,' 누이가 대답했습니다. '모렐 상회는 모렐 사람만이 이어 갈 수 있어요. 아버지의 이름을 불운과 파산의 위험에서 지켜 내는 일이 삼십만 프랑의 값어치가 없겠어요?'\n\n\"'나도 그렇게 생각했소,' 에마뉘엘이 대답했습니다. '하지만 당신 의견을 듣고 싶었소.'\n\n\"'내 생각은 이래요. 우리 장부는 정리됐고 어음도 다 갚았어요. 이제 더 이상 어음을 발행하지 말고, 사무소를 닫기만 하면 돼요.'\n\n\"이 일은 즉시 실행되었습니다. 세 시였지요. 십오 분 뒤, 한 상인이 배 두 척의 보험을 들러 찾아왔습니다. 만 오천 프랑의 순이익이 걸린 일이었지요.\n\n\"'선생,' 에마뉘엘이 말했습니다. '들로네 씨를 찾아가 주시지요. 우리는 사업을 그만두었습니다.'\n\n\"'언제부터요?' 놀란 상인이 물었습니다.\n\n\"'십오 분 전부터요.' 이것이 그 대답이었습니다.\n\n\"그래서, 백작님,\" 막시밀리앙이 말을 이었다. \"누이와 매형이 연 이만 오천 프랑밖에 갖지 못한 까닭이 바로 이것입니다.\"\n\n막시밀리앙이 이야기를 막 끝냈을 때, 그 이야기를 듣는 동안 백작의 가슴은 부풀어 올랐는데, 모자를 쓰고 정장을 차려입은 에마뉘엘이 들어왔다. 그는 손님의 지체를 아는 사람다운 태도로 백작에게 인사한 뒤, 작은 정원을 한 바퀴 안내하고는 집 안으로 돌아왔다.\n\n향기로 공기를 가득 채운 꽃이 꽂힌 커다란 일본 자기 화병이 응접실에 놓여 있었다. 단정히 차려입고 머리를 매만진 쥘리가, 이 모든 일을 십 분도 안 되어 해치우고서, 백작이 들어오자 그를 맞이했다. 바로 옆 새장에서 새들의 노래가 들려왔고, 금사슬나무와 분홍 아카시아 가지가 푸른 벨벳 커튼을 더없이 아름답게 둘러쌌다. 새들의 지저귐부터 안주인의 미소에 이르기까지, 이 매혹적인 안식처의 모든 것이 평온과 휴식을 내뿜고 있었다.\n\n백작은 집에 들어선 순간부터 이 행복의 기운을 느꼈다. 그래서 첫인사를 주고받은 뒤 끊긴 대화를 다시 이어 가야 한다는 것도 잊은 채 말없이 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 침묵이 거의 고통스러워질 무렵, 그는 안간힘을 써서 그 흐뭇한 몽상에서 자신을 떼어 냈다.\n\n\"부인,\" 그가 마침내 입을 열었다. \"제 감정을 용서하시기 바랍니다. 여기서 늘 누리는 행복에만 익숙하신 분께는 분명 놀라우시겠지만, 제게는 이런 만족스러운 광경이 너무도 새로워서, 부인과 부군을 바라보는 데 도무지 싫증이 나질 않는군요.\"\n\n\"저희는 무척 행복합니다, 백작님.\" 쥘리가 대답했다. \"하지만 저희도 불행을 겪어 보았답니다. 저희보다 더 쓰라린 고통을 겪은 사람은 드물 거예요.\"\n\n백작의 얼굴에 더없이 강렬한 호기심의 빛이 떠올랐다.\n\n\"오, 이건 다 집안 내력이에요. 며칠 전 샤토 르노가 백작님께 말씀드렸듯이요.\" 막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"부유하고 부지런한 사람들의 기쁨과 불행을 보는 데 익숙하신 백작님께야 이 보잘것없는 그림이 별 흥미가 없으시겠지만, 그래도 보시다시피 저희도 쓰라린 슬픔을 겪었답니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 신께서는 모든 괴로워하는 이들에게 그러시듯, 두 분의 상처에도 향유를 부어 주셨나요?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다.\n\n\"네, 백작님.\" 쥘리가 대답했다. \"정말 그렇게 말할 수 있어요. 신께서는 오직 택하신 이들에게만 베푸시는 일을 저희에게 해 주셨거든요. 저희에게 천사 하나를 보내 주셨답니다.\"\n\n백작의 두 뺨이 새빨갛게 물들었다. 그는 손수건을 입에 댈 구실을 만들려고 헛기침을 했다.\n\n\"부유하게 태어나 무엇이든 바라는 대로 채울 수 있는 사람들은,\" 에마뉘엘이 말했다. \"삶의 진정한 행복이 무엇인지 알지 못하지요. 마치 몇 장의 부서지기 쉬운 널빤지에 의지해 사나운 바다 위를 떠다녀 본 사람만이 맑은 날씨의 축복을 깨닫는 것처럼 말입니다.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 자리에서 일어나, 아무 대답도 하지 않고서—목소리가 떨려 감정을 들킬 것 같았으므로—느린 걸음으로 방 안을 거닐었다.\n\n\"저희의 호사스러움이 우습게 보이시는군요, 백작님.\" 눈으로 그를 좇던 막시밀리앙이 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 아닙니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. 죽은 사람처럼 창백해진 그는 한 손을 가슴에 얹어 두근거림을 가라앉히면서, 다른 손으로는 수정 덮개를 가리켰다. 그 아래 검은 벨벳 방석 위에 비단 지갑 하나가 놓여 있었다. \"저 지갑에 무슨 사연이 있나 궁금했을 뿐입니다. 한쪽 끝에는 종이가, 다른 쪽 끝에는 커다란 다이아몬드가 들어 있어서요.\"\n\n\"백작님,\" 막시밀리앙이 엄숙한 태도로 대답했다. \"저것이 저희 집안의 가장 소중한 보물입니다.\"\n\n\"보석이 무척 영롱하군요.\" 백작이 대답했다.\n\n\"오, 제 동생은 그 값어치를 말하는 게 아니랍니다. 십만 프랑으로 매겨지긴 했지만요. 동생 말은, 이 지갑 안에 든 물건들이 방금 말씀드린 그 천사의 유물이라는 뜻이에요.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이신지 모르겠습니다. 그렇다고 설명을 청할 수도 없겠지요, 부인.\" 몬테크리스토가 고개를 숙이며 대답했다. \"용서하십시오. 결례를 범할 뜻은 없었습니다.\"\n\n\"결례라니요. 오히려 이 이야기를 늘어놓을 구실을 주시니 저희가 기쁜걸요. 이 지갑이 기리는 고귀한 행동을 감추고 싶었다면, 이렇게 내놓고 보여 드리지도 않았겠지요. 오, 저희는 이 이야기를 어디서든, 누구에게든 들려주고 싶답니다. 그러면 저희의 알려지지 않은 은인이 마음이 동해 그 모습을 드러낼지도 모르니까요.\"\n\n\"아, 정말이지.\" 몬테크리스토가 반쯤 막힌 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"백작님,\" 막시밀리앙이 유리 덮개를 들어 올리고 비단 지갑에 정중히 입을 맞추며 대답했다. \"이 지갑은 한 사람의 손을 거친 것입니다. 제 아버지를 자살에서, 저희를 파멸에서, 저희 가문을 수치와 불명예에서 구해 주신 분이지요. 그분의 견줄 데 없는 자비 덕분에, 궁핍과 비참에 빠질 운명이었던 가엾은 저희 남매가 지금은 누구나 부러워하는 행복을 누리게 되었습니다. 이 편지는\" 그렇게 말하며 막시밀리앙은 지갑에서 편지 한 통을 꺼내 백작에게 건넸다. \"아버지가 절망에 빠져 끔찍한 결심을 하셨던 바로 그날, 그분이 쓰신 것입니다. 그리고 이 다이아몬드는 그 너그러운 미지의 분이 제 누이에게 지참금으로 주신 것이고요.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 편지를 펼쳐 형언할 수 없는 기쁨에 젖어 읽어 내려갔다. 그것은 (독자들이 아시다시피) 쥘리에게 쓰여 \"뱃사람 신드바드\"라고 서명된 바로 그 편지였다.\n\n\"이 일을 베풀어 주신 분이 알려지지 않은 사람이라고요? 두 분께 알려지지 않은?\"\n\n\"네. 저희는 그분의 손을 잡아 보는 행복을 누리지 못했답니다.\" 막시밀리앙이 말을 이었다. \"그 은혜를 베풀어 달라고 하늘에 빌고 또 빌었지만 헛수고였지요. 이 모든 일에는 저희가 헤아릴 수 없는 신비로운 의미가 깃들어 있었습니다. 저희는 보이지 않는 손에, 마법사의 손처럼 강력한 손에 이끌려 왔어요.\"\n\n\"오,\" 쥘리가 외쳤다. \"저는 언젠가 그 손에 입 맞출 희망을 버리지 않았어요. 지금 그분이 만지셨던 이 지갑에 입 맞추듯이요. 사 년 전, 페늘롱이 트리에스테에 있었어요. 페늘롱은, 백작님, 정원에서 보신 그 늙은 선원이랍니다. 갑판장에서 정원사가 된 사람이지요. 페늘롱이 트리에스테에 있을 때, 부두에서 막 요트에 오르려던 영국인 한 사람을 보았어요. 그런데 그가 1829년 6월 5일에 제 아버지를 찾아왔고, 또 9월 5일에 제게 이 편지를 써 준 바로 그 사람임을 알아본 거예요. 같은 사람이라고 확신했지만, 차마 말을 걸지는 못했답니다.\"\n\n\"영국인이라고요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. 쥘리가 자신을 뚫어지게 바라보는 통에 불안해진 것이었다. \"영국인이라고 하셨습니까?\"\n\n\"네.\" 막시밀리앙이 대답했다. \"영국인이었습니다. 로마에 있는 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회의 신임받는 직원이라고 자기를 소개했지요. 그래서 며칠 전 모르세르 씨 댁에서 백작님이 톰슨 앤드 프렌치가 백작님의 거래 은행이라고 하셨을 때 제가 깜짝 놀랐던 겁니다. 말씀드린 대로 그 일은 1829년에 있었지요. 제발 말씀해 주십시오. 백작님은 그 영국인을 아셨습니까?\"\n\n\"하지만 두 분 말씀으로는, 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회가 이 은혜를 베푼 적이 없다고 줄곧 부인해 왔다고 하셨지요?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 이 영국인이, 두 분의 아버님께서 베푸셨던, 그러나 아버님 자신은 잊고 계셨던 어떤 친절에 감사하는 마음으로, 그 신세를 갚으려고 이런 방법을 택한 누군가일 수도 있지 않겠습니까?\"\n\n\"이 일에서는 무엇이든 가능하지요. 기적까지도요.\"\n\n\"그 사람의 이름이 무엇이었습니까?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다.\n\n\"그는 다른 이름은 대지 않았어요.\" 쥘리가 백작을 진지하게 바라보며 대답했다. \"편지 끝에 적힌 이름, '뱃사람 신드바드' 말고는요.\"\n\n\"그건 분명 본명이 아니라 꾸며 낸 이름이겠지요.\"\n\n그러고는 쥘리가 자기 목소리에 흠칫하는 것을 알아채고 말을 이었다.\n\n\"말씀해 보십시오.\" 그가 계속했다. \"그 사람이 제 키쯤, 어쩌면 좀 더 컸고, 턱이 높은 크라바트에 파묻히다시피 했으며, 외투 단추를 빈틈없이 채우고, 끊임없이 연필을 꺼내 들지 않았습니까?\"\n\n\"오, 그럼 그분을 아세요?\" 쥘리가 외쳤다. 그녀의 눈이 기쁨으로 반짝였다.\n\n\"아닙니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"그저 짐작해 본 것뿐입니다. 제가 윌모어 경이라는 사람을 알았는데, 늘 이런 종류의 일을 하던 분이었지요.\"\n\n\"자기 정체를 밝히지 않고요?\"\n\n\"괴팍한 사람이었습니다. 감사라는 게 존재한다고 믿지 않았지요.\"\n\n\"오, 맙소사,\" 쥘리가 두 손을 맞잡으며 외쳤다. \"그럼 그분은 무엇을 믿으셨나요?\"\n\n\"제가 그를 알던 무렵에는 감사를 믿지 않았습니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 쥘리의 음성에 가슴이 뭉클해져 말했다. \"하지만 어쩌면, 그 뒤로는 감사라는 게 정말 존재한다는 증거를 얻었을지도 모르지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 그분을 아세요, 백작님?\" 에마뉘엘이 물었다.\n\n\"오, 정말 그분을 아신다면,\" 쥘리가 외쳤다. \"어디 계신지, 어디서 찾을 수 있는지 말씀해 주실 수 있나요? 막시밀리앙, 에마뉘엘, 우리가 그분을 찾아내기만 하면, 그분도 마음에서 우러난 감사가 있다는 걸 믿게 되실 거예요!\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 눈에 눈물이 차오르는 것을 느끼고, 다시 황급히 방 안을 거닐었다.\n\n\"제발 부탁드립니다.\" 막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"그분에 대해 무엇이든 아시는 게 있다면, 그것이 무엇인지 말씀해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"아아,\" 몬테크리스토가 감정을 누르려 애쓰며 외쳤다. \"윌모어 경이 두 분의 알려지지 않은 은인이라면, 다시는 그를 만나지 못하실 것 같아 두렵습니다. 저는 이 년 전 팔레르모에서 그와 헤어졌는데, 그때 그는 가장 먼 지방으로 떠나려던 참이었습니다. 그러니 다시는 돌아오지 않을까 걱정스럽군요.\"\n\n\"오, 백작님, 너무 잔인하세요.\" 쥘리가 깊이 마음 상해 말했다. 젊은 부인의 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁했다.\n\n\"부인,\" 몬테크리스토가 엄숙하게, 쥘리의 뺨을 타고 흐르는 두 알의 영롱한 눈물방울을 진지하게 바라보며 대답했다. \"윌모어 경이 지금 제가 보는 것을 보았더라면, 그는 삶에 애착을 느꼈을 겁니다. 부인이 흘리시는 눈물이 그를 인류와 화해시켜 주었을 테니까요.\" 그는 쥘리에게 손을 내밀었고, 백작의 눈빛과 말투에 사로잡힌 그녀는 그에게 자기 손을 맡겼다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"윌모어 경에게도 가족이나 친구가 있었을 테고, 누군가는 알았을 텐데요. 저희가 혹시……\"\n\n\"오, 알아보셔야 소용없습니다.\" 백작이 대답했다. \"어쩌면 결국 그는 두 분이 찾으시는 사람이 아닐지도 모릅니다. 그는 제 친구였고, 제게 아무것도 숨기지 않았으니, 만약 그런 일이 있었다면 제게 털어놓았을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그래서 그가 당신에게 아무 말도 하지 않았다고요?\"\n\n\"짐작이라도 할 만한 어떤 것도요?\"\n\n\"아무것도요.\"\n\n\"그런데도 곧바로 그분 이야기를 꺼내셨군요.\"\n\n\"아, 그런 경우에는 누구라도 짐작을 하는 법이지요……\"\n\n\"누나, 누나,\" 막시밀리앙이 백작을 거들며 말했다. \"백작님 말씀이 옳아요. 우리의 훌륭하신 아버지가 그토록 자주 하시던 말씀을 떠올려 봐요. '우리를 그렇게 구해 준 사람은 영국인이 아니었다'고 하셨잖아요.\"\n\n몬테크리스토가 흠칫했다. \"아버님이 뭐라고 하셨다고요, 모렐 씨?\" 그가 다급히 물었다.\n\n\"제 아버지는 그 일이 기적처럼 이루어졌다고 생각하셨습니다. 어떤 은인이 무덤에서 일어나 저희를 구했다고 믿으셨지요. 오, 그건 가슴 뭉클한 미신이었습니다, 백작님. 저 자신은 그것을 믿지 않았지만, 무슨 일이 있어도 아버지의 믿음만은 깨뜨리고 싶지 않았어요. 아버지가 얼마나 자주 그 일을 곱씹으며, 어느 소중한 친구의 이름을, 영영 잃어버린 친구의 이름을 부르셨던지요. 그리고 임종 자리에서, 영원이 가까이 다가오며 그분의 마음을 초자연적인 빛으로 밝혀 준 듯했을 때, 그때까지 의심에 불과했던 그 생각이 확신으로 굳어졌습니다. 아버지의 마지막 말씀은 이러했지요. '막시밀리앙, 그건 에드몽 당테스였다!'\"\n\n이 말에, 한동안 점점 더해 가던 백작의 창백함이 놀라울 지경에 이르렀다. 그는 말을 잇지 못했다. 시각을 잊은 사람처럼 시계를 들여다보고는, 에르보 부인에게 몇 마디 황급한 말을 건넸다. 그리고 에마뉘엘과 막시밀리앙의 손을 잡으며 말했다. \"부인, 부디 가끔 찾아뵙는 것을 허락해 주시기 바랍니다. 두 분의 우정을 소중히 여기며, 따뜻하게 맞아 주셔서 감사드립니다. 여러 해 만에 처음으로 이렇게 제 감정에 굴복해 본 것이니까요.\" 그러고서 그는 황급히 방을 나섰다.\n\n\"이 몬테크리스토 백작은 참 별난 사람이군.\" 에마뉘엘이 말했다.\n\n\"그래요.\" 막시밀리앙이 대답했다. \"하지만 저분은 더없이 훌륭한 마음을 지녔고, 우리를 좋아한다는 게 확실히 느껴져요.\"\n\n\"그분 목소리가 제 가슴에 와닿았어요.\" 쥘리가 말했다. \"그리고 두세 번쯤은, 전에 들어 본 목소리 같다는 생각이 들었답니다.\"\n\n# Chapter 51. Pyramus and Thisbe\n\n피라무스와 티스베●\n생토노레 거리를 따라 삼분의 이쯤 걸어 들어간 곳, 집집마다 설계의 우아함과 건축의 화려함을 다투는 그 부유한 동네에서도 가장 위풍당당한 저택 가운데 하나의 뒤편으로, 넓은 정원 하나가 펼쳐져 있었다. 그 정원에는 가지를 넓게 뻗은 밤나무들이 단단한 성벽처럼 담장 위로 높이 머리를 치켜들고 있었고, 봄이 올 때마다 섬세한 분홍빛과 흰빛 꽃잎을 비처럼 흩뿌렸다. 그 꽃잎들은 루이 13세 시절로 거슬러 올라가는, 기묘하게 세공된 철문의 네모진 두 기둥 위에 놓인 커다란 돌 화병 속으로 떨어져 내렸다.\n\n그러나 이 고귀한 출입구는, 그 인상적인 외관에도 불구하고, 또 두 화병에 심긴 제라늄들이 얼룩덜룩한 잎사귀를 바람에 흔들며 진홍빛 꽃으로 눈을 사로잡는 우아한 정취에도 불구하고, 완전히 쓰이지 않은 채 버려져 있었다. 저택의 주인들은 오래전부터 저택 본채만 지니는 편이 낫다고 여겼다. 본채는 나무가 빽빽이 심긴 안뜰과 함께 생토노레 거리로 통했고, 정원은 이 철문으로 가로막혀 있었는데, 그 철문은 예전에 한 에이커쯤 되는 훌륭한 채마밭으로 이어져 있었다. 투기의 악마가 그 채마밭 저편에 선을 하나 그었기 때문이다. 다시 말해, 거리 하나를 설계해 놓았던 것이다. 거리가 측량되고, 이름이 정해져 철판에 새겨 붙여졌다. 그러나 공사가 시작되기도 전에, 그 땅의 소유주는 한 가지 생각이 떠올랐다. 지금 과일과 채소에 바쳐진 이 땅을 따라 건물을 지어 올리면, 그래서 그 길을 파리에서 가장 중요한 대로 가운데 하나인 생토노레 거리 자체와 통하는 지선으로 만들면, 제법 두둑한 돈을 얻을 수 있으리라는 것이었다.\n\n그러나 투기라는 일에서는, \"사람이 꾀해도\" \"돈이 처리하는\" 법이다. 그런 비슷한 난관 탓에, 새로 이름까지 얻은 그 거리는 태어나자마자 죽고 말았다. 그리고 채마밭을 사들인 자는, 비싼 값을 치른 데다, 상당한 손해를 보지 않고서는 그 매물을 떠맡아 줄 사람을 도무지 찾을 수 없었다. 그러면서도 언젠가는 그 땅으로, 지난날의 지출은 물론이고 새로 사들인 자산에 묶인 자본의 이자까지 갚아 줄 만한 돈을 손에 넣게 되리라는 믿음을 여전히 버리지 못한 채, 결국 그 땅을 몇몇 채소 장수에게 한 해 500프랑의 세를 받고 임시로 빌려주는 것으로 만족했다.\n\n그리하여, 앞서 말했듯, 채마밭으로 통하는 철문은 닫힌 채 녹슬도록 방치되었고, 머지않아 그 녹이 경첩을 갉아먹어 버릴 듯했다. 한편 땅을 파고 일구는 자들의 천한 시선이 감히 저택에 딸린 귀족적인 울타리 안을 더럽히지 못하도록, 철문은 6피트 높이까지 널빤지로 막혀 있었다. 사실 그 널빤지들이 그리 빈틈없이 맞물려 있지는 않아서, 그 틈새로 황급히 안을 엿볼 수는 있었다. 그러나 저택에 사는 사람들의 엄격한 예법과 빈틈없는 품위로 보아, 누가 그런 틈을 악용하리라고 염려할 까닭은 전혀 없었다.\n\n그러나 버려진 채마밭에서는 원예가 손을 놓아 버린 듯했다. 한때 양배추며 당근, 무, 완두콩, 멜론이 무성하던 자리에는, 이제 듬성듬성한 자주개자리 한 무더기만이 그나마 가꿀 만한 가치가 있다고 여겨졌음을 증언하고 있었다. 우리가 지금껏 묘사해 온 담장으로 둘러싸인 공간에서 그 설계된 거리로 빠져나가는, 낮고 작은 문이 하나 있었다. 그 땅은 여러 소작인이 소출이 없다고 버린 곳이라, 이제는 세간의 평가가 완전히 땅에 떨어져, 처음 내던 임차료의 절반의 절반도 거두어들이지 못했다. 저택 쪽으로는 앞서 말한 밤나무들이 담장 위로 높이 솟아 있었지만, 그렇다고 다른 무성한 관목이며 꽃들이 자라는 것을 조금도 막지는 못했다. 그 식물들은 마치 빛과 공기라는 은혜를 누릴 권리가 제게 있다고 주장하듯, 빈자리를 메우려 앞다투어 몸을 내밀었다. 한쪽 구석에서는 잎이 어찌나 무성한지 거의 햇빛마저 가릴 지경이었는데, 그곳에 놓인 커다란 돌 의자와 몇몇 소박한 걸상들은, 이 아늑한 자리가 저택에 사는 누군가에게 두루 사랑받거나 특별히 쓰이는 곳임을 말해 주었다. 저택은 그곳을 일부 가리고 있는 빽빽한 초록빛 더미 사이로 어렴풋이 보였는데, 백 걸음밖에 떨어져 있지 않았는데도 그러했다.\n\n이 외진 정원 한 귀퉁이를 산책의 끝자락으로, 혹은 명상의 자리로 고른 사람이 누구였든, 그 선택은 충분히 정당했다. 눈부신 빛이라곤 없었고, 서늘하고 상쾌한 그늘이 있었으며, 한여름 가장 무더운 날에도 이곳에는 발을 들이지 못하는 작열하는 햇살을 막아 주는 차양이 있었고, 끊임없이 들려오는 새들의 고운 지저귐이 있었으며, 거리의 소음에서도 저택의 부산함에서도 완전히 벗어나 있었기 때문이다. 봄이 파리 사람들에게 베푼 가장 따뜻한 날들 가운데 어느 하루의 저녁, 돌 의자 위에는 책 한 권과 양산 하나, 그리고 일감 바구니 하나가 아무렇게나 놓여 있는 것이 보였다. 바구니에서는 절반쯤 수놓다 만 고운 무명 손수건이 늘어져 있었다. 그 물건들에서 조금 떨어진 곳에 한 젊은 여인이 철문 가까이 바짝 붙어 서서, 널빤지 틈으로 건너편의 무언가를 알아보려 애쓰고 있었다. 그 간절한 자세와, 바라던 대상을 찾으려는 듯한 고정된 눈길은, 그녀의 마음이 이 일에 얼마나 깊이 쏠려 있는지를 잘 드러내고 있었다.\n\n바로 그 순간, 버려진 땅에서 거리로 통하는 작은 옆문이 소리 없이 열리며, 키가 크고 다부진 젊은 남자가 나타났다. 그는 흔해 빠진 회색 작업복에 벨벳 모자 차림이었지만, 더없이 풍성하고 윤기 흐르는 검은빛으로 정성껏 다듬은 머리와 턱수염, 콧수염은 그 천한 옷차림과 어울리지 않았다. 그는 누가 보지 않는지 확인하려고 재빨리 주위를 한번 둘러본 뒤, 작은 문으로 들어와 조심스레 문을 닫아 잠그고는, 황급한 걸음으로 울타리를 향해 나아갔다.\n\n그녀가 기다리던 사람이긴 했으나 아마도 그런 차림은 아니었던 터라, 그를 보고 젊은 여인은 깜짝 놀라 황급히 물러서려 했다. 그러나 사랑에 빠진 눈은 이미, 좁은 나무 울타리 틈새로도, 하얀 옷자락이 움직이는 것을 보았고, 파란 띠가 나부끼는 것을 알아챘다. 그는 널빤지에 입술을 바짝 대고 외쳤다.\n\n\"놀라지 마요, 발랑틴. 나예요!\"\n\n겁먹은 처녀는 다시 용기를 내어 철문으로 돌아왔고, 그러면서 말했다.\n\n\"그런데 오늘은 왜 이렇게 늦게 오셨어요? 곧 저녁 식사 시간인걸요. 게다가 저는 늘 저를 감시하는 새어머니와, 지나치게 헌신적인 하녀와, 늘 저를 못살게 구는 성가신 동생을 떼어 내느라 적잖이 머리를 써야 했어요. 동생은 제가 수놓으러 온다고 놀려 대는데, 그 수는 영영 끝내지 못할 것 같아요. 그러니 저를 기다리게 한 일은 어떻게든 변명을 잘해 보시고, 그다음엔 어째서 제가 처음엔 못 알아볼 만큼 이상한 옷차림을 하고 계신지 말씀해 주세요.\"\n\n\"사랑하는 발랑틴,\" 젊은 남자가 말했다. \"우리 두 사람의 신분 차이 때문에, 내 사랑을 입에 담으면 당신의 기분을 상하게 할까 두렵소. 하지만 당신 앞에 서면, 내 영혼을 다 쏟아 내어, 내가 얼마나 애틋이 당신을 흠모하는지 말하고 싶은 마음을 도무지 억누를 수가 없구려. 그저 이런 달콤한 순간의 기억을 가슴에 품고 돌아가기 위해서라면, 나는 당신이 나를 나무란 것조차 고마워할 수 있소. 그 꾸지람은 내게 한 가닥 희망의 빛을 남겨 주니까. 비록 당신이 나를 기다린 게 아니었다 해도 (그렇게 여기는 건 정말이지 자만을 넘어선 일이겠지만), 적어도 내가 당신의 생각 속에는 있었다는 희망 말이오. 당신은 내가 왜 늦었는지, 또 왜 변장을 하고 왔는지 물었지요. 두 가지 다 솔직히 까닭을 밝히겠소. 그러고 나면 당신이 너그러이 나를 용서해 주리라 믿소. 나는 직업을 하나 골랐소.\"\n\n\"직업이요? 아, 막시밀리앙, 우리에게 이토록 깊은 근심거리가 있는 때에 어떻게 농담을 하실 수 있어요?\"\n\n\"내 목숨보다도 훨씬 더 소중한 것을 두고 농담을 하다니, 하늘이 막아 주시기를! 하지만 발랑틴, 내 말을 들어 보시오. 자초지종을 다 말해 주리다. 나는 들판을 헤매고 담을 기어오르는 일에 진력이 났소. 게다가 당신이 일러 준 생각, 그러니까 내가 이 근처를 어슬렁대다 들키면 당신 아버지가 나를 도둑으로 몰아 감옥에 처넣을지도 모른다는 생각에 진지하게 겁이 났소. 그건 프랑스군의 명예를 더럽히는 일일 테고, 또 전쟁 계획이 있을 리 없는 곳에 스파이 기병대 대위가 줄곧 나타난다면 사람들이 의아해할 게 뻔하다는 점은 말할 것도 없소. 그래서 나는 정원사가 되었고, 그 결과로 이 직업에 맞는 옷차림을 한 것이오.\"\n\n\"무슨 그런 터무니없는 말씀을 하세요, 막시밀리앙!\"\n\n\"터무니없다니? 부디 내 평생 가장 현명한 행동이라 여기는 일을 그런 식으로 부르지 마시오. 생각해 보시오. 정원사가 됨으로써 나는 우리의 만남을 온갖 의심이나 위험에서 완벽하게 가려 주는 거요.\"\n\n\"제발 부탁이에요, 막시밀리앙, 농담은 그만하시고, 정말 무슨 말씀이신지 알려 주세요.\"\n\n\"간단하오. 내가 지금 서 있는 이 땅뙈기가 세를 놓는다는 걸 알아내고, 그걸 빌리겠다고 신청했더니, 주인이 선뜻 받아 주었고, 그래서 나는 이제 이 훌륭한 자주개자리 밭의 주인이 된 거요. 생각해 보시오, 발랑틴! 이제 내가 내 농장에 작은 오두막을 한 채 짓고, 당신에게서 스무 걸음도 떨어지지 않은 곳에 사는 것을 막을 것이 아무것도 없소. 그게 내게 얼마나 큰 행복을 안겨 줄지 한번 상상이나 해 보시오. 그 생각만으로도 나는 가슴이 벅차오르는 것을 가눌 수가 없소. 그런 더없는 기쁨은 그 무엇으로도 값을 매길 수 없는, 불가능하고 손에 닿지 않는 일처럼만 보였소. 그런데 내 목숨에서 십 년쯤은 기꺼이 내놓았을 이 모든 기쁨과 환희와 행복을, 한 해 500프랑이라는 적은 값으로, 그것도 분기마다 나누어 치르고 사들였다면 믿겠소? 이제부터 우리는 두려워할 게 아무것도 없소. 나는 내 땅 위에 있으니, 담에 사다리를 세우고 마음 내킬 때 너머를 들여다볼 분명한 권리가 있고, 수상한 자로 몰려 경찰에 끌려갈 걱정도 없소. 또한 당신이 그 좋아하는 정자에 들를 때마다, 내 다정하고 한결같으며 변치 않는 사랑을 당신에게 다짐할 귀한 특권도 누릴 수 있소. 작업복에 모자를 쓴 가난한 일꾼의 입에서 나오는 사랑 고백을 듣는 게 당신의 자존심을 상하게 하지만 않는다면 말이오.\"\n\n기쁨과 놀라움이 뒤섞인 가느다란 탄성이 발랑틴의 입술에서 새어 나왔다. 그러나 그녀는 거의 곧바로, 마치 어떤 질투 어린 구름이 그녀의 가슴을 밝히던 기쁨을 어둡게 가리기라도 한 듯, 서글픈 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"아, 아니에요, 막시밀리앙, 이건 안 돼요. 여러 가지 이유에서요. 우리는 우리 자신의 힘을 너무 믿어서는 안 돼요. 어쩌면 다른 사람들처럼, 서로의 신중함을 맹목적으로 믿다가 그릇된 길로 빠질지도 몰라요.\"\n\n\"어떻게 한순간이라도 그런 부당한 생각을 품을 수 있소, 사랑하는 발랑틴? 우리가 알게 된 그 축복받은 첫 순간부터, 나는 내 말과 행동을 모두 당신의 마음과 생각에 맞추어 다스려 오지 않았소? 그리고 당신도 분명 내 명예를 더없이 믿고 있을 거요. 당신이 어렴풋하고 막연한, 다가올 위험의 예감이 든다고 말했을 때, 나는 아무런 보상도 바라지 않고 그저 당신에게 도움이 될 수 있다는 기쁨만을 바라며, 맹목적이고 헌신적으로 당신을 위해 몸을 바쳤소. 그 뒤로 내가 단 한 번이라도, 말로든 눈빛으로든, 당신을 위해 기꺼이 목숨을 내놓았을 그 숱한 사내들 가운데 나를 골라 준 것을 후회하게 만든 적이 있었소? 사랑하는 발랑틴, 당신은 내게 데피네 씨와 약혼한 사이라고, 당신 아버지가 그 혼사를 끝내 성사시키기로 굳게 마음먹었다고, 그리고 한번 정한 결심을 바꾼 적이 없는 빌포르 씨인지라 그 뜻에는 거역할 길이 없다고 말했소. 나는 당신이 원하는 대로 뒤로 물러나, 당신 마음의 결정도, 내 마음의 결정도 아닌, 다만 섭리가 자비로이 우리를 위해 끼어들어 일을 우리에게 유리하게 이끌어 주기를 바라며 기다렸소. 하지만 발랑틴, 당신이 나를 사랑한다고, 나를 가엾이 여긴다고 고백해 준 이상, 내가 지체나 어려움 따위를 무엇 때문에 마음에 두겠소? 가끔이라도 그 고백을 되풀이해 준다면, 나는 무엇이든 견딜 수 있소.\"\n\n\"아, 막시밀리앙, 바로 그 점이 당신을 그토록 대담하게 만들고, 또 저를 동시에 그토록 행복하면서도 불행하게 만드는 거예요. 그래서 저는 자주 스스로에게 물어요. 새어머니의 모진 대접과 제 자식만 눈먼 듯 편애하는 꼴을 견디는 편이 나을지, 아니면 지금처럼 양쪽 모두에게 위험천만한 이 만남에서 얻는 것 말고는 어떤 기쁨에도 무감각해진 채 사는 편이 나을지요.\"\n\n\"그 말은 받아들일 수 없소.\" 젊은 남자가 대꾸했다. \"그건 잔인하면서도 부당한 말이오. 나만큼 고분고분한 노예를 또 찾을 수 있겠소? 발랑틴, 당신은 내게 가끔 당신과 이야기 나누는 것은 허락했지만, 산책길에서든 어디서든 당신을 따라다니는 것은 금했소. 내가 그 말을 따르지 않은 적이 있소? 그리고 이 울타리 안으로 들어와 이 철문 너머로 당신과 몇 마디 주고받을 방도를 찾아낸 뒤로도, 정작 당신을 제대로 보지도 못한 채 곁에 있을 뿐인데도, 내가 당신 옷자락 끝이라도 만지게 해 달라고 청한 적이 있소? 아니면 나 같은 젊음과 힘을 가진 자에게는 하찮기 그지없는 이 울타리를 넘으려 한 적이 있소? 단 한 번도 불평이나 투정을 입에 담은 적이 없소. 나는 옛 기사들 못지않게 엄격히 내 약속에 매여 있었소. 자, 자, 사랑하는 발랑틴, 내 말이 옳다고 인정해 주시오. 그러지 않으면 나는 당신을 부당한 사람이라 부르고 싶어질 테니.\"\n\n\"맞아요.\" 발랑틴이 가느다란 손가락 끝을 널빤지의 작은 틈으로 밀어 넣어, 막시밀리앙이 거기에 입술을 갖다 대도록 허락하며 말했다. \"당신은 진실하고 한결같은 벗이에요. 하지만 사랑하는 막시밀리앙, 당신은 어쨌든 자기 잇속을 따져 그렇게 행동한 거예요. 당신이 그와 반대되는 마음을 드러내는 순간 우리 사이는 모두 끝이라는 걸 잘 알고 있었으니까요. 당신은 제게 오빠 같은 다정한 애정을 베풀겠다고 약속했어요. 이 세상에 제 벗이라곤 당신뿐이니까요. 아버지에게는 등한시되고 잊힌 채, 새어머니에게는 들볶이고 박해받으며, 말 못 하고 몸이 마비된 노인 한 분만을 유일한 길벗으로 둔 저예요. 그 시든 손은 더 이상 제 손을 잡아 주지도 못하고, 오직 눈으로만 제게 말을 건넬 수 있을 뿐이지요. 그래도 그분 마음속에는 아직 가엾은 손녀를 향한 더없이 따뜻한 정이 남아 있어요. 아, 제 운명은 얼마나 쓰라린지요. 저보다 힘센 모든 이에게 제물이 되거나 적이 되어야 하는데, 제 유일한 벗이자 의지가 되는 분은 살아 있는 시체나 다름없으니! 정말로, 정말로, 막시밀리앙, 저는 너무나 비참해요. 만약 당신이 저를 사랑한다면, 그건 가엾이 여기는 마음에서일 거예요.\"\n\n\"발랑틴,\" 깊이 감동한 젊은 남자가 대답했다. \"당신이 이 세상에서 내가 사랑하는 전부라고는 말하지 않겠소. 나는 내 누이와 매형을 무척 아끼니까. 하지만 그들을 향한 내 애정은 잔잔하고 평온해서, 당신에게 느끼는 것과는 도무지 닮은 데가 없소. 당신을 떠올리면 내 심장은 빠르게 뛰고, 피가 핏줄 속에서 타오르며, 숨조차 제대로 쉴 수가 없소. 하지만 나는 엄숙히 약속하겠소. 당신 자신이 나더러 이 모든 것을 당신을 돕거나 섬기는 데 쓰라고 요구할 그날까지, 이 모든 열정과, 이 뜨겁고 격렬한 감정을 억누르겠다고 말이오. 듣자 하니 데피네 씨는 앞으로 일 년은 지나야 돌아온다더군. 그동안 뜻밖의 좋은 기회들이 우리 편을 들어 줄지도 모르오. 그러니 우리 잘되기를 바랍시다. 희망이란 참으로 달콤한 위안이니까. 그런데 발랑틴, 당신은 나를 이기적이라 나무라면서도, 정작 당신이 내게 어떤 존재였는지 한번 생각해 보시오. 아름답지만 차가운, 대리석 비너스를 빼닮은 모습이었소. 내가 그토록 순종하고 복종해 보였건만, 당신은 내게 어떤 보답의 약속을 해 주었소? 아무것도 없었소. 내게 무엇을 허락해 주었소? 그조차 거의 없었소. 당신은 약혼자인 데피네 씨 이야기를 하면서, 그의 아내가 된다는 생각에 몸서리를 치오. 하지만 말해 보시오, 발랑틴, 당신 마음속에 다른 슬픔은 없소? 당신은 내가 몸도 마음도 당신에게 바친 것을 보고 있소. 내 목숨과, 내 심장을 도는 따뜻한 핏방울 하나하나가 당신을 섬기는 데 바쳐졌소. 당신은 내 존재가 당신의 존재에 묶여 있음을, 만약 당신을 잃는다면 그 짓누르는 비참함의 순간을 넘기고서는 살아남지 못하리라는 것을 너무도 잘 알고 있소. 그런데도 당신은 다른 사람의 아내가 될 거라는 앞날을 침착하게 입에 담는구려! 아, 발랑틴, 내가 당신 처지였다면, 그리고 당신처럼 내 사랑 같은 사랑으로 흠모받고 떠받들어지고 있음을 느꼈다면, 적어도 백 번은 이 철창 사이로 손을 내밀어 이렇게 말했을 거요. '이 손을 잡아요, 사랑하는 막시밀리앙. 그리고 믿어요, 살아서든 죽어서든 나는 당신의 것, 오직 당신만의 것, 영원히 당신의 것이라는 걸!'\"\n\n가엾은 처녀는 아무 대답도 하지 못했지만, 연인은 그녀의 흐느낌과 눈물 소리를 또렷이 들을 수 있었다. 젊은 남자의 마음에 급격한 변화가 일었다.\n\n\"사랑하는, 사랑하는 발랑틴,\" 그가 외쳤다. \"내가 당신의 기분을 상하게 했다면 용서해 주시오. 내 말이 본의 아니게 당신을 아프게 했다면 그 말은 잊어버려요.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 막시밀리앙, 기분이 상한 게 아니에요.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"하지만 제가 얼마나 가엾고 무력한 존재인지 모르세요? 아버지 집에서조차 거의 남이나 다름없고 버림받은 신세인걸요. 그 집에선 아버지마저 좀처럼 얼굴을 볼 수 없어요. 열 살 때부터 제 위에 그토록 모질게 드리워진 쇠막대 아래서 제 뜻은 꺾이고 기개는 부러져 버렸어요. 날마다, 시간마다, 분마다, 순간마다 억눌리고 모욕당하고 박해받아 왔지만, 누구 하나 제 고통을 보살펴 주기는커녕 눈여겨봐 준 적도 없어요. 그리고 저는 이 일을 두고 당신 말고는 누구에게도 단 한마디 입 밖에 낸 적이 없어요. 겉으로 보기에, 또 세상 사람들 눈에는, 제가 온갖 친절과 애정에 둘러싸여 있는 것 같겠지요. 하지만 실상은 정반대예요. 사람들은 흔히 이렇게 말해요. '아, 빌포르 씨처럼 그토록 엄격한 성품을 지닌 분이, 어떤 아버지들이 딸에게 쏟는 그런 다정함을 베풀기를 바랄 수야 없지. 어린 나이에 친어머니를 잃긴 했어도, 빌포르 부인이라는 새어머니를 얻는 행복을 누렸으니 다행이지 뭐.' 하지만 세상은 잘못 알고 있어요. 아버지는 철저한 무관심으로 저를 버려두었고, 새어머니는 저를 미워해요. 그것도 끊임없는 미소 뒤에 가려져 있어서 더욱더 무서운 증오로요.\"\n\n\"당신을 미워한다니, 사랑스러운 발랑틴,\" 젊은 남자가 외쳤다. \"어떻게 누가 그럴 수 있단 말이오?\"\n\n\"아아,\" 울고 있던 처녀가 대답했다. \"인정할 수밖에 없네요. 새어머니가 저를 싫어하는 건 아주 자연스러운 데서 비롯된 거예요. 바로 제 자식, 그러니까 제 동생 에두아르를 향한 지나친 사랑 말이에요.\"\n\n\"그렇다고 왜 그래야 하오?\"\n\n\"저도 몰라요. 하지만 지금 우리 이야기에 돈 문제를 끌어들이고 싶지는 않지만, 이 정도만은 말해 둘게요. 저를 향한 그 극심한 미움은 거기서 비롯된 거예요. 새어머니는, 제가 어머니에게서 물려받은 권리로 누리는 재산을 시샘하는 것 같아요. 게다가 그 재산은 생메랑 후작 부부께서 돌아가시면 갑절이 넘게 늘어날 텐데, 그 두 분의 유일한 상속녀가 바로 저거든요. 빌포르 부인은 자기 재산이라곤 한 푼도 없어서, 제가 그토록 풍족하게 물려받았다는 이유로 저를 미워하는 거예요. 아아, 적어도 아버지의 사랑이라도 나눠 가지는 행복을 얻을 수만 있다면, 저는 이 재산의 절반쯤은 기꺼이 내놓을 텐데요. 하늘이 아시지만, 행복하고 다정한 가정을 얻을 수만 있다면 전부라도 내던지고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"가엾은 발랑틴!\"\n\n\"저는 마치 속박의 삶을 사는 것 같아요. 그러면서도 동시에 제 자신의 나약함을 너무도 잘 알고 있어서, 지금 매여 있는 이 굴레를 깨뜨렸다가 완전히 무너져 버릴까 봐 두려워요. 게다가 아버지는 그 명령을 거역하고도 무사할 수 있는 분이 아니에요. 높은 지위와, 재능과 흔들림 없는 청렴함으로 굳건히 다져진 명성에 둘러싸여 있어서, 아무도 그분을 거스를 수 없어요. 아버지는 왕 앞에서도 무소불위의 힘을 지녔어요. 말 한마디로 당신을 짓밟아 버릴 거예요. 사랑하는 막시밀리앙, 제 말을 믿어 주세요. 제가 아버지의 명령에 맞서려 들지 않는 건, 저 자신보다도 당신을 위해서랍니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 발랑틴, 어째서 당신은 자꾸 최악을 미리 점치는 거요? 어째서 그토록 암울한 앞날을 그리는 거요?\"\n\n\"지나간 일로 미루어 판단하니까요.\"\n\n\"그래도 생각해 보시오. 비록 내가 엄밀히 말해 당신에게 걸맞은 빛나는 혼처는 아닐지 몰라도, 여러 이유로 당신과 맺어지기에 그리 못 미치는 사람은 아니오. 그런 신분 차이를 그토록 까다롭게 따지고 저울질하던 시절은 프랑스에서 이제 사라졌소. 왕정의 으뜸가는 가문들이 제국의 가문들과 혼인을 맺어 왔소. 창의 귀족이 대포의 귀족과 손을 잡은 거요. 그런데 나는 바로 그 후자에 속하오. 그리고 분명히, 군에서 승진할 내 앞날은 더없이 밝고 확실하오. 내 재산은, 비록 많지는 않아도, 빚 한 푼 없이 온전한 내 것이고, 돌아가신 내 아버지에 대한 기억은, 발랑틴, 우리 고장에서 그 도시의 가장 정직하고 명예로운 상인으로 존경받고 있소. 우리 고장이라 말한 건, 당신도 마르세유에서 그리 멀지 않은 곳에서 태어났기 때문이오.\"\n\n\"마르세유 이야기는 하지 말아 주세요, 부탁이에요, 막시밀리앙. 그 한마디가 제 어머니를 기억 속에 되살려 놓아요. 천사 같은 제 어머니, 저에게도, 또 어머니를 아는 모든 이에게도 너무 일찍 세상을 떠나 버린 분. 하지만 이 세상에서 허락된 그 짧은 동안 당신의 아이를 지켜봐 주신 뒤로, 지금은, 제 간절한 바람이지만, 하늘의 거처에서 저를 지켜보고 계실 거예요. 아, 제 어머니가 아직 살아 계셨다면, 막시밀리앙, 두려울 게 아무것도 없었을 거예요. 어머니께 당신을 사랑한다고 말씀드렸을 테고, 어머니가 우리를 지켜 주셨을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그게 두렵소, 발랑틴.\" 연인이 대답했다. \"어머니가 살아 계셨다면 나는 당신을 알게 되는 행복을 결코 누리지 못했을 거요. 그랬다면 당신은 너무도 행복해서, 그 고귀한 자리에서 몸을 굽혀 나 같은 사람에게 생각 한 자락 베풀어 줄 일이 없었을 테니까.\"\n\n\"이번엔 당신이 부당한 사람이군요, 막시밀리앙.\" 발랑틴이 외쳤다. \"하지만 한 가지 알고 싶은 게 있어요.\"\n\n\"그게 뭐요?\" 젊은 남자가 발랑틴이 머뭇거리는 것을 알아채고 물었다.\n\n\"솔직히 말해 주세요, 막시밀리앙. 지난날 우리 아버지들이 마르세유에 살던 시절, 두 분 사이에 무슨 불화 같은 게 있었나요?\"\n\n\"내가 아는 한은 없소.\" 젊은 남자가 대답했다. \"다만, 두 분이 서로 다른 당파에 속해 있던 데서 어떤 앙금이 생겼을 수는 있겠소. 당신 아버지는 알다시피 부르봉 왕가의 열렬한 지지자였고, 내 아버지는 황제께 온전히 헌신한 분이었으니. 그 밖에 두 분 사이에 다른 차이가 있었을 리는 없소. 그런데 왜 묻는 거요?\"\n\n\"말씀드릴게요.\" 처녀가 대답했다. \"당신이 알아 두는 게 마땅하니까요. 그러니까, 당신이 레지옹 도뇌르 훈장의 오피시에로 임명되었다는 소식이 신문에 실린 날이었어요. 우리는 모두 할아버지 누아르티에 씨와 함께 앉아 있었지요. 당글라르 씨도 거기 있었어요. 막시밀리앙, 당글라르 씨를 기억하지요? 그 은행가 말이에요. 그 사람 말들이 날뛰는 바람에 새어머니와 어린 동생이 거의 목숨을 잃을 뻔했잖아요. 다른 사람들이 당글라르 양의 다가오는 결혼 이야기를 하는 동안, 저는 할아버지께 신문을 읽어 드리고 있었어요. 그런데 당신에 관한 대목에 이르렀을 때, 저는 아침 내내 그 부분을 혼자 읽고 또 읽기만 했으면서도 (당신이 그 전날 저녁에 다 이야기해 주었던 거 알지요), 그 많은 사람 앞에서 당신 이름을 소리 내어 말한다는 생각에 어찌나 행복하면서도 떨리던지, 정말이지 그 대목을 그냥 건너뛰어 버렸을 거예요. 그러다 제가 입을 다물면 그 까닭을 두고 의심을 살까 두려워서, 저는 온 용기를 그러모아, 할 수 있는 한 또박또박 침착하게 읽었어요.\"\n\n\"사랑하는 발랑틴!\"\n\n\"그런데, 믿어지세요? 아버지는 당신 이름을 듣자마자 무척 황급히 고개를 돌리셨어요. 그리고 저는, 어리석은 바보처럼, 당신 이름을 입에 올리면 다들 저만큼이나 마음이 흔들릴 거라고 굳게 믿고 있었던 터라, 아버지가 흠칫 놀라며 거의 떠는 모습을 보고도 놀라지 않았어요. 하지만 (그건 분명 제 착각이었겠지만) 당글라르 씨까지 떠는 것 같았어요.\"\n\n\"'모렐, 모렐,' 아버지가 외쳤어요. '잠깐.' 그러더니 미간을 깊이 찌푸리며 덧붙이셨어요. '설마 이게 마르세유에 살면서 그 격렬한 보나파르트주의로 우리에게 그토록 골치를 썩이던 그 모렐 가문 사람은 아니겠지. 1815년 무렵 말이야.'\n\n\"'네,' 당글라르 씨가 대답했어요. '제 생각엔 그 늙은 선주의 아들인 듯합니다.'\"\n\n\"그래서,\" 막시밀리앙이 대꾸했다. \"그다음 당신 아버지는 뭐라고 하셨소, 발랑틴?\"\n\n\"아, 어찌나 끔찍한 말씀이던지, 차마 말씀드리기가 두려워요.\"\n\n\"내겐 늘 무엇이든 다 말해 주시오.\" 막시밀리앙이 미소를 띠며 말했다.\n\n\"'아,' 아버지가 여전히 얼굴을 찌푸린 채 말을 이으셨어요. '그자들이 떠받들던 황제가 그 미치광이들을 마땅히 받아야 할 대로 다루어 주었지. 그들을 '대포밥'이라 불렀으니, 그게 바로 그자들이 쓸모 있는 전부였어. 그리고 나는 지금 정부가 그 유익한 원칙을 본래의 기개 그대로 받아들인 것을 보니 더없이 기쁘구나. 알제리가 그토록 훌륭한 생각을 실행에 옮길 수단을 대는 것 말고는 아무 쓸모가 없다 해도, 그것만으로도 기를 쓰고 손에 넣을 가치가 충분한 땅이지. 프랑스가 그 미개한 나라에서 제 권리를 주장하는 데 적잖은 비용이 드는 건 사실이다만.'\"\n\n\"솔직히 말해 야만스러운 정치관이오.\" 막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"하지만 사랑하는 발랑틴, 당신 아버지가 한 말에 너무 깊이 마음 두지 마시오. 그런 식의 말이라면 내 아버지도 당신 아버지에 조금도 뒤지지 않았으니까. 아버지는 이러셨소. '전쟁 기술을 개선할 영리하고 효율적인 방법을 그토록 많이 고안해 낸 황제께서, 어째서 변호사며 판사며 법률가들로 연대를 하나 꾸려, 적이 퍼부을 수 있는 가장 거센 포화 속으로 들여보내, 그자들을 더 나은 사람들을 살리는 데 써먹지 않으시는 거지?' 보다시피, 사랑하는 발랑틴, 그림처럼 생생한 표현이나 통 큰 기개로 따지자면 어느 당파의 말이든 별반 고를 게 없소. 그런데 검사장의 그런 열변에 당글라르 씨는 뭐라 했소?\"\n\n\"아, 그 사람은 웃었어요. 그것도 그 사람 특유의 묘한 방식으로요. 반쯤은 악의적이고 반쯤은 사나운 그런 웃음 말이에요. 그러더니 거의 곧바로 일어나 작별을 고했지요. 그제야 처음으로, 저는 할아버지가 동요하시는 걸 알아챘어요. 그리고 막시밀리앙, 말해 두지만, 그 마비된 몸에서 감정을 읽어 낼 수 있는 사람은 저뿐이에요. 저는 그분 앞에서 오간 대화가 (사람들은 늘 그 정다운 노인의 심정 따위는 조금도 헤아리지 않고, 그분 앞에서 하고 싶은 말과 행동을 다 하거든요) 그분 마음에 강한 인상을 남겼다고 짐작했어요. 그도 그럴 것이, 그토록 헌신적으로 사랑하고 섬긴 황제가 그렇게 깎아내려지는 말을 듣는 건 분명 그분께 고통스러운 일이었을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"누아르티에 씨라는 이름은,\" 막시밀리앙이 끼어들었다. \"유럽 전역에 널리 알려져 있소. 그분은 높은 명망을 지닌 정치가였소. 그리고 당신이 아는지 모르는지 모르겠지만, 발랑틴, 그분은 부르봉 왕가가 복위한 동안 일어난 모든 보나파르트파 음모에서 주도적인 역할을 하셨소.\"\n\n\"아, 저도 더없이 기이하게 들리는 일들을 종종 수군대는 소리로 들었어요. 아버지는 보나파르트파, 아들은 왕당파라니. 당파와 정치 성향이 그토록 유별나게 갈린 까닭이 대체 무엇이었을까요? 그건 그렇고, 다시 제 이야기로 돌아갈게요. 저는 마치 할아버지께 동요하시는 까닭을 여쭙기라도 하려는 듯 그분 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸어요. 할아버지는 제가 읽고 있던 신문을 의미심장하게 바라보셨어요. '왜 그러세요, 할아버지?' 제가 여쭈었지요. '기쁘세요?' 할아버지가 그렇다는 신호를 보내셨어요. '방금 아버지가 하신 말씀에요?' 그러자 아니라는 신호를 보내셨어요. '그럼 혹시 당글라르 씨가 한 말이 마음에 드셨어요?' 또 아니라는 신호였어요. '아, 그럼 모렐 씨가 (저는 차마 막시밀리앙이라고는 말하지 못했어요) 레지옹 도뇌르 훈장의 오피시에가 되었다는 소식이 기쁘셨군요?' 할아버지가 그렇다는 뜻을 보이셨어요. 생각해 보세요, 그 가엾은 노인이, 당신처럼 그분께는 생판 남인 사람이 레지옹 도뇌르 훈장의 오피시에가 되었다는 것을 그토록 기뻐하시다니요! 어쩌면 그저 변덕이었는지도 몰라요. 사람들 말로는 그분이 다시 어린아이처럼 되어 가신다니까요. 하지만 저는 그분이 당신에게 그토록 관심을 보여 주신 게 사랑스러워요.\"\n\n\"참 묘한 일이오.\" 막시밀리앙이 중얼거렸다. \"당신 아버지는 나를 미워하는데, 반대로 당신 할아버지는…… 정치가 불러일으키는 감정이란 참으로 야릇하구려.\"\n\n\"쉿,\" 발랑틴이 별안간 외쳤다. \"누가 와요!\" 막시밀리앙은 한달음에 제 자주개자리 밭으로 뛰어들더니, 잡초를 뽑는 척하며 더없이 사정없이 그것들을 뽑아 대기 시작했다.\n\n\"아가씨, 아가씨!\" 나무들 뒤편에서 한 목소리가 외쳤다. \"마님께서 사방으로 아가씨를 찾고 계세요. 응접실에 손님이 와 계시거든요.\"\n\n\"손님이?\" 발랑틴이 몹시 동요하며 물었다. \"누구신데?\"\n\n\"어느 대단한 분이세요. 왕자님이라던가 그러던데요. 몬테크리스토 백작이라는 분이세요.\"\n\n\"곧 갈게요.\" 발랑틴이 큰 소리로 외쳤다.\n\n몬테크리스토라는 이름은 철문 건너편의 젊은 남자에게 전기 충격 같은 것을 흘려보냈다. 그에게 발랑틴의 \"곧 갈게요\"라는 말은 으레 작별을 알리는 신호였다.\n\n\"자, 그렇다면,\" 막시밀리앙이 삽자루에 몸을 기대며 말했다. \"몬테크리스토 백작이 빌포르 씨와 어떻게 아는 사이가 됐는지, 그걸 알아낼 수만 있다면 무엇이든 톡톡히 내놓겠는데.\"\n\n# Chapter 52. Toxicology\n\n바로 그 몬테크리스토 백작이 검사장의 답방을 하기 위해 빌포르 부인의 집에 막 도착한 것이었다. 그리고 쉽게 짐작할 수 있듯이, 그의 이름이 전해지자 온 집안이 술렁였다.\n\n빌포르 검사장의 답방을 위해 빌포르 부인의 집을 막 찾아온 사람은 다름 아닌 몬테크리스토 백작이었다. 능히 짐작할 수 있듯, 그의 이름이 전해지자 온 집안이 술렁였다.\n백작의 방문이 알려졌을 때 응접실에 혼자 있던 빌포르 부인은, 백작에게 다시 한번 감사 인사를 올리도록 아들을 당장 데려오게 했다. 에두아르는 이 대단한 인물에 관한 이야기를 꼬박 이틀 동안 들어 온 터라 부리나케 달려왔다. 어머니의 분부를 따라서도 아니고 백작에게 고마운 마음이 있어서도 아니었다. 그저 호기심 때문이었고, 어쩌다 한마디 던질 기회가 생기면 무례한 말을 내뱉어 어머니로 하여금 이렇게 말하게 하려는 속셈이었다.\n\n\"오, 저런 짓궂은 녀석! 하지만 저 애한테는 엄하게 굴 수가 없어요. 정말 어찌나 영리한지.\"\n\n여느 인사치레가 오간 뒤, 백작은 빌포르 씨의 안부를 물었다.\n\n\"제 남편은 법무대신과 만찬을 하러 갔답니다.\" 젊은 부인이 대답했다. \"방금 나갔어요. 떠나기 전에 백작님을 뵙는 기쁨을 누리지 못해 무척 안타까워할 거예요.\"\n\n백작이 도착했을 때 응접실에 있던 두 손님은 눈이 빠져라 그를 바라보더니, 예의가 허락하고 호기심이 요구하는 만큼의 적당한 시간이 흐른 뒤 물러갔다.\n\n\"네 누나 발랑틴은 뭘 하고 있니?\" 빌포르 부인이 에두아르에게 물었다. \"누구를 시켜서 이리로 오라고 일러라. 백작님께 소개하는 영광을 누리고 싶구나.\"\n\n\"그럼 따님이 계신 겁니까, 부인?\" 백작이 물었다. \"아주 어린 분이겠지요?\"\n\n\"빌포르 씨가 첫 결혼에서 얻은 딸이에요.\" 젊은 아내가 대답했다. \"다 자란 예쁜 아이지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 우울하죠.\" 에두아르가 끼어들며, 도금한 횃대 위에서 꽥꽥거리는 화려한 앵무새의 꽁지 깃털을 뽑아 제 모자에 꽂을 깃 장식을 만들었다.\n\n빌포르 부인은 그저 \"가만있어, 에두아르!\" 하고 외쳤다. 그러고는 덧붙였다. \"그래도 이 어린 말썽꾸러기가 거의 맞는 말을 했답니다. 제가 가슴 아파하며 백 번도 더 한 말을 그대로 따라 하는 것뿐이에요. 우리가 아무리 기운을 북돋우려 해도, 빌포르 양은 천성이 우울하고 말수가 적어서, 그게 자주 그 아이의 아름다움을 가린답니다. 그런데 왜 안 오는 걸까? 가 봐라, 에두아르, 가서 봐.\"\n\n\"누나가 없는 데서 찾고 있으니까 그렇죠.\"\n\n\"그럼 어디서 찾고 있는데?\"\n\n\"노르티에 할아버지 방에서요.\"\n\n\"누나가 거기 없다는 거니?\"\n\n\"아니, 아니, 아니, 아니, 아니, 없어요.\" 에두아르가 노래하듯 말꼬리를 늘이며 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 어디 있니? 알면 왜 말을 안 해?\"\n\n\"커다란 밤나무 아래 있어요.\" 응석받이 녀석이 대답하며, 어머니의 명령에도 아랑곳없이 산 파리들을 앵무새에게 주었다. 앵무새는 그런 먹이를 무척 좋아하는 듯했다.\n\n빌포르 부인이 손을 뻗어 종을 울리려 했다. 하녀에게 발랑틴이 있을 곳을 일러 데려오게 할 참이었다. 그때 발랑틴이 직접 응접실로 들어왔다. 그녀는 몹시 풀이 죽어 보였다. 그녀를 유심히 살펴보는 사람이라면 누구든 그 눈가에 방금 흘린 눈물 자국이 남아 있는 것을 알아챘을 터였다.\n\n발랑틴은 우리가 빠른 서사의 흐름 속에서 정식으로 소개하지 못한 채 독자들에게 내보인 인물이다. 그녀는 키가 크고 우아한 열아홉 살 처녀로, 밝은 밤색 머리에 깊은 푸른 눈을 지녔으며, 어머니에게서 물려받은 차분하고 고요한 기품이 감돌았다. 희고 가느다란 손가락, 진주 같은 목덜미, 색조가 변하는 두 뺨은, 그 자태가 백조의 우아함에 곧잘 시적으로 비유되곤 하던 사랑스러운 영국 여인들을 떠올리게 했다.\n\n그녀는 응접실로 들어와, 이미 그토록 많은 이야기를 들은 낯선 손님이 계모 곁에 있는 것을 보고 처녀다운 어색함도 없이, 심지어 눈을 내리깔지도 않은 채 그에게 인사했다. 그 우아함이 백작의 주의를 한층 끌었다.\n\n그는 인사에 답하려고 자리에서 일어섰다.\n\n\"제 의붓딸 빌포르 양이에요.\" 빌포르 부인이 소파에 등을 기댄 채 손으로 발랑틴 쪽을 가리키며 몬테크리스토에게 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 이쪽은 몬테크리스토 백작님, 중국의 왕이자 코친차이나의 황제.\" 어린 악동이 누나 쪽을 슬쩍 곁눈질하며 말했다.\n\n이 말에 빌포르 부인은 정말로 새파랗게 질렸고, 에두아르라는 이름으로 불리는 이 집안의 골칫거리에게 막 화를 낼 뻔했다. 하지만 백작은 도리어 미소를 지으며 만족스러운 눈으로 아이를 바라보는 듯했고, 그 모습에 어머니의 마음은 다시금 기쁨과 감격으로 뛰었다.\n\n\"그런데, 부인.\" 백작이 대화를 이어 가며 빌포르 부인과 발랑틴을 번갈아 바라보고 말했다. \"제가 전에 부인과 따님을 뵙는 영광을 누린 적이 있지 않습니까? 조금 전부터 그런 생각이 자꾸 들더군요. 문득 그런 느낌이 떠올랐고, 따님이 들어오시는 순간 그 모습이 어렴풋한 기억 위에 한 줄기 빛을 더 비춰 주었습니다. 무례를 용서하십시오.\"\n\n\"그럴 리는 없을 것 같은데요. 빌포르 양은 사교를 그리 좋아하지 않고, 저희도 좀처럼 외출을 하지 않거든요.\" 젊은 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 제가 따님이나 부인을, 또 이 귀엽고 명랑한 꼬마 도련님을 뵌 것은 사교 모임에서는 아니었군요. 게다가 파리의 사교계는 제게 전혀 낯선 곳입니다. 말씀드렸다시피, 저는 파리에 온 지 며칠 되지 않았으니까요. 아니, 하지만, 어쩌면 떠올려 보아도 괜찮으시다면 — 잠깐만요!\"\n\n백작은 생각을 가다듬으려는 듯 이마에 손을 얹었다.\n\n\"아닙니다 — 어딘가였어요 — 여기서 멀리 떨어진 — 그게 — 잘 모르겠지만 — 그 기억은 아름다운 하늘과 어떤 종교 축제와 이어져 있는 것 같습니다. 따님은 손에 꽃을 들고 계셨고, 이 흥미로운 도련님은 정원에서 아름다운 공작새를 쫓고 있었지요. 그리고 부인, 당신은 어느 정자의 격자 시렁 아래 계셨습니다. 부디 좀 거들어 주십시오, 부인. 이런 정황들이 기억나지 않으십니까?\"\n\n\"아니요, 정말로요.\" 빌포르 부인이 대답했다. \"그런데도 제 생각엔, 만약 어디서든 백작님을 뵈었다면, 그 기억이 제 머릿속에 새겨져 있었을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"백작님이 이탈리아에서 저희를 보셨는지도 몰라요.\" 발랑틴이 수줍게 말했다.\n\n\"그래요, 이탈리아입니다. 아마도 이탈리아였을 겁니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"그럼 따님은 이탈리아를 여행하셨군요?\"\n\n\"네, 어머니와 저는 이 년 전에 그곳에 있었어요. 제 폐를 염려한 의사들이 나폴리의 공기를 권했거든요. 저희는 볼로냐와 페루자, 로마를 거쳐 갔어요.\"\n\n\"아, 그래요 — 맞습니다, 따님.\" 몬테크리스토가, 마치 이 간단한 설명만으로 찾던 기억이 되살아나기에 충분하다는 듯 외쳤다. \"성체 축일에 페루자, 우편 호텔 정원에서, 우연이 우리를 만나게 했지요. 부인, 당신과 아드님 말입니다. 이제 당신을 뵙는 영광을 누렸던 게 떠오릅니다.\"\n\n\"페루자는 똑똑히 기억해요. 우편 호텔도, 말씀하신 그 축제도요.\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"하지만 아무리 기억을 더듬어 봐도 — 제 기억력이 이렇게 변변치 못한 게 부끄럽지만 — 전에 백작님을 뵙는 기쁨을 누린 적은 정말이지 떠오르지 않네요.\"\n\n\"이상하네요. 저도 백작님을 뵌 기억이 없어요.\" 발랑틴이 아름다운 두 눈을 들어 백작을 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 저는 똑똑히 기억해요.\" 사랑스러운 에두아르가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"제가 기억을 도와드리지요, 부인.\" 백작이 말을 이었다. \"그날은 타는 듯이 더웠습니다. 부인은 말을 기다리고 계셨는데, 축제 탓에 말이 늦어지고 있었지요. 따님은 정원 그늘에서 거닐고 있었고, 아드님은 공작새를 쫓아 사라졌습니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 내가 그놈을 잡았잖아요, 엄마, 기억 안 나요?\" 에두아르가 끼어들었다. \"그래서 그 예쁜 깃털을 세 개나 꽁지에서 뽑았다고요.\"\n\n\"부인, 당신은 정자 아래 남아 계셨습니다. 기억나지 않으십니까? 돌 벤치에 앉아 계셨고, 방금 말씀드린 대로 빌포르 양과 어린 아드님이 자리를 비운 사이, 당신은 누군가와 꽤 오랫동안 이야기를 나누셨지요.\"\n\n\"네, 정말, 그래요.\" 젊은 부인이 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했다. \"긴 모직 망토를 두른 사람과 이야기했던 게 기억나요. 제 생각엔 의사였던 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다, 부인. 그 사람이 저였습니다. 저는 그 호텔에 보름쯤 묵었는데, 그동안 제 시종의 열병을 고쳐 주고 주인의 황달도 낫게 해 주어, 정말이지 솜씨 좋은 의사라는 평판을 얻었지요. 우리는 여러 가지 화제로 오랫동안 이야기를 나누었습니다, 부인. 페루지노며, 라파엘로며, 풍속과 관습이며, 그 유명한 아쿠아 토파나며 말이지요. 부인은, 페루자의 어떤 사람들이 그 비법을 간직하고 있다는 말을 들으셨다고 하셨던 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"네, 맞아요.\" 빌포르 부인이 다소 불안한 기색으로 대답했다. \"이제 기억나네요.\"\n\n\"우리가 나눈 그 여러 화제를 지금 다 떠올리지는 못합니다, 부인.\" 백작이 더없이 침착하게 말을 이었다. \"하지만, 남들이 저를 두고 품었던 오해에 부인도 빠지셔서, 빌포르 양의 건강에 관해 제게 상담하셨던 건 똑똑히 기억합니다.\"\n\n\"네, 정말, 백작님은 사실상 의사셨던 거죠.\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"병자들을 고치셨으니까요.\"\n\n\"몰리에르나 보마르셰라면 이렇게 대답하겠지요, 부인. 바로 제가 의사가 아니었기에 환자들을 고친 거라고요. 저로서는, 화학과 자연과학을 다소 깊이 공부했다는 정도로 말씀드리는 걸로 만족하겠습니다. 그래도 어디까지나 아마추어로서요, 아시겠지요.\"\n\n이때 시계가 여섯 시를 알렸다.\n\n\"여섯 시네요.\" 빌포르 부인이 눈에 띄게 동요하며 말했다. \"발랑틴, 가서 할아버지가 식사를 하실지 좀 보고 오겠니?\"\n\n발랑틴이 일어나 백작에게 인사하고는 아무 말 없이 응접실을 나갔다.\n\n\"오, 부인.\" 발랑틴이 방을 나가자 백작이 말했다. \"혹시 저 때문에 빌포르 양을 내보내신 건가요?\"\n\n\"천만에요.\" 젊은 부인이 얼른 대답했다. \"지금이 노르티에 씨께 그분의 가련한 목숨을 이어 주는, 달갑지 않은 식사를 드리는 시간이거든요. 백작님도 제 시아버님의 딱한 처지를 아시지요?\"\n\n\"네, 부인. 빌포르 씨가 제게 말씀하셨습니다 — 마비, 였던 것 같은데요.\"\n\n\"아, 그래요. 가엾은 노인은 완전히 손쓸 수 없는 상태랍니다. 이 인간이라는 기계에서 이제 정신만이 여전히 살아 있는데, 그마저도 꺼져 가는 등불처럼 희미하게 깜박이지요. 하지만 집안의 불행을 늘어놓아 죄송해요, 백작님. 백작님이 솜씨 좋은 화학자라고 말씀하시던 참에 제가 끊어 버렸군요.\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 부인. 그렇게까지 말하지는 않았습니다.\" 백작이 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"오히려 그 반대지요. 제가 화학을 공부한 건, 동방에서 살기로 마음먹은 뒤 미트리다테스 왕의 본을 따르고 싶었기 때문입니다.\"\n\n\"미트리다테스, 폰토스의 왕.\" 어린 장난꾸러기가, 멋진 화첩에서 아름다운 초상화 몇 장을 찢어 내며 말했다. \"매일 아침 식사 때 독이 든 잔에 크림을 타서 마셨다는 그 사람이지.\"\n\n\"에두아르, 이 못된 녀석.\" 빌포르 부인이 망가진 책을 개구쟁이의 손에서 낚아채며 외쳤다. \"정말이지 더는 못 참겠구나. 너 때문에 대화가 다 끊기잖니. 가, 우리를 두고 가서, 노르티에 할아버지 방에서 누나 발랑틴이랑 같이 있어.\"\n\n\"화첩.\" 에두아르가 뾰로통하게 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 소리야 — 화첩이라니!\"\n\n\"화첩 줘요.\"\n\n\"감히 그림을 찢어 놓고선?\"\n\n\"아, 재밌으니까요.\"\n\n\"가 — 당장 가.\"\n\n\"화첩 안 주면 안 갈 거예요.\" 아이가, 한 번도 물러서지 않는 제 버릇대로 고집스레 안락의자에 주저앉으며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 가져가, 그리고 제발 더는 방해하지 마.\" 빌포르 부인이 에두아르에게 화첩을 건네며 말했다. 그러자 아이는 어머니의 손에 이끌려 문 쪽으로 갔다. 백작이 눈으로 그녀를 좇았다.\n\n\"저 여자가 아이를 내보낸 뒤 문을 닫는지 보자.\" 그가 중얼거렸다.\n\n빌포르 부인은 아이를 내보낸 뒤 조심스레 문을 닫았다. 백작은 그것을 눈치채지 못한 척했다. 그러고 나서 젊은 아내는 방 안을 살피는 눈길로 한 바퀴 둘러보고는 제 의자로 돌아와 앉았다.\n\n\"한마디 드려도 될까요, 부인.\" 백작이 그토록 능숙하게 지어낼 줄 아는 그 다정한 어조로 말했다. \"저 사랑스럽고 영리한 아이에게 정말이지 무척 엄하시군요.\"\n\n\"오, 때로는 엄격함도 꼭 필요한 법이지요.\" 빌포르 부인이 어머니다운 진정한 단호함으로 대답했다.\n\n\"에두아르 도련님이 미트리다테스 왕을 언급한 건 코르넬리우스 네포스를 외고 있었던 거였지요.\" 백작이 말을 이었다. \"그런데 부인이 그 인용을 끊으셨군요. 그건 가정교사가 결코 도련님을 소홀히 가르치지 않았다는 증거입니다. 아드님은 정말 나이에 비해 앞서 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"실은요, 백작님.\" 어머니가 흐뭇하게 으쓱해하며 대답했다. \"저 애는 재주가 아주 뛰어나서, 앞에 놓인 건 뭐든 다 익힌답니다. 흠이라면 단 하나, 좀 제멋대로라는 거지요. 그런데 정말, 잠깐 저 애가 한 말로 돌아가서요, 미트리다테스가 정말 그런 예방책을 썼고, 그게 효험이 있었다고 보세요?\"\n\n\"그렇게 봅니다, 부인. 저 자신이 그 방법을 써 왔으니까요. 나폴리에서, 팔레르모에서, 스미르나에서 독살당하지 않으려고 말입니다 — 다시 말해, 그런 예방책이 없었더라면 목숨을 잃었을 게 분명한 세 차례의 일에서요.\"\n\n\"그래서 그 예방책이 효험이 있었나요?\"\n\n\"완벽했습니다.\"\n\n\"네, 이제 기억나요. 페루자에서 제게 그 비슷한 이야기를 하셨던 게요.\"\n\n\"그랬습니까?\" 백작이, 놀랍도록 그럴듯하게 꾸며 낸 의외라는 표정으로 말했다. \"정말이지 기억이 안 나는군요.\"\n\n\"제가 백작님께, 독이 남방 사람들에게 그러듯이 북방 사람들에게도 똑같이, 같은 효력으로 작용하느냐고 여쭈었지요. 그러자 백작님은 차고 굼뜬 북방의 기질은 풍부하고 활기찬 남방 토박이들의 기질만 한 감수성을 보이지 않는다고 답하셨고요.\"\n\n\"맞는 말입니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"저는 러시아인들이, 나폴리 사람이나 아랍인이라면 틀림없이 죽었을 식물성 물질을 눈에 띄는 탈도 없이 먹어 치우는 걸 본 적이 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 결과가 동방에서보다 우리 쪽에서 한층 더 확실하리라고 정말 믿으세요? 안개와 비 속에서 사는 사람이 따뜻한 위도에서보다 이렇게 점진적으로 독을 흡수하는 데 더 쉽게 익숙해진다고요?\"\n\n\"물론입니다. 다만 그가 익숙하지 않던 독에 대해 충분히 단련되어 있어야 한다는 게 전제이지요.\"\n\n\"네, 그건 알겠어요. 그럼 어떻게 자신을, 이를테면, 아니, 백작님은 어떻게 그 독에 익숙해지셨나요?\"\n\n\"오, 아주 쉽습니다. 당신을 해칠 독이 무엇인지 미리 안다고 가정해 봅시다. 그 독이, 이를테면, 브루신이라고 한다면 —\"\n\n\"브루신은 가짜 앙고스투라에서 추출하는 거 아닌가요?\" 빌포르 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다, 부인.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"이거 제가 부인께 가르쳐 드릴 게 별로 없겠군요. 부인의 박식함에 경의를 표합니다. 그런 지식은 여성에게선 아주 드무니까요.\"\n\n\"오, 저도 알아요.\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"하지만 저는 신비학에 열정이 있어서요. 그건 시처럼 상상력에 말을 걸고, 대수 방정식처럼 숫자로 환원되거든요. 그런데 계속해 주세요, 부디. 백작님 말씀이 더없이 흥미로우니까요.\"\n\n\"좋습니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"그럼 이 독이 브루신이라고 하고, 부인이 첫날에는 1밀리그램, 둘째 날에는 2밀리그램, 이런 식으로 복용한다고 합시다. 자, 열흘이 지나면 1센티그램을 복용한 셈이 되고, 스무 날이 지나면 매번 1밀리그램씩 늘려 300센티그램을 복용하게 됩니다. 다시 말해, 부인은 아무 탈 없이 견디지만, 부인과 같은 예방책을 쓰지 않은 다른 사람에게는 몹시 위험할 분량이지요. 그렇게 한 달이 지나면, 같은 물병의 물을 마실 때, 부인은 함께 마신 사람을 죽이게 됩니다. 그런데도 부인 자신은, 가벼운 불편함 말고는, 그 물에 독성 물질이 섞여 있었다는 걸 느끼지도 못하는 거지요.\"\n\n\"다른 해독제도 알고 계세요?\"\n\n\"모릅니다.\"\n\n\"미트리다테스 이야기를 여러 번 읽고 또 읽었어요.\" 빌포르 부인이 곰곰이 생각에 잠긴 어조로 말했다. \"그러면서도 늘 꾸며 낸 이야기라고만 여겼지요.\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 부인. 대부분의 역사와 달리, 그건 사실입니다. 그런데 부인이 제게 하시는 말씀, 제게 물으시는 것은, 우연한 물음에서 나온 게 아니로군요. 이 년 전에도 부인은 제게 똑같은 질문을 하셨고, 그때 이 미트리다테스 이야기가 아주 오랫동안 머릿속을 떠나지 않았다고 하셨으니까요.\"\n\n\"맞아요, 백작님. 젊은 시절 제가 가장 좋아한 두 가지 공부가 식물학과 광물학이었어요. 그 뒤에 약초의 쓰임이 동방에서 한 민족의 역사 전체를, 또 개인들의 삶 전체를 자주 풀이해 준다는 걸, 마치 꽃이 연애 사건을 알리고 상징하듯이 말이에요, 그걸 알고 나서는, 제가 남자로 태어나지 못한 게 한스러웠답니다. 그랬다면 플라멜이나 폰타나, 혹은 카바니스 같은 사람이 될 수도 있었을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"더욱이, 부인.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"동방 사람들은 미트리다테스처럼 독으로 갑옷을 만드는 데 그치지 않고, 그것으로 단검까지 만들었으니까요. 그들의 손에서 학문은 방어 무기일 뿐 아니라, 그보다 더 자주 공격 무기가 됩니다. 하나는 온갖 육체적 고통에 맞서 쓰이고, 다른 하나는 모든 적에 맞서 쓰이지요. 아편과 벨라도나, 브루세아, 사목, 그리고 월계수벚나무로 그들은 길을 막는 자는 누구든 잠재웁니다. 이집트 여인이든 터키 여인이든 그리스 여인이든, 여기서 '착한 여인들'이라 부르는 그들 가운데, 화학으로 의사를 멍하게 만들고 심리술로 고해 신부를 경악하게 할 줄 모르는 사람은 하나도 없답니다.\"\n\n\"정말요.\" 이 대화에 두 눈이 묘한 불꽃으로 반짝이며 빌포르 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그럼요, 정말입니다, 부인.\" 몬테크리스토가 말을 이었다. \"동방의 은밀한 드라마는 사랑의 미약으로 시작해 죽음의 묘약으로 끝나지요 — 천국에서 시작해 — 지옥에서 끝나는 겁니다. 인간의 육체적, 정신적 본성에 변덕과 별난 점이 있는 만큼이나, 온갖 종류의 영약이 존재합니다. 한 가지 더 말씀드리자면 — 그 화학자들의 기술은 더없이 정밀하게, 약과 독을 사랑의 갈망이나 복수의 욕망에 맞게 조절하고 배합할 수 있지요.\"\n\n\"하지만, 백작님.\" 젊은 부인이 말했다. \"백작님이 생애의 한 시절을 보내신 그 동방 사회는, 그 낯선 땅에서 전해 오는 이야기들만큼이나 환상적이군요. 그럼 거기서는 사람 하나쯤 쉽게 치워 버릴 수 있겠어요. 정말이지 「천일야화」에 나오는 바그다드와 바스라로군요. 그곳 사회를 다스리는, 프랑스에서 우리가 정부라고 부르는 것을 이루는 술탄과 재상들은, 실은 하룬 알 라시드나 자파르 같은 자들이어서, 독살자를 용서할 뿐 아니라, 그 범죄가 교묘했다면 그를 재상으로 삼기까지 하고, 그런 경우에는 한가하고 권태로운 시간을 달래려고 그 이야기를 통째로 금박 글씨로 적게 한다는 거지요.\"\n\n\"천만에요, 부인. 동방에 이제 그런 환상 같은 건 더 없습니다. 그곳에도, 다른 이름으로 위장하고 다른 복색으로 감춘 경찰관과 치안판사, 검사장, 집행관들이 있지요. 그들은 더없이 보기 좋은 방식으로 죄인을 목매달고, 목 베고, 꼬챙이에 꿰지요. 다만 그중 몇몇은, 영리한 악당답게, 인간의 정의를 빠져나갈 궁리를 해내고, 교활한 술책으로 사기 행각에 성공한답니다. 우리 쪽에서는, 증오나 탐욕의 악마에 사로잡힌 어리석은 자가, 없애 버릴 원수나 처치할 가까운 친척이 있으면, 곧장 식료품상이나 약종상에게 가서, 진짜 이름보다 발각되기 더 쉬운 가짜 이름을 대고는, 쥐 때문에 잠을 못 잔다는 핑계로 비소 대여섯 그램을 삽니다 — 정말로 교활한 작자라면, 식료품상이나 약종상 대여섯 군데를 돌지요. 그래서 추적당하기가 그저 대여섯 배 더 쉬워질 뿐입니다. 그런 다음, 그 약을 손에 넣으면, 원수나 가까운 친척에게 매머드나 마스토돈도 터져 죽을 만큼의 비소를 정성껏 먹이고, 그러면 까닭도 영문도 없이 그 희생자가 온 동네를 놀라게 하는 신음을 토해 내지요. 그러면 경찰관과 순경들이 떼로 몰려옵니다. 의사를 불러오고, 의사는 시신을 갈라, 내장과 위에서 비소를 한 숟갈 그득 긁어냅니다. 이튿날 신문 백 군데가, 희생자와 살인자의 이름까지 들어 그 사실을 보도하지요. 그날 저녁이면 그 식료품상이나 약종상들이 와서 말합니다. '그 신사분에게 비소를 판 게 바로 접니다.' 그러고는 죄지은 손님을 못 알아보느니, 차라리 스무 명이라도 알아보겠다고 나서지요. 그래서 그 어리석은 범인은 붙잡혀 투옥되고, 심문당하고, 대질되고, 궁지에 몰리고, 유죄를 선고받고, 삼이나 쇠로 목숨을 끊깁니다. 혹은 어느 정도 신분 있는 여인이라면, 평생 가둬 두지요. 부인, 이게 바로 북방 사람들이 화학을 이해하는 방식입니다. 그래도 데뤼는, 솔직히 말씀드려, 좀 더 솜씨가 좋긴 했지요.\"\n\n\"어쩌겠어요, 백작님?\" 부인이 웃으며 말했다. \"우리야 할 수 있는 걸 할 뿐이죠. 세상 모두가 메디치나 보르자 가문의 비법을 아는 건 아니니까요.\"\n\n\"자, 이제.\" 백작이 어깨를 으쓱하며 대답했다. \"이 모든 어리석은 짓의 원인을 말씀드릴까요? 그건 부인들이 가는 극장에서, 적어도 상연되는 작품들을 읽어 본 제가 판단하기로는, 사람들이 약병의 내용물을 삼키거나 반지의 단추를 빨고는 그 자리에서 즉사하는 걸 보기 때문입니다. 오 분 뒤면 막이 내리고, 관객들은 떠나지요. 그들은 살인의 뒷일을 모릅니다. 직무 휘장을 단 경찰서장도, 부하 넷을 거느린 하사도 보지 못하지요. 그래서 그 가엾은 바보들은 그 모든 일이 거짓말처럼 쉬운 거라고 믿습니다. 하지만 프랑스에서 좀 벗어나 보세요 — 알레포나 카이로로 가든, 그저 나폴리나 로마로만 가도 — 거리에서 당신 곁을 스쳐 가는 사람들이 보일 겁니다. 꼿꼿이 서서, 미소 띠고, 혈색 좋은 사람들 말이지요. 만약 당신이 아스모데우스의 망토 자락을 붙들고 있다면, 그가 이렇게 말할 사람들입니다. '저자는 삼 주 전에 독을 먹었지. 한 달 안에 죽은 목숨이오.'\"\n\n\"그럼.\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"페루자에서 사라졌다던 그 유명한 아쿠아 토파나의 비법을 사람들이 다시 알아낸 거로군요.\"\n\n\"아, 하지만 부인, 인류가 무언가를 영영 잃는 법이 있던가요? 기술이란 자리를 바꿔 가며 세상을 한 바퀴 돌지요. 사물은 다른 이름을 얻고, 보통 사람들은 그걸 좇지 못하는 겁니다 — 그게 전부예요. 하지만 결과는 언제나 똑같지요. 독은 특히 이런저런 기관에 작용합니다 — 어떤 건 위에, 어떤 건 뇌에, 어떤 건 창자에요. 자, 독이 기침을 일으키고, 기침이 폐렴이나 학문의 책에 분류된 다른 병을 일으키지요. 그렇다고 그게 결코 치명적이지 않은 게 되는 건 아닙니다. 또 설사 치명적이지 않더라도, 어리석은 의사들이 — 대개 형편없는 화학자이지요 — 쓰는 치료약 덕분에 틀림없이 치명적인 게 됩니다. 그 약은 부인 뜻대로, 병에 유리하게도 불리하게도 작용하지요. 그렇게 해서 한 인간이 온갖 기술과 솜씨의 규칙에 따라 죽임을 당하는데, 정의는 그것에 대해 아무것도 알아내지 못합니다. 제가 아는 어느 무시무시한 화학자가 한 말이지요. 시칠리아 타오르미나의 훌륭한 아델몬테 신부인데, 이 민족 특유의 현상들을 아주 깊이 연구한 분입니다.\"\n\n\"정말 끔찍하지만, 더없이 흥미롭네요.\" 젊은 부인이 넋을 잃고 꼼짝 않은 채 말했다. \"솔직히 저는 그런 이야기들이 중세의 꾸며 낸 것인 줄로만 알았어요.\"\n\n\"네, 물론입니다. 하지만 우리 시대가 그걸 더 개량했지요. 사회를 더 완전한 경지로 이끌지 못한다면, 시간이며 공로 표창이며 메달이며 십자 훈장이며 몽티옹 상이 다 무슨 소용이겠습니까? 그렇지만 인간은 창조하고 파괴하는 법을 배우기 전에는 결코 완전해질 수 없을 겁니다. 인간은 파괴하는 법은 알지요. 그러니 절반은 해낸 셈입니다.\"\n\n\"그럼.\" 빌포르 부인이, 줄곧 제 관심사로 되돌아오며 덧붙였다. \"보르자며 메디치며 르네며 루지에리 가문의 독, 그리고 그 뒤로는, 아마도 현대 연극과 소설이 그토록 함부로 써먹은 트렁크 남작의 독까지 —\"\n\n\"그건 예술 작품이었을 뿐입니다, 부인. 그 이상은 아니에요.\" 백작이 대답했다. \"진정한 학자가 한낱 개인 따위를 어리석게 상대하리라 보십니까? 천만에요. 학문은 별난 것, 도약과 비약, 힘의 시험, 환상 — 그렇게 불러도 된다면요 — 을 사랑합니다. 그러니, 이를테면, 방금 말씀드린 그 훌륭한 아델몬테 신부는 이런 식으로 몇 가지 놀라운 실험을 했지요.\"\n\n\"정말요?\"\n\n\"네. 한 가지 말씀드리지요. 그분은 채소며 꽃이며 과일이 가득한, 유난히 멋진 정원을 가지고 있었습니다. 그 채소들 가운데서 가장 평범한 것 — 이를테면 양배추를 골랐지요. 사흘 동안 그 양배추에 비소 증류액을 뿌렸습니다. 사흘째가 되자 양배추는 시들며 누렇게 변하기 시작했지요. 바로 그 순간 그분은 그걸 베었습니다. 누가 봐도 식탁에 올릴 만했고, 멀쩡해 보이는 모습 그대로였지요. 오직 아델몬테 신부에게만 그건 독이 든 양배추였습니다. 그러고 나서 그분은 그 양배추를 토끼가 있는 방으로 가져갔지요 — 아델몬테 신부는 채소, 꽃, 과일 못지않게 훌륭한 토끼와 고양이, 기니피그 수집품을 가지고 있었으니까요. 자, 아델몬테 신부는 토끼 한 마리를 잡아 그 양배추 잎을 먹였습니다. 토끼는 죽었지요. 어느 치안판사가 이걸 두고 무얼 찾아내겠으며, 감히 무얼 넌지시 비추기라도 하겠습니까? 어떤 검사장이, 마장디 씨나 플루랑스 씨가 죽인 토끼며 고양이며 기니피그를 이유로 감히 그들을 고발한 적이 있던가요? 단 한 사람도 없습니다. 그러니 토끼가 죽어도 정의는 거들떠보지 않지요. 토끼가 죽으면, 아델몬테 신부는 요리사에게 그 내장을 끄집어내 거름 더미에 던지게 합니다. 그 거름 더미에는 암탉이 한 마리 있는데, 그 창자를 쪼아 먹고는 이번엔 그 암탉이 병들어 이튿날 죽지요. 암탉이 죽음의 경련에 몸부림치는 바로 그 순간, 독수리 한 마리가 날아갑니다 — 아델몬테의 고장에는 독수리가 꽤 많거든요. 이 새는 죽은 닭에게 달려들어 바위로 물고 가, 그 먹이로 식사를 하지요. 사흘 뒤, 그 식사 이후 줄곧 몹시 시름시름 앓던 이 가엾은 독수리는, 구름 높이 날다가 갑자기 어지럼을 느끼고 못으로 무겁게 떨어집니다. 강꼬치며 뱀장어며 잉어는, 누구나 알다시피, 늘 게걸스레 먹어 치우지요 — 자, 그것들이 독수리를 포식합니다. 그런데 이튿날, 네 단계나 건너 독에 물든 그 뱀장어나 강꼬치, 잉어 한 마리가 부인의 식탁에 오른다고 합시다. 그러면 부인의 손님은 다섯 단계째 독에 중독되어, 여드레나 열흘 뒤에 창자 통증이며 구토며 유문 농양으로 죽지요. 의사들은 시신을 갈라 보고는 더없이 박식한 척 말합니다. '이 사람은 간 종양으로, 혹은 장티푸스로 죽었군요!'\"\n\n\"하지만.\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"백작님이 그렇게 하나하나 이어 붙이신 그 모든 정황은, 사소한 우연 하나로도 깨질 수 있잖아요. 독수리가 닭을 못 볼 수도 있고, 못에서 백 미터쯤 떨어진 데 떨어질 수도 있고요.\"\n\n\"아, 바로 거기에 기술이 들어가는 겁니다. 동방에서 위대한 화학자가 되려면, 우연을 마음대로 다스려야 하지요. 그리고 그건 이뤄 낼 수 있는 일입니다.\"\n\n빌포르 부인은 깊은 생각에 잠겼으면서도 귀를 기울여 듣고 있었다.\n\n\"하지만.\" 그녀가 불쑥 외쳤다. \"비소는 지워지지도 파괴되지도 않잖아요. 어떤 식으로 흡수되든, 죽음을 일으킬 만큼 충분히 섭취된 순간부터는, 희생자의 몸에서 다시 발견되고요.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 외쳤다. \"바로 그래요. 저도 그 점을 제 훌륭한 아델몬테에게 말했지요. 그분은 곰곰이 생각하더니, 미소를 지으며 시칠리아 속담으로 답하더군요. 제 생각엔 프랑스 속담이기도 한 것 같은데요. '얘야, 세상은 하루 만에 만들어진 게 아니라 — 이레에 걸쳐 만들어졌단다. 일요일에 다시 오너라.' 그다음 일요일에 저는 그분께 다시 갔지요. 이번엔 양배추에 비소를 뿌리는 대신, 학자들이 스트리크노스 콜루브리나라 부르는, 스트리크닌을 기본으로 한 염류 용액을 뿌려 두었더군요. 자, 그 양배추는 세상 어디에도 병든 기색이라곤 없었고, 토끼도 조금도 의심하지 않았습니다. 그런데도 오 분 뒤에 토끼는 죽었지요. 닭이 그 토끼를 쪼아 먹었고, 이튿날엔 죽은 암탉이 되었습니다. 이번엔 우리가 독수리였지요. 그래서 우리는 그 새를 갈라 보았는데, 이번엔 특별한 증상이라곤 다 사라지고 일반적인 증상밖에 없더군요. 어느 기관에도 특이한 징후는 없었습니다 — 신경계의 흥분 — 그게 다였지요. 뇌충혈 사례 — 그뿐이었습니다. 닭은 독에 죽은 게 아니었어요 — 졸중으로 죽은 거였지요. 졸중은 닭에게는 드문 병이라고 알고 있습니다만, 사람에게는 아주 흔하지요.\"\n\n빌포르 부인은 점점 더 깊은 생각에 잠긴 듯 보였다.\n\n\"참 다행이에요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그런 물질들은 화학자만이 만들 수 있다는 게요. 안 그랬다면 온 세상이 서로 독살하고 있었을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"화학자와, 화학에 취미가 있는 사람들이지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 무심히 말했다.\n\n\"그리고요.\" 빌포르 부인이, 안간힘을 쓰고 애를 써서 제 생각에서 벗어나려 하며 말했다. \"아무리 솜씨 좋게 만든다 해도, 범죄는 늘 범죄이고, 인간의 눈은 피한다 해도 신의 눈은 벗어나지 못하지요. 동방 사람들은 양심 문제에서 우리보다 강해서, 아주 신중하게도, 지옥이라는 걸 두지 않아요 — 바로 그 점이에요.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 부인, 그건 부인처럼 순수한 마음에는 당연히 떠오를 망설임이지요. 하지만 건전한 추론 앞에서는 쉽사리 수그러들 겁니다. 인간 사고의 나쁜 면은 늘 장 자크 루소의 역설로 정의되지요 — 기억나시지요 — 손가락 끝을 까딱하는 것만으로 오백 리 밖에서 죽임을 당하는 그 만다린 말입니다. 인간의 일생은 그런 짓을 하며 흘러가고, 그 지성은 그런 것을 곱씹느라 소진되지요. 동료 인간의 심장에 잔인하게 칼을 꽂거나, 우리가 생기 있게 살아 움직이는 이 지구 표면에서 그자를 없애려고 아까 이야기한 그만큼의 비소를 먹일 사람은 아주 드물 겁니다. 그런 짓은 정말이지 정도에서 벗어난 일이지요 — 별나거나 어리석은 짓입니다. 그 지경에 이르려면, 피가 36도까지 끓어올라야 하고, 맥박이 적어도 90은 되어야 하며, 감정이 보통의 한계를 넘어 격해져야 하니까요. 하지만 어학에서 허용되듯, 그 말 자체에서 좀 더 부드러운 동의어로 옮겨 간다고 합시다. 그러면 비열한 암살을 저지르는 대신 '제거'를 하는 셈이 되지요. 길을 막는 자를 그저 단순히 길에서 치워 버리는 겁니다. 그것도 충격이나 폭력 없이, 또 형벌이 되었을 때 희생자를 순교자로 만들고 그것을 가하는 자를 어느 모로 보나 백정으로 만드는 그 고통의 광경을 드러내지 않고서 말이지요. 그러면 피도 없고, 신음도 없고, 경련도 없고, 무엇보다 그 끔찍하고 위태로운, 행위를 완수하는 순간의 의식도 없습니다 — 그렇게 해서 '사회를 어지럽히지 말라!'고 외치는 인간 법의 손아귀를 벗어나는 거지요. 이게 바로 동방의 풍토에서 그런 일을 처리하고 성공시키는 방식입니다. 그곳에는, 중대한 고비에서 시간 따위는 거의 개의치 않는, 점잖고 냉담한 사람들이 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"그래도 양심은 남잖아요.\" 빌포르 부인이 동요한 목소리로, 억눌린 한숨과 함께 말했다.\n\n\"네.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"다행히도, 그래요, 양심은 남지요. 그게 없다면 우리는 얼마나 비참하겠습니까! 애쓰지 않으면 안 되는 모든 행동 뒤에, 우리를 구하는 건 양심입니다. 양심은 우리에게 천 가지 그럴듯한 핑계를 대 주는데, 그걸 판단하는 건 오직 우리 자신뿐이지요. 그런 핑계들은, 잠을 이루는 데는 더없이 훌륭하지만, 우리가 목숨을 걸고 재판받는 법정 앞에서는 거의 도움이 안 될 겁니다. 가령 리처드 3세는, 에드워드 4세의 두 아이를 치워 버린 뒤 양심의 놀라운 도움을 받았지요. 실제로 그는 이렇게 말할 수 있었으니까요. '잔인하고 학정을 일삼던 왕의 이 두 아이는, 그 어린 성향에서 나만이 알아볼 수 있었던 아비의 악덕을 물려받았다 — 이 두 아이는 영국 백성의 행복을 도모하려는 내 길의 걸림돌이며, 그들이(이 아이들이) 백성에게 틀림없이 불행을 안겼을 것이다.' 맥베스 부인도 양심의 도움을 받았지요. 그녀가 — 셰익스피어가 뭐라 하든 — 남편이 아니라 제 아들에게 왕좌를 주려 했을 때 말입니다. 아, 모성애는 위대한 미덕이고, 강력한 동기이지요 — 어찌나 강력한지, 던컨이 죽은 뒤 맥베스 부인이 양심에 조금이나마 찔렸다 하더라도, 수많은 것을 변명해 주니까요.\"\n\n빌포르 부인은, 백작 특유의 그 빈정대는 듯한 천연덕스러움으로 풀어내는 이 소름 끼치는 격언과 끔찍한 역설들을 게걸스레 귀 기울여 들었다.\n\n잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"있잖아요, 백작님.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"백작님은 정말 무시무시한 논객이세요. 게다가 다소 병든 매개를 통해 세상을 바라보시는 것 같고요. 정말로 세심한 관찰로, 아니면 증류기와 도가니를 통해 세상을 재 보셨나요? 정말 백작님은 대단한 화학자임에 틀림없어요. 제 아들에게 먹여, 거의 순식간에 그 애를 되살려 낸 그 영약만 봐도 —\"\n\n\"오, 그걸 너무 믿진 마십시오, 부인. 그 영약 한 방울이면 죽어 가는 아이를 되살리기에 충분했지만, 세 방울이었다면 피를 폐로 몰아붙여 더없이 격렬한 심계항진을 일으켰을 겁니다. 여섯 방울이면 호흡을 멎게 해, 그 애가 빠졌던 것보다 더 위중한 실신을 불렀을 테고요. 열 방울이면 그 애를 죽였을 겁니다. 부인도 아시지요, 그 애가 그토록 경솔하게 만진 약병들에서 제가 얼마나 갑작스레 그 애를 낚아챘는지요?\"\n\n\"그럼 그렇게나 무서운 독인가요?\"\n\n\"오, 아닙니다! 우선, 독이라는 말은 존재하지 않는다는 데 동의하기로 하지요. 의학에서는 가장 격렬한 독들을 쓰는데, 그것이 쓰임새에 따라 더없이 이로운 치료약이 되니까요.\"\n\n\"그럼 그게 대체 뭐죠?\"\n\n\"제 친구인 그 훌륭한 아델몬테 신부의 솜씨 좋은 조제약입니다. 그분이 제게 그 쓰임새를 가르쳐 주셨지요.\"\n\n\"오.\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"그거 틀림없이 훌륭한 진경제겠군요.\"\n\n\"완벽하지요, 부인. 보셨다시피요.\" 백작이 대답했다. \"저도 자주 쓴답니다 — 다만, 말이 나왔으니, 가능한 한 신중을 다해서요.\" 그가 의미심장한 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그야 물론이지요.\" 빌포르 부인이 같은 어조로 응했다. \"저는 신경이 어찌나 예민하고 또 까무러치는 일이 잦은지, 어느 좋은 날 질식해 죽을까 봐 두려운 마음에, 숨을 편히 쉬고 마음을 가라앉힐 무슨 방법을 발명해 줄 아델몬테 박사 같은 분이 필요할 지경이에요. 그런데 그런 걸 프랑스에서 찾기는 어렵고, 백작님의 그 신부님도 저 때문에 파리까지 걸음하실 마음은 없으실 테니, 저는 계속 플랑슈 씨의 진경제를 써야겠지요. 박하랑 호프만 물약이 제가 즐겨 쓰는 약 중에 들어요. 여기 일부러 만들어 둔 정제가 좀 있는데, 약효를 두 배로 진하게 조제한 것이랍니다.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 부인이 건넨 거북 등딱지 갑을 열고, 그 조제법을 속속들이 음미하는 애호가의 표정으로 정제의 냄새를 들이마셨다.\n\n\"정말 절묘하군요.\" 그가 말했다. \"다만 이건 삼키는 과정을 반드시 거쳐야 하는데 — 까무러친 사람으로서는 그 일을 해내기가 흔히 불가능하지요 — 그래서 저는 제 약을 더 좋아한답니다.\"\n\n\"틀림없이 그렇겠죠. 저도 그 효력을 직접 본 터라 그쪽을 더 좋아하겠어요. 하지만 물론 그건 비밀이고, 저도 그걸 백작님께 여쭐 만큼 경솔하진 않아요.\"\n\n\"하지만 저는.\" 몬테크리스토가 말하며 자리에서 일어섰다. \"기꺼이 그걸 부인께 드릴 만큼 너그러운 사람이지요.\"\n\n\"어쩜 이리 친절하실까요.\"\n\n\"한 가지만 기억하십시오 — 적은 양은 약이고, 많은 양은 독입니다. 보셨다시피 한 방울이면 생명을 되살리지요. 다섯이나 여섯 방울이면 어김없이 죽이는데, 더더욱 무서운 건, 포도주 잔에 부어도 그 맛을 조금도 바꾸지 않는다는 점입니다. 하지만 더는 말하지 않겠습니다, 부인. 이러다간 정말 제가 부인께 처방을 내리는 꼴이 되겠군요.\"\n\n시계가 여섯 시 반을 알렸고, 한 부인의 방문이 알려졌다. 빌포르 부인의 벗으로, 함께 만찬을 들러 온 사람이었다.\n\n\"제가 백작님을 두 번째가 아니라 세 번째나 네 번째로 뵙는 영광을 누렸더라면요, 백작님.\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"그저 신세를 진 행복만이 아니라 백작님의 벗이 되는 영광을 누렸더라면, 만찬에 모시겠다고 우겨, 첫 거절쯤에 주눅 들지 않았을 거예요.\"\n\n\"천 번 감사드립니다, 부인.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"하지만 어길 수 없는 약속이 있어서요. 우리 그랜드 오페라를 한 번도 본 적 없는, 제 지인인 어느 그리스 공주를 아카데미로 모셔다 드리기로 했거든요. 거기로 안내해 달라고 제게 의지하고 있답니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 안녕히 가세요, 백작님. 처방은 잊지 마시고요.\"\n\n\"아, 정말이지, 부인. 그러려면 부인과 나눈 한 시간의 대화를 잊어야 할 텐데, 그건 도저히 불가능한 일이지요.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 인사하고 집을 나섰다. 빌포르 부인은 생각에 잠긴 채 남아 있었다.\n\n\"참 별난 사람이야.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"내가 보기엔, 저 사람이야말로 입에 올리는 그 아델몬테 본인인 것 같아.\"\n\n몬테크리스토로 말하자면, 결과는 그의 더없이 큰 기대마저 넘어선 것이었다.\n\n\"좋아.\" 그가 떠나며 말했다. \"여긴 비옥한 땅이야. 뿌린 씨앗이 메마른 땅에 떨어지진 않으리라 확신한다.\"\n\n이튿날 아침, 그는 약속을 지켜, 부인이 청한 처방을 보냈다.\n\n# Chapter 53. Robert le Diable\n\n오페라를 핑계 삼는 것은 한결 그럴듯한 일이었다. 마침 그날 밤 왕립 음악원에서는 평소보다 더한 볼거리가 펼쳐질 예정이었기 때문이다. 중병을 앓던 르바쇠르가 베르트랑 역으로 다시 무대에 오르는 데다, 늘 그렇듯 당대 최고 인기 작곡가의 가장 사랑받는 작품을 상연한다는 소식이 화려하고 세련된 관객들을 불러 모았다. 모르세르는 지체와 재산을 갖춘 여느 젊은이들이 그렇듯 일층 정면석을 가지고 있었고, 적어도 열두 개쯤 되는 주요 칸막이석 가운데 아는 사람들이 차지한 자리는 언제든 들어갈 수 있었다. 게다가 그는 합승석에 들어갈 권리도 있었다. 샤토르노는 그의 옆자리 정면석을 빌렸고, 신문 기자인 보샹은 극장 어디든 무제한으로 드나들 수 있었다. 그날 밤은 마침 장관의 칸막이석이 뤼시앵 드브레의 손에 맡겨졌는데, 드브레는 그것을 모르세르 백작에게 권했다. 모르세르 백작은 메르세데스가 거절하자 다시 그것을 당글라르에게 보냈고, 그 칸막이석을 받아들인다면 저녁 동안 남작 부인과 그 딸과 합석하는 영광을 누리게 될지도 모른다는 뜻을 넌지시 비쳤다. 부인들은 그 제안을 거절은 꿈도 꾸지 않을 만큼 기쁘게 받아들였다. 어떤 부류의 사람에게도, 조끼 주머니에 임금의 몸값을 넣고 다닌다고 뽐내면서도 여전히 절약에 매달리는 부유한 백만장자에게만큼 공짜 오페라 칸막이석이 반가운 일은 없는 법이다.\n\n그러나 당글라르는 장관의 칸막이석에 모습을 드러내기를 거부했다. 자신의 정치적 신념과 야당 의원이라는 의회 내 위치가 그렇게 처신하도록 허락하지 않는다는 것이었다. 그래서 남작 부인은 뤼시앵 드브레에게 자신들을 데리러 와 달라는 쪽지를 보냈다. 외제니와 단둘이 오페라에 간다는 것은 도저히 있을 수 없는 일이었기 때문이다.\n\n두 여자가 동반자도 없이 갔더라면 그 처사가 매우 좋지 않게 받아들여졌으리라는 것은 부인할 수 없는 사실이었다. 반면 어머니가 공공연히 인정한 연인이라는 세 번째 인물이 더해지자, 당글라르 양은 악의와 험담을 너끈히 비웃을 수 있었다. 세상은 있는 그대로 받아들여야 하는 법이다.\n\n막이 오를 때면 으레 그렇듯 객석은 거의 비어 있었다. 공연이 시작된 뒤에야 오페라에 모습을 드러내는 것이 파리 사교계의 어처구니없는 관습이었기에, 1막은 대개 아무런 주목도 받지 못한 채 흘러갔다. 이미 자리에 든 관객들은 새로 들어오는 사람들을 관찰하느라 여념이 없었고, 들리는 것이라고는 문을 여닫는 소리와 잡담 소리뿐이었다.\n\n\"틀림없어.\" 일층 첫째 줄 칸막이석 문이 열리자 알베르가 말했다. \"저분은 G 백작 부인이 분명해.\"\n\n\"G 백작 부인이 누군가?\" 샤토르노가 물었다.\n\n\"그런 걸 묻다니! 남작, 그런 질문을 한다면 내가 정말 싸움이라도 걸고 싶어지네. 마치 온 세상이 G 백작 부인이 누군지 모른다는 듯이 말이야.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇군.\" 샤토르노가 대답했다. \"그 어여쁜 베네치아 여인 말이지?\"\n\n\"바로 그분일세.\" 그 순간 백작 부인이 알베르를 알아보고 미소로 인사를 받았다.\n\n\"아는 사이인 모양이군?\" 샤토르노가 말했다.\n\n\"로마에서 프란츠가 소개해 줬네.\" 알베르가 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 프란츠가 로마에서 자네에게 해 준 만큼, 파리에서 나를 위해 해 줄 수 있겠나?\"\n\n\"기꺼이.\"\n\n관객석에서 \"조용히 해!\" 하는 소리가 터져 나왔다. 음악을 듣고 싶다는 관객들의 이런 의사 표시는 두 젊은이에게 조금도 영향을 미치지 못했고, 두 사람은 계속 이야기를 나누었다.\n\n\"백작 부인은 샹드마르스 경마장에도 와 있었네.\" 샤토르노가 말했다.\n\"오늘 말인가?\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n\"이런, 경마를 까맣게 잊고 있었군. 자넨 돈을 걸었나?\"\n\n\"아, 그저 루이 금화 쉰 닢이라는 보잘것없는 돈을 걸었을 뿐일세.\"\n\n\"그래, 누가 이겼나?\"\n\n\"노틸러스. 내가 그놈한테 걸었지.\"\n\n\"하지만 경주가 셋이었지, 안 그런가?\"\n\"그래. 자키 클럽이 내건 상이 있었네. 알다시피 금잔이지. 그런데 그 경주에서 아주 묘한 일이 벌어졌어.\"\n\n\"무슨 일이었나?\"\n\n\"어이, 조용히 좀 해!\" 또다시 관객 몇몇이 끼어들었다.\n\n\"경주장에서는 전혀 알려지지 않은 말과 기수가 우승했지 뭔가.\"\n\n\"그게 가능한 일인가?\"\n\n\"대낮처럼 분명한 사실일세. 사실은 이래. 밤파라는 이름으로 출전 등록된 말이나 조브라는 이름의 기수를 아무도 눈여겨보지 않았는데, 막판에 가서 멋진 적갈색 말이 나타났네. 자네 주먹만 한 기수가 올라타고 말이야. 출발선에 모습을 드러냈지. 그 작은 기수의 주머니에 적어도 이십 파운드는 되는 탄환을 채워 넣어야 했네. 무게를 맞추려고 말이야. 그런데도 그 말은 함께 달린 아리엘과 바르바르를 적어도 세 마신 차이로 따돌렸어.\"\n\n\"그래서 결국 말과 기수가 누구 것인지 밝혀지지 않았나?\"\n\n\"못 밝혔지.\"\n\"그 말이 밤파라는 이름으로 등록됐다고 했나?\"\n\n\"바로 그렇네. 그게 이름이었지.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 알베르가 대답했다. \"내가 자네보다 더 잘 아는군. 그 말 주인이 누군지 알겠어.\"\n\n\"거기, 조용히 해!\" 일층 입석에서 합창하듯 외쳤다. 이번에는 그 명령을 던지는 어조와 태도에 점점 짙어지는 적의가 배어 있어서, 두 젊은이는 비로소 그 명령이 자기들을 향한 것임을 깨달았다. 두 사람은 느긋하게 몸을 돌려, 주위의 여러 얼굴을 차분히 훑어보았다. 자기들이 보기에 지나친 무례라 여겨지는 그 일에 책임을 지겠다고 나설 자가 누군지 찾는 듯한 태도였다. 그러나 아무도 그 도전에 응하지 않자, 두 친구는 다시 무대 쪽으로 돌아앉아 공연에 열중하는 척했다. 그 순간 장관의 칸막이석 문이 열리며 당글라르 부인이 딸과 함께 들어왔고, 뤼시앵 드브레가 부지런히 두 사람을 자리로 안내하며 동행했다.\n\n\"하하,\" 샤토르노가 말했다. \"자작, 자네 친구분들이 오시네! 거기서 뭘 보고 있나? 저분들이 자네와 눈을 맞추려 하는 게 안 보이나?\"\n\n알베르가 돌아보았다. 마침 남작 부인이 우아하게 부채를 흔들어 보내는 인사를 받기에 딱 알맞은 때였다. 외제니 양으로 말하자면, 그 커다란 검은 눈의 시선을 무대 위 일에조차 좀처럼 던지려 하지 않았다.\n\n\"이보게,\" 샤토르노가 말했다. \"자네가 당글라르 양한테 대체 무슨 불만을 품고 있는지 도무지 모르겠네. 가문이 변변치 않고 신분도 다소 낮다는 점이야 그렇다 쳐도, 사실 그런 거야 자네가 그리 마음 쓰는 것 같지도 않고 말이야. 그런 걸 다 제쳐 두면, 어쨌든 굉장히 멋진 아가씨라고 봐야지!\"\n\n\"아름답긴 하지.\" 알베르가 대답했다. \"하지만 내 취향은 아닐세. 솔직히 말해 나는 좀 더 부드럽고 온화하고 여성스러운 쪽으로 마음이 기우는 편이라서.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇군.\" 샤토르노가 외쳤다. 그는 서른 번째 여름을 넘겼다는 이유만으로 더 젊은 친구 앞에서 아비 같은 태도를 취할 자격이 충분하다고 여기는 사람이었다. \"자네 같은 젊은이들은 도무지 만족할 줄을 몰라. 대체 더 뭘 바라나? 자네 부모님이 사냥의 여신 디아나를 본떠 만든 듯한 신붓감을 골라 주셨는데도 흡족하지 않다니.\"\n\n\"그래, 바로 그 닮은 점이 나는 두렵단 말일세. 나는 차라리 밀로나 카푸아의 비너스 같은 쪽이 좋았을 거야. 그런데 이 사냥을 즐기는 디아나는 늘 요정들에게 둘러싸여 있으니, 언젠가 나한테 악타이온 같은 운명을 안겨 줄까 봐 어쩐지 겁이 난단 말이지.\"\n\n과연 당글라르 양을 한 번 보기만 해도 모르세르의 말이 옳다는 것을 알 수 있었다. 그녀는 아름다웠지만, 그 아름다움은 까다로운 취향을 만족시키기에는 지나치게 두드러지고 단호한 성격을 띠고 있었다. 머리카락은 까마귀처럼 검었으나 타고난 곱슬기가 어쩐지 다루기 힘들어 보였다. 머리카락과 같은 빛깔의 두 눈 위로는 멋지게 둥근 눈썹이 솟아 있었는데, 그 가장 큰 흠은 거의 습관처럼 찌푸려져 있다는 점이었다. 게다가 그녀의 얼굴 전체에는 여성의 부드러운 속성과는 도무지 어울리지 않는 굳건함과 단호함의 표정이 깃들어 있었다. 코는 조각가가 유노 흉상을 깎을 때 골랐을 법한 바로 그 모양이었다. 입은 너무 크다고 흠잡을 만했으나, 진주처럼 흰 이가 드러나 있었고, 입술의 선명한 진홍빛이 그것을 한층 두드러지게 했다. 그 진홍빛은 본래 창백한 피부와 또렷이 대비되었다. 그런데 모르세르가 그토록 마음에 들어 하지 않던 그 거의 남성적인 인상을 완성하는 것은, 자연의 변덕이 빚어내는 것치고는 훨씬 큰 검은 점이었다. 그것은 바로 입가에 자리하고 있었고, 그 효과는 그녀의 얼굴을 특징짓는 자립심의 표정을 한층 짙게 만들었다.\n\n외제니 양의 나머지 모습도 방금 묘사한 얼굴과 완벽하게 어우러졌다. 과연 그녀는 샤토르노의 말마따나 디아나를 떠올리게 했으나, 자태는 한층 도도하고 단호했다.\n\n교양으로 말하자면, 그것에서 흠잡을 점이라고는 까다로운 감식가가 그녀의 아름다움에서 찾아낼 만한 것과 같은 한 가지뿐이었다. 즉 그토록 어린 나이의 사람치고는 다소 지나치게 박식하고 남성적이라는 점이었다. 그녀는 완벽한 어학가였고, 일류 화가였으며, 시를 쓰고 곡을 지었다. 특히 음악 공부에 온전히 몰두한다고 공언하며, 지칠 줄 모르는 끈기로 그것을 파고들었다. 학교 친구 한 명이 곁에서 도왔는데, 그 친구는 재산은 없으나 그 재능이 장차 가수로서 비범한 역량으로 꽃필 만한 젊은 여인이었다. 소문에 따르면, 그녀는 당대 주요 작곡가 가운데 한 사람에게서 거의 아버지 같은 관심을 받고 있었다. 그 작곡가는 그녀에게 목소리를 갈고닦는 데 온 힘을 쏟으라고 부추겼으니, 그 목소리가 훗날 부와 독립의 원천이 될 수 있다는 것이었다. 그러나 이 충고 때문에 당글라르 양은 장차 무대 인생을 살아갈 사람과 사람들 앞에 함께 나서서 처신을 그르치는 일은 결코 하지 않겠다고 단단히 마음먹었다. 그래서 그 원칙에 따라, 은행가의 딸은 낮 동안 루이즈 다르밀리 양과 — 그것이 그 젊은 명연주자의 이름이었다 — 함께 연습하는 것은 얼마든지 좋아하면서도, 그녀와 함께 있는 모습이 남의 눈에 띄지 않도록 각별히 조심했다. 그래도 루이즈는 당글라르 저택에서 공인된 친구로 대접받지는 못했지만, 보통 가정 교사에게 베푸는 것보다는 훨씬 다정하고 정중한 대우를 받았다.\n\n당글라르 부인이 칸막이석에 들어서기가 무섭게 막이 내렸고, 악단은 막간에 으레 주어지는 삼십 분의 휴식을 위해 오케스트라석을 떠났다. 관객들은 자유로이 살롱이나 복도를 거닐거나, 저마다의 칸막이석에서 방문을 주고받을 수 있었다.\n\n모르세르와 샤토르노는 이 허락을 가장 먼저 누린 축에 들었다. 한순간 당글라르 부인은 젊은 자작의 이런 서두름이 자기네 자리에 합석하고 싶은 조바심에서 비롯된 것이라는 생각이 들었고, 알베르가 자기들에게 인사하러 서둘러 오는 것이라는 기대를 딸에게 속삭였다. 그러나 외제니 양은 그저 못마땅하다는 듯 고개를 가로저을 뿐이었다. 그러고는 차가운 미소를 띠며, 일층 첫째 줄 맞은편 칸막이석으로 어머니의 주의를 돌렸다. 그곳에는 G 백작 부인이 앉아 있었고, 모르세르가 막 그곳에 모습을 드러낸 참이었다.\n\n\"또 만났군요, 나의 여행 동무님.\" 백작 부인이 외쳤다. 그녀는 오랜 친구다운 온기와 다정함을 담뿍 담아 그에게 손을 내밀었다. \"이렇게 빨리 저를 알아봐 주시다니 정말 고마운 일이에요. 게다가 첫 방문을 저에게 베풀어 주시니 더욱더 그렇고요.\"\n\n\"분명히 말씀드리지만,\" 알베르가 대답했다. \"부인께서 파리에 오신 걸 알았고 주소를 알았더라면, 진작에 찾아뵙고 인사드렸을 겁니다. 제 친구 샤토르노 남작을 소개하지요. 오늘날 프랑스에서 찾아보기 드문 진정한 신사 가운데 하나입니다. 어제 부인께서 샹드마르스 경마를 구경하셨다는 것도 방금 이 친구한테서 들었습니다.\"\n\n샤토르노가 백작 부인에게 인사했다.\n\n\"그래, 남작님도 경마장에 계셨나요?\" 백작 부인이 다급히 물었다.\n\n\"네, 부인.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" G 부인이 상당히 활기를 띠며 말을 이었다. \"혹시 자키 클럽 상을 누가 탔는지 말씀해 주실 수 있나요?\"\n\n\"유감스럽게도 그럴 수가 없습니다.\" 남작이 대답했다. \"마침 저도 알베르에게 똑같은 걸 묻던 참이었거든요.\"\n\n\"꼭 알고 싶으십니까, 백작 부인?\" 알베르가 물었다.\n\n\"무엇을요?\"\n\n\"우승마 주인의 이름을요.\"\n\n\"무척이나요. 글쎄, 한번 상상해 보세요. 아니, 그보다 말씀해 주세요, 자작님. 정말로 그걸 아세요, 모르세요?\"\n\n\"실례지만, 부인.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"무슨 이야기를 들려주시려던 참 아니었습니까? '한번 상상해 보세요'라고 하시고는 말을 멈추셨잖아요. 부디 계속하시지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 들어 보세요. 저는 그 멋진 적갈색 말과, 분홍 새틴 윗옷에 모자를 멋스럽게 차려입은 자그마한 기수에게 어찌나 마음이 끌렸던지, 마치 제 재산의 절반이 걸리기라도 한 듯 간절히 그들의 승리를 빌지 않을 수 없었답니다. 그리고 그들이 다른 말들을 모두 따돌리고 그토록 늠름하게 결승점에 들어오는 걸 봤을 땐, 정말이지 기뻐서 박수를 쳤지 뭐예요. 그런데 집으로 돌아왔을 때 제가 얼마나 놀랐는지 상상해 보세요. 계단에서 제일 먼저 마주친 게 바로 그 분홍 윗옷을 입은 기수였으니까요! 저는 무슨 묘한 우연으로 우승마 주인이 저와 같은 호텔에 묵나 보다 했어요. 그런데 제 방에 들어서니, 그 미지의 말과 기수에게 상으로 주어진 바로 그 금잔이 놓여 있는 게 아니겠어요. 잔 안에는 작은 종이쪽지가 들어 있었고, 거기에 이런 말이 적혀 있었답니다. '러스벤 경이 G 백작 부인께.'\"\n\n\"바로 그겁니다. 제 짐작이 맞았어요.\" 모르세르가 말했다.\n\n\"뭐가 맞았다는 거죠?\"\n\n\"그 말 주인이 바로 러스벤 경이라는 거요.\"\n\n\"러스벤 경이라니, 무슨 말씀이신지?\"\n\n\"왜요, 우리의 그 러스벤 경 말입니다. 아르헨티나 극장의 흡혈귀요!\"\n\n\"그게 가능한 일인가요?\" 백작 부인이 외쳤다. \"그분이 파리에 와 있다고요?\"\n\n\"그럼요. 안 될 게 뭐 있습니까?\"\n\n\"그래서 그분 댁을 찾아가시고요? 자기 댁에서도, 다른 데서도 만나시고요?\"\n\n\"분명히 말씀드리는데, 그분은 제 가장 막역한 친구입니다. 샤토르노 씨도 그분과 알고 지내는 영광을 누리고 있고요.\"\n\n\"그런데 그분이 자키 클럽 상의 우승자라는 걸 어떻게 그리 확신하시죠?\"\n\n\"우승마가 밤파라는 이름으로 등록되지 않았습니까?\"\n\n\"그게 어쨌다는 거죠?\"\n\n\"왜요, 저를 사로잡았던 그 이름난 산적의 이름이 기억나지 않으십니까?\"\n\n\"아, 그래요.\"\n\n\"그리고 백작님이 저를 그자의 손아귀에서 그토록 놀랍게 구해 내셨잖아요?\"\n\n\"맞아요, 이제 다 기억나는군요.\"\n\n\"그자가 자기를 밤파라고 했지요. 보세요, 백작님이 그 이름을 어디서 따왔는지 뻔하지 않습니까.\"\n\n\"하지만 그분이 무슨 까닭으로 그 잔을 저에게 보냈을까요?\"\n\n\"첫째로는, 짐작하시겠지만, 제가 그분에게 부인 얘기를 많이 했기 때문이지요. 둘째로는, 동향 사람이 자신의 승리에 그토록 생생한 관심을 보이는 걸 흐뭇해했기 때문이고요.\"\n\n\"우리가 그분에 대해 늘 떠들던 그 어리석은 소리들을 백작님께 다 옮기지는 않으셨겠지요?\"\n\n\"맹세코 안 그랬다고 단언하기는 어렵겠는데요. 게다가 그분이 러스벤 경이라는 이름으로 부인께 잔을 보낸 걸 보면……\"\n\n\"어머, 그건 너무하잖아요! 그렇다면 그분은 저한테 무시무시한 원한을 품은 게 분명해요.\"\n\n\"그분의 행동이 적의 행동 같습니까?\"\n\n\"아니요, 그럴 리는 없죠.\"\n\n\"그럼……\"\n\n\"그래서 그분이 파리에 있다는 거죠?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"그래서 어떤 반응을 일으키고 있나요?\"\n\n\"글쎄요,\" 알베르가 말했다. \"일주일쯤은 사람들 입에 오르내렸지요. 그러다 영국 여왕의 대관식이 있었고, 뒤이어 마르스 양의 다이아몬드 도난 사건이 터졌어요. 그래서 사람들은 다른 얘기를 하게 됐고요.\"\n\n\"여보게,\" 샤토르노가 말했다. \"백작은 자네 친구이니 자네가 두둔하는 거겠지. 백작 부인, 알베르 말은 믿지 마십시오. 몬테크리스토 백작의 등장이 파리 사교계에 불러일으킨 화제가 가라앉기는커녕, 제가 장담컨대 여전히 처음처럼 뜨겁습니다. 그분이 우리 사이에 나타나 처음 한 놀라운 일은 당글라르 부인께 삼만 이천 프랑짜리 말 두 필을 선사한 것이었지요. 두 번째는 빌포르 부인의 목숨을 거의 기적처럼 구한 일이었고요. 그런데 이제는 자키 클럽이 내건 상까지 거머쥔 모양입니다. 그러니 저는 모르세르가 뭐라 해도 이렇게 주장하겠습니다. 백작은 지금 이 순간 관심의 대상일 뿐 아니라, 그가 보여 주는 — 어쩌면 그에게는 그저 일상적인 삶의 방식일지도 모르는 — 그 별난 행동을 계속 즐겨 보이는 한, 앞으로 한 달은 더 그러할 거라고요.\"\n\n\"자네 말이 옳을지도 모르지.\" 모르세르가 말했다. \"그건 그렇고, 러시아 대사 칸막이석에는 누가 있나?\"\n\n\"어느 칸막이석을 말하는 건가?\" 백작 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"일층 기둥과 기둥 사이에 있는 자리요. 전부 새로 꾸민 것 같던데요.\"\n\n\"1막 동안 거기서 누굴 보셨나요?\" 샤토르노가 물었다.\n\"어디서요?\"\n\n\"그 칸막이석에서요.\"\n\n\"아니요,\" 백작 부인이 대답했다. \"1막 동안엔 분명 비어 있었어요.\" 그러고는 앞서 나누던 화제로 돌아가 말했다. \"그래, 그 수수께끼 같은 몬테크리스토 백작이 정말로 상을 탔다고 믿으시는 거예요?\"\n\n\"확신합니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 그 뒤에 저에게 잔을 보냈고요?\"\n\n\"틀림없이요.\"\n\n\"하지만 저는 그분을 모르는걸요.\" 백작 부인이 말했다. \"잔을 돌려보낼까 봐요.\"\n\n\"제발 그러지 마십시오, 부탁드립니다. 그분은 또 다른 잔을 보낼 겁니다. 이번엔 근사한 사파이어로 만든 것이거나, 어마어마한 루비를 파서 만든 것이거나요. 그게 그분 방식이니, 그분은 있는 그대로 받아들이셔야 합니다.\"\n\n그 순간 2막의 막이 오른다는 종이 울렸다. 알베르가 제자리로 돌아가려고 일어섰다.\n\n\"또 뵐 수 있을까요?\" 백작 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"다음 막이 끝나면, 허락하신다면 다시 와서 파리에서 제가 부인을 위해 해 드릴 일이 없는지 여쭙겠습니다.\"\n\n\"기억해 두세요.\" 백작 부인이 말했다. \"제가 지금 머무는 곳은 리볼리 거리 22번지이고, 토요일 저녁마다 친구분들을 맞이한답니다. 자, 이제 두 분 다 미리 알려 드렸어요.\"\n\n두 젊은이는 인사를 하고 칸막이석을 나섰다. 자기들 자리에 이르자, 일층 일반석 관객 전체가 일어서서 예전에 러시아 대사가 쓰던 칸막이석 쪽으로 시선을 던지고 있었다. 서른다섯에서 마흔쯤 되어 보이는, 새까만 옷을 차려입은 남자가 막 들어선 참이었고, 동방식으로 차려입은 젊은 여인이 함께였다. 그 여인은 빼어나게 아름다웠고, 화려하고 호사스러운 옷차림이 모든 이의 시선을 끌었다.\n\n\"이런,\" 알베르가 말했다. \"몬테크리스토와 그의 그리스 여인이로군!\"\n\n그 낯선 두 사람은 과연 백작과 하이데였다. 잠시 후 젊은 처녀는 극장 전체의 주목을 끌었고, 칸막이석에 든 사람들조차 몸을 앞으로 기울여 그녀의 화려한 다이아몬드를 살펴보았다.\n\n2막은 끊임없는 웅성거림 속에서 흘러갔다. 무언가 거대하고 만인의 관심을 끄는 사건이 벌어졌음을 알리는, 나직하게 깔린 속삭임이었다. 모든 눈, 모든 생각이 그 젊고 아름다운 여인에게 쏠려 있었다. 그녀의 눈부신 의상과 찬란한 보석은 더없이 비범한 볼거리였다.\n\n이 무렵 당글라르 부인은 2막이 끝나 막이 내리는 즉시 알베르를 자기 칸막이석에서 보고 싶다는 뜻을 분명한 신호로 전했다. 그토록 분명하게 건네진 초대를 모르세르의 예의나 분별이 차마 외면하게 두지는 않았다. 그래서 그는 막이 끝나자 남작 부인에게로 갔다.\n\n두 부인에게 인사를 한 뒤, 그는 드브레에게 손을 내밀었다. 남작 부인은 더없이 다정하게 그를 맞이했고, 외제니는 늘 그렇듯 차갑게 그를 대했다.\n\n\"여보게,\" 드브레가 말했다. \"마침 딱 좋은 때에 왔네. 여기 부인께서 백작에 관한 질문으로 나를 들볶고 계시지 뭔가. 부인은 내가 그자의 출생, 학력, 가문, 어디서 왔고 어디로 가는지까지 다 말해 줄 수 있다고 우기신다네. 나는 칼리오스트로의 제자도 아니니 도무지 그럴 수가 없었지. 그래서 곤경에서 빠져나오려고 이렇게 말했네. '모르세르에게 물어보십시오. 그자는 자기가 사랑하는 몬테크리스토 이야기라면 손바닥 들여다보듯 훤히 꿰고 있으니까요.' 그랬더니 부인께서 자네를 보고 싶다고 하셨네.\"\n\n\"거의 믿기지 않는 일 아닌가요.\" 당글라르 부인이 말했다. \"적어도 오십만 프랑이나 되는 비밀 정보비를 마음대로 주무르는 분이 그토록 아는 게 없다니 말이에요.\"\n\n\"분명히 말씀드리는데요, 부인.\" 뤼시앵이 말했다. \"제가 정말로 그만한 돈을 손에 쥐고 있다면, 몬테크리스토 백작에 관한 시시콜콜한 정보를 캐내느라 애쓰는 것보다는 더 보람 있게 쓸 겁니다. 제 눈에 그자의 유일한 장점이라곤 나바브의 두 배쯤 부자라는 것뿐이거든요. 어쨌든 그 일은 모르세르에게 넘겼으니, 부디 그와 마음껏 정리하시지요. 저로서는 백작이나 그의 알 수 없는 짓거리 따위엔 도통 관심이 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"제게 머리마다 오천 프랑짜리 다이아몬드를 넷씩 단 삼만 이천 프랑짜리 말 두 필을 보내 줄 나바브는 분명 없었을 거예요.\"\n\n\"그분은 다이아몬드에 미친 모양입니다.\" 모르세르가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"정말이지 제 생각엔, 그분은 포템킨처럼 주머니마다 다이아몬드를 채워 두는 것 같아요. 엄지 동자가 부싯돌을 흘리듯, 길에 뿌리고 다니려고 말입니다.\"\n\n\"어쩌면 어디서 광산이라도 찾아냈는지 모르죠.\" 당글라르 부인이 말했다. \"그분이 남작의 은행에 무제한 신용 거래 명령서를 가지고 있다는 거, 아세요?\"\n\n\"몰랐습니다.\" 알베르가 대답했다. \"하지만 충분히 믿을 만한 얘기로군요.\"\n\n\"게다가, 당글라르 씨에게 파리엔 일 년만 머물 작정이라고 했답니다. 그동안 육백만 프랑을 쓸 생각이라면서요.\"\n\n\"그분은 신분을 감추고 여행하는 페르시아의 샤가 분명하군요.\"\n\n\"저 젊은 여인의 빼어난 아름다움을 눈여겨보셨어요, 뤼시앵 씨?\" 외제니가 물었다.\n\n\"정말이지 저는 다른 여인의 아름다움을 아가씨만큼 기꺼이 인정하는 여인을 만나 본 적이 없습니다.\" 뤼시앵이 오페라글라스를 눈에 가져다 대며 대답했다. \"맙소사, 더없이 사랑스러운 여인이군요!\" 그가 내린 평이었다.\n\n\"저 젊은 여인이 누구죠, 모르세르 씨?\" 외제니가 물었다. \"누구 아는 사람 없나요?\"\n\n\"아가씨,\" 알베르가 이 직접적인 물음에 답하며 말했다. \"그 문제에 관해서라면, 또 지금 우리가 화제로 삼고 있는 그 수수께끼 같은 인물에 관한 대부분의 점에 관해서라면, 제가 아주 정확한 정보를 드릴 수 있습니다. 저 젊은 여인은 그리스 사람입니다.\"\n\n\"옷차림을 보고 저도 그쯤은 짐작했어요. 그것밖에 모르신다면, 여기 있는 누구나 자작님만큼은 알고 있겠는걸요.\"\n\n\"제가 그토록 무지한 안내자라니 정말 죄송하군요.\" 모르세르가 대답했다. \"하지만 유감스럽게도 더 전해 드릴 게 없다고 고백할 수밖에요. 아, 잠깐만요, 한 가지 더 아는 게 있습니다. 그녀가 음악가라는 사실이죠. 언젠가 백작과 함께 아침을 들던 날, 구즐라 소리를 들은 적이 있거든요. 그 여인의 손가락이 아니고서야 누가 켰을 리 없는 소리였지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 백작이 손님을 맞이하기도 하나요?\" 당글라르 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"물론입니다. 그것도 더없이 후하게 맞이하지요. 장담합니다.\"\n\n\"당글라르 씨를 설득해서 그분을 무도회나 만찬 같은 데 초대해야겠어요. 그래야 그분도 답례로 우리를 부르지 않을 수 없을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요?\" 드브레가 웃으며 말했다. \"정말로 그분 댁에 가시겠다는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"안 될 게 뭐예요? 남편이 함께 가면 되지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그 수수께끼 같은 백작이 독신이라는 거, 아십니까?\"\n\n\"맞은편을 보시면 그렇지 않다는 충분한 증거가 있지요.\" 남작 부인이 웃으며 그 아름다운 그리스 여인을 가리켰다.\n\n\"아니, 아닙니다!\" 드브레가 외쳤다. \"저 여인은 그의 아내가 아니에요. 그자가 직접 우리에게 자기 노예라고 했지요. 모르세르, 자네 집에서 아침 들 때 그가 그렇게 말한 거 기억 안 나나?\"\n\n\"그래요,\" 남작 부인이 말했다. \"노예라 해도, 저 여인은 영락없이 공주 같은 분위기와 자태를 지니고 있군요.\"\n\n\"『아라비안나이트』에 나올 법한 공주죠.\"\n\n\"좋으실 대로요. 하지만 말해 보세요, 뤼시앵. 공주를 공주답게 만드는 게 뭐죠? 바로 다이아몬드예요. 그런데 저 여인은 온통 다이아몬드로 덮여 있잖아요.\"\n\n\"제 눈엔 좀 과해 보이는데요.\" 외제니가 한마디 했다. \"좀 덜 걸쳤더라면 훨씬 보기 좋았을 거예요. 그러면 곱게 빠진 목과 손목도 볼 수 있었을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"저것 좀 봐, 예술가 기질이 다 드러나는구나!\" 당글라르 부인이 외쳤다. \"가엾은 우리 외제니, 너는 예술을 향한 그 열정을 좀 감추어야 한단다.\"\n\n\"저는 아름다운 건 뭐든 찬미해요.\" 젊은 아가씨가 대꾸했다.\n\n\"백작은 어떻게 보십니까?\" 드브레가 물었다. \"제 잘생김의 기준으로 보면 그리 나쁘진 않던데요.\"\n\n\"백작이라.\" 외제니가 마치 그를 더 일찍 살펴볼 생각은 하지도 못했다는 듯 되뇌었다. \"백작이요? 아, 그분은 너무 끔찍하게 창백하던데요.\"\n\n\"저도 같은 생각입니다.\" 모르세르가 말했다. \"그리고 바로 그 창백함의 비밀이야말로 우리가 알아내고 싶은 거지요. G 백작 부인은 그분이 흡혈귀라고 우기던걸요.\"\n\n\"그럼 G 백작 부인이 파리로 돌아왔단 말인가요?\" 남작 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"엄마, 저 사람이에요?\" 외제니가 물었다. \"우리 거의 맞은편에, 곱고 풍성한 금발을 늘어뜨린 저분이요?\"\n\n\"그래.\" 당글라르 부인이 말했다. \"저 사람이 바로 그 부인이야. 모르세르, 자네가 어떻게 하면 좋을지 말해 줄까?\"\n\n\"분부만 하십시오, 부인.\"\n\n\"그럼 가서 자네의 그 몬테크리스토 백작을 우리에게 데려오게.\"\n\n\"무엇 하러요?\" 외제니가 물었다.\n\n\"무엇 하러라니? 당연히 그분과 이야기를 나누려고지. 너는 정말 그분을 만나고 싶지 않니?\"\n\n\"조금도요.\" 외제니가 대답했다.\n\n\"별난 아이로구나.\" 남작 부인이 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"아마 그분은 제 발로 올 겁니다.\" 모르세르가 말했다. \"저것 보세요, 부인. 그분이 부인을 알아보고 인사하는군요.\"\n\n남작 부인이 더없이 환하고 우아한 태도로 인사를 받았다.\n\n\"좋습니다.\" 모르세르가 말했다. \"기왕이면 제가 너그러운 마음으로, 부인의 뜻을 받들기 위해 자리를 박차고 나서지요. 그럼 이만. 가서 그분과 이야기를 나눌 방법이 있는지 알아보겠습니다.\"\n\n\"곧장 그분 칸막이석으로 가게. 그게 가장 간단한 방법일 테니.\"\n\n\"하지만 저는 인사를 나눈 적이 없는데요.\"\n\n\"누구한테 인사를 나눠?\"\n\n\"그 아름다운 그리스 여인한테요.\"\n\n\"그 여인은 노예일 뿐이라면서요?\"\n\n\"그건 부인이 그 여인을 여왕, 적어도 공주라고 우기시기 때문이죠. 아니요, 제가 부인 곁을 떠나는 걸 그분이 보면, 직접 나오리라 기대합니다.\"\n\n\"그럴 수도 있겠지. 가 보게.\"\n\n\"가 봅니다.\" 알베르가 작별 인사를 하며 말했다.\n\n그가 백작의 칸막이석을 막 지나치려는데 문이 열리며 몬테크리스토가 밖으로 나왔다. 복도에 서 있던 알리에게 몇 가지 지시를 내린 뒤, 백작은 알베르의 팔을 잡았다. 알리는 칸막이석 문을 조심스레 닫고 그 앞을 막아섰고, 그 사이 구경꾼들 한 무리가 그 누비아인 주위로 몰려들었다.\n\n\"정말이지,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"파리는 묘한 도시이고, 파리 사람들은 아주 별난 족속이군요. 가엾은 알리 주위에 몰려든 저 사람들 좀 보십시오. 알리도 저들 못지않게 어리둥절해하고 있지요. 정말이지 알리가 저들이 여태껏 본 유일한 누비아인이라고 여길 만하군요. 그런데 장담컨대, 프랑스인이 튀니스든 콘스탄티노플이든 바그다드든 카이로든 사람들 앞에 나선다 해도 저런 대접은 받지 않을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그건 동방 사람들이 분별이 너무 깊어서, 그럴 가치도 없는 것에 시간과 관심을 허비하지 않기 때문이지요. 하지만 알리에 관해서라면, 그가 끄는 관심은 순전히 그가 백작님의 시종이기 때문이라는 걸 장담할 수 있습니다. 백작님이야말로 지금 이 순간 파리에서 가장 유명하고 가장 유행을 타는 인물이시니까요.\"\n\n\"정말입니까? 그래, 무엇이 제게 그토록 우쭐한 영예를 안겨 주었답니까?\"\n\n\"무엇이라니요? 바로 백작님 자신이지요! 천 루이짜리 말을 거저 주시고, 지체 높고 아름다운 부인들의 목숨을 구하시고, 블랙 소령이라는 이름으로 마멋만큼이나 자그마한 꼬마들이 모는 순종마를 출전시키시고, 게다가 그 황금 우승 트로피를 거머쥐고도 거기에 아무 값어치도 두지 않으시고는 제일 먼저 떠오른 미인에게 줘 버리시고!\"\n\n\"대체 누가 자네 머릿속에 그런 허튼소리를 잔뜩 채워 넣었답니까?\"\n\n\"우선은 당글라르 부인한테서 들었습니다. 그분은, 그러고 보니, 백작님을 자기 칸막이석에서 보거나, 아니면 백작님이 거기 계신 모습을 남들에게 보여 주고 싶어 안달이 나 있지요. 둘째로는 보샹의 신문에서 알았고, 셋째로는 제 상상에서요. 아니, 정체를 숨기고 싶으셨다면 어째서 말 이름을 밤파라고 지으셨습니까?\"\n\n\"그건 분명 부주의한 짓이었지요.\" 백작이 대답했다. \"그런데 말씀해 보십시오, 모르세르 백작은 오페라에 통 안 오십니까? 한참 찾아봤는데 보이질 않는군요.\"\n\n\"오늘 밤 오실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"극장 어느 자리에요?\"\n\n\"남작 부인의 칸막이석일 겁니다.\"\n\n\"부인과 함께 있는 그 매력적인 젊은 여인이 따님인가요?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"축하드립니다.\"\n\n모르세르가 미소 지었다.\n\n\"그 얘기는 언제 따로 길게 나누지요.\" 그가 말했다. \"그건 그렇고, 음악은 어떻게 들으셨습니까?\"\n\n\"무슨 음악이요?\"\n\n\"왜요, 방금까지 듣고 계시던 음악 말입니다.\"\n\n\"아, 인간 작곡가가 만들고, 작고한 디오게네스의 말을 빌리자면 깃털 없는 두 발 짐승들이 부르는 작품치고는 그런대로 괜찮더군요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 백작님께서는 마음만 먹으면 천국의 일곱 합창대가 들려주는 천사 같은 선율도 즐기실 수 있다는 말씀처럼 들리는군요?\"\n\n\"어느 정도는 옳은 말입니다. 죽을 운명의 귀로는 여태껏 들어 본 적 없는, 가락에 더없이 절묘하게 맞추어진 소리를 듣고 싶을 땐, 저는 잠을 잡니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 여기서 주무십시오, 백작님. 조건이 안성맞춤이지 않습니까. 오페라가 달리 무엇 하러 생겨났겠습니까?\"\n\n\"아니요, 됐습니다. 댁의 오케스트라는 너무 시끄럽군요. 제가 말한 식으로 잠들려면 완벽한 고요와 정적이 필요하고, 그다음엔 어떤 준비가 필요하지요……\"\n\n\"알겠습니다. 그 유명한 해시시 말씀이군요!\"\n\n\"바로 그겁니다. 그러니 자작, 음악으로 흥을 돋우고 싶으실 때면 언제든 저와 함께 밤참을 드시지요.\"\n\n\"이미 백작님과 아침을 들 때 그 호사를 누려 봤는걸요.\" 모르세르가 말했다.\n\n\"로마에서 말입니까?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇다면 하이데의 구즐라를 들으셨겠군요. 그 가엾은 망명자는 곧잘 고향의 가락을 제게 켜 주며 따분한 시간을 달래곤 하지요.\"\n\n모르세르는 그 화제를 더 이어 가지 않았고, 몬테크리스토도 말없는 상념에 잠겼다.\n\n그 순간 막이 오른다는 종이 울렸다.\n\n\"이만 자리를 비켜 줘야겠습니다.\" 백작이 자기 칸막이석 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며 말했다.\n\n\"네? 가시려고요?\"\n\n\"부디 G 백작 부인께, 그 친구인 흡혈귀가 보내는 온갖 다정한 인사를 전해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"그럼 남작 부인께는 무슨 전갈을 전할까요!\"\n\n\"허락하신다면, 저녁 동안 찾아뵙고 인사드리는 영광을 누리겠다고요.\"\n\n3막이 시작되었다. 그 막이 진행되는 동안 모르세르 백작은 약속대로 당글라르 부인의 칸막이석에 모습을 드러냈다. 모르세르 백작은 공공의 오락장에서 관심이나 호기심을 불러일으킬 만한 인물이 아니었다. 그래서 그의 등장은 방금 자리에 든 그 칸막이석 사람들 말고는 아무도 눈여겨보지 않았다.\n\n그러나 몬테크리스토의 날카로운 눈은 그가 오는 것을 놓치지 않았고, 의미심장하면서도 옅은 미소가 그의 입가를 스쳤다. 하이데는 영혼이 온통 무대 위 일에 쏠려 있는 듯했다. 꾸밈없는 모든 천성이 그러하듯, 그녀는 눈과 귀를 즐겁게 하는 것이라면 무엇에나 빠져들었다.\n\n3막은 여느 때처럼 흘러갔다. 노블레 양, 쥘리아 양, 르루 양이 으레 추는 피루엣을 선보였고, 로베르는 어김없이 그라나다 왕자에게 결투를 신청했으며, 이사벨라 공주의 부왕은 딸의 손을 잡고 위풍당당한 걸음으로 무대를 누비며 벨벳 가운과 망토의 풍성한 주름을 한껏 뽐냈다. 그런 뒤 다시 막이 내렸고, 관객들은 극장에서 복도와 살롱으로 쏟아져 나왔다.\n\n백작은 자기 칸막이석을 나섰고, 잠시 후 당글라르 남작 부인에게 인사를 건넸다. 부인은 반가움과 놀라움이 뒤섞인 탄성을 억누르지 못했다.\n\n\"어서 오세요, 백작님!\" 그가 들어서자 부인이 외쳤다. \"정말이지 백작님을 뵙고 싶어 안달이 났답니다. 글로는 도무지 다 표현하지 못한 감사의 말을 직접 입으로 전하고 싶어서요.\"\n\n\"그토록 사소한 일이 부인의 기억에 자리를 차지할 가치는 없을 텐데요. 믿어 주십시오, 부인, 저는 그 일을 까맣게 잊고 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만, 백작님, 그 어진 선물을 주신 바로 다음 날, 제 소중한 벗 빌포르 부인의 목숨을 구해 주셨다는 건 그렇게 쉽게 잊히는 일이 아니랍니다. 게다가 그 부인을 위태롭게 한 게 바로 백작님의 너그러움으로 제게 돌아온 그 말들이었으니까요.\"\n\n\"적어도 이번만큼은 부인의 감사를 받을 자격이 없습니다. 빌포르 부인께 그런 도움을 드린 건 제 누비아인 노예 알리였으니까요.\"\n\n\"제 아들을 산적들의 손에서 구해 준 게,\" 모르세르 백작이 물었다. \"그 알리였습니까?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 백작님.\" 몬테크리스토가 장군이 내민 손을 잡으며 대답했다. \"그 일에 관해서라면 저는 마땅히 거리낌 없이 감사를 받겠습니다. 하지만 이미 감사의 뜻을 전하셨고, 빚이 — 정말 빚이라는 게 있었다면 — 다 갚으셨지요. 그런데도 백작님께서 자꾸 그 일로 돌아오시니 저로서는 거의 민망할 지경입니다. 남작 부인, 따님을 제게 소개해 주시는 영광을 베풀어 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"아, 백작님은 낯선 분이 아니랍니다. 적어도 함자만큼은요.\" 당글라르 부인이 대답했다. \"지난 이삼 일 동안 우리가 백작님 얘기밖에 안 했거든요. 외제니,\" 남작 부인이 딸 쪽으로 돌아서며 말을 이었다. \"이분이 몬테크리스토 백작님이시다.\"\n\n백작이 인사하자, 당글라르 양은 살짝 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"오늘 밤 백작님과 함께 온 젊은 여인이 매력적이던데요.\" 외제니가 말했다. \"따님이신가요?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 아가씨.\" 몬테크리스토가 그 질문의 거리낌 없고 당돌함에 놀라며 말했다. \"제가 맡아 돌보는 가엾고 불행한 그리스 여인입니다.\"\n\n\"이름이 뭐죠?\"\n\n\"하이데입니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다.\n\n\"그리스 여인이라.\" 모르세르 백작이 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"그럼요, 백작님.\" 당글라르 부인이 말했다. \"그런데 말씀해 보세요. 백작님이 그토록 영광스럽고 용감하게 섬기셨다는 알리 테펠리니의 궁정에서, 저보다 더 빼어난 미인이나 더 화려한 의상을 보신 적이 있으신가요?\"\n\n\"제가 제대로 들었는지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"야니나에서 복무하셨다고요?\"\n\n\"파샤 군대의 총감독관이었습니다.\" 모르세르가 대답했다. \"그리고 변변찮으나마 제 재산이 그 이름난 알바니아 수장의 후한 인심 덕이라는 건 비밀도 아니지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 저것 좀 보세요!\" 당글라르 부인이 외쳤다.\n\n\"어디 말입니까?\" 모르세르가 더듬거렸다.\n\n\"저기요.\" 몬테크리스토가 백작의 어깨에 팔을 두르고 함께 칸막이석 앞으로 몸을 기울이며 말했다. 마침 하이데가 보호자를 찾아 눈으로 극장을 살피다가, 모르세르의 얼굴 가까이 있는 그의 창백한 이목구비를 알아본 순간이었다. 마치 젊은 처녀가 메두사의 머리를 본 것 같았다. 그녀는 자기가 본 것이 진짜인지 확인하려는 듯 몸을 앞으로 기울였다가, 희미한 비명을 지르며 자리에 몸을 던지듯 기댔다. 그 소리를 알리 곁에 있던 사람들이 들었고, 알리는 즉시 칸막이석 문을 열었다.\n\n\"아니, 백작님.\" 외제니가 외쳤다. \"보호하시는 분께 무슨 일이 생겼나요? 갑자기 몸이 안 좋아진 것 같은데요.\"\n\n\"아마 그럴 겁니다.\" 백작이 대답했다. \"하지만 저 아이 때문에 놀라지는 마십시오. 하이데는 신경이 섬세하게 짜여 있어서, 꽃향기에도 유난히 예민하답니다. 아니, 어떤 꽃은 곁에 가져오기만 해도 까무러치게 만들지요. 하지만,\" 몬테크리스토가 주머니에서 작은 약병을 꺼내며 말을 이었다. \"저에게는 틀림없는 약이 있습니다.\"\n\n그렇게 말하며 그는 남작 부인과 그 딸에게 인사하고, 드브레와 백작과 작별의 악수를 나눈 뒤 당글라르 부인의 칸막이석을 떠났다. 하이데에게 돌아와 보니 그녀는 여전히 몹시 창백했다. 그녀는 그를 보자마자 그의 손을 움켜쥐었다. 그녀의 두 손은 축축하고 얼음장처럼 차가웠다.\n\n\"저기서 누구와 이야기를 나누고 계셨어요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"모르세르 백작과.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"네 위대하신 아버지를 섬겼고, 그분 덕에 재산을 얻었다는구나.\"\n\n\"비열한 자!\" 하이데가 분노로 눈을 번뜩이며 외쳤다. \"그자가 제 아버지를 터키인들에게 팔아넘겼고, 그자가 뽐내는 재산은 그 배신의 대가였어요! 모르고 계셨나요, 주인님?\"\n\n\"그 비슷한 이야기를 에피루스에서 들은 적은 있다.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"하지만 자세한 사정은 아직 모르지. 네가 내게 들려주려무나, 얘야. 분명 기이하면서도 흥미로운 이야기일 테니.\"\n\n\"네, 네. 하지만 이만 가요. 저 끔찍한 자 곁에 오래 있다가는 죽을 것만 같아요.\"\n\n그렇게 말하며 하이데는 일어나, 진주와 산호로 수놓은 흰 캐시미어 부르누스를 둘러쓰고는, 4막의 막이 오르는 순간 서둘러 칸막이석을 떠났다.\n\n\"보셨어요?\" G 백작 부인이 자기 곁으로 돌아온 알베르에게 말했다. \"저 남자는 하나같이 남들과 다르게 행동하네요. 「로베르 르 디아블」 3막은 그토록 경건하게 듣더니, 4막이 시작되자 자리를 떠 버리잖아요.\"\n\n# Chapter 54. A Flurry in Stocks\n\n이 만남이 있고 며칠 뒤, 알베르 드 모르세르는 샹젤리제에 있는 몬테크리스토 백작의 저택을 찾아갔다. 그 저택은 이미 궁전 같은 면모를 갖추고 있었는데, 백작은 왕후 같은 재력으로 아무리 잠시 머무는 거처라 해도 그런 풍모를 부여할 수 있는 사람이었다. 알베르는 당글라르 부인이 백작에게 전하는 감사 인사를 다시 한번 전하러 온 것이었다. 그 인사는 이미 \"당글라르 남작 부인, 본명 에르민 드 세르비외\"라고 서명된 편지로 백작에게 전달된 바 있었다.\n\n알베르는 뤼시앵 드브레와 함께였다. 뤼시앵은 친구의 대화에 끼어들어 가볍게 몇 마디 칭찬을 보탰는데, 백작은 워낙 사람의 속을 꿰뚫어 보는 재주가 있어 그 말의 출처를 쉽게 짐작했다. 백작은 뤼시앵의 방문이 두 겹의 호기심에서 비롯되었으며, 그 절반 이상이 쇼세당탱 거리에서 나온 것임을 확신했다. 요컨대 당글라르 부인은 3만 프랑짜리 말을 선뜻 내주고, 백만 프랑어치 다이아몬드를 두른 그리스 노예를 데리고 오페라에 가는 사내의 살림살이와 집안 형편을 직접 세세히 살펴볼 수 없었으므로, 늘 자기 대신 눈 노릇을 해 주던 사람을 보내 그 헤아릴 수 없는 인물의 생활상을 충실히 전해 듣고자 한 것이었다. 그러나 백작은 뤼시앵의 방문과 남작 부인의 호기심 사이에 조금이라도 연관이 있으리라고는 전혀 눈치채지 못한 듯 보였다.\n\n\"당글라르 남작과 계속 연락을 주고받고 계십니까?\" 백작이 알베르 드 모르세르에게 물었다.\n\n\"네, 백작님. 제가 말씀드린 거 기억하시지요?\"\n\n\"그럼 그쪽 일은 여전히 그대로입니까?\"\n\n\"그 어느 때보다 확정된 일이지요.\" 뤼시앵이 말했다. 그러고는 지금 자기가 해야 할 말은 그게 전부라고 여겼는지, 외알 안경을 눈에 맞춰 끼우고 금장식 지팡이 끝을 깨물며 방 안을 한 바퀴 돌기 시작했다. 무기며 그림들을 살펴보면서였다.\n\n\"아,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그 일이 그렇게 빨리 매듭지어지리라곤 생각지 못했군요.\"\n\n\"오, 일이란 우리가 거들지 않아도 제 갈 길을 가는 법이지요. 우리가 그 일을 잊고 있는 동안에도 일은 정해진 순서대로 흘러가고, 다시 우리가 거기에 눈을 돌렸을 때는 목표를 향해 얼마나 나아갔는지 놀라게 되는 겁니다. 제 아버지와 당글라르 씨는 스페인에서 함께 복무하셨습니다. 아버지는 군에 계셨고 당글라르 씨는 병참부에 계셨지요. 혁명으로 몰락한 제 아버지와, 애초에 물려받은 재산이라곤 한 푼도 없던 당글라르 씨, 두 분이 각자 다른 재산의 토대를 닦은 곳이 바로 거기였습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇군요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그건 제가 찾아갔을 때 당글라르 씨가 이야기해 준 것 같습니다. 그리고,\" 그는 화첩을 넘기고 있는 뤼시앵에게 곁눈질을 보내며 말을 이었다. \"외제니 양은 어여쁘더군요. 그 이름이 맞지요, 아마?\"\n\n\"무척 어여쁘지요. 아니, 차라리 무척 아름답다고 해야겠지요.\" 알베르가 대답했다. \"하지만 제가 알아보지 못하는 부류의 아름다움입니다. 제가 배은망덕한 놈이지요.\"\n\n\"마치 벌써 그 아가씨의 남편이라도 된 것처럼 말씀하시는군요.\"\n\n\"아.\" 알베르가 이번에는 자기가 주위를 둘러보며 뤼시앵이 뭘 하고 있나 살폈다.\n\n\"솔직히,\" 몬테크리스토가 목소리를 낮추며 말했다. \"제가 보기엔 이 결혼에 그리 열의가 있어 보이지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"당글라르 양은 제게 너무 부유합니다.\" 모르세르가 대답했다. \"그게 저를 겁나게 하지요.\"\n\n\"허, 거참,\" 몬테크리스토가 소리쳤다. \"그거 참 그럴듯한 이유로군요. 당신도 부유하지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"제 아버지의 수입이 연 5만 프랑쯤 됩니다. 제가 결혼하면 만 프랑이나 만이천 프랑쯤 주실 테고요.\"\n\n\"그게, 특히 파리에서는, 큰돈으로 여겨지지 않을지도 모르지요.\" 백작이 말했다. \"하지만 모든 것이 재산에 달린 건 아닙니다. 좋은 가문의 이름을 지니고 사회에서 높은 지위를 차지하는 것도 훌륭한 일이지요. 당신의 이름은 명망 높고, 당신의 지위는 더없이 훌륭합니다. 게다가 모르세르 백작님은 군인이시지요. 바야르의 청렴함이 뒤게클랭의 가난함과 하나로 어우러진 모습을 보는 건 흐뭇한 일입니다. 사욕 없음이야말로 고결한 검이 빛날 수 있는 가장 밝은 광채니까요. 저로서는 당글라르 양과의 결합이 더없이 적절하다고 봅니다. 그녀는 당신을 부유하게 해 줄 것이고, 당신은 그녀를 고귀하게 해 줄 테니까요.\"\n\n알베르는 고개를 저으며 생각에 잠긴 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"아직 다른 무언가가 있습니다.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"솔직히,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"부유하면서도 아름다운 아가씨를 마다하시는 이유를 저는 잘 이해하기가 어렵군요.\"\n\n\"오,\" 모르세르가 말했다. \"이 거부감이, 거부감이라 부를 수 있다면 말이지요, 제 쪽에만 있는 게 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 어디서 비롯된 거지요? 아버님이 이 결혼을 바라신다고 하셨으니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"반대하시는 분은 제 어머니입니다. 어머니는 명료하고 예리한 판단력을 지니신 분인데, 이 혼사를 달가워하지 않으십니다. 까닭은 잘 모르겠지만, 당글라르 가문에 어떤 편견을 품고 계신 듯합니다.\"\n\n\"아,\" 백작이 다소 억지스러운 어조로 말했다. \"그건 쉽게 설명할 수 있겠군요. 귀족적 품위와 세련됨 그 자체이신 모르세르 백작 부인께서, 당신의 결혼으로 비천한 출신과 인척이 되는 걸 탐탁지 않게 여기시는 거지요. 충분히 자연스러운 일입니다.\"\n\n\"그게 어머니의 이유인지는 모르겠습니다.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"하지만 한 가지는 확실히 압니다. 이 결혼이 성사되면 어머니가 무척 비참해지시리라는 것이지요. 여섯 주 전에 이 일을 의논하고 매듭짓기 위한 모임이 있을 예정이었습니다. 그런데 제가 갑자기 몸이 좋지 않아서요……\"\n\n\"진짜로요?\" 백작이 미소 지으며 말을 가로챘다.\n\n\"오, 진짜였습니다, 의심할 바 없이 근심 때문에요. 어쨌든 그 일은 두 달 미뤄졌습니다. 서두를 일은 없으니까요, 아시다시피. 저는 아직 스물한 살도 안 됐고, 외제니는 겨우 열일곱이니까요. 그런데 그 두 달이 다음 주면 끝납니다. 해치워야 하는 거지요. 백작님, 제 마음이 얼마나 시달리는지 상상도 못 하실 겁니다. 이 모든 것에서 벗어나 계신 백작님은 얼마나 행복하신지요!\"\n\n\"그럼, 당신은 왜 자유로워질 수 없는 겁니까? 무엇이 당신을 가로막고 있지요?\"\n\n\"오, 제가 당글라르 양과 결혼하지 않으면 아버지께서 너무 크게 실망하실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 결혼하시지요.\" 백작이 의미심장하게 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다.\n\n\"네.\" 모르세르가 대답했다. \"하지만 그러면 어머니가 진짜 슬픔에 빠지실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 결혼하지 마시지요.\" 백작이 말했다.\n\n\"음, 두고 보겠습니다. 어떻게 하는 게 가장 좋을지 곰곰이 생각해 봐야지요. 조언을 해 주시겠지요, 그렇지요? 가능하다면 이 거북한 처지에서 저를 빼내 주시고요. 사랑하는 어머니를 괴롭히느니, 차라리 백작님 노여움을 살 위험을 무릅쓰겠습니다.\"\n\n몬테크리스토가 고개를 돌렸다. 이 마지막 말에 마음이 움직인 듯 보였다.\n\n\"아.\" 그가 드브레에게 말을 건넸다. 드브레는 살롱의 가장 안쪽 끝에 놓인 안락의자에 몸을 파묻은 채, 오른손에는 연필을, 왼손에는 장부를 들고 있었다. \"거기서 뭘 하고 계십니까? 푸생을 본떠 스케치라도 하시는 건가요?\"\n\n\"오, 아닙니다.\" 차분한 대답이 돌아왔다. \"저는 예술을 너무 사랑해서 그런 건 감히 시도하지 못합니다. 자그마한 셈을 좀 하고 있었지요.\"\n\n\"셈을요?\"\n\n\"네. 계산을 하고 있었습니다. 그런데, 모르세르, 이건 자네하고도 간접적으로 관련이 있는 일이야. 당글라르 상회가 지난번 아이티 국채 폭등으로 얼마나 벌었을지 계산하고 있었지요. 사흘 만에 206에서 409로 올랐는데, 그 신중한 은행가는 206에 사 뒀거든요. 그러니 30만 리브르는 벌었을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그건 그 사람 최대 수확도 아니야.\" 모르세르가 말했다. \"작년에 스페인 채권으로 백만을 벌지 않았나?\"\n\n\"이보게,\" 뤼시앵이 말했다. \"여기 몬테크리스토 백작님이 계시니, 이탈리아 사람들 말마따나 이렇게 말씀하실 걸세.\n\n'돈과 거룩함, 그 절반의 절반.'\n\n저는 누가 그런 이야기를 하면 그저 어깨를 으쓱하고 아무 말도 하지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"그런데 아이티 채권 얘기를 하고 계셨지요?\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 아이티 채권이요. 그건 또 전혀 다른 얘기지요! 아이티 채권은 프랑스 증권 투기의 에카르테 같은 겁니다. 부요트를 좋아할 수도, 휘스트에 빠질 수도, 보스턴에 황홀해할 수도 있지만, 결국엔 다 싫증이 나는 법이지요. 그런데 우리는 언제나 에카르테로 돌아옵니다. 그건 단순한 노름이 아니라 전채 요리거든요! 당글라르 씨는 어제 405에 팔아서 30만 프랑을 챙겼습니다. 만약 오늘까지 기다렸더라면 시세가 205로 떨어졌을 테고, 30만 프랑을 버는 대신 2만이나 2만5천 프랑을 잃었을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그런데 무엇 때문에 409에서 206으로 갑자기 떨어진 건가요?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다. \"저는 이런 증권 투기의 속내라곤 도무지 모르는 사람이라서요.\"\n\n\"왜냐하면,\" 알베르가 웃으며 말했다. \"한 가지 소식 뒤에 또 다른 소식이 잇따르는데, 그 둘이 종종 크게 어긋나기 때문이지요.\"\n\n\"아,\" 백작이 말했다. \"보아하니 당글라르 씨는 하루에 30만 프랑을 벌거나 잃는 노름에 익숙한 모양이군요. 엄청나게 부유한 사람임이 틀림없습니다.\"\n\n\"노름을 하는 건 그 사람이 아닙니다!\" 뤼시앵이 소리쳤다. \"당글라르 부인이지요. 정말 대담한 분입니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 이성적인 사람인 자네, 게다가 그런 소식이 얼마나 믿을 게 못 되는지 잘 아는 자네가, 소식의 원천에 있는 자네가, 당연히 그걸 막아야 하지 않나.\" 모르세르가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"남편도 못 막는 걸 내가 어떻게 막겠나?\" 뤼시앵이 물었다. \"남작 부인의 성격을 알지 않나. 아무도 그분을 좌우하지 못해. 자기 하고 싶은 대로 하시는 분이지.\"\n\n\"아, 내가 자네 처지라면……\" 알베르가 말했다.\n\n\"그러면?\"\n\n\"내가 그분을 바로잡아 드리겠네. 장차 사위 될 사람에게 봉사하는 셈이 될 테니.\"\n\n\"어떻게 그러겠다는 건가?\"\n\n\"아, 그거야 아주 쉽지. 본때를 보여 드리는 거야.\"\n\n\"본때를?\"\n\n\"그래. 장관 비서라는 자네 지위 덕에 정치 소식에 관해서는 자네 말이 큰 권위를 갖지 않나. 자네가 입을 열기만 하면 증권 중개인들이 즉각 그 말을 속기로 받아 적으니까. 그분께 십만 프랑을 잃게 해 드리면 신중함을 배우실 거야.\"\n\n\"무슨 말인지 모르겠는데.\" 뤼시앵이 더듬거렸다.\n\n\"그래도 아주 분명한 얘기야.\" 젊은이가 조금도 꾸밈없는 천진함으로 대답했다. \"어느 화창한 아침에 그분께 전대미문의 소식을, 자네만 손에 쥐고 있는 어떤 전보 같은 걸 흘려 주는 거야. 예를 들어 어제 앙리 4세가 가브리엘의 집에서 목격됐다는 식으로 말이지. 그러면 시세가 치솟을 테고, 그분은 잔뜩 사들이실 거야. 그러고는 이튿날 보샹이 자기 신문에 '평소 정통한 소식통이라는 일부 인사들이 어제 국왕께서 가브리엘의 집에서 목격되었다고 퍼뜨린 소문은 전혀 근거가 없다. 우리는 폐하께서 퐁뇌프를 한 발짝도 벗어나지 않으셨음을 단언할 수 있다'라고 발표하면 그분은 틀림없이 잃으시겠지.\"\n\n뤼시앵이 살짝 미소를 지었다. 몬테크리스토는 겉으로는 무심한 척하면서도 이 대화의 한 마디도 놓치지 않았고, 그 꿰뚫는 눈으로 비서의 당황한 태도에서 감춰진 비밀까지 읽어 냈다. 이 당황함을 알베르는 전혀 알아채지 못했지만, 뤼시앵은 그 때문에 방문을 서둘러 끝냈다. 그는 분명 마음이 편치 않았다. 백작은 그와 작별하면서 낮은 목소리로 뭐라 말을 건넸고, 그는 \"기꺼이요, 백작님. 받아들이겠습니다\"라고 대답했다. 백작은 젊은 모르세르에게로 돌아왔다.\n\n\"가만히 생각해 보니,\" 백작이 그에게 말했다. \"드브레 씨가 있는 자리에서 장모님 이야기를 그렇게 한 건 잘못한 일이 아니었나요?\"\n\n\"백작님,\" 모르세르가 말했다. \"그 호칭을 그리 성급하게 갖다 붙이지 말아 주시기를 부탁드립니다.\"\n\n\"자, 조금도 과장 없이 말씀해 보십시오. 어머님이 정말로 이 결혼을 그토록 싫어하십니까?\"\n\n\"어찌나 싫어하시는지 남작 부인은 저희 집에 좀처럼 오시지 않고, 제 어머니도 평생 당글라르 부인을 두 번도 채 찾아가신 적이 없을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 백작이 말했다. \"제가 솔직하게 말씀드릴 용기가 나는군요. 당글라르 씨는 제 거래 은행가입니다. 그리고 빌포르 씨는, 우연한 행운 덕에 제가 베풀게 된 어떤 도움에 대한 답례로 저를 친절로 넘치게 대해 주었지요. 이 모든 것으로 미루어 저는 만찬과 연회의 눈사태를 예감합니다. 그래서 이걸 당연하게 여기지 않으려고, 또 그들보다 한발 앞서고자, 당신만 괜찮다면 당글라르 부부와 빌포르 부부를 오퇴유에 있는 제 시골집으로 초대할 생각을 했습니다. 그런데 만약 이 만찬에 당신과 모르세르 백작 부부까지 초대한다면, 마치 혼담을 위한 모임처럼 보이게 만드는 셈이 되겠지요. 적어도 모르세르 백작 부인께서는 그 일을 그렇게 보실 겁니다. 더구나 당글라르 남작이 영광스럽게도 따님을 데려온다면 더욱 그럴 테고요. 그렇게 되면 어머님이 저를 미워하실 텐데, 저는 결코 그걸 바라지 않습니다. 오히려 그분의 존경을 받고 싶지요.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 백작님,\" 모르세르가 말했다. \"저에게 이토록 솔직하게 대해 주셔서 진심으로 감사드립니다. 제안하신 대로 저를 빼 주시는 것을 고맙게 받아들이겠습니다. 어머니의 호감을 바라신다고 하셨는데, 분명히 말씀드리지만 그 호감은 이미 더없이 각별하게 백작님의 것입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각하십니까?\" 몬테크리스토가 관심을 보이며 말했다.\n\n\"오, 확신합니다. 지난번에 백작님이 자리를 뜨신 지 한 시간 동안 저희가 백작님 이야기를 했거든요. 하지만 하던 이야기로 돌아가지요. 어머니가 백작님의 이 배려를 아신다면, 제가 감히 말씀드리겠지만, 분명 백작님께 무척 감사해하실 겁니다. 물론 그만큼 제 아버지는 화를 내시겠지요.\" 백작이 웃었다.\n\n\"음,\" 그가 모르세르에게 말했다. \"하지만 화내실 분이 아버님 한 분만은 아닐 것 같군요. 당글라르 부부는 저를 아주 무례한 사람으로 여길 겁니다. 제가 당신과 친하다는 걸, 사실 당신이 제 파리 지인 가운데 가장 오래된 분이라는 걸 그들도 아는데, 제 집에서 당신을 보지 못하면 분명 왜 당신을 초대하지 않았느냐고 물을 테니까요. 그러니 그럴듯해 보이는 선약을 꼭 하나 마련해 두었다가, 그 사실을 글로 한 줄 적어 제게 알려 주십시오. 은행가들에게는 글로 된 문서가 아니면 아무것도 유효하지 않다는 걸 아시지요.\"\n\n\"그보다 더 좋은 방법이 있습니다.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"제 어머니가 바닷가에 가고 싶어 하시거든요. 만찬은 며칠로 정하셨습니까?\"\n\n\"토요일입니다.\"\n\n\"오늘이 화요일이군요. 그럼 내일 저녁에 떠나서, 그다음 날이면 트레포르에 가 있겠습니다. 정말이지, 백작님은 사람을 편안하게 해 주는 멋진 재주가 있으십니다.\"\n\n\"과분한 평가입니다. 저는 그저 당신이 흡족할 만한 일을 하고 싶을 뿐, 그게 전부입니다.\"\n\n\"초대장은 언제 보내실 겁니까?\"\n\n\"바로 오늘이요.\"\n\n\"좋습니다. 그럼 곧장 당글라르 씨를 찾아가서, 어머니와 제가 내일 파리를 떠나야 한다고 말하겠습니다. 저는 백작님을 뵙지 못했으니, 만찬에 대해서는 아무것도 모르는 거지요.\"\n\n\"어리석은 분이로군요! 드브레 씨가 방금 제 집에서 당신을 봤다는 걸 잊으셨습니까?\"\n\n\"아, 그렇군요.\"\n\n\"이렇게 정합시다. 제가 당신을 만나 격식 없이 초대했는데, 당신이 곧바로 트레포르에 가야 해서 받아들일 수 없겠다고 대답한 걸로요.\"\n\n\"그럼 그렇게 정리하지요. 그런데 내일 전에 제 어머니를 찾아뵈러 오시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"내일 전에요? 그건 마련하기 어려운 일이겠는데요. 게다가 저는 출발 준비로 한창 정신이 없을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"음, 더 좋은 방법이 있습니다. 백작님은 지금까지는 매력적인 분이셨을 뿐이지만, 제 제안을 받아들이시면 흠모할 만한 분이 되실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그런 숭고한 경지에 이르려면 제가 무얼 해야 합니까?\"\n\n\"오늘 백작님은 바람처럼 자유로우시지요. 저와 함께 저녁을 드시지요. 단출한 자리가 될 겁니다. 백작님과 제 어머니, 그리고 저뿐이니까요. 백작님은 제 어머니를 거의 못 보셨으니, 더 가까이서 살펴보실 기회가 될 겁니다. 어머니는 비범한 분이지요. 다만 한 가지 아쉬운 건, 어머니와 똑같으면서 스무 살쯤 더 젊은 분이 또 없다는 거예요. 그런 분이 있었다면, 정말이지 머지않아 모르세르 백작 부인과 자작 부인이 함께 생겼을 텐데 말입니다. 아버지로 말하자면, 보지 못하실 겁니다. 공무가 있어서 수석 청원위원과 식사하시거든요. 저희는 여행 이야기를 나눌 겁니다. 그리고 온 세상을 두루 다니신 백작님은 모험담을 들려주시는 거지요. 지난번 오페라에서 백작님과 함께 있던, 백작님이 노예라고 부르면서도 공주처럼 대하는 그 아름다운 그리스 여인의 내력도 들려주십시오. 이탈리아어로도, 스페인어로도 이야기합시다. 자, 제 초대를 받아 주십시오. 그러면 어머니가 백작님께 감사할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"천번 감사드립니다.\" 백작이 말했다. \"참으로 고마운 초대입니다만, 받아들일 수 없는 처지라 몹시 유감입니다. 저는 당신이 생각하시는 만큼 그렇게 자유롭지가 않아요. 오히려 더없이 중요한 약속이 있답니다.\"\n\n\"아, 조심하십시오. 방금 백작님께서 저에게, 만찬 초대를 받았을 때 어떻게 그럴듯하게 핑계를 댈 수 있는지 가르쳐 주셨잖습니까. 저는 선약의 증거를 요구합니다. 저는 당글라르 씨 같은 은행가는 아니지만, 그 사람만큼이나 의심이 많거든요.\"\n\n\"증거를 보여 드리지요.\" 백작이 대답하며 종을 울렸다.\n\n\"흠,\" 모르세르가 말했다. \"이걸로 제 어머니와 식사하기를 거절하신 게 두 번째로군요. 어머니를 피하고 싶으신 게 분명합니다.\"\n\n몬테크리스토가 흠칫했다. \"오, 그런 뜻으로 하신 말씀은 아니시겠지요.\" 그가 말했다. \"게다가, 마침 제 말을 뒷받침해 줄 사람이 오는군요.\"\n\n밥티스탱이 들어와 문가에 선 채로 있었다.\n\n\"제가 당신의 방문을 미리 알지 못했지요, 그렇지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"솔직히, 백작님은 워낙 비범한 분이라, 그것까지는 장담 못 하겠는데요.\"\n\n\"어쨌든 당신이 저를 저녁 식사에 초대하리라곤 짐작할 수 없었습니다.\"\n\n\"아마 그러셨겠지요.\"\n\n\"자, 들어 보십시오. 밥티스탱, 오늘 아침 내가 실험실로 자네를 불렀을 때 뭐라고 했지?\"\n\n\"다섯 시 종이 치는 즉시 방문객을 받지 말고 문을 닫으라 하셨습니다.\" 하인이 대답했다.\n\n\"그다음은?\"\n\n\"아, 백작님.\" 알베르가 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 아니, 저는 당신이 제게 씌워 준 그 신비로운 평판을 떨쳐 버리고 싶습니다, 자작. 늘 만프레드 노릇을 하는 건 지겨운 일이에요. 저는 제 삶이 자유롭고 떳떳하기를 바랍니다. 계속하게, 밥티스탱.\"\n\n\"그다음엔 바르톨로메오 카발칸티 소령과 그 아드님 외에는 아무도 들이지 말라 하셨습니다.\"\n\n\"들으셨지요. 바르톨로메오 카발칸티 소령, 이탈리아에서 가장 유서 깊은 귀족 가문에 드는 분이지요. 단테가 『지옥편』 제10곡에서 그 이름을 기린 가문 말입니다, 기억나시지요? 그리고 그 아드님 안드레아가 있는데, 매력적인 청년이고 자작과 나이도 엇비슷하지요. 당신과 같은 작위를 지녔고, 아버지의 막대한 재산에 힘입어 파리 사교계에 막 발을 들이려는 참입니다. 오늘 저녁 소령이 아드님을, 이탈리아 말로 콘티노를 데려올 텐데, 그를 제게 맡기려는 거지요. 그 청년이 그럴 만한 자격이 있음을 보여 준다면, 저는 그의 앞날을 위해 할 수 있는 일을 다 할 생각입니다. 당신도 이 일을 거들어 주시겠지요, 그렇지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"물론입니다. 그럼 이 카발칸티 소령은 백작님의 오랜 벗이신가요?\"\n\n\"전혀 아닙니다. 그는 완벽한 귀족이에요. 무척 정중하고 겸손하며 호감 가는 인물이지요. 아주 오래된 가문의 후손으로 이탈리아에서 늘 볼 수 있는 그런 사람입니다. 저는 피렌체와 볼로냐, 루카에서 그를 여러 번 만났는데, 이번에 파리에 도착했다고 제게 알려 왔어요. 여행길에서 사귄 사람들은 일종의 권리를 주장하는 법이지요. 그들은 어디서든, 당신이 한때 우연히 베풀었던 것과 똑같은 관심을 받기를 기대합니다. 마치 스쳐 가는 한때의 친절이, 여행 중에 우연히 어울리게 된 사람을 향한 지속적인 관심을 불러일으킬 수 있기라도 한 것처럼 말이지요. 이 선량한 카발칸티 소령은 파리를 다시 한번 보러 온 겁니다. 제정 시절 모스크바로 향하던 길에 그저 지나치며 본 게 전부였으니까요. 저는 그에게 훌륭한 만찬을 대접할 테고, 그는 제게 아들을 맡길 것이며, 저는 그를 보살피겠다고 약속할 겁니다. 그러고는 그의 어리석음이 어떤 길로 이끌든 내버려 두는 거지요. 그러면 제 몫은 다한 셈입니다.\"\n\n\"분명히요. 백작님은 본보기가 될 만한 멘토시군요.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"그럼 안녕히 계십시오. 저희는 일요일에 돌아올 겁니다. 그런데 프란츠한테서 소식을 받았습니다.\"\n\n\"그래요? 아직도 이탈리아에서 즐겁게 지내고 있나요?\"\n\n\"그런 것 같습니다. 다만 백작님이 안 계셔서 무척 아쉬워하더군요. 백작님이 로마의 태양이셨다며, 백작님이 안 계시니 모든 게 어둡고 흐려 보인다고요. 비까지 온다고 한 건 아닌지 모르겠네요.\"\n\n\"그럼 저에 대한 그의 평가가 좋게 바뀐 모양이군요?\"\n\n\"아니요. 여전히 백작님을 세상에서 가장 헤아릴 수 없고 신비로운 존재로 여기는 데는 변함이 없습니다.\"\n\n\"매력적인 청년이지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"처음 만나 소개받던 그날 저녁부터 저는 그에게 깊은 관심을 느꼈습니다. 저녁거리를 찾아 헤매던 그를 만나, 제 것을 좀 나누자고 권했었지요. 그가, 제 생각엔, 데피네 장군의 아들이지요?\"\n\n\"맞습니다.\"\n\n\"1815년에 그토록 수치스럽게 암살당한 그분 말이지요?\"\n\n\"보나파르트파에게요.\"\n\n\"그래요. 정말이지 저는 그가 무척 마음에 듭니다. 그에게도 혼담이 오가고 있지 않나요?\"\n\n\"네, 빌포르 양과 결혼하게 되어 있지요.\"\n\n\"그래요?\"\n\n\"그리고 백작님도 아시다시피 저는 당글라르 양과 결혼하게 되어 있고요.\" 알베르가 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"웃으시는군요.\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"왜 그러시지요?\"\n\n\"제가 웃는 건, 그 혼약을 성사시키려는 마음이 제 혼사를 성사시키려는 마음만큼이나 있는 듯 보여서입니다. 그런데 정말이지, 백작님, 우리도 여자들이 우리 얘기를 하는 만큼 여자들 얘기를 하고 있군요. 용서받지 못할 일입니다.\"\n\n알베르가 일어섰다.\n\n\"가시려고요?\"\n\n\"이거 참 좋은 말씀이네요! 제가 두 시간이나 옆에 붙어서 백작님을 지루해 죽게 만들어 놓고는, 백작님은 더없이 정중하게 제가 가는지 물으시다니요. 정말이지, 백작님은 세상에서 가장 세련된 분이십니다. 백작님 하인들도 어찌나 예의 바른지요. 다들 품위가 있어요. 특히 밥티스탱 씨가요. 저는 도무지 그런 사람을 구할 수가 없습니다. 제 하인들은 연극에서 가끔 보는 그런 자들을 닮았어요. 대사라곤 한두 마디뿐인데도 더없이 어색하게 해치우는 그런 자들 말입니다. 그러니 혹시 밥티스탱 씨를 내보내시거든, 제게 먼저 거절할 기회를 주십시오.\"\n\n\"얼마든지요.\"\n\n\"그게 다가 아닙니다. 그 명망 높은 루카 분, 카발칸티 가문의 카발칸테 씨에게 안부를 전해 주십시오. 혹시라도 그분이 아드님을 짝지어 주고 싶어 하신다면, 적어도 어머니 쪽으로는 무척 부유하고 무척 고귀하며, 아버지 덕분에 남작 작위까지 지닌 신붓감을 찾아 드리세요. 저도 함께 찾아 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n\"하, 하. 그렇게까지 해 주시겠다는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"음, 정말이지, 세상에 확실한 건 없군요.\"\n\n\"오, 백작님, 저에게 얼마나 큰 도움을 베푸실 수 있는지요! 백작님께서 나서 주신 덕에 단 십 년만이라도 제가 독신으로 남을 수 있다면, 백작님을 백배는 더 좋아하게 될 텐데요.\"\n\n\"불가능한 건 없지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 진지하게 대답했다. 그리고 알베르와 작별한 뒤 집 안으로 돌아와 징을 세 번 울렸다. 베르투치오가 나타났다.\n\n\"베르투치오, 토요일에 오퇴유에서 손님을 대접할 생각이라는 걸 알아 두게.\" 베르투치오가 살짝 흠칫했다. \"모든 게 제대로 갖춰지도록 자네 손을 좀 빌려야겠어. 아름다운 집이지, 적어도 그렇게 꾸밀 수 있는 집이고.\"\n\n\"그런 이름값을 하려면 손볼 데가 많을 겁니다, 각하. 벽걸이 융단이 무척 낡았거든요.\"\n\n\"그럼 붉은 다마스크 천을 두른 침실만 빼고 전부 떼어 내서 바꾸게. 그 방은 지금 그대로 두고.\" 베르투치오가 고개를 숙였다. \"정원도 손대지 말게. 안마당은 자네 마음대로 해도 좋아. 알아볼 수 없을 만큼 완전히 바꿔 놓았으면 싶군.\"\n\n\"각하의 뜻을 받들어 제 힘닿는 데까지 다 하겠습니다. 다만 만찬에 관해서는 각하의 분부를 받았으면 합니다.\"\n\n\"이런, 베르투치오,\" 백작이 말했다. \"파리에 온 뒤로 자네가 아주 신경이 예민해지고, 도무지 제 물을 만난 것 같지가 않아. 더는 내 말을 못 알아듣는군.\"\n\n\"하지만 각하께서 누구를 맞으실 생각이신지 알려 주시면 좋겠습니다만.\"\n\n\"나도 아직 모르고, 자네가 알 필요도 없어. '루쿨루스가 루쿨루스와 식사한다.' 그걸로 충분하네.\"\n\n베르투치오가 고개를 숙이고 방을 나섰다.\n\n# Chapter 55. Major Cavalcanti\n\n백작과 바티스탱이 모르세르에게 소령의 방문이 예정되어 있다고 알렸을 때, 두 사람 모두 사실을 말한 것이었다. 그 방문은 몬테크리스토가 알베르의 초대를 거절하는 구실이 되었다. 일곱 시를 막 알리는 종이 울렸다. 베르투치오 씨는 미리 받은 명령에 따라 두 시간 전에 오퇴유로 떠난 뒤였다. 그때 삯마차 한 대가 문 앞에 멈춰 서더니, 손님을 대문 앞에 내려놓고는 마치 그 일이 부끄럽기라도 한 듯 황급히 떠나 버렸다. 손님은 쉰두 살쯤 된 사람으로, 오랫동안 유럽 전역에서 인기를 누려 온, 검은 장식끈이 달린 초록색 프록코트를 입고 있었다. 푸른 천으로 된 바지에, 그런대로 깨끗하지만 광이 가장 잘 나는 것은 아니고 밑창이 조금 두꺼운 장화를 신었다. 사슴 가죽 장갑을 끼고, 헌병들이 흔히 쓰는 것과 비슷한 모양의 모자를 썼으며, 흰 줄무늬가 있는 검은 넥타이를 맸는데, 그것은 주인이 제 발로 매지 않았더라면 교수형 밧줄로 보였을 만큼 그것과 닮아 있었다. 대문에서 초인종을 울리고, 샹젤리제 대로 30번지에 몬테크리스토 백작이 사는 게 맞느냐고 물은 사람의 그림 같은 차림새가 그러했다. 문지기가 그렇다고 대답하자, 그는 안으로 들어와 등 뒤로 문을 닫고는 계단을 오르기 시작했다.\n\n이 사내의 작고 모난 머리, 흰 머리카락과 숱 많은 잿빛 콧수염 덕분에, 바티스탱은 그를 쉽게 알아보았다. 그는 예정된 손님에 대해 정확한 인상착의를 미리 들었고, 현관에서 그를 기다리고 있었다. 그래서 낯선 사내가 이름을 댈 겨를도 거의 없이 백작은 그의 도착을 통보받았다. 그는 소박하고 우아한 응접실로 안내되었고, 백작이 미소 띤 얼굴로 일어나 그를 맞이했다.\n\n\"아, 선생, 잘 오셨습니다. 기다리고 있었지요.\"\n\n\"그렇습니까.\" 이탈리아인이 말했다. \"그럼 각하께서는 제 방문을 알고 계셨던 겁니까?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다. 오늘 일곱 시에 뵙게 될 거라고 들었습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 제 도착에 관해 빠짐없이 통보받으셨군요?\"\n\n\"물론입니다.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇다면 다행입니다. 이 작은 조처가 잊혔을까 봐 걱정했거든요.\"\n\n\"무슨 조처 말입니까?\"\n\n\"제가 올 거라고 미리 알려 드리는 것 말입니다.\"\n\n\"아니요, 잊히지 않았습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 분명 착오가 아니시지요.\"\n\n\"분명합니다.\"\n\n\"오늘 저녁 일곱 시에 각하께서 기다리신 사람이 정말 저였단 말씀이지요?\"\n\n\"의심할 여지가 없도록 증명해 드리지요.\"\n\n\"아, 아닙니다, 괘념치 마십시오.\" 이탈리아인이 말했다. \"그럴 것까진 없습니다.\"\n\n\"네, 네.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. 손님은 다소 불안해 보였다. \"어디 봅시다.\" 백작이 말했다. \"바르톨로메오 카발칸티 후작이 아니십니까?\"\n\n\"바르톨로메오 카발칸티.\" 이탈리아인이 기쁘게 대답했다. \"그렇습니다, 제가 바로 그 사람입니다.\"\n\n\"오스트리아군에서 복무하신 전직 소령이시지요?\"\n\n\"제가 소령이었습니까?\" 노병이 조심스럽게 물었다.\n\n\"그렇습니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"소령이셨지요. 이탈리아에서 맡으셨던 직책을 프랑스인들은 그렇게 부릅니다.\"\n\n\"좋습니다.\" 소령이 말했다. \"더 바랄 게 없지요, 무슨 말씀인지 아시겠지만……\"\n\n\"오늘 이곳을 방문하신 것은 선생 스스로의 생각이 아니지요?\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 분명 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"누군가 다른 사람이 보낸 겁니까?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"그 훌륭한 부소니 신부가요?\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다.\" 소령이 반색하며 말했다.\n\n\"편지를 가지고 계십니까?\"\n\n\"네, 여기 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 제게 주십시오.\" 몬테크리스토는 편지를 받아 펼쳐 읽었다. 소령은 크고 휑한 눈으로 백작을 바라보다가 방 안을 둘러보았으나, 그 시선은 곧바로 다시 방 주인에게로 돌아왔다.\n\n\"그렇군요, 그래요, 알겠습니다. '루카의 훌륭한 귀족이며 피렌체 카발칸티 가문의 후손인 카발칸티 소령은,'\" 몬테크리스토가 소리 내어 읽기를 이어 갔다. \"'오십만의 수입을 가진 분으로.'\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 종이에서 눈을 들어 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"오십만이라니.\" 그가 말했다. \"대단하십니다!\"\n\n\"오십만이라고요?\" 소령이 말했다.\n\n\"네, 글자 그대로요. 또 분명 그러할 겁니다. 신부님은 유럽의 큰 재산들의 액수를 모두 정확히 알고 계시니까요.\"\n\n\"오십만이라 칩시다. 하지만 맹세코, 그렇게나 많은 줄은 몰랐습니다.\"\n\n\"집사에게 도둑맞고 계시기 때문이지요. 그쪽을 좀 손보셔야겠습니다.\"\n\n\"눈을 뜨게 해 주셨군요.\" 이탈리아인이 근엄하게 말했다. \"그자들을 내쫓겠습니다.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 다시 편지를 읽어 내려갔다.\n\n\"'그리고 그를 행복하게 하는 데에는 단 한 가지가 더 필요할 뿐이니.'\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 정말이지 단 한 가지뿐이지요!\" 소령이 한숨을 쉬며 말했다.\n\n\"'그것은 잃어버린, 그토록 사랑하는 아들을 되찾는 일이라.'\"\n\n\"잃어버린, 그토록 사랑하는 아들이라니!\"\n\n\"'그 아이는 어릴 적에 고귀한 가문의 원수에게, 혹은 집시들에게 빼앗겼으니.'\"\n\n\"다섯 살 때였습니다!\" 소령이 깊은 한숨을 내쉬며 하늘로 눈을 들어 올렸다.\n\n\"불행한 아버지로군요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. 백작은 계속 읽어 나갔다.\n\n\"'나는 그에게 새로운 삶과 희망을 주었으니, 그가 십오 년 동안 헛되이 찾아 헤맨 아들을 그대가 되돌려 줄 힘이 있음을 확신하는 까닭이라.'\"\n\n소령은 형언할 수 없는 불안의 표정으로 백작을 바라보았다.\n\n\"제게 그럴 힘이 있지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. 소령은 평정을 되찾았다.\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 그가 말했다. \"이 편지는 끝까지 사실이었군요?\"\n\n\"의심하셨습니까, 친애하는 바르톨로메오 씨?\"\n\n\"아니요, 정말 아닙니다. 부소니 신부 같은 선량한 분, 성직을 맡으신 분이 사람을 속이거나 농을 거실 리야 없지요. 하지만 각하께서는 다 읽지 않으셨습니다.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇군요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"추신이 있군요.\"\n\n\"네, 그렇습니다.\" 소령이 되풀이했다. \"네, 추신이, 있습니다.\"\n\n\"'카발칸티 소령이 자기 거래 은행에 손을 댈 수고를 덜어 주고자, 나는 그에게 여비조로 이천 프랑의 환어음을 보내며, 그대에게는 그대가 아직 내게 빚진 사만 팔천 프랑의 추가 금액을 그에게 지급하도록 신용장을 보내노라.'\"\n\n소령은 추신의 결말을 기다렸는데, 보기에 몹시 불안한 기색이었다.\n\n\"아주 좋습니다.\" 백작이 말했다.\n\n\"'아주 좋다'고 하셨겠다.\" 소령이 중얼거리고는 \"그렇다면, 각하……\" 하고 말을 받았다.\n\n\"그렇다면 뭡니까?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다.\n\n\"그렇다면 추신은……\"\n\n\"네, 추신이 어떻단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"그렇다면 추신도 편지의 나머지 부분만큼이나 흡족하게 받아들여진 겁니까?\"\n\n\"물론입니다. 부소니 신부와 저 사이에는 자그마한 외상 거래가 트여 있지요. 제가 아직 그분께 빚진 게 정확히 사만 팔천 프랑인지는 기억나지 않습니다만, 그 차액을 두고 다툴 일은 없을 겁니다. 그러니까 이 추신을 무척 중요하게 여기셨군요, 친애하는 카발칸티 씨?\"\n\n\"설명을 드려야겠습니다.\" 소령이 말했다. \"부소니 신부의 서명을 전적으로 믿고서, 저는 다른 자금을 마련해 두지 않았습니다. 그러니 만약 이 자금줄이 끊겼다면, 저는 파리에서 무척 난처한 처지에 놓였을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"선생 같은 신분의 분이 어디서든 곤경에 처할 수 있단 말입니까?\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"실은, 아는 사람이 하나도 없어서요.\" 소령이 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 그럼 선생 자신은 다른 사람들에게 알려져 있지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"네, 저는 알려져 있지요, 그러니까……\"\n\n\"말씀해 보시지요, 친애하는 카발칸티 씨.\"\n\n\"그러니까 제게 그 사만 팔천 프랑을 내주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"물론입니다, 청하시는 즉시 드리지요.\" 소령의 눈이 흐뭇한 놀라움으로 휘둥그레졌다. \"그건 그렇고 앉으시지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"정말이지 제가 무슨 생각을 하고 있었는지 모르겠군요. 지난 십오 분 동안 손님을 줄곧 세워 두었다니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"별말씀을요.\" 소령은 안락의자를 끌어당겨 자리에 앉았다.\n\n\"자,\" 백작이 말했다. \"무엇을 드시겠습니까. 셰리주 한 잔, 아니면 포르토주, 알리칸테주?\"\n\n\"괜찮으시다면 알리칸테주로 하지요. 제가 가장 좋아하는 술입니다.\"\n\n\"아주 좋은 게 좀 있지요. 비스킷도 곁들이시겠지요?\"\n\n\"네, 이렇게 친절을 베푸시니 비스킷도 들겠습니다.\"\n\n몬테크리스토가 종을 울렸다. 바티스탱이 나타났다. 백작이 그를 맞으러 다가갔다.\n\n\"어떤가?\" 그가 낮은 목소리로 물었다.\n\n\"젊은이가 와 있습니다.\" 하인이 같은 어조로 대답했다.\n\n\"어느 방으로 안내했나?\"\n\n\"각하의 분부대로 푸른 응접실로 모셨습니다.\"\n\n\"잘했네. 자, 이제 알리칸테주와 비스킷을 가져오게.\"\n\n바티스탱이 방을 나갔다.\n\n\"정말이지,\" 소령이 말했다. \"이렇게 폐를 끼치니 몸 둘 바를 모르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그런 말씀 마십시오.\" 백작이 말했다. 바티스탱이 잔과 포도주, 비스킷을 들고 다시 들어왔다. 백작은 한 잔을 가득 채웠으나, 다른 잔에는 루비빛 액체를 몇 방울만 따랐다. 술병은 거미줄로 뒤덮여 있었고, 사람 얼굴의 주름보다도 더 정확하게 포도주의 연륜을 말해 주는 온갖 흔적이 가득했다. 소령은 현명한 선택을 했다. 그는 가득 찬 잔과 비스킷을 집었다. 백작은 바티스탱에게 접시를 손님 손이 닿는 곳에 두라고 일렀고, 손님은 우선 알리칸테주를 한 모금 음미하며 무척 흡족한 표정을 짓고는, 비스킷을 포도주에 정갈하게 적셨다.\n\n\"그래, 선생은 루카에 사셨다지요? 부유하고, 고귀하고, 큰 존경을 받으며, 사람을 행복하게 할 모든 것을 가지셨고요?\"\n\n\"전부 다요.\" 소령이 비스킷을 황급히 삼키며 말했다. \"정말이지 전부 다요.\"\n\n\"그런데도 그 행복을 완성하는 데에 한 가지가 빠져 있었군요?\"\n\n\"단 한 가지가요.\" 이탈리아인이 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 그 한 가지가, 바로 잃어버린 아이로군요.\"\n\n\"아.\" 소령이 두 번째 비스킷을 집으며 말했다. \"제 행복을 완성할 그것이 정말이지 빠져 있었지요.\" 갸륵한 소령은 하늘로 눈을 들어 올리며 한숨을 쉬었다.\n\n\"그럼 한번 들려주시지요.\" 백작이 말했다. \"그토록 애통해하시는 아드님이 누구인지를요. 저는 늘 선생을 독신으로 알고 있었거든요.\"\n\n\"세간의 평이 그러했지요, 각하.\" 소령이 말했다. \"그리고 저는……\"\n\n\"그렇지요.\" 백작이 받았다. \"선생도 그 소문을 굳히셨고요. 젊은 날의 경솔한 과오를 세상에 감추고 싶으셨던 게로군요?\"\n\n소령은 마음을 추슬러 다시 평소의 차분한 태도로 돌아갔다. 그러면서 눈을 내리깔았는데, 얼굴 표정을 가다듬을 시간을 벌려는 것인지 상상을 짜내려는 것인지 알 수 없었다. 그러는 내내 백작을 슬쩍슬쩍 곁눈질했는데, 백작의 입가에 길게 머무는 미소는 여전히 변함없는 정중한 호기심을 드러내고 있었다.\n\n\"그렇습니다.\" 소령이 말했다. \"저는 그 과오를 모든 이의 눈에서 감추고 싶었지요.\"\n\n\"설마 선생 자신을 위해서는 아니겠지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 받았다. \"사내라면 그런 일쯤은 초연한 법이니까요?\"\n\n\"아, 아닙니다, 분명 저 자신을 위해서는 아니었지요.\" 소령이 미소 지으며 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"그럼 그 어머니를 위해서?\" 백작이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇습니다, 그 어머니를 위해서지요. 가엾은 그 어미를!\" 소령이 세 번째 비스킷을 집으며 외쳤다.\n\n\"포도주를 좀 더 드시지요, 친애하는 카발칸티 씨.\" 백작이 그에게 알리칸테주를 두 번째 잔으로 따라 주며 말했다. \"감정이 북받쳐 어쩔 줄 모르시는군요.\"\n\n\"그 가엾은 어미를.\" 소령이 중얼거리며, 거짓 눈물로 눈가를 적시려고 눈물샘을 작동시키려 애썼다.\n\n\"제 생각에 그분은 이탈리아의 손꼽히는 가문 출신이었던 것 같은데, 그렇지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"피에솔레의 고귀한 가문 출신이었습니다, 백작님.\"\n\n\"그리고 그분 성함은……\"\n\n\"그 이름을 알고 싶으십니까……?\"\n\n\"아.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"굳이 말씀하실 것 없습니다. 제가 이미 알고 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"백작님은 모르시는 게 없으시군요.\" 이탈리아인이 고개를 숙이며 말했다.\n\n\"올리바 코르시나리, 아니었습니까?\"\n\n\"올리바 코르시나리!\"\n\n\"후작 부인이셨고요?\"\n\n\"후작 부인이었지요!\"\n\n\"그래서 가문의 반대에도 불구하고 마침내 그분과 결혼하셨고요?\"\n\n\"네, 그렇게 매듭지어졌지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 분명 서류를 모두 가져오셨겠지요?\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 서류 말씀입니까?\"\n\n\"올리바 코르시나리와의 혼인 증명서, 그리고 자제분의 출생 기록 말입니다.\"\n\n\"제 아이의 출생 기록이요?\"\n\n\"안드레아 카발칸티의, 그러니까 아드님의 출생 기록 말입니다. 이름이 안드레아 아닙니까?\"\n\n\"그런 것 같습니다.\" 소령이 말했다.\n\n\"뭐라고요? 그런 것 같다니요?\"\n\n\"워낙 오랫동안 잃어버렸던 터라, 확실히 단언하기는 어렵습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"서류는 모두 가지고 계시겠지요?\"\n\n\"각하, 송구하게도 그 서류들을 갖춰 와야 하는 줄 몰라서, 챙겨 오지 못했습니다.\"\n\n\"그것 참 안타깝군요.\" 몬테크리스토가 받았다.\n\n\"그게, 그렇게나 필요했습니까?\"\n\n\"없어서는 안 될 것이었지요.\"\n\n소령은 손으로 이마를 쓸었다. \"아, 페르바코, 없어서는 안 된다고요?\"\n\n\"물론이지요. 선생의 혼인이 유효한지, 자제분이 적자인지에 대해 의혹이 제기된다고 쳐 보십시오.\"\n\n\"그렇군요.\" 소령이 말했다. \"의혹이 제기될 수도 있겠지요.\"\n\n\"그렇게 되면 아드님은 무척 난처한 처지에 놓일 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그 애의 이익에 치명적이겠지요.\"\n\n\"바라 마지않던 혼인을 그르치게 될 수도 있고요.\"\n\n\"오, 페카토!\"\n\n\"아셔야 할 것이, 프랑스에서는 이런 점에 무척 까다롭습니다. 이탈리아에서처럼 신부님께 가서 '우리는 서로 사랑하니 결혼시켜 주십시오'라고 말하는 것으로는 충분치 않지요. 프랑스에서 결혼은 민사상의 일이라, 격식에 맞게 결혼하려면 신원을 의심할 여지 없이 입증하는 서류가 있어야 합니다.\"\n\n\"그게 바로 불운이지요! 보시다시피 저는 그 필요한 서류들이 없으니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"다행히도 제게는 그것들이 있습니다만.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\"각하께서요?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"각하께서 그걸 가지고 계신다고요?\"\n\n\"가지고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"아, 정말입니까?\" 소령이 말했다. 그는 서류가 없어 여행의 목적이 좌절될까, 또한 자신의 깜빡함이 사만 팔천 프랑을 둘러싼 곤란을 빚어낼까 두려워하던 참이었다. \"아, 정말이지, 그것 참 다행한 일이군요. 네, 정말 운이 좋습니다. 그것들을 가져올 생각은 미처 못 했거든요.\"\n\n\"조금도 이상하지 않습니다. 모든 걸 일일이 다 생각할 수는 없는 노릇이지요. 하지만 다행히도 부소니 신부가 선생 대신 생각해 두셨습니다.\"\n\n\"훌륭한 분이지요.\"\n\n\"대단히 신중하고 사려 깊은 분입니다.\"\n\n\"감탄스러운 분이지요.\" 소령이 말했다. \"그분이 그걸 각하께 보내셨군요?\"\n\n\"여기 있습니다.\"\n\n소령은 감탄의 표시로 두 손을 맞잡았다.\n\n\"선생은 산파올로델몬테카티니 성당에서 올리바 코르시나리와 결혼하셨지요. 여기 신부님의 증명서가 있습니다.\"\n\n\"네, 정말이지 그게 맞습니다, 틀림없어요.\" 이탈리아인이 놀라움에 차서 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 여기 안드레아 카발칸티의 세례 기록이 있습니다. 사라베차의 주임 신부가 작성한 것이지요.\"\n\n\"전부 아주 정확합니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 이 서류들을 받으십시오. 제게는 소용없는 것들이니까요. 이걸 아드님께 주십시오. 물론 잘 간수하겠지요.\"\n\n\"그렇고말고요! 만약 이걸 잃어버리기라도 한다면……\"\n\n\"그래요, 만약 잃어버린다면요?\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇게 되면,\" 소령이 대답했다. \"주임 신부에게 편지를 써서 사본을 받아야 할 텐데, 손에 넣기까지 한참 걸릴 겁니다.\"\n\n\"수습하기가 까다로운 일이겠군요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"거의 불가능한 일이지요.\" 소령이 대답했다.\n\n\"이 서류들의 가치를 알아주시니 무척 기쁩니다.\"\n\n\"더없이 귀한 것으로 여기지요.\"\n\n\"자, 이제.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그 젊은이의 어머니에 관해서는……\"\n\n\"그 젊은이의 어머니에 관해서는……\" 이탈리아인이 불안하게 되풀이했다.\n\n\"코르시나리 후작 부인에 대해 말입니다만……\"\n\n\"정말이지,\" 소령이 말했다. \"어려움이 점점 쌓여 가는 것 같군요. 그 사람도 어떤 식으로든 필요합니까?\"\n\n\"아니요, 선생.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"게다가, 그분은 이미……\"\n\n\"네, 각하.\" 소령이 말했다. \"그 사람은 이미……\"\n\n\"세상을 떠나셨지요?\"\n\n\"아아, 그렇습니다.\" 이탈리아인이 받았다.\n\n\"알고 있었습니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그분은 십 년 전에 돌아가셨지요.\"\n\n\"그리고 저는 아직도 그 사람을 잃은 슬픔에 잠겨 있답니다.\" 소령이 주머니에서 체크무늬 손수건을 꺼내, 왼쪽 눈과 오른쪽 눈을 번갈아 닦으며 외쳤다.\n\n\"어쩌겠습니까?\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"우리는 모두 죽게 마련이지요. 자, 친애하는 카발칸티 씨, 이제 아셔야 할 것이, 프랑스 사람들에게 아드님과 십오 년 동안 떨어져 지냈다고 말해 봐야 소용없습니다. 아이를 훔쳐 가는 집시 이야기 따위는 이 고장에서는 전혀 통하지 않고, 믿어 주지도 않을 테니까요. 선생은 아드님을 교육하려고 지방의 어느 학교에 보냈고, 이제는 파리 사교계에서 그 교육을 완성시키고자 하는 겁니다. 바로 그 때문에 부인이 돌아가신 뒤로 줄곧 살아온 비아레조를 떠나신 거고요. 그 정도면 충분할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각하십니까?\"\n\n\"물론입니다.\"\n\n\"좋습니다, 그럼.\"\n\n\"혹시 떨어져 지낸 일에 대해 사람들이 듣게 되면……\"\n\n\"아, 그렇군요. 뭐라고 말하면 됩니까?\"\n\n\"가문의 원수들에게 매수된 부정한 가정교사가……\"\n\n\"코르시나리 가문에게요?\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다. 선생의 가문을 끊어 버리려고 그 아이를 훔쳐 갔다고 하시면 됩니다.\"\n\n\"그럴듯하군요, 외아들이니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"자, 이제 모든 게 정리됐으니, 이렇게 새로 일깨워진 기억들을 잊지 마십시오. 제가 선생에게 깜짝 놀랄 일을 준비했다는 걸 분명 벌써 눈치채셨겠지요?\"\n\n\"기분 좋은 일입니까?\" 이탈리아인이 물었다.\n\n\"아, 보아하니 아버지의 눈은 그 마음만큼이나 속이기 어렵군요.\"\n\n\"흠!\" 소령이 말했다.\n\n\"누군가 선생께 비밀을 일러 주었거나, 아니면 그 애가 여기 와 있다는 걸 알아채셨군요.\"\n\n\"누가 여기 와 있다고요?\"\n\n\"선생의 아이, 선생의 아드님, 선생의 안드레아 말입니다!\"\n\n\"짐작하고 있었습니다.\" 소령이 더없이 태연하게 대답했다. \"그래, 그 애가 여기 와 있다고요?\"\n\n\"와 있습니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"방금 하인이 들어왔을 때, 그 애가 도착했다고 알려 주더군요.\"\n\n\"아, 좋습니다, 좋아요.\" 소령이 한 마디씩 외칠 때마다 코트 단추를 움켜쥐며 말했다.\n\n\"선생,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"감정이 북받치시는 걸 이해합니다. 마음을 추스를 시간이 필요하시겠지요. 그동안 저는 가서 이 그토록 바라던 만남에 대비하도록 그 젊은이를 준비시키겠습니다. 그 애도 선생 못지않게 이 만남을 애타게 기다리고 있을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"분명 그러리라 짐작됩니다.\" 카발칸티가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 십오 분 뒤에 그 애가 선생께 갈 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 직접 데려오시는 겁니까? 친히 그 애를 제게 소개해 주시는 데까지 친절을 베푸시는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"아니요. 저는 아버지와 아들 사이에 끼어들고 싶지 않습니다. 두 분의 만남은 단둘이 하셔야지요. 하지만 염려 마십시오. 설령 강력한 천륜의 목소리가 침묵하더라도, 그 애를 알아보지 못할 리는 없습니다. 이 문으로 들어올 테니까요. 잘생긴 젊은이입니다. 살결이 희고, 어쩌면 좀 지나치게 흴지도 모르지요. 태도가 상냥하고요. 하지만 직접 보고 판단하시지요.\"\n\n\"그런데 말입니다,\" 소령이 말했다. \"아시다시피 제게는 부소니 신부가 보내 준 이천 프랑밖에 없습니다. 그 돈은 여비로 다 써 버렸고, 그래서……\"\n\n\"그래서 돈이 필요하시다, 그야 당연한 일이지요, 친애하는 카발칸티 씨. 자, 여기 우선 팔천 프랑입니다.\"\n\n소령의 눈이 환하게 반짝였다.\n\n\"이제 제가 선생께 빚진 게 사만 프랑이군요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"각하께서는 영수증을 원하십니까?\" 소령이 그렇게 말하며 돈을 코트 안주머니에 슬그머니 집어넣었다.\n\n\"뭣 하러요?\" 백작이 말했다.\n\n\"부소니 신부께 보여 주려면 필요하실까 해서요.\"\n\n\"그럼, 나머지 사만 프랑을 받으실 때 전액 영수증을 한 장 써 주십시오. 정직한 사람들 사이에 그런 지나친 조심은, 제 생각에는 전혀 필요 없는 일이지요.\"\n\n\"네, 그렇고말고요, 완벽하게 올곧은 사람들 사이에서야.\"\n\n\"한마디만 더 하지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"말씀하시지요.\"\n\n\"한 가지 짚어 드려도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"물론입니다. 어서 그러시지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 충고하건대, 그런 차림새는 그만 입으시는 게 좋겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇습니까.\" 소령이 더없이 흡족한 표정으로 자신의 차림을 내려다보며 말했다.\n\n\"네. 비아레조에서야 입으셔도 괜찮겠지요. 하지만 그 차림은, 그 자체로는 아무리 우아하다 해도, 파리에서는 한물간 지 오래입니다.\"\n\n\"그것 참 안타깝군요.\"\n\n\"아, 정말로 그 옛 차림새에 애착이 있으시다면, 파리를 떠나실 때 얼마든지 다시 입으시면 됩니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 그럼 무엇을 입어야 합니까?\"\n\n\"가방 안에 있는 것을 입으시지요.\"\n\n\"제 가방 안에요? 제게는 여행 가방 하나뿐인데요.\"\n\n\"그것 말고 다른 건 아무것도 안 가져오셨겠지요. 그렇게 많은 짐으로 스스로를 번거롭게 할 필요가 어디 있겠습니까? 게다가 노병은 늘 가능한 한 짐을 적게 하고 행군하기를 좋아하는 법이지요.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다, 정확히 그렇지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 선생은 선견지명과 신중함을 갖춘 분이니, 짐은 미리 부쳐 두셨겠지요. 그것이 리슐리외 거리의 프랭스 호텔에 도착해 있습니다. 그곳에 거처를 정하시면 됩니다.\"\n\n\"그럼, 그 가방 안에는……\"\n\n\"하인에게 분부해서 필요할 만한 것은 다 챙겨 넣게 하셨겠지요. 평상복과 군복 말입니다. 큰 행사 때에는 군복을 입으셔야 합니다. 그게 아주 보기 좋을 테니까요. 훈장도 잊지 마시고요. 프랑스에서는 아직도 그런 걸 보고 비웃지만, 그러면서도 늘 달고 다니지요.\"\n\n\"좋습니다, 아주 좋아요.\" 소령은 백작이 베푸는 세심한 배려에 황홀해하며 말했다.\n\n\"자, 이제.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"온갖 괴로운 흥분에 단단히 대비하셨으니, 친애하는 카발칸티 씨, 마음의 준비를 하고 잃어버린 안드레아를 맞이하십시오.\"\n\n그렇게 말하며 몬테크리스토는 고개를 숙이고 벽걸이 천 뒤로 사라졌다. 소령은 백작에게서 받은 더없이 흐뭇한 환대에 형언할 수 없을 만큼 매료된 채 남겨졌다.\n\n# Chapter 56. Andrea Cavalcanti\n\n몬테크리스토 백작은 바티스탱이 응접실이라 일러 준 옆방으로 들어갔다. 거기에는 우아한 몸가짐과 세련된 풍모의 한 젊은이가 있었는데, 그는 반 시간쯤 전에 삯마차를 타고 도착해 있었다. 바티스탱은 문 앞에 나타나 들여보내 달라고 청한 사람을 알아보는 데 조금도 어려움이 없었다. 키가 크고 머리칼이 옅으며 수염은 붉고 눈은 검은 데다 안색이 환한 그 젊은이는, 주인이 그토록 세세하게 일러 준 바로 그 사람이 틀림없었다. 백작이 방으로 들어섰을 때 젊은이는 소파에 무심히 몸을 길게 뻗은 채, 손에 쥔 금장식 지팡이로 자기 장화를 톡톡 두드리고 있었다. 백작을 알아본 그는 재빨리 일어섰다.\n\n\"몬테크리스토 백작이시죠?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소. 그리고 안드레아 카발칸티 백작을 뵙는 영광을 누리는 듯하군요?\"\n\n\"안드레아 카발칸티 백작입니다.\" 젊은이가 말과 함께 고개를 숙이며 되풀이했다.\n\n\"내게 보내는 소개장을 지니고 계시지요?\" 백작이 말했다.\n\n\"그건 말씀드리지 않았습니다. 서명이 어찌나 기묘하던지요.\"\n\n\"'뱃사람 신드바드'라고 서명된 편지 아닙니까?\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다. 그런데 저는 신드바드라는 사람을 한 번도 안 적이 없습니다. 「천일야화」에 나오는 그 유명한 인물 말고는요.\"\n\n\"음, 그가 바로 그 신드바드의 후손 중 하나이고, 내 절친한 벗이지요. 광기에 가까울 만큼 괴팍한 대단한 부자 영국인인데, 본명은 윌모어 경입니다.\"\n\n\"아, 정말입니까? 그렇다면 모든 기이한 일이 다 설명되는군요.\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"그러니까 그분이, 제가 만났던 그 영국인이로군요. 어디였더라, 아, 그래요, 정말 그렇습니다. 자, 백작님, 분부만 내려 주십시오.\"\n\n\"당신 말씀이 사실이라면,\" 백작이 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"당신 자신과 가문에 대해 좀 들려주시는 친절을 베풀어 주시겠지요?\"\n\n\"물론입니다, 그렇게 하지요.\" 젊은이가 막힘없는 임기응변을 증명하는 빠른 말씨로 대답했다. \"저는 (말씀하신 대로) 안드레아 카발칸티 백작이며, 바르톨로메오 카발칸티 소령의 아들입니다. 피렌체의 황금 명부에 이름이 새겨진 카발칸티 가문의 후손이지요. 저희 가문은 아직 부유하기는 하나(제 아버지의 수입이 50만에 이르니까요), 숱한 불행을 겪었습니다. 저 자신도 다섯 살 때 가정교사의 배신으로 끌려갔고, 그리하여 십오 년 동안 저를 낳아 주신 분을 뵙지 못했습니다. 분별이 설 나이에 이르러 제 한 몸의 주인이 된 뒤로, 저는 끊임없이 아버지를 찾아 헤맸으나 모두 헛수고였습니다. 그러던 차에 백작님의 벗에게서 이 편지를 받았는데, 제 아버지가 파리에 계시며, 그분에 관한 소식을 얻으려거든 백작님을 찾아뵈라는 내용이었습니다.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 당신이 들려준 이야기가 더없이 흥미롭군요.\" 몬테크리스토가 음울한 만족을 띤 채 젊은이를 살피며 말했다. \"그리고 내 벗 신드바드의 뜻에 일일이 따른 것은 잘하셨소. 당신 아버지가 정말 이곳에 와서 당신을 찾고 계시니까.\"\n\n백작은 응접실에 처음 발을 들인 순간부터 젊은이의 표정을 단 한 번도 놓치지 않았다. 그 눈빛의 당당함과 목소리의 확신을 그는 감탄하며 지켜보았다. 그런데 그 자체로는 지극히 자연스러운 \"당신 아버지가 정말 이곳에 와서 당신을 찾고 계시니까.\"라는 말에, 젊은 안드레아는 움찔하며 소리쳤다. \"제 아버지요? 제 아버지가 여기 계신다고요?\"\n\n\"틀림없소.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"당신 아버지, 바르톨로메오 카발칸티 소령 말이오.\" 한순간 젊은이의 얼굴을 뒤덮었던 공포의 빛은 이제 사라지고 없었다.\n\n\"아, 그래요, 분명 그 이름입니다. 바르톨로메오 카발칸티 소령. 그래서 정말로, 백작님, 제 사랑하는 아버지가 여기 계시다는 말씀이신지요?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 게다가 방금 그분과 함께 있다가 온 길이라고까지 말할 수 있소. 그분이 잃어버린 아들에 관해 들려준 사연이 내 마음을 깊이 울렸지요. 정말이지, 그 문제에 얽힌 그분의 비탄과 희망과 두려움은 더없이 애절하고 가슴 저미는 시 한 편의 소재가 될 만했소. 그러던 어느 날, 그분은 편지 한 통을 받았소. 아들을 납치한 자들이 이제 그를 돌려주거나, 적어도 어디에서 찾을 수 있는지 알려 주겠노라고, 다만 몸값으로 거액을 받는 조건으로 말이오. 당신 아버지는 한순간도 망설이지 않았소. 그래서 그 돈은 이탈리아행 서명이 든 통행증과 함께 피에몬테 국경으로 보내졌소. 당신은 프랑스 남부에 있었지요, 아마?\"\n\n\"네.\" 안드레아가 거북한 기색으로 대답했다. \"프랑스 남부에 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"마차 한 대가 니스에서 당신을 기다리기로 되어 있었지요?\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다. 그 마차가 저를 니스에서 제노바로, 제노바에서 토리노로, 토리노에서 샹베리로, 샹베리에서 퐁드보부아쟁으로, 퐁드보부아쟁에서 파리로 데려다주었습니다.\"\n\n\"정말이오? 그렇다면 당신 아버지가 도중에 당신과 마주쳤어야 했는데. 그분이 몸소 지나온 길과 정확히 같은 경로니 말이오. 우리가 당신의 여정을 이곳까지 짚어 올 수 있었던 것도 그래서요.\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"아버지가 저와 마주쳤다 해도 저를 알아보셨을지 의문입니다. 마지막으로 보셨을 때와는 제가 꽤 달라졌을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"오, 천륜의 목소리가 있지 않소.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"맞습니다.\" 젊은이가 끼어들며 말했다. \"그렇게는 생각해 보지 못했습니다.\"\n\n\"자,\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"이제 당신 아버지 마음에는 한 가지 걱정거리만 남아 있소. 이런 것이오. 그분은 당신이 그 오랜 별리의 세월 동안 무슨 일을 하며 지냈는지, 당신을 박해한 자들에게 어떤 대접을 받았는지, 그들이 당신의 신분에 걸맞은 예우를 다하며 처신했는지를 몹시 알고 싶어 하시오. 끝으로 그분은, 당신이 노출되었던 그 나쁜 도덕적 영향, 어떤 육체적 고통보다 한없이 더 두려운 그 영향에서 다행히 벗어났는지를 보고 싶어 하시오. 또 자연이 당신에게 부여한 그 뛰어난 자질이 교양의 결핍으로 무뎌지지는 않았는지 확인하고 싶어 하시고요. 한마디로, 당신이 사교계에서 당신의 신분이 부여하는 그 높은 지위를 되찾아 지켜 나갈 만하다고 스스로 여기는지 알고 싶어 하시는 거요.\"\n\n\"백작님!\" 젊은이가 어안이 벙벙해 소리쳤다. \"부디 거짓된 소문이……\"\n\n\"나로 말하자면, 자선가인 내 벗 윌모어에게서 당신 이야기를 처음 들었소. 그가 당신을 어떤 곤란한 처지에서 만난 듯하나, 그게 어떤 성격의 것인지는 모르오. 캐묻기를 좋아하지 않는 터라 묻지 않았으니까. 당신의 불행이 그의 동정을 샀으니, 보다시피 당신은 흥미로운 사람이었던 셈이오. 그는 당신이 잃어버린 지위를 되찾아 주고 싶다고, 그리고 당신 아버지를 찾아낼 때까지 찾아 헤매겠노라고 내게 말했소. 그는 정말 찾아 나섰고, 보아하니 찾아낸 모양이오. 지금 여기 와 계시니 말이오. 그리고 마침내 내 벗은 당신이 온다는 것을 내게 알리고, 당신의 장래에 관한 몇 가지 다른 지시도 일러 주었소. 내 벗 윌모어가 별난 사람인 건 익히 알지만, 그는 진실한 사람이고 금광만큼이나 부유하오. 따라서 그 기벽들이 자신을 파산시킬 염려 없이 마음껏 누릴 수 있는 사람이지요. 나는 그의 지시를 따르겠노라 약속했소. 자, 백작, 이제 내가 당신에게 던질 질문에 부디 언짢아 마시오. 당신의 후견인으로서 마땅히 해야 할 일이라서 그런 것이니. 당신에게 닥친 그 불행들이, 전적으로 당신이 어찌할 수 없는 일이었고 내 존경심을 조금도 깎아내리지 않는 그 불행들이, 혹시라도 어느 정도, 당신의 재산과 이름이 마땅히 두드러진 인물이 되게끔 해 주는 그 세계에서 당신을 낯선 이로 만들어 버리지는 않았는지, 그것을 알고 싶소.\"\n\n\"백작님,\" 젊은이가 한결 안정된 태도로 대답했다. \"그 점에 관해서는 마음 놓으셔도 됩니다. 저를 아버지에게서 떼어 낸 자들은, 머지않아 저를 원래 주인에게 다시 팔아넘길 작정이었고, 지금 그렇게 한 셈인데, 그 거래에서 가장 큰 이득을 보려면 제 개인적·세습적 가치를 고스란히 지니게 두고, 가능하다면 그 값어치를 더 높여 두는 것이 영리한 처사라고 계산했지요. 그래서 저는 아주 훌륭한 교육을 받았고, 그 납치범들은 저를 소아시아의 노예처럼 대했습니다. 노예의 주인들이 로마 시장에서 더 비싼 값을 받으려고 그들을 문법학자며 의사며 철학자로 만들었던 것처럼 말입니다.\"\n\n몬테크리스토가 흡족한 듯 미소 지었다. 안드레아 카발칸티 씨에게서 이만큼은 기대하지 않았던 듯했다.\n\n\"게다가,\" 젊은이가 말을 이었다. \"혹 교육에 어떤 결함이 보이거나 정해진 예법에 어긋나는 점이 드러난다 해도, 제 출생에 따라붙고 젊은 시절 내내 저를 쫓아다닌 불행들을 헤아려 용서받으리라 생각합니다.\"\n\n\"음,\" 몬테크리스토가 무심한 어조로 말했다. \"백작, 당신 좋을 대로 하시오. 당신은 당신 행동의 주인이고, 이 일에 가장 깊이 얽힌 당사자니까. 그러나 내가 당신이라면, 그 모험담은 한마디도 발설하지 않겠소. 당신의 내력은 그야말로 한 편의 소설인데, 노란 표지의 소설을 좋아하는 세상은 정작 살아 있는 양피지에 묶인 소설은, 그게 당신처럼 도금이 되어 있다 해도, 이상하리만치 미심쩍어한단 말이오. 이게 바로 내가 당신에게 일러두고 싶은 종류의 곤란함이오, 친애하는 백작. 당신이 그 가슴 저미는 내력을 채 다 읊기도 전에, 그것은 세상에 퍼져 나가 있을 법하지 않고 부자연스럽다고 여겨질 거요. 당신은 더 이상 잃었다가 되찾은 아이가 아니라, 하룻밤 사이 버섯처럼 솟아난 벼락출세자로 취급받게 될 거란 말이오. 약간의 호기심은 불러일으킬지 몰라도, 누구나 다 남들의 이목이 쏠리는 중심이 되고 불쾌한 입방아의 대상이 되기를 좋아하는 건 아니니까.\"\n\n\"동감입니다, 백작님.\" 젊은이가 낯빛이 창백해지며, 상대의 꿰뚫는 듯한 눈길 아래 저도 모르게 떨면서 말했다. \"그런 결과는 정말이지 몹시 불쾌하겠지요.\"\n\n\"그렇다고 그 폐해를 과장해서도 안 되오.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"한 가지 잘못을 피하려다 다른 잘못에 빠질 수 있으니 말이오. 당신은 단순하고 한결같은 단 하나의 행동 방침을 정해야 하오. 당신만큼 지혜로운 사람에게 이 방책은 필요한 만큼이나 쉬운 일이오. 당신은 명예로운 친분을 쌓아야 하고, 그렇게 함으로써 당신의 흐릿한 지난날에 따라붙을지 모를 편견을 상쇄해야 하오.\"\n\n안드레아의 안색이 눈에 띄게 바뀌었다.\n\n\"내가 당신의 보증인이자 다정한 조언자로 나서고 싶소만,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"내게는 가장 가까운 벗조차 도덕적으로 미덥지 않게 여기는 버릇이 있고, 남들도 그들을 의심하도록 이끌고 싶은 일종의 성향이 있소. 그러니 그 원칙을 벗어난다면 나는 (배우들 말마따나) 내 배역에서 한참 벗어난 연기를 하는 꼴이 될 테고, 그리하여 야유를 받을 위험을 무릅쓰게 될 텐데, 그건 어리석은 짓이지요.\"\n\n\"하지만, 각하,\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"저를 각하께 천거해 주신 윌모어 경을 보아서라도……\"\n\n\"그렇소, 물론이오.\" 몬테크리스토가 가로막았다. \"하지만 윌모어 경은, 친애하는 안드레아 씨, 당신의 젊은 시절이 자못 폭풍 같았다는 것도 빠뜨리지 않고 일러 주었소. 아,\" 백작이 안드레아의 표정을 살피며 말했다. \"당신에게 어떤 고백을 요구하는 건 아니오. 바로 그런 필요를 면하게 하려고 당신 아버지를 루카에서 모셔 온 것이니까. 곧 그분을 보게 될 거요. 거동이 좀 뻣뻣하고 거드름을 피우는 데다 군복 탓에 볼품이 없긴 하나, 그분이 십팔 년 동안 오스트리아 군에서 복무했다는 사실이 알려지면 그 모든 게 용서될 거요. 우리는 대체로 오스트리아 사람들에게 그리 매몰차지 않으니까. 요컨대, 당신 아버지는 자못 내세울 만한 인물이라는 걸, 내 장담하오.\"\n\n\"아, 백작님, 제게 자신감을 주시는군요. 헤어진 지 워낙 오래라 그분에 대한 기억이라곤 조금도 없습니다. 게다가 백작님도 아시다시피, 세상의 눈에는 막대한 재산이 모든 결함을 덮어 주는 법이니까요.\"\n\n\"그분은 백만장자요. 수입이 50만 프랑이지요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 젊은이가 조바심 어린 어조로 말했다. \"저는 분명 흡족한 처지에 놓이겠군요.\"\n\n\"더없이 흡족한 처지 중 하나일 거요, 친애하는 양반. 그분은 당신이 파리에 머무는 동안 내내 연 5만 리브르의 수입을 허락하실 거요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그 경우, 저는 언제까지나 이곳에 머무는 쪽을 택하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"형편이란 마음대로 되는 게 아니오, 친애하는 양반. '계획은 사람이 하고, 결정은 신이 하는 법'이니까.\" 안드레아가 한숨을 내쉬었다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 그가 말했다. \"제가 파리에 정말 머무는 한, 그리고 그곳을 떠나야 할 일이 아무것도 없는 한, 방금 말씀하신 그 액수를 받으리라 믿어도 된다는 말씀이신지요?\"\n\n\"믿어도 되오.\"\n\n\"그 돈을 제 아버지에게서 받게 됩니까?\" 안드레아가 다소 불안한 기색으로 물었다.\n\n\"그렇소, 당신 아버지에게서 직접 받게 되오. 하지만 그 돈의 보증은 윌모어 경이 설 거요. 그분은 당신 아버지의 부탁으로 당글라르 씨네에 매달 5천 프랑짜리 계좌를 텄는데, 거기가 파리에서 가장 안전한 은행 중 하나지요.\"\n\n\"그런데 제 아버지는 파리에 오래 머무실 생각이신가요?\" 안드레아가 물었다.\n\n\"고작 며칠이오.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"그분의 직무상 한 번에 이삼 주 넘게 자리를 비울 수 없으니까.\"\n\n\"아, 사랑하는 제 아버지!\" 안드레아가 외쳤다. 아버지가 곧 떠난다는 생각에 분명히 마음이 흡족한 듯했다.\n\n\"그러니,\" 몬테크리스토가 그 속내를 짐짓 잘못 알아들은 척하며 말했다. \"그러니 나는 두 분이 만나는 기쁨을 한순간도 더 미루지 않겠소. 그 훌륭한 아버지를 끌어안을 준비가 되셨소?\"\n\n\"의심치 않으시길 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 젊은 벗이여, 저 응접실로 들어가시오. 거기서 아버지가 당신을 기다리고 계실 거요.\"\n\n안드레아는 백작에게 깊이 고개를 숙여 절하고 옆방으로 들어갔다. 몬테크리스토는 그가 사라질 때까지 지켜보다가, 그림처럼 보이도록 만든 벽판의 용수철을 눌렀다. 그러자 벽판이 틀에서 일부 미끄러져 나오면서 작은 구멍이 드러났는데, 어찌나 교묘하게 고안되었던지 이제 카발칸티와 안드레아가 들어가 있는 응접실에서 벌어지는 일이 죄다 보였다. 젊은이는 등 뒤로 문을 닫고, 발소리가 다가오는 것을 듣고 일어선 소령 쪽으로 다가갔다.\n\n\"아, 사랑하는 아버지!\" 안드레아가 옆방의 백작이 들을 수 있도록 큰 소리로 말했다. \"정말 아버지이십니까?\"\n\n\"잘 있었느냐, 사랑하는 아들아?\" 소령이 점잖게 말했다.\n\n\"이토록 오랜 세월 가슴 아픈 이별을 겪고 나서,\" 안드레아가 같은 어조로, 문 쪽을 흘끔거리며 말했다. \"다시 만나니 이 얼마나 큰 행복인지요!\"\n\n\"정말 그렇구나, 그토록 오랜 이별 끝이니.\"\n\n\"저를 안아 주지 않으시겠습니까, 아버지?\" 안드레아가 말했다.\n\n\"네가 원한다면, 아들아.\" 소령이 말했다. 그러고서 두 사내는 무대 위 배우들이 하듯 서로를 끌어안았다. 다시 말해, 각자 상대의 어깨에 제 머리를 얹었다.\n\n\"그럼 우리는 다시 한번 한자리에 모인 거군요?\" 안드레아가 말했다.\n\n\"다시 한번이지.\" 소령이 대답했다.\n\n\"이제 다시는 헤어지지 않는 거고요?\"\n\n\"글쎄다, 그 점에 대해서는, 사랑하는 아들아, 너도 이쯤 되면 프랑스에 워낙 익숙해져서 이곳을 거의 제2의 조국처럼 여기게 됐을 듯한데.\"\n\n\"실은,\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"이곳을 떠난다면 저는 무척이나 서글플 겁니다.\"\n\n\"나로 말하자면, 너도 알다시피 루카를 벗어나서는 도무지 살 수가 없단다. 그러니 나는 되도록 빨리 이탈리아로 돌아갈 작정이야.\"\n\n\"하지만 프랑스를 떠나시기 전에, 사랑하는 아버지, 제 혈통을 입증해 줄 서류들을 제게 넘겨주시길 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"물론이지. 바로 그 일로 일부러 온 것이란다. 너를 찾는 데 어지간히 애를 먹었다만, 그것들을 네 손에 쥐여 주리라 마음먹었지. 다시 처음부터 너를 찾아 나서야 한다 해도, 내게 남은 몇 안 되는 세월을 다 바쳤을 게다.\"\n\n\"그 서류들은 어디 있습니까?\"\n\n\"여기 있다.\"\n\n안드레아는 아버지의 혼인 증서와 자신의 세례 등록부를 낚아챘다. 그러고는 그런 상황에서 능히 짐작할 만한 열의로 그것을 펼쳐 든 뒤, 비슷한 서류를 다루는 데 익숙한 사람임을 증명하는 능란함으로, 그리고 그 내용에 남다른 관심을 품었음을 또렷이 드러내는 표정으로 읽어 내려갔다. 서류를 다 훑고 나자 형언할 수 없는 기쁨의 빛이 그의 얼굴을 환히 밝혔고, 그는 자못 묘한 미소를 띤 채 소령을 바라보며 더없이 훌륭한 토스카나 말로 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 이제 이탈리아에는 갤리선 노역형이라는 게 더는 없는 모양이군요?\"\n\n소령이 몸을 한껏 곧추세웠다.\n\n\"왜?, 그 질문은 무슨 뜻이냐?\"\n\n\"제 말은, 만약 그게 있다면 이런 증서 두 장을 무사히 꾸며 낸다는 건 불가능했을 거란 뜻입니다. 프랑스에서라면, 영감님, 이런 뻔뻔한 짓의 절반만 해도 바람이나 쐬라고 5년 동안 툴롱으로 후딱 보내질 텐데요.\"\n\n\"무슨 말인지 좀 설명해 주겠나?\" 소령이 한껏 위엄을 갖춘 태도를 지으려 애쓰며 말했다.\n\n\"친애하는 카발칸티 씨,\" 안드레아가 은밀하게 소령의 팔을 붙잡으며 말했다. \"제 아버지 노릇을 하는 대가로 얼마를 받으셨습니까?\"\n\n소령이 막 입을 열려는데, 안드레아가 낮은 목소리로 말을 이었다.\n\n\"에이, 그러지 마시고요. 제가 먼저 솔직한 본을 보이지요. 저는 당신 아들 노릇을 하는 대가로 일 년에 5만 프랑을 받습니다. 그러니 제가 부모를 부인할 리는 결코 없으리란 걸 아실 만하지요.\"\n\n소령이 불안한 듯 주위를 둘러보았다.\n\n\"마음 놓으세요, 우리 둘뿐입니다.\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"게다가 우리는 이탈리아 말로 얘기하고 있고요.\"\n\n\"음, 그렇다면,\" 소령이 대답했다. \"나는 일시불로 5만 프랑을 받았네.\"\n\n\"카발칸티 씨,\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"당신은 동화를 믿으십니까?\"\n\n\"예전엔 안 믿었네만, 이젠 정말이지 거의 믿지 않을 수 없게 됐어.\"\n\n\"그럼 생각을 바꾸도록 이끌릴 만한 일이 있었던 거군요. 그게 사실이라는 무슨 증거라도 보셨습니까?\" 소령이 주머니에서 금화 한 줌을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"더없이 또렷한 증거지.\" 그가 말했다. \"보다시피 말이야.\"\n\n\"그럼 백작의 약속을 믿어도 되겠다고 생각하시는 거지요?\"\n\n\"물론이지.\"\n\n\"그가 제게 한 말을 꼭 지키리라 확신하시고요?\"\n\n\"한 치 어김없이 지킬 거야. 다만 동시에, 잊지 말게나, 우리는 각자 제 배역을 계속 연기해야 해. 나는 자애로운 아버지로서……\"\n\n\"그리고 저는 효성스러운 아들로서요, 그들이 제가 당신의 후손이기를 바라니까요.\"\n\n\"그들이라니, 누구를 말하는 건가?\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 저도 딱히 말하기 어렵습니다만, 그 편지를 쓴 자들을 가리킨 거였습니다. 당신도 한 통 받으셨지요?\"\n\n\"받았네.\"\n\n\"누구한테서요?\"\n\n\"부소니 신부라는 자한테서.\"\n\n\"그자를 좀 아십니까?\"\n\n\"아니, 한 번도 본 적 없어.\"\n\n\"편지에 뭐라고 쓰여 있던가요?\"\n\n\"나를 발설하지 않겠다고 약속하겠나?\"\n\n\"그건 안심하셔도 됩니다. 우리 둘의 이해가 똑같다는 건 잘 아시잖습니까.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 직접 읽어 보게.\" 소령이 젊은이의 손에 편지 한 통을 건넸다. 안드레아가 낮은 목소리로 읽었다.\n\n\"'그대는 가난하고, 비참한 노년이 그대를 기다리고 있소. 부유해지고 싶지 않소, 아니면 적어도 남에게 매이지 않고 살고 싶지 않소? 즉시 파리로 떠나, 샹젤리제 거리 30번지의 몬테크리스토 백작에게 가서, 그대가 코르시나리 후작 부인에게서 얻었다가 다섯 살 때 빼앗긴 아들을 내놓으라 요구하시오. 그 아들의 이름은 안드레아 카발칸티요. 이 편지를 쓰는 이의 호의를 의심치 않도록, 피렌체의 고치 씨에게서 지급되는 2,400프랑짜리 어음을 동봉하오. 아울러 몬테크리스토 백작에게 보내는 소개장도 넣었으니, 그 백작 앞으로 4만 8천 프랑짜리 어음을 발행해 두었소. 5월 26일 저녁 일곱 시에 백작을 찾아갈 것을 잊지 마시오.\n\n(서명) 부소니 신부.'\"\n\n\"똑같군요.\"\n\n\"무슨 소린가?\" 소령이 말했다.\n\n\"저도 거의 똑같은 취지의 편지를 받았다는 말입니다.\"\n\n\"자네가?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"부소니 신부한테서?\"\n\n\"아니요.\"\n\n\"그럼 누구한테서?\"\n\n\"뱃사람 신드바드라는 이름을 쓰는 윌모어 경이라는 영국인한테서요.\"\n\n\"내가 부소니 신부를 모르는 것만큼이나 자네도 그자를 모르는 거고?\"\n\n\"틀리셨습니다. 그 점에선 제가 영감님보다 한 수 위지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 자네는 그자를 본 적이 있단 말인가?\"\n\n\"네, 한 번요.\"\n\n\"어디서?\"\n\n\"아, 그건 제가 말씀드릴 수 없는 부분입니다. 말씀드리면 영감님을 저만큼 똑똑하게 만들어 드리는 셈인데, 저는 그럴 생각이 없거든요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그 편지에는 뭐라고 쓰여 있던가?\"\n\n\"읽어 보세요.\"\n\n\"'그대는 가난하고, 그대의 앞날은 어둡고 음울하오. 이름을 얻고 싶소? 부유해지고, 제 한 몸의 주인이 되고 싶소?'\"\n\n\"세상에!\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"그런 물음에 대답이 둘일 수가 있겠습니까?\"\n\n\"'니스로 들어서면서 포르트 드 젠 앞에 대기 중인 역마차를 타시오. 토리노, 샹베리, 퐁드보부아쟁을 거치시오. 5월 26일 저녁 일곱 시에 샹젤리제 거리의 몬테크리스토 백작에게 가서, 그에게 그대의 아버지를 내놓으라 요구하시오. 그대는 카발칸티 후작과 올리바 코르시나리 후작 부인의 아들이오. 후작이 이 사실을 증명하고 그대가 그 이름으로 파리 사교계에 나설 수 있도록 해 줄 서류 몇 가지를 줄 것이오. 그대의 신분으로 말하자면, 연 5만 리브르의 수입이면 그 신분을 훌륭히 떠받쳐 줄 것이오. 니스의 은행가 페레아 씨에게서 지급되는 5천 리브르짜리 어음을 동봉하며, 몬테크리스토 백작에게 보내는 소개장도 넣었소. 그 백작에게 그대의 필요를 모두 채워 주도록 일러 두었소.\n\n뱃사람 신드바드.'\"\n\n\"흠,\" 소령이 말했다. \"아주 좋군. 그래, 백작을 만났다고 했지?\"\n\n\"방금 그한테서 나오는 길입니다.\"\n\n\"그래서 편지에 명시된 그대로 다 응해 주던가?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"무슨 영문인지 알겠나?\"\n\n\"전혀요.\"\n\n\"어딘가에 호구가 하나 있는 거야.\"\n\n\"어쨌든 그게 영감님이나 저는 아니지요.\"\n\n\"물론 아니지.\"\n\n\"그럼 뭐……\"\n\n\"그게 우리하고는 별 상관없는 일 아니냐, 안 그러냐?\"\n\n\"그렇지요. 그 점은 저도 동감입니다. 우리는 이 판을 끝까지 끌고 가야 하고, 눈가림을 당하는 데 기꺼이 응해야지요.\"\n\n\"아, 두고 보세요. 제가 제 배역을 기막히게 해낼 테니 약속드립니다.\"\n\n\"자네가 그러리라는 건 한 번도 의심한 적 없네.\" 몬테크리스토는 바로 이 순간을 골라 응접실로 다시 들어섰다. 그의 발소리가 들리자 두 사내는 서로의 품에 와락 안겼고, 그렇게 한창 끌어안고 있는 와중에 백작이 들어섰다.\n\n\"자, 후작,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"행운이 되돌려 준 그 아들에게 조금도 실망하지 않으신 모양이군요.\"\n\n\"아, 각하, 기쁨에 겨워 어찌할 바를 모르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 당신의 심정은 어떻소?\" 몬테크리스토가 젊은이를 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n\"저로 말하면, 가슴이 행복으로 벅차오릅니다.\"\n\n\"행복한 아버지, 행복한 아들이로군!\" 백작이 말했다.\n\n\"한 가지만이 저를 슬프게 합니다.\" 소령이 말했다. \"그건 제가 이토록 빨리 파리를 떠나야만 한다는 것이지요.\"\n\n\"아, 친애하는 카발칸티 씨, 부디 내 벗 몇몇에게 당신을 소개하는 영광을 누리기 전에는 떠나지 말아 주시오.\"\n\n\"분부대로 하지요.\" 소령이 대답했다.\n\n\"자, 그럼,\" 몬테크리스토가 안드레아를 향해 말했다. \"고백을 좀 해 보시지요.\"\n\n\"누구에게요?\"\n\n\"당신 형편이 어떤지 카발칸티 씨에게 좀 털어놓으시오.\"\n\n\"이런, 백작님, 아픈 데를 건드리시는군요.\"\n\n\"이 아이가 하는 말 들으셨소, 소령?\"\n\n\"물론 들었습니다.\"\n\n\"한데 무슨 뜻인지 알아들으셨소?\"\n\n\"알아들었습니다.\"\n\"당신 아들이 돈이 필요하다는군요.\"\n\n\"음, 그럼 저더러 어쩌라는 말씀이십니까?\" 소령이 말했다.\n\n\"당연히 좀 마련해 주셔야지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다.\n\"제가요?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 당신이요.\" 백작이 말하며 동시에 안드레아 쪽으로 다가가, 젊은이의 손에 지폐 한 다발을 슬며시 쥐여 주었다.\n\n\"이게 뭡니까?\"\n\n\"당신 아버지가 주시는 거요.\"\n\n\"제 아버지가요?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 방금 그분께 돈이 필요하다 말하지 않았소? 자, 그래서 그분이 이걸 당신에게 주라고 내게 맡기신 거요.\"\n\n\"이걸 제 수입의 일부를 미리 받는 걸로 여기면 됩니까?\"\n\n\"아니, 이건 당신이 파리에 자리 잡는 데 드는 첫 비용이오.\"\n\n\"아, 사랑하는 제 아버지는 어쩌면 이리도 좋으신지요!\"\n\n\"쉿,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그분은 이게 자신에게서 나온 것임을 당신이 알기를 바라지 않으시오.\"\n\n\"그 세심한 마음 씀씀이를 충분히 헤아립니다.\" 안드레아가 지폐를 황급히 주머니에 쑤셔 넣으며 말했다.\n\n\"자, 그럼 두 분, 안녕히 가시오.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"한데 각하, 언제 다시 뵙는 영광을 누릴 수 있겠습니까?\" 카발칸티가 물었다.\n\n\"아,\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"언제 그 기쁨을 기대해도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"좋으시다면 토요일에. 그래요. 가만있자, 토요일이라. 그날 나는 오퇴유에 있는 시골 별장에서 만찬을 들 예정이오. 퐁텐 거리 28번지지요. 여러 사람이 초대받았는데, 그중에 당신의 은행가인 당글라르 씨도 있소. 그에게 당신을 소개하리다. 그가 당신 돈을 지급할 사람이니, 당신을 알아둘 필요가 있을 테니까.\"\n\n\"정장을 해야 합니까?\" 소령이 반쯤 소리 내어 물었다.\n\n\"오, 그렇소, 물론이오.\" 백작이 말했다. \"군복에, 훈장에, 반바지까지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 저는 어떻게 차려입어야 합니까?\" 안드레아가 물었다.\n\n\"오, 아주 간소하게. 검은 바지에, 에나멜 가죽 장화, 흰 조끼, 검정이나 파랑 상의, 그리고 긴 넥타이면 되오. 옷은 블랭이나 베로니크에 가서 맞추시오. 주소를 모르거든 바티스탱이 어디인지 일러 줄 거요. 차림새에 허세가 적을수록 효과는 더 좋을 거요. 당신은 부자니까 말이오. 말을 좀 사려거든 드브되에서 구하고, 사륜마차를 살 거면 바티스트에게 가시오.\"\n\n\"몇 시에 가면 됩니까?\" 젊은이가 물었다.\n\n\"여섯 시 반쯤에.\"\n\n\"그 시각에 찾아뵙겠습니다.\" 소령이 말했다. 두 카발칸티는 백작에게 절하고 집을 나섰다. 몬테크리스토는 창가로 가, 그들이 팔짱을 낀 채 거리를 가로질러 가는 모습을 바라보았다.\n\n\"저기 악당 둘이 가는군.\" 그가 말했다. \"정말로 부자지간이 아닌 게 안타깝구나!\" 그러고는 잠시 음울한 상념에 잠겼다가, 입을 열었다. \"자, 모렐 가족을 보러 가야겠다. 혐오감이란 증오보다 한층 더 속을 메스껍게 하는 것이니.\"\n\n# Chapter 57. In the Lucern Patch\n\n이제 독자들을 다시 빌포르 씨 집을 에워싼 울타리 안으로 데려가야겠다. 사방으로 무성한 가지를 뻗은 커다란 밤나무들에 절반쯤 가려진 철문 뒤에서, 우리는 낯익은 사람 몇몇을 다시 만나게 될 것이다. 이번에는 막시밀리앙이 가장 먼저 도착해 있었다. 그는 나무들 사이로 그림자 하나가 나타나기를 골똘히 살피며, 자갈길 위로 가벼운 발소리가 들려오기를 애타게 기다리고 있었다.\n\n마침내 그토록 기다리던 소리가 들려왔다. 하지만 그가 예상했던 한 사람이 아니라, 두 사람이 다가오는 것이 보였다. 늦어진 것은 당글라르 부인과 외제니가 찾아왔기 때문이었는데, 그 방문이 발랑틴이 약속한 시간을 넘기도록 길어진 탓이었다. 막시밀리앙과의 약속을 어긴 것처럼 보이지 않으려고, 발랑틴은 당글라르 양에게 정원을 거닐자고 제안했다. 분명 그를 속상하게 했을 그 지체가 자신의 소홀함 때문이 아니라는 것을 보여 주고 싶은 마음에서였다. 사랑에 빠진 사람 특유의 직감으로, 청년은 그녀가 어쩔 수 없이 처한 상황을 곧바로 알아차렸고 마음이 놓였다. 게다가 발랑틴은 말을 건넬 수 있을 만큼 가까이 다가오지는 않으면서도, 막시밀리앙이 자신이 오가는 모습을 볼 수 있게 했다. 그리고 그 곁을 지날 때마다, 동행이 눈치채지 못하게 청년에게 의미심장한 눈길을 던졌다. 그 눈길은 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"조금만 참아요! 제 잘못이 아니라는 거 아시잖아요.\"\n\n그래서 막시밀리앙은 참고 기다리며, 마음속으로 두 아가씨를 견주어 보았다. 한 사람은 살결이 희고 부드럽고 그윽한 눈매에, 능수버들처럼 우아하게 휘어진 자태였다. 다른 한 사람은 머리가 검고 사납고 거만한 표정에, 미루나무처럼 꼿꼿했다. 청년의 눈에 발랑틴이 그 비교에서 조금도 뒤지지 않았다는 것은 새삼 말할 필요도 없다. 반 시간쯤 지나 두 아가씨가 자리를 떴고, 막시밀리앙은 당글라르 양의 방문이 마침내 끝났음을 알아차렸다. 잠시 후 발랑틴이 혼자서 정원으로 다시 들어왔다. 돌아오는 모습을 누가 볼까 봐 두려워 그녀는 천천히 걸었다. 그러고는 곧장 철문 쪽으로 가지 않고 벤치에 앉아, 자신을 지켜보는 사람이 없는지 확인하려고 조심스럽게 주위를 둘러보았다. 그런 다음에야 일어나 빠른 걸음으로 막시밀리앙에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"안녕하세요, 발랑틴.\" 귀에 익은 목소리가 말했다.\n\n\"안녕하세요, 막시밀리앙. 오래 기다리게 한 거 알아요. 하지만 제가 왜 늦었는지 보셨잖아요.\"\n\n\"네, 당글라르 양인 걸 알아봤어요. 그렇게 가까운 사이인 줄은 몰랐네요.\"\n\n\"우리가 가까운 사이라고 누가 그러던가요, 막시밀리앙?\"\n\n\"아무도요. 하지만 그렇게 보이던걸요. 둘이서 걸으며 이야기 나누는 모습이, 마치 비밀을 털어놓는 두 여학생 같았어요.\"\n\n\"우리는 은밀한 이야기를 나누고 있었어요.\" 발랑틴이 대답했다. \"그 애는 모르세르 씨와의 결혼이 싫다고 제게 털어놓았고, 저는 저대로 데피네 씨와 결혼한다는 생각만으로도 얼마나 비참한지 고백하던 참이었어요.\"\n\n\"발랑틴!\"\n\n\"그러니 외제니와 제가 그렇게 거리낌 없이 이야기 나눈 까닭을 아시겠죠. 사랑할 수 없는 남자 이야기를 하다 보니, 제 마음이 어느새 사랑하는 사람에게로 돌아갔던 거예요.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇게 말해 주다니 정말 다정하군요, 발랑틴! 당신에게는 당글라르 양은 결코 가질 수 없는 무언가가 있어요. 그건 형언할 수 없는 매력이에요. 꽃에게 향기와 같고, 열매에게 풍미와 같은 것이지요. 꽃이든 열매든, 우리가 찾는 게 아름다움 하나만은 아니니까요.\"\n\n\"그건 당신이 사랑에 빠져서 모든 걸 그렇게 보는 거예요.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 발랑틴. 정말 그렇지 않아요. 두 분이 정원을 거닐 때 제가 유심히 보았는데, 맹세코 당글라르 양의 아름다움을 깎아내릴 생각은 조금도 없지만, 어떤 남자가 진심으로 그녀를 사랑할 수 있을지 도무지 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"실은요, 막시밀리앙, 그 자리에 제가 있었으니, 제가 있어서 당신이 그 비교에서 공정하지 못했던 거예요.\"\n\n\"아니에요. 그런데 한 가지 물어봐도 될까요. 그저 호기심에서 묻는 건데, 당글라르 양과 관련해서 제 마음속에 떠오른 어떤 생각 때문에요……\"\n\n\"분명 무슨 헐뜯는 말을 하려는 거겠죠. 당신네 남자들에게서 너그러움이라곤 거의 기대할 수 없다는 걸 보여 주는 셈이네요.\" 발랑틴이 말을 가로막았다.\n\n\"적어도 여자들끼리 서로 아주 가혹하게 평가한다는 건 부인 못 하시겠죠.\"\n\n\"우리가 그런다면, 대개 흥분한 상태에서 판단하기 때문이에요. 하지만 질문으로 돌아가세요.\"\n\n\"당글라르 양이 모르세르 씨와의 결혼을 마다하는 게, 다른 사람을 사랑하기 때문인가요?\"\n\n\"외제니와 그렇게 막역한 사이는 아니라고 했잖아요.\"\n\n\"네, 하지만 여자들은 특별히 친하지 않아도 비밀을 주고받잖아요. 자, 솔직히 말해 봐요. 그 일을 캐물었죠? 아, 웃는 걸 보니 알겠네요.\"\n\n\"우리가 나눈 이야기를 벌써 다 알고 있다니, 우리와 당신 사이를 가로막은 나무 칸막이가 그리 단단한 차단막은 아니었나 봐요.\"\n\n\"자, 그 애가 뭐라던가요?\"\n\n\"누구도 사랑하지 않는다고 했어요.\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"결혼한다는 생각 자체가 싫고, 자유롭고 얽매이지 않는 삶이 훨씬 좋다고요. 그리고 차라리 아버지가 재산을 다 잃었으면 좋겠다고, 그러면 친구인 루이즈 다르밀리 양처럼 예술가가 될 수 있을 거라고 했어요.\"\n\n\"아, 그것 봐요……\"\n\n\"그래서, 그게 뭘 증명하나요?\" 발랑틴이 물었다.\n\n\"아무것도요.\" 막시밀리앙이 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 왜 웃었어요?\"\n\n\"아니, 발랑틴, 당신도 잘 알잖아요. 지금 당신 자신을 두고 하는 말이라는 걸요.\"\n\n\"제가 가 버리길 바라나요?\"\n\n\"아, 아니에요, 아니에요. 하지만 시간을 허비하지 맙시다. 제가 이야기하고 싶은 건 바로 당신이니까요.\"\n\n\"맞아요, 서둘러야 해요. 함께 있을 시간이 채 십 분도 남지 않았어요.\"\n\n\"이런!\" 막시밀리앙이 낙담하여 말했다.\n\n\"네, 당신 말이 맞아요. 저는 당신에게 부족한 사람이에요. 제가 당신을 어떤 삶으로 끌어들이고 있는지, 가엾은 막시밀리앙, 행복하게 살아야 할 당신을요! 정말이지 저 자신을 모질게 책망하고 있어요.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 상관이에요, 발랑틴, 제가 만족하는데요. 이 길고 괴로운 기다림도 당신과 함께하는 오 분, 당신 입에서 나오는 두 마디 말이면 충분히 보상받는걸요. 게다가 저는 굳게 믿어요. 하늘이 우리처럼 이토록 어우러지는 두 마음을 만들어 거의 기적처럼 맺어 주고서, 끝내 갈라놓지는 않으리라고요.\"\n\n\"다정하고 힘이 나는 말이네요. 우리 둘 몫의 희망을 당신이 품어 주세요, 막시밀리앙. 그러면 저도 적어도 절반쯤은 행복할 거예요.\"\n\n\"그런데 왜 이렇게 빨리 가 봐야 하나요?\"\n\n\"자세한 건 몰라요. 빌포르 부인이 저를 부르러 사람을 보냈다는 것밖에는요. 제 재산의 일부가 걸린 일로 할 말이 있다고요. 재산이야 가져가라죠, 저는 이미 너무 부유하니까요. 어쩌면 다 가져가고 나면 저를 평화롭고 조용하게 내버려 둘지도 모르죠. 제가 가난해도 지금처럼 사랑해 주시겠죠, 막시밀리앙?\"\n\n\"아, 저는 언제까지나 당신을 사랑할 거예요. 제 발랑틴이 곁에 있고, 누구도 당신을 제게서 앗아 갈 수 없다는 확신만 있다면, 부유함이든 가난함이든 제게 무슨 상관이겠어요? 그런데 혹시 그 할 말이라는 게 당신 결혼에 관한 건 아닐까 걱정되지 않나요?\"\n\n\"그건 아닐 거예요.\"\n\n\"어찌 됐든, 발랑틴, 놀라지 말아요. 맹세컨대, 제가 살아 있는 한 결코 다른 사람을 사랑하지 않을 테니까요!\"\n\n\"그렇게 말하면 제가 안심할 거라고 생각하세요, 막시밀리앙?\"\n\n\"미안해요, 당신 말이 맞아요. 제가 짐승 같았네요. 하지만 사실은 며칠 전에 모르세르 씨를 만났다는 얘기를 하려던 거였어요.\"\n\n\"그래서요?\"\n\"프란츠 씨가 그의 친구잖아요.\"\n\n\"그런데요?\"\n\n\"모르세르 씨가 프란츠한테서 편지를 받았는데, 곧 돌아오겠다는 거였어요.\" 발랑틴의 얼굴이 창백해지더니, 그녀는 철문에 손을 기댔다.\n\n\"아, 맙소사, 만약 그 일이라면! 하지만 아니에요, 그런 거라면 빌포르 부인을 통해 전해질 리가 없어요.\"\n\n\"왜 아니죠?\"\n\n\"왜냐하면…… 저도 잘은 모르지만…… 빌포르 부인이 드러내 놓고 반대하지는 않으면서도, 속으로는 그 결혼을 마뜩잖아하는 것 같았거든요.\"\n\n\"그래요? 그렇다면 빌포르 부인을 흠모할 수도 있을 것 같은데요.\"\n\n\"그렇게 서둘러 흠모하지는 말아요.\" 발랑틴이 서글픈 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"당신이 데피네 씨와 결혼하는 걸 반대한다면, 다른 청혼이라면 더 귀 기울여 들어 줄 가능성이 높잖아요.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 막시밀리앙. 빌포르 부인이 반대하는 건 구혼자가 아니라 결혼 그 자체예요.\"\n\n\"결혼이요? 결혼을 그토록 싫어한다면, 부인은 왜 결혼했답니까?\"\n\n\"제 말을 못 알아들으시네요, 막시밀리앙. 일 년쯤 전에 제가 수녀원에 들어가겠다고 했어요. 빌포르 부인은 의무라고 여겨 이런저런 말을 늘어놓기는 했지만, 속으로는 그 제안을 반겼지요. 부인이 부추기는 바람에 아버지도 동의했고요. 결국 제가 그 계획을 접은 건 오로지 가엾은 할아버지 때문이었어요. 그분이 저를 바라보실 때의 그 눈빛을, 당신은 상상도 못 할 거예요. 세상에서 그분이 사랑하는 단 한 사람이 저거든요. 그리고, 거의 단언컨대, 그분도 저에게 사랑받는 단 한 사람이고요. 제 결심을 아셨을 때, 그분이 제게 던진 그 책망 어린 눈빛과, 생기 없는 두 뺨을 줄줄이 타고 흐르던 절망의 눈물을 저는 결코 잊지 못할 거예요. 아, 막시밀리앙, 그 순간 저는 제 결심에 어찌나 후회가 밀려오던지, 그분 발치에 몸을 던지며 외쳤어요. '용서하세요, 부디 용서해 주세요, 할아버지. 저들이 저를 어떻게 하든, 저는 절대 할아버지를 떠나지 않을게요.' 제 말이 끝나자 그분은 고마워하며 하늘로 눈을 들어 올리셨지만, 한마디도 하지 않으셨어요. 아, 막시밀리앙, 제가 앞으로 많은 고통을 겪을지도 몰라요. 하지만 그 순간 할아버지의 그 눈빛이 그 모든 것을 보상하고도 남으리라는 느낌이 들어요.\"\n\n\"발랑틴, 당신은 완벽한 천사예요. 그런데 제가, 베두인족 사이를 누비며 좌우로 칼을 휘둘러 온 제가, 당신 같은 사람을 만날 자격이 있다고 한 일이 뭐가 있는지 정말 모르겠어요. 굳이 따지자면, 하늘이 제 칼에 쓰러진 자들이 이교도였다는 사실을 참작해 주신 거겠지요. 그런데 빌포르 부인이 당신이 결혼하지 않는 데서 무슨 이득을 본다는 거예요?\"\n\n\"제가 방금 부유하다고, 너무 부유하다고 하지 않았나요, 막시밀리앙? 어머니 쪽으로 5만 리브르 가까이 되는 재산이 제게 있어요. 외할아버지와 외할머니, 생메랑 후작 부부도 그만큼을 제게 남겨 주실 거고요. 누아르티에 할아버지도 분명 저를 상속인으로 삼으실 작정이세요. 어머니에게서 아무것도 물려받지 못하는 남동생 에두아르는 그러니 저에 비하면 가난할 거예요. 그런데 제가 수녀가 되었더라면, 이 모든 재산이 아버지에게로, 그리고 다시 그 아들에게로 넘어갔겠지요.\"\n\n\"아, 저토록 젊고 아름다운 여인이 그렇게 탐욕스러울 수 있다니 참 이상하네요.\"\n\n\"자기 자신을 위한 게 아니라 아들을 위한 거예요. 당신이 악덕이라 여기는 것도 모성애의 빛으로 비추어 보면 거의 미덕에 가까워지지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 절충할 수는 없나요? 당신 재산의 일부를 그 아들에게 떼어 주는 거예요.\"\n\n\"제가 어떻게 그런 제안을 하겠어요? 더구나 늘 자기는 사심이 전혀 없다고 공언하는 여자한테요.\"\n\n\"발랑틴, 저는 늘 우리의 사랑을 신성한 무언가로 여겨 왔어요. 그래서 그 사랑을 존중의 베일로 감싸, 제 영혼 가장 깊은 곳에 감추어 두었지요. 어떤 사람도, 제 누이조차도 그 존재를 몰라요. 발랑틴, 친구 한 사람을 믿고, 그에게 당신을 향한 제 사랑을 털어놓아도 될까요?\"\n\n발랑틴이 흠칫했다. \"친구라니요, 막시밀리앙. 그 친구가 누구죠? 허락하기가 떨려요.\"\n\n\"들어 봐요, 발랑틴. 누군가에게 갑작스럽고 거부할 수 없는 호감을 느낀 적 없나요? 실은 처음 만난 사람인데도, 마치 오래전부터 알고 지낸 친한 벗처럼 느껴지는 그런 감정 말이에요. 아니, 한 걸음 더 나아가, 그 사람과 예전에 어울렸던 때며 장소며 상황을 떠올려 보려 애쓰다가, 끝내 떠올리지 못하고는, 지금에 앞선 어떤 세상에서 우리 영혼들이 서로 이야기를 나누었던 게 틀림없으며, 지금은 그저 그 옛 기억을 더듬고 있을 뿐이라고 믿게 된 적은요?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"바로 그게 제가 그 비범한 사람을 처음 보았을 때 느낀 감정이에요.\"\n\n\"비범하다고요?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"그럼 안 지 꽤 됐나요?\"\n\n\"기껏해야 여드레나 열흘쯤 됐어요.\"\n\n\"안 지 여드레나 열흘밖에 안 된 사람을 친구라고 부르는 거예요? 아, 막시밀리앙, 저는 당신이 친구라는 이름에 더 높은 값을 매기는 줄 알았어요.\"\n\n\"당신 논리가 더없이 강력하군요, 발랑틴. 하지만 뭐라 하셔도, 본능적으로 제 마음을 사로잡은 이 감정을 저는 결코 버릴 수 없어요. 마치 미래가 제게 마련해 둔 온갖 좋은 일이 이 사람과 이어지도록 정해져 있는 것만 같아요. 그리고 가끔은 정말이지, 그의 눈이 다가올 일을 꿰뚫어 보고, 그의 손이 사건을 제 뜻대로 좌우하는 힘을 지닌 듯해요.\"\n\n\"그럼 예언자인가 보네요.\" 발랑틴이 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"정말이지,\" 막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"저는 종종 그에게 예언의 능력이 있다고 여기고 싶은 충동마저 느꼈어요. 어쨌든 그는 앞으로 닥칠 좋은 일을 내다보는 놀라운 능력을 지녔어요.\"\n\n\"아,\" 발랑틴이 구슬픈 목소리로 말했다. \"그 사람을 한번 만나게 해 주세요, 막시밀리앙. 제가 그동안 겪은 모든 고통을 보상받을 만큼 충분히 사랑받게 될지, 그가 말해 줄지도 모르잖아요.\"\n\n\"가엾은 발랑틴, 당신은 이미 그를 알고 있어요.\"\n\n\"제가 그를 안다고요?\"\n\n\"네. 당신 계모와 그 아들의 목숨을 구해 준 사람이 바로 그 사람이에요.\"\n\n\"몬테크리스토 백작이요?\"\n\n\"바로 그 사람이에요.\"\n\n\"아,\" 발랑틴이 외쳤다. \"그 사람은 빌포르 부인과 너무 가까운 친구라, 제 친구는 결코 될 수 없어요.\"\n\n\"빌포르 부인의 친구라니! 그럴 리가요. 발랑틴, 분명 잘못 아신 거예요.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 정말 그렇지 않아요. 맹세코, 우리 집안에서 그 사람의 영향력은 거의 무한해요. 그를 인간 지혜의 정수라 여기며 떠받드는 계모, 그렇게 숭고한 생각을 그토록 유려하게 풀어내는 걸 처음 들어 본다고 감탄하는 아버지, 백작의 커다란 검은 눈을 무서워하면서도 그가 도착하는 순간 달려가 손을 펴면 어김없이 근사한 선물이 쥐어지기에 그를 우상처럼 떠받드는 에두아르까지. 몬테크리스토 백작은 우리 집안 식구 모두에게 알 수 없는, 거의 거역할 수 없는 영향력을 행사하는 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 발랑틴, 당신 자신도 이미 그 존재의 영향을 느꼈거나, 아니면 머지않아 느끼게 될 거예요. 그가 이탈리아에서 알베르 드 모르세르를 만난 건 산적들 손아귀에서 그를 구해 내기 위해서였고, 당글라르 부인에게 자기를 소개한 건 그녀에게 왕에게나 어울릴 선물을 주기 위해서였어요. 당신 계모와 그 아들이 그의 집 앞을 지난 것도, 그의 누비아인이 그들을 죽음에서 구하게 하기 위해서였지요. 이 사람은 분명 사람이든 사물이든, 사건에 영향을 미치는 힘을 지녔어요. 그토록 소박한 취향과 그토록 대단한 호화로움이 한데 어우러진 사람을 저는 본 적이 없어요. 그가 제게 말을 건넬 때면 그 미소가 어찌나 다정한지, 그 미소가 다른 이들에게는 쓰디쓸 수도 있다는 걸 잊어버릴 정도예요. 아, 발랑틴, 말해 봐요. 그가 그 다정한 미소 중 하나로 당신을 바라본 적이 있나요? 그렇다면 분명, 당신은 행복해질 거예요.\"\n\n\"저요?\" 아가씨가 말했다. \"그는 저를 거들떠보지도 않아요. 오히려 제가 어쩌다 그의 앞을 지나가면, 차라리 저를 피하는 것 같던걸요. 아, 그는 너그럽지도 않고, 당신이 말하는 그 초자연적 통찰력을 지니지도 않았어요. 그게 있었다면 제가 불행하다는 걸 알아챘을 테니까요. 또 너그러웠다면, 슬프고 외로운 저를 보고 자기 영향력을 제게 유리하게 써 주었겠지요. 당신 말마따나 그가 태양을 닮았다면, 그 생명을 주는 빛줄기 하나로 제 마음을 데워 주었을 테고요. 막시밀리앙, 그가 당신을 사랑한다고 하셨죠? 그걸 어떻게 알아요? 다들 당신처럼 사나운 콧수염에 긴 군도를 찬 장교에게는 깍듯이 대하지만, 가엾게 흐느끼는 아가씨 하나쯤은 마음 놓고 짓밟아도 된다고 여기는 법이에요.\"\n\n\"아, 발랑틴, 정말 잘못 아신 거예요.\"\n\n\"만약 그렇지 않다면, 다시 말해 그가 저를 노련하게 대했다면, 그러니까 어떻게든 이 집안에 발판을 마련해 끝내 식구들을 마음대로 좌지우지할 힘을 얻으려는 사람처럼 굴었다면, 단 한 번이라도 당신이 그토록 칭송하는 그 미소로 저를 대접했을 거예요. 하지만 아니에요, 그는 제가 불행하다는 걸 보았고, 제가 자기에게 아무 쓸모도 없다는 걸 알아챈 거예요. 그래서 저에게는 도무지 관심을 두지 않은 거고요. 누가 알겠어요, 빌포르 부인과 아버지의 비위를 맞추려고, 온갖 수단을 동원해 저를 괴롭힐지도요? 아무 이유도 없이 저를 그토록 업신여기는 건 옳지 않아요. 아, 미안해요.\" 자기 말이 막시밀리앙에게 미치는 영향을 알아채고 발랑틴이 말했다. \"제가 잘못했어요. 제 마음속에 있는 줄도 몰랐던, 그 사람에 대한 생각을 내뱉었으니까요. 당신이 말하는 그 영향력을 부인하는 것도, 저 자신이 그걸 느껴 보지 못했다는 것도 아니에요. 다만 저에게는 그게 좋은 쪽보다 나쁜 쪽으로 작용했어요.\"\n\n\"좋아요, 발랑틴.\" 모렐이 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"이 문제는 더 이상 따지지 맙시다. 그를 믿고 털어놓지 않을게요.\"\n\n\"아아!\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"제가 당신을 아프게 한 모양이네요. 당신을 슬프게 한 것을, 얼마나 진심으로 용서를 구하는지 말씀드릴 수밖에 없네요. 하지만 정말, 저는 설득당하지 못할 만큼 고집스럽지는 않아요. 그 몬테크리스토 백작이 당신에게 무엇을 해 주었는지 말해 봐요.\"\n\n\"솔직히 그 질문에는 곤란하군요, 발랑틴. 백작이 제게 눈에 보이는 도움을 주었다고는 말할 수 없으니까요. 그래도 앞서 말했듯이, 저는 그에게 본능적인 애정을 느껴요. 그 근원이 무엇인지는 당신에게 설명할 수 없지만요. 태양이 제게 무얼 해 주었나요? 아니죠. 그 빛줄기로 저를 데워 주고, 그 빛 덕분에 제가 당신을 보지요. 그뿐이에요. 어떤 향기가 제게 무얼 해 주었나요? 아니죠. 그 냄새가 제 감각 하나를 매혹할 뿐, 왜 그걸 좋아하느냐고 물으면 그렇게밖에 대답할 수 없어요. 그를 향한 제 우정은, 저를 향한 그의 우정만큼이나 기이하고 설명할 길이 없어요. 어떤 은밀한 목소리가 제게 속삭이는 것 같아요. 이 뜻밖의 서로 통하는 우정에는 우연 이상의 무언가가 있을 거라고요. 그의 더없이 소박한 행동에서도, 더없이 은밀한 생각에서도, 저는 제 자신과의 어떤 연관을 발견해요. 이런 말을 하면 비웃으실지도 모르지만, 이 사람을 알게 된 뒤로 저는 제게 닥친 온갖 행운이 그에게서 비롯되었다는 생각을 저도 모르게 품게 됐어요. 하기야 이 보호 없이도 삼십 년을 살아오지 않았느냐고 하시겠죠. 하지만 제 말이 무슨 뜻인지 조금 풀어 볼게요. 그가 토요일에 함께 저녁을 들자고 저를 초대했는데, 그건 그로서는 아주 자연스러운 일이었어요. 그런데 그 뒤에 제가 뭘 알게 됐는지 아세요? 당신 어머니와 빌포르 씨도 그 저녁 식사에 온다는 거예요. 저는 거기서 그분들을 만날 테고, 그 만남에서 앞으로 어떤 이득이 생길지 누가 알겠어요? 당신에게는 별스러울 것 없는 상황의 우연쯤으로 보일지 몰라요. 그래도 저는 이 자리에 어떤 숨은 계략이, 사실 무심코 보아서는 드러나지 않는 무언가가 있다고 느껴요. 누구의 속내든 꿰뚫어 보는 듯한 이 기이한 사람이, 제가 빌포르 부부를 만나도록 일부러 자리를 마련했다고 저는 믿어요. 그리고 고백하건대, 가끔은 그가 우리 사랑의 비밀을 알고 있는지 그의 눈에서 읽어 내려고까지 했어요.\"\n\n\"막시밀리앙.\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"당신이 늘 이런 식으로 이야기하는 걸 들어야 한다면, 저는 당신을 몽상가로 여기고 당신의 분별력을 걱정할 거예요. 이 만남에서 그저 단순한 우연 이상의 무언가를 본다는 게 말이 되나요? 부디 조금만 생각해 봐요. 도무지 외출하는 법이 없는 아버지는 이 초대를 거절할 뻔한 적이 여러 번이었어요. 반면 빌포르 부인은 이 비범한 부자 나리를 그의 집에서 보고 싶어 안달이 나서, 갖은 애를 써서 아버지를 동행하게 만든 거고요. 아니에요, 아니에요. 제 말대로예요, 막시밀리앙. 이 세상에 제가 도움을 청할 수 있는 사람은 당신과, 송장이나 다름없는 할아버지뿐이에요. 매달릴 버팀목이라곤 하늘에 계신 어머니뿐이고요!\"\n\n\"논리로 따지면 당신 말이 옳다는 걸 알겠어요.\" 막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"하지만 평소에는 제게 그토록 큰 힘을 발휘하던 그 다정한 목소리도, 오늘은 저를 설득하지 못하는군요.\"\n\n\"저도 당신에 대해 똑같이 느껴요.\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"솔직히, 더 강력한 증거를 내놓지 못한다면……\"\n\n\"또 하나 있어요.\" 막시밀리앙이 대답했다. \"하지만 첫 번째보다 더 터무니없다고 여기실까 봐 걱정이네요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 더 안됐네요.\" 발랑틴이 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"그래도 제게는 결정적인 증거예요. 십 년 복무하는 동안에도 저는 갑작스러운 영감에 대한 제 생각을 굳혔어요. 어떤 신비로운 충동에 이끌려 단번에 오른쪽이나 왼쪽으로 몸을 움직인 덕분에, 곁에서 싸우던 전우를 쓰러뜨린 총탄을 피하고 저는 멀쩡했던 적이 여러 번이거든요.\"\n\n\"막시밀리앙, 그 위기를 모면한 게 당신의 안전을 비는 제 한결같은 기도 덕분이라고 생각하면 안 될까요? 당신이 떠나 있을 때, 저는 더 이상 저를 위해 기도하지 않고 당신을 위해 기도해요.\"\n\n\"그렇죠, 당신이 저를 알게 된 뒤로는요.\" 모렐이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"하지만 우리가 만나기 전에는 그 말이 들어맞지 않잖아요, 발랑틴.\"\n\n\"당신은 정말 사람을 약 올리네요. 제게 무얼 하나도 인정해 주질 않아요. 그래도 그 두 번째 증거를 들어 볼게요. 당신 스스로도 터무니없다고 인정한 거요.\"\n\n\"자, 이 틈으로 들여다봐요. 제가 타고 온 아름다운 새 말이 보일 거예요.\"\n\n\"어머! 정말 멋진 녀석이네요!\" 발랑틴이 외쳤다. \"왜 철문 가까이 데려오지 않았어요? 그러면 제가 말도 걸고 쓰다듬어 줄 수 있었을 텐데.\"\n\n\"보다시피 아주 값진 녀석이에요.\" 막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"제 형편이 빠듯하다는 거, 제가 이른바 분수에 맞게 사는 사람이라는 거 아시잖아요. 그런데 말 장수에게 갔다가 이 근사한 말을 봤어요. 제가 메데아라고 이름 붙였지요. 값을 물었더니 4천5백 프랑이라더군요. 그러니 짐작하시겠지만 어쩔 수 없이 포기해야 했어요. 그래도 솔직히 꽤 무거운 마음으로 돌아섰어요. 그 말이 저를 다정하게 바라보더니, 제게 머리를 비비고, 제가 올라타자 더없이 사랑스럽게 껑충껑충 뛰어서, 그만 완전히 반해 버렸거든요. 그날 저녁 친구 몇몇이 저를 찾아왔어요. 샤토르노 씨, 드브레 씨, 그리고 당신은 이름조차 모르는 대여섯 명의 멋쟁이들이었지요. 그들이 부요트 한 판 하자고 했어요. 저는 노름을 안 해요. 잃을 만큼 부유하지도, 따고 싶을 만큼 가난하지도 않으니까요. 하지만 제 집이었으니, 카드를 가져오라고 사람을 보내는 수밖에요. 그래서 그렇게 했지요.\"\n\n\"막 자리에 앉으려는데 몬테크리스토 백작이 도착했어요. 그도 자리에 끼었고, 다들 노름을 했는데 제가 땄어요. 말하기 거의 부끄러울 지경인데, 제가 딴 돈이 5천 프랑이나 됐어요. 우리는 자정에 헤어졌지요. 저는 그 기쁨을 미룰 수가 없어서, 이륜마차를 잡아타고 말 장수에게로 달려갔어요. 들뜨고 흥분한 채로 문 앞에서 초인종을 울렸지요. 문을 연 사람은 저를 미친 사람으로 여겼을 거예요. 제가 곧장 마구간으로 달려갔으니까요. 메데아는 여물통 앞에 서서 건초를 먹고 있었어요. 저는 곧바로 안장과 굴레를 채웠는데, 녀석은 더없이 얌전하게 응해 주었지요. 그러고는 어리둥절해하는 말 장수에게 4천5백 프랑을 쥐여 주고, 밤새 샹젤리제를 달리겠다는 생각을 실행에 옮겼어요. 백작의 집 앞을 지나는데, 창문 하나에 불빛이 보였고, 커튼 뒤로 그의 모습이 어른거리는 것 같았어요. 자, 발랑틴, 저는 굳게 믿어요. 그는 제가 이 말을 갖고 싶어 한다는 걸 알았고, 제게 그 말을 살 수단을 마련해 주려고 일부러 노름에서 져 준 거라고요.\"\n\n\"막시밀리앙, 당신은 정말 너무 공상이 심해요. 그러다간 저조차도 오래 사랑하지 못할 거예요. 그렇게 시와 상상의 세계에서 사는 데 익숙해진 남자는, 우리 같은 평범하고 일상적인 사랑에서는 너무나 흥미를 못 느낄 테니까요. 그런데 저를 부르고 있어요. 들리세요?\"\n\n\"아, 발랑틴.\" 막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"이 격자 틈으로 손가락 하나만, 손가락 하나만 내밀어 줘요. 가장 작은 새끼손가락이라도요. 거기에 입 맞추는 행복을 누리게요.\"\n\n\"막시밀리앙, 우리는 서로에게 두 개의 목소리, 두 개의 그림자로만 있기로 했잖아요.\"\n\n\"좋을 대로 해요, 발랑틴.\"\n\n\"제가 당신 바람대로 하면 행복하겠어요?\"\n\n\"아, 그럼요!\"\n\n발랑틴이 벤치에 올라서서, 손가락뿐 아니라 손 전체를 그 틈으로 내밀었다. 막시밀리앙은 환희의 탄성을 지르며 앞으로 뛰어들어, 자기에게 뻗어 온 손을 붙잡고는 그 위에 뜨겁고 정열적인 입맞춤을 찍었다. 그러자 작은 손이 곧바로 거두어졌고, 청년은 발랑틴이 집을 향해 황급히 달려가는 모습을 보았다. 마치 자기 자신의 감정에 깜짝 놀라기라도 한 듯이.\n\n# Chapter 58. M. Noirtier de Villefort\n\n당글라르 부인과 그 딸이 떠난 뒤, 그리고 방금 우리가 자세히 전한 막시밀리앙과 발랑틴의 대화가 오가던 동안, 검사장의 집에서 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있었는지를 이제 이야기하려 한다.\n\n빌포르 씨가 아버지의 방으로 들어섰고, 빌포르 부인이 그 뒤를 따랐다. 두 사람은 노인에게 인사하고, 그의 시중을 든 지 이십오 년 된 충직한 하인 바루아에게 몇 마디 건넨 뒤, 반신불수 노인의 양옆에 자리를 잡았다.\n\n누아르티에 씨는 바퀴 달린 안락의자에 앉아 있었다. 아침이면 그 의자에 실려 방으로 들어왔고, 밤이면 같은 방식으로 다시 밖으로 끌려 나갔다. 그는 방 전체를 비추는 커다란 거울 앞에 놓여 있었다. 그래서 그는 몸을 움직이려는 시도—그것은 애초에 불가능한 일이었다—를 하지 않고도, 방에 들어오는 모든 이와 주위에서 일어나는 모든 일을 볼 수 있었다. 누아르티에 씨는 시신이나 다름없이 거의 꼼짝하지 못했지만, 그 두 방문객을 기민하고 총명한 눈빛으로 바라보았다. 그들의 격식 차린 정중함을 보고, 그는 뜻밖의 공적인 용건으로 찾아왔음을 단번에 알아챘다.\n\n이제 그에게 남은 감각은 시각과 청각뿐이었고, 그 둘은 외로운 두 불꽃처럼 남아, 무덤 말고는 아무 데도 쓸모없어 보이는 가련한 육신에 생기를 불어넣고 있었다. 그러나 그가 아직 마음속을 채우고 있는 생각과 감정을 드러낼 수 있는 길은 이 두 감각 중 하나뿐이었다. 그가 자신의 내면을 표현하는 눈빛은, 밤길의 나그네가 황량한 들판 너머로 보는 멀리 가물거리는 촛불과도 같았다. 그 침묵과 어둠 너머에 살아 있는 존재가 깃들어 있음을 알려 주는 불빛이었다.\n\n누아르티에의 머리카락은 길고 희어 어깨 위로 흘러내렸다. 짙고 검은 속눈썹이 드리운 그의 눈에는, 한 기관이 다른 기관들의 몫까지 떠맡을 때 흔히 그러하듯, 한때 온몸에 퍼져 있던 모든 활기와 솜씨와 힘과 지성이 응축되어 있었다. 그래서 팔의 움직임도, 목소리도, 몸의 민첩함도 없었지만, 말하는 눈 하나로 모든 것이 충분했다. 그는 그 눈으로 명령했고, 그 눈을 통해 고마움을 전했다. 요컨대 그의 모습 전체는 살아 있는 눈을 가진 시신이라는 인상을 주었다. 그리고 분노나 기쁨의 표정이 그 눈에 별안간 타오르는 한편, 굳어 버린 대리석 같은 나머지 얼굴은 거기에 함께할 힘을 완전히 잃은 채로 있는 광경만큼 놀라운 것은 없었다. 이 가엾은 반신불수 노인의 언어를 이해할 수 있는 사람은 단 셋뿐이었다. 빌포르와 발랑틴, 그리고 앞서 말한 늙은 하인이었다. 그러나 빌포르는 어쩔 수 없을 때만, 그것도 드물게 아버지를 찾았고, 와서도 그를 기쁘게 하거나 흡족하게 해 주려는 수고를 결코 들이지 않았다. 그래서 노인의 모든 행복은 손녀에게 쏠려 있었다. 발랑틴은 사랑과 인내와 헌신으로, 누아르티에의 눈빛에서 그의 마음을 스쳐 가는 온갖 감정을 읽어 내는 법을 익혔다. 남들은 도무지 알아들을 수 없는 이 말 없는 언어에, 그녀는 자신의 온 영혼을 얼굴 표정에 담아 답했다. 이렇게 해서 꽃다운 처녀와 무력한 환자 사이에 대화가 이어졌다. 그 환자의 육신은 살아 있다고 말하기조차 어려웠으나, 그럼에도 그 안에는 풍부한 지식과 통찰이 깃들어 있었고, 충동에 따를 능력을 완전히 잃은 육신에 갇혀 있을지언정 여전히 예전 못지않게 강한 의지가 함께 자리하고 있었다.\n\n발랑틴은 그 수수께끼를 풀어내, 그의 생각을 쉽게 알아채고 자신의 생각도 되돌려 전할 수 있었다. 지칠 줄 모르는 헌신적인 정성 덕분에, 일상의 자질구레한 일들에서 살아 있는 그 정신의 바람이나 거의 죽은 듯한 육신의 필요를 그녀가 미리 헤아리지 못하는 일은 좀처럼 없었다.\n\n하인으로 말하자면, 앞서 말했듯이 주인을 이십오 년이나 모셔 온 터라, 그의 모든 습관을 알고 있었다. 그래서 누아르티에가 무언가를 청해야 할 일이 좀처럼 없었으니, 환자의 온갖 필요를 그토록 재빨리 채워 주었던 것이다.\n\n빌포르는 이제 막 시작하려는 아버지와의 기묘한 대화를 이어 가는 데 발랑틴의 도움도, 하인의 도움도 필요하지 않았다. 앞서 말했듯이 그는 노인의 어휘를 완벽하게 이해하고 있었고, 그것을 더 자주 쓰지 않은 까닭은 오로지 무관심과 권태 때문이었다. 그래서 그는 발랑틴을 정원으로 내보내고 바루아를 물러가게 한 뒤, 아버지의 오른쪽에 앉았다. 빌포르 부인은 왼쪽에 자리를 잡았다. 그러고는 그가 이렇게 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"불쾌하게 여기지 않으시기를 바랍니다, 아버지. 발랑틴이 함께 오지 않았고, 제가 바루아를 물린 것 말입니다. 우리가 나눌 이야기는 그 둘이 있는 자리에서 격식에 맞게 할 수 있는 것이 아니라서요. 빌포르 부인과 제가 아버지께 전할 말씀이 있습니다.\"\n\n누아르티에의 얼굴은 이 긴 서두가 이어지는 동안 더없이 무표정했다. 반면 빌포르의 눈은 노인의 마음속 가장 깊은 구석까지 꿰뚫어 보려 애쓰고 있었다.\n\n\"이 말씀은,\" 검사장이 일체의 논의를 단번에 가로막을 듯한 차갑고 단호한 어조로 말을 이었다. \"분명 아버지의 찬성을 얻으리라 믿습니다.\"\n\n환자의 눈은 여전히 텅 빈 표정을 띠고 있어, 아들은 그 마음속을 스치는 감정을 도무지 알아챌 수 없었다. 그는 듣기만 했다, 그뿐이었다.\n\n\"아버지,\" 빌포르가 다시 입을 열었다. \"저희는 발랑틴을 시집보낼 생각입니다.\" 노인의 얼굴이 밀랍으로 빚어진 것이었다 해도, 이 소식에 지금 그 얼굴에 떠오른 것보다 더 적은 감정을 드러내지는 못했을 것이다. \"결혼식은 석 달 안에 치러질 겁니다.\" 빌포르가 말했다.\n\n누아르티에의 눈은 여전히 생기 없는 표정을 띠고 있었다.\n\n이번에는 빌포르 부인이 대화에 끼어들어 이렇게 덧붙였다.\n\n\"늘 발랑틴을 그토록 아껴 오신 아버님이시니, 이 소식이 마음에 드시리라 생각했습니다. 그러니 이제 그 아이의 짝이 될 청년의 이름만 말씀드리면 되겠지요. 더없이 바람직한 혼처랍니다. 재산도 있고, 사회적 지위도 높으며, 발랑틴을 더없이 행복하게 해 줄 만한 인품도 두루 갖추었지요. 게다가 그 이름이 아버님께 아주 낯설지만은 않을 거예요. 프란츠 드 케넬, 데피네 남작이랍니다.\"\n\n아내가 말하는 동안, 빌포르는 노인의 얼굴을 유심히 살피고 있었다. 빌포르 부인이 프란츠라는 이름을 입에 올리자, 누아르티에 씨의 눈동자가 커지기 시작했고, 막 말을 하려는 사람의 입술에서 볼 법한 떨림이 그 눈꺼풀에 일었다. 그러고는 그가 빌포르 부인과 아들에게 번개 같은 눈길을 던졌다. 누아르티에 씨와 데피네 노인 사이에 한때 정치적 증오가 있었음을 아는 검사장은, 그 통고가 불러일으킨 동요와 분노를 잘 알아챘다. 그러나 그는 둘 다 못 본 척하며, 곧바로 아내가 꺼낸 이야기를 이어 갔다.\n\n\"아버지,\" 그가 말했다. \"발랑틴이 이제 열아홉 살로 접어든다는 걸 아시지요. 그러니 알맞은 혼처를 맺는 데 한시도 지체해서는 안 됩니다. 그렇다고 저희 계획에서 아버지를 잊은 것은 아닙니다. 미리 충분히 확인해 두었지요. 발랑틴의 장래 남편은—젊은 부부에게는 그게 달갑지 않을 수 있으니 이 집에서 함께 사는 것은 아니되—아버지께서 그들과 함께 지내시는 데 흔쾌히 동의할 거라고요. 그러면 그토록 서로를 아끼는 아버지와 발랑틴이 떨어질 일도 없을 테고, 지금껏 해 오신 것과 똑같은 생활을 그대로 이어 가실 수 있을 겁니다. 그러니 이번 일로 잃는 게 아니라 오히려 얻는 셈이지요. 아버지를 돌보고 위로해 줄 자식이 하나가 아니라 둘로 늘어날 테니까요.\"\n\n누아르티에의 눈빛이 사나워졌다. 노인의 마음속에 무언가 절박한 것이 휘몰아치고 있음이 또렷이 보였다. 분노와 비탄의 외침이 목구멍까지 치밀어 올랐으나 말로 터져 나올 길을 찾지 못해, 그를 거의 질식시킬 듯했다. 그 몸부림에 얼굴과 입술이 시퍼렇게 변했다. 빌포르는 조용히 창문을 열며 말했다. \"몹시 덥군요. 이 더위가 아버지께 무리가 되나 봅니다.\" 그러고는 제자리로 돌아왔으나 앉지는 않았다.\n\n\"이 결혼은,\" 빌포르 부인이 덧붙였다. \"데피네 씨와 그 집안의 뜻에도 더없이 합당하답니다. 게다가 그분에게는 숙부와 숙모 말고는 가까운 친척이 없어요. 어머니는 그가 태어날 때 돌아가셨고, 아버지는 1815년에, 그러니까 그가 겨우 두 살이었을 때 암살당하셨으니까요. 그래서 자연히 그 아이는 제 갈 길을 스스로 고르도록 맡겨졌고, 그러니 제 의지 말고는 어떤 권위도 좀처럼 인정한 적이 없답니다.\"\n\n\"그 암살은 알 수 없는 사건이었습니다.\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"여러 사람에게 혐의가 쏠리긴 했지만, 범인들은 지금껏 잡히지 않았지요.\"\n\n누아르티에는 어찌나 안간힘을 썼던지, 입술이 벌어져 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"한데,\" 빌포르가 말을 이었다. \"정말로 죄를 진 자들, 그 범행을 저지른 자들, 이승에서는 인간의 정의가, 저승에서는 하느님의 어김없는 심판이 그 머리 위에 내릴 자들이라면, 자기들이 그토록 무자비하게 목숨을 앗은 사람의 아들에게 발랑틴을 화해의 제물로 바칠 기회가 주어진 것을 기뻐할 겁니다.\" 누아르티에는, 그토록 쇠약하고 부서진 몸으로는 도저히 가능하리라 여겨지지 않을 만큼, 감정을 다스리는 데 성공했다.\n\n\"그래, 알겠다.\" 그의 눈빛에 담긴 대답은 그런 것이었다. 그리고 그 눈빛은 강한 분노에 깊은 경멸이 뒤섞인 감정을 드러냈다. 빌포르는 아버지의 뜻을 똑똑히 이해하고는, 어깨를 가볍게 으쓱하며 답했다. 그러고는 아내에게 물러가자는 신호를 보냈다.\n\n\"그럼 아버님,\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"이만 물러가겠습니다. 에두아르를 잠깐 아버님께 보내 드릴까요?\"\n\n미리 정해 둔 약속이 있었다. 노인은 찬성하면 눈을 감고, 거절하면 여러 번 눈을 깜박이며, 바라거나 표현하고 싶은 것이 있으면 눈을 하늘로 치켜뜨기로 했다. 발랑틴을 원하면 오른쪽 눈만 감고, 바루아를 원하면 왼쪽 눈만 감았다. 빌포르 부인의 제안에 그는 곧장 눈을 깜박였다.\n\n단호한 거절에 발끈한 그녀는 입술을 깨물며 말했다. \"그럼 발랑틴을 보내 드릴까요?\" 노인은 그러기를 바란다는 뜻으로 얼른 눈을 감았다.\n\n빌포르 부부는 인사하고 방을 나서며, 발랑틴을 할아버지에게 들도록 하라고 일렀다. 환자의 들끓는 마음을 가라앉히려면 그 아이가 무던히 애를 써야 하리라 확신하면서였다. 부모가 막 방을 나서자마자, 발랑틴이 격한 감정으로 여전히 발그레해진 얼굴로 들어왔다. 그녀는 한눈에 할아버지가 괴로워하고 있음을, 그리고 자신에게 전하고 싶은 무언가가 마음속에 잔뜩 쌓여 있음을 알아챘다.\n\n\"할아버지,\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"무슨 일이세요? 누가 할아버지를 속상하게 했군요, 화가 나셨죠?\"\n\n반신불수 노인이 그렇다는 표시로 눈을 감았다.\n\n\"누가 할아버지를 언짢게 했어요? 우리 아버지인가요?\"\n\n\"아니다.\"\n\"빌포르 부인인가요?\"\n\n\"아니다.\"\n\n\"저예요?\" 앞서의 신호가 되풀이되었다.\n\n\"저한테 화가 나신 거예요?\" 발랑틴이 놀라 외쳤다. 누아르티에 씨가 다시 눈을 감았다.\n\n\"제가 무얼 했기에 저한테 화를 내세요, 할아버지?\" 발랑틴이 외쳤다.\n\n아무 대답이 없었고, 그녀는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"하루 종일 할아버지를 못 뵈었는걸요. 누가 할아버지께 제 험담이라도 했나요?\"\n\n\"그렇다.\" 노인의 눈빛이 다급하게 말했다.\n\n\"잠깐 생각해 볼게요. 정말이지 할아버지—아, 아버지와 빌포르 부인이 방금 이 방에서 나가셨죠, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"그렇다.\"\n\n\"그분들이 할아버지께 무슨 말을 해서 화가 나신 거군요? 그게 뭐였나요? 가서 여쭤보고 와도 될까요? 그래야 할아버지와 화해할 길이 생길 테니까요.\"\n\n\"아니다, 아니다.\" 누아르티에의 눈빛이 말했다.\n\n\"아, 무서워요. 도대체 무슨 말을 한 걸까요?\" 그녀는 다시 그게 무엇일지 생각해 보려 애썼다.\n\n\"아, 알겠어요.\" 그녀가 목소리를 낮추며 노인에게 바짝 다가가 말했다. \"제 결혼 이야기를 하셨군요. 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"그렇다.\" 화난 눈빛이 답했다.\n\n\"알겠어요. 제가 그 일을 입 다물고 있던 게 언짢으셨군요. 그건 그분들이 비밀로 하라고 신신당부하면서, 할아버지께 아무것도 말씀드리지 말라고 부탁했기 때문이에요. 제게는 그분들 속셈조차 알려 주지 않아서, 우연히 알아챈 거랍니다. 그래서 할아버지께 그토록 입을 다물고 있었던 거예요. 부디 용서해 주세요.\"\n\n그러나 그녀를 안심시킬 만한 눈빛은 보이지 않았다. 그 눈빛이 말하는 것은 오직 이것뿐인 듯했다. \"나를 괴롭히는 건 네가 입을 다물었던 것만이 아니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 뭐예요?\" 처녀가 물었다. \"할아버지, 혹시 제가 결혼하면 할아버지를 버리고 잊어버릴 거라고 생각하시는 거예요?\"\n\n\"아니다.\"\n\"그럼 데피네 씨가 우리 모두 함께 사는 데 동의했다는 말을 들으셨군요?\"\n\n\"그렇다.\"\n\n\"그런데 왜 아직도 속상해하고 슬퍼하세요?\" 노인의 눈이 부드러운 애정의 표정으로 빛났다.\n\n\"알겠어요.\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"저를 사랑하셔서 그런 거죠.\" 노인이 그렇다고 했다.\n\n\"그리고 제가 불행해질까 봐 두려우신 거고요?\"\n\n\"그렇다.\"\n\n\"프란츠 씨가 마음에 안 드시는 거예요?\" 눈이 여러 번 되풀이했다. \"아니다, 아니다, 아니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 이 약혼이 언짢으신 거군요?\"\n\n\"그렇다.\"\n\n\"그럼 들어 보세요.\" 발랑틴이 무릎을 꿇고 할아버지의 목에 팔을 두르며 말했다. \"저도 언짢아요. 저도 프란츠 데피네 씨를 사랑하지 않으니까요.\"\n\n노인의 눈에 더없는 기쁨의 표정이 환히 떠올랐다.\n\n\"제가 수녀원에 들어가려 했을 때, 할아버지가 저한테 얼마나 화를 내셨는지 기억하시죠?\" 환자의 눈에 눈물이 어른거렸다. \"실은요,\" 발랑틴이 말을 이었다. \"제가 그러자고 한 까닭은 이 끔찍한 결혼을 피하고 싶어서였어요. 그 생각만 하면 절망에 빠지거든요.\" 누아르티에의 숨이 가쁘고 짧아졌다.\n\n\"그럼 이 결혼이 정말 할아버지도 슬프게 하는군요? 아, 할아버지가 저를 도울 수만 있다면—우리 둘이 함께 그분들 계획을 무너뜨릴 수만 있다면! 하지만 할아버지는 그분들에게 맞서실 수가 없잖아요. 그토록 명민한 정신과 그토록 굳센 의지를 지니셨는데도, 할아버지 역시 저처럼 이 싸움에 무력하고 부치는 처지인걸요. 아, 건강하고 정정하시던 시절이었다면 제게 그토록 든든한 보호자가 되어 주셨을 할아버지가, 이제는 제 기쁨과 슬픔에 함께 마음 아파하실 뿐, 거기에 적극적으로 나서지는 못하시는군요. 그래도 이것만으로도 큰일이고 고마운 일이에요. 할아버지의 공감과 다정함을 제게 남겨 주셨으니, 하늘이 제 모든 복을 앗아 간 건 아니랍니다.\"\n\n이 말에 누아르티에의 눈에 어찌나 깊은 뜻이 담긴 표정이 떠올랐던지, 처녀는 거기서 이런 말을 읽을 수 있을 것만 같았다. \"네가 잘못 알고 있다. 나는 아직 너를 위해 많은 일을 할 수 있다.\"\n\n\"할아버지, 저를 도와주실 수 있다고 생각하세요?\" 발랑틴이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다.\" 누아르티에가 눈을 치켜떴다. 무언가를 원할 때 그와 발랑틴 사이에 정해 둔 신호였다.\n\n\"무얼 원하세요, 할아버지?\" 발랑틴이 말하며, 그가 필요로 할 만한 모든 것을 떠올려 보려 애썼다. 생각이 떠오르는 대로 소리 내어 읊었으나, 그 모든 노력에 돌아오는 것이라곤 끊임없는 \"아니다\"뿐임을 알고는 이렇게 말했다. \"자, 이 방법이 안 통하니, 다른 방법을 써 볼게요.\"\n\n그러고는 알파벳 A부터 N까지 죄다 읊었다. N에 이르자 반신불수 노인은, 그녀가 자신이 원하는 것의 첫 글자를 말했음을 알려 주었다.\n\n\"아,\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"원하시는 게 N으로 시작하는군요. 그럼 N인 거네요. 자, 어디 볼까요, N으로 시작하는 무얼 원하실까? 나—느—니—노……\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 그래.\" 노인의 눈이 말했다.\n\n\"아, 그럼 '노'예요?\"\n\n\"그렇다.\"\n\n발랑틴은 사전을 가져와 누아르티에 앞 책상에 펼쳐 놓았다. 노인의 눈이 그 책장에 단단히 못 박힌 것을 보고, 그녀는 손가락으로 단을 따라 위아래로 재빨리 훑어 내려갔다. 누아르티에가 처음 이 가련한 처지에 빠진 뒤로 흐른 육 년 동안, 발랑틴의 기지는 하도 여러 번 시험대에 올랐던 터라, 그의 바람을 알아내는 방편을 짜내는 데 능숙하지 않을 수 없었다. 그 끊임없는 연습이 그녀를 이 기술에 완벽하게 단련시켜, 노인이 직접 원하는 것을 찾아낼 수 있었더라도 그만큼 빠를까 싶을 만큼, 그녀는 노인의 뜻을 단박에 알아맞혔다. '공증인'이라는 낱말에 이르자, 누아르티에가 그녀에게 멈추라는 신호를 보냈다.\n\n\"공증인이요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"공증인을 원하세요, 할아버지?\" 노인은 자신이 원하는 것이 공증인임을 다시 한번 알렸다.\n\n\"그럼 공증인을 불러오기를 바라시는 거예요?\" 발랑틴이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다.\"\n\n\"이 일을 아버지께 알려도 될까요?\"\n\n\"그렇다.\"\n\n\"공증인을 당장 불러오기를 바라세요?\"\n\"그렇다.\"\n\n\"그럼 지금 바로 사람을 보내 모셔 오게 할게요, 할아버지. 원하시는 게 그게 다예요?\"\n\n\"그렇다.\" 발랑틴은 종을 울려, 빌포르 씨나 부인께 누아르티에 씨 방으로 와 주십사 청한다고 하인에게 전하라 일렀다.\n\n\"이제 흡족하세요?\" 발랑틴이 물었다.\n\"그렇다.\"\n\n\"그러실 줄 알았어요. 그건 알아채기가 그리 어렵지 않은걸요.\" 그러고는 처녀가 마치 어린아이를 대하듯 할아버지에게 미소 지었다. 빌포르 씨가 들어왔고, 바루아가 그 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n\"무슨 일로 저를 부르셨습니까, 아버지?\" 그가 반신불수 노인에게 물었다.\n\n\"아버지,\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"할아버지가 공증인을 원하세요.\" 이 기이하고 뜻밖의 요구에, 빌포르 씨와 그의 아버지가 서로 눈길을 주고받았다.\n\n\"그렇다.\" 노인이 신호했다. 자신의 바람을 다 아는 발랑틴과 늙은 하인의 도움이 있으니 이 싸움을 끝까지 밀고 나갈 채비가 되어 있다고 선언하는 듯한 단호함이었다.\n\n\"공증인을 원하십니까?\" 빌포르가 물었다.\n\"그렇다.\"\n\n\"무엇 하시려고요?\"\n\n누아르티에는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"공증인을 불러 무얼 하시려는 겁니까?\" 빌포르가 다시 되물었다. 환자의 눈은 못 박힌 듯 움직이지 않았다. 그 표정으로 자신의 결심은 바꿀 수 없다는 뜻을 알리려는 것이었다.\n\n\"우리에게 해코지라도 하시려고요? 그럴 만한 가치가 있다고 보십니까?\" 빌포르가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 늙은 하인다운 거리낌 없는 충직함으로 바루아가 말했다. \"누아르티에 어른께서 공증인을 청하신다면, 정말로 공증인을 원하시는 거겠지요. 그러니 제가 당장 가서 한 분 모셔 오겠습니다.\" 바루아는 누아르티에 말고는 어떤 주인도 인정하지 않았고, 그의 바람이 어떤 식으로든 거스러지는 것을 결코 용납하지 않았다.\n\n\"그래, 나는 공증인을 원한다.\" 노인이 도전적인 눈빛으로 눈을 감으며 신호했다. 그 눈빛은 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"내 청을 감히 거절하는 자가 있다면 어디 한번 보고 싶구나.\"\n\n\"정 그토록 원하신다니 공증인을 들이겠습니다, 아버지.\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"다만 제가 그 사람에게 아버지의 건강 상태를 설명하고, 아버지를 위해 양해를 구해야겠지요. 이 일은 더없이 우스꽝스러운 광경이 될 게 뻔하니까요.\"\n\n\"그건 상관없습니다.\" 바루아가 말했다. \"어쨌든 저는 가서 공증인을 모셔 오겠습니다.\" 그러고는 늙은 하인이 의기양양하게 제 임무를 띠고 떠났다.\n\n# Chapter 59. The Will\n\n바루아가 방을 나서자마자 누아르티에는 발랑틴을 바라보았다. 그 눈빛에는 많은 것을 담은 짓궂은 기색이 어려 있었다. 처녀는 그 눈빛을 완벽히 알아들었고, 빌포르 역시 마찬가지였다. 그의 얼굴이 흐려지고, 화가 난 듯 눈썹이 찌푸려졌다. 그는 자리에 앉아 공증인이 오기를 조용히 기다렸다. 누아르티에는 그가 더없이 무심한 태도로 앉는 모습을 지켜보았고, 동시에 발랑틴에게 곁눈질을 보내 그녀 또한 방에 남아 있어야 한다는 것을 알렸다. 사십오 분 뒤, 바루아가 공증인을 데리고 돌아왔다.\n\n\"선생,\" 첫인사를 나눈 뒤 빌포르가 말했다. \"여기 보시는 누아르티에 씨께서 선생을 모셔 오라 하셨습니다. 사지가 완전히 마비되셨고, 목소리마저 잃으셨지요. 그래서 저희도 그 뜻을 단편적으로나마 알아내려고 무던히 애를 쓰고 있습니다.\"\n\n누아르티에가 발랑틴에게 간곡한 눈빛을 던졌다. 그 눈빛이 어찌나 진지하고도 위엄에 차 있었던지, 그녀는 곧바로 대답했다.\n\n\"선생님,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저는 언제든 할아버지의 뜻을 완벽하게 이해합니다.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇습니다.\" 바루아가 말했다. \"오는 길에도 제가 이 선생께 그 말씀을 드렸습지요.\"\n\n\"잠시만요.\" 공증인이 빌포르를 향했다가 다시 발랑틴을 향하며 말했다. \"이 사안은, 저 같은 공직자가 자칫 위험한 책임을 떠안지 않고서는 함부로 처리할 수 없는 부류의 일임을 말씀드려야겠습니다. 어떤 행위를 유효하게 만들려면, 무엇보다 공증인이 그 행위를 구술하는 사람의 의지와 소망을 충실하게 옮겼다는 확신을 가져야 합니다. 그런데 저로서는 말을 못 하는 의뢰인의 동의나 부동의를 확신할 수가 없습니다. 말을 못 하시니, 그분이 무엇을 바라거나 꺼리는지 제게 명확히 입증될 수가 없습니다. 그러니 이곳에서 제가 할 일은 전혀 쓸모가 없고, 법적으로도 행사될 수 없습니다.\"\n\n그러고서 공증인은 물러갈 채비를 했다. 검사의 입가에 알아챌 듯 말 듯한 승리의 미소가 떠올랐다. 누아르티에는 어찌나 슬픔이 가득한 표정으로 발랑틴을 바라보았던지, 그녀는 떠나려는 공증인을 붙들었다.\n\n\"선생님,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"제가 할아버지와 나누는 말은 쉽게 배우실 수 있습니다. 몇 분이면 선생님께서도 저만큼 이해하시도록 가르쳐 드릴 수 있어요. 이 일에 대해 양심을 완전히 놓으시려면 무엇이 필요한지 말씀해 주시겠어요?\"\n\n\"어떤 행위를 유효하게 하려면, 의뢰인의 동의나 부동의를 확신해야 합니다. 몸이 병들었다고 해서 증서의 효력이 손상되지는 않습니다만, 정신의 온전함은 절대적으로 필요한 조건이지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 선생님, 제가 지금 알려 드릴 두 가지 신호의 도움을 받으시면, 제 할아버지께서 여전히 모든 정신적 능력을 온전히 갖추고 계시다는 것을 완벽하게 확인하실 수 있습니다. 누아르티에 씨는 목소리도 움직임도 잃으신 터라, '예'라고 하실 때는 눈을 감으시고, '아니오'라고 하실 때는 눈을 깜박이셔서 뜻을 전하십니다. 이제 누아르티에 씨와 대화하시기에 충분히 아셨을 거예요. 한번 해 보세요.\"\n\n누아르티에가 발랑틴에게 어찌나 다정하고 고마운 눈빛을 보냈던지, 공증인마저 그 뜻을 알아차렸다.\n\n\"손녀분께서 하신 말씀을 들으시고 이해하셨습니까, 선생님?\" 공증인이 물었다. 누아르티에가 눈을 감았다.\n\n\"그리고 손녀분의 말씀에 동의하십니까? 다시 말해, 손녀분이 말씀하신 그 신호가 정녕 선생님께서 평소에 생각을 전하시는 신호가 맞다고 선언하시는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"예.\"\n\n\"저를 부르신 게 선생님이십니까?\"\n\n\"예.\"\n\n\"유언장을 작성하시려고요?\"\n\n\"예.\"\n\n\"그러니 제가 본래의 뜻을 이행하지 않고 떠나는 것을 바라지 않으시는 겁니까?\" 노인이 세차게 눈을 깜박였다.\n\n\"자, 선생님,\" 처녀가 말했다. \"이제 이해가 되셨나요? 이 일에 대해 양심이 완전히 편안해지셨고요?\"\n\n그러나 공증인이 대답하기도 전에, 빌포르가 그를 한쪽으로 끌어당겼다.\n\n\"선생,\" 그가 말했다. \"누아르티에 씨가 입은 것과 같은 육체적 충격을 받은 사람이, 정신적 능력에 아무런 손상 없이 그것을 견뎌 낼 수 있다고 잠시라도 생각하십니까?\"\n\n\"제 불안의 까닭은 정확히 그것이 아닙니다, 선생.\" 공증인이 말했다. \"다만 어려움은, 답변을 얻어 낼 수 있도록 그분의 생각과 의도를 말로 옮기는 데 있겠지요.\"\n\n\"그게 전혀 불가능한 일이라는 걸 아셔야 합니다.\" 빌포르가 말했다. 발랑틴과 노인은 이 대화를 들었고, 누아르티에가 어찌나 간절히 발랑틴을 응시했던지 그녀는 그 눈빛에 응답하지 않을 수 없다고 느꼈다.\n\n\"선생님,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그 점은 언뜻 아무리 어려워 보이더라도 불안해하실 필요가 없습니다. 제가 할아버지의 생각을 알아내어 설명해 드릴 수 있으니, 이 일에 대한 모든 의심과 두려움을 끝낼 수 있을 거예요. 저는 누아르티에 씨와 함께 지낸 지 벌써 육 년이 되었어요. 그동안 단 한 번이라도 제게 이해시키지 못한 생각을 품으신 적이 있는지, 할아버지께 여쭤보세요.\"\n\n\"없다.\" 노인이 신호했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 어디 한번 해 봅시다.\" 공증인이 말했다. \"이 아가씨를 통역자로 받아들이시겠습니까, 누아르티에 씨?\"\n\n\"예.\"\n\n\"좋습니다, 선생님. 제게 무엇을 바라시며, 어떤 문서를 작성하길 원하십니까?\"\n\n발랑틴이 알파벳의 모든 글자를 차례로 불렀다. W에 이르렀을 때, 누아르티에의 말 많은 눈이 멈추라는 신호를 보냈다.\n\n\"누아르티에 씨가 원하시는 게 W라는 글자임은 아주 분명하군요.\" 공증인이 말했다.\n\n\"기다려 보세요.\" 발랑틴이 말하고는 할아버지를 향해 되풀이했다. \"Wa…… We…… Wi…….\" 노인이 마지막 음절에서 그녀를 멈춰 세웠다. 그러자 발랑틴은 사전을 집어 들었고, 공증인은 그녀가 책장을 넘기는 모습을 지켜보았다.\n\n그녀가 손가락을 단(段)을 따라 천천히 내려가다가 'Will(유언)'이라는 단어에 이르자, 누아르티에의 눈이 멈추라고 일렀다.\n\n\"유언.\" 공증인이 말했다. \"누아르티에 씨가 유언장을 작성하고 싶어 하시는 게 아주 분명하군요.\"\n\n\"예, 예, 예.\" 환자가 신호했다.\n\n\"정말이지, 선생, 이건 더없이 비범한 일이라는 걸 인정하셔야겠습니다.\" 놀란 공증인이 빌포르 씨를 향해 말했다.\n\n\"그렇군요.\" 검사가 말했다. \"그리고 이 유언장은 앞으로 한층 더 비범해질 것 같습니다. 발랑틴이 끼어들지 않고서는 도무지 작성될 수가 없는데, 그 아이는 어쩌면 유언 내용에 너무 깊이 얽힌 당사자라, 모호하고 불분명한 할아버지의 뜻을 통역하기에 적합한 사람이라고 보기 어려울 테니까요.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니, 아니.\" 중풍 환자의 눈이 대답했다.\n\n\"뭐라고요?\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"발랑틴이 아버지의 유언과 아무 이해관계가 없다는 말씀이십니까?\"\n\"아니다.\"\n\n\"선생님,\" 공증인이 말했다. 그는 흥미가 잔뜩 동했고, 이 비범하고도 그림 같은 장면을 널리 알리리라 마음먹은 터였다. \"한 시간 전만 해도 제게 그토록 불가능해 보이던 일이, 이제는 아주 쉽고 실행 가능한 일이 되었군요. 그리고 이 유언장은 일곱 명의 증인 앞에서 낭독되고, 유언자의 승인을 받고, 증인들이 보는 앞에서 공증인이 봉인한다면 완벽하게 유효한 유언장이 될 수 있습니다. 시간으로 말하면, 보통의 유언장보다 그리 더 걸리지도 않을 겁니다. 반드시 거쳐야 하는 형식이 몇 가지 있는데, 그건 늘 똑같지요. 세부 사항으로 말하면, 그 대부분은 유언자의 재산 상태를 통해, 또 그 재산을 관리해 오신 만큼 분명 충분한 정보를 주실 수 있는 선생을 통해 나중에 채워질 겁니다. 다만 이 모든 것에 더해, 이 증서가 이의 제기를 받지 않도록 저는 가능한 한 최대의 진정성을 부여하고 싶습니다. 그러니 제 동료 한 사람이 저를 도울 것이고, 관례와는 달리 유언 구술에도 참여할 것입니다. 만족하십니까, 선생님?\" 공증인이 노인을 향해 말을 이었다.\n\n\"예.\" 환자의 눈빛이 말했다. 자신의 뜻이 척척 통역되는 것에 그의 눈이 기쁨으로 빛났다.\n\n'아버지가 무슨 일을 하려는 거지?' 빌포르는 생각했다. 그의 처지로서는 신중을 지켜야 했지만, 아버지의 의도가 무엇인지 몹시 알고 싶었다. 그는 또 다른 공증인을 부르라는 지시를 내리러 방을 나섰다. 그러나 그동안의 일을 모두 들은 바루아는 주인의 뜻을 짐작하고 이미 한 사람을 데리러 떠난 뒤였다. 그러자 검사는 아내에게 올라오라고 일렀다. 십오 분쯤 지나자 모두가 중풍 환자의 방에 모였고, 두 번째 공증인도 도착했다.\n\n두 법률가가 서로 뜻을 맞추는 데는 몇 마디면 충분했다. 그들은 그런 문서가 일반적으로 어떤 문구로 작성되는지 누아르티에에게 보여 주려고 유언장의 정식 사본 한 부를 읽어 주었다. 그런 다음, 유언자의 능력을 시험하려고 첫 번째 공증인이 그를 향해 돌아서며 말했다.\n\n\"사람이 유언을 할 때는, 대개 누군가에게 유리하게 하거나 불리하게 하기 마련이지요.\"\n\n\"예.\"\n\n\"선생님께서는 재산의 액수를 정확히 알고 계십니까?\"\n\n\"예.\"\n\n\"제가 차츰 액수를 늘려 가며 여러 금액을 부르겠습니다. 선생님 재산의 총액에 해당하는 금액에 이르면 저를 멈추십시오.\"\n\n\"예.\"\n\n이 질문에는 일종의 엄숙함이 깃들어 있었다. 정신과 물질의 싸움이 지금보다 더 또렷이 드러난 적은 없었으니, 숭고하지는 않을지언정 적어도 진기한 광경이었다. 사람들이 환자를 둘러싸고 원을 이루었다. 두 번째 공증인은 글을 쓰려고 채비한 채 탁자에 앉았고, 그의 동료는 유언자 앞에 서서 앞서 말한 사안에 관해 그에게 질문을 던지고 있었다.\n\n\"선생님 재산은 30만 프랑을 넘지요?\" 그가 물었다. 누아르티에가 그렇다는 신호를 보냈다.\n\n\"40만 프랑을 가지고 계십니까?\" 공증인이 물었다. 누아르티에의 눈은 움직이지 않았다.\n\n\"50만 프랑은요?\" 같은 표정이 계속되었다.\n\n\"60만…… 70만…… 80만…… 90만?\"\n\n누아르티에가 마지막으로 부른 금액에서 그를 멈추었다.\n\n\"그러면 선생님께서는 90만 프랑을 가지고 계신 겁니까?\" 공증인이 물었다.\n\n\"예.\"\n\n\"부동산으로요?\"\n\"아니오.\"\n\n\"증권으로요?\"\n\n\"예.\"\n\n\"그 증권을 직접 보관하고 계십니까?\"\n\n누아르티에가 바루아에게 던진 눈빛은, 자신이 어디에 있는지 아는 무언가가 빠져 있다는 것을 보여 주었다. 늙은 하인이 방을 나갔다가 곧 작은 함을 들고 돌아왔다.\n\n\"이 함을 열어도 되겠습니까?\" 공증인이 물었다. 누아르티에가 동의했다.\n\n그들이 함을 열자 90만 프랑어치의 은행 증권이 들어 있었다. 첫 번째 공증인은 한 장씩 살펴본 뒤 동료에게 건넸다.\n\n총액은 누아르티에가 말한 그대로였다.\n\n\"모든 게 말씀하신 대로군요. 정신이 여전히 온전한 힘과 활기를 지니고 있음이 아주 분명합니다.\" 그러고는 중풍 환자를 향해 말했다. \"그러니까 선생님께서는 90만 프랑의 자본을 가지고 계시고, 투자 방식에 따라 연 4만 리브르가량의 수입을 가져올 텐데요?\"\n\n\"예.\"\n\n\"이 재산을 누구에게 남기고 싶으십니까?\"\n\n\"오!\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"그 점은 별로 의심할 게 없지요. 누아르티에 씨는 손녀이신 빌포르 양을 끔찍이 사랑하십니다. 육 년 동안 그분을 간호하고 보살핀 사람이 바로 그 아이고, 그 헌신적인 정성으로 할아버지의 애정을, 거의 감사라고 할 만한 마음까지 온전히 얻었으니, 그 헌신의 열매를 거두는 것이 마땅하지요.\"\n\n누아르티에의 눈은, 자신이 품었으리라고 빌포르 부인이 짐작한 동기에 대해 그녀의 말과 태도가 보낸 거짓 동의에 자신이 속지 않았음을 표정으로 분명히 드러냈다.\n\n\"그러면 이 90만 프랑을 발랑틴 드 빌포르 양에게 남기시는 겁니까?\" 공증인이 물었다. 그는 이 조항만 써넣으면 되리라 여겼지만, 우선 누아르티에의 동의를 기다렸다. 그 동의는 이 기이한 장면의 모든 증인 앞에서 반드시 표해져야 했다.\n\n발랑틴은 자기 이름이 논의의 대상이 되자, 불편한 시선을 피하려고 뒤로 물러서 있었다. 두 눈을 내리깐 채 울고 있었다. 노인이 한순간 깊디깊은 다정함이 어린 표정으로 그녀를 바라보더니, 공증인을 향해 돌아서서 반대의 표시로 의미심장하게 눈을 깜박였다.\n\n\"뭐라고요.\" 공증인이 말했다. \"발랑틴 드 빌포르 양을 잔여 재산 수령인으로 삼지 않으시겠다는 겁니까?\"\n\"아니오.\"\n\n\"착오는 없으신 거지요?\" 공증인이 말했다. \"정말로 그것이 선생님의 뜻이 아니라고 선언하시는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"아니오.\" 누아르티에가 되풀이했다. \"아니오.\"\n\n발랑틴이 고개를 들었다. 놀라움에 말문이 막혔다. 그녀를 슬프게 한 것은 상속에서 제외되었다는 사실 자체가 아니라, 할아버지를 그런 행동으로 몰아간 감정을 도무지 헤아릴 수 없다는 점이었다. 그러나 누아르티에가 어찌나 애정 어린 다정함으로 그녀를 바라보았던지, 그녀는 소리쳤다.\n\n\"오, 할아버지, 이제 알겠어요. 제게서 거두시는 건 오직 재산뿐이군요. 제가 늘 누려 온 사랑은 여전히 남겨 주시는 거예요.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇다, 정녕 그렇다.\" 중풍 환자의 눈이 말했다. 그가 발랑틴이 오해할 수 없는 표정으로 눈을 감았기 때문이다.\n\n\"고맙습니다, 고맙습니다.\" 그녀가 속삭였다. 발랑틴이 자기 재산의 정해진 상속인이 아니라는 노인의 선언은 빌포르 부인의 기대를 부추겼다. 그녀는 슬며시 환자에게 다가가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면, 분명, 친애하는 누아르티에 씨, 재산을 손자이신 에두아르 드 빌포르에게 남기실 생각이시군요?\"\n\n이 말에 응답한 눈 깜박임은 더없이 단호하고도 끔찍했으며, 거의 증오에 가까운 감정을 드러냈다.\n\n\"아니라고요?\" 공증인이 말했다. \"그러면, 어쩌면 아드님이신 빌포르 씨에게요?\"\n\n\"아니오.\" 두 공증인이 유언자의 진짜 의도가 무엇인지 묻듯, 말없는 놀라움 속에 서로를 마주 보았다. 빌포르와 그의 아내는 둘 다 얼굴이 붉어졌다. 한쪽은 수치심으로, 다른 한쪽은 분노로.\n\n\"그럼 저희 모두가 무슨 잘못을 한 건가요, 할아버지?\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"이제 저희 누구도 사랑하지 않으시는 것 같아요.\"\n\n노인의 눈이 빌포르와 그의 아내를 빠르게 훑고 지나가더니, 형언할 수 없는 애정의 눈빛으로 발랑틴에게 머물렀다.\n\n\"그럼,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"할아버지, 저를 사랑하신다면, 지금 이 순간 그 사랑을 행동에 옮겨 보세요. 제가 할아버지 재산을 한 번도 생각해 본 적 없다는 건 잘 아시잖아요. 게다가 사람들은 제가 어머니 쪽으로 이미 부자라고, 지나치게 부자라고까지 하는걸요. 그러니 뜻을 밝혀 주세요.\"\n\n누아르티에가 영민한 눈을 발랑틴의 손에 고정했다.\n\n\"제 손이요?\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"예.\"\n\n\"손이라니!\" 모두가 외쳤다.\n\n\"오, 여러분, 보시다시피 다 소용없는 일이고, 제 아버지의 정신이 정말로 손상된 겁니다.\" 빌포르가 말했다.\n\n\"아.\" 발랑틴이 불쑥 외쳤다. \"알겠어요. 제 결혼을 말씀하시는 거죠, 할아버지?\"\n\n\"예, 예, 예.\" 중풍 환자가 신호했다. 자기 뜻을 알아맞힌 발랑틴에게 기쁘고 고마운 눈빛을 던지며.\n\n\"이 결혼 때문에 저희 모두에게 화가 나신 거죠, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"예.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 이건 너무 터무니없군요.\" 빌포르가 말했다.\n\n\"실례합니다, 선생.\" 공증인이 대꾸했다. \"오히려 누아르티에 씨의 뜻은 제게 아주 분명합니다. 그분 머릿속을 스쳐 가는 생각의 흐름을 저는 어렵지 않게 이을 수 있습니다.\"\n\n\"제가 프란츠 데피네 씨와 결혼하길 원치 않으시는 거죠?\" 발랑틴이 말했다.\n\n\"원치 않는다.\" 할아버지의 눈이 말했다.\n\n\"그래서 손녀분의 상속권을 박탈하시는 겁니까?\" 공증인이 말을 이었다. \"선생님의 뜻에 어긋나는 혼약을 맺었다는 이유로요?\"\n\n\"예.\"\n\n\"그러니까, 이 결혼만 아니었다면 손녀분이 상속인이 되었을 거란 말씀이군요?\"\n\n\"예.\"\n\n깊은 침묵이 흘렀다. 두 공증인은 이 일을 어떻게 진행하는 게 최선일지 의논하고 있었다. 발랑틴은 더없이 고마운 미소를 띠고 할아버지를 바라보았고, 빌포르는 분에 받쳐 입술을 깨물고 있었으며, 빌포르 부인은 자기도 모르게 온 얼굴에 드러나는 내심의 기쁨을 억누르지 못하고 있었다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 침묵을 가장 먼저 깬 빌포르가 말했다. \"이 결혼의 타당성을 가장 잘 판단할 사람은 저라고 봅니다. 제 딸의 혼사를 결정할 권리를 가진 사람은 오직 저뿐입니다. 그 아이가 프란츠 데피네 씨와 결혼하기를 저는 바라며, 그 아이는 그와 결혼할 것입니다.\"\n\n발랑틴이 흐느끼며 의자에 주저앉았다.\n\n\"선생,\" 공증인이 말했다. \"빌포르 양이 그래도 프란츠 씨와의 결혼을 고집한다면, 재산을 어떻게 처분하실 작정이십니까?\" 노인은 아무 대답도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"물론, 어떤 식으로든 처분하시겠지요?\"\n\n\"예.\"\n\n\"가족 중 누군가에게 유리하게요?\"\n\"아니오.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 자선 목적에 바치실 생각이십니까?\" 공증인이 계속 물었다.\n\n\"예.\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 공증인이 말했다. \"법이 아들에게서 상속분을 통째로 박탈하는 것은 허용하지 않는다는 걸 아십니까?\"\n\n\"예.\"\n\n\"그러면 법이 아드님의 상속분에서 제하도록 허용하는 부분만 처분하실 생각이십니까?\" 누아르티에는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"그래도 전부 다 처분하길 원하십니까?\"\n\n\"예.\"\n\n\"하지만 선생님이 돌아가신 뒤에 유언에 이의를 제기할 텐데요?\" \"아니오.\"\n\n\"제 아버지는 저를 아십니다.\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"아버지의 뜻을 제가 신성하게 받들리라 확신하고 계시지요. 게다가 제 처지로는 가난한 이들을 상대로 소송할 수 없다는 것도 아십니다.\" 누아르티에의 눈이 승리감으로 빛났다.\n\n\"선생은 어떻게 결정하시겠습니까?\" 공증인이 빌포르에게 물었다.\n\n\"아무것도 없습니다, 선생. 제 아버지가 내리신 결심이고, 한번 정한 마음을 결코 바꾸지 않으신다는 걸 저는 압니다. 저는 완전히 체념했습니다. 이 90만 프랑은 어느 병원을 살찌우려고 집안을 떠나겠지요. 하지만 늙은이의 변덕에 이렇게 굴복하는 건 우스운 일이니, 저는 제 양심에 따라 처신하겠습니다.\"\n\n이렇게 말하고 빌포르는 아내와 함께 방을 나갔고, 아버지가 마음대로 하도록 내버려 두었다. 그날로 유언장이 작성되었고, 증인들이 불려 왔으며, 노인의 승인을 받아 모두가 보는 앞에서 봉인된 뒤, 집안의 공증인인 데샹 씨에게 맡겨졌다.\n\n# Chapter 60. The Telegraph\n\n빌포르 부부가 돌아와 보니, 두 사람이 없는 사이에 찾아온 몬테크리스토 백작이 응접실로 안내되어 그곳에서 아직 그들을 기다리고 있었다. 빌포르 부인은 조금 전의 격한 감정에서 아직 충분히 회복되지 못해 손님을 곧바로 맞을 수 없었으므로 침실로 물러났고, 좀 더 자신을 다잡을 수 있는 검사는 곧장 응접실로 향했다.\n\n빌포르는 겉으로 보기에는 마음속을 스치는 감정을 완전히 감추었다고 자부했지만, 자신의 이마에 아직도 먹구름이 드리워 있다는 것은 알지 못했다. 그것이 어찌나 또렷했던지, 환한 미소를 짓고 있던 백작은 그의 어둡고 골똘한 기색을 곧바로 알아챘다.\n\n\"이런!\" 첫인사가 끝난 뒤 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"무슨 일이십니까, 빌포르 씨? 혹시 사형에 해당하는 죄의 기소장을 작성하시던 참에 제가 들이닥친 건가요?\"\n\n빌포르가 억지로 웃어 보였다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 백작.\" 그가 대답했다. \"이번 일의 희생자는 오직 저 하나뿐이지요. 송사에서 진 사람도 저이고, 그 일이 제게 불리하게 결판난 것도 불운과 고집과 어리석음 탓이니까요.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이십니까?\" 몬테크리스토가 그럴듯하게 꾸민 관심을 보이며 말했다. \"정말로 무슨 큰 불행이라도 겪으신 겁니까?\"\n\n\"아, 아닙니다, 백작.\" 빌포르가 쓴웃음을 지으며 말했다. \"그저 돈을 좀 잃은 것뿐입니다. 입에 올릴 가치도 없는 일이지요, 정말입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇군요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"당신처럼 큰 재산을 지닌 데다 그토록 달관한 정신을 가진 분에게는, 돈 얼마쯤 잃는 일은 거의 대수롭지 않은 것이겠지요.\"\n\n\"저를 괴롭히는 건 돈을 잃었다는 사실 자체가 아닙니다.\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"물론 90만 프랑이면 아쉬워할 만한 액수이긴 하지요. 하지만 제 희망과 재산을 무너뜨리고, 어쩌면 제 자식의 앞날까지 망쳐 놓을지 모르는 이 운명, 이 우연, 아니 뭐라 부르시든 그 힘에 더 화가 나는 것은, 이 모든 것이 다시 노망이 든 늙은이 하나 때문에 벌어진 일이라는 점입니다.\"\n\n\"뭐라고 하셨습니까?\" 백작이 말했다. \"90만 프랑이라고요? 그건 정말이지 철학자라도 아쉬워할 만한 액수로군요. 그런데 이 모든 골칫거리의 원인이 대체 누구입니까?\"\n\n\"제 아버지입니다, 말씀드렸다시피요.\"\n\n\"누아르티에 씨 말입니까? 하지만 제 기억으로는, 그분이 완전히 마비되어 모든 기능을 잃으셨다고 하지 않으셨던가요?\"\n\n\"네, 신체 기능은요. 움직이지도 말하지도 못하시니까요. 그런데도 제가 말씀드린 식으로 생각하고, 행동하고, 뜻을 관철하십니다. 한 오 분 전에 그 방에서 나왔는데, 지금 공증인 두 사람을 앞에 두고 유언장을 받아쓰게 하고 계십니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 그러려면 말씀을 하셔야 할 텐데요?\"\n\n\"그보다 더한 일을 하셨지요. 자기 뜻을 전달하셨습니다.\"\n\n\"그런 일이 어떻게 가능합니까?\"\n\n\"아직 생기로 가득한 두 눈의 힘을 빌려서지요. 보시다시피, 그 눈은 치명상을 입힐 힘까지 지니고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"여보,\" 막 방으로 들어선 빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"어쩌면 당신이 일을 너무 과장하는지도 몰라요.\"\n\n\"안녕하십니까, 부인.\" 백작이 인사하며 말했다.\n\n빌포르 부인이 더없이 우아한 미소로 인사를 받았다.\n\n\"빌포르 씨가 제게 들려주신 이야기가 대체 무엇입니까?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다. \"그 도무지 알 수 없는 불행이라는 게……\"\n\n\"알 수 없다는 말이 딱 맞지요!\" 검사가 어깨를 으쓱하며 말을 가로챘다. \"한 노인네의 변덕일 뿐입니다!\"\n\n\"그분의 결정을 번복하게 할 방법은 없습니까?\"\n\n\"있어요.\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"지금 발랑틴에게 불리하게 작성되고 있는 그 유언장을, 발랑틴에게 유리하도록 바꾸게 하는 것은 여전히 전적으로 제 남편의 손에 달려 있답니다.\"\n\n빌포르 부부가 수수께끼 같은 말투로 이야기를 시작하는 것을 알아챈 백작은, 대화에 전혀 관심이 없는 척하며 에두아르를 지켜보는 데 열중한 양 꾸몄다. 에두아르는 장난스럽게 새의 물그릇에 잉크를 따르고 있었다.\n\n\"여보,\" 빌포르가 아내에게 대답했다. \"내가 우리 집안에서 가장 노릇을 한 적도 없고, 내 고갯짓 하나로 온 세상의 운명이 결판난다고 여긴 적도 없다는 걸 당신도 알지 않소. 그렇지만 우리 집안에서 내 뜻만큼은 존중받아야 하고, 늙은이의 망령과 아이의 변덕이 내가 그토록 오랜 세월 품어 온 계획을 뒤엎도록 내버려 둘 수는 없소. 데피네 남작은, 당신도 알다시피, 내 친구였소. 그의 아들과 사돈을 맺는 것은 더없이 합당한 일이오.\"\n\n\"당신은,\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"발랑틴이 그분과 한패라고 생각하지 않으세요? 그 애는 늘 이 결혼을 반대해 왔잖아요. 방금 우리가 보고 들은 일이 그 둘이 짜고 꾸민 계략을 실행에 옮긴 것뿐이라 해도 저는 조금도 놀라지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"부인,\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"내 말을 믿으시오. 90만 프랑의 재산을 그렇게 쉽게 포기할 수는 없는 법이오.\"\n\n\"그래도 그 애는 세상을 등질 결심을 할 수도 있어요. 바로 일 년쯤 전에 그 애 스스로 수도원에 들어가겠다고 했었으니까요.\"\n\n\"상관없소.\" 빌포르가 대답했다. \"이 결혼은 반드시 성사될 것이라고 나는 말하는 거요.\"\n\n\"당신 아버님의 반대 뜻에도 불구하고요?\" 빌포르 부인이 새로운 공격 지점을 골라잡으며 말했다. \"그건 중대한 일이에요.\"\n\n귀를 기울이지 않는 척하던 몬테크리스토는, 그러나 오가는 말을 한마디도 빠짐없이 듣고 있었다.\n\n\"부인,\" 빌포르가 대답했다. \"나는 정말이지 늘 아버지를 깊이 존경해 왔다고 떳떳이 말할 수 있소. 혈육의 정이라는 본능적인 감정에, 그분의 도덕적 우월함에 대한 자각까지 더해졌기 때문이오. 아버지라는 이름은 두 가지 의미에서 신성하오. 우리를 존재하게 한 분으로서, 또 우리가 마땅히 따라야 할 주인으로서 받들어져야 하니까. 하지만 지금 같은 상황이라면, 아버지를 미워했다는 이유로 그 아들에게 분풀이를 하는 한 노인의 분별을 의심하는 것도 정당한 일이오. 그런 변덕에 맞춰 내 행동을 조절한다면 우스운 노릇이지. 나는 앞으로도 누아르티에 씨께 변함없는 존경을 지킬 것이오. 그분이 내게 안긴 금전적 손실도 불평 없이 견디겠소. 그러나 내 결심만큼은 흔들리지 않을 것이고, 세상은 어느 쪽이 옳은지 보게 될 거요. 그러니 나는 내 딸을 프란츠 데피네 남작에게 시집보내겠소. 그것이 그 애에게 마땅하고 어울리는 혼처라고 여기기 때문이고, 한마디로 내 딸의 손을 누구에게 내어 줄지는 내 마음이기 때문이오.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요?\" 백작이 말했다. 빌포르는 이 일장연설을 하는 내내 백작의 눈에서 동의의 빛을 거듭 구했었다. \"뭐라고요? 누아르티에 씨가 따님이 프란츠 데피네 남작과 결혼하려 한다는 이유로 빌포르 양의 상속권을 박탈하신다는 말씀입니까?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다. 그게 이유지요.\" 빌포르가 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다.\n\n\"적어도 겉으로 내세운 이유는 그래요.\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"진짜 이유입니다, 부인. 분명히 말씀드릴 수 있어요. 저는 제 아버지를 아니까요.\"\n\n\"하지만 저는 데피네 씨가 어떤 점에서 다른 누구보다도 아버님의 비위를 거슬렀는지 알고 싶군요.\"\n\n\"제가 프란츠 데피네 씨를 아는 것 같습니다.\" 백작이 말했다. \"샤를 10세에 의해 데피네 남작 작위를 받은 케넬 장군의 아들이 아닙니까?\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다.\" 빌포르가 말했다.\n\n\"음, 하지만 제가 보기에 그는 매력적인 청년인데요.\"\n\n\"그렇지요. 그러니 그건 누아르티에 씨가 손녀의 결혼을 막으려고 둘러댄 핑계에 불과하다고 저는 믿는 거예요. 늙은이들의 애정이란 늘 그렇게 이기적이니까요.\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그 증오의 원인을 전혀 모르신단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"아, 이런! 그걸 누가 알겠어요?\"\n\n\"혹시 어떤 정치적 견해 차이 때문은 아닐까요?\"\n\n\"제 아버지와 데피네 남작은 제가 그 끝자락만 본 그 격동의 시대를 사신 분들이지요.\" 빌포르가 말했다.\n\n\"아버님이 보나파르트파 아니셨습니까?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다. \"그런 비슷한 말씀을 제게 하셨던 것 같은데요.\"\n\n\"제 아버지는 무엇보다도 자코뱅파였습니다.\" 빌포르가 감정에 휩쓸려 신중함의 선을 넘어 버린 채 말했다. \"나폴레옹이 그분의 어깨에 걸쳐 준 원로원 의원의 예복은, 그 노인을 위장해 주었을 뿐 조금도 바꾸어 놓지는 못했지요. 제 아버지가 음모를 꾸민 것은 황제를 위해서가 아니라 부르봉 왕가에 맞서기 위해서였습니다. 누아르티에 씨에게는 이런 특이한 점이 있었거든요. 그분은 결코 실현될 수 없는 몽상적인 계획 따위는 꾀하지 않으셨고, 오직 가능한 일을 위해 싸우셨습니다. 그리고 그 가능한 일을 실현하기 위해, 산악파의 그 무시무시한 이론을 적용하셨지요. 원하는 결과를 얻기 위해 필요하다 싶은 수단이라면 어떤 것 앞에서도 결코 물러서지 않는 이론을 말입니다.\"\n\n* 산악파: 프랑스 혁명기 국민공회의 급진 좌파. —옮긴이\n\"음,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"제가 생각한 그대로군요. 누아르티에 씨와 데피네 씨를 개인적으로 얽히게 한 것은 정치였습니다. 데피네 장군은 나폴레옹 밑에서 복무했으면서도 여전히 왕당파의 신념을 간직하고 있지 않았습니까? 그리고 황제의 대의를 지지한다고 여겨져 초대받은 보나파르트파 모임에서 나오던 어느 날 저녁, 암살당한 바로 그 인물이 아닙니까?\"\n\n빌포르가 거의 공포에 질린 얼굴로 백작을 바라보았다.\n\n\"그럼 제가 잘못 알고 있는 겁니까?\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"아니에요, 백작. 사실은 정확히 말씀하신 그대로랍니다.\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"그리고 그 해묵은 원한이 되살아나는 것을 막으려고, 빌포르 씨가 그 철천지원수였던 두 사람의 자식들을 애정의 끈으로 맺어 주자는 생각을 하게 된 거예요.\"\n\n\"숭고하고 자비로운 생각이로군요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"온 세상이 박수를 보낼 만한 일입니다. 누아르티에 드 빌포르 양이 프란츠 데피네 부인이라는 칭호를 얻는 모습을 보게 된다면, 그야말로 고결한 일이지요.\"\n\n빌포르가 몸서리를 치며, 방금 자신이 내뱉은 말을 짓게 한 진짜 속내를 그 표정에서 읽어 내려는 듯 몬테크리스토를 바라보았다. 그러나 백작은 검사를 완전히 따돌렸고, 늘 한결같이 짓고 있는 그 변함없는 미소 아래에서 무엇 하나 알아챌 수 없게 만들었다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"발랑틴이 할아버지의 재산을 잃는다는 것은 중대한 일이긴 해도, 데피네 씨가 이 금전적 손실에 겁을 먹으리라고는 생각지 않습니다. 그는 어쩌면 그 돈 자체보다 저를 더 높이 평가할 겁니다. 제가 그와의 약속을 지키기 위해 모든 것을 희생하는 것을 보았으니까요. 게다가 그는 발랑틴이 어머니 쪽 권리로 부유하다는 것도, 그 애를 끔찍이 아끼는 외조부모인 생메랑 후작 부부의 재산을 십중팔구 물려받으리라는 것도 알고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그분들로 말하자면 누아르티에 씨 못지않게 사랑받고 보살핌을 받으실 만한 분들이지요.\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"게다가 한 달쯤 뒤면 파리로 오실 거고, 발랑틴도 이런 모욕을 당한 마당에 누아르티에 씨에게 갇혀 산 채로 파묻혀 지낼 필요가 더는 없게 될 거예요.\"\n\n백작은 이 상처 입은 자존심과 좌절된 야심의 사연을 흡족하게 들었다.\n\n\"하지만 제가 보기에는,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그리고 이제부터 드릴 말씀에 대해 먼저 양해를 구해야겠습니다만, 만약 누아르티에 씨가 빌포르 양이 자신이 증오하던 사람의 아들과 결혼하려 한다는 이유로 그녀의 상속권을 박탈하시는 것이라면, 이 사랑스러운 에두아르에 대해서는 똑같은 불만을 품으실 까닭이 없지 않습니까.\"\n\n\"맞아요.\" 빌포르 부인이 뭐라 형언할 수 없는 어조로 말했다. \"이건 부당한 일 아닌가요? 부끄러울 만큼 부당한 일이지요. 가엾은 에두아르도 발랑틴과 똑같이 누아르티에 씨의 손주인데, 만약 그 애가 프란츠 씨와 결혼하려 하지 않았다면 누아르티에 씨는 전 재산을 그 애에게 물려주었을 거예요. 게다가 발랑틴이 할아버지에게서 상속권을 박탈당한다 해도, 그 애는 여전히 에두아르보다 세 배는 더 부유할 테고요.\"\n\n백작은 듣기만 할 뿐 더는 말이 없었다.\n\n\"백작,\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"저희 집안의 불행으로 더는 당신을 붙들어 두지 않겠습니다. 사실 제 유산은 자선 단체로 넘어갈 것이고, 아버지는 아무런 까닭도 없이 제게서 정당한 상속분을 빼앗으신 셈이지요. 하지만 저는 분별 있고 정 있는 사람답게 처신했다는 것을 안다는 만족감만은 얻을 겁니다. 제가 그 돈의 이자를 약속했던 데피네 씨는, 설령 제가 더없이 가혹한 궁핍을 견뎌야 한다 해도 그것을 받게 될 것입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇지만,\" 빌포르 부인이 끊임없이 자기 머릿속을 맴도는 그 한 가지 생각으로 되돌아오며 말했다. \"어쩌면 이 불운한 일을 데피네 씨에게 설명해 드리는 편이 나을지도 몰라요. 그분 스스로 빌포르 양에 대한 청혼을 거두어들일 기회를 드리도록 말이에요.\"\n\n\"아, 그건 참으로 안타까운 일이지요.\" 빌포르가 말했다.\n\n\"참으로 안타까운 일이고말고요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"틀림없습니다.\" 빌포르가 목소리를 누그러뜨리며 말했다. \"한번 맺어졌다가 깨진 혼담은 처녀에게 일종의 불명예를 안기는 법이지요. 게다가 제가 그토록 끝내고 싶어 했던 그 해묵은 소문들이 당장 다시 고개를 들 겁니다. 아니, 모든 게 잘될 거예요. 데피네 씨가 명예를 아는 사람이라면, 자신이 그 어느 때보다도 더 빌포르 양에게 묶여 있다고 여길 겁니다. 노골적인 탐욕에 휘둘리는 자가 아니라면 말이지요. 하지만 그건 있을 수 없는 일이고요.\"\n\n\"저도 빌포르 씨의 의견에 동의합니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 빌포르 부인에게 시선을 고정한 채 말했다. \"그리고 만약 제가 충고를 드려도 될 만큼 그와 가까운 사이라면, 데피네 씨가 돌아오는 중이라고 들었으니, 이 일을 당장, 결코 번복할 수 없도록 매듭지으라고 권하겠습니다. 빌포르 씨에게 그토록 큰 명예가 될 그 계획의 성공을 제가 보증하지요.\"\n\n검사가 그 제안에 기뻐하며 자리에서 일어섰지만, 그의 아내는 안색이 살짝 변했다.\n\n\"좋습니다, 제가 바라던 게 바로 그것이고, 당신 같은 조언자의 인도를 따르겠습니다.\" 빌포르가 몬테크리스토에게 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"그러니 여기 있는 모두는 오늘 있었던 일을 아예 없었던 일로 여기고, 우리가 애초의 계획을 바꾸는 일 따위는 생각조차 한 적 없는 것으로 합시다.\"\n\n\"빌포르 씨,\" 백작이 말했다. \"세상은 부당하긴 해도 당신의 결단을 흡족히 여길 겁니다. 당신의 친구들은 당신을 자랑스러워할 것이고, 데피네 씨로 말하자면, 설령 그가 빌포르 양을 지참금 한 푼 없이 맞아들인다 해도, 물론 그러지는 않겠지만, 약속을 지키고 의무를 다하기 위해 그런 희생도 마다하지 않는 집안에 들어가게 되었다는 생각에 더없이 기뻐할 겁니다.\"\n\n이 말을 마치자 백작이 떠나려고 자리에서 일어섰다.\n\n\"가시려는 거예요, 백작?\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"유감스럽게도 그래야겠습니다, 부인. 토요일 약속을 다시 한번 일러 드리려고 들렀을 뿐이니까요.\"\n\n\"저희가 그걸 잊을까 봐 걱정하셨나요?\"\n\n\"부인은 참 친절하십니다만, 빌포르 씨는 워낙 중요하고 급한 일이 많으셔서요.\"\n\n\"제 남편이 약속을 했답니다.\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"방금 모든 것을 잃을 처지에서도 약속을 지키기로 결심하는 모습을 보셨잖아요. 모든 것을 얻을 일이라면 더더욱 그렇게 할 까닭이 있겠지요.\"\n\n\"그런데,\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"손님을 맞이하시는 곳이 샹젤리제의 댁입니까?\"\n\n\"아닙니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"바로 그 점이 두 분의 호의를 한층 더 값지게 하는 까닭이지요. 시골에 있는 집이거든요.\"\n\n\"시골이요?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"그럼 어디입니까? 파리 근교 아닌가요?\"\n\n\"아주 가깝습니다. 시문에서 반 리외밖에 안 되는 오퇴유랍니다.\"\n\n\"오퇴유라고요?\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"참, 빌포르 부인이 당신께서 오퇴유에 사신다고 하더군요. 부인이 실려 간 곳이 당신 댁이었으니까요. 그런데 오퇴유 어느 쪽에 사십니까?\"\n\n\"퐁텐 거리입니다.\"\n\n\"퐁텐 거리라고요!\" 빌포르가 동요한 어조로 외쳤다. \"몇 번지입니까?\"\n\"28번지입니다.\"\n\n\"그럼,\" 빌포르가 소리쳤다. \"생메랑 씨의 집을 사신 게 바로 당신이었군요!\"\n\n\"그게 생메랑 씨의 집이었습니까?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다.\n\n\"네.\" 빌포르 부인이 대답했다. \"그런데, 백작, 믿어지세요……\"\n\n\"무엇을 말입니까?\"\n\n\"그 집이 예쁘다고 생각하시죠, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"아주 매력적이라고 생각합니다.\"\n\n\"그런데 제 남편은 그 집에서는 도무지 살려고 하질 않았답니다.\"\n\n\"그래요?\" 몬테크리스토가 받아 말했다. \"그건 빌포르 씨의 편견인데, 저로서는 도무지 그 까닭을 알 수가 없군요.\"\n\n\"저는 오퇴유가 싫습니다.\" 검사가 애써 태연한 척하려고 안간힘을 쓰며 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 그 반감이 지나쳐서 제게서 당신과 함께할 기쁨까지 앗아 가시지는 않겠지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 백작. 그러길 바라고, 정말이지 최선을 다하겠습니다.\" 빌포르가 더듬거리며 말했다.\n\n\"아,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"어떤 핑계도 받아들이지 않겠습니다. 토요일 여섯 시입니다. 기다리고 있겠습니다. 만약 오시지 않으면 저는 이렇게 생각할 겁니다. 달리 알 길이 없으니 말이지요. 이십 년이나 사람이 살지 않은 그 집에 무슨 음울한 내력이나 끔찍한 전설이라도 얽혀 있는 게 틀림없다고요.\"\n\n\"가겠습니다, 백작. 꼭 가지요.\" 빌포르가 다급히 말했다.\n\n\"고맙습니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그럼 이제 작별을 고하게 해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"아까 가 보셔야 한다고 하셨지요.\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"그 까닭을 말씀하시려던 참에 다른 일로 주의를 돌리셨고요.\"\n\n\"실은, 부인,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"제가 어디로 가는지 감히 말씀드려도 될지 모르겠군요.\"\n\n\"별말씀을. 어서 말씀하세요.\"\n\n\"그럼 말씀드리지요. 제가 때때로 몇 시간이고 골똘히 생각에 잠기곤 했던 어떤 것을 보러 가는 길입니다.\"\n\n\"그게 뭔가요?\"\n\n\"전신기입니다. 자, 이제 제 비밀을 털어놓았군요.\"\n\n\"전신기요?\" 빌포르 부인이 되물었다.\n\n\"네, 전신기입니다. 길 끝 언덕배기에 세워진 그것을 저는 종종 보아 왔지요. 햇빛 속에서 사방으로 꺾이는 그 검은 팔들은 늘 거대한 딱정벌레의 발톱을 떠올리게 했습니다. 그리고 정말이지, 저는 그것을 바라볼 때마다 늘 가슴이 설레었답니다. 이 갖가지 신호들이 그토록 정확하게 허공을 가르며, 선의 한쪽 끝 탁자 앞에 앉은 사람의 생각과 바람을 삼백 리외나 떨어진 반대편 끝의 또 다른 사람에게, 그것도 메시지를 보내는 이의 단순한 의지 행위 하나만으로 전달한다는 것이 얼마나 경이로운 일인가 생각하지 않을 수 없었으니까요. 저는 정령이며 공기의 요정이며 땅의 요정, 한마디로 비밀스러운 학문을 관장하는 온갖 존재를 떠올리기 시작했고, 마침내 제 상상의 별난 장난에 소리 내어 웃고 말았지요. 그런데 길고 검은 발톱을 지닌 이 커다란 곤충들을 더 가까이 들여다보고 싶다는 생각은 도무지 들지 않았습니다. 그 돌로 된 날개 밑에서, 음모와 파벌과 정부의 책략에 시달려 죽을 지경이 된 어떤 자그마한 인간 정령을 발견하게 될까 봐 늘 두려웠거든요. 그런데 어느 화창한 날, 저는 이 전신기를 움직이는 자가 일 년에 천이백 프랑을 받고 고용된 가련한 신세에 불과하다는 것을 알게 되었습니다. 그는 천문학자처럼 하늘을 살피는 것도, 낚시꾼처럼 물을 바라보는 것도, 심지어 주위의 풍경을 둘러보는 특권을 누리는 것도 아니라, 하루 종일 단조로운 일생을 자기에게서 네댓 리외 떨어진, 흰 배에 검은 발톱을 가진 동료 곤충을 지켜보는 데 다 보낸다더군요. 마침내 저는 이 살아 있는 번데기를 더 가까이 들여다보고 싶은, 그리고 이 곤충 배우들이 그저 갖가지 줄을 잡아당기는 일에만 매달릴 때 그들이 맡은 그 은밀한 역할을 이해해 보고 싶은 욕구를 느꼈습니다.\"\n\n\"그래서 거기로 가시는 거군요?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\"\n\"어느 전신기를 찾아가실 작정인가요? 내무부 것인가요, 아니면 천문대 것인가요?\"\n\n\"아, 아닙니다. 거기 가면 제가 차라리 모르고 싶은 것들을 억지로 이해시키려 드는 사람들, 그리고 자기들조차 모르는 그 신비를 제 뜻과 상관없이 설명하려 드는 사람들을 만나게 될 테니까요. 이런! 저는 곤충에 대한 환상만큼은 고이 간직하고 싶습니다. 같은 인간에 대해 품었던 환상이 깨진 것만으로도 충분하니까요. 그러니 저는 그 어느 전신기도 찾지 않고, 탁 트인 시골에 있는 것을 찾아가렵니다. 거기서라면 자기가 다루는 기계 이상은 아무것도 모르는, 사람 좋은 얼뜨기 하나를 만날 수 있겠지요.\"\n\n\"당신은 별난 분이로군요.\" 빌포르가 말했다.\n\n\"어느 노선을 살펴보는 게 좋겠습니까?\"\n\n\"지금 한창 가장 많이 쓰이는 노선이지요.\"\n\n\"에스파냐 노선을 말씀하시는 거겠지요?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다. 장관에게 보내는 소개장이 필요하시면, 그쪽에서 당신에게 설명을……\"\n\n\"아닙니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"아까도 말씀드렸듯이, 저는 그것을 이해하고 싶지 않으니까요. 제가 그것을 이해하는 순간, 제게 전신기란 더는 존재하지 않게 될 겁니다. 그건 그저 뒤샤텔 씨나 몽탈리베 씨가 바욘의 도지사에게 보내는 신호, 그리스어 두 단어 '텔레'와 '그라페인'으로 그럴싸하게 꾸민 것에 지나지 않게 되겠지요. 제가 상상 속에 온전하고 중요한 모습 그대로 간직하고 싶은 것은, 바로 그 검은 발톱을 지닌 곤충과 그 경이로운 단어랍니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 가 보세요. 두 시간만 지나면 어두워져서 아무것도 못 보실 테니까요.\"\n\n\"이런! 겁을 주시는군요. 가장 가까운 길이 어느 쪽입니까? 바욘 쪽인가요?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다. 바욘으로 가는 길이지요.\"\n\n\"그다음엔 샤티용으로 가는 길이고요?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\"\n\"몽레리 탑을 지나서 말이지요?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\"\n\"고맙습니다. 안녕히 계십시오. 토요일에 전신기에 대한 제 소감을 들려드리겠습니다.\"\n\n문간에서 백작은 공증인 두 사람과 마주쳤다. 그들은 발랑틴의 상속권을 박탈하는 증서를 막 완성한 참이었고, 자기들의 평판에 적잖이 보탬이 될 일을 해냈다는 확신에 차서 떠나는 길이었다.\n\n# Chapter 61. How a Gardener May Get Rid of the Dormice that Eat His Peaches\n\n자기가 말한 그날 밤이 아니라 이튿날 아침, 몬테크리스토 백작은 앙페르 관문을 빠져나가 오를레앙으로 가는 길로 접어들었다. 리나스 마을을 지날 때 전신소가 거대한 뼈만 남은 팔을 휘둘렀지만, 백작은 멈추지 않고 지나쳐 그 유명한 몽틀레리 평원의 가장 높은 곳에 자리한 몽틀레리 탑에 다다랐다. 언덕 기슭에서 백작은 말에서 내려 폭이 한 자 반쯤 되는 구불구불한 작은 오솔길을 따라 올라가기 시작했다. 꼭대기에 이르자 산울타리가 길을 막았는데, 그 위에는 붉고 흰 꽃 자리에 풋과일이 맺혀 있었다.\n\n몬테크리스토는 울타리 안으로 들어가는 입구를 찾았고, 오래지 않아 버드나무 가지로 만든 경첩이 달리고 못과 끈으로 잠긴 작은 나무 쪽문을 발견했다. 백작은 곧 그 장치를 풀어냈고, 문이 열리자 길이 스무 자, 너비 열두 자쯤 되는 작은 정원에 들어섰다. 한쪽은 우리가 문이라 부른 그 기발한 장치가 달린 산울타리의 일부로, 다른 한쪽은 담쟁이덩굴로 뒤덮이고 갈란투스가 점점이 박힌 오래된 탑으로 막혀 있었다.\n\n낡고 비바람에 시달리고 꽃으로 단장한 이 탑을 보고서는—생일 잔치에 손주들을 맞으려고 곱게 차려입은 노부인에 비길 만한 그 탑이—이상한 이야기들을 들려줄 수 있으리라고는 아무도 생각하지 못했을 것이다. 속담에 모든 벽에는 위협적인 귀가 달려 있다지만, 만약 그 탑에 귀뿐 아니라 목소리까지 있었다면 말이다.\n\n정원에는 붉은 자갈을 깐 길이 가로질러 나 있었고, 그 가장자리에는 여러 해 자란 두툼한 회양목이 띠를 두르고 있었는데, 그 빛깔과 색조가 우리 시대의 루벤스라 할 들라크루아의 마음마저 흐뭇하게 했을 법했다. 이 길은 숫자 8 자 모양으로 나 있어, 구불구불 휘어지며 스무 자밖에 안 되는 정원에 예순 자나 되는 산책로를 만들어 냈다.\n\n정원사들의 싱그럽고 환히 웃는 여신 플로라가, 이 작은 울타리 안에서 바쳐진 것보다 더 순수하고 더 정성스러운 숭배를 받은 적은 일찍이 없었다. 실제로 화단을 이룬 스무 그루의 장미나무 가운데 민달팽이가 갉아먹은 자국이 있는 것은 하나도 없었고, 습한 흙에서 자라는 식물을 그토록 망쳐 놓는 진딧물 떼의 흔적도 어디에서도 찾아볼 수 없었다. 그렇다고 정원에서 습기를 몰아낸 것도 아니었다. 검댕처럼 까만 흙과 나무들의 무성한 잎이 습기의 존재를 일러 주었다. 게다가 자연의 습기가 모자랐다 해도, 정원 한구석에 묻어 둔 물탱크 덕분에 인공으로 곧장 채워 줄 수 있었으니, 그 물탱크 위에는 개구리 한 마리와 두꺼비 한 마리가 자리를 잡고 있었는데, 둘은 서로 미워해서인지 늘 수반의 양쪽 끝에 떨어져 있었다. 길에는 풀잎 하나 보이지 않았고, 꽃밭에는 잡초 한 포기 없었다. 어떤 귀부인도 도자기 화분에 심은 제라늄과 선인장, 진달래를 이 여태껏 본 적 없는 정원사가 자기 작은 울타리에 쏟은 만큼의 정성으로 가꾸고 물을 준 적은 없었다.\n\n몬테크리스토는 문을 닫고 끈을 못에 걸어 잠근 뒤 걸음을 멈추고 주위를 둘러보았다.\n\n\"이 전신소의 사람은,\" 그가 말했다. \"정원사를 고용했거나, 아니면 농사일에 푹 빠져 사는 게 틀림없군.\"\n\n문득 그는 잎이 가득 담긴 손수레 뒤에 웅크리고 있던 무언가에 부딪혔다. 그 무언가가 놀라 소리를 지르며 일어섰고, 몬테크리스토는 쉰 살쯤 된 사내와 마주 섰다. 사내는 딸기를 따서 포도 잎사귀 위에 올려놓고 있었다. 잎사귀 열두 장에 딸기도 그만큼 있었는데, 느닷없이 일어서느라 손에 들고 있던 것을 떨어뜨리고 말았다.\n\n\"수확하시는 중이군요, 선생?\" 몬테크리스토가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"실례했습니다, 선생.\" 사내가 모자에 손을 올리며 대답했다. \"저 위에 있어야 하는데, 잠깐 내려온 참입니다.\"\n\n\"내 일이 자네를 방해하게 두지 말게, 친구.\" 백작이 말했다. \"딸기를 따게나, 정말 남은 게 있다면 말이야.\"\n\n\"열 개 남았습니다.\" 사내가 말했다. \"여기 열한 개가 있고, 원래 스물한 개였으니까요. 작년보다 다섯 개 더 많지요. 하지만 놀랄 일은 아닙니다. 올해는 봄이 따뜻했는데, 딸기는 더위를 좋아하거든요, 선생. 그래서 작년에 열여섯 개였던 게 올해는, 보십시오, 벌써 열한 개—열두, 열세, 열넷, 열다섯, 열여섯, 열일곱, 열여덟 개나 땄지요. 아, 세 개가 모자라네. 어젯밤엔 여기 있었는데, 선생—분명히 있었어요—세어 봤거든요. 시몬 아주머니네 아들놈이 훔쳐 간 게 분명합니다. 오늘 아침에 그놈이 여기 어슬렁거리는 걸 봤거든요. 아, 어린 망나니 같으니—남의 정원에서 도둑질을 하다니—그게 어디까지 갈지 모르는 게지요.\"\n\n\"물론, 그건 나쁜 짓이지.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"하지만 그 죄지은 녀석이 어리고 식탐이 많다는 점도 헤아려 줘야지.\"\n\n\"그야 그렇지요.\" 정원사가 말했다. \"하지만 그렇다고 덜 불쾌한 건 아닙니다. 그런데, 선생, 다시 한번 용서를 빕니다. 혹시 제가 여기 붙잡아 두고 있는 분이 관리이신지요.\" 그러고는 백작의 푸른 외투를 머뭇머뭇 흘끔거렸다.\n\n\"안심하게, 친구.\" 백작이 말했다. 그는 마음먹은 대로 무섭게도, 다정하게도 지을 수 있는 그 미소를 띠었는데, 지금은 더없이 인자한 마음만을 드러내고 있었다. \"나는 감독관이 아니라 여행자라네. 절반쯤 후회되는 호기심에 이끌려 여기까지 왔지, 자네 시간을 빼앗고 있으니 말이야.\"\n\n\"아, 제 시간은 값나가는 게 아닙니다.\" 사내가 우울한 미소를 지으며 대답했다. \"그래도 나라에 속한 시간이니 허비해서는 안 되지요. 하지만 한 시간 쉬어도 좋다는 신호를 받은 데다\"—여기서 그는 해시계를 흘끗 보았다. 몽틀레리의 이 울타리 안에는 해시계까지, 없는 게 없었다—\"십 분쯤 여유가 있고, 딸기도 익었으니 말입니다. 하루만 더 있으면—그러고 보니, 선생, 겨울잠쥐가 딸기를 먹는다고 보십니까?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 그렇진 않을 듯한데.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"겨울잠쥐는 로마인들처럼 그놈을 절여 먹지 않는 우리에게는 골치 아픈 이웃이지.\"\n\n\"네? 로마인들이 그걸 먹었다고요?\" 정원사가 말했다. \"겨울잠쥐를 먹었다는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"페트로니우스에서 그렇게 읽었네.\" 백작이 말했다.\n\n\"정말입니까? '겨울잠쥐처럼 살쪘다'는 말이야 있다지만, 맛이 좋을 리가 없는데요. 하루 종일 자다가 밤새 먹으려고만 깨어나니 살이 안 찔 수가 없지요. 들어 보십시오. 작년에 살구가 네 개 있었는데—한 개를 훔쳐 갔습니다. 천도복숭아는 딱 하나 있었는데—글쎄, 선생, 그놈들이 담벼락에서 반을 먹어 치웠지 뭡니까. 끝내주는 천도복숭아였는데—그렇게 맛있는 건 먹어 본 적이 없습니다.\"\n\n\"자네가 먹었나?\"\n\n\"그러니까, 남은 반쪽 말입니다—아시잖습니까. 정말 기막혔지요, 선생. 아, 그 양반들이 제일 못한 놈을 고르는 법이 없어요. 제일 못한 딸기를 고르지 않은 시몬 아주머니네 아들놈처럼 말입니다. 하지만 올해는,\" 원예가가 말을 이었다. \"그런 일이 없도록 단단히 지킬 겁니다. 딸기가 익으면 밤새 옆에 앉아 망을 봐야 한다 해도 말이지요.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 충분히 보았다. 과일마다 벌레가 있듯, 사람마다 가슴속에 집어삼키는 열정이 하나씩 있는 법이다. 이 전신소 사내의 열정은 원예였다. 백작은 포도에 닿는 햇볕을 가리던 포도 잎사귀를 따 주기 시작했고, 그렇게 정원사의 마음을 사로잡았다.\n\n\"전신기를 보러 오셨습니까, 선생?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 규칙에 어긋나지 않는다면 말일세.\"\n\n\"오, 아니지요.\" 정원사가 말했다. \"조금도 어긋나지 않습니다. 우리가 무슨 말을 하는지 알아들을 사람이 있을 리 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"내가 듣기로는,\" 백작이 말했다. \"자네들 자신도 옮겨 보내는 신호를 늘 알아듣는 건 아니라던데.\"\n\n\"맞습니다, 선생. 바로 그 점이 제일 마음에 들지요.\" 사내가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"어째서 그 점이 제일 마음에 드나?\"\n\n\"그러면 제겐 아무 책임이 없으니까요. 그럴 때 저는 기계일 뿐, 그 이상도 아니지요. 그러니 제 일만 하면 그 이상은 아무것도 요구받지 않습니다.\"\n\n'야망이라곤 없는 사람을 만나다니, 이런 일이 있을 수 있나?' 몬테크리스토가 속으로 생각했다. '그렇다면 내 계획이 어그러질 텐데.'\n\n\"선생,\" 정원사가 해시계를 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"십 분이 거의 다 됐습니다. 제자리로 돌아가야겠습니다. 저와 함께 올라가시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"따라가겠네.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 삼 층으로 나뉜 탑 안으로 들어갔다. 탑에는 삽, 갈퀴, 물뿌리개 같은 연장들이 벽에 걸려 있었는데, 그게 가구의 전부였다. 둘째 층은 사내가 늘 지내는 거처, 정확히는 잠자는 곳이었다. 변변찮은 살림살이 몇 가지가 있었다—침대 하나, 탁자 하나, 의자 둘, 돌 항아리 하나—그리고 천장에 매달린 마른 풀 몇 다발이 있었는데, 백작은 그것이 스위트피임을 알아보았다. 사내는 그 씨를 받아 두고 있었다. 마치 식물원의 수석 식물학자라도 되는 양 정성껏 이름표까지 붙여 두었다.\n\n\"전신 기술을 익히려면 공부를 많이 해야 하나?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다.\n\n\"공부야 오래 걸리지 않습니다. 견습 시절이 그렇게 지루했지요.\"\n\n\"보수는 얼마나 받나?\"\n\n\"천 프랑입니다, 선생.\"\n\n\"별것 아니군.\"\n\n\"네, 하지만 보시다시피 거처는 마련해 주니까요.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 방을 둘러보았다. 두 사람은 셋째 층으로 올라갔다. 전신실이었다. 몬테크리스토는 기계를 작동시키는 두 개의 쇠손잡이를 번갈아 살펴보았다. \"퍽 흥미롭군.\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 평생 이 일을 하려면 무척 지루하겠어.\"\n\n\"네. 처음에는 이걸 들여다보느라 목이 뻣뻣해졌지만, 한 해가 지나니 익숙해지더군요. 게다가 쉬는 시간도 있고, 휴일도 있고요.\"\n\n\"휴일이?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"언제?\"\n\n\"안개가 낄 때요.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇겠군.\"\n\n\"그게 제겐 정말 휴일이지요. 정원에 나가서 씨를 심고, 가지를 치고, 다듬고, 하루 종일 벌레를 잡습니다.\"\n\n\"여기 온 지는 얼마나 됐나?\"\n\n\"십 년에다, 견습으로 오 년을 더하면 십오 년입니다.\"\n\n\"나이는——\"\n\n\"쉰다섯입니다.\"\n\n\"연금을 받으려면 얼마나 더 일해야 하나?\"\n\n\"아, 선생, 이십오 년이지요.\"\n\n\"연금은 얼마나 되고?\"\n\n\"백 에퀴입니다.\"\n\n\"가엾은 인생이로군!\" 몬테크리스토가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"뭐라고 하셨습니까, 선생?\" 사내가 물었다.\n\n\"무척 흥미롭다고 했네.\"\n\n\"자네가 보여 준 이 모든 게 말이야. 그래서 자네는 이 신호들을 정말 하나도 모른단 말인가?\"\n\n\"전혀 모릅니다.\"\n\n\"알아보려고 해 본 적도 없고?\"\n\n\"한 번도요. 그럴 까닭이 있겠습니까?\"\n\n\"하지만 그래도 자네한테만 보내는 신호가 더러 있을 텐데.\"\n\n\"그야 있지요.\"\n\n\"그건 알아듣고?\"\n\n\"늘 같은 것들이니까요.\"\n\n\"그게 뜻하는 건——\"\n\n\"'새로운 것 없음. 한 시간 쉬어도 좋음.' 아니면 '내일.'이지요.\"\n\n\"꽤 단순하군.\" 백작이 말했다. \"그런데 보게, 자네 상대편 기계가 움직이기 시작한 게 아닌가?\"\n\n\"아, 그렇군요. 고맙습니다, 선생.\"\n\n\"무어라고 하는 건가—알아들을 수 있는 건가?\"\n\n\"네. 준비됐느냐고 묻고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 자네는 어떻게 답하나?\"\n\n\"같은 신호로요. 그 신호는 동시에 오른쪽 상대편에게는 제가 준비됐다고 알리고, 왼쪽 상대편에게는 이번엔 그쪽이 준비하라고 일러 줍니다.\"\n\n\"아주 기발하군.\" 백작이 말했다.\n\n\"보십시오.\" 사내가 자랑스럽게 말했다. \"오 분 뒤면 저쪽이 말을 할 겁니다.\"\n\n'그렇다면 내겐 오 분이 있는 셈이군.' 몬테크리스토가 속으로 생각했다. '필요한 것보다 넉넉한 시간이야.' \"이보게, 선생, 한 가지 물어봐도 되겠나?\"\n\n\"무엇입니까, 선생?\"\n\n\"자네는 정원 가꾸는 걸 좋아하지?\"\n\n\"끔찍이 좋아하지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 이 스무 자짜리 좁은 터 대신, 두 에이커짜리 울타리를 갖게 된다면 기쁘겠나?\"\n\n\"선생, 그렇다면 지상 낙원을 만들어 보일 겁니다.\"\n\n\"천 프랑으로는 살림이 옹색하지?\"\n\n\"꽤 옹색하지요. 그래도 그럭저럭 살아갑니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 하지만 자네 정원은 형편없이 작잖나.\"\n\n\"맞습니다, 정원이 크진 않지요.\"\n\n\"게다가 그나마도 모든 걸 갉아먹는 겨울잠쥐로 가득하고.\"\n\n\"아, 그놈들이 제 재앙이지요.\"\n\n\"한 가지 묻겠네, 만일 운 나쁘게도 오른쪽 상대편이 신호를 보내는 동안 자네가 고개를 돌렸다면——\"\n\n\"못 보겠지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 어떻게 되나?\"\n\n\"신호를 옮길 수 없게 됩니다.\"\n\n\"그러면?\"\n\n\"신호를 옮기지 않으면, 부주의로 벌금을 물게 되지요.\"\n\n\"얼마나?\"\n\n\"백 프랑입니다.\"\n\n\"수입의 십분의 일이라—그것 참 대단한 일이로군.\"\n\n\"아!\" 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"자네한테 그런 일이 있었던 적이 있나?\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"한 번 있었습니다, 선생. 장미나무를 접붙이고 있을 때였지요.\"\n\n\"자, 그렇다면 만일 신호 하나를 바꿔서 다른 걸로 대신 보낸다면 어떻게 되나?\"\n\n\"아, 그건 다른 얘기지요. 쫓겨나고 연금도 잃습니다.\"\n\n\"삼백 프랑을?\"\n\n\"백 에퀴지요, 선생. 그러니 보시다시피 저는 그런 짓을 할 사람이 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"십오 년치 봉급을 준대도 안 한단 말인가? 자, 한번 생각해 볼 만하지 않나?\"\n\n\"만 오천 프랑을요?\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n\"선생, 저를 놀라게 하시는군요.\" \"별소릴 다 하는군.\"\n\n\"선생, 지금 저를 떠보시는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"바로 그렇지. 만 오천 프랑이야, 알아듣겠나?\"\n\n\"선생, 오른쪽 상대편 신호 좀 보게 해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"그 반대일세, 그쪽은 보지 말고 이걸 보게.\"\n\n\"이게 뭡니까?\"\n\n\"뭐냐고? 자네 이 종잇장들을 모르나?\"\n\n\"지폐로군요!\"\n\n\"바로 그렇지. 열다섯 장이 있네.\"\n\n\"이게 누구 겁니까?\"\n\n\"원한다면 자네 거지.\"\n\n\"제 거라고요?\" 사내가 숨이 막힐 듯 외쳤다.\n\n\"그래, 자네 거—자네 소유 말일세.\"\n\n\"선생, 오른쪽 상대편이 신호를 보내고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"보내라고 두게.\"\n\n\"선생, 정신을 빼놓으시는군요. 저 벌금 물게 생겼습니다.\"\n\n\"그래 봐야 백 프랑이지. 보게나, 내 지폐를 받는 게 자네한테 이득이라니까.\"\n\n\"선생, 오른쪽 상대편이 신호를 더 빨리 보냅니다. 안달이 났어요.\"\n\n\"신경 쓰지 말고—이걸 받게.\" 백작이 사내의 손에 지폐 뭉치를 쥐여 주었다. \"자, 이게 다가 아니야.\" 그가 말했다. \"만 오천 프랑으로는 살아갈 수 없을 테니.\"\n\n\"제 자리는 그대로 있지 않습니까.\"\n\n\"아니, 잃게 될 걸세. 자네가 상대편의 전언을 바꿀 테니까.\"\n\n\"오, 선생, 무슨 말씀을 하시는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"장난일세.\"\n\n\"선생, 억지로 시키시지 않는 한——\"\n\n\"내가 자네를 단단히 시킬 수 있을 것 같은데.\" 그러면서 몬테크리스토는 주머니에서 또 다른 뭉치를 꺼냈다. \"여기 만 프랑이 더 있네.\" 그가 말했다. \"이미 자네 주머니에 든 만 오천 프랑과 합치면 이만 오천 프랑이 되지. 오천이면 두 에이커짜리 땅이 딸린 예쁜 작은 집을 살 수 있고, 남은 이만 프랑은 자네에게 해마다 천 프랑을 안겨 줄 걸세.\"\n\n\"두 에이커짜리 땅이 딸린 정원이라!\"\n\n\"게다가 해마다 천 프랑씩.\"\n\n\"오, 하느님!\"\n\n\"자, 받게.\" 몬테크리스토가 지폐를 사내의 손에 억지로 쥐여 주었다.\n\n\"제가 뭘 해야 합니까?\"\n\n\"그리 어려울 것 없네.\"\n\n\"하지만 뭡니까?\"\n\n\"이 신호들을 옮기는 거지.\" 몬테크리스토가 주머니에서 종이 한 장을 꺼냈는데, 거기에는 세 가지 신호가 그려져 있고, 작동시킬 순서를 가리키는 번호가 적혀 있었다.\n\n\"보게, 오래 걸리지 않을 걸세.\"\n\n\"네, 하지만——\"\n\n\"이대로만 하게, 그러면 천도복숭아도, 나머지 다른 것도 다 갖게 될 테니.\"\n\n화살이 적중했다. 사내는 열에 들떠 얼굴이 벌게진 채, 이마에서 굵은 땀방울을 뚝뚝 흘리며 백작이 일러 준 세 가지 신호를 차례차례 작동시켰다. 그 와중에 오른쪽 상대편은 영문을 모른 채 끔찍하게 몸을 비틀어 댔으니, 그는 정원사가 미치기라도 한 줄 알기 시작했다. 한편 왼쪽 상대편은 그 신호들을 충실히 그대로 옮겼고, 신호는 마침내 내무부 장관에게까지 전해졌다.\n\n\"이제 자네는 부자일세.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"네.\" 사내가 대답했다. \"하지만 무슨 대가를 치르고서요!\"\n\n\"잘 듣게, 친구.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"나는 자네가 양심의 가책을 느끼게 할 생각이 없네. 그러니 내 맹세를 믿게. 자네는 아무에게도 해를 끼치지 않았고, 오히려 인류에게 이로운 일을 한 거라네.\"\n\n사내는 지폐를 바라보고, 만져 보고, 세어 보더니, 새파래졌다가 새빨개졌고, 이내 물 한 잔을 마시러 제 방으로 달려갔다. 그러나 물 항아리에 미처 닿기도 전에 마른 풀 다발들 한가운데서 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌다. 새 전보가 장관에게 닿고 오 분 뒤, 드브레는 마차에 말을 매게 하고는 당글라르의 집으로 달려갔다.\n\n\"부군께서 에스파냐 채권을 갖고 계십니까?\" 그가 남작 부인에게 물었다.\n\n\"네, 그럼요! 육백만 프랑어치나 갖고 계신걸요.\"\n\n\"무슨 값에라도 당장 파셔야 합니다.\"\n\n\"왜요?\"\n\n\"돈 카를로스가 부르주에서 달아나 에스파냐로 돌아갔거든요.\"\n\n\"그걸 어떻게 아세요?\" 드브레가 어깨를 으쓱했다.\n\n\"내가 그 소식을 어떻게 듣는지 물으시다니요.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n남작 부인은 두 번 말하길 기다리지 않았다. 그녀는 남편에게로 달려갔고, 남편은 곧장 중개인에게 달려가 값을 가리지 말고 팔라고 지시했다. 당글라르가 판다는 사실이 알려지자 에스파냐 공채는 곧바로 떨어졌다. 당글라르는 오십만 프랑을 잃었지만, 가진 에스파냐 주식을 모조리 처분했다. 그날 저녁 르 메사제에는 다음과 같은 기사가 실렸다.\n\n\"[전신 발신] 국왕 돈 카를로스가 부르주에서 감시자들의 눈을 피해 빠져나가, 카탈루냐 국경을 통해 에스파냐로 돌아갔다. 바르셀로나가 그를 지지하며 봉기했다.\"\n\n그날 저녁 내내 사람들은 주식을 미리 팔아 치운 당글라르의 선견지명과, 그런 일격을 받고도 고작 오십만 프랑밖에 잃지 않은 그 증권 투기꾼의 행운에 대해서만 떠들어 댔다. 주식을 그대로 쥐고 있던 사람들이나, 당글라르의 주식을 사들인 사람들은 스스로 파산했다고 여기며 몹시 괴로운 밤을 보냈다. 이튿날 아침 르 모니퇴르에는 다음과 같은 기사가 실렸다.\n\n\"어제 르 메사제가 보도한 돈 카를로스의 도주와 바르셀로나의 봉기는 아무런 근거가 없는 것이었다. 국왕(돈 카를로스)은 부르주를 떠나지 않았으며, 반도는 깊은 평화를 누리고 있다. 안개 탓에 잘못 해석된 전신 신호 하나가 이 오보의 원인이었다.\"\n\n공채는 떨어지기 전보다 일 퍼센트나 더 올랐다. 손실에다 놓친 이익까지 합치면, 이는 당글라르에게 백만 프랑의 차이가 났다.\n\n\"좋아.\" 당글라르가 희생양이 된 이 기묘한 운명의 역전 소식이 전해졌을 때 자기 집에 와 있던 모렐에게 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"방금 이만 오천 프랑으로 한 가지 발견을 했는데, 십만 프랑을 치르고서라도 알아낼 만한 거였지.\"\n\n\"무엇을 발견하셨는데요?\" 모렐이 물었다.\n\n\"정원사가 복숭아를 갉아먹는 겨울잠쥐를 쫓아내는 법을 방금 발견했다네.\"\n\n# Chapter 62. Ghosts\n\n처음 보기에 오퇴유 저택의 외관은 화려함의 기색이라곤 없었다. 위풍당당한 몬테크리스토 백작의 거처로 점지된 곳이라면 누구나 기대할 법한 그 무엇도 없었다. 그러나 이 소박함은 집주인의 뜻이었으니, 그는 바깥은 무엇 하나 손대지 말라고 단단히 일러두었던 것이다. 화려함은 안에 있었다. 정말이지, 문이 채 열리기도 전에 풍경은 바뀌었다.\n\n베르투치오는 집을 꾸미는 안목에서나, 그 일을 해치우는 속도에서나 자기 자신을 능가했다. 당탱 공작이 루이 14세의 심기를 거스르던 가로수길 전체를 단 하룻밤 사이에 옮겨 놓았다는 이야기가 전해 온다. 베르투치오는 사흘 만에 휑하던 안마당에 포플러를 심고, 집의 이곳저곳을 그늘지게 할 가지 넓게 뻗은 플라타너스를 들였다. 그리고 앞뜰에는 흔히 보이는, 풀에 반쯤 가린 포석 대신 그날 아침에야 깐 잔디밭이 펼쳐져 있었고, 그 위에는 아직 물기가 반짝였다. 나머지 지시는 모두 백작이 직접 내린 것이었다. 그는 직접 도면을 그려 베르투치오에게 건넸고, 나무를 한 그루씩 어디에 심을지, 포석을 대신할 잔디밭의 모양과 넓이는 어떠해야 할지를 표시해 두었다.\n\n이리하여 집은 알아볼 수 없게 변했고, 베르투치오 자신조차 나무 틀에 둘러싸인 그 집을 거의 알아보지 못하겠다고 말했다. 집사는 이왕 손을 댄 김에 정원에도 몇 가지 손질을 더하고 싶었으나, 백작은 정원만은 절대 건드리지 말라고 단단히 못 박았다. 대신 베르투치오는 대기실과 계단, 벽난로 선반마다 꽃을 가득 채우는 것으로 아쉬움을 달랬다.\n\n무엇보다도 집사의 영민함과 주인의 깊은 안목을, 하나가 다른 하나의 뜻을 구현해 내는 솜씨를 여실히 드러낸 것은, 바로 전날 밤만 해도 그토록 음울하고 침침하던 이 집이, 거의 세월의 냄새라 여길 만한 그 병적인 냄새에 절어 있던 이 집이, 단 하루 만에 생기를 띤 모습으로 바뀌어, 주인이 좋아하는 향기를 풍기고, 빛마저 그의 뜻에 맞게 조절되어 있다는 점이었다. 백작이 도착하자, 손이 닿는 곳에 그의 책과 무기가 놓여 있었고, 눈길은 그가 아끼는 그림에 머물렀다. 그가 쓰다듬기를 즐기던 개들이 대기실에서 그를 반겼고, 그가 노래를 사랑하던 새들이 그 음악으로 그를 즐겁게 해 주었다. 그리고 이 집은 긴 잠에서 깨어난 숲속의 잠자는 미녀처럼 살아 숨 쉬고, 노래하고, 꽃피웠다. 오래도록 정들었던, 그래서 떠나야 할 때면 영혼의 한 조각을 두고 가게 되는 그런 집들처럼.\n\n하인들은 멋진 안마당을 따라 흥겹게 오갔다. 주방에 속한 이들은 바로 전날 새로 손본 계단을 미끄러지듯 내려갔다. 마치 늘 그 집에서 살아온 것 같았다. 다른 이들은 마차고를 채웠는데, 덮개를 씌우고 번호를 매긴 마차들은 마치 지난 오십 년 동안 그 자리에 있어 온 듯했다. 마구간에서는 말들이 마부의 말에 울음소리로 답했고, 그 마부들은 어지간한 하인들이 주인을 대하는 것보다 훨씬 더 정중하게 말들에게 말을 걸었다.\n\n서재는 벽을 사이에 두고 양쪽으로 나뉘어 있었고, 이천 권이 넘는 책을 갖추고 있었다. 한쪽은 통째로 소설에 바쳐져 있었는데, 바로 전날 출간된 책마저도 붉은빛과 금빛 장정의 위엄을 온전히 갖춘 채 제자리에 꽂혀 있었다.\n\n집의 반대편에는 서재와 짝을 이루듯 온실이 있었고, 그곳에는 도자기 항아리에 핀 진귀한 꽃들이 장식되어 있었다. 보기에도 향기에도 경탄스러운 그 온실 한가운데에는 당구대가 놓여 있었는데, 마치 지난 한 시간 전쯤 천 위에 공을 놓아둔 채 떠나 버린 사람들의 흔적인 양 보였다.\n\n오직 한 방만은 그 위풍당당한 베르투치오의 손길을 면했다. 큰 계단으로 올라가 뒷계단으로 나오게 되어 있는 이 방 앞을, 하인들은 호기심에 차서 지나갔고, 베르투치오는 공포에 질려 지나갔다.\n\n정각 다섯 시에 백작이 알리를 데리고 오퇴유 저택 앞에 도착했다. 베르투치오는 불안이 뒤섞인 초조함으로 그의 도착을 기다리고 있었다. 그는 칭찬을 바라면서도, 동시에 찌푸린 얼굴을 마주할까 두려워했다. 몬테크리스토는 안마당으로 내려와 흡족하다거나 즐겁다는 기색 한 번 보이지 않은 채 집 안을 두루 돌아보았다. 그러다 닫힌 방의 맞은편에 있는 자신의 침실로 들어서서는, 지난번 방문 때 눈여겨봐 두었던 자단목으로 만든 작은 가구 하나로 다가갔다.\n\n\"이건 장갑을 넣어 두는 것밖에 안 되겠군.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"각하, 한번 열어 보시지요.\" 기뻐 어쩔 줄 모르며 베르투치오가 말했다. \"장갑이 들어 있을 겁니다.\"\n\n다른 곳에서도 백작은 필요한 것을 모두 발견했다. 향수병, 시가, 자질구레한 장식품까지.\n\n\"좋아.\" 그가 말했다. 그러자 베르투치오는 황홀해하며 물러갔다. 이 사람이 주위의 모든 이에게 미치는 영향력은 그토록 크고, 그토록 강하며, 그토록 실재하는 것이었다.\n\n정각 여섯 시에 현관 앞에서 말발굽 소리가 요란하게 들려왔다. 메데아를 타고 도착한, 우리의 스파히 대위였다. \"내가 제일 먼저 온 게 분명하군요.\" 모렐이 외쳤다. \"다들 오기 전에 백작님과 단둘이 잠깐 있고 싶어서 일부러 그랬습니다. 쥘리와 에마뉘엘이 백작님께 할 이야기가 산더미예요. 아, 정말이지 굉장하군요! 그런데 백작님, 댁의 사람들이 제 말을 돌봐 줄까요?\"\n\n\"걱정 말게, 막시밀리앙. 다들 알아서 할 걸세.\"\n\n\"녀석이 보살핌을 받아야 해서요. 어찌나 빨리 달려왔는지 보셨어야 하는데요. 바람 같았답니다!\"\n\n\"그럴 만하지. 오천 프랑이나 한 말 아닌가!\" 몬테크리스토가 아버지가 아들에게 쓰는 듯한 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"그게 아깝습니까?\" 모렐이 환하게 웃으며 물었다.\n\n\"내가? 천만에.\" 백작이 대답했다. \"아니지. 그 말이 좋은 말이 아니었다면 아까웠겠지만 말일세.\"\n\n\"어찌나 좋은 말인지, 프랑스 최고의 기수 중 하나인 샤토르노 씨와 데브레 씨를 따돌렸답니다. 둘 다 장관의 아라비아 말을 탔는데 말이지요. 그 뒤를 바짝 쫓아온 게 당글라르 부인의 말들인데, 늘 시속 육 리외로 달리는 녀석들이지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 자네를 따라온 건가?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다.\n\n\"보십시오, 도착했군요.\" 바로 그 순간, 김이 무럭무럭 나는 말들이 끄는 마차 한 대가, 말을 탄 두 신사를 거느린 채 정문 앞에 다다랐고, 문이 그들 앞에서 열렸다. 마차는 빙 돌아와 계단 앞에 멈춰 섰고, 말 탄 이들이 그 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n데브레는 땅에 발을 딛는 순간 마차 문 앞에 가 있었다. 그가 남작 부인에게 손을 내밀자, 부인은 마차에서 내리며 그 손을 잡았다. 그 잡는 태도에는 몬테크리스토 외에는 누구도 알아채지 못할 어떤 미묘함이 있었다. 그러나 백작의 눈을 벗어나는 것은 아무것도 없었다. 그는 잦은 연습에서 비롯된 듯한 능숙한 손놀림으로, 당글라르 부인의 손에서 장관 비서의 손으로 작은 쪽지 하나가 건네지는 것을 보았다.\n\n아내에 이어 은행가가 내렸는데, 마차가 아니라 무덤에서 나오기라도 한 듯 핼쑥한 얼굴이었다.\n\n당글라르 부인은 몬테크리스토만이 해석할 수 있을 빠르고 캐묻는 듯한 시선을 안마당에, 열주 회랑에, 그리고 집의 정면에 던졌다. 그러고는 가벼운 동요를 억눌렀는데, 만약 그녀가 안색을 잃지 않으려고 애쓰지 않았더라면 그 동요는 얼굴에 드러나고 말았을 것이다. 그녀는 계단을 오르며 모렐에게 말했다.\n\n\"선생님, 제 친구라면 그 말을 파실 의향이 있는지 여쭤보고 싶군요.\"\n\n모렐은 찡그림에 가까운 표정으로 미소 지었고, 곤란한 처지에서 빼내 달라는 듯 몬테크리스토 쪽으로 돌아섰다. 백작은 그 뜻을 알아챘다.\n\n\"아, 부인.\" 그가 말했다. \"어찌하여 제게 그 청을 하지 않으셨습니까?\"\n\n\"백작님께는요.\" 남작 부인이 대답했다. \"무엇 하나 바랄 수가 없답니다. 너무도 확실히 얻게 되니까요. 모렐 씨도 그랬더라면……\"\n\n\"안타깝게도,\" 백작이 대답했다. \"모렐 씨가 그 말을 내줄 수 없다는 건 제가 증인입니다. 그 말을 지키는 데 그의 명예가 걸려 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"어째서요?\"\n\n\"그는 육 개월 안에 메데아를 길들이겠다고 내기를 걸었습니다. 그러니 이제 아시겠지요, 정해진 기한 전에 그 짐승을 처분한다면 내기에 질 뿐만 아니라, 사람들이 그가 겁을 먹었다고 수군댈 겁니다. 용감한 스파히 대위는 그런 위험을 무릅쓸 수 없지요. 어여쁜 여인을 기쁘게 해 드리기 위해서라도 말입니다. 제 생각에 그건 세상에서 가장 신성한 의무 가운데 하나입니다만.\"\n\n\"제 입장을 아시겠지요, 부인.\" 모렐이 몬테크리스토에게 고마운 미소를 보내며 말했다.\n\n\"내가 보기엔,\" 당글라르가 억지웃음으로도 잘 가려지지 않는 거친 어조로 말했다. \"당신은 이미 말이 충분히 많지 않소.\"\n\n당글라르 부인은 이런 종류의 말을 그냥 넘기는 일이 좀처럼 없었지만, 젊은이들이 놀랍게도 그녀는 못 들은 척하며 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 몬테크리스토는 그녀의 보기 드문 겸손에 미소 지었고, 두 개의 거대한 자기 항아리를 보여 주었다. 그 위로는 자연만이 빚어낼 수 있을 크기와 섬세함을 지닌 해초가 휘감겨 있었다. 남작 부인은 깜짝 놀랐다.\n\n\"세상에.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"튈르리 정원의 밤나무 한 그루를 안에 심을 수도 있겠어요! 이렇게 거대한 항아리를 어떻게 만들 수 있었을까요?\"\n\n\"아! 부인.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"고급 자기를 만드는 우리에게 그런 질문을 하시면 안 됩니다. 이건 다른 시대의 작품으로, 흙과 물의 정령들이 빚은 것이니까요.\"\n\n\"어째서요? 대체 어느 시대에 만들어졌단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"저도 모릅니다. 다만 중국의 어느 황제가 특별히 가마를 짓게 했고, 그 가마에서 이런 항아리 열두 개를 차례로 구워 냈다는 이야기만 들었습니다. 그중 둘은 불의 열기로 깨졌고, 나머지 열은 바다 속 삼백 길 깊이에 가라앉혔답니다. 바다는 자기에게 무엇이 요구되는지 알았던지, 그 위에 해초를 덮고, 산호로 둘러싸고, 조개껍데기로 입혀 주었습니다. 그 모든 것은 거의 빛도 들지 않는 그 깊은 곳에서 이백 년 세월에 의해 굳어졌지요. 이 시도를 해 보고자 했던 황제를 혁명이 휩쓸어 가는 바람에, 남은 것이라곤 항아리를 만들었다는 사실과 그것을 바다에 가라앉혔다는 사실을 증명하는 문서뿐이었답니다. 이백 년이 지나 그 문서가 발견되었고, 사람들은 항아리를 끌어올릴 생각을 했지요. 잠수부들이 그 발견에 맞춰 특별히 만든 기계를 타고, 항아리를 던져 넣은 만으로 내려갔습니다. 그러나 열 개 중 셋만 남아 있었고, 나머지는 파도에 깨져 버렸지요. 저는 이 항아리들을 무척 좋아합니다. 어쩌면 흉측하고 끔찍한 괴물들이 그 차갑고 흐릿한 눈을 이 위에 박았을 테고, 또 무수히 많은 작은 물고기들이 적의 추격을 피해 숨어들어 그 안에서 잠들었을 테니까요.\"\n\n그러는 동안 골동품에는 별 관심이 없던 당글라르는, 멋들어진 오렌지나무의 꽃송이를 하나씩 기계적으로 뜯어내고 있었다. 오렌지나무를 다 뜯고 나자, 그는 선인장에 손을 댔다. 그러나 이것은 오렌지나무처럼 쉽게 뜯기지 않았고, 그를 끔찍하게 찔러 댔다. 그는 움찔하더니 꿈에서 깨어난 듯 눈을 비볐다.\n\n\"선생.\" 몬테크리스토가 그에게 말했다. \"그토록 훌륭한 회화를 소장하신 당신께 제 그림을 권하지는 않겠습니다. 그래도 여기 호베마 두 점, 파울 포터 한 점, 미리스 한 점, 헤라르트 도우 두 점, 라파엘로 한 점, 반 다이크 한 점, 수르바란 한 점, 그리고 무리요 두세 점 정도는 볼 만하실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"잠깐.\" 데브레가 말했다. \"이 호베마는 알아보겠는데요.\"\n\n\"아, 정말입니까!\"\n\n\"네, 미술관에 들이려고 제안되었던 작품이지요.\"\n\n\"제 생각엔, 미술관에 한 점도 없지 않습니까?\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"없지요. 그런데도 사들이기를 거부했답니다.\"\n\n\"왜요?\" 샤토르노가 물었다.\n\n\"모르는 척하시는군요. 정부에 돈이 넉넉지 않았기 때문이지요.\"\n\n\"아, 미안하지만.\" 샤토르노가 말했다. \"지난 팔 년 동안 매일같이 그런 이야기를 들어 왔는데도 아직 이해가 안 됩니다.\"\n\n\"머잖아 이해하게 될 겁니다.\" 데브레가 말했다.\n\n\"그럴 것 같지 않은데요.\" 샤토르노가 대답했다.\n\n\"바르톨로메오 카발칸티 소령님, 그리고 안드레아 카발칸티 백작님 드십니다.\" 바티스탱이 알렸다.\n\n장인의 손길에서 막 나온 듯한 검은 새틴 스톡, 희끗한 콧수염, 대담한 눈매, 훈장 셋과 십자장 다섯으로 장식된 소령 군복, 한마디로 노련한 군인의 영락없는 풍모, 이것이 우리가 이미 아는 그 다정한 아버지, 바르톨로메오 카발칸티 소령의 모습이었다. 그 곁에는 온통 새 옷을 차려입은 안드레아 카발칸티 백작이 미소를 띤 채 다가왔다. 우리가 역시 아는 그 효성스러운 아들이었다. 세 젊은이가 함께 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 새로 온 이들이 들어서자, 그들의 시선은 아버지에게서 아들에게로 옮겨 갔고, 그러다 자연스레 아들에게 머물러 그를 품평하기 시작했다.\n\n\"카발칸티라!\" 데브레가 말했다.\n\n\"멋진 이름이군.\" 모렐이 말했다.\n\n\"그래.\" 샤토르노가 말했다. \"저 이탈리아 사람들은 이름은 그럴듯한데 옷은 형편없이 입었어.\"\n\n\"까다롭군, 샤토르노.\" 데브레가 대꾸했다. \"저 옷은 재단도 잘됐고 아주 새 옷인걸.\"\n\n\"바로 그게 흠이라는 거지. 저 신사는 난생처음 옷을 잘 차려입은 사람 같단 말이야.\"\n\n\"저 신사분들은 누구입니까?\" 당글라르가 몬테크리스토에게 물었다.\n\n\"들으셨잖습니까. 카발칸티 부자입니다.\"\n\n\"이름만 들었지, 그 외엔 아무것도 모릅니다.\"\n\n\"아! 그렇군요. 이탈리아 귀족을 모르시는군요. 카발칸티 가문은 모두 군주의 후예입니다.\"\n\n\"재산은 좀 있습니까?\"\n\n\"막대하지요.\"\n\n\"무슨 일을 합니까?\"\n\n\"그걸 다 쓰려고 애쓰는 일이지요. 그저께 들은 바로는, 당신과 무슨 거래가 있는 모양이더군요. 사실 오늘 그분들을 이리로 초대한 것도 당신을 위해서랍니다. 소개해 드리지요.\"\n\n\"그런데 프랑스어를 아주 순수한 억양으로 구사하는 것 같던데요.\" 당글라르가 말했다.\n\n\"아들은 남부의 한 대학에서 교육을 받았답니다. 아마 마르세유 근처였을 겁니다. 아주 열정적인 사람이라는 걸 알게 되실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 주제에 대해서요?\" 당글라르 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"프랑스 여인들 말입니다, 부인. 파리에서 아내를 맞기로 마음을 굳혔답니다.\"\n\n\"별 좋은 생각도 다 하는군.\" 당글라르가 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다. 당글라르 부인은 다른 때 같았으면 한바탕 폭풍을 예고했을 표정으로 남편을 바라보았지만, 이번에도 두 번째로 자신을 억눌렀다.\n\n\"남작께서 오늘은 생각에 잠겨 계시는 듯하군요.\" 몬테크리스토가 그녀에게 말했다. \"혹시 입각이라도 시키려는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"아직은 아닌 듯해요. 그보다는 증권 거래소에서 투기를 하다가 돈을 잃은 게지요.\"\n\n\"빌포르 씨 부부 드십니다.\" 바티스탱이 외쳤다.\n\n두 사람이 들어왔다. 빌포르 씨는 그 자제력에도 불구하고 눈에 띄게 동요했고, 몬테크리스토가 그의 손을 잡았을 때 그 손이 떨리는 것이 느껴졌다.\n\n'역시 여자들만이 속내를 감출 줄 아는군.' 몬테크리스토는 속으로 중얼거리며, 검사에게 미소 지으며 그의 아내를 끌어안는 당글라르 부인을 흘끗 보았다.\n\n잠시 후, 백작은 그때까지 집의 반대편에서 일을 보고 있던 베르투치오가 옆방으로 슬그머니 들어가는 것을 보았다. 그는 그에게로 갔다.\n\n\"무슨 일인가, 베르투치오?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"각하께서 손님이 몇 분이신지 말씀해 주지 않으셔서요.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇지.\"\n\n\"몇 사람 분으로 차릴까요?\"\n\n\"자네가 세어 보게.\"\n\n\"다들 오신 겁니까, 각하?\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n베르투치오는 빠끔히 열린 문틈으로 안을 들여다보았다. 백작은 그를 지켜보았다. \"맙소사!\" 그가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"무슨 일인가?\" 백작이 말했다.\n\n\"저 여자, 저 여자가!\"\n\n\"누구 말인가?\"\n\n\"흰 드레스에 다이아몬드를 잔뜩 단, 저 금발 여자 말입니다.\"\n\n\"당글라르 부인 말인가?\"\n\n\"이름은 모릅니다. 하지만 저 여자입니다, 나리, 저 여자예요!\"\n\n\"누구 말인가?\"\n\n\"그 정원의 여자입니다! 아이를 밴 채로, 누군가를 기다리며 거닐던 그…… 그 여자……\"\n\n베르투치오는 열린 문 앞에 선 채, 눈이 튀어나올 듯하고 머리카락이 곤두서 있었다.\n\n\"누구를 기다렸다는 건가?\" 베르투치오는 대답 없이, 마치 맥베스가 뱅쿠오를 가리키던 그 몸짓으로 빌포르를 가리켰다.\n\n\"오, 오!\" 그가 마침내 중얼거렸다. \"보이십니까?\"\n\n\"무엇이? 누가?\"\n\n\"저 사람이요!\"\n\"저 사람? 검사 빌포르 씨 말인가? 물론 보이지.\"\n\n\"그럼 제가 그를 죽인 게 아니란 말입니까?\"\n\n\"정말이지 자네 미쳐 가는 것 같군, 착한 베르투치오.\" 백작이 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 그가 죽지 않았단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"그래. 보다시피 죽지 않았네. 자네 나라 사람들이 흔히 하듯 왼쪽 여섯 번째와 일곱 번째 갈비뼈 사이를 찔렀어야 했는데, 분명 더 위나 아래를 찔렀던 게지. 게다가 이 변호사들이란 목숨이 어찌나 질긴지. 아니, 그게 아니라면 자네가 내게 한 이야기에는 진실이라곤 없는 거야. 그건 상상이 빚은 공포, 환상이 만든 꿈이었던 게지. 자네는 복수 생각에 가득 차서 잠이 들었고, 그 생각이 위장을 무겁게 짓눌렀고, 그래서 악몽을 꾼 걸세. 그게 다야. 자, 진정하고 손님 수나 세어 보게. 빌포르 씨 부부, 둘. 당글라르 씨 부부, 넷. 샤토르노 씨, 데브레 씨, 모렐 씨, 일곱. 바르톨로메오 카발칸티 소령, 여덟.\"\n\n\"여덟!\" 베르투치오가 따라 말했다.\n\n\"잠깐! 어지간히 서둘러 가려는군. 손님 한 분을 빠뜨렸네. 왼쪽으로 조금 기대 보게. 자! 안드레아 카발칸티 씨를 보게. 무리요의 '성모'를 바라보고 있는, 검은 외투 차림의 저 젊은이 말일세. 자, 이제 돌아서는군.\"\n\n이번에는 베르투치오가 외마디 소리를 지를 뻔했으나, 몬테크리스토의 눈짓이 그를 침묵하게 했다.\n\n\"베네데토?\" 그가 중얼거렸다. \"운명의 장난이로구나!\"\n\n\"방금 여섯 시 반을 쳤네, 베르투치오.\" 백작이 엄하게 말했다. \"내가 저녁을 그 시각에 들이라고 일렀고, 나는 기다리는 걸 좋아하지 않아.\" 그러고는 손님들에게로 돌아갔다. 베르투치오는 벽에 기댄 채로 간신히 식당까지 다다랐다. 오 분 뒤 응접실 문이 활짝 열렸고, 베르투치오가 나타나 안간힘을 다해 말했다. \"식사 준비가 되었습니다.\"\n\n몬테크리스토 백작은 빌포르 부인에게 팔을 내밀었다. \"빌포르 씨.\" 그가 말했다. \"당글라르 남작 부인을 모셔 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n빌포르가 그 청에 따랐고, 사람들은 식당으로 향했다.\n\n# Chapter 63. The Dinner\n\n식당으로 들어서는 손님들 모두가 한 가지 같은 감정에 사로잡혀 있는 것이 분명했다. 저마다 무슨 묘한 힘에 이끌려 이 집까지 오게 되었는지 자문했지만, 놀라고 또 불안하면서도 그 자리에 빠지고 싶지는 않다는 마음이 들었다. 최근에 일어난 일들, 백작의 고독하고 별난 처지, 그의 막대한, 아니 거의 믿기 어려울 만큼의 재산, 이런 것들은 마땅히 사람들을 신중하게 만들고, 자신들을 맞아 줄 같은 성별의 사람이 하나도 없는 집에 부인들이 발길을 들이지 못하게 막았어야 했다. 그런데도 호기심이 신중함과 예법의 경계를 뛰어넘게 만들기에 충분했다.\n\n그 자리에 모인 모든 이들, 심지어 카발칸티 부자까지도, 한쪽은 뻣뻣하고 다른 한쪽은 무심했음에도, 이 헤아릴 수 없는 인물의 집에 모여 있다는 사실에 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 백작의 권유로 빌포르가 팔을 내밀자 당글라르 부인은 흠칫 놀랐다. 그리고 빌포르는 남작 부인의 팔이 자기 팔에 닿는 순간, 금테 안경 아래의 자기 눈길이 불안하게 흔들리는 것을 느꼈다. 이 모든 것이 백작의 눈을 피해 가지 못했다. 단지 이렇게 사람들이 서로 닿는 것만으로도, 그 광경은 관찰하는 이에게 이미 상당한 흥미를 자아내고 있었다.\n\n빌포르 씨의 오른편에는 당글라르 부인이, 왼편에는 모렐이 앉았다. 백작은 빌포르 부인과 당글라르 사이에 앉았다. 나머지 자리는 카발칸티 부자 사이에 앉은 드브레와, 빌포르 부인과 모렐 사이에 앉은 샤토르노가 채웠다.\n\n식사는 더없이 화려했다. 몬테크리스토는 파리 사람들의 통념을 완전히 뒤엎고, 손님들의 식욕만큼이나 호기심도 채워 주려고 애를 썼다. 그가 차려 놓은 것은 동방의 잔치였으나, 아라비아의 요정들이 마련했을 법한 그런 종류의 잔치였다. 지구의 네 모퉁이에서 구할 수 있는 온갖 진귀한 과일이 중국 도자기 항아리와 일본 단지에 수북이 담겨 있었다. 가장 화려한 깃털을 그대로 두른 진귀한 새들, 묵직한 은쟁반에 펼쳐진 거대한 물고기들, 거기에 군도(群島)와 소아시아, 희망봉에서 나는 온갖 포도주가 있었다. 그 기괴한 모양만으로도 술맛을 한층 더해 주는 듯한 병들 속에서 포도주가 반짝였다. 이 모든 것이, 옛날 아피키우스가 손님들을 흡족하게 했던 진수성찬처럼, 놀란 파리 사람들의 눈앞에 차례로 펼쳐졌다. 그들은 열 사람 저녁상에 천 루이를 쓰는 것이 가능하다는 것을 깨달았지만, 그것은 클레오파트라처럼 진주를 먹거나 로렌초 데 메디치처럼 정제한 황금을 마실 때에나 가능한 일이었다.\n\n몬테크리스토는 모두가 놀라워하는 것을 알아채고는 웃으며 농담을 던지기 시작했다.\n\n\"여러분,\" 그가 말했다. \"어느 정도의 재산에 이르고 나면 인생에서 바랄 수 있는 것은 군더더기뿐이라는 데 동의하실 겁니다. 그리고 부인들께서는, 어느 정도의 지위에 오르고 나면 오직 이상만이 더 높이 고양될 수 있다는 데 동의하실 겁니다. 자, 이 논리를 끝까지 밀고 나가 봅시다. 경이로운 것이란 무엇입니까? 우리가 이해하지 못하는 것이지요. 우리가 진정으로 갈망하는 것은 무엇입니까? 우리가 손에 넣을 수 없는 것입니다. 그러니 이해할 수 없는 것을 보고, 불가능한 것을 손에 넣는 것, 이것이 제 삶의 연구입니다. 저는 두 가지 수단으로 제 소망을 채웁니다. 의지와 돈이지요. 저는 어떤 변덕을 좇는 데서, 당글라르 씨가 새 철도 노선을 추진하는 데서 느끼는 것만큼, 빌포르 씨가 죄인에게 사형을 선고하는 데서, 드브레 씨가 한 왕국을 평정하는 데서, 샤토르노 씨가 여인의 마음을 사는 데서, 그리고 모렐 씨가 아무도 못 타는 말을 길들이는 데서 느끼는 것만큼의 흥미를 느낍니다. 예를 들어, 이 물고기 두 마리를 보십시오. 하나는 상트페테르부르크에서 오십 리외 너머에서 가져온 것이고, 다른 하나는 나폴리에서 오 리외 떨어진 곳에서 가져온 것입니다. 둘이 같은 식탁에 올라 있는 걸 보는 게 재미있지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"그 두 물고기가 무엇입니까?\" 당글라르가 물었다.\n\n\"러시아에서 사신 적이 있는 샤토르노 씨가 한 마리의 이름을 말씀해 주실 테고, 이탈리아인이신 카발칸티 소령이 다른 한 마리의 이름을 말씀해 주실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"이건 제 생각에 철갑상어인 것 같군요.\" 샤토르노가 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 저건 제가 틀리지 않았다면 칠성장어입니다.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다. 자, 당글라르 씨, 이분들께 이것들이 어디서 잡히는지 물어보십시오.\"\n\n\"철갑상어는,\" 샤토르노가 말했다. \"오직 볼가강에서만 잡힙니다.\"\n\n\"그리고,\" 카발칸티가 말했다. \"그만한 크기의 칠성장어는 푸사로 호수에서만 난다는 걸 압니다.\"\n\n\"정확합니다. 하나는 볼가강에서, 다른 하나는 푸사로 호수에서 왔지요.\"\n\n\"그럴 리가요!\" 손님들이 일제히 외쳤다.\n\n\"바로 그것이 저를 즐겁게 하는 겁니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"저는 네로와 같습니다. 불가능한 것을 갈망하는 자(cupitor impossibilium) 말이지요. 바로 그것이 지금 여러분을 즐겁게 하고 있는 겁니다. 여러분께 이토록 진귀해 보이는 이 물고기도, 십중팔구 농어나 연어보다 나을 게 없을 겁니다. 다만 그것을 구하는 일이 불가능해 보였고, 이렇게 여기 있는 것이지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 어떻게 이 물고기들을 프랑스까지 가져올 수 있었습니까?\"\n\n\"오, 그보다 쉬운 일은 없습니다. 물고기마다 통 하나에 담겨 운반되었지요. 하나는 강에서 나는 풀과 수초로, 다른 하나는 골풀과 호숫가 식물로 채운 통이었습니다. 그것들을 일부러 만든 짐수레에 실었더니, 철갑상어는 열이틀을 살았고 칠성장어는 여드레를 살았습니다. 둘 다 제 요리사가 손에 넣을 때까지 살아 있었고, 그는 한 마리는 우유로, 다른 한 마리는 포도주로 죽였습니다. 제 말을 안 믿으시는군요, 당글라르 씨!\"\n\n\"의심하지 않을 수가 없군요.\" 당글라르가 멍청한 미소를 띠며 대답했다.\n\n\"바티스탱,\" 백작이 말했다. \"다른 물고기들을 가져오너라. 다른 통에 담겨 와서 아직 살아 있는 철갑상어와 칠성장어 말이다.\"\n\n당글라르는 어리둥절한 눈을 크게 떴다. 좌중은 손뼉을 쳤다. 하인 넷이 수초로 덮인 통 두 개를 들고 들어왔는데, 그 안에는 식탁에 오른 것과 똑같은 물고기가 한 마리씩 숨 쉬고 있었다.\n\n\"하지만 왜 종류마다 두 마리씩 있는 겁니까?\" 당글라르가 물었다.\n\n\"그저 한 마리가 죽을 수도 있었으니까요.\" 몬테크리스토가 무심하게 대답했다.\n\n\"당신은 정말이지 비범한 분이십니다.\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"철학자들이 부자가 되는 건 멋진 일이라고 말할 만하군요.\"\n\n\"게다가 발상이 있어야지요.\" 당글라르 부인이 덧붙였다.\n\n\"오, 이걸 제 공으로 돌리지는 마십시오, 부인. 이건 로마인들이 하던 일입니다. 그들은 이런 것들을 무척 귀하게 여겼지요. 플리니우스의 기록에 따르면, 그들은 오스티아에서 로마로 노예들을 보냈는데, 그 노예들이 머리에 이고 온 물고기를 '물루스(mulus)'라 불렀고, 묘사로 보아 아마도 그건 금붕어였을 겁니다. 또 그것들을 산 채로 두는 것도 사치로 여겨졌는데, 죽어 가는 모습을 보는 것이 흥미로운 구경거리였기 때문입니다. 죽어 갈 때 그것들은 서너 번 빛깔이 바뀌었고, 스러지는 무지개처럼 프리즘의 온갖 색조를 거쳐 갔으며, 그런 뒤에야 주방으로 보내졌지요. 그 단말마의 고통이 그것들 가치의 일부였습니다. 산 모습을 보지 못하면, 죽은 것은 멸시받았으니까요.\"\n\n\"네,\" 드브레가 말했다. \"하지만 오스티아는 로마에서 겨우 몇 리외밖에 떨어져 있지 않잖습니까.\"\n\n\"맞습니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"하지만 루쿨루스보다 천팔백 년 뒤에 살면서, 그가 했던 것보다 더 나을 게 없다면 그 삶이 무슨 소용이 있겠습니까?\"\n\n카발칸티 부자는 그 커다란 눈을 휘둥그레 떴지만, 아무 말도 하지 않을 만한 분별은 있었다.\n\n\"이 모든 게 참으로 비범하군요.\" 샤토르노가 말했다. \"그래도 솔직히 제가 가장 감탄하는 건, 당신의 분부가 실로 놀랍도록 신속하게 이행된다는 점입니다. 이 집을 산 게 닷새인가 엿새 전이라는 게 사실 아닙니까?\"\n\n\"분명 그보다 더 오래되지는 않았지요.\"\n\n\"그런데 지난주 이후로 완전히 달라진 게 분명합니다. 제 기억이 맞다면, 입구가 다른 쪽에 있었고 안마당은 포장된 채 텅 비어 있었지요. 그런데 오늘은 백 년은 됐을 법한 나무들로 둘러싸인 멋진 잔디밭이 있군요.\"\n\n\"안 될 게 뭐 있겠습니까? 저는 풀과 그늘을 좋아하니까요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"네,\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"전에는 문이 길 쪽으로 나 있었지요. 제가 기적적으로 살아난 그날, 당신이 길에서 저를 집 안으로 데려다 주셨던 게 기억나요.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 부인.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"하지만 저는 대문 너머로 불로뉴 숲이 보이는 입구가 더 마음에 들었습니다.\"\n\n\"나흘 만에 말이지요.\" 모렐이 말했다. \"정말 놀랍습니다!\"\n\n\"정말이지,\" 샤토르노가 말했다. \"낡은 집을 새 집으로 탈바꿈시키다니 거의 기적 같군요. 무척 낡았고 또 음침했으니까요. 이삼 년 전에 생메랑 씨가 이 집을 매물로 내놓았을 때, 어머니를 모시고 보러 왔던 게 기억납니다.\"\n\n\"생메랑 씨라고요?\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"그럼 이 집은 당신이 사기 전에 생메랑 씨 소유였단 말인가요?\"\n\n\"그런 모양이더군요.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다.\n\n\"이 집을 누구한테 사셨는지도 모르신단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다. 제 집사가 그런 일을 다 처리하니까요.\"\n\n\"분명 십 년은 사람이 살지 않았던 집입니다.\" 샤토르노가 말했다. \"덧창은 닫히고 문은 잠긴 데다 안마당엔 잡초가 무성해서, 바라보기만 해도 참 울적했지요. 사실 이 집이 검사장의 장인 소유가 아니었다면, 끔찍한 범죄가 저질러진 저주받은 장소쯤으로 여겼을지도 모릅니다.\"\n\n이제껏 앞에 놓인 진귀한 포도주 서너 잔에 입도 대지 않았던 빌포르가, 여기서 한 잔을 들어 단숨에 비웠다. 몬테크리스토는 잠시 시간이 흐르도록 두었다가 말했다.\n\n\"이상하지만, 남작, 저도 이 집에 처음 왔을 때 똑같은 생각이 들었습니다. 어찌나 음침해 보이던지, 제 집사가 그 일을 직접 떠맡지 않았더라면 결코 사지 않았을 겁니다. 어쩌면 그 친구가 공증인한테 매수당했는지도 모르지요.\"\n\n\"그럴 법한 일입니다.\" 빌포르가 억지로 미소 지으려 애쓰며 더듬더듬 말했다. \"하지만 분명히 말씀드리건대, 저는 그런 거래와는 아무 상관이 없습니다. 이 집은 발랑틴의 혼인 지참금의 일부고, 생메랑 씨가 팔고 싶어 했지요. 일이 년만 더 사람이 살지 않았다면 폐허가 되었을 테니까요.\"\n\n이번에는 모렐의 얼굴이 창백해질 차례였다.\n\n\"무엇보다도 방이 하나 있었지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말을 이었다. \"겉보기엔 아주 수수하고 붉은 다마스크 천이 드리워진 방인데, 왜인지 모르겠지만 제겐 무척 극적으로 보였습니다.\"\n\n\"왜 그렇습니까?\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"왜 극적이라는 거지요?\"\n\n\"본능을 설명할 수 있겠습니까?\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"왠지 슬픔이 숨 쉬는 듯한 장소들이 있지 않습니까? 그 까닭은 알 수 없습니다. 그건 한 줄로 이어진 기억들이지요. 다른 시절, 다른 장소로 우리를 데려가는 어떤 관념인데, 십중팔구 지금 이 시간, 이 장소와는 아무 관련이 없는 것들 말입니다. 그리고 이 방에는 강가드 후작 부인이나 데스데모나의 침실을 강하게 떠올리게 하는 무언가가 있습니다. 자, 저녁 식사도 끝났으니, 그 방을 보여 드리지요. 그런 다음 정원에서 커피를 들도록 합시다. 식사 뒤에는 연극이 있어야지요.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 묻는 듯한 눈길로 손님들을 바라보았다. 빌포르 부인이 일어섰고, 몬테크리스토도 따라 일어섰으며, 나머지 사람들도 그들을 따랐다. 빌포르와 당글라르 부인만은 마치 자리에 뿌리내린 듯 잠시 그대로 있었다. 두 사람은 멍하고 막연한 눈길로 서로에게 물었다.\n\n\"들으셨어요?\" 당글라르 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"가야지요.\" 빌포르가 팔을 내밀며 대답했다.\n\n다른 이들은 호기심에 이끌려 이미 집 곳곳으로 흩어져 있었다. 그 구경이 방 하나에 그치지 않을 것이고, 동시에 몬테크리스토가 궁전처럼 만들어 놓은 건물의 나머지 부분도 볼 수 있으리라 여겼기 때문이다. 저마다 열려 있는 문으로 나갔다. 몬테크리스토는 남아 있는 두 사람을 기다렸다. 그러고는 두 사람이 지나가자 맨 뒤를 따랐다. 그의 얼굴에는 미소가 떠올라 있었는데, 만약 그들이 그 의미를 알아챘다면 곧 들어설 그 방을 구경하는 것보다 훨씬 더 섬뜩했을 미소였다. 그들은 먼저 여러 방을 거쳐 걸었는데, 그중 많은 방이 동방식으로 꾸며져 있었다. 침대 대신 방석과 긴 의자가, 가구 대신 담뱃대가 놓여 있었다. 응접실들은 옛 거장들의 더없이 진귀한 그림들로 장식되어 있었고, 규방들에는 기이한 빛깔과 환상적인 문양, 놀라운 질감의 중국산 휘장이 드리워져 있었다. 마침내 그들은 그 유명한 방에 다다랐다. 특별할 것은 없었으나, 다만 햇빛이 사라졌는데도 불이 켜져 있지 않았고, 다른 방들은 새로 단장된 반면 그 방의 모든 것은 옛날식 그대로였다. 이 두 가지만으로도 방에 음침한 분위기를 자아내기에 충분했다.\n\n\"어머.\" 빌포르 부인이 외쳤다. \"정말 무시무시하네요.\"\n\n당글라르 부인은 몇 마디 하려 했으나 아무도 듣지 못했다. 여러 말이 오갔는데, 그 요지는 그 방에 무언가 불길한 데가 있다는 한결같은 의견이었다.\n\n\"그렇지 않습니까?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다. \"저 크고 투박한 침대를 보십시오. 저토록 음침한 핏빛 휘장이 드리워져 있지요! 그리고 습기에 빛바랜 저 크레용 초상화 두 점. 핏기 없는 입술과 부릅뜬 눈으로 '우리는 보았노라'라고 말하는 것 같지 않습니까?\"\n\n빌포르는 새파랗게 질렸다. 당글라르 부인은 벽난로 가까이 놓인 긴 의자에 주저앉았다.\n\n\"어머,\" 빌포르 부인이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"어쩌면 바로 그 위에서 범죄가 저질러졌을지도 모르는 그 의자에 앉으시다니, 정말 용감하시네요?\"\n\n당글라르 부인이 벌떡 일어났다.\n\n\"그리고,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"이게 다가 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"또 뭐가 있습니까?\" 드브레가 말했다. 그는 당글라르 부인의 동요를 놓치지 않고 있었다.\n\n\"아, 또 뭐가 있다는 겁니까?\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"지금까지 저는 별다른 걸 본 것 같지 않은데요. 어떻게 생각하십니까, 카발칸티 씨?\"\n\n\"아,\" 그가 말했다. \"우리한테는 피사에 우골리노의 탑이 있고, 페라라에 타소의 감옥이 있고, 리미니에 프란체스카와 파올로의 방이 있지요.\"\n\n\"네, 하지만 여러분에게는 이 작은 계단은 없지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 휘장에 가려진 문을 열며 말했다. \"이걸 보시고, 어떻게 생각하시는지 말씀해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"참 음흉하게 생긴 비뚤어진 계단이군요.\" 샤토르노가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"키오스산 포도주가 우울을 자아내는지는 모르겠습니다만, 분명 이 집에서는 모든 게 시커멓게 보이는군요.\" 드브레가 말했다.\n\n발랑틴의 지참금 이야기가 나온 뒤로, 모렐은 줄곧 말이 없고 침울했다.\n\n\"상상해 보십시오.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"어떤 오셀로나 강가드 신부가, 폭풍이 몰아치는 어느 캄캄한 밤에, 신에게는 몰라도 사람의 눈에서는 감추고 싶은 짐을 안고서 이 계단을 한 칸 한 칸 내려가는 모습을 말입니다.\"\n\n당글라르 부인은 빌포르의 팔에 반쯤 기절하며 쓰러졌고, 빌포르는 벽에 몸을 기대지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"아, 부인,\" 드브레가 외쳤다. \"왜 그러십니까? 어쩌면 그렇게 창백하세요!\"\n\n\"무엇 때문인지는 뻔하지요.\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"몬테크리스토 씨가 우리한테 끔찍한 이야기를 들려주고 계시잖아요. 분명 우리를 겁에 질려 죽게 만들 작정이신 거예요.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"정말이지 백작, 부인들을 겁주고 계시는군요.\"\n\n\"무슨 일이십니까?\" 드브레가 당글라르 부인에게 속삭이며 물었다.\n\n\"아무것도 아니에요.\" 그녀가 안간힘을 쓰며 대답했다. \"그저 바람을 좀 쐬고 싶을 뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"정원으로 나가시겠습니까?\" 드브레가 뒤쪽 계단으로 다가서며 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 아니에요.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"차라리 여기 있는 게 낫겠어요.\"\n\n\"정말로 겁이 나십니까, 부인?\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"오, 아니에요, 백작님.\" 당글라르 부인이 말했다. \"다만 당신은 장면들을 떠올리는 솜씨가 어찌나 실감 나는지, 마치 진짜처럼 보여서요.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"다 상상하기 나름이지요. 어째서 이 방을 어느 정숙한 어머니의 방으로 상상하면 안 되겠습니까? 그리고 붉은 휘장이 드리워진 이 침대를, 출산의 여신 루키나가 찾아든 침대로요. 그리고 저 신비로운 계단을, 잠을 깨우지 않으려고 의사와 유모가, 혹은 잠든 아이를 안은 아버지가 지나다니는 통로로 말입니다.\"\n\n이때 당글라르 부인은 그 다정한 그림에 진정되기는커녕, 신음을 토하며 기절해 버렸다.\n\n\"당글라르 부인이 편찮으시군요.\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"마차로 모셔 가는 게 낫겠습니다.\"\n\n\"오, 이런(mon Dieu)!\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"제가 각성제 병을 잊고 있었군요!\"\n\n\"제게 있어요.\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다. 그러고는 백작이 에두아르에게 그 효능을 시험해 본 적이 있는 것과 똑같은 종류의 붉은 액체가 가득 든 병을 몬테크리스토에게 건넸다.\n\n\"아.\" 몬테크리스토가 그녀의 손에서 그것을 받아 들며 말했다.\n\n\"네,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신 조언대로 시험해 보았어요.\"\n\n\"그래서 성공하셨습니까?\"\n\n\"그런 것 같아요.\"\n\n당글라르 부인은 옆방으로 옮겨졌다. 몬테크리스토는 그 붉은 액체를 아주 조금 그녀의 입술에 떨어뜨렸고, 그녀는 의식을 되찾았다.\n\n\"아,\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"정말 끔찍한 꿈이었어요!\"\n\n빌포르는 그것이 꿈이 아니었다는 것을 알리려고 그녀의 손을 꽉 눌렀다. 사람들이 당글라르 씨를 찾았지만, 그는 시적인 발상에 별다른 흥미가 없던 터라 정원으로 나가 카발칸티 소령과 함께 리보르노에서 피렌체에 이르는 철도 계획에 관해 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 몬테크리스토는 절망한 듯한 기색이었다. 그는 당글라르 부인의 팔을 잡고 정원으로 데려갔는데, 거기서 카발칸티 부자 사이에 끼어 커피를 마시고 있는 당글라르를 만났다.\n\n\"정말이지, 부인,\" 그가 말했다. \"제가 부인을 그렇게나 놀라게 했습니까?\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요, 백작님.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"하지만 아시다시피, 마음 상태에 따라 같은 것도 다르게 와닿는 법이잖아요.\" 빌포르가 억지웃음을 지었다.\n\n\"게다가, 아시다시피,\" 그가 말했다. \"관념 하나, 가정 하나로도 충분하지요.\"\n\n\"음,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"믿거나 말거나 좋으실 대로 하십시오만, 제 생각엔 이 집에서 범죄가 저질러진 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"조심하세요.\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"여기 검사장님이 계시잖아요.\"\n\n\"아,\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"그렇다면 그분이 계신 김에 제 신고를 하도록 하지요.\"\n\n\"신고라니요?\" 빌포르가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 증인들 앞에서요.\"\n\n\"오, 이거 아주 흥미롭군요.\" 드브레가 말했다. \"정말로 범죄가 있었다면, 우리가 조사해 봐야지요.\"\n\n\"범죄가 있었습니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"이쪽으로 오시지요, 여러분. 오십시오, 빌포르 씨. 신고가 효력을 가지려면 마땅한 권한을 가진 분 앞에서 해야 하니까요.\"\n\n그러고는 빌포르의 팔을 잡고, 동시에 당글라르 부인의 팔도 자기 팔에 끼운 채, 그늘이 가장 짙은 플라타너스 나무 쪽으로 검사장을 끌고 갔다. 다른 손님들도 모두 뒤따랐다.\n\n\"보십시오.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"바로 여기, 이 자리에서\" 하고 그가 땅을 발로 굴렀다. \"저는 이 오래된 나무들에 기운을 북돋우려고 흙을 파내고 새 거름흙을 넣게 했습니다. 그런데 제 일꾼이 땅을 파다가 상자 하나를, 아니 정확히는 상자의 쇠붙이 부분을 찾아냈는데, 그 한가운데 갓 태어난 아기의 해골이 들어 있었지요.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 당글라르 부인의 팔이 뻣뻣하게 굳고, 빌포르의 팔이 떨리는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"갓 태어난 아기라.\" 드브레가 되뇌었다. \"이거 일이 심각해지는군요!\"\n\n\"거 보십시오.\" 샤토르노가 말했다. \"그러니까 아까 제가 틀린 말을 한 게 아니었군요. 집에도 사람처럼 영혼과 얼굴이 있고, 그 외양이 그 성격의 흔적을 지니고 있다고 한 말 말입니다. 이 집이 음침했던 건 회한에 차 있었기 때문입니다. 회한에 차 있었던 건 범죄를 감추고 있었기 때문이고요.\"\n\n\"누가 그게 범죄라고 했습니까?\" 빌포르가 마지막 안간힘을 다해 물었다.\n\n\"뭐라고요? 정원에 산 아이를 묻는 게 범죄가 아니란 말입니까?\" 몬테크리스토가 외쳤다. \"대체 그런 짓을 뭐라고 부르시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"하지만 누가 그 아이를 산 채로 묻었다고 했습니까?\"\n\n\"죽은 아이라면 왜 거기 묻겠습니까? 이 정원은 한 번도 묘지였던 적이 없는데요.\"\n\n\"이 나라에서는 영아 살해범을 어떻게 처벌합니까?\" 카발칸티 소령이 천연덕스럽게 물었다.\n\n\"오, 그런 자들은 곧바로 목이 잘리지요.\" 당글라르가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 그렇습니까?\" 카발칸티가 말했다.\n\n\"그럴 겁니다. 안 그렇습니까, 빌포르 씨?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다.\n\n\"그렇습니다, 백작.\" 빌포르가 이제는 거의 사람의 것이 아닌 듯한 목소리로 대답했다.\n\n몬테크리스토는 이 장면을 위해 준비한 두 사람이 거의 견디지 못하는 것을 보고, 또 너무 지나치게 몰고 가고 싶지는 않아서 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"자, 여러분, 커피를 좀 들지요. 깜빡 잊고 있었군요.\" 그러고는 손님들을 잔디밭의 식탁으로 다시 데려갔다.\n\n\"정말이지, 백작님,\" 당글라르 부인이 말했다. \"부끄럽지만 솔직히 말씀드리자면, 그 끔찍한 이야기들에 어찌나 마음이 어지러운지, 좀 앉게 해 주셔야겠어요.\" 그러고는 의자에 주저앉았다.\n\n몬테크리스토는 고개를 숙여 인사하고는 빌포르 부인에게 갔다.\n\n\"당글라르 부인께 다시 그 병이 필요한 듯합니다.\" 그가 말했다. 그러나 빌포르 부인이 친구에게 다가가기도 전에, 검사장은 당글라르 부인에게 속삭일 틈을 찾아냈다. \"당신과 이야기를 나눠야겠소.\"\n\n\"내일.\"\n\n\"어디서?\"\n\n\"어디서요?\"\n\"내 사무실에서, 아니면 원한다면 법원에서. 거기가 가장 안전한 곳이오.\"\n\n\"그리로 가지요.\"\n\n이때 빌포르 부인이 다가왔다.\n\n\"고마워요, 친애하는 벗이여.\" 당글라르 부인이 미소 지으려 애쓰며 말했다. \"이제 다 지나갔어요. 한결 나아졌답니다.\"\n\n# Chapter 64. The Beggar\n\n저녁 시간이 흘러갔다. 빌포르 부인은 파리로 돌아가고 싶다는 뜻을 비쳤다. 당글라르 부인은 불안한 마음에도 불구하고 차마 그러지 못하던 참이었다. 아내의 청에 따라 빌포르 씨가 가장 먼저 출발 신호를 보냈다. 그는 당글라르 부인이 자기 아내의 보살핌을 받을 수 있도록 자신의 사륜마차에 한 자리를 권했다. 당글라르 씨로 말하자면, 카발칸티 씨와 흥미로운 대화에 빠져 주위에서 벌어지는 일에는 도무지 관심을 두지 않았다. 몬테크리스토는 빌포르 부인에게 향수병을 청하던 와중에 빌포르가 당글라르 부인에게 다가가는 것을 눈여겨보았고, 두 사람 사이에 오간 모든 이야기를 곧 짐작해 냈다. 비록 그 말이 당글라르 부인조차 거의 알아듣지 못할 만큼 낮은 목소리로 오갔는데도 말이다. 그는 그들의 계획에 반대하지 않고, 모렐과 샤토르노, 데브레는 말을 타고 떠나도록, 부인들은 빌포르 씨의 마차로 떠나도록 내버려 두었다. 카발칸티 소령에게 점점 더 매료된 당글라르는 그에게 자기 마차의 자리를 권했다. 안드레아 카발칸티는 자신의 틸버리가 문 앞에서 기다리는 것을 발견했다. 모든 면에서 영국식 유행을 어설프게 흉내 낸 마부가 발끝으로 서서 커다란 철회색 말을 붙잡고 있었다.\n\n안드레아는 만찬 내내 거의 말을 하지 않았다. 그는 영리한 청년이라, 그토록 쟁쟁한 사람들 앞에서, 게다가 그 가운데에는 눈을 휘둥그렇게 뜨고 바라본 검사까지 있었으니, 행여 어리석은 말을 입에 담을까 두려웠던 것이다. 그러다 당글라르에게 붙들렸는데, 당글라르는 뻣뻣한 목을 한 늙은 소령과 그 겸손한 아들을 재빠르게 훑어보고, 백작의 환대까지 헤아린 끝에, 자기가 지금 상속자의 사교 교육을 마무리하려고 파리에 온 어느 갑부와 어울리고 있다고 마음속으로 단정했다. 그는 소령의 새끼손가락에서 빛나는 커다란 다이아몬드를 형언할 수 없는 기쁨으로 바라보았다. 소령은 신중한 사람답게, 지폐에 무슨 일이라도 생길 경우를 대비해 그것을 즉시 쓸 수 있는 자산으로 바꿔 두었던 것이다. 그러다 만찬 뒤에는 사업을 핑계로 부자에게 그들의 생활 방식을 캐물었다. 한 사람은 해마다 4만 8천 프랑을, 다른 한 사람은 5만 리브르를 당글라르를 통해 받게 된다는 것을 미리 들어 알고 있던 부자는 어찌나 살갑게 굴었던지, 그 고마움을 쏟아부을 대상이 어찌나 필요했던지, 은행가의 하인들과도 기꺼이 악수를 나누었을 정도였다.\n\n무엇보다도 당글라르가 카발칸티에게 품은 존경, 아니 거의 숭배에 가까운 마음을 한층 드높인 일이 하나 있었다. 카발칸티는 \"아무것도 경탄하지 말라\"는 호라티우스의 원칙에 충실하여, 어느 호수에서 최상의 칠성장어가 잡히는지 밝히는 정도로 자신의 식견을 드러내는 데 만족했다. 그러고는 더 말을 보태지 않고 그것을 몇 점 먹었다. 그리하여 당글라르는 이런 진미가 카발칸티의 그 빛나는 후예의 식탁에서는 흔한 것이려니 결론지었다. 아마도 그는 루카에서, 백작이 푸사로 호수에서 칠성장어를, 볼가강에서 철갑상어를 가져오는 것과 똑같은 방법으로, 스위스에서 가져온 송어와 영국에서 보내온 바닷가재를 먹고 살 터였다. 그리하여 그는 카발칸티가 다음과 같은 말을 꺼냈을 때 매우 정중한 태도로 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"내일, 선생, 사업차 찾아뵙는 영광을 누리겠습니다.\"\n\n\"저 또한, 선생,\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"기꺼이 맞이하겠습니다.\"\n\n그러면서 그는, 아들과 동행하는 즐거움을 빼앗는 것이 아니라면, 자기 마차로 카발칸티를 프랭스 호텔까지 모셔다드리겠노라 청했다. 이에 카발칸티는, 얼마 전부터 아들이 자기와 따로 살며 제 말과 마차를 두고 있고, 함께 오지 않았으니 따로 떠나는 것도 어렵지 않을 거라고 답했다. 그리하여 소령은 당글라르 곁에 자리를 잡았다. 당글라르는 이 사내를 지배하는 질서와 절약의 관념에 점점 더 매료되었다. 더구나 아들에게 한 해 6만 프랑을 줄 수 있는 사람이라면 응당 50만 내지 60만 리브르의 재산을 가졌으리라 짐작되었다.\n\n안드레아로 말하자면, 자신을 과시할 요량으로 마부를 꾸짖기 시작했다. 마부가 틸버리를 집의 현관 계단 앞으로 가져오지 않고 바깥문 쪽에 세워 두는 바람에, 거기까지 서른 걸음이나 걸어가는 수고를 끼쳤다는 것이었다. 마부는 공손히 그 꾸지람을 듣고서, 왼손으로 안달하는 말의 재갈을 잡고 오른손으로 안드레아에게 고삐를 내밀었다. 안드레아는 그 고삐를 받아 들고는 윤이 나는 장화를 발판에 가볍게 올렸다.\n\n그 순간 누군가의 손이 그의 어깨를 건드렸다. 청년은 당글라르나 몬테크리스토가 무언가 할 말을 잊고서 막 출발하려던 차에 되돌아온 것이려니 여기며 몸을 돌렸다. 그러나 그 둘 가운데 누구도 아니라, 낯선 얼굴 하나가 보일 뿐이었다. 햇볕에 그을린 데다 수염으로 둘러싸인 얼굴, 석류석처럼 번득이는 눈, 그리고 입가에는 늑대나 자칼의 것처럼 뾰족하고 날카로운 새하얀 이를 고스란히 드러낸 미소가 걸려 있었다. 붉은 손수건이 그 잿빛 머리를 동여매고 있었다. 찢기고 더러운 옷가지가 그 크고 앙상한 사지를 덮고 있었는데, 마치 해골의 뼈처럼 걸을 때마다 달그락거릴 것만 같았다. 그가 청년의 어깨를 짚고 있던 손, 안드레아가 가장 먼저 본 그 손은 거인의 것처럼 커 보였다.\n\n청년은 틸버리의 등불 빛으로 그 얼굴을 알아본 것일까, 아니면 그저 질문을 던지는 그자의 끔찍한 몰골에 놀란 것일까? 우리로서는 알 수 없다. 다만 그가 몸서리치며 별안간 뒷걸음질 쳤다는 사실만 전할 따름이다.\n\n\"나한테 뭘 원하는 거요?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"이보게, 친구, 방해해서 미안하네,\" 붉은 손수건을 두른 사내가 말했다. \"하지만 자네와 이야기를 좀 나누고 싶어서 말이야.\"\n\n\"밤에 구걸할 권리는 없소.\" 마부가 거추장스러운 침입자로부터 주인을 떼어 놓으려 애쓰며 말했다.\n\n\"구걸하는 게 아니다, 이 잘난 친구야.\" 낯선 사내가 하인을 향해 말했다. 그 눈빛이 어찌나 비웃는 듯하고 미소가 어찌나 섬뜩했던지 하인은 물러서고 말았다. \"그저 자네 주인한테 두세 마디 하려는 것뿐이야. 보름쯤 전에 나한테 일을 하나 맡긴 분이거든.\"\n\n\"이리 와.\" 안드레아가, 하인이 자신의 동요를 알아채지 못할 만큼 충분한 침착함을 짜내며 말했다. \"원하는 게 뭐요? 빨리 말해 보시오, 친구.\"\n\n사내가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"내가 바라는 건, 파리까지 걸어 돌아가는 수고를 덜어 줬으면 하는 거야. 어찌나 지쳤는지, 자네만큼 잘 차려진 저녁도 못 먹은 터라 서 있기도 힘들거든.\"\n\n청년은 이 기묘한 허물없음에 몸서리쳤다.\n\n\"말해 보시오.\" 그가 말했다. \"원하는 게 뭔지 말해 보란 말이오.\"\n\n\"그러니까, 자네의 그 멋진 마차에 나를 태워 데려다 줬으면 한다는 거야.\" 안드레아의 얼굴이 창백해졌으나, 그는 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"그래.\" 사내가 주머니에 두 손을 찔러 넣고 뻔뻔스럽게 청년을 바라보며 말했다. \"이런 변덕이 머릿속에 들어차 버렸지 뭐냐. 무슨 말인지 알겠나, 베네데토 도련님?\"\n\n이 이름에, 청년은 분명 잠시 생각에 잠겼다. 그가 마부에게로 다가가며 말했던 것이다.\n\n\"이 사람 말이 맞아. 내가 정말로 이 사람한테 일을 하나 맡겼는데, 그 결과를 들어야겠어. 자네는 검문소까지 걸어가서 거기서 합승 마차를 잡게. 너무 늦지 않도록 말이야.\"\n\n놀란 마부가 물러갔다.\n\n\"적어도 그늘진 데까지는 가게 해 주시오.\" 안드레아가 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그쯤이야, 근사한 데로 데려다주지.\" 손수건을 두른 사내가 말했다. 그러고는 말의 재갈을 잡고 틸버리를 끌고 갔다. 그곳은 분명, 안드레아가 그에게 베푸는 영광을 그 누구도 목격할 수 없을 만한 자리였다.\n\n\"내가 자네 멋진 마차를 타는 영예를 바란다고는 생각지 말게.\" 그가 말했다. \"오, 천만에, 그저 지친 데다, 자네와 의논할 자그마한 일이 좀 있어서 그러는 거야.\"\n\n\"자, 올라타시오.\" 청년이 말했다. 이 장면이 대낮에 벌어지지 않은 것은 안타까운 일이었다. 이 악당이 젊고 우아한 틸버리 운전자 곁의 방석 위로 육중하게 몸을 던지는 광경은 꽤나 볼만했을 테니 말이다. 안드레아는 마을의 마지막 집을 지날 때까지 일행에게 한마디도 하지 않았고, 사내는 이토록 안락한 탈것으로 여행하게 된 것이 흡족하기 그지없다는 듯 만족스럽게 미소 지었다. 오퇴유를 벗어나자 안드레아는 보는 이도 듣는 이도 없음을 확인하려고 주위를 둘러보았다. 그러고는 말을 세우고 사내 앞에서 두 팔을 가로지른 채 물었다.\n\n\"자, 무슨 일로 내 평온을 어지럽히러 온 건지 말해 보시오.\"\n\n\"자네야말로 왜 나를 속였는지 물어봐도 되겠나?\"\n\n\"내가 어떻게 당신을 속였다는 거요?\"\n\n\"'어떻게'라니? 우리가 바르 다리에서 헤어질 때, 자넨 피에몬테와 토스카나를 거쳐 여행하겠다고 했지. 그런데 그러기는커녕 파리로 왔지 않나.\"\n\n\"그게 당신을 언짢게 할 일이오?\"\n\n\"그렇지 않아. 오히려 내 목적에 들어맞을 것 같은걸.\"\n\n\"그러니까,\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"나를 두고 한몫 잡아 보겠다는 거요?\"\n\n\"말도 참 그럴듯하게 하는구나!\"\n\n\"미리 경고하는데, 카드루스 양반, 당신이 잘못 짚었소.\"\n\n\"자, 자, 화내지 마, 이 친구야. 불행이 어떤 건지는 자네도 잘 알 텐데. 불행은 사람을 시샘하게 만드는 법이지. 난 자네가 토스카나나 피에몬테에서 짐꾼이나 길잡이 노릇을 하며 입에 풀칠이나 하는 줄 알았어. 그래서 내 자식이라도 되는 양 진심으로 가엾게 여겼지. 알다시피 난 늘 자넬 내 자식이라 불렀잖나.\"\n\n\"좋아, 좋아, 그래서?\"\n\n\"진득하게, 진득하게!\"\n\n\"진득하게 듣고 있으니, 어서 계속하시오.\"\n\n\"그러던 어느 날 갑자기, 마부에 틸버리에 새 옷까지 멋들어지게 차려입고 검문소를 지나는 자네를 보게 된 거야. 광맥이라도 발견했거나, 아니면 주식 중개인이라도 됐겠지.\"\n\n\"그래서, 당신 입으로 인정하듯, 시샘이 난다는 거요?\"\n\n\"아니, 난 기쁘다네. 어찌나 기쁜지 축하를 해 주고 싶을 정도였지. 다만 내 행색이 영 변변치 못해서, 자네를 곤란하게 하지 않으려고 때를 골랐던 거야.\"\n\n\"그래, 참 근사한 때를 골랐구려!\" 안드레아가 소리쳤다. \"내 하인 앞에서 나한테 말을 걸다니.\"\n\n\"낸들 어쩌겠나, 이 친구야? 잡을 수 있을 때 말을 걸 수밖에. 자넨 빠른 말에, 가벼운 틸버리에, 본래 뱀장어처럼 미끄러운 사람이잖나. 오늘 밤 자넬 놓쳤다면, 다시는 기회가 없었을지도 모르지.\"\n\n\"보다시피, 난 몸을 숨기지 않소.\"\n\n\"운이 좋군. 나도 그렇게 말할 수 있다면 좋으련만, 난 몸을 숨기거든. 게다가 자네가 날 못 알아볼까 봐 걱정했는데, 알아보더군.\" 카드루스가 그 불쾌한 미소를 지으며 덧붙였다. \"참 예의 바른 일이었어.\"\n\n\"자,\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"원하는 게 뭐요?\"\n\n\"베네데토, 내 오랜 친구, 나한테 살갑게 말하지 않는구나. 그건 옳지 않아. 조심하게, 안 그러면 내가 성가신 사람이 될 수도 있으니.\"\n\n이 위협에 청년의 격정이 사그라들었다. 그는 다시 말을 몰아 빠른 걸음으로 나아가게 했다.\n\n\"방금 자네 입으로 말했듯, 나 같은 오랜 친구한테 그렇게 말하면 못쓰지, 카드루스. 자넨 마르세유 태생이고, 난——\"\n\n\"그러니까 자넨 이제 자기가 뭔지 알기는 하나?\"\n\n\"아니, 하지만 난 코르시카에서 자랐소. 당신은 늙고 고집스럽고, 난 젊고 제멋대로요. 우리 같은 사람들 사이에 위협은 어울리지 않소. 모든 건 사이좋게 풀어야지. 운명이 당신한테는 눈살을 찌푸리고 나한테는 너그러웠던 게 내 탓이오?\"\n\n\"그래, 운명이 자네한테는 너그러웠다? 그럼 자네 틸버리며, 마부며, 옷가지가 다 빌린 게 아니라는 거지? 좋아, 그렇다면 더 잘됐군.\" 카드루스가 탐욕으로 눈을 번득이며 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그건 나한테 말을 걸기 전에 이미 잘 알고 있었잖소.\" 안드레아가 점점 더 흥분하며 말했다. \"내가 당신처럼 머리에 손수건을 두르고, 등에는 누더기를 걸치고, 발에는 다 해진 신발을 신고 있었다면, 당신은 날 알아보지도 않았을 거요.\"\n\n\"그건 날 오해하는 거다, 이 친구야. 이제 자넬 찾았으니, 자네 마음씨가 얼마나 고운지 잘 아는 마당에, 나라고 누구 못지않게 잘 차려입지 못할 까닭이 없지. 자네한테 외투가 두 벌 있으면 그중 한 벌을 내게 줄 테고. 자네가 굶주릴 때, 난 내 수프와 콩을 자네와 나눠 먹곤 했잖나.\"\n\n\"맞소.\" 안드레아가 말했다.\n\n\"자네 그 식욕이란 게 어찌나 대단했던지! 지금도 여전한가?\"\n\n\"오, 그럼요.\" 안드레아가 웃으며 대답했다.\n\n\"방금 자네가 나온 그 저택의 공작과는 어쩌다 함께 식사를 하게 된 거지?\"\n\n\"공작이 아니오. 그저 백작이지.\"\n\n\"백작이라, 게다가 돈 많은 백작이렷다, 응?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 하지만 당신은 그 사람한테 무슨 볼일도 두지 않는 게 좋을 거요. 그리 너그러운 양반이 아니거든.\"\n\n\"오, 안심하게! 자네의 그 백작한테 무슨 꿍꿍이도 없으니, 그자는 통째로 자네 차지로 두지. 다만,\" 카드루스가 또다시 아까의 그 못마땅한 표정으로 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그 대가는 치러야겠어. 무슨 말인지 알겠지?\"\n\n\"좋소, 원하는 게 뭐요?\"\n\n\"내 생각엔, 한 달에 백 프랑이면——\"\n\"그러면?\"\n\n\"먹고살 수 있을 것 같은데——\"\n\n\"백 프랑으로!\"\n\n\"자, 내 말을 알아듣겠지. 다만 거기에——\"\n\n\"거기에?\"\n\n\"백오십 프랑이면 아주 흡족하겠어.\"\n\n\"여기 이백 프랑이 있소.\" 안드레아가 말했다. 그러고는 카드루스의 손에 금화 열 닢을 쥐여 주었다.\n\n\"좋아!\" 카드루스가 말했다.\n\n\"매달 초하루에 집사한테 가면, 같은 액수를 받을 거요.\"\n\n\"이런, 또 날 모욕하는군.\"\n\n\"어째서?\"\n\n\"자네하고만 거래하고 싶은데, 하인들한테 가게 만들다니.\"\n\n\"좋소, 정 그렇다면. 그럼 나한테서 직접 받으시오. 적어도 내가 내 수입을 받는 한, 당신도 당신 몫을 받게 될 거요.\"\n\n\"자, 자. 난 늘 자네가 멋진 친구라고 말했지. 자네 같은 사람한테 행운이 찾아오는 건 축복이야. 그런데 그 자초지종을 다 말해 주겠나?\"\n\n\"왜 알고 싶은 거요?\" 카발칸티가 물었다.\n\n\"뭐? 또 날 거역하는 거냐?\"\n\n\"아니오. 실은 내가 아버지를 찾았소.\"\n\n\"뭐? 진짜 아버지를?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 그자가 돈을 주는 한은——\"\n\n\"그러면 그를 떠받들고 믿어야지, 그게 맞아. 이름이 뭔가?\"\n\n\"카발칸티 소령이오.\"\n\n\"그자가 자네한테 만족하던가?\"\n\n\"지금까지는 내가 그 사람 목적에 맞아떨어진 듯하오.\"\n\n\"그런데 누가 자네한테 그런 아버지를 찾아 줬지?\"\n\n\"몬테크리스토 백작이오.\"\n\n\"방금 자네가 나온 그 저택의 사람 말인가?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"돈궤를 쥐고 있는 사람이라니, 자네가 나한테도 그자의 할아버지 자리라도 하나 찾아 줬으면 좋겠군!\"\n\n\"좋소, 그 사람한테 당신 얘기를 해 두지. 그건 그렇고, 당신은 앞으로 뭘 할 작정이오?\"\n\n\"내가?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 당신 말이오.\"\n\"날 그렇게 걱정해 주다니 참 고맙구나.\"\n\n\"당신이 내 일에 관심을 가지니, 이제 내가 당신한테 몇 가지 물어볼 차례인 것 같소만.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇지. 그럼, 어디 점잖은 집에 방을 하나 빌려서, 번듯한 외투를 걸치고, 매일 면도를 하고, 카페에 가서 신문이나 읽을 거야. 그러고는 저녁이면 극장에 가야지. 은퇴한 빵집 주인쯤으로 보이겠지. 내가 원하는 게 그거야.\"\n\n\"좋소, 그 계획대로만 하고 진득하게 처신한다면, 더없이 좋겠소.\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각하나, 보쉬에 선생? 그럼 자넨——자넨 뭐가 될 텐가? 프랑스의 귀족?\"\n\n\"아,\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"누가 알겠소?\"\n\n\"카발칸티 소령은 이미 귀족인지도 모르지. 하지만 세습 작위는 폐지됐으니 말이야.\"\n\n\"정치 얘기는 그만둡시다, 카드루스. 자, 이제 원하는 건 다 손에 넣었고, 우리도 서로 뜻이 통했으니, 틸버리에서 내려서 사라져 주시오.\"\n\n\"천만에, 이 친구야.\"\n\n\"뭐요? 천만에라니?\"\n\n\"아니, 잠깐만 생각해 보게. 이렇게 머리에 붉은 손수건을 두르고, 신발이라고 할 것도 없는 데다, 신분증도 없고, 주머니에는 금화 나폴레옹 열 닢에다, 거기에 본래 있던 것까지 합치면 대략 이백 프랑이 들었으니——나야 검문소에서 영락없이 붙들리고 말 거야. 그러면 변명을 하느라 자네가 준 돈이라고 말할 테고, 그러면 조사가 시작될 테지. 그러다 보면 내가 정당한 신고도 없이 툴롱을 떠난 게 들통날 테고, 결국 난 지중해 연안으로 도로 호송될 거야. 그러면 난 그저 죄수 106호가 되고 마는 거지. 은퇴한 빵집 주인을 닮아 보겠다는 내 꿈이여, 잘 가거라! 안 돼, 안 돼, 이 친구야. 난 수도에서 떳떳하게 지내는 편이 좋다네.\"\n\n안드레아가 인상을 찌푸렸다. 분명, 그 자신도 인정했듯, 카발칸티 소령의 양아들은 제멋대로인 위인이었다. 그는 잠시 말을 세우고 주위를 재빠르게 훑어보았다. 그러더니 그의 손이 곧장 주머니로 떨어져, 안에서 권총을 만지작거리기 시작했다. 그러나 그사이, 한순간도 일행에게서 눈을 떼지 않던 카드루스는 손을 등 뒤로 돌려, 만일에 대비해 늘 지니고 다니는 기다란 스페인식 칼을 폈다. 보다시피, 이 두 친구는 서로 어울리는 데다 서로의 속을 훤히 꿰뚫고 있었다. 안드레아의 손은 아무 해 끼치지 않고 주머니에서 빠져나와, 붉은 콧수염으로 올라가더니 한동안 그것을 만지작거렸다.\n\n\"착한 카드루스,\" 그가 말했다. \"당신은 얼마나 행복할까.\"\n\n\"최선을 다해 보지.\" 가르 다리 여인숙 주인이 칼을 도로 접으며 말했다.\n\n\"자, 그럼 파리로 갑시다. 그런데 의심을 사지 않고 검문소를 어떻게 지날 셈이오? 내가 보기엔 걸어가는 것보다 마차를 타는 게 더 위험할 것 같은데.\"\n\n\"기다려 보게.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"어디 두고 보자고.\" 그러고는 마부가 틸버리에 두고 간 큼직한 깃의 외투를 집어 등에 걸쳤다. 이어 카발칸티의 모자를 벗겨 자기 머리에 얹고는, 마침내 주인이 직접 마차를 모는 하인의 무심한 자세를 취했다.\n\n\"그런데 말이오,\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"난 그럼 맨머리로 있어야 하오?\"\n\n\"흥,\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"바람이 어찌나 거센지, 자네 모자가 날아가 버렸다고 해도 다들 곧이듣겠어.\"\n\n\"자, 자, 그만하면 됐소.\" 카발칸티가 말했다.\n\n\"뭘 기다리는 건가?\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"설마 나 때문은 아니겠지.\"\n\n\"쉿.\" 안드레아가 말했다. 두 사람은 무사히 검문소를 지났다. 첫 번째 교차로에서 안드레아는 말을 세웠고, 카드루스가 훌쩍 뛰어내렸다.\n\n\"이봐!\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"내 하인의 외투랑 내 모자는?\"\n\n\"아,\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"설마 나더러 감기에 걸릴 위험을 무릅쓰라는 건 아니겠지?\"\n\n\"하지만 난 어쩌란 말이오?\"\n\n\"자네? 오, 자넨 젊지 않나. 난 슬슬 늙어 가는 몸이고. 또 보세, 베네데토.\" 그러고는 어느 안마당으로 뛰어들어 자취를 감추었다.\n\n\"아아,\" 안드레아가 한숨지으며 말했다. \"이 세상에서 완전히 행복할 수는 없는 노릇이로군!\"\n\n# Chapter 65. A Conjugal Scene\n\n루이 15세 광장에서 세 젊은이는 헤어졌다. 다시 말해, 모렐은 큰길 쪽으로, 샤토르노는 레볼뤼시옹 다리 쪽으로, 드브레는 강변 쪽으로 갔다. 모렐과 샤토르노는 십중팔구 의회 회랑의 다듬어진 연설이나 리슐리외 거리 극장의 잘 쓰인 희곡에서 흔히 말하듯 자신들의 '가정의 화롯가'로 돌아갔을 것이다. 그러나 드브레는 그렇지 않았다. 그는 루브르의 작은 문에 이르자 왼쪽으로 꺾어 카루젤 광장을 가로질러 달렸고, 생로크 거리를 지나 미쇼디에르 거리에서 빠져나와, 마침 빌포르의 사륜마차가 빌포르 부부를 포부르생토노레에 내려준 뒤 남작 부인을 자기 집에 내려주려고 멈추는 바로 그 순간 당글라르의 집 문 앞에 도착했다.\n\n이 집에 익숙한 사람다운 태도로 드브레는 먼저 안마당으로 들어가 하인에게 말고삐를 던지고는 다시 문가로 돌아와 당글라르 부인을 맞이했다. 그는 부인에게 팔을 내밀어 그녀의 방까지 데려다주었다. 대문이 닫히고 안마당에 드브레와 남작 부인 단둘만 남자, 그가 물었다.\n\n\"무슨 일이 있었던 거요, 에르민? 어째서 백작이 들려준 그 이야기, 아니 그 우화 같은 것에 그렇게 마음이 흔들렸소?\"\n\n\"오늘 저녁 내내 기분이 너무 끔찍했거든요, 여보.\" 남작 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"아니오, 에르민.\" 드브레가 대답했다. \"그런 말로는 날 못 속여요. 오히려 당신은 백작의 집에 도착했을 때만 해도 기분이 더없이 좋았소. 당글라르 씨가 불쾌하게 굴긴 했지만, 당신이 그 사람 심사 따위 얼마나 마음에 두지 않는지 내가 잘 알지. 누군가 당신을 거슬리게 한 거요. 누구도 당신을 괴롭히게 두지 않겠소.\"\n\n\"오해예요, 뤼시앵. 정말이에요.\" 당글라르 부인이 대답했다. \"내가 말한 게 정말 사실이에요. 거기에 당신이 알아챈 그 언짢은 기분이 더해졌을 뿐, 그건 굳이 입에 올릴 가치도 없다고 생각했어요.\"\n\n당글라르 부인이 여자들이 흔히 스스로도 설명하지 못하는 그 신경질적 흥분에 시달리고 있거나, 아니면 드브레가 짐작한 대로 누구에게도 털어놓고 싶지 않은 어떤 은밀한 동요를 겪은 것이 분명했다. 앞의 증상이 여자라는 존재에 따라붙는 천성적 형벌 중 하나임을 아는 남자였기에, 그는 그 자리에서 캐묻기를 밀어붙이지 않고 다시 물어보거나 부인 스스로 고백할 더 적절한 기회를 기다렸다.\n\n방문 앞에서 남작 부인은 자신의 측근 하녀인 코르넬리 양과 마주쳤다.\n\n\"내 딸은 무얼 하고 있느냐?\" 당글라르 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"저녁 내내 연습을 하시다가 잠자리에 드셨습니다.\" 코르넬리 양이 대답했다.\n\n\"그런데 피아노 소리가 들리는 것 같은데.\"\n\n\"당글라르 아가씨가 잠자리에 드신 동안 루이즈 다르미이 양이 치고 계십니다.\"\n\n\"좋아.\" 당글라르 부인이 말했다. \"이리 와서 옷 벗는 걸 좀 도와다오.\"\n\n그들은 침실로 들어갔다. 드브레는 커다란 긴 의자에 몸을 뉘었고, 당글라르 부인은 코르넬리 양과 함께 화장실로 들어갔다.\n\n\"내 사랑 뤼시앵 씨.\" 당글라르 부인이 문 너머로 말했다. \"당신은 늘 외제니가 당신한테 한마디도 건네지 않는다고 불평하지요.\"\n\n\"부인.\" 뤼시앵이, 자신을 이 집의 친구로 알아보고 쓰다듬어 주기를 기대하며 다가오는 작은 개와 장난을 치면서 말했다. \"그런 불평을 하는 게 나만은 아닐 거요. 모르세르가 자기 약혼녀한테서도 말 한마디 끌어낼 수가 없다고 하는 걸 들은 것 같으니까.\"\n\n\"맞아요.\" 당글라르 부인이 말했다. \"하지만 다 지나갈 거예요. 언젠가는 그 애가 당신 집무실로 들어서는 걸 보게 될 테니까요.\"\n\n\"내 집무실로?\"\n\n\"적어도 장관 집무실로요.\"\n\n\"어째서요!\"\n\n\"오페라극장 계약을 청하러요. 정말이지, 그런 음악에 대한 열중은 본 적이 없어요. 상류 사회 아가씨로서는 우스꽝스럽기 짝이 없지요.\"\n\n드브레가 미소 지었다. \"그렇다면,\" 그가 말했다. \"당신과 남작의 승낙을 받아 그 아이를 보내요. 우리가 계약을 맺어 보도록 하지요. 비록 그 아이만 한 재능에 보수를 주기엔 우리 형편이 무척 빈약하지만 말이오.\"\n\n\"가 보거라, 코르넬리.\" 당글라르 부인이 말했다. \"이제 너는 필요 없다.\"\n\n코르넬리가 시키는 대로 물러갔고, 다음 순간 당글라르 부인이 매혹적인 헐렁한 실내복 차림으로 방에서 나와 드브레 곁에 바싹 다가앉았다. 그러고는 생각에 잠긴 채 작은 스패니얼을 쓰다듬기 시작했다. 뤼시앵은 잠시 말없이 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"자, 에르민.\" 그가 잠시 후 말했다. \"솔직하게 대답해 봐요. 뭔가 당신을 거슬리게 하고 있어. 그렇지 않소?\"\n\n\"아무것도 아니에요.\" 남작 부인이 대답했다.\n\n그러면서도 그녀는 숨을 제대로 쉬지 못해 자리에서 일어나 거울 쪽으로 걸어갔다. \"오늘 밤 나는 끔찍한 몰골이군요.\" 그녀가 말했다. 드브레가 미소 지으며 일어나, 이 마지막 말만큼은 반박하려는 참인데, 문이 갑자기 열렸다. 당글라르 씨가 나타났다. 드브레는 다시 자리에 앉았다. 문소리에 당글라르 부인이 돌아서서, 감추려는 기색도 없이 놀란 눈으로 남편을 바라보았다.\n\n\"안녕하시오, 부인.\" 은행가가 말했다. \"안녕하시오, 드브레 씨.\"\n\n남작 부인은 아마도 이 뜻밖의 방문이 낮 동안 그가 내뱉은 날카로운 말들을 만회하려는 뜻이라고 생각했을 것이다. 위엄 있는 태도를 취하며 그녀는 남편에게는 대답도 하지 않고 드브레 쪽으로 돌아앉았다.\n\n\"뭔가 좀 읽어 주세요, 드브레 씨.\" 그녀가 말했다. 이 방문에 약간 마음이 어수선했던 드브레는 남작 부인의 침착함을 보고 마음을 가다듬어, 금을 박은 자개 칼이 끼워져 있는 책 한 권을 집어 들었다.\n\n\"실례지만,\" 은행가가 말했다. \"이렇게 늦은 시각까지 깨어 있으면 지치실 거요, 부인. 게다가 드브레 씨는 여기서 좀 떨어진 곳에 사시니 말이오.\"\n\n드브레는 굳어 버렸다. 당글라르가 그토록 침착하고 정중하게 말하는 것을 들어서만이 아니라, 그 겉치레 정중함 아래 아내가 무얼 하려 하든 단호히 막겠다는 의지가 실제로 도사리고 있음이 분명했기 때문이다. 남작 부인 또한 놀라, 만일 남편이 폐장 시세를 확인하려고 신문에 골몰해 있지 않았더라면 틀림없이 어떤 효과를 발휘했을 눈길로 그 놀라움을 드러냈다. 그 결과, 그 도도한 눈길은 목적을 완전히 놓치고 말았다.\n\n\"뤼시앵 씨.\" 남작 부인이 말했다. \"정말이지 나는 잠잘 마음이 조금도 없고, 오늘 저녁 당신한테 들려줄 이야기가 천 가지나 있어요. 비록 듣는 동안 당신이 잠들어 버린다 해도 들어 주셔야 해요.\"\n\n\"분부대로 하겠습니다, 부인.\" 뤼시앵이 차갑게 대답했다.\n\n\"드브레 씨.\" 은행가가 말했다. \"오늘 밤 당글라르 부인의 어리석은 이야기를 듣느라 목숨을 깎지 마시오. 그런 이야기야 내일 들어도 되는 거 아니오. 허나 오늘 밤만은 내가 차지하겠소. 허락해 주신다면, 아내와 진지한 문제 몇 가지를 의논하는 데 쓰겠소.\"\n\n이번에는 그 일격이 워낙 정확히 겨누어져 곧장 명중한 터라, 뤼시앵과 남작 부인은 휘청거리며 마치 이 공격에 맞설 도움을 구하듯 서로 눈으로 물었다. 그러나 이 집 주인의 거역할 수 없는 의지가 우위를 점했고, 남편이 승리를 거두었다.\n\n\"당신을 내쫓으려는 거라고 생각진 마시오, 드브레 씨.\" 당글라르가 말을 이었다. \"오, 아니, 결코 그렇지 않소. 뜻밖의 일이 생겨서 아내와 잠깐 이야기를 나눠야 할 형편이 됐을 뿐이오. 내가 이런 청을 하는 일이야 워낙 드무니, 설마 그걸 아까워하시진 않겠지요.\"\n\n드브레는 뭐라고 중얼거리며 고개 숙여 인사하고 나갔는데, 「아탈리」에 나오는 나탕처럼 문 모서리에 부딪혔다.\n\n\"참 기이한 일이야.\" 등 뒤로 문이 닫히자 그가 말했다. \"우리가 비웃던 그 남편이라는 자들이 어쩌면 이렇게 손쉽게 우리를 누르는지.\"\n\n* 「아탈리」: 라신의 비극. 나탕은 그 등장인물. — 옮긴이\n뤼시앵이 떠나자 당글라르가 긴 의자의 그 자리에 앉아, 펼쳐진 책을 덮고는 무섭도록 위압적인 자세를 취하며 개와 장난을 치기 시작했다. 그러나 그 짐승은 드브레만큼 그를 좋아하지 않아 물려고 들었고, 당글라르는 목덜미 가죽을 움켜쥐어 방 건너편 긴 의자 위로 던져 버렸다. 짐승은 날아가는 동안 비명을 질렀으나, 떨어진 자리에 닿자 쿠션 뒤에 웅크려 그런 유별난 대접에 얼이 빠진 채 잠자코 꼼짝도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"당신, 점점 나아지고 있다는 걸 아세요?\" 남작 부인이 물었다. \"평소엔 무례하기만 하더니, 오늘 밤은 잔인하기까지 하군요.\"\n\n\"평소보다 기분이 더 나쁘기 때문이오.\" 당글라르가 대답했다. 에르민은 더없는 경멸의 눈으로 은행가를 바라보았다. 그런 눈길은 자주 당글라르의 자존심을 격분시키곤 했으나, 이날 저녁만은 그가 거들떠보지도 않았다.\n\n\"그런데 당신 언짢은 기분이 나와 무슨 상관이죠?\" 남편의 무감각함에 화가 난 남작 부인이 말했다. \"그런 일이 나와 관계라도 있나요? 언짢은 기분은 집에 있는 당신 금고에나 간직하든가, 아니면 봉급 주는 사무원들이 있으니 그자들한테나 풀어요.\"\n\n\"그렇지 않소.\" 당글라르가 대답했다. \"당신 충고는 틀렸으니 따르지 않겠소. 내 금고는, 데무스티에 씨가 한 말이었던가, 내 황금강이오. 그 흐름을 막지도, 그 고요를 흩뜨리지도 않겠소. 내 사무원들은 정직한 사람들이고, 내 재산을 벌어다 주지. 그들이 벌어들이는 것에 견주어 값을 매긴다면 받아 마땅한 것보다 훨씬 적은 보수를 주는 셈이니, 그들에게 성을 낼 까닭이 없소. 내가 성을 낼 자들은 내 밥을 먹고, 내 말을 타고, 내 재산을 거덜 내는 자들이오.\"\n\n\"그래, 부디, 당신 재산을 거덜 내는 자들이 누구죠? 더 분명히 말해 봐요, 부탁이에요.\"\n\n\"오, 마음 놓으시오! 수수께끼를 내는 게 아니니, 곧 무슨 뜻인지 알게 될 거요. 내 재산을 거덜 내는 자들은 한 시간 만에 70만 프랑을 빼내 가는 자들이오.\"\n\n\"무슨 말인지 모르겠군요.\" 남작 부인이 떨리는 목소리와 붉어진 얼굴을 감추려 애쓰며 말했다.\n\n\"천만에, 당신은 내 말을 완벽하게 알아듣고 있소.\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"하지만 정 우기겠다면, 내가 방금 스페인 공채로 70만 프랑을 잃었다고 말해 주리다.\"\n\n\"그래서요, 부디 묻건대,\" 남작 부인이 말했다. \"그 손실에 내가 책임이라도 있다는 건가요?\"\n\n\"아니라는 법이 있소?\"\n\"당신이 70만 프랑을 잃은 게 내 잘못이라고요?\"\n\n\"분명 내 잘못은 아니지.\"\n\n\"단번에 말해 두겠어요.\" 남작 부인이 매섭게 대꾸했다. \"나는 돈 이야기를 입에 올리는 걸 듣지 않겠어요. 부모님 집에서도, 첫 남편의 집에서도 들어 본 적 없는 말투니까요.\"\n\n\"오, 그야 그럴 만하지. 두 집 다 한 푼어치 값도 없었으니까.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 더더욱 내가 은행 은어 따위에 익숙할 까닭이 없지요. 아침부터 밤까지 여기서 내 귀를 울려 대는 그 은어 말이에요. 끊임없이 세고 또 세는 그 동전 짤랑거리는 소리가 나는 지긋지긋해요. 그보다 더 싫은 게 딱 하나 있는데, 바로 당신 목소리지요.\"\n\n\"정말이오?\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"허, 그것 참 놀랍군. 나는 당신이 내 사업 전부에 더없이 활기찬 관심을 두는 줄 알았는데 말이오!\"\n\n\"내가요? 누가 당신 머릿속에 그런 생각을 집어넣던가요?\"\n\n\"당신 자신이오.\"\n\n\"아? 그래서 또?\"\n\n\"틀림없는 사실이오.\"\n\"어떤 경우였는지 알고 싶군요.\"\n\n\"오, 맙소사! 그야 아주 간단하지. 지난 2월에 나한테 아이티 공채 이야기를 처음 꺼낸 게 바로 당신이었소. 당신은 르아브르 항구에 배 한 척이 들어오는 꿈을 꿨고, 그 배가 우리가 떼인 줄로 여겼던 대금이 곧 지불될 거라는 소식을 가져왔다고 했지. 당신 꿈이 얼마나 들어맞는지 내가 잘 아니까, 곧장 아이티 공채를 살 수 있는 만큼 사들였고, 그걸로 40만 프랑을 벌었소. 그중 10만 프랑은 정직하게 당신한테 건넸지. 당신은 그걸 마음대로 썼고, 그건 당신 일이오. 3월에는 어느 철도 부설권 문제가 있었지. 세 회사가 나섰는데, 저마다 똑같은 담보를 내걸었소. 당신은 자기 직감이—당신은 투기에 대해 아무것도 모르는 척하지만, 나는 오히려 어떤 일들에 대해선 당신 머리가 아주 명석하다고 보오—그래, 당신은 자기 직감으로 보아 그 부설권이 남부라는 회사에 떨어질 것 같다고 했지. 나는 그 회사 주식의 삼분의 이를 사들였고, 당신이 내다본 대로 주가가 세 배로 뛰어 나는 백만 프랑을 거머쥐었소. 그중 25만 프랑을 당신 용돈으로 건넸지. 그 25만 프랑을 어떻게 썼는지는—내 알 바 아니오.\"\n\n\"대체 언제 본론으로 들어올 거죠?\" 분노와 조바심에 떨며 남작 부인이 외쳤다.\n\n\"인내심을 가지시오, 부인. 이제 본론으로 가는 중이오.\"\n\n\"그거 다행이군요.\"\n\n\"4월에 당신은 장관 댁 만찬에 갔지. 거기서 스페인 정세에 관한, 돈 카를로스의 추방에 관한 은밀한 대화를 들었소. 나는 스페인 주식을 좀 사들였지. 추방이 일어났고, 카를 5세가 비다소아강을 다시 건너던 날 나는 60만 프랑을 챙겼소. 그 60만 프랑 중에서 당신은 5만 에퀴를 가져갔지. 그건 당신 것이었고, 당신 좋을 대로 처분했으며, 나는 아무것도 묻지 않았소. 그래도 당신이 올해 50만 리브르를 받았다는 사실엔 변함이 없지.\"\n\n\"좋아요, 그래서요, 그게 어쨌다는 거죠?\"\n\n\"아, 그렇지, 바로 그다음에 당신이 모든 걸 망쳐 놓았소.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 당신 말투란——\"\n\n\"내 뜻을 그대로 드러낼 뿐이오. 그거면 됐소. 자, 그 일이 있고 사흘 뒤에 당신은 드브레 씨와 정치 이야기를 나눴고, 그 사람 말을 듣고는 돈 카를로스가 스페인으로 돌아갔다고 넘겨짚었지. 그래서 나는 내 주식을 팔았소. 소문이 퍼졌고, 더는 팔 것도 없어 거저 줘 버리다시피 했지. 그런데 이튿날 그 소식이 거짓임이 드러났고, 그 거짓 보고 때문에 나는 70만 프랑을 잃었소.\"\n\n\"그래서요?\"\n\n\"그래서 말인데, 내가 이익의 사분의 일을 당신한테 주었으니, 당신도 내 손실의 사분의 일을 갚아야 마땅하다고 보오. 70만 프랑의 사분의 일은 17만 5천 프랑이지.\"\n\n\"말도 안 되는 소리예요. 게다가 이 일에 드브레 씨 이름이 왜 끼어드는지 모르겠군요.\"\n\n\"내가 돌려받겠다는 그 17만 5천 프랑이 당신한테 없다면, 당신 친구들한테 빌려준 게 분명하고, 드브레 씨가 바로 당신 친구 중 하나니까.\"\n\n\"부끄러운 줄 아세요!\" 남작 부인이 외쳤다.\n\n\"오, 손짓도, 비명도, 요즘 유행하는 연극도 그만둡시다. 안 그러면 당신더러 듣게 만들 거요. 드브레가 올해 당신한테서 넘겨받은 50만 리브르를 죄다 주머니에 챙겨 여기를 나서면서, 가장 노련한 노름꾼도 끝내 찾아내지 못한 것을 자기는 찾아냈다고 혼자 빙긋 웃는 꼴을 내가 본다고 말이오. 노름을 하지 않고도 따는, 잃어도 손해 볼 게 없는 룰렛판 말이오.\"\n\n남작 부인은 격분했다.\n\n\"이 비열한 인간!\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"지금 나를 책망하는 그 일을 당신이 몰랐다고 감히 말할 셈인가요?\"\n\n\"내가 알았다고도, 몰랐다고도 말하지 않겠소. 다만 우리가 부부이기를 그만둔 지난 사 년 동안 내 행실을 들여다보고, 그게 한결같지 않았는지 살펴보라고 할 뿐이오. 우리가 갈라선 얼마 뒤, 당신은 이탈리아 극장에서 그토록 성공적으로 등장한 그 유명한 바리톤에게 음악을 배우고 싶어 했지. 그 무렵 나는 런던에서 그토록 명성을 얻은 그 무희에게 춤을 배우고 싶은 마음이 들었고. 그게 당신 몫 내 몫 합쳐 10만 프랑이 들었소. 나는 아무 말도 하지 않았지. 집안의 평화는 지켜야 하니까. 그리고 신사 숙녀가 음악과 춤을 제대로 배우는 데 10만 프랑이면 과하지도 않고. 그런데 당신은 이내 노래에 싫증을 내더니, 이번엔 장관 비서한테 외교술을 배울 마음이 동했지. 알겠소만, 당신이 제 금고에서 수업료를 치르는 한 나야 아무래도 상관없소. 그런데 오늘 보니 당신은 내 금고를 끌어다 쓰고 있고, 그 수업 비용이 한 달에 70만 프랑씩 들 판이오. 거기서 멈추시오, 부인. 이건 더 이상 계속될 수 없소. 그 외교가가 수업을 거저 해 주든가—그렇다면 나도 눈감아 주겠소—아니면 다시는 우리 집에 발을 들이지 말든가 해야 하오. 알아듣겠소, 부인?\"\n\n\"오, 이건 너무하군요.\" 에르민이 숨이 막혀 외쳤다. \"당신은 비열함을 넘어선 사람이에요.\"\n\n\"그런데,\" 당글라르가 말을 이었다. \"보아하니 당신은 거기서 멈추지도 않았더군——\"\n\n\"모욕이야!\"\n\n\"옳은 말이오. 그런 일들은 그냥 두고, 냉정하게 따져 봅시다. 나는 당신을 위해서가 아니라면 당신 일에 한 번도 끼어든 적이 없소. 당신도 나를 그렇게 대해 주시오. 당신은 내 금고와 아무 상관 없다고 했지. 좋소. 당신 것은 마음대로 하되, 내 것은 채우지도 비우지도 마시오. 게다가 이게 정치적 술책이 아니라고 내가 어찌 장담하겠소? 내가 야당에 선 꼴을 보고 격분한, 또 내가 불러일으키는 대중의 호감을 시샘하는 장관이 드브레 씨와 짜고 나를 파멸시키려 한 게 아니라고 말이오?\"\n\n\"그럴듯도 하군요!\"\n\n\"아니라는 법이 있소? 이런 일이 일어났다는 말을 들어 본 적이나 있소? 거짓 전신 급보라니. 지난 두 전신처럼 잘못된 신호가 보내지는 건 거의 불가능한 일이오. 일부러 나를 노리고 한 짓이오—나는 확신하오.\"\n\n\"여보.\" 남작 부인이 풀죽은 목소리로 말했다. \"그곳에 고용된 자가 해고되었고, 그자를 법정에 세우자는 말까지 나왔으며, 체포하라는 명령이 내려졌고, 그가 달아나 도망치지 않았더라면 그 명령이 집행되었으리라는 걸 모르세요? 그건 그자가 미쳤거나 죄가 있다는 증거예요. 그저 실수였다고요.\"\n\n\"그렇지, 그 실수가 어리석은 자들을 웃게 만들었고, 장관에게 하룻밤 잠을 설치게 했고, 장관 비서들에게 종이 몇 장을 검게 채우게 했지. 하지만 내게는 70만 프랑을 치르게 한 실수요.\"\n\n\"하지만 여보.\" 에르민이 불쑥 말했다. \"이 모든 게, 당신 말대로 드브레 씨 탓이라면, 어째서 그 사람한테 곧장 가지 않고 나한테 와서 이런 이야기를 하는 거죠? 어째서 남자를 탓하면서 여자한테 따지는 거예요?\"\n\n\"내가 드브레 씨를 알기나 하오? 내가 그를 알고 싶기나 하오? 그가 충고를 한다는 걸 내가 알고 싶기나 하오? 내가 그 충고를 따르고 싶기나 하오? 내가 투기를 하오? 아니지. 그 모든 걸 하는 건 나 아닌 당신이오.\"\n\n\"그래도 당신이 그 덕을 보고 있으니——\"\n\n당글라르가 어깨를 으쓱했다. \"어리석은 사람 같으니.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"여자들은 파리의 입방아에 오르지 않고 두세 건의 정사를 꾸며 냈다고 자기한테 재주가 있는 줄 안다니까! 하지만 알아 두시오. 설령 당신이 그 외도를—그 기술의 초보 단계에나 와 있는—남편한테 감췄다 한들, 워낙 남편이란 대개 보려 들지 않으니, 당신은 사교계 여자 친구들 대부분의 어설픈 흉내쟁이에 지나지 않았을 거요. 그런데 나는 그렇지 않았소. 나는 지난 십육 년 동안 보아 왔고, 늘 보고 있었소. 당신은 어쩌면 생각 하나쯤 숨겼을지 모르지만, 걸음 하나, 행동 하나, 잘못 하나 내 눈을 벗어나지 못했소. 그러는 동안 당신은 제 수완에 우쭐대며, 나를 감쪽같이 속였다고 굳게 믿었지. 그 결과가 무엇이오? 내가 모르는 척해 준 덕분에, 빌포르 씨에서 드브레 씨에 이르기까지 당신 친구 중에 내 앞에서 떨지 않은 자가 하나도 없소. 나를 이 집 주인으로 대하지 않은 자가 하나도 없지—당신에 관한 한 내가 바라는 칭호는 그것뿐이오. 사실, 오늘 내가 그들을 두고 말한 대로 감히 나를 두고 말할 수 있었을 자도 하나 없소. 나를 미워하게 만드는 건 허락하겠소만, 나를 우스꽝스럽게 만드는 건 막겠소. 무엇보다도, 나를 파멸시키는 건 금하오.\"\n\n남작 부인은 빌포르라는 이름이 입에 오르기 전까지는 그런대로 평정을 지키고 있었다. 그러나 그 순간 그녀는 새파랗게 질려, 용수철에 튕긴 듯 벌떡 일어나, 마치 망령을 물리치려는 듯 두 손을 뻗었다. 그러고는 남편이 모르고 있거나, 아니면 어떤 흉악한 속셈에서—그의 속셈이란 다 그렇듯 흉악한—감추고 있는 그 비밀을 캐내려는 듯 그를 향해 두세 걸음 다가갔다.\n\n\"빌포르 씨라니!—무슨 뜻이죠?\"\n\n\"당신 첫 남편 나르곤 씨가 철학자도 은행가도 아니었거나, 어쩌면 둘 다였거나 간에, 왕실 검사한테서는 얻어 낼 게 없다는 걸 알고, 아홉 달을 비웠다 돌아와 보니 당신이 여섯 달째 임신해 있는 걸 알고는 비탄인지 분노인지로 죽어 버렸다는 뜻이오. 나는 잔인하오—그걸 인정할 뿐 아니라 자랑으로 여기지. 그게 내가 상업에서 성공한 까닭 중 하나요. 그 사람은 어째서 당신 대신 제 목숨을 끊었겠소? 구해 낼 돈이 없었으니까. 내 목숨은 내 돈에 달려 있소. 드브레 씨가 내게 70만 프랑을 잃게 했으니, 제 몫의 손실을 짊어지게 하시오. 그러면 우리는 전처럼 지내겠소. 그게 싫다면, 그 25만 리브르 때문에 파산하든가, 모든 파산자들이 하는 대로 하든가—자취를 감추든가 하라지. 인정하건대, 소식이 정확할 때야 그자도 매력적인 친구지. 하지만 그렇지 못할 때, 그보다 더 잘해 낼 자가 세상에 쉰 명은 있소.\"\n\n당글라르 부인은 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 굳어 버렸다. 이 마지막 공격에 대꾸하려고 안간힘을 썼으나, 그녀는 의자에 주저앉아 빌포르를, 그 만찬 자리의 광경을, 지난 며칠 사이 자기 집에서 벌어져 이 집의 평소 평온을 추문 가득한 언쟁의 무대로 바꿔 놓은 그 기이한 불행의 연속을 떠올렸다.\n\n당글라르는 그녀가 기절하려고 갖은 애를 쓰는데도 거들떠보지도 않았다. 그는 더는 한마디도 보태지 않고 등 뒤로 침실 문을 닫고 자기 방으로 돌아갔다. 그리고 당글라르 부인이 반쯤 기절했던 상태에서 깨어났을 때, 그녀는 자신이 불쾌한 꿈을 꾸었다고 거의 믿을 뻔했다.\n\n# Chapter 66. Matrimonial Projects\n\n이 일이 있은 다음 날, 평소 드브레가 출근길에 당글라르 부인을 찾아오던 시각이 되었는데도 그의 마차는 나타나지 않았다. 이 무렵, 그러니까 열두 시 반쯤 당글라르 부인은 마차를 대령하라 이르고는 외출했다. 당글라르는 커튼 뒤에 몸을 숨긴 채, 자신이 기다려 온 부인의 외출을 지켜보았다. 그는 부인이 돌아오면 곧바로 알리라고 일러 두었다. 그러나 두 시가 되어도 부인은 돌아오지 않았다. 그러자 그는 말을 대령하라 하여 의회로 가, 예산안에 반대하는 발언자 명단에 자기 이름을 올렸다. 열두 시부터 두 시까지 당글라르는 서재에 틀어박혀 들어온 전보들의 봉인을 뜯으며 시시각각 우울해져 갔다. 숫자 위에 숫자를 쌓아 올리던 그는 여러 방문객 가운데 카발칸티 소령의 방문도 받았다. 소령은 여느 때처럼 뻣뻣하고 빈틈없는 태도로, 전날 밤에 정해 둔 시각에 정확히 맞춰 와서 은행가와의 거래를 마무리 지었다.\n\n회의가 진행되는 동안 격한 동요의 기색을 드러냈고 그 어느 때보다 신랄하게 내각을 공격했던 당글라르는, 의회를 나서며 다시 마차에 올라 마부에게 샹젤리제 가 30번지로 가라고 일렀다.\n\n몬테크리스토는 집에 있었다. 다만 누군가와 면담 중이라, 잠시 응접실에서 기다려 달라고 당글라르에게 청했다. 은행가가 대기실에서 기다리는 동안 문이 열리더니, 사제 옷차림을 한 남자가 들어왔다. 그는 분명 당글라르보다 그 집을 잘 아는 듯, 기다리지도 않고 그저 가볍게 고개만 숙이고는 안쪽 방으로 지나가 사라졌다.\n\n사제가 들어갔던 그 문이 잠시 후 다시 열리며 몬테크리스토가 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n\"용서하시오.\" 그가 말했다. \"친애하는 남작, 내 친구 부소니 신부가 방금 파리에 도착했소. 아마 지나가는 걸 보셨을 거요. 오랫동안 보지 못한 터라, 그렇게 일찍 그를 두고 나올 마음이 들지 않더군요. 기다리시게 한 사정을 이만하면 양해해 주시리라 믿소.\"\n\n\"천만에요.\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"제 잘못입니다. 찾아온 때가 좋지 않았으니, 물러가겠습니다.\"\n\n\"전혀 그렇지 않소. 오히려 앉으시오. 그런데 무슨 일이라도 있소? 근심에 잠긴 얼굴이구려. 정말이지 걱정이 되는군요. 자본가의 우울이란, 혜성의 출현처럼 세상에 닥칠 불행을 예고하는 법이거든.\"\n\n\"며칠째 운이 좋지 않습니다.\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"나쁜 소식밖에 듣지 못했지요.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇소?\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"증권 거래소에서 또 한 번 손실을 보셨소?\"\n\n\"아닙니다. 적어도 며칠은 안전합니다. 트리에스테에서 파산한 자 때문에 골치가 아플 뿐이지요.\"\n\n\"정말이오? 혹시 그게 야코포 만프레디요?\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다. 한번 생각해 보십시오. 그자는 얼마나 오래되었는지도 모를 만큼 오랫동안 저와 거래해 왔고, 한 해에 80만, 90만 프랑에 이르는 거래를 했습니다. 단 한 번도 실수나 지체가 없었지요. 왕족처럼 척척 지불하던 자였습니다. 그런데 제가 그자에게 백만 프랑을 미리 내준 참에, 이 훌륭하신 야코포 만프레디가 지불을 정지해 버린 겁니다!\"\n\n\"정말이오?\"\n\n\"전례 없는 불운입니다. 제가 그자 앞으로 60만 프랑짜리 어음을 발행했더니 지불 거절로 되돌아왔습니다. 게다가 그것뿐만이 아니라, 이달 말 파리의 거래처에서 지불하기로 그자가 서명한 40만 프랑짜리 환어음까지 제가 들고 있지요. 오늘이 30일입니다. 그걸 제시하러 갔더니, 거래처가 사라져 버렸지 뭡니까. 이게 스페인 일까지 겹쳐서, 이번 달 끝이 참으로 볼만하게 되었습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 스페인 그 일로 정말 손해를 보신 게요?\"\n\n\"네. 제 금고에서 고작 70만 프랑이 나갔을 뿐이지요. 그것뿐입니다!\"\n\n\"아니, 어쩌다 그런 실수를 하셨소? 노련한 분께서?\"\n\n\"오, 다 제 아내 탓입니다. 돈 카를로스가 스페인으로 돌아오는 꿈을 꾸었다지 뭡니까. 아내는 꿈을 믿지요. 그게 자력 작용이라나요, 자기가 꿈에 본 것은 반드시 일어난다고 우기더군요. 그 확신을 믿고 저는 아내가 투기를 하도록 내버려 둡니다. 아내에게는 따로 거래 은행과 증권 중개인이 있거든요. 아내는 투기를 했고, 잃었습니다. 물론 제 돈이 아니라 자기 돈으로 한 일이긴 합니다. 그렇지만 아시다시피 아내 주머니에서 70만 프랑이 빠져나가면, 남편이 모를 리가 없지요. 그런데 정말 이 일을 못 들으셨다는 겁니까? 어찌나 큰 소동이 났는데요.\"\n\n\"그래요, 이야기는 들었소만 자세한 사정은 몰랐소. 게다가 거래소 일에 관해서라면 나만큼 무지한 사람도 없을 거요.\"\n\n\"그럼 백작께서는 투기를 안 하십니까?\"\n\n\"내가요? 가진 수입을 관리하는 것만도 골치가 아픈데 어떻게 투기를 하겠소? 그랬다가는 집사 말고도 서기 한 명에 사환 하나를 더 두어야 할 거요. 그런데 이 스페인 일을 두고 하는 말인데, 내 생각에는 남작 부인이 돈 카를로스 일을 통째로 꿈에서 본 건 아닐 거요. 신문에 그 비슷한 이야기가 실렸지 않소?\"\n\n\"그럼 백작께서는 신문을 믿으십니까?\"\n\n\"내가요? 천만에요. 다만 그 정직한 메사제만큼은 예외라서, 전신으로 들어온 급보만 싣는 줄 알았소.\"\n\n\"바로 그게 제가 알 수 없는 대목입니다.\" 당글라르가 대답했다. \"돈 카를로스가 돌아왔다는 소식은 전신으로 들어왔거든요.\"\n\n\"그러니까,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"이달에 거의 백칠십만 프랑을 잃으셨다는 말이군요.\"\n\n\"거의가 아니라, 딱 그만큼이 제 손실입니다.\"\n\n\"저런!\" 몬테크리스토가 동정 어린 어조로 말했다. \"삼류 재산에는 호된 타격이겠구려.\"\n\n\"삼류라니요.\" 당글라르가 다소 풀이 죽어 말했다. \"그게 무슨 뜻입니까?\"\n\n\"이렇게 보시오.\" 몬테크리스토가 말을 이었다. \"나는 재산을 셋으로 나누지요. 일류, 이류, 삼류 재산으로 말이오. 일류라 함은, 광산이나 토지, 프랑스나 오스트리아, 영국 같은 나라의 공채처럼 손안에 쥐고 있는 보물로 이루어진 재산을 말하오. 단, 이 보물과 자산이 합쳐 약 일억 프랑쯤은 되어야 하지요. 이류 재산이라 함은, 제조업이나 주식회사, 부왕령이나 공국에서 얻는 것으로, 그 수입이 백오십만 프랑을 넘지 않으며 전체 자본이 오천만 프랑쯤 되는 재산을 말하오. 끝으로 삼류 재산이라 함은, 남의 의지나 우연에 좌우되는 변동 자본으로 이루어진 것이오. 파산이 휘말려 들거나 거짓 전보 하나에도 흔들리는, 이를테면 은행이나 그날그날의 투기 같은 것 말이오. 요컨대 크고 작은 불운에 좌우되는 온갖 거래로, 그 전체가 실재든 허구든 약 천오백만 프랑의 자본을 가져다주는 재산이지요. 내 생각에는 이게 대략 남작의 처지 같소만, 아니오?\"\n\n\"제기랄, 맞습니다!\" 당글라르가 대답했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 이런 달이 여섯 번만 더 이어진다면, 삼류 가문은 절망에 빠지고 말겠구려.\"\n\n\"오,\" 당글라르가 새파랗게 질려 말했다. \"어찌 그리 말씀을 함부로 하십니까!\"\n\n\"이런 달이 일곱 번 이어진다고 쳐 봅시다.\" 몬테크리스토가 같은 어조로 말을 이었다. \"말해 보시오, 백칠십만 프랑의 일곱 배가 거의 천이백만 프랑이라는 걸 생각해 본 적 있소? 없겠지요. 음, 그게 옳소. 그런 생각에 빠졌다가는 결코 원금을 걸지 못할 테니까. 투기꾼에게 원금이란, 문명인에게 살갗과도 같은 것이오. 우리에게는 옷이 있고, 그중에는 더 화려한 것도 있지요. 그게 바로 신용이오. 하지만 사람이 죽으면 남는 건 살갗뿐이오. 마찬가지로, 사업에서 손을 떼면 남는 건 실제 원금뿐인데, 그래 봐야 기껏 오백만이나 육백만 프랑이오. 삼류 재산이란 겉보기의 사분의 일을 넘는 법이 없으니까. 철로 위의 기관차가 둘러싼 연기와 증기 때문에 실제보다 커 보이는 것과 같지요. 자, 남작의 실제 자본을 이루는 오백만이나 육백만 프랑 가운데, 방금 거의 이백만 프랑을 잃으셨소. 그만큼 당연히 남작의 신용과 허구의 재산도 줄어들 수밖에 없지요. 내 비유를 끝까지 따라가 보면, 남작의 살갗은 출혈로 갈라진 셈이오. 이게 서너 번 거듭되면 죽음에 이르고 말지요. 그러니 부디 조심하시오, 친애하는 당글라르 씨. 돈이 필요하시오? 내가 좀 빌려 드릴까요?\"\n\n\"백작께서는 참 셈이 서투르시군요!\" 당글라르가 자신의 온갖 철학과 위장술을 끌어모아 외쳤다. \"저는 같은 시기에 성공한 투기로 돈도 벌었습니다. 빠져나간 피를 영양으로 보충했지요. 스페인에서 한 전투를 졌고 트리에스테에서 패했지만, 인도에 있는 제 함대가 갈레온선 몇 척쯤은 나포했을 테고, 멕시코의 제 광산 개척자들이 광맥을 발견했을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"좋소, 아주 좋소! 하지만 상처는 그대로 남아, 다음 손실이 닥치는 순간 다시 벌어질 거요.\"\n\n\"아닙니다. 저는 확실한 것에만 발을 들이거든요.\" 당글라르가 제 자랑을 늘어놓는 약장수 같은 태도로 대답했다. \"저를 무너뜨리려면 세 나라 정부가 가루가 되도록 무너져야 할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"음, 그런 일도 있었던 법이오.\"\n\n\"기근이라도 들어야 한단 말이지요!\"\n\n\"살진 소 일곱 마리와 여윈 소 일곱 마리를 떠올려 보시오.\"\n\n\"아니면 파라오 시절처럼 바다가 말라붙어야 하겠지요. 그래 봐야 그땐 제 배들이 대상(隊商)으로 바뀔 테지만요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 더없이 좋은 일이오. 축하하오, 친애하는 당글라르 씨.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"내가 잘못 봤구려. 남작은 이류 재산에 속하는 분이군요.\"\n\n\"저도 그 영광은 넘볼 만하지 않나 합니다.\" 당글라르가 미소 지으며 말했다. 그 미소를 보고 몬테크리스토는, 서투른 화가들이 폐허 그림에 즐겨 처발라 넣는 병색 짙은 달을 떠올렸다. \"그런데 사업 이야기가 나온 김에,\" 당글라르가 화제를 돌릴 기회를 잡아 흐뭇해하며 덧붙였다. \"카발칸티 씨 건은 어떻게 하면 좋을지 일러 주시지요.\"\n\n\"그에게 돈을 주시오. 추천을 받았고, 그 추천이 믿을 만해 보인다면 말이오.\"\n\n\"더할 나위 없지요. 오늘 아침에 그가 일람불 채권 4만 프랑짜리를 들고 찾아왔습니다. 백작 앞으로 발행된 것이고, 부소니가 서명해서 백작께서 배서하여 제게 돌려보내신 것이었지요. 물론 저는 곧바로 지폐 마흔 장을 세어 주었습니다.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 동의의 표시로 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"하지만 그것뿐만이 아닙니다.\" 당글라르가 말을 이었다. \"그가 자기 아들 명의로 제 은행에 계좌를 열었거든요.\"\n\n\"그 젊은이에게 얼마나 내준다는지 여쭤도 되겠소?\"\n\n\"한 달에 오천 프랑입니다.\"\n\n\"일 년에 육만 프랑이라. 카발칸티가 인색한 자라고 본 게 옳았던 모양이오. 젊은이가 한 달에 오천 프랑으로 어찌 살아간단 말이오?\"\n\n\"하지만 아시다시피, 그 젊은이가 몇천 프랑쯤 더 필요로 한다면……\"\n\n\"미리 내주지 마시오. 아비가 결코 갚지 않을 테니까. 남작은 알프스 너머 그 백만장자들을 모르시는군요. 하나같이 지독한 구두쇠들이오. 그런데 그들을 누가 추천했소?\"\n\n\"오, 펜치 상회입니다. 피렌체에서 손꼽히는 곳이지요.\"\n\n\"손해 본다는 말은 아니오. 그래도 부디 계약 조건에서 벗어나지 않도록 단단히 지키시오.\"\n\n\"카발칸티를 못 믿으신단 말씀입니까?\"\n\n\"내가요? 오, 나라면 그자의 서명만 보고도 천만 프랑은 내줄 거요. 그저 방금 우리가 이야기한 이류 재산을 두고 한 말일 뿐이오.\"\n\n\"그러면서도 어찌나 겸손한지요! 저는 그를 한낱 소령 이상으로는 보지 않았을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그를 한껏 추켜세운 셈이오. 정말이지 남작 말대로, 그는 통 품위라고는 없으니까. 처음 봤을 때 나는 견장 밑에서 곰팡내가 날 만큼 늙어 버린 퇴역 중위 같다고 생각했소. 하지만 이탈리아인이란 다 그렇지요. 동방의 화려함으로 번쩍이지 않을 때면, 늙은 유대인 같다오.\"\n\n\"아들은 그보다 낫더군요.\" 당글라르가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소. 어쩌면 좀 신경질적이긴 해도, 대체로 봐줄 만했소. 그 청년 때문에 마음이 좀 불안했지요.\"\n\n\"왜요?\"\n\n\"남작이 우리 집에서 그를 만난 게, 사람들 말로는 막 사교계에 발을 들인 직후였으니 말이오. 그는 아주 엄한 가정교사와 함께 여행을 다녔고, 파리에는 한 번도 온 적이 없었다오.\"\n\n\"아, 귀족들은 자기들끼리 결혼하지 않습니까?\" 당글라르가 무심한 듯 물었다. \"재산을 합치고 싶어 하지요.\"\n\n\"물론 흔한 일이오. 하지만 카발칸티는 남들과는 다른 별난 사람이오. 아무래도 며느릿감을 고르려고 아들을 프랑스로 데려온 것 같단 말이지요.\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각하십니까?\"\n\n\"확신하오.\"\n\n\"그의 재산에 대해서는 무슨 말을 들으셨습니까?\"\n\n\"오로지 그 이야기뿐이었소. 다만 누구는 그가 수백만 프랑을 가졌다 하고, 누구는 한 푼도 없다고 하더군요.\"\n\n\"백작의 생각은 어떠십니까?\"\n\n\"내 의견에 남작이 휘둘려서는 안 되오. 그저 순전히 내 개인적인 인상일 뿐이니까.\"\n\n\"그래도, 그게 어떻다는……\"\n\n\"내 생각은 이렇소. 이 늙은 포데스타들, 이 옛 콘도티에리들은 — 카발칸티 가문은 군대를 지휘하고 지방을 다스린 집안이니까 — 내 생각에는 말이오, 그들이 수백만 프랑을 구석구석 파묻어 두고, 그 비밀을 오직 맏아들에게만 물려주고, 그 맏아들이 또 대를 이어 같은 짓을 해 온 거요. 그 증거가 바로 그들의 누렇고 메마른 얼굴이지요. 공화국의 플로린 금화를 하도 들여다본 나머지, 그 빛깔이 얼굴에 비친 거라오.\"\n\n\"확실히 그렇습니다.\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"땅 한 뼘 가진 게 없다는 사실도 그걸 뒷받침하지요.\"\n\n\"적어도 거의 없지요. 카발칸티가 가진 땅이라고는 루카에 있는 저택 말고는 들어 본 게 없소.\"\n\n\"아, 저택이 있긴 합니까?\" 당글라르가 웃으며 말했다. \"이런, 그래도 그건 뭔가 되는군요.\"\n\n\"그렇소. 게다가 그는 그 저택을 재무 장관에게 세놓고 자기는 수수한 집에 산다오. 오, 앞서 말했듯이 그 영감은 아주 인색한 사람 같소.\"\n\n\"이런, 그를 추켜세우진 않으시는군요.\"\n\n\"나는 그를 잘 모르오. 평생 세 번쯤 본 것 같소. 그에 관해 아는 건 다 부소니와 그자 본인을 통해 들은 거요. 오늘 아침에 그가 하는 말이, 죽은 나라나 다름없는 이탈리아에 자기 재산을 묵혀 두기가 지겨워서, 프랑스든 영국이든 어디서든 수백만 프랑을 불릴 방도를 찾고 싶다더군요. 하지만 명심하시오, 내가 부소니는 무척 신뢰하지만 이 일까지 책임지는 건 아니라오.\"\n\n\"괜찮습니다. 좋은 고객을 보내 주셔서 감사할 따름이지요. 제 장부에 올리기에 훌륭한 이름이고, 카발칸티 가문이 어떤 집안인지 설명해 주었더니 제 회계 담당도 무척 자랑스러워하더군요. 그건 그렇고, 이건 그저 단순한 질문입니다만, 이런 사람들은 아들을 결혼시킬 때 재산을 좀 떼어 줍니까?\"\n\n\"오, 그건 사정에 따라 다르오. 내가 아는 어느 이탈리아 공작은 금광처럼 부유하고 토스카나에서도 손꼽히는 명문 출신인데, 아들들이 제 뜻에 맞게 결혼하면 수백만 프랑을 주었지만, 제 뜻을 거슬러 결혼하면 한 달에 고작 삼십 크라운만 내주었다오. 안드레아도 제 아비의 뜻에 맞게 결혼한다면, 아마 백만이나 이백만, 삼백만 프랑쯤은 줄 거요. 가령 상대가 은행가의 딸이라면, 아들의 장인이 될 사람의 은행에 출자할 수도 있겠지요. 하지만 또 그 선택이 마음에 들지 않으면, 소령은 열쇠를 챙겨 금고를 이중으로 잠가 버릴 테고, 그러면 안드레아 도련님은 파리 가문 자제들처럼 카드를 섞거나 주사위를 굴려 가며 먹고살아야 할 거요.\"\n\n\"아, 그 녀석은 바이에른이나 페루의 공주라도 찾아내려 할 겁니다. 왕관과 엘도라도와 포토시를 원하겠지요.\"\n\n\"아니오. 알프스 저편의 이 대귀족들은 흔히 평범한 가문과도 결혼하지요. 유피테르처럼, 혈통을 섞는 걸 좋아한단 말이오. 그런데 친애하는 당글라르 씨, 그렇게 질문이 많은 걸 보니 안드레아를 사위로 삼고 싶으신 거요?\"\n\n\"맹세코,\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"나쁘지 않은 투기일 것 같습니다. 아시다시피 저는 투기꾼 아닙니까.\"\n\n\"설마 당글라르 양을 두고 하는 말은 아니길 바라오. 가엾은 안드레아가 알베르에게 목이 베이는 꼴을 보고 싶진 않으실 텐데?\"\n\n\"알베르라.\" 당글라르가 어깨를 으쓱하며 되뇌었다. \"아, 글쎄요. 그 친구야 별로 개의치 않을 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 그가 따님과 약혼한 사이 아니오?\"\n\n\"음, 모르세르 씨와 저는 그 혼사를 의논한 적이 있습니다. 하지만 모르세르 부인과 알베르는……\"\n\n\"그게 좋은 혼처가 아니라는 말씀은 아니겠지요?\"\n\n\"솔직히, 제 생각에 당글라르 양은 모르세르 씨에게 조금도 모자라지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"당글라르 양의 재산이야 대단하겠지요. 특히 전신이 더는 실수를 저지르지 않는다면 말이오.\"\n\n\"오, 재산만 두고 하는 말이 아닙니다. 그런데 말씀해 보시지요……\"\n\n\"무엇을?\"\n\n\"왜 모르세르 씨 부부를 만찬에 초대하지 않으셨습니까?\"\n\n\"초대했소. 하지만 그는 모르세르 부인이 바다 공기를 쐬러 디에프에 가야 한다며 사양하더군요.\"\n\n\"그래요, 그렇겠지요.\" 당글라르가 웃으며 말했다. \"부인에게는 더없이 좋은 일이겠지요.\"\n\n\"어째서요?\"\n\n\"젊은 시절 늘 들이마시던 공기니까요.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 이 짓궂은 말에 아무 내색도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"하지만 그래도, 알베르가 당글라르 양만큼 부유하지 않다 해도,\" 백작이 말했다. \"훌륭한 이름을 가졌다는 건 인정하셔야 할 거요.\"\n\n\"그야 그렇지요. 하지만 제 이름도 그에 못지않게 좋습니다.\"\n\n\"물론이오. 남작의 이름은 널리 알려졌고, 그 이름을 빛내 주는 작위에도 명예를 더하지요. 하지만 남작은 워낙 영민한 분이니, 좀처럼 뿌리 뽑기 힘들 만큼 굳어진 편견에 따라, 오백 년을 거슬러 올라가는 귀족 가문이 고작 이십 년밖에 헤아릴 수 없는 가문보다 가치 있게 여겨진다는 걸 모르지 않으실 거요.\"\n\n\"바로 그 이유 때문에,\" 당글라르가 비꼬는 투를 내려 애쓰며 미소 띤 채 말했다. \"저는 알베르 드 모르세르 씨보다 안드레아 카발칸티 씨가 더 마음에 듭니다.\"\n\n\"그래도 모르세르 가문이 카발칸티 가문에 밀린다고는 생각하기 어려운데요?\"\n\n\"모르세르 가문이라니요! 잠깐, 친애하는 백작.\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"백작께서는 세상 물정에 밝은 분이시지요, 안 그렇습니까?\"\n\n\"그런 것 같소.\"\n\n\"그럼 문장학(紋章學)도 좀 아십니까?\"\n\n\"조금은요.\"\n\n\"그럼 제 가문 문장을 보십시오. 모르세르의 것보다 더 값나갑니다.\"\n\n\"어째서요?\"\n\n\"저는 비록 태어날 때부터 남작은 아니었어도, 적어도 제 진짜 이름은 당글라르거든요.\"\n\n\"그래서, 그게 어떻다는 거요?\"\n\n\"하지만 그의 이름은 모르세르가 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요? 모르세르가 아니라니?\"\n\n\"전혀 아닙니다.\"\n\"계속해 보시오.\"\n\n\"저는 남작으로 만들어졌으니, 실제로 남작입니다. 그는 스스로를 백작으로 만들었으니, 사실 백작이 전혀 아니지요.\"\n\n\"그럴 리가!\"\n\n\"들어 보십시오, 친애하는 백작. 모르세르 씨는 지난 삼십 년간 제 친구였습니다. 아니, 정확히는 아는 사이였지요. 아시다시피 저는 제 문장을 한껏 활용해 왔습니다만, 제 출신을 결코 잊은 적은 없습니다.\"\n\n\"대단한 겸손이거나, 대단한 자만의 증거구려.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"제가 서기였을 때, 모르세르는 한낱 어부였습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 그때 그의 이름은……\"\n\n\"페르낭이었지요.\"\n\n\"그냥 페르낭이오?\"\n\n\"페르낭 몽데고입니다.\"\n\n\"확실하오?\"\n\n\"맙소사! 그자한테서 생선을 사도 어지간히 샀으니 이름쯤은 알지요.\"\n\n\"그런데 어째서 따님을 그에게 주려고 생각하셨소?\"\n\n\"페르낭과 당글라르가 둘 다 벼락출세한 자들이고, 둘 다 귀족이 되었고, 둘 다 부자이니, 그 값어치가 엇비슷하기 때문이지요. 다만 그자에 관해서는 저한테는 한 번도 나온 적 없는 어떤 말들이 떠돌았다는 점만 빼면요.\"\n\"무슨 말이오?\"\n\n\"오, 아무것도 아닙니다!\"\n\n\"아, 그렇군. 남작 말을 들으니 페르낭 몽데고라는 이름에 얽힌 무언가가 떠오르는구려. 그리스에서 그 이름을 들은 적이 있소.\"\n\n\"알리 파샤 사건과 얽혀서요?\"\n\n\"바로 그렇소.\"\n\n\"바로 그게 수수께끼입니다.\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"솔직히 그걸 알아낼 수만 있다면 무엇이든 내놓았을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"정말로 알고 싶다면 아주 쉬운 일일 텐데요?\"\n\n\"어떻게요?\"\n\n\"그리스에 거래처가 있을 것 아니오?\"\n\n\"있을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"야니나에도요?\"\n\n\"어디에든 있지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 야니나의 거래처에 편지를 써서, 알리 테펠리니의 참변에서 페르낭 몽데고라는 프랑스인이 어떤 역할을 했는지 물어보시오.\"\n\n\"옳은 말씀입니다.\" 당글라르가 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다. \"오늘 당장 편지를 쓰겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그러시오.\"\n\n\"그러겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 혹시 아주 추잡한 이야기라도 듣게 되거든……\"\n\n\"백작께 알려 드리지요.\"\n\n\"고맙겠소.\"\n\n당글라르는 방을 뛰쳐나가, 단숨에 자기 마차로 뛰어올랐다.\n\n# Chapter 67. The Office of the King’s Attorney\n\n은행가가 말들을 전속력으로 몰아대도록 내버려 두고, 우리는 당글라르 부인의 아침 나들이를 뒤따라가 보자. 앞서 말한 대로, 열두 시 반에 당글라르 부인은 말을 준비하라 일러두고 마차에 올라 집을 나섰다. 그녀는 생제르맹 구역 쪽으로 방향을 잡아 마자린가를 따라 내려가더니, 퐁뇌프 통로에서 멈추었다. 그녀는 마차에서 내려 통로를 가로질러 갔다. 옷차림은 아주 수수했는데, 아침나절에 산책하는 안목 있는 여인이라면 으레 그러할 법한 차림이었다. 그녀는 게네고가에서 삯마차를 불러, 마부에게 아를레가로 가자고 일렀다. 차에 앉자마자 그녀는 주머니에서 아주 두꺼운 검은 베일을 꺼내 밀짚 보닛에 둘러 묶었다. 그러고는 보닛을 다시 쓰고, 작은 손거울에 비친 자신의 모습을 보며 흐뭇해했다. 흰 살결과 빛나는 두 눈만 드러나 있었던 것이다. 삯마차는 퐁뇌프를 건너 도핀 광장을 지나 아를레가로 들어섰다. 마차 문이 열리자 마부에게 삯이 치러졌고, 당글라르 부인은 가뿐히 계단을 올라 곧 '잃어버린 발걸음의 방'에 다다랐다.\n\n그날 아침 법원에서는 무척 분주한 일들이 벌어지고 있었고, 일에 바쁜 사람들이 많았다. 일에 바쁜 사람들은 여인에게 거의 눈길을 주지 않는 법이라, 당글라르 부인은 변호사를 찾아온 여느 여인이나 다름없이 아무런 주목도 끌지 않고 홀을 가로질렀다.\n\n빌포르 씨의 대기실에는 사람들이 잔뜩 밀려 있었지만, 당글라르 부인은 이름조차 밝힐 필요가 없었다. 그녀가 모습을 드러내자마자 문지기가 일어나 그녀에게 다가오더니, 검사장과 약속한 분이 아니냐고 물었다. 그녀가 그렇다고 대답하자, 그는 별도의 통로로 그녀를 안내해 빌포르 씨의 집무실로 데려갔다.\n\n검사는 안락의자에 앉아 글을 쓰고 있었는데, 문을 등진 자세였다. 그는 문이 열리는 소리와 문지기가 \"들어가십시오, 부인\"이라고 하는 말, 그리고 다시 문이 닫히는 소리를 들으면서도 꼼짝하지 않았다. 그러나 문지기의 발소리가 잦아들기 무섭게 그는 벌떡 일어나 빗장을 지르고, 커튼을 치고, 방 안 구석구석을 살폈다. 그렇게 누구에게도 보이거나 들릴 염려가 없음을 확인하고 의혹에서 벗어나자, 그가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"고맙습니다, 부인. 시간을 지켜 와 주셔서요.\" 그는 당글라르 부인에게 의자를 권했고, 그녀는 그것을 받아들였다. 가슴이 어찌나 세차게 뛰던지 숨이 막힐 지경이었기 때문이다.\n\n\"참으로 오랜만입니다, 부인.\" 검사는 의자를 반원을 그리며 돌려, 당글라르 부인과 정면으로 마주 앉았다. \"부인과 단둘이 이야기를 나누는 기쁨을 누린 지가 참으로 오래되었습니다. 그런데 이렇게 다시 만난 자리가 하필 괴로운 이야기를 나누어야 하는 자리라니, 유감스럽습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇긴 하지만, 검사님, 보시다시피 저는 처음 부르심에 응해 이렇게 왔습니다. 비록 이 이야기가 검사님보다는 저에게 훨씬 더 괴로운 것임이 분명하지만요.\" 빌포르가 씁쓸하게 미소 지었다.\n\n\"그렇다면 사실이군요.\" 그는 상대에게 건네는 말이라기보다 제 생각을 소리 내어 읊조리듯 말했다. \"우리의 모든 행위가 우리가 걸어온 길 위에 흔적을 남긴다는 것이 사실이군요. 어떤 것은 슬프고, 어떤 것은 밝은 흔적을요. 우리 삶의 한 걸음 한 걸음이 모래 위를 기어가는 벌레의 자취와도 같다는 것이 사실이군요. 자취를 남기지요! 아아, 많은 이들에게 그 길은 눈물로 그려집니다.\"\n\n\"검사님,\" 당글라르 부인이 말했다. \"제 심정을 헤아려 주실 수 있겠지요? 그러니 부디 저를 가엾이 여겨 주십시오. 이 방을 둘러보노라면 — 수많은 죄인들이 떨면서 부끄러워하며 떠나간 이 방을, 지금 제가 떨면서 부끄러워하며 앉아 있는 이 의자를 바라보노라면 — 아, 제가 죄 많은 여인이 아니고 검사님이 위협하는 재판관이 아님을 스스로에게 납득시키려면 온 이성을 다 끌어모아야 한답니다.\"\n\n빌포르가 고개를 떨구고 한숨을 쉬었다.\n\n\"그리고 저는,\" 그가 말했다. \"제 자리가 재판관석이 아니라 피고석에 있다고 느낍니다.\"\n\n\"검사님께서요?\" 당글라르 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇습니다, 제가요.\"\n\"제 생각에는, 검사님, 처지를 너무 과장하시는 것 같군요.\" 당글라르 부인이 말했다. 그녀의 아름다운 두 눈이 한순간 반짝였다. \"방금 말씀하신 그런 길은 정열적인 상상력을 지닌 젊은이라면 누구나 걸어온 길이지요. 게다가 정념에 탐닉하면 쾌락 뒤에는 늘 회한이 따르는 법이고요. 그렇다 한들 검사님 같은 남자분들이 이런 일로 무엇을 두려워하시겠습니까? 세상은 너그러이 봐주고, 평판은 오히려 남자를 높여 주는걸요.\"\n\n\"부인,\" 빌포르가 대답했다. \"제가 위선자가 아니라는 것을, 적어도 까닭 없이 남을 속이는 사람이 아니라는 것을 부인도 아실 겁니다. 제 이마가 험상궂다면, 그것은 수많은 불행이 그 위에 그늘을 드리웠기 때문입니다. 제 심장이 돌처럼 굳어 있다면, 그것은 받은 타격들을 견뎌 내기 위함이었습니다. 젊은 시절의 저는 그렇지 않았습니다. 약혼하던 그날 밤, 우리 모두가 마르세유의 쿠르가에서 한 식탁에 둘러앉아 있던 그날의 저는 그렇지 않았습니다. 그러나 그 이후로 제 안의 모든 것, 제 둘레의 모든 것이 변했습니다. 저는 어려움에 맞서는 데 익숙해졌고, 그 싸움 속에서 제 길을 가로막는 자들을, 그들이 제 발로든 우연이든, 의도했든 아니든, 짓밟는 데 익숙해졌습니다. 우리가 가장 간절히 바라는 것일수록, 그것을 얻으려는 자들이나 우리가 빼앗으려는 자들에 의해 그만큼 간절히 가로막히는 것이 보통입니다. 그리하여 인간이 저지르는 잘못의 대부분은 필연이라는 그럴듯한 모습으로 위장한 채 우리 앞에 나타납니다. 그러다 흥분과 광란과 두려움의 한순간에 잘못을 저지르고 나면, 그제야 우리는 그것을 피하고 벗어날 수 있었음을 깨닫습니다. 눈먼 우리가 그때는 보지 못했던, 쓸 수도 있었던 방법들이 그제야 단순하고 쉬워 보이지요. 그러면 우리는 말합니다. '왜 나는 저렇게 하지 않고 이렇게 했던가?' 반면에 여인들은 회한에 시달리는 일이 드뭅니다. 결정이 부인들에게서 나온 것이 아니니까요. 부인들의 불행은 대개 떠안겨진 것이고, 부인들의 잘못은 남들이 저지른 죄의 결과일 뿐이지요.\"\n\n\"어찌 됐든, 검사님, 인정하셔야 할 것이 있습니다.\" 당글라르 부인이 대답했다. \"설령 그 잘못이 오롯이 저만의 것이었다 해도, 저는 어젯밤 그 죄에 대해 혹독한 벌을 받았으니까요.\"\n\n\"가엾은 분.\" 빌포르가 그녀의 손을 잡으며 말했다. \"부인의 기력으로 견디기에는 너무도 혹독했지요. 두 번이나 압도당하셨으니. 하지만 그래도——\"\n\n\"그래도요?\"\n\n\"그래도, 말씀드려야겠습니다. 용기를 다 끌어모으십시오. 아직 다 들으신 게 아니니까요.\"\n\n\"아,\" 당글라르 부인이 겁에 질려 외쳤다. \"더 들어야 할 게 있단 말씀인가요?\"\n\n\"부인은 과거만 돌아보고 계시지요. 사실 그것만으로도 충분히 끔찍합니다. 하지만 그보다 더 음울한 미래를 그려 보십시오. 분명 무시무시하고, 어쩌면 피로 물든 미래를요!\"\n\n남작 부인은 빌포르가 본래 얼마나 침착한 사람인지 알고 있었기에, 지금의 이 흥분이 그녀를 너무도 두렵게 하여 비명을 지르려 입을 벌렸으나, 소리는 목구멍에서 잦아들고 말았다.\n\n\"이 끔찍한 과거가 어떻게 다시 불려 나왔을까요?\" 빌포르가 외쳤다. \"그것이 어떻게 무덤 깊은 곳과 우리 마음속 후미진 자리에서, 그토록 깊이 묻어 두었던 그곳에서 빠져나와, 이제 와 유령처럼 우리를 찾아와 우리 두 뺨을 핏기 없이 만들고 우리 이마를 부끄러움으로 물들이는 걸까요?\"\n\n\"아아,\" 에르민이 말했다. \"필시 우연이겠지요.\"\n\n\"우연이라고요?\" 빌포르가 대답했다. \"아니요, 아닙니다, 부인. 우연 같은 것은 없습니다.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 우연이 맞아요. 운명적인 우연이 이 모든 걸 드러낸 게 아닌가요? 몬테크리스토 백작이 그 집을 산 것이 우연이 아니던가요? 그가 땅을 파게 한 것이 우연이 아니던가요? 그 불행한 아이가 나무들 밑에서 파헤쳐진 것이 우연이 아니던가요? 입 한번 맞춰 보지도 못한, 그러나 그토록 많고 많은 눈물을 흘리며 슬퍼했던 가엾고 죄 없는 제 핏줄이 말이에요. 아, 백작이 꽃 밑에서 발견된 그 가여운 잔해 이야기를 꺼냈을 때, 제 가슴이 그에게로 매달렸답니다.\"\n\n\"아니요, 부인, 그렇지 않습니다. 바로 이것이 제가 부인께 전해야 할 끔찍한 소식입니다.\" 빌포르가 공허한 목소리로 말했다. \"아닙니다, 꽃 밑에서는 아무것도 발견되지 않았습니다. 파헤쳐진 아이 같은 것은 없었습니다. 없었어요. 부인은 울어서는 안 됩니다, 아니, 신음해서도 안 됩니다, 부인은 떨어야 합니다!\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 말씀이세요?\" 당글라르 부인이 몸서리치며 물었다.\n\n\"몬테크리스토 씨가 그 나무들 밑을 파헤쳤을 때, 해골도 상자도 찾지 못했다는 뜻입니다. 둘 다 거기 없었으니까요!\"\n\n\"둘 다 거기 없었다고요?\" 당글라르 부인이 따라 말했다. 부릅뜬 채 휘둥그레진 두 눈이 그녀의 경악을 드러내고 있었다. \"둘 다 거기 없었다니!\" 그녀는 자신의 입에서 새어 나온 그 말의 뜻을 스스로에게 새기려 애쓰듯 다시 한번 말했다.\n\n\"없었습니다.\" 빌포르가 두 손에 얼굴을 파묻으며 말했다. \"없었어요, 백 번이라도 없었다 말하겠습니다!\"\n\n\"그렇다면 검사님이 그 가엾은 아이를 거기 묻으신 게 아니었나요? 어째서 저를 속이셨어요? 그 아이를 어디다 두셨어요? 말해 주세요. 어디에요?\"\n\n\"거기였습니다! 하지만 제 말을 들어 주십시오. 들어 주세요. 그러면 부인도 저를 가엾이 여기실 겁니다. 이십 년 동안 홀로, 부인께는 그 짐의 티끌만큼도 지우지 않고서, 이제 털어놓으려는 이 무거운 슬픔의 짐을 짊어져 온 저를 말입니다.\"\n\n\"아, 검사님은 저를 무섭게 하시는군요! 하지만 말씀하세요. 듣겠습니다.\"\n\n\"부인도 그 슬픈 밤을 기억하실 겁니다. 부인은 붉은 다마스크 천이 깔린 방의 그 침상에서 반쯤 숨이 넘어가고 있었고, 저는 부인 못지않게 마음을 졸이며 해산을 기다리고 있었지요. 아이가 태어났고, 제게 건네졌습니다. 미동도, 숨결도, 울음소리도 없었습니다. 우리는 아이가 죽은 줄 알았습니다.\"\n\n당글라르 부인이 의자에서 벌떡 일어서려는 듯 황급히 몸을 움직였으나, 빌포르가 멈춰 세우고는 그녀의 주의를 간청하듯 두 손을 맞잡았다.\n\n\"우리는 아이가 죽은 줄 알았습니다.\" 그가 거듭 말했다. \"저는 그 아이를 관 대신 쓸 상자에 넣었습니다. 정원으로 내려가 구덩이를 파고는 서둘러 던져 넣었지요. 흙으로 막 덮었을까 말까 했을 때, 코르시카인의 팔이 제 쪽으로 뻗어 왔습니다. 그림자 하나가 솟아오르는 것을 보았고, 동시에 번쩍하는 불빛이 일었습니다. 통증을 느꼈고, 소리를 지르려 했지만, 얼음장 같은 전율이 핏줄을 타고 흘러 제 목소리를 틀어막았습니다. 저는 숨이 끊긴 듯 쓰러졌고, 죽었다고 여겼습니다. 부인의 그 숭고한 용기를 저는 결코 잊지 못할 겁니다. 의식을 되찾은 제가 계단 아래까지 몸을 끌고 갔을 때, 부인은 거의 죽어 가는 몸으로 저를 맞으러 나오셨지요. 우리는 그 끔찍한 참사에 대해 입을 다물 수밖에 없었습니다. 부인은 유모의 부축을 받아 집으로 돌아갈 만큼의 굳센 마음을 지니고 계셨고요. 결투를 제 상처의 핑계로 둘러댔습니다. 우리로서는 거의 기대도 못 했지만, 우리의 비밀은 우리만의 것으로 남았습니다. 저는 베르사유로 옮겨졌고, 석 달 동안 죽음과 사투를 벌였습니다. 마침내 제가 목숨에 매달리는 듯 보이자, 남쪽으로 가라는 처방이 내려졌지요. 네 사람이 저를 파리에서 샬롱까지 떠메고, 하루에 6리외씩 걸어 옮겼습니다. 빌포르 부인은 자기 마차로 들것 뒤를 따랐고요. 샬롱에서 저는 손강에 배로 실렸고, 거기서 론강으로 옮겨 탄 뒤, 그저 물길을 따라 아를까지 내려갔습니다. 아를에서 다시 들것에 실려 마르세유까지 여정을 이어 갔지요. 회복하는 데 여섯 달이 걸렸습니다. 부인 이야기는 한마디도 듣지 못했고, 감히 부인의 소식을 물을 엄두도 내지 못했습니다. 파리로 돌아왔을 때, 저는 나르곤 씨의 미망인이 된 부인이 당글라르 씨와 결혼했다는 것을 알게 되었습니다.\n\n\"의식을 되찾은 이래로 제 생각을 사로잡은 것이 무엇이었겠습니까? 언제나 똑같았습니다. 언제나 그 아이의 시신이었지요. 밤마다 꿈속에 나타나 땅에서 솟아올라, 위협하는 눈빛과 몸짓으로 무덤 위를 맴돌았습니다. 파리로 돌아오자마자 저는 수소문했습니다. 그 집은 우리가 떠난 뒤로 사람이 살지 않았는데, 마침 구 년 계약으로 막 세를 놓은 참이었습니다. 저는 세입자를 찾아갔습니다. 제 장인 장모의 소유였던 집이 낯선 이들의 손에 넘어가는 것이 못마땅하다는 척했지요. 계약을 해지해 주면 보상을 하겠다고 제안했습니다. 그들은 6천 프랑을 요구하더군요. 저는 만 프랑이라도 줄 작정이었습니다. 2만 프랑이라도 줄 참이었지요. 마침 돈을 지니고 있었기에, 세입자에게 해지 증서에 서명을 받았고, 그토록 원하던 것을 손에 넣자마자 오퇴유로 말을 몰았습니다. 제가 떠난 뒤로 그 집에 발을 들인 사람은 아무도 없었습니다.\n\n\"오후 다섯 시였습니다. 저는 붉은 방으로 올라가 밤을 기다렸지요. 그곳에서, 일 년 내내 끊임없는 고뇌로 저를 어지럽혔던 온갖 생각들이 곱절의 힘으로 되살아났습니다. 저를 상대로 복수를 선포하고, 님에서 파리까지 저를 뒤쫓아 와, 정원에 숨어 저를 찌른 그 코르시카인 — 제가 구덩이를 파는 것을 보고, 제가 아이를 묻는 것을 본 그 사내가 — 부인의 신원을 알아낼 수도 있었습니다. 아니, 어쩌면 이미 알고 있었는지도 모릅니다. 그가 언젠가는 부인에게 이 끔찍한 비밀을 지킨 대가를 치르게 하지 않겠습니까? 제가 그의 단검에 죽지 않았음을 알게 된다면, 그에게는 그것이 달콤한 복수가 되지 않겠습니까? 그러므로 무엇보다 먼저, 어떤 위험을 무릅쓰고라도, 저는 과거의 모든 흔적을 없애야 했습니다. 물질적인 자취란 자취는 죄다 없애야 했지요. 어차피 제 기억 속에는 너무도 많은 현실이 영영 남아 있을 테니까요. 제가 계약을 해지한 것도, 제가 찾아온 것도, 제가 기다리고 있던 것도 모두 그 때문이었습니다.\n\n\"밤이 왔습니다. 저는 사위가 완전히 어두워지도록 내버려 두었습니다. 그 방에서 저는 불도 켜지 않았습니다. 바람이 모든 문을 흔들어 댈 때면, 그 문 뒤마다 첩자가 숨어 있을 것만 같아 끊임없이 떨었습니다. 사방에서 침상에 누운 부인의 신음이 제 등 뒤로 들려오는 것만 같아, 감히 뒤돌아볼 수가 없었습니다. 심장이 어찌나 세차게 뛰던지 상처가 터질까 두려웠습니다. 마침내 동네의 온갖 소음이 하나둘 그쳤습니다. 두려워할 것이 없음을, 누구에게도 보이거나 들리지 않으리라는 것을 깨닫고, 저는 정원으로 내려가기로 마음먹었습니다.\n\n\"들어 보세요, 에르민. 저는 제가 웬만한 사내들만큼은 용감하다고 자부합니다. 하지만 외투 속에서 찾아낸 그 계단의 작은 열쇠를 — 우리 둘이 그토록 소중히 여겼던, 부인이 황금 고리에 매달아 두고 싶어 했던 그 작은 열쇠를 — 품에서 꺼냈을 때, 문을 열고서 창백한 달이 나선 계단 위로 유령처럼 길게 흰 빛줄기를 드리우는 것을 보았을 때, 저는 벽에 기대선 채 하마터면 비명을 지를 뻔했습니다. 미쳐 가는 것만 같았지요. 마침내 저는 동요를 다스렸습니다. 한 계단 한 계단 내려갔지요. 도무지 어찌할 수 없었던 단 한 가지는 무릎이 묘하게 떨리는 것이었습니다. 저는 난간을 움켜쥐었습니다. 한순간이라도 손을 놓았더라면 그대로 굴러떨어졌을 겁니다. 저는 아래쪽 문에 다다랐습니다. 그 문 밖 벽에 삽 하나가 세워져 있더군요. 저는 그것을 집어 들고 덤불 쪽으로 나아갔습니다. 어둠 가리개가 달린 등불을 챙겨 둔 터였지요. 잔디밭 한가운데서 멈춰 그 불을 밝히고, 다시 가던 길을 이었습니다.\n\n\"십일월 말이라, 정원의 푸르름은 죄다 사라지고 없었습니다. 나무들은 길고 앙상한 팔뚝만 남은 해골에 지나지 않았고, 발밑 자갈 위로 마른 잎들이 바스락거렸습니다. 덤불에 다가갈수록 공포가 어찌나 저를 짓누르던지, 저는 주머니에서 권총을 꺼내 무장했습니다. 가지들 사이로 끊임없이 코르시카인의 형상이 보이는 것만 같았지요. 저는 어둠 가리개 등불로 덤불을 살폈습니다. 비어 있더군요. 주위를 찬찬히 둘러보았습니다. 정말로 저 혼자였습니다. 정적을 깨뜨리는 것이라곤 올빼미뿐이었는데, 그 날카로운 울음소리가 밤의 유령들을 불러내는 듯했습니다. 일 년 전, 구덩이를 파려고 멈춰 섰던 바로 그 자리에서 봐 두었던 갈라진 나뭇가지에 저는 등불을 매달았습니다.\n\n\"여름내 그곳에는 풀이 아주 무성하게 자라 있었고, 가을이 와도 그것을 베러 온 사람은 없었습니다. 그래도 풀이 듬성한 한 곳이 제 눈길을 끌었지요. 분명 제가 흙을 파헤쳐 놓았던 자리였습니다. 저는 일을 시작했습니다. 지난 일 년 내내 기다려 온 그 시간이 마침내 온 것이었지요. 제가 얼마나 파헤쳤는지, 얼마나 기대했는지, 삽에 무언가 걸리기를 바라며 잔디 한 조각 한 조각을 얼마나 내리쳤는지! 그러나 아니었습니다. 처음 것보다 두 배는 큰 구덩이를 팠는데도 아무것도 나오지 않았습니다. 제가 속았구나, 자리를 잘못 짚었구나 싶었습니다. 저는 몸을 돌려 나무들을 바라보며, 그때 제 마음에 새겨졌던 세세한 것들을 떠올리려 애썼습니다. 잎 떨어진 가지 사이로 차고 매서운 바람이 휘파람을 불었지만, 그래도 제 이마에서는 땀방울이 떨어졌습니다. 구덩이를 메우려 흙을 밟아 다지던 바로 그 순간에 칼을 맞았던 것이 떠올랐습니다. 그때 저는 금사슬나무에 몸을 기대고 있었고, 제 뒤로는 정원을 거니는 사람들이 쉬어 가도록 만든 인공 석가산이 있었지요. 쓰러지면서, 금사슬나무를 잡고 있던 손이 잡은 것을 놓치며 돌의 차가움을 느꼈습니다. 제 오른쪽에 나무가 보였고, 제 뒤에는 바위가 있었습니다. 저는 그때와 똑같은 자세로 서서 몸을 던졌지요. 그러고는 일어나 다시 구덩이를 파고 넓히기 시작했습니다. 그래도 아무것도, 아무것도 나오지 않았습니다. 상자는 더는 거기 없었던 겁니다!\"\n\n\"상자가 더는 거기 없었다고요?\" 당글라르 부인이 두려움에 목이 메어 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"제가 그 한 번의 시도로 만족했으리라고는 생각지 마십시오.\" 빌포르가 말을 이었다. \"아닙니다. 저는 덤불 전체를 뒤졌습니다. 그 살인자가 상자를 발견하고는 보물인 줄 알고 가져가려다가, 제 착각임을 깨닫고 다른 구덩이를 파 거기 묻어 둔 것이라고 생각했지요. 하지만 아무것도 찾지 못했습니다. 그러다 문득, 그가 그런 수고는 하지 않고 그저 구석에다 던져 두었으리라는 생각이 들었습니다. 그렇다면 날이 밝기를 기다려 다시 찾아봐야 했지요. 저는 방에 남아 기다렸습니다.\"\n\n\"오, 하느님!\"\n\n\"날이 밝자 저는 다시 내려갔습니다. 맨 먼저 덤불로 갔지요. 어둠 속에서 놓쳤던 흔적이라도 찾을 수 있기를 바랐습니다. 저는 가로세로 스무 자가 넘는 너비에 두 자 깊이로 땅을 파헤쳐 놓은 터였습니다. 인부라도 하루에 못 했을 일을 저는 한 시간 만에 해치운 것이지요. 하지만 아무것도, 정말 아무것도 찾을 수 없었습니다. 그래서 저는 다시 수색에 나섰습니다. 한쪽으로 던져졌다면 아마도 작은 쪽문으로 이어지는 길 위에 있으리라 싶었지요. 하지만 이 조사도 처음 것만큼이나 헛수고였고, 저는 터질 듯한 가슴을 안고 덤불로 돌아왔습니다. 이제 그곳에는 제게 아무런 희망도 남아 있지 않았습니다.\"\n\n\"오,\" 당글라르 부인이 외쳤다. \"그건 사람을 미치게 하기에 충분했겠어요!\"\n\n\"한순간 정말 미쳐 버렸으면 싶었습니다.\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"하지만 제게는 그런 행운조차 허락되지 않았지요. 그래도 기력과 생각을 추스르고는, '대체 왜 그자가 시신을 가져갔겠는가?' 하고 자문했습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 검사님은,\" 당글라르 부인이 대답했다. \"그자가 그걸 증거로 쓰려 했을 거라고 하셨잖아요.\"\n\n\"아, 아닙니다, 부인. 그럴 리 없습니다. 시신을 일 년씩 보관하지는 않으니까요. 시신은 판사에게 제시되고 증거 조사가 이루어지는 법입니다. 그런데 그런 일은 전혀 일어나지 않았습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 뭐란 말인가요?\" 에르민이 격하게 떨며 물었다.\n\n\"더 끔찍하고, 더 치명적이며, 우리에게 더 두려운 일입니다. 그 아이가 어쩌면 살아 있었고, 그 살인자가 아이를 구했을지도 모른다는 것이지요!\"\n\n당글라르 부인이 찢어지는 비명을 지르더니, 빌포르의 두 손을 부여잡고 외쳤다. \"제 아이가 살아 있었다고요?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"제 아이를 산 채로 묻으셨단 말인가요? 제 아이가 죽었는지도 확실하지 않은데 묻어 버리셨다고요? 아아——\"\n\n당글라르 부인은 자리에서 일어나 검사 앞에 마주 서서, 힘없는 손으로 그의 두 손을 비틀어 쥐고 있었다.\n\n\"저도 모릅니다. 다른 무엇이든 추측하듯, 그저 그렇게 추측할 뿐입니다.\" 빌포르가 대답했다. 그 눈길이 어찌나 한곳에 못 박혀 있던지, 그의 강인한 정신이 절망과 광기의 벼랑에 서 있음을 보여 주었다.\n\n\"아, 내 아이, 가엾은 내 아이!\" 남작 부인이 의자에 쓰러지며 손수건에 흐느낌을 틀어막고 외쳤다. 다소 마음을 추스른 빌포르는, 제 머리 위로 몰려오는 어미의 폭풍을 잠재우려면 자기가 느끼는 공포를 당글라르 부인에게 심어 주어야 한다는 것을 알아차렸다.\n\n\"그러니 부인도 아시겠지요, 만약 그렇다면,\" 그가 이번에는 자기가 일어나 남작 부인에게 다가서며, 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"우리는 끝장입니다. 그 아이가 살아 있고, 누군가 그 아이가 살아 있음을 알고 있습니다. 누군가 우리의 비밀을 쥐고 있는 겁니다. 그리고 몬테크리스토가 우리 앞에서 파헤쳐진 아이 이야기를 했는데, 정작 그 아이를 찾을 수 없었으니, 우리의 비밀을 쥐고 있는 자는 바로 그입니다.\"\n\n\"의로우신 하느님, 복수하시는 하느님!\" 당글라르 부인이 중얼거렸다.\n\n빌포르의 대답은 틀어막은 신음 하나뿐이었다.\n\n\"하지만 그 아이는, 그 아이는 어찌 됐나요, 검사님?\" 동요한 어미가 거듭 물었다.\n\n\"제가 그 아이를 얼마나 찾아 헤맸는지요.\" 빌포르가 두 손을 비틀며 대답했다. \"잠 못 이루는 긴긴밤마다 그 아이를 얼마나 불러 댔는지요. 백만 명의 사람에게서 백만 가지 비밀을 사들여 그 가운데서 제 비밀을 찾아낼 수 있도록, 왕의 재물을 얼마나 갈망했는지요! 마침내 어느 날, 백 번째로 삽을 집어 들었을 때, 저는 그 코르시카인이 아이를 어떻게 했을지 거듭거듭 자문했습니다. 아이는 도망자에게 짐이 됩니다. 어쩌면 아이가 아직 살아 있음을 알아채고는 강물에 던져 버렸을지도 모르지요.\"\n\n\"그럴 리 없어요!\" 당글라르 부인이 외쳤다. \"복수심에 다른 사람을 죽일 수는 있어도, 아이를 작정하고 물에 빠뜨려 죽이지는 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"어쩌면,\" 빌포르가 말을 이었다. \"그자가 아이를 기아 보호원에 맡겼을지도 모릅니다.\"\n\n\"오, 그래요, 그래요.\" 남작 부인이 외쳤다. \"내 아이가 거기 있군요!\"\n\n\"저는 보호원으로 달려갔고, 바로 그날 밤 — 구월 이십일 밤에 — 한 아이가 그곳에 맡겨졌다는 것을 알게 되었습니다. 일부러 반으로 찢은 고운 아마포 냅킨 조각에 싸여서요. 그 냅킨 조각에는 남작 관(冠)의 절반과 H라는 글자가 새겨져 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"정말, 정말이에요.\" 당글라르 부인이 말했다. \"제 아마포에는 죄다 그렇게 새겨져 있어요. 나르곤 씨는 남작이었고, 제 이름은 에르민이니까요. 오, 하느님, 그렇다면 제 아이가 죽지 않았던 거군요!\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 죽지 않았습니다.\"\n\n\"그러면서도 제가 기뻐서 죽을까 두려워하지도 않고 그 말씀을 하실 수 있나요? 그 아이는 어디 있나요?\"\n\n빌포르가 어깨를 으쓱했다.\n\n\"제가 그걸 알겠습니까?\" 그가 말했다. \"제가 안다면, 극작가나 소설가처럼 그 아이가 겪은 온갖 시련과 모험을 부인께 늘어놓을 것 같습니까? 아아, 아닙니다, 저는 모릅니다. 여섯 달쯤 뒤에 한 여인이 나머지 반쪽 냅킨을 들고 그 아이를 찾으러 왔습니다. 그 여인은 필요한 사항을 죄다 댔고, 아이는 그 여인에게 맡겨졌지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그 여인을 수소문하셨어야지요. 그 여인의 뒤를 쫓으셨어야지요.\"\n\n\"제가 어떻게 했을 것 같습니까? 저는 형사 사건을 꾸며 내, 가장 예리한 추적견 같은 자들과 가장 노련한 요원들을 죄다 동원해 그 여인을 찾았습니다. 그들은 샬롱까지 그 여인을 추적했지만, 거기서 놓치고 말았습니다.\"\n\n\"놓쳤다고요?\"\n\n\"네, 영영요.\"\n\n당글라르 부인은 이 이야기를 들으며 대목마다 한숨을, 눈물을, 비명을 쏟아 냈다. \"그게 다인가요?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"거기서 멈추신 거예요?\"\n\n\"오, 아닙니다.\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"저는 찾고 수소문하기를 결코 멈추지 않았습니다. 다만 지난 이삼 년은 제게 다소 휴식을 허락했지요. 하지만 이제는 그 어느 때보다 끈질기고 맹렬하게 다시 시작할 겁니다. 양심이 아니라 두려움이 저를 내모니까요.\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 당글라르 부인이 대답했다. \"몬테크리스토 백작은 아무것도 모를 거예요. 그렇지 않다면 저렇게 우리와 어울리려 들지 않았겠지요.\"\n\n\"오, 인간의 사악함은 참으로 큽니다.\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"하느님의 선하심을 능가할 만큼요. 그자가 우리에게 말할 때 그 눈을 살펴보셨습니까?\"\n\"아니요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그자를 유심히 지켜본 적이 있으십니까?\"\n\n\"변덕스러운 사람인 건 분명하지만, 그게 다예요. 한 가지만은 마음에 걸리더군요. 우리 앞에 차려 낸 그 진귀한 음식들 가운데 어느 것에도 그자가 손을 대지 않았다는 것이요. 우리를 독살하려는 게 아닐까 의심이 들 정도였지요.\"\n\n\"그러니 부인이 속으셨을 뻔한 거지요.\"\n\n\"네, 분명히요.\"\n\n\"하지만 제 말을 믿으십시오. 그자에게는 다른 속셈이 있습니다. 바로 그 때문에 제가 부인을 뵙고, 부인과 이야기를 나누고, 모든 사람을, 특히 그자를 경계하시라 일러 드리고 싶었던 겁니다. 말씀해 보십시오.\" 빌포르가 그 어느 때보다 더 굳게 그녀에게 시선을 못 박으며 외쳤다. \"누군가에게 우리 사이를 발설하신 적이 있습니까?\"\n\n\"아무에게도, 한 번도요.\"\n\n\"제 말뜻을 헤아려 주십시오.\" 빌포르가 다정하게 말했다. \"제가 누군가라고 할 때는 — 제 다그침을 용서하십시오 — 살아 있는 그 누구에게도 말입니다.\"\n\n\"네, 네, 잘 알아들었어요.\" 남작 부인이 내뱉었다. \"한 번도 없어요, 맹세코요.\"\n\n\"아침에 있었던 일을 저녁에 적어 두는 버릇이 있으셨습니까? 일기를 쓰십니까?\"\n\n\"아니요, 제 삶은 덧없는 일들로 흘러갔는걸요. 저 스스로도 그걸 잊고 싶답니다.\"\n\n\"잠꼬대를 하십니까?\"\n\n\"저는 아이처럼 곤히 잠든답니다. 기억 안 나세요?\"\n\n남작 부인의 얼굴에 붉은빛이 차올랐고, 빌포르는 무섭도록 창백해졌다.\n\n\"그렇지요.\" 그가 거의 들리지 않을 만큼 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"그래서요?\" 남작 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그래서, 이제 제가 무엇을 해야 할지 알겠습니다.\" 빌포르가 대답했다. \"지금부터 일주일도 안 되어 저는 알아낼 겁니다. 이 몬테크리스토라는 자가 누구인지, 어디서 왔는지, 어디로 가는지, 그리고 어째서 우리 앞에서 정원에 파묻혔던 아이 이야기를 떠벌리는지를요.\"\n\n빌포르는 이 말들을 어떤 어조로 내뱉었는데, 백작이 그 말을 들었더라면 몸서리를 쳤을 법한 어조였다. 그러고는 남작 부인이 마지못해 내미는 손을 잡고, 정중하게 그녀를 문까지 바래다주었다. 당글라르 부인은 다른 삯마차를 타고 그 통로로 돌아왔고, 통로 반대편에서는 그녀의 마차와, 그녀를 기다리며 마부석에서 평화로이 잠든 마부가 그녀를 맞았다.\n\n# Chapter 68. A Summer Ball\n\n당글라르 부인과 검사가 만나 이야기를 나누던 바로 그날, 여행용 마차 한 대가 엘데르 거리로 들어와 27번지 대문을 지나 안마당에 멈춰 섰다. 곧 문이 열리더니, 모르세르 부인이 아들의 팔에 기대어 내렸다. 알베르는 이내 어머니 곁을 떠나 말을 준비시키게 하고, 몸단장을 마친 뒤 샹젤리제에 있는 몬테크리스토의 저택으로 마차를 몰았다.\n\n백작은 여느 때처럼 미소를 띠고 그를 맞이했다. 이상한 일이지만, 그 누구도 이 사내의 호감 속으로 한 걸음도 더 들어서지 못하는 듯했다. 그의 마음으로 통하는 길을 억지로라도 열려는 사람들은 도무지 넘을 수 없는 장벽에 부딪히곤 했다. 두 팔을 벌리고 달려가던 모르세르도 다정한 미소에도 불구하고 다가갈수록 마음이 식어, 결국은 그저 손을 내밀 뿐이었다. 몬테크리스토는 변함없는 습관대로 그 손을 차갑게 쥐었다.\n\n\"왔습니다, 친애하는 백작님.\"\n\n\"무사히 돌아오셨군요.\"\n\n\"한 시간 전에 도착했습니다.\"\n\n\"디에프에서요?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 트레포르에서요.\"\n\n\"그렇습니까?\"\n\n\"그래서 곧장 백작님을 뵈러 온 겁니다.\"\n\n\"정말 친절하시군요.\" 몬테크리스토가 더없이 무심한 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 소식이라도 있습니까?\"\n\n\"이방인에게, 그것도 외국인에게 소식을 물으시면 안 되지요.\"\n\n\"압니다, 하지만 소식을 묻는다는 건, 그러니까 저를 위해 뭔가 해 주신 게 있느냐는 뜻입니다.\"\n\n\"제게 뭔가 맡기신 게 있던가요?\" 몬테크리스토가 곤란한 척하며 말했다.\n\n\"자, 자,\" 알베르가 말했다. \"그렇게 시치미 떼지 마십시오. 사람들이 그러더군요, 마음이 통하는 데에는 빠른 법이라고요. 트레포르에 있을 때 저는 전기에 닿은 듯한 충격을 느꼈습니다. 백작님이 저를 위해 일하고 계셨거나, 아니면 저를 생각하고 계셨던 거지요.\"\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"사실 당신을 생각하긴 했습니다. 하지만 제가 다루던 그 자기를 띤 전선은, 정말이지 제가 모르는 사이에 작동했답니다.\"\n\n\"정말요? 어떻게 된 일인지 어서 말씀해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 말씀드리지요. 당글라르 씨가 저와 함께 저녁을 들었습니다.\"\n\n\"압니다. 그 사람과 마주치지 않으려고 어머니와 제가 도시를 떠났던 거니까요.\"\n\n\"그런데 그가 여기서 안드레아 카발칸티 씨와 만났습니다.\"\n\n\"그 이탈리아 왕자 말입니까?\"\n\n\"그렇게 서두르지 마십시오. 안드레아 씨는 그저 자신을 백작이라 칭할 뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"칭한다고요?\"\n\n\"네, 칭한다는 말입니다.\"\n\n\"백작이 아니란 말입니까?\"\n\n\"제가 그에 대해 무얼 알겠습니까? 자신이 그렇게 부르니, 저도 당연히 같은 칭호로 부르고, 다른 사람들도 다 그렇게 하지요.\"\n\n\"참 이상한 분이시군요! 그래서 다음은요? 당글라르 씨가 여기서 저녁을 들었다고요?\"\n\n\"네, 카발칸티 백작과 그 아버지인 후작, 당글라르 부인, 빌포르 씨 부부 — 매력적인 분들이지요 — 그리고 드브레 씨, 막시밀리앙 모렐, 샤토르노 씨와 함께요.\"\n\n\"제 이야기를 했습니까?\"\n\n\"한마디도 안 했습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 더 나쁘군요.\" \"왜 그렇습니까? 저는 그 사람들이 당신을 잊어 주기를 바라는 줄 알았는데요?\"\n\n\"그들이 제 이야기를 하지 않았다면, 분명 속으로는 저를 생각했을 테니, 절망스럽지요.\"\n\n\"당글라르 양은 당신을 생각한 사람들 축에 끼지도 않았는데, 그게 무슨 상관입니까? 하기야, 그 아가씨라면 집에서 당신을 생각했을지도 모르겠군요.\"\n\n\"그런 걱정은 안 합니다. 설령 그랬다 해도, 제가 그녀를 생각하는 딱 그 정도였겠지요.\"\n\n\"가슴 뭉클한 공감이군요! 그러니까 두 분이 서로 미워한다는 말씀입니까?\" 백작이 말했다.\n\n\"들어 보십시오.\" 모르세르가 말했다. \"만일 당글라르 양이 저 때문에 순교라도 하는 양 여겨지는 제 처지를 가엾이 여겨, 우리 두 집안 사이의 혼인 절차를 죄다 없던 일로 해 준다면, 저는 기꺼이 그 합의에 응하겠습니다. 한마디로, 당글라르 양은 매혹적인 정부는 될 수 있어도, 아내라니 — 천만에요!\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그게 당신의 정혼자에 대한 생각이란 말입니까?\"\n\n\"네. 좀 야박한 말이라는 건 인정합니다만, 사실인걸요. 그러나 이 꿈은 이루어질 수 없으니, 당글라르 양은 제 정식 아내가 되어, 저와 평생을 함께 살며, 제게 노래를 들려주고, 제게서 열 걸음 떨어진 곳에서 시를 짓고 곡을 붙이겠지요. 그것도 제 평생토록요. 그러니 두렵습니다. 정부야 버릴 수 있지만, 아내라니 — 맙소사! 그 여자는 늘 곁에 있어야 하는 겁니다. 당글라르 양과 결혼한다는 건 끔찍한 일이지요.\"\n\n\"여간 까다로운 분이 아니시군요, 자작.\"\n\n\"네, 저는 곧잘 불가능한 것을 바라니까요.\"\n\n\"그게 무엇입니까?\"\n\n\"제 아버지가 찾으셨던 것 같은 아내를 만나는 일이지요.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 얼굴이 창백해지더니, 멋진 권총 몇 자루를 만지작거리며 알베르를 바라보았다.\n\n\"그럼 당신 아버님은 운이 좋으셨군요?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"제가 어머니를 어떻게 생각하는지 아시지 않습니까, 백작님. 어머니를 보십시오. 여전히 아름답고, 재치 있고, 그 어느 때보다 매력적이지요. 다른 아들이라면 트레포르에서 나흘이나 어머니와 함께 지내는 게 큰 양보거나 고역이었을 텐데, 저는 더 만족스럽고 더 평온한 마음으로 — 더 시적인 기분이라고 해야 할까요! — 돌아왔습니다. 마치 마브 여왕이나 티타니아를 길동무로 삼았던 것보다 더요.\"\n\n\"참으로 압도적인 증명이군요. 그렇게 말씀하시면 누구나 독신으로 살겠노라 맹세하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"제가 당글라르 양과 결혼하기 싫은 까닭이 바로 그겁니다. 무언가를 손에 넣는 순간 그 값어치가 얼마나 높아지는지 눈여겨보신 적 있습니까? 마를레나 포생의 진열창에서 반짝이던 다이아몬드도 막상 제 것이 되면 훨씬 더 찬란하게 빛나는 법이지요. 그러나 다른 것이 더 낫다는 걸 인정할 수밖에 없으면서도, 더 못한 쪽을 계속 지녀야 한다면, 그게 얼마나 견디기 힘든 일인지 아십니까?\"\n\n\"속물이로군.\" 백작이 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"그러니 외제니 양이 저를 그저 보잘것없는 티끌만 한 존재로, 그녀가 가진 수백만 프랑에 견주면 수십만 프랑도 못 가진 자로 여기게 되는 날이 오면, 저는 오히려 기뻐할 겁니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 미소 지었다. \"한 가지 계획이 떠올랐지요.\" 알베르가 말을 이었다. \"프란츠는 별난 것이라면 무엇이든 좋아하니까, 그를 당글라르 양과 사랑에 빠지게 만들어 보려 했습니다. 그래서 더없이 매혹적인 문체로 편지를 네 통이나 보냈는데, 그는 한결같이 이렇게 답하더군요. '내 별난 취향이 아무리 대단해도, 그것 때문에 약속을 깨지는 않겠네.'\"\n\n\"그게 바로 제가 말하는 헌신적인 우정이지요. 자기는 결혼하기 싫은 여자를 남에게 권하는 것 말입니다.\" 알베르가 미소 지었다.\n\n\"그건 그렇고,\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"프란츠가 곧 옵니다만, 당신에겐 관심 없는 일이겠지요. 백작님은 그를 싫어하시는 것 같으니까요?\"\n\n\"제가요?\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"친애하는 자작, 제가 프란츠 씨를 싫어한다는 걸 어떻게 알아내셨습니까? 저는 누구나 다 좋아합니다.\"\n\n\"그 '누구나'에 저도 들어가는 거지요 — 정말 감사하군요!\"\n\n\"오해는 마십시오.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"저는 하느님께서 명하신 대로, 그리스도인으로서 이웃을 사랑하듯 모두를 사랑합니다. 하지만 진심으로 미워하는 사람도 몇은 있지요. 다시 프란츠 데피네 씨 이야기로 돌아갑시다. 그가 온다고 하셨나요?\"\n\n\"네. 빌포르 씨가 불렀습니다. 당글라르 씨가 외제니 양을 시집보내고 싶어 안달하는 만큼이나, 그분도 발랑틴 양을 결혼시키고 싶어 안달이 난 모양입니다. 다 큰 딸을 둔 아버지 노릇이란 참 성가신 일인가 봅니다. 그 일을 마칠 때까지는 열에 들뜬 사람처럼 안절부절못하고, 맥박이 분당 아흔까지 뛰는 듯하니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 데피네 씨는 당신과 달리 그 불운을 묵묵히 견디는군요.\"\n\n\"그뿐이 아닙니다. 그는 그 일을 진지하게 이야기하고, 흰 넥타이를 매고, 자기 집안에 대해 늘어놓지요. 빌포르 씨 부부를 아주 높이 평가하고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그분들이 그럴 만하니까 그렇겠지요, 안 그렇습니까?\"\n\n\"그럴 만하다고 봅니다. 빌포르 씨는 늘 엄격하지만 공정한 사람으로 통해 왔으니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"가엾은 당글라르처럼 단죄하지 않는 사람도 하나쯤은 있는 셈이군요?\"\n\n\"아마 제가 그분 딸과 결혼할 처지가 아니라서겠지요.\" 알베르가 웃으며 대답했다.\n\n\"이런, 자작,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"정말이지 역겨울 만큼 멋이나 부리는 한량이시군요.\"\n\n\"제가 한량이라고요? 그게 무슨 말씀입니까?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다. 자, 시가나 한 대 피우시고, 그만 변명을 늘어놓으며 당글라르 양과의 결혼에서 벗어나려고 발버둥 치는 것도 그치시지요. 흘러가는 대로 두십시오. 어쩌면 그 말을 다시 주워 담을 필요가 없어질지도 모르니까요.\"\n\n\"천만에요!\" 알베르가 눈을 동그랗게 뜨고 말했다.\n\n\"틀림없이, 친애하는 자작, 당신을 억지로 끌고 가지는 않을 겁니다. 진지하게 묻는데, 그 약혼을 깨고 싶으십니까?\"\n\n\"그럴 수만 있다면 십만 프랑이라도 내놓겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 마음 푹 놓으십시오. 당글라르 씨는 같은 목적을 이루기 위해 그 곱절이라도 내놓을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"제가 정말 그렇게 운이 좋단 말입니까?\" 알베르가 말했다. 그러면서도 거의 알아챌 수 없는 그늘이 그의 이마를 스치는 것은 어쩔 수 없었다. \"하지만 친애하는 백작님, 당글라르 씨에게 무슨 까닭이라도 있습니까?\"\n\n\"아! 그게 바로 당신의 오만하고 이기적인 천성이지요. 남의 자존심은 도끼로 내려쳐 까발리면서, 정작 당신 것이 바늘로 살짝만 찔려도 움츠러드는군요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그래도 당글라르 씨는……\"\n\n\"당신을 마음에 들어 했다, 그 말이지요? 글쎄요, 그 사람은 취향이 형편없는 자라, 이제는 다른 사람에게 더 홀딱 빠져 있답니다. 누구인지는 저도 모릅니다. 직접 보시고 판단하시지요.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 알겠습니다. 그런데 제 어머니가 — 아니, 어머니가 아니라, 제가 잘못 말했군요 — 아버지가 무도회를 여실 작정입니다.\"\n\n\"이런 철에 무도회를요?\"\n\n\"여름 무도회가 유행이거든요.\"\n\n\"설령 유행이 아니더라도, 백작 부인께서 바라기만 하시면 곧 유행이 되겠지요.\"\n\n\"옳은 말씀입니다. 아시다시피 이건 가려 뽑힌 사람들만 모이는 자리지요. 칠월에 파리에 남아 있는 사람이라면 진짜 파리지앵일 테니까요. 카발칸티 부자에게 보낼 초대를 맡아 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"언제 열립니까?\"\n\n\"토요일입니다.\"\n\n\"카발칸티 씨의 아버지는 그때 떠나고 없을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 아들은 여기 있겠지요. 젊은 카발칸티 씨를 초대해 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"저는 그를 모릅니다, 자작.\"\n\n\"모르신다고요?\"\n\n\"네, 며칠 전에야 처음 봤을 뿐이고, 그에 대해 책임질 수 있는 입장도 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 댁에서 그를 맞이하시지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"그건 또 다른 문제입니다. 어느 훌륭한 신부님이 제게 추천하신 사람인데, 그 신부님도 속았을 수 있지요. 직접 초대장을 보내시되, 제게 그를 소개해 달라고는 마십시오. 만일 나중에 그가 당글라르 양과 결혼이라도 하게 되면, 당신은 제가 농간을 부렸다고 책망하며 결투를 청할 테니까요. 게다가 저는 거기 가지 못할 수도 있고요.\"\n\n\"어디서요?\" \"당신의 무도회 말입니다.\"\n\n\"왜 못 오신다는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"아직 저를 초대하지 않으셨으니까요.\"\n\n\"바로 그 일로 일부러 온 겁니다.\"\n\n\"참 친절하시군요. 하지만 사정이 생길지도 모르지요.\"\n\n\"한 가지만 말씀드리면, 어떤 핑계든 다 제쳐 두실 만큼 너그러워지실 텐데요.\"\n\n\"그게 무엇인지 말씀해 보십시오.\"\n\n\"어머니께서 꼭 와 주십사 청하셨습니다.\"\n\n\"모르세르 백작 부인께서요?\" 몬테크리스토가 흠칫하며 말했다.\n\n\"아, 백작님,\" 알베르가 말했다. \"정말이지 어머니는 제게 거리낌 없이 속을 털어놓으시는 분입니다. 만일 백작님이 아까 제가 말씀드린 그 공감의 가느다란 실이 마음속에서 떨리는 걸 느끼지 못하셨다면, 백작님께는 그런 게 아예 없는 거나 마찬가지일 겁니다. 지난 나흘 동안 우리는 오로지 백작님 이야기만 했으니까요.\"\n\n\"제 이야기를 하셨다고요?\"\n\n\"네, 그게 살아 있는 수수께끼가 치러야 할 대가지요!\"\n\n\"그럼 제가 당신 어머님께도 수수께끼란 말입니까? 저는 그분이 너무도 이성적인 분이라 상상에 휘둘리지 않으실 줄 알았는데요.\"\n\n\"수수께끼지요, 친애하는 백작님. 누구에게나요 — 제 어머니에게도, 다른 이들에게도 마찬가지입니다. 다들 골똘히 연구해도 풀지 못하니, 당신은 여전히 풀리지 않은 수수께끼로 남아 있습니다. 걱정 마십시오. 어머니는 그저 당신이 이토록 오래 풀리지 않는다는 데 놀라실 뿐입니다. G 백작 부인은 당신을 루스벤 경으로 여기지만, 어머니는 당신을 칼리오스트로나 생제르맹 백작쯤으로 상상하시는 것 같습니다. 기회가 닿거든 그 생각을 굳혀 드리십시오. 당신에게는 그쯤은 식은 죽 먹기일 겁니다. 한 사람의 철학과 다른 사람의 재치를 두루 갖추셨으니까요.\"\n\n\"일러 주셔서 감사합니다.\" 백작이 말했다. \"어떤 추측에든 대비하도록 애써 보지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 토요일에 오시는 거지요?\"\n\n\"네, 모르세르 부인께서 초대하셨으니까요.\"\n\n\"참 친절하시군요.\"\n\n\"당글라르 씨도 옵니까?\"\n\n\"아버지가 벌써 초대하셨습니다. 그 위대한 다게소 같은 빌포르 씨도 모셔 보려 애쓸 작정입니다만, 와 주실지는 별로 기대하지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"'무슨 일이든 결코 단념하지 말라'는 속담도 있지 않습니까.\"\n\n\"백작님은 춤추십니까?\"\n\n\"제가 춤을요?\"\n\n\"네, 백작님 말입니다. 그래도 이상할 거야 없지요.\"\n\n\"마흔이 넘기 전이라면 모를까요. 아니요, 저는 춤을 추지 않습니다. 다만 남이 추는 걸 보는 건 좋아하지요. 모르세르 부인은 춤을 추십니까?\"\n\n\"전혀 안 추십니다. 어머니와 이야기를 나누시면 됩니다. 어머니는 백작님과의 대화를 무척 즐기시니까요.\"\n\n\"정말입니까?\"\n\n\"네, 정말입니다. 장담하지요. 어머니가 그토록 관심을 가지고 이야기하시는 분은 백작님 말고는 없습니다.\" 알베르가 일어나 모자를 집어 들자, 백작은 그를 문까지 배웅했다.\n\n\"한 가지 제 자신을 나무랄 일이 있군요.\" 백작이 계단에서 알베르를 붙잡으며 말했다. \"그게 뭡니까?\"\n\n\"제가 당글라르에 대해 경솔하게 말했군요.\"\n\n\"천만에요, 그에 대해서라면 늘 그런 식으로 말씀해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"그 점에 대해 안심하게 되어 기쁩니다. 그건 그렇고, 데피네 씨는 언제쯤 오실 것 같습니까?\"\n\n\"늦어도 닷새나 엿새 뒤일 겁니다.\"\n\n\"결혼은 언제 합니까?\"\n\n\"생메랑 씨 부부가 도착하는 대로 곧바로요.\"\n\n\"제게 그를 데려와 주십시오. 제가 그를 좋아하지 않는다고 하셨지만, 정말이지 그를 만나면 반가울 겁니다.\"\n\n\"분부대로 하겠습니다, 백작님.\"\n\n\"그럼 안녕히 가십시오.\"\n\"토요일에 뵙는 거지요? 기다려도 되겠지요?\"\n\n\"네, 약속했으니까요.\" 백작은 손을 흔드는 알베르를 지켜보았다. 알베르가 사륜마차에 오르자, 몬테크리스토는 몸을 돌리다 베르투치오를 보고 말했다. \"무슨 소식이라도?\"\n\n\"부인께서 사법청에 가셨습니다.\" 집사가 대답했다.\n\n\"오래 머물렀나?\"\n\"한 시간 반쯤요.\"\n\n\"집으로 돌아갔고?\"\n\n\"곧장 돌아가셨습니다.\"\n\n\"좋아, 친애하는 베르투치오,\" 백작이 말했다. \"이제 자네에게 일러두는데, 전에 말했던 노르망디의 그 작은 영지를 알아보러 가게.\"\n\n베르투치오는 고개를 숙였다. 받은 명령이 자기 바람과 더없이 잘 맞아떨어졌으므로, 그는 그날 저녁으로 곧장 길을 떠났다.\n\n# Chapter 69. The Inquiry\n\n빌포르 씨는 당글라르 부인에게 한 약속, 곧 몬테크리스토 백작이 어떻게 오퇴유 저택의 내력을 알아냈는지 밝혀 보겠다는 약속을 지켰다. 그는 그날로 필요한 정보를 얻기 위해 보빌 씨에게 편지를 썼다. 보빌 씨는 형무소 감독관이었다가 경찰의 고위직으로 승진한 인물이었다. 보빌 씨는 누가 가장 자세한 사정을 알려 줄 만한지 정확히 알아보겠다며 이틀의 여유를 청했다. 이틀이 지났을 때 빌포르 씨는 다음과 같은 쪽지를 받았다.\n\n\"몬테크리스토 백작이라 불리는 인물은 윌모어 경과 막역한 사이입니다. 윌모어 경은 부유한 외국인으로 이따금 파리에서 모습을 보이는데, 지금도 파리에 있습니다. 그는 또 부소니 신부와도 아는 사이입니다. 부소니 신부는 시칠리아 출신 사제로, 동방에서 많은 선행을 베풀어 명망이 높습니다.\"\n\n빌포르 씨는 이 두 사람에 관해 가장 철저한 조사를 하라는 지시로 답했다. 명령은 그대로 이행되었고, 이튿날 저녁 그는 다음과 같은 상세한 보고를 받았다.\n\n\"신부는 파리에 머문 지 한 달밖에 되지 않았으며, 생쉴피스 성당 뒤편의 작은 이층집에 살고 있습니다. 층마다 방이 둘씩 있는데, 세입자는 그 한 사람뿐입니다. 아래층 두 방은 호두나무로 만든 식탁과 의자, 찬장을 갖춘 식당과, 장식도 양탄자도 시계도 없는 징두리널을 두른 응접실로 이루어져 있습니다. 신부가 꼭 필요한 물건에만 자신을 한정하고 있음이 분명합니다. 그는 위층의 거실을 즐겨 쓰는데, 그곳은 응접실이라기보다 서재에 가까웠습니다. 신학 서적과 양피지 문서들로 채워져 있었고, 시종의 말로는 신부가 몇 달씩 그 안에 파묻혀 지내기를 즐긴다고 합니다. 시종은 일종의 쪽문을 통해 방문객을 살피는데, 낯이 익지 않거나 마음에 들지 않는 얼굴이면 신부는 파리에 없다고 답합니다. 신부가 대단한 여행가로 알려진 까닭에, 그 대답에 대부분의 사람들이 수긍합니다. 게다가 집에 있든 없든, 파리에 있든 카이로에 있든, 신부는 늘 나누어 줄 무언가를 남겨 두며, 시종이 그 쪽문을 통해 주인의 이름으로 그것을 나누어 줍니다. 서재 옆의 다른 방은 침실입니다. 휘장 없는 침대 하나와 안락의자 넷, 노란 위트레흐트 벨벳을 씌운 긴 의자 하나가 기도대 하나와 더불어 가구의 전부였습니다.\n\n\"윌모어 경은 퐁텐생조르주 거리에 거주합니다. 그는 막대한 재산을 여행으로 탕진하는 영국 관광객 가운데 하나입니다. 그가 사는 집은 가구가 딸린 채로 빌린 것이며, 하루 중 몇 시간만 그곳에서 보낼 뿐 거기서 잠을 자는 일은 드뭅니다. 그의 특이한 버릇 하나는 프랑스어를 단 한마디도 입에 올리지 않는 것입니다. 다만 글로는 아주 유창하게 씁니다.\"\n\n이 중요한 정보가 검사에게 전해진 이튿날, 한 사내가 페루 거리 모퉁이에서 마차에서 내렸다. 그는 올리브색 문을 두드리며 부소니 신부가 안에 있느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"안 계십니다. 오늘 아침 일찍 나가셨습니다.\" 시종이 대답했다.\n\n\"나라면 늘 그 대답으로 만족할 수는 없을 텐데.\" 방문객이 대답했다. \"나는 누구든 집에 있어 주어야 마땅한 분의 명을 받고 왔으니 말이오. 그러니 수고스럽겠지만 부소니 신부께 전해——\"\n\n\"신부님은 안 계시다고 말씀드렸습니다.\" 시종이 거듭 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 돌아오시거든 이 명함과 봉인된 서류를 전해 주시오. 오늘 저녁 여덟 시에는 댁에 계시겠소?\"\n\n\"틀림없이 계실 겁니다. 일하고 계신 게 아니라면요. 일하실 때는 안 계신 것이나 마찬가지입니다만.\"\n\n\"그 시각에 다시 오겠소.\" 방문객은 이렇게 대답하고 물러갔다.\n\n약속한 시각에 같은 사내가 같은 마차를 타고 돌아왔다. 그런데 이번에는 마차가 페루 거리 끝에 서지 않고 곧장 초록색 문 앞까지 다가갔다. 그가 문을 두드리자 문이 곧바로 열리며 그를 들였다. 시종이 보이는 공손한 태도로 미루어, 그는 자신의 쪽지가 효과를 거두었음을 알아챘다.\n\n\"신부님은 댁에 계시오?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"네, 서재에서 일하고 계십니다만, 손님을 기다리고 계십니다.\" 시종이 대답했다. 낯선 사내는 투박한 계단을 올라갔다. 큼직한 갓이 빛을 한곳으로 모아 비추는 등불 아래 탁자 하나가 놓여 있었고, 방의 나머지는 어슴푸레한 어둠에 잠겨 있었다. 그 탁자 앞에서 그는 수도사 옷차림을 한 신부를 보았다. 신부는 중세의 학자들이 쓰던 것 같은 두건을 머리에 쓰고 있었다.\n\n\"부소니 신부님을 뵙는 영광을 누리는 것입니까?\" 방문객이 물었다.\n\n\"그렇습니다.\" 신부가 대답했다. \"그리고 당신은 전 형무소 감독관 보빌 씨가 경시총감의 뜻을 받들어 제게 보낸 분이로군요?\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"파리의 치안을 지키는 임무를 맡은 요원들 가운데 한 분이시고요?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\" 낯선 사내가 살짝 머뭇거리며, 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했다.\n\n신부는 눈만이 아니라 관자놀이까지 덮는 커다란 안경을 다시 고쳐 썼다. 그러고는 자리에 앉으며 방문객에게도 앉으라는 몸짓을 했다. \"무슨 일이신지 말씀하시지요.\" 신부가 뚜렷한 이탈리아 억양으로 말했다.\n\n\"제게 맡겨진 임무는 말입니다.\" 방문객이 머뭇거리며 말을 이었다. \"그것을 수행하는 자에게나, 그를 부린 분에게나 다 같이 은밀한 성격의 일입니다.\" 신부가 고개를 숙였다. \"신부님의 강직함은.\" 낯선 사내가 말을 이었다. \"경시총감께서도 익히 아시는 바라, 그분은 사법관으로서 공공의 안전과 관련된 몇 가지 사정을 신부님께 확인하고자 하십니다. 제가 그 일로 신부님을 뵙도록 파견된 것입니다. 부디 우정이나 인정에 매여 진실을 감추는 일이 없으시길 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"단, 알고자 하시는 일이 제 양심이나 신념에 어긋나지 않는다면 말입니다. 저는 사제입니다. 이를테면 고해의 비밀은 저와 하느님 사이에 머물러야지, 저와 인간의 정의 사이에 놓일 수 없는 법이지요.\"\n\n\"걱정 마십시오, 신부님. 신부님의 양심은 마땅히 존중하겠습니다.\"\n\n이때 신부가 갓의 한쪽을 눌러 반대편을 들어 올리니, 환한 빛이 낯선 사내의 얼굴로 쏟아지고 정작 신부 자신의 얼굴은 어둠에 잠겼다.\n\n\"실례합니다, 신부님.\" 경시총감의 사절이 말했다. \"빛이 눈을 너무 아프게 합니다.\" 신부가 갓을 다시 내렸다.\n\n\"자, 이제 듣고 있습니다. 말씀하시지요.\"\n\n\"곧장 본론으로 들어가겠습니다. 몬테크리스토 백작을 아십니까?\"\n\n\"자코네 씨를 말씀하시는 거겠지요?\"\n\n\"자코네라뇨? 그 사람 이름이 몬테크리스토가 아닙니까?\"\n\n\"몬테크리스토는 영지의 이름, 아니 좀 더 정확히는 바위의 이름이지, 가문의 성이 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"좋습니다, 그렇다 칩시다. 말꼬리를 잡고 다툴 일은 아니지요. 몬테크리스토 씨와 자코네 씨가 같은 사람이라면——\"\n\n\"완전히 같은 사람입니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 자코네 씨에 관해 이야기하지요.\"\n\n\"좋습니다.\"\n\n\"그를 아시느냐고 여쭈었습니다.\"\n\n\"아주 잘 압니다.\"\n\n\"그가 누구입니까?\"\n\n\"몰타의 부유한 조선업자의 아들입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇게들 말한다는 건 압니다. 하지만 아시다시피 경찰은 막연한 소문으로는 만족하지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇지만.\" 신부가 상냥한 미소를 띠며 대답했다. \"그 소문이 진실과 일치한다면, 경찰이든 누구든 다들 믿을 수밖에 없는 법이지요.\"\n\n\"확신하시는 바입니까?\"\n\n\"그 물음은 무슨 뜻입니까?\"\n\n\"이해해 주십시오, 신부님. 신부님의 진실됨을 조금도 의심하는 게 아닙니다. 다만 그것을 확신하시느냐고 여쭙는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"저는 그의 아버지 자코네 씨를 알았습니다.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇습니까?\"\n\n\"그리고 어렸을 적에 목재 야적장에서 그 아들과 자주 함께 놀았지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 백작이라는 작위는 어디서 얻은 것입니까?\"\n\n\"그런 건 살 수 있다는 걸 아실 텐데요.\"\n\n\"이탈리아에서요?\"\n\n\"어디서나 그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 막대한 재산은 어디서 났습니까?\"\n\n\"그리 대단한 재산은 아닐지도 모르지요.\"\n\n\"그가 얼마나 가졌다고 보십니까?\"\n\"연 십오만에서 이십만 리브르쯤 될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그건 그럴듯하군요.\" 방문객이 말했다. \"저는 그가 삼사백만은 가졌다고 들었습니다.\"\n\n\"연 이십만이면 자본으로 따져 사백만은 되는 셈입니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 저는 그가 연 사백만의 수입을 올린다고 들었습니다.\"\n\n\"그건 있을 법하지 않은 일입니다.\"\n\n\"몬테크리스토섬이라는 곳을 아십니까?\"\n\n\"물론입니다. 팔레르모나 나폴리, 로마에서 배로 프랑스에 온 사람이라면 누구나 알지요. 그 곁을 바짝 지나며 보았을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"듣자 하니 아주 멋진 곳이라던데요?\"\n\n\"바위덩어리입니다.\"\n\n\"그런데 백작은 어찌하여 바위를 샀을까요?\"\n\n\"백작이 되기 위해서지요. 이탈리아에서는 백작이 되려면 영지를 가져야 하니까요.\"\n\n\"자코네 씨가 젊은 시절에 겪은 일들에 대해서는 분명 들으셨겠지요?\"\n\n\"아버지 말입니까?\"\n\n\"아니요, 아들 말입니다.\"\n\n\"확실한 건 아무것도 모릅니다. 그 무렵 저는 어린 시절의 동무를 시야에서 놓쳤으니까요.\"\n\n\"그는 전쟁에 나갔습니까?\"\n\n\"군에 들어간 걸로 압니다.\"\n\n\"어느 병과에요?\"\n\n\"해군이었습니다.\"\n\n\"신부님은 그의 고해 신부가 아니십니까?\"\n\n\"아닙니다. 제가 알기로 그는 루터교도입니다.\"\n\n\"루터교도라고요?\"\n\n\"그런 것 같다는 말이지, 단언하는 건 아닙니다. 게다가 프랑스에서는 신앙의 자유가 보장되어 있지 않습니까.\"\n\n\"물론입니다. 지금 우리가 캐묻는 건 그의 신앙이 아니라 그의 행적이지요. 경시총감의 이름으로 여쭙겠습니다. 그에 대해 무엇을 알고 계십니까?\"\n\n\"그는 대단히 자선심이 깊은 사람으로 통합니다. 우리의 성부이신 교황께서는 그가 동방에서 기독교도들에게 베푼 공로를 기려 그를 그리스도 기사로 봉하셨습니다. 또 동방의 군주들에게서 공로의 증표로 받은 반지가 대여섯 개나 됩니다.\"\n\n\"그것들을 끼고 다닙니까?\"\n\n\"아니요. 하지만 그것들을 자랑스러워하지요. 그는 인류를 해친 자에게 주는 상보다 인류에게 은혜를 베푼 자에게 주는 상을 더 기뻐합니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 퀘이커교도입니까?\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다. 독특한 복장만 빼면 그는 퀘이커교도지요.\"\n\n\"그에게 벗이 있습니까?\"\n\n\"네, 그를 아는 사람은 누구나 그의 벗입니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 적도 있습니까?\"\n\n\"단 하나 있습니다.\"\n\n\"이름이 무엇입니까?\"\n\n\"윌모어 경입니다.\"\n\n\"그는 어디에 있습니까?\"\n\n\"지금 파리에 있습니다.\"\n\n\"제게 자세한 사정을 알려 줄 수 있을까요?\"\n\n\"중요한 사정을요. 그는 자코네와 함께 인도에 있었으니까요.\"\n\n\"그가 사는 곳을 아십니까?\"\n\n\"쇼세당탱 어디쯤입니다. 다만 거리도 번지도 모릅니다.\"\n\n\"그 영국인과 사이가 좋지 않으십니까?\"\n\n\"저는 자코네를 사랑하고, 그는 자코네를 미워합니다. 그러니 우리는 벗이 아니지요.\"\n\n\"몬테크리스토 백작이 이번에 파리를 찾기 전에 프랑스에 온 적이 있다고 보십니까?\"\n\n\"그 물음에는 분명히 답할 수 있습니다. 아니요, 온 적이 없습니다. 반년 전에 그가 필요한 정보를 얻으려고 제게 청해 왔는데, 제가 언제 다시 파리에 올지 알 수 없어 카발칸티 씨를 그에게 소개해 주었으니까요.\"\n\"안드레아 말입니까?\"\n\n\"안드레아 말입니까?\" \"아니요, 그의 아버지 바르톨로메오입니다.\"\n\n\"자, 신부님, 이제 한 가지만 더 여쭙겠습니다. 명예와 인정과 종교의 이름으로 청하건대, 솔직히 답해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"무엇입니까?\"\n\n\"몬테크리스토 씨가 무슨 의도로 오퇴유에 저택을 샀는지 아십니까?\"\n\n\"물론입니다. 그가 제게 말해 주었으니까요.\"\n\n\"무엇입니까?\"\n\n\"그것을 정신병자 요양원으로 만들겠다고 했습니다. 피사니 백작이 팔레르모에 세운 것과 같은 곳으로 말이지요. 그 시설에 대해 아십니까?\"\n\n\"들어 본 적이 있습니다.\"\n\n\"훌륭한 자선 사업이지요.\" 이렇게 말한 뒤 신부는 다시 학문에 몰두하고 싶다는 뜻으로 고개를 숙였다.\n\n방문객은 신부의 뜻을 알아챘는지, 아니면 더 물을 것이 없었는지, 자리에서 일어섰다. 신부가 그를 문까지 배웅했다.\n\n\"신부님은 대단한 자선가이십니다.\" 방문객이 말했다. \"부유하시다고들 하지만, 그래도 신부님의 가난한 이들을 위해 제가 무언가를 드려도 될지요. 제 성의를 받아 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"고맙습니다. 다만 저는 한 가지에 대해서만큼은 욕심이 있습니다. 제가 베푸는 구호는 오롯이 제 재산에서만 나와야 한다는 것이지요.\"\n\n\"그렇지만——\"\n\n\"제 결심은 변함이 없습니다. 다만 직접 찾아보시기만 하면, 안타깝게도 선행을 베풀 대상은 차고 넘치도록 많을 겁니다.\"\n\n신부가 문을 열며 다시 한번 고개를 숙였고, 낯선 사내도 고개를 숙이며 작별을 고했다. 마차는 그를 곧장 빌포르 씨의 저택으로 데려갔다. 한 시간 뒤 마차가 다시 불려 나왔고, 이번에는 퐁텐생조르주 거리로 가서 윌모어 경이 사는 5번지 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 낯선 사내는 윌모어 경에게 면담을 청하는 편지를 미리 보냈고, 윌모어 경은 열 시로 시각을 정해 두었다. 경시총감의 사절이 열 시 십 분 전에 도착하자, 정확함과 시간 엄수의 화신과도 같은 윌모어 경이 아직 돌아오지 않았으나 시계가 정각을 칠 때면 틀림없이 돌아올 것이라는 말을 들었다.\n\n방문객은 응접실로 안내되었다. 그곳은 여느 가구 딸린 응접실과 다를 바 없었다. 현대식 세브르 도자기 꽃병 둘이 놓인 벽난로 선반, 활을 당긴 큐피드를 본뜬 시계, 양옆에 판화가 하나씩 걸린 거울——하나는 길잡이를 업은 호메로스를, 다른 하나는 구걸하는 벨리사리우스를 그린 것이었다——거무스름한 회색 벽지, 붉고 검은 벽걸이 천. 윌모어 경의 응접실은 그런 모습이었다.\n\n응접실은 간유리 갓을 씌운 등불로 밝혀져 있었는데, 마치 사절의 약한 시력을 배려하기라도 한 듯 희미한 빛만을 내고 있었다. 십 분쯤 기다리자 시계가 열 시를 쳤다. 다섯 번째 종소리에 문이 열리며 윌모어 경이 나타났다. 그는 중키를 조금 넘는 키에, 불그스름한 구레나룻이 성기게 났고, 살갗이 희며 머리칼은 옅은 빛깔로 희끗희끗 세어 가고 있었다. 그는 영국풍 특유의 차림을 빠짐없이 갖추고 있었다. 곧 도금 단추가 달리고 깃을 높이 세운 1811년식 파란 코트에, 흰색 커지미어 조끼, 그리고 세 치쯤 짧은 난징 무명 바지를 입었는데, 바지는 멜빵끈 덕분에 무릎까지 말려 올라가지 않았다. 그가 들어서며 던진 첫마디는 이러했다.\n\n\"아시겠지만, 나는 프랑스어를 하지 않소.\"\n\n\"우리 말로 대화하시는 걸 좋아하지 않으신다는 건 압니다.\" 사절이 대답했다.\n\n\"하지만 당신은 써도 좋소.\" 윌모어 경이 대답했다. \"나는 알아듣소.\"\n\n\"저도.\" 방문객이 말을 바꾸며 대답했다. \"대화를 이어 갈 만큼은 영어를 압니다. 조금도 불편하실 것 없습니다.\"\n\n\"어?\" 윌모어 경이 영국 토박이들만이 낼 수 있는 그 어조로 말했다.\n\n사절이 소개장을 내밀자, 윌모어 경은 영국인다운 냉정함으로 그것을 읽고 나서 말했다.\n\n\"잘 알겠소.\" 그가 말했다. \"완벽하게 알겠소.\"\n\n그러고 나서 질문이 시작되었는데, 부소니 신부에게 던졌던 것과 비슷했다. 다만 윌모어 경은 백작의 적이라는 처지였으므로 답하는 데 거리낌이 덜했고, 그래서 질문도 더 많아졌다. 그는 몬테크리스토의 젊은 시절을 들려주었다. 그의 말로는, 자코네는 열 살에 영국과 전쟁을 벌이던 인도의 어느 소군주를 섬기기 시작했다고 했다. 윌모어가 그를 처음 만나 맞서 싸운 곳이 바로 거기였다. 그 전쟁에서 자코네는 포로로 잡혀 영국으로 보내져 죄수선에 갇혔으나, 헤엄을 쳐서 탈출했다. 그리하여 그의 떠돌이 생활과 결투와 변덕스러운 행각이 시작되었다. 그러다 그리스에서 봉기가 일어나자 그는 그리스 군대에 들어가 싸웠다. 그곳에서 복무하는 동안 그는 테살리아의 산속에서 은광을 발견했으나, 그것을 누구에게도 알리지 않으려 조심했다. 나바리노 해전 뒤 그리스 정부가 안정되자, 그는 오톤 왕에게 그 지역의 광산 채굴권을 청해 받아 냈다. 바로 거기서 그 막대한 재산이 나온 것이었다. 윌모어 경의 생각으로는 어쩌면 연 백만에서 이백만에 이를 수도 있겠으나, 광산이 바닥나면 한순간에 잃을 수도 있는 불안정한 재산이었다.\n\n\"그런데.\" 방문객이 물었다. \"그가 프랑스에 온 까닭을 아십니까?\"\n\n\"철도에 투기하고 있소.\" 윌모어 경이 말했다. \"게다가 그는 뛰어난 화학자이자 물리학자라, 새로운 전신 체계를 발명해 그것을 완성해 보려 애쓰고 있소.\"\n\n\"해마다 얼마나 씁니까?\" 총감이 물었다.\n\n\"기껏해야 오륙십만 프랑이오.\" 윌모어 경이 말했다. \"그는 구두쇠요.\" 이 영국인은 분명 증오에 차 있었다. 백작에게 달리 씌울 흠을 찾지 못하자 인색하다고 몰아붙이는 것이었다.\n\n\"오퇴유에 있는 그의 집을 아십니까?\"\n\n\"물론이오.\"\n\n\"그 집에 대해 무엇을 알고 계십니까?\"\n\n\"그가 왜 그 집을 샀는지 알고 싶소?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"백작은 투기꾼이라, 틀림없이 이런저런 실험으로 제 몸을 망칠 거요. 그는 자기가 산 집 근처에 바녜르나 뤼숑, 코트레의 것에 견줄 만한 광천이 있다고 믿고 있소. 그래서 그 집을 독일 사람들이 말하는 이른바 '바트하우스', 곧 온천장으로 바꾸려는 거요. 그는 그 유명한 샘을 찾겠다고 벌써 정원을 두세 번이나 죄다 파헤쳤는데, 끝내 찾지 못했으니 머지않아 이웃한 집들까지 모조리 사들일 거요. 한데 나는 그를 싫어하는지라, 그의 철도든, 전신이든, 온천 찾기든 그를 파멸시키기를 바라며 그 낭패를 지켜보고 있소. 그런 날이 곧 닥칠 게요.\"\n\n\"두 분이 다투신 까닭은 무엇입니까?\"\n\n\"그가 영국에 있을 때 내 벗 가운데 한 사람의 아내를 유혹했소.\"\n\n\"어찌하여 복수하지 않으십니까?\"\n\n\"이미 그와 결투를 세 번이나 했소.\" 영국인이 말했다. \"처음에는 권총으로, 두 번째는 검으로, 세 번째는 사브르로.\"\n\n\"그 결투들의 결과는 어떻게 되었습니까?\"\n\n\"처음에는 그가 내 팔을 부러뜨렸고, 두 번째는 가슴에 상처를 입혔으며, 세 번째에는 이렇게 큰 상처를 냈소.\" 영국인이 셔츠 깃을 내려 흉터를 보였는데, 그 붉은빛으로 보아 최근에 생긴 것임을 알 수 있었다. \"그러니 보다시피 우리 사이에는 목숨을 건 원한이 있는 거요.\"\n\n\"하지만.\" 사절이 말했다. \"제가 제대로 이해했다면, 그를 죽이려는 방식치고는 영 옳은 길이 아니신 듯합니다.\"\n\n\"어?\" 영국인이 말했다. \"나는 날마다 사격을 연습하고, 이틀에 한 번꼴로 그리지에가 우리 집에 와 주오.\"\n\n이것이 방문객이 알아내고자 한 전부였다. 아니, 좀 더 정확히 말하면 영국인이 아는 것의 전부인 듯했다. 요원이 자리에서 일어나 윌모어 경에게 고개를 숙이자, 윌모어 경도 영국인다운 뻣뻣한 정중함으로 인사를 받았고, 요원은 물러갔다. 윌모어 경은 등 뒤로 문이 닫히는 소리를 듣고는 침실로 돌아갔다. 그러고는 한 손으로 옅은 빛깔의 머리칼과 붉은 구레나룻, 가짜 턱과 상처를 떼어 내고, 다시 몬테크리스토 백작의 검은 머리와 가무잡잡한 살갗, 진주 같은 이를 되찾았다.\n\n빌포르 씨의 저택으로 돌아온 것은 경시총감의 사절이 아니라 빌포르 씨 자신이었다. 검사는 정작 만족스러운 것은 아무것도 알아내지 못했으면서도 한결 마음이 놓였고, 오퇴유의 만찬 이래 처음으로 깊은 잠을 잤다.\n\n# Chapter 70. The Ball\n\n칠월의 가장 무더운 날들이었다. 그리고 때가 무르익어, 모르세르 씨 댁에서 무도회가 열리기로 한 토요일이 찾아왔다. 밤 열 시였다. 백작 저택의 정원에 선 큰 나무들의 가지가, 황금빛 별들이 점점이 박힌 푸른 하늘의 천장을 배경으로 또렷이 솟아 있었다. 그 하늘에는 잦아드는 폭풍의 마지막 구름 조각들이 아직 머뭇거리고 있었다.\n\n일층의 방들에서는 음악 소리가, 왈츠와 갈로프의 소용돌이와 함께 들려왔고, 베네치아풍 덧창의 틈새로 눈부신 빛줄기가 새어 나왔다. 그 순간 정원에는 하인 열 명쯤만 나와 있었다. 그들은 방금 안주인에게서 저녁 식사를 준비하라는 분부를 받은 참이었다. 날씨가 점점 더 화창해지고 있었다. 지금까지도 저녁 식사를 식당에서 차릴지, 잔디밭에 세운 긴 천막 아래서 차릴지 정하지 못한 상태였다. 그러나 별이 점점이 박힌 아름다운 푸른 하늘이 잔디밭 쪽으로 결판을 내 주었다.\n\n정원은 이탈리아 풍습에 따라 색색의 등불로 환히 밝혀졌고, 식탁의 사치 — 온전한 형태를 갖추기로는 모든 사치 가운데 가장 드문 그 사치 — 가 잘 이해되는 나라들에서 으레 그렇듯, 만찬 식탁은 밀랍 촛불과 꽃들로 가득했다.\n\n분부를 마치고 모르세르 백작 부인이 방으로 돌아왔을 때, 손님들이 많이 도착하고 있었다. 그들은 백작의 높은 지위보다 백작 부인의 매혹적인 환대에 더 이끌린 사람들이었다. 메르세데스의 빼어난 취향 덕분에, 그녀의 연회에서는 묘사할 만한, 필요하다면 본떠 볼 만한 무언가를 반드시 발견하게 마련이었으니 말이다.\n\n앞서 우리가 이야기한 사건들로 깊은 불안에 빠져 있던 당글라르 부인은 모르세르 부인 댁에 갈지 망설이고 있었는데, 그날 아침 그녀의 마차가 우연히 빌포르의 마차와 마주쳤다. 빌포르가 신호를 보냈고, 두 마차가 바짝 다가서자 그가 말했다.\n\n\"모르세르 부인 댁에 가시는 길이지요, 그렇지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"아니요.\" 당글라르 부인이 대답했다. \"몸이 너무 안 좋아서요.\"\n\n\"그건 잘못된 생각입니다.\" 빌포르가 의미심장하게 대답했다. \"거기서 부인의 모습을 보이는 것이 중요합니다.\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각하세요?\" 남작 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 가야겠군요.\"\n\n그리고 두 마차는 각자의 목적지를 향해 지나갔다. 그리하여 당글라르 부인은 아름다운 자태로, 게다가 화려함으로 눈부시게 빛나며 도착했다. 메르세데스가 한쪽 문에 나타나는 바로 그 순간, 그녀는 다른 문으로 들어섰다. 백작 부인은 알베르를 데리고 당글라르 부인을 맞으러 갔다. 알베르가 다가가 부인의 차림새에 마땅한 찬사를 몇 마디 건넨 뒤, 자리로 안내하려고 팔을 내밀었다. 알베르가 주위를 둘러보았다.\n\n\"제 딸을 찾으시나요?\" 남작 부인이 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"솔직히 그렇습니다.\" 알베르가 대답했다. \"설마 따님을 데려오지 않으시는 모진 일을 하셨을 리가요?\"\n\n\"안심하세요. 빌포르 양과 마주쳐서 그 아이 팔을 잡고 있답니다. 보세요, 우리 뒤를 따라오고 있어요. 둘 다 흰 드레스를 입었는데, 하나는 동백꽃 다발을, 다른 하나는 물망초 다발을 들었지요. 그런데 말해 봐요……\"\n\n\"네, 무엇을 알고 싶으신지요?\"\n\n\"오늘 밤 몬테 크리스토 백작이 여기 오시나요?\"\n\n\"열일곱!\" 알베르가 대답했다.\n\n\"무슨 말씀이세요?\"\n\n\"백작이 굉장한 화제인 모양이라는 말입니다.\" 자작이 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"그리고 부인이 제게 같은 질문을 한 열일곱 번째 분이라는 말이고요. 백작은 유행이지요. 그 점은 그에게 축하를 보냅니다.\"\n\n\"그래서 모두에게 제게 한 것처럼 대답하셨나요?\"\n\n\"아, 그러고 보니 부인께는 대답을 안 했군요. 안심하세요, 우리는 이 '명사'를 모실 겁니다. 우리는 특권을 누리는 사람들 축에 들거든요.\"\n\n\"어제 오페라에 가셨어요?\"\n\"아니요.\"\n\n\"그분이 거기 계셨어요.\"\n\n\"아, 정말요? 그래서 그 별난 양반이 또 무슨 새로운 기행을 벌이던가요?\"\n\n\"그분이 그런 일 없이 사람들 눈에 띌 수가 있나요? 엘슬레르가 '절름발이 악마'를 추고 있었고, 그리스 공주는 황홀경에 빠져 있었지요. 카추차가 끝난 뒤, 그분이 화려한 반지 하나를 꽃다발 줄기에 끼워 그 매혹적인 무희에게 던졌어요. 그러자 무희가 삼막에서 그 선물에 경의를 표하느라 손가락에 반지를 끼고 다시 나왔답니다. 그런데 그리스 공주는 — 오늘 여기 오나요?\"\n\n\"아니요, 그 즐거움은 누리지 못하실 겁니다. 백작 집안에서 그녀의 위치가 충분히 알려지지 않았거든요.\"\n\n\"잠깐만요, 저는 여기 두고 빌포르 부인께 가서 말씀을 나누세요. 당신 시선을 끌려고 하고 계시잖아요.\"\n\n알베르는 당글라르 부인에게 고개 숙여 인사한 뒤 빌포르 부인 쪽으로 다가갔다. 그가 다가가자 부인이 입을 열었다.\n\n\"무엇이든 걸겠습니다.\" 알베르가 부인의 말을 가로막으며 말했다. \"방금 무슨 말씀을 하시려던 건지 제가 압니다.\"\n\n\"그래요, 그게 뭔가요?\"\n\n\"제가 옳게 맞히면, 인정하시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"명예를 걸고요?\"\n\n\"명예를 걸고요.\"\n\n\"몬테 크리스토 백작이 도착했는지, 아니면 올 예정인지 물으시려던 거지요.\"\n\n\"전혀 아니에요. 지금 제가 생각하는 건 그분이 아니랍니다. 프란츠 씨에게서 무슨 소식이라도 받으셨는지 물으려던 거예요.\"\n\n\"네, 어제 받았습니다.\"\n\n\"뭐라고 하던가요?\"\n\n\"편지와 같은 시기에 떠난다고요.\"\n\n\"그럼, 이제 백작은요?\"\n\n\"백작은 올 겁니다. 그 점은 안심하셔도 됩니다.\"\n\n\"그분에게 몬테 크리스토 말고 다른 이름이 있는 거 아세요?\"\n\n\"아니요, 몰랐습니다.\"\n\n\"몬테 크리스토는 섬 이름이고, 그분에겐 따로 성이 있어요.\"\n\n\"전혀 들어 본 적이 없습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 제가 당신보다 더 소식에 밝네요. 그분 이름은 자코네예요.\"\n\n\"그럴 수도 있겠지요.\"\n\n\"몰타 사람이고요.\"\n\n\"그것도 그럴 수 있고요.\"\n\n\"어느 선주의 아들이래요.\"\n\n\"정말이지 그걸 다 큰 소리로 이야기하셔야겠습니다. 대단한 성공을 거두실 텐데요.\"\n\n\"인도에서 복무했고, 테살리아에서 광산 하나를 발견했으며, 오퇴유에 광천 요양소를 세우려고 파리에 온 거래요.\"\n\n\"이거 참,\" 모르세르가 말했다. \"정말 새로운 소식이군요! 제가 이걸 옮겨도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"네, 하지만 조심해서요. 한 번에 한 가지씩만 말하고, 제가 했다는 말은 하지 마세요.\"\n\n\"왜 그러시지요?\"\n\"막 알려진 비밀이라서요.\"\n\n\"누가요?\"\n\n\"경찰이요.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 소식이 나온 곳은……\"\n\n\"어젯밤 경시청장 댁이요. 파리가, 아시다시피, 그런 보기 드문 화려함을 보고 깜짝 놀라서 경찰이 조사를 한 거예요.\"\n\n\"이런, 이런! 너무 부자라는 구실로 그 백작을 부랑자로 체포하는 일만 남았군요.\"\n\n\"실은, 만약 신원 증명이 그렇게 유리하지 않았다면 틀림없이 그렇게 됐을 거예요.\"\n\n\"가엾은 백작! 그분은 자기가 어떤 위험에 처했었는지 알고 계실까요?\"\n\n\"모를 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 알려 드리는 것이 자비로운 일이겠군요. 그분이 도착하면 꼭 그렇게 하겠습니다.\"\n\n바로 그때, 빛나는 눈과 검은 머리, 윤기 흐르는 콧수염을 한 잘생긴 청년이 빌포르 부인에게 정중하게 고개를 숙였다. 알베르가 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"부인,\" 알베르가 말했다. \"막시밀리앙 모렐 씨를 소개해 드리겠습니다. 스파히 기병대의 대위이자, 우리 군에서 가장 훌륭하고, 무엇보다 가장 용감한 장교 가운데 한 사람입니다.\"\n\n\"이미 오퇴유에서, 몬테 크리스토 백작 댁에서 이분을 만나는 기쁨을 누렸답니다.\" 빌포르 부인이 눈에 띄게 쌀쌀한 태도로 고개를 돌리며 대답했다.\n\n이 대답, 그리고 특히 그 말투가 가엾은 모렐의 가슴을 얼어붙게 했다. 그러나 그에게는 보상이 마련되어 있었다. 몸을 돌리자 문 가까이에서 아름다운 금발의 얼굴이 보였다. 커다란 파란 눈이, 별다른 표정 없이 그에게 고정되어 있었고, 그 사이 물망초 다발이 가만히 입술로 들어 올려졌다.\n\n그 인사가 어찌나 잘 통했던지, 모렐도 눈에 같은 표정을 띤 채 손수건을 입가로 가져갔다. 그리하여 대리석 같은 겉모습 아래 그토록 격렬하게 뛰는 두 살아 있는 조각상은, 방 전체 길이만큼 서로 떨어진 채로 한순간 자신들을 잊었다. 아니, 차라리 서로를 응시하느라 온 세상을 잊었다고 해야 할 것이다. 누구도 그들의 넋 나간 모습을 알아채지 못한 채, 둘은 훨씬 더 오래 서로에게 빠져 있을 수도 있었을 것이다. 몬테 크리스토 백작이 막 들어선 참이었다.\n\n우리는 백작에게는, 그가 어디에 나타나든 모든 사람의 시선을 끄는 무언가가 있다고 이미 말한 바 있다. 그것은 재단에 흠잡을 데 없으면서도 단순하고 장식 없는 그의 코트가 아니었다. 그것은 수수한 흰 조끼도 아니었다. 그것은 더없이 잘 빠진 발을 드러내는 바지도 아니었다. 시선을 끈 것은 그런 것들이 아니었다. 그의 창백한 안색, 물결치는 검은 머리카락, 차분하고 평온한 표정, 어둡고 우수에 잠긴 눈, 그토록 높은 경멸을 그토록 쉽게 드러내며 놀랍도록 섬세하게 깎아 놓은 듯한 입 — 모든 이의 시선을 그에게 못 박은 것은 바로 이런 것들이었다.\n\n그보다 더 잘생긴 사내는 많았을 것이다. 그러나 — 이런 표현을 써도 된다면 — 그의 모습보다 더 의미심장한 사람은 분명 없었을 것이다. 백작의 모든 것이 저마다 뜻을 지닌 듯했다. 그가 몸에 익힌 끊임없는 사색의 습관이 그의 얼굴 표정에, 심지어 가장 사소한 몸짓에까지, 좀처럼 헤아리기 힘든 편안함과 활력을 부여했기 때문이다. 그렇지만 파리의 사교계란 워낙 기묘한 곳이라, 막대한 재산으로 덧칠된 어떤 신비로운 이야기가 거기에 얽혀 있지 않았더라면, 이 모든 것조차 사람들의 관심을 끌지 못했을지도 모른다.\n\n그러는 동안 백작은 호기심 어린 시선들의 포화를 받으며 손님들 무리 사이를 헤치고 모르세르 부인 쪽으로 나아갔다. 꽃으로 장식된 벽난로 앞에 서 있던 부인은, 문 맞은편에 놓인 거울로 그가 들어서는 것을 보고 그를 맞을 채비를 하고 있었다. 그가 막 고개를 숙이는 순간, 부인이 평온한 미소를 띠며 그를 향해 돌아섰다. 부인은 분명 백작이 자기에게 말을 걸리라 여겼고, 백작 쪽에서도 부인이 막 말을 건네려는 줄 알았다. 그러나 둘 다 입을 다물었고, 그저 가벼운 인사만 나눈 뒤, 몬테 크리스토는 알베르 쪽으로 걸음을 옮겼다. 알베르가 그를 다정하게 맞았다.\n\n\"제 어머니를 뵈셨습니까?\" 알베르가 물었다.\n\n\"방금 그 기쁨을 누렸습니다.\" 백작이 대답했다. \"하지만 자작의 아버님은 뵙지 못했군요.\"\n\n\"보세요, 저기 계십니다. 저 위대한 천재들의 작은 무리와 정치 이야기를 나누고 계시지요.\"\n\n\"그래요?\" 몬테 크리스토가 말했다. \"그러니까 저기 저 신사분들이 대단한 재능을 지닌 분들이란 말이군요. 짐작도 못 했습니다. 그런데 어떤 종류의 재능으로 유명하신가요? 아시다시피 재능에도 여러 가지가 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"키가 크고 험상궂게 생긴 저분은 아주 박식한 분입니다. 로마 인근에서, 도마뱀류가 보통 가진 것보다 척추뼈가 하나 더 많은 도마뱀을 발견하고는, 곧장 그 발견을 학사원에 제출했지요. 그 일이 오랫동안 논의되었지만, 결국 그분에게 유리하게 결론이 났습니다. 장담하건대 그 척추뼈가 학계에 큰 파문을 일으켰고, 레지옹 도뇌르 슈발리에에 불과했던 그분이 오피시에로 승급했답니다.\"\n\n\"이런,\" 몬테 크리스토가 말했다. \"그 훈장은 현명하게 수여된 것 같군요. 척추뼈를 하나 더 발견했다면, 코망되르로 만들어 주었겠는데요.\"\n\n\"그럴 법하지요.\" 알베르가 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 초록색으로 수놓은 파란 코트로 몸을 감쌀 생각을 한 저 사람은 누구입니까?\"\n\n\"아, 저 코트는 저분 생각이 아닙니다. 공화국의 작품이지요. 학사원 회원들의 제복을 고안하라고 다비드에게 위임했거든요.\"\n\n\"그래요?\" 몬테 크리스토가 말했다. \"그럼 저 신사분은 학사원 회원이군요?\"\n\n\"지난주에 그 학식 높은 모임의 일원이 되었습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 저분의 특별한 재능은 무엇입니까?\"\n\n\"저분의 재능이요? 토끼 머리에 핀을 꽂고, 닭에게 꼭두서니를 먹이고, 고래수염으로 개의 척수를 빼내는 것으로 압니다.\"\n\n\"그런 일로 과학 학사원 회원이 되었단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"아니요, 프랑스 학사원입니다.\"\n\n\"그런데 프랑스 학사원이 그런 일과 무슨 상관이 있나요?\"\n\n\"제가 막 말씀드리려던 참입니다. 듣자 하니……\"\n\n\"그분의 실험이 과학의 대의를 상당히 진전시켰겠지요, 틀림없이?\"\n\n\"아니요, 그분의 글솜씨가 아주 뛰어나서랍니다.\"\n\n\"머리에 핀이 꽂힌 토끼들, 뼈가 붉게 물든 닭들, 척수가 뽑힌 개들로서는 무척이나 우쭐한 일이겠군요?\"\n\n알베르가 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"그럼 저 다른 분은요?\" 백작이 물었다.\n\n\"저분 말입니까?\"\n\n\"네, 세 번째 사람이요.\"\n\n\"짙은 파란색 코트를 입은 분이요?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"저분은 가친의 동료로, 귀족원에 제복을 도입하자는 안에 가장 적극적으로 반대한 사람 가운데 하나입니다. 그 문제에서 큰 성공을 거두었지요. 진보 성향 신문들과는 사이가 나빴지만, 궁정의 뜻에 맞선 그 고결한 반대 덕분에 지금은 언론인들의 호감을 사고 있습니다. 그분을 대사로 앉히자는 말까지 나오고 있어요.\"\n\n\"그분이 귀족원에 들어갈 자격은 무엇입니까?\"\n\n\"희가극을 두세 편 작곡했고, '시에클'지에 네다섯 편의 글을 썼으며, 대여섯 해 동안 내각 편에 표를 던졌습니다.\"\n\n\"브라보, 자작.\" 몬테 크리스토가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"당신은 더없이 즐거운 안내인이군요. 그런데 이제 제 부탁 하나 들어주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"무엇입니까?\"\n\n\"저 신사분들 가운데 누구에게도 저를 소개하지 말아 주세요. 그리고 혹시 그분들이 소개를 원하면 제게 미리 귀띔해 주시고요.\" 바로 그때 백작은 누군가 자기 팔을 잡는 것을 느꼈다. 돌아보니 당글라르였다.\n\n\"아! 당신이군요, 남작?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"왜 저를 남작이라고 부르십니까?\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"제가 작위 따위는 전혀 개의치 않는다는 걸 아시잖습니까. 저는 자작과는 다릅니다. 자작은 자기 작위를 좋아하지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"물론이지요.\" 알베르가 대답했다. \"작위가 없으면 저는 아무것도 아닐 테니까요. 반면에 당신은 남작 자리를 내놓아도 여전히 백만장자로 남을 테고요.\"\n\n\"그게 7월 왕정 아래서는 가장 훌륭한 작위인 듯합니다만.\" 당글라르가 대답했다.\n\n\"안타깝게도,\" 몬테 크리스토가 말했다. \"백만장자라는 칭호는 남작이나 프랑스 귀족, 학사원 회원 칭호와 달리 평생 가는 게 아니지요. 이를테면 프랑크푸르트의 백만장자 프랑크 운트 포울만 상회가 방금 파산했답니다.\"\n\n\"정말입니까?\" 당글라르가 안색이 창백해지며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 오늘 저녁 급사 편으로 그 소식을 받았습니다. 그들에게 백만쯤 맡겨 두었었는데, 제때 경고를 받아 한 달 전에 빼냈지요.\"\n\n\"아, 맙소사!\" 당글라르가 외쳤다. \"그들이 제 앞으로 20만 프랑짜리 어음을 발행했단 말입니다!\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그 어음은 던져 버리셔도 됩니다. 그들의 서명은 5퍼센트 값어치밖에 안 되니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇긴 합니다만, 너무 늦었습니다.\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"제가 그 어음을 결제해 버렸거든요.\"\n\n\"그럼,\" 몬테 크리스토가 말했다. \"여기 20만 프랑이 또 날아가 버린 셈이군요. 앞서 날린 돈에 더해서……\"\n\n\"쉿, 그런 이야기는 꺼내지 마십시오.\" 당글라르가 말했다. 그러고는 몬테 크리스토에게 다가서며 덧붙였다. \"특히 젊은 카발칸티 씨 앞에서는요.\" 그러더니 미소를 지으며 그 청년 쪽으로 돌아섰다.\n\n알베르는 어머니와 이야기하려고 백작 곁을 떠났고, 당글라르는 젊은 카발칸티와 대화하려고 자리를 옮겼다. 몬테 크리스토는 한순간 혼자가 되었다. 그러는 사이 더위가 견디기 힘들 만큼 심해졌다. 하인들이 얼음과자를 가득 실은 쟁반을 들고 방마다 분주히 오갔다. 몬테 크리스토는 이마의 땀을 닦았지만, 하인이 쟁반을 내밀자 뒤로 물러섰다. 그는 아무것도 들지 않았다. 모르세르 부인은 몬테 크리스토에게서 눈을 떼지 않고 있었다. 그가 아무것도 들지 않는 것을, 심지어 사양하는 그 몸짓까지 보았다.\n\n\"알베르,\" 부인이 물었다. \"저것 봤니?\"\n\n\"무엇을요, 어머니?\"\n\n\"백작이 모르세르 씨 댁 지붕 아래서는 결코 음식을 들려 하지 않는다는 거 말이다.\"\n\n\"네, 하지만 그분은 저와 아침을 함께 들었는걸요. 사실 그분이 사교계에 처음 모습을 드러낸 것이 바로 그 자리였고요.\"\n\n\"하지만 네 집은 모르세르 씨 댁이 아니잖니.\" 메르세데스가 중얼거렸다. \"여기 온 뒤로 줄곧 그분을 지켜봤단다.\"\n\"그래서요?\"\n\n\"그래서 말이다, 아직 아무것도 들지 않으셨어.\"\n\n\"백작은 무척 절제하는 분이세요.\"\n\n메르세데스가 서글프게 미소 지었다.\n\n\"그분에게 다가가렴.\" 부인이 말했다. \"그리고 다음번에 하인이 지나갈 때, 뭐라도 꼭 드시도록 권해 보아라.\"\n\n\"하지만 왜요, 어머니?\"\n\n\"그저 나를 기쁘게 해 다오, 알베르.\" 메르세데스가 말했다. 알베르는 어머니의 손에 입을 맞춘 뒤 백작에게 다가갔다. 앞서와 똑같이 가득 채워진 또 다른 쟁반이 지나갔다. 부인은 알베르가 백작을 설득하려 애쓰는 것을 보았지만, 백작은 완강히 거절했다. 알베르가 어머니에게로 돌아왔다. 부인은 몹시 창백했다.\n\n\"보았지?\" 부인이 말했다. \"거절하지 않니?\"\n\n\"네, 그런데 그게 왜 어머니를 언짢게 하나요?\"\n\n\"알베르, 너도 알다시피 여자들이란 묘한 존재란다. 백작이 우리 집에서 뭐라도, 얼음과자라도 드시는 모습을 보고 싶었어. 어쩌면 프랑스식 생활에 익숙해지지 못해서 다른 걸 더 좋아할지도 모르지.\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요. 이탈리아에서 그분이 무엇이든 잘 드시는 걸 봤는걸요. 틀림없이 오늘 저녁엔 그저 내키지 않으신 거예요.\"\n\n\"게다가,\" 백작 부인이 말했다. \"그분은 뜨거운 기후에 익숙하니, 어쩌면 우리만큼 더위를 느끼지 않는 걸지도 모르겠구나.\"\n\n\"그건 아닐 거예요. 거의 숨이 막힐 것 같다고 하시면서, 창문뿐 아니라 베네치아풍 덧창도 열지 않는 이유가 뭐냐고 물으셨거든요.\"\n\n\"한마디로,\" 메르세데스가 말했다. \"그분의 절식은 일부러 한 일이라고 내게 확실히 알려 준 셈이로구나.\"\n\n그러고는 방을 나섰다.\n\n잠시 후 덧창이 활짝 열렸고, 창가에 늘어진 재스민과 클레마티스 사이로, 등불로 장식된 정원과 천막 아래 차려진 만찬이 보였다. 춤추던 이들, 카드놀이를 하던 이들, 담소를 나누던 이들 모두가 기쁨의 탄성을 질렀다. 다들 안으로 흘러드는 산들바람을 황홀하게 들이마셨다. 동시에 메르세데스가 다시 나타났다. 아까보다 더 창백했지만, 이따금 짓곤 하는 그 흔들림 없는 표정을 띠고 있었다. 부인은 남편이 중심을 이루고 있는 무리 쪽으로 곧장 걸어갔다.\n\n\"백작, 저분들을 여기 붙잡아 두지 마세요.\" 부인이 말했다. \"카드놀이를 하는 것도 아니니, 여기서 숨 막혀 하느니 정원에서 바람을 쐬고 싶어 하실 거예요.\"\n\n\"아,\" 1809년에 '시리아로 떠나며'를 불렀던 한 멋스러운 노장군이 말했다. \"우리끼리만 정원에 가지는 않겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 메르세데스가 말했다. \"제가 앞장서지요.\"\n\n그러고는 몬테 크리스토를 향해 돌아서며 덧붙였다. \"백작, 팔을 좀 빌려주시겠어요?\"\n\n이 단순한 말에 백작은 거의 비틀거릴 뻔했다. 그러고는 메르세데스에게 눈길을 고정했다. 그저 한순간의 시선이었지만, 백작 부인에게는 한 세기만큼이나 길게 느껴졌다. 그 한 번의 눈길에 그토록 많은 것이 담겨 있었던 것이다. 백작이 백작 부인에게 팔을 내밀었다. 부인이 그 팔을 잡았다. 아니, 차라리 작은 손으로 살짝 닿았을 뿐이었다. 그렇게 두 사람은 진달래와 동백꽃이 늘어선 계단을 함께 내려갔다. 그들 뒤로, 다른 출구를 통해 스무 명쯤 되는 무리가 환호성을 지르며 정원으로 쏟아져 나왔다.\n\n# 제70장. 무도회\n\n모르세르 부인은 일행과 함께 나무 아치 아래로 들어섰다. 그 길은 보리수 숲을 지나 온실로 이어졌다.\n\n\"방 안이 너무 더웠지요, 백작님?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"네, 부인. 문과 덧창을 여시다니 참 좋은 생각이셨습니다.\" 말을 마치며 백작은 메르세데스의 손이 떨리는 것을 느꼈다. \"하지만 부인은,\" 그가 말했다. \"그렇게 얇은 옷에다 몸에 두른 것이라곤 망사 숄뿐이니, 어쩌면 추우신 게 아닙니까?\"\n\n\"제가 백작님을 어디로 모시고 가는지 아세요?\" 백작 부인이 그 질문에는 대답하지 않은 채 말했다.\n\n\"모릅니다, 부인.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"하지만 보시다시피 저는 거역하지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"저 숲 끝에 보이는 온실로 가는 길이에요.\"\n\n백작은 묻기라도 하듯 메르세데스를 바라보았다. 그러나 그녀는 말없이 계속 걸었고, 그도 입을 다물었다. 두 사람은 그 건물에 다다랐다. 그곳은 우리네 기후에서 그토록 자주 모습을 감추는 태양을 대신하는 인공의 온도로 7월 초면 익는 화려한 과일들로 장식되어 있었다. 백작 부인은 몬테크리스토의 팔에서 손을 떼고, 무스카텔 포도 한 송이를 땄다.\n\n\"보세요, 백작님.\" 그녀가 말했다. 그 미소가 어찌나 슬퍼 보였던지, 눈꺼풀에 맺힌 눈물마저 알아챌 수 있을 듯했다. \"우리 프랑스 포도가 시칠리아나 키프로스의 백작님 포도에 견줄 수 없다는 건 저도 압니다. 하지만 우리 북쪽 나라의 태양을 헤아려 주시겠지요.\" 백작은 고개를 숙였으나, 한 걸음 물러섰다.\n\n\"거절하시는 건가요?\" 메르세데스가 떨리는 목소리로 물었다.\n\n\"부디 양해해 주십시오, 부인.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"저는 무스카텔 포도를 결코 먹지 않습니다.\"\n\n메르세데스는 포도를 떨어뜨리며 한숨을 쉬었다. 곁에 있는 벽에 같은 인공의 열로 익은 탐스러운 복숭아 하나가 매달려 있었다. 메르세데스가 다가가 그 열매를 땄다.\n\n\"그럼 이 복숭아를 드세요.\" 그녀가 말했다. 백작은 또다시 거절했다. \"아니, 또요?\" 그녀가 외쳤다. 그 애처로운 목소리는 흐느낌을 억누르는 듯했다. \"정말이지, 저를 아프게 하시는군요.\"\n\n긴 침묵이 이어졌다. 포도가 그랬듯, 복숭아도 땅에 떨어졌다.\n\n\"백작님.\" 메르세데스가 애원하는 눈빛으로 덧붙였다. \"아라비아에는 아름다운 풍습이 하나 있어요. 한 지붕 아래에서 빵과 소금을 함께 나눈 사람들은 영원한 벗이 된다는 풍습이지요.\"\n\n\"저도 압니다, 부인.\" 백작이 대답했다. \"하지만 여기는 프랑스이지 아라비아가 아닙니다. 그리고 프랑스에서는 영원한 우정이란, 빵과 소금을 서로 나누는 풍습만큼이나 드문 것이지요.\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 백작 부인이 숨을 몰아쉬며 말했다. 그녀는 몬테크리스토에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채, 두 손으로 그의 팔을 발작하듯 움켜쥐고 있었다. \"우리는 벗이지요, 그렇지 않나요?\"\n\n백작은 죽은 사람처럼 새파래졌다. 피가 심장으로 몰렸다가 다시 솟구쳐 그의 두 뺨을 진홍빛으로 물들였다. 그의 두 눈은 갑자기 눈이 부신 사람처럼 흔들렸다.\n\n\"물론 우리는 벗입니다.\" 그가 대답했다. \"그렇지 않을 이유가 어디 있겠습니까?\"\n\n그 대답은 메르세데스가 바라던 것과는 너무도 동떨어진 것이어서, 그녀는 신음에 가까운 한숨을 토해 내려고 고개를 돌렸다. \"고맙습니다.\" 그녀가 말했다. 그리고 두 사람은 다시 걸음을 옮겼다. 그들은 한마디 말도 없이 정원을 끝까지 가로질렀다.\n\n\"백작님.\" 침묵 속에 십 분쯤 걷고 난 뒤, 백작 부인이 불쑥 물었다. \"그토록 많은 것을 보고, 그토록 멀리 다니고, 그토록 깊이 고통받으셨다는 게 사실인가요?\"\n\n\"저는 깊이 고통받았습니다, 부인.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다.\n\n\"하지만 지금은 행복하시지요?\"\n\n\"틀림없이 그렇습니다.\" 백작이 대답했다. \"제가 불평하는 걸 듣는 사람이 아무도 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"그럼 지금의 그 행복이 백작님의 마음을 누그러뜨렸나요?\"\n\n\"지금의 제 행복은 지난날의 비참함과 맞먹습니다.\" 백작이 말했다.\n\n\"결혼은 하지 않으셨나요?\" 백작 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"제가, 결혼이요?\" 몬테크리스토가 몸서리치며 외쳤다. \"누가 그런 말을 했습니까?\"\n\n\"결혼하셨다고 말한 사람은 없어요. 하지만 백작님이 젊고 사랑스러운 여인과 함께 오페라에 계신 모습을 사람들이 여러 번 보았으니까요.\"\n\n\"그 여인은 제가 콘스탄티노플에서 산 노예입니다, 부인. 어느 왕자의 딸이지요. 이 세상에 달리 사랑할 사람이 없어서, 제 딸로 거두었습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 혼자 사시나요?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"누이도, 아들도, 아버지도 없으시고요?\"\n\n\"아무도 없습니다.\"\n\n\"당신을 삶에 붙들어 매어 줄 사람이 아무도 없이, 어떻게 그렇게 사실 수가 있나요?\"\n\n\"제 탓이 아닙니다, 부인. 몰타에서 저는 한 처녀를 사랑했고, 그녀와 막 결혼하려던 참에 전쟁이 일어나 저를 끌고 갔습니다. 저는 그녀가 저를 기다려 줄 만큼, 또 제 기억에 변함없이 충실할 만큼 저를 사랑한다고 믿었습니다. 그런데 돌아와 보니 그녀는 결혼한 뒤였지요. 스무 살을 넘긴 남자라면 대개 겪는 이야기입니다. 어쩌면 제 마음이 보통 사람들의 마음보다 약했는지도 모르지요. 그래서 제 처지였다면 남들이 겪었을 것보다 더 많이 괴로워했던 것이고요. 그게 다입니다.\"\n\n백작 부인은 숨이 막히는 듯 잠시 멈춰 섰다. \"그래요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그래서 그 사랑을 아직도 마음속에 간직하고 계시는군요. 사람은 단 한 번만 사랑할 수 있는 법이니까요. 그래서 그녀를 다시 본 적이 있으신가요?\"\n\n\"한 번도 없습니다.\"\n\n\"한 번도요?\"\n\n\"저는 그녀가 살던 나라로 다시 돌아가지 않았습니다.\"\n\n\"몰타로요?\"\n\n\"네, 몰타입니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 그녀는 지금도 몰타에 있나요?\"\n\n\"그렇겠지요.\"\n\n\"그리고 그녀가 당신에게 안긴 그 모든 고통을, 다 용서하셨나요?\"\n\"그녀는, 용서했습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 그녀만이군요. 그럼 당신은 두 사람을 갈라놓은 자들을 아직도 미워하시나요?\"\n\n\"제가 그들을 미워한다고요? 천만에요. 무엇 때문에 그러겠습니까?\" 백작 부인은 향기로운 포도 한 자락을 여전히 손에 든 채, 몬테크리스토 앞을 가로막았다.\n\n\"좀 드세요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"부인, 저는 무스카텔 포도를 결코 먹지 않습니다.\" 몬테크리스토가, 그 이야기가 앞서 한 번도 나오지 않았던 것처럼 대답했다. 백작 부인은 절망에 찬 몸짓으로 포도를 가장 가까운 덤불 속으로 내던졌다.\n\n\"매정하신 분!\" 그녀가 중얼거렸다. 몬테크리스토는 그 책망이 자신을 향한 것이 아니기라도 한 듯, 조금도 동요하지 않은 채 서 있었다.\n\n바로 그때 알베르가 달려 들어왔다. \"아, 어머니.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"큰 변고가 생겼어요!\"\n\n\"무슨 일이냐? 무슨 일이 있었어?\" 백작 부인이 잠에서 깨어나 현실로 돌아온 사람처럼 물었다. \"변고라고 했느냐? 정말이지, 변고가 닥칠 만도 하지.\"\n\n\"빌포르 씨가 와 계세요.\"\n\n\"그래서?\"\n\n\"부인과 따님을 데리러 오셨어요.\"\n\n\"어째서?\"\n\n\"생메랑 부인이 막 파리에 도착하셨는데, 생메랑 씨가 돌아가셨다는 소식을 가지고 오셨거든요. 마르세유를 떠나 첫 역참에 다다랐을 때 일어난 일이래요. 빌포르 부인은 아주 기분이 좋으셨던 터라, 그 변고를 믿으려고도, 떠올리려고도 하지 않으셨어요. 하지만 발랑틴 양은 아버지가 그토록 조심했는데도 첫마디에 모든 진실을 알아채고 말았어요. 그 충격이 벼락처럼 그녀를 내리쳤고, 그녀는 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌어요.\"\n\n\"그런데 생메랑 씨는 빌포르 양과 어떤 관계요?\" 백작이 물었다.\n\n\"외할아버지세요. 발랑틴 양과 프란츠의 결혼을 서두르려고 이리로 오시던 길이셨고요.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇군!\"\n\n\"그러니 프란츠는 기다려야겠지요. 어째서 생메랑 씨는 당글라르 양의 외할아버지는 못 되셨을까요?\"\n\n\"알베르, 알베르.\" 모르세르 부인이 부드럽게 나무라는 어조로 말했다. \"그게 무슨 말이냐? 아, 백작님, 이 아이가 백작님을 무척 존경하니, 말을 함부로 했다고 좀 일러 주세요.\"\n\n그러고는 두세 걸음 앞으로 나아갔다. 몬테크리스토가 어찌나 깊은 생각에 잠긴, 또 다정한 흠모로 가득 찬 표정으로 그녀를 바라보았던지, 그녀는 돌아서서 그의 손을 잡았다. 동시에 아들의 손을 잡아, 두 손을 맞잡게 했다.\n\n\"우리는 벗이지요, 그렇지 않나요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"아, 부인, 저는 감히 저를 부인의 벗이라 일컫지는 못합니다. 그러나 저는 언제나 부인의 가장 공손한 종입니다.\" 백작 부인은 마음속에 형언할 수 없는 아픔을 품은 채 자리를 떴고, 채 열 걸음도 떼기 전에 손수건을 들어 눈가로 가져가는 모습을 백작은 보았다.\n\n\"어머니와 백작님이 뜻이 안 맞으시나요?\" 알베르가 놀라서 물었다.\n\n\"오히려 그 반대일세.\" 백작이 대답했다. \"우리가 벗이라고 어머님께서 말씀하시는 걸 자네도 듣지 않았나?\"\n\n그들은 발랑틴과 빌포르 부인이 막 떠난 응접실로 다시 들어섰다. 모렐도 거의 같은 때에 자리를 떴다는 말은, 굳이 덧붙일 필요가 없을지도 모르겠다.\n\n# Chapter 72. Madame de Saint-Méran\n\n빌포르 씨의 집에서도 마침 음울한 일이 벌어진 참이었다.\n부인들이 무도회로 떠난 뒤였다. 빌포르 부인이 아무리 간청해도 그를 함께 데려가지는 못했다. 검사는 늘 그러듯 서재에 틀어박혔다. 다른 사람이라면 누구나 질겁할 만큼 산더미처럼 쌓인 서류들과 함께였지만, 그것들조차 그의 끝없는 욕망을 거의 채워 주지 못하곤 했다.\n\n그러나 이번에는 서류가 한낱 구실에 지나지 않았다. 빌포르가 틀어박힌 것은 일을 하기 위해서가 아니라 생각에 잠기기 위해서였다. 문을 잠그고, 중요한 용무가 아니면 방해하지 말라고 일러둔 뒤, 그는 안락의자에 앉아 지난 여드레 동안 그토록 많은 음울한 상념과 쓰라린 기억으로 마음을 가득 채워 온 일들을 곱씹기 시작했다.\n\n그러고는 눈앞에 쌓인 서류 더미에 파고드는 대신, 책상 서랍을 열고 용수철을 눌러 소중히 간직해 온 비망록 꾸러미를 꺼냈다. 거기에는 그의 정치 인생에서든, 금전 문제에서든, 법정에서든, 아니면 은밀한 연애에서든 그의 적이 된 모든 자들의 이름이 오직 자신만이 아는 기호로 정성껏 정리되어 있었다.\n\n이제 그가 두려움을 품기 시작한 마당이라, 그 수효는 만만치 않았다. 그러나 그 이름들은, 비록 막강하긴 했어도, 산 정상에서 발아래로 험준한 봉우리와 거의 지날 수 없는 길과 무시무시한 골짜기를 굽어보는 나그네의 만족감 같은 것을 그에게 안겨 주며 곧잘 미소 짓게 했다. 그가 그 길들을 그토록 위태롭게 기어올라 왔던 것이다. 그 모든 이름을 기억 속에서 훑어보고, 다시 읽고 살피며 목록에 주석을 달던 그는 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"아니야.\" 그가 중얼거렸다. \"내 적 가운데 누구도 그토록 오랜 세월을 참을성 있게, 그토록 공들여 기다렸다가 이제 와서 이 비밀로 나를 짓밟으러 올 리는 없어. 햄릿의 말처럼, 이따금,\n\n'추악한 행실은 떠오르는 법,\n\n온 땅이 그것을 덮어 묻어도 사람들의 눈앞에.'\n\n하지만 그것들은 도깨비불처럼, 길을 잘못 들게 하려고 떠오를 뿐이다. 그 이야기는 코르시카 사람이 어느 사제에게 했고, 그 사제가 다시 옮긴 게야. 몬테 크리스토 씨가 그 얘기를 들었을 수도 있고, 진상을 알아보려고—\n\n\"하지만 그가 그 일에 관해 진상을 알아보려 할 까닭이 무엇이란 말인가?\" 빌포르가 잠시 생각한 끝에 자문했다. \"이 몬테 크리스토 씨, 아니 자코네 씨—몰타 선주의 아들이자 테살리아에서 광산을 발견하고 이제 처음으로 파리를 찾은 그자가, 이 음울하고 알 수 없고 쓸모없는 사실 하나를 캐내는 데 대체 무슨 이해관계가 있단 말인가? 어쨌든 부소니 신부와 윌모어 경, 그 벗과 그 적이 내게 들려준 그 모든 두서없는 이야기 가운데서도, 내 생각에 한 가지만은 분명하고 명백하다. 어느 시기에도, 어떤 경우에도, 어떤 상황에서도, 그와 나 사이에 접점이 있었을 리 없다는 것.\"\n\n그러나 빌포르가 입에 올린 말은 그 자신조차 믿지 않는 말이었다. 그가 두려워한 것은 폭로 자체가 아니었다. 그것이라면 반박하거나 부인할 수 있었으니까. 벽에 핏빛 글자로 느닷없이 나타난 그 '메네, 메네, 테켈, 우파르신' 따위는 별로 걱정되지 않았다. 정작 그가 애타게 알고 싶었던 것은 그 글자를 그어 놓은 손이 누구의 것이냐 하는 것이었다. 그가 두려움을 가라앉히려 애쓰며—그토록 자주 야심 찬 꿈의 주제가 되었던 정치적 미래를 곱씹는 대신, 그토록 오래 잠들어 있던 적을 깨울까 두려워하며 가정의 즐거움에 한정된 미래를 그려 보고 있을 때—마당에서 마차 소리가 울렸다. 이어 나이 든 사람이 계단을 오르는 발소리가 들렸고, 하인들이 주인의 슬픔에 관심 있는 척하려 할 때 으레 터뜨리는 울음과 탄식이 뒤따랐다.\n\n그가 문의 빗장을 풀자, 거의 곧바로 한 노부인이 통보도 없이 들어섰다. 숄은 팔에 걸치고 모자는 손에 쥔 채였다. 흰머리가 누런 이마에서 뒤로 넘겨져 있었고, 이미 세월의 주름으로 움푹 들어간 두 눈은 슬픔으로 부어오른 눈꺼풀에 가려 거의 보이지 않았다.\n\n\"오, 자네.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"오, 자네, 이 무슨 변고인가! 나는 이 일로 죽고 말 걸세. 오, 그래, 분명히 이 일로 죽고 말 게야!\"\n\n그러고는 문에서 가장 가까운 의자에 풀썩 주저앉아 격렬하게 흐느끼기 시작했다. 문간에 선 하인들은 감히 더 다가서지 못한 채, 누아르티에의 늙은 하인을 바라보고 있었다. 그자도 주인의 방에서 소리를 듣고 달려와 다른 이들 뒤에 서 있었다. 빌포르는 일어나 장모에게로 달려갔다. 그 노부인이 바로 그의 장모였던 것이다.\n\n\"아니, 무슨 일이 있으셨습니까?\" 그가 외쳤다. \"어쩌다 이렇게 심란해지셨습니까? 생메랑 후작께서는 함께 계신지요?\"\n\n\"생메랑 후작은 죽었네.\" 늙은 후작 부인이 서두도 없이, 아무런 감정도 없이 대답했다. 그녀는 넋이 나간 듯했다. 빌포르가 뒷걸음질 치며 두 손을 맞잡고 외쳤다.\n\n\"돌아가셨다고요? 그렇게 갑자기요?\"\n\n\"일주일 전이었네.\" 생메랑 부인이 말을 이었다. \"우리는 저녁을 든 뒤 함께 마차를 타고 나섰지. 생메랑 후작은 며칠 전부터 몸이 좋지 않았어. 그래도 사랑하는 발랑틴을 다시 본다는 생각에 용기를 냈고, 몸이 아픈데도 기어이 떠나려 했네. 마르세유에서 6리외쯤 갔을 때, 늘 드시던 알약을 몇 알 드신 후작이 어찌나 깊이 잠드는지, 내겐 그게 예사롭지 않아 보였어. 그래도 얼굴이 붉어진 듯하고 관자놀이의 핏줄이 평소보다 거세게 뛰는 것 같다고 여기면서도, 차마 깨우기를 망설였지. 하지만 날이 어두워져 더는 보이지 않게 되자 나도 잠이 들었네. 곧 꿈속에서 괴로워하는 사람이 내지르는 듯한 날카로운 비명에 깜짝 놀라 깼는데, 후작이 별안간 머리를 거세게 뒤로 젖히지 뭔가. 시종을 부르고, 마부를 멈춰 세우고, 생메랑 후작에게 말을 걸고, 각성제를 갖다 대었지만, 모든 게 이미 끝나 있었네. 나는 송장 곁에 앉은 채로 엑스에 도착했지.\"\n\n빌포르는 입을 반쯤 벌린 채, 완전히 넋이 나가 서 있었다.\n\n\"물론 의사를 부르셨겠지요?\"\n\n\"곧바로 불렀지. 하지만 말했듯이 너무 늦었더군.\"\n\n\"네, 하지만 그래도 의사가 가엾은 후작께서 무슨 병으로 돌아가셨는지는 말해 줄 수 있었을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"오, 그래, 자네, 말해 주더군. 졸중 발작이었던 듯하다고.\"\n\n\"그래서 어떻게 하셨습니까?\"\n\n\"생메랑 후작은 늘, 파리를 떠나 있는 동안 죽음이 닥칠 경우 시신을 가문의 납골당으로 옮겨 달라고 했네. 그래서 납으로 만든 관에 넣게 하고, 나는 며칠 앞서 온 것일세.\"\n\n\"오! 가엾은 어머님!\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"그 연세에, 그런 충격을 겪으신 뒤에 그런 일까지 치르셔야 하다니요!\"\n\n\"하느님께서 그 모든 일을 견디게 해 주셨네. 게다가, 사랑하는 후작도 내가 그를 위해 한 일을 분명 나를 위해 다 해 주었을 게야. 사실 그이 곁을 떠난 뒤로 나는 제정신이 아닌 것 같아. 울 수도 없네. 내 나이가 되면 더는 흘릴 눈물도 없다고들 하지만, 그래도 괴로울 때는 울 수 있어야 마땅하다고 나는 생각하네. 발랑틴은 어디 있나, 자네? 그 아이 때문에 내가 여기 온 게야. 발랑틴이 보고 싶네.\"\n\n발랑틴이 무도회에 가 있다고 대답하기가 끔찍하게 여겨진 빌포르는, 그저 그 애가 새어머니와 함께 외출했으며 곧 데려오겠다고만 말했다. \"지금 당장, 자네, 지금 당장 부탁하네!\" 노부인이 말했다. 빌포르는 생메랑 부인의 팔을 자신의 팔에 끼고 자기 방으로 모셨다.\n\n\"좀 쉬십시오, 어머님.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n이 말에 후작 부인이 고개를 들었다. 그토록 절절히 그리워하는 딸, 발랑틴 안에 아직 살아 있는 그 딸을 강렬하게 떠올리게 하는 사내를 바라보던 그녀는, 어머님이라는 말에 마음이 뭉클해져 눈물을 쏟으며 안락의자 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 그 위에 존엄한 머리를 묻었다. 빌포르는 그녀를 여인들의 보살핌에 맡겼고, 늙은 바루아는 반쯤 겁에 질려 제 주인에게 달려갔다. 죽음이 한 노인을 치려고 잠시 다른 노인에게서 경계의 눈길을 거둘 때보다 노인들을 더 두렵게 하는 것은 없으니까. 그런 뒤, 생메랑 부인이 무릎을 꿇은 채 간절히 기도를 올리는 동안, 빌포르는 삯마차를 불러 오게 하고는 몸소 모르세르 부인의 집으로 아내와 딸을 데리러 갔다. 그가 무도회장 입구에 나타났을 때 어찌나 창백했던지, 발랑틴이 그에게 달려오며 말했다.\n\n\"오, 아버지, 무슨 변고가 생겼군요!\"\n\n\"네 할머님께서 막 도착하셨다, 발랑틴.\" 빌포르 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"할아버님은요?\" 어린 아가씨가 불안에 떨며 물었다. 빌포르 씨는 딸에게 팔을 내미는 것으로만 대답했다. 마침맞은 일이었다. 발랑틴이 어지러워 비틀거렸던 것이다. 빌포르 부인이 곧장 달려와 거들며 남편이 딸을 마차로 끌고 가는 것을 도왔고, 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"이 무슨 기막힌 일인지! 누가 짐작이나 했겠어요? 아, 그래요, 정말이지 이상한 일이에요!\"\n\n그렇게 가엾은 가족은 떠나갔고, 남은 저녁 시간 위로 슬픔의 구름이 드리웠다. 계단 아래에서 발랑틴은 바루아가 자신을 기다리고 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"누아르티에 어르신께서 오늘 밤 아가씨를 뵙고자 하십니다.\" 그가 낮은 소리로 말했다.\n\n\"사랑하는 할머님 곁을 떠나거든 가겠다고 전해 주세요.\" 그녀가 대답했다. 지금 자신이 가장 도움이 될 수 있는 사람은 생메랑 부인이라고, 그녀는 참된 섬세함으로 느꼈던 것이다.\n\n발랑틴은 할머니가 침대에 누워 있는 것을 보았다. 말없는 어루만짐, 가슴을 쥐어짜는 흐느낌, 끊어지는 한숨, 뜨거운 눈물, 이 슬픈 만남에서 오간 것은 그게 전부였다. 그동안 빌포르 부인은 남편의 팔에 기댄 채, 적어도 그 가엾은 미망인에게만은 겉으로나마 온갖 예를 갖추었다. 이윽고 그녀가 남편에게 속삭였다.\n\n\"제가 자리를 비키는 편이 나을 것 같아요, 허락해 주신다면요. 제 모습이 아직도 어머님을 괴롭게 하는 것 같으니까요.\" 생메랑 부인이 그 말을 들었다.\n\n\"그래, 그래.\" 그녀가 발랑틴에게 나직이 말했다. \"저 사람은 가게 해라. 하지만 너는 남아 있거라.\"\n\n빌포르 부인이 나갔고, 발랑틴은 침대 곁에 홀로 남았다. 뜻밖의 죽음에 놀라움을 이기지 못한 검사가 아내를 따라 나갔기 때문이다. 한편 바루아는 처음으로 늙은 누아르티에에게 돌아갔다. 앞서 말했듯, 집 안의 소란을 듣고 그 까닭을 알아보라며 늙은 하인을 보냈던 그였다. 하인이 돌아오자, 그의 빠르고 영민한 눈이 심부름꾼에게 물음을 던졌다.\n\n\"아아, 어르신.\" 바루아가 외쳤다. \"큰 변고가 생겼습니다. 생메랑 부인께서 오셨는데, 부군께서 돌아가셨답니다!\"\n\n생메랑 후작과 누아르티에는 결코 두터운 우정으로 맺어진 사이가 아니었다. 그래도 한 노인의 죽음은 언제나 다른 노인에게 적잖은 충격을 주는 법이다. 누아르티에는 압도되어 깊은 생각에 잠긴 듯 고개를 가슴께로 떨구었다. 그러더니 묻겠다는 표시로 한쪽 눈을 감았다.\n\n바루아가 물었다. \"발랑틴 아가씨 말씀이십니까?\"\n\n누아르티에가 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"아시다시피 아가씨는 무도회에 가 계십니다. 정장 차림으로 어르신께 작별 인사를 드리러 오셨으니까요.\" 누아르티에가 다시 왼쪽 눈을 감았다.\n\n\"아가씨를 보고 싶으신 겁니까?\" 누아르티에가 또 한 번 그렇다는 표시를 했다.\n\n\"그럼 틀림없이 모르세르 부인 댁으로 아가씨를 데리러 갔을 테니, 아가씨가 돌아오시기를 기다렸다가 이리로 올라오시도록 청하겠습니다. 그렇게 하길 바라십니까?\"\n\n\"그래.\" 환자가 대답했다.\n\n그리하여 앞서 보았듯, 바루아는 발랑틴을 기다렸다가 할아버지의 뜻을 알렸다. 그래서 발랑틴은 생메랑 부인 곁을 떠나 누아르티에에게로 올라갔다. 부인은 슬픔에 잠긴 끝에 마침내 피로에 굴복하여 열에 들뜬 잠에 빠져 있었다. 손이 닿는 곳에 작은 탁자를 놓아 두었는데, 그 위에는 그녀가 늘 마시는 오렌지 음료 한 병과 잔이 놓여 있었다. 그런 뒤, 앞서 말했듯 어린 아가씨는 누아르티에 씨를 보러 침대 곁을 떠났다.\n\n발랑틴은 노인에게 입을 맞추었다. 노인이 어찌나 다정한 눈길로 바라보던지, 이제 샘이 마른 줄로만 여겼던 그녀의 눈에 다시 눈물이 그렁그렁 차올랐다. 노신사는 변함없는 표정으로 그녀를 계속 바라보았다.\n\n\"네, 네.\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"제겐 아직 다정한 할아버지가 남아 계시다는 말씀이시죠, 그렇지요?\" 노인이 바로 그런 뜻이라고 알렸다. \"아, 그래요, 다행히 그렇지요.\" 발랑틴이 대답했다. \"그게 아니라면 저는 어떻게 되겠어요?\"\n\n새벽 한 시였다. 자신도 잠자리에 들고 싶었던 바루아는, 이런 슬픈 일을 겪은 뒤에는 누구나 휴식이 필요하다고 일렀다. 누아르티에는 자신에게 필요한 단 하나의 휴식은 손녀를 보는 것뿐이라고 말하지는 못한 채, 손녀에게 잘 자라고 인사를 건넸다. 슬픔과 피로로 그녀가 몹시 아파 보였던 것이다.\n\n이튿날 아침, 그녀는 할머니가 침대에 누워 있는 것을 보았다. 열은 가라앉기는커녕 오히려 두 눈이 번들거렸고, 격렬한 신경의 동요로 괴로워하는 듯했다.\n\n\"오, 사랑하는 할머님, 더 안 좋으신 거예요?\" 이 모든 동요의 기색을 알아챈 발랑틴이 외쳤다.\n\n\"아니다, 얘야, 아니야.\" 생메랑 부인이 말했다. \"그저 네 아버지를 부르고 싶어서 네가 오기를 애타게 기다리고 있었단다.\"\n\n\"제 아버지를요?\" 발랑틴이 불안하게 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 그이와 이야기하고 싶구나.\"\n\n발랑틴은 까닭도 모른 채 할머니의 뜻을 거스를 엄두를 내지 못했고, 잠시 뒤 빌포르가 들어왔다.\n\n\"여보게.\" 생메랑 부인이 에두르는 법 없이, 마치 지체할 시간이 없는 사람처럼 말했다. \"이 아이의 혼사에 관해 내게 편지를 보냈었지?\"\n\n\"네, 부인.\" 빌포르가 대답했다. \"그저 계획만 한 게 아니라 다 정해진 일입니다.\"\n\n\"사위 될 사람이 프란츠 데피네 씨라고 했던가?\"\n\n\"네, 부인.\"\n\n\"우리 편이었다가 찬탈자가 엘바섬에서 돌아오기 며칠 전에 암살된 그 데피네 장군의 아들 아닌가?\"\n\n\"바로 그 사람입니다.\"\n\"그 사람은 자코뱅의 손녀와 혼인하는 걸 꺼리지 않는다던가?\"\n\n\"우리네 내전은 이제 다행히 사그라들었습니다, 어머님.\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"데피네 씨는 아버지가 죽었을 때 아직 어린아이였고, 누아르티에 씨에 대해 아는 게 거의 없습니다. 기껍지는 않더라도, 적어도 무심하게는 그분을 대할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"걸맞은 혼처인가?\"\n\n\"모든 면에서요.\"\n\n\"그 젊은이는?\"\n\n\"누구에게나 존경을 받지요.\"\n\n\"자네는 그를 인정하는가?\"\n\n\"제가 아는 가장 예의 바른 젊은이 가운데 하나입니다.\"\n\n이 모든 대화 내내 발랑틴은 말없이 있었다.\n\n\"좋네, 여보게.\" 생메랑 부인이 잠시 생각에 잠긴 뒤 말했다. \"내 혼사를 서둘러야겠어. 내게 남은 시간이 얼마 없으니까.\"\n\n\"부인께서요?\" \"할머님께서요?\" 빌포르 씨와 발랑틴이 동시에 외쳤다.\n\n\"내가 무슨 말을 하는지 나는 안다.\" 후작 부인이 말을 이었다. \"내 자네를 재촉해야겠어. 어미가 없는 아이니, 적어도 혼인을 축복해 줄 할미라도 있어야 하지 않겠나. 자네가 그토록 빨리 잊어버린 내 가엾은 르네에게서 그 아이에게 남은 사람은 나 하나뿐일세.\"\n\n\"아, 부인.\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"제가 제 아이에게 어머니를 새로 마련해 줘야 했다는 걸 잊으셨군요.\"\n\n\"새어머니는 결코 어머니가 아닐세, 여보게. 하지만 그건 지금 논할 바가 아니야. 우리 일은 발랑틴에 관한 것이니, 죽은 이는 편히 쉬게 두세.\"\n\n이 모든 말이 어찌나 빠르게 쏟아졌던지, 그 대화에는 헛소리의 시작 같은 무언가가 어려 있었다.\n\n\"부인 뜻대로 하겠습니다.\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"더구나 부인의 바람이 제 바람과 일치하니까요. 데피네 씨가 파리에 도착하는 대로—\"\n\n\"사랑하는 할머님.\" 발랑틴이 말을 가로막았다. \"예법을 생각하셔야지요. 이렇게 막 초상을 치른 마당에요. 이런 슬픈 정황 속에서 제가 혼인하기를 바라지는 않으시겠지요?\"\n\n\"얘야.\" 노부인이 매섭게 외쳤다. \"미래를 대비하는 것을 마음 약한 자들이나 주저하게 만드는 그런 관습 따위의 핑계는 듣고 싶지 않다. 나도 어머니의 임종 자리에서 혼례를 올렸지만, 그렇다고 덜 행복했던 것도 결코 아니란다.\"\n\n\"그래도 죽음을 입에 올리시다니요, 부인.\" 빌포르가 말했다.\n\n\"그래도라니? 늘 그렇지! 내 말하지 않았나, 나는 죽을 것이라고. 알겠나? 그러니 죽기 전에 사위를 보고 싶어. 내 아이를 행복하게 해 달라고 그에게 이르고 싶고, 그가 내 말을 따를 작정인지 그 눈에서 읽어 내고 싶네. 한마디로, 나는 그를 알아 둘 것이야. 기어이!\" 노부인이 무시무시한 표정으로 말을 이었다. \"그가 제 도리를 다하지 않는다면, 내 무덤 깊은 곳에서라도 일어나 그를 찾아낼 수 있도록 말일세!\"\n\n\"부인.\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"거의 광기처럼 보이는 그런 격앙된 생각은 떨쳐 버리셔야 합니다. 죽은 자는 일단 무덤에 묻히면 다시는 일어나지 못합니다.\"\n\n\"그러면 내 말하건대, 여보게, 자네가 틀렸네. 간밤에 나는 무서운 잠을 잤어. 마치 내 영혼이 이미 내 몸 위를 떠도는 것 같았지. 뜨려고 애써 본 두 눈은 내 뜻과 달리 감겼는데, 무엇보다도 자네에게는 도무지 믿기지 않을 일이겠지만, 나는 눈을 감은 채로 보았네. 지금 자네가 서 있는 바로 그 자리에서, 빌포르 부인의 화장실로 통하는 문이 있는 저 구석에서—내 말하건대, 나는 보았네, 소리 없이 들어서는 하얀 형체를 말일세.\"\n\n발랑틴이 비명을 질렀다.\n\n\"열 때문에 심란해지신 겁니다, 부인.\" 빌포르가 말했다.\n\n\"믿고 싶지 않거든 그러게나. 하지만 나는 내가 한 말을 확신하네. 나는 하얀 형체를 보았어. 그리고 마치 내가 한 가지 감각의 증언만으로는 못 미더워할까 봐 그러는 듯, 내 잔이 치워지는 소리도 들었네. 지금 저 탁자 위에 있는 바로 그 잔 말일세.\"\n\n\"오, 사랑하는 할머님, 그건 꿈이었어요.\"\n\n\"꿈이 아니었기에, 나는 종을 향해 손을 뻗었지. 하지만 그러자 그 그림자가 사라졌고, 곧 하녀가 불을 들고 들어왔어.\"\n\n\"하지만 하녀는 아무도 못 봤지요?\"\n\n\"유령은 마땅히 보아야 할 사람의 눈에만 보이는 법이다. 그것은 내 남편의 영혼이었어! 그래, 내 남편의 영혼이 내게 올 수 있다면, 어찌하여 내 영혼은 손녀를 지키러 다시 나타나지 못하겠느냐? 그 인연이 내가 보기엔 한층 더 직접적이지 않으냐.\"\n\n\"오, 부인.\" 빌포르가 자신도 모르게 깊이 마음이 흔들려 말했다. \"그런 음울한 생각에 휘둘리지 마십시오. 부인께서는 오래도록 저희와 함께, 행복하게, 사랑받고 존경받으며 사실 겁니다. 그리고 저희가 부인으로 하여금 잊게 해 드리겠습니다—\"\n\n\"결코, 결코, 결코 아닐세.\" 후작 부인이 말했다. \"데피네 씨는 언제 돌아오나?\"\n\n\"머지않아 올 겁니다.\"\n\n\"잘됐군. 그가 도착하는 대로 내게 알리게. 우리는 서둘러야 해. 그리고 나는 공증인도 보고 싶네. 우리 재산이 모두 발랑틴에게 돌아가도록 확실히 해 두고 싶으니까.\"\n\n\"아, 할머님.\" 발랑틴이 그 뜨거운 이마에 입술을 대며 속삭였다. \"저를 죽이실 작정이세요? 오, 이렇게 열이 나시는데. 공증인이 아니라 의사를 불러야 해요!\"\n\n\"의사라고?\" 그녀가 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다. \"나는 병난 게 아니다. 그저 목이 마를 뿐이야.\"\n\n\"무얼 드시겠어요, 사랑하는 할머님?\"\n\n\"늘 마시던 거다, 얘야. 내 잔이 저기 탁자 위에 있으니, 그걸 다오, 발랑틴.\" 발랑틴은 오렌지 음료를 잔에 따라 할머니에게 건넸지만, 어쩐지 두려운 마음이 들었다. 유령이 손을 댔다고 할머니가 여긴 바로 그 잔이었기 때문이다.\n\n후작 부인은 단숨에 잔을 비우고는 베개 위로 돌아누우며 되뇌었다.\n\n\"공증인을, 공증인을!\"\n\n빌포르 씨가 방을 나갔고, 발랑틴은 할머니의 침대 곁에 앉았다. 가엾은 아이는 자신이야말로, 늙은 친척에게 권했던 그 의사가 필요한 듯했다. 양 볼에는 붉은 반점이 타올랐고, 호흡은 짧고 가빴으며, 맥박은 열에 들떠 뛰었다. 그녀는 막시밀리앙의 절망을 떠올리고 있었다. 생메랑 부인이 자기편이기는커녕 자기도 모르게 그의 적 노릇을 하고 있다는 사실을 그가 알게 되면 어떻게 될까.\n\n여러 번 그녀는 할머니에게 모든 것을 털어놓을까 생각했고, 막시밀리앙 모렐의 이름이 알베르 드 모르세르나 라울 드 샤토르노였다면 한순간도 망설이지 않았을 것이다. 그러나 모렐은 평민 출신이었고, 도도한 생메랑 후작 부인이 귀족이 아닌 모든 자를 얼마나 멸시하는지 발랑틴은 잘 알고 있었다. 막 비밀을 털어놓으려 할 때마다, 그래 봐야 소용없으리라는 서글픈 확신에 그녀의 비밀은 번번이 억눌렸다. 일단 아버지와 어머니에게 발각되는 날이면, 모든 것이 끝장날 테니까.\n\n그렇게 두 시간이 흘렀다. 생메랑 부인은 열에 들뜬 잠에 빠져 있었고, 공증인이 도착했다. 아주 낮은 소리로 그가 왔음을 알렸는데도, 생메랑 부인은 베개에서 몸을 일으켰다.\n\n\"공증인이!\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"들어오라 하게.\"\n\n문 앞에 있던 공증인이 곧바로 들어왔다. \"가거라, 발랑틴.\" 생메랑 부인이 말했다. \"이분과 나만 있게 해다오.\"\n\n\"하지만, 할머님—\"\n\n\"나가거라. 가!\"\n\n어린 아가씨는 할머니에게 입을 맞추고 손수건을 눈에 댄 채 나갔다. 문간에서 그녀는 시종을 만났는데, 그가 의사가 식당에서 기다리고 있다고 일러 주었다. 발랑틴은 곧장 달려 내려갔다. 의사는 그 집안의 벗이자 당대의 가장 명민한 사람 가운데 하나였고, 자신이 태어나는 것을 지켜본 발랑틴을 무척 아꼈다. 그에게도 발랑틴 또래의 딸이 있었지만, 그 어미가 폐병을 앓았던 탓에 그 아이의 삶은 그에게 끊임없는 근심과 두려움의 원천이었다.\n\n\"오.\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"얼마나 애타게 선생님을 기다렸는지 몰라요, 다브리니 선생님. 그런데 우선, 마들렌과 앙투아네트는 잘 지내나요?\"\n\n마들렌은 다브리니 씨의 딸이었고, 앙투아네트는 그의 조카딸이었다. 다브리니 씨가 서글피 미소 지었다.\n\n\"앙투아네트는 아주 잘 지내고.\" 그가 말했다. \"마들렌도 그럭저럭 지낸단다. 그런데 네가 나를 불렀지, 얘야. 아버지나 빌포르 부인이 편찮으신 게 아니고. 너로 말하자면, 우리 의사들이 환자에게서 신경을 떼어 낼 수야 없다만, 내가 보기엔 네게 더 필요한 건, 공상이 너무 넓게 뻗어 나가도록 두지 말라는 당부 정도일 뿐인 것 같구나.\"\n\n발랑틴의 얼굴이 붉어졌다. 다브리니 씨는 거의 기적에 가까울 만큼 꿰뚫어 보는 재주를 지녔으니, 늘 마음을 통해 몸을 다스리는 의사들 가운데 하나였던 것이다.\n\n\"아니에요.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"제 가엾은 할머니 때문이에요. 저희에게 닥친 불행을 아시지요, 그렇지 않나요?\"\n\n\"나는 아무것도 모른단다.\" 다브리니 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아아.\" 발랑틴이 눈물을 참으며 말했다. \"제 할아버지가 돌아가셨어요.\"\n\n\"생메랑 후작께서?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"갑자기?\"\n\n\"졸중 발작으로요.\"\n\n\"졸중 발작이라고?\" 의사가 되뇌었다.\n\n\"네, 그래서 가엾은 제 할머니는, 한시도 곁을 떠나지 않았던 남편이 자기를 불렀다고, 그래서 자기도 가서 함께해야 한다고 여기세요. 오, 다브리니 선생님, 부탁이에요, 할머니를 위해 무언가 해 주세요!\"\n\n\"할머니는 어디 계시냐?\"\n\n\"공증인과 함께 방에 계세요.\"\n\n\"누아르티에 씨는?\"\n\n\"여전하세요. 정신은 더없이 또렷하시지만, 움직이지도 말하지도 못하시는 것도 그대로고요.\"\n\n\"그리고 너를 향한 그 사랑도 여전하시고, 응, 얘야?\"\n\n\"네.\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"할아버지는 저를 무척 아끼셨어요.\"\n\n\"너를 사랑하지 않을 사람이 어디 있겠니?\" 발랑틴이 서글피 미소 지었다. \"할머니의 증세는 어떤 것이냐?\"\n\n\"극심한 신경의 흥분과 이상하리만치 동요된 잠이에요. 오늘 아침에는 주무시면서 자기 영혼이 몸 위를 떠돌고, 동시에 그 몸을 내려다보고 있다고 여기셨어요. 분명 헛소리였을 거예요. 또 어떤 유령이 방으로 들어오는 걸 보았고, 그것이 잔을 만질 때 나는 소리까지 들었다고 하시고요.\"\n\n\"기이한 일이로구나.\" 의사가 말했다. \"생메랑 부인께서 그런 환각에 시달리시는 줄은 몰랐는데.\"\n\n\"제가 할머니를 이런 상태로 본 건 처음이에요.\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"오늘 아침엔 어찌나 저를 놀라게 하시던지, 미치신 줄로만 알았어요. 그리고 아시다시피 의지가 강한 분인 제 아버지조차 깊이 충격을 받은 듯 보이셨고요.\"\n\n\"가서 보자꾸나.\" 의사가 말했다. \"네 말이 무척 이상하게 들리는구나.\" 그때 공증인이 내려왔고, 발랑틴은 할머니가 혼자 계시다는 말을 들었다.\n\n\"올라가 보세요.\" 그녀가 의사에게 말했다.\n\n\"너는?\"\n\n\"오, 저는 엄두가 나질 않아요. 할머니가 선생님을 부르지 못하게 하셨거든요. 그리고 선생님 말씀대로, 저 자신이 동요되고 열이 나고 몸이 좋지 않아요. 정원에 나가 한 바퀴 돌면서 마음을 추슬러야겠어요.\"\n\n의사는 발랑틴의 손을 꼭 잡고는 할머니를 보러 갔고, 그녀는 계단을 내려갔다. 정원의 어느 쪽이 그녀가 가장 좋아하는 산책길인지는 굳이 말할 필요가 없으리라. 집을 둘러싼 화단에서 잠시 머물며 허리춤이나 머리에 꽂을 장미 한 송이를 꺾은 뒤, 그녀는 벤치로 이어지는 어두운 오솔길로 접어들었다. 그러고는 벤치에서 다시 철문 쪽으로 걸어갔다. 여느 때처럼 발랑틴은 잠시 꽃들 사이를 거닐었지만, 꽃을 꺾지는 않았다. 마음속의 슬픔이 이 소박한 장식조차 허락하지 않았던 것이다. 아직 겉으로 상복을 갖춰 입을 겨를은 없었지만 말이다.\n\n이윽고 그녀는 오솔길 쪽으로 발길을 돌렸다. 걸음을 옮기던 그녀는 자신의 이름을 부르는 목소리가 들리는 듯했다. 그녀는 놀라 멈춰 섰다. 그러자 그 목소리가 더 또렷이 귓가에 닿았고, 그녀는 그것이 막시밀리앙의 목소리임을 알아차렸다.\n\n# Chapter 73. The Promise\n\n막시밀리앙 모렐이었다. 그는 전날부터 비참한 시간을 보내고 있었다. 연인 특유의 직감으로, 생메랑 부인이 돌아오고 후작이 세상을 떠난 뒤 빌포르 씨 집안에 발랑틴을 향한 자신의 사랑과 관련된 무슨 일이 벌어지리라는 것을 그는 예감하고 있었다. 앞으로 보게 되겠지만 그 예감은 현실이 되었고, 불안한 예감에 떠밀린 그는 창백하게 떨면서 밤나무 아래의 철문 앞까지 와 있었다.\n\n발랑틴은 이 슬픔과 불안의 까닭을 알지 못했다. 평소 그가 찾아오는 시간도 아니었기에, 그녀는 그저 우연히, 아니 어쩌면 마음이 통한 탓에 그곳으로 나왔다. 모렐이 부르자 그녀가 철문으로 달려왔다.\n\n\"이 시간에 여기에?\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 가엾은 사람.\" 모렐이 대답했다. \"나쁜 소식을 전하러, 또 들으러 왔어.\"\n\n\"정말이지 이 집은 초상집이에요.\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"말해 주세요, 막시밀리앙. 슬픔의 잔이 이미 가득 찬 것 같지만요.\"\n\n\"사랑하는 발랑틴.\" 모렐이 자신의 동요를 감추려 애쓰며 말했다. \"부탁이니 들어 줘. 지금부터 하려는 이야기는 아주 심각한 거야. 결혼은 언제 하게 되는 거지?\"\n\n\"전부 말씀드릴게요.\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"당신한테 숨길 건 아무것도 없으니까요. 오늘 아침에 그 이야기가 나왔어요. 제 유일한 의지처라 믿었던 할머니께서 결혼에 찬성하셨을 뿐 아니라 너무도 간절히 바라고 계세요. 그래서 데피네 씨가 도착하기만 기다리고 있고, 그다음 날 계약서에 서명하기로 했어요.\"\n\n청년의 입에서 깊은 한숨이 새어 나왔다. 그는 사랑하는 여인을 오래도록 슬프게 바라보았다.\n\n\"아아.\" 그가 대답했다. \"이렇게 당신 입으로 직접 내 사형선고를 듣다니 끔찍하군. 판결은 내려졌고, 몇 시간 뒤면 집행될 거야. 그렇게 될 수밖에 없겠지. 나도 막으려 하지는 않겠어. 하지만 데피네 씨가 도착하기만 하면 계약서에 서명하고, 그다음 날 당신이 그의 사람이 된다고 했지. 그럼 내일이면 당신은 데피네 씨와 약혼하게 되는 거야. 그가 오늘 아침에 파리에 왔으니까.\" 발랑틴이 외마디 비명을 질렀다.\n\n\"한 시간 전에 나는 몬테크리스토의 집에 있었어.\" 모렐이 말했다. \"그가 당신 가족이 겪은 슬픔에 대해, 내가 당신의 비탄에 대해 이야기를 나누고 있는데, 마차 한 대가 안마당으로 굴러 들어왔지. 그때까지 나는 예감 따위를 믿어 본 적이 없었어. 하지만 이제는 믿지 않을 수 없게 됐어, 발랑틴. 그 마차 소리에 나는 몸서리쳤어. 곧 계단을 오르는 발소리가 들렸는데, 그 발소리가 마치 돈 후안에게 들이닥친 기사단장의 발소리처럼 나를 공포에 떨게 했지. 마침내 문이 열렸어. 알베르 드 모르세르가 먼저 들어왔지. 나는 내 두려움이 헛것이었기를 바라기 시작했어. 그런데 그 뒤로 다른 젊은이가 들어섰고, 백작이 외쳤지. '아, 여기 프란츠 데피네 남작이 오셨군!' 나는 온 힘과 용기를 끌어모아 버텼어. 아마 창백해지고 몸을 떨었겠지만, 분명히 미소는 지었지. 그리고 오 분 뒤에 그 자리를 떴어. 오간 말은 한마디도 듣지 못한 채로.\"\n\n\"가엾은 막시밀리앙!\" 발랑틴이 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"발랑틴, 이제 당신이 내게 답을 줘야 할 때야. 그리고 내 목숨이 당신 대답에 달려 있다는 걸 잊지 마. 어떻게 할 생각이지?\" 발랑틴이 고개를 떨구었다. 그녀는 짓눌려 있었다.\n\n\"들어 봐.\" 모렐이 말했다. \"당신이 지금 우리 처지를 헤아려 본 게 이번이 처음은 아니잖아. 우리 처지는 심각하고 급박해. 지금은 쓸데없는 슬픔에 빠질 때가 아니라고 생각해. 그런 건 한가로이 고통받기를 즐기고 남몰래 비탄에 빠지기를 바라는 사람들 몫으로 남겨 두자. 세상에는 그런 사람들도 있고, 하느님께서도 분명 이 땅에서의 그 체념을 천국에서 보상해 주시겠지. 하지만 맞서 싸우려는 사람은 단 한순간의 귀한 시간도 잃어서는 안 돼. 운명이 가하는 일격을 그 즉시 되돌려쳐야 해. 우리에게 닥친 불운에 맞서 싸울 생각이 있어? 말해 줘, 발랑틴. 그걸 알고 싶어서 온 거니까.\"\n\n발랑틴이 떨면서 놀란 눈으로 그를 바라보았다. 아버지와 할머니, 온 집안에 저항한다는 생각은 그녀에게 한 번도 떠오른 적이 없었다.\n\n\"무슨 말씀이세요, 막시밀리앙?\" 발랑틴이 물었다. \"싸운다는 게 무슨 뜻이죠? 오, 그건 신성모독이에요. 뭐라고요? 제가 아버지의 명령에, 돌아가시는 할머니의 소원에 거역하라고요? 불가능해요!\"\n\n모렐이 흠칫했다.\n\n\"당신은 워낙 고결해서 내 말을 이해 못 할 리가 없어. 너무도 잘 이해하기에 이미 굽히고 있는 거지, 사랑하는 막시밀리앙. 아니요, 아니요. 저 자신과 싸우고, 당신 말처럼 남몰래 슬픔을 견디려면 제 모든 힘이 필요할 거예요. 하지만 아버지를 슬프게 하고, 할머니의 마지막 순간을 어지럽히는 일은, 절대로 못 해요!\"\n\n\"당신 말이 맞아.\" 모렐이 차분하게 말했다.\n\n\"어쩌면 그런 말투로 말하세요!\" 발랑틴이 외쳤다.\n\n\"당신을 흠모하는 사람으로서 하는 말입니다, 마드무아젤.\"\n\n\"마드무아젤이라뇨.\" 발랑틴이 외쳤다. \"마드무아젤이라뇨! 오, 이기적인 사람! 절망에 빠진 저를 보면서 못 알아듣는 척하다니!\"\n\n\"오해야. 나는 당신을 완벽하게 이해해. 당신은 빌포르 씨에게 맞서지 않을 거고, 후작 부인의 비위를 거스르지 않을 거야. 그리고 내일이면 당신을 남편에게 묶어 둘 계약서에 서명하겠지.\"\n\n\"하지만, 맙소사! 말해 봐요, 제가 달리 어떻게 할 수 있겠어요?\"\n\n\"내게 묻지 마, 마드무아젤. 이런 일에서 나는 형편없는 판관일 거야. 내 이기심이 눈을 멀게 할 테니까.\" 모렐이 대답했다. 그의 낮아진 목소리와 꽉 쥔 두 주먹이 점점 커지는 절망을 드러내고 있었다.\n\n\"제가 받아들일 마음이 있다는 걸 알았다면, 당신은 뭐라고 제안하셨을까요, 막시밀리앙?\"\n\n\"그건 내가 할 말이 아니야.\"\n\n\"틀렸어요. 어떻게 해야 할지 당신이 조언해 주셔야 해요.\"\n\n\"진심으로 내 조언을 구하는 거야, 발랑틴?\"\n\n\"물론이죠, 사랑하는 막시밀리앙. 좋은 조언이라면 따를 거예요. 제가 당신에게 얼마나 헌신적인지 알잖아요.\"\n\n\"발랑틴.\" 모렐이 헐거워진 널빤지 하나를 밀어내며 말했다. \"내가 화낸 걸 용서한다는 표시로 손을 줘. 정신이 혼란스러워서, 지난 한 시간 동안 더없이 터무니없는 생각들이 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔어. 오, 당신이 내 조언을 거절한다면……\"\n\n\"무슨 조언인데요?\" 발랑틴이 눈을 들어 하늘을 보며 한숨을 쉬었다.\n\n\"나는 자유로운 몸이야.\" 막시밀리앙이 대답했다. \"당신을 부양할 만큼 넉넉하기도 하고. 맹세코, 내 입술이 당신 이마에 닿기도 전에 당신을 정식 아내로 맞이하겠어.\"\n\n\"당신은 저를 떨게 하는군요!\" 처녀가 말했다.\n\n\"나를 따라와.\" 모렐이 말했다. \"내 누이에게 데려갈게. 그녀도 당신의 누이가 될 자격이 있는 사람이야. 우리는 알제로, 영국으로, 미국으로 배를 타고 떠나는 거야. 아니면 당신이 원한다면 시골에 들어가 살다가, 친구들이 당신 가족을 화해시킨 뒤에야 파리로 돌아오면 돼.\"\n\n발랑틴이 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"그럴 줄 알았어요, 막시밀리앙.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그건 미친 사람의 조언이에요. 제가 그 자리에서 '불가능해요, 모렐, 불가능해요!'라는 말로 당신을 막지 않는다면, 저는 당신보다 더 미친 사람일 거예요.\"\n\n\"그럼 당신은 운명이 정해 준 것에, 맞서 싸워 보려는 시도조차 없이 순순히 따르겠다는 거야?\" 모렐이 슬프게 말했다.\n\n\"네, 죽는 한이 있더라도요!\"\n\n\"좋아, 발랑틴.\" 막시밀리앙이 다시 말했다. \"그럼 나는 당신이 옳다는 말밖에는 할 수가 없군. 정말이지 미친 사람은 나야. 더없이 선의를 가진 사람조차 격정에 눈이 먼다는 걸 당신이 내게 증명해 보이는군. 나는 당신의 차분한 분별력을 높이 평가해. 그러니까 정리하자면, 내일 당신은 프란츠 데피네 씨와 돌이킬 수 없이 약혼하게 되는 거야. 계약서 서명이라 부르는, 희극의 효과를 높이려고 고안된 그 연극 같은 형식 절차에 의해서뿐 아니라, 당신 자신의 의지로 말이야?\"\n\n\"또 저를 절망으로 몰아넣는군요, 막시밀리앙.\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"또 상처에 비수를 꽂는군요! 말해 봐요, 당신 누이가 이런 제안을 받는다면 당신은 어떻게 하겠어요?\"\n\n\"마드무아젤.\" 모렐이 쓰디쓴 미소를 지으며 대답했다. \"나는 이기적인 사람이야. 당신이 이미 그렇게 말했지. 그리고 이기적인 사람으로서, 내 처지에 놓인다면 남들이 어떻게 할지는 생각하지 않고, 나 자신이 무엇을 할 작정인지만 생각해. 나는 그저 당신을 안 지 채 일 년도 되지 않았다는 것만 생각해. 당신을 처음 본 그날부터 내 모든 행복의 희망은 당신의 애정을 얻는 데 걸려 있었어. 어느 날 당신은 나를 사랑한다고 고백했고, 그날 이후로 내 미래의 행복에 대한 희망은 당신을 얻는 데 달려 있게 됐지. 당신을 얻는 것이 내게는 곧 삶이니까. 이제 나는 더는 아무것도 생각하지 않아. 다만 운명이 내게 등을 돌렸다는 것만 말할 뿐이야. 천국을 얻을 줄 알았는데 이제 그걸 잃었지. 도박꾼이 가진 것뿐 아니라 가지지도 않은 것까지 잃는 건 날마다 일어나는 흔한 일이야.\"\n\n모렐은 이 말을 완벽하게 차분한 어조로 했다. 발랑틴은 크고 살피는 듯한 두 눈으로 잠시 그를 바라보며, 가슴속에서 몸부림치는 슬픔을 모렐이 알아채지 못하도록 애썼다.\n\n\"하지만, 한마디로 말해서, 당신은 어떻게 할 작정인가요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"마드무아젤, 영광스럽게도 당신께 작별 인사를 드릴 작정입니다. 그리고 당신의 삶이 너무도 평온하고, 너무도 행복하고, 너무도 충만하게 채워져, 당신의 기억 속에조차 내가 들어설 자리가 없기를 진심으로 바란다고 엄숙히 말씀드릴 작정입니다.\"\n\n\"오!\" 발랑틴이 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"안녕, 발랑틴, 안녕!\" 모렐이 고개를 숙이며 말했다.\n\n\"어디로 가는 거예요?\" 처녀가 외쳤다. 그녀는 틈새로 손을 뻗어 막시밀리앙의 외투를 붙잡았다. 자신의 격앙된 감정으로 미루어, 연인의 차분함이 진짜일 리 없다는 것을 알아챈 것이다. \"어디로 가는 거예요?\"\n\n\"가는 거야. 당신 가족에게 새로운 분란을 가져오지 않으려고. 그리고 나와 같은 처지에 놓인 모든 정직하고 헌신적인 사람이 따를 만한 본보기를 남기려고.\"\n\n\"나를 떠나기 전에, 무엇을 할 작정인지 말해 줘요, 막시밀리앙.\" 청년이 슬프게 미소 지었다.\n\n\"말해 줘요, 말해 줘요!\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"제발 부탁이에요.\"\n\n\"당신의 결심이 바뀌었어, 발랑틴?\"\n\n\"바뀔 수 없어요, 불행한 사람아. 바뀌어선 안 된다는 걸 당신도 알잖아요!\" 처녀가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그럼 안녕, 발랑틴!\"\n\n모렐이 떠나려 하자, 발랑틴은 도저히 가졌으리라 짐작할 수 없는 힘으로 철문을 흔들었다. 그리고 두 손을 틈새로 내밀어 그의 손을 맞잡고는 비틀듯 꼭 쥐었다. \"당신이 무엇을 할 작정인지 알아야겠어요!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"어디로 가는 거예요?\"\n\n\"오, 두려워 마.\" 막시밀리앙이 조금 떨어진 곳에서 멈추며 말했다. \"내게 예비된 가혹한 운명을 다른 사람에게 떠넘길 생각은 없어. 다른 사람 같으면 프란츠 씨를 찾아가, 그를 자극해, 결투를 벌이겠다고 위협할지도 모르지. 하지만 그건 다 어리석은 짓이야. 프란츠 씨가 이 일과 무슨 상관이 있겠어? 그는 오늘 아침에 나를 처음 봤고, 봤다는 사실조차 벌써 잊었을 거야. 당신네 두 집안이 당신들의 결합을 정했을 때, 그는 내가 존재하는지조차 몰랐어. 나는 프란츠 씨에게 아무런 원한이 없고, 그에게 벌이 떨어지지는 않을 거라고 약속할게.\"\n\n\"그럼 누구에게요? 저에게요?\"\n\n\"당신에게? 발랑틴! 오, 천만에! 여자는 신성해. 사랑하는 여자는 거룩한 존재야.\"\n\n\"그럼 당신 자신에게요, 불행한 사람아. 당신 자신에게요?\"\n\n\"죄가 있는 사람은 나 하나뿐이잖아, 안 그래?\" 막시밀리앙이 말했다.\n\n\"막시밀리앙!\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"막시밀리앙, 돌아와요, 제발 부탁이에요!\"\n\n그가 다정한 미소를 지으며 다가왔다. 창백한 얼굴만 아니라면 평소의 행복한 기분에 잠긴 사람으로 보일 만했다.\n\n\"들어 봐, 내 사랑, 내가 흠모하는 발랑틴.\" 그가 음악처럼 낭랑하면서도 진중한 어조로 말했다. \"우리처럼, 세상 앞에 부끄러워할 만한 생각을 단 한 번도 품어 본 적 없는 사람들은 서로의 마음을 읽을 수 있어. 나는 한 번도 낭만적인 사람이었던 적이 없고, 우울한 영웅도 아니야. 만프레드도 앤서니도 흉내 내지 않아. 하지만 말도, 항변도, 맹세도 없이, 내 삶은 당신의 삶과 얽혀 버렸어. 당신은 나를 떠나고, 그렇게 하는 게 옳아. 다시 말하지만 당신이 옳아. 하지만 당신을 잃으면, 나는 내 삶을 잃는 거야. 당신이 나를 떠나는 그 순간, 발랑틴, 나는 이 세상에 홀로 남아. 내 누이는 다행히 결혼해서 잘 살고, 매부는 그저 매부일 뿐, 다시 말해 사회생활의 끈으로만 내게 묶여 있는 사람이지. 그러니 내 쓸모없는 목숨을 더는 누구도 필요로 하지 않아. 내가 하려는 일은 이거야. 나는 당신이 결혼하는 바로 그 순간까지 기다릴 거야. 우리에게 때로 남겨지는 그 뜻밖의 기회들 가운데 그 그림자만큼이라도 놓치고 싶지 않으니까. 어쩌면 프란츠 씨가 결국 그전에 죽을 수도 있고, 당신이 제단에 다가서는 순간 벼락이 떨어질 수도 있어. 죽음을 선고받은 사람에게는 불가능해 보이는 게 아무것도 없고, 죽음에서 벗어나는 일이라면 기적도 지극히 그럴듯해 보이는 법이지. 그러니 마지막 순간까지 기다릴 거야. 그리고 내 불행이 확실해지고, 돌이킬 수 없고, 희망이 없어지면, 매부에게 한 통, 경찰청장에게 또 한 통, 내 결심을 알리는 비밀 편지를 쓸 거야. 그리고 어느 숲 모퉁이에서, 어느 심연의 가장자리에서, 어느 강가에서, 내 목숨을 끝낼 거야. 내가 프랑스에서 살았던 가장 정직한 사람의 아들이라는 사실만큼이나 분명하게.\"\n\n발랑틴이 발작하듯 떨었다. 철문을 잡았던 손이 풀리고, 두 팔이 옆구리로 떨어졌으며, 굵은 눈물 두 줄기가 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸다. 청년은 슬프지만 단호한 모습으로 그녀 앞에 서 있었다.\n\n\"오, 제발 자비를 베풀어요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신, 살아 줄 거죠, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"아니, 내 명예를 걸고 말하지만.\" 막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"하지만 그건 당신과 상관없는 일이야. 당신은 당신의 도리를 다했고, 양심에 거리낄 게 없을 테니까.\"\n\n발랑틴이 무릎을 꿇고, 터질 듯한 가슴을 부여잡았다. \"막시밀리앙.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"막시밀리앙, 내 친구, 이 땅에서의 내 오빠, 하늘에서의 내 진정한 남편이여, 제발 부탁이니, 나처럼 해 줘요. 고통 속에서라도 살아 줘요. 어쩌면 언젠가 우리가 맺어질 수도 있잖아요.\"\n\n\"안녕, 발랑틴.\" 모렐이 되풀이했다.\n\n\"오, 하느님.\" 발랑틴이 숭고한 표정으로 두 손을 하늘로 들어 올리며 말했다. \"저는 순종하는 딸로 남으려고 할 수 있는 모든 것을 다 했어요. 빌고, 애원하고, 매달렸어요. 그런데 그는 제 기도도, 제 애원도, 제 눈물도 거들떠보지 않았어요. 됐어요.\" 그녀가 눈물을 닦으며 다시 단호함을 되찾아 외쳤다. \"저는 회한으로 죽기보다는 차라리 수치로 죽기로 결심했어요. 살아 줘요, 막시밀리앙. 저는 당신 사람이 되겠어요. 말해 봐요, 언제로 할까요? 말해 줘요, 명령해 줘요, 따를게요.\"\n\n이미 몇 걸음 멀어졌던 모렐이 다시 돌아왔다. 그리고 기쁨에 창백해진 얼굴로 틈새 너머 발랑틴을 향해 두 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"발랑틴.\" 그가 말했다. \"사랑하는 발랑틴, 그렇게 말하면 안 돼. 차라리 나를 죽게 해 줘. 우리 사랑이 서로를 향한 것이라면, 내가 어찌 강압으로 당신을 얻겠어? 단지 인정 때문에 내게 살라고 하는 거야? 그렇다면 나는 차라리 죽는 편이 나아.\"\n\n\"정말로.\" 발랑틴이 중얼거렸다. \"이 세상에서 그이 말고 누가 나를 아끼겠어? 내 슬픔 속에서 그이 말고 누가 나를 위로해 줬지? 내 희망은 누구에게 걸려 있지? 피 흘리는 내 마음은 누구에게 기대지? 그이에게, 그이에게, 언제나 그이에게! 그래요, 당신이 옳아요, 막시밀리앙. 당신을 따르겠어요. 친정을 떠나겠어요, 전부 버리겠어요. 오, 배은망덕한 계집인 나는.\" 발랑틴이 흐느끼며 외쳤다. \"전부 버리겠어요, 하마터면 잊을 뻔한 사랑하는 할아버지까지도요.\"\n\n\"아니야.\" 막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"할아버지를 두고 떠나서는 안 돼. 당신 말로는 누아르티에 씨가 내게 호의를 보이셨다고 했지. 좋아, 떠나기 전에 할아버지께 전부 말씀드려. 할아버지의 동의가 하느님 눈앞에서 당신의 정당함이 되어 줄 거야. 우리가 결혼하면, 할아버지께서 우리와 함께 사시게 하자. 자식이 하나가 아니라 둘이 생기는 거지. 당신이 할아버지와 어떻게 대화하고 할아버지가 어떻게 대답하는지 내게 말해 줬잖아. 나도 그 신호 언어를 곧 익히겠어, 발랑틴. 그리고 엄숙히 약속할게. 우리를 기다리는 건 절망이 아니라 행복이라고.\"\n\n\"오, 보세요, 막시밀리앙, 당신이 제게 얼마나 큰 힘을 발휘하는지 보세요. 하마터면 당신 말을 믿을 뻔했어요. 그렇지만 당신 말은 미친 소리예요. 아버지는 저를 저주하실 거고, 워낙 완고한 분이라 절대로 용서하지 않으실 거니까요. 자, 들어 보세요, 막시밀리앙. 제가 꾀로든, 애원으로든, 우연으로든, 한마디로 무슨 수를 써서든 이 결혼을 미룰 수 있다면, 당신은 기다려 줄 건가요?\"\n\n\"그래, 약속할게. 이 끔찍한 결혼이 성사되지 않으리라는 것, 그리고 당신이 판사나 사제 앞에 끌려가더라도 거부하리라는 것을 당신이 내게 충실히 약속한 만큼이나 충실하게.\"\n\n\"이 세상에서 제게 가장 신성한 것을 걸고, 다시 말해 제 어머니를 걸고 약속할게요.\"\n\n\"그럼 우리는 기다리는 거야.\" 모렐이 말했다.\n\n\"네, 우리는 기다려요.\" 그 말에 되살아난 발랑틴이 대답했다. \"우리 같은 불행한 사람들을 구해 줄 수 있는 일들이 아주 많으니까요.\"\n\n\"나는 당신을 믿어, 발랑틴.\" 모렐이 말했다. \"당신이 하는 일은 다 잘될 거야. 다만 그들이 당신의 간청을 무시하고, 당신 아버지와 생메랑 부인이 내일 데피네 씨를 불러 계약서에 서명하라고 고집한다면……\"\n\n\"그땐 당신께 한 제 약속이 있잖아요, 막시밀리앙.\"\n\n\"서명하는 대신에……\"\n\n\"당신께 가겠어요. 그리고 우리는 달아나는 거예요. 하지만 지금부터 그때까지는, 하늘을 시험하지 말아요, 서로 만나지 말아요. 우리가 들키지 않은 건 기적이고, 하늘의 섭리예요. 들키기라도 하면, 우리가 이렇게 만난다는 게 알려지기라도 하면, 우리에겐 더 이상 방도가 없어요.\"\n\n\"당신 말이 맞아, 발랑틴. 그런데 어떻게 알아내지?\"\n\n\"공증인 데샹 씨에게서요.\"\n\n\"그분이라면 나도 알아.\"\n\n\"그리고 제 쪽에서도 편지를 쓸게요. 저를 믿어요. 저도 당신만큼이나 이 결혼이 두려워요, 막시밀리앙.\"\n\n\"고마워, 내가 흠모하는 발랑틴, 고마워. 그거면 충분해. 시간을 알게 되면, 곧장 이곳으로 달려올게. 당신은 내 도움으로 이 울타리를 쉽게 넘을 수 있을 거고, 철문 앞에 마차가 기다리고 있을 거야. 그 마차로 당신은 나와 함께 내 누이 집으로 가는 거지. 거기서 당신이 원하는 대로 숨어 살든, 사람들과 어울려 살든, 우리는 우리의 힘을 써서 억압에 맞설 수 있을 거야. 한숨으로밖에 자신을 지키지 못하는 양처럼 죽임을 당하지는 않을 거야.\"\n\n\"네.\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"이제 당신이 옳다는 걸 인정하겠어요, 막시밀리앙. 그래, 이제 당신의 약혼이 만족스러우세요?\" 처녀가 슬프게 말했다.\n\n\"내가 흠모하는 발랑틴, 내 만족의 절반도 말로는 다 표현할 수 없어.\"\n\n발랑틴이 다가왔다. 아니, 정확히는 울타리에 너무도 가까이 입술을 갖다 대어, 차갑고 무정한 그 장벽 반대편에 눌린 모렐의 입술과 거의 닿을 듯했다.\n\n\"그럼, 다시 만날 때까지 안녕히.\" 발랑틴이 몸을 떼어 내며 말했다. \"소식 주실 거죠?\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n\"고마워요, 고마워요, 내 사랑, 안녕히!\"\n\n입맞춤 소리가 들렸고, 발랑틴은 가로수 길을 따라 달아났다. 모렐은 그녀의 옷자락이 나뭇가지를 스치는 마지막 소리와 자갈을 밟는 발소리를 잡으려 귀 기울였다. 그러고는 이렇게 사랑받도록 허락받은 데 대한 형언할 수 없는 감사의 미소를 띠고 하늘로 눈을 들었다가, 그 역시 자취를 감추었다.\n\n청년은 집으로 돌아와 그날 저녁 내내, 그리고 이튿날 온종일 아무 전갈도 받지 못한 채 기다렸다. 그다음 날 아침 열 시쯤, 공증인 데샹 씨를 찾아가려고 막 나서던 참에야, 그는 우체부에게서 작은 쪽지 한 장을 받았다. 전에 그녀의 필체를 본 적은 없었지만, 그는 그것이 발랑틴이 보낸 것임을 단번에 알았다. 내용은 이러했다.\n\n\"눈물도, 간청도, 기도도 제게 아무 소용이 없었어요. 어제 저는 두 시간 동안 생필리프뒤룰 성당에 있으면서, 두 시간 내내 더없이 간절하게 기도했어요. 하늘은 사람만큼이나 완고했고, 계약서 서명은 오늘 저녁 아홉 시로 정해졌어요. 제게는 줄 약속이 하나뿐이고, 줄 마음도 하나뿐이에요. 그 약속은 당신께 바쳤고, 그 마음도 당신 것이에요. 그러니 오늘 저녁 아홉 시 십오 분 전에 철문 앞에서.\n\n당신의 약혼녀,\n\n발랑틴 드 빌포르.\"\n\n\"추신. 가엾은 할머니는 점점 더 나빠지세요. 어제는 열이 올라 헛소리를 하셨고, 오늘은 그 헛소리가 거의 광증에 가까워요. 이렇게 할머니를 두고 떠나는 제 슬픔을 잊게 해 주시려고 제게 무척 다정하게 대해 주실 거죠, 그렇죠, 모렐? 계약서가 오늘 저녁 서명된다는 사실은 누아르티에 할아버지께는 비밀에 부쳐진 것 같아요.\"\n\n모렐은 공증인에게도 들렀고, 공증인은 계약서가 그날 저녁 서명될 예정이라는 소식을 확인해 주었다. 그런 다음 그는 몬테크리스토를 찾아가서 더 많은 이야기를 들었다. 프란츠가 예식을 알리러 왔었고, 빌포르 부인도 그를 초대하지 못하는 것을 양해해 달라고 백작에게 편지를 보내왔다는 것이었다. 생메랑 씨의 죽음과 그 미망인의 위중한 병환이 모임에 어두운 그림자를 드리울 텐데, 온갖 행복을 빌어 마지않는 백작이 그런 자리를 함께하게 되는 것은 부인으로서 안타까운 일이라는 이유였다.\n\n전날 프란츠는 생메랑 부인에게 인사를 올렸다. 부인은 그를 맞으려고 자리에서 일어났으나, 곧바로 다시 누울 수밖에 없었다.\n\n모렐의 동요가 백작의 꿰뚫는 눈을 벗어나지 못했으리라는 것은 쉽게 짐작할 수 있다. 몬테크리스토는 그 어느 때보다 다정했다. 정말이지 그의 태도가 어찌나 친절했던지, 모렐은 몇 번이나 모든 것을 털어놓을 뻔했다. 하지만 발랑틴에게 한 약속을 떠올리고는 비밀을 지켰다.\n\n청년은 그날 하루 동안 발랑틴의 편지를 스무 번이나 읽었다. 그녀의 첫 편지였고, 게다가 그런 상황에서의 편지였다! 읽을 때마다 그는 그녀를 행복하게 해 주겠다는 맹세를 새로이 다졌다. 이토록 용감한 결심을 한 여인의 힘은 얼마나 위대한가! 자신을 위해 모든 것을 희생한 여인에게 그는 얼마나 헌신해야 마땅한가! 그녀는 정말이지 더없이 지극하게 사랑받아 마땅했다! 그녀는 단번에 여왕이자 아내가 되었으니, 아무리 감사하고 사랑해도 부족할 따름이었다.\n\n모렐은 발랑틴이 \"저 여기 있어요, 막시밀리앙. 와서 도와줘요.\" 하고 말하는 순간을 애타게 기다렸다. 그는 그녀의 탈출을 위해 모든 것을 준비해 두었다. 사다리 두 개를 클로버 밭에 숨겨 두었고, 막시밀리앙 혼자만 탈 이륜마차를 하인도 등불도 없이 불러 두었다. 첫 번째 거리 모퉁이를 돌면 등에 불을 밝힐 작정이었다. 너무 많은 조심을 부려 경찰의 눈길을 끄는 것은 어리석은 짓일 테니까. 이따금 그는 몸서리쳤다. 저 담장 꼭대기에서 사랑하는 발랑틴이 내려오는 것을 지켜 주고, 아직 그 가녀린 손에만 입 맞춰 보았던 그녀를 처음으로 두 팔에 안을 그 순간을 떠올렸던 것이다.\n\n오후가 되어 그 시간이 다가오고 있음을 느끼자, 그는 혼자 있고 싶어졌다. 동요가 극에 달해, 친구의 사소한 질문 하나에도 짜증이 날 지경이었다. 그는 방에 틀어박혀 책을 읽으려 했지만, 눈길은 한 단어도 이해하지 못한 채 책장 위를 미끄러져 갈 뿐이었다. 그는 책을 내던지고, 두 번째로 자리에 앉아 사다리와 울타리가 그려진 자신의 계획을 다시 그려 보았다.\n\n마침내 그 시간이 가까워졌다. 깊이 사랑에 빠진 남자가 시계를 평화롭게 가도록 내버려 둔 적은 없는 법이다. 모렐은 어찌나 시계를 들볶았던지, 시계는 여섯 시 반에 여덟 번을 쳤다. 그러자 그가 말했다. \"이제 출발할 시간이야. 서명은 분명 아홉 시로 정해졌지만, 어쩌면 발랑틴이 그때까지 기다리지 않을지도 몰라.\" 그리하여 모렐은 자기 시계로 여덟 시 반에 멜레 거리를 나섰고, 생필리프뒤룰 성당의 종이 여덟 번을 칠 때 클로버 밭으로 들어섰다. 말과 이륜마차는 작은 폐허 뒤에 숨겨 두었는데, 모렐이 자주 기다리던 곳이었다.\n\n밤이 차츰 깊어 갔고, 정원의 나뭇잎들은 한층 짙은 빛을 띠었다. 그러자 모렐은 두근거리는 가슴으로 숨어 있던 곳에서 나와, 철문의 작은 틈으로 안을 들여다보았다. 아직 아무도 보이지 않았다.\n\n종이 여덟 시 반을 쳤고, 또 한 시간의 절반이 기다림 속에 지나갔다. 그동안 모렐은 이리저리 거닐며 점점 더 자주 틈새로 안을 들여다보았다. 정원은 더욱 어두워졌지만, 그 어둠 속에서 그가 찾는 흰 드레스는 보이지 않았고, 그 정적 속에서 그가 기다리는 발소리도 헛되이 귀를 기울일 뿐이었다. 나무들 사이로 어렴풋이 보이는 집은 여전히 어둠에 잠겨, 혼인 계약서 서명 같은 중대한 일이 진행되고 있다는 기미는 조금도 비치지 않았다. 모렐은 회중시계를 들여다보았다. 열 시 십오 분 전을 가리키고 있었다. 그러나 곧, 이미 두세 번 치는 소리를 들었던 그 시계가 아홉 시 반을 쳐서 그 오차를 바로잡았다.\n\n이미 발랑틴이 정한 시간보다 삼십 분이 지난 셈이었다. 청년에게는 끔찍한 순간이었다. 나뭇잎이 사부작거리는 가장 미세한 소리에도, 바람이 휘파람처럼 스치는 가장 작은 소리에도 그의 신경이 곤두섰고, 이마에는 땀이 배어 나왔다. 그러면 그는 떨면서 사다리를 세웠고, 한순간도 허비하지 않으려고 첫 번째 가로대에 발을 올렸다. 이 모든 희망과 두려움의 교차 속에서 종이 열 시를 쳤다. \"불가능해.\" 막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"계약서 서명이 뜻밖의 방해 없이 이렇게 오래 걸릴 리가 없어. 나는 온갖 경우의 수를 따져 봤고, 모든 절차에 드는 시간을 계산해 봤어. 무슨 일이 생긴 게 분명해.\"\n\n그러고는 빠르게 이리저리 거닐며, 불타는 이마를 울타리에 갖다 댔다. 발랑틴이 기절했을까? 아니면 들켜서 도망치다 붙잡혔을까? 청년에게 떠오를 수 있는 장애는 이 두 가지뿐이었다.\n\n도망치려다 힘이 빠져, 어느 길목에서 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌으리라는 생각이 그의 마음을 가장 짓눌렀다. \"그렇다면.\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 그녀를 잃게 되겠지. 그것도 내 잘못으로.\" 그는 잠시 이 생각에 매달렸고, 이내 그것이 현실처럼 여겨졌다. 멀리 땅바닥에 무언가가 있는 것처럼 보이기까지 했다. 그는 용기를 내어 불러 보았는데, 바람이 거의 알아들을 수 없는 한숨 소리를 실어 돌려보내는 듯했다.\n\n마침내 종이 삼십 분을 쳤다. 더는 기다릴 수 없었다. 관자놀이가 격렬하게 뛰었고, 눈앞이 흐려졌다. 그는 한쪽 다리를 담장 위로 넘기더니, 순식간에 반대편으로 뛰어내렸다. 그는 빌포르의 부지 안에 있었다. 담을 넘어 들어온 것이었다. 그 결과가 어떨까? 그러나 여기까지 와서 물러설 그가 아니었다. 그는 담장에 바짝 붙어 잠시 걸은 다음, 길을 가로질러 나무 덤불 속으로 들어갔다. 순식간에 그것을 통과하자, 집이 또렷이 보였다.\n\n그제야 모렐은 집에 불이 켜져 있지 않으리라는 자신의 짐작이 옳았음을 알았다. 예식이 있는 날 으레 그렇듯 창마다 불이 켜져 있는 대신, 그는 잿빛 덩어리만을 보았는데, 그마저도 그 순간 달의 희미한 빛을 가린 구름에 뒤덮여 있었다. 이따금 불빛 하나가 이 층의 세 창문 앞을 빠르게 스쳐 지나갔다. 그 세 창은 생메랑 부인의 방에 있는 것이었다. 또 다른 불빛 하나는 빌포르 부인의 침실에 드리운 붉은 커튼 뒤에서 움직임 없이 머물러 있었다. 모렐은 이 모든 것을 알아챘다. 하루의 매 순간마다 발랑틴을 생각 속에서 좇기 위해, 그는 수도 없이 그녀에게 집 전체를 묘사하게 했었다. 그래서 직접 본 적은 없어도 그는 그 모든 것을 알고 있었다.\n\n이 어둠과 정적은 발랑틴이 나타나지 않은 것보다 더욱 모렐을 불안하게 했다. 슬픔에 거의 미칠 지경이 되어, 발랑틴을 한 번만 더 보고, 자신이 두려워하는 불행을 확인하기 위해서라면 무슨 일이든 무릅쓰기로 결심한 모렐은 나무 덤불 가장자리에 이르렀고, 되도록 빨리 화원을 가로지르려던 참이었다. 그때 아직 멀었지만 바람을 타고 실려 온 목소리 하나가 그의 귀에 닿았다. 그 소리에, 이미 어느 정도 모습이 드러나 있던 그는 뒷걸음질 쳐 완전히 몸을 숨기고, 미동도 없이 가만히 있었다.\n\n그는 결심을 굳혔다. 발랑틴 혼자라면, 그녀가 지나갈 때 말을 걸 것이다. 누군가와 함께 있어 말을 걸 수 없더라도, 그래도 그녀를 보고, 그녀가 무사하다는 것을 알 수는 있을 것이다. 낯선 사람들이라면, 그들의 대화에 귀 기울여 여태 알 수 없던 이 수수께끼의 무언가를 알아낼 수 있을지도 몰랐다.\n\n마침 그때 달이 자신을 가리고 있던 구름 뒤에서 빠져나왔고, 모렐은 빌포르가 계단으로 나오는 것을 보았다. 그 뒤로 검은 옷을 입은 신사가 따라 나왔다. 그들은 계단을 내려와 나무 덤불 쪽으로 다가왔고, 모렐은 곧 그 다른 신사가 다브리니 의사임을 알아보았다.\n\n청년은 그들이 다가오는 것을 보고 기계적으로 뒷걸음질 쳤다. 그러다 덤불 한가운데 있는 플라타너스 나무에 막혀 멈춰 섰고, 그곳에 머무를 수밖에 없었다. 곧 두 신사도 걸음을 멈췄다.\n\n\"아, 의사 양반.\" 검사가 말했다. \"하늘이 우리 집안에 등을 돌렸소! 이 무슨 끔찍한 죽음이며, 이 무슨 타격이오! 나를 위로하려 들지 마시오. 아, 이토록 큰 슬픔은 무엇으로도 누그러뜨릴 수 없소. 상처가 너무 깊고 너무 생생하오! 돌아가시다니, 돌아가시다니!\"\n\n청년의 이마에 식은땀이 솟았고, 이가 딱딱 부딪쳤다. 빌포르 자신이 저주받았다고 부른 저 집에서 누가 죽었단 말인가?\n\n\"친애하는 빌포르 씨.\" 의사가 청년의 공포를 곱절로 키우는 어조로 대답했다. \"내가 당신을 이리로 데려온 것은 위로하기 위해서가 아니오. 오히려 그 반대요……\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이오?\" 검사가 놀라 물었다.\n\n\"방금 당신에게 닥친 불행 뒤에, 어쩌면 그보다 더 큰 또 다른 불행이 있다는 말이오.\"\n\n\"그럴 수가 있단 말이오?\" 빌포르가 두 손을 맞잡으며 중얼거렸다. \"내게 무슨 말을 하려는 거요?\"\n\n\"우리 정말 단둘뿐이오, 여보시오?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 단둘뿐이오. 하지만 어째서 이렇게까지 조심하는 거요?\"\n\n\"당신에게 끔찍한 비밀을 전해야 하기 때문이오.\" 의사가 말했다. \"앉읍시다.\"\n\n빌포르는 앉았다기보다 주저앉았다. 의사는 그 앞에 서서 한 손을 그의 어깨에 얹었다. 공포에 질린 모렐은 한 손으로 머리를 받치고, 다른 손으로는 가슴을 눌렀다. 심장 뛰는 소리가 들릴까 봐서였다. \"돌아가셨어, 돌아가셨어!\" 그는 속으로 되뇌었다. 자기 자신마저 죽어 가는 듯한 기분이었다.\n\n\"말하시오, 의사 양반. 듣고 있소.\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"내리치시오. 무엇이든 각오가 되어 있으니!\"\n\n\"생메랑 부인은 분명 연세가 많으셨소. 하지만 더없이 건강하셨소.\" 모렐은 다시 자유롭게 숨을 쉬기 시작했다. 지난 십 분 동안 하지 못했던 일이었다.\n\n\"슬픔이 그분을 갉아먹은 거요.\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"그렇소, 슬픔이오, 의사 양반! 후작과 사십 년을 함께 사신 분이니……\"\n\n\"슬픔이 아니오, 친애하는 빌포르 씨.\" 의사가 말했다. \"슬픔이 사람을 죽일 수도 있소. 드물긴 하지만 말이오. 그러나 하루 만에, 한 시간 만에, 십 분 만에 죽이는 법은 결코 없소.\" 빌포르는 아무 대답도 하지 못하고, 그저 떨구었던 고개를 들어 놀란 눈으로 의사를 바라보았다.\n\n\"마지막 사투의 순간에 곁에 계셨소?\" 다브리니 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"있었소.\" 검사가 대답했다. \"당신이 떠나지 말아 달라고 했잖소.\"\n\n\"생메랑 부인을 쓰러뜨린 병의 증상을 눈여겨보셨소?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 생메랑 부인은 몇 분 간격을 두고 세 차례 잇따라 발작을 일으키셨는데, 한 번 한 번이 그 전보다 더 심했소. 당신이 도착했을 때, 생메랑 부인은 이미 몇 분 동안 가쁜 숨을 몰아쉬고 계셨소. 그러다 발작을 일으키셨는데, 나는 그저 신경성 발작이려니 했소. 그런데 부인이 침대에서 몸을 일으키시고, 팔다리와 목이 뻣뻣해지는 것을 보고서야 정말로 겁이 났소. 그때 당신 얼굴빛을 보고, 내가 생각했던 것보다 두려워할 일이 더 있다는 걸 알았소. 이 고비가 지나고 나서 당신과 눈을 맞추려 했지만 그러지 못했소. 당신은 부인의 손을 잡고 맥을 짚고 계셨는데, 당신이 내 쪽으로 돌아서기도 전에 두 번째 발작이 닥쳤소. 이건 처음보다 더 끔찍했소. 똑같은 신경성 경련이 되풀이됐고, 입은 일그러지며 보랏빛으로 변했소.\"\n\n\"그리고 세 번째 발작에 숨을 거두셨지요.\"\n\n\"첫 번째 발작이 끝났을 때 나는 파상풍의 증상을 알아챘소. 당신도 내 견해에 동의했고.\"\n\n\"그렇소, 다른 사람들 앞에서는.\" 의사가 대답했다. \"하지만 지금은 우리 둘뿐이니……\"\n\n\"무슨 말을 하려는 거요? 오, 제발 봐주시오!\"\n\n\"파상풍의 증상과 식물성 물질에 의한 중독의 증상은 똑같다는 말이오.\"\n\n빌포르 씨가 자리에서 벌떡 일어섰다가, 이내 다시 주저앉았다. 말없이, 미동도 없이. 모렐은 자신이 꿈을 꾸는 건지 깨어 있는 건지 분간할 수 없었다.\n\n\"들으시오.\" 의사가 말했다. \"나는 방금 내가 한 말이 얼마나 중대한지, 또 그 말을 들은 사람이 어떤 사람인지 잘 알고 있소.\"\n\n\"내게 판사로서 말하는 거요, 친구로서 말하는 거요?\" 빌포르가 물었다.\n\n\"친구로서, 지금 이 순간은 오로지 친구로서 하는 말이오. 파상풍과 식물성 물질에 의한 중독의 증상은 너무도 비슷해서, 내가 방금 한 말을 선서하고 단언해야 한다면 망설여질 거요. 그러니 다시 말하건대, 나는 판사가 아니라 친구에게 말하는 거요. 그리고 그 친구에게 말하건대, '한 시간 사십오 분 동안 사투가 이어지는 동안, 나는 생메랑 부인의 경련과 죽음을 지켜보았고, 그분의 죽음이 독에서 비롯되었을 뿐 아니라 그 독이 무엇인지까지 명시할 수 있다고 확신하오.'\"\n\n\"그럴 수가 있단 말이오?\"\n\n\"증상이 뚜렷하오, 알겠소? 신경성 경련으로 끊기는 잠, 뇌의 흥분, 신경 중추의 마비. 생메랑 부인은 어쩌면 어떤 착오로 투여된 브루신이나 스트리크닌의 강한 약량에 쓰러지신 거요.\"\n\n빌포르가 의사의 손을 붙잡았다.\n\n\"오, 그럴 리 없소.\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 꿈을 꾸고 있는 게 분명하오! 당신 같은 사람한테서 이런 말을 듣다니 끔찍하오! 말해 주시오, 부탁이오, 친애하는 의사 양반, 당신이 잘못 보았을 수도 있다고 말이오.\"\n\n\"물론 그럴 수도 있소. 하지만……\"\n\n\"하지만?\"\n\n\"하지만 나는 그렇게 생각하지 않소.\"\n\n\"제발 나를 가엾이 여겨 주시오, 의사 양반! 요즘 너무도 끔찍한 일들이 내게 일어나서, 나는 미칠 지경이오.\"\n\n\"나 말고 생메랑 부인을 본 사람이 또 있소?\"\n\n\"없소.\"\n\n\"내가 살펴보지 않은 약을 약방에서 사 온 게 있소?\"\n\n\"없소.\"\n\n\"생메랑 부인에게 적이라도 있었소?\"\n\n\"내가 아는 한 없소.\"\n\n\"부인의 죽음으로 누군가의 이익이 걸리는 일은 없소?\"\n\n\"있을 리가 없소. 내 딸이 유일한 상속녀요. 발랑틴뿐이오. 오, 그런 생각이 떠오를 수 있다면, 단 한순간이라도 그런 생각을 품은 내 마음을 벌하기 위해 나 자신을 칼로 찌르겠소.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 친애하는 친구.\" 다브리니 씨가 말했다. \"나는 누구도 고발하려는 게 아니오. 나는 그저 사고에 대해, 알겠소? 착오에 대해 말하는 거요. 하지만 사고든 착오든, 사실은 거기 있소. 그것이 내 양심에 걸려, 당신에게 소리 높여 말하지 않을 수 없게 하는 거요. 알아보시오.\"\n\n\"누구를? 어떻게? 무엇을 말이오?\"\n\n\"늙은 하인 바루아가 착오를 일으켜, 주인 몫으로 준비한 약을 생메랑 부인에게 드린 게 아니겠소?\"\n\"내 아버지 몫이라는 거요?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"하지만 누아르티에 씨 몫으로 준비한 약이 어떻게 생메랑 부인을 중독시킬 수 있단 말이오?\"\n\n\"그보다 간단한 일도 없소. 어떤 병에서는 독이 약이 되는 법이오. 마비도 그중 하나요. 가령 누아르티에 씨에게 운동과 언어 능력을 되찾아 주려고 온갖 다른 약을 다 써 본 끝에, 나는 마지막 수단 하나를 시도하기로 했소. 그래서 석 달 동안 그분에게 브루신을 투여해 왔소. 그러니 내가 마지막으로 처방한 약량에는 6그레인이 들어 있었소. 점차 브루신에 익숙해진 누아르티에 씨의 마비된 신체에는 더없이 안전하게 투여할 수 있는 이 양이, 다른 사람을 죽이기에는 충분한 양이오.\"\n\n\"친애하는 의사 양반, 누아르티에 씨의 방과 생메랑 부인의 방 사이에는 오갈 통로가 없소. 그리고 바루아는 한 번도 내 장모님의 방에 들어간 적이 없소. 한마디로 의사 양반, 나는 당신이 세상에서 가장 양심적인 사람이라는 걸 알고, 당신을 더없이 신뢰하지만, 내 확신에도 불구하고 이 격언을 믿고 싶소. '에라레 후마눔 에스트'.\"\n\n\"내 동료 의사들 가운데 나만큼 당신이 신뢰하는 사람이 있소?\"\n\n\"어째서 그런 걸 묻는 거요? 무엇을 바라는 거요?\"\n\n\"그를 부르시오. 내가 본 것을 그에게 말하고, 함께 상의하여 시신을 검사하겠소.\"\n\n\"그러면 독의 흔적을 찾아내겠다는 거요?\"\n\n\"아니, 나는 독이라고 하지 않았소. 다만 신체가 어떤 상태였는지는 증명할 수 있소. 우리는 부인의 급작스러운 죽음의 원인을 밝혀낼 것이고, 그러면 이렇게 말하겠소. '친애하는 빌포르, 이 일이 부주의로 인한 것이라면 하인들을 단속하시오. 증오로 인한 것이라면 당신의 적들을 경계하시오.'\"\n\n\"내게 무엇을 제안하는 거요, 다브리니?\" 빌포르가 절망하여 말했다. \"다른 사람이 우리 비밀을 알게 되는 순간 검시가 불가피해지고, 내 집에서 검시라니, 그건 불가능하오! 그래도,\" 검사가 불안한 눈으로 의사를 바라보며 말을 이었다. \"당신이 원한다면, 당신이 요구한다면, 그럼 그렇게 하겠소. 하지만 의사 양반, 보다시피 나는 이미 이토록 비탄에 빠져 있소. 이만한 슬픔을 겪은 뒤에 어떻게 내 집에 그토록 큰 추문을 끌어들일 수 있겠소? 내 아내와 딸은 그 때문에 죽고 말 거요! 그리고 나는, 의사 양반, 당신도 알다시피 내가 지금 차지한 자리에 그저 닿을 수 있는 게 아니오. 이십오 년 동안 검사로 지내며 어지간한 수의 적을 쌓지 않을 수가 없는 법이오. 내 적들은 많소. 이 일이 입에 오르내리면, 그건 그들에게 승리가 되어 그들을 기뻐 날뛰게 하고 나를 수치로 뒤덮을 거요. 이런 세속적인 생각을 용서하시오, 의사 양반. 당신이 사제라면 감히 이런 말을 못 하겠지만, 당신은 인간이고, 인간을 잘 알지 않소. 의사 양반, 부디 당신 말을 거두어 주시오. 당신은 아무 말도 안 한 거요, 그렇지 않소?\"\n\n\"친애하는 빌포르 씨.\" 의사가 대답했다. \"내 첫 번째 의무는 인류를 향한 것이오. 과학으로 할 수 있는 일이었다면 나는 생메랑 부인을 살렸을 거요. 하지만 부인은 돌아가셨고, 내 의무는 산 자들을 향한 것이오. 이 끔찍한 비밀을 우리 마음속 가장 깊은 곳에 묻어 둡시다. 누군가 이 일을 의심한다면, 이 일에 대한 내 침묵이 내 무지의 탓으로 돌려진다 해도 나는 기꺼이 받아들이겠소. 그러는 동안, 검사, 늘 경계하시오. 면밀히 경계하시오. 어쩌면 이 악행이 여기서 멈추지 않을지도 모르니. 그리고 범인을 찾아낸다면, 찾아낸다면 말이오, 내가 당신에게 말하겠소. '당신은 판사요, 마음대로 하시오!'\"\n\n\"고맙소, 의사 양반.\" 빌포르가 형언할 수 없는 기쁨에 차 말했다. \"당신보다 나은 친구는 가져 본 적이 없소.\" 그리고 다브리니 의사가 자신의 약속을 거둘까 두렵기라도 한 듯, 그는 의사를 서둘러 집 쪽으로 이끌었다.\n\n그들이 떠나자, 모렐은 나무 아래에서 조심스레 나왔고, 달빛이 그의 얼굴을 비추었다. 어찌나 창백한지 유령의 얼굴로 보일 만했다.\n\n\"나는 분명 더없이 놀랍지만 더없이 끔찍한 방식으로 보호받고 있구나.\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 발랑틴, 가엾은 사람, 이토록 큰 슬픔을 어떻게 견뎌 낼까?\"\n\n이렇게 생각하면서 그는 붉은 커튼이 드리운 창과 흰 커튼이 드리운 세 창을 번갈아 바라보았다. 앞쪽 창에서는 불빛이 거의 사라진 뒤였다. 빌포르 부인이 막 등불을 끈 것이 분명했고, 머리맡 등불만이 그 흐릿한 빛을 창에 비추고 있었다. 그런데 반대로 건물 맨 끝에서, 그는 세 창 가운데 하나가 열리는 것을 보았다. 벽난로 선반에 놓인 밀초 한 자루가 창백한 빛 몇 줄기를 밖으로 던지고 있었고, 발코니 위로 한순간 그림자 하나가 보였다. 모렐이 몸서리쳤다. 흐느낌 소리가 들리는 것 같았던 것이다.\n\n평소 그토록 용감하던 그의 마음이, 지금은 인간의 가장 강렬한 두 가지 격정인 사랑과 두려움에 흔들려, 미신적인 생각에 빠질 만큼 약해진 것도 무리는 아니었다. 이렇게 몸을 숨긴 자신을 발랑틴이 볼 수 있을 리 없는데도, 그는 창가의 그 그림자가 자신을 부르는 소리를 들은 것 같았다. 그의 어지러운 마음이 그렇게 일러 주었던 것이다. 이 이중의 착각은 거역할 수 없는 현실이 되었고, 청년 특유의 알 수 없는 충동에 휩싸여, 그는 숨어 있던 곳에서 뛰쳐나왔다. 들킬 위험을, 발랑틴을 놀라게 할 위험을, 처녀의 입에서 새어 나올지 모를 어떤 외마디 소리로 발각될 위험을 무릅쓰고, 그는 단 두 걸음에 화원을 가로질렀다. 달빛 아래 화원은 커다란 흰 호수처럼 보였다. 그리고 집 앞에 늘어선 오렌지나무 줄을 지나, 그는 계단에 이르렀다. 그것을 재빨리 뛰어 올라 문을 밀자, 문은 아무런 저항 없이 열렸다.\n\n발랑틴은 그를 보지 못했다. 하늘로 향한 그녀의 두 눈은, 푸른 창공을 미끄러져 가는 은빛 구름 한 점을 지켜보고 있었다. 그 구름의 모양은 하늘로 오르는 그림자 같았다. 시적이고 격앙된 그녀의 마음은 그것을 할머니의 영혼이라 그려 보았다.\n\n그러는 동안 모렐은 대기실을 지나 계단을 찾아냈다. 계단에는 양탄자가 깔려 있어 다가오는 그의 발소리가 들리지 않았다. 그는 빌포르 씨가 나타나더라도 놀라지 않을 만큼 마음을 다잡고 있었다. 그런 마주침에 대해서도 단단히 각오가 되어 있었다. 그는 곧장 발랑틴의 아버지에게 다가가 모든 것을 털어놓고, 다정하게 서로를 사랑하는 두 마음을 맺어 준 그 사랑을 용서하고 인정해 달라고 빌포르에게 청할 작정이었다. 모렐은 제정신이 아니었다.\n\n다행히 그는 아무도 마주치지 않았다. 이제 특히, 발랑틴이 집 안 구조에 대해 해 준 묘사가 그에게 요긴했다. 그는 무사히 계단 꼭대기에 이르렀고, 손으로 더듬어 길을 찾고 있을 때, 흐느낌 소리가 그가 가야 할 방향을 일러 주었다. 그는 몸을 돌렸고, 반쯤 열린 문 하나가 그에게 갈 길을 보여 주었으며 슬픔에 잠긴 누군가의 목소리를 들려주었다. 그는 문을 밀고 들어갔다. 방 저편에는, 그것을 덮은 흰 천 아래 시신이 누워 있었다. 그토록 뜻밖에 엿들은 이야기를 떠올린 모렐에게는 더욱 섬뜩했다. 그 곁에는 발랑틴이 무릎을 꿇고, 안락의자의 쿠션에 머리를 파묻은 채, 두 손을 머리 위로 뻗어 맞잡고 뻣뻣하게 굳힌 채 떨며 흐느끼고 있었다. 그녀는 열린 채로 둔 창에서 등을 돌리고, 더없이 무정한 사람조차 마음을 움직였을 어조로 기도하고 있었다. 그녀의 말은 빠르고, 두서없고, 알아들을 수 없었다. 타는 듯한 슬픔의 무게가 그녀의 말을 거의 막아 버린 탓이었다.\n\n열린 덧창 사이로 비쳐 드는 달빛에 등불은 더욱 창백하게 타오르는 듯 보였고, 장면 전체에 무덤 같은 빛깔을 드리웠다. 모렐은 이를 견딜 수 없었다. 그는 신앙심이 깊은 사람도 아니었고, 쉽게 감동받는 사람도 아니었다. 그러나 자기 앞에서 고통받으며 울고, 두 손을 비틀어 짜는 발랑틴은, 그가 침묵 속에서 견디기에는 너무도 벅찼다. 그는 한숨을 쉬며 어떤 이름을 속삭였다. 그러자 눈물에 젖어 의자의 벨벳 쿠션에 눌려 있던 머리, 코레조가 그린 막달레나 같은 그 머리가 들리며 그를 향해 돌아왔다. 발랑틴은 조금도 놀라는 기색 없이 그를 알아보았다. 하나의 커다란 슬픔에 짓눌린 마음은 자잘한 감정에는 무뎌지는 법이다. 모렐이 그녀에게 손을 내밀었다. 발랑틴은 그를 맞으러 나가지 못한 데 대한 유일한 변명으로, 천 아래의 시신을 가리키고는 다시 흐느끼기 시작했다.\n\n한동안 둘 다 그 방에서 감히 입을 떼지 못했다. 죽음이 강요하는 듯한 침묵을 깨기를 망설였던 것이다. 마침내 발랑틴이 용기를 냈다.\n\n\"내 친구.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"어떻게 여기까지 오셨어요? 아아, 죽음이 당신에게 이 집으로 드는 길을 열어 준 게 아니었다면, 잘 오셨다고 말했을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"발랑틴.\" 모렐이 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"여덟 시 반부터 기다렸는데 당신이 오는 게 보이지 않았어. 불안해진 나는 담을 넘어 정원을 가로질러 왔지. 그러다 그 끔찍한 일에 대해 이야기하는 목소리들이……\"\n\n\"무슨 목소리요?\" 발랑틴이 물었다. 모렐은 의사와 빌포르 씨의 대화를 떠올리며 몸서리쳤다. 천 너머로 뻗은 두 손과 뻣뻣한 목, 보랏빛 입술이 보이는 듯했다.\n\n\"당신네 하인들이.\" 그가 말했다. \"그 슬픈 이야기를 전부 되풀이하고 있었어. 그들한테서 다 들었지.\"\n\n\"하지만 여기까지 올라온 건 우리 계획을 그르칠 위험을 무릅쓴 거예요, 내 사랑.\"\n\n\"용서해.\" 모렐이 대답했다. \"가 볼게.\"\n\n\"아니요.\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"누군가와 마주칠지도 몰라요. 여기 있어요.\"\n\n\"하지만 누가 이리로 온다면……\"\n\n처녀가 고개를 저었다. \"아무도 안 와요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"걱정 말아요, 저기 우리의 방패가 있으니까요.\" 침상을 가리키며 말했다.\n\n\"그런데 데피네 씨는 어떻게 됐어?\" 모렐이 물었다.\n\n\"프란츠 씨는 제 할머니가 막 돌아가시려는 바로 그때 계약서에 서명하러 도착했어요.\"\n\n\"아아.\" 모렐이 이기적인 기쁨을 느끼며 말했다. 이 죽음으로 결혼이 무기한 미뤄지리라 생각했던 것이다.\n\n\"하지만 제 슬픔을 곱절로 키우는 건,\" 처녀가, 마치 그 감정이 곧바로 벌을 받기라도 하듯 말을 이었다. \"그 가엾은 노부인이 임종의 자리에서, 결혼을 되도록 빨리 치르게 해 달라고 부탁하셨다는 거예요. 할머니도 저를 지켜 주려는 생각으로, 오히려 제게 거역하는 일을 하신 거죠.\"\n\n\"쉿!\" 모렐이 말했다. 둘은 귀를 기울였다. 복도와 계단에서 발소리가 또렷하게 들렸다.\n\n\"제 아버지예요. 방금 서재에서 나오신 거예요.\"\n\n\"의사를 문까지 배웅하려고.\" 모렐이 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그게 의사인 줄 어떻게 아세요?\" 발랑틴이 놀라 물었다.\n\n\"그럴 거라고 짐작했어.\" 모렐이 말했다.\n\n발랑틴이 청년을 바라보았다. 둘은 거리 쪽 문이 닫히는 소리를 들었고, 이어 빌포르 씨가 정원 문을 잠그고 다시 위층으로 올라왔다. 그는 대기실에서 잠시 멈춰 섰다. 자기 방으로 갈지 생메랑 부인의 방으로 갈지 망설이는 듯했다. 모렐은 문 뒤에 몸을 숨겼다. 발랑틴은 미동도 없었다. 슬픔이 그녀에게서 모든 두려움을 앗아 간 듯했다. 빌포르 씨는 자기 방으로 지나갔다.\n\n\"이제.\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"당신은 정문으로도, 정원으로도 나갈 수 없어요.\"\n\n모렐이 놀란 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"당신에게 남은 안전한 길은 하나뿐이에요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"제 할아버지 방을 지나는 거죠.\" 그녀가 일어섰다. \"가요.\" 그녀가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"어디로?\" 막시밀리앙이 물었다.\n\n\"제 할아버지 방으로요.\"\n\n\"내가 누아르티에 씨의 방에?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"진심이야, 발랑틴?\"\n\n\"오래전부터 바라던 일이에요. 그분은 제게 남은 유일한 친구이고, 우리 둘 다 그분의 도움이 필요해요. 가요.\"\n\n\"조심해, 발랑틴.\" 모렐이 처녀의 뜻을 따르기를 망설이며 말했다. \"이제야 내 잘못이 보여. 여기 들어온 건 미친 사람이나 할 짓이었어. 당신은 정말 나보다 분별이 있다고 확신할 수 있어?\"\n\n\"네.\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"그리고 제게 망설여지는 건 한 가지뿐이에요. 지켜 드리기로 한 사랑하는 할머니의 유해를 두고 떠난다는 것.\"\n\n\"발랑틴.\" 모렐이 말했다. \"죽음은 그 자체로 신성한 거야.\"\n\n\"네.\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"게다가 오래 걸리지도 않을 거예요.\"\n\n그러고 나서 그녀는 복도를 가로질러, 좁은 계단을 따라 누아르티에 씨의 방으로 앞장서 갔다. 모렐은 발끝으로 그녀를 따랐다. 문 앞에서 그들은 늙은 하인과 마주쳤다.\n\n\"바루아.\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"문을 닫고, 아무도 들이지 마세요.\"\n\n그녀가 먼저 들어갔다.\n\n의자에 앉아 온갖 소리에 귀 기울이고 있던 누아르티에는 문을 지켜보고 있었다. 그는 발랑틴을 보았고, 그의 눈이 환해졌다. 처녀가 다가오는 모습에는 무언가 무겁고 엄숙한 데가 있어 노인을 사로잡았고, 그의 빛나는 눈이 곧바로 묻기 시작했다.\n\n\"사랑하는 할아버지.\" 그녀가 다급히 말했다. \"할아버지도 아시겠지만 가엾은 할머니가 한 시간 전에 돌아가셨어요. 이제 이 세상에서 저한테는 할아버지 말고는 친구가 없어요.\"\n\n그의 표정 풍부한 눈이 더없이 큰 다정함을 드러냈다.\n\n\"그러니 오직 할아버지께만 제 슬픔과 제 희망을 털어놓을 수 있겠죠?\"\n\n마비된 노인이 \"그렇다\"라고 신호했다.\n\n발랑틴이 막시밀리앙의 손을 잡았다.\n\n\"그럼 이 신사분을 주의 깊게 봐 주세요.\"\n\n노인이 살피는 듯한 시선을, 약간 놀란 빛을 띠고 모렐에게 고정했다.\n\n\"이분은 막시밀리앙 모렐 씨예요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"할아버지도 분명 기억하실 마르세유의 그 선량한 상인의 아드님이에요.\"\n\n\"그렇구나.\" 노인이 말했다.\n\n\"흠잡을 데 없는 가문의 이름을 가졌고, 막시밀리앙은 그 이름을 더욱 빛낼 사람이에요. 서른 살에 벌써 대위이자 레지옹 도뇌르 훈장을 받은 장교거든요.\"\n\n노인은 그를 기억한다는 표시를 했다.\n\n\"할아버지.\" 발랑틴이 그 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 막시밀리앙을 가리키며 말했다. \"저는 이분을 사랑하고, 오직 이분의 사람이 될 거예요. 다른 사람과 결혼하라고 강요당한다면 저는 스스로 목숨을 끊을 거예요.\"\n\n마비된 노인의 눈이 격동하는 수많은 생각을 드러냈다.\n\n\"할아버지는 막시밀리앙 모렐 씨가 마음에 드시죠, 그렇죠?\" 발랑틴이 물었다.\n\n\"그렇다.\"\n\n\"그리고 할아버지의 자식인 저희를, 제 아버지의 뜻에 맞서 지켜 주실 거죠?\"\n\n누아르티에가 모렐에게 총명한 눈길을 던졌다. 마치 \"어쩌면 그럴 수도 있지\"라고 말하는 듯했다.\n\n막시밀리앙은 그 뜻을 알아들었다.\n\n\"마드무아젤.\" 그가 말했다. \"당신은 돌아가신 할머니의 방에서 다해야 할 신성한 의무가 있습니다. 제가 누아르티에 씨와 몇 분 동안 이야기를 나누는 영광을 허락해 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"바로 그거다.\" 노인의 눈이 말했다. 이어 그는 발랑틴을 근심스레 바라보았다.\n\n\"이분이 못 알아들을까 봐 걱정하세요?\"\n\n\"그렇다.\"\n\n\"오, 우리가 할아버지 이야기를 워낙 자주 했기 때문에, 이분은 제가 할아버지와 어떻게 대화하는지 정확히 알고 있어요.\" 그러고는 막시밀리앙을 향해, 슬픔에 가려지긴 했어도 사랑스러운 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"이분은 제가 아는 걸 다 알아요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n발랑틴이 일어나, 모렐에게 의자를 놓아 주고, 바루아에게 아무도 들이지 말라고 당부했다. 그러고는 할아버지를 다정하게 끌어안고, 모렐에게 슬프게 작별을 고한 뒤 자리를 떴다. 자신이 발랑틴의 신뢰를 받고 있으며 그들의 비밀을 다 알고 있음을 누아르티에에게 증명하기 위해, 모렐은 사전과 펜, 종이를 집어 들어 등불이 놓인 탁자 위에 모두 놓았다.\n\n\"하지만 먼저,\" 모렐이 말했다. \"제가 누구인지, 제가 발랑틴 양을 얼마나 사랑하는지, 그녀에 대한 제 계획이 무엇인지 말씀드리도록 허락해 주십시오.\"\n\n누아르티에가 듣겠다는 신호를 했다.\n\n겉보기에는 그저 쓸모없는 짐 같던 이 노인이, 둘 다 젊고 아름답고 강건한 연인들의 유일한 보호자이자 의지처, 조언자가 되는 광경은 자못 위엄이 있었다. 그의 유난히 고결하고 준엄한 표정이 모렐을 사로잡았고, 모렐은 떨면서 이야기를 시작했다. 그는 발랑틴을 어떻게 알게 되었는지, 어떻게 그녀를 사랑하게 되었는지, 그리고 고독과 불행 속에 있던 발랑틴이 자신의 헌신을 받아들였다는 것을 이야기했다. 그는 자신의 출생과 처지, 재산을 말했고, 마비된 노인의 표정을 살필 때마다 그 표정은 이렇게 답했다. \"좋다, 계속하거라.\"\n\n\"그리고 이제,\" 첫 부분의 이야기를 마친 모렐이 말했다. \"이제 제 사랑과 제 희망을 말씀드렸으니, 제 계획을 알려 드려도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그렇다.\" 노인이 신호했다.\n\n\"저희의 결심은 이러했습니다. 이륜마차 한 대가 철문 앞에서 기다리고, 그 마차로 발랑틴을 제 누이 집으로 데려가 그녀와 결혼한 다음, 빌포르 씨의 용서를 정중히 기다리려 했습니다.\"\n\"안 된다.\" 누아르티에가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇게 해서는 안 됩니까?\"\n\n\"안 된다.\"\n\n\"저희 계획을 인정하지 않으시는군요?\"\n\n\"안 된다.\"\n\"다른 방법이 있습니다.\" 모렐이 말했다. 노인의 묻는 듯한 눈이 말했다. \"어떤 방법이냐?\"\n\n\"제가 가겠습니다.\" 막시밀리앙이 말을 이었다. \"프란츠 데피네 씨를 찾아가겠습니다. 빌포르 양이 없는 자리에서 이 말씀을 드릴 수 있어 다행입니다. 그가 저에게 결투를 신청할 수밖에 없도록 처신하겠습니다.\" 누아르티에의 눈길이 계속 묻고 있었다.\n\n\"제가 무엇을 할지 알고 싶으십니까?\"\n\n\"그렇다.\"\n\n\"말씀드린 대로 그를 찾아가겠습니다. 저와 발랑틴 양을 묶어 주는 인연을 그에게 말하겠습니다. 그가 분별 있는 사람이라면, 제 발로 약혼녀의 손을 단념함으로써 그 분별을 증명하고, 죽을 때까지 제 우정과 사랑을 얻을 것입니다. 만약 거절한다면, 이해관계 때문이든 우스꽝스러운 자존심 때문이든, 그가 제 아내를 제게서 강제로 빼앗는 것이며 발랑틴이 저를 사랑하고 다른 누구도 원하지 않는다는 것을 증명한 뒤에, 저는 그와 결투할 것입니다. 모든 이점을 그에게 주고, 제가 그를 죽이거나 그가 저를 죽이게 될 겁니다. 제가 이긴다면 그는 발랑틴과 결혼하지 못할 것이고, 제가 죽는다면 발랑틴이 그와 결혼하지 않을 것을 저는 확신합니다.\"\n\n누아르티에는 이 고결하고 진실한 얼굴을 형언할 수 없는 기쁨으로 지켜보았다. 그 얼굴에는 그의 혀가 내뱉는 모든 감정이 그려져 있었고, 그 잘생긴 이목구비의 표정이, 건실하고 충실한 그림에 색채가 더하는 모든 것을 더해 주고 있었다.\n\n그래도 모렐이 말을 마치자, 노인은 여러 번 눈을 감았다. 그것이 그가 \"안 된다\"라고 말하는 방식이었다.\n\n\"안 된다고요?\" 모렐이 말했다. \"이 두 번째 계획도 첫 번째 계획처럼 못마땅하십니까?\"\n\n\"그렇다.\" 노인이 신호했다.\n\n\"그럼 대체 어떻게 해야 합니까?\" 모렐이 물었다. \"생메랑 부인의 마지막 부탁은 결혼을 미루지 말라는 것이었습니다. 일이 그대로 흘러가게 두어야 합니까?\" 누아르티에는 미동도 하지 않았다. \"알겠습니다.\" 모렐이 말했다. \"저더러 기다리라는 말씀이군요.\"\n\n\"그렇다.\"\n\n\"하지만 지체하면 저희 계획이 무너질 수 있습니다.\" 청년이 대답했다. \"혼자서는 발랑틴에게 아무 힘이 없습니다. 그녀는 따를 수밖에 없게 될 겁니다. 제가 여기 온 것도 거의 기적이고, 이만한 기회가 다시 올 거라고는 기대하기 어렵습니다. 믿어 주십시오, 제가 말씀드린 두 가지 계획밖에 없습니다. 제 주제넘음을 용서하시고, 어느 쪽을 택하시는지 말씀해 주십시오. 발랑틴 양이 제 명예에 자신을 맡기는 것을 허락하시겠습니까?\"\n\"안 된다.\"\n\n\"제가 데피네 씨를 찾아가는 편이 낫겠습니까?\"\n\n\"안 된다.\"\n\n\"그럼 저희에게 필요한 도움은 어디서 옵니까? 우연에서요?\" 모렐이 다시 물었다.\n\"안 된다.\"\n\n\"할아버지께로부터요?\"\n\n\"그렇다.\"\n\n\"제 말을 정확히 이해하시는 거지요? 제 조급함을 용서해 주십시오, 제 목숨이 할아버지의 대답에 달려 있으니까요. 저희의 도움이 할아버지께로부터 오는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"그렇다.\"\n\"확신하십니까?\"\n\n\"그렇다.\" 이 대답을 보낸 눈빛에는 너무도 굳건함이 서려 있어, 적어도 그 의지만큼은, 설령 능력은 의심할지언정 누구도 의심할 수 없었다.\n\n\"오, 천 번이라도 감사드립니다! 하지만 어떻게요? 기적이 일어나 할아버지의 말과 몸짓과 움직임을 되돌려 주지 않는 한, 그 안락의자에 묶여 말 못 하고 꼼짝 못 하는 할아버지께서 어떻게 이 결혼을 막으실 수 있단 말입니까?\" 미소 하나가 노인의 얼굴을 밝혔다. 마비된 얼굴에 떠오른, 눈으로 짓는 기이한 미소였다.\n\n\"그럼 저는 기다려야 합니까?\" 청년이 물었다.\n\n\"그렇다.\"\n\n\"하지만 계약서는요?\" 똑같은 미소가 되돌아왔다. \"그것이 서명되지 않으리라고 장담하실 수 있습니까?\"\n\n\"그렇다.\" 누아르티에가 말했다.\n\n\"계약서는 서명되지 않을 거다!\" 모렐이 외쳤다. \"오, 용서하십시오, 할아버지. 이토록 큰 행복을 도무지 실감할 수가 없습니다. 정말 그들이 서명하지 않을까요?\"\n\n\"안 한다.\" 마비된 노인이 말했다. 그 장담에도 불구하고 모렐은 여전히 망설였다. 무력한 노인의 이 약속이 너무도 기이해서, 그것이 그의 의지의 힘에서 나온 것이 아니라 쇠약해진 신체 기관에서 비롯된 것일지도 모른다는 생각이 들었다. 자신의 어리석음을 모르는 광인이 제 힘에 부치는 일을 시도하는 것이 자연스러운 일이 아니던가? 약한 자는 자기가 들 수 있는 짐을 떠벌리고, 겁쟁이는 자기가 맞설 수 있는 거인을 떠벌리며, 가난뱅이는 자기가 쓸 보물을 떠벌리고, 더없이 비천한 농부도 오만함이 극에 달하면 자신을 유피테르라 부른다. 누아르티에가 청년의 망설임을 알아챘는지, 아니면 그의 순순함을 온전히 믿지 못했는지, 그는 불안한 눈으로 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"무엇을 바라십니까, 할아버지?\" 모렐이 물었다. \"제가 잠자코 있겠다는 약속을 다시 하기를 바라십니까?\" 누아르티에의 눈은 고정된 채 굳건히 머물러 있었다. 약속만으로는 충분치 않다는 듯이. 이윽고 그 눈길이 그의 얼굴에서 두 손으로 옮겨 갔다.\n\n\"맹세할까요, 할아버지?\" 막시밀리앙이 물었다.\n\n\"그렇다.\" 마비된 노인이 똑같은 엄숙함으로 말했다. 모렐은 노인이 맹세에 큰 의미를 둔다는 것을 알아챘다. 그는 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"제 명예를 걸고 맹세합니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"데피네 씨에 대해 제가 취할 행동에 관해 할아버지의 결정을 기다리겠다고.\"\n\n\"옳다.\" 노인이 말했다.\n\n\"이제.\" 모렐이 말했다. \"제가 물러가기를 바라십니까?\"\n\n\"그렇다.\"\n\"발랑틴 양을 보지 않고요?\"\n\n\"그렇다.\"\n\n모렐은 따르겠다는 신호를 했다. \"하지만,\" 그가 말했다. \"먼저 방금 따님이 그러셨듯이 할아버지를 안아 드리도록 허락해 주십시오.\" 누아르티에의 표정은 알 수 없었다. 청년은 발랑틴이 입맞춤했던 바로 그 자리, 노인의 이마에 입술을 눌렀다. 그러고 나서 다시 한번 고개를 숙이고 물러갔다.\n\n문밖에서 그는 발랑틴이 지시를 일러 둔 늙은 하인을 만났다. 모렐은 어두운 통로를 따라 안내받았고, 그 통로는 정원으로 통하는 작은 문으로 이어졌다. 그는 곧 자신이 들어왔던 자리를 찾아냈고, 관목들의 도움을 받아 담장 꼭대기에 올랐으며, 사다리를 타고 순식간에 이륜마차가 여전히 그를 기다리고 있는 클로버 밭에 내려섰다. 그는 마차에 올랐다. 그토록 많은 감정에 완전히 지친 그는 자정 무렵 멜레 거리에 도착했고, 침대에 몸을 던져 깊이 잠들었다.\n\n넷째 권\n\n# Chapter 74. The Villefort Family Vault\n\n74장. 빌포르 가문의 납골당\n이틀 뒤, 오전 열 시 무렵 빌포르 씨 저택의 문 앞에는 상당한 인파가 모여들었고, 영구 마차와 자가용 마차가 길게 줄지어 포부르생토노레 거리와 페피니에르 거리를 따라 늘어섰다. 그 가운데 멀리서 온 듯한, 몹시 기이한 모양의 마차가 하나 있었다. 검게 칠한 일종의 포장마차였는데, 가장 먼저 도착한 축에 들었다. 사람들이 까닭을 묻자, 기이한 우연으로 그 마차에 생메랑 후작의 유해가 실려 있다는 사실이 밝혀졌다. 한 번의 장례를 따르려고 온 이들이 두 번의 장례를 따르게 된 셈이었다. 그 수는 많았다. 루이 18세와 샤를 10세를 가장 열성적이고 충실하게 섬긴 고관 중 한 사람이었던 생메랑 후작은 많은 벗을 남겼고, 거기에 사교계의 관례상 빌포르와 인연이 있는 인사들까지 더해지니 상당한 무리를 이루었다.\n\n당국에 정식으로 통보가 들어갔고, 두 장례를 동시에 치러도 좋다는 허가가 떨어졌다. 똑같은 장례 의장으로 꾸민 두 번째 영구차가 빌포르 씨의 문 앞으로 옮겨졌고, 관이 포장마차에서 그리로 옮겨졌다. 두 유해는 페르라셰즈 묘지에 안장될 예정이었다. 빌포르 씨가 오래전에 가족을 위해 마련해 둔 무덤이 그곳에 있었다. 가엾은 르네의 유해는 이미 그 안에 안치되어 있었고, 이제 십 년간의 이별 끝에 그녀의 아버지와 어머니가 그녀와 다시 만나게 되는 것이었다.\n\n언제나 호기심 많고, 언제나 장례 행렬에 마음이 흔들리는 파리 시민들은, 그 호화로운 행렬이 옛 귀족 가운데 두 사람을 마지막 거처로 배웅하는 광경을 경건한 침묵 속에 지켜보았다. 상업의 가장 든든한 보호자이자 자신들의 신념에 충실했던 사람들이었다.\n\n영구 마차 중 한 대 안에서는 보샹과 드브레, 샤토르노가 후작 부인의 너무도 갑작스러운 죽음에 관해 이야기를 나누고 있었다.\n\n\"작년에 알제에서 돌아오는 길에 마르세유에서 생메랑 부인을 뵈었는데,\" 샤토르노가 말했다. \"겉으로 보기에 건강도 좋으시고 정신도 몸도 정정하셔서, 백 살까지 사실 분 같았소. 연세가 어찌 되셨던가요?\"\n\n\"프란츠가 그러는데,\" 알베르가 대답했다. \"예순여섯이셨답니다. 하지만 노환으로 돌아가신 게 아니라 슬픔으로 돌아가셨소. 후작님이 돌아가신 뒤로 그 충격이 워낙 컸던지, 부인은 끝내 온전히 정신을 추스르지 못하셨다고 하더군요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 무슨 병으로 돌아가셨소?\" 드브레가 물었다.\n\n\"뇌충혈, 아니면 뇌졸중이라던데, 그게 그거 아니오?\"\n\n\"비슷하지요.\"\n\n\"뇌졸중이라니 믿기 어렵소.\" 보샹이 말했다. \"내가 한 번 뵌 적 있는 생메랑 부인은 키가 작고 몸매가 가냘프며, 다혈질이라기보다는 훨씬 신경질적인 체질이셨소. 그런 체질에 슬픔이 뇌졸중을 일으키기는 어려울 텐데.\"\n\n\"어쨌든,\" 알베르가 말했다. \"어떤 병이, 또는 어떤 의사가 부인을 죽음에 이르게 했든 간에, 빌포르 씨가, 아니 정확히는 발랑틴 양이, 더 정확히는 우리 친구 프란츠가 막대한 재산을 물려받게 됐소. 내가 알기로 연 8만 리브르에 달한다더군요.\"\n\n\"게다가 그 늙은 자코뱅, 누아르티에가 죽으면 그 재산이 두 배로 불어날 거요.\"\n\n\"끈질긴 노인장이지요.\" 보샹이 말했다. \"테나켐 프로포시티 비룸, '제 뜻을 굳게 지키는 사람'이라더니. 내 생각에 그 노인은 모든 상속인보다 오래 살겠다고 죽음과 약조라도 맺은 것 같고, 또 그 약조를 지킬 모양이오. 1814년에 나폴레옹에게 이렇게 말한 93년의 그 늙은 국민공회 의원을 닮았소. '폐하의 제국은 빠른 성장으로 약해진 어린 줄기이기에 휘는 것입니다. 공화국을 스승으로 삼으십시오. 새로운 힘으로 다시 전장으로 나아갑시다. 그러면 약속드리건대 오십만 군사와 또 한 번의 마렝고, 두 번째 아우스터리츠를 안겨 드리겠습니다. 사상은 결코 소멸하지 않습니다, 폐하. 때로 잠들기는 하나, 완전히 잠들기 전에 더욱 강하게 되살아날 뿐입니다.'\"\n\n\"그 노인에게는 사상이나 사람이나 매한가지였던 모양이오.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"한 가지만 궁금하군요. 프란츠 데피네가, 아내와 떼어 놓을 수 없는 그런 할아버지를 어떻게 받아들일지 말이오. 그런데 프란츠는 어디 있소?\"\n\n\"맨 앞 마차에 빌포르 씨와 함께 타고 있소. 빌포르 씨는 벌써 그를 한 식구로 여기고 있더군요.\"\n\n거의 모든 마차 안에서 이런 대화가 오갔다. 이처럼 잇따라 빠르게 일어난 두 죽음에 모두가 놀랐지만, 다브리니 씨가 빌포르 씨에게 한밤의 산책 길에 털어놓았던 그 끔찍한 비밀을 짐작하는 이는 아무도 없었다. 한 시간쯤 지나 일행은 묘지에 닿았다. 날씨는 온화하나 흐려서 장례식과 잘 어울렸다. 가족 납골당으로 몰려가는 무리 가운데, 샤토르노는 모렐을 알아보았다. 모렐은 이륜마차를 타고 홀로 와서, 주목으로 가장자리를 두른 길을 따라 말없이 걷고 있었다.\n\n\"자네가 여기 웬일인가?\" 샤토르노가 젊은 대위의 팔에 자기 팔을 끼며 말했다. \"빌포르의 친구인가? 어째서 그 집에서는 한 번도 자네를 만나지 못했지?\"\n\n\"나는 빌포르 씨와는 아는 사이가 아닐세.\" 모렐이 대답했다. \"생메랑 부인과 아는 사이였지.\" 그때 알베르가 프란츠와 함께 그들에게 다가왔다.\n\n\"소개를 나누기에는 때도 장소도 마땅찮네만,\" 알베르가 말했다. \"우리야 미신을 따지는 사람들도 아니니. 모렐 씨, 프란츠 데피네 씨를 소개하지요. 함께 이탈리아를 두루 돌아본, 더없이 유쾌한 길동무였습니다. 그리고 프란츠, 이쪽은 막시밀리앙 모렐 씨일세. 자네가 없는 동안 사귄 더없이 훌륭한 벗으로, 내가 우정이니 재치니 다정함이니 하는 말을 꺼낼 때마다 그 이름을 입에 올리는 걸 자네도 듣게 될 걸세.\"\n\n모렐은 잠시 머뭇거렸다. 그는 자신이 은밀히 적으로 맞서고 있는 사내에게 다정하게 인사를 건네는 것이 위선처럼 느껴질까 두려웠다. 그러나 자신의 맹세와 사태의 엄중함이 다시 떠올랐다. 그는 애써 감정을 감추며 프란츠에게 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"빌포르 양은 몹시 슬퍼하고 있지요?\" 드브레가 프란츠에게 물었다.\n\n\"이루 말할 수 없을 만큼요.\" 그가 대답했다. \"오늘 아침에는 어찌나 창백하던지, 거의 알아보지 못할 뻔했습니다.\"\n\n겉으로는 단순해 보이는 이 말이 모렐의 가슴을 후벼 팠다. 이 사내가 발랑틴을 보았고, 또 그녀와 이야기를 나눴다니! 젊고 혈기 넘치는 그 장교는 맹세를 깨뜨리지 않으려고 온 힘을 다해 마음을 다잡아야 했다. 그는 샤토르노의 팔을 잡고, 인부들이 이미 두 관을 놓아둔 납골당 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"참으로 으리으리한 거처로군.\" 보샹이 영묘 쪽을 바라보며 말했다. \"여름 궁전이자 겨울 궁전이라. 데피네, 자네도 머지않아 이 가문의 일원으로 꼽힐 테니, 차례가 되면 들어가게 되겠지. 나로 말하자면, 철학자로서 차라리 시골의 작은 집, 저 나무들 아래 오두막 한 채가 좋겠소. 이 가엾은 몸뚱이 위에 그리 많은 다듬돌을 얹지 않은 곳 말이오. 죽을 때면 나는 곁의 사람들에게 볼테르가 피롱에게 써 보낸 말을 하겠소. '에오 루스', 그러면 만사가 끝나는 거지. 하지만 자, 프란츠, 기운을 내게. 자네 아내가 상속녀가 아닌가.\"\n\n\"이보게 보샹, 정말 못 말리겠군. 정치가 자네를 매사에 웃게 만들었고, 정치하는 자들이 자네를 매사에 못 믿게 만들었어. 하지만 평범한 사람들과 어울리는 영광을 누리고, 잠시나마 정치를 내려놓는 즐거움을 맛볼 때에는, 자네가 의회에 갈 때 지팡이와 함께 놓고 가는 그 다정한 마음을 좀 되찾아 보게.\"\n\n\"그렇지만 말해 보게.\" 보샹이 말했다. \"인생이란 무엇인가? 죽음의 대기실에서 잠시 멈춰 서는 것이 아닌가?\"\n\n\"나는 보샹한테는 손사래를 치겠네.\" 알베르가 프란츠를 끌고 가며 말했다. 그러고는 보샹이 드브레와 더불어 철학적 논설을 마저 풀어 가도록 내버려 두었다.\n\n빌포르 가문의 납골당은 높이 스무 자가량의 흰 돌로 쌓은 정방형 건물이었다. 안쪽 칸막이가 두 가문을 갈라놓았고, 각 칸에는 저마다 출입문이 나 있었다. 이곳에는 다른 무덤들처럼, 알뜰함이 죽은 이들을 차곡차곡 쌓아 놓고 박물관의 표본처럼 이름표를 붙여 둔 옹색한 서랍 따위는 없었다. 청동 문 안으로 보이는 것이라곤, 납골당 본채와 벽으로 가로막힌 음침한 방 하나뿐이었다. 앞서 말한 두 문이 그 벽 한가운데 나 있어, 빌포르 가문과 생메랑 가문의 관을 각기 품고 있었다. 그곳에서라면, 소풍을 나왔다가 페르라셰즈를 구경하러 들른 하찮은 한량들이나 이곳을 밀회 장소로 삼는 연인들에게 방해받지 않고 마음껏 슬픔을 쏟아 낼 수 있었다.\n\n두 관은 미리 마련해 둔 받침대 위에 놓였다. 생메랑 가문에 속한 오른쪽 지하 묘실이었다. 빌포르와 프란츠, 그리고 몇몇 가까운 친척만이 그 성소 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n종교 의례는 모두 문 앞에서 치러졌고 따로 추도사도 없었으므로, 일행은 모두 흩어졌다. 샤토르노와 알베르, 모렐은 한쪽으로, 드브레와 보샹은 다른 쪽으로 갔다. 프란츠는 빌포르 씨와 함께 남았다. 모렐은 묘지 정문에서 핑계를 대고 기다렸다. 그는 프란츠와 빌포르 씨가 같은 영구 마차에 오르는 것을 보았고, 그 만남이 흉조라고 생각했다. 그런 뒤 파리로 돌아왔는데, 샤토르노와 알베르와 같은 마차에 탔으면서도 그들의 대화는 한마디도 귀에 들어오지 않았다.\n\n프란츠가 빌포르 씨에게 작별을 고하려 할 때, 빌포르가 말했다. \"언제 다시 뵐 수 있을까요?\"\n\n\"원하시는 시간에 언제든지요.\" 프란츠가 대답했다.\n\n\"되도록 빨리.\"\n\n\"분부대로 하겠습니다. 함께 돌아갈까요?\"\n\n\"불편하지 않으시다면.\"\n\n\"불편하기는요, 오히려 기쁩니다.\"\n\n이리하여 장차 장인과 사위가 될 두 사람은 같은 마차에 올랐고, 그들이 지나가는 것을 본 모렐은 마음이 불안해졌다. 빌포르와 프란츠는 포부르생토노레로 돌아갔다. 검사는 아내도 딸도 보러 가지 않고 곧장 자기 서재로 가, 젊은이에게 의자를 권하며 말했다.\n\n\"데피네 씨,\" 그가 말했다. \"이 순간, 얼핏 보기에는 부적절해 보일지 모르나 실은 그리 잘못 고른 때가 아닐 듯싶은 이 순간에, 한 가지를 일깨워 드릴까 합니다. 고인의 뜻을 따르는 것이야말로 그 무덤에 바칠 첫 번째 공물이니까요. 그러니 생메랑 부인이 임종 자리에서 표하신 소망, 발랑틴의 혼례를 미루지 말라던 그 소망을 일깨워 드리고자 합니다. 아시다시피 고인의 재산은 완벽하게 정리되어 있고, 유언에 따라 생메랑 가문의 재산 전부가 발랑틴에게 상속됩니다. 어제 공증인이 그 서류들을 제게 보여 주었으니, 곧바로 계약서를 작성할 수 있습니다. 포부르생토노레의 보보 광장에 있는 공증인 데샹 씨를 찾아가시면 됩니다. 그 증서들을 살펴보셔도 좋다는 제 권한을 드리지요.\"\n\n\"각하,\" 데피네 씨가 대답했다. \"지금은 깊은 슬픔에 잠긴 발랑틴 양이 남편을 떠올릴 만한 때가 아닐지도 모릅니다. 사실 저는 염려스럽습니다……\"\n\n\"발랑틴에게는 할머니의 마지막 당부를 받드는 것보다 더 큰 기쁨이 없을 겁니다. 그쪽으로는 아무 걸림돌도 없을 테니, 안심하셔도 좋습니다.\"\n\n\"그러시다면,\" 프란츠가 대답했다. \"저로서도 걸림돌이 될 일은 없으니, 언제든 좋으실 때 절차를 진행하십시오. 저는 이미 약속을 드렸고, 그 약속을 지키는 일이 기쁘고도 행복합니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"더 필요한 것은 없군요. 계약서는 사흘 전에 서명되었어야 할 것이었으니, 모든 게 준비되어 있을 겁니다. 오늘 서명할 수 있습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 상중인데요?\" 프란츠가 망설이며 말했다.\n\n\"그 점은 염려 마십시오.\" 빌포르가 대답했다. \"내 집에서는 어떤 격식도 소홀히 하지 않을 테니까요. 빌포르 양은 정해진 석 달 동안 자기 영지인 생메랑으로 물러나 있을 수 있습니다. '자기 영지'라고 한 것은, 오늘부터 그 애가 그것을 상속받기 때문이지요. 그곳에서 며칠 지난 뒤, 원하신다면 화려한 의식도 격식도 없이 민사 혼인을 올리면 됩니다. 생메랑 부인도 딸이 그곳에서 혼례를 치르기를 바라셨으니까요. 그 일이 끝나면, 데피네 씨는 파리로 돌아오시고, 부인은 시어머니와 함께 상중의 기간을 보내면 됩니다.\"\n\n\"좋으실 대로 하십시오, 각하.\" 프란츠가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼,\" 빌포르 씨가 대답했다. \"삼십 분만 기다려 주시겠습니까. 발랑틴이 응접실로 내려올 겁니다. 데샹 씨를 부르겠습니다. 헤어지기 전에 계약서를 읽고 서명하지요. 그리고 오늘 저녁에 빌포르 부인이 발랑틴을 영지까지 데려다줄 것이고, 우리는 일주일 뒤에 그곳에서 합류하면 됩니다.\"\n\n\"각하,\" 프란츠가 말했다. \"한 가지 청이 있습니다.\"\n\n\"무엇입니까?\"\n\n\"이 서명 자리에 알베르 드 모르세르와 라울 드 샤토르노가 함께했으면 합니다. 아시다시피 제 증인들이니까요.\"\n\n\"삼십 분이면 그들에게 알리기에 충분하겠지요. 직접 다녀오시겠습니까, 아니면 사람을 보내시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"제가 직접 가는 편이 좋겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 삼십 분 뒤에 기다리겠습니다, 남작. 그때 발랑틴도 준비되어 있을 겁니다.\"\n\n프란츠는 고개를 숙이고 방을 나섰다. 문이 채 닫히기도 전에, 빌포르 씨는 발랑틴에게 삼십 분 뒤 응접실로 내려와 채비하고 있으라고 일렀다. 공증인과 데피네 씨, 그리고 그의 증인들을 기다리고 있다는 것이었다. 이 소식이 온 집안을 발칵 뒤집어 놓았다. 빌포르 부인은 그 말을 믿으려 들지 않았고, 발랑틴은 벼락이라도 맞은 듯했다. 그녀는 도움을 청하려 주위를 둘러보았고, 할아버지 방으로 내려가려 했으나, 계단에서 빌포르 씨와 마주쳤다. 그는 그녀의 팔을 잡아 응접실로 데려갔다. 대기실에서 발랑틴은 바루아와 마주쳤고, 절망에 찬 눈으로 그 늙은 하인을 바라보았다. 잠시 뒤, 빌포르 부인이 어린 에두아르를 데리고 응접실로 들어왔다. 그녀도 가족의 슬픔을 함께 나눈 것이 분명했으니, 안색이 창백하고 지쳐 보였다. 그녀는 자리에 앉아 에두아르를 무릎에 앉혔고, 이따금 자신의 애정이 온통 쏠려 있는 듯한 이 아이를, 거의 발작하듯 가슴에 끌어안았다.\n\n이윽고 마차 두 대가 안마당으로 들어서는 소리가 들렸다. 한 대는 공증인의 것이었고, 다른 한 대는 프란츠와 그 친구들의 것이었다. 순식간에 일행이 모두 모였다. 발랑틴은 어찌나 창백한지, 관자놀이에서 두 눈 둘레와 뺨을 타고 내려가는 푸른 핏줄까지 들여다보일 지경이었다. 프란츠는 깊이 마음이 흔들렸다. 샤토르노와 알베르는 놀란 눈빛으로 서로를 마주보았다. 방금 끝난 의식보다 이제 막 시작되려는 이 일이 더 침울해 보였던 것이다. 빌포르 부인은 벨벳 휘장 뒤 그늘 속에 자리를 잡았고, 줄곧 아이 위로 몸을 굽히고 있어 그 얼굴 표정을 읽기란 어려웠다. 빌포르 씨는 여느 때처럼 미동도 없었다.\n\n공증인은 관례대로 탁자 위에 서류를 정리하고, 안락의자에 자리를 잡고, 안경을 추켜올린 뒤 프란츠를 향해 돌아앉았다.\n\n\"당신이 프란츠 드 케넬, 데피네 남작이십니까?\" 그가 물었다. 뻔히 알고 있으면서도 던진 물음이었다.\n\n\"그렇습니다.\" 프란츠가 대답했다. 공증인이 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"그렇다면 알려 드릴 것이 있습니다. 빌포르 씨의 요청에 따라 말씀드립니다만, 당신이 빌포르 양과 혼인하기로 한 일이 누아르티에 씨가 손녀에게 품은 마음을 바꾸어 놓았고, 그리하여 그분이 본래 손녀에게 물려주려던 재산에서 그녀를 완전히 폐적하셨습니다. 다만 서둘러 덧붙이자면,\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"유언자는 재산의 일부만 처분할 권리가 있는데도 전부를 처분하셨으므로, 이 유언은 효력을 인정받지 못하고 무효로 선언됩니다.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"하지만 데피네 씨에게 미리 일러두건대, 내가 살아 있는 동안 내 아버지의 유언이 의심받는 일은 결코 없을 겁니다. 내 지위가 어떠한 의혹도 허용하지 않으니까요.\"\n\n\"각하,\" 프란츠가 말했다. \"발랑틴 양이 계신 자리에서 이런 문제가 거론되어 무척 유감스럽습니다. 저는 그녀의 재산이 얼마인지 한 번도 물은 적이 없습니다. 아무리 적다 한들 제 것보다는 많겠지요. 제 집안은 빌포르 씨와 맺는 이 혼사에서 명망을 구했지만, 제가 구하는 것은 오직 행복뿐입니다.\"\n\n발랑틴은 눈에 띄지 않게 그에게 고마움을 표했고, 그러는 동안 소리 없는 눈물 두 줄기가 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸다.\n\n\"게다가,\" 빌포르가 장차 사위가 될 사람을 향해 말했다. \"기대했던 몫의 일부를 잃은 것을 빼면, 이 뜻밖의 유언이 자네 개인을 욕보일 일은 없네. 누아르티에 씨의 정신이 쇠약해진 탓이라고 보면 충분히 설명되니까. 그분이 노여워하는 건 발랑틴 양이 자네와 결혼하기 때문이 아니라, 결혼을 한다는 사실 그 자체 때문일세. 다른 누구와 맺어진다 해도 그분께 똑같은 슬픔을 안겼을 거야. 늙으면 이기적이 되는 법이지. 발랑틴 양은 누아르티에 씨에게 충실한 길동무였는데, 데피네 남작 부인이 되고 나면 더는 그럴 수 없을 테니까. 내 아버지는 우울한 상태라 무슨 이야기든 나눌 수가 없네. 정신이 쇠약해져 알아듣지 못하시거든. 또 나는 확신하건대, 지금 그분은 손녀가 곧 결혼한다는 것은 아시지만, 손주사위가 될 사람의 이름조차 잊으셨을 거야.\" 빌포르 씨가 이 말을 채 끝내기도 전에 문이 열리고 바루아가 나타났다.\n\n\"여러분,\" 그가 말했다. 이런 엄숙한 자리에서 주인들에게 말을 건네는 하인치고는 묘하게 단호한 어조였다. \"여러분, 누아르티에 드 빌포르 어르신께서 프란츠 드 케넬, 데피네 남작님과 지금 당장 이야기를 나누고 싶어 하십니다.\" 그 역시 공증인과 마찬가지로, 인물을 혼동하지 않도록 신랑 후보의 모든 직함을 빠짐없이 붙였다.\n\n빌포르가 흠칫했고, 빌포르 부인은 무릎에 앉혔던 아들을 미끄러지듯 내려놓았으며, 발랑틴은 조각상처럼 창백하고 말없이 일어섰다. 알베르와 샤토르노는 처음보다 더 놀란 눈빛으로 두 번째 시선을 주고받았다. 공증인은 빌포르를 바라보았다.\n\n\"그건 불가능하오.\" 검사가 말했다. \"데피네 씨는 지금 응접실을 떠날 수 없소.\"\n\n\"바로 지금,\" 바루아가 변함없이 단호하게 대답했다. \"제 주인이신 누아르티에 어르신께서 프란츠 데피네 남작님과 중대한 일을 의논하고자 하십니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 누아르티에 할아버지가 이제 말을 할 수 있다는 거예요?\" 에두아르가 평소의 그 잽싼 말투로 끼어들었다. 그러나 그 말에 빌포르 부인조차 미소를 짓지 않았다. 모두의 마음이 그만큼 어딘가에 쏠려 있었고, 상황이 그만큼 엄숙했던 것이다.\n\n\"누아르티에 씨께 전하게.\" 빌포르가 다시 입을 열었다. \"그분이 청하시는 일은 불가능하다고.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 누아르티에 어르신께서 여러분께 일러두라 하십니다.\" 바루아가 대답했다. \"사람을 시켜 당신을 응접실로 모셔 오게 하시겠다고요.\"\n\n놀라움은 극에 달했다. 빌포르 부인의 얼굴에는 미소 비슷한 것이 어렸다. 발랑틴은 하늘에 감사라도 드리려는 듯 저도 모르게 눈을 들어 올렸다.\n\n\"발랑틴, 가 보아라.\" 빌포르 씨가 말했다. \"할아버지의 이 새로운 변덕이 대체 무엇인지 알아보거라.\" 발랑틴이 얼른 일어나 기쁜 마음으로 문 쪽으로 서둘러 가려는데, 빌포르 씨가 마음을 바꾸었다.\n\n\"멈추거라.\" 그가 말했다. \"나도 함께 가마.\"\n\n\"실례합니다, 각하.\" 프란츠가 말했다. \"누아르티에 씨가 저를 부르셨으니, 저는 그분의 뜻에 따를 준비가 되어 있습니다. 더구나 아직 인사를 올리는 영광을 누리지 못했으니, 기꺼이 문안을 드리고 싶습니다.\"\n\n\"부디, 데피네 씨.\" 빌포르가 역력한 불안을 드러내며 말했다. \"수고로이 나서지 마시오.\"\n\n\"용서하십시오, 각하.\" 프란츠가 단호한 어조로 말했다. \"저는 이 기회를 놓치고 싶지 않습니다. 누아르티에 씨께서 저에게 품으신 반감이 어떤 것이든, 제 헌신으로 반드시 누그러뜨리겠다고 마음먹은 터이니, 그런 마음을 키우시는 것이 얼마나 잘못된 일인지 그분께 보여 드리고 싶습니다.\"\n\n그러고는 빌포르의 말은 듣지도 않고 자리에서 일어나, 매달릴 바위를 찾은 난파 선원처럼 기쁨에 차 계단을 뛰어 내려가는 발랑틴을 뒤따랐다. 빌포르 씨도 그 뒤를 따랐다. 샤토르노와 모르세르는 더욱 커진 경이의 눈빛으로 세 번째 시선을 주고받았다.\n\n# Chapter 75. A Signed Statement\n\n누아르티에는 그들을 맞을 채비를 하고 있었다. 검은 옷을 차려입고 안락의자에 자리 잡은 모습이었다. 기다리던 세 사람이 들어서자, 그는 문 쪽을 바라보았고 하인이 곧바로 문을 닫았다.\n\n\"잘 듣거라.\" 빌포르가 기쁨을 감추지 못하는 발랑틴에게 속삭였다. \"만일 누아르티에 어른께서 네 결혼을 미루게 할 무언가를 전하려 하시거든, 그 뜻을 알아듣는 것을 금하겠다.\"\n\n발랑틴은 얼굴을 붉혔으나 대답하지 않았다. 빌포르가 누아르티에에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"프란츠 데피네 군입니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"보고 싶다 하셨지요. 저희 모두 이 만남을 바라 왔습니다. 발랑틴의 결혼에 대한 아버님의 반대가 얼마나 근거 없는 것인지 이 자리에서 깨닫게 되시리라 믿습니다.\"\n\n누아르티에는 빌포르의 피를 얼어붙게 하는 눈빛으로만 대답했다. 그는 발랑틴에게 가까이 오라는 몸짓을 했다. 할아버지와 대화하는 데 익숙한 발랑틴은 이내 그가 열쇠를 찾고 있다는 것을 알아챘다. 이어 그의 눈길이 두 창문 사이에 놓인 작은 궤의 서랍에 고정되었다. 그녀가 서랍을 열어 열쇠 하나를 찾아냈다. 그가 원하는 것임을 알아채고는 다시 그의 눈을 살폈다. 그 눈은 여러 해 동안 손도 대지 않아 쓸모없는 서류밖에 들어 있지 않으리라 여겨지던 낡은 책상 쪽을 향하고 있었다.\n\n\"이 책상을 열까요?\" 발랑틴이 물었다.\n\n\"그래.\" 노인이 말했다.\n\n\"서랍들도요?\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n\"옆쪽 서랍들이요?\"\n\n\"아니.\"\n\n\"가운데 서랍이요?\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n발랑틴이 그것을 열고 서류 한 묶음을 꺼냈다. \"이것을 원하시는 거예요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"아니.\"\n\n그녀는 서랍이 빌 때까지 나머지 서류들을 차례로 다 꺼냈다. \"하지만 더는 없는걸요.\" 그녀가 말했다. 누아르티에의 눈은 사전에 고정되어 있었다.\n\n\"네, 알겠어요, 할아버지.\" 처녀가 말했다.\n\n그녀는 알파벳 글자를 하나하나 짚어 갔다. S에 이르자 노인이 그녀를 멈추게 했다. 그녀가 사전을 펼쳐 '비밀'이라는 단어를 찾아냈다.\n\n\"아! 비밀 용수철이 있는 거예요?\" 발랑틴이 물었다.\n\n\"그래.\" 누아르티에가 말했다.\n\n\"누가 그걸 알고 있죠?\" 누아르티에는 하인이 나간 문 쪽을 바라보았다.\n\n\"바루아요?\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n\"그를 부를까요?\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n발랑틴이 문가로 가서 바루아를 불렀다. 이 장면이 이어지는 동안 빌포르의 초조함은 이마에 땀이 흘러내릴 지경이었고, 프란츠는 얼이 빠져 있었다. 늙은 하인이 들어왔다.\n\n\"바루아,\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"할아버지께서 책상의 저 서랍을 열라고 하셨는데, 그 안에 비밀 용수철이 있고 자네가 그걸 안다는구나. 좀 열어 주겠나?\"\n\n바루아가 노인을 바라보았다. \"따르거라.\" 누아르티에의 영민한 눈이 말했다. 바루아가 용수철 하나를 건드리자 거짓 바닥이 빠져나왔고, 검은 끈으로 묶인 서류 한 묶음이 드러났다.\n\n\"이것을 원하십니까?\" 바루아가 말했다.\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n\"이 서류를 빌포르 씨께 드릴까요?\" \"아니.\"\n\n\"발랑틴 아가씨께요?\"\n\n\"아니.\"\n\n\"프란츠 데피네 씨께요?\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n프란츠가 놀라 한 걸음 다가섰다. \"제게요?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n프란츠는 바루아에게서 그것을 받아 들고 겉장을 흘끗 보더니 읽었다.\n\n\"'내가 죽은 뒤 뒤랑 장군에게 전할 것. 장군은 이 꾸러미를 아들에게 물려주되, 중요한 문서가 들었으니 잘 보관하라 일러 둘 것.'\n\n\"자,\" 프란츠가 물었다. \"이 서류로 제가 무엇을 하길 바라시는지요?\"\n\n\"분명, 봉인된 그대로 보관해 두기를 바라시는 게지요.\" 검사가 말했다.\n\n\"아니.\" 누아르티에가 다급히 답했다.\n\n\"그가 이것을 읽기를 바라세요?\" 발랑틴이 물었다.\n\n\"그래.\" 노인이 답했다.\n\n\"남작님, 할아버지께서 이 서류를 읽어 주시기를 바라십니다.\" 발랑틴이 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 앉도록 하지.\" 빌포르가 초조하게 말했다. \"시간이 좀 걸릴 테니.\"\n\n\"앉거라.\" 노인이 말했다. 빌포르는 의자를 끌어다 앉았지만, 발랑틴은 아버지 곁에 선 채로 있었고, 프란츠는 그 수수께끼 같은 서류를 손에 든 채 노인 앞에 서 있었다. \"읽거라.\" 노인이 말했다. 프란츠가 끈을 풀었고, 더없이 깊은 침묵 속에서 읽어 내려갔다.\n\n\"'1815년 2월 5일, 생자크 거리에서 열린 보나파르트파 클럽 회합의 기록 발췌.'\"\n\n프란츠가 멈추었다. \"1815년 2월 5일이라니!\" 그가 말했다. \"제 아버지가 살해되신 날입니다.\" 발랑틴과 빌포르는 입을 다물었다. 오직 노인의 눈만이 또렷이 말하는 듯했다. \"계속하거라.\"\n\n\"하지만 아버지가 사라지신 것은,\" 그가 말했다. \"바로 이 클럽을 나서면서였습니다.\" 누아르티에의 눈은 여전히 \"읽거라.\"라고 말하고 있었다. 그가 다시 읽기 시작했다.\n\n\"'아래 서명한 포병 중령 루이자크 보르파르, 여단장 에티엔 뒤샹피, 그리고 산림 관리관 클로드 르샤르팔은 다음과 같이 진술한다. 2월 4일, 엘바섬에서 한 통의 편지가 도착하여, 플라비앵 드 케넬 장군을 보나파르트파 클럽의 호의와 신뢰에 부탁하였다. 그는 1804년부터 1814년까지 황제를 섬겼으므로, 루이 18세가 막 그에게 데피네 영지와 함께 남작 칭호를 내렸음에도, 나폴레옹 왕조의 이익에 충실하리라 여겨졌다.\n\n\"'이에 따라 케넬 장군에게 한 통의 쪽지가 보내져, 이튿날인 5일 회합에 참석해 달라고 청하였다. 쪽지에는 회합이 열리는 거리도 집 번지도 적혀 있지 않았고, 서명도 없었으나, 아홉 시에 채비를 갖추고 있으면 누군가 모시러 오리라고 장군에게 알렸다. 회합은 늘 그 시각부터 자정까지 열렸다. 아홉 시에 클럽의 의장이 나타났고, 장군은 채비를 마친 상태였다. 의장은 그를 안내하는 조건 가운데 하나가 회합 장소를 영영 모르는 것이며, 따라서 눈을 가리게 두되 가리개를 벗으려 하지 않겠다고 맹세해야 한다고 일렀다. 케넬 장군은 그 조건을 받아들이고, 자신들이 지나는 길을 알아내려 하지 않겠다고 명예를 걸고 약속했다. 장군의 마차가 준비되어 있었으나, 의장은 그것을 쓸 수 없다고 했다. 주인의 눈을 가린들 마부가 어느 거리를 지나는지 안다면 소용없는 일이기 때문이었다. \"그럼 어찌해야 하오?\" 장군이 물었다. \"제 마차가 여기 있습니다.\" 의장이 말했다.\n\n\"'\"그럼 당신은 하인을 어찌나 믿기에, 내게는 알리지 않으려는 비밀을 하인에게는 맡긴단 말이오?\"\n\n\"'\"우리 마부도 클럽의 일원입니다.\" 의장이 말했다. \"한 국참사관이 우리를 모실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"'\"그렇다면 또 다른 위험을 무릅쓰는 셈이군.\" 장군이 웃으며 말했다. \"마차가 뒤집힐 위험 말이오.\" 우리는 이 농담을 기록하여, 장군이 조금도 강요받아 회합에 나온 것이 아니라 스스로 기꺼이 왔음을 증명하고자 한다. 두 사람이 마차에 앉자, 의장은 눈을 가리겠다는 장군의 약속을 상기시켰고, 장군은 이에 반대하지 않았다. 길을 가던 중 의장은 장군이 가리개를 벗으려 하는 것을 본 듯하여 맹세를 일깨웠다. \"그렇지.\" 장군이 말했다. 마차는 생자크 거리에서 빠져나가는 어느 골목 어귀에 멈췄다. 장군은 의장의 팔에 기대어 내렸는데, 그의 지위는 알지 못한 채 그저 클럽의 일원으로만 여겼다. 두 사람은 골목을 지나 층계를 올라 회의실로 들어섰다.\n\n\"'심의는 이미 시작되어 있었다. 그날 저녁 어떤 소개가 이루어지리라는 것을 통보받은 회원들은 모두 자리에 있었다. 방 한가운데에서 가리개를 벗으라는 권유를 받자 장군은 곧바로 그렇게 했고, 그때껏 존재조차 모르던 모임에서 그토록 잘 아는 얼굴들을 그토록 많이 보고는 놀랐다. 사람들이 그의 견해를 묻자, 그는 엘바섬에서 온 편지가 이미 그것을 알려 주었을 것이라고만 대답하였다……'\"\n\n프란츠가 읽기를 멈추고 말했다. \"제 아버지는 왕당파였습니다. 누구나 다 아는 견해를 물을 필요도 없었지요.\"\n\n\"그래서,\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"내가 자네 아버지에게 애정을 품게 된 걸세, 친애하는 프란츠. 같은 생각을 품으면 손쉽게 결속의 끈이 되는 법이니까.\"\n\n\"다시 읽거라.\" 노인이 말했다.\n\n프란츠가 이어 읽었다.\n\n\"'그러자 의장은 그가 더 분명히 말하도록 청했으나, 케넬 씨는 먼저 자신에게 무엇을 원하는지 알고 싶다고 답했다. 이에 엘바섬에서 온 편지의 내용을 그에게 알려 주었는데, 거기서 그는 자기네 당의 이익을 증진시킬 만한 인물로 클럽에 천거되어 있었다. 한 대목은 보나파르트의 귀환을 언급하며, 마르세유의 조선업자 모렐의 소유인 파라옹호가 도착하는 대로 또 한 통의 편지와 더 자세한 정황을 알려 주겠다고 약속하고 있었다. 그 배의 선장은 황제에게 온전히 충성하는 자였다. 이 모든 이야기가 오가는 동안, 형제처럼 믿을 수 있으리라 여겼던 장군은 분명 불만과 거부의 기색을 드러냈다. 낭독이 끝나자 그는 미간을 찌푸린 채 말이 없었다.\n\n\"'\"자,\" 의장이 물었다. \"이 편지를 어찌 생각하십니까, 장군?\"\n\n\"'\"내가 루이 18세를 지지한다고 선언한 지 얼마 되지도 않았는데, 폐위된 황제를 위해 그 맹세를 깨기에는 너무 이르다고 생각하오.\" 이 대답은 그의 견해를 오해할 여지가 없을 만큼 분명했다. \"장군,\" 의장이 말했다. \"우리는 루이 18세 왕도, 폐위된 황제도 인정하지 않습니다. 오직 폭력과 반역으로 자신의 왕국인 프랑스에서 쫓겨난 황제 폐하만을 인정할 뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"'\"용서하시오, 여러분.\" 장군이 말했다. \"당신들은 루이 18세를 인정하지 않을지 모르나, 나는 인정하오. 그분이 나를 남작과 원수로 삼아 주셨으니까. 그리고 그 두 칭호가 그분의 다행스러운 프랑스 귀환 덕분임을 결코 잊지 않을 것이오.\"\n\n\"'\"선생,\" 의장이 엄숙히 일어서며 말했다. \"말을 조심하시오. 당신의 말은 엘바섬에서 당신에 대해 잘못 알았으며, 그래서 우리까지 속았음을 분명히 보여 주는군요! 당신에게 신뢰를 두었기에 이 사실을 전한 것이고, 그것은 당신에게 명예로운 일이오. 그런데 이제 우리의 잘못을 깨달았소. 칭호와 승진이 당신을, 우리가 무너뜨리려는 정부에 묶어 두고 있구려. 우리는 당신에게 협력을 강요하지 않겠소. 양심에 어긋나게 누구를 끌어들이지는 않으니까. 그러나 당신이 내키지 않더라도, 신사답게 처신하도록 강제하겠소.\"\n\n\"'\"당신들의 음모를 알면서도 고발하지 않는 것을 신사다운 처신이라 부른다면, 나라면 그것을 공범이 되는 것이라 부르겠소. 보다시피 나는 당신들보다 솔직하오.\"'\"\n\n\"아, 아버지!\" 프란츠가 읽기를 멈추고 말했다. \"이제야 그들이 왜 아버지를 죽였는지 알겠습니다.\" 발랑틴은 저도 모르게 그 젊은이를 향해 한 번 눈길을 던졌다. 효심으로 가득 찬 그의 모습은 바라보기에 흐뭇했다. 빌포르는 그들 뒤에서 이리저리 서성였다. 누아르티에는 저마다의 표정을 살피며 위엄 있고 당당한 자세를 그대로 지켰다. 프란츠가 다시 원고로 돌아가 읽어 갔다.\n\n\"'\"선생,\" 의장이 말했다. \"당신은 이 모임에 초대받은 것이지 강제로 끌려온 게 아니오. 눈을 가리고 오라는 청을 받았고, 당신은 받아들였소. 그 두 가지 청에 응했을 때, 당신은 우리가 루이 18세의 왕좌를 지키려는 게 아님을 잘 알았을 거요. 그게 아니라면 경찰의 감시를 피하려 이토록 애쓸 까닭이 없으니까. 당신이 가면을 써서 우리의 비밀을 알아낸 뒤, 다시 그것을 벗어 우리를 믿은 자들을 파멸시키도록 두는 것은 지나친 양보일 것이오. 안 되오, 안 돼. 먼저 말하시오. 지금 다스리는 하루짜리 왕을 지지하는지, 아니면 황제 폐하를 지지하는지.\"\n\n\"'\"나는 왕당파요.\" 장군이 답했다. \"루이 18세께 충성의 맹세를 했고, 그것을 지킬 것이오.\" 이 말에 좌중에 웅성거림이 일었고, 여러 회원이 장군에게 그 경솔함을 후회하게 만드는 것이 옳은지 의논하는 기색이 역력했다.\n\n\"'의장이 다시 일어나 침묵을 명한 뒤 말했다. \"선생, 당신은 너무도 진중하고 분별 있는 분이라, 지금 우리 처지가 어떤 결과를 부를지 모를 리 없을 거요. 게다가 당신의 솔직함이 우리가 당신에게 제시할 조건을 이미 정해 주었구려.\" 장군은 검에 손을 얹으며 외쳤다. \"명예를 논하려거든, 먼저 그 법도를 저버리지 마시오. 그리고 어떤 것도 폭력으로 강요하지 마시오.\"\n\n\"'\"그리고 당신은, 선생.\" 의장이 장군의 분노보다 더 무서운 침착함으로 말을 이었다. \"검에 손대지 않는 게 좋을 거요.\" 장군은 가벼운 불안을 느끼며 주위를 둘러보았다. 그러나 굴하지 않고 온 용기를 끌어모아 말했다. \"맹세하지 않겠소.\"\n\n\"'\"그렇다면 죽어야 하오.\" 의장이 차분히 답했다. 데피네 씨는 새파랗게 질렸다. 그가 다시 한 번 주위를 둘러보니, 여러 회원이 수군대며 망토 밑에서 무기를 꺼내고 있었다. \"장군,\" 의장이 말했다. \"겁먹을 것 없소. 당신은 명예로운 자들 가운데 있고, 그들은 마지막 수단에 이르기 전에 온갖 방법으로 당신을 설득하려 할 거요. 그러나 당신 말대로, 당신은 음모자들 가운데 있고, 우리의 비밀을 손에 쥐었으니, 그것을 우리에게 돌려주어야 하오.\" 이 말 뒤에 의미심장한 침묵이 흘렀고, 장군이 대답하지 않자 의장이 문지기에게 일렀다. \"문을 닫으시오.\"\n\n\"'같은 죽음 같은 침묵이 이 말 뒤에 이어졌다. 그러자 장군이 앞으로 나서며, 감정을 억누르려 격렬히 애쓰면서 말했다. \"내게는 아들이 있소. 살인자들 틈에 끼인 처지이니, 그 아이를 생각하지 않을 수 없구려.\"\n\n\"'\"장군,\" 모임의 우두머리가 말했다. \"한 사람이 쉰 사람을 모욕할 수도 있소. 그것은 약자의 특권이니까. 그러나 그 특권을 함부로 쓰는 것은 잘못이오. 내 충고를 따라 맹세하고, 모욕하지 마시오.\" 장군은 우두머리의 위세에 다시 기가 꺾여 잠시 망설였다. 그러다 의장의 책상 앞으로 나아가 말했다. \"그 문구가 무엇이오?\"\n\n\"'\"이러하오. '나는 1815년 2월 5일 저녁 아홉 시에서 열 시 사이에 보고 들은 것을 누구에게도 결코 발설하지 않을 것을 명예를 걸고 맹세한다. 만일 이 맹세를 어긴다면 죽어 마땅함을 인정한다.'\" 장군은 신경의 떨림에 사로잡힌 듯, 한동안 대답하지 못했다. 그러다 역력한 거부감을 누르고 요구받은 맹세를 했으나, 그 목소리가 너무 낮아 대다수 회원에게는 거의 들리지 않았다. 회원들이 또렷하고 분명하게 다시 말하라고 다그치자, 그가 그렇게 했다.\n\n\"'\"이제 물러가도 되겠소?\" 장군이 말했다. 의장이 일어나 세 명의 회원을 지명해 그를 따르게 하고는, 장군의 눈을 다시 가린 뒤 함께 마차에 올랐다. 그 세 사람 가운데 하나는 그들을 그곳까지 몰고 온 마부였다. 나머지 회원들은 말없이 흩어졌다. \"어디로 모셔다 드릴까요?\" 의장이 물었다. \"당신 눈앞에서 벗어날 수만 있다면 어디든 좋소.\" 데피네 씨가 답했다. \"조심하시오, 선생.\" 의장이 답했다. \"당신은 이제 모임에 있는 게 아니라 한 개인을 상대하는 것이오. 책임지고 싶지 않거든 그를 모욕하지 마시오.\" 그러나 데피네 씨는 듣지 않고 말을 이었다. \"당신은 모임에서나 마차에서나 여전히 용감하구려. 아직도 넷이서 하나를 상대하니까.\" 의장이 마차를 세웠다. 그들은 강으로 내려가는 층계가 있는 오름 강둑의 어느 지점에 있었다. \"왜 여기서 멈추는 거요?\" 데피네가 물었다.\n\n\"'\"선생, 당신이 한 사람을 모욕했고,\" 의장이 말했다. \"그 사람은 명예로운 보상을 요구하기 전에는 한 걸음도 더 나아가지 않을 것이기 때문이오.\"\n\n\"'\"또 다른 암살 방식이오?\" 장군이 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다.\n\n\"'\"소란 피우지 마시오, 선생. 방금 당신이 비겁자라 일컬은 자들, 제 약함을 방패 삼는 그런 자들과 똑같이 여겨지고 싶지 않다면 말이오. 당신은 혼자고, 혼자인 한 사람이 당신을 상대할 거요. 당신은 옆구리에 검을 찼고, 나는 지팡이 속에 한 자루가 있소. 당신에게는 입회인이 없으니, 이분들 중 하나가 그 역할을 맡을 거요. 자, 괜찮다면 가리개를 벗으시오.\" 장군이 눈에서 가리개를 잡아 뜯었다. \"마침내,\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 누구를 상대하는지 알게 되겠군.\" 문이 열리고 네 사람이 내렸다.'\"\n\n프란츠가 다시 읽기를 멈추고 이마의 식은땀을 닦았다. 그때껏 수수께끼였던 아버지의 죽음에 관한 이 세세한 이야기를, 아들이 떨리는 창백한 얼굴로 소리 내어 읽는 것을 듣는 데에는 무언가 끔찍한 데가 있었다. 발랑틴은 기도하듯 두 손을 맞잡았다. 누아르티에는 거의 숭고하다 할 경멸과 자부심의 표정으로 빌포르를 바라보았다.\n\n프란츠가 이어 읽었다.\n\n\"'앞서 말했듯이 때는 2월 5일이었다. 사흘째 수은주가 영하 오륙 도를 가리켰고, 층계는 얼음으로 덮여 있었다. 장군은 몸집이 크고 키가 컸으므로, 의장은 그가 내려가는 것을 돕고자 난간 쪽을 양보했다. 두 입회인이 뒤따랐다. 어두운 밤이었다. 층계에서 강에 이르는 땅바닥은 눈과 서리로 덮여 있었고, 강물은 검고 깊어 보였다. 입회인 하나가 가까운 석탄 거룻배로 가서 등롱을 가져왔고, 그 불빛으로 무기를 살폈다. 의장의 검은 그가 말한 대로 그저 지팡이 속에 지니고 다니던 것으로, 장군의 검보다 다섯 인치 짧고 손잡이 보호대도 없었다. 장군은 검을 제비뽑기로 정하자고 했으나, 의장은 도발한 사람이 자기이며, 도발할 때 저마다 제 무기를 쓰리라 여겼다고 말했다. 입회인들이 우겨 보려 했지만, 의장은 그들에게 잠자코 있으라 했다. 등롱이 땅에 놓이고 두 적수가 자리를 잡자, 결투가 시작되었다. 불빛 속에서 두 자루의 검은 번갯불처럼 번뜩였다. 사람의 모습은 어둠이 너무 짙어 거의 분간할 수 없었다.\n\n\"'데피네 장군은 군에서 손꼽히는 검객으로 통했으나, 첫 공세에서 너무 거세게 몰려 겨냥을 놓치고 쓰러졌다. 입회인들은 그가 죽은 줄 알았으나, 자신이 그를 찌르지 않았음을 아는 적수는 그가 일어서도록 손을 내밀어 주었다. 그 처사는 장군을 진정시키기는커녕 오히려 화나게 했고, 그는 적수에게 달려들었다. 그러나 상대는 자신의 방어를 뚫리도록 두지 않았다. 그는 장군을 검으로 받아 냈고, 장군은 너무 바싹 붙은 것을 깨닫고 세 번이나 물러섰다가 다시 덤벼들었다. 세 번째에 그가 또 쓰러졌다. 사람들은 처음처럼 그가 미끄러진 줄 알았으나, 그가 움직이지 않는 것을 보고 입회인들이 다가가 일으키려 했다. 그런데 그 몸에 팔을 두른 자가 거기 피가 흥건한 것을 알아챘다. 거의 정신을 잃었던 장군이 깨어났다. \"아,\" 그가 말했다. \"검술 사범을 보내 나와 싸우게 했군.\" 의장은 대꾸 없이 등롱을 든 입회인에게 다가가, 소매를 걷어 팔에 입은 두 군데 상처를 보였다. 이어 외투를 열고 조끼 단추를 풀어, 세 번째 상처로 꿰뚫린 옆구리를 드러냈다. 그러면서도 그는 신음 한 번 내지 않았다. 데피네 장군은 오 분 뒤에 숨을 거두었다.'\"\n\n프란츠는 이 마지막 구절을 너무 목멘 소리로 읽어 거의 알아들을 수 없을 지경이었다. 그러고는 멈추어, 구름이라도 걷어 내려는 듯 손으로 눈을 훔쳤다. 그러나 잠시 침묵한 뒤 다시 읽어 갔다.\n\n\"'의장은 검을 지팡이 속에 도로 밀어 넣고 층계를 올라갔다. 눈 위에 남은 핏자국이 그의 자취를 새겼다. 그가 꼭대기에 다다르자마자, 물에서 무언가 무겁게 첨벙 빠지는 소리가 들렸다. 그것은 장군의 시신으로, 입회인들이 그가 죽었음을 확인한 뒤 막 강에 던져 넣은 것이었다. 그러므로 장군은 보도될 수도 있었던 매복이 아니라, 정정당당한 결투에서 쓰러진 것이다. 이를 증명하기 위해, 이 끔찍한 장면의 어느 당사자든 언젠가 계획적 살인이나 명예의 법도를 어겼다는 혐의를 받게 될 경우에 대비하여, 우리는 사실의 진상을 밝히고자 이 문서에 서명한다.\n\n\"'서명, 보르파르, 뒤샹피, 르샤르팔.'\"\n\n아들에게는 그토록 끔찍한 이 기록을 프란츠가 다 읽었을 때, 감정에 북받쳐 창백해진 발랑틴이 눈물을 훔쳤을 때, 떨면서 한구석에 웅크린 빌포르가 무자비한 노인에게 애원하는 눈길을 보내 그 폭풍을 누그러뜨리려 애썼을 때―\n\n\"어르신,\" 데피네가 누아르티에에게 말했다. \"어르신께서는 명예로운 서명으로 입증된 이 모든 세세한 정황을 잘 아시고, 비록 지금껏 제게 슬픔을 안기는 것으로만 드러내셨을지언정 저에게 어떤 관심을 두고 계신 듯하니, 제게 마지막 한 가지 만족만은 거절하지 말아 주십시오. 그 클럽 의장의 이름을 알려 주십시오. 적어도 누가 제 아버지를 죽였는지는 알 수 있도록 말입니다.\"\n\n빌포르는 무심결에 문손잡이를 더듬었다. 누구보다 먼저 할아버지의 대답을 알아챈 발랑틴은, 그의 오른팔에 난 두 개의 흉터를 여러 번 보아 온 터라, 몇 걸음 뒤로 물러섰다.\n\n\"아가씨,\" 프란츠가 발랑틴을 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"제가 두 살에 고아가 되게 만든 그 사람의 이름을 밝히는 데 부디 힘을 보태 주십시오.\" 발랑틴은 말없이 꼼짝도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"그만하시오, 선생.\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"이 끔찍한 장면을 더 끌지 마시오. 이름은 일부러 감춰진 것이오. 내 아버지조차 그 의장이 누구였는지 모르고, 설령 안다 해도 당신에게 말해 줄 수 없소. 고유명사는 사전에 없으니까.\"\n\n\"오, 비참한 일이군요.\" 프란츠가 외쳤다. \"제가 끝까지 읽어 낼 수 있도록 저를 지탱해 준 단 하나의 희망은, 적어도 제 아버지를 죽인 자의 이름을 아는 것이었습니다! 어르신, 어르신,\" 그가 누아르티에를 향해 돌아서며 외쳤다. \"하실 수 있는 대로 해 주십시오. 어떻게든 제가 알아듣게 해 주십시오!\"\n\n\"그래.\" 누아르티에가 답했다.\n\n\"오, 아가씨, 아가씨!\" 프란츠가 외쳤다. \"할아버님께서 그 사람을 가리킬 수 있다고 하십니다. 저를 도와주십시오. 부디 도와주십시오!\"\n\n누아르티에가 사전을 바라보았다. 프란츠는 신경질적으로 떨며 그것을 집어 들고, 알파벳 글자를 차례로 짚어 가다가 M에 이르렀다. 그 글자에서 노인이 \"그래\"라는 뜻을 표했다.\n\n\"M.\" 프란츠가 되뇌었다. 젊은이의 손가락이 단어들 위로 미끄러졌으나, 단어마다 누아르티에는 아니라는 신호로 답했다. 발랑틴은 두 손에 얼굴을 묻었다. 마침내 프란츠가 '나 자신'이라는 단어에 이르렀다.\n\n\"그래!\"\n\n\"당신이!\" 프란츠가 외쳤다. 그의 머리카락이 곤두섰다. \"당신, 누아르티에 어르신이―당신이 제 아버지를 죽였습니까?\"\n\n\"그래!\" 누아르티에가 젊은이에게 위엄 어린 시선을 고정한 채 답했다. 프란츠는 맥없이 의자에 쓰러졌다. 빌포르는 문을 열고 달아났다. 이 무서운 노인의 가슴에 남은 얼마 안 되는 목숨을 짓눌러 꺼 버리고 싶다는 생각이 머릿속에 떠올랐던 것이다.\n\n# Chapter 76. Progress of Cavalcanti the Younger\n\n그동안 카발칸티 노인은 다시 본업으로 돌아가 있었다. 그가 봉직하는 곳은 오스트리아 황제 폐하의 군대가 아니라, 루카 온천장의 도박판이었다. 그는 그곳을 가장 부지런히 드나드는 단골 중 하나였다. 아버지라는 가짜 역할을 그토록 위엄 있고 근엄하게 해낸 보상으로 여행 경비로 받은 돈을 한 푼도 남김없이 거기서 탕진해 버렸다.\n\n안드레아 군은 그가 떠날 때, 자신이 바르톨로메오 후작과 올리바 코르시나리 후작 부인의 아들이라는 영광을 누리고 있음을 증명하는 모든 서류를 물려받았다. 그는 이제 파리 사교계에 제법 그럴듯하게 발을 들였다. 파리 사교계는 외국인을 손쉽게 받아들이고, 그들을 실제 모습 그대로가 아니라 그들이 보이고 싶어 하는 대로 대접해 주는 곳이었다. 게다가 파리에서 젊은이에게 요구되는 것이 무엇이겠는가? 프랑스어를 그런대로 구사하고, 외모가 번듯하며, 노름을 잘하고, 현금으로 셈을 치르는 것뿐이었다. 외국인에게는 프랑스인에게보다 분명 덜 까다로운 법이었다. 그리하여 안드레아는 보름 만에 꽤 그럴듯한 지위를 얻었다. 사람들은 그를 백작이라 불렀고, 한 해에 5만 리브르를 거둬들인다고 수군거렸으며, 사라베차 채석장에 묻혀 있다는 그 아버지의 막대한 재산이 늘 화젯거리가 되었다. 한 학식 있는 사람은 이 마지막 사실이 정말인 양 언급되는 자리에서, 자신이 바로 그 채석장을 직접 보았노라고 단언했다. 그러자 그때까지 다소 미심쩍던 이야기들이 단번에 무게를 얻으며 사실의 옷을 걸치게 되었다.\n\n지금 우리가 독자들 앞에 펼쳐 보이는 시기의 파리 사교계 형편이 그러했다. 그러던 어느 날 저녁, 몬테크리스토가 당글라르 씨를 찾아갔다. 당글라르 씨는 외출 중이었으나, 백작은 남작 부인을 뵙고 가시라는 청을 받았고, 그 청을 받아들였다. 오퇴유 만찬과 그 뒤에 이어진 일들이 있은 뒤로, 당글라르 부인은 몬테크리스토라는 이름이 알려질 때마다 신경질적인 전율을 느끼지 않을 수 없었다. 그가 오지 않으면 그 괴로운 감정이 극도로 심해졌다. 반대로 그가 나타나면, 그 고결한 용모와 빛나는 눈, 상냥함, 당글라르 부인을 향한 정중한 배려가 이내 두려움의 인상을 말끔히 흩어 버렸다. 그토록 더없이 유쾌하고 기분 좋은 태도를 지닌 사람이 자신을 해칠 흉계를 품고 있으리라고는 남작 부인으로서는 도무지 믿기지 않았다. 게다가 아무리 타락한 마음이라 해도, 무언가 이득이 걸려 있을 때에만 악을 의심하는 법이었다. 아무 이득도 없는 해코지란 어떤 마음에게도 거슬리는 것이었다.\n\n몬테크리스토가 규방으로 들어섰을 때 — 우리는 이미 한 번 독자들을 이곳으로 안내한 바 있다 — 남작 부인은 그림 몇 점을 들여다보고 있었다. 그 그림들은 딸이 카발칸티 군과 함께 본 다음 어머니에게 건넨 것이었다. 백작이 나타나자 이내 늘 그렇던 효과가 나타났다. 남작 부인은 그의 이름이 알려졌을 때 조금 당황했으면서도, 미소를 지으며 백작을 맞이했다. 백작은 한눈에 그 자리의 광경을 전부 알아차렸다.\n\n남작 부인은 소파에 반쯤 기대 누워 있었고, 외제니가 그 곁에 앉아 있었으며, 카발칸티는 서 있었다. 카발칸티는 괴테의 주인공들처럼 검은 옷차림에, 칠피 구두를 신고, 흰 비단 망사 양말을 신고 있었다. 그는 희고 제법 보기 좋은 손으로 옅은 빛깔의 머리카락을 쓸어 넘겼는데, 그러면서 반짝이는 다이아몬드를 드러내 보였다. 몬테크리스토의 충고에도 불구하고 그 허영심 많은 젊은이는 새끼손가락에 그 반지를 끼고 싶은 유혹을 끝내 이기지 못했던 것이다. 이 동작에는 당글라르 양을 향한 애를 태우는 듯한 눈길과, 같은 방향으로 내쉬는 한숨이 곁들여졌다.\n\n당글라르 양은 여전히 그대로였다. 차갑고, 아름답고, 빈정대는 듯했다. 그 눈길도, 그 한숨도 그녀에게는 단 하나도 통하지 않았다. 마치 미네르바의 방패에 부딪혀 떨어지는 것 같았다. 어떤 철학자들은 그 방패가 때로 사포의 가슴을 지켜 주었다고 주장하기도 한다. 외제니는 백작에게 차갑게 인사하고는, 대화가 진지해지는 첫 순간을 틈타 자신의 작업실로 빠져나갔다. 곧 그곳에서 명랑하고 떠들썩한 두 목소리가 이따금 피아노 가락과 어우러져 들려왔다. 당글라르 양이 백작과 카발칸티 군의 자리보다 노래 선생인 루이즈 다르밀리 양과 함께 있는 편을 더 좋아한다는 것을 몬테크리스토는 분명히 알 수 있었다.\n\n그러는 동안, 특히 당글라르 부인과 대화를 나누며 겉으로는 대화의 매력에 푹 빠진 듯하면서도, 백작은 안드레아 카발칸티 군의 안절부절못하는 모습을 눈여겨보았다. 그가 감히 넘어서지 못하는 문 앞에서 음악에 귀 기울이는 모양과, 그 감탄을 드러내는 태도를 말이다.\n\n이윽고 은행가가 돌아왔다. 그의 첫 시선은 분명 몬테크리스토를 향했으나, 두 번째 시선은 안드레아에게 갔다. 아내에게는 어떤 남편들이 아내에게 하듯 인사를 건넸는데, 그 방식은 부부 생활에 관한 아주 방대한 법전이 출간되기 전까지는 독신자들로서는 결코 이해하지 못할 그런 것이었다.\n\n\"부인들께서 자네에게 피아노 곁으로 와 함께하자고 청하지 않던가?\" 당글라르가 안드레아에게 말했다.\n\n\"안타깝게도, 그렇지 않았습니다.\" 안드레아가 앞서보다 한층 더 두드러진 한숨과 함께 대답했다. 당글라르는 곧장 문 쪽으로 다가가 문을 열었다.\n\n두 젊은 아가씨가 피아노 앞 같은 의자에 나란히 앉아 있는 것이 보였다. 두 사람은 각자 한 손씩으로 스스로 반주를 맞추고 있었는데, 그것은 그들이 익혀 온 취미였고 솜씨도 훌륭했다. 그때 열린 문틈으로 보이는 다르밀리 양은 외제니와 더불어 독일인들이 그토록 좋아하는 활인화 한 폭을 이루고 있었다. 그녀는 제법 아름답고 더없이 우아한 몸매를 지닌, 작고 요정 같은 모습이었다. 굵은 곱슬머리가 목덜미로 흘러내렸는데, 그 목이 다소 길어, 페루지노가 이따금 성모를 그릴 때처럼 보였고, 두 눈은 피로로 흐릿했다. 가슴이 약하다는 소문이 있었고, 〈크레모나의 바이올린〉에 나오는 안토니아처럼 언젠가 노래를 부르다 죽으리라는 말도 돌았다.\n\n몬테크리스토는 이 작은 성역을 한차례 빠르고도 호기심 어린 눈길로 둘러보았다. 그가 다르밀리 양을 직접 본 것은 이번이 처음이었다. 그녀에 관한 이야기는 익히 들어 왔던 터였다.\n\n\"그래서,\" 은행가가 딸에게 말했다. \"그럼 우리 모두는 빼놓겠다는 게냐?\"\n\n그러고는 젊은이를 작업실로 데리고 들어갔다. 우연이었는지 술수였는지, 안드레아가 들어간 뒤 문이 반쯤 닫혀, 두 사람이 앉은 자리에서는 백작도 남작 부인도 안을 들여다볼 수 없게 되었다. 그러나 은행가가 안드레아를 따라 들어갔으므로, 당글라르 부인은 그 일을 모르는 체하는 듯했다.\n\n이윽고 백작은 피아노 반주에 맞춰 코르시카 노래를 부르는 안드레아의 목소리를 들었다. 백작은 그 노래를 들으며 미소를 지었다. 그 노래 때문에 안드레아의 모습이 베네데토에 대한 기억 속으로 사라져 버렸기 때문이다. 그동안 당글라르 부인은 몬테크리스토에게 남편의 굳센 마음을 자랑하고 있었다. 바로 그날 아침에도 그는 밀라노에서 일어난 파산으로 삼사십만 프랑을 잃었다는 것이었다. 그 칭찬은 마땅한 것이었다. 백작이 남작 부인에게서, 혹은 그가 무엇이든 알아내는 그 여러 방편 가운데 하나로 듣지 않았더라면, 남작의 표정만 보고서는 그런 일을 짐작조차 하지 못했을 테니 말이다.\n\n'흠.' 몬테크리스토는 생각했다. '이제 손실을 감추기 시작하는군. 한 달 전만 해도 그걸 자랑하더니.'\n\n그러고는 소리 내어 말했다. \"오, 부인, 당글라르 씨는 워낙 수완이 좋으신 분이니, 다른 데서 잃은 것쯤은 곧 증권 거래소에서 되찾으실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"백작님께서도 흔한 오해에 빠져 계시는군요.\" 당글라르 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 오해 말씀입니까?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다.\n\n\"당글라르 씨가 투기를 한다는 오해 말이에요. 그이는 절대 투기를 하지 않는답니다.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 부인, 드브레 씨가 제게 말하기를…… 아 참, 그분은 요즘 어찌 지내십니까? 사나흘 동안 통 뵙지를 못했네요.\"\n\n\"저도 그래요.\" 당글라르 부인이 말했다. \"그런데 무슨 말씀을 시작하시고는 끝맺지 않으셨어요.\"\n\"무슨 말 말입니까?\"\n\n\"드브레 씨가 백작님께 말씀하셨다는……\"\n\n\"아, 그렇지요. 투기의 악마에게 몸을 바치는 분이 바로 부인이라고 하더군요.\"\n\n\"한때는 무척 좋아했지요. 하지만 이제는 손을 대지 않아요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 잘못하시는 겁니다, 부인. 재산이란 위태로운 것이지요. 만일 제가 여자이고 운명이 저를 은행가의 아내로 만들었다면, 남편의 좋은 운수를 아무리 믿는다 해도, 아시다시피 투기에는 큰 위험이 따르는 법이니까요. 그러니 저라면 남편과는 별개로 제 몫의 재산을 마련해 둘 겁니다. 설령 남편이 모르는 손에 제 이익을 맡겨서 얻은 것이라 할지라도 말이지요.\" 당글라르 부인은 아무리 애를 써도 얼굴이 붉어지는 것을 어쩔 수 없었다.\n\n\"참,\" 몬테크리스토가 마치 그녀의 당혹스러움을 알아채지 못한 듯 말했다. \"어제 나폴리 채권에서 한몫 톡톡히 챙긴 일이 있었다고 들었습니다.\"\n\n\"저는 그런 채권을 가지고 있지 않아요. 가져 본 적도 없고요. 한데 백작님, 우리가 돈 이야기를 너무 오래 했네요. 꼭 주식 중개인 둘이 마주 앉은 꼴이에요. 그건 그렇고, 운명이 가엾은 빌포르 집안을 어떻게 괴롭히고 있는지 들으셨어요?\"\n\n\"무슨 일이 있었습니까?\" 백작이 아무것도 모르는 척하며 물었다.\n\n\"생메랑 후작이 파리로 가는 여행길에 오른 지 며칠 만에 돌아가셨고, 후작 부인은 도착하신 지 며칠 만에 돌아가셨다는 거 아세요?\"\n\n\"네.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그 얘기는 들었습니다. 하지만 클라우디우스가 햄릿에게 말했듯이, '그것은 자연의 법칙이지요. 그들의 아버지가 그들보다 먼저 죽었고 그들이 그 죽음을 애도했듯, 그들도 자식들보다 먼저 죽을 것이고, 자식들이 또 그들을 슬퍼할 테니까요.'\"\n\n\"하지만 그게 다가 아니에요.\"\n\n\"다가 아니라고요!\"\n\n\"네. 그 집에서 딸을 시집보내려던 참이었거든요……\"\n\n\"프란츠 데피네 씨에게 말이지요. 그 혼사가 깨졌습니까?\"\n\n\"어제 아침에, 프란츠가 그 영예를 사양했다나 봐요.\"\n\n\"정말입니까? 그 까닭이 알려졌나요?\"\n\n\"아니요.\"\n\n\"참으로 별일이군요! 그래, 빌포르 씨는 그 일을 어떻게 받아들이던가요?\"\n\n\"여느 때처럼요. 철학자처럼 말이에요.\"\n\n바로 그때 당글라르가 혼자 돌아왔다.\n\n\"그래,\" 남작 부인이 말했다. \"카발칸티 씨를 딸아이와 단둘이 두고 오셨어요?\"\n\n\"다르밀리 양도 있잖소.\" 은행가가 말했다. \"그 아가씨는 사람으로 안 친단 말이오?\" 그러고는 몬테크리스토를 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"카발칸티 공자, 매력적인 젊은이지요, 안 그렇습니까? 그런데 정말로 공자가 맞기는 한 겁니까?\"\n\n\"그건 제가 장담할 수 없습니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그 부친이 제게 소개될 때 후작이라 하였으니, 그렇다면 아들은 백작쯤 되어야겠지요. 하지만 그가 그 작위를 내세울 만한 근거가 그리 있어 보이지는 않습니다.\"\n\n\"어째서요?\" 은행가가 말했다. \"공자라면, 제 신분을 지키지 않는 건 잘못이지요. 나는 누가 제 출신을 부인하는 걸 좋아하지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"오, 당신은 철저한 민주주의자시군요.\" 몬테크리스토가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 당신이 어떤 처지에 놓이는지 아세요?\" 남작 부인이 말했다. \"혹시라도 모르세르 씨가 온다면, 외제니의 약혼자인 그가 한 번도 들어가 본 적 없는 저 방에서 카발칸티 씨와 마주치게 될 거예요.\"\n\n\"혹시라도, 라는 말이 딱 맞소.\" 은행가가 대꾸했다. \"그 사람이 워낙 드물게 오니, 어쩌다 오는 게 순전히 우연처럼 보일 지경이지.\"\n\n\"하지만 그가 와서 저 젊은이가 딸과 함께 있는 걸 본다면, 기분이 상할지도 몰라요.\"\n\n\"그 사람이? 당신이 잘못 봤소. 알베르 군은 우리한테 질투해 주는 영예를 베풀 위인이 아니야. 외제니를 그만큼 좋아하지도 않고. 게다가 나는 그가 기분 상하든 말든 상관없소.\"\n\n\"그래도, 우리 처지가 처지인 만큼……\"\n\n\"그래, 우리 처지가 어떤지 아시오? 제 어머니의 무도회에서 그는 외제니와 딱 한 번 춤을 추었고, 카발칸티 씨는 세 번이나 추었는데, 그는 아무렇지도 않게 여기더군.\"\n\n하인이 알베르 드 모르세르 자작을 알렸다. 남작 부인이 황급히 일어나 작업실로 들어가려 하자, 당글라르가 그녀를 막았다.\n\n\"그냥 두시오.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n그녀는 놀란 눈으로 그를 바라보았다. 몬테크리스토는 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 모르는 듯했다. 알베르가 들어왔는데, 매우 잘생긴 모습에 기분이 한껏 좋아 보였다. 그는 남작 부인에게 정중하게, 당글라르에게 허물없이, 몬테크리스토에게 다정하게 인사했다. 그러고는 남작 부인을 향해 돌아서서 말했다. \"당글라르 양은 잘 지내는지 여쭈어도 될까요?\"\n\n\"아주 잘 지내네.\" 당글라르가 얼른 대답했다. \"지금 카발칸티 씨와 피아노 앞에 있다네.\"\n\n알베르는 차분하고 무심한 태도를 잃지 않았다. 어쩌면 속으로는 언짢았을지도 모르나, 몬테크리스토의 눈이 자신을 향해 있다는 것을 알고 있었다. \"카발칸티 씨는 훌륭한 테너 목소리를 지녔지요.\" 그가 말했다. \"외제니 양은 멋진 소프라노이고, 게다가 탈베르크처럼 피아노를 치니까요. 그 연주회는 분명 즐거운 것이겠습니다.\"\n\n\"둘이 아주 잘 어울리지.\" 당글라르가 말했다. 알베르는 이 말을 못 들은 척했으나, 그 말이 어찌나 무례했던지 당글라르 부인이 얼굴을 붉혔다.\n\n\"저도,\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"음악을 합니다. 적어도 제 선생님들은 그렇게 말씀하시곤 했지요. 하지만 이상하게도 제 목소리는 다른 어떤 목소리와도 도무지 어울리질 않았어요. 그중에서도 소프라노와는 가장 안 맞았고요.\"\n\n당글라르는 미소를 지었는데, 마치 \"그건 아무래도 상관없네.\" 하고 말하는 듯했다. 그러고는 분명 제 속셈을 이루려는 듯 말했다. \"공자와 내 딸이 어제 모두에게 큰 찬사를 받았다네. 자네는 그 자리에 없었지, 모르세르 군?\"\n\n\"무슨 공자 말씀입니까?\" 알베르가 물었다.\n\n\"카발칸티 공자 말일세.\" 당글라르는 끝끝내 그 젊은이에게 그 작위를 붙여 불렀다.\n\n\"실례지만,\" 알베르가 말했다. \"그가 공자인 줄은 몰랐습니다. 그래, 카발칸티 공자가 어제 외제니 양과 함께 노래를 불렀다고요? 정말 멋진 자리였겠습니다. 그 노래를 듣지 못한 게 유감이군요. 하지만 어제는 어머니를 모시고 샤토르노 남작 부인이 여신 독일 음악회에 가기로 약속한 터라, 초대에 응할 수가 없었습니다.\"\n\n이어 다소 어색한 침묵이 흘렀다.\n\n\"저도 당글라르 양께 인사를 드려도 될까요?\" 모르세르가 말했다.\n\n\"잠깐만.\" 은행가가 젊은이를 붙들며 말했다. \"저 멋진 카바티나가 들리나? 타, 타, 타, 티, 타, 티, 타, 타. 정말 근사하군. 끝까지 들어 보세. 잠깐만. 브라보, 브라비, 브라바!\" 은행가는 열렬히 박수를 쳤다.\n\n\"정말이지,\" 알베르가 말했다. \"더없이 훌륭하군요. 카발칸티 공자만큼 제 나라 음악을 잘 이해하기란 불가능한 일이지요. 공자라고 하셨지요? 하지만 아직 아니라 해도 얼마든지 공자가 될 수 있을 겁니다. 이탈리아에서는 드문 일도 아니니까요. 한데 그 멋진 음악가들 이야기로 돌아가자면 — 당글라르 씨, 저들에게 손님이 와 있다는 말은 하지 말고 우리에게 한 곡 더 들려주시지요. 부탁드립니다. 음악가들이 남의 눈을 의식하지 않을 때, 멀리서 음악을 듣는 건 참으로 즐거운 일이니까요.\"\n\n당글라르는 젊은이의 무심함에 적잖이 언짢았다. 그는 몬테크리스토를 한쪽으로 데리고 갔다.\n\n\"우리 구혼자를 어떻게 보십니까?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"냉랭해 보이는군요. 하지만 어쨌든 당신은 약속하신 거 아닙니까.\"\n\n\"그렇지요. 분명 내 딸을, 그 애를 사랑하는 사람에게 주겠다고 약속했지, 사랑하지 않는 사람에게 주겠다고는 안 했습니다. 저기 저 사람을 보시오. 대리석처럼 차갑고 제 아비처럼 거만하지 않습니까. 부자라면, 카발칸티만큼 재산이라도 있다면야 봐줄 수도 있겠지요. 정말이지, 내 딸에게 물어보지는 않았소만, 그 애가 안목이 있다면……\"\n\n\"오,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"제가 그를 아끼는 마음에 눈이 멀었는지는 모르겠으나, 분명히 말씀드리건대 저는 모르세르를 매력적인 젊은이로 봅니다. 그는 따님을 행복하게 해 줄 것이고, 머잖아 어느 정도 명성도 얻을 겁니다. 게다가 부친의 지위도 든든하지요.\"\n\n\"흠.\" 당글라르가 말했다.\n\n\"어째서 의심하십니까?\"\n\n\"과거 말이오. 그 과거에 드리운 그늘 말입니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 그게 아들에게 영향을 미치지는 않지요.\"\n\n\"지당한 말씀입니다.\"\n\n\"자, 부탁이니 부디 흥분하지 마십시오. 당신이 이 혼사를 생각해 온 지 벌써 한 달이오. 그러니 이 일이 내게도 어느 정도 책임을 지운다는 걸 아셔야 합니다. 당신이 이 젊은 카발칸티를 만난 게 바로 우리 집에서였으니 말이오. 나로서는 그를 사실 전혀 모르는데 말입니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 나는 압니다.\"\n\n\"알아보기라도 하셨소?\"\n\n\"그럴 필요가 어디 있겠습니까! 그 풍채가 그를 대변해 주지 않습니까? 게다가 그는 대단한 부자고요.\"\n\n\"그건 나도 그리 확신이 안 섭니다.\"\n\n\"그래도 돈이 있다고 하셨잖습니까.\"\n\n\"5만 리브르 — 그까짓 푼돈이지요.\"\n\n\"교양도 갖추었고요.\"\n\n\"흠.\" 이번에는 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"음악가이기도 하고요.\"\n\n\"이탈리아 사람이라면 다 그렇지요.\"\n\n\"이거 참, 백작님, 그 젊은이를 너무 박하게 보시는군요.\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 인정합니다만, 당신이 모르세르 집안과 맺은 인연을 아는 터라, 그가 끼어드는 걸 보면 마음이 거슬리는군요.\" 당글라르가 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"어쩜 그리 청교도시오!\" 그가 말했다. \"그런 일은 날마다 일어나는 일인걸요.\"\n\n\"하지만 이런 식으로 깨 버릴 수는 없는 노릇이지요. 모르세르 집안은 이 혼사에 기대를 걸고 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇소?\"\n\n\"틀림없습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그쪽이 분명히 입장을 밝혀야지요. 당신이 그 아버지에게 넌지시 일러 주시구려. 그 집안과 그토록 가까운 사이시니 말이오.\"\n\n\"내가요? 대체 어디서 그런 소릴 들으셨습니까?\"\n\n\"그 댁 무도회에서 말이오. 충분히 드러나 보이던걸요. 아니, 그 백작 부인, 그 도도한 메르세데스, 오랜 지기에게도 좀체 입을 열지 않는 그 새침한 카탈루냐 여인이, 당신 팔을 잡고 정원으로, 한적한 산책로로 이끌어 거기서 반 시간이나 머물지 않았습니까?\"\n\n\"아, 남작, 남작,\" 알베르가 말했다. \"남작께선 안 듣고 계시는군요 — 당신처럼 과대망상에 빠진 사람한테 이 무슨 야만적인 짓이오!\"\n\n\"오, 내 걱정은 마시오, 비꼬기 선생.\" 당글라르가 말했다. 그러고는 몬테크리스토를 향해 돌아서서 말했다.\n\n\"한데 그 아버지한테 얘기를 좀 해 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그러지요, 당신이 원하신다면요.\"\n\n\"하지만 분명하고 확실하게 해 주십시오. 그가 내 딸을 원한다면 날짜를 잡으라고, 조건을 내걸라고 하시오. 한마디로, 서로 분명히 하든지, 아니면 갈라서든지 합시다. 아시겠지요 — 더는 미루지 말자는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"네, 그러지요. 이 일에 마음을 쓰겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그의 결정을 즐거이 기다린다고는 못하겠소만, 어쨌든 기다리기는 하겠습니다. 아시다시피 은행가란 제 약속의 노예가 되어야 하는 법이니까요.\" 그러고서 당글라르는 반 시간 전 카발칸티가 그랬듯 한숨을 내쉬었다.\n\n\"브라비! 브라보! 브라바!\" 곡이 끝나자 모르세르가 은행가를 흉내 내며 외쳤다. 당글라르가 모르세르를 미심쩍은 눈으로 보기 시작할 무렵, 누군가 다가와 그에게 몇 마디를 속삭였다.\n\n\"곧 돌아오겠습니다.\" 은행가가 몬테크리스토에게 말했다. \"기다려 주십시오. 어쩌면 당신께 드릴 말씀이 있을 것 같으니까요.\" 그러고는 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n남작 부인은 남편이 자리를 비운 틈을 타 딸의 작업실 문을 밀어 열었다. 외제니 양과 함께 피아노 앞에 앉아 있던 안드레아 군이 깜짝 상자 인형처럼 벌떡 일어섰다. 알베르가 미소를 띠며 당글라르 양에게 인사했으나, 그녀는 조금도 동요하는 기색 없이 여느 때의 그 차가움으로 답례했다. 카발칸티는 분명 당황한 기색이었다. 그가 모르세르에게 인사하자, 모르세르는 더없이 무례한 표정으로 답했다. 그러고 나서 알베르는 당글라르 양의 목소리를 한껏 칭찬하며, 방금 들은 그 노래를 두고 어제저녁 그 자리에 함께하지 못한 것이 못내 아쉽다고 늘어놓았다.\n\n홀로 남겨진 카발칸티는 몬테크리스토를 향해 돌아섰다.\n\n\"자,\" 당글라르 부인이 말했다. \"음악이며 인사치레는 그만들 하고, 가서 차나 마십시다.\"\n\n\"가자, 루이즈.\" 당글라르 양이 친구에게 말했다.\n\n그들은 차가 준비된 옆 응접실로 자리를 옮겼다. 영국식으로 찻잔에 숟가락을 그냥 둔 채 막 차를 마시려는 참에, 다시 문이 열리며 당글라르가 들어왔다. 눈에 띄게 동요한 모습이었다. 몬테크리스토가 그것을 유심히 살피고는, 눈짓으로 은행가에게 설명을 청했다.\n\n\"방금 그리스에서 보낸 전령이 도착했습니다.\" 당글라르가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 그래서,\" 백작이 말했다. \"우리한테서 황급히 자리를 뜨셨던 거군요.\"\n\"그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"오톤 왕은 어떻게 지내십니까?\" 알베르가 더없이 쾌활한 어조로 물었다.\n\n당글라르는 대꾸도 없이 그에게 또 한 번 미심쩍은 눈길을 던졌고, 몬테크리스토는 제 얼굴에 스치는 연민의 빛을 감추려 고개를 돌렸으나, 그 빛은 한순간에 사라졌다.\n\n\"우리 함께 가는 거지요?\" 알베르가 백작에게 말했다.\n\n\"원하신다면요.\" 백작이 대답했다.\n\n알베르는 은행가의 눈빛을 이해할 수가 없어, 그것을 훤히 알아챈 몬테크리스토를 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"보셨습니까? 저 사람이 저를 어떻게 노려보는지요?\"\n\n\"네.\" 백작이 말했다. \"한데 그 눈빛에 뭔가 특별한 게 있다고 생각하셨습니까?\"\n\n\"정말 그랬는걸요. 그리고 그리스에서 온 소식이라는 게 대체 무슨 뜻이랍니까?\"\n\n\"그걸 제가 어찌 알겠습니까?\"\n\n\"백작께서는 그 나라에 연락하는 사람들을 두고 계실 것 같아서요.\"\n\n몬테크리스토가 의미심장하게 미소 지었다.\n\n\"잠깐,\" 알베르가 말했다. \"그가 이리로 오는군요. 아버지가 백작님과 이야기하시는 동안 저는 당글라르 양에게 카메오를 칭찬해야겠습니다.\"\n\n\"칭찬을 하시려거든, 적어도 그 목소리를 칭찬하시지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"안 됩니다. 그건 누구나 다 할 칭찬이잖습니까.\"\n\n\"이보시오, 자작, 당신은 지독히도 무례하군요.\"\n\n알베르가 미소를 띠며 외제니에게 다가갔다.\n\n그러는 동안 당글라르가 몬테크리스토의 귀에 대고 몸을 숙이며 말했다. \"당신 충고가 아주 훌륭했습니다. 페르낭과 야니나라는 이름에 얽힌 사연이 통째로 하나 있더군요.\"\n\n\"정말입니까?\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇습니다. 다 말씀드리지요. 하지만 저 젊은이를 좀 데려가 주십시오. 그가 곁에 있는 걸 도무지 견딜 수가 없으니.\"\n\n\"그는 저와 함께 갈 겁니다. 그 아버지를 당신께 보내 드릴까요?\"\n\n\"당장 보내 주십시오.\"\n\n\"좋습니다.\" 백작이 알베르에게 신호를 보내자, 두 사람은 부인들에게 인사하고 자리를 떴다. 알베르는 당글라르 양의 경멸에도 더없이 무심했고, 몬테크리스토는 은행가의 아내라면 장래를 대비하는 데 마땅히 신중을 기해야 한다고 당글라르 부인에게 거듭 충고했다.\n\n카발칸티 군은 그 자리의 주인으로 남았다.\n\n# Chapter 77. Haydée\n\n백작의 말들이 큰길 모퉁이를 채 벗어나기도 전에, 알베르는 백작을 돌아보며 한바탕 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 사실 그 웃음은 너무 커서, 오히려 억지로 꾸며 낸 부자연스러운 것임을 짐작하게 했다.\n\n\"자,\" 그가 말했다. \"샤를 9세가 성 바르톨로메오 축일의 학살이 끝난 뒤 카트린 드 메디시스에게 던졌던 질문을 그대로 백작님께 여쭙고 싶군요. '내가 내 작은 역할을 어떻게 해냈소?'\"\n\n\"무슨 말인가?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다.\n\n\"당글라르 씨 댁에 제 경쟁자를 들여놓은 일 말입니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 경쟁자?\"\n\n\"맙소사! 무슨 경쟁자라뇨? 백작님이 후원하시는 안드레아 카발칸티 씨 말이지요!\"\n\n\"아, 부디 농담은 그만두시지요, 자작. 나는 안드레아 씨를 후원하지 않습니다. 적어도 당글라르 씨에 관한 한은요.\"\n\n\"그런데 그 젊은이가 정말로 그 방면에서 백작님의 도움이 필요했다면, 도와주지 않으신 것은 비난받을 일이지요. 하지만 다행히도 제게는, 그가 도움 없이도 잘해 나가고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 자네는 그가 청혼이라도 하고 있다고 생각하나?\"\n\n\"확신합니다. 당글라르 양에게 말을 걸 때 그 흐느적거리는 눈빛과 곡조 붙인 목소리가 그 속내를 또렷이 드러내거든요. 그는 도도한 외제니의 손을 노리고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 상관인가, 정작 자네 구혼은 호의를 얻고 있다면야?\"\n\n\"하지만 그렇지가 않습니다, 백작님. 오히려 반대지요. 저는 사방에서 퇴짜를 맞고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"뭐라고!\"\n\n\"정말 그렇습니다. 외제니 양은 제게 거의 대꾸도 하지 않고, 그녀의 말벗인 다르밀리 양은 아예 제게 말을 걸지도 않습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 아버지는 자네를 더없이 좋게 여기고 있지.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"그가요? 오, 아닙니다. 그는 제 가슴에 단검을 천 자루나 꽂았는걸요. 인정하건대, 찌르기는커녕 칼끝이 제 손잡이 속으로 도로 들어가 버리는 연극용 소도구 같은 단검이긴 하지만요. 그래도 그는 그게 진짜이고 사람을 죽일 수 있다고 믿었습니다.\"\n\n\"질투는 애정의 표시일세.\"\n\n\"맞습니다. 하지만 저는 질투하지 않아요.\" \"그가 질투하는 거지.\"\n\n\"누구를? 드브레를?\"\n\n\"아니, 자네를.\"\n\n\"아니, 자네를.\"\n\"저를요? 일주일도 지나기 전에 제 앞에서 문이 닫힐 거라고 장담하지요.\"\n\n\"틀렸네, 자작.\"\n\n\"증명해 보십시오.\"\n\n\"내가 그러길 바라나?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"실은 내가 모르세르 백작에게 그 남작과 어떤 확실한 합의를 맺도록 설득해 보라는 임무를 맡았다네.\"\n\n\"누구에게서 그런 임무를 맡으셨습니까?\"\n\n\"남작 본인에게서.\"\n\n\"오,\" 알베르가 부릴 수 있는 온갖 아양을 떨며 말했다. \"백작님, 설마 그렇게 하시지는 않겠지요?\"\n\n\"당연히 할 걸세, 알베르. 그러기로 약속했으니까.\"\n\n\"그렇군요.\" 알베르가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"아무래도 백작님은 기어이 저를 결혼시키기로 작정하신 모양입니다.\"\n\n\"어쨌든 나는 모두와 좋은 사이로 지내려고 애써 보기로 작정했네.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그런데 드브레 이야기가 나왔으니 말인데, 요즘 남작 댁에서 그를 통 못 본 건 어찌 된 일인가?\"\n\n\"오해가 좀 있었거든요.\"\n\n\"뭐, 남작 부인과?\"\n\n\"아니요, 남작과요.\"\n\n\"그가 뭔가 눈치챈 건가?\"\n\n\"아, 그것참 재미있는 농담이군요!\"\n\n\"그가 의심하고 있다고 생각하나?\" 몬테크리스토가 더없이 천연덕스러운 표정으로 물었다.\n\n\"백작님, 대체 어디서 오신 분입니까?\" 알베르가 말했다.\n\n\"굳이 따지자면 콩고에서지.\"\n\n\"그보다 훨씬 더 먼 데서 오셨겠지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 내가 파리의 남편들에 대해 뭘 알겠나?\"\n\n\"오, 백작님, 남편이란 어디서나 거의 똑같습니다. 어느 나라든 남편 하나만 보면 그 종족 전체의 꽤 좋은 표본이 되지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그렇다면, 당글라르와 드브레 사이에 무슨 일로 다툼이 생긴 걸까? 둘은 서로 그렇게나 잘 통하는 것 같던데.\" 몬테크리스토가 다시금 흥미를 보이며 말했다.\n\n\"아, 이제 백작님은 제가 입문하지 못한 이시스의 비밀을 캐려 하시는군요. 안드레아 카발칸티 씨가 그 집안의 일원이 되거든, 그에게 물어보십시오.\"\n\n마차가 멈췄다.\n\n\"다 왔군.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"아직 열 시 반밖에 안 됐으니, 들어오게.\"\n\n\"물론, 그러지요.\"\n\n\"내 마차로 자네를 데려다주겠네.\"\n\n\"아니요, 괜찮습니다. 제 쿠페에게 따라오라고 일러두었거든요.\"\n\n\"저기 있군.\" 몬테크리스토가 마차에서 내리며 말했다. 두 사람은 집 안으로 들어갔다. 응접실에 불이 환히 켜져 있어, 그들은 그리로 들어갔다. \"우리에게 차를 내오게, 바티스탱.\" 백작이 말했다. 바티스탱은 대답을 기다리지도 않고 방을 나갔다가, 이 초도 안 되어 쟁반을 들고 다시 나타났다. 거기에는 주인이 명한 것이 모두 준비된 채 담겨 있었는데, 마치 동화 속에서나 읽는 그런 잔치처럼 땅에서 솟아난 듯했다.\n\n\"백작님, 정말이지,\" 모르세르가 말했다. \"제가 백작님께 감탄하는 건 그 재산이 아닙니다. 백작님보다 더 부유한 사람도 어쩌면 있을 테니까요. 그렇다고 백작님의 재치만도 아니지요. 보마르셰도 그만큼은 지녔을 테니까요. 제가 감탄하는 건 시중을 받는 방식입니다. 아무것도 묻지 않고, 순식간에, 일 초 만에 말입니다. 마치 백작님이 벨을 울리는 방식만으로 무얼 원하시는지 알아채고, 백작님이 바랄 법한 것은 무엇이든 늘 준비해 두기로 작정한 것 같단 말이지요.\"\n\n\"자네 말이 어쩌면 맞을 걸세. 그들은 내 습관을 알고 있으니까. 예를 들어, 보여 주지. 차를 마시는 동안 무엇을 하며 보내고 싶나?\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 담배나 한 대 피우고 싶군요.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 징을 들어 한 번 쳤다. 일 초쯤 지나자 안쪽 문이 열리며 알리가 나타났는데, 최상급 라타키아 담배를 채운 치부크 두 대를 들고 있었다.\n\n\"정말 놀랍군요.\" 알베르가 말했다.\n\n\"오, 아닐세. 더없이 단순한 일이지.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"알리는 내가 보통 차나 커피를 마시면서 담배를 피운다는 걸 알아. 내가 차를 내오라고 명한 것도 들었고, 자네를 집으로 데려온 것도 알지. 내가 그를 불렀으니, 그는 자연스레 그 까닭을 짐작했네. 그리고 그는 손님 대접이 특히 흡연을 통해 드러나는 나라에서 왔으니, 우리가 함께 담배를 피우리라고 자연스레 결론지어 한 대가 아니라 두 대의 치부크를 가져온 것이지. 자, 이제 수수께끼가 풀렸군.\"\n\n\"분명 백작님은 설명에 더없이 평범한 분위기를 입히시지만, 그렇다고 백작님이……아, 그런데 저게 무슨 소리지요?\" 모르세르가 문 쪽으로 고개를 기울였다. 그 문 너머에서 기타 소리 비슷한 음향이 새어 나오는 듯했다.\n\n\"이런, 자작. 자네는 오늘 저녁 음악을 들을 운명인가 보군. 당글라르 양의 피아노에서 겨우 벗어났더니, 이제 하이데의 구즐라에게 붙들리게 생겼으니 말이야.\"\n\n\"하이데, 정말 사랑스러운 이름이군요! 그렇다면 바이런의 시 말고 어딘가에 정말로 하이데라는 이름을 가진 여인들이 있단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"물론 있지. 하이데는 프랑스에서는 아주 드문 이름이지만, 알바니아와 에피루스에서는 흔하다네. 이를테면, 자네가 정숙, 겸손, 순결이라고 부르는 것과 같지. 자네 파리 사람들이 말하는 세례명 같은 것일세.\"\n\n\"오, 그거 멋지군요.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"제 동포 여인들이 선량 양, 침묵 양, 그리스도교의 자비 양으로 불리는 걸 한번 들어 보고 싶군요! 생각해 보십시오, 당글라르 양이 클레르마리외제니가 아니라 정숙겸손순결 당글라르 양이라는 이름이었다면 말입니다. 그녀의 결혼 발표에 얼마나 근사한 효과를 냈겠습니까!\"\n\n\"쉿,\" 백작이 말했다. \"그렇게 큰 소리로 농담하지 말게. 하이데가 들을지도 모르니.\"\n\n\"그러면 그녀가 화를 낼 거라고 생각하십니까?\"\n\n\"아니, 절대 그렇지 않네.\" 백작이 거만한 표정으로 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 그녀는 무척 상냥한 모양이군요, 그렇지요?\" 알베르가 말했다.\n\n\"그건 상냥함이라고 할 게 아니라, 그녀의 의무일세. 노예는 주인에게 이래라저래라 하지 않으니까.\"\n\n\"이런, 이제는 백작님이 농담을 하시는군요. 이 아름다운 이름을 가진 노예가 또 있습니까?\"\n\n\"틀림없이 있지.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 백작님은 하는 일도, 가진 것도 남들과는 다르군요. 몬테크리스토 백작의 노예라! 그건 프랑스에서는 그 자체로 하나의 지위입니다. 게다가 백작님이 돈을 뿌리는 걸 보면, 그건 연간 십만 프랑은 나갈 자리일 겁니다.\"\n\n\"십만 프랑이라! 그 가엾은 처녀는 본디 그보다 훨씬 더 많은 걸 지니고 있었네. 그녀는 보물을 타고난 몸이라, 천일야화에 기록된 보물도 그에 비하면 가난해 보일 정도였지.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 공주임이 분명하군요.\"\n\n\"맞네. 게다가 그녀는 제 나라에서 가장 지체 높은 사람들 중 하나였지.\"\n\n\"그럴 줄 알았습니다. 하지만 어쩌다 그런 위대한 공주가 노예가 되었을까요?\"\n\n\"폭군 디오니시우스가 어쩌다 교사가 되었겠나? 전쟁의 운명일세, 자작. 운명의 변덕이지. 이런 일들은 그렇게밖에 설명할 수 없다네.\"\n\n\"그녀의 이름은 비밀입니까?\"\n\n\"보통 사람들에게는 비밀이지. 하지만 자네에게는 아닐세, 자작. 자네는 내 가장 가까운 벗 중 하나이고, 내가 필요하다고 여겨 입단속을 당부하면 자네의 침묵을 믿을 수 있다고 생각하니까. 그래도 되겠지?\"\n\n\"물론입니다. 제 명예를 걸고요.\"\n\n\"야니나의 파샤 이야기를 알고 있겠지?\"\n\n\"알리 테펠레니 말입니까? 오, 그럼요. 제 아버지가 재산을 일군 것도 그를 섬기면서였습니다.\"\n\n\"맞아, 내가 그걸 잊고 있었군.\"\n\n\"그런데 하이데가 알리 테펠레니와 무슨 관계입니까?\"\n\n\"그저 그의 딸일세.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요? 알리 파샤의 딸이라고요?\"\n\n\"알리 파샤와 아름다운 바실리키의 딸이지.\"\n\n\"그런데 백작님의 노예라고요?\"\n\n\"그렇다네.\"\n\n\"하지만 어쩌다 그렇게 된 겁니까?\"\n\n\"뭐, 그저 어느 날 콘스탄티노플 시장을 지나다가 내가 그녀를 사들였다는 사정 때문이지.\"\n\n\"놀랍군요! 정말이지 백작님은 관련된 모든 일에 일종의 마법 같은 영향을 던지시는 것 같습니다. 백작님 말씀을 듣고 있노라면, 존재라는 게 더는 현실이 아니라 깨어 있는 채로 꾸는 꿈처럼 느껴집니다. 자, 이제 제가 어쩌면 경솔하고 생각 없는 부탁을 드리려는 참인데요, 하지만……\"\n\n\"말해 보게.\"\n\n\"하지만, 백작님은 하이데와 함께 외출하시고, 때로는 오페라에까지 데려가신다고 하니……\"\n\n\"그래서?\"\n\n\"감히 이 청을 드려도 될 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"내게는 무엇이든 감히 청해도 되네.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 백작님, 백작님의 공주님께 저를 소개해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"그러지. 하지만 두 가지 조건이 있네.\"\n\n\"당장 받아들이겠습니다.\"\n\n\"첫째는, 내가 그 자리를 허락했다는 걸 누구에게도 절대 말하지 않는 것일세.\"\n\n\"좋습니다.\" 알베르가 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"그러지 않겠다고 맹세합니다.\"\n\n\"둘째는, 자네 아버지가 그녀의 아버지를 섬긴 적이 있다는 걸 그녀에게 말하지 않는 것이네.\"\n\n\"그러지 않겠다고 맹세코 약속드립니다.\"\n\n\"됐네, 자작. 그 두 가지 맹세를 기억하겠지? 하지만 나는 자네가 명예를 아는 사람임을 알고 있네.\"\n\n백작이 다시 징을 쳤다. 알리가 나타났다. \"하이데에게 전하라.\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 그녀와 커피를 들겠다고, 그리고 내 벗 한 사람을 그녀에게 소개하고 싶으니 허락을 청한다고 일러 두어라.\"\n\n알리가 고개를 숙이고 방을 나갔다.\n\n\"자, 내 말을 명심하게.\" 백작이 말했다. \"직접 묻는 일은 없도록 하게, 모르세르. 무언가 알고 싶거든 내게 말하게. 그러면 내가 그녀에게 물어보겠네.\"\n\n\"알겠습니다.\"\n\n알리가 세 번째로 나타나, 문을 가린 융단 휘장을 젖혀 주인과 알베르가 안으로 들어가도 좋다는 뜻을 알렸다.\n\n\"들어가세.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n알베르는 머리카락을 손으로 쓸어 넘기고 콧수염을 매만진 다음, 제 차림새에 만족하고 나서 백작을 따라 방으로 들어갔다. 백작은 앞서 모자와 장갑을 다시 갖춘 뒤였다. 알리는 일종의 전위 보초처럼 자리를 지켰고, 문은 미르토가 지휘하는 세 명의 프랑스인 시종이 지키고 있었다.\n\n하이데는 자기 거처의 첫 번째 방, 곧 응접실에서 방문객들을 기다리고 있었다. 그녀의 커다란 눈은 놀라움과 기대로 한껏 커져 있었다. 몬테크리스토를 제외한 어떤 남자가 그녀 앞에 들어오도록 허락된 것은 이번이 처음이었기 때문이다. 그녀는 방 한쪽 모서리에 놓인 소파에 앉아, 동방식으로 두 다리를 몸 아래로 포갠 채였다. 마치 자신을 감싼 화려한 인도산 비단 속에 일종의 둥지를 틀어 놓은 듯했다. 그녀 곁에는 방금 연주하던 악기가 놓여 있었는데, 우아하게 만들어진 것이 그 주인에게 어울렸다. 몬테크리스토를 알아보자 그녀는 일어서서, 자기만의 독특한 미소로 그를 맞이했다. 더없는 순종과 더없이 깊은 사랑을 동시에 드러내는 미소였다. 몬테크리스토는 그녀에게 다가가 손을 내밀었고, 그녀는 늘 그렇듯 그 손을 들어 입술에 가져갔다.\n\n알베르는 문 앞에서 더 나아가지 못한 채, 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 서 있었다. 처음 보는, 그토록 빼어난 아름다움에 완전히 매혹된 것이었다. 더 북쪽 지방의 사람으로서는 도저히 제대로 상상할 수 없는 아름다움이었다.\n\n\"누구를 데려오셨어요?\" 처녀가 로마이크어로 몬테크리스토에게 물었다. \"벗인가요, 형제인가요, 그저 아는 사이인가요, 아니면 적인가요?\"\n\n\"벗이란다.\" 몬테크리스토가 같은 언어로 대답했다.\n\n\"이름이 무엇인가요?\"\n\n\"알베르 백작. 내가 로마에서 산적들의 손아귀에서 구해 낸 바로 그 사람이지.\"\n\n\"어떤 언어로 그와 이야기를 나누면 좋을까요?\"\n\n몬테크리스토가 알베르를 돌아보았다. \"현대 그리스어를 아나?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"아아! 모릅니다.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"고대 그리스어조차 모르지요, 백작님. 호메로스나 플라톤도 저보다 더 변변찮은 학생은 둔 적이 없을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 하이데가 말했다. 그 말로 보아 그녀는 몬테크리스토의 질문과 알베르의 대답을 완전히 알아들은 것이 분명했다. \"그렇다면 주인님이 그리 원하신다면, 저는 프랑스어나 이탈리아어로 말하겠어요.\"\n\n몬테크리스토가 잠시 생각에 잠겼다. \"이탈리아어로 말하렴.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n그러고는 알베르를 돌아보며 말했다. \"안됐네. 자네가 고대 그리스어도 현대 그리스어도 모르다니. 하이데는 둘 다 아주 유창하게 구사하거든. 가엾게도 저 아이는 자네에게 이탈리아어로 말해야 할 텐데, 그러면 자네는 그 애의 대화 솜씨에 대해 아주 잘못된 인상만 갖게 될 걸세.\"\n\n백작이 하이데에게 손님에게 말을 걸라는 신호를 보냈다. \"선생님,\" 그녀가 모르세르에게 말했다. \"제 주인이신 분의 벗으로서 진심으로 환영합니다.\" 이 말은 더없이 훌륭한 토스카나어로, 단테의 언어를 호메로스의 언어만큼이나 낭랑하게 울리게 하는 그 부드러운 로마식 억양으로 전해졌다. 그러고는 알리를 돌아보며 커피와 파이프를 가져오라고 일렀고, 그가 어린 여주인의 명을 따르러 방을 나가자 알베르에게 더 가까이 오라고 손짓했다. 몬테크리스토와 모르세르는 악보와 그림과 꽃병들이 놓인 작은 탁자 쪽으로 의자를 끌어당겼다. 이윽고 알리가 커피와 치부크를 들고 들어왔다. 바티스탱 씨로 말하자면, 건물의 이 구역은 그에게 출입이 금지되어 있었다. 알베르는 누비아인이 건네는 파이프를 사양했다.\n\n\"오, 받게나, 받아.\" 백작이 말했다. \"하이데는 거의 파리 사람만큼이나 문명화되어 있다네. 하바나 담배 냄새는 그 애에게 거슬리지만, 동방의 담배는 더없이 향긋한 향이거든. 알지 않나.\"\n\n알리가 방을 나갔다. 커피잔은 모두 준비되어 있었고, 알베르를 위해 따로 가져온 설탕도 곁들여졌다. 몬테크리스토와 하이데는 본래의 아라비아식대로, 곧 설탕 없이 그 음료를 들었다. 하이데는 가느다란 작은 손가락으로 도자기 잔을 들어 입으로 가져갔는데, 좋아하는 것을 먹거나 마시는 아이처럼 천진하고 꾸밈없는 모습이었다. 이 순간 두 여인이 들어와, 얼음과 셔벗이 가득 담긴 쟁반을 들고 와서 그 용도로 마련된 작은 탁자 두 개에 내려놓았다.\n\n\"친애하는 주인님, 그리고 부인,\" 알베르가 이탈리아어로 말했다. \"겉으로 드러나는 제 어리석음을 용서해 주십시오. 저는 완전히 얼이 빠졌는데, 그도 그럴 만합니다. 저는 지금 파리 한복판에 있습니다. 불과 조금 전만 해도 합승마차가 덜컹거리는 소리와 레모네이드 장수들의 방울이 딸랑거리는 소리를 들었는데, 지금은 마치 갑자기 동방으로 옮겨진 듯합니다. 제가 직접 본 동방이 아니라, 제 꿈이 그려 낸 동방으로 말이지요. 오, 부인, 제가 그리스어를 할 수만 있었다면, 부인의 대화에 저를 둘러싼 이 동화 같은 풍경이 더해져, 결코 잊을 수 없을 만큼 황홀한 저녁을 선사했을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"저는 선생님과 대화를 나눌 만큼은 이탈리아어를 합니다.\" 하이데가 조용히 말했다. \"그리고 동방의 것을 좋아하신다면, 여기 계시는 동안 그 취향을 채워 드리도록 최선을 다하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그녀와 무슨 주제로 이야기를 나누면 좋을까요?\" 알베르가 낮은 목소리로 몬테크리스토에게 말했다.\n\n\"무엇이든 좋을 대로 하게. 그녀의 나라나 어린 시절의 추억을 이야기해도 좋고, 더 마음에 든다면 로마나 나폴리, 피렌체 이야기를 해도 되고.\"\n\n\"오,\" 알베르가 말했다. \"그리스 여인과 함께 있으면서 파리 사람과 똑같은 식으로 이야기를 나누는 건 아무 소용이 없지요. 그녀에게 동방 이야기를 하게 해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"그러게나. 자네가 고를 수 있는 모든 주제 중에서 그게 그녀의 취향에 가장 맞을 테니.\"\n\n알베르가 하이데를 돌아보았다. \"부인, 몇 살에 그리스를 떠나셨습니까?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"겨우 다섯 살에 떠났어요.\" 하이데가 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 고향에 대한 기억이 좀 있으신지요?\"\n\n\"눈을 감고 생각하면, 그 모든 것이 다시 보이는 것 같아요. 마음은 몸만큼이나 잘 볼 수 있거든요. 몸은 때때로 잊지만, 마음은 언제나 기억하니까요.\"\n\n\"그럼 과거의 어디까지 기억이 미치십니까?\"\n\n\"제가 겨우 걸음마를 하던 때예요. 어머니는 — 왕족이라는 뜻을 지닌 바실리키라는 이름이셨지요.\" 처녀가 자랑스럽게 고개를 쳐들며 말했다. \"어머니가 제 손을 잡으시고, 우리가 가진 돈을 모두 지갑에 넣은 다음, 둘 다 베일을 쓰고 나가서 죄수들을 위해 적선을 청하셨어요. '가난한 자에게 베푸는 것은 주께 꾸어 드리는 것이니라'라고 말씀하시면서요. 그러다 지갑이 가득 차면 우리는 궁으로 돌아왔고, 아버지께는 한마디도 하지 않고 그 돈을 수도원으로 보냈어요. 거기서 죄수들에게 나누어 주었지요.\"\n\n\"그때 몇 살이셨습니까?\"\n\n\"세 살이었어요.\" 하이데가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 세 살 때부터 주변에서 일어난 모든 일을 기억하신단 말입니까?\" 알베르가 말했다.\n\n\"전부 다요.\"\n\n\"백작님,\" 알베르가 낮은 목소리로 몬테크리스토에게 말했다. \"부디 부인이 자신의 내력을 좀 들려주게 해 주십시오. 그녀 앞에서 제 아버지 이름을 입에 올리는 걸 금하셨지만, 어쩌면 이야기를 하다가 그녀 스스로 아버지를 언급할지도 모릅니다. 그토록 아름다운 입술로 우리 가문의 이름이 불리는 걸 듣는다면 제가 얼마나 기쁠지, 백작님은 모르실 겁니다.\"\n\n몬테크리스토가 하이데를 돌아보았다. 그리고 자기 말에 더없이 빈틈없이 주의를 기울이라고 명하는 표정으로, 그리스어로 말했다. \"Πατρὸς μὲν ἄτην μήζε τὸ ὄνομα προδότου καὶ προδοσίαν εἰπὲ ἡμῖν.\" 곧 \"네 아버지의 운명을 우리에게 들려다오. 다만 배신자의 이름도, 그 배신도 말하지 말아라\"라는 뜻이었다. 하이데가 깊은 한숨을 쉬었고, 그녀의 아름다운 이마에 슬픔의 그늘이 드리웠다.\n\n\"그녀에게 무슨 말씀을 하셨습니까?\" 모르세르가 나직이 물었다.\n\n\"자네가 벗이니 자네에게는 아무것도 숨길 필요가 없다고 다시 일러 주었네.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 알베르가 말했다. \"죄수들을 위한 그 경건한 순례가 부인의 첫 기억이로군요. 그다음은 무엇입니까?\"\n\n\"오, 그러고 나서 떠오르는 건 마치 어제 일처럼 또렷한데요, 호숫가에서 무화과나무 그늘 아래 앉아 있던 일이에요. 호수 물 위로는 흔들리는 잎새들이 거울처럼 비쳤지요. 그 나무들 중에서도 가장 늙고 가장 우거진 나무 아래, 쿠션에 기대어 아버지가 앉아 계셨어요. 어머니는 그 발치에 계셨고, 저는 아이답게 허리띠까지 늘어진 아버지의 길고 흰 수염을 가지고 놀거나, 허리띠에 매단 언월도의 다이아몬드 손잡이를 가지고 놀며 시간을 보냈지요. 그러다 이따금 알바니아 사람 하나가 아버지께 와서 무어라 말을 했는데, 저는 귀담아듣지 않았지만 아버지는 늘 같은 어조로 '죽여라' 아니면 '용서하라'라고 대답하셨어요.\"\n\n\"참으로 이상한 일입니다.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"무대 위 여배우가 아닌 다른 누군가의 입에서 그런 말이 나오는 걸 들으니 말입니다. 그래서 그게 사실임을 믿으려면 끊임없이 스스로에게 되뇌어야 하는군요. '이건 꾸며 낸 이야기가 아니다, 전부 현실이다'라고요. 그런데 그토록 황홀한 풍경을 보는 데 익숙한 부인의 눈에 프랑스는 어떻게 비치는지요?\"\n\n\"좋은 나라라고 생각해요.\" 하이데가 말했다. \"하지만 저는 프랑스를 있는 그대로 보지요. 한 여인의 눈으로 바라보니까요. 반면 제 고향은 어린아이의 마음에 남은 인상으로밖에 판단할 수 없으니, 늘 어렴풋한 안개에 싸여 있는 것처럼 보여요. 그에 대한 제 기억이 슬프냐 즐겁냐에 따라 그 안개는 빛나기도 하고 그렇지 않기도 하지요.\"\n\n\"그토록 어린 나이에,\" 알베르가, 노예 본인에게는 어떤 질문도 하지 말라던 백작의 분부를 그 순간 잊은 채 말했다. \"고통이라는 게 무엇인지 이름으로밖에 알지 못하실 줄 알았는데, 그렇지 않으셨다는 말씀입니까?\"\n\n하이데가 몬테크리스토 쪽으로 눈길을 돌렸다. 그는 동시에 알아챌 수 없는 신호를 보내며 나직이 말했다.\n\n\"Εἰπέ — 말하렴.\"\n\n\"우리 마음에 어린 시절의 기억만큼 단단히 새겨지는 것은 없어요. 그리고 방금 제가 들려드린 두 장면을 빼면, 가장 이른 제 기억들은 모두 더없이 깊은 슬픔으로 가득 차 있답니다.\"\n\n\"말씀하십시오, 말씀하세요, 부인.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"저는 부인이 하시는 모든 말씀을 더없이 강렬한 기쁨과 관심으로 듣고 있습니다.\"\n\n하이데는 그의 말에 우울한 미소로 답했다. \"그렇다면 지난날 제 슬픔의 내력을 들려드리길 바라시나요?\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"부디 그래 주십시오.\" 알베르가 대답했다.\n\n\"그래요, 제가 겨우 네 살이던 어느 날 밤, 저는 어머니에 의해 느닷없이 잠에서 깼어요. 우리는 야니나의 궁에 있었지요. 어머니는 제가 잠들어 있던 쿠션에서 저를 낚아채셨고, 제가 눈을 떠 보니 어머니의 눈에 눈물이 가득했어요. 어머니는 아무 말 없이 저를 데려가셨지요. 어머니가 우시는 걸 보고 저도 따라 울기 시작했어요. '쉿, 얘야!' 어머니가 말씀하셨어요. 다른 때 같으면 저는 어머니가 아무리 어르거나 으르거나 해도, 아이답게 변덕을 부리며 슬프거나 화나는 마음을 실컷 울어서 풀곤 했어요. 그런데 그날은 어머니가 조용히 하라고 이르시는 목소리에 어찌나 극심한 공포가 배어 있었는지, 어머니의 명령이 떨어지자마자 저는 울음을 그쳤어요. 어머니는 저를 안고 서둘러 데려가셨지요.\n\n그때 보니 우리는 커다란 계단을 내려가고 있었어요. 우리 주위에는 어머니의 하인들이 모두 짐궤와 자루와 장신구와 보석과 금화 지갑을 나르며, 더없이 정신없이 서둘러 달아나고 있었지요.\n\n여인들 뒤로는 긴 총과 권총으로 무장하고, 그리스인들이 다시 한 민족이 된 이래로 입게 된 복장을 한 스무 명의 호위병이 따랐어요. 짐작이 가시겠지만, 잠에서 절반밖에 깨지 못한, 아니 적어도 제게는 그렇게 보였던 — 저 자신도 채 깨지 못했으니까요 — 노예와 여인들의 그 긴 행렬에는 무언가 섬뜩하고 불길한 데가 있었어요.\" 하이데가 그 장면을 떠올리는 것만으로도 고개를 젓고 창백해지며 말했다. \"계단 벽 여기저기에는 거대한 그림자들이 비쳐, 소나무 횃불의 일렁이는 불빛 속에서 떨리며 마침내 머리 위 둥근 천장에까지 닿는 듯했어요.\n\n'서둘러라!' 회랑 끝에서 한 목소리가 외쳤어요. 이 목소리에 모두가 그 앞에 머리를 조아렸는데, 그 위력이란 마치 밀밭 위를 지나는 바람이 그 우월한 힘으로 모든 이삭을 굴복시켜 절하게 하는 것 같았지요. 저로 말하면, 그 목소리에 몸이 떨렸어요. 그것은 제 아버지의 목소리였어요. 아버지는 화려한 예복을 입고, 손에는 당신네 황제가 하사한 카빈총을 든 채 맨 마지막에 오셨어요. 총애하던 셀림의 어깨에 기대신 채, 목동이 흩어진 양 떼를 몰듯 우리 모두를 앞세워 모셨지요. 제 아버지는,\" 하이데가 고개를 쳐들며 말했다. \"유럽에 야니나의 파샤 알리 테펠레니라는 이름으로 알려진, 터키마저 그 앞에서 벌벌 떨던 그 이름난 분이셨어요.\"\n\n알베르는 영문도 모른 채, 그토록 거만하고 위엄 있는 어조로 발해진 이 말을 듣고 흠칫했다. 그에게는 이 순간 하이데의 빛나는 두 눈에서 번뜩이는 표정에 무언가 초자연적으로 음울하고 무시무시한 것이 깃든 듯이 보였다. 온 유럽이 공포에 떨며 들었던 그 사내의 끔찍한 죽음에 대한 기억을 알베르의 마음속에 불러일으키는 그녀는, 마치 망령을 불러내는 무녀와도 같았다.\n\n\"이윽고,\" 하이데가 말했다. \"우리는 가던 길에서 멈춰, 호숫가에 다다랐어요. 어머니는 두근거리는 가슴에 저를 끌어안으셨고, 몇 걸음 떨어진 곳에서 저는 불안하게 주위를 살피는 아버지를 보았지요. 대리석 계단 네 개가 물가로 내려가 있었고, 그 아래에는 물결 위로 배 한 척이 떠 있었어요.\n\n우리가 선 곳에서 저는 호수 한가운데 커다란 어둑한 덩어리가 보였어요. 그건 우리가 가려는 정자였지요. 그 정자는 제게 꽤 멀리 떨어진 듯 보였는데, 아마도 어떤 사물이든 일부밖에 분간할 수 없게 하던 밤의 어둠 탓이었을 거예요. 우리는 배에 올랐어요. 노가 물을 칠 때 아무런 소리도 나지 않던 게 똑똑히 기억나요. 까닭을 알아보려고 몸을 기울여 보니, 노에 우리 팔리카르들의 허리띠가 둘둘 감겨 있더군요. 노 젓는 이들 말고 배에는 여인들과 아버지, 어머니, 셀림, 그리고 저뿐이었어요. 팔리카르들은 우리의 퇴로를 엄호할 채비를 하고 호숫가에 남아 있었지요. 그들은 대리석 계단의 맨 아래 단에 무릎을 꿇고, 추격이 있을 경우 나머지 세 단을 방벽 삼으려는 것이었어요. 우리 배는 바람보다 빠르게 나아갔어요. '왜 배가 이렇게 빨리 가요?' 제가 어머니께 여쭈었어요.\n\n'조용히 해, 얘야! 쉿, 우리는 달아나는 중이야!' 저는 이해할 수가 없었어요. 어째서 아버지가 달아나야 하지? 모든 것을 거머쥔 분, 다른 이들이 그 앞에서 으레 달아나던 분, '저들이 나를 미워한다, 그러니 나를 두려워한다!'를 좌우명으로 삼으신 그분이 말이에요.\n\n정말로 아버지가 이루려던 것은 도주였어요. 나중에 들은 이야기로는, 야니나성의 수비대가 오랜 복무에 지쳐서……\"\n\n여기서 하이데가 몬테크리스토에게 의미심장한 눈길을 던졌다. 백작의 눈은 그녀의 이야기가 이어지는 내내 그녀의 얼굴에 못 박혀 있었다. 이윽고 처녀는, 들려주는 이야기의 어떤 대목을 지어내거나 감추는 사람처럼 천천히 말을 이었다.\n\n\"방금 말씀하시기를, 부인,\" 알베르가, 더없이 빈틈없이 이야기에 귀 기울이며 말했다. \"야니나의 수비대가 오랜 복무에 지쳐서……\"\n\n\"술탄이 제 아버지의 신병을 확보하라고 보낸 세라스키에르 쿠르시드와 협상을 맺었어요. 바로 그때 알리 테펠레니는 — 자신이 크게 신뢰하던 프랑스 장교 한 사람을 술탄에게 보낸 뒤 — 오래전부터 자신을 위해 마련해 둔 은신처로 물러나기로 결심하셨어요. 그곳을 카타피기온, 곧 피난처라고 부르셨지요.\"\n\n\"그 장교 말입니다만,\" 알베르가 물었다. \"그 이름을 기억하십니까, 부인?\"\n\n몬테크리스토가 처녀와 재빠른 눈길을 주고받았는데, 알베르는 전혀 알아채지 못했다.\n\n\"아니요,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"지금 당장은 기억나지 않아요. 하지만 곧 떠오르면 말씀드릴게요.\"\n\n알베르가 막 제 아버지의 이름을 입에 올리려는 찰나, 몬테크리스토가 나무라는 뜻으로 손가락을 가만히 들어 올렸다. 젊은이는 자기 약속을 떠올리고 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"우리가 노 저어 가던 곳이 바로 그 정자였어요. 아라베스크 무늬로 장식되고 테라스가 물에 잠겨 있는 일층과, 호수를 바라보는 또 한 층, 그게 눈에 보이는 전부였지요. 하지만 일층 아래, 섬 안쪽으로 뻗어 있는 곳에는 커다란 지하 동굴이 있었고, 어머니와 저와 여인들이 그리로 인도되었어요. 그곳에는 가죽 자루 육만 개와 통 이백 개가 함께 놓여 있었어요. 자루에는 금화로 이천오백만 냥이 들어 있었고, 통에는 화약 삼만 파운드가 가득 차 있었지요.\n\n그 통들 곁에는 제가 방금 말씀드린, 아버지가 총애하던 셀림이 서 있었어요. 그는 불붙인 화승을 매단 창을 손에 들고 밤낮으로 보초를 섰지요. 아버지의 첫 신호가 떨어지면 모든 것을 — 정자, 호위병, 여인들, 금, 그리고 알리 테펠레니 자신까지 — 폭파하라는 명을 받은 거였어요. 노예들이, 제 목숨이 얼마나 위태로운 줄에 매달려 있는지 알고서 밤낮으로 기도하고 울고 신음하며 지내던 게 똑똑히 기억나요. 저로 말하면, 그 젊은 병사의 창백한 얼굴빛과 검은 눈을 결코 잊을 수 없어요. 그리고 언젠가 죽음의 천사가 저를 저세상으로 부르더라도, 저는 분명 셀림을 알아볼 거예요. 우리가 그 상태로 얼마나 오래 머물렀는지는 말씀드릴 수 없어요. 그 시절에는 시간이라는 게 무엇인지조차 몰랐으니까요. 이따금, 아주 드물게 아버지가 어머니와 저를 궁의 테라스로 부르셨어요. 그게 제게는 즐거운 시간이었지요. 음울한 동굴 속에서는 노예들의 침울한 얼굴과 셀림의 불타는 창 말고는 아무것도 볼 수 없었으니까요. 아버지는 간절한 눈길로 지평선의 가장 먼 끝까지 꿰뚫어 보려 하셨고, 호수 위에 나타나는 검은 점 하나하나를 유심히 살피셨어요. 그동안 어머니는 그 곁에 기대어 아버지 어깨에 머리를 얹으셨고, 저는 그 발치에서 놀며 보이는 모든 것에 감탄했지요. 그 자체로는 보잘것없으나 아이의 눈에는 더없이 중대하게 비치는 것들에 매혹을 입히는, 그 꾸밈없는 어린 시절의 천진함으로 말이에요. 핀두스산의 봉우리들이 우리 위로 우뚝 솟아 있었고, 야니나성은 호수의 푸른 물에서 희고 모난 모습으로 솟아 있었어요. 멀리서 보면 바위에 들러붙은 이끼처럼 보이던 그 검은 초목의 거대한 덩어리들은, 실은 거대한 전나무와 도금양 나무였지요.\n\n어느 날 아침 아버지가 우리를 부르셨어요. 어머니는 밤새 우셨고 몹시 비참한 모습이셨지요. 파샤이신 아버지는 차분하셨지만 여느 때보다 창백하셨어요. '용기를 내거라, 바실리키.' 아버지가 말씀하셨어요. '오늘 주군의 칙서가 도착하면 내 운명이 결정될 거요. 내가 온전히 사면된다면 우리는 의기양양하게 야니나로 돌아갈 것이고, 소식이 불길하다면 우리는 오늘 밤 달아나야 하오.' — '하지만 적이 우리가 달아나도록 내버려두지 않으면요?' 어머니가 말씀하셨어요. '오, 그 점은 마음을 놓으시오.' 알리가 미소 지으며 말씀하셨어요. '셀림과 그의 불타는 창이 그 문제를 해결할 거요. 저들은 내가 죽기를 바라겠지만, 자기들이 나와 함께 죽기는 싫을 테니까.'\n\n어머니는 아버지의 진심에서 나온 게 아님을 아는 그 위안의 말에 한숨으로만 답하셨어요. 어머니는 아버지가 늘 마시던 얼음물을 준비하셨어요. 정자에 머무신 뒤로 아버지는 극심한 열로 입이 바싹 타셨거든요. 그러고는 향유를 발라 흰 수염을 매만지시고, 치부크에 불을 붙여 드렸어요. 아버지는 때때로 그것을 몇 시간씩 피우며, 나선을 그리며 피어오르다가 주위 공기 속으로 차츰 녹아드는 연기 자락을 가만히 바라보곤 하셨지요. 그러다 아버지가 어찌나 갑작스럽게 몸을 움직이셨는지, 저는 겁에 질려 옴짝달싹할 수가 없었어요. 그러더니 처음 눈길을 끈 그것에서 눈을 떼지 않으신 채 망원경을 달라고 하셨어요. 어머니가 건네드렸는데, 그러면서 기대고 있던 대리석보다도 더 하얗게 질려 보였어요. 저는 아버지의 손이 떨리는 걸 보았지요. '배다! 두 척! 세 척!' 아버지가 나직이 중얼거리셨어요. '네 척!' 그러고는 일어서서 무기를 잡고 권총에 화약을 재셨어요. '바실리키,' 아버지가 어머니께, 눈에 띄게 떨면서 말씀하셨어요. '모든 것을 결정할 순간이 다가오고 있소. 반 시간 안에 우리는 황제의 답을 알게 될 거요. 하이데를 데리고 동굴로 가시오.' — '저는 당신 곁을 떠나지 않겠어요.' 바실리키가 말씀하셨어요. '당신이 죽으면, 주인님, 저도 당신과 함께 죽겠어요.' — '셀림에게 가시오!' 아버지가 외치셨어요. '안녕히, 주인님.' 어머니가 죽음이 다가오기를 조용히 기다리기로 결심한 듯 나직이 말씀하셨어요. '바실리키를 데려가라!' 아버지가 팔리카르들에게 말씀하셨어요.\n\n저로 말하면, 그 난리통에 잊혀 있었어요. 저는 알리 테펠레니께로 달려갔지요. 아버지는 제가 그분께 두 팔을 내미는 걸 보시더니, 몸을 숙여 제 이마에 입을 맞추셨어요. 오, 그 입맞춤이 얼마나 또렷이 기억나는지요! 그건 아버지가 제게 주신 마지막 입맞춤이었고, 저는 그게 아직도 제 이마에 따뜻하게 남아 있는 것만 같아요. 내려가면서 우리는 격자창 너머로 차츰 또렷해지는 배 여러 척을 보았어요. 처음에는 검은 점들 같더니, 이제는 물결 위를 스치는 새들처럼 보이더군요. 그동안 정자에서는 아버지 발치에 팔리카르 스무 명이, 벽 모서리에 가려진 채 간절한 눈으로 배들이 다가오는 걸 지켜보며 앉아 있었어요. 그들은 자개와 은으로 상감된 긴 총으로 무장하고 있었고, 탄약통이 수없이 바닥에 흩어져 있었지요. 아버지는 시계를 보시며, 더없이 깊은 고뇌가 어린 얼굴로 이리저리 거니셨어요. 그 마지막 입맞춤 뒤 아버지 곁을 떠날 때 제 눈에 비친 광경이 바로 이런 것이었어요.\n\n어머니와 저는 동굴로 이어진 음울한 통로를 지났어요. 셀림은 여전히 제자리를 지키고 있었고, 우리가 들어서자 슬프게 미소 지었지요. 우리는 동굴 저쪽 끝에서 쿠션을 가져다가 셀림 곁에 앉았어요. 큰 위험 앞에서는 헌신적인 사람들끼리 서로 매달리는 법이지요. 그리고 저는 비록 어렸지만, 어떤 절박한 위험이 우리 머리 위에 드리워 있다는 걸 똑똑히 알았어요.\"\n\n알베르는 야니나 재상의 마지막 순간에 대한 묘사를 — 아버지에게서는 아니었다. 그는 그 일에 대해 결코 입을 열지 않았으니까. 다만 다른 사람들에게서 — 자주 들어 왔다. 그는 그의 죽음에 관한 여러 기록을 읽은 적도 있었다. 하지만 이 이야기는 처녀의 목소리와 표정을 통해 새로운 의미를 띠는 듯했고, 그녀의 가슴 저미는 어조와 얼굴에 어린 우울한 표정은 그를 동시에 매혹하고 또 전율케 했다.\n\n하이데로 말하면, 이 끔찍한 기억들이 그녀를 잠시 압도한 듯, 그녀는 말을 멈추었다. 폭풍의 거센 기세에 고개 숙인 아름다운 꽃처럼 머리를 손에 기댄 채, 허공을 응시하는 그녀의 두 눈은 그녀가 마음속으로 핀두스산의 푸른 봉우리와 야니나 호수의 푸른 물을 그리고 있음을 말해 주었다. 그것들은 마법의 거울처럼, 그녀가 그려 낸 음울한 그림을 비추는 듯했다. 몬테크리스토는 형언할 수 없는 관심과 연민의 표정으로 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"계속하렴, 얘야.\" 백작이 로마이크어로 말했다.\n\n하이데가, 몬테크리스토의 낭랑한 목소리가 자기를 꿈에서 깨우기라도 한 듯 불쑥 눈을 들었다. 그러고는 다시 이야기를 이어 갔다.\n\n\"오후 네 시쯤이었어요. 바깥은 눈부신 대낮이었지만, 우리는 동굴의 음울한 어둠에 싸여 있었지요. 그곳에는 단 하나의 외로운 불빛만이 타고 있었는데, 어둠의 하늘에 박힌 별처럼 보였어요. 그건 셀림의 불타는 창이었지요. 어머니는 그리스도교도셨고, 기도를 올리셨어요. 셀림은 이따금 거룩한 말을 되풀이했지요. '신은 위대하시다!' 그래도 어머니에게는 아직 한 가닥 희망이 있었어요. 내려오시면서 어머니는, 콘스탄티노플로 보냈던 그 프랑스 장교를, 아버지가 그토록 신뢰하던 그 사람을 알아본 것 같다고 여기셨거든요. 아버지는 프랑스 황제의 병사들은 천성이 고귀하고 너그럽다는 걸 아셨으니까요. 어머니는 계단 쪽으로 몇 걸음 다가가 귀를 기울이셨어요. '저들이 다가오고 있어.' 어머니가 말씀하셨어요. '어쩌면 우리에게 평화와 자유를 가져오는지도 몰라!'\n\n'무엇이 두려우십니까, 바실리키?' 셀림이, 한없이 부드럽지만 또한 한없이 당당한 목소리로 말했어요. '저들이 우리에게 평화를 가져오지 않으면, 우리가 저들에게 전쟁을 안기겠습니다. 저들이 생명을 가져오지 않으면, 우리가 저들에게 죽음을 안기겠습니다.' 그러고는 옛 크레타의 디오니소스를 떠올리게 하는 몸짓으로 창의 불꽃을 다시 돋우었어요. 하지만 저는 그저 어린아이였던지라, 제게는 사납고 무모해 보이기만 하던 그 두려움 없는 용기에 겁을 먹었고, 어쩌면 우리를 기다릴지도 모를 불과 화염 속의 끔찍한 죽음을 떠올리며 진저리를 치며 물러섰지요.\n\n어머니도 같은 마음이셨어요. 저는 어머니가 떠는 걸 느꼈으니까요. '엄마, 엄마,' 제가 말했어요. '우리 정말 죽는 거예요?' 그리고 제 목소리가 들리자 노예들은 울부짖음과 기도와 통곡을 더욱 거세게 쏟아 냈어요. '얘야,' 바실리키가 말씀하셨어요. '신께서 너를 지키시어, 네가 오늘 그토록 두려워하는 그 죽음을 언젠가 바라게 되는 일이 결코 없기를!' 그러고는 셀림에게 속삭이며, 주인의 명령이 무엇이냐고 물으셨어요. '주인께서 제게 단검을 보내시면, 그건 황제의 뜻이 호의적이지 않다는 뜻이고, 저는 화약에 불을 붙여야 합니다. 반대로 반지를 보내시면, 그건 황제가 그분을 사면했다는 표시이고, 저는 화승을 끄고 화약고는 손대지 않은 채 두어야 합니다.' — '셀림,' 어머니가 말씀하셨어요. '주인의 명령이 도착했을 때, 만일 보내신 게 단검이라면, 우리 둘 다 그토록 두려워하는 그 끔찍한 죽음으로 우리를 보내는 대신, 자비를 베풀어 바로 그 단검으로 우리를 죽여 주겠는가?' — '그러지요, 바실리키.' 셀림이 담담하게 대답했어요.\n\n갑자기 우리는 요란한 외침을 들었어요. 그리고 귀를 기울여 보니, 그것이 기쁨의 외침임을 알았지요. 콘스탄티노플로 보냈던 그 프랑스 장교의 이름이 우리 팔리카르들 사이에서 사방으로 울려 퍼졌어요. 그가 황제의 답을 가져왔고, 그게 호의적이라는 게 분명했지요.\"\n\n\"그 프랑스인의 이름이 기억나지 않으십니까?\" 모르세르가, 이야기하는 이의 기억을 거들어 줄 채비를 단단히 하고 말했다. 몬테크리스토가 그에게 잠자코 있으라는 신호를 보냈다.\n\n\"기억나지 않아요.\" 하이데가 말했다.\n\n\"소란이 커졌어요. 발걸음 소리가 점점 더 가까이 다가오는 게 들렸지요. 동굴로 이어진 계단을 내려오고 있었어요. 셀림이 창을 준비했어요. 이내 잿빛 어스름 속, 동굴 입구에 한 형상이 나타났지요. 그 어스름은 이 음울한 은신처로 새어 든 몇 가닥 햇빛이 반사되어 생긴 거였어요. '누구냐?' 셀림이 외쳤어요. '하지만 네가 누구든, 한 걸음도 더 다가오지 말라고 명한다.' — '황제 폐하 만세!' 그 형상이 말했어요. '폐하께서 재상 알리에게 온전한 사면을 베푸시어, 그 목숨을 살려 주실 뿐 아니라 재산과 영지까지 돌려주신다!' 어머니는 기쁨의 외침을 지르며 저를 가슴에 끌어안으셨어요. '멈추세요.' 셀림이, 어머니가 막 나가려는 걸 보고 말했어요. '아직 반지를 받지 못한 게 보이시지요.' — '그렇구나.' 어머니가 말씀하셨어요. 그러고는 무릎을 꿇으시며, 동시에 저를 하늘을 향해 들어 올리셨어요. 마치 저를 위해 신께 기도하면서, 저를 실제로 그분 앞으로 들어 올리고 싶어 하시는 것처럼요.\"\n\n그리고 두 번째로 하이데는 말을 멈추었다. 어찌나 격렬한 감정에 사로잡혔는지, 그녀의 창백한 이마에 땀이 맺혔고, 막힌 목소리는 좀처럼 말이 되어 나오지 못했다. 목과 입술이 그토록 바싹 말라 있었던 것이다.\n\n몬테크리스토가 유리잔에 얼음물을 조금 따라 그녀에게 건네며, 부드러우면서도 명령의 기색이 어린 어조로 말했다. \"용기를 내렴.\"\n\n하이데가 눈물을 닦고 말을 이었다.\n\n\"이때쯤엔 어둠에 익숙해진 우리 눈이 그 파샤의 전령을 알아보았어요. 그는 벗이었지요. 셀림도 그를 알아보았지만, 그 용감한 젊은이는 단 하나의 의무만을 인정했어요. 곧 복종이지요. '누구의 이름으로 왔는가?' 셀림이 그에게 물었어요. '우리 주군 알리 테펠레니의 이름으로 왔소.' — '알리 본인에게서 왔다면,' 셀림이 말했어요. '내게 무엇을 전하라고 맡겨졌는지 알 텐데?' — '그렇소.' 전령이 말했어요. '내가 그분의 반지를 가져왔소.' 이 말과 함께 그는 그 표를 보이려고 손을 머리 위로 들어 올렸어요. 하지만 너무 멀었고, 셀림이 서 있던 자리에서 눈앞에 내밀린 그 물건을 분간해 알아보기에는 빛이 충분하지 않았지요. '네 손에 무엇이 있는지 보이지 않는다.' 셀림이 말했어요. '그러면 가까이 오시오.' 전령이 말했어요. '아니면 원한다면 내가 그대에게 더 다가가겠소.' — '둘 다 안 된다.' 젊은 병사가 대답했어요. '내가 보고자 하는 그 물건을 저기 비치는 빛줄기 속에 놓고, 내가 살펴보는 동안 물러서 있어라.' — '그러지요.' 사절이 말했어요. 그러고는 셀림이 가리킨 자리에 약속된 그 표를 먼저 내려놓은 뒤 물러섰어요.\n\n오, 우리 가슴이 얼마나 두근거렸는지요. 거기 놓인 건 정말로 반지처럼 보였으니까요. 하지만 그게 제 아버지의 반지일까? 그게 문제였지요. 셀림은 여전히 불붙은 화승을 손에 든 채 동굴의 입구 쪽으로 걸어갔고, 동굴 어귀로 스며드는 희미한 빛에 의지해 그 표를 집어 들었어요.\n\n'됐다.' 그가 거기에 입 맞추며 말했어요. '내 주인의 반지다!' 그러고는 화승을 바닥에 던져 짓밟아 꺼 버렸지요. 전령은 기쁨의 외침을 지르며 손뼉을 쳤어요. 이 신호에 세라스키에르 쿠르시드의 병사 넷이 별안간 나타났고, 셀림은 다섯 군데를 찔린 채 쓰러졌어요. 한 사람씩 따로따로 그를 찔렀던 거예요. 그러고는 제 범죄에 도취해, 여전히 두려움에 창백한 채로, 불이 날 위험은 없는지 동굴 곳곳을 뒤졌어요. 그런 다음 금화 자루 위에서 뒹굴며 흥겨워했지요. 이 순간 어머니가 저를 두 팔에 끌어안으시고, 우리만 아는 숱한 굽이와 모퉁이를 소리 없이 서둘러 지나, 정자의 비밀 계단에 다다르셨어요. 거기는 끔찍한 소란과 혼란의 현장이었지요. 아래층 방들은 쿠르시드의 군대로, 곧 우리의 적들로 빼곡히 차 있었어요. 어머니가 막 작은 문 하나를 밀어 열려는 찰나, 우리는 파샤의 목소리가 우렁차고 위협적인 어조로 울리는 걸 들었어요. 어머니는 판자 틈새에 눈을 갖다 대셨고, 저는 운 좋게도 방 안과 그 안에서 벌어지는 일을 들여다볼 수 있는 작은 구멍을 찾아냈지요. '무엇을 원하느냐?' 아버지가, 금빛 글자가 적힌 종이를 든 몇 사람에게 말씀하셨어요. '저희가 원하는 것은,' 한 사람이 대답했어요. '전하의 뜻을 그대에게 전하는 것이오. 이 칙서가 보이오?' — '보인다.' 아버지가 말씀하셨어요. '그렇다면 읽으시오. 전하께서 그대의 목을 요구하시오.'\n\n아버지는 큰 웃음으로 답하셨는데, 그건 어떤 위협보다도 더 무시무시했어요. 그리고 그 웃음이 채 끝나기도 전에 권총 소리가 두 발 울렸지요. 아버지가 직접 쏘아, 두 사람을 죽이신 거예요. 아버지 발치에 엎드려 있던 팔리카르들이 벌떡 일어나 총을 쏘았고, 방은 불과 연기로 가득 찼어요. 같은 순간 반대편에서도 총격이 시작되어, 총알이 우리 사방의 판자를 꿰뚫었지요. 오, 그 순간 대재상이신 제 아버지가 날아드는 총탄 한가운데서 손에 언월도를 든 채, 적들의 화약으로 얼굴이 시커메진 모습으로 얼마나 고귀해 보였는지요! 그리고 그런 와중에도 아버지가 그들을 얼마나 두렵게 하고, 자기 앞에서 달아나게 만드셨는지! '셀림, 셀림!' 아버지가 외치셨어요. '불의 수호자여, 네 의무를 다하라!' — '셀림은 죽었습니다.' 땅속 깊은 데서 나오는 듯한 목소리가 대답했어요. '그리고 당신은 끝장났소, 알리!' 같은 순간 폭발음이 들렸고, 아버지가 앉아 계시던 방의 바닥이 별안간 뜯겨 산산조각이 났어요. 군대가 아래에서 쏘아 댄 거예요. 팔리카르 서넛이 온몸이 그야말로 상처로 갈가리 파헤쳐진 채 쓰러졌지요.\n\n아버지는 크게 울부짖으며, 총알이 뚫어 놓은 구멍에 손가락을 찔러 넣어 널빤지 하나를 통째로 뜯어내셨어요. 하지만 곧 그 구멍을 통해 스무 발이 더 쏟아졌고, 화염이 화산 분화구에서 솟는 불처럼 치솟아 이내 융단에 닿더니 순식간에 삼켜 버렸어요. 이 모든 끔찍한 소란과 무시무시한 외침 한가운데서, 소름 끼치도록 또렷한 두 발의 총성에 이어 그 무엇보다 가슴 미어지는 두 마디 비명이 들려와, 저는 공포로 얼어붙었어요. 그 두 발이 아버지에게 치명상을 입혔고, 그 끔찍한 비명을 지르신 분이 바로 아버지였지요. 그래도 아버지는 창문에 매달린 채 서 계셨어요. 어머니는 가서 아버지와 함께 죽으려고 문을 밀어 열려 하셨지만, 문은 안쪽에서 잠겨 있었어요. 아버지 주위에는 팔리카르들이 발작하듯 몸부림치며 쓰러져 있었고, 가벼운 상처만 입은 두셋은 창문에서 뛰어내려 달아나려 하고 있었지요. 이 위기의 순간, 바닥 전체가 별안간 무너져 내렸고, 아버지는 한쪽 무릎을 꿇으셨어요. 그와 동시에 스무 개의 손이 군도와 권총과 단검으로 무장한 채 쑥 뻗어 나왔고, 한 사람을 향해 스무 번의 일격이 일제히 가해졌어요. 그리고 아버지는 그 악마들이 일으킨 불과 연기의 소용돌이 속으로 사라지셨지요. 마치 그분 발밑에서 지옥 자체가 열린 것 같았어요. 저는 제가 바닥에 쓰러지는 걸 느꼈어요. 어머니는 정신을 잃으셨고요.\"\n\n하이데의 두 팔이 옆으로 툭 떨어졌고, 그녀는 깊은 신음을 토했다. 동시에 그녀는 백작 쪽을 바라보았다. 자신이 그의 명령에 잘 따랐는지 묻기라도 하는 듯이.\n\n몬테크리스토가 일어나 그녀에게 다가가, 그녀의 손을 잡고 로마이크어로 말했다.\n\n\"진정하렴, 얘야. 배신자들을 벌하실 신이 계심을 떠올리며 용기를 내거라.\"\n\n\"끔찍한 이야기로군요, 백작님.\" 알베르가 하이데의 창백한 얼굴빛에 질겁하며 말했다. \"그토록 잔인하고 생각 없는 부탁을 드린 제 자신이 이제 원망스럽습니다.\"\n\n\"오, 괜찮네.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. 그러고는 처녀의 머리를 토닥이며 말을 이었다. \"하이데는 무척 용감하고, 때로는 자신의 불행을 이야기하면서 오히려 위안을 얻기도 한다네.\"\n\n\"왜냐하면, 주인님,\" 하이데가 간절하게 말했다. \"제 비참함은 제게 주인님의 자비를 떠올리게 하니까요.\"\n\n알베르는 호기심 어린 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았다. 그녀가 그가 가장 알고 싶어 하는 것을 — 어떻게 백작의 노예가 되었는지를 — 아직 이야기하지 않았기 때문이다. 하이데는 두 청자의 얼굴에 똑같은 표정이 어린 것을 한눈에 알아채고 말을 이었다.\n\n\"어머니가 정신을 차렸을 때, 우리는 세라스키에르 앞에 있었어요. '죽이세요.' 어머니가 말씀하셨어요. '하지만 알리의 미망인의 명예는 지켜 주세요.' — '그건 내게 청할 일이 아니오.' 쿠르시드가 말했어요.\n\n'그럼 누구에게 청해야 하나요?' — '그대의 새 주인에게.'\n\n'그가 누구이고 어디 있나요?' — '여기 있소.'\n\n그러고서 쿠르시드는 누구보다도 제 아버지의 죽음에 크게 한몫한 자를 가리켰어요.\" 하이데가 억눌린 분노의 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 알베르가 말했다. \"부인은 그 사람의 소유가 되었던 겁니까?\"\n\n\"아니요.\" 하이데가 대답했다. \"그는 감히 우리를 데리고 있지 못했어요. 그래서 우리는 콘스탄티노플로 가던 노예 상인들에게 팔렸지요. 우리는 그리스를 가로질러, 반쯤 죽은 채로 궁성 문에 다다랐어요. 그 문은 사람들 무리에 둘러싸여 있었는데, 그들이 우리가 지나가도록 길을 터 주었어요. 그런데 갑자기 어머니가, 그들의 눈길을 끌던 어떤 것을 가까이 들여다보시더니 찢어지는 비명을 지르며 땅에 쓰러지셨어요. 그러면서 문 위에 걸린 머리 하나를 가리키셨지요. 그 아래에는 이런 말이 적혀 있었어요.\n\n'이것은 야니나의 파샤 알리 테펠레니의 머리이다.'\n\n저는 비통하게 울며 어머니를 땅에서 일으키려 했지만, 어머니는 이미 돌아가신 뒤였어요! 저는 노예 시장으로 끌려가, 어느 부유한 아르메니아인에게 팔렸어요. 그는 저를 가르치게 하고 스승들을 붙여 주었어요. 그리고 제가 열세 살이 되자 술탄 마흐무드에게 저를 팔았지요.\"\n\n\"내가 그에게서 그녀를 샀다네.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"알베르, 자네에게 말했듯이, 내 해시시 알약을 담을 상자로 만들었던 에메랄드와 짝을 이루던 또 하나의 에메랄드로 말이야.\"\n\n\"오, 주인님은 선하시고 위대하세요!\" 하이데가 백작의 손에 입 맞추며 말했다. \"그리고 저는 그런 주인님께 속해 있으니 참으로 행복해요!\"\n\n알베르는 자신이 보고 들은 모든 것에 완전히 얼이 빠진 채로 있었다.\n\n\"자, 커피를 마저 들게.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"이야기는 끝났으니까.\"\n\n# Chapter 78. We hear From Yanina\n\n발랑틴이 노아르티에 씨의 방을 나서는 프란츠의 떨리는 걸음과 흐트러진 얼굴을 보았더라면, 아무리 그녀라도 그를 가엾게 여기지 않을 수 없었을 것이다. 빌포르는 두서없는 말 몇 마디를 겨우 내뱉고는 서재로 물러갔고, 두어 시간쯤 뒤에 그곳에서 다음과 같은 편지를 받았다.\n\n“오늘 아침에 밝혀진 모든 사실에 비추어, 노아르티에 드 빌포르 씨께서는 댁의 가문과 프란츠 데피네 씨의 가문 사이에 어떤 혼인도 맺어질 수 없음을 분명히 아셨으리라 생각합니다. 데피네는, 오늘 아침에 낱낱이 드러난 모든 정황을 이미 알고 계셨던 듯한 빌포르 씨께서 이 통고를 먼저 해 주시지 않은 데 대해 놀랍고도 충격을 받았다는 점을 밝히지 않을 수 없습니다.”\n\n이 순간 검사를 본 사람이라면, 최근의 불길한 사정들이 한꺼번에 겹쳐 완전히 넋이 나간 그의 모습을 보고서, 그가 이런 곤욕을 미리 내다보고 있었다고는 한순간도 생각하지 못했을 것이다. 물론 그의 아버지가 솔직함을, 아니 차라리 무례함을 그토록 멀리까지 밀고 나가 그런 이야기를 다 털어놓으리라고는 빌포르도 전혀 예상하지 못했다. 그리고 빌포르를 위해 한마디 해 두자면, 무슨 일에서든 아들의 의견 따위는 안중에 없던 노아르티에 씨가 그 사건을 빌포르에게 한 번도 설명해 주지 않았다는 점을 알아 둘 필요가 있다. 그래서 빌포르는 평생, 케넬 장군이, 또는 말하는 사람이 그를 본래의 성으로 부르려 하느냐 아니면 그에게 주어진 작위로 부르려 하느냐에 따라 데피네 남작이라고도 불리던 그 인물이, 정정당당한 결투에서 쓰러진 것이 아니라 암살의 희생자가 된 것이라 믿어 왔다. 평소 그토록 정중하고 깍듯한 사람한테서 날아온 이 가혹한 편지는 빌포르의 자존심에 치명적인 일격을 가했다.\n\n그가 편지를 채 다 읽기도 전에 그의 아내가 들어왔다. 노아르티에 씨의 부름을 받고 나간 프란츠가 갑자기 사라져 버린 데에 모두가 어찌나 놀랐던지, 공증인과 증인들과 함께 홀로 남겨진 빌포르 부인의 처지는 시간이 갈수록 점점 더 거북해졌다. 더는 견딜 수 없다고 마음먹은 그녀는 자리에서 일어나 방을 나갔다. 그가 갑작스레 자취를 감춘 까닭을 알아보러 가겠다고 말하면서.\n\n이 일에 관한 빌포르 씨의 설명은 지극히 짧고 간략했다. 그는 사실, 노아르티에 씨와 데피네 씨와 자신 사이에 해명이 오갔으며, 그 결과 발랑틴과 프란츠의 혼인은 깨질 것이라고만 말했다. 이는 기다리고 있는 사람들에게 전하기에 난처하고 불쾌한 소식이었다. 그래서 그녀는, 논의가 시작될 무렵 노아르티에 씨가 일종의 졸중 발작을 일으켜 그 일이 어쩔 수 없이 며칠 더 미루어지게 되었다고만 말하는 데 그쳤다. 거짓이긴 했으나 최근 잇따라 일어난 두 차례의 비슷한 불행의 뒤를 묘하게 잇는 이 소식에 듣는 이들은 분명 놀란 기색이었고, 그들은 한마디 말도 없이 물러갔다.\n\n그동안 발랑틴은 겁에 질리는 동시에 행복한 마음으로, 그녀가 끊을 수 없다고만 여겨 온 그 사슬을 단 한 번에 끊어 준 그 가냘픈 노인을 끌어안고 감사를 표한 뒤, 마음을 가라앉히게 제 방으로 물러가도 좋겠느냐고 청했다. 노아르티에는 그녀가 청한 허락을 눈빛으로 보내 주었다. 그러나 일단 자유를 얻은 발랑틴은 제 방으로 가는 대신 회랑으로 들어섰고, 그 끝에 있는 작은 문을 열자 곧장 정원에 다다랐다.\n\n꼬리에 꼬리를 물고 밀어닥친 온갖 기이한 사건들 한가운데서, 형언할 수 없는 두려움이 발랑틴의 마음을 사로잡고 있었다. 그녀는 순간순간, 마치 「래머무어의 신부」 속 레이븐스우드의 영주처럼, 모렐이 창백하게 떨며 나타나 혼인 계약서에 서명하지 못하게 막는 장면을 보게 될 것만 같았다.\n\n그녀가 쪽문에 모습을 드러낼 때가 마침맞았다. 막시밀리앙이 오래전부터 그녀를 기다리고 있었기 때문이다. 그는 프란츠가 빌포르 씨와 함께 묘지를 떠나는 것을 보았을 때 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 절반쯤 짐작했다. 그는 데피네 씨를 따라가, 그가 들어가는 것을, 이어서 나오는 것을, 그러고는 알베르, 샤토르노와 함께 다시 들어가는 것을 보았다. 그 회담의 성격에 대해 더는 의심할 여지가 없었다. 그래서 그는 토끼풀밭의 쪽문으로 서둘러 가, 일이 어떻게 되었는지 들을 채비를 하고서, 발랑틴이 풀려나는 순간 곧장 자기에게 달려오리라 확신했다. 그의 짐작은 틀리지 않았다. 나무 칸막이의 틈새로 내다보던 그는 곧 그 처녀를 발견했는데, 그녀는 평소의 온갖 조심성을 다 내던지고 곧장 울타리로 걸어왔다. 막시밀리앙이 그녀에게 던진 첫 눈길에 그는 완전히 안심했고, 그녀가 입을 열어 처음 내뱉은 말에 그의 가슴은 기쁨으로 뛰었다.\n\n\"우린 살았어요!\" 발랑틴이 말했다.\n\n\"살았다고요?\" 모렐이 그토록 벅찬 행복을 도무지 믿을 수 없다는 듯 되물었다. \"누구 덕분에요?\"\n\n\"제 할아버지 덕분이에요. 아, 모렐, 우리에게 베풀어 주신 그 모든 은혜를 생각해서 부디 그분을 사랑해 주세요!\"\n\n모렐은 온 영혼을 다해 그분을 사랑하겠노라 맹세했다. 그리고 그 순간 그는 그 맹세를 거리낌 없이 할 수 있었다. 단지 친구로서, 아니 아버지로서 사랑하는 것만으로는 부족하게 느껴져, 그를 신처럼 우러러 받들었기 때문이다.\n\n\"하지만 말해 줘요, 발랑틴, 어떻게 이 모든 일이 이루어진 거죠? 이 복된 결말을 끌어내려고 그분이 어떤 기이한 방법을 쓰신 거예요?\"\n\n발랑틴은 그동안 있었던 일을 다 이야기하려다, 그러자면 할아버지뿐 아니라 다른 사람들과도 얽힌 끔찍한 비밀을 드러내야 한다는 데에 문득 생각이 미쳤고, 그래서 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"언젠가 다 이야기해 줄게요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그게 언제죠?\"\n\n\"제가 당신의 아내가 되었을 때요.\"\n\n이제 대화는 모렐에게 더없이 흐뭇한 화제로 옮겨 갔고, 그는 발랑틴이 어떤 제안을 하든 다 받아들일 마음이 되었다. 게다가 방금 들은 정도의 소식이라면 하루치 만족으로는 차고도 넘친다는 생각이 들었다. 그래도 그는 다음 날 밤 다시 발랑틴을 만나겠다는 약속을 받지 않고는 자리를 뜨려 하지 않았다. 발랑틴은 모렐이 요구하는 것을 다 약속해 주었다. 그리고 한 시간 전만 해도 자신이 프란츠와 결혼하지 않으리라고 스스로를 안심시키는 일이 무척 어려웠던 데 비해, 막시밀리앙과 결혼하게 되리라 믿는 일은 분명 이제 훨씬 덜 어려웠다.\n\n방금 이야기한 그 만남이 이루어지는 동안, 빌포르 부인은 노아르티에 씨를 찾아가 있었다. 노인은 늘 그녀를 맞을 때 짓던 그 엄격하고 쌀쌀맞은 표정으로 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"어르신,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"발랑틴의 혼인이 깨졌다는 말씀을 새삼 드릴 것도 없겠지요. 그 일이 매듭지어진 게 바로 이곳이니까요.\"\n\n노아르티에의 얼굴은 미동도 없었다.\n\n\"하지만 한 가지는 말씀드릴 수 있습니다. 어르신께서는 모르고 계시리라 생각되는 일이지요. 저는 늘 이 혼인에 반대해 왔고, 그 계약은 전적으로 제 동의나 찬성 없이 맺어진 것이었다는 점입니다.\"\n\n노아르티에는 설명을 바라는 사람의 눈빛으로 며느리를 바라보았다.\n\n\"어르신께서 그토록 싫어하셨던 이 혼인이 이제 깨졌으니, 저는 빌포르 씨도 발랑틴도 차마 떳떳이 맡을 수 없는 한 가지 부탁을 들고 어르신을 찾아왔습니다.\"\n\n노아르티에의 눈은 그녀의 용건이 무엇이냐고 묻고 있었다.\n\n\"제가 어르신께 간청드리러 온 것은,\" 빌포르 부인이 말을 이었다. \"오직 저만이 그럴 자격이 있기 때문입니다. 이 일에서 아무런 개인적 이익도 얻지 않을 사람은 저뿐이니까요. 어르신, 부디 손녀에게 사랑을, 그건 그 아이가 늘 받아 온 것이니 그게 아니라, 어르신의 재산을 돌려주시기를 간청드립니다.\"\n\n노아르티에의 눈에는 미심쩍어하는 빛이 어렸다. 그는 이 행동의 속내를 알아내려 애쓰는 게 분명했으나, 끝내 알아낼 수 없었다.\n\n\"어르신,\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"어르신의 뜻이 제 부탁과 같으리라고 기대해도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n노아르티에는 그렇다는 신호를 보냈다.\n\n\"그렇다면 어르신,\" 빌포르 부인이 말을 받았다. \"제 바람을 이토록 선뜻 받아들여 주신 데에 감사와 기쁨이 벅차오르는 마음으로 물러가겠습니다.\" 그러고는 노아르티에 씨에게 절을 올린 뒤 물러갔다.\n\n이튿날 노아르티에 씨는 공증인을 불렀다. 첫 번째 유언장은 찢겨 나가고 두 번째 유언장이 작성되었는데, 거기서 그는 자기 재산 전부를 발랑틴에게 남기되 그 아이가 결코 자기 곁을 떠나지 않는다는 조건을 달았다. 그러자 곧 사람들 사이에 소문이 돌았다. 생메랑 후작과 후작 부인의 상속인인 빌포르 양이 할아버지의 총애를 되찾았으며, 머지않아 30만 리브르의 수입을 손에 쥐게 되리라는 소문이었다.\n\n혼인 계약의 파기와 관련된 모든 일이 빌포르 씨 댁에서 진행되는 동안, 몬테크리스토는 모르세르 백작을 찾아가 있었다. 백작은 당글라르의 뜻에 한시도 지체 없이 응하는 한편 자신의 사회적 지위에 걸맞은 예를 다하려고, 중장 군복을 차려입고 거기에 받은 훈장을 모조리 단 차림으로, 가장 좋은 말들을 대령시켜 쇼세 당탱 거리로 향했다.\n\n당글라르는 한 달치 장부를 결산하고 있었으니, 그가 더없이 좋은 기분에 잠겨 있을 만한 때는 아마 아니었다. 옛 친구를 처음 본 순간 당글라르는 거드름 피우는 태도를 취하며 안락의자에 깊숙이 몸을 묻었다.\n\n평소 그토록 뻣뻣하고 격식 차리던 모르세르가 살갑고 웃음 띤 태도로 은행가에게 말을 건넸다. 그리고 자기가 꺼내려는 이야기가 좋게 받아들여지리라 확신했기에, 목적을 이루려고 무슨 술수를 부릴 필요도 없다고 여겨 곧장 본론으로 들어갔다.\n\n\"자, 남작,\" 그가 말했다. \"드디어 내가 왔소. 우리가 계획을 세운 지도 꽤 시간이 흘렀건만, 아직 실행에 옮기지 못했구려.\"\n\n모르세르는 이 말을 끝으로 잠시 멈추고, 당글라르의 이마에 드리운 구름이 걷히기를 조용히 기다렸다. 그는 그 구름이 자기가 그동안 잠자코 있었던 탓이려니 여겼다. 그러나 뜻밖에도, 그를 몹시 놀라게도, 그 구름은 점점 더 짙어져 갔다.\n\n\"무슨 말씀이신지요?\" 당글라르가 말했다. 마치 장군의 말이 무슨 뜻인지 도무지 헤아릴 수 없다는 듯이.\n\n\"아,\" 모르세르가 말했다. \"이런, 격식을 따지시는구려. 의례적인 절차를 빠뜨려선 안 된다고 일깨우시려는 게로군요. 이런, 미안하게 됐소. 한데 아들이 하나뿐인 데다 그 아이 혼인을 생각해 보는 것도 처음이라, 아직 견습 중이라 그렇소이다. 자, 고치리다.\"\n\n그러고는 모르세르가 억지웃음을 지으며 자리에서 일어나, 당글라르 씨에게 정중히 절하며 말했다.\n\n\"남작, 내 아들 알베르 드 모르세르 자작을 위해 외제니 당글라르 양의 손을 청하는 영광을 누리고자 하오.\"\n\n그러나 당글라르는 모르세르가 기대한 대로 이 청을 호의적으로 받아들이기는커녕 이맛살을 찌푸렸고, 여태 서 있는 백작에게 자리를 권하지도 않은 채 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"백작, 답을 드리기 전에 좀 생각해 봐야겠습니다.\"\n\n\"생각을 한다고?\" 모르세르가 점점 더 놀라며 말했다. \"이 혼담이 우리 사이에서 처음 거론된 지 여덟 해가 지났는데, 그동안 생각할 시간이 모자랐단 말이오?\"\n\n\"백작,\" 은행가가 말했다. \"세상에는 우리가 가장 굳게 세워 둔 견해마저 내려놓게 만드는, 혹은 적어도 정황의 변화에 따라 그 견해를 다시 빚게 만드는 일들이 끊임없이 일어나는 법입니다. 그 정황이라는 것이, 처음 우리가 보던 것과는 사뭇 다른 빛 아래 일을 옮겨 놓을 수도 있고 말입니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀인지 모르겠소, 남작.\" 모르세르가 말했다.\n\n\"제 말씀은 이렇습니다. 지난 보름 사이에 예기치 못한 일들이 생겼다는 것이지요.\"\n\n\"실례지만,\" 모르세르가 말했다. \"우리가 지금 연극이라도 하고 있는 거요?\"\n\n\"연극이라뇨?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 꼭 연극 같으니 말이오. 자, 좀 더 본론으로 들어가서 서로 속을 터놓읍시다.\"\n\n\"저도 바로 그러길 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"몬테크리스토 씨를 만난 적이 있으시지요?\"\n\n\"아주 자주 봅니다.\" 당글라르가 몸을 곧추세우며 말했다. \"각별한 벗이지요.\"\n\n\"한데 그와 최근에 나눈 대화 가운데서, 내가 이 혼사에 대해 잊은 듯, 마음을 정하지 못한 듯 보인다고 말씀하셨다지요?\"\n\n\"그렇게 말했습니다.\"\n\n\"자, 그래서 내가 이렇게 와서, 그 점에 대해 한 약속을 지켜 달라 청함으로써, 내가 그 둘 중 어느 쪽도 아님을 단번에 증명해 보이려는 거요.\"\n\n당글라르는 대답하지 않았다.\n\n\"이렇게 빨리 마음이 바뀌신 거요,\" 모르세르가 덧붙였다. \"아니면 그저 내가 굽히는 꼴을 보는 재미로 이런 청을 하게끔 부추기신 거요?\"\n\n당글라르는, 처음 시작한 그 어조로 계속 대화를 끌고 가다가는 자칫 모든 게 자기에게 불리하게 돌아갈 수 있겠다는 것을 깨닫고, 모르세르를 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n\"백작, 제가 이렇게 말을 아끼는 데에 분명 놀라셨을 겁니다. 백작께 이런 식으로 대하는 것이 제게도 무척 괴로운 일임을 분명히 말씀드립니다. 그러나 어쩔 수 없는 사정이 제게 이 괴로운 일을 떠맡겼다는 제 말을 믿어 주십시오.\"\n\n\"그건 다 빈말일 뿐이오.\" 모르세르가 말했다. \"새로 알게 된 사람이라면 그런 말에 만족할지 모르나, 모르세르 백작은 그런 부류에 들지 않소. 그리고 이런 사람이 저런 사람을 찾아가, 그가 맺은 언약을 상기시켰는데도 그 사람이 약조를 지키지 않는다면, 적어도 그렇게 하는 까닭이라도 떳떳이 들을 권리는 있는 법이오.\"\n\n당글라르는 겁쟁이였으나 그렇게 비치고 싶지는 않았다. 방금 모르세르가 취한 어조에 그는 발끈했다.\n\n\"제 행동에 그럴 만한 까닭이 없는 게 아닙니다.\" 은행가가 대답했다.\n\n\"무슨 말씀이시오?\"\n\n\"제게 그럴 만한 까닭이 있긴 하나, 설명하기가 어렵다는 말씀입니다.\"\n\n\"적어도 이것만은 아셔야 하오. 그 동기가 내게 설명되기 전에는 내가 그걸 헤아릴 길이 없다는 것 말이오. 그러나 한 가지만은 분명하구려. 당신이 내 가문과 인척을 맺기를 거절한다는 것 말이오.\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 백작.\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"그저 결정을 미루는 것뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 내가 당신의 온갖 변덕에 굴복해서, 다시 당신의 총애를 받게 될 날을 잠자코 머리를 조아리며 기다릴 거라고 정말로 우쭐대시는 거요?\"\n\n\"그럼 백작, 기다리지 않으시겠다면, 이 혼담은 아예 없던 일로 여길 수밖에요.\"\n\n백작은 거의 피가 솟구칠 만큼 입술을 깨물어, 거만하고 발끈하기 쉬운 그의 성미로는 좀처럼 누르기 어려운 분노가 끓어 넘치는 것을 가까스로 참았다. 그러나 지금 같은 상황에서는 웃음거리가 될 쪽이 분명 자기라는 것을 깨닫고, 그는 발걸음을 옮기던 문 쪽에서 돌아서서 다시 은행가와 마주 섰다. 방금까지 그의 이마를 채우던 모욕당한 자존심의 표정 대신, 이제는 분명한 불안과 초조함을 드러내는 구름이 거기 내려앉았다.\n\n\"이보시오, 당글라르,\" 모르세르가 말했다. \"우리가 알고 지낸 지도 여러 해이니, 마땅히 서로의 허물쯤은 좀 너그러이 봐줘야 하지 않겠소. 당신은 내게 해명할 의무가 있고, 어떤 사정이 생겨 내 아들이 당신의 호의를 잃게 되었는지 내가 아는 것이 도리에 맞는 일이오.\"\n\n\"자작에게 무슨 개인적인 악감정이 있어서가 아닙니다. 제가 드릴 수 있는 말은 그것뿐입니다.\" 당글라르가 대답했다. 그는 모르세르가 조금 누그러져 가라앉았음을 알아채자마자 다시 거만한 태도로 돌아갔다.\n\n\"그렇다면 대체 누구에게 그런 개인적인 악감정을 품고 있단 말이오?\" 모르세르가 분노로 창백해지며 말했다. 백작의 얼굴 표정이 은행가의 눈을 피해 가지 못했다. 그는 전보다 한층 자신만만한 눈길을 그에게 못박으며 말했다.\n\n\"제가 더 자세히 캐고 들지 않는 편이 백작께도 아마 더 흡족하실 겁니다.\"\n\n억눌린 분노에 백작의 온몸이 부르르 떨렸다. 그는 안간힘을 써 자신을 다스리며 말했다. \"내겐 당신이 해명하도록 요구할 권리가 있소. 모르세르 부인이 당신의 비위를 거슬렀단 말이오? 내 재산이 당신 보기에 변변치 않다는 거요? 아니면 내 생각이 당신과 다르다는 거요?\"\n\n\"그런 것은 전혀 아닙니다.\" 당글라르가 대답했다. \"만약 그랬다면 잘못은 오직 제게 있겠지요. 약조를 맺을 당시에 그 모든 것을 이미 알고 있었으니까요. 아닙니다, 그 까닭을 더 캐려 하지 마십시오. 백작께서 이토록 혹독하게 스스로를 돌아보시게 한 장본인이 되었다는 게 정말이지 부끄럽습니다. 이 이야기는 그만 접고, 절교도 약혼도 아닌 중간의 길, 곧 미루는 쪽을 택하기로 하지요. 정말이지, 서두를 것 없습니다. 제 딸은 이제 겨우 열일곱이고, 백작의 아드님은 스물하나입니다. 우리가 기다리는 동안 세월은 흘러갈 것이고, 사건이 사건을 잇따라 부를 것입니다. 저녁에는 어둡고 흐릿해 보이던 일들이 아침 햇살 아래서는 더없이 또렷이 드러나기도 하고, 때로는 말 한마디나 단 하루의 흐름이 가장 잔인한 중상을 밝혀내기도 하는 법이니까요.\"\n\n\"중상이라 하셨소?\" 모르세르가 분노로 새파랗게 질려 외쳤다. \"누가 감히 나를 모함한단 말이오?\"\n\n\"백작, 저는 모든 해명을 피하는 편이 낫다고 말씀드렸습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 나더러 당신의 거절을 잠자코 받아들이라는 거요?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 백작. 다만 그 거절이 백작께 받아들이시기 괴로운 만큼이나 제게도 드리기 괴로운 일임을 분명히 말씀드립니다. 저는 백작과 인척을 맺는 영광을 기대하고 있었으니까요. 게다가 혼인 계약이 깨지면 늘 손해를 보는 쪽은 남자보다 여자입니다.\"\n\n\"됐소이다.\" 모르세르가 말했다. \"이 일에 대해서는 더 말하지 맙시다.\"\n\n그러고는 분에 못 이겨 장갑을 움켜쥐고 방을 나섰다. 당글라르는, 대화가 오가는 내내 모르세르가 자신을 두고 약속을 거둔 것이냐고 단 한 번도 감히 묻지 못했다는 사실을 눈여겨보았다.\n\n그날 저녁 그는 여러 벗과 오랫동안 회담을 나누었다. 그리고 응접실에 부인들과 함께 남아 있던 카발칸티 씨가 은행가의 집을 마지막으로 떠났다.\n\n이튿날 아침 잠에서 깨자마자 당글라르는 신문을 가져오라고 했다. 신문이 그에게 들어왔다. 그는 서너 부를 옆으로 밀쳐 두고, 마침내 보샹이 편집장으로 있는 「랭파르시알」을 집어 들었다. 그는 황급히 띠지를 뜯고 신경질적인 조급함으로 신문을 펼치더니, 파리 단신 난을 경멸하듯 훑어 넘기고, 잡보 면에 이르러 심술궂은 미소를 띠며 다음과 같은 제목이 붙은 기사 앞에 멈춰 섰다.\n\n야니나로부터의 소식.\n\n\"아주 좋아.\" 기사를 다 읽고 나서 당글라르가 중얼거렸다. \"여기 페르낭 대령에 관한 짤막한 기사가 있군. 내가 잘못 본 게 아니라면, 모르세르 백작이 내게 요구하던 해명 따위를 완전히 쓸데없는 것으로 만들어 줄 기사야.\"\n\n바로 그 순간, 그러니까 아침 아홉 시에, 검은 코트를 턱밑까지 단추로 채워 입은 알베르 드 모르세르가 빠르고 흔들리는 걸음으로 샹젤리제에 있는 몬테크리스토의 저택 쪽으로 걸어가고 있는 모습이 보였을 것이다. 그가 대문 앞에 모습을 드러내자 문지기는 백작이 삼십 분쯤 전에 외출했다고 알려 주었다.\n\n\"바티스탱도 데리고 갔소?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 자작님.\"\n\n\"그럼 그를 불러 주시오. 그에게 할 말이 있소.\"\n\n문지기는 시종을 부르러 가더니 곧 그와 함께 돌아왔다.\n\n\"이보게,\" 알베르가 말했다. \"불쑥 찾아와 미안하네만, 자네 주인이 정말 외출하셨는지 자네 입으로 직접 듣고 싶어서 말일세.\"\n\n\"정말 외출하셨습니다, 자작님.\" 바티스탱이 대답했다.\n\n\"나한테까지 안 계신다는 건가?\"\n\n\"제 주인께서 자작님을 맞으실 때면 늘 얼마나 기뻐하시는지 잘 압니다.\" 바티스탱이 말했다. \"그러니 어찌 자작님을 흔한 부재중 명령에 넣을 생각이나 하겠습니까.\"\n\n\"옳은 말이네. 그런데 지금 아주 중요한 일로 그분을 뵈어야 하네. 곧 들어오실 것 같은가?\"\n\n\"네, 그러실 겁니다. 열 시에 아침 식사를 들이라 분부하셨으니까요.\"\n\n\"그럼 나는 샹젤리제를 좀 거닐다가 열 시에 다시 이리로 오겠네. 그동안 백작께서 들어오시거든, 나를 보지 않고는 다시 나가지 마시라고 청해 주겠나?\"\n\n\"믿고 맡겨 주십시오, 자작님.\" 바티스탱이 말했다.\n\n알베르는 타고 온 마차를 백작의 문 앞에 세워 둔 채 걸어서 한 바퀴 돌 작정이었다. 그는 뵈브 가로수길을 지나다, 백작의 말들이 고세 사격장 앞에 서 있는 것을 본 것 같았다. 다가가 보니 곧 마부를 알아볼 수 있었다.\n\n\"백작께서 사격장에서 사격을 하고 계신가?\" 모르세르가 물었다.\n\n\"네, 자작님.\" 마부가 대답했다. 그가 말하는 동안 알베르는 두세 발의 권총 소리를 들었다. 안으로 들어선 그는 가는 길에 사환과 마주쳤다.\n\n\"실례합니다, 자작님.\" 사환이 말했다. \"잠시 기다려 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"무엇 때문에 그러나, 필리프?\" 늘 드나들던 단골이라 자기를 막아서는 까닭을 알 수 없어 알베르가 물었다.\n\n\"지금 사격장에 계신 분이 혼자 있기를 원하셔서, 누구 앞에서도 절대 연습을 하지 않으시거든요.\"\n\n\"자네 앞에서도 말인가, 필리프? 그럼 누가 권총에 탄을 재나?\"\n\n\"그분의 하인입니다.\"\n\n\"누비아 사람인가?\"\n\n\"흑인입니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 그분이로군.\"\n\n\"이 신사분을 아십니까?\"\n\n\"알지. 바로 그분을 찾아온 걸세. 내 벗이라네.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇다면 사정이 다르지요. 곧 가서 자작님이 오셨다고 알려 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n그러고는 호기심에 못 이긴 필리프가 사격장 안으로 들어갔다. 잠시 뒤 몬테크리스토가 문턱에 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n\"용서하시오, 백작.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"이렇게 여기까지 백작을 쫓아온 것을 말이오. 우선 이게 댁 하인들의 잘못이 아님을 밝혀 두어야겠소. 그 무례는 오로지 내 탓이오. 댁에 갔더니 외출하셨다며, 열 시에 아침 식사를 하러 돌아오실 거라더군요. 열 시까지 시간을 죽이려고 거닐다가 백작의 마차와 말들이 눈에 띈 거요.\"\n\n\"방금 하신 말씀을 들으니, 나와 함께 아침을 들 생각이 있으신가 보군요.\"\n\n\"아니, 고맙소만, 지금은 아침 식사 말고 다른 일을 생각하고 있소. 어쩌면 그 식사는 더 늦은 시각에, 더 변변찮은 동석자와 함께하게 될지도 모르겠소.\"\n\n\"대체 무슨 말씀을 하시는 거요?\"\n\n\"오늘 결투를 하게 됐소.\"\n\n\"무엇 때문에요?\"\n\n\"결투를 하기 위해서지!\"\n\n\"그건 알겠소만, 무슨 다툼이오? 사람들은 온갖 이유로 결투를 하니 말이오.\"\n\n\"명예를 위해 싸우는 거요.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇다면 심각한 일이군요.\"\n\n\"어찌나 심각한지, 백작께 한 가지 도움을 청하러 왔소.\"\n\n\"무엇이오?\"\n\n\"내 입회인이 되어 주는 거요.\"\n\n\"그건 중대한 문제니, 여기서 의논할 일은 아니오. 집에 갈 때까지 아무 말도 맙시다. 알리, 물 좀 가져오게.\"\n\n백작은 소매를 걷어 올리고, 사격을 마친 신사들이 손을 씻곤 하는 작은 현관방으로 들어갔다.\n\n\"이리 와 보십시오, 자작님.\" 필리프가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"재미난 것을 보여 드리겠습니다.\" 모르세르가 들어가 보니, 늘 있던 과녁 대신 벽에 트럼프 카드들이 붙어 있었다. 멀찍이서 알베르는 그것이 한 벌 전부인 줄 알았다. 에이스에서 십까지를 세었기 때문이다.\n\n\"아하,\" 알베르가 말했다. \"카드놀이라도 채비하고 계셨던 모양이군요.\"\n\n\"아니오,\" 백작이 말했다. \"한 벌을 만들고 있었소.\"\n\n\"어떻게요?\" 알베르가 물었다.\n\n\"보시는 저것들은 사실 에이스와 둘이었는데, 내 사격으로 그게 셋, 다섯, 일곱, 여덟, 아홉, 십이 된 거요.\"\n\n알베르가 다가갔다. 정말로 총알들은, 그려진 무늬가 있어야 할 바로 그 자리를 정확히 꿰뚫어 놓았고, 선과 간격은 마치 연필과 자로 그은 듯 한 치의 어김 없이 지켜져 있었다. 과녁 쪽으로 올라가던 모르세르는 백작의 권총 사정거리 안에 겁 없이 날아들었다가 떨어진 제비 두세 마리를 주워 들었다.\n\n\"이런!\" 모르세르가 말했다.\n\n\"어쩌겠소, 자작?\" 몬테크리스토가 알리가 가져다준 수건에 손을 닦으며 말했다. \"한가한 시간을 이런저런 방식으로라도 채워야 하지 않겠소. 자, 가십시다. 기다리고 있겠소.\"\n\n두 사람은 몬테크리스토의 마차에 올랐고, 마차는 몇 분 만에 그들을 30번지에 무사히 내려놓았다. 몬테크리스토는 알베르를 서재로 데리고 들어가, 한 자리를 가리키고는 자기 자리도 따로 끌어다 놓았다. \"자, 이제 차분히 이 일을 따져 봅시다.\" 백작이 말했다.\n\n\"보시다시피 나는 아주 침착하오.\" 알베르가 말했다.\n\n\"누구와 싸우려는 거요?\"\n\n\"보샹과요.\"\n\n\"당신 친구 아니오!\"\n\n\"물론이지. 결투란 늘 친구와 하는 법이니까.\"\n\n\"무슨 다툼의 빌미가 있을 테지요?\"\n\n\"있소.\"\n\n\"그가 당신에게 무슨 짓을 했소?\"\n\n\"어젯밤 그의 신문에 이런 게 실렸소. 자, 직접 읽어 보시오.\" 그러고는 알베르가 신문을 백작에게 건넸고, 백작은 다음과 같이 읽었다.\n\n\"야니나 주재 통신원이 지금껏 우리가 모르고 있던 한 가지 사실을 알려 왔다. 그 도시를 지켜 주던 성채는 페르낭이라는 이름의 한 프랑스 장교가 튀르크 측에 넘겨주었는데, 대재상 알리 테펠리니는 그에게 더없는 신임을 두고 있었다.\"\n\n\"그래서,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"여기서 무엇이 당신을 불쾌하게 한다는 거요?\"\n\n\"여기서 무엇이냐고요?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 야니나의 성채를 한 프랑스 장교가 넘겨준 것이 당신과 무슨 상관이오?\"\n\n\"내 아버지, 모르세르 백작과 상관이 있소. 그분의 세례명이 페르낭이거든!\"\n\n\"당신 부친이 알리 파샤 휘하에서 복무하셨소?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 그러니까, 그리스의 독립을 위해 싸우셨고, 바로 거기서 이 중상이 비롯된 거요.\"\n\n\"오, 자작, 제발 사리에 맞게 말합시다!\"\n\n\"나도 그러지 않을 생각은 없소.\"\n\n\"자, 한번 말해 보시오. 대체 프랑스에서 누가 페르낭이라는 장교와 모르세르 백작이 같은 사람이라는 걸 알겠소? 그리고 1822년인가 1823년인가, 그 옛날에 함락된 야니나 따위를 지금 누가 마음에 두겠소?\"\n\n\"그게 바로 이 중상의 비열함을 보여 주는 거요. 이만큼이나 세월을 흘려보내 놓고, 갑자기 잊힌 사건을 들춰내 추문의 거리로 삼아, 우리의 높은 지위에 서린 광채를 더럽히려는 거요. 나는 아버지의 이름을 물려받았고, 그 이름에 치욕의 그림자가 드리우는 것을 두고 볼 수 없소. 이 기사가 실린 신문의 보샹에게 가서, 두 입회인 앞에서 그 주장을 철회하라고 요구할 작정이오.\"\n\n\"보샹은 결코 철회하지 않을 거요.\"\n\n\"그럼 우리는 싸워야겠지요.\"\n\n\"아니, 그러지 않을 거요. 그가 당신에게, 더없이 옳은 말이지만, 그리스 군에 같은 이름을 가진 장교가 쉰 명은 있었을지도 모른다고 할 테니까.\"\n\n\"그래도 우리는 싸울 거요. 나는 아버지의 명예에 묻은 그 오점을 지워 버리겠소. 그토록 용감한 군인이었고, 그토록 빛나는 이력을 쌓으신 내 아버지를……\"\n\n\"오, 그렇다면 그는 이렇게 덧붙이겠지요. '우리는 이 페르낭이 같은 세례명을 가진, 저 명망 높은 모르세르 백작과는 다른 인물이라고 믿을 만한 근거가 있다.'\"\n\n\"나는 완전한 철회가 아니고는 그 무엇에도 만족하지 않기로 마음먹었소.\"\n\n\"그러니까 두 입회인 앞에서 그가 그렇게 하게 만들 작정이란 말이지요?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"잘못 생각하셨소.\"\n\n\"그건 결국, 내가 청한 도움을 거절하시겠다는 뜻이겠지요?\"\n\n\"결투에 대한 내 지론을 알고 계시잖소. 기억하신다면, 우리가 로마에 있을 때 그 문제에 대한 내 의견을 말씀드렸지요.\"\n\n\"그렇지만 백작, 오늘 아침 나는, 평소 입에 올리시는 그 신조와는 영 어울리지 않는 일에 몰두해 계신 모습을 보았소.\"\n\n\"그건, 이보시오, 결코 별나게 굴어서는 안 된다는 걸 아셔야 하기 때문이오. 어리석은 자들 틈에 운명이 던져졌다면, 어리석음을 익혀 둘 필요가 있는 법이오. 나도 언젠가, 당신이 보샹에게 품은 것 이상으로 나와 다툴 진짜 빌미라고는 없는 어떤 머리 빈 망나니한테 결투를 신청받을지도 모르오. 그자가 무슨 하찮은 트집을 잡아 나를 몰아붙이고, 입회인들을 데려오거나, 어느 사람 많은 자리에서 나를 모욕할지도 모르오. 그래도 나는 그 때문에 그자를 죽여야 하는 거요.\"\n\n\"그럼 백작도 싸우겠다고 인정하시는 거군요? 그렇다면 왜 내가 싸우는 것에는 반대하시오?\"\n\n\"당신이 싸워서는 안 된다는 게 아니오. 다만 결투란 중대한 일이니, 충분히 숙고하지 않고 뛰어들어서는 안 된다는 거요.\"\n\n\"그자는 내 아버지를 모욕하기 전에 숙고하기라도 했답니까?\"\n\n\"만약 그가 성급하게 말한 거고, 자기가 그랬노라 인정한다면, 당신도 만족해야 하오.\"\n\n\"아, 백작, 당신은 너무 너그럽소.\"\n\n\"그리고 당신은 너무 까다롭소. 가령 말이오, 내가 지금부터 할 말에 화내지 마시오……\"\n\"말씀하시오.\"\n\n\"가령 그 주장이 정말로 사실이라면 어떻겠소?\"\n\n\"아들이라면 아버지의 명예에 묻은 그런 오점을 두고 볼 수 없는 법이오.\"\n\n\"이런! 우리는 받아들일 수밖에 없는 일이 많은 시대를 살고 있소.\"\n\n\"그게 바로 이 시대의 잘못이오.\"\n\n\"그래서 당신이 그걸 바로잡겠다는 거요?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 적어도 나 자신에 관한 한은.\"\n\n\"이런, 정말이지 까다로운 분이시군요, 이보시오!\"\n\n\"그렇소, 인정하오.\"\n\n\"좋은 충고에는 아주 귀를 닫으시는 거요?\"\n\n\"벗이 하는 충고라면 그렇지 않소.\"\n\n\"나를 그 벗으로 쳐 주시는 거요?\"\n\n\"물론 그렇소.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 입회인들을 데리고 보샹에게 가기 전에, 그 일에 대해 좀 더 알아보시오.\"\n\n\"누구한테서요?\"\n\n\"하이데한테서.\"\n\n\"이런, 이 일에 여자를 끌어들여서 무슨 소용이 있겠소? 그녀가 여기서 무얼 할 수 있단 말이오?\"\n\n\"예를 들어, 당신 부친이 그 재상의 패망과 죽음에 아무런 손도 대지 않았다고 당신에게 밝혀 줄 수 있소. 혹 만에 하나라도 정말 불행히 그런 일에……\"\n\n\"백작, 나는 그런 가정 따위는 한순간도 받아들이지 않겠다고 말씀드렸소.\"\n\n\"그럼 이 정보의 길마저 마다하시는 거요?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 단호히 그렇소.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 한마디만 더 충고하리다.\"\n\n\"하시오. 다만 그게 마지막이게 해 주오.\"\n\n\"어쩌면 듣고 싶지 않으실지도 모르겠소만?\"\n\n\"천만에, 청하는 바요.\"\n\n\"보샹에게 갈 때 입회인을 데려가지 마시오. 혼자 찾아가시오.\"\n\n\"그건 모든 관례에 어긋나오.\"\n\n\"당신의 경우는 예사로운 경우가 아니오.\"\n\n\"혼자 가라고 권하시는 이유는 무엇이오?\"\n\n\"그래야 이 일이 당신과 보샹 사이에만 머물 테니까.\"\n\n\"좀 더 설명해 보시오.\"\n\n\"그러리다. 만약 보샹이 철회할 마음이 있다면, 적어도 그가 제 발로 그렇게 할 기회를 주어야 하오. 그래도 당신이 얻는 만족은 똑같소. 반대로 그가 거절한다면, 그때 가서 두 낯선 사람을 당신의 비밀에 끌어들여도 늦지 않소.\"\n\n\"그들은 낯선 사람이 아니라 벗들이오.\"\n\n\"아, 하지만 오늘의 벗이 내일의 적이 되는 법이오. 보샹만 해도 그렇소.\"\n\n\"그럼 권하시는 바는……\"\n\n\"신중하시라는 거요.\"\n\n\"그럼 보샹에게 혼자 가라는 충고로군요?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 그 이유를 말씀드리리다. 어떤 사람의 자존심에서 무언가 양보를 얻어 내고자 한다면, 그 자존심을 건드리려는 기색조차 비쳐서는 안 되는 법이오.\"\n\n\"백작 말씀이 옳은 것 같소.\"\n\n\"그렇다니 기쁘오.\"\n\n\"그럼 혼자 가겠소.\"\n\n\"가시오. 하지만 아예 안 가는 편이 더 나을 거요.\"\n\n\"그건 불가능하오.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그렇게라도 하시오. 처음에 내놓으신 방안보다는 더 현명한 계책이오.\"\n\n\"하지만 그 모든 조심에도 불구하고 끝내 싸워야만 한다면, 내 입회인이 되어 주지 않으시겠소?\"\n\n\"이보시오, 자작,\" 몬테크리스토가 엄숙하게 말했다. \"당신은 오늘 이전에도 익히 보셨을 거요. 어느 때, 어느 곳에서든 나는 당신을 위해 늘 마음을 다해 왔다는 걸. 그러나 방금 내게 청하신 그 일만큼은, 내 힘으로는 들어드릴 수 없는 일이오.\"\n\n\"어째서요?\"\n\"어쩌면 언젠가 그 까닭을 알게 되실지도 모르오. 그동안은, 내가 그 이유를 당신께 밝히기를 마다하는 것을 부디 양해해 주시오.\"\n\n\"좋소, 그럼 프란츠와 샤토르노를 데리고 가겠소. 그들이라면 그 일에 딱 맞는 사람들이지.\"\n\n\"그렇게 하시오.\"\n\n\"하지만 정말 싸우게 된다면, 내게 사격과 검술을 한두 차례 가르쳐 주는 것쯤은 마다하지 않으시겠지요?\"\n\n\"그것 역시 불가능하오.\"\n\n\"참 별난 분이시군요! 어떤 일에도 끼어들지 않으시려 하니.\"\n\n\"옳은 말씀이오. 그게 바로 내가 따르고자 하는 원칙이오.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 일에 대해서는 더 말하지 맙시다. 잘 있으시오, 백작.\"\n\n모르세르는 모자를 집어 들고 방을 나섰다. 문 앞에서 마차를 발견한 그는 끓어오르는 분노를 한껏 억누르며 곧장 보샹을 찾아갔는데, 그는 사무실에 있었다. 그곳은 음침하고 먼지투성이로 보이는 방으로, 기자들의 사무실이 까마득한 옛날부터 늘 그래 왔듯한 모습이었다. 하인이 알베르 드 모르세르 씨가 왔다고 알렸다. 보샹은 제대로 들은 것이 맞는지 도무지 믿을 수 없다는 듯 그 이름을 혼자 되뇌더니, 그를 들여보내라 일렀다. 알베르가 들어섰다.\n\n보샹은 자기 친구가 방 안 여기저기 흩어진 신문들을 모조리 뛰어넘고 발로 짓밟는 것을 보고 놀라움의 탄성을 질렀다.\n\n\"이리로, 이리로 오게, 알베르!\" 그가 청년에게 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"정신이 나간 건가, 아니면 점잖게 나와 아침이라도 들러 온 건가? 자리를 좀 찾아 앉게. 저 제라늄 옆에 하나 있군. 이 방에서 세상에는 종이 잎사귀 말고도 다른 잎사귀가 있다는 걸 일깨워 주는 거라곤 저것뿐이라네.\"\n\n\"보샹,\" 알베르가 말했다. \"내가 이야기하러 온 건 자네 신문에 관한 일일세.\"\n\n\"그래? 그에 대해 무슨 할 말이 있나?\"\n\n\"거기 실린 어떤 기사를 바로잡아 주었으면 하네.\"\n\n\"무얼 두고 하는 말인가? 한데 우선 좀 앉게.\"\n\n\"고맙네.\" 알베르가 차갑고 격식 차린 인사를 건네며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 이제 자네 비위를 거스른 그 기사가 어떤 것인지 좀 설명해 주겠나?\"\n\n\"내 가문의 한 사람의 명예에 흠집을 내는 기사가 실렸네.\"\n\n\"그게 뭔가?\" 보샹이 몹시 놀라며 말했다. \"분명 자네가 뭔가 잘못 안 거겠지.\"\n\n\"야니나에서 자네에게 보내온 그 기사 말일세.\"\n\n\"야니나?\"\n\n\"그래. 정말이지 자네는 내가 여기 온 까닭을 전혀 모르는 모양이군.\"\n\n\"맹세코 정말 그렇다네! 바티스트, 어제 신문 좀 가져오게.\" 보샹이 소리쳤다.\n\n\"여기, 내 것을 가져왔네.\" 알베르가 대답했다.\n\n보샹은 신문을 받아 들고, 알베르가 가리킨 기사를 낮은 목소리로 읽었다.\n\n\"보다시피 이건 심각한 모욕일세.\" 보샹이 그 기사를 다 읽고 났을 때 모르세르가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 여기 언급된 그 장교가 자네 친척이란 말인가?\" 기자가 물었다.\n\n\"그렇다네.\" 알베르가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 내가 자네를 위해 무얼 해 주면 좋겠나?\" 보샹이 부드럽게 말했다.\n\n\"보샹, 이 기사를 부인해 주었으면 하네.\" 보샹은 인자한 표정으로 알베르를 바라보았다.\n\n\"이보게,\" 그가 말했다. \"이건 한참 이야기를 나눠 봐야 할 문제일세. 알다시피 철회란 늘 중대한 일이지. 앉게, 내가 한 번 더 읽어 보겠네.\"\n\n알베르는 다시 자리에 앉았고, 보샹은 친구가 규탄한 그 글줄을 처음보다 더 주의 깊게 읽었다.\n\n\"자,\" 알베르가 단호한 어조로 말했다. \"보다시피 자네 신문이 내 가문의 한 사람을 모욕했네. 나는 철회를 요구하네.\"\n\n\"요구한다고?\"\n\n\"그래, 요구하네.\"\n\n\"여긴 의회가 아니라는 걸 일깨워 줘도 되겠나, 자작.\"\n\n\"의회에 있고 싶은 마음도 없네.\" 청년이 자리에서 일어서며 대답했다. \"다시 말하지만, 나는 어제의 기사가 부인되게 하기로 마음먹었네. 자네는 나를 충분히 오래 알아 왔고,\" 알베르가 입술을 발작하듯 깨물며 말을 이었다. 보샹의 노여움이 일기 시작하는 것을 보았기 때문이다. \"자네는 내 벗이었고, 그러니 내가 이 점에 대한 결심을 끝내 지킬 사람이라는 걸 알 만큼 충분히 가까운 사이지.\"\n\n\"내가 자네 벗이었다면, 모르세르, 지금 자네의 그 말투를 들으니 내가 그 이름으로 불린 적이 있다는 사실마저 잊어버릴 지경일세. 하지만 잠깐만, 우리 화내지는 마세. 적어도 아직은 말이야. 자네는 잔뜩 약이 오르고 속이 상했군. 말해 보게, 이 페르낭이라는 사람이 자네와 어떻게 되는 사이인가?\"\n\n\"그저 내 아버지일 뿐이지.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"페르낭 몽데고 씨, 모르세르 백작, 스무 차례나 전장에서 싸우신 노병이지. 그분의 명예로운 흉터를 두고 저들은 치욕의 낙인이라 떠벌이는 걸세.\"\n\n\"자네 아버지라고?\" 보샹이 말했다. \"그렇다면 이야기가 전혀 다르지. 그렇다면 자네의 분노도 능히 이해가 가네, 알베르. 다시 한번 보겠네.\" 그러고는 한 마디 한 마디에 힘을 주어 가며 그 기사를 세 번째로 읽었다. \"하지만 신문은 어디에서도 이 페르낭을 자네 아버지와 같은 사람으로 못박지 않았네.\"\n\n\"그렇지. 하지만 다른 사람들은 그 연결을 알아챌 걸세. 그러니 나는 이 기사가 부인되게 하겠네.\"\n\n'하겠네'라는 말에 보샹은 알베르의 얼굴로 눈을 천천히 들어 올렸다가, 다시 그만큼 천천히 내리깔고는, 잠시 생각에 잠긴 채 잠자코 있었다.\n\n\"이 주장을 철회해 주겠지, 보샹?\" 알베르가 한층 커진, 그러나 억눌린 분노로 말했다.\n\n\"그러지.\" 보샹이 대답했다.\n\n\"당장?\" 알베르가 말했다.\n\n\"그 주장이 거짓이라는 확신이 서면.\"\n\n\"뭐라고?\"\n\n\"이건 들여다볼 가치가 있는 일이니, 내가 공을 들여 철저히 알아보겠네.\"\n\n\"하지만 알아볼 게 뭐가 있단 말인가?\" 보샹의 마지막 말에 분노가 끝 간 데까지 치민 알베르가 말했다. \"그가 내 아버지가 아니라고 믿는다면 당장 그렇다고 말하게. 반대로 그가 내 아버지라고 믿는다면, 그렇게 믿는 까닭을 대게.\"\n\n보샹은 그 특유의 미소를 띠며 알베르를 바라보았다. 그 미소는 갖가지로 변주되며 그의 온갖 다양한 감정을 다 드러내 보이곤 했다.\n\n\"이보게,\" 그가 대답했다. \"결투를 청할 생각으로 내게 온 거라면, 곧장 본론으로 들어왔어야지, 내가 지난 반 시간 동안 잠자코 들어 준 그 부질없는 이야기로 나를 붙들어 두지는 말았어야 했네. 자네 방문을 그런 뜻으로 새겨도 되겠나?\"\n\n\"그래. 자네가 그 파렴치한 중상을 철회하는 데 동의하지 않는다면.\"\n\n\"잠깐만. 협박은 사양하네, 페르낭 몽데고 씨, 모르세르 자작. 나는 적들한테서도 협박을 용납한 적이 없으니, 벗한테서라고 참아 넘기지는 않겠네. 자네는 페르낭 장군에 관한 기사를, 명예를 걸고 맹세하건대 내가 전혀 손대지 않은 그 기사를, 내가 부인하라고 요구하는 거지?\"\n\n\"그래, 요구하네.\" 알베르가 말했다. 그의 마음은 격해진 감정에 점점 갈피를 잃어 가고 있었다.\n\n\"그래서 내가 철회를 거절하면, 자네는 싸우겠다는 거지?\" 보샹이 차분한 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"그래.\" 알베르가 목소리를 높이며 대답했다.\n\n\"좋네,\" 보샹이 말했다. \"그럼 이게 내 대답일세, 이보게. 그 기사는 내가 실은 게 아니네. 나는 그게 실린 줄도 몰랐어. 하지만 자네가 취한 그 행동 때문에 내 주의가 그 기사로 쏠렸으니, 그것은 그럴 자격이 있는 누군가가 부인하거나 확인해 줄 때까지 그대로 남아 있을 걸세.\"\n\n\"이보게,\" 알베르가 일어서며 말했다. \"내 입회인들을 자네에게 보내는 영광을 누리겠네. 자네는 그들과 만남의 장소와 무기를 정해 주면 되겠네.\"\n\n\"물론이지, 이보게.\"\n\n\"오늘 저녁이라도, 괜찮다면, 늦어도 내일은 만나세.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니야. 나는 정해진 때에 그 자리에 나가겠네. 하지만 내 생각에는 — 도발을 받은 쪽이 나이니 사전 조건을 정할 권리는 내게 있네 — 내 생각에는 아직 그때가 아닐세. 나는 자네가 검술에 능하다는 걸 알고, 나는 그저 어지간한 정도지. 자네가 명사수라는 것도 알아. 그건 우리가 엇비슷하군. 우리 둘 사이의 결투가 중대한 일이 되리라는 것도 알지. 자네가 용감하고, 나 또한 용감하니까. 그러니 나는 까닭 없이 자네를 죽이고 싶지도, 까닭 없이 내가 죽고 싶지도 않네. 자, 이제 자네에게 한 가지 묻겠네. 아주 요긴한 물음이지. 자네는, 내가 그 일에 대해 아무것도 몰랐다고 한 번 이상 거듭 말하고 명예를 걸어 단언했는데도, 또 자네 말고는 누구도 페르낭이라는 이름 아래 모르세르 백작을 알아볼 수 없다고 내가 여전히 분명히 말하는데도, 내가 철회하지 않으면 나를 죽이겠다고까지 이 철회를 고집하는 건가?\"\n\n\"나는 처음 먹은 결심을 굽히지 않겠네.\"\n\n\"좋네, 이보게. 그럼 자네와 목을 베고 베이는 데 동의하지. 하지만 나는 삼 주의 준비 기간이 필요하네. 그 기간이 끝나면 자네에게 와서 '그 주장은 거짓이며, 나는 그것을 철회한다'고 하거나, '그 주장은 사실이다'라고 하겠네. 후자라면 그 즉시 칼집에서 검을, 아니면 상자에서 권총을, 자네가 원하는 대로 뽑겠네.\"\n\n\"삼 주라!\" 알베르가 외쳤다. \"내가 그동안 내내 치욕을 견디는데, 그 삼 주는 삼백 년처럼 더디게 흐를 걸세.\"\n\n\"자네가 나와 우호적인 사이로 남아 있었다면, 나는 '참게, 친구' 하고 말했을 걸세. 하지만 자네 스스로 나를 적으로 만들었으니, 나는 이렇게 말하겠네. '그게 나와 무슨 상관인가?'\"\n\n\"좋네, 그럼 삼 주로 하지.\" 모르세르가 말했다. \"하지만 명심하게. 그 기한이 다하면 어떤 지체나 핑계로도 자네는……\"\n\n\"알베르 드 모르세르 씨,\" 보샹이 이번에는 자기가 일어서며 말했다. \"나는 삼 주 동안은 — 그러니까 앞으로 스물나흘 동안은 — 자네를 창밖으로 내던질 수 없고, 자네 또한 그때가 지나기 전에는 내 머리통을 쪼갤 권리가 없네. 오늘이 8월 29일이니, 9월 21일이 우리가 합의한 기한의 끝이 되겠군. 그때가 오기까지는 — 그리고 지금부터 내가 자네에게 하는 충고는 신사로서 하는 충고일세 — 그때까지 우리, 서로 빤히 보이는 곳에 묶인 두 마리 개처럼 으르렁거리고 짖어 대는 짓은 삼가세.\"\n\n말을 마친 보샹은 알베르에게 쌀쌀맞게 절하고는 등을 돌려 인쇄실로 가 버렸다. 알베르는 신문 더미에 분풀이를 했는데, 지팡이로 사정없이 후려쳐 사무실 곳곳으로 날려 버렸다. 그러고 나서야 그는 떠났다. 그러나 마치 들어갈까 말까 망설이듯, 인쇄실 문 앞을 몇 번이나 오가고 나서였다.\n\n알베르가, 자기 낭패의 죄 없는 빌미였던 신문들을 후려쳤던 그 방식 그대로 마차 앞부분을 후려치며, 시 경계를 지날 무렵, 그는 빠른 걸음에 눈을 반짝이며 걸어가는 모렐을 알아보았다. 그는 중국식 목욕탕을 지나는 중이었는데, 생마르탱 문 쪽에서 와 마들렌 쪽으로 가는 듯했다.\n\n\"아,\" 모르세르가 말했다. \"저기 행복한 사람이 가는군!\" 그리고 공교롭게도 알베르의 그 짐작은 틀리지 않았다.\n\n# Chapter 79. The Lemonade\n\n사실 모렐은 무척 기뻐하고 있었다. 노르티에 씨가 방금 그를 부른 참이었고, 그 까닭이 어찌나 궁금했던지 그는 삯마차를 잡으려고 멈춰 서지도 않았다. 삯마차를 끄는 말 네 다리보다 제 두 다리를 한없이 더 믿었기 때문이다. 그래서 그는 멜레가에서부터 맹렬한 속도로 출발해, 포부르생토노레 방면으로 빠른 걸음을 재촉했다.\n\n모렐은 단단하고 사내다운 걸음으로 앞서 나아갔고, 가엾은 바루아는 그 뒤를 힘닿는 대로 따랐다. 모렐은 겨우 서른한 살이었고, 바루아는 예순이었다. 모렐은 깊은 사랑에 빠져 있었고, 바루아는 더위와 무리한 힘 때문에 죽을 지경이었다. 나이도 관심사도 이렇게 동떨어진 두 사람은 마치 삼각형의 두 변과 같아서, 서로 가장 멀리 떨어진 양 끝을 이루면서도 그럼에도 만나는 한 점을 지니고 있었다. 그 만나는 점이 바로 노르티에였다. 방금 모렐을 부른 사람이 바로 그였고, 지체 없이 와 달라는 청이었다. 모렐은 그 청을 글자 그대로 따랐으니, 바루아로서는 여간 곤혹스러운 일이 아니었다. 집에 다다랐을 때 모렐은 숨조차 차지 않았다. 사랑은 우리 욕망에 날개를 달아 주는 법이니까. 그러나 사랑이 무엇인지 잊은 지 오래인 바루아는, 어쩔 수 없이 따라야 했던 그 강행군 탓에 몹시 지쳐 있었다.\n\n늙은 하인은 모렐을 비밀 출입구로 안내한 뒤 서재 문을 닫았다. 곧이어 옷자락 스치는 소리가 발랑틴이 왔음을 알렸다. 짙은 상복 차림의 그녀는 놀랍도록 아름다웠고, 모렐은 그녀를 바라보는 것만으로도 어찌나 강렬한 기쁨을 느꼈던지, 할아버지와의 대화 따위는 없어도 좋을 것만 같았다.\n\n그러나 노인의 안락의자가 마룻바닥을 구르는 소리가 들리더니, 곧 그가 방 안에 모습을 드러냈다. 노르티에는 더없이 자상하고 다정한 눈빛으로, 발랑틴과 자신을 위해 때맞춰 나서 준 데에 모렐이 쏟아 낸 감사 인사를 받아들였다. 그 개입이 두 사람을 절망에서 건져 주었던 것이다. 이어서 모렐은 노인이 이번에는 또 어떤 은혜를 베풀려 하는지 묻는 눈빛을 던졌다. 발랑틴은 두 사람에게서 조금 떨어진 자리에 앉아, 말을 꺼내야 할 순간을 머뭇거리며 기다리고 있었다. 노르티에가 그녀에게 시선을 고정했다.\n\n\"할아버지가 제게 하신 말씀을 그대로 전할까요?\" 발랑틴이 물었다. 노르티에가 그렇게 하라는 신호를 보냈다.\n\n\"모렐 씨,\" 발랑틴이 더없이 진지한 관심으로 자기를 바라보는 젊은이에게 말했다. \"제 할아버지 노르티에 씨께서 사흘 전에 제게 하실 말씀이 아주 많으셨어요. 그래서 이제 당신을 부르신 거예요. 제가 그 말씀을 당신께 옮겨 드리도록요. 그러니 이제 전해 드리겠어요. 할아버지께서 저를 통역으로 택하셨으니, 저는 그 신뢰에 충실하게, 그분의 뜻을 한마디도 바꾸지 않고 그대로 전하겠어요.\"\n\n\"아, 더없이 애가 타도록 듣고 있습니다.\" 젊은이가 대답했다. \"부디 말씀해 주십시오.\"\n\n발랑틴이 눈을 내리깔았다. 그것은 모렐에게 좋은 징조였다. 발랑틴을 그렇게 만들 수 있는 것은 오직 행복뿐임을 그는 알고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"할아버지께서는 이 집을 떠나실 작정이세요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"바루아가 다른 집에 적당한 거처를 알아보고 있고요.\"\n\n\"하지만 당신은요, 빌포르 양, 노르티에 씨의 행복에 없어서는 안 될 당신은……\"\n\n\"저요?\" 발랑틴이 말을 가로챘다. \"저는 할아버지 곁을 떠나지 않을 거예요. 그건 저희끼리 이미 정해진 일이에요. 제 방은 할아버지 방 바로 옆에 둘 거고요. 이제 빌포르 씨가 이 계획에 찬성하시든 반대하시든 둘 중 하나예요. 찬성하시면 저는 곧바로 떠날 거고, 반대하시면 성년이 될 때까지 기다릴 거예요. 그게 한 열 달쯤 남았어요. 그러면 저는 자유로운 몸이 되고, 제 몫의 재산도 갖게 되고, 그리고……\"\n\n\"그리고 어떻게 됩니까?\" 모렐이 물었다.\n\n\"그리고 할아버지의 허락을 받아, 당신께 드린 약속을 지킬 거예요.\"\n\n발랑틴은 이 마지막 몇 마디를 어찌나 나직하게 내뱉었던지, 그녀의 말에 온 신경을 기울이고 있던 모렐이 아니었다면 도저히 알아듣지 못했을 정도였다.\n\n\"제가 할아버지의 뜻을 제대로 전했나요, 할아버지?\" 발랑틴이 노르티에를 향해 물었다.\n\n\"그렇다.\" 노인의 눈빛이 그렇게 말했다.\n\n\"일단 할아버지 댁에 살게 되면, 우리가 마음에 그리던 결합이 앞으로의 평안과 행복을 보장해 주리라는 믿음이 여전히 남아 있을 경우, 모렐 씨는 선량하고 훌륭하신 제 보호자가 계신 자리에서 저를 찾아오실 수 있어요. 그렇게 되면 저는 모렐 씨가 직접 제 손을 청하러 오시기를 기다리겠어요. 하지만, 아아, 욕망을 가로막는 장애물로 타오르던 마음도 안전해지면 시간이 지나 식어 버린다는 말을 들은 적이 있어요. 부디 우리는 결코 그런 일을 겪지 않기를 바라요!\"\n\n\"아,\" 모렐은 하마터면 노르티에와 발랑틴 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 그 두 사람을 두 분의 더 높은 존재로 우러러볼 뻔했다. \"제가 평생 무슨 일을 했기에 이토록 끝없는 행복을 누릴 자격이 있단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"그때까지,\" 젊은 아가씨가 차분하고 침착한 목소리로 말을 이었다. \"우리는 형편에 따르고, 친지들의 뜻에 이끌릴 거예요. 다만 그 뜻이 끝내 우리를 갈라놓으려 하지 않는 한에서요. 한마디로, 제가 전하고 싶은 말을 다 담고 있기에 다시 한번 말씀드리자면, 우리는 기다릴 거예요.\"\n\n\"그 말이 지우는 모든 희생을, 저는 기꺼이 치르겠다고 맹세합니다.\" 모렐이 말했다. \"체념해서가 아니라, 즐거운 마음으로요.\"\n\n\"그러니,\" 발랑틴이 막시밀리앙을 장난스럽게 바라보며 말을 이었다. \"더 이상 경솔한 행동은 안 돼요. 무모한 계획도 안 되고요. 오늘부터 당신의 이름을 떳떳하고 행복하게 지니도록 정해진 사람이라 스스로 여기는 이를, 당신은 설마 위태롭게 하고 싶진 않으시겠죠?\"\n\n모렐은 그 명령에 순종하는 눈빛을 보냈다. 노르티에는 이루 말할 수 없는 다정한 눈길로 두 연인을 바라보았고, 그동안 이 방에서 벌어지는 모든 일을 알 자격을 지닌 사람의 자격으로 방에 남아 있던 바루아는, 벗어진 이마에서 땀을 닦으며 젊은 두 사람을 보고 미소 지었다.\n\n\"몹시 더워 보이네요, 우리 바루아.\" 발랑틴이 말했다.\n\n\"아, 무척 빨리 달려왔거든요, 아가씨. 하지만 모렐 씨 얘기를 정직하게 해 드려야겠네요. 그분은 저보다 더 빨리 달리셨답니다.\"\n\n노르티에는 두 사람의 주의를 쟁반 쪽으로 돌렸다. 그 위에는 레모네이드가 담긴 유리병 하나와 잔 하나가 놓여 있었다. 병은 노르티에 씨가 이미 조금 마신 것을 빼고는 거의 가득 차 있었다.\n\n\"자, 바루아,\" 젊은 아가씨가 말했다. \"이 레모네이드 좀 마셔요. 한 모금 들이켜고 싶어서 군침을 흘리고 있잖아요.\"\n\n\"실은요, 아가씨,\" 바루아가 말했다. \"목이 타서 죽을 지경이었어요. 이렇게 친절히 권해 주시니, 한 잔 들이켜며 아가씨의 건강을 빌어도 좋을 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 좀 마시고, 곧장 돌아와요.\"\n\n바루아는 쟁반을 들고 나갔다. 그가 서두르느라 미처 닫지 못한 문 밖으로 나서기가 무섭게, 두 사람은 그가 고개를 뒤로 젖히고 발랑틴이 채워 준 잔을 마지막 한 방울까지 비우는 모습을 보았다. 발랑틴과 모렐이 노르티에 앞에서 작별 인사를 나누고 있을 때, 현관 초인종 소리가 들렸다. 손님이 왔다는 신호였다. 발랑틴이 시계를 보았다.\n\n\"정오가 지났네요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"오늘은 토요일이고요. 의사 선생님이실 거예요, 할아버지.\"\n\n노르티에는 그녀의 짐작이 옳다는 확신을 눈빛으로 드러냈다.\n\n\"선생님이 이리로 오실 테니, 모렐 씨는 그만 가시는 게 좋겠어요. 그렇지 않나요, 할아버지?\"\n\n\"그렇다.\" 노인이 신호를 보냈다.\n\n\"바루아,\" 발랑틴이 불렀다. \"바루아!\"\n\n\"갑니다, 아가씨.\" 그가 대답했다.\n\n\"바루아가 문을 열어 드릴 거예요.\" 발랑틴이 모렐에게 말했다. \"그리고 한 가지 잊지 마세요, 장교님. 할아버지께서는 우리의 행복을 위태롭게 할 만한 무모하거나 경솔한 짓을 절대 하지 말라고 당신께 명하셨어요.\"\n\n\"기다리겠다고 그분께 약속했습니다.\" 모렐이 대답했다. \"그러니 기다리겠습니다.\"\n\n바로 그때 바루아가 들어왔다. \"누가 초인종을 울렸지?\" 발랑틴이 물었다.\n\n\"다브리니 선생님이십니다.\" 바루아가 쓰러질 듯 비틀거리며 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 일이에요, 바루아?\" 발랑틴이 물었다. 늙은 하인은 대답하지 않고, 사납게 부릅뜬 눈으로 주인을 바라보았다. 한편 경련이 인 손으로는 똑바로 서 있으려고 가구 한 귀퉁이를 붙잡았다.\n\n\"쓰러지겠어요!\" 모렐이 외쳤다.\n\n바루아를 덮친 떨림은 차츰 심해졌고, 얼굴 생김새가 완전히 일그러졌으며, 근육의 경련은 가장 심각한 신경 발작이 다가오고 있음을 알리는 듯했다. 노르티에는 바루아의 그 처참한 꼴을 보며, 인간의 마음을 움직일 수 있는 온갖 슬픔과 연민의 감정을 눈빛으로 모두 드러냈다. 바루아가 주인을 향해 몇 걸음 내디뎠다.\n\n\"아, 주인님,\" 그가 말했다. \"제게 무슨 일이 일어난 건지 말씀해 주세요. 괴로워요. 앞이 안 보여요. 천 개의 불화살이 제 머리를 꿰뚫고 있어요. 아, 절 건드리지 마세요, 제발요.\"\n\n이때 그의 퀭한 두 눈은 금방이라도 눈구멍에서 튀어나올 것만 같았다. 머리가 뒤로 넘어갔고, 몸 아래쪽이 뻣뻣하게 굳기 시작했다. 발랑틴이 공포에 찬 비명을 질렀다. 모렐은 알 수 없는 위험으로부터 그녀를 지키려는 듯 그녀를 두 팔로 끌어안았다.\n\n\"다브리니 선생님, 다브리니 선생님.\" 그녀가 숨 막힌 목소리로 외쳤다. \"도와주세요, 도와주세요!\"\n\n바루아가 몸을 돌려 안간힘을 다해 몇 걸음 비틀거리더니, 노르티에의 발치에 쓰러져 노인의 무릎에 손을 얹고 외쳤다.\n\n\"주인님, 인자하신 주인님!\"\n\n그 순간 소리에 이끌린 빌포르 씨가 문지방에 나타났다. 모렐은 발랑틴을 안았던 팔을 풀고, 방 안 먼 구석으로 물러나 커튼 뒤에 반쯤 몸을 숨긴 채로 있었다. 마치 뱀이라도 본 듯 창백해진 그는, 겁에 질린 눈을 고통에 몸부림치는 가엾은 자에게 고정했다.\n\n노르티에는 초조함과 공포로 들끓으면서도, 하인이라기보다 벗에 가깝게 여기던 늙은 시종을 도무지 도울 수 없는 자신의 무력함에 절망하고 있었다. 이마에 무섭게 부풀어 오른 핏줄과 눈가 근육의 경련을 보면, 살아 활기 넘치는 정신과 움직이지 못하는 무력한 몸뚱이 사이에서 벌어지는 끔찍한 싸움을 읽어 낼 수 있었다.\n\n바루아는 얼굴이 경련으로 일그러지고, 두 눈은 피로 충혈되고, 머리는 뒤로 젖혀진 채 길게 뻗어 누워, 두 손으로 마룻바닥을 두드리고 있었다. 한편 두 다리는 어찌나 뻣뻣하게 굳었는지, 구부러지느니 차라리 부러질 것만 같았다. 입가에는 희미하게 거품이 비쳤고, 그는 괴롭게, 더없이 힘겹게 숨을 쉬었다.\n\n빌포르는 경악에 넋이 나간 듯, 눈앞의 광경을 한마디 말도 없이 뚫어지게 바라보고만 있었다. 그는 모렐을 보지 못했다. 얼굴이 창백해지고 머리카락이 곤두서는 듯한 그 말없는 응시의 한순간이 지나고, 그는 문을 향해 달려가며 외쳤다.\n\n\"의사 선생, 의사 선생! 당장 와 주세요, 제발 와 주세요!\"\n\n\"부인, 부인!\" 발랑틴이 계모를 부르며 그녀를 맞으러 위층으로 뛰어 올라가며 외쳤다. \"빨리, 빨리 오세요! 그리고 각성제 병도 가져오세요.\"\n\n\"무슨 일이냐?\" 빌포르 부인이 거칠고 딱딱한 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"아! 어서요! 어서요!\"\n\n\"그런데 의사는 어디 있소?\" 빌포르가 외쳤다. \"어디 있냐고?\"\n\n빌포르 부인이 이제 천천히 계단을 내려왔다. 한 손에는 얼굴을 닦는 듯 손수건을 들고 있었고, 다른 손에는 영국제 각성제 병을 들고 있었다. 그녀가 방에 들어서며 처음 던진 시선은 노르티에를 향했다. 이런 광경이 불러일으킬 수밖에 없는 동요를 빼면, 그의 얼굴은 여느 때처럼 멀쩡하다는 것을 알려 주고 있었다. 두 번째 시선은 죽어 가는 자를 향했다. 그녀는 창백해졌고, 그녀의 눈은 하인에게서 재빨리 옮겨 가 주인에게 머물렀다.\n\n\"제발, 부인,\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"의사는 어디 있소? 방금 당신과 함께 있었잖소. 보다시피 이건 졸중 발작이오. 피를 뽑아 줄 수만 있다면 살릴 수 있을지도 모르오!\"\n\n\"이 사람이 최근에 뭘 먹었나요?\" 빌포르 부인이 남편의 질문을 슬쩍 피하며 물었다.\n\n\"부인,\" 발랑틴이 대답했다. \"이 사람은 아침조차 거른걸요. 할아버지가 시키신 심부름으로 무척 빨리 달려갔다 왔고, 돌아와서는 레모네이드 한 잔밖에 마시지 않았어요.\"\n\n\"어머,\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"왜 포도주를 마시지 않았을까? 레모네이드는 저 사람한테 아주 나쁜 건데.\"\n\n\"할아버지의 레모네이드 병이 바로 옆에 놓여 있었거든요. 가엾은 바루아는 몹시 목이 말라서, 눈에 띄는 거라면 무엇이든 고맙게 마셨던 거예요.\"\n\n빌포르 부인이 흠칫했다. 노르티에는 더없이 깊이 캐묻는 눈빛으로 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"저 사람은 목이 워낙 짧아서요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"부인,\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"다브리니 선생이 어디 있느냐고 묻고 있잖소. 제발 대답 좀 해 주시오!\"\n\n\"에두아르와 함께 있어요. 그 애가 몸이 좀 안 좋아서요.\" 빌포르 부인이 더는 대답을 피할 수 없어 말했다.\n\n빌포르가 그를 데리러 위층으로 달려갔다.\n\n\"이걸 받아라.\" 빌포르 부인이 각성제 병을 발랑틴에게 건네며 말했다. \"틀림없이 이 사람의 피를 뽑을 테니, 나는 물러가마. 피를 보는 건 도저히 견딜 수가 없구나.\" 그러고는 남편을 따라 위층으로 올라갔다. 이제 모렐이 숨어 있던 자리에서 나왔다. 워낙 큰 혼란이 벌어진 터라 그가 거기 있는 줄은 아무도 알아채지 못했다.\n\n\"되도록 빨리 가세요, 막시밀리앙.\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"그리고 제가 사람을 보낼 때까지 기다리세요. 어서 가요.\"\n\n모렐은 물러가도 좋은지 허락을 구하려 노르티에를 바라보았다. 여전히 평소의 침착함을 잃지 않은 노인이 그렇게 하라는 신호를 보냈다. 젊은이는 발랑틴의 손을 입술에 가져다 댄 뒤, 뒷계단으로 집을 빠져나갔다.\n\n그가 방을 떠나는 바로 그 순간, 빌포르와 의사가 맞은편 문으로 들어왔다. 바루아는 이제 의식이 돌아오는 기미를 보이고 있었다. 고비는 지난 듯, 나직한 신음 소리가 들리더니 그가 한쪽 무릎을 짚고 몸을 일으켰다. 다브리니와 빌포르가 그를 긴 의자에 눕혔다.\n\n\"무엇을 처방하시겠소, 선생?\" 빌포르가 물었다.\n\n\"물과 에테르를 좀 주시오. 집에 있겠지요?\"\n\n\"있소.\"\n\n\"테레빈유와 토주석도 가져오라 이르시오.\"\n\n빌포르는 곧바로 심부름꾼을 보냈다. \"그리고 이제 모두 물러가게 해 주시오.\"\n\n\"저도 가야 하나요?\" 발랑틴이 머뭇거리며 물었다.\n\n\"네, 아가씨, 특히 아가씨가요.\" 의사가 퉁명스럽게 대답했다.\n\n발랑틴은 놀란 얼굴로 다브리니 씨를 바라보더니, 할아버지의 이마에 입을 맞추고 방을 나갔다. 의사는 침울한 표정으로 그녀의 뒤에서 문을 닫았다.\n\n\"보세요, 보세요, 선생.\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"이 사람이 다시 정신을 차리고 있잖소. 정말이지, 결국 별일 아닌 것 같소.\"\n\n다브리니 씨는 서글픈 미소로 대답을 대신했다.\n\n\"기분이 어떤가, 바루아?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"좀 나아졌습니다, 선생님.\"\n\n\"이 에테르와 물을 좀 마시겠나?\"\n\n\"마셔 보겠습니다. 하지만 절 건드리지 마세요.\"\n\n\"왜 그러나?\"\n\n\"손끝으로라도 절 건드리시면 발작이 다시 도질 것 같아서요.\"\n\n\"마시게.\"\n\n바루아는 잔을 받아 들고 시퍼렇게 질린 입술로 가져가, 건네받은 액체의 절반쯤을 마셨다.\n\n\"어디가 아픈가?\" 의사가 물었다.\n\n\"온몸이요. 몸 전체에 경련이 일어요.\"\n\n\"눈앞에 뭔가 번쩍이는 느낌이 드나?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"귀에서 무슨 소리가 들리나?\"\n\n\"끔찍할 만큼요.\"\n\n\"그게 처음 느껴진 게 언제였나?\"\n\n\"방금이요.\"\n\n\"갑자기?\"\n\n\"네, 벼락이 치듯이요.\"\n\n\"어제나 그저께는 아무렇지도 않았나?\"\n\"아무렇지도 않았습니다.\"\n\n\"졸음도 없었고?\"\n\n\"전혀요.\"\n\n\"오늘은 무얼 먹었나?\"\n\n\"아무것도 안 먹었습니다. 주인님의 레모네이드 한 잔만 마셨을 뿐이에요. 그게 전부입니다.\" 그러고서 바루아는 노르티에 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다. 노인은 안락의자에 꼼짝 않고 붙박인 채, 한마디 말도 한 번의 몸짓도 흘리지 않고 이 끔찍한 광경을 바라보고 있었다.\n\n\"그 레모네이드가 어디 있나?\" 의사가 다급히 물었다.\n\n\"아래층 유리병에요.\"\n\n\"아래층 어디?\"\n\n\"부엌에요.\"\n\n\"제가 가서 가져올까요, 선생?\" 빌포르가 물었다.\n\n\"아니오, 여기 남아서 바루아에게 이 에테르와 물을 마저 마시게 해 보시오. 레모네이드는 내가 직접 가서 가져오겠소.\"\n\n다브리니는 문 쪽으로 몸을 날려 뒷계단을 단숨에 뛰어 내려갔고, 서두르다가 마침 부엌으로 내려가던 빌포르 부인을 하마터면 쓰러뜨릴 뻔했다. 그녀가 비명을 질렀지만 다브리니는 아랑곳하지 않았다. 오직 한 가지 생각에 사로잡힌 그는 마지막 네 계단을 한걸음에 뛰어넘어 부엌으로 뛰어들었고, 거기서 쟁반 위에 놓아둔 그대로 세 부분쯤 비어 있는 유리병을 발견했다. 그는 독수리가 먹잇감을 낚아채듯 병을 덮쳤다. 숨이 차 헐떡이며 그는 방금 나온 방으로 돌아왔다. 빌포르 부인은 자기 방으로 이어지는 계단을 천천히 올라가고 있었다.\n\n\"부인이 말씀하신 그 유리병이 이것이오?\" 다브리니가 물었다.\n\n\"네, 선생.\"\n\n\"부인이 드셨다는 그 레모네이드와 같은 것이오?\"\n\n\"그런 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"맛이 어땠소?\"\n\n\"쓴맛이 났어요.\"\n\n의사는 레모네이드 몇 방울을 손바닥에 따라 입술을 갖다 댔고, 포도주를 음미하는 사람처럼 입안을 헹구더니 그 액체를 벽난로에 뱉었다.\n\n\"틀림없이 같은 것이군요.\" 그가 말했다. \"노르티에 씨, 당신도 좀 드셨습니까?\"\n\n\"그렇다.\"\n\n\"당신도 쓴맛을 느끼셨습니까?\"\n\"그렇다.\"\n\n\"아, 선생님,\" 바루아가 외쳤다. \"발작이 다시 도지고 있어요. 아, 제발 저를 좀 어떻게 해 주세요.\" 의사가 환자에게로 달려갔다.\n\n\"그 토주석, 빌포르 씨, 오는지 좀 보시오.\"\n\n빌포르가 복도로 뛰어나가며 외쳤다. \"토주석! 토주석! 아직 안 왔나?\" 아무도 대답하지 않았다. 집 안 전체에 더없이 깊은 공포가 감돌았다.\n\n\"폐에 바람을 불어넣을 만한 게 있다면,\" 다브리니가 주위를 둘러보며 말했다. \"질식만은 막을 수 있을지도 모르는데. 하지만 쓸 만한 게 아무것도 없군! 아무것도!\"\n\n\"아, 선생님,\" 바루아가 외쳤다. \"저를 이대로 도움도 없이 죽게 내버려 두실 겁니까? 아, 죽어요! 아, 살려 주세요!\"\n\n\"펜, 펜을!\" 의사가 말했다. 탁자 위에 펜 하나가 놓여 있었다. 그는 그것을 환자의 입에 밀어 넣으려 했다. 환자는 경련에 휩싸인 와중에도 헛되이 토하려고 안간힘을 쓰고 있었다. 그러나 턱이 어찌나 꽉 다물려 있는지 펜이 그 사이를 지날 수가 없었다. 이 두 번째 발작은 첫 번째보다 훨씬 격렬했고, 그는 긴 의자에서 바닥으로 미끄러져 고통 속에 몸부림쳤다. 의사는 그것을 가라앉히기 위해 자신이 할 수 있는 일이 없음을 알기에 그를 그 발작 속에 내버려 두고, 노르티에에게 다가가 불쑥 말했다.\n\n\"기분이 어떠십니까? 괜찮으십니까?\"\n\n\"그렇다.\"\n\n\"가슴이 답답하십니까? 아니면 속이 가볍고 편안하십니까, 예?\"\n\n\"그렇다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 제가 일요일마다 드리던 그 약을 드신 뒤와 거의 같은 느낌이시군요?\"\n\n\"그렇다.\"\n\n\"레모네이드는 바루아가 만들었습니까?\"\n\n\"그렇다.\"\n\n\"그에게 그것을 좀 마시라고 권하신 게 당신입니까?\" \"아니다.\"\n\n\"빌포르 씨입니까?\"\n\n\"아니다.\"\n\n\"부인입니까?\" \"아니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 손녀분이군요, 그렇지요?\"\n\n\"그렇다.\"\n\n바루아의 신음 소리가, 턱뼈가 으스러질 듯한 하품과 함께 다브리니 씨의 주의를 끌었다. 그는 노르티에 씨를 두고 병자에게로 돌아갔다.\n\n\"바루아,\" 의사가 말했다. \"말할 수 있겠나?\" 바루아가 알아들을 수 없는 말 몇 마디를 웅얼거렸다. \"어떻게든 애를 써서 말해 보게, 이 사람아.\" 다브리니가 말했다. 바루아가 충혈된 두 눈을 다시 떴다.\n\n\"레모네이드는 누가 만들었나?\"\n\n\"제가요.\"\n\n\"만든 즉시 주인께 가져갔나?\"\n\n\"아니요.\"\n\"그럼 그사이에 어딘가에 두었군?\"\n\n\"네. 식료품 저장실에 두었어요. 불려 나가는 바람에요.\"\n\n\"그럼 이 방에 그걸 가져온 사람은 누군가?\"\n\n\"발랑틴 아가씨요.\" 다브리니가 손으로 이마를 쳤다.\n\n\"맙소사.\" 그가 외쳤다.\n\n\"선생님, 선생님!\" 또 한 번의 발작이 오는 것을 느낀 바루아가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그 토주석은 끝내 안 가져오는 건가?\" 의사가 물었다.\n\n\"여기 미리 준비된 게 한 잔 있소.\" 빌포르가 방으로 들어서며 말했다.\n\n\"누가 준비했소?\"\n\n\"나와 함께 온 약제사가 했소.\"\n\n\"마시게.\" 의사가 바루아에게 말했다.\n\n\"안 됩니다, 선생님. 너무 늦었어요. 목이 막혀 와요. 숨이 막혀요! 아, 제 심장! 아, 제 머리! 아, 이 고통! 이렇게 오래 괴로워해야 합니까?\"\n\n\"아니, 아닐세, 이 사람아.\" 의사가 대답했다. \"곧 괴로움이 그칠 걸세.\"\n\n\"아, 무슨 말씀인지 알겠습니다.\" 가엾은 사내가 말했다. \"하느님, 저를 불쌍히 여기소서!\" 그러고서 무시무시한 비명을 지르며, 바루아는 벼락에 맞은 듯 뒤로 쓰러졌다. 다브리니는 그의 심장에 손을 대 보고, 그의 입술 앞에 유리잔을 갖다 댔다.\n\n\"어떻소?\" 빌포르가 말했다.\n\n\"부엌에 가서 제비꽃 시럽을 좀 가져다주시오.\"\n\n빌포르가 곧바로 갔다.\n\n\"놀라지 마십시오, 노르티에 씨.\" 다브리니가 말했다. \"환자를 옆방으로 옮겨 피를 뽑으려는 겁니다. 이런 종류의 발작은 지켜보기에 몹시 끔찍하니까요.\"\n\n그러고서 그는 바루아의 양 겨드랑이를 붙잡아 옆방으로 끌고 갔다. 그러나 거의 곧바로 레모네이드를 가지러 돌아왔다. 노르티에가 오른쪽 눈을 감았다.\n\n\"발랑틴을 원하시는 거지요? 사람을 시켜 아가씨를 보내 드리라 하겠습니다.\"\n\n빌포르가 돌아왔고, 다브리니가 복도에서 그를 맞았다.\n\n\"그래, 이제 좀 어떻소?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"이리로 들어오시오.\" 다브리니가 말하며 그를 병자가 누운 방으로 데려갔다.\n\n\"아직 발작 중이오?\" 검사가 말했다.\n\n\"죽었소.\"\n\n빌포르가 몇 걸음 물러서며 두 손을 맞잡고, 진정한 경악과 연민에 잠겨 외쳤다. \"죽었다고? 그것도 이렇게 빨리?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 아주 빠르군요.\" 의사가 눈앞의 시신을 바라보며 말했다. \"하지만 그게 놀랄 일은 아니지요. 생메랑 부부도 이만큼 빨리 죽었으니까요. 댁에서는 사람들이 참 갑작스레 죽는군요, 빌포르 씨.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요?\" 검사가 공포와 경악이 서린 어조로 외쳤다. \"여전히 그 끔찍한 생각을 곱씹고 계시는 거요?\"\n\n\"여전히 그렇소. 그리고 앞으로도 줄곧 그럴 거요.\" 다브리니가 대답했다. \"그 생각이 단 한순간도 내 머릿속을 떠난 적이 없으니까. 그리고 이번에는 내가 결코 틀리지 않았음을 분명히 알 수 있도록, 내가 지금부터 하는 말을 잘 들으시오, 빌포르 씨.\"\n\n검사가 발작하듯 떨었다.\n\n\"이렇다 할 흔적을 거의 남기지 않고 생명을 앗아 가는 독이 하나 있소. 나는 그것을 잘 아오. 온갖 형태로, 또 그것이 일으키는 온갖 작용으로 연구했으니까. 나는 가엾은 바루아의 경우에서도, 생메랑 부인의 경우에서와 마찬가지로 이 독의 존재를 알아보았소. 그것을 검출하는 방법이 있소. 그 독은 산에 의해 붉게 변한 리트머스 종이를 다시 푸르게 되돌리고, 제비꽃 시럽을 초록으로 바꾸어 놓소. 우리에게 리트머스 종이는 없지만, 보시오, 마침 제비꽃 시럽을 가져오는군요.\"\n\n의사의 말이 옳았다. 복도에서 발소리가 들렸다. 다브리니 씨는 문을 열고 하녀의 손에서 시럽 두세 숟가락이 담긴 잔을 받아 든 뒤, 조심스럽게 문을 닫았다.\n\n\"보시오,\" 그가 검사에게 말했다. 검사의 심장은 거의 들릴 만큼 요란하게 뛰고 있었다. \"이 잔에 제비꽃 시럽이 좀 담겨 있고, 이 유리병에는 노르티에 씨와 바루아가 마신 레모네이드의 나머지가 들어 있소. 만약 레모네이드가 깨끗하고 무해한 것이라면 시럽은 제 빛깔을 유지할 거요. 하지만 반대로 레모네이드에 독이 섞여 있다면 시럽은 초록으로 변할 거요. 똑똑히 보시오!\"\n\n그러고서 의사는 유리병에서 레모네이드 몇 방울을 천천히 잔에 따랐다. 그러자 순식간에 잔 바닥에 옅고 뿌연 침전물이 생기기 시작했다. 그 침전물은 처음에 푸른빛을 띠더니, 사파이어 빛깔에서 오팔 빛깔로, 다시 오팔에서 에메랄드로 옮겨 갔다. 이 마지막 빛깔에 이르자 더는 변하지 않았다. 실험의 결과는 어떤 의심의 여지도 남기지 않았다.\n\n\"가엾은 바루아는 독살된 거요.\" 다브리니가 말했다. \"그리고 나는 이 단언을 하느님 앞에서도 사람 앞에서도 굽히지 않을 거요.\"\n\n빌포르는 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 두 손을 맞잡고 퀭한 눈을 부릅떴다. 그러고는 복받치는 감정에 못 이겨 의자에 풀썩 주저앉았다.\n\n# 제79장 레모네이드\n\n다브리니 씨는 곧 검사를 의식으로 되돌려 놓았다. 그는 그 죽음의 방 안에서 또 하나의 시체처럼 보였다.\n\n\"오, 우리 집에 죽음이 들어왔어!\" 빌포르가 외쳤다.\n\n\"차라리 범죄라고 하시지요!\" 의사가 받았다.\n\n\"다브리니 씨,\" 빌포르가 외쳤다. \"지금 이 순간 제가 느끼는 것을 다 말씀드릴 수가 없습니다. 공포, 비탄, 광기…….\"\n\n\"그렇겠지요.\" 다브리니 씨가 위엄 어린 침착함으로 말했다. \"하지만 이제는 행동할 때라고 봅니다. 이 죽음의 격류를 멈춰야 할 때라고 봅니다. 저는 더 이상 이 비밀을 품고만 있을 수가 없습니다. 희생자들과 사회 전체의 원한이 풀리는 것을 보고 싶습니다.\"\n\n빌포르가 음울한 눈길로 주위를 둘러보았다. \"우리 집에서,\" 그가 중얼거렸다. \"우리 집에서!\"\n\n\"자, 검사님.\" 다브리니 씨가 말했다. \"사내답게 처신하십시오. 법을 해석하는 사람으로서, 사사로운 이익을 직무에 바쳐 그 직분에 명예를 더하십시오.\"\n\n\"의사 선생, 소름이 끼치는군요. 희생을 말씀하시는 겁니까?\"\n\"그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 누군가를 의심하고 계신 겁니까?\"\n\n\"저는 아무도 의심하지 않습니다. 죽음이 댁의 문을 두드립니다. 들어옵니다. 그리고 눈을 가린 채가 아니라 신중하게, 방에서 방으로 옮겨 다닙니다. 자, 저는 그 행로를 따라가고, 그 자취를 좇습니다. 옛사람들의 지혜를 빌려, 더듬어 길을 찾습니다. 댁의 가족에 대한 제 우정과 검사님에 대한 제 존경이 제 눈을 가린 두 겹의 띠와도 같으니 말입니다. 자…….\"\n\n\"오, 말씀하십시오, 말씀하십시오, 의사 선생. 견뎌 내겠습니다.\"\n\n\"자, 그럼 말씀드리지요. 검사님은 댁의 살림 안에, 혹은 어쩌면 가족 안에, 한 세기에 단 하나만 태어나는 끔찍한 괴물 가운데 하나를 두고 계십니다. 같은 시대에 살았던 로쿠스타와 아그리피나는 예외였고, 그토록 많은 범죄로 더럽혀진 로마 제국을 완전히 멸망시키려는 섭리의 결단을 증명한 존재였지요. 브룬힐데와 프레데군트는 문명이 그 유년기에 겪은 고통스러운 투쟁의 산물이었습니다. 어둠의 나라에서 온 사자(使者)의 손을 빌려서라도 인간이 정신을 다스리는 법을 배우려 하던 시절 말입니다. 이 여인들은 모두 아름다웠거나, 아름다운 이들이었습니다. 댁의 그 범인의 이마에 보이는 것과 똑같은 순결의 꽃이 그들의 이마에도 피었거나, 여전히 피어 있었지요.\"\n\n빌포르가 비명을 지르며 두 손을 마주 잡고 애원하는 표정으로 의사를 바라보았다. 그러나 의사는 가차 없이 말을 이었다.\n\n\"'범죄로 이득을 보는 자를 찾으라.' 법학의 한 격언입니다.\"\n\n\"의사 선생,\" 빌포르가 외쳤다. \"아아, 의사 선생, 그 숙명적인 말 때문에 인간의 정의가 얼마나 자주 속아 넘어갔습니까. 왜인지는 모르겠지만, 저는 이 범죄가…….\"\n\n\"그럼 범죄가 있다는 것은 인정하시는군요?\"\n\n\"네, 너무도 분명하게 그것이 존재한다는 걸 알겠습니다. 하지만 그것이 저 개인을 노린 것 같습니다. 이 모든 참화 끝에, 저 자신이 공격당할까 두렵습니다.\"\n\n\"오, 인간이여!\" 다브리니가 중얼거렸다. \"모든 짐승 가운데 가장 이기적이고, 모든 피조물 가운데 가장 자기중심적이며, 땅이 도는 것도, 해가 비치는 것도, 죽음이 내리치는 것도 오직 자기를 위한 일이라 믿는 존재여. 풀잎 끝에 올라앉아 신을 저주하는 개미로다! 그럼 목숨을 잃은 이들은 아무것도 잃지 않았단 말입니까? 생메랑 씨, 생메랑 부인, 누아르티에 씨께서는…….\"\n\n\"뭐라고요? 누아르티에 씨라고요?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다. 그 가엾은 하인의 목숨을 노렸다고 생각하십니까? 아니지요, 아닙니다. 셰익스피어의 폴로니우스처럼, 그는 다른 사람 대신 죽은 겁니다. 레모네이드는 누아르티에 씨를 노린 것이었습니다. 논리적으로 따지면 그것을 마셨어야 할 사람은 누아르티에 씨였지요. 다른 사람은 그저 우연히 마셨을 뿐입니다. 바루아가 죽긴 했어도, 죽기를 바란 대상은 누아르티에 씨였던 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그런데 어째서 제 아버지는 죽지 않았습니까?\"\n\n\"생메랑 부인이 돌아가신 뒤 어느 날 저녁, 정원에서 제가 말씀드렸지요. 그분의 몸이 바로 그 독에 익숙해져 있기 때문이라고요. 다른 사람에게는 치명적일 양이 그분께는 대수롭지 않았던 겁니다. 누아르티에 씨께서 마비 증세를 앓고 계셔서, 제가 지난 열두 달 동안 브루신을 처방해 왔다는 사실을 아무도, 심지어 살인자조차 모르기 때문입니다. 하지만 그 살인자는 브루신이 맹독이라는 사실을 모르지 않습니다. 직접 증명해 보였으니까요.\"\n\n\"오, 자비를, 제발 자비를!\" 빌포르가 두 손을 비틀며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"범인의 발자취를 따라가 봅시다. 그자는 먼저 생메랑 씨를 죽였습니다…….\"\n\n\"오, 의사 선생!\"\n\n\"맹세할 수 있습니다. 제가 전해 들은 그분의 증세가 제가 다른 사례들에서 직접 본 것과 너무도 잘 들어맞으니까요.\" 빌포르는 더 이상 항변하지 않았다. 그저 신음할 뿐이었다. \"그자는 먼저 생메랑 씨를 죽였습니다.\" 의사가 되풀이했다. \"그다음에 생메랑 부인을요. 물려받을 재산이 두 몫이지요.\" 빌포르가 이마에 맺힌 땀을 닦았다. \"주의 깊게 들으십시오.\"\n\n\"아아,\" 빌포르가 더듬거렸다. \"한마디도 놓치지 않고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"누아르티에 씨께서는,\" 다브리니 씨가 똑같은 무자비한 어조로 말을 이었다. \"누아르티에 씨께서는 한때 검사님과 검사님 가족에게 불리하게, 사실상 가난한 이들에게 유리하게 유언장을 작성하셨지요. 누아르티에 씨가 화를 면한 것은 그분에게서 아무것도 기대할 게 없었기 때문입니다. 그런데 그분이 첫 번째 유언장을 없애고 두 번째 유언장을 작성하시자마자, 세 번째 유언장을 작성하실까 두려워 누군가 그분을 쓰러뜨리려 든 겁니다. 유언장은 그저께 작성된 것으로 압니다. 보십시오, 한순간도 허비하지 않았지요.\"\n\n\"오, 자비를, 다브리니 씨!\"\n\n\"자비는 없습니다, 검사님! 의사에게는 이 땅에서 신성한 사명이 있습니다. 그 사명을 다하기 위해 그는 생명의 근원에서 시작하여 무덤의 신비로운 어둠에 이르기까지 내려갑니다. 범죄가 저질러지고, 신이 필시 진노하여 얼굴을 돌리실 때, 범인을 정의 앞에 세우는 것은 바로 의사의 몫입니다.\"\n\n\"제 아이에게 자비를 베풀어 주십시오, 선생.\" 빌포르가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"보십시오, 그 아이의 이름을 먼저 부른 것은 바로 검사님 자신입니다. 그 아이의 아버지인 검사님이요.\"\n\n\"발랑틴을 가엾이 여겨 주십시오! 들어 보세요, 그건 있을 수 없는 일입니다. 차라리 제 자신을 고발하겠습니다! 다이아몬드처럼, 백합처럼 순결한 마음을 지닌 발랑틴을요!\"\n\n\"자비는 없습니다, 검사님. 범죄의 냄새가 진동합니다. 생메랑 씨에게 보낸 약은 모두 그 아가씨가 손수 챙겼습니다. 그리고 생메랑 씨는 죽었지요. 생메랑 부인이 드신 진정제도 모두 빌포르 양이 조제했습니다. 그리고 생메랑 부인은 죽었지요. 누아르티에 씨가 매일 아침 드시던 레모네이드를, 심부름을 나가던 바루아의 손에서 받아 든 사람도 빌포르 양입니다. 그리고 그분은 기적적으로 화를 면했습니다. 빌포르 양이 범인입니다. 그 아가씨가 독살범입니다! 검사님께, 국왕의 검사이신 검사님께, 저는 빌포르 양을 고발합니다. 검사님의 직무를 다하십시오.\"\n\n\"의사 선생, 더 이상 버티지 않겠습니다. 더 이상 저 자신을 변호할 수 없습니다. 선생 말씀을 믿습니다. 하지만 제발, 제 목숨을, 제 명예를 살려 주십시오!\"\n\n\"빌포르 씨.\" 의사가 한층 격렬하게 받았다. \"어리석은 인간적 신중함을 모두 내던지는 때가 있는 법입니다. 따님이 범죄를 단 한 번만 저질렀고, 또 한 번을 꾸미는 것을 제가 보았다면, 저는 이렇게 말했을 겁니다. '그 아이에게 경고하시오, 벌하시오, 수녀원에서 남은 생을 울며 기도하며 보내게 하시오.' 두 번의 범죄를 저질렀다면, 저는 이렇게 말했을 겁니다. '빌포르 씨, 여기 그 죄수가 모르는 독이 있소. 알려진 해독제가 없고, 생각만큼 빠르며, 번개처럼 신속하고, 벼락처럼 치명적인 독이오. 그 독을 따님에게 주고, 그 영혼을 신께 맡기시오. 그렇게 당신의 명예와 목숨을 지키시오. 그 아이가 노리는 것은 바로 당신의 목숨이니. 위선적인 미소와 달콤한 권유로 당신의 베갯머리로 다가서는 그 아이의 모습이 눈에 선하오. 빌포르 씨, 당신이 먼저 치지 않으면 화가 미칠 것이오!' 따님이 두 사람만 죽였다면 저는 이렇게 말했을 겁니다. 하지만 그 아이는 세 번의 죽음을 보았습니다. 살해된 세 사람을 지켜보았습니다. 세 구의 시체 곁에 무릎 꿇었습니다! 독살범을 처형대로 보내시오, 처형대로! 명예를 말씀하십니까? 제가 시키는 대로 하십시오. 그러면 불멸의 명성이 당신을 기다릴 것입니다!\"\n\n빌포르가 무릎을 꿇었다.\n\n\"들어 보세요.\" 그가 말했다. \"저에게는 선생 같은 정신력이 없습니다. 아니, 차라리 선생도 갖지 못할 정신력이지요. 만일 제 딸 발랑틴이 아니라 선생의 딸 마들렌의 일이었다면 말입니다.\" 의사의 얼굴이 창백해졌다. \"의사 선생, 여인의 몸에서 태어난 자는 누구나 고통받고 죽도록 정해져 있습니다. 저는 고통받는 것도, 죽음을 기다리는 것도 달게 받겠습니다.\"\n\n\"조심하십시오.\" 다브리니 씨가 말했다. \"죽음은 더디게 올지도 모릅니다. 당신의 아버지를, 당신의 아내를, 어쩌면 당신의 아들을 친 뒤에야 다가오는 것을 보게 될지도 모릅니다.\"\n\n빌포르는 숨이 막힌 듯 의사의 팔을 움켜쥐었다.\n\n\"들어 보세요.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"저를 가엾이 여겨 주십시오. 저를 도와주십시오! 아니, 제 딸은 죄가 없습니다. 선생이 우리 둘을 법정으로 끌고 간다 해도, 저는 여전히 이렇게 말하겠습니다. '아니오, 내 딸은 죄가 없소. 우리 집에는 범죄가 없소. 나는 우리 집의 범죄를 인정하지 않겠소. 범죄가 한 집에 들어올 때는 죽음과도 같아서, 결코 혼자 오지 않으니까.' 들어 보세요. 제가 살해당한들 선생에게 무슨 상관입니까? 선생은 제 친구입니까? 선생은 사람입니까? 선생에게 심장이 있습니까? 아니지요, 선생은 의사일 뿐입니다! 자, 분명히 말씀드립니다. 저는 제 딸을 법정으로 끌고 가 사형 집행인에게 넘기지 않겠습니다! 그런 생각만으로도 저는 죽을 것 같습니다. 미친 사람처럼 손톱으로 제 심장을 후벼 파게 될 겁니다! 그리고 만일 선생이 틀렸다면, 의사 선생, 만일 제 딸이 아니라면, 만일 어느 날 제가 유령처럼 창백한 얼굴로 선생을 찾아가 '살인자, 당신이 내 아이를 죽였소!' 하고 말하게 된다면…… 멈추세요, 만일 그런 일이 일어난다면, 저는 기독교인이지만, 다브리니 씨, 저는 스스로 목숨을 끊을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"좋습니다.\" 잠시 침묵한 뒤 의사가 말했다. \"기다리지요.\"\n\n빌포르가 의사의 말을 의심하기라도 하는 듯 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"다만,\" 다브리니 씨가 느리고 엄숙한 어조로 말을 이었다. \"댁에서 누군가 병이 들거나, 검사님 자신이 공격당했다고 느끼시더라도, 저를 부르지 마십시오. 다시는 오지 않을 테니까요. 이 끔찍한 비밀을 검사님과 함께 나누는 것은 받아들이겠습니다. 하지만 댁에서 범죄와 불행이 자라나듯, 제 양심 속에서 수치와 회한이 자라나 커지도록 두지는 않겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 저를 버리시는 겁니까, 의사 선생?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다. 저는 더 이상 검사님을 따라갈 수 없고, 처형대 발치에서 멈출 뿐입니다. 어떤 새로운 발견이 이루어져, 이 끔찍한 비극이 막을 내리게 될 겁니다. 그럼 안녕히 계십시오.\"\n\n\"부탁입니다, 의사 선생!\"\n\n\"제 머릿속을 어지럽히는 그 모든 참혹함이 댁을 끔찍하고 흉한 집으로 만듭니다. 안녕히 계십시오, 검사님.\"\n\n\"한마디만, 한마디만 더요, 의사 선생! 선생은 떠나면서, 제게 밝혀 주신 사실로 제 처지의 공포를 더해 놓고는, 저를 그 온갖 공포 속에 남겨 두고 가십니다. 그런데 그 가엾은 늙은 하인의 갑작스러운 죽음은 어떻게 알릴까요?\"\n\n\"옳은 말씀입니다.\" 다브리니 씨가 말했다. \"다시 안으로 들어갑시다.\"\n\n의사가 앞서 나갔고, 빌포르 씨가 뒤따랐다. 겁에 질린 하인들이 의사가 지나갈 계단과 복도에 모여 있었다.\n\n\"여러분,\" 다브리니가 모두 들을 수 있도록 큰 소리로 빌포르에게 말했다. \"가엾은 바루아는 요즘 너무 앉아서만 지냈습니다. 예전에는 말을 타거나 마차로 유럽 구석구석을 누비던 사람인데, 그 안락의자 주위만 단조롭게 맴돌다 보니 목숨을 잃은 겁니다. 피가 걸쭉해진 거지요. 몸이 비대했고, 목이 짧고 굵었습니다. 뇌졸중이 그를 덮쳤는데, 제가 너무 늦게 불려 왔습니다. 그런데,\" 그가 나직한 목소리로 덧붙였다. \"그 제비꽃 시럽 잔은 잿더미 속에 버려 두도록 하십시오.\"\n\n의사는 빌포르와 악수도 나누지 않고, 방금 한 말에 한마디도 보태지 않은 채, 온 집안의 눈물과 통곡 속을 지나 밖으로 나갔다. 그날 저녁, 부엌에 모여 오랫동안 의논을 나눈 빌포르의 하인들이 모두 빌포르 부인에게 와서 떠나고 싶다고 말했다. 어떤 간청도, 봉급을 올려 주겠다는 제안도 그들을 붙잡아 둘 수 없었다. 무슨 말을 해도 그들은 이렇게 대답할 뿐이었다. \"저희는 떠나야 합니다. 이 집에는 죽음이 있으니까요.\"\n\n그들은 그토록 좋은 주인 내외를, 특히 그토록 착하고 다정하고 상냥한 발랑틴 아가씨를 두고 떠나는 것을 못내 아쉬워하면서도, 기도와 간청에도 아랑곳없이 모두 떠나갔다.\n\n하인들이 그렇게 말하는 동안 빌포르는 발랑틴을 바라보았다. 그 아이는 눈물을 흘리고 있었다. 그런데 이상하게도, 그 눈물을 보며 가슴이 미어지는 와중에도, 그는 빌포르 부인 쪽으로도 눈길을 돌렸다. 그러자 그 여인의 얇은 입술 위로 음울한 미소가 살짝 스쳐 지나간 것만 같았다. 폭풍우 치는 하늘에서 두 구름 사이로 불길하게 지나가는 유성처럼.\n\n# Chapter 81. The Room of the Retired Baker\n\n모르세르 백작이 약혼 계획이 거절당한 데 수치와 분노를 느끼며 당글라르의 집을 떠난 그날 저녁, 안드레아 카발칸티는 곱슬머리를 매만지고 콧수염을 흠잡을 데 없이 다듬고 손에 꼭 맞는 흰 장갑을 낀 차림으로 쇼세당탱 거리에 있는 은행가의 저택 안뜰로 들어섰다. 응접실에 들어선 지 십 분도 채 되지 않아 그는 당글라르를 활 모양 창의 움푹한 곳으로 끌고 가, 교묘한 서두를 늘어놓은 뒤 고귀한 아버지가 떠난 이후 자신이 겪은 온갖 근심과 걱정을 털어놓았다. 그는 은행가의 가족이 자신을 아들처럼 받아 준 그 지극한 친절에 감사를 표했고, 게다가 당글라르 양에게서 가장 뜨거운 애정을 쏟을 대상을 찾았노라고 고백했다.\n\n당글라르는 더없이 깊은 주의를 기울여 들었다. 그는 지난 이삼 일 동안 이 고백을 기다려 온 터였고, 마침내 그 말이 나오자 모르세르의 말을 들을 때 내리깔았던 그 눈만큼이나 두 눈이 반짝였다. 그러나 그는 젊은이의 청에 곧바로 응하지는 않고, 양심적인 척 몇 가지 이의를 제기했다.\n\n\"안드레아 군, 결혼을 생각하기엔 좀 젊지 않은가?\"\n\n\"그렇지 않다고 봅니다.\" 카발칸티가 대답했다. \"이탈리아에서는 귀족들이 대개 일찍 결혼합니다. 인생이란 워낙 불확실한 것이니, 행복이 손닿는 곳에 있을 때 붙잡아야 하지요.\"\n\n\"그렇군.\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"내게는 영광스러운 자네의 청혼이 만약 아내와 딸에게 받아들여진다면, 사전 절차는 누가 처리하게 되는 건가? 이렇게 중대한 협상은 내 생각에 두 집안의 아버지들이 직접 진행하는 것이 마땅할 텐데.\"\n\n\"제 아버지는 대단히 멀리 내다보고 신중하신 분입니다. 제가 프랑스에 정착하고 싶어 할지도 모른다고 생각하셔서, 떠나실 때 제 신분을 증명하는 서류들과 함께 편지 한 통을 남겨 주셨습니다. 제 선택을 승낙하신다면 결혼하는 날부터 해마다 십오만 리브르를 주겠다고 약속하는 편지였지요. 제가 판단하기로는, 그것이 아버지 수입의 사분의 일쯤 되리라 짐작합니다.\"\n\n\"나는,\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"늘 딸에게 지참금으로 오십만 프랑을 줄 작정이었네. 게다가 그 아이는 내 유일한 상속자이고 말일세.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 남작 부인과 따님만 동의하신다면 모든 일이 수월하게 풀리겠군요. 우리는 해마다 십칠만 오천 리브르의 수입을 가지게 됩니다. 게다가 만약 제가 후작께 제 자본을 직접 내어 달라고 설득할 수 있다면 — 그럴 가능성은 낮지만 아예 불가능한 것은 아니지요 — 그 이삼백만 프랑을 당글라르 씨 손에 맡길 수 있을 겁니다. 당글라르 씨의 수완이라면 그것으로 십 퍼센트의 수익을 낼 수도 있겠지요.\"\n\n\"나는 사 퍼센트 이상은 절대 주지 않고, 대개 삼 퍼센트 반밖에 안 주네. 하지만 사위에게라면 오 퍼센트를 주고, 수익은 나눠 갖도록 하지.\"\n\n\"아주 좋습니다, 장인어른.\" 카발칸티가 말했다. 그는 귀족적인 광택으로 감추려 애쓰던 천한 본성이 이따금 새어 나오는 것을 막지 못했다. 그는 곧바로 말을 바로잡았다. \"실례했습니다. 그저 희망만으로도 제정신이 아닐 지경인데, 현실이 되면 어떻겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그런데,\" 당글라르가 말했다. 그 또한 처음에는 사심 없이 시작된 대화가 어느새 사업 거래로 바뀌고 있다는 것을 알아차리지 못했다. \"분명 자네 재산 가운데 아버지가 거절할 수 없는 몫이 있을 텐데?\"\n\n\"어떤 것 말씀이십니까?\" 젊은이가 물었다.\n\n\"어머니에게서 물려받은 것 말일세.\"\n\n\"그렇군요, 제 어머니 레오노라 코르시나리에게서 물려받은 것이지요.\"\n\n\"얼마나 되는가?\"\n\"실은,\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"그 문제는 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없습니다만, 적어도 이백만 프랑은 되리라 짐작합니다.\"\n\n당글라르는 잃어버린 보물을 찾은 구두쇠처럼, 또는 자신을 삼키리라 여겼던 심연 대신 단단한 땅을 디딘 난파 선원처럼 기쁨에 휩싸였다.\n\n\"자, 그렇다면,\" 안드레아가 은행가에게 정중히 고개 숙이며 말했다. \"희망을 품어도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"희망을 품어도 좋을 뿐 아니라,\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"자네 쪽에서 아무런 장애만 생기지 않는다면, 다 정해진 일로 여겨도 좋네.\"\n\n\"정말로 기쁩니다.\" 안드레아가 말했다.\n\n\"그런데,\" 당글라르가 생각에 잠겨 말했다. \"자네 후원자인 몬테크리스토 백작이 자네를 위해 직접 청혼하지 않은 것은 어찌 된 일인가?\"\n\n안드레아의 얼굴이 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 살짝 붉어졌다.\n\n\"방금 백작을 뵙고 오는 길입니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"참으로 유쾌한 분이지만 생각하는 방식이 도무지 이해할 수 없을 만큼 별난 분이지요. 저를 무척 높이 평가하십니다. 제 아버지가 재산의 이자가 아니라 자본 자체를 제게 내어 주리라는 데 추호도 의심이 없다고까지 말씀하셨습니다. 그것을 얻을 수 있도록 영향력을 써 주겠노라 약속하셨지요. 하지만 남을 위해 청혼하는 책임은 한 번도 떠맡은 적이 없고 앞으로도 결코 그러지 않겠다고 분명히 말씀하셨습니다. 그래도 공정하게 한마디 덧붙이자면, 그런 일을 꺼리는 마음을 후회한 적이 있다면 바로 이번이라고 하셨습니다. 이 혼사가 행복하고 어울리는 결합이 되리라 여기셨기 때문이지요. 게다가 공식적으로는 아무것도 하지 않으시겠지만, 당글라르 씨가 물어보시는 것에는 무엇이든 답해 주실 겁니다. 자, 그럼 이제,\" 그가 더없이 매혹적인 미소를 띠며 말을 이었다. \"장인어른과의 이야기는 끝났으니, 은행가께 말씀드려야겠군요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그 은행가에게 무슨 할 말이 있는가?\" 당글라르가 이번에는 자기가 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"모레쯤 약 사천 프랑을 인출해야 할 것 같습니다. 다만 백작께서 제 독신자 수입으로는 다음 달 지출을 감당하기 어려우리라 보시고, 이만 프랑짜리 어음을 주셨습니다. 보시다시피 백작의 서명이 있으니, 그것으로 충분하지요.\"\n\n\"이런 어음을 백만 프랑어치 가져오게.\" 당글라르가 어음을 주머니에 넣으며 말했다. \"그러면 나야 기쁠 따름이지. 내일 편한 시각을 정하게. 우리 회계원이 팔만 프랑짜리 수표를 들고 자네를 찾아갈 테니.\"\n\n\"그럼 괜찮으시다면 열 시로 하지요. 내일 시골에 다녀올 참이라 이른 시각이 좋겠습니다.\"\n\n\"좋아, 열 시로 하지. 아직 프랭스 호텔에 묵고 있나?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n이튿날 아침, 은행가는 늘 그렇듯 정확히 약속을 지켜 팔만 프랑을 젊은이의 손에 건넸다. 안드레아는 카드루스 앞으로 이백 프랑을 남겨 둔 뒤 막 출발하려던 참이었다. 그는 주로 이 위험한 적을 피하려고 집을 나섰고, 저녁에도 되도록 늦게 돌아왔다.\n\n그러나 마차에서 내리기가 무섭게 수위가 손에 꾸러미 하나를 들고 그를 맞았다.\n\n\"나리,\" 그가 말했다. \"그 사람이 다녀갔습니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 사람 말인가?\" 안드레아가 무심하게 말했다. 너무도 잘 기억하고 있는 자를 까맣게 잊은 척하면서.\n\n\"나리께서 그 적은 연금을 보내 주시는 사람 말입니다.\"\n\n\"아,\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"내 아버지의 옛 하인 말이군. 그래, 내가 남겨 둔 이백 프랑은 그에게 주었나?\"\n\n\"네, 각하.\" 안드레아는 그렇게 불러 달라고 일러두었던 것이다. \"그런데,\" 수위가 말을 이었다. \"그가 받으려 하질 않았습니다.\"\n\n안드레아의 얼굴이 창백해졌으나, 어두운 탓에 그 핏기 가신 얼굴은 눈에 띄지 않았다. \"뭐라고? 받으려 하지 않았다고?\" 그가 살짝 동요하며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 각하와 직접 이야기하고 싶다고 했습니다. 제가 외출하셨다고 말하니, 한참 실랑이를 벌인 끝에야 제 말을 믿고는 이미 봉인해서 가져온 이 편지를 제게 맡기더군요.\"\n\n\"이리 주게.\" 안드레아가 말하고는 마차 등불에 비추어 읽었다.\n\n\"'자네는 내가 사는 곳을 알지. 내일 아침 아홉 시에 자네를 기다리겠네.'\"\n\n안드레아는 편지가 뜯긴 흔적이 있는지, 혹시 누군가의 경솔한 눈이 그 내용을 들여다본 것은 아닌지 확인하려고 꼼꼼히 살폈다. 그러나 어찌나 정성스럽게 접혀 있던지 아무도 읽었을 리 없었고, 봉인도 멀쩡했다.\n\n\"좋아.\" 그가 말했다. \"가엾은 사람, 참 갸륵한 자로군.\" 그는 이 말을 곱씹도록 수위를 남겨 두고 떠났고, 수위는 주인과 하인 중 누구를 더 우러러봐야 할지 알 수 없었다.\n\n\"어서 말들을 풀어 놓고 내 방으로 올라오게.\" 안드레아가 마부에게 말했다. 젊은이는 눈 깜짝할 새에 자기 방에 이르러 카드루스의 편지를 태웠다. 그 일을 막 끝냈을 때 하인이 들어왔다.\n\n\"피에르, 자네 키가 나와 비슷하지.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"영광입니다, 각하.\"\n\n\"어제 새 제복을 받았지?\"\n\n\"네, 나리.\"\n\n\"오늘 저녁에 예쁜 아가씨와 약속이 있는데, 누군지 들키고 싶지 않네. 내일까지 자네 제복을 좀 빌려주게. 어쩌면 여관에서 자게 될지도 모르겠어.\"\n\n피에르는 시키는 대로 했다. 오 분 뒤, 안드레아는 완전히 변장한 채로 호텔을 나서 이륜마차를 잡아타고는 마부에게 픽퓌스에 있는 슈발루주로 가자고 일렀다. 이튿날 아침, 그는 프랭스 호텔에서 그랬듯 아무에게도 들키지 않고 그 여관을 나서, 생탕투안 변두리를 따라 내려가 큰길을 거쳐 메닐몽탕 거리로 접어들었다. 그러고는 왼편 세 번째 집 문 앞에 멈춰, 수위가 없는 터라 물어볼 사람을 찾았다.\n\n\"누굴 찾으세요, 젊은이?\" 길 건너편의 과일 장수 여자가 물었다.\n\n\"파일레탱 씨를 찾는데요, 부인.\" 안드레아가 대답했다.\n\n\"퇴직한 빵집 주인 말인가요?\" 과일 장수가 물었다.\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다.\"\n\"안마당 끝, 왼쪽, 사 층에 살아요.\"\n\n안드레아는 일러 준 대로 갔고, 사 층에서 토끼발 모양의 초인종 손잡이를 발견했다. 그가 그것을 잡아당기는 다급한 소리로 보아, 상당히 언짢은 기분이라는 것이 분명했다. 잠시 후 문에 난 쇠창살 너머로 카드루스의 얼굴이 나타났다.\n\n\"아! 시간을 잘 지켰군.\" 그가 문을 열며 말했다.\n\n\"빌어먹을, 자네와 자네의 그 시간 타령 같으니!\" 안드레아가 말하며 의자에 털썩 주저앉았다. 차라리 그 의자를 집주인 머리통에 집어 던지고 싶다는 듯한 태도였다.\n\n\"이봐, 이봐, 친구, 화내지 말게. 보게나, 내가 자네 생각을 했다네. 우리가 먹을 이 근사한 아침을 좀 보라고. 죄다 자네가 좋아하는 것뿐이야.\"\n\n과연 안드레아는 무언가 익어 가는 냄새를 맡았고, 시장하던 터라 그것이 싫지만은 않았다. 그것은 하급 프로방스풍 부엌 특유의 기름과 마늘 냄새에 말린 생선 냄새가 더해지고, 무엇보다 사향과 정향의 톡 쏘는 냄새가 어우러진 것이었다. 그 냄새들은 뚜껑을 덮어 화덕 위에 올려놓은 깊은 접시 두 개와, 낡은 무쇠솥 속에 든 구리 냄비에서 새어 나왔다. 옆방에는 제법 깨끗한 식탁이 둘을 위해 차려진 것도 보였다. 봉인된 포도주 두 병이 놓여 있었는데 하나는 초록색, 하나는 노란색으로 봉해져 있었고, 유리병에 담긴 브랜디와 양배추 잎에 담은 과일이 질그릇 접시 위에 솜씨 좋게 담겨 있었다.\n\n\"어떤가, 친구?\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"응, 냄새 좋지! 내가 한때 솜씨 좋은 요리사였던 거 알잖나. 자네가 손가락을 쪽쪽 빨던 거 기억나나? 자네는 내 요리를 맨 처음 맛본 축에 들었고, 제법 입에 맞아 했지.\" 카드루스는 그렇게 말하면서 양파를 새로 까기 시작했다.\n\n\"그런데,\" 안드레아가 언짢게 말했다. \"정말이지, 고작 자네와 아침이나 먹자고 날 불러낸 거라면, 차라리 악마가 자네를 데려갔으면 좋겠군!\"\n\n\"이 사람아,\" 카드루스가 점잔 빼며 말했다. \"먹으면서도 얘기는 할 수 있는 법이지. 그리고, 이 배은망덕한 친구야, 옛 친구를 보는 게 그리 반갑지 않은가? 나는 기뻐서 눈물이 다 나는데.\"\n\n그는 정말로 울고 있었으나, 퐁뒤가르의 옛 여관 주인의 눈물샘을 가장 크게 자극한 것이 기쁨인지 양파인지는 가려내기 어려웠을 것이다.\n\n\"입 다물게, 위선자 같으니.\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"자네가 날 사랑한다고?\"\n\n\"그래, 사랑하지. 거짓말이면 악마가 날 잡아가도 좋네. 그게 약점인 줄은 알아.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"하지만 어쩔 수가 없어.\"\n\n\"그러면서도 날 불러내 무슨 수작을 부리는 건 막지 못했군그래.\"\n\n\"이봐.\" 카드루스가 앞치마에 큼직한 칼을 닦으며 말했다. \"내가 자넬 좋아하지 않았다면, 자네가 내게 떠안긴 이 비참한 삶을 견뎌 냈겠나? 잠깐 생각해 보게. 자네는 하인의 옷을 입었으니, 하인을 두고 있는 거야. 나는 하인이 없어 끼니도 직접 차려야 하지. 자네는 프랭스 호텔이나 카페 드 파리에서 정찬을 드니까 내 요리를 깔보지. 그래, 나도 하인을 둘 수 있고, 마차도 부릴 수 있고, 어디서든 마음대로 식사할 수 있어. 그런데 왜 안 그러겠나? 우리 어린 베네데토를 성가시게 하고 싶지 않아서지. 자, 인정하게. 나도 그럴 수 있다는 걸, 응?\"\n\n이 말에는 도무지 알아듣기 어렵지 않은 눈빛이 함께 따라붙었다.\n\n\"좋아.\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"자네 애정을 인정한다 치고, 그래서 왜 나더러 같이 아침을 먹자는 거지?\"\n\n\"자네를 보는 즐거움을 누리고 싶어서지, 친구.\"\n\n\"우리가 할 얘기를 다 끝낸 마당에 날 봐서 뭐 하게?\"\n\n\"이런, 친구.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"유언장에 부록이 안 붙는 법이 있나? 어쨌든 자네가 먼저 아침을 먹으러 온 거잖나? 자, 앉아서 이 정어리하고 이 신선한 버터부터 들지. 자네 좋으라고 포도 잎 위에 얹어 놓았다고, 못된 친구야. 아, 그래, 자네 내 방을 둘러보는군. 짚의자 네 개에, 한 개에 삼 프랑짜리 그림들이라니. 하지만 뭘 기대하나? 여긴 프랭스 호텔이 아니야.\"\n\n\"이봐, 자네 갈수록 불만이 늘어 가는군. 이제는 행복하지도 않고 말이야. 퇴직한 빵집 주인처럼 사는 게 소원이라더니.\"\n\n카드루스가 한숨을 내쉬었다.\n\n\"이런, 무슨 할 말이 있나? 자네 꿈이 이루어진 걸 두 눈으로 봤잖나.\"\n\n\"그래도 꿈이라고밖에 못 하겠어. 퇴직한 빵집 주인이라면, 가엾은 베네데토, 부자라야지. 연금이 있어야 한단 말일세.\"\n\n\"이런, 자네한테도 연금이 있잖나.\"\n\n\"내가?\"\n\n\"그래, 내가 자네한테 이백 프랑을 가져다주니 말이야.\"\n\n카드루스가 어깨를 으쓱했다.\n\n\"굴욕적이야.\" 그가 말했다. \"이렇게 마지못해 내주는 돈을, 언제 끊길지 모를 불안한 돈을 받는다는 게 말이야. 보다시피 나는 절약을 해야 해. 자네 형편이 기울 경우를 대비해서지. 그래, 친구, 운명이란 변덕스러운 거야, 연대 군목이 말했듯이 말일세. 자네 형편이 대단하다는 건 알지, 이 악당아. 자네는 당글라르의 딸과 결혼할 거잖나.\"\n\n\"뭐? 당글라르의 딸과?\"\n\n\"그래, 그렇고말고. 당글라르 남작이라고 해야 하나? 그럼 나도 베네데토 백작이라고 하지 뭐. 그자는 내 오랜 친구였어. 기억력이 그렇게 나쁘지만 않다면 자네 결혼식에 날 초대해야 마땅하지. 내 결혼식엔 왔으니까 말이야. 그래, 그래, 내 결혼식에 왔다고. 제기랄, 그때는 그렇게 거만하지 않았어. 그 선량한 모렐 씨 밑에서 일하는 하급 사무원이었으니까. 나는 그자랑 모르세르 백작이랑 여러 번 같이 식사했어. 그러니 보다시피 내게도 지체 높은 인맥이 좀 있는 거지. 내가 그걸 조금만 가꾸면 같은 응접실에서 마주칠 수도 있을걸.\"\n\n\"이봐, 자네 질투 때문에 모든 게 비뚤어져 보이는 거야.\"\n\n\"말이야 그럴듯하지, 내 사랑 베네데토. 하지만 나는 내가 무슨 말을 하는지 알아. 어쩌면 언젠가 내 가장 좋은 코트를 차려입고, 그 큰 대문 앞에 나타나 나 자신을 소개할지도 모르지. 그동안은 앉아서 먹기나 하세.\"\n\n카드루스는 앞장서서 왕성한 식욕으로 아침을 공략하며, 손님 앞에 내놓는 요리마다 칭찬을 늘어놓았다. 손님은 체념한 듯 보였다. 그는 마개를 뽑고, 마늘과 기름을 곁들인 생선을 푸짐하게 먹었다.\n\n\"아, 친구.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"옛 집주인하고 사이가 좀 좋아지는군!\"\n\n\"그래, 정말이지.\" 안드레아가 대답했다. 그의 허기가 다른 모든 감정을 눌렀던 것이다.\n\n\"맛이 좋단 말이지, 이 악동아?\"\n\n\"어찌나 좋은지, 이렇게 요리하는 사람이 어떻게 살림살이가 힘들다고 투덜대는지 모르겠어.\"\n\n\"보게나.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"내 행복은 단 한 가지 생각 때문에 다 망쳐진다네.\"\n\n\"그게 뭔데?\"\n\n\"내가 남에게 기대어 산다는 거지. 평생 정직하게 제 밥벌이를 해 온 이 몸이 말이야.\"\n\n\"그런 걸로 마음 졸이지 말게. 내겐 둘이 먹고도 남을 만큼 있으니까.\"\n\n\"아니야, 정말이야. 믿거나 말거나, 매달 말이면 나는 양심의 가책에 시달려.\"\n\n\"착한 카드루스 같으니!\"\n\n\"어찌나 시달리는지, 어제는 그 이백 프랑을 받지 않으려 했다네.\"\n\n\"그래, 자네 나하고 얘기하고 싶었다고 했지. 한데 정말로 그게 양심의 가책 때문이었나, 말해 보게?\"\n\n\"진짜 양심의 가책 때문이었어. 게다가, 한 가지 생각이 떠올랐거든.\"\n\n안드레아가 몸을 떨었다. 그는 카드루스의 '생각'이라는 말에 늘 이렇게 떨었다.\n\n\"비참한 일이지, 안 그래? 늘 월말까지 기다려야 하다니 말이야.\"\n\n\"이런.\" 안드레아가 짐짓 철학자인 양 말했다. 그는 상대를 면밀히 살피기로 작정한 터였다. \"인생이란 게 기다림 속에 흘러가는 것 아닌가? 가령 나라고 형편이 더 낫겠나? 그래도 나는 참을성 있게 기다리잖나, 안 그래?\"\n\n\"그래. 하지만 자네는 비참한 이백 프랑을 기다리는 게 아니라 오천이나 육천, 어쩌면 만, 어쩌면 만 이천을 기다리지. 자네는 그 최대치를 누구에게도 알리지 않으려고 조심하니까. 거기 있을 때도 자네는 늘 자잘한 선물이며 크리스마스 용돈을 받았고, 그걸 가엾은 친구 카드루스한테 숨기려 했지. 다행히도 그 친구 카드루스가 약은 사람이라서 말이야.\"\n\n\"또 시작이군. 또 옛날 얘기를 자꾸자꾸 늘어놓는단 말이야! 한데 그 일을 다시 죄다 들먹이며 날 괴롭혀서 뭐 하자는 건가?\"\n\n\"아, 자네는 겨우 스물한 살이니 과거를 잊을 수 있지. 나는 쉰이니 그걸 기억할 수밖에 없어. 하지만 본론으로 돌아가세.\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n\"내가 하려던 말은, 내가 자네 처지라면 말이야……\"\n\n\"그래서?\"\n\n\"현금으로 바꿔 두겠어……\"\n\n\"어떻게 바꾸겠다는 거지?\"\n\n\"농장을 하나 살 수 있다는 핑계로 여섯 달치를 미리 받아 내겠어. 그러고는 그 여섯 달치를 챙겨서 줄행랑을 치는 거지.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래.\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"나쁜 생각은 아니군.\"\n\n\"친구.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"내 빵을 먹었으면 내 충고도 받아들이게. 몸으로 보나 마음으로 보나 손해 볼 것 없을 테니.\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"자네는 왜 자네가 내게 준 그 충고대로 하지 않나? 자네는 왜 여섯 달치, 아니 일 년 치라도 미리 받아 내서 브뤼셀로 물러나지 않는 거지? 퇴직한 빵집 주인으로 사느니, 파산자의 특권을 누리며 파산자로 살 수도 있을 텐데. 그게 아주 좋을 것 같은데 말이야.\"\n\n\"한데 빌어먹을, 천이백 프랑으로 어떻게 물러나라는 건가?\"\n\n\"아, 카드루스.\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"자네 욕심도 참 많구먼! 두 달 전만 해도 굶어 죽어 가던 사람이.\"\n\n\"식욕이란 먹을수록 자라는 법이야.\" 카드루스가 이를 드러내며 씩 웃었다. 웃는 원숭이나 으르렁대는 호랑이 같은 모습이었다. \"그리고,\" 그가 그 크고 흰 이로 빵 한 덩이를 큼직하게 베어 물며 덧붙였다. \"내가 계획을 하나 세웠다네.\"\n\n카드루스의 '계획'은 그의 '생각'보다 안드레아를 한층 더 불안하게 했다. 생각이 씨앗에 불과하다면, 계획은 현실이었다.\n\n\"어디 그 계획 좀 들어 보세. 분명 그럴듯한 거겠지.\"\n\n\"왜 아니겠나? 우리가 ○○ 시설을 빠져나온 그 계획을 누가 세웠더라? 응? 그게 나 아니었나? 게다가 우리가 이렇게 여기 멀쩡히 있는 걸 보면 제법 괜찮은 계획이었던 셈이지!\"\n\n\"자네가 좋은 계획을 한 번도 못 세운다는 말은 아니야.\" 안드레아가 대답했다. \"어쨌든 그 계획이나 들어 보세.\"\n\n\"좋아.\" 카드루스가 말을 이었다. \"자네, 한 푼도 들이지 않고, 내가 만 오천 프랑을 손에 넣을 길을 마련해 줄 수 있겠나? 아니, 만 오천으로는 모자라. 삼만 프랑은 있어야 다시 정직한 사람이 될 수 있어.\"\n\n\"안 돼.\" 안드레아가 무뚝뚝하게 대답했다. \"안 돼, 못 하겠어.\"\n\n\"자네가 내 말을 못 알아들은 것 같군.\" 카드루스가 차분하게 대답했다. \"자네는 한 푼도 들이지 않아도 된다고 했잖나.\"\n\n\"내가 강도질을 해서, 내 행운을 — 그리고 내 행운과 함께 자네 행운까지 — 다 망쳐 버리고, 우리 둘이 다시 거기로 끌려가기라도 바라나?\"\n\n\"나야 다시 잡혀가도 별 상관없어.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"나는 혼자 사는 게 영 못 견디는 가엾은 인간이라, 가끔 옛 동료들이 그립거든. 그자들을 다시는 안 보면 좋겠다고 여길 자네 같은 매정한 인간과는 다르지.\"\n\n이번에 안드레아는 떨기만 한 것이 아니라 얼굴까지 새파래졌다.\n\n\"이봐, 카드루스, 허튼소리 말게!\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"겁먹지 말게, 내 어린 베네데토. 그저 자네 도움 없이 그 삼만 프랑을 손에 넣을 방법을 좀 일러 주게. 그럼 내가 알아서 꾸려 볼 테니.\"\n\n\"그래, 어디 보자고. 무슨 수가 없는지 궁리해 보지.\" 안드레아가 말했다.\n\n\"그동안 내 월 수당은 오백 프랑으로 올려 주겠지, 친구? 한 가지 바람이 생겼는데, 가정부를 하나 두려고.\"\n\n\"좋아, 오백 프랑을 받게 해 주지.\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"하지만 나로서는 무척 힘든 일이야, 가엾은 카드루스. 자네 자꾸 약점을 잡아……\"\n\n\"흥.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"자네는 셀 수도 없이 많은 곳간에 손을 댈 수 있으면서 뭘.\"\n\n안드레아가 상대의 말을 미리 예상하고 있었다고 해도 좋을 만큼, 그의 눈이 번개처럼 번뜩였다. 그러나 그것도 한순간뿐이었다.\n\n\"맞아.\" 그가 대답했다. \"내 후원자는 무척 너그러운 분이지.\"\n\n\"그 인자하신 후원자께서,\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"자네한테 매달 얼마나 주시는데?\"\n\n\"오천 프랑이야.\"\n\n\"자네가 내게 백 프랑 주는 만큼 그분은 자네에게 천 프랑씩 주는군! 정말이지, 이렇게 운 좋은 건 사생아들뿐이라니까. 매달 오천 프랑이라! 빌어먹을, 그 많은 돈으로 대체 뭘 하나?\"\n\n\"아, 그 정도 쓰는 거야 일도 아니지. 게다가 나도 자네처럼 목돈이 필요하거든.\"\n\n\"목돈이라…… 그래…… 알지…… 누군들 목돈이 갖고 싶지 않겠나.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래서 나는 그걸 손에 넣을 거야.\"\n\n\"누가 자네한테 주는데? 그 자네 영주께서?\"\n\n\"그래, 내 영주께서. 하지만 안타깝게도 기다려야 해.\"\n\n\"무얼 기다린다는 거지?\" 카드루스가 물었다.\n\n\"그분이 죽기를.\"\n\n\"자네 영주가 죽기를?\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n\"어째서?\"\n\n\"그분이 나를 수혜자로 유언장을 작성했거든.\"\n\n\"정말로?\"\n\n\"맹세코.\"\n\n\"얼마나?\"\n\n\"오십만이야.\"\n\n\"그게 다야? 그건 너무 적은데.\"\n\n\"하지만 그렇다네.\"\n\n\"아니, 그럴 리가!\"\n\n\"자네 내 친구지, 카드루스?\"\n\n\"그럼, 죽으나 사나.\"\n\n\"그럼 비밀 하나를 말해 주지.\"\n\n\"뭔데?\"\n\n\"하지만 명심하게……\"\n\n\"아! 제기랄, 잉어처럼 입 꾹 다물지.\"\n\n\"그게 말이야, 내 생각엔……\"\n\n안드레아가 말을 멈추고 주위를 둘러보았다.\n\n\"생각엔? 걱정 말게. 제기랄, 우리뿐이잖나.\"\n\n\"내 진짜 아버지를 찾아낸 것 같아.\"\n\n\"자네 진짜 아버지를?\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n\"늙은 카발칸티 말고?\"\n\n\"아니야, 그자는 또 떠나 버렸으니까. 자네 말마따나, 진짜 아버지 말이야.\"\n\n\"그래서 그 아버지가……\"\n\n\"그게 말이야, 카드루스, 몬테크리스토라네.\"\n\n\"설마!\"\n\n\"그래, 알겠나, 그러면 다 설명이 되지. 보아하니 그분은 날 드러내 놓고 아들로 인정할 수가 없어서, 카발칸티 씨를 내세워 그렇게 하고, 그 대가로 오만 프랑을 주는 거야.\"\n\n\"자네 아버지 노릇 하는 값으로 오만 프랑이라고? 나라면 그 절반에도, 이만에도, 만 오천에도 했을 텐데. 왜 날 생각하지 않았나, 이 배은망덕한 사람아?\"\n\n\"그게 다 내가 거기 있을 때 끝난 일인데, 내가 어찌 알았겠나?\"\n\n\"아, 정말로? 그래서 그 유언장에 따라……\"\n\n\"내게 오십만 리브르를 남기시는 거지.\"\n\n\"확실해?\"\n\n\"내게 직접 보여 주셨어. 한데 그게 다가 아니야. 아까 말했듯이 부록이 있거든.\"\n\n\"그럴 만도 하지.\"\n\n\"그리고 그 부록에서 나를 아들로 인정하셨어.\"\n\n\"오, 인자하신 아버지, 갸륵하신 아버지, 더없이 정직하신 아버지로다!\" 카드루스가 양손 사이에서 접시를 빙글빙글 돌리며 말했다.\n\n\"자, 내가 자네한테 뭐라도 숨기는 게 있던가?\"\n\n\"없어. 그렇게 털어놓으니 내 보기에 자네는 명예로운 사람이야. 그래, 그 영주 아버지께서는 부자신가? 아주 큰 부자야?\"\n\n\"그럼, 그렇고말고. 자기 재산이 얼마나 되는지 본인도 몰라.\"\n\n\"그게 가능한가?\"\n\n\"늘 그분 댁에 드나드는 내게는 충분히 분명한 사실이야. 며칠 전에는 은행 사무원이 자네 접시만 한 서류첩에 오만 프랑을 담아 가져왔어. 어제는 그분 거래 은행가가 금화로 십만 프랑을 가져왔고.\"\n\n카드루스는 경탄에 사로잡혔다. 젊은이의 말이 그에게는 쇳소리처럼 들렸고, 루이 금화가 폭포처럼 쏟아져 내리는 소리가 들리는 듯했다.\n\n\"그래서 자네가 그 집에 드나든단 말이지?\" 그가 잽싸게 외쳤다.\n\n\"내키는 대로.\"\n\n카드루스는 잠시 생각에 잠겼다. 그가 머릿속으로 무언가 불길한 궁리를 굴리고 있다는 것은 쉽게 알아챌 수 있었다. 그러더니 불쑥 말했다.\n\n\"나도 그걸 다 좀 봤으면 좋겠군!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"얼마나 근사할까!\"\n\n\"사실 정말 으리으리하지.\" 안드레아가 말했다.\n\n\"샹젤리제에 살지 않나?\"\n\"그래, 30번지야.\"\n\n\"아.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"30번지라.\"\n\n\"그래, 안뜰과 정원 사이에 외따로 서 있는 멋진 집이야. 자네도 알 만한 곳이지.\"\n\n\"그럴지도. 하지만 내가 마음 쓰는 건 겉모습이 아니야, 안이지. 그 안에 얼마나 멋진 가구들이 있을까!\"\n\n\"자네 튀일리궁을 본 적 있나?\"\n\n\"아니.\"\n\n\"음, 그보다 더하다네.\"\n\n\"그 인자하신 몬테크리스토 씨가 지갑을 떨어뜨리거든, 안드레아, 그걸 주우려고 허리 굽힐 만하겠는걸.\"\n\n\"그걸 기다릴 것까지도 없어.\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"그 집에는 과수원의 과일만큼이나 돈이 흔하니까.\"\n\n\"한데 자네 언제 한번 날 거기 데려가 주게.\"\n\n\"내가 어떻게? 무슨 핑계로?\"\n\n\"하긴 그렇군. 하지만 자네가 내 군침을 돌게 했어. 무슨 일이 있어도 꼭 봐야겠어. 방법을 찾아내고 말 거야.\"\n\n\"허튼소리 말게, 카드루스!\"\n\n\"마룻바닥 닦는 일꾼으로 나서지 뭐.\"\n\n\"방마다 죄다 양탄자가 깔려 있어.\"\n\n\"그럼 할 수 없지, 상상하는 걸로 만족해야겠군.\"\n\n\"그게 상책이야, 내 말 믿게.\"\n\n\"그럼 적어도 어떤 곳인지 짐작이라도 가게 해 주게.\"\n\n\"내가 어떻게?\"\n\n\"그보다 쉬운 게 어딨나. 큰가?\"\n\n\"그저 그래.\"\n\n\"어떻게 배치돼 있지?\"\n\n\"이런, 그걸 그리려면 펜이랑 잉크랑 종이가 있어야겠는데.\"\n\n\"여기 다 있어.\" 카드루스가 잽싸게 말했다. 그는 낡은 책상에서 흰 종이 한 장과 펜과 잉크를 꺼내 왔다. \"자.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"이 종이에 그걸 다 그려 보게, 친구.\"\n\n안드레아는 알아챌 수 없는 미소를 띠며 펜을 들고 그리기 시작했다.\n\n\"집은 말했다시피 안뜰과 정원 사이에 있어. 이런 식으로, 알겠나?\" 안드레아는 정원과 안뜰, 그리고 집을 그렸다.\n\n\"담은 높은가?\"\n\n\"기껏해야 팔구 피트 정도야.\"\n\n\"그건 신중하지 못한걸.\" 카드루스가 말했다.\n\n\"안뜰에는 화분에 심은 오렌지나무들이며 잔디, 꽃밭이 있어.\"\n\n\"덫 같은 건 없고?\"\n\n\"없어.\"\n\n\"마구간은?\"\n\n\"저기 보이는 대문 양옆에 있지.\" 그러면서 안드레아는 도면을 계속 그렸다.\n\n\"일 층을 좀 보세.\" 카드루스가 말했다.\n\n\"일 층에는 식당, 응접실 두 개, 당구실, 현관의 계단, 그리고 작은 뒤쪽 계단이 있어.\"\n\n\"창문은?\"\n\n\"으리으리한 창문들이지. 어찌나 아름답고 큰지, 자네만 한 덩치도 창틀마다 빠져나갈 수 있을 거야.\"\n\n\"빌어먹을, 그런 창문이 있는데 계단은 뭐 하러 둔담?\"\n\n\"호사스러운 데는 다 있는 법이지.\"\n\n\"그럼 덧창은?\"\n\n\"있긴 한데, 한 번도 쓰지 않아. 그 몬테크리스토 백작은 별난 사람이라, 밤에도 하늘 바라보는 걸 좋아하거든.\"\n\n\"그럼 하인들은 어디서 자나?\"\n\n\"아, 그들은 따로 쓰는 건물이 있어. 사다리들을 보관하는 오른편에 멋진 마차 차고가 있다고 상상해 보게. 그 차고 위가 하인들 방인데, 방마다 다른 방들과 연결된 초인종이 달려 있어.\"\n\n\"아, 제기랄! 초인종이라고 했나?\"\n\n\"무슨 소린가?\"\n\n\"아, 아무것도 아니야! 그저 그걸 다는 데 돈이 엄청 들 텐데, 대체 무슨 소용이 있나 싶어서 말이지.\"\n\n\"밤이면 안뜰에 개를 풀어 놓곤 했는데, 그건 오퇴유 집으로 데려갔어. 자네도 가 봤다는 그 집 말이야, 알지.\"\n\n\"알지.\"\n\n\"바로 어제 내가 그분께 이렇게 말씀드렸어. '백작님, 경솔하십니다. 오퇴유에 가실 때 하인들까지 데려가시면 이 집이 무방비로 남으니까요.' 그러자 '그래서?' 하시더군. '그러니까 언젠가는 도둑맞으실 겁니다.' 했지.\"\n\n\"그분이 뭐라 답하던가?\"\n\n\"태연히 말씀하시더군. '도둑맞은들 내가 무슨 상관인가?'\"\n\n\"안드레아, 그 사람한테 용수철이 달린 책상이 있군그래.\"\n\n\"그걸 자네가 어떻게 알아?\"\n\n\"그래, 도둑을 덫에 가두고 곡조를 울리는 그런 거 말이야. 지난번 박람회에 그런 게 있었다더군.\"\n\n\"그분께는 그냥 마호가니 책상이 하나 있을 뿐이야. 거기엔 늘 열쇠가 꽂혀 있고.\"\n\n\"그런데도 도둑을 안 맞는다고?\"\n\n\"안 맞아. 하인들이 죄다 그분께 충성스러우니까.\"\n\n\"그 책상 안에 돈이 좀 있겠군?\"\n\n\"있을지도. 안에 뭐가 있는지는 아무도 몰라.\"\n\n\"그게 어디 있는데?\"\n\n\"이 층에.\"\n\n\"일 층을 그렸듯이 그 층 도면도 그려 보게, 친구.\"\n\n\"그거야 아주 간단하지.\" 안드레아가 펜을 들었다. \"이 층에는, 보다시피, 대기실과 응접실이 있어. 응접실 오른쪽으로는 서재와 서실, 왼쪽으로는 침실과 화장실이 있지. 그 유명한 책상은 화장실에 있어.\"\n\n\"화장실에 창문이 있나?\"\n\n\"두 개야. 하나는 여기, 하나는 저기.\" 안드레아는 그 방에 창문 두 개를 그려 넣었는데, 도면에서 그것은 각을 이루며 침실 직사각형에 작은 정사각형이 덧붙은 모양으로 나타났다. 카드루스가 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n\"그분이 오퇴유엔 자주 가나?\" 그가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"일주일에 두세 번. 가령 내일도 거기서 낮과 밤을 보내실 거야.\"\n\n\"확실해?\"\n\n\"나더러 거기 와서 저녁을 들라고 초대하셨으니까.\"\n\n\"이게 바로 사는 거지.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"시내 저택에 시골 별장이라니.\"\n\n\"부자란 그런 거야.\"\n\n\"그래서 자네 거기 가서 저녁을 들 건가?\"\n\n\"아마도.\"\n\n\"거기서 저녁을 들면 거기서 자나?\"\n\n\"내키면. 그 집에선 내 집처럼 지내니까.\"\n\n카드루스는 그 마음속 밑바닥에서 진실을 캐내려는 듯 젊은이를 바라보았다. 그러나 안드레아는 주머니에서 담배 상자를 꺼내 하바나산 시가를 한 대 집고는 태연히 불을 붙여 피우기 시작했다.\n\n\"자네 천이백 프랑은 언제 필요한가?\" 그가 카드루스에게 말했다.\n\n\"지금. 가지고 있으면 말이야.\" 안드레아가 주머니에서 스물다섯 루이를 꺼냈다.\n\n\"노란 녀석들이로군?\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"아니, 됐네. 사양하지.\"\n\n\"오, 자네 그걸 업신여기는군.\"\n\n\"천만에, 귀히 여기지. 그래도 받지는 않겠어.\"\n\n\"바꾸면 되잖나, 이 멍청아. 금화는 다섯 수* 더 쳐주는데.\"\n\n\"바로 그거야. 그걸 바꾸는 자가 친구 카드루스를 미행해서 덜미를 잡고는, 어떤 소작인이 금화로 소작료를 내더냐고 캐물을 테니까. 허튼짓 말게, 친구. 그냥 은화로 주게. 어느 군주의 머리든 새겨진 둥근 동전 말이야. 오 프랑짜리야 누구든 가질 수 있는 거니까.\"\n\n\"그럼 내가 오백 프랑을 몸에 지니고 다닌다고 생각하나? 그러려면 짐꾼이 필요할걸.\"\n\n\"그럼 자네 수위한테 맡겨 두게. 믿을 만한 자니까. 내가 가지러 갈 테니.\"\n\n\"오늘?\"\n\n\"아니, 내일. 오늘은 시간이 없어.\"\n\n\"그럼 내일 오퇴유로 갈 때 맡겨 두지.\"\n\n\"믿어도 되겠지?\"\n\n\"물론이지.\"\n\n\"그래야 그걸 믿고 가정부를 들일 테니까 말이야.\"\n\n\"자, 이제 이게 다지? 응? 그리고 더는 날 괴롭히지 않겠지?\"\n\n\"결코.\"\n\n카드루스가 어찌나 침울해졌던지, 안드레아는 그 변화를 모른 척할 수 없게 될까 봐 두려웠다. 그는 한층 더 명랑하고 태평한 척했다.\n\n\"자네 참 활기차군.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"벌써 자네 재산을 손에 넣기라도 한 사람 같아.\"\n\n\"아니, 안타깝게도 아직. 하지만 일단 손에 넣으면……\"\n\n\"넣으면?\"\n\n\"옛 친구들을 잊지 않을 거야. 그것만은 장담하지.\"\n\n\"그래, 자네 기억력이 그렇게 좋으니 말이야.\"\n\n\"무얼 바라는 거지? 마치 날 등쳐 먹으려는 것 같군.\"\n\n\"내가? 무슨 그런 생각을! 자네한테 좋은 충고를 하나 더 해 주려는 이 몸이 말이야.\"\n\n\"그게 뭔데?\"\n\n\"자네 손가락에 낀 다이아몬드를 두고 가라는 거야. 우리 둘 다 곤경에 빠질 테니까. 자네 어리석음 때문에 자네 신세도 내 신세도 망칠 거라고.\"\n\n\"어째서?\" 안드레아가 말했다.\n\n\"어째서라니? 자네는 하인 제복을 걸치고 하인으로 변장한 주제에, 손가락엔 사오천 프랑짜리 다이아몬드를 끼고 있잖나.\"\n\n\"제법 잘 맞히는군.\"\n\n\"내가 다이아몬드는 좀 알지. 가져 본 적도 있고.\"\n\n\"그걸 자랑이라고 하는군.\" 안드레아가 말했다. 그는 카드루스가 걱정한 것과 달리 이 새로운 갈취에 화내지 않고, 태연히 반지를 내주었다. 카드루스가 어찌나 가까이 들여다보던지, 안드레아는 그가 모든 모서리가 흠 없는지 살피고 있다는 것을 똑똑히 알아챘다.\n\n\"이건 가짜 다이아몬드야.\" 카드루스가 말했다.\n\n\"이제 농담까지 하는군.\" 안드레아가 대답했다.\n\n\"화내지 말게, 시험해 보면 돼.\" 카드루스는 창가로 가서 그것으로 유리를 그어 보았고, 유리가 베이는 것을 확인했다.\n\n\"콘피테오르*!\" 카드루스가 다이아몬드를 새끼손가락에 끼며 말했다. \"내가 잘못 봤군. 한데 그 도둑놈 같은 보석상들이 어찌나 똑같이 흉내를 내는지, 이젠 보석상을 털 가치도 없어졌어. 한 가지 업종이 또 마비된 셈이지.\"\n\n\"다 됐나?\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"더 필요한 건 없고? 내 조끼나 모자라도 줄까? 기왕 시작했으니 마음껏 가져가게나.\"\n\n\"아니야. 자네는 어쨌든 좋은 친구야. 더 붙잡진 않겠어. 내 욕심도 좀 다스려 보도록 하지.\"\n\n\"하지만 조심하게, 자네가 금화에 대해 걱정한 그 일이 그 다이아몬드를 팔 때 똑같이 일어나지 않도록 말이야.\"\n\n\"그건 안 팔 거야. 걱정 말게.\"\n\n'적어도 모레까지는 안 팔겠지.' 젊은이는 속으로 생각했다.\n\n\"운 좋은 악당 같으니.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"이제 자네 하인들이며 말들이며 마차며 약혼녀를 만나러 가는군!\"\n\n\"그래.\" 안드레아가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼, 당글라르 양과 결혼하는 날엔 멋진 결혼 선물을 하나 해 주길 바라네.\"\n\n\"그건 자네 머릿속에서 멋대로 지어낸 망상이라고 이미 말했잖나.\"\n\n\"그 아가씨 재산이 얼만데?\"\n\n\"하지만 내 말은……\"\n\n\"백만이야?\"\n\n안드레아가 어깨를 으쓱했다.\n\n\"백만이라 치세.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"내가 자네한테 바라는 만큼은 결코 못 가질 테니까.\"\n\n\"고맙군.\" 젊은이가 말했다.\n\n\"오, 진심으로 바라는 거라네!\" 카드루스가 쉰 웃음을 흘리며 덧붙였다. \"잠깐, 내가 길을 안내하지.\"\n\n\"그럴 것 없어.\"\n\n\"왜?\"\n\n\"여기엔 자그마한 비밀이, 내가 해 둘 만하다고 여긴 조심책이 하나 있거든. 위레 앤드 피셰 회사의 자물쇠를 가스파르 카드루스가 손봐서 개량한 거지. 자네가 자본가가 되면 똑같은 걸 하나 만들어 주겠네.\"\n\n\"고맙군.\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"일주일 전에 미리 알려 주지.\"\n\n두 사람은 헤어졌다. 카드루스는 안드레아가 세 층을 다 내려가는 것은 물론, 안뜰을 가로지르는 것까지 지켜볼 때까지 층계참에 머물러 있었다. 그러고는 황급히 돌아와 문을 조심스레 닫고는, 영리한 건축가처럼 안드레아가 남기고 간 도면을 연구하기 시작했다.\n\n\"베네데토, 친구.\" 그가 말했다. \"내 생각엔 저 친구가 제 재산을 물려받는 걸 마다하지 않을 거야. 그러니 저 친구가 오십만 프랑을 손에 쥘 그날을 앞당겨 주는 자라면, 결코 저 친구의 가장 나쁜 친구는 아니겠지.\"\n\n# Chapter 82. The Burglary\n\n* 수: 옛 프랑스 화폐 단위로 20분의 1프랑.—옮긴이 * 콘피테오르: 라틴어로 '내 잘못을 고백하나이다'라는 뜻.—옮긴이\n우리가 앞서 전한 대화가 오간 그 이튿날, 몬테크리스토 백작은 알리와 하인 몇을 거느리고 오퇴유로 떠났다. 그러면서 성능을 시험해 보고 싶던 말 몇 마리도 데려갔다. 전날까지만 해도 그는 이 여행을 생각조차 하지 않았고 안드레아 역시 떠올리지 못했지만, 베르투초가 노르망디에서 집과 범선에 관한 소식을 가지고 돌아온 까닭에 그는 길을 나서게 되었다. 집은 준비가 끝났고, 일주일 전에 도착한 범선은 작은 만에 닻을 내린 채 정박해 있었다. 여섯 명의 선원은 필요한 모든 절차를 마치고 다시 바다로 나갈 채비가 되어 있었다.\n\n백작은 베르투초의 열성을 칭찬하며, 프랑스에 머무는 기간이 한 달을 넘기지 않을 테니 신속히 떠날 준비를 해 두라고 일렀다.\n\n\"이제,\" 그가 말했다. \"하룻밤 사이에 파리에서 트레포르까지 가야 할 일이 생길지도 모르네. 길목마다 싱싱한 말 여덟 마리를 대기시켜 두게. 그러면 열 시간에 오십 리외를 갈 수 있을 테니까.\"\n\n\"각하께서 이미 그 분부를 내리셨기에,\" 베르투초가 대답했다. \"말은 준비되어 있습니다. 제가 직접 사들여 가장 적합한 곳에, 그러니까 평소 아무도 머물지 않는 마을들에 배치해 두었습니다.\"\n\n\"잘했네.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"나는 여기서 하루이틀 머물 테니, 그에 맞춰 준비해 두게.\"\n\n베르투초가 필요한 지시를 내리려고 방을 나서려는데, 바티스탱이 문을 열었다. 그는 은쟁반에 편지 한 통을 받쳐 들고 있었다.\n\n\"여기서 뭘 하는 건가?\" 먼지를 뒤집어쓴 그를 보고 백작이 물었다. \"내가 자네를 부른 적은 없는 듯한데?\"\n\n바티스탱은 대답 없이 백작에게 다가가 편지를 내밀었다. \"중요하고 급한 일입니다.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n백작은 편지를 펼쳐 읽었다.\n\n\"몬테크리스토 백작께 알립니다. 오늘 밤 한 사내가 백작의 집에 들 것이며, 화장실의 비밀 서랍장에 들어 있으리라 짐작되는 서류 몇 점을 훔쳐 갈 작정입니다. 백작의 잘 알려진 용맹함이라면 경찰의 도움은 필요치 않을 것이며, 경찰이 끼어들면 이 충고를 보내는 자에게 심각한 해가 미칠 수도 있습니다. 백작은 침실의 어느 틈으로든, 혹은 화장실에 몸을 숨겨 직접 재산을 지킬 수 있을 것입니다. 하인을 여럿 두거나 눈에 띄는 경계를 하면 악당이 시도를 단념할 것이고, 그러면 백작은 우연이 드러내 준 적을 알아낼 기회를 잃게 됩니다. 지금 이 경고를 보내는 자는, 만약 이 첫 시도가 실패하고 또 다른 시도가 이어진다면 다음번에는 이 같은 경고를 보내지 못할 수도 있습니다.\"\n\n백작이 처음 떠올린 생각은 이것이 술책이라는 것이었다. 사소한 위험으로 주의를 돌려 더 큰 위험에 빠뜨리려는 조잡한 속임수 말이다. 그는 익명의 친구가 보낸 충고에도 불구하고, 어쩌면 바로 그 충고 때문에 이 편지를 경찰서장에게 보낼 참이었다. 그러다 문득, 어쩌면 자기 혼자만이 알아볼 수 있는 사사로운 적일지도 모른다는 생각이 떠올랐다. 만약 그렇다면, 피에스코가 자신을 죽이려던 무어인을 제압했듯, 오직 자기 혼자만이 이득을 얻을 수 있는 일이었다. 우리는 위인을 특징짓는 그 기백으로 어떤 일도 불가능하다고 인정하지 않는, 백작의 강인하고 대담한 정신을 알고 있다.\n\n지난 삶에서, 어떤 것 앞에서도 물러서지 않겠다는 결심에서, 백작은 자신이 뛰어들었던 싸움에 대해 형언할 수 없는 흥취를 느끼게 되었다. 때로는 자연을, 다시 말해 신을 상대로, 때로는 세상을, 다시 말해 악마를 상대로 한 싸움이었다.\n\n\"저들은 내 서류를 노리는 게 아니야.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"나를 죽이려는 거지. 저들은 도둑이 아니라 살인자야. 경찰청장이 내 사사로운 일에 끼어들도록 두지 않겠어. 이번 일에는 그자의 권한쯤 분배할 만큼 나는 부유하니까.\"\n\n백작은 편지를 전하고 방을 나갔던 바티스탱을 다시 불렀다.\n\n\"파리로 돌아가게.\" 그가 말했다. \"거기 남아 있는 하인들을 모두 모으게. 우리 집 식솔을 전부 오퇴유로 데려오게.\"\n\n\"하지만 집에는 아무도 남지 않습니까, 나리?\" 바티스탱이 물었다.\n\n\"있지, 문지기.\"\n\n\"나리께서도 아시다시피 문지기 방은 본채에서 멀리 떨어져 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그래서?\"\n\n\"문지기가 아무 소리도 듣지 못하는 사이에 집이 통째로 털릴 수도 있습니다.\"\n\n\"누가 턴다는 건가?\"\n\n\"도둑들이지요.\"\n\"자네는 어리석군, 바티스탱. 도둑들이 집을 턴다 한들, 내 명을 거역당하는 것보다는 덜 짜증스러울 거야.\" 바티스탱이 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"내 말 알아듣겠나?\" 백작이 말했다. \"동료들을 여기로 데려오게, 한 사람도 빠짐없이. 다만 모든 것은 평소대로 두고, 1층 덧창만 닫아 두게.\"\n\n\"2층 덧창은요?\"\n\n\"그건 한 번도 닫은 적이 없다는 걸 알지 않나. 가게!\"\n\n백작은 혼자 저녁을 들 것이며, 알리 말고는 아무도 시중들지 못하게 하라는 뜻을 밝혔다. 평소처럼 차분하고 절제 있게 저녁을 마친 백작은 알리에게 따라오라는 신호를 보내고 옆문으로 나섰다. 불로뉴 숲에 이르자 그는 별다른 의도 없는 듯 파리 쪽으로 방향을 틀었고, 땅거미가 질 무렵 샹젤리제에 있는 자기 집 맞은편에 다다랐다. 사방이 어두웠다. 바티스탱이 말한 대로 본채에서 마흔 걸음쯤 떨어진 문지기 방에 희미한 불빛 하나만 외로이 타고 있었다.\n\n몬테크리스토는 나무에 몸을 기댄 채, 좀처럼 속는 일이 없는 그 날카로운 눈길로 거리를 위아래로 훑었다. 지나는 사람들을 살피고, 누가 숨어 있지는 않은지 이웃한 거리들까지 꼼꼼히 들여다보았다. 그렇게 십 분이 지나자 그는 아무도 자신을 감시하지 않는다고 확신했다. 그는 알리와 함께 옆문으로 서둘러 들어갔다. 자기가 열쇠를 가진 하인용 계단을 통해, 커튼 하나 젖히거나 흐트러뜨리지 않고 침실에 이르렀다. 집이 비어 있다고 여기던 문지기는 그 집에 정작 주인이 들어와 있다는 것을 조금도 의심하지 못했다.\n\n침실에 이르자 백작은 알리에게 멈추라는 신호를 했다. 그러고는 화장실로 들어가 살펴보았다. 모든 것이 평소와 같아 보였다. 값진 비밀 서랍장은 제자리에 있었고, 열쇠도 서랍장에 꽂혀 있었다. 그는 서랍장을 이중으로 잠그고 열쇠를 뽑은 뒤, 침실 문으로 돌아가 빗장의 이중 고리를 떼어 내고 안으로 들어갔다. 그사이 알리는 백작이 요구한 무기를 가져다 두었다. 짧은 기병총 한 자루와 쌍열 권총 한 쌍이었다. 쌍열 권총은 단열 권총만큼이나 정확히 겨눌 수 있었다. 이렇게 무장한 백작은 다섯 사람의 목숨을 손안에 쥔 셈이었다. 시각은 아홉 시 반쯤이었다.\n\n백작과 알리는 빵 한 조각을 서둘러 먹고 스페인산 포도주 한 잔을 마셨다. 그러고 나서 몬테크리스토는 옆방을 들여다볼 수 있는 미닫이 칸막이 하나를 살짝 밀어 두었다. 그의 손이 닿는 곳에 권총과 기병총이 있었고, 곁에 선 알리는 작은 아랍식 손도끼 하나를 쥐고 있었다. 그 도끼의 모양은 십자군 시절 이래로 변하지 않은 것이었다. 화장실 창과 일직선에 놓인 침실 창 하나를 통해 백작은 거리를 내다볼 수 있었다.\n\n그렇게 두 시간이 지났다. 칠흑같이 어두웠다. 그래도 알리는 그 야성적인 천성 덕분에, 백작은 아마도 오랜 감옥살이 덕분에, 어둠 속에서도 나무들의 미세한 움직임까지 분간할 수 있었다. 문지기 방의 작은 불빛은 이미 오래전에 꺼져 있었다. 만약 정말로 침입이 계획된 것이라면, 그것은 창문이 아니라 1층 계단 쪽에서 시작되리라 예상할 수 있었다. 몬테크리스토가 보기에 그 악당들이 노리는 것은 그의 돈이 아니라 목숨이었다. 그들이 들이닥칠 곳은 그의 침실일 테고, 거기에 이르려면 뒤쪽 계단이나 화장실 창을 거쳐야 했다.\n\n앵발리드의 시계가 열두 시 십오 분 전을 알렸다. 서풍이 축축한 돌풍에 그 세 번의 울림이 빚어내는 음울한 떨림을 실어 날랐다.\n\n마지막 울림이 잦아들 무렵, 백작은 화장실에서 희미한 소리를 들은 듯했다. 첫 소리, 아니 첫 긁히는 소리에 이어 두 번째, 그리고 세 번째 소리가 들렸다. 네 번째 소리에 이르자 백작은 무슨 일인지 알아챘다. 단단하고 노련한 손이 다이아몬드로 유리창 한 장의 네 변을 잘라 내고 있었다. 백작은 심장이 한층 빠르게 뛰는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n사람이 아무리 위험에 단련되어 있고 아무리 위태로움을 미리 알고 있다 해도, 심장의 떨림과 몸의 전율을 통해 그들은 꿈과 현실의, 계획과 실행의 어마어마한 차이를 깨닫는 법이다. 그러나 몬테크리스토는 알리에게 신호 하나만 보냈고, 알리는 위험이 반대편에서 다가오고 있음을 알아채고 주인 곁으로 바싹 다가섰다. 몬테크리스토는 적의 힘과 수를 확인하고 싶어 안달이 났다.\n\n소리가 나는 그 창은 백작이 화장실을 들여다볼 수 있는 틈 맞은편에 있었다. 그는 그 창에 눈을 박았다. 어둠 속에서 그림자 하나가 어른거렸다. 이윽고 유리창 한 장이 바깥에서 종이를 한 장 붙인 것처럼 완전히 불투명해지더니, 그 네모난 유리가 떨어지지는 않은 채 금이 갔다. 그 틈으로 팔 하나가 들어와 걸쇠를 더듬었고, 이어 두 번째 팔이 들어왔다. 창이 돌쩌귀를 축 삼아 돌아갔고, 한 사내가 들어왔다. 혼자였다.\n\n\"대담한 놈이군.\" 백작이 속삭였다.\n\n그 순간 알리가 그의 어깨를 살짝 건드렸다. 백작이 돌아보자, 알리가 거리 쪽으로 난, 그들이 있는 방의 창을 가리켰다.\n\n\"보인다!\" 백작이 말했다. \"둘이군. 하나가 일을 하는 동안 다른 하나가 망을 보는 거야.\" 그는 알리에게 거리의 사내에게서 눈을 떼지 말라고 신호를 보내고, 화장실의 사내 쪽으로 돌아섰다.\n\n유리를 잘라 낸 사내는 안으로 들어와, 두 팔을 앞으로 뻗은 채 손으로 더듬으며 길을 찾고 있었다. 마침내 주변 지리를 익힌 듯했다. 문이 둘 있었는데, 사내는 두 문에 모두 빗장을 질렀다.\n\n사내가 침실 문 쪽으로 다가오자, 몬테크리스토는 그가 들어오려나 싶어 권총 하나를 들어 올렸다. 그러나 들려온 것은 빗장이 구리 고리 속에서 미끄러지는 소리뿐이었다. 그저 조심하느라 한 짓이었다. 백작이 고리를 떼어 냈다는 사실을 모르는 이 한밤의 방문객은 이제 제집인 양 마음 놓고 목적을 이룰 수 있다고 여겼을 것이다. 홀로 마음껏 움직일 수 있게 되자, 사내는 주머니에서 백작이 분간할 수 없는 무언가를 꺼내 받침대 위에 올려놓았다. 그러고는 곧장 비밀 서랍장으로 가 자물쇠를 더듬더니, 뜻밖에도 열쇠가 없다는 것을 알았다. 그러나 유리를 잘라 낸 그 사내는 온갖 비상사태에 대비해 둔 용의주도한 자였다. 곧 백작은 한 묶음의 곁쇠가 짤랑거리는 소리를 들었다. 자물쇠 장이가 자물쇠를 따 달라는 부름을 받고 가져오는 그런 것으로, 도둑들은 그것을 꾀꼬리라 불렀다. 빗장에 갈릴 때 나는 밤의 노랫소리 때문이리라.\n\n\"하, 이런.\" 몬테크리스토가 실망 어린 미소를 띠며 속삭였다. \"그저 도둑일 뿐이군.\"\n\n그러나 어둠 속의 사내는 맞는 열쇠를 찾지 못했다. 그는 받침대에 올려 둔 도구로 손을 뻗어 용수철을 건드렸다. 그러자 이내 창백한 빛이, 물체를 또렷이 분간할 만큼만 밝게, 그의 손과 얼굴에 비쳤다.\n\n\"맙소사.\" 몬테크리스토가 뒷걸음치며 외쳤다. \"저자는……\"\n\n알리가 손도끼를 들어 올렸다.\n\n\"움직이지 마라.\" 몬테크리스토가 속삭였다. \"도끼는 내려놓고. 무기는 필요 없을 테니.\"\n\n그러고는 낮은 목소리로 몇 마디 덧붙였다. 놀라움이 백작의 입에서 끌어낸 그 짧은 외침이, 아무리 희미했다 해도, 옛 칼갈이 같은 자세로 그대로 있던 사내를 흠칫하게 했던 것이다.\n\n백작이 방금 내린 것은 명령이었다. 알리가 곧장 소리 없이 나갔다가, 검은 옷 한 벌과 삼각모 하나를 들고 돌아왔다. 그사이 몬테크리스토는 외투와 조끼와 셔츠를 재빨리 벗었는데, 열린 칸막이 사이로 새어 드는 불빛에 그가 부드러운 강철 사슬 갑옷을 받쳐 입은 것이 분간되었다. 단검이 더는 두렵지 않은 프랑스에서 그런 갑옷을 마지막으로 입은 이는 루이 16세였다. 그는 가슴에 닿을 단검을 두려워했으나, 정작 그의 머리는 도끼에 쪼개졌다. 그 갑옷은 이내 긴 사제복 아래로 사라졌고, 머리카락도 사제의 가발 아래로 가려졌다. 그 위에 삼각모를 쓰자 백작은 영락없는 신부로 변모했다.\n\n더는 아무 소리도 들리지 않자, 사내는 몸을 곧추세웠다. 몬테크리스토가 변장을 마무리하는 동안 그는 곧장 비밀 서랍장으로 다가갔고, 자물쇠가 그의 꾀꼬리 아래에서 깨지기 시작했다.\n\n\"다시 해 봐라.\" 백작이 속삭였다. 그는 비밀 용수철을 믿고 있었는데, 아무리 솜씨 좋은 자물쇠 따는 자라도 그것은 알지 못했다. \"다시 해 봐, 거기 몇 분은 더 매달려야 할 테니.\"\n\n그러고는 창으로 다가갔다. 울타리에 걸터앉아 있던 사내는 어느새 내려와 여전히 거리를 서성이고 있었다. 그런데 이상하게도, 그는 샹젤리제 대로나 포부르생토노레 쪽에서 지나갈지 모를 사람들에게는 신경 쓰지 않았다. 온 정신이 백작의 집에서 벌어지는 일에 쏠려 있었고, 오직 화장실에서 일어나는 움직임 하나하나를 살피는 것만이 그의 목적인 듯했다.\n\n몬테크리스토는 문득 손가락으로 이마를 쳤고, 입가에 미소가 스쳤다. 그러고는 알리에게 다가가 속삭였다.\n\n\"여기 어둠 속에 숨어 있어라. 무슨 소리가 들리든, 무슨 일이 벌어지든, 내가 부를 때만 들어오거나 모습을 드러내라.\"\n\n알리는 절대복종의 표시로 고개를 숙였다. 이어 몬테크리스토는 벽장에서 불 켜진 양초 하나를 꺼냈다. 도둑이 자물쇠에 깊이 몰두해 있을 때, 그는 불빛이 곧장 제 얼굴에 비치도록 신경 쓰며 소리 없이 문을 열었다. 문이 어찌나 조용히 열렸던지 도둑은 아무 소리도 듣지 못했다. 그러나 놀랍게도 방이 갑자기 환해졌다. 그가 돌아보았다.\n\n\"아, 안녕하시오, 친애하는 카드루스 씨.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"이런 시각에 여기서 무얼 하고 계시오?\"\n\n\"부소니 신부님!\" 카드루스가 외쳤다. 문에 빗장을 질러 두었는데 이 기이한 환영이 어떻게 들어왔는지 알 수 없어, 그는 열쇠 묶음을 떨어뜨린 채 꼼짝도 못 하고 넋이 나가 버렸다. 백작은 카드루스와 창 사이에 자리를 잡아, 도둑이 달아날 단 하나의 길마저 끊어 버렸다.\n\n\"부소니 신부님!\" 카드루스가 핼쑥한 눈길을 백작에게 박은 채 되뇌었다.\n\n\"그렇소, 틀림없이 부소니 신부 본인이오.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"그리고 나를 알아봐 주니 참으로 기쁘구려, 친애하는 카드루스 씨. 기억력이 좋다는 증거 아니겠소. 우리가 마지막으로 만난 게 십 년쯤 전일 테니 말이오.\"\n\n부소니의 이 침착함이, 그 빈정거림과 대담함과 어우러져 카드루스를 휘청이게 했다.\n\n\"신부, 신부……\" 그가 주먹을 움켜쥐며, 이를 딱딱 부딪치며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"그래서, 몬테크리스토 백작의 집을 털려는 거였소?\" 가짜 신부가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"신부님,\" 카드루스가 백작이 무자비하게 막고 선 창을 다시 차지하려 애쓰며 중얼거렸다. \"신부님, 저는 모릅니다, 믿어 주십시오, 맹세컨대……\"\n\n\"유리창 한 장이 떨어졌고,\" 백작이 말을 이었다. \"각등에, 곁쇠 한 묶음에, 반쯤 뜯긴 비밀 서랍장까지. 이만하면 꽤 분명하지 않소……\"\n\n카드루스는 숨이 막혔다. 그는 몸을 숨길 구석을, 빠져나갈 길을 찾아 사방을 두리번거렸다.\n\n\"이런, 이런.\" 백작이 말을 이었다. \"당신은 여전히 똑같은 사람이군. 살인자 말이오.\"\n\n\"신부님, 모든 걸 아시니 그것이 제가 아니었다는 것도 아시겠지요. 라 카르콩트였습니다. 재판에서도 그게 입증되었으니, 저는 갤리선 형만 받았던 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 형기가 끝난 거요? 마침 다시 그리로 돌아가기 딱 좋은 길에 들어선 당신을 만났으니 말이오.\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 신부님. 누군가가 저를 풀어 주었습니다.\"\n\n\"그 누군가는 사회에 크나큰 선행을 베푼 셈이군.\"\n\n\"아,\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"제가 약속을 했는데……\"\n\n\"그런데 그 약속을 깨고 있는 거지!\" 몬테크리스토가 말을 잘랐다.\n\n\"아아, 그렇습니다!\" 카드루스가 몹시 불안해하며 말했다.\n\n\"고약한 재범이로군. 내 짐작이 틀리지 않다면, 그건 당신을 그레브 광장으로 끌고 갈 거요. 더더욱 안됐군, 더더욱 안됐어. 내 고향 말로 디아볼로●로다.\"\n\n\"신부님, 저는 떠밀려서……\"\n\n\"죄인은 누구나 똑같은 소리를 하지.\"\n\n\"가난이……\"\n\n\"흥!\" 부소니가 경멸하듯 말했다. \"가난은 사람을 구걸하게 하거나, 빵집 문 앞에서 빵 한 덩이를 훔치게는 할지언정, 사람이 산다고 여겨지는 집의 비밀 서랍장을 열게 하지는 않는 법이오. 그리고 보석상 요하네스가 내가 당신에게 준 다이아몬드 값으로 4만 5천 프랑을 막 치렀을 때, 당신이 다이아몬드와 돈을 둘 다 차지하려고 그를 죽였소. 그것도 가난 탓이었소?\"\n\n\"용서해 주십시오, 신부님.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"신부님께서는 한 번 제 목숨을 구해 주셨습니다. 다시 한 번 구해 주십시오!\"\n\n\"그것참 변변찮은 부추김이로군.\"\n\n\"신부님 혼자 오셨습니까, 아니면 저를 붙잡으려고 거기 군인들이라도 대기시켜 두셨습니까?\"\n\n\"나 혼자요.\" 신부가 말했다. \"그리고 다시 한 번 당신을 가엾이 여겨, 내 약한 마음이 어떤 새로운 화를 부르든 그 위험을 무릅쓰고 도망치게 해 주리다. 만약 당신이 사실을 말한다면 말이오.\"\n\n\"아, 신부님.\" 카드루스가 두 손을 모으고 몬테크리스토에게 다가서며 외쳤다. \"신부님이야말로 제 구원자이십니다!\"\n\n\"그러니까 감옥에서 풀려났단 말이지?\"\n\n\"네, 그건 사실입니다, 신부님.\"\n\n\"누가 당신을 해방시켰소?\"\n\n\"영국인이었소.\"\n\n\"이름이 뭐였소?\"\n\n\"윌모어 경입니다.\" \"내가 아는 사람이지. 당신이 거짓말을 하면 알아챌 거요.\"\n\n\"아, 신부님, 그저 있는 그대로의 진실을 말씀드리는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그 영국인이 당신을 비호하고 있었소?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 저는 아니고, 제 동료였던 젊은 코르시카 사람을요.\"\n\n\"그 젊은 코르시카 사람 이름이 뭐였소?\"\n\n\"베네데토입니다.\"\n\n\"그게 세례명이오?\"\n\n\"다른 이름은 없었습니다. 버려진 아이였으니까요.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 젊은이가 당신과 함께 탈출한 거요?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"어떻게?\"\n\n\"우리는 툴롱 근처 생망드리에에서 일하고 있었습니다. 생망드리에를 아십니까?\"\n\n\"알지.\"\n\n\"정오와 한 시 사이, 쉬는 시간에……\"\n\n\"갤리선 죄수들이 식사 뒤에 낮잠을 잔다니! 그 가엾은 자들이 참으로 딱하구려!\" 신부가 말했다.\n\n\"아니,\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"사람이 늘 일만 할 수는 없지 않습니까. 개도 아니고요.\"\n\n\"그럼 개들한테는 더없이 좋은 일이로군.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"아무튼 나머지가 잠든 사이에 우리는 좀 떨어진 곳으로 갔습니다. 영국인이 준 줄칼로 족쇄를 끊고 헤엄쳐 달아났지요.\"\n\n\"그래서 그 베네데토는 어찌 되었소?\"\n\n\"모릅니다.\"\n\n\"알아야 할 텐데.\"\n\n\"정말 모릅니다. 우리는 이에르에서 헤어졌습니다.\" 그러고는 자기 항변에 무게를 더하려고 카드루스가 신부 쪽으로 한 걸음 더 다가섰다. 신부는 여전히 그 자리에서 꼼짝 않고, 여느 때처럼 차분하게 심문을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"거짓말이오.\" 부소니 신부가 거역할 수 없는 위엄이 담긴 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"신부님!\"\n\n\"거짓말이야! 그 사내는 여전히 당신 친구이고, 당신은 어쩌면 그를 공범으로 부려 먹고 있는 거지.\"\n\n\"오, 신부님!\"\n\n\"툴롱을 떠난 뒤로 당신은 무엇으로 먹고살았소? 대답해 보시오!\"\n\n\"손에 넣을 수 있는 걸로요.\"\n\n\"거짓말이야.\" 신부가 한층 더 위압적인 어조로 세 번째로 되뇌었다. 카드루스는 겁에 질려 백작을 바라보았다. \"당신은 그자가 준 돈으로 먹고살았소.\"\n\n\"맞습니다.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"베네데토는 한 대귀족의 아들이 되었습니다.\"\n\n\"어떻게 대귀족의 아들이 될 수 있소?\"\n\n\"사생아지요.\"\n\n\"그 대귀족의 이름이 뭐요?\"\n\n\"몬테크리스토 백작, 바로 우리가 지금 들어와 있는 이 집의 주인입니다.\"\n\n\"베네데토가 백작의 아들이라고?\" 이번에는 몬테크리스토가 놀라며 되물었다.\n\n\"그렇겠지요. 백작이 그에게 가짜 아버지까지 붙여 주었으니까요. 백작이 그에게 다달이 4천 프랑을 주고, 유언장에 50만 프랑을 남겨 준다니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇군.\" 가짜 신부가 이제야 사정을 알아차리며 말했다. \"그래서 그 젊은이는 지금 무슨 이름을 쓰고 있소?\"\n\n\"안드레아 카발칸티입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 내 친구 몬테크리스토 백작이 자기 집에 받아들였고, 당글라르 양과 혼인하려는 그 젊은이가 바로 그자란 말이오?\"\n\"바로 그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"그런데 당신은 그걸 보고만 있단 말이오, 이 가증스러운 인간아! 그자의 삶도, 그자의 죄도 다 아는 당신이!\"\n\n\"제가 왜 동료의 앞길을 막겠습니까?\" 카드루스가 말했다.\n\n\"옳은 말이군. 당글라르 씨에게 알릴 사람은 당신이 아니라 바로 나야.\"\n\n\"그러지 마십시오, 신부님.\"\n\n\"왜 안 되오?\"\n\n\"우리를 파멸로 몰아넣으실 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그래서 당신 같은 악당들을 구해 주려고 내가 그 음모의 부추김꾼이, 그 범죄의 공범이 되어 주리라 생각하는 거요?\"\n\n\"신부님.\" 카드루스가 더욱 다가서며 말했다.\n\n\"나는 모두 폭로하겠소.\"\n\n\"누구에게요?\"\n\n\"당글라르 씨에게.\"\n\n\"맙소사!\" 카드루스가 조끼에서 칼날을 펼친 칼을 빼 들고 백작의 가슴을 찌르며 외쳤다. \"신부님, 당신은 아무것도 폭로하지 못하오!\"\n\n카드루스가 소스라치게 놀랍게도, 칼은 백작의 가슴을 꿰뚫기는커녕 무디게 튕겨 나왔다. 동시에 백작은 왼손으로 살인자의 손목을 붙잡아, 굳어 버린 손가락에서 칼이 떨어져 나갈 만큼 세게 비틀었다. 카드루스가 고통에 찬 비명을 질렀다. 그러나 백작은 그 비명에 아랑곳없이 도적의 손목을 계속 비틀었고, 마침내 팔이 탈구되자 카드루스는 먼저 무릎을 꿇었다가 바닥에 납작 엎어졌다.\n\n그러자 백작이 그의 머리를 발로 짓누르며 말했다. \"이 악당아, 무엇이 네 두개골을 으깨 버리지 못하게 나를 붙드는지 모르겠구나.\"\n\n\"아, 살려 주십시오, 살려 주십시오!\" 카드루스가 외쳤다.\n\n백작이 발을 거두었다.\n\n\"일어나라!\" 그가 말했다. 카드루스가 일어났다.\n\n\"신부님 손목이 어쩌면 그리 무섭습니까!\" 카드루스가, 그 살집 있는 집게에 붙들려 온통 멍든 팔을 어루만지며 말했다. \"어쩌면 그런 손목이!\"\n\n\"닥쳐라! 신께서 너 같은 들짐승을 제압할 힘을 내게 주시는 것이다. 나는 그 신의 이름으로 행하는 것이니, 그것을 명심해라, 이 가련한 인간아. 지금 이 순간 너를 살려 두는 것도 여전히 그분을 섬기는 일이다.\"\n\n\"오!\" 카드루스가 고통에 신음하며 말했다.\n\n\"이 펜과 종이를 들고, 내가 부르는 대로 받아써라.\"\n\n\"저는 글을 쓸 줄 모릅니다, 신부님.\"\n\n\"거짓말이야! 이 펜을 들고 써라!\"\n\n카드루스는 신부의 압도적인 힘에 기가 눌려 자리에 앉아 받아썼다.\n\n\"선생, 당신이 댁에 받아들였고 따님과 혼인시키려는 그 사내는, 툴롱에서 나와 함께 감옥을 탈출한 중죄인입니다. 그는 59번이었고 나는 58번이었습니다. 그는 베네데토라 불렸으나, 부모를 전혀 알지 못한 터라 제 진짜 이름은 모릅니다.\"\n\n\"서명해라!\" 백작이 말을 이었다.\n\n\"하지만 저를 파멸시키시려는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"내가 네 파멸을 노렸다면, 이 멍청아, 너를 가까운 초소로 끌고 갔겠지. 게다가 그 쪽지가 전달되고 나면, 십중팔구 너는 더 두려워할 게 없을 거다. 그러니 서명해라!\"\n\n카드루스가 서명했다.\n\n\"수신인은 '당글라르 남작, 은행가, 쇼세 당탱 거리.'라고 쓰고.\"\n\n카드루스가 주소를 적었다. 신부가 쪽지를 받아 들었다.\n\n\"자,\" 그가 말했다. \"이걸로 됐다. 어서 꺼져라!\"\n\n\"어느 쪽으로요?\"\n\n\"들어온 길로.\"\n\n\"저 창문으로 나가라는 말씀입니까?\"\n\n\"잘만 들어왔잖나.\"\n\n\"오, 신부님께서 저를 노리는 무슨 꿍꿍이가 있으시군요.\"\n\n\"멍청한 놈! 내게 무슨 꿍꿍이가 있단 말이냐?\"\n\n\"그럼 어째서 문으로 내보내 주시지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"문지기를 깨워서 좋을 게 뭐가 있겠나?\"\n\n\"아, 신부님, 말씀해 주십시오, 제가 죽기를 바라십니까?\"\n\n\"나는 신께서 바라시는 것을 바랄 뿐이다.\"\n\n\"하지만 제가 내려가는 동안 저를 치지 않겠다고 맹세해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"비겁한 멍청이로군!\"\n\n\"저를 어쩌실 작정입니까?\"\n\n\"내가 무엇을 할 수 있겠느냐? 나는 너를 행복한 사람으로 만들려고 애썼건만, 너는 살인자가 되어 버렸다.\"\n\n\"오, 신부님.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"한 번만 더 시험해 주십시오. 다시 한 번만 저를 시험해 주십시오!\"\n\n\"그러마.\" 백작이 말했다. \"들어라. 너는 내가 믿을 만한 사람인지 알 테지.\"\n\n\"네.\" 카드루스가 말했다.\n\n\"네가 무사히 집에 닿거든……\"\n\n\"신부님 말고 제가 두려워할 게 뭐가 있겠습니까?\"\n\n\"네가 무사히 집에 닿거든, 파리를 떠나라, 프랑스를 떠나라. 어디에 있든, 네가 처신을 바르게 하는 한, 내가 너에게 약간의 연금을 보내 주마. 만약 네가 무사히 집에 닿는다면, 그러면……\"\n\n\"그러면요?\" 카드루스가 몸을 떨며 물었다.\n\n\"그러면 신께서 너를 용서하셨다고 믿을 테고, 나 또한 너를 용서하마.\"\n\n\"제가 그리스도교도라는 것이 진실인 만큼이나 진실로,\" 카드루스가 더듬거렸다. \"신부님은 저를 두려움에 떨다 죽게 만드시는군요!\"\n\n\"이제 꺼져라.\" 백작이 창을 가리키며 말했다.\n\n카드루스는 그 약속을 아직 믿지 못한 채로 두 다리를 창밖으로 내밀어 사다리에 올라섰다.\n\n\"이제 내려가라.\" 신부가 팔짱을 끼며 말했다. 더는 그에게서 두려워할 게 없음을 깨닫고 카드루스가 내려가기 시작했다. 그러자 백작은 양초를 창가로 가져갔다. 한 사내가 창문을 빠져나가는 동안 다른 사내가 불을 들고 있는 모습을 샹젤리제에서 볼 수 있도록 하기 위해서였다.\n\n\"신부님, 뭐 하시는 겁니까? 야경꾼이라도 지나가면 어쩌시려고요?\" 그러고는 그가 불을 불어 껐다. 이어 그는 내려갔는데, 발이 땅에 닿는 것을 느끼고서야 비로소 안전하다고 안심했다.\n\n몬테크리스토는 침실로 돌아왔다. 정원에서 거리로 재빨리 눈길을 옮기던 그는 먼저 카드루스를 보았다. 카드루스는 정원 끝까지 걸어가더니, 들어온 곳과는 다른 자리에 사다리를 벽에 기대 세웠다. 이어 백작이 거리 쪽을 내려다보니, 기다리고 있던 듯한 그 사내가 같은 방향으로 달려가, 카드루스가 넘어올 벽 모퉁이에 몸을 붙였다. 카드루스는 천천히 사다리를 올라가, 거리가 조용한지 보려고 담장 위로 고개를 내밀었다. 보이는 사람도 들리는 소리도 없었다. 앵발리드의 시계가 한 시를 알렸다. 그러자 카드루스가 담장 꼭대기에 걸터앉아 사다리를 끌어 올려 벽 너머로 넘겼다. 이어 그는 내려가기 시작했는데, 아니 두 받침 기둥을 타고 미끄러져 내렸는데, 그 솜씨가 어찌나 익숙한지 그가 그런 짓에 얼마나 단련되어 있는지를 보여 주었다. 그러나 일단 내려가기 시작하자 멈출 수가 없었다. 절반쯤 내려왔을 때 그림자 속에서 한 사내가 튀어나오는 것을 보았으나 소용없었고, 땅에 발이 닿는 순간 팔 하나가 치켜올려지는 것을 보았으나 소용없었다.\n\n미처 몸을 지킬 새도 없이 그 팔이 어찌나 세차게 그의 등을 후려쳤던지, 그는 사다리를 놓치며 \"사람 살려!\" 하고 외쳤다. 곧이어 두 번째 일격이 그의 옆구리를 쳤고, 그는 \"사람 살려, 살인이야!\"라고 외치며 쓰러졌다. 이어 그가 바닥을 뒹구는 사이, 적이 그의 머리채를 움켜쥐고 가슴에 세 번째 일격을 가했다.\n\n이번에는 카드루스도 다시 소리치려 했으나 신음 한 가닥밖에 내지 못했고, 세 군데 상처에서 피가 흘러나오자 몸을 부르르 떨었다. 살인자는 그가 더는 소리치지 않자 머리채를 잡아 그의 머리를 들어 올렸다. 두 눈은 감겨 있었고 입은 일그러져 있었다. 죽었다고 여긴 살인자는 그 머리를 떨어뜨리고 사라졌다.\n\n그러자 카드루스는 그가 떠나는 것을 느끼고 팔꿈치로 몸을 일으켜, 죽어 가는 목소리로 안간힘을 다해 외쳤다.\n\n\"살인이야! 나 죽는다! 살려 주십시오, 신부님, 살려 주십시오!\"\n\n이 애처로운 호소가 어둠을 꿰뚫었다. 뒤쪽 계단의 문이 열리고, 이어 정원의 옆문이 열리더니, 알리와 그 주인이 불을 들고 그 자리에 나타났다.\n● 디아볼로: 이탈리아어로 '제기랄' 정도의 뜻. —옮긴이\n\n# Chapter 83. The Hand of God\n\n카드루스는 애처롭게 계속 울부짖었다. \"사람 살려요, 신부님, 살려 주세요!\"\n\n\"무슨 일이오?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다.\n\n\"사람 살려.\" 카드루스가 외쳤다. \"저는 칼에 찔렸어요!\"\n\n\"우리가 여기 있소. 기운을 내시오.\"\n\n\"아, 다 끝났어요! 너무 늦게 오셨어요. 제가 죽는 걸 보러 오신 거예요. 이렇게 찔리고, 이렇게 피가……!\"\n\n그는 정신을 잃었다. 알리와 그의 주인은 부상자를 방 안으로 옮겼다. 몬테크리스토가 알리에게 그의 옷을 벗기라고 손짓했고, 그런 뒤 끔찍한 상처들을 살펴보았다.\n\n\"오, 하느님!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"당신의 복수는 때로 늦어지지만, 그것은 더욱 어김없이 떨어지기 위함이로군요.\" 알리가 다음 지시를 기다리며 주인을 바라보았다. \"지금 당장 검찰총장 빌포르 씨를 모셔 오너라. 포부르생토노레에 사는 분이다. 수위실을 지날 때 수위를 깨워 외과 의사를 부르러 보내고.\"\n\n알리는 분부를 따랐고, 아직 의식을 되찾지 못한 카드루스와 신부만이 남았다.\n\n가엾은 사내가 다시 눈을 떴을 때, 백작은 슬픈 연민의 표정으로 그를 바라보았고, 그의 입술은 마치 기도하듯 움직였다. \"의사를요, 신부님, 의사를!\" 카드루스가 말했다.\n\n\"사람을 보냈소.\" 신부가 대답했다.\n\n\"제 목숨을 구할 수 없다는 건 압니다. 하지만 증언할 힘은 채워 줄 수 있을지도 몰라요.\"\n\n\"누구에 대한 증언이오?\"\n\n\"저를 죽인 자에 대한 증언이오.\"\n\n\"그자를 알아보았소?\"\n\n\"네, 베네데토였어요.\"\n\n\"그 젊은 코르시카 사람 말이오?\" \"바로 그자예요.\"\n\n\"당신 동료요?\"\n\n\"당신의 동료 아니오?\"\n\"네. 그자는 이 집 도면을 제게 주었지요. 분명 제가 백작을 죽이면 자기가 백작의 상속자가 되거나, 아니면 백작이 저를 죽여 제가 길에서 사라지기를 바랐던 거예요. 그러더니 길목에서 저를 기다렸다가 칼로 찔렀습니다.\"\n\n\"검사도 불러오게 했소.\"\n\n\"제때 오지 못할 거예요. 목숨이 빠르게 빠져나가는 게 느껴져요.\"\n\n\"잠깐만 기다리시오.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. 그는 방을 나갔다가 오 분 뒤 작은 약병을 들고 돌아왔다. 죽어 가는 사내의 눈은 그동안 내내 문에 못 박혀 있었다. 그 문으로 구원의 손길이 들어오기를 바라고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"서둘러 주세요, 신부님, 서둘러요! 또 정신을 잃을 것 같아요!\" 몬테크리스토가 다가가 그의 보랏빛 입술에 약병 속 내용물을 서너 방울 떨어뜨렸다. 카드루스가 깊은 숨을 들이쉬었다. \"오.\" 그가 말했다. \"이건 제게 생명이에요. 더, 더요!\"\n\n\"두 방울만 더 떨어뜨리면 당신은 죽소.\" 신부가 대답했다.\n\n\"오, 그 악당을 고발할 수 있게 누구든 불러 주세요!\"\n\n\"내가 당신의 진술을 받아 적을까? 서명만 하면 되오.\"\n\n\"네, 네.\" 카드루스가 말했다. 이 죽음 이후의 복수를 생각하니 그의 눈이 번득였다. 몬테크리스토가 받아 적었다.\n\n\"나는 툴롱 감옥의 동료, 59호 코르시카 사람 베네데토에게 살해당해 죽는다.\"\n\n\"빨리요, 빨리!\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"안 그러면 서명할 수 없을 거예요.\"\n\n몬테크리스토가 카드루스에게 펜을 건넸고, 그는 남은 힘을 다 끌어모아 서명을 한 뒤 침대에 다시 쓰러지며 말했다.\n\n\"나머지는 다 신부님이 말씀해 주세요. 그자는 스스로 안드레아 카발칸티라고 부른다고요. 프랭스 호텔에 묵고 있어요. 오, 죽겠어요!\" 그는 다시 정신을 잃었다. 신부가 약병의 내용물을 그에게 맡게 하자, 그가 다시 눈을 떴다. 복수심은 그를 떠나지 않았다.\n\n\"아, 제가 한 말을 다 전해 주실 거지요, 신부님?\"\n\n\"그러겠소. 그보다 더 많은 것도.\"\n\n\"무엇을 더 말씀하실 건데요?\"\n\n\"그자가 분명 당신에게 이 집 도면을 주었다고 말하겠소. 백작이 당신을 죽이기를 바라면서 말이오. 또한 그자가 쪽지로 백작에게 당신의 계획을 미리 알렸으나, 백작이 자리에 없어 내가 그 쪽지를 읽고 밤을 새우며 당신을 기다렸다고 말하겠소.\"\n\n\"그러면 그자는 단두대로 가겠지요?\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"그렇게 약속해 주세요. 그러면 그 희망을 품고 죽겠어요.\"\n\n\"그자가 내내 당신을 따라다니며 지켜보았고,\" 백작이 말을 이었다. \"당신이 집에서 나오는 걸 보자 몸을 숨기려고 담 모퉁이로 달려갔다고 말하겠소.\"\n\n\"그걸 다 보셨어요?\"\n\n\"내 말을 기억하시오. '만약 당신이 무사히 집에 돌아간다면, 나는 하느님이 당신을 용서하셨다고 믿겠고, 나 또한 당신을 용서하겠소.'\"\n\n\"그러면서 저한테 경고하지 않으셨군요!\" 카드루스가 팔꿈치로 몸을 일으키며 외쳤다. \"이 집에서 나가면 제가 살해당하리라는 걸 알면서도, 경고하지 않으셨어요!\"\n\n\"그렇소. 하느님의 정의가 베네데토의 손에 맡겨진 것을 보았으니, 섭리의 뜻을 거스르는 것은 신성모독이라 여겼기 때문이오.\"\n\n\"하느님의 정의라뇨! 그런 말 마세요, 신부님. 하느님이 정의로우셨다면, 지금 벌을 면하고 있는 자들이 얼마나 많이 벌을 받았을지 신부님도 아실 거예요.\"\n\n\"참으시오.\" 신부가 죽어 가는 사내를 몸서리치게 만드는 어조로 말했다. \"참고 견디시오!\"\n\n카드루스가 놀라움에 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"게다가,\" 신부가 말했다. \"하느님은 모두에게 자비로우시오. 당신에게 그러셨던 것처럼 말이오. 그분은 먼저 아버지이고, 그다음에 심판자이시오.\"\n\n\"그럼 신부님은 하느님을 믿으세요?\" 카드루스가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 지금껏 하느님을 믿지 못할 만큼 불행했다 해도,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"당신을 보면 믿지 않을 수 없을 것이오.\"\n\n카드루스가 움켜쥔 두 손을 하늘로 치켜들었다.\n\n\"들으시오.\" 신부가 마치 믿으라 명령하듯 부상자 위로 손을 뻗으며 말했다. \"당신이 죽음의 자리에서 믿기를 거부하는 그 하느님이 당신을 위해 하신 일은 이렇소. 그분은 당신에게 건강과 힘, 변변한 일자리, 심지어 친구까지 주셨소. 한마디로 사람이 떳떳한 양심으로 누릴 수 있는 삶을 주신 것이오. 그토록 넉넉히 베풀어진 일이 드문 이 선물을 더 키우기는커녕, 당신은 이런 길을 걸었소. 게으름과 술독에 자신을 내맡겼고, 만취한 끝에 가장 가까운 벗을 파멸시켰소.\"\n\n\"사람 살려!\" 카드루스가 외쳤다. \"저한테 필요한 건 신부가 아니라 의사예요. 어쩌면 치명상이 아닐지도 몰라요. 죽지 않을 수도 있어요. 아직 제 목숨을 구할 수 있을지도 모른다고요.\"\n\n\"당신의 상처는 치명적이오. 내가 준 세 방울이 없었다면 당신은 지금쯤 죽었을 것이오. 그러니 들으시오.\"\n\n\"아.\" 카드루스가 중얼거렸다. \"참 별난 신부도 다 있네. 죽어 가는 사람을 위로하기는커녕 절망으로 몰아넣다니.\"\n\n\"들으시오.\" 신부가 말을 이었다. \"당신이 벗을 배신했을 때, 하느님은 치시지 않고 먼저 당신에게 경고하셨소. 가난이 당신에게 닥쳤소. 당신은 이미 정직하게 얻을 수도 있었던 것을 탐내며 인생의 절반을 보냈소. 그리고 궁핍을 핑계 삼아 이미 범죄를 꿈꾸고 있었는데, 그때 하느님이 당신을 위해 기적을 행하셨소. 내 손을 빌려 당신에게 재산을 보내신 것이오. 한 번도 가져 본 적 없는 당신에게는 참으로 눈부신 재산이었지. 그러나 한때 그토록 뜻밖이고 바라지도 못했으며 들어 본 적도 없던 그 재산은, 일단 손에 넣고 나니 더는 당신을 만족시키지 못했소. 당신은 그것을 두 배로 늘리고 싶어 했소. 그런데 어떻게? 살인으로 말이오! 당신은 성공했고, 그러자 하느님이 그것을 당신에게서 빼앗아 당신을 법정에 세우셨소.\"\n\n\"그 유대인을 죽이려 한 건 제가 아니었어요.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"라 카르콩트였어요.\"\n\n\"그렇소.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그리고 하느님은, 정의로써라고는 말하지 않겠소. 그분의 정의였다면 당신을 죽이셨을 테니. 다만 자비로써, 하느님은 당신의 목숨을 살려 주셨소.\"\n\n\"빌어먹을! 종신 유형에 처해 놓고는, 그게 자비라니!\"\n\n\"그때는 당신도 그것을 자비로 여겼지, 이 가엾은 자여! 죽음을 두려워하던 겁쟁이가 영원한 치욕을 두고 기뻐했지. 모든 갤리선 죄수가 그러하듯, 당신도 이렇게 말했으니까. '감옥에서야 달아날 수 있어도, 무덤에서는 달아날 수 없다.' 그 말은 옳았소. 길이 뜻밖에 당신에게 열렸으니까. 한 영국인이 툴롱을 찾아왔소. 그는 두 사람을 치욕에서 구해 내겠다고 맹세한 사람이었고, 그 선택이 당신과 당신 동료에게 떨어졌소. 당신은 두 번째 재산을 받았고, 돈과 평온을 되찾았으며, 중죄인의 삶을 선고받았던 당신이 여느 사람들처럼 살 수 있게 되었소. 그런데, 가엾은 자여, 그때 당신은 세 번째로 하느님을 시험했소. '이걸로는 부족해.' 전에 가졌던 것보다 더 많이 가졌으면서도 당신은 그렇게 말했고, 아무런 이유도 없이, 변명할 거리도 없이 세 번째 범죄를 저질렀소. 하느님은 지치셨고, 당신을 벌하신 것이오.\"\n\n카드루스는 빠르게 가라앉고 있었다. \"마실 것을 주세요.\" 그가 말했다. \"목이 말라요. 타들어 가요!\" 몬테크리스토가 그에게 물 한 잔을 주었다. \"그런데도 그 악당, 베네데토는 빠져나가겠지요!\"\n\n\"누구도 빠져나가지 못하오, 내 장담하오. 베네데토도 벌을 받을 것이오.\"\n\n\"그럼 신부님도 벌을 받을 거예요. 신부님은 신부로서 의무를 다하지 않았으니까요. 베네데토가 저를 죽이지 못하게 막았어야 했어요.\"\n\n\"내가?\" 백작이 죽어 가는 사내를 얼어붙게 만드는 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"당신이 막 내 가슴을 지켜 준 사슬 갑옷에 칼을 부러뜨린 마당에 말이오! 그래도 어쩌면 당신이 겸허하고 뉘우치는 모습이었다면, 베네데토가 당신을 죽이지 못하게 막았을지도 모르오. 하지만 나는 거만하고 피에 굶주린 당신을 보았기에, 당신을 하느님의 손에 맡겼소.\"\n\n\"하느님 같은 건 없어요.\" 카드루스가 울부짖었다. \"신부님도 믿지 않잖아요. 거짓말이에요. 거짓말!\"\n\n\"조용히 하시오.\" 신부가 말했다. \"당신은 핏줄에서 마지막 한 방울까지 짜내고 말 것이오. 뭐라고! 하느님이 당신을 쳐서 죽이시는데도 그분을 믿지 않겠다고? 기도 한마디, 말 한마디, 눈물 한 방울만 바라실 뿐 그러면 용서하시는 분을 믿지 않겠다고? 암살자의 단검을 이끌어 당신의 삶을 한순간에 끝낼 수도 있었던 하느님이, 당신에게 뉘우칠 십오 분을 주셨소. 그러니 깊이 생각하고 뉘우치시오, 가엾은 자여.\"\n\n\"싫어요.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"싫어요. 뉘우치지 않겠어요. 하느님은 없어요. 섭리도 없어요. 모든 게 우연으로 일어나는 거예요.\"\n\n\"섭리는 있소. 하느님도 계시오.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"당신이 바로 그 명백한 증거요. 당신은 더없는 절망 속에 누워 그분을 부정하고 있는데, 나는 당신 앞에 부유하고 행복하며 안전하게 서서, 당신이 애써 믿지 않으려 하면서도 마음속으로는 여전히 믿고 있는 그 하느님께 빌고 있으니 말이오.\"\n\n\"그런데 당신은 대체 누구예요?\" 카드루스가 죽어 가는 눈을 백작에게 고정한 채 물었다.\n\n\"나를 똑똑히 보시오!\" 몬테크리스토가 불빛을 자기 얼굴 가까이 가져가며 말했다.\n\n\"음, 신부님이네요. 부소니 신부님.\" 몬테크리스토는 자신의 얼굴을 흉하게 만들던 가발을 벗었고, 검은 머리카락이 흘러내렸다. 그 검은 머리는 창백한 이목구비에 한층 더한 아름다움을 더했다.\n\n\"오?\" 카드루스가 벼락 맞은 듯 놀라 말했다. \"저 검은 머리만 아니라면, 당신을 그 영국인, 윌모어 경이라고 했을 거예요.\"\n\n\"나는 부소니 신부도 아니고 윌모어 경도 아니오.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"다시 생각해 보시오. 나를 기억하지 못하겠소?\"\n\n백작의 말에는 마법 같은 힘이 있어서, 다 꺼져 가던 가엾은 사내의 기운을 다시 한 번 되살렸다.\n\n\"네, 정말로.\" 그가 말했다. \"당신을 본 적이 있고, 예전에 알았던 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"그렇소, 카드루스. 당신은 나를 본 적이 있고, 한때 나를 알았소.\"\n\n\"그럼, 당신은 누구예요? 그리고 저를 알면서, 왜 저를 죽게 내버려 두는 거예요?\"\n\n\"그 무엇도 당신을 살릴 수 없기 때문이오. 당신의 상처는 치명적이오. 당신을 살릴 수 있었다면, 나는 그것을 하느님의 자비를 보여 주는 또 하나의 증거로 여겼을 것이고, 다시 한 번 당신을 살리려 애썼을 것이오. 내 아버지의 무덤에 두고 맹세하오.\"\n\n\"당신 아버지의 무덤에 두고!\" 카드루스가 어떤 초자연적인 힘에 떠받쳐진 듯, 모든 사람이 신성하게 여기는 그 맹세를 방금 한 사내를 더 또렷이 보려고 반쯤 몸을 일으키며 말했다. \"그럼, 당신은 누구예요?\"\n\n백작은 죽음이 다가오는 것을 지켜보고 있었다. 그는 이것이 마지막 발버둥임을 알았다. 그는 죽어 가는 사내에게 다가가 고요하고 우수에 찬 눈빛으로 그 위로 몸을 숙이고는 속삭였다. \"나는, 나는……\"\n\n그리고 거의 닫힌 그의 입술이 어찌나 낮게 한 이름을 내뱉었는지, 백작 자신마저 그 소리를 듣기 두려워하는 듯했다. 무릎으로 몸을 일으키고 팔을 뻗고 있던 카드루스는 뒤로 물러나려 하다가, 두 손을 맞잡고 필사적인 안간힘으로 그것을 치켜들며 말했다. \"오, 하느님, 하느님! 당신을 부정한 저를 용서하소서. 당신은 진실로 존재하시고, 참으로 하늘에 계신 인간의 아버지이며, 땅 위의 심판자이십니다. 하느님, 주님, 저는 오랫동안 당신을 업신여겼습니다! 저를 용서하소서, 하느님. 저를 받아 주소서, 오, 주님!\"\n\n카드루스는 깊이 한숨을 내쉬고는 신음과 함께 뒤로 쓰러졌다. 그의 상처에서는 더 이상 피가 흐르지 않았다. 그는 죽었다.\n\n\"하나!\" 백작이 그토록 끔찍한 죽음으로 일그러진 시신에 눈을 고정한 채, 알 수 없는 어조로 말했다.\n\n십 분 뒤 외과 의사와 검사가 도착했다. 한 사람은 수위와 함께, 다른 한 사람은 알리와 함께였다. 그들은 시신 곁에서 기도하고 있던 부소니 신부의 맞이를 받았다.\n\n# Chapter 84. Beauchamp\n\n벌어진 일은 다음 보름 동안 파리 전역의 화젯거리였다. 백작의 집을 털려던 그 대담한 시도 말이다. 숨을 거두던 사내는 베네데토가 살인범이라고 진술서에 서명했다. 경찰은 살인범을 엄밀히 수색하라는 명령을 받았다. 카드루스의 칼과 어둠상자등, 열쇠 꾸러미, 그리고 끝내 찾지 못한 조끼를 제외한 옷가지가 등기소에 보관되었다. 시신은 시체 안치소로 옮겨졌다. 백작은 이 사건이 자기가 오퇴유에 가 있어 자리를 비운 사이에 일어났다고 사람들에게 말했다. 자기는 그날 저녁 우연히 서재의 진귀한 책 몇 권을 살펴보겠다며 하룻밤 묵게 해 달라고 청한 부소니 신부에게서 들은 것밖에는 모른다고 했다.\n\n오직 베르투치오만이 자기 앞에서 베네데토의 이름이 거론될 때마다 얼굴이 창백해졌으나, 누군가 그것을 눈여겨볼 이유는 없었다.\n\n빌포르는 범죄를 입증하라는 임무를 맡아, 형사 사건에서 변론할 때 늘 발휘하던 그 열의를 다해 기소장을 준비하고 있었다.\n\n그러나 벌써 삼 주가 지났는데도 가장 부지런한 수색조차 성과가 없었다. 그 절도 미수 사건과 동료의 손에 죽은 도둑 이야기는, 다가오는 당글라르 양과 안드레아 카발칸티 백작의 결혼식에 대한 기대 속에 거의 잊혀 갔다. 이 결혼은 머지않아 이루어질 것으로 예상되었으니, 그 젊은이는 은행가의 집에서 약혼자로 대접받고 있었다.\n\n카발칸티 씨, 곧 백작의 아버지에게는 편지가 보내졌다. 그는 이 결합을 크게 찬성하면서도 그때 파르마를 떠날 수 없는 사정을 안타까워했고, 십오만 리브르를 결혼 선물로 주겠다고 약속했다. 삼백만은 당글라르에게 맡겨 투자하게 한다는 데 합의가 이루어졌다. 어떤 이들은 그 젊은이에게 장차 장인이 될 사람의 사정을 일러 주었다. 당글라르가 근래 거듭 손실을 입었다는 것이었다. 그러나 그 젊은이는 더없이 고결한 무사무욕과 신뢰로, 귀를 기울이려 하지도, 남작에게 일말의 의혹도 내비치려 하지 않았다.\n\n남작은 안드레아 카발칸티 백작을 끔찍이 아꼈다. 외제니 당글라르 양은 그렇지 않았다. 결혼이라면 본능적으로 싫어하던 그녀는 모르세르를 떼어 내려고 안드레아의 구애를 견뎌 냈다. 그러나 안드레아가 청혼을 밀어붙이자 그에 대한 노골적인 혐오를 드러냈다. 남작이 그것을 알아챘을지도 모르나, 그저 변덕으로 돌리고는 모르는 척했다.\n\n보상이 요구한 유예 기간이 거의 다 끝나 가고 있었다. 모르세르는 모든 일을 그냥 자연히 가라앉게 두라는 몬테크리스토의 충고를 마음에 새겼다. 그 장군에 관한 언급을 받아 든 사람은 아무도 없었고, 야니나성을 팔아넘긴 그 장교를 귀족원의 고결한 백작과 결부 짓는 사람도 없었다.\n\n그러나 알베르는 여전히 모욕을 느끼고 있었다. 그를 격분케 한 그 몇 줄은 분명 모욕할 작정으로 쓰인 것이었다. 게다가 보상이 그 대화를 끝맺던 태도가 그의 가슴에 쓰라린 기억을 남겼다. 그는 그 결투를 마음에 품고 있었고, 진짜 이유는 입회인들에게조차 숨기려 했다. 보상은 알베르를 찾아왔던 그날 이후로 모습을 보이지 않았다. 그의 행방을 물을 때마다 사람들은 언제나, 며칠은 더 걸릴 여행을 떠났다고만 했다. 어디에 있는지는 아무도 몰랐다.\n\n어느 날 아침, 알베르는 보상이 왔다고 알리는 시종의 목소리에 잠에서 깼다. 알베르는 눈을 비비고는, 그를 일층의 작은 흡연실로 안내하라고 일렀다. 그러고는 서둘러 옷을 입고 내려갔다.\n\n보상이 방 안을 서성이고 있었다. 그는 알베르를 보자 걸음을 멈추었다.\n\n\"내가 오늘 자네 집으로 찾아갈 때까지 기다리지 않고 이렇게 와 준 건 좋은 조짐이군.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"말해 보게. 자네와 악수하며 '보상, 자네가 내게 잘못했음을 인정하고 내 우정을 간직하게'라고 해도 되겠나, 아니면 그저 자네에게 무기를 고르라고 청해야겠나?\"\n\n\"알베르,\" 보상이 말했다. 그 슬픈 눈빛에 젊은이는 멍해졌다. \"우선 앉아서 이야기부터 하세.\"\n\n\"아니, 앉기 전에 나는 자네의 대답을 들어야겠네.\"\n\n\"알베르,\" 신문기자가 말했다. \"대답하기 어려운 질문일세.\"\n\n\"질문을 다시 해서 쉽게 만들어 주지. '취소하겠나, 안 하겠나?'\"\n\n\"모르세르, 프랑스의 귀족이자 모르세르 백작 중장 같은 사람의 명예와 사회적 이해와 목숨에 관한 질문에는 '예'나 '아니요'로 답하는 것만으로는 부족하네.\"\n\n\"그럼 어떻게 해야 한단 말인가?\"\n\n\"내가 한 대로 해야지, 알베르. 나는 이렇게 따져 보았네. 돈과 시간과 수고 따위는 한 가문 전체의 명성과 이해에 비하면 아무것도 아니라고. 개연성만으로는 부족하고, 오직 사실만이 친구와 목숨을 건 결투를 정당화할 수 있다고. 삼 년 동안 가까이 지내 온 사람에게 칼을 휘두르거나 권총을 쏘려면, 적어도 내가 왜 그러는지는 알아야 하지 않겠나. 제 팔로 제 목숨을 지켜야 하는 사람에게 필요한, 그 편안한 마음과 조용한 양심으로 그와 마주해야 하는 거야.\"\n\n\"그래서,\" 모르세르가 조바심을 내며 말했다. \"이 모든 게 무슨 뜻인가?\"\n\n\"내가 방금 야니나에서 돌아왔다는 뜻일세.\"\n\n\"야니나에서?\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n\"그럴 리가!\"\n\n\"여기 내 여권이 있네. 비자를 살펴보게. 제네바, 밀라노, 베네치아, 트리에스테, 델비노, 야니나. 공화국과 왕국과 제국의 정부를 못 믿겠나?\" 알베르는 여권에 눈길을 던졌다가, 놀란 눈을 들어 보상을 보았다.\n\n\"자네 야니나에 다녀왔단 말인가?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"알베르, 자네가 만약 모르는 사람이거나, 외국인이거나, 서너 달 전에 결투를 청하러 왔다가 귀찮아서 내가 죽여 버린 그 영국인 같은 한낱 귀족이었다면, 나는 이런 수고를 하지 않았을 걸세. 하지만 자네에게는 이런 배려를 해야 마땅하다고 여겼네. 가는 데 일주일, 돌아오는 데 일주일, 검역에 나흘, 그곳에 머무는 데 마흔여덟 시간이 걸렸지. 그러니 삼 주가 된 거야. 어젯밤에 돌아왔고, 이렇게 여기 있는 걸세.\"\n\n\"무슨 말을 그리 빙빙 돌리나! 내가 가장 알고 싶어 하는 걸 말하기까지 어찌 이리 오래 끄는 건가?\"\n\n\"실은 말일세, 알베르……\"\n\n\"망설이는 건가?\"\n\n\"그래. 두렵네.\"\n\n\"자네에게 제보한 사람이 자네를 속였다고 인정하기가 두려운가? 아, 자존심은 버리게, 보상. 인정하게, 보상. 자네 용기야 누가 의심하겠나.\"\n\n\"그게 아니야.\" 신문기자가 중얼거렸다. \"오히려 그 반대일세……\"\n\n알베르는 무섭도록 창백해졌다. 그는 입을 떼려 했으나, 말이 입술에서 죽어 버렸다.\n\n\"여보게,\" 보상이 더없이 다정한 어조로 말했다. \"나도 기꺼이 사과하고 싶네. 하지만, 아아……\"\n\"하지만 뭐란 말인가?\"\n\n\"그 기사는 사실이었네, 친구.\"\n\n\"뭐라고? 그 프랑스 장교가……\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n\"페르낭이?\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n\"자기가 섬기던 사람의 성을 팔아넘긴 그 배신자가……\"\n\n\"미안하네, 친구. 그 사람이 바로 자네 아버지였어!\"\n\n알베르는 격분하여 보상에게 달려들었으나, 보상은 내민 손보다도 그 부드러운 눈빛으로 그를 더 잘 막아 냈다.\n\n\"여보게,\" 그가 말했다. \"여기 그 증거가 있네.\"\n\n알베르는 종이를 펼쳤다. 야니나의 명망 있는 주민 네 사람의 진술서로, 알리 테펠리니를 섬기던 페르낭 몬데고 대령이 이백만 크라운을 받고 성을 넘겨주었음을 입증하는 것이었다. 서명은 완벽하게 합법적이었다. 알베르는 비틀거리다가 짓눌린 듯 의자에 쓰러졌다. 더는 의심할 수 없었다. 가문의 성씨가 고스란히 적혀 있었다. 잠시 침통한 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 그의 가슴이 북받쳐 올라 그는 눈물을 쏟아 냈다. 젊은이의 격렬한 슬픔을 진심 어린 연민으로 지켜보던 보상이 그에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"이제, 알베르,\" 그가 말했다. \"내 마음을 알겠지, 안 그런가? 나는 모든 걸 직접 보고 모든 걸 내 눈으로 판단하고 싶었네. 그 설명이 자네 아버지께 유리하기를, 그래서 내가 그분의 누명을 벗겨 드릴 수 있기를 바라면서 말이야. 그러나 오히려 거기 적힌 자세한 정황은, 알리 파샤에 의해 총독의 지위에까지 오른 페르낭 몬데고가 다름 아닌 모르세르의 페르낭 백작임을 증명하더군. 그래서 자네가 나를 우정으로 받아 준 그 영광을 떠올리고는, 서둘러 자네에게 달려온 걸세.\"\n\n알베르는 여전히 의자에 늘어진 채 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다. 빛이 닿는 것조차 막으려는 듯이.\n\n\"내가 자네에게 서둘러 온 것은,\" 보상이 말을 이었다. \"이 말을 하기 위해서일세, 알베르. 이 변화무쌍한 시대에 아버지의 잘못이 그 자식에게 되돌아올 수는 없다고 말이야. 우리가 태어난 그 혁명의 시기를, 군인의 군복이나 법관의 법복에 불명예와 피의 얼룩 하나 묻히지 않고 지나온 사람은 거의 없네. 자, 이제 내게 이 증거들이 있고, 나는 자네의 비밀을 알게 되었으니, 그 어떤 인간의 힘으로도 나를 결투로 끌어낼 수는 없네. 자네의 양심조차 그것을 범죄라며 자네를 책망할 테니까. 그래서 나는 자네가 더는 내게 요구할 수 없게 된 것을, 이렇게 자네에게 내놓으려 왔네. 나만이 가지고 있는 이 증거들, 이 진술서들을 없애 버리길 바라나? 이 끔찍한 비밀이 우리 둘만의 것으로 남길 바라나? 내게 맡겨진 이상, 그것은 결코 내 입 밖으로 새어 나가지 않을 걸세. 말해 보게, 알베르, 친구여. 그러기를 바라나?\"\n\n알베르는 보상의 목을 끌어안았다.\n\n\"아, 고귀한 친구여!\" 그가 외쳤다.\n\n\"이걸 받게.\" 보상이 알베르에게 그 서류들을 건네며 말했다.\n\n알베르는 경련하듯 떨리는 손으로 그것들을 움켜쥐어 갈기갈기 찢었다. 그리고 행여 작은 조각 하나라도 남아 언젠가 자기 앞에 다시 나타날까 두려워, 늘 시가를 피우려고 켜 두는 양초 불 곁으로 다가가 한 조각도 남김없이 태웠다.\n\n\"소중하고도 더없이 훌륭한 친구여.\" 알베르가 여전히 종이를 태우며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"모든 걸 슬픈 꿈처럼 잊어버리게.\" 보상이 말했다. \"저 까맣게 탄 종이에서 튀는 마지막 불꽃처럼 사라지게 하고, 저 고요한 잿더미에서 피어오르는 연기처럼 흩어지게 하게.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"그리고 오직, 나를 구해 준 자네에게 약속한 그 영원한 우정만이 남기를. 그 우정은 우리 자손의 자손에게까지 전해질 것이고, 내가 내 목숨과 가문의 명예를 자네에게 빚졌음을 늘 일깨워 주겠지. 만일 이 일이 알려졌다면, 아, 보상, 나는 스스로 목숨을 끊었을 걸세. 아니, 아니야. 가엾은 내 어머니! 같은 일격으로 어머니까지 죽일 수는 없으니, 나는 조국을 떠나 달아났을 거야.\"\n\n\"가엾은 알베르.\" 보상이 말했다. 그러나 그 갑작스럽고 꾸며진 듯한 기쁨은 이내 젊은이에게서 사라지고, 한층 더 깊은 슬픔이 그 자리를 대신했다.\n\n\"그래,\" 보상이 말했다. \"여전히 무엇이 자네를 짓누르나, 친구?\"\n\n\"가슴이 무너지는 것 같네.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"들어 보게, 보상! 나는 이렇게 한순간에, 아버지의 더럽혀지지 않은 이름이 아들에게 불어넣어 주던 그 존경과 신뢰와 자부심을 놓아 버릴 수가 없네. 아, 보상, 보상, 이제 내가 어떻게 아버지께 다가갈 수 있겠나? 아버지의 품에서 이마를 거두어야 하나, 아니면 그 손에서 내 손을 빼야 하나? 나는 세상에서 가장 비참한 사람일세. 아, 내 어머니, 가엾은 내 어머니!\" 알베르는 눈물 너머로 어머니의 초상화를 바라보며 말했다. \"어머니가 이 일을 아신다면, 얼마나 괴로워하실까!\"\n\n\"자,\" 보상이 그의 두 손을 잡으며 말했다. \"용기를 내게, 친구.\"\n\n\"하지만 그 첫 기사가 어떻게 자네 신문에 실리게 된 건가? 누군가 알 수 없는 적이, 보이지 않는 원수가 이런 짓을 한 거야.\"\n\n\"그러니 더욱 마음을 굳게 다잡아야 하네, 알베르. 자네 얼굴에 동요의 흔적이 조금도 비치지 않게 하게. 자네의 슬픔을, 그 안에 파멸과 죽음을 품은 먹구름처럼 견뎌 내게. 폭풍이 터질 때에야 비로소 드러나는 치명적인 비밀처럼 말일세. 가게, 친구. 파국이 닥칠 그 순간을 위해 힘을 아껴 두게.\"\n\n\"그럼 자네 생각엔, 아직 다 끝난 게 아니란 말인가?\" 알베르가 공포에 질려 말했다.\n\n\"나는 아무 생각도 안 하네, 친구. 하지만 무슨 일이든 일어날 수 있지. 그건 그렇고……\"\n\n\"뭔가?\" 보상이 머뭇거리는 것을 보고 알베르가 말했다.\n\n\"자네 당글라르 양과 결혼할 셈인가?\"\n\n\"왜 이제 와서 그걸 묻나?\"\n\n\"이 약혼이 깨지느냐 이루어지느냐가, 우리가 이야기하던 그 사람과 얽혀 있어서 말이야.\"\n\n\"어째서?\" 알베르가 이마를 붉히며 말했다. \"자네 생각엔 당글라르 씨가……\"\n\n\"나는 그저 자네 약혼이 어떻게 되었는지를 물을 뿐이야. 제발 내 말에 담지도 않은 뜻을 갖다 붙이지 말고, 부당하게 무게를 싣지도 말게.\"\n\n\"아니.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"약혼은 깨졌네.\"\n\n\"그렇군.\" 보상이 말했다. 그러고는 젊은이가 다시 우울에 빠져들려는 것을 보고 말했다. \"나가세, 알베르. 사두마차를 타든 말을 타든 숲을 한 바퀴 돌면 기운이 날 걸세. 그런 다음 돌아와 아침을 들고, 자네는 자네 일을 보고 나는 내 일을 보면 되지.\"\n\n\"좋아.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"하지만 걷세. 좀 움직이는 게 내게 도움이 될 것 같아.\"\n\n두 친구는 성벽 쪽으로 걸어 나갔다. 마들렌 성당에 이르렀을 때였다.\n\n\"이왕 나왔으니,\" 보상이 말했다. \"몬테크리스토 씨를 찾아가세. 그분은 사람의 기운을 북돋우는 데 더없이 알맞은 분이거든. 절대 캐묻는 법이 없으니까. 내 생각엔 아무것도 묻지 않는 사람이 가장 좋은 위로자일세.\"\n\n\"기꺼이.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"찾아가세. 나는 그분이 좋아.\"\n\n# Chapter 85. The Journey\n\n몬테크리스토는 두 젊은이가 함께 있는 것을 보고 반가운 탄성을 질렀다. \"아, 됐군요!\" 그가 말했다. \"다 끝나고, 해명도 끝나고, 정리도 됐겠지요.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\" 보샹이 말했다. \"그 터무니없는 소문들은 잦아들었고, 혹시라도 다시 나돈다면 제가 가장 먼저 나서서 막을 겁니다. 그러니 더 이야기하지 맙시다.\"\n\n\"알베르가 말해 줄 겁니다.\" 백작이 대답했다. \"저도 그에게 똑같은 충고를 했지요. 보세요.\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"정말 끔찍한 오전 일거리를 막 끝내는 참입니다.\"\n\n\"그게 뭡니까?\" 알베르가 물었다. \"보아하니 서류 정리를 하시는 모양인데요.\"\n\n\"제 서류라니요, 천만에요. 다행히도 제 서류는 모두 더할 나위 없이 정돈돼 있습니다, 아예 없으니까요. 카발칸티 씨의 서류입니다.\"\n\n\"카발칸티 씨의 서류라고요?\" 보샹이 물었다.\n\n\"네. 이 사람이 백작님께서 소개하시는 젊은이라는 걸 모르셨나요?\" 모르세르가 말했다.\n\n\"서로 오해는 하지 맙시다.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"저는 아무도 소개하지 않습니다. 카발칸티 씨는 더더욱 아니고요.\"\n\n\"그리고 그자가,\" 알베르가 억지웃음을 지으며 말했다. \"저 대신 당글라르 양과 결혼하게 되는데, 그게 어찌나 가슴 아픈지요.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요? 카발칸티가 당글라르 양과 결혼한다고요?\" 보샹이 물었다.\n\n\"물론이지요! 세상 끝에서라도 오신 겁니까?\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"기자에다, 소문의 남편이라는 분이? 파리가 온통 그 이야긴데요.\"\n\n\"그럼 백작님, 이 혼사를 주선하셨습니까?\" 보샹이 물었다.\n\n\"제가요? 조용히 하세요, 소문 장수 양반. 그런 말은 퍼뜨리지 마세요. 제가 혼사를 주선한다고요? 아니요, 당신은 저를 모릅니다. 저는 그걸 막으려고 할 수 있는 모든 일을 다 했어요.\"\n\n\"아, 알겠습니다.\" 보샹이 말했다. \"우리 친구 알베르를 위해서로군요.\"\n\n\"저를 위해서라고요?\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"오, 천만에요. 백작님께서 공정하게 증언해 주실 겁니다. 오히려 제가 늘 백작님께 제 약혼을 깨 달라고 간청했고, 다행히 끝이 났지요. 백작님은 그게 당신 덕은 아니라고 하시지만, 그렇다면 좋습니다, 저는 '알지 못하는 신께' 제단을 쌓겠습니다.\"\n\n* 알지 못하는 신께(Deo ignoto): 사도행전에 나오는 아테네의 제단 문구로, 누구 덕인지 모르나 감사를 바친다는 뜻—옮긴이.\n\"들어 보세요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"저는 이 일에 거의 관여하지 않았습니다. 장인 될 사람과도, 그 젊은이와도 사이가 틀어졌으니까요. 오직 외제니 양만이, 결혼이라는 생각에 별로 마음이 끌리지 않는 듯한데, 제가 그녀에게 소중한 자유를 버리라고 설득할 마음이 조금도 없는 걸 보고는 그나마 제게 약간의 호의를 품고 있지요.\"\n\n\"그런데 이 결혼이 임박했다고 하셨습니까?\"\n\n\"오, 그렇습니다, 제가 무슨 말을 해도 소용없이요. 저는 그 젊은이를 모릅니다. 가문도 좋고 재산도 많다고들 하지만, 저는 막연한 주장은 결코 믿지 않습니다. 당글라르 씨에게 지칠 때까지 경고했지만, 그는 자기 그 루카 사람한테 홀딱 빠져 있어요. 제가 아주 심각하게 여기는 사정까지 알려 주었지요. 그 젊은이는 유모에게 홀렸거나, 집시들에게 납치됐거나, 가정교사가 잃어버렸거나, 그중 무엇인지 저도 잘 모릅니다. 하지만 그 아버지가 십 년 넘게 그를 보지 못했다는 것만은 압니다. 그 십 년 동안 그가 무슨 짓을 했는지는 하느님만 아실 일이지요. 그런데도 다 소용없었습니다. 그들은 제게 그 소령에게 편지를 써서 서류를 요구하라고 맡겼고, 여기 그것들이 있습니다. 저는 보내기는 합니다만, 빌라도처럼 손을 씻는 심정으로요.\"\n\n\"그런데 다르밀리 양은 제자를 빼앗긴 일을 두고 백작님께 뭐라고 합니까?\"\n\n\"오, 글쎄요, 모르겠습니다. 다만 그녀가 이탈리아로 갈 거라는 건 압니다. 당글라르 부인이 제게 흥행주들에게 보낼 추천장을 부탁하더군요. 제게 신세를 진 발레 극장 지배인에게 보내는 몇 줄을 적어 주었지요. 그런데 무슨 일입니까, 알베르? 안색이 어두워 보여요. 설마, 자기도 모르게 외제니 양을 사랑하고 있는 건 아니겠지요?\"\n\n\"그건 저도 모르겠습니다.\" 알베르가 서글프게 미소 지으며 말했다. 보샹은 몇몇 그림을 보러 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 몬테크리스토가 말을 이었다. \"평소 같은 기색이 아니로군요?\"\n\n\"머리가 끔찍하게 아픕니다.\" 알베르가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면, 자작,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"내가 당신에게 권할 확실한 처방이 하나 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그게 뭡니까?\" 젊은이가 물었다.\n\n\"그래요?\" 알베르가 말했다.\n\n\"정말요?\" 알베르가 말했다.\n\"그래요. 마침 나도 지금 몹시 짜증스러운 일이 있어서 집을 떠나려던 참입니다. 함께 가시겠어요?\"\n\n\"짜증스러운 일이라니요, 백작님?\" 보샹이 말했다. \"무엇 때문에요?\"\n\n\"아, 당신은 그걸 아주 가볍게 여기시는군요. 당신 집을 겨냥해 기소장이 준비되고 있다면 어떤 기분일지 보고 싶군요.\"\n\n\"무슨 기소장요?\"\n\n\"빌포르 씨가 내 살가운 암살자를 상대로 준비 중인 그것 말입니다. 보아하니 교수대에서 도망친 어떤 산적인 모양이지요.\"\n\n\"맞아요.\" 보샹이 말했다. \"신문에서 봤습니다. 그 카드루스라는 자는 누굽니까?\"\n\n\"보아하니 프로방스 사람인 듯합니다. 빌포르 씨는 마르세유에서 그자에 대해 들었고, 당글라르 씨는 그를 본 적이 있다고 기억하더군요. 그래서 검사가 이 사건에 무척 적극적이고, 경찰청장도 큰 관심을 가지고 있지요. 그리고 그 관심 덕분에 — 정말 고맙기 그지없는 일이지만 — 그들은 파리와 인근의 도둑이란 도둑은 죄다 카드루스의 살인범이라는 구실로 내게 보내고 있습니다. 그래서 이대로 가면 석 달 안에 프랑스의 도둑과 암살자란 자들은 모조리 내 집 구조를 손금 보듯 훤히 알게 될 겁니다. 나는 그들을 버리고 땅 끝 외딴 구석으로 가기로 결심했습니다. 자작, 당신이 함께 가 준다면 더없이 기쁘겠어요.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 가겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 정해진 거지요?\"\n\n\"네, 그런데 어디로요?\"\n\n\"내가 말했잖아요, 공기가 맑고, 온갖 소리가 마음을 달래 주고, 천성이 아무리 오만한 사람이라도 반드시 겸허해지는 곳으로요. 나는 그 겸허함을 사랑합니다, 아우구스투스가 그랬듯 우주의 주인인 이 몸이 말이지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 정말로 어디로 가시는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"바다로요, 자작. 알다시피 나는 뱃사람입니다. 갓난아기 때 늙은 대양의 품에서, 아름다운 암피트리테의 가슴 위에서 흔들리며 자랐지요. 한쪽의 초록 망토와 다른 쪽의 푸른 옷자락을 가지고 놀았습니다. 나는 바다를 연인처럼 사랑해서, 자주 보지 못하면 애가 탑니다.\"\n* 암피트리테: 그리스 신화의 바다의 여신—옮긴이.\n\n\"갑시다, 백작님.\"\n\n\"바다로요?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"내 제안을 받아들이는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"받아들이지요.\"\n\"좋습니다, 자작. 오늘 저녁 내 안마당에 역마 네 필을 맨 좋은 여행용 브리츠카가 대기할 겁니다. 침대처럼 편히 쉴 수 있지요. 보샹 씨, 넷이 타도 넉넉합니다, 같이 가시겠어요?\"\n\n\"고맙지만, 나는 막 바다에서 돌아온 참입니다.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요? 바다에 다녀오셨다고요?\"\n\n\"네. 보로메오 제도로 작은 여행을 막 다녀왔지요.\"\n\n* 보로메오 제도: 이탈리아 마조레 호수의 섬들—옮긴이.\n\n\"그게 무슨 상관입니까? 우리와 같이 가시지요.\" 알베르가 말했다.\n\"아니요, 모르세르. 알다시피 나는 도저히 불가능할 때만 거절합니다. 게다가 중요한 일이 있어요.\" 그가 낮은 목소리로 덧붙였다. \"지금은 파리에 남아서 신문을 지켜봐야 합니다.\"\n\n\"아, 당신은 좋고 훌륭한 친구로군요.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"그래요, 당신 말이 옳습니다. 지켜봐 주세요, 보샹, 잘 지켜봐서 이 폭로를 한 적이 누군지 알아내 주세요.\"\n\n알베르와 보샹은 헤어졌다. 마지막으로 맞잡은 손은 낯선 사람 앞에서 혀로는 미처 하지 못한 말을 대신 전하고 있었다.\n\n\"보샹은 괜찮은 친구로군요.\" 기자가 떠나자 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그렇지 않습니까, 알베르?\"\n\n\"네, 진실한 친구지요. 진심으로 그를 사랑합니다. 그런데 이제 우리 둘뿐이니 — 저야 상관없습니다만 — 어디로 가는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"원한다면 노르망디로 가지요.\"\n\n\"좋습니다. 아주 한적할까요? 어울릴 사람도, 이웃도 없을까요?\"\n\n\"우리 동무는 승마용 말들과 사냥개들, 그리고 고깃배 한 척이 될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"바로 제가 바라던 바입니다. 어머니께 제 뜻을 알리고 다시 오겠습니다.\"\n\n\"한데 노르망디로 가도록 허락받을 수 있겠어요?\"\n\n\"저는 가고 싶은 곳 어디든 갈 수 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그래요, 혼자라면 갈 수 있다는 건 압니다, 언젠가 이탈리아에서 당신을 만났으니까요. 하지만 이 수수께끼 같은 몬테크리스토와 동행하는 거라면요?\"\n\n\"잊으셨군요, 백작님. 제 어머니가 당신에게 깊은 관심을 두고 계신다고 제가 자주 말씀드렸잖아요.\"\n\n\"'여자는 변덕스럽다'고 프랑수아 1세는 말했고, '여자는 바다의 물결 같다'고 셰익스피어는 말했지요. 그 위대한 왕도, 그 위대한 시인도 여자의 본성을 잘 알았을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"여자의 본성이라면 그렇지요. 하지만 제 어머니는 그런 '여자'가 아니라, '한 사람의 여인'입니다.\"\n\n\"나는 한낱 보잘것없는 외국인일 뿐이니, 당신 말의 그 미묘한 뉘앙스를 다 알아듣지 못해도 용서해 주세요.\"\n\n\"제 말은, 어머니는 쉽사리 신뢰를 주지 않으시지만, 한번 주면 결코 변하지 않으신다는 뜻입니다.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇군요, 정말로.\" 몬테크리스토가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"그래서 어머니께서 나에게 조금이라도 관심을 두신다고 생각합니까?\"\n\n\"다시 말씀드리지만, 당신은 정말 무척 기이하고 비범한 분임에 틀림없어요. 어머니는 당신이 불러일으킨 관심에 어찌나 사로잡혀 계신지, 제가 곁에 있으면 다른 누구의 이야기도 하지 않으십니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 어머니께서 당신이 나를 싫어하도록 만들려 하시던가요?\"\n\n\"오히려 그 반대입니다. 자주 말씀하시지요. '모르세르, 내가 보기엔 백작은 고결한 천성을 지닌 분이다. 그분의 존경을 얻도록 애써 보아라.'\"\n\n\"정말요?\" 몬테크리스토가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다.\n\n\"그러니 보십시오.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"어머니는 저를 말리기는커녕 오히려 부추기실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 다섯 시까지 작별입시다. 정확히 와요, 그래야 열두 시나 한 시에는 도착할 테니까요.\"\n\n\"트레포르에요?\"\n\n\"네, 아니면 그 근방에요.\"\n\n\"하지만 여덟 시간에 사십팔 리외를 갈 수 있을까요?\"\n\n\"쉽지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"당신은 정말 경이로운 분이로군요. 머지않아 철도를 앞지르는 건 물론이고 — 프랑스에서야 그리 어려운 일도 아니겠지만 — 전신까지 앞지르겠어요.\"\n\n\"하지만 자작, 그 여정을 일고여덟 시간 안에는 마칠 수 없으니, 나를 기다리게 하지는 마세요.\"\n\n\"걱정 마세요, 준비할 게 별로 없으니까요.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 알베르에게 고개를 끄덕이며 미소 지었고, 그러고는 잠시 깊은 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 그러나 몽상을 떨쳐 내려는 듯 이마를 손으로 한번 쓸더니, 종을 두 번 울렸고 베르투초가 들어왔다.\n\n\"베르투초,\" 그가 말했다. \"내일이나 모레가 아니라 오늘 저녁 노르망디로 떠날 생각이네. 다섯 시 전까지면 시간은 넉넉할 거야. 전령을 보내 첫 번째 역참의 마부들에게 알리게. 모르세르 씨가 나와 동행할 걸세.\"\n\n베르투초는 명령에 따라, 여행용 마차가 여섯 시에 도착할 거라고 알리려 퐁투아즈로 급사를 보냈다. 퐁투아즈에서 다시 다음 역참으로 급송이 띄워졌고, 여섯 시간 만에 도로변에 배치된 말들이 모두 준비를 마쳤다.\n\n떠나기 전에 백작은 하이데의 거처로 가서 자신의 계획을 알리고, 모든 일을 그녀의 손에 맡겼다.\n\n알베르는 시간을 정확히 지켰다. 여정은 그 빠르기 덕분에 곧 흥미진진해졌는데, 모르세르는 그런 속도를 미처 상상해 본 적이 없었다.\n\n\"정말이지,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"역마는 한 시간에 고작 두 리외를 가는 데다, 한 여행자가 허락 없이 다른 여행자를 앞지르지 못한다는 그 터무니없는 법까지 있어서, 병자나 성마른 여행자 하나가 멀쩡하고 활동적인 사람들을 붙들어 둘 수 있으니, 도무지 나아갈 수가 없지요. 나는 내 마부와 내 말을 데리고 다녀서 이 골칫거리를 피합니다. 그렇지 않나, 알리?\"\n\n백작이 창밖으로 고개를 내밀고 휘파람을 불자, 말들이 날아오르는 듯했다. 마차는 천둥 같은 소리를 내며 포석 위를 굴렀고, 모두가 그 눈부신 유성을 돌아보았다. 알리는 미소를 지으며 그 휘파람 소리를 따라 내더니, 단단한 손으로 고삐를 움켜쥐고 말들에 박차를 가했고, 말들의 아름다운 갈기가 바람에 나부꼈다. 이 사막의 아들은 제 세상을 만난 듯했고, 검은 얼굴과 번득이는 눈으로, 그가 일으킨 먼지구름 속에서 마치 사막 열풍의 정령이자 폭풍의 신처럼 보였다.\n\n\"지금껏 속도의 즐거움을 미처 몰랐습니다.\" 모르세르가 말했고, 그의 이마에서 마지막 그늘이 걷혔다. \"그런데 도대체 이런 말들을 어디서 구하십니까? 주문 제작이라도 하는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"바로 그렇지요.\" 백작이 말했다. \"육 년 전 헝가리에서 빠르기로 유명한 말 한 필을 샀습니다. 오늘 밤 우리가 쓸 서른두 필이 모두 그 말의 자손입니다. 이마에 별 무늬 하나가 있는 것만 빼면 전부 완전히 검지요.\"\n\n\"정말 감탄스럽군요. 그런데 백작님, 이 많은 말들로 무얼 하십니까?\"\n\n\"보다시피, 이 말들로 여행을 하지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 늘 여행을 하시는 건 아니잖아요.\"\n\n\"더는 필요 없어지면 베르투초가 팔 겁니다. 그는 그 판매로 삼사만 프랑은 챙길 셈이지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 유럽의 어떤 군주도 그걸 살 만큼 부유하지는 못할 텐데요.\"\n\n\"그러면 어느 동방의 재상에게 팔 겁니다. 그자는 곳간을 비워 가며 사들이고, 백성들에게 곤장을 쳐서 다시 곳간을 채우겠지요.\"\n* 곤장(bastinado): 발바닥을 매질하던 동방의 형벌—옮긴이.\n\n\"백작님, 한 가지 생각을 말씀드려도 될까요?\"\n\n\"물론이지요.\"\n\n\"당신 다음으로는 베르투초가 유럽에서 가장 부유한 신사일 게 틀림없다는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"틀렸습니다, 자작. 내 생각엔 그에게는 단돈 한 프랑도 없을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그는 정말 신기한 사람이로군요. 백작님, 이렇게 놀라운 이야기를 자꾸 들려주시면, 미리 말씀드리지만 저는 더 이상 믿지 않을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"나는 놀라운 일이라곤 조금도 부추기지 않습니다, 알베르 씨. 말해 보세요, 집사가 왜 주인의 것을 훔칠까요?\"\n\n\"제 생각엔, 훔치는 게 본성이라서겠지요, 훔치는 게 좋아서요.\"\n\n\"틀렸습니다. 아내와 가족이 있고, 자신과 그들을 위한 야심 찬 욕망이 있기 때문이지요. 또 늘 그 자리를 지킨다는 보장이 없으니 앞날에 대비하고 싶어서이고요. 그런데 베르투초는 이 세상에 홀로입니다. 그는 내 재산을 쓰면서도 그 씀씀이를 일일이 보고하지 않고, 결코 내 곁을 떠나지 않으리라 확신하지요.\"\n\n\"왜요?\"\n\n\"내가 더 나은 사람을 결코 얻지 못할 테니까요.\"\n\n\"개연성이란 사람을 속이는 법인데요.\"\n\n\"하지만 나는 확실한 것만 다룹니다. 그는 생사여탈권을 쥔 상대로서는 가장 훌륭한 하인이지요.\"\n\n\"베르투초에게 그런 권리를 가지고 계십니까?\"\n\"그렇습니다.\"\n\n쇠문처럼 대화를 단숨에 닫아 버리는 말들이 있는데, 백작의 그 \"그렇습니다\"가 바로 그런 말이었다.\n\n여정 내내 한결같은 빠르기로 달렸다. 일곱 역참에 흩어져 있던 서른두 필의 말이 여덟 시간 만에 그들을 목적지로 데려다주었다. 자정에 그들은 아름다운 정원의 문에 닿았다. 문지기가 대기하고 있었는데, 마지막 역참의 마부에게서 백작이 다가온다는 기별을 받은 터였다. 새벽 두 시 반에 모르세르는 자기 방으로 안내되었고, 그곳에는 목욕물과 야식이 마련되어 있었다. 마차 뒤편에 타고 온 하인이 그의 시중을 들었고, 앞쪽에 탔던 바티스탱은 백작의 시중을 들었다.\n\n알베르는 목욕을 하고 야식을 든 뒤 잠자리에 들었다. 밤새 그는 구슬픈 파도 소리에 잠겨 잠들었다. 일어나서 그는 창가로 갔는데, 창은 앞으로 바다를 면하고 뒤로는 작은 숲으로 둘러싸인 아담한 정원을 낀 테라스로 열려 있었다.\n\n작은 후미에는 좁은 용골과 높은 돛대를 단 작은 슬루프 한 척이 떠 있었고, 그 깃발에는 몬테크리스토의 문장이 새겨져 있었다. 푸른 바다 위에 솟은 황금빛 산, 그리고 그 위쪽에 붉은 십자가가 그려진 문장이었다. 이는 갈보리를 떠올리게 하는 그의 이름에 대한 암시일 수도 있었으니, 주님의 수난으로 황금보다 귀해진 그 산과, 그분의 피로 거룩해진 그 치욕의 십자가를 가리키는 것일 터였다. 아니면 이 수수께끼 같은 인물의 지나간 삶의 어둠 속에 묻힌, 고통과 거듭남에 얽힌 어떤 사사로운 기억일 수도 있었다.\n\n스쿠너 주위에는 이웃 마을 어부들의 작은 고깃배들이 여러 척 떠 있어, 마치 여왕의 분부를 기다리는 보잘것없는 신하들 같았다. 그곳에서도, 몬테크리스토가 단 이틀이라도 머무는 곳이라면 어디서나 그렇듯, 호화로움이 넘쳐흘렀고 삶은 더할 나위 없이 편안하게 흘러갔다.\n\n알베르는 대기실에서 사냥 도구 일습을 갖춘 총 두 자루를 발견했다. 또 일 층의 높다란 방 하나에는, 인내심 많고 느긋한 까닭에 낚시에 발군인 영국인들이 고안해 낸 갖가지 정교한 낚시 도구가 다 갖춰져 있었다. 그날은 몬테크리스토가 뛰어난 솜씨를 보이는 그 운동들을 즐기며 지나갔다. 그들은 정원에서 꿩 열두 마리를, 시내에서 송어 열두 마리를 잡았고, 바다가 내려다보이는 정자에서 점심을 들었으며, 서재에서 차를 마셨다.\n\n사흘째 되는 날 저녁 무렵, 몬테크리스토에게는 기운을 북돋워 준 그 운동에 완전히 지친 알베르는 창가의 안락의자에서 잠들어 있었고, 백작은 건축가와 함께 집에 들일 온실의 설계도를 그리고 있었다. 그때 한길에서 전속력으로 달려오는 말발굽 소리에 알베르가 고개를 들었다. 그는 몬테크리스토에게 폐가 될까 봐 데려오지 않았던 자기 시종이 와 있는 것을 보고 불쾌하게 놀랐다.\n\n\"플로랑탱이 여기 웬일이냐!\" 그가 벌떡 일어나며 외쳤다. \"어머니가 편찮으신 거냐?\" 그러고는 문 쪽으로 서둘러 갔다. 몬테크리스토는 그 모습을 지켜보았고, 알베르가 시종에게 다가가는 것을 보았다. 시종은 호주머니에서 봉인된 작은 꾸러미를 꺼냈는데, 그 안에는 신문 한 부와 편지 한 통이 들어 있었다.\n\n\"누구한테서 온 것이냐?\" 그가 다급히 물었다.\n\n\"보샹 씨한테서요.\" 플로랑탱이 대답했다.\n\n\"그가 너를 보낸 거냐?\"\n\n\"네, 도련님. 그분이 저를 자기 집으로 부르더니, 여비를 주고 말을 마련해 주시고는, 도련님께 닿기 전에는 절대 멈추지 말라고 약속하게 하셨습니다. 열다섯 시간 만에 달려왔습니다.\"\n\n알베르는 두려운 마음으로 편지를 펼쳤다가, 첫 줄을 읽고는 비명을 지르며 신문을 움켜쥐었다. 눈앞이 흐려지고 다리가 휘청거려, 플로랑탱이 받쳐 주지 않았더라면 쓰러졌을 것이다.\n\n\"가엾은 젊은이.\" 몬테크리스토가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"그렇다면 아버지의 죄가 자식들에게 삼사 대에 이르도록 미친다는 것이 참말이로구나.\"\n\n그사이 알베르는 정신을 차렸고, 계속 읽어 내려가다가 고개를 뒤로 젖히며 말했다.\n\n\"플로랑탱, 네 말이 곧바로 돌아갈 만한 상태냐?\"\n\n\"형편없는, 다리를 저는 역마입니다.\"\n\n\"네가 떠나올 때 집안 상황은 어땠느냐?\"\n\n\"다 조용했습니다. 그런데 보샹 씨 댁에서 돌아오니 마님께서 눈물을 흘리고 계셨습니다. 도련님이 언제 돌아오시는지 알아보려고 저를 부르신 거였지요. 보샹 씨에게서 받은 분부를 말씀드리니, 처음에는 막으시려고 두 팔을 뻗으셨다가, 잠시 생각하시더니 이렇게 말씀하셨습니다. '그래, 가거라, 플로랑탱. 부디 그 애가 어서 오기를.'\"\n\n\"네, 어머니,\" 알베르가 말했다. \"돌아가겠습니다, 그리고 그 파렴치한 놈에게 화 있으라! 하지만 무엇보다 먼저 거기에 닿아야 한다.\"\n\n그는 몬테크리스토를 두고 나왔던 방으로 돌아갔다. 오 분 만에 그의 모습은 완전히 딴사람이 되어 있었다. 목소리는 거칠고 쉬어 있었고, 얼굴에는 주름이 깊게 패었으며, 푸른 핏줄이 비치는 눈꺼풀 아래로 두 눈이 이글거렸고, 술 취한 사람처럼 비틀거렸다.\n\n\"백작님,\" 그가 말했다. \"베풀어 주신 환대에 감사드립니다. 더 오래 누리고 싶었습니다만, 파리로 돌아가야겠습니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 일이 있었습니까?\"\n\n\"엄청난 불행이, 제겐 목숨보다 더 중대한 일이 생겼습니다. 부디 캐묻지 말아 주십시오. 다만 말 한 필만 빌려주십시오.\"\n\n\"내 마구간은 당신 뜻대로 쓰세요, 자작. 하지만 말을 타고 가면 몸을 상할 겁니다. 역마차나 마차를 타세요.\"\n\n\"아니요, 그건 시간이 더 걸립니다. 게다가 저는 백작님이 경고하시는 그 고단함이 필요합니다. 그게 오히려 제겐 약이 될 겁니다.\"\n\n알베르는 총에라도 맞은 듯 휘청하더니 문가의 의자에 주저앉았다. 몬테크리스토는 이 두 번째 육체적 탈진의 기색을 보지 못했다. 그는 창가에서 외치고 있었다.\n\n\"알리, 모르세르 씨에게 말 한 필을, 어서! 그가 급해!\"\n\n이 말이 알베르를 다시 일으켰다. 그는 방에서 뛰쳐나갔고, 백작이 그 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n\"고맙습니다!\" 그가 말 위로 몸을 던지며 외쳤다.\n\n\"되도록 빨리 돌아오너라, 플로랑탱. 말을 구하는 데 무슨 암호라도 대야 합니까?\"\n\n\"내리기만 하면 됩니다. 곧바로 다른 말에 안장을 얹어 줄 겁니다.\"\n\n알베르는 잠시 망설였다. \"제가 이렇게 떠나는 게 이상하고 어리석어 보이실지도 모릅니다.\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"신문의 한 단락이 사람을 얼마나 미치게 만들 수 있는지 백작님은 모르실 겁니다. 제가 떠난 뒤에 그걸 읽어 보세요.\" 그가 말했다. \"제 분노를 목격하지 않으시도록요.\"\n\n백작이 신문을 집어 드는 사이, 알베르는 말에 박차를 가했고, 말은 그런 낯선 자극에 놀라 펄쩍 뛰어오르더니 화살처럼 쏜살같이 내달렸다. 백작은 연민의 감정으로 그를 지켜보았고, 그가 완전히 사라지자 다음과 같은 글을 읽었다.\n\n\"삼 주 전 앵파르시알지에서 언급된, 야니나의 알리 파샤 휘하에서 복무한 그 프랑스 장교는, 야니나 성채를 넘겨주었을 뿐 아니라 자신의 은인을 튀르크인들에게 팔아넘긴 자로, 우리 존경하는 동업지가 밝혔듯이 당시에는 분명 페르낭이라고 자칭했다. 그러나 그는 그 후 자신의 세례명에 귀족 칭호와 가문의 성을 덧붙였다. 그는 이제 자신을 모르세르 백작이라 칭하며, 귀족원 의원의 반열에 올라 있다.\"\n\n이리하여 보샹이 그토록 너그럽게 묻어 주었던 그 끔찍한 비밀이 무장한 망령처럼 다시 나타났다. 그리고 어떤 악의에 찬 출처에서 정보를 얻은 다른 신문이, 알베르가 노르망디로 떠난 지 이틀 만에, 그 불운한 젊은이를 거의 미치게 만든 그 몇 줄을 실은 것이었다.\n아침 여덟 시에 알베르는 보샹의 집 문 앞에 닿았다.\n\n# 제85장 여행\n\n시종은 그를 곧바로 안으로 안내하라는 분부를 받아 둔 터였다.\n보샹은 목욕 중이었다.\n\n\"왔네.\" 알베르가 말했다.\n\n\"음, 가엾은 친구.\" 보샹이 대답했다. \"기다리고 있었네.\"\n\n\"굳이 말할 필요도 없지만, 자네는 너무도 의리 있고 너무도 따뜻한 사람이라 그 고통스러운 일을 함부로 입에 올리지 않았겠지. 나를 부른 것 또한 자네의 우정을 보여 주는 또 하나의 증거일세. 그러니 시간을 허비하지 말고 말해 주게. 이 끔찍한 일격이 어디서 비롯된 것인지, 조금이라도 짐작 가는 게 있나?\"\n\n\"단서를 좀 잡은 것 같네.\"\n\n\"하지만 먼저 이 수치스러운 음모의 자초지종을 모두 들려주게.\"\n\n보샹은 수치심과 비통함에 짓눌린 젊은이에게 다음과 같은 사실들을 들려주기 시작했다. 이틀 전, 그 기사가 랭파르시알 외에 다른 신문에도 실렸는데, 더 심각한 것은 그것이 정부 기관지로 잘 알려진 신문이었다는 점이다. 보샹은 아침 식사를 하다가 그 기사를 읽었다. 그는 곧장 사람을 보내 이륜마차를 부르고 발행인의 사무실로 달려갔다. 다른 신문 편집장과 정반대의 신조를 내세우면서도, 보샹은 — 흔히, 아니 사실 자주 있는 일이지만 — 그와 막역한 친구였다. 편집장은 사탕무 설탕에 관한 같은 신문의 사설을 흐뭇한 기색으로 읽고 있었는데, 아마도 자신이 쓴 글인 모양이었다.\n\n\"아, 이런!\" 보샹이 말했다. \"그 신문을 손에 들고 있으니, 친구, 내가 왜 찾아왔는지 굳이 말하지 않아도 되겠군.\"\n\n\"설탕 문제에 관심이 있나?\" 정부 기관지 편집장이 물었다.\n\n\"아닐세.\" 보샹이 대답했다. \"그 문제는 생각해 본 적도 없네. 전혀 다른 주제가 궁금해서 왔지.\"\n\n\"무슨 일인데?\"\n\n\"모르세르에 관한 기사 말일세.\"\n\n\"아, 그래? 흥미로운 사건 아닌가?\"\n\n\"어찌나 흥미로운지, 자네가 명예 훼손으로 고소당할 큰 위험을 무릅쓰고 있는 것 같아서 말이야.\"\n\n\"천만에. 정보와 함께 필요한 증거를 모두 받았고, 모르세르 백작이 우리를 상대로 목소리를 높이지 않으리라는 것도 확신하네. 게다가 그들에게 베풀어진 명예에 어울리지 않는 그 비열한 죄인들을 고발하는 것은 나라에 봉사하는 일이지.\"\n\n보샹은 벼락이라도 맞은 듯했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 누가 자네에게 그토록 정확한 정보를 준 건가?\" 그가 물었다. \"이 일에 관해 제일 먼저 기사를 낸 우리 신문조차 증거가 없어서 멈출 수밖에 없었네. 게다가 모르세르 백작은 프랑스 귀족원 의원이고 우리는 야당이니, 그를 폭로하는 데에는 자네들보다 우리가 더 큰 관심이 있는데 말일세.\"\n\n\"오, 그건 아주 간단하네. 우리가 추문을 캐려고 나선 게 아니야. 이 소식이 우리에게 굴러들어 온 거지. 어제 야니나에서 한 사내가 무시무시한 양의 서류를 들고 찾아왔네. 우리가 그 고발 기사를 싣기를 망설이자, 그자는 다른 신문에라도 실릴 거라고 하더군.\"\n\n보샹은 이제 받아들이는 것 말고는 도리가 없음을 깨닫고, 모르세르에게 급사를 보내려고 사무실을 나섰다. 하지만 다음과 같은 자세한 내막은 알베르에게 미처 전하지 못했는데, 그 일들이 전령이 떠난 뒤에 벌어졌기 때문이다. 곧 같은 날, 평소 침착하던 귀족원의 위엄 있는 의원들 사이에 큰 동요가 일었다. 모두가 평소보다 일찍 도착해, 자기네 가장 저명한 동료 가운데 한 사람에게 대중의 이목이 쏠리게 될 그 침울한 사건을 두고 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 어떤 이들은 기사를 정독하고, 어떤 이들은 논평을 하며 혐의를 한층 더 뒷받침할 만한 정황들을 들추어냈다.\n\n모르세르 백작은 동료들 사이에서 호감을 사는 인물이 아니었다. 벼락출세한 자들이 다 그렇듯, 그는 자기 지위를 지키려고 숱한 오만함에 기대 왔다. 진짜 귀족들은 그를 비웃었고, 재능 있는 자들은 그를 멀리했으며, 명예로운 자들은 본능적으로 그를 경멸했다. 사실 그는 제물로 점찍힌 희생양의 불행한 처지에 놓여 있었다. 신의 손가락이 한번 그를 가리키자, 모두가 일제히 비난의 함성을 지를 채비를 하고 있었다.\n\n오직 모르세르 백작만이 이 소식을 모르고 있었다. 그는 그 명예 훼손 기사가 실린 신문을 받아 보지 않았고, 오전 내내 편지를 쓰고 말 한 마리를 시승하며 보냈다. 그는 평소와 같은 시각에, 거만한 표정과 오만한 태도로 도착했다. 마차에서 내려 복도를 지나 의사당으로 들어서면서, 문지기들의 머뭇거림도, 동료들의 냉랭함도 알아채지 못했다.\n\n그가 들어섰을 때 회의는 이미 삼십 분째 진행 중이었다. 모두가 그 고발 기사를 손에 쥐고 있었으나, 으레 그렇듯 누구도 먼저 공격에 나서는 책임을 떠맡으려 하지 않았다. 마침내 한 명예로운 의원이, 모르세르의 공공연한 적수가, 기다리던 순간이 왔음을 알리는 그 엄숙한 태도로 연단에 올랐다. 인상적인 침묵이 흘렀다. 평소 그리 흔쾌히 경청되지 않던 연사에게 어째서 이토록 깊은 주목이 쏠리는지, 오직 모르세르만이 영문을 몰랐다.\n\n백작은 서두를 알아채지 못했다. 연사는 자기 발언이 의회의 온전한 주목을 요할 만큼 중대한 것이라고 예고했으나, 백작은 그것을 흘려들었다. 그러나 야니나와 페르낭 대령이라는 말이 나오자, 그는 어찌나 무섭게 창백해졌던지 의원 모두가 몸서리치며 그에게 눈길을 박았다. 마음의 상처에는 이런 특성이 있다. 감출 수는 있어도 결코 아물지 않는다는 것. 늘 아프고, 건드리면 언제든 피 흘릴 채비가 되어 있으며, 가슴속에 생생하게 벌어진 채 남아 있다.\n\n뒤이은 고통스러운 정적 속에서 기사가 낭독되자, 의회 전체에 일제히 전율이 번졌고, 곧이어 연사가 다시 발언을 이어 가자 가장 집중된 주목이 그에게 쏟아졌다. 그는 자신의 망설임과 이 사안의 어려움을 토로했다. 그가 지키고자 하는 것은 모르세르 백작의 명예, 그리고 의회 전체의 명예라고 했다. 그러기 위해 개인적 문제를 둘러싼 토론을 발의하는데, 그런 것은 늘 그토록 거북한 논의 주제라고 했다. 그는 조사를 요구하며 발언을 마쳤다. 그 비방의 소문이 퍼질 틈을 주기 전에 처리하여, 모르세르 백작을 오래도록 누려 온 여론 속 자리로 되돌려 놓자는 것이었다.\n\n모르세르는 이 크고 뜻밖의 재앙에 완전히 압도되어, 의회를 둘러보며 겨우 몇 마디를 더듬거릴 뿐이었다. 이 머뭇거림은 결백함에서 오는 놀라움일 수도, 죄지은 자의 수치심일 수도 있었는데, 몇몇 사람의 마음을 그에게로 돌려놓았다. 진정으로 너그러운 사람이란, 적의 불행이 자신의 증오의 한계를 넘어설 때면 언제나 측은히 여길 준비가 되어 있는 법이니까.\n\n의장이 표결에 부쳤고, 조사를 진행하기로 결정되었다. 백작에게는 변론을 준비하는 데 얼마의 시간이 필요하냐고 물었다. 이 끔찍한 일격을 맞고도 살아남았음을 깨닫자 모르세르의 용기가 되살아났다.\n\n\"의원 여러분.\" 그가 대답했다. \"내게 알려지지 않은, 분명 어둠 속에 숨어 있을 적들이 가한 이 공격을, 나는 시간을 들여 물리치지 않겠습니다. 잠시나마 나를 흠칫하게 한 그 번갯불을, 나는 지금 당장, 벼락으로써 되받아치겠습니다. 오, 이 변론에 나서는 대신 마지막 한 방울의 피까지 흘려, 내가 고귀한 동료들과 가치에 있어 대등한 사람임을 증명할 수만 있다면.\"\n\n이 말은 피고에게 유리한 인상을 남겼다.\n\n\"그러니 가능한 한 빨리 심리가 열리기를 요청합니다. 의회에 필요한 모든 정보를 제출하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"어느 날로 정하시겠습니까?\" 의장이 물었다.\n\n\"오늘이라도 응하겠습니다.\" 백작이 대답했다.\n\n의장이 종을 울렸다. \"오늘 심리를 진행하는 데 의회는 찬성하십니까?\"\n\n\"찬성이오.\" 만장일치의 대답이었다.\n\n모르세르가 제출하는 증거를 검토할 열두 명의 위원회가 선출되었다. 조사는 그날 저녁 여덟 시에 위원회실에서 시작하고, 연기가 필요하면 매일 저녁 같은 시각에 절차를 다시 이어 가기로 했다. 모르세르는 물러가게 해 달라고 청했다. 그의 통찰이 진작 내다본 이 폭풍에 맞서 오래도록 준비해 온 서류들을 챙겨야 했던 것이다.\n\n보샹은 우리가 방금 들려준 사실들을 모두 젊은이에게 이야기했다. 다만 그의 이야기는, 죽은 것의 차가움에 견준 살아 있는 것의 생기를 모두 지니고 있어 우리 것보다 한결 나았다.\n\n알베르는 귀를 기울였다. 어느 때는 희망에, 어느 때는 분노에, 또 어느 때는 수치심에 떨었다. 보샹의 확신에 찬 말투에서 아버지가 유죄임을 알았기에, 유죄인 이상 어떻게 무죄를 입증할 수 있단 말인가 자문했던 것이다. 보샹은 이야기를 계속하기를 망설였다.\n\n\"그다음은?\" 알베르가 물었다.\n\n\"그다음? 친구, 자네가 내게 괴로운 임무를 떠맡기는군. 정말 다 알아야겠나?\"\n\n\"반드시. 그것도 다른 누구의 입보다 자네 입으로 듣고 싶네.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 있는 용기를 다 끌어모으게. 지금만큼 그것이 필요한 적은 없었을 테니.\"\n\n알베르는 마치 제 힘을 시험하려는 듯 이마를 손으로 쓸었다. 목숨을 지키려는 사람이 방패를 점검하고 칼을 휘어 보듯이. 그는 스스로 충분히 강하다고 여겼는데, 열병을 기력으로 착각하고 있었던 것이다. \"계속하게.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"저녁이 되었네. 온 파리가 기다리고 있었지. 많은 이들은 자네 아버지가 모습을 드러내기만 하면 자신에 대한 혐의를 짓뭉갤 수 있을 거라 했고, 또 많은 이들은 그가 나타나지 않을 거라 했네. 어떤 이들은 그가 브뤼셀로 떠나는 걸 보았다고 단언했고, 또 어떤 이들은 그가 여권을 발급받았는지 경찰서로 알아보러 갔지. 나는 위원회 위원 중 하나, 안면이 있는 젊은 귀족에게 온갖 영향력을 동원해 방청석 한 곳에 들어갈 자리를 얻었네. 그가 일곱 시에 나를 데리러 왔고, 아무도 도착하기 전에 문지기에게 부탁해 나를 한 칸막이 자리에 앉혀 주었네. 나는 기둥에 가려진 채, 곧 벌어질 그 끔찍한 장면을 처음부터 끝까지 지켜볼 수 있었지. 여덟 시가 되자 모두가 제자리에 앉았고, 마지막 종소리에 맞춰 모르세르 백작이 들어왔네. 손에 서류 몇 장을 들고 있었지. 얼굴은 차분했고 걸음은 굳건했으며, 군복을 더없이 정성스레 차려입고 턱까지 단추를 빈틈없이 채우고 있었네. 그의 등장은 좋은 인상을 주었어. 위원회는 자유주의자들로 꾸려져 있었는데, 그중 몇몇은 앞으로 나와 그와 악수를 나누었네.\"\n\n알베르는 이런 자세한 이야기에 가슴이 터질 듯했으나, 슬픔에 감사의 마음이 뒤섞였다. 아버지의 명예가 그토록 거세게 공격받는 순간에 존경의 뜻을 표해 준 그 사람들을 기꺼이 끌어안고 싶었던 것이다.\n\n\"바로 그때 문지기 하나가 의장에게 편지 한 통을 가져왔네. '발언하셔도 좋습니다, 모르세르 백작.' 의장이 편지의 봉인을 뜯으며 말했지. 그러자 백작이 변론을 시작했는데, 정말이지, 알베르, 더없이 능변이고 노련한 솜씨였네. 그는 야니나의 태수가 마지막 순간까지 자신에게 전적인 신임을 베풀었음을 입증하는 서류들을 내놓았네. 태수가 황제와 생사를 건 협상을 그에게 맡겼다는 거였지. 그는 알리 파샤가 평소 편지를 봉인할 때 쓰던 권위의 표지인 반지를 내보였는데, 태수가 그것을 주어, 그가 밤낮 어느 시각에 돌아오더라도, 심지어 하렘에까지 들 수 있게 했다는 거였네. 불행히도 협상은 실패했고, 그가 은인을 지키러 돌아왔을 때 은인은 이미 죽어 있었다더군. '하지만,' 백작이 말했네. '알리 파샤의 신임이 얼마나 컸던지, 임종의 자리에서 그는 자신이 아끼던 애첩과 그 딸을 내게 맡겼습니다.'\"\n\n알베르는 이 말을 듣고 흠칫했다. 하이데의 사연이 떠올랐던 것이다. 그녀가 그 전갈과 반지에 대해, 그리고 자신이 팔려 노예가 된 경위에 대해 했던 말이 생각났다.\n\n\"그래서 그 변론이 어떤 효과를 냈나?\" 알베르가 애타게 물었다.\n\n\"솔직히 말해 나도 마음이 움직였고, 사실 위원회 전체도 그랬네.\" 보샹이 말했다.\n\n\"그러는 동안 의장은 자신에게 전해진 편지를 무심히 펼쳤네. 그런데 첫 몇 줄이 그의 주의를 끌었지. 그는 그것을 읽고 또 읽더니, 모르세르 백작에게 눈길을 박고 말했네. '백작, 야니나의 태수가 자기 아내와 딸을 당신에게 맡겼다고 하셨지요?' '그렇습니다.' 모르세르가 대답했네. '하지만 그 일에서도, 다른 모든 일과 마찬가지로 불행이 나를 따라다녔습니다. 내가 돌아왔을 때 바실리키와 그 딸 하이데는 사라지고 없었으니까요.' '그들을 아셨습니까?' '내가 파샤와 가깝게 지냈고 그가 무한한 신임을 보낸 덕에 그들에게 소개받았고, 스무 번도 넘게 본 적이 있습니다.'\n\n\"'그들이 어떻게 되었는지 짐작 가는 바가 있습니까?' '있습니다. 그들이 슬픔에, 그리고 어쩌면 가난에 희생되었다고 들었습니다. 나는 부유하지 못했고, 늘 목숨이 위태로웠습니다. 그래서 큰 한이 되지만 그들을 찾아 나설 수 없었지요.' 의장은 알아챌 듯 말 듯 눈살을 찌푸렸네. '여러분,' 그가 말했네. '모르세르 백작의 변론을 들으셨습니다. 백작, 방금 진술하신 내용이 진실임을 입증할 증인을 댈 수 있습니까?' '아아, 그럴 수 없습니다.' 백작이 대답했네. '태수를 둘러싸고 있던 자들, 혹은 그의 궁정에서 나를 알던 자들은 모두 죽었거나, 어디론가 떠났는데 나는 그 행방을 모릅니다. 내 동포 모두를 통틀어 그 참혹한 전쟁에서 살아남은 사람은 나 하나뿐이라고 믿습니다. 내게 있는 것이라곤 여러분 앞에 내놓은 알리 테펠리니의 편지들, 그의 호의의 징표인 이 반지, 그리고 마지막으로, 익명의 공격을 받은 처지에서 내가 내놓을 수 있는 가장 설득력 있는 증거, 즉 나의 진실함과 나의 떳떳한 군 생활에 맞설 증인이 단 한 명도 없다는 사실뿐입니다.'\n\n\"의회에 동조하는 웅성거림이 번졌네. 그리고 그 순간, 알베르, 더 이상 아무 일도 일어나지 않았더라면 자네 아버지의 변론은 성공한 셈이었네. 이제 표결에 부치는 일만 남았는데, 그때 의장이 다시 입을 열었지. '여러분, 그리고 백작, — 짐작건대 자신을 매우 중요한 증인이라 칭하며 방금 나선 한 사람의 말을 듣는 것을 마다하지 않으시겠지요. 그자는 분명 우리 동료의 완벽한 결백을 입증하러 온 것일 겁니다. 여기 이 일에 관해 방금 받은 편지가 있습니다. 이것을 낭독할까요, 아니면 그냥 넘길까요? 이 일을 못 본 척할까요?' 모르세르 백작은 창백해지며 들고 있던 서류를 두 손으로 움켜쥐었네. 위원회는 편지를 듣기로 했고, 백작은 생각에 잠긴 채 말이 없었지. 의장이 낭독했네.\n\n\"'의장님께, — 저는 에피루스와 마케도니아에서의 육군 중장 모르세르 백작의 행적을 조사하는 위원회에 중요한 사실들을 제공할 수 있습니다.'\n\n\"의장이 잠시 멈추자 백작은 창백해졌네. 의장이 청중을 둘러보았지. '계속하시오.' 사방에서 그런 소리가 들렸네. 의장이 다시 읽었네.\n\n\"'저는 알리 파샤가 죽던 그 자리에 있었습니다. 그의 임종을 지켜보았습니다. 바실리키와 하이데가 어떻게 되었는지 압니다. 저는 위원회의 명에 따르겠으며, 발언의 영광을 베풀어 주시기를 청합니다. 이 쪽지가 전달될 때 저는 복도에 있겠습니다.'\n\n\"'그래서, 이 증인은, 아니 차라리 이 적은 누구입니까?' 백작이 물었는데, 그 목소리에 눈에 띄는 변화가 있었네. '곧 알게 될 것입니다, 백작.' 의장이 대답했지. '위원회는 이 증인의 말을 들을 의향이 있습니까?' '예, 예.' 모두가 한꺼번에 말했네. 문지기를 불렀지. '복도에 누가 있소?' 의장이 물었네.\n\n\"'예, 있습니다.' '누구요?' '한 여인이, 하인을 데리고 있습니다.' 모두가 옆 사람을 쳐다보았네. '들여보내시오.' 의장이 말했네. 오 분쯤 뒤 문지기가 다시 나타났고, 모든 눈이 문에 쏠렸네. 나 또한,\" 보샹이 말했다. \"모두의 기대와 불안을 함께 느꼈지. 문지기 뒤로, 커다란 베일에 온몸을 휘감아 완전히 가린 한 여인이 걸어 들어왔네. 그 몸매와 몸에서 풍기는 향내로 보아, 그녀가 젊고 취향이 까다로운 사람임은 분명했으나, 알 수 있는 것은 그뿐이었지. 의장이 베일을 걷어 달라고 청하자, 그제야 그녀가 그리스 의상을 차려입은, 놀랍도록 아름다운 여인임이 드러났네.\"\n\"아.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"그 여자였군.\"\n\n\"누구 말인가?\"\n\n\"하이데.\"\n\n\"누가 그 말을 해 주던가?\"\n\n\"아아, 짐작이 가네. 하지만 계속하게, 보샹. 보다시피 나는 차분하고 강하네. 그래도 우리는 진상에 가까워지고 있는 게 분명하군.\"\n\n\"모르세르 백작은,\" 보샹이 말을 이었다. \"놀라움과 공포에 차서 이 여인을 바라보았네. 그녀의 입술이 곧 그에게 삶이냐 죽음이냐의 선고를 내리려는 참이었지. 위원회로서는 이 사건이 너무도 기이하고 흥미로워서, 그들이 백작의 안위를 두고 품었던 관심이 이제는 완전히 뒷전이 되어 버렸네. 의장이 직접 나서서 그 젊은 여인에게 자리를 권했지만, 그녀는 사양했네. 백작으로 말하자면, 의자에 풀썩 주저앉았지. 다리가 그를 지탱하기를 거부한 게 분명했네.\n\n\"'부인,' 의장이 말했네. '부인은 야니나에서 일어난 사건에 관해 위원회에 몇 가지 중요한 사실을 제공하겠다고 하셨고, 그 사건을 직접 목격하셨다고 진술하셨습니다.' '그렇습니다, 정말로.' 낯선 여인이 달콤하고 우수에 찬 어조로, 동방 특유의 낭랑한 목소리로 말했네.\n\n\"'하지만 그렇다면 부인은 그때 무척 어렸을 텐데요.' '저는 네 살이었습니다. 하지만 그 사건들이 저와 깊이 얽혀 있었기에, 단 하나의 세부도 제 기억에서 빠져나가지 않았습니다.' '그 사건들이 어떻게 부인과 얽힐 수 있었습니까? 그리고 그것이 부인에게 그토록 깊은 인상을 남겼다니, 부인은 대체 누구십니까?' '그 사건들에 제 아버지의 목숨이 달려 있었습니다.' 그녀가 대답했네. '저는 하이데, 야니나의 파샤 알리 테펠리니와 그가 사랑한 아내 바실리키의 딸입니다.'\n\n\"긍지와 수줍음이 뒤섞인 홍조가 별안간 이 젊은 여인의 두 뺨에 번졌고, 그 눈동자의 광채와 더없이 중대한 그녀의 진술은 의회에 형언할 수 없는 효과를 자아냈네. 백작으로 말하자면, 벼락이 그의 발치에 떨어져 눈앞에 거대한 심연을 열어젖혔다 한들 그 이상 압도될 수는 없었을 거야.\n\n\"'부인,' 의장이 깊은 경의를 담아 고개 숙이며 대답했네. '한 가지만 여쭙겠습니다. 이것이 마지막입니다. 방금 진술하신 내용의 진위를 입증하실 수 있습니까?'\n\n\"'할 수 있습니다.' 하이데가 베일 밑에서 향내가 짙게 밴 새틴 주머니를 꺼내며 말했네. '여기 제 출생 증서가 있는데, 아버지와 그 휘하의 주요 장교들이 서명한 것입니다. 그리고 제 세례 증서도 있습니다. 아버지가 제가 어머니의 신앙으로 양육되는 것을 허락하셨거든요. — 이 후자에는 마케도니아와 에피루스의 대주교가 봉인했습니다. 그리고 마지막으로 (어쩌면 가장 중요한 것일 텐데), 한 프랑스 장교가 저와 제 어머니를 아르메니아 상인 엘코비르에게 판 매매 증서입니다. 그자는 오스만 정부와의 그 파렴치한 거래에서 자기 몫의 전리품으로 은인의 아내와 딸을 챙겨, 사십만 프랑에 팔아넘겼지요.' 백작의 두 뺨에 푸르스름한 창백함이 번졌고, 이 끔찍한 비난에 그의 눈은 핏발이 섰네. 의회는 불길한 침묵 속에 그 말에 귀 기울이고 있었지.\n\n\"하이데는 여전히 차분했으나, 그것은 다른 사람의 분노보다 더 무서운 차분함이었네. 그녀는 아랍어로 쓰인 자신의 매매 증서를 의장에게 건넸지. 서류 중 일부가 아랍어나 로마이크어, 혹은 터키어로 되어 있을 수 있다고 여겨, 의회의 통역관이 대기하고 있었네. 그 유명한 이집트 원정 때 아랍어를 익혀 그 말에 능통한 한 고귀한 귀족 의원이, 통역관이 소리 내어 읽는 동안 눈으로 따라 읽었네.\n\n\"'노예 상인이자 전하의 하렘 조달인인 나 엘코비르는, 프랑스 영주 몬테크리스토 백작으로부터, 지엄하신 황제께 전달하고자, 팔십만 프랑으로 평가되는 에메랄드 한 개를 받았음을 확인한다. 이는 하이데라는 이름의, 열한 살 난 어린 기독교도 노예의 몸값으로서, 그녀는 야니나의 파샤였던 고(故) 알리 테펠리니 영주와 그의 총애를 받던 바실리키 사이에서 난 것으로 인정된 딸이다. 그녀는 칠 년 전, 콘스탄티노플에 당도하던 중 사망한 그녀의 어머니와 함께, 태수 알리 테펠리니 휘하에서 복무하던 페르낭 몬데고라는 프랑스 대령에 의해 내게 팔렸다. 위에 언급한 매입은 전하의 명을 받들어 전하의 몫으로, 사십만 프랑에 이루어졌다.\n\n\"'헤지라력 1247년, 전하의 권위로써 콘스탄티노플에서 작성하다.\n\n\"'서명, 엘코비르.'\n\n\"'이 증서가 마땅한 효력을 모두 갖추도록, 판매인은 이에 황제의 옥새를 받아 찍을 의무가 있으며, 이로써 옥새를 날인하노라.'\n\n\"과연 상인의 서명 곁에는 지엄하신 황제의 옥새가 찍혀 있었네. 이 문서의 낭독이 끝나자 무시무시한 침묵이 뒤따랐지. 백작은 그저 멍하니 바라볼 뿐이었고, 무의식중인 듯 하이데에게 박힌 그의 시선은 불과 피의 시선 같았네. '부인,' 의장이 말했네. '지금 파리에 계신 것으로 아는 몬테크리스토 백작께 문의해 보아도 되겠습니까?'\n\n\"'백작님,' 하이데가 대답했네. '제 양아버지이신 몬테크리스토 백작께서는 지난 사흘간 노르망디에 계셨습니다.'\n\n\"'그렇다면, 부인의 출생과 불행을 헤아리건대 지극히 당연한 일이고 또 본 위원회가 부인께 깊이 감사할 일입니다만, 부인께 이런 조처를 취하라고 권한 사람은 누구입니까?' '의장님,' 하이데가 대답했네. '저는 존경과 비통함의 마음에서 이 조처를 취하게 되었습니다. 기독교도이지만, 신께서 용서하시기를, 저는 늘 저의 고귀한 아버지의 원수를 갚고자 했습니다. 프랑스 땅에 발을 들이고 그 배신자가 파리에 살고 있음을 알게 된 뒤로, 저는 주의 깊게 지켜보았습니다. 저는 고귀한 보호자의 집에서 은거하며 지내는데, 그것은 제가 택한 일입니다. 저는 은둔과 침묵을 사랑합니다. 그 속에서 제 생각과 지난날의 추억과 더불어 살 수 있으니까요. 하지만 몬테크리스토 백작께서는 아버지처럼 온갖 보살핌으로 저를 감싸 주시기에, 저는 세상에서 벌어지는 일을 하나도 모르지 않습니다. 저는 제 방의 고요 속에서 모든 것을 압니다. — 이를테면 저는 모든 신문, 모든 정기 간행물을, 또 새로 나온 악곡까지 다 봅니다. 그렇게 다른 이들의 삶의 흐름을 지켜보던 중, 오늘 아침 귀족원에서 무슨 일이 있었는지, 또 오늘 저녁에 무슨 일이 벌어질지를 알게 되었지요. 그래서 저는 편지를 썼습니다.'\n\n\"'그렇다면,' 의장이 말했네. '몬테크리스토 백작은 부인의 이번 행동에 대해 전혀 모르신단 말이군요?' '전혀 모르십니다. 그리고 제게는 단 한 가지 두려움이 있는데, 제가 한 일을 그분이 못마땅해하시지나 않을까 하는 것입니다. 하지만 제게는 영광스러운 날입니다.' 젊은 여인이 타오르는 시선을 하늘로 들어 올리며 말을 이었네. '마침내 제 아버지의 원수를 갚을 기회를 찾은 이 날이 말입니다!'\n\n\"백작은 이 시간 내내 단 한마디도 입에 올리지 않았네. 동료들이 그를 바라보았고, 한 여인의 향기로운 입김 아래 시들어 버린 그의 앞날을 분명 측은히 여겼지. 그의 비참함이 음산한 선을 그리며 그 얼굴에 드러나 있었네. '모르세르 백작,' 의장이 말했네. '당신은 이 부인을 야니나의 파샤 알리 테펠리니의 딸로 인정합니까?' '아니오.' 모르세르가 일어서려 애쓰며 말했네. '이것은 내 적들이 꾸며 낸 비열한 음모입니다.' 누군가를 기다리는 듯 문에 시선을 두고 있던 하이데가 황급히 돌아서더니, 서 있는 백작을 보고 비명을 질렀네. '당신은 나를 모른다고요?' 그녀가 말했네. '하지만 다행히 나는 당신을 알아봅니다! 당신은 나의 고귀한 아버지의 군대를 지휘한 프랑스 장교 페르낭 몬데고예요! 야니나 성을 넘긴 것도 당신입니다! 당신은 은인의 생사를 두고 황제와 담판하라며 그가 콘스탄티노플로 보냈는데, 완전한 사면을 허락한다는 거짓 칙령을 가지고 돌아왔지요! 그 칙령으로 파샤의 반지를 손에 넣어, 불지기 셀림을 좌지우지할 권한을 얻은 것도 당신입니다! 셀림을 찔러 죽인 것도 당신이에요. 나와 내 어머니를 상인 엘코비르에게 판 것도 당신입니다! 살인자, 살인자, 살인자, 당신 이마에는 아직도 당신 주인의 피가 묻어 있어요! 보세요, 여러분, 모두 보세요!'\n\n\"이 말이 어찌나 격정에 차고 또렷한 진실로 발해졌던지, 모든 눈이 백작의 이마에 쏠렸고, 그 자신도 알리의 피가 아직 거기 어려 있기라도 한 듯 손으로 이마를 쓸었네. '당신은 정녕 모르세르 백작을 그 장교 페르낭 몬데고로 알아보는 것입니까?' '정말로 그렇습니다!' 하이데가 외쳤네. '오, 어머니, 어머니께서 이렇게 말씀하셨지요. \"너는 자유로운 몸이었고, 사랑하는 아버지가 있었으며, 거의 왕비가 될 운명이었단다. 저 사내를 똑똑히 보아라. 창끝에 네 아버지의 머리를 꽂아 올린 자가 바로 저자다. 우리를 판 자가 저자고, 우리를 저버린 자가 저자다. 저자의 오른손을 잘 보아라, 거기에 큰 상처가 있단다. 얼굴은 잊을지라도, 상인 엘코비르의 금화가 한 닢 한 닢 떨어져 들어가던 저 손으로 너는 저자를 알아볼 것이다!\" 나는 저자를 압니다! 아, 이제 저자더러 나를 알아보지 못하겠는지 말해 보라 하세요!' 한마디 한마디가 단검처럼 모르세르에게 꽂혀, 그의 기력을 조금씩 앗아 갔네. 그녀가 마지막 말을 내뱉자, 그는 불구가 된 손을 황급히 가슴팍에 감추고는, 비참함과 절망에 짓눌려 의자에 털썩 주저앉았네. 이 장면은 피고가 된 백작에 대한 의회의 의견을 완전히 뒤바꿔 놓았네.\n\n\"'모르세르 백작,' 의장이 말했네. '낙담하지 말고 답하시오. 이 법정의 정의는 신의 정의처럼 지엄하고 공정합니다. 당신이 스스로를 변호할 기회조차 없이 적들에게 짓밟히는 것을 좌시하지 않을 것입니다. 추가 조사를 진행할까요? 의회에서 의원 두 명을 야니나로 보낼까요? 말하시오!' 모르세르는 대답하지 않았네. 그러자 의원 모두가 공포에 질려 서로를 바라보았지. 그들은 백작의 격렬하고 사나운 성미를 알고 있었네. 그에게서 스스로를 변호할 용기마저 앗아 갈 정도라면, 그것은 정말로 무시무시한 일격임이 틀림없었지. 그들은 그의 망연한 침묵에 뒤이어 불같은 폭발이 터지리라 예상했네. '자,' 의장이 물었네. '당신의 결정은 무엇입니까?'\n\n\"'할 말이 없습니다.' 백작이 나직한 목소리로 말했네.\n\n\"'알리 테펠리니의 딸이 진실을 말한 것입니까?' 의장이 말했네. '그렇다면 그녀가, 감히 \"무죄\"라 항변하지 못하게 만드는 그 무서운 증인이란 말입니까? 당신은 정말로 당신에게 씌워진 죄들을 저질렀습니까?' 백작은 호랑이라도 누그러뜨렸을 표정으로 주위를 둘러보았으나, 그것으로 판관들의 무장을 풀 수는 없었네. 이윽고 그는 천장을 향해 눈을 들었으나, 마치 지붕이 열려 그 괴로운 눈앞에 천국이라 불리는 또 다른 법정과 신이라 불리는 또 다른 판관이 드러날까 두려운 듯, 곧바로 시선을 거두었지. 그러더니 황급한 동작으로, 숨이 막힐 듯 답답한 상의를 잡아 찢고는, 미치광이처럼 방에서 뛰쳐나갔네. 잠시 복도에 그의 발소리가 들리더니, 곧 마차 바퀴가 요란하게 구르며 그가 황급히 실려 가는 소리가 들렸지. '여러분,' 정적이 되돌아오자 의장이 말했네. '모르세르 백작은 중죄와 반역, 그리고 본 의회 의원으로서 부적절한 행위에 대해 유죄입니까?' '유죄요.' 조사 위원회의 모든 위원이 만장일치의 목소리로 대답했네.\n\n\"하이데는 회의가 끝날 때까지 남아 있었네. 그녀는 기쁨이나 연민의 기색을 조금도 드러내지 않고 백작에게 내려지는 선고를 들었지. 그러고는 얼굴에 베일을 드리우고 위원들에게 위엄 있게 절한 뒤, 베르길리우스가 그의 여신들에게 부여한 그 기품 있는 걸음걸이로 떠났네.\"\n\n# Chapter 87. The Challenge\n\n\"그래서,\" 보샹이 말을 이었다. \"나는 그 침묵과 어둠을 틈타 들키지 않고 집을 빠져나왔네. 나를 안내했던 정리가 문 앞에서 기다리고 있다가, 복도를 지나 보지라르 거리로 통하는 쪽문까지 데려다주었지. 나는 슬픔과 기쁨이 뒤섞인 심정으로 그곳을 나왔네. 미안하네, 알베르. 자네 때문에 슬펐고, 그렇게 아버지의 복수를 좇는 그 고결한 처녀 때문에 기뻤지. 그래, 알베르, 그 일격이 어디서 비롯되었든 간에, 그것이 적의 손에서 나왔다 해도, 그 적은 섭리의 대리인에 지나지 않는 거라네.\"\n\n알베르는 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 있었다. 이윽고 그는 수치심으로 붉게 물들고 눈물에 젖은 얼굴을 들더니, 보샹의 팔을 붙잡았다.\n\n\"이보게,\" 그가 말했다. \"내 인생은 끝났네. 나는 자네처럼 '섭리가 그 일격을 내렸다'고 차분히 말할 수가 없어. 나는 누가 이런 증오로 나를 좇는지 반드시 알아내야 하네. 그자를 찾아내면 내가 그를 죽이든지, 아니면 그가 나를 죽이든지 할 거야. 자네의 우정에 기대고 있네, 보샹. 경멸이 자네 마음에서 그 우정을 몰아내지 않았다면 말이야.\"\n\n\"경멸이라니, 이 사람아? 이 불행이 자네와 무슨 상관이 있단 말인가? 다행히도 아버지의 행위를 아들에게 묻던 그 부당한 편견은 이제 사라졌네. 자네 삶을 돌아보게, 알베르. 이제 막 시작되었을 뿐이지만, 자네가 걸어온 길의 출발만큼 더없이 맑게 동튼 여름날이 일찍이 있었던가? 아니야, 알베르, 내 충고를 듣게. 자네는 젊고 부유하니, 파리를 떠나게. 흥분과 변덕스러운 취향으로 들끓는 이 거대한 바빌론에서는 무엇이든 금세 잊히는 법이야. 자네는 서너 해 뒤에 러시아 공주를 신부로 맞아 돌아올 테고, 그러면 어제 일어난 일을 두고 마치 십육 년 전 일인 양 누구도 더는 입에 올리지 않을 걸세.\"\n\n\"고맙네, 보샹, 자네의 충고를 이끌어 낸 그 따뜻한 마음이 정말 고마워. 하지만 그럴 수는 없어. 내 바람을, 아니 내 결심을 이미 자네에게 말했잖나. 자네도 알다시피, 이 일에 직접 얽혀 있는 나로서는 자네와 같은 눈으로 이 일을 볼 수가 없어. 자네에게는 하늘에서 비롯된 것처럼 보이는 일이, 내게는 그보다 훨씬 덜 순결한 데서 나온 것으로 보이거든. 내가 보기에 이 일에 섭리는 아무 몫도 없어. 그리고 그게 다행이지. 보이지도 만질 수도 없는, 하늘의 상벌을 집행하는 대리인 대신, 나는 만질 수도 볼 수도 있는 자를 찾아낼 테니까. 단언컨대 지난 한 달간 내가 겪은 모든 고통을 그자에게 갚아 줄 거야. 다시 말하네, 보샹, 나는 인간의, 물질의 세계로 돌아가고 싶어. 자네가 스스로 자처하는 그 친구라면, 그 일격을 가한 손을 찾아내도록 나를 도와주게.\"\n\n\"좋아,\" 보샹이 말했다. \"자네가 나를 이 땅으로 끌어내려야겠다면, 따르겠네. 자네가 적을 찾겠다면 내가 돕지. 반드시 찾아내고야 말겠어. 내 명예도 자네 못지않게 깊이 걸려 있으니 말이야.\"\n\n\"그래, 그럼, 보샹, 자네도 알겠지만, 우리는 당장 수색을 시작하는 거야. 한순간의 지체도 내게는 영원처럼 느껴지니까. 그 중상모략꾼은 아직 벌을 받지 않았고, 앞으로도 받지 않으리라 기대하고 있을지도 몰라. 하지만 내 명예를 걸고 말하네, 그렇게 생각한다면 큰 착각이야.\"\n\n\"자, 들어 보게, 모르세르.\"\n\n\"아, 보샹, 자네가 벌써 뭔가 알고 있는 게로군. 자네가 나를 다시 살려 내는구먼.\"\n\n\"내가 이제 하려는 말에 진실이 담겨 있다고 단언하는 건 아니야. 하지만 적어도 어두운 밤에 비치는 한 줄기 빛은 되겠지. 그걸 따라가다 보면, 어쩌면 좀 더 확실한 무언가를 알아낼 수 있을지도 모르네.\"\n\n\"말해 주게. 내 조바심을 좀 풀어 주게.\"\n\n\"좋아, 야니나에서 돌아왔을 때 차마 입에 올리지 못했던 이야기를 들려주지.\"\n\n\"어서 말하게.\"\n\n\"나는 당연히 그 도시의 우두머리 은행가를 찾아가 알아보았네. 그런데 첫마디부터, 내가 자네 아버지의 이름을 꺼내기도 전에 말이야……\"\n\n\"'아,' 그가 말하더군. '무슨 일로 오셨는지 짐작이 갑니다.'\"\n\n\"'어떻게, 또 왜 말입니까?'\"\n\"'보름 전에도 같은 일로 문의를 받았거든요.'\"\n\n\"'누구한테서요?'\"\n\n\"'파리의 어느 은행가, 제 거래처에게서요.'\"\n\n\"'그자의 이름이……'\"\n\n\"'당글라르입니다.'\"\n\n\"그자라니!\" 알베르가 외쳤다. \"그래, 그토록 오랫동안 질투 어린 증오로 내 아버지를 좇아 온 게 바로 그자야. 인기를 얻고 싶어 하는 그 사내가, 모르세르 백작이 귀족원 의원에 봉해진 걸 용서하지 못한 거라고. 그리고 아무 이유도 밝히지 않고 깨진 그 혼담도, 그래, 모두 같은 원인에서 나온 거야.\"\n\n\"알아보게, 알베르. 하지만 이유 없이 화부터 내지는 말게. 알아보고, 만일 사실이라면……\"\n\n\"오, 그래, 만일 사실이라면,\" 청년이 외쳤다. \"내가 겪은 모든 고통을 그자가 갚아야 할 거야.\"\n\n\"조심하게, 모르세르. 그는 이미 노인이야.\"\n\n\"그가 우리 가문의 명예를 존중해 준 만큼만 그의 나이를 존중해 주지. 내 아버지가 그를 모욕했다면, 어째서 직접 맞서지 않았느냔 말이야? 오, 아니지, 그는 정면으로 마주 서기가 두려웠던 거야.\"\n\n\"자네를 탓하는 게 아니야, 알베르. 다만 자네를 말리는 거지. 신중하게 행동하게.\"\n\n\"오, 걱정 말게. 게다가 자네가 나와 동행해 줄 거잖나. 보샹, 이런 엄숙한 거래에는 증인의 입회가 있어야 마땅하지. 오늘 해가 지기 전에, 당글라르 씨가 죄가 있다면 그는 목숨을 잃거나, 아니면 내가 죽을 거야. 맙소사, 보샹, 내 장례식은 성대할 걸세!\"\n\n\"그런 결심을 했다면, 알베르, 지체 없이 실행에 옮겨야지. 당글라르 씨에게 가고 싶나? 당장 가세.\"\n\n그들은 이륜마차를 불렀다. 은행가의 저택에 들어서면서, 그들은 안드레아 카발칸티 씨의 사륜마차와 하인을 알아보았다.\n\n\"아! 이런, 잘됐군.\" 알베르가 음울한 어조로 말했다. \"당글라르 씨가 나와 결투하지 않겠다면, 그의 사위를 죽이지. 카발칸티는 틀림없이 싸울 테니까.\"\n\n하인이 청년의 방문을 알렸다. 그러나 은행가는 전날 벌어진 일을 떠올리고는 그를 들이고 싶지 않았다. 하지만 이미 늦은 뒤였다. 알베르가 시종의 뒤를 따라왔다가, 그 명령이 떨어지는 것을 듣고는 문을 박차고 들어왔다. 보샹을 뒤세운 채, 그는 어느새 은행가의 서재에 들어서 있었다.\n\n\"이보시오,\" 당글라르가 외쳤다. \"내 집에서 내가 원하는 사람을 맞이할 자유조차 이젠 없단 말이오? 무례가 지나치신 것 같구려.\"\n\n\"아닙니다.\" 알베르가 차갑게 말했다. \"비겁함을 무릅쓰지 않고서는, 적어도 어떤 사람들의 출입만은 거절할 수 없는 경우가 있는 법이지요. 당신께 그 피난처를 권해 드리는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 내게 무슨 볼일이시오?\"\n\n\"제 뜻은,\" 알베르가 다가서며 말했다. 그는 벽난로를 등지고 선 카발칸티에게는 눈길도 주지 않는 듯했다. \"누구도 십 분간 우리를 방해하지 않을 외딴 구석에서 한번 만나자고 청하려는 겁니다. 그 정도면 충분하지요. 두 사람이 마주 서면, 그중 하나는 그 자리에 쓰러져 남게 될 테니까요.\"\n\n당글라르의 얼굴이 창백해졌다. 카발칸티가 한 걸음 앞으로 나섰고, 알베르가 그쪽으로 돌아섰다.\n\n\"당신도,\" 그가 말했다. \"원한다면 오시오, 무슈. 당신도 거의 한 식구나 다름없으니 자격이 있지요. 그런 만남이라면, 기꺼이 받아들이려는 사람을 찾는 만큼 얼마든지 마련해 드리리다.\"\n\n카발칸티가 얼이 빠진 얼굴로 당글라르를 바라보았다. 당글라르는 안간힘을 다해 몸을 일으키더니 두 청년 사이로 끼어들었다. 알베르가 안드레아를 공격한 덕분에 그는 한결 다른 처지에 놓이게 되었고, 이 방문이 처음에 짐작했던 것과는 다른 까닭에서 비롯되었기를 바랐다.\n\n\"정말이지, 이보시오,\" 그가 알베르에게 말했다. \"내가 당신보다 이분을 택했다고 해서 시비를 걸러 오신 거라면, 나는 이 일을 검사장에게 넘기겠소.\"\n\n\"오해십니다.\" 모르세르가 음울한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"나는 혼인 문제는 조금도 거론하지 않았습니다. 카발칸티 씨에게 말을 건 것도, 그가 우리 사이에 끼어들려는 듯 보여서였을 뿐이지요. 한 가지 점에서는 당신 말이 옳소. 나는 오늘 누구와도 시비를 가릴 작정이니까요. 하지만 가장 먼저 따질 상대는 당신이오, 당글라르 씨.\"\n\n\"이보시오,\" 당글라르가 분노와 두려움으로 창백해진 얼굴로 대답했다. \"내 미리 일러두겠소. 나는 불운하게 미친개와 마주치면 그놈을 죽여 버리오. 그러고도 죄를 지었다고 여기기는커녕, 사회에 이로운 일을 했다고 생각하지. 그러니 당신이 미쳐서 나를 물려 든다면, 나는 가차 없이 당신을 죽이겠소. 당신 아버지가 스스로 명예를 더럽힌 게 내 잘못이란 말이오?\"\n\n\"그래, 이 비열한 인간아!\" 모르세르가 외쳤다. \"그게 바로 네 잘못이야.\"\n\n당글라르가 몇 걸음 물러섰다. \"내 잘못이라고?\" 그가 말했다. \"당신 미쳤군! 그 그리스 사건에 대해 내가 뭘 안단 말이오? 내가 그 나라를 여행이라도 했소? 내가 당신 아버지에게 야니나 성채를 팔라고, 배신하라고 부추기기라도 했단 말이오……\"\n\n\"닥쳐!\" 알베르가 우레 같은 목소리로 말했다. \"아니, 직접 이 일을 폭로해 우리에게 이 슬픔을 안긴 건 네가 아니야. 하지만 위선을 떨며 그 일을 부추긴 건 너야.\"\n\"내가?\"\n\n\"그래, 너 말이야! 그게 어떻게 세상에 알려졌지?\"\n\n\"신문에서, 야니나에서 온 기사를 읽고 알게 된 거 아니오?\"\n\n\"누가 야니나로 편지를 보냈지?\"\n\n\"야니나로?\"\n\n\"그래. 누가 내 아버지에 관한 자세한 내막을 캐물었느냔 말이야?\"\n\n\"누구든 야니나로 편지를 보낼 수 있겠지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 편지를 보낸 사람은 단 한 명뿐이었어!\"\n\n\"단 한 명?\"\n\n\"그래, 그게 바로 너였지!\"\n\n\"내가 편지를 쓴 건 분명하오. 딸을 어느 청년에게 시집보내려 할 때, 그 집안에 대해 얼마간 알아보는 건 마땅한 일 아니겠소. 그건 권리일 뿐 아니라 의무지요.\"\n\n\"당신은 어떤 답이 돌아올지 알면서 편지를 쓴 거요.\"\n\n\"내가, 정말로? 단언컨대,\" 당글라르가 외쳤다. 그 확신과 침착함은 두려움보다는 그가 청년에게 진심으로 품고 있는 관심에서 비롯된 것이었다. \"엄숙히 맹세하건대, 알리 파샤의 불행에 대해 조금이라도 알았더라면, 나는 야니나로 편지를 보낼 생각조차 하지 않았을 거요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 누가 당신에게 편지를 쓰라고 부추겼소? 말해 보시오.\"\n\n\"맙소사! 세상에 그보다 단순한 일도 없소. 나는 당신 아버지의 과거 내력을 이야기하던 참이었지요. 그 재산의 출처가 분명치 않다고 말했소. 내 의구심을 털어놓은 상대가, 당신 아버지가 어디서 그 재산을 모았느냐고 묻더군. '그리스에서'라고 내가 대답했지요. 그러자 그가 '그렇다면 야니나로 편지를 보내 보시오'라고 했소.\"\n\n\"그래, 그렇게 당신에게 권한 자가 누구요?\"\n\n\"다름 아닌 당신 친구, 몬테크리스토였소.\"\n\n\"몬테크리스토 백작이 야니나로 편지를 보내라고 했단 말이오?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 그래서 내가 편지를 썼지요. 원한다면 그 왕복 서신을 보여 드리리다.\"\n\n알베르와 보샹이 서로를 마주 보았다.\n\n\"이보시오,\" 그때껏 한마디도 하지 않았던 보샹이 입을 열었다. \"당신은 지금 백작을 고발하는 듯한데, 그분은 지금 파리에 없어 자신을 변호할 수도 없소.\"\n\n\"나는 아무도 고발하지 않소.\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"그저 사실을 전할 뿐이고, 당신들에게 한 말을 백작 앞에서도 똑같이 되풀이하겠소.\"\n\n\"당신이 받은 답을 백작도 알고 있소?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 그에게 보여 주었으니까.\"\n\n\"내 아버지의 세례명이 페르낭이고, 성이 몬데고라는 걸 그가 알고 있었소?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 내 진작 그에게 말해 두었지. 나는 같은 처지라면 누구라도 했을 일을 했을 뿐이오. 어쩌면 그보다 덜한 일을 했을지도. 그 답장이 도착한 이튿날, 당신 아버지가 몬테크리스토의 권유로 찾아와 내 딸을 당신에게 달라고 청했을 때, 나는 단호히 거절했소. 하지만 아무런 해명도, 폭로도 하지 않았지. 한마디로, 내가 이 일에 더 끼어들 까닭이 어디 있겠소? 모르세르 씨의 명예든 치욕이든 그게 나와 무슨 상관이오? 내 수입이 늘지도 줄지도 않는데 말이오.\"\n\n알베르는 피가 이마로 솟구치는 것을 느꼈다. 이 문제에 더는 의심의 여지가 없었다. 당글라르는 비열하게, 그러나 동시에 적어도 일부는, 전부는 아닐지언정, 진실을 말하는 사람의 확신을 지니고 자신을 변호했다. 양심 때문이 아니라 두려움 때문이었지만. 게다가 모르세르가 찾는 게 무엇이었던가? 당글라르와 몬테크리스토 가운데 누가 더 죄가 무거우냐 가벼우냐가 아니었다. 그 모욕에, 사소하든 중대하든, 책임지고 답할 사람, 결투에 응할 사람이었다. 그런데 당글라르가 결투하지 않으리라는 것은 분명했다.\n\n게다가 그동안 잊혔거나 미처 알아채지 못했던 모든 일이 이제 그의 기억 속에 떠올랐다. 몬테크리스토는 알리 파샤의 딸을 사들였으니 모든 것을 알고 있었다. 그리고 모든 것을 알면서도 당글라르에게 야니나로 편지를 쓰라고 권했다. 그 답을 알게 되자, 그는 하이데를 소개받고 싶다는 알베르의 바람을 들어주었고, 대화가 알리의 죽음으로 흘러가도록 내버려 두었으며, 하이데의 이야기를 막지도 않았다(다만 그녀에게 건넨 몇 마디 로마이어로, 모르세르의 아버지를 끌어들이지 말라고 그 처녀에게 일러 두었을 것이 분명했다). 게다가 그는 하이데 앞에서 아버지의 이름을 입에 올리지 말라고 모르세르에게 당부하지 않았던가? 마지막으로, 그는 마지막 일격이 임박했음을 알고서 알베르를 노르망디로 데려갔다. 이 모든 것이 계산되고 미리 짜인 일이었음에 의심의 여지가 없었다. 그러니 몬테크리스토는 아버지의 적들과 한패였던 것이다. 알베르는 보샹을 한쪽으로 데려가 이 생각들을 털어놓았다.\n\n\"자네 말이 옳아.\" 보샹이 말했다. \"당글라르 씨는 이 서글픈 일에서 부차적인 대리인에 지나지 않았어. 자네가 해명을 요구해야 할 상대는 몬테크리스토 백작일세.\"\n\n알베르가 돌아섰다.\n\n\"이보시오,\" 그가 당글라르에게 말했다. \"내가 당신과 영영 작별하는 게 아님을 알아 두시오. 당신의 그 암시가 옳은지 반드시 확인해야겠으니, 지금 몬테크리스토 백작에게 가서 따져 보겠소.\"\n\n그는 은행가에게 고개를 숙여 인사하고는, 카발칸티에게는 눈길도 주지 않은 채 보샹과 함께 밖으로 나갔다. 당글라르가 문까지 그를 배웅하며, 모르세르 백작에게 맞선 데에는 개인적인 증오의 동기가 전혀 없었음을 다시 한번 알베르에게 다짐해 두었다.\n\n은행가의 집 문 앞에서 보샹이 모르세르를 멈춰 세웠다.\n\n\"잘 들어.\" 그가 말했다. \"방금 전에 내가 자네한테 말했지. 해명을 요구해야 할 상대는 몬테크리스토 백작이라고.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래서 우리가 그의 집으로 가는 거잖나.\"\n\n\"가기 전에 잠깐만 생각해 보게, 모르세르.\"\n\n\"무엇을 생각하란 말인가?\"\n\n\"자네가 지금 내딛으려는 걸음이 얼마나 중대한지를 말일세.\"\n\n\"당글라르 씨를 찾아가는 것보다 더 중대한 일인가?\"\n\n\"그래. 당글라르 씨는 돈을 좋아하는 사람이고, 자네도 알다시피 돈을 좋아하는 자들은 자기가 무엇을 잃을지부터 너무 깊이 따지기 때문에 결투에 쉽게 끌려들지 않아. 반면에 다른 쪽은 누가 봐도 진짜 귀족이야. 그렇지만 그가 거친 싸움꾼일까 봐 두렵지는 않은가?\"\n\n\"내가 두려운 건 단 한 가지뿐일세. 결투에 응하지 않을 사람을 만나는 것 말이야.\"\n\n\"걱정하지 말게.\" 보샹이 말했다. \"그는 자네와 맞설 걸세. 내 유일한 걱정은 그가 자네에게 너무 강한 상대라는 점이지.\"\n\n\"이보게.\" 모르세르가 부드러운 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"그거야말로 내가 바라는 바일세. 내게 일어날 수 있는 가장 행복한 일은 아버지를 대신해 죽는 것이야. 그러면 우리 모두가 구원받을 테니까.\"\n\n\"자네 어머니가 비탄에 잠겨 돌아가실 걸세.\"\n\n\"가엾은 어머니!\" 알베르가 손으로 두 눈을 가리며 말했다. \"그러시겠지, 나도 알아. 하지만 수치로 돌아가시는 것보다는 그편이 낫지.\"\n\n\"정말로 마음을 굳혔나, 알베르?\"\n\n\"그래. 가세.\"\n\n\"하지만 백작이 집에 있을 거라고 보나?\"\n\n\"나보다 몇 시간 뒤에 돌아오겠다고 했으니, 지금쯤은 분명 집에 있을 걸세.\"\n\n두 사람은 마부에게 샹젤리제 30번지로 가자고 일렀다. 보샹은 혼자 들어가려 했지만, 알베르는 이번 일이 워낙 특별한 상황이니 결투의 통상적인 예법에서 조금 벗어나도 괜찮을 거라고 했다. 젊은이가 떠맡은 명분이 너무도 신성한 것이어서, 보샹으로서는 그의 뜻을 모두 따르는 수밖에 없었다. 그는 양보하고 모르세르를 뒤따르는 것으로 만족했다. 알베르는 수위실에서 곧장 현관 계단으로 뛰어올랐다. 그를 맞이한 것은 바티스탱이었다. 백작은 과연 막 돌아온 참이었지만, 지금 목욕 중이라 누구도 들이지 말라는 분부가 내려져 있었다.\n\n\"그럼 목욕을 마친 뒤에는요?\" 모르세르가 물었다.\n\n\"주인님은 저녁 식사를 하실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"식사 뒤에는요?\"\n\n\"한 시간 주무실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그다음에는요?\"\n\n\"오페라극장에 가실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"확실한가?\" 알베르가 물었다.\n\n\"확실합니다. 주인님이 정확히 여덟 시에 마차를 대령하라고 분부하셨습니다.\"\n\n\"좋아.\" 알베르가 대답했다. \"내가 알고 싶던 건 그게 전부였네.\"\n\n그러고는 보샹 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다. \"처리할 일이 있으면, 보샹, 지금 바로 처리하게. 오늘 저녁에 약속이 있다면 내일로 미루고. 자네가 나와 함께 오페라극장에 가 주기를 바라네. 그리고 가능하다면 샤토르노도 데려와 주게.\"\n\n보샹은 알베르의 허락을 받아들여 그의 곁을 떠나면서, 여덟 시 십오 분 전에 데리러 오겠다고 약속했다. 집으로 돌아온 알베르는 프란츠와 데브레, 모렐에게 그날 저녁 오페라극장에서 보고 싶다는 뜻을 전했다. 그런 다음 어머니를 보러 갔다. 어머니는 전날의 사건 이후 누구도 만나기를 거부하며 방에 틀어박혀 있었다. 어머니는 그 공개적인 모욕에 비탄으로 짓눌린 채 침대에 누워 있었다.\n\n알베르의 모습은 메르세데스에게 당연히 예상될 만한 반응을 불러일으켰다. 어머니는 아들의 손을 꼭 쥐고 소리 내어 흐느꼈지만, 그 눈물이 마음을 한결 가볍게 해 주었다. 알베르는 잠시 어머니의 침대 곁에 말없이 서 있었다. 창백한 얼굴과 찌푸린 눈썹으로 보아, 복수하겠다는 그의 결심이 점점 약해지고 있는 것이 분명했다.\n\n\"어머니.\" 그가 말했다. \"혹시 모르세르 씨에게 적이 있는지 아십니까?\"\n\n메르세데스가 흠칫했다. 아들이 \"아버지\"라고 말하지 않았다는 것을 알아챈 것이다.\n\n\"얘야.\" 어머니가 말했다. \"백작 같은 처지에 있는 사람에게는 은밀한 적이 많은 법이란다. 드러난 적이 가장 위험한 적은 아니지.\"\n\n\"저도 압니다. 그래서 어머니의 통찰력에 기대는 거예요. 어머니는 워낙 뛰어난 분이라 무엇 하나 놓치는 법이 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"어째서 그런 말을 하니?\"\n\n\"이를테면, 우리가 무도회를 열었던 그날 저녁, 몬테크리스토 백작이 우리 집에서 아무것도 입에 대지 않았다는 걸 어머니는 알아채셨잖아요.\"\n\n메르세데스가 열에 들뜬 팔로 몸을 일으켰다.\n\n\"몬테크리스토 백작!\" 어머니가 외쳤다. \"그가 네가 방금 물은 일과 무슨 상관이 있다는 거니?\"\n\n\"어머니도 아시잖아요. 몬테크리스토 백작은 거의 동방 사람이나 다름없어요. 그리고 동방 사람들은 적의 집에서 먹지도 마시지도 않음으로써 복수할 완전한 자유를 확보하는 관습이 있지요.\"\n\n\"몬테크리스토 백작이 우리의 적이라는 말이냐?\" 메르세데스가 자기를 덮은 시트보다 더 창백해지며 대꾸했다. \"누가 그런 말을 하더냐? 너 미쳤구나, 알베르! 몬테크리스토 백작은 우리에게 호의만 베풀었어. 몬테크리스토 백작은 네 목숨을 구해 주었고, 그를 우리에게 소개한 사람은 바로 너잖니. 오, 부탁이다, 얘야. 만약 네가 그런 생각을 품었다면 떨쳐 버리렴. 그리고 너에게 권하는 것은, 아니 간곡히 비는 것은, 그와의 우정을 지키라는 거야.\"\n\n\"어머니.\" 젊은이가 대답했다. \"그 사람과 화해하라고 제게 말씀하시는 데에는 무슨 특별한 이유가 있으시군요.\"\n\n\"내가?\" 메르세데스가 조금 전 창백해졌던 것만큼이나 빠르게 얼굴을 붉혔다가, 다시 그 어느 때보다 더 창백해지며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 분명히요. 그가 우리에게 결코 해를 끼치지 못하도록 하려는 것 아닙니까?\"\n\n메르세데스가 몸서리쳤다. 그러고는 아들에게 살피는 듯한 시선을 고정한 채 말했다. \"이상한 말을 하는구나. 게다가 너는 무슨 묘한 편견을 품고 있는 것 같아. 백작이 무슨 짓을 했다는 거니? 사흘 전만 해도 너는 그와 노르망디에 함께 있었어. 불과 사흘 전만 해도 우리는 그를 가장 좋은 벗으로 여겼잖니.\"\n\n알베르의 입가에 비웃는 듯한 미소가 스쳤다. 메르세데스는 그것을 보았고, 여자이자 어머니로서의 이중의 본능으로 모든 것을 짐작했다. 하지만 그녀는 신중하고 굳센 사람이었기에 자신의 슬픔도 두려움도 감추었다. 알베르는 입을 다물었다. 잠시 후 백작 부인이 다시 입을 열었다.\n\n\"너는 내 안부를 물으러 왔지. 솔직히 말하마, 나는 몸이 좋지 않단다. 너는 여기 머물면서 내 외로움을 달래 주어야 해. 혼자 남겨지고 싶지 않구나.\"\n\n\"어머니.\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"어머니의 뜻이라면 제가 얼마나 기쁘게 따를지 아시잖아요. 하지만 급하고도 중대한 일이 있어 오늘 저녁 내내 어머니 곁을 떠나 있어야 합니다.\"\n\n\"그래.\" 메르세데스가 한숨을 쉬며 대답했다. \"가렴, 알베르. 너를 효심의 노예로 묶어 두지는 않으마.\"\n\n알베르는 못 들은 척하며 어머니에게 인사하고 그 곁을 떠났다. 그가 방문을 닫기가 무섭게 메르세데스는 믿을 만한 하인을 불러, 그날 저녁 알베르가 어디를 가든 따라다니다가 본 것을 즉시 와서 알리라고 일렀다. 그런 다음 하녀를 불러, 몸이 그토록 쇠약한데도 무슨 일이 일어나든 대비할 수 있도록 옷을 차려입었다. 하인의 임무는 어렵지 않았다. 알베르는 자기 방으로 가서 평소와 달리 정성껏 옷을 차려입었다. 여덟 시 십 분 전에 보샹이 도착했다. 그는 샤토르노를 만났는데, 샤토르노는 막이 오르기 전에 일층 객석에 와 있겠다고 약속했다. 두 사람은 알베르의 사륜마차에 올랐다. 젊은이는 자기가 어디로 가는지 숨길 이유가 없었으므로 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"오페라극장으로.\" 그는 조바심에 공연이 시작되기도 전에 도착했다.\n\n샤토르노는 제자리에 있었다. 보샹에게서 자초지종을 전해 들은 터라, 그는 알베르에게 따로 설명을 구할 필요가 없었다. 아버지의 원수를 갚으려는 아들의 행동은 너무도 당연한 것이어서, 샤토르노는 그를 말리려 들지 않았고, 헌신을 다시 한번 다짐하는 것으로 만족했다. 데브레는 아직 오지 않았지만, 알베르는 그가 오페라극장의 장면을 좀처럼 놓치는 법이 없다는 것을 알고 있었다.\n\n알베르는 막이 오를 때까지 극장 안을 서성였다. 그는 복도에서든 계단에서든 몬테크리스토 백작과 마주치기를 바랐다. 종이 울려 자리로 돌아가야 했고, 그는 샤토르노와 보샹과 함께 일층 객석으로 들어갔다. 하지만 그의 두 눈은 기둥 사이의 그 칸막이 좌석에서 좀처럼 떨어지지 않았다. 그 자리는 1막 내내 한사코 닫혀 있었다. 마침내, 알베르가 백 번쯤이나 시계를 들여다보고 있을 때, 2막이 시작되면서 문이 열렸다. 검은 옷을 입은 몬테크리스토가 들어오더니 좌석 앞쪽으로 몸을 기울이고 일층 관람석을 둘러보았다. 모렐이 그를 뒤따라 들어와 누이와 매제를 찾았다. 그는 곧 다른 칸막이 좌석에서 그들을 발견하고는 손에 입을 맞춰 인사를 보냈다.\n\n백작은 관람석을 살피다가 창백한 얼굴과 위협적인 눈초리와 마주쳤다. 그 눈은 분명 그의 주의를 끌려 하고 있었다. 그는 알베르임을 알아보았지만, 그가 너무나 성나고 흐트러진 모습이었기에 못 본 척하는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했다. 그는 동행에게 속내를 내비치지 않은 채 자리에 앉아 오페라글라스를 꺼내고는 다른 쪽을 바라보았다. 겉으로는 알베르를 주목하지 않는 듯했지만, 사실은 그에게서 한시도 눈을 떼지 않았다. 그래서 2막이 끝나 막이 내릴 때, 그는 알베르가 두 친구와 함께 일층 객석을 떠나는 것을 보았다. 이윽고 칸막이 좌석들 뒤쪽으로 그의 머리가 지나가는 것이 보였고, 백작은 다가오는 폭풍이 자기에게 떨어질 작정임을 알았다. 그는 그 순간 모렐과 유쾌하게 이야기를 나누고 있었지만, 무슨 일이 벌어지든 단단히 대비하고 있었다.\n\n문이 열렸고, 몬테크리스토는 돌아보다가 창백하게 떨고 있는 알베르를 보았다. 그 뒤로 보샹과 샤토르노가 따라오고 있었다.\n\n\"오.\" 그가 외쳤다. 그 인사를 세상의 흔한 인사치레와 구별 짓는 그 너그러운 정중함이 묻어났다. \"내 기사께서 마침내 목적을 이루셨군. 안녕하시오, 모르세르 씨.\"\n\n자신의 감정을 그토록 비범하게 다스리는 이 사내의 표정에는 더없이 완벽한 다정함이 어려 있었다. 모렐은 그제야 자작에게서 받은 편지가 떠올랐다. 그 편지에는 아무 이유도 적지 않은 채 오페라극장으로 와 달라는 부탁만 적혀 있었다. 하지만 그는 무언가 끔찍한 일이 꾸며지고 있음을 깨달았다.\n\n\"우리는 위선적인 인사치레나 거짓 우정을 주고받으러 온 것이 아닙니다.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"해명을 요구하러 온 겁니다.\"\n\n젊은이의 떨리는 목소리는 거의 들리지 않을 정도였다.\n\n\"오페라극장에서 해명을 하라고요?\" 백작이 말했다. 자기 명분이 옳다는 것을 아는 사람 특유의 그 차분한 어조와 꿰뚫어 보는 눈빛으로. \"파리 사람들의 풍습에는 익숙하지 못한 나로서도, 여기가 그런 요구를 할 만한 자리라고는 생각지 못했겠소만.\"\n\n\"그래도, 어떤 사람들이 제 집에 틀어박혀서는,\" 알베르가 말했다. \"목욕 중이라느니 식사 중이라느니 잠을 잔다느니 하며 만나 주지 않는다면, 그를 만날 수 있는 기회가 생길 때마다 그 틈을 이용해야지요.\"\n\n\"나는 만나기 어려운 사람이 아니오. 내 기억이 틀리지 않다면, 어제만 해도 당신은 내 집에 있었으니까.\"\n\n\"어제 제가 당신 집에 있었던 건,\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"그때는 당신이 누구인지 몰랐기 때문입니다.\"\n\n이 말을 내뱉으면서 알베르는 옆 칸막이 좌석들과 복도에 있는 사람들에게까지 들리도록 목소리를 높였다. 그리하여 이 언쟁에 많은 사람의 이목이 쏠렸다.\n\n\"당신, 대체 어디서 굴러먹다 온 거요?\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"제정신이 아닌 것 같소만.\"\n\n\"당신의 배신을 제가 알아챘고, 또 내가 반드시 복수하리라는 걸 당신이 깨닫게 만들기만 한다면, 저는 충분히 분별 있게 굴 겁니다.\" 알베르가 격분하여 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 말인지 모르겠소.\" 몬테크리스토가 대꾸했다. \"설령 안다 해도, 당신 목소리가 너무 높소. 여기는 내 자리고, 남보다 목소리를 높일 권리가 있는 사람은 오직 나뿐이오. 이 좌석에서 나가시오!\"\n\n몬테크리스토가 더없이 위엄 있는 손짓으로 문을 가리켰다.\n\n\"아, 당신 집에서 나오게 만드는 방법쯤은 내가 잘 알지!\" 알베르가 대답하며, 경련하는 손아귀에 장갑을 움켜쥐었다. 몬테크리스토는 그 장갑에서 눈을 떼지 않았다.\n\n\"좋소, 좋아.\" 몬테크리스토가 차분하게 말했다. \"당신이 나와 시비를 벌이고 싶어 하는 건 알겠소. 하지만 한 가지 충고를 해 주겠는데, 잘 새겨 두는 게 좋을 거요. 도전을 떠벌리며 과시하는 건 천박한 짓이오. 과시가 누구에게나 어울리는 건 아니거든, 모르세르 씨.\"\n\n이 이름이 나오자 이 장면을 지켜보던 구경꾼들 사이에 놀란 웅성거림이 번졌다. 그날 하루 종일 사람들은 모르세르 이야기밖에 하지 않았던 것이다. 알베르는 그 빈정거림을 단번에 알아채고 백작에게 장갑을 던지려 했다. 그때 모렐이 그의 손을 붙잡았고, 보샹과 샤토르노는 이 장면이 도전의 선을 넘을까 두려워 그를 뒤에서 잡아당겼다. 그러나 몬테크리스토는 자리에서 일어서지도 않고 의자에 앉은 채 몸만 앞으로 기울여, 팔을 뻗어 젊은이의 움켜쥔 손에서 축축하게 구겨진 장갑을 빼앗아 들었다.\n\n\"이보시오.\" 그가 엄숙한 어조로 말했다. \"나는 당신 장갑이 던져진 것으로 여기겠소. 그리고 탄알로 둘둘 감아서 돌려주리다. 자, 이제 나가시오. 그러지 않으면 하인들을 불러 당신을 문밖으로 내던지게 하겠소.\"\n\n알베르는 거의 정신이 나간 듯 격분하여 두 눈에 불을 켠 채 뒷걸음질 쳤고, 모렐이 문을 닫았다. 몬테크리스토는 아무 일도 없었다는 듯 다시 오페라글라스를 집어 들었다. 그의 얼굴은 대리석 같았고, 심장은 청동 같았다. 모렐이 속삭였다. \"그에게 무슨 짓을 하신 겁니까?\"\n\n\"내가요? 아무것도—적어도 직접적으로는 아무것도 하지 않았소.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 이런 기이한 장면에는 무슨 까닭이 있을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"모르세르 백작의 일이 저 젊은이를 분노케 한 거요.\"\n\n\"백작께서 그 일과 무슨 관계가 있습니까?\"\n\n\"의회에 그 아버지의 배신이 알려진 건 하이데를 통해서였소.\"\n\n\"정말입니까?\" 모렐이 말했다. \"바로 이 좌석에서 백작과 함께 있는 걸 본 그 그리스인 노예가 알리 파샤의 딸이라는 얘기를 듣긴 했지만, 차마 믿지 못했습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇지만 사실이오.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 모렐이 말했다. \"이제 다 알겠습니다. 이 장면은 미리 계획된 것이었군요.\"\n\n\"어째서 그렇소?\"\n\n\"네. 알베르가 오페라극장으로 와 달라고 제게 편지를 썼습니다. 분명 그가 백작께 가하려는 모욕을 제가 증인으로 지켜보게 하려는 것이었겠지요.\"\n\n\"아마 그렇겠지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 그 흔들림 없는 평정으로 말했다.\n\n\"그런데 그를 어떻게 하실 겁니까?\"\n\n\"누구를 말이오?\"\n\n\"알베르 말입니다.\"\n\n\"알베르를 어떻게 하겠느냐고요? 막시밀리앙, 지금 내가 당신의 손을 쥐고 있는 것만큼이나 확실하게, 내일 아침 열 시가 되기 전에 그를 죽일 거요.\" 이번에는 모렐이 두 손으로 몬테크리스토의 손을 잡았고, 그 손이 얼마나 차갑고 흔들림 없는지를 느끼고는 몸서리쳤다.\n\n\"아, 백작.\" 그가 말했다. \"그 애 아버지가 그 애를 그토록 사랑하는데요!\"\n\n\"그 얘기는 꺼내지 마시오.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. 그가 처음으로 드러낸 분노의 기색이었다. \"나는 그를 고통받게 할 거요.\"\n\n모렐은 깜짝 놀라 몬테크리스토의 손을 놓쳤다. \"백작, 백작!\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"막시밀리앙.\" 백작이 그의 말을 가로막았다. \"뒤프레가 저 대목을 얼마나 황홀하게 부르는지 들어 보시오.\n\n'오 마틸드! 내 영혼의 우상이여!'\n\n나는 나폴리에서 뒤프레를 제일 먼저 발견한 사람이고, 제일 먼저 그에게 박수를 보낸 사람이오. 브라보, 브라보!\"\n\n모렐은 더 말해 봐야 소용없음을 깨닫고 입을 다물었다. 알베르와의 장면이 끝날 무렵 올라갔던 막이 다시 내려왔고, 문에서 노크 소리가 들렸다.\n\n\"들어오시오.\" 몬테크리스토가 조금의 동요도 내비치지 않는 목소리로 말했다. 그러자 곧 보샹이 나타났다. \"안녕하시오, 보샹 씨.\" 몬테크리스토가, 마치 그날 저녁 그 기자를 처음 보기라도 하는 듯이 말했다. \"앉으시오.\"\n\n보샹이 고개를 숙이고 자리에 앉으며 말했다. \"백작님, 보셨다시피 저는 방금 모르세르 씨와 동행했습니다.\"\n\n\"그러니까 그 말은,\" 몬테크리스토가 웃으며 대꾸했다. \"아마 방금 함께 저녁 식사를 했다는 뜻이겠군요. 보샹 씨, 당신이 그 사람보다 더 정신이 멀쩡한 걸 보니 다행이오.\"\n\n\"백작님.\" 보샹 씨가 말했다. \"알베르가 그토록 분노를 드러낸 건 잘못이었습니다. 인정합니다. 그래서 제가 제 몫으로 그를 대신해 사과드리러 왔습니다. 그리고 이렇게 사과를 드리고 나서—어디까지나 순전히 제 몫으로 드리는 것임을 알아주십시오—한마디 덧붙이자면, 저는 백작님이 너무도 신사다운 분이라, 야니나와의 관계에 대해 그에게 어떤 해명을 해 주시기를 거절하지 않으시리라 믿습니다. 그런 다음 그 젊은 그리스 아가씨에 대해서도 두어 마디 덧붙이고자 합니다.\"\n\n몬테크리스토가 그에게 입을 다물라는 손짓을 했다. \"원, 이거.\" 그가 웃으며 말했다. \"내 모든 기대가 무너지려 하는군.\"\n\n\"어째서요?\" 보샹이 물었다.\n\n\"당신은 분명 나를 아주 별난 인물로 보이게 하고 싶어 했을 거요. 당신 생각에 나는 라라이고, 만프레드이고, 러스벤 경이지요. 그런데 막 내가 그 절정에 다다르려는 찰나에, 당신이 스스로 그 목적을 무너뜨리고 나를 평범한 사내로 만들려 하는구려. 나를 당신과 같은 수준으로 끌어내리고는 해명을 요구하다니! 정말이지, 보샹 씨, 우스운 노릇이오.\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 보샹이 거만하게 대꾸했다. \"정직함이 명하는 경우도 있는 법이지요——\"\n\n\"보샹 씨.\" 이 기이한 사내가 끼어들었다. \"몬테크리스토 백작은 오직 몬테크리스토 백작 자신에게만 머리를 숙이오. 더는 말하지 마시오, 부탁이오. 나는 내가 하고 싶은 대로 하고, 그것은 언제나 잘한 일이오, 보샹 씨.\"\n\n\"백작님.\" 젊은이가 대답했다. \"정직한 사람을 그런 식의 돈으로 치를 수는 없습니다. 저는 명예로운 보증을 요구합니다.\"\n\n\"이보시오, 나 자신이 살아 있는 보증이오.\" 몬테크리스토가 꼼짝도 하지 않은 채, 그러나 위협적인 눈빛으로 대답했다. \"우리 둘 다 흘리고 싶어 하는 피가 핏줄 속에 흐르고 있소—그게 바로 우리 서로의 보증이오. 자작에게 그렇게 전하시오. 그리고 내일 열 시가 되기 전에, 그자의 피가 무슨 색인지 내가 직접 보게 되리라고도.\"\n\n\"그럼 저는 결투를 주선하기만 하면 되겠군요.\" 보샹이 말했다.\n\n\"내게는 아무래도 상관없는 일이오.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그리고 그런 사소한 일로 오페라극장까지 나를 성가시게 할 필요는 전혀 없었소. 프랑스에서는 검이나 권총으로 싸우고, 식민지에서는 기병총으로, 아라비아에서는 단검으로 싸우지요. 당신 의뢰인에게 전하시오. 비록 모욕당한 쪽은 나지만, 내 별난 기질을 끝까지 밀고 나가기 위해 무기 선택권은 그에게 넘기겠다고. 그리고 무엇이든, 심지어 늘 어리석은 짓인 제비뽑기로 승부를 가르는 것까지도, 군말 없이 따지지 않고 받아들이겠다고. 다만 나의 경우는 다른 사람들과 달라서, 반드시 이길 테니까.\"\n\n\"반드시 이긴다고요!\" 보샹이 깜짝 놀라 백작을 바라보며 되뇌었다.\n\n\"물론이오.\" 몬테크리스토가 어깨를 살짝 으쓱하며 말했다. \"그렇지 않다면 모르세르 씨와 싸우지도 않을 거요. 나는 그를 죽일 거요—어쩔 수 없는 일이오. 오늘 저녁 내 집으로 무기와 시각만 단 한 줄 적어 보내 주시오. 나는 기다리는 걸 좋아하지 않으니까.\"\n\n\"그럼 권총으로, 여덟 시에, 뱅센 숲에서.\" 보샹이 완전히 당황한 채 말했다. 그는 자기가 오만한 허풍쟁이를 상대하는 것인지, 아니면 초자연적인 존재를 상대하는 것인지 알 수가 없었다.\n\n\"아주 좋소, 이보시오.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"이제 다 정해졌으니, 부디 공연을 좀 볼 수 있게 해 주시오. 그리고 당신 친구 알베르에게 오늘 저녁엔 더 오지 말라고 전하시오. 그렇게 어설프게 골라 쓴 야만스러운 말들로는 제 몸만 상할 테니까. 집에 가서 잠이나 자라고 하시오.\"\n\n보샹은 완전히 어리둥절한 채 좌석을 떠났다.\n\n\"자.\" 몬테크리스토가 모렐 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다. \"당신을 믿어도 되겠지요?\"\n\n\"물론입니다.\" 모렐이 말했다. \"백작께 힘이 되어 드리겠습니다. 다만——\"\n\n\"무엇이오?\"\n\"진짜 까닭을 알았으면 합니다.\"\n\n\"그러니까, 차라리 모르고 싶지는 않다는 거군요?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"저 젊은이 자신도 눈을 가린 채 행동하고 있고, 참된 까닭을 모르고 있소. 그건 오직 하느님과 나만 아는 일이오. 하지만 맹세하건대, 모렐, 그 까닭을 아시는 하느님께서 우리 편에 서 주실 거요.\"\n\n\"됐습니다.\" 모렐이 말했다. \"두 번째 입회인은 누구입니까?\"\n\n\"파리에서 그 영예를 맡길 만한 사람은, 당신과 당신 처남 에마뉘엘 말고는 아는 이가 없소, 모렐. 에마뉘엘이 응해 줄 것 같소?\"\n\n\"제가 그를 대신해 약속드리지요, 백작.\"\n\n\"좋소? 내게 필요한 건 그게 전부요. 내일 아침 일곱 시에 내 집으로 와 주겠지요?\"\n\n\"가겠습니다.\"\n\n\"쉿, 막이 오르고 있소. 들어 보시오! 나는 가능하면 이 오페라의 음 하나도 놓치는 법이 없소. '빌헬름 텔'의 음악은 정말이지 감미롭거든.\"\n\n# Chapter 89. The Night\n\n몬테크리스토는 늘 하던 대로, 뒤프레가 그 유명한 「쉬베모아!」를 부를 때까지 기다렸다가 자리에서 일어나 밖으로 나갔다. 모렐이 문 앞에서 작별을 고하며, 이튿날 아침 일곱 시에 에마뉘엘을 데리고 함께 가겠다는 약속을 다시 다졌다. 그런 뒤 백작은 차분하고 미소 띤 얼굴로 자기 마차에 올랐고, 오 분 만에 집에 닿았다. 백작을 아는 사람이라면, 그가 들어서며 이렇게 말할 때의 표정을 오해할 리 없었다.\n\n\"알리, 상아 십자가가 박힌 권총을 가져오너라.\"\n\n알리가 주인에게 권총 상자를 가져왔고, 백작은 작은 화약과 총알에 제 목숨을 맡기려는 사람이 으레 보일 법한 세심함으로 무기들을 살폈다. 이 권총은 특별한 양식으로 제작된 것으로, 몬테크리스토가 자기 방에서 사격 연습을 하려고 따로 만들게 한 것이었다. 뇌관 하나면 충분히 총알이 발사되었고, 옆방에서는 사냥꾼들의 말마따나 백작이 손을 풀고 있다는 것을 아무도 눈치채지 못했을 것이다.\n\n그가 막 권총 하나를 집어 들고, 과녁 삼아 쓰는 작은 철판 위에서 겨눌 지점을 찾던 참에, 서재 문이 열리고 바티스탱이 들어왔다. 그가 한마디 꺼내기도 전에, 백작은 옆방에서 베일을 쓴 여인이 바티스탱의 뒤를 바짝 따라온 것을 보았다. 여인은 백작이 손에 권총을 들고 탁자 위에 검들이 놓여 있는 것을 보더니 안으로 뛰어들었다. 바티스탱이 주인을 쳐다보자, 백작이 손짓을 했고, 그는 문을 닫고 물러갔다.\n\n\"누구십니까, 부인?\" 백작이 베일을 쓴 여인에게 물었다.\n\n낯선 여인이 주위를 한 번 둘러보며, 둘만 있는 것이 확실한지 확인했다. 그러고는 무릎을 꿇으려는 듯 몸을 숙이고 두 손을 모으더니, 절망에 찬 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"에드몽, 제 아들을 죽이지 말아 주세요!\"\n\n백작이 한 걸음 물러나며 짧은 탄성을 내뱉었고, 손에 들고 있던 권총을 떨어뜨렸다.\n\n\"방금 무슨 이름을 부르셨습니까, 모르세르 부인?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"당신 이름이에요!\" 여인이 베일을 뒤로 젖히며 외쳤다. \"당신 이름이요, 어쩌면 저 혼자만은 잊지 않은 그 이름 말이에요. 에드몽, 당신을 찾아온 것은 모르세르 부인이 아니라 메르세데스예요.\"\n\n\"메르세데스는 죽었습니다, 부인.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"이제 저는 그런 이름을 가진 사람을 알지 못합니다.\"\n\n\"메르세데스는 살아 있고, 기억하고 있어요. 그 여인만이 당신을 보고 알아보았으니까요. 아니, 보기도 전에, 당신 목소리만으로, 에드몽, 그저 그 목소리 하나만으로 알아챘어요. 그리고 그 순간부터 그 여인은 당신의 발자취를 따라다니며, 당신을 지켜보고, 당신을 두려워했어요. 지금 모르세르 씨를 내리치는 그 손이 누구의 손인지 물어볼 것도 없이요.\"\n\n\"페르낭 말씀이군요?\" 몬테크리스토가 쓰디쓴 빈정거림을 담아 대답했다. \"이름을 들추는 김에, 다 같이 떠올려 봅시다.\" 몬테크리스토가 어찌나 증오에 찬 어조로 페르낭이라는 이름을 입에 올렸던지, 메르세데스는 핏줄 하나하나에 소름이 끼치는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"보세요, 에드몽, 제가 틀리지 않았어요. 그러니 제가 '내 아들을 살려 주세요!'라고 말할 자격이 있는 거예요.\"\n\n\"그런데 누가 부인께, 제가 부인의 아들에게 적의를 품고 있다고 말하던가요?\"\n\n\"사실 아무도요. 하지만 어머니에게는 두 배로 보는 눈이 있답니다. 저는 모든 걸 짐작했어요. 오늘 저녁 그 애를 따라 오페라 극장에 갔고, 일층 칸막이 좌석에 숨어서 모든 걸 보았어요.\"\n\n\"모든 걸 보셨다면, 부인, 페르낭의 아들이 사람들 앞에서 저를 모욕했다는 것도 아시겠군요.\" 몬테크리스토가 소름 끼치도록 차분하게 말했다.\n\n\"오, 제발 부탁이에요!\"\n\n\"제 친구인 모렐이 막지 않았더라면, 그가 제 얼굴에 장갑을 던졌으리라는 것도 보셨겠지요.\"\n\n\"제 말을 들어 주세요. 제 아들도 당신이 누구인지 짐작했어요. 그 애는 제 아버지의 불행이 당신 탓이라고 여기고 있어요.\"\n\n\"부인, 잘못 아셨습니다. 그것은 불행이 아니라 벌입니다. 모르세르 씨를 내리치는 것은 제가 아닙니다. 그를 벌하는 것은 섭리입니다.\"\n\n\"그런데 어째서 당신이 섭리 노릇을 하는 거죠?\" 메르세데스가 외쳤다. \"섭리가 잊은 일을 어째서 당신이 기억하는 거예요? 야니나와 그곳 태수가 당신과 무슨 상관이에요, 에드몽? 페르낭 몽데고가 알리 테펠리니를 배신한 일이 당신에게 무슨 해를 끼쳤다는 거죠?\"\n\n\"아, 부인.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"그건 다 그 프랑스 장교와 바실리키의 딸 사이의 일이지요. 부인 말씀대로 저와는 상관없는 일입니다. 그리고 제가 복수를 맹세했다면, 그건 그 프랑스 장교나 모르세르 백작에게가 아니라, 어부 페르낭, 곧 카탈루냐 여인 메르세데스의 남편에게 한 맹세입니다.\"\n\n\"아, 백작님!\" 백작 부인이 외쳤다. \"운명이 저지르게 한 잘못 하나에 어쩌면 그토록 끔찍한 복수를! 죄인은 저 하나뿐이에요, 에드몽. 누군가에게 복수해야 한다면, 그건 바로 저예요. 당신의 부재와 제 외로움을 견뎌 낼 굳센 마음이 없었던 저 말이에요.\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 몬테크리스토가 소리쳤다. \"어째서 제가 없었습니까? 그리고 어째서 부인이 홀로 남았습니까?\"\n\n\"당신이 체포되어 죄수가 되었으니까요, 에드몽.\"\n\n\"그런데 어째서 제가 체포되었습니까? 어째서 제가 죄수가 되었습니까?\"\n\n\"저는 모릅니다.\" 메르세데스가 말했다.\n\n\"모르시는군요, 부인. 적어도 모르시기를 바랍니다. 하지만 제가 말씀드리지요. 제가 체포되어 죄수가 된 까닭은, 부인과 결혼하기로 한 전날, 라레제르브의 정자 아래에서 당글라르라는 자가 이 편지를 썼고, 그것을 어부 페르낭이 직접 부쳤기 때문입니다.\"\n\n몬테크리스토가 책상으로 가서 용수철로 서랍을 열고, 본디 색을 잃고 잉크가 녹슨 빛으로 변한 종이 한 장을 꺼냈다. 그것을 메르세데스의 손에 쥐여 주었다. 그것은 당글라르가 검사에게 보낸 편지로, 몬테크리스토 백작이 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회의 사무원으로 변장하고 보빌 씨에게 이십만 프랑을 치른 날, 에드몽 당테스를 고발한 서류철에서 빼낸 것이었다. 메르세데스는 다음과 같은 글줄을 두려움에 떨며 읽어 내려갔다.\n\n\"왕좌와 종교에 충성하는 한 벗이 검사께 알립니다. 스미르나에서 나폴리와 포르토페라요를 거쳐 오늘 입항한 파라옹호의 일등 항해사 에드몽 당테스라는 자가 뮈라가 찬탈자에게 보내는 편지 한 통과, 찬탈자가 파리의 보나파르트파 모임에 보내는 또 다른 편지 한 통을 지니고 있습니다. 이 진술의 충분한 증거는 위에 언급한 에드몽 당테스를 체포함으로써 얻을 수 있습니다. 그는 파리로 가는 그 편지를 몸에 지니고 있거나 아버지의 집에 두고 있을 것입니다. 부자 어느 쪽에서도 발견되지 않는다면, 그것은 분명 파라옹호에 있는 그 당테스의 선실에서 발견될 것입니다.\"\n\n\"이 무슨 끔찍한 일인지!\" 메르세데스가 땀에 젖은 이마를 손으로 훔치며 말했다. \"그리고 이 편지가……\"\n\n\"제가 이십만 프랑을 주고 사들였습니다, 부인.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"하지만 그건 사소한 일이지요. 덕분에 부인 앞에서 제 결백을 증명할 수 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"그리고 그 편지의 결과는……\"\n\n\"부인께서도 잘 아시지요. 제 체포였습니다. 하지만 그 체포가 얼마나 오래 이어졌는지는 모르실 겁니다. 제가 부인에게서 사 분의 일 리외도 안 되는 곳, 이프성의 토굴 감옥에서 십사 년을 갇혀 지냈다는 것을 모르실 겁니다. 그 십사 년 동안, 첫날 세웠던 복수의 맹세를 날마다 새로이 다졌다는 것도요. 그러면서도 저는 부인이 저를 모함한 페르낭과 결혼했다는 것도, 제 아버지가 굶어 죽었다는 것도 알지 못했습니다!\"\n\n\"그게 사실인가요?\" 메르세데스가 몸서리치며 외쳤다.\n\n\"갇힌 지 십사 년 만에 감옥을 나오며 들은 이야기가 바로 그것이었습니다. 그래서 저는 살아 있는 메르세데스와 세상을 떠난 제 아버지를 걸고 페르낭에게 복수하겠노라 맹세했고, 마침내 복수했습니다.\"\n\n\"그 불행한 페르낭이 정말 그런 짓을 했다고 확신하시나요?\"\n\n\"제가 말씀드린 그대로 했다고 확신합니다, 부인. 게다가 그 일은, 양자로 받아들여진 프랑스인이 영국 편으로 넘어가고, 태생이 에스파냐인인 자가 에스파냐인과 맞서 싸우고, 알리에게서 봉급을 받던 자가 알리를 배신하고 살해한 일에 비하면 그리 더 가증스러운 것도 아닙니다. 그런 일들에 견주면, 방금 읽으신 그 편지가 무엇이겠습니까? 한낱 연인의 배신일 뿐, 그 사내와 결혼한 여인이라면 마땅히 용서해야 할 것입니다. 하지만 그 여인과 결혼하기로 했던 연인이라면 그럴 수 없지요. 그런데 프랑스인들은 그 배신자에게 복수하지 않았고, 에스파냐인들은 그 배신자를 총살하지 않았으며, 무덤 속 알리도 그 배신자를 벌하지 않은 채 내버려 두었습니다. 그러나 배신당하고, 희생되고, 파묻혔던 제가, 하느님의 은총으로 무덤에서 일어나 그 사내를 벌하러 왔습니다. 하느님이 그 일을 위해 저를 보내셨고, 이렇게 제가 여기 있는 겁니다.\"\n\n가엾은 여인의 고개와 두 팔이 툭 떨어졌다. 다리에 힘이 풀려, 그녀는 무릎을 꿇고 말았다.\n\n\"용서해 주세요, 에드몽, 아직도 당신을 사랑하는 저를 봐서라도 용서해 주세요!\"\n\n아내로서의 위엄이 연인이자 어머니로서의 격정을 억눌렀다. 그녀의 이마가 양탄자에 거의 닿으려는 순간, 백작이 앞으로 달려들어 그녀를 일으켰다. 그러고는 의자에 앉아, 슬픔과 증오가 여전히 위협하는 표정으로 새겨진 몬테크리스토의 사내다운 얼굴을 바라보았다.\n\n\"저 저주받은 핏줄을 짓밟지 말라고?\" 그가 중얼거렸다. \"목적을 이루기 직전에 그것을 포기하라고? 안 됩니다, 부인. 그럴 수는 없습니다!\"\n\n\"에드몽,\" 온갖 수단을 다 써 보려는 가엾은 어머니가 말했다. \"제가 당신을 에드몽이라 부르는데, 어째서 당신은 저를 메르세데스라 부르지 않는 거죠?\"\n\n\"메르세데스!\" 몬테크리스토가 되뇌었다. \"메르세데스! 그래요, 옳은 말씀입니다. 그 이름은 아직도 매력을 잃지 않았군요. 그리고 이렇게 또렷하게 그 이름을 불러 본 것은 참으로 오랜만입니다. 오, 메르세데스, 저는 우울의 한숨과 함께, 비탄의 신음과 함께, 절망의 마지막 안간힘과 함께 당신의 이름을 불렀습니다. 추위에 얼어붙은 채 토굴 감옥의 짚더미 위에 웅크려서 그 이름을 불렀고, 열에 들떠 감옥의 돌바닥을 구르면서 그 이름을 불렀습니다. 메르세데스, 저는 복수해야 합니다. 십사 년을 괴로워했으니까요. 십사 년 동안 울었고, 저주했습니다. 이제 말씀드리지요, 메르세데스, 저는 반드시 복수해야 합니다.\"\n\n그토록 뜨겁게 사랑했던 여인의 애원에 굴복할까 두려웠던 백작은, 자신의 고통을 불러내 증오를 거들게 했다.\n\n\"그럼 복수하세요, 에드몽.\" 가엾은 어머니가 외쳤다. \"하지만 그 복수를 죄인들에게 내리세요. 그이에게, 저에게요. 하지만 제 아들에게는 안 돼요!\"\n\n\"성경에 이렇게 쓰여 있습니다,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"아비들의 죄가 그 자식들에게 삼대 사대까지 미치리라고요. 하느님께서 친히 당신의 예언자에게 그 말씀을 받아쓰게 하셨는데, 어찌 제가 하느님보다 나은 사람이 되려 하겠습니까?\"\n\n\"에드몽,\" 메르세데스가 백작을 향해 두 팔을 뻗으며 말을 이었다. \"당신을 처음 알게 된 그날부터, 저는 당신의 이름을 흠모했고, 당신에 대한 기억을 소중히 받들었어요. 에드몽, 내 친구, 제 마음의 거울에 끊임없이 비치던 그 고결하고 순결한 모습을 더럽히게 하지 말아 주세요. 에드몽, 당신이 살아 있다고 믿는 동안, 또 당신이 틀림없이 죽었으리라 여긴 뒤로, 제가 당신을 위해 하느님께 올린 그 모든 기도를 안다면! 그래요, 죽었다고요, 아아! 저는 당신의 주검이 어느 음산한 탑 아래에 묻혀 있거나, 잔인한 간수들 손에 구덩이 바닥으로 던져졌으리라 상상하며 울었어요. 기도하고 우는 것 말고, 제가 당신을 위해 무엇을 할 수 있었겠어요, 에드몽? 들어 보세요. 십 년 동안 저는 밤마다 똑같은 꿈을 꾸었어요. 사람들이 제게 말하기를, 당신이 탈출을 꾀했다고, 다른 죄수의 자리를 대신했다고, 시신의 수의 속으로 미끄러져 들어갔다고, 이프성 꼭대기에서 산 채로 내던져졌다고, 그리고 당신이 바위에 부딪치며 내지른 비명이 간수들에게 비로소 자신들이 당신을 죽인 살인자임을 알려 주었다고 했어요. 그래요, 에드몽, 제가 자비를 비는 그 아들의 목숨을 걸고 맹세하건대, 에드몽, 십 년 동안 밤마다 저는 그 끔찍한 비극의 낱낱을 보았고, 십 년 동안 밤마다 저를 깨우는 그 비명을 들으며 몸서리치고 얼어붙었어요. 저도요, 에드몽, 오! 믿어 주세요, 죄 많은 몸이었지만, 오, 그래요, 저도 무던히 괴로워했답니다!\"\n\n\"당신이 없는 사이에 아버지가 굶어 죽는다는 것이 어떤 것인지 아십니까?\" 몬테크리스토가 두 손을 머리카락 속에 쑤셔 넣으며 외쳤다. \"토굴 감옥 바닥에서 죽어 가는 동안, 사랑하던 여인이 연적에게 손을 내미는 것을 보는 것이 어떤 것인지 아십니까?\"\n\n\"몰라요.\" 메르세데스가 가로막았다. \"하지만 제가 사랑하던 사람이 제 아들을 죽이려 하는 것은 보았어요.\"\n\n메르세데스가 어찌나 깊은 고뇌로, 어찌나 사무친 절망의 어조로 이 말을 내뱉었던지, 몬테크리스토는 흐느낌을 억누를 수 없었다. 사자가 기가 꺾였고, 복수자가 굴복했다.\n\n\"제게 무엇을 바라십니까?\" 그가 말했다. \"아드님의 목숨 말입니까? 좋습니다, 그를 살려 두겠습니다!\"\n\n메르세데스가 외마디 소리를 질렀고, 그 소리에 몬테크리스토의 눈에서 눈물이 솟구쳤다. 하지만 그 눈물은 거의 순식간에 사라졌으니, 분명 하느님이 천사를 보내 그것을 거두어 가셨으리라. 그 눈물은 그의 눈에 구자라트와 오피르의 가장 값진 진주들보다도 훨씬 더 귀한 것이었다.\n\n\"오,\" 그녀가 백작의 손을 붙잡아 입술로 가져가며 말했다. \"오, 고마워요, 고마워요, 에드몽! 이제 당신은 제가 꿈꾸던 바로 그 모습이에요. 제가 언제나 사랑하던 그 사람이요. 오, 이제는 그렇게 말할 수 있어요!\"\n\n\"그렇다면 더욱 잘된 일입니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"그 가엾은 에드몽은 오래도록 부인의 사랑을 받지는 못할 테니까요. 죽음이 다시 무덤으로 돌아가고, 유령이 어둠 속으로 물러갈 참입니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이세요, 에드몽?\"\n\n\"부인이 제게 명하셨으니, 저는 죽어야 한다는 말입니다, 메르세데스.\"\n\n\"죽다니요? 어째서요? 누가 죽는다는 거예요? 어디서 그런 죽음 따위의 생각을 가져온 거죠?\"\n\n\"극장 전체가 보는 앞에서, 부인의 벗들과 아드님의 벗들이 보는 앞에서 공공연히 모욕당하고, 마치 승리라도 거둔 양 제 용서를 자랑스러워할 애송이에게 결투를 신청받은 제가, 단 한순간이라도 살기를 바라리라고는 생각지 마십시오. 메르세데스, 제가 부인 다음으로 가장 사랑한 것은 저 자신, 제 존엄, 그리고 저를 다른 사람들보다 우월하게 만들어 준 그 힘이었습니다. 그 힘이 곧 제 목숨이었지요. 부인은 단 한마디로 그것을 짓밟았고, 그래서 저는 죽습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 당신이 용서한다면, 결투는 벌어지지 않을 거 아니에요, 에드몽?\"\n\n\"결투는 벌어질 겁니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 더없이 엄숙한 어조로 말했다. \"다만 땅을 적시는 것이 아드님의 피가 아니라 제 피일 뿐이지요.\"\n\n메르세데스가 비명을 지르며 몬테크리스토에게 달려들었다. 그러다 문득 멈춰 섰다. \"에드몽,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"우리 위에는 하느님이 계세요. 당신이 살아 있고, 제가 당신을 다시 만났으니까요. 저는 진심으로 그분을 믿어요. 그분의 도움을 기다리는 동안, 저는 당신의 말을 믿겠어요. 제 아들이 살 거라고 말씀하셨죠, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 부인. 그는 살 것입니다.\" 메르세데스가 자신이 그녀를 위해 치르는 영웅적인 희생을 더 동요하는 기색도 없이 받아들이는 것에 놀라며 몬테크리스토가 말했다. 메르세데스가 백작에게 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"에드몽,\" 그녀가 말했다. 자기가 말을 건네는 그를 바라보는 그녀의 눈은 눈물에 젖어 있었다. \"당신은 정말 고결한 분이에요. 방금 당신이 한 일은 얼마나 위대한지요. 온갖 불리함을 무릅쓰고 당신에게 호소한 가엾은 여인을 가엾이 여겨 주신 것은 얼마나 숭고한 일인지요. 아아, 저는 세월보다 슬픔으로 더 늙어 버려서, 이제는 미소나 눈길로도 제 에드몽에게 그가 한때 그토록 오래 바라보던 그 메르세데스를 떠올리게 해 줄 수가 없네요. 아, 믿어 주세요, 에드몽, 제가 말했듯이 저도 무던히 괴로워했어요. 다시 말하지만, 떠올릴 기쁨 하나 없이, 단 하나의 희망도 간직하지 못한 채 한평생을 보낸다는 건 서글픈 일이에요. 하지만 그건 아직 모든 것이 끝나지 않았다는 증거예요. 아니에요, 끝나지 않았어요. 제 마음속에 남아 있는 것으로 그걸 느껴요. 오, 다시 말할게요, 에드몽. 방금 당신이 한 일은 아름다워요. 위대하고, 숭고해요.\"\n\n\"이제 와서 그런 말씀을 하시는군요, 메르세데스. 그렇다면 제가 부인을 위해 치르는 희생이 얼마나 큰지 아신다면 무어라 하시겠습니까? 지고하신 분께서 세상을 창조하고 혼돈을 비옥하게 하신 뒤, 어느 천사가 언젠가 인간의 죄로 인해 그 불멸의 눈에서 흘릴지도 모를 눈물을 덜어 주려고 그 일을 잠시 멈추셨다고 해 봅시다. 모든 것이 갖추어지고, 하느님이 당신의 작품을 굽어보며 보시기에 좋았더라 하실 순간이 왔을 때, 그분이 태양을 꺼 버리고 세상을 영원한 어둠 속으로 도로 내던지셨다고 해 봅시다. 그렇다 해도, 그렇다 해도, 메르세데스, 제가 지금 이 순간 제 목숨을 바치며 잃는 것이 무엇인지 부인은 상상조차 하실 수 없을 겁니다.\"\n\n메르세데스는 놀라움과 감탄과 감사를 한꺼번에 담은 눈길로 백작을 바라보았다. 몬테크리스토는 마치 더 이상 머릿속만으로는 제 생각의 무게를 홀로 감당할 수 없다는 듯, 불타는 두 손에 이마를 짓눌렀다.\n\n\"에드몽,\" 메르세데스가 말했다. \"당신에게 드릴 말이 한마디 더 남아 있어요.\"\n\n백작이 쓰디쓰게 미소 지었다.\n\n\"에드몽,\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"당신은 알게 될 거예요. 제 얼굴이 창백하고, 제 눈이 흐릿하고, 제 아름다움이 사라졌다 해도, 한마디로 메르세데스가 더 이상 그 옛날의 모습과 닮지 않았다 해도, 그 마음만은 여전히 그대로라는 것을요. 그럼 안녕히, 에드몽. 이제 하늘에 더 청할 것이 없어요. 당신을 다시 만났고, 옛날 그대로 고결하고 위대한 당신을 보았으니까요. 안녕히, 에드몽, 안녕히, 그리고 고마워요.\"\n\n하지만 백작은 대답하지 않았다. 메르세데스는 서재 문을 열었고, 좌절된 복수가 그를 빠뜨린 그 괴롭고 깊은 상념에서 그가 미처 헤어나기도 전에 사라져 버렸다.\n\n마차가 모르세르 부인을 태우고 샹젤리제의 포도(鋪道) 위를 굴러 멀어져 갈 때, 앵발리드의 시계가 한 시를 알렸고, 그 소리에 몬테크리스토가 고개를 들었다.\n\n\"내가 어찌나 어리석었던지,\" 그가 말했다. \"복수를 결심하던 그날, 내 심장을 뽑아내 버리지 못하다니!\"\n\n# Chapter 90. The Meeting\n\n메르세데스가 떠난 뒤, 몬테크리스토는 깊은 우울에 잠겼다.\n그를 둘러싼 것도, 그의 안에 있는 것도, 생각의 날갯짓이 멈춘 듯했다. 극도의 피로 뒤에 육신이 그러하듯, 그의 강인한 정신이 잠들어 있었다.\n\n\"무엇이란 말인가?\" 그가 혼잣말을 했다. 램프와 양초들은 거의 다 타들어 가고 있었고, 하인들은 대기실에서 초조하게 기다리고 있었다. \"무엇이란 말인가? 내가 그토록 오래 준비해 온 이 건물, 그 많은 정성과 수고로 쌓아 올린 이 건물이, 단 한 번의 손길에, 한마디 말에, 한 줄기 숨결에 무너지고 만단 말인가! 그렇다, 내가 그토록 소중히 여겼고, 그토록 자랑스러워했으며, 이프성의 지하 감옥에서는 그토록 하찮아 보였으나 내가 끝내 그토록 위대하게 만들어 낸 이 자아가, 내일이면 한 줌 진흙에 지나지 않게 될 것이다. 아아, 내가 아쉬워하는 것은 육신의 죽음이 아니다. 모든 것이 향해 가는 곳, 불행한 존재라면 누구나 갈망하는 안식, 생명의 원리가 소멸하는 것이야말로, 내가 그토록 오랜 세월 탄식하며 바라던 물질의 안식, 파리아가 내 감옥에 나타났을 때 굶주림이라는 고통스러운 방법으로 이루려 했던 그 안식이 아니던가? 나에게 죽음이란 무엇인가? 안식을 향한 한 걸음, 침묵을 향한, 어쩌면 두 걸음일 뿐이다. 아니, 그러니 내가 아쉬워하는 것은 존재가 아니라, 그토록 천천히 실행해 온, 그토록 공들여 짜낸 계획들이 무너지는 것이다. 가장 호의적이리라 믿었던 바로 그 순간에, 섭리는 이제 그 계획들에 등을 돌렸다. 그것들이 이루어지는 것은 신의 뜻이 아니다. 내가 들어 올려, 끝까지 짊어지려 했던 이 짐, 세상만큼이나 무거운 이 짐은 내 힘에 부쳤고, 나는 여정의 한복판에서 그것을 내려놓지 않을 수 없게 되었다. 오, 그렇다면 나는 다시 운명론자가 되는 것인가, 십사 년의 절망과 십 년의 희망이 섭리를 믿게 만든 이 운명론자로?\n\n\"이 모든 것이, 이 모든 것이, 죽었다고 여겼던 내 심장이 그저 잠들어 있었을 뿐이기 때문이다. 그것이 다시 깨어나 뛰기 시작했기 때문이며, 한 여인의 목소리가 내 가슴에 불러일으킨 감정의 고통에 내가 굴복했기 때문이다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 백작은 메르세데스가 받아들인 내일의 그 끔찍한 희생을 떠올리며 점점 더 깊이 빠져들었다. \"하지만, 그토록 고결한 마음을 지닌 여인이, 내가 한창때의 힘과 생명을 누리는 지금, 이기심 때문에 내 죽음에 동의한다는 것은 있을 수 없는 일이다. 그녀가 모성애를, 아니 광기에 가까운 그 마음을 그토록 극단까지 밀고 갈 수는 없다. 지나치면 죄가 되어 버리는 미덕도 있는 법. 아니, 그녀는 분명 어떤 애처로운 장면을 마음에 그려 두었을 것이다. 그녀가 달려와 우리 사이로 몸을 던질 것이고, 여기서는 숭고할 일이 거기서는 우스꽝스럽게 보일 것이다.\"\n\n이런 생각이 머리를 스치자 자존심의 홍조가 백작의 이마에 떠올랐다.\n\n\"우스꽝스럽다고?\" 그가 되뇌었다. \"그리고 그 비웃음은 내게로 떨어지겠지. 내가 우스꽝스럽다고? 아니, 차라리 죽는 편이 낫다.\"\n\n이렇게 백작은, 메르세데스에게 그녀의 아들을 살려 주겠노라 약속함으로써 스스로에게 선고한 다음 날의 불운을 마음속에서 한껏 부풀린 끝에, 마침내 이렇게 외쳤다.\n\n\"어리석다, 어리석어, 어리석은 짓이야! 그 젊은이가 겨눌 과녁으로 나 자신을 세워 두다니, 관용을 그토록 멀리까지 끌고 가다니. 그는 내 죽음이 자살이었다고는 결코 믿지 않을 것이다. 그러나 내 기억의 명예를 위해서는 중요한 일이다. 이것은 분명 허영이 아니라 정당한 자긍심이다. 이미 내려치려 들었던 이 팔을 내가 자유의지로 멈추었다는 것을, 그리고 남들에게 그토록 강력했던 이 팔로 나 자신을 쳤다는 것을, 세상이 알아야 한다는 것은 중요한 일이다. 그래야만 한다. 그렇게 될 것이다.\"\n\n펜을 집어 든 그는 책상의 비밀 서랍에서 종이 한 장을 꺼냈다. 그것은 다름 아닌, 파리에 도착한 뒤 작성해 둔 유언장이었다. 그는 그 문서 맨 아래에 자신의 죽음의 성격을 분명히 밝히는 일종의 추가 조항을 적었다.\n\n\"오, 하느님, 제가 이 일을 하는 것은,\" 그가 하늘을 향해 눈을 들며 말했다. \"제 명예를 위해서이기도 하나 그만큼 당신의 명예를 위해서이기도 합니다. 저는 십 년 동안 저 자신을 당신의 복수를 행하는 대리인으로 여겨 왔습니다. 모르세르, 당글라르, 빌포르 같은, 그리고 모르세르 그 자신 같은 다른 비열한 자들이, 우연히 자신들의 적에게서 벗어났다고 여겨서는 안 됩니다. 도리어 그들은 알아야 합니다. 섭리가 정해 놓은 그들의 벌이 지금의 제 결심으로 그저 미루어졌을 뿐이며, 이 세상에서는 그것을 피한다 해도 다른 세상에서 그들을 기다리고 있다는 것을, 그리고 그들은 그저 시간을 영원과 맞바꾸는 것일 뿐이라는 것을.\"\n\n이렇게 그가 음울한 불확실함에, 슬픔이 빚어낸 비참한 백일몽에 시달리고 있을 때, 동틀 녘의 첫 햇살이 창문을 뚫고 들어와, 방금 그가 섭리를 정당화하는 글을 적어 넣은 연푸른 종이 위로 비쳐 들었다.\n\n새벽 다섯 시 정각, 억눌린 한숨 같은 희미한 소리가 그의 귀에 닿았다. 그는 고개를 돌려 주위를 둘러보았으나 아무도 보이지 않았다. 그러나 그 소리가 또렷이 되풀이되어, 그것이 실제임을 확신하게 했다.\n\n그는 자리에서 일어나 응접실 문을 조용히 열었다. 하이데가 의자에 쓰러져 있었다. 두 팔은 늘어뜨린 채, 아름다운 머리는 뒤로 젖혀져 있었다. 그녀는 그가 자신을 보지 못한 채 나가지 못하도록 문가에 서 있었으나, 지켜보느라 지친 몸이 젊은이로서는 거역할 수 없는 잠에 굴복하고 만 것이었다. 문소리에도 그녀는 깨지 않았고, 몬테크리스토는 애틋한 회한이 담긴 눈길로 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"이 아이는 자기에게 아들이 있다는 것을 기억했지.\" 그가 말했다. \"그런데 나는 내게 딸이 있다는 것을 잊었구나.\" 그러더니 슬프게 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"가엾은 하이데. 나를 보고 싶어 했고, 내게 말하고 싶어 했지. 무언가를 두려워했거나 짐작한 게야. 오, 이 아이에게 작별 인사도 없이 갈 수는 없다. 이 아이를 누군가에게 맡기지 않고는 죽을 수 없다.\"\n\n그는 조용히 자리로 돌아와, 앞서 쓴 줄들 아래에 이렇게 적었다.\n\n\"나는 스파히 기병대 대위이자, 마르세유의 선주였던 나의 옛 후원자 피에르 모렐의 아들인 막시밀리앙 모렐에게 이천만 프랑을 물려준다. 그가 그의 누이 쥘리와 매부 에마뉘엘의 행복이 이 재산의 증가로 손상될까 두려워하지 않는다면, 그 일부를 그들에게 나누어 줄 수 있다. 이 이천만 프랑은 몬테크리스토 섬의 내 동굴에 감추어져 있으며, 베르투치오가 그 비밀을 알고 있다. 만일 그의 마음이 자유롭다면, 그리고 그가 야니나의 알리 파샤의 딸 하이데와 결혼한다면, 내가 아버지의 사랑으로 길러 왔고 나에게 딸의 사랑과 정성을 보여 준 그 아이와 결혼한다면, 그는 나의 마지막 소원을 이루어 주는 셈이 될 것이다. 이 유언장은 이미 하이데를 내 나머지 재산의 상속인으로 정해 두었으니, 그것은 토지, 영국·오스트리아·네덜란드의 자금, 여러 궁전과 저택에 있는 가구들로 이루어져 있으며, 이천만 프랑과 하인들에게 남긴 유산을 제외하고도 육천만 프랑에 이를 것이다.\"\n\n그가 마지막 줄을 끝맺으려는 순간, 등 뒤에서 들려온 외침에 그는 깜짝 놀랐고, 펜이 손에서 떨어졌다.\n\n\"하이데,\" 그가 말했다. \"그걸 읽었느냐?\"\n\n\"오, 주인님,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"어찌하여 이런 시각에 이런 글을 쓰고 계세요? 어찌하여 전 재산을 제게 물려주려 하세요? 저를 떠나시려는 건가요?\"\n\n\"여행을 떠나려 한단다, 얘야.\" 몬테크리스토가 한없는 다정함과 우수가 어린 표정으로 말했다. \"그런데 만일 내게 무슨 변고라도 생긴다면……\"\n\n백작은 말을 멈추었다.\n\n\"그러면요?\" 처녀가 물었다. 백작이 전에는 한 번도 들어 본 적 없는, 그를 놀라게 하는 단호한 어조였다.\n\n\"그러면, 내게 무슨 변고가 생긴다면,\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"나는 내 딸이 행복하기를 바란단다.\" 하이데가 슬프게 미소 지으며 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"죽음을 생각하고 계신가요, 주인님?\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"현자가 이렇게 말했단다, 얘야. '죽음을 생각하는 것은 좋은 일이다.'\"\n\n\"그러면, 만일 돌아가신다면,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"재산은 다른 이들에게 물려주세요. 주인님이 돌아가시면 저는 아무것도 필요 없을 테니까요.\" 그러더니 종이를 집어 네 조각으로 찢어 방 한가운데에 던졌다. 그러고는 그 안간힘에 기력이 다해, 이번에는 잠든 것이 아니라 정신을 잃고 바닥에 쓰러졌다.\n\n백작은 그녀에게 몸을 숙여 두 팔로 안아 올렸다. 그 달콤하고 창백한 얼굴, 감긴 그 사랑스러운 눈, 미동도 없이 보기에 따라서는 생기를 잃은 듯한 그 아름다운 몸을 보자, 어쩌면 그녀가 딸이 아버지를 사랑하는 것과는 다른 식으로 자신을 사랑하는지도 모른다는 생각이 그에게 처음으로 떠올랐다.\n\n\"아아,\" 그가 깊은 고통 속에 중얼거렸다. \"그렇다면 나는 아직도 행복할 수 있었을지 모른다.\"\n\n그러고 나서 그는 하이데를 그녀의 방으로 옮겨, 시녀들의 보살핌에 맡긴 뒤, 서재로 돌아와 이번에는 문을 재빨리 닫고는, 찢긴 유언장을 다시 베껴 적었다. 그가 다 마쳐 갈 무렵, 이륜마차 한 대가 마당으로 들어서는 소리가 들렸다. 몬테크리스토는 창가로 다가가, 막시밀리앙과 에마뉘엘이 내리는 것을 보았다. \"좋아.\" 그가 말했다. \"때맞춰 왔군.\" 그러고는 유언장에 세 개의 봉인을 찍었다.\n\n잠시 후 응접실에서 인기척이 들리자, 그는 직접 문을 열러 갔다. 모렐이 거기 있었다. 약속한 시각보다 이십 분이나 일찍 온 것이었다.\n\n\"제가 너무 일찍 왔는지도 모르겠습니다, 백작님.\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 솔직히 말씀드리면, 밤새 한숨도 못 잤습니다. 저희 집안 누구도 그랬고요. 저 자신을 추스르기 위해서라도, 백작님이 그 용감한 확신으로 굳건히 계신 모습을 봐야겠습니다.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 이 애정의 증표에 마음이 흔들리지 않을 수 없었다. 그는 젊은이에게 손을 내미는 데 그치지 않고, 두 팔을 벌려 그에게 달려갔다.\n\n\"모렐,\" 그가 말했다. \"자네 같은 사람에게 사랑받는다는 것을 느끼니, 내게는 행복한 날일세. 좋은 아침이네, 에마뉘엘. 그래, 자네는 나와 함께 가 주겠나, 막시밀리앙?\"\n\n\"의심하셨습니까?\" 젊은 대위가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 내가 틀린 거라면……\"\n\n\"어제 그 결투 신청이 벌어지는 동안 내내 백작님을 지켜보았습니다. 밤새도록 백작님의 그 굳건함을 생각했고, 정의는 분명 백작님 편에 있을 수밖에 없다고, 그렇지 않다면 사람의 얼굴은 더 이상 믿을 게 못 된다고 혼잣말을 했습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만, 모렐, 알베르는 자네 친구가 아닌가?\"\n\n\"그저 아는 사이일 뿐입니다, 백작님.\"\n\n\"나를 처음 본 바로 그날 만났다고 했지?\"\n\n\"네, 그렇습니다. 하지만 백작님이 일깨워 주지 않으셨다면 저는 기억조차 못 했을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"고맙네, 모렐.\" 그러고 나서 종을 한 번 울리고는, 곧바로 들어온 알리에게 말했다. \"이걸 내 변호사에게 가져다주어라. 내 유언장일세, 모렐. 내가 죽으면 자네가 가서 이것을 살펴보게.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이세요?\" 모렐이 말했다. \"백작님이 죽으신다니요?\"\n\n\"그래. 모든 일에 대비해 두어야 하지 않겠나, 친애하는 벗이여? 그런데 어제 나와 헤어진 뒤에 자네는 무엇을 했나?\"\n\n\"토르토니에 갔습니다. 예상대로 거기서 보샹과 샤토르노를 만났지요. 사실 그들을 찾고 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"왜? 모든 게 다 정해졌는데?\"\n\n\"들어 보십시오, 백작님. 이 일은 심각하고 피할 수 없는 일입니다.\"\n\n\"그걸 의심했나!\"\n\n\"아니요. 모욕은 공공연한 것이었고, 이미 모두가 그 얘기를 하고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그래서?\"\n\n\"그래서, 저는 무기를 바꿀 수 있기를 바랐습니다. 권총 대신 검으로 말입니다. 권총은 눈먼 무기니까요.\"\n\n\"성공했나?\" 몬테크리스토가 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 희미한 희망의 빛을 띠며 재빨리 물었다.\n\n\"아니요. 검을 다루는 백작님의 솜씨가 워낙 잘 알려져 있어서요.\"\n\n\"아? 누가 나를 일러바쳤나?\"\n\n\"백작님이 이긴 그 솜씨 좋은 검객이지요.\"\n\n\"그래서 실패한 건가?\"\n\n\"그들이 단호히 거절했습니다.\"\n\n\"모렐,\" 백작이 말했다. \"내가 권총 쏘는 걸 본 적이 있나?\"\n\n\"한 번도 없습니다.\"\n\"좋아, 시간이 있으니 보게.\" 몬테크리스토는 메르세데스가 들어왔을 때 손에 쥐고 있던 권총을 집어 들고, 클로버 에이스 카드를 철판에 고정해 두고는, 네 발로 클로버의 네 귀퉁이를 차례로 쏘아 떨어뜨렸다. 한 발 한 발이 울릴 때마다 모렐은 안색이 창백해졌다. 그는 몬테크리스토가 이 능란한 솜씨를 보이는 데 쓴 총알을 살펴보았는데, 그것이 산탄보다도 크지 않다는 것을 알았다.\n\n\"놀랍습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"보게, 에마뉘엘.\" 그러고 나서 몬테크리스토를 향해 돌아서서 말했다. \"백작님, 백작님이 소중히 여기시는 모든 것의 이름으로 간청합니다. 부디 알베르를 죽이지 말아 주십시오! 그 불행한 젊은이에게는 어머니가 있습니다.\"\n\n\"자네 말이 옳네.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그리고 내게는 어머니가 없지.\" 이 말은 모렐을 몸서리치게 하는 어조로 흘러나왔다.\n\n\"백작님이 모욕당한 쪽이십니다.\"\n\n\"틀림없지. 그게 무슨 뜻인가?\"\n\n\"백작님이 먼저 쏘신다는 뜻입니다.\"\n\n\"내가 먼저 쏜다고?\"\n\n\"오, 제가 얻어 냈습니다. 아니, 그것을 요구했지요. 그들이 그것만은 양보하게끔, 우리 쪽도 충분히 양보했으니까요.\"\n\n\"거리는 얼마나 되나?\"\n\n\"스무 걸음입니다.\" 무시무시한 의미가 담긴 미소가 백작의 입가를 스쳤다.\n\n\"모렐,\" 그가 말했다. \"방금 본 것을 잊지 말게.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 알베르가 살아남을 유일한 길은 백작님의 격한 감정에서 나오겠군요.\"\n\n\"내가 감정에 휘둘린다고?\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"아니면 백작님의 너그러움에서요, 친구여. 백작님처럼 뛰어난 사수에게는, 다른 사람에게라면 터무니없게 들릴 말도 할 수 있겠지요.\"\n\n\"그게 무엇인가?\"\n\n\"그의 팔을 부러뜨리든, 상처를 입히든 하시되, 죽이지는 말아 주십시오.\"\n\n\"내 말해 두지, 모렐.\" 백작이 말했다. \"나는 모르세르 씨의 목숨을 살리는 데 간청 같은 건 필요치 않네. 그는 아주 잘 살아남을 것이고, 두 친구와 함께 조용히 돌아갈 것일세. 그동안 나는……\"\n\n\"백작님은요?\"\n\n\"그건 다른 이야기가 되겠지. 나는 집으로 실려 갈 걸세.\"\n\n\"안 됩니다, 안 돼요.\" 막시밀리앙이 감정을 도무지 억누르지 못한 채 외쳤다.\n\n\"내가 말했듯이, 친애하는 모렐, 모르세르 씨가 나를 죽일 걸세.\"\n\n모렐은 완전히 어안이 벙벙해져 그를 바라보았다. \"그렇다면 어젯밤 이후로 무슨 일이 있었던 겁니까, 백작님?\"\n\n\"필리피 전투 전날 밤 브루투스에게 일어났던 것과 똑같은 일이지. 나는 유령을 보았네.\"\n\n\"그 유령이……\"\n\n\"내게 말했네, 모렐, 내가 충분히 오래 살았다고.\"\n\n막시밀리앙과 에마뉘엘이 서로를 마주 보았다. 몬테크리스토는 회중시계를 꺼냈다. \"가세.\" 그가 말했다. \"일곱 시 오 분이고, 약속은 여덟 시였으니까.\"\n\n마차 한 대가 문 앞에 채비를 마치고 있었다. 몬테크리스토는 두 친구와 함께 마차에 올랐다. 그는 통로에서 잠시 멈춰 어느 문에 귀를 기울였는데, 사려 깊게 몇 걸음 앞서 나가 있던 막시밀리앙과 에마뉘엘은 안에서 들려온 흐느낌에 그가 한숨으로 답하는 소리를 들은 듯했다. 시계가 여덟 시를 칠 때 그들은 약속 장소에 마차를 댔다.\n\n\"우리가 먼저 왔군요.\" 모렐이 창밖을 내다보며 말했다.\n\n\"죄송합니다, 주인님.\" 형용할 수 없는 두려움에 떨며 주인을 따라온 바티스탱이 말했다. \"하지만 저기 나무 아래 마차 한 대가 있는 것 같습니다.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 마차에서 가볍게 뛰어내려, 에마뉘엘과 막시밀리앙이 내리는 것을 도우려 손을 내밀었다. 막시밀리앙은 백작의 손을 자신의 두 손 사이에 잡아 두었다.\n\n\"좋습니다,\" 그가 말했다. \"그 임자가 자기 대의의 정당함을 믿고 있을 때, 이런 손을 느끼는 것이 좋습니다.\"\n\n\"제가 보기엔,\" 에마뉘엘이 말했다. \"저 아래에 분명히 기다리고 있는 듯한 젊은이 둘이 보입니다.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 모렐을 그의 매부 뒤로 한두 걸음 끌어당겼다.\n\n\"막시밀리앙,\" 그가 말했다. \"자네 마음은 누구에게도 매여 있지 않은가?\" 모렐이 놀라서 몬테크리스토를 바라보았다. \"자네의 속내를 알려는 게 아닐세, 친애하는 벗이여. 그저 간단한 질문 하나를 하는 것뿐이니, 대답해 주게. 내가 바라는 건 그것뿐일세.\"\n\n\"한 처녀를 사랑하고 있습니다, 백작님.\"\n\n\"많이 사랑하나?\"\n\n\"제 목숨보다 더요.\"\n\n\"또 하나의 희망이 꺾였군!\" 백작이 말했다. 그러고는 한숨을 쉬며 중얼거렸다. \"가엾은 하이데!\"\n\n\"솔직히 말씀드리면, 백작님, 제가 백작님을 잘 모른다면, 백작님이 실제보다 덜 용감한 분이라고 여겼을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"내가 두고 떠나는 누군가를 떠올리며 한숨짓는다고 해서? 이보게, 모렐, 군인이 용기를 그렇게 잘못 가늠한다는 건 어울리지 않는 일일세. 내가 삶을 아쉬워한다고? 이십 년을 삶과 죽음 사이에서 보낸 내게, 그게 무슨 대수겠나? 게다가 너무 걱정하지 말게, 모렐. 이 약함이, 그게 약함이라면 말이지, 드러난 것은 자네에게뿐이니. 나는 세상이 하나의 응접실이며, 우리는 거기서 정중하고 떳떳하게 물러나야 한다는 것을 알고 있네. 다시 말해, 인사를 하고, 명예의 빚을 다 갚고서 말일세.\"\n\n\"그게 바로 핵심이군요. 무기는 가져오셨습니까?\"\n\n\"내가? 무엇하러? 저 신사들이 무기를 가져왔기를 바라네.\"\n\n\"제가 물어보겠습니다.\" 모렐이 말했다.\n\n\"그러게. 하지만 어떤 협상도 하지 말게. 무슨 말인지 알겠나?\"\n\n\"염려 마십시오.\" 모렐은 보샹과 샤토르노 쪽으로 다가갔고, 그의 의도를 알아챈 두 사람이 그를 맞으러 나왔다. 세 젊은이는, 다정하지는 않더라도 정중하게 서로 인사를 나누었다.\n\n\"실례합니다, 신사 여러분.\" 모렐이 말했다. \"그런데 모르세르 씨가 보이지 않는군요.\"\n\n\"오늘 아침 우리에게 전갈을 보내왔습니다.\" 샤토르노가 대답했다. \"결투장에서 만나겠다고요.\"\n\n\"아.\" 모렐이 말했다. 보샹이 회중시계를 꺼냈다.\n\n\"이제 겨우 여덟 시 오 분입니다.\" 그가 모렐에게 말했다. \"아직 그리 많이 늦은 건 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"오, 저는 그런 뜻으로 말한 게 아니었습니다.\" 모렐이 대답했다.\n\n\"마차 한 대가 오고 있군요.\" 샤토르노가 말했다. 마차는 그들이 모여 있는 빈터로 이어지는 가로수길 가운데 하나를 따라 빠르게 다가왔다.\n\n\"신사 여러분, 분명 권총을 준비해 오셨겠지요? 몬테크리스토 씨는 자신의 권총을 쓸 권리를 양보하셨습니다.\"\n\n\"백작님의 그런 호의를 우리도 예상하고 있었습니다.\" 보샹이 말했다. \"그래서 제가 이런 경우에 쓸 일이 있으리라 생각하고 여드레인가 열흘 전에 사 둔 무기 몇 자루를 가져왔습니다. 아주 새것이라 아직 쓴 적이 없지요. 살펴보시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"오, 보샹 씨, 모르세르 씨가 이 권총들을 모른다고 장담하신다면, 당신의 말씀만으로도 충분하다는 걸 기꺼이 믿겠습니다.\"\n\n\"신사 여러분,\" 샤토르노가 말했다. \"저 마차로 오는 건 모르세르가 아닙니다. 이런, 프란츠와 드브레인데요!\"\n\n그가 알린 두 젊은이가 정말로 다가오고 있었다. \"무슨 일로 여기까지 오셨습니까, 신사 여러분?\" 샤토르노가 둘과 차례로 악수하며 말했다.\n\n\"그게 말이오,\" 드브레가 말했다. \"알베르가 오늘 아침 우리에게 사람을 보내 와 달라고 청했소.\" 보샹과 샤토르노가 놀란 눈빛을 주고받았다. \"제가 그 까닭을 알 것 같습니다.\" 모렐이 말했다.\n\n\"무엇입니까?\"\n\n\"어제 오후 모르세르 씨에게서 편지를 받았는데, 오페라에 와 달라는 부탁이었습니다.\"\n\n\"나도 그랬소.\" 드브레가 말했다.\n\n\"나도 그랬습니다.\" 프란츠가 말했다.\n\n\"우리도 그랬지요.\" 보샹과 샤토르노가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"여러분 모두에게 결투 신청을 지켜봐 달라고 청했으니, 이제 결투에도 참석해 달라는 것이군요.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다.\" 젊은이들이 말했다. \"아마 제대로 짚으셨군요.\"\n\n\"하지만 이 모든 준비를 해 놓고서, 정작 본인은 오지 않는군요.\" 샤토르노가 말했다. \"알베르가 십 분이나 늦었습니다.\"\n\n\"저기 오는군요.\" 보샹이 말했다. \"말을 타고 전속력으로 달려오고, 하인이 뒤따르고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"경솔하기도 하지.\" 샤토르노가 말했다. \"권총 결투를 하러 말을 타고 오다니, 내가 그렇게 일러 두었는데도 말이야.\"\n\n\"게다가,\" 보샹이 말했다. \"크라바트 위로 깃을 세우고, 외투를 풀어 헤치고, 흰 조끼라니! 차라리 심장에 표적을 하나 그려 두지 그랬을까. 그게 더 간단했을 텐데.\"\n\n그러는 사이 알베르는 다섯 젊은이가 이룬 무리에서 열 걸음 안까지 다가왔다. 그는 말에서 뛰어내려 고삐를 하인의 팔에 던지고는 그들에게 합류했다. 그는 안색이 창백했고, 눈은 충혈되어 부어 있었다. 한숨도 자지 못한 게 분명했다. 그의 얼굴에는 본래 그에게는 어울리지 않는 우울하고 무거운 그늘이 드리워 있었다.\n\n\"여러분, 고맙습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"제 청을 들어주셔서요. 이런 우정의 표시에 더없이 감사하게 생각합니다.\" 모르세르가 다가오자 모렐은 뒤로 물러나 조금 떨어진 곳에 머물러 있었다. \"그리고 당신에게도, 모렐 씨, 감사를 드려야겠군요. 자, 사람은 많을수록 좋지요.\"\n\n\"선생,\" 막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"어쩌면 모르실지 모르겠는데, 저는 몬테크리스토 씨의 친구입니다.\"\n\n\"확실히는 몰랐지만, 그럴지도 모른다고 생각했습니다. 그렇다면 더욱 잘된 일이지요. 여기 명예로운 분들이 많을수록 저는 더 흡족할 테니까요.\"\n\n\"모렐 씨,\" 샤토르노가 말했다. \"몬테크리스토 백작께 모르세르 씨가 도착했고 우리가 백작의 처분을 기다린다고 알려 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n모렐은 그 임무를 수행하려 채비했다. 그사이 보샹은 마차에서 권총 상자를 꺼냈다.\n\n\"잠깐만요, 여러분.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"몬테크리스토 백작께 드릴 말씀이 두 마디 있습니다.\"\n\n\"단둘이서요?\" 모렐이 물었다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 선생. 여기 계신 모든 분 앞에서요.\"\n\n알베르의 입회인들이 서로를 바라보았다. 프란츠와 드브레가 속삭이듯 몇 마디를 주고받았고, 이 뜻밖의 사태에 기뻐한 모렐은 백작을 데리러 갔다. 백작은 에마뉘엘과 함께 외진 오솔길을 걷고 있었다.\n\n\"그가 내게 무슨 볼일이 있다는 건가?\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"모르겠습니다. 하지만 백작님과 이야기하고 싶어 합니다.\"\n\n\"아?\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그가 또 새로운 모욕으로 나를 시험하려는 게 아니길 바라네!\"\n\n\"그런 의도는 아닌 듯합니다.\" 모렐이 말했다.\n\n백작은 막시밀리앙과 에마뉘엘을 데리고 앞으로 나아갔다. 그의 차분하고 평온한 표정은, 다른 네 젊은이를 거느린 채 역시 다가오는 알베르의 비탄에 잠긴 얼굴과 묘한 대조를 이루었다.\n\n서로 세 걸음 거리에 이르자, 알베르와 백작이 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"가까이 오십시오, 여러분.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"제가 이제 몬테크리스토 백작께 영광스럽게 드릴 말씀을, 한마디도 놓치지 않으시길 바랍니다. 여러분에게 아무리 이상하게 들리더라도, 이것은 그것을 들으려는 모든 사람에게 여러분이 전해 주셔야 할 말이기 때문입니다.\"\n\n\"말씀하시지요, 선생.\" 백작이 말했다.\n\n\"선생,\" 알베르가 처음에는 떨리는 목소리로, 그러나 점차 더 굳건해지는 목소리로 말했다. \"저는 당신이 에피루스에서의 모르세르 씨의 행적을 폭로한 것을 두고 당신을 비난했습니다. 그가 죄를 지었다는 것을 알면서도, 당신에게는 그를 벌할 권리가 없다고 여겼기 때문입니다. 그러나 그 후 저는 당신에게 그 권리가 있다는 것을 알게 되었습니다. 제가 이토록 선뜻 당신을 용서하게 된 것은 페르낭 몬데고가 알리 파샤에게 저지른 배신 때문이 아니라, 어부 페르낭이 당신에게 저지른 배신, 그리고 그로 인해 빚어진, 거의 들어 본 적 없는 비참함 때문입니다. 그러니 저는 말합니다, 그리고 공공연히 선언합니다. 당신이 제 아버지에게 복수한 것은 정당한 일이었으며, 그의 아들인 저는 당신이 더 가혹하게 굴지 않은 것에 감사드립니다.\"\n\n이 뜻밖의 장면을 지켜보던 이들 한가운데에 벼락이 떨어졌다 한들, 알베르의 선언만큼 그들을 놀라게 하지는 못했을 것이다. 몬테크리스토로 말하자면, 그의 두 눈이 한없는 감사의 빛을 띠며 천천히 하늘을 향해 들렸다. 그는 로마의 산적들 사이에서 숱하게 보았던 알베르의 그 불같은 성정이 어떻게 이런 굴욕에 갑자기 몸을 굽힐 수 있었는지 이해할 수 없었다. 그는 거기서 메르세데스의 영향력을 알아보았고, 어찌하여 그녀의 고결한 마음이, 소용없으리라는 것을 미리 알면서도 그 희생에 맞서지 않았는지를 깨달았다.\n\n\"이제, 선생,\" 알베르가 말했다. \"제 사과가 충분하다고 생각하신다면, 부디 제게 손을 내밀어 주십시오. 당신이 지닌 듯한 그 오류 없음의 미덕 다음으로, 저는 잘못을 솔직히 인정하는 미덕을 높이 칩니다. 하지만 이 고백은 저 자신에게만 관계된 일입니다. 저는 인간으로서 잘 처신했으나, 당신은 인간 이상으로 처신하셨습니다. 우리 둘 중 하나를 죽음에서 구할 수 있었던 것은 오직 천사뿐이었을 테고, 그 천사가 하늘에서 내려왔습니다. 우리를 친구로 만들기 위해서는 아니었더라도, 아아, 운명이 그것을 불가능하게 만드니까요, 적어도 서로를 존중하게 만들기 위해서 말입니다.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 촉촉해진 눈과 들썩이는 가슴, 반쯤 벌어진 입술로 알베르에게 손을 내밀었고, 알베르는 경외에 가까운 두려움이 어린 마음으로 그 손을 잡았다.\n\n\"여러분,\" 그가 말했다. \"몬테크리스토 씨가 제 사과를 받아 주셨습니다. 저는 그분께 성급하게 굴었습니다. 성급한 행동은 대개 나쁜 행동이지요. 이제 제 잘못은 바로잡혔습니다. 제가 양심이 이르는 대로 행동했다 하여, 세상이 저를 비겁하다 일컫지 않기를 바랍니다. 그러나 만일 누군가 저에 대해 잘못된 의견을 품는다면,\" 그가 마치 친구와 적 모두에게 도전하듯 몸을 곧추세우며 덧붙였다. \"저는 그 오해를 바로잡으려 애쓸 것입니다.\"\n\n\"밤사이 무슨 일이 있었던 거지?\" 보샹이 샤토르노에게 물었다. \"우리가 여기서 아주 한심한 꼴이 된 것 같은데.\"\n\n\"사실, 알베르가 방금 한 일은 아주 비열하거나, 아니면 아주 고결하거나, 둘 중 하나일세.\" 남작이 대답했다.\n\n\"이게 무슨 뜻일까?\" 드브레가 프란츠에게 말했다. \"몬테크리스토 백작이 모르세르 씨에게 명예롭지 못하게 행동했는데, 정작 그 아들이 그를 정당하다고 두둔하다니! 내 집안에 야니나가 열 개쯤 있다 해도, 나라면 오히려 열 번을 더 싸워야 할 의무가 있다고 여겼을 텐데.\"\n\n몬테크리스토로 말하자면, 그는 고개를 떨군 채 두 팔에 힘이 빠져 있었다. 이십사 년의 회상의 무게에 짓눌린 채, 그는 알베르도, 보샹도, 샤토르노도, 그 무리의 누구도 생각하지 않았다. 다만 그는, 아들의 목숨을 구걸하러 온 그 용감한 여인을 생각하고 있었다. 그가 자신의 목숨을 내어 준 그 여인, 그리고 이제 그 젊은이의 마음속에서 자식 된 도리의 온갖 정을 영원히 무너뜨릴 수 있는 끔찍한 집안의 비밀을 폭로함으로써 그 목숨을 구해 낸 그 여인을.\n\n\"역시 섭리로다.\" 그가 중얼거렸다. \"이제야 비로소 내가 신의 사자임을 온전히 확신한다!\"\n\n# Chapter 91. Mother and Son\n\n몬테크리스토 백작은 우수와 위엄이 어린 미소를 지으며 다섯 청년에게 고개 숙여 인사하고는, 막시밀리앙과 에마뉘엘과 함께 마차에 올랐다. 알베르와 보샹, 샤토르노 세 사람만 남았다. 알베르는 두 친구를 바라보았다. 겁먹은 눈빛은 아니었으나, 자신이 방금 한 일에 대한 두 사람의 생각을 묻는 듯한 눈빛이었다.\n\n\"솔직히 말하지, 친구.\" 가장 정이 깊거나 가장 속을 감추지 못하는 보샹이 먼저 입을 열었다. \"축하 한마디 하게 해 주게. 아주 불쾌한 일이 뜻밖의 방식으로 마무리됐으니 말일세.\"\n\n알베르는 말없이 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 샤토르노는 휘청거리는 단장으로 자기 장화를 톡톡 두드릴 뿐이었다.\n\n\"이제 가지 않겠나?\" 그가 거북한 침묵 끝에 말했다.\n\n\"자네가 원할 때 가지.\" 보샹이 대답했다. \"다만 모르세르 씨에게 한마디 칭찬은 하게 해 주게. 오늘 보기 드문 기사도적 아량을 보여 주었으니까.\"\n\n\"아, 그래.\" 샤토르노가 말했다.\n\n\"저렇게 자제력을 발휘할 수 있다니, 참으로 대단한 일이야.\" 보샹이 말을 이었다.\n\n\"분명히 그렇지. 나라면 도저히 못 했을 걸세.\" 샤토르노가 의미심장하리만치 냉랭하게 말했다.\n\n\"두 사람,\" 알베르가 끼어들었다. \"몬테크리스토 씨와 나 사이에 아주 중대한 일이 오갔다는 걸 자네들은 미처 모르는 것 같군.\"\n\n\"그럴 수도 있지, 그럴 수도 있어.\" 보샹이 곧바로 말했다. \"하지만 멍청이들은 죄다 자네의 그 영웅적 태도를 이해하지 못할 테고, 머지않아 자넨 그들에게 그걸 설명해야 할 처지에 놓일 걸세. 그것도 자네 몸 건강과 목숨이 견뎌 낼 수 있는 정도를 훨씬 넘어서는 격렬한 방식으로 말이야. 친구로서 한마디 충고해도 되겠나? 나폴리나 헤이그, 상트페테르부르크로 떠나게. 우리 다혈질 파리 사람들보다 명예의 문제를 더 잘 헤아리는 차분한 나라들이지. 그곳에서 평온과 망각을 찾고, 몇 해 뒤에 잠잠해지면 프랑스로 돌아오게나. 안 그런가, 샤토르노 씨?\"\n\n\"내 생각도 똑같네.\" 샤토르노가 말했다. \"거절당한 결투만큼 더 심각한 결투를 불러오는 것도 없으니까.\"\n\n\"고맙네, 두 사람.\" 알베르가 무심한 미소를 띠고 대답했다. \"충고를 따르도록 하지. 자네들이 권해서가 아니라, 나도 전부터 프랑스를 떠날 생각이었으니까. 내 입회인이 되어 준 일도 똑같이 고맙게 생각하네. 그건 내 마음 깊이 새겨졌으니, 방금 자네들이 한 말을 듣고서도 나는 그것만 기억할 걸세.\"\n\n샤토르노와 보샹은 서로를 마주 보았다. 두 사람의 인상은 같았고, 방금 모르세르가 고마움을 표한 말투가 어찌나 단호했던지 대화를 더 이어 갔다가는 모두에게 거북한 상황이 될 판이었다.\n\n\"잘 가게, 알베르.\" 보샹이 불쑥 말하며 무심하게 청년에게 손을 내밀었다. 알베르는 무기력에서 깨어날 기색이 없었고, 사실 내민 손을 알아채지도 못했다.\n\n\"잘 가게.\" 이번에는 샤토르노가 말했다. 그는 작은 단장을 왼손에 쥐고 오른손으로 인사를 건넸다.\n\n알베르의 입술은 가까스로 \"잘 가게.\"라고 속삭였을 뿐이었으나, 눈빛은 더 많은 것을 말하고 있었다. 그 눈빛에는 억눌린 분노와 도도한 경멸, 그리고 떳떳한 의분이 한 편의 시처럼 담겨 있었다. 그는 두 친구가 마차에 다시 오른 뒤에도 한동안 우수에 잠긴 채 미동도 없이 그 자리에 서 있었다. 그러다 하인이 작은 나무에 매어 둔 말을 풀더니, 훌쩍 올라타 파리 쪽으로 말을 몰아 내달렸다.\n\n십오 분 만에 그는 엘데 거리의 저택으로 들어섰다. 마차에서 내리던 그는 백작의 침실 커튼 뒤로 아버지의 창백한 얼굴이 보이는 듯했다. 알베르는 한숨을 쉬며 고개를 돌리고 제 방으로 향했다. 그는 어린 시절부터 삶을 그토록 편안하고 행복하게 만들어 준 온갖 사치품들을 미련 어린 눈길로 둘러보았다. 미소 짓는 듯한 얼굴들이 담긴 그림들을, 한층 환한 빛깔로 그려진 듯한 풍경화들을 바라보았다. 그런 다음 떡갈나무 액자에 든 어머니의 초상화를 떼어 냈다. 초상을 빼낸 자리에는 검고 텅 빈 금테 액자만 남았다. 이어 아름다운 터키 무기들과 멋진 영국 총들, 일본 자기, 은으로 받침을 두른 잔들, 푀셰르와 바리●가 만든 예술적인 청동 조각들을 가지런히 정돈했다. 찬장을 살피고는 저마다 열쇠를 꽂아 두었다. 열어 둔 책상 서랍 하나에 지니고 있던 잔돈을 전부 던져 넣고, 거기에 화병과 보석함에서 꺼낸 자질구레한 장신구 천 가지를 함께 넣었다. 그러고는 모든 물건의 정확한 목록을 작성해, 그곳에 쌓여 있던 책과 서류를 한쪽으로 치운 뒤 탁자 위 가장 눈에 잘 띄는 자리에 놓았다.\n* 푀셰르와 바리: 19세기 프랑스의 유명한 조각가들.―옮긴이\n\n이 일을 막 시작했을 때, 들어오지 말라는 분부에도 불구하고 하인이 그의 방으로 들어왔다.\n\n\"무슨 일인가?\" 그가 화났다기보다 서글픈 어조로 물었다.\n\n\"용서하십시오, 도련님.\" 하인이 대답했다. \"방해하지 말라고 분부하셨습니다만, 모르세르 백작님께서 저를 부르셨습니다.\"\n\n\"그래서!\" 알베르가 말했다.\n\n\"먼저 도련님을 뵙지 않고는 가고 싶지 않았습니다.\"\n\n\"왜?\"\n\n\"백작님께서 오늘 아침 제가 도련님을 모시고 그 자리에 갔던 걸 분명 알고 계실 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇겠지.\" 알베르가 말했다.\n\n\"백작님께서 저를 부르신 건 거기서 무슨 일이 있었는지 캐물으시려는 게 틀림없습니다. 뭐라고 답해야 할까요?\"\n\n\"사실대로.\"\n\n\"그럼 결투가 벌어지지 않았다고 말씀드릴까요?\"\n\n\"내가 몬테크리스토 백작에게 사과했다고 말하게. 가 보게.\"\n\n하인은 고개 숙여 인사하고 물러갔고, 알베르는 다시 목록 작성으로 돌아갔다. 그 일을 마무리할 무렵, 마당에서 말들이 발을 구르는 소리와 마차 바퀴가 창문을 흔드는 소리에 주의가 쏠렸다. 그는 창가로 다가갔다. 아버지가 마차에 올라 떠나는 모습이 보였다. 문이 닫히기가 무섭게 알베르는 어머니의 방으로 발걸음을 옮겼다. 그를 알릴 사람이 아무도 없었으므로 그는 곧장 침실로 들어섰고, 눈앞에 펼쳐진 광경과 짐작되는 바에 가슴이 미어져 잠시 문 앞에 멈춰 섰다.\n\n마치 같은 생각이 두 사람을 사로잡기라도 한 듯, 메르세데스는 자기 방에서 알베르가 방금 제 방에서 한 것과 똑같은 일을 하고 있었다. 모든 것이 정돈되어 있었다. 레이스며 드레스며 보석이며 속옷이며 돈이며, 전부 서랍 속에 정리되어 있었고, 백작 부인은 열쇠들을 조심스레 챙기고 있었다. 알베르는 이 모든 채비를 보았고 그 뜻을 알아차렸다. 그는 \"어머니!\" 하고 부르짖으며 두 팔로 어머니의 목을 끌어안았다.\n\n이 두 얼굴의 표정을 그려 낼 수 있는 화가라면 분명 그것으로 한 폭의 아름다운 그림을 완성했으리라. 단호한 결심을 보여 주는 이 모든 흔적이, 자기 자신을 위해서라면 두렵지 않았을 알베르였으나 어머니를 위해서는 그를 불안하게 만들었다. \"무엇을 하고 계신 겁니까?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"너는 무엇을 하고 있었느냐?\" 어머니가 되물었다.\n\n\"아, 어머니!\" 알베르가 너무도 북받쳐 가까스로 말을 이었다. \"어머니와 저는 다릅니다. 어머니가 저와 같은 결심을 하셨을 리 없어요. 저는 어머니께 이 집과, 그리고…… 어머니께 작별을 고하러 왔으니까요.\"\n\n\"나 역시,\" 메르세데스가 대답했다. \"떠나려던 참이었다. 솔직히 말하면 네가 나와 함께 가 주리라 믿고 있었지. 내가 잘못 생각한 거냐?\"\n\n\"어머니.\" 알베르가 단호하게 말했다. \"제가 스스로 정한 운명을 어머니께 나눠 지게 할 수는 없습니다. 저는 이제 지위도 재산도 없이 살아가야 하고, 이 고된 수련을 시작하려면 제 입에 풀칠할 빵 한 덩이를 벌어 내기까지는 친구에게 빌려야 할 형편입니다. 그래서, 어머니, 지금 당장 프란츠에게 가서 당장 필요한 만큼의 적은 돈을 빌려 달라고 부탁할 참입니다.\"\n\n\"내 가엾은 아들아, 네가 가난과 굶주림을 겪는다고? 오, 그런 말 마라. 내 결심이 무너질 것 같구나.\"\n\n\"하지만 제 결심은 무너지지 않습니다, 어머니.\" 알베르가 대답했다. \"저는 젊고 튼튼해요. 제게 용기가 있다고 믿고, 어제부터 의지의 힘을 배웠습니다. 아아, 어머니, 어떤 이들은 그토록 큰 고통을 겪고도 살아남아, 하늘이 약속했던 행복의 모든 언약이 무너진 폐허 위에, 신이 주셨던 모든 희망의 파편 위에 새로운 부를 쌓아 올렸습니다. 저는 그것을 보았습니다, 어머니. 적들이 그들을 빠뜨린 심연에서 그들이 어찌나 힘차고 영광스럽게 일어섰는지, 이번엔 도리어 자신들을 짓밟았던 자들을 다스리고 응징하기에 이르렀다는 것을 저는 압니다. 아니요, 어머니. 이 순간부터 저는 과거와 결별하고, 그로부터 아무것도 받지 않겠습니다. 이름조차 받지 않을 겁니다. 어머니도 아시다시피, 어떤 사람이 다른 이 앞에서 부끄러워 얼굴을 붉혀야 할 이름이라면, 그 아들이 그것을 짊어질 수는 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"알베르, 내 아들아.\" 메르세데스가 말했다. \"내게 좀 더 강한 마음이 있었더라면, 그것이 바로 내가 네게 해 주었을 충고다. 내 목소리가 너무 약해졌을 때 네 양심이 대신 말해 주었으니, 그 명령에 귀를 기울이려무나. 네게는 친구들이 있었지, 알베르. 그들과의 교제를 끊어라. 하지만 절망하지는 마라. 너는 앞날이 창창하다, 내 사랑하는 알베르. 아직 스물둘밖에 안 되었으니까. 그리고 너처럼 순결한 마음에는 티 없는 이름이 어울리니, 내 아버지의 이름을 쓰려무나. 에레라였단다. 분명히 말하건대, 내 사랑하는 알베르, 네 앞길이 어떠하든 너는 머지않아 그 이름을 빛낼 게다. 그러면, 내 아들아, 지난날의 슬픔이 있었기에 더욱 눈부신 모습으로 세상에 돌아오너라. 내 생각이 틀렸다 해도, 나는 이 희망만은 품고 싶구나. 내게는 바라볼 미래가 없으니 말이다. 이 집 문턱을 넘는 순간, 내게는 무덤이 열린단다.\"\n\n\"어머니의 소원을 모두 이루어 드리겠습니다, 어머니.\" 청년이 말했다. \"네, 저도 어머니의 희망을 함께 품겠습니다. 어머니는 순결하고 저는 결백하니, 하늘의 노여움이 우리를 뒤쫓지는 않을 겁니다. 하지만 결심을 굳힌 이상, 어서 움직이도록 해요. 모르세르 씨가 삼십 분쯤 전에 나가셨으니, 해명할 일 없이 빠져나가기에 좋은 기회입니다.\"\n\n\"준비됐다, 내 아들아.\" 메르세데스가 말했다.\n\n알베르는 마차를 부르러 달려갔다. 그는 생페르 거리에 세놓는 작은 가구 딸린 집이 하나 있다는 것이 떠올랐다. 어머니가 누추하나마 그럭저럭 지낼 만한 거처를 찾을 수 있는 곳이었고, 그는 그곳으로 백작 부인을 모실 작정이었다. 마차가 문 앞에 멈추고 알베르가 막 내리려는데, 한 사내가 다가와 그에게 편지 한 통을 건넸다.\n\n알베르는 편지를 가져온 자를 알아보았다. \"백작님께서 보내신 겁니다.\" 베르투치오가 말했다. 알베르는 편지를 받아 뜯어 읽고는 베르투치오를 찾아 둘러보았으나, 그는 이미 사라지고 없었다.\n\n알베르는 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁하고 가슴이 들먹이는 채로 메르세데스에게 돌아가, 한마디도 하지 않고 편지를 건넸다. 메르세데스는 읽어 내려갔다.\n\n\"알베르 군에게.―내가 자네의 계획을 알아차렸음을 보여 주면서, 동시에 나의 세심함도 자네에게 납득시킬 수 있기를 바라네. 자네는 자유로운 몸이니 백작의 집을 떠나, 어머니를 모시고 자네의 거처로 가겠지. 하지만 알베르, 생각해 보게. 자네는 그 가엾고 고결한 마음으로 갚을 수 있는 것보다 더 많은 것을 어머니께 빚지고 있네. 싸움은 자네 몫으로 남겨 두고 모든 고통을 떠안되, 어머니께만은 자네가 처음 발버둥 칠 때 따라올 수밖에 없는 그 가난의 시련을 면하게 해 드리게. 어머니는 오늘 그분께 닥친 불행의 그림자조차 받으실 까닭이 없는 분이고, 섭리는 죄 없는 이가 죄지은 자를 위해 고통받기를 바라지 않으니 말일세. 자네가 아무것도 지니지 않고 엘데 거리를 떠나려 한다는 것을 나는 알고 있네. 어떻게 알았는지 알려고 하지 말게. 나는 안다네. 그것으로 충분하지.\n\n자, 들어 보게, 알베르. 스물네 해 전, 나는 자랑스럽고 기쁜 마음으로 내 고향에 돌아왔네. 내게는 약혼녀가 있었지, 알베르. 내가 너무도 사랑하던 아리따운 처녀였네. 나는 약혼녀에게 줄 150루이를 가지고 갔지. 쉼 없는 노고로 어렵사리 모은 돈이었네. 그 돈은 그녀를 위한 것이었어. 그녀에게 주려고 마련한 것이었지. 바다의 변덕을 잘 알았기에, 나는 그 보물을 마르세유 메이앙 거리에 있던, 내 아버지가 살던 집의 작은 정원에 묻어 두었네. 알베르, 자네 어머니도 그 초라한 집을 잘 아실 걸세. 얼마 전 나는 마르세유를 지나는 길에 그 옛집을 보러 갔다네. 수많은 고통스러운 기억을 되살리는 곳이었지. 그날 저녁 나는 삽을 들고 보물을 숨겨 두었던 정원 구석을 팠네. 쇠 상자는 그대로 거기 있었네. 아무도 손대지 않았더군. 내가 태어나던 날 아버지가 심으신 아름다운 무화과나무 아래에, 그 자리를 그늘로 덮은 그 나무 아래에 말일세. 그러니, 알베르, 한때는 내가 사랑하던 여인의 안락과 평안을 위해 마련했던 이 돈이, 이제 기이하고 고통스러운 사정을 거쳐 같은 목적에 쓰일 수 있게 된 것이라네.\n\n오, 나를 헤아려 주게. 나는 그 가엾은 여인에게 수백만 금을 바칠 수도 있건만, 사랑하는 그녀에게서 떨어져 나오던 그날 이후 내 초라한 지붕 아래 잊혀 있던 검은 빵 한 조각밖에 돌려드릴 수 없는 사람일세. 자네는 너그러운 사람이네, 알베르. 하지만 어쩌면 자존심이나 원한에 눈이 멀어 있을지도 모르지. 만일 자네가 나를 거절한다면, 내가 마땅히 자네에게 베풀 권리가 있는 것을 다른 이에게 청한다면, 나는 이렇게 말하겠네. 자네 아버지가 그 부친을 가난과 절망의 온갖 참상 속에 죽도록 내버려 둔 바로 그 사람의 손에서 자네 어머니의 목숨을 거절하는 것은 너그럽지 못한 처사라고 말일세.\"\n\n알베르는 어머니가 이 편지를 다 읽고 나서 어떤 결정을 내릴지 들으려고 창백하게 굳은 채 서 있었다. 메르세데스는 형언할 수 없는 눈빛으로 하늘을 향해 눈을 들었다.\n\n\"받아들이겠다.\" 어머니가 말했다. \"그분에게는 지참금을 치를 권리가 있어. 그걸 가지고 어느 수도원으로 들어가마!\"\n\n편지를 가슴에 품은 그녀는 아들의 팔을 잡고, 스스로도 예상치 못한 굳센 걸음으로 계단을 내려갔다.\n\n# Chapter 92. The Suicide\n\n그동안 몬테크리스토 역시 에마뉘엘, 막시밀리앙과 함께 시내로 돌아왔다. 돌아오는 길은 유쾌했다. 에마뉘엘은 일이 평화롭게 마무리된 데 대한 기쁨을 감추지 못하고 큰 소리로 즐거움을 늘어놓았다. 모렐은 마차 한쪽 구석에서 처남이 마음껏 떠들도록 내버려 두었다. 그 자신도 그에 못지않은 기쁨을 속으로 느끼고 있었지만, 그것은 오직 얼굴에만 드러날 뿐이었다.\n\n바리에르 뒤 트론에 이르렀을 때 그들은 베르투초와 마주쳤다. 그는 마치 초소를 지키는 보초처럼 꼼짝도 않고 그곳에서 기다리고 있었다. 몬테크리스토가 창밖으로 고개를 내밀고 그와 낮은 목소리로 몇 마디 주고받자, 집사는 사라졌다.\n\n\"백작님,\" 플라스 루아얄 끝에 이르렀을 때 에마뉘엘이 말했다. \"저는 제 집 앞에 내려 주십시오. 제 아내가 저나 백작님 일로 단 한순간이라도 공연한 걱정을 하지 않도록 말입니다.\"\n\n\"우리의 승리를 떠벌리는 것이 우스운 일만 아니라면,\" 모렐이 말했다. \"백작님을 저희 집으로 모시고 싶습니다만. 게다가 백작님께서는 분명 위로해 드려야 할 떨리는 가슴이 따로 있으시겠지요. 그러니 우리 친구분과는 작별하고, 어서 댁으로 가시게 해 드립시다.\"\n\n\"잠깐만,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"내게서 두 길동무를 한꺼번에 빼앗지는 말게. 에마뉘엘, 자네는 그 사랑스러운 부인께 돌아가서 내 진심 어린 인사를 전해 주게. 그리고 모렐, 자네는 나와 함께 샹젤리제까지 가 주게.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그러지요,\" 막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"마침 그쪽에 볼일도 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"아침 식사는 두 분을 기다릴까요?\" 에마뉘엘이 물었다.\n\n\"아니,\" 젊은이가 대답했다. 문이 닫히고, 마차가 다시 나아갔다. \"보십시오, 제가 백작님께 얼마나 큰 행운을 가져다드렸는지!\" 백작과 단둘이 남게 되자 모렐이 말했다. \"그렇게 생각하지 않으십니까?\"\n\n\"그래,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그래서 자네를 곁에 두고 싶었던 걸세.\"\n\n\"정말 기적 같은 일입니다!\" 모렐이 자기 생각을 좇으며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"무엇이 말인가?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다.\n\n\"방금 일어난 일 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그래,\" 백작이 말했다. \"자네 말이 맞아. 기적 같은 일이지.\"\n\n\"알베르는 용감한 사람이니까요.\" 모렐이 다시 말했다.\n\n\"아주 용감하지,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"나는 그가 머리 위에 칼을 매달아 둔 채로 잠드는 것을 본 적이 있네.\"\n\n\"게다가 그가 두 번이나 결투를 치른 것도 알고 있습니다.\" 모렐이 말했다. \"그런 사람이 오늘 아침에 한 행동을 대체 어떻게 받아들여야 합니까?\"\n\n\"모두가 자네의 영향력 덕분이지.\" 몬테크리스토가 미소 지으며 대답했다.\n\n\"알베르가 군인이 아닌 게 다행입니다.\" 모렐이 말했다.\n\n\"왜?\"\n\n\"결투장에서 사과를 하다니요!\" 젊은 대위가 고개를 저으며 말했다.\n\n\"이보게,\" 백작이 부드럽게 말했다. \"여느 사람들처럼 편견에 사로잡히지 말게, 모렐! 인정하게나. 알베르가 용감하다면 비겁한 자일 리는 없네. 그러니 오늘 아침 그렇게 행동한 데에는 분명 무슨 까닭이 있었을 테고, 그의 행동은 오히려 영웅적이라 해야 할 걸세.\"\n\n\"물론입니다, 물론이지요.\" 모렐이 말했다. \"하지만 저는 스페인 사람들처럼 말하겠습니다. '그는 오늘 어제만큼 용감하지 못했다'고요.\"\n\n\"모렐, 나와 함께 아침을 들지 않겠나?\" 백작이 화제를 돌리려고 말했다.\n\n\"아닙니다. 열 시에는 백작님 곁을 떠나야 합니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 약속이 아침 식사 자리였나?\" 백작이 물었다.\n\n모렐이 미소 지으며 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"그래도 어디선가는 아침을 들어야 할 게 아닌가.\"\n\n\"하지만 배가 고프지 않다면요?\" 젊은이가 말했다.\n\n\"오,\" 백작이 말했다. \"식욕을 망치는 것은 내가 알기로 두 가지뿐일세. 하나는 슬픔인데, 자네가 이렇게 유쾌한 모습이라 다행히 그건 아닌 것 같고. 다른 하나는 사랑이지. 오늘 아침 자네가 자네 마음에 대해 들려준 이야기를 떠올리면, 나는 이렇게 믿어도 될 듯한데……\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 백작님.\" 모렐이 명랑하게 대답했다. \"그 점은 부정하지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 내게는 속내를 털어놓지 않을 셈인가, 막시밀리앙?\" 백작이 그 비밀을 얼마나 기꺼이 듣고 싶어 하는지를 드러내는 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"오늘 아침에 제게도 마음이 있다는 걸 보여 드리지 않았습니까, 백작님?\" 몬테크리스토는 대답 대신 젊은이에게 손을 내밀 뿐이었다. \"그렇다면,\" 모렐이 말을 이었다. \"그 마음이 더는 백작님과 함께 뱅센 숲에 있지 않고 다른 곳에 가 있으니, 저는 그것을 찾으러 가야겠습니다.\"\n\n\"가게,\" 백작이 천천히 말했다. \"가게, 친애하는 친구. 다만 약속해 주게. 어떤 장애에 부딪히거든, 내가 이 세상에서 적잖은 힘을 가지고 있다는 것, 내가 사랑하는 이들을 위해 그 힘을 기꺼이 쓴다는 것, 그리고 내가 자네를 사랑한다는 것을 기억하겠다고 말일세, 모렐.\"\n\n\"기억하겠습니다,\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"이기적인 자식들이 도움이 필요할 때 부모를 떠올리듯이요. 백작님의 도움이 필요한 순간이 오면, 그때 백작님을 찾아가겠습니다.\"\n\n\"좋아, 자네 약속을 믿겠네. 그럼 잘 가게.\"\n\n\"안녕히, 다시 만날 때까지요.\"\n\n두 사람은 샹젤리제에 다다랐다. 몬테크리스토가 마차 문을 열자 모렐이 보도로 뛰어내렸고, 베르투초가 계단에서 기다리고 있었다. 모렐은 마리니 거리 아래로 사라졌고, 몬테크리스토는 서둘러 베르투초에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"어떻게 됐나?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"부인이 집을 떠나려 하고 있습니다.\" 집사가 말했다.\n\n\"아들은?\"\n\n\"하인 플로랑탱의 말로는 도련님도 그러실 것 같다고 합니다.\"\n\n\"이리로 오게.\" 몬테크리스토는 베르투초를 서재로 데려가, 우리가 앞서 본 그 편지를 써서 집사에게 건넸다. \"가게,\" 그가 빠르게 말했다. \"하지만 먼저 하이데에게 내가 돌아왔다고 전해 주게.\"\n\n\"여기 있어요.\" 젊은 처녀가 말했다. 그녀는 마차 소리를 듣고 아래층으로 달려 내려온 것으로, 백작이 무사히 돌아온 것을 보고 얼굴이 기쁨으로 환하게 빛났다. 베르투초는 물러갔다. 그토록 애타게 기다려 온 이 만남의 첫 순간, 하이데는 아버지를 다시 찾은 딸의 온갖 환희와, 사랑하는 연인을 다시 본 여인의 온갖 기쁨을 한꺼번에 느꼈다. 비록 겉으로는 덜 드러났을지라도, 몬테크리스토의 기쁨 역시 그에 못지않게 강렬했으리라. 오래 고통받은 가슴에게 기쁨이란 긴 가뭄 끝에 땅에 내리는 이슬과 같다. 가슴도 땅도 그 위에 떨어지는 은혜로운 물기를 빨아들일 뿐, 겉으로는 아무것도 드러나지 않는 법이다.\n\n몬테크리스토는, 오랫동안 감히 믿지 못했던 것을 이제 비로소 생각하기 시작했다. 세상에는 두 사람의 메르세데스가 있으며, 자기도 아직 행복할 수 있을지 모른다는 것을. 행복으로 들뜬 그의 눈이 하이데의 눈물 어린 눈길을 열심히 읽고 있을 때, 별안간 문이 열렸다. 백작은 이맛살을 찌푸렸다.\n\n\"모르세르 씨이십니다!\" 바티스탱이 그 이름만으로 충분히 양해가 된다는 듯 말했다. 과연 백작의 얼굴이 환해졌다.\n\n\"누구 말인가,\" 그가 물었다. \"자작인가, 백작인가?\"\n\n\"백작이십니다.\"\n\n\"오,\" 하이데가 외쳤다. \"아직도 끝나지 않은 건가요?\"\n\n\"끝났는지는 모르겠구나, 사랑하는 아가야.\" 몬테크리스토가 젊은 처녀의 두 손을 잡으며 말했다. \"하지만 네가 더는 두려워할 것이 없다는 것만은 분명히 알고 있단다.\"\n\n\"하지만 저 비열한 자가……\"\n\n\"저 사람은 나를 해칠 수 없어, 하이데.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"두려워할 만한 자는 그의 아들 하나뿐이었지.\"\n\n\"제가 얼마나 괴로웠는지는,\" 젊은 처녀가 말했다. \"당신은 결코 모르실 거예요, 나리.\"\n\n몬테크리스토가 미소 지었다. \"내 아버지의 무덤에 걸고,\" 그가 젊은 처녀의 머리 위로 손을 뻗으며 말했다. \"맹세하마, 하이데. 만일 어떤 불행이 닥친다 해도, 그것은 내게 닥치는 일이 아닐 것이다.\"\n\n\"나리의 말씀을, 마치 하느님이 제게 들려주신 말씀인 양 한 치의 의심 없이 믿어요.\" 젊은 처녀가 그에게 이마를 내밀며 말했다. 몬테크리스토는 그 순결하고 아름다운 이마에 입을 맞추었고, 그 입맞춤은 두 사람의 가슴을 동시에 뛰게 했다. 하나는 격렬하게, 다른 하나는 은밀하게.\n\n\"오,\" 백작이 중얼거렸다. \"그렇다면 내게도 다시 사랑할 자격이 허락되는 것일까? 모르세르 씨를 응접실로 모시게.\" 그가 바티스탱에게 말하고는, 아름다운 그리스 처녀를 데리고 안쪽 계단으로 향했다.\n\n몬테크리스토에게는 예견된 일이었으나 우리 독자들에게는 뜻밖일 이 방문에 대해 설명을 해 두어야겠다. 앞서 말했듯이 메르세데스가 알베르처럼 자신의 재산 목록을 작성하고, 보석을 정리하고, 서랍을 닫고, 열쇠를 챙겨 모든 것을 완벽한 순서로 정돈해 두려는 동안, 그녀는 복도로 빛을 들이는 유리문에 어른거리는 창백하고 음산한 얼굴 하나를 알아채지 못했다. 그 문을 통해서는 모든 것을 보고 들을 수 있었다. 들키지도 보이지도 않은 채 그렇게 들여다보고 있던 그 사내는, 아마도 모르세르 부인의 거처에서 일어나는 모든 일을 듣고 보았을 것이다. 그 유리문에서 창백한 얼굴의 사내는 백작의 침실로 옮겨 가, 경련하는 손으로 안뜰이 내려다보이는 창의 커튼을 들어 올렸다. 그는 그곳에 십 분 동안 꼼짝도 않고 말도 없이 서서, 제 심장이 뛰는 소리에 귀를 기울였다. 그에게 그 십 분은 무척이나 길었다. 바로 그때 백작과의 만남을 마치고 돌아오던 알베르가, 커튼 뒤에서 자신이 오기를 살피고 있는 아버지를 알아보고는 고개를 돌려 비켜 갔다. 백작의 눈이 휘둥그레졌다. 알베르가 백작을 끔찍하게 모욕했다는 것, 그리고 세상 어느 나라에서든 그런 모욕은 목숨을 건 결투로 이어진다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다. 알베르가 무사히 돌아왔으니, 결국 백작은 복수를 한 셈이었다.\n\n형언할 수 없는 기쁨의 빛이 그 비참한 얼굴을 환하게 비추었다. 마치 구름 뒤로 사라지기 직전 마지막으로 비치는 햇살 같았으나, 그 구름은 포근한 침상이 아니라 무덤의 형상을 띠고 있었다. 하지만 앞서 말했듯, 그는 아들이 승리의 소식을 들고 자기 방으로 찾아오기를 헛되이 기다렸다. 아버지의 명예를 갚으러 가기 전에 아들이 자신을 보러 오지 않은 것은 쉽게 이해할 수 있었다. 그러나 그 일을 마친 뒤에도 어찌하여 아들은 와서 제 품에 안기지 않는단 말인가?\n\n바로 그때, 백작은 알베르를 볼 수 없게 되자 하인을 불렀다. 그 하인은 자신에게 아무것도 숨기지 못하도록 되어 있다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다. 십 분 뒤, 모르세르 장군이 군용 깃을 단 검은 외투에 검은 바지, 검은 장갑 차림으로 계단에 모습을 드러냈다. 그는 미리 지시를 내려 둔 모양이었다. 그가 마지막 계단에 이르자 마차고에서 마차가 채비를 갖춘 채 나왔다. 하인이 마차 안에 군용 망토를 던져 넣었는데, 그 안에는 검 두 자루가 싸여 있었다. 그러고는 문을 닫고 마부 옆자리에 올라탔다. 마부가 분부를 들으려 몸을 숙였다.\n\n\"샹젤리제로 가게.\" 장군이 말했다. \"몬테크리스토 백작 댁으로. 서둘러!\"\n\n말들이 채찍 아래 날뛰며 내달렸고, 오 분 만에 백작의 집 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 모르세르 씨가 직접 문을 열었고, 마차가 멀어져 가는 동안 그는 진입로를 따라 올라가 초인종을 울리고, 하인과 함께 열린 문으로 들어섰다.\n\n잠시 후, 바티스탱이 몬테크리스토에게 모르세르 백작이 왔음을 알렸고, 백작은 하이데를 곁에서 물러나게 하고는 모르세르를 응접실로 모시라 일렀다. 장군은 세 번째로 방 안을 거닐다가 몸을 돌리는 순간, 문가에 선 몬테크리스토를 알아보았다.\n\n\"아, 모르세르 씨로군요.\" 몬테크리스토가 차분하게 말했다. \"내가 잘못 들었나 했습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇소, 나요.\" 백작이 말했다. 입술이 무섭게 뒤틀려 말을 제대로 잇지 못했다.\n\n\"이 이른 시각에 모르세르 씨를 뵙는 기쁨을 어떤 연유로 누리게 되었는지 알 수 있겠습니까?\"\n\n\"오늘 아침 내 아들과 만나지 않았소?\" 장군이 물었다.\n\n\"만났습니다.\" 백작이 대답했다.\n\n\"그리고 내 아들이 당신과 싸워 당신을 죽이려 한 데에는 충분한 이유가 있었다는 것도 알고 있소.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 장군. 아드님에게는 아주 충분한 이유가 있었지요. 하지만 보시다시피, 그런 이유에도 불구하고 그는 나를 죽이지 못했고, 싸우려 들지조차 않았습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 내 아들은 당신을 제 아버지의 불명예의 원인으로, 내 집안에 닥친 그 끔찍한 파멸의 원인으로 여겼소.\"\n\n\"사실입니다, 장군.\" 몬테크리스토가 그 소름 끼치는 평정을 잃지 않고 말했다. \"부차적인 원인이긴 합니다만, 근본적인 원인은 아니지요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 당신이 무슨 사과나 해명이라도 한 모양이군.\"\n\n\"나는 아무 해명도 하지 않았습니다. 오히려 내게 사과한 쪽은 그였습니다.\"\n\n\"그런데 그런 행동을 무엇 탓으로 돌리겠소?\"\n\n\"아마도 나보다 더 죄 많은 자가 있다는 확신 때문이겠지요.\"\n\n\"그게 누구란 말이오?\"\n\n\"그의 아버지지요.\"\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지.\" 백작이 창백해지며 말했다. \"하지만 당신도 알 거요. 죄 있는 자는 자기 죄가 드러나는 걸 좋아하지 않는 법이라는 걸.\"\n\n\"알고 있습니다. 그래서 이런 결과를 예상하고 있었지요.\"\n\n\"내 아들이 비겁자가 되리라 예상했단 말이오?\" 백작이 외쳤다.\n\n\"알베르 드 모르세르 씨는 비겁자가 아닙니다!\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"손에 칼을 쥐고, 그 칼이 닿을 거리에 철천지원수를 두고도 싸우지 않는 자는 비겁자요! 그자가 어디 있소, 내가 직접 그 말을 해 줄 수 있게?\"\n\n\"장군,\" 몬테크리스토가 차갑게 대답했다. \"나는 당신이 이곳에 와서 자질구레한 집안일을 늘어놓으리라고는 예상치 못했습니다. 가서 알베르 씨에게 그 말을 하시지요. 그가 어떻게 대답해야 할지는 알 겁니다.\"\n\n\"오, 아니, 아니오.\" 장군이 희미하게 웃으며 말했다. \"그러려고 온 게 아니오. 당신 말이 맞소. 나는 당신에게, 나 또한 당신을 적으로 여긴다는 말을 하러 왔소. 본능적으로 당신을 증오한다는 말을 하러 왔단 말이오. 마치 내가 늘 당신을 알고 있었던 것처럼, 늘 당신을 증오해 온 것처럼 말이오. 요컨대, 요즘 젊은 것들이 싸우려 들지 않으니, 우리가 직접 싸우는 수밖에 남지 않은 거요. 그렇게 생각지 않소?\"\n\n\"물론입니다. 그리고 내가 이런 결과를 예견했다고 말했을 때, 바로 당신의 이 방문을 두고 한 말이었지요.\"\n\n\"잘됐군. 준비됐소?\"\n\"그렇습니다, 장군.\"\n\n\"우리 둘 중 하나가 죽을 때까지 싸우는 거요.\" 분노로 이를 악문 장군이 말했다.\n\n\"우리 둘 중 하나가 죽을 때까지.\" 몬테크리스토가 고개를 살짝 끄덕이며 되뇌었다.\n\n\"그럼 갑시다. 증인은 필요 없소.\"\n\n\"옳은 말씀입니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"필요 없지요. 우리는 서로를 너무도 잘 아니까요!\"\n\n\"천만에.\" 백작이 말했다. \"우리는 서로를 거의 모르오.\"\n\n\"그렇습니까?\" 몬테크리스토가 변함없이 굴복을 모르는 냉정함으로 말했다. \"어디 봅시다. 당신은 워털루 전투 전날 밤 탈영한 병사 페르낭이 아니오? 스페인에서 프랑스군의 길잡이와 첩자 노릇을 한 페르낭 중위가 아니오? 자신의 은인 알리를 배신하고, 팔아넘기고, 살해한 페르낭 대위가 아니오? 그리고 그 모든 페르낭들이 하나로 뭉쳐, 프랑스 중장이자 모르세르 백작, 프랑스 상원의원이 된 것 아니오?\"\n\n\"오,\" 장군이 마치 시뻘건 인두로 낙인이 찍힌 것처럼 외쳤다. \"비열한 놈, 나를 죽이려는 마당에 내 수치를 들먹이다니! 아니, 나는 당신이 내게 낯선 자라고 말한 적 없소. 잘 알고 있어, 악마 같은 놈. 네놈이 과거의 어둠 속을 파고들어, 무슨 횃불의 빛으로인지는 모르나, 내 인생의 모든 페이지를 읽어 냈다는 것을. 하지만 어쩌면 나는 내 수치 속에서도, 네놈이 그 거창한 껍데기를 뒤집어쓴 것보다 더 떳떳할지도 모르지. 아니, 아니, 네놈이 나를 안다는 건 인정한다. 하지만 나는 네놈을, 금과 보석을 휘감은 협잡꾼으로밖에 알지 못해. 파리에서는 몬테크리스토 백작, 이탈리아에서는 뱃사람 신드바드, 몰타에서는 또 무슨 이름이었는지 잊었다만. 내가 알고 싶은 건 네놈의 백 가지 이름 가운데 진짜 이름이다. 우리가 싸우러 마주섰을 때, 내 칼을 네놈의 심장에 꽂는 그 순간에 그 이름을 부를 수 있도록 말이야.\"\n\n몬테크리스토 백작은 무섭게 창백해졌다. 그의 눈은 모든 것을 집어삼킬 듯한 불길로 타오르는 듯했다. 그는 침실 옆 화장실로 뛰어들어, 눈 깜짝할 사이에 넥타이와 상의와 조끼를 벗어 던지고는 뱃사람의 웃옷과 모자를 걸쳤다. 그 모자 아래로 긴 검은 머리카락이 흘러내렸다. 그렇게 무시무시하고 가차 없는 모습으로 그는 가슴 위에 팔짱을 낀 채 장군을 향해 다가갔다. 장군은 그가 왜 사라졌었는지 영문을 알 수 없었으나, 다시 나타난 그를 보는 순간 이가 딱딱 부딪치고 다리가 풀려 뒷걸음질 쳤다. 그러다 꽉 쥔 손을 받칠 탁자를 찾고서야 비로소 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"페르낭,\" 그가 외쳤다. \"내 백 가지 이름 가운데 단 하나만 일러 주면 너를 무너뜨리기에 충분하지! 하지만 이젠 짐작이 가지 않나? 아니, 차라리 기억이 나는 게지? 온갖 슬픔과 고문에도 불구하고, 나는 오늘 복수의 기쁨으로 다시 젊어진 얼굴을 네게 보이고 있으니까. 네가 내 약혼녀 메르세데스와 혼인한 뒤로 꿈속에서 수없이 보았을 그 얼굴 말이다!\"\n\n장군은 머리를 뒤로 젖히고 두 손을 뻗은 채 시선을 못 박고서, 이 끔찍한 환영을 말없이 바라보았다. 그러더니 몸을 지탱할 벽을 더듬어, 그 벽에 바짝 붙어 미끄러지듯 문가에 이르렀고, 단 한마디 구슬프고 비통하고 애끓는 비명을 토하며 뒷걸음으로 문을 빠져나갔다.\n\n\"에드몽 당테스!\"\n\n그러고는 어떤 인간의 소리와도 같지 않은 신음을 흘리며 문까지 몸을 끌고 가, 안뜰을 비틀비틀 가로지르다 하인의 품에 쓰러지며 거의 알아들을 수 없는 목소리로 말했다. \"집으로, 집으로.\"\n\n신선한 바깥 공기와, 하인들 앞에서 추한 꼴을 드러냈다는 수치심이 그의 정신을 어느 정도 되돌려 놓았다. 그러나 길은 짧았고, 집에 가까워질수록 그의 온갖 비참함이 되살아났다. 그는 집에서 조금 떨어진 곳에 마차를 세우고 내렸다. 대문은 활짝 열려 있었고, 안뜰 한가운데에 삯마차 한 대가 서 있었다. 그토록 고귀한 저택 앞에는 어울리지 않는 낯선 광경이었다. 백작은 두려움에 찬 눈으로 그것을 바라보았으나, 감히 그 까닭을 물어볼 엄두도 내지 못한 채 자신의 거처로 달려갔다.\n\n두 사람이 계단을 내려오고 있었다. 그는 가까스로 벽감 안으로 몸을 숨겨 그들을 피했다. 메르세데스가 아들의 팔에 기대어 집을 떠나는 길이었다. 그들은 다마스크 커튼 뒤에 숨은 그 가련한 사내의 바로 곁을 스쳐 지났다. 그는 메르세데스의 옷자락이 자신을 스치는 것을, 그리고 다음과 같은 말을 내뱉는 아들의 따뜻한 숨결을 느낄 듯했다.\n\n\"용기를 내세요, 어머니! 가요, 이젠 우리 집이 아니에요!\"\n\n그 말은 점점 잦아들었고, 발소리는 멀리서 사라졌다. 장군은 커튼에 매달린 채 몸을 일으켰다. 그는 아내와 아들에게 동시에 버림받은 한 아버지의 가슴에서 터져 나올 수 있는 가장 끔찍한 흐느낌을 토해 냈다. 이윽고 그는 삯마차의 쇠 발판이 덜컹거리는 소리를, 뒤이어 마부의 목소리를, 그리고 그 묵직한 마차가 굴러가며 유리창을 뒤흔드는 소리를 들었다. 그는 이 세상에서 사랑했던 모든 것을 다시 한번 보려고 침실로 달려갔다. 그러나 삯마차는 이미 굴러가고 있었고, 메르세데스도 아들도 이 집을, 또 버림받은 아버지이자 남편을 마지막으로 보려고 창밖으로 고개를 내미는 일은 없었다.\n\n그리고 바로 그 마차의 바퀴가 대문을 지나는 순간, 총성이 울렸고, 폭발로 깨진 창유리 한 장 사이로 짙은 연기가 새어 나왔다.\n93장 발랑틴\n\n# Chapter 93. Valentine\n\n모렐이 어디로 약속을 잡았는지는 쉽게 짐작할 수 있다. 몬테크리스토를 떠난 그는 천천히 빌포르의 집을 향해 걸었다. 천천히라고 말한 것은, 오백 걸음을 가는 데 모렐에게 삼십 분 넘는 여유가 있었기 때문이다. 그래도 그는 서둘러 몬테크리스토에게 작별을 고했으니, 혼자서 생각에 잠기고 싶었던 것이다. 그는 자신의 시간을 잘 알고 있었다. 발랑틴이 누아르티에에게 아침 식사를 차려 드리는 시각이었고, 그 경건한 의무를 다하는 동안에는 방해받지 않으리라는 것이 분명했다. 누아르티에와 발랑틴은 그에게 일주일에 두 번 찾아오는 것을 허락해 주었고, 그는 지금 그 허락을 누리고 있는 참이었다.\n\n그가 도착했다. 발랑틴이 그를 기다리고 있었다. 불안하고 거의 정신이 나간 듯한 그녀가 그의 손을 붙잡고 할아버지에게로 이끌었다. 광기에 가까운 이 불안은 모르세르의 사건이 세상에 일으킨 소문에서 비롯된 것이었으니, 오페라 극장에서의 일은 널리 알려져 있었다. 빌포르의 집에서는 그 일로 결투가 벌어지리라는 것을 누구도 의심치 않았다. 발랑틴은 여자의 직감으로 모렐이 몬테크리스토의 입회인이 되리라 짐작했고, 그 젊은이의 익히 알려진 용기와 백작을 향한 깊은 애정으로 미루어, 그가 자신에게 주어진 소극적인 역할에 만족하지 않으리라 두려워했다. 그러니 자세한 사정을 얼마나 애타게 묻고, 또 그것을 얼마나 듣고 받아들였을지는 쉽게 짐작할 수 있다. 그 일이 뜻밖에도 행복하게 마무리되었다는 것을 알았을 때, 모렐은 사랑하는 여인의 눈 속에서 형언할 수 없는 기쁨을 읽을 수 있었다.\n\n\"자,\" 발랑틴이 모렐에게 할아버지 곁에 앉으라고 손짓하며 말했다. 그녀 자신은 할아버지의 발 받침대에 자리를 잡았다. \"자, 이제 우리 일을 이야기해 봐요. 막시밀리앙, 할아버지께서 한때 이 집을 떠나 빌포르 씨의 집에서 멀리 떨어진 거처를 얻을 생각을 하셨던 거 알죠.\"\n\n\"네,\" 막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"그 계획을 기억합니다. 저도 크게 찬성했지요.\"\n\n\"음,\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"다시 찬성해 주셔도 되겠어요. 할아버지께서 또 그 생각을 하고 계시거든요.\"\n\n\"잘됐군요.\" 막시밀리앙이 말했다.\n\n\"그런데,\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"할아버지께서 이 집을 떠나려 하시는 이유가 뭔지 아세요?\" 누아르티에가 침묵을 명하려고 발랑틴을 바라보았지만, 그녀는 알아차리지 못했다. 그녀의 표정도, 눈도, 미소도 온통 모렐을 향해 있었다.\n\n\"아, 누아르티에 씨의 이유가 무엇이든,\" 모렐이 대답했다. \"저는 그것이 분명 타당한 이유라고 기꺼이 믿겠습니다.\"\n\n\"아주 훌륭한 이유예요.\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"포부르생토노레의 공기가 제게 좋지 않다고 하시거든요.\"\n\n\"정말입니까?\" 모렐이 말했다. \"그 점에서는 누아르티에 씨가 옳으실지도 모르겠군요. 지난 보름 동안 당신은 건강이 좋아 보이지 않았으니까요.\"\n\n\"그리 좋지 않아요.\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"그래서 할아버지께서 제 주치의가 되셨답니다. 저는 할아버지를 더없이 믿어요. 모르시는 게 없으시니까요.\"\n\n\"그럼 정말로 어디가 아픈 겁니까?\" 모렐이 다급히 물었다.\n\n\"아, 아프다고까지 할 건 아니에요. 그냥 온몸이 어쩐지 불편할 뿐이에요. 입맛도 없고, 위가 무언가에 적응하려고 애쓰는 것 같은 느낌이 들어요.\" 누아르티에는 발랑틴이 하는 말을 한마디도 놓치지 않았다.\n\n\"그럼 그 묘한 증세에 어떤 치료를 받고 있습니까?\"\n\n\"아주 간단한 거예요.\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"매일 아침 할아버지께 드리려고 만든 물약을 한 숟가락씩 삼키고 있어요. 한 숟가락이라고 했지만, 처음엔 한 숟가락이었는데 지금은 네 숟가락이에요. 할아버지께서는 그게 만병통치약이라고 하세요.\" 발랑틴이 미소 지었지만, 그녀가 고통스러워하는 것은 분명했다.\n\n막시밀리앙은 헌신적인 마음으로 말없이 그녀를 바라보았다. 그녀는 무척 아름다웠지만, 평소의 창백함이 더 짙어져 있었다. 두 눈은 그 어느 때보다 빛났고, 평소에는 진주조개처럼 희던 두 손이 이제는 밀랍에 더 가까웠으니, 거기에 세월이 누르스름한 빛을 더하고 있었다.\n\n젊은이는 발랑틴에게서 시선을 돌려 누아르티에를 바라보았다. 노인은 애정에 사로잡힌 손녀를 기이하고도 깊은 관심으로 지켜보고 있었다. 그 역시 모렐처럼, 보통 사람의 눈에는 거의 드러나지 않아 할아버지와 연인 말고는 아무도 알아채지 못하는 그 내면의 고통의 흔적을 좇고 있었다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 모렐이 말했다. \"당신이 지금 네 숟가락씩 먹는다는 그 물약은 누아르티에 씨를 위해 만든 것이 아니었던가요?\"\n\n\"무척 쓰다는 건 알아요.\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"어찌나 쓴지, 그 뒤에 마시는 것은 죄다 똑같은 맛이 나는 것 같아요.\" 누아르티에가 캐묻는 눈빛으로 손녀를 바라보았다. \"네, 할아버지.\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"그래요. 방금도, 할아버지께 내려오기 전에 설탕물을 한 잔 마셨는데, 너무 써서 절반을 남겼어요.\" 누아르티에의 얼굴이 창백해지더니, 말을 하고 싶다는 신호를 보냈다.\n\n발랑틴이 사전을 가지러 일어섰다. 누아르티에가 역력한 고통을 띠고 그녀를 지켜보았다. 사실 벌써 피가 처녀의 머리로 솟구쳐, 두 뺨이 붉어지고 있었다.\n\n\"어머,\" 그녀가 명랑함을 조금도 잃지 않은 채 외쳤다. \"이상해라! 앞이 안 보여요! 햇빛이 눈에 들어왔나?\" 그러고는 창문에 몸을 기댔다.\n\n\"햇빛은 비치지 않습니다.\" 모렐이 말했다. 그는 발랑틴의 몸 상태보다 누아르티에의 표정에 더 놀라 있었다. 그가 그녀에게 달려갔다. 처녀가 미소 지었다.\n\n\"기운 내세요.\" 그녀가 누아르티에에게 말했다. \"놀라지 마세요, 막시밀리앙. 아무것도 아니에요. 벌써 다 지나갔어요. 그런데 들어 봐요! 안뜰에서 마차 소리가 들리지 않아요?\" 그녀가 누아르티에의 방문을 열고 복도의 창문으로 달려갔다가 황급히 돌아왔다. \"맞아요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당글라르 부인과 따님이 우리를 찾아왔어요. 안녕히—저는 가 봐야겠어요. 안 그러면 저를 부르러 사람을 보낼 테니까요. 아니, 다시 볼 때까지 안녕이라고 해 두죠. 할아버지 곁에 계세요, 막시밀리앙. 그분들을 붙잡아 두지 않겠다고 약속할게요.\"\n\n모렐은 그녀가 방을 나서는 모습을 지켜보았다. 빌포르 부인의 거처와 그녀의 거처로 동시에 이어지는 작은 계단을 그녀가 올라가는 소리가 들렸다. 그녀가 가 버리자 누아르티에가 모렐에게 사전을 가져오라고 신호했다. 모렐이 그대로 따랐다. 발랑틴의 가르침 덕분에 그는 노인의 뜻을 빨리 알아듣는 법을 익혀 두었다. 그러나 그 일에 익숙해졌다고는 해도, 알파벳 글자 대부분을 거듭 짚어 가며 사전에서 단어를 하나하나 찾아야 했다. 그래서 노인의 생각이 다음과 같은 말로 옮겨지기까지 십 분이 걸렸다.\n\n\"발랑틴의 방에서 물잔과 물병을 가져오게.\"\n\n모렐은 곧바로 종을 울려 바루아의 자리를 대신하게 된 하인을 불렀고, 누아르티에의 이름으로 그렇게 분부했다. 하인이 곧 돌아왔다. 물병도 잔도 완전히 비어 있었다. 누아르티에가 말을 하고 싶다는 신호를 보냈다.\n\n\"잔과 물병이 왜 비어 있나?\" 그가 물었다. \"발랑틴은 잔의 절반만 마셨다고 했는데.\"\n\n이 새로운 질문을 옮기는 데 다시 오 분이 걸렸다.\n\n\"저는 모르겠습니다.\" 하인이 말했다. \"하지만 하녀가 발랑틴 아가씨 방에 있습니다. 어쩌면 그 여자가 비웠는지도 모르지요.\"\n\n\"그 여자한테 물어보게.\" 모렐이 말했다. 이번에는 누아르티에의 생각을 그 눈빛으로 알아챈 것이었다. 하인이 나갔다가 거의 곧바로 돌아왔다. \"발랑틴 아가씨께서 빌포르 부인께 가시느라 그 방을 지나가셨답니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"지나가시다가 목이 마르셔서 잔에 남은 것을 드셨고요. 물병으로 말하자면, 에두아르 도련님이 오리에게 연못을 만들어 준다고 비워 버리셨습니다.\"\n\n누아르티에는, 모든 것을 단번에 거는 노름꾼이 그러하듯, 두 눈을 들어 하늘을 우러렀다. 그 순간부터 노인의 시선은 문에 고정된 채 떨어지지 않았다.\n\n발랑틴이 본 것은 정말로 당글라르 부인과 그 딸이었다. 두 사람은 빌포르 부인의 방으로 안내되었으니, 부인이 그곳에서 손님을 맞겠다고 했기 때문이다. 그래서 발랑틴은 부인의 방을 가로질러 갔던 것인데, 그 방은 발랑틴의 방과 같은 층에 있었고 에두아르의 방 하나로만 갈라져 있었다. 두 부인은 격식을 갖춘 통보에 앞서기 마련인 일종의 의례적인 딱딱함을 띠고 응접실로 들어섰다. 사교계 사람들 사이에서는 태도가 전염되는 법이다. 빌포르 부인도 그에 못지않은 엄숙함으로 그들을 맞았다. 마침 발랑틴이 들어섰고, 격식은 다시 차려졌다.\n\n\"이보세요, 부인,\" 두 젊은 처녀가 악수를 나누는 동안 남작 부인이 말했다. \"외제니와 제가 제일 먼저 알려 드리려고 왔답니다. 제 딸이 곧 카발칸티 공작과 혼인을 한답니다.\" 당글라르는 공작이라는 칭호를 계속 내세우고 있었다. 이 인기 있는 은행가는 그것이 백작보다 더 잘 먹힌다는 것을 알았던 것이다.\n\n\"진심으로 축하의 말씀을 드리게 해 주세요.\" 빌포르 부인이 대답했다. \"카발칸티 공작은 보기 드문 자질을 갖춘 청년 같더군요.\"\n\n\"들어 보세요,\" 남작 부인이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"벗으로서 말씀드리자면, 그 공작은 아직 장차 될 모습을 다 보여 주지는 못했어요. 프랑스 사람이 첫눈에 이탈리아나 독일 귀족을 알아보게 하는, 그런 외국풍의 분위기가 조금 있긴 하죠. 게다가 마음씨가 무척 너그럽고, 재치도 아주 날카로운 청년이에요. 어울림으로 말하자면, 당글라르 씨가 그의 재산이 그야말로 '장엄하다'고 장담하시던걸요—그게 그이 표현이에요.\"\n\n\"그리고,\" 외제니가 빌포르 부인의 화첩을 넘기며 말했다. \"어머니가 그 청년을 무척 마음에 들어 하신다는 것도 덧붙이셔야죠.\"\n\n\"그럼,\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"아가씨도 그 마음에 같이하는지는 굳이 물을 필요가 없겠군요.\"\n\n\"저요?\" 외제니가 여느 때처럼 솔직하게 대답했다. \"오, 조금도 그렇지 않아요, 부인! 제 소망은 집안일이나 어느 남자의 변덕에 매여 사는 게 아니라, 예술가가 되는 것이었어요. 그래서 마음도, 몸도, 생각도 자유로운 사람이 되고 싶었지요.\"\n\n외제니가 어찌나 단호한 어조로 이 말을 했던지, 발랑틴의 두 뺨에 핏기가 올랐다. 소심한 처녀로서는 여자다운 소심함이라고는 조금도 없어 보이는 그 강인한 천성을 이해할 수 없었다.\n\n\"어쨌든,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"원하든 원치 않든 저는 결혼을 해야 할 처지니까, 알베르 드 모르세르 씨와의 약혼에서 벗어나게 해 주신 섭리에 감사해야겠지요. 안 그랬다면 저는 오늘 명예를 잃은 남자의 아내가 되어 있었을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그건 사실이에요.\" 남작 부인이 말했다. 그것은 상류층 부인들에게서 이따금 마주치는, 평민과의 교류로도 결코 완전히 씻어 낼 수 없는 그 묘한 천진함이었다. \"모르세르 집안이 망설이지만 않았다면 제 딸이 알베르 씨와 혼인했으리라는 건 정말 사실이에요. 장군은 그 일에 잔뜩 기대를 걸었지요. 심지어 당글라르 씨를 압박하러 직접 찾아오기까지 했답니다. 가까스로 화를 면한 셈이에요.\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 발랑틴이 소심하게 말했다. \"아버지의 수치가 모조리 아들에게 되돌아가는 걸까요? 알베르 씨는 장군에게 씌워진 반역 혐의와는 전혀 무관해 보이는데요.\"\n\n\"미안하지만,\" 그 가차 없는 처녀가 말했다. \"알베르 씨도 자기 몫을 주장하고, 또 충분히 그럴 만해요. 어제 오페라 극장에서 몬테크리스토 씨에게 결투를 신청해 놓고, 오늘은 결투장에서 사과를 했다지 뭐예요.\"\n\n\"그럴 리가요.\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"아, 이보세요,\" 당글라르 부인이 앞서 보았던 그 천진함 그대로 말했다. \"사실이에요. 그 해명 자리에 있었던 드브레 씨한테서 들었는걸요.\"\n\n발랑틴도 진실을 알고 있었지만, 대답하지 않았다. 단 한 마디가, 모렐이 누아르티에 씨의 방에서 자신을 기다리고 있다는 것을 일깨운 것이다. 일종의 내면의 사색에 깊이 빠져, 발랑틴은 한순간 대화에 끼어들기를 멈추었다. 사실 지난 몇 분 동안 오간 말을 그대로 옮겨 보라 하면 도저히 할 수 없었을 것이다. 그때 갑자기 당글라르 부인의 손이 그녀의 팔을 누르며 무기력에서 그녀를 깨웠다.\n\n\"무슨 일이세요?\" 그녀가 마치 감전이라도 된 듯 당글라르 부인의 손길에 흠칫 놀라며 말했다.\n\n\"다름이 아니라, 발랑틴 양,\" 남작 부인이 말했다. \"아가씨가 분명 어디가 아픈 것 같아서요.\"\n\n\"제가요?\" 처녀가 화끈거리는 이마에 손을 갖다 대며 말했다.\n\n\"그래요, 저 거울에 자기 모습을 좀 비춰 봐요. 일 분 사이에 서너 번이나 창백해졌다 붉어졌다 하던걸요.\"\n\n\"정말이에요.\" 외제니가 외쳤다. \"얼굴이 아주 창백해요!\"\n\n\"아, 놀라지 마세요. 며칠째 이런걸요.\" 천진한 처녀였지만, 그녀는 이것이 자리를 뜰 기회임을 알았다. 게다가 빌포르 부인이 그녀를 거들어 주었다.\n\n\"물러가요, 발랑틴.\" 부인이 말했다. \"정말로 어디가 아픈 모양이니, 이분들도 양해해 주실 거예요. 맑은 물을 한 잔 마셔요. 그러면 좀 나아질 거예요.\"\n\n발랑틴은 외제니에게 입을 맞추고, 이미 작별 인사를 하려고 일어선 당글라르 부인에게 목례한 뒤 방을 나갔다.\n\n\"가엾은 아이.\" 발랑틴이 나가자 빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"저 애 때문에 마음이 무척 놓이지 않네요. 무슨 중병이라도 걸린 거라면 저로서는 놀랄 일도 아닐 거예요.\"\n\n한편 발랑틴은 스스로도 잘 알 수 없는 일종의 흥분에 사로잡힌 채, 에두아르가 무슨 장난을 쳐 둔 것도 알아채지 못하고 그 아이의 방을 가로질렀다. 그러고는 자기 방을 지나 작은 계단에 이르렀다.\n\n그녀가 계단 끝에서 세 칸을 남겨 두었을 때였다. 이미 모렐의 목소리가 들리던 그때, 갑자기 두 눈에 구름이 끼었다. 뻣뻣해진 발이 디딤판을 헛디뎠고, 두 손은 난간을 붙들 힘이 없었다. 벽에 부딪치며 그녀는 완전히 중심을 잃고 바닥으로 고꾸라졌다. 모렐이 문으로 뛰어들어 문을 열었다. 발랑틴이 계단 아래에 뻗어 있었다. 그는 번개처럼 빠르게 그녀를 두 팔로 안아 올려 의자에 앉혔다. 발랑틴이 눈을 떴다.\n\n\"아, 내가 정말 칠칠치 못하지.\" 그녀가 열에 들뜬 듯 빠르게 말했다. \"길을 못 찾겠어요. 층계참 앞에 세 칸이 더 남았다는 걸 깜빡했어요.\"\n\n\"혹시 다친 건 아닙니까.\" 모렐이 말했다. \"제가 뭘 해 드리면 좋을까요, 발랑틴?\"\n\n발랑틴이 주위를 둘러보았다. 누아르티에의 눈에 더없이 깊은 공포가 어려 있는 것이 보였다.\n\n\"걱정 마세요, 할아버지.\" 그녀가 애써 미소 지으며 말했다. \"아무것도 아니에요—아무것도 아니에요. 그냥 어지러웠을 뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"또 어지럼증이로군요.\" 모렐이 두 손을 맞잡으며 말했다. \"오, 부디 그걸 가벼이 여기지 마세요, 발랑틴. 제발 부탁입니다.\"\n\n\"아니에요,\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"아니에요, 다 지나갔다니까요. 아무것도 아니었어요. 자, 소식을 하나 들려 드릴게요. 외제니가 일주일 뒤에 결혼해요. 사흘 뒤에는 성대한 잔치, 약혼 축하연이 열린대요. 우리 모두 초대받았어요. 아버지와 빌포르 부인, 그리고 저까지—적어도 저는 그렇게 들었어요.\"\n\n\"우리가 이런 일들을 생각하게 될 차례는 언제일까요? 오, 발랑틴, 할아버지께 그토록 큰 영향을 미치는 당신이니, 부디 할아버지께서 '곧'이라고 대답하시도록 애써 봐요.\"\n\n\"그럼 당신은,\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"할아버지의 굼뜬 마음을 부추기고 그 기억을 일깨우는 일을 제게 맡기는 거예요?\"\n\n\"네,\" 모렐이 외쳤다. \"서둘러 줘요. 당신이 내 사람이 되기 전까지는, 발랑틴, 나는 언제나 당신을 잃을지도 모른다고 생각하게 될 테니까요.\"\n\n\"오,\" 발랑틴이 발작하듯 몸을 떨며 대답했다. \"오, 정말이지, 막시밀리앙, 당신은 장교치고는 너무 겁이 많아요. 두려움이라곤 모른다는 군인이면서요. 하하하!\"\n\n그녀가 억지스럽고 서글픈 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 두 팔이 뻣뻣해지며 뒤틀렸고, 고개가 의자 등받이로 떨어졌으며, 그녀는 꼼짝도 하지 않았다. 누아르티에의 입술에서 막혔던 공포의 외침이 그 두 눈에서 터져 나오는 듯했다. 모렐은 그것을 알아챘다. 그는 사람을 불러야 한다는 것을 깨달았다. 젊은이는 종을 거칠게 울렸다. 발랑틴 아가씨의 방에 있던 하녀와 바루아를 대신하게 된 하인이 동시에 달려 들어왔다. 발랑틴이 어찌나 창백하고, 차갑고, 생기가 없었던지, 두 사람은 자기들에게 하는 말을 듣지도 않고 그 집 안에 감도는 공포에 사로잡혀, 살려 달라고 외치며 복도로 뛰쳐나갔다. 마침 당글라르 부인과 외제니가 나가려던 참이었다. 그들은 소동이 일어난 까닭을 들었다.\n\n\"내가 그랬잖아요!\" 빌포르 부인이 외쳤다. \"가엾은 아이!\"\n\n# 제92장 자살\n\n바로 그 순간 빌포르의 목소리가 서재에서 들려왔다. \"무슨 일이냐?\"\n\n모렐은 어느새 평정을 되찾은 누아르티에를 바라보았다. 노인은 눈짓으로, 전에 비슷한 상황에서 한 번 몸을 숨겼던 그 골방을 가리켰다. 모자를 챙겨 숨이 턱에 닿도록 골방에 몸을 던질 시간밖에 없었다. 곧 검사의 발소리가 복도에서 들려왔다.\n\n빌포르가 방 안으로 뛰어들어 발랑틴에게 달려가 그녀를 품에 안았다.\n\n\"의사를, 의사를 불러라, 다브리니 선생님을!\" 빌포르가 외쳤다. \"아니, 내가 직접 가지.\"\n\n그는 방에서 뛰쳐나갔고, 같은 순간 모렐은 반대편 문으로 빠져나갔다. 한 가지 무서운 기억이 그의 가슴을 찔렀던 것이다. 생메랑 부인이 죽던 날 밤, 의사와 빌포르가 나누던 대화가 떠올랐다. 이 증상들은, 정도는 덜 끔찍하다 해도, 바루아의 죽음에 앞서 나타났던 것과 똑같았다. 동시에 불과 두 시간 전에 들었던 몬테크리스토의 목소리가 그의 귓전에 울리는 듯했다. \"무엇이 필요하든, 모렐, 내게 오시오. 내겐 큰 힘이 있소.\"\n\n생각보다 빠르게 그는 마티뇽 거리로 내달렸고, 거기서 샹젤리제 대로로 향했다.\n\n한편 빌포르는 삯마차를 타고 다브리니의 집 앞에 다다랐다. 어찌나 사납게 초인종을 울렸던지 문지기가 깜짝 놀랐다. 빌포르는 한마디도 없이 계단을 뛰어 올라갔다. 문지기는 그를 알아보고 지나가게 두었을 뿐, 다만 이렇게 외쳤다.\n\n\"서재에 계십니다, 검사님, 서재에요!\" 빌포르는 문을 밀쳤다, 아니 거의 부수다시피 열었다.\n\n\"아,\" 의사가 말했다. \"당신이오?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\" 빌포르가 문을 닫으며 말했다. \"이번엔 내가 와서 묻겠소. 우리 단둘뿐이오? 선생, 내 집은 저주받았소!\"\n\n\"뭐라고요?\" 의사가 겉으로는 냉정한 듯, 그러나 깊은 동요를 담아 말했다. \"또 환자가 생겼소?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 선생.\" 빌포르가 머리카락을 움켜쥐며 외쳤다. \"그렇소!\"\n\n다브리니의 눈빛이 말하고 있었다. '그렇게 될 거라고 내가 말하지 않았소.' 그러더니 그는 천천히 이렇게 말했다. \"이번엔 당신 집에서 누가 죽어 가고 있소? 어떤 새로운 희생자가 하느님 앞에서 당신의 나약함을 고발하려는 거요?\"\n\n빌포르의 가슴에서 비통한 흐느낌이 터져 나왔다. 그는 의사에게 다가가 그의 팔을 붙잡았다. \"발랑틴이오.\" 그가 말했다. \"이번엔 발랑틴 차례요!\"\n\n\"당신 딸이!\" 다브리니가 비통과 놀라움으로 외쳤다.\n\n\"보시오, 당신이 잘못 짚었던 거요.\" 검사가 중얼거렸다. \"가서 그 애를 봐 주시오. 그리고 그 고통의 침상 곁에서, 그 애를 의심한 일에 대해 용서를 빌어 주시오.\"\n\n\"당신이 나를 찾을 때마다,\" 의사가 말했다. \"늘 너무 늦었소. 그래도 가겠소. 하지만 서두릅시다. 당신이 상대하는 적들 앞에서는 한순간도 허비할 수 없으니.\"\n\n\"오, 이번엔, 선생, 나약하다고 나를 책망할 일은 없을 거요. 이번엔 살인자를 알아내어 끝까지 쫓겠소.\"\n\n\"복수를 생각하기 전에 먼저 희생자를 구해 봅시다.\" 다브리니가 말했다. \"갑시다.\"\n\n빌포르를 데려왔던 바로 그 삯마차가 두 사람을 전속력으로 다시 실어 갔다. 바로 이 순간 모렐이 몬테크리스토의 집 문을 두드렸다.\n\n백작은 서재에서, 베르투치오가 황급히 가져온 무언가를 잔뜩 화난 표정으로 읽고 있었다. 두 시간 전에 자신을 떠난 모렐의 이름을 듣자, 백작은 고개를 들고 일어나 그를 맞으러 뛰어나왔다.\n\n\"무슨 일이오, 막시밀리앙?\" 그가 물었다. \"안색이 창백하고, 이마에서 땀이 줄줄 흐르는군.\" 모렐이 의자에 쓰러지듯 앉았다.\n\n\"네,\" 그가 말했다. \"급히 왔습니다. 당신과 이야기를 나누고 싶었어요.\"\n\n\"가족분들은 모두 무탈하시오?\" 백작이 더없이 다정한 호의로 물었다. 그 진심을 누구도 한순간조차 의심할 수 없었다.\n\n\"고맙습니다, 백작님, 고맙습니다.\" 청년은 어떻게 말을 꺼내야 할지 몰라 곤혹스러운 기색이 역력했다. \"네, 제 가족은 모두 무탈합니다.\"\n\n\"그것참 다행이오. 그래도 내게 할 말이 있는 것이지요?\" 백작이 더욱 불안한 기색으로 대답했다.\n\n\"네,\" 모렐이 말했다. \"맞습니다. 방금 죽음이 막 들이닥친 어느 집을 떠나, 당신에게 달려온 길입니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 모르세르 씨 댁에서 오는 길이오?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다.\n\n\"아닙니다.\" 모렐이 말했다. \"그 댁에 누가 죽기라도 했습니까?\"\n\n\"장군이 방금 권총으로 머리를 쏘았소.\" 몬테크리스토가 더없이 냉정하게 대답했다.\n\n\"오, 얼마나 끔찍한 일입니까!\" 막시밀리앙이 외쳤다.\n\n\"백작 부인이나 알베르에게는 그렇지 않소.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"죽은 아버지나 남편이 명예를 잃은 아버지나 남편보다 낫지. 피는 치욕을 씻어 내는 법이오.\"\n\n\"가엾은 백작 부인.\" 막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"정말 안됐습니다. 그토록 고결한 분인데요!\"\n\n\"알베르도 가엾이 여기시오, 막시밀리앙. 정말이지 그는 백작 부인의 자식다운 자식이오. 하지만 당신 이야기로 돌아갑시다. 내게 급히 달려왔으니, 내가 당신에게 도움이 되는 기쁨을 누릴 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"네, 당신의 도움이 필요합니다. 그러니까, 저는 미친 사람처럼, 오직 하느님만이 구원하실 수 있는 일에 당신이 도움을 빌려주실 수 있으리라 생각했어요.\"\n\n\"무슨 일인지 말해 보시오.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다.\n\n\"오,\" 모렐이 말했다. \"정말이지 이 비밀을 사람의 귀에 털어놓아도 될지 모르겠습니다. 하지만 운명이 저를 떠밀고, 절박함이 저를 옭아맵니다, 백작님……\" 모렐이 머뭇거렸다.\n\n\"내가 당신을 아낀다고 생각하지 않소?\" 몬테크리스토가 청년의 손을 다정하게 자기 손으로 잡으며 말했다.\n\n\"오, 당신은 제게 용기를 주십니다. 그리고 여기서 무언가가 제게 말합니다.\" 그가 가슴에 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"당신께는 어떤 비밀도 감추어서는 안 된다고요.\"\n\n\"옳소, 모렐. 하느님이 당신의 마음에 말씀하시고, 당신의 마음이 당신에게 말하는 거요. 그 말이 무엇인지 들려주시오.\"\n\n\"백작님, 당신이 아는 어떤 사람의 안부를 알아보도록 바티스탱을 보내도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"나는 당신을 위해 있고, 내 하인들은 더더욱 그렇소.\"\n\n\"오, 그녀가 차도가 없으면 저는 살 수 없습니다.\"\n\n\"바티스탱을 부르도록 종을 울릴까?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 제가 직접 가서 말하겠습니다.\" 모렐이 나가 바티스탱을 불러 몇 마디를 속삭였다. 하인이 곧장 달려갔다.\n\n\"자, 사람을 보냈소?\" 모렐이 돌아오는 것을 보고 몬테크리스토가 물었다.\n\n\"네, 이제 좀 더 마음이 가라앉겠지요.\"\n\n\"내가 기다리고 있는 것을 알지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 말씀드리겠습니다. 어느 날 저녁, 저는 어느 정원에 있었습니다. 나무 덤불이 저를 가려 주어, 아무도 제가 거기 있는 줄 몰랐지요. 두 사람이 제 곁을 지나갔습니다. 지금은 그들의 이름을 감추도록 허락해 주십시오. 그들은 나직한 목소리로 이야기하고 있었는데, 저는 그들의 말에 어찌나 마음이 쏠렸던지 단 한마디도 놓치지 않았습니다.\"\n\n\"안색이 창백하고 떨고 있는 걸 보니, 음울한 도입부로구려, 모렐.\"\n\n\"오, 그렇습니다, 아주 음울합니다, 백작님. 그 정원이 딸린 집에서 누군가가 막 죽었던 참이었어요. 제가 엿들은 그 대화의 한 사람은 그 집 주인이었고, 다른 한 사람은 의사였습니다. 주인은 의사에게 자신의 슬픔과 두려움을 털어놓고 있었지요. 한 달 사이에 죽음이 그 집에 갑작스럽고 느닷없이 들이닥친 것이 벌써 두 번째였으니까요. 마치 어떤 멸절의 천사가, 하느님의 분노의 표적으로 삼아 그 집을 파멸시키기로 정해 놓은 듯했습니다.\"\n\n\"아, 정말이오?\" 몬테크리스토가 청년을 뚫어지게 바라보며 말했다. 그러고는 알아챌 수 없는 동작으로 의자를 돌려, 빛이 막시밀리앙의 얼굴에 가득 떨어지는 동안 자신은 그늘 속에 머물게 했다.\n\n\"네,\" 모렐이 말을 이었다. \"죽음이 한 달 사이에 그 집에 두 번 들이닥쳤던 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그래서 의사는 뭐라고 대답했소?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다.\n\n\"그는 대답했습니다, 그 죽음은 자연스러운 것이 아니며, 어떤 원인으로 돌려야 한다고요……\"\n\n\"무슨 원인으로?\"\n\n\"독약으로요.\"\n\n\"저런!\" 몬테크리스토가 가벼운 기침을 하며 말했다. 그 기침은 극도로 동요한 순간에 그가 얼굴이 붉어지거나 창백해지는 것을, 혹은 그가 귀 기울이는 격렬한 관심을 감추는 데 도움이 되곤 했다. \"저런, 막시밀리앙, 그 말을 들었단 말이오?\"\n\n\"네, 백작님, 들었습니다. 그리고 의사는 만약 또 한 번 비슷한 방식으로 죽음이 일어난다면 사법 당국에 호소할 수밖에 없다고 덧붙였습니다.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 더없이 침착하게 귀를 기울였다, 아니 기울이는 듯 보였다.\n\n\"그런데,\" 막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"죽음이 세 번째로 찾아왔는데, 집주인도 의사도 한마디 하지 않았습니다. 그리고 죽음은 지금, 어쩌면 네 번째 일격을 가하고 있는지도 모릅니다. 백작님, 이 비밀을 쥐고 있는 제가 마땅히 어떻게 해야 합니까?\"\n\n\"이보시오,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"당신은 우리 모두가 줄줄 외고 있는 어떤 이야기를 들려주는 것 같소. 나는 당신이 그 이야기를 들은 그 집을 알고 있소. 아니면 그와 아주 비슷한 집이거나. 정원이 있고, 주인이 있고, 의사가 있고, 느닷없고 갑작스러운 죽음이 세 번 있었던 집이지. 그런데 나는 당신의 비밀을 가로챈 적이 없는데도 당신만큼 그 모든 것을 알고 있고, 양심의 가책 따위는 없소. 아니지, 그건 내 일이 아니니까. 당신은 멸절의 천사가 하느님의 분노에 그 집을 바친 것 같다고 말했소. 그렇다면, 당신의 추측이 현실이 아니라고 누가 말하겠소? 그것을 보는 게 자기네 이익인 자들조차 못 본 척 넘기는 일들을, 당신이 눈여겨보지 마시오. 그 집을 거니는 것이 하느님의 분노가 아니라 하느님의 정의라면, 막시밀리앙, 얼굴을 돌리고 그 정의가 제 목적을 이루도록 내버려 두시오.\"\n\n모렐이 몸을 떨었다. 백작의 태도에는 음울하고 엄숙하며 무서운 무언가가 있었다.\n\n\"게다가,\" 그는 같은 사람이 말하는 것이라고는 아무도 짐작하지 못할 만큼 달라진 어조로 말을 이었다. \"게다가, 그 일이 또다시 시작되리라고 누가 말하오?\"\n\n\"또다시 시작됐습니다, 백작님.\" 모렐이 외쳤다. \"그래서 제가 당신께 달려온 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 내가 어떻게 해 주기를 바라오? 이를테면, 내가 검사에게 정보를 알려 주기를 바라오?\" 몬테크리스토가 이 마지막 말을 어찌나 의미심장하게 내뱉었던지, 모렐이 벌떡 일어나 외쳤다.\n\n\"제가 누구를 말하는지 아시는군요, 백작님, 그렇지요?\"\n\n\"아주 잘 알고 있소, 친애하는 벗이여. 그리고 그것을 증명해 보이겠소. 글자에 점을 찍듯, 아니, 차라리 그 사람들의 이름을 대 보겠소. 당신은 어느 날 저녁 빌포르 씨의 정원을 거닐고 있었소. 당신이 들려준 이야기로 보아, 그날 저녁이 생메랑 부인이 죽은 저녁이었으리라 짐작하오. 당신은 빌포르 씨가 다브리니 씨에게 생메랑 씨의 죽음에 대해, 그리고 못지않게 놀라운 부인의 죽음에 대해 이야기하는 것을 들었소. 다브리니 씨는 두 죽음 모두 독약에서 비롯된 것이라 믿는다고 말했고. 그리고 정직한 당신은, 그 이후로 줄곧 이 비밀을 폭로해야 할지 감추어야 할지 알고자 마음에 묻고 양심에 캐물어 왔던 거요. 우리는 이제 중세에 사는 게 아니오. 비밀 재판소나 자유 법정 따위는 이제 없소. 그 사람들에게 무엇을 묻고 싶은 거요? '양심아, 네가 나와 무슨 상관이냐?' 스턴이 한 말처럼 말이오. 이보시오, 그들이 잠들어 있다면 자게 두시오. 졸음 속에서 창백해지고 싶다면 그러게 두시오. 그리고 부디 당신은, 당신을 어지럽힐 회한 따위 없는 당신은, 평온히 지내시오.\"\n\n모렐의 얼굴에 깊은 슬픔이 어렸다. 그가 몬테크리스토의 손을 붙잡았다. \"하지만 또다시 시작됐다지 않습니까!\"\n\n\"그래,\" 백작이 말했다. 그는 도무지 이해할 수 없는 모렐의 집요함에 놀라, 막시밀리앙을 한층 더 뚫어지게 바라보았다. \"또다시 시작되라지. 그건 아트레우스 가문 같은 것이오. 하느님이 그들을 단죄하셨으니, 그들은 그 벌을 받아야 하오. 그들은 아이들이 카드로 쌓아 올린 집처럼 모조리 무너져 사라질 거요. 그게 이백 장이라 해도, 쌓은 자의 숨결 한 번에 하나하나 무너지는 법이지. 석 달 전에는 생메랑 씨였고, 두 달 전에는 생메랑 부인이었소. 며칠 전에는 바루아였고, 오늘은 늙은 누아르티에, 아니면 젊은 발랑틴이오.\"\n\n\"알고 계셨군요?\" 모렐이 외쳤다. 어찌나 극심한 공포에 사로잡혔던지, 하늘이 무너져도 꿈쩍 않을 몬테크리스토조차 흠칫했다. \"알고 계셨으면서, 아무 말도 하지 않으셨다고요?\"\n\n\"그게 나와 무슨 상관이오?\" 몬테크리스토가 어깨를 으쓱하며 대답했다. \"내가 그 사람들을 아오? 그리고 한 사람을 구하자고 다른 사람을 잃어야 한단 말이오? 정말이지, 천만에. 죄인과 희생자 사이에서 나는 어느 쪽도 택할 마음이 없으니까.\"\n\n\"하지만 저는,\" 모렐이 슬픔에 신음하며 외쳤다. \"저는 그녀를 사랑합니다!\"\n\n\"사랑한다고? 누구를?\" 몬테크리스토가 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다. 그러고는 모렐이 하늘을 향해 쳐들던 두 손을 붙잡았다.\n\n\"저는 더없이 애틋하게 사랑합니다, 미치도록 사랑합니다. 그녀의 눈물 한 방울을 덜어 줄 수 있다면 제 핏줄의 피라도 다 내어 줄 사람으로서 사랑합니다. 저는 지금 이 순간 살해당하고 있는 발랑틴 드 빌포르를 사랑합니다! 제 말을 알아들으시겠습니까? 저는 그녀를 사랑합니다. 그리고 하느님과 당신께 묻습니다, 어떻게 하면 그녀를 구할 수 있는지요?\"\n\n몬테크리스토가 부르짖었다. 상처 입은 사자의 포효를 들어 본 사람만이 떠올릴 수 있는 그런 부르짖음이었다. \"가엾은 사람아,\" 그가 이번엔 자기 두 손을 비틀며 외쳤다. \"당신이 발랑틴을 사랑하다니, 저주받은 가문의 그 딸을!\"\n\n모렐은 그런 표정을 본 적이 없었다. 그토록 무서운 눈빛이 자기 얼굴 앞에서 번득인 적이 없었다. 전쟁터에서, 또 알제리의 살육의 밤마다 그토록 자주 보아 온 공포의 화신조차, 그의 주위에 그토록 무서운 불길을 떨친 적이 없었다. 그는 겁에 질려 뒷걸음질 쳤다.\n\n몬테크리스토로 말하자면, 이 격발 뒤에 마치 내면의 빛에 눈이 부신 듯 두 눈을 감았다. 잠시 후 그는 어찌나 강하게 자신을 다스렸던지, 격렬하게 들썩이던 가슴이 가라앉았다. 구름이 걷히고 나면 거품 일며 사납게 일렁이던 파도가 따스한 햇살에 순응하듯이. 이 침묵과 자제와 분투는 이십 초쯤 이어졌다. 이윽고 백작이 창백한 얼굴을 들었다.\n\n\"보시오,\" 그가 말했다. \"친애하는 벗이여, 하느님께서는 가장 경솔하고 무정한 인간들에게 무서운 광경을 들이미심으로써 그들의 무관심을 벌하시는 법이오. 곁에서 지켜보던 나, 열렬하고 호기심 어린 구경꾼이던 나, 이 음울한 비극이 펼쳐지는 것을 관찰하던 나, 사악한 천사처럼 비밀에 가려진 채 인간들이 저지르는 악을 비웃던 나는—(비밀이란 부유하고 강한 자들에게는 쉽게 지켜지는 법이지)—이제 그 구불구불한 자취를 지켜보던 바로 그 뱀에게 물렸소. 그것도 가슴 깊이 물렸소!\"\n\n모렐이 신음했다.\n\n\"자, 자,\" 백작이 말을 이었다. \"탄식해 봐야 소용없소. 사내답게, 굳세게, 희망을 가득 품으시오. 내가 여기 있으니, 당신을 지켜 주겠소.\"\n\n모렐이 슬프게 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"희망을 가지라고 하지 않소. 내 말을 알아듣겠소?\" 몬테크리스토가 외쳤다. \"나는 단 한 번도 거짓을 말한 적이 없고, 결코 속는 법이 없다는 걸 기억하시오. 지금 열두 시요, 막시밀리앙. 당신이 저녁이나 내일 아침이 아니라 정오에 와 준 것을 하늘에 감사하시오. 잘 들으시오, 모렐, 지금은 정오요. 만약 발랑틴이 지금 죽지 않았다면, 그녀는 죽지 않을 거요.\"\n\n\"어떻게 그럴 수가 있습니까?\" 모렐이 외쳤다. \"제가 떠날 때 그녀는 죽어 가고 있었는데요?\"\n\n몬테크리스토가 두 손으로 이마를 짚었다. 그토록 무서운 비밀들로 가득 찬 저 머릿속에서는 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있었던 걸까? 무자비하면서도 너그러운 저 마음에게, 빛의 천사 혹은 어둠의 천사는 무어라 말하고 있었을까? 그것은 오직 하느님만이 아신다.\n\n몬테크리스토가 다시 한번 고개를 들었다. 이번엔 잠에서 깨어난 아이처럼 평온했다.\n\n\"막시밀리앙,\" 그가 말했다. \"집으로 돌아가시오. 명하노니 꼼짝도 하지 말고, 아무것도 시도하지 말며, 얼굴에 어떤 생각도 드러내지 마시오. 그러면 내가 소식을 전하겠소. 가시오.\"\n\n\"오, 백작님, 그 냉정함이 저를 압도합니다. 그렇다면 당신은 죽음에 맞설 힘을 가지셨습니까? 당신은 인간을 넘어선 존재입니까? 천사입니까?\" 그러고는, 단 한 번도 위험 앞에 움츠린 적 없던 청년이, 몬테크리스토 앞에서 형언할 수 없는 공포에 사로잡혀 움츠러들었다. 하지만 몬테크리스토가 어찌나 우수에 차고도 다정한 미소로 그를 바라보았던지, 막시밀리앙은 눈에 눈물이 차오르는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"나는 당신을 위해 많은 것을 할 수 있소, 벗이여.\" 백작이 대답했다. \"가시오. 나는 혼자 있어야 하오.\"\n\n모렐은 몬테크리스토가 주위의 모든 것에 행사하는 그 비범한 위력에 굴복하여, 거기에 저항하려 들지 않았다. 그는 백작의 손을 꼭 쥐고 떠났다. 문간에서 잠시 멈추어, 마티뇽 거리에서 달려오는 바티스탱을 기다렸다.\n\n한편 빌포르와 다브리니는 있는 힘껏 서둘렀다. 두 사람이 도착했을 때 발랑틴은 기절에서 깨어나지 못한 상태였다. 의사는 상황이 요구하는 온갖 주의를 기울여, 또 비밀을 알고 있기에 두 배로 깊어진 관심으로 환자를 살폈다. 빌포르는 그의 얼굴과 입술을 뚫어지게 지켜보며 진찰의 결과를 기다렸다. 누아르티에는 그 소녀보다도 더 창백한 얼굴로, 빌포르보다도 더 간절하게 판단을 기다리며, 역시 골똘하고 애틋하게 지켜보고 있었다.\n\n마침내 다브리니가 천천히 이렇게 말했다. \"아직 살아 있습니다!\"\n\n\"아직이라니?\" 빌포르가 외쳤다. \"오, 선생, 그 얼마나 무서운 말이오.\"\n\n\"그렇소,\" 의사가 말했다. \"다시 말하지만, 아직 살아 있고, 나는 그 점에 놀라고 있소.\"\n\n\"하지만 무사하겠소?\" 아버지가 물었다.\n\n\"그렇소, 살아 있으니 말이오.\"\n\n그 순간 다브리니의 시선이 누아르티에의 눈과 마주쳤다. 그 눈은 어찌나 비범한 기쁨으로, 어찌나 풍부하고 사려 깊게 빛나던지, 의사가 깜짝 놀랐다. 그는 소녀를 다시 안락의자에 앉혔다. 그녀의 입술은 얼굴 전체와 마찬가지로 어찌나 핏기 없이 희던지 거의 분간되지 않을 정도였다. 그는 꼼짝 않고 누아르티에를 바라보았다. 노인은 의사가 하는 모든 일을 미리 내다보고 또 칭찬하는 듯했다.\n\n\"선생,\" 다브리니가 빌포르에게 말했다. \"괜찮으시다면, 발랑틴 양의 하녀를 불러 주시지요.\"\n\n빌포르가 직접 하녀를 찾으러 갔고, 다브리니는 누아르티에에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"제게 하실 말씀이 있습니까?\" 그가 물었다. 노인이 눈을 의미심장하게 깜빡였다. 기억하다시피 그것은 그가 동의를 표하는 유일한 방법이었다.\n\n\"단둘이서요?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"좋습니다, 제가 당신 곁에 남겠습니다.\" 바로 그때 빌포르가 하녀를 데리고 돌아왔다. 그리고 그 뒤로 빌포르 부인이 들어왔다.\n\n\"대체 이 사랑스러운 아이에게 무슨 일이 생긴 거예요? 방금 저를 떠날 때 몸이 좀 불편하다고 하소연하긴 했지만, 저는 대수롭지 않게 여겼는데요.\"\n\n젊은 부인은 눈에 눈물을 글썽이며, 참된 어머니의 온갖 애정의 표시를 담아 발랑틴에게 다가가 그녀의 손을 잡았다. 다브리니는 계속 누아르티에를 바라보았다. 노인의 두 눈이 커지고 동그래지며, 두 뺨이 창백해지고 떨리며, 이마에 땀방울이 맺히는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"아,\" 그는 무심결에 누아르티에의 눈을 좇으며 말했다. 그 눈은 빌포르 부인에게 못 박혀 있었다. 부인이 거듭 말하고 있었다.\n\n\"이 가엾은 아이는 침대에 눕히는 게 좋겠어요. 자, 파니, 우리가 침대로 옮기자.\"\n\n다브리니는 그렇게 하면 누아르티에와 단둘이 남을 수 있겠다 싶어, 그것이 할 수 있는 가장 좋은 방법이라는 의견을 밝혔다. 다만 자신이 지시한 것 외에는 그녀에게 아무것도 주어서는 안 된다고 못 박았다.\n\n사람들이 발랑틴을 옮겨 갔다. 그녀는 정신이 돌아왔지만, 발작에 온몸이 어찌나 흔들렸던지 거의 움직이지도 말하지도 못했다. 그래도 할아버지에게 작별의 눈길 한 번을 보낼 힘은 남아 있었다. 노인은 손녀를 떠나보내며 제 영혼마저 내놓는 듯했다. 다브리니는 환자를 따라가, 처방전을 쓰고, 빌포르에게 직접 삯마차를 타고 약방에 가서 처방한 약을 받아 손수 가져온 다음 딸의 방에서 자신을 기다리라고 일렀다. 그러고는 발랑틴에게 아무것도 주지 말라는 당부를 거듭한 뒤, 다시 누아르티에에게로 내려가 문을 조심스레 닫았다. 그리고 아무도 엿듣지 않는다는 것을 확인한 뒤에 말했다.\n\n\"이 아가씨의 병에 대해 무언가 아시는 게 있습니까?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\" 노인이 말했다.\n\n\"우리에겐 허비할 시간이 없습니다. 제가 묻겠으니, 답해 주십시오.\" 누아르티에가 답할 준비가 되었다는 신호를 보냈다. \"손녀에게 일어난 이 변고를 미리 예상하셨습니까?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\" 다브리니가 잠시 생각에 잠겼다. 그러고는 누아르티에에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"제가 드릴 말씀을 용서하십시오.\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"하지만 이 끔찍한 상황에서는 어떤 단서도 놓쳐서는 안 됩니다. 가엾은 바루아가 죽는 것을 보셨지요?\" 누아르티에가 하늘을 향해 눈을 들었다.\n\n\"그가 무엇 때문에 죽었는지 아십니까?\" 다브리니가 누아르티에의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 물었다.\n\n\"그렇소.\" 노인이 대답했다.\n\n\"그가 자연사했다고 생각하십니까?\" 누아르티에의 굳어 버린 입술에 일종의 미소 같은 것이 어렸다.\n\n\"그러니까 바루아가 독살되었다고 생각하셨군요?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"그가 희생된 그 독약이 그를 노린 것이었다고 생각하십니까?\"\n\"아니오.\"\n\n\"바루아를 본의 아니게 친 바로 그 손이 이제 발랑틴을 공격했다고 생각하십니까?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그녀도 죽겠습니까?\" 다브리니가 꿰뚫는 듯한 시선을 누아르티에에게 못 박으며 물었다. 그는 이 질문이 노인에게 미치는 영향을 지켜보았다.\n\n\"아니오.\" 그가 대답했다. 그 의기양양한 기색은 제아무리 영리한 점쟁이라도 어리둥절하게 했을 것이다.\n\n\"그렇다면 희망을 품으시는 겁니까?\" 다브리니가 놀라며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\"무엇을 희망하십니까?\" 노인이 답할 수 없다는 것을 눈으로 알렸다.\n\n\"아, 그렇지, 맞습니다.\" 다브리니가 중얼거렸다. 그러고는 누아르티에를 향해 말했다. \"살인자가 재판받기를 희망하십니까?\"\n\"아니오.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그 독약이 발랑틴에게 효력을 미치지 않기를 희망하십니까?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"그녀를 독살하려는 시도가 있었다고 말씀드린들,\" 다브리니가 덧붙였다. \"당신께는 새삼스러울 게 없겠지요?\" 노인이 그 점에 대해선 추호의 의심도 없다는 신호를 보냈다. \"그렇다면 발랑틴이 어떻게 화를 면하리라 희망하시는 겁니까?\"\n\n누아르티에는 한곳에 시선을 굳게 박은 채 떼지 않았다. 다브리니가 그 방향을 좇아 보니, 그 시선은 노인이 매일 아침 복용하는 약을 담은 병에 박혀 있었다. \"아, 정말입니까?\" 문득 어떤 생각이 떠올라 다브리니가 말했다. \"혹시 당신은……\" 누아르티에가 그의 말을 끝까지 듣게 두지 않았다.\n\n\"그렇소.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"그녀의 몸이 독약에 저항하도록 만들 생각을 하셨군요?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\"조금씩 익숙하게 만들어서……\"\n\n\"그렇소, 그렇소, 그렇소.\" 누아르티에가 자기 뜻이 통한 데 기뻐하며 말했다.\n\n\"물론이지요. 제가 당신께 드리는 약에 브루신이 들어 있다고 말씀드렸으니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"그래서 그녀를 그 독약에 익숙해지게 함으로써, 비슷한 독약의 효력을 무력화하려 하신 거군요?\" 누아르티에의 기쁨이 가시지 않았다. \"그리고 성공하셨습니다.\" 다브리니가 외쳤다. \"그 대비책이 없었다면 발랑틴은 도움을 구하기도 전에 죽었을 겁니다. 약의 양이 지나치게 많긴 했지만, 그녀는 그것에 흔들렸을 뿐입니다. 적어도 이번에는, 발랑틴은 죽지 않을 겁니다.\"\n\n인간을 넘어선 기쁨이 노인의 두 눈을 크게 넓혔다. 그 눈은 무한한 감사의 빛을 담고 하늘을 향해 들렸다. 바로 그때 빌포르가 돌아왔다.\n\n\"자, 선생,\" 그가 말했다. \"심부름시키신 것을 가져왔소.\"\n\n\"이게 당신이 보는 앞에서 조제된 거요?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\" 검사가 대답했다.\n\n\"당신 손에서 떠난 적은 없었고요?\"\n\"없었소.\"\n\n다브리니가 병을 받아, 그 안에 든 약을 몇 방울 손바닥에 따라 삼켰다.\n\n\"좋소,\" 그가 말했다. \"발랑틴에게 갑시다. 내가 모두에게 지시를 내리겠소. 그리고 빌포르 씨, 당신은 누구도 그 지시에서 어긋나지 않도록 직접 살펴 주시오.\"\n\n다브리니가 빌포르를 데리고 발랑틴의 방으로 돌아가던 바로 그 순간, 진지한 거동에 차분하고 단호한 어조를 지닌 한 이탈리아인 사제가 빌포르 씨의 저택에 잇닿은 집을 빌려 자기 거처로 삼았다. 그 집의 이전 세입자 세 사람이 어떻게 그 집을 떠났는지는 아무도 몰랐다. 두 시간쯤 뒤에 그 집의 기초가 부실하다는 소문이 돌았다. 하지만 그 소문도 새 세입자가 바로 그날 다섯 시에 변변찮은 가구를 들이고 그곳에 자리 잡는 것을 막지는 못했다. 임대 계약서는 새 세입자가 삼 년, 육 년, 혹은 구 년으로 작성했고, 집주인의 관례에 따라 여섯 달 치를 선불로 냈다.\n\n이 새 세입자는, 앞서 말했듯이 이탈리아인으로, 일 시뇨르 자코모 부소니라 불렸다. 곧바로 일꾼들이 불려 왔고, 바로 그날 밤 변두리 끝을 지나던 행인들은 목수와 미장이들이 무너져 가는 그 집의 아래층을 수리하느라 분주한 것을 놀란 눈으로 바라보았다.\n\n# Chapter 95. Father and Daughter\n\n앞 장에서 우리는 당글라르 부인이 격식을 갖추어 빌포르 부인을 찾아가 외제니 당글라르와 안드레아 카발칸티의 머지않은 결혼을 알리는 장면을 보았다. 이 중대한 일에 관련된 모든 사람들의 동의를 함축하거나 함축하는 듯한 이 격식 있는 통고에는, 우리 독자들이 반드시 보아야 할 한 장면이 앞서 있었다. 독자들이 한 걸음 뒤로 물러나, 그 큰 재앙들이 닥친 날 아침으로 시간을 돌려, 앞서 보여 드린 바 있는 그 화려하고 금빛으로 치장된 살롱, 곧 그 주인인 당글라르 남작의 자랑거리인 방으로 함께 가 주기를 청한다.\n\n이 방에서, 아침 열 시쯤, 은행가 본인이 몇 분째 생각에 잠긴 채, 두 개의 문을 번갈아 지켜보고 작은 소리 하나하나에 귀를 기울이며, 역력한 불안에 휩싸여 이리저리 거닐고 있었다. 인내심이 바닥나자 그는 시종을 불렀다.\n\n\"에티엔,\" 그가 말했다. \"외제니 아가씨가 왜 나더러 응접실로 오라 했는지, 그리고 왜 이렇게 오래 기다리게 하는지 알아보고 오게.\"\n\n이렇게 언짢은 심기를 한 차례 토해 내고 나니 남작은 한결 차분해졌다. 그날 아침 당글라르 양은 아버지에게 면담을 청하면서 그 금빛 응접실을 만남의 장소로 정해 두었다. 이 일의 별스러움과, 무엇보다 그 격식이 은행가를 적잖이 놀라게 했고, 그는 즉시 딸의 뜻을 따라 먼저 응접실로 발걸음을 옮겼던 것이다. 에티엔은 곧 심부름을 마치고 돌아왔다.\n\n\"아가씨의 하녀가 말하기를, 나리, 아가씨가 지금 단장을 마무리하고 계시니 곧 이리로 오실 거랍니다.\"\n\n당글라르는 만족한다는 뜻으로 고개를 끄덕였다. 세상 사람들과 하인들 앞에서 당글라르는 사람 좋고 너그러운 아버지의 인물을 연기했다. 이것은 그가 펼치는 통속 희극에서 그가 맡은 배역 가운데 하나였고, 그가 골라 쓴 분장이었다. 그것은 고전 무대에서 아버지 역을 맡은 배우들이 쓰던 가면과 꼭 닮아 그에게 잘 어울렸다. 그 가면은 한쪽에서 보면 다정함 그 자체였지만, 다른 쪽에서 보면 입꼬리가 만성적인 심술로 아래로 처져 있었다. 그러나 서둘러 말해 두자면, 사사로운 자리에서는 그 다정한 쪽이 다른 쪽 수준으로 떨어져 버려서, 대개는 너그러운 사내가 사라지고 그 자리를 난폭한 남편이자 군림하는 아버지가 차지했다.\n\n\"나하고 이야기를 하고 싶다는 그 어리석은 계집애가 도대체 왜 내 서재로 오지 않는 거야? 게다가 도대체 무슨 할 말이 있다는 거지?\"\n\n이 생각을 머릿속에서 스무 번째 곱씹고 있을 때 문이 열리며 외제니가 모습을 드러냈다. 그녀는 무늬가 들어간 검은 새틴 드레스를 입고 머리를 매만지고 장갑을 낀 채, 마치 이탈리아 오페라극장에라도 가려는 듯한 차림이었다.\n\n\"그래, 외제니, 내게 무슨 볼일이냐? 게다가 서재가 그렇게 편한데 어째서 이 엄숙한 응접실이냐?\"\n\n\"왜 그렇게 물으시는지 잘 압니다, 아버지.\" 외제니가 아버지에게 앉으라는 손짓을 하며 말했다. \"사실 두 질문이 우리 대화의 주제를 고스란히 짚어 주고 있네요. 두 가지 다 답해 드리겠습니다. 다만 보통과는 거꾸로, 가장 어렵지 않은 것부터, 곧 마지막 질문부터 답하지요. 제가 응접실을 만남의 장소로 고른 것은 은행가의 서재가 주는 불쾌한 인상과 영향을 피하기 위해서입니다. 그 금박을 입힌 출납 장부들, 요새의 성문처럼 잠긴 서랍들, 어디서 온 것인지 알 수도 없는 산더미 같은 어음들, 그리고 영국, 네덜란드, 스페인, 인도, 중국, 페루에서 온 무더기 편지들은 대개 아버지의 마음에 묘한 영향을 끼쳐, 세상에는 거래처 사람들의 호평보다 더 크고 더 신성한 관심사가 있다는 것을 잊게 만들지요. 그래서 저는 이 응접실을 골랐습니다. 보시다시피 여기에는 호화로운 액자 속에서 미소 짓고 행복해하는 아버지의 초상, 제 초상, 어머니의 초상, 그리고 온갖 전원 풍경과 마음을 어루만지는 목가들이 걸려 있으니까요. 저는 외적인 인상을 무척 중시합니다. 아버지께는 어쩌면 그런 것이 아무것도 아닐 수 있지만, 제게 이런 취향이 없다면 저는 예술가라 할 수 없겠지요.\"\n\n\"아주 좋아.\" 당글라르가 대꾸했다. 그는 이 모든 서두를 흔들림 없는 냉정함으로 들었지만 한마디도 알아듣지 못했다. 지난 일들에 짓눌린 사람이 다 그렇듯, 그는 말하는 사람의 생각 속에서 제 자신의 생각의 실마리를 찾느라 정신이 팔려 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"그러니 둘째 문제는 풀린 셈이지요, 아니면 거의 풀린 셈이고요.\" 외제니가 조금도 당황하지 않고, 몸짓과 말씨에서 그녀를 돋보이게 하는 그 남자다운 날카로움으로 말했다. \"아버지도 그 설명에 만족하신 듯하군요. 이제 첫째 문제로 돌아가지요. 제가 왜 이 면담을 청했는지 물으셨지요. 두 마디로 말씀드리겠습니다, 아버지. 저는 안드레아 카발칸티 백작과 결혼하지 않겠어요.\"\n\n당글라르가 의자에서 펄쩍 뛰어 일어나 두 눈과 두 팔을 하늘을 향해 쳐들었다.\n\n\"네, 정말입니다, 아버지.\" 외제니가 여전히 아주 침착하게 말을 이었다. \"놀라셨군요, 알겠어요. 이 작은 일이 시작된 이래 저는 조금도 반대하는 기색을 보이지 않았으니까요. 하지만 저는 늘, 때가 오면, 저와 의논하지 않은 사람들과 제 마음에 들지 않는 일들에 맞서 단호하고 절대적인 의지를 내세우게 마련입니다. 그렇지만 이번에 제가 보인 평온함, 철학자들이 말하는 수동성은 다른 데서 비롯된 것이었어요. 그것은 순종하고 헌신하는 딸처럼\"—젊은 처녀의 자줏빛 입술에 옅은 미소가 스쳤다—\"순종을 연습해 보고 싶다는 바람에서 나온 것이었지요.\"\n\n\"그래서?\" 당글라르가 물었다.\n\n\"그래서요, 아버지.\" 외제니가 대답했다. \"끝까지 애써 봤습니다. 그리고 이제 그 순간이 닥치고 보니, 제 온갖 노력에도 불구하고 그것이 불가능하다는 걸 느끼겠어요.\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 당글라르가 말했다. 그의 나약한 정신은 처음에는 이 무자비한 논리의 무게에 완전히 짓눌렸는데, 그 논리에는 명백한 사전 계획과 의지의 힘이 배어 있었다. \"그렇게 거절하는 이유가 무엇이냐, 외제니? 무슨 까닭을 대겠다는 거냐?\"\n\n\"이유 말입니까?\" 젊은 처녀가 대답했다. \"글쎄요, 그 사람이 다른 누구보다 더 못생겼거나, 더 어리석거나, 더 불쾌해서는 아닙니다. 아니에요, 안드레아 카발칸티 씨는 사람의 얼굴과 풍채를 보는 이들에게는 자기 부류의 아주 훌륭한 표본으로 보일 수 있겠지요. 또 제 마음이 다른 누구보다 그 사람에게 덜 끌려서도 아니에요. 그건 여학생이나 댈 법한 이유이고, 저는 그런 걸 저 자신보다 한참 아래로 봅니다. 사실 저는 아무도 사랑하지 않아요, 아버지. 아시잖아요, 그렇지요? 그러니 진짜 필요도 없는데 어째서 제 인생을 평생의 동반자로 짐 지워야 하는지 모르겠어요. 어느 현인이 '무엇이든 지나치지 말라'고 하지 않았던가요? 또 다른 이는 '내 가진 것은 모두 몸에 지니고 다닌다'고 했고요. 저는 이 두 격언을 라틴어와 그리스어로 배웠어요. 하나는 파이드루스의 말이고, 다른 하나는 비아스의 말로 알고 있습니다. 자, 사랑하는 아버지, 인생이라는 난파 속에서—인생이란 우리 희망의 영원한 난파니까요—저는 쓸데없는 짐을 바다에 던져 버립니다. 그게 전부예요. 그리고 저는 제 의지만을 지닌 채, 완벽하게 혼자, 따라서 완벽하게 자유롭게 살 작정입니다.\"\n\n\"가엾은 것, 가엾은 것!\" 당글라르가 창백해지며 중얼거렸다. 오랜 경험으로 그는 자신이 이토록 느닷없이 맞닥뜨린 장애물이 얼마나 단단한지 알고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"가엾은 것이라고요.\" 외제니가 대꾸했다. \"가엾은 것이라 하셨습니까, 아버지? 천만에요. 그 탄식은 무척 연극조에다 꾸민 티가 나는군요. 오히려 행복하지요. 제게 부족한 게 무엇입니까? 세상은 저더러 아름답다고 합니다. 환대받는다는 건 좋은 일이에요. 저는 호의 어린 대접이 좋아요. 그러면 얼굴이 환해지고, 제 주변 사람들도 그리 흉해 보이지 않으니까요. 저는 약간의 재치와, 어느 정도의 상대적인 감수성을 지니고 있어서, 호두를 깨서 알맹이를 빼 먹는 원숭이처럼, 제 삶을 떠받치기 위해 인생 전반에서 마주치는 좋은 것을 모조리 끌어낼 수 있어요. 저는 부자입니다. 아버지가 프랑스에서 손꼽히는 재산가시니까요. 저는 외동딸이고, 아버지는 손주를 안겨 주지 않는다고 딸의 상속권을 박탈해 버리는 포르트생마르탱이나 게테 극장의 아버지들처럼 까다롭지도 않으시지요. 게다가 선견지명 있는 법이 아버지에게서 저를 적어도 완전히는 폐적할 권리를 빼앗아 두었고, 마찬가지로 저를 이 사람 저 사람과 강제로 결혼시킬 권리도 빼앗아 두었지요. 그러니—희극 오페라에서들 말하듯, 아름답고, 재치 있고, 어느 정도 재능 있고, 게다가 부자이니—이게 바로 행복이지요, 아버지—어째서 저를 가엾다 하십니까?\"\n\n당글라르는 딸이 오만하다 못해 방자하게까지 미소 짓는 것을 보고 난폭한 감정을 완전히 억누르지는 못했지만, 그 감정은 그저 짧은 탄식으로만 새어 나왔다. 저 아름다운 검은 눈썹 아래에서 그를 향해 쏟아지는 붙박인 듯한 캐묻는 시선 앞에서, 그는 신중하게 고개를 돌렸고, 단호한 정신의 위력에 기가 꺾여 곧바로 마음을 가라앉혔다.\n\n\"정말이지, 내 딸아.\" 그가 미소를 띠며 대답했다. \"너는 네가 뽐내는 그대로의 사람이로구나. 다만 한 가지만 빼고 말이다. 그게 무엇인지 성급히 말해 주지는 않으마. 차라리 네가 알아맞혀 보도록 남겨 두는 편이 낫겠지.\"\n\n외제니는 자신이 그토록 당당하게 치장해 둔 오만의 화관에서 꽃 한 송이를 두고 누가 시비를 건다는 사실에 몹시 놀라며 당글라르를 바라보았다.\n\n\"내 딸아,\" 은행가가 말을 이었다. \"너 같은 처녀, 결혼하지 않겠노라 작정한 처녀를 움직이는 심정이 무엇인지 너는 내게 완벽하게 설명해 주었다. 이제는 나 같은 아버지, 딸을 결혼시키기로 작정한 아버지의 동기를 내가 네게 말할 차례구나.\"\n\n외제니는 고개를 숙였다. 순종하는 딸로서가 아니라, 논쟁을 벌일 채비를 갖춘 적수로서였다.\n\n\"내 딸아,\" 당글라르가 말을 이었다. \"아버지가 딸에게 남편을 고르라 할 때는, 늘 그 애를 결혼시키고 싶어 하는 무슨 이유가 있는 법이다. 더러는 네가 방금 말한 그 광증, 곧 손주들 속에서 다시 살아 보겠다는 광증에 사로잡힌 이들도 있지. 그건 내 약점이 아니다, 미리 말해 두마. 가정의 즐거움이란 내겐 아무 매력도 없으니까. 내 무관심을 충분히 이해할 만큼, 그리고 그것을 죄로 몰지 않을 만큼 철학적인 딸이라는 걸 아니까, 너에게는 이런 말도 털어놓는 거다.\"\n\n\"그건 본론이 아니에요.\" 외제니가 말했다. \"솔직하게 말씀하시지요, 아버지. 저는 솔직함을 좋아합니다.\"\n\n\"오,\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"상황이 그러길 요구한다면 나도 네 방식을 따를 수 있지. 그게 내 평소 습관은 아니다만. 그러니 계속하마. 내가 너에게 결혼하라고 권한 것은 너를 위해서가 아니다. 사실 그 순간 나는 너 생각은 조금도 하지 않았으니까(너는 솔직함을 좋아하니, 이제 흡족하겠지). 그저 내가 벌이고 싶은 어떤 상업적 투기 때문에, 너를 되도록 빨리 결혼시키는 편이 내게 유리했던 거다.\" 외제니가 불안해지기 시작했다.\n\n\"정말이지 내가 말한 그대로다. 그리고 너는 나한테 화를 내서는 안 돼. 네가 이 고백을 끌어낸 거니까. 나는 너 같은 예술가와 굳이 산수 계산을 늘어놓고 싶지는 않다. 불쾌하고 시적이지 못한 인상과 감정에 물들까 봐 내 서재에 들어오기조차 꺼리는 예술가와 말이지. 하지만 바로 그 은행가의 서재, 네가 어제는 다달이 용돈으로 주는 천 프랑을 받으러 기꺼이 찾아왔던 그 서재에서는, 사랑하는 아가씨야, 결혼하지 않을 딸에게도 쓸모 있는 많은 것들을 배울 수 있단다. 가령 거기서는 이런 것을 배울 수 있지. 네 예민한 신경을 헤아려, 응접실에서 알려 주마. 곧 은행가의 신용이란 그의 육체적이고 정신적인 생명이라는 것, 숨이 몸에 생기를 불어넣듯 신용이 그를 떠받친다는 것 말이다. 몬테크리스토 씨가 언젠가 그 일에 대해 내게 한 차례 강의를 해 주었는데, 나는 그걸 결코 잊은 적이 없다. 거기서는 또 이런 것을 배울 수 있지. 신용이 가라앉으면 몸은 송장이 되고 만다는 것을. 그리고 너처럼 훌륭한 논리가를 딸로 둔 것을 자랑스러워하는 이 은행가에게도, 머지않아 바로 그런 일이 닥칠 거다.\"\n\n그러나 외제니는 그 일격에 몸을 움츠리기는커녕 오히려 꼿꼿이 일어섰다. \"파산했나요?\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"바로 그렇다, 내 딸아. 내가 말하려던 게 정확히 그거야.\" 당글라르가 말했다. 그는 거의 제 가슴팍에 손톱을 박아 넣을 지경이었으나, 그 거친 얼굴에는 무정하면서도 약삭빠른 사내의 미소를 그대로 띠고 있었다. \"파산—그래, 바로 그거다.\"\n\n\"아!\" 외제니가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 파산이다! 이제 비극 시인의 말처럼, 그토록 끔찍한 이 비밀이 드러났구나. 자, 내 딸아, 이 불행이 네게 미치는 한에서나마 어떻게 덜 수 있을지, 내 입으로 일러 주마.\"\n\n\"오,\" 외제니가 외쳤다. \"아버지가 제게 경고하시는 그 재앙을 제가 저 자신 때문에 한탄할 거라 여기신다면, 아버지는 관상을 영 못 보시는 분이군요. 제가 파산했다고요? 그게 제게 무슨 의미가 있겠어요? 제겐 재능이 남아 있지 않나요? 저도 파스타나 말리브란이나 그리지처럼, 아버지가 재산이 아무리 많았더라도 제게 결코 주지 않으셨을 것을 제 힘으로 벌어들일 수 있지 않나요? 연 십만, 아니 십오만 리브르를, 저 자신 말고는 누구에게도 신세 지지 않고서 말이에요. 게다가 그 돈은, 아버지가 그 보잘것없는 만 이천 프랑을 시큰둥한 얼굴로 제 낭비를 나무라며 주셨던 것과는 달리, 환호와 갈채와 꽃다발과 함께 따라올 거예요. 그리고 설령 제게 그 재능이 없다 해도—아버지의 그 미소를 보니 의심하고 계신 게 분명하지만—제겐 여전히 독립을 향한 저 뜨거운 사랑이 남아 있지 않나요? 그것은 재산을 대신해 줄 것이고, 제 마음속에서는 자기 보존의 본능마저 능가하는 것이지요. 아니에요, 저는 저 자신 때문에 슬퍼하지 않습니다. 저는 언제든 방도를 찾아낼 거예요. 제 책들, 제 그림붓들, 제 피아노, 돈이 거의 들지 않으면서도 제가 마련할 수 있는 그 모든 것들은 여전히 제 것으로 남을 테니까요.\n\n당글라르 부인 때문에 제가 슬퍼할 거라 생각하세요? 그 또한 착각이세요. 제가 크게 잘못 본 게 아니라면, 어머니는 아버지를 위협하는 그 재앙에 미리 대비를 해 두셨고, 그 재앙은 어머니께는 아무 영향도 미치지 않고 지나갈 거예요. 어머니는 자기 앞가림을 해 두셨어요—적어도 저는 그러길 바라고요—저를 돌보느라 자기 계획에서 주의를 돌리신 적이 없으니까요. 어머니는 제 자유에 대한 사랑을 짐짓 너그러이 받아 주는 척하며 제 독립심을 길러 주셨어요. 오, 아니에요, 아버지. 어린 시절부터 저는 제 주변에서 벌어진 일을 너무 많이 보았고 너무 많이 이해했어요. 그러니 불행이 제게 분에 넘치는 위력을 떨치지는 못해요. 제 가장 어린 기억부터, 저는 누구의 사랑도 받지 못했어요—그건 더 나쁜 일이지요. 그 탓에 저도 자연스레 아무도 사랑하지 않게 됐고요—그건 더 좋은 일이고요—자, 이제 제 신앙 고백을 들으셨습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 당글라르가 분노로 창백해지며 말했다. 그 분노는 상처받은 부정과는 전혀 무관한 것이었다. \"그렇다면, 아가씨, 너는 내 파산을 앞당기겠다는 그 결심을 굽히지 않겠다는 거냐?\"\n\n\"파산을 앞당긴다고요? 제가 아버지의 파산을 앞당긴다고요? 무슨 말씀이세요? 무슨 뜻인지 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 더 잘됐다. 내겐 아직 한 줄기 희망이 남아 있구나. 들어 보아라.\"\n\n\"귀를 기울이고 있어요.\" 외제니가 어찌나 골똘히 아버지를 바라보았던지, 아버지로서는 그 가차 없는 시선을 견디는 것이 힘겨운 일이었다.\n\n\"카발칸티 씨가,\" 당글라르가 말을 이었다. \"곧 너와 결혼하면서, 자기 재산을 내 손에 맡길 거다. 삼백만 리브르에 이르는 재산을 말이지.\"\n\n\"그것 참 훌륭하군요!\" 외제니가 더없는 경멸을 담아 말하며, 장갑 한 짝을 다른 짝 위로 매만져 폈다.\n\n\"내가 그 삼백만을 네게서 가로챌 거라 생각하느냐.\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"그건 걱정 마라. 그 돈은 적어도 천만은 낳을 운명이니까. 나와 어느 동료 은행가가 철도 부설권을 따냈다. 요즘 시대에 산업이라 할 만한 사업 가운데, 일찍이 로가 영원히 속아 넘어가는 파리 사람들에게 저 황당한 미시시피 계획으로 내걸었던 그 허황된 전망을 그나마 실현해 줄 유일한 사업이지. 내가 보기에 철도의 백만분의 일은 오하이오 강기슭의 황무지 한 에이커만큼의 값어치는 충분히 한다. 우리는 이 경우, 저당을 잡고 보증금을 거는 거다. 알다시피 그건 선금인 셈이지. 우리 돈을 내주는 대가로 적어도 열, 열다섯, 스물, 아니 백 리브르어치의 철을 얻는 거니까. 자, 일주일 안에 나는 내 몫으로 사백만을 예치해야 한다. 장담하건대 그 사백만이 천만이나 천이백만을 낳을 거다.\"\n\n\"하지만 그저께 제가 아버지를 찾아갔을 때—아버지도 그날을 아주 잘 기억하시는 듯한데—\" 외제니가 대꾸했다. \"저는 아버지가 예치금을—그게 맞는 말인가요?—오백오십만이나 마련하시는 걸 보았어요. 아버지는 그걸 국고 어음 두 장으로 제게 보여 주시기까지 했고, 그토록 값진 종이가 번개처럼 제 눈을 부시게 하지 않는다고 놀라셨지요.\"\n\n\"그래, 하지만 그 오백오십만은 내 것이 아니다. 그저 사람들이 내게 얼마나 큰 신뢰를 두고 있는지를 보여 주는 증거일 뿐이지. 평판 좋은 은행가라는 내 명성이 자선 단체들의 신뢰를 얻게 해 주었고, 그 오백오십만은 그들의 돈이다. 다른 때 같으면 나는 망설임 없이 그걸 써먹었겠지. 하지만 내가 최근에 입은 큰 손실이 다들 잘 알려져 있고, 너에게 말했듯 내 신용이 적잖이 흔들리고 있어. 그 예치금은 언제든 회수될 수 있고, 만약 내가 그걸 다른 데 써 버렸다면 나는 수치스러운 파산을 자초하게 될 거다. 나는 파산을 업신여기지는 않는다, 믿어 다오. 하지만 그 파산은 사람을 부유하게 하는 것이라야지, 망하게 하는 것이라서는 안 되지. 자, 네가 카발칸티 씨와 결혼하고 내가 그 삼백만을 손에 넣으면, 아니 내가 그걸 손에 넣으리라 사람들이 믿기만 해도, 내 신용은 회복될 거다. 그리고 지난 한두 달 사이 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 운명이 내 앞길에 파 놓은 심연 속으로 삼켜져 버린 내 재산도 되살아날 거야. 무슨 말인지 알겠느냐?\"\n\n\"완벽하게 알겠어요. 저를 삼백만에 저당 잡히겠다는 거지요, 그렇지요?\"\n\n\"액수가 클수록 네게는 더 영광스러운 일이지. 네 값어치가 어느 정도인지 가늠하게 해 주니까.\"\n\n\"고마워요. 한마디만 더 하지요, 아버지. 카발칸티 씨가 가져올 재산에 관한 소문을, 정작 그 돈에는 손대지 않고 최대한 활용하시겠다고 제게 약속해 주시겠어요? 이건 이기심에서가 아니라 양심에서 드리는 말씀이에요. 저는 기꺼이 아버지의 재산을 다시 일으키는 걸 돕겠지만, 남들을 망하게 하는 데 공범이 되지는 않겠어요.\"\n\n\"하지만 내가 말하지 않았느냐,\" 당글라르가 외쳤다. \"이 삼백만이면—\"\n\n\"그 삼백만에 손대지 않고도 자리를 회복하실 수 있다고 기대하세요, 아버지?\"\n\n\"그러길 바란다. 결혼이 성사되어 내 신용을 굳혀 준다면 말이지.\"\n\n\"제 지참금으로 약속하신 오십만 프랑은 카발칸티 씨에게 치를 수 있으시고요?\"\n\n\"그자가 시청에서 돌아오는 길에 받게 될 거다.\"\n\n\"좋아요!\"\n\n\"그다음은? 더 무엇을 바라느냐?\"\n\n\"제 서명을 요구하시면서, 제 몸에 관해서는 저를 완전히 자유롭게 놔두시는 건지 알고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"전적으로 그렇다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 앞서 말씀드렸듯이, 아버지—좋아요. 카발칸티 씨와 결혼할 준비가 됐습니다.\"\n\n\"한데 너는 무슨 꿍꿍이냐?\"\n\n\"아, 그건 제 일이지요. 아버지의 비밀을 알면서 제 비밀까지 말씀드린다면, 제가 아버지보다 무슨 이점을 누리겠어요?\"\n\n당글라르가 입술을 깨물었다. \"그렇다면,\" 그가 말했다. \"절대로 빠뜨릴 수 없는 그 공식 방문들에 응할 준비가 됐다는 거지?\"\n\n\"네.\" 외제니가 대답했다.\n\n\"사흘 안에 혼인 계약서에 서명하고?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 이번엔 내 차례구나. 나도 말하마, 좋다!\"\n\n당글라르가 딸의 손을 제 두 손으로 감싸 쥐었다. 그러나 참으로 기이하게도, 아버지는 \"고맙다, 내 아이야\"라고 말하지 않았고, 딸 또한 아버지에게 미소 짓지 않았다.\n\n\"면담은 끝났나요?\" 외제니가 일어서며 물었다.\n\n당글라르는 더 할 말이 없다는 몸짓을 했다. 오 분 뒤, 다르미이 양의 손가락이 피아노를 울렸고, 당글라르 양은 데스데모나를 향한 브라반티오의 저주를 노래하고 있었다. 곡이 끝날 무렵 에티엔이 들어와, 마차에 말이 매여 있으며 남작 부인이 방문길에 나서려고 아가씨를 기다린다고 외제니에게 알렸다. 우리는 그들을 빌포르의 집에서 보았다. 그러니 그들은 이제 그 길을 따라 나섰던 것이다.\n\n# Chapter 96. The Contract\n\n우리가 방금 묘사한 장면이 있은 지 사흘 뒤, 다시 말해 외제니 당글라르 양과 안드레아 카발칸티의 혼인 계약서에 서명하기로 정해진 날 오후 다섯 시 무렵이었다. 은행가는 여전히 카발칸티를 공작이라 부르기를 고집했다. 산뜻한 바람이 몬테크리스토 백작 저택 앞 작은 정원의 나뭇잎을 흔들고 있었고, 백작은 외출 채비를 하던 참이었다. 십오 분 전부터 마부석에 앉아 있던 마부가 말들을 붙들고 있었지만, 말들은 초조하게 땅을 긁어 댔다. 그때 우리에게 익숙한 그 멋진 사륜마차가 정문 모서리를 잽싸게 돌아 들어오더니, 현관 계단 앞에 안드레아 카발칸티 씨를 내려놓았다. 그는 마치 공주와 결혼하러 가는 사람처럼 한껏 차려입고 들떠 있었다.\n\n그는 여느 때처럼 허물없이 백작의 안부를 물으며 가뿐하게 이층으로 올라갔고, 계단 꼭대기에서 백작과 마주쳤다.\n\n백작은 젊은이를 보고 걸음을 멈추었다. 안드레아로 말하자면, 그는 이미 시동이 걸린 상태였고, 한번 시동이 걸리면 무엇으로도 그를 멈출 수 없었다.\n\n\"아, 안녕하십니까, 친애하는 백작님.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 안드레아 씨.\" 백작이 절반쯤 농을 섞은 어조로 말했다. \"어떻게 지내십니까?\"\n\n\"보시다시피 더할 나위 없이 좋습니다. 백작님께 드릴 이야기가 산더미처럼 있어서요. 하지만 먼저, 외출하시려던 참입니까, 아니면 막 돌아오신 참입니까?\"\n\n\"나가려던 참이었소.\"\n\n\"그러시다면 방해가 되지 않게, 괜찮으시다면 제가 백작님 마차에 함께 타겠습니다. 톰이 제 사륜마차를 끌고 뒤따르게 하지요.\"\n\n\"아니요.\" 백작이 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 희미한 경멸의 미소를 띠며 말했다. 그는 이 젊은이와 함께 있는 모습을 남에게 보이고 싶지 않았다. \"아니요, 여기서 당신 이야기를 듣는 편이 낫겠소, 친애하는 안드레아 씨. 안에서라야 더 편히 이야기를 나눌 수 있고, 우리 대화를 엿들을 마부도 없으니 말이오.\"\n\n백작은 이층의 작은 응접실로 되돌아가 자리에 앉았다. 그는 다리를 꼬고는 젊은이에게도 앉으라는 손짓을 했다. 안드레아는 한껏 들뜬 태도를 지어 보였다.\n\n\"아시다시피, 친애하는 백작님.\" 그가 말했다. \"오늘 저녁에 식이 거행됩니다. 아홉 시에 장인어른 댁에서 계약서에 서명하기로 했지요.\"\n\n\"아, 그래요?\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 처음 들으시는 겁니까? 당글라르 씨가 식에 대해 알려 드리지 않았습니까?\"\n\n\"아, 들었소.\" 백작이 말했다. \"어제 그분에게서 편지를 한 통 받았소만, 시각까지 적혀 있던 것 같지는 않군.\"\n\n\"아마 장인어른께서는 워낙 다들 아는 일이라 따로 적지 않으신 모양입니다.\"\n\n\"음.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"운이 좋으시오, 카발칸티 씨. 더없이 어울리는 혼사를 맺는 것이고, 당글라르 양은 아름다운 아가씨니 말이오.\"\n\n\"네, 정말 그렇습니다.\" 카발칸티가 자못 겸손한 어조로 대답했다.\n\n\"무엇보다 그 아가씨는 아주 부유하지, 적어도 내가 알기로는 그렇소.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"아주 부유하다고요, 그리 생각하십니까?\" 젊은이가 되물었다.\n\n\"의심할 여지가 없소. 당글라르 씨가 적어도 재산의 절반은 숨기고 있다는 말이 있으니까.\"\n\n\"그런데도 인정하는 것만 천오백만이나 이천만 프랑이라지요.\" 안드레아가 기쁨으로 반짝이는 눈빛을 띠며 말했다.\n\n\"게다가,\" 몬테크리스토가 덧붙였다. \"그분은 미국과 영국에서는 이미 유행하지만 프랑스에서는 아주 새로운 종류의 투자에 막 뛰어들려는 참이지.\"\n\n\"네, 네, 무슨 말씀이신지 압니다. 그분이 부설권을 따낸 그 철도 말씀이지요?\"\n\n\"바로 그거요. 그 사업으로 천만 프랑은 벌 거라는 게 중론이오.\"\n\n\"천만 프랑이라니! 그렇게 보십니까? 굉장하군요!\" 카발칸티가 외쳤다. 그는 그 황금 같은 말들이 내는 쇳소리에 완전히 정신이 나가 있었다.\n\n\"게다가,\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"그 모든 재산이 결국 당신에게 돌아올 거요. 당글라르 양이 외동딸이니, 그게 마땅하기도 하고. 더구나 당신 부친께서 내게 장담하신 바로는, 당신 자신의 재산도 약혼녀의 재산에 거의 맞먹는다지. 하지만 돈 이야기는 이쯤 합시다. 그런데 안드레아 씨, 내 보기엔 당신이 이 일을 제법 능숙하게 처리한 것 같소.\"\n\n\"어느 모로 보나 나쁘진 않았지요.\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"저는 외교관이 될 팔자로 태어났나 봅니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 외교관이 되어야겠군. 알다시피 외교라는 건 배워서 익힐 수 있는 게 아니오. 타고나는 거지. 그래, 마음을 빼앗기셨소?\"\n\n\"실은, 그런 것 같아 두렵습니다.\" 안드레아가 대답했다. 그 어조는 프랑스 극장에서 도랑트나 발레르가 알세스트에게 대답하던 말투 그대로였다.\n* 도랑트와 발레르, 알세스트는 몰리에르 희극의 등장인물들이다. ─옮긴이\n\n\"사랑을 돌려받고 계시오?\"\n\n\"그런 것 같습니다.\" 안드레아가 의기양양한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"받아들여졌으니까요. 하지만 한 가지 큰 사실을 잊어서는 안 되지요.\"\n\n\"그게 뭐요?\"\n\n\"제가 더없이 특별한 도움을 받았다는 사실 말입니다.\"\n\n\"별말씀을.\"\n\n\"정말입니다.\"\n\n\"운이 좋았다는 거요?\"\n\n\"아니요, 백작님 덕분이지요.\"\n\"내 덕분이라고? 천만의 말씀이오, 공작.\" 몬테크리스토가 그 호칭에 또렷이 힘을 주며 말했다. \"내가 당신을 위해 한 일이 무엇이오? 당신의 이름과 사회적 지위, 그리고 당신의 자질만으로 충분하지 않소?\"\n\n\"아닙니다.\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"아니에요, 아무리 그리 말씀하셔도 소용없습니다, 백작님. 백작님 같은 분의 지위가 제 이름과 사회적 지위, 자질을 다 합친 것보다 더 큰 몫을 했다고 저는 확신합니다.\"\n\n\"완전히 잘못 짚으셨소.\" 몬테크리스토가 차갑게 말했다. 그는 이 젊은이의 음흉한 술수를 간파했고, 그 말의 속내를 꿰뚫고 있었다. \"당신은 부친의 영향력과 재산이 확인된 뒤에야 비로소 내 비호를 얻었소. 따져 보면, 당신도 당신의 그 명망 높은 부친도 한 번 본 적 없는 내게 당신을 알게 되는 기쁨을 마련해 준 게 누구였소? 바로 내 두 친구, 윌모어 경과 부소니 신부였소. 내가 당신의 보증인이 되지는 않더라도 당신을 후원하도록 이끈 것은 무엇이었소? 이탈리아에 널리 알려지고 드높이 존경받는 당신 부친의 이름이었소. 개인적으로 나는 당신을 알지 못하오.\"\n\n이 차분한 어조와 완벽한 여유에 안드레아는 자신이 지금 이 순간 자기보다 더 억센 손에 제압당해 있으며, 그 손아귀를 쉽사리 벗어날 수 없음을 느꼈다.\n\n\"아, 그럼 제 아버지가 정말로 막대한 재산을 가지고 있는 거군요, 백작님?\"\n\n\"그런 모양이오.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다.\n\n\"아버지가 제게 약속하신 혼인 지참금이 도착했는지 아십니까?\"\n\n\"그렇다는 통지를 받았소.\"\n\n\"하지만 삼백만 프랑은요?\"\n\n\"삼백만은 아마 오는 중일 거요.\"\n\n\"그럼 정말로 제가 그 돈을 받게 되는 거군요?\"\n\n\"그야,\" 백작이 말했다. \"당신이 아직 돈이 궁한 처지를 겪어 본 적은 없는 것 같소만.\"\n\n안드레아는 어찌나 놀랐던지 잠시 그 문제를 곱씹었다. 그러다 상념에서 깨어나 말했다.\n\n\"백작님, 한 가지 청이 있습니다. 마음에 들지 않으실 수도 있겠지만, 이해는 해 주시리라 믿습니다.\"\n\n\"말해 보시오.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"운이 좋았던 덕에 저는 이름난 분들을 많이 알게 되었고, 적어도 지금은 친구가 넘쳐 납니다. 하지만 이제 곧 온 파리가 지켜보는 가운데 결혼하는 마당이니, 저는 명망 있는 이름의 후원을 받아야 마땅하고, 아버지의 손이 없는 자리에서는 누군가 힘 있는 분이 저를 제단까지 이끌어 주어야 합니다. 그런데 제 아버지는 파리에 오시지 않지요, 그렇지요?\"\n\n\"그분은 연로하신 데다 온몸이 상처투성이라, 본인 말로는 여행을 하면 끔찍이 고생하신다더군.\"\n\n\"알겠습니다. 그래서 한 가지 부탁을 드리러 왔습니다.\"\n\n\"내게?\"\n\n\"네, 백작님께요.\"\n\n\"대체 무엇이오?\"\n\n\"아버지 역할을 맡아 주시는 것입니다.\"\n\n\"이런, 이 사람아! 무슨 소리요? 내가 다행히도 당신과 갖가지 인연을 맺어 왔는데, 그러고도 나를 그렇게 모르고서 그런 부탁을 하는 거요? 차라리 오십만 프랑을 빌려 달라고 하시오. 그런 거액을 빌리는 일도 드물긴 하나, 맹세코, 그 편이 나를 덜 난처하게 할 거요! 그러니 알아 두시오, 이미 말한 줄 알았소만, 이 세상사에 끼어드는 일, 특히 그 도덕적인 측면에 관해서라면, 몬테크리스토 백작은 동방 특유의 조심성, 아니 미신까지도 단 한 번도 버린 적이 없소. 카이로에, 스미르나에, 콘스탄티노플에 각각 후궁을 둔 이 몸이 결혼식을 주관한다? 결단코 안 될 일이오!\"\n\n\"그럼 거절하시는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"단호히. 당신이 내 아들이든 형제든, 똑같이 거절했을 거요.\"\n\n\"그럼 어찌해야 합니까?\" 안드레아가 실망하여 말했다.\n\n\"방금 친구가 백 명이라고 하지 않았소.\"\n\n\"맞습니다, 하지만 저를 당글라르 씨 댁에 소개해 주신 분은 백작님이시지요.\"\n\n\"천만에! 사실을 정확히 되짚어 봅시다. 당신은 우리 집 만찬 자리에서 그를 만났고, 그의 집에는 당신 스스로 찾아갔소. 그건 전혀 다른 이야기요.\"\n\n\"네, 하지만 이번 혼인은 백작님이 밀어 주신 거잖습니까.\"\n\n\"내가? 추호도 아니오, 그렇게 믿어 주시오. 당신이 내게 청혼을 주선해 달라고 했을 때 내가 뭐라고 했는지 떠올려 보시오. '아, 나는 절대 중매는 서지 않소, 친애하는 공작. 그게 내 확고한 원칙이오.'\" 안드레아는 입술을 깨물었다.\n\n\"하지만, 적어도 그 자리에는 와 주시겠지요?\"\n\n\"온 파리가 다 갑니까?\"\n\n\"아, 그럼요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 온 파리처럼 나도 가리다.\" 백작이 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 계약서에도 서명해 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그건 마다할 이유가 없소. 내 조심성도 거기까지 미치지는 않으니.\"\n\n\"좋습니다, 더는 베풀어 주지 않으시겠다니, 주시는 것만으로 만족해야지요. 하지만 한마디만 더, 백작님.\"\n\n\"뭐요?\"\n\n\"조언입니다.\"\n\n\"조심하시오. 조언은 도움보다 더 고약한 거요.\"\n\n\"아, 이건 백작님 자신을 위태롭게 하지 않고도 주실 수 있는 것입니다.\"\n\n\"무엇인지 말해 보시오.\"\n\n\"제 아내의 재산이 오십만 리브르입니까?\"\n\n\"당글라르 씨가 직접 밝힌 액수가 그렇소.\"\n\n\"그 돈을 제가 직접 받아야 합니까, 아니면 공증인에게 맡겨 두어야 합니까?\"\n\n\"격식을 갖춰 처리하고 싶을 때는 보통 이렇게 합니다. 양측 대리인이 계약서에 서명한 다음 날이나 그다음 날에 모임을 정하고, 그 자리에서 양쪽 몫을 교환하며 서로 영수증을 주고받지요. 그런 다음 혼례가 거행되면, 그들은 그 금액을 혼인의 주체인 당신이 마음대로 쓸 수 있도록 내놓는 거요.\"\n\n\"실은,\" 안드레아가 어딘지 감추기 힘든 불안을 내비치며 말했다. \"장인어른께서 방금 말씀하신 그 유명한 철도 사업에 우리 재산을 투자할 작정이라고 하시는 걸 들은 것 같아서요.\"\n\n\"음.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"다들 말하길, 그게 열두 달 만에 재산을 세 배로 불리는 길이라더군. 당글라르 남작은 좋은 아버지고, 셈에도 밝은 분이오.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"백작님이 거절하신 것만 빼면 모든 게 더할 나위 없군요. 그 거절이 저를 몹시 슬프게 합니다만.\"\n\n\"그저 그런 상황에서 자연스레 드는 조심성 탓으로 여겨 주시오.\"\n\n\"좋습니다.\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"원하시는 대로 하지요. 그럼 오늘 저녁 아홉 시에 뵙겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그때까지 안녕히.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 입술이 창백해질 만큼 미약하게 저항했지만 의례적인 미소만은 잃지 않았다. 그런데도 안드레아는 백작의 손을 붙잡아 꾹 쥐고는, 자기 사륜마차에 뛰어올라 사라졌다.\n\n아홉 시까지 남은 너덧 시간 동안 안드레아는 말을 타고 돌아다니며 여기저기 방문을 했다. 앞서 이야기했던 사람들이 가장 화려한 마차를 몰고 은행가의 집에 나타나도록 부추기려는 속셈이었다. 그는 그 뒤로 모든 이의 머리를 어지럽혀 놓을, 또 당글라르가 막 주도권을 쥐고 있던 그 투자 사업의 지분을 약속하며 그들의 눈을 현혹시켰다.\n\n과연 저녁 여덟 시 반에는 대연회장과 거기 잇닿은 회랑, 그리고 같은 층의 다른 세 응접실이 향수 냄새 나는 사람들로 가득 찼다. 그들은 이 혼사 자체에는 별 관심이 없었지만, 무언가 새로운 볼거리가 있는 곳이라면 어디든 끼고 싶어 하는 그 욕망만은 다 함께 품고 있었다. 어느 한림원 회원이라면 이렇게 말했으리라. 사교계의 향연이란 변덕스러운 나비와 굶주린 벌, 윙윙대는 수벌을 끌어들이는 꽃밭이라고.\n\n그 방들이 눈부시게 밝혀져 있다는 것은 누구도 부인할 수 없었다. 빛이 금박 몰딩과 비단 휘장 위로 쏟아졌고, 그저 화려함 하나만 내세울 뿐인 그 모든 천박한 장식이 제 광채를 한껏 뽐냈다. 외제니 양은 무늬가 든 흰 비단 드레스를 입어 우아하면서도 단순하게 차려입었고, 칠흑 같은 검은 머리에 반쯤 가려진 흰 장미 한 송이가 유일한 장식이었다. 보석은 단 하나도 곁들이지 않았다. 그러나 그녀의 눈빛만은 이 수수한 차림의 소녀다운 단순함과 어긋나는 완벽한 자신감을 드러내고 있었다.\n\n당글라르 부인은 조금 떨어진 곳에서 드브레, 보샹, 샤토르노와 담소를 나누고 있었다. 드브레는 이 성대한 식을 위해 이 집에 출입을 허락받았으나, 다른 모든 이와 똑같은 입장이었고 어떤 특별한 대우도 없었다. 당글라르 씨는 의원들과 재정 관련 인사들에게 둘러싸여, 사태의 흐름이 정부로 하여금 자신을 입각시킬 수밖에 없게 되었을 때 도입할 작정인 새로운 조세 이론을 설명하고 있었다. 안드레아는 오페라극장의 더없이 완성된 멋쟁이 하나를 팔에 매단 채, 자신의 앞날의 계획과, 연 십칠만 오천 리브르의 수입으로 파리의 유행에 들여올 새로운 사치들을 제법 영리하게 늘어놓고 있었다. 태연한 척하려면 대담해질 수밖에 없었던 것이다.\n\n군중은 터키석과 루비, 에메랄드, 오팔, 다이아몬드의 밀물과 썰물처럼 방 안을 이리저리 오갔다. 늘 그렇듯, 가장 나이 많은 여인들이 가장 요란하게 치장했고, 가장 못생긴 여인들이 가장 눈에 띄었다. 아름다운 백합이나 어여쁜 장미 한 송이가 있다 해도, 터번을 쓴 어머니나 극락조 깃털을 단 이모 뒤 어느 구석에 가려진 그것을 찾아내려면 한참을 헤매야 했다.\n\n군중과 웅성거림, 웃음소리 한가운데서 시시각각 문지기의 목소리가 들려왔다. 재정 부문에서 이름난, 군에서 존경받는, 혹은 문단에서 명성 높은 누군가의 이름을 알리는 소리였고, 그 이름은 여기저기 무리들의 가벼운 술렁임으로 화답을 받았다. 그러나 그 인간 물결의 바다를 출렁이게 할 특권을 누리는 한 사람에 비하면, 무심한 눈길이나 경멸의 비웃음으로 맞아들여지는 자가 또 얼마나 많았던가!\n\n잠든 엔디미온을 새긴 묵직한 괘종시계의 바늘이 금빛 문자판 위 아홉 시를 가리키고, 기계적 사고의 충실한 표상인 망치가 아홉 번을 칠 그 순간, 이번에는 몬테크리스토 백작의 이름이 울려 퍼졌다. 그러자 마치 전기 충격이라도 받은 듯 좌중 전체가 일제히 문 쪽으로 돌아섰다. 백작은 검은 옷을 입었고 여느 때처럼 단순한 차림이었다. 흰 조끼가 넓고 기품 있는 가슴을 드러냈으며, 검은 넥타이는 시체처럼 창백한 그 얼굴과 대비되어 유난히 도드라졌다. 그가 지닌 유일한 장신구는 사슬 하나뿐이었는데, 어찌나 가는지 그 가느다란 금실이 흰 조끼 위에서 거의 보이지 않을 정도였다.\n\n곧바로 문 둘레로 사람들의 원이 둘러섰다. 백작은 한눈에 응접실 한쪽 끝에 있는 당글라르 부인, 반대편 끝에 있는 당글라르 씨, 그리고 자기 앞쪽의 외제니를 알아보았다. 그는 먼저 남작 부인 쪽으로 나아갔다. 부인은 빌포르 부인과 담소를 나누고 있었는데, 발랑틴이 아직 병중이라 빌포르 부인은 혼자 와 있었다. 그를 위해 길이 훤히 비워져 있었으므로, 그는 옆으로 비켜설 필요도 없이 남작 부인에게서 외제니에게로 넘어갔다. 그러고는 어찌나 빠르고 절제된 말로 그녀에게 찬사를 건넸던지, 그 도도한 예술가도 적잖이 놀랐다. 그녀 곁에는 루이즈 다르미이 양이 있었는데, 그녀는 백작이 친절히 마련해 준 이탈리아행 소개장에 감사를 표했다. 그녀는 곧 그 소개장을 쓸 작정이었다. 이 부인들 곁을 떠난 그는 자신을 맞으러 나온 당글라르와 마주쳤다.\n\n이렇게 세 가지 사교적 의무를 마친 몬테크리스토는 걸음을 멈추고 주위를 둘러보았다. 그 표정은 어떤 부류의 사람들에게서나 볼 수 있는, \"나는 내 도리를 다했으니, 이제 남들이 제 도리를 다할 차례다.\"라고 말하는 듯한 표정이었다.\n\n옆방에 있던 안드레아는 몬테크리스토의 등장이 일으킨 그 술렁임에 함께 휩쓸렸고, 이제 백작에게 인사를 드리러 앞으로 나왔다. 백작은 완전히 사람들에게 둘러싸여 있었다. 말수가 적고 그 한마디 한마디가 무게를 지닌 사람들이 늘 그렇듯, 다들 그에게 말을 건네려고 안달이었다. 마침 그때 대리인들이 도착해, 서명을 위해 마련된 탁자를 덮은 금실 자수의 벨벳 천 위에 자신들의 휘갈겨 쓴 서류들을 펼쳐 놓았다. 그것은 사자 발 모양의 다리로 받쳐진 금박 탁자였다. 공증인 하나는 자리에 앉고, 다른 하나는 선 채로 있었다. 이제 막 계약서 낭독에 들어가려는 참이었으니, 거기 모인 파리의 절반이 거기에 서명할 터였다. 모두가 제자리를 잡았다. 아니, 부인들은 둥글게 원을 이루었고, 신사들은 ─부알로가 이른바 '강건한 문체'의 구속에 더 무심한 자들답게─ 안드레아의 안절부절못하는 모습, 당글라르 씨의 못 박힌 듯한 집중, 외제니의 침착함, 그리고 남작 부인이 이 중대한 일을 가볍고 발랄하게 다루는 태도에 대해 한마디씩 했다.\n\n계약서는 깊은 침묵 속에서 낭독되었다. 그러나 낭독이 끝나기가 무섭게 웅성거림이 모든 응접실에 곱절로 일었다. 두 젊은이의 손에 들어올 그 휘황한 액수, 굴러 들어올 수백만 프랑은, 이를 위해 따로 마련한 방에 진열된 혼인 예물과 신부의 다이아몬드를 한층 빛내며, 시샘에 찬 좌중에 그 환상을 한껏 펼쳐 놓았다.\n\n당글라르 양의 매력은 젊은 남자들의 눈에 한층 돋보였고, 그 순간만큼은 태양의 광채마저 능가하는 듯했다. 부인들로 말하자면, 그들이 그 수백만 프랑을 탐내면서도 자신들에게는 그것이 필요 없다고 여겼다는 건 말할 것도 없다. 그런 게 없어도 그들은 충분히 아름다웠으니까. 친구들에게 둘러싸여 찬사와 아첨을 받던 안드레아는 자기 꿈이 진짜라고 믿기 시작했고, 거의 얼이 빠질 지경이었다. 공증인이 엄숙하게 펜을 집어 들어 머리 위로 한 번 휘두르고는 말했다.\n\n\"여러분, 이제 계약서에 서명하겠습니다.\"\n\n남작이 먼저 서명하고, 다음으로 카발칸티 노인 측 대리인, 그다음에 남작 부인, 그리고 법률 문서의 그 끔찍한 표현을 빌리자면 '장차 부부가 될 두 사람'의 순서였다.\n\n남작이 펜을 들어 서명했고, 이어서 대리인이 서명했다. 남작 부인이 빌포르 부인의 팔에 기대어 다가왔다.\n\n\"이보세요,\" 그녀가 펜을 들며 말했다. \"이거 참 분통 터지는 일 아니에요? 몬테크리스토 백작님 댁에서 일어난 살인 강도 사건 ─백작님도 하마터면 희생될 뻔했지요─ 그 일에 뜻밖의 일이 생기는 바람에, 빌포르 씨를 뵙는 즐거움을 빼앗기고 말았으니 말이에요.\"\n\n\"그렇습니까?\" 당글라르 씨가 말했다. 그 말투는 \"아, 뭐, 그게 나랑 무슨 상관이오?\"라고 말하는 투였다.\n\n\"실은,\" 몬테크리스토가 다가오며 말했다. \"그분이 못 오시게 된 게 본의 아니게 저 때문인 것 같아 몹시 송구합니다.\"\n\n\"아니, 백작님이요?\" 당글라르 부인이 서명하며 말했다. \"정말 그렇다면, 조심하셔야 할 거예요. 저는 절대 용서하지 않을 테니까요.\"\n\n안드레아가 귀를 쫑긋 세웠다.\n\n\"하지만 제 잘못은 아니라는 걸 입증해 보이지요.\"\n\n모두가 귀를 곤두세웠다. 좀처럼 입을 열지 않는 몬테크리스토가 이제 막 말을 하려던 참이었다.\n\n\"기억하시겠지요,\" 백작이 더없이 깊은 침묵 속에서 말했다. \"저를 털러 왔던 그 가엾은 자가 제 집에서 죽었던 일 말입니다. 그자가 집을 빠져나가려다 공범의 칼에 찔린 것으로 추정되지요.\"\n\n\"그렇소.\" 당글라르가 말했다.\n\n\"상처를 살펴보려고 그자의 옷을 벗겨 한쪽 구석에 던져 두었는데, 경찰이 그 옷을 거두어 갔습니다. 다만 조끼 하나만은 미처 못 보고 빠뜨렸지요.\"\n\n안드레아의 얼굴이 창백해졌고, 그는 슬그머니 문 쪽으로 다가갔다. 그는 지평선 위로 구름이 피어오르는 것을 보았고, 그것은 곧 닥쳐올 폭풍을 예고하는 듯했다.\n\n\"그런데 오늘 그 조끼가 발견된 겁니다. 피로 뒤덮이고, 심장께에 구멍이 뚫린 채로요.\" 부인들이 비명을 질렀고, 두세 명은 막 정신을 잃으려 했다. \"그게 제게로 왔지요. 그 더러운 누더기가 무엇인지 아무도 짐작하지 못했습니다. 오직 저만이 그것이 살해된 자의 조끼임을 알아챘지요. 제 하인이 그 음산한 유물을 살펴보다가 주머니에서 종이 한 장이 만져져 꺼냈는데, 그게 바로 남작님 앞으로 된 편지였습니다.\"\n\n\"내게?\" 당글라르가 외쳤다.\n\n\"네, 그렇습니다, 남작님께요. 편지를 적신 핏자국 아래로 남작님 성함을 간신히 읽어 냈지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답하자, 좌중이 일제히 경악을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 당글라르 부인이 불안한 눈으로 남편을 보며 물었다. \"그게 어째서 빌포르 씨가 못 오시게……\"\n\n\"이렇게 간단한 까닭이지요, 부인.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"그 조끼와 편지는 둘 다 이른바 정황 증거라는 것이었습니다. 그래서 저는 그것들을 검사에게 보냈지요. 친애하는 남작님도 아시겠지만, 형사 사건에서는 법적인 절차가 가장 안전한 법입니다. 어쩌면 남작님을 노린 어떤 음모였는지도 모르니까요.\" 안드레아는 몬테크리스토를 뚫어지게 바라보다가 두 번째 응접실로 사라졌다.\n\n\"그럴 수도 있겠군요.\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"그 살해된 자는 전직 갤리선 죄수가 아니었소?\"\n\n\"맞습니다.\" 백작이 대답했다. \"카드루스라는 이름의 중죄인이었지요.\" 당글라르의 얼굴이 살짝 창백해졌다. 안드레아는 작은 응접실 너머의 대기실에 다다랐다.\n\n\"하지만 서명을 계속하시지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"제 이야기가 좌중을 온통 들쑤셔 놓은 것 같군요. 남작 부인과 당글라르 양께 사과드립니다.\"\n\n서명을 마친 남작 부인이 공증인에게 펜을 돌려주었다.\n\n\"카발칸티 공작,\" 공증인이 말했다. \"카발칸티 공작, 어디 계십니까?\"\n\n\"안드레아, 안드레아.\" 이미 그와 이름을 부를 만큼 가까워진 몇몇 젊은이들이 따라 불렀다.\n\n\"공작을 불러오게. 이제 그가 서명할 차례라고 알리게.\" 당글라르가 시중꾼 하나에게 외쳤다.\n\n그러나 바로 그 순간, 손님들이 무리지어 겁에 질린 채 주연회장으로 몰려들었다. 마치 무시무시한 괴물 하나가, 삼킬 자를 찾아 헤매며 방 안으로 들어오기라도 한 듯했다. 과연 물러서고, 놀라고, 비명을 지를 만한 까닭이 있었다. 한 장교가 각 응접실 문마다 병사 둘씩을 배치하고는, 어깨띠를 두른 경찰서장을 앞세운 채 당글라르 쪽으로 다가오고 있었던 것이다. 당글라르 부인이 비명을 지르며 정신을 잃었다. 당글라르는 자신이 위협받는다고 여겼고 ─어떤 양심은 결코 평온하지 못한 법이다─ 손님들 앞에서마저 비굴한 공포로 일그러진 얼굴을 드러냈다.\n\n\"무슨 일이오, 선생?\" 몬테크리스토가 경찰서장을 맞으러 나아가며 물었다.\n\n\"여러분 가운데,\" 서장이 백작에게는 대꾸도 하지 않고 물었다. \"안드레아 카발칸티라는 이름을 쓰는 자가 누구입니까?\"\n\n방 안 곳곳에서 경악의 외침이 터져 나왔다. 사람들은 그를 찾았고, 서로 물었다.\n\n\"그런데 대체 안드레아 카발칸티가 누구요?\" 당글라르가 어리둥절하여 물었다.\n\n\"툴롱 감옥에서 탈출한 갤리선 죄수입니다.\"\n\n\"그자가 무슨 죄를 지었소?\"\n\n\"그는,\" 서장이 단호한 목소리로 말했다. \"몬테크리스토 백작 댁에서 도주하던 순간, 옛 감방 동료인 카드루스라는 자를 살해한 혐의를 받고 있습니다.\"\n\n몬테크리스토가 재빨리 주위를 둘러보았다. 안드레아는 사라지고 없었다.\n\n# Chapter 97. The Departure for Belgium\n\n* 라틴어 quærens quem devoret: '삼킬 자를 찾아 헤매며'라는 뜻으로, 성경 베드로전서의 구절에서 왔다. ─옮긴이\n당글라르의 살롱에 헌병대가 느닷없이 들이닥치고 뒤이어 그 정체가 밝혀지면서 일대 혼란이 벌어진 지 몇 분 뒤, 저택은 마치 손님들 사이에 흑사병이나 콜레라가 퍼지기라도 한 듯 순식간에 텅 비어 버렸다.\n\n모든 문으로, 모든 계단으로, 모든 출구로, 사람들은 너 나 할 것 없이 자리를 떠나려고, 아니 차라리 달아나려고 서둘렀다. 이런 상황에서는 으레 건네는 위로의 말이 — 큰 재난이 닥쳤을 때 절친한 벗들조차 앞다투어 내놓고 싶어 하는 그 위로의 말이 — 아무 소용도 없다는 것이 자명했기 때문이다. 은행가의 저택에 남은 사람이라곤 서재에 틀어박혀 헌병 장교에게 진술을 하고 있는 당글라르와, 우리에게 익숙한 그 규방에서 겁에 질려 있는 당글라르 부인, 그리고 오만한 태도와 경멸 어린 입매로 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 동무인 루이즈 다르밀리 양과 함께 자기 방으로 물러난 외제니뿐이었다.\n\n수많은 하인들로 말하자면(그날 저녁에는 카페 드 파리에서 온 요리사와 집사들이 더해져 평소보다 그 수가 많았다), 자신들이 당한 모욕이라 일컫는 일에 대한 분노를 주인들에게 쏟아 내며, 자연히 중단된 제 임무 따위는 안중에도 없이 현관에, 부엌에, 또 제 방에 끼리끼리 모여들었다. 이 모든 집안 사람들 가운데 우리가 눈여겨볼 만한 이는 단 둘뿐이니, 바로 외제니 당글라르 양과 루이즈 다르밀리 양이다.\n\n앞서 말했듯이 약혼녀는 오만한 태도와 경멸 어린 입매로, 모욕당한 여왕 같은 거동으로 물러났고, 그녀보다 더 창백하고 더 동요한 동무가 그 뒤를 따랐다. 방에 이르자 외제니는 문을 잠갔고, 루이즈는 의자에 풀썩 주저앉았다.\n\n\"아, 정말 끔찍한 일이야.\" 젊은 음악가가 말했다. \"누가 짐작이나 했겠어? 안드레아 카발칸티 씨가 살인자에, 탈주한 갤리선 죄수에, 죄인이라니!\"\n\n빈정거리는 미소가 외제니의 입가에 떠올랐다. \"정말이지 난 운명이 정해져 있었나 봐.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"모르세르를 피했더니 카발칸티에게 떨어진 셈이니.\"\n\n\"오, 그 둘을 같이 놓고 보지는 마, 외제니.\"\n\n\"입 다물어! 남자들이란 죄다 파렴치한 족속이야. 이제 그들을 증오하는 데서 더 나아갈 수 있게 되어 기뻐. 난 그들을 경멸해.\"\n\n\"우린 어쩌지?\" 루이즈가 물었다.\n\n\"우리가 어쩌긴?\"\n\n\"응.\"\n\n\"뭐긴, 사흘 전부터 하기로 했던 그대로지. 떠나는 거야.\"\n\n\"뭐라고? 이제 결혼하지 않게 됐는데도, 그래도 여전히……?\"\n\n\"들어 봐, 루이즈. 난 이 사교계의 삶이 싫어. 늘 정돈되고, 재어지고, 우리 악보처럼 규칙에 묶여 있잖아. 내가 늘 바라고, 꿈꾸고, 갈망해 온 건 예술가의 삶이야. 자유롭고 독립적이고, 오로지 내 힘에만 기대고, 오직 나 자신에게만 책임지는 삶 말이야. 여기 남아? 무엇 때문에? 한 달 뒤에 또 나를 결혼시키려 들게? 그것도 누구한테? 어쩌면 전에 한 번 거론됐던 대로 드브레 씨한테? 아니, 루이즈, 아니야! 오늘 저녁의 사달이 내 핑곗거리가 되어 줄 거야. 난 핑계를 찾지도, 청하지도 않았어. 하느님이 이걸 내게 보내 주신 거고, 난 기쁘게 받아들이겠어!\"\n\n\"넌 어쩜 그리 강하고 용감하니!\" 가냘프고 여린 금발의 처녀가 거무스름한 머리의 동무에게 말했다.\n\n\"아직도 날 모르겠어? 자, 루이즈, 우리 일 얘기나 하자. 역마차는……\"\n\n\"다행히 사흘 전에 사 뒀잖아.\"\n\n\"우리가 가서 찾을 곳으로 보내 두라고 했고?\"\n\n\"응.\"\n\n\"여권은?\"\n\n\"여기 있어.\"\n\n외제니는 여느 때처럼 빈틈없는 손길로 인쇄된 종이 한 장을 펼쳐 읽었다.\n\n\"레옹 다르밀리 씨, 스무 살, 직업은 예술가, 머리 검정, 눈 검정, 누이와 함께 여행 중.\"\n\n\"훌륭해! 이 여권은 어떻게 구했어?\"\n\n\"몬테 크리스토 씨한테 로마와 나폴리 극장 지배인들에게 보내는 소개장을 부탁하러 갔을 때, 여자 몸으로 여행하는 게 두렵다고 말씀드렸거든. 그분은 내 마음을 완전히 헤아리시고는 남자 여권을 구해 주겠다고 하셨어. 그러고 이틀 뒤에 이걸 받았지. 거기에 내가 직접 손으로 '누이와 함께 여행 중'이라고 덧붙인 거고.\"\n\n\"좋아.\" 외제니가 들뜬 목소리로 말했다. \"그럼 이제 짐만 꾸리면 돼. 결혼식 날 저녁이 아니라 혼인 계약서에 서명하는 날 저녁에 떠나는 것뿐이야. 그게 다지.\"\n\n\"하지만 진지하게 생각해 봐, 외제니!\"\n\n\"오, 난 생각하는 건 이제 그만이야! 증권 시세니, 월말 결산이니, 스페인 공채의 오르내림이니, 아이티 채권이니 하는 소리만 듣는 데 진력이 났어. 그런 것 대신, 루이즈, 알겠어? 공기, 자유, 새들의 노랫소리, 롬바르디아의 평원, 베네치아의 운하, 로마의 궁전, 나폴리만이야. 우리 가진 게 얼마지, 루이즈?\"\n\n이 질문을 받은 처녀가 상감 세공이 된 책상에서 자물쇠 달린 작은 지갑을 꺼내, 그 안의 지폐 스물세 장을 세었다.\n\n\"2만 3천 프랑이야.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"거기에 적어도 그만큼은 진주와 다이아몬드, 보석으로 가지고 있지.\" 외제니가 말했다. \"우린 부자야. 4만 5천 프랑이면 이태는 공주처럼, 사 년은 넉넉하게 살 수 있어. 게다가 여섯 달도 안 돼서 — 넌 네 음악으로, 난 내 목소리로 — 우리 밑천을 두 배로 불릴 거야. 자, 넌 돈을 맡고, 난 보석함을 맡자. 그래야 둘 중 하나가 불행히도 제 보물을 잃더라도, 다른 하나에게는 제 몫이 남아 있을 테니까. 자, 이제 가방이야. 서두르자. 가방!\"\n\n\"잠깐!\" 루이즈가 당글라르 부인의 방문께로 가서 귀를 기울이며 말했다.\n\n\"뭐가 두려운데?\"\n\n\"우리가 들킬까 봐.\"\n\n\"문은 잠겨 있어.\"\n\n\"문 열라고 할지도 모르잖아.\"\n\n\"그러라지, 우린 안 열 거니까.\"\n\n\"넌 정말 아마조네스 같아, 외제니!\" 그러고 두 처녀는 챙겨야겠다 싶은 물건들을 죄다 가방 속에 쌓아 넣기 시작했다.\n\n\"자, 이제.\" 외제니가 말했다. \"내가 옷을 갈아입는 동안 넌 가방을 잠가.\" 루이즈가 작은 두 손으로 있는 힘을 다해 가방 뚜껑을 눌렀다.\n\n\"근데 안 돼.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"힘이 모자라. 네가 닫아.\"\n\n\"아, 부탁하길 잘했네.\" 외제니가 웃으며 말했다. \"내가 헤라클레스고 넌 그저 창백한 옴팔레라는 걸 깜빡했지 뭐야!\"\n\n그러고 처녀는 가방 위에 무릎을 꿇고 앉아 가방의 두 짝을 한데 눌렀고, 다르밀리 양이 자물쇠 빗장을 끼워 넣었다. 일이 끝나자 외제니는 자기가 열쇠를 지니고 있던 서랍을 열어, 그 안에서 솜을 댄 보라색 비단 여행용 망토를 꺼냈다.\n\n\"자.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"봐, 난 다 생각해 뒀어. 이 망토만 있으면 넌 춥지 않을 거야.\"\n\n\"그럼 넌?\"\n\n\"오, 알잖아, 난 추위를 안 타. 게다가 이 남자 옷을 입으면……\"\n\n\"여기서 갈아입을 거야?\"\n\n\"물론이지.\"\n\n\"시간이 되겠어?\"\n\n\"걱정 마, 이 겁쟁이야! 하인들은 죄다 그 대사건을 두고 떠드느라 정신없어. 게다가 내가 마땅히 빠져 있어야 할 슬픔을 생각하면, 내가 방에 틀어박혀 있다 한들 놀랄 일이 뭐가 있겠어? 말해 봐!\"\n\n\"그래, 정말 그러네. 네 말을 들으니 마음이 놓여.\"\n\n\"이리 와서 도와줘.\"\n같은 서랍에서 그녀는 장화부터 외투까지 남자 옷 한 벌을 꺼냈고, 군더더기 하나 없이 필요한 것은 다 갖춘 속옷 한 벌도 함께 꺼냈다. 그러고는 이성의 옷을 차려입고 즐기는 것이 이번이 처음이 아님을 보여 주는 듯한 날렵한 손놀림으로, 외제니는 장화와 바지를 꿰입고, 넥타이를 매고, 조끼를 목까지 단추로 채우고, 그 아름다운 몸매에 기막히게 들어맞는 외투를 걸쳤다.\n\n\"오, 아주 멋져. 정말이지 아주 멋져!\" 루이즈가 감탄하며 그녀를 바라보았다. \"그런데 그 아름다운 검은 머리, 모든 부인들이 부러워서 한숨짓게 만들던 그 멋진 땋은 머리, 저 아래 보이는 저런 남자 모자 속에 다 들어갈까?\"\n\n\"두고 봐.\" 외제니가 말했다. 그러고는 왼손으로, 긴 손가락으로도 다 움켜쥐기 힘든 그 풍성한 머리 다발을 잡고, 오른손으로 긴 가위를 집어 들었다. 이내 가윗날이 그 풍성하고 눈부신 머리카락을 가르고 지나갔고, 외투에 닿지 않게 몸을 뒤로 젖히자 머리카락은 다발째 그녀의 발치에 떨어졌다. 이어 그녀는 앞머리를 움켜쥐고 그것마저 잘라 냈는데, 조금도 아쉬운 기색을 내비치지 않았다. 오히려 그 흑단 같은 눈썹 아래의 눈은 여느 때보다 더한 기쁨으로 반짝였다.\n\n\"오, 저 멋진 머릿결을!\" 루이즈가 아쉬워하며 말했다.\n\n\"이러니까 백배는 더 낫지 않아?\" 외제니가 이제 완연히 남자 같은 모양새가 된, 흩어진 곱슬머리를 매만지며 외쳤다. \"이러니까 내가 더 잘생겨 보이지 않아?\"\n\n\"오, 넌 아름다워. 언제나 아름답지!\" 루이즈가 외쳤다. \"자, 이제 어디로 갈 거야?\"\n\n\"네가 좋다면 브뤼셀로. 거기가 제일 가까운 국경이야. 브뤼셀로 갔다가, 리에주, 엑스라샤펠로 가고, 그다음엔 라인강을 거슬러 스트라스부르로 가는 거야. 스위스를 가로질러서, 생고타르를 넘어 이탈리아로 내려가는 거지. 그럼 되겠어?\"\n\n\"응.\"\n\n\"뭘 보고 있어?\"\n\n\"너를 보고 있어. 정말이지 그러고 있으니 사랑스럽다! 누가 보면 네가 날 납치해 가는 줄 알겠어.\"\n\n\"그렇게 보는 게 맞지, 그럼!\"\n\n\"오, 너 방금 욕한 것 같은데, 외제니.\"\n\n그러고 두 처녀는, 누구라도 슬픔에 잠겨 있으리라 여겼을 — 하나는 제 처지 때문에, 다른 하나는 벗을 염려하느라 — 두 처녀는 까르르 웃음을 터뜨리며, 도주를 준비하느라 자연히 따라온 어질러진 흔적을 눈에 띄는 대로 죄다 치웠다. 그러고 불을 끈 두 도망자는, 목을 길게 빼고 잔뜩 긴장한 채 살피고 귀 기울이며, 옆 계단을 통해 마당으로 이어지는 의상실 문을 열었다. 외제니가 앞장서서 한 팔로 가방을 들었고, 그 반대편 손잡이는 다르밀리 양이 두 손으로도 간신히 들어 올렸다. 마당은 텅 비어 있었고, 시계가 열두 시를 치고 있었다. 문지기는 아직 잠자리에 들지 않았다. 외제니가 살그머니 다가가 보니, 늙은이는 수위실 안락의자에 앉아 곤히 잠들어 있었다. 그녀는 루이즈에게 돌아가, 잠시 바닥에 내려놓았던 가방을 다시 들었고, 두 사람은 담장 그늘을 따라 아치형 통로에 이르렀다.\n\n외제니는 문지기가 혹 깨더라도 한 사람밖에 보이지 않도록, 루이즈를 출입구 모퉁이에 숨겼다. 그러고는 마당을 비추는 등불 빛 한가운데 서서 말했다.\n\n\"문 좀 열어 주시오!\" 그녀가 가장 고운 콘트랄토 목소리로 외치며 창을 두드렸다.\n\n외제니가 예상한 대로 문지기가 일어났고, 심지어 나가는 사람이 누구인지 알아보려 몇 걸음 다가오기까지 했으나, 한 젊은이가 채찍으로 제 장화를 초조하게 탁탁 치고 있는 것을 보고는 곧바로 문을 열어 주었다. 루이즈가 반쯤 열린 문 사이로 뱀처럼 미끄러져 나가 가볍게 앞으로 뛰어나갔다. 겉으로는 차분해 보였으나 십중팔구 심장이 여느 때보다 좀 더 빠르게 뛰었을 외제니도 뒤이어 나갔다.\n\n마침 짐꾼 하나가 지나가기에 그들은 그에게 가방을 맡겼다. 그러고 두 처녀는 빅투아르 거리 36번지로 가져다 달라 이르고는, 그 사내의 뒤를 따라 걸었으니, 그가 곁에 있다는 것만으로도 루이즈는 마음이 놓였다. 외제니로 말하자면, 유디트나 델릴라처럼 굳세었다. 그들은 정해진 곳에 다다랐다. 외제니는 짐꾼에게 가방을 내려놓으라 이르고 동전 몇 닢을 쥐여 준 뒤, 덧창을 두드리고는 그를 돌려보냈다. 외제니가 두드린 덧창은 미리 언질을 받아 아직 잠자리에 들지 않은 어느 젊은 세탁부의 것이었다. 그녀가 문을 열었다.\n\n\"아가씨.\" 외제니가 말했다. \"짐꾼더러 마차 보관소에서 역마차를 가져오고, 호텔에서 역마를 데려오라고 해 주세요. 수고비로 5프랑 여기 있어요.\"\n\n\"정말이지.\" 루이즈가 말했다. \"난 네가 감탄스러워. 거의 우러러볼 지경이야.\" 세탁부는 놀란 눈으로 지켜보았으나, 20루이를 주기로 약속받은 터라 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n십오 분쯤 지나 짐꾼이 마부 한 명과 말들을 데리고 돌아왔다. 말들은 순식간에 멍에를 씌워 역마차에 매였고, 그동안 짐꾼은 끈과 가죽띠를 써서 가방을 단단히 동여맸다.\n\n\"여권 여기 있습니다.\" 마부가 말했다. \"어느 길로 갈까요, 젊은 양반?\"\n\n\"퐁텐블로로.\" 외제니가 거의 남자 같은 목소리로 대답했다.\n\n\"뭐라고?\" 루이즈가 말했다.\n\n\"따돌리려는 거야.\" 외제니가 말했다. \"우리가 20루이를 준 저 여자가 40루이를 받으면 우릴 팔아넘길 수도 있잖아. 곧 방향을 바꿀 거야.\"\n\n그러고 처녀는 발판에 거의 발을 대지도 않고 마차에 훌쩍 올라탔으니, 그 마차는 안에서 잠을 자기에 더없이 알맞게 꾸며져 있었다.\n\n\"넌 언제나 옳아.\" 음악 교사가 벗의 곁에 앉으며 말했다.\n\n십오 분 뒤, 제대로 된 길로 들어선 마부가 채찍을 한 번 휘둘러 생마르탱 관문의 출입구를 빠져나갔다.\n\n\"아.\" 루이즈가 후련하게 숨을 내쉬며 말했다. \"이제 파리를 벗어났네.\"\n\n\"그래, 얘. 납치는 완수된 사실이 됐어.\" 외제니가 대답했다.\n\n\"그래, 그것도 폭력 없이.\" 루이즈가 말했다.\n\n\"그건 정상 참작의 사유로 내세워야지.\" 외제니가 대답했다.\n\n이 말은 라빌레트의 포석 위를 구르는 마차 소리에 묻혀 버렸다. 당글라르 씨에게는 이제 딸이 없었다.\n\n# Chapter 98. The Bell and Bottle Tavern\n\n이제 당글라르 양과 그녀의 친구가 브뤼셀로 향하도록 내버려 두고, 출세의 길에 오르던 차에 그토록 야속하게 가로막힌 가엾은 안드레아 카발칸티에게로 돌아가자. 안드레아 도련님은 나이는 어렸어도 무척 영리하고 머리가 잘 도는 청년이었다. 우리는 이미 그가 살롱에 처음 소문이 닿자마자 슬그머니 문 쪽으로 다가가, 방을 두세 개 가로질러 마침내 모습을 감춘 것을 보았다. 그런데 한 가지 사정을 빠뜨렸으니, 그것은 결코 흘려서는 안 될 일이다. 그가 지나친 방들 가운데 하나에는 신부가 될 사람의 혼수가 진열되어 있었다. 다이아몬드 함이며, 캐시미어 숄, 발랑시엔 레이스, 영국제 베일, 한마디로 입에 올리기만 해도 처녀들의 가슴이 기쁨으로 뛰게 만드는 온갖 탐스러운 물건들, 이른바 코르베유였다. 그런데 안드레아는 이 방을 지나면서 자신이 영리하고 머리가 좋을 뿐 아니라 앞날을 내다볼 줄도 안다는 것을 증명했다. 눈앞에 놓인 장신구들 가운데 가장 값진 것을 챙겼던 것이다.\n\n이 노획물을 갖추고 나니 안드레아는 한결 가벼운 마음으로 창문에서 뛰어내렸고, 헌병들의 손아귀를 빠져나갈 작정이었다. 고대의 검투사처럼 키가 크고 균형 잡힌 몸매에, 스파르타 사람처럼 근육질인 그는 어디로 발길을 옮겨야 할지도 모른 채 십오 분을 걸었다. 머뭇거렸다가는 틀림없이 붙잡히고 마는 그 자리에서 멀어져야 한다는 생각 하나에만 사로잡혀 있었다. 도둑들을 늘 가장 안전한 길로 이끄는 본능에 따라 몽블랑가를 지나치고 나니, 그는 라파예트가의 끝에 와 있었다. 거기서 그는 숨이 차고 헐떡이며 걸음을 멈추었다. 그는 완전히 혼자였다. 한쪽에는 생라자르의 광막한 황무지가, 다른 한쪽에는 어둠에 휩싸인 파리가 펼쳐져 있었다.\n\n\"내가 붙잡히고 말 것인가?\" 그가 외쳤다. \"아니야, 적들보다 더 민첩하게 움직일 수만 있다면 그럴 일은 없어. 이제 내 목숨은 그저 속도에 달린 문제일 뿐이다.\"\n\n바로 그때, 그는 푸아소니에르 변두리 위쪽에 합승마차 한 대가 있는 것을 보았다. 굼뜬 마부는 파이프를 물고 생드니 변두리의 경계 쪽으로 느릿느릿 가고 있었으니, 필시 평소 그곳에 마차를 대 두는 모양이었다.\n\n\"이보시오, 친구!\" 베네데토가 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 일이십니까, 선생?\" 마부가 물었다.\n\n\"당신 말이 지쳤소?\"\n\n\"지쳤느냐고요? 아, 그럼요, 지칠 만도 하지요. 이 빌어먹을 하루 종일 한 일이 없으니까요! 형편없는 손님 넷에 이십 수가 더 붙어, 다 합쳐야 칠 프랑밖에 못 벌었습죠. 주인한테는 십 프랑을 갖다 바쳐야 하는데 말입니다.\"\n\n\"당신이 번 칠 프랑에 이 이십 프랑을 보태 주면 어떻겠소?\"\n\n\"기꺼이 받지요, 선생. 이십 프랑이면 무시할 액수가 아닙니다요. 그 대신 제가 뭘 하면 됩니까?\"\n\n\"아주 쉬운 일이오, 당신 말이 지치지만 않았다면.\"\n\n\"바람처럼 달릴 거라니까요. 어느 쪽으로 몰면 되는지만 말씀하십쇼.\"\n\n\"루브르 쪽으로.\"\n\n\"아, 그 길은 압니다. 거기 가면 단 럼주를 맛 좋게 한잔할 수 있지요.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇소. 내일 샤펠앙세르발에서 함께 사냥할 친구 하나를 따라잡으려는 것뿐이오. 그가 이륜마차를 끌고 열한 시 반까지 여기서 나를 기다리기로 했는데, 지금이 열두 시니, 기다리다 지쳐서 먼저 떠난 게 분명하오.\"\n\n\"그럴 만하지요.\"\n\n\"자, 그를 따라잡아 보겠소?\"\n\n\"그보다 더 좋은 일이 어디 있겠습니까.\"\n\n\"부르제에 닿기 전에 따라잡으면 이십 프랑을 주고, 루브르 전에 잡으면 삼십 프랑을 주겠소.\"\n\n\"그럼 정말로 따라잡으면요?\"\n\n\"사십 프랑.\" 안드레아가 잠시 망설인 끝에 말했다. 그러고는 그 정도는 마음 놓고 약속해도 된다는 것을 떠올렸다.\n\n\"좋습니다요.\" 마부가 말했다. \"어서 타십쇼, 출발합시다! 이랴ー!\"\n\n안드레아가 마차에 올라타자, 마차는 빠르게 생드니 변두리를 지나 생마르탱 변두리를 따라 달리고, 시문을 빠져나가, 끝없이 이어진 라빌레트를 누비고 나아갔다. 끝내 그 허깨비 같은 친구는 따라잡지 못했지만, 안드레아는 길에서 마주친 행인들과 아직 문을 닫지 않은 여관에 자주 초록빛 이륜마차와 밤색 말을 보지 못했느냐고 물었다. 그런데 저지대 지방으로 가는 길에는 이륜마차가 무수히 많은 데다, 그중 열에 아홉은 초록빛이었으므로, 그 물음은 걸음을 옮길 때마다 늘어났다. 누구나 방금 그것이 지나갔다고 했다. 오백 걸음, 이백 걸음, 백 걸음 앞에 있다고 했다. 마침내 따라잡고 보면, 그건 그 친구가 아니었다. 한번은 두 마리 역마가 빠르게 끄는 사륜마차가 합승마차를 앞질러 가기도 했다.\n\n\"아,\" 카발칸티가 속으로 중얼거렸다. \"저 사륜마차와 저 좋은 역마 두 마리, 무엇보다 저들을 거침없이 달리게 해 주는 여권만 있다면!\" 그러고는 깊이 한숨을 내쉬었다.\n\n그 사륜마차에는 당글라르 양과 다르밀리 양이 타고 있었다.\n\n\"서둘러, 서둘러요!\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"곧 따라잡아야 하오.\"\n\n그러자 가엾은 말은 시문을 떠난 뒤로 줄곧 이어 온 필사적인 질주를 다시 시작했고, 김을 펄펄 내뿜으며 루브르에 다다랐다.\n\n\"분명코,\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"내 친구는 못 따라잡겠지만, 당신 말은 죽이고 말겠군. 그러니 여기서 멈추는 게 낫겠소. 자, 여기 삼십 프랑이오. 나는 르 슈발 루주에서 자고, 첫 마차에 자리를 잡으리다. 잘 자시오, 친구.\"\n\n그러고서 안드레아는 오 프랑짜리 동전 여섯 닢을 마부의 손에 쥐여 주고는, 가뿐하게 길섶으로 뛰어내렸다. 마부는 기쁘게 그 돈을 주머니에 넣고 파리로 가는 길로 되돌아갔다. 안드레아는 르 슈발 루주 여관 쪽으로 가는 척했지만, 잠시 문에 기대어 시야에서 사라져 가는 마차의 마지막 소리를 듣고 나서는, 다시 길을 나서 우렁찬 걸음으로 이내 이 리외에 이르는 거리를 가로질렀다. 그러고 나서 그는 쉬었다. 가려던 척했던 샤펠앙세르발 가까이에 와 있을 터였다.\n\n여기서 안드레아의 발을 붙든 것은 피로가 아니었다. 무언가 결심을 세우고, 어떤 계획을 짜기 위해서였다. 역마차를 이용하기란 불가능했고, 역마를 빌리는 것도 마찬가지였다. 어느 쪽으로 가든 여권이 필요했다. 우아즈 도에 머무는 것은 더더욱 불가능했다. 프랑스에서 가장 트여 있고 가장 삼엄하게 감시되는 곳 가운데 하나였기 때문이다. 그것은 아예 말도 안 되는 일이었고, 범죄에 관해서라면 훤히 꿰고 있는 안드레아 같은 사람에게는 더욱 그러했다.\n\n그는 해자 옆에 앉아 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 생각에 잠겼다. 십 분 뒤 그가 고개를 들었다. 결심이 섰다. 그는 외투에 흙을 좀 끼얹었다. 무도회 의상 위에 단추를 채워 걸쳐 입은 그 외투는, 미리 현관 옷걸이에서 떼어 챙길 짬이 있었던 것이다. 그러고는 샤펠앙세르발로 가서, 그 마을의 단 하나뿐인 여관 문을 요란하게 두드렸다.\n\n주인이 문을 열었다.\n\n\"여보시오,\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"모르트퐁텐에서 상리스로 가던 참인데, 성가신 놈인 내 말이 발을 헛디뎌 나를 내동댕이쳤소. 오늘 밤 안으로 콩피에뉴에 닿지 못하면 우리 식구가 몹시 애를 태울 거요. 혹시 말 한 필을 빌려줄 수 있겠소?\"\n\n여관 주인이란 좋든 나쁘든 빌려줄 말 한 필쯤은 늘 가지고 있는 법이다. 주인은 마부 소년을 불러 르 블랑에 안장을 얹으라 이르고, 일곱 살배기 아들을 깨워 신사 앞에 태우고 가서 말을 도로 끌고 오라 일렀다. 안드레아는 주인에게 이십 프랑을 주었는데, 주머니에서 그것을 꺼내다가 명함 한 장을 떨어뜨렸다. 그것은 카페 드 파리에서 알게 된 친구 가운데 하나의 것이었으므로, 안드레아가 떠난 뒤 그것을 주운 주인은 자신이 생도미니크가 25번지에 사는 몰레옹 백작에게 말을 빌려준 것이라 굳게 믿게 되었다. 명함에 적힌 이름과 주소가 바로 그것이었기 때문이다.\n\n르 블랑은 빠른 짐승은 아니었지만, 편안하고 한결같은 걸음을 유지했다. 세 시간 반 만에 안드레아는 자신을 콩피에뉴와 갈라놓던 구 리외를 가로질렀고, 마차가 서는 곳에 다다랐을 때 네 시를 알리는 종이 울렸다. 콩피에뉴에는 한 번이라도 가 본 사람이라면 잊지 못할 훌륭한 여관이 하나 있다. 파리 일대를 말로 돌아다니며 그곳에 자주 묵었던 안드레아는 라 클로슈 에 라 부테유 여관을 떠올렸다. 그는 몸을 돌려, 반사된 등불빛에 간판을 보고는, 가진 잔돈을 모조리 아이에게 쥐여 주어 돌려보낸 뒤, 문을 두드리기 시작했다. 이제 서너 시간의 여유가 있으니, 푹 자고 잘 먹어 두어 내일의 고생에 대비하는 편이 낫다는 지극히 합당한 판단에서였다. 종업원이 문을 열었다.\n\n\"여보시오,\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"생장오부아에서 저녁을 들고, 자정에 지나가는 마차를 잡을 생각이었소. 그런데 바보같이 길을 잃어 지난 네 시간을 숲속에서 헤맸지 뭐요. 안뜰이 내려다보이는 그 예쁘장한 작은 방들 가운데 하나로 안내해 주고, 차가운 닭고기 한 마리와 보르도 한 병을 가져다주시오.\"\n\n종업원은 아무 의심도 하지 않았다. 안드레아는 완벽하게 침착한 어조로 말했고, 입에는 시가를 물고, 두 손은 외투 주머니에 찔러 넣고 있었다. 옷은 멋스럽게 지어졌고, 턱은 매끈했으며, 부츠는 흠잡을 데가 없었다. 그저 밤늦게까지 밖에서 놀다 온 사람으로 보일 뿐, 그 이상은 아니었다. 종업원이 방을 준비하는 동안 여주인이 일어났다. 안드레아는 더없이 매력적인 미소를 지으며, 지난번 콩피에뉴에 묵었을 때 썼던 3호실을 쓸 수 있겠느냐고 물었다. 공교롭게도 3호실은 누이와 함께 여행 중인 젊은이 하나가 이미 들었다고 했다. 안드레아는 낙담한 기색을 보였으나, 그를 위해 마련한 7호실이 3호실과 똑같은 자리에 있다는 여주인의 말에 마음을 달랬다. 그러고는 발을 녹이며 샹티이의 지난 경마 이야기를 나누면서, 방이 준비되었다는 기별을 기다렸다.\n\n안드레아가 라 클로슈 여관의 안뜰이 내려다보이는 예쁜 방들 이야기를 꺼낸 데에는 다 까닭이 있었다. 극장의 그것처럼 삼 층으로 둘러친 회랑에, 가느다란 기둥마다 재스민과 으아리가 휘감겨, 누구라도 상상할 수 있는 가장 아름다운 여관 입구의 하나를 이루고 있었던 것이다. 닭고기는 연했고, 포도주는 잘 익었으며, 불은 환하게 반짝였다. 안드레아는 아무 일도 없었던 것처럼 입맛 좋게 먹고 있는 자신에게 스스로 놀랐다. 그러고 나서 그는 잠자리에 들었고, 거의 곧바로, 스무 살 청년에게라면 회한에 가슴이 찢기는 처지에서도 어김없이 찾아드는 그 깊은 잠에 빠졌다. 그런데 우리는 여기서, 안드레아가 마땅히 회한을 느꼈어야 했음에도 그러지 않았다는 사실을 인정하지 않을 수 없다.\n\n이것이 그가 보기에 가장 안전을 기할 만한 계획이었다. 동트기 전에 깨어, 셈을 꼼꼼히 치르고 여관을 떠나, 숲에 이르면 그림 공부를 하는 척하면서 어느 농부의 환대를 시험해 나무꾼의 옷과 도끼를 손에 넣고, 사자 가죽을 벗어 던지고 나무꾼의 가죽을 뒤집어쓰는 것이었다. 그런 다음 손에는 흙을 묻히고, 머리카락은 납빗으로 검게 물들이고, 안색은 옛 동료 하나가 비법을 일러 준 약으로 거뭇하게 그을려, 숲이 우거진 지대를 따라가 가장 가까운 국경에 닿을 작정이었다. 밤에는 걷고 낮에는 숲과 채석장에서 자며, 이따금 빵 한 덩이를 사러 갈 때나 사람 사는 고장에 들어갈 셈이었다.\n\n일단 국경을 넘으면, 안드레아는 다이아몬드를 돈으로 바꿀 작정이었다. 그것을 만일을 대비해 늘 몸에 지니고 다니는 지폐 열 장과 합치면, 약 오만 리브르의 소유자가 되는 셈이니, 그는 이를 두고 따져 보아도 그리 한심한 처지는 아니라고 태평하게 여겼다. 게다가 그는 당글라르 일가가 자기네 망신을 둘러싼 소문을 덮으려고 애쓰리라는 데에 크게 기대를 걸고 있었다. 이런 이유들이 피로에 더해져, 안드레아는 그토록 깊이 잠들었던 것이다. 일찍 깨려고 그는 덧창을 닫지 않고, 문에 빗장만 지른 채, 탁자 위에 칼날이 길고 뾰족한, 잘 벼려진 줄을 잘 아는, 한시도 몸에서 떼어 놓는 법이 없는 칼 한 자루를 펼쳐 놓는 것으로 만족했다.\n\n아침 일곱 시쯤, 안드레아는 따뜻하고 눈부시게 얼굴 위에서 노니는 햇살 한 줄기에 잠을 깼다. 잘 정돈된 머릿속이라면 어디나, 가장 으뜸가는 생각이ー그런 생각은 늘 하나 있게 마련이다ー잠들기 전 마지막 생각이자 아침에 깨어나 처음 떠오르는 생각이 되는 법이다. 안드레아가 눈을 채 뜨기도 전에, 그 으뜸가는 생각이 떠올라 너무 오래 잤다고 귓가에 속삭였다. 그는 침대에서 벌떡 일어나 창문으로 달려갔다. 헌병 하나가 안뜰을 가로지르고 있었다. 헌병이란, 아무 거리낌 없는 사람에게조차 세상에서 가장 눈에 확 띄는 존재다. 하물며 양심이 켕기고, 또 그럴 만한 까닭이 있는 사람에게라면, 노랑과 파랑과 흰빛의 제복은 정말이지 등골이 오싹할 만큼 무서운 것이다.\n\n\"저 헌병이 왜 거기 있지?\" 안드레아가 스스로에게 물었다.\n\n그러고는 대뜸, 독자가 분명 그에게서 눈여겨보았을 그 논리로 스스로에게 대답했다. \"여관에 헌병이 있는 게 뭐 놀랄 일이라고. 놀랄 게 아니라 어서 옷이나 입자.\" 그러고서 청년은, 파리에서 두 달간 누린 멋쟁이 생활 동안에도 시종이 끝내 빼앗아 가지 못한 그 손쉬운 솜씨로 옷을 갖춰 입었다.\n\n\"자 그럼,\" 안드레아가 옷을 입으며 말했다. \"저자가 갈 때까지 기다렸다가 살짝 빠져나가야지.\"\n\n그렇게 말하면서, 이제 부츠를 신고 넥타이까지 맨 안드레아는 살그머니 창가로 다가가 두 번째로 모슬린 커튼을 들어 올렸다. 처음의 헌병이 여전히 그 자리에 있을 뿐 아니라, 청년은 이제 내려갈 수 있는 유일한 길인 계단 발치에 또 하나의 노랑과 파랑과 흰빛 제복이 서 있는 것을 알아차렸다. 게다가 말을 탄 세 번째 헌병이 주먹에 총을 쥔 채, 빠져나갈 유일한 통로인 큰 거리 쪽 대문에 보초로 서 있었다. 세 번째 헌병의 등장이 일을 결판 지었다. 호기심에 찬 구경꾼들이 떼 지어 그 앞에 늘어서서 여관 입구를 완전히 막고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"나를 노리는구나!\" 안드레아의 머리에 가장 먼저 떠오른 생각이었다. \"제기랄!\"\n\n청년의 이마에 핏기가 가셨고, 그는 불안하게 주위를 둘러보았다. 그의 방은, 같은 층의 다른 방들이 다 그렇듯, 모두가 보는 앞에서 회랑으로 통하는 출구 하나밖에 없었다. \"끝장이다!\" 두 번째로 떠오른 생각이었다. 정말이지 안드레아 같은 처지의 사람에게 체포란 곧 중죄 재판과 심리, 그리고 죽음을 뜻했다. 자비도 유예도 없는 죽음을.\n\n한순간 그는 발작하듯 두 손으로 머리를 움켜쥐었고, 그 짧은 동안 공포로 거의 미칠 지경이 되었다. 그러나 이내, 그의 마음을 어지럽히던 무수한 생각들 가운데 한 줄기 희망의 빛이 어렴풋이 비쳤고, 핏기 없는 입술과 창백한 뺨 위로 희미한 미소가 떠올랐다. 그가 둘러보니 찾던 것들이 벽난로 선반 위에 놓여 있었다. 펜과 잉크와 종이였다. 그는 억지로 침착을 꾸며 펜을 잉크에 적시고, 종이 한 장에 이런 글을 적었다.\n\n\"셈을 치를 돈이 없습니다. 그러나 저는 부정한 사람이 아닙니다. 그 액수의 열 배는 나갈 이 핀을 담보로 남겨 둡니다. 동틀 녘에 떠나는 것을 너그러이 보아 주시기를. 부끄러웠던 탓입니다.\"\n\n그러고는 넥타이에서 핀을 뽑아 종이 위에 놓았다. 그런 다음, 문을 잠가 두는 대신 빗장을 도로 풀고, 마치 방을 나서며 닫는 것을 깜박한 것처럼 문을 빠끔히 열어 두기까지 했다. 그러고는 그런 곡예에 이골이 난 사람처럼 벽난로 속으로 미끄러져 들어갔다. 아킬레우스와 데이다메이아를 그린 벽난로 가림판을 도로 제자리에 놓고, 재 위에 찍힌 제 발자국마저 지운 뒤, 이제 그에게 남은 유일한 탈출구인 그 텅 빈 굴뚝을 기어오르기 시작했다.\n\n바로 그때, 안드레아가 보았던 첫 번째 헌병이 경찰서장을 앞세우고 계단을 올라왔다. 계단을 지키던 두 번째 헌병이 그를 거들었고, 그 헌병은 또 대문에 배치된 헌병의 지원을 받았다.\n\n안드레아가 이 방문을 받게 된 데에는 이런 사정이 있었다. 동틀 무렵, 사방으로 통신기가 가동되었고, 거의 곧바로 각 구역의 당국이 카드루스를 살해한 자를 붙잡으려고 온갖 노력을 다 기울였다. 왕실의 거처이자 요새 도시인 콩피에뉴는 당국이며 헌병, 경찰서장이 넉넉히 갖춰진 곳이었다. 그래서 그들은 전신 급보가 도착하자마자 작전에 착수했고, 라 클로슈 에 라 부테유가 이 도시에서 가장 이름난 여관이었으므로, 자연히 그곳부터 가장 먼저 탐문에 나섰다.\n\n그런데 라 클로슈 에 라 부테유 바로 옆에 있는 시청을 지키던 보초들의 보고에 더해, 밤사이 여러 길손이 들었다는 다른 이들의 진술도 있었다. 아침 여섯 시에 교대한 보초는, 자신이 네 시 몇 분에 막 보초를 서던 참에 한 젊은이가 어린아이를 앞에 태우고 말을 타고 닿았던 것을 또렷이 기억했다. 젊은이는 아이와 말을 돌려보낸 뒤 여관 문을 두드렸고, 문이 열렸다가 그가 들어가자 다시 닫혔다고 했다. 이 늦은 도착이 큰 의심을 샀고, 그 젊은이가 바로 다름 아닌 안드레아였으므로, 경찰서장과 헌병ー그는 분대장이었다ー은 그의 방으로 발걸음을 옮겼다. 문은 빠끔히 열려 있었다.\n\n\"오호,\" 그 수법을 훤히 꿰뚫어 본 분대장이 말했다. \"문이 열려 있다니 불길한 징조야! 차라리 빗장이 세 겹으로 걸려 있는 편이 낫지.\"\n\n과연 탁자 위의 짧은 쪽지와 핀이 슬픈 진실을 확인해 주었다, 아니, 뒷받침해 주었다. 안드레아는 달아난 것이다. 우리가 뒷받침해 주었다고 말하는 까닭은, 분대장이 단 하나의 증거만으로 납득하기에는 너무 노련했기 때문이다. 그는 주위를 흘끗 둘러보고, 침대 안을 살피고, 커튼을 흔들어 보고, 벽장을 열어 보고, 마침내 벽난로 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 안드레아는 재 위에 발자국을 남기지 않으려 조심했지만, 그래도 그것은 출구였고, 그런 점에서 진지한 조사 없이 그냥 지나칠 수는 없는 것이었다.\n\n분대장은 나뭇가지와 짚을 가져오게 하여, 굴뚝을 그것으로 가득 채우고 불을 붙였다. 불이 탁탁 튀었고, 연기는 화산에서 피어오르는 흐릿한 수증기처럼 솟아올랐다. 그러나 그들이 기대한 대로 죄수가 떨어져 내리지는 않았다. 사실인즉, 어려서부터 줄곧 사회와 싸워 온 안드레아는, 비록 분대장으로 진급한 헌병이라 해도 그 못지않게 닳고 닳은 자였다. 불을 능히 짐작하고 있던 그는 이미 지붕으로 기어 올라가 굴뚝 갓 옆에 웅크리고 있었다.\n\n한때 그는 살았다고 여겼다. 분대장이 두 헌병에게 큰 소리로 \"여기 없다!\" 하고 외치는 소리를 들었기 때문이다. 그러나 슬쩍 엿보니, 그 헌병들은 그 말에 으레 그러리라 짐작할 법하게 물러가기는커녕, 도리어 한층 주의 깊게 살피고 있었다.\n\n이번에는 그가 주위를 둘러볼 차례였다. 그의 오른쪽에는 묵직한 16세기 건물인 시청이 있었다. 누구라도 그 탑의 창들에서 내려와, 아래에 펼쳐진 지붕의 구석구석을 살필 수 있었으니, 안드레아는 그 창들 가운데 하나에 당장이라도 헌병의 머리가 나타날까 봐 마음을 졸였다. 한번 발각되면 끝장이라는 것을 그는 알고 있었다. 지붕에는 도망칠 구멍이 없었기 때문이다. 그래서 그는 내려가기로 마음먹었다. 올라온 그 굴뚝이 아니라, 다른 방으로 통하는 비슷한 굴뚝을 통해서였다.\n\n그는 연기가 나지 않는 굴뚝을 찾아 둘러보았고, 그것에 이르자 아무에게도 들키지 않고 그 구멍 속으로 사라졌다. 바로 그 순간, 시청의 작은 창 하나가 활짝 열리며 헌병의 머리가 나타났다. 그것은 잠시 건물의 돌 장식처럼 미동도 없이 머물렀다가, 길게 한숨을 내쉬며 실망한 듯 사라졌다. 자신이 대변하는 법처럼 차분하고 위엄 있는 분대장은, 자신에게 쏟아지는 수천 가지 물음에는 대꾸 하나 없이 군중 사이를 지나, 여관 안으로 다시 들어갔다.\n\n\"어떻게 됐습니까?\" 두 헌병이 물었다.\n\n\"음, 이보게들,\" 분대장이 말했다. \"그 도적놈은 정말 오늘 이른 아침에 달아난 게 분명해. 하지만 빌레르코트레와 누아용 가는 길로 사람을 보내고 숲을 뒤지면, 틀림없이 잡고 말 거야.\"\n\n이 점잖은 관리가 헌병 분대장 특유의 그 억양으로 이렇게 말을 막 마쳤을 때, 종이 거칠게 울려 대는 소리와 함께 날카로운 비명이 여관 안뜰에 울려 퍼졌다.\n\n\"아니, 저게 무슨 소리야?\" 분대장이 외쳤다.\n\n\"어느 길손이 조바심이 났나 봅니다.\" 주인이 말했다. \"몇 호실에서 울린 거지?\"\n\n\"3호실입니다.\"\n\n\"종업원, 어서 가 봐!\"\n이 순간, 비명과 종소리가 한층 더 요란해졌다.\n\n\"가만!\" 분대장이 종업원을 멈춰 세우며 말했다. \"저렇게 종을 울리는 사람은 종업원 말고 다른 무언가가 필요한 모양이군. 우리가 헌병을 데리고 가 보지. 3호실에 든 게 누구지?\"\n\n\"어젯밤 누이와 함께 역마차로 닿은 그 작은 친구입니다. 침대 둘 딸린 방을 청했지요.\"\n\n이때 종이 세 번째로 울렸고, 또 한 번 괴로움에 찬 비명이 뒤따랐다.\n\n\"날 따라오시오, 서장님!\" 분대장이 말했다. \"내 발자국을 밟으며 오시오.\"\n\n\"잠깐만.\" 주인이 말했다. \"3호실에는 계단이 둘 있습니다. 안쪽 계단과 바깥쪽 계단이지요.\"\n\n\"좋아.\" 분대장이 말했다. \"안쪽 계단은 내가 맡지. 기총은 장전돼 있나?\"\n\n\"예, 분대장님.\"\n\n\"좋아, 자네는 바깥쪽을 지키게. 그자가 달아나려 하거든 쏘아 버려. 전신 내용을 보면 대단한 범죄자임이 분명하니까.\"\n\n분대장은 서장을 거느리고 안쪽 계단으로 사라졌다. 안드레아에 관한 그의 단언이 군중 사이에 불러일으킨 소란이 그 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n사정인즉 이러했다. 안드레아는 아주 능란하게 굴뚝의 삼분의 이를 내려왔으나, 그때 발이 미끄러져, 안간힘을 썼는데도 그가 작정한 것보다 더 빠르고 요란하게 방 안으로 떨어졌다. 방이 비어 있었다면 그쯤이야 대수롭지 않았겠지만, 불행히도 그 방에는 사람이 있었다. 한 침대에서 자던 두 여인이 그 소리에 잠을 깼고, 소리가 난 쪽으로 눈을 돌리니 한 남자가 보였다. 두 여인 가운데 금발의 여인이, 집 안을 쩌렁쩌렁 울리는 그 끔찍한 비명을 질렀고, 다른 여인은 종 줄로 달려가 있는 힘껏 종을 흔들어 댔다. 보다시피, 안드레아는 온통 불운에 에워싸여 있었다.\n\n\"제발 부탁이오,\" 그가 누구에게 말하는지도 보지 못한 채, 창백하고 얼이 빠진 얼굴로 외쳤다. \"제발 부탁이니 사람을 부르지 마시오! 나를 구해 주오! 해치지 않겠소.\"\n\n\"안드레아, 살인자야!\" 여인 가운데 하나가 외쳤다.\n\n\"외제니! 당글라르 양!\" 안드레아가 어안이 벙벙해져 외쳤다.\n\n\"사람 살려, 사람 살려!\" 다르밀리 양이 동행의 손에서 종을 빼앗아, 한층 더 거칠게 흔들어 대며 외쳤다.\n\n\"날 구해 주오, 쫓기고 있소!\" 안드레아가 두 손을 맞잡으며 말했다. \"제발, 인정을 베풀어 날 넘기지 말아 주오!\"\n\n\"이미 늦었어요, 저들이 오고 있어요.\" 외제니가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 어디든 날 숨겨 주오. 공연히 놀란 거라고 둘러대면 되지 않소. 저들의 의심을 돌려 내 목숨을 구해 줄 수 있잖소!\"\n\n두 여인은 서로 바싹 붙어, 이불을 몸에 단단히 끌어당긴 채, 이 애원하는 목소리에 아무 대꾸도 없이 잠자코 있었다. 혐오와 두려움이 그들의 마음을 사로잡고 있었다.\n\n\"좋아요, 그렇게 해요.\" 마침내 외제니가 말했다. \"올라온 그 길로 도로 돌아가요. 그러면 우리는 당신에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않을 테니까요, 가엾은 인간 같으니.\"\n\n\"여기 있다, 여기 있어!\" 층계참에서 한 목소리가 외쳤다. \"여기 있어! 내 눈에 보인다!\"\n\n분대장이 열쇠 구멍에 눈을 대고, 애원하는 자세의 안드레아를 발견한 것이다. 개머리판으로 한 번 세차게 후려치자 자물쇠가 터졌고, 두 번 더 치니 빗장이 빠져나왔으며, 부서진 문이 안으로 쓰러졌다. 안드레아는 회랑으로 통하는 다른 문으로 달려가 뛰쳐나가려 했다. 그러나 그는 우뚝 멈춰 섰고, 상체를 약간 뒤로 젖힌 채, 창백한 얼굴로 쓸모없는 칼을 움켜쥔 손을 늘어뜨리고 서 있었다.\n\n\"그럼 달아나요!\" 다르밀리 양이 외쳤다. 두려움이 잦아들자 그녀에게 다시 연민이 돌아온 것이다. \"달아나요!\"\n\n\"아니면 스스로 목숨을 끊든가요!\" 외제니가 말했다. (원형 경기장의 베스타 여사제가 승리한 검투사에게 쓰러진 적수에게 마지막 일격을 가하라고 재촉할 때 썼을 법한 어조였다.) 안드레아는 몸서리를 쳤고, 그토록 사나운 명예심을 자신이 얼마나 이해하지 못하는지를 드러내는 표정으로 그 처녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"내 목숨을 끊으라고?\" 그가 칼을 내던지며 외쳤다. \"내가 왜 그래야 하오?\"\n\n\"왜라니, 당신이 말했잖아요.\" 당글라르 양이 대꾸했다. \"당신이 가장 흉악한 범죄자처럼 사형 선고를 받을 거라고요.\"\n\n\"흥,\" 카발칸티가 팔짱을 끼며 말했다. \"사람한테는 친구라는 게 있는 법이지.\"\n\n분대장이 칼을 빼 들고 그에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"자자,\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"그 칼은 도로 집어넣으시오, 양반. 내가 스스로 항복하는 마당에 그렇게 부산을 떨 것 없잖소.\" 그러고는 수갑을 채우라고 두 손을 내밀었다.\n\n두 처녀는 이 부끄러운 탈바꿈을 공포에 질려 바라보았다. 사교계의 신사가 제 껍데기를 벗어 던지고 갤리선의 노예로 드러난 것이다. 안드레아가 그들 쪽으로 돌아서더니, 무례한 미소를 띠고 물었다. \"아버님께 전할 말씀이라도 있소, 당글라르 양? 아무래도 나는 파리로 돌아가게 될 것 같으니 말이오.\"\n\n외제니는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다.\n\n\"오호,\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"부끄러워할 것 없소. 그래, 당신이 날 뒤쫓아 왔다 한들 어떻소. 내가 당신 남편이 될 뻔하지 않았소?\"\n\n이런 비아냥을 남기고 안드레아는 밖으로 나갔다. 두 처녀는 제 부끄러움과 군중의 입방아에 고스란히 내맡겨진 채였다. 한 시간 뒤 두 사람은 여인의 옷차림을 하고 사륜마차에 올랐다. 그들을 시선에서 가려 주려고 여관 대문을 닫아 두었지만, 문이 열리자 호기심에 찬 눈길과 수군대는 목소리의 무리 사이를 지나야만 했다.\n\n외제니는 눈을 감았다. 그러나 볼 수는 없어도 들을 수는 있었으니, 군중의 비웃음이 마차 안의 그녀에게까지 닿았다.\n\n\"오, 어째서 이 세상은 황무지가 아닌가?\" 그녀가 다르밀리 양의 품에 몸을 던지며 외쳤다. 그녀의 두 눈은, 로마 세계에 목이 하나뿐이어서 단칼에 베어 버릴 수 있기를 바랐던 네로의 그것과 같은 분노로 번득였다.\n\n이튿날 두 사람은 브뤼셀의 오텔 드 플랑드르에 묵었다. 같은 날 저녁, 안드레아는 콩시에르주리에 수감되었다.\n\n# Chapter 99. The Law\n\n당글라르 양과 다르미이 양이 얼마나 조용히 변장을 마치고 달아났는지 우리는 이미 보았다. 사실 누구나 제 일에 정신이 팔려 그들의 일까지 신경 쓸 겨를이 없었다.\n\n은행가는 파산의 망령 앞에서 제 빚의 어마어마한 규모를 곱씹게 내버려 두고, 우리는 남작 부인을 따라가 보자. 부인은 자신을 덮친 타격의 무게에 잠시 짓눌렸다가, 평소 자문을 구하던 뤼시앵 드브레를 찾아 나섰다. 남작 부인은 이 결혼을 후견의 짐에서 벗어날 방편으로 여겨 왔다. 외제니 같은 성격의 처녀를 후견하는 일은 여간 성가신 일이 아닐 수밖에 없었으니까. 가족이라는 끈을 유지하게 하는 암묵적 관계 속에서, 어머니는 딸에 대한 우위를 지키려면 늘 지혜의 본보기이자 완벽의 전형이어야 하기 때문이다.\n\n그런데 당글라르 부인은 외제니의 영민함과 다르미이 양의 영향력이 두려웠다. 부인은 딸이 드브레를 바라볼 때 짓는 경멸 어린 표정을 자주 보아 왔다. 그것은 어머니와 그 측근 비서 사이의 애정 관계며 금전 관계를 모두 꿰뚫고 있다는 듯한 표정이었다. 게다가 부인은 외제니가 드브레를 혐오한다는 것을 알았다. 그가 아버지의 지붕 아래 불화와 추문의 근원이기 때문만이 아니라, 딸이 그를 단번에 그 부류의 두 발 동물 목록에 끼워 넣었기 때문이다. 플라톤이 인간이라는 이름에서 떼어 내려 애썼고, 디오게네스가 깃털 없는 두 발 짐승이라 불렀던 그 부류 말이다.\n\n불행히도 우리가 사는 이 세상에서는 누구나 어떤 매개를 통해 사물을 보고, 그래서 남들과 같은 빛으로 보지 못한다. 그리하여 당글라르 부인은 외제니의 결혼이 성사되지 못한 것을 몹시 아쉬워했다. 그 혼처가 좋아서, 또 제 딸의 행복을 보장해 줄 만해서이기도 했지만, 무엇보다 그 결혼이 자신을 자유롭게 해 줄 터였기 때문이다. 그래서 부인은 드브레에게 달려갔다. 드브레는 파리의 다른 사람들처럼 혼인 계약 장면과 그에 따른 추문을 목격한 뒤, 서둘러 자기 클럽으로 물러나 몇몇 친구들과 그 사건을 두고 이야기를 나누던 참이었다. 그 사건은 세계의 수도라 불리는 그 도시 사람들 사 분의 삼의 화젯거리가 되어 있었다.\n\n당글라르 부인이 검은 옷차림에 긴 베일로 얼굴을 가린 채, 젊은 남자는 집에 없다는 문지기의 단언에도 아랑곳없이 드브레의 거처로 통하는 계단을 오르고 있던 바로 그 시각, 드브레는 한 친구의 부추김을 물리치느라 여념이 없었다. 그 친구는, 방금 벌어진 끔찍한 장면을 겪었으니 가족의 벗으로서 당글라르 양과 그녀의 이백만 프랑을 맞아들여야 마땅하다고 그를 설득하려 했다. 드브레는 그리 강하게 부인하지 않았다. 그 생각이 이따금 그의 머리를 스쳤기 때문이다. 그러면서도 외제니의 독립적이고 자존심 강한 기질을 떠올리면, 도무지 불가능한 일이라며 단호히 물리쳤다. 하지만 그 같은 생각은 계속 되살아나 그의 마음속에 자리를 잡았다. 차와 카드놀이, 그리고 그처럼 중대한 일을 논하는 동안 흥미진진해진 대화는 새벽 한 시까지 이어졌다.\n\n그동안 당글라르 부인은 베일을 쓴 채 불안에 떨며, 그날 아침 자신이 보낸 꽃 두 바구니 사이에 앉아 그 작은 초록 방에서 드브레가 돌아오기를 기다렸다. 솔직히 말하자면, 드브레가 손수 그 꽃을 그토록 정성스레 매만지고 물을 준 덕분에, 가엾은 여인의 눈에는 그의 부재가 절반쯤 용서되었다.\n\n열두 시 이십 분 전, 당글라르 부인은 기다림에 지쳐 집으로 돌아갔다. 어떤 신분의 여인들은 한 가지 점에서 형편 좋은 바느질꾼 처녀들과 비슷하다. 열두 시가 넘어 집으로 돌아가는 일이 좀처럼 없다는 점이다. 남작 부인은 외제니가 빠져나갈 때 썼던 만큼이나 조심스럽게 저택으로 돌아왔다. 가벼운 걸음으로 계단을 올라, 쓰린 마음으로 자신의 방으로 들어섰다. 우리가 알다시피 외제니의 방과 붙어 있는 방이었다. 부인은 행여 무슨 말이 날까 두려웠고, 딸이 결백하며 아버지의 지붕에 충실하다고 굳게 믿고 있었다. 외제니의 방문 앞에서 귀를 기울였으나 아무 소리도 들리지 않자 들어가려 했지만, 빗장이 걸려 있었다. 그제야 당글라르 부인은 그 처녀가 저녁의 끔찍한 흥분에 지쳐 잠자리에 들어 잠든 모양이라고 결론지었다. 부인은 하녀를 불러 물었다.\n\n\"외제니 아가씨는,\" 하녀가 말했다. \"다르미이 아가씨와 함께 방으로 드셨습니다. 그러고는 둘이 차를 드시더니, 더는 필요 없다며 저더러 물러가라고 하셨고요.\"\n\n그 뒤로 하녀는 아래층에 있었고, 다른 모두와 마찬가지로 두 아가씨가 자기들 방에 있는 줄 알았다. 그래서 당글라르 부인은 한 점 의심도 없이 잠자리에 들어, 최근의 일들을 곰곰이 되새기기 시작했다. 기억이 또렷해질수록 그날 저녁의 일들이 본래의 빛으로 드러났다. 혼란이라 여겼던 것은 소동이었고, 괴로운 일쯤으로 여겼던 것은 실은 치욕이었다. 그리고 남작 부인은, 남편과 아들 때문에 못지않게 호된 타격을 입은 가엾은 메르세데스에게 자신이 아무런 연민도 느끼지 못했음을 떠올렸다.\n\n'외제니는,' 부인은 속으로 생각했다. '끝장이고, 우리도 끝장이야. 이 일이 소문나는 모양새로는 우리는 수치로 뒤덮일 테지. 우리 같은 사회에서 비웃음이란 아프고도 낫지 않는 상처를 입히니까. 그래도 외제니가 그 별난 성격을 지닌 게 얼마나 다행인지. 그 성격 때문에 내가 그토록 자주 떨곤 했지만!'\n\n그러고는 그녀의 시선이 하늘로 향했다. 그곳에서는 신비로운 섭리가 만물을 다스리며, 잘못에서, 아니 악덕에서조차 때로는 축복을 빚어낸다. 이윽고 그녀의 생각은 공중의 새처럼 허공을 가르며 카발칸티에게 가닿았다. 그 안드레아라는 자는 비열한 놈에 도둑이며 살인자였다. 그런데도 그 몸가짐에는 완전치는 않더라도 어떤 교육의 흔적이 배어 있었다. 그는 명예로운 이름을 등에 업고 어마어마한 재산을 가진 인물로 사교계에 소개되었었다. 어떻게 하면 이 미궁에서 빠져나올 수 있을까? 이 괴로운 처지에서 헤어 나오도록 누구에게 도움을 청해야 할까? 사랑하는 남자를 향한 여자의 첫 본능으로 달려갔지만 그 남자는 그녀를 저버리는 드브레, 그 드브레는 조언밖에 줄 수 없었다. 그보다 더 힘 있는 누군가에게 의지해야 했다.\n\n그래서 남작 부인은 빌포르 씨를 떠올렸다. 자기 가족이 마치 남남이라도 되는 양 가차 없이 불행을 끌어들인 사람이 바로 빌포르 씨였다. 하지만 아니다. 다시 생각해 보니 그 검사는 무자비한 사람이 아니었다. 부패의 핵심을 거칠지만 단호하게 도려낸 것은 의무의 노예인 법관이 아니라 벗, 충실한 벗이었다. 사교계에 사위로 소개되었던 그 망신스러운 젊은이와 당글라르라는 이름이 불명예스럽게 엮이는 것을 끊어 내려 한 것은 사형 집행인이 아니라 외과 의사였다. 당글라르의 벗인 빌포르가 이렇게 행동한 이상, 그가 안드레아의 음모를 미리 알았거나 거기에 손을 빌려주었다고는 아무도 생각할 수 없으리라. 그러니 곰곰이 따져 보면 빌포르의 행동은 서로의 이익을 위해 빚어진 것처럼 보였다. 그러나 검사의 그 완고함은 거기서 멈춰야 했다. 이튿날 그를 만나, 법관으로서의 의무를 저버리게 하지는 못할지언정, 적어도 그가 베풀 수 있는 한의 관용은 얻어 내리라. 지난날을 들먹이고 옛 추억을 떠올리게 하리라. 죄스러우면서도 행복했던 나날의 기억으로 그에게 애원하리라. 빌포르 씨라면 이 일을 덮어 줄 수 있을 터였다. 그저 한쪽으로 눈을 돌려 안드레아를 달아나게 하고, 법정 모독이라 불리는 죄의 그늘 아래 그 범죄를 처리하기만 하면 되었다. 이렇게 따져 보고 나서 부인은 편히 잠들었다.\n\n이튿날 아침 아홉 시에 부인은 일어나, 하녀를 부르지도, 자신이 깨어 있다는 기색을 조금도 내비치지도 않은 채, 전날 밤과 똑같이 수수한 차림을 했다. 그러고는 계단을 뛰어 내려가 저택을 나서서 프로방스 거리까지 걸어가 삯마차를 잡아타고 빌포르 씨의 집으로 향했다.\n\n지난 한 달간 이 불행한 집은 페스트가 번진 격리 병동 같은 음울한 모습을 띠고 있었다. 몇몇 방들은 안팎으로 닫혀 있었다. 덧창은 잠깐 바람을 들이려 열릴 뿐이었고, 그때마다 겁에 질린 하인의 얼굴이 보였다가 곧바로 창이 닫혔다. 무덤 위로 떨어지는 묘석처럼. 그러면 이웃들은 서로 나직이 수군거렸다. \"오늘도 검사 댁에서 또 장례가 치러질까?\"\n\n당글라르 부인은 저택의 황량한 모습에 자기도 모르게 몸서리쳤다. 마차에서 내려, 후들거리는 무릎으로 문에 다가가 종을 울렸다. 종은 둔하고 무거운 소리로 세 번 울렸다. 마치 그 집 전체의 슬픔에 한몫 끼기라도 하듯이. 그런 뒤에야 문지기가 나타나 말소리가 들릴 만큼만 문을 빠끔히 열고 안을 내다보았다. 그는 한 부인을, 멋스럽고 우아하게 차려입은 부인을 보았다. 그런데도 문은 거의 닫힌 채였다.\n\n\"문을 열 생각인가요?\" 남작 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"먼저, 부인, 누구십니까?\"\n\n\"내가 누구냐고요? 나를 잘 알 텐데요.\"\n\n\"우리는 이제 아무도 모릅니다, 부인.\"\n\n\"이보게, 제정신이 아니로군.\" 남작 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"어디서 오셨습니까?\"\n\n\"오, 이건 너무하잖아요!\"\n\n\"부인, 이건 제가 받은 명령입니다. 양해해 주십시오. 성함이?\"\n\n\"당글라르 남작 부인이에요. 나를 스무 번은 보았을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"그럴 수도 있지요, 부인. 그래서, 무슨 용건이십니까?\"\n\n\"오, 별일도 다 있군요! 빌포르 씨한테 하인들의 무례함을 일러바치겠어요.\"\n\n\"부인, 이건 무례가 아니라 조심입니다. 다브리니 선생님의 허락 없이는, 또 검사님과 말씀을 나누지 않고는 아무도 여기 들어올 수 없습니다.\"\n\n\"좋아요, 검사님께 볼일이 있어요.\"\n\n\"급한 용건입니까?\"\n\n\"그렇게 짐작할 만하잖아요. 마차도 안 끌고 나왔으니까요. 하지만 이쯤 됐으면 됐고, 여기 내 명함이에요. 주인께 갖다 드려요.\"\n\n\"부인, 제가 돌아올 때까지 기다려 주시겠습니까?\" \"그래요, 가 봐요.\"\n\n문지기는 당글라르 부인을 거리에 세워 둔 채 문을 닫았다. 오래 기다릴 것도 없었다. 곧바로 문이 그녀가 들어갈 만큼 넓게 열렸고, 그녀가 지나가자 다시 닫혔다. 안마당에 들어서기 무섭게 문지기는 한순간도 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 주머니에서 호루라기를 꺼내 불었다. 그러자 시종이 현관 계단에 나타났다.\n\n\"이 가엾은 친구를 너그러이 봐주십시오, 부인.\" 시종이 남작 부인을 앞서 안내하며 말했다. \"명령이 워낙 엄격해서요. 빌포르 씨께서 달리 어쩔 수 없다고 부인께 전하라 이르셨습니다.\"\n\n안마당에는 같은 절차를 거쳐 들여보내진 한 상인이 물건을 늘어놓고 있었다. 남작 부인은 계단을 올랐다. 그녀는 이 집을 짓누르는 슬픔에 단단히 물든 듯한 기분이었고, 그 슬픔은 자신의 슬픔까지 키우는 것만 같았다. 한순간도 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 않는 시종에게 여전히 이끌려, 그녀는 법관의 서재로 안내되었다.\n\n당글라르 부인은 방문의 목적에 온통 정신이 팔려 있었으면서도, 그 하인들에게서 받은 대접이 어찌나 모욕적으로 느껴졌던지 우선 그 일부터 불평하기 시작했다. 그러나 슬픔에 짓눌려 고개를 숙이고 있던 빌포르가 머리를 들어, 어찌나 서글픈 미소로 그녀를 올려다보던지 불평은 입가에서 사그라들었다.\n\n\"제 하인들을 용서해 주십시오.\" 그가 말했다. \"제가 탓할 수 없는 그 공포 때문이니까요. 의심을 받다 보니 의심하는 자들이 되어 버린 겁니다.\"\n\n당글라르 부인은 그 법관이 말하는 공포에 대해 여러 번 들어 왔지만, 제 눈으로 직접 보지 않았다면 그 감정이 이토록까지 번졌으리라고는 결코 믿지 못했을 것이다.\n\n\"그렇습니다, 부인.\" 법관이 대답했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 저를 가엾게 여겨 주시겠군요!\"\n\n\"진심으로요, 부인.\"\n\n\"제가 무슨 일로 여기 왔는지도 아시겠지요?\"\n\n\"방금 일어난 그 일에 대해 제게 말씀하시려는 것이지요?\"\n\n\"네, 검사님. 무서운 불행이에요.\"\n\n\"불운이라고 하시는 게 맞겠지요.\"\n\n\"불운이라고요?\" 남작 부인이 되뇌었다.\n\n\"안타깝게도, 부인.\" 검사가 한결같은 침착함으로 말했다. \"저는 돌이킬 수 없는 일만을 불행이라 여깁니다.\"\n\n\"이 일이 잊힐 거라고 생각하세요?\"\n\n\"모든 것은 잊히게 마련입니다, 부인.\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"따님은 오늘이 아니면 내일 결혼할 테고, 내일이 아니면 일주일 안에 하겠지요. 그리고 따님의 사윗감이 될 뻔했던 그 사람을 부인께서 아쉬워하시리라고는 생각하지 않습니다.\"\n\n당글라르 부인은 그가 거의 모욕적이리만치 침착한 데에 어안이 벙벙하여 빌포르를 바라보았다. \"제가 벗을 찾아온 게 맞나요?\" 그녀가 서글픈 위엄이 가득 밴 어조로 물었다.\n\n\"그렇다는 걸 잘 아시지 않습니까, 부인.\" 빌포르가 말했다. 그 다짐을 건네면서 그의 창백한 뺨이 살짝 붉어졌다. 사실 그 다짐은 그를, 지금 남작 부인과 그가 매달려 있는 일과는 다른 사건들로 데려갔던 것이다.\n\n\"그렇다면 좀 더 다정하게 대해 주세요, 빌포르.\" 남작 부인이 말했다. \"법관이 아니라 벗으로서 제게 말해 주세요. 그리고 제가 마음의 쓰라린 고통에 잠겨 있을 때, 즐거워해야 한다고는 말하지 말아 주세요.\" 빌포르가 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"불행이라는 말을 들으면, 부인.\" 그가 말했다. \"저는 지난 몇 달 사이에 제 자신의 불행을 떠올리는 못된 버릇이 들었습니다. 그러면 마음속으로 이기적인 비교를 하지 않을 수가 없지요. 그래서 제 불행 곁에 놓고 보면 부인의 불행은 한낱 불운으로밖에 보이지 않는 겁니다. 제 끔찍한 처지 탓에 부인의 처지가 부러워 보이는 것도 그래서지요. 하지만 이런 말이 부인을 불편하게 하는군요. 화제를 바꿉시다. 부인, 말씀하시던 게……\"\n\n\"여쭤 보러 왔어요, 벗이여.\" 남작 부인이 말했다. \"그 사기꾼이 어떻게 될지를요.\"\n\n\"사기꾼이라.\" 빌포르가 되뇌었다. \"정말이지, 부인, 부인께서는 어떤 경우는 가볍게 보시고 어떤 경우는 부풀려 보시는 것 같습니다. 사기꾼이라니요! 안드레아 카발칸티 씨, 아니 베네데토라는 자는, 다름 아닌 살인자입니다!\"\n\n\"검사님, 정정해 주신 게 옳다는 걸 부인하지는 않겠어요. 하지만 그 불행한 사람을 호되게 칠수록, 우리 가족도 그만큼 더 깊이 베이게 돼요. 자, 잠시만 그를 잊고, 뒤쫓는 대신 그냥 놓아주세요.\"\n\n\"너무 늦었습니다, 부인. 명령이 이미 떨어졌습니다.\"\n\n\"음, 만약 체포된다면, 정말 잡을 수 있다고들 보세요?\"\n\n\"그러길 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"만약 체포된다면(감옥에서도 때로는 빠져나갈 길이 생긴다는 걸 압니다만), 그를 감옥에 그대로 두시겠어요?\"\n\n검사가 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"적어도 제 딸이 결혼할 때까지만이라도 거기 두세요.\"\n\n\"불가능합니다, 부인. 사법에는 절차라는 게 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요, 저를 위해서도요?\" 남작 부인이 반은 농담으로 반은 진심으로 말했다.\n\n\"모두를 위해서요. 그중에는 저 자신도 들어갑니다.\" 빌포르가 대답했다.\n\n\"아!\" 남작 부인이 그 탄성이 드러내는 속생각은 입에 담지 않은 채 외쳤다. 빌포르는 마음속 비밀을 읽어 내는 그 꿰뚫는 눈길로 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"그래요, 무슨 말씀이신지 압니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"지난 석 달간 저를 상중에 묶어 둔, 그리고 발랑틴만이 기적적으로 면한 그 죽음들이 자연스러운 원인으로 일어난 게 아니라는, 세상에 떠도는 그 끔찍한 소문을 두고 하시는 말씀이겠지요.\"\n\n\"그 생각을 한 게 아니에요.\" 당글라르 부인이 얼른 대답했다.\n\n\"아니요, 그 생각을 하셨고, 또 그럴 만합니다. 생각하지 않을 수 없으셨겠지요. 그리고 속으로 이렇게 되뇌셨을 겁니다. '그토록 집요하게 죄를 뒤쫓는 당신이, 이제 대답해 보시오. 어찌하여 당신의 집에는 벌받지 않은 죄들이 있는 거요?'\" 남작 부인의 얼굴이 창백해졌다. \"이렇게 말씀하셨지요, 안 그렇습니까?\"\n\n\"좋아요, 인정해요.\"\n\n\"제가 대답해 드리지요.\"\n\n빌포르는 안락의자를 당글라르 부인 쪽으로 더 가까이 끌어당겼다. 그러고는 두 손을 책상에 얹은 채 여느 때보다 더 공허한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"벌받지 않은 채 남는 죄들이 있습니다. 범인이 누구인지 알 수 없어, 죄인 대신 무고한 사람을 칠 수도 있기 때문이지요. 하지만 일단 범인이 밝혀지면\" (여기서 빌포르는 책상 맞은편에 놓인 커다란 십자가 쪽으로 손을 뻗었다.) \"일단 밝혀지면, 제가 가장 신성하게 여기는 모든 것을 걸고 부인께 맹세하건대, 그가 누구든 반드시 죽을 것입니다. 자, 제가 방금 한, 그리고 반드시 지킬 이 맹세를 듣고서도, 부인은 그 비열한 놈을 위해 자비를 청하시겠습니까!\"\n\n\"하지만 검사님, 그가 사람들이 말하는 만큼 정말로 죄가 있다고 확신하세요?\"\n\n\"들어 보십시오. 이게 그자의 신원입니다. '베네데토, 열여섯 살에 문서 위조죄로 오 년 형을 선고받고 갤리선에 보내짐.' 보시다시피 싹수가 노랬지요. 처음엔 탈주범, 그다음엔 살인자였으니까요.\"\n\n\"대체 이 비열한 놈은 누구죠?\"\n\n\"누가 알겠습니까? 부랑자에 코르시카 사람이지요.\"\n\n\"그를 자기 자식이라 한 사람이 아무도 없었나요?\"\n\n\"아무도요. 그 부모가 누군지도 알 수 없습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 그를 루카에서 데려온 사람은 누구였죠?\"\n\n\"그자와 똑같은 또 다른 악당이지요. 어쩌면 공범일지도요.\" 남작 부인이 두 손을 맞잡았다.\n\n\"빌포르.\" 그녀가 더없이 부드럽고 매혹적인 태도로 외쳤다.\n\n\"제발, 부인.\" 빌포르가 다소 매정한 기색마저 서린 단호한 표정으로 말했다. \"제발, 죄지은 비열한 놈을 위해 제게 용서를 청하지 마십시오! 제가 무엇입니까? 법입니다. 법에게 부인의 슬픔을 지켜볼 눈이 있습니까? 법에게 부인의 감미로운 목소리에 녹아들 귀가 있습니까? 법에게 부인이 떠올리려 애쓰는 그 모든 애틋한 추억을 기억할 머리가 있습니까? 없습니다, 부인. 법은 명령했고, 명령할 때 법은 내리칩니다. 부인께서는 제가 법전이 아니라 살아 있는 존재요, 한 권의 책이 아니라 인간이라고 하시겠지요. 저를 보십시오, 부인. 제 주위를 둘러보십시오. 인류가 저를 형제로 대해 주었습니까? 사람들이 저를 사랑했습니까? 저를 살려 주었습니까? 부인께서 지금 제 손에 청하시는 그 자비를 누가 제게 보여 준 적이 있습니까? 없습니다, 부인. 그들은 저를 쳤고, 늘 쳤습니다!\n\n여인이여, 세이렌 같은 여인이여, 제가 얼굴을 붉혀야 한다고 일깨우는 그 매혹의 눈길을 기어이 제게 고정하시겠습니까? 좋습니다, 그렇게 하지요. 부인이 아시는 잘못들 때문에, 어쩌면 그보다 더한 잘못들 때문에 제가 얼굴을 붉히게 하십시오! 하지만 저 또한 죄를 지었기에, 어쩌면 남들보다 더 깊이 지었기에, 저는 제 동족의 위장을 벗겨 내고 그들의 약점을 찾아내기 전에는 결코 쉬지 않습니다. 저는 늘 찾아냈습니다. 그리고 더한 것은, 기쁨으로, 승리감으로 거듭 말하건대, 저는 늘 인간의 사악함이나 과오의 증거를 찾아냈다는 겁니다. 제가 단죄하는 죄인 하나하나가 제게는, 제가 나머지 인간들과 동떨어진 흉측한 예외가 아니라는 살아 있는 증거로 보입니다. 아, 아, 아, 온 세상이 사악합니다. 그러니 그 사악함을 칩시다!\"\n\n빌포르는 이 마지막 말을 열에 들뜬 분노로 내뱉었고, 그 분노가 그의 말에 사나운 웅변을 실어 주었다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 마지막 안간힘을 써 보기로 마음먹은 당글라르 부인이 말했다. \"이 젊은이는, 살인자이긴 해도, 모두에게 버림받은 고아예요.\"\n\n\"그러니 더 나쁘지요. 아니, 차라리 더 잘된 일입니다. 자기 운명을 슬퍼해 줄 이가 아무도 없도록 그렇게 정해진 거니까요.\"\n\n\"하지만 이건 약자를 짓밟는 거예요, 검사님.\"\n\n\"살인자의 약함을요!\"\n\n\"그의 불명예가 우리에게까지 미쳐요.\"\n\n\"우리 집에는 죽음이 있지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"오, 검사님.\" 남작 부인이 외쳤다. \"당신은 남들에게 자비가 없군요. 그렇다면 말씀드리지요. 사람들도 당신에게 자비를 베풀지 않을 거예요!\"\n\n\"그래도 좋습니다!\" 빌포르가 위협하는 몸짓으로 두 팔을 하늘로 치켜들며 말했다.\n\n\"적어도 다음 순회재판으로 재판을 미뤄 주세요. 그러면 우리에게 여섯 달의 여유가 생기잖아요.\"\n\n\"안 됩니다, 부인.\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"지시가 이미 내려졌습니다. 아직 닷새가 남았지요. 닷새면 제게 필요한 것보다 넉넉합니다. 저라고 망각을 갈망하지 않는 줄 아십니까? 밤낮으로 일하다 보면, 저도 가끔은 과거의 기억을 모조리 잊습니다. 그러면 죽은 자들이 느끼리라 짐작되는 것과 같은 종류의 행복을 맛보지요. 그래도 그게 괴로워하는 것보다는 낫습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 검사님, 그는 달아났어요. 그냥 도망치게 두세요. 가만히 있는 건 용서받을 만한 잘못이잖아요.\"\n\n\"이미 늦었다고 말씀드리지 않습니까. 오늘 이른 아침에 전신을 띄웠으니, 바로 이 순간에도……\"\n\n\"검사님.\" 시종이 방으로 들어서며 말했다. \"용기병 하나가 내무대신께서 보내신 이 급보를 가져왔습니다.\"\n\n빌포르가 편지를 낚아채 황급히 봉인을 뜯었다. 당글라르 부인은 두려움에 떨었고, 빌포르는 기쁨에 흠칫했다.\n\n\"체포되었어요!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"콩피에뉴에서 붙잡혔답니다. 이제 다 끝났습니다.\"\n\n당글라르 부인은 창백하고 싸늘한 얼굴로 자리에서 일어섰다.\n\n\"안녕히 계세요, 검사님.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"안녕히 가십시오, 부인.\" 검사가 거의 기쁜 태도로 그녀를 문까지 배웅하며 대답했다. 그러고는 책상으로 돌아서서, 오른손 등으로 편지를 탁 치며 말했다.\n\n\"자, 위조 한 건에 절도 세 건, 방화 두 건이 있었고, 살인 하나만 있으면 됐는데, 여기 있구나. 멋진 개정이 되겠어!\"\n\n# Chapter 100. The Apparition\n\n빌포르가 당글라르 부인에게 말한 대로, 발랑틴은 아직 회복되지 않았다. 피로에 짓눌려 침대에 누워 있을 수밖에 없었다. 그녀는 자기 방에서, 빌포르 부인의 입을 통해 우리가 이야기한 그 기이한 사건들을 모두 전해 들었다. 외제니의 도주와 안드레아 카발칸티, 정확히는 베네데토의 체포, 그리고 그에게 내려진 살인 혐의에 관한 이야기였다. 그러나 발랑틴은 너무 쇠약했기에, 이 이야기가 평소의 건강한 상태였더라면 일으켰을 충격을 거의 주지 못했다. 사실 그녀의 머릿속에는 막연한 생각과 흐릿한 형상만이 자리 잡았고, 기이한 환상이 뒤섞여 눈앞에 떠오를 뿐이었다.\n\n낮 동안에는 발랑틴의 의식이 비교적 또렷했다. 노아르티에 씨가 늘 곁에 있었던 덕분이었다. 그는 손녀의 방으로 자신을 데려가게 하여 아버지처럼 자애로운 눈길로 그녀를 지켰다. 빌포르 역시 법원에서 돌아오면 아버지와 딸 곁에서 한두 시간씩 보내곤 했다.\n\n여섯 시가 되면 빌포르는 서재로 물러갔고, 여덟 시에는 다브리니 씨가 손수 와서 어린 처녀를 위해 마련한 밤 약을 가져왔다. 그러면 노아르티에 씨는 방에서 옮겨졌다. 의사가 고른 간병인이 그들을 대신해 자리를 지켰고, 발랑틴이 잠들고 나서야, 대략 열 시나 열한 시쯤이 되어서야 방을 떠났다. 그녀는 아래층으로 내려가면서 발랑틴의 방 열쇠를 빌포르 씨에게 건넸다. 그리하여 빌포르 부인과 어린 에두아르의 방을 거치지 않고는 아무도 병실에 들어갈 수 없었다.\n\n아침마다 모렐은 노아르티에를 찾아와 발랑틴의 소식을 들었다. 놀랍게도, 그는 날이 갈수록 불안이 줄어드는 것을 느꼈다. 발랑틴이 여전히 끔찍한 신경 발작에 시달리기는 했지만 분명 차도가 있었다. 게다가 그가 반쯤 정신이 나간 채 백작의 집으로 달려갔을 때, 몬테크리스토는 그녀가 두 시간 안에 죽지 않으면 살아날 것이라고 말했었다. 그런데 나흘이 지났는데도 발랑틴은 여전히 살아 있었다.\n\n우리가 말한 그 신경 발작은 잠 속에서도, 정확히는 깨어 있던 시간 뒤에 찾아오는 그 비몽사몽의 상태 속에서도 발랑틴을 따라다녔다. 밤의 고요 속에서, 벽난로 위 설화석고 등잔이 드리우는 희미한 빛 속에서, 그녀는 병상 위를 맴도는 그림자들이 오가는 것을 보았다. 그것들은 떨리는 날개로 열을 부채질했다. 처음에 그녀는 계모가 자신을 위협하는 모습을 본 듯했고, 그다음엔 모렐이 그녀를 향해 두 팔을 뻗었다. 때로는 몬테크리스토 백작 같은 낯선 이들이 찾아오기도 했다. 이 헛소리의 순간에는 가구마저 움직이는 듯했다. 이런 상태는 새벽 세 시쯤까지 이어졌고, 그제야 깊고 무거운 잠이 어린 처녀를 덮쳤다. 그녀는 날이 밝을 때까지 깨지 않았다.\n\n발랑틴이 외제니의 도주와 베네데토의 체포 소식을 들은 그날 저녁, 빌포르도 노아르티에도 다브리니도 모두 물러간 뒤, 그녀의 생각은 혼란스러운 미로 속을 헤맸다. 자신의 처지와 방금 들은 사건들을 번갈아 되짚었다.\n\n열한 시를 알리는 종이 울렸다. 간병인은 의사가 마련한 음료를 환자의 손이 닿는 곳에 놓고 문을 잠근 뒤, 부엌에서 하인들이 주고받는 이야기에 겁먹은 채 귀를 기울였다. 그리고 몇 달 전부터 검사장 댁 대기실 사람들의 입에 오르내리던 온갖 끔찍한 이야기들을 기억에 새겨 넣었다. 그러는 사이, 그토록 단단히 잠가 두었던 방 안에서는 뜻밖의 장면이 벌어지고 있었다.\n\n간병인이 떠난 지 십 분이 지났다. 한 시간 전부터 발랑틴은 밤마다 되돌아오는 열에 시달리고 있었다. 생각을 다스릴 수 없었던 그녀는 똑같은 환상과 영상을 끊임없이 만들어 내고 되풀이하다가 끝내 지쳐 버리는 그 흥분에 굴복할 수밖에 없었다. 야등은 수많은 빛줄기를 뿜어냈고, 그 하나하나가 그녀의 어지러운 상상 속에서 기이한 형상으로 변했다. 그러던 중 그 깜빡이는 빛 속에서, 발랑틴은 벽난로 옆 움푹 들어간 곳에 있는 서재 문이 천천히 열리는 것을 본 듯했다. 그러나 문이 돌아가는 돌쩌귀 소리를 들으려 애써도 아무 소리도 들리지 않았다.\n\n다른 때였다면 발랑틴은 비단 초인종 끈을 잡아당겨 도움을 청했을 것이다. 그러나 지금의 상태에서는 어떤 것에도 놀라지 않았다. 그녀가 보는 모든 환상은 상상이 빚어낸 것일 뿐이라고 이성이 말해 주었다. 게다가 밤의 유령들은 날이 밝으면 사라져 아침이면 흔적조차 남지 않는다는 사실이 그 확신을 굳혀 주었다.\n\n문 뒤에서 사람의 형체가 나타났다. 그러나 처녀는 그런 환영에 너무 익숙해진 터라 놀라지 않았다. 그저 모렐을 알아보길 바라며 가만히 바라볼 뿐이었다. 형체는 침대 쪽으로 다가오더니 무언가에 깊이 귀 기울이는 듯했다. 그 순간 한 줄기 빛이 한밤의 방문객의 얼굴을 스쳤다.\n\n\"그가 아니야.\" 그녀가 중얼거렸다. 그리고 이것이 꿈일 뿐이라 믿으며, 그 사람이 사라지거나 다른 모습으로 변하기를 기다렸다. 그래도 그녀는 자기 맥을 짚어 보았고, 맥이 거세게 뛰는 것을 알자, 그런 환상을 떨쳐 내는 가장 좋은 방법은 무언가를 마시는 것임을 떠올렸다. 의사가 열을 가라앉히려고 마련한 음료를 한 모금 마시면 머리에 반응이 일어, 잠시나마 고통이 덜해지는 듯했기 때문이다. 그래서 발랑틴은 잔을 향해 손을 뻗었다. 그러나 떨리는 팔이 침대를 벗어나기가 무섭게 환영은 더 빠르게 다가왔고, 어린 처녀에게 바싹 다가서서 그녀는 그의 숨소리를 들은 것 같았고 그의 손이 누르는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n이번에는 그 환각이, 아니 차라리 그 실제가, 발랑틴이 전에 겪었던 그 무엇보다도 생생했다. 그녀는 자신이 정말로 살아 깨어 있다고 믿기 시작했다. 이번만큼은 이성이 속고 있지 않다는 믿음에 그녀는 몸서리쳤다. 그녀가 느낀 압박은 분명 자신의 팔을 막으려는 것이었고, 그녀는 천천히 팔을 거두어들였다. 그러자 그녀가 눈을 뗄 수 없었던 그 형체는, 위협하기보다는 보호하려는 듯이, 잔을 집어 들고 야등 쪽으로 걸어가 마치 그 투명함을 살피려는 듯 빛에 비추어 보았다. 그것으로는 충분치 않은 모양이었다. 그 사내는, 아니 그 유령은 — 어찌나 발소리가 없는지 아무 소리도 들리지 않았으니 — 잔에 한 숟가락쯤 따라서 마셨다.\n\n발랑틴은 멍한 심정으로 이 장면을 지켜보았다. 매 순간 그것이 사라지고 또 다른 환상이 자리를 대신하리라 기대했다. 그러나 그 사내는 그림자처럼 흩어지기는커녕 다시 그녀에게 다가오더니 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"이제 마셔도 됩니다.\"\n\n발랑틴은 몸서리쳤다. 이런 환영들 가운데 하나가 살아 있는 목소리로 그녀에게 말을 건 것은 처음이었다. 그녀가 막 비명을 지르려는데, 사내가 그녀의 입술에 손가락을 갖다 댔다.\n\n\"몬테크리스토 백작님!\" 그녀가 중얼거렸다.\n\n이제 어린 처녀의 마음속에는 이 장면이 실제라는 데 한 점 의심도 남지 않았음을 쉽게 알 수 있었다. 그녀의 눈은 공포로 휘둥그레졌고, 손은 떨렸으며, 이불을 황급히 끌어당겨 몸을 감쌌다. 그래도 이런 시각에 몬테크리스토가 와 있다는 것, 벽을 통해 그녀의 방으로 들어온 그 신비롭고 기괴하며 터무니없는 등장은, 무너져 버린 그녀의 이성에는 도무지 있을 수 없는 일로만 보였다.\n\n\"아무도 부르지 마시오. 놀라지도 마시오.\" 백작이 말했다. \"한 점의 의심이나 불안도 가슴에 남겨 두지 마시오. 당신 앞에 서 있는 이 사람은, 발랑틴 — 이번엔 유령이 아니오 — 당신이 꿈꿀 수 있는 가장 다정한 아버지요, 가장 깍듯한 친구일 뿐이라오.\"\n\n발랑틴은 대답할 수 없었다. 방 안에 진짜로 존재하는 누군가를 알려 주는 그 목소리에 그녀는 너무도 겁이 나서 한마디도 입 밖에 낼 수 없었다. 그래도 그녀의 눈빛은 이렇게 묻는 듯했다. '당신의 뜻이 순수하다면, 어찌하여 여기 있는 거죠?' 백작은 놀라운 통찰력으로 어린 처녀의 마음속에 오가는 모든 것을 알아챘다.\n\n\"내 말을 들으시오.\" 그가 말했다. \"아니, 차라리 나를 보시오. 평소보다 더 창백한 내 얼굴을, 피로로 붉어진 내 눈을 보시오. 나흘 동안 한숨도 붙이지 못했소. 막시밀리앙을 위해 당신을 지키고 살리려고, 줄곧 당신을 지켜보았기 때문이오.\"\n\n발랑틴의 뺨에 피가 빠르게 올라왔다. 방금 백작이 입에 올린 그 이름이, 그의 존재가 불러일으켰던 모든 두려움을 흩어 놓았기 때문이다.\n\n\"막시밀리앙!\" 그녀가 외쳤다. 그 소리가 어찌나 달콤하게 들렸던지, 그녀는 다시 한번 되뇌었다. \"막시밀리앙! 그가 당신에게 모든 걸 털어놓았군요?\"\n\n\"전부 다요. 그는 자기 목숨이 당신에게 달려 있다고 말했고, 나는 당신을 살리겠다고 그에게 약속했소.\"\n\n\"제가 살아날 거라고 그에게 약속하셨다고요?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"하지만, 선생님, 방금 지키고 보호한다고 하셨잖아요. 의사이신가요?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 지금 이 순간 당신이 가질 수 있는 가장 훌륭한 의사라오, 믿으시오.\"\n\n\"하지만 줄곧 지켜보셨다고요?\" 발랑틴이 불안하게 물었다. \"어디 계셨나요? 저는 뵌 적이 없는데요.\"\n\n백작은 서재 쪽으로 손을 뻗었다.\n\n\"저 문 뒤에 숨어 있었소.\" 그가 말했다. \"저 문은 옆집으로 통하는데, 그 집을 내가 빌렸다오.\"\n\n발랑틴은 눈길을 돌렸다. 그리고 분노에 찬 자존심과 조심스러운 두려움이 어린 표정으로 외쳤다.\n\n\"선생님, 제가 보기에 선생님은 유례없는 무례를 저지르셨어요. 보호라고 하시는 것이 차라리 모욕에 가까워요.\"\n\n\"발랑틴,\" 그가 대답했다. \"당신을 오래 지켜보는 동안, 내가 살핀 것은 누가 당신을 찾아왔는지, 어떤 음식이 준비되고 어떤 음료가 나오는지뿐이었소. 그러다 그 음료가 위험해 보이면, 지금처럼 들어와 독약 대신 몸에 이로운 약을 갈아 두었소. 그 약은 당신을 죽이려던 그 독 대신, 당신의 핏줄에 생명을 돌게 했지요.\"\n\n\"독약, 죽음이라니!\" 발랑틴이 외쳤다. 그녀는 자신이 어떤 열에 들뜬 환각에 빠져 있는 게 아닌가 반쯤 의심하고 있었다. \"무슨 말씀이세요, 선생님?\"\n\n\"쉿, 내 아이.\" 몬테크리스토가 다시 그녀의 입술에 손가락을 갖다 대며 말했다. \"분명 독약이라, 죽음이라 했소. 하지만 이걸 좀 마시구려.\" 백작은 주머니에서 붉은 액체가 담긴 작은 병을 꺼내, 잔에 몇 방울 떨어뜨렸다. \"이걸 마시고, 오늘 밤엔 더 이상 아무것도 들지 마시오.\"\n\n발랑틴은 손을 뻗었으나, 잔에 닿기가 무섭게 겁이 나 다시 거두었다. 몬테크리스토는 잔을 들어 절반을 마신 뒤 발랑틴에게 건넸고, 그녀는 미소 지으며 나머지를 삼켰다.\n\n\"아, 그래요.\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"저를 그토록 개운하게 해 주고, 욱신거리는 머리를 가라앉혀 주는 듯했던 그 밤 음료의 맛이 나요. 고맙습니다, 선생님, 고맙습니다!\"\n\n\"지난 나흘 밤 동안 당신은 이렇게 살아남은 것이라오, 발랑틴.\" 백작이 말했다. \"하지만, 아, 나는 그 시간을 어떻게 보냈는지! 아, 내가 견뎌 낸 그 비참한 시간들이라니 — 당신 잔에 치명적인 독이 부어지는 것을 보았을 때 내가 감당한 그 고문이라니. 그리고 그것을 내가 미처 버릴 틈도 없이 당신이 마셔 버릴까 봐 얼마나 떨었던지!\"\n\n\"선생님,\" 발랑틴이 극도의 공포에 사로잡혀 말했다. \"제 잔에 치명적인 독이 부어지는 것을 보고 고문을 견디셨다고 하셨죠. 하지만 그걸 보셨다면, 그걸 부은 사람도 보셨겠군요?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n발랑틴은 침대에서 몸을 일으켜, 눈보다 희게 보이는 가슴 위로 수놓은 고운 천을 끌어당겼다. 그 천은 헛소리의 차가운 식은땀으로 아직 축축했고, 이제 거기엔 공포의 식은땀까지 더해졌다. \"그 사람을 보셨다고요?\" 처녀가 거듭 물었다.\n\n\"그렇소.\" 백작이 거듭 말했다.\n\n\"선생님, 말씀이 끔찍해요. 너무도 무서운 것을 제게 믿게 하려 하시는군요. 뭐라고요? 제 아버지의 집에서, 제 방에서, 병상에 누운 저를 죽이려 했다고요? 아, 저를 내버려 두세요, 선생님. 저를 부추기고 계세요. 섭리의 선함마저 의심하게 만드시네요. 그럴 리 없어요, 그럴 수가 없어요!\"\n\n\"이 손이 친 첫 번째 사람이 당신이라 생각하오? 생메랑 씨가, 생메랑 부인이, 바루아가 모두 쓰러지는 것을 보지 못했소? 지난 삼 년간 받아 온 치료가 독의 효력을 누그러뜨리지 않았더라면, 노아르티에 씨 또한 희생되지 않았겠소?\"\n\n\"오, 하느님.\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"그래서 할아버지가 지난 한 달 동안 당신의 모든 음료를 저와 나누어 드시게 한 거였군요?\"\n\n\"그 음료들이 모두 말린 오렌지 껍질 같은, 약간 쌉쌀한 맛이 나지 않았소?\"\n\n\"아, 그래요, 맞아요!\"\n\n\"그렇다면 모든 게 설명되는군.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"당신 할아버지는 이 집에 독을 쓰는 자가 산다는 것을 알고 있소. 어쩌면 그자가 누구인지 짐작하고 있을지도 모르오. 그분은 자신의 사랑하는 아이인 당신을 독의 치명적인 효력에 견딜 수 있도록 단련시켜 온 것이오. 그래서 독이 듣지 않았던 거요. 당신의 몸이 이미 그것에 길들여져 있었으니까. 하지만 나흘 전에 쓴 더 치명적인 독에는 그것조차 거의 소용이 없었을 거요. 그 독은 대개 너무도 어김없이 목숨을 앗아 가니까.\"\n\n\"하지만 그 살인자가, 그 흉악범이 대체 누군가요?\"\n\n\"나도 한 가지 묻고 싶소. 밤에 누군가 당신 방에 들어오는 것을 본 적이 없소?\"\n\n\"아, 있어요. 제 곁을 스쳐 지나가 다가왔다가 사라지는 그림자들을 자주 봤어요. 하지만 열에 들뜬 상상이 빚어낸 환영이라 여겼지요. 사실 선생님이 들어오셨을 때도 제가 헛것을 보고 있는 줄 알았어요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 당신은 누가 당신의 목숨을 노리는지 모르는 거요?\"\n\n\"몰라요.\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"누가 제 죽음을 바라겠어요?\"\n\n\"이제 곧 알게 될 거요.\" 몬테크리스토가 귀를 기울이며 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 뜻이세요?\" 발랑틴이 불안하게 주위를 둘러보며 물었다.\n\n\"오늘 밤 당신은 열에 들뜨지도, 헛소리를 하지도 않고, 또렷이 깨어 있으니까. 지금 자정을 알리는 종이 울리고 있소. 살인자들이 택하는 시각이지요.\"\n\n\"오, 맙소사.\" 발랑틴이 이마를 타고 흐르는 땀방울을 닦으며 외쳤다. 자정을 알리는 종이 느리고도 구슬프게 울렸다. 매 시각의 종소리가 가엾은 처녀의 가슴을 납덩이처럼 무겁게 짓누르는 듯했다.\n\n\"발랑틴,\" 백작이 말했다. \"온 힘을 다해 용기를 내시오. 심장의 고동을 가라앉히고, 어떤 소리도 내지 말고, 잠든 척하시오. 그러면 보게 될 거요.\"\n\n발랑틴이 백작의 손을 붙잡았다. \"무슨 소리가 들리는 것 같아요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"어서 가세요.\"\n\n\"그럼 잠시 작별이오.\" 백작이 발끝으로 서재 문을 향해 걸어가며 대답했다. 그는 어찌나 슬프고도 아버지 같은 표정으로 미소 지었던지, 어린 처녀의 가슴은 감사로 가득 찼다.\n\n문을 닫기 전에 그는 한 번 더 돌아서서 말했다. \"꼼짝도 하지 말고, 한마디도 하지 마시오. 그들이 당신을 잠들었다고 여기게 하시오. 안 그러면 내가 당신을 도울 새도 없이 당신이 죽임을 당할지도 모르오.\"\n\n이 섬뜩한 당부를 남기고 백작은 문 너머로 사라졌다. 문은 소리 없이 그의 뒤에서 닫혔다.\n\n# Chapter 101. Locusta\n\n발랑틴은 혼자였다. 생필리프뒤룰 성당보다 느린 다른 두 시계가 사방에서 자정을 알렸다. 마차 몇 대가 굴러가는 소리를 빼면 사방이 고요했다. 이윽고 발랑틴의 주의는 자기 방의 시계로 쏠렸다. 초를 새기는 그 시계였다. 그녀는 초를 헤아리기 시작했고, 그것이 심장 박동보다 훨씬 느리다는 것을 깨달았다. 그러면서도 의심은 가시지 않았다. 남에게 해를 끼친 적 없는 발랑틴으로서는 누군가 자신의 죽음을 바란다는 것을 상상조차 할 수 없었다. 어째서? 무슨 목적으로? 자신이 무슨 짓을 했기에 적의 악의를 불러일으켰단 말인가?\n\n잠들 염려는 없었다. 한 가지 끔찍한 생각이 그녀의 마음을 짓누르고 있었다. 이 세상에 자신을 죽이려 했던 누군가가 있고, 그자가 다시 그 일을 시도하려 한다는 생각이었다. 만약 그자가 독약이 듣지 않는 데 지쳐, 몬테크리스토가 넌지시 일렀듯이 칼에 의지한다면! 만약 백작이 자신을 구하러 달려올 틈이 없다면! 만약 마지막 순간이 다가오고 있어서, 다시는 모렐을 보지 못하게 된다면!\n\n이 끔찍한 생각의 사슬이 떠오르자, 발랑틴은 벨을 울려 도움을 청할 뻔했다. 그러나 문 너머로 백작의 빛나는 눈이 보이는 것만 같았다. 기억 속에 살아 있는 그 눈이었다. 그 기억이 어찌나 부끄러움으로 그녀를 짓눌렀던지, 아무리 큰 감사로도 그의 그 무모하고 헌신적인 우정을 갚을 수 있을까 하는 생각마저 들었다.\n\n이십 분, 지루한 이십 분이 그렇게 흘렀고, 다시 십 분이 지나, 마침내 시계가 삼십 분을 알렸다.\n\n바로 그때 서재 문을 가볍게 긁는 손톱 소리가, 백작이 여전히 지켜보고 있으니 그녀에게도 그렇게 하라고 일러 주었다. 동시에 반대편, 그러니까 에두아르의 방 쪽에서 마룻바닥이 삐걱이는 소리가 들리는 듯했다. 발랑틴은 숨이 막힐 만큼 호흡을 참으며 귀를 곤두세웠다. 자물쇠가 돌아가고, 문이 천천히 열렸다. 발랑틴은 팔꿈치로 몸을 일으켰다가, 가까스로 침대에 다시 몸을 던지고 팔로 눈을 가렸다. 그러고는 부들부들 떨며, 동요한 채, 형언할 수 없는 공포로 심장이 두근거리는 가운데 일어날 일을 기다렸다.\n\n누군가 침대로 다가와 휘장을 젖혔다. 발랑틴은 온 힘을 다해, 평온한 잠을 알리는 고른 호흡으로 숨을 쉬었다.\n\n\"발랑틴!\" 낮은 목소리가 말했다.\n\n소녀는 가슴 깊이 몸서리쳤으나 대답하지 않았다.\n\n\"발랑틴.\" 같은 목소리가 되풀이했다.\n\n여전히 침묵. 발랑틴은 깨어나지 않기로 약속했던 것이다. 그러자 모든 것이 고요해졌고, 다만 방금 비운 잔에 어떤 액체가 따라지는 거의 들리지 않는 소리만이 들려왔다. 그제야 그녀는 눈꺼풀을 떠, 뻗은 팔 너머로 흘끔 보았다. 흰 실내복 차림의 여인이 작은 병에서 잔으로 약물을 따르고 있었다. 그 짧은 사이에 발랑틴이 숨을 멈추었거나 아주 조금 몸을 움직였던 모양으로, 여인은 흠칫하더니 동작을 멈추고 발랑틴이 자고 있는지 더 잘 확인하려고 침대 위로 몸을 숙였다. 빌포르 부인이었다.\n\n계모를 알아본 발랑틴은 몸서리를 억누르지 못했고, 그 떨림이 침대를 진동시켰다. 빌포르 부인은 즉시 벽 가까이로 물러섰고, 침대 휘장에 가려진 채 발랑틴의 아주 미세한 움직임까지 소리 없이 주의 깊게 살폈다. 발랑틴은 몬테크리스토의 무서운 경고를 떠올렸다. 작은 병을 들지 않은 손이 길고 날카로운 칼을 쥐고 있는 것만 같았다. 그래서 남은 힘을 다 끌어모아 억지로 눈을 감으려 했다. 그러나 우리 몸의 가장 섬세한 기관에 하는 이 단순한 동작이, 평소에는 그토록 쉬운 일이건만, 이 순간에는 거의 불가능해졌다. 진실을 알고자 눈꺼풀을 뜬 채로 두려는 호기심이 그만큼 강하게 맞섰던 것이다. 그러나 빌포르 부인은 발랑틴의 고른 호흡 말고는 아무것도 흩뜨리지 않는 그 고요함에 안심했는지, 다시 손을 뻗어 휘장에 반쯤 가려진 채 작은 병의 내용물을 잔에 모두 비우는 데 성공했다. 그러고는 어찌나 살그머니 물러갔던지 발랑틴은 그녀가 방을 떠난 줄도 몰랐다. 그녀가 본 것은 팔이 거두어지는 모습뿐이었다. 이제 겨우 스물다섯 살, 그러면서도 주위에 죽음을 뿌리고 다니는 여인의 희고 둥근 팔이었다.\n\n빌포르 부인이 방에 머문 일 분 삼십 초 동안 발랑틴이 느낀 감정을 묘사하기란 불가능하다.\n\n서재 문을 긁는 소리가, 거의 감각을 잃을 지경에 이른 마비 상태에 빠져 있던 소녀를 일깨웠다. 그녀는 안간힘을 다해 고개를 들었다. 소리 없는 문이 다시 돌쩌귀를 돌렸고, 몬테크리스토 백작이 다시 나타났다.\n\n\"자,\" 그가 말했다. \"이래도 의심이 드십니까?\"\n\n\"오.\" 소녀가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"보셨지요?\"\n\n\"아아!\"\n\n\"알아보셨고요?\" 발랑틴이 신음했다.\n\n\"오, 네.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"보았어요, 하지만 믿을 수가 없어요!\"\n\n\"그럼 차라리 죽어, 막시밀리앙까지 죽게 하시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"오.\" 소녀가 거의 넋이 나간 채 되풀이했다. \"이 집을 떠날 수는 없나요? 달아날 수는 없나요?\"\n\n\"발랑틴, 지금 당신을 위협하는 그 손은 어디든 당신을 쫓아갈 겁니다. 하인들은 황금에 매수될 테고, 죽음은 온갖 모습으로 위장한 채 당신에게 다가올 겁니다. 샘에서 길어 마시는 물에서도, 나무에서 따 먹는 과일에서도 당신은 죽음을 만나게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 자상하신 할아버지의 대비책이 독을 무력화했다고 하지 않으셨나요?\"\n\n\"그랬지요, 하지만 강한 양에는 듣지 않습니다. 독이 바뀔 테고, 양도 늘어날 겁니다.\" 그는 잔을 들어 입가로 가져갔다. \"벌써 그렇게 되었습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"이제 브루신이 아니라 단순한 마취제를 쓰는군요! 이걸 녹인 알코올 냄새를 알아볼 수 있습니다. 만약 빌포르 부인이 당신 잔에 따른 것을 마셨더라면, 발랑틴, 발랑틴, 당신은 끝장이 났을 겁니다!\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 소녀가 외쳤다. \"어째서 제가 이렇게 쫓기는 거죠?\"\n\n\"어째서냐고요? 당신은 그토록 착하고, 그토록 선하고, 악을 의심할 줄 모르는 사람이라 이해하지 못하는 겁니까, 발랑틴?\"\n\n\"네, 저는 한 번도 그분을 해친 적이 없어요.\"\n\n\"하지만 당신은 부자입니다, 발랑틴. 당신에게는 연 이십만 리브르의 수입이 있고, 당신이 그 부인의 아들이 그 이십만 리브르를 누리지 못하게 막고 있는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"어떻게요? 그 재산은 그분이 준 것이 아니라 제 친족에게서 물려받은 것인데요.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇지요. 그래서 생메랑 부부가 돌아가신 겁니다. 그래서 누아르티에 씨가 당신을 상속자로 정한 날 사형을 선고받은 것이고요. 그래서 이번에는 당신이 죽어야 하는 겁니다. 당신 아버지가 당신 재산을 물려받고, 그의 외아들인 당신 남동생이 다시 아버지의 재산을 물려받도록 말입니다.\"\n\n\"에두아르가요? 가엾은 아이! 이 모든 범죄가 그 애를 위해 저질러진 건가요?\"\n\n\"아, 이제야 이해하시는군요?\"\n\n\"부디 이 일이 그 애에게 화로 돌아가지 않기를!\"\n\n\"발랑틴, 당신은 천사로군요!\"\n\n\"하지만 할아버지는 어째서 살려 두는 거죠?\"\n\n\"당신이 죽으면 재산은 자연히 동생에게 돌아갈 텐데, 그가 상속에서 제외되지 않는 한 그렇지요. 게다가 굳이 쓸데없는 범죄를 저지르는 건 어리석은 짓이라 여긴 겁니다.\"\n\n\"한 여자가 이토록 끔찍한 범죄의 조합을 꾸며냈다는 게 가능한 일인가요?\"\n\n\"페루자의 호텔 데 포스트 정자에서, 갈색 외투를 걸친 한 사내에게 당신 계모가 아쿠아 토파나에 대해 묻던 것을 기억하십니까? 그래요, 바로 그때부터 그 끔찍한 계획이 그 여인의 머릿속에서 무르익어 온 겁니다.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇다면 정말이지,\" 눈물에 젖은 그 사랑스러운 소녀가 말했다. \"저는 죽도록 정해진 거군요!\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 발랑틴. 나는 그들의 모든 음모를 미리 내다보았으니까요. 아닙니다, 우리가 그녀의 정체를 안 이상 당신의 적은 이미 진 것이고, 당신은 살 겁니다, 발랑틴. 살아서 당신 자신이 행복해지고, 또 어느 고귀한 마음에 행복을 안겨 줄 겁니다. 다만 그러려면 나를 믿으셔야 합니다.\"\n\n\"분부만 내려 주세요. 제가 무엇을 하면 되나요?\"\n\n\"내가 주는 것을 무조건 받아들이셔야 합니다.\"\n\n\"아아, 저 하나만 생각한다면 차라리 죽는 편이 낫겠어요!\"\n\n\"누구에게도, 당신 아버지에게조차 털어놓아서는 안 됩니다.\"\n\n\"제 아버지는 이 무서운 음모에 가담하지 않으셨지요, 그렇지요?\" 발랑틴이 두 손을 맞잡으며 물었다.\n\n\"그렇습니다. 하지만 당신 아버지는 법정의 고발에 익숙한 사람이니, 이 모든 죽음이 자연스럽게 일어난 것이 아님을 알았어야 했습니다. 당신을 지켰어야 할 사람은 바로 그입니다. 내 자리에 있었어야 할 사람이고, 저 잔을 비웠어야 할 사람이며, 그 암살자에 맞서 일어섰어야 할 사람입니다. 망령이 망령에 맞서는 격이로군!\" 그는 말을 맺으며 낮은 목소리로 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"백작님,\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"저는 살기 위해 할 수 있는 모든 것을 하겠어요. 저를 사랑하고, 제가 죽으면 따라 죽을 두 사람이 있으니까요. 할아버지와 막시밀리앙이요.\"\n\n\"그분들도 당신을 지켜 온 것처럼 내가 지키겠습니다.\"\n\n\"좋아요, 백작님, 저를 마음대로 하세요.\" 그러고 나서 그녀는 낮은 목소리로 덧붙였다. \"오, 하느님, 제게 무슨 일이 닥치게 될까요?\"\n\n\"무슨 일이 일어나든, 발랑틴, 놀라지 마십시오. 고통스럽더라도, 시력과 청력과 의식을 잃더라도 아무것도 두려워하지 마십시오. 깨어나서 자신이 어디 있는지 알 수 없더라도 두려워하지 마십시오. 설령 무덤 속이나 관 속에 있는 자신을 발견하더라도 말입니다. 그러니 마음을 다잡고 이렇게 되뇌십시오. '바로 이 순간, 나와 막시밀리앙의 행복을 위해 사는 한 친구, 한 아버지가 나를 지켜보고 있다!'라고요.\"\n\n\"아아, 아아, 이 얼마나 무서운 극단인지!\"\n\n\"발랑틴, 차라리 계모를 고발하시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"차라리 백 번이라도 죽겠어요. 오, 그래요, 죽겠어요!\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 당신은 죽지 않습니다. 다만 무슨 일이 일어나든 불평하지 않고 희망을 품겠다고 약속해 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"막시밀리앙을 생각하겠어요!\"\n\n\"당신은 내 사랑스러운 딸이로군요, 발랑틴! 오직 나만이 당신을 구할 수 있고, 나는 반드시 그렇게 할 겁니다.\"\n\n발랑틴은 극도의 공포 속에서 두 손을 모았다. 용기를 청할 순간이 왔음을 느꼈기 때문이다. 그러고는 기도하기 시작했다. 두서없는 말 몇 마디밖에 내뱉지 못하면서, 그녀는 자신의 흰 어깨를 긴 머리카락 말고는 아무것도 가리지 않았다는 것도, 잠옷의 레이스 너머로 심장의 고동이 비쳐 보인다는 것도 잊고 있었다. 몬테크리스토는 소녀의 팔에 가만히 손을 얹고, 벨벳 이불을 목 가까이까지 끌어 올려 주며 아버지 같은 미소를 띠고 말했다.\n\n\"내 딸이여, 섭리의 자비와 막시밀리앙의 사랑을 믿듯이, 당신을 향한 나의 헌신을 믿으십시오.\" 발랑틴은 감사로 가득한 눈길을 그에게 보내며, 아이처럼 고분고분 따랐다.\n\n그러자 그는 조끼 주머니에서 작은 에메랄드 상자를 꺼내, 금빛 뚜껑을 열고, 완두콩만 한 알약 하나를 꺼내 그녀의 손에 쥐여 주었다. 그녀는 그것을 받아 들고 백작을 주의 깊게 바라보았다. 그 용감한 보호자의 얼굴에는 그녀의 경외심을 자아내는 표정이 어려 있었다. 그녀는 눈빛으로 그에게 묻고 있는 것이 분명했다.\n\n\"네.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n발랑틴은 알약을 입으로 가져가 삼켰다.\n\n\"자, 이제, 사랑하는 딸이여, 당분간은 작별입니다. 나도 잠을 좀 청해 보겠습니다. 당신은 이제 구원받았으니까요.\"\n\n\"가 보세요.\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"무슨 일이 일어나든, 두려워하지 않겠다고 약속드려요.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 한동안 소녀에게서 눈을 떼지 않았다. 그녀는 백작이 준 마취제의 효력에 못 이겨 차츰 잠이 들었다. 이윽고 그는 잔을 들어, 발랑틴이 마신 것처럼 보이도록 그 내용물의 4분의 3을 벽난로에 비운 뒤 탁자에 도로 놓았다. 그러고는 발랑틴에게 작별의 눈길을 던지고 사라졌다. 그녀는 주님의 발치에 잠든 천사 같은 신뢰와 순결로 잠들어 있었다.\n\n# Chapter 102. Valentine\n\n벽난로 위에서는 등잔불이 여전히 타오르며, 물 위에 떠 있던 기름의 마지막 방울까지 태워 가고 있었다. 등잔의 유리갓은 불그스름한 빛을 띠었고, 불꽃은 꺼지기 직전에 다시 밝아지며 마지막 깜박임을 토해 냈다. 생명 없는 사물의 그 떨림은 죽어 가는 사람의 마지막 경련에 자주 비유되곤 하던 것이었다. 처녀를 둘러싼 이부자리와 휘장 위로 흐릿하고 음산한 빛이 번졌다. 거리의 모든 소음은 그쳤고, 그 정적은 끔찍했다.\n\n바로 그때 에두아르의 방문이 열리며, 앞서 보았던 한 사람의 머리가 맞은편 거울에 나타났다. 빌포르 부인이었다. 자신이 준비해 둔 약물의 효력을 확인하러 온 것이다. 그녀는 문턱에서 걸음을 멈추고, 그 적막한 방 안의 유일한 소리인 등잔불의 떨림에 잠시 귀를 기울였다. 그러고는 발랑틴의 잔이 비었는지 보려고 탁자로 다가갔다. 앞서 말했듯이 잔은 여전히 사분의 일가량 차 있었다. 빌포르 부인은 그 내용물을 잿더미에 쏟았고, 액체가 더 잘 스며들도록 재를 휘저었다. 그런 다음 잔을 정성껏 헹구고 손수건으로 닦아 다시 탁자 위에 놓았다.\n\n만약 그 순간 누군가 그 방을 들여다볼 수 있었다면, 빌포르 부인이 침대로 다가가 발랑틴을 뚫어지게 바라보던 그 머뭇거림을 알아차렸을 것이다. 희미한 불빛, 깊은 정적, 그 시각이 불러일으키는 음울한 상념, 그리고 무엇보다 그녀 자신의 양심이 한데 어우러져 두려움의 감각을 자아냈다. 독을 쓴 여자는 제 손으로 한 일을 바라보며 공포에 사로잡혔다.\n\n마침내 그녀는 마음을 다잡고 휘장을 젖힌 뒤 베개 위로 몸을 기울여 발랑틴을 뚫어지게 응시했다. 처녀는 더 이상 숨을 쉬지 않았다. 반쯤 다문 잇새로 어떤 숨결도 새어 나오지 않았다. 하얀 입술은 더 이상 떨리지 않았다. 두 눈은 푸르스름한 안개로 뒤덮였고, 긴 검은 속눈썹은 밀랍처럼 창백한 뺨 위에 드리워 있었다. 빌포르 부인은 그토록 고요한 가운데서도 풍부한 표정을 머금은 그 얼굴을 응시했다. 그러고는 용기를 내어 이불을 들추고 처녀의 심장 위에 손을 얹었다. 차갑고 미동도 없었다. 그녀는 오직 제 손가락의 맥박만을 느끼고는 몸서리치며 손을 거두었다. 한쪽 팔이 침대 밖으로 늘어져 있었다. 어깨에서 팔꿈치까지는 제르맹 필롱의 「삼미신」의 팔처럼 빚어진 듯했으나, 아래팔은 경련 탓에 약간 뒤틀린 듯했고, 그토록 곱게 빚어진 손은 뻣뻣하게 펼쳐진 손가락으로 침대 틀에 닿아 있었다. 손톱마저 푸르게 변해 가고 있었다.\n\n* 「삼미신」: 16세기 프랑스 조각가 제르맹 필롱이 만든 조각상. —옮긴이\n빌포르 부인은 더 이상 의심하지 않았다. 모든 것이 끝났다. 그녀는 자신이 해내야 할 마지막 끔찍한 일을 완수한 것이다. 이 방에서 더 할 일은 없었으므로, 독을 쓴 여자는 제 발소리마저 들릴까 두려운 듯 살그머니 물러났다. 그러나 물러나면서도 그녀는 여전히 휘장을 젖힌 채로 있었다. 그저 신비롭기만 할 뿐 아직 혐오를 불러일으키지 않는 한, 죽음의 그림이 늘 발휘하는 그 뿌리칠 수 없는 매혹에 사로잡혀 있었던 것이다.\n\n시간이 흘렀다. 빌포르 부인은 발랑틴의 머리맡에 장례용 천처럼 드리운 그 휘장을 차마 놓지 못했다. 그녀는 망상에 잠겼고, 죄의 망상이란 곧 회한이었다.\n\n바로 그때 등잔이 다시 깜박였다. 그 소리에 빌포르 부인은 흠칫 놀라 몸서리치며 휘장을 떨어뜨렸다. 곧이어 불빛이 꺼졌고, 방은 끔찍한 어둠 속에 잠겼다. 그 순간 시계가 네 시 반을 알렸다.\n\n극도의 동요에 짓눌린 채, 독을 쓴 여자는 가까스로 더듬더듬 문에 다다랐고, 두려움의 고통 속에서 제 방에 이르렀다. 어둠은 두 시간 더 이어졌다. 그러다 차츰 차가운 빛이 베네치아식 덧창 사이로 스며들었고, 마침내 방 안의 물건들을 드러냈다.\n\n그즈음 계단에서 간병인의 기침 소리가 들리더니, 그 여자가 잔을 손에 들고 방으로 들어왔다. 아버지나 연인의 다정한 눈에라면 한눈에 발랑틴의 상태가 드러났을 테지만, 이 고용된 여자에게는 발랑틴이 그저 잠든 것으로만 보였다.\n\n\"좋아.\" 간병인이 탁자로 다가가며 소리쳤다. \"물약을 좀 드셨군. 잔이 사분의 삼이나 비었어.\"\n\n그러고는 벽난로로 가서 불을 지폈다. 방금 잠자리에서 일어났음에도 그녀는 발랑틴의 잠이 주는 유혹을 떨치지 못하고, 안락의자에 몸을 던져 조금 더 쉬려 했다. 시계가 여덟 시를 치자 그녀는 잠에서 깼다. 환자의 잠이 너무 길어진 데 놀라고, 팔이 여전히 침대 밖으로 늘어져 있는 것을 보고 겁이 나서, 그녀는 발랑틴에게 다가갔고 그제야 처음으로 하얀 입술을 알아차렸다. 팔을 제자리에 놓으려 했으나, 팔은 간병인을 속일 수 없는 끔찍한 뻣뻣함으로 움직였다. 그녀는 비명을 질렀다. 그러고는 문으로 달려가 외쳤다.\n\n\"사람 살려, 사람 살려요!\"\n\n\"무슨 일인가?\" 다브리니 씨가 계단 아래에서 물었다. 그가 늘 환자를 찾아오던 시각이었다.\n\n\"무슨 일인가?\" 빌포르가 제 방에서 뛰쳐나오며 물었다. \"선생님, 사람 살리라고 외치는 소리가 들립니까?\"\n\n\"네, 네, 어서 올라갑시다. 발랑틴 양의 방에서 나는 소리였습니다.\"\n\n그러나 의사와 아버지가 방에 이르기도 전에, 같은 층에 있던 하인들이 먼저 들어가 있었다. 그들은 침대 위에 창백하게 미동도 없이 누운 발랑틴을 보고, 두 손을 하늘로 치켜든 채 벼락이라도 맞은 듯 그 자리에 못 박혀 서 있었다.\n\n\"빌포르 부인을 불러라! 빌포르 부인을 깨워라!\" 검사가 침실 문간에서 외쳤다. 그는 차마 그 문턱을 넘지 못하는 듯했다. 그러나 하인들은 그의 명령을 따르는 대신, 발랑틴에게 달려가 그녀를 두 팔로 안아 일으키는 다브리니 씨를 지켜보고 있었다.\n\n\"뭐라고? 이 아이마저?\" 그가 외쳤다. \"오, 대체 이 끝은 어디인가?\"\n\n빌포르가 방 안으로 뛰어들었다.\n\n\"무슨 말씀이십니까, 선생님?\" 그가 두 손을 하늘로 치켜들며 외쳤다.\n\n\"발랑틴 양이 죽었다는 말입니다!\" 다브리니가 대답했다. 그 목소리는 엄숙한 평정 속에서 더없이 끔찍했다.\n\n빌포르 씨는 비틀거리며 침대에 머리를 파묻었다. 의사의 그 말과 아버지의 그 외침에, 하인들은 저주의 말을 중얼거리며 모두 달아났다. 그들이 계단을 뛰어 내려가 긴 복도를 지나는 소리가 들렸고, 이어 안뜰에서 한바탕 부산스러운 소리가 나더니, 그 뒤로는 모든 것이 잠잠해졌다. 그들은 하나도 빠짐없이 저주받은 그 집을 버리고 떠난 것이다.\n\n바로 그때, 실내복을 막 걸치던 빌포르 부인이 휘장을 젖히고 잠시 미동도 없이 서 있었다. 마치 방 안에 있는 이들에게 무엇이냐고 묻는 듯한 모습으로, 그녀는 좀처럼 나오지 않는 눈물을 짜내려 애썼다. 별안간 그녀는 두 팔을 뻗은 채 탁자 쪽으로 걸음을, 아니 거의 뛰듯이 다가갔다. 다브리니가 그 잔을 호기심 어린 눈으로 살피고 있는 것이 보였다. 그녀가 밤사이에 분명히 비웠다고 확신하던 그 잔이었다. 그런데 잔은 그녀가 내용물을 잿더미에 쏟았을 때처럼 삼분의 일이 차 있었다. 독을 쓴 여자 앞에 발랑틴의 망령이 일어섰더라도 이만큼 그녀를 놀라게 하지는 못했을 것이다. 그것은 분명, 그녀가 잔에 따랐고 발랑틴이 마셨던 그 물약과 똑같은 빛깔이었다. 그것은 분명 그 독이었고, 다브리니 씨를 속일 수 없는 그 독을, 이제 그가 그토록 면밀히 살피고 있었다. 그녀가 그토록 조심했음에도 죄를 드러낼 어떤 흔적, 어떤 증거가 남아 있다는 것은 의심할 바 없이 하늘이 내린 기적이었다.\n\n빌포르 부인이 공포의 조상처럼 그 자리에 못 박혀 있고, 빌포르가 이불에 머리를 파묻은 채 주위의 아무것도 보지 못하는 사이, 다브리니는 잔의 내용물을 더 잘 살피려 창가로 다가갔다. 그는 손끝을 적셔 맛을 보았다.\n\n\"아.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"이제는 브루신을 쓰지 않는군. 무엇인지 어디 보자!\"\n\n그러고는 발랑틴의 방에서 약장으로 개조된 찬장 하나로 달려가, 은제 갑에서 작은 질산 병을 꺼내 그 약물에 조금 떨어뜨렸다. 그러자 약물은 곧바로 핏빛으로 변했다.\n\n\"아.\" 다브리니가 외쳤다. 그 목소리에는 진실을 드러내는 판관의 전율과 발견을 이룬 학자의 환희가 뒤섞여 있었다.\n\n빌포르 부인은 압도되었다. 그녀의 두 눈이 먼저 번득이더니 이내 흐려졌고, 그녀는 비틀거리며 문 쪽으로 가다 사라졌다. 곧이어 무거운 물체가 바닥에 떨어지는 둔탁한 소리가 멀리서 들렸으나, 아무도 거기에 주의를 기울이지 않았다. 간병인은 화학 분석을 지켜보는 데 정신이 팔려 있었고, 빌포르는 여전히 슬픔에 잠겨 있었다. 오직 다브리니 씨만이 빌포르 부인을 눈으로 좇으며 그녀의 황급한 퇴장을 지켜보았다. 그는 에두아르의 방으로 통하는 휘장을 들추었고, 그의 시선이 빌포르 부인의 방까지 닿자, 그녀가 바닥에 죽은 듯이 뻗어 있는 것이 보였다.\n\n\"빌포르 부인을 도우러 가게.\" 그가 간병인에게 말했다. \"빌포르 부인이 쓰러지셨네.\"\n\n\"하지만 빌포르 아가씨가……\" 간병인이 더듬거렸다.\n\n\"빌포르 양에게는 더 이상 도움이 필요 없네.\" 다브리니가 말했다. \"이미 죽었으니까.\"\n\n\"죽었어, 죽었어!\" 빌포르가 슬픔의 발작 속에서 신음하듯 토해 냈다. 그 강철 같은 사내의 가슴에는 낯선 감각이었기에 그 슬픔은 더욱 끔찍했다.\n\n\"죽었다고!\" 세 번째 목소리가 되풀이했다. \"누가 발랑틴이 죽었다고 했소?\"\n\n두 사내가 돌아보니, 모렐이 창백하게 공포에 질린 얼굴로 문가에 서 있었다. 일은 이러했다. 여느 때와 같은 시각에 모렐은 누아르티에의 방으로 통하는 작은 문 앞에 나타났다. 늘 그렇던 것과 달리 문은 열려 있었고, 종을 울릴 필요가 없어 그는 그대로 들어갔다. 그는 현관에서 잠시 기다리며 누아르티에 씨에게 안내해 줄 하인을 불렀으나, 아무도 대답하지 않았다. 우리가 알다시피 하인들이 집을 버리고 떠났기 때문이었다. 모렐에게는 딱히 불안해할 이유가 없었다. 몬테크리스토가 발랑틴이 살아날 것이라 약속했고, 지금껏 그는 늘 약속을 지켜 왔다. 매일 밤 백작은 그에게 소식을 전해 주었고, 그 소식은 이튿날 아침 누아르티에에 의해 확인되곤 했다. 그래도 이 유별난 정적은 그에게 이상하게 여겨졌고, 그는 두 번, 세 번 더 불렀다. 그래도 대답이 없었다. 그러자 그는 위층으로 올라가기로 마음먹었다. 누아르티에의 방은 다른 방들처럼 열려 있었다. 그가 가장 먼저 본 것은 늘 앉던 자리의 안락의자에 앉아 있는 노인이었다. 그러나 노인의 두 눈은 경악을 드러내고 있었고, 얼굴에 번진 창백함이 그것을 뒷받침하고 있었다.\n\n\"안녕하십니까, 어르신?\" 모렐이 가슴이 메는 듯한 심정으로 물었다.\n\n\"잘 있네.\" 노인이 두 눈을 감아 대답했다. 그러나 그 모습은 점점 커져 가는 불안을 드러내고 있었다.\n\n\"무슨 근심이 있으십니까, 어르신?\" 모렐이 말을 이었다. \"무엇이 필요하신가요? 하인을 부를까요?\"\n\n\"그래.\" 누아르티에가 대답했다.\n\n모렐이 종 줄을 잡아당겼으나, 줄이 끊어질 만큼 당겨도 아무도 대답하지 않았다. 그는 누아르티에를 돌아보았다. 노인의 얼굴에 드러난 창백함과 고뇌가 시시각각 더해 갔다.\n\n\"오.\" 모렐이 외쳤다. \"왜 아무도 오지 않는 겁니까? 집안에 누가 아픕니까?\" 누아르티에의 두 눈은 눈구멍에서 튀어나올 듯했다. \"무슨 일입니까? 어르신, 저를 두렵게 하시는군요. 발랑틴? 발랑틴인가요?\"\n\n\"그래, 그래.\" 누아르티에가 신호를 보냈다.\n\n막시밀리앙은 말을 하려 했으나 아무 말도 입에서 나오지 않았다. 그는 비틀거리며 벽판에 몸을 기댔다. 그러고는 문을 가리켰다.\n\n\"그래, 그래, 그래!\" 노인이 계속 신호를 보냈다.\n\n막시밀리앙이 작은 계단을 뛰어 올라가는 동안, 누아르티에의 두 눈은 \"더 빨리, 더 빨리!\" 하고 외치는 듯했다.\n\n순식간에 젊은이는 여러 방을 가로질러 마침내 발랑틴의 방에 이르렀다.\n\n문을 밀 필요도 없었다. 활짝 열려 있었다. 그가 들은 소리라고는 흐느낌뿐이었다. 그는 안개 속을 들여다보듯, 검은 형체 하나가 무릎을 꿇은 채 어지러이 흐트러진 하얀 휘장 더미에 파묻혀 있는 것을 보았다. 끔찍한 두려움이 그를 못 박았다. 바로 그때 그는 한 목소리가 \"발랑틴이 죽었다!\"고 외치는 것을, 그리고 또 다른 목소리가 메아리처럼 되풀이하는 것을 들었다.\n\n\"죽었어, 죽었어!\"\n\n# Chapter 103. Maximilian\n\n빌포르가 일어섰다. 그토록 격렬한 비탄에 빠진 모습을 들킨 것이 절반쯤 부끄러웠다. 이십오 년 동안 맡아 온 그 끔찍한 직무가 그를 인간 이상의, 또는 인간 이하의 존재로 만들어 놓았던 것이다. 처음엔 방황하던 그의 시선이 모렐에게 멎었다. \"당신은 누구요?\" 그가 물었다. \"이렇게 죽음이 덮친 집에 함부로 들어오는 법이 어디 있소? 나가시오, 어서 나가!\"\n\n그러나 모렐은 꼼짝도 하지 않았다. 헝클어진 침대와 그 위에 누운 처녀의 창백한 주검에서 눈을 뗄 수가 없었다.\n\n\"나가시오! 안 들리시오?\" 빌포르가 말했고, 다브리니가 모렐을 데리고 나가려고 다가갔다. 막시밀리앙은 잠시 주검을 응시하더니, 방 안을 빙 둘러보고, 다시 두 남자를 바라보았다. 무언가 말하려 입을 열었지만, 머릿속을 가득 메운 수많은 생각을 도무지 입 밖으로 낼 수 없었다. 그는 두 손으로 머리카락을 쥐어뜯듯 움켜잡고 밖으로 나갔다. 그 모습에 빌포르와 다브리니는 잠시 골몰하던 화제에서 벗어나 서로 눈길을 주고받았는데, 그 눈빛은 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"저 사람, 미쳤군!\"\n\n그러나 채 오 분도 지나지 않아 계단이 엄청난 무게에 짓눌려 신음했다. 모렐이 누아르티에가 앉은 안락의자를 초인적인 힘으로 들고 위층으로 올라오고 있었다. 층계참에 다다르자 그는 안락의자를 바닥에 내려놓고는 재빨리 발랑틴의 방으로 밀고 들어갔다. 이것은 격렬한 흥분이 가져다준 비정상적인 힘이 아니고서는 해낼 수 없는 일이었다. 그러나 가장 무서운 광경은 침대 쪽으로 밀려가는 누아르티에였다. 그의 얼굴은 마음속 모든 것을 그대로 드러냈고, 그의 두 눈은 다른 모든 기능을 대신하고 있었다. 그 창백한 얼굴과 불타는 눈빛이 빌포르에게는 끔찍한 유령처럼 보였다. 그가 아버지와 마주칠 때마다 무언가 무서운 일이 벌어지곤 했던 것이다.\n\n\"저들이 무슨 짓을 했는지 보십시오!\" 모렐이 한 손으로는 의자 등받이를 짚고, 다른 손으로는 발랑틴을 가리키며 외쳤다. \"보세요, 아버님, 보시라고요!\"\n\n빌포르는 뒷걸음치며, 자신에게는 거의 낯선 사람이나 다름없는 이 젊은이가 누아르티에를 아버지라고 부르는 모습을 경악스럽게 바라보았다. 그 순간 노인의 온 영혼이 두 눈에 모인 듯했고, 그 눈은 핏발이 섰다. 목의 핏줄이 부풀어 올랐고, 뺨과 관자놀이는 간질 발작이라도 일으킨 듯 검붉게 물들었다. 이 모든 것을 완성하는 데 모자란 것은 오직 비명 하나뿐이었다. 그리고 그 비명은, 이렇게 말해도 된다면, 그의 온몸의 땀구멍에서 터져 나왔다. 침묵 속에서 터지는, 그래서 더욱 끔찍한 비명이었다. 다브리니가 노인에게 달려가 강한 회복제를 들이마시게 했다.\n\n\"선생님,\" 모렐이 마비된 노인의 축축한 손을 붙잡으며 외쳤다. \"이 사람들이 제게 누구냐고, 무슨 자격으로 여기 있느냐고 묻습니다. 오, 아버님은 아시지요. 말씀해 주십시오, 말씀해 주세요!\" 젊은이의 목소리는 흐느낌에 막혔다.\n\n노인은 가쁜 숨을 몰아쉬며 가슴을 들썩였다. 죽음을 앞둔 단말마의 고통을 겪고 있는 것이라 여겨질 정도였다. 마침내, 흐느끼면서도 눈물은 흘리지 못하던 젊은이보다는 다행스럽게도, 누아르티에의 두 눈에 눈물이 반짝였다.\n\n\"말씀해 주세요.\" 모렐이 쉰 목소리로 말했다. \"제가 그녀의 약혼자라고 말씀해 주세요. 그녀가 제 사랑이었고, 제 고귀한 여인이었으며, 이 세상에서 제 유일한 축복이었다고 말씀해 주세요. 말씀해 주세요. 오, 말씀해 주세요, 저 주검은 제 것이라고요!\"\n\n비통함의 무게에 짓눌린 젊은이는 침대 앞에 털썩 무릎을 꿇었고, 손가락으로 발작하듯 침대를 움켜쥐었다. 다브리니는 이 가슴 저미는 감정을 차마 더 지켜볼 수 없어 고개를 돌렸다. 빌포르는 더 이상 아무 설명도 구하지 않고, 우리가 애도하는 이들을 사랑했던 사람에게로 끌려가게 만드는 그 거역할 수 없는 끌림에 이끌려 젊은이에게 손을 내밀었다.\n\n그러나 모렐은 아무것도 보지 못했다. 그는 발랑틴의 손을 움켜쥔 채, 눈물도 흘리지 못하고 침대보를 깨물며 신음으로 고통을 토해 냈다. 한동안 그 방 안에는 흐느낌과 절규와 기도 소리밖에 들리지 않았다. 마침내 그중 가장 차분한 빌포르가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"선생,\" 그가 막시밀리앙에게 말했다. \"당신은 발랑틴을 사랑했고, 그 아이와 약혼했다고 했소. 나는 그런 약혼도, 그런 사랑도 전혀 몰랐소. 하지만 그 애의 아비인 나는 당신을 용서하겠소. 당신의 슬픔이 진실하고 깊다는 것을 알기 때문이오. 게다가 내 자신의 슬픔이 너무 커서 분노가 들어설 자리가 마음에 없소. 하지만 보다시피, 당신이 바라던 그 천사는 이 땅을 떠났소. 이제 그 애는 인간들의 흠모와는 아무 상관이 없소. 선생, 그 애의 서글픈 유해에 마지막 작별을 고하시오. 당신 것으로 만들기를 바라던 그 손을 다시 한번 당신 손안에 쥐어 보고, 그런 다음 영원히 그 애에게서 떠나시오. 발랑틴에게 이제 필요한 것은 사제의 의식뿐이오.\"\n\n\"당신이 틀렸소, 선생.\" 모렐이 한쪽 무릎으로 몸을 일으키며 외쳤다. 지금까지 느껴 본 그 어떤 것보다 날카로운 고통이 그의 심장을 꿰뚫었다. \"당신이 틀렸소. 발랑틴은 이렇게 죽었으니, 사제만이 아니라 복수해 줄 자도 필요하오. 빌포르 씨, 당신은 사제를 부르시오. 복수해 줄 자는 내가 되겠소.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 말이오, 선생?\" 빌포르가 모렐의 헛소리가 불러일으킨 새로운 생각에 몸을 떨며 물었다.\n\n\"내 말은, 선생, 당신 안에 두 사람이 있다는 거요. 아버지는 충분히 슬퍼했으니, 이제 검사가 그 직무를 다하게 하시오.\"\n\n누아르티에의 두 눈이 번득였고, 다브리니가 다가왔다.\n\n\"여러분,\" 모렐이 이 장면을 지켜보는 이들의 마음속을 모두 읽어 내며 말했다. \"나는 내가 무슨 말을 하는지 알고, 여러분도 내가 무슨 말을 하려는지 나만큼 잘 압니다. 발랑틴은 살해되었습니다!\"\n\n빌포르는 고개를 떨구었고, 다브리니는 더 가까이 다가왔으며, 누아르티에는 두 눈으로 \"그렇다\"고 말했다.\n\n\"자, 선생.\" 모렐이 말을 이었다. \"요즘 세상에는 누구든 폭력적인 방법으로 죽임을 당하면 그 죽음의 원인을 캐는 조사가 따르기 마련이오. 설령 그 사람이 발랑틴처럼 젊고 아름답고 사랑스러운 사람이 아니라 해도 말이오. 자, 검사님.\" 모렐이 점점 격해지는 목소리로 말했다. \"어떤 자비도 허용되지 않소. 나는 이 범죄를 고발하오. 살인자를 찾아내는 것은 당신의 직무요.\"\n\n젊은이의 가차 없는 눈이 빌포르를 추궁했다. 빌포르는 누아르티에에게서 다브리니에게로 시선을 옮겼다. 그러나 의사와 아버지의 눈에서 동정을 찾는 대신, 그는 막시밀리앙의 것만큼이나 단호한 표정만을 보았다.\n\n\"그렇다.\" 노인이 눈으로 말했다.\n\n\"틀림없습니다.\" 다브리니가 말했다.\n\n\"선생,\" 빌포르가 이 세 겹의 압박과 자신의 감정에 맞서 싸우려 애쓰며 말했다. \"선생, 당신은 속고 있소. 여기서 범죄를 저지른 사람은 없소. 나는 운명에 짓밟힌 것이오. 정말이지 끔찍한 일이지만, 누구도 살인을 저지르지는 않았소.\"\n\n누아르티에의 두 눈이 분노로 타올랐고, 다브리니가 입을 열려 했다. 그러나 모렐이 팔을 뻗어 침묵을 명했다.\n\n\"내가 말하건대, 이 집에서는 살인이 저질러졌소.\" 모렐의 목소리는 한층 낮아졌으나 그 무서운 또렷함은 조금도 잃지 않았다. \"내가 말하건대, 이것은 지난 넉 달 동안 네 번째 희생자요. 내가 말하건대, 나흘 전 발랑틴은 독으로 목숨을 잃을 뻔했으나, 누아르티에 씨의 대비 덕분에 화를 면했소. 내가 말하건대, 이번에는 그 양이 두 배로 늘었고, 독이 바뀌었으며, 그래서 결국 성공한 것이오. 내가 말하건대, 당신은 이 사실들을 나만큼 잘 알고 있소. 여기 이분이 의사로서, 또 친구로서 당신에게 미리 경고했으니 말이오.\"\n\n\"오, 당신은 헛소리를 하고 있소, 선생.\" 빌포르가 자신이 걸려든 그물에서 벗어나려 헛되이 애쓰며 외쳤다.\n\n\"내가 헛소리를 한다고요?\" 모렐이 말했다. \"그렇다면, 다브리니 씨께 직접 여쭙겠소. 선생, 생메랑 부인이 돌아가시던 날 밤, 이 집 정원에서 하신 말씀을 기억하시는지 그분께 물어보시오. 당신들은 둘뿐인 줄 알고 그 비극적인 죽음에 대해 이야기했고, 그때 당신이 입에 올린 그 숙명이 바로 발랑틴을 살해한 것과 같은 것이오.\" 빌포르와 다브리니가 서로 눈길을 주고받았다.\n\n\"그래요, 그래.\" 모렐이 말을 이었다. \"그 장면을 떠올려 보시오. 당신들이 침묵과 고독에만 들려준 줄 알았던 그 말들이 내 귀에 들어왔으니 말이오. 빌포르 씨가 자기 친족에게 보인 그 죄스러운 무관심을 목격한 이상, 나는 마땅히 당국에 그를 고발했어야 했소. 그랬다면 지금처럼 그대의 죽음에 공범이 되지는 않았을 텐데, 가엾고 사랑하는 발랑틴이여. 그러나 그 공범이 이제 복수자가 되겠소. 이 네 번째 살인은 누가 봐도 명백하고, 그대의 아버지가 그대를 저버린다 해도, 살인자를 끝까지 쫓을 자는 바로 나라고, 내가 맹세하오.\"\n\n이번에는, 마치 자연이 마침내 그 힘에 겨워 터질 듯한 건장한 육체를 가엾이 여기기라도 한 듯, 모렐의 말은 목구멍에서 막혔다. 그의 가슴이 들썩였고, 그토록 오래 거역하던 눈물이 두 눈에서 쏟아졌다. 그는 침대 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 흐느꼈다.\n\n그러자 다브리니가 입을 열었다. \"나 역시,\" 그가 낮은 목소리로 외쳤다. \"범죄에 대한 정의를 요구하는 데 있어 모렐 씨와 뜻을 같이하겠소. 나의 비겁한 양보로 살인자를 부추겼다는 생각에 피가 끓어오르오.\"\n\n\"오, 자비로운 하느님!\" 빌포르가 중얼거렸다. 모렐이 고개를 들어, 기이한 광채로 번득이는 노인의 눈을 읽고는 말했다.\n\n\"잠깐.\" 그가 말했다. \"누아르티에 씨가 말씀하시려 합니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다.\" 누아르티에가 표정으로 말했는데, 모든 기능이 그 눈빛 하나에 집중되어 있는 만큼 한층 무서운 표정이었다.\n\n\"살인자를 아십니까?\" 모렐이 물었다.\n\n\"그렇다.\" 누아르티에가 답했다.\n\n\"그럼 저희를 인도해 주시겠습니까?\" 젊은이가 외쳤다. \"들으십시오, 다브리니 씨, 들어요!\"\n\n누아르티에는 발랑틴을 그토록 자주 행복하게 했던 그 우울한 미소 중 하나로 모렐을 바라보며 그의 주의를 끌었다. 그러고는 상대의 두 눈을 제 눈에 단단히 붙들어 둔 채, 문 쪽으로 시선을 던졌다.\n\n\"제가 나가기를 바라십니까?\" 모렐이 서글프게 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다.\" 누아르티에가 답했다.\n\n\"아아, 아아, 어르신, 저를 가엾이 여겨 주십시오!\"\n\n노인의 두 눈은 문에 고정된 채였다.\n\n\"적어도, 다시 돌아와도 됩니까?\" 모렐이 물었다.\n\n\"그렇다.\"\n\n\"저 혼자 나가야 합니까?\"\n\n\"아니다.\"\n\"누구를 데리고 가야 합니까? 검사를요?\"\n\n\"아니다.\"\n\n\"의사를요?\"\n\n\"그렇다.\"\n\n\"빌포르 씨와 단둘이 남고 싶으신 겁니까?\"\n\"그렇다.\"\n\n\"하지만 그가 어르신의 뜻을 알아들을 수 있겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그렇다.\"\n\n\"오,\" 빌포르가 그 조사가 자신 혼자 맡게 되리라는 생각에 형언할 수 없는 기쁨을 느끼며 말했다. \"오, 안심하시오. 나는 내 아버지의 뜻을 알아들을 수 있소.\" 기쁨에 찬 표정으로 이 말을 내뱉으면서도, 그의 이는 사납게 딱딱 부딪쳤다.\n\n다브리니가 젊은이의 팔을 잡아 방 밖으로 데리고 나갔다. 그러자 집 안에는 죽음보다 더한 침묵이 감돌았다. 십오 분쯤 지났을 때 비틀거리는 발소리가 들렸고, 다브리니와 모렐이 머물러 있던 방의 문가에 빌포르가 나타났다. 한 사람은 깊은 사색에, 다른 한 사람은 슬픔에 잠겨 있었다.\n\n\"들어오시오.\" 그가 말하고는 두 사람을 누아르티에에게로 데려갔다.\n\n모렐은 빌포르를 유심히 바라보았다. 그의 얼굴은 납빛이었고, 굵은 땀방울이 얼굴을 타고 흘러내렸으며, 손가락에는 산산조각으로 찢어 놓은 깃펜 조각들이 쥐여 있었다.\n\n\"여러분,\" 그가 쉰 목소리로 말했다. \"이 끔찍한 비밀이 영원히 우리끼리 묻혀 있으리라고 명예를 걸고 약속해 주시오!\" 두 사람이 뒤로 물러섰다.\n\n\"부탁이오……\" 빌포르가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 모렐이 말했다. \"그 죄인은, 그 살인자는, 그 암살자는요.\"\n\n\"안심하시오, 선생. 정의는 실현될 것이오.\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"내 아버지가 죄인의 이름을 밝혀 주셨소. 내 아버지도 당신만큼이나 복수를 갈망하시오. 그런데도 그분조차 나처럼 당신에게 이 비밀을 지켜 달라고 간청하시오. 그렇지 않습니까, 아버지?\"\n\n\"그렇다.\" 누아르티에가 단호히 답했다. 모렐은 공포와 놀라움이 뒤섞인 탄성을 흘렸다.\n\n\"오, 선생.\" 빌포르가 막시밀리앙의 팔을 붙잡으며 말했다. \"내 아버지처럼 한번 마음먹으면 굽히지 않는 분이 이런 청을 하신다면, 그건 발랑틴의 원수가 무섭게 갚아지리라는 것을 알고 계시기 때문이오, 확실하오. 그렇지 않습니까, 아버지?\"\n\n노인이 그렇다는 표시를 했다. 빌포르가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그분은 나를 아시고, 나는 그분께 내 말을 걸었소. 안심하시오, 여러분. 사흘 안에, 정의가 요구하는 것보다 짧은 시간 안에, 내 아이의 살해에 대해 내가 행할 복수는 가장 대담한 심장조차 떨게 만들 것이오.\" 이렇게 말하면서 그는 이를 갈았고, 노인의 감각 없는 손을 움켜쥐었다.\n\n\"이 약속이 지켜질까요, 누아르티에 씨?\" 모렐이 물었고, 다브리니는 묻는 듯한 눈빛으로 바라보았다.\n\n\"그렇다.\" 누아르티에가 음산한 기쁨이 어린 표정으로 답했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 맹세하시오.\" 빌포르가 모렐과 다브리니의 손을 맞잡게 하며 말했다. \"내 집안의 명예를 지켜 주고, 내 아이의 복수는 내게 맡기겠다고 맹세하시오.\"\n\n다브리니가 돌아서서 아주 미약하게 \"그렇소\"라고 내뱉었다. 그러나 모렐은 손을 빼내어 침대로 달려가, 발랑틴의 차가운 입술에 제 입술을 맞춘 뒤, 절망과 비통함이 어린 길고 깊은 신음을 토하며 황급히 떠났다.\n\n앞서 말했듯이 하인들은 모두 달아난 뒤였다. 그래서 빌포르는 다브리니에게, 대도시에서 사람이 죽었을 때, 더구나 이토록 의심스러운 정황 속에서 죽었을 때 뒤따르는 모든 절차를 맡아 처리해 달라고 부탁할 수밖에 없었다.\n\n누아르티에의 말 없는 고통, 그 소리 없는 절망을 지켜보는 것은 끔찍한 일이었다. 눈물이 그의 뺨을 타고 소리 없이 흘러내렸다. 빌포르는 서재로 물러갔고, 다브리니는 시청 소속 의사를 부르러 갔다. 그 의사의 직무는 사람이 죽은 뒤 시신을 검안하는 것으로, \"죽은 자의 의사\"라고 불리는 사람이었다. 누아르티에 씨는 아무리 권해도 손녀 곁을 떠나려 하지 않았다. 십오 분쯤 지나 다브리니가 그 동료와 함께 돌아왔다. 두 사람은 바깥 대문이 닫혀 있고 집 안에 하인 하나 남지 않은 것을 알았다. 빌포르가 직접 문을 열어 줄 수밖에 없었다. 그러나 그는 층계참에서 멈춰 섰다. 다시 죽음의 방으로 들어갈 용기가 나지 않았던 것이다. 그래서 두 의사만이 방으로 들어갔다. 누아르티에는 침대 곁에서 주검처럼 창백하게, 미동도 없이, 말없이 있었다. 시청 의사는 인생의 절반을 죽은 자들 틈에서 보내는 데 익숙한 사람 특유의 무심함으로 다가왔다. 그러고는 얼굴을 덮은 침대보를 들추고 입술을 살짝 벌려 보았다.\n\n\"아아,\" 다브리니가 말했다. \"정말로 죽었군요, 가엾은 아이가!\"\n\n\"그렇소.\" 의사가 들춰 올렸던 침대보를 도로 내려놓으며 간략히 답했다. 누아르티에가 일종의 쉰, 그르렁거리는 소리를 냈다. 노인의 두 눈이 번득였고, 마음씨 좋은 의사는 그가 손녀를 보고 싶어 한다는 것을 알아챘다. 그래서 그는 침대로 다가갔고, 그의 동료가 주검의 입술을 만진 손가락을 표백분 용액에 담그는 동안, 그는 잠든 천사처럼 보이는 그 평온하고 창백한 얼굴을 다시 드러내 보였다.\n\n노인의 눈에 맺힌 눈물 한 방울이 의사에게 보내는 감사를 표현했다. 죽은 자의 의사는 검안 허가서를 탁자 모서리에 내려놓고, 제 의무를 다한 뒤 다브리니의 안내를 받아 밖으로 나갔다. 빌포르가 서재 문 앞에서 그들을 맞았다. 그는 몇 마디로 시청 의사에게 감사를 표한 뒤, 다브리니에게 돌아서서 말했다.\n\n\"이제 사제를 부를 차례군요.\"\n\n\"발랑틴을 위해 기도해 줄 특별히 원하는 사제가 있소?\" 다브리니가 물었다.\n\n\"아니오.\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"가장 가까운 사람을 데려오시오.\"\n\n\"가장 가까운 분은,\" 시청 의사가 말했다. \"당신 댁 옆집에 사는 마음씨 좋은 이탈리아인 신부요. 지나는 길에 들러 청해 볼까요?\"\n\n\"다브리니.\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"부디 부탁이니, 이분과 동행해 주시오. 여기 문 열쇠가 있으니, 마음대로 드나들 수 있을 것이오. 그 사제를 모셔 와서, 내 아이의 방으로 안내해 주는 수고를 해 주시오.\"\n\n\"그를 만나 보시겠소?\"\n\n\"나는 그저 혼자 있고 싶을 뿐이오. 양해해 주시겠지요? 사제라면 아비의 슬픔을 이해할 수 있을 테니.\"\n\n그리고 빌포르 씨는 다브리니에게 열쇠를 건네고, 그 낯선 의사에게 다시 한번 작별을 고한 뒤, 서재로 물러가 일을 시작했다. 어떤 기질의 사람에게는 일이 모든 괴로움의 치료약이 되는 법이다.\n\n두 의사가 거리로 나서자, 옆집 문턱에 사제복 차림의 한 남자가 서 있는 것이 보였다.\n\n\"저분이 제가 말한 그 신부입니다.\" 의사가 다브리니에게 말했다. 다브리니가 사제에게 다가가 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"신부님,\" 그가 말했다. \"방금 딸을 잃은 불행한 아버지에게 큰 은혜를 베풀어 주실 뜻이 있으신지요? 국왕 검사 빌포르 씨를 말하는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"아,\" 사제가 뚜렷한 이탈리아 억양으로 말했다. \"그렇군요, 저 댁에 초상이 났다는 이야기는 들었습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그분이 신부님께 어떤 봉사를 바라는지는 말씀드릴 필요가 없겠군요.\"\n\n\"제가 먼저 자청하려던 참이었습니다, 선생.\" 사제가 말했다. \"우리의 사명이란 마땅히 해야 할 일을 미리 헤아리는 것이지요.\"\n\n\"젊은 처녀입니다.\"\n\n\"압니다, 선생. 그 집에서 달아난 하인들이 알려 주었지요. 이름이 발랑틴이라는 것도 알고, 이미 그녀를 위해 기도드렸습니다.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 신부님.\" 다브리니가 말했다. \"이미 그 성스러운 직무를 시작하셨으니, 부디 이어 가 주십시오. 오셔서 죽은 이의 곁을 지켜 주시면, 그 불행한 가족 모두가 감사할 것입니다.\"\n\n\"가겠습니다, 선생. 그리고 거리낌 없이 말씀드리거니와, 제 기도보다 더 간절한 기도는 없을 것입니다.\"\n\n다브리니가 사제의 손을 잡았다. 서재에 틀어박혀 있는 빌포르와 마주치지 않고, 그들은 발랑틴의 방에 이르렀다. 그 방은 이튿날 밤이면 장의사들이 차지하게 될 터였다. 방에 들어서자 누아르티에의 두 눈이 신부의 눈과 마주쳤는데, 그 안에서 무언가 특별한 표정을 읽었는지 신부는 방에 머물렀다. 다브리니는 신부에게 죽은 이뿐만 아니라 산 사람도 보살펴 달라고 당부했고, 신부는 발랑틴을 위해 기도하고 누아르티에를 돌보겠노라 약속했다.\n\n분명, 그 성스러운 사명을 다하는 동안 방해받지 않으려는 뜻에서, 신부는 다브리니가 떠나자마자 자리에서 일어나, 의사가 방금 나간 문에 빗장을 질렀을 뿐 아니라 빌포르 부인의 방으로 통하는 문에도 빗장을 질렀다.\n\n# Chapter 104. Danglars’ Signature\n\n이튿날 아침은 흐리고 음울하게 밝아 왔다. 밤사이 장의사들이 그 우울한 임무를 마쳐, 시신을 수의로 감쌌다. 죽음 앞에서는 모두가 평등하다지만, 그 수의야말로 살아생전 그토록 사랑받던 사치의 마지막 증거였다. 그 수의는 다름 아닌 아름다운 고급 무명천 한 폭이었는데, 그 처녀가 보름 전에 직접 사 둔 것이었다.\n\n저녁나절에는 이 일을 위해 고용된 두 사람이 누아르티에를 발랑틴의 방에서 그의 방으로 옮겼다. 누구도 예상하지 못한 일이었지만, 노인을 손녀 곁에서 떼어 내는 데는 아무런 어려움도 없었다. 부소니 신부는 동이 틀 때까지 곁을 지키다가 아무도 부르지 않고 떠났다. 다브리니는 아침 여덟 시쯤 다시 왔다. 그는 누아르티에의 방으로 가던 길에 빌포르를 만나, 노인이 어떻게 잤는지 함께 보러 갔다. 두 사람은 침대 대신 쓰는 커다란 안락의자에 앉아 평온하게, 아니 거의 미소를 머금은 채 잠든 노인을 발견했다. 둘은 문 앞에서 놀라 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"보십시오.\" 다브리니가 빌포르에게 말했다. \"자연은 가장 깊은 슬픔도 어떻게 달래는지 알고 있군요. 누아르티에 씨가 손녀를 사랑하지 않았다고는 아무도 말할 수 없을 텐데, 이렇게 주무시고 계시지 않습니까.\"\n\n\"그렇군요, 맞는 말씀입니다.\" 빌포르가 놀라며 대답했다. \"정말로 주무시는군요! 더 이상한 건, 사소한 일에 거슬리기만 해도 밤새 깨어 계시던 분이라는 점입니다.\"\n\n\"슬픔이 저분을 마비시킨 겁니다.\" 다브리니가 대답했다. 두 사람은 생각에 잠긴 채 검사의 서재로 돌아갔다.\n\n\"보십시오, 저는 한숨도 못 잤습니다.\" 빌포르가 손도 대지 않은 침대를 가리키며 말했다. \"슬픔은 저를 마비시키지 못합니다. 이틀 밤이나 침대에 들지 못했지요. 하지만 제 책상을 보십시오. 이 이틀 낮밤 동안 제가 쓴 것을 보세요. 저 서류들을 가득 채워, 살인자 베네데토에 대한 기소장을 작성해 냈습니다. 오, 일이여, 일이여. 나의 열정, 나의 기쁨, 나의 환희여. 내 슬픔을 달래 줄 것은 바로 너로구나!\" 그러고는 발작하듯 다브리니의 손을 부여잡았다.\n\n\"지금 제 도움이 필요하십니까?\" 다브리니가 물었다.\n\n\"아닙니다.\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"다만 열한 시에 다시 와 주십시오. 열두 시에는…… 그…… 그…… 오, 하느님, 가엾고 가엾은 내 아이!\" 검사는 다시 한낱 인간으로 돌아가, 눈을 들어 신음했다.\n\n\"접견실에 나와 계시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"아닙니다. 그 슬픈 일을 맡아 줄 사촌이 하나 있습니다. 저는 일을 하겠습니다, 박사님. 일을 할 때면 저는 모든 것을 잊으니까요.\"\n\n과연 박사가 방을 나서자마자 빌포르는 다시 일에 몰두했다. 문간 계단에서 다브리니는 빌포르가 말한 그 사촌과 마주쳤다. 그는 이 이야기 속에서나 그가 살아가는 세상에서나 하찮기 짝이 없는 인물이었다. 태어날 때부터 남에게 쓸모를 바치도록 점지된 부류의 인간이었다. 시간을 정확히 지켰고, 검은 옷을 입었으며, 모자에는 검은 띠를 둘렀다. 이런 자리에 맞춰 꾸민 얼굴로 사촌의 집에 나타났는데, 그 얼굴은 필요에 따라 얼마든지 바꿀 수 있었다.\n\n열한 시가 되자 장례 마차들이 포석을 깐 안마당으로 굴러 들어왔고, 포부르 생토노레 거리는 한가한 구경꾼들로 가득 찼다. 그들은 부자들의 경사를 구경하든 상사를 구경하든 똑같이 즐거워했고, 공작 부인의 결혼식이나 장례 행렬이나 한결같은 열성으로 몰려드는 자들이었다.\n\n접견실은 점차 들어찼고, 우리의 오랜 벗 몇몇이 모습을 드러냈다. 드브레와 샤토 르노, 그리고 보샹을 두고 하는 말이다. 거기에 법조계와 문단, 군부의 당대 명사들이 함께했다. 빌포르 씨는 사회적 지위보다는 개인적 자질 덕분에 파리 최고 사교계에 드나드는 인물이었던 것이다.\n\n문가에 선 사촌이 손님들을 안내했다. 무심한 사람들에게는 오히려 자기들만큼이나 무덤덤한 사람을 보는 것이 마음 편한 일이었다. 그는 아비나 형제나 연인이라면 으레 그러했을 슬픈 표정을 짓거나 억지 눈물을 강요하지 않았다. 안면이 있는 이들은 곧 작은 무리를 지었다. 그중 한 무리가 드브레와 샤토 르노, 보샹이었다.\n\n\"가엾은 처녀.\" 다른 이들처럼 슬픈 일에 무심코 경의를 표하며 드브레가 말했다. \"가엾은 처녀, 그토록 젊고, 그토록 부유하고, 그토록 아름다웠는데! 샤토 르노, 자네는 상상이나 했겠나, 길어야 삼 주 전에 그녀가 그 혼인 계약서에 서명하려는 모습을 보았을 때 이런 장면을?\"\n\n\"정말이지, 못 했지.\" 샤토 르노가 말했다.\n\n\"그녀를 알았나?\"\n\n\"모르세르 부인 댁에서 다른 사람들 틈에 끼어 한두 번 말을 나눴어. 매력적인 아가씨였지, 좀 우울해 보이긴 했지만. 계모는 어디 있나? 자네 아나?\"\n\n\"우리를 맞아 주는 저 훌륭하신 양반의 부인과 함께 하루를 보내고 있다더군.\"\n\n\"그게 누군가?\"\n\n\"누구 말인가?\"\n\n\"우리를 맞아 주는 저 양반 말일세. 하원의원인가?\"\n\n\"아, 아니야. 나는 매일같이 그런 양반들을 봐야 하는 신세지만,\" 보샹이 말했다. \"저 사람은 통 모르는 얼굴이야.\"\n\n\"이번 죽음을 자네 신문에 실었나?\"\n\n\"실리긴 했는데, 그 기사는 내가 쓴 게 아니야. 사실 빌포르 씨 마음에 들지 의심스러워. 검사 집안이 아닌 다른 어디서 잇따라 네 사람이 죽었더라면, 검사가 그 일에 좀 더 관심을 두었을 거라고 썼거든.\"\n\n\"그래도,\" 샤토 르노가 말했다. \"우리 어머니를 돌봐 주는 다브리니 박사는 이번 일로 절망에 빠졌다고 하던데. 그런데 누굴 찾고 있나, 드브레?\"\n\n\"몬테크리스토 백작을 찾고 있어.\" 청년이 말했다.\n\n\"여기 오는 길에 큰길에서 그를 만났네.\" 보샹이 말했다. \"파리를 떠나려는 모양이더군. 거래 은행으로 가는 길이었어.\"\n\n\"거래 은행? 당글라르가 그의 거래 은행가 아닌가?\" 샤토 르노가 드브레에게 물었다.\n\n\"그런 것 같아.\" 비서관이 약간 불안한 기색으로 대답했다. \"그런데 여기서 안 보이는 사람이 백작만은 아니야. 모렐이 안 보이는군.\"\n\n\"모렐? 그들이 모렐을 아나?\" 샤토 르노가 물었다. \"내 생각에 그는 빌포르 부인에게만 소개됐을 텐데.\"\n\n\"그래도 여기 와 있어야 할 사람인데.\" 드브레가 말했다. \"오늘 밤엔 무슨 얘기가 오갈지 궁금하군. 이 장례식이 오늘의 화제니까. 하지만 쉿, 우리 법무 장관께서 오시는군. 사촌에게 짧은 인사말이라도 건네야 한다고 생각하시겠지.\" 세 청년은 들으려고 가까이 다가갔다.\n\n장례식에 오는 길에 몬테크리스토를 만났다고 한 보샹의 말은 사실이었다. 백작은 쇼세 당탱 거리, 당글라르 씨의 집으로 발걸음을 옮기던 참이었다. 은행가는 백작의 마차가 안마당으로 들어오는 것을 보고, 슬프지만 상냥한 미소를 띠며 그를 맞으러 나왔다.\n\n\"이런,\" 그가 몬테크리스토에게 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"제게 위로의 말을 건네러 오신 모양이군요. 정말이지 불행이 제 집을 차지해 버렸으니까요. 백작을 알아보았을 때, 저는 마침 제가 저 가엾은 모르세르 집안에 못된 마음을 품지 않았던가 자문하던 참이었습니다. '남에게 불행이 닥치기를 바라는 자는 제가 그 불행을 겪는다'는 속담이 들어맞을까 봐서요. 한데 명예를 걸고 말씀드리지만, 저는 '아니다!'라고 답했습니다. 저는 모르세르에게 악의를 품은 적이 없습니다. 저처럼 맨주먹으로 일어선 사람치고는 좀 거만했는지 모르지만, 누구나 제 결점은 있는 법이지요. 백작, 우리 또래 사람들이—백작은 그 축에 들지 않으시지요, 아직 젊은 분이니까—아무튼 우리 또래 사람들이 올해 정말 운이 사나웠다는 걸 아십니까? 보십시오, 저 청교도 같은 검사를 말입니다. 방금 딸을 잃었지요. 사실 거의 온 가족을 그토록 기이한 방식으로 잃었습니다. 모르세르는 명예를 잃고 죽었고요. 그다음엔 저 자신이 베네데토라는 악당의 흉계로 웃음거리가 되었지요. 게다가……\"\n\n\"게다가 또 무엇이 있습니까?\" 백작이 물었다.\n\n\"아아, 모르고 계셨습니까?\"\n\n\"무슨 새로운 변고가요?\"\n\n\"제 딸이……\"\n\n\"당글라르 양 말씀입니까?\"\n\n\"외제니가 우리를 떠났습니다!\"\n\n\"맙소사, 무슨 말씀이십니까?\"\n\n\"사실입니다, 백작. 오, 백작은 아내도 자식도 없으니 얼마나 행복하시겠습니까!\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각하십니까?\"\n\n\"정말 그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"그래서 당글라르 양은……\"\n\n\"그 못된 자가 우리에게 안긴 모욕을 견디지 못하고, 여행을 떠나게 해 달라고 청했지요.\"\n\n\"그래서 떠났습니까?\"\n\n\"엊그제 밤에 떠났습니다.\"\n\n\"당글라르 부인과 함께요?\"\n\n\"아니요, 친척 한 사람과 함께요. 하지만 어쨌든 우리는 사랑하는 외제니를 완전히 잃은 셈입니다. 그 아이의 자존심으로는 다시는 프랑스로 돌아오지 않을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그래도, 남작,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"외동 자식이 유일한 보물인 사람이라면 짓눌렸을 그런 가족의 슬픔도, 아니 다른 어떤 고통도, 백만장자에게는 견딜 만한 것이지요. 철학자들이 흔히 말하고 실리적인 사람들도 늘 동조하듯이, 돈은 숱한 시련을 누그러뜨려 줍니다. 이 으뜸가는 영약의 효험을 인정하신다면, 당신이야말로 아주 쉽게 위로받을 수 있는 분 아니겠습니까. 금융계의 왕이요, 헤아릴 수 없는 권력의 중심이신 당신 말입니다.\"\n\n당글라르는 그가 진심으로 하는 말인지 가늠하려는 듯 곁눈질로 그를 보았다.\n\n\"그렇지요.\" 그가 대답했다. \"재산이 위로가 된다면, 저는 위로받아야 마땅합니다. 저는 부자이니까요.\"\n\n\"어찌나 부자이신지, 선생, 당신의 재산은 피라미드를 닮았습니다. 허물고 싶어도 허물 수 없고, 설령 가능하다 해도 감히 그러지 못할 만큼 말입니다!\"\n\n당글라르는 백작의 호의 어린 농담에 미소 지었다. \"그러고 보니,\" 그가 말했다. \"백작께서 들어오셨을 때 저는 막 작은 채권 다섯 장에 서명하려던 참이었습니다. 벌써 두 장은 서명했는데, 나머지에도 마저 서명해도 괜찮겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그러시지요.\"\n\n잠시 침묵이 흘렀고, 그동안 은행가의 펜 소리만 들렸다. 몬테크리스토는 천장의 금박 몰딩을 살폈다.\n\n\"에스파냐 채권입니까, 아이티 채권입니까, 아니면 나폴리 채권입니까?\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"아닙니다.\" 당글라르가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"프랑스 은행에 대한 채권으로, 소지인에게 지급되는 것입니다. 자, 백작,\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"제가 금융계의 왕이라면 황제라 불러 마땅한 분이신데, 한 장에 백만 프랑씩 나가는 이만한 크기의 종이를 많이 갖고 계십니까?\"\n\n백작은 당글라르가 그토록 자랑스레 내민 종이를 손에 받아 들고 읽었다.\n\n\"'은행 총재 귀하. 제가 예치한 자금에서 제 지시에 따라 백만 프랑을 지급하시고, 같은 금액을 제 계좌에 청구하여 주십시오.\n\n당글라르 남작.'\"\n\n\"하나, 둘, 셋, 넷, 다섯,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"오백만 프랑이라니, 당신은 그야말로 크로이소스로군요!\"\n\n\"제가 사업을 처리하는 방식이지요.\" 당글라르가 말했다.\n\n\"정말 놀랍습니다.\" 백작이 말했다. \"더구나 제 짐작대로 일람불이라면 더욱 그렇지요.\"\n\n\"맞습니다.\" 당글라르가 말했다.\n\n\"이만한 신용을 누리다니 대단한 일입니다. 정말이지 이런 일은 프랑스에서나 가능하지요. 작은 종이 다섯 장에 오백만 프랑이라니! 직접 보지 않고는 믿기 어렵습니다.\"\n\n\"의심하시는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"아니요!\"\n\"말투에 의심이 묻어나는군요. 자, 확인시켜 드리지요. 제 사무원을 데리고 은행에 가 보십시오. 그가 같은 금액의 국고 지급 명령서를 들고 나오는 것을 보시게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"아닙니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 다섯 장의 어음을 접으며 말했다. \"단연코 그럴 것 없습니다. 어찌나 신기한 일인지, 제가 직접 시험해 보겠습니다. 제가 당신 앞으로 가진 신용이 육백만 프랑이지요. 그중 구십만 프랑을 인출했으니, 당신은 아직 제게 오백십만 프랑을 빚지고 계신 셈입니다. 지금 제가 들고 있는 이 종이 다섯 장을 당신의 서명만으로 된 채권으로 받겠습니다. 그리고 여기 우리 사이의 육백만 프랑에 대한 영수증 전액이 있습니다. 미리 작성해 두었지요. 오늘 제게 돈이 몹시 필요하거든요.\"\n\n그러고는 한 손으로 채권을 주머니에 넣으면서, 다른 손으로 당글라르에게 영수증을 내밀었다. 벼락이 발치에 떨어졌다 해도 은행가가 이보다 더 큰 공포를 느끼지는 못했을 것이다.\n\n\"뭐라고요,\" 그가 더듬거렸다. \"그 돈을 가지실 작정입니까? 죄송합니다만, 죄송합니다만, 이 돈은 제가 자선 기금에 갚아야 할 것입니다. 오늘 아침에 지급하기로 약속한 예치금이지요.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇다면,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"저는 이 다섯 장의 어음에 굳이 매달리지 않겠습니다. 다른 형태로 지급해 주십시오. 그저 호기심에서 이걸 받아, 아무런 통지나 준비 없이 당글라르 상회가 단 일 분의 지체도 없이 제게 오백만 프랑을 지급했다고 말할 수 있기를 바랐던 것뿐입니다. 그랬다면 참으로 대단한 일이었겠지요. 하지만 여기 당신의 채권을 돌려드리지요. 다른 식으로 지급해 주십시오.\" 그가 채권을 당글라르 쪽으로 내밀자, 당글라르는 손아귀에서 빼앗기던 먹이를 도로 붙들려는 독수리가 발톱을 뻗치듯 그것을 낚아챘다.\n\n문득 그는 정신을 가다듬어, 격렬히 자제하려 애썼다. 그러자 그의 일그러졌던 얼굴에 차츰 미소가 번져 갔다.\n\n\"물론,\" 그가 말했다. \"백작의 영수증은 돈이나 다름없지요.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇고말고요. 만약 로마에 계셨다면, 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회도 제 영수증을 받고 돈을 지급하는 데 방금 당신만큼이나 군말이 없었을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"용서하십시오, 백작, 용서하십시오.\"\n\n\"그럼 이 돈을 제가 가져도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그러시지요.\" 당글라르가 말했다. 머리카락 뿌리에서부터 땀이 배어 나왔다. \"그러시지요. 가지십시오. 가지세요.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 형용할 수 없는 표정으로 어음을 도로 주머니에 넣었다. 그 표정은 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"자, 잘 생각해 보시오. 후회된다면 아직 시간은 있소.\"\n\n\"아닙니다.\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"아니요, 단연코 아닙니다. 제 서명을 가지십시오. 하지만 아시다시피 사업을 처리할 때 은행가만큼 격식을 따지는 사람도 없지요. 저는 이 돈을 자선 기금에 쓰려 했고, 바로 그 채권으로 지급하지 않으면 그들에게서 돈을 가로채는 것만 같았습니다. 어리석은 일이지요. 마치 한 닢이 다른 한 닢보다 못하기라도 한 것처럼 말입니다. 양해해 주십시오.\" 그러고는 큰 소리로, 그러나 초조하게 웃기 시작했다.\n\n\"물론 양해하지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 너그럽게 말했다. \"그러니 제 주머니에 넣어 두겠습니다.\" 그러고는 채권을 수첩에 넣었다.\n\n\"한데,\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"아직 십만 프랑이 남았는데요?\"\n\n\"아, 그까짓 거야 아무것도 아니지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"잔액이 그쯤 될 겁니다만, 그건 가지십시오. 그러면 우리 사이는 깨끗이 정산되는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"백작,\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"진심으로 하시는 말씀입니까?\"\n\n\"저는 은행가와는 결코 농담하지 않습니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 무례를 물리치는 얼음장 같은 어조로 말했다. 그러고는 문 쪽으로 돌아서는데, 마침 시종이 알렸다.\n\n\"자선 사업 총괄 수납관 보빌 씨께서 오셨습니다.\"\n\n\"맙소사,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"당신의 서명을 받아 내기에 딱 알맞은 때 도착한 모양이군요. 안 그랬으면 누가 이걸 두고 저와 다투었을 테니까요.\"\n\n당글라르는 다시 얼굴이 창백해져서, 서둘러 백작을 배웅했다. 몬테크리스토는 대기실에 서 있던 보빌 씨와 격식을 차린 인사를 나누었다. 백작이 떠나자마자 보빌 씨는 당글라르의 방으로 안내되었다.\n\n수납관이 손에 든 서류첩을 보고 백작의 진지한 얼굴에 옅은 미소가 번졌다. 문간에서 그는 자신의 마차를 발견하고, 곧장 은행으로 향했다. 한편 당글라르는 온갖 동요를 억누르며 수납관을 맞으러 나아갔다. 그의 입가에 짐짓 겸양의 미소가 새겨졌음은 말할 필요도 없겠다.\n\n\"안녕하십니까, 채권자 양반.\" 그가 말했다. \"무엇을 걸어도 좋습니다만, 저를 찾아오신 분은 채권자가 분명할 테니까요.\"\n\n\"맞습니다, 남작.\" 보빌 씨가 대답했다. \"자선 사업이 저를 통해 당신께 인사를 드리는 겁니다. 과부와 고아들이 당신에게서 오백만 프랑에 이르는 자선금을 받아 오라고 저를 보냈습니다.\"\n\n\"그런데도 사람들은 고아들을 가엾이 여겨야 한다고 하지요.\" 당글라르가 농을 이어 가려는 듯 말했다. \"딱한 것들 같으니!\"\n\n\"제가 그들을 대신해 왔습니다.\" 보빌 씨가 말했다. \"그런데 어제 제 편지를 받으셨습니까?\"\n\n\"받았습니다.\"\n\"영수증을 가져왔습니다.\"\n\n\"보빌 씨, 당신의 과부와 고아들이 제게 스물네 시간만 양보해 주셔야겠습니다. 방금 여기서 나가는 것을 보신 몬테크리스토 백작이—보셨지요, 그렇지요?\"\n\n\"네, 그런데요?\"\n\n\"그 몬테크리스토 백작이 방금 그 오백만 프랑을 가져가 버렸거든요.\"\n\n\"어떻게 그런 일이?\"\n\"백작은 제 앞으로 무제한 신용을 가지고 있습니다. 로마의 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회가 개설해 준 신용이지요. 그가 와서 오백만 프랑을 당장 내놓으라기에, 은행 앞으로 끊은 수표로 지급했습니다. 제 자금은 거기 예치돼 있는데, 같은 날 제가 천만 프랑을 인출하면 총재가 좀 이상하게 여기리라는 건 이해하시겠지요. 이틀이면 사정이 다르겠지만요.\" 당글라르가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"이거 참,\" 보빌이 도무지 못 믿겠다는 투로 말했다. \"방금 나가면서 저를 아는 듯이 인사한 그 양반에게 오백만 프랑을 주었다고요?\"\n\n\"그가 당신을 알지도 모르지요, 당신이 그를 모를지라도 말입니다. 몬테크리스토 백작은 누구든 다 아니까요.\"\n\n\"오백만 프랑이라!\"\n\n\"여기 그의 영수증이 있습니다. 당신 눈으로 직접 보십시오.\" 보빌 씨는 당글라르가 내민 종이를 받아 읽었다.\n\n\"당글라르 남작으로부터 오백십만 프랑을 수령하였으며, 이는 로마의 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회가 청구 시 상환할 것임.\"\n\n\"정말 사실이군요.\" 보빌 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회를 아십니까?\"\n\n\"네, 예전에 이십만 프랑짜리 거래를 한 적이 있지요. 하지만 그 뒤로는 이름조차 들어 보지 못했습니다.\"\n\n\"유럽에서 손꼽히는 상회 중 하나지요.\" 당글라르가 영수증을 책상 위로 아무렇게나 던지며 말했다.\n\n\"그런데 그가 당신 한 사람에게만 오백만 프랑을 맡겨 두었다니! 이런, 그 몬테크리스토 백작은 인도의 거부쯤 되는 모양이군요?\"\n\n\"솔직히 그가 뭐 하는 사람인지 저도 모릅니다. 무제한 신용을 세 군데에 갖고 있지요. 하나는 저한테, 하나는 로트실트한테, 하나는 라피트한테요. 게다가, 보십시오,\" 그가 무심하게 덧붙였다. \"십만 프랑의 잔액을 남겨 둠으로써 제게 우선권을 준 셈입니다.\"\n\n보빌 씨는 유난한 감탄의 기색을 드러냈다.\n\n\"그분을 한번 찾아뵈어야겠습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"자선 헌금을 좀 얻어 내야지요.\"\n\n\"아, 그분이라면 틀림없을 겁니다. 그분이 베푸는 자선만 해도 한 달에 이만 프랑에 이르니까요.\"\n\n\"대단하군요! 모르세르 부인과 그 아드님의 본보기를 그분께 들려 드려야겠습니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 본보기 말입니까?\"\n\n\"그들은 전 재산을 병원들에 기부했지요.\"\n\n\"무슨 재산을요?\"\n\n\"자기네 재산을요. 작고한 모르세르 씨의 재산 말입니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 까닭으로요?\"\n\n\"그토록 죄스럽게 모은 돈을 쓰지 않겠다고 해서지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 그들은 무엇으로 살아갑니까?\"\n\n\"어머니는 시골로 물러나고, 아들은 군에 입대한답니다.\"\n\n\"이거 참, 솔직히 별난 양심도 다 있군요.\"\n\n\"어제 제가 그들의 기증 증서를 등록해 주었습니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 그들이 얼마나 가지고 있었답니까?\"\n\n\"아, 많지는 않았어요. 백이삼십만 프랑쯤이었지요. 그건 그렇고, 우리 오백만 프랑 이야기로 돌아갑시다.\"\n\n\"그러지요.\" 당글라르가 세상에서 가장 자연스러운 어조로 말했다. \"그럼 이 돈이 급히 필요하신 겁니까?\"\n\n\"네, 내일 우리 금고 검사가 있거든요.\"\n\n\"내일이요? 어째서 진작 말씀하지 않으셨습니까? 그럼 한 세기나 다름없이 넉넉하지요! 검사는 몇 시에 합니까?\"\n\n\"두 시에요.\"\n\n\"열두 시에 사람을 보내십시오.\" 당글라르가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n보빌 씨는 아무 말 없이 고개를 끄덕이고는 서류첩을 집어 들었다.\n\n\"가만, 생각해 보니 더 나은 방법이 있군요.\" 당글라르가 말했다.\n\n\"어떻게요?\"\n\n\"몬테크리스토 백작의 영수증은 돈이나 마찬가지입니다. 그걸 로트실트나 라피트한테 가져가십시오. 그러면 그 자리에서 받아 줄 겁니다.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요, 로마에서 지급되는 것인데도요?\"\n\n\"물론이지요. 할인료로 오천이나 육천 프랑쯤 들 뿐일 겁니다.\"\n\n수납관이 흠칫 물러섰다.\n\n\"맙소사!\" 그가 말했다. \"내일까지 기다리는 편이 낫겠군요. 별 제안을 다 하시는군요!\"\n\n\"어쩌면,\" 당글라르가 더없는 무례함으로 말했다. \"당신이 메워야 할 부족분이라도 있는 게 아닌가 했지요.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\" 수납관이 말했다.\n\n\"그런 경우라면 다소간 희생을 감수할 만하지요.\"\n\"고맙습니다만, 사양하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 내일로 하지요.\"\n\n\"네, 다만 틀림없어야 합니다.\"\n\n\"아, 저를 놀리시는군요. 내일 열두 시에 사람을 보내십시오. 은행에 통지해 두겠습니다.\"\n\n\"제가 직접 오겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그러면 더욱 좋지요. 당신을 뵙는 기쁨을 누릴 테니까요.\" 두 사람은 악수를 나누었다.\n\n\"그런데,\" 보빌 씨가 말했다. \"여기 오는 길에 마주친 가엾은 빌포르 양의 장례식에는 가지 않으십니까?\"\n\n\"아니요.\" 은행가가 말했다. \"그 베네데토 사건 이후로 제가 좀 우스운 꼴이 되어서, 뒤로 물러나 있습니다.\"\n\n\"무슨, 그건 잘못 생각하시는 겁니다. 그 일에 당신이 무슨 잘못이 있다고요?\"\n\n\"들어 보십시오. 저처럼 흠 잡을 데 없는 이름을 지닌 사람은 좀 예민해지기 마련이지요.\"\n\n\"다들 당신을 안타까워합니다, 선생. 그중에서도 특히 당글라르 양을요!\"\n\n\"가엾은 외제니!\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"그 애가 수도 생활에 들어가려 한다는 걸 아십니까?\"\n\n\"몰랐습니다.\"\n\"아아, 불행히도 너무나 사실입니다. 그 일이 있은 다음 날, 그 애는 아는 수녀 한 사람과 함께 파리를 떠나기로 결심했습니다. 이탈리아나 에스파냐에서 아주 엄격한 수도원을 찾으러 떠났지요.\"\n\n\"오, 끔찍한 일이군요!\" 보빌 씨는 이렇게 탄식하며, 아버지에게 절절한 동정을 표한 뒤 물러갔다. 그러나 그가 떠나기가 무섭게 당글라르는, 프레데리크가 연기한 로베르 마케르를 본 사람만이 알 법한 격렬한 동작으로 외쳤다.\n\n\"멍청한 놈!\"\n그러고는 몬테크리스토의 영수증을 작은 수첩에 넣으며 덧붙였다. \"그래, 열두 시에 오너라. 그때쯤 나는 멀리 가 있을 테니.\"\n\n이어 그는 문을 이중으로 잠그고, 서랍을 모조리 비워 지폐로 오만 프랑가량을 챙겼다. 몇몇 서류는 불태우고, 다른 서류들은 눈에 띄게 펼쳐 둔 채, 편지 한 통을 쓰기 시작했다. 받는 사람은 이러했다.\n\n\"당글라르 남작 부인 귀하.\"\n\n\"오늘 밤 내가 직접 그 여자 탁자에 올려놓아야지.\" 그가 중얼거렸다. 그러고는 서랍에서 여권을 꺼내며 말했다. \"좋아, 두 달은 더 쓸 수 있겠군.\"\n\n# Chapter 105. The Cemetery of Père-Lachaise\n\n* 로베르 마케르: 19세기 프랑스 연극·풍자화에 등장하는 사기꾼 인물로, 배우 프레데리크 르메트르가 연기해 유명해졌다. ―옮긴이 보빌 씨는 정말로 발랑틴을 이 세상의 마지막 거처로 데려가는 장례 행렬과 마주쳤다. 날씨는 잔뜩 흐리고 폭풍이 일 듯했으며, 차가운 바람이 나뭇가지에 몇 잎 남지 않은 누런 잎사귀를 흔들어 떨어뜨리고는 큰길을 가득 메운 군중 사이로 흩뿌렸다. 진정한 파리 사람인 빌포르 씨는 페르라셰즈 묘지만이 파리 가문의 유해를 받아들일 자격이 있다고 여겼다. 그곳에서만 자신에게 속한 시신들이 합당한 이웃들에게 둘러싸일 수 있다는 것이었다. 그래서 그는 납골당을 하나 사들였고, 그곳은 곧 일가의 식구들로 채워졌다. 기념비 앞면에는 \"생메랑 가문과 빌포르 가문\"이라는 글귀가 새겨져 있었다. 발랑틴의 어머니인 가엾은 르네가 마지막으로 남긴 소원이 그것이었기 때문이다. 그리하여 화려한 행렬은 포부르 생토노레에서 페르라셰즈를 향해 길게 이어졌다. 파리를 가로지른 행렬은 포부르 뒤 탕플을 지났고, 외곽 큰길을 벗어나 묘지에 다다랐다. 스무 대의 영구 마차 뒤로 쉰 대가 넘는 자가용 마차가 따랐고, 그 뒤로는 오백 명이 넘는 사람들이 걸어서 행렬에 합류했다.\n\n이 사람들은 발랑틴의 죽음에 벼락을 맞은 듯 충격을 받은 젊은이들로, 살을 에는 추운 날씨에도 불구하고, 젊음의 꽃 한가운데서 그렇게 꺾여 버린 아름답고 정숙하며 사랑스러운 처녀의 추억에 마지막 경의를 표하지 않을 수 없었던 이들이었다.\n\n파리를 벗어날 무렵, 말 네 마리가 끄는 마차 한 대가 전속력으로 달려오다가 별안간 멈춰 서는 것이 보였다. 그 안에는 몬테크리스토가 타고 있었다. 백작은 마차에서 내려 걸어서 따라오는 군중 속에 섞여 들었다. 샤토르노가 그를 알아보고는 곧장 자기 쿠페에서 내려 그에게 다가왔다. 보샹도 똑같이 했다.\n\n백작은 군중 사이로 난 모든 틈을 유심히 살폈다. 누군가를 찾고 있는 것이 분명했지만, 그 수색은 실망으로 끝났다.\n\n\"모렐은 어디 있소?\" 그가 물었다. \"두 분 중 어느 분이라도 그가 어디 있는지 아시오?\"\n\n\"저희도 벌써 그 질문을 했습니다.\" 샤토르노가 말했다. \"저희 중 누구도 그를 보지 못했거든요.\"\n\n백작은 입을 다물었지만, 계속해서 주위를 둘러보았다. 마침내 그들은 묘지에 다다랐다. 몬테크리스토의 날카로운 눈이 덤불과 나무 무리 사이를 훑었고, 곧 모든 불안에서 벗어났다. 주목 나무들 사이로 그림자 하나가 미끄러지듯 움직이는 것을 보고, 몬테크리스토는 자신이 찾던 사람을 알아본 것이다.\n\n이 웅장한 대도시에서 장례식은 대개 서로 무척 비슷하다. 길고 하얀 길 위로 검은 형체들이 흩어진 모습이 보인다. 땅과 하늘의 침묵은 오직 기념비 둘레에 심긴 산울타리의 가지들이 부스럭거리며 부러지는 소리에만 깨진다. 그다음에는 사제들의 구슬픈 성가가 이어지고, 이따금 꽃 무더기 뒤에 숨은 어떤 여인에게서 새어 나오는 비통한 흐느낌이 그 소리에 섞여 든다.\n\n몬테크리스토가 눈여겨본 그림자는 아벨라르와 엘로이즈의 무덤 뒤를 재빠르게 지나, 영구차를 끄는 말들의 머리 가까이에 자리를 잡았고, 장의사 일꾼들을 따라 그들과 함께 매장이 예정된 자리에 이르렀다. 사람들은 저마다 무언가에 정신이 팔려 있었다. 몬테크리스토의 눈에는 그 그림자밖에 보이지 않았으나, 다른 누구도 그를 눈여겨보지 않았다. 백작은 두 번이나 행렬에서 빠져나와, 자신이 주목하는 그자가 옷 속에 무기를 감추고 있는지 살폈다. 행렬이 멈추자, 그 그림자는 모렐임이 드러났다. 그는 외투 단추를 목까지 채우고, 얼굴은 납빛이 된 채, 손가락 사이로 모자를 경련하듯 짓이기며, 영묘를 내려다볼 수 있는 높은 곳에 선 나무에 기대어 있었다. 그래서 장례의 어떤 세세한 장면도 그의 관찰을 벗어날 수 없었다.\n\n모든 것이 여느 때와 다름없이 진행되었다. 이 광경에 가장 마음이 동하지 않은 몇몇 사내들이 추도사를 했다. 어떤 이는 이 때 이른 죽음을 애도했고, 어떤 이는 아버지의 슬픔을 길게 늘어놓았으며, 무척 재치 있는 어떤 사람은 정의의 손길이 막 내리치려던 죄인들을 위해 발랑틴이 아버지에게 용서를 빌었다는 사실을 인용했다. 그러다 마침내 그들은 은유와 애도사의 밑천을, 말레르브가 뒤 페리에에게 바친 시구를 공들여 변주한 표현들을 다 써 버리고 말았다.\n\n몬테크리스토는 아무것도 듣지도 보지도 못했다. 아니, 오직 모렐만을 보고 있었다. 그의 마음속에 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 아는 사람들에게는 모렐의 그 평온함이 끔찍한 인상을 주었다.\n\n\"보게.\" 보샹이 모렐을 가리키며 데브레에게 말했다. \"저 위에서 뭘 하고 있는 거지?\" 그리고 그들은 샤토르노의 주의를 그쪽으로 돌렸다.\n\n\"얼굴이 정말 창백하군!\" 샤토르노가 몸서리치며 말했다.\n\n\"추워서 그렇겠지.\" 데브레가 말했다.\n\n\"전혀 아닐세.\" 샤토르노가 천천히 말했다. \"내가 보기엔 몹시 격앙된 것 같은데. 저 친구는 감수성이 아주 예민하거든.\"\n\n\"흥.\" 데브레가 말했다. \"그는 빌포르 양을 거의 알지도 못했어. 자네 입으로 그렇게 말하지 않았나.\"\n\n\"맞아. 그래도 모르세르 부인 댁에서 그가 그녀와 세 번이나 춤을 췄던 게 기억나는군. 백작님, 그 무도회 기억하십니까? 거기서 백작님이 그토록 큰 화제를 불러일으키셨잖습니까?\"\n\n\"아니요, 기억나지 않소.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. 그는 자신이 무슨 말을, 누구에게 하고 있는지조차 모를 만큼, 감정에 북받쳐 숨을 죽이고 있는 모렐을 지켜보는 데 온통 정신이 쏠려 있었다.\n\n\"추도사가 끝났군. 그럼 안녕히 계시오, 여러분.\" 백작이 격식도 차리지 않고 말했다.\n\n그리고 그는 어디로 가는지 아무도 보지 못한 채 사라져 버렸다.\n\n장례가 끝나자 참석자들은 파리로 돌아갔다. 샤토르노는 잠시 모렐을 찾아보았다. 그러나 그들이 백작이 떠나는 모습을 지켜보는 사이에 모렐은 제자리를 떠나 버렸고, 샤토르노는 찾기를 단념하고 데브레와 보샹에게 합류했다.\n\n몬테크리스토는 커다란 무덤 뒤에 몸을 숨기고 모렐이 오기를 기다렸다. 모렐은 이제 구경꾼들과 일꾼들이 떠나 버린 무덤으로 조금씩 다가왔다. 모렐이 주위를 한번 둘러보았으나, 그 시선이 몬테크리스토가 있는 자리에 닿기 전에 백작은 들키지 않은 채 더 가까이 다가서 있었다. 젊은이는 무릎을 꿇었다. 백작은 목을 빼고 눈을 번뜩이며, 첫 기회가 오면 곧장 모렐에게 덮칠 자세로 서 있었다. 모렐은 고개를 숙여 이마가 돌에 닿을 때까지 굽혔고, 두 손으로 철책을 움켜쥐고서 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"오, 발랑틴!\"\n\n이 두 마디를 듣는 순간 백작의 가슴이 꿰뚫렸다. 그는 앞으로 나아가 젊은이의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 말했다.\n\n\"자넬 찾고 있었네, 친구.\" 몬테크리스토는 격정이 폭발하리라 예상했으나, 그것은 빗나갔다. 모렐이 돌아서며 차분하게 말했던 것이다.\n\n\"보시다시피 저는 기도하고 있었습니다.\" 백작의 꿰뚫는 듯한 시선이 젊은이를 머리끝에서 발끝까지 살폈다. 그러자 그는 조금 마음이 놓이는 듯했다.\n\n\"파리까지 태워다 줄까?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"아니요, 괜찮습니다.\"\n\n\"무언가 필요한 게 있나?\"\n\n\"기도하게 내버려 두십시오.\"\n\n백작은 더 막아서지 않고 물러났다. 그러나 그것은 모렐의 모든 움직임을 지켜볼 수 있는 자리에 자기 몸을 두기 위함일 뿐이었다. 마침내 모렐이 일어나 무릎의 먼지를 털고, 한 번도 뒤돌아보지 않은 채 파리 쪽으로 향했다. 그는 라로케트 거리를 천천히 걸어 내려갔다. 백작은 마차를 돌려보내고 백 걸음쯤 뒤에서 그를 따라갔다. 막시밀리앙은 운하를 건너 큰길을 지나 메슬레 거리로 들어섰다.\n\n모렐이 들어가고 문이 닫힌 지 오 분 뒤에, 그 문은 다시 백작을 위해 열렸다. 쥘리는 정원 입구에 있었는데, 정원사 일에 열성을 다해 매달려 벵골장미 몇 그루를 접붙이느라 여념이 없는 페늘롱을 유심히 지켜보고 있었다. \"어머, 백작님.\" 그녀가 외쳤다. 백작이 메슬레 거리를 찾아올 때마다 이 집안 식구라면 누구나 보이는 그 기쁨을 담은 외침이었다.\n\n\"막시밀리앙이 방금 돌아왔지요, 부인?\" 백작이 물었다.\n\n\"네, 지나가는 걸 본 것 같아요. 그런데 부디, 에마뉘엘을 불러 주세요.\"\n\n\"미안합니다, 부인. 하지만 지금 당장 막시밀리앙의 방으로 올라가 봐야겠습니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"그에게 더없이 중요한 할 말이 있어서요.\"\n\n\"그럼 어서 올라가세요.\" 그녀가 매혹적인 미소를 띠며 말했고, 그 미소는 백작이 사라질 때까지 그를 배웅했다.\n\n몬테크리스토는 곧 일층에서 막시밀리앙의 방으로 이어지는 계단을 뛰어 올라갔다. 층계참에 이르자 그는 귀를 기울여 유심히 들었지만, 사방이 고요했다. 한 가족이 단독으로 쓰는 오래된 집들이 흔히 그렇듯, 방문에는 유리를 끼운 칸막이가 달려 있었다. 그러나 문은 잠겨 있었다. 막시밀리앙은 안에 틀어박혀 있었고, 유리 앞에 붉은 커튼이 쳐져 있어 방 안에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 들여다볼 수 없었다. 좀처럼 동요하지 않는 이 사내의 얼굴에 좀처럼 떠오르지 않던 선명한 핏기가 떠올라, 백작의 불안을 드러내고 있었다.\n\n\"어떻게 해야 하나!\" 그가 내뱉고는 잠시 생각에 잠겼다. \"벨을 울릴까? 아니, 손님이 왔다고 알리는 벨소리는, 막시밀리앙 같은 처지에 놓인 사람의 결심을 앞당길 뿐이다. 그러면 벨소리에 이어 더 요란한 소리가 따라오겠지.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 머리끝에서 발끝까지 부르르 떨었고, 마치 번개처럼 빠르게 결심을 굳힌 듯, 팔꿈치로 유리 한 장을 내리쳤다. 유리가 산산조각 나자, 그는 커튼을 걷어 내고 모렐을 보았다. 책상에서 무언가를 쓰고 있던 모렐이 유리 깨지는 소리에 자리에서 벌떡 튀어 올랐다.\n\n\"천번만번 용서를 비네.\" 백작이 말했다. \"별일 아니야. 미끄러지면서 팔꿈치로 자네 유리 한 장을 깨뜨렸을 뿐일세. 이왕 열린 김에, 이 틈을 타서 자네 방으로 들어가겠네. 신경 쓰지 말게, 신경 쓰지 마!\"\n\n그리고 백작은 깨진 유리 사이로 손을 넣어 문을 열었다. 모렐은 눈에 띄게 당황한 채, 몬테크리스토를 맞으러 왔다. 하지만 그를 맞아들이려는 것이라기보다는 그의 출입을 막으려는 의도에 더 가까웠다.\n\n\"맙소사.\" 몬테크리스토가 팔꿈치를 문지르며 말했다. \"다 자네 하인 탓일세. 자네 집 계단이 어찌나 반들반들한지, 유리 위를 걷는 것 같더군.\"\n\n\"다치셨습니까, 백작님?\" 모렐이 차갑게 물었다.\n\n\"아닌 것 같네. 그런데 거기서 뭘 하고 있었나? 무언가 쓰고 있었지.\"\n\n\"제가요?\"\n\"손가락에 잉크가 묻어 있군.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇군요. 쓰고 있었습니다. 저도 군인이긴 하지만 가끔 글을 쓰거든요.\"\n\n몬테크리스토가 방 안으로 들어섰다. 막시밀리앙은 어쩔 수 없이 그를 지나가게 했지만, 뒤를 바짝 따랐다.\n\n\"무언가 쓰고 있었다고?\" 몬테크리스토가 살피는 눈빛으로 말했다.\n\n\"이미 그렇다고 말씀드리는 영광을 누렸습니다만.\" 모렐이 말했다.\n\n백작이 주위를 둘러보았다.\n\n\"자네 권총이 책상 옆에 놓여 있군.\" 몬테크리스토가 탁자 위의 권총을 손가락으로 가리키며 말했다.\n\n\"여행을 막 떠나려던 참이라서요.\" 모렐이 경멸하듯 대답했다.\n\n\"이보게, 친구.\" 몬테크리스토가 더없이 부드러운 어조로 외쳤다.\n\n\"네?\"\n\n\"이보게, 나의 친애하는 막시밀리앙, 부디 성급한 결심은 하지 말게.\"\n\n\"제가 성급한 결심을 한다고요?\" 모렐이 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다. \"여행을 떠나는 데 무슨 별난 구석이라도 있습니까?\"\n\n\"막시밀리앙.\" 백작이 말했다. \"우리 둘 다 쓰고 있던 가면을 벗어 던지세. 자네가 그 거짓된 평온으로 나를 속이지 못하는 것은, 내가 그 경박한 걱정으로 자네를 속이지 못하는 것과 마찬가지일세. 자네도 이해할 수 있겠지? 내가 이렇게 행동한 것, 저 유리를 깨뜨린 것, 친구의 고독에 끼어든 것, 이 모든 일을 한 것은, 진짜 불안에, 아니 차라리 끔찍한 확신에 떠밀렸기 때문임을 말일세. 모렐, 자네는 스스로 목숨을 끊으려 하고 있어!\"\n\n\"아니, 백작님.\" 모렐이 몸서리치며 말했다. \"어쩌다 그런 생각을 머릿속에 담으셨습니까?\"\n\n\"자네가 스스로 목숨을 끊으려 한다고 내가 말하지 않았나.\" 백작이 말을 이었다. \"그리고 여기 그 증거가 있네.\" 그러고는 책상으로 다가가, 모렐이 쓰기 시작한 편지 위에 덮어 둔 종이를 치우고 그 편지를 손에 집어 들었다.\n\n모렐이 그것을 빼앗으려고 달려들었으나, 몬테크리스토가 그의 의도를 알아채고 무쇠 같은 손아귀로 그의 손목을 붙잡았다.\n\n\"자네는 스스로 목숨을 끊으려는 거야.\" 백작이 말했다. \"그렇게 써 놓았어.\"\n\n\"좋습니다.\" 모렐이 평온한 표정을 거칠고 격렬한 표정으로 바꾸며 말했다. \"좋아요, 제가 정말 이 권총을 제게로 겨누려 한다 칩시다. 누가 저를 막겠습니까? 누가 감히 저를 막을 수 있단 말입니까? 제 모든 희망은 시들어 버렸고, 제 마음은 부서졌으며, 제 삶은 짐덩어리가 되었고, 제 주위의 모든 것이 슬프고 침울합니다. 이 땅이 역겨워졌고, 사람들의 목소리가 저를 미치게 합니다. 제가 죽도록 내버려 두는 것이야말로 자비예요. 살아 있다가는 이성을 잃고 미쳐 버릴 테니까요. 백작님, 제가 진심에서 우러난 비통의 눈물을 흘리며 이 모든 것을 말씀드리는데, 제가 틀렸다고 대답하실 수 있습니까? 이 비참한 삶에 종지부를 찍으려는 저를 막으실 수 있겠습니까? 말씀해 보세요, 백작님, 그러실 용기가 있으십니까?\"\n\n\"있네, 모렐.\" 몬테크리스토가 젊은이의 흥분과는 묘하게 대조되는 평온함으로 말했다. \"있고말고, 나는 그렇게 할 걸세.\"\n\n\"백작님이요?\" 모렐이 점점 더 분노와 원망을 키우며 외쳤다. \"거짓된 희망으로 저를 속이고, 헛된 약속으로 저를 달래고 어르신 분이 말입니까? 그녀를 구하지는 못했다 해도, 적어도 제 품에서 숨을 거두는 모습은 볼 수 있었을 텐데 말입니다! 모든 것을, 심지어 감추어진 지식의 원천까지도 다 안다고 자처하시는 분이, 이 땅의 수호천사 노릇을 하시는 분이, 한 어린 처녀에게 투여된 독의 해독제 하나조차 찾아내지 못하셨잖습니까! 아, 백작님, 정말이지 제 눈에 가증스럽지만 않았던들, 백작님은 제 연민을 자아내셨을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"모렐……\"\n\n\"네, 가면을 벗으라고 하셨으니, 그렇게 하지요. 그럼 만족하시겠지요! 묘지에서 제게 말을 거셨을 때, 저는 대답했습니다. 제 마음이 누그러졌으니까요. 여기 오셨을 때, 저는 백작님을 들여보내 드렸습니다. 하지만 백작님이 제 믿음을 저버리고, 제가 온갖 고통을 다 겪었다고 여긴 마당에 또 다른 고문을 고안해 내셨으니, 그렇다면, 제 거짓 은인이신 몬테크리스토 백작님, 그렇다면, 만인의 수호자이신 몬테크리스토 백작님, 만족하십시오, 백작님은 친구의 죽음을 지켜보시게 될 겁니다.\" 그러고는 모렐이 미친 듯한 웃음을 터뜨리며 다시 권총을 향해 달려들었다.\n\n\"다시 한번 말하네만, 자네는 자살하지 못할 거야.\"\n\n\"그럼 막아 보시지요!\" 모렐이 다시 몸부림치며 대꾸했으나, 처음과 마찬가지로 백작의 무쇠 같은 손아귀에서 벗어나지는 못했다.\n\n\"내가 막을 걸세.\"\n\n\"그럼 대체 당신이 누구이기에, 자유롭고 이성적인 존재들 위에 이런 폭군 같은 권리를 함부로 휘두르는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"내가 누구냐고?\" 몬테크리스토가 되뇌었다. \"들어 보게. 나는 자네에게 '모렐, 자네 아버지의 아들은 오늘 죽지 않을 것이다'라고 말할 권리를 가진, 이 세상에 단 하나뿐인 사람일세.\" 그러고는 몬테크리스토가 위엄과 숭고함이 어린 표정으로 팔짱을 낀 채 젊은이를 향해 다가갔다. 이 사내의 위압적인 태도에 자기도 모르게 압도당한 젊은이는 한 걸음 뒤로 물러났다.\n\n\"어째서 제 아버지를 입에 올리십니까?\" 그가 더듬거렸다. \"어째서 오늘 일에 아버지에 대한 기억을 뒤섞으시는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"바로 내가, 오늘의 자네처럼 스스로 목숨을 끊으려 했던 자네 아버지의 생명을 구한 사람이기 때문일세. 바로 내가, 자네의 어린 누이에게 돈주머니를, 늙은 모렐에게 파라옹호를 보낸 사람이기 때문이지. 바로 내가, 어린 자네를 무릎에 앉히고 보살피던 에드몽 당테스이기 때문일세.\"\n\n모렐은 다시 한 걸음 뒤로 물러섰다. 비틀거리며, 숨이 막힌 채, 짓눌린 듯이. 그러더니 온몸의 힘이 풀려, 몬테크리스토의 발치에 엎드러졌다. 그 순간 그의 빼어난 천성이 완전히, 그리고 돌연히 뒤바뀌었다. 그는 벌떡 일어나 방을 뛰쳐나가 계단으로 달려가며 힘차게 외쳤다. \"쥘리, 쥘리! 에마뉘엘, 에마뉘엘!\"\n\n몬테크리스토도 그곳을 빠져나가려 했으나, 막시밀리앙은 죽는 한이 있어도 문손잡이를 놓지 않으려 했고, 백작을 안에 가둔 채 문을 닫아 버렸다. 쥘리와 에마뉘엘, 그리고 하인 몇이 막시밀리앙의 외침을 듣고 놀라 달려 올라왔다. 모렐은 그들의 손을 붙잡고 문을 열어젖히며 흐느낌에 막힌 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"무릎을 꿇어요, 무릎을 꿇어! 이분은 우리의 은인이셔, 우리 아버지를 구해 주신 분이야! 이분은……\"\n\n그는 \"에드몽 당테스\"라고 덧붙이려 했으나, 백작이 그의 팔을 붙잡아 막았다.\n\n쥘리는 백작의 품에 몸을 던졌다. 에마뉘엘은 수호천사를 끌어안듯 그를 껴안았다. 모렐은 다시 무릎을 꿇고 이마로 바닥을 짓찧었다. 그러자 무쇠 같은 심장을 지닌 그 사내도 가슴속에서 마음이 부풀어 오르는 것을 느꼈다. 목구멍에서 불꽃이 솟구쳐 눈으로 치미는 듯했고, 그는 고개를 숙이고 울었다. 한동안 방 안에는 잇따른 흐느낌 소리밖에 들리지 않았고, 그동안 그들의 감사하는 마음에서 피어오른 향이 하늘로 올라갔다. 쥘리는 깊은 감격에서 채 헤어나기도 전에 방을 뛰쳐나가 아래층으로 내려가, 어린아이 같은 기쁨에 휩싸여 응접실로 달려가서는, 메이앙 가로수길의 낯선 이가 준 돈주머니를 덮고 있던 수정 덮개를 들어 올렸다. 그러는 동안 에마뉘엘이 떨리는 목소리로 백작에게 말했다.\n\n\"오, 백작님, 저희가 그토록 자주 얼굴 모르는 은인 이야기를 하고, 그분의 기억에 그토록 감사와 흠모의 마음을 바치는 것을 보시고도, 어떻게 그렇게 오랫동안 정체를 밝히지 않으실 수 있었습니까? 오, 그건 저희에게 잔인한 일이었습니다. 그리고, 감히 말씀드리자면, 백작님 자신에게도요.\"\n\n\"들어 보게, 나의 친구들이여.\" 백작이 말했다. \"지난 십일 년 동안 우리는 정말로 친구였으니, 그렇게 불러도 되겠지. 이 비밀이 드러나게 된 것은 자네들이 결코 알아서는 안 될 어떤 큰 사건 때문이었네. 나는 그것을 평생 내 가슴속에 묻어 두고 싶었지만, 자네들의 형제 막시밀리앙이 폭력으로 그것을 내게서 빼앗아 낸 걸세. 지금은 그가 후회하고 있을 거라 확신하네만.\"\n\n그러고는 몸을 돌려, 여전히 무릎을 꿇고 있던 모렐이 안락의자에 몸을 내던지듯 기댄 것을 보고는, 에마뉘엘의 손을 의미심장하게 꽉 쥐며 낮은 목소리로 덧붙였다. \"저 친구를 잘 지켜보게.\"\n\n\"어째서요?\" 젊은이가 놀라며 물었다.\n\n\"내 입으로는 설명할 수 없네. 하지만 저 친구를 잘 지켜보게.\" 에마뉘엘은 방 안을 둘러보다가 권총을 발견했다. 그의 눈이 그 무기에 멎었고, 그는 손가락으로 그것을 가리켰다. 몬테크리스토가 고개를 숙였다. 에마뉘엘이 권총 쪽으로 다가갔다.\n\n\"그냥 두게.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. 그러고는 모렐에게로 걸어가 그의 손을 잡았다. 젊은이의 격렬한 동요는 깊은 망연자실로 바뀌어 있었다. 쥘리가 비단 돈주머니를 손에 든 채 돌아왔고, 기쁨의 눈물이 장미 위의 이슬방울처럼 그녀의 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸다.\n\n\"이게 그 유물이에요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이제 저희 은인을 알게 되었다고 해서 이게 덜 소중해지리라고는 생각지 마세요!\"\n\n\"얘야.\" 몬테크리스토가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"그 돈주머니는 내가 도로 가져가게 해 주겠니? 이제 너희가 내 얼굴을 알게 되었으니, 나는 오직 너희가 내게 베풀어 주기를 바라는 그 애정으로만 기억되고 싶구나.\"\n\n\"오.\" 쥘리가 돈주머니를 가슴에 꼭 끌어안으며 말했다. \"안 돼요, 안 돼요, 제발 가져가지 마세요. 언젠가 불행한 날이 오면 저희를 떠나실 거잖아요, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"제대로 알아맞혔구려, 부인.\" 몬테크리스토가 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"일주일 뒤면 나는 이 나라를 떠나 있을 거요. 하늘의 복수를 받아 마땅한 수많은 사람들이 행복하게 살아가는 동안, 내 아버지는 굶주림과 비탄 속에 돌아가신 이 나라를 말이오.\"\n\n자신의 떠남을 알리면서 백작은 모렐에게 눈길을 고정했고, \"나는 이 나라를 떠나 있을 거요\"라는 말이 그를 무기력에서 깨우지 못했음을 알아챘다. 그제야 그는 친구의 슬픔과 또 한 번 싸워야 한다는 것을 깨닫고, 에마뉘엘과 쥘리의 손을 잡아 자기 손 안에 꼭 쥐며, 아버지처럼 온화한 위엄을 담아 말했다.\n\n\"착한 친구들이여, 나와 막시밀리앙 둘만 있게 해 주게.\"\n\n쥘리는 몬테크리스토가 깜빡한 그 소중한 유물을 가지고 빠져나갈 길이 열렸음을 알아챘다. 그녀는 남편을 문 쪽으로 끌었다. \"우리는 자리를 비켜 줘요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n백작은 조각상처럼 꼼짝 않고 있는 모렐과 단둘이 남았다.\n\n\"자.\" 몬테크리스토가 손가락으로 그의 어깨를 건드리며 말했다. \"다시 사내대장부가 되었는가, 막시밀리앙?\"\n\n\"네, 다시 고통을 느끼기 시작했으니까요.\"\n\n백작은 눈살을 찌푸렸다. 침울한 망설임에 잠긴 듯했다.\n\n\"막시밀리앙, 막시밀리앙.\" 그가 말했다. \"자네가 빠져든 그 생각들은 그리스도교인답지 못하네.\"\n\n\"오, 염려 마십시오, 친구여.\" 모렐이 고개를 들고 백작을 향해 부드러운 표정으로 미소 지으며 말했다. \"이제 더는 제 목숨을 끊으려 들지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 이제 권총도, 절망도 더는 없는 건가?\"\n\n\"네. 총알이나 칼보다 더 나은 슬픔의 치료법을 찾아냈으니까요.\"\n\n\"가엾은 친구, 그게 무엇인가?\"\n\n\"제 슬픔이 절로 저를 죽여 줄 겁니다.\"\n\n\"이보게, 친구.\" 몬테크리스토가 그 못지않은 우수에 찬 표정으로 말했다. \"내 말을 들어 보게. 언젠가 나도 자네 같은 절망의 순간에, 그것이 비슷한 결심으로 이어졌으니, 나 역시 스스로 목숨을 끊으려 한 적이 있네. 언젠가 자네 아버지도, 똑같이 절망한 나머지 스스로 목숨을 끊으려 했지. 만약 누군가가 자네 아버지에게, 그가 권총을 머리에 갖다 댄 바로 그 순간에, 만약 누군가가 내게, 사흘 동안 입에 대지 않은 음식을 감옥에서 밀쳐 냈을 때, 만약 누군가가 그때 우리 둘 중 어느 한 사람에게라도 '살아라, 언젠가 그대가 행복해지고 삶을 축복하는 날이 오리라!' 하고 말했더라면, 그게 누구의 목소리였든 우리는 의심의 미소를 띠거나 못 믿겠다는 고뇌에 잠겨 들었을 걸세. 그런데도 자네 아버지는 자네를 끌어안으며 몇 번이나 삶을 축복했던가. 나 자신도 몇 번이나……\"\n\n\"아.\" 모렐이 백작의 말을 가로막으며 외쳤다. \"백작님은 자유를 잃으셨을 뿐이고, 제 아버지는 재산을 잃으셨을 뿐이지만, 저는 발랑틴을 잃었습니다.\"\n\n\"나를 보게.\" 몬테크리스토가, 때때로 그를 그토록 웅변적이고 설득력 있게 만들던 그 표정으로 말했다. \"나를 보게. 내 눈에는 눈물이 없고, 내 핏줄에는 열기가 없네. 그런데도 나는 자네가 괴로워하는 것을 보고 있어. 막시밀리앙, 나는 자네를 친자식처럼 사랑한다네. 자, 이것이 자네에게 말해 주지 않는가? 슬픔 속에도, 삶 속과 마찬가지로, 늘 그 너머에 기대할 무언가가 있다는 것을 말일세. 그러니 모렐, 내가 자네에게 살아 달라고 간청하고, 또 명령하는 것은, 언젠가 자네가 자기 목숨을 지켜 준 일을 두고 내게 고마워할 거라는 확신에서 나온 것이네.\"\n\n\"오, 하늘이여.\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"오, 하늘이여, 무슨 말씀을 하시는 겁니까, 백작님? 조심하십시오. 어쩌면 백작님은 한 번도 사랑해 본 적이 없으신지도 모르지요!\"\n\n\"어린애 같으니!\" 백작이 대꾸했다.\n\n\"제 말은, 제가 사랑하는 것처럼 말입니다. 보십시오, 저는 어른이 된 뒤로 줄곧 군인이었습니다. 사랑이라곤 모른 채 스물아홉 살이 되었지요. 그 전에 겪은 어떤 감정도 사랑이라는 이름을 붙일 만한 것은 못 되었으니까요. 그런데 스물아홉에 발랑틴을 만났습니다. 이 년 동안 그녀를 사랑했고, 이 년 동안 그녀의 마음속에 마치 책에 적힌 글처럼 딸로서, 또 아내로서의 온갖 미덕이 새겨져 있는 것을 보았습니다. 백작님, 발랑틴을 제 사람으로 갖는 것은 이 세상에 허락되기에는 너무나 무한하고, 너무나 황홀하고, 너무나 완전하고, 너무나 신성한 행복이었습니다. 그래서 제게 거부된 것이겠지요. 하지만 발랑틴이 없는 이 땅은 황량할 뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"내가 자네더러 희망을 가지라고 했네.\" 백작이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 조심하십시오, 다시 말씀드리지만요. 백작님은 저를 설득하려 하시는데, 만약 성공하신다면 저는 이성을 잃고 말 겁니다. 발랑틴을 다시 볼 수 있으리라는 희망을 품게 될 테니까요.\"\n\n백작이 미소 지었다.\n\n\"나의 친구여, 나의 아버지 같은 분이여.\" 모렐이 격앙되어 말했다. \"조심하십시오, 거듭 말씀드립니다. 백작님이 제게 휘두르시는 그 힘이 저를 두렵게 합니다. 말씀하시기 전에 그 말을 잘 헤아려 주십시오. 벌써 제 눈이 더 빛나기 시작했고, 제 심장이 거세게 뛰고 있으니까요. 부디 신중하셔야 합니다. 안 그러면 저로 하여금 초자연적인 힘을 믿게 만드실 겁니다. 백작님이 죽은 자를 불러내라 하시든, 물 위를 걸으라 하시든, 저는 따를 수밖에 없을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"희망을 가지게, 친구여.\" 백작이 되풀이했다.\n\n\"아.\" 모렐이 격앙의 절정에서 절망의 나락으로 떨어지며 말했다. \"아, 백작님은 저를 가지고 노시는군요. 비명이 거슬린다고 꿀 같은 말로 아이를 달래는, 그 착한, 아니 차라리 이기적인 어머니들처럼요. 아닙니다, 친구여, 제가 백작님께 경고한 것이 잘못이었습니다. 염려 마십시오, 제 슬픔을 가슴속 깊디깊은 곳에 묻어 두겠습니다. 어찌나 잘 감추는지, 백작님이 저를 가엾이 여기실 마음조차 들지 않게 할 겁니다. 안녕히 계십시오, 친구여, 안녕히!\"\n\n\"그 반대일세.\" 백작이 말했다. \"이제부터 자네는 나와 함께 살아야 하네. 나를 떠나서는 안 돼. 일주일 뒤면 우리는 프랑스를 등지고 떠나 있을 걸세.\"\n\n\"그러고도 여전히 제게 희망을 가지라 하십니까?\"\n\n\"내가 자네더러 희망을 가지라 하는 것은, 자네를 낫게 할 방법을 가지고 있기 때문일세.\"\n\n\"백작님, 그게 가능하다면, 저를 전보다 더 슬프게 만드시는군요. 백작님은 이 타격의 결과가 그저 흔한 슬픔이라 여기시고, 흔한 치료법으로, 그러니까 환경을 바꾸는 것으로 그걸 고치려 하시는 겁니다.\" 그러고는 모렐이 경멸 어린 불신에 잠겨 고개를 떨구었다.\n\n\"더 무슨 말을 할 수 있겠나?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다. \"나는 내가 제안하는 치료법을 믿고 있고, 다만 그것이 효험이 있다고 자네에게 장담할 수 있도록 허락해 달라고 청할 뿐일세.\"\n\n\"백작님, 제 고통을 길게 끌고 계실 뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면.\" 백작이 말했다. \"자네의 그 나약한 정신은 내가 청하는 시도조차 허락하지 않겠다는 건가? 자, 몬테크리스토 백작이 무엇을 할 수 있는지 자네는 아는가? 그가 이 땅의 존재들을 자기 손아귀에 쥐고 있다는 것을? 아니, 그가 거의 기적까지도 행할 수 있다는 것을 아느냔 말일세? 그러니 내가 이루고자 하는 그 기적을 기다려 보게. 그러지 않으면……\"\n\"그러지 않으면요?\" 모렐이 되뇌었다.\n\n\"그러지 않으면, 조심하게, 모렐. 내가 자네를 배은망덕한 자라 부르게 될지도 모르니까.\"\n\n\"저를 가엾이 여겨 주십시오, 백작님!\"\n\n\"막시밀리앙, 나는 자네를 무척이나 가엾이 여긴다네. 그러니 내 말을 주의 깊게 들어 보게. 만약 내가 한 달 안에, 그날, 바로 그 시각까지 자네를 낫게 하지 못한다면, 내 말을 똑똑히 새겨 두게, 모렐, 그때는 자네 앞에 장전된 권총과 가장 치명적인 이탈리아 독약 한 잔을 놓아 주겠네. 발랑틴을 죽인 그 독보다 더 확실하고 더 빠르게 듣는 독을 말일세.\"\n\n\"제게 약속해 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그러겠네. 나도 사람이고, 자네처럼 고통받아 보았으며, 또 자살을 생각해 본 적도 있으니까. 사실, 불행이 나를 떠난 뒤로도 나는 영원한 잠의 환희를 몇 번이나 갈망했다네.\"\n\n\"하지만 정말로 이걸 제게 약속하시는 거지요?\" 모렐이 도취된 듯 말했다.\n\n\"약속할 뿐만 아니라, 맹세하네!\" 몬테크리스토가 손을 내밀며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 한 달 뒤에, 백작님의 명예를 걸고, 제가 위로받지 못하거든, 제 목숨을 제 손에 맡겨 주시고, 무슨 일이 있어도 저를 배은망덕한 자라 부르지 않으시는 거지요?\"\n\n\"한 달 뒤, 그날, 바로 그 시각이라네. 그리고 그 날짜는 신성한 날일세, 막시밀리앙. 자네가 기억할는지 모르겠네만, 오늘이 9월 5일이라네. 죽기를 바라던 자네 아버지의 생명을 내가 구한 지 오늘로 꼭 십 년이 되는 날일세.\"\n\n모렐은 백작의 손을 붙잡아 입을 맞추었다. 백작은 그가 자신에게 마땅하다고 여기는 그 경의를 표하도록 내버려 두었다.\n\n\"한 달 뒤, 그때 우리가 마주 앉아 있을 그 탁자 위에서, 자네는 좋은 권총과 감미로운 약물 한 잔을 보게 될 걸세. 하지만 그 대신, 그때까지는 자네 목숨을 끊으려 들지 않겠다고 내게 약속해야 하네.\"\n\n\"오, 저도 그것을 맹세합니다!\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 젊은이를 자기 쪽으로 끌어당겨 한동안 가슴에 꼭 끌어안았다. \"이제.\" 그가 말했다. \"오늘부터 자네는 나에게 와서 함께 살게. 하이데의 거처를 써도 좋아. 그러면 적어도 내 딸의 빈자리를 내 아들이 채워 주는 셈이 되겠지.\"\n\n\"하이데요?\" 모렐이 말했다. \"그녀는 어떻게 되었습니까?\"\n\n\"어젯밤에 떠났네.\"\n\n\"백작님을 두고요?\"\n\n\"나를 기다리려고 말일세. 그러니 채비를 갖추고 있다가 샹젤리제에서 나와 합류해, 누구에게도 내가 떠나는 것을 들키지 않게 나를 이 집에서 데리고 나가 주게.\"\n\n막시밀리앙은 고개를 숙이고, 어린아이 같은 경건한 마음으로 그 말에 따랐다.\n\n# Chapter 106. Dividing the Proceeds\n\n생제르맹데프레 거리에 있는 집의 2층 방은 알베르 드 모르세르가 어머니를 위해 골라 둔 곳으로, 지금은 무척 수상쩍은 인물에게 세를 주고 있었다. 그 사내의 얼굴은 관리인조차 한 번도 본 적이 없었다. 겨울이면 추운 밤 마부들이 두르는 커다란 붉은 손수건에 턱을 파묻었고, 여름이면 문 앞에 다다를 때마다 으레 코를 푸는 시늉을 했기 때문이다. 관례와 달리 이 신사는 누구의 감시도 받지 않았다. 신분이 높은 사람이며 무례한 참견을 결코 용납하지 않는다는 소문이 돌았기에, 그의 익명은 엄격하게 존중되었다.\n\n그의 방문은 제법 규칙적이었다. 더러 정해진 시각보다 조금 일찍, 또는 조금 늦게 나타나기도 했지만, 여름이든 겨울이든 대개 네 시쯤이면 방을 차지했다. 다만 그곳에서 밤을 보내는 일은 결코 없었다. 겨울철 세 시 반이면 이 작은 방을 관리하는 신중한 하인이 불을 지폈고, 여름철 같은 시각이면 탁자 위에 얼음 음료가 놓였다. 그리고 이미 말했듯, 네 시가 되면 그 수수께끼의 인물이 도착했다.\n\n이십 분쯤 지나면 마차 한 대가 집 앞에 멈췄고, 검은색이나 짙은 파란색 드레스를 입고 언제나 두꺼운 베일을 쓴 한 부인이 내렸다. 그녀는 그림자처럼 수위실 앞을 지나, 가벼운 발끝에서 아무 소리도 새어 나오지 않게 계단을 뛰어 올라갔다. 누구도 그녀에게 어디로 가느냐고 묻지 않았다. 그러니 신사와 마찬가지로 그녀의 얼굴 또한 두 관리인에게는 전혀 알려지지 않았다. 어쩌면 그 신중함에 있어서는 파리 전체를 통틀어 둘도 없는 자들이었으리라. 그녀가 2층에서 멈췄다는 것은 굳이 말할 필요도 없다. 그러면 그녀는 어떤 독특한 방식으로 문을 두드렸고, 문이 열려 그녀를 들인 뒤 다시 잠겼으며, 호기심도 그 이상은 파고들지 못했다. 집을 나설 때도 들어올 때와 똑같이 조심했다. 언제나 부인이 먼저 나갔고, 마차에 오르기가 무섭게 마차는 떠났다. 어떤 때는 오른쪽으로, 어떤 때는 왼쪽으로. 그러고서 이십 분쯤 지나면 신사 역시 넥타이에 얼굴을 파묻거나 손수건으로 가린 채 떠나갔다.\n\n몬테크리스토가 당글라르를 찾아간 다음 날, 그 수수께끼의 세입자는 오후 네 시가 아니라 오전 열 시에 들어섰다. 거의 곧바로, 평소의 시간 간격도 없이 삯마차 한 대가 도착했고, 베일을 쓴 부인이 황급히 계단을 뛰어 올라갔다. 문이 열렸지만, 채 닫히기도 전에 부인이 외쳤다.\n\n\"오, 뤼시앵, 오, 내 친구!\"\n\n관리인은 그제야 처음으로 세입자의 이름이 뤼시앵이라는 것을 알게 되었다. 하지만 그는 문지기로서 완벽한 인물이었기에, 아내에게조차 말하지 않기로 마음먹었다.\n\n\"자, 무슨 일이오, 그대?\" 부인의 동요로 이름이 드러난 신사가 물었다. \"무슨 일인지 말해 보시오.\"\n\n\"오, 뤼시앵, 당신에게 털어놓아도 될까요?\"\n\n\"물론이오, 그래도 된다는 걸 잘 알잖소. 그런데 대체 무슨 일이오? 오늘 아침 당신 쪽지 때문에 나는 완전히 어리둥절했소. 이 다급함이며, 평소와 다른 약속이며. 자, 어서 내 불안을 풀어 주시오. 아니면 차라리 단번에 나를 겁먹게 하든가.\"\n\n\"뤼시앵, 큰일이 났어요!\" 부인이 뤼시앵을 살피듯 바라보며 말했다. \"당글라르 씨가 어젯밤에 떠났어요!\"\n\n\"떠나다니? 당글라르 씨가 떠났다고? 어디로 갔소?\"\n\n\"몰라요.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 말이오? 돌아오지 않을 작정으로 떠났단 말이오?\"\n\n\"틀림없어요. 밤 열 시에 그이의 말들이 그이를 샤랑통 관문까지 데려갔어요. 거기엔 역마차가 한 대 기다리고 있었고요. 그이는 하인을 데리고 마차에 올라타며, 퐁텐블로로 간다고 했어요.\"\n\n\"그럼 대체 무슨 뜻으로……\"\n\n\"잠깐만요. 그이가 제게 편지를 한 통 남겼어요.\"\n\n\"편지를?\"\n\n\"네, 읽어 보세요.\"\n\n남작 부인은 주머니에서 편지를 꺼내 드브레에게 건넸다. 드브레는 읽기에 앞서 잠시 멈추었다. 마치 그 내용을 짐작해 보려는 듯, 어쩌면 내용이 무엇이든 어떻게 처신할지 마음을 정하려는 듯했다. 그러나 생각은 단 몇 분 만에 정리된 게 틀림없었다. 그는 남작 부인의 마음에 그토록 큰 불안을 안긴 그 편지를 읽기 시작했고, 내용은 이러했다.\n\n\"'부인, 그리고 더없이 충실한 아내여.'\"\n\n드브레는 무심코 멈추고 남작 부인을 바라보았다. 그녀의 얼굴이 새빨갛게 물들었다.\n\n\"읽으세요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n드브레가 계속했다.\n\n\"'당신이 이 편지를 받을 때쯤이면, 당신에게는 더 이상 남편이 없을 것이오. 오, 놀랄 것 없소. 당신은 딸을 잃은 것처럼 남편을 잃었을 뿐이니. 다시 말해, 나는 프랑스를 빠져나가는 서른 갈래, 마흔 갈래의 길 가운데 어느 하나를 달리고 있을 거란 뜻이오. 내 행동에 대해 당신에게 약간의 설명을 빚진 셈이니, 당신은 나를 완벽하게 이해할 수 있는 여인이기에 그 설명을 해 주겠소. 그러니 들어 보시오. 나는 오늘 아침 500만 프랑을 받아 그대로 지불했소. 그러자 거의 곧바로 똑같은 액수의 또 다른 청구가 들어왔소. 나는 그 채권자를 내일까지 미뤄 두었고, 그 내일을 피하려고 오늘 떠날 작정이오. 내가 견디기엔 너무도 불쾌한 내일이 될 테니. 당신은 이해하겠지, 그렇지 않소, 더없이 소중한 아내여? 당신이 이해하리라 말하는 까닭은, 당신이 나만큼이나 내 사정을 훤히 꿰고 있기 때문이오. 아니, 오히려 당신이 나보다 더 잘 알고 있을 거요. 나는 한때 꽤 넉넉했던 내 재산의 상당 부분이 어디로 갔는지 모르지만, 부인, 당신은 분명 완벽하게 알고 있을 테니까. 여인들이란 결코 틀리는 법 없는 직감을 지녔으니 말이오. 심지어 그들은 자기들이 고안해 낸 대수 계산으로 그 경이로운 일까지 설명해 내지. 하지만 나는, 내 자신의 숫자밖에 모르는 나는, 어느 날 그 숫자들이 나를 속였다는 것 말고는 아무것도 모르오. 내 몰락의 속도에 감탄했소? 내 금괴들이 순식간에 녹아 사라지는 데 살짝 눈이 부셨소? 고백하건대 나는 불길밖에 보지 못했소. 부디 당신은 그 잿더미 속에서 금붙이라도 좀 찾아냈기를 바라오. 이 위안이 되는 생각을 품고, 나는 당신을 떠나오, 부인, 더없이 신중한 아내여. 당신을 버린다는 양심의 가책 따위는 조금도 없이. 당신에게는 친구들이 남아 있고, 앞서 말한 그 잿더미가 있으며, 무엇보다 내가 서둘러 돌려주는 자유가 있으니. 그리고 여기서, 부인, 한마디 더 덧붙여야겠소. 당신이 우리 집안의 이익과 우리 딸의 재산을 위해 애쓰고 있다고 믿는 동안에는, 나는 철학자처럼 눈을 감았소. 그러나 당신이 그 집안을 거대한 폐허로 만들어 버린 이상, 나는 다른 사내의 재산을 떠받치는 주춧돌은 되지 않겠소. 내가 당신과 결혼할 때 당신은 부유했으나 존경은 거의 받지 못했소. 이렇게 노골적으로 말하는 걸 용서하시오. 하지만 이건 오로지 우리 둘만의 이야기이니, 굳이 말을 가려야 할 이유를 모르겠소. 나는 우리 재산을 불렸고, 그 재산은 지난 십오 년 동안 계속 늘어났소. 그러다 예사롭지 않고 뜻밖의 재앙들이 별안간 그것을 뒤엎어 버렸지. 정직하게 단언하건대, 내 잘못은 조금도 없소. 부인, 당신은 오직 당신 자신의 재산을 불리는 데만 골몰했고, 나는 당신이 성공했으리라 확신하오. 그러니 나는 당신을 데려올 때와 똑같이 떠나오. 부유하나 존경은 거의 받지 못한 채로. 안녕히! 나 또한 이제부터는 내 몫을 위해 일할 작정이오. 당신이 내게 보여 준 본보기에 감사를 받아 주시오. 나도 그 본보기를 따를 생각이니.\n\n당신의 더없이 헌신적인 남편,\n\n당글라르 남작.'\"\n\n남작 부인은 드브레가 이 길고 고통스러운 편지를 읽는 동안 그를 지켜보았고, 그가 자제력을 발휘하면서도 한두 번 안색이 변하는 것을 보았다. 다 읽고 나자 드브레는 편지를 접고 다시 생각에 잠긴 자세로 돌아갔다.\n\n\"그래서요?\" 당글라르 부인이 물었다. 그 불안은 충분히 이해할 만한 것이었다.\n\n\"그래서라니요, 부인?\" 드브레가 망설임 없이 되물었다.\n\n\"그 편지를 읽고 어떤 생각이 드세요?\"\n\n\"오, 아주 간단합니다, 부인. 당글라르 씨가 의심스럽게 떠났다는 생각이 드는군요.\"\n\n\"물론이죠. 하지만 제게 하실 말씀이 그게 다인가요?\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이신지 모르겠군요.\" 드브레가 얼음장처럼 차갑게 말했다.\n\n\"그이는 가 버렸어요! 가서 다시는 돌아오지 않을 거예요!\"\n\n\"오, 부인, 그렇게 생각하지 마십시오!\"\n\n\"단언컨대 그이는 절대 돌아오지 않아요. 나는 그 사람 성격을 알아요. 자기 이익을 위해 내린 결심이라면 결코 굽히지 않는 사람이죠. 나를 조금이라도 써먹을 수 있었다면 데려갔을 거예요. 우리가 헤어지는 편이 자기에게 이득이 되니까 나를 파리에 남겨 둔 거고요. 그러니 그이는 떠났고, 나는 영원히 자유로워졌어요.\" 당글라르 부인이 여전히 애원하는 어조로 덧붙였다.\n\n드브레는 대답하는 대신, 그녀가 초조하게 답을 구하는 자세로 그대로 있게 내버려 두었다.\n\n\"그래서요?\" 마침내 그녀가 말했다. \"제게 대답을 안 하시겠어요?\"\n\n\"한 가지만 여쭙겠습니다. 앞으로 어쩌실 작정입니까?\"\n\n\"그걸 당신에게 물어보려던 참이었어요.\" 남작 부인이 가슴을 두근대며 대답했다.\n\n\"아, 그렇다면 제 조언을 구하시겠다는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"네, 당신 조언을 구하고 싶어요.\" 당글라르 부인이 애타게 기대하며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 제 조언을 받아들이실 생각이라면,\" 청년이 차갑게 말했다. \"여행을 떠나시기를 권하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"여행이라니!\" 그녀가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"그렇습니다. 당글라르 씨 말마따나 부인은 부유하고 완전히 자유로우시죠. 제 생각엔 당글라르 양의 파혼과 당글라르 씨의 실종이라는 이중의 재앙이 닥친 마당에, 파리를 떠나시는 게 절대적으로 필요합니다. 세상은 부인이 버림받고 가난해졌다고 여길 겁니다. 파산한 사내의 아내가 부유한 척 행세한다면 결코 용서받지 못할 테니까요. 부인은 그저 보름쯤 파리에 머무시면서, 세상에 버림받았노라 말하고, 가장 친한 벗들에게 이 유기의 자초지종을 들려주시면 됩니다. 그러면 그들이 곧 소문을 퍼뜨릴 겁니다. 그런 다음 보석을 남겨 두고 과부 산입금도 포기하면서 집을 떠나시면, 모두의 입에 부인의 무사무욕을 찬양하는 말이 가득할 겁니다. 사람들은 부인이 버림받았다는 걸 알 테고, 가난해졌다고도 여기겠지요. 부인의 진짜 재정 상태를 아는 사람은 저 하나뿐이고, 저는 정직한 동업자로서 언제든 회계를 넘겨 드릴 준비가 되어 있으니까요.\"\n\n창백하게 굳은 남작 부인이 이 말을 듣던 그 두려움은, 드브레가 그 말을 내뱉던 그 차분한 무관심에 견줄 만했다.\n\n\"버림받았다고요?\" 그녀가 되뇌었다. \"아, 그래요, 정말로 나는 버림받았어요! 당신 말이 옳아요. 누구도 내 처지를 의심하지 못하겠죠.\"\n\n이 도도하고 격렬하게 사랑에 빠진 여인이 드브레의 말에 내놓을 수 있었던 대답은 그것뿐이었다.\n\n\"하지만 그렇다 해도 부인은 부유하십니다. 그것도 아주 부유하시죠.\" 드브레가 수첩에서 서류 몇 장을 꺼내 탁자 위에 펼치며 말을 이었다. 당글라르 부인은 그것을 보지 못했다. 그녀는 두근대는 가슴을 가라앉히고, 당장이라도 쏟아질 듯한 눈물을 억누르느라 정신이 없었다. 마침내 자존심이 이겼고, 동요를 완전히 다스리지는 못했어도, 적어도 눈물 한 방울이 떨어지는 것만은 막아 냈다.\n\n\"부인,\" 드브레가 말했다. \"우리가 동업을 시작한 지도 거의 여섯 달이 됩니다. 부인이 원금 10만 프랑을 대셨지요. 우리 동업은 4월에 시작되었습니다. 5월에 거래에 착수해 그달에 45만 프랑을 벌었습니다. 6월에는 수익이 90만 프랑에 달했고요. 7월에는 170만 프랑을 보탰는데, 아시다시피 그게 에스파냐 공채의 달이었지요. 8월에는 초에 30만 프랑을 잃었지만 13일에 만회했습니다. 그리하여 동업 첫날부터 제가 회계를 마감한 어제까지 따져 보니, 우리 자본은 240만 프랑, 다시 말해 우리 각자에게 120만 프랑씩 돌아갑니다. 그리고 부인,\" 드브레가 증권 중개인 특유의 꼼꼼한 태도로 회계를 넘겨주며 말했다. \"이 돈에서 나온 이자 8만 프랑이 아직 제 수중에 있습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 남작 부인이 말했다. \"당신은 그 돈을 이자 놓는 데 쓰지 않는다고 했잖아요.\"\n\n\"죄송합니다, 부인.\" 드브레가 차갑게 말했다. \"부인의 허락을 받았기에 그렇게 한 겁니다. 그러니 부인 몫으로 4만 프랑이 있고, 처음에 대주신 10만 프랑을 더하면, 부인 몫은 전부 134만 프랑이 됩니다. 그런데 부인, 저는 그저께 부인의 돈을 미리 인출해 두는 신중함을 발휘했습니다. 보시다시피 그리 오래된 일이 아니지요. 언제 회계를 넘기라는 요청을 받을지 모른다는 생각을 줄곧 하고 있었거든요. 여기 부인의 돈이 있습니다. 절반은 지폐이고, 나머지 절반은 소지인 지급 수표입니다. '여기'라고 말씀드리는 까닭은, 제 집이 충분히 안전하다고도, 변호사들이 충분히 입이 무겁다고도 여기지 않았고, 부동산은 증거를 남기는 데다, 더구나 부인은 남편과 별개로 무언가를 소유할 권리가 없기에, 이제 부인의 전 재산이 된 이 금액을 저 벽장 밑에 감춰 둔 궤짝에 넣어 두었기 때문입니다. 더 안전하게 하려고 제 손으로 직접 거기 숨겨 두었지요.\n\n자, 부인,\" 드브레가 먼저 벽장을 열고, 이어 궤짝을 열며 말을 이었다. \"자, 부인, 여기 1,000프랑짜리 지폐 800장이 있습니다. 보시다시피 쇠로 제본한 커다란 책 같지요. 여기에 2만 5천 프랑짜리 공채 증서를 더하고, 나머지 잔돈, 제 생각엔 11만 프랑쯤 될 텐데, 그건 제 거래 은행 앞으로 끊은 수표가 있습니다. 당글라르 씨가 아니니, 그 은행은 틀림없이 부인께 금액을 지급할 겁니다, 안심하셔도 됩니다.\"\n\n당글라르 부인은 기계적으로 수표와 채권 증서, 그리고 지폐 다발을 받아 들었다. 이 엄청난 재산은 탁자 위에서 그리 대단해 보이지 않았다. 당글라르 부인은 눈물 없는 눈으로, 그러나 감춘 격정으로 가슴을 들썩이며, 지폐를 가방에 넣고 증서와 수표를 수첩에 끼워 넣었다. 그러고는 창백하게 입을 다문 채 서서, 따뜻한 위로의 말 한마디를 기다렸다.\n\n그러나 헛된 기다림이었다.\n\n\"자, 부인,\" 드브레가 말했다. \"부인은 이제 훌륭한 재산을, 연간 6만 리브르가량의 수입을 가지셨습니다. 이곳에서 적어도 일 년은 살림을 꾸릴 수 없는 여인에게 이건 막대한 액수지요. 마음 가는 대로 무엇이든 누리실 수 있을 겁니다. 게다가 혹시라도 수입이 모자라거든, 지난날을 봐서라도, 부인, 제 것을 쓰셔도 됩니다. 제가 가진 전부를 빌려 드릴 준비가 되어 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"고마워요, 고마워요.\" 남작 부인이 대답했다. \"당신이 방금 지불해 주신 것만 해도, 적어도 한동안은 세상에서 물러나 지내려는 가련한 여자에게 필요한 것보다 훨씬 많다는 걸 잊으셨군요.\"\n\n드브레는 잠시 놀랐지만, 곧 마음을 추스르고는 \"원하시는 대로 하시지요, 부인.\"이라고 말하는 듯한 태도로 고개를 숙였다.\n\n당글라르 부인은 그때까지도 어쩌면 무언가를 바랐을지 모른다. 그러나 드브레의 무심한 인사와, 그에 곁들여진 눈길, 그리고 그 의미심장한 침묵을 보자, 그녀는 고개를 들었다. 그러고는 격정도 격렬함도, 망설임조차 없이, 그렇게 자기를 떠날 수 있는 사내에게 마지막 작별 인사를 건네는 것조차 멸시하며 계단을 뛰어 내려갔다.\n\n\"흥,\" 그녀가 떠나자 드브레가 말했다. \"참으로 그럴듯한 계획이군! 집에 틀어박혀 소설이나 읽고, 더는 증권 거래소에서 투기를 못 할 테니 카드로 투기나 하겠지.\"\n\n그러고는 회계 장부를 집어 들어, 방금 지불한 금액의 모든 기재 사항을 더없이 세심하게 지워 나갔다.\n\n\"내게는 106만 프랑이 남았군.\" 그가 말했다. \"빌포르 양이 죽다니 안타까운 일이야. 모든 면에서 내게 딱 맞는 여자였는데, 결혼했을 텐데 말이지.\"\n\n그리고 그는 당글라르 부인이 떠난 뒤 이십 분이 지나기를 차분히 기다린 다음에야 집을 나섰다. 그동안 그는 곁에 시계를 놓아두고 숫자 계산에 몰두했다.\n\n아스모데우스—그 악마 같은 인물, 만일 르사주가 그 위대한 걸작에서 먼저 선수를 치지 않았더라면 풍부한 상상력을 지닌 누구라도 만들어 냈을 그 인물—라면, 드브레가 숫자를 계산하는 동안 생제르맹데프레 거리의 그 작은 집 지붕을 들어 올렸다면 진귀한 구경거리를 누렸으리라.\n\n드브레가 당글라르 부인과 250만 프랑을 나누던 그 방 위층에는 또 다른 방이 있었고, 그곳에는 우리가 이야기해 온 사건들에서 너무도 두드러진 역할을 맡았던 사람들이 살고 있었으니, 그들의 등장은 얼마간 관심을 불러일으키지 않을 수 없다.\n\n메르세데스와 알베르가 그 방에 있었다.\n\n메르세데스는 지난 며칠 사이 크게 변해 있었다. 부유하던 시절에도 그녀가, 수수하고 소박한 차림으로 나타난 여인을 더는 알아볼 수 없게 만드는 그런 화려한 치장을 한 적은 결코 없었다. 그렇다고 비참함의 옷차림을 감출 수 없는 그런 의기소침한 상태에 빠진 것도 아니었다. 아니, 메르세데스의 변화는 그녀의 눈이 더 이상 반짝이지 않고, 입술이 더 이상 미소 짓지 않으며, 예전엔 그토록 거침없는 재치에서 술술 흘러나오던 말이 이제는 머뭇거리며 나온다는 데 있었다.\n\n그녀의 기개를 꺾은 것은 가난이 아니었다. 가난을 견디기 힘겹게 만든 것도 용기가 모자라서가 아니었다. 메르세데스는 자신이 누리던 높은 자리에서 끌어내려졌고, 스스로 택한 이 영역에서 길을 잃은 채—마치 휘황하게 밝혀진 방에서 완전한 어둠 속으로 옮겨 간 사람처럼—궁전에서 오두막으로 떨어진 여왕 같았다. 빠듯한 살림에 내몰려, 손수 식탁에 올려야 하는 질그릇에도, 잠자리가 되어 버린 초라한 짚요에도 도무지 마음을 붙이지 못하는 그런 여왕 말이다.\n\n아름답던 카탈루냐 처녀이자 고귀한 백작 부인은 그 도도한 눈빛도, 매혹적인 미소도 잃어버렸으니, 주위를 둘러봐도 비참함밖에 보이지 않았기 때문이다. 벽에는 인색한 집주인들이 때가 잘 타지 않는다고 골라 바르는 회색 벽지 가운데 하나가 발려 있었다. 바닥에는 양탄자도 깔려 있지 않았다. 가구는 호사를 흉내 낸 그 가련한 시도로 눈길을 끌었다. 한마디로, 우아하고 세련된 것에 길든 눈에는 무엇 하나 거슬리지 않는 것이 없었다.\n\n모르세르 부인은 집을 떠난 뒤로 그곳에서 지내 왔다. 그곳의 끊임없는 적막이 그녀를 짓눌렀다. 그래도 알베르가 자기 심정을 가늠하려 끊임없이 안색을 살핀다는 것을 알았기에, 그녀는 억지로 입술만 단조롭게 웃어 보였다. 평소 그녀의 눈에서 빛나던 다정하고 환한 표정과 대조되는 그 미소는 마치 \"조각상 위에 비친 달빛\" 같았다—온기는 없이 빛만 내어 주는.\n\n알베르 역시 마음이 편치 않았다. 호사의 흔적이 그를 실제 처지로 가라앉지 못하게 했다. 장갑 없이 나가려 하면 손이 너무 희어 보였고, 거리를 걸으려 하면 장화가 너무 번들거려 보였다. 그러나 모자의 정과 자식의 정이라는 끊을 수 없는 끈으로 맺어진 이 고귀하고 영민한 두 사람은, 말없이도 서로를 이해하고 살림을 아끼는 데 성공했다. 알베르는 어머니의 안색 하나 흐트러뜨리지 않고 이렇게 말할 수 있었다.\n\n\"어머니, 우리에겐 더 이상 돈이 없어요.\"\n\n메르세데스는 한 번도 비참을 겪어 본 적이 없었다. 젊은 시절 가난을 입에 올린 적은 많았지만, 결핍과 궁핍이라는, 서로 같은 뜻인 이 두 말 사이에는 커다란 차이가 있었다.\n\n카탈루냐 마을에서 메르세데스는 온갖 것을 바랐지만, 그래도 정말로 무언가가 모자랐던 적은 결코 없었다. 그물만 성하면 물고기가 잡혔고, 그 물고기를 팔기만 하면 새 그물을 짤 노끈을 살 수 있었다. 게다가 우정에서도 차단된 채, 일상의 일과는 도무지 섞일 수 없는 단 하나의 애정만을 품고서, 그녀는 자기 자신만을—오직 자기만을 생각했다. 그 적은 벌이로도 그녀는 형편껏 그런대로 잘 지냈다. 그런데 이제는 부양할 사람이 둘인데, 살아갈 거리는 아무것도 없었다.\n\n겨울이 다가오고 있었다. 메르세데스는 그 춥고 헐벗은 방에 불 한 점 피우지 못했다—현관에서 안방까지 집 안을 데우는 난로에 익숙했던 그녀가. 작은 꽃 한 송이조차 없었다—그녀의 거처가 값비싼 이국 화초로 가득한 온실 같았던 그녀가. 그러나 그녀에게는 아들이 있었다. 지금껏 의무를 다한다는 흥분이 두 사람을 지탱해 주었다. 흥분은 열정과 마찬가지로 이따금 우리를 세상일에 무감각하게 만든다. 그러나 그 흥분이 가라앉자, 두 사람은 꿈에서 현실로 내려와야만 한다는 것을 느꼈다. 이상을 다 소진하고 나니, 이제 실제를 이야기해야 한다는 것을 깨달았다.\n\n\"어머니,\" 마침 당글라르 부인이 계단을 내려가던 바로 그때, 알베르가 외쳤다. \"괜찮으시다면 우리 재산을 따져 봐요. 제 계획을 세울 밑천이 필요하거든요.\"\n\n\"밑천이라니, 아무것도 없잖니!\" 메르세데스가 서글픈 미소를 띠며 대답했다.\n\n\"아니에요, 어머니. 밑천이 3,000프랑 있어요. 그리고 이 3,000프랑으로 우리가 즐거운 삶을 꾸려 갈 묘안이 떠올랐어요.\"\n\n\"얘야!\" 메르세데스가 한숨지었다.\n\n\"아아, 어머니,\" 청년이 말했다. \"불행히도 제가 어머니 돈을 너무 많이 써 본 탓에 그 값어치를 모르지 않아요. 이 3,000프랑은 막대한 돈이에요. 저는 이 토대 위에 앞날을 위한 기적 같은 확실함을 세울 작정이고요.\"\n\n\"네 말이 그렇긴 한데, 정말로 이 3,000프랑을 받아도 된다고 생각하니?\" 메르세데스가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼요.\" 알베르가 단호한 어조로 대답했다. \"더더욱 기꺼이 받아야지요. 그 돈이 지금 여기 있는 것도 아니니까요. 아시다시피 그건 마르세유 메이앙 가로수길의 그 작은 집 정원에 묻혀 있잖아요. 200프랑이면 마르세유까지 갈 수 있어요.\"\n\n\"200프랑으로? 정말이니, 알베르?\"\n\n\"오, 그 점이라면 역마차와 증기선을 다 알아봤고, 계산도 다 해 뒀어요. 어머니는 샬롱까지 쿠페석에 자리를 잡으세요. 보세요, 어머니, 35프랑이면 어머니를 융숭하게 모시는 거예요.\"\n\n그러고서 알베르는 펜을 들어 이렇게 적었다.\n\n쿠페석, 35프랑............ 35. 샬롱에서 리옹까지는 증기선으로... 6. 리옹에서 아비뇽까지 (역시 증기선으로)......... 16. 아비뇽에서 마르세유까지, 7프랑....... 7. 도중 경비, 약 50프랑....... 50. 합계............ 114프랑.\n\n\"120으로 적어 두죠.\" 알베르가 미소 지으며 덧붙였다. \"보세요, 제가 후하죠, 안 그래요, 어머니?\"\n\n\"그런데 너는, 가엾은 내 아들아?\"\n\n\"저요? 제 몫으로 80프랑을 떼어 두는 게 안 보이세요? 젊은 사내한텐 호사가 필요 없어요. 게다가 여행이 어떤 건지 저도 잘 알고요.\"\n\n\"역마차와 하인을 거느리고 다니던 네가?\"\n\n\"어떻게든 해 가요, 어머니.\"\n\n\"그래, 좋아. 그런데 그 200프랑은?\"\n\n\"여기 있어요. 게다가 200프랑이 더 있고요. 보세요, 제 시계를 100프랑에 팔았고, 줄과 인장은 300프랑에 팔았어요. 장식이 시계보다 값이 더 나갔으니 얼마나 다행이에요. 여전히 사치품 이야기로군요! 자, 이제 우리는 부자가 된 셈이에요. 여행에 필요한 114프랑 대신 250프랑을 손에 쥐고 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"하지만 이 집에 진 빚이 좀 있잖니?\"\n\n\"30프랑이요. 하지만 그건 제 150프랑에서 갚을게요—그렇게 하기로 한 거고요—그리고 제 여행엔 80프랑밖에 안 드니, 보세요, 저는 호사에 파묻혀 있는 셈이에요. 그런데 그게 다가 아니에요. 이건 어떻게 생각하세요, 어머니?\"\n\n그러고서 알베르는 금장식 걸쇠가 달린 작은 수첩—옛 사치 취향의 잔재이거나, 어쩌면 그의 작은 문을 두드리곤 하던 그 베일 쓴 수수께끼의 부인들 가운데 누군가가 준 애틋한 추억의 물건—에서 1,000프랑짜리 지폐 한 장을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"이게 뭐니?\" 메르세데스가 물었다.\n\n\"1,000프랑이요.\"\n\n\"그런데 그걸 어디서 났니?\"\n\n\"제 말을 들어 보세요, 어머니. 그리고 너무 동요하지 마세요.\" 그러고서 알베르는 일어나 어머니의 두 뺨에 입을 맞춘 뒤, 가만히 그녀를 바라보았다. \"어머니, 제가 어머니를 얼마나 아름답다고 여기는지 모르실 거예요!\" 청년이 자식으로서의 깊은 사랑에 사무쳐 말했다. \"어머니는 정말이지 제가 본 가장 아름답고 가장 고귀한 여인이에요!\"\n\n\"가엾은 내 아들아!\" 메르세데스가 눈가에 반짝이는 눈물을 애써 참으며 말했다. \"정말이지, 너는 불행을 겪고 나서야 너를 향한 내 사랑이 경탄으로 바뀌게 만드는구나. 내 아들이 있는 한 나는 불행하지 않아!\"\n\n\"아, 바로 그거예요.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"이제부터가 시련이에요. 우리가 어떤 결정을 내렸는지 아세요, 어머니?\"\n\n\"우리가 무슨 결정을 내렸던가?\"\n\n\"네. 어머니는 마르세유에서 사시고, 저는 아프리카로 떠나기로 했어요. 거기서 제가 내던진 이름 대신 지금 지닌 이름을 쓸 권리를 스스로 벌어 올 거예요.\" 메르세데스가 한숨지었다. \"그런데 어머니, 저는 어제 스파이 부대에 대리 입대했어요.\" 청년이 어떤 부끄러움에 눈을 내리깔며 덧붙였다. 사실 그조차 자신의 그 자기 비하가 얼마나 숭고한 것인지 깨닫지 못하고 있었다. \"제 몸은 제 것이니 팔 수도 있다고 생각했어요. 어제 다른 사람 대신 입대했고요. 제가 생각한 값어치보다 더 비싸게 저를 팔았지요.\" 그가 미소를 지어 보이려 애쓰며 덧붙였다. \"2,000프랑을 받았어요.\"\n\n\"그럼 이 1,000프랑은……\" 메르세데스가 몸서리치며 말했다.\n\n\"그 금액의 절반이에요, 어머니. 나머지는 일 년 뒤에 받고요.\"\n\n메르세데스는 이루 형언할 수 없는 표정으로 하늘을 향해 눈을 들었고, 지금껏 참아 온 눈물이 마침내 격정에 무너져 두 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸다.\n\n\"제 피를 판 값이로구나!\" 그녀가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"네, 제가 죽는다면요.\" 알베르가 웃으며 말했다. \"하지만 어머니, 단언컨대 저는 제 한 몸을 지키겠다는 굳은 각오가 있어요. 그리고 지금만큼 살고 싶은 마음이 간절했던 적이 없어요.\"\n\n\"자비로우신 하늘이여!\"\n\n\"게다가 어머니, 어째서 제가 죽는다고 단정하세요? 라모리시에르, 저 남부의 네라 불리는 그 사람이 죽었던가요? 샹가르니에가 죽었나요? 베도가 죽었나요? 우리가 아는 모렐이 죽었나요? 제가 수놓은 군복을 입고 돌아오는 걸 보실 때 어머니가 누리실 기쁨을 떠올려 보세요! 정말이지, 그 군복을 입으면 제 모습이 근사할 거라 기대하고 있어요. 순전히 허영심에서 그 연대를 고른 거라니까요.\"\n\n메르세데스는 미소 지으려 애쓰며 한숨을 내쉬었다. 헌신적인 어머니는 그 희생의 무게를 온통 아들에게만 지워서는 안 된다고 느꼈다.\n\n\"자, 이제 아시겠죠, 어머니!\" 알베르가 말을 이었다. \"여기 4,000프랑 넘는 돈이 어머니 앞으로 마련돼 있어요. 이걸로 적어도 이 년은 사실 수 있고요.\"\n\n\"정말 그럴까?\" 메르세데스가 말했다.\n\n이 말이 어찌나 서글픈 어조로 나왔던지, 그 참뜻이 알베르를 비껴가지 못했다. 그는 가슴이 두근거리는 것을 느꼈고, 어머니의 손을 자기 두 손으로 감싸 쥐며 다정하게 말했다.\n\n\"네, 어머니는 사실 거예요!\"\n\n\"내가 살 거라고? 그럼 너는 나를 떠나지 않을 거지, 알베르?\"\n\n\"어머니, 저는 가야 해요.\" 알베르가 단호하고 차분한 목소리로 말했다. \"어머니는 저를 너무도 사랑하시니, 제가 쓸모없이 빈둥거리며 어머니 곁에 남기를 바라지 않으실 거예요. 게다가 저는 이미 서명까지 했는걸요.\"\n\n\"네 뜻과 하늘의 뜻을 따르겠다는 거구나!\"\n\n\"제 뜻이 아니라, 어머니, 이성이에요—필요고요. 우리는 절망에 빠진 두 사람 아닌가요? 어머니에게 삶이 무엇인가요? 아무것도 아니죠. 저에게 삶이 무엇인가요? 어머니가 안 계시면 별것 아니에요. 정말이지, 어머니가 아니었다면 저는 아버지를 의심하고 그 이름을 버린 그날 살기를 그만뒀을 거예요. 좋아요, 저는 살겠어요. 어머니가 여전히 희망을 품겠다고 약속하신다면요. 그리고 어머니의 앞날을 돌보는 일을 제게 맡겨 주신다면, 어머니는 제 힘을 갑절로 키워 주시는 거예요. 그러면 저는 알제리 총독을 찾아가겠어요. 그분은 왕 같은 심성을 지닌, 무엇보다 진정한 군인이에요. 그분께 제 어두운 사연을 털어놓고, 이따금 저를 살펴봐 달라고 청하겠어요. 그분이 약속을 지켜 저를 돌봐 주신다면, 여섯 달 안에 저는 장교가 되거나 죽어 있겠죠. 제가 장교가 되면 어머니의 살림은 확실해져요. 두 사람 몫의 돈이 충분할 테고, 게다가 우리 둘 다 자랑스러워할 이름까지 생길 테니까요. 우리 본래의 이름일 테니 말이에요. 제가 죽으면—그럼 어머니, 어머니도 죽으셔도 돼요. 그러면 우리 불행도 끝이 나겠죠.\"\n\n\"좋아.\" 메르세데스가 그 웅변하는 듯한 눈빛으로 대답했다. \"네 말이 옳다, 내 사랑. 우리 행동을 지켜보는 자들에게 우리가 동정받을 만한 사람들임을 증명해 보이자.\"\n\n\"하지만 음울한 걱정에 굴복하진 말아요.\" 청년이 말했다. \"단언컨대 우리는 행복해요. 아니, 행복해질 거예요. 어머니는 기개와 체념을 함께 지닌 분이고, 저는 취향이 소박해졌으며, 바라건대 격정도 없어졌어요. 일단 군에 들어가면 저는 부자가 될 거고, 일단 당테스 씨 댁에 들어가면 어머니는 편안히 지내실 거예요. 우리 애써 봐요, 부탁이에요—애써서 즐겁게 지내 봐요.\"\n\n\"그래, 애써 보자. 너는 살아야 하고, 행복해야 하니까, 알베르.\"\n\n\"그럼 이제 우리 몫 나누기는 끝났네요, 어머니.\" 청년이 마음 편한 척하며 말했다. \"이제 헤어질 수 있겠어요. 자, 제가 어머니 차표를 끊을게요.\"\n\n\"그럼 너는, 내 아들아?\"\n\n\"저는 며칠 더 여기 머물 거예요. 우리도 이별에 익숙해져야죠. 추천서도 받아야 하고 아프리카에 관한 정보도 좀 얻어야 하고요. 마르세유에서 다시 어머니와 합류할게요.\"\n\n\"그래, 좋아—헤어지자.\" 메르세데스가 챙겨 온 단 하나의 숄을 어깨에 둘렀다. 우연찮게도 그것은 값나가는 검은 캐시미어 숄이었다. 알베르는 서둘러 서류를 챙기고, 집주인에게 진 빚 30프랑을 갚으려 종을 울린 뒤, 어머니에게 팔을 내밀어 두 사람은 계단을 내려갔다.\n\n누군가가 그들 앞에서 계단을 내려가고 있었는데, 그 사람은 비단 드레스가 스치는 소리를 듣고 뒤를 돌아보았다. \"드브레!\" 알베르가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"자네, 모르세르 아닌가?\" 비서가 계단에 멈춰 서며 대답했다. 호기심이 익명을 지키려는 욕구를 이긴 탓에, 그는 정체를 들키고 말았다. 정말이지 이 낯선 곳에서, 그 불행이 파리를 그토록 떠들썩하게 만든 청년을 만나다니 묘한 일이었다.\n\n\"모르세르!\" 드브레가 되뇌었다. 그러다 어슴푸레한 빛 속에서 여전히 젊고 베일을 쓴 모르세르 부인의 모습을 알아채고는 이렇게 덧붙였다.\n\n\"실례하네.\" 그가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"이만 가 보겠네, 알베르.\" 알베르는 그의 속내를 알아차렸다.\n\n\"어머니,\" 그가 메르세데스를 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"이분은 드브레 씨예요. 내무대신 비서이고, 한때 제 친구였지요.\"\n\n\"한때라니?\" 드브레가 말을 더듬었다. \"그게 무슨 뜻인가?\"\n\n\"그렇게 말한 겁니다, 드브레 씨. 이제 제겐 친구가 없고, 또 있어서도 안 되니까요. 저를 알아봐 주셔서 감사합니다, 선생님.\" 드브레가 한 걸음 앞으로 나서더니, 상대의 손을 다정하게 부여잡았다.\n\n\"믿어 주게, 알베르.\" 그가 자신이 느낄 수 있는 온갖 감정을 담아 말했다. \"믿어 주게, 자네의 불행에 나는 깊이 가슴 아프고, 무슨 식으로든 자네에게 도움이 될 수 있다면 나는 자네 편일세.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 선생님.\" 알베르가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"이런 불행의 한가운데서도, 우리는 아직 누구의 도움도 필요 없을 만큼은 넉넉합니다. 우리는 파리를 떠납니다. 여비를 치르고 나면 5,000프랑이 남을 거고요.\"\n\n수첩 속에 100만 프랑을 지닌 드브레의 관자놀이로 피가 솟구쳤다. 상상력이라곤 없는 그조차도, 같은 집에 두 여인이 있었다는 사실을 떠올리지 않을 수 없었다. 한 여인은 당연히도 명예를 잃은 채, 망토 밑에 150만 프랑을 감추고 가난한 척 떠났고, 다른 여인은 부당하게 짓밟혔으나 그 불행 속에서도 숭고하게, 몇 푼 안 되는 동전만으로도 여전히 부유했다. 이 대비가 그의 여느 때의 정중함을 흐트러뜨렸다. 자기 눈앞에 펼쳐진 그 철학이 그를 질리게 했고, 그는 그저 그런 인사치레의 말 몇 마디를 중얼거리고는 계단을 뛰어 내려갔다.\n\n그날 대신의 서기들과 부하들은 그의 언짢은 심기를 적잖이 감당해야 했다. 그러나 바로 그날 밤, 그는 마들렌 대로에 자리한 멋진 저택과 연간 5만 리브르의 수입을 거머쥔 몸이 되어 있었다.\n\n이튿날, 드브레가 그 증서에 서명하고 있던 바로 그때, 그러니까 오후 다섯 시쯤, 모르세르 부인은 아들을 다정하게 끌어안은 뒤 역마차 쿠페석에 올랐고, 문이 그녀를 가두며 닫혔다.\n\n라피트 은행 건물 안, 책상마다 위에 나 있는 작은 아치형 창문 가운데 하나 뒤에 한 사내가 숨어 있었다. 그는 메르세데스가 역마차에 오르는 것을 보았고, 알베르가 물러가는 것도 보았다. 그러고는 의혹으로 그늘진 이마를 손으로 쓸었다.\n\n\"아아,\" 그가 외쳤다. \"이 가엾고 죄 없는 사람들에게서 내가 앗아 간 행복을 어떻게 되돌려 줄 수 있단 말인가? 하느님, 저를 도우소서!\"\n\n# Chapter 107. The Lions’ Den\n\n* 스파이: 프랑스가 알제리에 두었던 원주민 기병 부대.—옮긴이\n라포르스 감옥에서 가장 위험하고 흉포한 죄수들을 가두는 한 구역은 생베르나르 안뜰이라 불린다. 죄수들은 자기들 특유의 생생한 말로 이곳을 \"사자 우리\"라 이름 붙였는데, 아마도 갇힌 자들의 이빨이 곧잘 창살을 갉아 대고 때로는 간수까지 물어뜯기 때문일 것이다. 이곳은 감옥 안의 감옥이었다. 벽은 다른 곳보다 두 배나 두꺼웠다. 창살은 날마다 간수들이 꼼꼼히 살폈는데, 그들의 헤라클레스 같은 체격과 차갑고 무자비한 표정은 그들이 남다른 활동력과 영민함 덕분에 그 신민들 위에 군림하도록 뽑힌 자들임을 입증했다.\n\n이 구역의 안뜰은 거대한 벽에 둘러싸여 있었고, 도덕적이고 육체적인 추악함이 들끓는 이 심연에 햇빛이 기어이 비집고 들어올 때면 그 벽 위로 비스듬히 빛이 스쳐 갔다. 이 돌바닥 안뜰에서는 아침부터 밤까지 이리저리 서성이는 사람들을 볼 수 있었다. 창백하고 근심에 찌든 초췌한 얼굴로, 마치 그림자처럼 떠도는 그들은 정의가 갈고 있는 칼날 아래 붙들린 자들이었다. 그곳에서 그들은 가장 많은 열기를 끌어모아 간직하는 벽 한쪽에 웅크린 채, 때로는 서로 이야기를 나누는 모습으로, 그러나 더 흔히는 홀로 문을 지켜보는 모습으로 보였다. 그 문은 이따금 열려 이 음울한 무리에서 한 사람을 불러내거나, 사회에서 내쳐진 또 다른 자를 던져 넣곤 했다.\n\n생베르나르 안뜰에는 면회객을 맞이하는 전용 공간이 따로 있었다. 길쭉한 직사각형 방으로, 방문객이 죄수와 악수를 하거나 무언가를 건네지 못하도록 90센티미터쯤 간격을 두고 두 개의 곧추선 창살로 나뉘어 있었다. 음습하고 눅눅한, 아니 끔찍하기까지 한 곳이었다. 그 쇠창살 사이로 오갔던 가슴 미어지는 면담들을 떠올리면 더욱 그러했다. 그러나 이토록 끔찍한 곳임에도, 살날이 얼마 남지 않은 자들에게는 일종의 낙원처럼 여겨졌다. 그들이 사자 우리를 떠나는 곳이라곤 생자크 처형장이나 갤리선, 아니면 독방뿐이었으니, 그 밖의 어디로든 나가는 일은 그토록 드물었다.\n\n우리가 묘사하려 애쓴 그 안뜰, 눅눅한 김이 피어오르는 그곳에서 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣은 한 젊은이가 거니는 모습이 보였다. 그는 \"우리\"의 거주자들 사이에서 적잖은 호기심을 불러일으킨 자였다. 그의 옷은 누더기로 찢기지만 않았더라면 그를 우아한 신사로 통하게 했을 만한 재단이었다. 그래도 옷은 닳은 기색이 없었고, 죄수의 정성스러운 손길 아래 고급 천은 아직 멀쩡한 부분부터 곧 윤기를 되찾았다. 입은 이가 그것을 새 옷처럼 보이게 하려고 안간힘을 썼기 때문이다. 그는 감옥에 들어온 뒤로 색이 꽤 변해 버린 셔츠의 고운 삼베 가슴팍에도 똑같은 정성을 쏟았고, 작은 관(冠) 무늬 아래 머리글자가 수놓인 손수건 모서리로 칠을 한 가죽 부츠를 문질러 닦았다.\n\n\"사자 우리\"의 수감자들 가운데 몇몇이 그 죄수의 몸단장을 자못 흥미롭게 지켜보고 있었다.\n\n\"저것 봐, 왕자님께서 깃털을 다듬고 계시네.\" 도둑들 중 하나가 말했다.\n\n\"잘생긴 친구로군.\" 또 다른 자가 말했다. \"빗하고 머릿기름만 있으면 흰 새끼염소 가죽 장갑 낀 신사들도 무색하게 만들겠어.\"\n\n\"저 옷은 거의 새것 같고, 부츠는 번쩍번쩍하네. 저렇게 잘 차려입은 형제를 두니 흐뭇하군. 그런데 그 헌병들 행실 한번 고약하지. 저런 옷을 다 찢어 놓다니, 어찌나 시샘이 많은지!\"\n\n\"거물처럼 보이는데.\" 또 한 사람이 말했다. \"차림새가 으리으리하잖아. 게다가 이 어린 나이에 여기 들어오다니! 야, 거참 신나는 일이군!\"\n\n그러는 사이 이 끔찍한 찬탄의 대상은 간수 하나가 기대어 선 쪽문으로 다가갔다.\n\n\"여보시오,\" 그가 말했다. \"20프랑만 빌려 주시오. 곧 갚을 테니까. 나한테는 떼일 염려가 없소. 잘 들으시오, 내겐 당신이 가진 드니에르●보다 더 많은 백만 프랑을 가진 친척들이 있단 말이오. 자, 제발 부탁이오, 20프랑만 빌려 주시오. 실내복 한 벌 사게 말이오. 늘 외투에 부츠 차림으로 있으려니 견딜 수가 없소! 그것도 이런 외투를, 그것도 카발칸티 가문의 왕자가 말이오!\"\n● 드니에르: 옛 프랑스의 소액 화폐 단위.—옮긴이\n\n간수는 등을 돌리고 어깨를 으쓱했다. 다른 사람이라면 웃음을 터뜨렸을 법한 말에도 그는 웃지조차 않았다. 똑같은 말을 지껄이는 자를 하도 많이 들어 온 탓이었다. 아니, 실은 그런 말밖에 듣지 못했던 것이다.\n\n\"이보시오,\" 안드레아가 말했다. \"당신은 동정심이라곤 없는 사람이군. 내 당신을 쫓겨나게 만들겠소.\"\n\n이 말에 간수가 돌아서더니 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그러자 죄수들이 다가와 둥글게 둘러섰다.\n\n\"내 말해 두는데, 그 보잘것없는 돈만 있으면,\" 안드레아가 말을 이었다. \"외투 한 벌하고, 내가 날마다 기다리는 그 고귀한 손님을 맞이할 방 한 칸은 마련할 수 있단 말이오.\"\n\n\"그렇고말고, 그렇고말고.\" 죄수들이 말했다. \"딱 봐도 신사 양반이신데!\"\n\n\"좋아, 그럼 20프랑 빌려 줘.\" 간수가 반대쪽 어깨로 몸을 기대며 말했다. \"설마 동료를 거절하진 않겠지!\"\n\n\"난 이런 자들의 동료가 아니오.\" 젊은이가 거만하게 말했다. \"당신은 나를 그렇게 모욕할 권리가 없소.\"\n\n도둑들은 낮게 웅성거리며 서로를 쳐다보았고, 그 귀족 행세하는 죄수의 머리 위로 폭풍이 모여들었다. 그것은 그의 말 때문이라기보다 간수의 태도 때문에 일어난 것이었다. 간수는 파도가 너무 거세지면 폭풍을 가라앉힐 수 있다고 자신했기에, 성가신 안드레아에게 앙갚음을 하려고 일정한 수위까지 파도가 솟구치도록 내버려 두었다. 게다가 그것은 긴 하루 동안 그에게 얼마간의 소일거리가 되어 줄 터였다.\n\n도둑들은 어느새 안드레아에게 다가들었고, 더러는 \"라 사바트! 라 사바트!\"●라고 소리쳤다. 이는 잔혹한 짓으로, 미운털이 박힌 동료를 헌 신발이 아니라 쇠굽을 댄 신발로 후려치는 것이었다. 또 더러는 \"랑기유\"●●라는 또 다른 놀음을 제안했는데, 손수건에 모래와 자갈, 그리고 가진 자라면 2수짜리 동전까지 채워 넣고는, 그 비참한 희생자의 머리와 어깨를 도리깨질하듯 두들겨 대는 것이었다.\n● 라 사바트: '헌 신발'이라는 뜻.—옮긴이 ●● 랑기유: '뱀장어'라는 뜻.—옮긴이\n\n\"저 멋쟁이 양반을 채찍으로 갈겨 주자!\" 또 다른 자들이 말했다.\n\n그러나 안드레아는 그들을 향해 돌아서서 눈을 찡긋하고, 혀를 볼 안쪽으로 굴리고, 입맛을 다셨다. 그것은 강요된 침묵 속에서 도둑들끼리 백 마디 말에 맞먹는 표시였다. 카드루스가 그에게 가르쳐 준 비밀 결사의 신호였던 것이다. 그는 곧바로 같은 패거리의 일원으로 인정받았다. 손수건은 내던져졌고, 쇠굽 신발은 그 임자의 발로 되돌아갔다.\n\n저 양반 말이 옳다는 소리가, 그가 그 나름의 방식으로 예의를 차리려 한 것이니 양심의 자유를 존중하는 본보기를 보여 주자는 소리가 여기저기서 들렸다. 그러고는 무리가 물러갔다. 간수는 이 광경에 어찌나 어안이 벙벙했던지, 안드레아의 두 손을 붙잡고 그의 몸을 뒤지기 시작했다. 사자 우리의 수감자들이 그토록 갑작스레 굴복한 것을 단순한 매력 이상의 무언가, 좀 더 실질적인 무언가 때문이라 여긴 것이다.\n\n안드레아는 항의는 하면서도 저항하지는 않았다. 그때 갑자기 쪽문에서 목소리가 들려왔다.\n\n\"베네데토!\" 한 감독관이 외쳤다. 간수가 붙잡은 손을 풀었다.\n\n\"날 부르는군.\" 안드레아가 말했다.\n\n\"면회실로!\" 그 목소리가 말했다.\n\n\"보시오, 누가 날 면회 왔잖소. 아, 이보시오, 카발칸티를 여느 평범한 자처럼 다뤄도 되는지 어디 두고 보시지!\"\n\n그러고는 안드레아가 시커먼 그림자처럼 안뜰을 미끄러져, 동료들은 물론 간수까지 어리둥절하게 내버려 둔 채 쪽문 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔다. 면회실로 부른다는 것은 사실 다른 누구 못지않게 안드레아 자신도 적잖이 놀라게 한 일이었다. 그 교활한 젊은이는 라포르스에 들어올 때 면회를 청구받을 때까지 기다릴 수 있는 권리를 행사하기는커녕, 철저히 침묵을 지켜 왔기 때문이다.\n\n'모든 것이,' 그는 생각했다. '내가 어떤 막강한 인물의 비호를 받고 있음을 증명하지. 이 갑작스러운 행운, 모든 장애물을 손쉽게 넘어선 것, 난데없는 가문과 내게 주어진 빛나는 이름, 머리 위로 쏟아진 황금, 곧 맺어질 더없이 화려한 혼사들. 불행한 운명의 추락과 후견인의 부재가 분명 나를 떨어뜨렸지만, 영영 그런 것은 아니야. 잠시 거두어진 그 손은, 내가 막 심연으로 가라앉는다고 여길 바로 그 순간에 다시 뻗어 나와 나를 구해 줄 거야. 무엇 하러 경솔한 짓을 무릅쓰겠어? 그랬다간 후견인의 마음이 돌아설지도 모르지. 그분에게는 이 곤경에서 나를 빼낼 방법이 두 가지 있어. 하나는 뇌물로 손을 써서 감쪽같이 탈옥시키는 것이고, 다른 하나는 황금으로 내 재판관들을 매수하는 거지. 그분이 나를 완전히 저버렸다고 확신하기 전까지는, 아무 말도 아무 짓도 하지 않겠어. 그러고 나서야……'\n\n안드레아가 세운 계획은 제법 영리한 것이었다. 그 불운한 젊은이는 공격에는 대담했고 방어에는 거칠었다. 그는 공동 감옥도, 온갖 결핍도 견뎌 냈다. 그러나 차츰 본성이, 아니 차라리 습관이 고개를 들어, 그는 헐벗고 더럽고 굶주린 처지에 괴로워했다. 바로 이 곤궁한 순간에 감독관의 목소리가 그를 면회실로 불렀던 것이다. 안드레아는 기쁨에 가슴이 뛰는 것을 느꼈다. 예심 판사가 면회 오기에는 너무 일렀고, 감옥장이나 의사가 오기에는 너무 늦은 시각이었다. 그렇다면 그가 바라던 바로 그 손님임이 틀림없었다. 안드레아가 인도된 방의 창살 너머로, 그는 놀라움에 눈을 크게 뜨고 베르투치오 씨의 거무스름하고 영민한 얼굴을 보았다. 베르투치오 역시 쇠창살과 빗장 걸린 문, 그리고 맞은편 창살 뒤에서 움직이는 그림자를 슬픈 놀라움으로 바라보고 있었다.\n\n\"아.\" 안드레아가 깊이 동요하여 말했다.\n\n\"잘 있었느냐, 베네데토.\" 베르투치오가 깊고 공허한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"당신이…… 당신이오?\" 젊은이가 겁먹은 듯 주위를 둘러보며 말했다.\n\n\"날 못 알아보겠느냐, 가엾은 녀석아?\"\n\n\"쉿, 조용히 하시오!\" 안드레아가 말했다. 그는 벽이 지닌 예민한 청각을 알고 있었다. \"제발 부탁이니, 그렇게 큰 소리로 말하지 마시오!\"\n\n\"너 나하고 단둘이 이야기하고 싶은 게로구나, 안 그러냐?\" 베르투치오가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 그렇소.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 됐다.\"\n그러고는 베르투치오가 주머니를 뒤지더니, 쪽문 창 너머로 보이는 간수에게 손짓을 했다.\n\n\"읽어 보시오.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"그게 뭐요?\" 안드레아가 물었다.\n\n\"너를 어느 방으로 데려가, 나와 이야기하도록 거기 남겨 두라는 지시서다.\"\n\n\"오.\" 안드레아가 기뻐 펄쩍 뛰며 외쳤다. 그러고는 속으로 덧붙였다. '역시 내 정체 모를 후견인이로구나! 난 잊히지 않았어. 우리가 따로 떨어진 방에서 이야기를 나누게 됐으니, 저들은 은밀함을 원하는 거야. 알겠다, 후견인이 베르투치오를 보낸 거로군.'\n\n간수는 잠시 어느 직원과 이야기를 나누더니, 쇠문을 열고 안드레아를 이층 방으로 데려갔다. 방은 감옥의 관례대로 흰 회칠이 되어 있었지만, 죄수의 눈에는 자못 으리으리해 보였다. 비록 난로 하나, 침대 하나, 의자 하나, 탁자 하나가 그 호사스러운 세간의 전부였지만 말이다. 베르투치오는 의자에 앉았고, 안드레아는 침대에 몸을 던졌다. 간수가 물러갔다.\n\n\"자,\" 집사가 말했다. \"내게 할 말이 무엇이냐?\"\n\n\"그쪽은?\" 안드레아가 말했다.\n\n\"네가 먼저 말해라.\"\n\n\"오, 싫소. 날 찾아온 걸 보면 당신이야말로 내게 할 말이 많을 텐데.\"\n\n\"좋다, 그러지. 너는 악행을 계속해 왔구나. 도둑질을 하고, 사람을 죽였어.\"\n\n\"흥, 그게 다요! 고작 그 말을 하려고 날 따로 방까지 부른 거라면, 수고를 더실 뻔했소. 그런 건 나도 다 아는 일이니까. 하지만 도리어 내가 모르는 것들이 있단 말이오. 괜찮다면 그 이야기를 합시다. 누가 당신을 보냈소?\"\n\n\"이런, 이런, 성급하게 구는군, 베네데토 씨!\"\n\n\"그렇소, 단도직입적으로 갑시다. 쓸데없는 말은 집어치우고. 누가 당신을 보냈소?\"\n\n\"아무도 없다.\"\n\n\"내가 감옥에 있는 줄은 어떻게 알았소?\"\n\n\"얼마 전에 너를 알아봤지. 샹젤리제에서 그토록 우아하게 말에 올라타던 그 건방진 멋쟁이를 말이다.\"\n\n\"오, 샹젤리제? 아, 그렇지. 핀셋 놀이에서 하는 말마따나 '뜨거워지고 있군.' 샹젤리제라? 자, 그럼 내 아버지 이야기나 좀 합시다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 나는 누구냐?\"\n\n\"당신 말이오? 당신은 나의 양아버지지. 하지만 4, 5개월 만에 다 써 버린 10만 프랑을 내 손에 쥐여 준 건 당신이 아니었겠지. 내 아버지랍시고 이탈리아 신사 하나를 빚어낸 것도 당신이 아니었고. 나를 사교계에 들여보내, 오퇴유의 어느 만찬에 초대받게 한 것도 당신이 아니었소. 그 만찬을 나는 지금 이 순간에도 곱씹고 있는 것만 같소. 파리에서 가장 내로라하는 사람들과 한자리에서 말이오. 그중에는 어느 검사도 있었는데, 그자와 가깝게 지내지 못한 건 내 큰 실수였소. 지금 같아선 그자가 무척 쓸모 있었을 테니까. 결국 내 작은 비밀이 그 끔찍하게 들통났을 때, 1, 2백만 프랑으로 내 보석금을 대 준 것도 당신이 아니었소. 자, 말해 보시오, 훌륭하신 코르시카 양반, 어서 말해 보란 말이오!\"\n\n\"내가 무슨 말을 하기를 바라느냐?\"\n\n\"내 거들어 드리지. 조금 전에 당신이 샹젤리제 이야기를 했잖소, 훌륭하신 양아버님.\"\n\n\"그래서?\"\n\n\"그래서, 샹젤리제에는 아주 부유한 신사 한 분이 살고 계시지.\"\n\n\"그자의 집에서 네가 도둑질을 하고 사람을 죽였지, 안 그러냐?\"\n\n\"그런 것 같소.\"\n\n\"몬테크리스토 백작이냐?\"\n\n\"라신● 선생 말마따나, 그 이름을 댄 건 당신이오. 그럼 어디 내가 그분의 품으로 달려들어 가슴에 와락 끌어안고는, 픽세레쿠르●● 씨의 작품에서처럼 '아버지, 아버지!' 하고 외쳐야 한단 말이오?\"\n● 라신: 17세기 프랑스의 비극 작가.—옮긴이 ●● 픽세레쿠르: 통속극으로 유명했던 19세기 프랑스 극작가.—옮긴이\n\n\"농담은 그만두자.\" 베르투치오가 엄숙하게 대답했다. \"그리고 그 이름을 두 번 다시 네가 방금 내뱉은 식으로 입에 담지 마라.\"\n\n\"흥,\" 안드레아가 베르투치오의 엄숙한 태도에 조금 압도되어 말했다. \"왜 안 된다는 거요?\"\n\n\"그 이름을 지닌 분은 너 같은 비열한 자의 아버지가 되기에는 하늘의 은총을 너무도 많이 받은 분이기 때문이다.\"\n\n\"오, 그것참 듣기 좋은 말이군.\"\n\n\"네가 조심하지 않으면, 듣기 좋지 않은 일이 벌어질 게다.\"\n\n\"협박이라면, 난 두렵지 않소. 내 말하겠는데——\"\n\n\"네가 너 따위 난쟁이를 상대하고 있는 줄 아느냐?\" 베르투치오가 어찌나 차분한 어조로, 어찌나 흔들림 없는 눈빛으로 말했던지, 안드레아는 영혼 깊은 곳까지 흔들렸다. \"네가 갤리선 죄수들이나 세상 물정 모르는 풋내기들을 상대하고 있는 줄 알아? 베네데토, 너는 무시무시한 손아귀에 떨어진 게다. 그 손은 너를 위해 기꺼이 열릴 채비가 되어 있으니, 그것을 잘 이용해라. 그들이 잠시 내려놓은 벼락을 가지고 장난치지 마라. 네가 그들의 움직임을 막으려 든다면, 그들은 그것을 당장에라도 다시 집어 들 수 있으니까.\"\n\n\"내 아버지가 누구인지, 난 기어이 알아낼 거요.\" 고집 센 젊은이가 말했다. \"죽는 한이 있어도, 기필코 알아내고 말겠소. 추문 따위가 나하고 무슨 상관이오? 보세요● 말마따나 내게 무슨 재산이, 무슨 명성이, 무슨 '끗발'이 있단 말이오? 당신들 같은 거물들은 백만금을 가졌어도 추문이 나면 늘 무언가를 잃지. 자, 내 아버지가 누구요?\"\n● 보세: 신문 편집인으로 등장하는 인물.—옮긴이\n\n\"그걸 말해 주러 온 게다.\"\n\n\"아.\" 베네데토가 기쁨으로 눈을 반짝이며 외쳤다. 바로 그때 문이 열리고, 간수가 베르투치오에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"실례합니다, 선생님. 하지만 예심 판사께서 죄수를 기다리고 계십니다.\"\n\n\"그래서 우리 면담은 이렇게 끝이로군.\" 안드레아가 그 훌륭한 집사에게 말했다. \"저 성가신 작자, 악마한테나 끌려갔으면 좋겠소!\"\n\n\"내일 다시 오마.\" 베르투치오가 말했다.\n\n\"좋소! 헌병 양반들, 어서 가십시다. 아, 이보시오, 문간에 내 앞으로 몇 크라운만 남겨 두시오. 필요한 물건 좀 사게 말이오!\"\n\n\"그리해 주마.\" 베르투치오가 대답했다.\n\n안드레아가 손을 내밀었다. 베르투치오는 자기 손을 주머니에 그대로 둔 채, 그저 동전 몇 닢을 짤랑거릴 뿐이었다.\n\n\"내가 바란 게 바로 그거요.\" 안드레아가 베르투치오의 야릇한 평온함에 완전히 압도당한 채 억지로 미소를 지으려 애쓰며 말했다.\n\n\"내가 속고 있는 건 아닐까?\" 그는 사람들이 \"샐러드 바구니\"●라 부르는 그 길쭉하고 창살 박힌 호송차에 올라타며 중얼거렸다.\n● 샐러드 바구니: 죄수 호송차를 일컫는 속어.—옮긴이\n\n\"상관없어, 어디 두고 보자고! 그럼, 내일!\" 그가 베르투치오 쪽으로 돌아서며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"내일!\" 집사가 대답했다.\n\n# Chapter 108. The Judge\n\n우리는 부소니 신부가 죽음의 방에서 누아르티에와 단둘이 남았던 것을, 그리고 그 노인과 사제만이 젊은 처녀의 시신을 지키고 있었던 것을 기억한다. 신부의 기독교적 권면이었는지, 그의 다정한 자비였는지, 아니면 설득력 있는 말이었는지, 어쨌든 무언가가 누아르티에의 용기를 되살려 놓았다. 사제와 이야기를 나눈 뒤로 그의 격렬한 절망은 차분한 체념으로 바뀌었고, 발랑틴을 향한 그의 지나친 애정을 아는 사람이라면 누구나 그 변화에 놀랐다.\n\n빌포르 씨는 발랑틴이 죽은 날 아침 이후로 아버지를 보지 못했다. 집안의 모든 것이 바뀌어 있었다. 그 자신을 위해 새로운 하인이 들어왔고, 누아르티에를 위해서도 새 하인이 들어왔으며, 빌포르 부인의 시중을 들 여자 둘이 새로 들어왔다. 사실상 어디나, 문지기에서 마부에 이르기까지, 새로운 얼굴들이 집안의 여러 주인들 앞에 나타났고, 그리하여 같은 가족 구성원들 사이에 늘 있어 온 분열이 한층 깊어졌다. 게다가 중죄 재판이 곧 시작될 참이었고, 빌포르는 방에 틀어박혀 카드루스를 살해한 자에 대한 기소를 준비하느라 열병에 걸린 듯한 불안 속에서 진력하고 있었다. 몬테크리스토 백작이 관여한 다른 모든 일이 그러했듯, 이 사건 역시 파리에서 커다란 반향을 일으켰다. 증거는 확실히 결정적이지 못했다. 죽음을 앞둔 탈주한 갤리선 죄수가 임종의 자리에서 적어 놓은 몇 마디 말에 근거한 것이었고, 그 죄수는 증오나 복수심에 사로잡혀 자기 동료를 고발했을 수도 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 검사장의 마음은 이미 굳어져 있었다. 그는 베네데토가 유죄라고 확신했고, 이 무거운 사건을 능숙하게 이끌어 자신의 자존심을 만족시키기를 바랐다. 그 자존심이야말로 그의 얼어붙은 가슴에 남은 유일한 약점이었다.\n\n그리하여 사건은 빌포르의 쉼 없는 노력 덕분에 준비되었고, 그는 그것이 다가오는 재판의 첫 번째 안건이 되기를 바랐다. 그는 어느 때보다도 더 깊이 틀어박혀야 했는데, 재판 당일 법정 방청권을 얻으려고 그에게 밀려드는 엄청난 수의 청탁을 피하기 위해서였다. 게다가 가엾은 발랑틴이 죽은 지 얼마 되지 않았고, 집안을 뒤덮은 어둠도 너무나 최근의 일이었으므로, 아버지가 직무에 그토록 몰두하는 모습을 보고도 이상하게 여기는 이는 아무도 없었다. 그 직무야말로 그가 슬픔을 떨쳐 낼 수 있는 유일한 수단이었으니까.\n\n빌포르는 단 한 번 아버지를 보았다. 그것은 베르투치오가 베네데토를 두 번째로 찾아간, 그러니까 베네데토가 제 아버지의 이름을 알게 될 예정이었던 그날의 다음 날이었다. 시달리고 지친 그 법관은 자기 집 정원으로 내려가, 타르퀴니우스가 가장 키 큰 양귀비 꽃대를 쳐 내던 것과 비슷한 음울한 기분으로, 지팡이를 휘둘러 장미나무의 길고 시들어 가는 가지들을 쳐 내기 시작했다. 산책로를 따라 늘어선 그 가지들은 지난 계절에 화려하게 피었던 꽃들의 망령처럼 보였다.\n\n그는 그 유명한 판자 문이 황량한 빈터를 내려다보며 서 있는 정원의 그 부분에 여러 번 다다랐다가, 늘 같은 길로 되돌아와 같은 걸음걸이와 같은 몸짓으로 산책을 다시 시작하곤 했다. 그러던 중 그는 우연히 집 쪽으로 눈을 돌렸고, 거기서 아들이 떠들썩하게 노는 소리를 들었다. 아이는 학교에서 돌아와 일요일과 월요일을 어머니와 보내고 있었다.\n\n그렇게 하던 중 그는 누아르티에 씨가 열린 창문 하나에 자리하고 있는 것을 보았다. 노인은 아직 얼마쯤 온기를 머금은 마지막 햇살을 쬘 수 있도록 거기에 옮겨져 있었고, 그 햇살은 발코니를 휘감은 덩굴의 시들어 가는 꽃과 붉은 잎사귀를 비추고 있었다.\n\n노인의 눈은 빌포르가 거의 분간할 수 없는 한 지점에 못 박혀 있었다. 그 시선은 증오와 사나움, 야만적인 초조함으로 가득 차 있어서, 빌포르는 그 어두운 눈길이 어떤 사람을 향하고 있는지 보려고 걷던 길에서 벗어났다.\n\n그러자 그는 잎이 거의 다 떨어진 보리수나무의 무성한 덤불 아래에서 빌포르 부인이 책을 손에 들고 앉아 있는 것을 보았다. 그녀는 책 읽기를 자주 멈추고는 아들에게 미소를 짓거나, 응접실에서 정원으로 자꾸만 던지는 아이의 탄력 있는 공을 도로 던져 주곤 했다.\n\n빌포르는 새파랗게 질렸다. 그는 노인이 무엇을 뜻하는지 알아차렸다.\n\n누아르티에는 계속 같은 대상을 바라보았으나, 그의 시선은 갑자기 아내에게서 남편에게로 옮겨졌고, 빌포르 자신도 그 눈의 꿰뚫는 듯한 추궁을 견뎌 내야 했다. 그 눈은 방향을, 심지어 그 언어까지 바꾸면서도 위협하는 표정만은 조금도 잃지 않았다. 빌포르 부인은 제 머리 위에서 그렇게 불길이 다 타오르고 있는 그 격정을 알지 못한 채, 그 순간 아들의 공을 손에 쥐고는 입맞춤으로 공을 되찾으라고 아이에게 손짓하고 있었다. 에두아르는 한참을 졸랐는데, 아마도 어머니의 입맞춤이 공을 얻으려고 들여야 하는 수고에 비해 충분한 보상이 되지 못했기 때문이리라. 그래도 마침내 아이는 마음을 정하고, 창문에서 헬리오트로프와 데이지 덤불 속으로 뛰어내려, 이마에 땀을 줄줄 흘리며 어머니에게 달려갔다. 빌포르 부인은 아이의 이마를 닦아 주고 거기에 입을 맞춘 다음, 한 손에는 공을, 다른 손에는 봉봉 몇 알을 들려 도로 돌려보냈다.\n\n빌포르는 뱀에게 이끌리는 새처럼 저항할 수 없는 힘에 끌려 집 쪽으로 걸어갔다. 그가 다가가자 누아르티에의 눈길이 그를 좇았고, 그 눈은 어찌나 이글거리는 광채를 띠었는지 빌포르는 그것이 자기 가슴 깊은 곳까지 꿰뚫는 것을 느꼈다. 그 진지한 눈길 속에서는 깊은 질책과 함께 무시무시한 위협을 읽을 수 있었다. 그러더니 누아르티에는 잊힌 맹세를 아들에게 일깨우려는 듯 하늘로 눈을 들었다.\n\n\"알겠습니다, 아버지.\" 빌포르가 아래에서 대답했다. \"알겠습니다. 하루만 더 참아 주십시오. 제가 말한 것은 반드시 하겠습니다.\"\n\n누아르티에는 그 말에 진정된 듯, 무심하게 눈을 다른 쪽으로 돌렸다. 빌포르는 자기 목을 죄는 듯한 외투의 단추를 거칠게 풀어헤치고는, 핏기 없는 손으로 이마를 쓸며 서재로 들어갔다.\n\n밤은 춥고 고요했다. 가족은 모두 잠자리에 들었으나 빌포르만은 홀로 깨어 있었고, 새벽 다섯 시까지 일했다. 그는 예심 판사들이 전날 밤 행한 마지막 신문 기록을 검토하고, 증인들의 진술을 정리하며, 기소장에 마지막 손질을 가했다. 그것은 그가 지금껏 작성한 어떤 것보다도 가장 힘차고 가장 잘 구상된 기소장 가운데 하나였다.\n\n이튿날인 월요일은 중죄 재판이 처음 열리는 날이었다. 아침은 흐리고 음울하게 밝아 왔고, 빌포르는 그 희뿌연 잿빛 빛이 자신이 붉은 잉크로 그어 놓은 줄들 위로 비치는 것을 보았다. 등불이 마지막으로 발버둥 치는 동안 법관은 잠깐 잠이 들었었다. 그 등불의 깜빡임이 그를 깨웠고, 그는 자신의 손가락이 마치 피에 담갔던 것처럼 축축하고 자줏빛인 것을 발견했다.\n\n그는 창문을 열었다. 환한 노란 줄기 하나가 하늘을 가로지르며, 지평선 위로 검게 도드라진 포플러나무들을 절반으로 가르는 듯했다. 밤나무 너머 클로버 밭에서는 종달새 한 마리가 맑은 아침 노래를 쏟아 내며 하늘로 솟구치고 있었다. 이슬의 습기가 빌포르의 머리를 적시며 그의 기억을 새롭게 했다.\n\n\"오늘,\" 그가 애써 말했다. \"오늘 정의의 칼날을 쥔 자는 죄가 있는 곳이라면 어디든 내리쳐야 한다.\"\n\n자기도 모르게 그의 눈은 전날 밤 아버지를 보았던 누아르티에의 방 창문 쪽으로 향했다. 커튼이 쳐져 있었으나, 그의 마음속에 아버지의 모습이 어찌나 생생했던지, 그는 마치 창문이 열려 있고 그 틈으로 위협하는 노인을 본 것처럼 닫힌 창문을 향해 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"그래.\" 그가 중얼거렸다. \"그래, 만족하십시오.\"\n\n그의 고개가 가슴 위로 떨어졌고, 그 자세로 그는 서재 안을 서성였다. 그러더니 옷을 입은 채로 소파에 몸을 던졌는데, 잠을 자려는 것이라기보다는 추위와 일로 굳어 버린 사지를 쉬게 하려는 것이었다. 하나둘씩 모두가 잠에서 깼다. 빌포르는 서재에서, 집안의 삶에 따라붙는 소리들이 잇따라 들려오는 것을 들었다. 문을 여닫는 소리, 빌포르 부인이 하녀를 부르는 종소리, 그리고 거기에 섞여 제 나이의 즐거움으로 가득 차 일어난 아이의 첫 외침이 들렸다. 빌포르도 종을 울렸다. 새 하인이 서류를 가져왔고, 그와 함께 초콜릿 한 잔도 들고 왔다.\n\n\"무엇을 가져온 건가?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"초콜릿 한 잔입니다.\"\n\n\"난 그런 걸 부탁하지 않았네. 누가 이런 마음을 써 준 건가?\"\n\n\"마님이십니다. 살인 사건 재판에서 말씀을 많이 하셔야 할 테니, 기운을 유지하시려면 뭐라도 좀 드셔야 한다고 하셨습니다.\" 그러고는 하인이 소파에서 가장 가까운 탁자 위에 잔을 놓았다. 그 탁자도 나머지와 마찬가지로 서류로 뒤덮여 있었다.\n\n하인은 방을 나갔다. 빌포르는 잠시 음울한 표정으로 그것을 바라보더니, 갑자기 신경질적인 동작으로 잔을 집어 들고는 단숨에 들이켰다. 그가 그 음료가 치명적이기를 바랐다고, 그리하여 차라리 죽는 한이 있어도 하기 싫은 그 의무에서 자신을 벗어나게 해 줄 죽음을 구하고 있었다고 여겨질 만도 했다. 그러더니 그는 일어나, 차마 보기 끔찍했을 미소를 띤 채 방 안을 서성였다. 초콜릿은 해롭지 않았다. 빌포르 씨는 아무런 이상도 느끼지 못했으니까.\n\n아침 식사 시간이 되었으나, 빌포르 씨는 식탁에 없었다. 하인이 다시 들어왔다.\n\n\"마님이 일깨워 드리라고 하셨습니다.\" 하인이 말했다. \"방금 열한 시를 알리는 종이 울렸고, 재판은 열두 시에 시작된다고요.\"\n\n\"그래서,\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"그게 어쨌다는 건가?\"\n\n\"마님은 채비를 다 마치셨습니다. 이제 준비가 되셨고, 모시고 가실 건지 여쭤보라고 하셨습니다.\"\n\n\"어디로 말인가?\"\n\n\"법원으로요.\"\n\n\"무엇하러?\"\n\"마님은 재판에 꼭 참석하고 싶어 하십니다.\"\n\n\"아,\" 빌포르가 섬뜩한 어조로 말했다. \"부인이 그걸 원한다고?\"\n\n하인이 뒤로 물러서며 말했다. \"혼자 가시려거든, 나리, 제가 가서 마님께 그리 전하겠습니다.\"\n\n빌포르는 잠시 잠자코 있다가, 손톱으로 핏기 없는 두 뺨을 후벼 자국을 냈다.\n\n\"마님께 전하게.\" 그가 마침내 대답했다. \"내가 할 이야기가 있으니, 자기 방에서 나를 기다려 달라고 말일세.\"\n\n\"네, 나리.\"\n\n\"그리고 와서 옷 입는 것과 면도하는 걸 거들게.\"\n\n\"곧 가겠습니다, 나리.\"\n하인은 거의 곧바로 다시 나타나, 주인의 면도를 마친 뒤 온통 검은색으로 옷을 입는 것을 도왔다. 일을 마치고 그가 말했다.\n\n\"마님이, 옷을 다 입으시는 대로 기다리고 계시겠다고 하셨습니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"부인에게 가겠네.\"\n\n그리하여 빌포르는 서류를 옆구리에 끼고 모자를 손에 든 채, 아내의 방으로 발걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n문 앞에서 그는 잠시 멈춰 서서 축축하고 핏기 없는 이마를 닦았다. 그러고는 방으로 들어섰다. 빌포르 부인은 오토만에 앉아, 신문과 소책자 몇 가지를 초조하게 넘기고 있었는데, 어린 에두아르가 심심풀이로 어머니가 다 읽기도 전에 그것들을 갈가리 찢어 놓고 있었다. 그녀는 외출 채비를 갖춘 차림이었고, 모자는 의자 위 옆자리에 놓여 있었으며, 두 손에는 장갑이 끼워져 있었다.\n\n\"아, 오셨군요, 여보.\" 그녀가 본디 차분한 목소리로 말했다. \"그런데 어쩌면 그렇게 안색이 창백하세요! 밤새 일하셨어요? 왜 아침 식사하러 내려오지 않으셨죠? 자, 저를 데려가실래요, 아니면 제가 에두아르를 데려갈까요?\"\n\n빌포르 부인은 답 하나라도 끌어내려고 질문을 거듭 쏟아 냈으나, 그 모든 물음에 빌포르 씨는 조각상처럼 차갑게 입을 다물고 있었다.\n\n\"에두아르,\" 빌포르가 위압적인 눈길을 아이에게 고정하며 말했다. \"응접실에 가서 놀려무나, 얘야. 엄마에게 할 이야기가 있단다.\"\n\n빌포르 부인은 그 차가운 얼굴, 단호한 어조, 그리고 무섭도록 이상한 서두에 몸을 떨었다. 에두아르는 고개를 들어 어머니를 보았고, 어머니가 그 명령을 거들지 않는 것을 알자 자기 납 병정들의 머리를 잘라 내기 시작했다.\n\n\"에두아르,\" 빌포르 씨가 어찌나 매섭게 외쳤던지 아이가 바닥에서 벌떡 일어났다. \"내 말 들리느냐? 가거라!\"\n\n이런 대접에 익숙하지 않던 아이는 새파랗게 질려 떨면서 일어섰다. 그 감정이 두려움 때문인지 분노 때문인지는 가늠하기 어려웠다. 아버지는 아이에게 다가가 품에 안고는 이마에 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"가거라.\" 그가 말했다. \"가, 내 아들아.\" 에두아르는 뛰어나갔다.\n\n빌포르 씨는 문으로 가, 아이의 뒤로 문을 닫고 빗장을 질렀다.\n\n\"이런!\" 젊은 여인이 남편의 마음속 깊은 생각을 읽어 내려 애쓰며 말했다. 그러는 동안 그녀의 얼굴 위로 미소가 스쳤으나, 그 미소는 빌포르의 무표정을 더욱 얼어붙게 했다. \"무슨 일이에요?\"\n\n\"부인, 평소에 쓰는 독약을 어디에 두고 있소?\" 법관이 아무런 서두도 없이, 아내와 문 사이에 자리를 잡으며 말했다.\n\n빌포르 부인은 위를 올려다보다 제 머리 위로 살기 어린 덫이 닫히는 것을 본 새가 느낄 법한 감각을 틀림없이 맛보았으리라.\n\n비명도 한숨도 아닌, 쉬고 끊어진 소리가 그녀의 입에서 새어 나왔고, 그녀는 죽은 사람처럼 새파랗게 질렸다.\n\n\"여보,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"전, 전 무슨 말씀인지 모르겠어요.\"\n\n그리고 처음 일어난 공포의 발작에서 그녀는 소파에서 몸을 일으켰다가, 다음 순간 그보다 더 강했을 발작에 다시 쿠션 위로 쓰러졌다.\n\n\"내가 물었소.\" 빌포르가 더없이 차분한 어조로 말을 이었다. \"당신이 그 독약으로 내 장인 생메랑 씨, 내 장모 생메랑 부인, 바루아, 그리고 내 딸 발랑틴을 죽인 그 독약을 어디에 감춰 두었느냐고.\"\n\n\"아, 여보.\" 빌포르 부인이 두 손을 마주 잡으며 외쳤다. \"무슨 말씀을 하시는 거예요?\"\n\n\"신문하는 것은 당신이 할 일이 아니라, 대답하는 것이 할 일이오.\"\n\n\"판사에게 하는 건가요, 남편에게 하는 건가요?\" 빌포르 부인이 더듬거리며 물었다.\n\n\"판사에게요. 판사에게 하는 거요, 부인!\" 그 여인의 무서운 창백함, 그 눈길의 고뇌, 온몸의 떨림은 보기에 끔찍했다.\n\n\"아, 여보.\" 그녀가 중얼거렸다. \"아, 여보.\" 그러고는 그게 전부였다.\n\n\"대답을 못 하는군, 부인!\" 그 무서운 신문관이 외쳤다. 그러더니 그는 분노보다도 더 무서운 미소를 띠고 덧붙였다. \"그렇다면 사실이로군. 당신은 그것을 부인하지 않는군!\" 그녀가 앞으로 나섰다. \"그리고 부인할 수도 없지!\" 빌포르가 마치 정의의 이름으로 그녀를 붙잡으려는 듯 손을 그녀 쪽으로 뻗으며 덧붙였다. \"당신은 이 여러 범죄를 뻔뻔스러운 솜씨로 해치웠소. 하지만 그건 당신을 향한 애정에 눈먼 자들만 속일 수 있는 솜씨였지. 생메랑 부인이 죽은 뒤로 나는 우리 집에 독살자가 살고 있다는 걸 알았소. 다브리니 씨가 내게 경고했지. 바루아가 죽은 뒤 나의 의심은 한 천사를 향했소. 죄가 없을 때조차 내 가슴속에 늘 살아 있는 그런 의심 말이오. 하지만 발랑틴이 죽은 뒤로, 부인, 내 마음속엔 한 점 의혹도 없어졌소. 내 마음속뿐 아니라 다른 이들의 마음속에서도 말이오. 그러니 두 사람이 알고 여러 사람이 의심하는 당신의 범죄는 곧 세상에 드러날 것이오. 그리고 방금 말했듯, 당신은 더 이상 남편에게 말하고 있는 게 아니라 판사에게 말하고 있는 거요.\"\n\n젊은 여인은 두 손에 얼굴을 묻었다.\n\n\"아, 여보.\" 그녀가 더듬거렸다. \"부탁이에요, 겉모습만 믿지 마세요.\"\n\n\"그래, 당신은 비겁자인가?\" 빌포르가 경멸하는 목소리로 외쳤다. \"하지만 나는 독살자들이 늘 비겁자라는 걸 줄곧 봐 왔지. 당신이 비겁자일 수 있다니, 당신, 자기 손으로 죽인 노인 둘과 처녀 하나의 죽음을 지켜볼 용기를 지녔던 당신이?\"\n\n\"여보! 여보!\"\n\n\"당신이 비겁자일 수 있다니,\" 빌포르가 점점 더 흥분하며 말을 이었다. \"네 번의 단말마를 일 분 일 분 하나하나 헤아릴 수 있었던 당신이? 그 지옥 같은 계획을 짜고, 거의 기적에 가까운 재주와 정확함으로 음료를 치워 버린 당신이? 그렇게 모든 걸 그토록 세밀하게 계산한 당신이, 한 가지를 계산하는 걸 잊었단 말이오? 당신의 범죄가 드러나면 당신이 어디로 끌려가게 될지 말이오. 오, 그럴 리 없지. 당신은 마땅히 받아야 할 벌을 피할 수 있도록, 그 어떤 것보다 더 확실하고 더 교묘하며 더 치명적인 독약을 따로 남겨 두었을 테지. 당신은 그렇게 해 놓았을 거요. 적어도 나는 그러길 바라오.\"\n\n빌포르 부인은 두 손을 뻗으며 무릎을 꿇었다.\n\n\"알겠소.\" 그가 말했다. \"당신은 자백하는군. 하지만 판사들 앞에서 한 자백, 마지막 순간에 한 자백, 범죄를 더 이상 부인할 수 없을 때 억지로 끌려 나온 자백은 죄인에게 내려지는 벌을 조금도 줄여 주지 못하오!\"\n\n\"벌이라고요?\" 빌포르 부인이 외쳤다. \"벌이라뇨, 여보? 그 말을 벌써 두 번이나 하시는군요!\"\n\n\"물론이오. 당신이 네 번이나 죄를 지었으니 벌을 면할 수 있으리라 바랐소? 그것을 선고하는 자의 아내이니 벌이 보류되리라 생각했소? 아니오, 부인, 아니오. 독살자에게는, 그가 누구든, 단두대가 기다리고 있소. 방금 말했듯, 그 독살자가 자기 몫으로 가장 치명적인 독약 몇 방울을 남겨 두는 신중함을 발휘하지 않았다면 말이오.\"\n\n빌포르 부인이 거친 비명을 질렀고, 일그러진 얼굴 위로 흉측하고 걷잡을 수 없는 공포가 번졌다.\n\n\"오, 단두대는 두려워 마시오, 부인.\" 법관이 말했다. \"나는 당신에게 치욕을 안기지 않겠소. 그건 곧 나 자신에게 치욕이 될 테니까. 아니, 당신이 내 말을 똑똑히 들었다면, 당신이 단두대에서 죽지 않으리라는 걸 알아차렸을 거요.\"\n\n\"아니요, 모르겠어요. 무슨 말씀이세요?\" 가엾은 여인이 완전히 압도당한 채 더듬거렸다.\n\n\"내 말은, 이 수도의 으뜸가는 법관의 아내가 자기 오명으로 흠 없는 이름을 더럽혀서는 안 된다는 것이오. 단번에 자기 남편과 자식에게 치욕을 안겨서는 안 된다는 것이오.\"\n\"아니요, 아니요, 오, 아니요!\"\n\n\"그러니, 부인, 그것은 당신으로서는 칭찬받을 만한 행동이 될 것이고, 나는 당신에게 그 일을 고마워할 거요!\"\n\n\"고마워하신다고요, 무엇을?\"\n\n\"방금 당신이 한 말에 대해서요.\"\n\n\"제가 무슨 말을 했죠? 오, 머릿속이 빙빙 도네요. 이제 아무것도 모르겠어요. 오, 하느님, 하느님!\"\n\n그러더니 그녀는 머리카락을 풀어헤치고 입가에 거품을 문 채 일어섰다.\n\n\"방에 들어오면서 내가 한 질문에 대답했소? 평소에 쓰는 독약을 어디에 두고 있소, 부인?\"\n\n빌포르 부인은 두 팔을 하늘로 들어 올리고는, 발작적으로 한 손으로 다른 손을 내리쳤다.\n\n\"아니요, 아니요.\" 그녀가 악을 썼다. \"아니요, 그걸 바라실 리 없어요!\"\n\n\"내가 바라지 않는 것은, 부인, 당신이 단두대에서 스러지는 것이오. 알아듣겠소?\" 빌포르가 물었다.\n\n\"오, 자비를, 자비를 베풀어 주세요, 여보!\"\n\n\"내가 요구하는 것은 정의가 실현되는 것이오. 나는 벌하기 위해 이 땅에 있소, 부인.\" 그가 이글거리는 눈길로 덧붙였다. \"다른 여자라면, 설령 그게 왕비라 해도, 나는 사형 집행인에게 보낼 것이오. 하지만 당신에게만은 자비를 베풀겠소. 당신에게는 이렇게 말하겠소. '부인, 가장 확실하고 가장 치명적이며 가장 빠른 독약을 좀 따로 챙겨 두지 않았소?'\"\n\n\"오, 용서해 주세요, 여보. 살게 해 주세요!\"\n\n\"비겁한 여자로군.\" 빌포르가 말했다.\n\n\"제가 당신의 아내라는 걸 생각하세요!\"\n\n\"당신은 독살자요.\"\n\n\"하늘의 이름으로!\"\n\"안 되오!\"\n\n\"한때 당신이 제게 품었던 사랑의 이름으로!\"\n\n\"안 되오, 안 돼!\"\n\"우리 아이의 이름으로! 아, 우리 아이를 봐서, 살게 해 주세요!\"\n\n\"안 되오, 안 돼, 안 된다고 했소. 언젠가, 내가 당신을 살려 두면, 당신은 다른 이들에게 그랬듯 그 아이마저 죽일지 모르오!\"\n\n\"제가요? 제가 제 아이를 죽인다고요?\" 정신이 나간 어머니가 빌포르에게 달려들며 외쳤다. \"제가 제 아들을 죽여요? 하, 하, 하!\" 그러더니 무시무시한 악마 같은 웃음이 그 말을 끝맺었고, 그 웃음은 쉰 가르랑거림 속으로 잦아들었다.\n\n빌포르 부인은 남편의 발치에 쓰러졌다. 그가 그녀에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"잘 생각하시오, 부인.\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 돌아왔을 때 정의가 실현되어 있지 않으면, 나는 내 입으로 당신을 고발하고, 내 손으로 당신을 체포하겠소!\"\n\n그녀는 숨을 헐떡이며, 압도당하고 짓눌린 채 귀를 기울였다. 그녀의 눈만이 살아서 끔찍하게 번득였다.\n\n\"내 말을 알아듣겠소?\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 이제 저 아래로 내려가 한 살인자에게 사형 선고를 내릴 것이오. 돌아왔을 때 당신이 살아 있는 걸 발견하면, 당신은 오늘 밤 구치소에서 잠들게 될 거요.\"\n\n빌포르 부인이 한숨을 내쉬었다. 신경이 무너져 내렸고, 그녀는 양탄자 위로 주저앉았다. 검사장은 어떤 연민 같은 것을 느낀 듯했다. 그는 한결 덜 가혹한 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았고, 그녀에게 고개를 숙이며 천천히 말했다.\n\n\"잘 있으시오, 부인, 잘 있으시오!\"\n\n그 작별 인사는 빌포르 부인에게 사형 집행인의 칼처럼 내리꽂혔다. 그녀는 정신을 잃었다. 검사장은 문을 이중으로 잠근 뒤 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n# 제107장 사자 우리\n\n팔레●에서, 그리고 일반 사람들 사이에서 베네데토 사건이라 불리던 그 사건은 엄청난 반향을 불러일으켰다. 짧은 영광의 시절 동안 카페 드 파리와 강 대로, 불로뉴 숲을 드나들면서, 가짜 카발칸티는 수많은 지인을 만들어 두었다. 신문들은 그가 사교계 인사로서, 또 갤리선 죄수로서 겪은 갖가지 사연을 상세히 보도했다. 안드레아 카발칸티 공자와 직접 안면이 있던 사람들은 모두 그의 운명에 대해 강한 호기심을 느꼈고, 사슬에 묶인 동료를 죽인 죄로 열리는 베네데토 씨의 재판을 어떻게든 직접 보려고 애쓰기로 마음먹었다.\n\n많은 사람들의 눈에 베네데토는, 법의 오류에 희생된 자까지는 아니더라도 적어도 그 오류를 보여 주는 사례로 비쳤다. 그의 아버지 카발칸티 씨가 파리에서 목격된 적이 있었으므로, 그 고명한 인사가 다시 나타나 자신의 명예로운 사생아를 거두려 하지 않겠느냐는 기대가 있었다. 또한 그가 파리를 떠난 정황을 모르는 많은 이들은, 그 늙은 귀족이 보여 준 점잖은 풍채와 신사다운 거동, 세상사에 밝은 식견에 강한 인상을 받았다. 사실 그 노인은, 입을 다물고 산수 계산만 하지 않는 한, 귀족 연기를 제법 그럴듯하게 해냈던 것이다.\n\n피고 본인으로 말하자면, 많은 이들이 그를 어찌나 상냥하고 잘생기고 통이 큰 사람으로 기억하던지, 그를 어떤 음모의 희생자로 여기고 싶어 했다. 이 세상에서 막대한 재산이란 알지 못하는 적의 악의와 질투를 자주 부추기는 법이니 말이다.\n\n그리하여 모두가 법정으로 달려갔다. 어떤 이는 구경하러, 어떤 이는 거기에 대해 한마디 보태러. 아침 일곱 시부터 철문 앞에 군중이 진을 쳤고, 재판이 시작되기 한 시간 전에 이미 특권을 가진 사람들로 법정이 가득 찼다. 어떤 특별한 재판이 열리는 날이면, 판사들이 입장하기 전에는 — 그리고 입장한 뒤에도 종종 — 법정은 마치 응접실 같은 모습이 된다. 많은 사람이 서로를 알아보고, 자리를 잃지 않고도 가능하다면 대화를 나누며, 너무 많은 변호사들에 의해 떨어져 있을 때는 몸짓으로 의사를 주고받는다.\n\n그날은 짧은 여름을 보상해 주는 화창한 가을날 가운데 하루였다. 빌포르 씨가 해 뜰 무렵 보았던 구름은 마법에라도 걸린 듯 모조리 사라졌고, 9월의 가장 부드럽고 가장 눈부신 하루가 온전한 광채를 뽐내며 빛나고 있었다.\n\n언론계의 제왕 가운데 하나로, 그래서 어디에서나 옥좌의 권리를 주장하는 보샹은 단안경 너머로 모든 사람을 살피고 있었다. 그는 샤토르노와 드브레를 발견했다. 두 사람은 방금 정리 한 사람의 호의를 얻어 내, 마땅히 그래야 할 위치인 그의 뒤가 아니라 앞에 서도 좋다는 허락을 받아 낸 참이었다. 그 갸륵한 정리는 장관 비서와 백만장자를 알아보았고, 고귀한 이웃들에게 각별한 배려를 베푸는 뜻에서, 두 사람이 보샹을 만나러 가 있는 동안 자리를 지켜 주겠노라 약속했다.\n\n\"이런,\" 보샹이 말했다. \"우리 친구를 보게 되겠군!\"\n\n\"그래, 정말이지!\" 드브레가 대꾸했다. \"그 갸륵한 공자 말이지. 그 이탈리아 공자들이라면 지긋지긋해!\"\n\n\"단테를 족보 작성자로 자랑하고, 「신곡」까지 거슬러 올라가 가문을 따질 수 있는 사람이라네.\"\n\n\"교수형 밧줄 귀족이지.\" 샤토르노가 무덤덤하게 말했다.\n\n\"그자, 유죄 선고를 받겠지, 안 그런가?\" 드브레가 보샹에게 물었다.\n\n\"이보게, 그 질문이라면 우리가 자네에게 해야 할 것 같은데. 그런 소식은 우리보다 자네가 훨씬 더 잘 알지 않나. 어젯밤 장관 댁에서 재판장을 보았나?\"\n\n\"보았네.\"\n\n\"뭐라던가?\"\n\n\"자넬 놀라게 할 만한 말을 하더군.\"\n\n\"오, 그럼 어서 말해 보게. 그런 일이 일어난 건 참으로 오랜만이니.\"\n\n\"음, 그가 말하길, 교활함의 화신이자 간계의 거인으로 여겨지는 그 베네데토가 실은 지극히 평범하고 어리석은 악당에 지나지 않으며, 죽은 뒤에 그 골상 기관을 가지고 행할 실험을 받을 가치조차 전혀 없다더군.\"\n\n\"흥,\" 보샹이 말했다. \"공자 연기는 제법 잘하던데.\"\n\n\"그렇겠지, 그 가엾은 공자들을 혐오하고 늘 그들에게서 흠을 잡아 즐거워하는 자네에게는 말이야, 보샹. 하지만 본능으로 신사를 알아보고, 문장학의 사냥개처럼 귀족 가문 냄새를 맡아 내는 나에게는 그렇지 않아.\"\n\n\"그럼 자네는 그 공국이란 걸 한 번도 믿지 않았던 건가?\"\n\n\"믿었지. 공국은 믿었지만, 공자는 믿지 않았어.\"\n\n\"제법인데.\" 보샹이 말했다. \"그래도 분명히 말하지만, 그자는 많은 사람에게 그럴듯하게 통했다네. 나는 장관들 집에서 그자를 보았어.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇지.\" 샤토르노가 말했다. \"장관들이 공자에 대해 뭘 좀 안다고 생각하다니, 그 발상하고는!\"\n\n\"방금 자네 말에도 일리가 있군.\" 보샹이 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"그런데,\" 드브레가 보샹에게 말했다. \"내가 재판장과 이야기했다면, 자네는 분명 검사와 함께 있었겠지.\"\n\n\"그건 불가능했네. 지난 한 주 동안 빌포르 씨는 두문불출하고 있으니까. 그럴 만도 하지. 그 기이한 집안의 우환이 줄을 잇더니, 그에 못지않게 기이한 딸의 죽음까지 뒤따랐으니……\"\n\n\"기이하다고? 그게 무슨 말인가, 보샹?\"\n\n\"오, 그래. 설마 그 일이 장관 댁에서 전혀 입에 오르지 않았다고 우길 셈인가?\" 보샹이 단안경을 눈에 끼워 넣으며 말했다. 그러고는 그것을 그 자리에 머물게 하려고 애썼다.\n\n\"이보게,\" 샤토르노가 말했다. \"자네는 그 단안경 다루는 솜씨가 드브레의 절반에도 못 미친다고 말해 두지. 드브레, 한 수 가르쳐 주게.\"\n\n\"가만,\" 보샹이 말했다. \"분명 내가 잘못 본 게 아니야.\"\n\n\"뭔데 그러나?\"\n\n\"저 여자야!\"\n\n\"누구 말인가?\"\n\n\"떠났다고들 하던데.\"\n\n\"외제니 양 말인가?\" 샤토르노가 물었다. \"돌아왔다고?\"\n\n\"아니, 그 어머니 말일세.\"\n\n\"당글라르 부인? 말도 안 돼! 그럴 리가!\" 샤토르노가 말했다. \"딸이 달아난 지 겨우 열흘에, 남편이 파산한 지 사흘밖에 안 됐는데?\"\n\n드브레의 얼굴이 살짝 붉어졌고, 그는 보샹의 시선이 향한 방향을 눈으로 좇았다.\n\n\"이보게,\" 그가 말했다. \"그냥 베일을 쓴 부인일 뿐이야. 어느 외국 공녀거나, 어쩌면 카발칸티의 어머니일 수도 있지. 그나저나 보샹, 자네 방금 아주 흥미로운 이야기를 꺼내던 참이었잖나.\"\n\n\"내가?\"\n\"그래. 발랑틴의 기이한 죽음에 대해 들려주고 있었지.\"\n\n\"아, 그래, 그랬지. 그런데 빌포르 부인은 어째서 여기 와 있지 않은 걸까?\"\n\n\"가엾고 어여쁜 부인이지.\" 드브레가 말했다. \"틀림없이 병원에 보낼 향유를 증류하거나, 자신과 친구들을 위한 화장품을 만드느라 바쁠 거야. 그런 취미에 한 해 2천이나 3천 에퀴를 쓴다는 걸 아나? 그래도 여기 없다니 이상하군. 그 부인을 보고 싶었는데. 나는 그 부인을 아주 좋아하거든.\"\n\n\"나는 그 여자를 싫어해.\" 샤토르노가 말했다.\n\n\"왜?\"\n\n\"나도 모르지. 우리가 왜 사랑하고, 왜 미워하겠나? 그냥 반감 때문에 그 여자가 싫어.\"\n\n\"아니, 그보다는 본능 때문이겠지.\"\n\n\"그럴지도. 하지만 보샹, 자네가 하던 이야기로 돌아가세.\"\n\n\"음, 빌포르 씨 댁에서 사람이 그렇게 떼로 죽어 나가는 이유를 아나?\"\n\n\"'떼로'라니 적절한 표현이군.\" 샤토르노가 말했다.\n\n\"이보게, 그 단어는 생시몽●●에서도 찾을 수 있다네.\"\n\n\"하지만 정작 그 일 자체는 빌포르 씨 댁에서 벌어지고 있지. 자, 본론으로 돌아가세.\"\n\n\"그 이야기가 나왔으니 말인데,\" 드브레가 말했다. \"그 부인이 그 집에 대해 캐묻더군. 지난 석 달 동안 검은 휘장을 드리우고 있는 그 집 말일세.\"\n\n\"그 부인이 누군데?\" 샤토르노가 물었다.\n\n\"장관 부인 말일세, 당연하지 않나!\"\n\n\"오, 미안하네! 나는 장관들을 찾아가는 법이 없어서. 그건 공자들에게 맡겨 두지.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 자네는 아까만 해도 그저 반짝이는 정도였는데, 이제는 눈부시게 빛나는군. 우리를 좀 가엾게 여겨 주게. 안 그러면 유피테르처럼 우리를 바싹 태워 버릴 테니.\"\n\n\"이제 입을 다물겠네.\" 샤토르노가 말했다. \"부디 나를 가엾게 여겨, 내가 하는 말마다 일일이 꼬투리를 잡지 말아 주게.\"\n\n\"자, 보샹, 이야기를 끝까지 마쳐 보세. 어제 그 부인이 이 일에 대해 내게 물었다고 했잖나. 나를 좀 깨우쳐 주게. 그러면 내가 알게 된 걸 그 부인에게 전해 줄 테니.\"\n\n\"그럼, 여러분, 빌포르 씨 댁에서 사람이 그렇게 떼로(이 단어가 마음에 드는군) 죽어 나가는 이유는, 그 집에 살인자가 있기 때문이라네!\"\n\n두 젊은이는 몸을 떨었다. 같은 생각이 그들에게도 한두 번이 아니게 떠올랐던 것이다.\n\n\"그래서 그 살인자가 누구란 말인가?\" 두 사람이 동시에 물었다.\n\n\"꼬마 에두아르라네!\" 청중의 웃음이 터졌지만, 그것은 화자를 조금도 당황하게 하지 못했다. 그는 말을 이었다. \"그래, 여러분. 에두아르라네. 사람 죽이는 기술에 통달한 그 신동 말일세.\"\n\n\"농담하는 거겠지.\"\n\n\"천만에. 어제 나는 빌포르 씨 댁에서 막 나온 하인 하나를 고용했네 — 내일이면 내보낼 작정이지만 말이야. 그 집에서 공포에 질려 강요당했던 단식을 벌충하느라 어찌나 엄청나게 먹어 대는지. 자, 그럼 들어 보게.\"\n\n\"듣고 있네.\"\n\n\"그 귀여운 아이가 어떤 약이 든 병 하나를 손에 넣었던 모양이야. 그러고는 자기 비위를 거스른 자들에게 그 약을 이따금 써먹었다는 거지. 처음에는 생메랑 후작 부부가 그 아이의 비위를 거슬렀어. 그래서 자기 영약을 세 방울 따랐지 — 세 방울이면 충분했다네. 다음은 누아르티에 씨의 늙은 하인 바루아였어. 이 작은 악당을 가끔 퇴짜 놓곤 했거든. 그래서 그도 같은 양의 영약을 받았지. 발랑틴도 마찬가지였어. 아이가 발랑틴을 질투했거든. 다른 사람들에게 준 것과 똑같은 양을 먹였고, 그렇게 발랑틴도 나머지 사람들처럼 끝장이 난 거야.\"\n\n\"아니, 무슨 그런 터무니없는 소릴 하나?\" 샤토르노가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 정말 별난 이야기지.\" 보샹이 말했다. \"안 그런가?\"\n\n\"말도 안 되는 소리야.\" 드브레가 말했다.\n\n\"아,\" 보샹이 말했다. \"나를 못 믿겠다는 건가? 그럼 내 하인에게, 아니 내일이면 더 이상 내 하인이 아닐 그자에게 물어보게. 그 집에서 다들 하던 이야기였으니까.\"\n\n\"그래, 그 영약은 어디 있나? 대체 뭐란 말인가?\"\n\n\"그 아이가 감추고 있지.\"\n\n\"한데 어디서 그걸 찾아냈다는 건가?\"\n\n\"제 어머니의 실험실에서.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 아이 어머니는 실험실에 독약을 보관해 둔단 말인가?\"\n\n\"내가 어찌 알겠나? 자네는 마치 검사처럼 나를 추궁하는군. 나는 그저 들은 대로 옮길 뿐, 내게 일러 준 자처럼 그 이상은 할 수 없네. 그 가엾은 친구는 겁에 질려 아무것도 먹질 못하더군.\"\n\n\"믿기지 않는 일이야!\"\n\n\"아니, 이보게, 전혀 믿기지 않을 일도 아니라네. 작년에 리슐리외 거리를 지나가던 그 아이를 보지 않았나. 형제자매들이 자는 동안 귀에 핀을 찔러 죽이며 즐거워하던 아이 말일세. 우리 뒤를 잇는 세대는 아주 조숙하다네.\"\n\n\"이보게, 보샹,\" 샤토르노가 말했다. \"자네가 지금까지 늘어놓은 이야기를 한마디도 믿지 않는다는 데 뭐든 걸겠네. 그건 그렇고, 여기 몬테크리스토 백작이 안 보이는군.\"\n\n\"그는 기진맥진해 있어.\" 드브레가 말했다. \"게다가 사람들 앞에 나서기도 어려운 처지지. 카발칸티 부자에게 속아 넘어갔으니 말이야. 듣자 하니 그자들이 위조 신용장을 들고 그를 찾아가서는, 이 공국이라는 허상을 빌미로 10만 프랑을 가로챘다더군.\"\n\n\"그나저나, 샤토르노 씨,\" 보샹이 물었다. \"모렐은 좀 어떤가?\"\n\n\"맹세컨대, 세 번이나 찾아갔는데 한 번도 만나지 못했네. 그래도 그 누이는 별로 걱정하는 기색이 없더군. 이삼 일 동안 그를 보지 못했지만 분명 잘 있을 거라고 했어.\"\n\n\"아, 이제 생각났는데, 몬테크리스토 백작은 이 법정에 나타날 수 없겠군.\" 보샹이 말했다.\n\n\"왜 안 되나?\"\n\n\"이 드라마의 배우 가운데 하나니까.\"\n\n\"그럼 그가 누구라도 죽였단 말인가?\"\n\n\"아니, 오히려 사람들이 그를 죽이려 했지. 카드루스 씨가 그 친구 베네데토에게 살해당한 게 바로 그의 집을 나서던 길에서였다는 걸 알지 않나. 그 유명한 조끼가 그의 집에서 발견됐고, 그 안에 결혼 계약서 서명을 멈추게 한 편지가 들어 있었다는 것도 알 거야. 그 조끼가 보이나? 저기 있군. 온통 피로 얼룩진 채, 범죄의 증거로 책상 위에 놓여 있어.\"\n\n\"아, 잘됐군.\"\n\n\"쉿, 여러분, 재판부가 들어오네. 우리 자리로 돌아가세.\"\n\n법정 안에서 소란이 일었다. 정리가 힘찬 \"에헴!\" 소리로 자기가 모시는 두 사람을 불렀고, 문지기가 나타나 보마르셰 시절 이래로 그 직분 특유의 째지는 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"재판부 입정, 여러분!\"\n\n# Chapter 110. The Indictment\n\n● 팔레: 파리의 법원을 가리킨다. ―옮긴이 ●● 생시몽: 17~18세기 프랑스의 회고록 작가. ―옮긴이\n\n깊디깊은 정적 속에서 판사들이 자리에 앉았다.\n\n배심원들도 자리를 잡았다. 유난한 주목, 거의 만인의 찬탄이라 해도 좋을 시선을 한 몸에 받으며 빌포르 씨가 안락의자에 앉아 차분한 눈길로 주위를 둘러보았다. 모두가 그 근엄하고 준엄한 얼굴을 놀란 눈으로 바라보았다. 개인적 슬픔조차 흐트러뜨리지 못한 그 평온한 표정, 인간의 모든 감정과 무관해 보이는 그 사람의 모습은 거의 공포에 가까운 무언가를 불러일으켰다.\n\"헌병들, 피고인을 들여보내시오.\" 재판장이 말했다.\n\n이 말에 방청객의 주의가 한층 팽팽해졌고, 모든 눈이 베네데토가 들어올 문 쪽으로 쏠렸다. 이윽고 문이 열리고 피고인이 모습을 드러냈다.\n그 자리에 있던 모두가 같은 인상을 받았고, 그의 표정에 속는 사람은 아무도 없었다. 그의 얼굴에는 심장의 고동을 멎게 하고 두 뺨을 핏기 없이 만드는 그 깊은 동요의 기색이 조금도 없었다. 한 손은 모자 위에, 다른 손은 흰 조끼의 트인 곳에 우아하게 얹은 그의 두 손은 전혀 떨리지 않았다. 그의 눈은 차분했고, 심지어 빛나기까지 했다. 그는 법정에 들어서기가 무섭게 판사단과 보조진 전체를 한번 훑었다. 그의 눈은 재판장에게 좀 더 오래 머물렀고, 검사에게는 그보다 더 오래 머물렀다.\n\n안드레아 곁에는 그의 변호를 맡을 변호사가 서 있었다. 법원이 선임한 변호사였는데, 안드레아가 그런 세세한 일에는 조금도 관심을 두려 하지 않았고, 아무런 중요성도 부여하지 않는 듯 보였기 때문이다. 변호사는 머리가 옅은 색인 젊은이로, 그 얼굴은 피고인의 표정보다 백 배는 더한 동요를 드러내고 있었다.\n\n재판장이 공소장 낭독을 요청했다. 우리가 알다시피 빌포르의 영민하고 가차 없는 펜으로 수정된 그 공소장이었다. 긴 낭독이 이어지는 동안 방청객의 주의는 끊임없이 안드레아에게 쏠렸으나, 그는 스파르타인 같은 무심함으로 그 시선을 견뎌 냈다. 빌포르가 그토록 간결하고 능변이었던 적은 없었다. 범죄는 더없이 생생한 색채로 묘사되었다. 피고인의 지난 삶, 그 변모, 가장 이른 시기부터의 그의 인생 전반에 대한 검토가, 인간 삶에 대한 지식이 검사 같은 정신에게 제공할 수 있는 온갖 재능을 다해 펼쳐졌다. 이렇게 해서 베네데토는 법의 선고가 내려지기도 전에 여론 속에서 영원히 단죄되었다.\n\n안드레아는 자신에게 잇달아 제기되는 혐의들에 아무런 주의도 기울이지 않았다. 그를 유심히 살피며, 의심할 바 없이 평소 즐겨 쓰던 온갖 심리적 기술을 그에게 시험하던 빌포르 씨는, 그 깊고도 그윽한 눈빛에도 불구하고 그의 눈을 내리깔게 하려 애썼으나 헛수고였다. 마침내 공소장 낭독이 끝났다.\n\n\"피고인, 성과 이름은?\" 재판장이 말했다.\n\n안드레아가 일어섰다.\n\n\"실례합니다, 재판장님.\" 그가 또렷한 목소리로 말했다. \"그런데 제가 따라갈 수 없는 방식으로 질문을 진행하시려는 것 같군요. 저는 통상적인 심문 형식에 예외를 두려는 생각이 있고, 그것은 차차 설명드리겠습니다. 그러니 부디 제가 다른 순서로 답하도록 허락해 주십시오. 아니면 아예 답하지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n놀란 재판장이 배심원들을 바라보았고, 배심원들은 다시 빌포르를 바라보았다. 좌중 전체가 크게 놀랐으나, 안드레아는 조금도 동요하지 않는 듯했다.\n\n\"나이는?\" 재판장이 말했다. \"이 질문에는 답하겠소?\"\n\n\"그 질문에도, 나머지 질문에도 답하겠습니다, 재판장님. 다만 차례가 되었을 때 말입니다.\"\n\n\"나이는?\" 재판장이 거듭 물었다.\n\n\"저는 스물한 살입니다. 아니, 정확히는 며칠 후에 그렇게 됩니다. 저는 1817년 9월 27일 밤에 태어났으니까요.\"\n\n뭔가 메모를 적느라 분주하던 빌포르 씨가 이 날짜가 언급되자 고개를 들었다.\n\n\"어디서 태어났소?\" 재판장이 계속 물었다.\n\n\"파리 근교 오퇴유에서요.\"\n\n빌포르 씨가 두 번째로 고개를 들고, 마치 메두사의 머리라도 응시하듯 베네데토를 바라보았다. 그러더니 얼굴이 새파랗게 질렸다. 베네데토로 말하자면, 고운 마직 손수건으로 우아하게 입술을 닦았다.\n\n\"직업은?\"\n\n\"처음에는 위조범이었습니다.\" 안드레아가 더없이 차분하게 대답했다. \"그다음에는 도둑이 되었고, 최근에는 살인자가 되었지요.\"\n\n좌중 곳곳에서 분노의 웅성거림이, 아니 폭풍이 터져 나왔다. 판사들마저 망연자실한 듯했고, 배심원들은 멋쟁이 신사한테서 나오리라고는 전혀 예상치 못한 그 냉소에 혐오의 기색을 드러냈다. 빌포르 씨는 처음에 핏기가 없다가 이내 붉어지고 화끈거리던 이마를 손으로 짚었다. 그러더니 갑자기 일어나 정신을 잃기라도 한 사람처럼 주위를 둘러보았다. 그는 바람이 필요했다.\n\n\"뭔가 찾으십니까, 검사님?\" 베네데토가 더없이 비위 맞추는 미소를 띠며 물었다.\n\n빌포르 씨는 아무 대답도 하지 않고, 의자에 다시 앉았다. 아니, 차라리 몸을 던지듯 주저앉았다.\n\n\"자, 그럼 피고인, 이제 이름을 말하겠소?\" 재판장이 말했다. \"그대가 자신의 죄목을 열거하고 분류한 그 잔인한 허세는, 도덕의 이름으로도, 인간에게 마땅히 바쳐야 할 존중을 위해서도, 본 법정으로부터 엄중한 질책을 받아 마땅하오. 그대는 이것을 명예의 문제로 여기는 듯한데, 어쩌면 그런 이유로 이름 밝히기를 미룬 것이겠지. 그 이름 앞에 이 모든 직함이 붙기를 바란 게요.\"\n\n\"참으로 놀랍습니다, 재판장님. 제 생각을 그토록 속속들이 읽어 내시다니요.\" 베네데토가 더없이 부드러운 목소리와 더없이 정중한 태도로 말했다. \"바로 그것이 제가 질문의 순서를 바꿔 달라고 청한 이유입니다.\"\n\n방청객의 놀라움은 절정에 달했다. 이제 피고인의 태도에는 기만도 허세도 없었다. 좌중은 이 불길한 서막에 뒤이어 놀라운 폭로가 따르리라는 것을 직감했다.\n\n\"좋소.\" 재판장이 말했다. \"이름은?\"\n\n\"제 이름은 말씀드릴 수 없습니다. 저도 모르니까요. 하지만 제 아버지의 이름은 압니다. 그것은 말씀드릴 수 있지요.\"\n\n고통스러운 현기증이 빌포르를 덮쳤다. 발작하듯 떨리는 손에 쥔 서류 위로 그의 얼굴에서 떨어진 굵은 식은땀 방울이 떨어졌다.\n\n\"아버지의 이름을 말하시오.\" 재판장이 말했다.\n\n그 광대한 좌중에서는 속삭임 하나, 숨소리 하나 들리지 않았다. 모두가 마음 졸이며 기다렸다.\n\n\"제 아버지는 검사입니다.\" 안드레아가 차분히 대답했다.\n\n\"검사라고?\" 재판장이 빌포르 씨의 얼굴에 번지는 동요는 알아차리지도 못한 채 망연자실해 말했다. \"검사라니?\"\n\n\"네. 그리고 그분의 이름을 알고 싶으시다면 말씀드리지요. 그분의 이름은 빌포르입니다.\"\n\n법정에 대한 존중의 마음에서 그토록 오래 억눌려 있던 폭발이, 이제 그 자리에 있던 모든 이들의 가슴에서 천둥처럼 터져 나왔다. 법정 자체도 방청객의 격정을 억누르려 하지 않았다. 베네데토를 향해 쏟아지는 외침과 욕설—그는 더없이 태연했다—거센 손짓, 헌병들의 움직임, 소란이 일 때면 으레 표면으로 떠오르는 군중 속 천한 무리의 비웃음, 이 모든 것이 문지기와 판사들이 정적을 되찾기까지 오 분간 이어졌다. 이 소동 한가운데서 재판장의 목소리가 외치는 것이 들렸다.\n\n\"피고인, 그대는 사법을 희롱하는 것인가? 그래서 감히 이 시대에도 유례없던 무질서의 본을 동료 시민들에게 보이려는 것인가?\"\n\n몇 사람이 의자에 반쯤 엎드려 있는 빌포르 씨에게로 황급히 다가가 위로와 격려, 그리고 열의와 동정의 말을 건넸다. 법정은 질서를 되찾았다. 다만 몇몇 사람들은 여전히 자리를 옮겨 다니며 서로 속삭였다. 어떤 부인이 방금 기절했다는 말이 돌았다. 사람들이 정신 들게 하는 약병을 건네주었고, 그녀는 정신을 차렸다. 소란이 이는 동안 안드레아는 미소 띤 얼굴을 좌중으로 돌렸다. 그러더니 한 손을 피고인석의 떡갈나무 난간에 짚고, 더없이 우아한 자세로 말했다.\n\n\"여러분, 저는 결코 법정을 모욕하거나 이 명예로운 좌중 앞에서 쓸데없는 소란을 일으킬 생각이 없었음을 분명히 말씀드립니다. 제 나이를 물으시기에 답했고, 어디서 태어났느냐 물으시기에 답했습니다. 이름을 물으셨지만, 부모가 저를 버렸기에 답할 수 없습니다. 그러나 제 이름은 가진 것이 없어 말할 수 없어도, 제 아버지의 이름은 말씀드릴 수 있습니다. 거듭 말하건대, 제 아버지의 이름은 빌포르 씨이고, 저는 그것을 입증할 준비가 되어 있습니다.\"\n\n그 젊은이의 태도에는 힘과 확신과 진실함이 있어, 소란을 잠재웠다. 모든 눈이 잠시 검사에게로 향했다. 그는 벼락이라도 맞아 시체로 변한 듯 미동도 없이 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"여러분.\" 안드레아가 목소리와 태도로 좌중을 침묵시키며 말했다. \"제가 한 말에 대해 증거와 설명을 드려야 마땅하겠지요.\"\n\n\"하지만.\" 격앙된 재판장이 말했다. \"그대는 스스로를 베네데토라 칭했고, 고아라고 했으며, 코르시카를 고향이라 주장하지 않았소.\"\n\n\"제가 방금 한 엄숙한 진술이 가로막히지 않도록 하기 위해 닥치는 대로 둘러댄 것입니다. 그렇게 하지 않았다면 분명 가로막혔을 테니까요. 다시 말씀드리지만, 저는 1817년 9월 27일 밤 오퇴유에서 태어났고, 검사 빌포르 씨의 아들입니다. 더 상세한 내용을 원하십니까? 말씀드리지요. 저는 퐁텐 거리 28번지, 붉은 다마스크 천이 드리워진 방에서 태어났습니다. 제 아버지는 어머니에게 제가 죽었다고 말하며 저를 두 팔에 안아 들었고, H와 N이라는 글자가 새겨진 보자기에 저를 싸서 정원으로 데려가 산 채로 묻었습니다.\"\n\n피고인의 자신감이 빌포르 씨의 공포에 비례해 커지는 것을 보자 좌중에 전율이 흘렀다.\n\n\"한데 그대는 이 모든 상세한 내막을 어떻게 알게 되었소?\" 재판장이 물었다.\n\n\"말씀드리지요, 재판장님. 제 아버지에게 복수를 맹세하고 오랫동안 그를 죽일 기회를 노리던 한 사내가, 그날 밤 아버지가 저를 묻은 그 정원에 잠입해 있었습니다. 그는 덤불 속에 몸을 숨기고 있었지요. 그는 제 아버지가 땅에 무언가를 묻는 것을 보고 그를 칼로 찔렀습니다. 그러고는 그 묻힌 것에 무슨 보물이라도 들어 있을까 싶어 땅을 파헤쳤고, 아직 살아 있는 저를 발견했습니다. 그 사내는 저를 기아 보호소로 데려갔고, 그곳에서 저는 37번으로 등록되었습니다. 석 달 뒤 한 여인이 저를 데리러 롤리아노에서 파리로 와, 저를 자기 아들이라고 주장하며 데려갔습니다. 그래서 보시다시피 저는 파리에서 태어났으나 코르시카에서 자랐습니다.\"\n\n잠시 침묵이 흘렀고, 그동안 정적이 어찌나 깊었던지 법정이 텅 빈 듯한 착각이 들 정도였다.\n\n\"계속하시오.\" 재판장이 말했다.\n\n\"분명 저는 저를 끔찍이 아껴 주던 그 선한 사람들 틈에서 행복하게 살 수도 있었습니다. 그러나 제 비뚤어진 천성이, 양어머니가 제 마음속에 심으려 애쓴 미덕들을 이겨 버렸습니다. 저는 점점 더 사악해져 마침내 범죄를 저질렀습니다. 어느 날 제가 저를 이토록 사악하게 만들고 이런 운명에 처하게 한 섭리를 저주하자, 양아버지가 제게 말했습니다. '신을 모독하지 마라, 가엾은 아이야. 그 죄는 네 아버지의 죄이지 네 죄가 아니다. 네가 죽으면 지옥에, 기적으로 살아남으면 비참에 너를 내던진 네 아버지의 죄란다.' 그 뒤로 저는 신을 모독하기를 그쳤으나, 제 아버지를 저주했습니다. 그래서 제가 여러분이 저를 책망하시는 그 말을 입에 올린 것이고, 그래서 제가 이 좌중 전체를 공포로 채운 것입니다. 제가 거기에 더해 또 다른 죄를 지었다면 저를 벌하십시오. 그러나 제가 태어난 날부터 제 운명이 슬프고 쓰라리고 한탄스러웠음을 인정하신다면, 저를 가엾이 여겨 주십시오.\"\n\n\"하지만 그대의 어머니는?\" 재판장이 물었다.\n\n\"제 어머니는 제가 죽은 줄 알았습니다. 그분에게는 죄가 없습니다. 저는 그분의 이름을 알고 싶지도 않았고, 지금도 모릅니다.\"\n\n바로 그때 흐느낌으로 끝맺는 날카로운 비명이 군중 한가운데서 터져 나왔다. 사람들이 둘러싼 그 부인, 앞서 기절했던 그 부인이 이제 격렬한 발작을 일으킨 것이었다. 그녀가 법정 밖으로 실려 나갈 때 얼굴을 가리고 있던 두꺼운 베일이 흘러내렸고, 사람들은 그녀가 당글라르 부인임을 알아보았다. 만신창이가 된 신경에도, 귓속에서 울리는 윙윙거림에도, 머릿속을 미치게 하는 혼란에도 불구하고, 빌포르는 그녀를 알아보고 일어섰다.\n\n\"증거를, 증거를 대시오!\" 재판장이 말했다. \"이 끔찍한 이야기의 얼개는 더없이 명백한 증거로 뒷받침되어야 함을 명심하시오.\"\n\n\"증거요?\" 베네데토가 웃으며 말했다. \"증거를 원하십니까?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 빌포르 씨를 보십시오. 그런 다음 제게 증거를 청하십시오.\"\n\n모두가 검사 쪽으로 고개를 돌렸다. 이제 자신에게만 못 박힌 만인의 시선을 견딜 수 없었던 그는, 머리카락이 헝클어지고 손톱자국이 패인 얼굴로 비틀거리며 법정 한가운데로 나아갔다. 좌중 전체가 길게 놀라움의 웅성거림을 토해 냈다.\n\n\"아버지.\" 베네데토가 말했다. \"사람들이 제게 증거를 청합니다. 제가 그것을 대기를 바라십니까?\"\n\n\"아니, 아니, 그럴 필요 없다.\" 빌포르 씨가 쉰 목소리로 더듬거렸다. \"아니, 그럴 필요 없어!\"\n\n\"필요 없다니?\" 재판장이 외쳤다. \"그게 무슨 말이오?\"\n\n\"저를 짓누르는 이 죽음 같은 무게에 맞서 싸우는 일이 불가능하다는 뜻입니다. 여러분, 저는 제가 복수하시는 신의 손안에 있음을 압니다! 증거는 필요 없습니다. 이 젊은이와 관련된 모든 것이 사실입니다.\"\n\n자연의 어떤 무서운 현상에 앞서는 듯한, 음울하고 무거운 침묵이 좌중에 감돌았고, 사람들은 경악하여 몸을 떨었다.\n\n\"아니, 빌포르 씨.\" 재판장이 외쳤다. \"환각에 굴복하시는 겁니까? 이미 제정신이 아니신 게요? 이 기이하고 뜻밖이며 끔찍한 고발이 그대의 이성을 어지럽혔구려. 자, 정신을 차리시오.\"\n\n검사는 고개를 떨구었다. 그의 이는 격렬한 열병에 시달리는 사람처럼 딱딱 부딪쳤으나, 그럼에도 그의 얼굴은 죽은 사람처럼 창백했다.\n\n\"저는 온전히 제정신입니다, 재판장님.\" 그가 말했다. \"짐작하시다시피 고통받는 것은 제 몸뿐입니다. 저는 이 젊은이가 제게 제기한 모든 것에 대해 제가 유죄임을 인정하며, 지금 이 시각부터 저를 이어받을 검사의 권한 아래 두겠습니다.\"\n\n그리고 그는 쉬고 메인 목소리로 이 말을 내뱉으며, 문지기가 기계적으로 열어 준 문을 향해 비틀비틀 걸어갔다. 좌중 전체가 그 폭로와 자백에 너무도 놀라 말을 잃었다. 지난 보름간 파리 사교계가 예상하던 것과는 너무도 다른 파국을 빚어낸 폭로요 자백이었다.\n\n\"자.\" 보샹이 말했다. \"이래도 사람들이 연극은 작위적이라고 할 수 있을까!\"\n\n\"맹세코,\" 샤토르노가 말했다. \"나라면 차라리 모르세르 씨처럼 생을 끝내겠네. 권총 한 발이 이 파국에 비하면 아주 유쾌해 보이는걸.\"\n\n\"게다가 그건 적어도 죽기라도 하지.\" 보샹이 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 내가 그 사람 딸과 결혼할 생각까지 했었다니.\" 드브레가 말했다. \"잘 죽었어, 가엾은 아가씨!\"\n\n\"여러분, 본 공판을 휴정합니다.\" 재판장이 말했다. \"추가 수사가 진행될 것이며, 이 사건은 다음 회기에 다른 판사가 심리할 것입니다.\"\n\n안드레아로 말하자면, 그 어느 때보다 차분하고 흥미로운 모습으로, 자기도 모르게 그에게 얼마간 예우를 차리는 헌병들의 호위를 받으며 법정을 나섰다.\n\n\"그래, 이걸 어떻게 보나, 여보게?\" 드브레가 헌병 부사관의 손에 루이 금화 한 닢을 슬쩍 쥐여 주며 물었다.\n\n\"정상 참작이 될 겁니다.\" 그가 대답했다.\n\n# Chapter 111. Expiation\n\n군중이 빽빽이 들어차 있었지만, 빌포르 씨는 그들이 자기 앞에서 길을 열어 주는 것을 보았다. 커다란 고통에는 사람을 숙연하게 만드는 무언가가 있어서, 아무리 험한 시절이라도 군중이 처음 느끼는 감정은 대개 큰 비극에 빠진 사람을 동정하는 것이었다. 많은 이들이 소란 속에서 목숨을 잃었지만, 재판을 받는 동안에는 죄인조차 모욕당하는 일이 드물었다. 그렇게 빌포르는 구경꾼과 법원 관리들 사이를 지나 자리를 떴다. 그는 스스로 죄를 인정했으나, 그의 슬픔이 그를 지켜 주었다. 사람들이 본능으로 이해하면서도 이성으로는 설명할 수 없는 상황이 있다. 그런 경우 가장 위대한 시인은 가장 자연스럽고 격렬한 슬픔의 토로를 입에 담는 사람이다. 그 쓰라린 절규를 듣는 이들은 한 편의 시 전체를 듣는 것만큼이나 깊은 인상을 받으며, 고통받는 자가 진실할 때 그 토로를 숭고하다 여기는 것은 옳다.\n\n빌포르가 법원을 떠날 때의 망연자실한 상태를 묘사하기란 어려운 일일 것이다. 모든 맥박이 열병처럼 들끓었고, 모든 신경이 팽팽히 당겨졌으며, 모든 혈관이 부풀어 올랐고, 몸의 각 부분이 저마다 따로 고통을 겪는 듯하여 그의 괴로움은 천 배로 늘어났다. 그는 습관에 이끌려 복도를 지나갔다. 그는 법복을 벗어 던졌는데, 예법을 따라서가 아니라 그것이 견딜 수 없는 짐, 고문을 멈추지 않는 진정한 네소스의 옷이었기 때문이다. 도핀 거리까지 비틀거리며 다다른 그는 자신의 마차를 발견했고, 직접 문을 열어 잠든 마부를 깨운 뒤, 방석 위에 몸을 던지고 생토노레 변두리 쪽을 가리켰다. 마차가 달리기 시작했다.\n\n무너진 운명의 무게가 갑자기 그를 짓누르는 듯했다. 그는 그 결과를 내다볼 수 없었다. 그는 이미 익숙한 사태를 그저 마주할 뿐인 굳어 버린 죄인의 무심함으로 미래를 바라볼 수 없었다.\n\n하느님은 여전히 그의 마음속에 계셨다. \"하느님.\" 그가 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지도 모른 채 중얼거렸다. \"하느님, 하느님!\" 자신을 짓밟은 사건 너머에서 그는 하느님의 손을 보았다. 마차는 빠르게 굴러갔다. 빌포르는 방석 위에서 안절부절못하며 몸을 뒤척이다가 무언가가 자기 몸에 눌리는 것을 느꼈다. 그는 손을 뻗어 그 물건을 치우려 했다. 빌포르 부인이 마차에 두고 간 부채였다. 이 부채가 번개처럼 그의 머릿속을 스치는 기억을 일깨웠다. 그는 아내를 떠올렸다.\n\n\"아!\" 그가 벌겋게 달군 쇠가 심장을 꿰뚫는 듯 외쳤다.\n\n지난 한 시간 동안 그의 머릿속에는 오직 자신의 죄만이 떠올랐다. 이제 그에 못지않게 끔찍한 또 다른 것이 불쑥 모습을 드러냈다. 그의 아내! 그는 방금 아내에게 가차 없는 재판관 노릇을 했고, 그녀에게 사형을 선고했다. 그리고 그녀는, 회한에 짓눌리고 공포에 질렸으며 흠 하나 없는 그의 미덕이 토해 낸 웅변이 안긴 수치심에 휩싸인 그녀는, 가엾고 연약한 한 여인으로서, 그의 절대적이고 지고한 의지에 맞서 자신을 변호할 도움도 힘도 없는 그녀는, 어쩌면 바로 그 순간에 죽을 준비를 하고 있을지도 몰랐다!\n\n그녀가 선고를 받은 지 한 시간이 흘렀다. 지금 이 순간 그녀는 틀림없이 자신의 모든 죄를 기억 속에서 떠올리고 있을 것이며, 자신의 죄에 대해 용서를 빌고 있을 것이다. 어쩌면 정숙한 남편에게 용서를 간청하는 편지를, 자신의 죽음으로 사들이는 그 용서를 청하는 편지를 쓰고 있을지도 몰랐다! 빌포르는 다시 고뇌와 절망에 신음했다.\n\n\"아.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"저 여자는 오직 나와 함께한 탓에 죄인이 된 것이다! 내가 죄의 병균을 지니고 다녔고, 그녀는 그것을 발진티푸스나 콜레라나 페스트처럼 옮은 것이다! 그런데도 나는 그녀를 벌했다. 감히 그녀에게 말했다. 내가, '회개하고 죽으라!'고. 아니, 안 된다, 그녀는 죽어서는 안 된다. 그녀는 살 것이다, 나와 함께. 우리는 파리를 떠나 땅끝까지 달아날 것이다. 나는 그녀에게 단두대를 들먹였구나. 오, 하느님, 그것이 나도 기다리고 있다는 걸 잊었다니! 내가 어찌 그런 말을 입에 담을 수 있었던가? 그래, 우리는 달아날 것이다. 나는 그녀에게 모든 것을 고백할 것이다. 나도 죄를 저질렀노라고 날마다 말할 것이다! 오, 이 무슨 짝인가, 호랑이와 뱀이라니. 나 같은 자에게 어울리는 아내로다! 내 치욕이 그녀의 치욕을 덜어 주도록, 그녀는 살아야만 한다.\"\n\n그러고 빌포르는 마차 앞쪽 창을 거칠게 열어젖혔다.\n\n\"더 빨리, 더 빨리!\" 그가 마부의 등골을 오싹하게 하는 목소리로 외쳤다. 말들은 공포에 내몰려 집을 향해 날듯이 내달렸다.\n\n\"그래, 그래.\" 빌포르가 집에 다가가며 거듭 말했다. \"그래, 저 여자는 살아야 한다. 그녀는 회개하고, 내 아들을 길러야 한다. 그 죽지 않는 늙은이를 빼면, 무너진 우리 집안에서 유일하게 살아남은 그 아이를. 그녀는 그 아이를 사랑한다. 그녀가 이 죄들을 저지른 것도 그 아이를 위해서였다. 제 자식을 사랑하는 어머니의 마음을 누그러뜨리는 일을 두고 결코 절망해서는 안 되는 법이다. 그녀는 회개할 것이고, 그녀가 죄를 지었다는 사실을 아무도 모를 것이다. 우리 집에서 벌어진 일들은 지금이야 세상 사람들의 입에 오르내리지만, 세월이 흐르면 잊힐 것이다. 설령 몇몇 적들이 끝내 그것을 기억하겠다고 고집한다면, 그렇다면 나는 그것을 내 죄목 명단에 더하면 그만이다. 하나, 둘, 아니 셋쯤 더 늘어난들 무슨 상관이랴? 내 아내와 아들은 보물을 지닌 채 이 심연에서 빠져나갈 것이다. 그녀는 살아갈 것이고, 어쩌면 행복할 수도 있을 것이다. 그녀의 모든 사랑이 쏠린 그 아이가 그녀 곁에 있을 테니. 나는 선한 일을 한 셈이 될 것이고, 내 마음도 한결 가벼워질 것이다.\"\n\n그러자 검사는 얼마 만인지 모르게 한결 편하게 숨을 쉬었다.\n\n마차가 집 문 앞에 멈췄다. 빌포르는 마차에서 뛰어내렸고, 하인들이 그가 일찍 돌아온 것에 놀라는 것을 알아챘다. 그들의 얼굴에서 다른 표정은 읽을 수 없었다. 둘 중 누구도 그에게 말을 걸지 않았다. 그저 늘 그렇듯이 그가 지나가도록 옆으로 비켜설 뿐, 그 이상은 없었다. 누아르티에 씨의 방을 지날 때, 그는 반쯤 열린 문틈으로 두 사람의 형상을 보았다. 그러나 누가 아버지를 찾아왔는지 알고 싶은 호기심은 일지 않았다. 불안이 그를 더 멀리 떠밀었다.\n\n\"자.\" 아내의 방으로 이어지는 계단을 오르며 그가 말했다. \"여기는 아무것도 변한 게 없군.\"\n\n그러고는 층계참의 문을 닫았다.\n\n\"아무도 우리를 방해해서는 안 된다.\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 그녀에게 거리낌 없이 말하고, 내 죄를 고하고, 이렇게 말해야 한다.\" 그는 문에 다가가 수정 손잡이를 만졌고, 손잡이는 그의 손길에 순순히 돌아갔다. \"잠겨 있지 않군.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"잘됐어.\"\n\n그러고 그는 에두아르가 잠든 작은 방으로 들어섰다. 그 아이는 낮에는 학교에 다녔지만, 어머니는 밤에 아이가 자기 곁을 떠나 있는 것을 견디지 못했다. 빌포르의 눈길이 한 번에 방 안을 훑었다.\n\n\"여기엔 없군.\" 그가 말했다. \"분명 침실에 있겠지.\" 그는 문으로 달려갔으나, 빗장이 걸려 있는 것을 알고는 몸서리치며 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"엘로이즈!\" 그가 외쳤다. 가구 하나가 옮겨지는 소리가 들리는 것 같았다.\n\n\"엘로이즈!\" 그가 다시 외쳤다.\n\n\"누구세요?\" 그가 찾는 여인의 목소리가 대답했다. 그 목소리가 여느 때보다 가냘프게 느껴졌다.\n\n\"문 열어!\" 빌포르가 외쳤다. \"열어, 나야.\"\n\n그러나 이 요청에도, 그것이 토해 낸 고뇌에 찬 목소리에도, 문은 닫힌 채로였다. 빌포르가 힘껏 내리쳐 문을 부수었다. 자신의 내실로 통하는 방 입구에 빌포르 부인이 꼿꼿이 서 있었다. 창백하게, 얼굴은 일그러지고, 두 눈은 끔찍하게 번득였다.\n\n\"엘로이즈, 엘로이즈!\" 그가 말했다. \"무슨 일이오? 말을 해!\" 젊은 여인이 뻣뻣하게 굳은 하얀 두 손을 그에게 내밀었다.\n\n\"끝났어요, 여보.\" 그녀가 목을 찢어 내는 듯한 그르렁거리는 소리로 말했다. \"더 무엇을 바라세요?\" 그러고는 마룻바닥에 길게 쓰러졌다.\n\n빌포르가 그녀에게 달려가 손을 붙잡았다. 그 손은 황금 마개가 달린 수정 병을 경련하듯 움켜쥐고 있었다. 빌포르 부인은 죽어 있었다. 빌포르는 공포에 미쳐 문턱까지 뒷걸음질 치며 시신에 눈을 박았다.\n\n\"내 아들!\" 그가 갑자기 외쳤다. \"내 아들은 어디 있나? 에두아르, 에두아르!\" 그러고는 \"에두아르, 에두아르!\" 하고 계속 외치며 방에서 뛰쳐나갔다. 그 이름이 어찌나 고뇌에 찬 목소리로 불렸던지 하인들이 달려왔다.\n\n\"내 아들 어디 있나?\" 빌포르가 물었다. \"그 아이를 이 집에서 데리고 나가, 보지 못하게……\"\n\n\"에두아르 도련님은 아래층에 안 계십니다, 나리.\" 하인이 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 정원에서 놀고 있겠군. 가서 봐.\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 나리. 빌포르 부인께서 반 시간 전에 도련님을 부르셨습니다. 도련님은 마님 방으로 들어가셨고, 그 뒤로 아래층에 내려오지 않으셨습니다.\"\n\n빌포르의 이마에 식은땀이 솟구쳤다. 다리가 후들거렸고, 생각들이 고장 난 시계의 톱니바퀴처럼 머릿속에서 미친 듯이 맴돌았다.\n\n\"빌포르 부인 방으로?\" 그가 중얼거리며 한 손으로 이마를 닦고 다른 손으로 벽을 짚어 몸을 지탱한 채 천천히 되돌아갔다. 방으로 들어가려면 불행한 아내의 시신을 다시 보아야 했다. 에두아르를 부르려면 이제 무덤처럼 변해 버린 그 방의 메아리를 다시 깨워야 했다. 입을 여는 것은 무덤의 정적을 침범하는 일 같았다. 그의 혀가 입안에서 마비되었다.\n\n\"에두아르!\" 그가 더듬거렸다. \"에두아르!\"\n\n아이는 대답하지 않았다. 그렇다면 어머니의 방에 들어가서 그 뒤로 나오지 않았다면, 대체 어디에 있을 수 있단 말인가? 그가 앞으로 나아갔다. 빌포르 부인의 시신이 에두아르가 있을 방으로 통하는 문간을 가로질러 길게 뻗어 있었다. 그 번득이는 두 눈은 문턱을 지키고 있는 듯했고, 입술에는 끔찍하고 알 수 없는 조소의 흔적이 어려 있었다. 열린 문 너머로 내실의 일부가 보였다. 곧추선 피아노와 푸른 새틴 소파가 있었다. 빌포르는 두세 걸음 앞으로 나아갔고, 자신의 아이가 소파에 누워 있는 것을 보았다. 틀림없이 잠든 것이었다. 불행한 사내는 기쁨의 탄성을 질렀다. 한 줄기 빛이 절망과 어둠의 심연을 뚫고 비쳐 드는 듯했다. 그는 시신을 넘어가 내실로 들어가서 아이를 품에 안고 멀리, 멀리 달아나기만 하면 되었다.\n\n빌포르는 더 이상 문명인이 아니었다. 그는 상처를 입고 죽어 가며 상처 속에서 이를 가는 호랑이였다. 그는 이제 현실이 아니라 환영을 두려워했다. 그는 불타는 화로라도 되는 양 시신을 뛰어넘었다. 그는 아이를 품에 안고 끌어안고 흔들며 불렀지만, 아이는 아무 대답이 없었다. 그는 불타는 입술을 그 뺨에 갖다 댔으나, 그 뺨은 얼음처럼 차갑고 창백했다. 그는 뻣뻣하게 굳은 팔다리를 만져 보았고, 손을 심장에 갖다 댔으나, 심장은 더 이상 뛰지 않았다. 아이는 죽어 있었다.\n\n접힌 종이 한 장이 에두아르의 가슴에서 떨어졌다. 빌포르는 벼락을 맞은 듯 무릎을 꿇었다. 아이가 그의 품에서 떨어져 제 어머니 곁의 마룻바닥으로 굴렀다. 그는 종이를 집어 들었고, 아내의 글씨를 알아본 뒤 그 내용을 재빨리 눈으로 훑었다. 거기에는 이렇게 적혀 있었다.\n\n\"제가 좋은 어머니였다는 것을 당신은 아실 거예요. 제 아들을 위해 죄인이 되었으니까요. 좋은 어머니는 아들 없이 떠날 수 없는 법이지요.\"\n\n빌포르는 자신의 눈을 믿을 수 없었다. 자신의 이성을 믿을 수 없었다. 그는 아이의 시신을 향해 몸을 끌고 가, 죽은 새끼를 들여다보는 암사자처럼 그것을 살폈다. 그러더니 가슴에서 찢어지는 듯한 절규가 터져 나왔고, 그가 외쳤다.\n\n\"여전히 하느님의 손이로구나.\"\n\n두 희생자가 함께 있다는 사실이 그를 두렵게 했다. 그는 두 구의 시신만이 함께하는 고독을 견딜 수 없었다. 그때까지 그는 분노로, 정신력으로, 절망으로, 거인족을 하늘로 기어오르게 하고 아이아스로 하여금 신들에게 맞서게 했던 그 지고한 고뇌로 버텨 왔다. 이제 그는 슬픔의 무게에 고개를 숙인 채 일어나, 축축하게 헝클어진 머리카락을 흔들며, 누구에게도 연민을 느껴 본 적 없던 그가 아버지를 찾아가기로 마음먹었다. 자신의 불행을 털어놓을 누군가를, 그 곁에서 울 수 있는 누군가를 갖기 위해서였다.\n\n그는 우리에게 익숙한 그 작은 계단을 내려가 누아르티에의 방으로 들어섰다. 노인은 여느 때처럼 차갑고 차분해 보이는 부소니 신부의 말에, 자신의 병약함이 허락하는 한 정성껏 귀를 기울이고 있는 듯했다. 빌포르는 신부를 알아보고 손으로 이마를 쓸었다. 과거가, 다른 어떤 것보다 더 거세게 분노로 거품을 일으키는 그런 파도처럼 그에게 밀려왔다.\n\n그는 오퇴유에서의 만찬 뒤에 자신이 신부를 찾아갔던 일과, 발랑틴이 죽던 날 신부가 직접 자기 집을 방문했던 일을 떠올렸다.\n\n\"당신이 여기에!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"그렇다면 당신은 죽음을 호위하는 일이 아니면 결코 모습을 드러내지 않는 거요?\"\n\n부소니가 몸을 돌렸고, 재판관의 얼굴에 드러난 흥분과 그 두 눈의 사나운 광채를 보고는, 중죄 재판에서 진상이 폭로되었음을 알아챘다. 그러나 그 이상은 알지 못했다.\n\n\"나는 당신 딸의 시신 곁에서 기도하러 왔소.\"\n\n\"그런데 지금은 어찌하여 여기 있는 거요?\"\n\n\"당신이 그 빚을 충분히 갚았다는 것을, 그리고 이 순간부터 내가 당신을 용서하듯 하느님께도 당신을 용서해 주십사 기도하겠다는 것을 말하러 왔소.\"\n\n\"맙소사!\" 빌포르가 겁에 질려 뒷걸음질 치며 외쳤다. \"분명 저것은 부소니 신부의 목소리가 아니야!\"\n\n\"그렇소!\" 신부가 가발을 벗어 던지고 고개를 흔들자, 더는 묶이지 않은 그의 머리카락이 사내다운 얼굴 둘레로 검은 물결처럼 흘러내렸다.\n\n\"저것은 몬테크리스토 백작의 얼굴이다!\" 검사가 초췌한 표정으로 외쳤다.\n\n\"꼭 맞는 건 아니오, 검사 양반. 더 거슬러 올라가야 하오.\"\n\n\"저 목소리, 저 목소리! 내가 저것을 처음 들은 게 어디였더라?\"\n\n\"당신은 그 목소리를 이십삼 년 전 마르세유에서, 생메랑 양과 결혼하던 날 처음 들었소. 당신의 서류를 들춰 보시오.\"\n\n\"당신은 부소니가 아니고, 몬테크리스토도 아니오? 오, 맙소사! 그렇다면 당신은 무슨 은밀하고 무자비한, 죽음을 부르는 원수로구나! 내가 마르세유에서 어떤 식으로든 당신에게 몹쓸 짓을 했음이 틀림없어. 오, 화로다, 내게 화로다!\"\n\n\"그렇소, 이제 제대로 길을 찾았소.\" 백작이 넓은 가슴 위로 팔짱을 끼며 말했다. \"찾으시오, 찾으시오!\"\n\n\"하지만 내가 당신에게 무슨 짓을 했단 말이오?\" 빌포르가 외쳤다. 그의 정신은 꿈도 현실도 아닌 그 안개 속에서 이성과 광기 사이를 오가고 있었다. \"내가 당신에게 무슨 짓을 했소? 어서 말하시오! 말을 해!\"\n\n\"당신은 나를 끔찍하고 지루한 죽음으로 몰아넣었소. 당신은 내 아버지를 죽였소. 당신은 내게서 자유를, 사랑을, 행복을 빼앗았소.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 당신은 누구요? 당신은 누구냐고?\"\n\n\"나는 당신이 이프성 지하 감옥에 파묻어 버린 어느 가엾은 자의 망령이오. 그 망령이 마침내 무덤에서 나왔을 때 하느님께서 그에게 몬테크리스토 백작의 모습을 주셨고, 황금과 다이아몬드로 그를 부유하게 하셨으며, 그를 당신에게로 이끄셨소!\"\n\n\"아, 알겠다, 너를 알아보겠어!\" 국왕의 검사가 외쳤다. \"너는……\"\n\n\"나는 에드몽 당테스다!\"\n\n\"네가 에드몽 당테스로구나.\" 빌포르가 백작의 손목을 움켜쥐며 외쳤다. \"그렇다면 이리로 와!\"\n\n그러고는 몬테크리스토를 계단 위로 끌고 올라갔다. 백작은 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 알지 못한 채 어리둥절하여 그를 따라가며, 무언가 새로운 참사를 예감했다.\n\n\"여기다, 에드몽 당테스!\" 그가 아내와 아이의 시신을 가리키며 말했다. \"봐라, 이만하면 복수를 톡톡히 한 거냐?\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 이 끔찍한 광경에 창백해졌다. 그는 자신이 복수의 경계를 넘어섰음을, 그리고 더는 \"하느님은 나를 위하시고 나와 함께하신다\"고 말할 수 없음을 느꼈다. 그는 형언할 수 없는 고뇌의 표정으로 아이의 시신에 몸을 던져, 그 눈을 다시 열어 보고 맥을 짚어 본 뒤, 아이를 안고 발랑틴의 방으로 달려가 문을 이중으로 잠가 버렸다.\n\n\"내 아이!\" 빌포르가 외쳤다. \"저자가 내 아이의 시신을 데려간다! 오, 저주를, 화를, 죽음을 너에게!\"\n\n그는 몬테크리스토를 뒤쫓으려 했다. 그러나 꿈속에서처럼 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 움직일 수 없었다. 그의 두 눈은 눈구멍 밖으로 튀어나올 듯 번득였고, 손톱이 피로 물들 때까지 자신의 가슴 살을 움켜쥐었으며, 관자놀이의 혈관이 그 좁은 경계를 터뜨리고 살아 있는 불길로 뇌를 적셔 버리기라도 할 듯 부풀어 끓어올랐다. 이것이 몇 분간 이어지더니, 마침내 무시무시한 이성의 전복이 완성되었다. 그러자 그는 커다란 비명을 지르고 이어서 웃음을 터뜨리며 계단을 뛰어 내려갔다.\n\n십오 분 뒤, 발랑틴 방의 문이 열리고 몬테크리스토가 다시 나타났다. 창백하게, 흐릿한 눈빛으로, 무거운 마음으로, 평소 그토록 차분하고 평온하던 그 얼굴의 고귀한 모든 윤곽이 슬픔에 뒤덮여 있었다. 그의 품에는 어떤 솜씨로도 되살릴 수 없었던 아이가 안겨 있었다. 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고, 그는 아이를 경건하게 제 어머니 곁에, 머리를 어머니의 가슴에 기대게 하여 뉘었다. 그러고는 일어나 밖으로 나갔고, 계단에서 하인 하나를 만나 물었다.\n\n\"빌포르 씨는 어디 계신가?\"\n\n하인은 대답 대신 정원을 가리켰다. 몬테크리스토는 계단을 뛰어 내려가, 가리켜진 자리로 다가가 보니, 빌포르가 하인들에게 둘러싸인 채 손에 삽을 들고 미친 듯이 땅을 파고 있었다.\n\n\"여기가 아니야!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"여기가 아니라고!\"\n\n그러고는 더 멀리 자리를 옮겨 다시 파기 시작했다.\n\n몬테크리스토가 그에게 다가가 거의 겸손하다 할 만한 표정으로 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"검사 양반, 당신은 정말로 아들을 잃었소. 하지만……\"\n\n빌포르가 그의 말을 가로막았다. 그는 듣지도, 알아듣지도 못했다.\n\n\"오, 나는 그것을 찾아낼 거야.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"당신은 그 아이가 여기 없는 척하지만, 나는 그를 찾아낼 거야, 영원히 파헤치는 한이 있더라도!\"\n\n몬테크리스토가 공포에 질려 물러섰다.\n\n\"오.\" 그가 말했다. \"그가 미쳤구나!\" 그러고는 저주받은 집의 벽들이 자기 주위로 무너져 내리기라도 할까 두려운 듯 거리로 뛰쳐나갔다. 자신이 행한 일을 행할 권리가 자신에게 있었는지 처음으로 의심하면서. \"오, 이만하면 됐다, 이만하면 됐어.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"마지막 하나만은 내가 구하게 해 다오.\" 집에 들어서니 모렐이 있었다. 그는 무덤으로 돌아가라는 하늘의 명령을 기다리는 유령처럼 서성이고 있었다.\n\n\"채비하게, 막시밀리앙.\" 그가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"우리는 내일 파리를 떠난다.\"\n\n\"여기서 더 하실 일이 없으신가요?\" 모렐이 물었다.\n\n\"없네.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"내가 이미 너무 많은 일을 한 것이 아니기를, 하느님께 빌 뿐이지.\"\n\n이튿날 그들은 정말로 떠났다. 바티스탱 한 사람만이 동행했다. 하이데가 알리를 데려갔고, 베르투치오는 누아르티에 곁에 남았다.\n\n# Chapter 112. The Departure\n\n최근에 일어난 사건들이 파리 전역의 화젯거리였다. 에마뉘엘과 그의 아내는 메슬레가의 작은 아파트에서 모르세르, 당글라르, 빌포르에게 잇따라 닥친, 갑작스럽고 더없이 뜻밖이었던 세 차례의 재앙을 두고 당연한 놀라움으로 이야기를 나누었다. 그 집을 찾아온 막시밀리앙은 두 사람의 대화에 귀를 기울이는 듯, 아니 그저 자리만 지킨 채 평소의 무기력에 잠겨 있었다.\n\n\"정말이지,\" 쥘리가 말했다. \"에마뉘엘, 그런 생각이 들지 않아요? 어제까지만 해도 그토록 부유하고 행복했던 저 사람들이, 번영에 취한 나머지 한 가지를 잊고 있었던 거예요. 페로의 이야기에 나오는 못된 요정들처럼, 결혼식이나 세례식에 청하지도 않았는데 제멋대로 나타나는 그런 악령이 자기들 머리 위를 맴돌고 있다는 걸요. 그러다 어느 순간 그 악령이 불쑥 모습을 드러내, 자기를 잊고 소홀히 한 죄를 갚으려 한 게 아닐까요?\"\n\n\"이 무슨 끔찍한 불행인가!\" 에마뉘엘이 모르세르와 당글라르를 떠올리며 말했다.\n\n\"이 무슨 무서운 고통인지!\" 쥘리가 발랑틴을 떠올리며 말했다. 그러나 여자다운 섬세함으로, 동생 앞에서 그 이름을 입에 올리지는 않았다.\n\n\"신께서 그 가혹한 일격을 내리셨다면,\" 에마뉘엘이 말했다. \"그분의 크나큰 자비로도 저 사람들의 지난 삶에서 그 무서운 형벌을 덜어 줄 만한 점을 단 하나도 찾지 못하셨기 때문일 거요.\"\n\n\"너무 성급하게 단정하는 거 아니에요, 에마뉘엘?\" 쥘리가 말했다. \"예전에 아버지가 권총을 손에 쥐고 막 스스로 목숨을 끊으려 하셨을 때, 만약 그때 누군가가 '이 사람은 이런 불행을 당해도 싸다'라고 했다면, 그 사람이 잘못 본 게 아니었을까요?\"\n\n\"그렇지. 하지만 당신 아버님은 무너지도록 내버려 두어지지 않았소. 그분께 막 떨어지려던 죽음의 손길을 멈추도록, 한 존재가 보내졌으니까.\"\n\n에마뉘엘이 이 말을 채 끝내기도 전에 종소리가 들렸다. 손님이 찾아왔음을 알리는, 문지기가 보내는 익숙한 신호였다. 거의 같은 순간에 문이 열렸고, 몬테크리스토 백작이 문턱에 모습을 드러냈다. 젊은 부부는 기쁨의 탄성을 질렀다. 막시밀리앙은 고개를 들었으나, 곧바로 다시 떨구었다.\n\n\"막시밀리앙,\" 백작이 말했다. 자신의 등장이 이 작은 무리에게 저마다 다른 인상을 불러일으킨 것을 알아채지 못한 듯한 태도였다. \"자네를 데리러 왔네.\"\n\n\"저를 데리러요?\" 모렐이 꿈에서 깨어나는 사람처럼 되물었다.\n\n\"그래.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"내가 자네를 데려가기로 하지 않았나. 어제 떠날 채비를 하라고 이르지 않았던가?\"\n\n\"준비됐습니다.\" 막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"마침 누님 내외에게 작별 인사를 하러 온 참이었습니다.\"\n\n\"어디로 가시는 건가요, 백작님?\" 쥘리가 물었다.\n\n\"우선은 마르세유로 갑니다, 부인.\"\n\n\"마르세유로요!\" 젊은 부부가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그렇습니다. 동생분을 함께 데려가겠습니다.\"\n\n\"아, 백작님.\" 쥘리가 말했다. \"이 아이의 우울을 말끔히 고쳐서 돌려보내 주실 건가요?\" 모렐은 얼굴에 드러난 당혹감을 감추려 고개를 돌렸다.\n\n\"그러니까 부인도 동생분이 행복하지 않다는 걸 아시는군요?\" 백작이 말했다.\n\n\"네.\" 젊은 부인이 대답했다. \"그리고 이 아이가 우리 집을 그저 따분하게만 여기는 게 아닐까 무척 걱정스러워요.\"\n\n\"제가 동생분의 기분을 풀어 드리겠습니다.\" 백작이 대답했다.\n\n\"동행할 준비가 됐습니다, 백작님.\" 막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"안녕히 계세요, 다정한 분들! 에마뉘엘 형님, 쥘리 누님, 안녕히!\"\n\n\"안녕히라니?\" 쥘리가 외쳤다. \"이렇게 떠난다고? 이렇게 갑자기, 여행 채비도 없이, 여권조차 없이?\"\n\n\"공연한 지체는 이별의 슬픔만 키울 뿐입니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"막시밀리앙은 필요한 건 다 챙겼을 겁니다. 적어도 제가 그렇게 하라고 일러 두었으니까요.\"\n\n\"여권도 있고, 옷가지도 다 꾸려 두었습니다.\" 모렐이 차분하면서도 침울한 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"좋아.\" 몬테크리스토가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"이렇게 척척 채비를 갖춘 걸 보니, 잘 단련된 군인의 절도가 느껴지는군.\"\n\n\"그런데 이렇게 한순간에 우리 곁을 떠나신다고요?\" 쥘리가 말했다. \"단 하루도, 아니 출발 전에 단 한 시간도 우리에게 내주지 않으시는 건가요?\"\n\n\"마차가 문 앞에 와 있습니다, 부인. 닷새 안에 로마에 닿아야 하거든요.\"\n\n\"하지만 막시밀리앙이 로마까지 가는 건가요?\" 에마뉘엘이 외쳤다.\n\n\"백작님이 데려가시는 곳이라면 어디든 갑니다.\" 모렐이 슬픔 가득한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"앞으로 한 달간은 백작님의 명을 따르기로 했습니다.\"\n\n\"오, 세상에, 말투가 어쩜 저렇게 이상한지요, 백작님!\" 쥘리가 말했다.\n\n\"막시밀리앙은 저와 함께 갑니다.\" 백작이 더없이 다정하고 설득력 있는 태도로 말했다. \"그러니 동생분 일로 마음 졸이지 마십시오.\"\n\n\"다시 한번 안녕, 사랑하는 누님. 에마뉘엘 형님, 안녕히!\" 모렐이 거듭 인사했다.\n\n\"저 무심하고 덤덤한 태도가 가슴을 저미네요.\" 쥘리가 말했다. \"아, 막시밀리앙, 막시밀리앙, 너 분명히 우리한테 뭔가 숨기고 있구나.\"\n\n\"천만에요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"동생분이 명랑하게, 웃으며, 기쁨에 차서 부인 곁으로 돌아오는 걸 보시게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n막시밀리앙이 백작에게 경멸에 가까운, 거의 분노에 찬 눈길을 던졌다.\n\n\"이제 가 봐야겠습니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다.\n\n\"떠나시기 전에, 백작님.\" 쥘리가 말했다. \"지난번 일에 대해 저희가 드리고 싶은 말씀을 모두 전하도록 허락해 주시겠어요……\"\n\n\"부인,\" 백작이 그녀의 두 손을 마주 잡으며 말을 가로막았다. \"부인이 말로 전하실 수 있는 그 무엇도, 제가 부인의 눈에서 읽어 낸 것을 결코 다 담아내지 못할 겁니다. 부인 마음속 생각은 제 마음이 온전히 헤아리고 있습니다. 소설 속 은인들처럼, 저도 다시 모습을 보이지 않고 떠나야 마땅했겠지요. 하지만 그건 제 힘에 부치는 미덕이었습니다. 저는 나약하고 허영심 많은 사람이라, 동료 인간들의 다정하고 친절하고 고마워하는 눈길을 좋아하니까요. 떠나는 마당에 제 이기심이 어찌나 심한지, 이렇게까지 말하게 되는군요. '저를 잊지 말아 주십시오, 다정한 벗들이여. 아마 다시는 저를 보지 못할 테니까요.'\"\n\n\"다시는 못 뵙는다니요?\" 에마뉘엘이 외쳤다. 그 사이 쥘리의 뺨으로 굵은 눈물 두 줄기가 흘러내렸다. \"다시는 못 뵙는다고요? 그렇다면 우리 곁을 떠나는 분은 사람이 아니라 천사군요. 선을 행하려고 이 땅에 내려왔다가, 이제 막 하늘로 돌아가려는 천사 말이에요.\"\n\n\"그런 말씀 마십시오.\" 몬테크리스토가 얼른 받았다. \"그런 말씀 마십시오, 벗들이여. 천사는 결코 그르치지 않고, 천상의 존재들은 자기가 머물고자 하는 곳에 머무릅니다. 운명도 그들보다 강하지 못하지요. 오히려 그들이 운명을 이겨 냅니다. 아닙니다, 에마뉘엘, 저는 한낱 인간일 뿐이고, 당신의 찬미는 분에 넘치며, 당신의 말은 신성모독입니다.\"\n\n그러고서 백작은 자기 품으로 달려든 쥘리의 손에 입술을 댄 다음, 다른 손을 에마뉘엘에게 내밀었다. 그런 뒤 평화와 행복이 깃든 이 보금자리를 뿌리치듯 떠나며, 막시밀리앙에게 신호를 보냈다. 막시밀리앙은 발랑틴의 죽음이 그를 그토록 망연케 한 이래로 줄곧 내비치던 그 무심한 태도로, 잠자코 백작의 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n\"제 동생에게 평화와 행복을 되찾아 주세요.\" 쥘리가 몬테크리스토에게 속삭였다. 백작은 대답 대신 그녀의 손을 꼭 쥐었다. 십일 년 전, 모렐의 서재로 올라가는 계단에서 그랬던 것처럼.\n\n\"그럼 아직도 뱃사람 신드바드를 믿으시는 겁니까?\" 그가 미소 지으며 물었다.\n\n\"아, 그럼요.\" 곧바로 대답이 돌아왔다.\n\n\"그렇다면 마음 편히 주무십시오. 그리고 주님께 모든 걸 맡기십시오.\"\n\n앞서 말했듯이 역마차가 기다리고 있었다. 힘센 말 네 마리가 벌써 조바심에 발굽으로 땅을 긁고 있었고, 멀리서 한참을 걸어온 듯한 알리가 땀에 흠뻑 젖은 얼굴로 계단 아래에 서 있었다.\n\n\"그래,\" 백작이 아랍어로 물었다. \"그 노인을 만나고 왔느냐?\" 알리가 그렇다는 몸짓을 했다.\n\n\"내가 시킨 대로, 그분 앞에 편지를 놓아 두었느냐?\"\n\n노예가 공손하게 그렇게 했음을 표시했다.\n\n\"그래서 그분이 뭐라고 하시더냐, 아니 어찌하시더냐?\" 알리는 주인이 자기를 똑똑히 볼 수 있도록 빛이 드는 자리로 가서, 그 영리한 솜씨로 노인의 표정을 흉내 내며 눈을 감았다. 누아르티에가 \"예\"라고 할 때 늘 하던 그대로였다.\n\n\"좋아, 받아들이셨군.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"자, 이제 가자.\"\n\n이 말이 채 입에서 떨어지기도 전에 마차가 출발했고, 말발굽이 포석을 차며 불꽃을 소나기처럼 튀겼다. 막시밀리앙은 한마디도 없이 구석 자리에 몸을 묻었다. 반 시간쯤 지났을 때 마차가 돌연 멈춰 섰다. 백작이 알리의 손가락에 매어 둔 비단 신호줄을 막 잡아당긴 참이었다. 누비아인은 곧바로 내려가 마차 문을 열었다. 별빛이 아름다운 밤이었다. 그들은 막 빌쥐프 언덕 꼭대기에 다다른 참이었다. 그곳에서 내려다보면 파리는 수백만의 인광 물결을 빛 속으로 일렁이는 어두운 바다처럼 보였다. 폭풍이 몰아치는 대양의 물결보다 더 시끄럽고, 더 격정적이고, 더 변덕스럽고, 더 사납고, 더 탐욕스러운 물결이었다. 바다 물결은 이따금 잦아들기도 하건만 결코 쉬는 법이 없는 물결, 끊임없이 부서지고, 끊임없이 거품을 일으키며, 손아귀에 든 것은 무엇이든 집어삼키는 물결이었다.\n\n백작은 홀로 섰다. 그가 손짓하자 마차가 조금 앞으로 나아갔다. 팔짱을 낀 채 그는 한동안 그 거대한 도시를 응시했다. 신앙에 사로잡힌 자도, 유물론자도, 냉소가도 한결같이 그 앞에서 사색에 잠기게 하는 이 현대의 바빌론에, 그가 꿰뚫는 듯한 시선을 못 박고는 입을 열었다.\n\n\"위대한 도시여,\" 그가 고개를 숙이고, 기도하듯 두 손을 모으며 중얼거렸다. \"내가 처음 네 문을 들어선 지 채 여섯 달도 지나지 않았구나. 신의 영이 내 발걸음을 네게로 이끌었고, 또한 그분이 나를 승리 속에 너를 떠나게 해 주신다고 나는 믿는다. 내가 네 성벽 안에 있었던 그 은밀한 까닭은, 오직 내 마음을 읽을 힘을 지니신 그분께만 털어놓았다. 내가 교만도 증오도 없이, 그러나 적잖은 회한을 안고 너를 떠난다는 것을 오직 신만이 아신다. 내게 맡겨진 그 힘이 단 한 번도 내 사사로운 이익이나 부질없는 목적에 쓰이지 않았음을 오직 그분만이 아신다. 오, 위대한 도시여, 내가 찾던 것을 발견한 곳이 바로 고동치는 네 가슴속이었다. 끈기 있는 광부처럼, 나는 거기서 악을 뿌리째 뽑아내려 네 창자 깊은 곳까지 파고들었다. 이제 내 일은 다 이루어졌고, 내 사명은 끝났다. 이제 너는 내게 고통도 기쁨도 줄 수 없다. 잘 있거라, 파리여, 잘 있거라!\"\n\n그의 시선이 밤의 정령처럼 광막한 평야 위를 헤맸다. 그는 이마를 손으로 한번 쓸고는 마차에 올랐고, 문이 그의 등 뒤로 닫혔다. 마차는 먼지와 굉음의 회오리 속에 언덕 반대편으로 순식간에 사라졌다.\n\n십 리외를 가는 동안 말 한마디 오가지 않았다. 모렐은 상념에 잠겨 있었고, 몬테크리스토는 그 상념에 잠긴 사내를 바라보고 있었다.\n\n\"모렐,\" 마침내 백작이 그에게 말했다. \"나를 따라온 걸 후회하나?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 백작님. 하지만 파리를 떠나는 게……\"\n\n\"파리에 자네를 기다리는 행복이 있다고 여겼다면, 모렐, 나는 자네를 거기 두고 왔을 걸세.\"\n\n\"발랑틴이 파리의 성벽 안에 잠들어 있습니다. 파리를 떠나는 건 그녀를 두 번째로 잃는 것과 같아요.\"\n\n\"막시밀리앙,\" 백작이 말했다. \"우리가 잃은 벗들은 땅의 품속에 잠드는 게 아니라, 우리 가슴 깊은 곳에 묻히는 거라네. 그래서 우리는 늘 그들과 함께하도록 정해진 것이지. 내게도 그렇게 결코 내 곁을 떠나지 않는 벗이 둘 있어. 하나는 내게 생명을 준 이요, 다른 하나는 내게 지식과 지혜를 준 이라네. 그들의 영혼이 내 안에 살아 있어. 의심이 들 때면 나는 그들에게 묻고, 내가 무슨 선한 일을 한다면 그건 그들의 자애로운 조언 덕분이지. 자네 마음의 소리에 귀 기울여 보게, 모렐. 그리고 나를 대하는 이 침울한 낯빛을 계속 지녀야 하는지 그 마음에 물어보게.\"\n\n\"백작님,\" 막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"제 마음의 소리는 너무도 슬프고, 제게 불행 말고는 아무것도 약속해 주지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"세상만사를 검은 구름 너머로 바라보는 것, 그게 나약해진 정신이 빠지는 길이라네. 영혼은 스스로 자기 지평을 짓는 법이야. 자네 영혼이 어두워졌으니, 미래의 하늘도 폭풍우가 몰아칠 듯, 가망 없어 보이는 게지.\"\n\n\"그 말씀이 옳을지도 모르지요.\" 막시밀리앙은 그렇게 말하고는 다시 깊은 생각에 잠겨 들었다.\n\n여정은 백작의 무한한 권력이 늘 부리던 그 놀라운 속도로 이어졌다. 도시들이 길 위의 그림자처럼 그들에게서 달아났고, 첫 가을바람에 흔들리는 나무들은 미친 듯이 달려와 그들을 맞이하다가 막상 닿으면 그만큼 빠르게 물러가는 거인들 같았다. 이튿날 아침 그들은 샬롱에 닿았고, 그곳에는 백작의 증기선이 기다리고 있었다. 한순간도 지체하지 않고 마차를 배에 실었고, 두 여행자는 곧장 승선했다. 속도를 내도록 만들어진 배였다. 양쪽의 외륜 두 짝은 날개와도 같아서, 배는 새처럼 수면을 스쳐 나아갔다.\n\n빠르게 바람을 가르며 나아갈 때 흔히 맛보는 그 황홀한 기분을, 모렐도 느끼지 못하는 것은 아니었다. 이따금 이마에서 머리카락을 흩날리는 바람은, 거기 모여든 구름을 한순간이나마 걷어 낼 듯도 했다.\n\n여행자들과 파리 사이의 거리가 멀어질수록, 거의 초인적인 평온이 백작을 감싸는 듯했다. 마치 고국으로 돌아가는 망명객처럼 보였다.\n\n이윽고 마르세유가 눈앞에 모습을 드러냈다. 희고, 뜨겁고, 생기와 활력이 넘치는 마르세유. 티레와 카르타고의 아우뻘이요, 지중해의 패권을 이어받은 후계자인 마르세유. 늙었으되 언제나 젊은 마르세유. 둥근 탑과 생니콜라 요새, 퓌제가 설계한 시청사, 그리고 두 사람 다 어린 시절 뛰놀던 벽돌 부두가 있는 항구를 보자, 강렬한 추억들이 그들 가슴속에서 일렁였다. 그리하여 두 사람은 약속이나 한 듯 카느비에르 거리에서 발걸음을 멈췄다.\n\n알제로 떠날 배 한 척에서, 으레 출항에 따르기 마련인 분주함이 한창이었다. 승객들과 그 가족들이 갑판을 가득 메웠고, 벗들은 다정하면서도 서글픈 작별을 나누고 있었다. 더러는 눈물을 흘렸고, 더러는 큰 소리로 슬픔을 토했다. 그 모든 광경은, 그런 장면을 날마다 보는 이들에게조차 가슴 설레게 할 만한 것이었다. 그러나 부두의 너른 포석에 발을 디딘 순간부터 막시밀리앙의 마음을 사로잡은 상념의 흐름만은 흔들어 놓지 못했다.\n\n\"여기예요,\" 그가 몬테크리스토의 팔에 몸을 무겁게 기대며 말했다. \"여기가 바로, 파라옹호가 입항할 때 제 아버지가 멈춰 서 계시던 자리예요. 백작님이 죽음과 불명예에서 구해 주신 그 마음씨 좋은 노인이, 바로 여기서 제 품으로 뛰어드셨지요. 아직도 제 얼굴에 그분의 뜨거운 눈물이 느껴집니다. 눈물을 흘린 게 그분만은 아니었어요. 우리가 만나는 광경을 지켜보던 많은 이들도 함께 울었으니까요.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 가만히 미소 지으며 말했다. \"나도 그 자리에 있었네.\" 그러면서 어느 골목 모퉁이를 가리켰다. 그가 말하며 가리킨 바로 그 방향에서, 비통한 슬픔이 묻어나는 신음 소리가 들려왔다. 한 여인이 막 출항하려는 배 위의 승객을 향해 손을 흔들고 있었다. 몬테크리스토는 어떤 감정을 담고 그녀를 바라보았다. 모렐의 눈이 배에 못 박혀 있지 않았더라면 분명 알아챘을 만한 감정이었다.\n\n\"오, 세상에!\" 모렐이 외쳤다. \"잘못 본 게 아니에요. 저기 모자를 흔들고 있는 저 청년, 중위 군복을 입은 저 젊은이는 알베르 드 모르세르예요!\"\n\n\"그렇다네.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"나도 알아봤네.\"\n\n\"어떻게요? 백작님은 다른 쪽을 보고 계셨잖아요.\"\n\n백작은, 대답하고 싶지 않을 때면 으레 그랬듯이 미소를 지었다. 그러고는 다시 베일을 쓴 그 여인 쪽으로 시선을 돌렸다. 여인은 이내 골목 모퉁이로 사라졌다. 백작이 벗을 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n\"막시밀리앙, 이 고장에서 자네가 할 일이 있나?\"\n\n\"아버지의 무덤 앞에서 울어야 합니다.\" 모렐이 갈라진 목소리로 대답했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 가 보게. 거기서 나를 기다리게. 나도 곧 가겠네.\"\n\n\"그럼 저를 두고 가시는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"그래. 나도 경건히 들러야 할 곳이 있다네.\"\n\n모렐은 백작이 내미는 손에 자기 손을 맡겼다. 그런 뒤 이루 말할 수 없이 서글픈 몸짓으로 고개를 숙이고는 백작 곁을 떠나, 도시 동쪽으로 발걸음을 옮겼다. 몬테크리스토는 막시밀리앙이 시야에서 사라질 때까지 그 자리에 그대로 서 있었다. 그런 뒤 메양 가로수길 쪽으로 천천히 걸음을 옮겼다. 이 이야기 첫머리에서 독자들이 익히 알게 된 작은 집을 찾아가려는 것이었다.\n\n그 집은 여전히 그 자리에, 마르세유의 한량들이 가장 즐겨 거니는 산책로 가운데 하나인 그 멋진 보리수 가로수 그늘 아래 서 있었다. 거대한 포도 덩굴이 집을 뒤덮고, 늙어 거뭇해진 가지를 남국의 뜨거운 햇살에 누렇게 그을린 돌벽 위로 뻗고 있었다. 수많은 발길에 닳아빠진 돌계단 두 단을 오르면 널빤지 세 장으로 짠 문이 있었다. 한 번도 페인트칠이나 니스칠을 한 적 없는 문이라, 마른 철에는 큼직한 틈이 쩍 벌어졌다가 비가 오면 다시 아물곤 했다. 그 집은 허물어져 가는 낡은 모습에 가난이 역력했지만, 그래도 어딘가 정겹고 그림 같았다. 옛날에 당테스 노인이 살던 바로 그 집이었다. 다만 노인은 다락방 하나만 쓰던 것에 반해, 이제는 백작의 뜻에 따라 집 전체가 메르세데스의 차지가 되었다는 점이 다를 뿐이었다.\n\n백작이 그토록 안타까운 마음으로 그 자리를 떠나는 것을 지켜보았던 그 여인이 바로 이 집으로 들어섰다. 그녀가 등 뒤로 문을 채 닫기도 전에 몬테크리스토가 골목 모퉁이에 모습을 드러냈다. 그리하여 그는 거의 같은 순간에 그녀를 찾았다가 다시 놓쳤다. 닳아빠진 그 계단은 그에게 오랜 벗과도 같았다. 안쪽 빗장을 들어 올리는 데 쓰는, 대가리 굵은 못이 박힌 그 비바람에 시달린 문을 어떻게 여는지, 그는 누구보다 잘 알았다. 그는 노크도, 자신이 왔음을 알리는 그 어떤 기척도 없이 안으로 들어섰다. 마치 이 집의 벗이거나 주인이라도 되는 듯이. 벽돌이 깔린 통로 끝에는 햇살이 흠뻑 든, 온기와 빛이 가득한 작은 뜰이 있었다. 바로 이 뜰에서, 백작이 일러 준 자리에서 메르세데스는 돈 한 꾸러미를 찾아냈다. 백작이 섬세한 배려로, 이십사 년 전에 거기 묻어 두었던 것이라 둘러댄 그 돈이었다. 뜰의 나무들은 길가 문간 계단에서도 한눈에 보였다.\n\n집으로 발을 들이는 순간, 몬테크리스토는 깊은 흐느낌에 가까운 한숨 소리를 들었다. 소리가 나는 쪽으로 눈길을 돌리니, 짙은 잎과 길고 아름다운 자줏빛 꽃이 어우러진 버지니아재스민 정자 아래, 메르세데스가 고개를 숙이고 앉아 쓰라리게 울고 있었다. 그녀는 베일을 들어 올린 채, 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고서, 아들이 곁에 있는 동안 그토록 오래 억눌러 온 한숨과 눈물을 마음껏 쏟아 내고 있었다.\n\n몬테크리스토가 몇 걸음 다가서자, 자갈 위로 발소리가 났다. 메르세데스가 고개를 들었다. 그리고 자기 앞에 한 사내가 서 있는 것을 보고 공포에 찬 비명을 질렀다.\n\n\"부인,\" 백작이 말했다. \"이제 부인에게 행복을 돌려드리는 것은 제 힘으로 할 수 있는 일이 아닙니다. 하지만 위로는 드릴 수 있지요. 한 벗이 드리는 것이라 여기고 받아 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"저는 정말이지 더없이 비참해요.\" 메르세데스가 대답했다. \"이 세상에 홀로 남았어요. 제겐 아들 하나뿐이었는데, 그 아이마저 저를 떠나 버렸으니!\"\n\n\"아드님은 고귀한 마음을 지녔습니다, 부인.\" 백작이 대답했다. \"그리고 옳게 처신했지요. 사람은 누구나 제 조국에 마땅히 바칠 것이 있다는 걸 그는 알고 있습니다. 어떤 이는 재능을, 어떤 이는 노력을 바치지요. 또 어떤 이는 피를, 어떤 이는 밤을 새우는 수고를 같은 대의에 바칩니다. 만약 그가 부인 곁에 남았더라면, 그의 삶은 견디기 힘든 짐이 되었을 테고, 부인의 슬픔에 함께하지도 못했을 겁니다. 그는 역경과 맞서 싸우며 더욱 강해지고 더욱 명예로워질 것이며, 그 역경을 번영으로 바꿔 놓을 겁니다. 그가 부인을 위해 미래를 일구도록 맡겨 두십시오. 감히 말씀드리건대, 부인은 그것을 믿음직한 손에 맡기시는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"오,\" 가엾은 여인이 슬프게 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"말씀하시는 그 번영, 자비로우신 하느님께서 그 애에게 베풀어 주십사 제 마음 깊은 곳에서 비는 그 번영을, 저는 결코 누리지 못할 거예요. 역경의 쓰디쓴 잔을 저는 찌꺼기 한 방울까지 다 들이켰고, 이제 무덤이 멀지 않았음을 느낍니다. 제가 그토록 큰 행복을 누렸던 이곳으로 저를 데려와 주신 것은 친절한 일이셨어요, 백작님. 한때 온전히 제 것이었던 행복이 깃든 바로 그 자리에서, 저도 죽음을 맞아야 마땅하니까요.\"\n\n\"아아,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"부인의 말씀이 제 가슴을 지지고 쓰라리게 합니다. 더구나 부인께서는 저를 미워하실 온갖 이유가 있으니 더욱 그렇지요. 제가 부인의 모든 불행의 원인이었습니다. 그런데 어찌하여 저를 탓하지 않고 가엾이 여기시는 겁니까? 부인은 저를 더욱 불행하게 만드십니다……\"\n\n\"당신을 미워하다니요, 탓하다니요. 당신을, 에드몽을요! 제 아들의 목숨을 살려 준 사람을 미워하고 책망하라고요! 모르세르 씨가 그토록 자랑스러워하던 그 아들을 없애 버리는 게, 당신의 치명적이고 피비린내 나는 뜻이 아니었던가요? 오, 저를 똑똑히 보세요. 그리고 할 수 있다면, 제게서 책망하는 기색이 한 자락이라도 있는지 찾아보세요.\"\n\n백작이 고개를 들어 메르세데스에게 시선을 못 박았다. 그녀는 의자에서 반쯤 몸을 일으키며 두 손을 그에게 내밀었다.\n\n\"오, 저를 보세요,\" 그녀가 깊은 우수에 잠긴 채 말을 이었다. \"제 눈은 더 이상 광채로 사람을 현혹하지 못해요. 저쪽 다락방 창가에서, 그 시절 그곳에 살던 늙은 아버지 곁에서 저를 애타게 내다보던 에드몽 당테스에게 미소 짓던 그때로부터 너무도 오랜 세월이 흘렀으니까요. 슬픔의 세월이 그 시절과 지금 사이에 깊은 골을 파 놓았어요. 저는 당신을 책망하지도 미워하지도 않아요, 벗이여. 오, 아니에요, 에드몽, 제가 탓하는 건 바로 저 자신이고, 제가 미워하는 것도 바로 저 자신이에요. 오, 가엾은 이 몸!\" 그녀가 두 손을 맞잡고 하늘로 눈을 들어 올리며 외쳤다. \"한때 저는 신심과 순결과 사랑을 지녔어요. 천사들의 행복을 이루는 그 세 가지를요. 그런데 지금의 저는 무엇인가요?\"\n\n몬테크리스토가 그녀에게 다가가, 말없이 그 손을 잡았다.\n\n\"안 돼요,\" 그녀가 손을 가만히 빼며 말했다. \"안 돼요, 벗이여, 저를 만지지 마세요. 당신은 저를 살려 주셨지만, 당신의 복수 아래 쓰러진 그 모든 이들 가운데 가장 죄 많은 사람은 바로 저였어요. 그들은 증오에, 탐욕에, 자기애에 휘둘렸지요. 하지만 저는 비열했어요. 용기가 없어서, 제 판단을 거스르며 행동했으니까요. 아니에요, 제 손을 잡지 마세요, 에드몽. 저를 위로할 다정한 말 한마디를 떠올리고 계신 거 분명해요. 하지만 제게는 하지 마세요. 당신의 친절을 받을 자격이 더 있는 다른 이들을 위해 아껴 두세요. 보세요.\" (그러면서 그녀는 얼굴을 온전히 드러냈다.) \"보세요, 불행이 제 머리카락을 세어 놓았고, 제 눈은 하도 많이 울어서 자줏빛 테가 둘러졌으며, 제 이마엔 주름이 잡혔어요. 그런데 에드몽, 당신은 반대로 여전히 젊고, 잘생겼고, 위엄이 있어요. 그건 당신에게 믿음이 있었기 때문이고, 당신에게 힘이 있었기 때문이며, 당신이 하느님을 의지했고 하느님이 당신을 지켜 주셨기 때문이에요. 하지만 저로 말하자면, 저는 겁쟁이였어요. 하느님을 저버렸고, 그래서 하느님도 저를 버리신 거예요.\"\n\n메르세데스가 울음을 터뜨렸다. 추억의 무게에 짓눌려 그녀의 여인다운 마음이 무너지고 있었다. 몬테크리스토가 그녀의 손을 잡아 그 위에 입을 맞추었다. 그러나 그녀 자신도 느꼈다. 그것이 어느 성인의 대리석상 손에 입맞춤할 때나 보였을 법한, 그 이상도 이하도 아닌 온기의 입맞춤이라는 것을.\n\n\"흔히 있는 일이지요,\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"단 한 번의 잘못이 한평생의 앞날을 송두리째 무너뜨리는 일이요. 저는 당신이 죽은 줄 알았어요. 그런데 어째서 저는 당신보다 더 살아남았을까요? 마음속 은밀한 구석에서 영영 당신을 애도한 게 제게 무슨 득이 되었나요? 그저 서른아홉 살 먹은 여자를 쉰 살 노파처럼 보이게 만들었을 뿐인걸요. 어째서, 당신을 알아본 사람이, 그것도 오직 저 하나뿐이었으면서, 어째서 저는 제 아들만 구할 수 있었을까요? 죄가 있다 한들, 제가 남편으로 받아들였던 그 사람도 함께 구해야 마땅하지 않았을까요? 그런데 저는 그를 죽도록 내버려 두었어요! 무슨 말을 하는 거죠? 오, 자비로우신 하느님, 그가 저를 위해 배신자가 되고 위증자가 되었다는 걸 기억하지 못한 채, 아니 기억하려 하지 않은 채, 제 무기력한 냉담함으로, 그에 대한 제 경멸로, 저는 그의 죽음에 한몫한 게 아니었나요? 아들을 이 먼 곳까지 따라온 게 제게 무슨 소용이 있나요? 이제 그 애를 버려두고, 홀로 아프리카의 그 해로운 땅으로 떠나가게 했으니 말이에요. 오, 거듭 말하지만, 저는 비열하고 비겁했어요. 제 애정을 저버렸고, 모든 배교자가 그렇듯, 제 곁에 있는 이들에게 불길한 징조가 되는 사람이에요!\"\n\n\"아니에요, 메르세데스,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"아닙니다. 부인은 스스로를 너무 가혹하게 단죄하고 있어요. 부인은 고귀한 마음을 지닌 여인이고, 저를 무장 해제시킨 것은 바로 부인의 슬픔이었습니다. 그래도 저는 한낱 도구였을 뿐, 보이지 않는, 노여움에 찬 신께서 저를 이끄셨던 겁니다. 그분은 제가 내리치도록 정해진 그 치명적인 일격을 거두지 않기로 택하셨지요. 지난 십 년간 날마다 그 발치에 엎드려 온 그 하느님을 증인으로 삼아 말하건대, 저는 부인을 위해서라면 제 목숨을, 그리고 그 목숨과 떼려야 뗄 수 없이 얽힌 그 계획들까지 기꺼이 바쳤을 겁니다. 하지만, 그리고 이건 다소 자랑스럽게 말씀드리는 것입니다만, 메르세데스, 하느님께 제가 필요했기에 저는 살아남았습니다. 지난날과 지금을 살펴보시고, 또 앞날을 헤아려 보려 애써 보십시오. 그런 다음에 제가 신의 도구가 아닌지 말씀해 보십시오. 더없이 무서운 불행들, 더없이 끔찍한 고통들, 저를 사랑하던 모든 이들의 외면, 저를 알지도 못하는 자들의 박해, 그것들이 제 젊은 날의 시련이었습니다. 그러던 어느 순간, 유폐와 고독과 비참함에서 저는 빛과 자유를 되찾았고, 더없이 눈부시고, 더없이 끝 간 데 없으며, 더없이 전대미문의 재산을 손에 넣은 사람이 되었지요. 하느님이 그분의 위대한 뜻을 이루시려 그것을 제게 내려 주셨음을 깨닫지 못한다면, 저는 눈먼 자였을 겁니다. 그때부터 저는 이 재산을, 어떤 특별한 목적을 위해 제게 맡겨진 것으로 여겼습니다. 한때 부인이, 메르세데스, 부인이 더없이 복되게 만들어 줄 수도 있었던 그 삶에는 단 한 생각도 두지 않았지요. 평온하고 고요한 시간은 단 한 시간도 제 것이 아니었습니다. 저는 절멸의 천사처럼 떠밀려 나아가는 저 자신을 느꼈으니까요. 위험으로 가득한 어떤 사업에 막 뛰어들려는 모험심 강한 선장들처럼, 저는 식량을 비축하고, 무기를 장전하고, 공격과 방어의 온갖 수단을 그러모았습니다. 제 몸은 더없이 격렬한 단련에, 제 영혼은 더없이 쓰라린 시련에 길들였지요. 제 팔에는 죽이는 법을, 제 눈에는 처참한 고통을 지켜보는 법을, 제 입에는 더없이 끔찍한 광경 앞에서도 미소 짓는 법을 가르쳤습니다. 사람 좋고, 잘 믿고, 잘 용서하던 제가, 복수심에 차고, 교활하고, 사악한 사람이, 아니 차라리 운명처럼 흔들림 없는 사람이 되었지요. 그런 다음 제 앞에 열린 길로 나아갔습니다. 모든 장애물을 넘어 목표에 다다랐지요. 하지만 제 길을 가로막은 자들에게는 화가 있으리니!\"\n\n\"그만하세요,\" 메르세데스가 말했다. \"그만하세요, 에드몽! 믿어 주세요, 오직 당신을 알아본 그 여자만이 당신을 이해한 유일한 사람이었어요. 그리고 그 여자가 당신의 길을 가로막아, 당신이 그녀를 유리처럼 으스러뜨렸다 해도, 에드몽, 그래도 그녀는 당신을 우러러볼 수밖에 없었을 거예요! 저와 지난날 사이에 깊은 골이 있듯, 에드몽, 당신과 나머지 인류 사이에도 깊은 심연이 있어요. 그리고 솔직히 말하지만, 당신과 다른 사내들을 견주어 보는 일은 앞으로도 제게 가장 큰 고통 중 하나로 남을 거예요. 아니에요, 가치로 보나 선함으로 보나 이 세상에 당신과 견줄 만한 건 아무것도 없어요! 하지만 우리는 작별해야 해요, 에드몽. 이제 헤어지기로 해요.\"\n\n\"부인 곁을 떠나기 전에, 메르세데스, 제게 부탁할 일은 없으십니까?\" 백작이 말했다.\n\n\"이 세상에서 제가 바라는 건 단 하나예요, 에드몽. 제 아들의 행복이요.\"\n\n\"전능하신 분께 그의 목숨을 지켜 주십사 기도하십시오. 그의 행복을 이루는 일은 제가 떠맡겠습니다.\"\n\n\"고마워요, 에드몽.\"\n\n\"하지만 부인 자신을 위해서는 부탁할 게 없으십니까, 메르세데스?\"\n\n\"저 자신을 위해서는 아무것도 바라지 않아요. 저는 말하자면 두 무덤 사이에서 살아가고 있어요. 하나는 오래오래전에 제게서 사라져 버린 에드몽 당테스의 무덤이에요. 그는 제 사랑을 받았지요! 이제 빛바랜 제 입술에는 어울리지 않는 말이지만, 제 가슴에 소중한 추억이고, 이 세상 모든 것을 다 준다 해도 잃고 싶지 않은 추억이에요. 다른 하나는 에드몽 당테스의 손에 죽음을 맞은 그 사내의 무덤이에요. 저는 그 일을 옳다고 여기지만, 그래도 죽은 이를 위해 기도는 해야 하니까요.\"\n\n\"부인의 아드님은 행복해질 겁니다, 메르세데스.\" 백작이 거듭 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 저는 이 세상이 줄 수 있는 만큼의 행복은 누리는 셈이겠지요.\"\n\n\"그런데 앞으로 어찌하실 작정이십니까?\"\n\n메르세데스가 슬프게 미소 지었다.\n\n\"옛날의 그 메르세데스처럼 여기서 일해 빵을 벌며 살겠다고 말한다면, 그건 사실이 아닐 거예요. 당신도 믿지 않으실 테고요. 이젠 기도로 하루하루를 보내는 것 말고는 아무것도 할 힘이 없어요. 그래도 일할 필요는 없을 거예요. 당신이 묻어 두었던, 일러 주신 자리에서 제가 찾아낸 그 얼마 안 되는 돈이면 저 한 몸 건사하기엔 넉넉할 테니까요. 아마 저를 두고, 제가 하는 일이며 사는 모양새를 두고 소문이 무성하겠지요. 하지만 그런 건 거의 아무래도 좋아요. 그건 하느님과 당신과 저, 우리만의 일이니까요.\"\n\n\"메르세데스,\" 백작이 말했다. \"탓하려고 드리는 말씀은 아닙니다만, 부인은 모르세르 씨가 모은 재산 전부를 내놓음으로써 공연한 희생을 치르셨습니다. 부인의 알뜰함과 절약을 생각하면, 적어도 그 절반은 당연히 부인 몫이었으니까요.\"\n\n\"제게 무슨 말씀을 하시려는지 알겠어요. 하지만 받을 수 없어요, 에드몽. 제 아들이 허락하지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"알베르 드 모르세르의 온전한 동의 없이는 아무 일도 하지 않겠습니다. 제가 그의 뜻을 알아보고, 거기에 따르겠습니다. 하지만 만약 그가 제 제안을 받아들이려 한다면, 부인은 반대하시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"잘 아시잖아요, 에드몽. 저는 이제 무얼 따져 헤아릴 줄 아는 사람이 못 돼요. 제겐 의지가 없어요. 결코 아무것도 결정하지 않겠다는 의지 말고는요. 제 머리 위로 몰아친 그 숱한 폭풍에 너무도 압도된 나머지, 저는 독수리 발톱에 잡힌 참새처럼 전능하신 분의 손안에서 모든 걸 맡긴 사람이 되어 버렸어요. 제가 살아 있는 건, 제게 죽음이 정해지지 않았기 때문이에요. 누가 제게 도움을 보내 준다면, 저는 받아들일게요.\"\n\n\"아, 부인,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그렇게 말씀하시면 안 됩니다! 하늘의 뜻에 순응하는 마음은 그렇게 드러내는 게 아닙니다. 오히려 우리는 모두 자유로이 행동하는 존재인걸요.\"\n\n\"아아!\" 메르세데스가 외쳤다. \"만약 그렇다면, 만약 제게 자유로운 의지가 있으면서도 그 의지를 이룰 힘이 없다면, 그건 저를 절망으로 몰아넣을 거예요.\"\n\n몬테크리스토가 고개를 떨구며, 그녀의 격렬한 슬픔 앞에서 움찔 물러섰다.\n\n\"다시 뵙겠다는 말씀조차 해 주지 않으시렵니까?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"아니에요, 우리는 다시 만날 거예요.\" 메르세데스가 엄숙하게 하늘을 가리키며 말했다. \"제게 아직 희망이 있다는 걸 보여 드리려고 이렇게 말하는 거예요.\"\n\n그러고서 떨리는 자기 손을 백작의 손 위에 꼭 포갠 다음, 메르세데스는 계단 위로 달려 올라가 사라졌다. 몬테크리스토는 천천히 집을 나서 부두 쪽으로 향했다. 그러나 메르세데스는 그가 떠나는 모습을 보지 못했다. 당테스 노인이 쓰던 방의 작은 창가에 앉아 있었으면서도 말이다. 그녀의 눈은 아들을 싣고 망망대해를 건너가는 배를 좇느라 애를 쓰고 있었다. 그러면서도 그녀의 입에서는 저도 모르게 가만히 중얼거림이 새어 나오고 있었다.\n\n\"에드몽, 에드몽, 에드몽!\"\n\n# Chapter 113. The Past\n\n백작은 메르세데스를 남겨 두고 온 그 집을 무거운 마음으로 떠났다. 아마도 다시는 그녀를 보지 못하리라. 어린 에두아르가 죽은 뒤로 몬테크리스토에게는 커다란 변화가 일어났다. 길고 구불구불한 길을 지나 마침내 복수의 정점에 다다른 그는, 눈앞에 의혹의 심연이 입을 벌리고 있는 것을 보았다. 그뿐만이 아니었다. 방금 메르세데스와 나눈 대화가 그의 가슴속에 너무도 많은 기억을 일깨워 놓았기에, 그는 그것들과 맞서 싸워야만 한다고 느꼈다. 백작 같은 기질의 사내는 평범한 사람들 속에서는 존재할 수 있으나 뛰어난 사람들은 파괴하고 마는 그런 우울에 오래 잠겨 있을 수 없었다. 만일 지금 자신을 탓할 까닭을 찾았다면, 그것은 자신의 계산에 어떤 오류가 있었던 게 틀림없다고 그는 생각했다.\n\n\"나는 나 자신을 속였을 리 없다.\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 과거를 그릇된 빛 아래에서 바라보고 있는 게 틀림없어. 뭐라고!\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"내가 잘못된 길을 따라온 것일까? 내가 세운 목표가 그릇된 목표였단 말인가? 단 한 시간이면, 한 건축가가 모든 희망을 걸었던 그 작업이 불가능한, 아니 신성모독에 가까운 일이었음을 증명하기에 충분하단 말인가? 나는 이 생각과 화해할 수 없다. 그것은 나를 미치게 만들 것이다. 내가 지금 만족하지 못하는 까닭은 과거를 또렷이 헤아리지 못하기 때문이다. 과거란, 우리가 지나온 들판처럼, 앞으로 나아갈수록 흐릿해지는 법이지. 내 처지는 꿈속에서 상처 입은 사람과 같다. 그는 상처를 느끼지만, 언제 그 상처를 입었는지는 기억하지 못한다.\n\n\"자, 그렇다면, 거듭난 자여, 분에 넘치게 탕진하는 자여, 잠에서 깨어난 자여, 전능한 몽상가여, 무적의 백만장자여, 굶주림과 비참으로 점철된 네 지난 삶을 다시 한번 돌아보아라. 운명과 불행이 너를 끌고 갔던, 절망이 너를 맞이했던 그 자리들을 다시 찾아가라. 몬테크리스토가 그 안에서 당테스를 보려 하는 거울에는 지금 너무 많은 다이아몬드와 너무 많은 황금과 광채가 비치고 있다. 네 다이아몬드를 감추고, 네 황금을 묻고, 네 광채를 가려라. 부를 가난과, 자유를 감옥과, 살아 있는 몸을 시체와 맞바꾸어라!\"\n\n이렇게 곱씹으며 몬테크리스토는 케스리 거리를 걸어 내려갔다. 스물네 해 전, 말없는 야간 경비대에 끌려 지나갔던 바로 그 거리였다. 오늘은 이토록 환하고 활기 넘치는 집들이, 그날 밤에는 어둡고 침묵에 잠긴 채 굳게 닫혀 있었다.\n\n\"그래도 똑같은 집들이로군.\" 몬테크리스토가 중얼거렸다. \"다만 지금은 밤이 아니라 환한 대낮일 뿐이지. 이 자리를 밝히고 이토록 명랑하게 만드는 것은 태양이다.\"\n\n그는 생로랑 거리를 지나 부두 쪽으로 나아가, 콩시뉴까지 걸어갔다. 그가 배에 올랐던 바로 그 지점이었다. 줄무늬 차양을 친 유람선 한 척이 지나가고 있었다. 몬테크리스토가 주인을 부르자, 그는 좋은 삯을 바라는 뱃사공 특유의 열의로 곧장 노를 저어 다가왔다.\n\n날씨는 더없이 화창했고, 뱃놀이는 즐거운 일이었다. 붉게 타오르는 태양이 반갑게 맞아 주는 바다의 품으로 가라앉고 있었다. 수정처럼 매끄러운 바다는 이따금 물고기가 뛰어오를 때마다 일렁였다. 보이지 않는 적에게 쫓긴 물고기들이 다른 세계에서 안전을 구하느라 솟구치는 것이었다. 한편 수평선 끝에서는 갈매기처럼 희고 우아한 어부들의 배며, 코르시카나 에스파냐로 향하는 상선들이 보였다.\n\n그러나 맑은 하늘과 우아한 자태의 배들과 온 풍경을 적시는 황금빛 햇살에도 불구하고, 망토를 두른 몬테크리스토 백작은 오직 그 끔찍한 항해만을 떠올리고 있었다. 그 세세한 장면들이 하나하나 그의 기억 속으로 되살아났다. 카탈루냐 마을에 외로이 타오르던 불빛, 자신을 어디로 끌고 가는지를 일러 주던 이프성의 첫 모습, 바다로 뛰어들려 했을 때 헌병들과 벌인 몸싸움, 끝내 제압당했음을 깨달았을 때의 절망, 그리고 기병총의 총구가 이마에 닿던 그 감각. 이 모든 것이 생생하고 무시무시한 현실로 그의 앞에 되살아났다.\n\n여름의 열기에 말라붙었던 시냇물이 가을 폭풍이 지나간 뒤 한 방울 한 방울 스며 나오기 시작하듯, 백작은 일찍이 에드몽 당테스를 거의 짓눌렀던 그 쓰라림이 가슴속에 서서히 차오르는 것을 느꼈다. 맑은 하늘도, 쏜살같이 미끄러지는 배들도, 눈부신 햇살도 사라졌다. 하늘에는 검은 휘장이 드리워졌고, 이프성의 거대한 구조물은 죽일 듯 미워하던 적의 망령처럼 보였다. 기슭에 닿자 백작은 본능적으로 배의 끝자락으로 몸을 움츠렸고, 주인은 더없이 다정한 목소리로 외쳐야만 했다.\n\n\"손님, 부두에 닿았습니다.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 떠올렸다. 바로 이 자리, 바로 이 바위 위에서, 경비병들에게 거칠게 끌려 총검 끝에 떠밀리며 비탈을 올라야 했음을. 당테스에게는 그 길이 무척 길게 느껴졌으나, 몬테크리스토에게는 그만큼 짧게 느껴졌다. 노가 한 번 저어질 때마다 새로운 상념의 무리가 깨어나, 흩날리는 바다 물보라와 함께 솟구치는 듯했다.\n\n7월 혁명 이래로 이프성에는 갇힌 죄수가 한 명도 없었다. 밀수를 막기 위해 배치된 경비대만이 머물고 있을 뿐이었다. 문에서는 한 관리인이 기다리고 있다가, 한때 공포의 무대였던 이 진기한 유적을 방문객들에게 보여 주었다.\n\n백작은 옛 간수들이 아직 남아 있는지 물었다. 그러나 그들은 모두 연금을 받고 물러났거나 다른 일자리로 옮겨 갔다. 그를 안내하는 관리인은 1830년부터 이곳에 있었을 뿐이었다. 그는 자신의 지하 감방을 찾아갔다. 좁은 틈으로 헛되이 비집고 들어오려 애쓰는 흐릿한 빛을 다시 보았다. 그의 눈길은 침대가 놓여 있던 자리에 머물렀다. 침대는 그 후로 치워졌고, 그 뒤편으로 새로 끼워진 돌들이 파리아 신부가 뚫었던 통로의 흔적을 드러내고 있었다. 몬테크리스토는 사지가 떨려 오는 것을 느꼈다. 그는 통나무 토막 위에 주저앉았다.\n\n\"미라보의 독살 이야기 말고도, 이 감옥에 얽힌 다른 이야기들이 있소?\" 백작이 물었다. \"사람이 같은 사람을 가두어 둘 수 있었다고는 믿기 어려운 이 음산한 거처에 관해 전해 내려오는 이야기가 있느냐는 말이오.\"\n\n\"네, 손님. 사실 간수 앙투안이 바로 이 지하 감방에 얽힌 이야기를 하나 들려준 적이 있습니다.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 몸서리쳤다. 앙투안은 그의 간수였다. 그 이름도 얼굴도 거의 잊고 있었으나, 이름이 입에 오르자 그는 늘 보던 그 모습을 떠올렸다. 수염에 둘러싸인 얼굴, 갈색 웃옷, 열쇠 꾸러미, 그 짤랑거리는 소리가 아직도 귓가에 들리는 듯했다. 백작은 돌아보았고, 관리인이 든 횃불 탓에 한층 더 어두워진 복도에서 그를 본 것만 같았다.\n\n\"이야기를 들어 보시겠습니까, 손님?\"\n\n\"그래, 들려주게.\" 몬테크리스토가 손을 가슴에 대어 거칠게 뛰는 심장을 진정시키며 말했다. 그는 제 자신의 이야기를 듣게 될까 봐 두려웠다.\n\n\"이 지하 감방은요,\" 관리인이 말했다. \"얼마 전까지 아주 위험한 죄수가 갇혀 있던 곳이라고 합니다. 더구나 그자는 손재주가 비상해서 더욱 위험했지요. 같은 시기에 또 다른 사람도 이 성에 갇혀 있었는데, 그 사람은 악한 자가 아니라 그저 미친 가엾은 신부였습니다.\"\n\n\"아, 그래? 미쳤다고?\" 몬테크리스토가 되뇌었다. \"그래, 무슨 망상에 사로잡혀 있었나?\"\n\n\"자기를 풀어 주는 사람에게 수백만 프랑을 주겠다고 했답니다.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 눈을 들었으나 하늘이 보이지 않았다. 그와 창공 사이에는 돌로 된 휘장이 가로놓여 있었다. 그는 생각했다. 파리아가 보물을 내밀었던 자들의 눈앞에도 이에 못지않게 두꺼운 휘장이 드리워져 있었으리라고.\n\n\"죄수들이 서로 만날 수 있었나?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"오, 아닙니다, 손님. 그건 엄격히 금지되어 있었지요. 하지만 두 사람은 경비병들의 감시를 피해, 한 감방에서 다른 감방으로 통하는 길을 뚫었습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 둘 중 누가 그 길을 뚫었지?\"\n\n\"오, 그야 분명 젊은 쪽이었겠지요. 그는 힘이 세고 부지런했으니까요. 반면에 신부는 늙고 약했습니다. 게다가 그 신부는 마음이 너무 흔들려서 어떤 생각 하나도 끝까지 밀고 나가지 못했답니다.\"\n\n\"눈먼 바보들 같으니.\" 백작이 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"어쨌든, 사정이야 어떻든 간에, 젊은이가 굴을 팠습니다. 어떻게, 무슨 수단으로 했는지는 아무도 모릅니다. 하지만 그는 굴을 팠고, 그 작업의 흔적이 아직도 남아 있지요. 보이십니까?\" 그러고는 횃불을 벽에 들이댔다.\n\n\"아, 그래, 보이는군.\" 백작이 감정에 잠겨 쉰 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"그래서 두 사람은 서로 통하게 되었지요. 얼마나 오래 그랬는지는 아무도 모릅니다. 어느 날 늙은이가 병이 들어 죽었습니다. 자, 젊은이가 무슨 짓을 했는지 알아맞혀 보십시오.\"\n\n\"말해 보게.\"\n\n\"그는 시체를 옮겨다가 자기 침대에 얼굴이 벽을 향하도록 눕혔습니다. 그러고는 빈 감방으로 들어가 입구를 막고, 시체가 담겨 있던 자루 속으로 들어갔지요. 이런 생각을 들어 보신 적 있습니까?\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 눈을 감았다. 죽음의 차가운 이슬에 아직 축축한 거친 천이 얼굴에 닿았을 때 느꼈던 그 모든 감각을 다시 겪는 듯했다.\n\n간수가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"자, 이게 그자의 계획이었습니다. 그는 이프성에서 죽은 자들을 땅에 묻는 줄 알았고, 죄수의 무덤에야 별로 공을 들이지 않으리라 짐작하고는, 어깨로 흙을 밀어 올릴 작정이었지요. 그런데 불행히도 이 성의 방식이 그자의 계획을 어그러뜨렸습니다. 이곳에서는 죽은 자를 결코 묻지 않았거든요. 그저 발에 무거운 포탄을 매달아 바다에 던질 뿐이었지요. 그래서 그렇게 했습니다. 젊은이는 바위 꼭대기에서 던져졌습니다. 이튿날 침대에서 진짜 시체가 발견되었고, 모든 진상이 짐작되었지요. 그 일을 맡았던 자들이 그제야, 전에는 감히 입에 올리지 못했던 사실을 털어놓았으니까요. 시체를 깊은 바다에 던지는 순간, 비명이 들렸는데, 그것이 사라지는 물속에서 거의 곧바로 잦아들었다는 겁니다.\"\n\n백작은 숨을 제대로 쉬지 못했다. 차가운 땀방울이 이마를 타고 흘러내렸고, 가슴은 고뇌로 가득 찼다.\n\n\"아니다.\" 그가 중얼거렸다. \"내가 느낀 의혹은 망각의 시작일 뿐이었어. 하지만 여기서 상처가 다시 벌어지고, 가슴은 또다시 복수에 목마르는구나. 그래, 그 죄수는,\" 그가 소리 내어 말을 이었다. \"그 후로 소식이 들렸나?\"\n\n\"오, 아니요. 물론 들리지 않았지요. 두 가지 중 하나가 일어났으리라는 건 짐작이 가실 겁니다. 납작하게 떨어졌다면 구십 척 높이에서 받은 충격으로 그 자리에서 즉사했을 테고, 아니면 똑바로 떨어졌다면 무게에 끌려 바닥까지 가라앉아 거기 머물렀겠지요. 가엾은 사람!\"\n\n\"그래, 그자가 가엾은가?\" 백작이 물었다.\n\n\"맙소사, 그렇지요. 자기가 살던 곳이었다고는 해도요.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 말인가?\"\n\n\"소문으로는 그자가 해군 장교였는데, 보나파르트파와 음모를 꾸민 죄로 갇혔다고 했습니다.\"\n\n\"위대하도다, 진실이여.\" 백작이 중얼거렸다. \"불도 진실을 태우지 못하고, 물도 진실을 익사시키지 못하는구나! 이렇듯 그 가엾은 선원은 제 이야기를 들려주는 자들의 기억 속에 살아 있다. 그의 끔찍한 사연은 난롯가에서 읊어지고, 그가 공중을 가르며 떨어져 바다에 삼켜졌다는 대목에서는 누구나 몸서리를 친다.\" 그러고는 백작이 소리 내어 덧붙였다. \"그자의 이름은 알려졌나?\"\n\n\"오, 네. 하지만 34호라는 번호로만요.\"\n\n\"오, 빌포르, 빌포르.\" 백작이 중얼거렸다. \"이 장면이 잠 못 이루는 네 밤들을 얼마나 자주 따라다녔겠는가!\"\n\n\"더 보실 것이 있습니까, 손님?\" 관리인이 말했다.\n\n\"그래. 특히 그 가엾은 신부의 방을 보여 준다면 말일세.\"\n\n\"아! 27호 말씀이군요.\"\n\n\"그래, 27호.\" 이름을 물었을 때 신부가 바로 그 말로 벽 너머에서 대답하던 목소리가 들리는 듯하여, 백작이 되뇌었다.\n\"이리 오시지요, 손님.\"\n\n\"잠깐.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"이 방을 마지막으로 한 번 둘러보고 싶네.\"\n\n\"마침 잘됐습니다.\" 안내인이 말했다. \"다른 열쇠를 깜빡하고 안 가져왔거든요.\"\n\n\"가서 가져오게.\"\n\n\"횃불은 두고 가겠습니다, 손님.\"\n\n\"아니, 가져가게. 나는 어둠 속에서도 볼 수 있으니까.\"\n\n\"아니, 손님은 꼭 34호 같으시군요. 그자는 어둠에 어찌나 익숙했던지 감방의 가장 캄캄한 구석에 떨어진 바늘도 볼 수 있었다고들 하던데요.\"\n\n\"그렇게 되기까지 십사 년을 보냈지.\" 백작이 중얼거렸다.\n\n안내인이 횃불을 들고 나갔다. 백작의 말은 옳았다. 몇 초도 채 지나지 않아 그는 대낮처럼 모든 것을 또렷이 볼 수 있었다. 그러자 그는 주위를 둘러보았고, 자신의 감방을 정말로 알아보았다.\n\n\"그래.\" 그가 말했다. \"저기 내가 앉곤 하던 돌이 있고, 저기 벽에 내 어깨가 남긴 자국이 있고, 어느 날 벽에 머리를 부딪쳤을 때 묻은 핏자국이 있구나. 오, 저 숫자들, 어찌 이리 또렷이 기억나는지! 어느 날 아버지가 살아 계실지를 알아보려고 나이를 헤아리고, 메르세데스가 아직 자유로운 몸일지를 알아보려고 그녀의 나이를 헤아리느라 적어 둔 것이지. 그 계산을 끝마치고 나서 나는 잠깐 희망을 품었어. 굶주림과 변심은 미처 셈에 넣지 못했지!\" 그러고는 쓰디쓴 웃음이 백작의 입에서 새어 나왔다.\n\n그는 환영처럼 아버지의 장례와 메르세데스의 결혼을 보았다. 감방 맞은편 벽에는 새겨진 글귀가 보였는데, 그 흰 글자가 푸르스름한 벽 위에 아직 또렷이 남아 있었다.\n\n\"'오, 하느님!'\" 그가 읽었다. \"'제 기억을 지켜 주소서!'\"\n\n\"오, 그래.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"그것이 결국 내 유일한 기도였지. 나는 더 이상 자유를 빌지 않았어. 오직 기억을 빌었지. 미쳐서 모든 것을 잊게 될까 두려웠으니까. 오, 하느님, 당신은 제 기억을 지켜 주셨습니다. 감사합니다, 감사합니다!\"\n\n그 순간 횃불 빛이 벽에 비쳤다. 안내인이 오고 있었다. 몬테크리스토는 그를 맞으러 나갔다.\n\n\"따라오시지요, 손님.\" 안내인은 계단을 오르지 않고 지하 통로를 지나 다른 입구로 그를 데려갔다. 거기서 또다시 몬테크리스토는 수많은 상념에 휩싸였다. 가장 먼저 눈에 들어온 것은 신부가 시간을 헤아리려고 벽에 그어 둔 자오선이었다. 이어 그 가엾은 죄수가 죽음을 맞은 침대의 잔해가 보였다. 지하 감방에서 백작이 겪었던 고뇌와는 달리, 이 광경은 그의 가슴을 부드럽고 감사한 정으로 채워 주었고, 두 눈에서 눈물이 흘러내렸다.\n\n\"여기가 그 미친 신부가 갇혀 있던 곳입니다, 손님. 그리고 저기가 젊은이가 들어왔던 곳이지요.\" 안내인이 아직 막히지 않은 채로 남아 있는 구멍을 가리켰다. \"이 돌의 모양을 보고,\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"어느 학식 있는 신사분이, 두 죄수가 십 년 동안 서로 통할 수 있었으리라는 걸 알아냈습니다. 가엾은 사람들! 그 십 년은 분명 고단한 세월이었겠지요.\"\n\n당테스는 주머니에서 루이 금화 몇 닢을 꺼내, 두 번이나 자신도 모르게 자기를 가엾이 여겼던 그 사내에게 건넸다. 안내인은 그것을 받으며 그저 값나가지 않는 동전 몇 닢이려니 여겼다. 그러나 횃불 빛이 그 참된 가치를 드러냈다.\n\n\"손님.\" 그가 말했다. \"착각하셨습니다. 금화를 주셨군요.\"\n\n\"알고 있네.\"\n\n관리인이 놀란 눈으로 백작을 바라보았다.\n\n\"손님.\" 그가 제 행운을 믿기 어려운 듯 외쳤다. \"손님, 이 너그러우심을 도무지 이해할 수가 없습니다!\"\n\n\"오, 아주 간단한 일이라네, 이 친구야. 나도 한때 선원이었던지라, 자네 이야기가 다른 사람들보다 내 마음을 더 깊이 흔들었던 거지.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 손님, 그토록 후하게 베풀어 주시니, 저도 무언가 드려야 마땅하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"내게 무얼 주겠다는 건가, 친구? 조개껍데기? 짚 세공품? 사양하겠네!\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 손님. 그런 것이 아니라, 이 이야기와 관련된 것입니다.\"\n\n\"정말인가? 그게 뭔가?\"\n\n\"들어 보십시오.\" 안내인이 말했다. \"저는 속으로 생각했지요. '한 죄수가 십오 년이나 살았던 감방에는 늘 무언가 남는 법이지.' 그래서 벽을 두드려 보기 시작했습니다.\"\n\n\"아.\" 몬테크리스토가 신부의 두 은닉처를 떠올리며 외쳤다.\n\n\"한참을 뒤지다가, 침대 머리맡과 난로 근처 바닥에서 텅 빈 소리가 나는 걸 알아냈지요.\"\n\n\"그래.\" 백작이 말했다. \"그래.\"\n\n\"돌을 들어내 보니, 거기에……\"\n\n\"밧줄 사다리와 연장 몇 가지가 있었나?\"\n\n\"그걸 어떻게 아십니까?\" 안내인이 놀라 물었다.\n\n\"내가 아는 게 아니라, 그저 짐작했을 뿐이네. 그런 것들은 대개 죄수의 감방에서 나오는 법이니까.\"\n\n\"맞습니다, 손님. 밧줄 사다리와 연장이었지요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그것들을 아직 가지고 있나?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 손님. 그건 진귀한 물건이라며 탐내는 방문객들에게 팔아 버렸습니다. 하지만 아직 남은 게 하나 있지요.\"\n\n\"그게 뭔가?\" 백작이 조바심을 내며 물었다.\n\n\"천 조각에 글을 적은, 책 비슷한 것입니다.\"\n\n\"가서 가져오게, 친구. 그것이 내가 바라는 그것이라면, 자네에게 좋은 일이 될 걸세.\"\n\n\"얼른 다녀오겠습니다, 손님.\" 그러고는 안내인이 나갔다.\n\n그러자 백작은 죽음이 제단으로 바꾸어 놓은 그 침대 곁에 무릎을 꿇었다.\n\n\"오, 제 두 번째 아버지여.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"제게 자유와 지식과 부를 주신 분이여. 우리보다 한 차원 높은 존재들처럼, 선과 악의 이치를 깨우치셨던 분이여. 무덤 깊은 곳에도 지상에 남은 자들의 목소리에 응답할 수 있는 무언가가 우리 안에 아직 남아 있다면, 죽은 뒤에도 영혼이 우리가 살며 고통받던 곳을 다시 찾아온다면, 그렇다면, 고귀한 마음이여, 숭고한 영혼이여, 제게 베푸셨던 아버지의 사랑으로, 또 제가 당신께 맹세했던 자식의 순종으로, 부디 어떤 징표를, 어떤 계시를 내려 주소서! 제게서 의혹의 잔재를 거두어 주소서. 그것이 확신으로 바뀌지 않는다면, 회한이 되고 말 것이니!\" 백작은 고개를 숙이고 두 손을 맞잡았다.\n\n\"여기 있습니다, 손님.\" 등 뒤에서 목소리가 들렸다.\n\n몬테크리스토는 몸서리치며 일어섰다. 관리인이 파리아 신부가 제 정신의 풍요를 펼쳐 놓았던 그 천 조각들을 내밀었다. 그 필사본은 이탈리아 여러 왕국에 관한 파리아 신부의 대작이었다. 백작은 그것을 황급히 움켜쥐었고, 그의 눈은 곧장 머리글에 가닿았다. 그가 읽었다.\n\n\"너는 용들의 이빨을 뽑을 것이며, 사자들을 발아래 짓밟으리라, 주께서 말씀하시느니라.\"\n\n\"아.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"여기 내 대답이 있구나. 고맙습니다, 아버지, 고맙습니다.\" 그러고는 주머니를 더듬어 작은 수첩 하나를 꺼냈는데, 그 안에는 한 장에 천 프랑짜리 지폐가 열 장 들어 있었다.\n\n\"자.\" 그가 말했다. \"이 수첩을 받게.\"\n\n\"제게 주시는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"그래. 다만 내가 떠난 뒤에 열어 본다는 조건일세.\" 그러고는 가장 값진 보석보다도 더 소중한, 방금 찾아낸 그 보물을 가슴에 품은 채, 복도를 빠져나가 배에 이르러 외쳤다. \"마르세유로!\"\n\n그러고는 떠나가며, 그 음산한 감옥에 두 눈을 고정했다.\n\n\"화 있을지어다.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"나를 저 비참한 감옥에 가둔 자들에게! 또한 화 있을지어다, 내가 그 안에 있다는 것을 잊어버린 자들에게!\"\n\n카탈루냐 마을을 다시 지나칠 때, 백작은 돌아서서 망토에 얼굴을 파묻고 한 여인의 이름을 중얼거렸다. 승리는 완전했다. 두 번이나 그는 의혹을 이겨 냈다. 거의 사랑에 가까운, 그 다정한 목소리로 그가 부른 이름은 바로 하이데였다.\n\n뭍에 내린 백작은 묘지로 향했다. 거기서 모렐을 만나리라고 확신했다. 그 역시 십 년 전, 경건한 마음으로 무덤 하나를 찾았으나 헛수고로 끝났었다. 수백만 프랑을 지니고 프랑스로 돌아온 그였지만, 굶주림으로 세상을 떠난 아버지의 무덤은 찾지 못했다. 모렐이 그 자리에 십자가를 세워 두긴 했으나, 그것은 쓰러졌고, 무덤 파는 일꾼이 묘지의 낡은 나무들을 다 그러하듯 그것마저 태워 버렸던 것이다.\n\n그 훌륭한 상인은 더 운이 좋았다. 자식들의 품에서 눈을 감은 그는, 그들 손에 의해 이태 앞서 영원의 세계로 떠난 아내 곁에 나란히 묻혔다. 두 사람의 이름이 새겨진 커다란 대리석 판 두 개가, 난간이 둘리고 사이프러스 네 그루가 그늘을 드리운 작은 울타리 양편에 놓여 있었다. 모렐은 그 나무 한 그루에 몸을 기댄 채, 넋 나간 듯 무덤들에 두 눈을 고정하고 있었다. 그의 슬픔이 어찌나 깊은지, 거의 의식을 잃은 듯했다.\n\n\"막시밀리앙.\" 백작이 말했다. \"무덤을 바라보아서는 안 되네. 저기를 보게.\" 그러고는 위쪽을 가리켰다.\n\n\"죽은 자들은 어디에나 있지요.\" 모렐이 말했다. \"파리를 떠날 때 백작님께서 직접 제게 그리 말씀하지 않으셨습니까?\"\n\n\"막시밀리앙.\" 백작이 말했다. \"오는 길에 자네는 며칠 마르세유에 머물게 해 달라고 내게 청했지. 아직도 그러길 바라나?\"\n\n\"제겐 바라는 것이 없습니다, 백작님. 다만 이곳이 다른 어디보다 덜 괴롭게 시간을 보낼 수 있을 것 같다는 생각이 들 뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 더욱 잘됐군. 나는 자네를 떠나야 하니까. 하지만 자네의 약속을 가지고 가는 거겠지, 그렇지 않나?\"\n\n\"아, 백작님, 저는 그 약속을 잊고 말 겁니다.\"\n\n\"아니, 자네는 잊지 않을 걸세. 자네는 명예를 아는 사람이니까, 모렐. 자네는 맹세를 했고, 또 한 번 하려 하고 있으니 말일세.\"\n\n\"오, 백작님, 부디 저를 가엾이 여겨 주십시오. 저는 너무도 불행합니다.\"\n\n\"나는 자네보다 훨씬 더 불행했던 사람을 알고 있네, 모렐.\"\n\n\"그럴 리가요!\"\n\n\"아아.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"곁에서 신음하는 자들보다 자기가 훨씬 더 불행하다고 늘 믿는 것, 그것이 우리 인간의 약함일세!\"\n\n\"세상에서 사랑하고 바라던 모든 것을 잃은 사람보다 더 비참한 사람이 어디 있겠습니까?\"\n\n\"들어 보게, 모렐. 내가 자네에게 들려줄 이야기에 귀를 기울이게. 나는 자네처럼 한 여인에게 모든 행복의 희망을 걸었던 사람을 알고 있네. 그는 젊었고, 사랑하는 늙은 아버지가 있었으며, 흠모하는 약혼녀가 있었지. 그는 막 그녀와 결혼하려던 참이었네. 그런데 운명의 변덕 하나가, 그 변덕이란 만일 섭리가 나중에 모든 것이 한 목적으로 이끄는 수단일 뿐임을 증명해 보이지 않았더라면 우리로 하여금 거의 섭리의 선하심을 의심케 했을 그런 변덕이, 그 변덕 하나가 그에게서 연인을, 그가 꿈꾸어 온 미래를 (눈이 먼 그는 자기가 오직 현재만을 읽을 수 있다는 것을 잊고 있었으니까) 앗아 가고, 그를 지하 감방에 던져 넣었다네.\"\n\n\"아.\" 모렐이 말했다. \"지하 감방이야 일주일이나 한 달, 길어야 일 년이면 벗어나는 것 아닙니까.\"\n\n\"그는 거기서 십사 년을 머물렀네, 모렐.\" 백작이 젊은이의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 말했다. 막시밀리앙은 몸서리쳤다.\n\n\"십사 년이라니!\" 그가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"십사 년일세!\" 백작이 되뇌었다. \"그동안 그는 숱한 절망의 순간을 겪었지. 그 역시, 모렐, 자네처럼 스스로를 세상에서 가장 불행한 사람이라 여겼다네.\"\n\n\"그래서요?\" 모렐이 물었다.\n\n\"그래서, 절망의 절정에서 하느님이 사람의 손을 빌려 그를 도우셨지. 처음에는 어쩌면 그가 주님의 무한한 자비를 알아보지 못했을지도 모르네. 하지만 끝내 그는 인내심을 가지고 기다렸지. 어느 날 그는 기적처럼 감옥을 벗어났다네. 완전히 달라진 모습으로, 부유하고 강력하게 말이지. 그가 가장 먼저 부르짖은 것은 아버지였네. 그러나 그 아버지는 이미 세상을 떠난 뒤였지.\"\n\n\"제 아버지도 돌아가셨습니다.\" 모렐이 말했다.\n\n\"그래. 하지만 자네 아버지는 자네의 품에서, 행복하게, 존경받으며, 부유하게, 천수를 누리고 돌아가셨네. 그의 아버지는 가난하게, 절망 속에서, 섭리마저 거의 의심하며 돌아가셨지. 그리고 십 년 뒤 아들이 그 무덤을 찾았을 때, 무덤은 사라지고 없었고, 아무도 '자네가 그토록 사랑한 아버지가 여기 잠들어 있네'라고 말해 줄 수 없었네.\"\n\n\"오!\" 모렐이 외쳤다.\n\n\"그러니 그는 자네보다 더 불행한 아들이었지, 모렐. 아버지의 무덤조차 찾지 못했으니 말일세.\"\n\n\"하지만 그래도 그에게는 사랑하던 여인이 아직 남아 있지 않았습니까?\"\n\n\"잘못 알았네, 모렐. 그 여인은……\"\n\n\"죽었습니까?\"\n\n\"그보다 더 나빴지. 그녀는 변심하여, 제 약혼자를 박해한 자들 가운데 하나와 혼인했네. 그러니 알겠나, 모렐. 그는 자네보다 더 불행한 연인이었던 거지.\"\n\n\"그래, 그는 위안을 얻었습니까?\"\n\n\"적어도 평안만은 얻었네.\"\n\n\"그럼 언젠가 행복해지기를 바라고 있습니까?\"\n\n\"그렇게 바라고 있지, 막시밀리앙.\"\n\n젊은이는 고개를 가슴 위로 떨구었다.\n\n\"제 약속을 드리겠습니다.\" 잠시 침묵한 끝에 그가 몬테크리스토에게 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"다만 잊지 말아 주십시오……\"\n\n\"10월 5일에, 모렐, 몬테크리스토섬에서 자네를 기다리겠네. 4일에는 바스티아 항구에서 요트 한 척이 자네를 기다릴 걸세. 이름은 에우루스호일세. 자네가 선장에게 이름을 대면, 그가 자네를 내게로 데려다줄 걸세. 알겠지? 그렇지 않나?\"\n\n\"하지만 백작님, 10월 5일이라면 그날이……\"\n\n\"이 사람아.\" 백작이 대답했다. \"사내의 약속이 지닌 값어치를 모른단 말인가! 자네가 그날 죽기를 바란다면 내가 도와주겠다고 스무 번도 더 말하지 않았나. 모렐, 잘 있게!\"\n\n\"저를 떠나시는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"그래. 이탈리아에 볼일이 있네. 자네를 불행과의 싸움 속에 홀로 남겨 두고 가네. 하느님이 택한 자들을 그 발치로 들어 올리도록 보내신, 그 힘찬 날개를 가진 독수리와 함께 홀로 남겨 두고 가는 거지. 가니메데스의 이야기는, 막시밀리앙, 한낱 우화가 아니라 하나의 비유라네.\"\n\n\"언제 떠나십니까?\"\n\n\"지금 곧. 증기선이 기다리고 있네. 한 시간 뒤면 나는 자네에게서 멀리 떨어져 있겠지. 항구까지 나를 배웅해 주겠나, 막시밀리앙?\"\n\n\"전 백작님의 사람입니다, 백작님.\"\n\n모렐은 백작을 항구까지 배웅했다. 검은 굴뚝에서는 하얀 증기가 깃털 장식처럼 피어오르고 있었다. 증기선은 곧 사라졌고, 한 시간 뒤에는, 백작이 말한 대로, 밤안개 속 수평선 너머로 거의 분간할 수 없게 되었다.\n\n# Chapter 114. Peppino\n\n증기선이 모르지우곶 뒤로 사라지던 바로 그 무렵, 피렌체에서 로마로 가는 길을 역마차로 달리던 한 사내가 막 아콰펜덴테라는 작은 마을을 지나고 있었다. 그는 의심을 사지 않으면서도 상당한 거리를 주파할 만큼 빠르게 달렸다. 이 사내는 두꺼운 외투, 정확히는 프록코트를 걸치고 있었는데, 여행으로 다소 해진 그 옷에는 여전히 산뜻하고 빛나는 레지옹 도뇌르 훈장 리본이 달려 있었다. 그 안에 받쳐 입은 상의에도 같은 훈장이 장식되어 있었다. 그는 이런 표시뿐만 아니라 마부에게 말을 거는 억양만으로도 프랑스 사람임을 알아볼 수 있었다.\n\n그가 보편의 나라 태생이라는 또 다른 증거는, 음악에서 쓰이는 용어 말고는 다른 이탈리아 말을 전혀 모른다는 사실에서 드러났다. 그 용어들은 피가로의 \"고댐\"처럼 온갖 언어적 요구를 다 채워 주었다. \"알레그로!\" 그는 오르막마다 마부들에게 외쳤다. \"모데라토!\" 내리막에서는 이렇게 소리쳤다. 그리고 하느님은 아시리라, 아콰펜덴테를 거쳐 로마와 피렌체 사이에는 언덕이 충분히도 많다는 것을! 이 두 마디는 그 말을 듣는 사내들을 무척 즐겁게 했다. 로마가 처음으로 보이기 시작하는 지점인 라스토르타에 이르렀을 때, 이 여행자는 흔히 낯선 이들을 일으켜 세워 성 베드로 대성당의 둥근 지붕을 보려 애쓰게 만드는 그런 열광적인 호기심을 조금도 내비치지 않았다. 그 둥근 지붕은 다른 어떤 것이 분간되기도 한참 전부터 보이는 법인데도 말이다. 아니, 그는 그저 주머니에서 수첩을 꺼내 네 겹으로 접힌 종이 한 장을 빼냈고, 그것을 거의 경건하다 할 만한 태도로 살펴본 뒤 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"좋아! 아직 가지고 있군!\"\n\n마차는 포폴로 문으로 들어와 왼쪽으로 돌더니 에스파냐 호텔 앞에 멈췄다. 우리의 옛 지인인 늙은 파스트리니가 모자를 손에 쥔 채 문 앞에서 여행자를 맞았다. 여행자는 마차에서 내려 푸짐한 저녁을 주문하고는 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회의 주소를 물었다. 그 상회는 로마에서 가장 이름난 곳 가운데 하나였으므로 주소는 즉시 알려졌다. 그곳은 성 베드로 대성당 근처 비아 데이 반키에 있었다.\n\n다른 곳에서와 마찬가지로 로마에서도 역마차의 도착은 하나의 사건이었다. 맨발에 팔꿈치가 다 해진 마리우스와 그라쿠스의 젊은 후예 열 명이 한 손은 허리에 얹고 다른 손은 머리 위로 우아하게 구부린 채 여행자와 역마차와 말들을 빤히 바라보았다. 여기에 교황령 출신의 부랑아 쉰 명가량이 더해졌는데, 이들은 물이 불어나면 산탄젤로 다리에서 티베르강으로 뛰어들어 푼돈을 벌었다. 그런데 파리의 부랑아들보다 운이 좋은 이 로마의 거리 떠돌이들은 모든 언어, 특히 프랑스어를 알아들었으므로, 여행자가 방과 저녁 식사를 주문하고 마침내 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회로 가는 길을 묻는 것을 다 들었다.\n\n그 결과, 새로 온 사내가 안내인과 함께 호텔을 나설 때, 한 사내가 다른 건달들 무리에서 빠져나와 여행자에게 들키지도 않고 안내인의 주의도 전혀 끌지 않은 채, 파리의 경찰 첩자나 부릴 법한 능숙한 솜씨로 그 낯선 사내를 뒤쫓았다.\n\n프랑스 사람은 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회에 닿고 싶은 마음이 어찌나 조급했던지 말에 마구를 채우기를 기다리지도 않았다. 마차더러 길에서 자기를 따라잡거나 은행가의 문 앞에서 기다리라는 말을 남기고 떠났다. 그는 마차가 도착하기 전에 그곳에 닿았다. 프랑스 사람은 안으로 들어가면서 대기실에 안내인을 남겨 두었고, 안내인은 곧장 로마의 은행이며 교회며 박물관이며 극장 문 앞에 늘 어슬렁거리는 부지런한 건달 두세 명과 이야기를 나누기 시작했다. 프랑스 사람과 함께 그를 뒤쫓던 사내도 들어왔다. 프랑스 사람이 안쪽 문을 두드리고 첫 번째 방으로 들어서자, 그의 그림자도 똑같이 했다.\n\n\"톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회입니까?\" 낯선 사내가 물었다.\n\n첫 번째 책상에 앉은 측근 직원이 신호를 보내자 사환 하나가 일어났다.\n\n\"어느 분이라고 전할까요?\" 사환이 말했다.\n\n\"당글라르 남작이오.\"\n\n\"따라오시지요.\" 사환이 말했다.\n\n문이 열리고, 사환과 남작은 그 안으로 사라졌다. 당글라르를 뒤쫓던 사내는 벤치에 앉았다. 직원은 그 뒤로 오 분 동안 계속 글을 썼다. 사내는 깊은 침묵을 지키며 미동도 하지 않았다. 그러다 직원의 펜이 종이 위에서 멈췄다. 그는 고개를 들더니, 아무도 없다는 것을 완전히 확신한 듯한 표정으로 말했다.\n\n\"아, 하, 여기 있었군, 페피노!\"\n\n\"네.\" 짤막한 대답이 돌아왔다. \"저 덩치 큰 양반한테 챙길 만한 게 있다는 걸 알아내셨나요?\"\n\n\"내 공이랄 건 없어. 우리는 미리 통보를 받았으니까.\"\n\n\"그럼 저 사람이 여기서 무슨 일을 보는지도 아시겠군요.\"\n\n\"제기랄, 돈을 찾으러 온 거지. 다만 얼마인지는 나도 몰라!\"\n\n\"곧 알게 되실 겁니다, 친구.\"\n\n\"좋아요, 다만 지난번처럼 거짓 정보는 주지 마세요.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 소린가? 누구 얘기를 하는 거야? 지난번에 여기서 삼천 크라운을 가져간 그 영국 사람 말인가?\"\n\n\"아닙니다. 그자는 정말 삼천 크라운이 있었고, 우리도 그걸 찾아냈죠. 제가 말하는 건 러시아 공작 말입니다. 삼만 리브르가 있다고 하셨는데, 우리는 겨우 이만 이천밖에 못 찾았어요.\"\n\n\"네놈들이 제대로 뒤지지 못한 거겠지.\"\n\n\"루이지 밤파가 직접 뒤졌습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그자가 빚을 갚았거나……\"\n\n\"러시아 사람이 그럴 리가요?\"\n\n\"아니면 돈을 다 써버렸나?\" \"그럴 수도 있겠네요, 따지고 보면.\"\n\n\"틀림없어. 하지만 내가 살펴볼 수 있게 가만있어야 해. 안 그러면 저 프랑스 사람이 거래를 끝내 버려서 내가 액수를 모르게 될 테니까.\"\n\n페피노는 고개를 끄덕이더니 주머니에서 묵주를 꺼내 기도문 몇 마디를 중얼거리기 시작했고, 그동안 직원은 당글라르와 사환이 나간 바로 그 문으로 사라졌다. 십 분쯤 지나 직원은 환한 얼굴로 돌아왔다.\n\"그래서요?\" 페피노가 자기 동료에게 물었다.\n\n\"기쁜 일이야, 기쁜 일이라고. 액수가 어마어마해!\"\n\n\"오백만이나 육백만쯤 되지요?\"\n\n\"그래, 너도 액수를 알고 있구나.\"\n\n\"몬테크리스토 백작의 영수증을 받고서요?\"\n\n\"아니, 어떻게 이 모든 걸 그렇게 훤히 알고 있는 거지?\"\n\n\"우리가 미리 통보를 받았다고 말씀드렸잖습니까.\"\n\n\"그럼 왜 내게 물어보는 거야?\"\n\n\"제대로 된 사람을 잡았는지 확실히 해 두려고요.\"\n\n\"그래, 정말 그자가 맞아. 오백만이라, 제법 큰돈이지, 안 그래, 페피노?\"\n\n\"쉿, 우리 양반이 오네요!\" 직원은 펜을 잡았고 페피노는 묵주를 잡았다. 문이 열렸을 때 한 사람은 글을 쓰고 다른 사람은 기도를 하고 있었다. 당글라르는 기쁨으로 환히 빛났다. 은행가가 그를 문까지 배웅했다. 페피노가 당글라르를 뒤따랐다.\n\n약속대로 마차가 문 앞에서 기다리고 있었다. 안내인이 마차 문을 열어 잡고 있었다. 안내인이란 무슨 일에든 손을 보태는 쓸모 있는 사람들이다. 당글라르는 스무 살 청년처럼 마차 안으로 훌쩍 뛰어올랐다. 안내인은 다시 문을 닫고 마부 곁으로 뛰어올랐다. 페피노는 뒷자리에 올라탔다.\n\n\"각하, 성 베드로 대성당을 둘러보시겠습니까?\" 안내인이 물었다.\n\n\"나는 구경하러 로마에 온 게 아니오.\" 당글라르가 큰 소리로 말하더니, 탐욕스러운 미소를 지으며 나직이 덧붙였다. \"나는 손에 넣으러 온 거요!\" 그러고는 방금 편지 한 통을 넣어 둔 수첩을 톡톡 두드렸다.\n\n\"그럼 각하께서는 어디로……\"\n\n\"호텔로.\"\n\n\"카사 파스트리니!\" 안내인이 마부에게 말했고, 마차는 빠르게 달려 나갔다.\n\n십 분 뒤 남작은 자기 방으로 들어갔고, 페피노는 호텔 문밖 벤치에 자리를 잡았다. 그러기 전에 그는 이 장 첫머리에서 보았던 마리우스와 그라쿠스의 후예 가운데 한 명의 귀에 무언가를 속삭였는데, 그 아이는 곧장 카피톨로 이어지는 길을 전속력으로 달려 내려갔다. 당글라르는 피곤하고 졸렸으므로 수첩을 베개 밑에 넣어 두고 잠자리에 들었다. 페피노는 시간이 좀 남아서 짐꾼들과 모라 놀이를 한 판 했다가 삼 크라운을 잃었고, 그 분풀이로 오르비에토 포도주를 한 병 마셨다.\n\n이튿날 아침 당글라르는 일찍 잠자리에 들었는데도 늦게 일어났다. 닷새인가 엿새 동안, 설령 잤다 해도 깊이 잠들지 못했던 것이다. 그는 푸짐하게 아침을 먹고는, 자기 말마따나 영원의 도시의 아름다움에는 도무지 관심이 없어 정오에 역마를 주문했다. 그러나 당글라르는 경찰의 절차와 역참장의 게으름은 미처 헤아리지 못했다. 말은 두 시가 되어서야 도착했고, 안내인은 세 시가 되어서야 통행증을 가져왔다.\n\n이 모든 준비로 파스트리니 영감의 문 앞에는 구경꾼들이 잔뜩 모여들었다. 마리우스와 그라쿠스의 후예들도 빠지지 않았다. 남작은 돈을 노리고 그를 \"각하\"라 부르는 군중 사이를 의기양양하게 걸어 나갔다. 그동안 남작이라는 호칭으로 만족해 온 당글라르는 각하라는 칭호에 다소 우쭐해졌고, 거지들에게 은화 열두 닢을 나눠 주었다. 거지들은 열두 닢만 더 주면 기꺼이 그를 \"전하\"라 불러 줄 태세였다.\n\n\"어느 길로 갑니까?\" 마부가 이탈리아어로 물었다.\n\n\"안코나 길로.\" 남작이 대답했다. 파스트리니 영감이 질문과 대답을 통역했고, 말들은 질주해 나갔다.\n\n당글라르는 베네치아로 가서 재산의 일부를 받은 다음 빈으로 가 나머지를 찾을 생각이었고, 환락의 도시라고 들은 그 빈에 거처를 마련할 작정이었다.\n\n로마를 벗어나 삼 리외도 채 못 갔을 때 날이 저물기 시작했다. 당글라르는 이렇게 늦게 출발할 생각이 아니었기에, 알았다면 머물렀을 것이다. 그는 고개를 내밀고 마부에게 다음 마을까지 얼마나 걸리겠느냐고 물었다. \"논 카피스코\"(못 알아듣겠는데요)라는 대답이 돌아왔다. 당글라르는 \"좋아\"라는 뜻으로 고개를 숙였다. 마차는 다시 달리기 시작했다.\n\n\"첫 번째 역참에 멈추도록 하지.\" 당글라르는 혼잣말을 했다.\n\n그는 전날 저녁 느꼈던, 그리고 그토록 단잠을 안겨 주었던 그 자족감을 여전히 느끼고 있었다. 그는 이중 용수철이 달린 좋은 영국제 사륜마차에 호사스럽게 몸을 뻗고 있었다. 훌륭한 말 네 마리가 전속력으로 마차를 끌고 있었고, 다음 역참까지는 칠 리외 거리라는 것도 알고 있었다. 그토록 운 좋게 파산한 이 은행가에게 무슨 사색거리가 떠오를 수 있었겠는가?\n\n당글라르는 십 분 동안 파리에 있는 아내를 생각했고, 또 십 분 동안은 다르밀리 양과 함께 여행 중인 딸을 생각했다. 그리고 같은 시간 동안 채권자들과 그들의 돈을 어떻게 쓸지를 생각했다. 그러고 나서는 더 이상 떠올릴 거리가 없자 눈을 감고 잠이 들었다. 이따금 유난히 심한 덜컹거림에 눈을 뜨면, 자신이 여전히 부서진 수도교가 빽빽이 흩어진 그 똑같은 땅 위를 빠른 속도로 실려 가고 있음을 느꼈다. 그 수도교들은 경주를 하다가 그대로 굳어 버린 화강암 거인들처럼 보였다. 그러나 밤은 춥고 침침하고 비가 내렸으므로, 여행자에게는 창밖으로 고개를 내밀어 \"논 카피스코\"라는 대답밖에 할 줄 모르는 마부에게 묻는 것보다 따뜻한 마차 안에 있는 편이 훨씬 더 편했다.\n\n그래서 당글라르는 역참에 닿으면 틀림없이 깨어날 거라고 스스로 다독이며 계속 잤다. 마차가 멈췄다. 당글라르는 그토록 바라던 지점에 도착한 줄 알았다. 그는 눈을 뜨고 창밖을 내다보며, 어느 마을 한복판이거나 적어도 어느 촌락에 와 있으리라 기대했다. 그러나 그가 본 것은 폐허처럼 보이는 무언가뿐이었고, 그 안에서 서너 명의 사내가 그림자처럼 오가고 있었다.\n\n당글라르는 마부가 와서 구간이 끝났으니 삯을 달라고 하기를 잠시 기다렸다. 그는 그 기회를 틈타 새 마부에게 다시 길을 물을 작정이었다. 그런데 누구도 여행자에게 돈을 요구하지 않은 채 말들의 마구가 풀리고 다른 말들로 바뀌었다. 당글라르는 놀라서 문을 열었다. 그러나 억센 손이 그를 도로 밀어 넣었고, 마차는 다시 굴러갔다. 남작은 완전히 잠이 깼다.\n\n\"이봐?\" 그가 마부에게 말했다. \"이봐, 미오 카로?\"\n\n이것은 남작이 딸이 카발칸티와 함께 이탈리아 이중창을 부르는 것을 들으며 익힌 또 다른 이탈리아어 한 토막이었다. 그러나 미오 카로는 대답이 없었다. 당글라르는 창문을 열었다.\n\n\"이보게, 친구.\" 그가 틈새로 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"우리가 어디로 가는 건가?\"\n\n\"덴트로 라 테스타!\" 위협적인 몸짓과 함께 엄숙하고 위압적인 목소리가 대답했다.\n\n당글라르는 덴트로 라 테스타가 \"머리를 집어넣어!\"라는 뜻이라고 생각했다. 그는 이탈리아어 실력이 빠르게 늘고 있었다. 그는 약간의 불안감과 함께 그 말에 따랐고, 그 불안감은 시시각각 커져, 여행을 시작할 때처럼 텅 비어 있던 그의 머릿속을 여행자를, 더구나 당글라르 같은 처지의 여행자를 깨어 있게 만들기 십상인 온갖 생각으로 가득 채웠다. 그의 눈은 강한 감정의 첫 순간에 사물을 또렷이 보게 해 주는, 그러나 그 뒤로 너무 혹사당해 이내 흐려지는 그런 능력을 띠었다. 놀라기 전에 우리는 정확히 본다. 놀라는 순간에는 두 개로 겹쳐 본다. 그리고 놀라고 난 뒤에는 곤경밖에 보이지 않는다. 당글라르는 외투를 걸친 한 사내가 마차의 오른편에서 말을 달리는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"헌병이로군!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"내가 프랑스에서 교황청 당국으로 보낸 전보에 걸려든 건가?\"\n\n그는 불안을 끝장내기로 마음먹었다. \"나를 어디로 데려가는 거요?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"덴트로 라 테스타.\" 똑같은 목소리가 똑같이 위협적인 억양으로 대답했다.\n\n당글라르는 왼쪽으로 고개를 돌렸다. 또 다른 사내가 그쪽에서 말을 달리고 있었다.\n\n\"틀림없어.\" 당글라르가 이마에 땀을 흘리며 말했다. \"내가 체포된 게 분명해.\" 그러고는 사륜마차 등받이에 몸을 던졌는데, 이번에는 자려는 게 아니라 생각하기 위해서였다.\n\n곧이어 달이 떠올랐다. 그러자 그는 아까 눈여겨보았던 그 거대한 수도교들, 그 돌 유령들을 다시 보게 되었는데, 다만 그때는 오른편에 있던 것이 지금은 왼편에 있었다. 그는 마차가 원을 그리며 자기를 로마로 도로 데려가고 있음을 깨달았다.\n\n\"오, 불운하구나!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"저들이 내 체포 영장을 받아 둔 게 틀림없어.\"\n\n마차는 무시무시한 속도로 계속 굴러갔다. 공포의 한 시간이 흘렀다. 지나치는 곳마다 그들이 돌아가는 길 위에 있음을 알려 주었기 때문이다. 마침내 그는 어두운 덩어리 하나를 보았고, 마차가 거기에 부딪힐 것만 같았다. 그러나 마차는 한쪽으로 방향을 틀어 장벽을 뒤로하고 지나갔고, 당글라르는 그것이 로마를 두른 성벽 가운데 하나임을 알아보았다.\n\n\"맙소사!\" 당글라르가 외쳤다. \"우리는 로마로 돌아가는 게 아니야. 그렇다면 나를 뒤쫓는 건 사법 당국이 아니로구나! 오, 자비로운 하느님. 또 다른 생각이 떠오르는데, 만에 하나 저들이……\"\n\n그의 머리카락이 곤두섰다. 그는 파리에서는 거의 믿지 않던, 로마 산적들에 관한 그 흥미진진한 이야기들을 떠올렸다. 그는 자기 딸 외제니와 알베르 드 모르세르의 결혼이 추진되던 무렵 알베르가 들려주었던 그 모험담을 떠올렸다. \"어쩌면 강도들인지도 몰라.\" 그가 중얼거렸다.\n\n바로 그때 마차가 자갈길보다 단단한 무언가 위를 굴러갔다. 당글라르는 길 양옆으로 조심스레 눈길을 던졌고, 기묘한 형태의 기념물들을 보았다. 이제 그의 머릿속에 모르세르가 들려준 모든 세부가 떠올랐고, 그것을 자기 처지와 견주어 보니 자신이 아피아 가도 위에 있는 게 틀림없다는 확신이 들었다. 왼편 골짜기 같은 곳에서 그는 둥글게 파인 구덩이를 발견했다. 그것은 카라칼라 원형 경기장이었다. 마차 옆에서 달리던 사내가 한마디 하자 마차가 멈췄다. 동시에 문이 열렸다. \"셴디!\" 명령조의 목소리가 외쳤다.\n\n당글라르는 즉시 내렸다. 아직 이탈리아어를 할 줄은 몰랐지만 알아듣기는 아주 잘했다. 그는 산 사람도 죽은 사람도 아닌 몰골로 주위를 둘러보았다. 마부 말고도 네 명의 사내가 그를 에워싸고 있었다.\n\n\"디 콰.\" 사내들 가운데 하나가 아피아 가도에서 빠지는 작은 오솔길을 내려가며 말했다. 당글라르는 저항 없이 안내인을 따라갔고, 나머지 세 명이 뒤따르는지 굳이 돌아볼 필요도 없었다. 그래도 그들은 보초들처럼 서로 같은 간격을 두고 자리한 듯했다. 십 분쯤 걸어가는 동안 당글라르는 안내인과 단 한마디도 나누지 않았고, 어느새 작은 언덕과 키 큰 잡초 덤불 사이에 와 있었다. 말없이 선 세 사내가 삼각형을 이루었고, 그가 그 한가운데였다. 그는 말을 하고 싶었지만 혀가 움직이려 하지 않았다.\n\n\"아반티!\" 똑같이 날카롭고 위압적인 목소리가 말했다.\n\n이번에는 당글라르가 알아들을 이유가 갑절이었다. 그 말과 몸짓이 화자의 뜻을 설명해 주지 못했다 해도, 뒤에서 걷던 사내가 어찌나 거칠게 떠밀었던지 그는 안내인에게 부딪혔고, 그것으로 뜻은 분명히 전해졌다. 이 안내인은 다름 아닌 우리의 친구 페피노였는데, 그는 도마뱀이나 족제비나 길로 여길 법한 통로를 따라 키 큰 잡초 덤불 속으로 뛰어들었다.\n\n페피노는 빽빽한 덤불이 드리운 바위 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 반쯤 갈라진 그 바위는 젊은이에게 통로를 내주었고, 그는 동화 속 악령들처럼 사라졌다. 당글라르를 뒤따르던 사내의 목소리와 몸짓이 그에게도 똑같이 하라고 명했다. 더 이상 의심의 여지가 없었다. 이 파산자는 로마 산적들의 손아귀에 떨어진 것이다. 당글라르는 두 가지 위태로운 처지 사이에 놓여 두려움 덕에 도리어 용감해진 사람답게 행동했다. 캄파냐의 바위틈을 비집고 들어가기에는 전혀 어울리지 않는 큰 배를 가졌는데도, 그는 페피노처럼 미끄러져 내려갔고, 눈을 감은 채 두 발로 떨어졌다. 땅에 닿자 그는 눈을 떴다.\n\n통로는 넓었지만 어두웠다. 이제 자기 영역에 들어왔으니 얼굴이 알려져도 상관없다고 여긴 페피노는 부싯돌을 쳐서 횃불을 밝혔다. 다른 두 사내가 후위를 이루며 당글라르를 뒤따라 내려왔고, 당글라르가 멈출 때마다 그를 떠밀어 완만한 비탈을 따라 두 갈래 통로가 교차하는 지점에 이르렀다. 벽에는 무덤들이 위아래로 층층이 파여 있었는데, 그것은 흰 돌과 대비되어, 마치 죽은 자의 얼굴에서 보이는 그런 크고 어두운 눈을 부릅뜨고 있는 듯했다. 보초 하나가 자기 기병총의 고리를 왼손에 부딪쳐 소리를 냈다.\n\n\"거기 누구냐?\" 그가 외쳤다.\n\n\"친구다, 친구!\" 페피노가 말했다. \"그런데 두목은 어디 계시지?\"\n\n\"저기.\" 보초가 어깨 너머로, 바위를 파서 만든 널찍한 지하실을 가리키며 말했다. 그 안의 불빛이 큰 아치형 입구들을 통해 통로로 새어 나오고 있었다.\n\n\"멋진 노획물입니다, 두목. 멋진 노획물이에요!\" 페피노가 이탈리아어로 말하더니, 당글라르의 외투 깃을 잡고 문처럼 생긴 입구로 그를 끌고 갔다. 그들은 그 입구를 통해 두목이 거처로 삼은 듯한 방으로 들어갔다.\n\n\"이자가 그 사람인가?\" 플루타르코스의 『알렉산드로스 전기』를 골똘히 읽고 있던 두목이 물었다.\n\n\"바로 그자입니다, 두목. 그자예요.\"\n\n\"좋아, 어디 좀 보여 줘 봐.\"\n\n다소 무례한 이 명령에 페피노는 횃불을 당글라르의 얼굴 쪽으로 들어 올렸고, 당글라르는 속눈썹이 그을릴까 봐 황급히 물러났다. 동요한 그의 얼굴에는 창백하고 흉측한 공포가 어려 있었다.\n\n\"이 사람은 지쳤군.\" 두목이 말했다. \"잠자리로 데려가.\"\n\n\"오.\" 당글라르가 중얼거렸다. \"저 잠자리란 게 아마도 벽에 파인 관들 중 하나일 테고, 내가 누리게 될 잠이란 어둠 속에서 번득이는 저 단검들 중 하나에 찔려 맞는 죽음이겠지.\"\n\n방 안쪽 마른 잎이나 늑대 가죽으로 된 잠자리에서, 알베르 드 모르세르에게는 『카이사르 전기』를 읽는 모습으로, 당글라르에게는 『알렉산드로스 전기』를 들여다보는 모습으로 발견된 그 사내의 동료들이 이제 몸을 일으켰다. 은행가는 신음을 내뱉으며 안내인을 따라갔다. 그는 애원하지도 부르짖지도 않았다. 그에게는 더 이상 힘도 의지도 기력도 감각도 남아 있지 않았다. 그는 그들이 이끄는 대로 따라갔다. 마침내 그는 계단 아래에 이르렀고, 기계적으로 발을 대여섯 번 들어 올렸다. 그러자 그의 앞에 낮은 문이 열렸고, 그는 이마를 부딪치지 않으려고 고개를 숙인 채 바위를 깎아 만든 작은 방으로 들어갔다. 그 방은 비어 있긴 했어도 깨끗했고, 땅속 헤아릴 수 없는 깊이에 있는데도 건조했다. 한쪽 구석에는 염소 가죽을 덮은 마른 풀 침상이 놓여 있었다. 당글라르는 그것을 보자 무언가 안전을 약속받은 듯한 기분에 얼굴이 밝아졌다.\n\n\"오, 하느님 감사합니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"진짜 침대로구나!\"\n\n이것은 그가 한 시간 안에 하느님의 이름을 부른 두 번째 일이었다. 그는 그 전 십 년 동안 그런 일이 없었다.\n\n\"에코!\" 안내인이 말하고는, 당글라르를 방 안으로 떠밀어 넣고 문을 닫았다.\n\n빗장이 삐걱 소리를 냈고, 당글라르는 갇힌 신세가 되었다. 빗장이 없었다 해도, 그가 성 세바스티아노 카타콤을 지키며 한 두목 주위에 진을 친 그 수비대 한복판을 빠져나가기란 불가능했을 것이다. 그 두목이 바로 독자들이 알아챘을 그 유명한 루이지 밤파였다.\n\n당글라르 역시 그 산적을 알아보았는데, 알베르 드 모르세르가 파리에서 그를 언급했을 때만 해도 당글라르는 그런 자가 실재한다는 것을 믿으려 하지 않았다. 그리고 그는 그 산적뿐 아니라, 알베르가 갇혔던 그 방, 아마도 손님 접대용으로 마련된 듯한 그 방까지 알아보았다. 당글라르는 어느 정도 흡족한 마음으로 이런 기억들을 곱씹었고, 그러자 마음이 어느 정도 진정되었다. 산적들이 그를 당장 처치하지 않은 것을 보니, 결국 죽이지는 않으리라는 생각이 들었다. 그들은 강탈할 목적으로 그를 붙잡았는데, 그가 몸에 지닌 것이라곤 루이 금화 몇 닢뿐이었으므로, 몸값을 받고 풀어 줄 것이 틀림없다고 그는 믿었다.\n\n그는 모르세르의 몸값이 사천 크라운으로 매겨졌던 것을 떠올렸고, 자신이 모르세르보다 훨씬 더 중요한 인물이라고 여겼으므로 제 몸값을 팔천 크라운으로 정했다. 팔천 크라운이면 사만 팔천 리브르였다. 그러면 그에게는 오백오만 프랑가량이 남을 것이다. 그 정도 돈이면 곤경에서 벗어날 수 있었다. 그러므로 자신이 오백오만 프랑이라는 터무니없는 액수로 매겨지지만 않는다면 이 처지에서 빠져나올 수 있으리라 어지간히 안심한 그는, 침상에 몸을 뻗고는 두세 번 뒤척이다가 루이지 밤파가 그 생애를 공부하고 있던 영웅 같은 평온함으로 잠들었다.\n\n# Chapter 115. Luigi Vampa’s Bill of Fare\n\n우리는 어떤 잠에서도 깨어난다. 단 하나, 당글라르가 두려워하는 그 잠만은 예외다. 그가 깨어났다.\n비단 휘장과 벨벳을 두른 벽, 그리고 우아한 곡선을 그리며 방 안을 떠도는 흰 연기를 피워 올리는 장작의 은은한 향내에 익숙한 파리 사람에게, 잠에서 깨어 눈에 들어온 회칠한 토굴의 광경은 어떤 불쾌한 꿈의 연장처럼만 느껴졌다. 그러나 이런 상황에서는 한순간이면 가장 완강한 의심도 확신으로 바뀌기에 충분하다.\n\n\"그래, 그래.\" 그가 중얼거렸다. \"알베르 드 모르세르가 말하던 그 산적들 손에 잡혀 있는 거야.\" 그의 첫 번째 생각은 숨을 쉬어 보아, 자신이 다쳤는지 알아보자는 것이었다. 이건 그가 여태 읽은 유일한 책, 그러나 아직 어렴풋이 기억하고 있는 「돈키호테」에서 빌려 온 방법이었다.\n\n\"아니야.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"다친 데는 없군. 하지만 어쩌면 강도질은 당했을지도!\" 그러고는 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣었다. 손도 대지 않은 그대로였다. 로마에서 베네치아까지 가는 여비로 챙겨 둔 백 루이가 바지 주머니에 있었고, 외투 주머니에는 505만 프랑짜리 신용장이 든 작은 지갑이 들어 있었다.\n\n\"별난 도둑들이로군!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"지갑도 수첩도 그대로 두다니. 어젯밤에 내가 말했듯이, 이놈들은 나를 인질로 잡아 몸값을 받아 낼 셈이야. 어럽쇼, 시계도 있네! 어디 몇 시인지 보자.\"\n\n당글라르의 시계는 브레게 회사의 자명종 시계로, 그가 전날 밤 정성껏 태엽을 감아 둔 것이었다. 시계가 다섯 시 반을 알렸다. 이게 아니었더라면 당글라르는 시간을 도통 알 길이 없었을 것이다. 햇빛이 토굴까지 들지 않았기 때문이다. 산적들에게 설명을 요구해야 할까, 아니면 그들이 먼저 입을 열 때까지 참고 기다려야 할까? 후자가 더 신중한 길로 보였기에, 그는 열두 시까지 기다렸다. 그동안 여덟 시에 교대한 보초 하나가 줄곧 그의 방문을 지키고 있었다.\n\n당글라르는 문득 자신을 감시하는 자의 얼굴이 몹시 보고 싶어졌다. 그는 잘 맞물리지 않은 문짝 틈으로 가느다란 빛줄기 몇 가닥이 새어 드는 것을 알아챘다. 햇빛이 아니라 등불 빛이었다. 그가 문가로 다가갔을 때, 마침 산적은 가죽 부대에 든 브랜디를 한 모금 들이켜며 목을 축이고 있었다. 그 가죽 부대 탓에 풍겨 나는 냄새가 당글라르에게는 견딜 수 없이 역겨웠다. \"윽!\" 그가 외치며 토굴의 맨 안쪽 구석으로 물러섰다.\n\n열두 시가 되자 이 사내는 다른 자와 교대했고, 당글라르는 새 감시자의 얼굴을 보고 싶은 마음에 다시 문가로 다가갔다.\n\n그는 체격이 우람한 거구의 산적이었다. 눈은 크고 입술은 두툼하며 코는 납작했다. 붉은 머리카락은 어깨를 감싸고 뱀처럼 헝클어진 채 늘어져 있었다.\n\n\"이런, 이런.\" 당글라르가 외쳤다. \"이놈은 사람이라기보다 식인귀에 더 가깝군. 그래도 나는 너무 늙고 질겨서 맛있게 먹기는 어려울걸!\"\n\n이로써 당글라르가 농담을 던질 만큼은 침착함을 되찾았음을 알 수 있다. 한편, 마치 그 식인귀 같은 인상을 부정이라도 하려는 듯, 사내는 자루에서 검은 빵과 치즈, 양파를 꺼내더니 게걸스럽게 먹어 치우기 시작했다.\n\n\"내 목을 매달아도 좋아.\" 당글라르가 문틈으로 산적의 식사를 들여다보며 말했다. \"어떻게 저런 더러운 걸 먹을 수 있는지 도무지 이해가 안 가면, 차라리 내 목을 매달라지!\" 그러고는 물러나 염소 가죽 위에 앉았는데, 그 가죽에서 또 브랜디 냄새가 떠올랐다.\n\n그러나 자연의 신비란 헤아릴 수 없는 법이고, 가장 거친 음식에도 굶주린 위장에는 도저히 거역할 수 없게 호소해 오는 어떤 유혹이 깃들어 있는 법이다. 당글라르는 마침 자기 위장이 그리 든든히 채워져 있지 않다는 것을 느꼈다. 차츰 그 사내는 덜 흉해 보였고, 빵은 덜 검어 보였으며, 치즈는 더 신선해 보였다. 그 끔찍하고 천박한 양파마저도, 그가 \"드니조 씨, 오늘은 맛있는 프리카세를 좀 만들어 주게.\"라고 할 때마다 요리사가 더없이 솜씨 좋게 차려 내던 어떤 소스며 곁들이 요리를 떠오르게 했다. 그는 일어나 문을 두드렸다. 산적이 고개를 들었다. 당글라르는 자기 소리가 들렸음을 알았기에, 두드림을 더 세차게 거듭했다.\n\n\"케 코사?\" 산적이 물었다.\n\n\"이봐, 이봐.\" 당글라르가 손가락으로 문을 두드리며 말했다. \"이제 내게 뭐라도 먹을 걸 줄 때가 되지 않았나 싶은데!\"\n\n그러나 사내는 그의 말을 못 알아들었는지, 아니면 당글라르에게 먹을 것을 주라는 지시를 받지 못했는지, 대꾸도 없이 식사를 계속했다. 당글라르는 자존심이 상했다. 이 짐승 같은 놈에게 신세를 지고 싶지 않았던 은행가는 다시 염소 가죽 위에 몸을 던지고는 더 이상 한마디도 내뱉지 않았다.\n\n네 시간이 흘렀고, 거구의 사내는 또 다른 산적과 교대했다. 정말이지 위장에서 갖가지 쓰라림이 일기 시작한 당글라르는 가만히 일어나 다시 문틈에 눈을 갖다 댔고, 영리해 보이는 길잡이의 얼굴을 알아보았다. 다름 아닌 페피노였다. 그는 가능한 한 편안하게 보초를 설 채비를 하던 참이었다. 문을 마주 보고 앉아, 두 다리 사이에 베이컨을 넣고 끓인 병아리콩이 담긴 토기 냄비를 놓아두었다. 냄비 옆에는 빌레트리산 포도가 담긴 예쁜 작은 바구니와 오르비에토산 포도주 한 병도 놓여 있었다. 페피노는 확실히 식도락가였다. 당글라르는 이런 준비를 지켜보았고, 입안에 군침이 돌았다.\n\n\"좋아.\" 그가 혼잣말을 했다. \"이놈이 저번 놈보다 좀 더 말이 통하는지 시험해 보자.\" 그러고는 문을 가만히 두드렸다.\n\n\"오니 바!\"(갑니다!) 페피노가 외쳤다. 그는 파스트리니 씨네 집을 자주 드나든 덕에 프랑스어를 관용구까지 완벽하게 알아들었다.\n\n당글라르는 곧바로 그가 그토록 사납게 \"머리 집어넣어!\"라고 소리치던 자라는 것을 알아보았다. 그러나 지금은 따질 때가 아니었으므로, 그는 더없이 상냥한 태도를 취하며 정중한 미소를 띠고 말했다.\n\n\"실례지만, 선생, 식사는 안 주는 거요?\"\n\n\"각하께서 혹 시장하신지요?\"\n\n\"혹 시장하냐고, 그것참 좋은 말이군, 스물네 시간이나 굶었는데!\" 당글라르가 투덜거렸다. 그러더니 소리 내어 덧붙였다. \"그렇소, 선생, 배가 고프오. 몹시 고프오.\"\n\n\"각하께서는 뭔가 드시고 싶으신 거군요?\"\n\n\"가능하면 당장.\"\n\n\"그보다 쉬운 일도 없지요.\" 페피노가 말했다. \"여기서는 원하시는 건 뭐든 구하실 수 있습니다. 물론 정직한 사람들 사이에서처럼 값을 치르셔야지요.\"\n\n\"물론이지!\" 당글라르가 외쳤다. \"하지만 따지고 보면, 사람을 붙잡아 가둔 자들이라면 적어도 먹이기는 해야 도리가 아닌가.\"\n\n\"그게 관례가 아니라서요, 각하.\" 페피노가 말했다.\n\n\"형편없는 핑계로군.\" 감시인의 마음을 누그러뜨릴 셈이던 당글라르가 대꾸했다. \"그래도 좋아. 식사나 좀 갖다주게!\"\n\n\"당장 올리지요! 각하께서는 무얼 드시겠습니까?\"\n\n페피노는 냄비를 바닥에 내려놓아, 김이 곧장 당글라르의 콧구멍 밑으로 피어오르게 했다. \"분부하시지요.\"\n\n\"여기 주방이 있나?\"\n\n\"주방이요? 물론이지요, 없는 것 없이 갖춰져 있습니다.\"\n\n\"요리사도?\"\n\n\"훌륭한 요리사가요!\"\n\n\"그럼 닭고기든 생선이든 사냥한 고기든, 먹을 수만 있다면 무엇이든 좋네.\"\n\n\"각하 뜻대로 하시지요. 닭고기라 하셨던가요?\"\n\n\"그래, 닭고기.\"\n\n페피노가 돌아서더니 외쳤다. \"각하께 닭 한 마리!\" 그 목소리가 아치형 통로에 채 메아리치기도 전에, 잘생기고 우아하며 반쯤 벗은 젊은이 하나가 나타났다. 그는 은 접시에 닭 한 마리를 담아 손도 대지 않고 머리에 인 채였다.\n\n\"꼭 파리의 카페에 와 있는 기분이군.\" 당글라르가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"자, 각하.\" 페피노가 젊은 산적에게서 닭을 받아, 벌레 먹은 탁자 위에 올려놓으며 말했다. 그 탁자는 의자와 염소 가죽 침상과 함께 토굴 안 가구의 전부였다. 당글라르가 나이프와 포크를 청했다.\n\n\"여기 있습니다, 각하.\" 페피노가 무딘 작은 칼과 회양목 포크를 내밀며 말했다. 당글라르는 한 손에 칼을, 다른 손에 포크를 쥐고 닭을 자르려 했다.\n\n\"죄송합니다, 각하.\" 페피노가 은행가의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"여기서는 먹기 전에 값을 치르는 법입니다. 자칫 만족하지 못하실 수도 있고, 그러면……\"\n\n\"이런, 이런.\" 당글라르는 속으로 생각했다. \"여긴 파리랑 영 딴판이군. 십중팔구 가죽이 벗겨지겠어! 그래도 상관없다, 잘 처리하면 돼. 이탈리아에서는 닭고기가 싸다는 얘기를 늘 들었으니, 로마에서 닭 한 마리쯤이야 한 열두 수 하겠지. 자, 받게.\" 그가 1루이를 던지며 말했다.\n\n페피노는 루이를 주워 들었고, 당글라르는 다시 닭을 자를 채비를 했다.\n\n\"잠깐만요, 각하.\" 페피노가 일어서며 말했다. \"아직 제게 갚으실 게 남았습니다.\"\n\n\"가죽이 벗겨질 거랬지.\" 당글라르는 속으로 생각했다. 그러나 이 갈취에 맞서기로 마음먹고 말했다. \"좋아, 이 닭값으로 자네에게 얼마를 갚아야 하나?\"\n\n\"각하께서는 선금으로 1루이를 주셨습니다.\"\n\n\"닭 한 마리 선금이 1루이라고?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다. 그리고 각하께서는 이제 제게 4,999루이를 갚으셔야 합니다.\"\n\n이 어마어마한 농담을 듣고 당글라르는 가뜩이나 큰 눈을 한껏 부릅떴다.\n\n\"아주 재미있군.\" 그가 중얼거렸다. \"정말 아주 재미있어.\" 그러고는 다시 닭을 자르기 시작했다. 그때 페피노가 왼손으로 남작의 오른손을 막고는, 다른 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"자, 이제.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"농담이 아닌가?\" 당글라르가 말했다.\n\n\"저희는 농담 같은 건 하지 않습니다.\" 페피노가 퀘이커 교도처럼 근엄하게 대꾸했다.\n\n\"뭐라고! 닭 한 마리에 십만 프랑이라니!\"\n\n\"아, 각하, 이 끔찍한 동굴 속에서 닭을 기르기가 얼마나 힘든지 짐작도 못 하실 겁니다!\"\n\n\"이봐, 이봐, 아주 우습군, 아주 재미있어, 인정하네. 하지만 내가 몹시 시장하니, 제발 먹게 해 주게. 자, 여기 1루이 더 있네.\"\n\n\"그럼 4,998루이만 더 갚으시면 되겠군요.\" 페피노가 여전히 무심하게 말했다. \"그것도 차차 다 받겠지요.\"\n\n\"오, 그거라면,\" 농담이 길어지는 데 화가 난 당글라르가 말했다. \"그거라면 한 푼도 못 받을 줄 알아. 꺼져 버려! 네가 누구를 상대하는지 모르는 모양인데!\"\n\n페피노가 신호를 보내자, 젊은이가 잽싸게 닭을 치웠다. 당글라르는 염소 가죽 위에 몸을 던졌고, 페피노는 문을 다시 닫고서 콩과 베이컨을 다시 먹기 시작했다. 당글라르에게 페피노의 모습은 보이지 않았지만, 그 이 부딪는 소리만으로도 그가 무얼 하는지는 의심할 여지가 없었다. 분명 그는 먹고 있었고, 그것도 버릇없는 자처럼 요란하게 먹고 있었다. \"짐승 같은 놈!\" 당글라르가 말했다. 페피노는 못 들은 척하며, 고개조차 돌리지 않고 천천히 계속 먹었다. 당글라르의 위장은 어찌나 텅 빈 것 같았던지, 다시는 도저히 채울 수 없을 것만 같았다. 그래도 그는 다시 반 시간을 참아 냈는데, 그 시간이 그에게는 한 세기처럼 느껴졌다. 그는 또 일어나 문가로 갔다.\n\n\"이보게, 선생, 여기서 더는 나를 굶기지 말고, 저들이 원하는 게 뭔지 말해 주게.\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 각하. 무얼 원하시는지 말씀하셔야 할 분은 바로 각하시지요. 분부만 하시면 저희가 그대로 받들겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 당장 문을 열게.\" 페피노가 그대로 따랐다. \"자, 잘 들어. 나는 먹을 게 필요하다! 먹을 것 말이야, 알겠나?\"\n\n\"시장하십니까?\"\n\n\"이봐, 알아들었잖나.\"\n\n\"각하께서는 무얼 드시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"마른 빵 한 조각이면 됐네. 이 빌어먹을 곳에서는 닭값이 천정부지로 비싸니 말이야.\"\n\n\"빵이요? 좋습니다. 거기 누구 없나, 빵 좀 가져와!\" 그가 외쳤다. 젊은이가 작은 빵 한 덩이를 가져왔다. \"얼마인가?\" 당글라르가 물었다.\n\n\"4,998루이입니다.\" 페피노가 말했다. \"각하께서는 선금으로 2루이를 치르셨으니까요.\"\n\n\"뭐라고? 빵 한 덩이에 십만 프랑이라고?\"\n\n\"십만 프랑입니다.\" 페피노가 거듭 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 자네는 닭 한 마리에도 십만 프랑밖에 안 불렀잖나!\"\n\n\"저희는 모든 식료품에 고정된 값을 매겨 두었습니다. 많이 드시든 적게 드시든, 열 접시를 드시든 한 접시를 드시든, 아무 상관 없습니다. 값은 늘 똑같지요.\"\n\n\"뭐야, 아직도 이 어리석은 농담을 계속하는 건가? 이보게, 정말이지 우스꽝스럽고 멍청하기 짝이 없네! 차라리 나를 굶겨 죽일 작정이라고 솔직히 말하는 게 낫겠어.\"\n\n\"오, 천만에요, 각하. 각하께서 스스로 목숨을 끊으실 작정이 아니라면요. 값을 치르고 드시지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 뭘로 치르라는 거냐, 이 짐승 같은 놈아?\" 당글라르가 격분하여 말했다. \"내가 주머니에 십만 프랑이라도 넣고 다니는 줄 아나?\"\n\n\"각하 주머니에는 505만 프랑이 들어 있지요. 한 마리에 십만 프랑씩이면 닭 쉰 마리에다, 나머지 5만 프랑으로 닭 반 마리를 더 드실 수 있는 액수입니다.\"\n\n당글라르는 몸서리쳤다. 눈에서 가리개가 벗겨졌고, 그는 비로소 그 농담을 이해했다. 조금 전만큼 어리석은 농담은 아니라는 생각이 들었다.\n\n\"좋아.\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 자네에게 십만 프랑을 치르면, 그걸로 만족하고 마음 편히 먹게 해 주겠나?\"\n\n\"물론입니다.\" 페피노가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 어떻게 치른단 말인가?\"\n\n\"오, 그보다 쉬운 일도 없지요. 각하께서는 로마 비아데이반키의 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회에 거래 계좌를 두고 계시지 않습니까. 그 회사 앞으로 4,998루이짜리 어음을 한 장 써 주시면, 저희 은행가가 받아 올 겁니다.\"\n\n당글라르는 순순히 따르는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했다. 그래서 페피노가 내미는 펜과 잉크, 종이를 받아 어음을 쓰고 서명했다.\n\n\"자.\" 그가 말했다. \"여기 일람불 어음일세.\"\n\n\"그리고 여기 각하의 닭입니다.\"\n\n당글라르는 한숨을 내쉬며 닭을 잘랐다. 치른 값에 비하면 어지간히도 빈약해 보이는 닭이었다. 한편 페피노는 종이를 꼼꼼히 살피더니 주머니에 넣고는, 다시 콩을 먹기 시작했다.\n\n# 제114장 페피노\n\n이튿날 당글라르는 다시 허기를 느꼈다. 분명 그 지하 감방의 공기는 식욕을 몹시 돋우는 것이었다. 죄수는 그날만큼은 비용을 치를 일이 없으리라 기대했다. 알뜰한 사람답게 닭고기 절반과 빵 한 조각을 감방 구석에 감춰 두었으니 말이다. 그러나 그것을 먹기가 무섭게 그는 목이 말라 왔다. 그 점은 미처 생각하지 못했던 것이다. 혀가 입천장에 들러붙을 때까지 갈증과 싸우다가, 더는 견딜 수 없게 되자 소리쳐 불렀다. 보초가 문을 열었다. 처음 보는 얼굴이었다. 그는 안면이 있는 사람과 흥정하는 편이 낫겠다고 생각하여 페피노를 불러 달라고 했다.\n\n\"여기 있습니다, 각하.\" 페피노가 말했다. 당글라르가 보기에 자기에게 호의적인 듯한 그 열의를 담은 목소리였다. \"무엇을 원하십니까?\"\n\n\"마실 것 좀.\"\n\n\"로마 근처에서는 포도주 값이 비싸기 짝이 없다는 걸 각하께서도 아실 텐데요.\"\n\n\"그럼 물을 주게.\" 당글라르가 그 일격을 받아넘기려 애쓰며 외쳤다.\n\n\"아, 물은 포도주보다 더 귀합니다, 각하. 어찌나 가물었는지요.\"\n\n'이런,' 당글라르는 생각했다. '여전히 똑같은 수작이군.' 그러고는 이 일을 농담으로 여기려는 듯 미소를 지었으나, 관자놀이가 땀으로 축축해지는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"이보게, 친구.\" 페피노에게 아무 인상도 주지 못한 것을 깨닫고 당글라르가 말했다. \"포도주 한 잔쯤은 거절하지 않겠지?\"\n\n\"낱개로는 팔지 않는다고 이미 말씀드렸습니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 그렇다면 제일 싼 걸로 한 병 주게.\"\n\n\"전부 같은 값입니다.\"\n\n\"그게 얼마인데?\"\n\n\"한 병에 2만 5천 프랑입니다.\"\n\n\"말해 보게.\" 당글라르가 아르파공●만이 드러낼 수 있을 법한 쓰디쓴 어조로 외쳤다. \"나를 통째로 털어먹고 싶다고 말이야. 그게 한 조각씩 야금야금 뜯어먹는 것보다 빠를 테니까.\"\n\n\"주인님의 뜻이 그러할지도 모르지요.\"\n\n\"주인이라니, 그게 누구인가?\"\n\n\"어제 각하를 모셔 갔던 그분 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그는 어디 있나?\"\n\n\"여기 있습니다.\" \"좀 만나게 해 주게.\"\n\n\"그러지요.\"\n\n그러자 이내 루이지 밤파가 당글라르 앞에 나타났다.\n\n\"나를 불렀소?\" 그가 죄수에게 말했다.\n\n\"당신이 나를 이리로 끌고 온 자들의 두목이오?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 각하. 그래서요?\"\n\n\"몸값으로 얼마를 원하오?\"\n\n\"각하가 지니고 계신 500만 프랑이면 됩니다.\" 당글라르는 심장을 꿰뚫는 끔찍한 경련을 느꼈다.\n\n\"하지만 이게 내가 세상에 가진 전부요.\" 그가 말했다. \"막대한 재산 가운데 남은 거라곤 이게 다란 말이오. 이걸 빼앗는다면, 내 목숨도 같이 가져가시오.\"\n\n\"당신의 피를 흘리게 하는 것은 금지되어 있소.\"\n\n\"누가 그걸 금했단 말이오?\"\n\n\"우리가 따르는 분이오.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 당신도 누군가를 따른단 말이오?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 두목 말이오.\"\n\n\"당신이 두목이라고 하지 않았소?\"\n\n\"이 사람들에게는 그렇소. 하지만 내 위에 또 한 분이 계시오.\"\n\n\"그래, 당신 윗사람이 나를 이런 식으로 대하라고 명령한 거요?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"하지만 내 지갑은 바닥날 거요.\"\n\n\"아마 그렇겠지요.\"\n\n\"이보시오.\" 당글라르가 말했다. \"100만 프랑이면 받겠소?\"\n\n\"안 되오.\"\n\"200만? 300만? 400만은 어떻소? 자, 400만이오. 나를 풀어 주는 조건이라면 그걸 다 주겠소.\"\n\n\"500만의 값어치가 있는 것에 어째서 400만을 내겠다는 거요? 그건 일종의 고리대 아니오, 은행가 양반. 나로서는 도무지 이해할 수 없는 셈법이군.\"\n\n\"그럼 다 가져가시오. 다 가져가라니까. 그리고 나를 죽이시오!\"\n\n\"자, 자, 진정하시오. 그렇게 흥분하면 피가 끓어올라 식욕이 솟을 텐데, 그걸 채우자면 하루에 100만씩은 들 거요. 좀 더 아껴 쓰시오.\"\n\n\"하지만 당신들에게 치를 돈이 한 푼도 남지 않으면?\" 격분한 당글라르가 물었다.\n\n\"그땐 굶주림을 견뎌야지요.\"\n\n\"굶주림을 견디라고?\" 당글라르가 새파랗게 질려 말했다.\n\n\"십중팔구 그렇소.\" 밤파가 차갑게 대꾸했다.\n\n\"하지만 나를 죽이고 싶지는 않다고 하지 않았소?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\"그러면서도 나를 굶어 죽게 내버려 두겠다는 거요?\"\n\n\"아, 그건 별개의 문제요.\"\n\n\"좋소, 그렇다면, 비열한 놈들!\" 당글라르가 외쳤다. \"내 너희의 더러운 셈법에 맞서 주마. 차라리 단번에 죽고 말겠다! 나를 괴롭히고 고문하고 죽여도 좋지만, 내 서명만은 두 번 다시 받지 못할 거다!\"\n\n\"각하 좋으실 대로 하시지요.\" 밤파는 이렇게 말하고 감방을 떠났다.\n\n당글라르는 미친 듯이 염소 가죽 위로 몸을 내던졌다. 이자들은 대체 누구란 말인가? 그 보이지 않는 두목은 누구인가? 자기에게 무슨 속셈을 품고 있는 것일까? 다른 사람들은 모두 몸값을 치르고 풀려나는데, 어째서 자기만은 안 된단 말인가? 오, 그래, 분명 빠르고 격렬한 죽음이야말로 그 무자비한 적들을 따돌릴 좋은 방법일 것이다. 그들은 도무지 알 수 없는 원한을 품고 자기를 쫓는 듯 보였다. 그러나 죽는다고? 난생처음으로 당글라르는 두려움과 갈망이 뒤섞인 심정으로 죽음을 떠올렸다. 인간이라면 누구나 마음속에 품고 있는 그 가차 없는 망령이 그의 주의를 사로잡고, 심장이 고동칠 때마다 \"너는 죽으리라!\" 하고 외치는 때가 온 것이다.\n\n당글라르는 사냥에 쫓겨 흥분한 겁 많은 짐승과도 같았다. 처음엔 달아나고, 다음엔 절망하다가, 마침내 그 절망의 힘 자체로 때때로 추격자를 따돌리는 데 성공하는 그런 짐승 말이다. 당글라르는 탈출을 궁리했다. 그러나 벽은 단단한 바위였고, 감방의 단 하나뿐인 출구에는 한 사내가 앉아 책을 읽고 있었으며, 그 사내 뒤로는 총을 든 형체들이 끊임없이 오갔다. 서명하지 않겠다는 결심은 이틀을 갔다. 그러고 나서 그는 먹을 것을 위해 100만 프랑을 내놓았다. 그들은 훌륭한 만찬을 보내 주고 그의 100만 프랑을 가져갔다.\n\n이때부터 죄수는 더는 견디지 않고 원하는 것은 무엇이든 가지기로 작정했다. 열이틀이 지나, 거한 식사를 마치고 셈을 따져 보니 남은 돈이 5만 프랑뿐이었다. 그러자 이상한 반작용이 일어났다. 방금 500만 프랑을 포기한 자가 이번에는 남은 5만 프랑을 지키려고 안간힘을 썼다. 그것을 내놓느니 차라리 다시 궁핍한 생활로 돌아가기로 결심한 것이다. 그는 광기의 전조인 희망에 사로잡혀 있었다.\n\n그토록 오랫동안 신을 잊고 살아온 그가 기적이 가능할지도 모른다고 생각하기 시작했다. 그 저주받은 동굴을 교황령● 관헌들이 발견해 자기를 풀어 줄지도 모른다고, 그러면 5만 프랑이 남아 굶어 죽는 것만은 면할 수 있으리라고 말이다. 그리하여 마침내 그는 그 돈만은 자기에게 남겨지기를 기도했고, 기도하면서 눈물을 흘렸다. 그렇게 사흘이 흘렀고, 그동안 그의 기도는 진심에서 우러난 것은 아닐지언정 잦았다. 때로는 헛것을 보아, 한 노인이 초라한 침상에 누워 있는 환영을 보았다. 그 노인 역시 굶주림으로 죽어 가고 있었다.\n\n나흘째에 그는 더 이상 사람이 아니라 살아 있는 송장이었다. 지난 끼니에서 떨어진 부스러기란 부스러기는 죄다 주워 먹고는, 이제 감방 바닥을 덮은 거적까지 뜯어먹기 시작했다. 그러고는 페피노에게 수호천사를 대하듯 애원하며 먹을 것을 달라고 했다. 빵 한 입에 1천 프랑을 내겠다고 했다. 그러나 페피노는 대답하지 않았다. 닷새째에 그는 감방 문 앞까지 몸을 끌고 갔다.\n\n\"당신은 기독교인이 아니오?\" 그가 무릎을 꿇으며 말했다. \"하늘이 보기에 형제인 사람을 죽이려는 거요? 오, 나의 옛 친구들, 나의 옛 친구들이여!\" 그는 이렇게 중얼거리며 얼굴을 땅에 박고 쓰러졌다. 그러다 절망에 차서 다시 몸을 일으키며 외쳤다. \"두목! 두목을 불러 주오!\"\n\n\"여기 있소.\" 밤파가 곧장 나타나며 말했다. \"무엇을 원하시오?\"\n\n\"내 마지막 금화를 가져가시오.\" 당글라르가 수첩을 내밀며 웅얼거렸다. \"그리고 나를 여기서 살게 해 주오. 자유를 달라는 게 아니오. 그저 살게만 해 달라는 거요!\"\n\n\"그렇게나 괴롭소?\"\n\n\"오, 그렇소, 그렇소, 끔찍하게 괴롭소!\"\n\n\"하지만 당신보다 더 괴로워한 사람들도 있었소.\"\n\n\"그럴 리 없소.\"\n\n\"있소. 굶어 죽은 사람들 말이오.\"\n\n당글라르는 헛것에 사로잡혀 있던 동안 침상에서 신음하던 모습을 보았던 그 노인을 떠올렸다. 그는 이마를 땅에 짓찧으며 신음했다. \"그렇소.\" 그가 말했다. \"나보다 더 괴로워한 사람들이 있었겠지. 하지만 그들은 적어도 순교자였을 거요.\"\n\n\"뉘우치는가?\" 깊고 엄숙한 목소리가 물었다. 그 소리에 당글라르의 머리카락이 곤두섰다. 그의 흐릿한 눈이 사물을 분간하려 애썼다. 산적 뒤편으로, 망토를 두르고 돌기둥 그림자에 반쯤 묻힌 한 사내가 보였다.\n\n\"무엇을 뉘우치란 말이오?\" 당글라르가 더듬거렸다.\n\n\"네가 저지른 악행을 말이다.\" 목소리가 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그렇소. 오, 그렇소. 정말로 뉘우치고 있소.\" 그러고는 앙상한 주먹으로 제 가슴을 쳤다.\n\n\"그렇다면 너를 용서하노라.\" 사내가 망토를 떨치며 빛 속으로 걸어 나오며 말했다.\n\n\"몬테크리스토 백작!\" 당글라르가 말했다. 조금 전 굶주림과 비참함으로 질렸을 때보다 더 공포로 새파랗게 질려 있었다.\n\n\"잘못 보았다. 나는 몬테크리스토 백작이 아니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 누구요?\"\n\n\"나는 네가 팔아넘기고 모욕한 사람이다. 나는 네가 그 약혼녀를 욕보인 사람이다. 나는 네가 출세하려고 짓밟은 사람이다. 나는 네가 그 아버지를 굶겨 죽인 사람이다. 나는 네가 굶어 죽도록 단죄한 사람이며, 그러고도 너를 용서하는 사람이다. 나 또한 용서받기를 바라기 때문이다. 나는 에드몽 당테스다!\"\n\n당글라르는 외마디 비명을 지르며 엎어졌다.\n\n\"일어나라.\" 백작이 말했다. \"네 목숨은 무사하다. 네 공범들에게는 그런 행운이 따르지 않았다. 하나는 미쳤고, 하나는 죽었다. 남은 5만 프랑은 그대로 가져라. 내가 너에게 준다. 네가 자선 병원에서 훔친 500만 프랑은 알 수 없는 손길에 의해 그들에게 되돌려졌다. 자, 이제 먹고 마셔라. 오늘 밤 내가 너를 대접하겠다. 밤파, 이 사람이 만족하거든 풀어 주게.\"\n\n당글라르는 백작이 물러갈 때까지 엎드린 채로 있었다. 고개를 들었을 때, 그는 복도 저편으로 사라지는 그림자 하나밖에 보지 못했고, 그 앞에서 산적들이 머리를 조아렸다.\n\n백작의 지시에 따라 밤파가 당글라르의 시중을 들었다. 밤파는 이탈리아 최상급의 포도주와 과일을 가져다주었다. 그런 다음 그를 길까지 데려가 역마차를 가리켜 보이고는, 나무에 기대 선 그를 남겨 두고 떠났다. 그는 자신이 어디에 있는지도 모른 채 밤새 그 자리에 머물렀다. 동이 틀 무렵, 자기가 시냇가 가까이에 있음을 알아차렸다. 목이 말랐던 그는 시내를 향해 몸을 끌고 갔다. 물을 마시려고 몸을 굽히던 순간, 그는 자기 머리카락이 온통 하얗게 세어 버린 것을 보았다.\n\n# Chapter 117. The Fifth of October\n\n● 아르파공: 몰리에르의 희극 「수전노」에 나오는 지독한 구두쇠. —옮긴이 ● 교황령: 당시 로마를 중심으로 교황이 다스리던 영토. —옮긴이\n저녁 여섯 시쯤이었다. 유백색 빛이 가을 햇살의 금빛 줄기를 머금은 채 푸른 바다 위로 내려앉았다. 한낮의 열기는 차츰 가라앉았고, 남쪽의 뜨거운 낮잠에서 깨어난 자연의 숨결처럼 가벼운 미풍이 일었다. 감미로운 산들바람이 지중해 연안을 따라 노닐며, 신선한 바다 내음에 뒤섞인 풀잎의 달콤한 향기를 이 해안에서 저 해안으로 실어 날랐다.\n\n순결하고 우아한 자태의 가벼운 요트 한 척이, 지브롤터에서 다르다넬스 해협까지, 또 튀니스에서 베네치아까지 펼쳐진 이 광대한 호수 위로, 밤의 첫 이슬을 가르며 미끄러져 갔다. 그 배는 바람을 향해 날개를 펼친 채 물 위를 미끄러지는 백조와도 같았다. 배는 빠르고도 우아하게 나아가며, 뒤로 반짝이는 물거품의 자취를 길게 남겼다. 차츰 해가 서쪽 수평선 너머로 사라졌다. 그러나 이교 신화의 그 환상적인 상상이 진실임을 증명이라도 하려는 듯, 그 무람없는 햇살은 물결 하나하나의 꼭대기에 다시 떠올랐다. 마치 불의 신이 방금 암피트리테의 품속으로 가라앉았는데, 그녀가 푸른 망토 아래 연인을 숨기려 애썼지만 끝내 헛수고가 된 것처럼.\n\n요트는 빠르게 나아갔다. 한 소녀의 머리카락 곱슬을 흩뜨릴 만한 바람조차 없어 보였는데도 말이다. 뱃머리에는 거무스름한 안색의 키 큰 남자가 서 있었다. 그는 점점 커지는 두 눈으로, 카탈루냐 사람의 모자처럼 물결 한가운데서 솟아오른 원뿔 모양의 어두운 땅덩어리가 다가오는 것을 바라보았다.\n\n\"저것이 몬테크리스토섬인가?\" 그 순간 요트가 명령을 따르고 있던 여행자가 침울한 목소리로 물었다.\n\n\"네, 각하.\" 선장이 말했다. \"다 왔습니다.\"\n\n\"다 왔다고!\" 여행자가 형언할 수 없는 슬픔이 깃든 어조로 되뇌었다.\n\n그러고는 낮은 목소리로 덧붙였다. \"그래, 저것이 안식처지.\"\n\n그런 다음 그는 다시 생각에 잠겼다. 그 생각의 성격은 눈물보다도 슬픈 미소 하나로 더 잘 드러났다. 몇 분 뒤, 섬에서 한 줄기 빛이 번쩍였다가 곧바로 꺼졌고, 총소리가 요트에까지 들려왔다.\n\n\"각하,\" 선장이 말했다. \"저것이 육지의 신호입니다. 직접 답하시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"무슨 신호 말인가?\"\n\n선장이 섬을 가리켰다. 섬 비탈을 따라 한 줄기 연기가 피어올라, 오를수록 점점 짙어지고 있었다.\n\n\"아, 그래.\" 그가 꿈에서 깨어나듯 말했다. \"그것을 이리 주게.\"\n\n선장이 장전된 소총을 건넸다. 여행자는 천천히 그것을 들어 올려 허공에 한 발 쏘았다. 십 분 뒤 돛이 거두어졌고, 작은 항구에서 백 길쯤 떨어진 곳에 닻을 내렸다. 거룻배는 이미 내려져 있었고, 그 안에는 네 명의 노잡이와 한 명의 키잡이가 타고 있었다. 여행자가 내려왔다. 그러나 그는 자신을 위해 푸른 양탄자가 깔린 고물 자리에 앉는 대신, 두 팔을 가슴에 엇갈리게 낀 채 그대로 서 있었다. 노잡이들은 새가 날개를 말리듯 노를 물 밖으로 반쯤 들어 올린 채 기다렸다.\n\n\"저어라.\" 여행자가 말했다. 여덟 개의 노가 물 한 방울 튀기지 않고 동시에 바다에 잠겼고, 배는 그 추진력에 실려 앞으로 미끄러져 나갔다. 순식간에 그들은 자연이 만든 후미에 형성된 작은 항구에 닿았다. 배는 고운 모래 위에 얹혔다.\n\n\"각하, 송구하오나 저희 사람 둘의 어깨에 올라타 주시겠습니까? 뭍까지 모시겠습니다.\" 젊은이는 이 권유에 무심한 몸짓으로 답하더니, 배에서 그대로 내려섰다. 바닷물이 곧바로 그의 허리께까지 차올랐다.\n\n\"아, 각하.\" 키잡이가 중얼거렸다. \"그러시면 안 되는데요. 주인님께 저희가 꾸중을 듣겠습니다.\"\n\n젊은이는 단단한 발판을 골라 디디는 선원들을 따라 계속 앞으로 나아갔다. 서른 걸음을 떼자 마른 땅에 닿았다. 젊은이는 물기를 털어 내려고 발을 굴렀고, 길을 일러 줄 사람을 찾아 주위를 둘러보았다. 사방이 캄캄했기 때문이다. 그가 막 몸을 돌리는 순간, 한 손이 그의 어깨에 얹혔고, 그를 몸서리치게 하는 목소리가 외쳤다.\n\n\"잘 왔네, 막시밀리앙. 시간을 정확히 지켜 주었군, 고맙네!\"\n\n\"아, 백작님이십니까?\" 젊은이가 거의 환희에 찬 어조로 말하며, 두 손으로 몬테크리스토의 손을 꽉 잡았다.\n\n\"그래. 보다시피 나도 자네만큼 정확하지. 그런데 흠뻑 젖었군, 이 사람아. 옷을 갈아입어야겠어. 칼립소가 텔레마코스에게 말했듯이 말일세. 자, 자네를 위해 거처를 마련해 두었네. 거기서 곧 피곤도 추위도 잊게 될 걸세.\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 젊은이가 몸을 돌리는 것을 알아챘다. 과연 모렐은 자신을 데려다준 사람들이 삯도 받지 않고, 한마디 말도 없이 떠나 버린 것을 보고 놀랐다. 벌써 그들이 요트로 돌아가며 젓는 노 소리가 들려왔다.\n\n\"아, 그래.\" 백작이 말했다. \"선원들을 찾고 있군.\"\n\n\"네. 제가 삯을 한 푼도 치르지 않았는데, 그냥 가 버렸군요.\"\n\n\"그건 마음 쓰지 말게, 막시밀리앙.\" 몬테크리스토가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"내가 해군과 약조를 맺어 두었네. 내 섬을 드나드는 데는 어떤 삯도 받지 않기로 말일세. 그런 거래를 해 둔 거지.\"\n\n모렐이 놀란 눈으로 백작을 바라보았다. \"백작님.\" 그가 말했다. \"여기서는 파리에서와 같은 분이 아니시군요.\"\n\n\"어째서?\"\n\n\"여기서는 웃으시니까요.\" 백작의 이마가 흐려졌다.\n\n\"내 본분을 일깨워 주어 고맙네, 막시밀리앙.\" 그가 말했다. \"자네를 다시 만나 기뻐서, 모든 행복이 덧없다는 사실을 잠시 잊었던 모양일세.\"\n\n\"아, 아닙니다, 백작님.\" 막시밀리앙이 백작의 두 손을 붙잡으며 외쳤다. \"부디 웃으십시오. 행복해하시고, 그 무심함으로 제게 증명해 주십시오. 고통받는 자에게도 삶이 견딜 만한 것임을 말입니다. 아, 백작님은 얼마나 인정 많고 친절하고 선하신 분인지요. 제게 용기를 북돋아 주려고 일부러 그 명랑함을 꾸며 보이시는 거지요.\"\n\n\"틀렸네, 모렐. 나는 정말로 행복했어.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 저를 잊으신 거군요. 오히려 잘된 일입니다.\"\n\n\"어째서?\"\n\n\"네. 검투사가 원형 경기장에 들어서며 황제에게 했던 말처럼요. '이제 죽으러 가는 자, 당신께 경례를 올립니다.'\"\n\n\"그러면 자네는 마음을 추스르지 못했단 말인가?\" 백작이 놀라 물었다.\n\n\"아.\" 모렐이 쓰디쓴 원망이 가득한 눈길로 외쳤다. \"제가 그럴 수 있으리라 생각하셨습니까?\"\n\n\"내 말을 들어 보게.\" 백작이 말했다. \"자네는 내 말뜻을 이해하나? 자네는 나를 평범한 사람으로, 막연하고 의미 없는 소리나 떠들어 대는 한낱 수다쟁이로 여기지는 않겠지. 내가 자네에게 마음을 추슬렀느냐고 물을 때, 나는 인간의 마음에 아무런 비밀도 없는 사람으로서 자네에게 말하는 걸세. 자, 모렐, 우리 함께 자네 마음의 밑바닥을 들여다보세. 자네는 아직도 상처 입은 사자처럼 펄쩍 뛰게 만들던 그 열병 같은 슬픔의 조바심을 느끼나? 오직 무덤에서만 가실 수 있는 그 집어삼킬 듯한 갈증이 아직도 남아 있나? 산 자를 죽음의 추구로 끌어당기는 그 회한에 아직도 휘둘리고 있나? 아니면 그저 피로에 지치고, 미뤄진 희망에 지쳐 무너져 내린 것뿐인가? 기억을 잃은 탓에 더는 울 수조차 없게 된 건가? 아, 친구여, 만약 그렇다면, 자네가 더는 울 수 없다면, 자네의 얼어붙은 마음이 죽어 버렸다면, 자네의 모든 믿음을 신께 맡겼다면, 그렇다면 막시밀리앙, 자네는 마음을 추스른 걸세. 그러니 한탄하지 말게.\"\n\n\"백작님.\" 모렐이 단호하면서도 부드러운 목소리로 말했다. \"비록 땅에 발붙이고 있으나 그 생각만은 하늘로 향한 사람을 대하듯 제 말을 들어 주십시오. 저는 친구의 품에서 죽으러 왔습니다. 물론 제게도 사랑하는 사람들이 있습니다. 누이 쥘리를 사랑하고, 그 남편 에마뉘엘을 사랑합니다. 하지만 제 마지막 순간에 미소 지어 줄 강인한 사람이 필요합니다. 누이는 눈물에 젖어 정신을 잃을 테고, 저는 그녀가 괴로워하는 모습을 차마 견딜 수 없습니다. 에마뉘엘은 제 손에서 무기를 빼앗고, 비명을 질러 온 집안을 발칵 뒤집어 놓겠지요. 하지만 백작님, 인간 이상의 존재이신 당신께서는 분명 저를 편안한 길로 인도해 죽음에 이르게 해 주시겠지요, 그렇지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"친구여.\" 백작이 말했다. \"내게는 아직 한 가지 의심이 남아 있네. 자네는 자신의 고통을 자랑스러워할 만큼 나약한 사람인가?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 정말로요. 저는 평온합니다.\" 모렐이 백작에게 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"제 맥박은 평소보다 느리지도 빠르지도 않습니다. 그렇습니다. 저는 종착점에 다다랐음을 느끼고, 더는 나아가지 않을 겁니다. 백작님은 제게 기다리고 희망하라 하셨지요. 가엾은 충고자여, 당신이 무슨 짓을 하신 줄이나 아십니까? 저는 한 달을 기다렸습니다. 아니, 한 달을 고통받았다고 해야겠지요! 저는 정말로 희망했습니다. 인간이란 가련하고 비참한 존재이니까요. 정말로 희망했습니다. 무엇을 희망했는지는 저도 말할 수 없습니다. 무언가 경이로운 것, 터무니없는 것, 기적 같은 것을요. 그것이 어떤 성질의 것인지는, 우리의 이성에 희망이라 부르는 그 어리석음을 섞어 놓은 분만이 아실 겁니다. 그렇습니다, 저는 기다렸고, 희망했습니다, 백작님. 그리고 우리가 이렇게 함께 이야기를 나눈 십오 분 동안, 당신은 자신도 모르게 제 마음을 상하게 하고 고문하셨습니다. 당신이 내뱉으신 모든 말이, 제게는 아무 희망도 없다는 것을 증명했으니까요. 아, 백작님, 저는 죽음의 품속에서 평온하게, 달콤하게 잠들겠습니다.\"\n\n모렐이 이 말을 어찌나 힘주어 내뱉었던지, 백작이 몸서리쳤다.\n\n\"친구여.\" 모렐이 말을 이었다. \"백작님은 십월 오일을 기다림의 끝으로 정해 주셨지요. 오늘이 바로 십월 오일입니다.\" 그가 시계를 꺼냈다. \"지금이 아홉 시군요. 제게는 아직 살 시간이 세 시간 남았습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 좋네.\" 백작이 말했다. \"이리 오게.\" 모렐은 기계적으로 백작을 따라갔고, 미처 알아채기도 전에 동굴 안으로 들어와 있었다. 발밑에 양탄자가 밟혔고, 문 하나가 열렸으며, 향기가 그를 에워쌌고, 눈부신 빛이 그의 눈을 어지럽혔다. 모렐은 선뜻 앞으로 나아가기를 망설였다. 눈앞의 모든 것이 자신의 의지를 무르게 만들까 봐 두려웠던 것이다. 몬테크리스토가 부드럽게 그를 안으로 이끌었다.\n\n\"우리에게 남은 생의 마지막 세 시간을, 저 옛 로마인들처럼 보내지 않을 이유가 어디 있겠나? 황제이자 상속자인 네로에게 사형을 선고받았을 때, 그들은 꽃으로 뒤덮인 식탁에 앉아 헬리오트로프와 장미의 향기 속에서 고요히 죽음 속으로 미끄러져 들어갔다네.\"\n\n모렐이 미소 지었다. \"원하시는 대로 하지요.\" 그가 말했다. \"죽음은 어차피 죽음이니까요. 그것은 망각이고, 안식이며, 삶으로부터의, 따라서 슬픔으로부터의 격리입니다.\"\n\n그가 자리에 앉았고, 몬테크리스토는 그 맞은편에 앉았다. 그들은 앞서 묘사된 그 경이로운 식당 안에 있었다. 조각상들이 머리에 인 바구니에는 언제나 과일과 꽃이 가득했다. 모렐은 무심히 주위를 둘러보았으나, 아마 아무것도 눈에 들어오지 않았을 것이다.\n\n\"사내답게 이야기를 나누어 보지요.\" 그가 백작을 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"말해 보게!\"\n\n\"백작님.\" 모렐이 말했다. \"당신은 인간이 가진 모든 지식의 정수이시며, 우리보다 더 지혜롭고 더 진보한 세계에서 내려오신 존재 같습니다.\"\n\n\"자네 말에는 진실이 담겨 있네.\" 백작이 그를 그토록 잘생겨 보이게 하는 그 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"나는 슬픔이라는 별에서 내려왔지.\"\n\n\"저는 당신이 하시는 모든 말씀을 그 뜻을 따지지 않고 믿습니다. 이를테면 당신은 제게 살라고 하셨고, 저는 살았습니다. 희망하라 하셨고, 저는 거의 희망할 뻔했지요. 마치 당신이 죽음을 겪어 보신 분인 양, 이렇게 여쭙고 싶을 정도입니다. '죽는다는 것은 고통스럽습니까?'\"\n\n몬테크리스토는 형언할 수 없는 다정함으로 모렐을 바라보았다. \"그렇다네.\" 그가 말했다. \"그래, 분명 고통스럽지. 끈질기게 삶을 구걸하는 그 바깥 껍질을 거칠게 찢어 낸다면 말일세. 단검을 자네 살에 찔러 넣거나, 작은 충격에도 흐트러지는 자네의 뇌에 총알을 박아 넣는다면, 그렇다면 자네는 분명 고통을 느낄 테고, 그토록 비싼 값을 치르고 사들인 안식을 위해 삶을 버린 것을 후회할 걸세.\"\n\n\"네. 저도 삶에서와 마찬가지로 죽음에도 호사와 고통의 비밀이 있음을 압니다. 다만 그것을 이해하는 것이 관건이지요.\"\n\n\"옳은 말일세, 막시밀리앙. 우리가 죽음에 얼마나 정성을 들이느냐에 따라, 죽음은 유모처럼 우리를 부드럽게 흔들어 재우는 친구가 되기도 하고, 영혼을 육신에서 거칠게 잡아 뜯는 적이 되기도 하지. 언젠가 세상이 훨씬 더 나이 들고, 인류가 자연의 모든 파괴적인 힘을 다스려 인류 전체의 이로움에 쓰게 될 날이 오면, 그리고 인류가 방금 자네가 말했듯 죽음의 비밀을 알아내게 되면, 그때 죽음은 사랑하는 이의 품에서 잠드는 것처럼 달콤하고 황홀한 것이 될 걸세.\"\n\n\"그래서 만약 죽기를 바라신다면, 백작님은 이런 죽음을 택하시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그렇다네.\"\n모렐이 손을 내밀었다. \"이제 알겠습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"왜 저를 이곳, 망망대해 한가운데의 황량한 곳, 이 지하 궁전으로 데려오셨는지요. 저를 사랑하셨기 때문이지요, 그렇지 않습니까, 백작님? 우리가 이야기 나누던 그 달콤한 죽음의 방법 가운데 하나를 제게 베풀어 주실 만큼 저를 깊이 사랑하셨기 때문이지요. 고통 없는 죽음, 발랑틴의 이름을 부르며 당신의 손을 잡은 채 스러져 갈 수 있는 죽음을요.\"\n\n\"그래, 제대로 알아맞혔네, 모렐.\" 백작이 말했다. \"그것이 내 뜻이었지.\"\n\n\"감사합니다. 내일이면 더는 고통받지 않으리라는 생각이, 제 마음에 달콤하게 와닿습니다.\"\n\n\"그러면 자네는 아무것도 미련이 없나?\"\n\n\"없습니다.\" 모렐이 대답했다.\n\n\"나조차도?\" 백작이 깊은 감동에 잠겨 물었다. 모렐의 맑은 눈이 한순간 흐려지더니, 이내 예사롭지 않은 광채로 빛났고, 굵은 눈물 한 방울이 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸다.\n\n\"뭐라고?\" 백작이 말했다. \"자네는 아직 이 세상에 미련이 남았는데도, 죽으려는 건가?\"\n\n\"아, 제발 부탁입니다.\" 모렐이 낮은 목소리로 외쳤다. \"더는 한마디도 마십시오, 백작님. 제 형벌을 길게 끌지 말아 주십시오.\"\n\n백작은 그가 마음을 돌리려 한다고 생각했고, 그 믿음은 이프성에서 자신을 짓눌렀던 그 끔찍한 의심을 되살려 놓았다.\n\n'나는 이 사람을 행복하게 해 주려 애쓰고 있다.' 그가 생각했다. '나는 이 보상을, 내가 저지른 악행과 균형을 맞추기 위해 저울에 던져 넣는 추로 여긴다. 그런데 만약 내가 속고 있는 거라면, 만약 이 사람이 행복을 누릴 자격이 있을 만큼 불행하지는 않았던 거라면? 아아, 오직 선행으로만 악을 속죄할 수 있는 나는 대체 어찌 되는 것인가?'\n\n그러고는 소리 내어 말했다. \"들어 보게, 모렐. 자네의 슬픔이 크다는 건 알겠네. 하지만 그래도 자네는 자네의 영혼을 거는 것은 내키지 않겠지.\" 모렐이 슬프게 미소 지었다.\n\n\"백작님.\" 그가 말했다. \"맹세컨대 제 영혼은 더 이상 제 것이 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"막시밀리앙, 알다시피 나는 이 세상에 혈육이 하나도 없네. 나는 자네를 내 아들로 여기는 데 익숙해졌어. 그러니, 내 아들을 구하기 위해서라면 나는 내 목숨을, 아니 내 재산까지도 바치겠네.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이십니까?\"\n\n\"내 말은, 자네가 삶을 버리려는 까닭이 큰 재산이 가져다주는 온갖 즐거움을 다 헤아리지 못하기 때문이라는 걸세. 모렐, 나는 거의 일억에 가까운 돈을 가지고 있고, 그것을 자네에게 주겠네. 그만한 재산이 있으면 자네는 어떤 소망이든 이룰 수 있어. 자네는 야심이 있나? 어떤 길이든 자네 앞에 열려 있네. 세상을 뒤엎고, 그 성질을 바꾸고, 미친 생각에 몸을 맡기고, 심지어 죄인이 되어도 좋아. 다만 살게.\"\n\n\"백작님, 저는 당신의 약속을 받았습니다.\" 모렐이 차갑게 말했다. 그러고는 시계를 꺼내 덧붙였다. \"열한 시 반입니다.\"\n\n\"모렐, 정녕 내 집에서, 바로 내 눈앞에서 그럴 작정인가?\"\n\n\"그러면 저를 보내 주십시오.\" 막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"그렇지 않으면 저는 당신이 저를 저 자신을 위해서가 아니라 당신 자신을 위해 아끼셨다고 여길 겁니다.\" 그러고는 자리에서 일어났다.\n\n\"좋네.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다. 그 말에 그의 얼굴이 환해졌다. \"자네가 정녕 원하는 거라면, 자네는 한 치도 굽히지 않는군. 그래, 자네 말대로 자네는 정말로 비참하고, 오직 기적만이 자네를 고칠 수 있겠지. 앉게, 모렐. 그리고 기다리게.\"\n\n모렐이 그 말에 따랐다. 백작이 일어나, 금줄에 매달린 열쇠로 벽장을 열고, 그 안에서 아름답게 깎고 새긴 작은 은상자 하나를 꺼냈다. 상자의 네 귀퉁이에는, 카리아티데스와 비슷하게 몸을 굽힌 네 개의 형상이 새겨져 있었는데, 그것은 여인의 형상으로, 하늘로 오르기를 갈망하는 천사들을 상징하는 것이었다.\n\n그는 상자를 식탁 위에 놓았다. 그런 다음 그것을 열어 작은 황금 갑을 꺼냈는데, 숨겨진 용수철을 건드리자 뚜껑이 탁 열렸다. 그 갑 안에는 일부는 굳어 있는 기름진 물질이 들어 있었는데, 갑을 장식한 윤이 나는 황금과 사파이어, 루비, 에메랄드의 반사 때문에 그 색을 가늠하기란 불가능했다. 그것은 푸른빛과 붉은빛, 금빛이 뒤섞인 덩어리였다.\n\n백작이 금빛 숟가락으로 그 물질을 조금 떠서 모렐에게 내밀며, 그에게 길고 흔들림 없는 시선을 고정했다. 그제야 그 물질이 푸르스름한 빛을 띠고 있다는 것이 드러났다.\n\n\"이것이 자네가 청한 것이고,\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 주기로 약속한 것일세.\"\n\n\"진심으로 감사드립니다.\" 젊은이가 몬테크리스토의 손에서 숟가락을 받아 들며 말했다. 백작이 또 하나의 숟가락을 집어 다시 황금 갑에 담갔다. \"무엇을 하시려는 겁니까, 친구여?\" 모렐이 그의 손을 붙잡으며 물었다.\n\n\"실은 말일세, 모렐, 나도 삶에 지쳤다는 생각이 들었거든. 마침 이런 기회가 왔으니……\"\n\n\"멈추십시오!\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"사랑하고, 또 사랑받으시는 분, 믿음과 희망을 지니신 분께서, 아, 부디 제 본보기를 따르지 마십시오. 당신의 경우라면 그것은 죄악입니다. 안녕히 계십시오, 고결하고 너그러우신 친구여, 안녕히. 저는 가서 발랑틴에게 당신이 저를 위해 무엇을 해 주셨는지 전하겠습니다.\"\n\n그러고는 천천히, 그러나 조금의 망설임도 없이, 다만 백작의 손을 뜨겁게 한 번 잡으려 멈췄을 뿐, 그는 몬테크리스토가 내민 그 신비로운 물질을 삼켰다. 그러자 두 사람은 모두 침묵에 잠겼다. 말없이 빈틈없는 알리가 담뱃대와 커피를 가져다 놓고 사라졌다. 차츰 대리석 조각상들의 손에 들린 등불 빛이 점점 희미해졌고, 모렐에게는 향기도 덜 강하게 느껴졌다. 맞은편에 앉은 몬테크리스토는 어둠 속에서 그를 지켜보았고, 모렐의 눈에는 백작의 빛나는 두 눈 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않았다. 압도적인 슬픔이 젊은이를 사로잡았고, 그의 두 손에서 힘이 풀렸으며, 방 안의 물건들이 차츰 형태와 색을 잃어 갔고, 흐트러진 그의 시야에는 벽 안의 문과 휘장이 열리는 것처럼 보였다.\n\n\"친구여.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"제가 죽어 가는 것이 느껴집니다. 감사합니다!\"\n\n그는 마지막 안간힘을 다해 손을 내밀려 했으나, 그 손은 힘없이 옆으로 떨어졌다. 그러자 몬테크리스토가 미소 짓는 것처럼 보였다. 이따금 그에게 자기 마음속 비밀을 드러내 보이던 그 기이하고 무서운 표정이 아니라, 자식을 향한 아버지의 자애로운 다정함이 담긴 미소였다. 동시에 백작의 키가 점점 커지는 듯했고, 평소의 거의 두 배에 이른 그의 모습이 붉은 벽걸이를 배경으로 도드라졌으며, 그의 검은 머리카락은 뒤로 젖혀졌고, 그는 복수하는 천사의 자세로 서 있었다. 모렐은 압도당한 채 안락의자에 몸을 기댔고, 달콤한 마비가 혈관 구석구석으로 스며들었다. 만화경 위의 새로운 무늬처럼, 갖가지 생각이 그의 뇌리에 떠올랐다. 기력이 빠지고, 늘어지고, 숨이 막힌 채, 그는 바깥 사물을 더 이상 의식하지 못하게 되었다. 그는 죽음에 앞서는 그 어렴풋한 착란 속으로 들어서는 듯했다. 그는 다시 한번 백작의 손을 잡고 싶었으나, 자신의 손은 꼼짝도 하지 않았다. 마지막 작별 인사를 입에 담고 싶었으나, 그의 혀는 무덤 입구를 막은 돌처럼 목구멍 속에 무겁게 굳어 있었다. 저도 모르게 나른한 두 눈이 감겼고, 그래도 속눈썹 사이로, 자신을 감쌌다고 여긴 그 어둠 속에서 낯익은 형상 하나가 움직이는 듯했다.\n\n백작이 방금 문 하나를 연 참이었다. 곧바로 옆방, 아니 그보다는 잇닿은 궁전에서 흘러나온 눈부신 빛이, 그가 마지막 잠 속으로 고요히 미끄러져 들어가고 있던 방을 비추었다. 그때 그는 두 방을 가르는 문지방에 경이로운 아름다움을 지닌 한 여인이 나타나는 것을 보았다. 창백한 얼굴로 부드럽게 미소 짓는 그녀는, 복수의 천사를 달래는 자비의 천사처럼 보였다.\n\n'내 앞에 열리는 것이 천국인가?' 죽어 가는 사내가 생각했다. '저 천사는 내가 잃은 그 사람을 닮았구나.'\n\n몬테크리스토가 젊은 여인에게 모렐을 가리켜 보였고, 그녀는 두 손을 맞잡고 입가에 미소를 띤 채 그에게로 다가왔다.\n\n\"발랑틴, 발랑틴!\" 그가 마음속으로 부르짖었다. 그러나 그의 입술은 아무 소리도 내지 못했고, 마치 그의 모든 힘이 그 내면의 격정 속에 모인 듯, 그는 한숨을 내쉬며 눈을 감았다. 발랑틴이 그에게로 달려갔다. 그의 입술이 다시 움직였다.\n\n\"그가 당신을 부르고 있소.\" 백작이 말했다. \"당신이 운명을 맡긴 사람, 죽음이 당신과 갈라놓았을 사람이, 당신을 제 곁으로 부르고 있소. 다행히 내가 죽음을 이겨 냈소. 이제부터 발랑틴, 당신들은 이 땅에서 다시는 헤어지지 않을 거요. 그가 당신을 찾으러 죽음 속으로 뛰어들었으니까. 내가 없었다면 두 사람 다 죽었을 거요. 이 두 생명을 지킨 것으로, 부디 신께서 내 속죄를 받아 주시기를!\"\n\n발랑틴이 백작의 손을 붙잡더니, 억누를 수 없는 기쁨의 충동에 휩싸여 그것을 입술로 가져갔다.\n\n\"아, 다시 한번 내게 고맙다고 해 주오!\" 백작이 말했다. \"지칠 때까지 말해 주오, 내가 당신을 행복으로 되돌려 놓았다고 말이오. 내가 이 확언을 얼마나 간절히 바라는지 당신은 모를 거요.\"\n\n\"아, 네, 네, 진심으로 감사드립니다.\" 발랑틴이 말했다. \"제 감사가 진심인지 의심스러우시거든, 아, 그렇다면 하이데에게 물어보세요! 제 사랑하는 언니 하이데에게요. 프랑스를 떠나온 뒤로 줄곧 당신 이야기를 들려주며, 제가 이 행복한 날을 인내심 있게 기다리게 해 준 사람이랍니다.\"\n\n\"그러면 당신은 하이데를 사랑하오?\" 몬테크리스토가 애써 감추려 했으나 끝내 감추지 못한 감동에 잠겨 물었다.\n\n\"아, 네, 온 영혼을 다해서요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 들어 보오, 발랑틴.\" 백작이 말했다. \"당신에게 한 가지 청이 있소.\"\n\n\"제게요? 아, 제가 그럴 자격이 있을 만큼 행복한 사람인가요?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 당신은 하이데를 언니라 불렀소. 그러니 그녀를 정말로 언니로 삼아 주오, 발랑틴. 당신이 내게 빚졌다고 여기는 그 모든 감사를 그녀에게 베풀어 주오. 그녀를 지켜 주오. 왜냐하면\" 백작의 목소리가 감동으로 잠겨 갔다. \"이제부터 그녀는 이 세상에 홀로 남을 테니까.\"\n\n\"이 세상에 홀로요!\" 백작 뒤에서 한 목소리가 되뇌었다. \"어째서요?\"\n\n몬테크리스토가 몸을 돌렸다. 하이데가 창백한 얼굴로 꼼짝도 않고 서서, 두려운 경악이 어린 표정으로 백작을 바라보고 있었다.\n\n\"내일이면, 하이데, 너는 자유의 몸이 될 테니까. 그때 너는 사회에서 너에게 마땅한 자리를 되찾을 것이다. 나는 내 운명이 네 운명을 어둡게 뒤덮도록 내버려 두지 않을 것이다. 한 군주의 딸이여, 나는 네게 네 아버지의 재산과 이름을 돌려주겠다.\"\n\n하이데의 얼굴이 창백해졌다. 그녀는 투명한 두 손을 하늘로 들어 올리며, 눈물에 잠겨 막히는 목소리로 외쳤다. \"그러면 저를 떠나시는 건가요, 주인님?\"\n\n\"하이데, 하이데, 너는 젊고 아름답다. 내 이름조차 잊고, 행복하게 살아라.\"\n\n\"알겠습니다.\" 하이데가 말했다. \"분부대로 따르겠습니다, 주인님. 저는 당신의 이름조차 잊고, 행복하게 살겠습니다.\" 그러고는 물러가려고 뒷걸음질을 쳤다.\n\n\"아, 맙소사.\" 모렐의 머리를 자기 어깨에 기대게 하고 있던 발랑틴이 외쳤다. \"저 언니가 얼마나 창백한지 안 보이세요? 얼마나 괴로워하는지 안 보이시나요?\"\n\n하이데가 가슴 찢어지는 표정으로 대답했다.\n\n\"저이가 그것을 어찌 이해하겠어요, 동생? 저이는 제 주인이고, 저는 그분의 노예예요. 그분께는 아무것도 알아채지 않을 권리가 있답니다.\"\n\n백작은 자기 마음 가장 깊은 곳까지 파고드는 그 목소리의 울림에 몸서리쳤다. 그의 눈이 젊은 처녀의 눈과 마주쳤고, 그는 그 눈의 광채를 견딜 수 없었다.\n\n\"아, 맙소사.\" 몬테크리스토가 외쳤다. \"내 의심이 옳단 말인가? 하이데, 나를 떠나지 않는 것이 네 마음에 들겠느냐?\"\n\n\"저는 젊습니다.\" 하이데가 부드럽게 대답했다. \"저는 당신이 그토록 달콤하게 만들어 주신 이 삶을 사랑합니다. 그러니 죽는다면 슬플 거예요.\"\n\n\"그러면 네 말은, 내가 너를 떠나면, 하이데……\"\n\n\"저는 죽을 거예요. 네, 주인님.\"\n\n\"그러면 너는 나를 사랑하느냐?\"\n\n\"아, 발랑틴, 이분이 나를 사랑하느냐고 물으시네. 발랑틴, 네가 막시밀리앙을 사랑하는지 이분께 말씀드려 줘.\"\n\n백작은 자기 심장이 부풀어 오르며 고동치는 것을 느꼈다. 그가 두 팔을 벌리자, 하이데가 외마디 소리를 지르며 그 품속으로 뛰어들었다.\n\n\"아, 네.\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"저는 당신을 사랑해요! 아버지를, 오라비를, 남편을 사랑하듯 당신을 사랑합니다! 저는 제 목숨처럼 당신을 사랑해요. 당신은 창조된 모든 존재 가운데 가장 선하고 가장 고귀한 분이니까요!\"\n\n\"그러면 네가 원하는 대로 하자꾸나, 사랑스러운 천사여. 신께서는 내가 적들과 싸우는 동안 나를 지탱해 주셨고, 내게 이 보상을 내려 주셨다. 신께서는 내 승리를 고통 속에서 끝내도록 두지 않으실 것이다. 나는 스스로를 벌하려 했으나, 신께서 나를 용서해 주셨다. 그러니 나를 사랑해 다오, 하이데! 누가 알겠느냐? 어쩌면 네 사랑이, 내가 기억하고 싶지 않은 그 모든 것을 잊게 해 줄지도 모르지.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이세요, 주인님?\"\n\n\"내 말은, 네 말 한마디가 이십 년에 걸친 더딘 경험보다도 나를 더 환히 깨우쳤다는 것이다. 하이데, 내게는 이 세상에 너밖에 없다. 너를 통해 나는 다시 삶을 붙잡고, 너를 통해 괴로워하며, 너를 통해 기뻐할 것이다.\"\n\n\"들었어, 발랑틴?\" 하이데가 외쳤다. \"이분이 나를 통해 괴로워하실 거래. 그분을 위해서라면 내 목숨도 내놓을 이 나를 통해서 말이야.\"\n\n백작이 잠시 물러섰다. '내가 진실을 알아낸 것인가?' 그가 말했다. '하지만 그것이 보상이든 형벌이든, 나는 내 운명을 받아들이겠다. 가자, 하이데, 가자!' 그러고는 한 팔로 젊은 처녀의 허리를 감싸 안고, 발랑틴의 손을 한 번 잡은 뒤 사라졌다.\n\n거의 한 시간이 흘렀고, 그동안 발랑틴은 숨죽인 채 꼼짝도 않고 모렐을 한결같이 지켜보았다. 마침내 그녀는 그의 심장이 뛰는 것을 느꼈고, 희미한 숨결이 그의 입술 위로 노닐었으며, 생명이 돌아오고 있음을 알리는 가벼운 떨림이 젊은이의 온몸을 스쳐 지나갔다. 이윽고 그의 두 눈이 뜨였으나, 처음에는 초점 없이 아무 표정도 없었다. 그러다 시력이 돌아왔고, 그와 더불어 감각과 슬픔도 돌아왔다.\n\n\"아.\" 그가 절망에 찬 어조로 외쳤다. \"백작이 나를 속였구나. 나는 아직 살아 있어.\" 그러고는 식탁 쪽으로 손을 뻗어 칼 한 자루를 움켜쥐었다.\n\n\"여보.\" 발랑틴이 사랑스러운 미소를 띠며 외쳤다. \"깨어나세요, 그리고 저를 봐요!\" 모렐이 큰 소리로 외마디를 내질렀고, 천상의 환영이라도 본 듯 미친 듯이, 미심쩍어하며, 눈이 부신 채로, 그는 무릎을 꿇고 쓰러졌다.\n\n이튿날 동틀 무렵, 발랑틴과 모렐은 팔짱을 끼고 바닷가를 거닐었다. 발랑틴은 몬테크리스토가 어떻게 자기 방에 나타나 모든 것을 설명하고, 그 범죄를 밝혀냈으며, 끝내 그녀가 죽음을 가장할 수 있게 함으로써 어떻게 목숨을 구해 주었는지를 이야기했다.\n\n그들은 동굴 문이 열려 있는 것을 발견하고 밖으로 나온 참이었다. 하늘의 푸른 둥근 천장에는 아직 몇 개의 별이 반짝이고 있었다.\n\n모렐은 곧 바위틈에 한 남자가 서서, 다가오라는 신호를 기다리는 듯한 모습을 알아채고는 발랑틴에게 가리켜 보였다.\n\n\"아, 야코포군요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"요트의 선장이에요.\" 그러고는 그를 가까이 오라고 손짓했다.\n\n\"우리에게 할 말이 있나요?\" 모렐이 물었다.\n\n\"백작님께서 두 분께 전하라는 편지가 있습니다.\"\n\n\"백작님께서!\" 두 젊은이가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"네. 읽어 보십시오.\"\n\n모렐이 편지를 펼쳐 읽었다.\n\n\"사랑하는 막시밀리앙,\n\n자네를 위해 작은 범선 한 척이 닻을 내리고 있네. 야코포가 자네를 리보르노까지 데려다줄 걸세. 그곳에서 누아르티에 씨가 손녀를 기다리고 있다네. 자네가 그 아이를 제단으로 인도하기 전에 축복을 내려 주고 싶으시다는군. 친구여, 이 동굴 안에 있는 모든 것과, 샹젤리제의 내 집, 그리고 트레포르의 내 성은, 에드몽 당테스가 옛 주인 모렐의 아들에게 보내는 결혼 선물일세. 빌포르 양도 자네와 함께 그것을 나누어 갖게 될 거야. 그녀에게 부탁하건대, 이제 미치광이가 되어 버린 아버지와, 지난 구월에 어머니와 함께 죽은 남동생으로부터 그녀에게 돌아오는 그 막대한 재산은 가난한 이들에게 나누어 주기를 바라네. 모렐, 자네의 앞날을 지켜 줄 그 천사에게 전해 주게. 한때 사탄처럼 한순간 자신을 신과 대등하다 여겼으나, 이제는 그리스도교인의 겸허함으로 오직 신만이 지고의 권능과 무한한 지혜를 지니셨음을 인정하는 한 사내를 위해, 가끔은 기도해 달라고 말일세. 어쩌면 그 기도가, 그의 마음속에 있는 회한을 누그러뜨려 줄지도 모르지. 그리고 자네에게는, 모렐, 이것이 자네를 대한 내 행동의 비밀일세. 이 세상에는 행복도 불행도 없다네. 다만 한 상태를 다른 상태와 견주는 것이 있을 뿐, 그 이상은 없어. 가장 깊은 슬픔을 맛본 사람만이 지고의 행복을 누릴 수 있는 법일세. 모렐, 우리는 죽는다는 것이 어떤 것인지 느껴 보아야만, 산다는 것의 즐거움을 제대로 음미할 수 있다네.\n\n그러니 살게, 그리고 행복하게나, 내 마음의 사랑하는 아이들이여. 그리고 신께서 인간에게 미래를 드러내 보여 주실 그날까지, 인간의 모든 지혜는 이 두 마디 말로 요약된다는 것을 결코 잊지 말게. '기다리고 희망하라.' 자네의 친구,\n\n에드몽 당테스, 몬테크리스토 백작.\"\n\n발랑틴은 아버지의 광증과 남동생의 죽음을 이 편지로 처음 알게 되었는데, 그것을 읽어 내려가는 동안 그녀의 얼굴이 창백해졌고, 가슴에서 무거운 한숨이 새어 나왔으며, 소리 없는 만큼 더 가슴 아픈 눈물이 두 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸다. 그녀의 행복은 너무도 값비싼 대가를 치른 것이었다.\n\n모렐이 불안하게 주위를 둘러보았다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 그가 말했다. \"백작님의 너그러움은 너무도 압도적이오. 발랑틴은 내 보잘것없는 재산으로도 만족할 거요. 백작님은 어디 계시오, 친구? 그분께로 나를 데려다주오.\"\n\n야코포가 수평선을 가리켰다.\n\n\"무슨 뜻이지요?\" 발랑틴이 물었다. \"백작님은 어디 계세요? 하이데는 어디 있고요?\"\n\n\"보십시오!\" 야코포가 말했다.\n\n두 사람의 눈이 선원이 가리킨 지점에 고정되었고, 하늘과 지중해를 가르는 그 푸른 선 위로, 그들은 커다란 흰 돛 하나를 알아보았다.\n\n\"떠나셨군.\" 모렐이 말했다. \"떠나셨어! 안녕히, 나의 친구여, 안녕히, 나의 아버지여!\"\n\n\"떠나셨어.\" 발랑틴이 중얼거렸다. \"안녕, 나의 사랑스러운 하이데, 안녕, 나의 언니!\"\n\n\"우리가 그분들을 언젠가 다시 만날 수 있을지, 누가 알겠소?\" 모렐이 눈물 어린 눈으로 말했다.\n\n\"여보.\" 발랑틴이 대답했다. \"백작님이 방금 우리에게 말씀해 주시지 않았나요? 인간의 모든 지혜는 두 마디 말로 요약된다고요.\n\n'기다리고 희망하라!'\"\n각주\n\n[1] \"악한 자들은 물을 많이 들이켜는 자들이라, 그 옛날 대홍수가 단번에 증명하였듯이.\"\n\n[2] 1894년 기준 260만 달러.\n\n[3] 머리를 한 대 얻어맞다.\n\n[4] 참수당하다.\n\n[5] 물론 스콧이다. \"불운한 아버지의 아들이자, 그보다 더 불행한 가문의 아버지였던 그는, 그 얼굴에 불길한 우수의 빛을 띠고 있었으니, 당시의 관상가들은 그것으로 격렬하고 불행한 죽음을 타고난 자들을 가려낸다고 주장하였다.\" ―『수도원장』 22장.\n\n[6] 단두대.\n\n[7] 기요탱 박사는 이탈리아에서 사형 집행을 목격하고 그 유명한 기계에 대한 착상을 얻었다.\n\n[8] 브루케아 페루기네아.\n\n[9] '돈과 거룩함, 저마다 절반씩.'\n\n[10] 엘리자베트 드 로상, 강주 후작 부인은 루이 14세 궁정의 이름난 여인들 가운데 하나로, 그곳에서 \"라 벨 프로방살(아름다운 프로방스 여인)\"로 불렸다. 그녀는 카스텔란 후작의 미망인이던 시절에 강주와 재혼했는데, 불행히도 새 시숙들의 적의를 사는 바람에, 그들의 강요로 독약을 마셔야 했다. 그리고 그들은 권총과 단검으로 그녀의 숨통을 끊었다. ―편집자\n\n[11] 위대한 웅변을 지닌 법관이자 연설가―루이 15세 치하 프랑스의 대법관.\n\n[12] 자크루이 다비드, 프랑스의 유명한 화가(1748~1825).\n\n[13] \"사자\"라 불린 알리 파샤는 1741년, 클리수라산맥 기슭의 알바니아 마을 테펠리니에서 태어났다. 그는 외교와 무공으로 알바니아, 에피루스 및 인접 영토의 거의 절대적인 지배자가 되었다. 술탄의 적의를 산 끝에, 그는 추방령을 받고 1822년 여든의 나이로 배신에 의해 살해되었다. ―편집자\n\n[14] 독립 전쟁에 참전한 그리스 민병들. ―편집자\n\n[15] 한 지방의 군대를 지휘하는 튀르크의 파샤. ―편집자\n\n[16] 그리스 신화에 나오는 풍요의 신. 크레타에서 그는 초목이 시드는 겨울에 죽임을 당하고 봄에 되살아난다고 여겨졌다. 하이데의 박식한 인용은 디오니소스 축제에서 배우가 보이는 행동을 가리킨다. ―편집자\n\n[17] 제노바의 음모가.\n\n[18] 마조레호.\n\n[19] 옛 그리스 전설에서, 아트레우스의 자손들인 아트레이다이는 그 아버지가 저지른 가증스러운 죄로 인해 벌을 받을 운명이었다. 아이스킬로스의 『아가멤논』은 이 전설에 바탕을 두고 있다.\n\n[20] 민법상의 혼인 절차.\n\n[21] 몰리에르의 희극 『인간 혐오자』에서.\n\n[22] 글자 그대로는 \"바구니\". 결혼 선물을 본래 그런 용기에 담아 가져왔기 때문이다.\n\n[23] 제르맹 필롱은 프랑스의 유명한 조각가(1535~1598)였다. 가장 잘 알려진 작품은 현재 루브르에 있는 「세 우미의 여신」이다.\n\n[24] 프레데리크 르메트르―프랑스의 배우(1800~1876). 로베르 마케르는 두 인기 멜로드라마―「몽타르지의 개」와 「오브리의 개」―의 주인공으로, 그 이름은 대담한 범죄자들에게 조롱조로 붙여진다.\n\n[25] 스파이는 아프리카 복무를 위해 따로 둔 프랑스 기병대다.\n\n[26] 사바트: 낡은 신발.\n\n[27] 길베르 드 픽세레쿠르, 프랑스의 극작가(1773~1844).\n\n[28] 조각가이자 건축가인 가스파르 퓌제는 1615년 마르세유에서 태어났다.\n\n[29] 버지니아가 아니라 캐롤라이나 재스민, 곧 젤세미움 셈페르비렌스(엄밀히 말하면 재스민이 전혀 아니다)는 노란 꽃이 핀다. 이 언급은 분명 위스타리아 프루테스켄스(미국등나무)를 가리키는 것이다. ―편집자\n\n[30] 몰리에르의 희극 『수전노』에 나오는 구두쇠. ―편집자";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-1184"]=[[["VOLUME ONE Chapter 1. Marseilles—The Arrival Chapter 2. Father and Son Chapter 3. The Catalans Chapter 4. Conspiracy Chapter 5. The Marriage Feast Chapter 6. The Deputy Procureur du Roi Chapter 7. The Examination Chapter 8. The Château d’If Chapter 9. The Evening of the Betrothal Chapter 10. The King’s Closet at the Tuileries Chapter 11. The Corsican Ogre Chapter 12. Father and Son Chapter 13. The Hundred Days Chapter 14. The Two Prisoners Chapter 15. Number 34 and Number 27 Chapter 16. A Learned Italian Chapter 17. The Abbé’s Chamber Chapter 18. The Treasure Chapter 19. The Third Attack Chapter 20. The Cemetery of the Château d’If Chapter 21. The Island of Tiboulen Chapter 22. The Smugglers Chapter 23. The Island of Monte Cristo Chapter 24. The Secret Cave Chapter 25. The Unknown Chapter 26. The Pont du Gard Inn Chapter 27. The Story\nVOLUME TWO Chapter 28. The Prison Register Chapter 29. The House of Morrel & Son Chapter 30. The Fifth of September Chapter 31. Italy: Sinbad the Sailor Chapter 32. The Waking Chapter 33. Roman Bandits Chapter 34. The Colosseum Chapter 35. La Mazzolata Chapter 36. The Carnival at Rome. Chapter 37. The Catacombs of Saint Sebastian Chapter 38. The Rendezvous Chapter 39. The Guests Chapter 40. The Breakfast Chapter 41. The Presentation Chapter 42. Monsieur Bertuccio Chapter 43. The House at Auteuil Chapter 44. The Vendetta Chapter 45. The Rain of Blood Chapter 46. Unlimited Credit Chapter 47. The Dappled Grays", "1815년 2월 24일, 노트르담드라가르드 망루의 감시병이 스미르나와 트리에스테, 나폴리를 거쳐 들어오는 삼범선 파라옹호를 발견하고 신호를 보냈다."]], [["VOLUME THREE Chapter 48. Ideology Chapter 49. Haydée Chapter 50. The Morrel Family Chapter 51. Pyramus and Thisbe Chapter 52. Toxicology Chapter 53. Robert le Diable Chapter 54. A Flurry in Stocks Chapter 55. Major Cavalcanti Chapter 56. Andrea Cavalcanti Chapter 57. In the Lucern Patch Chapter 58. M. Noirtier de Villefort Chapter 59. The Will Chapter 60. The Telegraph Chapter 61. How a Gardener May Get Rid of the Dormice Chapter 62. Ghosts Chapter 63. The Dinner Chapter 64. The Beggar Chapter 65. A Conjugal Scene Chapter 66. Matrimonial Projects Chapter 67. The Office of the King’s Attorney Chapter 68. A Summer Ball Chapter 69. The Inquiry Chapter 70. The Ball Chapter 71. Bread and Salt Chapter 72. Madame de Saint-Méran Chapter 73. The Promise\nVOLUME FOUR Chapter 74. The Villefort Family Vault Chapter 75. A Signed Statement Chapter 76. Progress of Cavalcanti the Younger Chapter 77. Haydée Chapter 78. We hear From Yanina Chapter 79. The Lemonade Chapter 80. The Accusation Chapter 81. The Room of the Retired Baker Chapter 82. The Burglary Chapter 83. The Hand of God Chapter 84. Beauchamp Chapter 85. The Journey Chapter 86. The Trial Chapter 87. The Challenge Chapter 88. The Insult Chapter 89. The Night Chapter 90. The Meeting Chapter 91. Mother and Son Chapter 92. The Suicide Chapter 93. Valentine Chapter 94. Maximilian’s Avowal Chapter 95. Father and Daughter", "여느 때처럼 곧바로 도선사가 작은 배를 띄웠고, 이프성을 돌아 모르지우곶과 리옹섬 사이에서 배에 올랐다."]], [["# Chapter 1. Marseilles—The Arrival", "# Chapter 1. Marseilles—The Arrival"]], [["VOLUME FIVE Chapter 96. The Contract Chapter 97. The Departure for Belgium Chapter 98. The Bell and Bottle Tavern Chapter 99. The Law Chapter 100. The Apparition Chapter 101. Locusta Chapter 102. Valentine Chapter 103. Maximilian Chapter 104. Danglars’ Signature Chapter 105. The Cemetery of Père-Lachaise Chapter 106. Dividing the Proceeds Chapter 107. The Lions’ Den Chapter 108. The Judge Chapter 109. The Assizes Chapter 110. The Indictment Chapter 111. Expiation Chapter 112. The Departure Chapter 113. The Past Chapter 114. Peppino Chapter 115. Luigi Vampa’s Bill of Fare Chapter 116. The Pardon Chapter 117. The Fifth of October\nOn the 24th of February, 1815, the look-out at Notre-Dame de la Garde signalled the three-master, the _Pharaon_ from Smyrna, Trieste, and Naples.", "언제나 그렇듯 곧 생장 요새의 성벽이 구경꾼들로 뒤덮였다. 마르세유에서 배 한 척이 입항하는 것은 늘 하나의 사건이었다. 더구나 그 배가 파라옹호처럼 오래된 포세 부두에서 건조되고 의장을 갖추고 짐을 실은 데다, 그 도시 선주의 소유라면 말할 것도 없었다."]], [["As usual, a pilot put off immediately, and rounding the Château d’If, got on board the vessel between Cape Morgiou and Rion island.", "배는 점점 다가와 칼라사레뉴섬과 자로스섬 사이에 화산의 충격이 만들어 놓은 해협을 무사히 통과했고, 포메그섬을 돌아 윗돛과 삼각돛, 후장 종범을 펼친 채 항구로 다가왔다. 그러나 그 속도가 너무 느리고 침울해서, 불길한 일을 미리 감지하는 본능을 지닌 구경꾼들은 배에 무슨 변고가 생긴 게 아니냐고 서로 물었다. 그러나 항해에 밝은 사람들은 사고가 있었다 해도 배 자체에 일어난 일은 아니라는 것을 한눈에 알아보았다."], ["Immediately, and according to custom, the ramparts of Fort Saint-Jean were covered with spectators; it is always an event at Marseilles for a ship to come into port, especially when this ship, like the _Pharaon_, has been built, rigged, and laden at the old Phocée docks, and belongs to an owner of the city.", "배는 능숙하게 다루어진 흔적이 역력했다. 닻은 내릴 채비가 되어 있었고, 제2사장 받침줄은 이미 풀려 있었으며, 도선사 곁에는 한 젊은이가 서서 파라옹호를 내항의 좁은 입구로 이끌고 있었다. 그는 민첩하고 경계 어린 눈으로 배의 모든 움직임을 살피며 도선사의 지시를 하나하나 복창했다."]], [["The ship drew on and had safely passed the strait, which some volcanic shock has made between the Calasareigne and Jaros islands; had doubled Pomègue, and approached the harbor under topsails, jib, and spanker, but so slowly and sedately that the idlers, with that instinct which is the forerunner of evil, asked one another what misfortune could have happened on board. However, those experienced in navigation saw plainly that if any accident had occurred, it was not to the vessel herself, for she bore down with all the evidence of being skilfully handled, the anchor a-cockbill, the jib-boom guys already eased off, and standing by the side of the pilot, who was steering the _Pharaon_ towards the narrow entrance of the inner port, was a young man, who, with activity and vigilant eye, watched every motion of the ship, and repeated each direction of the pilot.", "구경꾼들 사이에 감도는 막연한 불안이 무리 가운데 한 사람을 어찌나 자극했던지, 그는 배가 항구에 들어오기를 기다리지 못하고 작은 거룻배에 뛰어올라 파라옹호 곁으로 저어 가게 했다."], ["The vague disquietude which prevailed among the spectators had so much affected one of the crowd that he did not await the arrival of the vessel in harbor, but jumping into a small skiff, desired to be pulled alongside the _Pharaon_, which he reached as she rounded into La Réserve basin.", "그는 배가 라레제르브 정박지로 돌아드는 지점에서 배에 닿았다."]], [["When the young man on board saw this person approach, he left his station by the pilot, and, hat in hand, leaned over the ship’s bulwarks.", "배 위의 젊은이는 그 사람이 다가오는 것을 보자 도선사 곁의 자리를 떠나 모자를 손에 쥐고 뱃전 난간 너머로 몸을 기울였다."]], [["He was a fine, tall, slim young fellow of eighteen or twenty, with black eyes, and hair as dark as a raven’s wing; and his whole appearance bespoke that calmness and resolution peculiar to men accustomed from their cradle to contend with danger.", "그는 열여덟이나 스무 살쯤 된, 잘생기고 키가 크며 호리호리한 젊은이였다. 까만 눈에 머리카락은 까마귀 날개처럼 검었다. 그 모습 전체에서 요람에서부터 위험과 맞서 온 사람 특유의 침착함과 결단력이 묻어났다."]], [["“Ah, is it you, Dantès?”", "\"아, 자넨가, 당테스?\""], ["cried the man in the skiff.", "거룻배에 탄 사람이 외쳤다."], ["“What’s the matter?", "\"무슨 일인가?"], ["and why have you such an air of sadness aboard?”", "어쩌자고 배 위에서 그렇게 침울한 얼굴을 하고 있나?\""]], [["“A great misfortune, M. Morrel,”", "\"큰 변고가 있었습니다, 모렐 씨.\""], ["replied the young man, “a great misfortune, for me especially!", "젊은이가 대답했다. \"큰 변고가요, 특히 저에게는요!"], ["Off Civita Vecchia we lost our brave Captain Leclere.”", "치비타베키아 앞바다에서 우리의 용감하신 르클레르 선장님을 잃었습니다.\""]], [["“And the cargo?”", "\"그럼 화물은?\""], ["inquired the owner, eagerly.", "선주가 다급히 물었다."]], [["“Is all safe, M. Morrel; and I think you will be satisfied on that head.", "\"모두 무사합니다, 모렐 씨. 그 점은 만족하실 겁니다."], ["But poor Captain Leclere——”", "하지만 가엾은 르클레르 선장님은……\""]], [["“What happened to him?” asked the owner, with an air of considerable resignation.", "\"그분에게 무슨 일이 있었나?\" 선주가 적잖이 체념한 듯한 기색으로 물었다."], ["“What happened to the worthy captain?”", "\"그 훌륭하신 선장에게 무슨 일이 있었어?\""]], [["“He died.”", "\"돌아가셨습니다.\""]], [["“Fell into the sea?”", "\"바다에 빠지셨나?\""]], [["“No, sir, he died of brain-fever in dreadful agony.”", "\"아닙니다, 선주님. 뇌염으로 끔찍한 고통 속에 돌아가셨습니다.\""], ["Then turning to the crew, he said, “Bear a hand there, to take in sail!”", "그러고는 선원들 쪽으로 돌아서서 말했다. \"거기, 손들 빌려서 돛을 거두게!\""]], [["All hands obeyed, and at once the eight or ten seamen who composed the crew, sprang to their respective stations at the spanker brails and outhaul, topsail sheets and halyards, the jib downhaul, and the topsail clewlines and buntlines.", "모두가 명령에 따랐다. 여덟이나 열 명쯤 되는 선원들이 일제히 제자리로 달려갔다. 후장 종범의 졸인줄과 끌어내는 줄, 윗돛 아딧줄과 올림줄, 삼각돛 내림줄, 윗돛 거두는 줄과 묶는 줄로 흩어졌다."], ["The young sailor gave a look to see that his orders were promptly and accurately obeyed, and then turned again to the owner.", "젊은 선원은 자신의 명령이 신속하고 정확하게 이행되는지 한번 살피고는 다시 선주에게로 돌아섰다."]], [["“And how did this misfortune occur?” inquired the latter, resuming the interrupted conversation.", "\"그래, 어쩌다 그런 변고가 생겼나?\" 선주가 끊겼던 대화를 이어 물었다."]], [["0023m\n“Alas, sir, in the most unexpected manner.", "\"아아, 선주님, 정말 뜻밖의 일이었습니다."], ["After a long talk with the harbor-master, Captain Leclere left Naples greatly disturbed in mind.", "르클레르 선장님은 항만장과 한참 이야기를 나누신 뒤 몹시 심란한 마음으로 나폴리를 떠나셨습니다."], ["In twenty-four hours he was attacked by a fever, and died three days afterwards.", "스물네 시간 만에 열병에 걸리셨고, 사흘 뒤에 돌아가셨지요."], ["We performed the usual burial service, and he is at his rest, sewn up in his hammock with a thirty-six-pound shot at his head and his heels, off El Giglio island.", "우리는 관례대로 수장식을 치렀습니다. 선장님은 엘질리오섬 앞바다에서 머리맡과 발치에 36파운드 포탄을 매단 채 해먹에 꿰매어져 편히 잠드셨습니다."], ["We bring to his widow his sword and cross of honor. It was worth while, truly,”", "미망인께는 선장님의 검과 명예 십자훈장을 가져다 드릴 겁니다. 정말이지,\""], ["added the young man with a melancholy smile, “to make war against the English for ten years, and to die in his bed at last, like everybody else.”", "젊은이가 서글픈 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다. \"십 년 동안 영국군과 싸우신 분이 결국 다른 사람들처럼 침상에서 돌아가시다니, 그럴 만한 가치가 있었던 일이었습니다.\""]], [["“Why, you see, Edmond,”", "\"이보게, 에드몽,\""], ["replied the owner, who appeared more comforted at every moment, “we are all mortal, and the old must make way for the young.", "선주가 대답했다. 그는 시간이 갈수록 마음이 놓이는 듯했다. \"우리 모두 죽게 마련이고, 늙은이는 젊은이에게 자리를 내주어야 하는 법이지."], ["If not, why, there would be no promotion; and since you assure me that the cargo——”", "안 그러면 승진이라는 게 어디 있겠나. 게다가 자네가 화물은 모두 무사하다고 장담하니……\""]], [["“Is all safe and sound, M.", "\"모두 멀쩡합니다, 모렐 씨."], ["Morrel, take my word for it; and I advise you not to take 25,000 francs for the profits of the voyage.”", "제 말을 믿으셔도 됩니다. 이번 항해의 수익으로 2만 5천 프랑은 받으셔야지, 그보다 적게 받으시면 안 됩니다.\""]], [["Then, as they were just passing the Round Tower, the young man shouted: “Stand by there to lower the topsails and jib; brail up the spanker!”", "그때 마침 라운드 타워를 지나는 참이라, 젊은이가 외쳤다. \"거기, 윗돛과 삼각돛 내릴 준비! 후장 종범 거두어 올려!\""]], [["The order was executed as promptly as it would have been on board a man-of-war.", "명령은 군함에서나 볼 법한 신속함으로 이행되었다."]], [["“Let go—and clue up!” At this last command all the sails were lowered, and the vessel moved almost imperceptibly onwards.", "\"풀어라, 그리고 묶어 올려!\" 이 마지막 명령에 모든 돛이 내려졌고, 배는 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 천천히 앞으로 나아갔다."]], [["“Now, if you will come on board, M.", "\"자, 이제 올라오시지요, 모렐 씨.\""], ["Morrel,” said Dantès, observing the owner’s impatience, “here is your supercargo, M.", "선주의 조바심을 알아챈 당테스가 말했다. \"마침 회계 담당인 당글라르 씨가 선실에서 나오는군요."], ["Danglars, coming out of his cabin, who will furnish you with every particular.", "그가 자세한 사정을 다 알려 드릴 겁니다."], ["As for me, I must look after the anchoring, and dress the ship in mourning.”", "저는 닻을 내리는 일을 마저 살피고, 배에 조의를 표할 채비를 해야 합니다.\""]], [["The owner did not wait for a second invitation. He seized a rope which Dantès flung to him, and with an activity that would have done credit to a sailor, climbed up the side of the ship, while the young man, going to his task, left the conversation to Danglars, who now came towards the owner.", "선주는 두 번 권할 것도 없이, 당테스가 던져 준 밧줄을 붙잡고 선원에게도 뒤지지 않을 민첩함으로 뱃전을 타고 올라왔다. 그동안 젊은이는 제 일을 보러 가며 대화를 당글라르에게 넘겼고, 당글라르가 선주에게 다가왔다."], ["He was a man of twenty-five or twenty-six years of age, of unprepossessing countenance, obsequious to his superiors, insolent to his subordinates; and this, in addition to his position as responsible agent on board, which is always obnoxious to the sailors, made him as much disliked by the crew as Edmond Dantès was beloved by them.", "그는 스물대여섯 살가량의 사내로, 호감이 가지 않는 인상에, 윗사람에게는 굽실거리고 아랫사람에게는 거만했다. 게다가 선상에서 회계 책임자라는 직책 자체가 늘 선원들의 미움을 사는 자리였으니, 에드몽 당테스가 선원들에게 사랑받는 만큼이나 그는 미움을 받았다."]], [["“Well, M. Morrel,”", "\"그래, 모렐 씨,\""], ["said Danglars, “you have heard of the misfortune that has befallen us?”", "당글라르가 말했다. \"저희에게 닥친 변고에 대해 들으셨습니까?\""]], [["“Yes—yes: poor Captain Leclere!", "\"그래, 그래. 가엾은 르클레르 선장!"], ["He was a brave and an honest man.”", "용감하고 정직한 사람이었지.\""]], [["“And a first-rate seaman, one who had seen long and honorable service, as became a man charged with the interests of a house so important as that of Morrel & Son,”", "\"그리고 일류 선원이기도 했지요. 오랫동안 명예롭게 복무한 분이라, 모렐 부자 상회처럼 중요한 상회의 이익을 맡길 만한 분이었습니다.\""], ["replied Danglars.", "당글라르가 대답했다."]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["replied the owner, glancing after Dantès, who was watching the anchoring of his vessel, “it seems to me that a sailor needs not be so old as you say, Danglars, to understand his business, for our friend Edmond seems to understand it thoroughly, and not to require instruction from anyone.”", "선주가 자기 배의 닻을 내리는 모습을 지켜보는 당테스 쪽을 흘끗 보며 대답했다. \"내가 보기에는, 당글라르, 자네 말처럼 그렇게 나이가 많아야만 일을 제대로 아는 건 아닌 것 같네. 우리 에드몽만 봐도 일을 훤히 꿰뚫고 있고, 누구한테 가르침을 받을 것도 없어 보이니 말일세.\""]], [["“Yes,” said Danglars, darting at Edmond a look gleaming with hate.", "\"네,\" 당글라르가 에드몽에게 증오로 번득이는 눈길을 던지며 말했다."], ["“Yes, he is young, and youth is invariably self-confident.", "\"네, 그는 젊지요. 젊은이란 늘 자신만만한 법이니까요."], ["Scarcely was the captain’s breath out of his body when he assumed the command without consulting anyone, and he caused us to lose a day and a half at the Island of Elba, instead of making for Marseilles direct.” 0025m", "선장님이 숨을 거두기가 무섭게 누구와도 상의하지 않고 지휘권을 잡더니, 마르세유로 곧장 오지 않고 엘바섬에서 하루 반나절이나 허비하게 만들었습니다.\""]], [["“As to taking command of the vessel,”", "\"배의 지휘권을 잡은 것은,\""], ["replied Morrel, “that was his duty as captain’s mate; as to losing a day and a half off the Island of Elba, he was wrong, unless the vessel needed repairs.”", "모렐이 대답했다. \"일등 항해사로서 마땅한 의무였네. 엘바섬 앞바다에서 하루 반나절을 허비한 것은, 배에 수리가 필요한 게 아니었다면 잘못한 일이지.\""]], [["“The vessel was in as good condition as I am, and as, I hope you are, M.", "\"배는 저만큼이나, 또 모렐 씨도 그러시길 바랍니다만 그만큼이나 멀쩡한 상태였습니다."], ["Morrel, and this day and a half was lost from pure whim, for the pleasure of going ashore, and nothing else.”", "그 하루 반나절은 순전히 변덕으로, 그저 뭍에 내려 보고 싶다는 즐거움 때문에 허비한 것이었지요. 다른 이유는 없습니다.\""]], [["“Dantès,”", "\"당테스,\""], ["said the shipowner, turning towards the young man, “come this way!”", "선주가 젊은이 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다. \"이리로 좀 와 보게!\""]], [["“In a moment, sir,”", "\"곧 가겠습니다, 선주님.\""], ["answered Dantès, “and I’m with you.”", "당테스가 대답했다. \"잠시면 됩니다.\""], ["Then calling to the crew, he said, “Let go!”", "그러고는 선원들에게 외쳤다. \"놓아라!\""]], [["The anchor was instantly dropped, and the chain ran rattling through the port-hole.", "닻이 곧바로 떨어지면서 사슬이 뱃전 구멍을 통해 요란하게 풀려 나갔다."], ["Dantès continued at his post in spite of the presence of the pilot, until this manœuvre was completed, and then he added, “Half-mast the colors, and square the yards!”", "당테스는 도선사가 곁에 있는데도 이 작업이 끝날 때까지 제자리를 지켰고, 그런 뒤에 덧붙였다. \"깃발을 반기로 내리고, 활대를 가지런히 하라!\""]], [["“You see,”", "\"보십시오,\""], ["said Danglars, “he fancies himself captain already, upon my word.”", "당글라르가 말했다. \"정말이지, 벌써 자기가 선장인 줄 안다니까요.\""]], [["“And so, in fact, he is,”", "\"실제로도 선장이나 다름없지.\""], ["said the owner.", "선주가 말했다."]], [["“Except your signature and your partner’s, M. Morrel.”", "\"모렐 씨와 동업자분의 서명만 빠졌지요.\""]], [["“And why should he not have this?”", "\"그가 그걸 받지 못할 이유가 있나?\""], ["asked the owner; “he is young, it is true, but he seems to me a thorough seaman, and of full experience.”", "선주가 물었다. \"젊긴 하지만, 내가 보기엔 더할 나위 없는 선원이고 경험도 충분해 보이는데.\""]], [["A cloud passed over Danglars’ brow.", "당글라르의 이마에 그늘이 스쳤다."]], [["“Your pardon, M. Morrel,”", "\"실례합니다, 모렐 씨.\""], ["said Dantès, approaching, “the vessel now rides at anchor, and I am at your service.", "당테스가 다가오며 말했다. \"이제 배가 닻을 내리고 정박했으니, 분부만 하십시오."], ["You hailed me, I think?”", "저를 부르셨지요?\""]], [["Danglars retreated a step or two.", "당글라르가 한두 걸음 물러섰다."], ["“I wished to inquire why you stopped at the Island of Elba?”", "\"왜 엘바섬에 들렀는지 물어보고 싶었네.\""]], [["“I do not know, sir; it was to fulfil the last instructions of Captain Leclere, who, when dying, gave me a packet for Marshal Bertrand.”", "\"저도 모릅니다, 선주님. 르클레르 선장님의 마지막 분부를 따른 것뿐이었습니다. 선장님이 돌아가시면서 베르트랑 원수께 전할 꾸러미를 제게 맡기셨거든요.\""]], [["“Then did you see him, Edmond?”", "\"그래, 원수를 만났나, 에드몽?\""]], [["“Who?”", "\"누구를요?\""]], [["“The marshal.”", "\"원수 말일세.\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["Morrel looked around him, and then, drawing Dantès on one side, he said suddenly—", "모렐이 주위를 둘러보더니 당테스를 한쪽으로 끌어당기고는 불쑥 물었다."]], [["“And how is the emperor?”", "\"그래, 황제께서는 어떠시던가?\""]], [["“Very well, as far as I could judge from the sight of him.”", "\"제가 뵌 바로는, 아주 건강하셨습니다.\""]], [["“You saw the emperor, then?”", "\"그럼 자네가 황제를 뵈었단 말인가?\""]], [["“He entered the marshal’s apartment while I was there.”", "\"제가 거기 있는 동안 황제께서 원수의 방으로 들어오셨습니다.\""]], [["“And you spoke to him?”", "\"그래서 말을 나눴나?\""]], [["“Why, it was he who spoke to me, sir,”", "\"실은 황제께서 먼저 제게 말을 거셨습니다, 선주님.\""], ["said Dantès, with a smile.", "당테스가 미소를 띠며 말했다."]], [["“And what did he say to you?”", "\"그래, 자네에게 뭐라고 하시던가?\""]], [["“Asked me questions about the vessel, the time she left Marseilles, the course she had taken, and what was her cargo.", "\"배에 관해 이것저것 물으셨습니다. 마르세유를 언제 떠났는지, 어떤 항로를 택했는지, 화물은 무엇인지 말입니다."], ["I believe, if she had not been laden, and I had been her master, he would have bought her.", "배에 짐이 실리지 않았고 제가 선주였더라면, 황제께서 그 배를 사셨을지도 모릅니다."], ["But I told him I was only mate, and that she belonged to the firm of Morrel & Son.", "하지만 저는 일등 항해사일 뿐이고 배는 모렐 부자 상회의 소유라고 말씀드렸지요. '아, 그렇군.'"], ["‘Ah, yes,’ he said, ‘I know them.", "하시며 '나도 그들을 안다."], ["The Morrels have been shipowners from father to son; and there was a Morrel who served in the same regiment with me when I was in garrison at Valence.’”", "모렐 가문은 대대로 선주였지. 내가 발랑스에 주둔하던 시절, 같은 연대에서 복무한 모렐이라는 자가 있었어.' 하셨습니다.\""]], [["“_Pardieu!_ and that is true!”", "\"이런! 그게 사실일세!\""], ["cried the owner, greatly delighted.", "선주가 크게 기뻐하며 외쳤다."], ["“And that was Policar Morrel, my uncle, who was afterwards a captain.", "\"그게 바로 내 삼촌 폴리카르 모렐이야. 나중에 대위가 되셨지."], ["Dantès, you must tell my uncle that the emperor remembered him, and you will see it will bring tears into the old soldier’s eyes.", "당테스, 황제께서 삼촌을 기억하시더라고 꼭 전해 드리게. 그러면 그 늙은 군인의 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁 맺히는 걸 보게 될 걸세."], ["Come, come,”", "자, 자,\""], ["continued he, patting Edmond’s shoulder kindly, “you did very right, Dantès, to follow Captain Leclere’s instructions, and touch at Elba, although if it were known that you had conveyed a packet to the marshal, and had conversed with the emperor, it might bring you into trouble.” 0027m", "그가 에드몽의 어깨를 다정하게 두드리며 말을 이었다. \"당테스, 르클레르 선장님의 분부를 따라 엘바섬에 들른 것은 아주 잘한 일이야. 다만 자네가 원수께 꾸러미를 전하고 황제와 이야기를 나눴다는 사실이 알려지면 곤란해질 수도 있겠네.\""]], [["“How could that bring me into trouble, sir?”", "\"그게 어찌 저를 곤란하게 하겠습니까, 선주님?\""], ["asked Dantès; “for I did not even know of what I was the bearer; and the emperor merely made such inquiries as he would of the first comer.", "당테스가 물었다. \"저는 제가 무엇을 전하는지도 몰랐고, 황제께서도 처음 보는 사람에게 으레 던질 법한 질문을 하셨을 뿐인걸요."], ["But, pardon me, here are the health officers and the customs inspectors coming alongside.”", "그런데 실례합니다, 검역관과 세관 검사관들이 배 곁으로 다가오는군요.\""], ["And the young man went to the gangway.", "젊은이는 현문 쪽으로 걸어갔다."], ["As he departed, Danglars approached, and said,—", "그가 자리를 뜨자 당글라르가 다가와 말했다."]], [["“Well, it appears that he has given you satisfactory reasons for his landing at Porto-Ferrajo?”", "\"그래, 그가 포르토페라요에 내린 까닭을 흡족하게 설명하던가요?\""]], [["“Yes, most satisfactory, my dear Danglars.”", "\"그래, 아주 흡족했네, 당글라르.\""]], [["“Well, so much the better,”", "\"음, 그렇다면 다행이지요.\""], ["said the supercargo; “for it is not pleasant to think that a comrade has not done his duty.”", "회계 담당이 말했다. \"동료가 제 의무를 다하지 않았다고 생각하는 건 유쾌한 일이 아니니까요.\""]], [["“Dantès has done his,”", "\"당테스는 제 의무를 다했네.\""], ["replied the owner, “and that is not saying much.", "선주가 대답했다. \"그것도 새삼스러운 말이 아니지."], ["It was Captain Leclere who gave orders for this delay.”", "이번 지체는 르클레르 선장이 명령한 일이었으니까.\""]], [["“Talking of Captain Leclere, has not Dantès given you a letter from him?”", "\"르클레르 선장 이야기가 나왔으니 말인데, 당테스가 선장한테서 받은 편지를 전해 드리지 않던가요?\""]], [["“To me?—no—was there one?”", "\"내게? 아니, 편지가 있었나?\""]], [["“I believe that, besides the packet, Captain Leclere confided a letter to his care.”", "\"제 생각에는, 꾸러미 말고도 르클레르 선장이 그에게 편지 한 통을 맡긴 듯합니다.\""]], [["“Of what packet are you speaking, Danglars?”", "\"무슨 꾸러미를 말하는 건가, 당글라르?\""]], [["“Why, that which Dantès left at Porto-Ferrajo.”", "\"아, 당테스가 포르토페라요에 두고 온 그 꾸러미 말입니다.\""]], [["“How do you know he had a packet to leave at Porto-Ferrajo?”", "\"그가 포르토페라요에 두고 올 꾸러미가 있었다는 걸 자네가 어떻게 아나?\""]], [["Danglars turned very red.", "당글라르의 얼굴이 새빨개졌다."]], [["“I was passing close to the door of the captain’s cabin, which was half open, and I saw him give the packet and letter to Dantès.”", "\"선장 선실 문 가까이를 지나가는데 문이 반쯤 열려 있었고, 선장이 당테스에게 꾸러미와 편지를 건네는 걸 보았습니다.\""]], [["“He did not speak to me of it,”", "\"그가 내게는 그 얘기를 하지 않았네.\""], ["replied the shipowner; “but if there be any letter he will give it to me.”", "선주가 대답했다. \"하지만 편지가 있다면 내게 줄 걸세.\""]], [["Danglars reflected for a moment.", "당글라르가 잠시 생각에 잠겼다."], ["“Then, M. Morrel, I beg of you,”", "\"그렇다면 모렐 씨, 부탁드립니다.\""], ["said he, “not to say a word to Dantès on the subject.", "그가 말했다. \"그 일에 대해서는 당테스에게 한마디도 하지 말아 주십시오."], ["I may have been mistaken.”", "제가 잘못 봤을 수도 있으니까요.\""]], [["At this moment the young man returned; Danglars withdrew.", "바로 그때 젊은이가 돌아왔다. 당글라르는 물러갔다."]], [["“Well, my dear Dantès, are you now free?”", "\"그래, 당테스, 이제 자유로운 몸인가?\""], ["inquired the owner.", "선주가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "\"네, 선주님.\""]], [["“You have not been long detained.”", "\"오래 붙잡혀 있지는 않았군.\""]], [["“No.", "\"네."], ["I gave the custom-house officers a copy of our bill of lading; and as to the other papers, they sent a man off with the pilot, to whom I gave them.”", "세관원들에게는 선하증권 사본을 한 부 주었습니다. 다른 서류들은 도선사 편에 사람을 딸려 보내, 그 사람에게 넘겼고요.\""]], [["“Then you have nothing more to do here?”", "\"그럼 이제 여기서 더 할 일은 없나?\""]], [["“No—everything is all right now.”", "\"네, 이제 다 정리됐습니다.\""]], [["“Then you can come and dine with me?”", "\"그럼 나와 함께 저녁을 들겠나?\""]], [["“I really must ask you to excuse me, M. Morrel.", "\"정말이지 양해해 주셔야겠습니다, 모렐 씨."], ["My first visit is due to my father, though I am not the less grateful for the honor you have done me.”", "제일 먼저 아버지를 찾아뵈어야 하니까요. 베풀어 주신 호의가 고맙지 않아서가 아닙니다.\""]], [["0029m\n“Right, Dantès, quite right. I always knew you were a good son.”", "\"옳지, 당테스, 옳은 말이야. 자네가 효성 깊은 아들이라는 건 진작부터 알고 있었지.\""]], [["“And,”", "\"그런데,\""], ["inquired Dantès, with some hesitation, “do you know how my father is?”", "당테스가 조금 망설이며 물었다. \"혹시 제 아버지가 어떻게 지내시는지 아십니까?\""]], [["“Well, I believe, my dear Edmond, though I have not seen him lately.”", "\"잘 지내시는 듯하네, 에드몽. 요즘 뵙지는 못했지만.\""]], [["“Yes, he likes to keep himself shut up in his little room.”", "\"네, 아버지는 그 작은 방에 틀어박혀 지내시는 걸 좋아하시지요.\""]], [["“That proves, at least, that he has wanted for nothing during your absence.”", "\"그건 적어도 자네가 없는 동안에도 아쉬운 것 없이 지내셨다는 증거 아니겠나.\""]], [["Dantès smiled.", "당테스가 미소 지었다."], ["“My father is proud, sir, and if he had not a meal left, I doubt if he would have asked anything from anyone, except from Heaven.”", "\"제 아버지는 자존심이 강하셔서요, 선주님. 끼니가 떨어졌다 해도, 하늘에 비는 것 말고는 누구한테도 아무것도 청하지 않으셨을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Well, then, after this first visit has been made we shall count on you.”", "\"그럼 그 첫 방문을 마치고 나면 우리 쪽도 기대해도 되겠지.\""]], [["“I must again excuse myself, M. Morrel, for after this first visit has been paid I have another which I am most anxious to pay.”", "\"또 양해를 구해야겠습니다, 모렐 씨. 그 첫 방문을 마치면 꼭 가 봐야 할 또 다른 곳이 있어서요.\""]], [["“True, Dantès, I forgot that there was at the Catalans someone who expects you no less impatiently than your father—the lovely Mercédès.”", "\"그렇지, 당테스, 내가 깜빡했군. 카탈루냐 마을에 자네 아버지 못지않게 애타게 자네를 기다리는 사람이 있었지. 사랑스러운 메르세데스 말일세.\""]], [["Dantès blushed.", "당테스가 얼굴을 붉혔다."]], [["“Ah, ha,”", "\"하하,\""], ["said the shipowner, “I am not in the least surprised, for she has been to me three times, inquiring if there were any news of the _Pharaon_.", "선주가 말했다. \"조금도 놀랍지 않네. 그 아가씨가 세 번이나 나를 찾아와 파라옹호 소식이 없느냐고 물었거든. 이런!"], ["_Peste!_ Edmond, you have a very handsome mistress!”", "에드몽, 자네 아주 어여쁜 연인을 두었더군!\""]], [["“She is not my mistress,”", "\"그녀는 제 연인이 아닙니다.\""], ["replied the young sailor, gravely; “she is my betrothed.”", "젊은 선원이 진지하게 대답했다. \"제 약혼녀입니다.\""]], [["“Sometimes one and the same thing,” said Morrel, with a smile.", "\"때로는 그게 그거지.\" 모렐이 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Not with us, sir,” replied Dantès.", "\"저희에게는 그렇지 않습니다, 선주님.\" 당테스가 대답했다."]], [["“Well, well, my dear Edmond,”", "\"그래, 그래, 에드몽.\""], ["continued the owner, “don’t let me detain you.", "선주가 말을 이었다. \"내가 자네를 붙잡지 않으마."], ["You have managed my affairs so well that I ought to allow you all the time you require for your own.", "자네가 내 일을 워낙 잘 처리해 주었으니, 자네 자신의 일에도 필요한 만큼 시간을 내 줘야 마땅하지."], ["Do you want any money?”", "돈이 좀 필요한가?\""]], [["“No, sir; I have all my pay to take—nearly three months’ wages.”", "\"아닙니다, 선주님. 받을 봉급이 그대로 남아 있는걸요. 거의 석 달치 급료입니다.\""]], [["“You are a careful fellow, Edmond.”", "\"자네는 알뜰한 사람이야, 에드몽.\""]], [["“Say I have a poor father, sir.”", "\"가난한 아버지를 둔 탓이라고 해 두지요, 선주님.\""]], [["“Yes, yes, I know how good a son you are, so now hasten away to see your father.", "\"그래, 그래, 자네가 얼마나 효성스러운 아들인지 잘 알지. 자, 어서 아버지를 뵈러 가게."], ["I have a son too, and I should be very wroth with those who detained him from me after a three months’ voyage.”", "나도 아들이 하나 있는데, 석 달 항해를 마치고 돌아온 아들을 누가 붙잡아 두기라도 한다면 몹시 화가 날 걸세.\""]], [["“Then I have your leave, sir?”", "\"그럼 가 봐도 되겠습니까, 선주님?\""]], [["“Yes, if you have nothing more to say to me.”", "\"그래, 내게 더 할 말이 없다면 말이야.\""]], [["“Nothing.”", "\"없습니다.\""]], [["“Captain Leclere did not, before he died, give you a letter for me?”", "\"르클레르 선장이 죽기 전에, 내게 전할 편지를 자네에게 주지 않았나?\""]], [["“He was unable to write, sir. But that reminds me that I must ask your leave of absence for some days.”", "\"선장님은 글을 쓰실 수 없는 상태였습니다, 선주님. 아, 그러고 보니 며칠 동안 휴가를 청해야겠습니다.\""]], [["“To get married?”", "\"결혼하려고?\""]], [["“Yes, first, and then to go to Paris.”", "\"네, 그게 우선이고, 그다음엔 파리에 다녀오려고요.\""]], [["“Very good; have what time you require, Dantès.", "\"좋아, 필요한 만큼 시간을 쓰게, 당테스."], ["It will take quite six weeks to unload the cargo, and we cannot get you ready for sea until three months after that; only be back again in three months, for the _Pharaon_,”", "화물을 다 부리려면 족히 여섯 주는 걸릴 테고, 그 뒤로도 석 달은 지나야 다시 출항 채비가 될 테니까. 다만 석 달 안에는 돌아오게. 파라옹호는,\""], ["added the owner, patting the young sailor on the back, “cannot sail without her captain.”", "선주가 젊은 선원의 등을 두드리며 덧붙였다. \"선장 없이는 출항할 수 없으니까.\""]], [["“Without her captain!”", "\"선장 없이는요!\" 당테스가 외쳤다."], ["cried Dantès, his eyes sparkling with animation; “pray mind what you say, for you are touching on the most secret wishes of my heart.", "그의 눈이 생기로 반짝였다. \"부디 말씀을 가려 해 주십시오. 제 마음속 가장 은밀한 소망을 건드리고 계시니까요."], ["Is it really your intention to make me captain of the _Pharaon_?”", "정말로 저를 파라옹호의 선장으로 삼으실 생각이십니까?\""]], [["“If I were sole owner we’d shake hands on it now, my dear Dantès, and call it settled; but I have a partner, and you know the Italian proverb—_Chi ha compagno ha padrone_—‘He who has a partner has a master.’ But the thing is at least half done, as you have one out of two votes. Rely on me to procure you the other; I will do my best.”", "\"내가 단독 선주라면 지금 당장 악수하고 결정된 일로 칠 텐데 말일세, 당테스. 하지만 내겐 동업자가 있고, 자네도 이탈리아 속담을 알 거야. '키 아 콤파뇨 아 파드로네' — '동업자를 둔 사람은 주인을 둔 셈이다'라는 말이지. 그래도 적어도 절반은 된 일이야. 두 표 중 한 표는 이미 자네 것이니까. 나머지 한 표는 내게 맡기게. 최선을 다해 보겠네.\""]], [["“Ah, M. Morrel,”", "\"아, 모렐 씨,\""], ["exclaimed the young seaman, with tears in his eyes, and grasping the owner’s hand, “M.", "젊은 선원이 눈에 눈물을 글썽이며 선주의 손을 부여잡고 외쳤다."], ["Morrel, I thank you in the name of my father and of Mercédès.”", "\"모렐 씨, 제 아버지와 메르세데스의 이름으로 감사드립니다.\""]], [["“That’s all right, Edmond.", "\"됐네, 에드몽."], ["There’s a providence that watches over the deserving.", "마땅한 사람을 굽어살피는 섭리가 있는 법이지."], ["Go to your father; go and see Mercédès, and afterwards come to me.”", "어서 아버지께 가 보게. 메르세데스도 만나고, 그런 뒤에 내게 오게나.\""]], [["“Shall I row you ashore?”", "\"제가 뭍까지 모셔다 드릴까요?\""]], [["“No, thank you; I shall remain and look over the accounts with Danglars.", "\"아니, 고맙지만 됐네. 나는 남아서 당글라르와 함께 장부를 살펴볼 참이네."], ["Have you been satisfied with him this voyage?”", "이번 항해에서 그자에게 만족했나?\""]], [["“That is according to the sense you attach to the question, sir.", "\"그건 선주님이 그 질문에 어떤 뜻을 두시느냐에 달렸습니다."], ["Do you mean is he a good comrade? No, for I think he never liked me since the day when I was silly enough, after a little quarrel we had, to propose to him to stop for ten minutes at the island of Monte Cristo to settle the dispute—a proposition which I was wrong to suggest, and he quite right to refuse.", "좋은 동료냐고 물으시는 거라면, 아닙니다. 언젠가 사소한 다툼 끝에 제가 어리석게도 몬테크리스토섬에 십 분만 들러 시비를 가리자고 제안한 적이 있는데, 그날 이후로 그자가 저를 좋아한 적이 없는 것 같으니까요."], ["If you mean as responsible agent when you ask me the question, I believe there is nothing to say against him, and that you will be content with the way in which he has performed his duty.”", "제가 그런 제안을 한 것이 잘못이었고, 그가 거절한 것이 옳았지요. 회계 책임자로서 어떠냐고 물으시는 거라면, 흠잡을 데가 없다고 봅니다. 그가 제 의무를 수행한 방식에 만족하실 겁니다.\""]], [["“But tell me, Dantès, if you had command of the _Pharaon_ should you be glad to see Danglars remain?”", "\"그런데 말해 보게, 당테스. 자네가 파라옹호를 지휘하게 된다면, 당글라르가 남아 있는 게 좋겠나?\""]], [["“Captain or mate, M. Morrel, I shall always have the greatest respect for those who possess the owners’ confidence.”", "\"선장이든 항해사든, 모렐 씨, 저는 선주들의 신임을 받는 사람들을 언제나 가장 존중할 것입니다.\""]], [["“That’s right, that’s right, Dantès!", "\"옳지, 옳아, 당테스!"], ["I see you are a thoroughly good fellow, and will detain you no longer.", "자네가 정말 훌륭한 사람이라는 걸 알겠네. 더는 붙잡지 않으마. 어서 가게."], ["Go, for I see how impatient you are.”", "자네가 얼마나 조바심이 나는지 다 보이는구먼.\""]], [["“Then I have leave?”", "\"그럼 가 봐도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["“Go, I tell you.”", "\"가라니까.\""]], [["“May I have the use of your skiff?”", "\"거룻배를 좀 써도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["“Certainly.”", "\"물론이지.\""]], [["“Then, for the present, M. Morrel, farewell, and a thousand thanks!”", "\"그럼 이만 작별 인사를 드립니다, 모렐 씨. 천 번이라도 감사드립니다!\""]], [["“I hope soon to see you again, my dear Edmond. Good luck to you.”", "\"곧 다시 보세, 에드몽. 행운을 비네.\""]], [["The young sailor jumped into the skiff, and sat down in the stern sheets, with the order that he be put ashore at La Canebière.", "젊은 선원은 거룻배에 뛰어올라 고물 자리에 앉으며, 라카느비에르에 내려 달라고 일렀다."], ["The two oarsmen bent to their work, and the little boat glided away as rapidly as possible in the midst of the thousand vessels which choke up the narrow way which leads between the two rows of ships from the mouth of the harbor to the Quai d’Orléans.", "두 사공이 노에 몸을 실었고, 작은 배는 항구 어귀에서 오를레앙 부두로 이어지는, 두 줄로 늘어선 배들 사이의 좁은 물길을 가득 메운 수천 척의 배 사이를 최대한 빠르게 미끄러져 갔다."]], [["The shipowner, smiling, followed him with his eyes until he saw him spring out on the quay and disappear in the midst of the throng, which from five o’clock in the morning until nine o’clock at night, swarms in the famous street of La Canebière,—a street of which the modern Phocéens are so proud that they say with all the gravity in the world, and with that accent which gives so much character to what is said, “If Paris had La Canebière, Paris would be a second Marseilles.” On turning round the owner saw Danglars behind him, apparently awaiting orders, but in reality also watching the young sailor,—but there was a great difference in the expression of the two men who thus followed the movements of Edmond Dantès.", "선주는 미소를 띤 채 그의 모습을 눈으로 좇았다. 그가 부두로 뛰어올라 군중 속으로 사라질 때까지였다. 그 유명한 라카느비에르 거리는 아침 다섯 시부터 밤 아홉 시까지 사람들로 들끓었다. 오늘날의 포세 사람들은 이 거리를 어찌나 자랑스러워하는지, 세상 더없이 진지한 얼굴로, 또 말에 묘한 무게를 싣는 그 특유의 억양으로 이렇게 말한다. \"파리에 라카느비에르가 있다면, 파리도 제2의 마르세유가 될 텐데.\" 선주가 몸을 돌리자, 당글라르가 등 뒤에 서서 겉으로는 지시를 기다리는 듯했으나 실은 그 역시 젊은 선원을 지켜보고 있었다. 그러나 에드몽 당테스의 움직임을 좇는 두 사람의 표정에는 커다란 차이가 있었다."]], [["# Chapter 2. Father and Son", "# Chapter 2. Father and Son"]], [["We will leave Danglars struggling with the demon of hatred, and endeavoring to insinuate in the ear of the shipowner some evil suspicions against his comrade, and follow Dantès, who, after having traversed La Canebière, took the Rue de Noailles, and entering a small house, on the left of the Allées de Meilhan, rapidly ascended four flights of a dark staircase, holding the baluster with one hand, while with the other he repressed the beatings of his heart, and paused before a half-open door, from which he could see the whole of a small room.", "당글라르가 증오의 악령과 씨름하며 선주의 귀에 동료를 헐뜯는 사악한 의심을 슬그머니 불어넣으려 애쓰도록 내버려 두고, 우리는 당테스를 따라가 보자. 그는 라카느비에르 거리를 가로질러 노아유 거리로 접어들었고, 메이앙 가로수길 왼편의 작은 집으로 들어가 한 손으로는 난간을 붙들고 다른 손으로는 쿵쾅대는 심장을 억누르며 어두운 계단을 네 층이나 빠르게 올라갔다. 그리고 반쯤 열린 문 앞에서 걸음을 멈추었다. 그 문틈으로 작은 방 안이 한눈에 들여다보였다."]], [["This room was occupied by Dantès’ father.", "이 방에는 당테스의 아버지가 살고 있었다."], ["The news of the arrival of the _Pharaon_ had not yet reached the old man, who, mounted on a chair, was amusing himself by training with trembling hand the nasturtiums and sprays of clematis that clambered over the trellis at his window.", "파라옹호가 들어왔다는 소식은 아직 노인에게 닿지 않았고, 그는 의자에 올라선 채 떨리는 손으로 창가 격자 시렁을 타고 오른 한련화와 으아리 덩굴을 손질하며 시간을 보내고 있었다."], ["Suddenly, he felt an arm thrown around his body, and a well-known voice behind him exclaimed, “Father—dear father!”", "그때 문득 누군가의 팔이 자신의 몸을 감싸 안는 것이 느껴졌고, 등 뒤에서 귀에 익은 목소리가 외쳤다. \"아버지, 아버지!\""]], [["The old man uttered a cry, and turned round; then, seeing his son, he fell into his arms, pale and trembling.", "노인이 짧은 소리를 지르며 돌아섰다. 그러고는 아들을 보자 창백하게 떨면서 그 품에 안겼다."]], [["“What ails you, my dearest father?", "\"무슨 일이세요, 아버지?"], ["Are you ill?” inquired the young man, much alarmed.", "어디 편찮으세요?\" 젊은이가 몹시 놀라 물었다."]], [["“No, no, my dear Edmond—my boy—my son!—no; but I did not expect you; and joy, the surprise of seeing you so suddenly—Ah, I feel as if I were going to die.”", "\"아니다, 아니야, 내 사랑하는 에드몽, 내 아들아! 아니다. 다만 네가 올 줄을 몰랐던 데다, 이렇게 갑자기 너를 보게 된 기쁨과 놀라움에…… 아, 꼭 죽을 것만 같구나.\""]], [["“Come, come, cheer up, my dear father! ’Tis I—really I!", "\"자, 자, 기운 차리세요, 아버지! 저예요, 진짜 저라고요!"], ["They say joy never hurts, and so I came to you without any warning.", "기쁨은 사람을 해치지 않는다고들 하기에, 미리 기별도 없이 이렇게 찾아왔어요."], ["Come now, do smile, instead of looking at me so solemnly.", "자, 그렇게 엄숙한 얼굴로 보지 마시고 어서 웃으세요."], ["Here I am back again, and we are going to be happy.”", "제가 돌아왔으니, 이제 우린 행복하게 지낼 거예요.\""]], [["“Yes, yes, my boy, so we will—so we will,”", "\"그래, 그래, 내 아들아, 그래야지, 그래야 하고말고.\""], ["replied the old man; “but how shall we be happy?", "노인이 대답했다. \"한데 어떻게 행복하게 지낸단 말이냐?"], ["Shall you never leave me again?", "이제 다시는 날 떠나지 않을 게냐?"], ["Come, tell me all the good fortune that has befallen you.”", "자, 네게 찾아온 그 좋은 일을 다 들려주려무나.\""]], [["“God forgive me,” said the young man, “for rejoicing at happiness derived from the misery of others, but, Heaven knows, I did not seek this good fortune; it has happened, and I really cannot pretend to lament it.", "\"남의 불행에서 비롯된 행복을 기뻐하는 저를 하느님께서 용서해 주시기를.\" 젊은이가 말했다."], ["The good Captain Leclere is dead, father, and it is probable that, with the aid of M.", "\"하지만 하늘이 아시다시피, 저는 이 행운을 바란 적이 없습니다. 그저 일어난 일일 뿐이고, 솔직히 슬픈 척할 수도 없는걸요. 르클레르 선장님이 돌아가셨어요, 아버지."], ["Morrel, I shall have his place. Do you understand, father?", "그래서 아마 모렐 씨의 도움으로 제가 그 자리를 잇게 될 것 같아요. 아시겠어요, 아버지?"], ["Only imagine me a captain at twenty, with a hundred louis pay, and a share in the profits!", "스무 살에 선장이 되어 봉급으로 백 루이를 받고, 거기에 이익까지 나눠 받는다고 한번 상상해 보세요!"], ["Is this not more than a poor sailor like me could have hoped for?”", "저 같은 가난한 선원이 감히 바랄 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더한 일 아니겠어요?\""]], [["“Yes, my dear boy,”", "\"그래, 내 아들아.\""], ["replied the old man, “it is very fortunate.”", "노인이 대답했다. \"참으로 다행스러운 일이구나.\""]], [["“Well, then, with the first money I touch, I mean you to have a small house, with a garden in which to plant clematis, nasturtiums, and honeysuckle.", "\"그러니까 첫 봉급을 손에 쥐면, 아버지께 작은 집을 한 채 마련해 드릴 거예요. 으아리며 한련화며 인동덩굴을 심을 정원이 딸린 집으로요."], ["But what ails you, father?", "그런데 무슨 일이세요, 아버지?"], ["Are you not well?”", "어디 안 좋으세요?\""]], [["“’Tis nothing, nothing; it will soon pass away”—and as he said so the old man’s strength failed him, and he fell backwards.", "\"아무것도 아니다, 아무것도. 곧 괜찮아질 게야.\" 그렇게 말하면서 노인은 힘이 다해 뒤로 쓰러졌다."]], [["“Come, come,”", "\"이런, 이런.\""], ["said the young man, “a glass of wine, father, will revive you.", "젊은이가 말했다. \"포도주 한 잔이면 기운이 나실 거예요, 아버지."], ["Where do you keep your wine?”", "포도주는 어디 두셨어요?\""]], [["“No, no; thanks.", "\"아니다, 아니야, 됐다."], ["You need not look for it; I do not want it,”", "찾을 것 없다. 마시고 싶지 않구나.\""], ["said the old man.", "노인이 말했다."]], [["“Yes, yes, father, tell me where it is,”", "\"아니에요, 아버지, 어디 있는지 말씀하세요.\""], ["and he opened two or three cupboards.", "그러고는 찬장을 두세 개 열어 보았다."]], [["“It is no use,”", "\"소용없다.\""], ["said the old man, “there is no wine.”", "노인이 말했다. \"포도주가 없어.\""]], [["“What, no wine?”", "\"네?"], ["said Dantès, turning pale, and looking alternately at the hollow cheeks of the old man and the empty cupboards.", "포도주가 없다고요?\" 당테스가 창백해지며, 노인의 움푹 꺼진 두 뺨과 텅 빈 찬장을 번갈아 바라보며 말했다."], ["“What, no wine?", "\"포도주가 없다니요?"], ["Have you wanted money, father?”", "돈이 떨어지셨던 거예요, 아버지?\""]], [["“I want nothing now that I have you,” said the old man.", "\"네가 곁에 있으니 이제 아무것도 아쉬울 게 없다.\" 노인이 말했다."]], [["“Yet,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["stammered Dantès, wiping the perspiration from his brow,—“yet I gave you two hundred francs when I left, three months ago.”", "당테스가 이마의 땀을 닦으며 더듬더듬 말했다. \"하지만 제가 석 달 전에 떠나면서 이백 프랑을 드렸잖아요.\""]], [["“Yes, yes, Edmond, that is true, but you forgot at that time a little debt to our neighbor, Caderousse.", "\"그래, 그래, 에드몽, 그건 맞다. 하지만 너는 그때 우리 이웃 카드루스에게 진 작은 빚을 잊고 있었지."], ["He reminded me of it, telling me if I did not pay for you, he would be paid by M.", "그가 내게 그 일을 일깨우면서, 너 대신 갚지 않으면 모렐 씨에게서 받아 내겠다고 하더구나."], ["Morrel; and so, you see, lest he might do you an injury——”", "그러니 알겠지, 그자가 네게 해코지라도 할까 봐……\" \"그래서요?\""]], [["“Well?” “Why, I paid him.”", "\"그래서, 내가 그에게 갚았다.\""]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["cried Dantès, “it was a hundred and forty francs I owed Caderousse.”", "당테스가 외쳤다. \"제가 카드루스에게 진 빚은 백사십 프랑이었는걸요.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["stammered the old man.", "노인이 더듬거리며 말했다."]], [["“And you paid him out of the two hundred francs I left you?”", "\"그래서 제가 드린 이백 프랑에서 그 돈을 갚으셨어요?\""]], [["The old man nodded.", "노인이 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["“So that you have lived for three months on sixty francs,”", "\"그럼 석 달을 육십 프랑으로 사셨다는 거잖아요.\""], ["muttered Edmond.", "에드몽이 중얼거렸다."]], [["“You know how little I require,” said the old man.", "\"내가 얼마나 적게 쓰는지 너도 알지 않느냐.\" 노인이 말했다."]], [["“Heaven pardon me,” cried Edmond, falling on his knees before his father.", "\"하느님, 저를 용서하소서.\" 에드몽이 외치며 아버지 앞에 무릎을 꿇었다."]], [["“What are you doing?”", "\"무얼 하는 게냐?\""]], [["“You have wounded me to the heart.”", "\"제 가슴을 후벼 파셨어요.\""]], [["“Never mind it, for I see you once more,”", "\"마음 쓰지 마라. 이렇게 너를 다시 보지 않느냐.\""], ["said the old man; “and now it’s all over—everything is all right again.” 0035m", "노인이 말했다. \"이제 다 지난 일이다. 모든 게 다시 제자리로 돌아왔어.\""]], [["“Yes, here I am,”", "\"네, 제가 돌아왔어요.\""], ["said the young man, “with a promising future and a little money.", "젊은이가 말했다. \"앞날도 밝고, 돈도 조금 있고요. 자, 아버지, 여기요!\""], ["Here, father, here!”", "그가 말했다."], ["he said, “take this—take it, and send for something immediately.”", "\"이걸 받으세요, 받으시고 어서 뭐든 사 오라고 사람을 보내세요.\""], ["And he emptied his pockets on the table, the contents consisting of a dozen gold pieces, five or six five-franc pieces, and some smaller coin.", "그러고는 주머니를 탁자 위에 털어 놓았다. 금화 열두어 닢에, 오 프랑짜리 은화 대여섯 닢, 그리고 잔돈 몇 푼이 쏟아져 나왔다."], ["The countenance of old Dantès brightened.", "늙은 당테스의 얼굴이 환해졌다."]], [["“Whom does this belong to?”", "\"이게 다 누구 거냐?\""], ["he inquired.", "노인이 물었다."]], [["“To me, to you, to us!", "\"제 거고, 아버지 거고, 우리 거예요! 받으세요."], ["Take it; buy some provisions; be happy, and tomorrow we shall have more.”", "먹을 걸 사세요. 마음 편히 지내시고요. 내일이면 더 생길 테니까요.\""]], [["“Gently, gently,”", "\"천천히, 천천히 하자꾸나.\""], ["said the old man, with a smile; “and by your leave I will use your purse moderately, for they would say, if they saw me buy too many things at a time, that I had been obliged to await your return, in order to be able to purchase them.”", "노인이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그리고 네가 허락한다면 네 지갑을 아껴 쓰마. 내가 한꺼번에 너무 많은 걸 사들이는 걸 사람들이 보면, 이걸 살 수 있을 때까지 네가 돌아오기만 기다렸다고들 할 테니까.\""]], [["“Do as you please; but, first of all, pray have a servant, father.", "\"마음대로 하세요. 하지만 무엇보다도, 아버지, 부디 사람을 하나 두세요."], ["I will not have you left alone so long.", "이렇게 오래도록 혼자 지내시게 둘 수는 없어요."], ["I have some smuggled coffee and most capital tobacco, in a small chest in the hold, which you shall have tomorrow.", "선창의 작은 궤짝에 밀수해 들여온 커피랑 아주 상등품 담배가 있으니, 내일 가져다 드릴게요."], ["But, hush, here comes somebody.”", "그런데 쉿, 누가 오네요.\""]], [["“’Tis Caderousse, who has heard of your arrival, and no doubt comes to congratulate you on your fortunate return.”", "\"카드루스로구나. 네가 돌아왔다는 소식을 듣고, 무사히 돌아온 걸 축하하러 오는 게 분명하다.\""]], [["“Ah, lips that say one thing, while the heart thinks another,”", "\"아, 입으로는 한 가지를 말하면서 속으로는 딴생각을 하는 자라니까요.\""], ["murmured Edmond.", "에드몽이 중얼거렸다."], ["“But, never mind, he is a neighbor who has done us a service on a time, so he’s welcome.”", "\"하지만 괜찮아요. 한때 우리에게 도움을 준 이웃이니, 반갑게 맞아야지요.\""]], [["As Edmond paused, the black and bearded head of Caderousse appeared at the door.", "에드몽이 말을 멈춘 사이, 검고 수염 난 카드루스의 머리가 문가에 나타났다."], ["He was a man of twenty-five or six, and held a piece of cloth, which, being a tailor, he was about to make into a coat-lining.", "스물대여섯쯤 된 사내였고, 천 조각을 손에 들고 있었다. 재단사인 그는 그것으로 외투 안감을 대려는 참이었다."]], [["“What, is it you, Edmond, back again?”", "\"아니, 에드몽, 자네 돌아왔나?\""], ["said he, with a broad Marseillaise accent, and a grin that displayed his ivory-white teeth.", "그가 마르세유 사투리가 짙게 밴 말투로, 상아처럼 흰 이를 드러내며 히죽 웃었다."]], [["“Yes, as you see, neighbor Caderousse; and ready to be agreeable to you in any and every way,”", "\"네, 보시다시피요, 카드루스 아저씨. 어떤 일이든 기꺼이 도와 드릴 준비가 돼 있어요.\""], ["replied Dantès, but ill-concealing his coldness under this cloak of civility.", "당테스가 대답했지만, 예의라는 겉치레 아래 냉랭함을 잘 감추지는 못했다."]], [["“Thanks—thanks; but, fortunately, I do not want for anything; and it chances that at times there are others who have need of me.”", "\"고맙네, 고마워. 하지만 다행히 난 아쉬운 게 없어. 오히려 가끔은 나를 필요로 하는 사람들이 있지.\""], ["Dantès made a gesture.", "당테스가 무슨 몸짓을 했다."], ["“I do not allude to you, my boy. No!—no!", "\"자네 얘기가 아니야, 이 사람아. 아니지, 아니고말고!"], ["I lent you money, and you returned it; that’s like good neighbors, and we are quits.”", "내가 자네한테 돈을 빌려줬고 자네가 갚았으니, 그게 좋은 이웃 사이지. 우린 셈이 끝난 거야.\""]], [["“We are never quits with those who oblige us,”", "\"우리를 도와준 이들과는 결코 셈이 끝나지 않지요.\""], ["was Dantès’ reply; “for when we do not owe them money, we owe them gratitude.”", "당테스가 대답했다. \"돈을 갚고 나면, 이번엔 고마운 마음을 빚지게 되니까요.\""]], [["“What’s the use of mentioning that?", "\"그런 얘긴 해서 뭐 하나?"], ["What is done is done.", "끝난 건 끝난 거지."], ["Let us talk of your happy return, my boy.", "자네가 무사히 돌아온 얘기나 하세, 이 사람아."], ["I had gone on the quay to match a piece of mulberry cloth, when I met friend Danglars.", "내가 오디색 천을 맞춰 보려고 부두에 나갔다가 친구 당글라르를 만났지 뭔가. '아니, 자네 마르세유에 있었나?'"], ["‘You at Marseilles?’—‘Yes,’ says he.", "하니까 '그래.' 하더군."]], [["“‘I thought you were at Smyrna.’—‘I was; but am now back again.’", "\"'난 자네가 스미르나에 있는 줄 알았는데.' 했더니 '있었지. 한데 이제 돌아왔어.' 하더라고."]], [["“‘And where is the dear boy, our little Edmond?’", "\"'그럼 그 귀여운 친구, 우리 어린 에드몽은 어디 있나?'"]], [["“‘Why, with his father, no doubt,’ replied Danglars.", "\"'아버지하고 같이 있겠지, 뭐.' 당글라르가 그러더군."], ["And so I came,”", "그래서 이렇게,\""], ["added Caderousse, “as fast as I could to have the pleasure of shaking hands with a friend.”", "카드루스가 덧붙였다. \"한달음에 달려왔지. 친구하고 악수라도 나누는 기쁨을 누리려고 말이야.\""]], [["0037m\n“Worthy Caderousse!”", "\"고마운 카드루스 같으니!\""], ["said the old man, “he is so much attached to us.”", "노인이 말했다. \"우리한테 정이 참 깊은 사람이야.\""]], [["“Yes, to be sure I am.", "\"그럼요, 정말 그렇고말고요."], ["I love and esteem you, because honest folks are so rare.", "제가 두 분을 사랑하고 존경하는 건, 정직한 사람이 워낙 드물기 때문이지요."], ["But it seems you have come back rich, my boy,”", "한데 자네, 부자가 되어 돌아온 모양이군.\""], ["continued the tailor, looking askance at the handful of gold and silver which Dantès had thrown on the table.", "재단사가 당테스가 탁자 위에 던져 놓은 한 줌의 금화와 은화를 곁눈질하며 말을 이었다."]], [["The young man remarked the greedy glance which shone in the dark eyes of his neighbor. “Eh,”", "젊은이는 이웃의 검은 눈에 번득이는 탐욕스러운 눈빛을 알아챘다. \"아.\""], ["he said, negligently, “this money is not mine.", "그가 무심한 듯 말했다. \"이 돈은 제 게 아니에요."], ["I was expressing to my father my fears that he had wanted many things in my absence, and to convince me he emptied his purse on the table.", "제가 없는 동안 아버지가 여러모로 궁하셨을까 봐 걱정된다고 했더니, 그게 아니란 걸 보여 주시려고 지갑을 탁자에 털어 놓으신 거예요. 자, 아버지.\""], ["Come, father”", "당테스가 덧붙였다."], ["added Dantès, “put this money back in your box—unless neighbor Caderousse wants anything, and in that case it is at his service.”", "\"이 돈은 도로 상자에 넣어 두세요. 카드루스 아저씨가 뭔가 필요하지 않으시다면요. 필요하시면 얼마든지 쓰셔도 되고요.\""]], [["“No, my boy, no,” said Caderousse.", "\"아니야, 이 사람아, 아니야.\""], ["“I am not in any want, thank God, my living is suited to my means.", "카드루스가 말했다. \"난 아쉬울 게 없어. 고맙게도 내 형편에 맞춰 살고 있으니까."], ["Keep your money—keep it, I say;—one never has too much;—but, at the same time, my boy, I am as much obliged by your offer as if I took advantage of it.”", "그 돈은 자네가 간직하게, 간직하라니까. 돈이란 아무리 많아도 지나친 법이 없지. 하지만 자네가 권해 준 것만으로도, 정말 받아 쓴 것만큼이나 고맙네.\""]], [["“It was offered with good will,” said Dantès.", "\"진심으로 권한 거예요.\" 당테스가 말했다."]], [["“No doubt, my boy; no doubt.", "\"그렇겠지, 이 사람아, 그렇고말고."], ["Well, you stand well with M. Morrel I hear,—you insinuating dog, you!”", "그건 그렇고, 듣자 하니 자네 모렐 씨 눈에 단단히 들었다던데. 이 능청맞은 친구 같으니!\""]], [["“M. Morrel has always been exceedingly kind to me,”", "\"모렐 씨는 늘 제게 더없이 잘해 주셨어요.\""], ["replied Dantès.", "당테스가 대답했다."]], [["“Then you were wrong to refuse to dine with him.”", "\"그렇다면 그분과 저녁을 같이 들자는 걸 마다한 건 잘못한 일이지.\""]], [["“What, did you refuse to dine with him?”", "\"아니, 그분과 저녁 드는 걸 마다했단 말이냐?\""], ["said old Dantès; “and did he invite you to dine?”", "늙은 당테스가 말했다. \"그분이 너를 저녁 식사에 초대하셨어?\""]], [["“Yes, my dear father,”", "\"네, 아버지.\""], ["replied Edmond, smiling at his father’s astonishment at the excessive honor paid to his son.", "에드몽이, 아들에게 베풀어진 과분한 영예에 놀라워하는 아버지를 보며 미소 지으며 대답했다."]], [["“And why did you refuse, my son?”", "\"그런데 왜 마다했느냐, 아들아?\""], ["inquired the old man.", "노인이 물었다."]], [["“That I might the sooner see you again, my dear father,”", "\"한시라도 빨리 아버지를 다시 뵙고 싶어서였지요.\""], ["replied the young man.", "젊은이가 대답했다."], ["“I was most anxious to see you.”", "\"아버지가 무척 보고 싶었거든요.\""]], [["“But it must have vexed M. Morrel, good, worthy man,”", "\"하지만 그건 모렐 씨를, 그 선량하고 훌륭한 분을 언짢게 했을 게야.\""], ["said Caderousse.", "카드루스가 말했다."], ["“And when you are looking forward to be captain, it was wrong to annoy the owner.”", "\"더구나 선장이 되기를 바라는 마당에, 선주를 거슬리게 한 건 잘못이지.\""]], [["“But I explained to him the cause of my refusal,”", "\"하지만 거절한 까닭을 그분께 말씀드렸고,\""], ["replied Dantès, “and I hope he fully understood it.”", "당테스가 대답했다. \"충분히 이해하셨으리라 믿어요.\""]], [["“Yes, but to be captain one must do a little flattery to one’s patrons.”", "\"그래도 선장이 되려면 윗사람한테 약간의 아첨은 떨어야 하는 법이야.\""]], [["“I hope to be captain without that,”", "\"그런 것 없이 선장이 되고 싶어요.\""], ["said Dantès.", "당테스가 말했다."]], [["“So much the better—so much the better!", "\"그럴수록 더 좋지, 더 좋고말고!"], ["Nothing will give greater pleasure to all your old friends; and I know one down there behind the Saint Nicolas citadel who will not be sorry to hear it.”", "자네 옛 친구들 모두에게 그보다 더 큰 기쁨은 없을 거야. 그리고 저기 생니콜라 요새 너머에 그 소식을 들으면 섭섭해하지 않을 사람이 하나 있다는 것도 내가 알지.\""]], [["“Mercédès?” said the old man.", "\"메르세데스 말인가?\" 노인이 말했다."]], [["“Yes, my dear father, and with your permission, now I have seen you, and know you are well and have all you require, I will ask your consent to go and pay a visit to the Catalans.”", "\"네, 아버지. 아버지가 허락해 주신다면, 이렇게 아버지를 뵙고 건강하신 것도, 필요한 게 다 갖춰진 것도 확인했으니, 이제 카탈루냐 마을에 다녀와도 좋다고 허락해 주세요.\""]], [["“Go, my dear boy,”", "\"가거라, 내 아들아.\""], ["said old Dantès; “and Heaven bless you in your wife, as it has blessed me in my son!”", "늙은 당테스가 말했다. \"내가 아들 덕에 복을 받았듯, 하늘이 네게 좋은 아내로 복을 내려 주시기를!\""]], [["“His wife!”", "\"아내라니!\""], ["said Caderousse; “why, how fast you go on, father Dantès; she is not his wife yet, as it seems to me.”", "카드루스가 말했다. \"아니, 어찌 그리 앞서 가시나, 당테스 영감님. 내가 보기엔 아직 저 사람 아내가 아닌데요.\""]], [["“No, but according to all probability she soon will be,”", "\"아직은 아니지요. 하지만 머잖아 그렇게 될 게 틀림없는걸요.\""], ["replied Edmond.", "에드몽이 대답했다."]], [["“Yes—yes,”", "\"그래, 그래.\""], ["said Caderousse; “but you were right to return as soon as possible, my boy.”", "카드루스가 말했다. \"하지만 되도록 빨리 돌아온 건 잘한 일이야, 이 사람아.\""]], [["“And why?”", "\"왜요?\""]], [["“Because Mercédès is a very fine girl, and fine girls never lack followers; she particularly has them by dozens.”", "\"메르세데스는 아주 어여쁜 처녀고, 어여쁜 처녀에겐 늘 따라다니는 사내들이 끊이질 않거든. 더구나 그 처녀한테는 떼로 몰려들지.\""]], [["“Really?” answered Edmond, with a smile which had in it traces of slight uneasiness.", "\"정말요?\" 에드몽이, 희미한 불안의 기색이 어린 미소를 띠며 대답했다."]], [["0039m\n“Ah, yes,”", "\"아, 그럼.\""], ["continued Caderousse, “and capital offers, too; but you know, you will be captain, and who could refuse you then?”", "카드루스가 말을 이었다. \"그것도 썩 괜찮은 청혼들이지. 하지만 알다시피 자넨 선장이 될 몸이니, 그땐 누가 자넬 마다하겠나?\""]], [["“Meaning to say,”", "\"그러니까,\""], ["replied Dantès, with a smile which but ill-concealed his trouble, “that if I were not a captain——”", "당테스가, 속마음을 잘 감추지 못하는 미소를 띠며 대답했다. \"제가 선장이 못 된다면……\""]], [["“Eh—eh!” said Caderousse, shaking his head.", "\"어허, 그게 말이지!\" 카드루스가 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했다."]], [["“Come, come,”", "\"자, 자.\""], ["said the sailor, “I have a better opinion than you of women in general, and of Mercédès in particular; and I am certain that, captain or not, she will remain ever faithful to me.”", "선원이 말했다. \"저는 아저씨보다 여자들을, 특히 메르세데스를 더 좋게 봅니다. 그리고 제가 선장이 되든 못 되든, 그녀가 언제까지나 제게 한결같으리란 걸 확신해요.\""]], [["“So much the better—so much the better,”", "\"그럴수록 더 좋지, 더 좋고말고.\""], ["said Caderousse.", "카드루스가 말했다."], ["“When one is going to be married, there is nothing like implicit confidence; but never mind that, my boy,—go and announce your arrival, and let her know all your hopes and prospects.”", "\"결혼을 앞둔 사람한테 굳건한 믿음만 한 게 없으니까. 하지만 그런 건 신경 쓰지 말게, 이 사람아. 어서 가서 자네가 돌아온 걸 알리고, 자네의 모든 꿈과 앞날의 일들을 그 처녀에게 들려주게나.\""]], [["“I will go directly,”", "\"바로 가야지요.\""], ["was Edmond’s reply; and, embracing his father, and nodding to Caderousse, he left the apartment.", "에드몽이 대답했다. 그러고는 아버지를 끌어안고 카드루스에게 가볍게 고개를 까딱한 뒤 방을 나섰다."]], [["Caderousse lingered for a moment, then taking leave of old Dantès, he went downstairs to rejoin Danglars, who awaited him at the corner of the Rue Senac.", "카드루스는 잠시 머뭇거리다가, 늙은 당테스에게 작별을 고하고 아래층으로 내려가 세나크 거리 모퉁이에서 기다리던 당글라르에게 다시 합류했다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래,\""], ["said Danglars, “did you see him?”", "당글라르가 말했다. \"만나 봤나?\""]], [["“I have just left him,”", "\"방금 헤어졌네.\""], ["answered Caderousse.", "카드루스가 대답했다."]], [["“Did he allude to his hope of being captain?”", "\"선장이 되리란 기대에 대해 무슨 말을 하던가?\""]], [["“He spoke of it as a thing already decided.”", "\"이미 정해진 일처럼 말하더군.\""]], [["“Indeed!”", "\"이런!\""], ["said Danglars, “he is in too much hurry, it appears to me.”", "당글라르가 말했다. \"내가 보기엔 너무 서두르는 것 같은데.\""]], [["“Why, it seems M. Morrel has promised him the thing.”", "\"글쎄, 모렐 씨가 그 자리를 약속한 모양이야.\""]], [["“So that he is quite elated about it?”", "\"그래서 잔뜩 우쭐해 있던가?\""]], [["“Why, yes, he is actually insolent over the matter—has already offered me his patronage, as if he were a grand personage, and proffered me a loan of money, as though he were a banker.”", "\"우쭐 정도가 아니라, 아주 거만하게 굴더라니까. 무슨 대단한 인물이라도 되는 양 나한테 자기 후원을 받으라고 하질 않나, 무슨 은행가라도 되는 양 돈을 빌려주겠다고 나서질 않나.\""]], [["“Which you refused?”", "\"그래서 거절했고?\""]], [["“Most assuredly; although I might easily have accepted it, for it was I who put into his hands the first silver he ever earned; but now M.", "\"두말하면 잔소리지. 마음만 먹었으면 쉽게 받을 수도 있었어. 그 친구가 난생처음 손에 쥔 은화를 쥐여 준 게 바로 나였으니까."], ["Dantès has no longer any occasion for assistance—he is about to become a captain.”", "하지만 이제 당테스 씨는 남의 도움 따위 필요 없으시다네. 곧 선장이 되실 몸이니 말이야.\""]], [["“Pooh!”", "\"흥!\""], ["said Danglars, “he is not one yet.”", "당글라르가 말했다. \"아직 선장이 된 것도 아니잖아.\""]], [["“_Ma foi!_ it will be as well if he is not,”", "\"그럼! 차라리 안 되는 편이 나아.\""], ["answered Caderousse; “for if he should be, there will be really no speaking to him.”", "카드루스가 대답했다. \"선장이라도 됐다간, 정말이지 말도 못 붙이게 될 테니까.\""]], [["“If we choose,”", "\"우리가 마음만 먹으면,\""], ["replied Danglars, “he will remain what he is; and perhaps become even less than he is.”", "당글라르가 대답했다. \"저 친구는 지금 그대로일 거야. 어쩌면 지금보다 못한 신세가 될지도 모르지.\""]], [["“What do you mean?”", "\"그게 무슨 소린가?\""]], [["“Nothing—I was speaking to myself.", "\"아무것도 아니야. 혼잣말이었어."], ["And is he still in love with the Catalane?”", "그래, 저 친구 아직도 그 카탈루냐 처녀한테 푹 빠져 있나?\""]], [["“Over head and ears; but, unless I am much mistaken, there will be a storm in that quarter.”", "\"머리끝까지 빠졌지. 하지만 내가 크게 잘못 본 게 아니라면, 그쪽에 한바탕 풍파가 일 거야.\""]], [["0041m\n“Explain yourself.”", "\"무슨 말인지 좀 풀어 보게.\""]], [["“Why should I?”", "\"내가 왜?\""]], [["“It is more important than you think, perhaps.", "\"자네가 생각하는 것보다 중요한 일일지도 몰라."], ["You do not like Dantès?”", "자네, 당테스를 좋아하지 않지?\""]], [["“I never like upstarts.”", "\"난 벼락출세하는 자들을 좋아한 적이 없어.\""]], [["“Then tell me all you know about the Catalane.”", "\"그럼 그 카탈루냐 처녀에 대해 아는 걸 다 말해 보게.\""]], [["“I know nothing for certain; only I have seen things which induce me to believe, as I told you, that the future captain will find some annoyance in the vicinity of the Vieilles Infirmeries.”", "\"확실히 아는 건 없네. 다만 아까 말했듯이, 그 미래의 선장 양반이 비에유 앵피르므리 근처에서 골치깨나 썩일 거라고 믿게 할 만한 것들을 봤을 뿐이지.\""]], [["“What have you seen?—come, tell me!”", "\"뭘 봤는데? 자, 어서 말해 보게!\""]], [["“Well, every time I have seen Mercédès come into the city she has been accompanied by a tall, strapping, black-eyed Catalan, with a red complexion, brown skin, and fierce air, whom she calls cousin.”", "\"음, 메르세데스가 시내로 들어오는 걸 볼 때마다, 늘 키 크고 떡 벌어진 검은 눈의 카탈루냐 사내가 따라붙어 있더라고. 불그레한 낯빛에 가무잡잡한 살갗, 사나운 인상을 한 자였어. 처녀는 그자를 사촌이라고 부르더군.\""]], [["“Really; and you think this cousin pays her attentions?”", "\"그래? 그럼 그 사촌이 처녀한테 마음을 두고 있다고 보는 건가?\""]], [["“I only suppose so.", "\"그저 짐작일 뿐이야."], ["What else can a strapping chap of twenty-one mean with a fine wench of seventeen?”", "안 그러면 떡대 좋은 스물한 살짜리 사내가 열일곱짜리 어여쁜 처녀한테 무슨 다른 속셈이 있겠나?\""]], [["“And you say that Dantès has gone to the Catalans?”", "\"그래서 당테스가 카탈루냐 마을에 갔다는 거지?\""]], [["“He went before I came down.”", "\"내가 내려오기 전에 벌써 갔어.\""]], [["“Let us go the same way; we will stop at La Réserve, and we can drink a glass of La Malgue, whilst we wait for news.”", "\"우리도 같은 쪽으로 가세. 라레제르브에 들러 라말그 한 잔 마시면서 소식을 기다리면 되겠군.\""]], [["“Come along,”", "\"가세.\""], ["said Caderousse; “but you pay the score.”", "카드루스가 말했다. \"대신 술값은 자네가 내게.\""]], [["“Of course,”", "\"물론이지.\""], ["replied Danglars; and going quickly to the designated place, they called for a bottle of wine, and two glasses.", "당글라르가 대답했다. 둘은 그곳으로 서둘러 가서 포도주 한 병과 잔 두 개를 청했다."]], [["Père Pamphile had seen Dantès pass not ten minutes before; and assured that he was at the Catalans, they sat down under the budding foliage of the planes and sycamores, in the branches of which the birds were singing their welcome to one of the first days of spring.", "팡필 영감은 채 십 분도 안 돼 당테스가 지나가는 것을 보았다고 했다. 그가 카탈루냐 마을에 있는 게 분명하다고 확신한 두 사람은, 막 움트기 시작한 플라타너스와 시카모어 잎사귀 아래 자리를 잡고 앉았다. 그 가지에서는 새들이 봄의 첫 며칠 가운데 하루를 반기며 지저귀고 있었다."]], [["# Chapter 3. The Catalans", "# Chapter 3. The Catalans"]], [["Beyond a bare, weather-worn wall, about a hundred paces from the spot where the two friends sat looking and listening as they drank their wine, was the village of the Catalans.", "세 친구가 포도주를 마시며 보고 듣던 자리에서 백 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳, 비바람에 닳은 헐벗은 벽 너머에 카탈루냐 마을이 있었다."], ["Long ago this mysterious colony quitted Spain, and settled on the tongue of land on which it is to this day.", "오래전 이 수수께끼 같은 무리는 에스파냐를 떠나, 오늘날까지도 그들이 머무는 그 좁은 땅에 정착했다."], ["Whence it came no one knew, and it spoke an unknown tongue.", "어디서 왔는지 아는 사람은 없었고, 그들은 알 수 없는 말을 썼다."], ["One of its chiefs, who understood Provençal, begged the commune of Marseilles to give them this bare and barren promontory, where, like the sailors of old, they had run their boats ashore.", "프로방스 말을 할 줄 아는 그들의 우두머리 중 한 사람이 마르세유시 당국에 이 헐벗고 메마른 곶을 달라고 청했다. 옛 뱃사람들처럼 그들이 배를 끌어 올려 둔 곳이었다."], ["The request was granted; and three months afterwards, around the twelve or fifteen small vessels which had brought these gypsies of the sea, a small village sprang up.", "청은 받아들여졌고, 석 달 뒤에는 이 바다 집시들을 실어 온 열두어 척에서 열다섯 척쯤 되는 작은 배들을 둘러싸고 작은 마을이 생겨났다."], ["This village, constructed in a singular and picturesque manner, half Moorish, half Spanish, still remains, and is inhabited by descendants of the first comers, who speak the language of their fathers.", "무어풍과 에스파냐풍이 반반씩 섞인 독특하고 그림 같은 모양으로 지어진 이 마을은 지금도 남아 있으며, 처음 건너온 이들의 후손이 살면서 조상의 말을 쓴다."], ["For three or four centuries they have remained upon this small promontory, on which they had settled like a flight of seabirds, without mixing with the Marseillaise population, intermarrying, and preserving their original customs and the costume of their mother-country as they have preserved its language.", "서너 세기 동안 그들은 한 무리의 바닷새가 내려앉듯 자리 잡은 이 작은 곶 위에 머물렀다. 마르세유 사람들과 섞이지 않고 자기들끼리만 혼인하며, 모국의 언어를 지켜 온 것처럼 본래의 풍습과 옷차림도 고스란히 간직해 왔다."]], [["Our readers will follow us along the only street of this little village, and enter with us one of the houses, which is sunburned to the beautiful dead-leaf color peculiar to the buildings of the country, and within coated with whitewash, like a Spanish posada.", "독자들은 이 작은 마을의 단 하나뿐인 골목길을 따라 우리와 함께 걸어, 어느 집으로 들어서게 될 것이다. 그 집은 햇볕에 그을려 이 고장 건물 특유의, 마른 나뭇잎 같은 아름다운 빛깔을 띠었고, 안쪽은 에스파냐의 여인숙처럼 흰 회칠이 되어 있었다. 칠흑처럼 검은 머리에 영양처럼 부드러운 눈을 가진 젊고 아름다운 처녀가 벽판에 등을 기대고 서 있었다."], ["A young and beautiful girl, with hair as black as jet, her eyes as velvety as the gazelle’s, was leaning with her back against the wainscot, rubbing in her slender delicately moulded fingers a bunch of heath blossoms, the flowers of which she was picking off and strewing on the floor; her arms, bare to the elbow, brown, and modelled after those of the Arlesian Venus, moved with a kind of restless impatience, and she tapped the earth with her arched and supple foot, so as to display the pure and full shape of her well-turned leg, in its red cotton, gray and blue clocked, stocking.", "가늘고 섬세하게 빚어진 손가락으로 히스 꽃 한 다발을 비벼, 떨어진 꽃잎을 바닥에 흩뿌리고 있었다. 팔꿈치까지 드러난 갈색 팔은 아를의 비너스 상에서 본뜬 듯한 모양이었는데, 안절부절못하는 듯한 조바심으로 움직였다. 그녀가 활처럼 휘고 유연한 발로 땅을 톡톡 두드릴 때마다, 회색과 파란색 무늬가 박힌 붉은 무명 양말 속, 매끈하게 빠진 다리의 순결하고 풍만한 윤곽이 드러났다."], ["At three paces from her, seated in a chair which he balanced on two legs, leaning his elbow on an old worm-eaten table, was a tall young man of twenty, or two-and-twenty, who was looking at her with an air in which vexation and uneasiness were mingled.", "그녀에게서 세 걸음 떨어진 곳, 벌레 먹은 낡은 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 의자를 뒷다리 둘로만 기우뚱하게 세운 채 앉아 있는 사람은 스물에서 스물둘쯤 된 키 큰 청년이었다. 그는 짜증과 불안이 뒤섞인 표정으로 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["He questioned her with his eyes, but the firm and steady gaze of the young girl controlled his look.", "두 눈으로 그녀에게 묻고 있었지만, 처녀의 굳고 흔들림 없는 눈길이 그의 시선을 눌렀다."]], [["“You see, Mercédès,”", "\"봐, 메르세데스.\""], ["said the young man, “here is Easter come round again; tell me, is this the moment for a wedding?”", "청년이 말했다. \"또 부활절이 돌아왔어. 말해 봐, 이제는 결혼할 때가 되지 않았어?\""]], [["“I have answered you a hundred times, Fernand, and really you must be very stupid to ask me again.”", "\"백 번도 더 대답했잖아, 페르낭. 또 묻는 걸 보면 정말 어지간히 둔하구나.\""]], [["“Well, repeat it,—repeat it, I beg of you, that I may at last believe it!", "\"그래, 그럼 다시, 다시 말해 줘, 제발. 그래야 나도 마침내 믿을 수 있을 테니까!"], ["Tell me for the hundredth time that you refuse my love, which had your mother’s sanction.", "백 번째로 말해 줘. 네 어머니까지 허락하셨던 내 사랑을 거절한다고."], ["Make me understand once for all that you are trifling with my happiness, that my life or death are nothing to you.", "네가 내 행복을 가지고 장난치고 있다는 걸, 내가 죽든 살든 너한테는 아무것도 아니라는 걸 한 번에 깨닫게 해 줘."], ["Ah, to have dreamed for ten years of being your husband, Mercédès, and to lose that hope, which was the only stay of my existence!”", "아, 십 년 동안 네 남편이 되는 꿈을 꿔 왔는데, 메르세데스, 내 삶을 지탱해 준 유일한 희망을 잃다니!\""]], [["“At least it was not I who ever encouraged you in that hope, Fernand,”", "\"적어도 그 희망을 부추긴 건 내가 아니야, 페르낭.\" 메르세데스가 대답했다."], ["replied Mercédès; “you cannot reproach me with the slightest coquetry.", "\"넌 내가 조금이라도 교태를 부렸다고 탓할 수는 없어. 난 늘 너에게 말했잖아. '난 너를 오빠처럼 사랑해."], ["I have always said to you, ‘I love you as a brother; but do not ask from me more than sisterly affection, for my heart is another’s.’ Is not this true, Fernand?”", "하지만 누이의 정 이상은 바라지 마. 내 마음은 다른 사람의 것이니까.' 안 그래, 페르낭?\""]], [["“Yes, that is very true, Mercédès,”", "\"그래, 정말 그랬지, 메르세데스.\""], ["replied the young man, “Yes, you have been cruelly frank with me; but do you forget that it is among the Catalans a sacred law to intermarry?”", "청년이 대답했다. \"그래, 넌 잔인할 만큼 솔직했어. 하지만 카탈루냐 사람들끼리 혼인하는 것이 신성한 율법이라는 걸 잊었어?\""]], [["0045m\n“You mistake, Fernand; it is not a law, but merely a custom, and, I pray of you, do not cite this custom in your favor.", "\"틀렸어, 페르낭. 그건 율법이 아니라 그저 관습일 뿐이야. 그러니 부탁인데, 네게 유리하게 그 관습을 들먹이지 마."], ["You are included in the conscription, Fernand, and are only at liberty on sufferance, liable at any moment to be called upon to take up arms.", "넌 징집 대상이잖아, 페르낭. 지금 풀려나 있는 것도 잠시 봐주는 것뿐이고, 언제라도 무기를 들라는 부름을 받을 수 있어."], ["Once a soldier, what would you do with me, a poor orphan, forlorn, without fortune, with nothing but a half-ruined hut and a few ragged nets, the miserable inheritance left by my father to my mother, and by my mother to me?", "일단 군인이 되면, 가난한 고아인 나를 어쩌려고? 의지할 데도 없고 재산도 없는, 다 쓰러져 가는 오두막 한 채와 다 해진 그물 몇 개밖에 없는 나를. 그것도 아버지가 어머니께, 어머니가 내게 남긴 초라한 유산일 뿐이야."], ["She has been dead a year, and you know, Fernand, I have subsisted almost entirely on public charity.", "어머니가 돌아가신 지 일 년이 됐는데, 페르낭, 너도 알다시피 난 거의 남의 적선으로 연명해 왔어."], ["Sometimes you pretend I am useful to you, and that is an excuse to share with me the produce of your fishing, and I accept it, Fernand, because you are the son of my father’s brother, because we were brought up together, and still more because it would give you so much pain if I refuse.", "넌 가끔 내가 너에게 쓸모가 있는 척하지만, 그건 네가 잡은 물고기를 나와 나누기 위한 핑계일 뿐이지. 그래도 난 그걸 받아, 페르낭. 네가 아버지 형제의 아들이고, 우리가 함께 자랐고, 무엇보다 내가 거절하면 네가 너무나 마음 아파할 테니까."], ["But I feel very deeply that this fish which I go and sell, and with the produce of which I buy the flax I spin,—I feel very keenly, Fernand, that this is charity.”", "하지만 내가 가져다 파는 그 물고기, 그걸 판 돈으로 실을 잣는 아마를 사는 그 물고기가 사실은 적선이라는 걸, 페르낭, 난 뼈저리게 느끼고 있어.\""]], [["“And if it were, Mercédès, poor and lone as you are, you suit me as well as the daughter of the first shipowner or the richest banker of Marseilles!", "\"설령 그렇다 해도, 메르세데스, 가난하고 외로운 너라도, 나에게는 마르세유 제일가는 선주의 딸이나 제일 부유한 은행가의 딸만큼이나 어울리는 사람이야!"], ["What do such as we desire but a good wife and careful housekeeper, and where can I look for these better than in you?”", "우리 같은 사람이 바라는 게 좋은 아내와 알뜰한 살림꾼 말고 뭐가 있겠어? 그런 사람을 너 말고 어디서 찾겠어?\""]], [["“Fernand,”", "\"페르낭.\""], ["answered Mercédès, shaking her head, “a woman becomes a bad manager, and who shall say she will remain an honest woman, when she loves another man better than her husband?", "메르세데스가 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"여자가 제 남편보다 다른 남자를 더 사랑하면, 살림은 엉망이 되고, 그 여자가 정숙하게 남을지 누가 장담하겠어?"], ["Rest content with my friendship, for I say once more that is all I can promise, and I will promise no more than I can bestow.”", "내 우정으로 만족해. 다시 말하지만 그게 내가 약속할 수 있는 전부고, 난 줄 수 있는 것 이상은 약속하지 않을 거야.\""]], [["“I understand,”", "\"알겠어.\""], ["replied Fernand, “you can endure your own wretchedness patiently, but you are afraid to share mine.", "페르낭이 대답했다. \"넌 네 비참함은 참고 견딜 수 있어도, 내 비참함을 함께 나누는 건 두려운 거야."], ["Well, Mercédès, beloved by you, I would tempt fortune; you would bring me good luck, and I should become rich.", "좋아, 메르세데스, 네 사랑을 받는다면 난 운명에 맞서 보겠어. 네가 행운을 가져다줄 테고, 난 부자가 될 거야."], ["I could extend my occupation as a fisherman, might get a place as clerk in a warehouse, and become in time a dealer myself.”", "어부 일을 넓혀 나가고, 어느 창고의 사무원 자리를 얻고, 때가 되면 나도 장사꾼이 될 수 있어.\""]], [["“You could do no such thing, Fernand; you are a soldier, and if you remain at the Catalans it is because there is no war; so remain a fisherman, and contented with my friendship, as I cannot give you more.”", "\"그런 건 못 해, 페르낭. 넌 군인이야. 네가 카탈루냐에 머물러 있는 건 전쟁이 없기 때문일 뿐이야. 그러니 어부로 남아, 내 우정에 만족해. 그 이상은 줄 수 없으니까.\""]], [["“Well, I will do better, Mercédès.", "\"좋아, 그럼 더 잘해 보겠어, 메르세데스."], ["I will be a sailor; instead of the costume of our fathers, which you despise, I will wear a varnished hat, a striped shirt, and a blue jacket, with an anchor on the buttons.", "난 뱃사람이 되겠어. 네가 경멸하는 우리 조상의 옷차림 대신, 칠을 한 모자에 줄무늬 셔츠, 단추에 닻이 새겨진 파란 웃옷을 입겠어."], ["Would not that dress please you?”", "그런 차림이면 네 마음에 들겠어?\""]], [["“What do you mean?”", "\"무슨 말이야?\""], ["asked Mercédès, with an angry glance,—“what do you mean?", "메르세데스가 성난 눈길로 물었다. \"무슨 소리야?"], ["I do not understand you?”", "무슨 말인지 모르겠어.\""]], [["“I mean, Mercédès, that you are thus harsh and cruel with me, because you are expecting someone who is thus attired; but perhaps he whom you await is inconstant, or if he is not, the sea is so to him.”", "\"내 말은, 메르세데스, 네가 나한테 이렇게 모질고 잔인한 건, 그런 차림을 한 누군가를 기다리고 있기 때문이라는 거야. 하지만 어쩌면 네가 기다리는 그 사람은 변덕스러운 자일지도 몰라. 설령 그가 그렇지 않더라도, 바다가 변덕스러우니까.\""]], [["“Fernand,”", "\"페르낭!\""], ["cried Mercédès, “I believed you were good-hearted, and I was mistaken!", "메르세데스가 외쳤다. \"난 네가 착한 사람인 줄 알았는데, 내가 잘못 봤구나!"], ["Fernand, you are wicked to call to your aid jealousy and the anger of God!", "페르낭, 질투와 하느님의 진노를 끌어들이다니, 너 정말 못됐어!"], ["Yes, I will not deny it, I do await, and I do love him of whom you speak; and, if he does not return, instead of accusing him of the inconstancy which you insinuate, I will tell you that he died loving me and me only.”", "그래, 부인하지 않겠어. 난 네가 말하는 그 사람을 기다리고 있고, 사랑해. 그리고 만약 그가 돌아오지 않는다 해도, 네가 넌지시 비추는 변덕을 탓하는 대신, 그가 나만을, 오직 나만을 사랑하며 죽었다고 말하겠어.\""], ["The young girl made a gesture of rage.", "처녀는 분노에 찬 몸짓을 했다."], ["“I understand you, Fernand; you would be revenged on him because I do not love you; you would cross your Catalan knife with his dirk.", "\"네 속을 알겠어, 페르낭. 내가 너를 사랑하지 않으니까 그 사람에게 복수하려는 거지. 네 카탈루냐 칼을 그의 단검과 맞대 보려는 거야."], ["What end would that answer?", "그래서 뭘 얻겠어?"], ["To lose you my friendship if he were conquered, and see that friendship changed into hate if you were victor.", "그가 진다면 넌 내 우정을 잃을 테고, 네가 이긴다면 그 우정이 증오로 바뀌는 걸 보게 될 거야."], ["Believe me, to seek a quarrel with a man is a bad method of pleasing the woman who loves that man.", "내 말을 믿어, 한 남자에게 싸움을 거는 건 그 남자를 사랑하는 여자의 마음을 얻는 데 나쁜 방법이야."], ["No, Fernand, you will not thus give way to evil thoughts.", "안 돼, 페르낭, 넌 그런 사악한 생각에 굴복하지 않을 거야."], ["Unable to have me for your wife, you will content yourself with having me for your friend and sister; and besides,”", "나를 아내로 둘 수 없으니, 누이이자 벗으로 두는 것에 만족할 거야. 그리고,\" 그녀는 눈물로 흐려진 눈을 하고 덧붙였다."], ["she added, her eyes troubled and moistened with tears, “wait, wait, Fernand; you said just now that the sea was treacherous, and he has been gone four months, and during these four months there have been some terrible storms.”", "\"기다려, 기다려, 페르낭. 방금 네가 바다는 변덕스럽다고 했지. 그가 떠난 지 넉 달이 됐고, 그 넉 달 동안 무서운 폭풍이 몇 번이나 있었어.\""]], [["Fernand made no reply, nor did he attempt to check the tears which flowed down the cheeks of Mercédès, although for each of these tears he would have shed his heart’s blood; but these tears flowed for another.", "페르낭은 아무 대답도 하지 않았고, 메르세데스의 두 뺨을 타고 흐르는 눈물을 막으려 하지도 않았다. 그 눈물 한 방울 한 방울을 위해서라면 제 심장의 피라도 흘렸을 그였지만, 그 눈물은 다른 사람을 위한 것이었다."], ["He arose, paced a while up and down the hut, and then, suddenly stopping before Mercédès, with his eyes glowing and his hands clenched,—“Say, Mercédès,”", "그는 일어나 오두막 안을 잠시 서성이다가, 불현듯 메르세데스 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 두 눈은 이글거리고 주먹은 움켜쥔 채였다. \"말해 봐, 메르세데스.\""], ["he said, “once for all, is this your final determination?”", "그가 말했다. \"단 한 번만 묻겠어. 이게 네 마지막 결심이야?\""]], [["“I love Edmond Dantès,”", "\"난 에드몽 당테스를 사랑해.\""], ["the young girl calmly replied, “and none but Edmond shall ever be my husband.”", "처녀가 차분하게 대답했다. \"그리고 에드몽 말고는 그 누구도 내 남편이 될 수 없어.\""]], [["“And you will always love him?”", "\"그리고 언제까지나 그를 사랑할 거야?\""]], [["“As long as I live.”", "\"내가 살아 있는 한.\""]], [["Fernand let fall his head like a defeated man, heaved a sigh that was like a groan, and then suddenly looking her full in the face, with clenched teeth and expanded nostrils, said,—“But if he is dead——”", "페르낭은 패배한 사람처럼 고개를 떨구고는 신음 같은 한숨을 내쉬었다. 그러더니 갑자기 이를 악물고 콧구멍을 벌름거리며 그녀의 얼굴을 똑바로 쳐다보고 말했다. \"하지만 그가 죽었다면……\""]], [["“If he is dead, I shall die too.”", "\"그가 죽었다면, 나도 죽을 거야.\""]], [["“If he has forgotten you——”", "\"그가 너를 잊었다면……\""]], [["“Mercédès!”", "\"메르세데스!\""], ["called a joyous voice from without,—“Mercédès!”", "밖에서 명랑한 목소리가 불렀다. \"메르세데스!\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아.\""], ["exclaimed the young girl, blushing with delight, and fairly leaping in excess of love, “you see he has not forgotten me, for here he is!”", "처녀가 기쁨으로 얼굴을 붉히며, 넘쳐흐르는 사랑으로 펄쩍 뛰며 외쳤다. \"봤지, 그는 나를 잊지 않았어. 저기 그가 왔잖아!\""], ["And rushing towards the door, she opened it, saying, “Here, Edmond, here I am!”", "그러고는 문으로 달려가 문을 열며 말했다. \"여기, 에드몽, 나 여기 있어!\""]], [["Fernand, pale and trembling, drew back, like a traveller at the sight of a serpent, and fell into a chair beside him.", "페르낭은 뱀을 본 나그네처럼 창백하게 떨며 뒷걸음질 치다가 곁에 있던 의자에 주저앉았다."], ["Edmond and Mercédès were clasped in each other’s arms.", "에드몽과 메르세데스는 서로의 품에 안겼다."], ["The burning Marseilles sun, which shot into the room through the open door, covered them with a flood of light.", "열린 문으로 쏟아져 들어온 마르세유의 타는 듯한 햇살이 두 사람을 빛의 홍수로 뒤덮었다."], ["At first they saw nothing around them.", "처음에 그들은 주위의 아무것도 보지 못했다."], ["Their intense happiness isolated them from all the rest of the world, and they only spoke in broken words, which are the tokens of a joy so extreme that they seem rather the expression of sorrow.", "벅찬 행복이 그들을 세상 모든 것에서 떼어 놓았고, 두 사람은 끊긴 말로만 이야기를 나누었다. 그것은 기쁨이 너무도 지극할 때 나오는 표시여서, 오히려 슬픔을 드러내는 것처럼 보였다."], ["Suddenly Edmond saw the gloomy, pale, and threatening countenance of Fernand, as it was defined in the shadow.", "그때 문득 에드몽은 그늘 속에 윤곽을 드러낸 페르낭의 어둡고 창백하며 위협적인 얼굴을 보았다."], ["By a movement for which he could scarcely account to himself, the young Catalan placed his hand on the knife at his belt.", "젊은 카탈루냐 사내는 스스로도 거의 까닭을 알 수 없는 동작으로 허리띠에 찬 칼에 손을 얹었다."]], [["“Ah, your pardon,”", "\"아, 미안합니다.\""], ["said Dantès, frowning in his turn; “I did not perceive that there were three of us.”", "당테스가 이번에는 그가 눈살을 찌푸리며 말했다. \"여기 우리 셋이 있는 줄은 미처 몰랐군요.\""], ["Then, turning to Mercédès, he inquired, “Who is this gentleman?”", "그러고는 메르세데스 쪽으로 돌아서서 물었다. \"이분은 누구지?\""]], [["“One who will be your best friend, Dantès, for he is my friend, my cousin, my brother; it is Fernand—the man whom, after you, Edmond, I love the best in the world.", "\"당신의 가장 좋은 벗이 될 사람이에요, 당테스. 내 친구이자 사촌이고 오빠니까요. 페르낭이에요."], ["Do you not remember him?”", "에드몽, 당신 다음으로 내가 이 세상에서 가장 사랑하는 사람이죠. 기억 안 나세요?\""]], [["“Yes!”", "\"기억나지!\""], ["said Dantès, and without relinquishing Mercédès’ hand clasped in one of his own, he extended the other to the Catalan with a cordial air.", "당테스가 말했다. 그는 한 손에 메르세데스의 손을 꼭 쥔 채, 다른 손을 다정하게 카탈루냐 사내에게 내밀었다."], ["But Fernand, instead of responding to this amiable gesture, remained mute and trembling.", "그러나 페르낭은 그 정겨운 몸짓에 응하기는커녕, 입을 다문 채 떨고만 있었다."], ["Edmond then cast his eyes scrutinizingly at the agitated and embarrassed Mercédès, and then again on the gloomy and menacing Fernand.", "에드몽은 어쩔 줄 몰라 당황하는 메르세데스에게, 그리고 다시 어둡고 위협적인 페르낭에게 살피는 듯한 눈길을 던졌다."], ["This look told him all, and his anger waxed hot.", "그 눈길로 그는 모든 것을 알아차렸고, 분노가 끓어올랐다."]], [["“I did not know, when I came with such haste to you, that I was to meet an enemy here.”", "\"이렇게 한달음에 당신에게 달려오면서, 여기서 적을 만나리라고는 생각도 못 했군요.\""]], [["“An enemy!”", "\"적이라뇨!\""], ["cried Mercédès, with an angry look at her cousin.", "메르세데스가 사촌을 향해 성난 눈길을 던지며 외쳤다."], ["“An enemy in my house, do you say, Edmond!", "\"내 집에 적이 있다니, 그게 무슨 말이에요, 에드몽!"], ["If I believed that, I would place my arm under yours and go with you to Marseilles, leaving the house to return to it no more.”", "만약 그렇게 믿었다면, 난 당신 팔에 내 팔을 끼고 마르세유로 떠나, 이 집에 다시는 돌아오지 않았을 거예요.\""]], [["Fernand’s eye darted lightning.", "페르낭의 눈에서 번갯불이 일었다."], ["“And should any misfortune occur to you, dear Edmond,”", "\"그리고 당신에게 무슨 불행한 일이라도 닥친다면, 사랑하는 에드몽.\""], ["she continued with the same calmness which proved to Fernand that the young girl had read the very innermost depths of his sinister thought, “if misfortune should occur to you, I would ascend the highest point of the Cape de Morgiou and cast myself headlong from it.”", "그녀는 조금 전과 같은 차분함으로 말을 이었는데, 그 차분함은 처녀가 페르낭의 음흉한 생각의 가장 깊은 속내까지 읽어 냈다는 것을 그에게 증명해 보였다. \"당신에게 불행한 일이 닥친다면, 난 모르지우곶의 가장 높은 곳에 올라가 거기서 곤두박질쳐 몸을 던지겠어요.\""]], [["Fernand became deadly pale.", "페르낭의 얼굴이 죽은 듯이 창백해졌다."], ["“But you are deceived, Edmond,”", "\"하지만 당신이 잘못 봤어요, 에드몽.\""], ["she continued.", "그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["“You have no enemy here—there is no one but Fernand, my brother, who will grasp your hand as a devoted friend.”", "\"여기 당신의 적은 없어요. 오직 내 오빠 페르낭뿐이고, 그는 헌신적인 벗으로서 당신 손을 잡아 줄 거예요.\""]], [["And at these words the young girl fixed her imperious look on the Catalan, who, as if fascinated by it, came slowly towards Edmond, and offered him his hand.", "이렇게 말하며 처녀는 카탈루냐 사내에게 위엄 어린 눈길을 고정시켰다. 그는 그 눈길에 홀린 듯, 천천히 에드몽에게 다가가 손을 내밀었다."], ["His hatred, like a powerless though furious wave, was broken against the strong ascendancy which Mercédès exercised over him.", "그의 증오는, 사납기는 해도 힘없는 파도처럼, 메르세데스가 그에게 행사하는 강한 지배력에 부딪혀 부서졌다."], ["Scarcely, however, had he touched Edmond’s hand when he felt he had done all he could do, and rushed hastily out of the house.", "그러나 에드몽의 손에 닿기가 무섭게, 그는 자기가 할 수 있는 일은 다 했다고 느끼고는, 황급히 집 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔다."]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오.\""], ["he exclaimed, running furiously and tearing his hair—“Oh, who will deliver me from this man?", "그가 미친 듯이 달리며 머리를 쥐어뜯고 외쳤다. \"오, 누가 나를 저 사내한테서 구해 줄까?"], ["Wretched—wretched that I am!”", "비참해, 난 정말 비참해!\""]], [["“Hallo, Catalan!", "\"이봐, 카탈루냐 사람!"], ["Hallo, Fernand!", "이봐, 페르낭!"], ["where are you running to?”", "어디로 그렇게 달려가나?\""], ["exclaimed a voice.", "한 목소리가 외쳤다."]], [["The young man stopped suddenly, looked around him, and perceived Caderousse sitting at table with Danglars, under an arbor.", "청년은 우뚝 멈춰 서서 주위를 둘러보았고, 정자 아래 탁자에 당글라르와 함께 앉아 있는 카드루스를 보았다."]], [["“Well”, said Caderousse, “why don’t you come?", "\"이봐.\" 카드루스가 말했다."], ["Are you really in such a hurry that you have no time to pass the time of day with your friends?”", "\"왜 안 오나? 자네 정말 그렇게 바빠서 친구들하고 잠깐 인사 나눌 틈도 없나?\""]], [["“Particularly when they have still a full bottle before them,”", "\"더구나 친구들 앞에 술이 가득 든 병이 아직 남아 있는데 말이야.\""], ["added Danglars.", "당글라르가 덧붙였다."], ["Fernand looked at them both with a stupefied air, but did not say a word.", "페르낭은 멍한 얼굴로 두 사람을 바라보았지만, 한마디도 하지 않았다."]], [["“He seems besotted,”", "\"넋이 나간 것 같군.\""], ["said Danglars, pushing Caderousse with his knee.", "당글라르가 무릎으로 카드루스를 쿡 찌르며 말했다."], ["“Are we mistaken, and is Dantès triumphant in spite of all we have believed?”", "\"우리가 잘못 짚은 건가, 우리가 그렇게 믿었는데도 당테스가 결국 이긴 건가?\""]], [["“Why, we must inquire into that,”", "\"그야 알아봐야지.\""], ["was Caderousse’s reply; and turning towards the young man, said, “Well, Catalan, can’t you make up your mind?”", "카드루스가 대답하고는 청년 쪽으로 돌아서서 말했다. \"이봐, 카탈루냐 사람, 마음을 못 정하겠나?\""]], [["Fernand wiped away the perspiration steaming from his brow, and slowly entered the arbor, whose shade seemed to restore somewhat of calmness to his senses, and whose coolness somewhat of refreshment to his exhausted body.", "페르낭은 이마에 김처럼 솟아오른 땀을 닦고는, 천천히 정자 안으로 들어섰다. 그늘이 다소나마 그의 정신에 평온함을 되돌려 주는 듯했고, 그 서늘함이 지친 몸에 얼마간 생기를 불어넣어 주는 듯했다."]], [["“Good-day,” said he.", "\"안녕들 하신가.\""], ["“You called me, didn’t you?”", "그가 말했다. \"나를 불렀지?\""], ["And he fell, rather than sat down, on one of the seats which surrounded the table.", "그러고는 탁자를 둘러싼 의자 중 하나에 앉았다기보다 쓰러지듯 주저앉았다."]], [["“I called you because you were running like a madman, and I was afraid you would throw yourself into the sea,”", "\"자네가 미친 사람처럼 달려가기에 불렀지, 바다에라도 뛰어들까 봐 겁이 났거든.\""], ["said Caderousse, laughing.", "카드루스가 웃으며 말했다."], ["“Why, when a man has friends, they are not only to offer him a glass of wine, but, moreover, to prevent his swallowing three or four pints of water unnecessarily!”", "\"이봐, 친구가 있다는 건 술 한잔 권하는 것뿐만 아니라, 쓸데없이 물을 서너 됫박씩 들이켜는 걸 막아 주기 위한 것이기도 하다고!\""]], [["Fernand gave a groan, which resembled a sob, and dropped his head into his hands, his elbows leaning on the table.", "페르낭은 흐느낌 같은 신음을 내뱉고는, 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 두 손에 얼굴을 묻었다."]], [["“Well, Fernand, I must say,”", "\"이봐, 페르낭, 내 말해야겠는데.\""], ["said Caderousse, beginning the conversation, with that brutality of the common people in which curiosity destroys all diplomacy, “you look uncommonly like a rejected lover;”", "카드루스가 말문을 열었다. 호기심이 모든 외교적 수완을 망쳐 버리는, 서민 특유의 그 거친 말투였다. \"자네 영락없이 차인 연인 꼴이로군.\""], ["and he burst into a hoarse laugh.", "그러고는 쉰 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“Bah!”", "\"쳇!\""], ["said Danglars, “a lad of his make was not born to be unhappy in love.", "당글라르가 말했다. \"저런 체격을 타고난 녀석이 사랑 때문에 불행해질 리가 있나."], ["You are laughing at him, Caderousse.”", "자네 저 친구를 놀리는 거지, 카드루스.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니야.\""], ["he replied, “only hark how he sighs!", "그가 대답했다. \"저 한숨 소리나 좀 들어 보라고!"], ["Come, come, Fernand,”", "이봐, 이봐, 페르낭.\""], ["said Caderousse, “hold up your head, and answer us.", "카드루스가 말했다. \"고개를 들고 대답 좀 해 봐."], ["It’s not polite not to reply to friends who ask news of your health.”", "친구들이 안부를 묻는데 대꾸도 안 하는 건 예의가 아니지.\""]], [["“My health is well enough,”", "\"내 안부는 괜찮아.\""], ["said Fernand, clenching his hands without raising his head.", "페르낭이 고개도 들지 않고 두 손을 움켜쥐며 말했다."]], [["“Ah, you see, Danglars,” said Caderousse, winking at his friend, “this is how it is; Fernand, whom you see here, is a good and brave Catalan, one of the best fishermen in Marseilles, and he is in love with a very fine girl, named Mercédès; but it appears, unfortunately, that the fine girl is in love with the mate of the _Pharaon_; and as the _Pharaon_ arrived today—why, you understand!”", "\"아, 이거 봐, 당글라르.\" 카드루스가 친구에게 눈짓하며 말했다. \"사정이 이렇다네. 여기 이 페르낭은 착하고 용감한 카탈루냐 사람이고, 마르세유 최고의 어부 중 하나야. 그런데 메르세데스라는 아주 어여쁜 처녀에게 반했지. 그런데 불행히도 그 어여쁜 처녀는 파라옹호의 항해사한테 반한 모양이야. 게다가 파라옹호가 오늘 들어왔으니, 그래, 자네도 알 만하지!\""]], [["“No; I do not understand,” said Danglars.", "\"아니, 모르겠는데.\" 당글라르가 말했다."]], [["“Poor Fernand has been dismissed,”", "\"가엾은 페르낭이 차인 거지.\""], ["continued Caderousse.", "카드루스가 말을 이었다."]], [["“Well, and what then?”", "\"그래서, 그게 어쨌단 말인가?\""], ["said Fernand, lifting up his head, and looking at Caderousse like a man who looks for someone on whom to vent his anger; “Mercédès is not accountable to any person, is she?", "페르낭이 고개를 들고, 분풀이할 상대를 찾는 사람처럼 카드루스를 노려보며 말했다. \"메르세데스는 누구한테도 해명할 의무가 없어, 안 그래?"], ["Is she not free to love whomsoever she will?”", "그녀가 누구를 사랑하든 자유 아닌가?\""]], [["“Oh, if you take it in that sense,”", "\"오, 그런 식으로 받아들인다면야.\""], ["said Caderousse, “it is another thing.", "카드루스가 말했다. \"얘기가 다르지."], ["But I thought you were a Catalan, and they told me the Catalans were not men to allow themselves to be supplanted by a rival.", "하지만 난 자네가 카탈루냐 사람인 줄 알았는데. 카탈루냐 사람들은 연적한테 밀려나는 걸 가만히 두고 보는 위인들이 아니라고들 하던데."], ["It was even told me that Fernand, especially, was terrible in his vengeance.”", "특히 페르낭, 자네는 복수가 무섭기로 소문났다고 들었거든.\""]], [["Fernand smiled piteously. “A lover is never terrible,”", "페르낭이 가련하게 웃었다. \"사랑에 빠진 사람은 결코 무섭지 않은 법이지.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Poor fellow!” remarked Danglars, affecting to pity the young man from the bottom of his heart.", "\"가엾은 친구!\" 당글라르가 진심으로 청년을 동정하는 체하며 말했다."], ["“Why, you see, he did not expect to see Dantès return so suddenly—he thought he was dead, perhaps; or perchance faithless!", "\"이거 봐, 저 친구는 당테스가 이렇게 갑자기 돌아올 줄은 몰랐던 거야. 아마 죽은 줄 알았겠지. 아니면 마음이 변했거나!"], ["These things always come on us more severely when they come suddenly.”", "이런 일은 갑자기 닥칠수록 더 모질게 느껴지는 법이거든.\""]], [["“Ah, _ma foi_, under any circumstances!”", "\"아, 정말이지, 어떤 경우라도 말이야!\""], ["said Caderousse, who drank as he spoke, and on whom the fumes of the wine began to take effect,—“under any circumstances Fernand is not the only person put out by the fortunate arrival of Dantès; is he, Danglars?”", "카드루스가 말하면서 술을 들이켰다. 술기운이 슬슬 오르기 시작한 참이었다. \"어떤 경우라도, 당테스가 운 좋게 돌아온 탓에 곤란해진 사람이 페르낭 혼자만은 아니지, 안 그런가, 당글라르?\""]], [["“No, you are right—and I should say that would bring him ill-luck.”", "\"그래, 자네 말이 맞아. 내 보기엔 그게 그자한테 불운을 가져다줄 것 같은데.\""]], [["“Well, never mind,”", "\"뭐, 아무래도 좋아.\""], ["answered Caderousse, pouring out a glass of wine for Fernand, and filling his own for the eighth or ninth time, while Danglars had merely sipped his.", "카드루스가 페르낭에게 술을 한 잔 따라 주고, 제 잔도 여덟 번째인지 아홉 번째인지 채우며 대답했다. 당글라르는 제 잔을 찔끔 입에 댔을 뿐이었다. \"아무래도 좋아."], ["“Never mind—in the meantime he marries Mercédès—the lovely Mercédès—at least he returns to do that.”", "어쨌든 그자는 메르세데스와, 그 어여쁜 메르세데스와 결혼할 테니까. 적어도 그러려고 돌아온 거지.\""]], [["During this time Danglars fixed his piercing glance on the young man, on whose heart Caderousse’s words fell like molten lead.", "그동안 당글라르는 청년에게 날카로운 눈길을 고정하고 있었다. 카드루스의 말은 청년의 가슴에 녹아내린 납처럼 떨어졌다."]], [["“And when is the wedding to be?”", "\"그래, 결혼은 언제 한답니까?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“Oh, it is not yet fixed!”", "\"오, 아직 정해지지 않았어요!\""], ["murmured Fernand.", "페르낭이 중얼거렸다."]], [["“No, but it will be,”", "\"아니, 곧 정해지지.\""], ["said Caderousse, “as surely as Dantès will be captain of the _Pharaon_—eh, Danglars?”", "카드루스가 말했다. \"당테스가 파라옹호의 선장이 될 게 틀림없는 것만큼이나 확실하게 말이야, 안 그런가, 당글라르?\""]], [["Danglars shuddered at this unexpected attack, and turned to Caderousse, whose countenance he scrutinized, to try and detect whether the blow was premeditated; but he read nothing but envy in a countenance already rendered brutal and stupid by drunkenness.", "당글라르는 이 뜻밖의 일격에 흠칫 놀라, 카드루스 쪽으로 돌아서서 그 얼굴을 살피며 그 한 방이 미리 계산된 것인지 알아내려 했다. 그러나 이미 취기로 둔하고 멍청해진 얼굴에서 시샘 말고는 아무것도 읽어 내지 못했다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"좋아.\""], ["said he, filling the glasses, “let us drink to Captain Edmond Dantès, husband of the beautiful Catalane!”", "그가 잔을 채우며 말했다. \"아름다운 카탈루냐 처녀의 남편, 에드몽 당테스 선장을 위하여 마시세!\""]], [["Caderousse raised his glass to his mouth with unsteady hand, and swallowed the contents at a gulp. Fernand dashed his on the ground.", "카드루스가 흔들리는 손으로 잔을 입에 가져가 단숨에 비웠다. 페르낭은 자기 잔을 땅바닥에 내동댕이쳤다."]], [["“Eh, eh, eh!”", "\"어, 어, 어!\""], ["stammered Caderousse.", "카드루스가 더듬거렸다."], ["“What do I see down there by the wall, in the direction of the Catalans?", "\"저기 저 벽 옆, 카탈루냐 마을 쪽에 보이는 게 뭐지?"], ["Look, Fernand, your eyes are better than mine.", "봐, 페르낭, 자네 눈이 내 눈보다 낫잖아."], ["I believe I see double.", "내 눈엔 둘로 보이는데."], ["You know wine is a deceiver; but I should say it was two lovers walking side by side, and hand in hand.", "술이 사람을 속이는 건 알지만, 내 보기엔 연인 두 사람이 나란히, 손을 맞잡고 걸어오는 것 같단 말이야."], ["Heaven forgive me, they do not know that we can see them, and they are actually embracing!”", "맙소사, 우리가 보고 있는 줄도 모르고, 저것들이 정말 끌어안고 있잖아!\""]], [["Danglars did not lose one pang that Fernand endured.", "당글라르는 페르낭이 겪는 괴로움을 하나도 놓치지 않았다."]], [["“Do you know them, Fernand?” he said.", "\"자네 저들을 아나, 페르낭?\" 그가 말했다."]], [["“Yes,” was the reply, in a low voice.", "\"알지.\" 낮은 목소리로 대답이 돌아왔다."], ["“It is Edmond and Mercédès!”", "\"에드몽과 메르세데스야!\""]], [["“Ah, see there, now!”", "\"아, 저것 봐, 정말!\""], ["said Caderousse; “and I did not recognize them!", "카드루스가 말했다. \"난 못 알아봤네!"], ["Hallo, Dantès!", "이봐, 당테스!"], ["hello, lovely damsel!", "이봐, 어여쁜 아가씨!"], ["Come this way, and let us know when the wedding is to be, for Fernand here is so obstinate he will not tell us.”", "이리로 와서, 결혼식이 언제인지 좀 알려 주게. 여기 이 페르낭이 어찌나 고집을 부리는지 도통 말을 안 해 주니 말이야.\""]], [["“Hold your tongue, will you?”", "\"입 좀 다물게, 응?\""], ["said Danglars, pretending to restrain Caderousse, who, with the tenacity of drunkards, leaned out of the arbor.", "당글라르가 카드루스를 말리는 체하며 말했다. 카드루스는 술꾼 특유의 끈질김으로 정자 밖으로 몸을 내밀고 있었다."], ["“Try to stand upright, and let the lovers make love without interruption.", "\"똑바로 좀 서 보게, 그리고 저 연인들이 방해받지 않고 사랑을 속삭이게 내버려 둬."], ["See, look at Fernand, and follow his example; he is well-behaved!”", "봐, 페르낭을 보고 본받으라고. 저 친구는 점잖게 굴잖아!\""]], [["0051m\nFernand, probably excited beyond bearing, pricked by Danglars, as the bull is by the bandilleros, was about to rush out; for he had risen from his seat, and seemed to be collecting himself to dash headlong upon his rival, when Mercédès, smiling and graceful, lifted up her lovely head, and looked at them with her clear and bright eyes.", "페르낭은, 투우사의 작살에 찔린 황소처럼 당글라르에게 들쑤셔진 탓에 아마도 참을 수 없을 만큼 격해진 듯, 막 뛰쳐나가려던 참이었다. 자리에서 일어나, 마음을 다잡고 연적에게 곤두박질쳐 달려들 기세였다. 바로 그때 메르세데스가 미소 띤 우아한 얼굴로 어여쁜 고개를 들어, 맑고 빛나는 눈으로 그들을 바라보았다."], ["At this Fernand recollected her threat of dying if Edmond died, and dropped again heavily on his seat.", "그 순간 페르낭은 에드몽이 죽으면 자기도 죽겠다던 그녀의 위협을 떠올리고는, 다시 의자에 털썩 주저앉았다."], ["Danglars looked at the two men, one after the other, the one brutalized by liquor, the other overwhelmed with love.", "당글라르는 두 사내를, 술에 절어 망가진 자와 사랑에 짓눌린 자를 차례로 번갈아 바라보았다."]], [["“I shall get nothing from these fools,”", "'이 멍청이들한테서는 아무것도 얻어 낼 게 없겠군.'"], ["he muttered; “and I am very much afraid of being here between a drunkard and a coward.", "그는 중얼거렸다. '술주정뱅이와 겁쟁이 사이에 끼어 있는 게 영 마음에 안 들어."], ["Here’s an envious fellow making himself boozy on wine when he ought to be nursing his wrath, and here is a fool who sees the woman he loves stolen from under his nose and takes on like a big baby.", "분노를 곱씹어야 할 판에 시샘이나 하며 술에 취하는 녀석이 하나 있고, 제가 사랑하는 여자를 코앞에서 빼앗기고도 큰 어린애처럼 구는 멍청이가 하나 있으니."], ["Yet this Catalan has eyes that glisten like those of the vengeful Spaniards, Sicilians, and Calabrians, and the other has fists big enough to crush an ox at one blow.", "그래도 이 카탈루냐 녀석은 복수심에 불타는 에스파냐인이나 시칠리아인, 칼라브리아인처럼 번득이는 눈을 가졌고, 저쪽 녀석은 황소를 한 방에 때려눕힐 만한 주먹을 가졌단 말이지. 의심할 여지 없이 에드몽의 별이 떠오르고 있어."], ["Unquestionably, Edmond’s star is in the ascendant, and he will marry the splendid girl—he will be captain, too, and laugh at us all, unless”—a sinister smile passed over Danglars’ lips—“unless I take a hand in the affair,”", "저 멋진 처녀와 결혼하고, 선장도 될 테고, 우리 모두를 비웃겠지. 만일—' 음흉한 미소가 당글라르의 입가를 스쳤다. '만일 내가 이 일에 한 손 거들지 않는다면 말이지.'"], ["he added.", "그가 덧붙였다."]], [["“Hallo!”", "\"이봐!\""], ["continued Caderousse, half-rising, and with his fist on the table, “hallo, Edmond!", "카드루스가 탁자에 주먹을 짚고 반쯤 몸을 일으키며 계속 외쳤다. \"이봐, 에드몽!"], ["do you not see your friends, or are you too proud to speak to them?”", "친구들이 안 보이나, 아니면 너무 잘나서 우리한테는 말도 안 거는 건가?\""]], [["“No, my dear fellow!”", "\"아니야, 친구!\""], ["replied Dantès, “I am not proud, but I am happy, and happiness blinds, I think, more than pride.”", "당테스가 대답했다. \"잘나서가 아니라 행복해서 그래. 행복은 자만보다 더 사람의 눈을 멀게 하는 모양이야.\""]], [["“Ah, very well, that’s an explanation!”", "\"아, 그렇군, 그거 말 되는 설명이로군!\""], ["said Caderousse.", "카드루스가 말했다."], ["“How do you do, Madame Dantès?”", "\"안녕하시오, 당테스 부인?\""]], [["Mercédès courtesied gravely, and said—“That is not my name, and in my country it bodes ill fortune, they say, to call a young girl by the name of her betrothed before he becomes her husband.", "메르세데스가 점잖게 인사하고 말했다. \"그건 제 이름이 아니에요. 우리 고장에서는 처녀를 정혼자가 남편이 되기도 전에 그의 이름으로 부르면 불행을 부른다고들 해요."], ["So call me Mercédès, if you please.”", "그러니 부디 메르세데스라고 불러 주세요.\""]], [["“We must excuse our worthy neighbor, Caderousse,”", "\"이 사람 좋은 우리 이웃을 너그러이 봐줘야겠어, 메르세데스.\""], ["said Dantès, “he is so easily mistaken.”", "당테스가 말했다. \"워낙 실수가 잦은 사람이라.\""]], [["“So, then, the wedding is to take place immediately, M.", "\"그렇다면, 당테스 씨, 결혼식을 곧 올리시겠군요.\""], ["Dantès,” said Danglars, bowing to the young couple.", "당글라르가 젊은 연인에게 고개를 숙이며 말했다."]], [["“As soon as possible, M.", "\"되도록 빨리요, 당글라르 씨."], ["Danglars; today all preliminaries will be arranged at my father’s, and tomorrow, or next day at latest, the wedding festival here at La Réserve.", "오늘 아버지 댁에서 모든 준비를 마치고, 내일이나 늦어도 모레는 여기 라레제르브에서 결혼 잔치를 열 겁니다. 제 친구들도 와 주시면 좋겠습니다."], ["My friends will be there, I hope; that is to say, you are invited, M. Danglars, and you, Caderousse.”", "그러니까, 당글라르 씨, 당신도 초대하는 거고, 카드루스, 자네도 초대하는 거야.\""]], [["“And Fernand,”", "\"그리고 페르낭도.\""], ["said Caderousse with a chuckle; “Fernand, too, is invited!”", "카드루스가 킬킬거리며 말했다. \"페르낭도 초대받은 거지!\""]], [["“My wife’s brother is my brother,”", "\"제 아내의 오빠는 곧 제 형제죠.\""], ["said Edmond; “and we, Mercédès and I, should be very sorry if he were absent at such a time.”", "에드몽이 말했다. \"메르세데스도 저도, 이런 날 그가 빠진다면 무척 서운할 겁니다.\""]], [["Fernand opened his mouth to reply, but his voice died on his lips, and he could not utter a word.", "페르낭은 대꾸하려고 입을 열었지만, 목소리가 입술에서 잦아들어 한마디도 내뱉을 수 없었다."]], [["“Today the preliminaries, tomorrow or next day the ceremony! You are in a hurry, captain!”", "\"오늘 준비를 하고, 내일이나 모레 예식이라! 선장님, 어지간히 서두르시는군요!\""]], [["“Danglars,”", "\"당글라르.\" 에드몽이 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["said Edmond, smiling, “I will say to you as Mercédès said just now to Caderousse, ‘Do not give me a title which does not belong to me’; that may bring me bad luck.”", "\"방금 메르세데스가 카드루스에게 한 말을 당신에게도 그대로 해야겠군요. '내게 어울리지 않는 호칭은 붙이지 말아 주세요.' 그러다 불운을 부를 수도 있으니까요.\""]], [["“Your pardon,”", "\"이거 실례했습니다.\" 당글라르가 대답했다."], ["replied Danglars, “I merely said you seemed in a hurry, and we have lots of time; the _Pharaon_ cannot be under weigh again in less than three months.”", "\"그저 당신이 서두르는 것 같다고 했을 뿐인데요. 시간이야 얼마든지 있지요. 파라옹호는 석 달이 지나야 다시 출항할 수 있을 테니까요.\""]], [["“We are always in a hurry to be happy, M. Danglars; for when we have suffered a long time, we have great difficulty in believing in good fortune.", "\"행복을 누리는 일에는 늘 마음이 급한 법이지요, 당글라르 씨. 오랫동안 고생을 한 사람은 행운을 좀처럼 믿기 어려운 법이니까요."], ["But it is not selfishness alone that makes me thus in haste; I must go to Paris.”", "하지만 제가 이렇게 서두르는 게 이기심 때문만은 아닙니다. 파리에 다녀와야 하거든요.\""]], [["“Ah, really?—to Paris! and will it be the first time you have ever been there, Dantès?”", "\"아, 정말요? 파리에요! 거기 가는 게 처음이오, 당테스?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Have you business there?”", "\"거기 무슨 볼일이 있소?\""]], [["“Not of my own; the last commission of poor Captain Leclere; you know to what I allude, Danglars—it is sacred.", "\"제 일은 아닙니다. 가엾은 르클레르 선장님의 마지막 부탁이지요. 무슨 말인지 아시겠지요, 당글라르."], ["Besides, I shall only take the time to go and return.”", "그건 신성한 일입니다. 게다가 다녀올 만큼의 시간만 쓸 겁니다.\""]], [["“Yes, yes, I understand,”", "\"그래, 그래, 알겠소.\""], ["said Danglars, and then in a low tone, he added, “To Paris, no doubt to deliver the letter which the grand marshal gave him.", "당글라르가 말했다. 그러고는 낮은 목소리로 덧붙였다. '파리라, 틀림없이 대원수가 맡긴 그 편지를 전하러 가는 거겠지."], ["Ah, this letter gives me an idea—a capital idea!", "아, 이 편지가 한 가지 생각을 떠오르게 하는군."], ["Ah; Dantès, my friend, you are not yet registered number one on board the good ship _Pharaon_;”", "기막힌 생각이야! 아, 당테스, 이 친구야, 자네는 아직 파라옹호의 일등 선원으로 등록되지 않았어.'"], ["then turning towards Edmond, who was walking away, “A pleasant journey,” he cried.", "그러고는 멀어져 가는 에드몽 쪽으로 돌아서서 외쳤다. \"즐거운 여행 되시오!\""]], [["“Thank you,”", "\"고맙소.\""], ["said Edmond with a friendly nod, and the two lovers continued on their way, as calm and joyous as if they were the very elect of heaven.", "에드몽이 다정하게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. 그리고 두 연인은 마치 하늘이 특별히 점지한 사람들처럼 평온하고 즐겁게 가던 길을 계속 갔다."]], [["# Chapter 4. Conspiracy", "# Chapter 4. Conspiracy"]], [["Danglars followed Edmond and Mercédès with his eyes until the two lovers disappeared behind one of the angles of Fort Saint Nicolas; then, turning round, he perceived Fernand, who had fallen, pale and trembling, into his chair, while Caderousse stammered out the words of a drinking-song.", "당글라르는 에드몽과 메르세데스가 생니콜라 요새의 한 모퉁이 뒤로 사라질 때까지 두 연인을 눈으로 좇았다. 그러고는 몸을 돌려, 의자에 푹 주저앉아 창백하게 떨고 있는 페르낭과, 술 노래 가사를 더듬더듬 흥얼대는 카드루스를 보았다."]], [["“Well, my dear sir,”", "\"이보게,\""], ["said Danglars to Fernand, “here is a marriage which does not appear to make everybody happy.”", "당글라르가 페르낭에게 말했다. \"이 결혼이 모두를 행복하게 해 주는 건 아닌 모양이군.\""]], [["“It drives me to despair,” said Fernand.", "\"미칠 것 같습니다.\" 페르낭이 말했다."]], [["“Do you, then, love Mercédès?”", "\"그럼 자네, 메르세데스를 사랑하나?\""]], [["“I adore her!”", "\"흠모합니다!\""]], [["“For long?”", "\"오래됐나?\""]], [["“As long as I have known her—always.”", "\"그녀를 안 뒤로 줄곧, 늘 그랬습니다.\""]], [["“And you sit there, tearing your hair, instead of seeking to remedy your condition; I did not think that was the way of your people.”", "\"한데 자넨 처지를 바로잡을 궁리는 않고, 거기 앉아 머리만 쥐어뜯고 있군. 자네 같은 사람들이 그런 줄은 몰랐는데.\""]], [["“What would you have me do?”", "\"그럼 어쩌란 말입니까?\""], ["said Fernand.", "페르낭이 말했다."]], [["“How do I know?", "\"내가 그걸 어찌 알겠나?"], ["Is it my affair?", "그게 내 일인가?"], ["I am not in love with Mademoiselle Mercédès; but for you—in the words of the gospel, seek, and you shall find.”", "난 메르세데스 양을 사랑하지 않아. 하지만 자넨 다르지. 복음서에도 있잖나, 찾으라, 그러면 찾을 것이다.\""]], [["“I have found already.”", "\"이미 찾았습니다.\""]], [["“What?”", "\"무얼?\""]], [["“I would stab the man, but the woman told me that if any misfortune happened to her betrothed, she would kill herself.”", "\"그 사내를 칼로 찔러 버리고 싶지만, 그녀는 약혼자에게 무슨 변고라도 생기면 스스로 목숨을 끊겠다고 했습니다.\""]], [["“Pooh! Women say those things, but never do them.”", "\"흥! 여자들이야 다들 그런 말을 하지만, 정작 실행에 옮기진 않아.\""]], [["“You do not know Mercédès; what she threatens she will do.”", "\"당신은 메르세데스를 모릅니다. 그녀는 한다고 하면 합니다.\""]], [["“Idiot!”", "\"멍청이 같으니.\""], ["muttered Danglars; “whether she kill herself or not, what matter, provided Dantès is not captain?”", "당글라르가 중얼거렸다. '그녀가 죽든 안 죽든, 당테스가 선장만 안 되면 그만이지.'"]], [["“Before Mercédès should die,”", "\"메르세데스가 죽기 전에,\""], ["replied Fernand, with the accents of unshaken resolution, “I would die myself!”", "페르낭이 흔들림 없는 결의에 찬 목소리로 대꾸했다. \"제가 먼저 죽을 겁니다!\""]], [["“That’s what I call love!”", "\"그게 바로 내가 말하는 사랑이지!\""], ["said Caderousse with a voice more tipsy than ever.", "카드루스가 그 어느 때보다도 취한 목소리로 말했다."], ["“That’s love, or I don’t know what love is.”", "\"저게 사랑이야, 아니면 난 사랑이 뭔지도 모르는 거고.\""]], [["“Come,”", "\"이보게,\""], ["said Danglars, “you appear to me a good sort of fellow, and hang me, I should like to help you, but——”", "당글라르가 말했다. \"자넨 괜찮은 사람 같으니, 빌어먹을, 자넬 좀 도와주고 싶네만……\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래요,\""], ["said Caderousse, “but how?”", "카드루스가 말했다. \"근데 어떻게?\""]], [["“My dear fellow,”", "\"이보게,\""], ["replied Danglars, “you are three parts drunk; finish the bottle, and you will be completely so.", "당글라르가 대꾸했다. \"자넨 거의 다 취했어. 그 병을 비우면 아주 곤드레가 될 걸세."], ["Drink then, and do not meddle with what we are discussing, for that requires all one’s wit and cool judgment.”", "그러니 마시기나 하고, 우리가 의논하는 일에는 끼어들지 말게. 이건 머리도 맑아야 하고 냉정한 판단력도 필요한 일이거든.\""]], [["“I—drunk!” said Caderousse; “well that’s a good one!", "\"내가 취했다고!\" 카드루스가 말했다."], ["I could drink four more such bottles; they are no bigger than cologne flasks.", "\"이거 참 우습군! 이런 병이라면 네 병은 더 마실 수 있어. 오드콜로뉴 병만 한 것들인데 뭘."], ["Père Pamphile, more wine!”", "팡필 영감, 술 더 주쇼!\""]], [["And Caderousse rattled his glass upon the table.", "그러고는 카드루스가 잔을 식탁에 탁탁 두드렸다."]], [["“You were saying, sir——” said Fernand, awaiting with great anxiety the end of this interrupted remark.", "\"방금 무슨 말씀을 하시려던 거였죠……\" 페르낭이 끊긴 말의 끝을 몹시 애타게 기다리며 물었다."]], [["“What was I saying? I forget.", "\"내가 무슨 말을 했더라? 잊었군."], ["This drunken Caderousse has made me lose the thread of my sentence.”", "이 취한 카드루스 때문에 말의 흐름을 놓쳤어.\""]], [["“Drunk, if you like; so much the worse for those who fear wine, for it is because they have bad thoughts which they are afraid the liquor will extract from their hearts;”", "\"취했지, 그러시든가. 술을 두려워하는 자들이나 손해 보는 거야. 그건 마음속에 못된 생각을 품고 있어서, 술이 그걸 끄집어낼까 봐 무서운 거거든.\""], ["and Caderousse began to sing the two last lines of a song very popular at the time:", "카드루스는 그러더니 그 무렵 크게 유행하던 노래의 마지막 두 행을 부르기 시작했다."]], [["‘Tous les méchants sont buveurs d’eau;", "'못된 자들은 모두 물만 마신다네."]], [["C’est bien prouvé par le déluge.’1", "대홍수가 그걸 똑똑히 증명했지.'"]], [["“You said, sir, you would like to help me, but——”", "\"도와주고 싶지만, 하셨잖습니까……\""]], [["“Yes; but I added, to help you it would be sufficient that Dantès did not marry her you love; and the marriage may easily be thwarted, methinks, and yet Dantès need not die.”", "\"그래. 그런데 내가 덧붙이길, 자넬 도우려면 당테스가 자네 사랑하는 여인과 결혼만 못 하게 하면 충분하다고 했지. 내 생각엔 그 결혼은 쉽게 깨뜨릴 수 있어. 게다가 당테스가 꼭 죽을 필요도 없고 말이야.\""]], [["“Death alone can separate them,”", "\"죽음만이 둘을 갈라놓을 수 있습니다.\""], ["remarked Fernand.", "페르낭이 말했다."]], [["“You talk like a noodle, my friend,”", "\"자넨 얼간이처럼 말하는군, 친구.\""], ["said Caderousse; “and here is Danglars, who is a wide-awake, clever, deep fellow, who will prove to you that you are wrong.", "카드루스가 말했다. \"여기 당글라르는 눈치 빠르고 영리하고 속이 깊은 사람이야. 자네가 틀렸다는 걸 증명해 줄 거라고."], ["Prove it, Danglars.", "증명해 봐, 당글라르."], ["I have answered for you.", "내가 자네 대신 장담했으니."], ["Say there is no need why Dantès should die; it would, indeed, be a pity he should.", "당테스가 죽을 필요는 없다고 말해 봐. 그가 죽는다면 정말이지 안타까운 일이지."], ["Dantès is a good fellow; I like Dantès.", "당테스는 좋은 녀석이야. 난 당테스가 좋아."], ["Dantès, your health.”", "당테스, 자네 건강을 위하여.\""]], [["Fernand rose impatiently.", "페르낭이 조바심을 내며 일어섰다."], ["“Let him run on,”", "\"내버려 두게.\""], ["said Danglars, restraining the young man; “drunk as he is, he is not much out in what he says.", "당글라르가 젊은이를 붙들며 말했다. \"저렇게 취했어도, 하는 말이 그리 틀리진 않아."], ["Absence severs as well as death, and if the walls of a prison were between Edmond and Mercédès they would be as effectually separated as if he lay under a tombstone.” 0056m", "떨어져 있는 것도 죽음 못지않게 사람을 갈라놓지. 만약 에드몽과 메르세데스 사이에 감옥의 벽이 가로놓인다면, 그가 묘비 아래 누운 것이나 다름없이 둘은 떼어지는 거야.\""]], [["“Yes; but one gets out of prison,”", "\"그래요, 하지만 감옥에서는 나오기 마련이죠.\""], ["said Caderousse, who, with what sense was left him, listened eagerly to the conversation, “and when one gets out and one’s name is Edmond Dantès, one seeks revenge——”", "남은 정신을 그러모아 대화에 열심히 귀를 기울이던 카드루스가 말했다. \"그리고 일단 나오면, 그것도 이름이 에드몽 당테스라면, 복수를 하려 들 거고……\""]], [["“What matters that?”", "\"그게 무슨 상관이야?\""], ["muttered Fernand.", "페르낭이 중얼거렸다."]], [["“And why, I should like to know,”", "\"그리고 대체 왜,\""], ["persisted Caderousse, “should they put Dantès in prison?", "카드루스가 끈질기게 물었다. \"당테스를 감옥에 처넣어야 한단 말이야?"], ["he has neither robbed, nor killed, nor murdered.”", "그는 훔치지도, 죽이지도, 살해하지도 않았는데.\""]], [["“Hold your tongue!” said Danglars.", "\"입 다물어!\" 당글라르가 말했다."]], [["“I won’t hold my tongue!”", "\"입 안 다물 거야!\" 카드루스가 대꾸했다."], ["replied Caderousse; “I say I want to know why they should put Dantès in prison; I like Dantès; Dantès, your health!”", "\"난 왜 당테스를 감옥에 넣어야 하는지 알고 싶다니까. 난 당테스가 좋아. 당테스, 자네 건강을 위하여!\""], ["and he swallowed another glass of wine.", "그러고는 술 한 잔을 또 들이켰다."]], [["0057m\nDanglars saw in the muddled look of the tailor the progress of his intoxication, and turning towards Fernand, said, “Well, you understand there is no need to kill him.”", "당글라르는 양복장이의 흐리멍덩한 눈빛에서 취기가 깊어 가는 것을 보고, 페르낭 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다. \"자, 알겠지, 그를 죽일 필요는 없는 거야.\""]], [["“Certainly not, if, as you said just now, you have the means of having Dantès arrested.", "\"물론 죽일 필요야 없지요. 방금 말씀하신 대로, 당테스를 체포되게 만들 수단이 있다면 말입니다."], ["Have you that means?”", "그런 수단이 있습니까?\""]], [["“It is to be found for the searching. But why should I meddle in the matter?", "\"찾으면 나오는 법이지. 한데 내가 왜 이 일에 끼어들어야 하나?"], ["it is no affair of mine.”", "내 일도 아닌데.\""]], [["“I know not why you meddle,”", "\"왜 끼어드는지는 모르겠습니다.\" 페르낭이 그의 팔을 붙잡으며 말했다."], ["said Fernand, seizing his arm; “but this I know, you have some motive of personal hatred against Dantès, for he who himself hates is never mistaken in the sentiments of others.”", "\"하지만 이건 압니다. 당신은 당테스에게 개인적인 원한이 있어요. 스스로 미워하는 사람은 남의 감정도 결코 잘못 읽지 않는 법이니까요.\""]], [["“I! motives of hatred against Dantès?", "\"내가! 당테스에게 원한이라?"], ["None, on my word!", "천만에, 맹세코 없네!"], ["I saw you were unhappy, and your unhappiness interested me; that’s all; but since you believe I act for my own account, adieu, my dear friend, get out of the affair as best you may;”", "자네가 불행해 보였고, 그 불행이 내 관심을 끌었을 뿐이야. 그게 다라고. 한데 내가 내 잇속을 챙기려 그런다고 믿는다면, 그럼 잘 있게, 친구."], ["and Danglars rose as if he meant to depart.", "이 일은 자네 알아서 풀어 보게나.\" 그러고는 당글라르가 떠나려는 듯 일어섰다."]], [["“No, no,” said Fernand, restraining him, “stay!", "\"아니, 아닙니다.\" 페르낭이 그를 붙들며 말했다. \"계세요!"], ["It is of very little consequence to me at the end of the matter whether you have any angry feeling or not against Dantès. I hate him!", "따지고 보면, 당신이 당테스에게 악감정이 있든 없든 제겐 별로 중요하지 않습니다. 전 그자를 증오합니다!"], ["I confess it openly.", "드러내 놓고 고백하지요."], ["Do you find the means, I will execute it, provided it is not to kill the man, for Mercédès has declared she will kill herself if Dantès is killed.”", "당신이 수단을 찾아 주면 제가 실행하겠습니다. 다만 그를 죽이는 일만은 안 됩니다. 당테스가 죽으면 메르세데스가 스스로 목숨을 끊겠다고 했으니까요.\""]], [["Caderousse, who had let his head drop on the table, now raised it, and looking at Fernand with his dull and fishy eyes, he said, “Kill Dantès!", "식탁에 머리를 떨구고 있던 카드루스가 이제 고개를 들더니, 흐리멍덩하고 멍한 눈으로 페르낭을 바라보며 말했다. \"당테스를 죽인다고!"], ["who talks of killing Dantès?", "누가 당테스를 죽인단 말이야?"], ["I won’t have him killed—I won’t!", "난 그를 못 죽이게 할 거야, 안 돼!"], ["He’s my friend, and this morning offered to share his money with me, as I shared mine with him.", "그는 내 친구야. 오늘 아침에도 내가 그에게 그랬듯, 제 돈을 나랑 나누겠다고 했단 말이야."], ["I won’t have Dantès killed—I won’t!”", "난 당테스를 죽이게 두지 않아, 안 돼!\""]], [["“And who has said a word about killing him, muddlehead?”", "\"누가 그를 죽인다고 했나, 이 멍청아?\" 당글라르가 대꾸했다."], ["replied Danglars. “We were merely joking; drink to his health,”", "\"그냥 농담했던 거야. 그의 건강을 위해 마시기나 해.\""], ["he added, filling Caderousse’s glass, “and do not interfere with us.”", "그는 카드루스의 잔을 채우며 덧붙였다. \"그리고 우리 일에 끼어들지 마.\""]], [["“Yes, yes, Dantès’ good health!”", "\"그래, 그래, 당테스의 건강을 위하여!\""], ["said Caderousse, emptying his glass, “here’s to his health!", "카드루스가 잔을 비우며 말했다. \"그의 건강을 위하여!"], ["his health—hurrah!”", "그의 건강을, 만세!\""]], [["“But the means—the means?”", "\"한데 수단은, 그 수단 말입니다?\""], ["said Fernand.", "페르낭이 말했다."]], [["“Have you not hit upon any?”", "\"자넨 아무것도 떠오른 게 없나?\""], ["asked Danglars.", "당글라르가 물었다."]], [["“No!—you undertook to do so.”", "\"없습니다! 당신이 찾아 주겠다고 하셨잖습니까.\""]], [["“True,”", "\"맞아.\""], ["replied Danglars; “the French have the superiority over the Spaniards, that the Spaniards ruminate, while the French invent.”", "당글라르가 대꾸했다. \"프랑스인이 에스파냐인보다 나은 점이 바로 이거지. 에스파냐인은 곱씹기만 하지만, 프랑스인은 만들어 내거든.\""]], [["“Do you invent, then,”", "\"그럼 만들어 내 보세요.\""], ["said Fernand impatiently.", "페르낭이 조바심을 내며 말했다."]], [["“Waiter,”", "\"여보게,\""], ["said Danglars, “pen, ink, and paper.”", "당글라르가 종업원을 불렀다. \"펜이랑 잉크, 종이 좀 주게.\""]], [["“Pen, ink, and paper,”", "\"펜, 잉크, 종이라.\""], ["muttered Fernand.", "페르낭이 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Yes; I am a supercargo; pen, ink, and paper are my tools, and without my tools I am fit for nothing.”", "\"그래. 난 회계 담당 아닌가. 펜, 잉크, 종이가 내 연장이지. 연장이 없으면 난 아무짝에도 쓸모없어.\""]], [["“Pen, ink, and paper, then,”", "\"펜이랑 잉크, 종이 주게.\""], ["called Fernand loudly.", "페르낭이 큰 소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“There’s what you want on that table,”", "\"필요하신 게 저 식탁에 있습니다.\""], ["said the waiter.", "종업원이 말했다."]], [["“Bring them here.”", "\"이리 가져오게.\""], ["The waiter did as he was desired.", "종업원이 시키는 대로 했다."]], [["0059m\n“When one thinks,”", "\"생각해 보면 말이야,\""], ["said Caderousse, letting his hand drop on the paper, “there is here wherewithal to kill a man more sure than if we waited at the corner of a wood to assassinate him!", "카드루스가 종이 위에 손을 툭 떨어뜨리며 말했다. \"여기 이것들이야말로, 숲 모퉁이에 숨어 누굴 죽이려고 기다리는 것보다 더 확실하게 사람을 죽일 수 있는 물건이라고!"], ["I have always had more dread of a pen, a bottle of ink, and a sheet of paper, than of a sword or pistol.”", "난 늘 칼이나 권총보다도 펜 한 자루, 잉크 한 병, 종이 한 장이 더 무서웠어.\""]], [["“The fellow is not so drunk as he appears to be,” said Danglars.", "\"이 친구, 보기만큼 취하진 않았군.\" 당글라르가 말했다."], ["“Give him some more wine, Fernand.”", "\"술 좀 더 주게, 페르낭.\""], ["Fernand filled Caderousse’s glass, who, like the confirmed toper he was, lifted his hand from the paper and seized the glass.", "페르낭이 카드루스의 잔을 채우자, 골수 술꾼답게 카드루스는 종이에서 손을 떼고 잔을 움켜쥐었다."]], [["The Catalan watched him until Caderousse, almost overcome by this fresh assault on his senses, rested, or rather dropped, his glass upon the table.", "카탈루냐 사내는, 이 새로운 일격에 정신을 거의 잃다시피 한 카드루스가 잔을 식탁에 내려놓는, 아니 떨구는 것을 지켜보았다."]], [["“Well!”", "\"자!\""], ["resumed the Catalan, as he saw the final glimmer of Caderousse’s reason vanishing before the last glass of wine.", "카탈루냐 사내는 마지막 한 잔 앞에서 카드루스의 이성이 마지막 불꽃마저 꺼져 가는 것을 보며 다시 입을 열었다."]], [["“Well, then, I should say, for instance,”", "\"좋아, 그럼 이를테면 이렇게 말해 볼 수 있겠지.\""], ["resumed Danglars, “that if after a voyage such as Dantès has just made, in which he touched at the Island of Elba, someone were to denounce him to the king’s procureur as a Bonapartist agent——”", "당글라르가 말을 이었다. \"당테스가 방금 마친 것 같은 항해, 그러니까 엘바섬에 들렀던 항해를 한 뒤에, 누군가가 그를 보나파르트파 첩자라고 검찰관에게 고발한다면 말이야……\""]], [["“I will denounce him!”", "\"내가 고발하겠습니다!\""], ["exclaimed the young man hastily.", "젊은이가 황급히 외쳤다."]], [["“Yes, but they will make you then sign your declaration, and confront you with him you have denounced; I will supply you with the means of supporting your accusation, for I know the fact well.", "\"그래, 하지만 그러면 자네더러 진술서에 서명하게 하고, 자네가 고발한 자와 대질시킬 걸세. 나야 자네가 그 고발을 뒷받침할 수단을 마련해 주겠지. 그 사실을 잘 알고 있으니까."], ["But Dantès cannot remain forever in prison, and one day or other he will leave it, and the day when he comes out, woe betide him who was the cause of his incarceration!”", "하지만 당테스가 영영 감옥에 있을 수는 없는 노릇, 언젠가는 나올 거야. 그리고 그가 나오는 날, 그를 가둔 장본인에게 화가 미칠 걸세!\""]], [["“Oh, I should wish nothing better than that he would come and seek a quarrel with me.”", "\"오, 그가 와서 나한테 싸움을 걸어 준다면야 더 바랄 게 없죠.\""]], [["“Yes, and Mercédès! Mercédès, who will detest you if you have only the misfortune to scratch the skin of her dearly beloved Edmond!”", "\"그래, 그럼 메르세데스는! 그녀가 그토록 사랑하는 에드몽의 살갗에 흠집 하나라도 내는 불행을 저지른다면, 메르세데스가 자넬 증오할 텐데!\""]], [["“True!”", "\"맞습니다!\""], ["said Fernand.", "페르낭이 말했다."]], [["“No, no,”", "\"아니, 아니야.\""], ["continued Danglars; “if we resolve on such a step, it would be much better to take, as I now do, this pen, dip it into this ink, and write with the left hand (that the writing may not be recognized) the denunciation we propose.”", "당글라르가 말을 이었다. \"그런 일을 벌이기로 했다면, 차라리 내가 지금 하는 것처럼 이 펜을 들어 잉크에 적시고, 왼손으로(필체를 알아보지 못하게) 우리가 꾸미는 고발장을 쓰는 편이 훨씬 낫지.\""], ["And Danglars, uniting practice with theory, wrote with his left hand, and in a writing reversed from his usual style, and totally unlike it, the following lines, which he handed to Fernand, and which Fernand read in an undertone:", "그러고는 당글라르는 이론에 실천을 곁들여, 왼손으로, 제 평소 필체와는 거꾸로, 전혀 딴판인 글씨로 다음과 같은 몇 줄을 써서 페르낭에게 건넸다. 페르낭은 그것을 낮은 목소리로 읽었다."]], [["“The honorable, the king’s attorney, is informed by a friend of the throne and religion, that one Edmond Dantès, mate of the ship _Pharaon_, arrived this morning from Smyrna, after having touched at Naples and Porto-Ferrajo, has been intrusted by Murat with a letter for the usurper, and by the usurper with a letter for the Bonapartist committee in Paris.", "\"왕좌와 종교의 벗 된 한 사람이 검찰관 각하께 알리는 바입니다. 파라옹호의 일등 항해사 에드몽 당테스라는 자가, 나폴리와 포르토페라요에 들른 뒤 오늘 아침 스미르나에서 입항하였으며, 뮈라에게서 찬탈자에게 전할 편지를, 또 찬탈자에게서 파리의 보나파르트파 위원회에 전할 편지를 맡았습니다."], ["Proof of this crime will be found on arresting him, for the letter will be found upon him, or at his father’s, or in his cabin on board the _Pharaon_.”", "이 죄의 증거는 그를 체포하면 드러날 것입니다. 그 편지는 그의 몸에서, 혹은 그의 아버지 집에서, 혹은 파라옹호의 그의 선실에서 발견될 것입니다.\""]], [["“Very good,”", "\"아주 좋아.\" 당글라르가 말을 이었다."], ["resumed Danglars; “now your revenge looks like common sense, for in no way can it revert to yourself, and the matter will thus work its own way; there is nothing to do now but fold the letter as I am doing, and write upon it, ‘To the king’s attorney,’ and that’s all settled.”", "\"이제 자네 복수도 제법 이치에 맞아 보이는군. 어떤 식으로도 자네한테 화살이 돌아오지 않을 테고, 일은 저절로 굴러가게 될 거야. 이제 할 일이라곤 내가 하는 대로 편지를 접어서, 그 위에 '검찰관 앞'이라고 쓰는 것뿐이지."], ["And Danglars wrote the address as he spoke.", "그럼 다 끝나는 거고.\" 당글라르는 말하면서 수신인을 적었다."]], [["“Yes, and that’s all settled!”", "\"그래, 이걸로 다 끝났군!\""], ["exclaimed Caderousse, who, by a last effort of intellect, had followed the reading of the letter, and instinctively comprehended all the misery which such a denunciation must entail.", "마지막 안간힘으로 정신을 차려 편지 읽는 소리를 따라가던 카드루스가 외쳤다. 그는 이런 고발이 불러올 온갖 참상을 본능적으로 깨달았다."], ["“Yes, and that’s all settled; only it will be an infamous shame;”", "\"그래, 이걸로 다 끝나. 다만 이건 파렴치한 짓이야.\""], ["and he stretched out his hand to reach the letter.", "그러고는 편지를 잡으려 손을 뻗었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇지.\""], ["said Danglars, taking it from beyond his reach; “and as what I say and do is merely in jest, and I, amongst the first and foremost, should be sorry if anything happened to Dantès—the worthy Dantès—look here!”", "당글라르가 그의 손이 닿지 않는 곳으로 편지를 빼며 말했다. \"내가 말하고 행한 건 다 그저 농담일 뿐이야. 그리고 누구보다도 내가 가장 먼저, 당테스에게, 그 훌륭한 당테스에게 무슨 일이라도 생긴다면 가슴 아파할 사람이지."], ["And taking the letter, he squeezed it up in his hands and threw it into a corner of the arbor.", "자, 보라고!\" 그러고는 편지를 집어 두 손으로 구겨 정자 한구석에 던져 버렸다."]], [["“All right!”", "\"좋았어!\""], ["said Caderousse.", "카드루스가 말했다."], ["“Dantès is my friend, and I won’t have him ill-used.”", "\"당테스는 내 친구야. 그를 괴롭히게 두지 않을 거라고.\""]], [["“And who thinks of using him ill?", "\"누가 그를 괴롭힌단 말인가?"], ["Certainly neither I nor Fernand,”", "나도 페르낭도 분명 아니지.\""], ["said Danglars, rising and looking at the young man, who still remained seated, but whose eye was fixed on the denunciatory sheet of paper flung into the corner.", "당글라르가 일어서며, 여전히 앉은 채 한구석에 내던져진 고발장에 눈을 박고 있는 젊은이를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“In this case,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["replied Caderousse, “let’s have some more wine.", "카드루스가 대꾸했다. \"술이나 더 마시자고."], ["I wish to drink to the health of Edmond and the lovely Mercédès.”", "에드몽과 어여쁜 메르세데스의 건강을 위해 마시고 싶어.\""]], [["“You have had too much already, drunkard,”", "\"자넨 이미 너무 많이 마셨어, 이 술꾼아.\""], ["said Danglars; “and if you continue, you will be compelled to sleep here, because unable to stand on your legs.”", "당글라르가 말했다. \"계속 마시다간 제 다리로 설 수도 없어서 여기서 자게 될 거야.\""]], [["“I?” said Caderousse, rising with all the offended dignity of a drunken man, “I can’t keep on my legs?", "\"내가?\" 카드루스가 술 취한 사람 특유의 모욕당한 위엄을 한껏 세우며 일어섰다."], ["Why, I’ll wager I can go up into the belfry of the Accoules, and without staggering, too!”", "\"내가 다리로 못 선다고? 흥, 내기해도 좋아, 난 아쿨 종탑까지 올라갈 수 있어, 그것도 비틀거리지 않고!\""]], [["“Done!”", "\"좋아!\""], ["said Danglars, “I’ll take your bet; but tomorrow—today it is time to return.", "당글라르가 말했다. \"그 내기 받지. 하지만 내일 하세."], ["Give me your arm, and let us go.”", "오늘은 돌아갈 시간이야. 내 팔을 잡게, 가자고.\""]], [["“Very well, let us go,”", "\"좋아, 가자.\""], ["said Caderousse; “but I don’t want your arm at all.", "카드루스가 말했다. \"근데 자네 팔 따윈 필요 없어."], ["Come, Fernand, won’t you return to Marseilles with us?”", "자, 페르낭, 우리랑 마르세유로 안 돌아갈 텐가?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"안 갑니다.\""], ["said Fernand; “I shall return to the Catalans.”", "페르낭이 말했다. \"전 카탈루냐 마을로 돌아갈 겁니다.\""]], [["“You’re wrong. Come with us to Marseilles—come along.”", "\"그러면 안 되지. 우리랑 마르세유로 가자고, 어서.\""]], [["“I will not.”", "\"안 갑니다.\""]], [["“What do you mean?", "\"무슨 소리야?"], ["you will not?", "안 가겠다고?"], ["Well, just as you like, my prince; there’s liberty for all the world.", "그래, 좋을 대로 하시게, 우리 왕자님. 누구에게나 자유는 있는 법이니까."], ["Come along, Danglars, and let the young gentleman return to the Catalans if he chooses.”", "가자고, 당글라르. 이 젊은 양반은 가고 싶다면 카탈루냐 마을로 가게 내버려 두지.\""]], [["Danglars took advantage of Caderousse’s temper at the moment, to take him off towards Marseilles by the Porte Saint-Victor, staggering as he went.", "당글라르는 그 순간 카드루스의 기분을 틈타, 비틀거리는 그를 데리고 생빅토르 문을 지나 마르세유 쪽으로 데려갔다."]], [["When they had advanced about twenty yards, Danglars looked back and saw Fernand stoop, pick up the crumpled paper, and putting it into his pocket then rush out of the arbor towards Pillon.", "스무 걸음쯤 갔을 때, 당글라르가 뒤를 돌아보니 페르낭이 몸을 굽혀 구겨진 종이를 집어 주머니에 넣고는, 정자에서 뛰쳐나가 피용 쪽으로 달려가는 것이 보였다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"이런,\""], ["said Caderousse, “why, what a lie he told!", "카드루스가 말했다. \"원, 새빨간 거짓말을 했군!"], ["He said he was going to the Catalans, and he is going to the city.", "카탈루냐 마을로 간다더니, 시내로 가고 있잖아. 어이, 페르낭!"], ["Hallo, Fernand! You are coming, my boy!”", "너 길을 잘못 들었어, 이 친구야!\""]], [["“Oh, you don’t see straight,”", "\"오, 자네 눈이 비뚤어졌군.\""], ["said Danglars; “he’s gone right by the road to the Vieilles Infirmeries.”", "당글라르가 말했다. \"저 친구는 비에유앵피르므리 길로 똑바로 갔는데.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래.\""], ["said Caderousse, “I should have sworn that he turned to the right—how treacherous wine is!”", "카드루스가 말했다. \"난 분명 저 친구가 오른쪽으로 도는 걸 봤다고 맹세할 수도 있었는데. 참, 술이란 게 이렇게 사람을 속이는군!\""]], [["“Come, come,”", "\"좋아, 좋아.\""], ["said Danglars to himself, “now the thing is at work and it will effect its purpose unassisted.”", "당글라르가 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다. '이제 일은 시작됐고, 누구 손도 빌리지 않고 제 갈 길을 갈 거야.'"]], [["# Chapter 5. The Marriage Feast", "# Chapter 5. The Marriage Feast"]], [["The morning’s sun rose clear and resplendent, touching the foamy waves into a network of ruby-tinted light.", "아침 해가 맑고 눈부시게 떠올라, 거품 이는 물결을 루비빛 그물망처럼 물들였다."]], [["The feast had been made ready on the second floor at La Réserve, with whose arbor the reader is already familiar.", "잔치는 라레제르브의 이층에 차려졌다. 독자들은 그 정자를 이미 알고 있다."], ["The apartment destined for the purpose was spacious and lighted by a number of windows, over each of which was written in golden letters for some inexplicable reason the name of one of the principal cities of France; beneath these windows a wooden balcony extended the entire length of the house.", "잔치를 위해 마련된 방은 널찍했고, 여러 개의 창문으로 빛이 들어왔다. 무슨 까닭에서인지 알 수 없지만 창마다 위에는 프랑스 주요 도시들의 이름이 하나씩 금빛 글자로 적혀 있었다. 그 창들 아래로는 나무 발코니가 집 전체 길이만큼 뻗어 있었다."], ["And although the entertainment was fixed for twelve o’clock, an hour previous to that time the balcony was filled with impatient and expectant guests, consisting of the favored part of the crew of the _Pharaon_, and other personal friends of the bridegroom, the whole of whom had arrayed themselves in their choicest costumes, in order to do greater honor to the occasion.", "잔치는 정오로 정해져 있었지만, 그보다 한 시간 전부터 발코니는 조바심 내며 기다리는 손님들로 가득 찼다. 파라옹호 선원들 가운데 특별히 초대받은 이들과 신랑의 다른 가까운 벗들이었다. 모두들 이 자리를 더욱 빛내려고 가장 좋은 옷차림으로 단장하고 나섰다."]], [["Various rumors were afloat to the effect that the owners of the _Pharaon_ had promised to attend the nuptial feast; but all seemed unanimous in doubting that an act of such rare and exceeding condescension could possibly be intended.", "파라옹호의 선주들이 혼인 잔치에 참석하기로 약속했다는 소문이 여기저기 떠돌았다. 그러나 그토록 드물고 과분한 호의가 정말로 베풀어지리라는 데에는 모두가 한결같이 의심을 품는 듯했다."]], [["Danglars, however, who now made his appearance, accompanied by Caderousse, effectually confirmed the report, stating that he had recently conversed with M. Morrel, who had himself assured him of his intention to dine at La Réserve.", "그런데 카드루스를 데리고 나타난 당글라르가 그 소문을 확실하게 못 박았다. 그는 얼마 전 모렐 씨와 이야기를 나누었는데, 모렐 씨가 라레제르브에서 저녁을 들겠다고 직접 장담했다는 것이다."]], [["In fact, a moment later M.", "과연 잠시 뒤 모렐 씨가 모습을 드러냈고, 파라옹호 선원들의 열렬한 박수갈채가 그를 맞았다."], ["Morrel appeared and was saluted with an enthusiastic burst of applause from the crew of the _Pharaon_, who hailed the visit of the shipowner as a sure indication that the man whose wedding feast he thus delighted to honor would ere long be first in command of the ship; and as Dantès was universally beloved on board his vessel, the sailors put no restraint on their tumultuous joy at finding that the opinion and choice of their superiors so exactly coincided with their own.", "선원들은 선주의 방문을, 그가 이렇게 기꺼이 혼인 잔치를 빛내 주려는 그 사내가 머지않아 배의 첫 지휘권을 맡게 되리라는 확실한 징표로 받아들였다. 당테스는 배에서 두루 사랑받는 사람이었기에, 선원들은 윗사람들의 생각과 선택이 자신들의 것과 이토록 꼭 들어맞는 것을 알고 터져 나오는 기쁨을 조금도 억누르지 않았다."]], [["With the entrance of M. Morrel, Danglars and Caderousse were despatched in search of the bridegroom to convey to him the intelligence of the arrival of the important personage whose coming had created such a lively sensation, and to beseech him to make haste.", "모렐 씨가 들어오자, 당글라르와 카드루스는 신랑을 찾으러 보내졌다. 그토록 떠들썩한 화제를 불러일으킨 귀빈이 도착했음을 신랑에게 알리고, 서둘러 오라고 청하기 위해서였다."]], [["Danglars and Caderousse set off upon their errand at full speed; but ere they had gone many steps they perceived a group advancing towards them, composed of the betrothed pair, a party of young girls in attendance on the bride, by whose side walked Dantès’ father; the whole brought up by Fernand, whose lips wore their usual sinister smile.", "당글라르와 카드루스는 곧장 심부름을 하러 전속력으로 달려 나갔다. 그러나 몇 걸음 가기도 전에 자기들 쪽으로 다가오는 한 무리를 보았다. 약혼한 한 쌍과 신부를 모시는 처녀들의 행렬이었고, 그 곁으로는 당테스의 아버지가 걸어왔다. 행렬의 맨 뒤를 페르낭이 따랐는데, 그의 입가에는 여느 때처럼 음험한 미소가 걸려 있었다."]], [["Neither Mercédès nor Edmond observed the strange expression of his countenance; they were so happy that they were conscious only of the sunshine and the presence of each other.", "메르세데스도 에드몽도 그의 얼굴에 떠오른 그 야릇한 표정을 알아채지 못했다. 두 사람은 너무 행복해서 햇살과 서로의 존재만을 느낄 뿐이었다."]], [["Having acquitted themselves of their errand, and exchanged a hearty shake of the hand with Edmond, Danglars and Caderousse took their places beside Fernand and old Dantès,—the latter of whom attracted universal notice.", "심부름을 마치고 에드몽과 정겹게 손을 맞잡은 뒤, 당글라르와 카드루스는 페르낭과 늙은 당테스 곁에 자리를 잡았다. 늙은 당테스는 모두의 시선을 한 몸에 끌었다."]], [["The old man was attired in a suit of glistening watered silk, trimmed with steel buttons, beautifully cut and polished.", "노인은 반짝이는 물결무늬 비단 양복을 입고 있었는데, 곱게 재단하고 광을 낸 강철 단추로 장식되어 있었다."], ["His thin but wiry legs were arrayed in a pair of richly embroidered clocked stockings, evidently of English manufacture, while from his three-cornered hat depended a long streaming knot of white and blue ribbons.", "가늘지만 다부진 두 다리에는 화려한 자수가 놓인, 한눈에 영국제임을 알 수 있는 줄무늬 양말을 신었고, 삼각모에서는 희고 푸른 리본 매듭이 길게 늘어져 나부꼈다."], ["Thus he came along, supporting himself on a curiously carved stick, his aged countenance lit up with happiness, looking for all the world like one of the aged dandies of 1796, parading the newly opened gardens of the Luxembourg and Tuileries.", "이렇게 차려입은 그는 정교하게 조각된 지팡이에 몸을 의지한 채, 늙은 얼굴에 행복이 환하게 피어난 모습으로 다가왔다. 그 모습은 영락없이 1796년 무렵의 늙은 멋쟁이가 막 문을 연 뤽상부르나 튈르리 정원을 으스대며 거니는 것 같았다."]], [["Beside him glided Caderousse, whose desire to partake of the good things provided for the wedding party had induced him to become reconciled to the Dantès, father and son, although there still lingered in his mind a faint and unperfect recollection of the events of the preceding night; just as the brain retains on waking in the morning the dim and misty outline of a dream.", "그 곁으로는 카드루스가 슬그머니 따라왔다. 혼인 잔치에 차려진 좋은 음식을 맛보고 싶다는 욕심에, 그는 당테스 부자와 화해하기로 한 터였다. 그래도 그의 마음 한구석에는 지난밤 일에 대한 희미하고 어렴풋한 기억이 남아 있었다. 마치 아침에 잠에서 깬 뒤에도 머릿속에 흐릿하게 어른거리는 꿈의 윤곽처럼."]], [["0065m\nAs Danglars approached the disappointed lover, he cast on him a look of deep meaning, while Fernand, as he slowly paced behind the happy pair, who seemed, in their own unmixed content, to have entirely forgotten that such a being as himself existed, was pale and abstracted; occasionally, however, a deep flush would overspread his countenance, and a nervous contraction distort his features, while, with an agitated and restless gaze, he would glance in the direction of Marseilles, like one who either anticipated or foresaw some great and important event.", "당글라르는 실연당한 사내에게 다가가며 깊은 뜻이 담긴 눈길을 던졌다. 페르낭은 행복한 한 쌍의 뒤를 천천히 걸어왔다. 두 사람은 자기들만의 순수한 만족에 빠져, 페르낭 같은 존재가 있다는 사실조차 까맣게 잊은 듯했다. 그는 창백한 얼굴로 넋이 나가 있었다. 그러다 이따금 얼굴에 시뻘건 홍조가 번지고, 신경질적인 경련이 이목구비를 일그러뜨렸다. 그럴 때면 그는 불안하고 초조한 눈길로 마르세유 쪽을 흘끔거렸다. 어떤 크고 중대한 일이 일어나기를 기다리거나, 아니면 미리 내다보고 있는 사람처럼."]], [["Dantès himself was simply, but becomingly, clad in the dress peculiar to the merchant service—a costume somewhat between a military and a civil garb; and with his fine countenance, radiant with joy and happiness, a more perfect specimen of manly beauty could scarcely be imagined.", "당테스 자신은 수수하지만 어울리는 차림이었다. 상선 선원 특유의 복장으로, 군복과 평상복의 중간쯤 되는 옷이었다. 기쁨과 행복으로 환히 빛나는 잘생긴 얼굴과 어우러져, 그보다 더 완벽한 남성미의 본보기는 좀처럼 떠올리기 어려울 정도였다."]], [["Lovely as the Greek girls of Cyprus or Chios, Mercédès boasted the same bright flashing eyes of jet, and ripe, round, coral lips.", "키프로스나 키오스섬의 그리스 처녀들만큼이나 아리따운 메르세데스는 그들과 똑같이 까만 눈을 반짝였고, 무르익은 둥근 산호빛 입술을 지녔다. 그녀는 아를 여인이나 안달루시아 여인처럼 가볍고 자유로운 걸음으로 움직였다."], ["She moved with the light, free step of an Arlesienne or an Andalusian.", "대도시의 처세술에 더 능한 여자였다면 베일로 홍조를 가리거나, 적어도 짙은 속눈썹을 내리깔아 생기 넘치는 눈의 촉촉한 광채를 감추었을 것이다."], ["One more practiced in the arts of great cities would have hid her blushes beneath a veil, or, at least, have cast down her thickly fringed lashes, so as to have concealed the liquid lustre of her animated eyes; but, on the contrary, the delighted girl looked around her with a smile that seemed to say: “If you are my friends, rejoice with me, for I am very happy.”", "그러나 정반대로, 기쁨에 찬 처녀는 이렇게 말하는 듯한 미소를 띠고 주위를 둘러보았다. \"당신들이 내 벗이라면, 나와 함께 기뻐해 주세요. 저는 너무나 행복하니까요.\""]], [["As soon as the bridal party came in sight of La Réserve, M. Morrel descended and came forth to meet it, followed by the soldiers and sailors there assembled, to whom he had repeated the promise already given, that Dantès should be the successor to the late Captain Leclere.", "혼인 행렬이 라레제르브에 모습을 드러내자마자, 모렐 씨가 내려와 맞으러 나왔다. 그곳에 모인 군인들과 선원들이 그 뒤를 따랐는데, 그는 그들에게 당테스가 작고한 르클레르 선장의 후임이 되리라는, 이미 한 약속을 다시 한번 들려준 터였다."], ["Edmond, at the approach of his patron, respectfully placed the arm of his affianced bride within that of M.", "자신의 후원자가 다가오자, 에드몽은 공손하게 약혼녀의 팔을 모렐 씨의 팔에 끼워 주었다."], ["Morrel, who, forthwith conducting her up the flight of wooden steps leading to the chamber in which the feast was prepared, was gayly followed by the guests, beneath whose heavy tread the slight structure creaked and groaned for the space of several minutes.", "모렐 씨는 곧장 그녀를 이끌어 잔치가 마련된 방으로 통하는 나무 계단을 올랐고, 손님들이 흥겹게 그 뒤를 따랐다. 그 묵직한 발걸음에 짓눌려 부실한 계단은 몇 분 동안이나 삐걱대며 신음했다."]], [["“Father,”", "\"아버님,\" 메르세데스가 식탁 한가운데에 이르러 걸음을 멈추며 말했다."], ["said Mercédès, stopping when she had reached the centre of the table, “sit, I pray you, on my right hand; on my left I will place him who has ever been as a brother to me,”", "\"부디 제 오른편에 앉으세요. 왼편에는 늘 저에게 오라버니 같았던 분을 모실게요.\" 그녀는 부드럽고 다정한 미소를 지으며 페르낭을 가리켰다."], ["pointing with a soft and gentle smile to Fernand; but her words and look seemed to inflict the direst torture on him, for his lips became ghastly pale, and even beneath the dark hue of his complexion the blood might be seen retreating as though some sudden pang drove it back to the heart.", "그러나 그녀의 말과 눈길은 페르낭에게 더없이 끔찍한 고통을 안기는 듯했다. 그의 입술이 끔찍하게 창백해졌고, 가무잡잡한 얼굴빛 아래에서조차 피가 물러나는 것이 보였다. 마치 갑작스러운 격통이 피를 심장으로 되몰아넣는 것 같았다."]], [["During this time, Dantès, at the opposite side of the table, had been occupied in similarly placing his most honored guests. M.", "그동안 식탁 맞은편에서는 당테스가 자기 쪽 귀빈들을 비슷하게 자리에 앉히고 있었다."], ["Morrel was seated at his right hand, Danglars at his left; while, at a sign from Edmond, the rest of the company ranged themselves as they found it most agreeable.", "모렐 씨를 오른편에, 당글라르를 왼편에 앉혔다. 그리고 에드몽이 눈짓을 하자, 나머지 사람들은 저마다 편한 대로 자리를 잡았다."]], [["Then they began to pass around the dusky, piquant, Arlesian sausages, and lobsters in their dazzling red cuirasses, prawns of large size and brilliant color, the echinus with its prickly outside and dainty morsel within, the clovis, esteemed by the epicures of the South as more than rivalling the exquisite flavor of the oyster, North.", "이윽고 거뭇하고 매콤한 아를식 소시지가 돌려졌고, 눈부신 붉은 갑옷을 두른 바닷가재, 큼지막하고 빛깔 고운 새우, 가시투성이 겉껍질 속에 입에 살살 녹는 살을 품은 성게, 남부의 미식가들이 북부의 굴 못지않은 절묘한 맛으로 친다는 조개도 차례로 돌았다."], ["All the delicacies, in fact, that are cast up by the wash of waters on the sandy beach, and styled by the grateful fishermen “fruits of the sea.”", "한마디로 물결에 밀려 모래밭에 올라오는 온갖 진미였으니, 고마운 어부들이 \"바다의 열매\"라 부르는 것들이었다."]], [["“A pretty silence truly!”", "\"참으로 곱기도 한 침묵이로구나!\""], ["said the old father of the bridegroom, as he carried to his lips a glass of wine of the hue and brightness of the topaz, and which had just been placed before Mercédès herself.", "신랑의 늙은 아버지가 말했다. 그는 토파즈처럼 빛깔 곱고 환한 포도주 한 잔을 입가로 가져갔는데, 마침 메르세데스 앞에 놓여 있던 잔이었다."], ["“Now, would anybody think that this room contained a happy, merry party, who desire nothing better than to laugh and dance the hours away?”", "\"자, 이 방에 웃고 춤추며 시간을 보내는 것보다 더 바라는 게 없는, 행복하고 흥겨운 손님들이 모여 있다고 누가 생각이나 하겠나?\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["sighed Caderousse, “a man cannot always feel happy because he is about to be married.”", "카드루스가 한숨지었다. \"결혼을 앞두고 있다고 해서 사람이 늘 행복할 수만은 없는 법이지요.\""]], [["“The truth is,”", "\"사실은,\" 당테스가 대답했다."], ["replied Dantès, “that I am too happy for noisy mirth; if that is what you meant by your observation, my worthy friend, you are right; joy takes a strange effect at times, it seems to oppress us almost the same as sorrow.”", "\"제가 너무 행복해서 떠들썩하게 즐기지 못하는 겁니다. 그게 자네가 말하려던 거라면, 친애하는 친구, 자네 말이 옳아. 기쁨이란 때때로 야릇한 작용을 해서, 슬픔만큼이나 사람을 짓누르는 것 같으니 말이야.\""]], [["Danglars looked towards Fernand, whose excitable nature received and betrayed each fresh impression.", "당글라르는 페르낭 쪽을 바라보았다. 그 격하기 쉬운 천성은 새로운 인상을 하나하나 받아들이고 그대로 드러냈다."]], [["“Why, what ails you?”", "\"아니, 자넨 무슨 일인가?\""], ["asked he of Edmond.", "그가 에드몽에게 물었다."], ["“Do you fear any approaching evil?", "\"무슨 불행이라도 닥칠까 두려운가?"], ["I should say that you were the happiest man alive at this instant.”", "내가 보기엔 지금 이 순간 자네야말로 세상에서 가장 행복한 사람일 텐데.\""]], [["“And that is the very thing that alarms me,”", "\"바로 그게 저를 두렵게 합니다.\""], ["returned Dantès.", "당테스가 대답했다."], ["“Man does not appear to me to be intended to enjoy felicity so unmixed; happiness is like the enchanted palaces we read of in our childhood, where fierce, fiery dragons defend the entrance and approach; and monsters of all shapes and kinds, requiring to be overcome ere victory is ours.", "\"사람은 이토록 순수한 행복을 누리도록 만들어진 존재가 아닌 것 같거든요. 행복이란 우리가 어린 시절 이야기로 읽던 마법의 궁전 같은 것입니다. 사납고 불을 뿜는 용이 입구와 길목을 지키고, 온갖 모양과 종류의 괴물들이 버티고 있어, 승리를 거머쥐기 전에 그것들을 모두 물리쳐야 하지요."], ["I own that I am lost in wonder to find myself promoted to an honor of which I feel myself unworthy—that of being the husband of Mercédès.”", "솔직히 저는 제가 어울리지 않는다고 여기는 영예, 곧 메르세데스의 남편이 되는 영예로 올라선 것이 그저 놀랍기만 합니다.\""]], [["“Nay, nay!”", "\"아니, 아니지!\""], ["cried Caderousse, smiling, “you have not attained that honor yet.", "카드루스가 웃으며 외쳤다. \"자네는 아직 그 영예에 이르지 못했네."], ["Mercédès is not yet your wife.", "메르세데스는 아직 자네 아내가 아니야."], ["Just assume the tone and manner of a husband, and see how she will remind you that your hour is not yet come!”", "어디 한번 남편 같은 말투와 태도를 부려 보게. 그러면 자네 차례가 아직 오지 않았다고 그녀가 일깨워 줄 테니!\""]], [["The bride blushed, while Fernand, restless and uneasy, seemed to start at every fresh sound, and from time to time wiped away the large drops of perspiration that gathered on his brow.", "신부는 얼굴을 붉혔고, 페르낭은 안절부절 불안해하며 무슨 소리가 날 때마다 흠칫 놀라는 듯했다. 그러고는 이따금 이마에 맺힌 굵은 땀방울을 닦아 냈다."]], [["“Well, never mind that, neighbor Caderousse; it is not worthwhile to contradict me for such a trifle as that.", "\"뭐, 그건 괜찮네, 이웃 카드루스. 그런 사소한 일로 내 말에 토를 달 것까진 없지."], ["’Tis true that Mercédès is not actually my wife; but,”", "메르세데스가 아직 정말로 내 아내가 아닌 건 맞아. 하지만,\""], ["added he, drawing out his watch, “in an hour and a half she will be.”", "그가 회중시계를 꺼내며 덧붙였다. \"한 시간 반 뒤면 내 아내가 될 걸세.\""]], [["A general exclamation of surprise ran round the table, with the exception of the elder Dantès, whose laugh displayed the still perfect beauty of his large white teeth.", "늙은 당테스만 빼고 식탁에 빙 둘러앉은 사람들 사이에서 놀라움의 탄성이 터졌다. 노인은 웃음을 터뜨렸는데, 여전히 완벽하게 고운 큼직하고 새하얀 이가 드러났다."], ["Mercédès looked pleased and gratified, while Fernand grasped the handle of his knife with a convulsive clutch.", "메르세데스는 흐뭇하고 만족스러운 표정이었고, 페르낭은 경련하듯 칼자루를 부여잡았다."]], [["“In an hour?” inquired Danglars, turning pale.", "\"한 시간 뒤라고?\" 당글라르가 창백해지며 물었다."], ["“How is that, my friend?”", "\"그게 어떻게 된 일인가, 친구?\""]], [["“Why, thus it is,”", "\"이렇게 된 거지.\""], ["replied Dantès.", "당테스가 대답했다."], ["“Thanks to the influence of M. Morrel, to whom, next to my father, I owe every blessing I enjoy, every difficulty has been removed.", "\"아버지 다음으로 제가 누리는 모든 복을 빚지고 있는 모렐 씨의 힘 덕분에, 모든 걸림돌이 치워졌습니다."], ["We have purchased permission to waive the usual delay; and at half-past two o’clock the Mayor of Marseilles will be waiting for us at the city hall.", "으레 거쳐야 하는 대기 기간을 면제받도록 허가를 사 두었거든요. 두 시 반에 마르세유 시장님이 시청에서 저희를 기다리실 겁니다."], ["Now, as a quarter-past one has already struck, I do not consider I have asserted too much in saying, that, in another hour and thirty minutes Mercédès will have become Madame Dantès.” 0069m", "자, 벌써 한 시 십오 분을 알리는 종이 울렸으니, 한 시간 삼십 분 뒤면 메르세데스가 당테스 부인이 되어 있으리라고 말해도 지나친 장담은 아니겠지요.\""]], [["Fernand closed his eyes, a burning sensation passed across his brow, and he was compelled to support himself by the table to prevent his falling from his chair; but in spite of all his efforts, he could not refrain from uttering a deep groan, which, however, was lost amid the noisy felicitations of the company.", "페르낭은 두 눈을 감았다. 이마를 타고 타는 듯한 감각이 스쳤고, 그는 의자에서 굴러떨어지지 않으려고 식탁을 짚어 몸을 가눠야 했다. 그러나 아무리 안간힘을 써도 깊은 신음만은 새어 나오는 것을 막을 수 없었다. 다만 그 소리는 떠들썩한 손님들의 축하 속에 묻혀 버렸다."]], [["“Upon my word,”", "\"맹세코,\""], ["cried the old man, “you make short work of this kind of affair.", "노인이 외쳤다. \"자네는 이런 일을 참 후딱 해치우는구먼."], ["Arrived here only yesterday morning, and married today at three o’clock!", "어제 아침에야 도착해서 오늘 세 시에 결혼이라니!"], ["Commend me to a sailor for going the quick way to work!”", "일을 빨리 해치우는 데는 역시 선원이 으뜸이라니까!\""]], [["“But,”", "\"그런데,\""], ["asked Danglars, in a timid tone, “how did you manage about the other formalities—the contract—the settlement?”", "당글라르가 조심스러운 말투로 물었다. \"다른 절차들은 어떻게 처리했나? 혼인 계약이라든가, 재산 정리 말일세.\""]], [["“The contract,”", "\"혼인 계약이라,\""], ["answered Dantès, laughingly, “it didn’t take long to fix that.", "당테스가 웃으며 대답했다. \"그건 정하는 데 오래 걸리지도 않았네."], ["Mercédès has no fortune; I have none to settle on her. So, you see, our papers were quickly written out, and certainly do not come very expensive.”", "메르세데스에게는 재산이 없고, 나도 그녀에게 정해 줄 재산이 없으니까. 그러니 알겠나, 서류는 금세 작성됐고, 돈도 그리 많이 들지 않았지.\""], ["This joke elicited a fresh burst of applause.", "이 농담에 또 한 차례 박수가 터졌다."]], [["“So that what we presumed to be merely the betrothal feast turns out to be the actual wedding dinner!” said Danglars.", "\"그렇다면 우리가 그저 약혼 잔치인 줄 알았던 게 사실은 진짜 결혼 만찬이었군!\" 당글라르가 말했다."]], [["“No, no,”", "\"아니, 아니지.\""], ["answered Dantès; “don’t imagine I am going to put you off in that shabby manner.", "당테스가 대답했다. \"내가 자네들을 그렇게 야박하게 떼어 보내리라고는 생각지 말게."], ["Tomorrow morning I start for Paris; four days to go, and the same to return, with one day to discharge the commission entrusted to me, is all the time I shall be absent.", "내일 아침 나는 파리로 떠나네. 가는 데 나흘, 오는 데 나흘, 거기다 내게 맡겨진 심부름을 처리하는 데 하루를 더하면, 그게 내가 자리를 비우는 시간 전부일세."], ["I shall be back here by the first of March, and on the second I give my real marriage feast.”", "3월 1일까지는 이곳에 돌아올 테고, 2일에는 진짜 결혼 잔치를 열겠네.\""]], [["This prospect of fresh festivity redoubled the hilarity of the guests to such a degree, that the elder Dantès, who, at the commencement of the repast, had commented upon the silence that prevailed, now found it difficult, amid the general din of voices, to obtain a moment’s tranquillity in which to drink to the health and prosperity of the bride and bridegroom.", "새로운 잔치가 또 있으리라는 기대에 손님들의 흥은 한층 더 달아올랐다. 그래서 식사가 시작될 무렵 침묵이 흐른다고 한마디 했던 늙은 당테스는, 이제 사방에서 떠들어대는 목소리들 틈에서 신랑 신부의 건강과 번영을 비는 건배를 위해 잠깐의 고요를 얻기조차 힘들 지경이었다."]], [["Dantès, perceiving the affectionate eagerness of his father, responded by a look of grateful pleasure; while Mercédès glanced at the clock and made an expressive gesture to Edmond.", "당테스는 아버지의 애정 어린 열의를 알아채고, 고마움과 기쁨이 담긴 눈길로 화답했다. 메르세데스는 시계를 흘끗 보고는 에드몽에게 의미심장한 몸짓을 했다."]], [["Around the table reigned that noisy hilarity which usually prevails at such a time among people sufficiently free from the demands of social position not to feel the trammels of etiquette.", "식탁 주위로는 이런 자리에서 으레 감도는 떠들썩한 흥겨움이 넘쳤다. 사회적 지위의 굴레에 매이지 않을 만큼 자유로운 사람들 사이에서나 볼 수 있는 흥이었다."], ["Such as at the commencement of the repast had not been able to seat themselves according to their inclination rose unceremoniously, and sought out more agreeable companions.", "식사가 시작될 때 마음에 드는 자리에 앉지 못했던 이들은 거리낌 없이 일어나 더 마음 맞는 짝을 찾아갔다."], ["Everybody talked at once, without waiting for a reply and each one seemed to be contented with expressing his or her own thoughts.", "모두가 대답을 기다리지도 않고 한꺼번에 떠들어댔으며, 저마다 제 생각을 입 밖에 내는 것만으로 만족하는 듯했다."]], [["Fernand’s paleness appeared to have communicated itself to Danglars.", "페르낭의 창백한 기운이 당글라르에게도 옮은 듯했다."], ["As for Fernand himself, he seemed to be enduring the tortures of the damned; unable to rest, he was among the first to quit the table, and, as though seeking to avoid the hilarious mirth that rose in such deafening sounds, he continued, in utter silence, to pace the farther end of the salon.", "페르낭 자신으로 말하자면, 그는 지옥에 떨어진 자의 고통을 견디는 것 같았다. 한자리에 가만히 있지 못하고 가장 먼저 식탁을 떠난 그는, 귀가 먹먹할 만큼 요란하게 솟구치는 흥겨운 웃음소리를 피하려는 듯, 완전한 침묵 속에서 홀의 저쪽 끝을 계속 서성였다."]], [["Caderousse approached him just as Danglars, whom Fernand seemed most anxious to avoid, had joined him in a corner of the room.", "페르낭이 한사코 피하려는 눈치였던 당글라르가 방 한구석에서 그에게 다가가던 바로 그때, 카드루스가 그쪽으로 걸어왔다."]], [["“Upon my word,”", "\"맹세코,\""], ["said Caderousse, from whose mind the friendly treatment of Dantès, united with the effect of the excellent wine he had partaken of, had effaced every feeling of envy or jealousy at Dantès’ good fortune,—“upon my word, Dantès is a downright good fellow, and when I see him sitting there beside his pretty wife that is so soon to be.", "카드루스가 말했다. 그의 마음속에서는 당테스의 다정한 대접과 실컷 마신 좋은 포도주의 기운이 어우러져, 당테스의 행운에 대한 시샘이나 질투의 감정이 말끔히 지워진 터였다. \"맹세코, 당테스는 정말이지 더없이 좋은 친구야."], ["I cannot help thinking it would have been a great pity to have served him that trick you were planning yesterday.”", "곧 자기 아내가 될 저 어여쁜 색시 곁에 앉아 있는 걸 보면, 어제 자네가 꾸미던 그런 장난을 저 친구에게 쳤더라면 정말 못할 짓이었겠다 싶은 생각이 절로 든다니까.\""]], [["“Oh, there was no harm meant,”", "\"아, 나쁜 뜻은 없었네.\""], ["answered Danglars; “at first I certainly did feel somewhat uneasy as to what Fernand might be tempted to do; but when I saw how completely he had mastered his feelings, even so far as to become one of his rival’s attendants, I knew there was no further cause for apprehension.”", "당글라르가 대답했다. \"처음에는 페르낭이 무슨 짓을 저지를까 솔직히 좀 불안하긴 했어. 하지만 그가 제 감정을 완전히 다스려, 연적의 들러리 노릇까지 하는 걸 보고 나니 더는 걱정할 게 없다는 걸 알았지.\""], ["Caderousse looked full at Fernand—he was ghastly pale.", "카드루스가 페르낭을 똑바로 바라보았다. 그는 끔찍하게 창백했다."]], [["“Certainly,”", "\"하긴,\""], ["continued Danglars, “the sacrifice was no trifling one, when the beauty of the bride is concerned.", "당글라르가 말을 이었다. \"신부가 저렇게 아리따우니, 그 희생이 결코 가벼운 건 아니었지."], ["Upon my soul, that future captain of mine is a lucky dog! Gad!", "정말이지, 앞으로 내 선장이 될 저 친구는 운수 좋은 녀석이야! 이런!"], ["I only wish he would let me take his place.”", "그가 나더러 자기 자리를 대신하라고 해 주기만 한다면 좋으련만.\""]], [["“Shall we not set forth?”", "\"이제 출발하지 않으실 거예요?\""], ["asked the sweet, silvery voice of Mercédès; “two o’clock has just struck, and you know we are expected in a quarter of an hour.” 0071m", "메르세데스의 곱고 은쟁반 같은 목소리가 물었다. \"방금 두 시 종이 울렸어요. 다들 아시다시피 십오 분 뒤에 우리를 기다리고 있잖아요.\""]], [["“To be sure!—to be sure!”", "\"그렇고말고! 그렇고말고!\""], ["cried Dantès, eagerly quitting the table; “let us go directly!”", "당테스가 신이 나서 식탁에서 일어나며 외쳤다. \"어서 갑시다!\""]], [["His words were re-echoed by the whole party, with vociferous cheers.", "그의 말은 손님들 전체가 우렁찬 환호로 받아 메아리쳤다."]], [["At this moment Danglars, who had been incessantly observing every change in Fernand’s look and manner, saw him stagger and fall back, with an almost convulsive spasm, against a seat placed near one of the open windows.", "바로 그 순간, 페르낭의 표정과 태도의 변화를 줄곧 살피고 있던 당글라르는, 그가 비틀거리다가 거의 경련하듯 발작을 일으키며 열린 창문 곁에 놓인 의자에 쓰러지듯 기대는 것을 보았다."], ["At the same instant his ear caught a sort of indistinct sound on the stairs, followed by the measured tread of soldiery, with the clanking of swords and military accoutrements; then came a hum and buzz as of many voices, so as to deaden even the noisy mirth of the bridal party, among whom a vague feeling of curiosity and apprehension quelled every disposition to talk, and almost instantaneously the most deathlike stillness prevailed.", "같은 순간 그의 귀에 계단 쪽에서 무언가 어렴풋한 소리가 들렸고, 뒤이어 군화의 박자 맞춘 발소리와 칼, 군장이 부딪치는 쩔그렁 소리가 들려왔다. 그러고는 여럿이 웅성거리는 듯한 소리가 일어, 혼인 잔치의 떠들썩한 흥겨움마저 잦아들게 했다. 손님들 사이에서는 막연한 호기심과 불안이 떠들고 싶은 마음을 모조리 짓눌렀고, 거의 순식간에 죽음 같은 정적이 감돌았다."]], [["The sounds drew nearer.", "소리가 점점 가까워졌다."], ["Three blows were struck upon the panel of the door.", "문짝을 세 번 두드리는 소리가 났다."], ["The company looked at each other in consternation.", "손님들은 서로 얼굴을 바라보며 어쩔 줄 몰라 했다."]], [["“I demand admittance,”", "\"문을 여시오.\""], ["said a loud voice outside the room, “in the name of the law!”", "방 밖에서 우렁찬 목소리가 말했다. \"법의 이름으로!\""], ["As no attempt was made to prevent it, the door was opened, and a magistrate, wearing his official scarf, presented himself, followed by four soldiers and a corporal.", "아무도 막으려 하지 않자, 문이 열리며 관복 띠를 두른 사법관이 모습을 드러냈고, 그 뒤로 병사 넷과 하사 하나가 따랐다."], ["Uneasiness now yielded to the most extreme dread on the part of those present.", "자리에 있던 사람들의 불안은 이제 더없이 극심한 공포로 바뀌었다."]], [["“May I venture to inquire the reason of this unexpected visit?” said M.", "\"이 뜻밖의 방문이 무슨 까닭인지 여쭤도 되겠습니까?\""], ["Morrel, addressing the magistrate, whom he evidently knew; “there is doubtless some mistake easily explained.”", "모렐 씨가 사법관에게 물었다. 그는 분명 그를 아는 사이였다. \"필시 쉽게 풀릴 무슨 착오가 있는 것이겠지요.\""]], [["“If it be so,”", "\"만약 그렇다면,\""], ["replied the magistrate, “rely upon every reparation being made; meanwhile, I am the bearer of an order of arrest, and although I most reluctantly perform the task assigned me, it must, nevertheless, be fulfilled.", "사법관이 대답했다. \"남김없이 바로잡힐 거라 믿으셔도 좋습니다. 그동안 저는 체포 명령을 집행하러 왔고, 비록 제게 맡겨진 이 일이 더없이 내키지 않지만, 그래도 이행해야 합니다."], ["Who among the persons here assembled answers to the name of Edmond Dantès?”", "여기 모이신 분들 가운데 에드몽 당테스라는 이름을 쓰는 분이 누구입니까?\""]], [["Every eye was turned towards the young man who, spite of the agitation he could not but feel, advanced with dignity, and said, in a firm voice:", "모두의 시선이 젊은이에게로 쏠렸다. 그는 동요를 느끼지 않을 수 없으면서도 위엄 있게 앞으로 나서며, 굳은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“I am he; what is your pleasure with me?”", "\"제가 바로 그 사람입니다. 제게 무슨 볼일이십니까?\""]], [["“Edmond Dantès,”", "\"에드몽 당테스,\""], ["replied the magistrate, “I arrest you in the name of the law!”", "사법관이 대답했다. \"법의 이름으로 당신을 체포합니다!\""]], [["“Me!”", "\"저를요!\""], ["repeated Edmond, slightly changing color, “and wherefore, I pray?”", "에드몽이 약간 안색이 변하며 되뇌었다. \"대체 무슨 까닭입니까?\""]], [["“I cannot inform you, but you will be duly acquainted with the reasons that have rendered such a step necessary at the preliminary examination.”", "\"제가 알려 드릴 수는 없습니다. 다만 예심에서 이러한 조치가 필요해진 이유를 정식으로 알게 되실 겁니다.\""]], [["M. Morrel felt that further resistance or remonstrance was useless.", "모렐 씨는 더 버티거나 항의해 봐야 소용없음을 깨달았다."], ["He saw before him an officer delegated to enforce the law, and perfectly well knew that it would be as unavailing to seek pity from a magistrate decked with his official scarf, as to address a petition to some cold marble effigy. Old Dantès, however, sprang forward.", "그의 앞에는 법을 집행하도록 위임받은 관리가 있었고, 관복 띠를 두른 사법관에게 동정을 구하는 것은 차디찬 대리석상에 탄원하는 것만큼이나 부질없음을 그는 너무도 잘 알았다. 그러나 늙은 당테스는 앞으로 뛰쳐나갔다."], ["There are situations which the heart of a father or a mother cannot be made to understand.", "아버지나 어머니의 마음으로는 도저히 헤아릴 수 없는 상황이라는 게 있는 법이다."], ["He prayed and supplicated in terms so moving, that even the officer was touched, and, although firm in his duty, he kindly said, “My worthy friend, let me beg of you to calm your apprehensions.", "그는 어찌나 절절한 말로 빌고 애원했던지, 관리마저 마음이 움직일 정도였다. 그는 직무에는 단호했지만, 친절하게 말했다. \"친애하는 분, 부디 마음을 가라앉히시지요."], ["Your son has probably neglected some prescribed form or attention in registering his cargo, and it is more than probable he will be set at liberty directly he has given the information required, whether touching the health of his crew, or the value of his freight.”", "아드님은 아마 화물을 등록하면서 정해진 어떤 형식이나 절차를 빠뜨렸을 겁니다. 선원들의 건강에 관해서든 화물의 가치에 관해서든, 요구되는 정보를 제출하기만 하면 곧바로 풀려날 가능성이 큽니다.\""]], [["“What is the meaning of all this?”", "\"이게 다 무슨 영문이오?\""], ["inquired Caderousse, frowningly, of Danglars, who had assumed an air of utter surprise.", "카드루스가 잔뜩 놀란 표정을 지어 보이는 당글라르에게 인상을 찌푸리며 물었다."]], [["0073m\n“How can I tell you?”", "\"낸들 어떻게 알겠나?\""], ["replied he; “I am, like yourself, utterly bewildered at all that is going on, and cannot in the least make out what it is about.”", "그가 대답했다. \"나도 자네처럼 이게 다 무슨 일인지 도무지 어리둥절할 뿐, 무슨 영문인지 조금도 짚이는 게 없네.\""], ["Caderousse then looked around for Fernand, but he had disappeared.", "그러자 카드루스는 페르낭을 찾아 주위를 둘러보았다. 그러나 그는 사라지고 없었다."]], [["The scene of the previous night now came back to his mind with startling clearness.", "그 순간 지난밤의 장면이 놀랍도록 또렷하게 그의 머릿속으로 되돌아왔다."], ["The painful catastrophe he had just witnessed appeared effectually to have rent away the veil which the intoxication of the evening before had raised between himself and his memory.", "방금 목격한 가슴 아픈 변고가, 전날 밤의 취기가 그와 기억 사이에 드리워 놓았던 장막을 완전히 찢어 버린 듯했다."]], [["“So, so,” said he, in a hoarse and choking voice, to Danglars, “this, then, I suppose, is a part of the trick you were concerting yesterday?", "\"그러니까, 그러니까,\" 그가 쉬고 막힌 목소리로 당글라르에게 말했다."], ["All I can say is, that if it be so, ’tis an ill turn, and well deserves to bring double evil on those who have projected it.”", "\"이게 결국, 어제 자네가 꾸미던 그 장난의 일부란 말이지? 내가 할 수 있는 말은, 정말 그렇다면 이건 고약한 짓이고, 그런 일을 꾀한 자들에게는 갑절의 화가 내려도 마땅하다는 거야.\""]], [["“Nonsense,”", "\"말도 안 되는 소리.\""], ["returned Danglars, “I tell you again I have nothing whatever to do with it; besides, you know very well that I tore the paper to pieces.”", "당글라르가 대꾸했다. \"다시 말하지만 나는 그 일과 아무 상관도 없네. 게다가 자네도 잘 알다시피, 내가 그 종이를 갈기갈기 찢어 버렸잖나.\""]], [["“No, you did not!”", "\"아니, 안 찢었어!\""], ["answered Caderousse, “you merely threw it by—I saw it lying in a corner.”", "카드루스가 대답했다. \"자네는 그냥 그걸 던져 버렸을 뿐이야. 한구석에 떨어져 있는 걸 내가 봤다고.\""]], [["“Hold your tongue, you fool!—what should you know about it?—why, you were drunk!”", "\"입 닥치게, 이 멍청아! 자네가 뭘 안다고 그래? 자넨 그때 취해 있었잖나!\""]], [["“Where is Fernand?”", "\"페르낭은 어디 있나?\""], ["inquired Caderousse.", "카드루스가 물었다."]], [["“How do I know?”", "\"낸들 어찌 아나?\""], ["replied Danglars; “gone, as every prudent man ought to be, to look after his own affairs, most likely.", "당글라르가 대답했다. \"신중한 사람이라면 누구나 그러듯, 제 일을 챙기러 갔겠지."], ["Never mind where he is, let you and I go and see what is to be done for our poor friends.”", "어디 있든 신경 쓰지 말고, 자네하고 나하고 우리 가엾은 친구들을 위해 뭘 할 수 있을지나 보러 가세.\""]], [["During this conversation, Dantès, after having exchanged a cheerful shake of the hand with all his sympathizing friends, had surrendered himself to the officer sent to arrest him, merely saying, “Make yourselves quite easy, my good fellows, there is some little mistake to clear up, that’s all, depend upon it; and very likely I may not have to go so far as the prison to effect that.” 0075m", "이런 대화가 오가는 동안, 당테스는 동정 어린 벗들 모두와 정겹게 손을 맞잡고 나서, 자신을 체포하러 온 관리에게 순순히 몸을 맡겼다. 그저 이렇게 말할 뿐이었다. \"마음들 푹 놓게, 친구들. 풀어야 할 자그마한 착오가 있을 뿐이야, 그게 전부니까 믿어 주게. 어쩌면 그걸 풀려고 감옥까지 갈 것도 없을지 몰라.\""]], [["“Oh, to be sure!”", "\"오, 그렇고말고!\""], ["responded Danglars, who had now approached the group, “nothing more than a mistake, I feel quite certain.”", "어느새 무리 곁으로 다가온 당글라르가 거들었다. \"착오일 뿐이야, 나는 확신하네.\""]], [["Dantès descended the staircase, preceded by the magistrate, and followed by the soldiers.", "당테스는 사법관을 앞세우고 병사들을 뒤따르게 한 채 계단을 내려갔다."], ["A carriage awaited him at the door; he got in, followed by two soldiers and the magistrate, and the vehicle drove off towards Marseilles.", "문 앞에는 마차가 기다리고 있었다. 그가 올라타자 병사 둘과 사법관이 뒤따라 탔고, 마차는 마르세유를 향해 달려갔다."]], [["“Adieu, adieu, dearest Edmond!”", "\"안녕, 안녕, 사랑하는 에드몽!\""], ["cried Mercédès, stretching out her arms to him from the balcony.", "메르세데스가 발코니에서 두 팔을 그에게 뻗으며 외쳤다."]], [["The prisoner heard the cry, which sounded like the sob of a broken heart, and leaning from the coach he called out, “Good-bye, Mercédès—we shall soon meet again!”", "죄수는 그 외침을 들었다. 그것은 찢긴 가슴이 터뜨리는 흐느낌처럼 들렸다. 그는 마차 밖으로 몸을 내밀고 소리쳤다."], ["Then the vehicle disappeared round one of the turnings of Fort Saint Nicholas.", "\"잘 있어요, 메르세데스. 우리 곧 다시 만납시다!\" 그러고는 마차가 생니콜라 요새의 모퉁이 하나를 돌아 사라졌다."]], [["“Wait for me here, all of you!”", "\"다들 여기서 나를 기다려 주시오!\""], ["cried M. Morrel; “I will take the first conveyance I find, and hurry to Marseilles, whence I will bring you word how all is going on.”", "모렐 씨가 외쳤다. \"내가 제일 먼저 눈에 띄는 마차를 잡아타고 마르세유로 서둘러 가서, 일이 어떻게 돌아가는지 소식을 가져오겠소.\""]], [["“That’s right!”", "\"옳습니다!\""], ["exclaimed a multitude of voices, “go, and return as quickly as you can!”", "여러 목소리가 한꺼번에 외쳤다. \"어서 다녀오세요, 되도록 빨리요!\""]], [["This second departure was followed by a long and fearful state of terrified silence on the part of those who were left behind.", "이 두 번째 출발 뒤로, 남겨진 사람들 사이에는 겁에 질린 침묵이 길고도 무섭게 이어졌다."], ["The old father and Mercédès remained for some time apart, each absorbed in grief; but at length the two poor victims of the same blow raised their eyes, and with a simultaneous burst of feeling rushed into each other’s arms.", "늙은 아버지와 메르세데스는 한동안 따로 떨어진 채 저마다 슬픔에 잠겨 있었다. 그러나 마침내 같은 일격에 쓰러진 두 가엾은 희생자는 눈을 들었고, 동시에 북받치는 감정으로 서로의 품에 뛰어들었다."]], [["Meanwhile Fernand made his appearance, poured out for himself a glass of water with a trembling hand; then hastily swallowing it, went to sit down at the first vacant place, and this was, by mere chance, placed next to the seat on which poor Mercédès had fallen half fainting, when released from the warm and affectionate embrace of old Dantès.", "그사이 페르낭이 모습을 드러내, 떨리는 손으로 물을 한 잔 따랐다. 그러고는 황급히 들이켜고 가장 가까운 빈자리에 가서 앉았는데, 그곳은 우연히도 가엾은 메르세데스가 앉은 자리 바로 옆이었다. 그녀는 늙은 당테스의 따뜻하고 다정한 포옹에서 풀려나자 반쯤 정신을 잃고 그 자리에 쓰러져 있었다."], ["Instinctively Fernand drew back his chair.", "페르낭은 본능적으로 의자를 뒤로 물렸다."]], [["“He is the cause of all this misery—I am quite sure of it,”", "\"저자가 이 모든 불행의 원흉이야. 나는 그걸 확신하네.\""], ["whispered Caderousse, who had never taken his eyes off Fernand, to Danglars.", "페르낭에게서 한순간도 눈을 떼지 않던 카드루스가 당글라르에게 속삭였다."]], [["“I don’t think so,”", "\"난 그렇게 생각 안 해.\""], ["answered the other; “he’s too stupid to imagine such a scheme.", "상대가 대답했다. \"저 친구는 그런 계략을 짜내기엔 너무 멍청해."], ["I only hope the mischief will fall upon the head of whoever wrought it.”", "나는 그저 그 짓을 꾸민 자가 누구든 그 머리 위로 화가 떨어지기만 바랄 뿐이야.\""]], [["“You don’t mention those who aided and abetted the deed,”", "\"그 일을 거들고 부추긴 자들은 입에 올리지 않는군.\""], ["said Caderousse.", "카드루스가 말했다."]], [["“Surely,”", "\"이보게,\""], ["answered Danglars, “one cannot be held responsible for every chance arrow shot into the air.”", "당글라르가 대답했다. \"허공에 쏘아 올린 화살이 어디로 떨어지든 그 책임을 다 질 수야 없는 노릇 아닌가.\""]], [["“You can, indeed, when the arrow lights point downward on somebody’s head.”", "\"질 수 있지, 그 화살촉이 아래로 떨어져 누군가의 머리에 꽂힌다면 말이야.\""]], [["Meantime the subject of the arrest was being canvassed in every different form.", "그러는 동안 체포 사건은 온갖 형태로 입에 오르내리고 있었다."]], [["“What think you, Danglars,” said one of the party, turning towards him, “of this event?”", "\"당글라르, 자네는 이 일을 어떻게 보나?\" 일행 가운데 한 사람이 그를 돌아보며 물었다."]], [["“Why,”", "\"글쎄,\""], ["replied he, “I think it just possible Dantès may have been detected with some trifling article on board ship considered here as contraband.”", "그가 대답했다. \"내 생각엔 당테스가 배에 여기서 금제품으로 여겨질 만한 자그마한 물건을 싣고 있다가 들켰을 가능성이 있어 보여.\""]], [["“But how could he have done so without your knowledge, Danglars, since you are the ship’s supercargo?”", "\"하지만 자네가 배의 회계 책임자인데, 어떻게 자네 모르게 그런 짓을 할 수 있었겠나, 당글라르?\""]], [["“Why, as for that, I could only know what I was told respecting the merchandise with which the vessel was laden.", "\"뭐, 그거야, 나는 배에 실린 상품에 관해 들은 것만 알 뿐이지."], ["I know she was loaded with cotton, and that she took in her freight at Alexandria from Pastret’s warehouse, and at Smyrna from Pascal’s; that is all I was obliged to know, and I beg I may not be asked for any further particulars.”", "면화를 실었다는 것, 그 화물을 알렉산드리아에서는 파스트레 창고에서, 스미르나에서는 파스칼 창고에서 실었다는 것, 그게 내가 알아야 할 전부였고, 더 자세한 건 묻지 말아 주길 바라네.\""]], [["“Now I recollect,”", "\"이제 생각나는군.\""], ["said the afflicted old father; “my poor boy told me yesterday he had got a small case of coffee, and another of tobacco for me!”", "비탄에 잠긴 늙은 아버지가 말했다. \"내 가엾은 아들이 어제 그러더군, 나한테 줄 자그마한 커피 한 상자랑 담배 한 상자를 가져왔다고!\""]], [["“There, you see,”", "\"거 보세요.\""], ["exclaimed Danglars.", "당글라르가 외쳤다."], ["“Now the mischief is out; depend upon it the custom-house people went rummaging about the ship in our absence, and discovered poor Dantès’ hidden treasures.”", "\"이제 화근이 드러났군요. 분명 우리가 없는 사이에 세관 사람들이 배를 샅샅이 뒤지다가 가엾은 당테스가 감춰 둔 보물을 찾아낸 겁니다.\""]], [["Mercédès, however, paid no heed to this explanation of her lover’s arrest.", "그러나 메르세데스는 연인의 체포에 대한 이런 설명에 조금도 귀 기울이지 않았다."], ["Her grief, which she had hitherto tried to restrain, now burst out in a violent fit of hysterical sobbing.", "그때까지 억누르려 애써 온 그녀의 슬픔이, 이제 격렬한 흐느낌 발작으로 터져 나왔다."]], [["“Come, come,”", "\"자, 자,\""], ["said the old man, “be comforted, my poor child; there is still hope!”", "노인이 말했다. \"기운을 내거라, 가엾은 것아. 아직 희망이 있단다!\""]], [["“Hope!” repeated Danglars.", "\"희망이라!\" 당글라르가 되뇌었다."]], [["“Hope!”", "\"희망이라……\""], ["faintly murmured Fernand, but the word seemed to die away on his pale agitated lips, and a convulsive spasm passed over his countenance.", "페르낭이 희미하게 중얼거렸다. 그러나 그 말은 창백하게 떨리는 입술 위에서 그대로 잦아드는 듯했고, 경련의 발작이 그의 얼굴을 스쳐 지나갔다."]], [["“Good news!", "\"좋은 소식이오!"], ["good news!”", "좋은 소식!\""], ["shouted forth one of the party stationed in the balcony on the lookout.", "발코니에서 망을 보던 일행 가운데 한 사람이 큰 소리로 외쳤다."], ["“Here comes M. Morrel back.", "\"모렐 씨가 돌아오시는군."], ["No doubt, now, we shall hear that our friend is released!”", "이제 틀림없이 우리 친구가 풀려났다는 소식을 듣게 될 거요!\""]], [["Mercédès and the old man rushed to meet the shipowner and greeted him at the door. He was very pale.", "메르세데스와 노인이 선주를 맞으러 달려가, 문간에서 그를 맞았다. 그는 몹시 창백했다."]], [["“What news?” exclaimed a general burst of voices.", "\"무슨 소식입니까?\" 여러 목소리가 한꺼번에 터져 나왔다."]], [["“Alas, my friends,”", "\"아아, 여러분.\""], ["replied M. Morrel, with a mournful shake of his head, “the thing has assumed a more serious aspect than I expected.”", "모렐 씨가 슬프게 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"내가 생각했던 것보다 일이 더 심각해졌소.\""]], [["“Oh, indeed—indeed, sir, he is innocent!”", "\"오, 정말로, 정말로요, 선주님, 그이는 죄가 없어요!\""], ["sobbed forth Mercédès.", "메르세데스가 흐느끼며 말했다."]], [["“That I believe!”", "\"나도 그렇게 믿소!\""], ["answered M. Morrel; “but still he is charged——”", "모렐 씨가 대답했다. \"하지만 그래도 그는 혐의를 받고 있소……\""]], [["“With what?” inquired the elder Dantès.", "\"무슨 혐의요?\" 늙은 당테스가 물었다."]], [["“With being an agent of the Bonapartist faction!”", "\"보나파르트파의 첩자라는 혐의요!\""], ["Many of our readers may be able to recollect how formidable such an accusation became in the period at which our story is dated.", "우리 이야기가 펼쳐지는 시대에 그런 고발이 얼마나 무시무시한 것이었는지, 독자들 가운데 많은 분이 기억할 수 있을 것이다."]], [["A despairing cry escaped the pale lips of Mercédès; the old man sank into a chair.", "메르세데스의 창백한 입술에서 절망의 외침이 새어 나왔다. 노인은 의자에 털썩 주저앉았다."]], [["“Ah, Danglars!”", "\"아, 당글라르!\" 카드루스가 속삭였다."], ["whispered Caderousse, “you have deceived me—the trick you spoke of last night has been played; but I cannot suffer a poor old man or an innocent girl to die of grief through your fault.", "\"자네가 나를 속였어. 어젯밤 자네가 말한 그 장난이 결국 벌어진 거야. 하지만 나는 자네 잘못 때문에 가엾은 노인이나 죄 없는 처녀가 슬픔으로 죽어 가는 걸 그냥 두고 볼 수는 없네."], ["I am determined to tell them all about it.”", "모든 걸 다 털어놓고 말겠어.\""]], [["“Be silent, you simpleton!”", "\"입 닥치게, 이 얼간이야!\""], ["cried Danglars, grasping him by the arm, “or I will not answer even for your own safety.", "당글라르가 그의 팔을 움켜쥐며 외쳤다. \"안 그러면 자네 목숨도 내가 책임 못 져."], ["Who can tell whether Dantès be innocent or guilty?", "당테스가 죄가 없는지 있는지 누가 알겠나?"], ["The vessel did touch at Elba, where he quitted it, and passed a whole day in the island.", "배는 분명 엘바섬에 들렀고, 거기서 당테스가 내려 섬에서 꼬박 하루를 보냈어."], ["Now, should any letters or other documents of a compromising character be found upon him, will it not be taken for granted that all who uphold him are his accomplices?”", "자, 그에게서 무슨 편지나 그 밖에 위험한 문서라도 나온다면, 그를 두둔하는 자들은 모조리 한패로 여겨지지 않겠나?\""]], [["With the rapid instinct of selfishness, Caderousse readily perceived the solidity of this mode of reasoning; he gazed, doubtfully, wistfully, on Danglars, and then caution supplanted generosity.", "이기심에서 비롯된 빠른 직감으로, 카드루스는 이내 그 논리의 단단함을 알아챘다. 그는 미심쩍은 듯, 안타까운 듯 당글라르를 바라보았다. 그러고는 조심성이 의협심을 밀어냈다."]], [["“Suppose we wait a while, and see what comes of it,”", "\"잠시 기다리면서 어떻게 되어 가는지 두고 보세.\""], ["said he, casting a bewildered look on his companion.", "그가 어리둥절한 눈길로 동료를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“To be sure!”", "\"그렇고말고!\""], ["answered Danglars.", "당글라르가 대답했다."], ["“Let us wait, by all means.", "\"무슨 일이 있어도 기다려 보세."], ["If he be innocent, of course he will be set at liberty; if guilty, why, it is no use involving ourselves in a conspiracy.”", "그가 죄가 없다면 당연히 풀려날 테고, 죄가 있다면, 뭐, 우리가 음모에 휘말려 봐야 좋을 게 없지.\""]], [["“Let us go, then.", "\"그럼 가세."], ["I cannot stay here any longer.”", "나는 더 이상 여기 못 있겠어.\""]], [["“With all my heart!”", "\"기꺼이 그러지!\""], ["replied Danglars, pleased to find the other so tractable.", "당글라르가 상대가 이렇게 고분고분한 게 흡족해서 대답했다."], ["“Let us take ourselves out of the way, and leave things for the present to take their course.”", "\"우리는 슬그머니 빠져나가고, 일은 당분간 흘러가는 대로 내버려 두세.\""]], [["After their departure, Fernand, who had now again become the friend and protector of Mercédès, led the girl to her home, while some friends of Dantès conducted his father, nearly lifeless, to the Allées de Meilhan.", "두 사람이 떠난 뒤, 다시 메르세데스의 벗이자 보호자가 된 페르낭은 처녀를 집까지 데려다주었고, 당테스의 벗 몇몇은 거의 송장이나 다름없는 그의 아버지를 메이앙 가로수길까지 모셔 갔다."]], [["The rumor of Edmond’s arrest as a Bonapartist agent was not slow in circulating throughout the city.", "에드몽이 보나파르트파 첩자로 체포되었다는 소문은 어느새 도시 전체로 퍼져 나갔다."]], [["“Could you ever have credited such a thing, my dear Danglars?” asked M.", "\"이런 일이 있으리라고 자네는 짐작이나 했겠나, 친애하는 당글라르?\""], ["Morrel, as, on his return to the port for the purpose of gleaning fresh tidings of Dantès, from M. de Villefort, the assistant procureur, he overtook his supercargo and Caderousse.", "모렐 씨가 물었다. 그는 차장검사 빌포르 씨에게서 당테스에 관한 새 소식을 알아보려고 항구로 돌아오던 길에, 회계 책임자와 카드루스를 따라잡은 참이었다."], ["“Could you have believed such a thing possible?”", "\"이런 일이 가능하리라고 믿을 수 있었겠나?\""]], [["“Why, you know I told you,”", "\"뭐, 제가 말씀드렸잖습니까.\""], ["replied Danglars, “that I considered the circumstance of his having anchored at the Island of Elba as a very suspicious circumstance.”", "당글라르가 대답했다. \"그가 엘바섬에 닻을 내렸다는 게 무척 수상한 정황이라고 여겼다고 말입니다.\""]], [["“And did you mention these suspicions to any person beside myself?”", "\"그런데 그 의심을 나 말고 다른 사람에게도 이야기했나?\""]], [["0079m\n“Certainly not!” returned Danglars.", "\"천만에요!\" 당글라르가 대답했다."], ["Then added in a low whisper, “You understand that, on account of your uncle, M. Policar Morrel, who served under the _other_ government, and who does not altogether conceal what he thinks on the subject, you are strongly suspected of regretting the abdication of Napoleon.", "그러더니 낮은 목소리로 속삭이듯 덧붙였다. \"아시겠지만, 다른 정부 아래에서 복무하셨고 그 일에 대해 자기 생각을 그다지 숨기지 않으시는 백부님, 폴리카르 모렐 씨 때문에, 선주님께서 나폴레옹의 퇴위를 아쉬워하신다는 의심을 단단히 받고 계십니다."], ["I should have feared to injure both Edmond and yourself, had I divulged my own apprehensions to a soul.", "제가 제 우려를 누구한테든 발설했다면, 에드몽과 선주님 두 분 모두에게 해를 끼쳤을까 봐 두려웠던 거지요."], ["I am too well aware that though a subordinate, like myself, is bound to acquaint the shipowner with everything that occurs, there are many things he ought most carefully to conceal from all else.”", "저야 저 같은 아랫사람은 일어나는 모든 일을 선주께 알릴 의무가 있지만, 그 밖의 사람들에게는 더없이 조심해서 감춰야 할 일들이 많다는 걸 너무도 잘 알고 있으니까요.\""]], [["“’Tis well, Danglars—’tis well!”", "\"잘했네, 당글라르, 잘했어!\""], ["replied M. Morrel.", "모렐 씨가 대답했다."], ["“You are a worthy fellow; and I had already thought of your interests in the event of poor Edmond having become captain of the _Pharaon_.”", "\"자네는 쓸 만한 친구야. 가엾은 에드몽이 파라옹호의 선장이 될 경우에 대비해서, 나는 이미 자네 앞날도 생각해 두고 있었네.\""]], [["“Is it possible you were so kind?”", "\"그토록 마음 써 주셨단 말입니까?\""]], [["“Yes, indeed; I had previously inquired of Dantès what was his opinion of you, and if he should have any reluctance to continue you in your post, for somehow I have perceived a sort of coolness between you.”", "\"그래, 정말이지. 진작에 당테스에게 자네를 어떻게 생각하는지, 또 자네를 그 자리에 계속 두는 데 무슨 거리낌이라도 있는지 물어봤네. 어쩐지 자네들 사이에 일종의 서먹함이 느껴졌거든.\""]], [["“And what was his reply?”", "\"그래서 그가 뭐라고 대답하던가요?\""]], [["“That he certainly did think he had given you offence in an affair which he merely referred to without entering into particulars, but that whoever possessed the good opinion and confidence of the ship’s owners would have his preference also.”", "\"자기가 자세한 내막은 그저 넌지시 언급만 하고 들어가지 않은 어떤 일로 자네 기분을 상하게 한 것 같다고 분명히 인정하더군. 하지만 선주들의 호평과 신임을 받는 사람이라면 누구든 자기도 그를 우선시하겠다고 했네.\""]], [["“The hypocrite!”", "\"위선자 같으니!\""], ["murmured Danglars.", "당글라르가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Poor Dantès!”", "\"가엾은 당테스!\""], ["said Caderousse.", "카드루스가 말했다."], ["“No one can deny his being a noble-hearted young fellow.”", "\"그가 고결한 마음씨를 지닌 젊은이라는 건 누구도 부정 못 하지.\""]], [["“But meanwhile,” continued M.", "\"하지만 그러는 동안,\""], ["Morrel, “here is the _Pharaon_ without a captain.”", "모렐 씨가 말을 이었다. \"파라옹호는 선장이 없게 되었네.\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오,\""], ["replied Danglars, “since we cannot leave this port for the next three months, let us hope that ere the expiration of that period Dantès will be set at liberty.”", "당글라르가 대답했다. \"앞으로 석 달은 이 항구를 떠날 수 없으니, 그 기한이 다하기 전에 당테스가 풀려나기를 바라야지요.\""]], [["“No doubt; but in the meantime?”", "\"틀림없이 그래야겠지. 하지만 그동안은?\""]], [["“I am entirely at your service, M. Morrel,”", "\"저는 전적으로 선주님의 분부를 따르겠습니다, 모렐 씨.\""], ["answered Danglars.", "당글라르가 대답했다."], ["“You know that I am as capable of managing a ship as the most experienced captain in the service; and it will be so far advantageous to you to accept my services, that upon Edmond’s release from prison no further change will be requisite on board the _Pharaon_ than for Dantès and myself each to resume our respective posts.”", "\"아시다시피 저는 어느 노련한 선장 못지않게 배를 다룰 줄 압니다. 게다가 제 도움을 받아들이시면 이런 이점도 있지요. 에드몽이 감옥에서 풀려나면, 파라옹호에서는 당테스와 제가 각자 본래 자리로 돌아가기만 하면 될 뿐, 더 손볼 게 없으니까요.\""]], [["“Thanks, Danglars—that will smooth over all difficulties.", "\"고맙네, 당글라르. 그러면 모든 골칫거리가 매끄럽게 풀리겠군."], ["I fully authorize you at once to assume the command of the _Pharaon_, and look carefully to the unloading of her freight.", "자네에게 즉시 파라옹호의 지휘를 맡길 권한을 주겠네. 화물 하역을 빈틈없이 살펴 주게."], ["Private misfortunes must never be allowed to interfere with business.”", "개인적인 불행 때문에 일이 어그러지는 일은 결코 있어선 안 되니까.\""]], [["“Be easy on that score, M. Morrel; but do you think we shall be permitted to see our poor Edmond?”", "\"그 점은 마음 놓으십시오, 모렐 씨. 그런데 우리가 가엾은 에드몽을 만나 볼 수 있을 것 같습니까?\""]], [["“I will let you know that directly I have seen M.", "\"빌포르 씨를 만나는 대로 곧 알려 주겠네."], ["de Villefort, whom I shall endeavor to interest in Edmond’s favor.", "그를 에드몽 편에 서도록 설득해 볼 작정이야."], ["I am aware he is a furious royalist; but, in spite of that, and of his being king’s attorney, he is a man like ourselves, and I fancy not a bad sort of one.”", "그가 광적인 왕당파라는 건 나도 알지만, 그래도, 또 국왕의 검사라는 점에도 불구하고, 그도 우리 같은 사람이니, 그리 나쁜 사람은 아닐 거라고 보네.\""]], [["“Perhaps not,”", "\"아마 아니겠지요.\""], ["replied Danglars; “but I hear that he is ambitious, and that’s rather against him.”", "당글라르가 대답했다. \"하지만 듣자 하니 그가 야심가라더군요. 그 점은 좀 마음에 걸립니다.\""]], [["“Well, well,”", "\"뭐, 뭐,\""], ["returned M. Morrel, “we shall see.", "모렐 씨가 대꾸했다. \"두고 보면 알겠지."], ["But now hasten on board, I will join you there ere long.”", "자, 이제 어서 배로 가게. 나도 머지않아 그리로 가겠네.\""]], [["So saying, the worthy shipowner quitted the two allies, and proceeded in the direction of the Palais de Justice. 0081m", "이렇게 말하고 쓸 만한 선주는 두 동맹자를 떠나 재판소 쪽으로 걸어갔다."]], [["“You see,”", "\"보았나,\""], ["said Danglars, addressing Caderousse, “the turn things have taken.", "당글라르가 카드루스에게 말했다. \"일이 어떻게 돌아가는지."], ["Do you still feel any desire to stand up in his defence?”", "자네 아직도 그를 변호하겠다고 나설 마음이 있나?\""]], [["“Not the slightest, but yet it seems to me a shocking thing that a mere joke should lead to such consequences.”", "\"손톱만큼도 없네. 하지만 그래도 한낱 장난이 이런 결과를 부른다는 게 끔찍한 일로 여겨지는군.\""]], [["“But who perpetrated that joke, let me ask?", "\"그런데 그 장난을 저지른 게 누군지 묻고 싶네만?"], ["neither you nor myself, but Fernand; you knew very well that I threw the paper into a corner of the room—indeed, I fancied I had destroyed it.”", "자네도 나도 아니고 페르낭이야. 자네도 잘 알다시피 나는 그 종이를 방구석에 던져 버렸어. 사실 나는 그걸 없애 버린 줄로만 알았지.\""]], [["“Oh, no,”", "\"오, 아니야.\""], ["replied Caderousse, “that I can answer for, you did not.", "카드루스가 대답했다. \"그건 내가 장담할 수 있어, 자넨 그러지 않았어."], ["I only wish I could see it now as plainly as I saw it lying all crushed and crumpled in a corner of the arbor.”", "정자 구석에 구겨지고 짓이겨진 채 놓여 있던 그 종이가, 그때 봤던 것처럼 지금도 또렷이 보였으면 좋겠군.\""]], [["“Well, then, if you did, depend upon it, Fernand picked it up, and either copied it or caused it to be copied; perhaps, even, he did not take the trouble of recopying it.", "\"그래, 그렇다면, 분명 페르낭이 그걸 주워서 베껴 썼거나, 누구한테 베껴 쓰게 했을 거야. 어쩌면 다시 베껴 쓰는 수고조차 안 했을지도 몰라."], ["And now I think of it, by Heavens, he may have sent the letter itself!", "그러고 보니, 맙소사, 그 편지 그대로를 보내 버렸을 수도 있겠군!"], ["Fortunately, for me, the handwriting was disguised.”", "다행히 나로서는, 필체를 위장해 뒀으니까.\""]], [["“Then you were aware of Dantès being engaged in a conspiracy?”", "\"그럼 자네는 당테스가 음모에 가담했다는 걸 알고 있었단 말인가?\""]], [["“Not I.", "\"천만에."], ["As I before said, I thought the whole thing was a joke, nothing more.", "앞서 말했듯이 나는 그게 다 장난, 그 이상도 이하도 아니라고 생각했네."], ["It seems, however, that I have unconsciously stumbled upon the truth.”", "그런데 보아하니 내가 나도 모르게 우연히 진실을 짚어 버린 모양이야.\""]], [["“Still,”", "\"그래도,\""], ["argued Caderousse, “I would give a great deal if nothing of the kind had happened; or, at least, that I had had no hand in it.", "카드루스가 따졌다. \"이런 일이 아예 일어나지 않았더라면, 아니, 적어도 내가 거기 끼지만 않았더라면, 무엇을 줘서라도 그렇게 됐으면 싶네."], ["You will see, Danglars, that it will turn out an unlucky job for both of us.”", "두고 보게, 당글라르, 이건 우리 둘 모두에게 재수 없는 일로 판가름 날 거야.\""]], [["“Nonsense! If any harm come of it, it should fall on the guilty person; and that, you know, is Fernand.", "\"말도 안 되는 소리! 무슨 화가 닥친다 해도 그건 죄지은 자에게 떨어져야 마땅하고, 자네도 알다시피 그건 페르낭이야."], ["How can we be implicated in any way?", "우리가 무슨 수로 얽혀들겠나?"], ["All we have got to do is, to keep our own counsel, and remain perfectly quiet, not breathing a word to any living soul; and you will see that the storm will pass away without in the least affecting us.”", "우리가 해야 할 일이라곤 입을 꾹 다물고 쥐 죽은 듯 잠자코 있는 것, 살아 있는 누구에게도 한마디 흘리지 않는 것뿐이야. 그러면 두고 보게, 폭풍은 우리를 조금도 건드리지 않고 지나갈 테니.\""]], [["“Amen!”", "\"아멘!\""], ["responded Caderousse, waving his hand in token of adieu to Danglars, and bending his steps towards the Allées de Meilhan, moving his head to and fro, and muttering as he went, after the manner of one whose mind was overcharged with one absorbing idea.", "카드루스가 당글라르에게 작별의 표시로 손을 흔들며 대답했다. 그러고는 메이앙 가로수길 쪽으로 발걸음을 옮기며, 머리를 이리저리 흔들고 무언가 중얼거렸다. 하나의 강박에 사로잡혀 머릿속이 가득 찬 사람 같은 모습이었다."]], [["“So far, then,” said Danglars, mentally, “all has gone as I would have it.", "'그러니까 여기까지는,' 당글라르가 속으로 생각했다."], ["I am, temporarily, commander of the _Pharaon_, with the certainty of being permanently so, if that fool of a Caderousse can be persuaded to hold his tongue.", "'모든 게 내 뜻대로 풀린 셈이군. 나는 당장은 파라옹호의 지휘관이고, 저 멍청한 카드루스가 입을 다물도록 구슬릴 수만 있다면 그 자리를 영영 차지하게 될 게 확실해."], ["My only fear is the chance of Dantès being released.", "내가 두려운 건 오직 당테스가 풀려날 가능성뿐이야."], ["But, there, he is in the hands of Justice; and,”", "하지만, 흥, 그는 사법의 손에 들어갔고,'"], ["added he with a smile, “she will take her own.”", "그가 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다."], ["So saying, he leaped into a boat, desiring to be rowed on board the _Pharaon_, where M. Morrel had agreed to meet him.", "'사법은 제 몫을 챙길 테지.' 이렇게 중얼거리며 그는 보트에 뛰어올라, 모렐 씨가 만나기로 한 파라옹호로 저어 가 달라고 일렀다."]], [["# Chapter 6. The Deputy Procureur du Roi", "# Chapter 6. The Deputy Procureur du Roi"]], [["In one of the aristocratic mansions built by Puget in the Rue du Grand Cours opposite the Medusa fountain, a second marriage feast was being celebrated, almost at the same hour with the nuptial repast given by Dantès.", "메두사 분수 맞은편, 그랑쿠르 거리에 퓌제가 세운 귀족풍 저택 가운데 한 곳에서, 당테스가 베푼 약혼 잔치와 거의 같은 시각에 또 다른 약혼 잔치가 벌어지고 있었다."], ["In this case, however, although the occasion of the entertainment was similar, the company was strikingly dissimilar.", "그러나 이 경우에는, 잔치의 명분이야 비슷했어도 모인 사람들은 사뭇 달랐다."], ["Instead of a rude mixture of sailors, soldiers, and those belonging to the humblest grade of life, the present assembly was composed of the very flower of Marseilles society,—magistrates who had resigned their office during the usurper’s reign; officers who had deserted from the imperial army and joined forces with Condé; and younger members of families, brought up to hate and execrate the man whom five years of exile would convert into a martyr, and fifteen of restoration elevate to the rank of a god.", "선원과 군인, 그리고 가장 비천한 계급의 사람들이 거칠게 뒤섞이는 대신, 지금 이 자리에는 마르세유 사교계의 정수만이 모여 있었다. 찬탈자의 치세 동안 관직에서 물러난 행정관들, 제국군에서 탈영해 콩데 진영에 합류한 장교들, 그리고 오 년의 유배가 한 사람을 순교자로 만들고 십오 년의 왕정복고가 그를 신의 반열에 올려놓게 될, 바로 그 사람을 증오하고 저주하도록 길러진 가문의 젊은이들이었다."]], [["The guests were still at table, and the heated and energetic conversation that prevailed betrayed the violent and vindictive passions that then agitated each dweller of the South, where unhappily, for five centuries religious strife had long given increased bitterness to the violence of party feeling.", "손님들은 아직 식탁에 둘러앉아 있었고, 그 자리를 지배하던 격하고 열띤 대화는 당시 남부의 주민이라면 누구나 마음속에 품고 있던 격렬하고 앙심에 찬 격정을 고스란히 드러냈다. 안타깝게도 그곳에서는 오백 년 동안 이어진 종교 분쟁이 당파 감정의 폭력에 더한층 쓰라림을 더해 온 터였다."]], [["The emperor, now king of the petty Island of Elba, after having held sovereign sway over one-half of the world, counting as his subjects a small population of five or six thousand souls,—after having been accustomed to hear the “_Vive Napoléons_”", "세계의 절반을 통치하던 황제는, 이제 고작 대여섯 천 명의 백성을 거느린 작은 엘바섬의 왕으로 전락해 있었다. 열 개의 다른 언어로 외쳐 대는 일억 이천만 인류의 \"나폴레옹 만세\""], ["of a hundred and twenty millions of human beings, uttered in ten different languages,—was looked upon here as a ruined man, separated forever from any fresh connection with France or claim to her throne.", "소리를 듣는 데 익숙했던 그가, 이곳에서는 프랑스와의 새로운 인연이나 왕위에 대한 권리에서 영영 떨어져 나간, 몰락한 사내로 여겨졌다."]], [["The magistrates freely discussed their political views; the military part of the company talked unreservedly of Moscow and Leipsic, while the women commented on the divorce of Josephine.", "행정관들은 거리낌 없이 정치적 견해를 주고받았고, 좌중의 군인들은 모스크바와 라이프치히에 관해 스스럼없이 이야기를 나누었으며, 부인들은 조제핀의 이혼을 두고 입방아를 찧었다."], ["It was not over the downfall of the man, but over the defeat of the Napoleonic idea, that they rejoiced, and in this they foresaw for themselves the bright and cheering prospect of a revivified political existence.", "그들이 기뻐한 것은 한 사람의 몰락이 아니라 나폴레옹 사상의 패배였으며, 거기에서 그들은 되살아난 정치적 삶이라는 밝고 활기찬 앞날을 미리 그려 보았다."]], [["An old man, decorated with the cross of Saint Louis, now rose and proposed the health of King Louis XVIII.", "생루이 십자훈장을 단 한 노인이 일어서서 루이 십팔세의 건강을 위해 건배를 청했다."], ["It was the Marquis de Saint-Méran.", "생메랑 후작이었다."], ["This toast, recalling at once the patient exile of Hartwell and the peace-loving King of France, excited universal enthusiasm; glasses were elevated in the air _à l’Anglaise_, and the ladies, snatching their bouquets from their fair bosoms, strewed the table with their floral treasures.", "하트웰에서 참고 견딘 유배 생활과 평화를 사랑하는 프랑스 국왕을 한꺼번에 떠올리게 한 이 건배사는 좌중의 열광을 불러일으켰다. 잔이 영국식으로 허공에 치켜올려졌고, 부인들은 고운 가슴에서 꽃다발을 빼내어 식탁 위에 그 꽃의 보물들을 흩뿌렸다."], ["In a word, an almost poetical fervor prevailed.", "한마디로 거의 시적인 열기가 자리를 휘감았다."]], [["“Ah,” said the Marquise de Saint-Méran, a woman with a stern, forbidding eye, though still noble and distinguished in appearance, despite her fifty years—“ah, these revolutionists, who have driven us from those very possessions they afterwards purchased for a mere trifle during the Reign of Terror, would be compelled to own, were they here, that all true devotion was on our side, since we were content to follow the fortunes of a falling monarch, while they, on the contrary, made their fortune by worshipping the rising sun; yes, yes, they could not help admitting that the king, for whom we sacrificed rank, wealth, and station was truly our ‘Louis the well-beloved,’ while their wretched usurper has been, and ever will be, to them their evil genius, their ‘Napoleon the accursed.’ Am I not right, Villefort?”", "\"아,\" 생메랑 후작 부인이 입을 열었다. 그녀는 쉰 줄에 들어섰음에도 여전히 기품 있고 고상한 풍모를 지녔으나, 눈빛만은 매섭고 위압적이었다. \"아, 우리를 영지에서 내쫓고는 공포정치 때 그 땅을 헐값에 사들인 그 혁명파들이 여기 있었다면 인정할 수밖에 없었을 거예요. 진정한 충절은 모두 우리 편에 있었다고요. 우리는 몰락하는 군주의 운명을 기꺼이 따랐지만, 그들은 그와 반대로 떠오르는 태양을 숭배하며 제 잇속을 채웠으니 말이에요. 그래요, 그래, 그들도 인정하지 않을 수 없을 거예요. 우리가 지위와 재산과 신분을 바친 그 임금이야말로 진정 우리의 '사랑받는 루이'였고, 그들의 가련한 찬탈자는 그들에게 악령, 그들의 '저주받은 나폴레옹'이었으며 앞으로도 영영 그러하리라는 것을요. 내 말이 틀렸나요, 빌포르?\""]], [["“I beg your pardon, madame.", "\"용서하십시오, 부인."], ["I really must pray you to excuse me, but—in truth—I was not attending to the conversation.”", "정말이지 너그러이 봐주셔야겠습니다. 실은 제가 대화에 귀를 기울이지 못하고 있었습니다.\""]], [["“Marquise, marquise!”", "\"후작 부인, 후작 부인!\""], ["interposed the old nobleman who had proposed the toast, “let the young people alone; let me tell you, on one’s wedding day there are more agreeable subjects of conversation than dry politics.”", "건배를 청했던 노귀족이 끼어들었다. \"젊은 사람들은 좀 내버려 두구려. 내 말하지만, 혼례를 치르는 날에는 메마른 정치보다 훨씬 즐거운 이야깃거리가 많은 법이오.\""]], [["“Never mind, dearest mother,”", "\"신경 쓰지 마세요, 어머니.\""], ["said a young and lovely girl, with a profusion of light brown hair, and eyes that seemed to float in liquid crystal, “’tis all my fault for seizing upon M. de Villefort, so as to prevent his listening to what you said.", "연갈색 머리채가 탐스럽고, 두 눈은 맑은 수정 속에 떠 있는 듯한 젊고 어여쁜 아가씨가 말했다. \"제가 빌포르 씨를 붙들고 있어서 어머니 말씀을 못 듣게 한 거예요, 다 제 잘못이지요."], ["But there—now take him—he is your own for as long as you like. M.", "그러니 자, 이제 데려가세요. 원하시는 만큼 어머니 차지예요."], ["Villefort, I beg to remind you my mother speaks to you.”", "빌포르 씨, 어머니가 말씀하고 계신다는 걸 일러 드릴게요.\""]], [["“If the marquise will deign to repeat the words I but imperfectly caught, I shall be delighted to answer,”", "\"후작 부인께서 제가 제대로 듣지 못한 말씀을 다시 한번 들려주신다면, 기꺼이 대답해 올리겠습니다.\""], ["said M. de Villefort.", "빌포르가 말했다."]], [["“Never mind, Renée,”", "\"괜찮다, 르네.\""], ["replied the marquise, with a look of tenderness that seemed out of keeping with her harsh dry features; but, however all other feelings may be withered in a woman’s nature, there is always one bright smiling spot in the desert of her heart, and that is the shrine of maternal love.", "후작 부인이 대답했다. 메마르고 딱딱한 이목구비와는 어울리지 않는 다정한 눈빛이었다. 여인의 본성에서 다른 모든 감정이 시들어 버린다 해도, 그 마음의 사막에는 늘 밝게 미소 짓는 한 곳이 남아 있게 마련이니, 그것이 바로 모성애의 성소다."], ["“I forgive you.", "\"용서하마."], ["What I was saying, Villefort, was, that the Bonapartists had not our sincerity, enthusiasm, or devotion.”", "내가 하던 말은, 빌포르, 보나파르트파에게는 우리 같은 진심도, 열정도, 충절도 없었다는 거예요.\""]], [["“They had, however, what supplied the place of those fine qualities,”", "\"하지만 그들에게는 그 훌륭한 자질들을 대신할 만한 것이 있었지요.\""], ["replied the young man, “and that was fanaticism.", "청년이 대답했다. \"바로 광신입니다."], ["Napoleon is the Mahomet of the West, and is worshipped by his commonplace but ambitious followers, not only as a leader and lawgiver, but also as the personification of equality.”", "나폴레옹은 서양의 마호메트로, 평범하지만 야심에 찬 추종자들에게 지도자이자 입법자로서만이 아니라 평등의 화신으로 숭배받습니다.\""]], [["“He!”", "\"그가!\""], ["cried the marquise: “Napoleon the type of equality!", "후작 부인이 외쳤다. \"나폴레옹이 평등의 전형이라니!"], ["For mercy’s sake, then, what would you call Robespierre?", "그렇다면 제발 묻겠는데, 로베스피에르는 뭐라고 부르겠어요?"], ["Come, come, do not strip the latter of his just rights to bestow them on the Corsican, who, to my mind, has usurped quite enough.” 0085m", "자, 자, 로베스피에르에게서 그가 마땅히 누려야 할 권리를 빼앗아 그 코르시카인에게 안겨 주지 말아요. 내가 보기에 그자는 이미 충분히 많은 걸 찬탈했으니까.\""]], [["“Nay, madame; I would place each of these heroes on his right pedestal—that of Robespierre on his scaffold in the Place Louis Quinze; that of Napoleon on the column of the Place Vendôme.", "\"아닙니다, 부인. 저는 이 두 영웅을 각자 마땅한 받침대 위에 세우려는 것입니다. 로베스피에르는 루이 십오세 광장의 단두대 위에, 나폴레옹은 방돔 광장의 기둥 위에 말입니다."], ["The only difference consists in the opposite character of the equality advocated by these two men; one is the equality that elevates, the other is the equality that degrades; one brings a king within reach of the guillotine, the other elevates the people to a level with the throne.", "다만 두 사람이 내세운 평등의 성격이 정반대라는 점이 다를 뿐이지요. 하나는 끌어올리는 평등이고, 다른 하나는 끌어내리는 평등입니다. 하나는 왕을 단두대에 닿을 수 있는 데까지 끌어내리고, 다른 하나는 백성을 왕좌와 같은 높이로 끌어올립니다."], ["Observe,”", "보십시오.\""], ["said Villefort, smiling, “I do not mean to deny that both these men were revolutionary scoundrels, and that the 9th Thermidor and the 4th of April, in the year 1814, were lucky days for France, worthy of being gratefully remembered by every friend to monarchy and civil order; and that explains how it comes to pass that, fallen, as I trust he is forever, Napoleon has still retained a train of parasitical satellites.", "빌포르가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"저는 이 두 사람이 모두 혁명의 악당이었음을, 또 테르미도르 9일과 1814년 4월 4일이 프랑스에는 다행스러운 날이었으며 군주제와 시민 질서를 사랑하는 이라면 누구나 고맙게 기억할 만한 날이었음을 부인할 생각은 없습니다. 그래서 제가 영영 몰락했으리라 믿는 나폴레옹이 여전히 기생충 같은 추종자들을 거느리고 있다는 사실도 이로써 설명이 되는 것이지요."], ["Still, marquise, it has been so with other usurpers—Cromwell, for instance, who was not half so bad as Napoleon, had his partisans and advocates.”", "하지만 후작 부인, 다른 찬탈자들도 마찬가지였습니다. 가령 크롬웰만 해도 나폴레옹의 절반만큼도 악하지 않았지만, 그에게도 당파와 옹호자들이 있었으니까요.\""]], [["“Do you know, Villefort, that you are talking in a most dreadfully revolutionary strain?", "\"빌포르, 당신 지금 끔찍하리만치 혁명적인 어조로 말하고 있다는 걸 알기나 해요?"], ["But I excuse it, it is impossible to expect the son of a Girondin to be free from a small spice of the old leaven.”", "하지만 봐주겠어요. 지롱드파의 아들에게 그 낡은 효모의 잔재가 한 점도 없기를 바랄 수는 없는 노릇이니까.\""], ["A deep crimson suffused the countenance of Villefort.", "빌포르의 얼굴이 새빨갛게 물들었다."]], [["“’Tis true, madame,”", "\"사실입니다, 부인.\" 그가 대답했다."], ["answered he, “that my father was a Girondin, but he was not among the number of those who voted for the king’s death; he was an equal sufferer with yourself during the Reign of Terror, and had well-nigh lost his head on the same scaffold on which your father perished.”", "\"제 아버지가 지롱드파였던 것은 맞습니다. 하지만 국왕의 사형에 찬성표를 던진 자들 축에는 들지 않았습니다. 아버지도 공포정치 동안 부인과 마찬가지로 고초를 겪으셨고, 부인의 아버님이 목숨을 잃으신 바로 그 단두대에서 하마터면 머리를 잃을 뻔하셨지요.\""]], [["“True,” replied the marquise, without wincing in the slightest degree at the tragic remembrance thus called up; “but bear in mind, if you please, that our respective parents underwent persecution and proscription from diametrically opposite principles; in proof of which I may remark, that while my family remained among the staunchest adherents of the exiled princes, your father lost no time in joining the new government; and that while the Citizen Noirtier was a Girondin, the Count Noirtier became a senator.”", "\"맞아요.\" 후작 부인이 대답했다. 그렇게 떠오른 비극적 기억에도 그녀는 조금도 움찔하지 않았다. \"하지만 부디 명심하세요. 우리 양가의 부모는 정반대 원칙에서 박해와 추방을 당했답니다. 그 증거로 한마디 하자면, 우리 집안은 망명한 왕족들의 가장 굳건한 지지자로 남았던 반면, 당신 아버지는 지체 없이 새 정부에 가담했지요. 그리고 시민 누아르티에는 지롱드파였지만, 누아르티에 백작은 상원의원이 되었고요.\""]], [["“Dear mother,”", "\"어머니,\""], ["interposed Renée, “you know very well it was agreed that all these disagreeable reminiscences should forever be laid aside.”", "르네가 끼어들었다. \"이런 불쾌한 옛이야기는 영영 묻어 두기로 했잖아요. 잘 아시면서.\""]], [["“Suffer me, also, madame,”", "\"부인, 저 또한 간청드립니다.\""], ["replied Villefort, “to add my earnest request to Mademoiselle de Saint-Méran’s, that you will kindly allow the veil of oblivion to cover and conceal the past.", "빌포르가 말했다. \"생메랑 양의 청에 제 간절한 부탁을 보태도록 허락해 주십시오. 부디 망각의 장막으로 과거를 덮어 가려 주십시오."], ["What avails recrimination over matters wholly past recall? For my own part, I have laid aside even the name of my father, and altogether disown his political principles.", "이미 돌이킬 수 없는 일을 두고 서로 헐뜯어 봤자 무슨 소용이 있겠습니까? 저로 말하자면, 아버지의 이름조차 버렸고, 그분의 정치적 신념을 송두리째 부인하고 있습니다."], ["He was—nay, probably may still be—a Bonapartist, and is called Noirtier; I, on the contrary, am a staunch royalist, and style myself de Villefort.", "아버지는 보나파르트파였고 — 어쩌면 지금도 그러할 테고 — 누아르티에라 불립니다. 저는 그와 반대로 확고한 왕당파이며, 스스로를 드 빌포르라 칭합니다."], ["Let what may remain of revolutionary sap exhaust itself and die away with the old trunk, and condescend only to regard the young shoot which has started up at a distance from the parent tree, without having the power, any more than the wish, to separate entirely from the stock from which it sprung.”", "혁명의 수액이 무엇이든 남아 있다면 늙은 줄기와 함께 다 소진되어 시들어 사라지게 두십시오. 그리고 어미 나무에서 멀찍이 돋아난 어린 가지만을 굽어살펴 주십시오. 그 가지는 제가 뻗어 나온 그루터기로부터 완전히 떨어져 나갈 힘도, 그럴 뜻도 없는 것입니다.\""]], [["“Bravo, Villefort!”", "\"옳거니, 빌포르!\""], ["cried the marquis; “excellently well said!", "후작이 외쳤다. \"참으로 훌륭한 말이오!"], ["Come, now, I have hopes of obtaining what I have been for years endeavoring to persuade the marquise to promise; namely, a perfect amnesty and forgetfulness of the past.”", "자, 이제 나는 여러 해 동안 후작 부인에게 약속받으려 애써 온 것을 얻을 수 있겠다는 희망이 생기는구려. 다름 아니라, 과거에 대한 완전한 사면과 망각 말이오.\""]], [["“With all my heart,”", "\"기꺼이 그러지요.\""], ["replied the marquise; “let the past be forever forgotten.", "후작 부인이 대답했다. \"과거는 영영 잊기로 해요."], ["I promise you it affords _me_ as little pleasure to revive it as it does you.", "분명히 말하지만, 과거를 다시 들추는 일은 당신만큼이나 내게도 즐겁지 않으니까요."], ["All I ask is, that Villefort will be firm and inflexible for the future in his political principles.", "다만 내가 바라는 건, 빌포르가 앞으로 정치적 신념에서 굳건하고 흔들림이 없어야 한다는 것뿐이에요."], ["Remember, also, Villefort, that we have pledged ourselves to his majesty for your fealty and strict loyalty, and that at our recommendation the king consented to forget the past, as I do”", "그리고 빌포르, 이것도 잊지 말아요. 우리가 폐하께 당신의 충절과 철저한 충성을 두고 보증을 섰다는 것을, 그리고 우리의 천거로 폐하께서 과거를 잊기로 윤허하셨다는 것을요."], ["(and here she extended to him her hand)—“as I now do at your entreaty.", "내가 그러하듯이,\" 여기서 그녀는 그에게 손을 내밀었다."], ["But bear in mind, that should there fall in your way anyone guilty of conspiring against the government, you will be so much the more bound to visit the offence with rigorous punishment, as it is known you belong to a suspected family.”", "\"당신의 간청에 따라 지금 내가 그러하듯이 말이에요. 하지만 명심해요. 만일 정부에 맞서 음모를 꾸민 자가 당신 앞에 나타나거든, 당신이 의심받는 집안 출신임이 알려져 있는 만큼 더더욱 그 죄를 가차 없이 처벌해야 한다는 것을요.\""]], [["“Alas, madame,”", "\"안타깝지만 부인,\""], ["returned Villefort, “my profession, as well as the times in which we live, compels me to be severe.", "빌포르가 대답했다. \"제 직업도, 우리가 사는 시대도 저를 준엄하게 만듭니다."], ["I have already successfully conducted several public prosecutions, and brought the offenders to merited punishment.", "저는 이미 여러 건의 공소를 성공리에 이끌어 죄인들을 마땅한 벌에 처했습니다."], ["But we have not done with the thing yet.” 0087m", "하지만 그 일이 아직 끝난 것은 아닙니다.\""]], [["“Do you, indeed, think so?”", "\"정말로 그렇게 생각하나요?\""], ["inquired the marquise.", "후작 부인이 물었다."]], [["“I am, at least, fearful of it.", "\"적어도 저는 그것을 두려워하고 있습니다."], ["Napoleon, in the Island of Elba, is too near France, and his proximity keeps up the hopes of his partisans.", "엘바섬의 나폴레옹은 프랑스에 너무 가까이 있고, 그 근접함이 추종자들의 희망을 떠받치고 있습니다."], ["Marseilles is filled with half-pay officers, who are daily, under one frivolous pretext or other, getting up quarrels with the royalists; from hence arise continual and fatal duels among the higher classes of persons, and assassinations in the lower.”", "마르세유에는 반액 봉급을 받는 퇴역 장교들이 가득한데, 그들은 날마다 시시한 핑계를 대며 왕당파와 시비를 붙고 있습니다. 그래서 상류층에서는 끊임없이 치명적인 결투가 벌어지고, 하류층에서는 암살이 일어나는 것이지요.\""]], [["“You have heard, perhaps,”", "\"혹시 들으셨소,\""], ["said the Comte de Salvieux, one of M. de Saint-Méran’s oldest friends, and chamberlain to the Comte d’Artois, “that the Holy Alliance purpose removing him from thence?”", "생메랑 씨의 가장 오랜 벗이자 아르투아 백작의 시종인 살비외 백작이 말했다. \"신성 동맹이 그를 그곳에서 옮길 작정이라는 이야기를?\""]], [["“Yes; they were talking about it when we left Paris,” said M.", "\"그렇소. 우리가 파리를 떠날 무렵 그 이야기가 오가고 있었지요.\""], ["de Saint-Méran; “and where is it decided to transfer him?”", "생메랑 씨가 말했다. \"그래, 어디로 옮기기로 정해졌소?\""]], [["“To Saint Helena.”", "\"세인트헬레나로요.\""]], [["“For heaven’s sake, where is that?”", "\"맙소사, 거기가 어디예요?\""], ["asked the marquise.", "후작 부인이 물었다."]], [["“An island situated on the other side of the equator, at least two thousand leagues from here,”", "\"적도 너머에 자리한 섬으로, 여기서 적어도 이천 리외는 떨어져 있습니다.\""], ["replied the count.", "백작이 대답했다."]], [["“So much the better.", "\"그렇다면 다행이군요."], ["As Villefort observes, it is a great act of folly to have left such a man between Corsica, where he was born, and Naples, of which his brother-in-law is king, and face to face with Italy, the sovereignty of which he coveted for his son.”", "빌포르가 말한 대로, 그런 자를 그가 태어난 코르시카와 그의 매부가 왕으로 있는 나폴리 사이에, 게다가 그가 제 아들을 위해 그 주권을 탐냈던 이탈리아를 마주 보는 자리에 내버려 둔 것은 크나큰 어리석음이었어요.\""]], [["“Unfortunately,”", "\"안타깝지만,\""], ["said Villefort, “there are the treaties of 1814, and we cannot molest Napoleon without breaking those compacts.”", "빌포르가 말했다. \"1814년의 조약들이 있는지라, 그 협정을 깨뜨리지 않고서는 나폴레옹을 건드릴 수가 없습니다.\""]], [["“Oh, well, we shall find some way out of it,”", "\"오, 글쎄, 어떻게든 빠져나갈 길을 찾아내겠지요.\""], ["responded M. de Salvieux.", "살비외 씨가 대꾸했다."], ["“There wasn’t any trouble over treaties when it was a question of shooting the poor Duc d’Enghien.”", "\"가엾은 앙기앵 공작을 총살할 때는 조약 따위로 골치를 썩이지 않았으니 말이오.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"자,\""], ["said the marquise, “it seems probable that, by the aid of the Holy Alliance, we shall be rid of Napoleon; and we must trust to the vigilance of M.", "후작 부인이 말했다. \"보아하니 신성 동맹의 도움으로 우리는 나폴레옹을 떨쳐 낼 수 있을 듯하군요. 그리고 마르세유에서 그 추종자들을 숙청하는 일은 빌포르 씨의 경계심에 맡겨야겠어요."], ["de Villefort to purify Marseilles of his partisans.", "임금은 임금이거나 임금이 아니거나 둘 중 하나예요."], ["The king is either a king or no king; if he be acknowledged as sovereign of France, he should be upheld in peace and tranquillity; and this can best be effected by employing the most inflexible agents to put down every attempt at conspiracy—’tis the best and surest means of preventing mischief.”", "프랑스의 군주로 인정받는다면, 평화와 안녕 속에서 떠받들어져야 마땅하지요. 그리고 그것은 가장 흔들림 없는 일꾼들을 부려 모든 음모의 시도를 짓밟는 것으로써 가장 잘 이뤄질 수 있어요. 그게 화근을 막는 최선이자 가장 확실한 방법이니까요.\""]], [["“Unfortunately, madame,”", "\"안타깝지만 부인,\""], ["answered Villefort, “the strong arm of the law is not called upon to interfere until the evil has taken place.”", "빌포르가 대답했다. \"법의 강한 팔은 악이 이미 벌어진 뒤에야 비로소 개입하라는 부름을 받습니다.\""]], [["“Then all he has got to do is to endeavor to repair it.”", "\"그렇다면 그가 할 일은 그것을 바로잡으려 애쓰는 것뿐이겠군요.\""]], [["“Nay, madame, the law is frequently powerless to effect this; all it can do is to avenge the wrong done.”", "\"아닙니다, 부인. 법은 그것을 바로잡기에는 무력할 때가 많습니다. 법이 할 수 있는 일이라곤 저질러진 잘못에 복수하는 것뿐이지요.\""]], [["“Oh, M. de Villefort,”", "\"오, 빌포르 씨,\""], ["cried a beautiful young creature, daughter to the Comte de Salvieux, and the cherished friend of Mademoiselle de Saint-Méran, “do try and get up some famous trial while we are at Marseilles.", "살비외 백작의 딸이자 생메랑 양의 아끼는 벗인 아름다운 젊은 처녀가 외쳤다. \"우리가 마르세유에 있는 동안 무슨 떠들썩한 재판이라도 한번 열어 주세요."], ["I never was in a law-court; I am told it is so very amusing!”", "저는 한 번도 법정에 가 본 적이 없어요. 굉장히 재미있다던데요!\""]], [["“Amusing, certainly,”", "\"재미있고말고요.\""], ["replied the young man, “inasmuch as, instead of shedding tears as at the fictitious tale of woe produced at a theatre, you behold in a law-court a case of real and genuine distress—a drama of life.", "청년이 대답했다. \"극장에서 꾸며 낸 비극을 보며 눈물을 흘리는 것과는 달리, 법정에서는 진짜로, 진정으로 고통받는 사건을, 인생의 한 편 드라마를 목격하게 되니까요."], ["The prisoner whom you there see pale, agitated, and alarmed, instead of—as is the case when a curtain falls on a tragedy—going home to sup peacefully with his family, and then retiring to rest, that he may recommence his mimic woes on the morrow,—is removed from your sight merely to be reconducted to his prison and delivered up to the executioner.", "거기서 보게 되는 죄수는 창백하고 동요하고 겁에 질려 있는데, 비극의 막이 내릴 때처럼 집으로 돌아가 가족과 평온히 저녁을 들고 잠자리에 들어 이튿날 다시 거짓 슬픔을 연기하는 것이 아니라, 그저 다시 감옥으로 끌려가 사형 집행인에게 넘겨지기 위해 당신 눈앞에서 치워질 뿐이지요."], ["I leave you to judge how far your nerves are calculated to bear you through such a scene.", "그런 장면을 당신의 신경이 얼마나 견뎌 낼 수 있을지는 당신의 판단에 맡기겠습니다."], ["Of this, however, be assured, that should any favorable opportunity present itself, I will not fail to offer you the choice of being present.”", "다만 이것만은 믿어 두십시오. 적당한 기회가 생긴다면, 그 자리에 함께하실지 말지 그 선택권을 반드시 드리겠습니다.\""]], [["“For shame, M. de Villefort!”", "\"부끄러운 줄 아세요, 빌포르 씨!\""], ["said Renée, becoming quite pale; “don’t you see how you are frightening us?—and yet you laugh.”", "르네가 새파랗게 질려 말했다. \"지금 우리를 얼마나 겁주고 있는지 모르시겠어요? 그러면서 웃기까지 하다니.\""]], [["“What would you have? ’Tis like a duel.", "\"어쩌란 말입니까? 그건 결투와 같은 겁니다."], ["I have already recorded sentence of death, five or six times, against the movers of political conspiracies, and who can say how many daggers may be ready sharpened, and only waiting a favorable opportunity to be buried in my heart?”", "저는 이미 정치적 음모의 주모자들에게 다섯 번인가 여섯 번 사형을 선고한 바 있습니다. 그러니 얼마나 많은 단검이 날을 갈고서, 그저 좋은 기회만을 기다리며 제 심장에 박힐 채비를 하고 있을지 누가 알겠습니까?\""]], [["“Gracious heavens, M. de Villefort,”", "\"맙소사, 빌포르 씨,\""], ["said Renée, becoming more and more terrified; “you surely are not in earnest.”", "르네가 점점 더 겁에 질려 말했다. \"설마 진심은 아니시겠죠.\""]], [["“Indeed I am,”", "\"진심이고말고요.\""], ["replied the young magistrate with a smile; “and in the interesting trial that young lady is anxious to witness, the case would only be still more aggravated.", "젊은 행정관이 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"그리고 저 아가씨가 그토록 보고 싶어 하는 흥미로운 재판이라면, 사정이 한층 더 심각해질 따름입니다."], ["Suppose, for instance, the prisoner, as is more than probable, to have served under Napoleon—well, can you expect for an instant, that one accustomed, at the word of his commander, to rush fearlessly on the very bayonets of his foe, will scruple more to drive a stiletto into the heart of one he knows to be his personal enemy, than to slaughter his fellow-creatures, merely because bidden to do so by one he is bound to obey?", "가령, 죄수가 — 충분히 있을 법한 일입니다만 — 나폴레옹 휘하에서 복무한 자라고 칩시다. 그렇다면 지휘관의 명령 한마디에 적의 총검 속으로 두려움 없이 돌진하던 데 익숙한 자가, 그저 따르도록 명받았다는 이유만으로 제 동족을 살육하던 자가, 제 개인적 원수임을 잘 아는 사람의 심장에 단검을 찔러 넣기를 한순간이라도 망설이리라 기대할 수 있겠습니까?"], ["Besides, one requires the excitement of being hateful in the eyes of the accused, in order to lash one’s self into a state of sufficient vehemence and power.", "게다가 충분히 격렬하고 힘 있는 상태로 스스로를 몰아세우려면, 피고의 눈에 미움받는 자가 되는 자극이 필요한 법입니다."], ["I would not choose to see the man against whom I pleaded smile, as though in mockery of my words.", "저는 제가 논고하는 상대가 제 말을 비웃듯 미소 짓는 꼴을 보고 싶지 않습니다."], ["No; my pride is to see the accused pale, agitated, and as though beaten out of all composure by the fire of my eloquence.”", "그게 아니라, 제 웅변의 불길에 모든 평정을 잃고 창백하게 동요하는 피고를 보는 것, 그것이 제 긍지입니다.\""], ["Renée uttered a smothered exclamation.", "르네가 짓눌린 탄성을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“Bravo!”", "\"훌륭하오!\""], ["cried one of the guests; “that is what I call talking to some purpose.”", "손님 가운데 하나가 외쳤다. \"저것이야말로 핵심을 찌르는 말이지.\""]], [["“Just the person we require at a time like the present,” said a second.", "\"지금 같은 때에 꼭 필요한 인물이로군요.\" 또 다른 사람이 말했다."]], [["“What a splendid business that last case of yours was, my dear Villefort!”", "\"여보게 빌포르, 자네의 그 지난번 사건은 참으로 굉장했네!\""], ["remarked a third; “I mean the trial of the man for murdering his father.", "세 번째 사람이 말했다. \"아비를 살해한 사내를 재판한 일 말일세."], ["Upon my word, you killed him ere the executioner had laid his hand upon him.”", "정말이지, 사형 집행인이 손을 대기도 전에 자네가 그자를 죽여 버린 셈이었지.\""]], [["“Oh, as for parricides, and such dreadful people as that,”", "\"오, 아비를 죽인 자나 그런 끔찍한 인간들이라면,\""], ["interposed Renée, “it matters very little what is done to them; but as regards poor unfortunate creatures whose only crime consists in having mixed themselves up in political intrigues——”", "르네가 끼어들었다. \"그들에게 무슨 짓을 하든 별 상관이 없어요. 하지만 정치적 음모에 휘말린 것이 유일한 죄인 가엾고 불행한 사람들로 말하자면……\""]], [["“Why, that is the very worst offence they could possibly commit; for, don’t you see, Renée, the king is the father of his people, and he who shall plot or contrive aught against the life and safety of the parent of thirty-two millions of souls, is a parricide upon a fearfully great scale?”", "\"이런, 그거야말로 그들이 저지를 수 있는 가장 무거운 죄요. 르네, 모르겠소? 임금은 백성의 아버지이고, 삼천이백만 인구의 아버지의 목숨과 안위를 해치려 음모하거나 획책하는 자야말로 무시무시하리만치 거대한 규모의 존속 살해범이 아니겠소?\""]], [["“I don’t know anything about that,”", "\"그런 건 잘 모르겠어요.\""], ["replied Renée; “but, M.", "르네가 대답했다."], ["de Villefort, you have promised me—have you not?—always to show mercy to those I plead for.”", "\"하지만 빌포르 씨, 제게 약속하셨잖아요. 그렇죠? 제가 변호하는 사람들에게는 늘 자비를 베풀겠다고요.\""]], [["“Make yourself quite easy on that point,”", "\"그 점은 안심하셔도 됩니다.\""], ["answered Villefort, with one of his sweetest smiles; “you and I will always consult upon our verdicts.”", "빌포르가 더없이 다정한 미소를 지으며 대답했다. \"당신과 저는 늘 함께 의논하여 판결을 내릴 테니까요.\""]], [["“My love,”", "\"얘야,\""], ["said the marquise, “attend to your doves, your lap-dogs, and embroidery, but do not meddle with what you do not understand.", "후작 부인이 말했다. \"너는 비둘기며 애완견이며 자수에나 마음을 쓰고, 알지도 못하는 일에는 끼어들지 마라."], ["Nowadays the military profession is in abeyance and the magisterial robe is the badge of honor.", "요즈음 군인의 직업은 한물갔고, 법복이야말로 명예의 표지란다."], ["There is a wise Latin proverb that is very much in point.”", "꼭 들어맞는 라틴 격언이 하나 있지.\""]], [["“_Cedant arma togæ_,”", "\"케단트 아르마 토게●.\""], ["said Villefort with a bow.", "빌포르가 머리를 숙이며 말했다."]], [["“I cannot speak Latin,”", "\"나는 라틴어를 못 한다오.\""], ["responded the marquise.", "후작 부인이 대꾸했다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"있잖아요,\""], ["said Renée, “I cannot help regretting you had not chosen some other profession than your own—a physician, for instance.", "르네가 말했다. \"저는 당신이 지금 하는 일 말고 다른 직업을 택하지 않은 게 아쉬워요. 가령 의사라든가요. 아세요?"], ["Do you know I always felt a shudder at the idea of even a _destroying_ angel?”", "저는 '파괴하는' 천사라는 생각만으로도 늘 몸서리가 쳐졌답니다.\""]], [["“Dear, good Renée,” whispered Villefort, as he gazed with unutterable tenderness on the lovely speaker.", "\"사랑스럽고 착한 르네.\" 빌포르가 그 어여쁜 말상대를 형언할 수 없는 다정함으로 바라보며 속삭였다."]], [["“Let us hope, my child,”", "\"바라건대, 얘야,\""], ["cried the marquis, “that M.", "후작이 외쳤다."], ["de Villefort may prove the moral and political physician of this province; if so, he will have achieved a noble work.”", "\"빌포르 씨가 이 지방의 도덕적, 정치적 의사가 되어 주기를 바라자꾸나. 그리한다면 고귀한 과업을 이룬 셈이 될 테니.\""]], [["“And one which will go far to efface the recollection of his father’s conduct,”", "\"그리고 그 일은 제 아비의 행적에 대한 기억을 지우는 데에도 크게 보탬이 되겠지요.\""], ["added the incorrigible marquise.", "구제 불능의 후작 부인이 덧붙였다."]], [["“Madame,”", "\"부인,\""], ["replied Villefort, with a mournful smile, “I have already had the honor to observe that my father has—at least, I hope so—abjured his past errors, and that he is, at the present moment, a firm and zealous friend to religion and order—a better royalist, possibly, than his son; for he has to atone for past dereliction, while I have no other impulse than warm, decided preference and conviction.”", "빌포르가 서글픈 미소를 띠며 대답했다. \"이미 말씀드리는 영광을 누렸습니다만, 제 아버지는 — 적어도 저는 그러기를 바랍니다 — 지난날의 과오를 버렸으며, 지금 이 순간 종교와 질서의 굳건하고 열렬한 벗이 되어 있습니다. 어쩌면 그 아들보다 더 나은 왕당파일지도 모르지요."], ["Having made this well-turned speech, Villefort looked carefully around to mark the effect of his oratory, much as he would have done had he been addressing the bench in open court.", "아버지에게는 지난날의 배신을 속죄해야 할 일이 있는 반면, 제게는 따뜻하고 단호한 선호와 확신 말고는 다른 동기가 없으니 말입니다.\" 이 잘 다듬어진 말을 마친 빌포르는, 마치 공개 법정에서 판사석을 향해 변론이라도 한 듯, 제 웅변의 효과를 가늠하려 좌중을 조심스레 둘러보았다."]], [["“Do you know, my dear Villefort,”", "\"여보게 빌포르, 알고 있나,\""], ["cried the Comte de Salvieux, “that is exactly what I myself said the other day at the Tuileries, when questioned by his majesty’s principal chamberlain touching the singularity of an alliance between the son of a Girondin and the daughter of an officer of the Duc de Condé; and I assure you he seemed fully to comprehend that this mode of reconciling political differences was based upon sound and excellent principles.", "살비외 백작이 외쳤다. \"그게 바로 며칠 전 튀일리궁에서 폐하의 수석 시종이 지롱드파의 아들과 콩데 공작 휘하 장교의 딸 사이의 혼인이 기묘하다며 물었을 때, 내가 직접 했던 말과 똑같다는 걸 말이야. 분명히 말하지만, 그는 정치적 차이를 이렇게 화해시키는 방식이 건전하고 훌륭한 원칙에 바탕을 두고 있음을 충분히 이해하는 듯했네."], ["Then the king, who, without our suspecting it, had overheard our conversation, interrupted us by saying, ‘Villefort’—observe that the king did not pronounce the word Noirtier, but, on the contrary, placed considerable emphasis on that of Villefort—‘Villefort,’ said his majesty, ‘is a young man of great judgment and discretion, who will be sure to make a figure in his profession; I like him much, and it gave me great pleasure to hear that he was about to become the son-in-law of the Marquis and Marquise de Saint-Méran.", "그러자 우리가 눈치채지 못하는 사이 대화를 엿듣고 계시던 폐하께서 우리 말을 끊고 이렇게 말씀하셨지. '빌포르' — 폐하께서 누아르티에라는 말은 입에 올리지 않으시고, 오히려 빌포르라는 말에 상당히 힘을 주셨음을 눈여겨보게 — '빌포르는,' 폐하께서 말씀하셨네."], ["I should myself have recommended the match, had not the noble marquis anticipated my wishes by requesting my consent to it.’”", "'판단력과 분별력이 뛰어난 젊은이로, 틀림없이 그 직업에서 두각을 나타낼 것이다. 나는 그를 무척 마음에 들어 하며, 그가 생메랑 후작 부부의 사위가 될 참이라는 소식을 듣고 크게 기뻤다. 고귀한 후작이 내 뜻을 앞질러 윤허를 청하지 않았더라면, 내가 직접 그 혼사를 천거했을 것이다.'\""]], [["“Is it possible the king could have condescended so far as to express himself so favorably of me?”", "\"폐하께서 그토록 저를 호의적으로 말씀하시는 데까지 마음을 베푸셨을 수가 있단 말입니까?\""], ["asked the enraptured Villefort.", "황홀해진 빌포르가 물었다."]], [["“I give you his very words; and if the marquis chooses to be candid, he will confess that they perfectly agree with what his majesty said to him, when he went six months ago to consult him upon the subject of your espousing his daughter.” 0091m", "\"폐하의 말씀을 그대로 옮긴 걸세. 후작이 솔직하시기로 마음먹는다면, 폐하의 말씀이 여섯 달 전 자네와 따님의 혼인 건을 의논하러 갔을 때 폐하께서 그에게 하신 말씀과 완벽히 일치한다는 걸 인정하실 거야.\""]], [["“That is true,” answered the marquis.", "\"그건 사실이오.\" 후작이 대답했다."]], [["“How much do I owe this gracious prince!", "\"제가 이 자애로운 군주께 진 빚이 얼마나 큰지요!"], ["What is there I would not do to evince my earnest gratitude!”", "이 간절한 감사를 보여 드리기 위해서라면 못 할 일이 무엇이겠습니까!\""]], [["“That is right,”", "\"옳거니.\""], ["cried the marquise.", "후작 부인이 외쳤다."], ["“I love to see you thus. Now, then, were a conspirator to fall into your hands, he would be most welcome.”", "\"그렇게 나오는 게 보기 좋군요. 자, 그럼, 음모자가 당신 손에 떨어진다면 더없이 환영받을 거예요.\""]], [["“For my part, dear mother,”", "\"저로서는, 어머니,\""], ["interposed Renée, “I trust your wishes will not prosper, and that Providence will only permit petty offenders, poor debtors, and miserable cheats to fall into M.", "르네가 끼어들었다. \"어머니의 바람이 이뤄지지 않기를 빌어요. 그리고 섭리가 빌포르 씨의 손에 떨어뜨리는 것이 고작 좀도둑이나 가엾은 빚쟁이, 비루한 사기꾼뿐이기를 바라요."], ["de Villefort’s hands,—then I shall be contented.”", "그래야 제 마음이 편할 테니까요.\""]], [["“Just the same as though you prayed that a physician might only be called upon to prescribe for headaches, measles, and the stings of wasps, or any other slight affection of the epidermis.", "\"그건 의사더러 두통이나 홍역, 벌에 쏘인 상처, 그 밖의 사소한 피부 질환에나 처방을 내리게 해 달라고 비는 거나 마찬가지요."], ["If you wish to see me the king’s attorney, you must desire for me some of those violent and dangerous diseases from the cure of which so much honor redounds to the physician.”", "나를 임금의 검사로 만들고 싶다면, 의사에게 큰 명예를 안겨 주는 그런 격렬하고 위험한 병들을 좀 바라 주어야 합니다.\""]], [["At this moment, and as though the utterance of Villefort’s wish had sufficed to effect its accomplishment, a servant entered the room, and whispered a few words in his ear.", "바로 그 순간, 마치 빌포르가 입에 올린 소망이 그것을 이루어 내기에 충분하기라도 했던 듯, 하인 하나가 방에 들어와 그의 귀에 몇 마디를 속삭였다."], ["Villefort immediately rose from table and quitted the room upon the plea of urgent business; he soon, however, returned, his whole face beaming with delight.", "빌포르는 곧장 식탁에서 일어나 급한 용무를 핑계로 방을 나섰다. 그러나 이내 돌아왔는데, 온 얼굴이 기쁨으로 환히 빛나고 있었다."], ["Renée regarded him with fond affection; and certainly his handsome features, lit up as they then were with more than usual fire and animation, seemed formed to excite the innocent admiration with which she gazed on her graceful and intelligent lover.", "르네는 다정한 애정을 담아 그를 바라보았다. 그 잘생긴 이목구비가 여느 때보다 더한 불꽃과 생기로 환히 빛나는 모습은, 우아하고 총명한 연인을 바라보는 그녀의 순진한 찬탄을 자아내려 빚어진 것만 같았다."]], [["“You were wishing just now,”", "\"방금 당신은,\""], ["said Villefort, addressing her, “that I were a doctor instead of a lawyer.", "빌포르가 그녀를 향해 말했다. \"제가 변호사 대신 의사였더라면 하고 바라셨지요."], ["Well, I at least resemble the disciples of Esculapius in one thing [people spoke in this style in 1815], that of not being able to call a day my own, not even that of my betrothal.”", "그런데 적어도 한 가지 점에서는 제가 아스클레피오스의 제자들을 닮았답니다(1815년에는 이런 식으로 말하곤 했다). 바로 단 하루도 제 것이라 부를 수 없다는 점이지요. 약혼식 날조차도요.\""]], [["“And wherefore were you called away just now?” asked Mademoiselle de Saint-Méran, with an air of deep interest.", "\"그런데 방금 무슨 일로 불려 가셨나요?\" 생메랑 양이 깊은 관심을 담아 물었다."]], [["“For a very serious matter, which bids fair to make work for the executioner.”", "\"아주 중대한 일입니다. 자칫하면 사형 집행인에게 일거리를 만들어 줄 만한 일이지요.\""]], [["“How dreadful!”", "\"끔찍해라!\""], ["exclaimed Renée, turning pale.", "르네가 새파랗게 질리며 외쳤다."]], [["“Is it possible?”", "\"그게 정말이오?\""], ["burst simultaneously from all who were near enough to the magistrate to hear his words.", "행정관의 말을 들을 만큼 가까이 있던 이들 모두의 입에서 동시에 터져 나왔다."]], [["“Why, if my information prove correct, a sort of Bonapartist conspiracy has just been discovered.”", "\"실은, 제가 받은 정보가 옳다면, 일종의 보나파르트파 음모가 방금 발각되었습니다.\""]], [["“Can I believe my ears?”", "\"내 귀를 믿어도 되나요?\""], ["cried the marquise.", "후작 부인이 외쳤다."]], [["“I will read you the letter containing the accusation, at least,”", "\"적어도 그 고발 내용이 담긴 편지를 읽어 드리지요.\""], ["said Villefort:", "빌포르가 말했다."]], [["“‘The king’s attorney is informed by a friend to the throne and the religious institutions of his country, that one named Edmond Dantès, mate of the ship _Pharaon_, this day arrived from Smyrna, after having touched at Naples and Porto-Ferrajo, has been the bearer of a letter from Murat to the usurper, and again taken charge of another letter from the usurper to the Bonapartist club in Paris.", "\"'국왕의 검사께, 왕좌와 조국의 종교 기관을 위하는 한 벗이 알려 드립니다. 오늘 스미르나에서 나폴리와 포르토페라요를 거쳐 입항한 파라옹호의 항해사 에드몽 당테스라는 자가 뮈라가 찬탈자에게 보내는 편지를 전달했으며, 다시 찬탈자가 파리의 보나파르트파 클럽에 보내는 또 다른 편지를 맡았습니다."], ["Ample corroboration of this statement may be obtained by arresting the above-mentioned Edmond Dantès, who either carries the letter for Paris about with him, or has it at his father’s abode.", "위에 언급한 에드몽 당테스를 체포하면 이 진술을 충분히 입증할 수 있을 것입니다. 그자는 파리로 보낼 편지를 몸에 지니고 있거나, 그 아비의 거처에 두었을 것입니다."], ["Should it not be found in the possession of father or son, then it will assuredly be discovered in the cabin belonging to the said Dantès on board the _Pharaon_.’”", "만일 부자 어느 쪽에서도 발견되지 않거든, 파라옹호 선상의 당테스 선실에서 반드시 찾아낼 수 있을 것입니다.'\""]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["said Renée, “this letter, which, after all, is but an anonymous scrawl, is not even addressed to you, but to the king’s attorney.”", "르네가 말했다. \"이 편지는, 따지고 보면 익명의 휘갈겨 쓴 쪽지에 지나지 않는 데다, 당신이 아니라 국왕의 검사 앞으로 되어 있잖아요.\""]], [["0093m\n“True; but that gentleman being absent, his secretary, by his orders, opened his letters; thinking this one of importance, he sent for me, but not finding me, took upon himself to give the necessary orders for arresting the accused party.”", "\"맞습니다. 하지만 그분이 부재중이라, 그 비서가 지시에 따라 편지들을 개봉했는데, 이것이 중요하다고 여겨 저를 부른 것이지요. 그런데 저를 찾지 못하자 스스로 책임지고 피고발인을 체포하라는 필요한 명령을 내렸습니다.\""]], [["“Then the guilty person is absolutely in custody?”", "\"그렇다면 그 죄인은 이미 구금되어 있나요?\""], ["said the marquise.", "후작 부인이 물었다."]], [["“Nay, dear mother, say the accused person.", "\"아닙니다, 어머니. 피고발인이라고 하셔야지요."], ["You know we cannot yet pronounce him guilty.”", "아직 그를 죄인이라 단정할 수는 없으니까요.\""]], [["“He is in safe custody,”", "\"그자는 안전하게 구금되어 있습니다.\""], ["answered Villefort; “and rely upon it, if the letter is found, he will not be likely to be trusted abroad again, unless he goes forth under the especial protection of the headsman.”", "빌포르가 대답했다. \"그리고 믿으셔도 됩니다, 만일 그 편지가 발견된다면 그자가 다시 바깥세상을 믿고 활보하는 일은 없을 겁니다. 처형인의 특별한 보호를 받으며 나서는 것이 아니라면 말이지요.\""]], [["“And where is the unfortunate being?”", "\"그래, 그 가엾은 자는 어디 있나요?\""], ["asked Renée.", "르네가 물었다."]], [["“He is at my house.”", "\"제 집에 있습니다.\""]], [["“Come, come, my friend,”", "\"자, 자, 여보게.\""], ["interrupted the marquise, “do not neglect your duty to linger with us.", "후작 부인이 끼어들었다. \"우리와 머무느라 자네 본분을 소홀히 하지 말게."], ["You are the king’s servant, and must go wherever that service calls you.”", "자네는 임금의 종복이니, 그 임무가 부르는 곳이라면 어디든 가야 하네.\""]], [["“Oh, Villefort!”", "\"오, 빌포르!\""], ["cried Renée, clasping her hands, and looking towards her lover with piteous earnestness, “be merciful on this the day of our betrothal.”", "르네가 두 손을 맞잡고 애처로울 만큼 간절하게 연인을 바라보며 외쳤다. \"우리 약혼식 날이니, 부디 자비를 베풀어 주세요.\""]], [["The young man passed round to the side of the table where the fair pleader sat, and leaning over her chair said tenderly:", "청년은 어여쁜 탄원자가 앉은 식탁 쪽으로 돌아가, 그녀의 의자에 몸을 기울이며 다정하게 말했다."]], [["“To give you pleasure, my sweet Renée, I promise to show all the lenity in my power; but if the charges brought against this Bonapartist hero prove correct, why, then, you really must give me leave to order his head to be cut off.”", "\"당신을 기쁘게 하기 위해, 사랑하는 르네, 제 힘이 닿는 한 온갖 관용을 베풀겠다고 약속하지요. 하지만 이 보나파르트파 영웅에게 씌워진 혐의가 사실로 드러난다면, 그땐 정말이지 제가 그자의 목을 치라 명할 수 있도록 허락해 주셔야 합니다.\""]], [["Renée shuddered at the word _cut_, for the growth in question had a head.", "르네는 '친다'는 말에 몸서리를 쳤다. 문제의 그 존재에게는 목이 달려 있었으니 말이다."]], [["“Never mind that foolish girl, Villefort,”", "\"저 어리석은 아이는 신경 쓰지 말게, 빌포르.\""], ["said the marquise.", "후작 부인이 말했다."], ["“She will soon get over these things.”", "\"이런 일쯤은 곧 익숙해질 테니.\""], ["So saying, Madame de Saint-Méran extended her dry bony hand to Villefort, who, while imprinting a son-in-law’s respectful salute on it, looked at Renée, as much as to say, “I must try and fancy ’tis your dear hand I kiss, as it should have been.”", "그러면서 생메랑 부인은 메마르고 앙상한 손을 빌포르에게 내밀었다. 그는 사위의 정중한 입맞춤을 그 손에 새기면서 르네를 바라보았는데, 그 눈빛은 마치 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. '마땅히 그래야 했을 당신의 사랑스러운 손에 입 맞추는 거라고 여기려 애써야겠군요.'"]], [["“These are mournful auspices to accompany a betrothal,” sighed poor Renée.", "\"약혼식에 따르는 징조치고는 음울하기도 하네요.\" 가엾은 르네가 한숨지었다."]], [["“Upon my word, child!”", "\"이런, 얘야!\""], ["exclaimed the angry marquise, “your folly exceeds all bounds.", "화가 난 후작 부인이 외쳤다. \"네 어리석음이 도를 넘는구나."], ["I should be glad to know what connection there can possibly be between your sickly sentimentality and the affairs of the state!”", "도대체 너의 그 병약한 감상과 나랏일 사이에 무슨 관련이 있을 수 있는지 알고 싶구나!\""]], [["“Oh, mother!”", "\"오, 어머니!\""], ["murmured Renée.", "르네가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Nay, madame, I pray you pardon this little traitor.", "\"아닙니다, 부인, 부디 이 작은 배반자를 용서해 주십시오."], ["I promise you that to make up for her want of loyalty, I will be most inflexibly severe;”", "약속드리지요, 이 아이의 충절 부족을 메우기 위해서라도 저는 더없이 가차 없이 준엄하게 굴겠습니다.\""], ["then casting an expressive glance at his betrothed, which seemed to say, “Fear not, for your dear sake my justice shall be tempered with mercy,”", "그러면서 그는 약혼녀에게 의미심장한 눈길을 던졌는데, 그 눈빛은 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. '걱정 마세요, 사랑하는 당신을 위해 제 정의에는 자비가 깃들 테니까요.'"], ["and receiving a sweet and approving smile in return, Villefort departed with paradise in his heart.", "그리고 그 대가로 달콤하고 흡족해하는 미소를 돌려받은 빌포르는, 마음속에 천국을 품은 채 자리를 떠났다."]], [["# Chapter 7. The Examination", "# Chapter 7. The Examination"]], [["No sooner had Villefort left the salon, than he assumed the grave air of a man who holds the balance of life and death in his hands.", "● 케단트 아르마 토게: '무기여 법복에 자리를 내주어라'라는 뜻의 라틴어 격언."], ["Now, in spite of the nobility of his countenance, the command of which, like a finished actor, he had carefully studied before the glass, it was by no means easy for him to assume an air of judicial severity.", "—옮긴이\n빌포르는 응접실을 나서기가 무섭게, 사람의 생사를 손에 쥔 자다운 근엄한 표정을 지었다. 그는 노련한 배우처럼 거울 앞에서 그 위엄 있는 얼굴을 미리 갈고닦아 두었지만, 그럼에도 재판관다운 준엄한 기색을 꾸며 내기란 결코 쉬운 일이 아니었다."], ["Except the recollection of the line of politics his father had adopted, and which might interfere, unless he acted with the greatest prudence, with his own career, Gérard de Villefort was as happy as a man could be.", "자신의 아버지가 택한 정치적 노선에 대한 기억만 아니었다면, 그래서 자칫 큰 신중을 기하지 않으면 그것이 자기 출셋길을 가로막을지 모른다는 우려만 없었다면, 제라르 드 빌포르는 더 바랄 것 없이 행복한 사내였다."], ["Already rich, he held a high official situation, though only twenty-seven.", "이미 부유한 데다, 겨우 스물일곱의 나이에 높은 관직에 올라 있었다."], ["He was about to marry a young and charming woman, whom he loved, not passionately, but reasonably, as became a deputy attorney of the king; and besides her personal attractions, which were very great, Mademoiselle de Saint-Méran’s family possessed considerable political influence, which they would, of course, exert in his favor.", "그는 사랑하는 젊고 매력적인 여인과 곧 결혼할 참이었는데, 차석 검사답게 열정적으로가 아니라 분별 있게 사랑하는 여인이었다. 그리고 생메랑 양은 그 자체로 빼어난 매력을 지녔을 뿐 아니라, 그 집안이 상당한 정치적 영향력을 지니고 있어 당연히 그것을 그에게 유리하게 행사해 줄 터였다."], ["The dowry of his wife amounted to fifty thousand crowns, and he had, besides, the prospect of seeing her fortune increased to half a million at her father’s death.", "아내가 가져올 지참금은 5만 에퀴에 달했고, 게다가 장인이 세상을 떠나면 그 재산이 50만으로 불어나는 것을 지켜볼 전망까지 있었다."], ["These considerations naturally gave Villefort a feeling of such complete felicity that his mind was fairly dazzled in its contemplation.", "이런 사정들이 빌포르에게 더없이 완벽한 행복감을 안겨 주었으니, 그 행복을 떠올리는 것만으로도 그의 마음은 그야말로 눈이 부실 지경이었다."]], [["At the door he met the commissary of police, who was waiting for him.", "문 앞에서 그는 자신을 기다리던 경찰서장과 마주쳤다. 이 관리의 모습은 빌포르를 셋째 하늘에서 다시 땅으로 끌어내렸다. 그는 앞서 묘사한 대로 표정을 가다듬고 말했다."], ["The sight of this officer recalled Villefort from the third heaven to earth; he composed his face, as we have before described, and said, “I have read the letter, sir, and you have acted rightly in arresting this man; now inform me what you have discovered concerning him and the conspiracy.”", "\"편지는 읽었네. 그 사내를 체포한 건 잘한 일이야. 자, 이제 그자와 음모에 관해 알아낸 바를 보고하게.\""]], [["“We know nothing as yet of the conspiracy, monsieur; all the papers found have been sealed up and placed on your desk.", "\"음모에 대해서는 아직 아무것도 모릅니다, 검사님. 발견된 서류는 모두 봉인해 검사님 책상에 올려 두었습니다."], ["The prisoner himself is named Edmond Dantès, mate on board the three-master the _Pharaon_, trading in cotton with Alexandria and Smyrna, and belonging to Morrel & Son, of Marseilles.”", "죄인의 이름은 에드몽 당테스이고, 삼범선 파라옹호의 일등 항해사로, 알렉산드리아와 스미르나를 오가며 면화를 거래하는 마르세유의 모렐 부자 상회 소속입니다.\""]], [["“Before he entered the merchant service, had he ever served in the marines?”", "\"상선에 오르기 전에, 해군에 복무한 적이 있던가?\""]], [["“Oh, no, monsieur, he is very young.”", "\"아, 아닙니다, 검사님. 아주 젊은 자입니다.\""]], [["“How old?”", "\"몇 살인가?\""]], [["“Nineteen or twenty at the most.”", "\"많아야 열아홉이나 스물입니다.\""]], [["At this moment, and as Villefort had arrived at the corner of the Rue des Conseils, a man, who seemed to have been waiting for him, approached; it was M. Morrel.", "바로 그때, 빌포르가 콩세유 거리 모퉁이에 이르렀을 때, 그를 기다리고 있었던 듯한 한 사내가 다가왔다. 모렐 씨였다."]], [["“Ah, M. de Villefort,”", "\"아, 빌포르 씨,\" 그가 외쳤다."], ["cried he, “I am delighted to see you.", "\"이렇게 뵙게 되어 정말 다행입니다."], ["Some of your people have committed the strangest mistake—they have just arrested Edmond Dantès, mate of my vessel.”", "당신 부하들이 참으로 어처구니없는 실수를 저질렀습니다. 방금 제 배의 일등 항해사인 에드몽 당테스를 체포했지 뭡니까.\""]], [["“I know it, monsieur,”", "\"알고 있습니다, 모렐 씨.\""], ["replied Villefort, “and I am now going to examine him.”", "빌포르가 대답했다. \"지금 그자를 신문하러 가는 길입니다.\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"아,\""], ["said Morrel, carried away by his friendship, “you do not know him, and I do.", "모렐이 우정에 못 이겨 말했다. \"당신은 그를 모르시지만, 저는 압니다."], ["He is the most estimable, the most trustworthy creature in the world, and I will venture to say, there is not a better seaman in all the merchant service.", "그는 세상에서 가장 존경할 만하고 가장 믿음직한 사람이며, 감히 말씀드리건대 상선 통틀어 그보다 나은 선원은 없습니다."], ["Oh, M. de Villefort, I beseech your indulgence for him.”", "아, 빌포르 씨, 부디 그에게 너그러이 자비를 베풀어 주십시오.\""]], [["Villefort, as we have seen, belonged to the aristocratic party at Marseilles, Morrel to the plebeian; the first was a royalist, the other suspected of Bonapartism.", "이미 보았듯이 빌포르는 마르세유의 귀족파에, 모렐은 평민파에 속했다. 전자는 왕당파였고, 후자는 보나파르트주의자로 의심받았다."], ["Villefort looked disdainfully at Morrel, and replied coldly:", "빌포르가 모렐을 경멸 어린 눈으로 바라보며 차갑게 대답했다."]], [["“You are aware, monsieur, that a man may be estimable and trustworthy in private life, and the best seaman in the merchant service, and yet be, politically speaking, a great criminal.", "\"아시다시피, 모렐 씨, 사람이 사생활에서는 존경할 만하고 믿음직하며 상선 최고의 선원이라 해도, 정치적으로 말하자면 중죄인일 수 있는 법입니다."], ["Is it not true?”", "그렇지 않습니까?\""]], [["The magistrate laid emphasis on these words, as if he wished to apply them to the owner himself, while his eyes seemed to plunge into the heart of one who, interceding for another, had himself need of indulgence.", "판사는 마치 이 말을 선주 자신에게 들이대려는 듯 한 마디 한 마디에 힘을 주었고, 그 눈은 남을 위해 변호하면서 정작 제 자신이 자비를 필요로 하는 자의 가슴속을 파고드는 것 같았다. 모렐의 얼굴이 붉어졌다."], ["Morrel reddened, for his own conscience was not quite clear on politics; besides, what Dantès had told him of his interview with the grand-marshal, and what the emperor had said to him, embarrassed him.", "정치 문제에 관한 한 그 자신의 양심도 완전히 떳떳하지는 않았던 것이다. 게다가 당테스가 대원수와 만난 일과 황제가 그에게 했다는 말을 들려준 터라, 그것이 그를 거북하게 했다."], ["He replied, however, in a tone of deep interest:", "그러나 그는 깊은 관심이 묻어나는 어조로 대답했다."]], [["“I entreat you, M. de Villefort, be, as you always are, kind and equitable, and give him back to us soon.”", "\"간청드립니다, 빌포르 씨. 늘 그러시듯이 부디 친절하고 공정하게 처분하시어, 그를 어서 우리에게 돌려보내 주십시오.\""], ["This _give us_ sounded revolutionary in the deputy’s ears.", "이 \"우리에게\"라는 말이 차석 검사의 귀에는 혁명적으로 들렸다."]], [["“Ah, ah,”", "\"허허,\""], ["murmured he, “is Dantès then a member of some Carbonari society, that his protector thus employs the collective form?", "그가 중얼거렸다. \"그럼 당테스가 무슨 카르보나리 결사의 일원이라, 그 비호자가 이렇게 복수형을 쓰는 건가?"], ["He was, if I recollect, arrested in a tavern, in company with a great many others.”", "내 기억이 맞다면, 그자는 술집에서 수많은 사람들과 어울려 있다가 체포되었지.\" 그러고는 덧붙였다."], ["Then he added, “Monsieur, you may rest assured I shall perform my duty impartially, and that if he be innocent you shall not have appealed to me in vain; should he, however, be guilty, in this present epoch, impunity would furnish a dangerous example, and I must do my duty.”", "\"모렐 씨, 안심하셔도 됩니다. 저는 공정하게 제 의무를 다할 것이고, 만약 그가 결백하다면 당신이 저를 찾아온 것이 헛되지 않을 겁니다. 그러나 만약 그가 유죄라면, 지금 같은 시국에 처벌을 면해 주는 것은 위험한 선례가 될 테니, 저는 제 의무를 다해야만 합니다.\""]], [["0097m\nAs he had now arrived at the door of his own house, which adjoined the Palais de Justice, he entered, after having, coldly saluted the shipowner, who stood, as if petrified, on the spot where Villefort had left him.", "이제 그는 법원 청사에 맞붙은 자기 집 문 앞에 이르렀으므로, 선주에게 차갑게 인사하고는 안으로 들어갔다. 선주는 빌포르가 떠난 자리에 못 박힌 듯 그대로 서 있었다."], ["The antechamber was full of police agents and gendarmes, in the midst of whom, carefully watched, but calm and smiling, stood the prisoner.", "대기실은 경찰 요원과 헌병들로 가득했고, 그 한가운데에 죄인이 삼엄한 감시를 받으면서도 침착하게 미소를 띤 채 서 있었다."], ["Villefort traversed the antechamber, cast a side glance at Dantès, and taking a packet which a gendarme offered him, disappeared, saying, “Bring in the prisoner.”", "빌포르는 대기실을 가로지르며 당테스에게 곁눈질을 던지고는, 헌병이 건네는 꾸러미를 받아 들고 \"죄인을 들여보내라.\"라고 말하며 사라졌다."]], [["Rapid as had been Villefort’s glance, it had served to give him an idea of the man he was about to interrogate.", "빌포르의 시선은 순식간에 스쳐 갔지만, 자신이 신문하려는 사내가 어떤 인물인지 가늠하기에는 충분했다."], ["He had recognized intelligence in the high forehead, courage in the dark eye and bent brow, and frankness in the thick lips that showed a set of pearly teeth.", "그는 그 훤한 이마에서 총기를, 검은 눈과 찌푸린 눈썹에서 용기를, 진주처럼 가지런한 이를 드러내는 두툼한 입술에서 솔직함을 읽어 냈다."], ["Villefort’s first impression was favorable; but he had been so often warned to mistrust first impulses, that he applied the maxim to the impression, forgetting the difference between the two words.", "빌포르의 첫인상은 호의적이었다. 그러나 그는 첫 충동을 믿지 말라는 경고를 하도 자주 들어 온 터라, 그 격언을 첫인상에까지 적용하고 말았다. 충동과 인상이라는 두 말의 차이는 잊어버린 채였다."], ["He stifled, therefore, the feelings of compassion that were rising, composed his features, and sat down, grim and sombre, at his desk.", "그래서 그는 솟아오르는 연민의 감정을 억누르고, 표정을 가다듬은 뒤, 책상 앞에 험상궂고 음울한 얼굴로 앉았다."], ["An instant after Dantès entered.", "잠시 후 당테스가 들어왔다."], ["He was pale, but calm and collected, and saluting his judge with easy politeness, looked round for a seat, as if he had been in M.", "그는 창백했지만 침착하고 차분했으며, 편안한 정중함으로 재판관에게 인사하고는, 마치 모렐 씨의 응접실에 와 있기라도 한 듯 앉을 자리를 둘러보았다."], ["Morrel’s salon. It was then that he encountered for the first time Villefort’s look,—that look peculiar to the magistrate, who, while seeming to read the thoughts of others, betrays nothing of his own.", "바로 그때 그는 처음으로 빌포르의 시선과 마주쳤다. 남의 생각을 읽어 내는 듯하면서도 정작 제 속은 조금도 드러내지 않는, 판사 특유의 그 시선이었다."]], [["“Who and what are you?”", "\"이름이 무엇이고 무얼 하는 자인가?\" 빌포르가 물었다."], ["demanded Villefort, turning over a pile of papers, containing information relative to the prisoner, that a police agent had given to him on his entry, and that, already, in an hour’s time, had swelled to voluminous proportions, thanks to the corrupt espionage of which “the accused” is always made the victim.", "그는 죄인에 관한 정보가 담긴 서류 뭉치를 뒤적이고 있었다. 그가 들어올 때 경찰 요원이 건네준 것으로, \"피고인\"이 늘 희생양이 되는 부패한 밀정 활동 덕분에 불과 한 시간 만에 방대한 분량으로 불어나 있었다."]], [["“My name is Edmond Dantès,”", "\"제 이름은 에드몽 당테스입니다.\""], ["replied the young man calmly; “I am mate of the _Pharaon_, belonging to Messrs. Morrel & Son.”", "젊은이가 차분하게 대답했다. \"모렐 부자 상회 소속 파라옹호의 일등 항해사입니다.\""]], [["“Your age?”", "\"나이는?\""], ["continued Villefort.", "빌포르가 이어 물었다."]], [["“Nineteen,”", "\"열아홉입니다.\""], ["returned Dantès.", "당테스가 대답했다."]], [["“What were you doing at the moment you were arrested?”", "\"체포될 당시에는 무얼 하고 있었나?\""]], [["“I was at the festival of my marriage, monsieur,”", "\"제 결혼 잔치 중이었습니다, 검사님.\" 젊은이가 말했다."], ["said the young man, his voice slightly tremulous, so great was the contrast between that happy moment and the painful ceremony he was now undergoing; so great was the contrast between the sombre aspect of M. de Villefort and the radiant face of Mercédès.", "그 목소리는 살짝 떨렸다. 그 행복했던 순간과 지금 겪고 있는 이 괴로운 절차가 너무도 대조적이었고, 빌포르 씨의 음울한 표정과 메르세데스의 빛나는 얼굴이 너무도 대조적이었던 것이다."]], [["“You were at the festival of your marriage?” said the deputy, shuddering in spite of himself.", "\"결혼 잔치 중이었다고?\" 차석 검사가 자기도 모르게 흠칫하며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, monsieur; I am on the point of marrying a young girl I have been attached to for three years.”", "\"네, 검사님. 삼 년 동안 사랑해 온 처녀와 곧 결혼하려던 참이었습니다.\""], ["Villefort, impassive as he was, was struck with this coincidence; and the tremulous voice of Dantès, surprised in the midst of his happiness, struck a sympathetic chord in his own bosom—he also was on the point of being married, and he was summoned from his own happiness to destroy that of another.", "빌포르는 무심한 사람이었지만 이 우연의 일치에 충격을 받았다. 행복의 한복판에서 불시에 붙들려 온 당테스의 떨리는 목소리가 그 자신의 가슴속에서도 공명의 현을 울렸다. 그 역시 곧 결혼을 앞두고 있었고, 자신의 행복으로부터 불려 나와 남의 행복을 짓밟으려는 참이었다."], ["“This philosophic reflection,”", "'이 철학적인 성찰은,'"], ["thought he, “will make a great sensation at M. de Saint-Méran’s;”", "그는 생각했다. '생메랑 씨 댁에서 큰 반향을 불러일으키겠군.'"], ["and he arranged mentally, while Dantès awaited further questions, the antithesis by which orators often create a reputation for eloquence.", "그리고 당테스가 다음 질문을 기다리는 동안, 그는 웅변가들이 종종 달변가라는 명성을 얻는 데 쓰는 그 대조법을 속으로 짜 맞추었다."], ["When this speech was arranged, Villefort turned to Dantès.", "이 연설을 다 다듬고 나자 빌포르가 당테스에게로 고개를 돌렸다."]], [["0099m\n“Go on, sir,” said he.", "\"계속하게.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["“What would you have me say?”", "\"무엇을 말씀드리면 되겠습니까?\""]], [["“Give all the information in your power.”", "\"아는 바를 모조리 진술하게.\""]], [["“Tell me on which point you desire information, and I will tell all I know; only,”", "\"어느 점에 대해 알고 싶으신지 말씀해 주시면 제가 아는 것을 전부 말씀드리겠습니다. 다만,\""], ["added he, with a smile, “I warn you I know very little.”", "그가 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다. \"미리 말씀드리지만 제가 아는 것은 거의 없습니다.\""]], [["“Have you served under the usurper?”", "\"찬탈자 밑에서 복무한 적이 있나?\""]], [["“I was about to be mustered into the Royal Marines when he fell.”", "\"그가 몰락했을 때 저는 막 왕립 해군에 편입될 참이었습니다.\""]], [["“It is reported your political opinions are extreme,”", "\"자네의 정치적 견해가 극단적이라는 보고가 있던데.\""], ["said Villefort, who had never heard anything of the kind, but was not sorry to make this inquiry, as if it were an accusation.", "빌포르가 말했다. 그는 그런 말을 들은 적이 전혀 없었지만, 마치 그것이 하나의 혐의라도 되는 양 이렇게 물어보는 것이 싫지 않았다."]], [["“My political opinions!”", "\"제 정치적 견해라고요!\""], ["replied Dantès.", "당테스가 대답했다."], ["“Alas, sir, I never had any opinions.", "\"아아, 검사님, 저는 견해라는 것을 가져 본 적이 없습니다."], ["I am hardly nineteen; I know nothing; I have no part to play.", "저는 겨우 열아홉이고, 아는 것도 없으며, 맡을 역할도 없습니다."], ["If I obtain the situation I desire, I shall owe it to M.", "제가 바라는 자리를 얻게 된다면 그건 모렐 씨 덕분이겠지요."], ["Morrel. Thus all my opinions—I will not say public, but private—are confined to these three sentiments,—I love my father, I respect M.", "그러니 제 모든 견해는, 공적인 것은 말할 것도 없고 사적인 것까지도, 이 세 가지 감정에 한정됩니다. 저는 아버지를 사랑하고, 모렐 씨를 존경하며, 메르세데스를 흠모합니다."], ["Morrel, and I adore Mercédès. This, sir, is all I can tell you, and you see how uninteresting it is.”", "검사님, 이것이 제가 말씀드릴 수 있는 전부이고, 보시다시피 하나도 흥미로울 게 없습니다.\""], ["As Dantès spoke, Villefort gazed at his ingenuous and open countenance, and recollected the words of Renée, who, without knowing who the culprit was, had besought his indulgence for him.", "당테스가 말하는 동안 빌포르는 그 천진하고 거리낌 없는 얼굴을 바라보며, 죄인이 누구인지도 모른 채 그를 너그러이 봐 달라고 간청하던 르네의 말을 떠올렸다."], ["With the deputy’s knowledge of crime and criminals, every word the young man uttered convinced him more and more of his innocence.", "범죄와 범죄자를 잘 아는 차석 검사로서는, 젊은이가 내뱉는 말 한 마디 한 마디가 점점 더 그의 결백을 확신하게 만들었다."], ["This lad, for he was scarcely a man,—simple, natural, eloquent with that eloquence of the heart never found when sought for; full of affection for everybody, because he was happy, and because happiness renders even the wicked good—extended his affection even to his judge, spite of Villefort’s severe look and stern accent.", "이 젊은이는, 아니 아직 어른이라 하기도 어려운 이 소년은, 단순하고 자연스러웠으며, 애써 구할 때는 결코 찾을 수 없는 그 진심에서 우러난 웅변으로 말했다. 행복한 까닭에, 그리고 행복은 악인마저도 선하게 만드는 까닭에 모두에게 애정이 넘쳤다. 그 애정은 빌포르의 준엄한 눈빛과 단호한 말투에도 불구하고 재판관에게까지 미쳤다."], ["Dantès seemed full of kindness.", "당테스는 선의로 가득 차 있는 듯했다."]], [["_“Pardieu!”_ said Villefort, “he is a noble fellow.", "\"이런!\" 빌포르가 말했다. \"고결한 청년이로군."], ["I hope I shall gain Renée’s favor easily by obeying the first command she ever imposed on me.", "르네가 내게 처음으로 내린 부탁을 들어주면 그녀의 환심을 쉽게 살 수 있겠어."], ["I shall have at least a pressure of the hand in public, and a sweet kiss in private.”", "적어도 사람들 앞에서는 손을 꼭 쥐어 줄 테고, 단둘이 있을 때는 달콤한 입맞춤을 해 주겠지.\""], ["Full of this idea, Villefort’s face became so joyous, that when he turned to Dantès, the latter, who had watched the change on his physiognomy, was smiling also.", "이런 생각으로 가득 차자 빌포르의 얼굴이 어찌나 환해졌던지, 그가 당테스에게로 고개를 돌렸을 때, 그 표정의 변화를 지켜보던 당테스 역시 미소를 짓고 있었다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"이보게,\""], ["said Villefort, “have you any enemies, at least, that you know.”", "빌포르가 말했다. \"자네에게 적이 있나? 적어도 자네가 아는 한에서 말일세.\""]], [["“I have enemies?”", "\"제게 적이라고요?\""], ["replied Dantès; “my position is not sufficiently elevated for that.", "당테스가 대답했다. \"제 처지는 적을 둘 만큼 높지 않습니다."], ["As for my disposition, that is, perhaps, somewhat too hasty; but I have striven to repress it.", "성미로 말하자면, 어쩌면 좀 급한 편일지도 모르지만, 그것도 누르려고 애써 왔습니다."], ["I have had ten or twelve sailors under me, and if you question them, they will tell you that they love and respect me, not as a father, for I am too young, but as an elder brother.”", "제 밑에 선원이 열에서 열둘쯤 있었는데, 그들에게 물어보시면 저를 사랑하고 존경한다고 할 겁니다. 제가 너무 어리니 아버지로서는 아니더라도, 맏형으로서 말이지요.\""]], [["“But you may have excited jealousy.", "\"하지만 누군가의 시기를 샀을 수도 있지 않나."], ["You are about to become captain at nineteen—an elevated post; you are about to marry a pretty girl, who loves you; and these two pieces of good fortune may have excited the envy of someone.”", "자네는 열아홉에 선장이 되려는 참이니, 이는 높은 자리야. 게다가 자네를 사랑하는 예쁜 처녀와 결혼하려 하고 있지. 이 두 가지 행운이 누군가의 질투를 불러일으켰을 수도 있어.\""]], [["“You are right; you know men better than I do, and what you say may possibly be the case, I confess; but if such persons are among my acquaintances I prefer not to know it, because then I should be forced to hate them.”", "\"옳은 말씀입니다. 검사님은 저보다 사람을 더 잘 아시니, 말씀하신 대로일 수도 있겠지요. 인정합니다. 하지만 그런 자들이 제 지인 가운데 있다면, 저는 차라리 모르는 편이 낫습니다. 알게 되면 그들을 미워할 수밖에 없을 테니까요.\""]], [["“You are wrong; you should always strive to see clearly around you.", "\"그건 잘못된 생각일세. 사람은 늘 자기 주위를 똑똑히 살피려 애써야 하는 법이야."], ["You seem a worthy young man; I will depart from the strict line of my duty to aid you in discovering the author of this accusation.", "자네는 훌륭한 청년으로 보이니, 내 의무의 엄격한 선을 넘어서라도 이 고발을 꾸민 자를 밝혀내도록 도와주겠네."], ["Here is the paper; do you know the writing?”", "여기 그 종이가 있네. 이 필적을 아는가?\""], ["As he spoke, Villefort drew the letter from his pocket, and presented it to Dantès. Dantès read it.", "그러면서 빌포르는 주머니에서 편지를 꺼내 당테스에게 내밀었다. 당테스가 그것을 읽었다."], ["A cloud passed over his brow as he said:", "그의 이마에 그늘이 스쳤다. 그가 말했다."]], [["“No, monsieur, I do not know the writing, and yet it is tolerably plain.", "\"아닙니다, 검사님. 이 필적은 모르겠습니다. 그래도 글씨는 제법 또렷하군요."], ["Whoever did it writes well.", "이걸 쓴 자가 누구든 글솜씨가 좋습니다."], ["I am very fortunate,”", "저는 참으로 운이 좋습니다.\""], ["added he, looking gratefully at Villefort, “to be examined by such a man as you; for this envious person is a real enemy.”", "그가 빌포르를 고마운 눈길로 바라보며 덧붙였다. \"검사님 같은 분에게 신문을 받게 되었으니까요. 이 시기심에 찬 자는 진짜 적이니 말입니다.\""], ["And by the rapid glance that the young man’s eyes shot forth, Villefort saw how much energy lay hid beneath this mildness.", "그리고 젊은이의 눈에서 번뜩 스쳐 간 그 눈빛을 보고, 빌포르는 이 온순함 아래 얼마나 큰 열정이 숨어 있는지를 알아차렸다."]], [["“Now,”", "\"자,\""], ["said the deputy, “answer me frankly, not as a prisoner to a judge, but as one man to another who takes an interest in him, what truth is there in the accusation contained in this anonymous letter?”", "차석 검사가 말했다. \"이제 솔직하게 대답하게. 죄인이 재판관에게 답하듯이가 아니라, 자네에게 관심을 가진 한 사람에게 답하듯이 말일세."], ["And Villefort threw disdainfully on his desk the letter Dantès had just given back to him.", "이 익명의 편지에 담긴 고발에 어느 정도의 진실이 있는가?\" 그러면서 빌포르는 당테스가 방금 돌려준 편지를 경멸하듯 책상 위로 던졌다."]], [["“None at all.", "\"전혀 없습니다."], ["I will tell you the real facts.", "실제 사실을 말씀드리지요."], ["I swear by my honor as a sailor, by my love for Mercédès, by the life of my father——”", "선원으로서의 제 명예를 걸고, 메르세데스를 향한 제 사랑을 걸고, 제 아버지의 목숨을 걸고 맹세컨대……\""]], [["“Speak, monsieur,”", "\"말해 보게.\""], ["said Villefort.", "빌포르가 말했다."], ["Then, internally, “If Renée could see me, I hope she would be satisfied, and would no longer call me a decapitator.”", "그러고는 속으로 생각했다. '르네가 지금 나를 본다면 흡족해할 테고, 더는 나를 목 자르는 사람이라 부르지 않겠지.'"]], [["“Well, when we quitted Naples, Captain Leclere was attacked with a brain fever.", "\"그러니까, 저희가 나폴리를 떠났을 때, 르클레르 선장님이 뇌염에 걸리셨습니다."], ["As we had no doctor on board, and he was so anxious to arrive at Elba, that he would not touch at any other port, his disorder rose to such a height, that at the end of the third day, feeling he was dying, he called me to him.", "배에는 의사가 없었고, 선장님은 엘바섬에 도착하기를 어찌나 애타게 바라셨는지 다른 어떤 항구에도 들르려 하지 않으셨습니다. 그래서 병세가 점점 심해져, 사흘째 되던 날 끝에 자신이 죽어 가고 있음을 느끼시고는 저를 부르셨습니다. '여보게 당테스,'"], ["‘My dear Dantès,’ said he, ‘swear to perform what I am going to tell you, for it is a matter of the deepest importance.’", "선장님이 말씀하셨습니다. '내가 이제 하는 말을 꼭 지키겠다고 맹세하게. 이건 더없이 중대한 일이니까.'"]], [["“‘I swear, captain,’ replied I.", "\"'맹세합니다, 선장님.' 제가 대답했습니다."]], [["“‘Well, as after my death the command devolves on you as mate, assume the command, and bear up for the Island of Elba, disembark at Porto-Ferrajo, ask for the grand-marshal, give him this letter—perhaps they will give you another letter, and charge you with a commission.", "\"'좋아, 내가 죽고 나면 일등 항해사인 자네에게 지휘권이 넘어가니, 지휘를 맡아 엘바섬으로 뱃머리를 돌리게. 포르토페라요에 내려 대원수를 찾아가 이 편지를 전하게. 어쩌면 그쪽에서 다른 편지 한 통을 건네며 자네에게 어떤 임무를 맡길지도 모르네."], ["You will accomplish what I was to have done, and derive all the honor and profit from it.’", "그러면 자네가 내가 해야 했던 일을 대신 완수하는 것이고, 그 모든 명예와 이익도 자네가 차지하게 될 걸세.'"]], [["“‘I will do it, captain; but perhaps I shall not be admitted to the grand-marshal’s presence as easily as you expect?’", "\"'그리하겠습니다, 선장님. 하지만 어쩌면 선장님 생각만큼 쉽게 대원수를 뵙지 못할 수도 있지 않겠습니까?'"]], [["“‘Here is a ring that will obtain audience of him, and remove every difficulty,’ said the captain.", "\"'여기 반지가 하나 있네. 이것이면 그분을 알현할 수 있고, 어떤 어려움도 사라질 걸세.' 선장님이 말씀하셨습니다."], ["At these words he gave me a ring.", "이 말과 함께 선장님은 제게 반지를 건네셨습니다."], ["It was time—two hours after he was delirious; the next day he died.”", "마침맞은 때였습니다. 두 시간 뒤 선장님은 헛소리를 하기 시작하셨고, 이튿날 돌아가셨으니까요.\""]], [["“And what did you do then?”", "\"그래서 그다음엔 어떻게 했나?\""]], [["“What I ought to have done, and what everyone would have done in my place.", "\"제가 마땅히 해야 할 일을, 그리고 제 처지였다면 누구라도 했을 일을 했습니다."], ["Everywhere the last requests of a dying man are sacred; but with a sailor the last requests of his superior are commands.", "죽어 가는 사람의 마지막 부탁은 어디서나 신성한 법이지만, 선원에게 상관의 마지막 부탁은 곧 명령입니다."], ["I sailed for the Island of Elba, where I arrived the next day; I ordered everybody to remain on board, and went on shore alone.", "저는 엘바섬을 향해 떠나 이튿날 그곳에 도착했습니다. 모두에게 배에 남아 있으라 이르고 저 혼자 뭍에 내렸습니다."], ["As I had expected, I found some difficulty in obtaining access to the grand-marshal; but I sent the ring I had received from the captain to him, and was instantly admitted.", "예상대로 대원수를 만나기는 다소 어려웠지만, 선장님께 받은 반지를 그분께 보내자 곧바로 안으로 들여보내 주었습니다."], ["He questioned me concerning Captain Leclere’s death; and, as the latter had told me, gave me a letter to carry on to a person in Paris.", "그분은 르클레르 선장님의 죽음에 관해 제게 물으셨고, 선장님이 말씀하신 대로 파리에 있는 어떤 사람에게 전할 편지를 제게 주셨습니다."], ["I undertook it because it was what my captain had bade me do.", "저는 그 일을 맡았습니다. 선장님이 그리하라 분부하신 일이었으니까요."], ["I landed here, regulated the affairs of the vessel, and hastened to visit my affianced bride, whom I found more lovely than ever. Thanks to M.", "이곳에 내려 배에 관한 일을 정리한 뒤, 서둘러 약혼녀를 찾아갔더니 그녀는 그 어느 때보다 사랑스러웠습니다."], ["Morrel, all the forms were got over; in a word I was, as I told you, at my marriage feast; and I should have been married in an hour, and tomorrow I intended to start for Paris, had I not been arrested on this charge which you as well as I now see to be unjust.”", "모렐 씨 덕분에 모든 절차가 마무리되었고, 한마디로 말씀드린 대로 저는 결혼 잔치 자리에 있었습니다. 한 시간만 더 있었으면 결혼식을 올렸을 테고, 내일은 파리로 떠날 작정이었습니다. 검사님도 저도 부당하다는 것을 이제 아는 이 혐의로 체포되지만 않았다면 말입니다.\""]], [["“Ah,” said Villefort, “this seems to me the truth.", "\"아,\" 빌포르가 말했다."], ["If you have been culpable, it was imprudence, and this imprudence was in obedience to the orders of your captain.", "\"내가 보기에도 이게 사실인 것 같군. 자네에게 잘못이 있었다면 그건 경솔함이었고, 그 경솔함도 선장의 명령에 따른 것이었어."], ["Give up this letter you have brought from Elba, and pass your word you will appear should you be required, and go and rejoin your friends.", "엘바섬에서 가져온 그 편지를 내놓게. 그리고 부르면 출두하겠다고 약조하게. 그런 뒤 친구들에게 돌아가게.\""]], [["“I am free, then, sir?” cried Dantès joyfully.", "\"그럼 저는 자유로운 몸입니까, 검사님?\" 당테스가 기뻐하며 외쳤다."]], [["“Yes; but first give me this letter.”", "\"그렇네. 하지만 먼저 그 편지를 내게 주게.\""]], [["“You have it already, for it was taken from me with some others which I see in that packet.”", "\"이미 가지고 계십니다. 다른 것들과 함께 제게서 압수되었는데, 저기 그 꾸러미 안에 보이는군요.\""]], [["“Stop a moment,”", "\"잠깐,\""], ["said the deputy, as Dantès took his hat and gloves.", "당테스가 모자와 장갑을 집어 들자 차석 검사가 말했다."], ["“To whom is it addressed?”", "\"그 편지는 누구 앞으로 된 건가?\""]], [["_“To Monsieur Noirtier, Rue Coq-Héron, Paris.”_ Had a thunderbolt fallen into the room, Villefort could not have been more stupefied.", "\"파리 코크에롱 거리의 누아르티에 씨 앞으로 된 것입니다.\" 벼락이 방 안에 떨어졌다 해도 빌포르가 이보다 더 아연실색하지는 못했을 것이다."], ["He sank into his seat, and hastily turning over the packet, drew forth the fatal letter, at which he glanced with an expression of terror.", "그는 의자에 털썩 주저앉아 황급히 꾸러미를 뒤져 그 운명의 편지를 꺼냈고, 공포에 질린 표정으로 그것을 흘끗 보았다."]], [["“M. Noirtier, Rue Coq-Héron, No. 13,”", "\"코크에롱 거리 13번지, 누아르티에 씨라.\""], ["murmured he, growing still paler.", "그가 더욱 창백해지며 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네,\""], ["said Dantès; “do you know him?”", "당테스가 말했다. \"그분을 아십니까?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니,\""], ["replied Villefort; “a faithful servant of the king does not know conspirators.”", "빌포르가 대답했다. \"왕의 충실한 신하는 음모자를 알지 못하네.\""]], [["0103m\n“It is a conspiracy, then?”", "\"그럼 음모란 말입니까?\""], ["asked Dantès, who after believing himself free, now began to feel a tenfold alarm.", "당테스가 물었다. 자유로워졌다고 믿었던 그는 이제 열 배는 더한 두려움에 사로잡히기 시작했다."], ["“I have, however, already told you, sir, I was entirely ignorant of the contents of the letter.”", "\"하지만 검사님, 저는 이미 말씀드렸듯이 편지의 내용은 전혀 모릅니다.\""]], [["“Yes; but you knew the name of the person to whom it was addressed,”", "\"그래, 하지만 자네는 그 편지를 받을 사람의 이름은 알고 있었지.\""], ["said Villefort.", "빌포르가 말했다."]], [["“I was forced to read the address to know to whom to give it.”", "\"누구에게 건네야 할지 알려면 받는 사람을 읽을 수밖에 없었습니다.\""]], [["“Have you shown this letter to anyone?”", "\"이 편지를 누군가에게 보인 적이 있나?\""], ["asked Villefort, becoming still more pale.", "빌포르가 더욱 창백해지며 물었다."]], [["“To no one, on my honor.”", "\"아무에게도 보이지 않았습니다, 명예를 걸고.\""]], [["“Everybody is ignorant that you are the bearer of a letter from the Island of Elba, and addressed to M. Noirtier?”", "\"자네가 엘바섬에서 온, 누아르티에 씨 앞으로 된 편지를 지니고 있다는 사실을 아무도 모른다는 말이지?\""]], [["“Everybody, except the person who gave it to me.”", "\"제게 그것을 건넨 분 말고는 아무도 모릅니다.\""]], [["“And that was too much, far too much,”", "\"그것조차도 너무 많아, 너무도 많지.\""], ["murmured Villefort.", "빌포르가 중얼거렸다."], ["Villefort’s brow darkened more and more, his white lips and clenched teeth filled Dantès with apprehension.", "빌포르의 이마는 점점 더 어두워졌고, 핏기 없는 입술과 앙다문 이가 당테스를 불안하게 했다."], ["After reading the letter, Villefort covered his face with his hands.", "편지를 다 읽고 나서 빌포르는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다."]], [["“Oh,” said Dantès timidly, “what is the matter?”", "\"아,\" 당테스가 머뭇거리며 말했다."], ["Villefort made no answer, but raised his head at the expiration of a few seconds, and again perused the letter.", "\"무슨 일이십니까?\" 빌포르는 아무 대답도 하지 않다가, 몇 초 뒤 고개를 들고 다시 편지를 꼼꼼히 읽었다."]], [["“And you say that you are ignorant of the contents of this letter?”", "\"그러니까 자네는 이 편지의 내용을 모른다는 거지?\""]], [["“I give you my word of honor, sir,”", "\"명예를 걸고 맹세합니다, 검사님.\""], ["said Dantès; “but what is the matter?", "당테스가 말했다. \"그런데 무슨 일이십니까?"], ["You are ill—shall I ring for assistance?—shall I call?”", "안색이 안 좋으십니다. 사람을 부를까요? 누구라도 부를까요?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니,\""], ["said Villefort, rising hastily; “stay where you are.", "빌포르가 황급히 일어서며 말했다. \"그 자리에 가만히 있게."], ["It is for me to give orders here, and not you.”", "여기서 명령을 내리는 건 나지 자네가 아니야.\""]], [["“Monsieur,”", "\"검사님,\""], ["replied Dantès proudly, “it was only to summon assistance for you.”", "당테스가 자존심 상한 듯 대답했다. \"그저 도움을 청해 드리려던 것뿐입니다.\""]], [["“I want none; it was a temporary indisposition.", "\"필요 없네. 잠깐 몸이 좋지 않았을 뿐이야."], ["Attend to yourself; answer me.” Dantès waited, expecting a question, but in vain.", "자네 일이나 신경 쓰고, 내 질문에 답하게.\" 당테스는 질문을 기다렸으나 헛일이었다."], ["Villefort fell back on his chair, passed his hand over his brow, moist with perspiration, and, for the third time, read the letter.", "빌포르는 의자에 다시 주저앉아 땀에 젖은 이마를 손으로 쓸고는, 세 번째로 편지를 읽었다."]], [["“Oh, if he knows the contents of this!”", "'아, 이자가 이 편지의 내용을 안다면,'"], ["murmured he, “and that Noirtier is the father of Villefort, I am lost!”", "그가 중얼거렸다. '그리고 누아르티에가 빌포르의 아버지라는 걸 안다면, 나는 끝장이다!'"], ["And he fixed his eyes upon Edmond as if he would have penetrated his thoughts.", "그러고는 마치 그 생각을 꿰뚫어 보려는 듯 에드몽에게 시선을 고정했다."]], [["“Oh, it is impossible to doubt it,”", "\"오, 의심할 여지가 없어.\""], ["cried he, suddenly.", "그가 불쑥 외쳤다."]], [["“In heaven’s name!”", "\"제발 부탁드립니다!\""], ["cried the unhappy young man, “if you doubt me, question me; I will answer you.”", "불행한 젊은이가 외쳤다. \"저를 의심하신다면 신문해 주십시오. 답하겠습니다.\""], ["Villefort made a violent effort, and in a tone he strove to render firm:", "빌포르는 안간힘을 쓰며, 애써 단호하게 들리도록 한 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"이보게,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “I am no longer able, as I had hoped, to restore you immediately to liberty; before doing so, I must consult the trial justice; what my own feeling is you already know.”", "\"유감스럽게도 내가 바랐던 대로 자네를 당장 풀어 줄 수가 없게 되었네. 그러기에 앞서 예심 판사와 상의해야 하니까. 내 마음이 어떤지는 자네도 이미 알 걸세.\""]], [["“Oh, monsieur,”", "\"오, 검사님,\""], ["cried Dantès, “you have been rather a friend than a judge.” 0105m", "당테스가 외쳤다. \"검사님은 재판관이라기보다 차라리 벗이셨습니다.\""]], [["“Well, I must detain you some time longer, but I will strive to make it as short as possible.", "\"그래서 자네를 좀 더 붙들어 두어야겠네만, 되도록 짧게 끝내도록 애쓰겠네. 자네에게 걸린 주된 혐의는 이 편지이니, 보게……\""], ["The principal charge against you is this letter, and you see——” Villefort approached the fire, cast it in, and waited until it was entirely consumed.", "빌포르는 벽난로로 다가가 편지를 불 속에 던지고는, 그것이 완전히 다 타 버릴 때까지 기다렸다. \"보다시피, 내가 이걸 없애 버리지 않나?\""]], [["“You see, I destroy it?” “Oh,”", "\"오,\" 당테스가 외쳤다."], ["exclaimed Dantès, “you are goodness itself.”", "\"검사님은 그야말로 선의 그 자체이십니다.\""]], [["“Listen,”", "\"잘 듣게,\""], ["continued Villefort; “you can now have confidence in me after what I have done.”", "빌포르가 말을 이었다. \"내가 이렇게까지 했으니 이제 자네도 나를 믿을 수 있겠지.\""]], [["“Oh, command, and I will obey.”", "\"오, 명령만 하십시오. 따르겠습니다.\""]], [["“Listen; this is not a command, but advice I give you.”", "\"잘 듣게. 이건 명령이 아니라 내가 자네에게 주는 충고일세.\""]], [["“Speak, and I will follow your advice.”", "\"말씀하십시오. 충고를 따르겠습니다.\""]], [["“I shall detain you until this evening in the Palais de Justice.", "\"오늘 저녁까지 자네를 법원 청사에 붙들어 두겠네."], ["Should anyone else interrogate you, say to him what you have said to me, but do not breathe a word of this letter.”", "혹시 다른 누군가가 자네를 신문하거든, 내게 말한 대로 말하되, 이 편지에 대해서는 한마디도 입 밖에 내지 말게.\""]], [["“I promise.” It was Villefort who seemed to entreat, and the prisoner who reassured him.", "\"약속합니다.\" 간청하는 쪽은 빌포르인 듯했고, 안심시키는 쪽은 죄인인 듯했다."]], [["“You see,” continued he, glancing toward the grate, where fragments of burnt paper fluttered in the flames, “the letter is destroyed; you and I alone know of its existence; should you, therefore, be questioned, deny all knowledge of it—deny it boldly, and you are saved.”", "\"보다시피,\" 그가 불꽃 속에서 타다 남은 종잇조각이 나풀거리는 화덕 쪽을 흘끗 보며 말을 이었다. \"편지는 없어졌네. 이것이 있었다는 사실은 자네와 나 둘만 아는 일이야. 그러니 누가 묻거든 그런 건 전혀 모른다고 부인하게. 대담하게 부인하란 말이야. 그러면 자네는 살아날 걸세.\""]], [["“Be satisfied; I will deny it.”", "\"안심하십시오. 부인하겠습니다.\""]], [["“It was the only letter you had?”", "\"편지는 그것 하나뿐이었나?\""]], [["“It was.” “Swear it.”", "\"하나뿐이었습니다.\" \"맹세하게.\""]], [["“I swear it.”", "\"맹세합니다.\""]], [["Villefort rang.", "\"맹세합니다.\""], ["A police agent entered.", "빌포르가 종을 울렸다."], ["Villefort whispered some words in his ear, to which the officer replied by a motion of his head.", "경찰 요원이 들어왔다. 빌포르가 그의 귀에 몇 마디 속삭이자, 요원이 고갯짓으로 대답했다."]], [["“Follow him,” said Villefort to Dantès.", "\"이 사람을 따라가게.\" 빌포르가 당테스에게 말했다."], ["Dantès saluted Villefort and retired.", "당테스는 빌포르에게 인사하고 물러갔다."], ["Hardly had the door closed when Villefort threw himself half-fainting into a chair.", "문이 닫히기가 무섭게 빌포르는 반쯤 정신을 잃은 채 의자에 몸을 던졌다."]], [["“Alas, alas,”", "\"아아, 큰일 날 뻔했어.\""], ["murmured he, “if the procureur himself had been at Marseilles I should have been ruined.", "그가 중얼거렸다. \"검사장이 직접 마르세유에 있었더라면 나는 파멸했을 거야."], ["This accursed letter would have destroyed all my hopes.", "이 저주받은 편지가 내 모든 희망을 무너뜨렸겠지."], ["Oh, my father, must your past career always interfere with my successes?”", "오, 아버지, 아버지의 지난 행적이 언제까지나 제 성공을 가로막아야 한단 말입니까?\""], ["Suddenly a light passed over his face, a smile played round his set mouth, and his haggard eyes were fixed in thought.", "문득 그의 얼굴에 한 줄기 빛이 스치고, 앙다문 입가에 미소가 어렸으며, 초췌하던 눈이 골똘한 생각에 잠겨 한 점에 멎었다."]], [["“This will do,”", "\"이거면 되겠어.\""], ["said he, “and from this letter, which might have ruined me, I will make my fortune. Now to the work I have in hand.”", "그가 말했다. \"나를 파멸시킬 뻔했던 이 편지로, 나는 도리어 내 출세의 발판을 마련하겠어."], ["And after having assured himself that the prisoner was gone, the deputy procureur hastened to the house of his betrothed.", "자, 이제 내가 할 일을 해야지.\" 그러고는 죄인이 떠난 것을 확인한 뒤, 차석 검사는 서둘러 약혼녀의 집으로 향했다."]], [["# Chapter 8. The Château d’If", "# Chapter 8. The Château d’If"]], [["0107m\nThe commissary of police, as he traversed the antechamber, made a sign to two gendarmes, who placed themselves one on Dantès’ right and the other on his left.", "경찰서장은 대기실을 가로지르며 헌병 두 명에게 신호를 보냈고, 그들은 한 사람은 당테스의 오른쪽에, 다른 한 사람은 왼쪽에 섰다."], ["A door that communicated with the Palais de Justice was opened, and they went through a long range of gloomy corridors, whose appearance might have made even the boldest shudder.", "재판소로 통하는 문이 열렸고, 일행은 음산한 복도를 한참 지나갔다. 그 모습은 아무리 담대한 사람이라도 몸서리치게 할 만했다. 재판소는 감옥과 이어져 있었다."], ["The Palais de Justice communicated with the prison,—a sombre edifice, that from its grated windows looks on the clock-tower of the Accoules. After numberless windings, Dantès saw a door with an iron wicket.", "음울한 건물이었고, 창살 박힌 창문 너머로 아쿨의 시계탑이 내다보였다. 수없이 굽이진 길을 지난 끝에 당테스는 쇠살문이 달린 문 하나를 보았다."], ["The commissary took up an iron mallet and knocked thrice, every blow seeming to Dantès as if struck on his heart.", "서장이 쇠망치를 집어 들고 세 번 두드렸는데, 한 번 두드릴 때마다 당테스에게는 그것이 제 심장을 치는 것만 같았다."], ["The door opened, the two gendarmes gently pushed him forward, and the door closed with a loud sound behind him.", "문이 열리자 헌병 두 명이 그를 가만히 앞으로 밀어 넣었고, 문은 등 뒤에서 요란한 소리를 내며 닫혔다."], ["The air he inhaled was no longer pure, but thick and mephitic,—he was in prison.", "그가 들이마신 공기는 더 이상 맑지 않고 탁하고 역겨웠다. 그는 감옥 안에 있었다."]], [["He was conducted to a tolerably neat chamber, but grated and barred, and its appearance, therefore, did not greatly alarm him; besides, the words of Villefort, who seemed to interest himself so much, resounded still in his ears like a promise of freedom.", "그는 제법 깨끗한 방으로 끌려갔지만, 창살과 빗장이 둘러쳐져 있었다. 그래서 그 모습은 그를 크게 놀라게 하지는 않았다. 게다가 그토록 자신에게 마음을 써 주는 듯하던 빌포르의 말이 자유를 약속하듯 여전히 귓가에 울렸다."], ["It was four o’clock when Dantès was placed in this chamber.", "당테스가 이 방에 갇힌 것은 네 시였다."], ["It was, as we have said, the 1st of March, and the prisoner was soon buried in darkness.", "앞서 말했듯이 그날은 3월 1일이었고, 죄수는 곧 어둠 속에 묻혔다."], ["The obscurity augmented the acuteness of his hearing; at the slightest sound he rose and hastened to the door, convinced they were about to liberate him, but the sound died away, and Dantès sank again into his seat.", "어둠은 그의 청각을 더욱 예민하게 만들었다. 아주 작은 소리에도 그는 벌떡 일어나 문으로 달려갔고, 곧 풀려나리라 확신했다. 그러나 소리는 잦아들었고, 당테스는 다시 제자리에 주저앉았다."], ["At last, about ten o’clock, and just as Dantès began to despair, steps were heard in the corridor, a key turned in the lock, the bolts creaked, the massy oaken door flew open, and a flood of light from two torches pervaded the apartment.", "마침내 열 시쯤, 당테스가 절망에 빠지기 시작할 무렵, 복도에서 발소리가 들렸다. 자물쇠에서 열쇠가 돌아갔고, 빗장이 삐걱거렸고, 묵직한 떡갈나무 문이 활짝 열렸다. 횃불 두 개에서 쏟아지는 불빛이 방 안에 가득 찼다."]], [["By the torchlight Dantès saw the glittering sabres and carbines of four gendarmes.", "횃불 빛에 당테스는 헌병 넷의 번득이는 군도와 기병총을 보았다."], ["He had advanced at first, but stopped at the sight of this display of force.", "그는 처음에 앞으로 나섰으나, 이렇게 무력을 과시하는 광경을 보고 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“Are you come to fetch me?”", "\"저를 데리러 오신 겁니까?\""], ["asked he.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“Yes,” replied a gendarme.", "\"그렇다.\" 헌병 하나가 대답했다."]], [["“By the orders of the deputy procureur?”", "\"검사 대리의 명령으로요?\""]], [["“I believe so.”", "\"그런 모양이다.\""], ["The conviction that they came from M. de Villefort relieved all Dantès’ apprehensions; he advanced calmly, and placed himself in the centre of the escort.", "빌포르 씨가 보낸 사람들이라는 확신이 당테스의 모든 불안을 가라앉혔다. 그는 차분히 앞으로 나아가 호송대 한가운데에 섰다."], ["A carriage waited at the door, the coachman was on the box, and a police officer sat beside him.", "문 앞에 마차 한 대가 기다리고 있었고, 마부는 마부석에 앉아 있었으며, 그 옆에는 경찰관 한 명이 자리 잡고 있었다."]], [["“Is this carriage for me?”", "\"이 마차가 저를 위한 겁니까?\""], ["said Dantès.", "당테스가 말했다."]], [["“It is for you,”", "\"너를 위한 것이다.\""], ["replied a gendarme.", "헌병 하나가 대답했다."]], [["Dantès was about to speak; but feeling himself urged forward, and having neither the power nor the intention to resist, he mounted the steps, and was in an instant seated inside between two gendarmes; the two others took their places opposite, and the carriage rolled heavily over the stones.", "당테스가 무어라 말하려 했다. 그러나 앞으로 떠밀리는 데다 저항할 힘도, 그럴 마음도 없었기에 그는 발판을 밟고 올라갔고, 순식간에 헌병 둘 사이에 자리 잡았다. 나머지 둘은 맞은편에 앉았고, 마차는 돌바닥 위를 무겁게 굴러갔다."]], [["The prisoner glanced at the windows—they were grated; he had changed his prison for another that was conveying him he knew not whither.", "죄수는 창문을 흘끗 보았다. 창에는 창살이 박혀 있었다. 그는 감옥 하나를, 자신을 어디론가 데려가는 또 다른 감옥으로 바꾼 셈이었고, 그곳이 어디인지는 알지 못했다."], ["Through the grating, however, Dantès saw they were passing through the Rue Caisserie, and by the Rue Saint-Laurent and the Rue Taramis, to the quay.", "그래도 창살 너머로 당테스는 일행이 케스리 거리를 지나, 생로랑 거리와 타라미 거리를 거쳐 부두로 향하는 것을 보았다."], ["Soon he saw the lights of La Consigne.", "곧 라콩시뉴의 불빛이 보였다."]], [["The carriage stopped, the officer descended, approached the guardhouse, a dozen soldiers came out and formed themselves in order; Dantès saw the reflection of their muskets by the light of the lamps on the quay.", "마차가 멈추자 경찰관이 내려 경비소로 다가갔고, 병사 열두 명가량이 나와 대오를 갖추었다. 당테스는 부두의 등불 빛에 그들의 총이 반사되는 것을 보았다."]], [["“Can all this force be summoned on my account?”", "'이 많은 병력이 정녕 나 하나 때문에 동원된 것인가?'"], ["thought he.", "그는 생각했다."]], [["The officer opened the door, which was locked, and, without speaking a word, answered Dantès’ question; for he saw between the ranks of the soldiers a passage formed from the carriage to the port.", "경찰관이 잠겨 있던 문을 열었고, 한마디 말도 없이 당테스의 물음에 답해 주었다. 병사들이 늘어선 사이로 마차에서 항구까지 통로가 트여 있는 것이 보였기 때문이다."], ["The two gendarmes who were opposite to him descended first, then he was ordered to alight and the gendarmes on each side of him followed his example.", "맞은편에 있던 헌병 둘이 먼저 내렸고, 이어 당테스에게 내리라는 명령이 떨어졌으며, 좌우의 헌병들도 그를 따랐다."], ["They advanced towards a boat, which a custom-house officer held by a chain, near the quay.", "그들은 세관원이 부두 가까이에서 사슬로 붙잡고 있는 작은 배 한 척으로 다가갔다."]], [["The soldiers looked at Dantès with an air of stupid curiosity.", "병사들은 멍청한 호기심 어린 얼굴로 당테스를 바라보았다."], ["In an instant he was placed in the stern-sheets of the boat, between the gendarmes, while the officer stationed himself at the bow; a shove sent the boat adrift, and four sturdy oarsmen impelled it rapidly towards the Pilon.", "순식간에 그는 헌병들 사이로 배의 고물 자리에 앉혀졌고, 경찰관은 뱃머리에 자리 잡았다. 한 번 떠밀자 배가 물 위로 미끄러졌고, 건장한 노잡이 넷이 배를 빠르게 필롱 쪽으로 몰아갔다."], ["At a shout from the boat, the chain that closes the mouth of the port was lowered and in a second they were, as Dantès knew, in the Frioul and outside the inner harbor.", "배에서 외치는 소리가 들리자 항구 입구를 막고 있던 사슬이 내려졌고, 순식간에 그들은 당테스가 알기로 프리울 해역, 곧 내항 밖으로 나와 있었다."]], [["The prisoner’s first feeling was of joy at again breathing the pure air—for air is freedom; but he soon sighed, for he passed before La Réserve, where he had that morning been so happy, and now through the open windows came the laughter and revelry of a ball.", "죄수가 처음 느낀 것은 다시 맑은 공기를 들이마시는 기쁨이었다. 공기란 곧 자유이기 때문이다. 그러나 그는 곧 한숨을 내쉬었다."], ["Dantès folded his hands, raised his eyes to heaven, and prayed fervently. 0111m", "그날 아침 그토록 행복했던 라레제르브 앞을 지나는 참이었고, 이제 그 열린 창문으로 무도회의 웃음소리와 흥겨운 소리가 흘러나왔다. 당테스는 두 손을 모으고 하늘을 향해 눈을 들어 간절히 기도했다."]], [["The boat continued her voyage.", "배는 계속 나아갔다."], ["They had passed the Tête de Mort, were now off the Anse du Pharo, and about to double the battery.", "그들은 테트드모르를 지나, 이제 앙스뒤파로 앞바다에 이르렀고, 포대를 돌아 나가려는 참이었다."], ["This manœuvre was incomprehensible to Dantès.", "이 항로가 당테스로서는 도무지 이해되지 않았다."]], [["“Whither are you taking me?”", "\"저를 어디로 데려가는 겁니까?\""], ["asked he.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“You will soon know.”", "\"곧 알게 될 것이다.\""]], [["“But still——”", "\"하지만 그래도……\""]], [["“We are forbidden to give you any explanation.”", "\"우리는 너에게 아무런 설명도 해 줄 수 없다.\""], ["Dantès, trained in discipline, knew that nothing would be more absurd than to question subordinates, who were forbidden to reply; and so he remained silent.", "규율에 익숙한 당테스는 대답이 금지된 부하들에게 캐묻는 것만큼 어리석은 일은 없음을 알았다. 그래서 그는 입을 다물었다."]], [["The most vague and wild thoughts passed through his mind.", "가장 막연하고 터무니없는 생각들이 그의 머릿속을 스쳐 갔다."], ["The boat they were in could not make a long voyage; there was no vessel at anchor outside the harbor; he thought, perhaps, they were going to leave him on some distant point.", "그들이 탄 배로는 먼 항해를 할 수 없었다. 항구 밖에는 닻을 내린 배가 한 척도 없었다. 그는 어쩌면 자신을 어느 먼 곳에 버려 두려는 것이 아닐까 생각했다."], ["He was not bound, nor had they made any attempt to handcuff him; this seemed a good augury.", "그는 묶이지도 않았고, 수갑을 채우려는 시도도 없었다. 이것은 좋은 징조처럼 보였다."], ["Besides, had not the deputy, who had been so kind to him, told him that provided he did not pronounce the dreaded name of Noirtier, he had nothing to apprehend?", "게다가 그토록 친절하게 대해 주던 검사 대리가, 그 무시무시한 누아르티에라는 이름만 입에 올리지 않으면 두려워할 것이 없다고 말하지 않았던가?"], ["Had not Villefort in his presence destroyed the fatal letter, the only proof against him?", "빌포르가 자기 앞에서 그 치명적인 편지를, 자신에게 불리한 유일한 증거를 없애 버리지 않았던가?"]], [["He waited silently, striving to pierce through the darkness.", "그는 어둠을 꿰뚫어 보려 애쓰며 말없이 기다렸다."]], [["They had left the Ile Ratonneau, where the lighthouse stood, on the right, and were now opposite the Point des Catalans.", "그들은 등대가 서 있는 라토노섬을 오른쪽으로 두고 지났고, 이제 카탈루냐곶 맞은편에 있었다."], ["It seemed to the prisoner that he could distinguish a feminine form on the beach, for it was there Mercédès dwelt.", "죄수에게는 바닷가에서 여인의 형체 하나가 어렴풋이 보이는 듯했다. 메르세데스가 사는 곳이 바로 그곳이었기 때문이다."], ["How was it that a presentiment did not warn Mercédès that her lover was within three hundred yards of her?", "어째서 메르세데스에게는 자신의 연인이 삼백 야드도 채 안 되는 곳에 있다는 예감이 들지 않는 것일까?"]], [["One light alone was visible; and Dantès saw that it came from Mercédès’ chamber.", "불빛이라곤 단 하나만 보였다. 당테스는 그것이 메르세데스의 방에서 새어 나오는 것임을 알았다."], ["Mercédès was the only one awake in the whole settlement.", "그 마을 전체에서 깨어 있는 사람은 메르세데스뿐이었다."], ["A loud cry could be heard by her.", "큰 소리로 외치면 그녀에게 들릴 만했다."], ["But pride restrained him and he did not utter it.", "그러나 자존심이 그를 가로막았고, 그는 소리치지 않았다."], ["What would his guards think if they heard him shout like a madman?", "그가 미친 사람처럼 고함을 지른다면 호위병들이 어떻게 생각하겠는가?"]], [["He remained silent, his eyes fixed upon the light; the boat went on, but the prisoner thought only of Mercédès.", "그는 그 불빛에 눈을 고정한 채 잠자코 있었다. 배는 계속 나아갔으나, 죄수는 오직 메르세데스만을 생각했다."], ["An intervening elevation of land hid the light.", "가로막은 육지의 융기가 그 불빛을 가렸다."], ["Dantès turned and perceived that they had got out to sea.", "당테스가 고개를 돌려 보니, 어느새 그들은 먼바다로 나와 있었다."], ["While he had been absorbed in thought, they had shipped their oars and hoisted sail; the boat was now moving with the wind.", "그가 생각에 잠겨 있는 동안 그들은 노를 거두고 돛을 올렸고, 배는 이제 바람을 받으며 나아가고 있었다."]], [["In spite of his repugnance to address the guards, Dantès turned to the nearest gendarme, and taking his hand,", "호위병들에게 말 거는 것이 내키지 않았지만, 당테스는 가장 가까이 있는 헌병 쪽으로 몸을 돌려 그의 손을 잡았다."]], [["“Comrade,”", "\"여보시오,\""], ["said he, “I adjure you, as a Christian and a soldier, to tell me where we are going.", "그가 말했다. \"기독교인이자 군인으로서 청하니, 우리가 어디로 가는지 말해 주시오."], ["I am Captain Dantès, a loyal Frenchman, thought accused of treason; tell me where you are conducting me, and I promise you on my honor I will submit to my fate.”", "나는 당테스 선장이고, 반역죄로 고발당했으나 충성스러운 프랑스인이오. 나를 어디로 데려가는지 말해 주면, 명예를 걸고 내 운명에 순순히 따르겠다고 약속하겠소.\""]], [["The gendarme looked irresolutely at his companion, who returned for answer a sign that said, “I see no great harm in telling him now,”", "헌병은 머뭇거리며 동료를 바라보았고, 동료는 \"이제 말해 줘도 큰 해는 없을 것 같다\"는 뜻의 신호로 답했다."], ["and the gendarme replied:", "그러자 헌병이 대답했다."]], [["“You are a native of Marseilles, and a sailor, and yet you do not know where you are going?”", "\"당신은 마르세유 토박이에 뱃사람이면서, 어디로 가는지 모른단 말이오?\""]], [["“On my honor, I have no idea.”", "\"명예를 걸고, 정말 모르겠소.\""]], [["“Have you no idea whatever?”", "\"전혀 짐작도 못 하오?\""]], [["“None at all.”", "\"전혀.\""]], [["“That is impossible.”", "\"그럴 리가.\""]], [["“I swear to you it is true.", "\"정말이라고 맹세하오."], ["Tell me, I entreat.”", "제발 말해 주시오.\""]], [["“But my orders.”", "\"하지만 명령이 있어서.\""]], [["“Your orders do not forbid your telling me what I must know in ten minutes, in half an hour, or an hour.", "\"내가 십 분 뒤, 삼십 분 뒤, 한 시간 뒤면 알게 될 일을 말해 주는 것까지 명령이 금하지는 않을 거요."], ["You see I cannot escape, even if I intended.”", "보다시피 나는 달아나려 해도 달아날 수가 없소.\""]], [["“Unless you are blind, or have never been outside the harbor, you must know.”", "\"당신이 눈먼 사람이거나, 한 번도 항구 밖에 나와 본 적이 없는 사람이 아니라면, 알 텐데.\""]], [["“I do not.”", "\"모르겠소.\""]], [["“Look round you then.”", "\"그럼 주위를 둘러보시오.\""], ["Dantès rose and looked forward, when he saw rise within a hundred yards of him the black and frowning rock on which stands the Château d’If.", "당테스가 일어나 앞쪽을 보니, 백 야드도 채 안 되는 곳에 이프성이 서 있는 검고 험상궂은 바위가 솟아오르는 것이 보였다."], ["This gloomy fortress, which has for more than three hundred years furnished food for so many wild legends, seemed to Dantès like a scaffold to a malefactor.", "삼백 년 넘게 수많은 황당한 전설의 먹잇감이 되어 온 이 음울한 요새가, 당테스에게는 죄인에게 들이닥친 처형대처럼 보였다."]], [["“The Château d’If?”", "\"이프성?\" 그가 외쳤다."], ["cried he, “what are we going there for?”", "\"거기는 무엇하러 가는 거요?\""]], [["The gendarme smiled.", "헌병이 빙긋 웃었다."]], [["“I am not going there to be imprisoned,”", "\"나는 거기 갇히러 가는 게 아니오.\""], ["said Dantès; “it is only used for political prisoners.", "당테스가 말했다. \"거기는 정치범들만 가두는 곳이잖소."], ["I have committed no crime.", "나는 아무 죄도 짓지 않았소."], ["Are there any magistrates or judges at the Château d’If?”", "이프성에 치안판사나 재판관이 있기라도 하오?\""]], [["“There are only,”", "\"거기 있는 거라곤,\""], ["said the gendarme, “a governor, a garrison, turnkeys, and good thick walls.", "헌병이 말했다. \"성주 한 명에 수비대, 간수들, 그리고 두툼한 성벽뿐이오."], ["Come, come, do not look so astonished, or you will make me think you are laughing at me in return for my good nature.”", "자, 자, 그렇게 놀란 얼굴 하지 마시오. 안 그러면 내가 친절을 베푼 보답으로 당신이 나를 놀린다고 여길 테니.\""]], [["Dantès pressed the gendarme’s hand as though he would crush it.", "당테스는 짓이겨 버릴 듯 헌병의 손을 움켜쥐었다."]], [["“You think, then,”", "\"그러니까,\""], ["said he, “that I am taken to the Château d’If to be imprisoned there?”", "그가 말했다. \"내가 이프성에 갇히려고 끌려가는 거라고 생각하는 거요?\""]], [["“It is probable; but there is no occasion to squeeze so hard.”", "\"아마 그럴 거요. 하지만 그렇게 세게 쥘 것까지는 없잖소.\""]], [["“Without any inquiry, without any formality?”", "\"아무런 심문도, 아무런 절차도 없이?\""]], [["“All the formalities have been gone through; the inquiry is already made.”", "\"절차는 다 끝났소. 심문도 이미 마쳤고.\""]], [["“And so, in spite of M. de Villefort’s promises?”", "\"그렇다면 빌포르 씨의 약속에도 불구하고?\""]], [["“I do not know what M. de Villefort promised you,”", "\"빌포르 씨가 당신에게 무얼 약속했는지는 모르오.\""], ["said the gendarme, “but I know we are taking you to the Château d’If.", "헌병이 말했다. \"하지만 우리가 당신을 이프성으로 데려가고 있다는 건 아오."], ["But what are you doing?", "그런데, 뭐 하는 거요?"], ["Help, comrades, help!”", "도와줘, 동료들, 도와줘!\""]], [["By a rapid movement, which the gendarme’s practiced eye had perceived, Dantès sprang forward to precipitate himself into the sea; but four vigorous arms seized him as his feet quitted the bottom of the boat.", "헌병의 노련한 눈이 알아챈 잽싼 동작으로, 당테스는 바다로 몸을 던지려 앞으로 솟구쳤다. 그러나 발이 배 바닥에서 떨어지는 순간, 억센 팔 넷이 그를 붙들었다."], ["He fell back cursing with rage.", "그는 분노에 차 욕설을 퍼부으며 뒤로 넘어졌다."]], [["“Good!”", "\"좋아!\""], ["said the gendarme, placing his knee on his chest; “this is the way you keep your word as a sailor!", "헌병이 그의 가슴을 무릎으로 누르며 말했다. \"이게 뱃사람으로서 약속을 지키는 방식이로군!"], ["Believe soft-spoken gentlemen again!", "말솜씨 좋은 양반들 말을 다시 또 믿어 보시지!"], ["Hark ye, my friend, I have disobeyed my first order, but I will not disobey the second; and if you move, I will blow your brains out.”", "잘 들어, 친구, 나는 첫 명령은 어겼지만, 두 번째 명령은 어기지 않을 거야. 움직이면, 네 머리통을 날려 버리겠어.\""], ["And he levelled his carbine at Dantès, who felt the muzzle against his temple.", "그러고는 기병총을 당테스에게 겨누었고, 당테스는 총구가 자기 관자놀이에 닿는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["For a moment the idea of struggling crossed his mind, and of so ending the unexpected evil that had overtaken him.", "한순간 몸부림쳐서 자신을 덮친 이 뜻밖의 재앙을 그렇게 끝내 버릴까 하는 생각이 그의 머리를 스쳤다."], ["But he bethought him of M. de Villefort’s promise; and, besides, death in a boat from the hand of a gendarme seemed too terrible.", "그러나 그는 빌포르 씨의 약속을 떠올렸다. 게다가 배 안에서 헌병의 손에 죽는다는 것은 너무도 끔찍하게 여겨졌다."], ["He remained motionless, but gnashing his teeth and wringing his hands with fury.", "그는 꼼짝 않고 있었지만, 이를 갈고 두 손을 비틀어 대며 분노에 떨었다."]], [["At this moment the boat came to a landing with a violent shock.", "이때 배가 거센 충격과 함께 뭍에 닿았다."], ["One of the sailors leaped on shore, a cord creaked as it ran through a pulley, and Dantès guessed they were at the end of the voyage, and that they were mooring the boat.", "뱃사람 하나가 물에 뛰어내렸고, 도르래를 타고 밧줄이 미끄러지며 삐걱거렸다. 당테스는 항해가 끝났으며, 그들이 배를 매고 있다는 것을 짐작했다."]], [["His guards, taking him by the arms and coat-collar, forced him to rise, and dragged him towards the steps that lead to the gate of the fortress, while the police officer carrying a musket with fixed bayonet followed behind.", "호위병들은 그의 팔과 옷깃을 붙잡아 억지로 일으켜 세웠고, 요새 정문으로 이어지는 계단 쪽으로 끌고 갔다. 그동안 경찰관은 착검한 총을 들고 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["Dantès made no resistance; he was like a man in a dream; he saw soldiers drawn up on the embankment; he knew vaguely that he was ascending a flight of steps; he was conscious that he passed through a door, and that the door closed behind him; but all this indistinctly as through a mist. He did not even see the ocean, that terrible barrier against freedom, which the prisoners look upon with utter despair.", "당테스는 저항하지 않았다. 그는 꿈속을 헤매는 사람 같았다. 그는 둑 위에 늘어선 병사들을 보았다. 자신이 계단을 오르고 있다는 것을 어렴풋이 알았다. 문 하나를 지났고, 그 문이 등 뒤에서 닫혔다는 것을 의식했다. 그러나 이 모든 것이 안개 너머로 보듯 흐릿했다. 그는 죄수들이 깊은 절망에 잠겨 바라보는 그 바다, 자유를 가로막는 그 무시무시한 장벽조차 보지 못했다."]], [["They halted for a minute, during which he strove to collect his thoughts.", "그들은 잠시 멈춰 섰고, 그동안 그는 흩어진 생각을 추스르려 애썼다."], ["He looked around; he was in a court surrounded by high walls; he heard the measured tread of sentinels, and as they passed before the light he saw the barrels of their muskets shine.", "그는 주위를 둘러보았다. 그는 높은 성벽으로 둘러싸인 안뜰에 있었다. 보초들의 절도 있는 발소리가 들렸고, 그들이 불빛 앞을 지날 때면 총신이 번쩍이는 것이 보였다."]], [["They waited upwards of ten minutes.", "그들은 십 분 남짓 기다렸다."], ["Certain Dantès could not escape, the gendarmes released him.", "당테스가 달아날 수 없음을 확신한 헌병들은 그를 놓아주었다."], ["They seemed awaiting orders.", "그들은 명령을 기다리는 듯했다."], ["The orders came.", "명령이 떨어졌다."]], [["“Where is the prisoner?” said a voice.", "\"죄수는 어디 있나?\" 한 목소리가 말했다."]], [["“Here,” replied the gendarmes.", "\"여기 있습니다.\" 헌병들이 대답했다."]], [["“Let him follow me; I will take him to his cell.”", "\"나를 따라오게 하라. 내가 감방으로 데려가겠다.\""]], [["“Go!” said the gendarmes, thrusting Dantès forward.", "\"가!\" 헌병들이 당테스를 앞으로 떠밀며 말했다."]], [["The prisoner followed his guide, who led him into a room almost under ground, whose bare and reeking walls seemed as though impregnated with tears; a lamp placed on a stool illumined the apartment faintly, and showed Dantès the features of his conductor, an under-jailer, ill-clothed, and of sullen appearance.", "죄수는 안내자를 따라갔고, 그는 그를 거의 지하나 다름없는 방으로 이끌었다. 그곳의 헐벗고 축축한 벽은 눈물에 흠뻑 젖어 있는 듯했다. 걸상 위에 놓인 등잔 하나가 방을 희미하게 비추었고, 당테스는 그 빛에 안내자의 얼굴을 보았다. 옷차림이 추레하고 음침한 인상의 하급 간수였다."]], [["0113m\n“Here is your chamber for tonight,”", "\"오늘 밤은 여기가 당신 방이오.\""], ["said he. “It is late, and the governor is asleep.", "그가 말했다. \"시간이 늦어서 성주님은 주무시오."], ["Tomorrow, perhaps, he may change you.", "내일이면 어쩌면 당신을 옮겨 줄지도 모르겠소."], ["In the meantime there is bread, water, and fresh straw; and that is all a prisoner can wish for.", "그동안 빵과 물, 그리고 새 짚이 있소. 죄수가 바랄 수 있는 건 그게 다요."], ["Goodnight.”", "잘 자시오.\""], ["And before Dantès could open his mouth—before he had noticed where the jailer placed his bread or the water—before he had glanced towards the corner where the straw was, the jailer disappeared, taking with him the lamp and closing the door, leaving stamped upon the prisoner’s mind the dim reflection of the dripping walls of his dungeon.", "그러고는 당테스가 입을 열기도 전에, 간수가 빵과 물을 어디에 두었는지 살피기도 전에, 짚이 놓인 구석을 흘끗 보기도 전에, 간수는 등잔을 들고 문을 닫으며 사라져 버렸다. 죄수의 머릿속에는 물방울 떨어지는 지하 감옥 벽의 희미한 잔상만이 남았다."]], [["Dantès was alone in darkness and in silence—cold as the shadows that he felt breathe on his burning forehead.", "당테스는 어둠 속에, 정적 속에 홀로 있었다. 그 어둠은 그의 달아오른 이마에 숨을 불어 대는 그림자처럼 차가웠다."], ["With the first dawn of day the jailer returned, with orders to leave Dantès where he was. He found the prisoner in the same position, as if fixed there, his eyes swollen with weeping.", "동이 트자마자 간수가 당테스를 그대로 두라는 명령을 받고 돌아왔다. 그는 죄수가 마치 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 똑같은 자세로 있는 것을 보았고, 그 두 눈은 울어서 퉁퉁 부어 있었다."], ["He had passed the night standing, and without sleep.", "그는 밤새 선 채로, 잠도 자지 않고 보낸 것이었다."], ["The jailer advanced; Dantès appeared not to perceive him.", "간수가 다가왔다. 당테스는 그를 알아보지 못하는 듯했다."], ["He touched him on the shoulder. Edmond started.", "간수가 그의 어깨를 건드렸다. 에드몽이 흠칫 놀랐다."]], [["“Have you not slept?”", "\"잠을 못 잤소?\""], ["said the jailer.", "간수가 말했다."]], [["“I do not know,”", "\"모르겠소.\""], ["replied Dantès.", "당테스가 대답했다."], ["The jailer stared.", "간수가 빤히 쳐다보았다."]], [["“Are you hungry?”", "\"배가 고프오?\""], ["continued he.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“I do not know.”", "\"모르겠소.\""]], [["“Do you wish for anything?”", "\"뭐 필요한 거라도 있소?\""]], [["“I wish to see the governor.”", "\"성주를 만나고 싶소.\""]], [["The jailer shrugged his shoulders and left the chamber.", "간수는 어깨를 으쓱하고는 방을 나갔다."]], [["Dantès followed him with his eyes, and stretched forth his hands towards the open door; but the door closed.", "당테스는 눈으로 그를 좇으며 열린 문 쪽으로 두 손을 뻗었다. 그러나 문은 닫혔다."], ["All his emotion then burst forth; he cast himself on the ground, weeping bitterly, and asking himself what crime he had committed that he was thus punished.", "그러자 억눌렸던 온갖 감정이 터져 나왔다. 그는 바닥에 몸을 던지고 비통하게 울며, 대체 무슨 죄를 지었기에 이런 벌을 받는 것인지 자문했다."]], [["The day passed thus; he scarcely tasted food, but walked round and round the cell like a wild beast in its cage.", "하루가 그렇게 흘러갔다. 그는 음식에 거의 손도 대지 않은 채, 우리에 갇힌 들짐승처럼 감방 안을 빙빙 돌았다. 한 가지 생각이 특히 그를 괴롭혔다."], ["One thought in particular tormented him: namely, that during his journey hither he had sat so still, whereas he might, a dozen times, have plunged into the sea, and, thanks to his powers of swimming, for which he was famous, have gained the shore, concealed himself until the arrival of a Genoese or Spanish vessel, escaped to Spain or Italy, where Mercédès and his father could have joined him.", "곧 이곳으로 오는 동안 자신이 그토록 가만히 앉아 있었다는 것이었다. 마음만 먹었다면 열두 번도 더 바다로 뛰어들 수 있었고, 그 유명한 수영 솜씨 덕분에 뭍에 닿아, 제노바나 에스파냐 배가 올 때까지 몸을 숨겼다가, 에스파냐나 이탈리아로 달아날 수 있었을 텐데. 그러면 메르세데스와 아버지도 그곳으로 와서 합칠 수 있었을 것이다."], ["He had no fears as to how he should live—good seamen are welcome everywhere.", "어떻게 먹고살지는 걱정도 되지 않았다. 솜씨 좋은 뱃사람은 어디서나 환영받으니까."], ["He spoke Italian like a Tuscan, and Spanish like a Castilian; he would have been free, and happy with Mercédès and his father, whereas he was now confined in the Château d’If, that impregnable fortress, ignorant of the future destiny of his father and Mercédès; and all this because he had trusted to Villefort’s promise.", "그는 토스카나 사람처럼 이탈리아어를 했고, 카스티야 사람처럼 에스파냐어를 했다. 그랬다면 그는 자유로웠을 것이고, 메르세데스와 아버지와 함께 행복했을 것이다. 그런데 지금 그는 난공불락의 요새 이프성에 갇혀, 아버지와 메르세데스의 앞날이 어찌 될지도 모르는 처지였다."], ["The thought was maddening, and Dantès threw himself furiously down on his straw.", "이 모든 것이 빌포르의 약속을 믿었기 때문이었다. 그 생각은 미칠 듯한 것이었고, 당테스는 분에 못 이겨 짚더미 위에 몸을 내던졌다."], ["The next morning at the same hour, the jailer came again.", "이튿날 아침 같은 시각에 간수가 다시 왔다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"자,\""], ["said the jailer, “are you more reasonable today?”", "간수가 말했다. \"오늘은 좀 분별이 생겼소?\""], ["Dantès made no reply.", "당테스는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Come, cheer up; is there anything that I can do for you?”", "\"자, 기운을 내시오. 내가 해 줄 수 있는 일이 뭐 있겠소?\""]], [["“I wish to see the governor.”", "\"성주를 만나고 싶소.\""]], [["“I have already told you it was impossible.”", "\"그건 안 된다고 이미 말했잖소.\""]], [["“Why so?”", "\"어째서요?\""]], [["“Because it is against prison rules, and prisoners must not even ask for it.”", "\"감옥 규칙에 어긋나기 때문이오. 죄수는 그런 걸 청해서도 안 되오.\""]], [["“What is allowed, then?”", "\"그럼 허락되는 건 뭐요?\""]], [["“Better fare, if you pay for it, books, and leave to walk about.”", "\"값을 치른다면 더 나은 식사, 그리고 책, 또 바깥을 거니는 것 정도요.\""]], [["“I do not want books, I am satisfied with my food, and do not care to walk about; but I wish to see the governor.”", "\"책은 필요 없고, 음식은 이대로 만족하고, 거니는 데도 관심 없소. 다만 성주를 만나고 싶소.\""]], [["“If you worry me by repeating the same thing, I will not bring you any more to eat.”", "\"같은 말만 되풀이해서 나를 성가시게 하면, 더는 먹을 것을 가져다주지 않겠소.\""]], [["“Well, then,”", "\"그럼, 좋소.\""], ["said Edmond, “if you do not, I shall die of hunger—that is all.”", "에드몽이 말했다. \"안 가져다주면 나는 굶어 죽겠지. 그뿐이오.\""]], [["The jailer saw by his tone he would be happy to die; and as every prisoner is worth ten sous a day to his jailer, he replied in a more subdued tone.", "간수는 그 말투로 그가 기꺼이 죽으려 한다는 것을 알아챘다. 그리고 죄수 하나가 간수에게 하루 십 수의 값어치가 있는 터라, 그는 한결 누그러진 목소리로 대답했다."]], [["“What you ask is impossible; but if you are very well behaved you will be allowed to walk about, and some day you will meet the governor, and if he chooses to reply, that is his affair.”", "\"당신이 청하는 건 불가능하오. 하지만 아주 얌전히 굴면 바깥을 거니는 것이 허락될 것이고, 그러다 언젠가 성주를 마주칠지도 모르오. 그때 성주가 대답해 주고 싶다면, 그건 그분 마음이지.\""]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["asked Dantès, “how long shall I have to wait?”", "당테스가 물었다. \"그게 얼마나 기다려야 하는 거요?\""]], [["“Ah, a month—six months—a year.”", "\"아, 한 달, 여섯 달, 아니면 일 년.\""]], [["“It is too long a time.", "\"너무 긴 시간이오."], ["I wish to see him at once.”", "당장 만나고 싶소.\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["said the jailer, “do not always brood over what is impossible, or you will be mad in a fortnight.”", "간수가 말했다. \"불가능한 일만 곱씹지 마시오. 안 그러면 보름 만에 미쳐 버릴 테니.\""]], [["“You think so?”", "\"그렇게 생각하오?\""]], [["“Yes; we have an instance here; it was by always offering a million of francs to the governor for his liberty that an abbé became mad, who was in this chamber before you.”", "\"그렇소. 여기 본보기가 하나 있소. 당신보다 먼저 이 방에 있던 어떤 신부가, 자유를 달라며 성주에게 줄곧 백만 프랑을 주겠다고 하다가 미쳐 버렸지.\""]], [["0119m\n“How long has he left it?”", "\"그가 여기를 떠난 지 얼마나 됐소?\""]], [["“Two years.”", "\"이 년.\""]], [["“Was he liberated, then?”", "\"그럼 풀려난 거요?\""]], [["“No; he was put in a dungeon.”", "\"아니. 지하 감옥에 처넣어졌소.\""]], [["“Listen!” said Dantès.", "\"잘 들으시오!\" 당테스가 말했다."], ["“I am not an abbé, I am not mad; perhaps I shall be, but at present, unfortunately, I am not.", "\"나는 신부가 아니고, 미치지도 않았소. 어쩌면 미칠지도 모르지만, 지금은 안타깝게도 멀쩡하오."], ["I will make you another offer.”", "다른 제안을 하나 하겠소.\""]], [["“What is that?”", "\"그게 뭐요?\""]], [["“I do not offer you a million, because I have it not; but I will give you a hundred crowns if, the first time you go to Marseilles, you will seek out a young girl named Mercédès, at the Catalans, and give her two lines from me.” 0120m", "\"당신에게 백만 프랑을 주겠다는 건 아니오. 내겐 그런 돈이 없으니까. 하지만 다음번 마르세유에 갈 때, 카탈루냐 마을에 사는 메르세데스라는 처녀를 찾아가 내 글 두 줄을 전해 준다면, 백 크라운을 주겠소.\""]], [["“If I took them, and were detected, I should lose my place, which is worth two thousand francs a year; so that I should be a great fool to run such a risk for three hundred.”", "\"그걸 가져갔다가 들키면 나는 일자리를 잃을 거요. 일 년에 이천 프랑짜리 자리란 말이오. 그러니 삼백 프랑 때문에 그런 위험을 무릅쓴다면 내가 천하의 바보지.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"좋소,\""], ["said Dantès, “mark this; if you refuse at least to tell Mercédès I am here, I will some day hide myself behind the door, and when you enter I will dash out your brains with this stool.”", "당테스가 말했다. \"그럼 이 말을 새겨들으시오. 적어도 내가 여기 있다는 걸 메르세데스에게 알려 주는 것조차 거절한다면, 언젠가 문 뒤에 숨어 있다가 당신이 들어올 때 이 걸상으로 당신 머리통을 박살 내겠소.\""]], [["“Threats!”", "\"협박이라니!\""], ["cried the jailer, retreating and putting himself on the defensive; “you are certainly going mad.", "간수가 뒤로 물러서며 방어 자세를 취하고 외쳤다. \"당신 정말 미쳐 가는군. 그 신부도 당신처럼 시작했지."], ["The abbé began like you, and in three days you will be like him, mad enough to tie up; but, fortunately, there are dungeons here.”", "사흘이면 당신도 그자처럼 될 거요. 꽁꽁 묶어 둘 만큼 단단히 미칠 테지. 하지만 다행히도 여기엔 지하 감옥이 있소.\""]], [["Dantès whirled the stool round his head.", "당테스가 걸상을 머리 위로 휘둘렀다."]], [["“All right, all right,”", "\"알았소, 알았어.\""], ["said the jailer; “all right, since you will have it so.", "간수가 말했다. \"알았으니, 정 그렇다면."], ["I will send word to the governor.”", "성주께 말씀을 전하겠소.\""]], [["“Very well,”", "\"좋소.\""], ["returned Dantès, dropping the stool and sitting on it as if he were in reality mad.", "당테스가 걸상을 내려놓고는 정말로 미친 사람처럼 그 위에 앉으며 대꾸했다."], ["The jailer went out, and returned in an instant with a corporal and four soldiers.", "간수는 나갔다가, 곧 하사 한 명과 병사 넷을 데리고 돌아왔다."]], [["“By the governor’s orders,”", "\"성주의 명령이다.\""], ["said he, “conduct the prisoner to the tier beneath.”", "그가 말했다. \"이 죄수를 아래층으로 데려가라.\""]], [["“To the dungeon, then,”", "\"그럼 지하 감옥으로군.\""], ["said the corporal.", "하사가 말했다."]], [["“Yes; we must put the madman with the madmen.”", "\"그렇다. 미친놈은 미친놈들 틈에 넣어야지.\""], ["The soldiers seized Dantès, who followed passively.", "병사들이 당테스를 붙잡았고, 그는 순순히 따라갔다."]], [["He descended fifteen steps, and the door of a dungeon was opened, and he was thrust in.", "그는 열다섯 계단을 내려갔고, 지하 감옥 문이 열리자 그 안으로 떠밀려 들어갔다."], ["The door closed, and Dantès advanced with outstretched hands until he touched the wall; he then sat down in the corner until his eyes became accustomed to the darkness.", "문이 닫혔고, 당테스는 두 손을 앞으로 뻗은 채 벽에 닿을 때까지 나아갔다. 그런 다음 눈이 어둠에 익을 때까지 구석에 앉아 있었다."], ["The jailer was right; Dantès wanted but little of being utterly mad.", "간수의 말이 옳았다. 당테스는 완전히 미치기까지 그리 멀지 않았다."]], [["# Chapter 9. The Evening of the Betrothal", "# Chapter 9. The Evening of the Betrothal"]], [["Villefort had, as we have said, hastened back to Madame de Saint-Méran’s in the Place du Grand Cours, and on entering the house found that the guests whom he had left at table were taking coffee in the salon.", "빌포르는 앞서 말한 대로 그랑쿠르 광장에 있는 생메랑 부인의 저택으로 서둘러 돌아왔다. 집에 들어서니 식탁에 두고 떠났던 손님들이 살롱에서 커피를 마시고 있었다."], ["Renée was, with all the rest of the company, anxiously awaiting him, and his entrance was followed by a general exclamation.", "르네는 다른 사람들과 함께 그를 애타게 기다리고 있었고, 그가 들어서자 모두가 일제히 탄성을 질렀다."]], [["“Well, Decapitator, Guardian of the State, Royalist, Brutus, what is the matter?”", "\"자, 단두대 집행관, 국가의 수호자, 왕당파, 브루투스 양반, 무슨 일이오?\" 한 사람이 말했다."], ["said one. “Speak out.”", "\"어디 털어놓아 보시오.\""]], [["“Are we threatened with a fresh Reign of Terror?” asked another.", "\"또다시 공포 정치의 위협이라도 닥친 거요?\" 다른 사람이 물었다."]], [["“Has the Corsican ogre broken loose?” cried a third.", "\"코르시카의 식인귀가 풀려나기라도 했소?\" 또 다른 사람이 외쳤다."]], [["“Marquise,”", "\"후작 부인,\""], ["said Villefort, approaching his future mother-in-law, “I request your pardon for thus leaving you.", "빌포르가 장차 장모가 될 사람에게 다가가며 말했다. \"이렇게 자리를 비우게 되어 용서를 구합니다."], ["Will the marquis honor me by a few moments’ private conversation?”", "후작님께 잠시 단둘이 말씀드릴 영광을 베풀어 주실 수 있겠습니까?\""]], [["“Ah, it is really a serious matter, then?”", "\"아, 그렇다면 정말 심각한 일이로군?\""], ["asked the marquis, remarking the cloud on Villefort’s brow.", "후작이 빌포르의 이마에 드리운 그늘을 알아채고 물었다."]], [["“So serious that I must take leave of you for a few days; so,”", "\"며칠 동안 떠나 있어야 할 만큼 심각합니다. 그러니,\""], ["added he, turning to Renée, “judge for yourself if it be not important.”", "그가 르네 쪽으로 돌아서며 덧붙였다. \"그것이 중요한 일인지 아닌지는 직접 판단해 보시지요.\""]], [["“You are going to leave us?”", "\"저희를 떠나신다고요?\""], ["cried Renée, unable to hide her emotion at this unexpected announcement.", "르네가 이 뜻밖의 통보에 동요를 감추지 못하고 외쳤다."]], [["“Alas,”", "\"아아,\""], ["returned Villefort, “I must!”", "빌포르가 대답했다. \"어쩔 수 없습니다!\""]], [["“Where, then, are you going?”", "\"그래, 어디로 가시는 겁니까?\""], ["asked the marquise.", "후작 부인이 물었다."]], [["“That, madame, is an official secret; but if you have any commissions for Paris, a friend of mine is going there tonight, and will with pleasure undertake them.”", "\"그건, 부인, 공무상의 비밀입니다. 다만 파리에 부탁하실 일이 있으시면, 오늘 밤 제 친구 하나가 그리로 가니 기꺼이 맡아 줄 겁니다.\""], ["The guests looked at each other.", "손님들이 서로 얼굴을 마주 보았다."]], [["“You wish to speak to me alone?”", "\"나하고만 이야기하고 싶다는 건가?\""], ["said the marquis.", "후작이 말했다."]], [["“Yes, let us go to the library, please.” The marquis took his arm, and they left the salon.", "\"네, 서재로 가시지요.\" 후작이 그의 팔을 잡았고, 두 사람은 살롱을 나섰다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"자,\""], ["asked he, as soon as they were by themselves, “tell me what it is?”", "단둘이 되자마자 후작이 물었다. \"무슨 일인지 말해 보게.\""]], [["“An affair of the greatest importance, that demands my immediate presence in Paris. Now, excuse the indiscretion, marquis, but have you any landed property?”", "\"더없이 중대한 사안이라, 제가 당장 파리로 가야 합니다. 그런데, 실례를 무릅쓰고 여쭙겠습니다만, 후작님께서는 토지 재산이 있으신지요?\""]], [["“All my fortune is in the funds; seven or eight hundred thousand francs.”", "\"내 재산은 전부 공채에 들어 있네. 칠팔십만 프랑쯤 되지.\""]], [["“Then sell out—sell out, marquis, or you will lose it all.”", "\"그렇다면 파십시오. 어서 파십시오, 후작님. 안 그러면 전부 잃으실 겁니다.\""]], [["0123m\n“But how can I sell out here?”", "\"하지만 여기서 어떻게 판단 말인가?\""]], [["“You have a broker, have you not?”", "\"증권 중개인이 있으시지요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"있네.\""]], [["“Then give me a letter to him, and tell him to sell out without an instant’s delay, perhaps even now I shall arrive too late.”", "\"그럼 그에게 편지를 한 통 써 주십시오. 한순간도 지체 말고 팔아 치우라고 하십시오. 어쩌면 지금도 이미 늦었을지 모릅니다.\""]], [["“The deuce you say!”", "\"이런 변고가!\""], ["replied the marquis, “let us lose no time, then!”", "후작이 대답했다. \"그렇다면 한시도 허비할 수 없지!\""]], [["And, sitting down, he wrote a letter to his broker, ordering him to sell out at the market price.", "그러고는 자리에 앉아 중개인에게 시세대로 팔아 달라는 편지를 썼다."]], [["“Now, then,”", "\"자, 그럼,\""], ["said Villefort, placing the letter in his pocketbook, “I must have another!”", "빌포르가 편지를 수첩에 넣으며 말했다. \"한 통이 더 필요합니다!\" \"누구한테?\""]], [["“To whom?” “To the king.”", "\"왕께 보낼 편지입니다.\""]], [["“To the king?”", "\"왕께?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“I dare not write to his majesty.”", "\"나는 감히 폐하께 편지를 쓸 수 없네.\""]], [["“I do not ask you to write to his majesty, but ask M. de Salvieux to do so.", "\"폐하께 직접 쓰시라는 게 아닙니다. 살비외 씨께 부탁해 써 달라고 하십시오."], ["I want a letter that will enable me to reach the king’s presence without all the formalities of demanding an audience; that would occasion a loss of precious time.”", "알현을 청하는 온갖 격식을 거치지 않고도 왕 앞에 나아갈 수 있게 해 줄 편지가 필요합니다. 그런 절차를 밟다가는 귀중한 시간을 잃게 될 테니까요.\""]], [["“But address yourself to the keeper of the seals; he has the right of entry at the Tuileries, and can procure you audience at any hour of the day or night.”", "\"하지만 옥새 상서에게 부탁하게. 그분은 튀일리 궁에 출입할 권한이 있어서, 밤이든 낮이든 언제든 알현을 주선해 줄 수 있네.\""]], [["“Doubtless; but there is no occasion to divide the honors of my discovery with him.", "\"물론입니다. 하지만 제 발견의 영예를 그분과 나눌 까닭이 없지요."], ["The keeper would leave me in the background, and take all the glory to himself.", "옥새 상서는 저를 뒷전으로 밀어내고 모든 공을 제 것으로 가로챌 겁니다."], ["I tell you, marquis, my fortune is made if I only reach the Tuileries the first, for the king will not forget the service I do him.”", "분명히 말씀드리지만, 후작님, 제가 튀일리 궁에 가장 먼저 닿기만 한다면 제 출세는 따 놓은 당상입니다. 왕께서는 제가 베푼 봉사를 잊지 않으실 테니까요.\""]], [["“In that case go and get ready.", "\"그렇다면 어서 가서 채비하게."], ["I will call Salvieux and make him write the letter.”", "내가 살비외를 불러 편지를 쓰게 하겠네.\""]], [["“Be as quick as possible, I must be on the road in a quarter of an hour.”", "\"되도록 빨리 부탁드립니다. 십오 분 안에 길을 나서야 하니까요.\""]], [["“Tell your coachman to stop at the door.”", "\"마부에게 문 앞에 대기하라고 일러 두게.\""]], [["“You will present my excuses to the marquise and Mademoiselle Renée, whom I leave on such a day with great regret.”", "\"후작 부인과 르네 양께 제 사정을 잘 전해 주십시오. 하필 이런 날 떠나게 되어 더없이 안타깝습니다.\""]], [["“You will find them both here, and can make your farewells in person.”", "\"두 사람 다 여기 있을 테니, 직접 작별 인사를 하면 되네.\""]], [["“A thousand thanks—and now for the letter.”", "\"정말 감사합니다. 그럼 이제 편지를 부탁드립니다.\""]], [["The marquis rang, a servant entered.", "후작이 종을 울리자 하인이 들어왔다."]], [["“Say to the Comte de Salvieux that I would like to see him.”", "\"살비외 백작께 좀 뵙고 싶다고 전하게.\""]], [["“Now, then, go,” said the marquis.", "\"자, 그럼 가 보게.\" 후작이 말했다."]], [["“I shall be gone only a few moments.”", "\"잠깐이면 돌아오겠습니다.\""]], [["Villefort hastily quitted the apartment, but reflecting that the sight of the deputy procureur running through the streets would be enough to throw the whole city into confusion, he resumed his ordinary pace.", "빌포르는 황급히 방을 나섰다. 그러나 차장 검사가 거리를 내달리는 모습이 온 도시를 혼란에 빠뜨릴 수 있다는 데 생각이 미쳐, 평소의 걸음걸이로 돌아갔다."], ["At his door he perceived a figure in the shadow that seemed to wait for him.", "자기 집 문 앞에 이르자 그림자 속에서 그를 기다리는 듯한 사람의 형체가 보였다."], ["It was Mercédès, who, hearing no news of her lover, had come unobserved to inquire after him.", "메르세데스였다. 연인의 소식을 듣지 못한 그녀가 그를 찾아 남몰래 온 것이었다."]], [["As Villefort drew near, she advanced and stood before him.", "빌포르가 다가가자 그녀가 앞으로 나서서 그의 앞에 섰다."], ["Dantès had spoken of Mercédès, and Villefort instantly recognized her.", "당테스가 메르세데스 이야기를 한 적이 있어, 빌포르는 그녀를 단번에 알아보았다."], ["Her beauty and high bearing surprised him, and when she inquired what had become of her lover, it seemed to him that she was the judge, and he the accused.", "그녀의 아름다움과 기품 있는 자태에 그는 놀랐고, 그녀가 자기 연인이 어떻게 되었느냐고 묻자, 마치 그녀가 재판관이고 자기가 피고가 된 듯한 기분이 들었다."]], [["“The young man you speak of,”", "\"그대가 말하는 그 젊은이는,\""], ["said Villefort abruptly, “is a great criminal, and I can do nothing for him, mademoiselle.”", "빌포르가 퉁명스럽게 말했다. \"중대한 죄인이오. 나로서는 그를 위해 해 줄 수 있는 일이 없소, 아가씨.\""], ["Mercédès burst into tears, and, as Villefort strove to pass her, again addressed him.", "메르세데스가 울음을 터뜨렸고, 빌포르가 그녀를 지나치려 하자 다시 말을 걸었다."]], [["“But, at least, tell me where he is, that I may know whether he is alive or dead,”", "\"하지만 적어도 그이가 어디 있는지는 알려 주세요. 살아 있는지 죽었는지라도 알 수 있게요.\""], ["said she. 0125m", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“I do not know; he is no longer in my hands,”", "\"나는 모르오. 그는 더 이상 내 손에 있지 않소.\""], ["replied Villefort.", "빌포르가 대답했다."]], [["And desirous of putting an end to the interview, he pushed by her, and closed the door, as if to exclude the pain he felt.", "그러고는 그 대면을 끝내고 싶은 마음에, 그녀를 밀치고 지나가 마치 자신이 느끼는 고통을 차단하려는 듯 문을 닫았다."], ["But remorse is not thus banished; like Virgil’s wounded hero, he carried the arrow in his wound, and, arrived at the salon, Villefort uttered a sigh that was almost a sob, and sank into a chair.", "그러나 양심의 가책은 그렇게 쫓아낼 수 있는 것이 아니었다. 베르길리우스가 그린 부상당한 영웅처럼, 그는 화살을 상처에 박은 채로 살롱에 이르렀고, 빌포르는 흐느낌에 가까운 한숨을 내쉬며 의자에 털썩 주저앉았다."]], [["Then the first pangs of an unending torture seized upon his heart.", "그러자 끝없는 고문의 첫 고통이 그의 가슴을 사로잡았다."], ["The man he sacrificed to his ambition, that innocent victim immolated on the altar of his father’s faults, appeared to him pale and threatening, leading his affianced bride by the hand, and bringing with him remorse, not such as the ancients figured, furious and terrible, but that slow and consuming agony whose pangs are intensified from hour to hour up to the very moment of death.", "자신의 야망을 위해 제물로 바친 사내, 아비의 죄과를 위해 제단에 바쳐진 그 무고한 희생자가, 창백하고 위협하는 모습으로 약혼녀의 손을 잡아 이끌고 그의 앞에 나타났다. 그리고 그와 함께 양심의 가책을 끌고 왔다. 그것은 고대인들이 그린, 광포하고 끔찍한 모습이 아니라, 죽음의 바로 그 순간까지 시시각각 그 고통이 더해 가는, 더디고 좀먹는 듯한 번민이었다."], ["Then he had a moment’s hesitation.", "그러자 그는 잠시 망설였다."], ["He had frequently called for capital punishment on criminals, and owing to his irresistible eloquence they had been condemned, and yet the slightest shadow of remorse had never clouded Villefort’s brow, because they were guilty; at least, he believed so; but here was an innocent man whose happiness he had destroyed.", "그는 그동안 죄인들에게 극형을 내려야 한다고 숱하게 주장해 왔고, 거역할 수 없는 그의 웅변 덕에 그들은 유죄 판결을 받았다. 그런데도 양심의 가책이 그 그림자 한 점조차 빌포르의 이마를 흐린 적이 없었다. 그들은 죄가 있었기 때문이다."], ["In this case he was not the judge, but the executioner.", "적어도 그는 그렇게 믿었다. 하지만 지금 여기 있는 것은 자기가 행복을 짓밟아 버린 무고한 사람이었다. 이번에는 그가 재판관이 아니라 사형 집행인이었다."]], [["As he thus reflected, he felt the sensation we have described, and which had hitherto been unknown to him, arise in his bosom, and fill him with vague apprehensions.", "그렇게 곱씹는 사이, 그는 앞서 묘사한, 여태껏 그가 알지 못했던 그 감각이 가슴속에서 솟아올라 막연한 불안으로 자신을 채우는 것을 느꼈다."], ["It is thus that a wounded man trembles instinctively at the approach of the finger to his wound until it be healed, but Villefort’s was one of those that never close, or if they do, only close to reopen more agonizing than ever.", "그것은 부상당한 사람이 상처가 아물 때까지 그 상처로 손가락이 다가오면 본능적으로 떠는 것과 같았다. 그러나 빌포르의 상처는 결코 아물지 않는 부류의 것이었다. 설령 아문다 해도 그것은 전보다 더 고통스럽게 다시 벌어지기 위해 아무는 것일 뿐이었다."], ["If at this moment the sweet voice of Renée had sounded in his ears pleading for mercy, or the fair Mercédès had entered and said, “In the name of God, I conjure you to restore me my affianced husband,”", "만약 이 순간 르네의 다정한 목소리가 그의 귀에 자비를 청하며 들려왔거나, 아름다운 메르세데스가 들어와 \"하느님의 이름으로 간청하니, 제 약혼자를 돌려주세요.\""], ["his cold and trembling hands would have signed his release; but no voice broke the stillness of the chamber, and the door was opened only by Villefort’s valet, who came to tell him that the travelling carriage was in readiness.", "하고 말했더라면, 차갑게 떨리는 그의 두 손은 그의 석방 서류에 서명하고 말았을 것이다. 그러나 그 어떤 목소리도 방 안의 정적을 깨뜨리지 않았고, 문이 열린 것은 그저 여행 마차가 준비되었음을 알리러 온 빌포르의 하인에 의해서였다."]], [["Villefort rose, or rather sprang, from his chair, hastily opened one of the drawers of his desk, emptied all the gold it contained into his pocket, stood motionless an instant, his hand pressed to his head, muttered a few inarticulate sounds, and then, perceiving that his servant had placed his cloak on his shoulders, he sprang into the carriage, ordering the postilions to drive to M.", "빌포르가 자리에서 일어났다, 아니 벌떡 솟구쳐 올랐다. 그는 황급히 책상 서랍 하나를 열어 안에 든 금화를 전부 호주머니에 쏟아 넣고는, 한 손을 머리에 짚은 채 잠시 꼼짝 않고 서 있다가, 알아들을 수 없는 소리를 몇 마디 중얼거렸다. 그러다 하인이 자기 어깨에 외투를 걸쳐 준 것을 알아채고는 마차 안으로 뛰어들어, 마부에게 생메랑 씨 댁으로 몰라고 명령했다."], ["de Saint-Méran’s. The hapless Dantès was doomed.", "가엾은 당테스의 운명은 결정된 것이었다."]], [["As the marquis had promised, Villefort found the marquise and Renée in waiting.", "후작이 약속한 대로, 빌포르는 후작 부인과 르네가 기다리고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["He started when he saw Renée, for he fancied she was again about to plead for Dantès.", "르네를 보자 그는 흠칫했다. 그녀가 또다시 당테스를 위해 호소하려는 줄 알았기 때문이다."], ["Alas, her emotions were wholly personal: she was thinking only of Villefort’s departure.", "그러나 아아, 그녀의 감정은 온전히 자신에 관한 것이었다. 그녀는 오직 빌포르가 떠난다는 사실만을 생각하고 있었다."]], [["She loved Villefort, and he left her at the moment he was about to become her husband.", "그녀는 빌포르를 사랑했고, 그는 막 그녀의 남편이 되려는 순간에 그녀를 두고 떠나는 것이었다."], ["Villefort knew not when he should return, and Renée, far from pleading for Dantès, hated the man whose crime separated her from her lover.", "빌포르는 자신이 언제 돌아올지 알 수 없었고, 르네는 당테스를 위해 호소하기는커녕, 자신을 연인에게서 갈라놓은 그 죄인을 미워했다."]], [["0127m\nMeanwhile what of Mercédès?", "그러는 사이 메르세데스는 어찌 되었는가?"], ["She had met Fernand at the corner of the Rue de la Loge; she had returned to the Catalans, and had despairingly cast herself on her couch.", "그녀는 로주 거리 모퉁이에서 페르낭과 마주쳤다. 그녀는 카탈루냐 마을로 돌아와 절망에 빠진 채 침상에 몸을 던졌다."], ["Fernand, kneeling by her side, took her hand, and covered it with kisses that Mercédès did not even feel.", "페르낭이 그녀 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 그녀의 손을 잡아 입맞춤을 퍼부었으나, 메르세데스는 그것을 느끼지조차 못했다."], ["She passed the night thus.", "그녀는 그렇게 밤을 지새웠다."], ["The lamp went out for want of oil, but she paid no heed to the darkness, and dawn came, but she knew not that it was day. Grief had made her blind to all but one object—that was Edmond.", "등불은 기름이 떨어져 꺼졌으나 그녀는 어둠에도 아랑곳하지 않았고, 새벽이 왔으나 날이 밝은 줄도 몰랐다. 슬픔이 그녀를 단 하나의 대상, 곧 에드몽 외에는 아무것도 보지 못하게 만든 것이었다."]], [["“Ah, you are there,”", "\"아, 거기 계셨군요.\""], ["said she, at length, turning towards Fernand.", "그녀가 마침내 페르낭 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다."]], [["“I have not quitted you since yesterday,”", "\"어제부터 줄곧 곁을 떠나지 않았어.\""], ["returned Fernand sorrowfully.", "페르낭이 서글프게 대답했다."]], [["M. Morrel had not readily given up the fight.", "모렐 씨는 호락호락 싸움을 포기하지 않았다."], ["He had learned that Dantès had been taken to prison, and he had gone to all his friends, and the influential persons of the city; but the report was already in circulation that Dantès was arrested as a Bonapartist agent; and as the most sanguine looked upon any attempt of Napoleon to remount the throne as impossible, he met with nothing but refusal, and had returned home in despair, declaring that the matter was serious and that nothing more could be done.", "그는 당테스가 감옥으로 끌려갔다는 것을 알고는, 친구란 친구와 도시의 유력자들을 모조리 찾아다녔다. 그러나 당테스가 보나파르트파 첩자로 체포되었다는 소문이 이미 돌고 있었다. 게다가 가장 낙관적인 사람들조차 나폴레옹이 다시 왕좌에 오르려는 시도는 불가능한 일로 여겼던 터라, 그는 거절만 당하다가 절망에 빠져 집으로 돌아왔고, 사안이 심각해서 더 이상 할 수 있는 일이 없다고 토로했다."]], [["Caderousse was equally restless and uneasy, but instead of seeking, like M.", "카드루스도 못지않게 안절부절못하며 불안에 시달렸다."], ["Morrel, to aid Dantès, he had shut himself up with two bottles of black currant brandy, in the hope of drowning reflection.", "그러나 그는 모렐 씨처럼 당테스를 도우려 하기는커녕, 까막까치밥나무 브랜디 두 병을 들고 틀어박혀 생각을 잊어 보려 했다."], ["But he did not succeed, and became too intoxicated to fetch any more drink, and yet not so intoxicated as to forget what had happened.", "그러나 뜻대로 되지 않았다. 술을 더 가지러 갈 수 없을 만큼은 취했으나, 일어난 일을 잊을 만큼 취하지는 못한 것이다."], ["With his elbows on the table he sat between the two empty bottles, while spectres danced in the light of the unsnuffed candle—spectres such as Hoffmann strews over his punch-drenched pages, like black, fantastic dust.", "그는 두 팔꿈치를 탁자에 괴고 빈 병 두 개 사이에 앉아 있었고, 심지를 다듬지 않은 촛불 빛 속에서 망령들이 춤을 추었다. 호프만이 펀치에 흠뻑 젖은 책장 위에 검은 환상의 먼지처럼 흩뿌려 놓은 그런 망령들이었다."]], [["Danglars alone was content and joyous—he had got rid of an enemy and made his own situation on the _Pharaon_ secure.", "오직 당글라르만이 흡족하고 즐거웠다. 그는 적을 없앴고, 파라옹호에서 자기 자리를 굳건히 했다."], ["Danglars was one of those men born with a pen behind the ear, and an inkstand in place of a heart.", "당글라르는 귀 뒤에 펜을 꽂고, 심장 대신 잉크병을 단 채 태어난 부류의 사람이었다."], ["Everything with him was multiplication or subtraction.", "그에게는 모든 것이 곱셈 아니면 뺄셈이었다."], ["The life of a man was to him of far less value than a numeral, especially when, by taking it away, he could increase the sum total of his own desires.", "한 인간의 목숨도 그에게는 숫자 하나보다 훨씬 하찮은 것이었다. 더구나 그것을 없앰으로써 자신의 욕망을 합산한 총액을 늘릴 수 있을 때라면 더욱 그러했다."], ["He went to bed at his usual hour, and slept in peace.", "그는 여느 때와 같은 시각에 잠자리에 들었고, 평온하게 잠들었다."]], [["Villefort, after having received M. de Salvieux’s letter, embraced Renée, kissed the marquise’s hand, and shaken that of the marquis, started for Paris along the Aix road.", "빌포르는 살비외 씨의 편지를 받고 나서 르네를 끌어안고, 후작 부인의 손에 입을 맞추고, 후작과 악수를 나눈 뒤, 엑스 가도를 따라 파리로 출발했다."]], [["Old Dantès was dying with anxiety to know what had become of Edmond.", "늙은 당테스 노인은 에드몽이 어찌 되었는지 알고 싶은 마음에 애가 타 죽을 지경이었다."], ["But we know very well what had become of Edmond.", "그러나 에드몽이 어찌 되었는지는 우리가 잘 알고 있다."]], [["# Chapter 10. The King’s Closet at the Tuileries", "# Chapter 10. The King’s Closet at the Tuileries"]], [["We will leave Villefort on the road to Paris, travelling—thanks to trebled fees—with all speed, and passing through two or three apartments, enter at the Tuileries the little room with the arched window, so well known as having been the favorite closet of Napoleon and Louis XVIII., and now of Louis Philippe.", "우리는 빌포르를 파리로 향하는 길 위에 — 세 배의 요금 덕분에 전속력으로 달리며 — 남겨 두기로 하고, 두세 개의 방을 지나, 튈르리궁에 있는 아치형 창이 달린 작은 방으로 들어가자. 나폴레옹과 루이 18세, 그리고 지금은 루이 필리프가 가장 아끼는 방으로 잘 알려진 곳이다."]], [["There, seated before a walnut table he had brought with him from Hartwell, and to which, from one of those fancies not uncommon to great people, he was particularly attached, the king, Louis XVIII., was carelessly listening to a man of fifty or fifty-two years of age, with gray hair, aristocratic bearing, and exceedingly gentlemanly attire, and meanwhile making a marginal note in a volume of Gryphius’s rather inaccurate, but much sought-after, edition of Horace—a work which was much indebted to the sagacious observations of the philosophical monarch.", "그곳, 하트웰에서 가져온 호두나무 탁자 앞에 — 위인들에게 드물지 않은 그런 변덕 가운데 하나로, 그는 그 탁자에 유독 애착이 깊었다 — 국왕 루이 18세가 앉아, 쉰이나 쉰두 살쯤 된 한 사내의 말을 무심히 듣고 있었다. 그 사내는 머리가 희끗하고, 귀족적인 풍채에, 더없이 신사다운 차림새였다. 그러는 동안 국왕은 그리피우스판 호라티우스 시집 — 다소 부정확하지만 무척 찾는 이가 많은 판본 — 의 여백에 주석을 달고 있었는데, 그 판본은 이 철학적인 군주의 명민한 관찰에 적잖이 빚지고 있었다."]], [["“You say, sir——”", "\"경의 말씀은……\""], ["said the king.", "국왕이 말했다."]], [["“That I am exceedingly disquieted, sire.”", "\"제가 몹시 불안하다는 것입니다, 폐하.\""]], [["“Really, have you had a vision of the seven fat kine and the seven lean kine?”", "\"정말이지, 살진 암소 일곱 마리와 야윈 암소 일곱 마리의 환영이라도 보았소?\""]], [["“No, sire, for that would only betoken for us seven years of plenty and seven years of scarcity; and with a king as full of foresight as your majesty, scarcity is not a thing to be feared.”", "\"아닙니다, 폐하. 그것이라면 우리에게 칠 년의 풍년과 칠 년의 흉년을 뜻할 뿐이니까요. 그리고 폐하처럼 선견지명이 가득하신 군주를 모신 마당에, 흉년 따위는 두려워할 일이 아니지요.\""]], [["“Then of what other scourge are you afraid, my dear Blacas?”", "\"그렇다면 또 무슨 재앙이 두렵단 말이오, 나의 블라카스 경?\""]], [["“Sire, I have every reason to believe that a storm is brewing in the south.”", "\"폐하, 저는 남부에 폭풍이 일고 있다고 믿을 만한 근거가 충분합니다.\""]], [["“Well, my dear duke,”", "\"이보게, 공작,\""], ["replied Louis XVIII., “I think you are wrongly informed, and know positively that, on the contrary, it is very fine weather in that direction.”", "루이 18세가 대답했다. \"내 생각엔 경이 잘못된 정보를 들은 게요. 오히려 그쪽은 아주 날씨가 좋다는 걸 나는 분명히 알고 있소.\""], ["Man of ability as he was, Louis XVIII. liked a pleasant jest.", "유능한 사람답게, 루이 18세는 기분 좋은 익살을 즐겼다."]], [["“Sire,”", "\"폐하,\""], ["continued M. de Blacas, “if it only be to reassure a faithful servant, will your majesty send into Languedoc, Provence, and Dauphiné, trusty men, who will bring you back a faithful report as to the feeling in these three provinces?”", "블라카스 씨가 말을 이었다. \"충직한 신하 하나를 안심시키기 위해서라도, 폐하께서 랑그도크와 프로방스, 도피네에 믿을 만한 사람들을 보내시어, 이 세 지방의 민심에 관해 충실한 보고를 받아 보시는 것이 어떻겠습니까?\""]], [["“_Canimus surdis_,”", "\"카니무스 수르디스.\""], ["replied the king, continuing the annotations in his Horace.", "국왕이 호라티우스에 주석을 계속 달며 대답했다."]], [["“Sire,”", "\"폐하,\""], ["replied the courtier, laughing, in order that he might seem to comprehend the quotation, “your majesty may be perfectly right in relying on the good feeling of France, but I fear I am not altogether wrong in dreading some desperate attempt.”", "신하가 그 인용을 알아듣는 척하려고 웃으며 대답했다. \"프랑스의 선량한 민심을 믿으시는 폐하의 판단이 더없이 옳을지도 모릅니다. 하오나 어떤 무모한 시도를 염려하는 저의 두려움도 아주 틀리지만은 않을 듯합니다.\""]], [["“By whom?” “By Bonaparte, or, at least, by his adherents.”", "\"누구에 의해서요?\" \"보나파르트, 적어도 그의 추종자들에 의한 것입니다.\""]], [["“My dear Blacas,”", "\"나의 블라카스 경,\""], ["said the king, “you with your alarms prevent me from working.”", "국왕이 말했다. \"경은 그 불안 타령으로 내가 일을 못 하게 만드는구려.\""]], [["“And you, sire, prevent me from sleeping with your security.”", "\"그리고 폐하께서는 그 태평하심으로 제가 잠을 이루지 못하게 하십니다.\""]], [["“Wait, my dear sir, wait a moment; for I have such a delightful note on the _Pastor quum traheret_—wait, and I will listen to you afterwards.”", "\"기다리시오, 경, 잠깐만 기다리시오. 마침 '파스토르 쿰 트라헤레트' 구절에 대해 아주 흥미로운 주석거리가 떠올랐으니. 기다리시오, 그런 뒤에 경의 말을 듣겠소.\""]], [["There was a brief pause, during which Louis XVIII. wrote, in a hand as small as possible, another note on the margin of his Horace, and then looking at the duke with the air of a man who thinks he has an idea of his own, while he is only commenting upon the idea of another, said:", "잠시 침묵이 흘렀고, 그동안 루이 18세는 호라티우스의 여백에 되도록 잔글씨로 또 하나의 주석을 적었다. 그러고는 남의 생각에 주석을 다는 것뿐이면서 마치 제 나름의 착상이 떠올랐다고 여기는 사람의 표정으로 공작을 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Go on, my dear duke, go on—I listen.”", "\"계속하시오, 공작, 계속하시오 — 듣고 있소.\""]], [["“Sire,”", "\"폐하,\""], ["said Blacas, who had for a moment the hope of sacrificing Villefort to his own profit, “I am compelled to tell you that these are not mere rumors destitute of foundation which thus disquiet me; but a serious-minded man, deserving all my confidence, and charged by me to watch over the south”", "블라카스가 말했다. 그는 한순간 빌포르를 제 잇속을 위해 희생시킬 수 있으리라는 희망을 품었다. \"감히 말씀드리오니, 저를 이토록 불안하게 하는 것은 근거 없는 한낱 풍문 따위가 아닙니다."], ["(the duke hesitated as he pronounced these words), “has arrived by post to tell me that a great peril threatens the king, and so I hastened to you, sire.”", "제가 전적으로 신뢰하며 남부를 감시하라고 맡긴 진중한 인물이\" (공작은 이 말을 하면서 잠시 머뭇거렸다) \"역마를 달려와, 국왕께 큰 위험이 닥쳐오고 있다고 전했습니다. 그래서 서둘러 폐하께 달려온 것입니다.\""]], [["“_Mala ducis avi domum_,”", "\"말라 두키스 아비 도뭄.\""], ["continued Louis XVIII., still annotating.", "루이 18세가 여전히 주석을 달며 말을 이었다."]], [["“Does your majesty wish me to drop the subject?”", "\"이 이야기를 그만두기를 바라시옵니까, 폐하?\""]], [["“By no means, my dear duke; but just stretch out your hand.”", "\"천만에, 공작. 다만 손 좀 뻗어 보시오.\" \"어느 쪽을요?\""]], [["“Which?” “Whichever you please—there to the left.”", "\"아무 쪽이나 좋소 — 저기 왼쪽에.\""]], [["“I tell you to the left, and you are looking to the right; I mean on my left—yes, there.", "\"여기 말씀입니까, 폐하?\" \"왼쪽이라 했더니 오른쪽을 보고 있구려."], ["You will find yesterday’s report of the minister of police.", "내 왼쪽 말이오 — 그래, 거기. 어제 자 경찰청장 보고서가 있을 게요."], ["But here is M. Dandré himself;” and M.", "아, 마침 당드레 청장이 직접 왔군.\""], ["Dandré, announced by the chamberlain-in-waiting, entered.", "그러자 시종이 알린 대로 당드레 씨가 들어왔다."]], [["“Come in,”", "\"들어오시오,\""], ["said Louis XVIII., with repressed smile, “come in, Baron, and tell the duke all you know—the latest news of M.", "루이 18세가 웃음을 억누르며 말했다. \"들어와서 공작에게 그대가 아는 것을 모두 말해 주시오 — 보나파르트 씨에 관한 최신 소식 말이오."], ["de Bonaparte; do not conceal anything, however serious,—let us see, the Island of Elba is a volcano, and we may expect to have issuing thence flaming and bristling war—_bella, horrida bella_.”", "아무리 심각한 것이라도 숨기지 마시오. 자, 봅시다, 엘바섬은 화산이니, 그곳에서 화염을 내뿜고 가시 돋친 전쟁이 솟아 나오리라 — 벨라, 호리다 벨라.\""]], [["M. Dandré leaned very respectfully on the back of a chair with his two hands, and said:", "당드레 씨는 두 손으로 의자 등받이에 아주 공손히 기대며 말했다."]], [["“Has your majesty perused yesterday’s report?”", "\"폐하께서는 어제 자 보고서를 읽어 보셨습니까?\""]], [["“Yes, yes; but tell the duke himself, who cannot find anything, what the report contains—give him the particulars of what the usurper is doing in his islet.”", "\"읽었소, 읽었소. 허나 아무것도 못 찾는 공작에게 직접 그 보고서의 내용을 말해 주시오 — 그 찬탈자가 제 작은 섬에서 무슨 짓을 하고 있는지 소상히 일러 주시오.\""]], [["“Monsieur,”", "\"공작님,\""], ["said the baron to the duke, “all the servants of his majesty must approve of the latest intelligence which we have from the Island of Elba.", "남작이 공작에게 말했다. \"폐하의 모든 신하께서 엘바섬에서 온 최신 정보를 반기실 것입니다."], ["Bonaparte——”", "보나파르트는……\""]], [["M. Dandré looked at Louis XVIII., who, employed in writing a note, did not even raise his head.", "당드레 씨가 루이 18세를 바라보았으나, 국왕은 주석을 적느라 고개조차 들지 않았다. \"보나파르트는,\""], ["“Bonaparte,” continued the baron, “is mortally wearied, and passes whole days in watching his miners at work at Porto-Longone.”", "남작이 말을 이었다. \"지독한 권태에 빠져, 포르토롱고네에서 광부들이 일하는 모습을 지켜보며 날을 통째로 보내고 있습니다.\""]], [["“And scratches himself for amusement,”", "\"그리고 심심풀이로 제 몸을 긁어 대지.\""], ["added the king.", "국왕이 덧붙였다."]], [["“Scratches himself?”", "\"몸을 긁다니요?\""], ["inquired the duke, “what does your majesty mean?”", "공작이 물었다. \"폐하, 무슨 말씀이십니까?\""]], [["“Yes, indeed, my dear duke.", "\"그렇다오, 공작."], ["Did you forget that this great man, this hero, this demigod, is attacked with a malady of the skin which worries him to death, _prurigo_?”", "그 위대한 인물, 그 영웅, 그 반신(半神)이 사람을 죽도록 괴롭히는 피부병에 걸렸다는 걸 잊으셨소? 가려움증 말이오, 프루리고.\""]], [["“And, moreover, my dear duke,”", "\"게다가 말입니다, 공작님,\""], ["continued the minister of police, “we are almost assured that, in a very short time, the usurper will be insane.”", "경찰청장이 말을 이었다. \"머지않아 그 찬탈자가 미쳐 버릴 거라는 게 거의 확실합니다.\""]], [["“Insane?”", "\"미쳐?\""]], [["“Raving mad; his head becomes weaker.", "\"완전히 광인이 되는 겁니다."], ["Sometimes he weeps bitterly, sometimes laughs boisterously, at other time he passes hours on the seashore, flinging stones in the water and when the flint makes ‘duck-and-drake’ five or six times, he appears as delighted as if he had gained another Marengo or Austerlitz.", "머리가 점점 약해지고 있어요. 어느 때는 비통하게 울고, 어느 때는 떠들썩하게 웃고, 또 어느 때는 몇 시간씩 해변에서 물수제비를 뜬답니다. 그러다 돌멩이가 대여섯 번 튀면, 마치 또 한 번 마렝고나 아우스터리츠에서 승리라도 거둔 양 기뻐한다더군요."], ["Now, you must agree that these are indubitable symptoms of insanity.”", "자, 이것이야말로 광기의 확실한 징후라는 데 동의하실 겁니다.\""]], [["“Or of wisdom, my dear baron—or of wisdom,”", "\"아니면 지혜의 징후일 수도 있지, 친애하는 남작 — 지혜의 징후 말이오.\""], ["said Louis XVIII., laughing; “the greatest captains of antiquity amused themselves by casting pebbles into the ocean—see Plutarch’s life of Scipio Africanus.”", "루이 18세가 웃으며 말했다. \"고대의 가장 위대한 장군들도 바다에 돌멩이를 던지며 즐겼다오 — 플루타르코스가 쓴 스키피오 아프리카누스 전기를 보시오.\""]], [["M. de Blacas pondered deeply between the confident monarch and the truthful minister.", "블라카스 씨는 자신만만한 군주와 진실을 말하는 청장 사이에서 깊은 생각에 잠겼다."], ["Villefort, who did not choose to reveal the whole secret, lest another should reap all the benefit of the disclosure, had yet communicated enough to cause him the greatest uneasiness.", "빌포르는 폭로의 이득을 남이 다 거둘까 봐 비밀을 전부 털어놓지는 않았으나, 공작을 더없이 불안하게 만들 만큼은 일러 둔 터였다."]], [["“Well, well, Dandré,”", "\"좋소, 좋소, 당드레,\""], ["said Louis XVIII., “Blacas is not yet convinced; let us proceed, therefore, to the usurper’s conversion.”", "루이 18세가 말했다. \"블라카스가 아직도 수긍하지 못하니, 그렇다면 그 찬탈자의 개심(改心) 이야기로 넘어갑시다.\""], ["The minister of police bowed.", "경찰청장이 고개를 숙였다."]], [["“The usurper’s conversion!”", "\"찬탈자의 개심이라!\""], ["murmured the duke, looking at the king and Dandré, who spoke alternately, like Virgil’s shepherds.", "공작이 국왕과 당드레를 번갈아 바라보며 중얼거렸다. 두 사람이 베르길리우스의 목동들처럼 번갈아 말을 주고받았던 것이다."], ["“The usurper converted!”", "\"찬탈자가 개심을 했다고!\""]], [["“Decidedly, my dear duke.”", "\"분명히 그렇소, 공작.\""]], [["“In what way converted?”", "\"어떤 식으로 개심을 했다는 말씀입니까?\""]], [["“To good principles.", "\"올바른 신념으로 말이오."], ["Tell him all about it, baron.”", "남작, 그에게 자초지종을 일러 주시오.\""]], [["“Why, this is the way of it,”", "\"실은 이렇습니다.\" 청장이 세상에 더없이 진중한 표정으로 말했다."], ["said the minister, with the gravest air in the world: “Napoleon lately had a review, and as two or three of his old veterans expressed a desire to return to France, he gave them their dismissal, and exhorted them to ‘serve the good king.’ These were his own words, of that I am certain.”", "\"나폴레옹이 얼마 전 사열을 했는데, 늙은 노병 두셋이 프랑스로 돌아가고 싶다는 뜻을 비치자, 그들을 제대시키며 '선량하신 국왕을 섬기라'고 권했답니다. 이것이 바로 그자가 직접 한 말입니다. 그 점은 제가 확신합니다.\""]], [["“Well, Blacas, what think you of this?” inquired the king triumphantly, and pausing for a moment from the voluminous scholiast before him.", "\"자, 블라카스, 이 일을 어찌 생각하시오?\" 국왕이 의기양양하게 물으며, 앞에 놓인 방대한 주석서에서 잠시 손을 멈췄다."]], [["“I say, sire, that the minister of police is greatly deceived or I am; and as it is impossible it can be the minister of police as he has the guardianship of the safety and honor of your majesty, it is probable that I am in error.", "\"폐하, 제 말씀은, 경찰청장이 크게 속고 있거나, 아니면 제가 속고 있다는 것입니다. 그런데 폐하의 안전과 명예를 지키는 일을 맡은 경찰청장이 속았을 리 없으니, 아마도 제가 잘못 안 것이겠지요."], ["However, sire, if I might advise, your majesty will interrogate the person of whom I spoke to you, and I will urge your majesty to do him this honor.”", "하오나 폐하, 제 의견을 올려도 된다면, 제가 말씀드린 그 인물을 직접 신문해 주시기를 청하옵고, 부디 그자에게 그런 영광을 베풀어 주시기를 폐하께 간곡히 권하옵니다.\""]], [["“Most willingly, duke; under your auspices I will receive any person you please, but you must not expect me to be too confiding.", "\"기꺼이 그러겠소, 공작. 경이 천거하는 사람이라면 누구든 맞이하겠소. 다만 내가 너무 쉽게 믿으리라 기대하지는 마시오."], ["Baron, have you any report more recent than this, dated the 20th February, and this is the 3rd of March?”", "남작, 이 2월 20일 자 보고서보다 더 최근의 것은 없소? 오늘이 벌써 3월 3일인데.\""]], [["“No, sire, but I am hourly expecting one; it may have arrived since I left my office.”", "\"없습니다, 폐하. 하오나 시시각각 도착하기를 기다리고 있습니다. 제가 청을 나선 사이에 이미 도착했을지도 모르지요.\""]], [["“Go thither, and if there be none—well, well,”", "\"그리로 가 보시오. 만일 없거든 — 뭐, 좋소,\""], ["continued Louis XVIII., “make one; that is the usual way, is it not?”", "루이 18세가 말을 이었다. \"하나 지어내시오. 그게 으레 하는 방식이지 않소?\""], ["and the king laughed facetiously.", "그러고는 국왕이 익살스럽게 웃었다."]], [["“Oh, sire,”", "\"오, 폐하,\" 청장이 대답했다. \"굳이 지어낼 필요도 없습니다."], ["replied the minister, “we have no occasion to invent any; every day our desks are loaded with most circumstantial denunciations, coming from hosts of people who hope for some return for services which they seek to render, but cannot; they trust to fortune, and rely upon some unexpected event in some way to justify their predictions.”", "날마다 저희 책상에는 더없이 소상한 밀고가 산더미처럼 쌓이니까요. 자기가 베풀려 하나 베풀지 못하는 공로에 대해 무언가 보답을 바라는 무리에게서 옵니다. 그들은 요행에 기대어, 어떤 뜻밖의 사건이 어떻게든 제 예언을 입증해 주기를 바라는 것이지요.\""]], [["“Well, sir, go,”", "\"좋소, 그럼 가 보시오.\""], ["said Louis XVIII., “and remember that I am waiting for you.”", "루이 18세가 말했다. \"내가 기다리고 있다는 걸 잊지 마시오.\""]], [["“I will but go and return, sire; I shall be back in ten minutes.”", "\"다녀와서 곧 돌아오겠습니다, 폐하. 십 분이면 돌아오겠습니다.\""]], [["“And I, sire,” said M.", "\"그리고 저는, 폐하,\""], ["de Blacas, “will go and find my messenger.”", "블라카스 씨가 말했다. \"제 전령을 데려오겠습니다.\""]], [["“Wait, sir, wait,” said Louis XVIII.", "\"기다리시오, 경, 기다리시오.\" 루이 18세가 말했다."], ["“Really, M. de Blacas, I must change your armorial bearings; I will give you an eagle with outstretched wings, holding in its claws a prey which tries in vain to escape, and bearing this device—_Tenax_.” 0133m", "\"정말이지, 블라카스 경, 내 경의 문장(紋章)을 바꿔 주어야겠소. 날개를 활짝 편 독수리가 발톱에 헛되이 달아나려 발버둥치는 먹이를 움켜쥔 그림에, '테낙스'라는 명구를 붙여 주리다.\""]], [["“Sire, I listen,” said De Blacas, biting his nails with impatience.", "\"폐하, 듣고 있습니다.\" 드 블라카스가 초조함에 손톱을 깨물며 말했다."]], [["“I wish to consult you on this passage, ‘_Molli fugiens anhelitu_,’ you know it refers to a stag flying from a wolf.", "\"이 구절에 대해 경의 의견을 듣고 싶소. '몰리 푸기엔스 안헬리투', 늑대에게서 달아나는 사슴을 두고 한 말인 줄 경도 알 게요."], ["Are you not a sportsman and a great wolf-hunter?", "경은 사냥꾼이자 이름난 늑대 사냥꾼이 아니오?"], ["Well, then, what do you think of the _molli anhelitu_?”", "그러니, 이 '몰리 안헬리투'를 어찌 생각하시오?\""]], [["“Admirable, sire; but my messenger is like the stag you refer to, for he has posted two hundred and twenty leagues in scarcely three days.”", "\"훌륭합니다, 폐하. 하오나 제 전령이야말로 폐하께서 말씀하신 그 사슴 같습니다. 사흘이 채 안 되어 220리외를 역마로 달려왔으니까요.\""]], [["“Which is undergoing great fatigue and anxiety, my dear duke, when we have a telegraph which transmits messages in three or four hours, and that without getting in the least out of breath.”", "\"그건 큰 피로와 노심초사를 겪은 셈이오, 공작. 우리에게는 서너 시간 만에 소식을 전하는 전신이 있고, 그것도 숨 한 번 가빠지지 않으니 말이오.\""]], [["“Ah, sire, you recompense but badly this poor young man, who has come so far, and with so much ardor, to give your majesty useful information.", "\"아, 폐하, 폐하께서는 이토록 멀리서, 이토록 열성으로 폐하께 유익한 정보를 바치러 온 가엾은 젊은이에게 너무 박하게 대하십니다."], ["If only for the sake of M. de Salvieux, who recommends him to me, I entreat your majesty to receive him graciously.”", "그를 제게 천거한 드 살비외 씨의 낯을 보아서라도, 부디 그를 너그러이 맞아 주시기를 폐하께 간청하옵니다.\""]], [["“M. de Salvieux, my brother’s chamberlain?”", "\"드 살비외라면, 내 아우의 시종 말이오?\""]], [["“Yes, sire.”", "\"그렇습니다, 폐하.\""]], [["“He is at Marseilles.”", "\"그는 마르세유에 있소.\""]], [["“And writes me thence.”", "\"거기서 제게 편지를 보냈습니다.\""]], [["“Does he speak to you of this conspiracy?”", "\"그가 경에게 이 음모에 대해 말하던가?\""]], [["“No; but strongly recommends M. de Villefort, and begs me to present him to your majesty.”", "\"아닙니다. 다만 드 빌포르 씨를 강력히 천거하며, 그를 폐하께 알현시켜 달라고 청했습니다.\""]], [["“M. de Villefort!”", "\"드 빌포르 씨라니!\""], ["cried the king, “is the messenger’s name M. de Villefort?”", "국왕이 외쳤다. \"그 전령의 이름이 드 빌포르 씨란 말이오?\""]], [["“Yes, sire.”", "\"그렇습니다, 폐하.\""]], [["“And he comes from Marseilles?”", "\"그리고 마르세유에서 왔다고?\""]], [["“In person.”", "\"직접 말입니다.\""]], [["“Why did you not mention his name at once?”", "\"어찌하여 진작 그 이름을 말하지 않았소?\""], ["replied the king, betraying some uneasiness.", "국왕이 약간의 불안을 내비치며 대답했다."]], [["“Sire, I thought his name was unknown to your majesty.”", "\"폐하, 그 이름을 폐하께서 모르시리라 여겼습니다.\""]], [["“No, no, Blacas; he is a man of strong and elevated understanding, ambitious, too, and, _pardieu!_ you know his father’s name!”", "\"아니오, 아니오, 블라카스. 그는 강하고 고매한 식견을 지닌 자요, 야심도 크지. 게다가 빌어먹을, 경도 그 아비의 이름을 알지 않소!\""]], [["“His father?”", "\"그 아비를요?\""]], [["“Yes, Noirtier.”", "\"그렇소, 누아르티에 말이오.\""]], [["“Noirtier the Girondin?—Noirtier the senator?”", "\"지롱드파의 누아르티에 말씀입니까? — 원로원 의원 누아르티에?\""]], [["“He himself.”", "\"바로 그자요.\""]], [["“And your majesty has employed the son of such a man?”", "\"그런 자의 아들을 폐하께서 쓰셨단 말씀입니까?\""]], [["“Blacas, my friend, you have but limited comprehension.", "\"블라카스, 친구, 경은 도무지 이해가 짧구려."], ["I told you Villefort was ambitious, and to attain this ambition Villefort would sacrifice everything, even his father.”", "내 빌포르가 야심가라 하지 않았소. 그 야심을 이루기 위해서라면 빌포르는 무엇이든, 제 아비조차도 희생할 자요.\""]], [["“Then, sire, may I present him?”", "\"그렇다면, 폐하, 그를 들여보내도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["“This instant, duke! Where is he?”", "\"당장이오, 공작! 그가 어디 있소?\""]], [["“Waiting below, in my carriage.”", "\"아래, 제 마차 안에서 기다리고 있습니다.\""]], [["“Seek him at once.”", "\"어서 데려오시오.\""]], [["“I hasten to do so.”", "\"서둘러 그리하겠습니다.\""]], [["The duke left the royal presence with the speed of a young man; his really sincere royalism made him youthful again.", "공작은 젊은이 같은 날렵함으로 어전을 물러났다. 진심에서 우러난 왕당파 충정이 그를 다시 젊게 만든 것이다."], ["Louis XVIII. remained alone, and turning his eyes on his half-opened Horace, muttered:", "루이 18세는 홀로 남아, 반쯤 펼쳐진 호라티우스로 눈길을 돌리며 중얼거렸다."]], [["“_Justum et tenacem propositi virum_.”", "\"유스툼 에트 테나켐 프로포시티 비룸.\""]], [["M. de Blacas returned as speedily as he had departed, but in the antechamber he was forced to appeal to the king’s authority.", "드 블라카스 씨는 떠날 때와 똑같이 잰걸음으로 돌아왔으나, 대기실에서 국왕의 권위를 빌려야 했다."], ["Villefort’s dusty garb, his costume, which was not of courtly cut, excited the susceptibility of M. de Brezé, who was all astonishment at finding that this young man had the audacity to enter before the king in such attire.", "빌포르의 먼지투성이 옷차림, 궁정식 재단과는 거리가 먼 그 의복이 드 브레제 씨의 신경을 곤두서게 했던 것이다. 그는 이 젊은이가 그런 차림으로 감히 국왕 앞에 들겠다는 데 그저 경악할 뿐이었다."], ["The duke, however, overcame all difficulties with a word—his majesty’s order; and, in spite of the protestations which the master of ceremonies made for the honor of his office and principles, Villefort was introduced.", "하나 공작은 한마디 말로 모든 난관을 물리쳤으니 — 폐하의 명령이었다. 그리하여 의전관이 제 직분과 신조의 명예를 걸고 항의했음에도, 빌포르는 안으로 들여보내졌다."]], [["The king was seated in the same place where the duke had left him.", "국왕은 공작이 그를 떠났던 바로 그 자리에 앉아 있었다."], ["On opening the door, Villefort found himself facing him, and the young magistrate’s first impulse was to pause.", "문을 열자 빌포르는 정면으로 국왕과 마주하게 되었고, 젊은 판사는 첫 충동에 걸음을 멈추었다."]], [["“Come in, M. de Villefort,”", "\"들어오시오, 드 빌포르 씨.\""], ["said the king, “come in.”", "국왕이 말했다. \"들어오시오.\""]], [["Villefort bowed, and advancing a few steps, waited until the king should interrogate him.", "빌포르는 절을 하고, 몇 걸음 나아간 뒤, 국왕이 물어 오기를 기다렸다."]], [["“M. de Villefort,”", "\"드 빌포르 씨,\""], ["said Louis XVIII., “the Duc de Blacas assures me you have some interesting information to communicate.”", "루이 18세가 말했다. \"드 블라카스 공작이 그대가 전할 흥미로운 정보를 가지고 있다고 장담하더군.\""]], [["“Sire, the duke is right, and I believe your majesty will think it equally important.”", "\"폐하, 공작의 말씀이 옳습니다. 그리고 폐하께서도 그에 못지않게 중대하다 여기시리라 믿습니다.\""]], [["0137m\n“In the first place, and before everything else, sir, is the news as bad in your opinion as I am asked to believe?”", "\"무엇보다 먼저 말이오, 그대 생각에도 그 소식이 내가 들은 만큼이나 나쁜 것이오?\""]], [["“Sire, I believe it to be most urgent, but I hope, by the speed I have used, that it is not irreparable.”", "\"폐하, 제가 보기에는 더없이 긴박합니다. 하오나 제가 서둘러 온 덕에, 돌이킬 수 없는 지경은 아니기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["“Speak as fully as you please, sir,”", "\"마음껏 소상히 말해 보시오.\""], ["said the king, who began to give way to the emotion which had showed itself in Blacas’s face and affected Villefort’s voice.", "국왕이 말했다. 블라카스의 얼굴에 드러났고 빌포르의 목소리에 배어 있던 그 동요가 국왕에게도 번지기 시작했다."], ["“Speak, sir, and pray begin at the beginning; I like order in everything.”", "\"말해 보시오, 그리고 부디 처음부터 시작하시오. 나는 무슨 일이든 순서가 있는 것을 좋아하오.\""]], [["“Sire,”", "\"폐하,\""], ["said Villefort, “I will render a faithful report to your majesty, but I must entreat your forgiveness if my anxiety leads to some obscurity in my language.”", "빌포르가 말했다. \"폐하께 충실히 보고를 올리겠습니다. 다만 제 불안으로 말미암아 말이 다소 분명치 못하더라도 용서를 구하옵니다.\""], ["A glance at the king after this discreet and subtle exordium, assured Villefort of the benignity of his august auditor, and he went on:", "이 신중하고 교묘한 서두를 던진 뒤 국왕을 슬쩍 살핀 빌포르는, 자신의 존귀한 청자가 너그러운 마음임을 확인하고 말을 이었다."]], [["“Sire, I have come as rapidly to Paris as possible, to inform your majesty that I have discovered, in the exercise of my duties, not a commonplace and insignificant plot, such as is every day got up in the lower ranks of the people and in the army, but an actual conspiracy—a storm which menaces no less than your majesty’s throne.", "\"폐하, 저는 직무를 수행하던 중에, 날마다 하층민이나 군대에서 꾸며지는 흔하고 하찮은 음모가 아니라, 실로 폐하의 옥좌마저 위협하는 폭풍과도 같은 진짜 역모를 발견하였기에, 되도록 빨리 파리로 달려와 이를 폐하께 아뢰는 것입니다. 폐하, 그 찬탈자가 배 세 척을 무장시키고 있습니다."], ["Sire, the usurper is arming three ships, he meditates some project, which, however mad, is yet, perhaps, terrible.", "그는 어떤 계획을 꾸미고 있는데, 아무리 미친 짓이라 해도 어쩌면 무서운 결과를 낳을지 모릅니다."], ["At this moment he will have left Elba, to go whither I know not, but assuredly to attempt a landing either at Naples, or on the coast of Tuscany, or perhaps on the shores of France.", "지금쯤 그는 엘바섬을 떠났을 것입니다. 어디로 향했는지는 알 수 없으나, 분명 나폴리든 토스카나 해안이든, 혹은 프랑스 연안이든 어딘가에 상륙을 꾀하려는 것입니다."], ["Your majesty is well aware that the sovereign of the Island of Elba has maintained his relations with Italy and France?”", "폐하께서도 엘바섬의 주인이 이탈리아 및 프랑스와 관계를 유지해 왔음을 잘 아시지 않습니까?\""]], [["“I am, sir,”", "\"알고 있소.\""], ["said the king, much agitated; “and recently we have had information that the Bonapartist clubs have had meetings in the Rue Saint-Jacques.", "국왕이 몹시 동요하여 말했다. \"그리고 최근에는 보나파르트파 모임이 생자크 거리에서 회합을 가졌다는 정보도 들었소."], ["But proceed, I beg of you.", "허나 계속하시오, 부탁이오."], ["How did you obtain these details?”", "그런 자세한 것들을 어찌 알아냈소?\""]], [["“Sire, they are the results of an examination which I have made of a man of Marseilles, whom I have watched for some time, and arrested on the day of my departure.", "\"폐하, 그것들은 제가 한동안 감시하다가 출발하던 날 체포한 마르세유의 한 사내를 신문하여 얻은 결과입니다. 이 자는 성격이 거친 뱃사람으로, 제가 보나파르트파라고 의심하던 자인데, 몰래 엘바섬에 다녀왔습니다."], ["This person, a sailor, of turbulent character, and whom I suspected of Bonapartism, has been secretly to the Island of Elba.", "그곳에서 그자는 대원수를 만났고, 대원수는 그자에게 파리에 있는 어느 보나파르트파에게 구두로 전할 전갈을 맡겼습니다."], ["There he saw the grand-marshal, who charged him with an oral message to a Bonapartist in Paris, whose name I could not extract from him; but this mission was to prepare men’s minds for a return (it is the man who says this, sire)—a return which will soon occur.”", "그자에게서 그 사람의 이름은 끝내 캐내지 못했으나, 그 임무란 곧 다가올 복귀에 대비해 민심을 준비시키는 것이었습니다 — 이건 그자가 한 말입니다, 폐하 — 머지않아 일어날 복귀 말입니다.\""]], [["“And where is this man?”", "\"그래, 그자는 지금 어디 있소?\""]], [["“In prison, sire.”", "\"감옥에 있습니다, 폐하.\""]], [["“And the matter seems serious to you?”", "\"그래, 그대가 보기에 사태가 심각한 것 같소?\""]], [["“So serious, sire, that when the circumstance surprised me in the midst of a family festival, on the very day of my betrothal, I left my bride and friends, postponing everything, that I might hasten to lay at your majesty’s feet the fears which impressed me, and the assurance of my devotion.”", "\"심히 심각하여, 폐하, 그 일이 가족 잔치 한복판에서, 그것도 제 약혼 바로 그날 저를 덮쳤을 때, 저는 신부와 벗들을 두고 만사를 미룬 채 달려왔습니다. 저를 사로잡은 두려움과 폐하를 향한 충정을 폐하의 발치에 바치고자 함이었습니다.\""]], [["“True,”", "\"옳지,\""], ["said Louis XVIII., “was there not a marriage engagement between you and Mademoiselle de Saint-Méran?”", "루이 18세가 말했다. \"그대와 드 생메랑 양 사이에 혼약이 있지 않았소?\""]], [["“Daughter of one of your majesty’s most faithful servants.”", "\"폐하의 더없이 충직한 신하 가운데 한 분의 따님입니다.\""]], [["“Yes, yes; but let us talk of this plot, M. de Villefort.”", "\"그래, 그래. 허나 이 음모 이야기를 합시다, 드 빌포르 씨.\""]], [["“Sire, I fear it is more than a plot; I fear it is a conspiracy.”", "\"폐하, 두렵건대 이는 음모를 넘어선 것입니다. 역모일까 두렵습니다.\""]], [["“A conspiracy in these times,”", "\"이런 시국에 역모란,\""], ["said Louis XVIII., smiling, “is a thing very easy to meditate, but more difficult to conduct to an end, inasmuch as, re-established so recently on the throne of our ancestors, we have our eyes open at once upon the past, the present, and the future.", "루이 18세가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"꾸미기는 매우 쉬우나 끝까지 밀고 나가기는 더 어려운 법이오. 우리가 조상의 옥좌에 오른 지 얼마 되지 않은 만큼, 과거와 현재, 미래를 한꺼번에 똑똑히 지켜보고 있기 때문이오."], ["For the last ten months my ministers have redoubled their vigilance, in order to watch the shore of the Mediterranean.", "지난 열 달 동안 내 대신들은 지중해 연안을 감시하느라 경계를 배로 강화해 왔소."], ["If Bonaparte landed at Naples, the whole coalition would be on foot before he could even reach Piombino; if he land in Tuscany, he will be in an unfriendly territory; if he land in France, it must be with a handful of men, and the result of that is easily foretold, execrated as he is by the population.", "보나파르트가 나폴리에 상륙한다면, 그가 피옴비노에 닿기도 전에 연합군 전체가 들고일어날 게요. 토스카나에 상륙한다면 적지에 발을 들이는 셈이고, 프랑스에 상륙한다면 한 줌의 병력으로일 텐데, 그 결과는 뻔하지 않소. 백성이 그토록 그를 증오하니 말이오."], ["Take courage, sir; but at the same time rely on our royal gratitude.”", "용기를 내시오. 다만 이와 더불어 우리 왕가의 감사도 믿어도 좋소.\""]], [["“Ah, here is M. Dandré!” cried de Blacas.", "\"아, 마침 당드레 씨가 왔습니다!\""], ["At this instant the minister of police appeared at the door, pale, trembling, and as if ready to faint.", "드 블라카스가 외쳤다. 바로 그 순간 경찰청장이 문가에 나타났는데, 얼굴이 창백하고 떨고 있어 금방이라도 쓰러질 듯했다."], ["Villefort was about to retire, but M. de Blacas, taking his hand, restrained him.", "빌포르가 물러나려 하자, 드 블라카스 씨가 그의 손을 붙잡아 만류했다."]], [["# Chapter 11. The Corsican Ogre", "# Chapter 11. The Corsican Ogre"]], [["At the sight of this agitation Louis XVIII. pushed from him violently the table at which he was sitting.", "루이 18세는 그 동요하는 모습을 보자 앉아 있던 탁자를 거칠게 밀쳐냈다."]], [["“What ails you, baron?”", "\"무슨 일인가, 남작?\""], ["he exclaimed.", "그가 소리쳤다."], ["“You appear quite aghast.", "\"몹시 질겁한 얼굴이군."], ["Has your uneasiness anything to do with what M. de Blacas has told me, and M. de Villefort has just confirmed?” M.", "그대의 불안이 블라카스 씨가 내게 말하고 빌포르 씨가 방금 확인해 준 그 일과 관계가 있는가?\""], ["de Blacas moved suddenly towards the baron, but the fright of the courtier pleaded for the forbearance of the statesman; and besides, as matters were, it was much more to his advantage that the prefect of police should triumph over him than that he should humiliate the prefect.", "블라카스 씨가 불쑥 남작 쪽으로 다가섰으나, 신하의 겁먹은 모습이 정치가의 관용을 청하고 있었다. 게다가 사정이 이러하니, 그로서는 경찰청장을 모욕하기보다 경찰청장이 자기보다 우위에 서게 두는 편이 훨씬 이로웠다."]], [["“Sire,——” stammered the baron.", "\"폐하…….\" 남작이 더듬거렸다."]], [["“Well, what is it?” asked Louis XVIII.", "\"그래, 무슨 일이냐고?\""], ["The minister of police, giving way to an impulse of despair, was about to throw himself at the feet of Louis XVIII., who retreated a step and frowned.", "루이 18세가 물었다. 경찰청장은 절망에 휩쓸린 나머지 루이 18세의 발치에 몸을 던지려 했고, 왕은 한 걸음 물러서며 이맛살을 찌푸렸다."]], [["“Will you speak?” he said.", "\"말을 하겠는가?\" 그가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, sire, what a dreadful misfortune!", "\"오, 폐하, 이 무슨 끔찍한 불행입니까!"], ["I am, indeed, to be pitied.", "정말이지 저는 가엾은 처지입니다."], ["I can never forgive myself!”", "저 자신을 결코 용서할 수 없을 것입니다!\""]], [["“Monsieur,”", "\"경,\""], ["said Louis XVIII., “I command you to speak.”", "루이 18세가 말했다. \"내 명하노니, 말하라.\""]], [["“Well, sire, the usurper left Elba on the 26th February, and landed on the 1st of March.”", "\"그러시다면 폐하, 그 찬탈자가 2월 26일에 엘바섬을 떠나 3월 1일에 상륙했습니다.\""]], [["“And where?", "\"어디에?"], ["In Italy?” asked the king eagerly.", "이탈리아에 말인가?\" 왕이 다급히 물었다."]], [["“In France, sire,—at a small port, near Antibes, in the Gulf of Juan.”", "\"프랑스입니다, 폐하. 앙티브 부근, 쥐앙만의 작은 항구입니다.\""]], [["“The usurper landed in France, near Antibes, in the Gulf of Juan, two hundred and fifty leagues from Paris, on the 1st of March, and you only acquired this information today, the 3rd of March!", "\"찬탈자가 프랑스에, 앙티브 부근 쥐앙만에, 파리에서 이백오십 리외나 떨어진 곳에 3월 1일에 상륙했는데, 그대는 오늘에야, 3월 3일에야 그 사실을 알았단 말인가!"], ["Well, sir, what you tell me is impossible.", "이보게, 그대가 하는 말은 있을 수 없는 일이야."], ["You must have received a false report, or you have gone mad.”", "잘못된 보고를 받았거나, 아니면 정신이 나간 게지.\""]], [["“Alas, sire, it is but too true!” Louis made a gesture of indescribable anger and alarm, and then drew himself up as if this sudden blow had struck him at the same moment in heart and countenance.", "\"아아, 폐하, 너무도 분명한 사실입니다!\" 루이는 형언할 수 없는 분노와 경악의 몸짓을 보였고, 이내 그 갑작스러운 일격이 가슴과 얼굴을 한꺼번에 강타하기라도 한 듯 몸을 곧추세웠다."]], [["“In France!”", "\"프랑스에!\""], ["he cried, “the usurper in France!", "그가 외쳤다. \"그 찬탈자가 프랑스에!"], ["Then they did not watch over this man.", "그렇다면 아무도 그자를 감시하지 않았던 게로군."], ["Who knows?", "누가 알겠나?"], ["they were, perhaps, in league with him.”", "어쩌면 그자와 한통속이었는지도.\""]], [["“Oh, sire,” exclaimed the Duc de Blacas, “M.", "\"오, 폐하,\" 블라카스 공작이 외쳤다."], ["Dandré is not a man to be accused of treason!", "\"당드레 씨는 반역으로 의심받을 사람이 아닙니다!"], ["Sire, we have all been blind, and the minister of police has shared the general blindness, that is all.”", "폐하, 저희 모두가 눈이 멀었던 것입니다. 경찰청장도 그 보편적인 맹목을 함께 나누었을 뿐, 그것이 전부입니다.\""]], [["“But——”", "\"하지만…….\""], ["said Villefort, and then suddenly checking himself, he was silent; then he continued, “Your pardon, sire,”", "빌포르가 입을 열었다가 문득 자신을 억누르며 침묵했다. 그러더니 다시 말을 이었다. \"용서하십시오, 폐하.\""], ["he said, bowing, “my zeal carried me away.", "그가 고개를 숙이며 말했다. \"저의 충정이 그만 저를 앞서게 했습니다."], ["Will your majesty deign to excuse me?”", "폐하께서 너그러이 용서해 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“Speak, sir, speak boldly,”", "\"말하라, 경, 거리낌 없이 말하라.\""], ["replied Louis.", "루이가 대답했다."], ["“You alone forewarned us of the evil; now try and aid us with the remedy.”", "\"이 환란을 미리 알린 사람은 그대뿐이니, 이제 그 해법으로 우리를 도와 보라.\""]], [["“Sire,”", "\"폐하,\""], ["said Villefort, “the usurper is detested in the south; and it seems to me that if he ventured into the south, it would be easy to raise Languedoc and Provence against him.”", "빌포르가 말했다. \"그 찬탈자는 남부에서 미움을 받고 있습니다. 제 생각으로는, 그자가 무모하게 남부로 들어선다면 랑그도크와 프로방스를 들고일어나게 하기는 쉬울 것입니다.\""]], [["“Yes, assuredly,”", "\"그래, 그건 확실하지.\""], ["replied the minister; “but he is advancing by Gap and Sisteron.”", "청장이 대답했다. \"그러나 그자는 가프와 시스트롱을 거쳐 진군하고 있습니다.\""]], [["“Advancing—he is advancing!”", "\"진군이라니, 진군한다고!\""], ["said Louis XVIII.", "루이 18세가 말했다."], ["“Is he then advancing on Paris?” The minister of police maintained a silence which was equivalent to a complete avowal.", "\"그렇다면 그자가 파리로 진군한단 말인가?\" 경찰청장은 침묵을 지켰고, 그것은 완전한 시인이나 다름없었다."]], [["“And Dauphiné, sir?” inquired the king, of Villefort.", "\"그럼 도피네는 어떤가, 경?\" 왕이 빌포르에게 물었다."], ["“Do you think it possible to rouse that as well as Provence?”", "\"프로방스처럼 그곳도 들고일어나게 할 수 있을 것 같은가?\""]], [["“Sire, I am sorry to tell your majesty a cruel fact; but the feeling in Dauphiné is quite the reverse of that in Provence or Languedoc.", "\"폐하, 폐하께 가혹한 사실을 아뢰어야 함을 송구하게 여깁니다. 그러나 도피네의 민심은 프로방스나 랑그도크와는 정반대입니다."], ["The mountaineers are Bonapartists, sire.”", "그 산악 지방 사람들은 보나파르트파입니다, 폐하.\""]], [["“Then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["murmured Louis, “he was well informed.", "루이가 중얼거렸다. \"그자는 정보를 잘 갖추고 있었던 게로군."], ["And how many men had he with him?”", "그래, 그자가 거느린 병력은 얼마나 되는가?\""]], [["“I do not know, sire,” answered the minister of police.", "\"모르겠습니다, 폐하.\" 경찰청장이 대답했다."]], [["“What, you do not know!", "\"뭐라, 모른다고!"], ["Have you neglected to obtain information on that point?", "그 점에 대해 정보 얻기를 게을리한 것인가?"], ["Of course it is of no consequence,”", "하기야 별로 중요한 일도 아니지.\""], ["he added, with a withering smile.", "그가 매서운 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다."]], [["“Sire, it was impossible to learn; the despatch simply stated the fact of the landing and the route taken by the usurper.”", "\"폐하, 알아낼 길이 없었습니다. 급보에는 그저 상륙 사실과 찬탈자가 택한 경로만 적혀 있었습니다.\""]], [["“And how did this despatch reach you?” inquired the king.", "\"그래, 그 급보는 어떻게 그대에게 닿았는가?\" 왕이 물었다."], ["The minister bowed his head, and while a deep color overspread his cheeks, he stammered out:", "청장은 고개를 떨구었고, 뺨에 짙은 홍조가 번지는 가운데 더듬거리며 말했다."]], [["“By the telegraph, sire.”", "\"전신으로 받았습니다, 폐하.\""], ["Louis XVIII. advanced a step, and folded his arms over his chest as Napoleon would have done.", "루이 18세는 한 걸음 나아가, 나폴레옹이 그러했을 법한 자세로 가슴 위에 팔짱을 꼈다."]], [["0141m\n“So then,”", "\"그러니까,\""], ["he exclaimed, turning pale with anger, “seven conjoined and allied armies overthrew that man.", "그가 분노로 창백해지며 외쳤다. \"일곱 나라의 연합군이 힘을 합쳐 그자를 무너뜨렸다."], ["A miracle of heaven replaced me on the throne of my fathers after five-and-twenty years of exile.", "하늘의 기적이 이십오 년의 망명 끝에 나를 선조의 옥좌에 다시 앉혀 주었다."], ["I have, during those five-and-twenty years, spared no pains to understand the people of France and the interests which were confided to me; and now, when I see the fruition of my wishes almost within reach, the power I hold in my hands bursts and shatters me to atoms!”", "그 이십오 년 동안, 나는 프랑스 백성과 내게 맡겨진 이익을 헤아리려 온갖 수고를 아끼지 않았다. 그런데 이제 내 소망의 결실이 거의 손에 잡히려는 순간, 내 손안에 쥔 권력이 터져 나를 산산조각으로 부수어 버리는구나!\""]], [["“Sire, it is fatality!”", "\"폐하, 이는 운명입니다!\""], ["murmured the minister, feeling that the pressure of circumstances, however light a thing to destiny, was too much for any human strength to endure.", "청장이 중얼거렸다. 그는 상황의 무게가, 운명에게는 한낱 가벼운 것일지언정, 인간의 힘으로 견디기에는 너무 무겁다는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“What our enemies say of us is then true.", "\"우리의 적들이 우리를 두고 하는 말이 결국 옳았던 게로군."], ["We have learnt nothing, forgotten nothing!", "우리는 아무것도 배우지 못했고, 아무것도 잊지 못했다! 만일 내가 그자처럼 배신을 당했다면 차라리 스스로를 위로하겠다."], ["If I were betrayed as he was, I would console myself; but to be in the midst of persons elevated by myself to places of honor, who ought to watch over me more carefully than over themselves,—for my fortune is theirs—before me they were nothing—after me they will be nothing, and perish miserably from incapacity—ineptitude!", "그러나 내가 손수 영예로운 자리에 올려 준 자들, 제 몸보다 나를 더 충실히 살펴야 마땅한 자들 한가운데에서 이런 일을 당하다니. 나의 운명이 곧 그들의 운명이거늘. 내가 있기 전에 그들은 아무것도 아니었고, 내가 사라지면 다시 아무것도 아닐 것이며, 무능과 무력으로 비참하게 스러질 것이다!"], ["Oh, yes, sir, you are right—it is fatality!”", "오, 그래, 경, 그대 말이 옳다. 이는 운명이야!\""]], [["The minister quailed before this outburst of sarcasm. M.", "청장은 이 빈정거림의 폭발 앞에 몸을 움츠렸다."], ["de Blacas wiped the moisture from his brow.", "블라카스 씨는 이마에 맺힌 땀을 닦았다."], ["Villefort smiled within himself, for he felt his increased importance.", "빌포르는 자신의 비중이 커진 것을 느끼며 속으로 미소 지었다."]], [["“To fall,”", "\"몰락하다니,\""], ["continued King Louis, who at the first glance had sounded the abyss on which the monarchy hung suspended,—“to fall, and learn of that fall by telegraph!", "첫눈에 왕정이 매달려 있는 심연을 가늠한 루이 왕이 말을 이었다. \"몰락하고, 게다가 그 몰락을 전신으로 알게 되다니!"], ["Oh, I would rather mount the scaffold of my brother, Louis XVI., than thus descend the staircase at the Tuileries driven away by ridicule.", "오, 차라리 내 아우 루이 16세의 단두대에 오를지언정, 비웃음에 쫓겨 튀일리궁의 계단을 이렇게 내려가지는 않으리라."], ["Ridicule, sir—why, you know not its power in France, and yet you ought to know it!”", "비웃음이라니, 경, 그대는 프랑스에서 그것이 지닌 위력을 모르는 게야. 그러면서도 마땅히 알아야 하거늘!\""]], [["“Sire, sire,”", "\"폐하, 폐하,\""], ["murmured the minister, “for pity’s——”", "청장이 중얼거렸다. \"부디 자비를…….\""]], [["“Approach, M. de Villefort,”", "\"이리 오시오, 빌포르 씨.\""], ["resumed the king, addressing the young man, who, motionless and breathless, was listening to a conversation on which depended the destiny of a kingdom.", "왕이 그 젊은이에게 말을 건네며 다시 입을 열었다. 빌포르는 한 왕국의 운명이 걸린 그 대화에 꼼짝도 하지 않고 숨죽인 채 귀를 기울이고 있었다."], ["“Approach, and tell monsieur that it is possible to know beforehand all that he has not known.”", "\"이리 와서, 그가 알지 못한 모든 것을 미리 알아내는 일이 가능하다고 청장에게 일러 주시오.\""]], [["“Sire, it was really impossible to learn secrets which that man concealed from all the world.”", "\"폐하, 그자가 온 세상으로부터 숨긴 비밀을 알아내기란 정말로 불가능한 일이었습니다.\""]], [["“Really impossible!", "\"정말로 불가능이라!"], ["Yes—that is a great word, sir.", "그래, 그것 참 대단한 말이로군, 경."], ["Unfortunately, there are great words, as there are great men; I have measured them.", "불행히도 대단한 사람이 있듯 대단한 말도 있는 법, 나는 그것들을 재어 보았다."], ["Really impossible for a minister who has an office, agents, spies, and fifteen hundred thousand francs for secret service money, to know what is going on at sixty leagues from the coast of France!", "청사와 요원과 첩자를 거느리고 비밀 공작 자금으로 백오십만 프랑을 쥔 청장이, 프랑스 해안에서 육십 리외 떨어진 곳에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 알아내기가 정말로 불가능하다니!"], ["Well, then, see, here is a gentleman who had none of these resources at his disposal—a gentleman, only a simple magistrate, who learned more than you with all your police, and who would have saved my crown, if, like you, he had the power of directing a telegraph.”", "그렇다면 자, 보라. 여기 그런 수단을 하나도 갖추지 못한 한 신사가 있다. 일개 평범한 판사에 지나지 않는 신사가, 온갖 경찰을 거느린 그대보다 더 많이 알아냈고, 만일 그대처럼 전신을 부릴 권한이 있었다면 내 왕관을 지켜냈을 것이다.\""], ["The look of the minister of police was turned with concentrated spite on Villefort, who bent his head in modest triumph.", "경찰청장의 시선이 응축된 원한을 담아 빌포르에게 향했고, 빌포르는 겸손한 승리감에 고개를 숙였다."]], [["“I do not mean that for you, Blacas,”", "\"블라카스, 그대를 두고 하는 말이 아니오.\""], ["continued Louis XVIII.; “for if you have discovered nothing, at least you have had the good sense to persevere in your suspicions.", "루이 18세가 말을 이었다. \"그대는 아무것도 알아내지 못했을지언정, 적어도 자신의 의심을 끝까지 밀고 나간 분별은 있었으니까."], ["Any other than yourself would have considered the disclosure of M. de Villefort insignificant, or else dictated by venal ambition.”", "그대가 아닌 다른 누구였다면 빌포르 씨의 폭로를 하찮게 여기거나, 아니면 매수당한 야심에서 나온 말로 치부했을 것이오.\""], ["These words were an allusion to the sentiments which the minister of police had uttered with so much confidence an hour before.", "이 말은 한 시간 전에 경찰청장이 그토록 자신만만하게 늘어놓았던 견해를 빗댄 것이었다."]], [["Villefort understood the king’s intent.", "빌포르는 왕의 의도를 알아차렸다."], ["Any other person would, perhaps, have been overcome by such an intoxicating draught of praise; but he feared to make for himself a mortal enemy of the police minister, although he saw that Dandré was irrevocably lost.", "다른 사람이었다면 이처럼 사람을 도취시키는 찬사의 술잔에 그만 정신을 잃었을지도 모른다. 그러나 그는 당드레가 돌이킬 수 없이 끝장났음을 보면서도, 경찰청장을 자신의 철천지원수로 만드는 일이 두려웠다."], ["In fact, the minister, who, in the plenitude of his power, had been unable to unearth Napoleon’s secret, might in despair at his own downfall interrogate Dantès and so lay bare the motives of Villefort’s plot.", "사실 그 청장은, 권세가 절정에 있을 때에도 나폴레옹의 비밀을 캐내지 못한 자였으니, 제 몰락에 절망한 나머지 당테스를 심문하여 빌포르가 꾸민 음모의 동기를 낱낱이 드러낼 수도 있었다."], ["Realizing this, Villefort came to the rescue of the crest-fallen minister, instead of aiding to crush him.", "이를 깨달은 빌포르는 의기소침해진 청장을 짓밟는 데 가세하는 대신 그를 구해 주기로 했다."]], [["“Sire,”", "\"폐하,\""], ["said Villefort, “the suddenness of this event must prove to your majesty that the issue is in the hands of Providence; what your majesty is pleased to attribute to me as profound perspicacity is simply owing to chance, and I have profited by that chance, like a good and devoted servant—that’s all.", "빌포르가 말했다. \"이 사건의 갑작스러움이야말로, 그 결말이 섭리의 손에 달려 있음을 폐하께 입증하는 것입니다. 폐하께서 황송하게도 깊은 통찰력으로 돌려 주신 것은 그저 우연에서 비롯된 일이며, 저는 충성스럽고 헌신적인 종복으로서 그 우연을 활용했을 뿐, 그것이 전부입니다."], ["Do not attribute to me more than I deserve, sire, that your majesty may never have occasion to recall the first opinion you have been pleased to form of me.”", "폐하, 분에 넘치는 공을 제게 돌리지 마십시오. 그래야 폐하께서 처음에 황송하게도 저에 대해 품으신 견해를 거두실 일이 없을 것입니다.\""], ["The minister of police thanked the young man by an eloquent look, and Villefort understood that he had succeeded in his design; that is to say, that without forfeiting the gratitude of the king, he had made a friend of one on whom, in case of necessity, he might rely.", "경찰청장은 의미심장한 눈길로 그 젊은이에게 고마움을 표했고, 빌포르는 자신의 계획이 성공했음을 알아차렸다. 다시 말해, 왕의 환심을 잃지 않으면서도, 필요할 때면 의지할 수 있는 한 사람을 벗으로 삼은 것이다."]], [["“’Tis well,”", "\"좋다.\""], ["resumed the king. “And now, gentlemen,”", "왕이 다시 말했다."], ["he continued, turning towards M.", "\"자, 경들,\""], ["de Blacas and the minister of police, “I have no further occasion for you, and you may retire; what now remains to do is in the department of the minister of war.”", "그가 블라카스 씨와 경찰청장 쪽으로 돌아서며 말을 이었다. \"이제 그대들에게 더 볼일이 없으니 물러가도 좋소. 이제 남은 일은 육군대신의 소관이오.\""]], [["“Fortunately, sire,” said M.", "\"다행히도, 폐하,\" 블라카스 씨가 말했다."], ["de Blacas, “we can rely on the army; your majesty knows how every report confirms their loyalty and attachment.”", "\"저희는 군대를 믿을 수 있습니다. 모든 보고가 군대의 충성과 결속을 확인해 준다는 것을 폐하께서도 아십니다.\""]], [["“Do not mention reports, duke, to me, for I know now what confidence to place in them.", "\"보고 이야기는 내게 꺼내지 마시오, 공작. 이제 그것을 어디까지 믿어야 할지 잘 알았으니."], ["Yet, speaking of reports, baron, what have you learned with regard to the affair in the Rue Saint-Jacques?”", "그래도 보고 이야기가 나온 김에, 남작, 생자크 거리 사건에 관해서는 무엇을 알아냈는가?\""]], [["“The affair in the Rue Saint-Jacques!”", "\"생자크 거리 사건이라니요!\" 빌포르가 외쳤다. 그는 탄성을 억누르지 못했다."], ["exclaimed Villefort, unable to repress an exclamation.", "그러더니 문득 말을 멈추고 덧붙였다. \"용서하십시오, 폐하. 폐하를 향한 저의 충정이 그만 저를 잊게 만들었습니다."], ["Then, suddenly pausing, he added, “Your pardon, sire, but my devotion to your majesty has made me forget, not the respect I have, for that is too deeply engraved in my heart, but the rules of etiquette.”", "제가 품은 존경심을 잊은 것이 아닙니다. 그것은 제 가슴에 너무도 깊이 새겨져 있으니까요. 다만 예법을 잊었던 것입니다.\""]], [["“Go on, go on, sir,”", "\"계속하시오, 계속하시오, 경.\""], ["replied the king; “you have today earned the right to make inquiries here.”", "왕이 대답했다. \"그대는 오늘 이곳에서 물을 자격을 얻었으니까.\""]], [["“Sire,”", "\"폐하,\""], ["interposed the minister of police, “I came a moment ago to give your majesty fresh information which I had obtained on this head, when your majesty’s attention was attracted by the terrible event that has occurred in the gulf, and now these facts will cease to interest your majesty.”", "경찰청장이 끼어들었다. \"조금 전 이 건에 관해 새로 입수한 정보를 폐하께 아뢰러 들어왔습니다만, 그만 만에서 벌어진 그 끔찍한 사건에 폐하의 관심이 쏠리고 말았습니다. 이제 이 일들은 폐하의 흥미를 끌지 못할 듯합니다.\""]], [["“On the contrary, sir,—on the contrary,”", "\"천만에, 경, 천만에.\""], ["said Louis XVIII., “this affair seems to me to have a decided connection with that which occupies our attention, and the death of General Quesnel will, perhaps, put us on the direct track of a great internal conspiracy.”", "루이 18세가 말했다. \"이 사건은 내게는 지금 우리의 주의를 사로잡고 있는 일과 분명한 연관이 있어 보이오. 케넬 장군의 죽음이 어쩌면 우리를 중대한 내부 음모의 직접적인 단서로 이끌어 줄지도 모르지.\""], ["At the name of General Quesnel, Villefort trembled.", "케넬 장군이라는 이름에 빌포르는 몸을 떨었다."]], [["“Everything points to the conclusion, sire,”", "\"모든 정황이 한 결론을 가리키고 있습니다, 폐하.\""], ["said the minister of police, “that death was not the result of suicide, as we first believed, but of assassination.", "경찰청장이 말했다. \"그 죽음은 처음에 저희가 믿었던 것처럼 자살이 아니라 암살이었습니다."], ["General Quesnel, it appears, had just left a Bonapartist club when he disappeared.", "케넬 장군은 자취를 감추기 직전에 어느 보나파르트파 모임을 막 나섰던 것으로 보입니다."], ["An unknown person had been with him that morning, and made an appointment with him in the Rue Saint-Jacques; unfortunately, the general’s valet, who was dressing his hair at the moment when the stranger entered, heard the street mentioned, but did not catch the number.”", "그날 아침 정체 모를 인물이 그를 찾아와 생자크 거리에서 만나기로 약속을 잡았습니다. 안타깝게도, 그 낯선 자가 들어왔을 때 마침 장군의 머리를 매만지던 시종이 거리 이름은 들었으나 번지는 듣지 못했습니다.\""], ["As the police minister related this to the king, Villefort, who looked as if his very life hung on the speaker’s lips, turned alternately red and pale.", "경찰청장이 이 이야기를 왕에게 들려주는 동안, 자기 목숨이 그 입술에 매달리기라도 한 듯한 표정의 빌포르는 붉어졌다 창백해지기를 번갈아 했다."], ["The king looked towards him.", "왕이 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“Do you not think with me, M. de Villefort, that General Quesnel, whom they believed attached to the usurper, but who was really entirely devoted to me, has perished the victim of a Bonapartist ambush?”", "\"빌포르 씨, 그대도 나와 같은 생각이 아니오? 사람들은 케넬 장군이 찬탈자에게 속한 줄 알았으나 실은 온전히 내게 충성하던 사람이었는데, 그가 보나파르트파의 매복에 희생되어 죽은 것이라고 말이오?\""]], [["“It is probable, sire,”", "\"그럴 법한 일입니다, 폐하.\""], ["replied Villefort.", "빌포르가 대답했다."], ["“But is this all that is known?”", "\"그런데 알려진 것이 이게 전부입니까?\""]], [["“They are on the track of the man who appointed the meeting with him.”", "\"그와 만나기로 약속을 잡은 자의 자취를 쫓고 있소.\""]], [["“On his track?”", "\"그자의 자취를요?\""], ["said Villefort.", "빌포르가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, the servant has given his description.", "\"그렇소. 시종이 인상착의를 일러 주었지."], ["He is a man of from fifty to fifty-two years of age, dark, with black eyes covered with shaggy eyebrows, and a thick moustache.", "쉰에서 쉰둘쯤 된 사내로, 거무스름한 피부에 검은 눈을 짙은 눈썹이 덮고 있고, 콧수염이 텁수룩하다고 하오."], ["He was dressed in a blue frock-coat, buttoned up to the chin, and wore at his button-hole the rosette of an officer of the Legion of Honor.", "턱까지 단추를 채운 푸른 프록코트를 입었고, 단춧구멍에는 레지옹 도뇌르 장교 약장을 달고 있었다 하오."], ["Yesterday a person exactly corresponding with this description was followed, but he was lost sight of at the corner of the Rue de la Jussienne and the Rue Coq-Héron.”", "어제 이 인상착의에 꼭 들어맞는 자를 미행했으나, 쥐시엔 거리와 코크에롱 거리 모퉁이에서 놓치고 말았소.\""], ["Villefort leaned on the back of an armchair, for as the minister of police went on speaking he felt his legs bend under him; but when he learned that the unknown had escaped the vigilance of the agent who followed him, he breathed again.", "경찰청장이 말을 이어 가는 동안 빌포르는 안락의자 등받이에 몸을 기댔다. 다리가 후들거리는 것이 느껴졌기 때문이다. 그러나 그 낯선 자가 미행하던 요원의 감시를 따돌렸다는 말을 듣자 다시 숨을 돌렸다."]], [["“Continue to seek for this man, sir,”", "\"그 사내를 계속 찾으시오, 경.\""], ["said the king to the minister of police; “for if, as I am all but convinced, General Quesnel, who would have been so useful to us at this moment, has been murdered, his assassins, Bonapartists or not, shall be cruelly punished.”", "왕이 경찰청장에게 말했다. \"이 순간 우리에게 그토록 요긴했을 케넬 장군이, 내가 거의 확신하는 대로 살해당한 것이라면, 그 암살자들은 보나파르트파든 아니든 가차 없이 벌하겠소.\""], ["It required all Villefort’s coolness not to betray the terror with which this declaration of the king inspired him.", "이 왕의 선언이 불러일으킨 공포를 드러내지 않으려면 빌포르의 온갖 침착함이 다 필요했다."]], [["“How strange,”", "\"참으로 이상하지,\""], ["continued the king, with some asperity; “the police think that they have disposed of the whole matter when they say, ‘A murder has been committed,’ and especially so when they can add, ‘And we are on the track of the guilty persons.’”", "왕이 다소 신랄하게 말을 이었다. \"경찰이란 '살인이 저질러졌습니다'라고 말하기만 하면 모든 일을 처리한 것으로 여기고, 더욱이 '그리고 저희는 범인의 자취를 쫓고 있습니다'라고 덧붙일 수 있을 때면 그러하단 말이오.\""]], [["“Sire, your majesty will, I trust, be amply satisfied on this point at least.”", "\"폐하, 적어도 이 점에 관해서는 폐하께서 충분히 만족하시리라 믿습니다.\""]], [["“We shall see.", "\"두고 봅시다."], ["I will no longer detain you, M. de Villefort, for you must be fatigued after so long a journey; go and rest.", "더는 그대를 붙잡지 않겠소, 빌포르 씨. 그토록 먼 길을 왔으니 지쳤을 것이오."], ["Of course you stopped at your father’s?”", "가서 쉬시오. 물론 부친 댁에 들렀겠지?\""], ["A feeling of faintness came over Villefort. 0145m", "빌포르는 아찔한 기운에 휩싸였다."]], [["“No, sire,”", "\"아닙니다, 폐하.\""], ["he replied, “I alighted at the Hotel de Madrid, in the Rue de Tournon.”", "그가 대답했다. \"투르농 거리의 마드리드 호텔에 묵었습니다.\""]], [["“But you have seen him?”", "\"하지만 부친은 만났겠지?\""]], [["“Sire, I went straight to the Duc de Blacas.”", "\"폐하, 저는 곧장 블라카스 공작께로 갔습니다.\""]], [["“But you will see him, then?”", "\"그럼 만나기는 할 테지?\""]], [["“I think not, sire.”", "\"그러지 않을 듯합니다, 폐하.\""]], [["“Ah, I forgot,”", "\"아, 내가 깜빡했군.\""], ["said Louis, smiling in a manner which proved that all these questions were not made without a motive; “I forgot you and M.", "루이가 미소를 지으며 말했다. 그 미소는 이 모든 물음이 까닭 없이 던져진 것이 아님을 드러내고 있었다."], ["Noirtier are not on the best terms possible, and that is another sacrifice made to the royal cause, and for which you should be recompensed.”", "\"그대와 누아르티에 씨가 그다지 좋은 사이가 아니라는 걸 잊고 있었소. 그 또한 왕가의 대의를 위해 그대가 치른 또 하나의 희생이니, 마땅히 보답받아야 할 일이지.\""]], [["“Sire, the kindness your majesty deigns to evince towards me is a recompense which so far surpasses my utmost ambition that I have nothing more to ask for.”", "\"폐하, 폐하께서 황송하게도 제게 베풀어 주시는 자애야말로 저의 가장 큰 야심마저 훌쩍 뛰어넘는 보답이오니, 더 청할 것이 없습니다.\""]], [["“Never mind, sir, we will not forget you; make your mind easy.", "\"걱정 마시오, 경, 그대를 잊지 않을 것이오."], ["In the meanwhile”", "마음을 놓으시오."], ["(the king here detached the cross of the Legion of Honor which he usually wore over his blue coat, near the cross of St. Louis, above the order of Notre-Dame-du-Mont-Carmel and St. Lazare, and gave it to Villefort)—“in the meanwhile take this cross.”", "그러는 동안에\"(이때 왕은 평소 푸른 코트 위, 생루이 십자훈장 곁, 노트르담뒤몽카르멜과 생라자르 훈장 위에 달고 다니던 레지옹 도뇌르 십자훈장을 떼어 빌포르에게 건넸다) \"그러는 동안 이 십자훈장을 받아 두시오.\""]], [["“Sire,”", "\"폐하,\""], ["said Villefort, “your majesty mistakes; this is an officer’s cross.”", "빌포르가 말했다. \"폐하께서 잘못 아셨습니다. 이것은 장교용 십자훈장입니다.\""]], [["“_Ma foi!_”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["said Louis XVIII., “take it, such as it is, for I have not the time to procure you another.", "루이 18세가 말했다. \"있는 그대로 받아 두시오. 다른 것을 마련해 줄 시간이 없으니."], ["Blacas, let it be your care to see that the brevet is made out and sent to M.", "블라카스, 임명장을 작성해 빌포르 씨에게 보내는 일을 그대가 맡아 주시오.\""], ["de Villefort.” Villefort’s eyes were filled with tears of joy and pride; he took the cross and kissed it.", "빌포르의 두 눈에 기쁨과 긍지의 눈물이 그렁그렁 맺혔다. 그는 십자훈장을 받아 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“And now,”", "\"그럼 이제,\""], ["he said, “may I inquire what are the orders with which your majesty deigns to honor me?”", "그가 말했다. \"폐하께서 황송하게도 제게 내리시는 분부가 무엇인지 여쭈어도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["“Take what rest you require, and remember that if you are not able to serve me here in Paris, you may be of the greatest service to me at Marseilles.”", "\"필요한 만큼 쉬도록 하시오. 그리고 여기 파리에서 나를 섬길 수 없거든, 마르세유에서 내게 더없이 큰 도움이 되어 줄 수 있다는 것을 잊지 마시오.\""]], [["“Sire,”", "\"폐하,\""], ["replied Villefort, bowing, “in an hour I shall have quitted Paris.”", "빌포르가 고개를 숙이며 대답했다. \"한 시간 안에 파리를 떠나겠습니다.\""]], [["“Go, sir,”", "\"가시오, 경.\""], ["said the king; “and should I forget you (kings’ memories are short), do not be afraid to bring yourself to my recollection.", "왕이 말했다. \"그리고 혹 내가 그대를 잊거든(왕의 기억이란 짧은 법이오) 주저 말고 그대 자신을 내 기억에 떠올리게 하시오."], ["Baron, send for the minister of war. Blacas, remain.”", "남작, 육군대신을 불러오라. 블라카스, 그대는 남으시오.\""]], [["“Ah, sir,”", "\"아, 경,\""], ["said the minister of police to Villefort, as they left the Tuileries, “you entered by luck’s door—your fortune is made.”", "경찰청장이 튀일리궁을 나서며 빌포르에게 말했다. \"그대는 행운의 문으로 들어섰소. 그대의 출세는 따 놓은 당상이오.\""]], [["“Will it be long first?”", "\"머지않아 그리되겠지요?\""], ["muttered Villefort, saluting the minister, whose career was ended, and looking about him for a hackney-coach.", "빌포르가 중얼거렸다. 그는 이미 경력이 끝장난 청장에게 인사를 건네고는 삯마차를 찾아 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["One passed at the moment, which he hailed; he gave his address to the driver, and springing in, threw himself on the seat, and gave loose to dreams of ambition.", "마침 한 대가 지나가자 그것을 불러 세웠다. 마부에게 주소를 일러 주고는 안으로 뛰어들어 좌석에 몸을 던졌고, 야심에 찬 몽상에 한껏 빠져들었다."]], [["Ten minutes afterwards Villefort reached his hotel, ordered horses to be ready in two hours, and asked to have his breakfast brought to him.", "십 분쯤 뒤 빌포르는 호텔에 다다라, 두 시간 안에 말을 준비해 두라 이르고 아침 식사를 가져오게 했다."], ["He was about to begin his repast when the sound of the bell rang sharp and loud.", "막 식사를 시작하려는데 초인종 소리가 날카롭고 크게 울렸다."], ["The valet opened the door, and Villefort heard someone speak his name. 0147m", "시종이 문을 열었고, 빌포르는 누군가 자기 이름을 말하는 것을 들었다."]], [["“Who could know that I was here already?”", "\"내가 벌써 여기 와 있다는 걸 누가 안단 말인가?\""], ["said the young man.", "젊은이가 말했다."], ["The valet entered.", "시종이 들어왔다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래,\" 빌포르가 말했다."], ["said Villefort, “what is it?—Who rang?—Who asked for me?”", "\"무슨 일인가? 누가 종을 울렸지? 누가 나를 찾던가?\""]], [["“A stranger who will not send in his name.”", "\"이름을 밝히지 않으려는 낯선 분입니다.\""]], [["“A stranger who will not send in his name!", "\"이름을 밝히지 않으려는 낯선 자라니!"], ["What can he want with me?”", "내게 무슨 볼일이라던가?\""]], [["“He wishes to speak to you.”", "\"나리와 이야기를 나누고 싶다 하십니다.\""]], [["“To me?”", "\"나와?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Did he mention my name?”", "\"내 이름을 대던가?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“What sort of person is he?”", "\"어떤 사람인가?\""]], [["“Why, sir, a man of about fifty.”", "\"글쎄요, 나리, 쉰쯤 된 분입니다.\""]], [["“Short or tall?”", "\"키는 작던가, 크던가?\""]], [["“About your own height, sir.”", "\"나리와 비슷한 키입니다.\""]], [["“Dark or fair?”", "\"거무스름하던가, 희던가?\""]], [["“Dark,—very dark; with black eyes, black hair, black eyebrows.”", "\"거무스름합니다. 아주 거무스름하고요. 검은 눈에, 검은 머리, 검은 눈썹입니다.\""]], [["“And how dressed?”", "\"옷차림은 어떻던가?\""], ["asked Villefort quickly.", "빌포르가 다급히 물었다."]], [["“In a blue frock-coat, buttoned up close, decorated with the Legion of Honor.”", "\"단추를 단단히 채운 푸른 프록코트에 레지옹 도뇌르 훈장을 달고 계십니다.\""]], [["“It is he!”", "\"그자로구나!\""], ["said Villefort, turning pale.", "빌포르가 창백해지며 말했다."]], [["0148m\n“Eh, _pardieu!_”", "\"이런, 빌어먹을!\""], ["said the individual whose description we have twice given, entering the door, “what a great deal of ceremony!", "우리가 두 번이나 인상착의를 일러 준 그 인물이 문으로 들어서며 말했다. \"예의도 한참 차리는구나!"], ["Is it the custom in Marseilles for sons to keep their fathers waiting in their anterooms?”", "마르세유에서는 아들이 제 아비를 대기실에 기다리게 두는 게 풍습이더냐?\""]], [["“Father!”", "\"아버지!\""], ["cried Villefort, “then I was not deceived; I felt sure it must be you.”", "빌포르가 외쳤다. \"역시 제가 잘못 짚은 게 아니었군요. 아버지일 거라고 확신하고 있었습니다.\""]], [["“Well, then, if you felt so sure,”", "\"음, 그렇게나 확신했다면,\""], ["replied the new-comer, putting his cane in a corner and his hat on a chair, “allow me to say, my dear Gérard, that it was not very filial of you to keep me waiting at the door.”", "새로 들어선 사람이 지팡이를 한구석에 세워 두고 모자를 의자에 내려놓으며 대답했다. \"이거 한마디 해야겠구나, 내 사랑하는 제라르. 이 아비를 문간에 기다리게 한 건 그리 효성스러운 짓이 아니었어.\""]], [["“Leave us, Germain,” said Villefort.", "\"우리만 있게 해 주게, 제르맹.\""], ["The servant quitted the apartment with evident signs of astonishment.", "빌포르가 말했다. 하인은 역력한 놀라움의 기색을 띤 채 방을 나갔다."]], [["# Chapter 12. Father and Son", "# Chapter 12. Father and Son"]], [["M.", "누아르티에 씨는, 방으로 들어온 사람이 정말로 그였는데, 문이 닫힐 때까지 하인의 뒤를 지켜보았다."], ["Noirtier—for it was, indeed, he who entered—looked after the servant until the door was closed, and then, fearing, no doubt, that he might be overheard in the antechamber, he opened the door again, nor was the precaution useless, as appeared from the rapid retreat of Germain, who proved that he was not exempt from the sin which ruined our first parents. M.", "그러고는 누군가 대기실에서 엿들을지도 모른다는 걱정에서였는지, 다시 문을 열었다. 그 조심성은 헛되지 않았다. 제르맹이 황급히 물러나는 모습이 드러났기 때문이다."], ["Noirtier then took the trouble to close and bolt the antechamber door, then that of the bedchamber, and then extended his hand to Villefort, who had followed all his motions with surprise which he could not conceal.", "그것은 우리 최초의 조상을 파멸시킨 죄에서 그 역시 자유롭지 못함을 증명하는 일이었다. 누아르티에 씨는 그제야 수고스럽게 대기실 문을 닫고 빗장을 지른 뒤, 침실 문도 마저 잠그고서, 자신의 모든 동작을 감추지 못한 놀라움으로 좇던 빌포르에게 손을 내밀었다."]], [["“Well, now, my dear Gérard,”", "\"자, 이보게, 사랑하는 제라르.\""], ["said he to the young man, with a very significant look, “do you know, you seem as if you were not very glad to see me?”", "그가 의미심장한 눈길로 젊은이에게 말했다. \"내가 보기엔, 자네가 나를 만난 걸 그리 반기지 않는 것 같군그래.\""]], [["“My dear father,”", "\"사랑하는 아버지.\""], ["said Villefort, “I am, on the contrary, delighted; but I so little expected your visit, that it has somewhat overcome me.”", "빌포르가 말했다. \"오히려 그 반대로, 무척 기쁩니다. 다만 아버지께서 찾아오시리라고는 전혀 예상치 못했던 터라, 다소 정신이 없을 뿐입니다.\""]], [["“But, my dear fellow,” replied M.", "\"하지만 이 사람아.\""], ["Noirtier, seating himself, “I might say the same thing to you, when you announce to me your wedding for the 28th of February, and on the 3rd of March you turn up here in Paris.”", "누아르티에 씨가 자리에 앉으며 대답했다. \"나야말로 자네에게 같은 말을 할 수 있겠네. 2월 28일에 결혼한다고 알려 놓고서, 3월 3일에 여기 파리에 불쑥 나타났으니 말일세.\""]], [["“And if I have come, my dear father,”", "\"제가 오긴 했습니다만, 사랑하는 아버지.\""], ["said Gérard, drawing closer to M.", "제라르가 누아르티에 씨에게 더 가까이 다가서며 말했다."], ["Noirtier, “do not complain, for it is for you that I came, and my journey will be your salvation.”", "\"탓하지는 마십시오. 아버지를 위해서 온 것이고, 제 여정이 아버지를 구할 테니까요.\""]], [["“Ah, indeed!” said M.", "\"오, 그래?\""], ["Noirtier, stretching himself out at his ease in the chair.", "누아르티에 씨가 의자에 느긋하게 몸을 뻗으며 말했다."], ["“Really, pray tell me all about it, for it must be interesting.”", "\"정말이지, 어디 다 들려주게나. 흥미로운 이야기일 게 틀림없으니.\""]], [["“Father, you have heard speak of a certain Bonapartist club in the Rue Saint-Jacques?”", "\"아버지, 생자크 거리에 있는 어떤 보나파르트파 클럽 이야기를 들어 보셨습니까?\""]], [["“No. 53; yes, I am vice-president.”", "\"53번지 말이지. 그래, 내가 거기 부회장일세.\""]], [["“Father, your coolness makes me shudder.”", "\"아버지, 그 태연하심에 소름이 끼칩니다.\""]], [["“Why, my dear boy, when a man has been proscribed by the mountaineers, has escaped from Paris in a hay-cart, been hunted over the plains of Bordeaux by Robespierre’s bloodhounds, he becomes accustomed to most things.", "\"이보게, 산악파에게 추방당하고, 건초 수레에 숨어 파리를 빠져나오고, 로베스피에르의 사냥개들에게 보르도 평원을 가로질러 쫓겨 본 사람이라면, 웬만한 일에는 다 익숙해지는 법이라네."], ["But go on, what about the club in the Rue Saint-Jacques?”", "어쨌든 계속해 보게. 생자크 거리의 클럽이 어쨌다는 건가?\""]], [["“Why, they induced General Quesnel to go there, and General Quesnel, who quitted his own house at nine o’clock in the evening, was found the next day in the Seine.”", "\"그자들이 케넬 장군을 그곳으로 꾀어냈는데, 저녁 아홉 시에 집을 나선 케넬 장군이 이튿날 센강에서 발견되었습니다.\""]], [["0151m\n“And who told you this fine story?”", "\"한데 누가 자네에게 그 그럴듯한 이야기를 들려줬나?\""]], [["“The king himself.” “Well, then, in return for your story,”", "\"국왕 폐하께서 직접 하셨습니다.\" \"좋아, 그렇다면 자네 이야기에 답하여.\""], ["continued Noirtier, “I will tell you another.”", "누아르티에가 말을 이었다. \"내가 다른 이야기를 하나 들려주지.\""]], [["“My dear father, I think I already know what you are about to tell me.”", "\"사랑하는 아버지, 제가 보기엔 아버지께서 하시려는 말씀을 이미 알 것 같습니다.\""]], [["“Ah, you have heard of the landing of the emperor?”", "\"아, 황제께서 상륙하셨다는 소식을 들었나?\""]], [["“Not so loud, father, I entreat of you—for your own sake as well as mine.", "\"좀 낮춰 말씀해 주십시오, 아버지, 간청드립니다. 아버지를 위해서나 저를 위해서나요. 네, 그 소식은 들었습니다."], ["Yes, I heard this news, and knew it even before you could; for three days ago I posted from Marseilles to Paris with all possible speed, half-desperate at the enforced delay.”", "아버지보다 먼저 알았지요. 사흘 전에 마르세유에서 파리까지 갖은 속도를 다해 달려왔으니까요. 어쩔 수 없이 지체될 때마다 반쯤 미칠 지경이었습니다.\""]], [["“Three days ago?", "\"사흘 전이라고?"], ["You are crazy.", "제정신이 아니로군."], ["Why, three days ago the emperor had not landed.”", "사흘 전이면 황제께서 아직 상륙하시기도 전이었네.\""]], [["“No matter, I was aware of his intention.”", "\"그래도 상관없습니다. 저는 황제의 의도를 알고 있었으니까요.\""]], [["“How did you know about it?”", "\"그건 어떻게 알았나?\""]], [["“By a letter addressed to you from the Island of Elba.”", "\"엘바섬에서 아버지께 보낸 편지 한 통을 통해서요.\""]], [["“To me?”", "\"내게?\""]], [["“To you; and which I discovered in the pocket-book of the messenger.", "\"아버지께요. 그 편지를 전령의 수첩에서 발견했습니다."], ["Had that letter fallen into the hands of another, you, my dear father, would probably ere this have been shot.”", "만일 그 편지가 다른 사람의 손에 들어갔다면, 사랑하는 아버지, 아버지는 지금쯤 십중팔구 총살당하셨을 겁니다.\""], ["Villefort’s father laughed.", "빌포르의 아버지가 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“Come, come,”", "\"이런, 이런.\""], ["said he, “will the Restoration adopt imperial methods so promptly?", "그가 말했다. \"왕정복고 정권이 그리도 잽싸게 제정의 수법을 따라 하겠나?"], ["Shot, my dear boy?", "총살이라니, 이 사람아?"], ["What an idea!", "무슨 그런 생각을!"], ["Where is the letter you speak of?", "자네가 말하는 그 편지는 어디 있나?"], ["I know you too well to suppose you would allow such a thing to pass you.”", "자네가 그런 걸 그냥 지나칠 사람이 아니라는 건 내가 잘 알지.\""]], [["“I burnt it, for fear that even a fragment should remain; for that letter must have led to your condemnation.”", "\"태워 버렸습니다. 한 조각이라도 남을까 두려워서요. 그 편지는 아버지를 단죄하는 빌미가 되었을 테니까요.\""]], [["“And the destruction of your future prospects,”", "\"그리고 자네 앞날의 전망도 무너뜨렸겠지.\""], ["replied Noirtier; “yes, I can easily comprehend that.", "누아르티에가 대답했다. \"그래, 충분히 이해하네."], ["But I have nothing to fear while I have you to protect me.”", "하지만 나를 지켜 줄 자네가 있는 한, 나는 두려울 게 없어.\""]], [["“I do better than that, sir—I save you.”", "\"저는 그보다 더한 일을 하고 있습니다. 아버지를 구하는 거지요.\""]], [["“You do?", "\"자네가?"], ["Why, really, the thing becomes more and more dramatic—explain yourself.”", "이거 참, 점점 더 극적으로 흘러가는군. 어디 설명해 보게.\""]], [["“I must refer again to the club in the Rue Saint-Jacques.”", "\"다시 생자크 거리의 클럽 이야기로 돌아가야겠습니다.\""]], [["“It appears that this club is rather a bore to the police.", "\"그 클럽이 경찰에게는 꽤나 골칫거리인 모양이군."], ["Why didn’t they search more vigilantly?", "어째서 더 철저히 뒤지지 않았을까?"], ["they would have found——”", "그랬다면 찾아냈을 텐데……\""]], [["“They have not found; but they are on the track.”", "\"찾아내지는 못했지만, 단서를 쫓고 있습니다.\""]], [["“Yes, that the usual phrase; I am quite familiar with it.", "\"그래, 늘 그 소리지. 나도 익히 아는 말이야."], ["When the police is at fault, it declares that it is on the track; and the government patiently awaits the day when it comes to say, with a sneaking air, that the track is lost.”", "경찰이 헛다리를 짚으면, 단서를 쫓고 있다고 떠벌리지. 그러면 정부는 경찰이 머쓱한 낯으로 와서 단서를 놓쳤다고 말하는 날을 끈기 있게 기다리는 거고.\""]], [["“Yes, but they have found a corpse; the general has been killed, and in all countries they call that a murder.”", "\"그렇지만 시신이 발견됐습니다. 장군이 살해된 거고, 어느 나라에서나 그런 건 살인이라고 부르지요.\""]], [["“A murder do you call it?", "\"살인이라고?"], ["why, there is nothing to prove that the general was murdered.", "이런, 장군이 살해됐다는 증거는 아무것도 없네."], ["People are found every day in the Seine, having thrown themselves in, or having been drowned from not knowing how to swim.”", "센강에서는 날마다 사람이 발견돼. 스스로 몸을 던졌거나, 헤엄칠 줄 몰라 익사했거나 해서 말이야.\""]], [["“Father, you know very well that the general was not a man to drown himself in despair, and people do not bathe in the Seine in the month of January.", "\"아버지, 장군이 절망에 빠져 강에 몸을 던질 사람이 아니라는 걸, 그리고 1월에는 누구도 센강에서 멱을 감지 않는다는 걸 아버지께서도 잘 아시지 않습니까."], ["No, no, do not be deceived; this was murder in every sense of the word.”", "아니에요, 아니에요, 속지 마십시오. 이건 어느 모로 보나 살인이었습니다.\""]], [["“And who thus designated it?”", "\"그래, 그걸 그렇게 규정한 게 누군가?\""]], [["“The king himself.”", "\"국왕 폐하께서 직접 하셨습니다.\""]], [["“The king!", "\"폐하가!"], ["I thought he was philosopher enough to allow that there was no murder in politics.", "정치에는 살인이란 없다고 인정할 만큼은 사려 깊은 분인 줄 알았는데."], ["In politics, my dear fellow, you know, as well as I do, there are no men, but ideas—no feelings, but interests; in politics we do not kill a man, we only remove an obstacle, that is all.", "이 사람아, 정치에는 자네도 나만큼이나 잘 알다시피, 사람이 아니라 사상이 있을 뿐이고, 감정이 아니라 이해관계가 있을 뿐이라네. 정치에서 우리는 사람을 죽이는 게 아니라, 그저 장애물을 치울 뿐이지, 그게 전부야."], ["Would you like to know how matters have progressed?", "일이 어떻게 진행됐는지 알고 싶나?"], ["Well, I will tell you.", "좋아, 내가 들려주지."], ["It was thought reliance might be placed in General Quesnel; he was recommended to us from the Island of Elba; one of us went to him, and invited him to the Rue Saint-Jacques, where he would find some friends.", "케넬 장군은 믿을 만하다고들 여겼네. 엘바섬에서 우리에게 천거된 사람이었거든. 우리 중 한 사람이 그를 찾아가, 친구들을 만날 수 있을 거라며 생자크 거리로 초대했지."], ["He came there, and the plan was unfolded to him for leaving Elba, the projected landing, etc.", "그가 그리로 왔고, 엘바섬을 떠나는 계획이며 예정된 상륙이며 하는 것들을 그에게 펼쳐 보였네."], ["When he had heard and comprehended all to the fullest extent, he replied that he was a royalist.", "그가 그 모든 것을 남김없이 듣고 이해한 뒤, 자기는 왕당파라고 대답하더군. 그러자 모두가 서로 얼굴을 마주 보았네."], ["Then all looked at each other,—he was made to take an oath, and did so, but with such an ill grace that it was really tempting Providence to swear thus, and yet, in spite of that, the general was allowed to depart free—perfectly free.", "그에게 맹세를 시켰고, 그도 맹세를 했네만, 어찌나 마지못해 하던지 그렇게 맹세하는 건 정말이지 신의 뜻을 시험하는 격이었어. 그런데도 장군은 자유롭게 떠나도록 허락받았네. 완전히 자유롭게 말이야."], ["Yet he did not return home.", "그런데도 그는 집으로 돌아가지 않았어."], ["What could that mean?", "그게 무슨 뜻이겠나?"], ["why, my dear fellow, that on leaving us he lost his way, that’s all.", "이 사람아, 우리한테서 나가다가 길을 잃은 것뿐이야, 그게 전부라고."], ["A murder?", "살인이라니?"], ["really, Villefort, you surprise me.", "정말이지, 빌포르, 자네에겐 놀랐네."], ["You, a deputy procureur, to found an accusation on such bad premises!", "자네 같은 검사 대리가 그런 빈약한 전제 위에 고발을 세우다니! 자네가 왕당파 노릇을 하면서 우리 편 한 사람의 목을 쳤을 때, 내가 자네한테 '아들아, 너는 살인을 저질렀다'고 말하던가?"], ["Did I ever say to you, when you were fulfilling your character as a royalist, and cut off the head of one of my party, ‘My son, you have committed a murder?’ No, I said, ‘Very well, sir, you have gained the victory; tomorrow, perchance, it will be our turn.’”", "아닐세, 나는 이렇게 말했지. '잘했네, 자네가 이겼어. 어쩌면 내일은 우리 차례일지도 모르지.'\""]], [["“But, father, take care; when our turn comes, our revenge will be sweeping.”", "\"하지만 아버지, 조심하십시오. 우리 차례가 오면, 우리의 복수는 가차 없을 겁니다.\""]], [["“I do not understand you.”", "\"무슨 말인지 모르겠군.\""]], [["“You rely on the usurper’s return?”", "\"찬탈자가 돌아오리라 믿고 계시는군요?\""]], [["“We do.”", "\"그렇다네.\""]], [["“You are mistaken; he will not advance two leagues into the interior of France without being followed, tracked, and caught like a wild beast.”", "\"틀리셨습니다. 그자는 프랑스 내륙으로 2리외도 못 들어가서 들짐승처럼 쫓기고 추적당하고 붙잡히고 말 겁니다.\""]], [["“My dear fellow, the emperor is at this moment on the way to Grenoble; on the 10th or 12th he will be at Lyons, and on the 20th or 25th at Paris.”", "\"이 사람아, 황제께서는 지금 이 순간 그르노블로 가시는 중일세. 10일이나 12일이면 리옹에, 20일이나 25일이면 파리에 닿으실 거야.\""]], [["“The people will rise.”", "\"백성들이 들고일어날 겁니다.\""]], [["“Yes, to go and meet him.”", "\"그래, 그분을 맞으러 일어나겠지.\""]], [["“He has but a handful of men with him, and armies will be despatched against him.”", "\"황제 곁에는 한 줌의 병력밖에 없고, 그를 치러 군대가 파견될 겁니다.\""]], [["“Yes, to escort him into the capital.", "\"그래, 그분을 수도까지 호위하러 파견되겠지."], ["Really, my dear Gérard, you are but a child; you think yourself well informed because the telegraph has told you, three days after the landing, ‘The usurper has landed at Cannes with several men.", "정말이지, 사랑하는 제라르, 자네는 아직 어린애일세. 전신이 상륙 사흘 뒤에야 '찬탈자가 몇 명을 거느리고 칸에 상륙했다. 추격 중이다'라고 전해 주었다고 해서, 자네는 자신이 정통한 줄 알고 있어."], ["He is pursued.’ But where is he?", "한데 그분은 지금 어디 계신가?"], ["what is he doing?", "무얼 하고 계신가?"], ["You do not know at all, and in this way they will chase him to Paris, without drawing a trigger.”", "자네는 전혀 모르지. 이런 식이라면 그분을 파리까지 쫓는 동안 방아쇠 한 번 당기지 못할 걸세.\""]], [["“Grenoble and Lyons are faithful cities, and will oppose to him an impassable barrier.”", "\"그르노블과 리옹은 충성스러운 도시라, 그자에게 넘을 수 없는 장벽이 될 겁니다.\""]], [["“Grenoble will open her gates to him with enthusiasm—all Lyons will hasten to welcome him.", "\"그르노블은 환호하며 성문을 열어 줄 것이고, 리옹은 온통 그분을 맞으러 달려 나갈 걸세."], ["Believe me, we are as well informed as you, and our police are as good as your own.", "내 말을 믿게, 우리도 자네만큼 정통하고, 우리 정보망도 자네네 것 못지않아."], ["Would you like a proof of it?", "그 증거를 보고 싶나?"], ["well, you wished to conceal your journey from me, and yet I knew of your arrival half an hour after you had passed the barrier.", "자, 자네는 내게 여정을 숨기려 했지만, 나는 자네가 관문을 지난 지 반 시간 만에 도착 사실을 알았네."], ["You gave your direction to no one but your postilion, yet I have your address, and in proof I am here the very instant you are going to sit at table.", "자네는 마부 말고는 아무에게도 행선지를 일러 주지 않았는데, 나는 자네 주소를 알고 있었고, 그 증거로 자네가 막 식탁에 앉으려는 바로 그 순간에 이렇게 와 있지 않은가."], ["Ring, then, if you please, for a second knife, fork, and plate, and we will dine together.”", "그러니 종을 울려서, 나이프와 포크와 접시를 하나씩 더 가져오라 하게. 함께 저녁을 들지.\""]], [["“Indeed!”", "\"그렇군요!\""], ["replied Villefort, looking at his father with astonishment, “you really do seem very well informed.”", "빌포르가 놀라움에 차 아버지를 바라보며 대답했다. \"정말이지 무척 정통하신 것 같습니다.\""]], [["“Eh? the thing is simple enough.", "\"엉? 간단한 일이야."], ["You who are in power have only the means that money produces—we who are in expectation, have those which devotion prompts.”", "권력을 쥔 자네들에게는 돈이 만들어 내는 수단밖에 없지만, 때를 기다리는 우리에게는 헌신이 불러일으키는 수단이 있거든.\""]], [["“Devotion!” said Villefort, with a sneer.", "\"헌신이라니요!\" 빌포르가 비웃듯 말했다."]], [["“Yes, devotion; for that is, I believe, the phrase for hopeful ambition.”", "\"그래, 헌신. 내가 알기로는 그게 희망에 찬 야심을 일컫는 말이거든.\""]], [["And Villefort’s father extended his hand to the bell-rope, to summon the servant whom his son had not called.", "그러고는 빌포르의 아버지가 아들이 부르지도 않은 하인을 부르려고 종 줄에 손을 뻗었다."], ["Villefort caught his arm.", "빌포르가 그 팔을 붙잡았다."]], [["“Wait, my dear father,”", "\"잠깐만요, 사랑하는 아버지.\""], ["said the young man, “one word more.”", "젊은이가 말했다. \"한마디만 더 드리겠습니다.\""]], [["“Say on.”", "\"말해 보게.\""]], [["“However stupid the royalist police may be, they do know one terrible thing.”", "\"왕당파 경찰이 아무리 멍청하다 해도, 한 가지 끔찍한 사실만은 알고 있습니다.\""]], [["“What is that?”", "\"그게 뭔가?\""]], [["“The description of the man who, on the morning of the day when General Quesnel disappeared, presented himself at his house.”", "\"케넬 장군이 사라지던 날 아침, 그의 집을 찾아간 남자의 인상착의입니다.\""]], [["“Oh, the admirable police have found that out, have they?", "\"오, 그 대단하신 경찰이 그걸 알아냈단 말이지?"], ["And what may be that description?”", "그래, 그 인상착의가 어떻다던가?\""]], [["“Dark complexion; hair, eyebrows, and whiskers black; blue frock-coat, buttoned up to the chin; rosette of an officer of the Legion of Honor in his button-hole; a hat with wide brim, and a cane.”", "\"가무잡잡한 얼굴빛, 검은 머리에 검은 눈썹과 구레나룻, 턱까지 단추를 채운 푸른 프록코트, 단춧구멍에 단 레지옹 도뇌르 장교 표장, 챙 넓은 모자에 지팡이.\""]], [["“Ah, ha, that’s it, is it?”", "\"하, 그렇단 말이지?\""], ["said Noirtier; “and why, then, have they not laid hands on him?”", "누아르티에가 말했다. \"그렇다면 어째서 아직 그자를 붙잡지 못했단 말인가?\""]], [["“Because yesterday, or the day before, they lost sight of him at the corner of the Rue Coq-Héron.”", "\"어제인가 그제, 코케롱 거리 모퉁이에서 그를 놓쳤기 때문이지요.\""]], [["“Didn’t I say that your police were good for nothing?”", "\"자네네 경찰은 아무짝에도 쓸모없다고 내가 말하지 않았나?\""]], [["“Yes; but they may catch him yet.”", "\"네, 하지만 아직 그를 붙잡을 수도 있지요.\""]], [["“True,” said Noirtier, looking carelessly around him, “true, if this person were not on his guard, as he is;”", "\"그렇겠지.\" 누아르티에가 무심하게 주위를 둘러보며 말했다."], ["and he added with a smile, “He will consequently make a few changes in his personal appearance.”", "\"그렇겠지, 만일 그 사람이 지금처럼 경계하고 있지 않다면 말이야.\" 그러고는 미소 지으며 덧붙였다."], ["At these words he rose, and put off his frock-coat and cravat, went towards a table on which lay his son’s toilet articles, lathered his face, took a razor, and, with a firm hand, cut off the compromising whiskers.", "\"그러니 그 사람은 외모를 좀 바꾸겠지.\" 이 말과 함께 그는 자리에서 일어나 프록코트와 크라바트를 벗고, 아들의 세면도구가 놓인 탁자로 다가가 얼굴에 비누 거품을 칠하고 면도칼을 집어 들더니, 단호한 손놀림으로 의심을 살 만한 구레나룻을 잘라 냈다."], ["Villefort watched him with alarm not devoid of admiration.", "빌포르는 경탄이 섞이지 않았다고는 할 수 없는 불안한 마음으로 그를 지켜보았다."]], [["His whiskers cut off, Noirtier gave another turn to his hair; took, instead of his black cravat, a colored neckerchief which lay at the top of an open portmanteau; put on, in lieu of his blue and high-buttoned frock-coat, a coat of Villefort’s of dark brown, and cut away in front; tried on before the glass a narrow-brimmed hat of his son’s, which appeared to fit him perfectly, and, leaving his cane in the corner where he had deposited it, he took up a small bamboo switch, cut the air with it once or twice, and walked about with that easy swagger which was one of his principal characteristics.", "구레나룻을 잘라 낸 누아르티에는 머리 모양도 한 번 매만지고, 검은 크라바트 대신 열린 여행 가방 맨 위에 놓인 색깔 있는 목수건을 둘렀다. 그리고 푸른색에 단추를 높이 채운 프록코트 대신, 빌포르의 짙은 갈색 코트, 앞이 비스듬히 재단된 것을 걸치고, 아들의 챙 좁은 모자를 거울 앞에서 써 보았는데 그에게 꼭 맞는 듯했다. 그리고 좀 전에 세워 둔 모퉁이에 지팡이를 남겨 둔 채, 가느다란 등나무 단장을 집어 들어 허공을 한두 번 가르고는, 자신의 주된 특징 가운데 하나인 그 느긋하고 거들먹거리는 걸음걸이로 방 안을 거닐었다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"자, 어떤가.\""], ["he said, turning towards his wondering son, when this disguise was completed, “well, do you think your police will recognize me now.”", "변장을 마친 그가 어리둥절해하는 아들 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다. \"자, 이제 자네네 경찰이 나를 알아볼 것 같나?\""]], [["“No, father,”", "\"못 할 겁니다, 아버지.\""], ["stammered Villefort; “at least, I hope not.”", "빌포르가 더듬거리며 말했다. \"적어도, 그러기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["“And now, my dear boy,”", "\"그럼 이제, 이보게.\""], ["continued Noirtier, “I rely on your prudence to remove all the things which I leave in your care.”", "누아르티에가 말을 이었다. \"내가 자네에게 맡기고 가는 물건들은 자네가 알아서 신중하게 치워 주리라 믿네.\""]], [["“Oh, rely on me,”", "\"오, 제게 맡기십시오.\""], ["said Villefort.", "빌포르가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, yes; and now I believe you are right, and that you have really saved my life; be assured I will return the favor hereafter.”", "\"그래, 그래. 이제 나도 자네 말이 옳았다고, 자네가 정말로 내 목숨을 구했다고 인정하네. 이 은혜는 훗날 꼭 갚으마, 안심하게.\""]], [["Villefort shook his head.", "빌포르가 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["“You are not convinced yet?”", "\"아직도 확신이 안 서나?\""]], [["“I hope at least, that you may be mistaken.”", "\"적어도 아버지께서 틀리시기를 바랄 뿐입니다.\""]], [["“Shall you see the king again?”", "\"국왕을 다시 알현하게 되나?\""]], [["“Perhaps.”", "\"아마도요.\""]], [["“Would you pass in his eyes for a prophet?”", "\"그분 눈에 예언자로 보이고 싶은가?\""]], [["“Prophets of evil are not in favor at the court, father.”", "\"불행을 예언하는 자는 궁정에서 환영받지 못합니다, 아버지.\""]], [["“True, but some day they do them justice; and supposing a second restoration, you would then pass for a great man.”", "\"그렇지, 하지만 언젠가는 그들에게 마땅한 평가를 내려 주는 법이지. 그리고 만일 두 번째 왕정복고가 온다면, 자네는 그때 위인으로 통하게 될 걸세.\""]], [["“Well, what should I say to the king?”", "\"그럼, 국왕께 뭐라고 아뢰어야 합니까?\""]], [["“Say this to him: ‘Sire, you are deceived as to the feeling in France, as to the opinions of the towns, and the prejudices of the army; he whom in Paris you call the Corsican ogre, who at Nevers is styled the usurper, is already saluted as Bonaparte at Lyons, and emperor at Grenoble.", "\"이렇게 아뢰게. '폐하, 폐하께서는 프랑스의 민심에 대해서도, 도시들의 여론에 대해서도, 군대의 정서에 대해서도 잘못 알고 계십니다. 파리에서 폐하께서 코르시카의 식인귀라 부르고 느베르에서는 찬탈자라 일컫는 그자가, 리옹에서는 벌써 보나파르트로, 그르노블에서는 황제로 경례를 받고 있습니다."], ["You think he is tracked, pursued, captured; he is advancing as rapidly as his own eagles.", "폐하께서는 그가 추적당하고 쫓기고 사로잡혔다 여기시지만, 그는 제 독수리 깃발만큼이나 빠르게 진군하고 있습니다. 폐하께서 굶주림에 죽어 가고 피로에 지쳐 탈영할 채비가 된 줄로만 아시는 병사들은, 굴러가며 점점 커지는 눈덩이에 들러붙는 눈송이처럼 그에게로 모여들고 있습니다. 폐하, 떠나십시오, 프랑스를 그 진정한 주인에게, 돈으로 사들인 것이 아니라 정복의 권리로 얻은 자에게 넘기십시오."], ["The soldiers you believe to be dying with hunger, worn out with fatigue, ready to desert, gather like atoms of snow about the rolling ball as it hastens onward.", "떠나십시오, 폐하, 무슨 위험을 무릅쓰셔서가 아닙니다. 폐하의 적수는 폐하께 자비를 베풀 만큼 강대하니까요. 다만 성왕 루이의 후손이 아르콜라와 마렝고와 아우스터리츠의 사나이에게 목숨을 빚지는 것은 굴욕스러운 일이기 때문입니다.'"], ["Sire, go, leave France to its real master, to him who acquired it, not by purchase, but by right of conquest; go, sire, not that you incur any risk, for your adversary is powerful enough to show you mercy, but because it would be humiliating for a grandson of Saint Louis to owe his life to the man of Arcola, Marengo, Austerlitz.’ Tell him this, Gérard; or, rather, tell him nothing.", "그분께 이렇게 아뢰게, 제라르. 아니, 차라리 아무 말도 하지 말게. 자네 여정은 비밀에 부치게."], ["Keep your journey a secret; do not boast of what you have come to Paris to do, or have done; return with all speed; enter Marseilles at night, and your house by the back-door, and there remain, quiet, submissive, secret, and, above all, inoffensive; for this time, I swear to you, we shall act like powerful men who know their enemies.", "파리에 무엇을 하러 왔는지, 무엇을 했는지 떠벌리지 말게. 갖은 속도를 다해 돌아가게. 밤에 마르세유로 들어가, 뒷문으로 집에 들고, 거기 그대로 조용히, 순순히, 은밀히, 그리고 무엇보다 누구의 눈에도 거슬리지 않게 머무르게."], ["Go, my son—go, my dear Gérard, and by your obedience to my paternal orders, or, if you prefer it, friendly counsels, we will keep you in your place.", "이번에는, 내 자네에게 맹세하는데, 우리는 제 적을 아는 강한 자들답게 움직일 테니까. 가게, 아들아, 가게, 사랑하는 제라르. 아비의 명령에, 아니 자네가 그렇게 부르고 싶다면 정다운 충고에 따른다면, 우리가 자네를 그 자리에 지켜 주겠네."], ["This will be,”", "이 또한,\""], ["added Noirtier, with a smile, “one means by which you may a second time save me, if the political balance should some day take another turn, and cast you aloft while hurling me down.", "누아르티에가 미소 지으며 덧붙였다. \"언젠가 정치의 저울추가 다시 한번 기울어 자네를 높이 띄우고 나를 내동댕이친다면, 자네가 두 번째로 나를 구할 수 있는 한 가지 방편이 되겠지."], ["Adieu, my dear Gérard, and at your next journey alight at my door.”", "잘 있게, 사랑하는 제라르. 다음번 여정에는 내 집 문 앞에 내리게.\""]], [["Noirtier left the room when he had finished, with the same calmness that had characterized him during the whole of this remarkable and trying conversation.", "말을 마친 누아르티에는, 이 놀랍고도 마음 졸이는 대화 내내 그를 한결같이 감돌던 그 침착함 그대로 방을 나섰다."], ["Villefort, pale and agitated, ran to the window, put aside the curtain, and saw him pass, cool and collected, by two or three ill-looking men at the corner of the street, who were there, perhaps, to arrest a man with black whiskers, and a blue frock-coat, and hat with broad brim.", "창백하게 질려 동요하던 빌포르는 창가로 달려가 커튼을 젖히고, 그가 거리 모퉁이에 서 있던 두세 명의 험상궂은 사내들 곁을 태연하고 침착하게 지나치는 모습을 지켜보았다. 그자들은 어쩌면 검은 구레나룻에 푸른 프록코트, 챙 넓은 모자를 쓴 남자를 체포하려고 거기 있었을지도 몰랐다."]], [["Villefort stood watching, breathless, until his father had disappeared at the Rue Bussy.", "빌포르는 숨죽인 채 서서, 아버지가 뷔시 거리에서 사라질 때까지 지켜보았다. 그러고는 아버지가 남기고 간 갖가지 물건으로 돌아서서, 검은 크라바트와 푸른 프록코트를 여행 가방 맨 밑에 넣고, 모자는 어두운 벽장에 던져 넣고, 지팡이는 자잘하게 부러뜨려 불 속에 던졌다."], ["Then he turned to the various articles he had left behind him, put the black cravat and blue frock-coat at the bottom of the portmanteau, threw the hat into a dark closet, broke the cane into small bits and flung it in the fire, put on his travelling-cap, and calling his valet, checked with a look the thousand questions he was ready to ask, paid his bill, sprang into his carriage, which was ready, learned at Lyons that Bonaparte had entered Grenoble, and in the midst of the tumult which prevailed along the road, at length reached Marseilles, a prey to all the hopes and fears which enter into the heart of man with ambition and its first successes.", "그리고 여행용 모자를 쓰고, 시종을 불러 막 쏟아 내려던 수많은 질문을 눈짓 하나로 막은 뒤, 셈을 치르고는 채비된 마차에 뛰어올랐다. 리옹에서 보나파르트가 그르노블에 입성했다는 소식을 듣고, 길마다 들끓는 소란 한가운데를 지나, 마침내 마르세유에 다다랐다. 야심과 그 첫 성공이 사람의 마음속에 불러일으키는 온갖 희망과 두려움에 사로잡힌 채로."]], [["# Chapter 13. The Hundred Days", "# 제13장 백일천하"]], [["M. Noirtier was a true prophet, and things progressed rapidly, as he had predicted.", "누아르티에 씨는 진정한 예언자였고, 그의 말대로 사태는 빠르게 진행되었다."], ["Everyone knows the history of the famous return from Elba, a return which was unprecedented in the past, and will probably remain without a counterpart in the future.", "그 유명한 엘바섬 탈출의 역사를 모르는 사람은 없다. 과거에 전례가 없던 사건이었고, 앞으로도 견줄 만한 일은 아마 없을 것이다."]], [["Louis XVIII. made but a faint attempt to parry this unexpected blow; the monarchy he had scarcely reconstructed tottered on its precarious foundation, and at a sign from the emperor the incongruous structure of ancient prejudices and new ideas fell to the ground.", "루이 18세는 이 뜻밖의 일격을 막아 보려 했으나 그 시도는 미약하기 짝이 없었다. 그가 간신히 재건한 왕정은 불안한 토대 위에서 휘청거렸고, 황제가 손짓 한 번 하자 낡은 편견과 새로운 사상이 뒤섞인 그 어색한 구조물은 와르르 무너져 내렸다."], ["Villefort, therefore, gained nothing save the king’s gratitude (which was rather likely to injure him at the present time) and the cross of the Legion of Honor, which he had the prudence not to wear, although M. de Blacas had duly forwarded the brevet.", "그리하여 빌포르가 얻은 것이라고는 왕의 감사(지금 같은 시국에는 오히려 해가 될 만한 것이었다)와 레지옹 도뇌르 십자훈장뿐이었다. 블라카스 씨가 정식으로 임명장을 보내 주었으나, 빌포르는 신중하게도 그 훈장을 달지 않았다."]], [["Napoleon would, doubtless, have deprived Villefort of his office had it not been for Noirtier, who was all powerful at court, and thus the Girondin of ’93 and the Senator of 1806 protected him who so lately had been his protector.", "나폴레옹은 분명 빌포르의 자리를 빼앗았을 것이다. 궁정에서 절대적인 영향력을 지닌 누아르티에가 없었더라면 말이다. 그리하여 93년의 지롱드파이자 1806년의 원로원 의원이었던 그가, 얼마 전 자신을 보호해 주었던 아들을 이제는 거꾸로 보호하게 되었다."], ["All Villefort’s influence barely enabled him to stifle the secret Dantès had so nearly divulged.", "빌포르가 지닌 모든 영향력을 끌어모아 봐야, 당테스가 하마터면 누설할 뻔했던 비밀을 겨우 덮을 수 있을 정도였다."], ["The king’s procureur alone was deprived of his office, being suspected of royalism.", "자리를 잃은 것은 검찰총장뿐이었는데, 그는 왕당파라는 의심을 받았다."]], [["However, scarcely was the imperial power established—that is, scarcely had the emperor re-entered the Tuileries and begun to issue orders from the closet into which we have introduced our readers,—he found on the table there Louis XVIII.’s half-filled snuff-box,—scarcely had this occurred when Marseilles began, in spite of the authorities, to rekindle the flames of civil war, always smouldering in the south, and it required but little to excite the populace to acts of far greater violence than the shouts and insults with which they assailed the royalists whenever they ventured abroad. 0159m", "그러나 제국의 권력이 채 자리 잡기도 전에 — 다시 말해 황제가 튀일리궁에 다시 들어와, 우리가 독자들을 안내했던 그 밀실에서 명령을 내리기 시작하고, 그 방 탁자 위에서 루이 18세가 반쯤 채워 둔 코담뱃갑을 발견하기가 무섭게 — 마르세유는 당국의 만류에도 아랑곳없이 다시 내란의 불길을 지피기 시작했다. 남부에서는 늘 그 불씨가 잿더미 속에 도사리고 있었던 것이다. 왕당파가 밖으로 나서기만 하면 사람들은 고함과 욕설을 퍼부었는데, 그보다 훨씬 더 거센 폭력으로 민중을 들끓게 하는 데에는 그리 많은 것이 필요치 않았다."]], [["Owing to this change, the worthy shipowner became at that moment—we will not say all powerful, because Morrel was a prudent and rather a timid man, so much so, that many of the most zealous partisans of Bonaparte accused him of “moderation”—but sufficiently influential to make a demand in favor of Dantès.", "이러한 변화 덕분에 그 선량한 선주는 그 순간 — 전능했다고까지는 말하지 않겠다. 모렐은 신중하고 다소 소심한 사람이어서, 보나파르트의 가장 열성적인 추종자들 가운데 상당수가 그를 두고 \"미온적\"이라고 비난할 정도였으니까 — 그래도 당테스를 위해 청원을 넣을 만큼은 영향력을 갖추게 되었다."]], [["Villefort retained his place, but his marriage was put off until a more favorable opportunity.", "빌포르는 자리를 지켰지만, 결혼은 좀 더 유리한 때를 기다리며 미루어졌다."], ["If the emperor remained on the throne, Gérard required a different alliance to aid his career; if Louis XVIII.", "황제가 왕좌를 지킨다면, 제라르는 출셋길을 위해 다른 혼처를 구해야 했다."], ["returned, the influence of M. de Saint-Méran, like his own, could be vastly increased, and the marriage be still more suitable.", "루이 18세가 돌아온다면, 생메랑 씨의 영향력은 그 자신의 영향력과 마찬가지로 크게 불어날 테고, 그 결혼은 한층 더 어울리는 일이 될 터였다."], ["The deputy procureur was, therefore, the first magistrate of Marseilles, when one morning his door opened, and M. Morrel was announced.", "그리하여 차장검사는 마르세유의 으뜸가는 사법관이 되었는데, 어느 날 아침 그의 방문이 열리며 모렐 씨가 왔다는 전갈이 들어왔다."]], [["Anyone else would have hastened to receive him; but Villefort was a man of ability, and he knew this would be a sign of weakness.", "다른 사람 같았으면 서둘러 그를 맞이했을 것이다. 그러나 빌포르는 수완가였고, 그것이 약점을 드러내는 짓임을 알았다. 그는 모렐을 대기실에서 기다리게 했다."], ["He made Morrel wait in the antechamber, although he had no one with him, for the simple reason that the king’s procureur always makes everyone wait, and after passing a quarter of an hour in reading the papers, he ordered M. Morrel to be admitted.", "함께 있는 사람도 없었지만, 그저 검찰관이란 누구든 늘 기다리게 한다는 단순한 이유에서였다. 그러고는 십오 분 동안 서류를 읽은 뒤에야 모렐 씨를 들이라고 일렀다."]], [["Morrel expected Villefort would be dejected; he found him as he had found him six weeks before, calm, firm, and full of that glacial politeness, that most insurmountable barrier which separates the well-bred from the vulgar man.", "모렐은 빌포르가 풀이 죽어 있으리라 짐작했다. 그러나 그는 육 주 전 보았던 그대로였다. 차분하고 단호하며, 교양 있는 자와 천박한 자를 가르는 가장 넘기 힘든 장벽, 그 얼음장 같은 정중함으로 가득 차 있었다."]], [["He had entered Villefort’s office expecting that the magistrate would tremble at the sight of him; on the contrary, he felt a cold shudder all over him when he saw Villefort sitting there with his elbow on his desk, and his head leaning on his hand.", "모렐은 자신을 보면 사법관이 부들부들 떨리라 기대하며 빌포르의 집무실에 들어섰다. 그러나 정작 그 자신이 온몸에 차가운 전율을 느꼈다. 빌포르가 책상에 팔꿈치를 괴고 손으로 머리를 받친 채 앉아 있는 모습을 보았을 때였다."], ["He stopped at the door; Villefort gazed at him as if he had some difficulty in recognizing him; then, after a brief interval, during which the honest shipowner turned his hat in his hands,", "모렐은 문간에 멈춰 섰다. 빌포르는 그를 알아보기 어렵다는 듯 빤히 바라보았다. 정직한 선주가 손에 든 모자를 만지작거리는 동안 잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤,"]], [["“M. Morrel, I believe?”", "\"모렐 씨, 맞으시지요?\""], ["said Villefort.", "빌포르가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "\"네, 그렇습니다.\""]], [["“Come nearer,”", "\"가까이 오시지요.\""], ["said the magistrate, with a patronizing wave of the hand, “and tell me to what circumstance I owe the honor of this visit.”", "사법관이 거들먹거리는 손짓으로 말했다. \"어인 일로 이렇게 찾아오시는 영광을 베푸셨는지 말씀해 보십시오.\""]], [["“Do you not guess, monsieur?”", "\"짐작이 안 가십니까, 검사님?\""], ["asked Morrel.", "모렐이 물었다."]], [["“Not in the least; but if I can serve you in any way I shall be delighted.”", "\"전혀요. 다만 무슨 일로든 도움이 되어 드릴 수 있다면 기쁘겠습니다.\""]], [["“Everything depends on you.”", "\"모든 것이 검사님께 달려 있습니다.\""]], [["“Explain yourself, pray.”", "\"분명히 말씀해 보시지요.\""]], [["“Monsieur,”", "\"검사님,\""], ["said Morrel, recovering his assurance as he proceeded, “do you recollect that a few days before the landing of his majesty the emperor, I came to intercede for a young man, the mate of my ship, who was accused of being concerned in correspondence with the Island of Elba?", "모렐이 말을 이어 가며 차츰 침착함을 되찾았다. \"기억하십니까? 황제 폐하께서 상륙하시기 며칠 전, 제가 한 젊은이를 위해 탄원하러 찾아왔던 일 말입니다."], ["What was the other day a crime is today a title to favor.", "제 배의 항해사인데, 엘바섬과 내통한 혐의를 받고 있었지요. 며칠 전만 해도 죄였던 것이 오늘은 칭찬받을 자격이 되었습니다."], ["You then served Louis XVIII., and you did not show any favor—it was your duty; today you serve Napoleon, and you ought to protect him—it is equally your duty; I come, therefore, to ask what has become of him?” 0161m", "그때 검사님은 루이 18세를 섬기며 그에게 아무런 호의도 보이지 않으셨습니다 — 그것이 검사님의 의무였으니까요. 오늘 검사님은 나폴레옹을 섬기시니 마땅히 그를 보호하셔야 합니다 — 그 역시 검사님의 의무이지요. 그러니 그가 어떻게 되었는지 여쭈러 왔습니다.\""]], [["Villefort by a strong effort sought to control himself.", "빌포르는 안간힘을 다해 자제하려 애썼다. \"그 사람 이름이 무엇입니까?\""], ["“What is his name?”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he. “Tell me his name.”", "\"이름을 말씀해 보십시오.\""]], [["“Edmond Dantès.”", "\"에드몽 당테스입니다.\""]], [["Villefort would probably have rather stood opposite the muzzle of a pistol at five-and-twenty paces than have heard this name spoken; but he did not blanch.", "빌포르는 이 이름을 듣느니 차라리 스물다섯 걸음 앞에서 권총 총구를 마주하는 편이 나았을 것이다. 그러나 그는 낯빛 하나 변하지 않았다."]], [["“Dantès,”", "\"당테스,\""], ["repeated he, “Edmond Dantès.”", "그가 되뇌었다. \"에드몽 당테스.\""]], [["“Yes, monsieur.”", "\"네, 그렇습니다.\""], ["Villefort opened a large register, then went to a table, from the table turned to his registers, and then, turning to Morrel,", "빌포르는 커다란 등록부를 펼치더니 탁자로 갔다가, 탁자에서 다시 등록부로 돌아왔다. 그러고는 모렐을 향해 돌아서며,"]], [["“Are you quite sure you are not mistaken, monsieur?”", "\"잘못 아신 게 아니라고 확신하십니까, 선생?\""], ["said he, in the most natural tone in the world.", "세상에서 가장 자연스러운 어조로 그가 물었다."]], [["Had Morrel been a more quick-sighted man, or better versed in these matters, he would have been surprised at the king’s procureur answering him on such a subject, instead of referring him to the governors of the prison or the prefect of the department.", "모렐이 좀 더 눈치 빠른 사람이거나 이런 일에 밝았더라면, 검찰관이 자신을 교도소장이나 도지사에게 보내지 않고 직접 이런 사안에 답하는 것을 의아하게 여겼을 것이다."], ["But Morrel, disappointed in his expectations of exciting fear, was conscious only of the other’s condescension.", "그러나 두려움을 불러일으키리라던 기대가 빗나간 모렐은, 그저 상대의 호의에만 마음을 쓰고 있었다."], ["Villefort had calculated rightly.", "빌포르의 계산은 옳았다."]], [["“No,” said Morrel; “I am not mistaken.", "\"아닙니다.\" 모렐이 말했다."], ["I have known him for ten years, the last four of which he was in my service.", "\"잘못 안 게 아닙니다. 그를 십 년 동안 알고 지냈고, 그중 최근 사 년은 제 밑에서 일했습니다."], ["Do not you recollect, I came about six weeks ago to plead for clemency, as I come today to plead for justice.", "기억나지 않으십니까, 육 주쯤 전에 제가 선처를 호소하러 왔던 일이요. 오늘 정의를 호소하러 온 것처럼 말입니다."], ["You received me very coldly. Oh, the royalists were very severe with the Bonapartists in those days.”", "그때 검사님은 저를 몹시 차갑게 맞으셨지요. 아, 그 시절 왕당파는 보나파르트파에게 무척 가혹했으니까요.\""]], [["“Monsieur,”", "\"선생,\""], ["returned Villefort, “I was then a royalist, because I believed the Bourbons not only the heirs to the throne, but the chosen of the nation.", "빌포르가 대꾸했다. \"그때 저는 왕당파였습니다. 부르봉가가 왕위의 상속자일 뿐 아니라 국민이 선택한 자들이라 믿었기 때문이지요."], ["The miraculous return of Napoleon has conquered me, the legitimate monarch is he who is loved by his people.”", "나폴레옹의 기적 같은 귀환이 저를 굴복시켰습니다. 정당한 군주란 백성에게 사랑받는 자이니까요.\""]], [["“That’s right!”", "\"옳은 말씀입니다!\""], ["cried Morrel.", "모렐이 외쳤다."], ["“I like to hear you speak thus, and I augur well for Edmond from it.”", "\"그렇게 말씀하시는 걸 들으니 좋습니다. 에드몽에게도 좋은 징조라 여기겠습니다.\""]], [["“Wait a moment,”", "\"잠깐만 기다리십시오.\""], ["said Villefort, turning over the leaves of a register; “I have it—a sailor, who was about to marry a young Catalan girl.", "빌포르가 등록부의 책장을 넘기며 말했다. \"여기 있군요 — 어느 카탈루냐 처녀와 결혼하려던 선원이라."], ["I recollect now; it was a very serious charge.”", "이제 기억이 납니다. 아주 중대한 혐의였지요.\""]], [["“How so?”", "\"어째서요?\""]], [["“You know that when he left here he was taken to the Palais de Justice.”", "\"이곳을 떠나 재판소로 끌려갔다는 건 아시겠지요.\""]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서요?\""]], [["“I made my report to the authorities at Paris, and a week after he was carried off.”", "\"제가 파리 당국에 보고서를 올렸고, 일주일 뒤에 그는 이송되었습니다.\""]], [["“Carried off!”", "\"이송이라니!\""], ["said Morrel.", "모렐이 말했다."], ["“What can they have done with him?”", "\"그를 어떻게 했단 말입니까?\""]], [["“Oh, he has been taken to Fenestrelles, to Pignerol, or to the Sainte-Marguérite islands.", "\"아, 페네스트렐이나 피네롤, 아니면 생트마르그리트섬으로 보내졌겠지요."], ["Some fine morning he will return to take command of your vessel.”", "언젠가 어느 화창한 아침에 돌아와 다시 선생의 배를 지휘하게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["“Come when he will, it shall be kept for him.", "\"언제 돌아오든, 그 자리는 비워 두겠습니다."], ["But how is it he is not already returned?", "하지만 어째서 아직 돌아오지 않은 겁니까?"], ["It seems to me the first care of government should be to set at liberty those who have suffered for their adherence to it.”", "제가 보기엔, 정부가 가장 먼저 해야 할 일은 자기를 지지하다 고초를 겪은 이들을 풀어 주는 것일 텐데요.\""]], [["“Do not be too hasty, M. Morrel,”", "\"너무 서두르지 마십시오, 모렐 씨.\""], ["replied Villefort.", "빌포르가 대답했다."], ["“The order of imprisonment came from high authority, and the order for his liberation must proceed from the same source; and, as Napoleon has scarcely been reinstated a fortnight, the letters have not yet been forwarded.”", "\"투옥 명령은 높은 곳에서 내려왔으니, 석방 명령도 같은 곳에서 나와야 합니다. 그런데 나폴레옹이 복위한 지 겨우 보름밖에 지나지 않았으니, 아직 서신들이 발송되지 않았을 겁니다.\""]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["said Morrel, “is there no way of expediting all these formalities—of releasing him from arrest?”", "모렐이 말했다. \"이 모든 절차를 앞당길 방법은 없습니까 — 그를 구금에서 풀어 줄 방법 말입니다.\""]], [["“There has been no arrest.”", "\"구금된 적이 없습니다.\""]], [["“How?”", "\"네?\""]], [["“It is sometimes essential to government to cause a man’s disappearance without leaving any traces, so that no written forms or documents may defeat their wishes.”", "\"정부로서는 때때로 어떤 사람을 흔적 하나 남기지 않고 사라지게 만드는 것이 긴요할 때가 있지요. 어떤 서류나 문서로도 그 뜻을 거스를 수 없게 말입니다.\""]], [["“It might be so under the Bourbons, but at present——”", "\"부르봉가 치하에서는 그랬을지 몰라도, 지금은……\""]], [["“It has always been so, my dear Morrel, since the reign of Louis XIV.", "\"늘 그래 왔습니다, 모렐 씨. 루이 14세 치세 이래로 줄곧 말입니다."], ["The emperor is more strict in prison discipline than even Louis himself, and the number of prisoners whose names are not on the register is incalculable.”", "황제는 루이 본인보다도 옥살이 규율에 더 엄격하시고, 이름이 등록부에 오르지 않은 죄수의 수는 헤아릴 수조차 없습니다.\""], ["Had Morrel even any suspicions, so much kindness would have dispelled them.", "모렐에게 어떤 의심이 있었다 한들, 이만한 친절이라면 모두 흩어졌을 것이다."]], [["“Well, M. de Villefort, how would you advise me to act?”", "\"그렇다면, 빌포르 씨, 제가 어떻게 하면 좋겠습니까?\""], ["asked he.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“Petition the minister.”", "\"장관에게 청원하십시오.\""]], [["“Oh, I know what that is; the minister receives two hundred petitions every day, and does not read three.”", "\"아, 그게 어떤 건지 압니다. 장관은 매일 이백 통의 청원을 받지만 세 통도 읽지 않지요.\""]], [["“That is true; but he will read a petition countersigned and presented by me.”", "\"맞습니다. 하지만 제가 부서하여 직접 제출한 청원이라면 읽을 겁니다.\""]], [["“And will you undertake to deliver it?”", "\"그걸 검사님이 전해 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“With the greatest pleasure. Dantès was then guilty, and now he is innocent, and it is as much my duty to free him as it was to condemn him.”", "\"기꺼이 그러지요. 당테스는 그때는 죄인이었으나 지금은 무고한 사람이니, 그를 풀어 주는 것 또한 그를 단죄했던 것 못지않은 제 의무입니다.\""], ["Villefort thus forestalled any danger of an inquiry, which, however improbable it might be, if it did take place would leave him defenceless.", "이렇게 빌포르는 행여 있을지 모를 조사의 위험을 미리 막았다. 그럴 가능성은 희박했지만, 만약 조사가 이루어진다면 그는 속수무책이 될 터였다."]], [["“But how shall I address the minister?”", "\"그런데 장관에게 어떻게 써야 합니까?\""]], [["“Sit down there,”", "\"거기 앉으십시오.\""], ["said Villefort, giving up his place to Morrel, “and write what I dictate.”", "빌포르가 자기 자리를 모렐에게 내주며 말했다. \"제가 부르는 대로 받아 적으시지요.\""]], [["“Will you be so good?”", "\"그렇게까지 해 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“Certainly.", "\"물론입니다."], ["But lose no time; we have lost too much already.”", "다만 지체하지 마십시오. 이미 너무 많은 시간을 허비했으니까요.\""]], [["“That is true. Only think what the poor fellow may even now be suffering.”", "\"맞습니다. 그 가엾은 친구가 지금 이 순간에도 얼마나 고초를 겪고 있을지 생각하면……\""]], [["Villefort shuddered at the suggestion; but he had gone too far to draw back.", "빌포르는 그 말에 몸서리쳤다. 그러나 이미 돌이킬 수 없을 만큼 멀리 와 버렸다."], ["Dantès must be crushed to gratify Villefort’s ambition.", "빌포르의 야망을 채우려면 당테스는 짓밟혀야만 했다."]], [["Villefort dictated a petition, in which, from an excellent intention, no doubt, Dantès’ patriotic services were exaggerated, and he was made out one of the most active agents of Napoleon’s return.", "빌포르는 청원서를 받아쓰게 했는데, 분명 더없이 선한 의도에서였겠지만, 당테스의 애국적 공로가 부풀려졌고, 그는 나폴레옹의 귀환을 도운 가장 적극적인 첩자 가운데 하나로 그려졌다."], ["It was evident that at the sight of this document the minister would instantly release him.", "이 문서를 보면 장관이 그를 즉시 풀어 주리라는 것은 불 보듯 뻔했다."], ["The petition finished, Villefort read it aloud.", "청원서가 완성되자 빌포르가 소리 내어 읽었다."]], [["“That will do,”", "\"이만하면 됐습니다.\""], ["said he; “leave the rest to me.”", "그가 말했다. \"나머지는 제게 맡기십시오.\""]], [["“Will the petition go soon?”", "\"청원서는 곧 보내집니까?\""]], [["“Today.”", "\"오늘 보냅니다.\""]], [["“Countersigned by you?”", "\"검사님이 부서하시고요?\""]], [["“The best thing I can do will be to certify the truth of the contents of your petition.”", "\"제가 할 수 있는 최선은 이 청원서 내용이 사실임을 보증하는 것입니다.\""], ["And, sitting down, Villefort wrote the certificate at the bottom.", "빌포르는 앉아서 맨 아래에 증명서를 써 넣었다."]], [["“What more is to be done?”", "\"이제 또 무엇을 하면 됩니까?\""]], [["“I will do whatever is necessary.”", "\"필요한 일은 제가 다 하겠습니다.\""], ["This assurance delighted Morrel, who took leave of Villefort, and hastened to announce to old Dantès that he would soon see his son.", "이 다짐에 모렐은 기뻐했다. 그는 빌포르에게 작별을 고하고, 늙은 당테스에게 곧 아들을 보게 될 거라고 알리러 서둘러 떠났다."]], [["As for Villefort, instead of sending to Paris, he carefully preserved the petition that so fearfully compromised Dantès, in the hopes of an event that seemed not unlikely,—that is, a second restoration.", "빌포르로 말하자면, 그는 청원서를 파리로 보내기는커녕, 당테스를 무섭게 옭아매는 그 청원서를 조심스레 간직했다. 충분히 일어날 법한 어떤 사건 — 즉 두 번째 왕정복고를 기대하면서 말이다."], ["Dantès remained a prisoner, and heard not the noise of the fall of Louis XVIII.’s throne, or the still more tragic destruction of the empire.", "당테스는 여전히 죄수로 남았고, 루이 18세의 왕좌가 무너지는 소리도, 그보다 더 비극적인 제국의 멸망 소리도 듣지 못했다."]], [["Twice during the Hundred Days had Morrel renewed his demand, and twice had Villefort soothed him with promises.", "백일천하 동안 모렐은 두 번 청을 거듭했고, 빌포르는 두 번 다 약속으로 그를 달랬다."], ["At last there was Waterloo, and Morrel came no more; he had done all that was in his power, and any fresh attempt would only compromise himself uselessly.", "마침내 워털루가 닥쳤고, 모렐은 다시 찾아오지 않았다. 그는 자기가 할 수 있는 일을 다 했고, 새로이 무언가를 시도해 봐야 부질없이 제 처지만 위태롭게 할 뿐이었다."]], [["Louis XVIII.", "루이 18세가 다시 왕좌에 올랐다."], ["remounted the throne; Villefort, to whom Marseilles had become filled with remorseful memories, sought and obtained the situation of king’s procureur at Toulouse, and a fortnight afterwards he married Mademoiselle de Saint-Méran, whose father now stood higher at court than ever.", "마르세유가 회한 어린 기억으로 가득 차 버린 빌포르는 툴루즈의 검찰관 자리를 청하여 얻어 냈고, 보름 뒤 생메랑 양과 결혼했다. 그녀의 아버지는 이제 궁정에서 그 어느 때보다 높은 자리를 차지하고 있었다."]], [["And so Dantès, after the Hundred Days and after Waterloo, remained in his dungeon, forgotten of earth and heaven.", "그리하여 당테스는 백일천하가 지나고 워털루가 지난 뒤에도 여전히 지하 감옥에 갇힌 채, 땅에서도 하늘에서도 잊힌 존재로 남았다."]], [["Danglars comprehended the full extent of the wretched fate that overwhelmed Dantès; and, when Napoleon returned to France, he, after the manner of mediocre minds, termed the coincidence, _a decree of Providence_.", "당글라르는 당테스를 짓누른 비참한 운명이 얼마나 가혹한 것인지 속속들이 알았다. 그래서 나폴레옹이 프랑스로 돌아왔을 때, 그는 평범한 인간들이 으레 그러듯 그 우연의 일치를 두고 섭리의 뜻이라 불렀다."], ["But when Napoleon returned to Paris, Danglars’ heart failed him, and he lived in constant fear of Dantès’ return on a mission of vengeance.", "그러나 나폴레옹이 파리로 돌아오자 당글라르는 간담이 서늘해졌고, 당테스가 복수의 사명을 띠고 돌아올까 봐 늘 두려움 속에 살았다."], ["He therefore informed M. Morrel of his wish to quit the sea, and obtained a recommendation from him to a Spanish merchant, into whose service he entered at the end of March, that is, ten or twelve days after Napoleon’s return.", "그리하여 그는 모렐 씨에게 바다를 떠나고 싶다는 뜻을 전했고, 그에게서 어느 스페인 상인에게 보내는 추천장을 받아 냈다. 그는 3월 말, 다시 말해 나폴레옹이 돌아온 지 열흘이나 열이틀쯤 뒤에 그 상인의 밑으로 들어갔다."], ["He then left for Madrid, and was no more heard of.", "그러고는 마드리드로 떠났고, 그 뒤로는 소식이 끊겼다."]], [["Fernand understood nothing except that Dantès was absent.", "페르낭은 당테스가 사라졌다는 것 말고는 아무것도 알지 못했다."], ["What had become of him he cared not to inquire.", "그가 어떻게 되었는지는 알아보려고도 하지 않았다."], ["Only, during the respite the absence of his rival afforded him, he reflected, partly on the means of deceiving Mercédès as to the cause of his absence, partly on plans of emigration and abduction, as from time to time he sat sad and motionless on the summit of Cape Pharo, at the spot from whence Marseilles and the Catalans are visible, watching for the apparition of a young and handsome man, who was for him also the messenger of vengeance.", "다만 연적이 사라져 한숨 돌리게 된 그동안, 그는 한편으로는 그가 사라진 까닭을 메르세데스에게 둘러댈 방도를, 다른 한편으로는 이주와 납치의 계획을 궁리했다. 이따금 그는 마르세유와 카탈루냐 마을이 한눈에 내려다보이는 파로곶 꼭대기에 슬프고도 미동도 없이 앉아, 젊고 잘생긴 사내가 나타나기를 살피곤 했다. 그에게도 그 사내는 복수를 알리는 전령이었던 것이다."], ["Fernand’s mind was made up; he would shoot Dantès, and then kill himself.", "페르낭은 마음을 정했다. 당테스를 쏘아 죽이고, 그런 뒤에 자기도 목숨을 끊으리라."], ["But Fernand was mistaken; a man of his disposition never kills himself, for he constantly hopes.", "그러나 페르낭의 생각은 빗나갔다. 그런 기질의 사내는 결코 스스로 목숨을 끊지 않는다. 끊임없이 무언가를 바라기 때문이다."]], [["During this time the empire made its last conscription, and every man in France capable of bearing arms rushed to obey the summons of the emperor.", "이 무렵 제국은 마지막 징집을 단행했고, 무기를 들 수 있는 프랑스의 사내란 사내는 모두 황제의 부름에 따르려 달려갔다."], ["Fernand departed with the rest, bearing with him the terrible thought that while he was away, his rival would perhaps return and marry Mercédès.", "페르낭도 다른 이들과 함께 떠났다. 자기가 떠나 있는 동안 연적이 어쩌면 돌아와 메르세데스와 결혼해 버릴지 모른다는 끔찍한 생각을 품은 채로."], ["Had Fernand really meant to kill himself, he would have done so when he parted from Mercédès.", "만약 페르낭이 정말로 스스로 죽을 작정이었다면, 메르세데스와 헤어지던 그 순간에 그렇게 했을 것이다."], ["His devotion, and the compassion he showed for her misfortunes, produced the effect they always produce on noble minds—Mercédès had always had a sincere regard for Fernand, and this was now strengthened by gratitude.", "그의 헌신과, 그녀의 불행을 향해 보인 연민은 고귀한 마음에 으레 불러일으키는 효과를 자아냈다 — 메르세데스는 늘 페르낭에게 진심 어린 정을 품고 있었고, 이제 거기에 고마움까지 더해졌다."]], [["“My brother,”", "\"오라버니,\""], ["said she, as she placed his knapsack on his shoulders, “be careful of yourself, for if you are killed, I shall be alone in the world.”", "그녀가 그의 어깨에 배낭을 메어 주며 말했다. \"부디 몸조심하세요. 오라버니마저 죽으면, 저는 이 세상에 홀로 남게 되니까요.\""], ["These words carried a ray of hope into Fernand’s heart.", "이 말은 페르낭의 가슴에 한 줄기 희망의 빛을 던졌다."], ["Should Dantès not return, Mercédès might one day be his.", "당테스가 돌아오지 않는다면, 언젠가 메르세데스가 자기 사람이 될지도 몰랐다."]], [["0165m\nMercédès was left alone face to face with the vast plain that had never seemed so barren, and the sea that had never seemed so vast.", "메르세데스는 홀로 남았다. 한 번도 그토록 황량해 보인 적 없는 드넓은 들판과, 한 번도 그토록 끝없어 보인 적 없는 바다를 마주한 채로."], ["Bathed in tears she wandered about the Catalan village.", "그녀는 눈물에 젖어 카탈루냐 마을을 헤매고 다녔다."], ["Sometimes she stood mute and motionless as a statue, looking towards Marseilles, at other times gazing on the sea, and debating as to whether it were not better to cast herself into the abyss of the ocean, and thus end her woes.", "어떤 때는 조각상처럼 말없이 꼼짝 않고 서서 마르세유 쪽을 바라보았고, 또 어떤 때는 바다를 응시하며, 차라리 저 바다의 심연에 몸을 던져 모든 슬픔을 끝내는 편이 낫지 않을까 고민했다."], ["It was not want of courage that prevented her putting this resolution into execution; but her religious feelings came to her aid and saved her.", "그 결심을 행동으로 옮기지 못한 것은 용기가 없어서가 아니었다. 신앙심이 그녀를 도와 구해 준 것이었다."]], [["Caderousse was, like Fernand, enrolled in the army, but, being married and eight years older, he was merely sent to the frontier.", "카드루스도 페르낭처럼 군대에 편입되었으나, 결혼한 데다 여덟 살 더 많았으므로 그저 국경으로 보내졌을 뿐이다."], ["Old Dantès, who was only sustained by hope, lost all hope at Napoleon’s downfall.", "오로지 희망으로만 버텨 온 늙은 당테스는 나폴레옹이 몰락하자 모든 희망을 잃었다."], ["Five months after he had been separated from his son, and almost at the hour of his arrest, he breathed his last in Mercédès’ arms. M.", "아들과 헤어진 지 다섯 달 뒤, 거의 아들이 체포되던 그 시각에, 그는 메르세데스의 품에서 숨을 거두었다."], ["Morrel paid the expenses of his funeral, and a few small debts the poor old man had contracted.", "모렐 씨가 장례 비용과 그 가엾은 노인이 진 자잘한 빚 몇 가지를 갚아 주었다."]], [["There was more than benevolence in this action; there was courage; the south was aflame, and to assist, even on his death-bed, the father of so dangerous a Bonapartist as Dantès, was stigmatized as a crime.", "이 행동에는 선의 이상의 것이 담겨 있었다. 거기에는 용기가 있었다. 남부 전역이 불타오르고 있었고, 당테스처럼 위험한 보나파르트파의 아비를, 그것도 임종의 자리에서까지 도왔다는 것은 죄로 낙인찍히는 일이었으니까."]], [["# Chapter 14. The Two Prisoners", "# Chapter 14. The Two Prisoners"]], [["A year after Louis XVIII.’s restoration, a visit was made by the inspector-general of prisons.", "루이 18세가 복위하고 일 년이 지났을 무렵, 감옥 총감독관이 시찰을 나왔다."], ["Dantès in his cell heard the noise of preparation,—sounds that at the depth where he lay would have been inaudible to any but the ear of a prisoner, who could hear the splash of the drop of water that every hour fell from the roof of his dungeon.", "당테스는 자신의 독방에서 시찰 준비로 분주한 소리를 들었다. 그가 갇혀 있는 깊은 곳에서는, 매시간 천장에서 떨어지는 물방울 소리까지 들을 수 있는 죄수의 귀가 아니고서는 도무지 알아챌 수 없는 소리였다."], ["He guessed something uncommon was passing among the living; but he had so long ceased to have any intercourse with the world, that he looked upon himself as dead.", "그는 살아 있는 사람들 사이에 무언가 심상치 않은 일이 벌어지고 있음을 짐작했다. 그러나 너무 오랫동안 세상과 단절되어 있던 터라, 그는 스스로를 죽은 사람이나 다름없다고 여기고 있었다."]], [["The inspector visited, one after another, the cells and dungeons of several of the prisoners, whose good behavior or stupidity recommended them to the clemency of the government.", "감독관은 여러 죄수들의 독방과 지하 감방을 차례로 둘러보았다. 품행이 바르거나 어리석은 까닭에 정부의 관대한 처분을 받을 만한 자들이었다."], ["He inquired how they were fed, and if they had any request to make.", "그는 식사는 어떤지, 청할 것은 없는지 물었다."], ["The universal response was, that the fare was detestable, and that they wanted to be set free.", "한결같은 대답은, 음식이 형편없으며 풀려나고 싶다는 것이었다."]], [["The inspector asked if they had anything else to ask for.", "감독관은 그 밖에 청할 것이 더 없는지 물었다."], ["They shook their heads.", "죄수들은 고개를 저었다."], ["What could they desire beyond their liberty?", "자유 말고 그들이 무엇을 바랄 수 있겠는가?"], ["The inspector turned smilingly to the governor.", "감독관이 미소를 지으며 소장을 돌아보았다."]], [["“I do not know what reason government can assign for these useless visits; when you see one prisoner, you see all,—always the same thing,—ill fed and innocent.", "\"정부가 이런 쓸데없는 시찰에 무슨 명분을 댈 수 있는지 모르겠소. 죄수 한 명을 보면 다 본 거나 마찬가지요. 늘 똑같지."], ["Are there any others?”", "제대로 먹지도 못하고, 죄도 없다는 거요. 다른 자들은 없소?\""]], [["“Yes; the dangerous and mad prisoners are in the dungeons.”", "\"있습니다. 위험한 죄수들과 미친 죄수들이 지하 감방에 있습니다.\""]], [["“Let us visit them,”", "\"가 봅시다.\""], ["said the inspector with an air of fatigue. “We must play the farce to the end.", "감독관이 지친 기색으로 말했다. \"기왕 시작한 익살극이니 끝까지 해야지."], ["Let us see the dungeons.”", "지하 감방을 봅시다.\""]], [["“Let us first send for two soldiers,”", "\"먼저 병사 둘을 부르는 게 좋겠습니다.\""], ["said the governor.", "소장이 말했다."], ["“The prisoners sometimes, through mere uneasiness of life, and in order to be sentenced to death, commit acts of useless violence, and you might fall a victim.”", "\"죄수들이 더러는, 삶이 견딜 수 없이 괴로운 나머지, 사형 선고를 받으려고 부질없는 폭력을 휘두르는 일이 있습니다. 그러다 변을 당하실 수도 있습니다.\""]], [["“Take all needful precautions,”", "\"필요한 만큼 만반의 대비를 하시오.\""], ["replied the inspector.", "감독관이 대답했다."]], [["Two soldiers were accordingly sent for, and the inspector descended a stairway, so foul, so humid, so dark, as to be loathsome to sight, smell, and respiration.", "이에 병사 둘을 불러왔고, 감독관은 계단을 따라 내려갔다. 어찌나 더럽고 축축하고 어두운지, 보기에도 냄새 맡기에도 숨쉬기에도 역겨운 곳이었다."]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오.\""], ["cried the inspector, “who can live here?”", "감독관이 외쳤다. \"여기서 어떻게 사람이 살 수 있단 말이오?\""]], [["“A most dangerous conspirator, a man we are ordered to keep the most strict watch over, as he is daring and resolute.”", "\"극히 위험한 음모자입니다. 대담하고 단호한 자라, 가장 철저히 감시하라는 명을 받은 사람입니다.\""]], [["“He is alone?”", "\"혼자 갇혀 있소?\""]], [["“Certainly.”", "\"그렇습니다.\""]], [["“How long has he been there?”", "\"여기 들어온 지는 얼마나 됐소?\""]], [["“Nearly a year.”", "\"거의 일 년 됐습니다.\""]], [["“Was he placed here when he first arrived?”", "\"처음 들어왔을 때부터 여기에 가뒀소?\""]], [["“No; not until he attempted to kill the turnkey, who took his food to him.”", "\"아닙니다. 음식을 가져다주던 간수를 죽이려 한 뒤부터입니다.\""]], [["“To kill the turnkey?”", "\"간수를 죽이려 했다고?\""]], [["“Yes, the very one who is lighting us.", "\"그렇습니다. 바로 지금 우리에게 불을 밝혀 주고 있는 저 사람입니다."], ["Is it not true, Antoine?”", "그렇지 않나, 앙투안?\""], ["asked the governor.", "소장이 물었다."]], [["“True enough; he wanted to kill me!”", "\"그렇고말고요. 저를 죽이려 했습니다!\""], ["returned the turnkey.", "간수가 대답했다."]], [["“He must be mad,”", "\"미친 게 틀림없군.\""], ["said the inspector.", "감독관이 말했다."]], [["“He is worse than that,—he is a devil!”", "\"그보다 더합니다. 악마입니다!\""], ["returned the turnkey.", "간수가 대답했다."]], [["“Shall I complain of him?”", "\"내가 이자를 고발할까?\""], ["demanded the inspector.", "감독관이 물었다."]], [["“Oh, no; it is useless.", "\"아, 아닙니다."], ["Besides, he is almost mad now, and in another year he will be quite so.”", "그래 봤자 소용없습니다. 게다가 이자는 이제 거의 미쳤고, 일 년만 더 지나면 완전히 미칠 겁니다.\""]], [["“So much the better for him,—he will suffer less,”", "\"차라리 잘된 일이지. 그러면 덜 괴로울 테니.\""], ["said the inspector.", "감독관이 말했다."], ["He was, as this remark shows, a man full of philanthropy, and in every way fit for his office.", "이 말에서 드러나듯, 그는 박애심이 넘치고 모든 면에서 자기 직책에 더없이 어울리는 사람이었다."]], [["“You are right, sir,”", "\"옳으신 말씀입니다.\""], ["replied the governor; “and this remark proves that you have deeply considered the subject.", "소장이 대답했다. \"그 말씀을 들으니 이 문제를 깊이 헤아리고 계신 것을 알겠습니다."], ["Now we have in a dungeon about twenty feet distant, and to which you descend by another stair, an old abbé, formerly leader of a party in Italy, who has been here since 1811, and in 1813 he went mad, and the change is astonishing.", "자, 여기서 스무 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳에, 또 다른 계단을 내려가야 나오는 지하 감방에 늙은 신부가 하나 있습니다. 한때 이탈리아에서 한 당파의 지도자였던 자로, 1811년부터 이곳에 있었습니다. 1813년에 미쳤는데, 그 변화가 놀랍습니다."], ["He used to weep, he now laughs; he grew thin, he now grows fat.", "예전에는 울더니 이제는 웃고, 예전에는 비쩍 말랐는데 이제는 살이 오릅니다."], ["You had better see him, for his madness is amusing.”", "한번 보시는 게 좋을 겁니다. 그자의 광기는 제법 재미있거든요.\""]], [["“I will see them both,”", "\"둘 다 보겠소.\""], ["returned the inspector; “I must conscientiously perform my duty.”", "감독관이 대답했다. \"내 직무를 양심껏 수행해야 하니.\""]], [["This was the inspector’s first visit; he wished to display his authority.", "이번이 감독관의 첫 시찰이었다. 그는 자신의 권위를 과시하고 싶었다."]], [["“Let us visit this one first,” added he.", "\"이자부터 봅시다.\" 그가 덧붙였다."]], [["“By all means,” replied the governor, and he signed to the turnkey to open the door.", "\"여부가 있겠습니까.\" 소장이 대답하고는 간수에게 문을 열라는 신호를 보냈다."], ["At the sound of the key turning in the lock, and the creaking of the hinges, Dantès, who was crouched in a corner of the dungeon, whence he could see the ray of light that came through a narrow iron grating above, raised his head.", "자물쇠에 열쇠가 돌아가는 소리와 경첩이 삐걱이는 소리에, 위쪽의 좁은 쇠창살 사이로 들어오는 한 줄기 빛이 보이는 감방 한구석에 웅크리고 있던 당테스가 고개를 들었다."], ["Seeing a stranger, escorted by two turnkeys holding torches and accompanied by two soldiers, and to whom the governor spoke bareheaded, Dantès, who guessed the truth, and that the moment to address himself to the superior authorities was come, sprang forward with clasped hands.", "횃불을 든 간수 둘의 호위를 받고 병사 둘을 거느린, 게다가 소장이 모자를 벗고 말을 건네는 낯선 사람을 보자, 당테스는 사정을 단번에 알아채고 상부 권력에 호소할 순간이 왔음을 깨달았다. 그는 두 손을 맞잡고 앞으로 뛰쳐나갔다."]], [["The soldiers interposed their bayonets, for they thought that he was about to attack the inspector, and the latter recoiled two or three steps. Dantès saw that he was looked upon as dangerous.", "병사들이 그가 감독관을 공격하려는 줄 알고 총검을 들이밀자, 감독관은 두세 걸음 물러섰다. 당테스는 자신이 위험한 자로 비치고 있음을 알아챘다."], ["Then, infusing all the humility he possessed into his eyes and voice, he addressed the inspector, and sought to inspire him with pity.", "그러자 가진 모든 겸손을 두 눈과 목소리에 담아 감독관에게 호소하며 연민을 자아내려 애썼다."]], [["The inspector listened attentively; then, turning to the governor, observed, “He will become religious—he is already more gentle; he is afraid, and retreated before the bayonets—madmen are not afraid of anything; I made some curious observations on this at Charenton.”", "감독관은 주의 깊게 귀 기울이더니, 소장을 돌아보며 말했다. \"이자는 신앙심을 갖게 되겠군. 벌써 한결 온순해졌어."], ["Then, turning to the prisoner, “What is it you want?”", "겁을 먹고 총검 앞에서 물러섰지 않소. 미친 자들은 아무것도 두려워하지 않는 법이오. 내가 샤랑통에서 이에 관해 흥미로운 관찰을 좀 했지.\""], ["said he.", "그러고는 죄수를 향해 말했다. \"원하는 게 무엇이오?\""]], [["“I want to know what crime I have committed—to be tried; and if I am guilty, to be shot; if innocent, to be set at liberty.”", "\"제가 무슨 죄를 지었는지 알고 싶습니다. 재판을 받고 싶습니다. 죄가 있다면 총살을 당하고, 죄가 없다면 풀려나기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["“Are you well fed?”", "\"식사는 잘 나오시오?\""], ["said the inspector.", "감독관이 물었다."]], [["“I believe so; I don’t know; it’s of no consequence.", "\"그런 것 같습니다. 모르겠습니다. 그건 중요하지 않습니다."], ["What matters really, not only to me, but to officers of justice and the king, is that an innocent man should languish in prison, the victim of an infamous denunciation, to die here cursing his executioners.”", "정말로 중요한 것은, 저뿐 아니라 사법 관리들과 임금님께도 마찬가지인데, 죄 없는 사람이 파렴치한 밀고의 희생양이 되어 감옥에서 시들어 가다가, 자신을 처형한 자들을 저주하며 이곳에서 죽어 간다는 사실입니다.\""]], [["“You are very humble today,”", "\"오늘은 아주 겸손하구먼.\""], ["remarked the governor; “you are not so always; the other day, for instance, when you tried to kill the turnkey.”", "소장이 한마디 했다. \"늘 그런 건 아니지. 이를테면 며칠 전, 간수를 죽이려 들었을 때만 해도 말이야.\""]], [["“It is true, sir, and I beg his pardon, for he has always been very good to me, but I was mad.”", "\"맞습니다. 그리고 그에게 용서를 빕니다. 그는 늘 제게 잘해 주었으니까요. 하지만 그때 저는 제정신이 아니었습니다.\""]], [["“And you are not so any longer?”", "\"그럼 지금은 제정신이라는 건가?\""]], [["“No; captivity has subdued me—I have been here so long.”", "\"그렇습니다. 갇혀 지내면서 저도 누그러졌습니다. 이곳에 너무 오래 있었으니까요.\""]], [["“So long?—when were you arrested, then?”", "\"너무 오래라니, 그럼 언제 체포됐소?\""], ["asked the inspector.", "감독관이 물었다."]], [["“The 28th of February, 1815, at half-past two in the afternoon.”", "\"1815년 2월 28일, 오후 두 시 반이었습니다.\""]], [["“Today is the 30th of July, 1816,—why, it is but seventeen months.”", "\"오늘이 1816년 7월 30일이니, 아니, 겨우 열일곱 달밖에 안 됐잖소.\""]], [["“Only seventeen months,”", "\"겨우 열일곱 달이라고요.\""], ["replied Dantès.", "당테스가 대답했다."], ["“Oh, you do not know what is seventeen months in prison!—seventeen ages rather, especially to a man who, like me, had arrived at the summit of his ambition—to a man, who, like me, was on the point of marrying a woman he adored, who saw an honorable career opened before him, and who loses all in an instant—who sees his prospects destroyed, and is ignorant of the fate of his affianced wife, and whether his aged father be still living!", "\"오, 감옥에서의 열일곱 달이 어떤 것인지 모르시는 말씀입니다! 열일곱 세기라고 하는 편이 낫지요. 특히 저처럼 야망의 정점에 막 다다랐던 사람에게는요."], ["Seventeen months’ captivity to a sailor accustomed to the boundless ocean, is a worse punishment than human crime ever merited.", "저처럼 사랑하는 여인과 막 결혼하려던 참이었고, 눈앞에 명예로운 앞길이 열려 있었는데, 그 모든 것을 한순간에 잃어버린 사람에게는요. 자신의 앞날이 무너지는 것을 지켜보면서도, 약혼한 아내가 어찌 되었는지, 늙으신 아버지가 아직 살아 계신지조차 알지 못하는 사람에게는요! 끝없는 바다에 익숙한 뱃사람에게 열일곱 달의 감금이란, 어떤 인간의 죄로도 마땅하지 않을 만큼 가혹한 형벌입니다."], ["Have pity on me, then, and ask for me, not intelligence, but a trial; not pardon, but a verdict—a trial, sir, I ask only for a trial; that, surely, cannot be denied to one who is accused!”", "그러니 저를 가엾이 여기시어, 정보가 아니라 재판을 청해 주십시오. 사면이 아니라 판결을, 재판을, 나리, 저는 그저 재판을 청할 뿐입니다. 죄를 추궁당하는 사람에게 그것만은 거절할 수 없는 일 아니겠습니까!\""]], [["“We shall see,”", "\"두고 봅시다.\""], ["said the inspector; then, turning to the governor, “On my word, the poor devil touches me.", "감독관이 말하고는 소장을 돌아보았다. \"정말이지 저 가엾은 자가 마음을 움직이는구려."], ["You must show me the proofs against him.”", "저자에 대한 증거를 보여 주시오.\""]], [["“Certainly; but you will find terrible charges.”", "\"여부가 있겠습니까. 다만 끔찍한 혐의들을 보시게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["“Monsieur,”", "\"나리.\""], ["continued Dantès, “I know it is not in your power to release me; but you can plead for me—you can have me tried—and that is all I ask.", "당테스가 말을 이었다. \"저를 풀어 주시는 것이 나리의 권한 밖이라는 것은 압니다. 하지만 저를 위해 변호해 주실 수는 있습니다."], ["Let me know my crime, and the reason why I was condemned.", "재판을 받게 해 주실 수는 있습니다. 제가 청하는 것은 그것뿐입니다. 제 죄가 무엇인지, 무슨 까닭으로 제가 유죄가 되었는지 알게 해 주십시오."], ["Uncertainty is worse than all.”", "모르고 있는 것이 그 무엇보다 괴롭습니다.\""]], [["“Go on with the lights,”", "\"불을 들고 가지.\""], ["said the inspector.", "감독관이 말했다."]], [["“Monsieur,”", "\"나리.\""], ["cried Dantès, “I can tell by your voice you are touched with pity; tell me at least to hope.”", "당테스가 외쳤다. \"목소리만 들어도 연민을 느끼고 계신 것을 알겠습니다. 적어도 희망을 가져도 좋다고 말씀해 주십시오.\""]], [["“I cannot tell you that,”", "\"그렇게는 말해 줄 수 없소.\""], ["replied the inspector; “I can only promise to examine into your case.”", "감독관이 대답했다. \"그대의 사정을 살펴보겠다고 약속할 수 있을 뿐이오.\""]], [["“Oh, I am free—then I am saved!”", "\"오, 저는 자유의 몸이 되겠군요. 그렇다면 저는 살았습니다!\""]], [["“Who arrested you?”", "\"누가 그대를 체포했소?\""]], [["“M. Villefort.", "\"빌포르 씨입니다."], ["See him, and hear what he says.”", "그분을 만나, 그분이 하는 말을 들어 보십시오.\""]], [["“M. Villefort is no longer at Marseilles; he is now at Toulouse.”", "\"빌포르 씨는 이제 마르세유에 없소. 지금은 툴루즈에 있소.\""]], [["“I am no longer surprised at my detention,” murmured Dantès, “since my only protector is removed.”", "\"제 유일한 보호자가 떠나 버렸으니, 제가 갇혀 있는 것도 이젠 놀랍지 않군요.\" 당테스가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Had M. de Villefort any cause of personal dislike to you?”", "\"빌포르 씨가 그대에게 개인적인 반감을 품을 만한 까닭이 있었소?\""]], [["“None; on the contrary, he was very kind to me.”", "\"전혀 없습니다. 오히려 그분은 제게 무척 친절했습니다.\""]], [["“I can, then, rely on the notes he has left concerning you?”", "\"그렇다면 그가 그대에 관해 남긴 기록을 믿어도 되겠군?\""]], [["“Entirely.”", "\"전적으로요.\""]], [["“That is well; wait patiently, then.”", "\"좋소. 그럼 참고 기다리시오.\""]], [["Dantès fell on his knees, and prayed earnestly.", "당테스는 무릎을 꿇고 간절히 기도했다. 문이 닫혔다."], ["The door closed; but this time a fresh inmate was left with Dantès—Hope. 0173m", "그러나 이번에는 당테스 곁에 새로운 동거인이 남겨졌으니, 바로 희망이었다."]], [["“Will you see the register at once,”", "\"명부를 지금 바로 보시겠습니까,\""], ["asked the governor, “or proceed to the other cell?”", "소장이 물었다. \"아니면 다른 감방으로 가시겠습니까?\""]], [["“Let us visit them all,”", "\"전부 둘러봅시다.\""], ["said the inspector.", "감독관이 말했다."], ["“If I once went up those stairs. I should never have the courage to come down again.”", "\"이 계단을 한번 올라가고 나면, 다시 내려올 엄두가 나지 않을 테니.\""]], [["“Ah, this one is not like the other, and his madness is less affecting than this one’s display of reason.”", "\"아, 이자는 저자와는 다릅니다. 이자의 광기는 저자가 보여 준 멀쩡한 정신만큼 마음을 흔들지는 않을 겁니다.\""]], [["“What is his folly?”", "\"무슨 망상에 빠져 있소?\""]], [["“He fancies he possesses an immense treasure.", "\"제가 어마어마한 보물을 가지고 있다고 믿습니다."], ["The first year he offered government a million of francs for his release; the second, two; the third, three; and so on progressively.", "첫해에는 풀어 주는 대가로 정부에 백만 프랑을 내놓겠다고 하더니, 이듬해에는 이백만, 그다음 해에는 삼백만, 그렇게 해마다 늘려 갔습니다."], ["He is now in his fifth year of captivity; he will ask to speak to you in private, and offer you five millions.”", "지금 갇힌 지 다섯 해째인데, 나리께 따로 말씀드리고 싶다고 청한 뒤 오백만을 내놓겠다고 할 겁니다.\""]], [["“How curious!—what is his name?”", "\"참 별나군! 이름이 무엇이오?\""]], [["“The Abbé Faria.”", "\"파리아 신부입니다.\""]], [["“No. 27,” said the inspector.", "\"27호로군.\" 감독관이 말했다."]], [["“It is here; unlock the door, Antoine.”", "\"여깁니다. 문을 열게, 앙투안.\""]], [["The turnkey obeyed, and the inspector gazed curiously into the chamber of the _mad abbé_, as the prisoner was usually called.", "간수가 시키는 대로 하자, 감독관은 사람들이 흔히 부르는 대로 미친 신부의 방 안을 호기심 어린 눈으로 들여다보았다."]], [["In the centre of the cell, in a circle traced with a fragment of plaster detached from the wall, sat a man whose tattered garments scarcely covered him.", "감방 한가운데, 벽에서 떨어져 나온 회반죽 조각으로 그린 원 안에 한 남자가 앉아 있었다. 다 해진 옷이 몸을 겨우 가리고 있을 뿐이었다."], ["He was drawing in this circle geometrical lines, and seemed as much absorbed in his problem as Archimedes was when the soldier of Marcellus slew him. He did not move at the sound of the door, and continued his calculations until the flash of the torches lighted up with an unwonted glare the sombre walls of his cell; then, raising his head, he perceived with astonishment the number of persons present.", "그는 그 원 안에 기하학 도형의 선들을 그리고 있었는데, 마르켈루스의 병사가 아르키메데스를 죽일 때 그가 문제에 골몰해 있던 것만큼이나 자신의 문제에 깊이 빠져 있는 듯했다. 그는 문소리에도 꿈쩍하지 않고 계산을 이어 가다가, 횃불의 불빛이 감방의 침침한 벽을 평소와 다른 환한 빛으로 밝히자, 그제야 고개를 들고 그곳에 모인 사람들의 수를 보고 놀랐다."], ["He hastily seized the coverlet of his bed, and wrapped it round him.", "그는 황급히 침대 덮개를 움켜쥐어 몸에 둘렀다."]], [["“What is it you want?”", "\"원하는 게 무엇이오?\""], ["said the inspector.", "감독관이 말했다."]], [["“I, monsieur,”", "\"제가요, 나리?\""], ["replied the abbé with an air of surprise,—“I want nothing.”", "신부가 놀란 기색으로 대답했다. \"저는 아무것도 바라는 게 없습니다.\""]], [["“You do not understand,”", "\"잘못 알아들으셨군.\""], ["continued the inspector; “I am sent here by government to visit the prison, and hear the requests of the prisoners.”", "감독관이 말을 이었다. \"나는 정부의 명을 받고 감옥을 시찰하며 죄수들의 청을 듣고자 온 사람이오.\""]], [["“Oh, that is different,”", "\"오, 그렇다면 이야기가 다릅니다.\""], ["cried the abbé; “and we shall understand each other, I hope.”", "신부가 외쳤다. \"우리가 서로 잘 통할 수 있겠군요.\""]], [["“There, now,”", "\"보십시오.\""], ["whispered the governor, “it is just as I told you.”", "소장이 속삭였다. \"제가 말씀드린 그대로지요.\""]], [["“Monsieur,”", "\"나리.\""], ["continued the prisoner, “I am the Abbé Faria, born at Rome.", "죄수가 말을 이었다. \"저는 파리아 신부로, 로마에서 태어났습니다."], ["I was for twenty years Cardinal Spada’s secretary; I was arrested, why, I know not, toward the beginning of the year 1811; since then I have demanded my liberty from the Italian and French government.”", "이십 년 동안 스파다 추기경의 비서로 일했지요. 1811년 초쯤에 체포되었는데, 무슨 까닭인지는 모릅니다. 그 뒤로 이탈리아 정부와 프랑스 정부에 줄곧 석방을 요구해 왔습니다.\""]], [["“Why from the French government?”", "\"어찌하여 프랑스 정부에 말이오?\""]], [["“Because I was arrested at Piombino, and I presume that, like Milan and Florence, Piombino has become the capital of some French department.”", "\"피옴비노에서 체포되었으니까요. 밀라노나 피렌체처럼, 피옴비노도 어느 프랑스 도(道)의 중심지가 되었으리라 짐작합니다.\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아.\""], ["said the inspector, “you have not the latest news from Italy?”", "감독관이 말했다. \"이탈리아의 최근 소식은 모르시는군.\""]], [["“My information dates from the day on which I was arrested,”", "\"제가 아는 소식은 체포되던 날에 멈춰 있습니다.\""], ["returned the Abbé Faria; “and as the emperor had created the kingdom of Rome for his infant son, I presume that he has realized the dream of Machiavelli and Cæsar Borgia, which was to make Italy a united kingdom.”", "파리아 신부가 대답했다. \"황제께서 갓난 아드님을 위해 로마 왕국을 세우셨으니, 이탈리아를 하나의 통일된 왕국으로 만들겠다는 마키아벨리와 체사레 보르자의 꿈을 이루셨으리라 짐작합니다.\""]], [["“Monsieur,”", "\"나리.\""], ["returned the inspector, “Providence has changed this gigantic plan you advocate so warmly.”", "감독관이 대답했다. \"그대가 그토록 열렬히 옹호하는 그 원대한 계획을, 섭리가 바꾸어 놓았소이다.\""]], [["“It is the only means of rendering Italy strong, happy, and independent.”", "\"그것이야말로 이탈리아를 강하고 행복하며 독립된 나라로 만드는 유일한 길입니다.\""]], [["“Very possibly; only I am not come to discuss politics, but to inquire if you have anything to ask or to complain of.”", "\"그럴 수도 있겠지. 다만 나는 정치를 논하러 온 것이 아니라, 그대가 청하거나 불평할 것이 있는지 물으러 온 것이오.\""]], [["“The food is the same as in other prisons,—that is, very bad; the lodging is very unhealthful, but, on the whole, passable for a dungeon; but it is not that which I wish to speak of, but a secret I have to reveal of the greatest importance.”", "\"음식은 다른 감옥과 마찬가지입니다. 다시 말해 아주 형편없지요. 거처는 몹시 건강에 해롭지만, 지하 감방치고는 그런대로 견딜 만합니다. 하지만 제가 말하고 싶은 것은 그게 아니라, 더없이 중대한, 밝혀야 할 비밀입니다.\""]], [["“We are coming to the point,”", "\"이제 본론으로 들어가는군요.\""], ["whispered the governor.", "소장이 속삭였다."]], [["“It is for that reason I am delighted to see you,”", "\"바로 그 때문에 나리를 뵙게 되어 기쁩니다.\""], ["continued the abbé, “although you have disturbed me in a most important calculation, which, if it succeeded, would possibly change Newton’s system.", "신부가 말을 이었다. \"비록 나리께서 더없이 중요한 계산을 방해하셨지만 말입니다. 그 계산이 성공한다면 뉴턴의 체계를 뒤바꿀 수도 있을 텐데요."], ["Could you allow me a few words in private.”", "잠시 따로 말씀을 나눌 수 있게 해 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“What did I tell you?”", "\"제가 뭐라고 했습니까?\""], ["said the governor.", "소장이 말했다."]], [["“You knew him,” returned the inspector with a smile.", "\"그대가 이자를 잘 알고 있었군.\" 감독관이 미소를 띠며 대답했다."]], [["“What you ask is impossible, monsieur,”", "\"그대가 청하는 것은 불가능하오, 나리.\""], ["continued he, addressing Faria.", "그가 파리아를 향해 말을 이었다."]], [["0175m\n“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["said the abbé, “I would speak to you of a large sum, amounting to five millions.”", "신부가 말했다. \"오백만에 이르는 막대한 금액에 관한 이야기입니다.\""]], [["“The very sum you named,”", "\"바로 그 액수로군.\""], ["whispered the inspector in his turn.", "이번에는 감독관이 속삭였다."]], [["“However,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["continued Faria, seeing that the inspector was about to depart, “it is not absolutely necessary for us to be alone; the governor can be present.”", "감독관이 떠나려 하는 것을 본 파리아가 말을 이었다. \"우리 둘만 있어야 하는 건 아닙니다. 소장도 함께 있어도 됩니다.\""]], [["“Unfortunately,”", "\"안타깝게도,\""], ["said the governor, “I know beforehand what you are about to say; it concerns your treasures, does it not?”", "소장이 말했다. \"당신이 무슨 말을 할지 나는 미리 다 알고 있소. 당신의 보물에 관한 이야기 아니오?\""], ["Faria fixed his eyes on him with an expression that would have convinced anyone else of his sanity.", "파리아는 다른 사람이라면 누구라도 그가 멀쩡하다고 믿게 만들 만한 표정으로 소장을 응시했다."]], [["“Of course,”", "\"물론이오.\""], ["said he; “of what else should I speak?”", "그가 말했다. \"그것 말고 또 무슨 이야기를 하겠소?\""]], [["“Mr. Inspector,”", "\"감독관님.\""], ["continued the governor, “I can tell you the story as well as he, for it has been dinned in my ears for the last four or five years.”", "소장이 말을 이었다. \"그 이야기라면 저도 이자만큼 잘 풀어놓을 수 있습니다. 지난 네댓 해 동안 줄곧 제 귀에 못이 박이도록 들어 왔으니까요.\""]], [["“That proves,”", "\"그것이 바로,\""], ["returned the abbé, “that you are like those of Holy Writ, who having eyes see not, and having ears hear not.”", "신부가 대답했다. \"당신이 성서에 나오는 자들과 같다는 증거요. 눈이 있어도 보지 못하고, 귀가 있어도 듣지 못하는 자들 말이오.\""]], [["“My dear sir, the government is rich and does not want your treasures,”", "\"이보시오, 정부는 부유하여 당신의 보물 따위는 필요로 하지 않소.\""], ["replied the inspector; “keep them until you are liberated.”", "감독관이 대답했다. \"풀려날 때까지 잘 간직하시구려.\""], ["The abbé’s eyes glistened; he seized the inspector’s hand.", "신부의 두 눈이 번득였다. 그는 감독관의 손을 붙잡았다."]], [["“But what if I am not liberated,” cried he, “and am detained here until my death?", "\"하지만 제가 풀려나지 못한다면 어쩌겠습니까,\" 그가 외쳤다."], ["this treasure will be lost.", "\"죽을 때까지 여기 갇혀 있다면요?"], ["Had not government better profit by it?", "그러면 이 보물은 사라지고 말 겁니다."], ["I will offer six millions, and I will content myself with the rest, if they will only give me my liberty.”", "차라리 정부가 그것으로 이득을 보는 편이 낫지 않겠습니까? 육백만을 내놓겠습니다. 자유만 준다면 나머지는 가지고서 만족하겠습니다.\""]], [["“On my word,”", "\"정말이지,\""], ["said the inspector in a low tone, “had I not been told beforehand that this man was mad, I should believe what he says.”", "감독관이 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"이자가 미쳤다는 말을 미리 듣지 않았더라면, 저자의 말을 믿었을 거요.\""]], [["“I am not mad,”", "\"저는 미치지 않았습니다.\""], ["replied Faria, with that acuteness of hearing peculiar to prisoners.", "파리아가 죄수 특유의 예민한 청각으로 알아듣고 대답했다. \"제가 말하는 보물은 실제로 존재합니다. 나리와 약정을 맺고 서명할 것을 제안합니다."], ["“The treasure I speak of really exists, and I offer to sign an agreement with you, in which I promise to lead you to the spot where you shall dig; and if I deceive you, bring me here again,—I ask no more.”", "나리께서 땅을 파게 될 그 장소로 안내하겠다고 약속하지요. 만일 제가 나리를 속인다면, 저를 다시 이리로 데려오십시오. 그 이상은 바라지 않습니다.\""]], [["The governor laughed.", "소장이 웃었다."], ["“Is the spot far from here?”", "\"그 장소가 여기서 먼가?\""]], [["“A hundred leagues.”", "\"백 리외입니다.\""]], [["“It is not ill-planned,”", "\"제법 그럴듯하게 꾸몄군.\""], ["said the governor.", "소장이 말했다."], ["“If all the prisoners took it into their heads to travel a hundred leagues, and their guardians consented to accompany them, they would have a capital chance of escaping.”", "\"죄수들이 너도나도 백 리외를 여행하겠다고 마음먹고, 간수들이 그걸 따라나서 준다면, 도망칠 절호의 기회가 생기겠지.\""]], [["“The scheme is well known,”", "\"빤한 수법이오.\" 감독관이 말했다."], ["said the inspector; “and the abbé’s plan has not even the merit of originality.”", "\"이 신부의 계획은 독창성이라는 미덕조차 없소이다.\" 그러고는 파리아를 향해 말했다."], ["Then turning to Faria, “I inquired if you are well fed?” said he.", "\"식사는 잘 나오느냐고 물었소만.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["“Swear to me,”", "\"맹세해 주십시오.\""], ["replied Faria, “to free me if what I tell you prove true, and I will stay here while you go to the spot.”", "파리아가 대답했다. \"제 말이 사실로 드러나면 저를 풀어 주겠다고요. 나리께서 그 장소로 가시는 동안 저는 여기 남아 있겠습니다.\""]], [["“Are you well fed?”", "\"식사는 잘 나오시오?\""], ["repeated the inspector.", "감독관이 거듭 물었다."]], [["“Monsieur, you run no risk, for, as I told you, I will stay here; so there is no chance of my escaping.”", "\"나리, 나리께서는 아무 위험도 무릅쓰지 않으십니다. 말씀드렸듯이 저는 여기 남아 있을 테니까요. 그러니 제가 도망칠 가능성은 없습니다.\""]], [["“You do not reply to my question,”", "\"내 물음에는 대답을 않는군.\""], ["replied the inspector impatiently.", "감독관이 못마땅한 듯 말했다."]], [["“Nor you to mine,” cried the abbé.", "\"나리께서도 제 물음에 답하지 않으십니다.\" 신부가 외쳤다."], ["“You will not accept my gold; I will keep it for myself.", "\"나리는 제 황금을 받지 않으시겠다니, 그것은 제가 가지겠습니다."], ["You refuse me my liberty; God will give it me.” And the abbé, casting away his coverlet, resumed his place, and continued his calculations. 0177m", "나리는 제게 자유를 주지 않으시겠다니, 그것은 하느님이 주실 겁니다.\" 그러고는 신부가 덮개를 내던지고 제자리로 돌아가 계산을 이어 갔다."]], [["“What is he doing there?” said the inspector.", "\"저자가 거기서 뭘 하는 거요?\" 감독관이 말했다."]], [["“Counting his treasures,” replied the governor.", "\"제 보물을 세고 있는 거지요.\" 소장이 대답했다."]], [["Faria replied to this sarcasm with a glance of profound contempt.", "파리아는 이 빈정거림에 깊은 경멸의 눈길로 응답했다."], ["They went out. The turnkey closed the door behind them.", "그들은 밖으로 나왔다. 간수가 그들 뒤로 문을 닫았다."]], [["“He was wealthy once, perhaps?”", "\"저자가 한때는 부유했을지도 모르겠소?\""], ["said the inspector.", "감독관이 말했다."]], [["“Or dreamed he was, and awoke mad.”", "\"아니면 부유하다고 꿈을 꾸다가, 깨어나 미쳐 버린 거겠지요.\""]], [["“After all,”", "\"하긴,\""], ["said the inspector, “if he had been rich, he would not have been here.”", "감독관이 말했다. \"정말로 부유했더라면 여기 와 있지도 않았겠지.\""]], [["So the matter ended for the Abbé Faria.", "파리아 신부에 관한 일은 그렇게 끝이 났다."], ["He remained in his cell, and this visit only increased the belief in his insanity.", "그는 자신의 감방에 그대로 남았고, 이번 시찰은 그가 미쳤다는 믿음을 굳혀 놓았을 뿐이었다."]], [["Caligula or Nero, those treasure-seekers, those desirers of the impossible, would have accorded to the poor wretch, in exchange for his wealth, the liberty he so earnestly prayed for.", "칼리굴라나 네로, 보물을 좇고 불가능한 것을 탐하던 그 황제들이었다면, 이 가엾은 자에게 그 부와 맞바꾸어 그토록 간절히 빌던 자유를 내주었으리라."], ["But the kings of modern times, restrained by the limits of mere probability, have neither courage nor desire.", "그러나 한낱 개연성의 한계에 매여 있는 근대의 군주들에게는 그럴 용기도 욕망도 없다."], ["They fear the ear that hears their orders, and the eye that scrutinizes their actions.", "그들은 자신의 명령을 듣는 귀와 자신의 행동을 살피는 눈을 두려워한다."], ["Formerly they believed themselves sprung from Jupiter, and shielded by their birth; but nowadays they are not inviolable.", "옛날에는 군주들이 스스로를 유피테르의 후손으로 여기며 그 출신으로 보호받는다 믿었으나, 오늘날에는 그들도 침범당하지 않는 존재가 아니다."]], [["It has always been against the policy of despotic governments to suffer the victims of their persecutions to reappear.", "박해의 희생자들이 다시 모습을 드러내게 두는 것은 늘 전제 정권의 방침에 어긋나는 일이었다."], ["As the Inquisition rarely allowed its victims to be seen with their limbs distorted and their flesh lacerated by torture, so madness is always concealed in its cell, from whence, should it depart, it is conveyed to some gloomy hospital, where the doctor has no thought for man or mind in the mutilated being the jailer delivers to him.", "종교 재판소가 사지가 뒤틀리고 살이 고문으로 찢긴 희생자들의 모습이 드러나는 것을 좀처럼 허락하지 않았듯이, 광기는 언제나 감방 안에 감추어진다. 만일 거기서 나간다 해도, 어느 음침한 병원으로 보내질 뿐인데, 그곳의 의사는 간수가 넘겨주는 그 망가진 존재 속에서 인간도 정신도 헤아리지 않는다."], ["The very madness of the Abbé Faria, gone mad in prison, condemned him to perpetual captivity.", "감옥에서 미쳐 버린 파리아 신부의 광기야말로 그를 영영 갇혀 지내도록 만든 셈이었다."]], [["The inspector kept his word with Dantès; he examined the register, and found the following note concerning him:", "감독관은 당테스와 한 약속을 지켰다. 그는 명부를 살펴보았고, 그에 관한 다음과 같은 기록을 찾아냈다."]], [["_Edmond Dantès:_", "에드몽 당테스."]], [["Violent Bonapartist; took an active part in the return from Elba.", "과격한 보나파르트파. 엘바섬에서의 귀환에 적극 가담함."]], [["The greatest watchfulness and care to be exercised.", "각별히 엄중하게 감시하고 다룰 것."]], [["This note was in a different hand from the rest, which showed that it had been added since his confinement.", "이 기록은 다른 부분과는 다른 필체로 쓰여 있었는데, 그가 수감된 뒤에 덧붙여진 것임을 알 수 있었다."], ["The inspector could not contend against this accusation; he simply wrote, _Nothing to be done._", "감독관은 이 고발에 맞설 도리가 없었다. 그는 그저 손쓸 것 없음이라고 적었을 뿐이다."]], [["This visit had infused new vigor into Dantès; he had, till then, forgotten the date; but now, with a fragment of plaster, he wrote the date, 30th July, 1816, and made a mark every day, in order not to lose his reckoning again.", "이번 시찰은 당테스에게 새로운 활력을 불어넣었다. 그때까지 그는 날짜를 잊고 있었다. 그러나 이제 회반죽 조각으로 1816년 7월 30일이라는 날짜를 적고, 다시는 셈을 놓치지 않으려고 날마다 표시를 해 나갔다."], ["Days and weeks passed away, then months—Dantès still waited; he at first expected to be freed in a fortnight.", "며칠이 지나고, 몇 주가 지나고, 이어 몇 달이 흘렀다. 당테스는 여전히 기다렸다."], ["This fortnight expired, he decided that the inspector would do nothing until his return to Paris, and that he would not reach there until his circuit was finished, he therefore fixed three months; three months passed away, then six more.", "처음에는 보름 안에 풀려나리라 기대했다. 그 보름이 지나자, 그는 감독관이 파리로 돌아가기 전에는 아무것도 하지 않을 것이고, 시찰을 다 마쳐야 그곳에 닿으리라 판단하여, 석 달로 기한을 잡았다. 석 달이 지나고, 다시 여섯 달이 흘렀다."], ["Finally ten months and a half had gone by and no favorable change had taken place, and Dantès began to fancy the inspector’s visit but a dream, an illusion of the brain.", "마침내 열 달 반이 지나도록 좋은 쪽으로의 변화는 일어나지 않았고, 당테스는 감독관의 시찰이 한낱 꿈, 머릿속의 환상이 아니었나 여기기 시작했다."]], [["At the expiration of a year the governor was transferred; he had obtained charge of the fortress at Ham.", "일 년이 다 지났을 무렵, 소장이 전임되었다. 그는 앙 요새의 책임을 맡게 되었다."], ["He took with him several of his subordinates, and amongst them Dantès’ jailer.", "그는 부하 몇을 데려갔는데, 그중에는 당테스의 간수도 있었다."], ["A new governor arrived; it would have been too tedious to acquire the names of the prisoners; he learned their numbers instead.", "새 소장이 부임했다. 죄수들의 이름을 일일이 익히는 것은 너무 번거로운 일이었으므로, 그는 이름 대신 번호를 익혔다."], ["This horrible place contained fifty cells; their inhabitants were designated by the numbers of their cell, and the unhappy young man was no longer called Edmond Dantès—he was now number 34.", "이 끔찍한 곳에는 감방이 쉰 개 있었다. 그 안에 든 자들은 자기 감방의 번호로 불렸고, 이 불행한 젊은이는 더 이상 에드몽 당테스가 아니었다. 그는 이제 34호였다."]], [["# Chapter 15. Number 34 and Number 27", "# Chapter 15. Number 34 and Number 27"]], [["Dantès passed through all the stages of torture natural to prisoners in suspense.", "당테스는 불안 속에 갇힌 죄수들이 으레 겪는 모든 고통의 단계를 차례로 거쳐 갔다."], ["He was sustained at first by that pride of conscious innocence which is the sequence to hope; then he began to doubt his own innocence, which justified in some measure the governor’s belief in his mental alienation; and then, relaxing his sentiment of pride, he addressed his supplications, not to God, but to man.", "처음에는 무죄를 확신하는 자긍심이 그를 떠받쳐 주었으니, 그것은 희망에 잇닿은 감정이었다. 그러다 그는 자신의 무죄를 의심하기 시작했는데, 이는 그가 정신이 나갔다는 소장의 믿음을 어느 정도 뒷받침하는 셈이었다. 그러고는 자긍심마저 누그러뜨린 채, 하느님이 아니라 인간에게 탄원하기에 이르렀다."], ["God is always the last resource.", "하느님은 언제나 마지막 의지처다."], ["Unfortunates, who ought to begin with God, do not have any hope in him till they have exhausted all other means of deliverance.", "하느님부터 찾아야 마땅한 불행한 사람들도, 다른 모든 구원의 수단을 다 써 버리기 전에는 하느님께 아무 희망도 걸지 않는 법이다."]], [["Dantès asked to be removed from his present dungeon into another, even if it were darker and deeper, for a change, however disadvantageous, was still a change, and would afford him some amusement.", "당테스는 지금 갇힌 지하 감방에서 다른 감방으로 옮겨 달라고 청했다. 그곳이 더 어둡고 깊은 곳이라 해도 상관없었다. 아무리 불리한 변화라도 변화는 변화였고, 그것이 그에게 약간의 위안이라도 줄 터였다."], ["He entreated to be allowed to walk about, to have fresh air, books, and writing materials.", "그는 밖을 거닐게 해 달라고, 신선한 공기와 책과 필기도구를 달라고 애원했다."], ["His requests were not granted, but he went on asking all the same.", "그의 청은 받아들여지지 않았으나, 그래도 그는 끊임없이 청했다."], ["He accustomed himself to speaking to the new jailer, although the latter was, if possible, more taciturn than the old one; but still, to speak to a man, even though mute, was something.", "그는 새 간수에게 말을 거는 데에도 익숙해졌다. 새 간수는 옛 간수보다 더하면 더했지 덜하지 않을 만큼 말이 없는 사람이었다. 그래도 사람에게 말을 거는 것은, 비록 그가 벙어리나 다름없다 해도, 무언가는 되었다."], ["Dantès spoke for the sake of hearing his own voice; he had tried to speak when alone, but the sound of his voice terrified him.", "당테스는 제 목소리를 듣기 위해 말을 했다. 혼자 있을 때도 말해 보려 했으나, 제 목소리에 그만 소스라쳤다."]], [["Often, before his captivity, Dantès’ mind had revolted at the idea of assemblages of prisoners, made up of thieves, vagabonds, and murderers.", "갇히기 전, 당테스는 도둑과 부랑자와 살인자로 이루어진 죄수들의 무리를 떠올리며 몸서리치곤 했다. 이제 그는 그들 틈에 끼고 싶었다."], ["He now wished to be amongst them, in order to see some other face besides that of his jailer; he sighed for the galleys, with the infamous costume, the chain, and the brand on the shoulder.", "간수의 얼굴 말고 다른 얼굴이라도 보고 싶었기 때문이다. 그는 갤리선을 그리워했다. 그 치욕스러운 죄수복과 쇠사슬, 어깨에 찍힌 낙인까지도."], ["The galley-slaves breathed the fresh air of heaven, and saw each other. They were very happy.", "갤리선의 노예들은 하늘의 신선한 공기를 들이마셨고, 서로의 얼굴을 보았다. 그들은 더없이 행복한 자들이었다."]], [["He besought the jailer one day to let him have a companion, were it even the mad abbé.", "어느 날 그는 간수에게 동무를 하나 붙여 달라고 간청했다. 그 미친 신부라도 좋다고 했다."], ["The jailer, though rough and hardened by the constant sight of so much suffering, was yet a man.", "간수는 그토록 많은 고통을 늘 보아 온 탓에 거칠고 무뎌졌으나, 그래도 사람이었다."], ["At the bottom of his heart he had often had a feeling of pity for this unhappy young man who suffered so; and he laid the request of number 34 before the governor; but the latter sapiently imagined that Dantès wished to conspire or attempt an escape, and refused his request.", "마음 깊은 곳에서는 이토록 괴로워하는 가엾은 젊은이에게 종종 연민을 느꼈다. 그래서 그는 34호의 청을 소장에게 전했다. 그러나 소장은 당테스가 음모를 꾸미거나 탈옥을 꾀하려는 것이라고 그럴듯하게 짐작하고는 그 청을 거절했다."], ["Dantès had exhausted all human resources, and he then turned to God.", "당테스는 인간의 모든 방도를 다 써 버렸고, 그제야 하느님께로 돌아섰다."]], [["All the pious ideas that had been so long forgotten, returned; he recollected the prayers his mother had taught him, and discovered a new meaning in every word; for in prosperity prayers seem but a mere medley of words, until misfortune comes and the unhappy sufferer first understands the meaning of the sublime language in which he invokes the pity of heaven!", "그토록 오래 잊고 있던 경건한 생각들이 모두 되살아났다. 그는 어머니가 가르쳐 준 기도를 떠올렸고, 그 한마디 한마디에서 새로운 뜻을 발견했다. 형편이 좋을 때 기도란 그저 말의 뒤섞임일 뿐이지만, 불행이 닥치고서야 비로소 불행한 자는 하늘의 자비를 구하는 그 숭고한 언어의 뜻을 처음으로 깨닫는 법이다."], ["He prayed, and prayed aloud, no longer terrified at the sound of his own voice, for he fell into a sort of ecstasy.", "그는 기도했고, 소리 내어 기도했다. 더는 제 목소리에 소스라치지 않았으니, 일종의 황홀경에 빠져들었기 때문이다."], ["He laid every action of his life before the Almighty, proposed tasks to accomplish, and at the end of every prayer introduced the entreaty oftener addressed to man than to God: “Forgive us our trespasses as we forgive them that trespass against us.”", "그는 제 삶의 모든 행적을 전능하신 분 앞에 펼쳐 놓고, 이루어야 할 과업을 다짐했다. 그리고 기도가 끝날 때마다 하느님보다 인간에게 더 자주 던져지는 그 탄원을 덧붙였다. \"저희에게 잘못한 이를 저희가 용서하듯이, 저희 죄를 용서하소서.\""], ["Yet in spite of his earnest prayers, Dantès remained a prisoner.", "그러나 그 간절한 기도에도 당테스는 여전히 죄수였다."]], [["Then gloom settled heavily upon him.", "이윽고 어둠이 그를 무겁게 짓눌렀다."], ["Dantès was a man of great simplicity of thought, and without education; he could not, therefore, in the solitude of his dungeon, traverse in mental vision the history of the ages, bring to life the nations that had perished, and rebuild the ancient cities so vast and stupendous in the light of the imagination, and that pass before the eye glowing with celestial colors in Martin’s Babylonian pictures.", "당테스는 생각이 지극히 단순하고 교육을 받지 못한 사람이었다. 그러니 지하 감방의 고독 속에서 그는 마음의 눈으로 시대의 역사를 가로지를 수도, 스러진 민족들을 되살릴 수도, 마틴●의 바빌론 그림 속에서 천상의 빛깔로 눈앞에 떠오르는 그 광대하고 경이로운 고대 도시들을 상상 속에 다시 세울 수도 없었다."], ["He could not do this, he whose past life was so short, whose present so melancholy, and his future so doubtful.", "그는 그럴 수 없었다. 과거는 너무도 짧았고, 현재는 너무도 우울했으며, 미래는 너무도 불확실했으니까."], ["Nineteen years of light to reflect upon in eternal darkness!", "영원한 어둠 속에서 곱씹어야 할 빛이라곤 고작 열아홉 해뿐이었다!"], ["No distraction could come to his aid; his energetic spirit, that would have exalted in thus revisiting the past, was imprisoned like an eagle in a cage.", "어떤 위안도 그를 도우러 올 수 없었다. 그렇게 과거를 되짚으며 드높이 솟구쳤을 그의 활기찬 정신은, 새장에 갇힌 독수리처럼 갇혀 버렸다. 그는 한 가지 생각에 매달렸다."], ["He clung to one idea—that of his happiness, destroyed, without apparent cause, by an unheard-of fatality; he considered and reconsidered this idea, devoured it (so to speak), as the implacable Ugolino devours the skull of Archbishop Roger in the Inferno of Dante.", "아무런 분명한 까닭도 없이 전대미문의 운명에 짓밟힌 자신의 행복이라는 생각이었다. 그는 그 생각을 곱씹고 또 곱씹었으며, 말하자면 그것을 게걸스레 뜯어먹었다. 단테의 『지옥편』에서 무자비한 우골리노가 로제 대주교의 두개골을 뜯어먹듯이."]], [["Rage supplanted religious fervor.", "종교적 열정의 자리를 분노가 차지했다."], ["Dantès uttered blasphemies that made his jailer recoil with horror, dashed himself furiously against the walls of his prison, wreaked his anger upon everything, and chiefly upon himself, so that the least thing,—a grain of sand, a straw, or a breath of air that annoyed him, led to paroxysms of fury.", "당테스는 간수가 끔찍해하며 뒷걸음치게 만들 만한 신성모독의 말을 내뱉었고, 감방의 벽에 미친 듯이 제 몸을 부딪쳤으며, 닥치는 대로, 특히 제 자신에게 분풀이를 했다. 그래서 아주 사소한 것에도, 이를테면 모래 한 알, 지푸라기 한 가닥, 거슬리는 한 줄기 바람에도 발작적인 광기에 사로잡혔다."], ["Then the letter that Villefort had showed to him recurred to his mind, and every line gleamed forth in fiery letters on the wall like the _mene, mene, tekel upharsin_ of Belshazzar.", "그러다 빌포르가 보여 준 그 편지가 다시 떠올랐고, 한 줄 한 줄이 벨사살의 메네 메네 데겔 우바르신●●처럼 벽 위에 불타는 글자로 어른거렸다."], ["He told himself that it was the enmity of man, and not the vengeance of Heaven, that had thus plunged him into the deepest misery.", "그는 자신을 이토록 깊은 비참 속으로 빠뜨린 것이 하늘의 복수가 아니라 인간의 적의였다고 되뇌었다."], ["He consigned his unknown persecutors to the most horrible tortures he could imagine, and found them all insufficient, because after torture came death, and after death, if not repose, at least the boon of unconsciousness.", "그는 정체 모를 박해자들을 상상할 수 있는 가장 끔찍한 고문에 처했지만, 그 어느 것에도 만족하지 못했다. 고문 뒤에는 죽음이 오고, 죽음 뒤에는, 안식까지는 아니더라도 적어도 무의식이라는 은총이 따르기 때문이었다."]], [["By dint of constantly dwelling on the idea that tranquillity was death, and if punishment were the end in view other tortures than death must be invented, he began to reflect on suicide.", "평온함이 곧 죽음이고, 만일 형벌이 목적이라면 죽음 말고 다른 고문을 고안해 내야 한다는 생각을 끊임없이 곱씹은 끝에, 그는 자살을 떠올리기 시작했다."], ["Unhappy he, who, on the brink of misfortune, broods over ideas like these!", "불행하여라, 불행의 벼랑 끝에서 이런 생각에 잠기는 자여!"]], [["Before him is a dead sea that stretches in azure calm before the eye; but he who unwarily ventures within its embrace finds himself struggling with a monster that would drag him down to perdition.", "그의 앞에는 푸르고 잔잔하게 펼쳐진 죽음의 바다가 눈앞에 놓여 있다. 그러나 무심코 그 품으로 뛰어든 자는 자신을 파멸로 끌어내리려는 괴물과 씨름하는 처지가 된다."], ["Once thus ensnared, unless the protecting hand of God snatch him thence, all is over, and his struggles but tend to hasten his destruction.", "일단 그렇게 옭매이면, 하느님의 보호하는 손길이 그를 거기서 낚아채지 않는 한 모든 것이 끝나며, 발버둥 칠수록 파멸만 앞당길 뿐이다."], ["This state of mental anguish is, however, less terrible than the sufferings that precede or the punishment that possibly will follow.", "그러나 이 마음의 고뇌는 그에 앞서는 고통이나 뒤따를지도 모를 형벌보다는 덜 끔찍하다."], ["There is a sort of consolation at the contemplation of the yawning abyss, at the bottom of which lie darkness and obscurity.", "입을 떡 벌린 심연, 그 바닥에 어둠과 암흑이 깔린 그 심연을 바라보는 데에는 일종의 위안이 있다."]], [["Edmond found some solace in these ideas.", "에드몽은 이런 생각에서 얼마간 위안을 얻었다."], ["All his sorrows, all his sufferings, with their train of gloomy spectres, fled from his cell when the angel of death seemed about to enter.", "죽음의 천사가 막 들어서려는 듯하자, 그의 모든 슬픔과 모든 고통이, 음울한 망령들을 거느린 채 감방에서 달아났다."], ["Dantès reviewed his past life with composure, and, looking forward with terror to his future existence, chose that middle line that seemed to afford him a refuge.", "당테스는 침착하게 지난 삶을 돌아보았고, 앞으로의 삶을 두려운 마음으로 내다보며, 자신에게 피난처가 되어 줄 듯한 그 중간의 길을 택했다."]], [["“Sometimes,”", "\"이따금,\""], ["said he, “in my voyages, when I was a man and commanded other men, I have seen the heavens overcast, the sea rage and foam, the storm arise, and, like a monstrous bird, beating the two horizons with its wings.", "그가 말했다. \"내가 한 사람의 남자로서 다른 사람들을 지휘하며 항해할 때, 하늘이 잔뜩 흐려지고 바다가 미쳐 날뛰며 거품을 일으키고, 폭풍이 일어 괴물 같은 새가 양쪽 수평선을 날개로 후려치는 광경을 보았지."], ["Then I felt that my vessel was a vain refuge, that trembled and shook before the tempest.", "그럴 때면 나는 내 배가 부질없는 피난처임을, 폭풍 앞에서 떨고 흔들리는 피난처임을 느꼈다."], ["Soon the fury of the waves and the sight of the sharp rocks announced the approach of death, and death then terrified me, and I used all my skill and intelligence as a man and a sailor to struggle against the wrath of God.", "이내 성난 파도와 날카로운 바위가 죽음의 임박을 알렸고, 그때는 죽음이 나를 두렵게 했지. 그래서 나는 한 사람의 남자로서, 또 뱃사람으로서 가진 모든 솜씨와 지혜를 다해 하느님의 노여움에 맞서 싸웠다."], ["But I did so because I was happy, because I had not courted death, because to be cast upon a bed of rocks and seaweed seemed terrible, because I was unwilling that I, a creature made for the service of God, should serve for food to the gulls and ravens.", "하지만 그렇게 한 것은 내가 행복했기 때문이고, 죽음을 청하지 않았기 때문이며, 바위와 해초로 된 잠자리에 내던져지는 것이 끔찍했기 때문이고, 하느님을 섬기기 위해 지어진 내가 갈매기와 까마귀의 먹이가 되는 것을 견딜 수 없었기 때문이지. 하지만 이제는 다르다. 나를 삶에 붙들어 매던 모든 것을 잃었고, 죽음이 미소 지으며 안식으로 나를 부르고 있어."], ["But now it is different; I have lost all that bound me to life, death smiles and invites me to repose; I die after my own manner, I die exhausted and broken-spirited, as I fall asleep when I have paced three thousand times round my cell,—that is thirty thousand steps, or about ten leagues.”", "나는 내 방식대로 죽으리라. 감방을 삼천 번 맴돌고 나서 잠드는 것처럼, 기진맥진 넋이 꺾인 채로 죽으리라. 삼천 번이면 삼만 걸음, 그러니까 십 리외쯤 되는 길이지.\""]], [["No sooner had this idea taken possession of him than he became more composed, arranged his couch to the best of his power, ate little and slept less, and found existence almost supportable, because he felt that he could throw it off at pleasure, like a worn-out garment.", "이 생각이 그를 사로잡자마자, 그는 한결 차분해졌다. 그는 할 수 있는 한 잠자리를 정돈했고, 적게 먹고 더 적게 잤으며, 삶이 거의 견딜 만하다고 느꼈다. 다 낡은 옷처럼 마음 내키면 언제든 벗어 던질 수 있다고 느꼈기 때문이다."], ["Two methods of self-destruction were at his disposal.", "스스로 목숨을 끊는 두 가지 방법이 그에게 있었다."], ["He could hang himself with his handkerchief to the window bars, or refuse food and die of starvation.", "손수건으로 목을 매어 창살에 매달릴 수도 있었고, 음식을 거부해 굶어 죽을 수도 있었다."], ["But the first was repugnant to him.", "그러나 첫 번째 방법은 그에게 역겨웠다."], ["Dantès had always entertained the greatest horror of pirates, who are hung up to the yard-arm; he would not die by what seemed an infamous death.", "당테스는 활대에 매달려 죽는 해적들에게 늘 더없는 혐오를 품어 왔다. 그는 치욕스러워 보이는 그런 죽음으로는 죽지 않으리라 했다."], ["He resolved to adopt the second, and began that day to carry out his resolve. 0185m", "그는 두 번째 방법을 택하기로 마음먹고, 그날부터 결심을 실행에 옮기기 시작했다."]], [["Nearly four years had passed away; at the end of the second he had ceased to mark the lapse of time.", "거의 사 년이 흘렀다. 둘째 해가 끝날 무렵 그는 세월의 흐름을 헤아리기를 그만두었다."], ["Dantès said, “I wish to die,” and had chosen the manner of his death, and fearful of changing his mind, he had taken an oath to die.", "당테스는 \"죽고 싶다\"라고 말했고, 죽음의 방식을 골랐으며, 마음이 바뀔까 두려워 죽겠노라 맹세까지 했다."], ["“When my morning and evening meals are brought,”", "'아침과 저녁 끼니가 들어오면,'"], ["thought he, “I will cast them out of the window, and they will think that I have eaten them.”", "그는 생각했다. '창밖으로 던져 버리리라. 그러면 저들은 내가 먹은 줄 알겠지.'"]], [["He kept his word; twice a day he cast out, through the barred aperture, the provisions his jailer brought him—at first gayly, then with deliberation, and at last with regret.", "그는 약속을 지켰다. 하루에 두 번, 그는 간수가 가져온 음식을 창살 사이로 던져 버렸다. 처음에는 즐거이, 다음에는 신중하게, 마침내는 미련을 품은 채로."], ["Nothing but the recollection of his oath gave him strength to proceed.", "오로지 맹세에 대한 기억만이 그에게 계속할 힘을 주었다."], ["Hunger made viands once repugnant, now acceptable; he held the plate in his hand for an hour at a time, and gazed thoughtfully at the morsel of bad meat, of tainted fish, of black and mouldy bread.", "굶주림은 한때 역겹던 음식을 이제 군침 도는 것으로 만들었다. 그는 한 시간이고 접시를 손에 든 채, 질 나쁜 고기 한 점, 상한 생선, 검고 곰팡이 핀 빵 조각을 골똘히 바라보았다."], ["It was the last yearning for life contending with the resolution of despair; then his dungeon seemed less sombre, his prospects less desperate.", "그것은 삶에 대한 마지막 갈망이 절망의 결심과 다투는 것이었다. 그럴 때면 지하 감방도 덜 음침해 보였고, 앞날도 덜 절망스러워 보였다."], ["He was still young—he was only four or five-and-twenty—he had nearly fifty years to live.", "그는 아직 젊었다. 고작 스물네다섯이었고, 살날이 오십 년 가까이 남아 있었다."], ["What unforseen events might not open his prison door, and restore him to liberty?", "어떤 뜻밖의 일이 일어나 감방 문을 열고 그를 다시 자유의 몸으로 돌려보내 줄지도 모를 일이었다."], ["Then he raised to his lips the repast that, like a voluntary Tantalus, he refused himself; but he thought of his oath, and he would not break it.", "그러면 그는, 스스로를 가둔 탄탈로스처럼 제게 금했던 음식을 입가로 가져갔다. 그러나 맹세를 떠올리고는 그것을 깨려 하지 않았다."], ["He persisted until, at last, he had not sufficient strength to rise and cast his supper out of the loophole.", "그는 버티고 또 버텼고, 마침내는 일어나 저녁밥을 창살 틈으로 내던질 힘조차 남지 않았다. 이튿날 아침 그는 보지도 듣지도 못했다."], ["The next morning he could not see or hear; the jailer feared he was dangerously ill. Edmond hoped he was dying.", "간수는 그가 위독한 줄 알고 겁을 냈다. 에드몽은 자기가 죽어 가는 것이기를 바랐다."]], [["Thus the day passed away.", "그렇게 하루가 흘렀다."], ["Edmond felt a sort of stupor creeping over him which brought with it a feeling almost of content; the gnawing pain at his stomach had ceased; his thirst had abated; when he closed his eyes he saw myriads of lights dancing before them like the will-o’-the-wisps that play about the marshes.", "에드몽은 일종의 혼미한 기운이 자신을 덮쳐 오는 것을 느꼈고, 그것은 거의 만족에 가까운 느낌을 함께 가져왔다. 속을 쥐어뜯던 통증은 멎었고, 갈증도 누그러졌다. 눈을 감으면 수많은 불빛이 눈앞에서 춤추는 것이 보였는데, 늪 위에서 노니는 도깨비불 같았다."], ["It was the twilight of that mysterious country called Death!", "그것은 죽음이라 불리는 신비로운 나라의 여명이었다!"]], [["Suddenly, about nine o’clock in the evening, Edmond heard a hollow sound in the wall against which he was lying.", "그러던 어느 날 저녁 아홉 시쯤, 에드몽은 자신이 기대 누운 벽 안쪽에서 공허한 소리를 들었다."]], [["So many loathsome animals inhabited the prison, that their noise did not, in general, awake him; but whether abstinence had quickened his faculties, or whether the noise was really louder than usual, Edmond raised his head and listened.", "워낙 많은 흉측한 짐승들이 감옥에 깃들어 살아서, 그놈들의 소리는 대개 그를 깨우지 못했다. 그러나 굶주림이 그의 감각을 예민하게 했는지, 아니면 정말로 그 소리가 여느 때보다 컸는지, 에드몽은 고개를 들고 귀를 기울였다."], ["It was a continual scratching, as if made by a huge claw, a powerful tooth, or some iron instrument attacking the stones.", "그것은 끊임없이 긁는 소리였다. 마치 거대한 발톱이나 강력한 이빨, 혹은 어떤 쇠붙이 연장이 돌을 파고드는 것 같았다."]], [["Although weakened, the young man’s brain instantly responded to the idea that haunts all prisoners—liberty!", "쇠약해진 몸이었지만, 젊은이의 머릿속에는 모든 죄수를 따라다니는 그 생각이 곧바로 떠올랐다. 자유!"], ["It seemed to him that heaven had at length taken pity on him, and had sent this noise to warn him on the very brink of the abyss.", "마침내 하늘이 자기를 가엾이 여겨, 바로 심연의 벼랑 끝에서 그를 일깨우려고 이 소리를 보낸 것만 같았다."], ["Perhaps one of those beloved ones he had so often thought of was thinking of him, and striving to diminish the distance that separated them.", "어쩌면 그가 그토록 자주 떠올리던 사랑하는 이들 가운데 누군가가 그를 생각하며, 둘 사이를 가르는 거리를 좁히려 애쓰고 있는지도 몰랐다."]], [["No, no, doubtless he was deceived, and it was but one of those dreams that forerun death!", "아니, 아니, 분명 착각일 것이다. 그저 죽음에 앞서 찾아오는 그런 꿈 중 하나일 뿐이리라!"]], [["Edmond still heard the sound.", "에드몽은 여전히 그 소리를 들었다."], ["It lasted nearly three hours; he then heard a noise of something falling, and all was silent.", "소리는 거의 세 시간을 이어졌다. 그러다 그는 무언가 떨어지는 소리를 들었고, 모든 것이 고요해졌다."]], [["Some hours afterwards it began again, nearer and more distinct.", "몇 시간 뒤 소리가 다시 시작되었다. 더 가깝고 더 또렷했다."], ["Edmond was intensely interested.", "에드몽은 온 신경을 곤두세웠다."], ["Suddenly the jailer entered.", "갑자기 간수가 들어왔다."]], [["For a week since he had resolved to die, and during the four days that he had been carrying out his purpose, Edmond had not spoken to the attendant, had not answered him when he inquired what was the matter with him, and turned his face to the wall when he looked too curiously at him; but now the jailer might hear the noise and put an end to it, and so destroy a ray of something like hope that soothed his last moments.", "죽기로 결심한 일주일 동안, 그리고 그 결심을 실행에 옮긴 나흘 동안, 에드몽은 간수에게 말을 걸지 않았고, 무슨 일이냐고 물어도 대답하지 않았으며, 간수가 너무 호기심 어린 눈으로 들여다보면 얼굴을 벽 쪽으로 돌렸다. 그러나 이제 간수가 그 소리를 듣고 멈추게 하기라도 하면, 그의 마지막 순간을 달래 주던 그 희망 비슷한 한 줄기 빛마저 사라질 판이었다."]], [["The jailer brought him his breakfast.", "간수가 그에게 아침을 가져왔다."], ["Dantès raised himself up and began to talk about everything; about the bad quality of the food, about the coldness of his dungeon, grumbling and complaining, in order to have an excuse for speaking louder, and wearying the patience of his jailer, who out of kindness of heart had brought broth and white bread for his prisoner.", "당테스는 몸을 일으켜 이것저것 떠들기 시작했다. 음식의 질이 나쁘다느니, 감방이 춥다느니, 투덜대고 불평하며 더 크게 말할 핑계를 만들고, 간수의 인내심을 시험했다. 간수는 마음씨가 고운 사람이라 죄수를 위해 수프와 흰 빵을 가져온 터였다."]], [["Fortunately, he fancied that Dantès was delirious; and placing the food on the rickety table, he withdrew.", "다행히도 간수는 당테스가 헛소리를 하는 줄로만 알았다. 그는 음식을 흔들거리는 탁자에 놓고 물러갔다."], ["Edmond listened, and the sound became more and more distinct.", "에드몽은 귀를 기울였고, 소리는 점점 더 또렷해졌다."]], [["“There can be no doubt about it,”", "'틀림없어.' 그는 생각했다."], ["thought he; “it is some prisoner who is striving to obtain his freedom. Oh, if I were only there to help him!”", "'자유를 찾으려고 애쓰는 어떤 죄수야. 아, 내가 거기 있어 도울 수만 있다면!'"]], [["Suddenly another idea took possession of his mind, so used to misfortune, that it was scarcely capable of hope—the idea that the noise was made by workmen the governor had ordered to repair the neighboring dungeon.", "그러다 또 다른 생각이 그의 마음을 사로잡았다. 불행에 너무 익숙해져 좀처럼 희망을 품지 못하는 그의 마음을 말이다. 소장이 이웃 감방을 수리하라고 시킨 일꾼들이 내는 소리라는 생각이었다."]], [["It was easy to ascertain this; but how could he risk the question?", "이를 확인하기는 쉬웠다. 그러나 어떻게 그 위험을 무릅쓰고 물어본단 말인가?"], ["It was easy to call his jailer’s attention to the noise, and watch his countenance as he listened; but might he not by this means destroy hopes far more important than the short-lived satisfaction of his own curiosity?", "간수에게 그 소리에 주의를 돌리게 하고, 그가 귀 기울이는 동안 표정을 살피기란 쉬운 일이었다. 그러나 그렇게 하다가는 한순간의 호기심을 채우는 보잘것없는 만족보다 훨씬 더 중요한 희망을 무너뜨릴 수도 있지 않은가?"], ["Unfortunately, Edmond’s brain was still so feeble that he could not bend his thoughts to anything in particular.", "안타깝게도 에드몽의 머리는 여전히 너무 흐릿해서 무엇 하나에도 생각을 집중할 수 없었다."], ["He saw but one means of restoring lucidity and clearness to his judgment.", "그는 판단력에 다시 또렷함과 명료함을 되찾을 방법이 단 하나뿐임을 알았다."], ["He turned his eyes towards the soup which the jailer had brought, rose, staggered towards it, raised the vessel to his lips, and drank off the contents with a feeling of indescribable pleasure.", "그는 간수가 가져온 수프 쪽으로 눈길을 돌렸고, 일어나 비틀비틀 그쪽으로 다가가, 그릇을 입가로 들어 올려 형언할 수 없는 기쁨을 느끼며 내용물을 들이켰다."]], [["He had the resolution to stop with this.", "그는 거기서 멈추는 결단력을 발휘했다."], ["He had often heard that shipwrecked persons had died through having eagerly devoured too much food.", "그는 난파당한 사람들이 음식을 게걸스레 너무 많이 먹다가 죽었다는 이야기를 자주 들어 왔다."], ["Edmond replaced on the table the bread he was about to devour, and returned to his couch—he did not wish to die.", "에드몽은 막 삼키려던 빵을 다시 탁자에 내려놓고 잠자리로 돌아왔다. 그는 죽고 싶지 않았다."], ["He soon felt that his ideas became again collected—he could think, and strengthen his thoughts by reasoning.", "곧 그는 생각이 다시 가다듬어지는 것을 느꼈다. 이제 사고할 수 있었고, 추론으로 생각에 힘을 실을 수 있었다."], ["Then he said to himself:", "그러고는 스스로에게 말했다."]], [["“I must put this to the test, but without compromising anybody.", "\"이걸 시험해 봐야 해. 하지만 누구도 위험에 빠뜨리지 않으면서."], ["If it is a workman, I need but knock against the wall, and he will cease to work, in order to find out who is knocking, and why he does so; but as his occupation is sanctioned by the governor, he will soon resume it.", "일꾼이라면 벽을 두드리기만 하면 돼. 그러면 누가 왜 두드리는지 알아보려고 일을 멈추겠지. 하지만 그 일은 소장이 허락한 것이니 곧 다시 시작할 거야."], ["If, on the contrary, it is a prisoner, the noise I make will alarm him, he will cease, and not begin again until he thinks everyone is asleep.”", "반대로 죄수라면, 내가 내는 소리에 놀라 멈출 테고, 다들 잠들었다고 여겨질 때까지 다시 시작하지 않겠지.\""]], [["Edmond rose again, but this time his legs did not tremble, and his sight was clear; he went to a corner of his dungeon, detached a stone, and with it knocked against the wall where the sound came.", "에드몽은 다시 일어났다. 그러나 이번에는 다리가 떨리지 않았고 시야도 또렷했다. 그는 감방 구석으로 가서 돌 하나를 떼어 냈고, 그것으로 소리가 나는 쪽 벽을 두드렸다."], ["He struck thrice.", "세 번 두드렸다."]], [["At the first blow the sound ceased, as if by magic.", "첫 번째 타격에 소리가 마치 마법처럼 멎었다."]], [["Edmond listened intently; an hour passed, two hours passed, and no sound was heard from the wall—all was silent there.", "에드몽은 온 신경을 곤두세워 귀를 기울였다. 한 시간이 흘렀고, 두 시간이 흘렀으나, 벽에서는 아무 소리도 들리지 않았다. 그곳은 온통 고요했다."]], [["Full of hope, Edmond swallowed a few mouthfuls of bread and water, and, thanks to the vigor of his constitution, found himself well-nigh recovered.", "희망에 부푼 에드몽은 빵과 물을 몇 입 삼켰고, 타고난 튼튼한 체질 덕분에 거의 회복된 자신을 느꼈다."]], [["The day passed away in utter silence—night came without recurrence of the noise.", "그날 하루는 완전한 침묵 속에 지나갔고, 밤이 와도 그 소리는 다시 들리지 않았다."]], [["“It is a prisoner,” said Edmond joyfully.", "\"죄수야.\" 에드몽이 기쁨에 차서 말했다."], ["His brain was on fire, and life and energy returned.", "그의 머리는 불타올랐고, 생기와 기운이 돌아왔다."]], [["The night passed in perfect silence.", "밤은 완벽한 고요 속에 지나갔다."], ["Edmond did not close his eyes.", "에드몽은 눈을 붙이지 않았다."]], [["In the morning the jailer brought him fresh provisions—he had already devoured those of the previous day; he ate these listening anxiously for the sound, walking round and round his cell, shaking the iron bars of the loophole, restoring vigor and agility to his limbs by exercise, and so preparing himself for his future destiny.", "아침에 간수가 새 음식을 가져왔다. 그는 전날 것을 이미 게걸스레 먹어 치운 뒤였다. 그는 음식을 먹으면서 그 소리가 다시 나는지 애타게 귀를 기울였고, 감방을 빙빙 돌았으며, 창살을 흔들고, 운동으로 팔다리에 다시 힘과 민첩함을 불어넣어 앞으로의 운명에 대비했다."], ["At intervals he listened to learn if the noise had not begun again, and grew impatient at the prudence of the prisoner, who did not guess he had been disturbed by a captive as anxious for liberty as himself.", "틈틈이 그는 소리가 다시 시작되지 않았는지 귀를 기울였고, 자기 못지않게 자유를 갈망하는 죄수가 자기를 방해한 것임을 알아채지 못한 채 너무도 신중하기만 한 그에게 조바심을 냈다."]], [["Three days passed—seventy-two long tedious hours which he counted off by minutes!", "사흘이 흘렀다. 분 단위로 헤아린 일흔두 시간, 길고 지루한 시간이었다!"]], [["At length one evening, as the jailer was visiting him for the last time that night, Dantès, with his ear for the hundredth time at the wall, fancied he heard an almost imperceptible movement among the stones.", "마침내 어느 날 저녁, 간수가 그날 밤의 마지막 순회를 도는 참에, 당테스는 백 번째로 벽에 귀를 댄 채 돌들 사이에서 거의 알아챌 수 없는 움직임을 들은 것 같았다."], ["He moved away, walked up and down his cell to collect his thoughts, and then went back and listened.", "그는 자리를 옮겨 감방을 오르락내리락하며 생각을 가다듬었고, 그런 뒤 다시 돌아와 귀를 기울였다."]], [["The matter was no longer doubtful.", "이제는 의심의 여지가 없었다."], ["Something was at work on the other side of the wall; the prisoner had discovered the danger, and had substituted a lever for a chisel.", "벽 저편에서 무언가가 작업 중이었다. 죄수는 위험을 알아채고는 끌 대신 지렛대를 쓰고 있었다."]], [["Encouraged by this discovery, Edmond determined to assist the indefatigable laborer.", "이 발견에 용기를 얻은 에드몽은 지칠 줄 모르는 그 일꾼을 돕기로 마음먹었다."], ["He began by moving his bed, and looked around for anything with which he could pierce the wall, penetrate the moist cement, and displace a stone.", "그는 우선 침대를 옮기고, 벽을 뚫고 축축한 회반죽을 파고들어 돌 하나를 빼낼 만한 것이 없는지 둘러보았다."]], [["He saw nothing, he had no knife or sharp instrument, the window grating was of iron, but he had too often assured himself of its solidity.", "아무것도 눈에 띄지 않았다. 칼도 날카로운 연장도 없었고, 창살은 쇠로 되어 있었지만 그 단단함은 이미 너무도 자주 확인한 터였다."], ["All his furniture consisted of a bed, a chair, a table, a pail, and a jug.", "그의 가구라곤 침대, 의자, 탁자, 양동이, 물주전자가 전부였다."], ["The bed had iron clamps, but they were screwed to the wood, and it would have required a screw-driver to take them off.", "침대에는 쇠 죔쇠가 달려 있었으나 나무에 나사로 박혀 있어, 그것을 떼어 내려면 드라이버가 필요했다."], ["The table and chair had nothing, the pail had once possessed a handle, but that had been removed. 0189m", "탁자와 의자에는 아무것도 없었고, 양동이에는 한때 손잡이가 있었지만 그것마저 떼어 낸 뒤였다."]], [["Dantès had but one resource, which was to break the jug, and with one of the sharp fragments attack the wall.", "당테스에게 남은 방도는 단 하나, 물주전자를 깨뜨려 그 날카로운 조각 하나로 벽을 공략하는 것이었다."], ["He let the jug fall on the floor, and it broke in pieces.", "그는 물주전자를 바닥에 떨어뜨렸고, 주전자는 산산조각이 났다."]], [["Dantès concealed two or three of the sharpest fragments in his bed, leaving the rest on the floor.", "당테스는 가장 날카로운 조각 두세 개를 침대에 감추고 나머지는 바닥에 그대로 두었다."], ["The breaking of his jug was too natural an accident to excite suspicion.", "물주전자가 깨진 것은 너무도 자연스러운 사고라 의심을 사지 않을 터였다."], ["Edmond had all the night to work in, but in the darkness he could not do much, and he soon felt that he was working against something very hard; he pushed back his bed, and waited for day.", "에드몽에게는 밤새 일할 시간이 있었지만, 어둠 속에서는 별로 손을 쓰지 못했고, 곧 무언가 아주 단단한 것에 부딪혀 일하고 있음을 느꼈다. 그는 침대를 도로 밀어 놓고 날이 밝기를 기다렸다."]], [["All night he heard the subterranean workman, who continued to mine his way.", "밤새 그는 땅속의 일꾼이 굴을 파 나가는 소리를 들었다."], ["Day came, the jailer entered.", "날이 밝자 간수가 들어왔다."], ["Dantès told him that the jug had fallen from his hands while he was drinking, and the jailer went grumblingly to fetch another, without giving himself the trouble to remove the fragments of the broken one.", "당테스는 물을 마시다 주전자를 손에서 떨어뜨렸다고 둘러댔고, 간수는 투덜대며 새것을 가지러 갔다. 깨진 조각들을 치우는 수고는 하지 않은 채였다."], ["He returned speedily, advised the prisoner to be more careful, and departed.", "그는 금세 돌아와 죄수에게 더 조심하라고 이르고는 떠났다."]], [["Dantès heard joyfully the key grate in the lock; he listened until the sound of steps died away, and then, hastily displacing his bed, saw by the faint light that penetrated into his cell, that he had labored uselessly the previous evening in attacking the stone instead of removing the plaster that surrounded it.", "당테스는 자물쇠에서 열쇠가 돌아가는 소리를 기쁘게 들었다. 그는 발소리가 멀어져 사라질 때까지 귀를 기울였고, 그런 뒤 서둘러 침대를 옮겨, 감방으로 스며드는 희미한 빛에 의지해 깨달았다. 전날 저녁 자신이 돌을 에워싼 회반죽을 걷어 내는 대신 돌 자체를 공략하느라 헛수고를 했다는 것을."]], [["The damp had rendered it friable, and Dantès was able to break it off—in small morsels, it is true, but at the end of half an hour he had scraped off a handful; a mathematician might have calculated that in two years, supposing that the rock was not encountered, a passage twenty feet long and two feet broad, might be formed.", "습기 탓에 회반죽은 푸석푸석해져 있었고, 당테스는 그것을 부숴 낼 수 있었다. 비록 작은 조각으로였지만, 반 시간 만에 그는 한 줌을 긁어냈다. 수학자라면, 만일 암반에 부딪히지 않는다고 가정할 때, 이 년이면 길이 스무 자에 너비 두 자짜리 통로를 낼 수 있으리라 계산했을 것이다."]], [["The prisoner reproached himself with not having thus employed the hours he had passed in vain hopes, prayer, and despondency.", "죄수는 부질없는 희망과 기도와 낙담으로 흘려보낸 시간을 이렇게 쓰지 못한 것을 자책했다."], ["During the six years that he had been imprisoned, what might he not have accomplished?", "갇혀 지낸 육 년 동안, 그는 무엇인들 이루지 못했겠는가?"]], [["This idea imparted new energy, and in three days he had succeeded, with the utmost precaution, in removing the cement, and exposing the stone-work.", "이 생각이 그에게 새로운 기운을 불어넣었고, 사흘 만에 그는 더없이 신중하게 회반죽을 걷어 내 돌 쌓은 부분을 드러내는 데 성공했다."], ["The wall was built of rough stones, among which, to give strength to the structure, blocks of hewn stone were at intervals imbedded.", "벽은 거친 돌로 쌓여 있었고, 그 사이사이에 구조를 튼튼히 하려고 다듬은 돌덩이들이 박혀 있었다."], ["It was one of these he had uncovered, and which he must remove from its socket.", "그가 드러낸 것은 그 돌덩이 가운데 하나였고, 그것을 박힌 자리에서 빼내야 했다."]], [["Dantès strove to do this with his nails, but they were too weak.", "당테스는 손톱으로 그 일을 하려 했으나, 손톱은 너무 약했다."], ["The fragments of the jug broke, and after an hour of useless toil, Dantès paused with anguish on his brow.", "주전자 조각들은 부러졌고, 한 시간의 헛된 노고 끝에 당테스는 이마에 괴로움을 띤 채 손을 멈췄다."]], [["Was he to be thus stopped at the beginning, and was he to wait inactive until his fellow workman had completed his task?", "이렇게 첫걸음에서 가로막혀, 동료 일꾼이 제 일을 끝낼 때까지 손 놓고 기다려야 한단 말인가?"], ["Suddenly an idea occurred to him—he smiled, and the perspiration dried on his forehead.", "갑자기 한 가지 생각이 떠올랐다. 그는 미소를 지었고, 이마의 땀이 말라 갔다."]], [["The jailer always brought Dantès’ soup in an iron saucepan; this saucepan contained soup for both prisoners, for Dantès had noticed that it was either quite full, or half empty, according as the turnkey gave it to him or to his companion first.", "간수는 늘 쇠로 된 냄비에 당테스의 수프를 담아 왔다. 이 냄비에는 두 죄수 몫의 수프가 들어 있었으니, 당테스는 간수가 자기에게 먼저 주느냐 동무에게 먼저 주느냐에 따라 냄비가 가득 차 있거나 절반쯤 비어 있는 것을 눈여겨봐 둔 터였다."]], [["The handle of this saucepan was of iron; Dantès would have given ten years of his life in exchange for it.", "이 냄비의 손잡이는 쇠로 되어 있었다. 당테스라면 그것과 맞바꾸기 위해 제 목숨의 십 년이라도 내놓았으리라."]], [["0191m\nThe jailer was accustomed to pour the contents of the saucepan into Dantès’ plate, and Dantès, after eating his soup with a wooden spoon, washed the plate, which thus served for every day.", "간수는 냄비의 내용물을 당테스의 접시에 따라 주곤 했고, 당테스는 나무 숟가락으로 수프를 먹은 뒤 접시를 씻어 날마다 같은 접시를 썼다."], ["Now when evening came Dantès put his plate on the ground near the door; the jailer, as he entered, stepped on it and broke it.", "그런데 저녁이 되자 당테스는 접시를 문 가까이 바닥에 놓았다. 간수는 들어오다 그것을 밟아 깨뜨렸다."]], [["This time he could not blame Dantès.", "이번에는 간수도 당테스를 탓할 수 없었다."], ["He was wrong to leave it there, but the jailer was wrong not to have looked before him.", "거기에 접시를 둔 것은 당테스의 잘못이었으나, 앞을 살피지 않은 것은 간수의 잘못이었다."], ["The jailer, therefore, only grumbled.", "그래서 간수는 그저 투덜대기만 했다."], ["Then he looked about for something to pour the soup into; Dantès’ entire dinner service consisted of one plate—there was no alternative.", "그러고는 수프를 따를 만한 것을 찾아 두리번거렸다. 당테스의 식기라곤 접시 하나가 전부였으니, 달리 방법이 없었다."]], [["“Leave the saucepan,”", "\"냄비를 두고 가시오.\""], ["said Dantès; “you can take it away when you bring me my breakfast.”", "당테스가 말했다. \"아침을 가져올 때 도로 가져가면 되잖소.\""]], [["This advice was to the jailer’s taste, as it spared him the necessity of making another trip.", "이 충고는 간수의 마음에 들었다. 다시 한 번 걸음할 수고를 덜어 주었기 때문이다."], ["He left the saucepan.", "그는 냄비를 두고 갔다."]], [["Dantès was beside himself with joy.", "당테스는 기쁨에 어쩔 줄 몰랐다."], ["He rapidly devoured his food, and after waiting an hour, lest the jailer should change his mind and return, he removed his bed, took the handle of the saucepan, inserted the point between the hewn stone and rough stones of the wall, and employed it as a lever.", "그는 음식을 후딱 먹어 치웠고, 간수가 마음을 바꿔 되돌아올까 봐 한 시간을 기다린 뒤, 침대를 치우고 냄비 손잡이를 집어 들었다. 그는 그 끝을 다듬은 돌과 거친 돌 사이에 끼워 넣고 지렛대로 썼다."], ["A slight oscillation showed Dantès that all went well. At the end of an hour the stone was extricated from the wall, leaving a cavity a foot and a half in diameter.", "약간 흔들리는 것을 보고 당테스는 일이 잘되어 가고 있음을 알았다. 한 시간 만에 돌이 벽에서 빠져나왔고, 지름 한 자 반쯤 되는 구멍이 생겼다."]], [["Dantès carefully collected the plaster, carried it into the corner of his cell, and covered it with earth.", "당테스는 회반죽을 조심스레 그러모아 감방 구석으로 날라다 흙으로 덮었다."], ["Then, wishing to make the best use of his time while he had the means of labor, he continued to work without ceasing.", "그러고는 일할 수단이 있는 동안 시간을 한껏 쓰고 싶어, 쉬지 않고 작업을 이어 갔다."], ["At the dawn of day he replaced the stone, pushed his bed against the wall, and lay down.", "동틀 무렵 그는 돌을 도로 끼워 넣고, 침대를 벽에 밀어붙인 뒤 누웠다."], ["The breakfast consisted of a piece of bread; the jailer entered and placed the bread on the table.", "아침은 빵 한 조각이었다. 간수가 들어와 빵을 탁자에 놓았다."]], [["“Well, don’t you intend to bring me another plate?” said Dantès.", "\"이런, 접시를 새로 가져다줄 생각은 없소?\" 당테스가 물었다."]], [["“No,” replied the turnkey; “you destroy everything.", "\"없소.\" 간수가 대답했다."], ["First you break your jug, then you make me break your plate; if all the prisoners followed your example, the government would be ruined.", "\"당신은 죄다 부숴 버리잖소. 처음엔 주전자를 깨더니, 이번엔 나더러 접시를 깨게 만들고. 죄수들이 죄다 당신처럼 굴면 나라가 거덜 나겠소."], ["I shall leave you the saucepan, and pour your soup into that.", "냄비를 두고 갈 테니 거기다 수프를 따라 먹으시오."], ["So for the future I hope you will not be so destructive.”", "앞으로는 그렇게 살림을 거덜 내지 않기를 바라오.\""]], [["Dantès raised his eyes to heaven and clasped his hands beneath the coverlet.", "당테스는 하늘로 눈을 들었고, 이불 밑에서 두 손을 맞잡았다."], ["He felt more gratitude for the possession of this piece of iron than he had ever felt for anything.", "그는 이 쇳조각 하나를 손에 넣은 것에, 여태껏 그 무엇에도 느껴 본 적 없는 깊은 감사를 느꼈다."], ["He had noticed, however, that the prisoner on the other side had ceased to labor; no matter, this was a greater reason for proceeding—if his neighbor would not come to him, he would go to his neighbor.", "그러나 그는 벽 저편의 죄수가 작업을 멈췄음을 알아챈 터였다. 그래도 상관없었다. 오히려 그것은 계속해야 할 더 큰 이유였다."], ["All day he toiled on untiringly, and by the evening he had succeeded in extracting ten handfuls of plaster and fragments of stone.", "이웃이 그에게 오지 않는다면, 그가 이웃에게 가면 될 일이었다. 그는 온종일 지칠 줄 모르고 일했고, 저녁이 되자 회반죽과 돌 부스러기를 열 줌이나 긁어내는 데 성공했다."], ["When the hour for his jailer’s visit arrived, Dantès straightened the handle of the saucepan as well as he could, and placed it in its accustomed place.", "간수가 들를 시간이 되자, 당테스는 냄비 손잡이를 할 수 있는 한 곧게 펴서 제자리에 두었다. 간수는 거기에 수프를 따라 주었고, 생선도 곁들였다."], ["The turnkey poured his ration of soup into it, together with the fish—for thrice a week the prisoners were deprived of meat. This would have been a method of reckoning time, had not Dantès long ceased to do so.", "일주일에 세 번은 죄수들에게 고기가 주어지지 않았기 때문이다. 이것은 시간을 헤아리는 방법이 될 수도 있었으나, 당테스는 이미 오래전에 그러기를 그만둔 터였다."], ["Having poured out the soup, the turnkey retired.", "수프를 따라 준 뒤 간수는 물러갔다."]], [["Dantès wished to ascertain whether his neighbor had really ceased to work.", "당테스는 이웃이 정말로 작업을 그만두었는지 확인하고 싶었다."], ["He listened—all was silent, as it had been for the last three days.", "그는 귀를 기울였다. 지난 사흘 내내 그랬듯이 모든 것이 고요했다."], ["Dantès sighed; it was evident that his neighbor distrusted him.", "당테스는 한숨을 내쉬었다. 이웃이 자기를 믿지 못하는 것이 분명했다."], ["However, he toiled on all the night without being discouraged; but after two or three hours he encountered an obstacle.", "그래도 그는 낙담하지 않고 밤새 일을 이어 갔다. 그러나 두세 시간 뒤 장애물에 부딪혔다."], ["The iron made no impression, but met with a smooth surface; Dantès touched it, and found that it was a beam.", "쇠가 아무런 자국도 내지 못한 채 매끈한 표면에 막혔다. 당테스가 만져 보니, 그것은 들보였다."], ["This beam crossed, or rather blocked up, the hole Dantès had made; it was necessary, therefore, to dig above or under it.", "이 들보가 당테스가 뚫어 놓은 구멍을 가로질러, 아니 가로막고 있었다. 그러니 그 위나 아래를 파야 했다."], ["The unhappy young man had not thought of this.", "가엾은 젊은이는 이런 것까지는 생각하지 못했다."]], [["“Oh, my God, my God!”", "\"오, 하느님, 하느님!\""], ["murmured he, “I have so earnestly prayed to you, that I hoped my prayers had been heard.", "그가 중얼거렸다. \"이토록 간절히 당신께 기도드렸으니, 제 기도가 닿았으리라 믿었습니다."], ["After having deprived me of my liberty, after having deprived me of death, after having recalled me to existence, my God, have pity on me, and do not let me die in despair!” 0193m", "제게서 자유를 앗아 가시고, 죽음마저 앗아 가시고, 다시 삶으로 불러내셨으니, 하느님, 저를 가엾이 여기시어 절망 속에 죽게 내버려 두지 마소서!\""]], [["“Who talks of God and despair at the same time?”", "\"누가 하느님과 절망을 한꺼번에 입에 올리는가?\""], ["said a voice that seemed to come from beneath the earth, and, deadened by the distance, sounded hollow and sepulchral in the young man’s ears.", "땅속에서 들려오는 듯한 목소리가 말했다. 거리에 짓눌린 그 소리는 젊은이의 귀에 공허하고 무덤 같은 울림으로 닿았다."], ["Edmond’s hair stood on end, and he rose to his knees.", "에드몽은 머리카락이 곤두섰고, 무릎을 꿇고 몸을 일으켰다."]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아.\""], ["said he, “I hear a human voice.”", "그가 말했다. \"사람의 목소리가 들린다.\""], ["Edmond had not heard anyone speak save his jailer for four or five years; and a jailer is no man to a prisoner—he is a living door, a barrier of flesh and blood adding strength to restraints of oak and iron.", "에드몽은 사오 년 동안 간수 말고는 누구의 말도 들어 본 적이 없었다. 그리고 간수란 죄수에게 사람이 아니다. 그는 살아 있는 문이요, 참나무와 쇠의 구속에 힘을 더하는 살과 피의 장벽일 뿐이다."]], [["“In the name of Heaven,”", "\"하늘에 맹세코,\""], ["cried Dantès, “speak again, though the sound of your voice terrifies me.", "당테스가 외쳤다. \"다시 말해 주시오. 당신 목소리가 나를 떨게 할지언정."], ["Who are you?”", "당신은 누구요?\""]], [["“Who are you?”", "\"당신은 누구요?\""], ["said the voice.", "목소리가 물었다."]], [["“An unhappy prisoner,”", "\"불행한 죄수요.\""], ["replied Dantès, who made no hesitation in answering.", "당테스가 망설임 없이 대답했다."]], [["“Of what country?”", "\"어느 나라 사람이오?\""]], [["“A Frenchman.”", "\"프랑스인이오.\""]], [["“Your name?”", "\"이름은?\""]], [["“Edmond Dantès.”", "\"에드몽 당테스요.\""]], [["“Your profession?”", "\"직업은?\""]], [["“A sailor.”", "\"뱃사람이오.\""]], [["“How long have you been here?”", "\"여기 온 지는 얼마나 됐소?\""]], [["“Since the 28th of February, 1815.”", "\"1815년 2월 28일부터요.\""]], [["“Your crime?”", "\"죄목은?\""]], [["“I am innocent.”", "\"나는 무죄요.\""]], [["“But of what are you accused?”", "\"하지만 무슨 죄로 고발당했소?\""]], [["“Of having conspired to aid the emperor’s return.”", "\"황제의 귀환을 도우려고 음모를 꾸몄다는 죄로요.\""]], [["“What!", "\"뭐라고!"], ["For the emperor’s return?—the emperor is no longer on the throne, then?”", "황제의 귀환을? 그럼 황제는 이제 왕좌에 계시지 않단 말이오?\""]], [["“He abdicated at Fontainebleau in 1814, and was sent to the Island of Elba.", "\"1814년 퐁텐블로에서 퇴위하시고 엘바섬으로 보내지셨소."], ["But how long have you been here that you are ignorant of all this?”", "한데 당신은 여기 온 지 얼마나 됐기에 이 모든 걸 모른단 말이오?\""]], [["“Since 1811.”", "\"1811년부터요.\""]], [["Dantès shuddered; this man had been four years longer than himself in prison.", "당테스는 몸서리쳤다. 이 사람은 자기보다 사 년이나 더 오래 갇혀 있었던 것이다."]], [["“Do not dig any more,”", "\"더는 파지 마시오.\""], ["said the voice; “only tell me how high up is your excavation?”", "목소리가 말했다. \"당신이 판 굴이 얼마나 높은 데 있는지만 말해 주시오.\""]], [["“On a level with the floor.”", "\"바닥과 같은 높이요.\""]], [["“How is it concealed?”", "\"어떻게 감춰 놨소?\""]], [["“Behind my bed.”", "\"침대 뒤에요.\""]], [["“Has your bed been moved since you have been a prisoner?”", "\"갇힌 뒤로 침대를 옮긴 적이 있소?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"없소.\""]], [["“What does your chamber open on?”", "\"당신 방은 어디로 통하오?\""]], [["“A corridor.”", "\"복도로요.\""]], [["“And the corridor?”", "\"복도는?\""]], [["“On a court.”", "\"안뜰로요.\""]], [["“Alas!”", "\"아아!\""], ["murmured the voice.", "목소리가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Oh, what is the matter?”", "\"오, 무슨 일이오?\""], ["cried Dantès.", "당테스가 외쳤다."]], [["“I have made a mistake owing to an error in my plans.", "\"설계가 틀려서 실수를 했소."], ["I took the wrong angle, and have come out fifteen feet from where I intended.", "각도를 잘못 잡아, 의도한 곳에서 열다섯 자나 빗나가 나왔구려."], ["I took the wall you are mining for the outer wall of the fortress.”", "당신이 파던 그 벽을 요새의 바깥벽으로 여겼던 거요.\""]], [["“But then you would be close to the sea?”", "\"하지만 그렇다면 당신은 바다 가까이에 있는 거 아니오?\""]], [["“That is what I hoped.”", "\"그게 내가 바라던 바였소.\""]], [["“And supposing you had succeeded?”", "\"그래서 만일 성공했다면?\""]], [["“I should have thrown myself into the sea, gained one of the islands near here—the Isle de Daume or the Isle de Tiboulen—and then I should have been safe.”", "\"바다에 몸을 던져, 이 근처 섬 가운데 하나에, 돔섬이나 티불랑섬에 닿았을 거요. 그러면 안전했겠지.\""]], [["“Could you have swum so far?”", "\"그렇게 멀리까지 헤엄칠 수 있었겠소?\""]], [["“Heaven would have given me strength; but now all is lost.” “All?”", "\"하늘이 내게 힘을 주셨겠지. 하지만 이제 모든 게 끝났소.\""]], [["“Yes; stop up your excavation carefully, do not work any more, and wait until you hear from me.”", "\"모든 게요?\" \"그렇소. 당신이 판 굴을 조심스레 막고, 더는 일하지 말고, 내게서 소식이 올 때까지 기다리시오.\""]], [["“Tell me, at least, who you are?”", "\"적어도 당신이 누구인지는 말해 주시오.\""]], [["“I am—I am No. 27.”", "\"나는, 나는 27호요.\""]], [["“You mistrust me, then,”", "\"그럼 나를 못 믿는 거구려.\""], ["said Dantès.", "당테스가 말했다."], ["Edmond fancied he heard a bitter laugh resounding from the depths.", "에드몽은 그 깊은 곳에서 씁쓸한 웃음이 울려오는 것을 들은 듯했다."]], [["“Oh, I am a Christian,”", "\"오, 나는 기독교인이오.\""], ["cried Dantès, guessing instinctively that this man meant to abandon him.", "당테스가 외쳤다. 이 사람이 자기를 저버리려 한다는 것을 본능적으로 알아챘던 것이다."], ["“I swear to you by him who died for us that naught shall induce me to breathe one syllable to my jailers; but I conjure you do not abandon me.", "\"우리를 위해 돌아가신 분께 맹세하건대, 그 무엇도 나를 부추겨 간수들에게 한 마디도 흘리게 하지 못할 거요. 하지만 부디 나를 버리지 마시오."], ["If you do, I swear to you, for I have got to the end of my strength, that I will dash my brains out against the wall, and you will have my death to reproach yourself with.”", "만일 버린다면, 맹세코 말하지만, 나는 이미 기력이 다했으니, 이 벽에 머리를 박살 내 버릴 거요. 그러면 당신은 내 죽음을 두고 스스로를 책망하게 될 거요.\""]], [["“How old are you?", "\"나이가 몇이오?"], ["Your voice is that of a young man.”", "목소리를 들으니 젊은 사람 같은데.\""]], [["“I do not know my age, for I have not counted the years I have been here.", "\"내 나이를 모르겠소. 여기 온 뒤로 햇수를 세지 않았으니까."], ["All I do know is, that I was just nineteen when I was arrested, the 28th of February, 1815.”", "다만 아는 건, 1815년 2월 28일에 붙잡혔을 때 막 열아홉이었다는 것뿐이오.\""]], [["“Not quite twenty-six!”", "\"채 스물여섯도 안 됐군!\""], ["murmured the voice; “at that age he cannot be a traitor.”", "목소리가 중얼거렸다. \"그 나이라면 배신자일 리 없지.\""]], [["“Oh, no, no,” cried Dantès.", "\"오, 아니오, 아니오.\""], ["“I swear to you again, rather than betray you, I would allow myself to be hacked in pieces!”", "당테스가 외쳤다. \"다시 맹세하건대, 당신을 배신하느니 차라리 내 몸이 갈가리 찢기는 편을 택하겠소!\""]], [["“You have done well to speak to me, and ask for my assistance, for I was about to form another plan, and leave you; but your age reassures me.", "\"내게 말을 걸고 도움을 청한 건 잘한 일이오. 나는 막 다른 계획을 세워 당신을 떠나려던 참이었으니까. 하지만 당신의 나이가 나를 안심시키는구려."], ["I will not forget you. Wait.”", "당신을 잊지 않으리다. 기다리시오.\""]], [["“How long?”", "\"얼마나요?\""]], [["“I must calculate our chances; I will give you the signal.”", "\"우리의 가능성을 따져 봐야 하오. 내가 신호를 보내리다.\""]], [["“But you will not leave me; you will come to me, or you will let me come to you.", "\"하지만 나를 떠나지는 마시오. 당신이 내게로 오든지, 아니면 내가 당신에게 가게 해 주시오."], ["We will escape, and if we cannot escape we will talk; you of those whom you love, and I of those whom I love.", "우리는 탈출할 거요. 탈출하지 못한다면 이야기라도 나눕시다. 당신은 당신이 사랑하는 이들에 대해, 나는 내가 사랑하는 이들에 대해."], ["You must love somebody?”", "당신도 누군가를 사랑하겠지요?\""]], [["“No, I am alone in the world.”", "\"아니오, 나는 세상에 혼자요.\""]], [["“Then you will love me.", "\"그럼 당신은 나를 사랑하게 될 거요."], ["If you are young, I will be your comrade; if you are old, I will be your son.", "당신이 젊다면 내가 당신의 벗이 되겠소. 당신이 늙었다면 내가 당신의 아들이 되겠소."], ["I have a father who is seventy if he yet lives; I only love him and a young girl called Mercédès.", "내겐 아버지가 계신데, 살아 계신다면 일흔이오. 나는 오직 그분과, 메르세데스라는 젊은 처녀만을 사랑하오."], ["My father has not yet forgotten me, I am sure, but God alone knows if she loves me still; I shall love you as I loved my father.”", "아버지는 아직 나를 잊지 않으셨을 거요, 분명히. 하지만 그녀가 여전히 나를 사랑하는지는 하느님만이 아실 일이오. 나는 아버지를 사랑하듯 당신을 사랑하겠소.\""]], [["“It is well,”", "\"좋소.\""], ["returned the voice; “tomorrow.”", "목소리가 대답했다. \"내일.\""]], [["These few words were uttered with an accent that left no doubt of his sincerity; Dantès rose, dispersed the fragments with the same precaution as before, and pushed his bed back against the wall.", "이 몇 마디는 그의 진심을 의심할 여지 없는 어조로 발음되었다. 당테스는 일어나 전과 같은 조심성으로 부스러기를 흩어 놓고, 침대를 벽으로 도로 밀어붙였다."], ["He then gave himself up to his happiness.", "그러고는 행복에 자신을 내맡겼다."], ["He would no longer be alone.", "그는 더 이상 혼자가 아니리라."], ["He was, perhaps, about to regain his liberty; at the worst, he would have a companion, and captivity that is shared is but half captivity.", "어쩌면 자유를 되찾으려는 참인지도 몰랐다. 최악의 경우라도 동무가 생길 터였고, 함께 나누는 갇힘이란 절반의 갇힘에 지나지 않았다."], ["Plaints made in common are almost prayers, and prayers where two or three are gathered together invoke the mercy of heaven.", "함께 드리는 탄식은 거의 기도나 다름없고, 두셋이 모여 드리는 기도는 하늘의 자비를 불러온다."]], [["All day Dantès walked up and down his cell.", "당테스는 온종일 감방을 오르락내리락했다."], ["He sat down occasionally on his bed, pressing his hand on his heart.", "이따금 침대에 앉아 가슴에 손을 얹었다."], ["At the slightest noise he bounded towards the door.", "작은 소리에도 그는 문 쪽으로 튀어 갔다."], ["Once or twice the thought crossed his mind that he might be separated from this unknown, whom he loved already; and then his mind was made up—when the jailer moved his bed and stooped to examine the opening, he would kill him with his water jug.", "한두 번은 이미 사랑하게 된 그 미지의 사람과 떨어지게 될지도 모른다는 생각이 머리를 스쳤다. 그러자 그는 마음을 굳혔다. 간수가 침대를 옮기고 몸을 숙여 그 구멍을 살피려 들면, 물주전자로 그를 죽이리라."], ["He would be condemned to die, but he was about to die of grief and despair when this miraculous noise recalled him to life.", "그러면 사형을 선고받겠지만, 그는 이 기적 같은 소리가 그를 다시 삶으로 불러내기 전까지는 슬픔과 절망으로 죽어 가던 참이었다."]], [["The jailer came in the evening. Dantès was on his bed.", "저녁에 간수가 들어왔다. 당테스는 침대에 누워 있었다."], ["It seemed to him that thus he better guarded the unfinished opening.", "그렇게 하면 끝나지 않은 구멍을 더 잘 지킬 수 있을 것 같았다."], ["Doubtless there was a strange expression in his eyes, for the jailer said, “Come, are you going mad again?”", "분명 그의 눈빛에 묘한 기색이 있었던지, 간수가 말했다. \"이봐, 또 정신이 나가려는 거요?\""]], [["Dantès did not answer; he feared that the emotion of his voice would betray him.", "당테스는 대답하지 않았다. 목소리에 묻어날 격한 감정이 자신을 드러낼까 두려웠다."], ["The jailer went away shaking his head.", "간수는 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 떠났다."], ["Night came; Dantès hoped that his neighbor would profit by the silence to address him, but he was mistaken.", "밤이 왔다. 당테스는 이웃이 이 고요를 틈타 자기에게 말을 걸어 주기를 바랐으나, 그것은 착각이었다."], ["The next morning, however, just as he removed his bed from the wall, he heard three knocks; he threw himself on his knees.", "그러나 이튿날 아침, 막 침대를 벽에서 떼어 놓는 참에, 그는 똑똑똑 세 번 두드리는 소리를 들었다. 그는 무릎을 꿇었다."]], [["“Is it you?”", "\"당신이오?\""], ["said he; “I am here.”", "그가 말했다. \"여기 있소.\""]], [["“Is your jailer gone?”", "\"당신 간수는 갔소?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇소.\""], ["said Dantès; “he will not return until the evening; so that we have twelve hours before us.”", "당테스가 말했다. \"저녁까지는 돌아오지 않을 거요. 그러니 우리에겐 열두 시간이 있소.\""]], [["“I can work, then?”", "\"그럼 일해도 되겠소?\""], ["said the voice.", "목소리가 물었다."]], [["“Oh, yes, yes; this instant, I entreat you.”", "\"오, 그럼, 그럼이오. 지금 당장, 부디 부탁하오.\""]], [["In a moment that part of the floor on which Dantès was resting his two hands, as he knelt with his head in the opening, suddenly gave way; he drew back smartly, while a mass of stones and earth disappeared in a hole that opened beneath the aperture he himself had formed.", "순간, 당테스가 머리를 구멍에 들이민 채 무릎을 꿇고 두 손을 짚고 있던 바닥의 그 부분이 갑자기 푹 꺼졌다. 그가 잽싸게 물러서는 사이, 돌과 흙더미가 그 자신이 뚫어 놓은 구멍 아래로 열린 구덩이 속으로 사라졌다."], ["Then from the bottom of this passage, the depth of which it was impossible to measure, he saw appear, first the head, then the shoulders, and lastly the body of a man, who sprang lightly into his cell. 0197m", "그러더니 깊이를 가늠할 길 없는 그 통로 밑바닥에서, 먼저 머리가, 다음에 어깨가, 마지막으로 한 사내의 몸이 나타나는 것을 그는 보았다. 사내는 가뿐히 그의 감방 안으로 뛰어들었다."]], [["# Chapter 16. A Learned Italian", "# Chapter 16. A Learned Italian"]], [["Seizing in his arms the friend so long and ardently desired, Dantès almost carried him towards the window, in order to obtain a better view of his features by the aid of the imperfect light that struggled through the grating.", "오랫동안 간절히 바라던 친구를 두 팔로 끌어안은 당테스는 그를 거의 안다시피 창가로 데려갔다. 쇠창살 사이로 비쳐 드는 희미한 빛에 의지해서나마 그의 얼굴을 좀 더 잘 보기 위해서였다."]], [["He was a man of small stature, with hair blanched rather by suffering and sorrow than by age. He had a deep-set, penetrating eye, almost buried beneath the thick gray eyebrow, and a long (and still black) beard reaching down to his breast.", "오래도록 애타게 바라던 친구를 두 팔로 끌어안은 당테스는, 창살 사이로 비쳐 드는 희미한 빛에 기대어 그 얼굴을 좀 더 잘 보려고 노인을 거의 안다시피 하여 창가로 데려갔다. 키가 작은 사내였다."], ["His thin face, deeply furrowed by care, and the bold outline of his strongly marked features, betokened a man more accustomed to exercise his mental faculties than his physical strength.", "머리카락은 나이보다는 고통과 슬픔으로 하얗게 세어 있었다. 짙은 잿빛 눈썹 아래 거의 파묻히다시피 한 눈은 움푹 들어가 있으면서도 꿰뚫는 듯했고, 길고 여전히 검은 수염은 가슴까지 늘어져 있었다."], ["Large drops of perspiration were now standing on his brow, while the garments that hung about him were so ragged that one could only guess at the pattern upon which they had originally been fashioned.", "근심으로 깊게 골이 팬 야윈 얼굴과 뚜렷하게 새겨진 이목구비의 대담한 윤곽은, 육체의 힘보다 정신의 능력을 단련하는 데 더 익숙한 사람임을 말해 주었다. 지금 그의 이마에는 굵은 땀방울이 맺혀 있었고, 몸에 걸친 옷은 어찌나 누더기인지 본래 어떤 모양으로 지어진 것인지 짐작만 할 수 있을 따름이었다."]], [["The stranger might have numbered sixty or sixty-five years; but a certain briskness and appearance of vigor in his movements made it probable that he was aged more from captivity than the course of time.", "낯선 사내는 예순이나 예순다섯쯤 되어 보였다. 그러나 동작에 어딘가 민첩하고 활기찬 데가 있어, 세월보다는 감금 생활 탓에 더 늙어 보이는 것이라 짐작케 했다."], ["He received the enthusiastic greeting of his young acquaintance with evident pleasure, as though his chilled affections were rekindled and invigorated by his contact with one so warm and ardent.", "그는 젊은 친구의 열렬한 인사를 분명 기쁜 마음으로 받아들였다. 얼어붙어 있던 그의 애정이, 이토록 따뜻하고 뜨거운 사람과 닿아 다시 불붙고 되살아나는 듯했다."], ["He thanked him with grateful cordiality for his kindly welcome, although he must at that moment have been suffering bitterly to find another dungeon where he had fondly reckoned on discovering a means of regaining his liberty.", "그는 따뜻한 환대에 고마운 마음을 담아 진심으로 감사를 표했다. 자유를 되찾을 길을 발견하리라 굳게 믿었던 곳에서 또 다른 감방을 발견했으니, 그 순간 그는 분명 쓰라린 고통을 느끼고 있었을 텐데도 그러했다."]], [["“Let us first see,”", "\"우선,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “whether it is possible to remove the traces of my entrance here—our future tranquillity depends upon our jailers being entirely ignorant of it.”", "\"내가 여기 들어온 흔적을 지울 수 있을지부터 봅시다. 앞으로 우리가 평온하게 지낼 수 있느냐는, 간수들이 이 일을 전혀 모르게 하는 데 달려 있으니까요.\""]], [["Advancing to the opening, he stooped and raised the stone easily in spite of its weight; then, fitting it into its place, he said:", "그가 구멍 쪽으로 다가가 몸을 굽히더니, 무거운데도 손쉽게 돌을 들어 올렸다. 그러고는 그것을 제자리에 끼워 맞추며 말했다."]], [["“You removed this stone very carelessly; but I suppose you had no tools to aid you.”", "\"이 돌을 꽤 허술하게 들어냈군요. 하지만 도와줄 연장이 없었을 테니 그럴 만하지요.\""]], [["“Why,”", "\"아니,\""], ["exclaimed Dantès, with astonishment, “do you possess any?”", "당테스가 놀라 소리쳤다. \"선생님은 연장을 가지고 계십니까?\""]], [["“I made myself some; and with the exception of a file, I have all that are necessary,—a chisel, pincers, and lever.” 0201m", "\"몇 개 만들었지요. 줄칼만 빼면 필요한 건 다 있습니다. 끌과 집게, 그리고 지렛대요.\""]], [["“Oh, how I should like to see these products of your industry and patience.”", "\"아, 선생님의 그 솜씨와 인내가 빚어낸 물건들을 어찌나 보고 싶은지요.\""]], [["“Well, in the first place, here is my chisel.”", "\"그럼 우선 이게 내 끌이오.\""]], [["So saying, he displayed a sharp strong blade, with a handle made of beechwood.", "그러면서 그는 너도밤나무로 손잡이를 댄, 날카롭고 튼튼한 날을 보여 주었다."]], [["“And with what did you contrive to make that?”", "\"그런데 그걸 무엇으로 만드셨습니까?\""], ["inquired Dantès.", "당테스가 물었다."]], [["“With one of the clamps of my bedstead; and this very tool has sufficed me to hollow out the road by which I came hither, a distance of about fifty feet.”", "\"내 침대 틀의 꺾쇠 하나로 만들었지요. 바로 이 연장 하나로 여기까지 오는 길을 파냈습니다. 한 오십 피트쯤 되는 거리지요.\""]], [["“Fifty feet!”", "\"오십 피트요!\""], ["responded Dantès, almost terrified.", "당테스가 거의 겁에 질린 듯 되물었다."]], [["“Do not speak so loud, young man—don’t speak so loud.", "\"그렇게 큰 소리로 말하지 마시오, 젊은이."], ["It frequently occurs in a state prison like this, that persons are stationed outside the doors of the cells purposely to overhear the conversation of the prisoners.”", "큰 소리로 말하면 안 돼요. 이런 국사범 감옥에서는, 죄수들이 나누는 이야기를 엿들으려고 일부러 감방 문밖에 사람을 세워 두는 일이 흔하니까요.\""]], [["“But they believe I am shut up alone here.”", "\"하지만 사람들은 제가 여기 혼자 갇혀 있다고 알고 있습니다.\""]], [["“That makes no difference.”", "\"그래도 마찬가지요.\""]], [["“And you say that you dug your way a distance of fifty feet to get here?”", "\"그러니까 여기까지 오시려고 오십 피트나 길을 파셨다는 말씀입니까?\""]], [["“I do; that is about the distance that separates your chamber from mine; only, unfortunately, I did not curve aright; for want of the necessary geometrical instruments to calculate my scale of proportion, instead of taking an ellipsis of forty feet, I made it fifty.", "\"그렇소. 당신 방과 내 방을 갈라놓은 거리가 그쯤 되지요. 다만 운 나쁘게도 방향을 제대로 굽히지 못했어요."], ["I expected, as I told you, to reach the outer wall, pierce through it, and throw myself into the sea; I have, however, kept along the corridor on which your chamber opens, instead of going beneath it.", "비례를 가늠할 기하 도구가 없는 탓에, 사십 피트짜리 타원으로 잡았어야 할 것을 오십 피트로 만들고 만 거지요. 아까 말했듯이, 나는 바깥벽까지 닿아 그 벽을 뚫고 바다로 몸을 던질 작정이었소. 그런데 당신 방으로 통하는 복도 밑을 지나가야 했을 것을, 그 복도를 따라 나란히 파 버린 셈이오."], ["My labor is all in vain, for I find that the corridor looks into a courtyard filled with soldiers.”", "내 노고가 다 헛것이 된 게, 알고 보니 그 복도가 군인들로 가득한 안마당을 면하고 있더란 말입니다.\""]], [["“That’s true,”", "\"맞습니다.\""], ["said Dantès; “but the corridor you speak of only bounds _one_ side of my cell; there are three others—do you know anything of their situation?”", "당테스가 말했다. \"하지만 선생님이 말씀하시는 그 복도는 제 감방의 한쪽 면만 막고 있을 뿐입니다. 나머지 세 면이 더 있는데, 그 사정에 대해서는 뭔가 아시는 게 있습니까?\""]], [["“This one is built against the solid rock, and it would take ten experienced miners, duly furnished with the requisite tools, as many years to perforate it.", "\"이쪽은 단단한 바위에 기대어 세워졌으니, 노련한 광부 열 명이 제대로 된 연장을 갖추고 달려들어도 뚫는 데 십 년은 걸릴 거요."], ["This adjoins the lower part of the governor’s apartments, and were we to work our way through, we should only get into some lock-up cellars, where we must necessarily be recaptured. The fourth and last side of your cell faces on—faces on—stop a minute, now where does it face?”", "이쪽은 소장 관저의 아래층에 잇닿아 있어서, 뚫고 나간다 해도 잠긴 지하 창고 따위로 들어가게 될 테고, 그러면 영락없이 다시 붙잡히고 말지요. 당신 감방의 네 번째, 마지막 면은 어딜 향하고 있느냐 하면, 어딜 향하고 있느냐 하면, 잠깐만, 가만, 그게 어딜 향하고 있더라?\""]], [["The wall of which he spoke was the one in which was fixed the loophole by which light was admitted to the chamber.", "그가 말하는 벽은, 방 안으로 빛이 들어오는 총안이 뚫려 있는 그 벽이었다."], ["This loophole, which gradually diminished in size as it approached the outside, to an opening through which a child could not have passed, was, for better security, furnished with three iron bars, so as to quiet all apprehensions even in the mind of the most suspicious jailer as to the possibility of a prisoner’s escape.", "이 총안은 바깥으로 갈수록 차츰 좁아져, 끝에 가서는 어린아이도 빠져나갈 수 없을 만한 구멍이 되어 있었다. 게다가 더 확실히 하려고 세 개의 쇠창살까지 박아 두어, 아무리 의심 많은 간수라 해도 죄수가 탈출할 수 있으리라는 염려 따위는 깨끗이 거둘 만했다."], ["As the stranger asked the question, he dragged the table beneath the window.", "낯선 사내는 그렇게 물으면서 탁자를 창문 밑으로 끌어다 놓았다."]], [["“Climb up,”", "\"올라가 보시오.\""], ["said he to Dantès.", "그가 당테스에게 말했다."]], [["The young man obeyed, mounted on the table, and, divining the wishes of his companion, placed his back securely against the wall and held out both hands.", "젊은이는 시키는 대로 탁자 위로 올라갔다. 그러고는 동료의 뜻을 헤아려, 등을 벽에 단단히 기대고 두 손을 내밀었다."], ["The stranger, whom as yet Dantès knew only by the number of his cell, sprang up with an agility by no means to be expected in a person of his years, and, light and steady on his feet as a cat or a lizard, climbed from the table to the outstretched hands of Dantès, and from them to his shoulders; then, bending double, for the ceiling of the dungeon prevented him from holding himself erect, he managed to slip his head between the upper bars of the window, so as to be able to command a perfect view from top to bottom.", "아직 당테스가 감방 번호로만 알고 있던 그 낯선 사내는, 그 나이로는 도무지 기대하기 어려운 날렵함으로 뛰어올랐다. 고양이나 도마뱀처럼 가볍고 안정된 몸놀림으로 탁자에서 당테스가 내민 두 손으로, 다시 거기서 그의 어깨로 기어올랐다. 그러고는 천장이 낮아 똑바로 설 수 없었기에 몸을 반으로 굽힌 채, 머리를 창문 위쪽 창살 사이로 밀어 넣어 위에서 아래까지 환히 내다볼 수 있게 했다."]], [["An instant afterwards he hastily drew back his head, saying, “I thought so!”", "잠시 뒤 그는 황급히 머리를 빼며 \"그럴 줄 알았어!\""], ["and sliding from the shoulders of Dantès as dextrously as he had ascended, he nimbly leaped from the table to the ground.", "하고는, 올라갈 때처럼 능숙하게 당테스의 어깨에서 미끄러져 내려와 탁자에서 바닥으로 가뿐히 뛰어내렸다."]], [["“What was it that you thought?” asked the young man anxiously, in his turn descending from the table.", "\"무엇을 그럴 줄 아셨단 말입니까?\" 이번에는 당테스가 탁자에서 내려오며 불안한 듯 물었다."]], [["The elder prisoner pondered the matter. “Yes,”", "나이 든 죄수는 그 문제를 곰곰이 따져 보았다. \"그래,\""], ["said he at length, “it is so.", "마침내 그가 말했다. \"그렇군."], ["This side of your chamber looks out upon a kind of open gallery, where patrols are continually passing, and sentries keep watch day and night.”", "당신 방의 이쪽 면은 일종의 트인 회랑을 향하고 있소. 거기로는 순찰병이 끊임없이 오가고, 보초가 밤낮으로 망을 보지요.\""]], [["“Are you quite sure of that?”", "\"그게 정말 확실합니까?\""]], [["“Certain.", "\"확실하오."], ["I saw the soldier’s shape and the top of his musket; that made me draw in my head so quickly, for I was fearful he might also see me.”", "병사의 형체와 머스킷 총 끝이 보였어요. 그래서 그렇게 얼른 머리를 뺀 거요. 그자 역시 나를 볼까 봐 겁이 났으니까.\""]], [["“Well?” inquired Dantès.", "\"그래서요?\" 당테스가 물었다."]], [["“You perceive then the utter impossibility of escaping through your dungeon?”", "\"그러니 당신 감방을 통해 탈출하기란 도저히 불가능하다는 걸 알겠지요?\""]], [["“Then——” pursued the young man eagerly.", "\"그렇다면……\" 젊은이가 다급하게 말을 이었다."]], [["“Then,” answered the elder prisoner, “the will of God be done!”", "\"그렇다면,\" 나이 든 죄수가 대답했다."], ["And as the old man slowly pronounced those words, an air of profound resignation spread itself over his careworn countenance.", "\"하느님의 뜻이 이루어지기를 바랄밖에!\" 노인이 그 말을 천천히 내뱉는 동안, 근심에 찌든 그의 얼굴에는 깊은 체념의 빛이 번졌다."], ["Dantès gazed on the man who could thus philosophically resign hopes so long and ardently nourished with an astonishment mingled with admiration.", "그토록 오래도록 애타게 키워 온 희망을 이처럼 의연하게 내려놓을 수 있는 사람을, 당테스는 경탄과 놀라움이 뒤섞인 눈으로 바라보았다."]], [["“Tell me, I entreat of you, who and what you are?”", "\"부디 말씀해 주십시오, 선생님은 대체 누구시고 무얼 하시던 분입니까?\""], ["said he at length.", "마침내 그가 입을 열었다."], ["“Never have I met with so remarkable a person as yourself.”", "\"이제껏 선생님처럼 비범한 분은 만나 본 적이 없습니다.\""]], [["“Willingly,”", "\"기꺼이 말해 드리지요.\""], ["answered the stranger; “if, indeed, you feel any curiosity respecting one, now, alas, powerless to aid you in any way.”", "낯선 사내가 대답했다. \"이제는, 아아, 당신에게 아무런 도움도 줄 수 없게 된 사람에 대해 정말로 궁금한 게 있다면 말이오.\""]], [["“Say not so; you can console and support me by the strength of your own powerful mind.", "\"그렇게 말씀하지 마십시오. 선생님은 그 강인한 정신의 힘으로 저를 위로하고 떠받쳐 주실 수 있습니다."], ["Pray let me know who you really are?”", "부디 선생님이 진정 누구신지 알려 주십시오.\""]], [["The stranger smiled a melancholy smile.", "낯선 사내는 우수에 찬 미소를 지었다."], ["“Then listen,” said he.", "\"그럼 들어 보시오.\" 그가 말했다."], ["“I am the Abbé Faria, and have been imprisoned as you know in this Château d’If since the year 1811; previously to which I had been confined for three years in the fortress of Fenestrelle.", "\"나는 파리아 신부요. 당신도 알다시피 1811년부터 이 이프성에 갇혀 있었지요. 그 전에는 페네스트렐 요새에 삼 년 동안 갇혀 있었고요."], ["In the year 1811 I was transferred to Piedmont in France.", "1811년에 나는 피에몬테에 있다가 프랑스로 이송되었소."], ["It was at this period I learned that the destiny which seemed subservient to every wish formed by Napoleon, had bestowed on him a son, named king of Rome even in his cradle.", "나폴레옹이 품은 온갖 소망에 고분고분 따르는 듯하던 운명이, 그에게 아들 하나를 점지해 주어 요람에서부터 로마 왕이라 불리게 했다는 소식을 들은 게 바로 그 무렵이었지요."], ["I was very far then from expecting the change you have just informed me of; namely, that four years afterwards, this colossus of power would be overthrown.", "그때만 해도 나는 방금 당신이 알려 준 그 변화를, 그러니까 사 년 뒤에 그 권력의 거인이 무너지리라고는 도무지 예상하지 못했소."], ["Then who reigns in France at this moment—Napoleon II.?”", "그래, 지금 프랑스를 다스리는 게 누구요, 나폴레옹 2세입니까?\""]], [["“No, Louis XVIII.”", "\"아닙니다, 루이 18세입니다.\""]], [["“The brother of Louis XVI.!", "\"루이 16세의 아우 말이오!"], ["How inscrutable are the ways of Providence—for what great and mysterious purpose has it pleased Heaven to abase the man once so elevated, and raise up him who was so abased?”", "섭리의 길이란 얼마나 헤아릴 수 없는 것인지. 한때 그토록 높이 올랐던 사람을 낮추고, 그토록 낮았던 사람을 들어 올리신 것은, 하늘께서 대체 어떤 크고 신비로운 뜻을 품으셨기에 그러신 것일까?\""]], [["Dantès’ whole attention was riveted on a man who could thus forget his own misfortunes while occupying himself with the destinies of others.", "남의 운명에 골몰하느라 자기 자신의 불행마저 잊을 수 있는 이 사람에게, 당테스의 온 신경이 쏠렸다."]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"그래, 그래.\""], ["continued he, “’Twill be the same as it was in England.", "그가 말을 이었다. \"영국에서 그러했던 것과 똑같이 되겠지요."], ["After Charles I., Cromwell; after Cromwell, Charles II., and then James II., and then some son-in-law or relation, some Prince of Orange, a stadtholder who becomes a king.", "찰스 1세 다음에 크롬웰, 크롬웰 다음에 찰스 2세, 그다음엔 제임스 2세, 그러고는 어떤 사위든 친척이든, 어느 오라녜 공이라는 자가, 총독 자리에 있던 자가 왕이 되는 게요."], ["Then new concessions to the people, then a constitution, then liberty.", "그러고 나면 백성에게 새로운 양보가 베풀어지고, 이어서 헌법이, 그다음엔 자유가 오는 거지요."], ["Ah, my friend!”", "아, 친구여!\""], ["said the abbé, turning towards Dantès, and surveying him with the kindling gaze of a prophet, “you are young, you will see all this come to pass.”", "신부가 당테스를 돌아보며 예언자의 타오르는 눈빛으로 그를 살피며 말했다. \"당신은 젊으니, 이 모든 일이 일어나는 걸 보게 될 거요.\""]], [["“Probably, if ever I get out of prison!”", "\"제가 이 감옥을 빠져나갈 수만 있다면, 아마 그렇겠지요!\""]], [["“True,”", "\"맞는 말이오.\" 파리아가 대답했다."], ["replied Faria, “we are prisoners; but I forget this sometimes, and there are even moments when my mental vision transports me beyond these walls, and I fancy myself at liberty.”", "\"우리는 죄수지요. 하지만 나는 가끔 그걸 잊는다오. 심지어 마음의 눈이 나를 이 벽 너머로 데려가서, 내가 자유로운 몸인 양 여겨지는 순간마저 있지요.\""]], [["“But wherefore are you here?”", "\"그런데 선생님은 어쩌다 여기 들어오시게 되었습니까?\""]], [["“Because in 1807 I dreamed of the very plan Napoleon tried to realize in 1811; because, like Machiavelli, I desired to alter the political face of Italy, and instead of allowing it to be split up into a quantity of petty principalities, each held by some weak or tyrannical ruler, I sought to form one large, compact, and powerful empire; and, lastly, because I fancied I had found my Cæsar Borgia in a crowned simpleton, who feigned to enter into my views only to betray me.", "\"1807년에 나는 나폴레옹이 1811년에 실현하려던 바로 그 계획을 꿈꾸었기 때문이오. 마키아벨리처럼, 나는 이탈리아의 정치 지형을 바꾸고 싶었소. 약하거나 폭압적인 군주들이 저마다 차지한 자잘한 공국들로 쪼개진 채 내버려 두는 대신, 하나의 크고 단단하며 강력한 제국을 세우려 했지요."], ["It was the plan of Alexander VI.", "그리고 마지막으로, 왕관을 쓴 어느 멍청이를 내 체사레 보르자로 점찍었기 때문이오."], ["and Clement VII., but it will never succeed now, for they attempted it fruitlessly, and Napoleon was unable to complete his work.", "그자는 나를 배신하려고 내 뜻에 동조하는 척했던 거였소. 그것은 알렉산데르 6세와 클레멘스 7세의 계획이었지만, 이제는 결코 성공하지 못할 거요. 그들도 헛되이 시도했고, 나폴레옹 역시 제 과업을 끝내지 못했으니."], ["Italy seems fated to misfortune.”", "이탈리아는 불행을 타고난 듯하오.\""], ["And the old man bowed his head.", "그러고는 노인이 고개를 숙였다."]], [["Dantès could not understand a man risking his life for such matters.", "당테스는 그런 일들 때문에 목숨을 거는 사람을 이해할 수 없었다."], ["Napoleon certainly he knew something of, inasmuch as he had seen and spoken with him; but of Clement VII.", "나폴레옹이라면 직접 보고 말까지 나눠 본 터라 어느 정도 알았다."], ["and Alexander VI. he knew nothing.", "그러나 클레멘스 7세나 알렉산데르 6세에 대해서는 아무것도 몰랐다."]], [["“Are you not,”", "\"혹시 선생님이,\""], ["he asked, “the priest who here in the Château d’If is generally thought to be—ill?”", "그가 물었다. \"이 이프성에서 다들 좀, 편찮으시다고 여기는 그 신부님 아니십니까?\""]], [["“Mad, you mean, don’t you?”", "\"미쳤다는 말이지요, 안 그렇소?\""]], [["“I did not like to say so,”", "\"차마 그렇게는 말씀드리기 어려웠습니다.\""], ["answered Dantès, smiling.", "당테스가 미소 지으며 대답했다."]], [["“Well, then,” resumed Faria with a bitter smile, “let me answer your question in full, by acknowledging that I am the poor mad prisoner of the Château d’If, for many years permitted to amuse the different visitors with what is said to be my insanity; and, in all probability, I should be promoted to the honor of making sport for the children, if such innocent beings could be found in an abode devoted like this to suffering and despair.”", "\"그렇다면,\" 파리아가 씁쓸한 미소를 지으며 말을 이었다. \"당신 물음에 빠짐없이 답해 드리리다. 나는 이프성의 가엾은 미친 죄수요. 여러 해 동안 이곳을 찾는 갖가지 방문객들에게 이른바 내 광기로 즐거움을 베푸는 일을 허락받았지요. 그리고 십중팔구는, 아이들에게 웃음거리가 되어 주는 영예로까지 승진했을 거요. 다만 이처럼 고통과 절망에 바쳐진 거처에서 그런 순진한 어린것들을 찾을 수만 있었다면 말이오.\""]], [["Dantès remained for a short time mute and motionless; at length he said:", "당테스는 잠시 말없이 꼼짝도 하지 않고 있었다. 이윽고 그가 입을 열었다."]], [["“Then you abandon all hope of escape?”", "\"그럼 선생님은 탈출의 희망을 모두 버리셨단 말씀입니까?\""]], [["“I perceive its utter impossibility; and I consider it impious to attempt that which the Almighty evidently does not approve.”", "\"그게 전혀 불가능하다는 걸 알았으니까요. 게다가 전능하신 분께서 분명 허락지 않으시는 일을 굳이 꾀하는 건 불경하다고 여깁니다.\""]], [["“Nay, be not discouraged. Would it not be expecting too much to hope to succeed at your first attempt?", "\"아닙니다, 낙심하지 마십시오. 첫 시도에 성공하기를 바라는 건 너무 큰 욕심이 아니겠습니까?"], ["Why not try to find an opening in another direction from that which has so unfortunately failed?”", "이번에 그토록 안타깝게 실패한 방향 말고, 다른 쪽으로 길을 뚫어 보시는 건 어떻습니까?\""]], [["“Alas, it shows how little notion you can have of all it has cost me to effect a purpose so unexpectedly frustrated, that you talk of beginning over again.", "\"아아, 다시 시작하자는 말을 하는 걸 보니, 그토록 뜻밖에 좌절된 일을 이루느라 내가 얼마나 많은 대가를 치렀는지 당신은 거의 짐작도 못 하는구려."], ["In the first place, I was four years making the tools I possess, and have been two years scraping and digging out earth, hard as granite itself; then what toil and fatigue has it not been to remove huge stones I should once have deemed impossible to loosen.", "우선, 지금 가진 연장들을 만드는 데 사 년이 걸렸소. 그리고 화강암처럼 단단한 흙을 긁고 파내는 데 이 년을 보냈지요. 그다음엔, 한때는 도무지 떼어 낼 수 없으리라 여겼던 거대한 돌들을 들어내느라 또 얼마나 애쓰고 지쳤는지 모른다오."], ["Whole days have I passed in these Titanic efforts, considering my labor well repaid if, by night-time I had contrived to carry away a square inch of this hard-bound cement, changed by ages into a substance unyielding as the stones themselves; then to conceal the mass of earth and rubbish I dug up, I was compelled to break through a staircase, and throw the fruits of my labor into the hollow part of it; but the well is now so completely choked up, that I scarcely think it would be possible to add another handful of dust without leading to discovery.", "그런 엄청난 노고에 며칠씩을 다 바치면서도, 밤이 될 무렵 이 단단하게 굳은 회반죽을, 세월이 흘러 돌만큼이나 굳어 버린 그것을 한 치 사방만큼이라도 떼어 낼 수 있으면 그 수고가 충분히 보상받았다고 여겼지요. 또 파낸 흙과 부스러기 더미를 감추려고 계단 하나를 부수고, 그 노고의 결실을 그 빈 속에 쏟아 넣어야 했소. 그런데 이제 그 구덩이가 어찌나 꽉 들어찼는지, 한 줌의 흙이라도 더 넣었다간 들통이 날 판이오."], ["Consider also that I fully believed I had accomplished the end and aim of my undertaking, for which I had so exactly husbanded my strength as to make it just hold out to the termination of my enterprise; and now, at the moment when I reckoned upon success, my hopes are forever dashed from me.", "게다가 생각해 보시오, 나는 내 과업의 목적과 목표를 다 이루었다고 굳게 믿었던 거요. 그 일이 끝날 때까지 딱 버틸 만큼만 힘을 쓰도록 그토록 정확히 아껴 두었으니 말이오. 그런데 이제 성공을 손꼽아 기다리던 바로 그 순간, 내 희망은 영영 산산이 부서져 버렸소."], ["No, I repeat again, that nothing shall induce me to renew attempts evidently at variance with the Almighty’s pleasure.”", "아니, 다시 한번 말하지만, 무엇으로도 나를 움직여, 전능하신 분의 뜻에 분명히 어긋나는 시도를 되풀이하게 할 수는 없을 거요.\""]], [["Dantès held down his head, that the other might not see how joy at the thought of having a companion outweighed the sympathy he felt for the failure of the abbé’s plans.", "당테스는 동료가 생겼다는 생각에 차오르는 기쁨이, 신부의 계획이 실패한 데 대한 안타까움을 짓누르고 있음을 들킬까 봐 고개를 숙였다."]], [["The abbé sank upon Edmond’s bed, while Edmond himself remained standing.", "신부는 에드몽의 침대에 주저앉았고, 에드몽은 그대로 서 있었다."], ["Escape had never once occurred to him. There are, indeed, some things which appear so impossible that the mind does not dwell on them for an instant.", "탈출이라는 생각은 단 한 번도 그의 머리에 떠오른 적이 없었다. 정말이지, 어떤 일들은 너무나 불가능해 보여서 마음이 한순간도 거기에 머물지 않는 법이다."], ["To undermine the ground for fifty feet—to devote three years to a labor which, if successful, would conduct you to a precipice overhanging the sea—to plunge into the waves from the height of fifty, sixty, perhaps a hundred feet, at the risk of being dashed to pieces against the rocks, should you have been fortunate enough to have escaped the fire of the sentinels; and even, supposing all these perils past, then to have to swim for your life a distance of at least three miles ere you could reach the shore—were difficulties so startling and formidable that Dantès had never even dreamed of such a scheme, resigning himself rather to death.", "오십 피트나 땅을 파고 들어가는 것, 성공한다 해도 바다 위로 깎아지른 벼랑으로나 데려다줄 일에 삼 년을 바치는 것, 보초들의 총격을 운 좋게 피했다 쳐도 오십이나 육십, 어쩌면 백 피트나 되는 높이에서 바위에 부딪쳐 산산조각 날 위험을 무릅쓰고 파도 속으로 뛰어드는 것, 그 모든 위험을 다 넘겼다고 가정하더라도 뭍에 닿으려면 적어도 삼 마일은 죽을힘을 다해 헤엄쳐야 하는 것, 이 모든 것이 너무나 아찔하고 엄청난 난관이라, 당테스는 그런 계획을 꿈에도 그려 본 적이 없었고, 차라리 죽음에 자신을 내맡기는 편이었다."]], [["But the sight of an old man clinging to life with so desperate a courage, gave a fresh turn to his ideas, and inspired him with new courage.", "그러나 그토록 필사적인 용기로 삶에 매달리는 노인을 보자, 그의 생각은 새롭게 방향을 틀었고 가슴에는 새로운 용기가 솟았다."], ["Another, older and less strong than he, had attempted what he had not had sufficient resolution to undertake, and had failed only because of an error in calculation.", "자기보다 나이 많고 힘도 약한 다른 사람이, 자신은 감히 엄두조차 내지 못했던 일을 시도했고, 그것도 단지 계산이 어긋난 탓에 실패했을 뿐이었다."], ["This same person, with almost incredible patience and perseverance, had contrived to provide himself with tools requisite for so unparalleled an attempt.", "바로 그 사람이, 거의 믿기 어려운 인내와 끈기로, 이토록 유례없는 시도에 필요한 연장들을 스스로 마련해 냈다. 다른 사람이 이 모든 걸 해냈는데, 그렇다면 어째서 당테스에게는 불가능하단 말인가?"], ["Another had done all this; why, then, was it impossible to Dantès?", "파리아가 오십 피트를 파고 나아갔다면, 당테스는 백 피트라도 팔 것이다."], ["Faria had dug his way through fifty feet, Dantès would dig a hundred; Faria, at the age of fifty, had devoted three years to the task; he, who was but half as old, would sacrifice six; Faria, a priest and savant, had not shrunk from the idea of risking his life by trying to swim a distance of three miles to one of the islands—Daume, Rattonneau, or Lemaire; should a hardy sailor, an experienced diver, like himself, shrink from a similar task; should he, who had so often for mere amusement’s sake plunged to the bottom of the sea to fetch up the bright coral branch, hesitate to entertain the same project?", "파리아가 쉰 살의 나이로 그 일에 삼 년을 바쳤다면, 그 절반밖에 안 된 자기는 육 년이라도 바칠 것이다. 신부이자 학자인 파리아가, 도므섬이든 라토노섬이든 르메르섬이든 그 어딘가로 삼 마일을 헤엄쳐 가며 목숨을 거는 일을 마다하지 않았다면, 자기처럼 강인한 뱃사람이자 노련한 잠수부가 그런 일을 두려워해서야 되겠는가. 그저 재미 삼아 그토록 자주 바다 밑바닥까지 잠겨 들어가 빛나는 산호 가지를 건져 올리던 자기가, 같은 계획을 품기를 망설여서야 되겠는가."], ["He could do it in an hour, and how many times had he, for pure pastime, continued in the water for more than twice as long!", "한 시간이면 해낼 수 있는 일이었고, 순전히 심심풀이로 그 두 배도 넘게 물속에 머문 적이 또 얼마나 많았던가!"], ["At once Dantès resolved to follow the brave example of his energetic companion, and to remember that what has once been done may be done again.", "당테스는 곧장 기운찬 동료의 용감한 본보기를 따르기로, 그리고 한 번 이루어진 일은 다시 이루어질 수 있다는 사실을 마음에 새기기로 결심했다."]], [["After continuing some time in profound meditation, the young man suddenly exclaimed, “I have found what you were in search of!”", "한동안 깊은 생각에 잠겨 있던 젊은이가 불쑥 외쳤다. \"선생님이 찾으시던 걸 제가 찾아냈습니다!\""]], [["Faria started: “Have you, indeed?”", "파리아가 흠칫했다. \"정말로 그랬소?\""], ["cried he, raising his head with quick anxiety; “pray, let me know what it is you have discovered?”", "그가 재빨리 불안한 기색으로 고개를 들며 소리쳤다. \"부디, 당신이 무얼 알아냈는지 말해 보시오.\""]], [["“The corridor through which you have bored your way from the cell you occupy here, extends in the same direction as the outer gallery, does it not?” 0207m", "\"선생님이 지금 계신 감방에서부터 뚫고 오신 그 복도는, 바깥 회랑과 같은 방향으로 뻗어 있지요, 그렇지 않습니까?\""]], [["“It does.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["“And is not above fifteen feet from it?”", "\"그리고 그 회랑과 십오 피트 이상 떨어져 있지는 않고요?\""]], [["“About that.”", "\"그쯤 되지요.\""]], [["“Well, then, I will tell you what we must do.", "\"그렇다면, 우리가 무얼 해야 할지 말씀드리겠습니다."], ["We must pierce through the corridor by forming a side opening about the middle, as it were the top part of a cross.", "복도의 한가운데쯤에 옆으로 구멍을 내서 뚫고 나가는 겁니다. 마치 십자가의 윗부분처럼요."], ["This time you will lay your plans more accurately; we shall get out into the gallery you have described; kill the sentinel who guards it, and make our escape.", "이번에는 선생님이 계획을 좀 더 정확하게 세우시는 겁니다. 그렇게 해서 선생님이 말씀하신 그 회랑으로 나가, 거길 지키는 보초를 죽이고 탈출하는 거지요."], ["All we require to insure success is courage, and that you possess, and strength, which I am not deficient in; as for patience, you have abundantly proved yours—you shall now see me prove mine.”", "성공을 거두는 데 필요한 건 오직 용기뿐인데, 그건 선생님이 가지셨고, 또 힘인데, 그건 제게 모자라지 않습니다. 인내로 말하면, 선생님은 이미 넘치도록 증명하셨으니, 이제 제가 그걸 증명하는 걸 보시게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["“One instant, my dear friend,”", "\"잠깐만, 친구여.\""], ["replied the abbé; “it is clear you do not understand the nature of the courage with which I am endowed, and what use I intend making of my strength.", "신부가 대답했다. \"당신은 내가 지닌 용기가 어떤 성질의 것인지, 또 내가 내 힘을 어디에 쓰려 하는지를 잘 모르는 게 분명하오."], ["As for patience, I consider that I have abundantly exercised that in beginning every morning the task of the night before, and every night renewing the task of the day.", "인내로 말하면, 매일 아침 전날 밤의 일을 다시 시작하고 매일 밤 그날 낮의 일을 다시 이어 가면서, 나는 그걸 넉넉히 발휘했다고 생각하오."], ["But then, young man (and I pray of you to give me your full attention), then I thought I could not be doing anything displeasing to the Almighty in trying to set an innocent being at liberty—one who had committed no offence, and merited not condemnation.”", "하지만 젊은이여, 부디 내 말에 온전히 귀 기울여 주시오. 그때 나는, 죄 없는 사람 하나를, 아무 잘못도 저지르지 않았고 단죄받을 까닭도 없는 사람 하나를 자유롭게 풀어 주려 애쓰는 일이라면 전능하신 분의 노여움을 살 일은 없으리라 여겼던 거요.\""]], [["“And have your notions changed?”", "\"그럼 그 생각이 바뀌셨습니까?\""], ["asked Dantès with much surprise; “do you think yourself more guilty in making the attempt since you have encountered me?”", "당테스가 몹시 놀라며 물었다. \"저를 만났다고 해서, 이제 그 시도를 하는 일이 더 죄스럽다고 여기시는 건가요?\""]], [["“No; neither do I wish to incur guilt.", "\"아니오. 나 또한 죄를 짓고 싶지는 않소."], ["Hitherto I have fancied myself merely waging war against circumstances, not men.", "지금껏 나는 그저 사람이 아니라 처지와 싸우는 거라 여겨 왔소."], ["I have thought it no sin to bore through a wall, or destroy a staircase; but I cannot so easily persuade myself to pierce a heart or take away a life.”", "벽을 뚫거나 계단을 부수는 건 죄가 아니라고 생각했지요. 하지만 가슴을 꿰뚫고 목숨을 앗는 일이라면, 그렇게 쉽게 스스로를 납득시킬 수가 없구려.\""]], [["A slight movement of surprise escaped Dantès.", "당테스의 입에서 가벼운 놀라움의 기색이 새어 나왔다."]], [["“Is it possible,”", "\"그럴 수가 있습니까,\""], ["said he, “that where your liberty is at stake you can allow any such scruple to deter you from obtaining it?”", "그가 말했다. \"선생님의 자유가 걸린 일인데, 그런 거리낌 따위가 그걸 얻는 일을 막도록 내버려 두실 수 있단 말입니까?\""]], [["“Tell me,”", "\"말해 보시오.\""], ["replied Faria, “what has hindered you from knocking down your jailer with a piece of wood torn from your bedstead, dressing yourself in his clothes, and endeavoring to escape?”", "파리아가 대답했다. \"당신은 침대 틀에서 나뭇조각 하나를 뜯어내 간수를 때려눕히고, 그자의 옷으로 갈아입은 다음 탈출을 꾀할 수도 있었을 텐데, 무엇이 당신을 가로막았소?\""]], [["“Simply the fact that the idea never occurred to me,”", "\"그저 그런 생각이 한 번도 떠오르지 않았다는 사실뿐입니다.\""], ["answered Dantès.", "당테스가 대답했다."]], [["“Because,”", "\"그건,\" 노인이 말했다. \"그런 죄를 저지르는 데 대한 타고난 거부감이 당신이 그런 생각을 하지 못하게 막은 거요."], ["said the old man, “the natural repugnance to the commission of such a crime prevented you from thinking of it; and so it ever is because in simple and allowable things our natural instincts keep us from deviating from the strict line of duty.", "언제나 그렇소. 단순하고 허락된 일들에 있어서는, 우리의 타고난 본능이 우리가 의무의 엄정한 선에서 벗어나지 않게 붙잡아 주거든요. 피 흘리는 데서 기쁨을 느끼도록 천성을 타고난 호랑이는, 먹이가 닿을 거리 안에 들어왔음을 후각만으로 알아채고, 그 본능을 좇아 제 먹이에게 덮치는 데 필요한 도약의 거리를 가늠할 수 있소."], ["The tiger, whose nature teaches him to delight in shedding blood, needs but the sense of smell to show him when his prey is within his reach, and by following this instinct he is enabled to measure the leap necessary to permit him to spring on his victim; but man, on the contrary, loathes the idea of blood—it is not alone that the laws of social life inspire him with a shrinking dread of taking life; his natural construction and physiological formation——”", "그러나 사람은 그와 반대로 피라는 것을 끔찍이 싫어하지요. 그건 단지 사회생활의 법도가 목숨을 빼앗는 데 대한 움츠러드는 두려움을 불어넣기 때문만이 아니오. 사람의 타고난 구조와 생리적 형성이……\""]], [["Dantès was confused and silent at this explanation of the thoughts which had unconsciously been working in his mind, or rather soul; for there are two distinct sorts of ideas, those that proceed from the head and those that emanate from the heart.", "당테스는 자기도 모르게 마음속에서, 아니 영혼 속에서 꿈틀거리고 있던 생각을 이렇게 풀이해 주는 말 앞에서 어리둥절해져 입을 다물었다. 본디 생각에는 뚜렷이 다른 두 종류가 있으니, 머리에서 나오는 것과 가슴에서 우러나오는 것이 그것이다."]], [["0209m\n“Since my imprisonment,”", "\"갇혀 지내는 동안,\""], ["said Faria, “I have thought over all the most celebrated cases of escape on record.", "파리아가 말했다. \"나는 기록에 남은 가장 유명한 탈옥 사례들을 모두 곰곰이 생각해 보았소."], ["They have rarely been successful.", "성공한 경우는 드물었지요."], ["Those that have been crowned with full success have been long meditated upon, and carefully arranged; such, for instance, as the escape of the Duc de Beaufort from the Château de Vincennes, that of the Abbé Dubuquoi from For l’Evêque; of Latude from the Bastille.", "온전한 성공으로 빛난 것들은 오래도록 궁리하고 세심하게 짜여진 것들이었소. 이를테면 보포르 공작이 뱅센성을 빠져나온 일, 뒤뷔쿠아 신부가 포르레베크를 탈출한 일, 라튀드가 바스티유에서 도망친 일이 그렇지요."], ["Then there are those for which chance sometimes affords opportunity, and those are the best of all.", "그다음으로는 더러 우연이 기회를 마련해 주는 경우가 있는데, 그것이야말로 무엇보다 가장 나은 것이오."], ["Let us, therefore, wait patiently for some favorable moment, and when it presents itself, profit by it.”", "그러니 우리도 어떤 알맞은 순간이 올 때까지 참을성 있게 기다렸다가, 그 순간이 찾아오거든 그것을 잘 살립시다.\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["said Dantès, “you might well endure the tedious delay; you were constantly employed in the task you set yourself, and when weary with toil, you had your hopes to refresh and encourage you.”", "당테스가 말했다. \"선생님이야 그 지루한 기다림을 견디실 만했겠지요. 스스로 정해 둔 일에 끊임없이 매달리셨고, 노고에 지치면 다시 기운을 북돋아 주고 격려해 줄 희망이 있었으니까요.\""]], [["“I assure you,”", "\"정말이지,\""], ["replied the old man, “I did not turn to that source for recreation or support.”", "노인이 대답했다. \"나는 기분 전환이나 버팀목을 얻으려고 그 희망이라는 샘에 기댄 게 아니었소.\""]], [["“What did you do then?”", "\"그럼 무얼 하셨습니까?\""]], [["“I wrote or studied.”", "\"글을 쓰거나 연구를 했지요.\""]], [["“Were you then permitted the use of pens, ink, and paper?”", "\"그럼 펜과 잉크와 종이를 쓰는 게 허락되었습니까?\""]], [["“Oh, no,”", "\"오, 아니오.\""], ["answered the abbé; “I had none but what I made for myself.”", "신부가 대답했다. \"내가 직접 만든 것 말고는 아무것도 없었지요.\""]], [["“You made paper, pens and ink?” “Yes.”", "\"종이와 펜과 잉크를 직접 만드셨다고요?\""]], [["Dantès gazed with admiration, but he had some difficulty in believing.", "\"그렇소.\" 당테스는 경탄하며 바라보았지만, 좀처럼 믿기 어려워했다."], ["Faria saw this.", "파리아는 그것을 알아차렸다."]], [["“When you pay me a visit in my cell, my young friend,”", "\"젊은 친구여, 당신이 내 감방을 찾아오거든,\""], ["said he, “I will show you an entire work, the fruits of the thoughts and reflections of my whole life; many of them meditated over in the shades of the Colosseum at Rome, at the foot of St.", "그가 말했다. \"온전한 저작 한 편을 보여 주리다. 내 평생의 사색과 성찰이 맺은 열매지요."], ["Mark’s column at Venice, and on the borders of the Arno at Florence, little imagining at the time that they would be arranged in order within the walls of the Château d’If.", "그 가운데 많은 것을 로마의 콜로세움 그늘에서, 베네치아의 산마르코 광장 기둥 아래에서, 또 피렌체의 아르노강 기슭에서 곰곰이 궁리했더랬소. 그것들이 언젠가 이프성 담장 안에서 차곡차곡 정리되리라고는 그때는 꿈에도 몰랐지요."], ["The work I speak of is called _A Treatise on the Possibility of a General Monarchy in Italy_, and will make one large quarto volume.”", "내가 말하는 그 저작은 『이탈리아 통일 군주국 수립 가능성에 관한 논고』라는 것인데, 큰 사절판으로 두툼한 한 권은 될 거요.\""]], [["“And on what have you written all this?”", "\"그런데 그 모든 걸 어디에다 쓰셨습니까?\""]], [["“On two of my shirts. I invented a preparation that makes linen as smooth and as easy to write on as parchment.”", "\"내 셔츠 두 장에다 썼지요. 아마포를 양피지처럼 매끄럽고 글쓰기 좋게 만드는 처리법을 고안해 냈거든요.\""]], [["“You are, then, a chemist?”", "\"그럼 선생님은 화학자이십니까?\""]], [["“Somewhat; I know Lavoisier, and was the intimate friend of Cabanis.”", "\"조금은 그렇소. 라부아지에를 알고, 카바니스와는 절친한 사이였으니까요.\""]], [["“But for such a work you must have needed books—had you any?”", "\"하지만 그런 저작을 쓰시려면 책이 있어야 했을 텐데, 책은 좀 가지고 계셨습니까?\""]], [["“I had nearly five thousand volumes in my library at Rome; but after reading them over many times, I found out that with one hundred and fifty well-chosen books a man possesses, if not a complete summary of all human knowledge, at least all that a man need really know.", "\"로마에 있는 내 서재에는 책이 오천 권 가까이 있었소. 그런데 그것들을 여러 번 거듭 읽고 나니, 잘 골라낸 백쉰 권만 있으면, 인간 지식 전부를 빠짐없이 간추린 건 아닐지라도, 적어도 사람이 정말로 알아야 할 모든 것은 손에 넣은 셈이라는 걸 깨달았지요."], ["I devoted three years of my life to reading and studying these one hundred and fifty volumes, till I knew them nearly by heart; so that since I have been in prison, a very slight effort of memory has enabled me to recall their contents as readily as though the pages were open before me.", "나는 그 백쉰 권을 읽고 공부하는 데 내 삶의 삼 년을 바쳤고, 거의 외우다시피 했소. 그래서 감옥에 들어온 뒤로는, 아주 가벼운 기억의 노력만으로도 마치 책장이 눈앞에 펼쳐진 듯 그 내용을 술술 떠올릴 수 있다오."], ["I could recite you the whole of Thucydides, Xenophon, Plutarch, Titus Livius, Tacitus, Strada, Jornandes, Dante, Montaigne, Shakespeare, Spinoza, Machiavelli, and Bossuet.", "투키디데스, 크세노폰, 플루타르코스, 티투스 리비우스, 타키투스, 스트라다, 요르난데스, 단테, 몽테뉴, 셰익스피어, 스피노자, 마키아벨리, 그리고 보쉬에를 통째로 외워 들려줄 수도 있지요."], ["I name only the most important.”", "가장 중요한 이들만 꼽아도 그렇소.\""]], [["“You are, doubtless, acquainted with a variety of languages, so as to have been able to read all these?”", "\"그 책들을 다 읽으실 수 있었다니, 선생님은 분명 여러 언어에 정통하시겠군요?\""]], [["“Yes, I speak five of the modern tongues—that is to say, German, French, Italian, English, and Spanish; by the aid of ancient Greek I learned modern Greek—I don’t speak it so well as I could wish, but I am still trying to improve myself.”", "\"그렇소. 나는 현대어 다섯 가지를 하지요. 그러니까 독일어, 프랑스어, 이탈리아어, 영어, 그리고 에스파냐어를 말이오. 고대 그리스어의 도움으로 현대 그리스어도 익혔는데, 바라는 만큼 잘하지는 못해서 아직도 갈고닦으려 애쓰는 중이오.\""]], [["“Improve yourself!”", "\"갈고닦으신다고요!\""], ["repeated Dantès; “why, how can you manage to do so?”", "당테스가 되뇌었다. \"아니, 어떻게 그러실 수가 있습니까?\""]], [["“Why, I made a vocabulary of the words I knew; turned, returned, and arranged them, so as to enable me to express my thoughts through their medium.", "\"내가 아는 낱말들로 어휘집을 만들어, 이리저리 뒤집고 또 뒤집고 짜 맞춰서, 그것들을 통해 내 생각을 표현할 수 있게 했지요."], ["I know nearly one thousand words, which is all that is absolutely necessary, although I believe there are nearly one hundred thousand in the dictionaries.", "나는 천 개 가까운 낱말을 아는데, 그게 꼭 필요한 전부라오. 사전에는 십만 개 가까운 낱말이 있다지만 말이오."], ["I cannot hope to be very fluent, but I certainly should have no difficulty in explaining my wants and wishes; and that would be quite as much as I should ever require.”", "아주 유창하기를 바랄 수는 없지만, 내가 바라고 원하는 바를 설명하는 데는 분명 아무 어려움이 없을 거요. 그리고 그것이야말로 내가 평생 필요로 할 전부일 테고요.\""]], [["Stronger grew the wonder of Dantès, who almost fancied he had to do with one gifted with supernatural powers; still hoping to find some imperfection which might bring him down to a level with human beings, he added, “Then if you were not furnished with pens, how did you manage to write the work you speak of?”", "당테스의 경이는 점점 더 커져, 마치 초자연적인 능력을 타고난 사람을 상대하고 있는 듯한 기분마저 들었다. 그래도 이 사람을 보통의 인간들과 같은 수준으로 끌어내릴 만한 무슨 흠이라도 찾아낼까 하는 마음에, 그는 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"그럼 펜도 마련돼 있지 않았는데, 말씀하신 그 저작은 어떻게 쓰셨습니까?\""]], [["“I made myself some excellent ones, which would be universally preferred to all others if once known.", "\"훌륭한 펜을 몇 자루 만들었지요. 한번 알려지기만 하면 누구나 다른 모든 펜보다 그걸 더 좋아할 만한 것들이라오."], ["You are aware what huge whitings are served to us on _maigre_ days.", "금육일이면 우리에게 어마어마하게 큰 대구가 나오는 걸 당신도 알 거요."], ["Well, I selected the cartilages of the heads of these fishes, and you can scarcely imagine the delight with which I welcomed the arrival of each Wednesday, Friday, and Saturday, as affording me the means of increasing my stock of pens; for I will freely confess that my historical labors have been my greatest solace and relief.", "그래서 나는 그 생선 머릿속의 연골을 골라냈는데, 수요일과 금요일과 토요일이 돌아올 때마다 내 펜 재고를 늘릴 수단이 생긴다는 생각에 얼마나 반가워했는지는 당신은 짐작도 못 할 거요. 솔직히 털어놓자면, 그 역사 저술이야말로 내게 가장 큰 위안이자 안식이었으니까."], ["While retracing the past, I forget the present; and traversing at will the path of history I cease to remember that I am myself a prisoner.”", "지난날을 되짚는 동안에는 현재를 잊고, 역사의 길을 마음껏 거니노라면 나 자신이 죄수라는 사실마저 잊게 되거든요.\""]], [["“But the ink,”", "\"하지만 잉크는요.\""], ["said Dantès; “of what did you make your ink?”", "당테스가 말했다. \"잉크는 무엇으로 만드셨습니까?\""]], [["“There was formerly a fireplace in my dungeon,”", "\"내 감방에는 예전에 벽난로가 하나 있었소.\""], ["replied Faria, “but it was closed up long ere I became an occupant of this prison.", "파리아가 대답했다. \"내가 이 감옥에 들어오기 한참 전에 막아 버렸지만요."], ["Still, it must have been many years in use, for it was thickly covered with a coating of soot; this soot I dissolved in a portion of the wine brought to me every Sunday, and I assure you a better ink cannot be desired.", "그래도 오랜 세월 쓰였던 모양인지, 그을음이 두텁게 한 켜 덮여 있었소. 나는 그 그을음을 일요일마다 내게 나오는 포도주에 조금 풀었는데, 정말이지 그보다 더 나은 잉크는 바랄 수 없을 거요."], ["For very important notes, for which closer attention is required, I pricked one of my fingers, and wrote with my own blood.”", "더 정신을 바짝 차려야 하는 아주 중요한 메모를 할 때는, 손가락 하나를 찔러서 내 피로 썼지요.\""]], [["“And when,” asked Dantès, “may I see all this?”", "\"그럼 그 모든 걸 언제쯤 볼 수 있겠습니까?\" 당테스가 물었다."]], [["“Whenever you please,” replied the abbé.", "\"당신이 바라는 때면 언제든지요.\" 신부가 대답했다."]], [["“Oh, then let it be directly!” exclaimed the young man.", "\"오, 그렇다면 지금 당장 보고 싶습니다!\" 젊은이가 소리쳤다."]], [["“Follow me, then,”", "\"그럼 나를 따라오시오.\""], ["said the abbé, as he re-entered the subterranean passage, in which he soon disappeared, followed by Dantès.", "신부는 그렇게 말하며 지하 통로로 다시 들어갔고, 이내 그 안으로 사라졌다. 당테스가 그 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["# Chapter 17. The Abbé’s Chamber", "# Chapter 17. The Abbé’s Chamber"]], [["After having passed with tolerable ease through the subterranean passage, which, however, did not admit of their holding themselves erect, the two friends reached the further end of the corridor, into which the abbé’s cell opened; from that point the passage became much narrower, and barely permitted one to creep through on hands and knees.", "지하 통로를 비교적 수월하게 빠져나왔다. 다만 그 통로는 몸을 똑바로 세울 만큼 높지 않았다. 두 친구는 복도 끝에 다다랐고, 그곳에서 신부의 독방으로 이어지는 입구가 열려 있었다."], ["The floor of the abbé’s cell was paved, and it had been by raising one of the stones in the most obscure corner that Faria had been able to commence the laborious task of which Dantès had witnessed the completion.", "그 지점부터 통로는 훨씬 좁아져, 손과 무릎으로 겨우 기어 들어갈 만했다. 신부의 독방 바닥은 돌로 포장되어 있었는데, 가장 어두운 구석의 돌 하나를 들어 올리는 것에서부터 파리아는 그 고된 작업을 시작했고, 당테스는 그 작업이 완성되는 광경을 직접 보았다."]], [["As he entered the chamber of his friend, Dantès cast around one eager and searching glance in quest of the expected marvels, but nothing more than common met his view.", "친구의 방에 들어서면서 당테스는 기대하던 경이로운 물건들을 찾아 간절하고 탐색하는 눈길을 사방으로 던졌지만, 평범한 것들 말고는 아무것도 눈에 들어오지 않았다."]], [["“It is well,”", "\"됐네.\""], ["said the abbé; “we have some hours before us—it is now just a quarter past twelve o’clock.”", "신부가 말했다. \"우리에게는 몇 시간이 있어. 지금이 막 열두 시 십오 분이니까.\""], ["Instinctively Dantès turned round to observe by what watch or clock the abbé had been able so accurately to specify the hour.", "당테스는 본능적으로 몸을 돌려, 신부가 대체 어떤 시계로 그렇게 정확하게 시간을 짚어 냈는지 살폈다."]], [["“Look at this ray of light which enters by my window,”", "\"내 창문으로 들어오는 저 빛줄기를 보게.\""], ["said the abbé, “and then observe the lines traced on the wall.", "신부가 말했다. \"그런 다음 벽에 그어 놓은 선들을 보게나."], ["Well, by means of these lines, which are in accordance with the double motion of the earth, and the ellipse it describes round the sun, I am enabled to ascertain the precise hour with more minuteness than if I possessed a watch; for that might be broken or deranged in its movements, while the sun and earth never vary in their appointed paths.”", "자, 지구의 이중 운동과 지구가 태양 둘레로 그리는 타원에 따라 그린 저 선들 덕분에, 나는 시계를 가졌을 때보다 더 세밀하게 정확한 시간을 알아낼 수 있다네. 시계라면 부서지거나 작동이 어긋날 수 있지만, 태양과 지구는 정해진 길에서 결코 벗어나는 법이 없으니까.\""]], [["This last explanation was wholly lost upon Dantès, who had always imagined, from seeing the sun rise from behind the mountains and set in the Mediterranean, that it moved, and not the earth.", "이 마지막 설명은 당테스에게 전혀 와닿지 않았다. 그는 태양이 산 너머에서 떠올라 지중해로 지는 것을 보아 왔기에, 늘 움직이는 것은 태양이지 지구가 아니라고 여겼던 것이다."], ["A double movement of the globe he inhabited, and of which he could feel nothing, appeared to him perfectly impossible.", "자신이 사는 지구가 이중으로 움직이고 있는데도 그것을 조금도 느끼지 못한다는 사실이 그에게는 도무지 있을 수 없는 일로 보였다."], ["Each word that fell from his companion’s lips seemed fraught with the mysteries of science, as worthy of digging out as the gold and diamonds in the mines of Guzerat and Golconda, which he could just recollect having visited during a voyage made in his earliest youth.", "친구의 입에서 떨어지는 말 한마디 한마디가 학문의 신비로 가득 차 있는 듯했고, 그것은 그가 아주 어린 시절의 항해 중에 들렀던 기억이 어렴풋이 남아 있는 구제라트와 골콘다의 광산에서 나는 금이나 다이아몬드만큼이나 캐낼 가치가 있는 것으로 느껴졌다."]], [["“Come,”", "\"자,\""], ["said he to the abbé, “I am anxious to see your treasures.”", "그가 신부에게 말했다. \"어서 신부님의 보물들을 보고 싶습니다.\""]], [["The abbé smiled, and, proceeding to the disused fireplace, raised, by the help of his chisel, a long stone, which had doubtless been the hearth, beneath which was a cavity of considerable depth, serving as a safe depository of the articles mentioned to Dantès. 0213m", "신부가 미소 지었다. 그러고는 쓰지 않는 벽난로 쪽으로 가서, 끌의 도움을 빌려 긴 돌 하나를 들어 올렸다. 분명 화덕이었을 그 돌 밑에는 제법 깊은 구멍이 있었고, 그것은 당테스에게 말했던 물건들을 안전하게 보관하는 창고 노릇을 하고 있었다."]], [["“What do you wish to see first?”", "\"무엇부터 보고 싶은가?\""], ["asked the abbé.", "신부가 물었다."]], [["“Oh, your great work on the monarchy of Italy!”", "\"오, 이탈리아 군주제에 관한 신부님의 대작을요!\""]], [["Faria then drew forth from his hiding-place three or four rolls of linen, laid one over the other, like folds of papyrus.", "그러자 파리아는 은닉처에서 아마포 두루마리 서너 개를 꺼냈다. 파피루스를 접어 놓은 것처럼 차곡차곡 포개져 있었다."], ["These rolls consisted of slips of cloth about four inches wide and eighteen long; they were all carefully numbered and closely covered with writing, so legible that Dantès could easily read it, as well as make out the sense—it being in Italian, a language he, as a Provençal, perfectly understood.", "이 두루마리들은 폭이 약 4인치, 길이가 18인치쯤 되는 천 조각들로 이루어져 있었다. 모두 꼼꼼히 번호가 매겨져 있었고, 빽빽하게 글씨가 적혀 있었는데, 어찌나 또렷한지 당테스는 어렵지 않게 읽을 수 있었을 뿐 아니라 뜻도 알아챌 수 있었다. 프로방스 사람인 그가 완벽하게 이해하는 이탈리아어로 쓰여 있었기 때문이다."]], [["“There,”", "\"여기 있네.\""], ["said he, “there is the work complete.", "그가 말했다. \"이게 완성된 작품일세."], ["I wrote the word _finis_ at the end of the sixty-eighth strip about a week ago.", "일주일쯤 전에 예순여덟 번째 천 조각 끝에 '피니스'라는 단어를 써넣었지."], ["I have torn up two of my shirts, and as many handkerchiefs as I was master of, to complete the precious pages.", "이 귀한 페이지들을 마저 채우려고 셔츠 두 벌과 가진 손수건을 죄다 찢었다네."], ["Should I ever get out of prison and find in all Italy a printer courageous enough to publish what I have composed, my literary reputation is forever secured.”", "언젠가 감옥을 나가서, 온 이탈리아를 통틀어 내가 쓴 것을 출판할 만큼 용기 있는 인쇄업자를 만나게 된다면, 내 문인으로서의 명성은 영원히 보장될 걸세.\" * 피니스: 라틴어로 '끝'이라는 뜻. —옮긴이"]], [["“I see,” answered Dantès.", "\"알겠습니다.\" 당테스가 대답했다."], ["“Now let me behold the curious pens with which you have written your work.”", "\"이제 신부님이 이 작품을 쓰신 진기한 펜을 좀 보여 주십시오.\""]], [["“Look!”", "\"보게!\""], ["said Faria, showing to the young man a slender stick about six inches long, and much resembling the size of the handle of a fine painting-brush, to the end of which was tied, by a piece of thread, one of those cartilages of which the abbé had before spoken to Dantès; it was pointed, and divided at the nib like an ordinary pen.", "파리아가 길이 6인치쯤 되는 가느다란 막대기를 젊은이에게 보여 주며 말했다. 가는 그림 붓의 자루만 한 굵기였는데, 그 끝에는 실로 연골 한 조각이 묶여 있었다. 앞서 신부가 당테스에게 말했던 바로 그 연골이었다."], ["Dantès examined it with intense admiration, then looked around to see the instrument with which it had been shaped so correctly into form.", "끝은 뾰족하게 다듬어졌고, 보통 펜처럼 펜촉이 갈라져 있었다. 당테스는 깊은 감탄에 잠겨 그것을 살펴보았다. 그러고는 이토록 정확하게 모양을 다듬은 도구가 무엇인지 보려고 주위를 둘러보았다."]], [["“Ah, yes,” said Faria; “the penknife.", "\"아, 그래.\" 파리아가 말했다."], ["That’s my masterpiece.", "\"주머니칼 말이군."], ["I made it, as well as this larger knife, out of an old iron candlestick.”", "그게 내 걸작이야. 저 큰 칼과 마찬가지로, 낡은 쇠 촛대로 만들었지.\""], ["The penknife was sharp and keen as a razor; as for the other knife, it would serve a double purpose, and with it one could cut and thrust.", "주머니칼은 면도날처럼 날카롭고 예리했다. 다른 칼은 두 가지 용도로 쓸 수 있어서, 베기도 하고 찌르기도 할 수 있었다."]], [["Dantès examined the various articles shown to him with the same attention that he had bestowed on the curiosities and strange tools exhibited in the shops at Marseilles as the works of the savages in the South Seas from whence they had been brought by the different trading vessels.", "당테스는 마르세유 상점들에 진열된 진귀한 물건들과 기이한 연장들을 살펴볼 때와 똑같은 주의를 기울여 보여 주는 물건들을 하나하나 살폈다. 그 물건들은 남태평양 미개인들의 작품으로, 여러 무역선이 가져온 것들이었다."]], [["“As for the ink,”", "\"잉크로 말하자면,\""], ["said Faria, “I told you how I managed to obtain that—and I only just make it from time to time, as I require it.”", "파리아가 말했다. \"내가 어떻게 그걸 구했는지 자네에게 말했지. 그리고 그저 필요할 때마다 이따금 만들어 쓴다네.\""]], [["“One thing still puzzles me,”", "\"한 가지가 아직도 풀리지 않습니다.\""], ["observed Dantès, “and that is how you managed to do all this by daylight?”", "당테스가 말했다. \"이 모든 일을 어떻게 대낮에 하셨는지 말입니다.\""]], [["“I worked at night also,” replied Faria.", "\"밤에도 일했네.\" 파리아가 대답했다."]], [["“Night!—why, for Heaven’s sake, are your eyes like cats’, that you can see to work in the dark?”", "\"밤에요? 아니, 도대체, 신부님 눈이 고양이 눈이라도 됩니까? 어둠 속에서도 보고 일을 하시다니요?\""]], [["“Indeed they are not; but God has supplied man with the intelligence that enables him to overcome the limitations of natural conditions.", "\"그럴 리가 있겠나. 하지만 하느님은 인간에게 지성을 주셔서, 타고난 조건의 한계를 넘어설 수 있게 하셨지."], ["I furnished myself with a light.”", "나는 등불을 마련했다네.\""]], [["“You did? Pray tell me how.” “I separated the fat from the meat served to me, melted it, and so made oil—here is my lamp.”", "\"내게 나오는 고기에서 기름기를 떼어 내 녹여서 기름을 만들었지."], ["So saying, the abbé exhibited a sort of torch very similar to those used in public illuminations.", "자, 이게 내 등불일세.\" 그렇게 말하며 신부는 거리 축제 때 쓰는 횃불과 아주 비슷한 것을 보여 주었다."]], [["“But how do you procure a light?”", "\"하지만 불은 어떻게 붙이십니까?\""]], [["“Oh, here are two flints and a piece of burnt linen.”", "\"오, 여기 부싯돌 두 개와 태운 아마포 조각이 있지 않은가.\""]], [["“And matches?”", "\"성냥은요?\""]], [["“I pretended that I had a disorder of the skin, and asked for a little sulphur, which was readily supplied.”", "\"피부병에 걸린 척하면서 유황을 조금 달라고 했더니, 선뜻 내주더군.\""]], [["Dantès laid the different things he had been looking at on the table, and stood with his head drooping on his breast, as though overwhelmed by the perseverance and strength of Faria’s mind.", "당테스는 보고 있던 물건들을 탁자 위에 내려놓고, 파리아의 정신력과 끈기에 압도된 듯 고개를 가슴께로 떨군 채 서 있었다."]], [["0215m\n“You have not seen all yet,”", "\"자네는 아직 다 보지 못했네.\""], ["continued Faria, “for I did not think it wise to trust all my treasures in the same hiding-place.", "파리아가 말을 이었다. \"내 보물을 전부 한 은닉처에 맡기는 게 현명하다고 생각하지 않았거든."], ["Let us shut this one up.”", "이건 이제 닫아 두세.\""], ["They put the stone back in its place; the abbé sprinkled a little dust over it to conceal the traces of its having been removed, rubbed his foot well on it to make it assume the same appearance as the other, and then, going towards his bed, he removed it from the spot it stood in.", "두 사람은 돌을 제자리에 도로 놓았다. 신부는 그 위에 먼지를 살짝 뿌려 들어낸 흔적을 감추고, 발로 잘 문질러 다른 돌과 똑같아 보이게 만들었다. 그런 다음 침대 쪽으로 가더니, 침대를 놓여 있던 자리에서 치웠다."], ["Behind the head of the bed, and concealed by a stone fitting in so closely as to defy all suspicion, was a hollow space, and in this space a ladder of cords between twenty-five and thirty feet in length.", "침대 머리맡 뒤에는 어떤 의심도 허락하지 않을 만큼 딱 맞물린 돌에 가려진 빈 공간이 있었고, 그 공간 안에는 길이가 25피트에서 30피트쯤 되는 밧줄 사다리가 들어 있었다."], ["Dantès closely and eagerly examined it; he found it firm, solid, and compact enough to bear any weight.", "당테스는 그것을 가까이서 열심히 살폈다. 그것은 어떤 무게도 견딜 만큼 튼튼하고 단단하고 촘촘하게 짜여 있었다."]], [["“Who supplied you with the materials for making this wonderful work?”", "\"이 놀라운 물건을 만드실 재료는 누가 대 주었습니까?\""]], [["“I tore up several of my shirts, and ripped out the seams in the sheets of my bed, during my three years’ imprisonment at Fenestrelle; and when I was removed to the Château d’If, I managed to bring the ravellings with me, so that I have been able to finish my work here.”", "\"페네스트렐에서 삼 년 동안 갇혀 있을 때, 셔츠 여러 벌을 찢고 침대 시트의 솔기를 뜯어냈지. 그리고 이프성으로 옮겨질 때, 그 실밥들을 용케 함께 가져왔다네. 덕분에 여기서 작업을 마칠 수 있었지.\""]], [["“And was it not discovered that your sheets were unhemmed?”", "\"그런데 시트의 단이 풀린 게 발각되지 않았습니까?\""]], [["“Oh, no, for when I had taken out the thread I required, I hemmed the edges over again.”", "\"오, 그렇지 않았네. 필요한 실을 뽑아낸 뒤에는 가장자리를 다시 꿰매 두었으니까.\""]], [["“With what?”", "\"무엇으로요?\""]], [["“With this needle,”", "\"이 바늘로.\""], ["said the abbé, as, opening his ragged vestments, he showed Dantès a long, sharp fish-bone, with a small perforated eye for the thread, a small portion of which still remained in it.", "신부가 말하며 누더기 같은 옷을 헤쳐, 길고 뾰족한 생선 뼈를 당테스에게 보여 주었다. 실을 꿸 작은 구멍이 뚫려 있었고, 거기에는 아직 실 한 토막이 남아 있었다."]], [["“I once thought,”", "\"한때는,\""], ["continued Faria, “of removing these iron bars, and letting myself down from the window, which, as you see, is somewhat wider than yours, although I should have enlarged it still more preparatory to my flight; however, I discovered that I should merely have dropped into a sort of inner court, and I therefore renounced the project altogether as too full of risk and danger.", "파리아가 말을 이었다. \"이 쇠창살을 떼어 내고 창문으로 내려갈 생각도 했었네. 보다시피 이 창문이 자네 것보다 조금 넓긴 하지만, 그래도 탈출에 앞서 더 넓혀야 했겠지."], ["Nevertheless, I carefully preserved my ladder against one of those unforeseen opportunities of which I spoke just now, and which sudden chance frequently brings about.”", "하지만 알고 보니 그저 일종의 안마당으로 떨어질 뿐이더군. 그래서 위험과 모험이 너무 크다고 여겨 그 계획을 통째로 단념했지. 그래도 아까 말한 그런 뜻밖의 기회, 갑작스러운 운이 곧잘 가져다주는 그런 기회를 대비해, 이 사다리만은 소중히 간직해 두었네.\""]], [["While affecting to be deeply engaged in examining the ladder, the mind of Dantès was, in fact, busily occupied by the idea that a person so intelligent, ingenious, and clear-sighted as the abbé might probably be able to solve the dark mystery of his own misfortunes, where he himself could see nothing.", "당테스는 사다리를 자세히 들여다보는 척했지만, 실은 마음속으로 다른 생각에 골몰해 있었다. 신부처럼 그토록 지혜롭고 영리하며 통찰력 있는 사람이라면, 정작 자신은 아무것도 알아내지 못하는 자기 불행의 어두운 수수께끼를 풀어 줄 수 있을지도 모른다는 생각이었다."]], [["“What are you thinking of?”", "\"무슨 생각을 하고 있나?\""], ["asked the abbé smilingly, imputing the deep abstraction in which his visitor was plunged to the excess of his awe and wonder.", "신부가 미소 지으며 물었다. 그는 손님이 깊은 상념에 빠진 것을, 지나친 경외감과 경이로움 탓으로 여겼다."]], [["“I was reflecting, in the first place,”", "\"우선,\" 당테스가 대답했다."], ["replied Dantès, “upon the enormous degree of intelligence and ability you must have employed to reach the high perfection to which you have attained.", "\"신부님이 지금 이르신 이 높은 경지에 다다르려면 얼마나 엄청난 지성과 능력을 쏟아부으셨을지를 생각하고 있었습니다."], ["What would you not have accomplished if you had been free?”", "만약 자유로운 몸이셨다면 무엇인들 못 이루셨겠습니까?\""]], [["“Possibly nothing at all; the overflow of my brain would probably, in a state of freedom, have evaporated in a thousand follies; misfortune is needed to bring to light the treasures of the human intellect.", "\"아마 아무것도 못 이뤘을 걸세. 자유로운 처지였다면 내 머릿속 넘쳐 나는 것들도 아마 천 가지 어리석은 일로 흩어져 버렸겠지. 인간 지성의 보물을 빛으로 끌어내려면 불행이 필요한 법일세."], ["Compression is needed to explode gunpowder.", "화약을 터뜨리려면 압축이 필요하듯이 말이야."], ["Captivity has brought my mental faculties to a focus; and you are well aware that from the collision of clouds electricity is produced—from electricity, lightning, from lightning, illumination.”", "갇혀 지내는 처지가 내 정신력을 한곳에 모아 주었네. 자네도 잘 알겠지만, 구름이 부딪치면 전기가 생기고, 전기에서 번개가, 번개에서 빛이 나오지 않는가.\""]], [["“No,” replied Dantès.", "\"아닙니다.\" 당테스가 대답했다."], ["“I know nothing.", "\"저는 아무것도 모릅니다."], ["Some of your words are to me quite empty of meaning.", "신부님의 말씀 가운데 더러는 제게 전혀 뜻 없는 소리로 들립니다."], ["You must be blessed indeed to possess the knowledge you have.”", "그런 지식을 지니셨으니, 신부님은 참으로 복 받은 분이십니다.\""]], [["The abbé smiled.", "신부가 미소 지었다. \"그건 그렇고,\""], ["“Well,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “but you had another subject for your thoughts; did you not say so just now?” “I did!”", "\"자네에겐 또 다른 생각거리가 있지 않았나? 방금 그렇게 말하지 않았어?\""]], [["“You have told me as yet but one of them—let me hear the other.”", "\"그랬습니다!\" \"여태 그중 하나만 말했으니, 나머지 하나도 들려주게.\""]], [["“It was this,—that while you had related to me all the particulars of your past life, you were perfectly unacquainted with mine.”", "\"이런 것이었습니다. 신부님은 제게 지나온 삶의 자초지종을 모두 들려주셨는데, 정작 제 삶에 대해서는 전혀 모르고 계신다는 것 말입니다.\""]], [["“Your life, my young friend, has not been of sufficient length to admit of your having passed through any very important events.”", "\"젊은 친구, 자네 인생은 아주 중요한 사건들을 두루 겪었다고 하기엔 아직 그리 길지 않잖은가.\""]], [["“It has been long enough to inflict on me a great and undeserved misfortune.", "\"제게 크나큰, 그것도 까닭 없는 불행을 안기기에는 충분히 길었습니다."], ["I would fain fix the source of it on man that I may no longer vent reproaches upon Heaven.”", "저는 그 불행의 근원을 인간에게서 찾고 싶습니다. 그래야 더는 하늘을 원망하지 않을 테니까요.\""]], [["“Then you profess ignorance of the crime with which you are charged?”", "\"그렇다면 자네는 자신에게 씌워진 죄목을 정말 모른다는 건가?\""]], [["“I do, indeed; and this I swear by the two beings most dear to me upon earth,—my father and Mercédès.”", "\"정말 모릅니다. 이 땅에서 제게 가장 소중한 두 사람, 제 아버지와 메르세데스의 이름을 걸고 맹세합니다.\""]], [["“Come,”", "\"자,\""], ["said the abbé, closing his hiding-place, and pushing the bed back to its original situation, “let me hear your story.”", "신부가 은닉처를 닫고 침대를 본래 자리로 밀어 놓으며 말했다. \"자네 이야기를 들려주게.\""]], [["Dantès obeyed, and commenced what he called his history, but which consisted only of the account of a voyage to India, and two or three voyages to the Levant, until he arrived at the recital of his last cruise, with the death of Captain Leclere, and the receipt of a packet to be delivered by himself to the grand marshal; his interview with that personage, and his receiving, in place of the packet brought, a letter addressed to a Monsieur Noirtier—his arrival at Marseilles, and interview with his father—his affection for Mercédès, and their nuptial feast—his arrest and subsequent examination, his temporary detention at the Palais de Justice, and his final imprisonment in the Château d’If.", "당테스는 시키는 대로, 자신이 일생이라 부른 이야기를 시작했다. 그러나 그것은 인도로의 항해와 레반트로의 두세 차례 항해에 관한 이야기일 뿐이었고, 마침내 마지막 항해 이야기에 이르렀다. 르클레르 선장의 죽음, 자신이 직접 대원수에게 전하라며 받은 꾸러미, 그 인물과의 만남, 그리고 가져간 꾸러미 대신 받은, 누아르티에라는 인물에게 보내는 편지 이야기였다."], ["From this point everything was a blank to Dantès—he knew nothing more, not even the length of time he had been imprisoned.", "마르세유에 도착해 아버지를 만난 일, 메르세데스를 향한 사랑과 결혼 잔치, 체포와 뒤이은 심문, 재판소에서의 일시적 구금, 그리고 마침내 이프성에 갇힌 일까지. 이 지점부터는 당테스에게 모든 것이 백지였다. 그는 그 이상은 아무것도 몰랐고, 자신이 갇혀 있던 기간이 얼마나 되는지조차 알지 못했다."], ["His recital finished, the abbé reflected long and earnestly.", "그의 이야기가 끝나자 신부는 오랫동안 골똘히 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["“There is,”", "\"있다네.\""], ["said he, at the end of his meditations, “a clever maxim, which bears upon what I was saying to you some little while ago, and that is, that unless wicked ideas take root in a naturally depraved mind, human nature, in a right and wholesome state, revolts at crime.", "그가 한참 깊이 생각한 끝에 입을 열었다. \"조금 전에 내가 자네에게 한 말과도 통하는, 영리한 격언이 하나 있지. 본디 타락한 마음에 사악한 생각이 뿌리내리지 않는 한, 인간 본성이란 올바르고 건전한 상태에서는 범죄를 거부한다는 것일세."], ["Still, from an artificial civilization have originated wants, vices, and false tastes, which occasionally become so powerful as to stifle within us all good feelings, and ultimately to lead us into guilt and wickedness.", "그래도 인위적인 문명에서 비롯된 욕망과 악덕과 그릇된 취향이 있어, 그것들이 이따금 너무 강해지면 우리 안의 온갖 선한 감정을 질식시키고 끝내 우리를 죄와 악으로 이끌지."], ["From this view of things, then, comes the axiom that if you visit to discover the author of any bad action, seek first to discover the person to whom the perpetration of that bad action could be in any way advantageous.", "그러니 이런 관점에서 보면 한 가지 공리가 나오네. 어떤 나쁜 짓의 장본인을 찾으려거든, 먼저 그 나쁜 짓이 저질러져 어떤 식으로든 이득을 볼 사람이 누구인지부터 찾으라는 것이지."], ["Now, to apply it in your case,—to whom could your disappearance have been serviceable?”", "자, 이걸 자네 경우에 적용해 보세. 자네가 사라져서 누가 덕을 볼 수 있었겠나?\""]], [["“To no one, by Heaven!", "\"맹세코, 아무도 없습니다!"], ["I was a very insignificant person.”", "저는 아주 보잘것없는 사람이었으니까요.\""]], [["“Do not speak thus, for your reply evinces neither logic nor philosophy; everything is relative, my dear young friend, from the king who stands in the way of his successor, to the employee who keeps his rival out of a place.", "\"그렇게 말하지 말게. 자네 대답에는 논리도 철학도 없네. 젊은 친구, 세상 모든 것은 상대적이라네."], ["Now, in the event of the king’s death, his successor inherits a crown,—when the employee dies, the supernumerary steps into his shoes, and receives his salary of twelve thousand livres.", "후계자의 앞길을 막고 선 왕에서부터, 경쟁자가 자리를 차지하지 못하게 막는 말단 직원에 이르기까지 말이야. 왕이 죽으면 후계자가 왕관을 물려받고, 그 직원이 죽으면 후보가 그 자리를 차지해 연봉 1만 2천 리브르를 받지."], ["Well, these twelve thousand livres are his civil list, and are as essential to him as the twelve millions of a king.", "자, 그 1만 2천 리브르는 그에게 왕의 1천 2백만이나 다름없이 긴요한, 그의 생계 목록일세."], ["Everyone, from the highest to the lowest degree, has his place on the social ladder, and is beset by stormy passions and conflicting interests, as in Descartes’ theory of pressure and impulsion.", "가장 높은 자에서 가장 낮은 자에 이르기까지 누구나 사회라는 사다리에서 제 자리를 차지하고 있고, 데카르트의 압력과 추진 이론에서처럼 격렬한 욕망과 상충하는 이해관계에 둘러싸여 있지."], ["But these forces increase as we go higher, so that we have a spiral which in defiance of reason rests upon the apex and not on the base.", "그런데 이 힘들은 위로 올라갈수록 커지기 때문에, 이치를 거슬러 밑바닥이 아니라 꼭대기로 떠받쳐진 나선이 생기는 거야."], ["Now let us return to your particular world.", "자, 이제 자네의 작은 세계로 돌아가 보세."], ["You say you were on the point of being made captain of the _Pharaon_?”", "자네가 파라옹호의 선장이 되기 직전이었다고 했지?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“And about to become the husband of a young and lovely girl?”", "\"그리고 젊고 아리따운 아가씨의 남편이 되려던 참이었고?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Now, could anyone have had any interest in preventing the accomplishment of these two things?", "\"자, 이 두 가지가 이루어지는 것을 막아서 이득을 볼 사람이 있었겠나?"], ["But let us first settle the question as to its being the interest of anyone to hinder you from being captain of the _Pharaon_. What say you?”", "하지만 우선 자네가 파라옹호의 선장이 되는 걸 방해해서 누군가 득을 볼 일이 있었는지부터 따져 보세. 어떤가?\""]], [["“I cannot believe such was the case. I was generally liked on board, and had the sailors possessed the right of selecting a captain themselves, I feel convinced their choice would have fallen on me.", "\"그런 일이 있었다고는 믿기 어렵습니다. 저는 배 안에서 두루 사랑받았고, 선원들이 직접 선장을 뽑을 권리가 있었다면 분명 저를 골랐으리라 확신합니다."], ["There was only one person among the crew who had any feeling of ill-will towards me.", "선원들 가운데 제게 악감정을 품은 사람은 딱 한 명뿐이었습니다."], ["I had quarelled with him some time previously, and had even challenged him to fight me; but he refused.”", "얼마 전에 그와 다툰 적이 있었고, 결투를 신청하기까지 했지만, 그가 거절했지요.\""]], [["“Now we are getting on.", "\"이제 좀 풀려 가는군."], ["And what was this man’s name?”", "그 사람 이름이 뭐였나?\""]], [["“Danglars.”", "\"당글라르입니다.\""]], [["“What rank did he hold on board?”", "\"배에서 무슨 직책을 맡고 있었지?\""]], [["“He was supercargo.”", "\"회계 담당이었습니다.\""]], [["“And had you been captain, should you have retained him in his employment?”", "\"자네가 선장이 되었다면, 그를 그 자리에 그대로 두었겠나?\""]], [["“Not if the choice had remained with me, for I had frequently observed inaccuracies in his accounts.”", "\"제게 선택권이 있었다면 그러지 않았을 겁니다. 그의 장부에서 부정확한 점을 자주 보았으니까요.\""]], [["“Good again!", "\"또 좋아!"], ["Now then, tell me, was any person present during your last conversation with Captain Leclere?”", "그럼 말해 보게. 르클레르 선장과 마지막으로 나눈 대화 자리에 누가 있었나?\""]], [["“No; we were quite alone.”", "\"아니요. 저희 둘뿐이었습니다.\""]], [["“Could your conversation have been overheard by anyone?”", "\"누가 그 대화를 엿들을 수도 있었을까?\""]], [["“It might, for the cabin door was open—and—stay; now I recollect,—Danglars himself passed by just as Captain Leclere was giving me the packet for the grand marshal.”", "\"그럴 수도 있었겠지요. 선실 문이 열려 있었으니까요. 그리고…… 가만, 이제 생각났습니다. 르클레르 선장님이 제게 대원수에게 전할 꾸러미를 건네던 바로 그때, 당글라르가 곁을 지나갔습니다.\""]], [["“That’s better,”", "\"그게 더 낫군.\""], ["cried the abbé; “now we are on the right scent.", "신부가 외쳤다. \"이제 제대로 냄새를 맡았네."], ["Did you take anybody with you when you put into the port of Elba?”", "엘바섬 항구에 들렀을 때 누구를 데려갔나?\""]], [["“Nobody.”", "\"아무도요.\""]], [["“Somebody there received your packet, and gave you a letter in place of it, I think?”", "\"거기서 누군가 자네 꾸러미를 받고, 그 대신 편지를 한 통 주었지?\""]], [["“Yes; the grand marshal did.”", "\"네, 대원수가요.\""]], [["“And what did you do with that letter?”", "\"그 편지로는 무엇을 했나?\""]], [["“Put it into my portfolio.”", "\"제 서류첩에 넣었습니다.\""]], [["“You had your portfolio with you, then?", "\"그럼 그때 서류첩을 가지고 있었단 말인가?"], ["Now, how could a sailor find room in his pocket for a portfolio large enough to contain an official letter?”", "그런데 선원이 어떻게 주머니에 공문서가 들어갈 만한 큰 서류첩을 넣고 다닐 수 있나?\""]], [["“You are right; it was left on board.”", "\"맞습니다. 그건 배에 두고 갔었습니다.\""]], [["“Then it was not till your return to the ship that you put the letter in the portfolio?”", "\"그렇다면 배로 돌아온 뒤에야 편지를 서류첩에 넣은 거로군?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“And what did you do with this same letter while returning from Porto-Ferrajo to the vessel?”", "\"그럼 포르토페라요에서 배로 돌아오는 동안에는 그 편지를 어떻게 했나?\""]], [["“I carried it in my hand.”", "\"손에 들고 있었습니다.\""]], [["“So that when you went on board the _Pharaon_, everybody could see that you held a letter in your hand?”", "\"그러니까 자네가 파라옹호에 올랐을 때, 누구나 자네가 손에 편지를 쥐고 있는 걸 볼 수 있었겠군?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Danglars, as well as the rest?”", "\"당글라르도, 다른 사람들과 마찬가지로?\""]], [["“Danglars, as well as others.”", "\"당글라르도, 다른 사람들과 마찬가지로요.\""]], [["“Now, listen to me, and try to recall every circumstance attending your arrest.", "\"자, 내 말을 잘 듣고, 자네 체포에 얽힌 정황을 하나하나 떠올려 보게."], ["Do you recollect the words in which the information against you was formulated?”", "자네를 고발한 밀고장이 어떤 문구로 적혀 있었는지 기억하나?\""]], [["“Oh yes, I read it over three times, and the words sank deeply into my memory.”", "\"오, 그럼요. 세 번이나 거듭 읽어서, 그 문구가 기억에 깊이 박혀 있습니다.\""]], [["“Repeat it to me.”", "\"내게 그대로 말해 보게.\""]], [["Dantès paused a moment, then said, “This is it, word for word: ‘The king’s attorney is informed by a friend to the throne and religion, that one Edmond Dantès, mate on board the _Pharaon_, this day arrived from Smyrna, after having touched at Naples and Porto-Ferrajo, has been intrusted by Murat with a packet for the usurper; again, by the usurper, with a letter for the Bonapartist Club in Paris.", "당테스는 잠시 멈추었다가 말했다. \"한 글자 한 글자 그대로 옮기면 이렇습니다. '왕좌와 종교를 받드는 한 벗이 검사장께 알려 드립니다."], ["This proof of his guilt may be procured by his immediate arrest, as the letter will be found either about his person, at his father’s residence, or in his cabin on board the _Pharaon_.’”", "파라옹호의 일등 항해사 에드몽 당테스라는 자가 나폴리와 포르토페라요를 거쳐 오늘 스미르나에서 입항했는데, 뮈라에게서 찬탈자에게 전할 꾸러미를 맡았고, 다시 그 찬탈자에게서 파리의 보나파르트파 클럽에 전할 편지를 받았습니다. 그자를 즉시 체포하면 이 죄의 증거를 확보할 수 있을 것입니다. 그 편지는 그자의 몸에서든, 그의 아버지 집에서든, 아니면 파라옹호의 그의 선실에서든 발견될 것입니다.'\""]], [["The abbé shrugged his shoulders.", "신부가 어깨를 으쓱했다."], ["“The thing is clear as day,”", "\"이건 대낮처럼 명백하군.\""], ["said he; “and you must have had a very confiding nature, as well as a good heart, not to have suspected the origin of the whole affair.”", "그가 말했다. \"이 일의 출처를 의심하지 않았다니, 자네는 천성이 무척 사람을 잘 믿는 데다 마음씨도 착했던 게 틀림없어.\""]], [["“Do you really think so?", "\"정말 그렇게 보십니까?"], ["Ah, that would indeed be infamous.”", "아, 그렇다면 정말 비열한 짓이로군요.\""]], [["“How did Danglars usually write?”", "\"당글라르는 평소에 어떻게 썼나?\""]], [["“In a handsome, running hand.”", "\"멋들어진 흘림체로 썼습니다.\""]], [["“And how was the anonymous letter written?”", "\"그 익명의 편지는 어떻게 쓰여 있었지?\""]], [["Again the abbé smiled.", "신부가 다시 미소 지었다."], ["“Disguised.”", "\"꾸민 글씨로군.\""]], [["“It was very boldly written, if disguised.”", "\"꾸민 글씨치고는 아주 대담하게 쓰여 있었습니다.\""]], [["“Stop a bit,”", "\"잠깐.\""], ["said the abbé, taking up what he called his pen, and, after dipping it into the ink, he wrote on a piece of prepared linen, with his left hand, the first two or three words of the accusation.", "신부가 자신의 펜이라 부르던 것을 집어 들고 잉크에 적신 다음, 손질해 둔 아마포 조각에 왼손으로 그 고발장의 첫 두세 단어를 써 보였다."], ["Dantès drew back, and gazed on the abbé with a sensation almost amounting to terror.", "당테스는 뒷걸음질하며, 거의 공포에 가까운 감정으로 신부를 바라보았다."]], [["“How very astonishing!”", "\"이럴 수가!\""], ["cried he at length.", "그가 마침내 외쳤다."], ["“Why your writing exactly resembles that of the accusation.”", "\"아니, 신부님 글씨가 그 고발장 글씨와 똑 닮았습니다.\""]], [["“Simply because that accusation had been written with the left hand; and I have noticed that——”", "\"그 고발장이 왼손으로 쓰였기 때문일 뿐일세. 내가 알아챈 것이 있는데……\" \"무엇입니까?\""]], [["“What?” “That while the writing of different persons done with the right hand varies, that performed with the left hand is invariably uniform.”", "\"오른손으로 쓴 글씨는 사람마다 다르지만, 왼손으로 쓴 글씨는 한결같이 똑같다는 것일세.\""]], [["“You have evidently seen and observed everything.”", "\"신부님은 정말이지 모든 것을 보고 살피셨군요.\""]], [["“Let us proceed.”", "\"계속하세.\""]], [["“Oh, yes, yes!”", "\"네, 네!\""]], [["“Now as regards the second question.”", "\"이제 두 번째 문제로 가 보세.\""]], [["“I am listening.”", "\"듣고 있습니다.\""]], [["“Was there any person whose interest it was to prevent your marriage with Mercédès?”", "\"자네와 메르세데스의 결혼을 막아서 이득을 볼 사람이 있었나?\""]], [["“Yes; a young man who loved her.”", "\"네, 그녀를 사랑한 젊은이가 있었습니다.\""]], [["“And his name was——”", "\"그 이름은……\""]], [["“Fernand.”", "\"페르낭입니다.\""]], [["“That is a Spanish name, I think?”", "\"그건 스페인 이름인 것 같은데?\""]], [["“He was a Catalan.”", "\"카탈루냐 사람이었습니다.\""]], [["“You imagine him capable of writing the letter?”", "\"그가 그 편지를 쓸 만한 사람이라고 보나?\""]], [["“Oh, no; he would more likely have got rid of me by sticking a knife into me.”", "\"오, 아닙니다. 그자였다면 차라리 제게 칼을 꽂아 저를 없애려 했을 겁니다.\""]], [["“That is in strict accordance with the Spanish character; an assassination they will unhesitatingly commit, but an act of cowardice, never.”", "\"그게 바로 스페인 사람의 기질에 딱 들어맞지. 살인은 망설임 없이 저질러도, 비겁한 짓은 결코 하지 않으니까.\""]], [["“Besides,”", "\"게다가,\""], ["said Dantès, “the various circumstances mentioned in the letter were wholly unknown to him.”", "당테스가 말했다. \"편지에 적힌 여러 정황은 그가 전혀 모르는 것들이었습니다.\""]], [["“You had never spoken of them yourself to anyone?”", "\"자네 입으로 그것들을 누구에게도 말한 적이 없나?\""]], [["“To no one.”", "\"누구에게도요.\""]], [["“Not even to your mistress?”", "\"연인에게조차?\""]], [["“No, not even to my betrothed.”", "\"네, 약혼녀에게조차 말하지 않았습니다.\""]], [["“Then it is Danglars.”", "\"그렇다면 당글라르군.\""]], [["“I feel quite sure of it now.”", "\"이제 확신이 듭니다.\""]], [["“Wait a little.", "\"잠깐만."], ["Pray, was Danglars acquainted with Fernand?”", "당글라르가 페르낭을 알고 있었나?\""]], [["“No—yes, he was.", "\"아니요…… 아니, 알고 있었습니다."], ["Now I recollect——”", "이제 생각이 나는군요.\""]], [["“What?”", "\"무엇이?\""]], [["“To have seen them both sitting at table together under an arbor at Père Pamphile’s the evening before the day fixed for my wedding. They were in earnest conversation.", "\"제 결혼식 전날 저녁, 두 사람이 팡필 영감네 정자 아래 탁자에 함께 앉아 있는 걸 본 적이 있습니다. 두 사람은 진지하게 이야기를 나누고 있었지요."], ["Danglars was joking in a friendly way, but Fernand looked pale and agitated.”", "당글라르는 정답게 농을 던지고 있었지만, 페르낭은 창백하고 안절부절못하는 얼굴이었습니다.\""]], [["“Were they alone?”", "\"둘뿐이었나?\""]], [["“There was a third person with them whom I knew perfectly well, and who had, in all probability made their acquaintance; he was a tailor named Caderousse, but he was very drunk.", "\"제가 아주 잘 아는 세 번째 사람이 함께 있었습니다. 아마 그가 두 사람을 서로 소개했을 겁니다. 카드루스라는 재단사였는데, 몹시 취해 있었지요. 가만! 가만!"], ["Stay!—stay!—How strange that it should not have occurred to me before!", "어째서 이게 진작 떠오르지 않았을까요! 이제 똑똑히 기억납니다."], ["Now I remember quite well, that on the table round which they were sitting were pens, ink, and paper.", "그들이 둘러앉아 있던 탁자 위에 펜과 잉크와 종이가 놓여 있었습니다."], ["Oh, the heartless, treacherous scoundrels!”", "오, 그 매정하고 음흉한 악당들!\""], ["exclaimed Dantès, pressing his hand to his throbbing brows.", "당테스가 욱신거리는 이마에 손을 갖다 대며 부르짖었다."]], [["“Is there anything else I can assist you in discovering, besides the villany of your friends?”", "\"자네 친구들의 악행 말고도, 내가 밝혀내는 걸 도와줄 일이 더 있겠나?\""], ["inquired the abbé with a laugh.", "신부가 웃으며 물었다."]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"네, 네.\""], ["replied Dantès eagerly; “I would beg of you, who see so completely to the depths of things, and to whom the greatest mystery seems but an easy riddle, to explain to me how it was that I underwent no second examination, was never brought to trial, and, above all, was condemned without ever having had sentence passed on me?”", "당테스가 간절히 대답했다. \"사물의 밑바닥까지 환히 꿰뚫어 보시고, 더없이 큰 수수께끼도 쉬운 수수께끼처럼 푸시는 신부님께 부탁드립니다. 제가 어째서 두 번째 심문을 받지도 않았고, 재판에 회부되지도 않았으며, 무엇보다도 선고 한 번 받지 않고 형을 살게 되었는지, 그 까닭을 설명해 주십시오.\""]], [["“That is altogether a different and more serious matter,”", "\"그건 전혀 다른, 훨씬 더 심각한 문제일세.\""], ["responded the abbé.", "신부가 대답했다."], ["“The ways of justice are frequently too dark and mysterious to be easily penetrated.", "\"사법의 길이란 너무 어둡고 신비로워서 쉽게 꿰뚫어 보기 어려운 경우가 흔하지."], ["All we have hitherto done in the matter has been child’s play.", "우리가 여태 이 일에서 한 것은 어린애 장난에 지나지 않아."], ["If you wish me to enter upon the more difficult part of the business, you must assist me by the most minute information on every point.”", "더 어려운 부분으로 들어가길 바란다면, 모든 점에 대해 더없이 세세한 정보로 나를 도와야 하네.\""]], [["“Pray ask me whatever questions you please; for, in good truth, you see more clearly into my life than I do myself.”", "\"무엇이든 물어보십시오. 사실 신부님은 저 자신보다 제 인생을 더 또렷이 보고 계시니까요.\""]], [["“In the first place, then, who examined you,—the king’s attorney, his deputy, or a magistrate?”", "\"그렇다면 우선, 누가 자네를 심문했나? 검사장인가, 그 대리인인가, 아니면 판사인가?\""]], [["“The deputy.”", "\"대리인이었습니다.\""]], [["“Was he young or old?”", "\"젊었나, 나이가 들었나?\""]], [["“About six or seven-and-twenty years of age, I should say.”", "\"스물여섯이나 일곱쯤 됐을 겁니다.\""]], [["“So,” answered the abbé.", "\"그렇군.\" 신부가 대답했다."], ["“Old enough to be ambitious, but too young to be corrupt.", "\"야심을 품을 만큼은 나이가 들었지만, 부패하기에는 너무 젊은 나이로군."], ["And how did he treat you?”", "그가 자네를 어떻게 대했나?\""]], [["“With more of mildness than severity.”", "\"가혹하다기보다는 너그럽게 대했습니다.\""]], [["“Did you tell him your whole story?” “I did.”", "\"자네 이야기를 전부 했나?\""]], [["“And did his conduct change at all in the course of your examination?”", "\"전부 했습니다.\" \"심문하는 동안 그의 태도가 조금이라도 변하던가?\""]], [["“He did appear much disturbed when he read the letter that had brought me into this scrape.", "\"저를 이 곤경에 빠뜨린 편지를 읽었을 때는 무척 동요하는 듯 보였습니다."], ["He seemed quite overcome by my misfortune.”", "제 불행에 완전히 마음이 무너진 것 같았지요.\""]], [["“By your misfortune?”", "\"자네의 불행에?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Then you feel quite sure that it was your misfortune he deplored?”", "\"그럼 자네는 그가 한탄한 게 정말 자네의 불행이라고 확신하나?\""]], [["“He gave me one great proof of his sympathy, at any rate.”", "\"적어도 그는 제게 동정의 큰 증거를 하나 보여 주었습니다.\""]], [["“And that?”", "\"그게 뭐지?\""]], [["“He burnt the sole evidence that could at all have criminated me.”", "\"저를 죄에 빠뜨릴 수 있는 유일한 증거를 태워 버렸습니다.\""]], [["“What? the accusation?”", "\"뭐라고? 그 고발장을?\""]], [["“No; the letter.”", "\"아니요, 그 편지를요.\""]], [["“Are you sure?”", "\"확실한가?\""]], [["“I saw it done.”", "\"제 눈으로 직접 봤습니다.\""]], [["“That alters the case. This man might, after all, be a greater scoundrel than you have thought possible.”", "\"그렇다면 사정이 달라지는군. 어쩌면 그자가 자네가 짐작하던 것보다 훨씬 더 큰 악당일지도 모르겠네.\""]], [["“Upon my word,”", "\"맹세코,\""], ["said Dantès, “you make me shudder.", "당테스가 말했다. \"신부님 말씀에 몸서리가 쳐집니다."], ["Is the world filled with tigers and crocodiles?”", "세상이 호랑이와 악어로 가득 차 있단 말입니까?\""]], [["“Yes; and remember that two-legged tigers and crocodiles are more dangerous than the others.”", "\"그렇다네. 그리고 명심하게, 두 발 달린 호랑이와 악어가 다른 것들보다 더 위험한 법이야.\""]], [["“Never mind; let us go on.”", "\"상관없습니다. 계속하시지요.\""]], [["“With all my heart! You tell me he burned the letter?”", "\"기꺼이! 그가 편지를 태웠다고 했지?\""]], [["“He did; saying at the same time, ‘You see I thus destroy the only proof existing against you.’”", "\"네. 그러면서 동시에 이렇게 말했습니다. '보시오, 이렇게 당신에게 불리한 유일한 증거를 없애 드리는 거요.'\""]], [["“This action is somewhat too sublime to be natural.”", "\"그 행동은 자연스럽다기에는 너무 숭고하군.\""]], [["“You think so?”", "\"그렇게 보십니까?\""]], [["“I am sure of it. To whom was this letter addressed?”", "\"확신하네. 그 편지는 누구에게 보내는 것이었나?\""]], [["“To M. Noirtier, Rue Coq-Héron, No. 13, Paris.”", "\"파리 코크에롱 거리 13번지의 누아르티에 씨에게요.\""]], [["“Now can you conceive of any interest that your heroic deputy could possibly have had in the destruction of that letter?”", "\"자, 그 영웅적인 대리인이 그 편지를 없애서 얻을 만한 이득이 무엇이었을지 자네는 짐작이 가나?\""]], [["“Why, it is not altogether impossible he might have had, for he made me promise several times never to speak of that letter to anyone, assuring me he so advised me for my own interest; and, more than this, he insisted on my taking a solemn oath never to utter the name mentioned in the address.”", "\"글쎄요, 전혀 없다고는 할 수 없습니다. 그는 그 편지에 대해 누구에게도 절대 말하지 말라고 여러 번 다짐을 받았으니까요. 다 저를 위해 하는 충고라고 하면서요. 게다가 그것도 모자라, 그 수신인에 적힌 이름을 절대 입에 올리지 않겠다고 엄숙히 맹세하라고 다그쳤습니다.\""]], [["“Noirtier!”", "\"누아르티에라!\" 신부가 되뇌었다."], ["repeated the abbé; “Noirtier!—I knew a person of that name at the court of the Queen of Etruria,—a Noirtier, who had been a Girondin during the Revolution!", "\"누아르티에! 나는 에트루리아 여왕의 궁정에서 그 이름을 가진 사람을 알았네. 혁명 때 지롱드파였던 누아르티에라는 자였지!"], ["What was your deputy called?”", "자네를 심문한 그 대리인의 이름이 뭐였나?\""]], [["“De Villefort!” The abbé burst into a fit of laughter, while Dantès gazed on him in utter astonishment.", "\"드 빌포르였습니다!\" 신부는 와락 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 당테스는 어안이 벙벙해져 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“What ails you?”", "\"왜 그러십니까?\""], ["said he at length.", "그가 마침내 물었다."]], [["“Do you see that ray of sunlight?”", "\"저 햇살이 보이나?\" \"보입니다.\""]], [["“I do.” “Well, the whole thing is more clear to me than that sunbeam is to you.", "\"음, 이 모든 일이 내게는 저 햇살이 자네에게 보이는 것보다 더 환하게 보이네."], ["Poor fellow!", "가엾은 사람!"], ["poor young man!", "가엾은 젊은이!"], ["And you tell me this magistrate expressed great sympathy and commiseration for you?”", "그런데 자네는 그 판사가 자네에게 큰 동정과 연민을 보였다고 했지?\""]], [["“He did.”", "\"그랬습니다.\""]], [["“And the worthy man destroyed your compromising letter?”", "\"그 갸륵한 양반이 자네에게 불리한 편지를 없앴고?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“And then made you swear never to utter the name of Noirtier?”", "\"그러고는 누아르티에라는 이름을 절대 입에 올리지 않겠다고 맹세하게 했고?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Why, you poor short-sighted simpleton, can you not guess who this Noirtier was, whose very name he was so careful to keep concealed? This Noirtier was his father!”", "\"이런, 가엾고 눈먼 어수룩한 친구야, 그가 그토록 조심스레 숨기려 했던 그 누아르티에가 누구인지 짐작도 못 하겠나? 그 누아르티에가 바로 그의 아버지였네!\""]], [["Had a thunderbolt fallen at the feet of Dantès, or hell opened its yawning gulf before him, he could not have been more completely transfixed with horror than he was at the sound of these unexpected words.", "벼락이 당테스의 발치에 떨어졌거나 지옥이 그의 앞에 아가리를 쩍 벌렸더라도, 이 뜻밖의 말을 들었을 때보다 더 완전히 공포에 사로잡히지는 않았을 것이다."], ["Starting up, he clasped his hands around his head as though to prevent his very brain from bursting, and exclaimed, “His father!", "벌떡 일어선 그는 머릿속이 터져 나가는 것을 막으려는 듯 두 손으로 머리를 감싸 쥐며 부르짖었다. \"그의 아버지!"], ["his father!”", "그의 아버지라고요!\""]], [["“Yes, his father,”", "\"그래, 그의 아버지지.\""], ["replied the abbé; “his right name was Noirtier de Villefort.”", "신부가 대답했다. \"그자의 본명은 누아르티에 드 빌포르였네.\""]], [["At this instant a bright light shot through the mind of Dantès, and cleared up all that had been dark and obscure before.", "이 순간, 한 줄기 밝은 빛이 당테스의 머릿속을 꿰뚫으며, 여태 어둡고 막연했던 모든 것을 환히 밝혀 주었다."], ["The change that had come over Villefort during the examination, the destruction of the letter, the exacted promise, the almost supplicating tones of the magistrate, who seemed rather to implore mercy than to pronounce punishment,—all returned with a stunning force to his memory.", "심문 도중 빌포르에게 일어난 변화, 편지를 없앤 일, 받아 낸 약속, 처벌을 선고한다기보다 차라리 자비를 간청하는 듯하던 그 판사의 애원하는 목소리까지, 모든 것이 충격적인 힘으로 그의 기억에 되살아났다."], ["He cried out, and staggered against the wall like a drunken man, then he hurried to the opening that led from the abbé’s cell to his own, and said, “I must be alone, to think over all this.”", "그는 비명을 지르며 술 취한 사람처럼 벽에 비틀거리며 기댔다가, 신부의 독방에서 자기 방으로 통하는 입구로 서둘러 가서 말했다. \"혼자 있어야겠습니다. 이 모든 걸 곰곰이 생각해 봐야 해요.\""]], [["When he regained his dungeon, he threw himself on his bed, where the turnkey found him in the evening visit, sitting with fixed gaze and contracted features, dumb and motionless as a statue.", "자기 감방으로 돌아온 그는 침대에 몸을 던졌고, 저녁 순찰을 돌던 간수가 그를 발견했을 때, 그는 시선을 한곳에 고정한 채 일그러진 얼굴로 앉아 있었다. 동상처럼 말 없고 미동도 없었다."], ["During these hours of profound meditation, which to him had seemed only minutes, he had formed a fearful resolution, and bound himself to its fulfilment by a solemn oath.", "그에게는 그저 몇 분처럼 느껴진 그 깊은 사색의 시간 동안, 그는 무시무시한 결심을 굳혔고, 엄숙한 맹세로 그것을 반드시 이루리라 스스로 다짐했다."]], [["Dantès was at length roused from his reverie by the voice of Faria, who, having also been visited by his jailer, had come to invite his fellow-sufferer to share his supper.", "마침내 당테스는 파리아의 목소리에 상념에서 깨어났다. 파리아 역시 간수의 순찰을 받은 뒤, 함께 고통받는 벗을 저녁 식사에 초대하러 온 것이었다."], ["The reputation of being out of his mind, though harmlessly and even amusingly so, had procured for the abbé unusual privileges.", "비록 해롭지 않고 심지어 재미있는 쪽이긴 했지만, 정신이 나갔다는 평판 덕분에 신부는 보기 드문 특권을 누리고 있었다."], ["He was supplied with bread of a finer, whiter quality than the usual prison fare, and even regaled each Sunday with a small quantity of wine.", "그에게는 여느 감옥 식사보다 곱고 흰 빵이 나왔고, 일요일마다 약간의 포도주까지 곁들여졌다."], ["Now this was a Sunday, and the abbé had come to ask his young companion to share the luxuries with him.", "마침 그날이 일요일이었고, 신부는 젊은 벗에게 이 호사를 함께 누리자고 청하러 온 것이었다."]], [["Dantès followed him; his features were no longer contracted, and now wore their usual expression, but there was that in his whole appearance that bespoke one who had come to a fixed and desperate resolve.", "당테스는 그를 따라갔다. 그의 얼굴은 더 이상 일그러져 있지 않았고, 이제 평소의 표정을 되찾고 있었다. 하지만 그의 온 모습에는 굳고 절박한 결심에 다다른 사람의 기색이 어려 있었다."], ["Faria bent on him his penetrating eye.", "파리아는 꿰뚫는 듯한 눈길을 그에게 던졌다."]], [["“I regret now,”", "\"이제 와 후회가 되는군.\""], ["said he, “having helped you in your late inquiries, or having given you the information I did.”", "그가 말했다. \"조금 전 자네 추궁을 도왔던 것이, 또 내가 그런 정보를 주었던 것이 말일세.\""]], [["“Why so?”", "\"어째서요?\""], ["inquired Dantès.", "당테스가 물었다."]], [["“Because it has instilled a new passion in your heart—that of vengeance.”", "\"그것이 자네 마음에 새로운 열정을 심어 놓았으니까. 바로 복수라는 열정 말이야.\""]], [["Dantès smiled. “Let us talk of something else,”", "당테스가 미소 지었다. \"다른 이야기를 하시지요.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["Again the abbé looked at him, then mournfully shook his head; but in accordance with Dantès’ request, he began to speak of other matters.", "신부는 다시 한번 그를 바라보더니, 서글프게 고개를 저었다. 하지만 당테스의 청에 따라 다른 이야기를 꺼내기 시작했다."], ["The elder prisoner was one of those persons whose conversation, like that of all who have experienced many trials, contained many useful and important hints as well as sound information; but it was never egotistical, for the unfortunate man never alluded to his own sorrows.", "이 나이 든 죄수는, 숱한 시련을 겪은 모든 이가 그러하듯, 그의 대화 속에 건전한 지식뿐 아니라 유익하고 중요한 가르침이 가득한 사람이었다. 하지만 그것은 결코 제 자랑이 아니었다. 이 불행한 사람은 자신의 슬픔을 결코 입에 올리지 않았기 때문이다."], ["Dantès listened with admiring attention to all he said; some of his remarks corresponded with what he already knew, or applied to the sort of knowledge his nautical life had enabled him to acquire.", "당테스는 그가 하는 모든 말을 감탄 어린 주의를 기울여 들었다. 그의 말 가운데 더러는 당테스가 이미 알고 있던 것과 들어맞았고, 더러는 그가 뱃사람 생활로 익힌 지식과 통했다."], ["A part of the good abbé’s words, however, were wholly incomprehensible to him; but, like the aurora which guides the navigator in northern latitudes, opened new vistas to the inquiring mind of the listener, and gave fantastic glimpses of new horizons, enabling him justly to estimate the delight an intellectual mind would have in following one so richly gifted as Faria along the heights of truth, where he was so much at home.", "그러나 선량한 신부의 말 가운데 일부는 당테스로서는 도무지 이해할 수 없는 것이었다. 그럼에도 그 말들은, 북쪽 바다에서 항해사를 인도하는 오로라처럼, 알고자 하는 청자의 마음에 새로운 전망을 열어 주었다. 그리고 새 지평을 환상적으로 엿보게 해 주어, 파리아처럼 풍성한 재능을 지닌 사람이 마치 제집처럼 노니는 진리의 높은 곳을 따라가는 일에서, 지성을 갖춘 정신이 누릴 기쁨이 어떤 것인지를 그가 제대로 가늠할 수 있게 해 주었다."]], [["“You must teach me a small part of what you know,”", "\"신부님이 아시는 것의 작은 부분이라도 제게 가르쳐 주십시오.\""], ["said Dantès, “if only to prevent your growing weary of me.", "당테스가 말했다. \"신부님이 저를 지겨워하지 않으시도록 말입니다."], ["I can well believe that so learned a person as yourself would prefer absolute solitude to being tormented with the company of one as ignorant and uninformed as myself.", "신부님처럼 학식 높은 분이라면, 저처럼 무지하고 배움 없는 자와 어울려 시달리느니 차라리 완전한 고독을 택하시리라는 걸 잘 압니다."], ["If you will only agree to my request, I promise you never to mention another word about escaping.”", "제 부탁만 들어주신다면, 다시는 탈출 이야기를 입에 올리지 않겠다고 약속드리겠습니다.\""]], [["The abbé smiled.", "신부가 미소 지었다."]], [["“Alas, my boy,”", "\"아아, 얘야,\""], ["said he, “human knowledge is confined within very narrow limits; and when I have taught you mathematics, physics, history, and the three or four modern languages with which I am acquainted, you will know as much as I do myself.", "그가 말했다. \"인간의 지식이란 아주 좁은 한계 안에 갇혀 있단다. 자네에게 수학과 물리, 역사, 그리고 내가 아는 서너 가지 현대어를 가르치고 나면, 자네는 나만큼 알게 될 걸세."], ["Now, it will scarcely require two years for me to communicate to you the stock of learning I possess.”", "그런데 내가 가진 학문의 밑천을 자네에게 전하는 데 채 이 년도 걸리지 않을 거야.\""]], [["“Two years!”", "\"이 년이라고요!\""], ["exclaimed Dantès; “do you really believe I can acquire all these things in so short a time?”", "당테스가 외쳤다. \"이 모든 것을 그렇게 짧은 기간에 익힐 수 있다고 정말 믿으십니까?\""]], [["“Not their application, certainly, but their principles you may; to learn is not to know; there are the learners and the learned.", "\"그 응용까지야 물론 아니지만, 원리는 익힐 수 있지. 배우는 것이 곧 아는 것은 아니야. 배우는 자가 있고, 깨우친 자가 있지."], ["Memory makes the one, philosophy the other.”", "기억이 배우는 자를 만들고, 철학이 깨우친 자를 만드는 법일세.\""]], [["“But cannot one learn philosophy?”", "\"하지만 철학은 배울 수 없습니까?\""]], [["“Philosophy cannot be taught; it is the application of the sciences to truth; it is like the golden cloud in which the Messiah went up into heaven.”", "\"철학은 가르칠 수 있는 것이 아니야. 그것은 학문을 진리에 적용하는 것이지. 메시아가 하늘로 오를 때 타고 간 황금 구름과도 같은 것이라네.\""]], [["“Well, then,”", "\"그럼 좋습니다.\""], ["said Dantès, “What shall you teach me first?", "당테스가 말했다. \"무엇부터 가르쳐 주시겠습니까?"], ["I am in a hurry to begin.", "어서 시작하고 싶습니다."], ["I want to learn.”", "배우고 싶어요.\""]], [["“Everything,”", "\"전부 다.\""], ["said the abbé. And that very evening the prisoners sketched a plan of education, to be entered upon the following day.", "신부가 말했다. 그리고 바로 그날 저녁, 두 죄수는 이튿날부터 시작할 학습 계획을 짰다."], ["Dantès possessed a prodigious memory, combined with an astonishing quickness and readiness of conception; the mathematical turn of his mind rendered him apt at all kinds of calculation, while his naturally poetical feelings threw a light and pleasing veil over the dry reality of arithmetical computation, or the rigid severity of geometry.", "당테스는 경이로운 기억력에다 놀라울 만큼 빠르고 민첩한 이해력을 두루 갖추고 있었다. 수학적인 머리 덕분에 온갖 계산에 능했고, 타고난 시적 감수성은 무미건조한 산수 계산이나 딱딱하고 엄정한 기하학 위에 가볍고 즐거운 베일을 드리워 주었다."], ["He already knew Italian, and had also picked up a little of the Romaic dialect during voyages to the East; and by the aid of these two languages he easily comprehended the construction of all the others, so that at the end of six months he began to speak Spanish, English, and German.", "그는 이미 이탈리아어를 알았고, 동방으로의 항해 중에 로마이어도 조금 익혀 두었다. 이 두 언어의 도움으로 그는 다른 모든 언어의 짜임새를 쉽게 이해했고, 그리하여 여섯 달이 지날 무렵에는 스페인어, 영어, 독일어를 말하기 시작했다."]], [["In strict accordance with the promise made to the abbé, Dantès spoke no more of escape.", "신부와 한 약속을 철저히 지켜, 당테스는 더 이상 탈출 이야기를 입에 올리지 않았다."], ["Perhaps the delight his studies afforded him left no room for such thoughts; perhaps the recollection that he had pledged his word (on which his sense of honor was keen) kept him from referring in any way to the possibilities of flight.", "어쩌면 공부가 안겨 주는 기쁨이 그런 생각이 들어설 자리를 남기지 않았는지도 모른다. 어쩌면 자신이 약속을 했다는 기억이(그는 명예에 대한 감각이 예민했다) 탈출 가능성을 어떤 식으로든 입에 올리지 못하게 막았는지도 모른다."], ["Days, even months, passed by unheeded in one rapid and instructive course.", "빠르고 유익한 한 줄기 과정 속에서 며칠, 아니 몇 달이 알지 못하는 사이에 흘러갔다."], ["At the end of a year Dantès was a new man.", "일 년이 지날 무렵 당테스는 새사람이 되어 있었다."], ["Dantès observed, however, that Faria, in spite of the relief his society afforded, daily grew sadder; one thought seemed incessantly to harass and distract his mind.", "그러나 당테스는, 파리아가 자신의 벗이 되어 주는 위안에도 불구하고 날이 갈수록 더 침울해지는 것을 알아챘다. 한 가지 생각이 끊임없이 그의 마음을 들볶고 어지럽히는 듯했다."], ["Sometimes he would fall into long reveries, sigh heavily and involuntarily, then suddenly rise, and, with folded arms, begin pacing the confined space of his dungeon.", "이따금 그는 긴 상념에 잠겨 무겁고 저도 모르게 한숨을 내쉬다가, 별안간 일어나 팔짱을 끼고 좁은 감방 안을 서성이곤 했다."], ["One day he stopped all at once, and exclaimed:", "어느 날 그는 갑자기 걸음을 멈추더니 외쳤다."]], [["“Ah, if there were no sentinel!”", "\"아, 저 보초만 없다면!\""]], [["“There shall not be one a minute longer than you please,”", "\"신부님이 원하시는 순간, 보초는 단 일 분도 더 거기 있지 못할 겁니다.\""], ["said Dantès, who had followed the working of his thoughts as accurately as though his brain were enclosed in crystal so clear as to display its minutest operations.", "당테스가 말했다. 그는 마치 신부의 머릿속이 가장 미세한 작동까지 들여다보일 만큼 투명한 수정으로 감싸여 있기라도 한 듯, 그 생각의 흐름을 정확히 따라가고 있었다."]], [["“I have already told you,”", "\"전에도 말했지만,\""], ["answered the abbé, “that I loathe the idea of shedding blood.”", "신부가 대답했다. \"나는 피를 흘리게 한다는 생각만으로도 진저리가 나네.\""]], [["“And yet the murder, if you choose to call it so, would be simply a measure of self-preservation.”", "\"하지만 그 살인은, 굳이 그렇게 부르신다면, 그저 자기 보존을 위한 조치일 뿐입니다.\""]], [["“No matter!", "\"그래도 안 돼!"], ["I could never agree to it.”", "나는 결코 거기에 동의할 수 없네.\""]], [["“Still, you have thought of it?”", "\"그래도 그 생각을 해 보신 적은 있지 않습니까?\""]], [["“Incessantly, alas!” cried the abbé.", "\"끊임없이 했지, 안타깝게도!\" 신부가 외쳤다."]], [["“And you have discovered a means of regaining our freedom, have you not?”", "\"그리고 우리가 자유를 되찾을 방법을 찾아내셨지요, 그렇지 않습니까?\""], ["asked Dantès eagerly.", "당테스가 간절히 물었다."]], [["“I have; if it were only possible to place a deaf and blind sentinel in the gallery beyond us.”", "\"찾았네. 우리 너머 회랑에 귀먹고 눈먼 보초를 세워 둘 수만 있다면 말이지.\""]], [["“He shall be both blind and deaf,”", "\"그자를 눈멀고 귀먹게 만들겠습니다.\""], ["replied the young man, with an air of determination that made his companion shudder.", "젊은이가 대답했다. 그 단호한 태도에 벗은 몸서리를 쳤다."]], [["“No, no,”", "\"안 돼, 안 돼.\""], ["cried the abbé; “impossible!”", "신부가 외쳤다. \"그건 안 될 말이야!\""]], [["Dantès endeavored to renew the subject; the abbé shook his head in token of disapproval, and refused to make any further response.", "당테스는 그 이야기를 다시 꺼내려 했지만, 신부는 안 된다는 뜻으로 고개를 저었고, 더는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다."], ["Three months passed away.", "석 달이 흘렀다."]], [["“Are you strong?”", "\"자네, 힘이 센가?\""], ["the abbé asked one day of Dantès.", "어느 날 신부가 당테스에게 물었다."], ["The young man, in reply, took up the chisel, bent it into the form of a horseshoe, and then as readily straightened it.", "젊은이는 대답 대신 끌을 집어 들어 말굽 모양으로 구부렸다가, 다시 그만큼 손쉽게 곧게 폈다."]], [["“And will you engage not to do any harm to the sentry, except as a last resort?”", "\"그리고 최후의 수단이 아니고서는 보초에게 어떤 해도 끼치지 않겠다고 약속하겠나?\""]], [["“I promise on my honor.”", "\"명예를 걸고 약속합니다.\""]], [["“Then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["said the abbé, “we may hope to put our design into execution.”", "신부가 말했다. \"우리 계획을 실행에 옮길 수 있겠군.\""]], [["“And how long shall we be in accomplishing the necessary work?”", "\"그 일을 끝내는 데 얼마나 걸릴까요?\""]], [["“At least a year.”", "\"적어도 일 년은.\""]], [["“And shall we begin at once?”", "\"그럼 당장 시작할까요?\""]], [["“At once.”", "\"당장.\""]], [["“We have lost a year to no purpose!”", "\"우리가 일 년을 헛되이 날렸군요!\""], ["cried Dantès.", "당테스가 외쳤다."]], [["“Do you consider the last twelve months to have been wasted?”", "\"지난 열두 달이 헛되이 보낸 것이라 생각하나?\""], ["asked the abbé.", "신부가 물었다."]], [["“Forgive me!”", "\"용서하십시오!\""], ["cried Edmond, blushing deeply.", "에드몽이 얼굴을 새빨갛게 붉히며 외쳤다."]], [["“Tut, tut!”", "\"쯧쯧!\""], ["answered the abbé, “man is but man after all, and you are about the best specimen of the genus I have ever known.", "신부가 대답했다. \"인간은 결국 인간일 뿐이고, 자네는 내가 아는 한 그 종족 가운데 가장 훌륭한 본보기일세."], ["Come, let me show you my plan.”", "자, 내 계획을 보여 주지.\""]], [["The abbé then showed Dantès the sketch he had made for their escape.", "그리하여 신부는 탈출을 위해 그려 둔 도면을 당테스에게 보여 주었다."], ["It consisted of a plan of his own cell and that of Dantès, with the passage which united them.", "그것은 자신의 감방과 당테스의 감방, 그리고 그 둘을 잇는 통로를 그린 도면이었다."], ["In this passage he proposed to drive a level as they do in mines; this level would bring the two prisoners immediately beneath the gallery where the sentry kept watch; once there, a large excavation would be made, and one of the flag-stones with which the gallery was paved be so completely loosened that at the desired moment it would give way beneath the feet of the soldier, who, stunned by his fall, would be immediately bound and gagged by Dantès before he had power to offer any resistance.", "그는 광산에서 하듯이 이 통로에 수평 갱도를 뚫을 작정이었다. 이 갱도는 두 죄수를 보초가 망을 보는 회랑 바로 밑까지 데려다줄 터였다. 일단 거기에 이르면, 큰 구덩이를 파고, 회랑 바닥에 깔린 포석 하나를 완전히 헐겁게 만들어, 원하는 순간에 그것이 병사의 발밑에서 꺼지게 만드는 것이었다."], ["The prisoners were then to make their way through one of the gallery windows, and to let themselves down from the outer walls by means of the abbé’s ladder of cords.", "그러면 떨어지면서 정신을 잃은 병사를, 그가 저항할 새도 없이 당테스가 곧바로 결박하고 재갈을 물릴 참이었다. 그런 다음 두 죄수는 회랑 창문 중 하나로 빠져나가, 신부의 밧줄 사다리를 이용해 바깥벽을 타고 내려갈 것이었다."]], [["Dantès’ eyes sparkled with joy, and he rubbed his hands with delight at the idea of a plan so simple, yet apparently so certain to succeed.", "당테스의 눈이 기쁨으로 반짝였고, 단순하면서도 보기에는 틀림없이 성공할 듯한 그 계획에 신이 나서 두 손을 비볐다."], ["That very day the miners began their labors, with a vigor and alacrity proportionate to their long rest from fatigue and their hopes of ultimate success.", "바로 그날 광부들은 작업에 착수했고, 오랜 휴식으로 쌓인 기력과 끝내 성공하리라는 희망에 걸맞은 활력과 민첩함으로 임했다."], ["Nothing interrupted the progress of the work except the necessity that each was under of returning to his cell in anticipation of the turnkey’s visits.", "간수의 순찰에 대비해 각자 자기 감방으로 돌아가야 하는 것 말고는, 아무것도 작업의 진척을 방해하지 않았다."], ["They had learned to distinguish the almost imperceptible sound of his footsteps as he descended towards their dungeons, and happily, never failed of being prepared for his coming.", "그들은 간수가 그들의 감방을 향해 내려올 때 들리는, 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 희미한 발소리를 분간하는 법을 익혔고, 다행히 그가 올 때를 대비하지 못한 적이 한 번도 없었다."], ["The fresh earth excavated during their present work, and which would have entirely blocked up the old passage, was thrown, by degrees and with the utmost precaution, out of the window in either Faria’s or Dantès’ cell, the rubbish being first pulverized so finely that the night wind carried it far away without permitting the smallest trace to remain.", "이번 작업에서 파낸 새 흙은 옛 통로를 죄다 막아 버렸을 텐데, 파리아나 당테스의 감방 창문 밖으로 조금씩, 더없이 조심스럽게 내버렸다. 그 부스러기 흙을 먼저 아주 곱게 빻아 두어, 밤바람이 그것을 멀리 날려 보내, 아주 작은 흔적조차 남기지 않게 했다."]], [["More than a year had been consumed in this undertaking, the only tools for which had been a chisel, a knife, and a wooden lever; Faria still continuing to instruct Dantès by conversing with him, sometimes in one language, sometimes in another; at others, relating to him the history of nations and great men who from time to time have risen to fame and trodden the path of glory.", "이 작업에 일 년 넘는 시간이 들었는데, 연장이라고는 끌 하나, 칼 하나, 그리고 나무 지렛대 하나가 전부였다. 그동안에도 파리아는 당테스와 이야기를 나누며 그를 계속 가르쳤다. 어떤 때는 이 언어로, 어떤 때는 저 언어로, 또 어떤 때는 이따금 명성을 얻어 영광의 길을 걸은 민족들과 위인들의 역사를 들려주었다."], ["The abbé was a man of the world, and had, moreover, mixed in the first society of the day; he wore an air of melancholy dignity which Dantès, thanks to the imitative powers bestowed on him by nature, easily acquired, as well as that outward polish and politeness he had before been wanting in, and which is seldom possessed except by those who have been placed in constant intercourse with persons of high birth and breeding.", "신부는 세상 물정에 밝은 사람이었고, 더구나 당대의 일류 사교계에 몸담은 적도 있었다. 그에게는 우수에 잠긴 기품 같은 것이 어려 있었는데, 당테스는 타고난 모방력 덕분에 그것을 쉽게 익혔다. 또한 전에는 갖추지 못했던 외양의 세련됨과 예의범절도 익혔는데, 그것은 높은 가문과 좋은 교양을 지닌 사람들과 끊임없이 어울려 본 자라야 좀처럼 지닐 수 있는 것이었다."]], [["At the end of fifteen months the level was finished, and the excavation completed beneath the gallery, and the two workmen could distinctly hear the measured tread of the sentinel as he paced to and fro over their heads.", "열다섯 달이 지날 무렵 수평 갱도가 완성되었고, 회랑 밑의 구덩이도 다 파였다. 두 일꾼은 보초가 그들의 머리 위를 오가며 내딛는 일정한 발소리를 또렷이 들을 수 있었다."], ["Compelled, as they were, to await a night sufficiently dark to favor their flight, they were obliged to defer their final attempt till that auspicious moment should arrive; their greatest dread now was lest the stone through which the sentry was doomed to fall should give way before its right time, and this they had in some measure provided against by propping it up with a small beam which they had discovered in the walls through which they had worked their way.", "탈출에 알맞은 어두운 밤을 기다려야 하는 처지였기에, 그 좋은 순간이 올 때까지 마지막 결행을 미룰 수밖에 없었다. 이제 그들의 가장 큰 두려움은, 보초가 떨어지게 되어 있는 그 돌이 제때보다 먼저 무너지지나 않을까 하는 것이었다. 그래서 그들은 작업하며 파고든 벽에서 찾아낸 작은 들보로 그 돌을 받쳐, 어느 정도 대비를 해 두었다."], ["Dantès was occupied in arranging this piece of wood when he heard Faria, who had remained in Edmond’s cell for the purpose of cutting a peg to secure their rope-ladder, call to him in a tone indicative of great suffering.", "당테스가 이 나무토막을 손보고 있을 때, 밧줄 사다리를 고정할 못을 깎으려고 에드몽의 감방에 남아 있던 파리아가 큰 고통이 묻어나는 목소리로 그를 부르는 소리를 들었다."], ["Dantès hastened to his dungeon, where he found him standing in the middle of the room, pale as death, his forehead streaming with perspiration, and his hands clenched tightly together.", "당테스는 서둘러 그의 감방으로 달려갔고, 거기서 방 한가운데 서 있는 그를 발견했다. 죽은 사람처럼 창백하고, 이마에서는 땀이 줄줄 흐르고, 두 손은 꽉 움켜쥐고 있었다."]], [["“Gracious heavens!”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["exclaimed Dantès, “what is the matter?", "당테스가 외쳤다. \"무슨 일입니까?"], ["what has happened?”", "무슨 일이 있었던 거예요?\""]], [["“Quick! quick!”", "\"빨리! 빨리!\""], ["returned the abbé, “listen to what I have to say.”", "신부가 대답했다. \"내 말을 잘 듣게.\""]], [["Dantès looked in fear and wonder at the livid countenance of Faria, whose eyes, already dull and sunken, were surrounded by purple circles, while his lips were white as those of a corpse, and his very hair seemed to stand on end.", "당테스는 두려움과 놀라움에 차서 파리아의 새파랗게 질린 얼굴을 들여다보았다. 이미 흐릿하고 움푹 꺼진 그의 두 눈은 자줏빛 테두리에 둘러싸여 있었고, 입술은 시체의 것처럼 하얬으며, 머리카락마저 곤두서는 듯했다."]], [["“Tell me, I beseech you, what ails you?”", "\"제발, 무엇이 잘못된 건지 말씀해 주세요!\""], ["cried Dantès, letting his chisel fall to the floor.", "당테스가 끌을 바닥에 떨어뜨리며 외쳤다."]], [["“Alas,”", "\"아아,\""], ["faltered out the abbé, “all is over with me.", "신부가 더듬더듬 말했다. \"나는 이제 끝났네."], ["I am seized with a terrible, perhaps mortal illness; I can feel that the paroxysm is fast approaching.", "무서운 병에, 어쩌면 목숨을 앗아 갈 병에 사로잡혔어. 발작이 빠르게 다가오는 게 느껴진다네. 갇히기 전해에도 비슷한 발작을 겪었지."], ["I had a similar attack the year previous to my imprisonment.", "이 병에는 단 하나의 약밖에 없어. 그게 뭔지 말해 주겠네."], ["This malady admits but of one remedy; I will tell you what that is.", "되도록 빨리 내 감방으로 가게. 침대를 받치는 다리 하나를 빼내 보게."], ["Go into my cell as quickly as you can; draw out one of the feet that support the bed; you will find it has been hollowed out for the purpose of containing a small phial you will see there half-filled with a red-looking fluid.", "그 안이 일부러 비워져 있어서, 거기 작은 약병이 보일 걸세. 불그스름한 액체가 반쯤 차 있지. 그걸 내게 가져오게나."], ["Bring it to me—or rather—no, no!—I may be found here, therefore help me back to my room while I have the strength to drag myself along.", "아니, 그보다는, 안 돼, 안 돼! 여기서 들킬 수도 있어. 그러니 내가 몸을 끌고 갈 힘이 남아 있는 동안 나를 내 방으로 데려가 주게."], ["Who knows what may happen, or how long the attack may last?”", "무슨 일이 일어날지, 발작이 얼마나 갈지 누가 알겠나?\""]], [["In spite of the magnitude of the misfortune which thus suddenly frustrated his hopes, Dantès did not lose his presence of mind, but descended into the passage, dragging his unfortunate companion with him; then, half-carrying, half-supporting him, he managed to reach the abbé’s chamber, when he immediately laid the sufferer on his bed.", "이렇게 갑작스레 자신의 희망이 좌절되는 크나큰 불행 앞에서도 당테스는 침착함을 잃지 않았다. 그는 불행한 벗을 끌며 통로로 내려갔다. 그러고는 반쯤 안고 반쯤 부축해 가까스로 신부의 방에 이르렀고, 곧바로 환자를 침대에 눕혔다."]], [["“Thanks,”", "\"고맙네.\""], ["said the poor abbé, shivering as though his veins were filled with ice.", "가엾은 신부가 핏줄에 얼음이라도 가득 찬 듯 부들부들 떨며 말했다."], ["“I am about to be seized with a fit of catalepsy; when it comes to its height I shall probably lie still and motionless as though dead, uttering neither sigh nor groan.", "\"이제 곧 강경증 발작이 일어날 거야. 그게 절정에 이르면, 나는 아마 죽은 사람처럼 미동도 없이 누운 채, 한숨 한 번 신음 한 번 내지 못하겠지."], ["On the other hand, the symptoms may be much more violent, and cause me to fall into fearful convulsions, foam at the mouth, and cry out loudly.", "반대로 증상이 훨씬 더 격렬해서, 무서운 경련을 일으키고 입에 거품을 물고 크게 비명을 지를 수도 있어."], ["Take care my cries are not heard, for if they are it is more than probable I should be removed to another part of the prison, and we be separated forever.", "내 비명이 들리지 않도록 조심하게. 만일 들리면, 십중팔구 나는 감옥 다른 곳으로 옮겨질 테고, 우리는 영영 헤어지게 될 테니까."], ["When I become quite motionless, cold, and rigid as a corpse, then, and not before,—be careful about this,—force open my teeth with the knife, pour from eight to ten drops of the liquor contained in the phial down my throat, and I may perhaps revive.”", "내가 완전히 미동도 없이, 차갑고 뻣뻣하게 시체처럼 굳어지면, 그때, 그 전에는 절대 안 되네, 이 점을 명심하게, 칼로 내 이를 억지로 벌리고, 약병에 든 약을 여덟에서 열 방울쯤 목구멍에 흘려 넣게. 그러면 어쩌면 내가 다시 깨어날지도 몰라.\""]], [["“Perhaps!” exclaimed Dantès in grief-stricken tones.", "\"어쩌면이라니요!\" 당테스가 비통한 목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“Help!", "\"사람 살려!"], ["help!”", "사람 살려!\""], ["cried the abbé, “I—I—die—I——” 0229m", "신부가 부르짖었다. \"나, 나는, 죽는다, 나는……\""]], [["So sudden and violent was the fit that the unfortunate prisoner was unable to complete the sentence; a violent convulsion shook his whole frame, his eyes started from their sockets, his mouth was drawn on one side, his cheeks became purple, he struggled, foamed, dashed himself about, and uttered the most dreadful cries, which, however, Dantès prevented from being heard by covering his head with the blanket. The fit lasted two hours; then, more helpless than an infant, and colder and paler than marble, more crushed and broken than a reed trampled under foot, he fell back, doubled up in one last convulsion, and became as rigid as a corpse.", "발작이 어찌나 갑작스럽고 격렬한지, 불행한 죄수는 그 문장을 채 끝맺지 못했다. 격렬한 경련이 그의 온몸을 뒤흔들었고, 두 눈이 눈구멍에서 튀어나올 듯했으며, 입은 한쪽으로 비뚤어지고, 두 뺨은 자줏빛으로 물들었다. 그는 발버둥치고, 거품을 물고, 몸을 마구 내던지며, 더없이 끔찍한 비명을 질러 댔다. 그러나 당테스가 담요로 그의 머리를 덮어, 그 소리가 새어 나가지 못하게 막았다. 발작은 두 시간 동안 이어졌다. 그런 뒤 그는 갓난아기보다 더 무력하게, 대리석보다 더 차갑고 창백하게, 발에 짓밟힌 갈대보다 더 으스러지고 부러진 채, 마지막 경련에 몸을 잔뜩 웅크리며 뒤로 쓰러졌다. 그리고 시체처럼 뻣뻣하게 굳어졌다."]], [["Edmond waited till life seemed extinct in the body of his friend, then, taking up the knife, he with difficulty forced open the closely fixed jaws, carefully administered the appointed number of drops, and anxiously awaited the result.", "에드몽은 벗의 몸에서 생명이 다한 듯 보일 때까지 기다렸다가, 칼을 집어 들고 굳게 다물린 턱을 가까스로 벌렸다. 그러고는 정해진 방울 수만큼 조심스레 약을 흘려 넣고, 애타게 결과를 기다렸다."], ["An hour passed away and the old man gave no sign of returning animation.", "한 시간이 지났지만 노인은 다시 살아난다는 기미를 전혀 보이지 않았다."], ["Dantès began to fear he had delayed too long ere he administered the remedy, and, thrusting his hands into his hair, continued gazing on the lifeless features of his friend.", "당테스는 약을 너무 늦게 먹인 것은 아닐까 두려워지기 시작했고, 머리카락에 두 손을 찔러 넣은 채 벗의 죽은 듯한 얼굴을 계속 들여다보았다."], ["At length a slight color tinged the livid cheeks, consciousness returned to the dull, open eyeballs, a faint sigh issued from the lips, and the sufferer made a feeble effort to move.", "마침내 새파랗던 두 뺨에 옅은 핏기가 돌고, 흐릿하게 뜬 눈동자에 의식이 되돌아왔으며, 입술에서 가느다란 한숨이 새어 나왔다. 그리고 환자는 가까스로 몸을 움직이려 애썼다."]], [["“He is saved! he is saved!”", "\"살았다! 살았어!\""], ["cried Dantès in a paroxysm of delight.", "당테스가 미칠 듯한 기쁨에 차서 외쳤다."]], [["The sick man was not yet able to speak, but he pointed with evident anxiety towards the door.", "병자는 아직 말을 할 수 없었지만, 역력한 불안을 담아 문 쪽을 가리켰다."], ["Dantès listened, and plainly distinguished the approaching steps of the jailer.", "당테스가 귀를 기울이자, 간수가 다가오는 발소리가 또렷이 들렸다."], ["It was therefore near seven o’clock; but Edmond’s anxiety had put all thoughts of time out of his head.", "그러니 일곱 시가 가까웠던 것이다. 그러나 에드몽은 불안에 사로잡혀 시간 따위는 까맣게 잊고 있었다."]], [["The young man sprang to the entrance, darted through it, carefully drawing the stone over the opening, and hurried to his cell.", "젊은이는 입구로 뛰어가 그 사이로 빠져나간 뒤, 조심스레 돌을 끌어다 구멍을 덮고 서둘러 자기 감방으로 돌아갔다."], ["He had scarcely done so before the door opened, and the jailer saw the prisoner seated as usual on the side of his bed.", "그가 그러기 무섭게 문이 열렸고, 간수는 죄수가 여느 때처럼 침대 한쪽에 앉아 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Almost before the key had turned in the lock, and before the departing steps of the jailer had died away in the long corridor he had to traverse, Dantès, whose restless anxiety concerning his friend left him no desire to touch the food brought him, hurried back to the abbé’s chamber, and raising the stone by pressing his head against it, was soon beside the sick man’s couch.", "자물쇠에서 열쇠가 돌아가기가 무섭게, 간수가 거쳐 가야 할 긴 복도에서 그 멀어지는 발소리가 채 잦아들기도 전에, 벗에 대한 들끓는 불안 탓에 가져다준 음식에는 손댈 마음도 없던 당테스는 서둘러 신부의 방으로 돌아갔다. 그러고는 돌에 머리를 들이대 밀어 올리고, 이내 병자의 침상 곁에 다다랐다."], ["Faria had now fully regained his consciousness, but he still lay helpless and exhausted on his miserable bed.", "파리아는 이제 의식을 완전히 되찾았지만, 여전히 비참한 침대 위에 무력하고 기진맥진한 채 누워 있었다."]], [["“I did not expect to see you again,”", "\"자네를 다시 보게 될 줄은 몰랐네.\""], ["said he feebly, to Dantès.", "그가 힘없이 당테스에게 말했다."]], [["“And why not?”", "\"어째서요?\""], ["asked the young man.", "젊은이가 물었다."], ["“Did you fancy yourself dying?”", "\"신부님이 돌아가시는 줄 아셨습니까?\""]], [["“No, I had no such idea; but, knowing that all was ready for flight, I thought you might have made your escape.”", "\"아니, 그런 생각은 아니었네. 다만 탈출 준비가 다 되어 있다는 걸 아니까, 자네가 도망쳤을지도 모른다고 생각했지.\""]], [["The deep glow of indignation suffused the cheeks of Dantès.", "당테스의 두 뺨에 분노의 진한 홍조가 번졌다."]], [["“Without you?", "\"신부님을 두고요?"], ["Did you really think me capable of that?”", "정말 제가 그럴 사람이라고 생각하셨습니까?\""]], [["“At least,”", "\"적어도,\""], ["said the abbé, “I now see how wrong such an opinion would have been.", "신부가 말했다. \"그런 생각이 얼마나 잘못된 것이었는지 이제 알겠네."], ["Alas, alas!", "아아, 아아!"], ["I am fearfully exhausted and debilitated by this attack.”", "이번 발작으로 나는 무섭도록 기진하고 쇠약해졌네.\""]], [["“Be of good cheer,”", "\"기운을 내십시오.\""], ["replied Dantès; “your strength will return.”", "당테스가 대답했다. \"기력은 되돌아올 겁니다.\""], ["And as he spoke he seated himself near the bed beside Faria, and took his hands.", "그렇게 말하며 그는 침대 곁 파리아 옆에 앉아 그의 두 손을 잡았다."], ["The abbé shook his head.", "신부가 고개를 저었다."]], [["“The last attack I had,”", "\"지난번 발작은,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “lasted but half an hour, and after it I was hungry, and got up without help; now I can move neither my right arm nor leg, and my head seems uncomfortable, which shows that there has been a suffusion of blood on the brain.", "\"겨우 반 시간밖에 가지 않았네. 그러고 나서는 배가 고팠고, 부축 없이도 일어났지. 한데 지금은 오른팔도 오른다리도 움직일 수가 없고, 머릿속이 거북한 게, 뇌에 피가 번졌다는 뜻이야."], ["The third attack will either carry me off, or leave me paralyzed for life.”", "세 번째 발작이 오면 나는 죽거나, 아니면 평생 마비된 채로 남을 걸세.\""]], [["“No, no,”", "\"아니요, 아니요.\""], ["cried Dantès; “you are mistaken—you will not die!", "당테스가 외쳤다. \"잘못 생각하시는 겁니다. 신부님은 죽지 않으실 거예요!"], ["And your third attack (if, indeed, you should have another) will find you at liberty.", "그리고 세 번째 발작이(정말 또 온다 하더라도) 닥칠 때, 신부님은 자유로운 몸이실 겁니다."], ["We shall save you another time, as we have done this, only with a better chance of success, because we shall be able to command every requisite assistance.”", "이번처럼 우리가 다시 신부님을 구할 거예요. 다만 그때는 더 큰 성공의 가망을 안고서요. 필요한 모든 도움을 마음대로 부를 수 있을 테니까요.\""]], [["“My good Edmond,”", "\"착한 에드몽,\""], ["answered the abbé, “be not deceived.", "신부가 대답했다. \"착각하지 말게."], ["The attack which has just passed away, condemns me forever to the walls of a prison. None can fly from a dungeon who cannot walk.”", "방금 지나간 이 발작이 나를 영영 감옥 벽 안에 가두었네. 걷지 못하는 자는 누구도 감옥에서 달아날 수 없는 법이야.\""]], [["“Well, we will wait,—a week, a month, two months, if need be,—and meanwhile your strength will return.", "\"그럼 기다리지요. 한 주든, 한 달이든, 필요하다면 두 달이든요. 그동안 신부님 기력은 되돌아올 겁니다."], ["Everything is in readiness for our flight, and we can select any time we choose.", "탈출 채비는 다 갖춰져 있으니, 우리가 원하는 때를 고르면 됩니다."], ["As soon as you feel able to swim we will go.”", "신부님이 헤엄칠 수 있다고 느껴지자마자 떠나면 돼요.\""]], [["“I shall never swim again,”", "\"나는 다시는 헤엄치지 못할 걸세.\""], ["replied Faria.", "파리아가 대답했다."], ["“This arm is paralyzed; not for a time, but forever.", "\"이 팔은 마비됐어. 한동안이 아니라 영영 말이야."], ["Lift it, and judge if I am mistaken.”", "들어 보게, 내가 잘못 짚은 건지 판단해 보게.\""]], [["The young man raised the arm, which fell back by its own weight, perfectly inanimate and helpless.", "젊은이가 그 팔을 들어 올리자, 팔은 제 무게에 못 이겨 도로 툭 떨어졌다. 완전히 생기를 잃고 무력했다."], ["A sigh escaped him.", "그의 입에서 한숨이 새어 나왔다."]], [["“You are convinced now, Edmond, are you not?”", "\"이제 자네도 수긍이 가지, 에드몽, 그렇지 않은가?\""], ["asked the abbé.", "신부가 물었다."], ["“Depend upon it, I know what I say. Since the first attack I experienced of this malady, I have continually reflected on it.", "\"내 말을 믿게, 나는 내가 무슨 말을 하는지 알고 있네. 이 병으로 첫 발작을 겪은 뒤로, 나는 끊임없이 그것을 곱씹어 왔어."], ["Indeed, I expected it, for it is a family inheritance; both my father and grandfather died of it in a third attack.", "사실 나는 이미 예상하고 있었지. 이건 집안 내력이거든. 내 아버지도 할아버지도 세 번째 발작에 돌아가셨으니까."], ["The physician who prepared for me the remedy I have twice successfully taken, was no other than the celebrated Cabanis, and he predicted a similar end for me.” 0233m", "내가 두 번이나 효험을 본 그 약을 지어 준 의사는 다름 아닌 저 유명한 카바니스였는데, 그도 내게 같은 최후를 예언했다네.\""]], [["“The physician may be mistaken!”", "\"의사가 틀릴 수도 있지 않습니까!\""], ["exclaimed Dantès.", "당테스가 외쳤다."], ["“And as for your poor arm, what difference will that make?", "\"그리고 그 가엾은 팔이야, 그게 무슨 문제가 되겠습니까?"], ["I can take you on my shoulders, and swim for both of us.”", "제가 신부님을 어깨에 짊어지고, 우리 둘 몫을 헤엄치면 됩니다.\""]], [["“My son,”", "\"얘야,\""], ["said the abbé, “you, who are a sailor and a swimmer, must know as well as I do that a man so loaded would sink before he had done fifty strokes.", "신부가 말했다. \"뱃사람이자 헤엄꾼인 자네라면 나만큼이나 잘 알 게 아닌가. 그렇게 짐을 진 사람은 채 쉰 번도 팔을 젓기 전에 가라앉고 만다는 걸 말이야."], ["Cease, then, to allow yourself to be duped by vain hopes, that even your own excellent heart refuses to believe in.", "그러니 자네의 그 갸륵한 마음조차 믿으려 들지 않는 헛된 희망에 더는 자네 자신을 속이지 말게."], ["Here I shall remain till the hour of my deliverance arrives, and that, in all human probability, will be the hour of my death.", "나는 풀려날 시간이 올 때까지 여기 남을 걸세. 그리고 그 시간이란, 인간으로서 헤아릴 수 있는 모든 가망으로 보아, 내가 죽는 시간일 테지."], ["As for you, who are young and active, delay not on my account, but fly—go—I give you back your promise.”", "젊고 활기찬 자네는, 나 때문에 지체하지 말고 달아나게, 떠나게. 자네 약속은 내가 도로 거두어 주겠네.\""]], [["“It is well,”", "\"좋습니다.\""], ["said Dantès.", "당테스가 말했다."], ["“Then I shall also remain.”", "\"그렇다면 저도 남겠습니다.\""], ["Then, rising and extending his hand with an air of solemnity over the old man’s head, he slowly added, “By the blood of Christ I swear never to leave you while you live.”", "그러고는 일어나, 엄숙한 기색으로 노인의 머리 위로 손을 뻗으며 천천히 덧붙였다. \"그리스도의 피를 걸고 맹세합니다. 신부님이 살아 계신 한 결코 곁을 떠나지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["Faria gazed fondly on his noble-minded, single-hearted, high-principled young friend, and read in his countenance ample confirmation of the sincerity of his devotion and the loyalty of his purpose.", "파리아는 고결한 마음과 한결같은 마음, 드높은 신념을 지닌 젊은 벗을 애정 어린 눈으로 바라보았고, 그 얼굴에서 헌신의 진심과 결의의 충직함을 넉넉히 확인했다."]], [["“Thanks,” murmured the invalid, extending one hand.", "\"고맙네.\" 병자가 한 손을 내밀며 나직이 말했다."], ["“I accept. You may one of these days reap the reward of your disinterested devotion.", "\"그 마음 받아들이지. 언젠가 자네는 그 사심 없는 헌신의 보답을 거두게 될지도 몰라."], ["But as I cannot, and you will not, quit this place, it becomes necessary to fill up the excavation beneath the soldier’s gallery; he might, by chance, hear the hollow sound of his footsteps, and call the attention of his officer to the circumstance.", "한데 나는 이곳을 떠날 수 없고 자네는 떠나려 하지 않으니, 병사의 회랑 밑에 판 구덩이를 메워야겠네. 그자가 어쩌다 제 발소리가 텅 비게 울리는 걸 듣고, 그 일을 상관에게 알릴지도 모르니까."], ["That would bring about a discovery which would inevitably lead to our being separated.", "그러면 발각될 테고, 그러면 우리는 틀림없이 헤어지게 될 거야. 자, 가서 그 일에 착수하게."], ["Go, then, and set about this work, in which, unhappily, I can offer you no assistance; keep at it all night, if necessary, and do not return here tomorrow till after the jailer has visited me.", "안타깝게도 그 일에는 내가 도움을 줄 수 없네. 필요하다면 밤새 매달리게. 그리고 내일은 간수가 나를 다녀간 뒤에야 이리로 돌아오게."], ["I shall have something of the greatest importance to communicate to you.”", "자네에게 더없이 중요한 이야기를 들려줄 게 있으니.\""]], [["Dantès took the hand of the abbé in his, and affectionately pressed it.", "당테스는 신부의 손을 잡아 애정 어린 마음으로 꼭 쥐었다."], ["Faria smiled encouragingly on him, and the young man retired to his task, in the spirit of obedience and respect which he had sworn to show towards his aged friend.", "파리아가 그에게 격려하듯 미소 지었고, 젊은이는 나이 든 벗에게 보이겠다고 맹세한 순종과 존경의 마음으로 자신의 일을 하러 물러갔다."]], [["# Chapter 18. The Treasure", "# Chapter 18. The Treasure"]], [["When Dantès returned next morning to the chamber of his companion in captivity, he found Faria seated and looking composed.", "이튿날 아침, 당테스가 함께 갇혀 있는 동료의 방으로 돌아왔을 때, 파리아는 자리에 앉아 차분한 얼굴을 하고 있었다."], ["In the ray of light which entered by the narrow window of his cell, he held open in his left hand, of which alone, it will be recollected, he retained the use, a sheet of paper, which, from being constantly rolled into a small compass, had the form of a cylinder, and was not easily kept open.", "감방의 좁은 창으로 들어오는 한 줄기 빛 속에서, 그는—기억하다시피 그에게 쓸 수 있는 손이라곤 그것뿐인 왼손에—종이 한 장을 펼쳐 들고 있었다. 종이는 작게 둘둘 말려 있던 탓에 원통 모양이 되어 좀처럼 펼친 채로 두기가 어려웠다."], ["He did not speak, but showed the paper to Dantès.", "그는 아무 말 없이 종이를 당테스에게 내밀어 보였다."]], [["“What is that?”", "\"이게 뭡니까?\""], ["he inquired.", "당테스가 물었다."]], [["“Look at it,”", "\"한번 보게.\""], ["said the abbé with a smile.", "신부가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“I have looked at it with all possible attention,”", "\"있는 힘껏 주의를 기울여 보았습니다.\""], ["said Dantès, “and I only see a half-burnt paper, on which are traces of Gothic characters inscribed with a peculiar kind of ink.”", "당테스가 말했다. \"하지만 반쯤 타 버린 종이밖에 보이지 않는군요. 거기에 묘한 잉크로 쓴 고딕체 글자의 흔적이 남아 있고요.\""]], [["“This paper, my friend,”", "\"이 종이가 말일세, 친구.\" 파리아가 말했다. \"이제는 자네에게 털어놓아도 되겠지."], ["said Faria, “I may now avow to you, since I have the proof of your fidelity—this paper is my treasure, of which, from this day forth, one-half belongs to you.”", "자네의 충실함을 확인했으니 말이야. 이 종이가 바로 내 보물일세. 그리고 오늘부터 그 절반은 자네 것이지.\""]], [["The sweat started forth on Dantès’ brow.", "당테스의 이마에 땀이 배어났다."], ["Until this day and for how long a time!—he had refrained from talking of the treasure, which had brought upon the abbé the accusation of madness.", "오늘 이날까지—그것도 얼마나 오랜 세월 동안!—그는 보물 이야기를 입에 올리지 않으려 애써 왔다. 바로 그 보물이 신부에게 미치광이라는 누명을 씌운 것이었다."], ["With his instinctive delicacy Edmond had preferred avoiding any touch on this painful chord, and Faria had been equally silent.", "에드몽은 타고난 섬세함으로 그 아픈 자리를 건드리지 않으려 했고, 파리아 역시 똑같이 입을 다물어 왔다."], ["He had taken the silence of the old man for a return to reason; and now these few words uttered by Faria, after so painful a crisis, seemed to indicate a serious relapse into mental alienation.", "에드몽은 노인의 침묵을 이성의 회복으로 여겼다. 그런데 이토록 고통스러운 발작이 지나간 뒤에 파리아가 내뱉은 이 몇 마디 말은, 정신착란으로의 심각한 재발을 암시하는 듯했다."]], [["“Your treasure?”", "\"보물이라고요?\""], ["stammered Dantès.", "당테스가 더듬거렸다."], ["Faria smiled.", "파리아가 미소 지었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“You have, indeed, a noble nature, Edmond, and I see by your paleness and agitation what is passing in your heart at this moment.", "\"에드몽, 자네는 정말 고결한 천성을 지녔어. 자네의 창백한 얼굴과 동요를 보니 지금 자네 마음속에 무슨 생각이 오가는지 알겠네. 아니야, 안심하게."], ["No, be assured, I am not mad.", "나는 미치지 않았어. 이 보물은 실제로 있다네, 당테스. 그리고 내가 그것을 가질 수 없게 된다면, 자네가 갖게 될 걸세."], ["This treasure exists, Dantès, and if I have not been allowed to possess it, you will.", "그래—자네가 말이야. 아무도 내 말을 들어주거나 믿어 주지 않았지. 모두 나를 미친 사람으로 여겼으니까."], ["Yes—you. No one would listen or believe me, because everyone thought me mad; but you, who must know that I am not, listen to me, and believe me so afterwards if you will.”", "하지만 내가 미치지 않았다는 걸 분명히 아는 자네는, 내 말을 들어 보게. 그러고 나서도 그렇게 믿고 싶다면 그때 미쳤다 여기게나.\""]], [["“Alas,”", "\"아아.\""], ["murmured Edmond to himself, “this is a terrible relapse!", "에드몽이 속으로 중얼거렸다. '끔찍한 재발이로구나!"], ["There was only this blow wanting.”", "이 일격만이 남아 있던 거였어.'"], ["Then he said aloud, “My dear friend, your attack has, perhaps, fatigued you; had you not better repose awhile?", "그러고는 소리 내어 말했다. \"친애하는 친구여, 발작 때문에 지치셨을지도 모릅니다. 잠시 쉬시는 게 어떻겠습니까?"], ["Tomorrow, if you will, I will hear your narrative; but today I wish to nurse you carefully.", "원하신다면 내일 그 이야기를 듣겠습니다. 하지만 오늘은 정성껏 간병해 드리고 싶군요. 게다가,\""], ["Besides,” he said, “a treasure is not a thing we need hurry about.”", "그가 말했다. \"보물이라는 건 서둘러야 할 일도 아니지 않습니까.\""]], [["“On the contrary, it is a matter of the utmost importance, Edmond!”", "\"오히려 그 반대일세, 에드몽. 이건 더없이 중대한 문제야!\""], ["replied the old man.", "노인이 대답했다."], ["“Who knows if tomorrow, or the next day after, the third attack may not come on? and then must not all be over?", "\"내일이나 모레, 세 번째 발작이 닥치지 않으리란 법이 어디 있겠나? 그러면 모든 게 끝나 버리는 게 아닌가?"], ["Yes, indeed, I have often thought with a bitter joy that these riches, which would make the wealth of a dozen families, will be forever lost to those men who persecute me.", "그래, 정말이지 나는 쓰디쓴 기쁨에 잠겨 종종 이런 생각을 했네. 열두 가문을 부유하게 만들 이 재물이, 나를 박해한 자들에게서 영원히 사라지리라고 말이야."], ["This idea was one of vengeance to me, and I tasted it slowly in the night of my dungeon and the despair of my captivity.", "이 생각은 내게 복수와도 같았고, 나는 지하 감옥의 어둠 속에서, 갇힌 자의 절망 속에서 그것을 천천히 음미했네."], ["But now I have forgiven the world for the love of you; now that I see you, young and with a promising future,—now that I think of all that may result to you in the good fortune of such a disclosure, I shudder at any delay, and tremble lest I should not assure to one as worthy as yourself the possession of so vast an amount of hidden wealth.”", "하지만 이제 나는 자네를 사랑하는 마음으로 세상을 용서했어. 이제 젊고 앞날이 창창한 자네를 보고 있으니, 이런 사실을 털어놓는 행운으로 자네에게 돌아갈 모든 것을 생각하니, 조금이라도 지체하는 게 두렵네. 자네처럼 마땅한 사람에게 그토록 막대한 숨은 재물의 소유를 보장해 주지 못할까 봐 떨린다네.\""]], [["Edmond turned away his head with a sigh.", "에드몽은 한숨을 내쉬며 고개를 돌렸다."]], [["“You persist in your incredulity, Edmond,”", "\"자네는 끝내 믿지 못하는군, 에드몽.\""], ["continued Faria.", "파리아가 말을 이었다."], ["“My words have not convinced you.", "\"내 말이 자네를 설득하지 못한 거야."], ["I see you require proofs.", "증거가 필요하다는 걸 알겠네."], ["Well, then, read this paper, which I have never shown to anyone.”", "그렇다면 이 종이를 읽어 보게. 지금껏 누구에게도 보여 준 적이 없는 거라네.\""]], [["“Tomorrow, my dear friend,” said Edmond, desirous of not yielding to the old man’s madness.", "\"내일 읽겠습니다, 친애하는 친구여.\" 노인의 광기에 굴복하고 싶지 않았던 에드몽이 말했다."], ["“I thought it was understood that we should not talk of that until tomorrow.”", "\"내일까지는 그 이야기를 하지 않기로 한 줄 알았습니다만.\""]], [["“Then we will not talk of it until tomorrow; but read this paper today.”", "\"그럼 내일까지 이야기는 하지 않세. 하지만 이 종이만은 오늘 읽어 보게.\""]], [["“I will not irritate him,”", "'노인을 자극하진 말아야지.'"], ["thought Edmond, and taking the paper, of which half was wanting,—having been burnt, no doubt, by some accident,—he read:", "에드몽은 그렇게 생각하며, 절반이 사라진—틀림없이 어떤 사고로 타 버렸을—종이를 받아 들고 읽어 내려갔다."]], [["“this treasure, which may amount to two...", "\"이 보물은, 이백만에 이를 수도……"]], [["of Roman crowns in the most distant a...", "로마 화폐로 가장 먼 곳……"]], [["of the second opening wh...", "두 번째 입구에서 ……"]], [["declare to belong to him alo...", "그에게만 속한다고 선언하……"]], [["heir.", "상속인."]], [["“25th April, 149’”", "1498년 4월 25일\""]], [["“Well!” said Faria, when the young man had finished reading it.", "\"어떤가!\" 청년이 다 읽고 나자 파리아가 말했다."]], [["“Why,”", "\"글쎄요.\""], ["replied Dantès, “I see nothing but broken lines and unconnected words, which are rendered illegible by fire.”", "당테스가 대답했다. \"끊긴 줄과 이어지지 않는 낱말들밖에 보이지 않습니다. 불에 타서 알아볼 수가 없는걸요.\""]], [["“Yes, to you, my friend, who read them for the first time; but not for me, who have grown pale over them by many nights’ study, and have reconstructed every phrase, completed every thought.”", "\"그래, 처음 읽는 자네에게는 그렇겠지. 하지만 여러 밤을 그 위에서 창백해지도록 연구하며 모든 구절을 다시 짜 맞추고 모든 생각을 완성한 내게는 그렇지 않다네.\""]], [["“And do you believe you have discovered the hidden meaning?”", "\"그래서 숨겨진 뜻을 알아내셨다는 겁니까?\""]], [["“I am sure I have, and you shall judge for yourself; but first listen to the history of this paper.”", "\"확실히 알아냈네. 자네가 직접 판단해 보게. 하지만 우선 이 종이의 내력을 들어 보게나.\""]], [["“Silence!”", "\"쉿!\" 당테스가 외쳤다."], ["exclaimed Dantès.", "\"발소리가 다가옵니다."], ["“Steps approach—I go—adieu!”", "가 보겠습니다. 안녕히 계십시오!\""]], [["And Dantès, happy to escape the history and explanation which would be sure to confirm his belief in his friend’s mental instability, glided like a snake along the narrow passage; while Faria, restored by his alarm to a certain amount of activity, pushed the stone into place with his foot, and covered it with a mat in order the more effectually to avoid discovery.", "친구의 정신이 불안정하다는 믿음을 굳혀 줄 게 뻔한 그 내력과 설명에서 벗어나게 된 게 다행스러워, 당테스는 뱀처럼 좁은 통로를 미끄러져 나갔다. 한편 놀란 덕분에 어느 정도 기력을 되찾은 파리아는 발로 돌을 밀어 제자리에 끼워 넣고, 더 확실히 발각을 피하려고 그 위에 거적을 덮었다."]], [["It was the governor, who, hearing of Faria’s illness from the jailer, had come in person to see him.", "찾아온 이는 소장이었다. 간수에게서 파리아가 아프다는 말을 듣고 직접 보러 온 것이었다."]], [["Faria sat up to receive him, avoiding all gestures in order that he might conceal from the governor the paralysis that had already half stricken him with death.", "파리아는 그를 맞으려 몸을 일으키되, 자신을 이미 절반쯤 죽음으로 몰아넣은 마비를 소장에게 감추려고 모든 몸짓을 삼갔다."], ["His fear was lest the governor, touched with pity, might order him to be removed to better quarters, and thus separate him from his young companion.", "소장이 연민에 마음이 움직여 더 나은 거처로 옮기라 명하고, 그리하여 자신을 젊은 동료에게서 떼어 놓을까 두려웠던 것이다."], ["But fortunately this was not the case, and the governor left him, convinced that the poor madman, for whom in his heart he felt a kind of affection, was only troubled with a slight indisposition.", "그러나 다행히 그런 일은 없었고, 소장은 자신이 마음속으로 일종의 애정을 품고 있던 그 가엾은 미치광이가 가벼운 병을 앓을 뿐이라고 확신한 채 그를 떠났다."]], [["During this time, Edmond, seated on his bed with his head in his hands, tried to collect his scattered thoughts.", "그동안 에드몽은 침대에 앉아 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 흩어진 생각들을 가다듬으려 애썼다."], ["Faria, since their first acquaintance, had been on all points so rational and logical, so wonderfully sagacious, in fact, that he could not understand how so much wisdom on all points could be allied with madness.", "파리아는 처음 알게 된 이래로 모든 점에서 그토록 이성적이고 논리적이며, 실로 놀라울 만큼 명민했다. 그래서 그 모든 분야에 걸친 그토록 깊은 지혜가 어떻게 광기와 한데 어울릴 수 있는지 도무지 이해할 수 없었다."], ["Was Faria deceived as to his treasure, or was all the world deceived as to Faria?", "파리아가 자기 보물에 관해 속고 있는 것인가, 아니면 온 세상이 파리아에 관해 속고 있는 것인가?"]], [["Dantès remained in his cell all day, not daring to return to his friend, thinking thus to defer the moment when he should be convinced, once for all, that the abbé was mad—such a conviction would be so terrible!", "당테스는 온종일 감방에 머물며 감히 친구에게 돌아가지 못했다. 그렇게 함으로써, 신부가 미쳤다는 확신을 끝내 굳히게 될 순간을 미루려 한 것이었다. 그런 확신은 너무도 끔찍할 테니까!"]], [["But, towards the evening after the hour for the customary visit had gone by, Faria, not seeing the young man appear, tried to move and get over the distance which separated them.", "그러나 저녁 무렵, 여느 때의 방문 시각이 지났는데도 청년이 나타나지 않자, 파리아는 둘을 갈라놓은 거리를 몸소 움직여 건너 보려 했다."], ["Edmond shuddered when he heard the painful efforts which the old man made to drag himself along; his leg was inert, and he could no longer make use of one arm.", "노인이 몸을 끌고 오느라 애쓰는 고통스러운 소리를 듣고 에드몽은 몸서리쳤다. 노인의 한쪽 다리는 움직이지 못했고, 한쪽 팔도 더는 쓸 수 없었다."], ["Edmond was obliged to assist him, for otherwise he would not have been able to enter by the small aperture which led to Dantès’ chamber.", "에드몽은 그를 도와줄 수밖에 없었다. 그러지 않으면 당테스의 방으로 통하는 작은 구멍으로 노인은 들어올 수 없었을 테니까."]], [["“Here I am, pursuing you remorselessly,”", "\"자, 이렇게 나는 끈질기게 자네를 쫓아왔네.\""], ["he said with a benignant smile.", "노인이 인자한 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["“You thought to escape my munificence, but it is in vain. Listen to me.”", "\"내 후한 선물에서 달아날 줄 알았겠지만 헛수고일세. 내 말을 들어 보게.\""]], [["Edmond saw there was no escape, and placing the old man on his bed, he seated himself on the stool beside him.", "에드몽은 벗어날 길이 없음을 깨닫고, 노인을 침대에 눕힌 뒤 그 곁의 걸상에 앉았다."]], [["“You know,”", "\"자네도 알다시피,\""], ["said the abbé, “that I was the secretary and intimate friend of Cardinal Spada, the last of the princes of that name.", "신부가 말했다. \"나는 그 가문의 마지막 군주였던 스파다 추기경의 비서이자 가까운 벗이었네."], ["I owe to this worthy lord all the happiness I ever knew.", "내가 누린 모든 행복은 이 훌륭한 분 덕분이었지."], ["He was not rich, although the wealth of his family had passed into a proverb, and I heard the phrase very often, ‘As rich as a Spada.’ But he, like public rumor, lived on this reputation for wealth; his palace was my paradise.", "비록 그분 가문의 재산이 속담이 될 만큼 유명해서 '스파다처럼 부유하다'는 말을 자주 듣긴 했지만, 정작 그분은 부유하지 않으셨네. 그분도, 세상의 소문도 그 부의 명성으로 살아갔던 셈이지. 그분의 궁전은 내겐 천국이었네."], ["I was tutor to his nephews, who are dead; and when he was alone in the world, I tried by absolute devotion to his will, to make up to him all he had done for me during ten years of unremitting kindness.", "나는 그분의 조카들을 가르치는 가정교사였는데, 그 조카들은 다 세상을 떠났지. 그리고 그분이 세상에 홀로 남으셨을 때, 나는 그분의 뜻에 절대적으로 헌신함으로써, 십 년간 한결같은 친절로 내게 베풀어 주신 모든 것에 보답하려 애썼다네."], ["The cardinal’s house had no secrets for me.", "추기경 댁에는 내게 비밀이라곤 없었어."], ["I had often seen my noble patron annotating ancient volumes, and eagerly searching amongst dusty family manuscripts.", "나는 고귀한 후원자께서 옛 책에 주를 달고, 먼지 쌓인 집안 필사본들 사이를 열심히 뒤지시는 모습을 자주 보았네."], ["One day when I was reproaching him for his unavailing searches, and deploring the prostration of mind that followed them, he looked at me, and, smiling bitterly, opened a volume relating to the History of the City of Rome.", "어느 날, 내가 그 헛된 탐색을 두고 그분을 나무라며, 그 뒤에 찾아오는 정신의 쇠진을 안타까워하자, 그분은 나를 바라보며 쓸쓸히 미소 짓더니 로마시의 역사에 관한 책 한 권을 펼치셨네."], ["There, in the twentieth chapter of the Life of Pope Alexander VI., were the following lines, which I can never forget:—", "거기, 교황 알렉산데르 6세의 생애를 다룬 스무 번째 장에 다음과 같은 구절이 있었는데, 나는 그 구절을 결코 잊을 수가 없네."]], [["“‘The great wars of Romagna had ended; Cæsar Borgia, who had completed his conquest, had need of money to purchase all Italy.", "\"'로마냐의 큰 전쟁들이 끝났다. 정복을 마친 체사레 보르자는 온 이탈리아를 사들이기 위해 돈이 필요했다."], ["The pope had also need of money to bring matters to an end with Louis XII. King of France, who was formidable still in spite of his recent reverses; and it was necessary, therefore, to have recourse to some profitable scheme, which was a matter of great difficulty in the impoverished condition of exhausted Italy.", "교황 또한 최근의 패배에도 여전히 만만찮았던 프랑스 왕 루이 12세와의 문제를 매듭짓기 위해 돈이 필요했다. 따라서 어떤 수익성 있는 방책에 기대지 않을 수 없었는데, 피폐해진 이탈리아의 궁핍한 형편에서 이는 무척 어려운 일이었다."], ["His holiness had an idea. He determined to make two cardinals.’", "교황 성하께 한 가지 묘안이 떠올랐다. 그는 추기경 둘을 임명하기로 했다.'"]], [["“By choosing two of the greatest personages of Rome, especially rich men—_this_ was the return the Holy Father looked for.", "\"로마의 가장 큰 인물 둘을, 특히 부유한 자들을 고름으로써—바로 이것이 교황 성하께서 노린 보답이었네."], ["In the first place, he could sell the great appointments and splendid offices which the cardinals already held; and then he had the two hats to sell besides.", "우선 그는 그 추기경들이 이미 가지고 있던 큰 직책과 화려한 관직들을 팔 수 있었지. 그리고 그 밖에도 추기경 모자 두 개를 팔 수 있었고."], ["There was a third point in view, which will appear hereafter.", "또 세 번째 노림수가 있었는데, 그건 나중에 드러날 걸세."]], [["“The pope and Cæsar Borgia first found the two future cardinals; they were Giovanni Rospigliosi, who held four of the highest dignities of the Holy See, and Cæsar Spada, one of the noblest and richest of the Roman nobility; both felt the high honor of such a favor from the pope.", "\"교황과 체사레 보르자는 먼저 미래의 추기경 두 사람을 골랐네. 교황청의 최고위직 가운데 넷을 차지하고 있던 조반니 로스피글리오시와, 로마 귀족 중에서도 가장 고귀하고 부유한 자 가운데 하나였던 체사레 스파다였지. 둘 다 교황으로부터 그런 은총을 받는 것을 더없는 영광으로 여겼네."], ["They were ambitious, and Cæsar Borgia soon found purchasers for their appointments.", "두 사람은 야심가였고, 체사레 보르자는 곧 그들의 직책을 살 사람들을 찾아냈지."], ["The result was, that Rospigliosi and Spada paid for being cardinals, and eight other persons paid for the offices the cardinals held before their elevation, and thus eight hundred thousand crowns entered into the coffers of the speculators.", "그 결과 로스피글리오시와 스파다는 추기경이 되기 위해 돈을 치렀고, 다른 여덟 사람은 두 추기경이 승진하기 전에 가지고 있던 관직들을 사려고 돈을 치렀네. 그리하여 팔십만 크라운이 그 투기꾼들의 금고로 들어갔지."]], [["0239m\n“It is time now to proceed to the last part of the speculation.", "\"이제 이 투기의 마지막 단계로 넘어갈 때가 됐네."], ["The pope heaped attentions upon Rospigliosi and Spada, conferred upon them the insignia of the cardinalate, and induced them to arrange their affairs and take up their residence at Rome.", "교황은 로스피글리오시와 스파다에게 온갖 호의를 베풀고 추기경의 표장을 내려 주었으며, 그들에게 신변을 정리하고 로마에 거처를 두라고 권했네."], ["Then the pope and Cæsar Borgia invited the two cardinals to dinner.", "그런 다음 교황과 체사레 보르자는 두 추기경을 만찬에 초대했지."], ["This was a matter of dispute between the Holy Father and his son.", "이건 교황 성하와 그 아들 사이에 의견이 갈린 문제였네."], ["Cæsar thought they could make use of one of the means which he always had ready for his friends, that is to say, in the first place, the famous key which was given to certain persons with the request that they go and open a designated cupboard.", "체사레는 자신이 친구들을 위해 늘 준비해 두던 수단 가운데 하나를 쓸 수 있으리라 여겼지. 우선 그 유명한 열쇠가 있었네. 특정한 사람들에게 그것을 건네며 가서 지정된 찬장을 열어 보라고 청하는 것이었지."], ["This key was furnished with a small iron point,—a negligence on the part of the locksmith.", "이 열쇠에는 작은 쇠바늘이 달려 있었네. 자물쇠공의 부주의인 척했지만 말이야."], ["When this was pressed to effect the opening of the cupboard, of which the lock was difficult, the person was pricked by this small point, and died next day.", "자물쇠가 빡빡한 그 찬장을 열려고 열쇠를 누르면, 그 사람은 그 작은 바늘에 찔렸고 이튿날 죽었지."], ["Then there was the ring with the lion’s head, which Cæsar wore when he wanted to greet his friends with a clasp of the hand.", "그리고 사자 머리가 새겨진 반지도 있었네. 체사레는 친구들을 악수로 맞이하고 싶을 때 그것을 끼었지."], ["The lion bit the hand thus favored, and at the end of twenty-four hours, the bite was mortal.", "사자는 그 영광스러운 손을 물었고, 스물네 시간이 지나면 그 물린 상처는 치명적이었네."]], [["“Cæsar proposed to his father, that they should either ask the cardinals to open the cupboard, or shake hands with them; but Alexander VI.", "\"체사레는 추기경들에게 찬장을 열어 보라고 하거나 악수를 청하자고 아버지에게 제안했네. 하지만 알렉산데르 6세는 이렇게 답했지."], ["replied: ‘Now as to the worthy cardinals, Spada and Rospigliosi, let us ask both of them to dinner, something tells me that we shall get that money back.", "'저 훌륭한 추기경들, 스파다와 로스피글리오시 말이다, 둘 다 만찬에 청하기로 하자. 어쩐지 그 돈을 되찾을 것 같은 예감이 드는구나."], ["Besides, you forget, Cæsar, an indigestion declares itself immediately, while a prick or a bite occasions a delay of a day or two.’ Cæsar gave way before such cogent reasoning, and the cardinals were consequently invited to dinner.", "게다가 너는 잊고 있구나, 체사레. 체기는 곧바로 나타나지만, 바늘이나 물린 상처는 하루 이틀의 지체를 부른다는 걸 말이다.' 체사레는 그토록 설득력 있는 논리 앞에 물러섰고, 그리하여 추기경들은 만찬에 초대되었네."]], [["“The table was laid in a vineyard belonging to the pope, near San Pierdarena, a charming retreat which the cardinals knew very well by report.", "\"식탁은 산피에르다레나 부근에 있는 교황 소유의 포도밭에 차려졌네. 추기경들도 소문으로 잘 알고 있던 매혹적인 별장이었지."], ["Rospigliosi, quite set up with his new dignities, went with a good appetite and his most ingratiating manner.", "새 지위에 잔뜩 우쭐해진 로스피글리오시는 좋은 식욕과 더없이 비위 맞추는 태도로 갔네."], ["Spada, a prudent man, and greatly attached to his only nephew, a young captain of the highest promise, took paper and pen, and made his will.", "신중한 사람인 데다 더없이 촉망받는 젊은 대위인 외동 조카를 무척 아끼던 스파다는, 종이와 펜을 들어 유언장을 작성했네."], ["He then sent word to his nephew to wait for him near the vineyard; but it appeared the servant did not find him.", "그러고는 조카에게 포도밭 근처에서 자신을 기다리라는 전갈을 보냈지. 하지만 하인이 그를 찾지 못한 모양이었네."]], [["“Spada knew what these invitations meant; since Christianity, so eminently civilizing, had made progress in Rome, it was no longer a centurion who came from the tyrant with a message, ‘Cæsar wills that you die.’ but it was a legate _à latere_, who came with a smile on his lips to say from the pope, ‘His holiness requests you to dine with him.’", "\"스파다는 그런 초대가 무엇을 뜻하는지 알고 있었네. 그토록 두드러지게 문명을 일으킨 기독교가 로마에서 진보한 이래로, 폭군의 명을 받아 '체사레가 그대의 죽음을 원한다'는 전갈을 들고 오는 건 더 이상 백인대장이 아니었지. 입가에 미소를 띤 채 와서 교황의 말을 전하는 특사였네. '성하께서 그대에게 함께 식사하기를 청하십니다.'"]], [["“Spada set out about two o’clock to San Pierdarena. The pope awaited him.", "\"스파다는 두 시쯤 산피에르다레나로 출발했네. 교황이 그를 기다리고 있었지."], ["The first sight that attracted the eyes of Spada was that of his nephew, in full costume, and Cæsar Borgia paying him most marked attentions.", "스파다의 눈에 가장 먼저 들어온 것은, 성장을 차려입은 그의 조카와, 그에게 더없이 두드러진 호의를 베풀고 있는 체사레 보르자의 모습이었네."], ["Spada turned pale, as Cæsar looked at him with an ironical air, which proved that he had anticipated all, and that the snare was well spread.", "스파다는 새파랗게 질렸지. 체사레가 비웃는 듯한 기색으로 그를 바라보았는데, 그것은 그가 모든 것을 미리 내다보았으며 덫이 잘 펼쳐졌음을 보여 주는 것이었네."]], [["“They began dinner and Spada was only able to inquire of his nephew if he had received his message.", "\"만찬이 시작되었고, 스파다는 가까스로 조카에게 자신의 전갈을 받았는지 물을 수 있었네."], ["The nephew replied no; perfectly comprehending the meaning of the question.", "조카는 못 받았다고 대답했지. 그 질문의 뜻을 완벽히 알아차린 채로 말이야."], ["It was too late, for he had already drunk a glass of excellent wine, placed for him expressly by the pope’s butler.", "하지만 이미 늦었네. 그가 교황의 집사가 일부러 그를 위해 따라 둔 훌륭한 포도주 한 잔을 이미 마셔 버린 뒤였으니까."], ["Spada at the same moment saw another bottle approach him, which he was pressed to taste.", "바로 그 순간 스파다는 또 다른 술병이 자기 쪽으로 다가오는 것을 보았고, 맛보라는 권유를 받았네."], ["An hour afterwards a physician declared they were both poisoned through eating mushrooms.", "한 시간 뒤 의사 하나가 두 사람이 버섯을 먹고 독에 중독되었다고 선언했지."], ["Spada died on the threshold of the vineyard; the nephew expired at his own door, making signs which his wife could not comprehend.", "스파다는 포도밭 문턱에서 숨을 거두었고, 조카는 제집 문간에서 아내가 알아듣지 못하는 손짓을 하며 숨을 거두었네."]], [["“Then Cæsar and the pope hastened to lay hands on the heritage, under pretense of seeking for the papers of the dead man.", "\"그러자 체사레와 교황은 죽은 자의 서류를 찾는다는 구실로 서둘러 유산에 손을 댔네. 하지만 그 유산이라는 건 오직 이것뿐이었지."], ["But the inheritance consisted in this only, a scrap of paper on which Spada had written:—‘I bequeath to my beloved nephew my coffers, my books, and, amongst others, my breviary with the gold corners, which I beg he will preserve in remembrance of his affectionate uncle.’", "스파다가 적어 둔 종이쪽지 한 장이었네. '나는 사랑하는 조카에게 내 금고와 내 책들을, 그리고 그 가운데서도 특히 금박 모서리를 두른 내 기도서를 남긴다. 부디 다정한 숙부를 기억하며 그것을 간직해 주기를 바란다.'"]], [["“The heirs sought everywhere, admired the breviary, laid hands on the furniture, and were greatly astonished that Spada, the rich man, was really the most miserable of uncles—no treasures—unless they were those of science, contained in the library and laboratories.", "\"상속자들은 곳곳을 뒤졌고, 그 기도서에 감탄했으며, 가구에 손을 댔네. 그러고는 부자였던 스파다가 실은 가장 비참한 숙부였다는 사실에 크게 놀랐지. 보물이라곤 없었던 거야—서재와 실험실에 담긴 학문의 보물이라면 또 모를까."], ["That was all.", "그게 전부였네. 체사레와 그 아버지는 찾고, 살피고, 샅샅이 뒤졌지만 아무것도, 아니 적어도 변변한 건 찾지 못했네. 금붙이로 몇천 크라운, 현금으로 그만한 액수를 넘지 않았지."], ["Cæsar and his father searched, examined, scrutinized, but found nothing, or at least very little; not exceeding a few thousand crowns in plate, and about the same in ready money; but the nephew had time to say to his wife before he expired: ‘Look well among my uncle’s papers; there is a will.’", "하지만 조카는 숨을 거두기 전에 아내에게 이렇게 말할 틈이 있었네. '숙부의 서류를 잘 살펴보시오. 거기 유언장이 있소.'"]], [["“They sought even more thoroughly than the august heirs had done, but it was fruitless.", "\"그들은 고귀한 상속자들보다 더 철저히 뒤졌지만 헛수고였네."], ["There were two palaces and a vineyard behind the Palatine Hill; but in these days landed property had not much value, and the two palaces and the vineyard remained to the family since they were beneath the rapacity of the pope and his son.", "팔라티노 언덕 뒤편에 궁전 두 채와 포도밭 하나가 있었지. 하지만 그 시절엔 토지 재산이 그리 값나가지 않았고, 그 두 궁전과 포도밭은 교황과 그 아들의 탐욕을 끌 만한 것이 못 되었기에 가문에 남았네."], ["Months and years rolled on.", "달이 가고 해가 흘렀지."], ["Alexander VI. died, poisoned,—you know by what mistake.", "알렉산데르 6세는 독에 중독되어 죽었네—무슨 착오로 그리됐는지는 자네도 알 거야."], ["Cæsar, poisoned at the same time, escaped by shedding his skin like a snake; but the new skin was spotted by the poison till it looked like a tiger’s.", "같은 때에 독에 중독된 체사레는 뱀처럼 허물을 벗고 살아남았지만, 그 새 허물은 독에 얼룩져 호랑이 가죽처럼 보였지."], ["Then, compelled to quit Rome, he went and got himself obscurely killed in a night skirmish, scarcely noticed in history.", "그 뒤 로마를 떠나야 했던 그는, 역사에 거의 주목받지도 못한 어느 야간 전투에서 이름도 없이 죽고 말았네."]], [["“After the pope’s death and his son’s exile, it was supposed that the Spada family would resume the splendid position they had held before the cardinal’s time; but this was not the case.", "\"교황이 죽고 그 아들이 추방되자, 사람들은 스파다 가문이 추기경 시절 이전에 누렸던 화려한 지위를 되찾으리라 여겼네. 하지만 그렇지 않았지."], ["The Spadas remained in doubtful ease, a mystery hung over this dark affair, and the public rumor was, that Cæsar, a better politician than his father, had carried off from the pope the fortune of the two cardinals.", "스파다 가문은 그저 그런 형편에 머물렀고, 이 어두운 사건에는 수수께끼가 드리워졌네. 세간의 소문은, 제 아버지보다 더 능란한 정치가였던 체사레가 두 추기경의 재산을 교황에게서 빼돌렸다는 것이었네."], ["I say the two, because Cardinal Rospigliosi, who had not taken any precaution, was completely despoiled.", "두 추기경이라 말하는 건, 아무런 조심도 하지 않았던 로스피글리오시 추기경은 완전히 털렸기 때문이지."]], [["“Up to this point,”", "\"여기까지는,\""], ["said Faria, interrupting the thread of his narrative, “this seems to you very meaningless, no doubt, eh?”", "파리아가 이야기의 흐름을 끊으며 말했다. \"자네에겐 분명 아무런 의미도 없게 들렸겠지, 안 그런가?\""]], [["“Oh, my friend,”", "\"오, 친구여,\" 당테스가 외쳤다."], ["cried Dantès, “on the contrary, it seems as if I were reading a most interesting narrative; go on, I beg of you.”", "\"오히려 반대입니다. 무척 흥미로운 이야기를 읽는 것만 같습니다. 계속해 주십시오, 부탁드립니다.\""]], [["“I will.", "\"그러지."], ["The family began to get accustomed to their obscurity.", "가문은 그 무명의 처지에 차츰 익숙해졌네."], ["Years rolled on, and amongst the descendants some were soldiers, others diplomatists; some churchmen, some bankers; some grew rich, and some were ruined.", "세월이 흘렀고, 후손들 가운데 더러는 군인, 더러는 외교관이 되었지. 더러는 성직자, 더러는 은행가가 되었고. 부유해진 자도 있고 몰락한 자도 있었네."], ["I come now to the last of the family, whose secretary I was—the Count of Spada.", "이제 나는 가문의 마지막 인물, 곧 내가 비서로 모셨던 스파다 백작에게 이르렀네."], ["I had often heard him complain of the disproportion of his rank with his fortune; and I advised him to invest all he had in an annuity.", "나는 그분이 자신의 지위에 어울리지 않는 재산을 두고 한탄하시는 걸 자주 들었지. 그래서 나는 가진 모든 것을 연금에 넣으시라 권했네."], ["He did so, and thus doubled his income. The celebrated breviary remained in the family, and was in the count’s possession.", "그분은 그렇게 하셨고, 그리하여 수입이 갑절로 늘었지. 그 유명한 기도서는 가문에 남아 백작의 손에 있었네."], ["It had been handed down from father to son; for the singular clause of the only will that had been found, had caused it to be regarded as a genuine relic, preserved in the family with superstitious veneration.", "아버지에게서 아들로 대물림되어 온 것이었지. 유일하게 발견된 유언장의 그 기묘한 조항 때문에, 그것은 진짜 유물로 여겨져 미신에 가까운 숭배 속에 가문에 보존되어 왔거든."], ["It was an illuminated book, with beautiful Gothic characters, and so weighty with gold, that a servant always carried it before the cardinal on days of great solemnity.", "아름다운 고딕체 글자로 채색된 책이었고, 금이 어찌나 무겁게 박혀 있었던지, 큰 의식이 있는 날이면 늘 하인 하나가 추기경 앞에서 그것을 받쳐 들고 다녔다네."]], [["“At the sight of papers of all sorts,—titles, contracts, parchments, which were kept in the archives of the family, all descending from the poisoned cardinal, I in my turn examined the immense bundles of documents, like twenty servitors, stewards, secretaries before me; but in spite of the most exhaustive researches, I found—nothing.", "\"독살된 추기경에게서 전부 내려온, 가문의 문서고에 보관된 온갖 종류의 서류들—권리증, 계약서, 양피지 문서들을 보며, 이번엔 내가 나설 차례였네. 나보다 앞서 스무 명쯤의 하인과 집사와 비서가 그랬듯이, 그 방대한 문서 뭉치들을 살폈지. 하지만 더없이 철저히 뒤졌는데도 나는 아무것도 찾지 못했네."], ["Yet I had read, I had even written a precise history of the Borgia family, for the sole purpose of assuring myself whether any increase of fortune had occurred to them on the death of the Cardinal Cæsar Spada; but could only trace the acquisition of the property of the Cardinal Rospigliosi, his companion in misfortune.", "그래도 나는 보르자 가문의 정확한 역사를 읽고, 심지어 직접 써 보기까지 했네. 오로지 체사레 스파다 추기경이 죽고 나서 그들에게 재산의 증식이 있었는지 확인하려는 목적에서였지. 하지만 그 불운의 동반자였던 로스피글리오시 추기경의 재산을 손에 넣은 흔적밖에는 찾을 수 없었네."]], [["“I was then almost assured that the inheritance had neither profited the Borgias nor the family, but had remained unpossessed like the treasures of the Arabian Nights, which slept in the bosom of the earth under the eyes of the genie.", "\"그래서 나는 그 유산이 보르자 가문에도, 스파다 가문에도 득이 되지 않았으며, 마치 정령의 눈앞에서 땅속에 잠들어 있던 아라비안나이트의 보물처럼 임자 없이 남아 있다고 거의 확신하게 되었네."], ["I searched, ransacked, counted, calculated a thousand and a thousand times the income and expenditure of the family for three hundred years.", "나는 삼백 년에 걸친 가문의 수입과 지출을 천 번이고 만 번이고 찾고, 뒤지고, 세고, 계산했지."], ["It was useless.", "헛수고였네."], ["I remained in my ignorance, and the Count of Spada in his poverty.", "나는 여전히 깜깜했고, 스파다 백작은 여전히 가난했지."]], [["“My patron died. He had reserved from his annuity his family papers, his library, composed of five thousand volumes, and his famous breviary.", "\"내 후원자께서 돌아가셨네. 그분은 연금에서 집안 서류와, 오천 권으로 이루어진 서재와, 그 유명한 기도서를 떼어 두셨네."], ["All these he bequeathed to me, with a thousand Roman crowns, which he had in ready money, on condition that I would have anniversary masses said for the repose of his soul, and that I would draw up a genealogical tree and history of his house.", "그 모든 것을, 가지고 계시던 현금 천 로마 크라운과 함께 내게 남겨 주셨지. 다만 내가 그분의 영혼의 안식을 위해 해마다 추도 미사를 올리고, 그분 가문의 족보와 역사를 작성한다는 조건이었네."], ["All this I did scrupulously.", "나는 그 모든 것을 빈틈없이 해냈지."], ["Be easy, my dear Edmond, we are near the conclusion.", "안심하게, 친애하는 에드몽, 이제 결말에 가까워졌으니까."]], [["“In 1807, a month before I was arrested, and a fortnight after the death of the Count of Spada, on the 25th of December (you will see presently how the date became fixed in my memory), I was reading, for the thousandth time, the papers I was arranging, for the palace was sold to a stranger, and I was going to leave Rome and settle at Florence, intending to take with me twelve thousand francs I possessed, my library, and the famous breviary, when, tired with my constant labor at the same thing, and overcome by a heavy dinner I had eaten, my head dropped on my hands, and I fell asleep about three o’clock in the afternoon.", "\"1807년, 내가 체포되기 한 달 전이자 스파다 백작이 돌아가시고 보름 뒤인 12월 25일에—이 날짜가 어떻게 내 기억에 박혔는지는 곧 알게 될 걸세—나는 정리하던 그 서류들을 천 번째로 읽고 있었네. 궁전이 어떤 낯선 사람에게 팔려, 나는 로마를 떠나 피렌체에 정착할 참이었거든. 가진 돈 만 이천 프랑과 내 서재, 그리고 그 유명한 기도서를 가지고 갈 작정이었지. 그런데 같은 일을 끊임없이 하느라 지치고, 거하게 먹은 저녁 탓에 노곤해져, 머리가 두 손 위로 떨어지면서 오후 세 시쯤 잠이 들고 말았네."]], [["0243m\n“I awoke as the clock was striking six.", "\"시계가 여섯 시를 칠 때 나는 깨어났네."], ["I raised my head; I was in utter darkness.", "고개를 들어 보니 칠흑 같은 어둠 속이었지."], ["I rang for a light, but, as no one came, I determined to find one for myself.", "불을 가져오라고 종을 울렸지만 아무도 오지 않기에, 직접 불을 구하기로 했네."], ["It was indeed but anticipating the simple manners which I should soon be under the necessity of adopting.", "사실 머지않아 어쩔 수 없이 받아들여야 할 그 소박한 생활방식을 미리 맛본 셈이었지."], ["I took a wax-candle in one hand, and with the other groped about for a piece of paper (my match-box being empty), with which I proposed to get a light from the small flame still playing on the embers.", "나는 한 손에 밀랍 초를 들고, 다른 손으로 종이쪽지 하나를 더듬어 찾았네—성냥갑이 비어 있었거든—잿불 위에 아직 가물거리는 작은 불꽃에서 불을 옮겨 붙일 작정이었지."], ["Fearing, however, to make use of any valuable piece of paper, I hesitated for a moment, then recollected that I had seen in the famous breviary, which was on the table beside me, an old paper quite yellow with age, and which had served as a marker for centuries, kept there by the request of the heirs.", "하지만 값나가는 종이를 쓸까 봐 잠시 망설였네. 그러다 문득, 곁의 탁자 위에 놓인 그 유명한 기도서 안에서, 세월에 누렇게 바랜 채 수백 년 동안 책갈피 노릇을 해 온, 상속자들의 청에 따라 거기 끼워 둔 오래된 종이 한 장을 본 게 떠올랐네."], ["I felt for it, found it, twisted it up together, and putting it into the expiring flame, set light to it.", "나는 그것을 더듬어 찾아 둘둘 비틀어 말고는, 꺼져 가는 불꽃에 갖다 대어 불을 붙였지."]], [["“But beneath my fingers, as if by magic, in proportion as the fire ascended, I saw yellowish characters appear on the paper.", "\"그런데 내 손가락 밑에서, 마치 마법처럼, 불길이 타올라 가는 데 따라 누르스름한 글자들이 종이 위에 나타나는 게 보였네. 나는 그것을 손에 움켜쥐고 되도록 빨리 불을 끈 뒤, 바로 그 불에 초를 켜고는, 형언할 수 없는 감격에 휩싸여 구겨진 종이를 펼쳤네."], ["I grasped it in my hand, put out the flame as quickly as I could, lighted my taper in the fire itself, and opened the crumpled paper with inexpressible emotion, recognizing, when I had done so, that these characters had been traced in mysterious and sympathetic ink, only appearing when exposed to the fire; nearly one-third of the paper had been consumed by the flame.", "그러고 나서 알아챘지. 그 글자들이 신비로운 발색 잉크로 쓰여, 불에 쬘 때만 나타난다는 것을. 종이의 거의 삼분의 일이 불에 타 버린 뒤였네."], ["It was that paper you read this morning; read it again, Dantès, and then I will complete for you the incomplete words and unconnected sense.”", "그게 바로 자네가 오늘 아침 읽은 종이일세. 다시 읽어 보게, 당테스. 그러면 끊긴 낱말과 이어지지 않는 뜻을 자네를 위해 채워 주겠네.\""]], [["Faria, with an air of triumph, offered the paper to Dantès, who this time read the following words, traced with an ink of a reddish color resembling rust:", "파리아는 의기양양한 기색으로 당테스에게 종이를 내밀었고, 이번에 당테스는 녹처럼 불그스름한 잉크로 쓰인 다음 글자들을 읽었다."]], [["“This 25th day of April, 1498, be...", "\"1498년 4월 25일, 이날에……"]], [["Alexander VI., and fearing that not...", "알렉산데르 6세, 그리고 ……않을까 두려워……"]], [["he may desire to become my heir, and re...", "그가 나의 상속인이 되고자 하여, 그리고 다……"]], [["and Bentivoglio, who were poisoned,...", "독살된 벤티볼리오를 ……"]], [["my sole heir, that I have bu...", "나의 유일한 상속인에게, 내가 묻……"]], [["and has visited with me, that is, in...", "나와 함께 가 본 곳, 곧……"]], [["Island of Monte Cristo, all I poss...", "몬테크리스토섬에, 내가 가진 모든 것……"]], [["jewels, diamonds, gems; that I alone...", "보석, 다이아몬드, 보물을. 나만이 홀로……"]], [["may amount to nearly two mil...", "거의 이백만……에 이를 수도……"]], [["will find on raising the twentieth ro...", "스무 번째 바위를 들어 올리면 찾……"]], [["creek to the east in a right line. Two open...", "동쪽 만에서 일직선으로. 두 개의 입……"]], [["in these caves; the treasure is in the furthest a...", "이 동굴들 안에. 보물은 가장 깊은……"]], [["which treasure I bequeath and leave en...", "그 보물을 나는 남기며 전부 물려……"]], [["as my sole heir.", "나의 유일한 상속인으로서."]], [["“25th April, 1498.", "1498년 4월 25일."]], [["“Cæs...", "체사……\""]], [["“And now,”", "\"그리고 이제,\""], ["said the abbé, “read this other paper;”", "신부가 말했다. \"이 다른 종이를 읽어 보게.\""], ["and he presented to Dantès a second leaf with fragments of lines written on it, which Edmond read as follows:", "그가 당테스에게 두 번째 장을 내밀었다. 거기에는 토막 난 줄들이 적혀 있었고, 에드몽은 다음과 같이 읽었다."]], [["“...ing invited to dine by his Holiness", "\"……성하의 만찬에 초대받아"]], [["...content with making me pay for my hat,", "……내게 모자값을 치르게 한 것에 만족하지 않고"]], [["...serves for me the fate of Cardinals Caprara", "……카프라라 추기경의 운명을 내게 마련해 두니"]], [["...I declare to my nephew, Guido Spada", "……나의 조카 귀도 스파다에게 선언하노라"]], [["...ried in a place he knows", "……그가 아는 곳에 묻혀"]], [["...the caves of the small", "……작은 섬의 동굴들"]], [["...essed of ingots, gold, money,", "……주괴, 금, 돈,"]], [["...know of the existence of this treasure, which", "……이 보물의 존재를 아는 것은"]], [["...lions of Roman crowns, and which he", "……로마 크라운으로, 그리고 그가"]], [["...ck from the small", "……작은 ……에서"]], [["...ings have been made", "……입구가 만들어졌으며"]], [["...ngle in the second;", "……두 번째의 모퉁이에"]], [["...tire to him", "……그에게 전부 물려"]], [["...ar † Spada.”", "체사 † 스파다.\""]], [["Faria followed him with an excited look.", "파리아는 흥분한 눈빛으로 그를 지켜보았다."]], [["“And now,”", "\"자, 이제,\""], ["he said, when he saw that Dantès had read the last line, “put the two fragments together, and judge for yourself.”", "당테스가 마지막 줄을 다 읽은 것을 보고 그가 말했다. \"두 조각을 한데 맞춰 보게. 그리고 자네 스스로 판단해 보게나.\""], ["Dantès obeyed, and the conjointed pieces gave the following:", "당테스는 그대로 했고, 이어 맞춘 조각들은 다음과 같았다."]], [["0245m\n“This 25th day of April, 1498, be...ing invited to dine by his Holiness Alexander VI., and fearing that not...content with making me pay for my hat, he may desire to become my heir, and re...serves for me the fate of Cardinals Caprara and Bentivoglio, who were poisoned,...I declare to my nephew, Guido Spada, my sole heir, that I have bu...ried in a place he knows and has visited with me, that is, in...the caves of the small Island of Monte Cristo, all I poss...essed of ingots, gold, money, jewels, diamonds, gems; that I alone...know of the existence of this treasure, which may amount to nearly two mil...lions of Roman crowns, and which he will find on raising the twentieth ro...ck from the small creek to the east in a right line.", "\"1498년 4월 25일, 이날에……성하의 만찬에 초대받아 알렉산데르 6세, 그리고 ……않을까 두려워……내게 모자값을 치르게 한 것에 만족하지 않고, 그가 나의 상속인이 되고자 하여, 그리고 다……카프라라 추기경과 독살된 벤티볼리오의 운명을 내게 마련해 두니,……나의 조카 귀도 스파다, 나의 유일한 상속인에게 선언하노라. 내가 묻……그가 아는 곳, 나와 함께 가 본 곳, 곧……작은 몬테크리스토섬의 동굴들에, 내가 가진 모든 것……주괴, 금, 돈, 보석, 다이아몬드, 보물을. 나만이 홀로……이 보물의 존재를 아는 것은, 거의 이백만……로마 크라운에 이를 수도 있으며, 그가……작은 만에서 스무 번째 바위를 들어 올리면 동쪽으로 일직선으로 찾……게 될 것이다."], ["Two open...ings have been made in these caves; the treasure is in the furthest a...ngle in the second; which treasure I bequeath and leave en...tire to him as my sole heir.", "두 개의 입……구가 이 동굴들 안에 만들어졌으며. 보물은 가장 깊은……두 번째의 모퉁이에. 그 보물을 나는 나의 유일한 상속인으로서 그에게 전부 물려……주며 남기노라."], ["“25th April, 1498. “Cæs...ar † Spada.”", "1498년 4월 25일. 체사……체사 † 스파다.\""]], [["“Well, do you comprehend now?” inquired Faria.", "\"자, 이제 알겠나?\" 파리아가 물었다."]], [["“It is the declaration of Cardinal Spada, and the will so long sought for,”", "\"스파다 추기경의 선언이자, 그토록 오래 찾아 헤맨 유언장이군요.\""], ["replied Edmond, still incredulous.", "여전히 믿지 못하며 에드몽이 대답했다."]], [["“Yes; a thousand times, yes!”", "\"그래. 천 번이고 그렇다네, 그래!\""]], [["“And who completed it as it now is?”", "\"그런데 지금처럼 이렇게 완성한 사람은 누굽니까?\""]], [["“I did.", "\"나일세."], ["Aided by the remaining fragment, I guessed the rest; measuring the length of the lines by those of the paper, and divining the hidden meaning by means of what was in part revealed, as we are guided in a cavern by the small ray of light above us.”", "남아 있는 조각의 도움을 받아 나머지를 짐작했지. 종이의 길이로 줄의 길이를 가늠하고, 일부 드러난 것을 실마리 삼아 숨겨진 뜻을 헤아렸네. 마치 동굴 속에서 머리 위의 작은 빛줄기에 인도되듯이 말이야.\""]], [["“And what did you do when you arrived at this conclusion?”", "\"그래서 이 결론에 이르렀을 때 어떻게 하셨습니까?\""]], [["“I resolved to set out, and did set out at that very instant, carrying with me the beginning of my great work, the unity of the Italian kingdom; but for some time the imperial police (who at this period, quite contrary to what Napoleon desired so soon as he had a son born to him, wished for a partition of provinces) had their eyes on me; and my hasty departure, the cause of which they were unable to guess, having aroused their suspicions, I was arrested at the very moment I was leaving Piombino.", "\"길을 떠나기로 결심했고, 바로 그 순간 떠났네. 내 위대한 과업, 곧 이탈리아 왕국 통일의 첫 단초를 지니고 말이야. 하지만 한동안 황제의 경찰이—이 무렵 그들은 나폴레옹이 아들을 얻자마자 그토록 바라던 것과는 정반대로, 지방들의 분할을 원하고 있었지—나를 감시하고 있었네. 그리고 그들이 까닭을 짐작하지 못한 내 황급한 출발이 그들의 의심을 불러일으켜, 나는 피옴비노를 떠나려던 바로 그 순간 체포되고 말았지."]], [["“Now,”", "\"이제,\""], ["continued Faria, addressing Dantès with an almost paternal expression, “now, my dear fellow, you know as much as I do myself.", "파리아가 거의 아버지 같은 표정으로 당테스를 향해 말을 이었다. \"이제 자네는 나만큼이나 다 알게 됐네."], ["If we ever escape together, half this treasure is yours; if I die here, and you escape alone, the whole belongs to you.”", "우리가 언젠가 함께 탈출한다면 이 보물의 절반은 자네 것일세. 내가 여기서 죽고 자네 혼자 탈출한다면, 그 전부가 자네 것이고.\""]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["inquired Dantès hesitating, “has this treasure no more legitimate possessor in the world than ourselves?”", "당테스가 머뭇거리며 물었다. \"이 보물에는 우리 말고 더 정당한 임자가 세상에 없는 겁니까?\""]], [["“No, no, be easy on that score; the family is extinct.", "\"없네, 없어. 그 점은 안심하게. 가문은 끊어졌으니까."], ["The last Count of Spada, moreover, made me his heir, bequeathing to me this symbolic breviary, he bequeathed to me all it contained; no, no, make your mind satisfied on that point.", "게다가 마지막 스파다 백작은 나를 상속인으로 삼아, 이 상징적인 기도서를 내게 남기면서 그 안에 담긴 모든 것을 내게 물려주셨네. 아니, 아니야, 그 점은 마음 놓아도 되네."], ["If we lay hands on this fortune, we may enjoy it without remorse.”", "우리가 이 재산에 손을 댄다 해도, 양심의 가책 없이 누릴 수 있어.\""]], [["“And you say this treasure amounts to——”", "\"그래서 이 보물이 얼마나 된다고 하셨지요……\""]], [["“Two millions of Roman crowns; nearly thirteen millions of our money.”2", "\"이백만 로마 크라운. 우리 돈으로 거의 천삼백만 프랑일세.\""]], [["“Impossible!”", "\"그럴 리가요!\""], ["said Dantès, staggered at the enormous amount.", "당테스가 그 엄청난 액수에 아연실색하며 말했다."]], [["“Impossible? and why?”", "\"그럴 리가 없다고? 어째서 말인가?\""], ["asked the old man.", "노인이 물었다."], ["“The Spada family was one of the oldest and most powerful families of the fifteenth century; and in those times, when other opportunities for investment were wanting, such accumulations of gold and jewels were by no means rare; there are at this day Roman families perishing of hunger, though possessed of nearly a million in diamonds and jewels, handed down by entail, and which they cannot touch.”", "\"스파다 가문은 15세기의 가장 오래되고 강력한 가문 가운데 하나였네. 그리고 그 시절에는 달리 투자할 기회가 없었으니, 그렇게 금과 보석을 쌓아 두는 일이 결코 드물지 않았지. 오늘날에도 한정 상속으로 물려받은, 거의 백만에 이르는 다이아몬드와 보석을 가지고 있으면서도 손을 댈 수 없어 굶어 죽어 가는 로마 가문들이 있다네.\""]], [["Edmond thought he was in a dream—he wavered between incredulity and joy.", "에드몽은 꿈을 꾸는 것만 같았다. 그는 불신과 기쁨 사이에서 갈팡질팡했다."]], [["“I have only kept this secret so long from you,”", "\"내가 이 비밀을 자네에게 이토록 오래 감춰 둔 것은,\""], ["continued Faria, “that I might test your character, and then surprise you.", "파리아가 말을 이었다. \"자네의 사람됨을 시험해 본 다음, 자네를 놀라게 해 주려는 마음에서였네."], ["Had we escaped before my attack of catalepsy, I should have conducted you to Monte Cristo; now,”", "내가 강경증 발작을 일으키기 전에 우리가 탈출했더라면, 나는 자네를 몬테크리스토섬으로 데려갔을 걸세. 하지만 이제는,\""], ["he added, with a sigh, “it is you who will conduct me thither.", "그가 한숨을 쉬며 덧붙였다. \"자네가 나를 그리로 데려가게 되겠지."], ["Well, Dantès, you do not thank me?”", "그래, 당테스, 자네 내게 고맙다는 말도 안 하는군?\""]], [["“This treasure belongs to you, my dear friend,”", "\"이 보물은 당신 것입니다, 친애하는 친구여.\" 당테스가 대답했다."], ["replied Dantès, “and to you only. I have no right to it.", "\"오직 당신만의 것이지요. 저에겐 그것에 대한 아무런 권리도 없습니다."], ["I am no relation of yours.”", "저는 당신의 친척도 아닌걸요.\""]], [["“You are my son, Dantès,” exclaimed the old man.", "\"자네는 내 아들일세, 당테스.\" 노인이 외쳤다."], ["“You are the child of my captivity.", "\"자네는 내 갇힌 세월이 낳은 자식이야."], ["My profession condemns me to celibacy.", "내 직분이 나를 독신으로 묶어 두었지."], ["God has sent you to me to console, at one and the same time, the man who could not be a father, and the prisoner who could not get free.”", "하느님께서 아버지가 될 수 없었던 사내와, 자유로워질 수 없었던 죄수를 한꺼번에 위로하시려고 자네를 내게 보내 주신 걸세.\""]], [["And Faria extended the arm of which alone the use remained to him to the young man, who threw himself upon his neck and wept.", "그러고는 파리아가 자신에게 유일하게 쓸 수 있는 팔을 청년에게 뻗었고, 청년은 그의 목에 몸을 던지며 흐느꼈다."]], [["# Chapter 19. The Third Attack", "# Chapter 19. The Third Attack"]], [["Now that this treasure, which had so long been the object of the abbé’s meditations, could insure the future happiness of him whom Faria really loved as a son, it had doubled its value in his eyes, and every day he expatiated on the amount, explaining to Dantès all the good which, with thirteen or fourteen millions of francs, a man could do in these days to his friends; and then Dantès’ countenance became gloomy, for the oath of vengeance he had taken recurred to his memory, and he reflected how much ill, in these times, a man with thirteen or fourteen millions could do to his enemies.", "* 발색 잉크: 열을 가하면 글자가 드러나는 잉크. —옮긴이\n그토록 오랫동안 신부의 명상의 대상이었던 그 보물이 이제 파리아가 친아들처럼 사랑하는 사람의 앞날에 행복을 보장해 줄 수 있게 되자, 그의 눈에 그 가치는 두 배로 불어났다. 그는 날마다 그 액수를 늘어놓으며, 천삼백만이나 천사백만 프랑을 손에 쥔 사람이 요즘 세상에서 벗들에게 얼마나 많은 선을 베풀 수 있는지 당테스에게 설명했다. 그러면 당테스의 얼굴은 어두워졌다. 그가 맹세한 복수의 다짐이 떠올랐기 때문이었고, 요즘 세상에서 천삼백만이나 천사백만을 가진 사람이 자기 원수들에게 얼마나 많은 해악을 끼칠 수 있는지를 곱씹었기 때문이었다."]], [["The abbé did not know the Island of Monte Cristo; but Dantès knew it, and had often passed it, situated twenty-five miles from Pianosa, between Corsica and the Island of Elba, and had once touched there.", "신부는 몬테크리스토섬을 알지 못했다. 그러나 당테스는 그 섬을 알았고, 여러 번 그 곁을 지난 적이 있었다. 코르시카섬과 엘바섬 사이, 피아노사에서 이십오 마일 떨어진 곳에 자리한 섬이었고, 한 번은 그곳에 닿은 적도 있었다."], ["This island was, always had been, and still is, completely deserted.", "이 섬은 늘 완전히 무인도였고, 지금도 그러하다."], ["It is a rock of almost conical form, which looks as though it had been thrust up by volcanic force from the depth to the surface of the ocean.", "거의 원뿔 모양의 바위로, 마치 화산의 힘이 바다 밑바닥에서 수면 위로 밀어 올린 것처럼 보였다."], ["Dantès drew a plan of the island for Faria, and Faria gave Dantès advice as to the means he should employ to recover the treasure.", "당테스가 파리아에게 그 섬의 지도를 그려 주자, 파리아는 보물을 되찾기 위해 써야 할 방법에 대해 당테스에게 조언했다."], ["But Dantès was far from being as enthusiastic and confident as the old man.", "그러나 당테스는 노인만큼 열정적이지도, 확신에 차 있지도 않았다."], ["It was past a question now that Faria was not a lunatic, and the way in which he had achieved the discovery, which had given rise to the suspicion of his madness, increased Edmond’s admiration of him; but at the same time Dantès could not believe that the deposit, supposing it had ever existed, still existed; and though he considered the treasure as by no means chimerical, he yet believed it was no longer there.", "파리아가 미치광이가 아니라는 것은 이제 의심할 여지가 없었고, 그가 광기로 의심받게 만든 그 발견에 이른 과정을 알게 되자 에드몽의 존경심은 더욱 커졌다. 그러나 동시에 당테스는 그 보물이 — 설령 한때 존재했다 하더라도 — 지금까지 그대로 남아 있으리라고는 믿을 수 없었다. 보물이 결코 허황된 이야기는 아니라고 여기면서도, 이제는 그 자리에 없으리라고 생각했던 것이다."]], [["However, as if fate resolved on depriving the prisoners of their last chance, and making them understand that they were condemned to perpetual imprisonment, a new misfortune befell them; the gallery on the sea side, which had long been in ruins, was rebuilt.", "그러나 운명은 이 죄수들에게서 마지막 기회마저 앗아 가, 그들이 영원한 감금형에 처해졌음을 깨닫게 하려는 듯, 새로운 불행을 안겼다. 오랫동안 허물어진 채 방치되어 있던 바다 쪽 회랑이 다시 세워진 것이다."], ["They had repaired it completely, and stopped up with vast masses of stone the hole Dantès had partly filled in.", "그들은 그곳을 완전히 보수했고, 당테스가 일부 메워 두었던 구멍을 거대한 돌덩이들로 틀어막았다."], ["But for this precaution, which, it will be remembered, the abbé had made to Edmond, the misfortune would have been still greater, for their attempt to escape would have been detected, and they would undoubtedly have been separated.", "기억하다시피 신부가 에드몽에게 일러 준 그 조심성이 아니었더라면, 불행은 더 컸을 것이다. 그들의 탈출 시도가 발각되었을 테고, 두 사람은 틀림없이 떨어뜨려졌을 테니까."], ["Thus a new, a stronger, and more inexorable barrier was interposed to cut off the realization of their hopes.", "이렇게 더 새롭고, 더 견고하며, 더 가차 없는 장벽이 가로놓여 그들의 희망이 실현될 길을 끊어 놓았다."]], [["“You see,”", "\"보십시오.\""], ["said the young man, with an air of sorrowful resignation, to Faria, “that God deems it right to take from me any claim to merit for what you call my devotion to you.", "젊은이가 슬프게 체념한 표정으로 파리아에게 말했다. \"하느님께서는 신부님이 제 헌신이라고 부르시는 것에 대해 제가 어떤 공로도 주장하지 못하도록 하시는 게 옳다고 여기시나 봅니다."], ["I have promised to remain forever with you, and now I could not break my promise if I would.", "저는 신부님 곁에 영원히 남겠다고 약속드렸고, 이제는 설령 그러고 싶다 해도 그 약속을 깰 수 없게 되었으니까요."], ["The treasure will be no more mine than yours, and neither of us will quit this prison.", "그 보물은 제 것도, 신부님 것도 아니게 될 테고, 우리 둘 다 이 감옥을 떠나지 못할 겁니다."], ["But my real treasure is not that, my dear friend, which awaits me beneath the sombre rocks of Monte Cristo, it is your presence, our living together five or six hours a day, in spite of our jailers; it is the rays of intelligence you have elicited from my brain, the languages you have implanted in my memory, and which have taken root there with all their philological ramifications.", "하지만 제 진짜 보물은 몬테크리스토섬의 음침한 바위 밑에서 저를 기다리는 그것이 아닙니다, 사랑하는 벗이시여. 제 진짜 보물은 신부님이 곁에 계신다는 것, 간수들의 눈을 피해 하루에 대여섯 시간을 함께 살아간다는 것입니다. 신부님이 제 머릿속에서 끌어내 주신 지성의 빛이고, 제 기억 속에 심어 주신 언어들이며, 그것들이 온갖 문헌학적 가지를 뻗으며 거기 뿌리내린 것입니다."], ["These different sciences that you have made so easy to me by the depth of the knowledge you possess of them, and the clearness of the principles to which you have reduced them—this is my treasure, my beloved friend, and with this you have made me rich and happy.", "신부님이 그토록 깊이 알고 계신 지식과, 그것을 명료한 원리로 간추려 주신 덕분에 제게 그토록 쉬워진 이 갖가지 학문들 — 이것이 제 보물입니다, 사랑하는 벗이시여. 신부님은 이것으로 저를 부유하고 행복하게 만들어 주셨습니다. 제 말을 믿으시고, 위안을 얻으십시오."], ["Believe me, and take comfort, this is better for me than tons of gold and cases of diamonds, even were they not as problematical as the clouds we see in the morning floating over the sea, which we take for _terra firma_, and which evaporate and vanish as we draw near to them.", "이것이 제게는 수 톤의 금이나 상자에 가득한 다이아몬드보다 낫습니다. 설령 그것들이, 아침에 바다 위를 떠다니는 구름처럼 — 우리가 육지인 줄 알았다가 다가서면 흩어져 사라지는 그 구름처럼 — 허황된 것이 아니라 해도 말입니다."], ["To have you as long as possible near me, to hear your eloquent speech,—which embellishes my mind, strengthens my soul, and makes my whole frame capable of great and terrible things, if I should ever be free,—so fills my whole existence, that the despair to which I was just on the point of yielding when I knew you, has no longer any hold over me; and this—this is my fortune—not chimerical, but actual.", "신부님을 되도록 오래 곁에 두는 것, 신부님의 그 유창한 말씀을 듣는 것 — 그 말씀은 제 정신을 아름답게 하고, 제 영혼을 굳세게 하며, 언젠가 제가 자유로워진다면 위대하고도 무서운 일들을 해낼 수 있는 사람으로 제 온몸을 빚어 줍니다 — 그것이 제 삶 전체를 가득 채워서, 신부님을 알기 직전까지 제가 막 굴복하려던 그 절망이 이제는 저를 조금도 사로잡지 못합니다. 그리고 이것이 — 이것이 제 재산입니다. 허황된 것이 아니라, 실제로 존재하는 것이지요."], ["I owe you my real good, my present happiness; and all the sovereigns of the earth, even Cæsar Borgia himself, could not deprive me of this.”", "저는 신부님께 제 진정한 행복을, 제 지금의 행복을 빚지고 있습니다. 그리고 이 땅의 어떤 군주도, 심지어 체사레 보르자조차도 제게서 이것을 빼앗을 수 없을 겁니다.\""]], [["Thus, if not actually happy, yet the days these two unfortunates passed together went quickly.", "이렇게, 정녕 행복하다고는 할 수 없어도, 이 두 불운한 사람이 함께 보내는 나날은 빠르게 흘러갔다."], ["Faria, who for so long a time had kept silence as to the treasure, now perpetually talked of it.", "그토록 오랫동안 보물에 대해 입을 다물고 있던 파리아는 이제 끊임없이 그 이야기를 했다."], ["As he had prophesied would be the case, he remained paralyzed in the right arm and the left leg, and had given up all hope of ever enjoying it himself.", "그가 예언했던 대로, 그는 오른팔과 왼다리가 마비된 채로 지냈고, 자신이 직접 그 보물을 누리리라는 희망은 모두 버린 상태였다."], ["But he was continually thinking over some means of escape for his young companion, and anticipating the pleasure he would enjoy.", "그러나 그는 자기 젊은 동료가 탈출할 방도를 끊임없이 궁리했고, 당테스가 누릴 즐거움을 미리 그려 보았다."], ["For fear the letter might be some day lost or stolen, he compelled Dantès to learn it by heart; and Dantès knew it from the first to the last word.", "그 편지가 언젠가 분실되거나 도둑맞을 것을 염려한 그는, 당테스에게 그것을 통째로 외우게 했다. 당테스는 첫 글자부터 마지막 글자까지 그것을 알게 되었다."], ["Then he destroyed the second portion, assured that if the first were seized, no one would be able to discover its real meaning.", "그러자 파리아는 편지의 둘째 부분을 없애 버렸다. 설령 첫째 부분이 압수된다 해도 누구도 그 진짜 뜻을 알아낼 수 없으리라 확신했기 때문이다."], ["Whole hours sometimes passed while Faria was giving instructions to Dantès,—instructions which were to serve him when he was at liberty.", "파리아가 당테스에게 지시를 내리느라 몇 시간이 통째로 지나가기도 했다 — 당테스가 자유의 몸이 되었을 때 도움이 될 지시들이었다."], ["Then, once free, from the day and hour and moment when he was so, he could have but one only thought, which was, to gain Monte Cristo by some means, and remain there alone under some pretext which would arouse no suspicions; and once there, to endeavor to find the wonderful caverns, and search in the appointed spot,—the appointed spot, be it remembered, being the farthest angle in the second opening.", "그렇게 일단 자유로워지면, 그날 그 시각 그 순간부터, 그는 오직 한 가지 생각만 품어야 했다. 어떻게든 몬테크리스토섬에 닿아, 어떤 의심도 사지 않을 구실을 대고 홀로 그곳에 머물 것. 그리고 일단 그곳에 닿으면, 그 놀라운 동굴들을 찾아내어 정해진 자리를 뒤질 것 — 기억하다시피 그 정해진 자리란, 두 번째로 뚫린 입구의 가장 안쪽 구석이었다."]], [["In the meanwhile the hours passed, if not rapidly, at least tolerably.", "그러는 동안 시간은, 빠르다고는 못해도 적어도 견딜 만하게 흘러갔다."], ["Faria, as we have said, without having recovered the use of his hand and foot, had regained all the clearness of his understanding, and had gradually, besides the moral instructions we have detailed, taught his youthful companion the patient and sublime duty of a prisoner, who learns to make something from nothing.", "앞서 말했듯이 파리아는 손과 발의 기능은 되찾지 못했어도 정신의 명료함은 모두 되찾았고, 우리가 상세히 설명한 도덕적 가르침 외에도, 무에서 무언가를 만들어 내는 법을 배우는 죄수의 인내롭고 숭고한 의무를 자기 젊은 동료에게 차츰 가르쳤다. 그리하여 두 사람은 끊임없이 무언가에 매달렸다."], ["They were thus perpetually employed,—Faria, that he might not see himself grow old; Dantès, for fear of recalling the almost extinct past which now only floated in his memory like a distant light wandering in the night.", "파리아는 자신이 늙어 가는 것을 보지 않기 위해, 당테스는 이제 밤중에 떠도는 먼 불빛처럼 기억 속에 가물거리는, 거의 꺼져 버린 과거를 떠올릴까 두려워서였다."], ["So life went on for them as it does for those who are not victims of misfortune and whose activities glide along mechanically and tranquilly beneath the eye of Providence.", "그렇게 그들의 삶은, 불행의 희생자가 아닌 사람들에게서, 섭리의 눈길 아래 기계적으로 또 평온하게 흘러가는 사람들에게서 그러하듯 이어졌다."]], [["But beneath this superficial calm there were in the heart of the young man, and perhaps in that of the old man, many repressed desires, many stifled sighs, which found vent when Faria was left alone, and when Edmond returned to his cell.", "그러나 이 겉으로 드러난 평온 아래, 젊은이의 가슴속에는, 어쩌면 노인의 가슴속에도, 억눌린 숱한 욕망과 짓눌린 숱한 한숨이 도사리고 있었다. 그것들은 파리아가 홀로 남고 에드몽이 제 감방으로 돌아갔을 때 비로소 터져 나왔다."]], [["One night Edmond awoke suddenly, believing that he heard someone calling him.", "어느 날 밤 에드몽은 누군가 자기를 부르는 소리를 들은 것 같아 문득 잠에서 깼다."], ["He opened his eyes upon utter darkness.", "그가 눈을 떠 보니 칠흑 같은 어둠뿐이었다."], ["His name, or rather a plaintive voice which essayed to pronounce his name, reached him.", "자기 이름이, 아니 그 이름을 부르려 애쓰는 애처로운 목소리가 그에게 들려왔다."], ["He sat up in bed and a cold sweat broke out upon his brow.", "그는 침대에서 몸을 일으켰고, 이마에 식은땀이 솟았다."], ["Undoubtedly the call came from Faria’s dungeon.", "그 부르는 소리는 틀림없이 파리아의 지하 감방에서 나오고 있었다."]], [["“Alas,”", "\"아아.\""], ["murmured Edmond; “can it be?”", "에드몽이 중얼거렸다. \"설마, 그럴 리가?\""]], [["He moved his bed, drew up the stone, rushed into the passage, and reached the opposite extremity; the secret entrance was open.", "그는 침대를 옮기고, 돌을 들어 올린 뒤, 통로로 뛰어들어 반대편 끝에 이르렀다. 비밀 입구가 열려 있었다."], ["By the light of the wretched and wavering lamp, of which we have spoken, Dantès saw the old man, pale, but yet erect, clinging to the bedstead.", "앞서 말한 그 초라하고 가물거리는 등불 빛으로, 당테스는 노인이 창백한 얼굴로, 그래도 몸을 세운 채 침대 틀에 매달려 있는 것을 보았다."], ["His features were writhing with those horrible symptoms which he already knew, and which had so seriously alarmed him when he saw them for the first time.", "그의 얼굴은, 이미 알고 있던, 처음 보았을 때 그를 그토록 심각하게 놀라게 했던 그 끔찍한 증상들로 일그러지고 있었다."]], [["“Alas, my dear friend,”", "\"아아, 사랑하는 벗이여.\""], ["said Faria in a resigned tone, “you understand, do you not, and I need not attempt to explain to you?”", "파리아가 체념한 어조로 말했다. \"자네는 알아챘겠지, 안 그런가? 굳이 설명할 것도 없겠지?\""]], [["Edmond uttered a cry of agony, and, quite out of his senses, rushed towards the door, exclaiming, “Help, help!”", "에드몽은 비통한 외침을 토하며, 거의 제정신이 아닌 채로 문을 향해 달려가 소리쳤다. \"도와줘요, 도와줘요!\""]], [["Faria had just sufficient strength to restrain him.", "파리아는 그를 가까스로 붙잡을 만큼의 힘은 남아 있었다."]], [["“Silence,” he said, “or you are lost.", "\"조용히 하게.\" 그가 말했다."], ["We must now only think of you, my dear friend, and so act as to render your captivity supportable or your flight possible.", "\"안 그러면 자네는 끝장이야. 이제 우리는 자네만 생각해야 하네, 사랑하는 벗이여. 자네의 옥살이를 견딜 만하게 하든가, 자네의 탈출을 가능하게 하든가, 그렇게 움직여야 해."], ["It would require years to do again what I have done here, and the results would be instantly destroyed if our jailers knew we had communicated with each other.", "내가 여기서 한 일을 다시 하려면 몇 년이 걸릴 테고, 우리가 서로 통했다는 걸 간수들이 알게 되면 그 성과는 당장에 무너지고 말 걸세."], ["Besides, be assured, my dear Edmond, the dungeon I am about to leave will not long remain empty; some other unfortunate being will soon take my place, and to him you will appear like an angel of salvation.", "게다가 안심하게, 사랑하는 에드몽. 내가 곧 떠날 이 지하 감방은 오래 비어 있지 않을 거야. 또 다른 불운한 자가 곧 내 자리를 차지할 테고, 그에게 자네는 구원의 천사처럼 보일 걸세."], ["Perhaps he will be young, strong, and enduring, like yourself, and will aid you in your escape, while I have been but a hindrance.", "어쩌면 그자는 자네처럼 젊고, 강하고, 끈질긴 사람이라, 자네의 탈출을 도울 수 있을지도 모르지. 나는 그저 걸림돌이었을 뿐인데 말이야."], ["You will no longer have half a dead body tied to you as a drag to all your movements.", "자네는 더 이상 자네의 모든 움직임에 족쇄처럼 매달린 반쯤 죽은 몸뚱이를 끌고 다니지 않아도 돼."], ["At length Providence has done something for you; he restores to you more than he takes away, and it was time I should die.”", "마침내 섭리가 자네를 위해 무언가를 해 준 거야. 빼앗아 가는 것보다 더 많은 것을 자네에게 되돌려 주는 셈이고, 내가 죽을 때도 된 거지.\""]], [["Edmond could only clasp his hands and exclaim, “Oh, my friend, my friend, speak not thus!”", "에드몽은 그저 두 손을 맞잡고 외칠 따름이었다. \"오, 벗이여, 벗이여, 그런 말씀 마십시오!\""], ["and then resuming all his presence of mind, which had for a moment staggered under this blow, and his strength, which had failed at the words of the old man, he said, “Oh, I have saved you once, and I will save you a second time!”", "그러고는 이 충격에 한순간 휘청거렸던 침착함을 모두 되찾고, 노인의 말에 꺾였던 기운을 다시 추슬러 말했다. \"오, 제가 한 번 신부님을 살려 냈으니, 두 번째로도 살려 내겠습니다!\""], ["And raising the foot of the bed, he drew out the phial, still a third filled with the red liquor.", "그러고는 침대 발치를 들어 올려, 아직 삼분의 일쯤 붉은 액체가 남아 있는 작은 병을 꺼냈다."]], [["“See,”", "\"보십시오.\""], ["he exclaimed, “there remains still some of the magic draught.", "그가 외쳤다. \"이 신비한 물약이 아직 좀 남아 있습니다."], ["Quick, quick!", "어서요, 어서!"], ["tell me what I must do this time; are there any fresh instructions?", "이번에는 제가 무얼 해야 하는지 말씀해 주세요. 새로 일러 주실 게 있습니까?"], ["Speak, my friend; I listen.”", "말씀하세요, 벗이여. 듣고 있습니다.\""]], [["“There is not a hope,”", "\"희망은 없네.\" 파리아가 고개를 저으며 대답했다."], ["replied Faria, shaking his head, “but no matter; God wills it that man whom he has created, and in whose heart he has so profoundly rooted the love of life, should do all in his power to preserve that existence, which, however painful it may be, is yet always so dear.”", "\"하지만 상관없어. 하느님은 당신이 지으신 인간이, 또 그 가슴속에 삶에 대한 사랑을 그토록 깊이 심어 놓으신 그 인간이, 제 목숨을 지키기 위해 할 수 있는 모든 일을 다하기를 바라시지. 그 목숨이란, 아무리 고통스러울지언정 언제나 그토록 소중한 법이니까.\""]], [["“Oh, yes, yes!”", "\"오, 그렇고말고요, 그렇고말고요!\""], ["exclaimed Dantès; “and I tell you that I will save you yet.”", "당테스가 외쳤다. \"분명히 말씀드리지만, 저는 기어이 신부님을 살려 내겠습니다.\""]], [["“Well, then, try. The cold gains upon me.", "\"좋아, 그럼 해 보게."], ["I feel the blood flowing towards my brain.", "한기가 점점 더 나를 덮쳐 오는군."], ["These horrible chills, which make my teeth chatter and seem to dislocate my bones, begin to pervade my whole frame; in five minutes the malady will reach its height, and in a quarter of an hour there will be nothing left of me but a corpse.”", "피가 머리로 몰리는 게 느껴져. 이가 딱딱 부딪치게 하고 뼈마디를 어긋나게 하는 것 같은 이 끔찍한 오한이 온몸으로 퍼지기 시작했어. 오 분 뒤면 발작이 절정에 이를 테고, 십오 분 뒤면 내게 남는 건 송장뿐일 걸세.\""]], [["“Oh!” exclaimed Dantès, his heart wrung with anguish.", "\"오!\" 당테스가 비통함에 가슴이 찢어지는 듯 외쳤다."]], [["“Do as you did before, only do not wait so long, all the springs of life are now exhausted in me, and death,”", "\"전에 했던 대로 하게. 다만 그렇게 오래 기다리지는 말게. 이제 내 안의 생명의 샘은 모두 말라 버렸고, 죽음은,\""], ["he continued, looking at his paralyzed arm and leg, “has but half its work to do.", "그가 마비된 팔과 다리를 바라보며 말을 이었다. \"이미 절반은 일을 끝내 놓았으니까."], ["If, after having made me swallow twelve drops instead of ten, you see that I do not recover, then pour the rest down my throat.", "열 방울 대신 열두 방울을 삼키게 한 뒤에도 내가 깨어나지 않거든, 그땐 남은 것을 모두 목에 부어 넣게."], ["Now lift me on my bed, for I can no longer support myself.”", "자, 이제 나를 침대에 눕혀 주게. 더는 내 몸을 가눌 수가 없어.\""]], [["Edmond took the old man in his arms, and laid him on the bed.", "에드몽은 노인을 두 팔로 안아 침대에 눕혔다."]], [["“And now, my dear friend,”", "\"그리고 이제, 사랑하는 벗이여.\""], ["said Faria, “sole consolation of my wretched existence,—you whom Heaven gave me somewhat late, but still gave me, a priceless gift, and for which I am most grateful,—at the moment of separating from you forever, I wish you all the happiness and all the prosperity you so well deserve.", "파리아가 말했다. \"내 비참한 삶의 유일한 위안이었던 사람 — 하늘이 다소 늦게 내게 보내 주었으나 그래도 보내 준, 값을 매길 수 없는 선물, 내가 더없이 감사하는 그 선물이여 — 자네와 영원히 헤어지는 이 순간에, 나는 자네가 마땅히 누릴 모든 행복과 모든 번영을 빌어 주네."], ["My son, I bless thee!”", "내 아들아, 너를 축복하노라!\""]], [["The young man cast himself on his knees, leaning his head against the old man’s bed.", "젊은이는 무릎을 꿇고, 노인의 침대에 머리를 기댔다."]], [["“Listen, now, to what I say in this my dying moment.", "\"이제, 죽어 가는 이 순간에 내가 하는 말을 들어 두게."], ["The treasure of the Spadas exists.", "스파다 가문의 보물은 실재하네."], ["God grants me the boon of vision unrestricted by time or space.", "하느님이 내게 시간과 공간에 얽매이지 않는 환시의 은총을 베푸셨어."], ["I see it in the depths of the inner cavern.", "안쪽 동굴 깊숙한 곳에 있는 그것이 보여."], ["My eyes pierce the inmost recesses of the earth, and are dazzled at the sight of so much riches.", "내 눈이 땅속 가장 깊은 구석까지 꿰뚫고 들어가, 그 엄청난 재물을 보고 눈이 부시는구나."], ["If you do escape, remember that the poor abbé, whom all the world called mad, was not so.", "만일 자네가 탈출하거든, 온 세상이 미치광이라 불렀던 그 가엾은 신부가 실은 미치지 않았다는 것을 기억하게."], ["Hasten to Monte Cristo—avail yourself of the fortune—for you have indeed suffered long enough.”", "어서 몬테크리스토섬으로 가게 — 그 재산을 차지하게 — 자네는 정말이지 충분히, 너무도 오래 고통받았으니까.\""]], [["A violent convulsion attacked the old man.", "격렬한 경련이 노인을 덮쳤다."], ["Dantès raised his head and saw Faria’s eyes injected with blood.", "당테스가 고개를 들어 보니 파리아의 두 눈에 피가 몰려 있었다."], ["It seemed as if a flow of blood had ascended from the chest to the head.", "마치 핏줄기가 가슴에서 머리로 거슬러 올라온 듯했다."]], [["“Adieu, adieu!”", "\"잘 있게, 잘 있게!\""], ["murmured the old man, clasping Edmond’s hand convulsively—“adieu!”", "노인이 에드몽의 손을 발작적으로 부여잡으며 중얼거렸다. \"잘 있게!\""]], [["“Oh, no,—no, not yet,”", "\"오, 안 돼요 — 안 돼요, 아직은 안 됩니다.\""], ["he cried; “do not forsake me!", "그가 외쳤다. \"저를 버리지 마세요!"], ["Oh, succor him!", "오, 이분을 구해 주세요!"], ["Help—help—help!”", "도와줘요 — 도와줘요 — 도와줘요!\""]], [["“Hush! hush!”", "\"쉿! 쉿!\""], ["murmured the dying man, “that they may not separate us if you save me!”", "죽어 가는 사람이 중얼거렸다. \"자네가 나를 살린다 해도, 저들이 우리를 떼어 놓지 못하게 해야지!\""]], [["“You are right.", "\"옳은 말씀입니다."], ["Oh, yes, yes; be assured I shall save you!", "오, 그래요, 그래요. 안심하세요, 제가 꼭 살려 내겠습니다!"], ["Besides, although you suffer much, you do not seem to be in such agony as you were before.”", "게다가, 신부님이 몹시 괴로워하시긴 해도, 전처럼 그렇게 고통스러워 보이지는 않으시는걸요.\""]], [["“Do not mistake!", "\"착각하지 말게!"], ["I suffer less because there is in me less strength to endure.", "내가 덜 괴로워하는 건, 견딜 힘이 그만큼 줄었기 때문이야."], ["At your age we have faith in life; it is the privilege of youth to believe and hope, but old men see death more clearly.", "자네 나이엔 삶을 믿지. 믿고 바라는 것은 젊음의 특권이지만, 늙은이는 죽음을 더 또렷이 본다네."], ["Oh, ’tis here—’tis here—’tis over—my sight is gone—my senses fail! Your hand, Dantès!", "오, 여기 왔어 — 여기 왔어 — 끝이야 — 시야가 사라지네 — 감각이 스러져! 자네 손을, 당테스! 잘 있게!"], ["Adieu! adieu!”", "잘 있게!\""]], [["And raising himself by a final effort, in which he summoned all his faculties, he said,—“Monte Cristo, forget not Monte Cristo!”", "그리고 그는 마지막 안간힘으로, 온 정신을 끌어모아 몸을 일으키며 말했다. \"몬테크리스토, 몬테크리스토를 잊지 말게!\""], ["And he fell back on the bed.", "그러고는 침대 위로 쓰러졌다."]], [["The crisis was terrible, and a rigid form with twisted limbs, swollen eyelids, and lips flecked with bloody foam, lay on the bed of torture, in place of the intellectual being who so lately rested there.", "발작은 끔찍했다. 좀 전까지 그곳에 누워 있던 그 지성적인 존재 대신, 사지가 뒤틀리고 눈꺼풀이 부어오르고 입술에 피거품이 얼룩진 뻣뻣한 형체가 그 고통의 침상에 누워 있었다."]], [["Dantès took the lamp, placed it on a projecting stone above the bed, whence its tremulous light fell with strange and fantastic ray on the distorted countenance and motionless, stiffened body.", "당테스는 등불을 집어 침대 위로 돌출된 돌 위에 올려놓았다. 거기서 떨리는 불빛이 기이하고 환상적인 빛줄기로 일그러진 얼굴과, 움직임 없이 굳어 버린 몸뚱이 위로 떨어졌다."], ["With steady gaze he awaited confidently the moment for administering the restorative.", "그는 흔들림 없는 눈길로, 회복약을 먹일 순간을 확신에 차서 기다렸다."]], [["When he believed that the right moment had arrived, he took the knife, pried open the teeth, which offered less resistance than before, counted one after the other twelve drops, and watched; the phial contained, perhaps, twice as much more.", "적절한 순간이 왔다고 여겨지자, 그는 칼을 들어, 전보다 덜 버티는 이를 비집어 벌리고, 열두 방울을 하나하나 세어 넣은 뒤 지켜보았다. 병에는 아마 그 두 배쯤 되는 액체가 더 남아 있었을 것이다."], ["He waited ten minutes, a quarter of an hour, half an hour,—no change took place.", "그는 십 분을, 십오 분을, 삼십 분을 기다렸다. 아무런 변화도 없었다."], ["Trembling, his hair erect, his brow bathed with perspiration, he counted the seconds by the beating of his heart.", "그는 부들부들 떨며, 머리카락을 곤두세운 채, 이마를 땀으로 적시며, 제 심장 박동으로 초를 세었다."], ["Then he thought it was time to make the last trial, and he put the phial to the purple lips of Faria, and without having occasion to force open his jaws, which had remained extended, he poured the whole of the liquid down his throat. 0255m", "그러고는 마지막 시도를 할 때라고 생각했다. 그는 작은 병을 파리아의 검푸른 입술에 갖다 댔고, 벌어진 채로 있던 턱을 억지로 벌릴 것도 없이, 남은 액체를 모두 그의 목으로 흘려 넣었다."]], [["The draught produced a galvanic effect, a violent trembling pervaded the old man’s limbs, his eyes opened until it was fearful to gaze upon them, he heaved a sigh which resembled a shriek, and then his convulsed body returned gradually to its former immobility, the eyes remaining open.", "물약은 전기 충격 같은 작용을 일으켰다. 격렬한 떨림이 노인의 사지를 훑고 지나갔고, 두 눈이 차마 바라볼 수 없을 만큼 크게 떠졌으며, 그는 비명에 가까운 한숨을 토했다. 그러더니 경련하던 몸뚱이가 차츰 본래의 부동 상태로 돌아갔고, 두 눈은 뜬 채로 남았다."]], [["Half an hour, an hour, an hour and a half elapsed, and during this period of anguish, Edmond leaned over his friend, his hand applied to his heart, and felt the body gradually grow cold, and the heart’s pulsation become more and more deep and dull, until at length it stopped; the last movement of the heart ceased, the face became livid, the eyes remained open, but the eyeballs were glazed.", "삼십 분이, 한 시간이, 한 시간 반이 지났다. 이 비통한 시간 동안, 에드몽은 벗 위로 몸을 숙이고 손을 그의 심장에 갖다 댄 채, 몸이 차츰 식어 가는 것을, 그리고 심장의 박동이 점점 더 깊고 둔해지다가 마침내 멎는 것을 느꼈다. 심장의 마지막 움직임이 그쳤고, 얼굴은 납빛이 되었으며, 두 눈은 뜬 채로였으나 눈동자는 흐릿하게 풀려 있었다."]], [["It was six o’clock in the morning, the dawn was just breaking, and its feeble ray came into the dungeon, and paled the ineffectual light of the lamp.", "아침 여섯 시였다. 동이 막 트고 있었고, 그 희미한 빛줄기가 지하 감방으로 들어와 등불의 무력한 불빛을 바래게 했다."], ["Strange shadows passed over the countenance of the dead man, and at times gave it the appearance of life.", "기이한 그림자들이 죽은 사람의 얼굴 위로 지나가며, 이따금 그것을 살아 있는 것처럼 보이게 했다."], ["While the struggle between day and night lasted, Dantès still doubted; but as soon as the daylight gained the pre-eminence, he saw that he was alone with a corpse.", "낮과 밤의 다툼이 계속되는 동안 당테스는 여전히 의심을 품었다. 그러나 햇빛이 우위를 차지하자마자, 그는 자신이 한 구의 시신과 단둘이 남아 있음을 깨달았다."], ["Then an invincible and extreme terror seized upon him, and he dared not again press the hand that hung out of bed, he dared no longer to gaze on those fixed and vacant eyes, which he tried many times to close, but in vain—they opened again as soon as shut.", "그러자 걷잡을 수 없는 극도의 공포가 그를 사로잡았다. 그는 침대 밖으로 늘어진 그 손을 다시 쥐어 볼 엄두를 내지 못했고, 그 멍하니 고정된 두 눈을 더는 바라볼 엄두도 내지 못했다. 그는 여러 번 그 눈을 감기려 했으나 헛수고였다."], ["He extinguished the lamp, carefully concealed it, and then went away, closing as well as he could the entrance to the secret passage by the large stone as he descended.", "감기면 이내 다시 떠졌던 것이다. 그는 등불을 끄고, 그것을 조심스레 감춘 뒤, 그곳을 떠났다. 내려가면서 비밀 통로의 입구를 그 큰 돌로 되도록 잘 막아 두었다."]], [["It was time, for the jailer was coming.", "때마침이었다. 간수가 오고 있었던 것이다."], ["On this occasion he began his rounds at Dantès’ cell, and on leaving him he went on to Faria’s dungeon, taking thither breakfast and some linen.", "이번에 그는 당테스의 감방부터 순회를 시작했고, 거기서 나오자 파리아의 지하 감방으로 가, 아침 식사와 새 속옷을 가져갔다."], ["Nothing betokened that the man knew anything of what had occurred.", "그 사내가 무슨 일이 일어났는지 알고 있다는 기색은 전혀 없었다."], ["He went on his way.", "그는 제 갈 길을 갔다."]], [["Dantès was then seized with an indescribable desire to know what was going on in the dungeon of his unfortunate friend.", "그러자 당테스는 불운한 벗의 지하 감방에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 알고 싶은, 형언할 수 없는 욕망에 사로잡혔다."], ["He therefore returned by the subterraneous gallery, and arrived in time to hear the exclamations of the turnkey, who called out for help.", "그리하여 그는 지하 회랑을 통해 되돌아갔고, 마침 도움을 청하며 소리치는 간수의 외침을 들을 수 있었다."], ["Other turnkeys came, and then was heard the regular tramp of soldiers.", "다른 간수들이 달려왔고, 이어 병사들의 규칙적인 발소리가 들려왔다."], ["Last of all came the governor.", "마지막으로 소장이 왔다."]], [["Edmond heard the creaking of the bed as they moved the corpse, heard the voice of the governor, who asked them to throw water on the dead man’s face; and seeing that, in spite of this application, the prisoner did not recover, they sent for the doctor.", "에드몽은 그들이 시신을 옮길 때 침대가 삐걱대는 소리를 들었고, 죽은 자의 얼굴에 물을 끼얹으라고 지시하는 소장의 목소리를 들었다. 그렇게 했는데도 죄수가 깨어나지 않자, 그들은 의사를 부르러 사람을 보냈다."], ["The governor then went out, and words of pity fell on Dantès’ listening ears, mingled with brutal laughter.", "그러고 나서 소장이 나갔고, 연민의 말들이, 거친 웃음소리와 뒤섞여 귀 기울이고 있던 당테스의 귀에 들려왔다."]], [["“Well, well,”", "\"흥, 흥.\""], ["said one, “the madman has gone to look after his treasure.", "누군가 말했다. \"미치광이가 제 보물을 찾으러 갔구먼."], ["Good journey to him!”", "잘 가라지!\""]], [["“With all his millions, he will not have enough to pay for his shroud!” said another.", "\"제깟 수백만을 다 가져도, 제 수의값도 못 치를걸!\" 다른 자가 말했다."]], [["“Oh,”", "\"아.\""], ["added a third voice, “the shrouds of the Château d’If are not dear!”", "세 번째 목소리가 덧붙였다. \"이프성의 수의야 비쌀 것도 없지!\""]], [["0257m\n“Perhaps,”", "\"하긴.\""], ["said one of the previous speakers, “as he was a churchman, they may go to some expense in his behalf.”", "앞서 말한 자들 중 하나가 말했다. \"성직자였으니, 그자를 위해 좀 돈을 쓸지도 모르지.\""]], [["“They may give him the honors of the sack.”", "\"자루의 영예를 베풀어 줄지도.\""]], [["Edmond did not lose a word, but comprehended very little of what was said.", "에드몽은 한마디도 놓치지 않았지만, 무슨 말인지는 거의 알아듣지 못했다."], ["The voices soon ceased, and it seemed to him as if everyone had left the cell.", "이내 목소리들이 그쳤고, 모두가 감방을 떠난 것처럼 여겨졌다."], ["Still he dared not to enter, as they might have left some turnkey to watch the dead.", "그래도 그는 안으로 들어갈 엄두를 내지 못했다. 죽은 자를 지키도록 간수 하나를 남겨 두었을지도 모를 일이었다."], ["He remained, therefore, mute and motionless, hardly venturing to breathe.", "그래서 그는 숨조차 제대로 쉬지 못한 채, 말없이 꼼짝도 않고 있었다."], ["At the end of an hour, he heard a faint noise, which increased.", "한 시간이 지나자 희미한 소리가 들려왔고, 점점 커졌다."], ["It was the governor who returned, followed by the doctor and other attendants.", "의사와 다른 수행원들을 거느리고 돌아온 소장이었다. 잠시 침묵이 흘렀다."], ["There was a moment’s silence,—it was evident that the doctor was examining the dead body.", "의사가 시신을 살펴보고 있는 게 분명했다."], ["The inquiries soon commenced.", "이내 조사가 시작되었다."]], [["The doctor analyzed the symptoms of the malady to which the prisoner had succumbed, and declared that he was dead.", "의사는 죄수가 굴복한 병의 증상들을 분석한 뒤, 그가 죽었다고 선언했다."], ["Questions and answers followed in a nonchalant manner that made Dantès indignant, for he felt that all the world should have for the poor abbé a love and respect equal to his own.", "질문과 대답이 무심한 투로 오갔고, 그것이 당테스를 분노케 했다. 그는 온 세상이 가엾은 신부에게 자신과 똑같은 사랑과 존경을 품어야 마땅하다고 느꼈기 때문이었다."]], [["“I am very sorry for what you tell me,”", "\"방금 한 말이 참 안됐군.\""], ["said the governor, replying to the assurance of the doctor, “that the old man is really dead; for he was a quiet, inoffensive prisoner, happy in his folly, and required no watching.”", "의사의 확언에 답하며 소장이 말했다. \"그 노인이 정말로 죽었다니 말이야. 조용하고 해를 끼치지 않는 죄수였고, 제 광기 속에서 행복했으며, 감시할 필요도 없었는데.\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"그렇습죠.\""], ["added the turnkey, “there was no occasion for watching him; he would have stayed here fifty years, I’ll answer for it, without any attempt to escape.”", "간수가 덧붙였다. \"그자는 감시할 까닭이 없었습니다. 장담컨대, 탈출이라곤 시도도 않고 여기서 오십 년이라도 지냈을 위인이었으니까요.\""]], [["“Still,”", "\"그래도.\""], ["said the governor, “I believe it will be requisite, notwithstanding your certainty, and not that I doubt your science, but in discharge of my official duty, that we should be perfectly assured that the prisoner is dead.”", "소장이 말했다. \"확신하시는 바와는 별개로, 그리고 선생의 의술을 의심해서가 아니라, 내 직무를 다하기 위해서, 죄수가 죽었다는 것을 완벽하게 확인해 두는 게 필요할 듯하오.\""]], [["There was a moment of complete silence, during which Dantès, still listening, knew that the doctor was examining the corpse a second time.", "완전한 침묵이 잠시 흘렀고, 그동안 여전히 귀 기울이고 있던 당테스는 의사가 두 번째로 시신을 살펴보고 있음을 알아챘다."]], [["“You may make your mind easy,”", "\"마음 놓으셔도 됩니다.\""], ["said the doctor; “he is dead.", "의사가 말했다. \"이 사람은 죽었습니다."], ["I will answer for that.”", "제가 보증하지요.\""]], [["“You know, sir,”", "\"아시다시피, 선생.\""], ["said the governor, persisting, “that we are not content in such cases as this with such a simple examination.", "소장이 고집스레 말했다. \"이런 경우에는 그처럼 간단한 검진만으로 만족할 수가 없소."], ["In spite of all appearances, be so kind, therefore, as to finish your duty by fulfilling the formalities described by law.”", "그러니 겉모습이야 어떻든, 부디 법이 정한 절차를 마저 밟아 직무를 끝내 주시오.\""]], [["“Let the irons be heated,”", "\"인두를 달구라고 하시오.\""], ["said the doctor; “but really it is a useless precaution.”", "의사가 말했다. \"하지만 정말이지 쓸데없는 조치입니다.\""]], [["This order to heat the irons made Dantès shudder.", "인두를 달구라는 그 명령에 당테스는 몸서리쳤다."], ["He heard hasty steps, the creaking of a door, people going and coming, and some minutes afterwards a turnkey entered, saying:", "그는 황급한 발소리와, 문이 삐걱대는 소리와, 사람들이 오가는 소리를 들었고, 몇 분 뒤 간수 하나가 들어오며 말하는 소리를 들었다."]], [["“Here is the brazier, lighted.”", "\"불 피운 화로 가져왔습니다.\""]], [["There was a moment’s silence, and then was heard the crackling of burning flesh, of which the peculiar and nauseous smell penetrated even behind the wall where Dantès was listening in horror.", "잠시 침묵이 흘렀고, 이어 살이 타들어 가는 자글거리는 소리가 들렸다. 그 독특하고 역겨운 냄새가, 당테스가 공포에 질려 귀 기울이고 있던 벽 뒤까지 스며들었다."], ["The perspiration poured forth upon the young man’s brow, and he felt as if he should faint.", "젊은이의 이마에서 땀이 줄줄 흘렀고, 그는 금방이라도 기절할 것만 같았다."]], [["“You see, sir, he is really dead,”", "\"보십시오, 선생. 이 사람은 정말로 죽었습니다.\""], ["said the doctor; “this burn in the heel is decisive.", "의사가 말했다. \"발뒤꿈치의 이 화상이 결정적인 증거지요."], ["The poor fool is cured of his folly, and delivered from his captivity.”", "이 가엾은 바보는 제 광기에서 벗어났고, 옥살이에서도 풀려난 겁니다.\""]], [["“Wasn’t his name Faria?” inquired one of the officers who accompanied the governor.", "\"이름이 파리아 아니었소?\" 소장을 수행한 관리들 중 하나가 물었다."]], [["0259m\n“Yes, sir; and, as he said, it was an ancient name.", "\"그렇소, 선생. 본인 말로는 유서 깊은 이름이라더군."], ["He was, too, very learned, and rational enough on all points which did not relate to his treasure; but on that, indeed, he was intractable.”", "게다가 무척 박식했고, 제 보물에 관한 것만 아니면 매사에 꽤 분별이 있었지요. 하지만 그 보물 얘기에 들어가면, 정말이지 손쓸 도리가 없었소.\""]], [["“It is the sort of malady which we call monomania,”", "\"우리가 편집광이라 부르는 부류의 병입니다.\""], ["said the doctor.", "의사가 말했다."]], [["“You had never anything to complain of?”", "\"불평할 만한 일은 한 번도 없었나?\""], ["said the governor to the jailer who had charge of the abbé.", "소장이 신부를 맡았던 간수에게 물었다."]], [["“Never, sir,”", "\"한 번도 없었습니다, 소장님.\""], ["replied the jailer, “never; on the contrary, he sometimes amused me very much by telling me stories.", "간수가 대답했다. \"한 번도요. 오히려 이따금 이야기를 들려줘서 저를 아주 즐겁게 해 줬습죠."], ["One day, too, when my wife was ill, he gave me a prescription which cured her.”", "한번은 제 아내가 앓아누웠을 때, 처방을 일러 줘서 아내를 낫게 한 적도 있고요.\""]], [["“Ah, ah!”", "\"이런, 이런!\""], ["said the doctor, “I did not know that I had a rival; but I hope, governor, that you will show him all proper respect in consequence.”", "의사가 말했다. \"내게 경쟁자가 있는 줄은 몰랐는걸. 하지만 소장, 그러니 그자에게 마땅한 예우를 모두 갖춰 주시기 바라오.\""]], [["“Yes, yes, make your mind easy, he shall be decently interred in the newest sack we can find.", "\"그래요, 그래, 마음 놓으시오. 우리가 구할 수 있는 가장 새것인 자루에 점잖게 묻어 주겠소."], ["Will that satisfy you?”", "그러면 만족하겠소?\""]], [["“Must this last formality take place in your presence, sir?”", "\"이 마지막 절차는 소장님이 입회하신 자리에서 치러야 합니까?\""], ["inquired a turnkey.", "간수 하나가 물었다."]], [["“Certainly.", "\"물론이지."], ["But make haste—I cannot stay here all day.”", "하지만 서두르게 — 나는 온종일 여기 있을 수 없으니.\""], ["Other footsteps, going and coming, were now heard, and a moment afterwards the noise of rustling canvas reached Dantès’ ears, the bed creaked, and the heavy footfall of a man who lifts a weight sounded on the floor; then the bed again creaked under the weight deposited upon it.", "사람들이 오가는 또 다른 발소리가 들렸고, 잠시 후 천이 바스락거리는 소리가 당테스의 귀에 닿았다. 침대가 삐걱대더니, 무게를 들어 올리는 사내의 무거운 발소리가 바닥에 울렸다. 이어 다시 침대가, 그 위에 내려놓인 무게에 눌려 삐걱거렸다."]], [["“This evening,” said the governor.", "\"오늘 저녁이오.\" 소장이 말했다."]], [["“Will there be any mass?”", "\"미사는 있습니까?\""], ["asked one of the attendants.", "수행원 하나가 물었다."]], [["“That is impossible,”", "\"그건 불가능하네.\""], ["replied the governor.", "소장이 대답했다."], ["“The chaplain of the château came to me yesterday to beg for leave of absence, in order to take a trip to Hyères for a week.", "\"성 안의 사제가 어제 내게 와서, 이예르로 일주일간 다녀오겠다며 휴가를 청했지."], ["I told him I would attend to the prisoners in his absence.", "자리를 비운 동안 죄수들은 내가 돌보겠다고 했어."], ["If the poor abbé had not been in such a hurry, he might have had his requiem.”", "가엾은 신부가 그렇게 서두르지만 않았더라면, 진혼 미사쯤은 받을 수 있었을 텐데.\""]], [["“Pooh, pooh;”", "\"흥, 흥.\""], ["said the doctor, with the impiety usual in persons of his profession; “he is a churchman.", "의사가 그 직업의 사람들에게 흔한 불경한 투로 말했다. \"성직자 아닙니까."], ["God will respect his profession, and not give the devil the wicked delight of sending him a priest.”", "하느님께서 그 직분을 존중하셔서, 악마에게 사제를 보내 주는 고약한 즐거움 따위는 안기지 않으실 겁니다.\""], ["A shout of laughter followed this brutal jest.", "이 거친 농담 뒤로 왁자한 웃음이 터졌다."], ["Meanwhile the operation of putting the body in the sack was going on.", "그러는 동안 시신을 자루에 넣는 작업이 진행되고 있었다."]], [["“This evening,” said the governor, when the task was ended.", "\"오늘 저녁이오.\" 작업이 끝나자 소장이 말했다."]], [["“At what hour?”", "\"몇 시에요?\""], ["inquired a turnkey.", "간수 하나가 물었다."]], [["“Why, about ten or eleven o’clock.”", "\"음, 열 시나 열한 시쯤.\""]], [["“Shall we watch by the corpse?”", "\"시신을 지킬까요?\""]], [["“Of what use would it be?", "\"그래 봐야 무슨 소용인가?"], ["Shut the dungeon as if he were alive—that is all.”", "살아 있을 때처럼 감방을 잠가 두게 — 그게 다일세.\""]], [["Then the steps retreated, and the voices died away in the distance; the noise of the door, with its creaking hinges and bolts ceased, and a silence more sombre than that of solitude ensued,—the silence of death, which was all-pervasive, and struck its icy chill to the very soul of Dantès.", "이윽고 발소리가 멀어졌고, 목소리들이 저 멀리로 잦아들었다. 삐걱대는 돌쩌귀와 빗장이 달린 문의 소리가 그쳤고, 고독의 정적보다 더 음울한 침묵이 뒤따랐다 — 죽음의 침묵이었다. 그것은 사방으로 퍼져 나가, 당테스의 영혼 깊은 곳까지 얼음장 같은 한기를 박아 넣었다."]], [["Then he raised the flag-stone cautiously with his head, and looked carefully around the chamber.", "그러자 그는 머리로 조심스레 포석을 들어 올리고, 방 안을 찬찬히 둘러보았다."], ["It was empty, and Dantès emerged from the tunnel.", "방은 비어 있었고, 당테스는 굴에서 빠져나왔다."]], [["# Chapter 20. The Cemetery of the Château d’If", "# Chapter 20. The Cemetery of the Château d’If"]], [["On the bed, at full length, and faintly illuminated by the pale light that came from the window, lay a sack of canvas, and under its rude folds was stretched a long and stiffened form; it was Faria’s last winding-sheet,—a winding-sheet which, as the turnkey said, cost so little.", "침대 위에 길게, 창에서 들어오는 희미한 빛에 어렴풋이 드러난 채, 캔버스 자루 하나가 놓여 있었다. 거친 주름 아래로 길고 뻣뻣하게 굳은 형체가 뻗어 있었다. 그것은 파리아의 마지막 수의였다."], ["Everything was in readiness. A barrier had been placed between Dantès and his old friend.", "간수가 말했듯이 비용이 얼마 들지 않은 수의였다. 모든 것이 준비되어 있었다."], ["No longer could Edmond look into those wide-open eyes which had seemed to be penetrating the mysteries of death; no longer could he clasp the hand which had done so much to make his existence blessed.", "당테스와 그의 오랜 벗 사이에 장벽이 놓인 것이다. 에드몽은 이제 죽음의 신비를 꿰뚫어 보는 듯하던 그 크게 뜬 두 눈을 다시는 들여다볼 수 없었다. 그의 삶을 축복으로 만들어 준 그 손을 다시는 잡을 수 없었다."], ["Faria, the beneficent and cheerful companion, with whom he was accustomed to live so intimately, no longer breathed.", "그토록 가깝게 지내던 자비롭고 쾌활한 동료 파리아는 더 이상 숨 쉬지 않았다."], ["He seated himself on the edge of that terrible bed, and fell into melancholy and gloomy reverie.", "그는 그 끔찍한 침대 가장자리에 걸터앉아 우울하고 음울한 상념에 잠겼다."]], [["Alone!", "홀로!"], ["he was alone again!", "그는 다시 홀로였다!"], ["again condemned to silence—again face to face with nothingness!", "다시 침묵에 갇히고, 다시 허무와 마주 선 것이다! 홀로라니!"], ["Alone!—never again to see the face, never again to hear the voice of the only human being who united him to earth!", "그를 이 땅에 붙들어 매 주던 유일한 사람의 얼굴을 다시는 볼 수 없고, 그 목소리를 다시는 들을 수 없다니!"], ["Was not Faria’s fate the better, after all—to solve the problem of life at its source, even at the risk of horrible suffering?", "결국 파리아의 운명이 더 나은 것이 아니었을까. 끔찍한 고통의 위험을 무릅쓰고서라도, 그 근원에서 삶의 문제를 풀어 버린 것이."]], [["The idea of suicide, which his friend had driven away and kept away by his cheerful presence, now hovered like a phantom over the abbé’s dead body.", "벗의 쾌활한 존재가 멀리 쫓아 막아 주던 자살이라는 생각이, 이제 신부의 주검 위로 유령처럼 떠돌았다."]], [["“If I could die,”", "\"내가 죽을 수만 있다면,\""], ["he said, “I should go where he goes, and should assuredly find him again.", "그가 말했다. \"그가 가는 곳으로 가서, 틀림없이 그를 다시 만날 텐데."], ["But how to die? It is very easy,”", "그런데 어떻게 죽는단 말인가?"], ["he went on with a smile; “I will remain here, rush on the first person that opens the door, strangle him, and then they will guillotine me.”", "아주 쉬운 일이지.\" 그가 미소를 띠며 말을 이었다. \"여기 그대로 있다가, 문을 여는 첫 번째 사람에게 달려들어 목을 졸라 버리면, 나를 단두대에 세우겠지.\""]], [["But excessive grief is like a storm at sea, where the frail bark is tossed from the depths to the top of the wave.", "그러나 지나친 슬픔은 바다의 폭풍과도 같아서, 연약한 조각배가 물결의 골에서 마루로 내던져지듯 한다."], ["Dantès recoiled from the idea of so infamous a death, and passed suddenly from despair to an ardent desire for life and liberty.", "당테스는 그토록 치욕스러운 죽음이라는 생각에서 움찔 물러섰고, 절망에서 삶과 자유를 향한 뜨거운 갈망으로 돌연 옮겨 갔다."]], [["“Die? oh, no,”", "\"죽는다고?"], ["he exclaimed—“not die now, after having lived and suffered so long and so much! Die?", "아, 아니야.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"이렇게 오래, 이토록 많이 살고 고통받은 끝에 지금 죽다니, 안 될 말이다! 죽는다고?"], ["yes, had I died years ago; but now to die would be, indeed, to give way to the sarcasm of destiny.", "그래, 몇 해 전에 죽었더라면 모르지. 하지만 지금 죽는다면, 그야말로 운명의 비웃음에 굴복하는 꼴이 아닌가."], ["No, I want to live; I shall struggle to the very last; I will yet win back the happiness of which I have been deprived.", "안 돼, 나는 살고 싶다. 마지막 순간까지 싸울 것이다. 빼앗긴 행복을 기어이 되찾고 말 것이다."], ["Before I die I must not forget that I have my executioners to punish, and perhaps, too, who knows, some friends to reward.", "죽기 전에 잊지 말아야 한다. 내게는 벌해야 할 형리들이 있고, 또 어쩌면, 누가 알겠는가, 보답해야 할 벗들도 있다는 것을."], ["Yet they will forget me here, and I shall die in my dungeon like Faria.”", "그런데도 여기 사람들은 나를 잊을 테고, 나는 파리아처럼 이 토굴에서 죽고 말겠지.\""]], [["As he said this, he became silent and gazed straight before him like one overwhelmed with a strange and amazing thought.", "이렇게 말하다가 그는 입을 다물고, 기이하고 놀라운 생각에 사로잡힌 사람처럼 앞을 똑바로 응시했다."], ["Suddenly he arose, lifted his hand to his brow as if his brain were giddy, paced twice or thrice round the dungeon, and then paused abruptly by the bed.", "갑자기 그는 벌떡 일어나, 머리가 어지러운 듯 이마에 손을 갖다 대고는, 토굴 안을 두세 바퀴 돌다가 침대 곁에서 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“Just God!”", "\"공정하신 하느님!\""], ["he muttered, “whence comes this thought?", "그가 중얼거렸다. \"이 생각은 어디서 온 것입니까?"], ["Is it from thee?", "당신에게서 온 것입니까?"], ["Since none but the dead pass freely from this dungeon, let me take the place of the dead!”", "이 토굴에서 자유로이 나가는 것은 오직 죽은 자뿐이니, 내가 그 죽은 자의 자리를 차지하게 해 주십시오!\""]], [["Without giving himself time to reconsider his decision, and, indeed, that he might not allow his thoughts to be distracted from his desperate resolution, he bent over the appalling shroud, opened it with the knife which Faria had made, drew the corpse from the sack, and bore it along the tunnel to his own chamber, laid it on his couch, tied around its head the rag he wore at night around his own, covered it with his counterpane, once again kissed the ice-cold brow, and tried vainly to close the resisting eyes, which glared horribly, turned the head towards the wall, so that the jailer might, when he brought the evening meal, believe that he was asleep, as was his frequent custom; entered the tunnel again, drew the bed against the wall, returned to the other cell, took from the hiding-place the needle and thread, flung off his rags, that they might feel only naked flesh beneath the coarse canvas, and getting inside the sack, placed himself in the posture in which the dead body had been laid, and sewed up the mouth of the sack from the inside.", "자신의 결정을 다시 곱씹을 틈도 주지 않고, 또한 그 필사적인 결심에서 마음이 흐트러지지 않도록, 그는 그 끔찍한 수의 위로 몸을 숙여 파리아가 만든 칼로 자루를 갈랐다. 그러고는 주검을 자루에서 끌어내 굴을 따라 자기 방으로 옮겨와 자신의 침상에 눕혔다. 자기가 밤마다 머리에 두르던 헝겊을 주검의 머리에 둘러 주고, 자기 이불로 덮어 주었다. 다시 한번 얼음장처럼 차가운 이마에 입을 맞추고는, 끔찍하게 부릅뜬 채 좀처럼 감기지 않는 두 눈을 감기려 했으나 헛수고였다. 그래서 머리를 벽 쪽으로 돌려놓았으니, 간수가 저녁 식사를 가져왔을 때 그가 평소처럼 잠든 줄로 믿게 하기 위해서였다. 그는 다시 굴로 들어가 침대를 벽에 붙여 놓고, 다른 감방으로 돌아와 은닉처에서 바늘과 실을 꺼냈다. 거친 캔버스 아래로 벌거벗은 살결만 만져지도록 누더기를 벗어 던지고, 자루 안으로 들어가 주검이 놓여 있던 그대로 자세를 잡은 뒤, 안쪽에서 자루 입구를 꿰맸다."]], [["He would have been discovered by the beating of his heart, if by any mischance the jailers had entered at that moment.", "만에 하나 간수가 그 순간 들어오기라도 했다면, 그의 심장 박동 소리에 들통이 났을 것이다."], ["Dantès might have waited until the evening visit was over, but he was afraid that the governor would change his mind, and order the dead body to be removed earlier.", "당테스는 저녁 순찰이 끝날 때까지 기다릴 수도 있었다. 그러나 그는 소장이 마음을 바꿔 주검을 더 일찍 치우라고 명할까 봐 두려웠다."], ["In that case his last hope would have been destroyed.", "그렇게 되면 그의 마지막 희망은 부서지고 말 것이었다."]], [["Now his plans were fully made, and this is what he intended to do.", "이제 계획은 빈틈없이 짜였고, 그가 하려는 일은 이러했다."], ["If while he was being carried out the grave-diggers should discover that they were bearing a live instead of a dead body, Dantès did not intend to give them time to recognize him, but with a sudden cut of the knife, he meant to open the sack from top to bottom, and, profiting by their alarm, escape; if they tried to catch him, he would use his knife to better purpose.", "자기를 운반해 나가는 동안 인부들이 죽은 시신이 아니라 산 사람을 나르고 있음을 알아챈다면, 당테스는 그들에게 자신을 알아볼 틈을 주지 않을 작정이었다. 칼로 자루를 위에서 아래까지 단숨에 가르고, 그들이 놀란 틈을 타 달아날 셈이었다. 그들이 붙잡으려 한다면, 칼을 더 요긴하게 쓸 것이었다."]], [["0263m\nIf they took him to the cemetery and laid him in a grave, he would allow himself to be covered with earth, and then, as it was night, the grave-diggers could scarcely have turned their backs before he would have worked his way through the yielding soil and escaped.", "자기를 묘지로 데려가 무덤에 눕힌다면, 그는 흙에 덮이도록 가만히 있을 작정이었다. 그러고는, 때가 밤이니, 인부들이 미처 등을 돌리기도 전에 그는 무른 흙을 헤치고 빠져나가 달아날 수 있을 것이었다."], ["He hoped that the weight of earth would not be so great that he could not overcome it.", "그는 흙의 무게가 도저히 헤쳐 나올 수 없을 만큼 무겁지는 않기를 바랐다."], ["If he was detected in this and the earth proved too heavy, he would be stifled, and then—so much the better, all would be over.", "만약 이 일이 들통나고 흙이 너무 무거워 빠져나올 수 없다면, 그는 질식하고 말 테고, 그러면 그것대로 잘된 일, 모든 것이 끝나는 셈이었다."]], [["Dantès had not eaten since the preceding evening, but he had not thought of hunger, nor did he think of it now.", "당테스는 전날 저녁 이후로 아무것도 먹지 못했으나, 배고픔은 생각조차 하지 않았고 지금도 마찬가지였다."], ["His situation was too precarious to allow him even time to reflect on any thought but one.", "처지가 너무도 위태로워, 오직 한 가지 생각 말고는 다른 어떤 것을 헤아릴 겨를조차 없었다."]], [["The first risk that Dantès ran was, that the jailer, when he brought him his supper at seven o’clock, might perceive the change that had been made; fortunately, twenty times at least, from misanthropy or fatigue, Dantès had received his jailer in bed, and then the man placed his bread and soup on the table, and went away without saying a word.", "당테스가 무릅쓴 첫 번째 위험은, 일곱 시에 저녁을 가져오는 간수가 바뀐 것을 알아챌지도 모른다는 것이었다. 다행히도 당테스는 적어도 스무 번은, 사람이 싫어서든 피곤해서든, 침대에 누운 채로 간수를 맞이한 적이 있었다. 그럴 때면 간수는 빵과 수프를 탁자에 놓고 한마디 말도 없이 나가곤 했다."], ["This time the jailer might not be as silent as usual, but speak to Dantès, and seeing that he received no reply, go to the bed, and thus discover all.", "그러나 이번에는 간수가 평소처럼 잠자코 있지 않고 당테스에게 말을 걸 수도 있었다. 그리고 아무 대답이 없는 것을 보고 침대로 다가가, 그렇게 모든 것을 알아챌 수도 있었다."]], [["When seven o’clock came, Dantès’ agony really began.", "일곱 시가 되자, 당테스의 고뇌가 정말로 시작되었다."], ["His hand placed upon his heart was unable to redress its throbbings, while, with the other he wiped the perspiration from his temples.", "심장에 얹은 한 손으로도 그 두근거림을 가라앉힐 수 없었고, 다른 손으로는 관자놀이에 맺힌 땀을 닦아 냈다."], ["From time to time chills ran through his whole body, and clutched his heart in a grasp of ice.", "이따금 온몸에 오한이 훑고 지나가며 얼음 같은 손아귀로 심장을 옥죄었다."], ["Then he thought he was going to die.", "그럴 때면 그는 자기가 죽는 줄 알았다."], ["Yet the hours passed on without any unusual disturbance, and Dantès knew that he had escaped the first peril.", "그러나 별다른 소란 없이 시간이 흘러갔고, 당테스는 첫 번째 위기를 넘겼음을 깨달았다."], ["It was a good augury.", "좋은 징조였다."]], [["At length, about the hour the governor had appointed, footsteps were heard on the stairs.", "마침내 소장이 정한 시각쯤 되어, 계단에서 발소리가 들렸다."], ["Edmond felt that the moment had arrived, summoned up all his courage, held his breath, and would have been happy if at the same time he could have repressed the throbbing of his veins.", "에드몽은 때가 왔음을 느끼고 온 용기를 끌어모아 숨을 죽였다. 그와 동시에 핏줄의 두근거림까지 억누를 수 있었다면 더없이 좋았을 것이다."], ["The footsteps—they were double—paused at the door—and Dantès guessed that the two grave-diggers had come to seek him—this idea was soon converted into certainty, when he heard the noise they made in putting down the hand-bier.", "두 사람의 발소리가 문 앞에서 멈췄다. 당테스는 인부 둘이 자기를 데리러 왔음을 짐작했다. 이내 그들이 들것을 내려놓는 소리가 들리자, 그 짐작은 확신으로 바뀌었다."]], [["The door opened, and a dim light reached Dantès’ eyes through the coarse sack that covered him; he saw two shadows approach his bed, a third remaining at the door with a torch in its hand.", "문이 열렸고, 그를 덮은 거친 자루 너머로 희미한 빛이 당테스의 눈에 닿았다. 두 그림자가 침대로 다가오고, 세 번째 그림자는 횃불을 손에 든 채 문가에 남는 것이 보였다."], ["The two men, approaching the ends of the bed, took the sack by its extremities.", "두 사내가 침대 양 끝으로 다가와 자루의 끝을 붙잡았다."]], [["“He’s heavy, though, for an old and thin man,”", "\"늙고 비쩍 마른 양반치고는 꽤 무겁네.\""], ["said one, as he raised the head.", "머리 쪽을 들어 올리던 사내가 말했다."]], [["“They say every year adds half a pound to the weight of the bones,” said another, lifting the feet.", "\"해마다 뼈 무게가 반 파운드씩 늘어난다고들 하지.\" 발치를 들어 올리던 다른 사내가 말했다."]], [["“Have you tied the knot?”", "\"매듭은 묶었나?\""], ["inquired the first speaker.", "먼저 말한 사내가 물었다."]], [["“What would be the use of carrying so much more weight?”", "\"이렇게 무거운 걸 더 무겁게 해서 뭐 하게?\""], ["was the reply, “I can do that when we get there.”", "대답이 돌아왔다. \"거기 도착해서 묶으면 되지.\""]], [["“Yes, you’re right,” replied the companion.", "\"그래, 자네 말이 맞아.\" 동료가 대답했다."]], [["“What’s the knot for?”", "'매듭이라니, 무슨 매듭이지?'"], ["thought Dantès.", "당테스는 생각했다."]], [["They deposited the supposed corpse on the bier.", "그들은 시신으로 짐작되는 것을 들것 위에 내려놓았다."], ["Edmond stiffened himself in order to play the part of a dead man, and then the party, lighted by the man with the torch, who went first, ascended the stairs.", "에드몽은 죽은 사람 노릇을 하려고 몸을 뻣뻣하게 굳혔다. 이윽고 일행은 앞장선 횃불 든 사내의 불빛을 따라 계단을 올랐다."], ["Suddenly he felt the fresh and sharp night air, and Dantès knew that the mistral was blowing.", "갑자기 그는 차고 매서운 밤공기를 느꼈고, 미스트랄이 불고 있음을 알아차렸다."], ["It was a sensation in which pleasure and pain were strangely mingled.", "쾌감과 고통이 묘하게 뒤섞인 감각이었다."]], [["The bearers went on for twenty paces, then stopped, putting the bier down on the ground.", "운반하는 자들은 스무 걸음쯤 가다가 멈춰 서서 들것을 땅에 내려놓았다."], ["One of them went away, and Dantès heard his shoes striking on the pavement.", "그중 하나가 자리를 떴고, 당테스는 그의 신발이 포석을 딛는 소리를 들었다."]], [["“Where am I?” he asked himself.", "'내가 지금 어디 있는 거지?' 그는 자문했다."]], [["“Really, he is by no means a light load!”", "\"이거 참, 결코 가벼운 짐이 아니라니까!\""], ["said the other bearer, sitting on the edge of the hand-barrow.", "들것 가장자리에 걸터앉으며 다른 사내가 말했다."]], [["Dantès’ first impulse was to escape, but fortunately he did not attempt it.", "당테스는 당장 달아나고 싶은 충동이 일었으나, 다행히 그러지 않았다."]], [["“Give us a light,”", "\"불 좀 비춰 줘.\""], ["said the other bearer, “or I shall never find what I am looking for.”", "다른 사내가 말했다. \"안 그러면 찾는 걸 도무지 못 찾겠어.\""]], [["The man with the torch complied, although not asked in the most polite terms.", "횃불을 든 사내는, 그리 정중한 부탁은 아니었으나 시키는 대로 했다."]], [["“What can he be looking for?” thought Edmond.", "'대체 뭘 찾는 거지?' 에드몽은 생각했다."], ["“The spade, perhaps.”", "'아마 삽이겠지.'"]], [["An exclamation of satisfaction indicated that the grave-digger had found the object of his search.", "만족스러운 외침으로 보아, 인부가 찾던 물건을 찾아낸 모양이었다."], ["“Here it is at last,”", "\"드디어 찾았네.\""], ["he said, “not without some trouble, though.”", "그가 말했다. \"꽤나 애를 먹었지만.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["was the answer, “but it has lost nothing by waiting.”", "대답이 돌아왔다. \"그래도 기다린 보람은 있는 물건이지.\""]], [["As he said this, the man came towards Edmond, who heard a heavy metallic substance laid down beside him, and at the same moment a cord was fastened round his feet with sudden and painful violence.", "이렇게 말하며 사내가 에드몽 쪽으로 다가왔고, 에드몽은 묵직한 쇠붙이가 자기 곁에 놓이는 소리를 들었다. 동시에 어떤 줄이 그의 발에 느닷없이, 아프도록 거칠게 매여졌다."]], [["“Well, have you tied the knot?”", "\"자, 매듭은 묶었나?\""], ["inquired the grave-digger, who was looking on.", "곁에서 지켜보던 인부가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, and pretty tight too, I can tell you,”", "\"그래, 그것도 아주 단단히 묶었지, 장담하네.\""], ["was the answer.", "대답이 돌아왔다."]], [["“Move on, then.”", "\"그럼 가자고.\""], ["And the bier was lifted once more, and they proceeded.", "그러자 들것이 다시 들렸고, 그들은 걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["They advanced fifty paces farther, and then stopped to open a door, then went forward again.", "그들은 쉰 걸음쯤 더 나아가다가 문을 열려고 멈춰 섰고, 그런 뒤 다시 앞으로 나아갔다."], ["The noise of the waves dashing against the rocks on which the château is built, reached Dantès’ ear distinctly as they went forward.", "성이 세워진 바위에 물결이 부딪치는 소리가 걸음을 옮길수록 당테스의 귀에 또렷이 들려왔다."]], [["“Bad weather!”", "\"날씨 한번 고약하군!\""], ["observed one of the bearers; “not a pleasant night for a dip in the sea.”", "운반하는 자 하나가 말했다. \"바다에 한 번 담그기엔 영 안 좋은 밤이야.\""]], [["“Why, yes, the abbé runs a chance of being wet,”", "\"그러게 말이야, 신부 양반이 흠뻑 젖게 생겼군.\""], ["said the other; and then there was a burst of brutal laughter.", "다른 자가 말했다. 그러더니 한바탕 거친 웃음이 터졌다."]], [["Dantès did not comprehend the jest, but his hair stood erect on his head.", "당테스는 그 농담을 알아듣지 못했으나, 머리카락이 곤두섰다."]], [["“Well, here we are at last,”", "\"자, 드디어 다 왔다.\""], ["said one of them.", "그중 하나가 말했다."]], [["“A little farther—a little farther,” said the other.", "\"조금만 더, 조금만 더.\" 다른 자가 말했다."], ["“You know very well that the last was stopped on his way, dashed on the rocks, and the governor told us next day that we were careless fellows.”", "\"지난번 그자는 가다가 걸려서 바위에 부딪혔던 거 잘 알잖아. 다음 날 소장이 우리더러 칠칠치 못한 놈들이라고 했지.\""]], [["They ascended five or six more steps, and then Dantès felt that they took him, one by the head and the other by the heels, and swung him to and fro.", "그들은 대여섯 계단을 더 올랐다. 그러고 나서 당테스는 그들이 한 사람은 머리를, 다른 사람은 발꿈치를 잡고서 자기를 앞뒤로 흔들어 대는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“One!”", "\"하나!\""], ["said the grave-diggers, “two! three!”", "인부들이 말했다. \"둘! 셋!\""]], [["And at the same instant Dantès felt himself flung into the air like a wounded bird, falling, falling, with a rapidity that made his blood curdle.", "그와 동시에 당테스는 상처 입은 새처럼 허공으로 내던져지는 것을 느꼈다. 피가 얼어붙을 만큼 빠른 속도로, 떨어지고 또 떨어졌다."], ["Although drawn downwards by the heavy weight which hastened his rapid descent, it seemed to him as if the fall lasted for a century.", "그 빠른 추락을 재촉하는 묵직한 무게에 아래로 끌려가면서도, 그에게는 그 추락이 한 세기나 이어지는 듯했다."], ["At last, with a horrible splash, he darted like an arrow into the ice-cold water, and as he did so he uttered a shrill cry, stifled in a moment by his immersion beneath the waves.", "마침내 그는 끔찍한 물보라와 함께 화살처럼 얼음장 같은 물속으로 곤두박질쳤고, 그 순간 날카로운 비명을 질렀으나, 물결 아래로 잠기면서 곧 잦아들고 말았다."]], [["Dantès had been flung into the sea, and was dragged into its depths by a thirty-six-pound shot tied to his feet.", "당테스는 바다로 내던져진 것이었고, 발에 묶인 36파운드 포탄에 끌려 깊은 물속으로 가라앉고 있었다."]], [["The sea is the cemetery of the Château d’If.", "바다, 그것이 바로 이프성의 묘지였다."]], [["# Chapter 21. The Island of Tiboulen", "# Chapter 21. The Island of Tiboulen"]], [["0267m\nDantès, although stunned and almost suffocated, had sufficient presence of mind to hold his breath, and as his right hand (prepared as he was for every chance) held his knife open, he rapidly ripped up the sack, extricated his arm, and then his body; but in spite of all his efforts to free himself from the shot, he felt it dragging him down still lower.", "당테스는 정신이 멍하고 거의 숨이 막힐 지경이었지만, 그래도 숨을 참을 만큼의 침착함은 잃지 않았다. 어떤 상황에도 대비하고 있던 그의 오른손에는 펼쳐진 칼이 쥐어져 있었다. 그는 재빨리 자루를 찢고 팔을 빼낸 다음 몸을 빼냈다."], ["He then bent his body, and by a desperate effort severed the cord that bound his legs, at the moment when it seemed as if he were actually strangled.", "그러나 포탄에서 벗어나려는 온갖 몸부림에도 불구하고, 포탄이 자신을 점점 더 깊은 곳으로 끌어내리는 것이 느껴졌다. 그는 몸을 굽혔고, 정말로 목이 졸리는 듯한 바로 그 순간, 필사적인 힘을 쏟아 다리를 묶은 밧줄을 끊어 냈다."], ["With a mighty leap he rose to the surface of the sea, while the shot dragged down to the depths the sack that had so nearly become his shroud.", "그러고는 힘껏 솟구쳐 바다 표면으로 떠올랐고, 그동안 포탄은 하마터면 그의 수의가 될 뻔했던 자루를 깊은 바닷속으로 끌고 내려갔다."]], [["Dantès waited only to get breath, and then dived, in order to avoid being seen.", "당테스는 숨을 돌리기만을 기다렸다가, 들키지 않으려고 다시 물속으로 잠수했다."], ["When he arose a second time, he was fifty paces from where he had first sunk.", "두 번째로 떠올랐을 때, 그는 처음 가라앉았던 곳에서 쉰 걸음쯤 떨어진 지점에 있었다."], ["He saw overhead a black and tempestuous sky, across which the wind was driving clouds that occasionally suffered a twinkling star to appear; before him was the vast expanse of waters, sombre and terrible, whose waves foamed and roared as if before the approach of a storm.", "머리 위로는 검고 사나운 하늘이 펼쳐져 있었고, 바람이 구름을 몰아가는 사이로 이따금 반짝이는 별 하나가 모습을 드러냈다. 앞쪽으로는 음울하고 무서운 망망한 바다가 펼쳐졌고, 폭풍이 다가오기라도 하는 듯 파도가 거품을 일으키며 포효했다."], ["Behind him, blacker than the sea, blacker than the sky, rose phantom-like the vast stone structure, whose projecting crags seemed like arms extended to seize their prey, and on the highest rock was a torch lighting two figures.", "등 뒤로는 바다보다 검고 하늘보다 검은, 유령처럼 우뚝 솟은 거대한 돌 건축물이 있었다. 그 돌출한 바위들은 마치 먹잇감을 움켜쥐려고 뻗은 팔처럼 보였고, 가장 높은 바위 위에는 횃불 하나가 두 사람의 형체를 비추고 있었다."]], [["He fancied that these two forms were looking at the sea; doubtless these strange grave-diggers had heard his cry.", "그는 그 두 형체가 바다를 바라보고 있다고 생각했다. 분명 그 기이한 무덤 파는 자들이 자신의 비명을 들었을 것이다."], ["Dantès dived again, and remained a long time beneath the water.", "당테스는 다시 잠수해 오랫동안 물속에 머물렀다."], ["This was an easy feat to him, for he usually attracted a crowd of spectators in the bay before the lighthouse at Marseilles when he swam there, and was unanimously declared to be the best swimmer in the port.", "그에게는 쉬운 일이었다. 마르세유 등대 앞 만에서 헤엄을 칠 때면 늘 구경꾼들이 떼로 몰려들었고, 그는 항구에서 으뜸가는 수영 선수로 모두에게 인정받았기 때문이다."], ["When he came up again the light had disappeared.", "다시 떠올랐을 때 횃불은 사라지고 없었다."]], [["He must now get his bearings.", "이제 방향을 가늠해야 했다."], ["Ratonneau and Pomègue are the nearest islands of all those that surround the Château d’If, but Ratonneau and Pomègue are inhabited, as is also the islet of Daume.", "이프성을 둘러싼 모든 섬 가운데 라토노섬과 포메그섬이 가장 가까웠지만, 라토노섬과 포메그섬에는 사람이 살았고, 돔섬도 마찬가지였다."], ["Tiboulen and Lemaire were therefore the safest for Dantès’ venture.", "그러니 당테스의 모험에 가장 안전한 곳은 티불랑섬과 르메르섬이었다."], ["The islands of Tiboulen and Lemaire are a league from the Château d’If; Dantès, nevertheless, determined to make for them.", "티불랑섬과 르메르섬은 이프성에서 1리외쯤 떨어져 있었다. 그래도 당테스는 그쪽으로 가기로 마음먹었다."], ["But how could he find his way in the darkness of the night?", "그러나 이 캄캄한 밤중에 어떻게 길을 찾는단 말인가?"]], [["At this moment he saw the light of Planier, gleaming in front of him like a star.", "그 순간 그는 앞쪽에서 별처럼 반짝이는 플라니에 등대의 불빛을 보았다."], ["By leaving this light on the right, he kept the Island of Tiboulen a little on the left; by turning to the left, therefore, he would find it.", "이 불빛을 오른쪽에 두면 티불랑섬은 약간 왼쪽에 놓이게 되니, 왼쪽으로 돌면 섬을 찾을 수 있을 터였다."], ["But, as we have said, it was at least a league from the Château d’If to this island. Often in prison Faria had said to him, when he saw him idle and inactive:", "그러나 앞서 말했듯이, 이프성에서 그 섬까지는 적어도 1리외였다. 감옥에 있을 때 파리아는 그가 빈둥거리며 무기력하게 있는 것을 볼 때면 종종 이렇게 말하곤 했다."]], [["“Dantès, you must not give way to this listlessness; you will be drowned if you seek to escape, and your strength has not been properly exercised and prepared for exertion.”", "\"당테스, 그렇게 늘어져 있으면 안 되네. 탈출을 시도하다간 물에 빠져 죽고 말 거야. 자네 체력이 제대로 단련되지도, 힘쓸 준비가 되지도 않았으니 말일세.\""]], [["These words rang in Dantès’ ears, even beneath the waves; he hastened to cleave his way through them to see if he had not lost his strength.", "이 말이 파도 밑에서까지 당테스의 귓전에 울렸다. 그는 자신의 힘을 잃지 않았는지 확인하려고 서둘러 물살을 갈랐다."], ["He found with pleasure that his captivity had taken away nothing of his power, and that he was still master of that element on whose bosom he had so often sported as a boy.", "그는 갇혀 지낸 세월이 자신의 힘을 조금도 앗아 가지 않았으며, 어린 시절 그토록 자주 그 품에서 뛰놀던 이 물이라는 존재를 여전히 자신이 다스리고 있음을 기쁘게 깨달았다."]], [["Fear, that relentless pursuer, clogged Dantès’ efforts. He listened for any sound that might be audible, and every time that he rose to the top of a wave he scanned the horizon, and strove to penetrate the darkness.", "가차 없이 따라붙는 추격자, 두려움이 당테스의 동작을 더디게 했다. 그는 들릴 만한 소리가 없는지 귀를 기울였고, 파도 마루에 올라설 때마다 수평선을 살피며 어둠을 꿰뚫어 보려 애썼다."], ["He fancied that every wave behind him was a pursuing boat, and he redoubled his exertions, increasing rapidly his distance from the château, but exhausting his strength.", "등 뒤의 파도 하나하나가 추격해 오는 배처럼 느껴졌고, 그는 더욱더 힘을 쏟아 성에서 빠르게 멀어졌지만, 그만큼 기력도 소진했다."], ["He swam on still, and already the terrible château had disappeared in the darkness.", "그래도 그는 계속 헤엄쳤고, 이미 그 무시무시한 성은 어둠 속으로 사라지고 없었다."], ["He could not see it, but he _felt_ its presence.", "보이지는 않았으나, 그는 그 존재를 느낄 수 있었다."]], [["An hour passed, during which Dantès, excited by the feeling of freedom, continued to cleave the waves.", "한 시간이 흘렀고, 그동안 당테스는 자유롭다는 감격에 들떠 계속 파도를 갈랐다."]], [["“Let us see,”", "\"어디 보자.\""], ["said he, “I have swum above an hour, but as the wind is against me, that has retarded my speed; however, if I am not mistaken, I must be close to Tiboulen.", "그가 혼잣말을 했다. \"한 시간 넘게 헤엄쳤지만, 바람이 거슬러 부니 속도가 더뎌졌어. 그래도 내 짐작이 틀리지 않다면, 이제 티불랑섬에 가까이 왔을 거야."], ["But what if I were mistaken?”", "그런데 만약 내가 틀렸다면?\""]], [["A shudder passed over him. He sought to tread water, in order to rest himself; but the sea was too violent, and he felt that he could not make use of this means of recuperation.", "몸서리가 그를 훑고 지나갔다. 그는 쉬려고 선헤엄을 치려 했지만, 바다가 너무 거칠어 그런 식으로는 기력을 회복할 수 없음을 깨달았다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"좋아.\""], ["said he, “I will swim on until I am worn out, or the cramp seizes me, and then I shall sink;”", "그가 말했다. \"기진맥진하거나 쥐가 날 때까지 헤엄치자. 그러면 가라앉겠지.\""], ["and he struck out with the energy of despair.", "그러고는 절망의 힘을 끌어모아 헤엄쳐 나아갔다."]], [["Suddenly the sky seemed to him to become still darker and more dense, and heavy clouds seemed to sweep down towards him; at the same time he felt a sharp pain in his knee.", "문득 하늘이 더욱 어둡고 짙어지는 듯했고, 무거운 구름이 그를 향해 내리덮치는 듯했다. 동시에 무릎에 날카로운 통증이 느껴졌다."], ["He fancied for a moment that he had been shot, and listened for the report; but he heard nothing.", "그는 한순간 총에 맞은 줄 알고 총성을 기다렸으나, 아무 소리도 들리지 않았다."], ["Then he put out his hand, and encountered an obstacle and with another stroke knew that he had gained the shore.", "그러고는 손을 뻗자 무언가 걸리는 것이 있었고, 한 번 더 손을 젓고 나서야 자신이 뭍에 닿았음을 알았다."]], [["Before him rose a grotesque mass of rocks, that resembled nothing so much as a vast fire petrified at the moment of its most fervent combustion. It was the Island of Tiboulen.", "그의 앞에는 기괴한 바위 덩어리가 솟아 있었는데, 그 무엇보다도 가장 맹렬히 타오르던 순간에 그대로 굳어 버린 거대한 불길처럼 보였다. 티불랑섬이었다."], ["Dantès rose, advanced a few steps, and, with a fervent prayer of gratitude, stretched himself on the granite, which seemed to him softer than down.", "당테스는 일어나 몇 걸음 나아갔고, 간절한 감사의 기도를 올린 뒤 화강암 위에 몸을 뉘었다. 그에게는 그 화강암이 솜털보다 부드럽게 느껴졌다."], ["Then, in spite of the wind and rain, he fell into the deep, sweet sleep of utter exhaustion.", "그러고는 바람과 비에도 아랑곳없이, 완전히 탈진한 자만이 빠질 수 있는 깊고 달콤한 잠에 빠져들었다."], ["At the expiration of an hour Edmond was awakened by the roar of thunder.", "한 시간이 지났을 무렵, 에드몽은 천둥소리에 잠에서 깼다."], ["The tempest was let loose and beating the atmosphere with its mighty wings; from time to time a flash of lightning stretched across the heavens like a fiery serpent, lighting up the clouds that rolled on in vast chaotic waves.", "폭풍이 풀려나 그 거대한 날개로 대기를 후려치고 있었다. 이따금 번개가 불뱀처럼 하늘을 가로지르며, 거대하고 무질서한 파도처럼 굽이쳐 가는 구름을 밝혔다."]], [["Dantès had not been deceived—he had reached the first of the two islands, which was, in fact, Tiboulen.", "당테스는 속지 않았다. 그는 두 섬 가운데 첫 번째 섬에 닿았고, 그곳은 과연 티불랑섬이었다."], ["He knew that it was barren and without shelter; but when the sea became more calm, he resolved to plunge into its waves again, and swim to Lemaire, equally arid, but larger, and consequently better adapted for concealment.", "그는 그 섬이 메마르고 몸을 숨길 곳이 없다는 것을 알았다. 그래서 바다가 좀 더 잔잔해지면 다시 파도 속으로 뛰어들어, 마찬가지로 황량하지만 더 크고 따라서 몸을 숨기기에 더 알맞은 르메르섬으로 헤엄쳐 가기로 마음먹었다."]], [["An overhanging rock offered him a temporary shelter, and scarcely had he availed himself of it when the tempest burst forth in all its fury.", "불쑥 튀어나온 바위 하나가 그에게 임시 피난처가 되어 주었다. 그가 막 그 아래에 몸을 숨기자마자 폭풍이 온 위세를 떨치며 몰아쳤다."], ["Edmond felt the trembling of the rock beneath which he lay; the waves, dashing themselves against it, wetted him with their spray.", "에드몽은 자신이 누운 바위가 떨리는 것을 느꼈다. 파도가 바위에 부딪치며 물보라로 그를 적셨다."], ["He was safely sheltered, and yet he felt dizzy in the midst of the warring of the elements and the dazzling brightness of the lightning.", "안전하게 몸을 숨기고 있었지만, 그래도 그는 맹렬히 다투는 자연의 힘과 눈부신 번갯불 한가운데서 어지러움을 느꼈다."], ["It seemed to him that the island trembled to its base, and that it would, like a vessel at anchor, break moorings, and bear him off into the centre of the storm.", "섬이 그 밑바닥까지 흔들리는 것 같았고, 닻을 내린 배처럼 닻줄을 끊고 자신을 폭풍의 한복판으로 끌고 가 버릴 것만 같았다."]], [["He then recollected that he had not eaten or drunk for four-and-twenty hours.", "그제야 그는 스물네 시간 동안 아무것도 먹지도 마시지도 못했다는 사실을 떠올렸다."], ["He extended his hands, and drank greedily of the rainwater that had lodged in a hollow of the rock.", "그는 두 손을 뻗어, 바위 우묵한 곳에 고인 빗물을 게걸스럽게 들이켰다."]], [["As he rose, a flash of lightning, that seemed to rive the remotest heights of heaven, illumined the darkness.", "그가 몸을 일으키는데, 하늘 가장 먼 끝까지 갈라놓는 듯한 번개 한 줄기가 어둠을 밝혔다."], ["By its light, between the Island of Lemaire and Cape Croiselle, a quarter of a league distant, Dantès saw a fishing-boat driven rapidly like a spectre before the power of winds and waves.", "그 불빛으로, 르메르섬과 크루아젤곶 사이 4분의 1리외쯤 떨어진 곳에서, 당테스는 바람과 파도의 위세에 떠밀려 유령처럼 빠르게 내달리는 고깃배 한 척을 보았다."], ["A second after, he saw it again, approaching with frightful rapidity.", "잠시 후, 그는 그 배가 무서운 속도로 다가오는 것을 다시 보았다."], ["Dantès cried at the top of his voice to warn them of their danger, but they saw it themselves.", "당테스는 위험을 알리려고 목청껏 소리쳤으나, 그들도 이미 위험을 알고 있었다."], ["Another flash showed him four men clinging to the shattered mast and the rigging, while a fifth clung to the broken rudder.", "또 한 번 번쩍이는 빛에, 그는 네 사람이 부러진 돛대와 삭구에 매달려 있고, 다섯 번째 사람은 부서진 키에 매달려 있는 것을 보았다."], ["The men he beheld saw him undoubtedly, for their cries were carried to his ears by the wind.", "그가 본 사람들도 분명 그를 보았으니, 그들의 외침이 바람을 타고 그의 귀에 실려 왔기 때문이다."], ["Above the splintered mast a sail rent to tatters was waving; suddenly the ropes that still held it gave way, and it disappeared in the darkness of the night like a vast sea-bird.", "쪼개진 돛대 위에서 갈가리 찢긴 돛 한 폭이 펄럭이고 있었다. 갑자기 그 돛을 붙들고 있던 밧줄이 끊어졌고, 돛은 거대한 바닷새처럼 밤의 어둠 속으로 사라졌다."]], [["At the same moment a violent crash was heard, and cries of distress.", "그 순간 요란한 충돌 소리와 함께 비명이 들려왔다."], ["Dantès from his rocky perch saw the shattered vessel, and among the fragments the floating forms of the hapless sailors. Then all was dark again.", "당테스는 바위 위 자리에서 부서진 배와, 그 잔해 사이로 떠다니는 가엾은 선원들의 몸뚱이를 보았다. 그러고는 다시 온통 어두워졌다."]], [["Dantès ran down the rocks at the risk of being himself dashed to pieces; he listened, he groped about, but he heard and saw nothing—the cries had ceased, and the tempest continued to rage.", "당테스는 제 몸이 산산조각 날 위험을 무릅쓰고 바위를 타고 내려갔다. 귀를 기울이고 더듬어 보았으나, 아무것도 들리지도 보이지도 않았다. 비명은 그쳤고, 폭풍만 여전히 사납게 몰아쳤다."], ["By degrees the wind abated, vast gray clouds rolled towards the west, and the blue firmament appeared studded with bright stars.", "차츰 바람이 잦아들었고, 거대한 잿빛 구름이 서쪽으로 굴러갔으며, 푸른 하늘이 밝은 별들을 총총히 박은 채 모습을 드러냈다."], ["Soon a red streak became visible in the horizon, the waves whitened, a light played over them, and gilded their foaming crests with gold. It was day.", "이윽고 수평선에 붉은 띠가 어렸고, 파도가 하얗게 부서지더니, 빛이 그 위에서 일렁이며 거품 이는 마루를 황금빛으로 물들였다. 날이 밝은 것이다."]], [["Dantès stood mute and motionless before this majestic spectacle, as if he now beheld it for the first time; and indeed since his captivity in the Château d’If he had forgotten that such scenes were ever to be witnessed.", "당테스는 이 장엄한 광경 앞에 말없이 꼼짝 않고 서 있었다. 마치 처음 보는 광경인 듯했다. 실제로 이프성에 갇힌 뒤로 그는 이런 장면을 볼 수 있다는 사실조차 잊고 지냈다."], ["He turned towards the fortress, and looked at both sea and land.", "그는 요새 쪽으로 몸을 돌려 바다와 뭍을 둘러보았다."], ["The gloomy building rose from the bosom of the ocean with imposing majesty and seemed to dominate the scene.", "그 음울한 건물은 위풍당당한 위용으로 바다 한복판에 솟아 온 풍경을 지배하는 듯했다."], ["It was about five o’clock. The sea continued to get calmer.", "다섯 시쯤이었다. 바다는 점점 더 잔잔해지고 있었다."]], [["“In two or three hours,”", "'두세 시간 안에,'"], ["thought Dantès, “the turnkey will enter my chamber, find the body of my poor friend, recognize it, seek for me in vain, and give the alarm.", "당테스는 생각했다. '간수가 내 방에 들어와 가엾은 친구의 시신을 발견하고, 그게 누구인지 알아본 다음, 나를 찾으려다 헛수고하고는 경보를 울리겠지."], ["Then the tunnel will be discovered; the men who cast me into the sea and who must have heard the cry I uttered, will be questioned.", "그러면 굴이 발각될 테고, 나를 바다에 던진 자들, 내가 지른 비명을 분명 들었을 그자들이 추궁을 받겠지."], ["Then boats filled with armed soldiers will pursue the wretched fugitive.", "그런 다음 무장한 병사들을 가득 태운 배들이 가엾은 도망자를 뒤쫓을 테고."], ["The cannon will warn everyone to refuse shelter to a man wandering about naked and famished.", "대포가 울려, 벌거벗고 굶주린 채 떠도는 사내에게 누구도 거처를 내주지 말라고 모두에게 경고하겠지."], ["The police of Marseilles will be on the alert by land, whilst the governor pursues me by sea.", "마르세유 경찰은 뭍에서 경계를 펼 테고, 성주는 바다에서 나를 추격하겠지."], ["I am cold, I am hungry.", "나는 춥고, 배가 고프다."], ["I have lost even the knife that saved me.", "나를 살려 준 칼마저 잃어버렸어."], ["Oh, my God, I have suffered enough surely!", "오, 하느님, 저는 분명 충분히 고통받았습니다!"], ["Have pity on me, and do for me what I am unable to do for myself.”", "부디 저를 가엾이 여기시어, 제 힘으로 할 수 없는 일을 대신해 주소서.'"]], [["As Dantès (his eyes turned in the direction of the Château d’If) uttered this prayer, he saw off the farther point of the Island of Pomègue a small vessel with lateen sail skimming the sea like a gull in search of prey; and with his sailor’s eye he knew it to be a Genoese tartan.", "당테스가 이프성 쪽으로 눈을 돌린 채 이 기도를 올리고 있을 때, 그는 포메그섬의 먼 끄트머리 너머로 작은 배 한 척이 삼각돛을 달고, 먹이를 찾는 갈매기처럼 바다 위를 스치듯 나아가는 것을 보았다. 선원다운 눈으로 그는 그것이 제노바의 타르탄선임을 알아보았다."], ["She was coming out of Marseilles harbor, and was standing out to sea rapidly, her sharp prow cleaving through the waves.", "그 배는 마르세유 항구를 빠져나와, 날카로운 뱃머리로 파도를 가르며 빠르게 먼바다로 나아가고 있었다."]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오.\""], ["cried Edmond, “to think that in half an hour I could join her, did I not fear being questioned, detected, and conveyed back to Marseilles!", "에드몽이 외쳤다. \"추궁당하고, 정체가 드러나, 마르세유로 도로 끌려갈 게 두렵지만 않다면, 반 시간 안에 저 배에 닿을 수 있을 텐데!"], ["What can I do?", "어떻게 하지?"], ["What story can I invent?", "무슨 이야기를 지어내야 할까?"], ["under pretext of trading along the coast, these men, who are in reality smugglers, will prefer selling me to doing a good action.", "해안을 따라 장사하는 척하지만 실은 밀수꾼인 저자들은, 선행을 베푸느니 나를 팔아넘기는 편을 택하겠지. 기다려야 해."], ["I must wait. But I cannot—I am starving.", "하지만 그럴 수 없어. 굶어 죽을 지경이니까."], ["In a few hours my strength will be utterly exhausted; besides, perhaps I have not been missed at the fortress.", "몇 시간만 지나면 내 힘은 완전히 바닥날 거야. 게다가 어쩌면 요새에서는 아직 내가 사라진 걸 모를지도 몰라."], ["I can pass as one of the sailors wrecked last night.", "나는 어젯밤 난파한 선원 중 하나로 행세할 수 있어."], ["My story will be accepted, for there is no one left to contradict me.”", "내 이야기를 받아들일 거야. 나를 반박할 사람이 아무도 남지 않았으니까.\""]], [["As he spoke, Dantès looked toward the spot where the fishing-vessel had been wrecked, and started.", "이렇게 중얼거리며 당테스는 고깃배가 난파한 지점 쪽을 바라보았고, 흠칫 놀랐다."], ["The red cap of one of the sailors hung to a point of the rock and some timbers that had formed part of the vessel’s keel, floated at the foot of the crag.", "선원 하나가 쓰던 빨간 모자가 바위 끄트머리에 걸려 있었고, 배의 용골 일부였던 목재 몇 토막이 바위 아래에 떠다니고 있었다."], ["In an instant Dantès’ plan was formed.", "순식간에 당테스의 계획이 섰다."], ["He swam to the cap, placed it on his head, seized one of the timbers, and struck out so as to cut across the course the vessel was taking.", "그는 그 모자로 헤엄쳐 가 머리에 쓰고, 목재 하나를 붙잡은 다음, 배가 나아가는 항로를 가로지르도록 헤엄쳐 나갔다."]], [["“I am saved!”", "\"나는 살았다!\""], ["murmured he. And this conviction restored his strength.", "그가 중얼거렸다. 이 확신이 그의 힘을 되살렸다."]], [["He soon saw that the vessel, with the wind dead ahead, was tacking between the Château d’If and the tower of Planier.", "이윽고 그는 그 배가 정면으로 바람을 받으며 이프성과 플라니에 탑 사이에서 갈지자로 항해하고 있음을 알아보았다."], ["For an instant he feared lest, instead of keeping in shore, she should stand out to sea; but he soon saw that she would pass, like most vessels bound for Italy, between the islands of Jaros and Calaseraigne.", "한순간 그는 배가 해안에 붙어 있지 않고 먼바다로 나가 버리지 않을까 두려웠다. 그러나 곧 그 배가, 이탈리아로 향하는 대부분의 배들처럼, 자로스섬과 칼라사레뉴섬 사이를 지나가리라는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["However, the vessel and the swimmer insensibly neared one another, and in one of its tacks the tartan bore down within a quarter of a mile of him.", "그러는 사이 배와 헤엄치는 사람은 자신도 모르게 점점 가까워졌고, 한 번 방향을 트는 사이에 타르탄선은 그에게서 4분의 1마일 안쪽으로 내려왔다."], ["He rose on the waves, making signs of distress; but no one on board saw him, and the vessel stood on another tack.", "그는 파도 위로 솟구쳐 조난 신호를 보냈으나, 배 위의 누구도 그를 보지 못했고, 배는 다시 방향을 틀어 나아갔다."], ["Dantès would have shouted, but he knew that the wind would drown his voice.", "당테스는 소리치고 싶었으나, 바람이 그 목소리를 삼켜 버리리라는 것을 알고 있었다."]], [["It was then he rejoiced at his precaution in taking the timber, for without it he would have been unable, perhaps, to reach the vessel—certainly to return to shore, should he be unsuccessful in attracting attention.", "그제야 그는 목재를 챙겨 둔 자신의 신중함이 다행스러웠다. 그것이 없었다면 그는 어쩌면 배에 닿지도 못했을 테고, 사람들의 눈길을 끄는 데 실패할 경우 해안으로 돌아오는 것도 분명 불가능했을 것이다."]], [["Dantès, though almost sure as to what course the vessel would take, had yet watched it anxiously until it tacked and stood towards him.", "당테스는 배가 어느 항로를 택할지 거의 확신하면서도, 배가 방향을 틀어 자신에게로 다가올 때까지 마음 졸이며 지켜보았다."], ["Then he advanced; but before they could meet, the vessel again changed her course.", "그러고는 앞으로 나아갔으나, 미처 닿기도 전에 배가 또다시 항로를 바꾸었다."], ["By a violent effort he rose half out of the water, waving his cap, and uttering a loud shout peculiar to sailors.", "그는 안간힘을 쏟아 물 밖으로 반쯤 솟구쳐 모자를 흔들며, 선원 특유의 우렁찬 고함을 질렀다."], ["This time he was both seen and heard, and the tartan instantly steered towards him.", "이번에는 그의 모습이 보이고 소리도 들렸는지, 타르탄선이 곧장 그를 향해 키를 돌렸다."], ["At the same time, he saw they were about to lower the boat.", "동시에 그는 그들이 보트를 내리려는 것을 보았다."]], [["An instant after, the boat, rowed by two men, advanced rapidly towards him.", "잠시 후, 두 사람이 노를 젓는 보트가 그를 향해 빠르게 다가왔다."], ["Dantès let go of the timber, which he now thought to be useless, and swam vigorously to meet them.", "당테스는 이제 쓸모없어졌다고 여긴 목재를 놓고, 그들을 만나러 힘차게 헤엄쳤다."], ["But he had reckoned too much upon his strength, and then he realized how serviceable the timber had been to him.", "그러나 그는 제 힘을 너무 과신했고, 그제야 그 목재가 자신에게 얼마나 큰 도움이 되었는지 깨달았다."], ["His arms became stiff, his legs lost their flexibility, and he was almost breathless.", "팔이 뻣뻣해지고, 다리가 유연함을 잃었으며, 숨이 거의 막힐 지경이었다."]], [["He shouted again.", "그가 다시 소리쳤다."], ["The two sailors redoubled their efforts, and one of them cried in Italian, “Courage!”", "두 선원이 더욱 힘껏 노를 저었고, 그중 한 사람이 이탈리아어로 외쳤다. \"힘내요!\""]], [["The word reached his ear as a wave which he no longer had the strength to surmount passed over his head.", "그 말이 그의 귀에 닿는 순간, 이제는 넘어설 힘조차 없는 파도 하나가 그의 머리 위를 덮쳤다."], ["He rose again to the surface, struggled with the last desperate effort of a drowning man, uttered a third cry, and felt himself sinking, as if the fatal cannon shot were again tied to his feet.", "그는 다시 수면으로 떠올라, 물에 빠진 사람의 마지막 필사적인 안간힘으로 발버둥 치며 세 번째 비명을 질렀고, 마치 그 죽음의 포탄이 다시 발에 매달린 것처럼 가라앉는 것을 느꼈다."], ["The water passed over his head, and the sky turned gray.", "물이 그의 머리 위를 넘었고, 하늘이 잿빛으로 변했다."], ["A convulsive movement again brought him to the surface.", "발작하듯 한 번 몸을 틀자 그는 다시 수면으로 떠올랐다."], ["He felt himself seized by the hair, then he saw and heard nothing.", "누군가 그의 머리채를 붙잡는 것이 느껴졌고, 그다음에는 아무것도 보이지도 들리지도 않았다."], ["He had fainted.", "그는 정신을 잃었다."]], [["When he opened his eyes Dantès found himself on the deck of the tartan.", "당테스가 눈을 떴을 때, 그는 타르탄선 갑판 위에 누워 있었다."], ["His first care was to see what course they were taking. They were rapidly leaving the Château d’If behind.", "그가 가장 먼저 신경 쓴 것은 배가 어느 쪽으로 가고 있는지였다. 배는 이프성을 빠르게 뒤로하고 있었다."], ["Dantès was so exhausted that the exclamation of joy he uttered was mistaken for a sigh.", "당테스는 너무 기진맥진한 나머지, 그가 내뱉은 기쁨의 탄성이 한숨으로 오해받을 정도였다."]], [["As we have said, he was lying on the deck.", "앞서 말했듯이 그는 갑판에 누워 있었다."], ["A sailor was rubbing his limbs with a woollen cloth; another, whom he recognized as the one who had cried out “Courage!” held a gourd full of rum to his mouth; while the third, an old sailor, at once the pilot and captain, looked on with that egotistical pity men feel for a misfortune that they have escaped yesterday, and which may overtake them tomorrow.", "한 선원이 모직 천으로 그의 팔다리를 문지르고 있었고, \"힘내요!\"라고 외쳤던 그 사람으로 짐작되는 또 한 선원은 럼주가 가득 든 호리병을 그의 입에 대 주고 있었다. 한편 세 번째 사람, 도선사이자 선장을 겸한 늙은 선원은, 어제 자신은 모면했으나 내일은 자신에게 닥칠지도 모르는 불행에 대해 사람들이 느끼는 그 이기적인 연민의 눈길로 지켜보고 있었다."]], [["A few drops of the rum restored suspended animation, while the friction of his limbs restored their elasticity.", "럼주 몇 방울이 멎었던 숨을 되살렸고, 팔다리를 문질러 주자 다시 탄력이 돌아왔다."]], [["“Who are you?”", "\"당신은 누구요?\""], ["said the pilot in bad French.", "도선사가 서툰 프랑스어로 물었다."]], [["“I am,”", "\"저는,\""], ["replied Dantès, in bad Italian, “a Maltese sailor.", "당테스가 서툰 이탈리아어로 대답했다. \"몰타 선원입니다."], ["We were coming from Syracuse laden with grain.", "시러큐스에서 곡물을 싣고 오던 길이었지요."], ["The storm of last night overtook us at Cape Morgiou, and we were wrecked on these rocks.”", "어젯밤 폭풍이 모르지우곶에서 우리를 덮쳤고, 우리는 이 바위에 부딪혀 난파했습니다.\""]], [["“Where do you come from?”", "\"어디서 오는 길이오?\""]], [["“From these rocks that I had the good luck to cling to while our captain and the rest of the crew were all lost.", "\"제가 운 좋게 매달릴 수 있었던 저 바위에서 옵니다. 우리 선장과 나머지 선원들은 모두 목숨을 잃었지요."], ["I saw your vessel, and fearful of being left to perish on the desolate island, I swam off on a piece of wreckage to try and intercept your course.", "저는 당신네 배를 보았고, 황량한 섬에 버려져 죽을까 두려워, 잔해 한 조각에 의지해 헤엄쳐 나와 당신들 항로를 가로막아 보려 했습니다."], ["You have saved my life, and I thank you,”", "당신들이 제 목숨을 구해 주셨으니, 감사드립니다.\""], ["continued Dantès.", "당테스가 말을 이었다."], ["“I was lost when one of your sailors caught hold of my hair.”", "\"당신네 선원 한 분이 제 머리채를 붙잡았을 때, 저는 죽은 목숨이었습니다.\""]], [["“It was I,”", "\"그게 바로 나요.\""], ["said a sailor of a frank and manly appearance; “and it was time, for you were sinking.”", "솔직하고 사내다운 인상의 선원이 말했다. \"마침맞은 때였지요. 당신은 가라앉고 있었으니까.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["returned Dantès, holding out his hand, “I thank you again.”", "당테스가 손을 내밀며 대답했다. \"다시 한번 감사드립니다.\""]], [["“I almost hesitated, though,”", "\"그래도 나는 망설일 뻔했소.\""], ["replied the sailor; “you looked more like a brigand than an honest man, with your beard six inches, and your hair a foot long.”", "선원이 대답했다. \"당신은 정직한 사람이라기보다 산적처럼 보였거든. 수염은 여섯 치나 되고, 머리카락은 한 자나 길었으니 말이오.\""]], [["Dantès recollected that his hair and beard had not been cut all the time he was at the Château d’If.", "당테스는 이프성에 있는 내내 머리도 수염도 한 번 자르지 않았다는 사실을 떠올렸다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “I made a vow, to our Lady of the Grotto not to cut my hair or beard for ten years if I were saved in a moment of danger; but today the vow expires.”", "\"동굴의 성모님께 맹세를 했지요. 위급한 순간에 목숨을 구하게 되면 십 년 동안 머리와 수염을 자르지 않겠다고요. 그런데 오늘로 그 맹세가 끝났네요.\""]], [["“Now what are we to do with you?”", "\"자, 이제 당신을 어떻게 하면 좋겠소?\""], ["said the captain.", "선장이 물었다."]], [["“Alas, anything you please.", "\"아아, 좋으실 대로 하십시오."], ["My captain is dead; I have barely escaped; but I am a good sailor.", "제 선장은 죽었고, 저는 가까스로 살아났지요. 하지만 저는 훌륭한 선원입니다."], ["Leave me at the first port you make; I shall be sure to find employment.”", "처음 닿는 항구에 저를 내려 주십시오. 일자리쯤은 틀림없이 찾을 수 있을 테니까요.\""]], [["“Do you know the Mediterranean?”", "\"지중해를 잘 아시오?\""]], [["“I have sailed over it since my childhood.”", "\"어릴 적부터 그 바다를 누볐습니다.\""]], [["“You know the best harbors?”", "\"좋은 항구들을 잘 아시오?\""]], [["“There are few ports that I could not enter or leave with a bandage over my eyes.”", "\"눈을 가린 채로도 드나들 수 있는 항구가 한둘이 아니지요.\""]], [["“I say, captain,”", "\"이보세요, 선장님.\""], ["said the sailor who had cried “Courage!”", "당테스에게 \"힘내요!\"라고 외쳤던 선원이 말했다."], ["to Dantès, “if what he says is true, what hinders his staying with us?”", "\"이 사람 말이 사실이라면, 우리와 함께 지내지 못할 이유가 어디 있겠습니까?\""]], [["“If he says true,” said the captain doubtingly.", "\"그 말이 사실이라면 말이지.\" 선장이 미심쩍은 듯 말했다."], ["“But in his present condition he will promise anything, and take his chance of keeping it afterwards.”", "\"하지만 지금 같은 처지라면 무슨 약속이든 하고서, 나중에 지킬지 말지는 운에 맡길 테지.\""]], [["“I will do more than I promise,”", "\"저는 약속한 것보다 더 해 보이겠습니다.\""], ["said Dantès.", "당테스가 말했다."]], [["“We shall see,”", "\"두고 봅시다.\""], ["returned the other, smiling.", "상대가 미소 지으며 대답했다."]], [["“Where are you going?”", "\"어디로 가는 길입니까?\""], ["asked Dantès.", "당테스가 물었다."]], [["“To Leghorn.”", "\"리보르노로 가오.\""]], [["“Then why, instead of tacking so frequently, do you not sail nearer the wind?”", "\"그렇다면 어째서 그렇게 자주 방향을 틀지 않고, 바람을 좀 더 바짝 받으며 가지 않으십니까?\""]], [["“Because we should run straight on to the Island of Rion.”", "\"그랬다간 곧장 리옹섬으로 처박힐 테니까.\""]], [["“You shall pass it by twenty fathoms.”", "\"제가 그 섬을 20패덤 차이로 비껴 지나가게 해 보이겠습니다.\""]], [["“Take the helm, and let us see what you know.”", "\"키를 잡아 보시오. 당신 솜씨가 어떤지 봅시다.\""]], [["The young man took the helm, felt to see if the vessel answered the rudder promptly and seeing that, without being a first-rate sailor, she yet was tolerably obedient.", "젊은이는 키를 잡고, 배가 키에 곧바로 반응하는지 가늠해 보았다. 일류 선박은 아니었으나, 그래도 제법 말을 잘 듣는 편이었다."]], [["“To the sheets,”", "\"아딧줄을 잡아라.\""], ["said he. The four seamen, who composed the crew, obeyed, while the pilot looked on.", "그가 말했다. 선원으로 일하는 네 사람이 명령에 따랐고, 도선사는 지켜보았다."], ["“Haul taut.”", "\"팽팽히 당겨라.\""]], [["They obeyed.", "그들이 따랐다."]], [["“Belay.”", "\"매어라.\""], ["This order was also executed; and the vessel passed, as Dantès had predicted, twenty fathoms to windward.", "이 명령도 그대로 이행되었다. 그리고 배는 당테스가 예언한 대로 바람 부는 쪽으로 20패덤 차이를 두고 지나갔다."]], [["“Bravo!” said the captain.", "\"브라보!\" 선장이 외쳤다."]], [["“Bravo!”", "\"브라보!\""], ["repeated the sailors.", "선원들이 따라 외쳤다."], ["And they all looked with astonishment at this man whose eye now disclosed an intelligence and his body a vigor they had not thought him capable of showing.", "그들은 모두 놀란 눈으로 이 사내를 바라보았다. 이제 그의 눈에서는 그들이 예상치 못했던 총기가, 그의 몸에서는 미처 짐작지 못했던 활력이 드러나고 있었다."]], [["“You see,”", "\"보십시오.\""], ["said Dantès, quitting the helm, “I shall be of some use to you, at least during the voyage.", "당테스가 키에서 손을 떼며 말했다. \"적어도 항해하는 동안에는 제가 당신들께 도움이 될 겁니다."], ["If you do not want me at Leghorn, you can leave me there, and I will pay you out of the first wages I get, for my food and the clothes you lend me.”", "리보르노에서 제가 필요 없으시면, 거기 내려 주십시오. 그러면 제 먹은 음식값과 빌려 입은 옷값을, 처음 받는 봉급에서 갚아 드리지요.\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아.\""], ["said the captain, “we can agree very well, if you are reasonable.”", "선장이 말했다. \"당신이 사리에 밝은 사람이라면, 우리 잘 합의할 수 있겠소.\""]], [["“Give me what you give the others, and it will be all right,”", "\"다른 사람들에게 주시는 만큼만 주십시오. 그러면 됩니다.\""], ["returned Dantès.", "당테스가 대답했다."]], [["“That’s not fair,”", "\"그건 공정하지 않은데요.\""], ["said the seaman who had saved Dantès; “for you know more than we do.”", "당테스를 구한 선원이 말했다. \"당신은 우리보다 아는 게 많으니까요.\""]], [["“What is that to you, Jacopo?” returned the Captain.", "\"그게 자네와 무슨 상관인가, 자코포?\" 선장이 대꾸했다."], ["“Everyone is free to ask what he pleases.”", "\"누구든 제가 원하는 만큼 청할 자유는 있는 거지.\""]], [["“That’s true,”", "\"그건 그렇습니다.\""], ["replied Jacopo; “I only make a remark.”", "자코포가 대답했다. \"그저 한마디 한 것뿐인걸요.\""]], [["“Well, you would do much better to find him a jacket and a pair of trousers, if you have them.”", "\"그럴 거면, 가지고 있거든 이 사람에게 윗옷하고 바지나 한 벌 찾아 주는 게 훨씬 나을 걸세.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"윗옷은 없습니다.\""], ["said Jacopo; “but I have a shirt and a pair of trousers.”", "자코포가 말했다. \"하지만 셔츠 한 장하고 바지 한 벌은 있지요.\""]], [["“That is all I want,”", "\"그거면 충분합니다.\""], ["interrupted Dantès.", "당테스가 말을 가로챘다."], ["Jacopo dived into the hold and soon returned with what Edmond wanted.", "자코포는 선창으로 뛰어 내려가, 곧 에드몽이 원하던 것을 가지고 돌아왔다."]], [["“Now, then, do you wish for anything else?”", "\"자, 그럼 그 밖에 더 필요한 건 없소?\""], ["said the patron.", "선장이 물었다."]], [["“A piece of bread and another glass of the capital rum I tasted, for I have not eaten or drunk for a long time.”", "\"빵 한 조각하고, 아까 맛본 그 일품 럼주 한 잔만 더 주십시오. 오랫동안 아무것도 먹지도 마시지도 못했거든요.\""], ["He had not tasted food for forty hours.", "그는 마흔 시간 동안 음식을 입에 대지 못했다."], ["A piece of bread was brought, and Jacopo offered him the gourd.", "빵 한 조각이 건네졌고, 자코포가 호리병을 내밀었다."]], [["“Larboard your helm,”", "\"키를 좌현으로!\""], ["cried the captain to the steersman.", "선장이 키잡이에게 외쳤다."], ["Dantès glanced that way as he lifted the gourd to his mouth; then paused with hand in mid-air.", "당테스는 호리병을 입으로 가져가다 그쪽을 흘끗 보았고, 그러고는 손을 허공에 든 채 멈췄다."]], [["“Hollo!", "\"이런!"], ["what’s the matter at the Château d’If?”", "이프성에 무슨 일이 생겼나?\""], ["said the captain.", "선장이 말했다."]], [["A small white cloud, which had attracted Dantès’ attention, crowned the summit of the bastion of the Château d’If.", "당테스의 눈길을 끌었던 작고 하얀 구름 한 점이 이프성 능보 꼭대기에 걸려 있었다."], ["At the same moment the faint report of a gun was heard.", "동시에 어렴풋한 포성이 들려왔다."], ["The sailors looked at one another.", "선원들이 서로를 마주 보았다."]], [["“What is this?” asked the captain.", "\"이게 무슨 일이지?\" 선장이 물었다."]], [["“A prisoner has escaped from the Château d’If, and they are firing the alarm gun,” replied Dantès.", "\"이프성에서 죄수 하나가 탈출해서, 경보 대포를 쏘는 겁니다.\" 당테스가 대답했다."], ["The captain glanced at him, but he had lifted the rum to his lips and was drinking it with so much composure, that suspicions, if the captain had any, died away. 0277m", "선장이 그를 흘끗 보았으나, 그가 럼주를 입에 대고 어찌나 태연하게 들이켜는지, 선장에게 의심이 있었더라도 이내 사그라지고 말았다."]], [["“Pretty strong rum!”", "\"꽤 독한 럼주로군요!\""], ["said Dantès, wiping his brow with his sleeve.", "당테스가 소매로 이마를 훔치며 말했다."]], [["“At any rate,”", "'어쨌든,'"], ["murmured he, “if it be, so much the better, for I have made a rare acquisition.” 0279m", "선장이 속으로 중얼거렸다. '정말 그렇다면, 더욱 잘된 일이지. 좀처럼 보기 드문 일꾼을 하나 얻은 셈이니까.'"]], [["Under pretence of being fatigued, Dantès asked to take the helm; the steersman, glad to be relieved, looked at the captain, and the latter by a sign indicated that he might abandon it to his new comrade.", "당테스는 피곤하다는 핑계로 키를 잡겠다고 청했다. 키잡이는 짐을 덜어 기뻐하며 선장을 바라보았고, 선장은 새 동료에게 키를 맡겨도 좋다는 신호를 보냈다."], ["Dantès could thus keep his eyes on Marseilles.", "그리하여 당테스는 마르세유에서 눈을 뗄 수 있게 되었다."]], [["“What is the day of the month?”", "\"오늘이 며칠이오?\""], ["asked he of Jacopo, who sat down beside him.", "그가 곁에 앉은 자코포에게 물었다."]], [["“The 28th of February.”", "\"2월 28일이오.\""]], [["“In what year?”", "\"몇 년도요?\""]], [["“In what year—you ask me in what year?”", "\"몇 년도냐니, 그걸 나한테 묻는 거요?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["replied the young man, “I ask you in what year!”", "젊은이가 대답했다. \"몇 년도냐고 묻는 겁니다!\""]], [["“You have forgotten then?”", "\"그새 잊어버린 거요?\""]], [["“I got such a fright last night,”", "\"어젯밤 어찌나 혼이 났는지,\""], ["replied Dantès, smiling, “that I have almost lost my memory.", "당테스가 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"기억을 거의 잃어버릴 지경이었거든요."], ["I ask you what year is it?”", "그래서 올해가 몇 년도냐고 묻는 겁니다.\""]], [["“The year 1829,”", "\"1829년이지요.\""], ["returned Jacopo.", "자코포가 대답했다."]], [["It was fourteen years, day for day, since Dantès’ arrest. He was nineteen when he entered the Château d’If; he was thirty-three when he escaped.", "당테스가 체포된 지, 날짜까지 꼭 들어맞게 14년이 흐른 것이었다. 그가 이프성에 들어갈 때 열아홉이었고, 탈출한 지금은 서른셋이었다."], ["A sorrowful smile passed over his face; he asked himself what had become of Mercédès, who must believe him dead.", "서글픈 미소가 그의 얼굴을 스쳤다. 그는 분명 자신을 죽은 사람으로 여기고 있을 메르세데스가 어떻게 되었을지 자문했다."], ["Then his eyes lighted up with hatred as he thought of the three men who had caused him so long and wretched a captivity.", "이윽고, 그토록 길고 비참한 옥살이를 안긴 세 사내를 떠올리자 그의 눈이 증오로 번득였다."], ["He renewed against Danglars, Fernand, and Villefort the oath of implacable vengeance he had made in his dungeon.", "그는 지하 감옥에서 했던, 가차 없는 복수의 맹세를 당글라르와 페르낭과 빌포르를 향해 다시금 새겼다."]], [["This oath was no longer a vain menace; for the fastest sailor in the Mediterranean would have been unable to overtake the little tartan, that with every stitch of canvas set was flying before the wind to Leghorn.", "이 맹세는 더 이상 헛된 위협이 아니었다. 지중해에서 가장 빠른 배라 해도, 돛이란 돛은 죄다 펼친 채 바람을 타고 리보르노로 날아가는 이 작은 타르탄선을 따라잡지는 못할 터였기 때문이다."]], [["# Chapter 22. The Smugglers", "# Chapter 22. The Smugglers"]], [["Dantès had not been a day on board before he had a very clear idea of the men with whom his lot had been cast.", "당테스는 배에 오른 지 하루도 지나지 않아 자신이 어떤 부류의 사람들 틈에 끼게 되었는지 또렷이 파악했다."], ["Without having been in the school of the Abbé Faria, the worthy master of _La Jeune Amélie_ (the name of the Genoese tartan) knew a smattering of all the tongues spoken on the shores of that large lake called the Mediterranean, from the Arabic to the Provençal, and this, while it spared him interpreters, persons always troublesome and frequently indiscreet, gave him great facilities of communication, either with the vessels he met at sea, with the small boats sailing along the coast, or with the people without name, country, or occupation, who are always seen on the quays of seaports, and who live by hidden and mysterious means which we must suppose to be a direct gift of Providence, as they have no visible means of support.", "라죈아멜리호—그 제노바산 삼각돛배의 이름이었다—를 부리는 노련한 선장은 파리아 신부의 가르침을 받은 적이 없었지만, 지중해라 불리는 그 거대한 호수의 연안에서 쓰이는 온갖 언어를 아랍어부터 프로방스어까지 얼추 알고 있었다. 덕분에 그는 늘 성가시고 입이 가벼운 통역을 쓸 필요가 없었고, 바다에서 마주치는 배들이나 해안을 따라 항해하는 작은 배들, 또는 이름도 나라도 직업도 없이 항구의 부두에서 늘 어슬렁거리는 사람들과 손쉽게 소통할 수 있었다. 그런 자들은 눈에 보이는 생계 수단이 없으니, 그들이 살아가는 은밀하고 알 수 없는 방편이란 섭리가 직접 내려 준 선물이라 여길 수밖에 없었다."], ["It is fair to assume that Dantès was on board a smuggler.", "당테스가 밀수선에 올랐다고 보는 것이 타당했다."]], [["At first the captain had received Dantès on board with a certain degree of distrust.", "처음에 선장은 당테스를 어느 정도 미심쩍어하며 배에 받아들였다."], ["He was very well known to the customs officers of the coast; and as there was between these worthies and himself a perpetual battle of wits, he had at first thought that Dantès might be an emissary of these industrious guardians of rights and duties, who perhaps employed this ingenious means of learning some of the secrets of his trade.", "그는 해안의 세관원들에게 아주 잘 알려진 인물이었고, 그 위인들과 자기 사이에는 끊임없는 두뇌 싸움이 벌어지고 있었기에, 당테스가 그 부지런한 권리와 의무의 파수꾼들이 보낸 첩자일지도 모른다고 처음에는 생각했다. 그들이 자기 일의 비밀을 캐내려고 이런 교묘한 수단을 쓴 게 아닐까 의심한 것이다."], ["But the skilful manner in which Dantès had handled the lugger had entirely reassured him; and then, when he saw the light plume of smoke floating above the bastion of the Château d’If, and heard the distant report, he was instantly struck with the idea that he had on board his vessel one whose coming and going, like that of kings, was accompanied with salutes of artillery.", "그러나 당테스가 능숙하게 배를 다루는 솜씨를 보고는 의심을 말끔히 거두었다. 그러다 이프성 보루 위로 떠오르는 가벼운 연기 깃털을 보고 멀리서 들려오는 포성을 들었을 때, 그는 문득 자기 배에 왕의 행차처럼 오가는 길마다 예포가 울리는 인물을 태우고 있다는 생각이 들었다."], ["This made him less uneasy, it must be owned, than if the new-comer had proved to be a customs officer; but this supposition also disappeared like the first, when he beheld the perfect tranquillity of his recruit.", "솔직히 말해 이런 생각은, 새로 온 자가 세관원으로 드러나는 것보다는 그를 덜 불안하게 했다. 그러나 이 추측 역시 신참이 더할 나위 없이 태연한 모습을 보이자 첫 번째 추측과 마찬가지로 사라져 버렸다."]], [["Edmond thus had the advantage of knowing what the owner was, without the owner knowing who he was; and however the old sailor and his crew tried to “pump”", "이리하여 에드몽은 선주가 자신이 누구인지 모르는 채로 선주가 어떤 인물인지를 아는 잇점을 누렸다. 그 늙은 선원과 선원들이 아무리 그의 속을 떠보려 해도 더 이상 아무것도 캐내지 못했다."], ["him, they extracted nothing more from him; he gave accurate descriptions of Naples and Malta, which he knew as well as Marseilles, and held stoutly to his first story.", "그는 마르세유만큼이나 잘 아는 나폴리와 몰타를 정확하게 묘사하며, 처음 한 이야기를 끝까지 굳건히 고수했다. 이렇게 해서 교활한 그 제노바 사람은 에드몽에게 보기 좋게 속아 넘어갔다."], ["Thus the Genoese, subtle as he was, was duped by Edmond, in whose favor his mild demeanor, his nautical skill, and his admirable dissimulation, pleaded.", "에드몽의 온화한 태도와 항해 솜씨, 그리고 감탄스러울 만큼 능청스러운 위장술이 그에게 유리하게 작용했다."], ["Moreover, it is possible that the Genoese was one of those shrewd persons who know nothing but what they should know, and believe nothing but what they should believe.", "더구나 그 제노바 사람은 알아야 할 것만 알고 믿어야 할 것만 믿는 약삭빠른 부류 가운데 하나였는지도 모른다."]], [["In this state of mutual understanding, they reached Leghorn.", "이런 상호 양해 속에서 그들은 리보르노에 닿았다."], ["Here Edmond was to undergo another trial; he was to find out whether he could recognize himself, as he had not seen his own face for fourteen years.", "여기서 에드몽은 또 다른 시험을 치러야 했다. 십사 년 동안 제 얼굴을 본 적이 없으니, 자기 자신을 알아볼 수 있을지 확인해야 했던 것이다."], ["He had preserved a tolerably good remembrance of what the youth had been, and was now to find out what the man had become.", "그는 젊은 시절의 자기 모습을 그런대로 잘 기억하고 있었고, 이제 그 사내가 어떻게 변했는지 알아볼 참이었다."], ["His comrades believed that his vow was fulfilled.", "동료들은 그가 서원을 다 마쳤다고 믿었다."], ["As he had twenty times touched at Leghorn, he remembered a barber in St. Ferdinand Street; he went there to have his beard and hair cut.", "리보르노에는 스무 번도 넘게 들렀던 터라, 그는 생페르디낭 거리의 어느 이발사를 떠올렸다. 수염과 머리를 깎으러 그곳에 갔다."], ["The barber gazed in amazement at this man with the long, thick and black hair and beard, which gave his head the appearance of one of Titian’s portraits.", "이발사는 그 길고 숱 많은 검은 머리와 수염을 보고 깜짝 놀랐다. 티치아노의 초상화 속 인물 같은 머리였다."], ["At this period it was not the fashion to wear so large a beard and hair so long; now a barber would only be surprised if a man gifted with such advantages should consent voluntarily to deprive himself of them.", "그 시절에는 그토록 긴 수염과 머리가 유행이 아니었다. 요즘 같으면 이발사는 그런 멋진 장점을 타고난 사내가 제 발로 그것을 없애겠다고 할 때나 놀랐을 것이다."], ["The Leghorn barber said nothing and went to work.", "리보르노의 이발사는 아무 말 없이 일을 시작했다."]], [["When the operation was concluded, and Edmond felt that his chin was completely smooth, and his hair reduced to its usual length, he asked for a looking-glass.", "작업이 끝나고 에드몽이 턱이 매끈해지고 머리가 평소 길이로 돌아온 것을 느끼자, 그는 거울을 청했다."], ["He was now, as we have said, three-and-thirty years of age, and his fourteen years’ imprisonment had produced a great transformation in his appearance.", "앞서 말했듯이 그는 이제 서른세 살이었고, 십사 년의 옥살이는 그의 모습을 크게 바꿔 놓았다."]], [["Dantès had entered the Château d’If with the round, open, smiling face of a young and happy man, with whom the early paths of life have been smooth, and who anticipates a future corresponding with his past.", "당테스는 둥글고 환하게 웃는 얼굴로 이프성에 들어갔었다. 인생의 첫 길이 순탄하기만 했고 과거에 걸맞은 미래를 꿈꾸던, 젊고 행복한 사내의 얼굴이었다. 그 모든 것이 이제 달라졌다."], ["This was now all changed.", "갸름하던 얼굴은 길어졌고, 미소 짓던 입가에는 결단을 드러내는 단단하고 또렷한 선이 패었다. 사색으로 골이 진 이마 아래 눈썹은 활처럼 휘었고, 두 눈에는 우수가 가득했으며, 그 깊은 곳에서 이따금 인간에 대한 혐오와 증오의 음울한 불꽃이 번득였다."], ["The oval face was lengthened, his smiling mouth had assumed the firm and marked lines which betoken resolution; his eyebrows were arched beneath a brow furrowed with thought; his eyes were full of melancholy, and from their depths occasionally sparkled gloomy fires of misanthropy and hatred; his complexion, so long kept from the sun, had now that pale color which produces, when the features are encircled with black hair, the aristocratic beauty of the man of the north; the profound learning he had acquired had besides diffused over his features a refined intellectual expression; and he had also acquired, being naturally of a goodly stature, that vigor which a frame possesses which has so long concentrated all its force within itself. 0283m", "오랫동안 햇빛을 보지 못한 그의 안색은 이제 창백한 빛을 띠었는데, 검은 머리에 둘러싸인 그 얼굴은 북방 사내의 귀족적인 아름다움을 자아냈다. 게다가 그가 쌓아 올린 깊은 학식은 그의 이목구비에 세련된 지성의 표정을 퍼뜨렸다. 또한 본래 늠름한 체격을 타고난 그는, 오랫동안 모든 힘을 제 안에 응축해 온 몸이 지니는 그 강건함을 얻었다."]], [["To the elegance of a nervous and slight form had succeeded the solidity of a rounded and muscular figure.", "가냘프고 예민했던 몸의 우아함은 이제 둥글고 근육진 체구의 단단함으로 바뀌어 있었다."], ["As to his voice, prayers, sobs, and imprecations had changed it so that at times it was of a singularly penetrating sweetness, and at others rough and almost hoarse.", "목소리로 말하자면, 기도와 흐느낌과 저주가 그것을 바꿔 놓아, 때로는 유난히 사무치는 부드러움을 띠다가도 때로는 거칠고 거의 쉰 소리가 났다."]], [["Moreover, from being so long in twilight or darkness, his eyes had acquired the faculty of distinguishing objects in the night, common to the hyena and the wolf.", "게다가 오랫동안 어스름과 어둠 속에 있었던 탓에 그의 눈은 하이에나나 늑대처럼 밤에도 사물을 분간하는 능력을 얻었다. 에드몽은 제 모습을 보고 미소 지었다."], ["Edmond smiled when he beheld himself; it was impossible that his best friend—if, indeed, he had any friend left—could recognize him; he could not recognize himself.", "가장 친한 친구가—설령 아직 그에게 친구가 남아 있다 한들—그를 알아보기란 불가능했다. 그 자신조차 자기를 알아보지 못했으니까."]], [["The master of _La Jeune Amélie_, who was very desirous of retaining amongst his crew a man of Edmond’s value, had offered to advance him funds out of his future profits, which Edmond had accepted.", "라죈아멜리호의 선장은 에드몽처럼 쓸모 있는 사내를 선원으로 붙들어 두고 싶은 마음이 간절해, 앞으로의 수익에서 미리 자금을 빌려주겠다고 제안했고 에드몽은 그것을 받아들였다."], ["His next care on leaving the barber’s who had achieved his first metamorphosis was to enter a shop and buy a complete sailor’s suit—a garb, as we all know, very simple, and consisting of white trousers, a striped shirt, and a cap.", "그를 처음으로 탈바꿈시켜 준 이발소를 나선 뒤, 그가 다음으로 한 일은 가게에 들러 선원 복장 한 벌을 통째로 사는 것이었다. 누구나 알다시피 아주 단순한 차림으로, 흰 바지와 줄무늬 셔츠, 그리고 모자로 이루어진 옷이었다."]], [["It was in this costume, and bringing back to Jacopo the shirt and trousers he had lent him, that Edmond reappeared before the captain of the lugger, who had made him tell his story over and over again before he could believe him, or recognize in the neat and trim sailor the man with thick and matted beard, hair tangled with seaweed, and body soaking in seabrine, whom he had picked up naked and nearly drowned.", "에드몽은 이 차림으로, 자코포에게 빌렸던 셔츠와 바지를 돌려주며 그 삼각돛배의 선장 앞에 다시 나타났다. 선장은 그의 이야기를 몇 번이나 거듭 들은 뒤에야 겨우 믿을 수 있었고, 또 그 말끔하고 단정한 선원이 자기가 발가벗긴 채 거의 익사 직전에 건져 올렸던 그 사내—숱이 엉킨 수염에 머리는 해초로 헝클어지고 몸은 바닷물에 절어 있던 그 사내—임을 알아볼 수 있었다."], ["Attracted by his prepossessing appearance, he renewed his offers of an engagement to Dantès; but Dantès, who had his own projects, would not agree for a longer time than three months.", "호감 가는 그의 모습에 끌린 선장은 당테스에게 다시 한번 고용 제안을 했다. 그러나 자기 나름의 계획이 있던 당테스는 석 달 이상은 안 된다고 했다."]], [["_La Jeune Amélie_ had a very active crew, very obedient to their captain, who lost as little time as possible.", "라죈아멜리호에는 아주 활기찬 선원들이 있었고, 그들은 시간을 최대한 아끼는 선장에게 매우 순종적이었다."], ["He had scarcely been a week at Leghorn before the hold of his vessel was filled with printed muslins, contraband cottons, English powder, and tobacco on which the excise had forgotten to put its mark.", "리보르노에 닿은 지 일주일도 안 되어 배의 선창은 무늬 찍은 모슬린과 밀수 면포, 영국제 화약, 그리고 세금 도장이 깜빡 잊힌 담배로 가득 찼다."], ["The master was to get all this out of Leghorn free of duties, and land it on the shores of Corsica, where certain speculators undertook to forward the cargo to France.", "선장은 이 모든 것을 세금 한 푼 안 내고 리보르노에서 빼내, 코르시카 해안에 부려야 했다. 그곳에서 어떤 투기꾼들이 화물을 프랑스로 보내는 일을 맡기로 되어 있었다."]], [["They sailed; Edmond was again cleaving the azure sea which had been the first horizon of his youth, and which he had so often dreamed of in prison.", "그들은 출항했다. 에드몽은 다시금 젊은 시절 처음 보았던 그 지평선, 감옥에서 그토록 자주 꿈꾸었던 그 쪽빛 바다를 가르고 있었다."], ["He left Gorgone on his right and La Pianosa on his left, and went towards the country of Paoli and Napoleon.", "그는 고르고나섬을 오른쪽에, 라피아노자섬을 왼쪽에 두고 파올리와 나폴레옹의 고장을 향해 나아갔다."]], [["The next morning going on deck, as he always did at an early hour, the patron found Dantès leaning against the bulwarks gazing with intense earnestness at a pile of granite rocks, which the rising sun tinged with rosy light. It was the Island of Monte Cristo.", "이튿날 아침, 늘 그렇듯 이른 시각에 갑판에 오른 선장은 당테스가 뱃전에 기댄 채 화강암 바위 더미를 골똘히 바라보고 있는 것을 발견했다. 떠오르는 해가 장밋빛으로 물들이고 있는 그 바위 더미는 바로 몬테크리스토섬이었다."]], [["_La Jeune Amélie_ left it three-quarters of a league to the larboard and kept on for Corsica.", "라죈아멜리호는 그 섬을 왼쪽으로 4분의 3리외쯤 떨어뜨려 두고 코르시카를 향해 나아갔다."], ["Dantès thought, as they passed so closely to the island whose name was so interesting to him, that he had only to leap into the sea and in half an hour be at the promised land.", "당테스는 그토록 흥미로운 이름을 지닌 그 섬 곁을 이렇게 가까이 지나며 생각했다. 바다로 뛰어들기만 하면 반 시간 안에 약속의 땅에 닿을 수 있으리라고."], ["But then what could he do without instruments to discover his treasure, without arms to defend himself?", "그러나 그러고 나서, 보물을 찾아낼 도구도 없고 제 몸을 지킬 무기도 없으니 무엇을 할 수 있단 말인가?"], ["Besides, what would the sailors say?", "게다가 선원들이 뭐라고 하겠는가?"], ["What would the patron think?", "선장은 무엇을 떠올리겠는가?"], ["He must wait.", "기다려야 했다."]], [["Fortunately, Dantès had learned how to wait; he had waited fourteen years for his liberty, and now he was free he could wait at least six months or a year for wealth.", "다행히 당테스는 기다리는 법을 배운 사람이었다. 자유를 얻기까지 십사 년을 기다렸으니, 이제 자유로워진 그는 부를 위해서라면 적어도 여섯 달, 아니 일 년쯤은 기다릴 수 있었다."], ["Would he not have accepted liberty without riches if it had been offered to him?", "만약 재물 없는 자유가 주어졌더라도 그는 그것을 받아들이지 않았겠는가?"], ["Besides, were not those riches chimerical?—offspring of the brain of the poor Abbé Faria, had they not died with him?", "게다가 그 재물이 한낱 헛것은 아닐까? 가엾은 파리아 신부의 머릿속에서 태어난 그것이, 신부와 함께 죽어 버린 것은 아닐까?"], ["It is true, the letter of the Cardinal Spada was singularly circumstantial, and Dantès repeated it to himself, from one end to the other, for he had not forgotten a word.", "하기야 스파다 추기경의 편지는 유난히 자세했고, 당테스는 그것을 한 글자도 잊지 않았기에 처음부터 끝까지 속으로 되뇌어 보았다."]], [["0285m\nEvening came, and Edmond saw the island tinged with the shades of twilight, and then disappear in the darkness from all eyes but his own, for he, with vision accustomed to the gloom of a prison, continued to behold it last of all, for he remained alone upon deck.", "저녁이 왔다. 에드몽은 그 섬이 황혼의 그늘에 물들었다가, 이윽고 어둠 속으로 모든 이의 눈에서 사라지는 것을 지켜보았다. 다만 그만은 예외였다."], ["The next morn broke off the coast of Aleria; all day they coasted, and in the evening saw fires lighted on land; the position of these was no doubt a signal for landing, for a ship’s lantern was hung up at the mast-head instead of the streamer, and they came to within a gunshot of the shore.", "감옥의 어둠에 길든 그의 눈은 그것을 가장 마지막까지 바라볼 수 있었고, 그는 갑판에 홀로 남아 있었으니까. 이튿날 아침은 알레리아 앞바다에서 밝았다. 그들은 하루 종일 해안을 따라 항해했고, 저녁이 되자 뭍에 피워 놓은 불을 보았다."], ["Dantès noticed that the captain of _La Jeune Amélie_ had, as he neared the land, mounted two small culverins, which, without making much noise, can throw a four ounce ball a thousand paces or so.", "그 위치는 분명 상륙 신호였으니, 돛대 꼭대기에는 깃발 대신 배의 등불이 내걸렸고, 그들은 해안에서 포 한 발 거리까지 다가갔다. 당테스는 라죈아멜리호의 선장이 뭍에 가까워지자 작은 회전포 두 문을 거치하는 것을 알아챘다. 그 포들은 큰 소리를 내지 않고도 4온스짜리 탄알을 천 보가량 날려 보낼 수 있었다."]], [["But on this occasion the precaution was superfluous, and everything proceeded with the utmost smoothness and politeness.", "그러나 이번만큼은 그 대비가 쓸데없었고, 모든 일이 더할 나위 없이 매끄럽고 정중하게 진행되었다."], ["Four shallops came off with very little noise alongside the lugger, which, no doubt, in acknowledgement of the compliment, lowered her own shallop into the sea, and the five boats worked so well that by two o’clock in the morning all the cargo was out of _La Jeune Amélie_ and on _terra firma_.", "작은 거룻배 네 척이 거의 소리도 없이 삼각돛배 곁으로 다가왔고, 배는 그 인사에 답하듯 제 거룻배를 바다에 내렸다. 다섯 척의 배가 어찌나 일을 잘 해냈던지, 새벽 두 시 무렵에는 라죈아멜리호의 화물이 죄다 빠져나가 단단한 땅에 올라 있었다."], ["The same night, such a man of regularity was the patron of _La Jeune Amélie_, the profits were divided, and each man had a hundred Tuscan livres, or about eighty francs.", "라죈아멜리호의 선장은 어찌나 빈틈없는 사내였던지, 바로 그날 밤 수익을 나누었고, 저마다 토스카나 리브르로 백 리브르, 그러니까 약 80프랑씩을 받았다."]], [["But the voyage was not ended.", "그러나 항해는 끝나지 않았다."], ["They turned the bowsprit towards Sardinia, where they intended to take in a cargo, which was to replace what had been discharged.", "그들은 뱃머리를 사르데냐 쪽으로 돌렸다. 그곳에서 부려 낸 짐을 대신할 새 화물을 실을 작정이었다."], ["The second operation was as successful as the first, _La Jeune Amélie_ was in luck.", "두 번째 작업도 첫 번째만큼 성공적이었다. 라죈아멜리호는 운이 좋았다."], ["This new cargo was destined for the coast of the Duchy of Lucca, and consisted almost entirely of Havana cigars, sherry, and Malaga wines.", "이 새 화물은 루카 공국 연안으로 보내질 것이었고, 거의 전부가 아바나산 시가와 셰리주, 말라가 포도주였다."]], [["There they had a bit of a skirmish in getting rid of the duties; the excise was, in truth, the everlasting enemy of the patron of _La Jeune Amélie_.", "그곳에서 그들은 세금을 피하느라 작은 충돌을 겪었다. 사실 세관은 라죈아멜리호 선장의 영원한 숙적이었다."], ["A customs officer was laid low, and two sailors wounded; Dantès was one of the latter, a ball having touched him in the left shoulder.", "세관원 하나가 쓰러졌고 선원 둘이 다쳤는데, 당테스가 그중 하나였다. 탄알이 그의 왼쪽 어깨를 스친 것이다."], ["Dantès was almost glad of this affray, and almost pleased at being wounded, for they were rude lessons which taught him with what eye he could view danger, and with what endurance he could bear suffering.", "당테스는 이 싸움이 거의 반가웠고, 다친 것조차 거의 기뻤다. 어떤 눈으로 위험을 바라볼 수 있는지, 또 어떤 인내로 고통을 견딜 수 있는지를 가르쳐 준 모진 수업이었으니까."], ["He had contemplated danger with a smile, and when wounded had exclaimed with the great philosopher, “Pain, thou art not an evil.”", "그는 미소를 띤 채 위험을 마주했고, 부상을 입었을 때는 저 위대한 철학자처럼 외쳤다. \"고통이여, 너는 악이 아니다.\""]], [["He had, moreover, looked upon the customs officer wounded to death, and, whether from heat of blood produced by the encounter, or the chill of human sentiment, this sight had made but slight impression upon him.", "게다가 그는 치명상을 입은 세관원을 바라보았는데, 싸움이 끓어오르게 한 격정 탓인지 아니면 인간적 정의 차가워진 탓인지, 그 광경은 그에게 거의 아무런 인상도 남기지 못했다."], ["Dantès was on the way he desired to follow, and was moving towards the end he wished to achieve; his heart was in a fair way of petrifying in his bosom.", "당테스는 자기가 가고자 하는 길 위에 있었고, 이루고자 하는 목표를 향해 나아가고 있었다. 그의 가슴속 심장은 차츰 돌처럼 굳어 가고 있었다."], ["Jacopo, seeing him fall, had believed him killed, and rushing towards him raised him up, and then attended to him with all the kindness of a devoted comrade.", "그가 쓰러지는 것을 본 자코포는 그가 죽은 줄로 알고 달려가 그를 일으켜 세웠고, 헌신적인 동료의 온갖 정성으로 그를 돌봐 주었다."]], [["This world was not then so good as Doctor Pangloss believed it, neither was it so wicked as Dantès thought it, since this man, who had nothing to expect from his comrade but the inheritance of his share of the prize-money, manifested so much sorrow when he saw him fall.", "그렇다면 이 세상은 팡글로스 박사가 믿었던 것만큼 좋지도 않았지만, 당테스가 생각하던 것만큼 악하지도 않았다. 동료에게서 기대할 것이라곤 그가 받을 몫의 상금을 물려받는 일뿐인 이 사내가, 그가 쓰러지는 것을 보고 그토록 슬퍼했으니 말이다."], ["Fortunately, as we have said, Edmond was only wounded, and with certain herbs gathered at certain seasons, and sold to the smugglers by the old Sardinian women, the wound soon closed.", "다행히 앞서 말했듯 에드몽은 다치기만 했고, 사르데냐의 노파들이 일정한 철에 캐어 밀수꾼들에게 파는 어떤 약초 덕분에 상처는 곧 아물었다."], ["Edmond then resolved to try Jacopo, and offered him in return for his attention a share of his prize-money, but Jacopo refused it indignantly.", "그러자 에드몽은 자코포를 시험해 보기로 마음먹고, 보살펴 준 보답으로 자기 몫의 상금을 나눠 주겠다고 했다. 그러나 자코포는 분개하며 그것을 거절했다."]], [["As a result of the sympathetic devotion which Jacopo had from the first bestowed on Edmond, the latter was moved to a certain degree of affection.", "자코포가 처음부터 에드몽에게 쏟은 따뜻한 헌신에 에드몽도 어느 정도의 애정을 품게 되었다. 그러나 자코포에게는 그것으로 충분했다."], ["But this sufficed for Jacopo, who instinctively felt that Edmond had a right to superiority of position—a superiority which Edmond had concealed from all others.", "그는 에드몽이 더 높은 자리에 설 자격이 있는 사람임을 본능적으로 느꼈으니까. 에드몽이 다른 모든 이에게는 숨겨 온 그 우월함을 말이다."], ["And from this time the kindness which Edmond showed him was enough for the brave seaman.", "그리하여 이때부터 에드몽이 보여 주는 친절만으로도 그 용감한 선원에게는 족했다."]], [["Then in the long days on board ship, when the vessel, gliding on with security over the azure sea, required no care but the hand of the helmsman, thanks to the favorable winds that swelled her sails, Edmond, with a chart in his hand, became the instructor of Jacopo, as the poor Abbé Faria had been his tutor.", "그러다 배 위의 긴 나날, 순풍이 돛을 부풀려 준 덕에 배가 쪽빛 바다 위를 안전하게 미끄러져 나가며 키잡이의 손길 외에는 아무런 보살핌도 필요로 하지 않을 때면, 에드몽은 손에 해도를 들고 자코포의 스승이 되었다. 가엾은 파리아 신부가 자신의 스승이었던 것처럼 말이다."], ["He pointed out to him the bearings of the coast, explained to him the variations of the compass, and taught him to read in that vast book opened over our heads which they call heaven, and where God writes in azure with letters of diamonds.", "그는 자코포에게 해안의 방위를 일러 주고, 나침반의 편차를 설명해 주었으며, 우리 머리 위에 펼쳐진 저 거대한 책, 사람들이 하늘이라 부르고 신이 다이아몬드 글자로 쪽빛 위에 글을 써 놓은 그 책을 읽는 법을 가르쳐 주었다."]], [["And when Jacopo inquired of him, “What is the use of teaching all these things to a poor sailor like me?” Edmond replied, “Who knows?", "자코포가 그에게 \"나 같은 가난한 선원에게 이런 것들을 다 가르쳐서 무슨 소용이 있습니까?\" 하고 물으면, 에드몽은 이렇게 대답했다."], ["You may one day be the captain of a vessel.", "\"누가 알겠나? 자네도 언젠가는 배의 선장이 될지."], ["Your fellow-countryman, Bonaparte, became emperor.”", "자네 동향 사람인 보나파르트도 황제가 되지 않았나.\""], ["We had forgotten to say that Jacopo was a Corsican.", "자코포가 코르시카 사람이었다는 사실을 미처 말하지 못했다."]], [["Two months and a half elapsed in these trips, and Edmond had become as skilful a coaster as he had been a hardy seaman; he had formed an acquaintance with all the smugglers on the coast, and learned all the Masonic signs by which these half pirates recognize each other.", "이런 항해로 두 달 반이 흘렀고, 에드몽은 강인한 선원이었던 만큼이나 능숙한 연안 항해사가 되었다. 그는 해안의 밀수꾼들과 죄다 안면을 텄고, 그 반쯤은 해적이나 다름없는 자들이 서로를 알아보는 암호 신호를 모조리 익혔다."], ["He had passed and re-passed his Island of Monte Cristo twenty times, but not once had he found an opportunity of landing there.", "그는 자신의 몬테크리스토섬 곁을 스무 번이나 오갔지만, 그곳에 상륙할 기회는 단 한 번도 잡지 못했다."]], [["He then formed a resolution.", "그래서 그는 한 가지 결심을 굳혔다."], ["As soon as his engagement with the patron of _La Jeune Amélie_ ended, he would hire a small vessel on his own account—for in his several voyages he had amassed a hundred piastres—and under some pretext land at the Island of Monte Cristo.", "라죈아멜리호 선장과의 계약이 끝나는 대로, 그는 자기 돈으로 작은 배를 한 척 빌릴 작정이었다. 여러 차례 항해하는 동안 백 피아스터를 모아 둔 터였다. 그러고는 무슨 핑계를 대고 몬테크리스토섬에 내리는 것이다."], ["Then he would be free to make his researches, not perhaps entirely at liberty, for he would be doubtless watched by those who accompanied him.", "그러면 그는 자유롭게 탐색에 나설 수 있을 것이었다. 아주 완전히 자유롭지는 않을지도 모른다. 함께 온 자들이 분명 그를 지켜볼 테니까."], ["But in this world we must risk something.", "그러나 이 세상에서는 무언가를 걸어야 하는 법이다."], ["Prison had made Edmond prudent, and he was desirous of running no risk whatever.", "감옥은 에드몽을 신중하게 만들었고, 그는 그 어떤 위험도 감수하고 싶지 않았다."], ["But in vain did he rack his imagination; fertile as it was, he could not devise any plan for reaching the island without companionship.", "그러나 아무리 상상력을 짜내 봐도, 그 풍부한 상상력으로도 그는 동행 없이 그 섬에 닿을 방도를 도무지 떠올릴 수 없었다."]], [["Dantès was tossed about on these doubts and wishes, when the patron, who had great confidence in him, and was very desirous of retaining him in his service, took him by the arm one evening and led him to a tavern on the Via del’ Oglio, where the leading smugglers of Leghorn used to congregate and discuss affairs connected with their trade.", "당테스가 이런 의심과 소망 사이에서 흔들리고 있을 때였다. 그를 깊이 신뢰하며 곁에 붙들어 두고 싶어 하던 선장이 어느 날 저녁 그의 팔을 붙잡고 비아델롤리오 거리의 한 선술집으로 데려갔다. 리보르노의 내로라하는 밀수꾼들이 모여 자기네 일에 관해 의논하던 곳이었다."], ["Already Dantès had visited this maritime Bourse two or three times, and seeing all these hardy free-traders, who supplied the whole coast for nearly two hundred leagues in extent, he had asked himself what power might not that man attain who should give the impulse of his will to all these contrary and diverging minds.", "당테스는 이미 이 바다의 거래소에 두세 번 들른 적이 있었다. 거의 이백 리외에 이르는 해안 전역에 물건을 대는 그 강인한 자유 무역상들을 보면서, 그는 자문하곤 했다. 이 제각각 엇갈리는 자들에게 한 사람의 의지로 추진력을 불어넣는 자라면 과연 어떤 권세에 오를 수 있을까 하고."], ["This time it was a great matter that was under discussion, connected with a vessel laden with Turkey carpets, stuffs of the Levant, and cashmeres.", "이번에는 중대한 안건이 논의되고 있었다. 터키 양탄자와 레반트산 직물, 캐시미어를 실은 배에 관한 일이었다."], ["It was necessary to find some neutral ground on which an exchange could be made, and then to try and land these goods on the coast of France.", "거래를 치를 중립 지대를 어딘가 찾아낸 다음, 그 물건들을 프랑스 해안에 부려 보려는 것이었다."], ["If the venture was successful the profit would be enormous, there would be a gain of fifty or sixty piastres each for the crew.", "만약 이 모험이 성공한다면 이익은 어마어마할 것이었고, 선원 한 사람당 쉰에서 예순 피아스터의 벌이가 될 터였다."]], [["The patron of _La Jeune Amélie_ proposed as a place of landing the Island of Monte Cristo, which being completely deserted, and having neither soldiers nor revenue officers, seemed to have been placed in the midst of the ocean since the time of the heathen Olympus by Mercury, the god of merchants and robbers, classes of mankind which we in modern times have separated if not made distinct, but which antiquity appears to have included in the same category.", "라죈아멜리호의 선장은 상륙지로 몬테크리스토섬을 제안했다. 완전히 인적 없는 섬으로, 병사도 세관원도 없으니, 이교도의 올림포스 시절부터 상인과 도둑의 신 메르쿠리우스가 망망대해 한가운데에 떡하니 놓아둔 것만 같은 곳이었다. 상인과 도둑이라는 두 부류는 오늘날 우리가 구별까지는 아니어도 갈라놓기는 했으나, 고대에는 같은 범주에 넣었던 모양이다."]], [["At the mention of Monte Cristo Dantès started with joy; he rose to conceal his emotion, and took a turn around the smoky tavern, where all the languages of the known world were jumbled in a _lingua franca_.", "몬테크리스토라는 이름이 나오자 당테스는 기쁨에 흠칫했다. 그는 감정을 감추려 일어서서, 알려진 세상의 온갖 언어가 공통어로 뒤섞이는 그 연기 자욱한 선술집을 한 바퀴 돌았다."]], [["When he again joined the two persons who had been discussing the matter, it had been decided that they should touch at Monte Cristo and set out on the following night.", "그가 그 일을 의논하던 두 사람에게 다시 합류했을 때는, 몬테크리스토섬에 들렀다가 이튿날 밤에 출발하기로 결정이 나 있었다."], ["Edmond, being consulted, was of opinion that the island afforded every possible security, and that great enterprises to be well done should be done quickly.", "의견을 묻자 에드몽은, 그 섬이 더할 나위 없이 안전하며, 큰일을 제대로 해내려면 빠르게 해치워야 한다고 말했다."]], [["Nothing then was altered in the plan, and orders were given to get under weigh next night, and, wind and weather permitting, to make the neutral island by the following day.", "그리하여 계획은 조금도 바뀌지 않았고, 이튿날 밤에 닻을 올려, 바람과 날씨가 허락한다면 그다음 날에는 그 중립의 섬에 닿으라는 명령이 내려졌다."]], [["# Chapter 23. The Island of Monte Cristo", "# Chapter 23. The Island of Monte Cristo"]], [["0289m\nThus, at length, by one of the unexpected strokes of fortune which sometimes befall those who have for a long time been the victims of an evil destiny, Dantès was about to secure the opportunity he wished for, by simple and natural means, and land on the island without incurring any suspicion.", "이리하여 마침내, 오랜 세월 악운의 희생자로 살아온 사람들에게 가끔 찾아드는 뜻밖의 행운 가운데 하나로, 당테스는 그토록 바라던 기회를 손에 넣게 되었다. 단순하고 자연스러운 방법으로, 누구의 의심도 사지 않고 섬에 발을 디딜 수 있게 된 것이다."], ["One night more and he would be on his way.", "하룻밤만 더 지나면 그는 떠날 수 있었다."]], [["The night was one of feverish distraction, and in its progress visions, good and evil, passed through Dantès’ mind.", "그 밤은 열병처럼 어수선한 밤이었고, 시간이 흐르는 동안 좋고 나쁜 온갖 환영이 당테스의 머릿속을 스쳐 갔다."], ["If he closed his eyes, he saw Cardinal Spada’s letter written on the wall in characters of flame—if he slept for a moment the wildest dreams haunted his brain.", "눈을 감으면 스파다 추기경의 편지가 불꽃 같은 글자로 벽에 적혀 있는 것이 보였다. 잠깐이라도 잠이 들면 더없이 기괴한 꿈이 그의 머리를 휘저었다."], ["He ascended into grottos paved with emeralds, with panels of rubies, and the roof glowing with diamond stalactites.", "그는 에메랄드로 포장되고 벽판은 루비로 이루어졌으며 천장은 다이아몬드 종유석으로 빛나는 동굴 속으로 올라갔다."], ["Pearls fell drop by drop, as subterranean waters filter in their caves.", "지하수가 동굴에서 스며 나오듯, 진주가 한 방울 한 방울 떨어졌다."], ["Edmond, amazed, wonderstruck, filled his pockets with the radiant gems and then returned to daylight, when he discovered that his prizes had all changed into common pebbles.", "에드몽은 놀라움에 넋을 잃은 채 주머니마다 그 찬란한 보석을 가득 채우고 다시 햇빛 아래로 돌아왔는데, 그제야 자신이 거머쥔 것들이 모두 평범한 조약돌로 변해 버린 것을 알았다."], ["He then endeavored to re-enter the marvellous grottos, but they had suddenly receded, and now the path became a labyrinth, and then the entrance vanished, and in vain did he tax his memory for the magic and mysterious word which opened the splendid caverns of Ali Baba to the Arabian fisherman.", "그는 그 경이로운 동굴로 다시 들어가려 했지만, 동굴은 어느새 멀찍이 물러나 있었고, 이제 길은 미궁이 되었으며, 그러다 입구마저 사라져 버렸다. 그는 아라비아의 어부 앞에서 알리바바의 찬란한 동굴을 열어 준 그 마법의 신비로운 주문을 떠올리려고 헛되이 기억을 쥐어짰다."], ["All was useless, the treasure disappeared, and had again reverted to the genii from whom for a moment he had hoped to carry it off.", "모두 소용없었다. 보물은 사라졌고, 잠시나마 그가 가로채려 했던 그 정령들에게 다시 돌아가 버렸다."]], [["The day came at length, and was almost as feverish as the night had been, but it brought reason to the aid of imagination, and Dantès was then enabled to arrange a plan which had hitherto been vague and unsettled in his brain.", "마침내 날이 밝았고, 낮 역시 밤만큼이나 열에 들떠 있었지만, 그래도 그 낮은 상상력에 이성을 보태 주었다. 덕분에 당테스는 그때까지 머릿속에서 막연하고 어수선했던 계획을 정리할 수 있었다."], ["Night came, and with it the preparation for departure, and these preparations served to conceal Dantès’ agitation.", "밤이 왔고, 그와 함께 출항 준비가 시작되었으며, 그 준비는 당테스의 동요를 감추는 데 도움이 되었다."], ["He had by degrees assumed such authority over his companions that he was almost like a commander on board; and as his orders were always clear, distinct, and easy of execution, his comrades obeyed him with celerity and pleasure.", "그는 어느새 동료들에게 상당한 권위를 행사하게 되어, 배 위에서 거의 지휘관이나 다름없었다. 그의 명령은 언제나 분명하고 또렷하며 이행하기 쉬웠으므로, 동료들은 기꺼이 신속하게 그에게 복종했다."]], [["The old patron did not interfere, for he too had recognized the superiority of Dantès over the crew and himself.", "늙은 선장은 끼어들지 않았다. 그 역시 당테스가 선원들과 자신보다 한 수 위라는 것을 인정하고 있었던 것이다."], ["He saw in the young man his natural successor, and regretted that he had not a daughter, that he might have bound Edmond to him by a more secure alliance.", "그는 이 젊은이에게서 자신의 당연한 후계자를 보았고, 자신에게 딸이 없어 더 든든한 인연으로 에드몽을 붙잡아 두지 못하는 것을 아쉬워했다."], ["At seven o’clock in the evening all was ready, and at ten minutes past seven they doubled the lighthouse just as the beacon was kindled.", "저녁 일곱 시에 모든 준비가 끝났고, 일곱 시 십 분에 등대의 불빛이 막 켜지는 순간 그들은 등대를 돌아 나갔다."], ["The sea was calm, and, with a fresh breeze from the south-east, they sailed beneath a bright blue sky, in which God also lighted up in turn his beacon lights, each of which is a world.", "바다는 잔잔했고, 남동쪽에서 불어오는 산뜻한 바람을 받으며, 그들은 맑고 푸른 하늘 아래로 나아갔다. 그 하늘에도 하느님이 차례차례 등불을 밝혀 두셨으니, 그 하나하나가 곧 하나의 세계였다."], ["Dantès told them that all hands might turn in, and he would take the helm.", "당테스는 모두 들어가 쉬어도 좋다고, 자신이 키를 잡겠다고 말했다."], ["When the Maltese (for so they called Dantès) had said this, it was sufficient, and all went to their bunks contentedly.", "몰타 사람이(사람들은 당테스를 그렇게 불렀다) 그렇게 말하면, 그것으로 충분했다. 모두 흡족한 마음으로 침상으로 돌아갔다."]], [["This frequently happened.", "이런 일은 자주 있었다."], ["Dantès, cast from solitude into the world, frequently experienced an imperious desire for solitude; and what solitude is more complete, or more poetical, than that of a ship floating in isolation on the sea during the obscurity of the night, in the silence of immensity, and under the eye of Heaven?", "고독에서 세상으로 내던져진 당테스는, 종종 고독을 향한 거역할 수 없는 갈망을 느꼈다. 밤의 어둠 속, 광막함의 침묵 속, 하늘의 눈길 아래, 바다 위에 외따로 떠 있는 배의 고독보다 더 완전하고 더 시적인 고독이 어디 있겠는가?"]], [["Now this solitude was peopled with his thoughts, the night lighted up by his illusions, and the silence animated by his anticipations.", "이제 이 고독은 그의 상념으로 가득 찼고, 밤은 그의 환상으로 환해졌으며, 침묵은 그의 기대로 생기를 띠었다."], ["When the patron awoke, the vessel was hurrying on with every sail set, and every sail full with the breeze.", "선장이 잠에서 깼을 때, 배는 돛을 모두 펼친 채, 그 돛마다 바람을 가득 머금고 질주하고 있었다."], ["They were making nearly ten knots an hour.", "시속 거의 십 노트로 나아가고 있었다."], ["The Island of Monte Cristo loomed large in the horizon.", "몬테크리스토섬이 수평선 위로 크게 떠올랐다."], ["Edmond resigned the lugger to the master’s care, and went and lay down in his hammock; but, in spite of a sleepless night, he could not close his eyes for a moment.", "에드몽은 범선을 선장에게 맡기고 자신의 해먹에 가서 누웠지만, 한숨도 못 잔 밤을 보냈으면서도 잠시도 눈을 붙일 수 없었다."]], [["Two hours afterwards he came on deck, as the boat was about to double the Island of Elba.", "두 시간 뒤 그는 갑판으로 올라왔다. 배가 막 엘바섬을 돌아 나가려는 참이었다."], ["They were just abreast of Mareciana, and beyond the flat but verdant Island of La Pianosa.", "그들은 바로 마르치아나 앞에, 평평하지만 푸르른 라피아노사섬 너머에 있었다."], ["The peak of Monte Cristo reddened by the burning sun, was seen against the azure sky.", "이글거리는 태양에 붉게 물든 몬테크리스토의 봉우리가 짙푸른 하늘을 배경으로 솟아 있었다."], ["Dantès ordered the helmsman to put down his helm, in order to leave La Pianosa to starboard, as he knew that he should shorten his course by two or three knots.", "당테스는 키잡이에게 라피아노사를 우현으로 두도록 키를 내리라고 명했다. 그렇게 하면 항로를 이삼 노트 줄일 수 있다는 것을 알았던 것이다."], ["About five o’clock in the evening the island was distinct, and everything on it was plainly perceptible, owing to that clearness of the atmosphere peculiar to the light which the rays of the sun cast at its setting.", "저녁 다섯 시쯤 되자 섬이 또렷이 보였고, 해질녘 햇살이 빚어내는 특유의 청명한 공기 덕에 섬 위의 모든 것이 선명하게 드러났다."]], [["Edmond gazed very earnestly at the mass of rocks which gave out all the variety of twilight colors, from the brightest pink to the deepest blue; and from time to time his cheeks flushed, his brow darkened, and a mist passed over his eyes.", "에드몽은 가장 밝은 분홍빛부터 가장 짙은 푸른빛까지, 황혼의 온갖 빛깔을 뿜어내는 그 바위 더미를 몹시 진지하게 바라보았다. 때때로 그의 두 뺨이 달아올랐고, 이마가 어두워졌으며, 두 눈에 안개가 서렸다."], ["Never did a gamester, whose whole fortune is staked on one cast of the die, experience the anguish which Edmond felt in his paroxysms of hope.", "주사위 한 번에 전 재산을 거는 노름꾼도, 희망의 발작 속에서 에드몽이 느낀 그 고통만큼은 결코 겪어 보지 못했으리라."]], [["Night came, and at ten o’clock they anchored.", "밤이 왔고, 열 시에 그들은 닻을 내렸다."], ["_La Jeune Amélie_ was first at the rendezvous.", "라죈아멜리호가 약속 장소에 가장 먼저 도착했다."], ["In spite of his usual command over himself, Dantès could not restrain his impetuosity.", "평소 누구보다 자제력이 강한 당테스였지만, 이번에는 자신의 격정을 억누를 수 없었다."], ["He was the first to jump on shore; and had he dared, he would, like Lucius Brutus, have “kissed his mother earth.”", "그는 가장 먼저 뭍으로 뛰어내렸다. 감히 그럴 수 있었다면, 그는 루키우스 브루투스처럼 \"어머니 대지에 입을 맞추었을\" 것이다."], ["It was dark, but at eleven o’clock the moon rose in the midst of the ocean, whose every wave she silvered, and then, “ascending high,”", "어두웠지만, 열한 시가 되자 바다 한가운데서 달이 떠올라 물결마다 은빛으로 물들였고, 그러다 \"높이 솟아오르며\""], ["played in floods of pale light on the rocky hills of this second Pelion.", "이 또 하나의 펠리온산의 바위 언덕 위로 창백한 빛을 쏟아부었다."]], [["The island was familiar to the crew of _La Jeune Amélie_,—it was one of her regular haunts.", "이 섬은 라죈아멜리호의 선원들에게 낯익은 곳이었다. 그들이 늘 드나드는 곳 가운데 하나였던 것이다."], ["As to Dantès, he had passed it on his voyage to and from the Levant, but never touched at it.", "당테스로 말하자면, 레반트를 오가는 항해 때 이 섬을 지나친 적은 있어도 발을 디딘 적은 한 번도 없었다."], ["He questioned Jacopo.", "그는 야코포에게 물었다."]], [["“Where shall we pass the night?”", "\"오늘 밤은 어디서 지내나?\""], ["he inquired.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“Why, on board the tartan,”", "\"그야 배 위에서 지내지요.\""], ["replied the sailor.", "선원이 대답했다."]], [["“Should we not do better in the grottos?”", "\"동굴에서 자는 게 낫지 않겠나?\""]], [["“What grottos?”", "\"무슨 동굴이요?\""]], [["“Why, the grottos—caves of the island.”", "\"그야 동굴 말일세, 이 섬의 동굴들.\""]], [["“I do not know of any grottos,”", "\"동굴 같은 건 모르겠는데요.\""], ["replied Jacopo.", "야코포가 대답했다."]], [["The cold sweat sprang forth on Dantès’ brow.", "당테스의 이마에 식은땀이 솟았다."]], [["“What, are there no grottos at Monte Cristo?”", "\"뭐라고, 몬테크리스토에 동굴이 없단 말인가?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“None.”", "\"없습니다.\""]], [["For a moment Dantès was speechless; then he remembered that these caves might have been filled up by some accident, or even stopped up, for the sake of greater security, by Cardinal Spada.", "당테스는 잠시 말을 잃었다. 그러다, 어쩌면 그 동굴들이 무슨 사고로 메워졌거나, 아니면 더 안전하게 하려고 스파다 추기경이 일부러 막아 버렸을지도 모른다는 생각이 떠올랐다."], ["The point was, then, to discover the hidden entrance.", "그렇다면 문제는 숨겨진 입구를 찾아내는 것이었다."], ["It was useless to search at night, and Dantès therefore delayed all investigation until the morning.", "밤에 뒤지는 것은 소용없는 일이라, 당테스는 모든 탐색을 아침으로 미루었다."], ["Besides, a signal made half a league out at sea, and to which _La Jeune Amélie_ replied by a similar signal, indicated that the moment for business had come.", "게다가 바다 쪽 반 리외 떨어진 곳에서 신호가 올라왔고, 라죈아멜리호가 같은 신호로 응답한 것을 보면, 거래의 순간이 다가왔다는 뜻이었다."]], [["The boat that now arrived, assured by the answering signal that all was well, soon came in sight, white and silent as a phantom, and cast anchor within a cable’s length of shore.", "이제 다가오는 배는, 응답 신호로 모든 일이 잘 풀렸음을 확인한 뒤, 유령처럼 희고 고요하게 곧 모습을 드러냈고, 해안에서 밧줄 길이만큼 떨어진 곳에 닻을 내렸다."]], [["Then the landing began.", "그러고는 짐 부리기가 시작되었다."], ["Dantès reflected, as he worked, on the shout of joy which, with a single word, he could evoke from all these men, if he gave utterance to the one unchanging thought that pervaded his heart; but, far from disclosing this precious secret, he almost feared that he had already said too much, and by his restlessness and continual questions, his minute observations and evident preoccupation, aroused suspicions.", "당테스는 일을 하면서 생각했다. 가슴속에 한결같이 자리 잡은 그 한 가지 생각을 입 밖에 내기만 하면, 단 한마디로 이 모든 사내들에게서 환호성을 끌어낼 수 있으리라고. 그러나 그 귀한 비밀을 털어놓기는커녕, 그는 자신이 이미 너무 많은 말을 흘린 것은 아닌지, 안절부절못하는 모습과 끊임없는 질문, 자질구레한 관찰과 뻔히 드러나는 골몰함으로 의심을 산 것은 아닌지 거의 두려워했다."], ["Fortunately, as regarded this circumstance at least, his painful past gave to his countenance an indelible sadness, and the glimmerings of gayety seen beneath this cloud were indeed but transitory.", "다행히, 적어도 이 점에서는, 그의 고통스러운 과거가 그의 얼굴에 지울 수 없는 슬픔을 새겨 두었고, 그 구름 아래로 언뜻언뜻 비치는 즐거움의 빛은 정말이지 한순간의 것에 지나지 않았다."]], [["No one had the slightest suspicion; and when next day, taking a fowling-piece, powder, and shot, Dantès declared his intention to go and kill some of the wild goats that were seen springing from rock to rock, his wish was construed into a love of sport, or a desire for solitude.", "아무도 조금도 의심하지 않았다. 이튿날 당테스가 엽총과 화약과 탄환을 챙기며 바위에서 바위로 뛰어다니는 야생 염소 몇 마리를 잡으러 가겠다고 했을 때, 사람들은 그저 사냥을 좋아하거나 혼자 있고 싶어 하는 것쯤으로 여겼다."], ["However, Jacopo insisted on following him, and Dantès did not oppose this, fearing if he did so that he might incur distrust.", "그래도 야코포가 따라가겠다고 우겼고, 당테스는 거절하면 불신을 살까 두려워 막지 않았다."], ["Scarcely, however, had they gone a quarter of a league when, having killed a kid, he begged Jacopo to take it to his comrades, and request them to cook it, and when ready to let him know by firing a gun.", "그러나 사반 리외도 채 못 갔을 무렵, 그는 새끼 염소 한 마리를 잡고서 야코포에게 그것을 동료들에게 가져다주고 요리를 부탁해 달라고, 그리고 다 되면 총을 한 발 쏘아 알려 달라고 청했다."], ["This and some dried fruits and a flask of Monte Pulciano, was the bill of fare.", "이 염소 고기에 말린 과일 몇 알과 몬테풀치아노 한 병이 그날의 차림표였다."]], [["Dantès went on, looking from time to time behind and around about him.", "당테스는 이따금 등 뒤와 사방을 살피며 계속 나아갔다."], ["Having reached the summit of a rock, he saw, a thousand feet beneath him, his companions, whom Jacopo had rejoined, and who were all busy preparing the repast which Edmond’s skill as a marksman had augmented with a capital dish.", "어느 바위 꼭대기에 이르자, 천 피트 아래로 동료들이 보였다. 야코포가 합류한 그들은 모두 식사를 준비하느라 분주했는데, 명사수 에드몽의 솜씨 덕에 그 식탁에 근사한 한 접시가 더해진 참이었다."]], [["Edmond looked at them for a moment with the sad and gentle smile of a man superior to his fellows.", "에드몽은 동료들보다 한 수 위인 사람 특유의 슬프고도 부드러운 미소를 띤 채 잠시 그들을 바라보았다."]], [["“In two hours’ time,”", "\"두 시간만 지나면,\""], ["said he, “these persons will depart richer by fifty piastres each, to go and risk their lives again by endeavoring to gain fifty more; then they will return with a fortune of six hundred francs, and waste this treasure in some city with the pride of sultans and the insolence of nabobs.", "그가 말했다. \"저 사람들은 각자 오십 피아스터씩 더 부자가 되어 떠나, 다시 오십을 더 벌어 보겠다고 목숨을 걸겠지. 그러고는 육백 프랑쯤 되는 재산을 안고 돌아와, 그 보물을 어느 도시에서 술탄 같은 거만함과 졸부 같은 오만함으로 탕진해 버릴 테고."], ["At this moment hope makes me despise their riches, which seem to me contemptible.", "지금 이 순간, 희망이 나로 하여금 저들의 재물을 하찮게 여기게 하니, 그것이 내게는 보잘것없어 보이는구나."], ["Yet perchance tomorrow deception will so act on me, that I shall, on compulsion, consider such a contemptible possession as the utmost happiness. Oh, no!”", "하지만 어쩌면 내일이면 환멸이 나를 휘저어, 마지못해 그토록 하찮은 재물을 더없는 행복으로 여기게 될지도 모르지. 아, 아니야!\""], ["exclaimed Edmond, “that will not be.", "에드몽이 외쳤다. \"그럴 리는 없어."], ["The wise, unerring Faria could not be mistaken in this one thing.", "지혜롭고 한 치의 오차도 없던 파리아 신부님이 이 한 가지만은 틀렸을 리 없어."], ["Besides, it were better to die than to continue to lead this low and wretched life.”", "게다가 이 비천하고 가련한 삶을 계속하느니 차라리 죽는 편이 나아.\""]], [["Thus Dantès, who but three months before had no desire but liberty had now not liberty enough, and panted for wealth.", "이렇게 석 달 전만 해도 자유 말고는 아무 바람도 없던 당테스는, 이제 자유만으로는 부족해 부를 갈망하고 있었다."], ["The cause was not in Dantès, but in Providence, who, while limiting the power of man, has filled him with boundless desires.", "그 원인은 당테스에게 있는 것이 아니라, 인간의 힘은 제한하면서도 그 안에 끝없는 욕망을 가득 채워 넣으신 섭리에 있었다."]], [["Meanwhile, by a cleft between two walls of rock, following a path worn by a torrent, and which, in all human probability, human foot had never before trod, Dantès approached the spot where he supposed the grottos must have existed.", "그러는 동안, 두 바위 벽 사이의 갈라진 틈을 따라, 급류가 깎아 놓은 길을 따라, 사람의 발이라면 십중팔구 한 번도 밟은 적 없었을 그 길을 따라, 당테스는 동굴이 있으리라 짐작한 지점으로 다가갔다."], ["Keeping along the shore, and examining the smallest object with serious attention, he thought he could trace, on certain rocks, marks made by the hand of man.", "해안을 따라가며 아무리 작은 것이라도 진지하게 살피던 그는, 어떤 바위에서 사람 손으로 새긴 자국을 알아볼 수 있을 것 같았다."]], [["Time, which encrusts all physical substances with its mossy mantle, as it invests all things of the mind with forgetfulness, seemed to have respected these signs, which apparently had been made with some degree of regularity, and probably with a definite purpose.", "모든 물질에 이끼 외투를 씌우고 모든 정신의 일에 망각을 입히는 세월도, 이 표식만큼은 손대지 않은 듯했다. 그것은 어느 정도 규칙적으로, 아마도 분명한 목적을 가지고 새겨진 것이 분명했다."], ["Occasionally the marks were hidden under tufts of myrtle, which spread into large bushes laden with blossoms, or beneath parasitical lichen.", "때때로 그 자국은 꽃을 가득 단 커다란 덤불로 퍼져 나간 도금양 무더기 아래, 혹은 기생 지의류 아래 숨어 있었다."], ["So Edmond had to separate the branches or brush away the moss to know where the guide-marks were.", "그래서 에드몽은 길잡이 표식이 어디 있는지 알아보려고 가지를 헤치거나 이끼를 쓸어내야 했다."], ["The sight of marks renewed Edmond’s fondest hopes.", "그 표식을 보자 에드몽의 더없이 간절한 희망이 되살아났다."], ["Might it not have been the cardinal himself who had first traced them, in order that they might serve as a guide for his nephew in the event of a catastrophe, which he could not foresee would have been so complete.", "어쩌면 추기경 자신이 가장 먼저 이 자국을 새긴 것은 아닐까. 자신이 미처 그토록 완전하리라고는 내다보지 못했을 재앙이 닥쳤을 때, 조카에게 길잡이가 되도록 말이다."], ["This solitary place was precisely suited to the requirements of a man desirous of burying treasure.", "이 외딴곳은 보물을 묻고자 하는 사람의 요건에 그야말로 안성맞춤이었다."], ["Only, might not these betraying marks have attracted other eyes than those for whom they were made?", "다만, 정작 그 표식이 본디 새겨진 이가 아닌 다른 눈을 끌어들이지는 않았을까?"], ["and had the dark and wondrous island indeed faithfully guarded its precious secret? 0295m", "그리고 이 어둡고 경이로운 섬은 정말로 그 귀한 비밀을 충실히 지켜 냈을까?"]], [["It seemed, however, to Edmond, who was hidden from his comrades by the inequalities of the ground, that at sixty paces from the harbor the marks ceased; nor did they terminate at any grotto.", "그러나 땅의 굴곡에 가려 동료들의 눈에서 벗어나 있던 에드몽이 보기에, 항구에서 예순 걸음쯤 떨어진 지점에서 표식이 끊겼다. 게다가 그것은 어떤 동굴에서 끝나는 것도 아니었다."], ["A large round rock, placed solidly on its base, was the only spot to which they seemed to lead.", "큼직하고 둥근 바위 하나가 밑동을 단단히 박고 자리 잡고 있었는데, 표식이 향하는 곳이라곤 오직 그곳뿐인 듯했다."], ["Edmond concluded that perhaps instead of having reached the end of the route he had only explored its beginning, and he therefore turned round and retraced his steps.", "에드몽은, 어쩌면 자신이 길의 끝에 다다른 것이 아니라 그 시작만 더듬은 것일지도 모른다고 결론짓고, 몸을 돌려 왔던 길을 되짚어 갔다."]], [["Meanwhile his comrades had prepared the repast, had got some water from a spring, spread out the fruit and bread, and cooked the kid.", "그동안 동료들은 식사를 준비해 두었다. 샘에서 물을 길어 오고, 과일과 빵을 펼쳐 놓고, 염소 고기를 구웠다."], ["Just at the moment when they were taking the dainty animal from the spit, they saw Edmond springing with the boldness of a chamois from rock to rock, and they fired the signal agreed upon.", "마침 꼬챙이에서 그 먹음직한 짐승을 막 빼내려는 순간, 그들은 에드몽이 영양처럼 대담하게 바위에서 바위로 뛰어다니는 것을 보고는, 약속한 신호로 총을 한 발 쏘았다."], ["The sportsman instantly changed his direction, and ran quickly towards them.", "사냥꾼은 즉시 방향을 바꾸어 그들을 향해 재빨리 달려왔다."], ["But even while they watched his daring progress, Edmond’s foot slipped, and they saw him stagger on the edge of a rock and disappear.", "그러나 그의 대담한 발걸음을 지켜보던 바로 그때, 에드몽의 발이 미끄러졌고, 그들은 그가 바위 끝에서 비틀거리다 사라지는 것을 보았다."], ["They all rushed towards him, for all loved Edmond in spite of his superiority; yet Jacopo reached him first.", "모두 그에게로 달려갔다. 한 수 위인 그를 다들 사랑했던 것이다. 그래도 야코포가 가장 먼저 그에게 다다랐다."]], [["He found Edmond lying prone, bleeding, and almost senseless.", "야코포는 에드몽이 엎어진 채 피를 흘리며 거의 정신을 잃은 것을 발견했다."], ["He had rolled down a declivity of twelve or fifteen feet.", "그는 열두어 피트 비탈을 굴러떨어진 것이었다."], ["They poured a little rum down his throat, and this remedy which had before been so beneficial to him, produced the same effect as formerly.", "그들이 그의 목구멍에 럼주를 조금 흘려 넣자, 전에도 그에게 그토록 효험을 보였던 이 처방이 이번에도 똑같은 효과를 냈다."], ["Edmond opened his eyes, complained of great pain in his knee, a feeling of heaviness in his head, and severe pains in his loins.", "에드몽이 눈을 뜨더니, 무릎이 몹시 아프고 머리가 무겁고 허리가 심하게 결린다고 호소했다."], ["They wished to carry him to the shore; but when they touched him, although under Jacopo’s directions, he declared, with heavy groans, that he could not bear to be moved.", "그들은 그를 해안으로 옮기려 했지만, 야코포의 지시에 따라 손을 댔는데도, 그는 무겁게 신음하며 도저히 움직일 수가 없다고 했다."]], [["It may be supposed that Dantès did not now think of his dinner, but he insisted that his comrades, who had not his reasons for fasting, should have their meal.", "당테스가 지금 저녁밥 생각을 했을 리는 없을 것이다. 그러나 그는 자신처럼 굶을 까닭이 없는 동료들에게는 식사를 하라고 우겼다."], ["As for himself, he declared that he had only need of a little rest, and that when they returned he should be easier.", "자신으로 말하자면, 잠깐 쉬기만 하면 된다고, 그들이 돌아올 즈음이면 한결 나아져 있을 거라고 했다."], ["The sailors did not require much urging.", "선원들은 굳이 권할 것도 없었다."], ["They were hungry, and the smell of the roasted kid was very savory, and your tars are not very ceremonious.", "다들 배가 고팠고, 구운 염소 고기 냄새가 무척 구수했으며, 뱃사람들이란 격식 같은 건 그다지 차리지 않는 법이다."], ["An hour afterwards they returned.", "한 시간 뒤 그들이 돌아왔다."], ["All that Edmond had been able to do was to drag himself about a dozen paces forward to lean against a moss-grown rock.", "에드몽이 할 수 있었던 일이라곤 이끼 낀 바위에 기대려고 열 걸음쯤 몸을 끌고 앞으로 나아간 것뿐이었다."]], [["But, instead of growing easier, Dantès’ pains appeared to increase in violence.", "그러나 당테스의 통증은 가라앉기는커녕 점점 더 심해지는 듯했다."], ["The old patron, who was obliged to sail in the morning in order to land his cargo on the frontiers of Piedmont and France, between Nice and Fréjus, urged Dantès to try and rise.", "아침에 니스와 프레쥐스 사이, 피에몬테와 프랑스 국경에 화물을 내리려면 출항해야 했던 늙은 선장은, 당테스에게 일어나 보라고 다그쳤다."], ["Edmond made great exertions in order to comply; but at each effort he fell back, moaning and turning pale.", "에드몽은 따르려고 무진 애를 썼다. 그러나 애쓸 때마다 신음하며 새파랗게 질려 다시 주저앉았다."]], [["“He has broken his ribs,”", "\"갈비뼈가 부러졌군.\""], ["said the commander, in a low voice.", "선장이 낮은 목소리로 말했다."], ["“No matter; he is an excellent fellow, and we must not leave him.", "\"상관없어. 좋은 친구니, 두고 갈 수는 없지."], ["We will try and carry him on board the tartan.”", "어떻게든 배로 옮겨 보세.\""]], [["Dantès declared, however, that he would rather die where he was than undergo the agony which the slightest movement cost him.", "그러나 당테스는, 조금만 움직여도 겪는 그 고통을 견디느니 차라리 이 자리에서 죽는 편이 낫다고 했다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"좋네.\""], ["said the patron, “let what may happen, it shall never be said that we deserted a good comrade like you.", "선장이 말했다. \"무슨 일이 있어도, 자네 같은 좋은 동료를 버리고 갔다는 소리는 결코 듣지 않을 걸세."], ["We will not go till evening.”", "저녁까지는 떠나지 않겠네.\""]], [["This very much astonished the sailors, although, not one opposed it.", "이 말에 선원들은 무척 놀랐지만, 누구 하나 반대하지는 않았다."], ["The patron was so strict that this was the first time they had ever seen him give up an enterprise, or even delay in its execution.", "선장이 워낙 엄격한 사람이라, 그가 일을 포기하거나 그 실행을 미루는 것을 보는 건 이번이 처음이었던 것이다."], ["Dantès would not allow that any such infraction of regular and proper rules should be made in his favor.", "당테스는 자기 때문에 정당하고 정해진 규칙을 그렇게 어기는 일은 결코 허락할 수 없다고 했다."]], [["“No, no,” he said to the patron, “I was awkward, and it is just that I pay the penalty of my clumsiness.", "\"아니요, 아닙니다.\" 그가 선장에게 말했다."], ["Leave me a small supply of biscuit, a gun, powder, and balls, to kill the kids or defend myself at need, and a pickaxe, that I may build a shelter if you delay in coming back for me.”", "\"제가 서툴렀으니, 그 서투름의 대가를 치르는 것이 마땅하지요. 비스킷이나 조금 남겨 주십시오. 그리고 염소를 잡거나 만일에 대비해 제 몸을 지킬 총과 화약, 탄알, 그리고 저를 데리러 오시는 게 늦어지면 거처라도 지을 수 있게 곡괭이 한 자루만 주십시오.\""]], [["“But you’ll die of hunger,”", "\"하지만 굶어 죽고 말 텐데.\""], ["said the patron.", "선장이 말했다."]], [["“I would rather do so,”", "\"그래도 그편이 낫습니다.\""], ["was Edmond’s reply, “than suffer the inexpressible agonies which the slightest movement causes me.”", "에드몽이 대답했다. \"조금만 움직여도 겪는 그 말로 다 할 수 없는 고통을 견디느니 말입니다.\""]], [["The patron turned towards his vessel, which was rolling on the swell in the little harbor, and, with sails partly set, would be ready for sea when her toilet should be completed.", "선장은 작은 항구의 너울에 흔들리는 자기 배 쪽으로 고개를 돌렸다. 돛을 반쯤 펼친 그 배는, 채비를 마치기만 하면 곧 바다로 나설 참이었다."]], [["“What are we to do, Maltese?”", "\"어떻게 해야겠나, 몰타 친구?\""], ["asked the captain. “We cannot leave you here so, and yet we cannot stay.”", "선장이 물었다. \"이렇게 자네를 여기 두고 갈 수도 없고, 그렇다고 머무를 수도 없으니.\""]], [["“Go, go!” exclaimed Dantès.", "\"가십시오, 가세요!\" 당테스가 외쳤다."]], [["“We shall be absent at least a week,”", "\"적어도 일주일은 걸릴 거야.\""], ["said the patron, “and then we must run out of our course to come here and take you up again.”", "선장이 말했다. \"그러고는 또 항로를 벗어나 여기까지 와서 자네를 데려가야 하고.\""]], [["“Why,” said Dantès, “if in two or three days you hail any fishing-boat, desire them to come here to me.", "\"이러면 어떻겠습니까.\" 당테스가 말했다."], ["I will pay twenty-five piastres for my passage back to Leghorn.", "\"이삼 일 안에 고기잡이배라도 만나거든, 저에게 와 달라고 일러 주십시오. 리보르노까지 데려다주면 뱃삯으로 이십오 피아스터를 내겠습니다."], ["If you do not come across one, return for me.”", "그런 배를 못 만나거든, 그때 저를 데리러 오시면 됩니다.\""], ["The patron shook his head.", "선장은 고개를 저었다."]], [["“Listen, Captain Baldi; there’s one way of settling this,”", "\"발디 선장님, 들어 보세요. 이걸 해결할 방법이 하나 있습니다.\""], ["said Jacopo.", "야코포가 말했다."], ["“Do you go, and I will stay and take care of the wounded man.”", "\"선장님은 떠나시고, 제가 남아 다친 사람을 돌보겠습니다.\""]], [["“And give up your share of the venture,”", "\"이번 일의 자기 몫까지 포기하고,\""], ["said Edmond, “to remain with me?”", "에드몽이 말했다. \"나와 함께 남겠다고?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said Jacopo, “and without any hesitation.”", "야코포가 말했다. \"조금도 망설이지 않고요.\""]], [["“You are a good fellow and a kind-hearted messmate,”", "\"자넨 참 좋은 사람이고 마음씨 고운 동료야.\""], ["replied Edmond, “and heaven will recompense you for your generous intentions; but I do not wish anyone to stay with me.", "에드몽이 대답했다. \"자네의 그 너그러운 마음을 하늘이 갚아 주실 걸세. 하지만 나는 누구도 함께 남는 걸 바라지 않아."], ["A day or two of rest will set me up, and I hope I shall find among the rocks certain herbs most excellent for bruises.”", "하루 이틀 쉬면 나아질 테고, 이 바위들 사이에서 타박상에 더없이 잘 듣는 약초도 좀 찾을 수 있겠지.\""]], [["A peculiar smile passed over Dantès’ lips; he squeezed Jacopo’s hand warmly, but nothing could shake his determination to remain—and remain alone.", "당테스의 입가에 묘한 미소가 스쳤다. 그는 야코포의 손을 따뜻하게 꼭 쥐었지만, 남겠다는, 그것도 혼자 남겠다는 결심만은 무엇으로도 흔들 수 없었다."]], [["The smugglers left with Edmond what he had requested and set sail, but not without turning about several times, and each time making signs of a cordial farewell, to which Edmond replied with his hand only, as if he could not move the rest of his body.", "밀수꾼들은 에드몽이 청한 것들을 그의 곁에 두고 돛을 올렸다. 그러면서도 여러 번 뒤를 돌아보았고, 그때마다 다정한 작별의 손짓을 보냈다. 에드몽은 마치 나머지 몸은 꼼짝할 수 없다는 듯, 오직 손만 들어 그 인사에 답했다."]], [["Then, when they had disappeared, he said with a smile,—“’Tis strange that it should be among such men that we find proofs of friendship and devotion.”", "이윽고 그들이 시야에서 사라지자, 그는 미소 지으며 말했다. \"참으로 이상한 일이군. 우정과 헌신의 증거를 바로 저런 사람들한테서 찾게 되다니.\""], ["Then he dragged himself cautiously to the top of a rock, from which he had a full view of the sea, and thence he saw the tartan complete her preparations for sailing, weigh anchor, and, balancing herself as gracefully as a water-fowl ere it takes to the wing, set sail.", "그러고는 조심스레 몸을 끌고 바위 꼭대기로 올라갔다. 거기서는 바다가 훤히 내다보였는데, 그곳에서 그는 그 배가 출항 준비를 마치고 닻을 올린 뒤, 날아오르기 직전의 물새처럼 우아하게 몸을 가누고 돛을 펼치는 것을 보았다."]], [["At the end of an hour she was completely out of sight; at least, it was impossible for the wounded man to see her any longer from the spot where he was.", "한 시간이 지나자 배는 완전히 시야에서 사라졌다. 적어도 다친 사람이 있던 그 자리에서는 더 이상 배를 볼 수 없었다."], ["Then Dantès rose more agile and light than the kid among the myrtles and shrubs of these wild rocks, took his gun in one hand, his pickaxe in the other, and hastened towards the rock on which the marks he had noted terminated.", "그러자 당테스는 이 거친 바위들의 도금양과 관목 사이의 새끼 염소보다도 더 날쌔고 가뿐하게 몸을 일으켜, 한 손에 총을, 다른 한 손에 곡괭이를 쥐고, 자신이 눈여겨본 표식이 끝나던 그 바위를 향해 서둘러 갔다."]], [["“And now,”", "\"자, 이제.\""], ["he exclaimed, remembering the tale of the Arabian fisherman, which Faria had related to him, “now, Open Sesame!”", "그가 파리아 신부에게서 들은 아라비아 어부 이야기를 떠올리며 외쳤다. \"자, 열려라 참깨!\""]], [["# Chapter 24. The Secret Cave", "# Chapter 24. The Secret Cave"]], [["The sun had nearly reached the meridian, and his scorching rays fell full on the rocks, which seemed themselves sensible of the heat.", "해는 거의 중천에 이르렀고, 타는 듯한 햇살이 바위들 위로 가득 쏟아졌다. 바위들마저 그 열기를 느끼는 듯했다."], ["Thousands of grasshoppers, hidden in the bushes, chirped with a monotonous and dull note; the leaves of the myrtle and olive trees waved and rustled in the wind.", "덤불 속에 숨은 수천 마리 메뚜기가 단조롭고 둔탁한 소리로 울어 댔다. 미르테와 올리브나무 잎들이 바람결에 흔들리며 바스락거렸다."], ["At every step that Edmond took he disturbed the lizards glittering with the hues of the emerald; afar off he saw the wild goats bounding from crag to crag.", "에드몽은 한 걸음 옮길 때마다 에메랄드빛으로 반짝이는 도마뱀들을 놀라게 했다. 저 멀리서는 야생 염소들이 바위에서 바위로 뛰어다니는 것이 보였다."], ["In a word, the island was inhabited, yet Edmond felt himself alone, guided by the hand of God.", "한마디로 그 섬에는 생명이 깃들어 있었다. 그런데도 에드몽은 신의 손에 이끌리는, 홀로 남겨진 자신을 느꼈다."]], [["He felt an indescribable sensation somewhat akin to dread—that dread of the daylight which even in the desert makes us fear we are watched and observed.", "그는 형언할 수 없는 감각에 사로잡혔다. 그것은 일종의 두려움, 사막 한가운데에서조차 누군가가 지켜보고 감시한다는 불안을 일으키는 그 대낮의 두려움과 비슷한 것이었다."], ["This feeling was so strong that at the moment when Edmond was about to begin his labor, he stopped, laid down his pickaxe, seized his gun, mounted to the summit of the highest rock, and from thence gazed round in every direction.", "그 느낌이 어찌나 강했던지, 에드몽은 막 작업을 시작하려던 참에 멈추어 곡괭이를 내려놓고 총을 집어 든 뒤, 가장 높은 바위 꼭대기로 올라가 사방을 둘러보았다."]], [["But it was not upon Corsica, the very houses of which he could distinguish; or on Sardinia; or on the Island of Elba, with its historical associations; or upon the almost imperceptible line that to the experienced eye of a sailor alone revealed the coast of Genoa the proud, and Leghorn the commercial, that he gazed.", "그러나 그가 바라본 것은 집들까지 분간할 수 있는 코르시카섬도, 사르데냐섬도, 역사적 사연이 깃든 엘바섬도 아니었다. 노련한 선원의 눈에만 보일 듯 말 듯한, 도도한 제노바와 상업의 도시 레그혼의 해안을 드러내는 그 희미한 선도 아니었다."], ["It was at the brigantine that had left in the morning, and the tartan that had just set sail, that Edmond fixed his eyes.", "에드몽이 눈길을 박은 것은 아침에 떠난 쌍돛 범선과 방금 닻을 올린 타르탄선이었다."]], [["The first was just disappearing in the straits of Bonifacio; the other, following an opposite direction, was about to round the Island of Corsica.", "앞엣것은 막 보니파시오 해협 속으로 사라지는 참이었고, 뒤엣것은 반대 방향으로 나아가 코르시카섬을 돌아가려는 참이었다."]], [["This sight reassured him.", "이 광경에 그는 안심했다."], ["He then looked at the objects near him.", "그런 다음 가까이 있는 것들을 살펴보았다."], ["He saw that he was on the highest point of the island,—a statue on this vast pedestal of granite, nothing human appearing in sight, while the blue ocean beat against the base of the island, and covered it with a fringe of foam.", "자신이 섬에서 가장 높은 지점에, 마치 이 거대한 화강암 받침대 위에 놓인 조각상처럼 서 있다는 것을 알았다. 사람이라곤 그림자도 보이지 않았고, 그저 푸른 바다가 섬의 밑동에 부딪치며 하얀 거품의 띠로 그것을 둘렀다."], ["Then he descended with cautious and slow step, for he dreaded lest an accident similar to that he had so adroitly feigned should happen in reality.", "그러고는 조심스럽고 느린 걸음으로 내려왔다. 그렇게 능숙하게 꾸며 냈던 사고가 정말로 일어날까 봐 두려웠기 때문이다."]], [["Dantès, as we have said, had traced the marks along the rocks, and he had noticed that they led to a small creek, which was hidden like the bath of some ancient nymph.", "앞서 말했듯이 당테스는 바위를 따라 새겨진 표시들을 더듬어 갔고, 그것들이 어느 옛 요정의 욕탕처럼 숨겨진 작은 후미로 이어진다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["This creek was sufficiently wide at its mouth, and deep in the centre, to admit of the entrance of a small vessel of the lugger class, which would be perfectly concealed from observation.", "이 후미는 어귀가 제법 넓고 한가운데가 깊어, 러거형 작은 배 한 척쯤은 들어와 누구의 눈에도 띄지 않게 완벽히 숨길 수 있을 만했다."]], [["Then following the clew that, in the hands of the Abbé Faria, had been so skilfully used to guide him through the Dædalian labyrinth of probabilities, he thought that the Cardinal Spada, anxious not to be watched, had entered the creek, concealed his little barque, followed the line marked by the notches in the rock, and at the end of it had buried his treasure.", "그런 뒤 파리아 신부의 손에서 그토록 능란하게 다루어져, 갖가지 추측이 얽힌 미궁을 헤쳐 나가도록 그를 인도해 준 그 실마리를 따라가며, 그는 이렇게 생각했다. 스파다 추기경은 감시당하지 않으려고 이 후미로 들어와 작은 배를 숨기고, 바위에 새긴 홈이 표시한 길을 따라가, 그 끝에 보물을 묻었으리라고."], ["It was this idea that had brought Dantès back to the circular rock.", "바로 이 생각이 당테스를 둥근 바위로 다시 데려온 것이었다."], ["One thing only perplexed Edmond, and destroyed his theory.", "다만 한 가지가 에드몽을 당혹스럽게 하며 그의 추론을 무너뜨렸다."], ["How could this rock, which weighed several tons, have been lifted to this spot, without the aid of many men?", "몇 톤이나 나가는 이 바위가, 어떻게 여러 사람의 힘 없이 이 자리까지 들어 올려질 수 있었단 말인가?"]], [["Suddenly an idea flashed across his mind.", "문득 한 가지 생각이 머릿속을 스쳤다."], ["Instead of raising it, thought he, they have lowered it.", "'들어 올린 게 아니라, 끌어 내린 거야.'"], ["And he sprang from the rock in order to inspect the base on which it had formerly stood.", "그는 이렇게 생각하며, 바위가 예전에 놓여 있던 밑동을 살펴보려고 바위에서 뛰어내렸다."]], [["He soon perceived that a slope had been formed, and the rock had slid along this until it stopped at the spot it now occupied.", "곧 그는 비탈이 만들어져 있었고, 바위가 그 비탈을 따라 미끄러져 지금의 자리에 멈추었다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["A large stone had served as a wedge; flints and pebbles had been inserted around it, so as to conceal the orifice; this species of masonry had been covered with earth, and grass and weeds had grown there, moss had clung to the stones, myrtle-bushes had taken root, and the old rock seemed fixed to the earth. 0301m", "큰 돌멩이 하나가 쐐기 노릇을 했고, 그 둘레에 부싯돌과 자갈을 끼워 넣어 입구를 가렸다. 이렇게 돌을 쌓은 위에는 흙을 덮었고, 거기에 풀과 잡초가 자랐으며, 돌에는 이끼가 들러붙고 미르테 덤불이 뿌리를 내려, 그 오래된 바위는 마치 땅에 단단히 박힌 것처럼 보였다."]], [["Dantès dug away the earth carefully, and detected, or fancied he detected, the ingenious artifice.", "당테스는 조심스럽게 흙을 파헤쳤고, 그 기발한 술책을 알아챘다, 아니 알아챘다고 여겼다."], ["He attacked this wall, cemented by the hand of time, with his pickaxe.", "그는 세월의 손길로 굳어진 이 벽을 곡괭이로 공격했다."], ["After ten minutes’ labor the wall gave way, and a hole large enough to insert the arm was opened.", "십 분쯤 작업하자 벽이 무너지며, 팔을 넣을 만한 구멍이 뚫렸다."]], [["Dantès went and cut the strongest olive-tree he could find, stripped off its branches, inserted it in the hole, and used it as a lever.", "당테스는 가서 찾을 수 있는 가장 단단한 올리브나무를 잘라 가지를 쳐 낸 뒤, 그것을 구멍에 끼워 지렛대로 썼다."], ["But the rock was too heavy, and too firmly wedged, to be moved by anyone man, were he Hercules himself.", "그러나 바위는 너무 무겁고 너무 단단히 박혀 있어, 설령 헤라클레스라 해도 혼자서는 움직일 수 없을 정도였다."], ["Dantès saw that he must attack the wedge. But how?", "당테스는 쐐기를 공략해야 한다는 것을 깨달았다. 그런데 어떻게?"]], [["He cast his eyes around, and saw the horn full of powder which his friend Jacopo had left him.", "그는 주위를 둘러보다가, 친구 자코포가 남겨 준 화약이 가득 든 뿔통을 보았다."], ["He smiled; the infernal invention would serve him for this purpose.", "그는 미소 지었다. 그 흉악한 발명품이 이번 목적에 쓸모가 있을 터였다."]], [["With the aid of his pickaxe, Dantès, after the manner of a labor-saving pioneer, dug a mine between the upper rock and the one that supported it, filled it with powder, then made a match by rolling his handkerchief in saltpetre.", "당테스는 곡괭이를 써서, 일손을 더는 데 능한 개척자처럼 위쪽 바위와 그것을 받친 바위 사이에 갱도를 파고는 화약을 채웠다. 그런 다음 손수건을 초석에 굴려 도화선을 만들었다."], ["He lighted it and retired.", "거기에 불을 붙이고 물러섰다."]], [["The explosion soon followed; the upper rock was lifted from its base by the terrific force of the powder; the lower one flew into pieces; thousands of insects escaped from the aperture Dantès had previously formed, and a huge snake, like the guardian demon of the treasure, rolled himself along in darkening coils, and disappeared.", "곧 폭발이 뒤따랐다. 화약의 무시무시한 힘에 위쪽 바위가 밑동에서 들렸고, 아래쪽 바위는 산산조각이 났다. 앞서 당테스가 뚫어 놓은 틈에서 수천 마리 벌레가 쏟아져 나왔고, 보물을 지키는 수호 악마 같은 커다란 뱀 한 마리가 시커먼 똬리를 풀며 기어가 사라졌다."]], [["Dantès approached the upper rock, which now, without any support, leaned towards the sea.", "당테스는 위쪽 바위로 다가갔다. 이제 받쳐 줄 것이 없어진 그 바위는 바다 쪽으로 기울어 있었다."], ["The intrepid treasure-seeker walked round it, and, selecting the spot from whence it appeared most susceptible to attack, placed his lever in one of the crevices, and strained every nerve to move the mass.", "겁 없는 보물 사냥꾼은 바위를 빙 돌며, 가장 쉽게 공략할 만한 지점을 골라 그 틈새 하나에 지렛대를 끼우고는, 온 신경을 곤두세워 그 덩어리를 움직이려 했다."]], [["The rock, already shaken by the explosion, tottered on its base.", "폭발로 이미 흔들린 바위가 밑동에서 휘청거렸다."], ["Dantès redoubled his efforts; he seemed like one of the ancient Titans, who uprooted the mountains to hurl against the father of the gods.", "당테스는 더욱 힘을 쏟았다. 그는 신들의 아버지를 향해 던지려고 산을 통째로 뽑아 든 옛 티탄족 같았다."], ["The rock yielded, rolled over, bounded from point to point, and finally disappeared in the ocean.", "바위는 굴복했고, 굴러떨어지며 이 지점 저 지점을 튕기다가, 마침내 바닷속으로 사라졌다."]], [["On the spot it had occupied was a circular space, exposing an iron ring let into a square flag-stone.", "바위가 차지하고 있던 자리에는 둥근 공간이 드러났고, 네모난 판석에 박힌 쇠고리가 그 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["Dantès uttered a cry of joy and surprise; never had a first attempt been crowned with more perfect success.", "당테스는 기쁨과 놀라움의 탄성을 질렀다. 첫 시도가 이보다 더 완벽한 성공으로 마무리된 적은 없었다."], ["He would fain have continued, but his knees trembled, and his heart beat so violently, and his sight became so dim, that he was forced to pause.", "그는 계속하고 싶었지만, 무릎이 떨리고 심장이 어찌나 거칠게 뛰던지, 눈앞이 흐려져 잠시 멈출 수밖에 없었다."]], [["This feeling lasted but for a moment.", "그 느낌은 잠깐뿐이었다."], ["Edmond inserted his lever in the ring and exerted all his strength; the flag-stone yielded, and disclosed steps that descended until they were lost in the obscurity of a subterraneous grotto.", "에드몽은 고리에 지렛대를 끼우고 온 힘을 다했다. 판석이 들리며, 지하 동굴의 어둠 속으로 사라지듯 이어지는 계단이 드러났다."]], [["Anyone else would have rushed on with a cry of joy.", "다른 사람 같으면 기쁨의 함성을 지르며 달려 내려갔을 것이다."], ["Dantès turned pale, hesitated, and reflected.", "당테스는 새파랗게 질려 머뭇거리며 곰곰이 생각했다."]], [["“Come,”", "\"자,\""], ["said he to himself, “be a man.", "그는 혼잣말을 했다. \"사내답게 굴자."], ["I am accustomed to adversity.", "나는 역경에 익숙한 사람이야."], ["I must not be cast down by the discovery that I have been deceived. What, then, would be the use of all I have suffered?", "속았다는 걸 알게 됐다고 해서 무너져선 안 돼. 그렇다면 내가 견뎌 온 그 모든 고생이 무슨 소용이란 말인가?"], ["The heart breaks when, after having been elated by flattering hopes, it sees all its illusions destroyed.", "달콤한 희망에 부풀었다가 그 모든 환상이 무너지는 걸 보게 되면, 가슴이 찢어지는 법이지. 파리아가 꿈을 꾼 거야."], ["Faria has dreamed this; the Cardinal Spada buried no treasure here; perhaps he never came here, or if he did, Cæsar Borgia, the intrepid adventurer, the stealthy and indefatigable plunderer, has followed him, discovered his traces, pursued them as I have done, raised the stone, and descending before me, has left me nothing.”", "스파다 추기경은 여기에 아무 보물도 묻지 않았어. 어쩌면 여기 온 적조차 없을지도 몰라. 설령 왔다 해도, 겁 없는 모험가이자 은밀하고 지칠 줄 모르는 약탈자 체사레 보르자가 그를 뒤따라와 그 흔적을 찾아내, 나처럼 그것을 쫓아 돌을 들어 올리고는, 나보다 먼저 내려가 내게 아무것도 남기지 않았을 수도 있어.\""]], [["He remained motionless and pensive, his eyes fixed on the gloomy aperture that was open at his feet.", "그는 발치에 입을 벌린 음울한 구멍에 눈을 박은 채, 움직임 없이 생각에 잠겨 있었다."]], [["“Now that I expect nothing, now that I no longer entertain the slightest hopes, the end of this adventure becomes simply a matter of curiosity.”", "\"이제 아무것도 기대하지 않으니, 더는 손톱만큼의 희망도 품지 않으니, 이 모험의 끝은 그저 호기심의 문제일 뿐이야.\""], ["And he remained again motionless and thoughtful.", "그리고 그는 다시 움직임 없이 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["“Yes, yes; this is an adventure worthy a place in the varied career of that royal bandit.", "\"그래, 그래. 이건 그 왕족 출신 도적의 파란만장한 생애에 한자리를 차지할 만한 모험이지."], ["This fabulous event formed but a link in a long chain of marvels.", "이 전설 같은 사건도 길게 이어진 경이의 사슬에서 한 고리에 지나지 않았던 거야. 그래, 보르자가 여기 왔었어."], ["Yes, Borgia has been here, a torch in one hand, a sword in the other, and within twenty paces, at the foot of this rock, perhaps two guards kept watch on land and sea, while their master descended, as I am about to descend, dispelling the darkness before his awe-inspiring progress.” 0303m", "한 손엔 횃불을, 다른 손엔 칼을 들고서. 그리고 스무 걸음 떨어진 이 바위 밑에는, 어쩌면 보초 둘이 뭍과 바다를 지켜보았겠지. 그동안 그들의 주인은, 내가 지금 막 내려가려는 것처럼, 위풍당당한 발걸음 앞의 어둠을 몰아내며 내려갔을 거야.\""]], [["“But what was the fate of the guards who thus possessed his secret?”", "\"그런데 그렇게 그의 비밀을 알게 된 보초들의 운명은 어찌 되었을까?\""], ["asked Dantès of himself.", "당테스는 스스로에게 물었다."]], [["“The fate,”", "\"운명이라,\""], ["replied he, smiling, “of those who buried Alaric, and were interred with the corpse.”", "그는 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"알라리크를 묻은 자들이 그 시신과 함께 묻혔듯 되었겠지.\""]], [["“Yet, had he come,”", "'하지만 그가 정말 왔었다면,'"], ["thought Dantès, “he would have found the treasure, and Borgia, he who compared Italy to an artichoke, which he could devour leaf by leaf, knew too well the value of time to waste it in replacing this rock.", "당테스는 생각했다. '보물을 찾아냈겠지. 이탈리아를 잎사귀 하나하나 따 먹을 수 있는 아티초크에 비유했던 그 보르자라면, 시간의 값어치를 너무도 잘 알았을 테니 이 바위를 도로 갖다 놓느라 허비하진 않았을 거야."], ["I will go down.”", "내려가 보자.'"]], [["Then he descended, a smile on his lips, and murmuring that last word of human philosophy, “Perhaps!”", "그러고는 입가에 미소를 띤 채, 인간 철학의 그 마지막 말을 중얼거리며 내려갔다. \"어쩌면!\""]], [["But instead of the darkness, and the thick and mephitic atmosphere he had expected to find, Dantès saw a dim and bluish light, which, as well as the air, entered, not merely by the aperture he had just formed, but by the interstices and crevices of the rock which were visible from without, and through which he could distinguish the blue sky and the waving branches of the evergreen oaks, and the tendrils of the creepers that grew from the rocks.", "그러나 당테스가 마주하리라 예상했던 어둠과 짙고 탁한 공기 대신, 흐릿한 푸르스름한 빛이 보였다. 그 빛은 공기와 마찬가지로, 방금 그가 뚫어 놓은 구멍으로만이 아니라 밖에서도 보이던 바위의 틈새와 갈라진 곳들을 통해 들어왔다. 그 틈으로 그는 푸른 하늘과 흔들리는 상록 떡갈나무 가지들, 그리고 바위에서 자라난 덩굴손들을 분간할 수 있었다."]], [["After having stood a few minutes in the cavern, the atmosphere of which was rather warm than damp, Dantès’ eye, habituated as it was to darkness, could pierce even to the remotest angles of the cavern, which was of granite that sparkled like diamonds.", "축축하다기보다는 따뜻한 동굴 안에 몇 분쯤 서 있자, 어둠에 익숙해진 당테스의 눈은 다이아몬드처럼 반짝이는 화강암으로 된 동굴의 가장 외진 구석까지도 꿰뚫어 볼 수 있었다."]], [["“Alas,”", "\"아아,\""], ["said Edmond, smiling, “these are the treasures the cardinal has left; and the good abbé, seeing in a dream these glittering walls, has indulged in fallacious hopes.”", "에드몽이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"추기경이 남긴 보물이란 게 이거였군. 그리고 그 선량한 신부는 꿈속에서 이 반짝이는 벽을 보고 헛된 희망을 품었던 거야.\""]], [["But he called to mind the words of the will, which he knew by heart.", "그러나 그는 외워 두었던 유언장의 글귀를 떠올렸다."], ["“In the farthest angle of the second opening,”", "'두 번째 입구의 가장 깊은 구석에.'"], ["said the cardinal’s will. He had only found the first grotto; he had now to seek the second.", "추기경의 유언장은 그렇게 적고 있었다. 그는 첫 번째 동굴만 찾아냈을 뿐이었다."], ["Dantès continued his search.", "이제 두 번째 것을 찾아야 했다."], ["He reflected that this second grotto must penetrate deeper into the island; he examined the stones, and sounded one part of the wall where he fancied the opening existed, masked for precaution’s sake.", "당테스는 탐색을 이어 갔다. 그는 이 두 번째 동굴이 섬 안쪽으로 더 깊이 파고들었으리라 생각했다. 그는 돌들을 살피고, 만일을 위해 가려 놓은 입구가 있을 법한 벽 한쪽을 두드려 보았다."]], [["The pickaxe struck for a moment with a dull sound that drew out of Dantès’ forehead large drops of perspiration.", "곡괭이가 잠시 둔탁한 소리를 내며 부딪쳤고, 당테스의 이마에서 굵은 땀방울이 솟았다."], ["At last it seemed to him that one part of the wall gave forth a more hollow and deeper echo; he eagerly advanced, and with the quickness of perception that no one but a prisoner possesses, saw that there, in all probability, the opening must be.", "마침내 벽 한 부분이 더 텅 비고 깊은 울림을 내는 것 같았다. 그는 다급히 다가가, 죄수만이 지닐 수 있는 예민한 지각으로, 십중팔구 그곳에 입구가 있으리라는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["However, he, like Cæsar Borgia, knew the value of time; and, in order to avoid fruitless toil, he sounded all the other walls with his pickaxe, struck the earth with the butt of his gun, and finding nothing that appeared suspicious, returned to that part of the wall whence issued the consoling sound he had before heard.", "그러나 그 역시 체사레 보르자처럼 시간의 값어치를 알았다. 그래서 헛수고를 피하려고, 곡괭이로 다른 벽들을 모두 두드려 보고 총 개머리판으로 바닥을 쳐 보았다. 수상쩍은 데가 아무것도 없자, 그는 앞서 위안이 되는 소리를 들었던 그 벽 부분으로 돌아왔다."]], [["He again struck it, and with greater force.", "그는 다시, 더 큰 힘으로 그곳을 쳤다."], ["Then a singular thing occurred.", "그러자 기이한 일이 벌어졌다."], ["As he struck the wall, pieces of stucco similar to that used in the ground work of arabesques broke off, and fell to the ground in flakes, exposing a large white stone.", "그가 벽을 치자, 아라베스크 무늬의 바탕에 쓰는 것과 비슷한 회반죽 조각들이 떨어져 나와 부스러기째 바닥으로 떨어지며, 커다란 흰 돌이 드러났다."], ["The aperture of the rock had been closed with stones, then this stucco had been applied, and painted to imitate granite.", "바위의 입구를 돌로 막은 뒤, 그 위에 이 회반죽을 발라 화강암처럼 보이게 칠해 놓았던 것이다."], ["Dantès struck with the sharp end of his pickaxe, which entered someway between the interstices.", "당테스가 곡괭이의 뾰족한 끝으로 치자, 그것이 틈새 사이로 어느 정도 파고들었다."]], [["It was there he must dig.", "바로 거기를 파야 했다."]], [["But by some strange play of emotion, in proportion as the proofs that Faria, had not been deceived became stronger, so did his heart give way, and a feeling of discouragement stole over him.", "그러나 묘하게도 감정이 엇갈려, 파리아가 속은 것이 아니라는 증거가 점점 더 분명해질수록 그의 마음은 도리어 무너져 갔고, 낙담의 기운이 그를 휘감았다. 이 마지막 증거는 그에게 새로운 힘을 주기는커녕 오히려 힘을 앗아 갔다."], ["This last proof, instead of giving him fresh strength, deprived him of it; the pickaxe descended, or rather fell; he placed it on the ground, passed his hand over his brow, and remounted the stairs, alleging to himself, as an excuse, a desire to be assured that no one was watching him, but in reality because he felt that he was about to faint.", "곡괭이가 내려갔다, 아니 떨어졌다. 그는 그것을 바닥에 내려놓고 손으로 이마를 쓸어내린 뒤 계단을 다시 올라갔다. 누가 자신을 지켜보고 있지나 않은지 확인하고 싶어서라고 스스로 핑계를 댔지만, 사실은 정신을 잃을 것만 같았기 때문이었다."]], [["The island was deserted, and the sun seemed to cover it with its fiery glance; afar off, a few small fishing boats studded the bosom of the blue ocean.", "섬은 인적이 없었고, 해는 불타는 눈길로 섬을 뒤덮은 듯했다. 저 멀리, 작은 고깃배 몇 척이 푸른 바다의 품을 점점이 수놓고 있었다."]], [["Dantès had tasted nothing, but he thought not of hunger at such a moment; he hastily swallowed a few drops of rum, and again entered the cavern.", "당테스는 아무것도 먹지 않은 상태였지만, 그런 순간에 허기 따위는 떠오르지 않았다. 그는 럼주 몇 방울을 황급히 들이켜고 다시 동굴로 들어갔다."]], [["The pickaxe that had seemed so heavy, was now like a feather in his grasp; he seized it, and attacked the wall.", "그토록 무겁게 느껴지던 곡괭이가 이제는 손안에서 깃털처럼 가벼웠다. 그는 그것을 움켜쥐고 벽을 공격했다."], ["After several blows he perceived that the stones were not cemented, but had been merely placed one upon the other, and covered with stucco; he inserted the point of his pickaxe, and using the handle as a lever, with joy soon saw the stone turn as if on hinges, and fall at his feet.", "몇 번 내려치고 나서, 그는 돌들이 회반죽으로 붙여진 것이 아니라 그저 하나하나 포개어 쌓은 뒤 회반죽으로 덮어 놓은 것임을 알아챘다. 그는 곡괭이 끝을 끼워 넣고 자루를 지렛대 삼았다. 그러자 곧, 돌이 마치 경첩에 달린 듯 돌아가 발치에 떨어지는 것을 기쁘게 지켜보았다."]], [["He had nothing more to do now, but with the iron tooth of the pickaxe to draw the stones towards him one by one.", "이제 그가 할 일은, 곡괭이의 쇠 이빨로 돌들을 하나씩 자기 쪽으로 끌어내는 것뿐이었다."], ["The aperture was already sufficiently large for him to enter, but by waiting, he could still cling to hope, and retard the certainty of deception.", "구멍은 이미 그가 들어갈 만큼 넓어졌지만, 좀 더 기다리면 여전히 희망에 매달릴 수 있었고, 환멸의 확정을 미룰 수 있었다."], ["At last, after renewed hesitation, Dantès entered the second grotto.", "마침내, 거듭 망설인 끝에 당테스는 두 번째 동굴로 들어갔다."]], [["The second grotto was lower and more gloomy than the first; the air that could only enter by the newly formed opening had the mephitic smell Dantès was surprised not to find in the outer cavern.", "두 번째 동굴은 첫 번째 것보다 낮고 더 음침했다. 새로 뚫은 입구로만 들어올 수 있는 공기에서는, 당테스가 바깥 동굴에서 맡지 못해 놀랐던 그 탁한 냄새가 났다."], ["He waited in order to allow pure air to displace the foul atmosphere, and then went on.", "그는 맑은 공기가 그 더러운 기운을 밀어낼 때까지 기다렸다가 안으로 나아갔다."]], [["At the left of the opening was a dark and deep angle.", "입구 왼쪽에는 어둡고 깊숙한 구석이 있었다."], ["But to Dantès’ eye there was no darkness.", "그러나 당테스의 눈에는 어둠이란 없었다."], ["He glanced around this second grotto; it was, like the first, empty.", "그는 이 두 번째 동굴을 둘러보았다. 그곳도 첫 번째와 마찬가지로 텅 비어 있었다."]], [["The treasure, if it existed, was buried in this corner.", "보물이 존재한다면, 이 구석에 묻혀 있을 터였다."], ["The time had at length arrived; two feet of earth removed, and Dantès’ fate would be decided.", "마침내 그때가 온 것이다. 흙 두 자만 걷어 내면 당테스의 운명이 결판날 것이었다."]], [["He advanced towards the angle, and summoning all his resolution, attacked the ground with the pickaxe.", "그는 그 구석으로 다가가, 온 결심을 그러모아 곡괭이로 바닥을 공략했다."], ["At the fifth or sixth blow the pickaxe struck against an iron substance.", "다섯 번째인가 여섯 번째 내려쳤을 때, 곡괭이가 무언가 쇠붙이에 부딪쳤다."], ["Never did funeral knell, never did alarm-bell, produce a greater effect on the hearer.", "어떤 장례 종소리도, 어떤 경종 소리도 듣는 이에게 이보다 더한 충격을 준 적은 없었다."], ["Had Dantès found nothing he could not have become more ghastly pale.", "차라리 아무것도 찾지 못했다 해도, 당테스가 이보다 더 송장처럼 새파래질 수는 없었을 것이다."]], [["He again struck his pickaxe into the earth, and encountered the same resistance, but not the same sound.", "그는 다시 곡괭이를 흙 속으로 내리쳤고, 같은 저항을 만났으나 소리는 같지 않았다."]], [["“It is a casket of wood bound with iron,” thought he.", "'쇠를 두른 나무 상자로군.' 그는 생각했다."]], [["At this moment a shadow passed rapidly before the opening; Dantès seized his gun, sprang through the opening, and mounted the stair.", "바로 그때, 입구 앞으로 그림자 하나가 빠르게 스쳐 갔다. 당테스는 총을 움켜쥐고 구멍을 빠져나가 계단을 올랐다."], ["A wild goat had passed before the mouth of the cave, and was feeding at a little distance.", "야생 염소 한 마리가 동굴 어귀 앞을 지나가더니, 조금 떨어진 곳에서 풀을 뜯고 있었다."], ["This would have been a favorable occasion to secure his dinner; but Dantès feared lest the report of his gun should attract attention.", "저녁거리를 확보하기에 더없이 좋은 기회였다. 그러나 당테스는 총소리가 누군가의 주의를 끌까 봐 두려웠다."]], [["He thought a moment, cut a branch of a resinous tree, lighted it at the fire at which the smugglers had prepared their breakfast, and descended with this torch.", "그는 잠시 생각하더니, 진이 흐르는 나무에서 가지 하나를 잘라, 밀수꾼들이 아침을 지어 먹던 불에 불을 붙이고는, 그 횃불을 들고 내려갔다."]], [["He wished to see everything.", "그는 모든 것을 보고 싶었다."], ["He approached the hole he had dug, and now, with the aid of the torch, saw that his pickaxe had in reality struck against iron and wood.", "그는 자신이 파 놓은 구덩이로 다가갔고, 이제 횃불의 도움으로, 곡괭이가 정말로 쇠와 나무에 부딪쳤음을 보았다."], ["He planted his torch in the ground and resumed his labor.", "그는 횃불을 바닥에 꽂고 작업을 다시 시작했다."]], [["In an instant a space three feet long by two feet broad was cleared, and Dantès could see an oaken coffer, bound with cut steel; in the middle of the lid he saw engraved on a silver plate, which was still untarnished, the arms of the Spada family—viz., a sword, _en pale_, on an oval shield, like all the Italian armorial bearings, and surmounted by a cardinal’s hat.", "순식간에 길이 석 자, 너비 두 자의 공간이 드러났고, 당테스는 잘라 낸 강철로 테를 두른 떡갈나무 궤를 볼 수 있었다. 뚜껑 한가운데에는, 아직 빛이 바래지 않은 은판에 스파다 가문의 문장이 새겨져 있었다. 다른 모든 이탈리아 문장처럼 타원형 방패 위에 세로로 놓인 칼 한 자루, 그 위에 추기경의 모자가 얹힌 모양이었다."]], [["Dantès easily recognized them, Faria had so often drawn them for him.", "당테스는 그것을 단번에 알아보았다. 파리아가 어찌나 자주 그려 주었던지."], ["There was no longer any doubt: the treasure was there—no one would have been at such pains to conceal an empty casket.", "이제 더는 의심할 여지가 없었다. 보물은 거기 있었다. 텅 빈 궤를 감추려고 이토록 공을 들였을 리는 없었다."], ["In an instant he had cleared every obstacle away, and he saw successively the lock, placed between two padlocks, and the two handles at each end, all carved as things were carved at that epoch, when art rendered the commonest metals precious.", "그는 순식간에 모든 장애물을 치웠고, 자물쇠 두 개 사이에 놓인 자물쇠 하나와 양쪽 끝의 손잡이 둘을 차례로 보았다. 그 모든 것은, 가장 흔한 금속마저 예술이 귀하게 빚어 내던 그 시대의 방식대로 조각되어 있었다."]], [["Dantès seized the handles, and strove to lift the coffer; it was impossible.", "당테스는 손잡이를 움켜쥐고 궤를 들어 올리려 했다. 불가능했다."], ["He sought to open it; lock and padlock were fastened; these faithful guardians seemed unwilling to surrender their trust.", "그는 그것을 열어 보려 했다. 자물쇠와 맹꽁이자물쇠가 단단히 채워져 있었다. 이 충직한 수호자들은 맡은 것을 내어 줄 마음이 없는 듯했다."], ["Dantès inserted the sharp end of the pickaxe between the coffer and the lid, and pressing with all his force on the handle, burst open the fastenings.", "당테스는 궤와 뚜껑 사이에 곡괭이의 뾰족한 끝을 끼우고, 자루에 온 힘을 실어 눌러 잠금쇠들을 터뜨려 열었다."], ["The hinges yielded in their turn and fell, still holding in their grasp fragments of the wood, and the chest was open. 0307m", "경첩들도 차례로 굴복해, 나뭇조각을 여전히 문 채로 떨어져 나갔다. 그렇게 궤가 열렸다."]], [["Edmond was seized with vertigo; he cocked his gun and laid it beside him.", "에드몽은 현기증에 사로잡혔다. 그는 총의 공이를 당겨 곁에 내려놓았다."], ["He then closed his eyes as children do in order that they may see in the resplendent night of their own imagination more stars than are visible in the firmament; then he re-opened them, and stood motionless with amazement.", "그런 다음, 아이들이 제 상상의 찬란한 밤하늘에서 실제 창공보다 더 많은 별을 보려고 그러듯 두 눈을 감았다. 그러고는 다시 눈을 뜨고, 놀라움에 옴짝달싹 못 한 채 서 있었다."]], [["Three compartments divided the coffer.", "궤는 세 칸으로 나뉘어 있었다."], ["In the first, blazed piles of golden coin; in the second, were ranged bars of unpolished gold, which possessed nothing attractive save their value; in the third, Edmond grasped handfuls of diamonds, pearls, and rubies, which, as they fell on one another, sounded like hail against glass.", "첫째 칸에는 금화 더미가 번쩍였다. 둘째 칸에는, 그 값어치 말고는 아무 매력도 없는, 다듬지 않은 금괴들이 줄지어 놓여 있었다. 셋째 칸에서 에드몽은 다이아몬드와 진주, 루비를 한 움큼씩 그러쥐었고, 그것들이 서로 부딪쳐 떨어지며 유리에 우박이 떨어지는 듯한 소리를 냈다."]], [["After having touched, felt, examined these treasures, Edmond rushed through the caverns like a man seized with frenzy; he leaped on a rock, from whence he could behold the sea.", "이 보물들을 만지고 더듬고 살피고 난 뒤, 에드몽은 광기에 사로잡힌 사람처럼 동굴을 가로질러 달려 나갔다. 그는 바다를 굽어볼 수 있는 바위 위로 뛰어올랐다."], ["He was alone—alone with these countless, these unheard-of treasures!", "그는 혼자였다. 이 헤아릴 수 없는, 전대미문의 보물과 단둘이 있었다!"], ["Was he awake, or was it but a dream?", "그는 깨어 있는 것인가, 아니면 한낱 꿈인가?"], ["Was it a transient vision, or was he face to face with reality?", "스쳐 가는 환영인가, 아니면 그가 현실과 얼굴을 맞대고 있는 것인가?"]], [["He would fain have gazed upon his gold, and yet he had not strength enough; for an instant he leaned his head in his hands as if to prevent his senses from leaving him, and then rushed madly about the rocks of Monte Cristo, terrifying the wild goats and scaring the sea-fowls with his wild cries and gestures; then he returned, and, still unable to believe the evidence of his senses, rushed into the grotto, and found himself before this mine of gold and jewels.", "그는 제 황금을 바라보고 싶었지만, 그럴 기운조차 없었다. 잠시 그는 정신이 달아나지 못하게 막으려는 듯 두 손에 머리를 묻었다가, 미친 듯이 몬테크리스토섬의 바위들을 헤집고 다니며, 거친 고함과 몸짓으로 야생 염소들을 겁주고 바닷새들을 놀라게 했다. 그러고는 돌아와, 여전히 제 감각이 전하는 증거를 믿지 못한 채 동굴 안으로 뛰어들어, 이 금과 보석의 광맥 앞에 다시 마주 섰다."]], [["This time he fell on his knees, and, clasping his hands convulsively, uttered a prayer intelligible to God alone.", "이번에는 그가 무릎을 꿇고 두 손을 발작하듯 맞잡은 채, 오직 신만이 알아들을 수 있는 기도를 올렸다."], ["He soon became calmer and more happy, for only now did he begin to realize his felicity.", "그는 곧 더 차분해지고 더 행복해졌다. 이제야 비로소 자신의 복락을 실감하기 시작했기 때문이다."]], [["He then set himself to work to count his fortune.", "그런 다음 그는 자기 재산을 헤아리는 일에 착수했다."], ["There were a thousand ingots of gold, each weighing from two to three pounds; then he piled up twenty-five thousand crowns, each worth about eighty francs of our money, and bearing the effigies of Alexander VI.", "금괴가 천 개 있었는데, 저마다 무게가 두세 파운드씩 나갔다. 다음으로 그는 이만 오천 닢의 크라운화를 쌓았는데, 우리 돈으로 한 닢에 팔십 프랑쯤 되는 것으로, 알렉산데르 6세와 그 선대들의 초상이 새겨져 있었다."], ["and his predecessors; and he saw that the complement was not half empty.", "그리고 그 양이 절반도 비지 않았음을 알았다."], ["And he measured ten double handfuls of pearls, diamonds, and other gems, many of which, mounted by the most famous workmen, were valuable beyond their intrinsic worth.", "또 그는 진주와 다이아몬드, 그 밖의 보석을 두 손 가득 열 번 그러쥐어 헤아렸는데, 그 가운데 상당수는 가장 이름난 세공사들이 박아 넣은 것이라 본래 값어치 이상으로 귀했다."]], [["Dantès saw the light gradually disappear, and fearing to be surprised in the cavern, left it, his gun in his hand.", "당테스는 빛이 차츰 사라지는 것을 보았고, 동굴 안에서 불시에 들킬까 두려워 총을 손에 든 채 그곳을 나섰다."], ["A piece of biscuit and a small quantity of rum formed his supper, and he snatched a few hours’ sleep, lying over the mouth of the cave.", "비스킷 한 조각과 럼주 약간이 그의 저녁이 되었고, 그는 동굴 어귀에 누워 몇 시간의 잠을 청했다."]], [["It was a night of joy and terror, such as this man of stupendous emotions had already experienced twice or thrice in his lifetime.", "그것은 기쁨과 공포의 밤이었다. 어마어마한 감정의 소유자인 이 사내가 평생에 두세 번쯤은 이미 겪어 본 그런 밤이었다."]], [["# Chapter 25. The Unknown", "# Chapter 25. The Unknown"]], [["Day, for which Dantès had so eagerly and impatiently waited with open eyes, again dawned.", "에드몽이 두 눈을 부릅뜬 채 그토록 애타게 기다리던 날이 다시 밝았다."], ["With the first light Dantès resumed his search.", "동이 트자마자 당테스는 다시 수색에 나섰다."], ["Again he climbed the rocky height he had ascended the previous evening, and strained his view to catch every peculiarity of the landscape; but it wore the same wild, barren aspect when seen by the rays of the morning sun which it had done when surveyed by the fading glimmer of eve.", "그는 전날 저녁에 올랐던 바위 봉우리를 또 한 번 기어올라, 풍경의 작은 특징 하나라도 놓치지 않으려 시야를 곤두세웠다. 그러나 아침 햇살 속에서 본 풍경은 저녁의 스러지는 빛 속에서 살폈을 때와 똑같이 거칠고 메마른 모습이었다."]], [["Descending into the grotto, he lifted the stone, filled his pockets with gems, put the box together as well and securely as he could, sprinkled fresh sand over the spot from which it had been taken, and then carefully trod down the earth to give it everywhere a uniform appearance; then, quitting the grotto, he replaced the stone, heaping on it broken masses of rocks and rough fragments of crumbling granite, filling the interstices with earth, into which he deftly inserted rapidly growing plants, such as the wild myrtle and flowering thorn, then carefully watering these new plantations, he scrupulously effaced every trace of footsteps, leaving the approach to the cavern as savage-looking and untrodden as he had found it.", "동굴 안으로 내려간 그는 돌을 들어 올리고 주머니마다 보석을 가득 채운 뒤, 상자를 최대한 단단하고 빈틈없이 다시 맞추어 놓았다. 상자가 있던 자리에는 새 모래를 뿌렸고, 흙을 꼼꼼히 다져 어디나 한결같은 모양이 되게 했다. 그런 다음 동굴을 빠져나와 돌을 제자리에 되돌려 놓고, 그 위에 부서진 바윗덩이며 부스러지는 화강암 조각들을 쌓아 올렸다."], ["This done, he impatiently awaited the return of his companions.", "틈새는 흙으로 메우고, 그 흙 속에 야생 도금양과 꽃핀 가시나무처럼 빨리 자라는 식물들을 솜씨 좋게 끼워 넣었다. 그러고는 이 새로 심은 것들에 정성껏 물을 주고, 발자국이라곤 하나도 남기지 않도록 모든 흔적을 세심히 지워, 동굴로 가는 길을 처음 찾았을 때처럼 거칠고 사람의 발길이 닿지 않은 모습으로 만들어 놓았다. 이 일을 마친 그는 일행이 돌아오기를 초조하게 기다렸다."], ["To wait at Monte Cristo for the purpose of watching like a dragon over the almost incalculable riches that had thus fallen into his possession satisfied not the cravings of his heart, which yearned to return to dwell among mankind, and to assume the rank, power, and influence which are always accorded to wealth—that first and greatest of all the forces within the grasp of man.", "자기 손에 굴러든 그 헤아릴 수도 없는 재물을 용처럼 지키며 몬테크리스토섬에 눌러앉아 있는 것은 그의 마음의 갈망을 채워 주지 못했다. 그의 마음은 다시 인간 세상으로 돌아가, 부에 늘 따라붙는 지위와 권력과 영향력을 누리기를 갈망했다. 부야말로 인간이 손에 쥘 수 있는 모든 힘 가운데 으뜸가는 가장 큰 힘이었다."]], [["On the sixth day, the smugglers returned.", "엿새째 되는 날, 밀수꾼들이 돌아왔다."], ["From a distance Dantès recognized the rig and handling of _La Jeune Amélie_, and dragging himself with affected difficulty towards the landing-place, he met his companions with an assurance that, although considerably better than when they quitted him, he still suffered acutely from his late accident.", "멀리서도 당테스는 죈아멜리호의 의장과 조종 방식을 알아보았다. 그는 짐짓 힘겨운 척 몸을 끌며 상륙 지점으로 나아갔고, 떠날 때보다는 한결 나아졌으나 지난번 사고로 아직도 몹시 고통스러운 척하며 일행을 맞이했다."], ["He then inquired how they had fared in their trip.", "이어 그는 항해가 어떠했는지 물었다."], ["To this question the smugglers replied that, although successful in landing their cargo in safety, they had scarcely done so when they received intelligence that a guard-ship had just quitted the port of Toulon and was crowding all sail towards them.", "이 물음에 밀수꾼들은 무사히 화물을 부리는 데까지는 성공했으나, 그러기가 무섭게 툴롱 항구를 막 떠난 경비선 한 척이 그들을 향해 돛을 한껏 펼치고 다가온다는 소식을 받았다고 대답했다."], ["This obliged them to make all the speed they could to evade the enemy, when they could but lament the absence of Dantès, whose superior skill in the management of a vessel would have availed them so materially.", "그래서 적을 따돌리려고 있는 힘껏 속도를 내야 했고, 그때만큼 당테스가 없는 것을 아쉬워한 적이 없었다고 했다. 배를 다루는 그의 뛰어난 솜씨가 있었다면 큰 도움이 되었을 거라는 것이었다."], ["In fact, the pursuing vessel had almost overtaken them when, fortunately, night came on, and enabled them to double the Cape of Corsica, and so elude all further pursuit.", "실제로 추격선이 거의 따라잡았을 무렵 다행히 밤이 내려, 그들은 코르시카곶을 돌아 더 이상의 추격을 따돌릴 수 있었다."], ["Upon the whole, however, the trip had been sufficiently successful to satisfy all concerned; while the crew, and particularly Jacopo, expressed great regrets that Dantès had not been an equal sharer with themselves in the profits, which amounted to no less a sum than fifty piastres each. 0311m", "그러나 전체적으로 보면 이번 항해는 관련된 모두를 만족시킬 만큼 충분히 성공적이었다. 선원들, 특히 자코포는 이번 수익을 당테스가 자기들과 똑같이 나눠 갖지 못한 것을 몹시 아쉬워했다. 그 수익은 한 사람당 무려 오십 피아스터에 이르렀다."]], [["Edmond preserved the most admirable self-command, not suffering the faintest indication of a smile to escape him at the enumeration of all the benefits he would have reaped had he been able to quit the island; but as _La Jeune Amélie_ had merely come to Monte Cristo to fetch him away, he embarked that same evening, and proceeded with the captain to Leghorn.", "에드몽은 더없이 훌륭하게 자신을 다스리며, 만약 섬을 떠날 수 있었다면 얻었을 온갖 이득을 늘어놓는 그 말에도 희미한 미소 한 자락 새어 나오게 하지 않았다. 그러나 죈아멜리호는 그저 그를 데려가려고 몬테크리스토섬에 들른 것이었으므로, 그는 바로 그날 저녁 배에 올라 선장과 함께 레그혼으로 향했다."]], [["Arrived at Leghorn, he repaired to the house of a Jew, a dealer in precious stones, to whom he disposed of four of his smallest diamonds for five thousand francs each.", "레그혼에 도착하자 그는 보석상을 하는 어느 유대인의 집을 찾아가, 가장 작은 다이아몬드 네 개를 개당 오천 프랑씩에 팔았다."], ["Dantès half feared that such valuable jewels in the hands of a poor sailor like himself might excite suspicion; but the cunning purchaser asked no troublesome questions concerning a bargain by which he gained a round profit of at least eighty per cent.", "당테스는 자기 같은 가난한 선원의 손에 그런 값진 보석이 들려 있으면 의심을 살까 봐 내심 두려워했다. 그러나 약삭빠른 매입자는 적어도 팔 할의 두둑한 이문을 남기는 거래를 두고, 성가신 질문 따위는 하나도 하지 않았다."]], [["The following day Dantès presented Jacopo with an entirely new vessel, accompanying the gift by a donation of one hundred piastres, that he might provide himself with a suitable crew and other requisites for his outfit, upon condition that he would go at once to Marseilles for the purpose of inquiring after an old man named Louis Dantès, residing in the Allées de Meilhan, and also a young woman called Mercédès, an inhabitant of the Catalan village.", "이튿날 당테스는 자코포에게 완전히 새 배 한 척을 선물하고, 거기에 백 피아스터를 얹어 주었다. 알맞은 선원들과 의장에 필요한 그 밖의 물품들을 갖추는 데 쓰라는 것이었다. 다만 곧장 마르세유로 가서, 메이앙 거리에 사는 루이 당테스라는 노인과 카탈루냐 마을에 사는 메르세데스라는 젊은 여인의 소식을 알아본다는 조건이었다."]], [["Jacopo could scarcely believe his senses at receiving this magnificent present, which Dantès hastened to account for by saying that he had merely been a sailor from whim and a desire to spite his family, who did not allow him as much money as he liked to spend; but that on his arrival at Leghorn he had come into possession of a large fortune, left him by an uncle, whose sole heir he was.", "자코포는 이 굉장한 선물을 받고도 제 감각을 좀처럼 믿지 못했다. 그러자 당테스는 서둘러 사연을 둘러댔다. 자기는 그저 변덕에서, 또 마음대로 쓸 만큼 돈을 주지 않던 가족에게 앙갚음하려고 선원 노릇을 했을 뿐이라고."], ["The superior education of Dantès gave an air of such extreme probability to this statement that it never once occurred to Jacopo to doubt its accuracy.", "그런데 레그혼에 도착하니 어느 삼촌이 남긴 큰 재산이 굴러들어 왔고, 자기가 그 유일한 상속자라는 것이었다. 당테스의 빼어난 학식이 이 이야기에 더없이 그럴듯한 빛을 입혀 주었으므로, 자코포는 그 진위를 의심할 생각조차 하지 않았다."]], [["The term for which Edmond had engaged to serve on board _La Jeune Amélie_ having expired, Dantès took leave of the captain, who at first tried all his powers of persuasion to induce him to remain as one of the crew, but having been told the history of the legacy, he ceased to importune him further.", "에드몽이 죈아멜리호에서 일하기로 한 기간이 다 차자, 당테스는 선장에게 작별을 고했다. 선장은 처음엔 온갖 말로 그를 구슬려 선원으로 남게 하려 했으나, 유산 이야기를 듣고는 더 이상 졸라 대지 않았다."]], [["The following morning Jacopo set sail for Marseilles, with directions from Dantès to join him at the Island of Monte Cristo.", "이튿날 아침 자코포는 마르세유를 향해 출항했다. 일을 마치면 몬테크리스토섬에서 합류하라는 당테스의 지시를 받은 채였다."]], [["Having seen Jacopo fairly out of the harbor, Dantès proceeded to make his final adieus on board _La Jeune Amélie_, distributing so liberal a gratuity among her crew as to secure for him the good wishes of all, and expressions of cordial interest in all that concerned him.", "자코포가 항구를 무사히 빠져나가는 것을 지켜본 뒤, 당테스는 죈아멜리호에 올라 마지막 작별 인사를 나누었다. 선원들에게 후한 사례금을 두루 나누어 주어 모두의 호의와 그에 관한 모든 일에 진심 어린 관심을 사 두었다."], ["To the captain he promised to write when he had made up his mind as to his future plans. Then Dantès departed for Genoa.", "선장에게는 앞으로의 계획이 정해지면 편지를 쓰겠노라 약속했다. 그런 다음 당테스는 제노바로 떠났다."]], [["At the moment of his arrival a small yacht was under trial in the bay; this yacht had been built by order of an Englishman, who, having heard that the Genoese excelled all other builders along the shores of the Mediterranean in the construction of fast-sailing vessels, was desirous of possessing a specimen of their skill; the price agreed upon between the Englishman and the Genoese builder was forty thousand francs.", "그가 도착한 바로 그 무렵, 만에서는 작은 요트 한 척이 시험 운항 중이었다. 이 요트는 어느 영국인의 주문으로 건조된 것이었다. 그는 제노바 사람들이 지중해 연안의 다른 어떤 조선공들보다 쾌속선 건조에 뛰어나다는 말을 듣고, 그 솜씨의 본보기 하나를 갖고 싶어 했다."], ["Dantès, struck with the beauty and capability of the little vessel, applied to its owner to transfer it to him, offering sixty thousand francs, upon condition that he should be allowed to take immediate possession.", "영국인과 제노바 조선공 사이에 합의된 값은 사만 프랑이었다. 그 작은 배의 아름다움과 성능에 마음을 빼앗긴 당테스는 그 배를 자기에게 넘겨 달라고 주인에게 청하며, 즉시 인도받는다는 조건으로 육만 프랑을 제안했다."], ["The proposal was too advantageous to be refused, the more so as the person for whom the yacht was intended had gone upon a tour through Switzerland, and was not expected back in less than three weeks or a month, by which time the builder reckoned upon being able to complete another.", "거절하기엔 너무도 유리한 제안이었다. 더구나 그 요트를 받기로 한 사람은 스위스 일주 여행을 떠나, 적어도 서너 주는 지나야 돌아올 터였고, 그동안 조선공은 다른 배 한 척을 완성할 수 있으리라 셈했다."], ["A bargain was therefore struck.", "그리하여 거래가 성사되었다."], ["Dantès led the owner of the yacht to the dwelling of a Jew; retired with the latter for a few minutes to a small back parlor, and upon their return the Jew counted out to the shipbuilder the sum of sixty thousand francs in bright gold pieces.", "당테스는 요트 주인을 어느 유대인의 집으로 데려가, 그와 함께 몇 분간 작은 뒷방으로 물러나 있었다. 두 사람이 돌아오자 유대인은 조선공에게 반짝이는 금화로 육만 프랑을 세어 주었다."]], [["The delighted builder then offered his services in providing a suitable crew for the little vessel, but this Dantès declined with many thanks, saying he was accustomed to cruise about quite alone, and his principal pleasure consisted in managing his yacht himself; the only thing the builder could oblige him in would be to contrive a sort of secret closet in the cabin at his bed’s head, the closet to contain three divisions, so constructed as to be concealed from all but himself.", "기뻐하던 조선공은 그 작은 배에 알맞은 선원들을 구해 주겠노라 자청했으나, 당테스는 거듭 고마움을 표하며 그 제안을 사양했다. 자기는 늘 혼자서 항해하는 데 익숙하고, 가장 큰 즐거움이 바로 직접 요트를 다루는 데 있다는 것이었다. 다만 조선공이 그를 도와줄 수 있는 한 가지는, 선실 침대 머리맡에 일종의 비밀 벽장을 만들어 주는 것이었다."], ["The builder cheerfully undertook the commission, and promised to have these secret places completed by the next day, Dantès furnishing the dimensions and plan in accordance with which they were to be constructed. 0313m", "그 벽장은 세 칸으로 나뉘어 있되, 자신 말고는 누구의 눈에도 띄지 않도록 만들어야 했다. 조선공은 흔쾌히 그 일을 맡아, 이튿날까지 이 은밀한 공간들을 완성해 놓겠노라 약속했다. 당테스는 그 칸들을 어떤 치수와 도면대로 만들어야 할지 일러 주었다."]], [["Two hours afterward Dantès sailed from the port of Genoa, under the inspection of an immense crowd drawn together by curiosity to see the rich Spanish nobleman who preferred managing his own yacht.", "두 시간 뒤 당테스는 제노바 항구를 떠났다. 자기 요트를 손수 다루겠다는 그 부유한 스페인 귀족을 구경하려고 호기심에 끌려 모여든 어마어마한 군중이 그 모습을 지켜보았다."], ["But their wonder was soon changed to admiration at seeing the perfect skill with which Dantès handled the helm.", "그러나 그들의 놀라움은 이내 감탄으로 바뀌었다. 당테스가 키를 다루는 솜씨가 그토록 완벽했던 것이다."], ["The boat, indeed, seemed to be animated with almost human intelligence, so promptly did it obey the slightest touch; and Dantès required but a short trial of his beautiful craft to acknowledge that the Genoese had not without reason attained their high reputation in the art of shipbuilding.", "사실 그 배는 거의 인간의 지성이라도 깃든 듯, 아주 가벼운 손길에도 곧바로 응했다. 당테스는 그 아름다운 배를 잠깐 다루어 보고도, 제노바 사람들이 조선술에서 그토록 드높은 명성을 얻은 것이 까닭 없는 일이 아님을 인정하기에 충분했다."]], [["The spectators followed the little vessel with their eyes as long as it remained visible; they then turned their conjectures upon her probable destination.", "구경꾼들은 그 작은 배가 보이지 않을 때까지 눈으로 좇았다. 이윽고 그들은 그 배의 행선지를 두고 이런저런 추측을 나누었다. 어떤 이들은 코르시카로 간다고 우겼고, 또 어떤 이들은 엘바섬이라고 했다."], ["Some insisted she was making for Corsica, others the Island of Elba; bets were offered to any amount that she was bound for Spain; while Africa was positively reported by many persons as her intended course; but no one thought of Monte Cristo.", "스페인으로 향하는 거라며 얼마든지 내기를 걸겠다는 이들도 있었다. 한편 여러 사람은 아프리카가 그 배의 목적지라고 단언했다. 그러나 몬테크리스토섬을 떠올린 사람은 아무도 없었다."]], [["Yet thither it was that Dantès guided his vessel, and at Monte Cristo he arrived at the close of the second day; his boat had proved herself a first-class sailor, and had come the distance from Genoa in thirty-five hours.", "하지만 당테스가 배를 몰고 간 곳은 바로 그곳이었다. 그는 이튿날 저물녘 몬테크리스토섬에 닿았다. 그의 배는 일류 쾌속선임을 증명해, 제노바에서 그 거리를 서른다섯 시간 만에 주파했다."], ["Dantès had carefully noted the general appearance of the shore, and, instead of landing at the usual place, he dropped anchor in the little creek.", "당테스는 해안의 전체적인 모습을 눈여겨 새겨 두었던 터라, 늘 쓰던 상륙 지점이 아니라 작은 만에 닻을 내렸다."], ["The island was utterly deserted, and bore no evidence of having been visited since he went away; his treasure was just as he had left it.", "섬은 완전히 인적이 끊겨 있었고, 그가 떠난 뒤로 누가 다녀간 흔적이라곤 없었다. 그의 보물은 두고 갔던 그대로였다."]], [["Early on the following morning he commenced the removal of his riches, and ere nightfall the whole of his immense wealth was safely deposited in the compartments of the secret locker.", "이튿날 이른 아침 그는 재물을 옮기는 일에 착수해, 날이 저물기 전에 그 어마어마한 재산 전부를 비밀 벽장 칸칸이 안전하게 들여놓았다."]], [["A week passed by.", "한 주가 지나갔다."], ["Dantès employed it in manœuvring his yacht round the island, studying it as a skilful horseman would the animal he destined for some important service, till at the end of that time he was perfectly conversant with its good and bad qualities.", "당테스는 그동안 요트를 몰아 섬을 빙빙 돌며, 솜씨 좋은 기수가 중요한 일에 쓸 말을 살피듯 배를 연구했다. 그렇게 그 기간이 끝날 무렵에는 배의 장점과 단점을 완벽하게 꿰뚫게 되었다."], ["The former Dantès proposed to augment, the latter to remedy.", "장점은 더 키우고, 단점은 손보려는 것이었다."]], [["Upon the eighth day he discerned a small vessel under full sail approaching Monte Cristo.", "여드레째 되는 날, 그는 작은 배 한 척이 돛을 한껏 펼치고 몬테크리스토섬으로 다가오는 것을 알아보았다."], ["As it drew near, he recognized it as the boat he had given to Jacopo.", "가까이 다가오자, 그는 그것이 자코포에게 준 배임을 알아챘다."], ["He immediately signalled it.", "그는 곧바로 신호를 보냈다."], ["His signal was returned, and in two hours afterwards the new-comer lay at anchor beside the yacht.", "신호가 되돌아왔고, 두 시간 뒤 그 새 배는 요트 곁에 닻을 내렸다."]], [["A mournful answer awaited each of Edmond’s eager inquiries as to the information Jacopo had obtained.", "자코포가 알아 온 소식을 두고 에드몽이 애타게 묻는 물음마다, 비통한 답이 그를 기다리고 있었다."], ["Old Dantès was dead, and Mercédès had disappeared.", "늙은 당테스는 죽었고, 메르세데스는 자취를 감추었다."]], [["Dantès listened to these melancholy tidings with outward calmness; but, leaping lightly ashore, he signified his desire to be quite alone.", "당테스는 이 서글픈 소식을 겉으로는 차분하게 들었다. 그러나 가볍게 뭍으로 뛰어내리며, 혼자 있고 싶다는 뜻을 내비쳤다."], ["In a couple of hours he returned.", "두 시간쯤 뒤 그는 돌아왔다."], ["Two of the men from Jacopo’s boat came on board the yacht to assist in navigating it, and he gave orders that she should be steered direct to Marseilles.", "자코포의 배에서 두 사람이 요트에 올라 항해를 돕기로 했고, 그는 곧장 마르세유로 키를 잡으라 명했다."], ["For his father’s death he was in some manner prepared; but he knew not how to account for the mysterious disappearance of Mercédès.", "아버지의 죽음은 어느 정도 마음의 준비가 되어 있었다. 그러나 메르세데스가 알 수 없이 사라진 일은 도무지 설명할 길이 없었다."]], [["Without divulging his secret, Dantès could not give sufficiently clear instructions to an agent.", "비밀을 털어놓지 않고서는 당테스가 대리인에게 충분히 분명한 지시를 내릴 수 없었다."], ["There were, besides, other particulars he was desirous of ascertaining, and those were of a nature he alone could investigate in a manner satisfactory to himself.", "게다가 그가 확인하고 싶은 다른 세부 사정들도 있었는데, 그것은 오직 그 자신만이 스스로 만족할 만한 방식으로 캐낼 수 있는 성질의 것이었다."], ["His looking-glass had assured him, during his stay at Leghorn, that he ran no risk of recognition; moreover, he had now the means of adopting any disguise he thought proper.", "레그혼에 머무는 동안 거울은 그에게 누가 알아볼 위험은 없다고 일러 주었다. 더구나 이제 그는 마음에 드는 어떤 변장이든 할 수 있는 수단을 갖추고 있었다."], ["One fine morning, then, his yacht, followed by the little fishing-boat, boldly entered the port of Marseilles, and anchored exactly opposite the spot from whence, on the never-to-be-forgotten night of his departure for the Château d’If, he had been put on board the boat destined to convey him thither. 0315m", "그리하여 어느 화창한 아침, 그의 요트는 작은 고깃배를 뒤에 거느리고 당당하게 마르세유 항구로 들어가, 바로 그 잊을 수 없던 밤 — 이프성으로 떠나던 그날 밤 — 그를 그곳으로 실어 갈 배에 태웠던 바로 그 자리 맞은편에 닻을 내렸다."]], [["Still Dantès could not view without a shudder the approach of a gendarme who accompanied the officers deputed to demand his bill of health ere the yacht was permitted to hold communication with the shore; but with that perfect self-possession he had acquired during his acquaintance with Faria, Dantès coolly presented an English passport he had obtained from Leghorn, and as this gave him a standing which a French passport would not have afforded, he was informed that there existed no obstacle to his immediate debarkation.", "그래도 당테스는 헌병 한 명이 다가오는 모습을 몸서리치지 않고는 바라볼 수 없었다. 그 헌병은 요트가 뭍과 교통하도록 허락받기 전에 검역 증명을 요구하러 온 관리들을 따라온 것이었다. 그러나 파리아와 알고 지내는 동안 익힌 그 완벽한 침착함으로, 당테스는 레그혼에서 발급받은 영국 여권을 태연히 내밀었다. 그것이 프랑스 여권으로는 누릴 수 없는 신분을 보장해 준 덕분에, 그는 즉시 상륙하는 데 아무런 걸림돌이 없다는 통보를 받았다."]], [["The first person to attract the attention of Dantès, as he landed on the Canebière, was one of the crew belonging to the _Pharaon_.", "카느비에르 거리에 내린 당테스의 눈길을 가장 먼저 끈 사람은 파라옹호 선원 가운데 하나였다."], ["Edmond welcomed the meeting with this fellow—who had been one of his own sailors—as a sure means of testing the extent of the change which time had worked in his own appearance.", "에드몽은 자기 부하 선원이었던 그 사내와의 만남을, 세월이 자기 모습에 일으킨 변화의 정도를 가늠할 확실한 방편으로 반겼다."], ["Going straight towards him, he propounded a variety of questions on different subjects, carefully watching the man’s countenance as he did so; but not a word or look implied that he had the slightest idea of ever having seen before the person with whom he was then conversing.", "그는 곧장 그에게 다가가 이런저런 주제로 온갖 질문을 던지며, 그러는 내내 사내의 얼굴빛을 유심히 살폈다. 그러나 지금 이야기를 나누는 상대를 전에 본 적이 있다는 기색은 단 한마디 말에서도, 단 한 번의 눈빛에서도 비치지 않았다."]], [["Giving the sailor a piece of money in return for his civility, Dantès proceeded onwards; but ere he had gone many steps he heard the man loudly calling him to stop.", "당테스는 그 친절에 대한 답례로 사내에게 돈 한 닢을 쥐여 주고는 발걸음을 옮겼다. 그러나 몇 걸음 가기도 전에, 그 사내가 멈추라고 큰 소리로 부르는 것을 들었다."]], [["Dantès instantly turned to meet him.", "당테스는 즉시 돌아서서 그를 맞았다."]], [["“I beg your pardon, sir,”", "\"죄송합니다, 나리.\""], ["said the honest fellow, in almost breathless haste, “but I believe you made a mistake; you intended to give me a two-franc piece, and see, you gave me a double Napoleon.”", "정직한 사내가 숨이 턱에 닿도록 다급하게 말했다. \"아무래도 착각하신 것 같습니다. 이 프랑짜리를 주시려던 모양인데, 보세요, 두 배짜리 나폴레옹 금화를 주셨습니다.\""]], [["“Thank you, my good friend.", "\"고맙네, 이 사람."], ["I see that I have made a trifling mistake, as you say; but by way of rewarding your honesty I give you another double Napoleon, that you may drink to my health, and be able to ask your messmates to join you.”", "자네 말대로 내가 사소한 실수를 했군. 하지만 자네 정직함에 대한 보답으로, 두 배짜리 나폴레옹 금화를 한 닢 더 주겠네. 내 건강을 위해 한잔하고, 동료들도 불러 함께 마시게나.\""]], [["So extreme was the surprise of the sailor, that he was unable even to thank Edmond, whose receding figure he continued to gaze after in speechless astonishment. “Some nabob from India,”", "사내는 어찌나 놀랐던지 에드몽에게 고맙다는 말조차 하지 못한 채, 멀어져 가는 그의 뒷모습을 말없이 멍하니 바라볼 뿐이었다. \"인도에서 온 무슨 큰 부자인가 보군.\""], ["was his comment.", "그가 중얼거렸다."]], [["Dantès, meanwhile, went on his way.", "그러는 동안 당테스는 제 갈 길을 갔다."], ["Each step he trod oppressed his heart with fresh emotion; his first and most indelible recollections were there; not a tree, not a street, that he passed but seemed filled with dear and cherished memories.", "한 걸음 내디딜 때마다 새로운 감회가 그의 가슴을 짓눌렀다. 가장 처음의, 가장 지워지지 않는 기억들이 그곳에 있었다. 지나치는 나무 한 그루, 거리 한 자락도 정겹고 소중한 추억으로 가득 차지 않은 것이 없었다."], ["And thus he proceeded onwards till he arrived at the end of the Rue de Noailles, from whence a full view of the Allées de Meilhan was obtained.", "그렇게 그는 노아유 거리 끝에 이를 때까지 걸어 나갔고, 그곳에서는 메이앙 거리가 한눈에 들어왔다."], ["At this spot, so pregnant with fond and filial remembrances, his heart beat almost to bursting, his knees tottered under him, a mist floated over his sight, and had he not clung for support to one of the trees, he would inevitably have fallen to the ground and been crushed beneath the many vehicles continually passing there.", "자애로운 아버지에 대한 추억으로 가득한 이 자리에서, 그의 심장은 터질 듯이 뛰었고 무릎은 휘청거렸으며 눈앞에는 안개가 어렸다. 나무 한 그루에 매달려 몸을 가누지 않았더라면, 그는 어김없이 땅바닥에 쓰러져 그곳을 끊임없이 오가는 숱한 마차들에 깔리고 말았을 것이다."], ["Recovering himself, however, he wiped the perspiration from his brows, and stopped not again till he found himself at the door of the house in which his father had lived.", "그러나 가까스로 정신을 차린 그는 이마의 땀을 닦고는, 아버지가 살던 집 문 앞에 다다를 때까지 다시는 멈추지 않았다."]], [["The nasturtiums and other plants, which his father had delighted to train before his window, had all disappeared from the upper part of the house.", "아버지가 창가에 손수 가꾸기를 즐기던 한련화며 다른 화초들은 집 위층에서 모조리 사라지고 없었다."]], [["Leaning against the tree, he gazed thoughtfully for a time at the upper stories of the shabby little house.", "나무에 몸을 기댄 채, 그는 한동안 그 초라한 작은 집의 위층들을 생각에 잠겨 바라보았다."], ["Then he advanced to the door, and asked whether there were any rooms to be let.", "이윽고 그는 문 앞으로 나아가 세놓을 방이 있는지 물었다."], ["Though answered in the negative, he begged so earnestly to be permitted to visit those on the fifth floor, that, in despite of the oft-repeated assurance of the _concierge_ that they were occupied, Dantès succeeded in inducing the man to go up to the tenants, and ask permission for a gentleman to be allowed to look at them.", "없다는 대답을 들었지만, 그는 오 층 방들을 좀 둘러보게 해 달라고 어찌나 간곡히 청했던지, 거기에 세 든 사람이 있다는 관리인의 거듭된 장담에도 불구하고, 당테스는 끝내 그를 설득해 세입자에게 올라가 어느 신사분이 방을 좀 보아도 될지 허락을 구하게 만들었다."]], [["The tenants of the humble lodging were a young couple who had been scarcely married a week; and seeing them, Dantès sighed heavily.", "그 초라한 셋방에 든 사람들은 결혼한 지 채 일주일도 안 된 젊은 부부였다. 그들을 본 당테스는 무겁게 한숨을 내쉬었다."], ["Nothing in the two small chambers forming the apartments remained as it had been in the time of the elder Dantès; the very paper was different, while the articles of antiquated furniture with which the rooms had been filled in Edmond’s time had all disappeared; the four walls alone remained as he had left them.", "두 칸짜리 작은 방 어디에도 늙은 당테스가 살던 시절 그대로 남아 있는 것은 없었다. 벽지마저 달라졌고, 에드몽이 살던 때 방을 채우던 낡은 가구들도 죄다 사라지고 없었다. 오직 네 벽만이 그가 떠날 때 그대로 남아 있었다."]], [["The bed belonging to the present occupants was placed as the former owner of the chamber had been accustomed to have his; and, in spite of his efforts to prevent it, the eyes of Edmond were suffused in tears as he reflected that on that spot the old man had breathed his last, vainly calling for his son.", "지금 사는 사람들의 침대는 이전 방 주인이 늘 두던 자리에 놓여 있었다. 바로 그 자리에서 노인이, 아들을 헛되이 부르며 마지막 숨을 거두었으리라는 생각이 들자, 에드몽은 애써 참으려 했음에도 두 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁 차올랐다."]], [["The young couple gazed with astonishment at the sight of their visitor’s emotion, and wondered to see the large tears silently chasing each other down his otherwise stern and immovable features; but they felt the sacredness of his grief, and kindly refrained from questioning him as to its cause, while, with instinctive delicacy, they left him to indulge his sorrow alone. 0317m", "젊은 부부는 손님이 보이는 감정에 놀라 빤히 바라보며, 평소엔 엄하고 미동도 없을 그의 얼굴 위로 굵은 눈물이 소리 없이 잇따라 흘러내리는 것을 의아하게 여겼다. 그러나 그들은 그의 슬픔이 신성한 것임을 느꼈고, 그 까닭을 캐묻지 않는 친절을 베풀었다. 그러고는 본능적인 섬세함으로, 그가 홀로 슬픔에 잠기도록 자리를 비켜 주었다."]], [["When he withdrew from the scene of his painful recollections, they both accompanied him downstairs, reiterating their hope that he would come again whenever he pleased, and assuring him that their poor dwelling would ever be open to him.", "그가 그 고통스러운 추억의 자리에서 물러나오자, 두 사람은 함께 아래층까지 그를 배웅했다. 언제든 마음 내키면 다시 찾아오기 바란다고 거듭 말하며, 자기들의 누추한 집은 언제나 그에게 열려 있을 거라고 다짐해 주었다."]], [["As Edmond passed the door on the fourth floor, he paused to inquire whether Caderousse the tailor still dwelt there; but he received for reply, that the person in question had got into difficulties, and at the present time kept a small inn on the route from Bellegarde to Beaucaire.", "에드몽은 사 층 문 앞을 지나다가, 양복장이 카드루스가 아직 거기 사는지 물어보려 걸음을 멈추었다. 그러나 그 사람은 형편이 어려워져, 지금은 벨가르드에서 보케르로 가는 길목에서 작은 여인숙을 하고 있다는 답이 돌아왔다."]], [["Having obtained the address of the person to whom the house in the Allées de Meilhan belonged, Dantès next proceeded thither, and, under the name of Lord Wilmore (the name and title inscribed on his passport), purchased the small dwelling for the sum of twenty-five thousand francs, at least ten thousand more than it was worth; but had its owner asked half a million, it would unhesitatingly have been given.", "메이앙 거리의 그 집 주인이 누구인지 주소를 알아낸 당테스는, 이번엔 그곳으로 가서 윌모어 경(여권에 적힌 이름과 작위였다)의 이름으로 그 작은 집을 이만 오천 프랑에 사들였다. 적어도 제값보다 만 프랑은 더 얹어 준 셈이었다. 그러나 주인이 오십만 프랑을 불렀더라도 그는 망설임 없이 내주었을 것이다."]], [["The very same day the occupants of the apartments on the fifth floor of the house, now become the property of Dantès, were duly informed by the notary who had arranged the necessary transfer of deeds, etc., that the new landlord gave them their choice of any of the rooms in the house, without the least augmentation of rent, upon condition of their giving instant possession of the two small chambers they at present inhabited.", "바로 그날, 이제 당테스의 소유가 된 그 집 오 층에 사는 사람들은, 증서 양도 따위의 필요한 절차를 처리한 공증인을 통해 정식으로 통보를 받았다. 새 집주인이 그들에게 집 안 어느 방이든 골라 쓰도록 하되 집세는 한 푼도 올리지 않겠다는 것, 다만 지금 살고 있는 두 칸짜리 작은 방을 즉시 비워 준다는 조건이었다."]], [["This strange event aroused great wonder and curiosity in the neighborhood of the Allées de Meilhan, and a multitude of theories were afloat, none of which was anywhere near the truth.", "이 기이한 일은 메이앙 거리 일대에 큰 놀라움과 호기심을 불러일으켰고, 온갖 추측이 떠돌았으나 그 어느 것도 진실 근처에도 미치지 못했다."], ["But what raised public astonishment to a climax, and set all conjecture at defiance, was the knowledge that the same stranger who had in the morning visited the Allées de Meilhan had been seen in the evening walking in the little village of the Catalans, and afterwards observed to enter a poor fisherman’s hut, and to pass more than an hour in inquiring after persons who had either been dead or gone away for more than fifteen or sixteen years.", "그러나 사람들의 놀라움을 절정으로 끌어올리고 모든 억측을 무색하게 만든 것은, 아침에 메이앙 거리를 찾았던 바로 그 낯선 사내가 저녁에는 카탈루냐 마을을 거니는 모습으로 목격되었다는 사실이었다. 게다가 그 뒤로 그가 어느 가난한 어부의 오두막에 들어가, 죽었거나 떠난 지 열대여섯 해도 더 된 사람들의 소식을 묻느라 한 시간 넘게 머물렀다는 것이었다."]], [["But on the following day the family from whom all these particulars had been asked received a handsome present, consisting of an entirely new fishing-boat, with two seines and a tender.", "그런데 이튿날, 이 모든 사정을 캐물음당했던 그 가족은 완전히 새 고깃배 한 척에 두릿그물 두 벌과 딸린 거룻배까지 곁들인 푸짐한 선물을 받았다."]], [["The delighted recipients of these munificent gifts would gladly have poured out their thanks to their generous benefactor, but they had seen him, upon quitting the hut, merely give some orders to a sailor, and then springing lightly on horseback, leave Marseilles by the Porte d’Aix.", "이 후한 선물을 받고 기뻐 어쩔 줄 모르던 사람들은 너그러운 은인에게 고마움을 쏟아붓고 싶었으나, 그가 오두막을 나서며 어느 선원에게 몇 마디 지시만 내리고는 가볍게 말에 뛰어올라 엑스 문으로 마르세유를 떠나는 것을 보았을 뿐이었다. 퐁뒤가르 여관"]], [["# Chapter 26. The Pont du Gard Inn", "# Chapter 26. The Pont du Gard Inn"]], [["Such of my readers as have made a pedestrian excursion to the south of France may perchance have noticed, about midway between the town of Beaucaire and the village of Bellegarde,—a little nearer to the former than to the latter,—a small roadside inn, from the front of which hung, creaking and flapping in the wind, a sheet of tin covered with a grotesque representation of the Pont du Gard.", "프랑스 남부로 도보 여행을 떠나 본 독자라면, 보케르 시와 벨가르드 마을 사이쯤, 그러니까 마을보다는 도시 쪽에 좀 더 가까운 곳에서 길가의 작은 여관 하나를 보았을지도 모른다. 그 정면에는 양철판 하나가 바람에 삐걱거리며 펄럭이고 있었는데, 거기에는 퐁뒤가르●를 우스꽝스럽게 그린 그림이 새겨져 있었다."], ["This modern place of entertainment stood on the left-hand side of the post road, and backed upon the Rhône.", "이 변변찮은 객점은 역참 도로 왼편에 서서 론강을 등지고 있었다."], ["It also boasted of what in Languedoc is styled a garden, consisting of a small plot of ground, on the side opposite to the main entrance reserved for the reception of guests. A few dingy olives and stunted fig-trees struggled hard for existence, but their withered dusty foliage abundantly proved how unequal was the conflict.", "또 랑그도크 지방에서 정원이라 부르는 것도 갖추고 있었는데, 손님맞이용 정문 맞은편에 자리한 작은 땅뙈기에 불과했다. 거기서는 거무튀튀한 올리브나무 몇 그루와 발육이 멈춘 무화과나무들이 살겠다고 힘겹게 버티고 있었지만, 시들고 먼지 앉은 잎사귀가 그 싸움이 얼마나 가망 없는 것인지를 넉넉히 증명하고 있었다."], ["Between these sickly shrubs grew a scanty supply of garlic, tomatoes, and eschalots; while, lone and solitary, like a forgotten sentinel, a tall pine raised its melancholy head in one of the corners of this unattractive spot, and displayed its flexible stem and fan-shaped summit dried and cracked by the fierce heat of the sub-tropical sun.", "그 병든 관목들 사이에는 마늘과 토마토, 골파가 빈약하게 자라났다. 그리고 한쪽 구석에는 잊힌 보초병처럼 외롭고 쓸쓸하게 키 큰 소나무 한 그루가 우울한 머리를 쳐들고서, 아열대의 태양이 내리쬐는 사나운 열기에 말라 갈라진 낭창한 줄기와 부챗살 모양의 우듬지를 드러내고 있었다."]], [["All these trees, great or small, were turned in the direction to which the Mistral blows, one of the three curses of Provence, the others being the Durance and the Parliament.", "크든 작든 이 나무들은 모두 미스트랄●●이 불어 가는 쪽으로 휘어 있었다. 미스트랄은 프로방스의 세 가지 재앙 가운데 하나였고, 나머지 둘은 뒤랑스강과 의회였다."]], [["In the surrounding plain, which more resembled a dusty lake than solid ground, were scattered a few miserable stalks of wheat, the effect, no doubt, of a curious desire on the part of the agriculturists of the country to see whether such a thing as the raising of grain in those parched regions was practicable.", "단단한 땅이라기보다 먼지로 뒤덮인 호수에 더 가까운 주변 평야에는 비참한 밀 줄기 몇 가닥이 흩어져 있었다. 이는 분명, 그토록 메마른 지역에서도 곡식을 키우는 일이 과연 가능한지 알아보려는 이 고장 농부들의 별난 욕심이 빚어낸 결과였다."], ["Each stalk served as a perch for a grasshopper, which regaled the passers-by through this Egyptian scene with its strident, monotonous note.", "밀 줄기마다 메뚜기가 한 마리씩 앉아 횃대로 삼고서, 이 이집트 같은 풍경을 지나는 행인들에게 거슬리고 단조로운 울음소리를 들려주었다."]], [["For about seven or eight years the little tavern had been kept by a man and his wife, with two servants,—a chambermaid named Trinette, and a hostler called Pecaud.", "이 작은 선술집은 한 일고여덟 해 동안 한 남자와 그의 아내가 두 하인을 거느리고 꾸려 왔다. 하인은 트리네트라는 객실 하녀와 페코라는 마부였다."], ["This small staff was quite equal to all the requirements, for a canal between Beaucaire and Aiguemortes had revolutionized transportation by substituting boats for the cart and the stagecoach.", "이 단출한 일손으로 모든 일을 감당하기에 충분했다. 보케르와 에그모르트 사이에 운하가 뚫리면서, 짐수레와 역마차 대신 배가 쓰이게 되어 운송 방식에 혁명이 일어났기 때문이다."], ["And, as though to add to the daily misery which this prosperous canal inflicted on the unfortunate innkeeper, whose utter ruin it was fast accomplishing, it was situated between the Rhône from which it had its source and the post-road it had depleted, not a hundred steps from the inn, of which we have given a brief but faithful description.", "그리고 이 번창하는 운하가 가엾은 여관 주인에게 날마다 안기는 고통을 더하기라도 하려는 듯, 운하는 그의 완전한 파멸을 빠르게 이루어 가면서, 제 물줄기의 발원지인 론강과 제가 말려 버린 역참 도로 사이에 놓여 있었다. 우리가 짧지만 충실하게 묘사한 이 여관에서 백 걸음도 떨어지지 않은 곳에 말이다."]], [["The innkeeper himself was a man of from forty to fifty-five years of age, tall, strong, and bony, a perfect specimen of the natives of those southern latitudes; he had dark, sparkling, and deep-set eyes, hooked nose, and teeth white as those of a carnivorous animal; his hair, like his beard, which he wore under his chin, was thick and curly, and in spite of his age but slightly interspersed with a few silvery threads.", "여관 주인은 마흔에서 쉰다섯쯤 된 남자로, 키가 크고 다부지며 뼈대가 굵은, 이 남부 위도 지방 토박이의 완벽한 본보기였다. 그는 거무스름하게 반짝이는 움푹한 눈에 매부리코, 그리고 육식 짐승처럼 흰 이를 지녔다. 머리카락은 턱밑에 기른 수염과 마찬가지로 숱이 많고 곱슬곱슬했으며, 나이에 비해 은빛 올이 몇 가닥 섞였을 뿐이었다."], ["His naturally dark complexion had assumed a still further shade of brown from the habit the unfortunate man had acquired of stationing himself from morning till eve at the threshold of his door, on the lookout for guests who seldom came, yet there he stood, day after day, exposed to the meridional rays of a burning sun, with no other protection for his head than a red handkerchief twisted around it, after the manner of the Spanish muleteers.", "본디 가무잡잡한 그의 얼굴빛은 한층 더 짙은 갈색을 띠게 되었는데, 이 가엾은 사내가 아침부터 저녁까지 제 문턱에 서서 좀처럼 오지 않는 손님을 기다리는 버릇이 든 탓이었다. 그래도 그는 날이면 날마다 그 자리에 서서, 머리에는 스페인 노새몰이꾼들처럼 붉은 손수건을 둘둘 동여맨 것 말고는 아무런 가림막도 없이, 한낮에 타오르는 태양의 직사광선에 몸을 드러내고 있었다."], ["This man was our old acquaintance, Gaspard Caderousse.", "이 사내가 바로 우리의 옛 지인, 가스파르 카드루스였다."]], [["His wife, on the contrary, whose maiden name had been Madeleine Radelle, was pale, meagre, and sickly-looking.", "반면에 그의 아내는, 처녀 적 이름이 마들렌 라델이었는데, 창백하고 야위었으며 병약해 보였다."], ["Born in the neighborhood of Arles, she had shared in the beauty for which its women are proverbial; but that beauty had gradually withered beneath the devastating influence of the slow fever so prevalent among dwellers by the ponds of Aiguemortes and the marshes of Camargue.", "아를 근방에서 태어난 그녀는 그곳 여인들 특유의, 소문난 미모를 한때 지녔었다. 그러나 그 아름다움은 에그모르트의 연못과 카마르그의 늪지 주민들 사이에 만연한 만성 열병의 황폐한 영향 아래 차츰 시들어 버렸다. 그녀는 거의 늘 이 층 방에 틀어박혀 의자에 앉아 오들오들 떨거나, 침대에 나른하고 기운 없이 늘어져 있었다."], ["She remained nearly always in her second-floor chamber, shivering in her chair, or stretched languid and feeble on her bed, while her husband kept his daily watch at the door—a duty he performed with so much the greater willingness, as it saved him the necessity of listening to the endless plaints and murmurs of his helpmate, who never saw him without breaking out into bitter invectives against fate; to all of which her husband would calmly return an unvarying reply, in these philosophic words:", "그동안 남편은 날마다 문간에서 망을 보았는데, 그는 그 일을 한층 기꺼이 해냈다. 그래야 끝없이 이어지는 제 아내의 푸념과 넋두리를 듣지 않아도 되었기 때문이다. 아내는 남편을 볼 때마다 운명을 향해 모진 욕설을 퍼부었고, 그럴 때면 남편은 한결같이 이런 철학적인 말로 차분히 대꾸했다."]], [["“Hush, La Carconte. It is God’s pleasure that things should be so.”", "\"조용히 해, 라카르콩트. 일이 이리된 건 다 하느님의 뜻이야.\""]], [["The sobriquet of La Carconte had been bestowed on Madeleine Radelle from the fact that she had been born in a village, so called, situated between Salon and Lambesc; and as a custom existed among the inhabitants of that part of France where Caderousse lived of styling every person by some particular and distinctive appellation, her husband had bestowed on her the name of La Carconte in place of her sweet and euphonious name of Madeleine, which, in all probability, his rude guttural language would not have enabled him to pronounce.", "마들렌 라델에게 라카르콩트라는 별명이 붙은 것은, 그녀가 살롱과 랑베스크 사이에 있는 그 이름의 마을에서 태어났기 때문이었다. 카드루스가 사는 프랑스의 그 지방에서는 사람마다 저마다 특별하고 남다른 호칭으로 부르는 관습이 있었던 터라, 남편은 마들렌이라는 곱고 듣기 좋은 이름 대신 라카르콩트라는 이름을 붙여 준 것이다. 아마도 그의 거칠고 목쉰 발음으로는 마들렌이라는 이름을 제대로 부르기 어려웠을 것이다."]], [["Still, let it not be supposed that amid this affected resignation to the will of Providence, the unfortunate innkeeper did not writhe under the double misery of seeing the hateful canal carry off his customers and his profits, and the daily infliction of his peevish partner’s murmurs and lamentations.", "그렇다고 해서, 섭리의 뜻에 짐짓 순응하는 척하는 이 가엾은 여관 주인이 이중의 비참함 아래 몸부림치지 않았다고 생각해서는 안 된다. 그는 그 얄미운 운하가 손님과 수익을 앗아 가는 것을 보았고, 또 까탈스러운 아내의 푸념과 넋두리를 날마다 견뎌야 했으니 말이다."]], [["0323m\nLike other dwellers in the south, he was a man of sober habits and moderate desires, but fond of external show, vain, and addicted to display.", "남부의 다른 주민들처럼, 그도 술버릇이 검소하고 욕심도 적은 사내였지만, 겉치레를 좋아하고 허영심이 강하며 과시하기를 즐겼다."], ["During the days of his prosperity, not a festivity took place without himself and wife being among the spectators.", "형편이 넉넉하던 시절에는, 그와 아내가 구경꾼 축에 끼지 않는 잔치가 한 번도 없었다."], ["He dressed in the picturesque costume worn upon grand occasions by the inhabitants of the south of France, bearing equal resemblance to the style adopted both by the Catalans and Andalusians; while La Carconte displayed the charming fashion prevalent among the women of Arles, a mode of attire borrowed equally from Greece and Arabia.", "그는 남부 프랑스 주민들이 큰 행사 때 입는 그림 같은 의상을 차려입었는데, 그것은 카탈루냐인과 안달루시아인이 즐겨 입는 양식을 똑같이 닮아 있었다. 한편 라카르콩트는 아를 여인들 사이에 유행하던 매혹적인 옷차림을 뽐냈으니, 그리스와 아라비아에서 두루 빌려 온 복식이었다."], ["But, by degrees, watch-chains, necklaces, parti-colored scarves, embroidered bodices, velvet vests, elegantly worked stockings, striped gaiters, and silver buckles for the shoes, all disappeared; and Gaspard Caderousse, unable to appear abroad in his pristine splendor, had given up any further participation in the pomps and vanities, both for himself and wife, although a bitter feeling of envious discontent filled his mind as the sound of mirth and merry music from the joyous revellers reached even the miserable hostelry to which he still clung, more for the shelter than the profit it afforded.", "그러나 차츰 시곗줄과 목걸이, 색색의 스카프, 수놓은 보디스, 벨벳 조끼, 정교하게 짠 양말, 줄무늬 각반, 구두에 다는 은 버클이 모조리 사라지고 말았다. 가스파르 카드루스는 예전의 화려한 차림으로 바깥나들이를 할 수 없게 되자, 저와 아내를 위한 온갖 호사와 허영을 더는 누리지 않기로 했다. 그래도 흥겨운 잔치꾼들의 웃음소리와 즐거운 음악 소리가, 그가 수익보다는 비바람을 피할 곳으로 여겨 여태 매달려 있던 이 초라한 객점에까지 들려올 때면, 그의 마음은 시샘 어린 불만으로 쓰라리게 차오르곤 했다."]], [["Caderousse, then, was, as usual, at his place of observation before the door, his eyes glancing listlessly from a piece of closely shaven grass—on which some fowls were industriously, though fruitlessly, endeavoring to turn up some grain or insect suited to their palate—to the deserted road, which led away to the north and south, when he was aroused by the shrill voice of his wife, and grumbling to himself as he went, he mounted to her chamber, first taking care, however, to set the entrance door wide open, as an invitation to any chance traveller who might be passing.", "그러니까 카드루스는 여느 때처럼 문 앞 제 망보는 자리에 서서, 짧게 깎인 풀밭에서 닭 몇 마리가 입맛에 맞는 곡식 낟알이나 벌레를 찾아 부지런히, 그러나 헛되이 헤집는 모습을 무심히 바라보다가, 다시 남북으로 뻗은 그 인적 끊긴 길로 눈길을 옮기고 있었다. 그때 아내의 날카로운 목소리에 정신이 번쩍 든 그는, 투덜거리며 아내의 방으로 올라갔다. 다만 올라가기 전에, 지나갈지도 모르는 어떤 길손이라도 들어오라는 뜻으로, 현관문을 활짝 열어 두는 것은 잊지 않았다."]], [["At the moment Caderousse quitted his sentry-like watch before the door, the road on which he so eagerly strained his sight was void and lonely as a desert at midday.", "카드루스가 문 앞에서 보초처럼 지키던 자리를 떠난 순간, 그가 그토록 애타게 눈여겨보던 길은 한낮의 사막처럼 텅 비고 적막했다."], ["There it lay stretching out into one interminable line of dust and sand, with its sides bordered by tall, meagre trees, altogether presenting so uninviting an appearance, that no one in his senses could have imagined that any traveller, at liberty to regulate his hours for journeying, would choose to expose himself in such a formidable Sahara.", "길은 끝없는 먼지와 모래의 한 줄기로 뻗어 나갔고, 양옆으로는 키 크고 앙상한 나무들이 늘어서 있어, 그 모습이 어찌나 삭막한지, 제정신을 가진 사람이라면 누구도, 길 떠날 시간을 제 마음대로 고를 수 있는 어떤 길손이 굳이 이런 무시무시한 사하라 사막에 제 몸을 드러내려 들리라고는 상상조차 할 수 없었다."]], [["Nevertheless, had Caderousse but retained his post a few minutes longer, he might have caught a dim outline of something approaching from the direction of Bellegarde; as the moving object drew nearer, he would easily have perceived that it consisted of a man and horse, between whom the kindest and most amiable understanding appeared to exist.", "그렇지만 카드루스가 몇 분만 더 자리를 지켰더라면, 벨가르드 쪽에서 무언가 다가오는 흐릿한 윤곽을 어렴풋이 보았을 것이다. 그 움직이는 물체가 가까이 다가올수록, 그는 그것이 한 사람과 한 마리 말이며, 둘 사이에 더없이 다정하고 정겨운 교감이 흐르는 듯하다는 것을 어렵잖게 알아보았을 것이다."], ["The horse was of Hungarian breed, and ambled along at an easy pace.", "말은 헝가리 종으로, 느긋한 걸음으로 천천히 나아가고 있었다."], ["His rider was a priest, dressed in black, and wearing a three-cornered hat; and, despite of the ardent rays of a noonday sun, the pair came on with a fair degree of rapidity.", "말 탄 이는 검은 옷을 입고 삼각모를 쓴 사제였다. 한낮 태양의 뜨거운 햇살에도 아랑곳없이, 그 한 쌍은 제법 빠른 속도로 다가오고 있었다."]], [["Having arrived before the Pont du Gard, the horse stopped, but whether for his own pleasure or that of his rider would have been difficult to say.", "퐁뒤가르 앞에 이르자 말이 멈춰 섰는데, 제 뜻으로 그런 것인지 주인의 뜻으로 그런 것인지는 가늠하기 어려웠다."], ["However that might have been, the priest, dismounting, led his steed by the bridle in search of some place to which he could secure him.", "어쨌든 사제는 말에서 내려, 매어 둘 만한 곳을 찾아 고삐를 잡고 말을 끌었다."], ["Availing himself of a handle that projected from a half-fallen door, he tied the animal safely and having drawn a red cotton handkerchief, from his pocket, wiped away the perspiration that streamed from his brow, then, advancing to the door, struck thrice with the end of his iron-shod stick.", "반쯤 떨어져 나간 문에 손잡이 하나가 삐죽 튀어나와 있는 것을 발견하고, 그는 그 짐승을 단단히 붙들어 맨 뒤, 주머니에서 붉은 면 손수건을 꺼내 이마에 줄줄 흐르는 땀을 닦았다. 그러고는 문 쪽으로 다가가, 끝에 쇠를 댄 지팡이로 세 번 두드렸다."]], [["At this unusual sound, a huge black dog came rushing to meet the daring assailant of his ordinarily tranquil abode, snarling and displaying his sharp white teeth with a determined hostility that abundantly proved how little he was accustomed to society.", "이 낯선 소리에, 평소 고요하던 제 거처에 겁도 없이 쳐들어온 침입자를 맞으러 커다란 검은 개 한 마리가 달려 나왔다. 개는 으르렁거리며 날카로운 흰 이를 드러냈는데, 그 단호한 적의는 이 개가 손님 맞는 일에 얼마나 익숙지 않은지를 넉넉히 증명했다."], ["At that moment a heavy footstep was heard descending the wooden staircase that led from the upper floor, and, with many bows and courteous smiles, the host of the Pont du Gard besought his guest to enter.", "그때 위층에서 내려오는 나무 계단을 밟는 묵직한 발소리가 들리더니, 퐁뒤가르의 주인이 연신 허리를 굽히고 공손히 웃으며 손님에게 들어오기를 청했다."]], [["0319m\n“You are welcome, sir, most welcome!”", "\"어서 오십시오, 손님, 정말 잘 오셨습니다!\""], ["repeated the astonished Caderousse.", "어리둥절해진 카드루스가 거듭 말했다."], ["“Now, then, Margotin,”", "\"자, 마르고탱,\""], ["cried he, speaking to the dog, “will you be quiet?", "그가 개를 향해 외쳤다. \"좀 조용히 못 하겠니?"], ["Pray don’t heed him, sir!—he only barks, he never bites.", "부디 신경 쓰지 마십시오, 손님! 짖기만 하지, 물지는 않는 놈입니다."], ["I make no doubt a glass of good wine would be acceptable this dreadfully hot day.”", "이렇게 지독하게 더운 날에는 좋은 포도주 한 잔이 반가우시겠지요.\""], ["Then perceiving for the first time the garb of the traveller he had to entertain, Caderousse hastily exclaimed: “A thousand pardons!", "그러다가 자기가 모실 길손의 옷차림을 그제야 처음 알아보고는, 카드루스가 황급히 외쳤다. \"천 번 만 번 용서하십시오!"], ["I really did not observe whom I had the honor to receive under my poor roof.", "누추한 제 지붕 아래 모시게 된 분이 누구신지 정말 미처 알아뵙지 못했습니다."], ["What would the abbé please to have?", "신부님, 무엇을 드시겠습니까?"], ["What refreshment can I offer? All I have is at his service.”", "무슨 음식을 올릴까요? 제게 있는 건 무엇이든 다 신부님 것입니다.\""]], [["The priest gazed on the person addressing him with a long and searching gaze—there even seemed a disposition on his part to court a similar scrutiny on the part of the innkeeper; then, observing in the countenance of the latter no other expression than extreme surprise at his own want of attention to an inquiry so courteously worded, he deemed it as well to terminate this dumb show, and therefore said, speaking with a strong Italian accent, “You are, I presume, M.", "사제는 자기에게 말을 건네는 사람을 길고 꿰뚫는 듯한 눈길로 바라보았다. 심지어 여관 주인 쪽에서도 자기를 그렇게 살펴봐 주기를 바라는 듯한 기색마저 있었다. 그러나 상대의 얼굴에서, 그토록 정중하게 건넨 물음에 제가 미처 주의를 기울이지 못한 데 대한 극도의 놀라움 말고는 아무런 표정도 읽어 내지 못하자, 그는 이 무언극을 끝내는 편이 낫겠다고 여기고, 강한 이탈리아 억양으로 말했다."], ["Caderousse?”", "\"카드루스 씨가 맞으시지요?\""]], [["“Yes, sir,”", "\"네, 손님.\""], ["answered the host, even more surprised at the question than he had been by the silence which had preceded it; “I am Gaspard Caderousse, at your service.”", "주인이 대답했는데, 앞선 침묵보다도 이 물음에 한층 더 놀란 기색이었다. \"제가 가스파르 카드루스입니다, 분부만 하십시오.\""]], [["“Gaspard Caderousse,”", "\"가스파르 카드루스.\""], ["rejoined the priest.", "사제가 다시 말했다."], ["“Yes,—Christian and surname are the same.", "\"그렇군요, 세례명과 성이 그대로네요."], ["You formerly lived, I believe in the Allées de Meilhan, on the fourth floor?”", "전에는 메이앙 가로의 사 층에 사셨지요?\""]], [["0325m\n“I did.”", "\"그랬습니다.\""]], [["“And you followed the business of a tailor?”", "\"그리고 재단사 일을 하셨고요?\""]], [["“True, I was a tailor, till the trade fell off.", "\"맞습니다, 장사가 망하기 전까지는 재단사였지요."], ["It is so hot at Marseilles, that really I believe that the respectable inhabitants will in time go without any clothing whatever.", "마르세유는 어찌나 더운지, 정말이지 점잖은 주민들이 머잖아 옷이라곤 한 오라기도 안 걸치고 다닐 것만 같습니다."], ["But talking of heat, is there nothing I can offer you by way of refreshment?”", "그건 그렇고, 더운 얘기가 나왔으니 말인데, 뭐 좀 시원하게 드실 것을 올릴까요?\""]], [["“Yes; let me have a bottle of your best wine, and then, with your permission, we will resume our conversation from where we left off.”", "\"좋소. 가장 좋은 포도주로 한 병 주시오. 그런 다음, 괜찮다면 아까 끊긴 데서 다시 이야기를 이어 갑시다.\""]], [["“As you please, sir,”", "\"분부대로 하지요, 손님.\""], ["said Caderousse, who, anxious not to lose the present opportunity of finding a customer for one of the few bottles of Cahors still remaining in his possession, hastily raised a trap-door in the floor of the apartment they were in, which served both as parlor and kitchen.", "카드루스가 말했다. 그는 아직 몇 병 남지 않은 카오르산 포도주 가운데 한 병을 팔 이 좋은 기회를 놓치지 않으려고, 두 사람이 있던 방—응접실 겸 부엌으로 쓰는 곳이었다—의 바닥에 난 들창문을 황급히 들어 올렸다."]], [["Upon issuing forth from his subterranean retreat at the expiration of five minutes, he found the abbé seated upon a wooden stool, leaning his elbow on a table, while Margotin, whose animosity seemed appeased by the unusual command of the traveller for refreshments, had crept up to him, and had established himself very comfortably between his knees, his long, skinny neck resting on his lap, while his dim eye was fixed earnestly on the traveller’s face.", "오 분 뒤 지하 저장고에서 올라온 그는, 신부가 나무 걸상에 앉아 식탁에 팔꿈치를 괴고 있는 것을 보았다. 길손이 음식을 청한 뜻밖의 일로 적의가 누그러진 듯한 마르고탱은, 어느새 그에게 슬그머니 다가가 두 무릎 사이에 아주 편안히 자리를 잡고 있었다. 길고 비쩍 마른 목을 그의 무릎에 얹은 채, 흐릿한 눈을 길손의 얼굴에 골똘히 박고 있었다."]], [["“Are you quite alone?”", "\"혼자 계신 거요?\""], ["inquired the guest, as Caderousse placed before him the bottle of wine and a glass.", "카드루스가 포도주 병과 잔을 앞에 놓자 손님이 물었다."]], [["“Quite, quite alone,”", "\"네, 아주 혼자입니다.\""], ["replied the man—“or, at least, practically so, for my poor wife, who is the only person in the house besides myself, is laid up with illness, and unable to render me the least assistance, poor thing!”", "사내가 대답했다. \"아니, 적어도 사실상 그런 셈이지요. 이 집에 저 말고 있는 사람이라곤 제 가엾은 아내뿐인데, 병으로 자리에 누워 제게 손톱만큼도 거들지 못하는 형편이니까요, 가엾은 사람!\""]], [["“You are married, then?”", "\"그럼 결혼하셨군요?\""], ["said the priest, with a show of interest, glancing round as he spoke at the scanty furnishings of the apartment.", "사제가 관심을 보이며 말했다. 말하는 동안 그의 눈길은 방 안의 빈약한 세간을 휘둘러보았다."]], [["“Ah, sir,”", "\"아아, 손님.\""], ["said Caderousse with a sigh, “it is easy to perceive I am not a rich man; but in this world a man does not thrive the better for being honest.”", "카드루스가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"보시다시피 제가 넉넉한 사람이 못 됩니다. 하지만 이 세상에서는 정직하다고 해서 더 잘 풀리는 법이 없지요.\""], ["The abbé fixed on him a searching, penetrating glance.", "사제가 그에게 캐묻는 듯한 날카로운 눈길을 던졌다."]], [["“Yes, honest—I can certainly say that much for myself,”", "\"네, 정직하다는 것—적어도 제 입으로 그만큼은 떳떳이 말할 수 있습니다.\""], ["continued the innkeeper, fairly sustaining the scrutiny of the abbé’s gaze; “I can boast with truth of being an honest man; and,”", "여관 주인이 사제의 응시를 제법 의젓하게 견뎌 내며 말을 이었다. \"저는 정직한 사람이라고 사실대로 자랑할 수 있습니다. 그리고,\""], ["continued he significantly, with a hand on his breast and shaking his head, “that is more than everyone can say nowadays.” 0327m", "그가 한 손을 가슴에 얹고 고개를 절레절레 저으며 의미심장하게 덧붙였다. \"그건 요즘 세상에 아무나 할 수 있는 말이 아니지요.\""]], [["“So much the better for you, if what you assert be true,”", "\"하신 말씀이 사실이라면, 그건 당신에게 잘된 일이오.\""], ["said the abbé; “for I am firmly persuaded that, sooner or later, the good will be rewarded, and the wicked punished.”", "사제가 말했다. \"나는 늦든 빠르든 선한 자는 상을 받고 악한 자는 벌을 받는다고 굳게 믿고 있으니까요.\""]], [["“Such words as those belong to your profession,”", "\"그런 말씀이야 신부님 직업에 어울리는 것이지요.\""], ["answered Caderousse, “and you do well to repeat them; but,”", "카드루스가 대답했다. \"그러니 신부님이 거듭 말씀하시는 것도 당연합니다. 하지만,\""], ["added he, with a bitter expression of countenance, “one is free to believe them or not, as one pleases.”", "그가 씁쓸한 표정을 지으며 덧붙였다. \"그 말을 믿고 안 믿고는 사람 마음에 달린 거지요.\""]], [["“You are wrong to speak thus,”", "\"그렇게 말하는 건 잘못이오.\""], ["said the abbé; “and perhaps I may, in my own person, be able to prove to you how completely you are in error.”", "사제가 말했다. \"그리고 어쩌면 내가 직접, 당신이 얼마나 철저히 틀렸는지 증명해 보일 수 있을지도 모르오.\""]], [["“What mean you?” inquired Caderousse with a look of surprise.", "\"무슨 말씀이신지요?\" 카드루스가 놀란 얼굴로 물었다."]], [["“In the first place, I must be satisfied that you are the person I am in search of.”", "\"우선, 당신이 내가 찾는 그 사람이 맞는지부터 확인해야겠소.\""]], [["“What proofs do you require?”", "\"무슨 증거가 필요하십니까?\""]], [["“Did you, in the year 1814 or 1815, know anything of a young sailor named Dantès?”", "\"1814년이나 1815년에, 당테스라는 젊은 선원에 대해 무얼 좀 아셨소?\""]], [["“Dantès?", "\"당테스요?"], ["Did I know poor dear Edmond?", "가엾고 그리운 에드몽을 알았느냐고요?"], ["Why, Edmond Dantès and myself were intimate friends!”", "아니, 에드몽 당테스와 저는 둘도 없는 친구 사이였습니다!\""], ["exclaimed Caderousse, whose countenance flushed darkly as he caught the penetrating gaze of the abbé fixed on him, while the clear, calm eye of the questioner seemed to dilate with feverish scrutiny.", "카드루스가 외쳤다. 그러나 사제의 꿰뚫는 듯한 눈길이 자기에게 박혀 있는 것을 알아채자 그의 얼굴은 검붉게 달아올랐고, 한편 묻는 이의 맑고 차분한 눈은 열에 들뜬 듯 응시하며 점점 커지는 것 같았다."]], [["“You remind me,”", "\"그러고 보니,\""], ["said the priest, “that the young man concerning whom I asked you was said to bear the name of Edmond.”", "신부가 말했다. \"내가 물은 그 젊은이가 에드몽이라는 이름으로 불렸다고 했던 게 떠오르는군요.\""]], [["“Said to bear the name!”", "\"불렸다니요!\""], ["repeated Caderousse, becoming excited and eager.", "카드루스가 흥분하여 안달하며 거듭 말했다."], ["“Why, he was so called as truly as I myself bore the appellation of Gaspard Caderousse; but tell me, I pray, what has become of poor Edmond?", "\"아니, 제가 가스파르 카드루스라는 이름을 가진 게 분명한 것만큼이나, 그도 분명 그렇게 불렸습니다. 그런데 부디 말씀해 주십시오, 가엾은 에드몽은 어찌 되었습니까?"], ["Did you know him?", "그를 아셨습니까?"], ["Is he alive and at liberty?", "살아서 자유의 몸이 되었나요?"], ["Is he prosperous and happy?”", "잘되어 행복하게 지냅니까?\""]], [["“He died a more wretched, hopeless, heart-broken prisoner than the felons who pay the penalty of their crimes at the galleys of Toulon.”", "\"그는 툴롱의 갤리선에서 죗값을 치르는 죄수들보다도 더 비참하고 가망 없이, 가슴이 찢어진 채 죽었소.\""]], [["A deadly pallor followed the flush on the countenance of Caderousse, who turned away, and the priest saw him wiping the tears from his eyes with the corner of the red handkerchief twisted round his head.", "카드루스의 얼굴에서 붉은빛이 가시고 죽은 사람처럼 핏기가 가셨다. 그는 고개를 돌렸고, 사제는 그가 머리에 둘둘 감은 붉은 손수건 귀퉁이로 눈물을 훔치는 것을 보았다."]], [["“Poor fellow, poor fellow!”", "\"가엾은 친구, 가엾은 친구!\""], ["murmured Caderousse.", "카드루스가 중얼거렸다."], ["“Well, there, sir, is another proof that good people are never rewarded on this earth, and that none but the wicked prosper. Ah,”", "\"자, 보십시오, 손님, 이거야말로 착한 사람들은 이 땅에서 결코 보답받지 못하고, 악한 자들만 잘 풀린다는 또 하나의 증거가 아니겠습니까. 아아,\""], ["continued Caderousse, speaking in the highly colored language of the South, “the world grows worse and worse.", "카드루스가 남부 특유의 과장된 말투로 말을 이었다. \"세상은 갈수록 험악해집니다."], ["Why does not God, if he really hates the wicked, as he is said to do, send down brimstone and fire, and consume them altogether?”", "하느님이 정말로 사람들이 말하듯 악한 자를 미워하신다면, 어째서 유황불을 내려 그것들을 죄다 태워 버리지 않으시는 걸까요?\""]], [["“You speak as though you had loved this young Dantès,” observed the abbé, without taking any notice of his companion’s vehemence.", "\"이 젊은 당테스를 무척 아꼈던 것처럼 말씀하시는군요.\" 사제가 상대의 격앙된 태도에는 아랑곳없이 말했다."]], [["“And so I did,”", "\"그랬지요.\""], ["replied Caderousse; “though once, I confess, I envied him his good fortune.", "카드루스가 대답했다. \"한때는, 솔직히, 그의 좋은 팔자를 시샘한 적도 있었습니다."], ["But I swear to you, sir, I swear to you, by everything a man holds dear, I have, since then, deeply and sincerely lamented his unhappy fate.”", "하지만 맹세컨대, 손님, 사람이 소중히 여기는 모든 것을 두고 맹세컨대, 저는 그 뒤로 그의 불행한 운명을 깊고 진심으로 애통해했습니다.\""]], [["There was a brief silence, during which the fixed, searching eye of the abbé was employed in scrutinizing the agitated features of the innkeeper.", "잠시 침묵이 흘렀고, 그동안 사제의 못 박힌 듯한 탐색의 눈은 여관 주인의 동요하는 얼굴을 샅샅이 살피고 있었다."]], [["“You knew the poor lad, then?”", "\"그럼 그 가엾은 친구를 아셨던 거군요?\""], ["continued Caderousse.", "카드루스가 말을 이었다."]], [["“I was called to see him on his dying bed, that I might administer to him the consolations of religion.”", "\"그가 죽어 가는 자리에 불려 가, 종교의 위안을 베풀어 주었소.\""]], [["“And of what did he die?”", "\"무엇 때문에 죽었습니까?\""], ["asked Caderousse in a choking voice.", "카드루스가 목멘 소리로 물었다."]], [["“Of what, think you, do young and strong men die in prison, when they have scarcely numbered their thirtieth year, unless it be of imprisonment?”", "\"젊고 건강한 사람이, 서른도 채 못 넘기고 감옥에서 죽는다면, 그게 옥살이 말고 무엇 때문이겠소?\""], ["Caderousse wiped away the large beads of perspiration that gathered on his brow. 0329m", "카드루스가 이마에 맺힌 굵은 땀방울을 닦았다."]], [["“But the strangest part of the story is,”", "\"하지만 이 이야기에서 가장 이상한 대목은,\""], ["resumed the abbé, “that Dantès, even in his dying moments, swore by his crucified Redeemer, that he was utterly ignorant of the cause of his detention.”", "사제가 다시 말을 이었다. \"당테스가 죽어 가는 순간에도, 십자가에 못 박히신 구세주를 두고, 자기가 어째서 갇혔는지 그 까닭을 도무지 모른다고 맹세했다는 거요.\""]], [["“And so he was,” murmured Caderousse.", "\"그야 정말 그랬으니까요.\" 카드루스가 중얼거렸다."], ["“How should he have been otherwise? Ah, sir, the poor fellow told you the truth.”", "\"어떻게 알 수가 있었겠습니까? 아아, 손님, 그 가엾은 친구는 신부님께 사실대로 말한 겁니다.\""]], [["“And for that reason, he besought me to try and clear up a mystery he had never been able to penetrate, and to clear his memory should any foul spot or stain have fallen on it.”", "\"그래서 그는, 자기가 끝내 풀지 못한 수수께끼를 밝혀 달라고, 그리고 혹시 자기 명예에 더러운 오점이나 얼룩이 묻었다면 그것을 씻어 달라고 내게 간곡히 부탁했소.\""]], [["And here the look of the abbé, becoming more and more fixed, seemed to rest with ill-concealed satisfaction on the gloomy depression which was rapidly spreading over the countenance of Caderousse.", "그러면서 사제의 눈길은 점점 더 못 박힌 듯해지더니, 카드루스의 얼굴에 빠르게 번져 가는 음울한 침울함 위에 좀처럼 감추지 못한 만족감을 띤 채 머무는 듯했다."]], [["“A rich Englishman,” continued the abbé, “who had been his companion in misfortune, but had been released from prison during the second restoration, was possessed of a diamond of immense value; this jewel he bestowed on Dantès upon himself quitting the prison, as a mark of his gratitude for the kindness and brotherly care with which Dantès had nursed him in a severe illness he underwent during his confinement. Instead of employing this diamond in attempting to bribe his jailers, who might only have taken it and then betrayed him to the governor, Dantès carefully preserved it, that in the event of his getting out of prison he might have wherewithal to live, for the sale of such a diamond would have quite sufficed to make his fortune.”", "\"한 부유한 영국인이,\" 사제가 말을 이었다. \"불행을 함께 겪은 그의 동료였는데, 두 번째 왕정복고 때 감옥에서 풀려났소. 그자는 어마어마한 값어치의 다이아몬드를 지니고 있었지요. 그는 감옥을 떠나면서 그 보석을 당테스에게 주었소. 갇혀 지내는 동안 중병을 앓았을 때, 당테스가 형제처럼 살뜰히 보살펴 준 친절에 대한 고마움의 표시였소. 당테스는 그 다이아몬드를 간수들을 매수하는 데 쓰지 않았소. 그자들은 그것만 챙기고 그를 소장에게 밀고했을지도 모르니까요. 대신 그는 그것을 고이 간직했소. 혹여 감옥에서 나가게 되면 살아갈 밑천으로 삼으려고 말이오. 그런 다이아몬드를 팔면 한밑천 톡톡히 잡기에 충분했을 테니까요.\""]], [["“Then, I suppose,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["asked Caderousse, with eager, glowing looks, “that it was a stone of immense value?”", "카드루스가 눈을 번뜩이며 간절하게 물었다. \"어마어마한 값어치의 보석이었단 말씀이지요?\""]], [["“Why, everything is relative,”", "\"그야 다 상대적인 거지요.\""], ["answered the abbé.", "사제가 대답했다."], ["“To one in Edmond’s position the diamond certainly was of great value.", "\"에드몽 같은 처지에 놓인 사람에게야, 그 다이아몬드는 분명 대단한 값어치가 있었소."], ["It was estimated at fifty thousand francs.”", "오만 프랑은 나가는 것으로 매겨졌으니까요.\""]], [["“Bless me!”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["exclaimed Caderousse, “fifty thousand francs!", "카드루스가 외쳤다. \"오만 프랑이라니!"], ["Surely the diamond was as large as a nut to be worth all that.”", "그만한 값이 나가려면 다이아몬드가 호두만큼은 컸겠군요.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니오.\""], ["replied the abbé, “it was not of such a size as that; but you shall judge for yourself.", "사제가 대답했다. \"그렇게 큰 건 아니었소. 하지만 직접 보고 판단하시오."], ["I have it with me.”", "내가 그걸 지니고 있으니까.\""]], [["The sharp gaze of Caderousse was instantly directed towards the priest’s garments, as though hoping to discover the location of the treasure.", "카드루스의 날카로운 눈길이 그 보물이 어디 있는지 찾아내기라도 하려는 듯, 곧장 사제의 옷자락으로 향했다."], ["Calmly drawing forth from his pocket a small box covered with black shagreen, the abbé opened it, and displayed to the dazzled eyes of Caderousse the sparkling jewel it contained, set in a ring of admirable workmanship.", "사제는 주머니에서 검은 가죽으로 싼 작은 갑을 차분히 꺼내 열고는, 그 안에 든 반짝이는 보석을 카드루스의 휘둥그레진 눈앞에 드러내 보였다. 보석은 솜씨가 빼어난 반지에 박혀 있었다."]], [["“And that diamond,”", "\"그래서 그 다이아몬드가,\""], ["cried Caderousse, almost breathless with eager admiration, “you say, is worth fifty thousand francs?”", "카드루스가 간절한 감탄에 숨이 막힐 지경으로 외쳤다. \"오만 프랑 값어치가 나간다는 말씀이지요?\""]], [["“It is, without the setting, which is also valuable,”", "\"그렇소, 그것도 받침대 값은 빼고 말이오."], ["replied the abbé, as he closed the box, and returned it to his pocket, while its brilliant hues seemed still to dance before the eyes of the fascinated innkeeper.", "받침대도 값나가는 것이지만.\" 사제가 갑을 닫아 다시 주머니에 넣으며 대답했다. 그러나 그 찬란한 빛깔은 넋이 나간 여관 주인의 눈앞에 여전히 어른거리는 듯했다."]], [["“But how comes the diamond in your possession, sir?", "\"한데 그 다이아몬드가 어쩌다 신부님 손에 들어왔습니까?"], ["Did Edmond make you his heir?”", "에드몽이 신부님을 상속인으로 삼았나요?\""]], [["“No, merely his testamentary executor.", "\"아니오, 그저 유언 집행인으로 삼았을 뿐이오."], ["‘I once possessed four dear and faithful friends, besides the maiden to whom I was betrothed’ he said; ‘and I feel convinced they have all unfeignedly grieved over my loss.", "'내게는 약혼했던 처녀 말고도, 소중하고 의리 있는 벗이 넷 있었습니다.' 그가 말했소. '그들 모두 내 죽음을 거짓 없이 슬퍼했으리라 믿습니다."], ["The name of one of the four friends is Caderousse.’”", "그 네 벗 가운데 하나가 카드루스입니다.'\""], ["The innkeeper shivered.", "여관 주인이 부르르 떨었다."]], [["“‘Another of the number,’”", "\"'그 가운데 또 하나는,'\""], ["continued the abbé, without seeming to notice the emotion of Caderousse, “‘is called Danglars; and the third, in spite of being my rival, entertained a very sincere affection for me.’”", "사제가 카드루스의 동요는 알아채지 못한 척하며 말을 이었다. \"'당글라르라고 합니다. 그리고 세 번째는, 내 연적이었음에도, 나에게 아주 진심 어린 애정을 품고 있었지요.'\""]], [["A fiendish smile played over the features of Caderousse, who was about to break in upon the abbé’s speech, when the latter, waving his hand, said, “Allow me to finish first, and then if you have any observations to make, you can do so afterwards. ‘The third of my friends, although my rival, was much attached to me,—his name was Fernand; that of my betrothed was’—Stay, stay,” continued the abbé, “I have forgotten what he called her.”", "카드루스의 얼굴에 악마 같은 미소가 스쳤다. 그가 사제의 말을 막 가로채려는 참에, 사제가 손을 내저으며 말했다. \"내가 먼저 끝내게 해 주시오. 그런 다음 할 말이 있거든 그때 하면 되오. '내 세 번째 벗은, 비록 연적이었지만, 나를 무척 따랐습니다. 그의 이름은 페르낭이지요. 그리고 내 약혼녀의 이름은—' 잠깐, 잠깐,\" 사제가 말을 이었다. \"그가 그 처녀를 뭐라고 불렀는지 잊어버렸군요.\""]], [["“Mercédès,” said Caderousse eagerly.", "\"메르세데스입니다.\" 카드루스가 다급히 말했다."]], [["“True,”", "\"맞소.\""], ["said the abbé, with a stifled sigh, “Mercédès it was.”", "사제가 한숨을 억누르며 말했다. \"메르세데스였소.\""]], [["“Go on,” urged Caderousse.", "\"계속하십시오.\" 카드루스가 재촉했다."]], [["“Bring me a _carafe_ of water,”", "\"물 한 병만 갖다주시오.\""], ["said the abbé.", "사제가 말했다."]], [["Caderousse quickly performed the stranger’s bidding; and after pouring some into a glass, and slowly swallowing its contents, the abbé, resuming his usual placidity of manner, said, as he placed his empty glass on the table:", "카드루스가 얼른 낯선 이의 분부를 따랐다. 사제는 잔에 물을 따라 천천히 들이켜고는, 평소의 차분한 태도를 되찾아, 빈 잔을 식탁에 내려놓으며 말했다."]], [["“Where did we leave off?”", "\"어디까지 했더라?\""]], [["“The name of Edmond’s betrothed was Mercédès.”", "\"에드몽 약혼녀의 이름이 메르세데스라고 하셨지요.\""]], [["“To be sure.", "\"그렇지."], ["‘You will go to Marseilles,’ said Dantès,—for you understand, I repeat his words just as he uttered them.", "'자네는 마르세유로 가게.' 당테스가 말했소—내가 그의 말을 그가 한 그대로 옮기는 거니 알아들으셔야 하오."], ["Do you understand?”", "알아듣겠소?\""]], [["“Perfectly.”", "\"잘 알겠습니다.\""]], [["“‘You will sell this diamond; you will divide the money into five equal parts, and give an equal portion to these good friends, the only persons who have loved me upon earth.’”", "\"'자네는 이 다이아몬드를 팔게. 그 돈을 똑같이 다섯 몫으로 나누어, 이 좋은 벗들에게 한 몫씩 똑같이 주게. 이 땅에서 나를 사랑해 준 단 한 사람들이니까.'\""]], [["“But why into five parts?”", "\"한데 왜 다섯 몫입니까?\""], ["asked Caderousse; “you only mentioned four persons.”", "카드루스가 물었다. \"넷밖에 말씀 안 하셨는데요.\""]], [["“Because the fifth is dead, as I hear.", "\"다섯째는 죽었다고 들었기 때문이오."], ["The fifth sharer in Edmond’s bequest, was his own father.”", "에드몽의 유산을 나누어 받을 다섯째는, 바로 그의 아버지였소.\""]], [["“Too true, too true!”", "\"맞습니다, 정말 그렇지요!\""], ["ejaculated Caderousse, almost suffocated by the contending passions which assailed him, “the poor old man did die.”", "카드루스가 밀려드는 갖가지 감정에 숨이 막힐 듯 외쳤다. \"그 가엾은 노인은 정말 돌아가셨지요.\""]], [["“I learned so much at Marseilles,”", "\"그건 마르세유에서 들어 알고 있었소.\""], ["replied the abbé, making a strong effort to appear indifferent; “but from the length of time that has elapsed since the death of the elder Dantès, I was unable to obtain any particulars of his end.", "사제가 애써 무심한 척하며 대답했다. \"하지만 노 당테스가 죽은 지 너무 오랜 세월이 흘러서, 그가 어떻게 최후를 맞았는지 자세한 내막은 알아낼 수가 없었소."], ["Can you enlighten me on that point?”", "그 점에 대해 좀 일러 줄 수 있겠소?\""]], [["“I do not know who could if I could not,”", "\"제가 모른다면 알 사람이 또 어디 있겠습니까.\""], ["said Caderousse.", "카드루스가 말했다."], ["“Why, I lived almost on the same floor with the poor old man.", "\"아니, 저는 그 가엾은 노인과 거의 같은 층에서 살았는걸요."], ["Ah, yes, about a year after the disappearance of his son the poor old man died.”", "아아, 그렇지요, 아들이 종적을 감추고 한 해쯤 지나서, 그 가엾은 노인은 돌아가셨습니다.\""]], [["“Of what did he die?”", "\"무엇 때문에 돌아가셨소?\""]], [["“Why, the doctors called his complaint gastro-enteritis, I believe; his acquaintances say he died of grief; but I, who saw him in his dying moments, I say he died of——”", "\"글쎄요, 의사들은 그 병을 위장염이라고 불렀던 것 같습니다. 그분을 아는 사람들은 슬픔으로 죽었다고들 하지요. 하지만 그분이 숨을 거두는 순간을 지켜본 저는, 이렇게 말합니다, 그분이 죽은 건—\""]], [["Caderousse paused.", "카드루스가 말을 멈췄다."]], [["“Of what?” asked the priest, anxiously and eagerly.", "\"무엇 때문이오?\" 사제가 애타게 다그치며 물었다."]], [["“Why, of downright starvation.”", "\"굶어서, 그야말로 쫄쫄 굶어서 돌아가셨습니다.\""]], [["“Starvation!”", "\"굶어서라니!\""], ["exclaimed the abbé, springing from his seat.", "사제가 자리에서 벌떡 일어나며 외쳤다."], ["“Why, the vilest animals are not suffered to die by such a death as that.", "\"아니, 가장 천한 짐승도 그런 식으로 죽게 내버려 두지는 않소."], ["The very dogs that wander houseless and homeless in the streets find some pitying hand to cast them a mouthful of bread; and that a man, a Christian, should be allowed to perish of hunger in the midst of other men who call themselves Christians, is too horrible for belief.", "집도 절도 없이 거리를 떠도는 개조차 빵 한 조각이라도 던져 주는 가엾은 손길을 만나는 법이오. 그런데 사람이, 그것도 그리스도교인이, 스스로를 그리스도교인이라 부르는 다른 사람들 한복판에서 굶어 죽도록 내버려졌다니, 너무 끔찍해서 믿을 수가 없소."], ["Oh, it is impossible!—utterly impossible!”", "오, 그럴 리가 없소!—절대로 그럴 리가 없소!\""]], [["“What I have said, I have said,”", "\"제가 말한 건 말한 그대로입니다.\""], ["answered Caderousse.", "카드루스가 대답했다."]], [["“And you are a fool for having said anything about it,”", "\"그런 일을 입에 올리다니 당신은 멍청이야.\""], ["said a voice from the top of the stairs.", "계단 위에서 누군가의 목소리가 들렸다."], ["“Why should you meddle with what does not concern you?”", "\"당신과 상관도 없는 일에 왜 끼어드는 거예요?\""]], [["The two men turned quickly, and saw the sickly countenance of La Carconte peering between the baluster rails; attracted by the sound of voices, she had feebly dragged herself down the stairs, and, seated on the lower step, head on knees, she had listened to the foregoing conversation.", "두 사람이 홱 돌아보니, 난간 살 사이로 라카르콩트의 병색 짙은 얼굴이 들여다보고 있었다. 말소리에 이끌려, 그녀는 기진한 몸을 계단 아래로 간신히 끌고 내려와, 맨 아래 계단에 머리를 무릎에 묻고 앉아, 앞서 오간 대화를 죄다 듣고 있었던 것이다."]], [["“Mind your own business, wife,”", "\"당신 일이나 신경 써, 마누라.\""], ["replied Caderousse sharply.", "카드루스가 쏘아붙였다."], ["“This gentleman asks me for information, which common politeness will not permit me to refuse.”", "\"이분이 내게 물으시니, 사람의 도리상 대답을 안 할 수가 없는 거지.\""]], [["“Politeness, you simpleton!” retorted La Carconte.", "\"도리는 무슨 도리, 이 멍청한 양반아!\""], ["“What have you to do with politeness, I should like to know?", "라카르콩트가 되받아쳤다. \"당신이 도리하고 무슨 상관이람?"], ["Better study a little common prudence.", "차라리 분별이라는 걸 좀 배우는 게 나을걸."], ["How do you know the motives that person may have for trying to extract all he can from you?”", "저 사람이 당신한테서 캐낼 수 있는 건 죄다 캐내려는 속셈이 무엇인지 어떻게 알아요?\""]], [["“I pledge you my word, madam,”", "\"맹세컨대, 부인,\""], ["said the abbé, “that my intentions are good; and that your husband can incur no risk, provided he answers me candidly.”", "사제가 말했다. \"제 의도는 선한 것이며, 바깥양반이 솔직하게만 대답하신다면 어떤 위험도 닥칠 일은 없습니다.\""]], [["“Ah, that’s all very fine,”", "\"흥, 그거야 다 듣기 좋은 말이지요.\""], ["retorted the woman.", "여자가 되받았다."], ["“Nothing is easier than to begin with fair promises and assurances of nothing to fear; but when poor, silly folks, like my husband there, have been persuaded to tell all they know, the promises and assurances of safety are quickly forgotten; and at some moment when nobody is expecting it, behold trouble and misery, and all sorts of persecutions, are heaped on the unfortunate wretches, who cannot even see whence all their afflictions come.”", "\"걱정할 것 없다고 그럴싸하게 약속하고 다짐하는 거야 누군들 못 하겠어요. 하지만 우리 집 양반 같은 가엾고 어리석은 사람들이 아는 걸 죄다 털어놓고 나면, 그 약속이며 안전하다던 다짐이며 금세 다 잊히는 법이지요. 그러다 아무도 예상치 못한 어느 순간에, 보세요, 온갖 곤경과 비참함과 별별 박해가 그 가엾은 사람들 머리 위로 쏟아지는데, 정작 그들은 그 모든 고난이 어디서 오는지조차 알지도 못한단 말이에요.\""]], [["“Nay, nay, my good woman, make yourself perfectly easy, I beg of you.", "\"아니, 아니오, 부인, 부디 마음 푹 놓으십시오."], ["Whatever evils may befall you, they will not be occasioned by my instrumentality, that I solemnly promise you.”", "어떤 화가 닥치든, 그것이 내 손으로 말미암은 것은 결코 아닐 것입니다, 엄숙히 약속드리지요.\""]], [["La Carconte muttered a few inarticulate words, then let her head again drop upon her knees, and went into a fit of ague, leaving the two speakers to resume the conversation, but remaining so as to be able to hear every word they uttered.", "라카르콩트는 알아들을 수 없는 말을 몇 마디 중얼거리더니, 다시 머리를 무릎에 떨구고는 오한에 부르르 떨었다. 그러고는 두 사람이 대화를 다시 잇도록 내버려 두었지만, 그들이 주고받는 말을 한마디도 빠짐없이 들을 수 있는 자리에 그대로 남아 있었다."], ["Again the abbé had been obliged to swallow a draught of water to calm the emotions that threatened to overpower him.", "사제는 또다시 자기를 압도하려는 감정을 가라앉히려고 물을 한 모금 들이켜야 했다."]], [["When he had sufficiently recovered himself, he said, “It appears, then, that the miserable old man you were telling me of was forsaken by everyone.", "마음을 충분히 추스른 뒤, 그가 말했다. \"그렇다면, 당신이 말한 그 가엾은 노인은 모두에게 버림받은 모양이군요."], ["Surely, had not such been the case, he would not have perished by so dreadful a death.”", "그렇지 않고서야, 그리 끔찍한 죽음으로 스러지지는 않았을 테니 말이오.\""]], [["“Why, he was not altogether forsaken,”", "\"아니, 아주 버림받은 건 아니었습니다.\""], ["continued Caderousse, “for Mercédès the Catalan and Monsieur Morrel were very kind to him; but somehow the poor old man had contracted a profound hatred for Fernand—the very person,”", "카드루스가 말을 이었다. \"카탈루냐 처녀 메르세데스와 모렐 씨가 그분께 무척 잘해 드렸으니까요. 다만 어쩐 일인지 그 가엾은 노인은 페르낭을 뼛속 깊이 미워하게 되셨지요."], ["added Caderousse with a bitter smile, “that you named just now as being one of Dantès’ faithful and attached friends.”", "바로 그 사람,\" 카드루스가 씁쓸한 미소를 지으며 덧붙였다. \"신부님이 방금 당테스의 의리 있고 충실한 벗 가운데 하나로 꼽으신 그 사람 말입니다.\""]], [["“And was he not so?” asked the abbé.", "\"그가 정말 그런 벗이 아니었단 말이오?\" 사제가 물었다."]], [["“Gaspard, Gaspard!”", "\"가스파르, 가스파르!\""], ["murmured the woman, from her seat on the stairs, “mind what you are saying!”", "계단에 앉은 여자가 중얼거렸다. \"말조심해요!\""]], [["Caderousse made no reply to these words, though evidently irritated and annoyed by the interruption, but, addressing the abbé, said, “Can a man be faithful to another whose wife he covets and desires for himself?", "카드루스는 끼어드는 말에 짜증과 거슬림이 역력했지만, 이 말에는 대꾸하지 않고 사제를 향해 말했다. \"남의 아내를 탐내고 제 것으로 삼고 싶어 하는 자에게, 사람이 어찌 의리를 지킬 수 있겠습니까?"], ["But Dantès was so honorable and true in his own nature, that he believed everybody’s professions of friendship.", "하지만 당테스는 천성이 워낙 곧고 진실해서, 누구든 친구라고 하는 사람의 말을 다 믿었지요."], ["Poor Edmond, he was cruelly deceived; but it was fortunate that he never knew, or he might have found it more difficult, when on his deathbed, to pardon his enemies.", "가엾은 에드몽, 그는 잔인하게 속았습니다. 하지만 그가 끝내 몰랐던 게 차라리 다행이었지요. 안 그랬다면 죽음의 자리에서 원수들을 용서하기가 더 어려웠을 테니까요."], ["And, whatever people may say,”", "그리고 사람들이 뭐라고 하든,\""], ["continued Caderousse, in his native language, which was not altogether devoid of rude poetry, “I cannot help being more frightened at the idea of the malediction of the dead than the hatred of the living.”", "카드루스가 거친 시정이 없지 않은 제 고장 말씨로 말을 이었다. \"저는 산 자의 미움보다 죽은 자의 저주가 더 무섭게 느껴지는 걸 어쩔 수가 없습니다.\""]], [["“Imbecile!” exclaimed La Carconte.", "\"멍청한 양반!\" 라카르콩트가 외쳤다."]], [["“Do you, then, know in what manner Fernand injured Dantès?”", "\"그렇다면, 페르낭이 어떤 식으로 당테스를 해쳤는지 당신은 알고 있소?\""], ["inquired the abbé of Caderousse.", "사제가 카드루스에게 물었다."]], [["“Do I? No one better.”", "\"제가요? 저보다 더 잘 아는 사람은 없지요.\""]], [["“Speak out then, say what it was!”", "\"그럼 어서 말해 보시오, 그게 무엇이었는지!\""]], [["“Gaspard!”", "\"가스파르!\""], ["cried La Carconte, “do as you will; you are master—but if you take my advice you’ll hold your tongue.”", "라카르콩트가 외쳤다. \"맘대로 해요. 당신이 주인이니까—하지만 내 충고를 듣겠거든 입 다물고 있는 게 좋을걸.\""]], [["“Well, wife,”", "\"음, 마누라,\""], ["replied Caderousse, “I don’t know but what you’re right!”", "카드루스가 대답했다. \"당신 말이 옳을지도 모르겠군!\""]], [["“So you will say nothing?” asked the abbé.", "\"그럼 아무 말도 안 하시겠다는 거요?\" 사제가 물었다."]], [["“Why, what good would it do?”", "\"그래 봤자 무슨 소용이 있겠습니까?\""], ["asked Caderousse.", "카드루스가 물었다."], ["“If the poor lad were living, and came to me and begged that I would candidly tell which were his true and which his false friends, why, perhaps, I should not hesitate.", "\"그 가엾은 친구가 살아 있어서, 제게 와서 누가 참된 벗이고 누가 거짓된 벗인지 솔직히 말해 달라고 사정한다면, 글쎄요, 어쩌면 저도 망설이지 않았겠지요."], ["But you tell me he is no more, and therefore can have nothing to do with hatred or revenge, so let all such feeling be buried with him.”", "하지만 신부님 말씀으로는 그가 이미 이 세상 사람이 아니라니, 미움이니 복수니 하는 것과는 아무 상관도 없게 된 거지요. 그러니 그런 감정일랑 죄다 그와 함께 묻어 둡시다.\""]], [["“You prefer, then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["said the abbé, “that I should bestow on men you say are false and treacherous, the reward intended for faithful friendship?”", "사제가 말했다. \"당신 말로는 거짓되고 음흉하다는 자들에게, 의리 있는 우정에 돌아갈 보답을 내가 베풀어 주는 편이 낫겠다는 거요?\""]], [["“That is true enough,”", "\"그건 정말 맞는 말씀입니다.\""], ["returned Caderousse.", "카드루스가 대답했다."], ["“You say truly, the gift of poor Edmond was not meant for such traitors as Fernand and Danglars; besides, what would it be to them?", "\"옳은 말씀이지요, 가엾은 에드몽의 선물은 페르낭이나 당글라르 같은 배신자들을 위한 게 아니었습니다. 게다가, 그자들에게 그게 무슨 대수겠습니까?"], ["no more than a drop of water in the ocean.”", "바다에 물 한 방울 떨어뜨리는 거나 다를 바 없지요.\""]], [["“Remember,”", "\"잊지 마세요,\""], ["chimed in La Carconte, “those two could crush you at a single blow!”", "라카르콩트가 끼어들었다. \"그 둘은 당신쯤은 단번에 짓뭉개 버릴 수 있는 사람들이라고요!\""]], [["“How so?” inquired the abbé.", "\"어째서요?\" 사제가 물었다."], ["“Are these persons, then, so rich and powerful?”", "\"그렇다면 그자들이 그토록 부유하고 권세가 있단 말이오?\""]], [["“Do you not know their history?”", "\"그자들 내력을 모르신단 말입니까?\""]], [["“I do not.", "\"모르오."], ["Pray relate it to me!”", "부디 내게 들려주시오!\""]], [["Caderousse seemed to reflect for a few moments, then said, “No, truly, it would take up too much time.”", "카드루스는 잠시 생각에 잠기는 듯하더니 말했다. \"아니요, 정말이지, 그러자면 시간이 너무 많이 걸립니다.\""]], [["“Well, my good friend,”", "\"그럼, 이보시오,\""], ["returned the abbé, in a tone that indicated utter indifference on his part, “you are at liberty, either to speak or be silent, just as you please; for my own part, I respect your scruples and admire your sentiments; so let the matter end.", "사제가 자기로서는 전혀 관심 없다는 듯한 어조로 대꾸했다. \"말을 하든 입을 다물든, 그건 당신 마음이오. 나로서는 당신의 망설임을 존중하고 당신의 마음 씀씀이에 감탄할 따름이니, 이 일은 이쯤에서 끝냅시다."], ["I shall do my duty as conscientiously as I can, and fulfil my promise to the dying man.", "나는 양심껏 내 의무를 다하고, 죽어 가는 이에게 한 약속을 지킬 것이오."], ["My first business will be to dispose of this diamond.”", "우선 이 다이아몬드를 처분하는 일부터 시작해야겠소.\""]], [["So saying, the abbé again drew the small box from his pocket, opened it, and contrived to hold it in such a light, that a bright flash of brilliant hues passed before the dazzled gaze of Caderousse.", "그렇게 말하며 사제는 다시 주머니에서 작은 갑을 꺼내 열고는, 빛이 잘 들도록 요리조리 손에 쥐어, 찬란한 빛깔의 환한 섬광이 카드루스의 휘둥그레진 눈앞을 스쳐 가게 했다."]], [["“Wife, wife!”", "\"마누라, 마누라!\""], ["cried he in a hoarse voice, “come here!”", "그가 쉰 목소리로 외쳤다. \"이리 좀 와 봐!\""]], [["“Diamond!”", "\"다이아몬드!\""], ["exclaimed La Carconte, rising and descending to the chamber with a tolerably firm step; “what diamond are you talking about?”", "라카르콩트가 자리에서 일어나 제법 단단한 걸음걸이로 방으로 내려오며 외쳤다. \"무슨 다이아몬드 얘기를 하는 거예요?\""]], [["“Why, did you not hear all we said?”", "\"아니, 우리가 한 얘기를 다 듣지 않았어?\""], ["inquired Caderousse.", "카드루스가 물었다."], ["“It is a beautiful diamond left by poor Edmond Dantès, to be sold, and the money divided between his father, Mercédès, his betrothed bride, Fernand, Danglars, and myself.", "\"가엾은 에드몽 당테스가 남긴 아름다운 다이아몬드야. 이걸 팔아서, 그 돈을 그의 아버지와 약혼녀 메르세데스, 페르낭, 당글라르, 그리고 나, 이렇게 나눠 가지라는 거지."], ["The jewel is worth at least fifty thousand francs.”", "이 보석이 적어도 오만 프랑 값어치가 나간대.\""]], [["“Oh, what a magnificent jewel!”", "\"오, 정말 굉장한 보석이네!\""], ["cried the astonished woman.", "깜짝 놀란 여자가 외쳤다."]], [["“The fifth part of the profits from this stone belongs to us then, does it not?”", "\"그럼 이 보석에서 나오는 돈의 오분의 일이 우리 몫이라는 거지, 안 그래?\""], ["asked Caderousse.", "카드루스가 물었다."]], [["“It does,”", "\"그렇소.\""], ["replied the abbé; “with the addition of an equal division of that part intended for the elder Dantès, which I believe myself at liberty to divide equally with the four survivors.”", "사제가 대답했다. \"거기에 노 당테스 몫으로 정해진 부분도 똑같이 나눌 텐데, 나는 그 몫을 살아남은 네 사람에게 고루 나누어도 괜찮다고 생각하오.\""]], [["“And why among us four?” inquired Caderousse.", "\"그런데 왜 우리 넷에게 나눠 줍니까?\" 카드루스가 물었다."]], [["“As being the friends Edmond esteemed most faithful and devoted to him.”", "\"에드몽이 자기에게 가장 의리 있고 헌신적이었다고 여긴 벗들이니까요.\""]], [["“I don’t call those friends who betray and ruin you,”", "\"배신하고 망쳐 먹는 자들을 친구라고 부를 수는 없지요.\""], ["murmured the wife in her turn, in a low, muttering voice.", "이번에는 아내가 낮게 웅얼대는 목소리로 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Of course not!”", "\"물론이지!\""], ["rejoined Caderousse quickly; “no more do I, and that was what I was observing to this gentleman just now.", "카드루스가 얼른 맞장구쳤다. \"나도 그렇게 생각해, 방금 이분께 내가 한 말이 바로 그거였어."], ["I said I looked upon it as a sacrilegious profanation to reward treachery, perhaps crime.”", "나는 배신을, 어쩌면 죄악을 보답하는 게 신성모독이나 다름없다고 생각한다고 말했지.\""]], [["“Remember,” answered the abbé calmly, as he replaced the jewel and its case in the pocket of his cassock, “it is your fault, not mine, that I do so.", "\"잊지 마시오,\" 사제가 보석과 그 갑을 사제복 주머니에 도로 넣으며 차분히 대답했다."], ["You will have the goodness to furnish me with the address of both Fernand and Danglars, in order that I may execute Edmond’s last wishes.”", "\"내가 그러는 건 내 탓이 아니라 당신 탓이오. 에드몽의 마지막 소원을 이룰 수 있도록, 페르낭과 당글라르 두 사람의 주소를 좀 일러 주시오.\""]], [["The agitation of Caderousse became extreme, and large drops of perspiration rolled from his heated brow.", "카드루스의 동요는 극에 달했고, 달아오른 이마에서 굵은 땀방울이 굴러떨어졌다."], ["As he saw the abbé rise from his seat and go towards the door, as though to ascertain if his horse were sufficiently refreshed to continue his journey, Caderousse and his wife exchanged looks of deep meaning.", "사제가 자리에서 일어나, 말이 길을 다시 떠날 만큼 충분히 쉬었는지 확인하려는 듯 문 쪽으로 걸어가자, 카드루스와 그의 아내는 깊은 속뜻이 담긴 눈길을 주고받았다."]], [["0335m\n“There, you see, wife,”", "\"자, 봐, 마누라,\" 카드루스가 말했다."], ["said the former, “this splendid diamond might all be ours, if we chose!”", "\"우리가 마음만 먹으면, 이 굉장한 다이아몬드가 죄다 우리 것이 될 수도 있다고!\""]], [["“Do you believe it?”", "\"그 말을 믿어요?\""]], [["“Why, surely a man of his holy profession would not deceive us!”", "\"아니, 저렇게 거룩한 직업을 가진 분이 설마 우릴 속이겠어!\""]], [["“Well,” replied La Carconte, “do as you like.", "\"그래요,\" 라카르콩트가 대답했다."], ["For my part, I wash my hands of the affair.”", "\"당신 좋을 대로 해요. 나는 이 일에서 손 떼겠어요.\""]], [["So saying, she once more climbed the staircase leading to her chamber, her body convulsed with chills, and her teeth rattling in her head, in spite of the intense heat of the weather.", "그렇게 말하며 그녀는 다시 제 방으로 통하는 계단을 올라갔다. 날씨가 그토록 무더운데도 그녀의 몸은 오한으로 떨렸고, 이가 딱딱 부딪쳤다."], ["Arrived at the top stair, she turned round, and called out, in a warning tone, to her husband, “Gaspard, consider well what you are about to do!”", "맨 위 계단에 다다르자, 그녀는 돌아서서 경고하는 어조로 남편을 향해 소리쳤다. \"가스파르, 당신이 무슨 짓을 하려는지 잘 생각해 봐요!\""]], [["“I have both reflected and decided,”", "\"이미 생각도 하고 마음도 정했어.\""], ["answered he.", "그가 대답했다."]], [["La Carconte then entered her chamber, the flooring of which creaked beneath her heavy, uncertain tread, as she proceeded towards her armchair, into which she fell as though exhausted.", "라카르콩트는 그러고서 제 방으로 들어갔다. 무겁고 휘청대는 발걸음에 방바닥이 삐걱거렸고, 그녀는 안락의자 쪽으로 가더니 기진한 듯 의자에 풀썩 주저앉았다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"자,\""], ["asked the abbé, as he returned to the apartment below, “what have you made up your mind to do?”", "아래층 방으로 돌아온 사제가 물었다. \"어떻게 하기로 마음을 정했소?\""]], [["“To tell you all I know,”", "\"제가 아는 걸 죄다 말씀드리지요.\""], ["was the reply.", "그가 대답했다."]], [["“I certainly think you act wisely in so doing,”", "\"분명, 그렇게 하시는 게 현명한 처사라고 생각하오.\""], ["said the priest.", "신부가 말했다."], ["“Not because I have the least desire to learn anything you may please to conceal from me, but simply that if, through your assistance, I could distribute the legacy according to the wishes of the testator, why, so much the better, that is all.”", "\"내게 감추고 싶은 무언가를 알아내고 싶은 마음이 손톱만큼이라도 있어서가 아니라, 그저 당신의 도움으로 유언한 이의 뜻대로 유산을 나눠 줄 수 있다면, 글쎄, 그야말로 더없이 좋은 일이니, 그뿐이오.\""]], [["“I hope it may be so,”", "\"그렇게 되기를 바랍니다.\""], ["replied Caderousse, his face flushed with cupidity.", "카드루스가 탐욕으로 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했다."]], [["“I am all attention,”", "\"귀 기울여 듣겠소.\""], ["said the abbé.", "사제가 말했다."]], [["“Stop a minute,”", "\"잠깐만요.\""], ["answered Caderousse; “we might be interrupted in the most interesting part of my story, which would be a pity; and it is as well that your visit hither should be made known only to ourselves.”", "카드루스가 대답했다. \"이야기의 가장 흥미로운 대목에서 방해를 받을 수도 있으니, 그러면 아까울 테고요. 그리고 신부님이 여길 다녀가셨다는 건 우리끼리만 아는 게 좋겠습니다.\""]], [["With these words he went stealthily to the door, which he closed, and, by way of still greater precaution, bolted and barred it, as he was accustomed to do at night.", "이렇게 말하며 그는 살그머니 문으로 가서 문을 닫고는, 한층 더 단단히 조심하느라, 밤마다 하던 대로 빗장을 지르고 가로대를 걸었다."]], [["During this time the abbé had chosen his place for listening at his ease.", "그동안 사제는 마음 편히 이야기를 들을 자리를 골라 두었다."], ["He removed his seat into a corner of the room, where he himself would be in deep shadow, while the light would be fully thrown on the narrator; then, with head bent down and hands clasped, or rather clenched together, he prepared to give his whole attention to Caderousse, who seated himself on the little stool, exactly opposite to him.", "그는 자기 자리를 방구석으로 옮겨, 자신은 깊은 그늘에 들고 이야기하는 이에게는 빛이 환히 비치도록 했다. 그러고는 고개를 숙이고 두 손을 맞잡은 채—아니, 차라리 꽉 움켜쥔 채—제 맞은편 작은 걸상에 앉은 카드루스에게 온 정신을 쏟을 채비를 했다."]], [["“Remember, this is no affair of mine,”", "\"이건 내 일이 아니라는 거 잊지 마.\""], ["said the trembling voice of La Carconte, as though through the flooring of her chamber she viewed the scene that was enacting below.", "라카르콩트가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. 마치 자기 방 마룻바닥 너머로 아래에서 벌어지는 장면을 지켜보기라도 하는 것처럼."]], [["“Enough, enough!”", "“됐어요, 됐어!”"], ["replied Caderousse; “say no more about it; I will take all the consequences upon myself.”", "카드루스가 대답했다. “그 이야기는 그만하시죠. 모든 결과는 제가 책임지겠습니다.”"], ["And he began his story.", "그러고는 이야기를 시작했다."]], [["First, sir,”", "“우선, 선생님,”"], ["said Caderousse, “you must make me a promise.”", "카드루스가 말했다. “제게 한 가지 약속을 해 주셔야 합니다.”"]], [["“What is that?” inquired the abbé.", "“무슨 약속이오?” 신부가 물었다."]], [["“Why, if you ever make use of the details I am about to give you, that you will never let anyone know that it was I who supplied them; for the persons of whom I am about to talk are rich and powerful, and if they only laid the tips of their fingers on me, I should break to pieces like glass.”", "“그러니까, 제가 지금부터 말씀드리는 이야기를 혹시라도 쓰시게 되더라도, 그 내용을 누구에게 알려 준 사람이 저라는 걸 절대 발설하지 않으시겠다는 약속 말입니다. 제가 이제부터 말할 사람들은 부유하고 권세가 있는 자들이라, 그자들이 손가락 끝만 제게 갖다 대도 저는 유리처럼 산산조각이 나고 말 테니까요.”"]], [["“Make yourself easy, my friend,”", "“안심하시오, 벗이여.”"], ["replied the abbé.", "신부가 대답했다."], ["“I am a priest, and confessions die in my breast.", "“나는 사제이고, 고해는 내 가슴속에서 함께 묻힙니다."], ["Recollect, our only desire is to carry out, in a fitting manner, the last wishes of our friend.", "명심하시오, 우리의 유일한 바람은 우리 벗의 마지막 소원을 합당한 방식으로 이루는 것뿐이오. 그러니 거리낌 없이, 또 미움 없이 말하시오. 진실을, 오직 진실만을 말하시오."], ["Speak, then, without reserve, as without hatred; tell the truth, the whole truth; I do not know, never may know, the persons of whom you are about to speak; besides, I am an Italian, and not a Frenchman, and belong to God, and not to man, and I shall shortly retire to my convent, which I have only quitted to fulfil the last wishes of a dying man.”", "나는 그대가 말할 사람들을 알지 못하고, 앞으로도 결코 알 일이 없을 것이오. 게다가 나는 프랑스인이 아니라 이탈리아인이며, 사람이 아니라 하느님께 속한 몸이오. 머지않아 나는 수도원으로 돌아갈 것인데, 임종을 맞는 사람의 마지막 소원을 이루기 위해 잠시 떠나왔을 뿐이오.”"]], [["This positive assurance seemed to give Caderousse a little courage.", "이 분명한 다짐이 카드루스에게 약간의 용기를 주는 듯했다."]], [["“Well, then, under these circumstances,”", "“그렇다면, 이런 사정이라면 말이지요,”"], ["said Caderousse, “I will, I even believe I ought to undeceive you as to the friendship which poor Edmond thought so sincere and unquestionable.”", "카드루스가 말했다. “말씀드리겠습니다, 아니, 마땅히 말씀드려야겠지요. 가엾은 에드몽이 그토록 진실하고 의심할 여지 없다고 믿었던 우정에 대해, 선생님의 오해를 풀어 드려야겠습니다.”"]], [["“Begin with his father, if you please.”", "“괜찮다면 그의 아버지 이야기부터 시작해 주시오.”"], ["said the abbé; “Edmond talked to me a great deal about the old man for whom he had the deepest love.”", "신부가 말했다. “에드몽은 자기가 가장 깊이 사랑한 그 노인에 대해 내게 무척 많은 이야기를 했소.”"]], [["“The history is a sad one, sir,”", "“슬픈 이야기입니다, 선생님.”"], ["said Caderousse, shaking his head; “perhaps you know all the earlier part of it?”", "카드루스가 고개를 저으며 말했다. “앞부분은 아마 다 알고 계시겠지요?”"]], [["“Yes.”", "“그렇소.”"], ["answered the abbé; “Edmond related to me everything until the moment when he was arrested in a small cabaret close to Marseilles.”", "신부가 대답했다. “에드몽은 마르세유 근처의 작은 선술집에서 체포되던 순간까지의 모든 일을 내게 들려주었소.”"]], [["“At La Réserve!", "“라레제르브에서였지요!"], ["Oh, yes; I can see it all before me this moment.”", "아, 그렇습니다. 지금도 눈앞에 생생히 보이는 듯합니다.”"]], [["“Was it not his betrothal feast?”", "“그날이 그의 약혼 잔치 자리가 아니었소?”"]], [["“It was and the feast that began so gayly had a very sorrowful ending; a police commissary, followed by four soldiers, entered, and Dantès was arrested.”", "“그랬지요. 그토록 흥겹게 시작된 잔치가 아주 슬프게 끝나고 말았습니다. 경찰서장이 병사 넷을 데리고 들어와서, 당테스가 체포되었으니까요.”"]], [["“Yes, and up to this point I know all,”", "“그렇소, 거기까지는 나도 다 알고 있소.”"], ["said the priest.", "신부가 말했다."], ["“Dantès himself only knew that which personally concerned him, for he never beheld again the five persons I have named to you, or heard mention of anyone of them.”", "“당테스 자신은 자기 자신에게 직접 관련된 일밖에 알지 못했소. 그는 내가 그대에게 말한 다섯 사람을 다시는 보지 못했고, 그들 누구의 소식도 들은 적이 없으니까.”"]], [["“Well, when Dantès was arrested, Monsieur Morrel hastened to obtain the particulars, and they were very sad.", "“그러니까, 당테스가 체포되자 모렐 씨가 서둘러 자세한 사정을 알아보려 했는데, 알아낸 것은 무척 슬픈 일들뿐이었습니다. 노인은 홀로 집으로 돌아와, 눈물을 글썽이며 아들의 결혼 예복을 접어 놓고는, 종일 방 안을 서성거렸습니다. 잠자리에도 들지 않았지요."], ["The old man returned alone to his home, folded up his wedding suit with tears in his eyes, and paced up and down his chamber the whole day, and would not go to bed at all, for I was underneath him and heard him walking the whole night; and for myself, I assure you I could not sleep either, for the grief of the poor father gave me great uneasiness, and every step he took went to my heart as really as if his foot had pressed against my breast.", "제가 그 아래층에 살았던 터라, 밤새도록 그분이 걸어다니는 소리를 들었으니까요. 저 또한 한숨도 잘 수 없었습니다. 정말이지, 가엾은 아버지의 슬픔이 저를 몹시 괴롭게 했고, 그분이 내딛는 발걸음 하나하나가 마치 제 가슴을 짓밟는 것처럼 가슴에 사무쳤으니까요."]], [["“The next day Mercédès came to implore the protection of M. de Villefort; she did not obtain it, however, and went to visit the old man; when she saw him so miserable and heart-broken, having passed a sleepless night, and not touched food since the previous day, she wished him to go with her that she might take care of him; but the old man would not consent. ‘No,’ was the old man’s reply, ‘I will not leave this house, for my poor dear boy loves me better than anything in the world; and if he gets out of prison he will come and see me the first thing, and what would he think if I did not wait here for him?’ I heard all this from the window, for I was anxious that Mercédès should persuade the old man to accompany her, for his footsteps over my head night and day did not leave me a moment’s repose.”", "“이튿날 메르세데스가 빌포르 씨의 도움을 청하러 찾아갔습니다. 하지만 도움을 얻지 못했고, 노인을 보러 왔지요. 그녀는 그분이 그토록 비참하고 상심에 잠겨, 한숨도 못 자고 전날부터 음식 한 입 대지 않은 모습을 보고는, 자기가 돌봐 드릴 수 있도록 함께 가자고 했습니다. 하지만 노인은 한사코 거절했습니다. ‘아니다.’ 노인은 이렇게 대답했지요. ‘나는 이 집을 떠나지 않겠다. 내 가엾은 아들은 세상 무엇보다 나를 사랑하니, 감옥에서 풀려나면 제일 먼저 나를 보러 올 게다. 그런데 내가 여기서 그 아이를 기다리지 않는다면, 그 아이가 어찌 생각하겠느냐?’ 저는 이 모든 이야기를 창문 너머로 들었습니다. 메르세데스가 노인을 설득해 데려가 주기를 간절히 바랐으니까요. 밤이고 낮이고 제 머리 위에서 울리는 그분의 발소리가 저를 한시도 편히 쉬게 두지 않았거든요.”"]], [["“But did you not go upstairs and try to console the poor old man?”", "“하지만 그대는 위층으로 올라가 그 가엾은 노인을 위로해 드리려 하지 않았소?”"], ["asked the abbé.", "신부가 물었다."]], [["“Ah, sir,” replied Caderousse, “we cannot console those who will not be consoled, and he was one of these; besides, I know not why, but he seemed to dislike seeing me. One night, however, I heard his sobs, and I could not resist my desire to go up to him, but when I reached his door he was no longer weeping but praying. I cannot now repeat to you, sir, all the eloquent words and imploring language he made use of; it was more than piety, it was more than grief, and I, who am no canter, and hate the Jesuits, said then to myself, ‘It is really well, and I am very glad that I have not any children; for if I were a father and felt such excessive grief as the old man does, and did not find in my memory or heart all he is now saying, I should throw myself into the sea at once, for I could not bear it.’”", "“아, 선생님,” 카드루스가 대답했다. “위로받기를 마다하는 사람은 위로할 수가 없는 법이지요. 그분이 바로 그런 분이었습니다. 게다가 왜 그런지는 모르겠으나, 그분은 저를 보는 걸 싫어하시는 것 같았습니다. 그래도 어느 날 밤엔 그분의 흐느낌이 들려와, 올라가 보고 싶은 마음을 도저히 참을 수 없었지요. 그런데 막상 문 앞에 이르렀을 때는, 그분이 더 이상 울지 않고 기도를 드리고 계셨습니다. 그분이 쏟아 놓은 그 절절한 말들과 애원의 기도를 지금 그대로 옮길 수는 없습니다, 선생님. 그것은 신앙심 이상이었고, 슬픔 이상이었습니다. 저는 위선자도 아니고 예수회 사람들을 질색하지만, 그때만은 속으로 이렇게 중얼거렸지요. ‘이것 참 대단하구나. 내게 자식이 없는 게 정말 다행이다. 만일 내가 아비가 되어 저 노인처럼 그토록 사무친 슬픔을 겪으면서도, 저분이 지금 하시는 저런 말들을 내 기억이나 가슴속에서 찾아내지 못한다면, 나는 차라리 바다에 몸을 던지고 말 테니까. 도저히 견딜 수 없을 테니까.’”"]], [["“Poor father!”", "“가엾은 아버지!”"], ["murmured the priest.", "사제가 나직이 중얼거렸다."]], [["“From day to day he lived on alone, and more and more solitary. M.", "“그렇게 그분은 날이 갈수록 점점 더 외롭게, 홀로 살아가셨습니다. 모렐 씨와 메르세데스가 보러 왔지만 문은 닫혀 있었고, 분명 집에 계시는데도 아무 대답을 하지 않으셨지요."], ["Morrel and Mercédès came to see him, but his door was closed; and, although I was certain he was at home, he would not make any answer.", "그러다 어느 날, 평소와 달리 메르세데스를 들이셨는데, 그 가엾은 처녀가 자기 슬픔과 절망에도 불구하고 그분을 위로하려 애쓰자, 그분이 이렇게 말씀하셨습니다. ‘안심하거라, 내 사랑하는 딸아. 그 아이는 죽었다."], ["One day, when, contrary to his custom, he had admitted Mercédès, and the poor girl, in spite of her own grief and despair, endeavored to console him, he said to her,—‘Be assured, my dear daughter, he is dead; and instead of expecting him, it is he who is awaiting us; I am quite happy, for I am the oldest, and of course shall see him first.’", "우리가 그 아이를 기다리는 게 아니라, 그 아이가 우리를 기다리고 있는 거다. 나는 아주 행복하구나. 내가 가장 늙었으니, 당연히 그 아이를 가장 먼저 보게 될 테니까.’"]], [["“However well disposed a person may be, why, you see we leave off after a time seeing persons who are in sorrow, they make one melancholy; and so at last old Dantès was left all to himself, and I only saw from time to time strangers go up to him and come down again with some bundle they tried to hide; but I guessed what these bundles were, and that he sold by degrees what he had to pay for his subsistence.", "“아무리 마음씨 좋은 사람이라도, 보십시오, 시간이 지나면 슬픔에 잠긴 사람을 더 이상 찾지 않게 됩니다. 그런 사람을 보면 마음이 울적해지니까요. 그렇게 결국 늙은 당테스는 완전히 홀로 남겨졌고, 저는 이따금 낯선 자들이 그분 집으로 올라갔다가 무언가 꾸러미를 감추려 애쓰며 다시 내려오는 것만 보았을 뿐입니다."], ["At length the poor old fellow reached the end of all he had; he owed three quarters’ rent, and they threatened to turn him out; he begged for another week, which was granted to him.", "하지만 그 꾸러미가 무엇인지, 그분이 끼니를 잇기 위해 가진 것을 조금씩 팔고 있다는 걸 저는 짐작했지요. 마침내 그 가엾은 노인은 가진 것을 모조리 다 써 버렸습니다. 집세가 세 분기나 밀려, 사람들이 그분을 내쫓겠다고 으름장을 놓았지요."], ["I know this, because the landlord came into my apartment when he left his.", "그분이 일주일만 더 말미를 달라고 청했고, 그것은 허락되었습니다. 제가 이걸 아는 것은, 집주인이 그분 방을 나와 제 방으로 들어왔기 때문입니다."]], [["“For the first three days I heard him walking about as usual, but, on the fourth I heard nothing.", "“처음 사흘 동안은 그분이 평소처럼 걸어다니는 소리가 들렸습니다. 그런데 나흘째 되는 날에는 아무 소리도 들리지 않았지요."], ["I then resolved to go up to him at all risks.", "그래서 무슨 일이 있어도 올라가 봐야겠다고 마음먹었습니다."], ["The door was closed, but I looked through the keyhole, and saw him so pale and haggard, that believing him very ill, I went and told M. Morrel and then ran on to Mercédès.", "문은 닫혀 있었지만 열쇠 구멍으로 들여다보니, 그분이 어찌나 창백하고 수척한지, 중병에 걸린 줄로만 알고 모렐 씨에게 달려가 알린 다음 메르세데스에게까지 달려갔습니다."], ["They both came immediately, M. Morrel bringing a doctor, and the doctor said it was inflammation of the bowels, and ordered him a limited diet.", "두 사람이 곧장 왔는데, 모렐 씨는 의사를 데려왔지요. 의사는 장염이라며 음식을 제한하라는 처방을 내렸습니다."], ["I was there, too, and I never shall forget the old man’s smile at this prescription.", "저도 그 자리에 있었는데, 그 처방을 듣고 노인이 짓던 미소를 저는 결코 잊지 못할 겁니다."]], [["“From that time he received all who came; he had an excuse for not eating any more; the doctor had put him on a diet.”", "“그때부터 그분은 찾아오는 사람을 모두 맞이했습니다. 더 이상 음식을 먹지 않을 핑계가 생긴 셈이었으니까요. 의사가 식사를 제한하라고 했으니 말입니다.”"]], [["The abbé uttered a kind of groan.", "신부가 일종의 신음 같은 소리를 냈다."]], [["“The story interests you, does it not, sir?”", "“이 이야기에 마음이 움직이시나 봅니다, 선생님, 그렇지요?”"], ["inquired Caderousse.", "카드루스가 물었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "“그렇소.”"], ["replied the abbé, “it is very affecting.”", "신부가 대답했다. “무척 가슴 아픈 이야기요.”"]], [["“Mercédès came again, and she found him so altered that she was even more anxious than before to have him taken to her own home. This was M.", "“메르세데스가 다시 찾아왔는데, 그분이 어찌나 달라져 있던지, 전보다 더더욱 자기 집으로 모셔 가고 싶어 했습니다. 모렐 씨의 바람도 그러해서, 그분의 뜻을 거슬러서라도 모셔 가려 했지요."], ["Morrel’s wish also, who would fain have conveyed the old man against his consent; but the old man resisted, and cried so that they were actually frightened.", "하지만 노인이 어찌나 거세게 버티고 울부짖던지, 두 사람이 정말로 겁을 먹을 정도였습니다. 그래서 메르세데스는 그분 머리맡에 남았고, 모렐 씨는 자기 지갑을 벽난로 선반에 두고 왔다는 신호를 그 카탈루냐 처녀에게 보내고는 떠났지요."], ["Mercédès remained, therefore, by his bedside, and M.", "하지만 노인은 의사의 명령을 핑계 삼아 아무것도 입에 대려 하지 않았습니다."], ["Morrel went away, making a sign to the Catalan that he had left his purse on the chimney-piece; but, availing himself of the doctor’s order, the old man would not take any sustenance; at length (after nine days of despair and fasting), the old man died, cursing those who had caused his misery, and saying to Mercédès, ‘If you ever see my Edmond again, tell him I die blessing him.’”", "마침내 (아흐레 동안의 절망과 단식 끝에) 노인은 숨을 거두었습니다. 자기를 그토록 비참하게 만든 자들을 저주하면서, 메르세데스에게 이렇게 말하면서요. ‘혹시라도 내 에드몽을 다시 보거든, 내가 그 아이를 축복하며 죽는다고 전해 다오.’”"]], [["The abbé rose from his chair, made two turns round the chamber, and pressed his trembling hand against his parched throat.", "신부가 의자에서 일어나 방 안을 두 바퀴 돌더니, 떨리는 손을 바싹 마른 목에 갖다 댔다."]], [["“And you believe he died——”", "“그렇다면 그대는 그분이 굶어서 돌아가셨다고……”"]], [["“Of hunger, sir, of hunger,”", "“굶주려서요, 선생님, 굶주려서지요.”"], ["said Caderousse.", "카드루스가 말했다."], ["“I am as certain of it as that we two are Christians.”", "“우리 둘이 그리스도교인이라는 것만큼이나 확실합니다.”"]], [["The abbé, with a shaking hand, seized a glass of water that was standing by him half-full, swallowed it at one gulp, and then resumed his seat, with red eyes and pale cheeks.", "신부는 떨리는 손으로 곁에 놓인 반쯤 채워진 물잔을 집어 단숨에 들이켜고는, 벌게진 눈과 핏기 없는 뺨으로 다시 자리에 앉았다."]], [["“This was, indeed, a horrid event,”", "“참으로 끔찍한 일이었구려.”"], ["said he in a hoarse voice.", "그가 쉰 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“The more so, sir, as it was men’s and not God’s doing.”", "“하느님이 아니라 사람이 저지른 일이었으니, 더욱 그렇지요, 선생님.”"]], [["“Tell me of those men,”", "“그 사람들에 대해 말해 보시오.”"], ["said the abbé, “and remember too,”", "신부가 말했다. “그리고 명심하시오,”"], ["he added in an almost menacing tone, “you have promised to tell me everything.", "그가 거의 위협하는 듯한 어조로 덧붙였다. “그대는 내게 모든 걸 말하겠다고 약속했소."], ["Tell me, therefore, who are these men who killed the son with despair, and the father with famine?”", "그러니 말하시오. 아들은 절망으로, 아버지는 굶주림으로 죽인 그자들이 누구요?”"]], [["“Two men jealous of him, sir; one from love, and the other from ambition,—Fernand and Danglars.”", "“그를 시기하던 두 사람입니다, 선생님. 하나는 사랑 때문에, 다른 하나는 야심 때문이었지요. 페르낭과 당글라르입니다.”"]], [["“How was this jealousy manifested? Speak on.”", "“그 시기심이 어떤 식으로 드러났소? 계속 말하시오.”"]], [["“They denounced Edmond as a Bonapartist agent.”", "“그들은 에드몽을 보나파르트파의 첩자라고 밀고했습니다.”"]], [["“Which of the two denounced him?", "“둘 중 누가 밀고했소?"], ["Which was the real delinquent?”", "누가 진짜 죄인이었소?”"]], [["“Both, sir; one with a letter, and the other put it in the post.”", "“둘 다입니다, 선생님. 하나는 편지를 썼고, 다른 하나는 그것을 부쳤지요.”"]], [["“And where was this letter written?”", "“그 편지는 어디서 쓴 것이오?”"]], [["“At La Réserve, the day before the betrothal feast.”", "“라레제르브에서, 약혼 잔치 전날이었습니다.”"]], [["“’Twas so, then—’twas so, then,”", "“그랬구나, 그래, 그랬어.”"], ["murmured the abbé.", "신부가 중얼거렸다."], ["“Oh, Faria, Faria, how well did you judge men and things!”", "“아, 파리아, 파리아, 그대는 사람과 세상을 얼마나 정확히 꿰뚫어 보았던가!”"]], [["“What did you please to say, sir?”", "“뭐라고 하셨습니까, 선생님?”"], ["asked Caderousse.", "카드루스가 물었다."]], [["“Nothing, nothing,”", "“아무것도, 아무것도 아니오.”"], ["replied the priest; “go on.”", "사제가 대답했다. “계속하시오.”"]], [["“It was Danglars who wrote the denunciation with his left hand, that his writing might not be recognized, and Fernand who put it in the post.”", "“글씨가 알아보이지 않게 하려고 왼손으로 밀고장을 쓴 것이 당글라르였고, 그것을 부친 것이 페르낭이었습니다.”"]], [["“But,”", "“하지만,”"], ["exclaimed the abbé suddenly, “you were there yourself.”", "신부가 불쑥 외쳤다. “그대도 거기 있었구려.”"]], [["“I!”", "“제가요!”"], ["said Caderousse, astonished; “who told you I was there?”", "카드루스가 놀라서 말했다. “제가 거기 있었다고 누가 그러던가요?”"]], [["The abbé saw he had overshot the mark, and he added quickly,—“No one; but in order to have known everything so well, you must have been an eye-witness.”", "신부는 자기가 도를 넘어 너무 앞질러 갔음을 깨닫고는, 얼른 덧붙였다. “아무도 그러지 않았소. 하지만 모든 일을 그토록 소상히 알고 있으려면, 그대가 직접 본 목격자였을 수밖에 없지 않겠소.”"]], [["“True, true!”", "“맞습니다, 맞아요!”"], ["said Caderousse in a choking voice, “I was there.”", "카드루스가 목멘 소리로 말했다. “저도 거기 있었습니다.”"]], [["“And did you not remonstrate against such infamy?”", "“그런데도 그런 비열한 짓에 항의하지 않았소?”"], ["asked the abbé; “if not, you were an accomplice.”", "신부가 물었다. “그러지 않았다면, 그대도 한패였던 셈이오.”"]], [["“Sir,”", "“선생님,”"], ["replied Caderousse, “they had made me drink to such an excess that I nearly lost all perception.", "카드루스가 대답했다. “그자들이 저를 어찌나 취하게 만들었던지, 거의 분별을 잃을 지경이었습니다."], ["I had only an indistinct understanding of what was passing around me.", "주위에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 흐릿하게밖에 알지 못했지요."], ["I said all that a man in such a state could say; but they both assured me that it was a jest they were carrying on, and perfectly harmless.”", "그런 상태의 사람이 할 수 있는 말은 다 했습니다. 하지만 두 사람 다 그저 장난삼아 하는 짓일 뿐, 전혀 해될 게 없다고 제게 장담했습니다.”"]], [["“Next day—next day, sir, you must have seen plain enough what they had been doing, yet you said nothing, though you were present when Dantès was arrested.”", "“이튿날, 이튿날에는, 선생님, 그자들이 무슨 짓을 저질렀는지 똑똑히 보았을 텐데도, 당테스가 체포될 때 그 자리에 있었으면서도, 아무 말도 하지 않았구려.”"]], [["“Yes, sir, I was there, and very anxious to speak; but Danglars restrained me.", "“그렇습니다, 선생님, 저도 거기 있었고, 입을 열고 싶어 안달이 났습니다."], ["‘If he should really be guilty,’ said he, ‘and did really put in to the Island of Elba; if he is really charged with a letter for the Bonapartist committee at Paris, and if they find this letter upon him, those who have supported him will pass for his accomplices.’ I confess I had my fears, in the state in which politics then were, and I held my tongue.", "하지만 당글라르가 저를 말렸지요. ‘만일 그가 정말로 죄가 있다면,’ 그가 말하더군요. ‘만일 그가 정말 엘바섬에 들렀다면, 만일 그가 정말 파리의 보나파르트파 위원회에 전할 편지를 지니고 있다가 그 편지가 몸에서 나온다면, 그를 두둔한 자들은 모두 공범으로 몰릴 거야.’ 솔직히 그 무렵의 정치 상황이 그러했던지라, 저도 겁이 나서 입을 다물고 말았습니다."], ["It was cowardly, I confess, but it was not criminal.” 0341m", "비겁한 짓이었지요, 인정합니다. 하지만 죄를 지은 건 아니었습니다.”"]], [["“I understand—you allowed matters to take their course, that was all.”", "“알겠소. 그저 일이 되어 가는 대로 내버려 두었다는 거구려.”"]], [["“Yes, sir,” answered Caderousse; “and remorse preys on me night and day.", "“그렇습니다, 선생님.” 카드루스가 대답했다."], ["I often ask pardon of God, I swear to you, because this action, the only one with which I have seriously to reproach myself in all my life, is no doubt the cause of my abject condition.", "“그 후로 회한이 밤낮으로 저를 갉아먹고 있습니다. 맹세코, 저는 자주 하느님께 용서를 빕니다. 제 평생 진심으로 자책할 만한 유일한 짓인 그 일이, 틀림없이 지금 이 비참한 처지의 원인일 테니까요."], ["I am expiating a moment of selfishness, and so I always say to La Carconte, when she complains, ‘Hold your tongue, woman; it is the will of God.’”", "저는 한순간의 이기심을 속죄하고 있는 셈이지요. 그래서 카르콩트가 불평을 늘어놓을 때마다 늘 이렇게 말합니다. ‘입 다물어, 여편네야."], ["And Caderousse bowed his head with every sign of real repentance.", "이게 다 하느님의 뜻이야.’” 그러면서 카드루스는 진심으로 뉘우치는 기색을 온통 내비치며 고개를 떨구었다."]], [["“Well, sir,”", "“좋소, 선생.”"], ["said the abbé, “you have spoken unreservedly; and thus to accuse yourself is to deserve pardon.”", "신부가 말했다. “그대는 거리낌 없이 털어놓았소. 이렇게 스스로를 탓하는 것은 곧 용서받을 만한 일이오.”"]], [["“Unfortunately, Edmond is dead, and has not pardoned me.”", "“하지만 안타깝게도 에드몽은 죽었고, 저를 용서해 주지 못했지요.”"]], [["“He did not know,”", "“그는 몰랐소.”"], ["said the abbé.", "신부가 말했다."]], [["“But he knows it all now,”", "“하지만 이제는 다 알 겁니다.”"], ["interrupted Caderousse; “they say the dead know everything.”", "카드루스가 말을 가로챘다. “죽은 자는 모든 걸 안다고들 하니까요.”"]], [["There was a brief silence; the abbé rose and paced up and down pensively, and then resumed his seat.", "잠시 침묵이 흘렀다. 신부는 일어나 생각에 잠긴 채 방 안을 거닐다가 다시 자리에 앉았다."]], [["“You have two or three times mentioned a M.", "“그대가 두세 번 모렐 씨라는 사람을 언급했소.”"], ["Morrel,” he said; “who was he?”", "그가 말했다. “그가 누구요?”"]], [["“The owner of the _Pharaon_ and patron of Dantès.”", "“파라옹호의 선주이자 당테스의 후원자였습니다.”"]], [["“And what part did he play in this sad drama?”", "“그 사람은 이 슬픈 비극에서 어떤 역할을 했소?”"], ["inquired the abbé.", "신부가 물었다."]], [["“The part of an honest man, full of courage and real regard.", "“용기와 진심 어린 정으로 가득한 정직한 사람의 역할이었지요."], ["Twenty times he interceded for Edmond.", "스무 번도 넘게 에드몽을 위해 탄원했습니다."], ["When the emperor returned, he wrote, implored, threatened, and so energetically, that on the second restoration he was persecuted as a Bonapartist.", "황제가 돌아왔을 때는 어찌나 열성적으로 편지를 쓰고 애원하고 위협했던지, 두 번째 왕정복고 때 보나파르트파로 몰려 박해를 받았을 정도였습니다."], ["Ten times, as I told you, he came to see Dantès’ father, and offered to receive him in his own house; and the night or two before his death, as I have already said, he left his purse on the mantelpiece, with which they paid the old man’s debts, and buried him decently; and so Edmond’s father died, as he had lived, without doing harm to anyone.", "말씀드렸듯이 그는 열 번이나 당테스의 아버지를 찾아가, 자기 집에 모시겠다고 했지요. 그리고 노인이 돌아가시기 하루 이틀 전에는, 이미 말씀드렸듯이 벽난로 선반에 지갑을 두고 갔고, 그 돈으로 노인의 빚을 갚고 장례를 제대로 치렀습니다. 그렇게 에드몽의 아버지는 평생 누구에게도 해를 끼치지 않고 사셨던 것처럼, 그렇게 돌아가셨습니다."], ["I have the purse still by me—a large one, made of red silk.”", "그 지갑은 지금도 제게 있습니다. 빨간 비단으로 만든 큼직한 지갑이지요.”"]], [["“And,”", "“그래,”"], ["asked the abbé, “is M. Morrel still alive?”", "신부가 물었다. “모렐 씨는 아직 살아 있소?”"]], [["“Yes,” replied Caderousse.", "“그렇습니다.” 카드루스가 대답했다."]], [["“In that case,”", "“그렇다면,”"], ["replied the abbé, “he should be a man blessed of God, rich, happy.”", "신부가 말했다. “그는 하느님의 축복을 받은 사람이겠구려. 부유하고, 행복하고.”"]], [["Caderousse smiled bitterly.", "카드루스가 씁쓸하게 웃었다."], ["“Yes, happy as myself,”", "“네, 저만큼이나 행복하지요.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“What! M.", "“뭐라고!"], ["Morrel unhappy?” exclaimed the abbé.", "모렐 씨가 불행하단 말이오?” 신부가 외쳤다."]], [["“He is reduced almost to the last extremity—nay, he is almost at the point of dishonor.”", "“거의 막다른 지경까지 몰렸습니다. 아니, 거의 파산의 불명예 직전에 와 있지요.” “어찌하다가?”"]], [["“How?” “Yes,”", "“그렇습니다.”"], ["continued Caderousse, “so it is; after five-and-twenty years of labor, after having acquired a most honorable name in the trade of Marseilles, M.", "카드루스가 말을 이었다. “사정이 그렇습니다. 이십오 년 동안 일하며 마르세유 상업계에서 더없이 명예로운 이름을 얻었던 모렐 씨가, 완전히 몰락하고 말았습니다."], ["Morrel is utterly ruined; he has lost five ships in two years, has suffered by the bankruptcy of three large houses, and his only hope now is in that very _Pharaon_ which poor Dantès commanded, and which is expected from the Indies with a cargo of cochineal and indigo.", "이태 만에 배 다섯 척을 잃었고, 큰 상회 셋이 파산하는 바람에 손해를 입었지요. 이제 그의 유일한 희망은 바로 가엾은 당테스가 지휘했던 그 파라옹호뿐인데, 코치닐과 인디고를 싣고 인도에서 돌아오기로 되어 있습니다."], ["If this ship founders, like the others, he is a ruined man.”", "만일 이 배마저 다른 배들처럼 가라앉는다면, 그는 끝장난 사람입니다.”"]], [["“And has the unfortunate man wife or children?”", "“그 불운한 사람에게 아내나 자식이 있소?”"], ["inquired the abbé.", "신부가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, he has a wife, who through everything has behaved like an angel; he has a daughter, who was about to marry the man she loved, but whose family now will not allow him to wed the daughter of a ruined man; he has, besides, a son, a lieutenant in the army; and, as you may suppose, all this, instead of lessening, only augments his sorrows.", "“네, 아내가 있는데, 어떤 일이 닥쳐도 천사처럼 처신해 온 분입니다. 딸도 하나 있는데, 사랑하는 남자와 결혼하려던 참이었지만, 이제 그 남자의 집안에서 몰락한 사람의 딸을 며느리로 들이지 않으려 합니다. 그 밖에 아들도 하나 있는데, 군대의 중위지요."], ["If he were alone in the world he would blow out his brains, and there would be an end.”", "짐작하시겠지만, 이 모든 것이 그의 슬픔을 덜기는커녕 오히려 키울 뿐입니다. 만일 그가 이 세상에 혼자였다면, 머리에 총알을 박고 모든 걸 끝내 버렸을 겁니다.”"]], [["“Horrible!” ejaculated the priest.", "“끔찍한 일이로군!” 사제가 내뱉었다."]], [["“And it is thus heaven recompenses virtue, sir,”", "“하늘이 미덕에 보답하는 방식이 이렇습니다, 선생님.”"], ["added Caderousse.", "카드루스가 덧붙였다."], ["“You see, I, who never did a bad action but that I have told you of—am in destitution, with my poor wife dying of fever before my very eyes, and I unable to do anything in the world for her; I shall die of hunger, as old Dantès did, while Fernand and Danglars are rolling in wealth.”", "“보십시오, 방금 말씀드린 그 한 가지 말고는 평생 나쁜 짓이라곤 한 적이 없는 저는 궁핍 속에 살고 있고, 가엾은 아내는 제 눈앞에서 열병으로 죽어 가는데도, 저는 그녀를 위해 이 세상에서 아무것도 해 줄 수가 없습니다. 저는 늙은 당테스처럼 굶어 죽고 말 텐데, 페르낭과 당글라르는 떵떵거리며 부귀를 누리고 있지요.”"]], [["“How is that?”", "“어찌하여 그렇소?”"]], [["“Because their deeds have brought them good fortune, while honest men have been reduced to misery.”", "“그자들이 저지른 짓이 그들에게는 행운을 가져다주었고, 정직한 사람들은 비참한 처지로 몰렸으니까요.”"]], [["“What has become of Danglars, the instigator, and therefore the most guilty?”", "“그 일을 사주했으니 가장 죄가 무거운 당글라르는 어떻게 되었소?”"]], [["“What has become of him?", "“그가 어떻게 되었느냐고요?"], ["Why, he left Marseilles, and was taken, on the recommendation of M. Morrel, who did not know his crime, as cashier into a Spanish bank.", "그는 마르세유를 떠나, 그의 죄를 알 리 없는 모렐 씨의 추천으로 어느 스페인 은행에 출납계로 들어갔습니다."], ["During the war with Spain he was employed in the commissariat of the French army, and made a fortune; then with that money he speculated in the funds, and trebled or quadrupled his capital; and, having first married his banker’s daughter, who left him a widower, he has married a second time, a widow, a Madame de Nargonne, daughter of M.", "스페인과의 전쟁 동안에는 프랑스군의 군수 부서에서 일하며 한밑천을 잡았지요. 그러고는 그 돈으로 공채에 투자해 자본을 서너 배로 불렸습니다. 처음엔 은행가의 딸과 결혼했는데 그 아내가 죽어 홀아비가 되었고, 두 번째로 어느 미망인과 재혼했지요."], ["de Servieux, the king’s chamberlain, who is in high favor at court.", "나르곤 부인이라는 여자인데, 궁정에서 큰 총애를 받는 국왕 시종 세르비외 씨의 딸입니다."], ["He is a millionaire, and they have made him a baron, and now he is the Baron Danglars, with a fine residence in the Rue du Mont-Blanc, with ten horses in his stables, six footmen in his antechamber, and I know not how many millions in his strongbox.”", "그는 백만장자가 되었고, 사람들이 그를 남작으로 만들어 주어, 이제 당글라르 남작이 되었지요. 몽블랑 거리에 으리으리한 저택을 두고, 마구간에는 말이 열 필, 대기실에는 하인이 여섯 명, 그리고 금고에는 몇백만 프랑인지 모를 돈이 들어 있습니다.”"]], [["“Ah!”", "“아!”"], ["said the abbé, in a peculiar tone, “he is happy.”", "신부가 묘한 어조로 말했다. “그는 행복하군.”"]], [["“Happy? Who can answer for that?", "“행복하다고요? 누가 그걸 장담할 수 있겠습니까?"], ["Happiness or unhappiness is the secret known but to one’s self and the walls—walls have ears but no tongue; but if a large fortune produces happiness, Danglars is happy.”", "행복이든 불행이든, 그건 오직 자기 자신과 벽만이 아는 비밀이지요. 벽에는 귀는 있어도 혀는 없으니까요. 하지만 막대한 재산이 행복을 가져다주는 거라면, 당글라르는 행복한 셈입니다.”"]], [["“And Fernand?”", "“그럼 페르낭은?”"]], [["“Fernand?", "“페르낭이요?"], ["Why, much the same story.”", "글쎄요, 거의 똑같은 이야기지요.”"]], [["“But how could a poor Catalan fisher-boy, without education or resources, make a fortune? I confess this staggers me.”", "“하지만 교육도 재산도 없던 가난한 카탈루냐 어부 소년이 어떻게 부를 일굴 수 있었소? 솔직히 그건 도무지 이해가 안 가는구려.”"]], [["“And it has staggered everybody.", "“그건 누구나 다 이해가 안 가는 일입니다."], ["There must have been in his life some strange secret that no one knows.”", "그의 삶에는 아무도 모르는 어떤 기이한 비밀이 있었던 게 분명합니다.”"]], [["“But, then, by what visible steps has he attained this high fortune or high position?”", "“그렇다면 대체 눈에 보이는 어떤 단계들을 거쳐 그런 높은 부와 높은 지위에 이른 것이오?”"]], [["“Both, sir—he has both fortune and position—both.”", "“둘 다입니다, 선생님. 그는 부도 지위도 가졌어요. 둘 다 말입니다.”"]], [["“This must be impossible!”", "“그건 있을 수 없는 일이오!”"]], [["“It would seem so; but listen, and you will understand.", "“그렇게 보이지요. 하지만 들어 보시면 이해가 되실 겁니다."], ["Some days before the return of the emperor, Fernand was drafted.", "황제가 돌아오기 며칠 전, 페르낭이 징집되었습니다."], ["The Bourbons left him quietly enough at the Catalans, but Napoleon returned, a special levy was made, and Fernand was compelled to join.", "부르봉 왕가는 그를 카탈루냐 마을에 조용히 내버려 두었지만, 나폴레옹이 돌아오면서 특별 징집령이 내려졌고, 페르낭도 군에 들어가야 했지요."], ["I went too; but as I was older than Fernand, and had just married my poor wife, I was only sent to the coast.", "저도 갔습니다. 하지만 저는 페르낭보다 나이가 많았고 막 가엾은 아내와 결혼한 참이라, 해안 경비로만 보내졌지요."], ["Fernand was enrolled in the active army, went to the frontier with his regiment, and was at the battle of Ligny.", "페르낭은 야전군에 편입되어 연대와 함께 국경으로 갔고, 리니 전투에 참가했습니다."], ["The night after that battle he was sentry at the door of a general who carried on a secret correspondence with the enemy.", "그 전투가 끝난 날 밤, 그는 적과 은밀히 내통하던 어느 장군의 막사 문 앞에서 보초를 서고 있었지요."], ["That same night the general was to go over to the English.", "바로 그날 밤 그 장군은 영국군 쪽으로 넘어가기로 되어 있었습니다."], ["He proposed to Fernand to accompany him; Fernand agreed to do so, deserted his post, and followed the general.", "장군이 페르낭에게 함께 가자고 제안했고, 페르낭은 그러기로 하고는 초소를 버리고 장군을 따라갔습니다."]], [["“Fernand would have been court-martialed if Napoleon had remained on the throne, but his action was rewarded by the Bourbons.", "“만일 나폴레옹이 왕위에 남아 있었다면 페르낭은 군법 회의에 회부되었겠지만, 그의 행동은 부르봉 왕가에 의해 보상받았습니다."], ["He returned to France with the epaulet of sub-lieutenant, and as the protection of the general, who is in the highest favor, was accorded to him, he was a captain in 1823, during the Spanish war—that is to say, at the time when Danglars made his early speculations.", "그는 소위 견장을 달고 프랑스로 돌아왔고, 궁정에서 최고의 총애를 받는 그 장군의 비호를 받게 되어, 스페인 전쟁이 벌어진 1823년에는 대위가 되었지요. 다시 말해, 당글라르가 처음 투자에 손을 댄 바로 그 무렵 말입니다."], ["Fernand was a Spaniard, and being sent to Spain to ascertain the feeling of his fellow-countrymen, found Danglars there, got on very intimate terms with him, won over the support of the royalists at the capital and in the provinces, received promises and made pledges on his own part, guided his regiment by paths known to himself alone through the mountain gorges which were held by the royalists, and, in fact, rendered such services in this brief campaign that, after the taking of Trocadero, he was made colonel, and received the title of count and the cross of an officer of the Legion of Honor.”", "페르낭은 스페인 사람이었던 터라, 동포들의 심중을 살피라는 임무를 띠고 스페인으로 파견되었는데, 거기서 당글라르를 만나 무척 가까운 사이가 되었습니다. 그는 수도와 지방의 왕당파의 지지를 끌어내고, 약속을 받아 내고 또 자기 쪽에서도 약속을 했으며, 왕당파가 장악한 산악 협곡들을 자기만 아는 길로 연대를 이끌고 통과했지요. 사실, 이 짧은 원정에서 어찌나 큰 공을 세웠던지, 트로카데로를 함락한 뒤 대령으로 진급했고, 백작 작위와 레지옹 도뇌르 오피시에 십자훈장을 받았습니다.”"]], [["“Destiny!", "“운명이로다!"], ["destiny!”", "운명이야!”"], ["murmured the abbé.", "신부가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Yes, but listen: this was not all.", "“네, 하지만 들어 보십시오."], ["The war with Spain being ended, Fernand’s career was checked by the long peace which seemed likely to endure throughout Europe.", "그게 전부가 아닙니다. 스페인 전쟁이 끝나자, 유럽 전역에 오래도록 이어질 듯한 긴 평화 때문에 페르낭의 출셋길이 막히고 말았습니다."], ["Greece only had risen against Turkey, and had begun her war of independence; all eyes were turned towards Athens—it was the fashion to pity and support the Greeks.", "오직 그리스만이 터키에 맞서 일어나 독립 전쟁을 시작했고, 모든 눈길이 아테네로 쏠렸지요. 그리스인을 동정하고 지원하는 것이 유행이었으니까요."], ["The French government, without protecting them openly, as you know, gave countenance to volunteer assistance.", "아시다시피 프랑스 정부는 그리스를 드러내 놓고 보호하지는 않았지만, 의용군의 지원은 묵인했습니다."], ["Fernand sought and obtained leave to go and serve in Greece, still having his name kept on the army roll. 0345m", "페르낭은 그리스에 가서 복무하게 해 달라고 청해 허락을 받았는데, 여전히 자기 이름은 군적에 남겨 둔 채였지요."]], [["Some time after, it was stated that the Comte de Morcerf (this was the name he bore) had entered the service of Ali Pasha with the rank of instructor-general.", "“얼마 후, 모르세르 백작(이것이 그가 지닌 이름이었습니다)이 교관 총감 직위로 알리 파샤를 섬기게 되었다는 소문이 돌았습니다."], ["Ali Pasha was killed, as you know, but before he died he recompensed the services of Fernand by leaving him a considerable sum, with which he returned to France, when he was gazetted lieutenant-general.”", "아시다시피 알리 파샤는 살해되었지만, 죽기 전에 페르낭의 공로에 보답하여 상당한 금액을 남겨 주었지요. 페르낭은 그 돈을 가지고 프랑스로 돌아왔고, 관보에 중장으로 발표되었습니다.”"]], [["“So that now——?” inquired the abbé.", "“그래서 지금은……?” 신부가 물었다."]], [["“So that now,”", "“그래서 지금은,”"], ["continued Caderousse, “he owns a magnificent house—No. 27, Rue du Helder, Paris.”", "카드루스가 말을 이었다. “파리의 엘데르 거리 27번지에 으리으리한 저택을 가지고 있지요.”"]], [["The abbé opened his mouth, hesitated for a moment, then, making an effort at self-control, he said, “And Mercédès—they tell me that she has disappeared?”", "신부가 입을 열다가 잠시 머뭇거리더니, 애써 마음을 다잡고는 말했다. “그럼 메르세데스는? 듣자 하니 그녀가 자취를 감추었다던데?”"]], [["“Disappeared,”", "“자취를 감추었지요.”"], ["said Caderousse, “yes, as the sun disappears, to rise the next day with still more splendor.”", "카드루스가 말했다. “네, 해가 사라지는 것처럼요. 이튿날 더욱 찬란하게 떠오르려고 말입니다.”"]], [["“Has she made a fortune also?”", "“그녀도 부를 일구었소?”"], ["inquired the abbé, with an ironical smile.", "신부가 빈정대는 미소를 띠며 물었다."]], [["“Mercédès is at this moment one of the greatest ladies in Paris,”", "“메르세데스는 지금 파리에서 가장 지체 높은 귀부인 가운데 하나입니다.”"], ["replied Caderousse.", "카드루스가 대답했다."]], [["“Go on,”", "“계속하시오.”"], ["said the abbé; “it seems as if I were listening to the story of a dream.", "신부가 말했다. “꿈 이야기를 듣고 있는 것만 같구려."], ["But I have seen things so extraordinary, that what you tell me seems less astonishing than it otherwise might.”", "하지만 나는 워낙 기이한 일들을 많이 보아 온 터라, 그대가 들려주는 이야기가 다른 때만큼 놀랍지는 않소.”"]], [["“Mercédès was at first in the deepest despair at the blow which deprived her of Edmond.", "“메르세데스는 처음엔 에드몽을 앗아 간 그 타격에 깊은 절망에 빠져 있었습니다."], ["I have told you of her attempts to propitiate M. de Villefort, her devotion to the elder Dantès.", "빌포르 씨의 마음을 돌려 보려 애쓴 일이며, 늙은 당테스에게 정성을 다한 일은 이미 말씀드렸지요."], ["In the midst of her despair, a new affliction overtook her.", "그 절망의 한가운데서 또 다른 고통이 그녀를 덮쳤습니다."], ["This was the departure of Fernand—of Fernand, whose crime she did not know, and whom she regarded as her brother.", "페르낭이 떠난 일이었지요. 그녀는 페르낭의 죄를 알지 못했고, 그를 친오빠처럼 여겼으니까요."], ["Fernand went, and Mercédès remained alone.", "페르낭이 떠나고, 메르세데스는 홀로 남았습니다."]], [["“Three months passed and still she wept—no news of Edmond, no news of Fernand, no companionship save that of an old man who was dying with despair.", "“석 달이 지나도록 그녀는 여전히 울었습니다. 에드몽의 소식도, 페르낭의 소식도 없었고, 절망 속에 죽어 가는 노인 하나 말고는 곁에 아무도 없었지요."], ["One evening, after a day of accustomed vigil at the angle of two roads leading to Marseilles from the Catalans, she returned to her home more depressed than ever.", "어느 날 저녁, 카탈루냐 마을에서 마르세유로 이어지는 두 갈래 길이 만나는 모퉁이에서 여느 때처럼 하루 종일 지켜본 끝에, 그녀는 그 어느 때보다 풀이 죽어 집으로 돌아왔습니다."], ["Suddenly she heard a step she knew, turned anxiously around, the door opened, and Fernand, dressed in the uniform of a sub-lieutenant, stood before her.", "그때 문득 귀에 익은 발소리가 들렸고, 그녀가 불안하게 돌아보는데 문이 열리더니, 소위 군복을 입은 페르낭이 그녀 앞에 서 있었습니다."]], [["“It was not the one she wished for most, but it seemed as if a part of her past life had returned to her.", "“그녀가 가장 간절히 바라던 사람은 아니었지만, 마치 지나간 삶의 한 조각이 되돌아온 듯했지요."]], [["“Mercédès seized Fernand’s hands with a transport which he took for love, but which was only joy at being no longer alone in the world, and seeing at last a friend, after long hours of solitary sorrow.", "“메르세데스는 페르낭의 두 손을 와락 붙잡았는데, 그는 그것을 사랑이라 여겼지만, 사실은 더 이상 이 세상에 혼자가 아니라는 기쁨, 오랜 시간 홀로 슬퍼하던 끝에 마침내 벗 하나를 보게 된 기쁨일 뿐이었습니다. 게다가 솔직히 말하자면, 페르낭이 미움을 받은 적은 한 번도 없었지요. 다만 딱히 사랑받지 못했을 뿐입니다."], ["And then, it must be confessed, Fernand had never been hated—he was only not precisely loved.", "메르세데스의 마음은 온통 다른 사람의 차지였으니까요. 그 다른 사람은 곁에 없었고, 사라졌고, 어쩌면 죽었는지도 몰랐습니다."], ["Another possessed all Mercédès’ heart; that other was absent, had disappeared, perhaps was dead.", "이 마지막 생각에 메르세데스는 왈칵 눈물을 쏟으며 고통스레 두 손을 쥐어짰지요. 하지만 전에는 남이 넌지시 일러 줄 때마다 늘 물리쳤던 그 생각이, 이제는 온전한 힘으로 그녀의 마음을 덮쳐 왔습니다."], ["At this last thought Mercédès burst into a flood of tears, and wrung her hands in agony; but the thought, which she had always repelled before when it was suggested to her by another, came now in full force upon her mind; and then, too, old Dantès incessantly said to her, ‘Our Edmond is dead; if he were not, he would return to us.’", "게다가 늙은 당테스도 끊임없이 그녀에게 이렇게 말했지요. ‘우리 에드몽은 죽었다. 살아 있다면 우리에게 돌아왔을 게다.’"]], [["“The old man died, as I have told you; had he lived, Mercédès, perchance, had not become the wife of another, for he would have been there to reproach her infidelity.", "“말씀드렸듯이 노인은 죽었습니다. 만일 그분이 살아 계셨더라면, 어쩌면 메르세데스는 다른 사람의 아내가 되지 않았을지도 모릅니다. 그분이 그 자리에 계셔서 그녀의 변심을 나무랐을 테니까요."], ["Fernand saw this, and when he learned of the old man’s death he returned.", "페르낭도 이것을 알았기에, 노인이 죽었다는 소식을 듣고는 돌아왔습니다."], ["He was now a lieutenant.", "그때 그는 중위가 되어 있었지요."], ["At his first coming he had not said a word of love to Mercédès; at the second he reminded her that he loved her.", "처음 왔을 때는 메르세데스에게 사랑한다는 말을 한마디도 하지 않았지만, 두 번째 왔을 때는 자기가 그녀를 사랑한다는 것을 일깨웠습니다."]], [["“Mercédès begged for six months more in which to await and mourn for Edmond.”", "“메르세데스는 에드몽을 기다리며 애도할 시간을 여섯 달만 더 달라고 청했지요.”"]], [["“So that,”", "“그러니까,”"], ["said the abbé, with a bitter smile, “that makes eighteen months in all.", "신부가 씁쓸한 미소를 지으며 말했다. “다 합쳐 열여덟 달이로군."], ["What more could the most devoted lover desire?”", "아무리 헌신적인 연인이라 한들 그 이상을 바랄 수 있겠소?”"], ["Then he murmured the words of the English poet, “‘Frailty, thy name is woman.’”", "그러더니 영국 시인의 말을 나직이 읊조렸다. “‘약한 자여, 그대 이름은 여자로다.’”"]], [["“Six months afterwards,”", "“여섯 달 뒤,”"], ["continued Caderousse, “the marriage took place in the church of Accoules.”", "카드루스가 말을 이었다. “결혼식이 아쿨 성당에서 거행되었습니다.”"]], [["“The very church in which she was to have married Edmond,”", "“바로 그녀가 에드몽과 결혼하기로 했던 그 성당이로군.”"], ["murmured the priest; “there was only a change of bridegrooms.”", "사제가 중얼거렸다. “신랑만 바뀐 셈이오.”"]], [["“Well, Mercédès was married,”", "“어쨌든 메르세데스는 결혼했습니다.”"], ["proceeded Caderousse; “but although in the eyes of the world she appeared calm, she nearly fainted as she passed La Réserve, where, eighteen months before, the betrothal had been celebrated with him whom she might have known she still loved, had she looked to the bottom of her heart.", "카드루스가 계속했다. “하지만 세상 사람들 눈에는 차분해 보였어도, 라레제르브를 지날 때는 거의 정신을 잃을 뻔했지요. 열여덟 달 전 바로 그곳에서, 자기 마음 밑바닥을 들여다보았다면 여전히 사랑하고 있음을 알았을 그 사람과 약혼 잔치를 벌였으니까요."], ["Fernand, more happy, but not more at his ease—for I saw at this time he was in constant dread of Edmond’s return—Fernand was very anxious to get his wife away, and to depart himself.", "페르낭은 더 행복했지만, 그렇다고 마음이 더 편한 것도 아니었습니다. 그 무렵 그가 에드몽이 돌아오지 않을까 끊임없이 두려워하는 걸 제가 보았으니까요. 페르낭은 아내를 데리고 떠나고, 자기도 떠나려고 무척 애를 썼습니다."], ["There were too many unpleasant possibilities associated with the Catalans, and eight days after the wedding they left Marseilles.”", "카탈루냐 마을에는 불편한 일이 너무 많이 얽혀 있었으니까요. 결혼하고 여드레 뒤, 두 사람은 마르세유를 떠났습니다.”"]], [["“Did you ever see Mercédès again?” inquired the priest.", "“그 후로 메르세데스를 다시 본 적이 있소?” 사제가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, during the Spanish war, at Perpignan, where Fernand had left her; she was attending to the education of her son.”", "“네, 스페인 전쟁 때, 페르낭이 그녀를 두고 간 페르피냥에서요. 그녀는 아들의 교육에 정성을 쏟고 있었습니다.”"]], [["The abbé started.", "신부가 흠칫했다."], ["“Her son?”", "“아들이라?”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "“네.”"], ["replied Caderousse, “little Albert.”", "카드루스가 대답했다. “어린 알베르 말입니다.”"]], [["“But, then, to be able to instruct her child,”", "“하지만 그렇다면 자기 아이를 가르칠 수 있으려면,”"], ["continued the abbé, “she must have received an education herself.", "신부가 말을 이었다. “그녀 자신이 교육을 받았어야 할 텐데."], ["I understood from Edmond that she was the daughter of a simple fisherman, beautiful but uneducated.”", "에드몽한테 듣기로는 그녀가 그저 평범한 어부의 딸로, 아름답지만 못 배운 처녀라고 했소.”"]], [["“Oh,”", "“오,”"], ["replied Caderousse, “did he know so little of his lovely betrothed?", "카드루스가 대답했다. “그가 그 사랑스러운 약혼녀를 그렇게 몰랐단 말입니까?"], ["Mercédès might have been a queen, sir, if the crown were to be placed on the heads of the loveliest and most intelligent.", "가장 아름답고 가장 영리한 사람의 머리에 왕관을 씌운다면, 메르세데스는 왕비가 되고도 남았을 겁니다, 선생님."], ["Fernand’s fortune was already waxing great, and she developed with his growing fortune.", "페르낭의 재산이 이미 불어나고 있었고, 그녀도 그 불어나는 재산과 더불어 함께 성장했지요."], ["She learned drawing, music—everything.", "그림이며 음악이며, 온갖 것을 다 배웠습니다."], ["Besides, I believe, between ourselves, she did this in order to distract her mind, that she might forget; and she only filled her head in order to alleviate the weight on her heart.", "게다가 우리끼리 얘기지만, 제 생각엔 마음을 다른 데로 돌려 잊어버리려고 그렇게 한 것 같습니다. 가슴을 짓누르는 무게를 덜어 보려고 머릿속을 채운 것이지요."], ["But now her position in life is assured,”", "하지만 이제 그녀의 처지는 안정되었습니다.”"], ["continued Caderousse; “no doubt fortune and honors have comforted her; she is rich, a countess, and yet——”", "카드루스가 말을 이었다. “재산과 영예가 그녀를 위로해 준 게 분명하지요. 그녀는 부유하고, 백작 부인이고, 그런데도……”"]], [["Caderousse paused.", "카드루스가 말을 멈추었다."]], [["“And yet what?”", "“그런데도 뭐요?”"], ["asked the abbé.", "신부가 물었다."]], [["“Yet, I am sure, she is not happy,”", "“그런데도, 분명 그녀는 행복하지 않습니다.”"], ["said Caderousse.", "카드루스가 말했다."]], [["“What makes you believe this?”", "“어찌하여 그렇게 생각하오?”"]], [["“Why, when I found myself utterly destitute, I thought my old friends would, perhaps, assist me.", "“그러니까, 제가 완전히 빈털터리가 되었을 때, 옛 친구들이 어쩌면 저를 도와줄지도 모른다고 생각했지요."], ["So I went to Danglars, who would not even receive me.", "그래서 당글라르를 찾아갔는데, 그는 저를 만나 주지도 않았습니다."], ["I called on Fernand, who sent me a hundred francs by his valet-de-chambre.”", "페르낭을 찾아가니, 자기 시종을 시켜 백 프랑을 보내왔지요.”"]], [["“Then you did not see either of them?”", "“그럼 두 사람 다 만나지 못했단 말이오?”"]], [["“No, but Madame de Morcerf saw me.”", "“네, 하지만 모르세르 부인은 저를 보았습니다.”"]], [["“How was that?”", "“그건 또 어찌 된 일이오?”"]], [["“As I went away a purse fell at my feet—it contained five-and-twenty louis; I raised my head quickly, and saw Mercédès, who at once shut the blind.”", "“제가 돌아서는데 발치에 지갑 하나가 떨어졌습니다. 그 안에는 스물다섯 루이가 들어 있었지요. 얼른 고개를 들어 보니 메르세데스가 보였는데, 곧바로 덧창을 닫아 버리더군요.”"]], [["“And M. de Villefort?”", "“그럼 빌포르 씨는?”"], ["asked the abbé.", "신부가 물었다."]], [["“Oh, he never was a friend of mine, I did not know him, and I had nothing to ask of him.”", "“오, 그는 한 번도 제 친구였던 적이 없습니다. 저는 그를 알지도 못했고, 그에게 청할 것도 없었지요.”"]], [["“Do you not know what became of him, and the share he had in Edmond’s misfortunes?”", "“그가 어찌 되었는지, 에드몽의 불행에 그가 어떤 몫을 했는지 모르오?”"]], [["“No; I only know that some time after Edmond’s arrest, he married Mademoiselle de Saint-Méran, and soon after left Marseilles; no doubt he has been as lucky as the rest; no doubt he is as rich as Danglars, as high in station as Fernand.", "“모릅니다. 다만 에드몽이 체포되고 얼마 후에 그가 생메랑 양과 결혼했고, 곧 마르세유를 떠났다는 것만 압니다. 틀림없이 그도 다른 자들처럼 운이 좋았겠지요."], ["I only, as you see, have remained poor, wretched, and forgotten.”", "틀림없이 당글라르만큼 부유하고, 페르낭만큼 높은 지위에 올랐을 겁니다. 보시다시피 저 혼자만 가난하고 비참하게, 잊힌 채 남았지요.”"]], [["“You are mistaken, my friend,”", "“그건 그대가 잘못 생각하는 거요, 벗이여.”"], ["replied the abbé; “God may seem sometimes to forget for a time, while his justice reposes, but there always comes a moment when he remembers—and behold—a proof!”", "신부가 대답했다. “하느님은 때로 그분의 정의가 잠시 쉬는 동안 한동안 잊으시는 듯 보이지만, 언제나 그분이 다시 기억하시는 순간이 찾아오는 법이오. 자, 보시오, 그 증거가 여기 있소!”"]], [["As he spoke, the abbé took the diamond from his pocket, and giving it to Caderousse, said, “Here, my friend, take this diamond, it is yours.”", "이렇게 말하면서 신부는 주머니에서 다이아몬드를 꺼내 카드루스에게 건네며 말했다. “자, 벗이여, 이 다이아몬드를 받으시오. 이건 그대의 것이오.”"]], [["“What, for me only?”", "“뭐라고요, 저 혼자만요?”"], ["cried Caderousse, “ah, sir, do not jest with me!”", "카드루스가 외쳤다. “아, 선생님, 저를 놀리지 마십시오!”"]], [["“This diamond was to have been shared among his friends.", "“이 다이아몬드는 본래 그의 벗들에게 나누어 주려던 것이었소."], ["Edmond had one friend only, and thus it cannot be divided.", "그런데 에드몽에게는 벗이 오직 하나뿐이었으니, 나눌 수가 없게 되었구려."], ["Take the diamond, then, and sell it; it is worth fifty thousand francs, and I repeat my wish that this sum may suffice to release you from your wretchedness.” 0351m", "그러니 이 다이아몬드를 받아 파시오. 오만 프랑은 나갈 것이오. 거듭 말하지만, 이 돈이 그대를 비참한 처지에서 건져 내기에 충분하기를 바라오.”"]], [["“Oh, sir,”", "“오, 선생님,”"], ["said Caderousse, putting out one hand timidly, and with the other wiping away the perspiration which bedewed his brow,—“Oh, sir, do not make a jest of the happiness or despair of a man.”", "카드루스가 한 손은 머뭇거리며 내밀고 다른 손으로는 이마에 맺힌 땀을 닦으면서 말했다. “오, 선생님, 한 사람의 행복과 절망을 가지고 농담하지 마십시오.”"]], [["“I know what happiness and what despair are, and I never make a jest of such feelings.", "“나는 행복이 무엇이고 절망이 무엇인지 잘 아오. 그런 감정을 가지고는 결코 농담하지 않소."], ["Take it, then, but in exchange——”", "그러니 받으시오. 다만 그 대신에……”"]], [["Caderousse, who touched the diamond, withdrew his hand.", "다이아몬드를 만지작거리던 카드루스가 손을 거두었다."]], [["The abbé smiled.", "신부가 미소 지었다."]], [["“In exchange,”", "“그 대신에,”"], ["he continued, “give me the red silk purse that M. Morrel left on old Dantès’ chimney-piece, and which you tell me is still in your hands.”", "그가 말을 이었다. “모렐 씨가 늙은 당테스의 벽난로 선반에 두고 갔다는, 그래서 아직 그대 손에 있다고 한 그 빨간 비단 지갑을 내게 주시오.”"]], [["Caderousse, more and more astonished, went toward a large oaken cupboard, opened it, and gave the abbé a long purse of faded red silk, round which were two copper runners that had once been gilt.", "카드루스는 점점 더 어리둥절해하며 커다란 떡갈나무 찬장으로 가서 문을 열고, 빛바랜 빨간 비단으로 만든 긴 지갑을 신부에게 건넸다. 한때 도금했던 구리 고리 두 개가 지갑을 빙 두르고 있었다."], ["The abbé took it, and in return gave Caderousse the diamond.", "신부는 그것을 받고, 그 대신 카드루스에게 다이아몬드를 주었다."]], [["“Oh, you are a man of God, sir,”", "“오, 선생님은 하느님의 사람이십니다.”"], ["cried Caderousse; “for no one knew that Edmond had given you this diamond, and you might have kept it.”", "카드루스가 외쳤다. “에드몽이 선생님께 이 다이아몬드를 주었다는 걸 아무도 몰랐으니, 그냥 가지셔도 됐을 텐데요.”"]], [["“Which,” said the abbé to himself, “you would have done.”", "‘바로 그대라면 그렇게 했겠지.’ 신부가 속으로 중얼거렸다."], ["The abbé rose, took his hat and gloves.", "신부는 일어나 모자와 장갑을 집어 들었다. “자,”"], ["“Well,” he said, “all you have told me is perfectly true, then, and I may believe it in every particular.”", "그가 말했다. “그럼 그대가 들려준 이야기는 모두 빈틈없는 사실이고, 나는 그 모든 세세한 부분까지 믿어도 되는 것이오.”"]], [["“See, sir,”", "“보십시오, 선생님.”"], ["replied Caderousse, “in this corner is a crucifix in holy wood—here on this shelf is my wife’s testament; open this book, and I will swear upon it with my hand on the crucifix.", "카드루스가 대답했다. “저 모퉁이에 성스러운 나무로 만든 십자가가 있고, 이 선반 위에는 제 아내의 성경책이 있습니다. 이 책을 펴 주십시오."], ["I will swear to you by my soul’s salvation, my faith as a Christian, I have told everything to you as it occurred, and as the recording angel will tell it to the ear of God at the day of the last judgment!”", "제가 한 손을 십자가에 얹고 그 위에 대고 맹세하겠습니다. 제 영혼의 구원과 그리스도교인으로서의 믿음을 걸고 맹세하건대, 저는 일어난 그대로 모든 것을 선생님께 말씀드렸습니다. 최후 심판의 날에 기록의 천사가 하느님의 귀에 고할 그대로 말입니다!”"]], [["“’Tis well,”", "“좋소.”"], ["said the abbé, convinced by his manner and tone that Caderousse spoke the truth.", "카드루스의 태도와 말투에서 그가 진실을 말하고 있음을 확신한 신부가 말했다."], ["“’Tis well, and may this money profit you!", "“좋소, 부디 이 돈이 그대에게 보탬이 되기를!"], ["Adieu; I go far from men who thus so bitterly injure each other.”", "그럼 안녕히. 나는 이렇게 서로를 모질게 해치는 사람들에게서 멀리 떠나가오.”"]], [["The abbé with difficulty got away from the enthusiastic thanks of Caderousse, opened the door himself, got out and mounted his horse, once more saluted the innkeeper, who kept uttering his loud farewells, and then returned by the road he had travelled in coming.", "신부는 카드루스의 격렬한 감사 인사에서 간신히 벗어나, 직접 문을 열고 나가 말에 올랐다. 그러고는 큰 소리로 작별 인사를 연거푸 외쳐 대는 여관 주인에게 다시 한번 인사를 건넨 뒤, 올 때 지나왔던 길로 되돌아갔다."]], [["When Caderousse turned around, he saw behind him La Carconte, paler and trembling more than ever.", "카드루스가 돌아서자, 뒤에 그 어느 때보다 핏기 없이 떨고 있는 카르콩트가 보였다."]], [["“Is, then, all that I have heard really true?”", "“그럼 내가 들은 게 정말 다 사실이에요?”"], ["she inquired.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“What?", "“뭐가?"], ["That he has given the diamond to us only?”", "그 사람이 우리한테만 다이아몬드를 주었다는 거?”"], ["inquired Caderousse, half bewildered with joy; “yes, nothing more true!", "기쁨에 반쯤 정신이 나간 카드루스가 물었다. “그래, 더없이 사실이야!"], ["See, here it is.”", "자, 여기 있잖아.”"]], [["The woman gazed at it a moment, and then said, in a gloomy voice, “Suppose it’s false?”", "여자는 잠시 그것을 뚫어지게 바라보더니, 음울한 목소리로 말했다. “가짜면 어쩌려고요?”"]], [["Caderousse started and turned pale.", "카드루스가 흠칫하며 얼굴이 하얘졌다."]], [["“False!”", "“가짜라고!”"], ["he muttered.", "그가 중얼거렸다."], ["“False!", "“가짜라니!"], ["Why should that man give me a false diamond?” 0349m", "그 사람이 뭐 하러 내게 가짜 다이아몬드를 주겠어?”"]], [["“To get your secret without paying for it, you blockhead!”", "“값도 치르지 않고 당신 비밀을 캐내려고, 이 멍청아!”"]], [["Caderousse remained for a moment aghast under the weight of such an idea.", "카드루스는 그런 생각의 무게에 짓눌려 한순간 망연자실했다."]], [["“Oh!”", "“오!”"], ["he said, taking up his hat, which he placed on the red handkerchief tied round his head, “we will soon find out.”", "그가 모자를 집어 머리에 동여맨 빨간 손수건 위에 얹으며 말했다. “곧 알아낼 수 있지.”"]], [["“In what way?”", "“어떻게요?”"]], [["“Why, the fair is on at Beaucaire, there are always jewellers from Paris there, and I will show it to them.", "“보케르에서 장이 서고 있잖아. 거기엔 늘 파리에서 온 보석상들이 있으니, 그 사람들한테 보여 주면 돼."], ["Look after the house, wife, and I shall be back in two hours,”", "여보, 집 잘 보고 있어. 두 시간 안에 돌아올 테니까.”"], ["and Caderousse left the house in haste, and ran rapidly in the direction opposite to that which the priest had taken.", "그러고는 카드루스가 황급히 집을 나서더니, 사제가 간 길과 반대 방향으로 쏜살같이 달려갔다."]], [["“Fifty thousand francs!”", "“오만 프랑이라!”"], ["muttered La Carconte when left alone; “it is a large sum of money, but it is not a fortune.” 20009m", "홀로 남은 카르콩트가 중얼거렸다. “큰돈이긴 하지만, 재산이라고 할 정도는 아니지.”"]], [["# Chapter 28. The Prison Register", "# Chapter 28. The Prison Register"]], [["20019m\nThe day after that in which the scene we have just described had taken place on the road between Bellegarde and Beaucaire, a man of about thirty or two-and-thirty, dressed in a bright blue frock coat, nankeen trousers, and a white waistcoat, having the appearance and accent of an Englishman, presented himself before the mayor of Marseilles.", "벨가르드와 보케르 사이의 길에서 방금 우리가 묘사한 장면이 벌어진 그다음 날, 서른이나 서른두 살쯤 된 한 남자가 산뜻한 푸른색 프록코트에 난징목 바지, 흰 조끼 차림으로, 영국인 특유의 풍모와 말씨를 풍기며 마르세유 시장 앞에 나타났다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"시장님,\""], ["said he, “I am chief clerk of the house of Thomson & French, of Rome.", "그가 말했다. \"저는 로마에 있는 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회의 수석 사무원입니다."], ["We are, and have been these ten years, connected with the house of Morrel & Son, of Marseilles.", "저희는 지난 십 년 동안 마르세유의 모렐 부자 상회와 거래를 이어 왔습니다."], ["We have a hundred thousand francs or thereabouts loaned on their securities, and we are a little uneasy at reports that have reached us that the firm is on the brink of ruin.", "그 상회의 증권을 담보로 십만 프랑가량을 대출해 주었는데, 그 회사가 파산 직전에 몰려 있다는 소문이 들려와 마음이 좀 불안합니다."], ["I have come, therefore, express from Rome, to ask you for information.”", "그래서 정보를 여쭤보려고 로마에서 일부러 찾아왔습니다.\""]], [["“Sir,” replied the mayor.", "\"선생,\" 시장이 대답했다."], ["“I know very well that during the last four or five years misfortune has seemed to pursue M.", "\"내가 잘 아는 바로는, 지난 사오 년 동안 불운이 모렐 씨를 따라다닌 듯합니다."], ["Morrel. He has lost four or five vessels, and suffered by three or four bankruptcies; but it is not for me, although I am a creditor myself to the amount of ten thousand francs, to give any information as to the state of his finances.", "배를 네댓 척 잃었고, 서너 차례 파산의 피해를 보았지요. 하지만 나 자신도 만 프랑의 채권자이긴 하나, 그분의 재정 상태에 대해 무어라 정보를 드릴 처지는 못 됩니다."], ["Ask of me, as mayor, what is my opinion of M. Morrel, and I shall say that he is a man honorable to the last degree, and who has up to this time fulfilled every engagement with scrupulous punctuality.", "시장으로서 모렐 씨를 어떻게 보느냐고 물으신다면, 더없이 명예로운 사람이며 지금까지 모든 약속을 한 치의 어김도 없이 지켜 온 분이라고 말하겠습니다. 내가 할 수 있는 말은 이것뿐입니다, 선생."], ["This is all I can say, sir; if you wish to learn more, address yourself to M. de Boville, the inspector of prisons, No.", "더 알고 싶으시거든, 누아유 거리 15번지의 교도소 감찰관 드 보빌 씨를 찾아가 보십시오."], ["15, Rue de Nouailles; he has, I believe, two hundred thousand francs in Morrel’s hands, and if there be any grounds for apprehension, as this is a greater amount than mine, you will most probably find him better informed than myself.”", "내가 알기로 그분은 모렐 씨에게 이십만 프랑을 맡겨 두었으니, 만약 우려할 만한 근거가 있다면, 액수가 나보다 큰 만큼 나보다 사정을 더 잘 알고 있을 것입니다.\""]], [["The Englishman seemed to appreciate this extreme delicacy, made his bow and went away, proceeding with a characteristic British stride towards the street mentioned.", "영국인은 이 극진한 신중함을 높이 사는 듯, 가볍게 고개를 숙이고는 영국인 특유의 성큼성큼한 걸음으로 일러 준 거리를 향해 떠나갔다."]], [["M. de Boville was in his private room, and the Englishman, on perceiving him, made a gesture of surprise, which seemed to indicate that it was not the first time he had been in his presence.", "드 보빌 씨는 집무실에 있었다. 영국인은 그를 보자 놀란 듯한 몸짓을 했는데, 마치 그 앞에 선 것이 처음이 아니라는 듯한 기색이었다."], ["As to M. de Boville, he was in such a state of despair, that it was evident all the faculties of his mind, absorbed in the thought which occupied him at the moment, did not allow either his memory or his imagination to stray to the past.", "한편 드 보빌 씨로 말하자면, 어찌나 절망에 빠져 있던지, 그 순간 자기를 사로잡은 생각에 온 정신이 빨려 들어가, 기억이든 상상이든 과거로 거슬러 올라갈 여유가 전혀 없어 보였다."]], [["The Englishman, with the coolness of his nation, addressed him in terms nearly similar to those with which he had accosted the mayor of Marseilles.", "영국인은 제 나라 사람다운 냉정함으로, 마르세유 시장에게 건넸던 것과 거의 비슷한 말로 그에게 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["“Oh, sir,” exclaimed M.", "\"오, 선생,\""], ["de Boville, “your fears are unfortunately but too well founded, and you see before you a man in despair.", "드 보빌 씨가 외쳤다. \"불행히도 당신의 우려는 너무나 잘 들어맞았고, 지금 당신 앞에는 절망에 빠진 사람이 있소이다."], ["I had two hundred thousand francs placed in the hands of Morrel & Son; these two hundred thousand francs were the dowry of my daughter, who was to be married in a fortnight, and these two hundred thousand francs were payable, half on the 15th of this month, and the other half on the 15th of next month.", "나는 이십만 프랑을 모렐 부자 상회에 맡겨 두었소. 그 이십만 프랑은 보름 뒤에 결혼할 내 딸의 지참금이었소. 그 돈은 절반은 이달 15일에, 나머지 절반은 다음 달 15일에 받기로 되어 있었지요."], ["I had informed M. Morrel of my desire to have these payments punctually, and he has been here within the last half-hour to tell me that if his ship, the _Pharaon_, did not come into port on the 15th, he would be wholly unable to make this payment.”", "나는 그 지급을 제때에 받고 싶다는 뜻을 모렐 씨에게 알렸는데, 바로 반 시간 전에 그가 여기 와서, 자기 배 파라옹호가 15일까지 입항하지 못하면 도저히 이 돈을 치를 수 없을 거라고 하지 뭐요.\""]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["said the Englishman, “this looks very much like a suspension of payment.”", "영국인이 말했다. \"그건 지급 정지와 다름없어 보입니다.\""]], [["“It looks more like bankruptcy!”", "\"파산에 더 가깝지요!\""], ["exclaimed M. de Boville despairingly.", "드 보빌 씨가 절망에 차서 외쳤다."]], [["The Englishman appeared to reflect a moment, and then said, “From which it would appear, sir, that this credit inspires you with considerable apprehension?”", "영국인은 잠시 생각에 잠기는 듯하더니 입을 열었다. \"그렇다면, 선생, 이 채권이 당신께 상당한 불안을 안겨 주는 모양이군요?\""]], [["“To tell you the truth, I consider it lost.”", "\"솔직히 말하면, 잃은 돈으로 치고 있소.\""]], [["“Well, then, I will buy it of you!”", "\"그렇다면, 제가 그것을 사겠습니다!\" \"당신이?\""]], [["“You?” “Yes, I!”", "\"네, 제가요!\""]], [["“But at a tremendous discount, of course?”", "\"하지만 엄청나게 깎아서 사려는 거겠지요?\""]], [["“No, for two hundred thousand francs.", "\"아닙니다, 이십만 프랑 그대로요."], ["Our house,”", "저희 상회는,\""], ["added the Englishman with a laugh, “does not do things in that way.”", "영국인이 웃으며 덧붙였다. \"그런 식으로 일을 처리하지 않습니다.\""]], [["“And you will pay——”", "\"그래, 지급은……\""]], [["“Ready money.”", "\"현금으로요.\""]], [["20023m\nAnd the Englishman drew from his pocket a bundle of bank-notes, which might have been twice the sum M. de Boville feared to lose.", "영국인은 주머니에서 지폐 뭉치를 꺼냈는데, 드 보빌 씨가 잃을까 두려워하던 액수의 두 배는 됨 직했다."], ["A ray of joy passed across M. de Boville’s countenance, yet he made an effort at self-control, and said:", "드 보빌 씨의 얼굴에 기쁨의 빛이 스쳤으나, 그는 애써 자제하며 말했다."]], [["“Sir, I ought to tell you that, in all probability, you will not realize six per cent of this sum.”", "\"선생, 한 가지 말해 두어야겠소. 십중팔구 이 돈의 육 퍼센트도 건지지 못할 거요.\""]], [["“That’s no affair of mine,”", "\"그건 제가 알 바 아닙니다.\""], ["replied the Englishman, “that is the affair of the house of Thomson & French, in whose name I act.", "영국인이 대답했다. \"그건 제가 대리하는 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회의 일이지요."], ["They have, perhaps, some motive to serve in hastening the ruin of a rival firm.", "어쩌면 그쪽은 경쟁 회사의 몰락을 앞당기려는 속셈이 있는지도 모릅니다."], ["But all I know, sir, is, that I am ready to hand you over this sum in exchange for your assignment of the debt. I only ask a brokerage.”", "하지만 제가 아는 것이라곤, 채권의 양도를 받는 대가로 이 돈을 당신께 넘겨 드릴 채비가 되어 있다는 것뿐입니다. 다만 중개 수수료는 하나 청하겠습니다.\""]], [["“Of course, that is perfectly just,”", "\"그야 당연히 지극히 정당한 일이지요.\""], ["cried M. de Boville.", "드 보빌 씨가 외쳤다."], ["“The commission is usually one and a half; will you have two—three—five per cent, or even more?", "\"수수료는 보통 일 점 오 퍼센트요. 이 퍼센트, 삼 퍼센트, 오 퍼센트, 아니 그 이상이라도 좋소?"], ["Whatever you say.”", "말씀만 하시오.\""]], [["“Sir,”", "\"선생,\""], ["replied the Englishman, laughing, “I am like my house, and do not do such things—no, the commission I ask is quite different.”", "영국인이 웃으며 대답했다. \"저는 저희 상회와 같아서, 그런 식으로는 하지 않습니다. 아니, 제가 청하는 수수료는 전혀 다른 것입니다.\""]], [["“Name it, sir, I beg.”", "\"부디 말씀해 보시오, 선생.\""]], [["“You are the inspector of prisons?”", "\"당신은 교도소 감찰관이시지요?\""]], [["“I have been so these fourteen years.”", "\"십사 년째 그 일을 하고 있소.\""]], [["“You keep the registers of entries and departures?”", "\"입소와 퇴소 기록부를 관리하시고요?\""]], [["“I do.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["“To these registers there are added notes relative to the prisoners?”", "\"그 기록부에는 수감자에 관한 기록도 덧붙여 있겠지요?\""]], [["“There are special reports on every prisoner.”", "\"수감자마다 특별 보고서가 있소.\""]], [["“Well, sir, I was educated at Rome by a poor devil of an abbé, who disappeared suddenly.", "\"그런데, 선생, 저는 로마에서 한 가엾은 신부에게 교육을 받았는데, 그분이 어느 날 갑자기 종적을 감추었습니다."], ["I have since learned that he was confined in the Château d’If, and I should like to learn some particulars of his death.”", "그 뒤에 그분이 이프성에 갇혀 있었다는 사실을 알게 되었지요. 그분이 어떻게 돌아가셨는지 자세한 내막을 좀 알고 싶습니다.\""]], [["“What was his name?”", "\"이름이 무엇이었소?\""]], [["“The Abbé Faria.”", "\"파리아 신부입니다.\""]], [["“Oh, I recollect him perfectly,”", "\"오, 그 사람이라면 똑똑히 기억하지요.\""], ["cried M. de Boville; “he was crazy.”", "드 보빌 씨가 외쳤다. \"미친 사람이었소.\""]], [["“So they said.”", "\"그렇게들 말하더군요.\""]], [["“Oh, he was, decidedly.”", "\"오, 틀림없이 그랬소.\""]], [["“Very possibly; but what sort of madness was it?”", "\"그럴 수도 있겠지요. 하지만 어떤 광기였습니까?\""]], [["“He pretended to know of an immense treasure, and offered vast sums to the government if they would liberate him.”", "\"엄청난 보물의 소재를 안다고 주장하면서, 자기를 풀어 주면 정부에 막대한 돈을 내놓겠다고 했소.\""]], [["“Poor devil!—and he is dead?”", "\"가엾은 양반!…… 그래서 돌아가셨습니까?\""]], [["“Yes, sir, five or six months ago, last February.”", "\"그렇소, 선생. 대여섯 달 전, 지난 이월에요.\""]], [["“You have a good memory, sir, to recollect dates so well.”", "\"날짜까지 그리 잘 기억하시다니, 기억력이 대단하시군요.\""]], [["“I recollect this, because the poor devil’s death was accompanied by a singular incident.”", "\"이건 기억하지요. 그 가엾은 양반의 죽음에 기이한 사건이 뒤따랐으니까요.\""]], [["“May I ask what that was?” said the Englishman with an expression of curiosity, which a close observer would have been astonished at discovering in his phlegmatic countenance.", "\"무슨 일이었는지 여쭈어도 되겠습니까?\" 영국인이 호기심 어린 표정으로 말했는데, 그의 무덤덤한 얼굴에서 그런 기색을 발견했다면 세심한 관찰자는 적잖이 놀랐을 것이다."]], [["“Oh dear, yes, sir; the abbé’s dungeon was forty or fifty feet distant from that of one of Bonaparte’s emissaries,—one of those who had contributed the most to the return of the usurper in 1815, a very resolute and very dangerous man.”", "\"오, 그러지요, 선생. 그 신부의 지하 감방은 보나파르트의 밀사 가운데 한 명의 감방에서 사오십 피트쯤 떨어져 있었소. 1815년 그 찬탈자의 복귀에 누구보다 크게 기여한 자였는데, 아주 결단력 있고 위험한 사내였지요.\""]], [["“Indeed!”", "\"저런!\""], ["said the Englishman.", "영국인이 말했다."]], [["“Yes,” replied M.", "\"그렇소,\""], ["de Boville; “I myself had occasion to see this man in 1816 or 1817, and we could only go into his dungeon with a file of soldiers.", "드 보빌 씨가 대답했다. \"나도 1816년인가 1817년인가에 그 사내를 볼 일이 있었는데, 병사들을 한 줄로 늘어세우지 않고는 그 감방에 들어갈 수가 없었소."], ["That man made a deep impression on me; I shall never forget his countenance!”", "그 사내는 내게 깊은 인상을 남겼소. 그 얼굴은 결코 잊지 못할 거요!\""]], [["20025m\nThe Englishman smiled imperceptibly.", "영국인은 눈에 띄지 않을 만큼 살며시 미소 지었다."]], [["“And you say, sir,”", "\"그러니까 선생,\""], ["he interposed, “that the two dungeons——”", "그가 끼어들었다. \"그 두 감방이……\""]], [["“Were separated by a distance of fifty feet; but it appears that this Edmond Dantès——”", "\"오십 피트 거리로 떨어져 있었지요. 그런데 그 에드몽 당테스라는 자가……\""]], [["“This dangerous man’s name was——”", "\"그 위험한 사내의 이름이……\""]], [["“Edmond Dantès.", "\"에드몽 당테스였소."], ["It appears, sir, that this Edmond Dantès had procured tools, or made them, for they found a tunnel through which the prisoners held communication with one another.”", "그런데 선생, 그 에드몽 당테스라는 자가 연장을 구했거나 직접 만든 모양이오. 두 죄수가 서로 연락을 주고받던 굴이 발견되었으니 말이오.\""]], [["“This tunnel was dug, no doubt, with an intention of escape?”", "\"그 굴은 분명 탈옥할 작정으로 판 것이었겠지요?\""]], [["“No doubt; but unfortunately for the prisoners, the Abbé Faria had an attack of catalepsy, and died.”", "\"의심할 여지가 없소. 하지만 죄수들로서는 불행하게도, 파리아 신부가 강경증 발작을 일으켜 죽고 말았소.\""]], [["“That must have cut short the projects of escape.”", "\"그래서 탈옥 계획이 중단되고 말았겠군요.\""]], [["“For the dead man, yes,” replied M.", "\"죽은 자에게는 그랬지요.\""], ["de Boville, “but not for the survivor; on the contrary, this Dantès saw a means of accelerating his escape.", "드 보빌 씨가 대답했다. \"하지만 살아남은 자에게는 아니었소."], ["He, no doubt, thought that prisoners who died in the Château d’If were interred in an ordinary burial-ground, and he conveyed the dead man into his own cell, took his place in the sack in which they had sewed up the corpse, and awaited the moment of interment.”", "오히려 그 당테스라는 자는 탈옥을 앞당길 방도를 본 거요. 그자는 분명, 이프성에서 죽은 죄수는 보통 묘지에 묻힌다고 여기고는, 죽은 자를 제 감방으로 옮겨 와, 시체를 꿰매 넣은 자루 속에 대신 들어가 매장될 순간을 기다린 거요.\""]], [["“It was a bold step, and one that showed some courage,” remarked the Englishman.", "\"대담한 짓이었고, 어지간한 배짱이 아니고서는 못 할 일이었군요.\" 영국인이 말했다."]], [["“As I have already told you, sir, he was a very dangerous man; and, fortunately, by his own act disembarrassed the government of the fears it had on his account.”", "\"이미 말씀드렸다시피, 선생, 그자는 아주 위험한 사내였소. 그런데 다행히도, 제 손으로 한 짓 덕분에 정부는 그자 때문에 품고 있던 두려움을 덜게 되었지요.\""]], [["“How was that?”", "\"어떻게 그리되었습니까?\""]], [["“How? Do you not comprehend?”", "\"어떻게라니? 모르시겠소?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“The Château d’If has no cemetery, and they simply throw the dead into the sea, after fastening a thirty-six-pound cannon-ball to their feet.”", "\"이프성에는 묘지가 없소. 그래서 죽은 자의 발에 삼십육 파운드짜리 포탄을 매달아 그대로 바다에 던져 버린다오.\""]], [["“Well?” observed the Englishman as if he were slow of comprehension.", "\"그래서요?\" 영국인이 마치 알아듣는 데 더딘 사람처럼 물었다."]], [["“Well, they fastened a thirty-six-pound ball to his feet, and threw him into the sea.”", "\"그래서, 그자의 발에 삼십육 파운드짜리 포탄을 매달아 바다에 던졌단 말이오.\""]], [["“Really!” exclaimed the Englishman.", "\"정말입니까!\" 영국인이 외쳤다."]], [["“Yes, sir,” continued the inspector of prisons.", "\"그렇소, 선생.\" 교도소 감찰관이 말을 이었다."], ["“You may imagine the amazement of the fugitive when he found himself flung headlong over the rocks!", "\"바위 너머로 곤두박질쳤을 때 그 도망자가 얼마나 기겁했을지 상상이 가시오?"], ["I should like to have seen his face at that moment.”", "그 순간 그자의 얼굴을 봤더라면 좋았을 텐데 말이오.\""]], [["“That would have been difficult.”", "\"그건 어려웠겠지요.\""]], [["“No matter,”", "\"상관없소.\""], ["replied De Boville, in supreme good-humor at the certainty of recovering his two hundred thousand francs,—“no matter, I can fancy it.”", "드 보빌은 이십만 프랑을 되찾게 되리라는 확신에 더없이 기분이 좋아져서 대꾸했다. \"상관없어요, 머릿속으로 그려 볼 수 있으니까.\""], ["And he shouted with laughter.", "그러고는 큰 소리로 웃어 댔다."]], [["“So can I,”", "\"저도 그렇습니다.\""], ["said the Englishman, and he laughed too; but he laughed as the English do, “at the end of his teeth.”", "영국인이 말하며 함께 웃었다. 하지만 그 웃음은 영국인답게 \"이 끝으로만 짓는\" 웃음이었다."]], [["“And so,”", "\"그래서,\""], ["continued the Englishman who first gained his composure, “he was drowned?”", "먼저 평정을 되찾은 영국인이 말을 이었다. \"그자는 물에 빠져 죽은 겁니까?\""]], [["“Unquestionably.”", "\"의심할 여지가 없소.\""]], [["“So that the governor got rid of the dangerous and the crazy prisoner at the same time?”", "\"그러니까 소장은 위험한 죄수와 미친 죄수를 한꺼번에 처치한 셈이군요?\""]], [["“Precisely.” 20027m", "\"바로 그렇소.\""]], [["“But some official document was drawn up as to this affair, I suppose?”", "\"하지만 이 일에 대해 무슨 공식 문서가 작성되었겠지요?\""], ["inquired the Englishman.", "영국인이 물었다."]], [["“Yes, yes, the mortuary deposition.", "\"그렇소, 그렇소. 사망 진술서 말이오."], ["You understand, Dantès’ relations, if he had any, might have some interest in knowing if he were dead or alive.”", "아시겠지만, 당테스에게 친척이 있다면, 그자가 죽었는지 살았는지 아는 데 이해관계가 걸려 있을 수도 있으니까요.\""]], [["“So that now, if there were anything to inherit from him, they may do so with easy conscience. He is dead, and no mistake about it.”", "\"그러니까 이제 그자에게서 물려받을 것이 있다면, 마음 편히 상속받을 수 있겠군요. 그자는 죽었고, 거기엔 의심의 여지가 없으니까요.\""]], [["“Oh, yes; and they may have the fact attested whenever they please.”", "\"오, 그렇소. 원할 때면 언제든 그 사실을 입증할 수 있소.\""]], [["“So be it,”", "\"그렇군요.\""], ["said the Englishman.", "영국인이 말했다."], ["“But to return to these registers.”", "\"하지만 그 기록부 이야기로 돌아갑시다.\""]], [["“True, this story has diverted our attention from them.", "\"맞소, 이 이야기 때문에 우리 정신이 거기서 벗어났구려."], ["Excuse me.”", "미안하오.\""]], [["“Excuse you for what?", "\"미안하다니요?"], ["For the story? By no means; it really seems to me very curious.”", "그 이야기 때문에요? 천만에요. 정말이지 무척 흥미롭던걸요.\""]], [["“Yes, indeed.", "\"그렇소, 정말 그렇지요."], ["So, sir, you wish to see all relating to the poor abbé, who really was gentleness itself.”", "자, 선생, 그 가엾은 신부에 관한 것을 모두 보고 싶으시다는 거지요. 정말이지 온화함 그 자체인 분이었는데.\""]], [["“Yes, you will much oblige me.”", "\"네, 그렇게 해 주시면 정말 고맙겠습니다.\""]], [["“Go into my study here, and I will show it to you.”", "\"여기 내 서재로 들어오시오. 보여 드리리다.\""]], [["And they both entered M. de Boville’s study.", "그리하여 두 사람은 드 보빌 씨의 서재로 들어갔다."], ["Everything was here arranged in perfect order; each register had its number, each file of papers its place.", "이곳은 모든 것이 완벽한 질서를 갖추고 정돈되어 있었다. 기록부마다 번호가 매겨져 있었고, 서류철마다 제자리가 정해져 있었다."], ["The inspector begged the Englishman to seat himself in an armchair, and placed before him the register and documents relative to the Château d’If, giving him all the time he desired for the examination, while De Boville seated himself in a corner, and began to read his newspaper.", "감찰관은 영국인에게 안락의자에 앉기를 권하고는, 그 앞에 이프성에 관한 기록부와 문서들을 펼쳐 놓으며 살펴볼 시간을 얼마든지 가지라고 했다. 그러는 동안 드 보빌은 구석에 자리를 잡고 신문을 읽기 시작했다. 영국인은 파리아 신부에 관한 기록을 손쉽게 찾아냈다."], ["The Englishman easily found the entries relative to the Abbé Faria; but it seemed that the history which the inspector had related interested him greatly, for after having perused the first documents he turned over the leaves until he reached the deposition respecting Edmond Dantès.", "그러나 감찰관이 들려준 이야기가 그의 흥미를 크게 돋운 모양인지, 첫 문서들을 죽 훑어본 뒤에는 장을 넘겨 에드몽 당테스에 관한 진술서가 나올 때까지 거슬러 갔다. 거기에는 모든 것이 차례대로 정돈되어 있었다."], ["There he found everything arranged in due order,—the accusation, examination, Morrel’s petition, M. de Villefort’s marginal notes.", "고발장, 심문 기록, 모렐의 청원서, 드 빌포르 씨의 여백 주석까지. 그는 고발장을 조용히 접어, 그만큼이나 조용히 주머니에 넣었다."], ["He folded up the accusation quietly, and put it as quietly in his pocket; read the examination, and saw that the name of Noirtier was not mentioned in it; perused, too, the application dated 10th April, 1815, in which Morrel, by the deputy procureur’s advice, exaggerated with the best intentions (for Napoleon was then on the throne) the services Dantès had rendered to the imperial cause—services which Villefort’s certificates rendered indisputable.", "심문 기록을 읽고는 거기에 누아르티에라는 이름이 언급되지 않았음을 확인했다. 또한 1815년 4월 10일 자 신청서도 죽 훑어보았는데, 거기서 모렐은 검사대리의 조언에 따라, 더없이 선의로(당시는 나폴레옹이 제위에 있었으므로) 당테스가 제국의 대의에 바친 공로를 부풀려 적어 두었다. 그 공로는 빌포르의 증명서들로 인해 반박할 수 없는 것이 되어 있었다."], ["Then he saw through the whole thing.", "그제야 그는 모든 내막을 꿰뚫어 보았다."], ["This petition to Napoleon, kept back by Villefort, had become, under the second restoration, a terrible weapon against him in the hands of the king’s attorney.", "빌포르가 가로챈 이 나폴레옹에게 보내는 청원서가, 제2차 왕정복고 아래에서는 왕의 검사 손에서 그를 겨누는 무시무시한 무기로 둔갑한 것이었다."], ["He was no longer astonished when he searched on to find in the register this note, placed in a bracket against his name:", "그는 기록부를 계속 뒤지다가 당테스의 이름 옆 괄호 안에 적힌 이 주석을 발견하고도 더는 놀라지 않았다."]], [["Edmond Dantès.", "에드몽 당테스."]], [["An inveterate Bonapartist; took an active part in the return from the Island of Elba.", "골수 보나파르트파. 1815년 엘바섬에서의 복귀에 적극 가담함."]], [["To be kept in strict solitary confinement, and to be closely watched and guarded.", "엄중한 독방에 수감하고, 면밀히 감시·감금할 것."]], [["Beneath these lines was written in another hand: “See note above—nothing can be done.”", "이 글줄 아래에는 다른 필체로 이렇게 적혀 있었다. \"위 주석 참조 — 어찌해 볼 도리가 없음.\""]], [["He compared the writing in the bracket with the writing of the certificate placed beneath Morrel’s petition, and discovered that the note in the bracket was the same writing as the certificate—that is to say, was in Villefort’s handwriting.", "그는 괄호 안의 필체를 모렐의 청원서 아래에 붙은 증명서의 필체와 견주어 보았고, 괄호 안 주석이 증명서와 같은 필체임을, 다시 말해 빌포르의 글씨임을 알아냈다."]], [["20029m\nAs to the note which accompanied this, the Englishman understood that it might have been added by some inspector who had taken a momentary interest in Dantès’ situation, but who had, from the remarks we have quoted, found it impossible to give any effect to the interest he had felt.", "이에 딸린 주석으로 말하자면, 영국인은 그것이 한때 당테스의 처지에 잠시 관심을 가졌던 어느 감찰관이 덧붙인 것이되, 앞서 인용한 글귀 때문에 그 관심을 어떻게도 실행에 옮길 수 없음을 깨달았으리라는 것을 알아차렸다."]], [["As we have said, the inspector, from discretion, and that he might not disturb the Abbé Faria’s pupil in his researches, had seated himself in a corner, and was reading _Le Drapeau Blanc_.", "앞서 말했듯이, 감찰관은 신중을 기하느라, 또 파리아 신부의 제자가 조사하는 것을 방해하지 않으려고 구석에 자리를 잡고 「르 드라포 블랑」을 읽고 있었다."], ["He did not see the Englishman fold up and place in his pocket the accusation written by Danglars under the arbor of La Réserve, and which had the postmark, “Marseilles, 27th February, delivery 6 o’clock, P.M.”", "그래서 영국인이 라레제르브의 정자 아래에서 당글라르가 쓴 고발장을 접어 주머니에 넣는 것을 보지 못했다. 그 고발장에는 \"마르세유, 2월 27일, 오후 6시 배달\"이라는 소인이 찍혀 있었다."]], [["But it must be said that if he had seen it, he attached so little importance to this scrap of paper, and so much importance to his two hundred thousand francs, that he would not have opposed whatever the Englishman might do, however irregular it might be.", "그러나 한 가지 말해 두어야 할 것은, 설령 그가 그 광경을 보았다 하더라도, 그 종잇조각을 워낙 대수롭지 않게 여기고 자신의 이십만 프랑을 워낙 중하게 여겼던 터라, 영국인이 무슨 짓을 하든, 그것이 아무리 규정에 어긋나든 막아서지 않았으리라는 점이다."]], [["“Thanks,”", "\"고맙습니다.\""], ["said the latter, closing the register with a slam, “I have all I want; now it is for me to perform my promise.", "영국인이 기록부를 탁 덮으며 말했다. \"원하던 것은 다 얻었습니다. 이제 제 약속을 지킬 차례군요."], ["Give me a simple assignment of your debt; acknowledge therein the receipt of the cash, and I will hand you over the money.”", "채권 양도서를 간단히 한 장 써 주십시오. 거기에 현금을 받았다는 사실을 적어 주시면, 제가 돈을 넘겨 드리겠습니다.\""]], [["He rose, gave his seat to M. de Boville, who took it without ceremony, and quickly drew up the required assignment, while the Englishman counted out the bank-notes on the other side of the desk.", "그는 일어나 드 보빌 씨에게 자리를 내주었고, 드 보빌 씨는 사양 없이 자리에 앉아 재빨리 양도서를 작성했다. 그러는 동안 영국인은 책상 맞은편에서 지폐를 헤아렸다."]], [["# Chapter 29. The House of Morrel & Son", "# 제28장. 감옥 기록부"]], [["Anyone who had quitted Marseilles a few years previously, well acquainted with the interior of Morrel’s warehouse, and had returned at this date, would have found a great change.", "몇 해 전 마르세유를 떠나면서 모렐 상회의 내부 사정을 훤히 꿰뚫고 있던 사람이 지금 다시 돌아온다면, 큰 변화를 발견할 것이다."], ["Instead of that air of life, of comfort, and of happiness that permeates a flourishing and prosperous business establishment—instead of merry faces at the windows, busy clerks hurrying to and fro in the long corridors—instead of the court filled with bales of goods, re-echoing with the cries and the jokes of porters, one would have immediately perceived all aspect of sadness and gloom.", "번창하고 잘나가는 사업체에 감돌던 생기와 풍요와 행복의 분위기 대신, 창가의 명랑한 얼굴들 대신, 긴 복도를 분주히 오가던 직원들 대신, 짐 꾸러미로 가득 차 짐꾼들의 외침과 농담이 메아리치던 마당 대신, 누구든 곧바로 슬픔과 우울의 기색만을 알아챘을 것이다."], ["Out of all the numerous clerks that used to fill the deserted corridor and the empty office, but two remained.", "한때 복도와 사무실을 가득 메우던 그 많은 직원들 중 이제 단 둘만 남아 있었다."], ["One was a young man of three or four-and-twenty, who was in love with M. Morrel’s daughter, and had remained with him in spite of the efforts of his friends to induce him to withdraw; the other was an old one-eyed cashier, called “Cocles,”", "한 사람은 스물서너 살쯤 된 젊은이로, 모렐 씨의 딸을 사랑하여, 손을 떼라는 친구들의 갖은 설득에도 그의 곁에 남아 있었다. 다른 한 사람은 외눈박이 늙은 회계원으로, \"코클레스\""], ["or “Cock-eye,” a nickname given him by the young men who used to throng this vast now almost deserted bee-hive, and which had so completely replaced his real name that he would not, in all probability, have replied to anyone who addressed him by it.", "혹은 \"외눈\"이라 불렸다. 이제는 거의 텅 비어 버린 이 거대한 벌집 같은 상회를 한때 가득 채우던 젊은이들이 붙여 준 별명이었는데, 그 별명이 그의 진짜 이름을 어찌나 완전히 대신해 버렸는지, 누가 본명으로 그를 불렀다면 십중팔구 대답조차 하지 않았을 것이다."]], [["Cocles remained in M. Morrel’s service, and a most singular change had taken place in his position; he had at the same time risen to the rank of cashier, and sunk to the rank of a servant.", "코클레스는 모렐 씨 밑에 그대로 남아 있었고, 그의 처지에는 아주 기묘한 변화가 일어나 있었다. 그는 회계원의 지위로 올라서는 동시에 하인의 지위로 떨어진 셈이었다."], ["He was, however, the same Cocles, good, patient, devoted, but inflexible on the subject of arithmetic, the only point on which he would have stood firm against the world, even against M.", "그래도 그는 여전히 코클레스, 착하고 참을성 있고 헌신적인 사람이었다. 다만 셈에 관해서만은 굽힐 줄 몰라서, 그 한 가지 일에서만큼은 온 세상을 상대로, 심지어 모렐 씨를 상대로도 뜻을 굽히지 않았을 것이다."], ["Morrel; and strong in the multiplication-table, which he had at his fingers’ ends, no matter what scheme or what trap was laid to catch him.", "또한 손바닥 보듯 외고 있는 구구단에는 어찌나 강했던지, 어떤 술책을 부리고 어떤 함정을 파 놓아도 그를 걸려들게 할 수 없었다."]], [["In the midst of the disasters that befell the house, Cocles was the only one unmoved.", "상회에 닥친 재난들 한가운데서, 코클레스만이 유일하게 흔들리지 않았다."], ["But this did not arise from a want of affection; on the contrary, from a firm conviction.", "그러나 이는 애정이 부족해서가 아니었다. 오히려 그 반대로, 확고한 신념에서 비롯된 것이었다."], ["Like the rats that one by one forsake the doomed ship even before the vessel weighs anchor, so all the numerous clerks had by degrees deserted the office and the warehouse.", "가라앉을 운명의 배에서, 닻을 올리기도 전에 한 마리씩 떠나가는 쥐들처럼, 그 많던 직원들도 차츰차츰 사무실과 창고를 버리고 떠났다."], ["Cocles had seen them go without thinking of inquiring the cause of their departure.", "코클레스는 그들이 떠나는 것을 지켜보면서도 그 이유를 물어볼 생각조차 하지 않았다."], ["Everything was as we have said, a question of arithmetic to Cocles, and during twenty years he had always seen all payments made with such exactitude, that it seemed as impossible to him that the house should stop payment, as it would to a miller that the river that had so long turned his mill should cease to flow.", "앞서 말했듯이 코클레스에게는 모든 것이 셈의 문제였고, 이십 년 동안 그는 모든 대금이 어찌나 정확하게 치러지는지를 늘 보아 온 터라, 이 상회가 지급을 멈춘다는 것은, 물방앗간 주인에게 그토록 오래 물레방아를 돌려 온 강물이 흐르기를 멈춘다는 것만큼이나 있을 수 없는 일로 여겨졌다."]], [["Nothing had as yet occurred to shake Cocles’ belief; the last month’s payment had been made with the most scrupulous exactitude; Cocles had detected an overbalance of fourteen sous in his cash, and the same evening he had brought them to M.", "아직 코클레스의 믿음을 흔들 만한 일은 아무것도 일어나지 않았다. 지난달 지급도 더없이 꼼꼼하고 정확하게 이루어졌다. 코클레스는 자기 금고에서 십사 수가 남는 것을 발견하고는, 그날 저녁 그것을 모렐 씨에게 가져갔다."], ["Morrel, who, with a melancholy smile, threw them into an almost empty drawer, saying:", "모렐 씨는 서글픈 미소를 지으며 거의 텅 빈 서랍에 그것을 던져 넣고는 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“Thanks, Cocles; you are the pearl of cashiers.”", "\"고맙네, 코클레스. 자네는 회계원 중의 진주야.\""]], [["Cocles went away perfectly happy, for this eulogium of M. Morrel, himself the pearl of the honest men of Marseilles, flattered him more than a present of fifty crowns.", "코클레스는 더없이 흡족한 마음으로 물러갔다. 마르세유의 정직한 사람들 중에서도 진주와 같은 모렐 씨가 이렇게 칭찬해 주니, 그로서는 오십 크라운을 선물 받는 것보다 더 흐뭇했던 것이다."], ["But since the end of the month M. Morrel had passed many an anxious hour.", "그러나 그달 말부터 모렐 씨는 불안에 떠는 시간을 숱하게 보내고 있었다."]], [["In order to meet the payments then due; he had collected all his resources, and, fearing lest the report of his distress should get bruited abroad at Marseilles when he was known to be reduced to such an extremity, he went to the Beaucaire fair to sell his wife’s and daughter’s jewels and a portion of his plate.", "당시 만기가 닥친 대금을 치르기 위해, 그는 모든 자금을 끌어모았다. 그리고 자신이 그토록 막다른 지경에 몰렸다는 사실이 알려지면 곤경에 처했다는 소문이 마르세유에 퍼질까 두려워, 그는 보케르 시장으로 가서 아내와 딸의 보석, 그리고 은식기 일부를 팔았다."], ["By this means the end of the month was passed, but his resources were now exhausted.", "이렇게 해서 그달 말은 넘겼지만, 이제 자금은 바닥나 버렸다."], ["Credit, owing to the reports afloat, was no longer to be had; and to meet the one hundred thousand francs due on the 15th of the present month, and the one hundred thousand francs due on the 15th of the next month to M. de Boville, M.", "떠도는 소문 탓에 신용은 더 이상 얻을 수 없었다. 이달 15일에 만기가 돌아오는 십만 프랑과, 다음 달 15일에 보빌 씨에게 갚아야 할 십만 프랑을 마련하기 위해, 모렐 씨에게 남은 희망이라고는 사실상 파라옹호의 귀환뿐이었다."], ["Morrel had, in reality, no hope but the return of the _Pharaon_, of whose departure he had learnt from a vessel which had weighed anchor at the same time, and which had already arrived in harbor.", "그는 같은 시기에 닻을 올려 이미 항구에 들어와 있는 어느 배로부터 파라옹호가 출항했다는 소식을 들었던 것이다."]], [["But this vessel which, like the _Pharaon_, came from Calcutta, had been in for a fortnight, while no intelligence had been received of the _Pharaon_.", "그러나 파라옹호처럼 캘커타에서 온 그 배가 입항한 지 보름이 지나도록, 파라옹호에 관해서는 아무 소식도 들려오지 않았다."]], [["20033m\nSuch was the state of affairs when, the day after his interview with M. de Boville, the confidential clerk of the house of Thomson & French of Rome, presented himself at M. Morrel’s.", "보빌 씨와 면담을 가진 다음 날, 로마의 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회의 신임 받는 직원이 모렐 씨를 찾아왔을 때, 사정은 이러했다."]], [["Emmanuel received him; this young man was alarmed by the appearance of every new face, for every new face might be that of a new creditor, come in anxiety to question the head of the house.", "에마뉘엘이 그를 맞았다. 이 젊은이는 새로운 얼굴이 나타날 때마다 가슴이 철렁했다. 새로운 얼굴이란 곧 새로운 채권자, 불안에 차서 상회의 주인을 따지러 온 사람일지도 몰랐기 때문이다."], ["The young man, wishing to spare his employer the pain of this interview, questioned the new-comer; but the stranger declared that he had nothing to say to M.", "젊은이는 고용주에게 이 면담의 고통을 덜어 주고자 찾아온 이에게 용건을 물었다."], ["Emmanuel, and that his business was with M. Morrel in person.", "그러나 낯선 손님은 에마뉘엘 씨에게는 할 말이 없으며, 자기 용건은 모렐 씨 본인과 직접 처리할 일이라고 잘라 말했다."]], [["Emmanuel sighed, and summoned Cocles.", "에마뉘엘은 한숨을 내쉬며 코클레스를 불렀다."], ["Cocles appeared, and the young man bade him conduct the stranger to M. Morrel’s apartment.", "코클레스가 나타나자, 젊은이는 그에게 손님을 모렐 씨의 방으로 안내하라고 일렀다."], ["Cocles went first, and the stranger followed him.", "코클레스가 앞장서고 낯선 손님이 그 뒤를 따랐다."], ["On the staircase they met a beautiful girl of sixteen or seventeen, who looked with anxiety at the stranger.", "계단에서 그들은 열여섯이나 열일곱쯤 된 아름다운 아가씨와 마주쳤는데, 그녀는 불안한 눈빛으로 손님을 바라보았다."]], [["“M. Morrel is in his room, is he not, Mademoiselle Julie?”", "\"모렐 씨는 방에 계시지요, 쥘리 아가씨?\""], ["said the cashier.", "회계원이 물었다."]], [["“Yes; I think so, at least,”", "\"네, 적어도 그러실 거예요.\""], ["said the young girl hesitatingly.", "아가씨가 머뭇거리며 말했다."], ["“Go and see, Cocles, and if my father is there, announce this gentleman.”", "\"가서 보세요, 코클레스. 아버지가 계시면 이분이 오셨다고 알려 드리고요.\""]], [["“It will be useless to announce me, mademoiselle,”", "\"제 이름을 알릴 필요는 없습니다, 아가씨.\""], ["returned the Englishman. “M.", "영국인이 대답했다."], ["Morrel does not know my name; this worthy gentleman has only to announce the confidential clerk of the house of Thomson & French of Rome, with whom your father does business.”", "\"모렐 씨는 제 이름을 모르십니다. 이분께서는 그저 아버님과 거래하시는 로마의 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회에서 신임 받는 직원이 왔다고만 알려 주시면 됩니다.\""]], [["The young girl turned pale and continued to descend, while the stranger and Cocles continued to mount the staircase.", "아가씨는 얼굴이 창백해지더니 계단을 마저 내려갔고, 낯선 손님과 코클레스는 계단을 계속 올라갔다."], ["She entered the office where Emmanuel was, while Cocles, by the aid of a key he possessed, opened a door in the corner of a landing-place on the second staircase, conducted the stranger into an antechamber, opened a second door, which he closed behind him, and after having left the clerk of the house of Thomson & French alone, returned and signed to him that he could enter.", "그녀는 에마뉘엘이 있는 사무실로 들어갔다. 그동안 코클레스는 자기가 가진 열쇠로 두 번째 계단의 층계참 한구석에 있는 문을 열어, 손님을 대기실로 안내한 다음, 두 번째 문을 열고 그 문을 뒤로 닫고는, 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회의 직원을 홀로 남겨 두고 갔다가, 다시 돌아와 들어가도 좋다는 신호를 보냈다."]], [["The Englishman entered, and found Morrel seated at a table, turning over the formidable columns of his ledger, which contained the list of his liabilities.", "영국인이 들어서니, 모렐 씨가 탁자 앞에 앉아 자기 부채 목록이 적힌 장부의 그 어마어마한 숫자 행렬을 넘겨 보고 있었다."], ["At the sight of the stranger, M. Morrel closed the ledger, arose, and offered a seat to the stranger; and when he had seen him seated, resumed his own chair.", "낯선 손님을 보자 모렐 씨는 장부를 덮고 일어나 손님에게 자리를 권했고, 손님이 앉는 것을 보고 나서 자기도 다시 의자에 앉았다."], ["Fourteen years had changed the worthy merchant, who, in his thirty-sixth year at the opening of this history, was now in his fiftieth; his hair had turned white, time and sorrow had ploughed deep furrows on his brow, and his look, once so firm and penetrating, was now irresolute and wandering, as if he feared being forced to fix his attention on some particular thought or person.", "십사 년의 세월은 이 선량한 상인을 바꾸어 놓았다. 이 이야기가 시작될 무렵 서른여섯이던 그는 이제 쉰이 되어 있었다. 머리는 하얗게 세었고, 세월과 슬픔이 그의 이마에 깊은 고랑을 파 놓았으며, 한때 그토록 굳세고 꿰뚫어 보던 눈빛은 이제 어떤 생각이나 사람에게 억지로 시선을 붙잡혀야 할까 봐 두려워하는 듯 흔들리며 갈피를 잡지 못했다."]], [["The Englishman looked at him with an air of curiosity, evidently mingled with interest. “Monsieur,”", "영국인은 호기심 어린, 그러면서도 분명 관심이 섞인 표정으로 그를 바라보았다. \"선생,\""], ["said Morrel, whose uneasiness was increased by this examination, “you wish to speak to me?”", "이 시선에 불안이 더해진 모렐이 말했다. \"제게 하실 말씀이 있으신지요?\""]], [["“Yes, monsieur; you are aware from whom I come?”", "\"그렇습니다, 선생. 제가 어디서 왔는지 아시겠지요?\""]], [["“The house of Thomson & French; at least, so my cashier tells me.”", "\"톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회. 적어도 제 회계원이 그렇게 전하더군요.\""]], [["“He has told you rightly.", "\"맞게 전했습니다."], ["The house of Thomson & French had 300,000 or 400,000 francs to pay this month in France; and, knowing your strict punctuality, have collected all the bills bearing your signature, and charged me as they became due to present them, and to employ the money otherwise.”", "톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회는 이달에 프랑스에서 삼사십만 프랑을 치러야 했습니다. 그래서 선생의 철저한 신용을 알기에, 선생의 서명이 든 어음을 모두 거두어들였고, 만기가 되는 대로 제게 그것을 제시하고 그 돈을 달리 쓰라는 임무를 맡겼습니다.\""]], [["Morrel sighed deeply, and passed his hand over his forehead, which was covered with perspiration.", "모렐은 깊은 한숨을 쉬며 땀으로 뒤덮인 이마를 손으로 훑었다."]], [["“So then, sir,”", "\"그러니까, 선생,\""], ["said Morrel, “you hold bills of mine?”", "모렐이 말했다. \"제 어음을 가지고 계시다는 말씀이군요?\""]], [["“Yes, and for a considerable sum.”", "\"그렇습니다. 그것도 상당한 액수입니다.\""]], [["“What is the amount?”", "\"얼마나 됩니까?\""], ["asked Morrel with a voice he strove to render firm.", "모렐이 애써 침착하게 들리도록 목소리를 가다듬으며 물었다."]], [["20035m\n“Here is,”", "\"여기,\""], ["said the Englishman, taking a quantity of papers from his pocket, “an assignment of 200,000 francs to our house by M.", "영국인이 주머니에서 한 뭉치의 서류를 꺼내며 말했다. \"교도소 감독관인 보빌 씨가 저희 상회로 양도한 이십만 프랑짜리 채권이 있습니다."], ["de Boville, the inspector of prisons, to whom they are due.", "그분께 갚으셔야 할 돈이지요."], ["You acknowledge, of course, that you owe this sum to him?”", "물론 이 금액을 그분께 빚지고 계심을 인정하시지요?\""]], [["“Yes; he placed the money in my hands at four and a half per cent nearly five years ago.”", "\"그렇습니다. 거의 오 년 전에 그분이 연 사 푼 오 리로 제게 그 돈을 맡기셨습니다.\""]], [["“When are you to pay?”", "\"언제 갚으셔야 합니까?\""]], [["“Half the 15th of this month, half the 15th of next.”", "\"절반은 이달 15일, 절반은 다음 달 15일입니다.\""]], [["“Just so; and now here are 32,500 francs payable shortly; they are all signed by you, and assigned to our house by the holders.”", "\"그렇군요. 그리고 여기, 곧 만기가 돌아오는 삼만 이천오백 프랑짜리가 있습니다. 모두 선생이 서명한 것으로, 소지인들이 저희 상회에 양도한 것입니다.\""]], [["“I recognize them,”", "\"제 것이 맞습니다.\""], ["said Morrel, whose face was suffused, as he thought that, for the first time in his life, he would be unable to honor his own signature.", "모렐이 말했다. 그의 얼굴은 벌겋게 달아올랐다. 평생 처음으로 자신의 서명을 이행하지 못하게 되리라는 생각이 든 것이다."], ["“Is this all?”", "\"이게 전부입니까?\""]], [["“No, I have for the end of the month these bills which have been assigned to us by the house of Pascal, and the house of Wild & Turner of Marseilles, amounting to nearly 55,000 francs; in all, 287,500 francs.”", "\"아닙니다. 월말 만기로, 마르세유의 파스칼 상회와 와일드 앤드 터너 상회가 저희에게 양도한 어음들이 있는데, 거의 오만 오천 프랑에 이릅니다. 다 합치면 이십팔만 칠천오백 프랑입니다.\""]], [["It is impossible to describe what Morrel suffered during this enumeration. “Two hundred and eighty-seven thousand five hundred francs,”", "이 금액들을 하나하나 읊는 동안 모렐이 어떤 고통을 겪었는지는 이루 다 말할 수 없다. \"이십팔만 칠천오백 프랑.\""], ["repeated he.", "그가 되뇌었다."]], [["“Yes, sir,” replied the Englishman.", "\"그렇습니다, 선생.\""], ["“I will not,”", "영국인이 대답했다."], ["continued he, after a moment’s silence, “conceal from you, that while your probity and exactitude up to this moment are universally acknowledged, yet the report is current in Marseilles that you are not able to meet your liabilities.”", "\"숨기지 않고 말씀드리겠습니다.\" 그가 잠시 침묵한 뒤 말을 이었다. \"지금까지 선생의 정직함과 정확함은 누구나 인정하는 바이지만, 선생이 부채를 감당하지 못하리라는 소문이 마르세유에 떠돌고 있습니다.\""]], [["At this almost brutal speech Morrel turned deathly pale.", "거의 무례하다 할 이 말에 모렐의 얼굴은 죽은 사람처럼 창백해졌다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"선생,\""], ["said he, “up to this time—and it is now more than four-and-twenty years since I received the direction of this house from my father, who had himself conducted it for five-and-thirty years—never has anything bearing the signature of Morrel & Son been dishonored.”", "그가 말했다. \"지금까지, 선친에게서 이 상회의 경영을 물려받은 지 이제 이십사 년이 넘었고 선친께서도 그전에 삼십오 년을 이끌어 오셨습니다만, 모렐 부자 상회의 서명이 든 것이 부도난 적은 한 번도 없었습니다.\""]], [["“I know that,”", "\"압니다.\""], ["replied the Englishman.", "영국인이 대답했다."], ["“But as a man of honor should answer another, tell me fairly, shall you pay these with the same punctuality?”", "\"하지만 명예를 아는 사람이 다른 명예로운 사람에게 답하듯, 솔직히 말씀해 주십시오. 이것들도 그 같은 신용으로 치르실 수 있겠습니까?\""]], [["Morrel shuddered, and looked at the man, who spoke with more assurance than he had hitherto shown.", "모렐은 몸을 떨며 그 사람을 바라보았다. 그는 지금까지보다 한층 확신에 찬 어조로 말하고 있었다."]], [["“To questions frankly put,”", "\"솔직하게 묻는 말에는,\""], ["said he, “a straightforward answer should be given.", "모렐이 말했다. \"솔직하게 답하는 것이 도리겠지요. 그렇습니다, 갚을 것입니다."], ["Yes, I shall pay, if, as I hope, my vessel arrives safely; for its arrival will again procure me the credit which the numerous accidents, of which I have been the victim, have deprived me; but if the _Pharaon_ should be lost, and this last resource be gone——”", "바라는 대로 제 배가 무사히 도착한다면 말입니다. 그 배가 도착하면, 제가 거듭 당해 온 그 숱한 사고들로 잃어버린 신용을 다시 얻을 수 있을 테니까요. 하지만 파라옹호가 침몰하고, 이 마지막 방편마저 사라진다면……\""]], [["The poor man’s eyes filled with tears.", "가엾은 이의 눈에 눈물이 차올랐다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["said the other, “if this last resource fail you?”", "상대가 말했다. \"이 마지막 방편마저 어그러진다면 어떻게 하시겠습니까?\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["returned Morrel, “it is a cruel thing to be forced to say, but, already used to misfortune, I must habituate myself to shame.", "모렐이 대답했다. \"입에 담기 잔인한 말이지만, 이미 불행에 익숙해진 몸이니 치욕에도 저를 길들여야겠지요."], ["I fear I shall be forced to suspend payment.”", "지급을 중단할 수밖에 없을 것 같습니다.\""]], [["“Have you no friends who could assist you?”", "\"도와줄 친구는 없으십니까?\""]], [["Morrel smiled mournfully.", "모렐은 서글프게 미소 지었다."]], [["“In business, sir,”", "\"사업에는, 선생,\""], ["said he, “one has no friends, only correspondents.”", "그가 말했다. \"친구라는 게 없습니다. 거래처가 있을 뿐이지요.\""]], [["“It is true,”", "\"맞는 말씀입니다.\""], ["murmured the Englishman; “then you have but one hope.”", "영국인이 중얼거렸다. \"그렇다면 희망은 단 하나뿐이군요.\" \"단 하나입니다.\""]], [["“But one.” “The last?”", "\"그 마지막 희망 말입니까?\""]], [["“The last.”", "\"마지막 희망이지요.\""]], [["“So that if this fail——”", "\"그것이 어그러진다면……\""]], [["“I am ruined,—completely ruined!”", "\"저는 파산입니다. 완전한 파산이지요!\""]], [["“As I was on my way here, a vessel was coming into port.”", "\"제가 이리로 오는 길에, 배 한 척이 항구로 들어오고 있더군요.\""]], [["“I know it, sir; a young man, who still adheres to my fallen fortunes, passes a part of his time in a belvedere at the top of the house, in hopes of being the first to announce good news to me; he has informed me of the arrival of this ship.”", "\"압니다, 선생. 몰락해 가는 제 신세를 아직도 저버리지 않은 한 젊은이가, 좋은 소식을 제일 먼저 제게 전하고 싶은 마음에 집 꼭대기 전망대에서 시간을 보내곤 합니다. 그가 그 배가 들어왔다고 알려 주었습니다.\""]], [["“And it is not yours?”", "\"그게 선생의 배는 아니고요?\""]], [["“No, she is a Bordeaux vessel, _La Gironde_; she comes from India also; but she is not mine.”", "\"네, 그것은 보르도 선적의 라지롱드호입니다. 그 배도 인도에서 왔지만, 제 배는 아닙니다.\""]], [["“Perhaps she has spoken to the _Pharaon_, and brings you some tidings of her?”", "\"혹시 파라옹호와 교신해서, 선생께 무슨 소식을 가져왔을지도 모르지 않습니까?\""]], [["“Shall I tell you plainly one thing, sir?", "\"솔직히 한 가지 말씀드릴까요, 선생?"], ["I dread almost as much to receive any tidings of my vessel as to remain in doubt.", "저는 제 배의 소식을 듣는 것이, 모르는 채로 남아 있는 것만큼이나 두렵습니다."], ["Uncertainty is still hope.”", "불확실함은 그래도 희망이니까요.\""], ["Then in a low voice Morrel added,—“This delay is not natural.", "그러고는 낮은 목소리로 모렐이 덧붙였다. \"이렇게 늦는 건 정상이 아닙니다."], ["The _Pharaon_ left Calcutta the 5th of February; she ought to have been here a month ago.”", "파라옹호는 2월 5일에 캘커타를 떠났습니다. 한 달 전에는 이곳에 도착했어야 합니다.\""]], [["“What is that?” said the Englishman.", "\"저게 무슨 소리지요?\" 영국인이 말했다."], ["“What is the meaning of that noise?”", "\"저 소란은 무슨 영문입니까?\""]], [["“Oh, my God!”", "\"오, 맙소사!\""], ["cried Morrel, turning pale, “what is it?”", "모렐이 창백해지며 외쳤다. \"무슨 일이지?\""]], [["A loud noise was heard on the stairs of people moving hastily, and half-stifled sobs.", "계단에서 사람들이 황급히 움직이는 요란한 소리와, 반쯤 삼킨 흐느낌이 들려왔다."], ["Morrel rose and advanced to the door; but his strength failed him and he sank into a chair.", "모렐은 일어나 문 쪽으로 나아갔다. 그러나 힘이 빠져 그는 의자에 주저앉고 말았다."], ["The two men remained opposite one another, Morrel trembling in every limb, the stranger gazing at him with an air of profound pity.", "두 사람은 서로 마주한 채로 있었다. 모렐은 온몸을 부들부들 떨었고, 낯선 손님은 깊은 연민의 표정으로 그를 바라보았다."], ["The noise had ceased; but it seemed that Morrel expected something—something had occasioned the noise, and something must follow.", "소란은 그쳤다. 그러나 모렐은 무언가를 기다리는 듯했다. 무언가가 그 소란을 일으켰고, 무언가가 뒤따를 것이었다."], ["The stranger fancied he heard footsteps on the stairs; and that the footsteps, which were those of several persons, stopped at the door.", "낯선 손님은 계단에서 발소리가 들리는 것 같았다. 여러 사람의 것인 그 발소리가 문 앞에서 멈추는 듯했다."], ["A key was inserted in the lock of the first door, and the creaking of hinges was audible.", "첫 번째 문의 자물쇠에 열쇠가 꽂히고, 경첩이 삐걱대는 소리가 들렸다."]], [["“There are only two persons who have the key to that door,”", "\"저 문의 열쇠를 가진 사람은 둘뿐인데.\""], ["murmured Morrel, “Cocles and Julie.”", "모렐이 중얼거렸다. \"코클레스와 쥘리.\""]], [["At this instant the second door opened, and the young girl, her eyes bathed with tears, appeared.", "바로 그 순간 두 번째 문이 열리고, 눈물에 젖은 눈을 한 아가씨가 나타났다."], ["Morrel rose tremblingly, supporting himself by the arm of the chair.", "모렐은 떨리는 몸으로 의자의 팔걸이에 의지해 일어섰다."], ["He would have spoken, but his voice failed him.", "그는 말을 하려 했으나 목소리가 나오지 않았다."]], [["“Oh, father!”", "\"오, 아버지!\""], ["said she, clasping her hands, “forgive your child for being the bearer of evil tidings.”", "그녀가 두 손을 맞잡으며 말했다. \"나쁜 소식을 전하는 딸을 용서하세요.\""]], [["Morrel again changed color.", "모렐의 안색이 다시 변했다."], ["Julie threw herself into his arms.", "쥘리는 그의 품으로 뛰어들었다."]], [["“Oh, father, father!”", "\"오, 아버지, 아버지!\""], ["murmured she, “courage!”", "그녀가 흐느꼈다. \"힘을 내세요!\""]], [["“The _Pharaon_ has gone down, then?”", "\"그렇다면 파라옹호가 가라앉았단 말이냐?\""], ["said Morrel in a hoarse voice.", "모렐이 쉰 목소리로 말했다."], ["The young girl did not speak; but she made an affirmative sign with her head as she lay on her father’s breast.", "아가씨는 말을 하지 못했다. 그러나 아버지의 가슴에 얼굴을 묻은 채 고개를 끄덕여 그렇다는 표시를 했다."]], [["“And the crew?”", "\"선원들은?\""], ["asked Morrel.", "모렐이 물었다."]], [["“Saved,”", "\"무사해요.\""], ["said the girl; “saved by the crew of the vessel that has just entered the harbor.”", "아가씨가 말했다. \"방금 항구에 들어온 배의 선원들이 구해 주었대요.\""]], [["Morrel raised his two hands to heaven with an expression of resignation and sublime gratitude.", "모렐은 체념과 숭고한 감사가 어린 표정으로 두 손을 하늘로 들어 올렸다."]], [["“Thanks, my God,”", "\"감사합니다, 하느님.\""], ["said he, “at least thou strikest but me alone.”", "그가 말했다. \"적어도 저 하나만 치셨으니까요.\""]], [["A tear moistened the eye of the phlegmatic Englishman.", "냉정한 영국인의 눈가에 눈물이 어렸다."]], [["“Come in, come in,”", "\"들어오시오, 들어와요.\""], ["said Morrel, “for I presume you are all at the door.”", "모렐이 말했다. \"다들 문 앞에 와 있을 테니 말이오.\""]], [["Scarcely had he uttered those words when Madame Morrel entered weeping bitterly.", "이 말이 떨어지기가 무섭게 모렐 부인이 비통하게 울며 들어왔다."], ["Emmanuel followed her, and in the antechamber were visible the rough faces of seven or eight half-naked sailors.", "에마뉘엘이 그 뒤를 따랐고, 대기실에는 반쯤 헐벗은 일고여덟 명 선원들의 거친 얼굴이 보였다."], ["At the sight of these men the Englishman started and advanced a step; then restrained himself, and retired into the farthest and most obscure corner of the apartment.", "이 사람들을 보자 영국인은 흠칫하며 한 걸음 나섰다가, 이내 스스로를 억누르고는 방의 가장 멀고 가장 어두운 구석으로 물러났다."], ["Madame Morrel sat down by her husband and took one of his hands in hers, Julie still lay with her head on his shoulder, Emmanuel stood in the centre of the chamber and seemed to form the link between Morrel’s family and the sailors at the door.", "모렐 부인은 남편 곁에 앉아 그의 한 손을 자기 두 손으로 감쌌고, 쥘리는 여전히 아버지의 어깨에 머리를 기댄 채 있었으며, 에마뉘엘은 방 한가운데에 서서 모렐의 가족과 문 앞의 선원들 사이를 잇는 연결 고리처럼 보였다."]], [["“How did this happen?”", "\"어쩌다 이런 일이 일어났는가?\""], ["said Morrel.", "모렐이 말했다."]], [["“Draw nearer, Penelon,”", "\"가까이 오게, 페늘롱.\""], ["said the young man, “and tell us all about it.”", "젊은이가 말했다. \"그리고 자초지종을 다 말해 주게.\""]], [["An old seaman, bronzed by the tropical sun, advanced, twirling the remains of a hat between his hands.", "열대의 태양에 검게 그을린 늙은 뱃사람이 망가진 모자를 두 손으로 빙빙 돌리며 앞으로 나섰다."]], [["“Good-day, M. Morrel,”", "\"안녕하십니까, 모렐 씨.\""], ["said he, as if he had just quitted Marseilles the previous evening, and had just returned from Aix or Toulon.", "그가 말했다. 마치 전날 저녁에 마르세유를 떠났다가 엑스나 툴롱에서 막 돌아온 사람처럼."]], [["“Good-day, Penelon,”", "\"잘 있었나, 페늘롱.\""], ["returned Morrel, who could not refrain from smiling through his tears, “where is the captain?”", "모렐이 대답했다. 그는 눈물을 흘리면서도 미소를 참지 못했다. \"선장은 어디 있나?\""]], [["“The captain, M.", "\"선장님은요, 모렐 씨."], ["Morrel,—he has stayed behind sick at Palma; but please God, it won’t be much, and you will see him in a few days all alive and hearty.”", "팔마에서 병이 나 뒤에 남으셨습니다. 하지만 하느님 덕분에 큰 탈은 아닐 테니, 며칠 후면 멀쩡하게 살아 돌아온 선장님을 보시게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["“Well, now tell your story, Penelon.” 20039m", "\"자, 이제 자네 이야기를 해 보게, 페늘롱.\""]], [["Penelon rolled his quid in his cheek, placed his hand before his mouth, turned his head, and sent a long jet of tobacco-juice into the antechamber, advanced his foot, balanced himself, and began.", "페늘롱은 입안의 씹는담배를 한쪽 볼로 굴리고, 손을 입 앞에 갖다 대고, 고개를 돌려 대기실 쪽으로 담뱃진 한 줄기를 길게 내뱉고는, 한 발을 앞으로 내밀어 몸의 균형을 잡고 이야기를 시작했다."]], [["“You see, M.", "\"그러니까 말입니다, 모렐 씨.\" 그가 말했다. \"저희는 블랑곶과 보야도르곶 사이 어디쯤에 있었습죠."], ["Morrel,”", "일주일간 바람이 잔잔하다가 남남서풍 순풍을 받아 잘 나가던 참이었는데, 고마르 선장님이 제게 다가오시더군요. 그때 제가 키를 잡고 있었다는 걸 말씀드려야겠네요. 선장님이 그러시는 겁니다."], ["said he, “we were somewhere between Cape Blanc and Cape Boyador, sailing with a fair breeze, south-south-west after a week’s calm, when Captain Gaumard comes up to me—I was at the helm I should tell you—and says, ‘Penelon, what do you think of those clouds coming up over there?’ I was just then looking at them myself.", "'페늘롱, 저기 몰려오는 저 구름 어떻게 보나?' 마침 저도 그걸 보고 있던 참이었지요. '제가 어떻게 보냐고요, 선장님?"], ["‘What do I think, captain?", "글쎄요, 제가 보기엔 저것들이 분수에 넘치게 빨리 솟아오르고 있고, 저 정도로 시커먼 걸 보면 분명 심술을 부릴 작정인 것 같습니다.' 그랬더니 '내 생각도 그래.' 하시며 선장님이 말씀하셨죠."], ["Why I think that they are rising faster than they have any business to do, and that they would not be so black if they didn’t mean mischief.’—‘That’s my opinion too,’ said the captain, ‘and I’ll take precautions accordingly.", "'그러니 그에 맞춰 대비를 해야겠어. 돛을 너무 많이 달았어. 자, 거기, 모두 손 빌리세!"], ["We are carrying too much canvas.", "보조돛을 거두고 비행 삼각돛을 접게.' 때를 잘 맞춘 거였습죠. 돌풍이 들이닥치자 배가 기울기 시작했으니까요. '아,'"], ["Avast, there, all hands!", "선장님이 말씀하셨죠. '아직도 돛을 너무 많이 달았어. 모두 큰돛을 내려!'"], ["Take in the studding-sails and stow the flying jib.’ It was time; the squall was on us, and the vessel began to heel.", "오 분 후에 큰돛이 내려갔고, 저희는 뒷돛대 윗돛과 큰돛대 맨 위 돛만 달고 나아갔습니다. '그래, 페늘롱,' 선장님이 말씀하셨죠."], ["‘Ah,’ said the captain, ‘we have still too much canvas set; all hands lower the mainsail!’ Five minutes after, it was down; and we sailed under mizzen-topsails and top-gallant sails.", "'왜 고개를 젓나?' '글쎄요,' 제가 말했죠."], ["‘Well, Penelon,’ said the captain, ‘what makes you shake your head?’ ‘Why,’ I says, ‘I still think you’ve got too much on.’ ‘I think you’re right,’ answered he, ‘we shall have a gale.’ ‘A gale?", "'아직도 너무 많이 단 것 같아서요.' '자네 말이 맞는 것 같군.' 선장님이 대답하셨죠."], ["More than that, we shall have a tempest, or I don’t know what’s what.’ You could see the wind coming like the dust at Montredon; luckily the captain understood his business.", "'강풍이 닥치겠어.' '강풍이요? 그 정도가 아니라, 폭풍이 닥칠 겁니다."], ["‘Take in two reefs in the top-sails,’ cried the captain; ‘let go the bowlin’s, haul the brace, lower the top-gallant sails, haul out the reef-tackles on the yards.’”", "안 그러면 제가 뭘 모르는 거고요.' 몽트르동에 모래바람 일듯 바람이 몰려오는 게 보이더군요. 다행히 선장님은 자기 일을 잘 아는 분이셨죠."], ["20041m", "'윗돛에 두 단 줄이게!' 선장님이 외치셨습니다. '바람줄을 늦추고, 활대줄을 당기고, 맨 위 돛을 내리고, 활대의 줄임줄을 빼게.'\""]], [["“That was not enough for those latitudes,”", "\"그 위도에서는 그것으로 부족했습니다.\""], ["said the Englishman; “I should have taken four reefs in the topsails and furled the spanker.”", "영국인이 말했다. \"나라면 윗돛에 네 단을 줄이고 후장 종범을 접었을 겁니다.\""]], [["His firm, sonorous, and unexpected voice made everyone start.", "그의 굳건하고 낭랑하며 느닷없는 목소리에 모두가 흠칫했다."], ["Penelon put his hand over his eyes, and then stared at the man who thus criticized the manœuvres of his captain.", "페늘롱은 손을 눈 위에 갖다 대고는, 자기 선장의 조처를 이렇게 비평하는 사람을 빤히 쳐다보았다."]], [["“We did better than that, sir,”", "\"저희는 그보다 더한 것을 했습니다, 선생.\""], ["said the old sailor respectfully; “we put the helm up to run before the tempest; ten minutes after we struck our top-sails and scudded under bare poles.”", "늙은 뱃사람이 정중하게 말했다. \"키를 올려 폭풍 앞으로 달아나게 했지요. 십 분 후에는 윗돛을 거두고 맨 돛대만으로 질주했습니다.\""]], [["“The vessel was very old to risk that,”", "\"그러기엔 배가 너무 낡았는데요.\""], ["said the Englishman.", "영국인이 말했다."]], [["“Eh, it was that that did the business; after pitching heavily for twelve hours we sprung a leak. ‘Penelon,’ said the captain, ‘I think we are sinking, give me the helm, and go down into the hold.’ I gave him the helm, and descended; there was already three feet of water. ‘All hands to the pumps!’ I shouted; but it was too late, and it seemed the more we pumped the more came in. ‘Ah,’ said I, after four hours’ work, ‘since we are sinking, let us sink; we can die but once.’ ‘Is that the example you set, Penelon?’ cries the captain; ‘very well, wait a minute.’ He went into his cabin and came back with a brace of pistols. ‘I will blow the brains out of the first man who leaves the pump,’ said he.”", "\"에, 그게 바로 화근이 됐습죠. 열두 시간을 호되게 곤두박질치다가 물이 새기 시작했으니까요. '페늘롱,' 선장님이 말씀하셨죠. '아무래도 가라앉는 것 같아. 키를 내게 주고, 자넨 선창으로 내려가 보게.' 저는 선장님께 키를 넘기고 내려갔습니다. 이미 물이 석 자나 차 있더군요. '모두 펌프로!' 제가 소리쳤죠. 하지만 너무 늦었고, 퍼낼수록 더 차오르는 것 같았습니다. '아,' 네 시간을 퍼낸 뒤 제가 말했죠. '어차피 가라앉을 거라면, 그냥 가라앉읍시다. 사람은 한 번밖에 못 죽으니까요.' '그게 자네가 보이는 본보긴가, 페늘롱?' 선장님이 외치셨죠. '좋아, 잠깐 기다려.' 선장님은 선실로 들어가 권총 두 자루를 들고 나오셨습니다. '펌프를 떠나는 첫 번째 놈은 머리통을 날려 버리겠다.' 그러시더군요.\""]], [["“Well done!”", "\"잘하셨군!\""], ["said the Englishman.", "영국인이 말했다."]], [["20043m\n“There’s nothing gives you so much courage as good reasons,”", "\"그럴듯한 이유만큼 용기를 북돋아 주는 것도 없죠.\" 뱃사람이 말을 이었다."], ["continued the sailor; “and during that time the wind had abated, and the sea gone down, but the water kept rising; not much, only two inches an hour, but still it rose.", "\"그러는 사이 바람이 잦아들고 파도도 가라앉았지만, 물은 계속 차올랐습니다. 많이는 아니고 한 시간에 두 치씩, 그래도 차오르긴 했지요. 한 시간에 두 치는 별것 아닌 것 같아도, 열두 시간이면 두 자가 됩니다."], ["Two inches an hour does not seem much, but in twelve hours that makes two feet, and three we had before, that makes five.", "거기에 먼저 차 있던 석 자를 더하면 다섯 자가 되는 거죠. '자,' 선장님이 말씀하셨죠. '우리는 할 수 있는 건 다 했고, 모렐 씨도 우릴 탓하실 일은 없을 거야."], ["‘Come,’ said the captain, ‘we have done all in our power, and M.", "배를 구하려고 애썼으니, 이제 우리 목숨이나 건지자. 보트로, 다들 어서, 서둘러.'"], ["Morrel will have nothing to reproach us with, we have tried to save the ship, let us now save ourselves.", "그런데 말입니다,\" 페늘롱이 말을 이었다. \"보시다시피, 모렐 씨, 뱃사람은 자기 배에 정이 들기 마련이지만, 그래도 제 목숨에는 더 정이 드는 법이죠."], ["To the boats, my lads, as quick as you can.’ Now,” continued Penelon, “you see, M.", "그래서 저희는 두 번 말할 것도 없었습니다. 더구나 배가 발밑에서 가라앉으면서 '어서 가라, 목숨을 건져라.'"], ["Morrel, a sailor is attached to his ship, but still more to his life, so we did not wait to be told twice; the more so, that the ship was sinking under us, and seemed to say, ‘Get along—save yourselves.’ We soon launched the boat, and all eight of us got into it.", "하고 말하는 것 같았으니까요. 저희는 곧 보트를 내렸고, 여덟이 다 거기 올라탔습니다. 선장님이 마지막에 내려오셨죠."], ["The captain descended last, or rather, he did not descend, he would not quit the vessel; so I took him round the waist, and threw him into the boat, and then I jumped after him.", "아니, 내려오신 게 아니라, 도통 배를 떠나려 하지 않으셨습니다. 그래서 제가 선장님 허리를 안아 보트로 던지고, 그 뒤를 따라 뛰어내렸습니다. 때를 잘 맞춘 거였죠."], ["It was time, for just as I jumped the deck burst with a noise like the broadside of a man-of-war.", "제가 뛰어내리는 바로 그 순간, 군함의 일제 포격 같은 소리를 내며 갑판이 터져 버렸으니까요."], ["Ten minutes after she pitched forward, then the other way, spun round and round, and then good-bye to the _Pharaon_.", "십 분 후에 배는 앞으로 곤두박질치더니, 다시 반대쪽으로, 빙글빙글 돌다가, 그렇게 파라옹호와는 작별이었습니다."], ["As for us, we were three days without anything to eat or drink, so that we began to think of drawing lots who should feed the rest, when we saw _La Gironde_; we made signals of distress, she perceived us, made for us, and took us all on board.", "저희로 말하자면, 사흘을 먹지도 마시지도 못했습니다. 그래서 누가 나머지의 양식이 될지 제비를 뽑을까 생각하던 차에, 라지롱드호를 본 거죠. 저희가 조난 신호를 보내자, 그쪽이 저희를 알아보고 다가와 모두 배에 태워 주었습니다."], ["There now, M. Morrel, that’s the whole truth, on the honor of a sailor; is not it true, you fellows there?”", "자, 이제, 모렐 씨, 이게 뱃사람의 명예를 걸고 말씀드리는 사실 그대로입니다. 안 그런가, 거기 자네들?\""], ["A general murmur of approbation showed that the narrator had faithfully detailed their misfortunes and sufferings.", "일제히 터져 나온 동의의 웅성거림은 이야기꾼이 그들의 불행과 고초를 빠짐없이 충실하게 전했음을 보여 주었다."]], [["“Well, well,”", "\"좋아, 좋아.\""], ["said M. Morrel, “I know there was no one in fault but destiny.", "모렐 씨가 말했다. \"잘못한 사람은 아무도 없고 다만 운명이 그랬다는 걸 나도 아네."], ["It was the will of God that this should happen, blessed be his name.", "이리되는 것이 하느님의 뜻이었으니, 그분의 이름에 축복이 있기를."], ["What wages are due to you?”", "자네들에게 줄 품삯이 얼마인가?\""]], [["“Oh, don’t let us talk of that, M. Morrel.”", "\"오, 그 얘긴 그만두시지요, 모렐 씨.\""]], [["“Yes, but we will talk of it.”", "\"아니, 그 얘기를 해야 하네.\""]], [["“Well, then, three months,”", "\"그럼, 석 달 치입니다.\""], ["said Penelon.", "페늘롱이 말했다."]], [["“Cocles, pay two hundred francs to each of these good fellows,”", "\"코클레스, 이 착한 친구들에게 한 사람당 이백 프랑씩 지급하게.\""], ["said Morrel.", "모렐이 말했다."], ["“At another time,”", "\"다른 때 같으면,\""], ["added he, “I should have said, Give them, besides, two hundred francs over as a present; but times are changed, and the little money that remains to me is not my own, so do not think me mean on this account.”", "그가 덧붙였다. \"여기에 이백 프랑씩 더 얹어 선물로 주라고 했을 걸세. 하지만 시절이 변했고, 내게 남은 얼마 안 되는 돈도 내 것이 아니니, 이 일로 나를 인색하다 여기지는 말게.\""]], [["Penelon turned to his companions, and exchanged a few words with them.", "페늘롱은 동료들 쪽으로 돌아서서 몇 마디 주고받았다."]], [["“As for that, M. Morrel,”", "\"그게 말입니다, 모렐 씨,\""], ["said he, again turning his quid, “as for that——”", "그가 다시 씹는담배를 굴리며 말했다. \"그게 말이죠……\""]], [["“As for what?”", "\"그게 뭔가?\""]], [["“The money.” “Well——”", "\"그 돈 말입니다.\""]], [["“Well, we all say that fifty francs will be enough for us at present, and that we will wait for the rest.”", "\"그래서……\" \"그러니까, 저희 모두 지금은 오십 프랑이면 충분하고, 나머지는 기다리겠다는 겁니다.\""]], [["“Thanks, my friends, thanks!”", "\"고맙네, 친구들, 고마워!\" 모렐이 감격하여 외쳤다."], ["cried Morrel gratefully; “take it—take it; and if you can find another employer, enter his service; you are free to do so.”", "\"받게, 받아. 그리고 다른 고용주를 찾을 수 있거든, 그 사람 밑으로 들어가게. 그래도 좋아.\""]], [["These last words produced a prodigious effect on the seaman.", "이 마지막 말은 뱃사람에게 엄청난 충격을 주었다."], ["Penelon nearly swallowed his quid; fortunately he recovered.", "페늘롱은 씹는담배를 하마터면 삼킬 뻔했으나, 다행히 가까스로 추슬렀다."]], [["“What, M. Morrel!”", "\"아니, 모렐 씨!\""], ["said he in a low voice, “you send us away; you are then angry with us!”", "그가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"저희를 내치시는 겁니까? 그럼 저희에게 화가 나신 거고요!\""]], [["“No, no,” said M.", "\"아니, 아니야.\" 모렐 씨가 말했다."], ["Morrel, “I am not angry, quite the contrary, and I do not send you away; but I have no more ships, and therefore I do not want any sailors.”", "\"화가 난 게 아니라, 오히려 그 반대일세. 자네들을 내치는 것도 아니고. 하지만 내겐 더 이상 배가 없으니, 뱃사람도 필요가 없는 게야.\""]], [["“No more ships!”", "\"배가 더 없으시다고요!\""], ["returned Penelon; “well, then, you’ll build some; we’ll wait for you.”", "페늘롱이 대꾸했다. \"그럼, 새로 지으시면 되죠. 저희가 기다리겠습니다.\""]], [["“I have no money to build ships with, Penelon,”", "\"배를 지을 돈이 없네, 페늘롱.\""], ["said the poor owner mournfully, “so I cannot accept your kind offer.”", "가엾은 선주가 서글프게 말했다. \"그러니 자네들의 고마운 제안을 받아들일 수가 없구먼.\""]], [["“No more money?", "\"돈이 더 없으시다고요?"], ["Then you must not pay us; we can scud, like the _Pharaon_, under bare poles.”", "그럼 저희한테 품삯을 안 주시면 됩니다. 저희도 파라옹호처럼 맨 돛대로 질주할 수 있으니까요.\""]], [["“Enough, enough!”", "\"그만, 그만!\""], ["cried Morrel, almost overpowered; “leave me, I pray you; we shall meet again in a happier time.", "모렐이 거의 무너질 듯 외쳤다. \"제발, 나를 두고 가 주게. 더 좋은 시절에 우리 다시 만나세."], ["Emmanuel, go with them, and see that my orders are executed.”", "에마뉘엘, 이들과 함께 가서 내 지시가 이행되는지 살피게.\""]], [["“At least, we shall see each other again, M. Morrel?”", "\"적어도, 저희가 다시 뵐 수는 있는 거지요, 모렐 씨?\""], ["asked Penelon.", "페늘롱이 물었다."]], [["“Yes; I hope so, at least.", "\"그래. 적어도 그러기를 바라네."], ["Now go.”", "자, 이제 가게.\""], ["He made a sign to Cocles, who went first; the seamen followed him and Emmanuel brought up the rear. “Now,”", "그가 코클레스에게 신호를 보내자 코클레스가 앞장섰고, 뱃사람들이 그 뒤를 따랐으며 에마뉘엘이 맨 뒤를 맡았다. \"자,\" 선주가 아내와 딸에게 말했다."], ["said the owner to his wife and daughter, “leave me; I wish to speak with this gentleman.” 20045m", "\"나를 좀 두고 나가 주오. 이 신사분과 이야기를 나눠야겠소.\""]], [["And he glanced towards the clerk of Thomson & French, who had remained motionless in the corner during this scene, in which he had taken no part, except the few words we have mentioned.", "그러고는 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회의 직원 쪽으로 눈길을 던졌다. 그는 이 장면 내내 한구석에서 미동도 없이, 앞서 말한 몇 마디 외에는 아무런 관여도 하지 않은 채 있었다."], ["The two women looked at this person whose presence they had entirely forgotten, and retired; but, as she left the apartment, Julie gave the stranger a supplicating glance, to which he replied by a smile that an indifferent spectator would have been surprised to see on his stern features.", "두 여인은 그 존재를 까맣게 잊고 있던 이 사람을 바라보고는 물러갔다. 그러나 쥘리는 방을 나서면서 낯선 손님에게 애원하는 눈길을 보냈고, 그는 미소로 답했다. 무심한 구경꾼이라면 그 근엄한 얼굴에 떠오른 그런 미소를 보고 놀랐을 것이다."], ["The two men were left alone. “Well, sir,”", "두 남자만 남았다. \"자, 선생.\""], ["said Morrel, sinking into a chair, “you have heard all, and I have nothing further to tell you.”", "모렐이 의자에 주저앉으며 말했다. \"다 들으셨으니, 제가 더 드릴 말씀은 없습니다.\""]], [["“I see,”", "\"보아하니,\""], ["returned the Englishman, “that a fresh and unmerited misfortune has overwhelmed you, and this only increases my desire to serve you.”", "영국인이 대답했다. \"새롭고도 부당한 불행이 선생을 덮친 것 같군요. 이것이 선생을 돕고 싶은 제 마음을 더욱 키울 뿐입니다.\""]], [["“Oh, sir!”", "\"오, 선생!\""], ["cried Morrel.", "모렐이 외쳤다."]], [["“Let me see,”", "\"어디 봅시다.\""], ["continued the stranger, “I am one of your largest creditors.”", "낯선 손님이 말을 이었다. \"저는 선생의 가장 큰 채권자 중 하나입니다.\""]], [["“Your bills, at least, are the first that will fall due.”", "\"적어도 선생의 어음이 가장 먼저 만기가 돌아오지요.\""]], [["“Do you wish for time to pay?”", "\"갚으실 시간이 필요하십니까?\""]], [["“A delay would save my honor, and consequently my life.”", "\"기한을 늦춰 주신다면 제 명예를, 따라서 제 목숨을 구하는 셈이 될 겁니다.\""]], [["“How long a delay do you wish for?”", "\"얼마나 늦춰 드리면 되겠습니까?\""]], [["Morrel reflected.", "모렐은 곰곰이 생각했다."], ["“Two months,”", "\"두 달입니다.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“I will give you three,”", "\"석 달을 드리지요.\""], ["replied the stranger.", "낯선 손님이 대답했다."]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["asked Morrel, “will the house of Thomson & French consent?”", "모렐이 물었다. \"톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회가 동의하겠습니까?\""]], [["“Oh, I take everything on myself.", "\"오, 모든 것을 제가 책임지겠습니다."], ["Today is the 5th of June.” “Yes.”", "오늘이 6월 5일이지요.\""]], [["“Well, renew these bills up to the 5th of September; and on the 5th of September at eleven o’clock (the hand of the clock pointed to eleven), I shall come to receive the money.”", "\"그렇습니다.\" \"좋습니다. 그럼 이 어음들을 9월 5일까지로 갱신하십시오. 그리고 9월 5일 열한 시에 (시계 바늘이 열한 시를 가리키고 있었다), 제가 돈을 받으러 오겠습니다.\""]], [["“I shall expect you,”", "\"기다리고 있겠습니다.\""], ["returned Morrel; “and I will pay you—or I shall be dead.”", "모렐이 대답했다. \"그리고 갚아 드리겠습니다. 아니면 저는 죽은 목숨일 테고요.\""], ["These last words were uttered in so low a tone that the stranger could not hear them.", "이 마지막 말은 어찌나 낮은 목소리로 내뱉었던지 낯선 손님은 알아듣지 못했다."], ["The bills were renewed, the old ones destroyed, and the poor ship-owner found himself with three months before him to collect his resources.", "어음들이 갱신되고, 옛 어음들이 폐기되니, 가엾은 선주는 자금을 모을 석 달의 말미를 손에 쥐게 되었다."], ["The Englishman received his thanks with the phlegm peculiar to his nation; and Morrel, overwhelming him with grateful blessings, conducted him to the staircase.", "영국인은 그 나라 사람 특유의 냉정함으로 그의 감사를 받았다. 모렐은 감사의 축복을 쏟아부으며 그를 계단까지 배웅했다. 낯선 손님은 계단에서 쥘리와 마주쳤다."], ["The stranger met Julie on the stairs; she pretended to be descending, but in reality she was waiting for him.", "그녀는 내려가는 척했지만, 사실은 그를 기다리고 있었던 것이다. \"오, 선생……\""], ["“Oh, sir”—said she, clasping her hands.", "그녀가 두 손을 맞잡으며 말했다."]], [["“Mademoiselle,”", "\"아가씨,\""], ["said the stranger, “one day you will receive a letter signed ‘Sinbad the Sailor.’ Do exactly what the letter bids you, however strange it may appear.”", "낯선 손님이 말했다. \"언젠가 '뱃사람 신드바드'라고 서명된 편지를 한 통 받게 될 겁니다. 그 편지가 아무리 이상하게 보이더라도, 시키는 대로 정확히 따르세요.\""]], [["“Yes, sir,”", "\"네, 선생.\""], ["returned Julie.", "쥘리가 대답했다."]], [["“Do you promise?”", "\"약속하시겠습니까?\""]], [["“I swear to you I will.”", "\"맹세코 그렇게 하겠어요.\""]], [["“It is well.", "\"좋습니다."], ["Adieu, mademoiselle.", "그럼 안녕히, 아가씨."], ["Continue to be the good, sweet girl you are at present, and I have great hopes that Heaven will reward you by giving you Emmanuel for a husband.”", "지금처럼 착하고 다정한 아가씨로 계세요. 그러면 하늘이 에마뉘엘을 남편으로 주어 아가씨에게 보답하리라고 저는 크게 기대하고 있습니다.\""]], [["Julie uttered a faint cry, blushed like a rose, and leaned against the baluster.", "쥘리는 가냘픈 탄성을 지르며 장미처럼 얼굴을 붉히고 난간에 몸을 기댔다."], ["The stranger waved his hand, and continued to descend.", "낯선 손님은 손을 흔들고는 계단을 계속 내려갔다."], ["In the court he found Penelon, who, with a rouleau of a hundred francs in either hand, seemed unable to make up his mind to retain them.", "마당에서 그는 페늘롱과 마주쳤는데, 페늘롱은 양손에 백 프랑짜리 돈뭉치를 하나씩 들고서도 그것을 가져야 할지 마음을 정하지 못하는 듯했다."], ["“Come with me, my friend,”", "\"나와 함께 가지, 친구.\""], ["said the Englishman; “I wish to speak to you.”", "영국인이 말했다. \"자네에게 할 말이 있네.\""]], [["# Chapter 30. The Fifth of September", "# Chapter 30. The Fifth of September"]], [["The extension provided for by the agent of Thomson & French, at the moment when Morrel expected it least, was to the poor shipowner so decided a stroke of good fortune that he almost dared to believe that fate was at length grown weary of wasting her spite upon him.", "톰슨앤드프렌치 상회의 대리인이 내준 유예는, 모렐이 전혀 기대하지 않던 순간에 찾아온 것이라, 가엾은 선주에게는 운명이 마침내 자신을 향한 악의를 거두는 데 지쳤다고 믿어도 좋을 만큼 분명한 행운의 손길이었다."], ["The same day he told his wife, Emmanuel, and his daughter all that had occurred; and a ray of hope, if not of tranquillity, returned to the family.", "그날 그는 아내와 에마뉘엘, 딸에게 그동안 일어난 모든 일을 들려주었다. 그러자 평온까지는 아니어도 희망의 빛 한 줄기가 가족에게 되돌아왔다."], ["Unfortunately, however, Morrel had not only engagements with the house of Thomson & French, who had shown themselves so considerate towards him; and, as he had said, in business he had correspondents, and not friends.", "그러나 불행히도 모렐은 그토록 사려 깊게 대해 준 톰슨앤드프렌치 상회하고만 거래가 있는 것이 아니었다. 그의 말처럼, 사업에는 거래처가 있을 뿐 친구는 없었다."], ["When he thought the matter over, he could by no means account for this generous conduct on the part of Thomson & French towards him; and could only attribute it to some such selfish argument as this: “We had better help a man who owes us nearly 300,000 francs, and have those 300,000 francs at the end of three months than hasten his ruin, and get only six or eight per cent of our money back again.”", "그는 곰곰이 생각해 보아도 톰슨앤드프렌치 상회가 자기에게 보여 준 그 너그러운 처사를 도무지 이해할 수 없었다. 다만 이런 식의 타산적인 계산 탓이려니 짐작할 뿐이었다. \"우리에게 30만 프랑 가까이 빚진 사람을 도와 석 달 뒤에 그 30만 프랑을 온전히 받는 편이, 그를 서둘러 파산시켜 빌려준 돈의 육 퍼센트나 팔 퍼센트밖에 못 건지는 것보다 낫다.\""]], [["Unfortunately, whether through envy or stupidity, all Morrel’s correspondents did not take this view; and some even came to a contrary decision.", "그러나 불행히도, 시기심 때문이든 어리석음 때문이든, 모렐의 거래처들이 모두 이런 견해를 가진 것은 아니었다. 더러는 정반대의 결정을 내리기도 했다."], ["The bills signed by Morrel were presented at his office with scrupulous exactitude, and, thanks to the delay granted by the Englishman, were paid by Cocles with equal punctuality.", "모렐이 서명한 어음들은 한 치의 오차도 없이 정확하게 그의 사무실로 제시되었고, 영국인이 준 유예 덕분에 코클레스가 그만큼 한 치의 오차도 없이 그것들을 지불했다."], ["Cocles thus remained in his accustomed tranquillity.", "코클레스는 그렇게 평소의 평온을 유지했다."], ["It was Morrel alone who remembered with alarm, that if he had to repay on the 15th the 50,000 francs of M. de Boville, and on the 30th the 32,500 francs of bills, for which, as well as the debt due to the inspector of prisons, he had time granted, he must be a ruined man.", "다만 모렐 혼자만이 불안에 떨며 기억하고 있었다. 15일에 보빌 씨에게 5만 프랑을 갚아야 하고, 30일에는 어음 3만 2천 5백 프랑을 갚아야 한다는 것을, 그리고 이 어음과 형무소 감독관에게 진 빚 모두 유예를 받은 것일 뿐이니, 그것을 갚지 못하면 그는 끝장난 사람이라는 것을."]], [["The opinion of all the commercial men was that, under the reverses which had successively weighed down Morrel, it was impossible for him to remain solvent.", "모든 상인의 의견은 한결같았다. 연이어 모렐을 짓눌러 온 불운을 보건대 그가 지불 능력을 유지하기란 불가능하다는 것이었다."], ["Great, therefore, was the astonishment when at the end of the month, he cancelled all his obligations with his usual punctuality.", "그러니 월말에 그가 여느 때처럼 정확하게 모든 채무를 청산했을 때 사람들의 놀라움은 컸다."], ["Still confidence was not restored to all minds, and the general opinion was that the complete ruin of the unfortunate shipowner had been postponed only until the end of the month.", "그래도 모두의 마음에 신뢰가 회복되지는 않았다. 이 불운한 선주의 완전한 파산은 그저 월말까지 미루어진 것뿐이라는 게 일반적인 견해였다."]], [["The month passed, and Morrel made extraordinary efforts to get in all his resources.", "한 달이 지나는 동안 모렐은 모든 자금을 끌어모으려고 갖은 애를 썼다."], ["Formerly his paper, at any date, was taken with confidence, and was even in request.", "예전에는 그의 어음이라면 어떤 만기든 신용을 받았고, 심지어 서로 사려고 들 정도였다."], ["Morrel now tried to negotiate bills at ninety days only, and none of the banks would give him credit.", "그런데 지금은 모렐이 만기 구십 일짜리 어음만이라도 융통해 보려 했지만, 어느 은행도 그에게 신용을 내주지 않았다."], ["Fortunately, Morrel had some funds coming in on which he could rely; and, as they reached him, he found himself in a condition to meet his engagements when the end of July came.", "다행히 모렐에게는 기댈 만한 수입이 얼마간 들어올 예정이었다. 그 돈이 들어오자, 그는 7월 말이 왔을 때 채무를 감당할 수 있는 처지가 되었다."]], [["The agent of Thomson & French had not been again seen at Marseilles; the day after, or two days after his visit to Morrel, he had disappeared; and as in that city he had had no intercourse but with the mayor, the inspector of prisons, and M.", "톰슨앤드프렌치 상회의 대리인은 마르세유에서 다시 모습을 보이지 않았다. 모렐을 방문한 다음 날, 혹은 이틀 뒤에 그는 자취를 감추었다. 그 도시에서 그가 상대한 사람이라고는 시장과 형무소 감독관, 그리고 모렐 씨뿐이었으니, 그가 떠난 자리에는 이 세 사람의 기억 말고는 아무런 흔적도 남지 않았다."], ["Morrel, his departure left no trace except in the memories of these three persons. As to the sailors of the _Pharaon_, they must have found snug berths elsewhere, for they also had disappeared.", "파라옹호 선원들로 말하자면, 그들도 자취를 감춘 것으로 보아 어딘가 다른 곳에서 아늑한 일자리를 찾았음이 분명했다."]], [["Captain Gaumard, recovered from his illness, had returned from Palma.", "병에서 회복된 고마르 선장은 팔마에서 돌아왔다."], ["He delayed presenting himself at Morrel’s, but the owner, hearing of his arrival, went to see him.", "그는 모렐을 찾아가기를 미루었지만, 선주는 그가 도착했다는 소식을 듣고 그를 만나러 갔다."], ["The worthy shipowner knew, from Penelon’s recital, of the captain’s brave conduct during the storm, and tried to console him. He brought him also the amount of his wages, which Captain Gaumard had not dared to apply for.", "마음씨 좋은 선주는 페늘롱의 이야기를 통해 폭풍 속에서 선장이 보여 준 용감한 행동을 알고 있던 터라, 그를 위로하려 했다. 또한 고마르 선장이 감히 청하지 못하던 봉급도 가져다주었다."]], [["As he descended the staircase, Morrel met Penelon, who was going up.", "계단을 내려오던 모렐은 올라오던 페늘롱과 마주쳤다."], ["Penelon had, it would seem, made good use of his money, for he was newly clad.", "페늘롱은 자기 돈을 잘 쓴 모양인지 새 옷을 차려입고 있었다."], ["When he saw his employer, the worthy tar seemed much embarrassed, drew on one side into the corner of the landing-place, passed his quid from one cheek to the other, stared stupidly with his great eyes, and only acknowledged the squeeze of the hand which Morrel as usual gave him by a slight pressure in return.", "고용주를 보자 마음씨 좋은 이 뱃사람은 몹시 당황한 듯 계단참 구석으로 비켜서더니, 씹던 담배를 이쪽 볼에서 저쪽 볼로 옮기며 커다란 두 눈으로 멍하니 쳐다보았다. 모렐이 여느 때처럼 손을 잡아 주었지만, 그저 가볍게 마주 쥐는 것으로 답할 뿐이었다."], ["Morrel attributed Penelon’s embarrassment to the elegance of his attire; it was evident the good fellow had not gone to such an expense on his own account; he was, no doubt, engaged on board some other vessel, and thus his bashfulness arose from the fact of his not having, if we may so express ourselves, worn mourning for the _Pharaon_ longer.", "모렐은 페늘롱이 당황한 것을 그 말끔한 옷차림 탓으로 여겼다. 이 선량한 친구가 제 돈으로 그만한 사치를 부렸을 리 없으니, 분명 다른 배에 일자리를 얻은 것이고, 그래서 말하자면 파라옹호의 상을 더 오래 입지 못한 것이 부끄러웠으리라 생각한 것이다."], ["Perhaps he had come to tell Captain Gaumard of his good luck, and to offer him employment from his new master.", "어쩌면 고마르 선장에게 자신의 행운을 알리고, 새 주인 밑의 일자리를 권하러 온 것인지도 몰랐다."]], [["“Worthy fellows!”", "\"착한 사람들 같으니!\""], ["said Morrel, as he went away, “may your new master love you as I loved you, and be more fortunate than I have been!”", "모렐은 자리를 떠나며 말했다. \"그대들의 새 주인이 내가 그대들을 아꼈던 만큼 아껴 주기를, 그리고 나보다 더 운이 좋기를!\""]], [["20049m\nAugust rolled by in unceasing efforts on the part of Morrel to renew his credit or revive the old.", "8월은 신용을 새로 트거나 옛 신용을 되살리려는 모렐의 끊임없는 노력 속에 흘러갔다."], ["On the 20th of August it was known at Marseilles that he had left town in the mailcoach, and then it was said that the bills would go to protest at the end of the month, and that Morrel had gone away and left his chief clerk Emmanuel, and his cashier Cocles, to meet the creditors.", "8월 20일, 마르세유에는 그가 역마차를 타고 마을을 떠났다는 소식이 돌았다. 그러자 월말이면 어음들이 부도가 날 것이고, 모렐은 채권자들을 상대하라며 수석 서기 에마뉘엘과 출납계 코클레스만 남겨 두고 떠나 버렸다는 말이 나돌았다."], ["But, contrary to all expectation, when the 31st of August came, the house opened as usual, and Cocles appeared behind the grating of the counter, examined all bills presented with the usual scrutiny, and, from first to last, paid all with the usual precision.", "그러나 모두의 예상과는 달리, 8월 31일이 되자 상회는 여느 때처럼 문을 열었고, 코클레스는 계산대 창살 뒤에 나타나 제시되는 어음을 모두 평소대로 꼼꼼히 살펴보고는, 처음부터 끝까지 전부 평소처럼 정확하게 지불했다."], ["There came in, moreover, two drafts which M. Morrel had fully anticipated, and which Cocles paid as punctually as the bills which the shipowner had accepted.", "게다가 모렐 씨가 충분히 예상하고 있던 두 건의 환어음까지 들어왔고, 코클레스는 그것들도 선주가 인수한 어음들만큼 정확하게 지불했다."], ["All this was incomprehensible, and then, with the tenacity peculiar to prophets of bad news, the failure was put off until the end of September.", "이 모든 것이 도무지 이해되지 않았고, 그러자 불길한 소식을 전하는 예언자 특유의 끈질김으로, 파산은 9월 말로 미루어졌다."]], [["On the 1st, Morrel returned; he was awaited by his family with extreme anxiety, for from this journey to Paris they hoped great things.", "9월 1일, 모렐이 돌아왔다. 가족은 극도의 불안 속에 그를 기다리고 있었다. 그 파리행에 가족은 큰 기대를 걸고 있었던 것이다."], ["Morrel had thought of Danglars, who was now immensely rich, and had lain under great obligations to Morrel in former days, since to him it was owing that Danglars entered the service of the Spanish banker, with whom he had laid the foundations of his vast wealth.", "모렐은 당글라르를 생각하고 있었다. 당글라르는 이제 엄청난 부자가 되어 있었고, 예전에 모렐에게 큰 신세를 진 적이 있었다. 당글라르가 스페인 은행가의 밑에 들어가 막대한 부의 기초를 닦을 수 있었던 것이 바로 모렐 덕분이었기 때문이다."], ["It was said at this moment that Danglars was worth from six to eight millions of francs, and had unlimited credit.", "당시 당글라르는 6백만에서 8백만 프랑의 재산가이며 무한한 신용을 누리고 있다는 말이 돌았다."], ["Danglars, then, without taking a crown from his pocket, could save Morrel; he had but to pass his word for a loan, and Morrel was saved.", "그러니 당글라르는 제 주머니에서 동전 한 닢 꺼내지 않고도 모렐을 구할 수 있었다. 대출에 보증만 서 주면 모렐은 살아나는 것이었다."], ["Morrel had long thought of Danglars, but had kept away from some instinctive motive, and had delayed as long as possible availing himself of this last resource.", "모렐은 오래전부터 당글라르를 떠올리고 있었지만, 어떤 본능적인 까닭에 그를 멀리해 왔고, 이 마지막 방편에 기대는 일을 가능한 한 미루어 왔다."], ["And Morrel was right, for he returned home crushed by the humiliation of a refusal.", "그리고 모렐의 판단은 옳았다. 그는 거절당한 굴욕에 짓눌린 채 집으로 돌아왔으니까."]], [["Yet, on his arrival, Morrel did not utter a complaint, or say one harsh word.", "그러나 모렐은 돌아와서도 불평 한마디, 모진 말 한마디 하지 않았다."], ["He embraced his weeping wife and daughter, pressed Emmanuel’s hand with friendly warmth, and then going to his private room on the second floor had sent for Cocles.", "그는 흐느끼는 아내와 딸을 끌어안고, 에마뉘엘의 손을 다정하고 따뜻하게 마주 잡았다. 그러고는 이층에 있는 자기 방으로 올라가 코클레스를 불렀다."]], [["“Then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["said the two women to Emmanuel, “we are indeed ruined.”", "두 여인이 에마뉘엘에게 말했다. \"우리는 정말 파산하는 거예요.\""]], [["It was agreed in a brief council held among them, that Julie should write to her brother, who was in garrison at Nîmes, to come to them as speedily as possible.", "그들끼리 짧게 의논한 끝에, 쥘리가 님에 주둔 중인 오빠에게 편지를 써서 되도록 빨리 와 달라고 청하기로 했다."], ["The poor women felt instinctively that they required all their strength to support the blow that impended.", "가엾은 두 여인은 닥쳐오는 타격을 견디려면 온 힘을 다 끌어모아야 한다는 것을 본능적으로 느끼고 있었다."], ["Besides, Maximilian Morrel, though hardly two-and-twenty, had great influence over his father.", "게다가 막시밀리앙 모렐은 이제 겨우 스물두 살이었지만 아버지에게 큰 영향력을 지니고 있었다."]], [["He was a strong-minded, upright young man.", "그는 의지가 굳고 올곧은 청년이었다."], ["At the time when he decided on his profession his father had no desire to choose for him, but had consulted young Maximilian’s taste.", "그가 자기 직업을 정할 때, 아버지는 아들 대신 골라 주려 하지 않고 젊은 막시밀리앙의 뜻을 물었다."], ["He had at once declared for a military life, and had in consequence studied hard, passed brilliantly through the Polytechnic School, and left it as sub-lieutenant of the 53rd of the line.", "막시밀리앙은 곧바로 군인의 길을 택했고, 그리하여 열심히 공부해 우수한 성적으로 종합기술학교를 졸업한 뒤 보병 제53연대 소위로 임관했다."], ["For a year he had held this rank, and expected promotion on the first vacancy.", "그는 일 년째 그 계급에 머물러 있었고, 자리가 나는 대로 진급하기를 기다리고 있었다."], ["In his regiment Maximilian Morrel was noted for his rigid observance, not only of the obligations imposed on a soldier, but also of the duties of a man; and he thus gained the name of “the stoic.”", "막시밀리앙 모렐은 연대에서 군인에게 지워진 의무뿐 아니라 한 인간으로서의 도리까지 엄격히 지키기로 이름이 나 있었고, 그리하여 \"금욕주의자\"라는 별명을 얻었다."], ["We need hardly say that many of those who gave him this epithet repeated it because they had heard it, and did not even know what it meant.", "그에게 이 별명을 붙인 자들 가운데 많은 수가 그저 남이 하는 말을 따라 했을 뿐, 그게 무슨 뜻인지조차 모른 채 되뇌었다는 것은 굳이 말할 필요도 없으리라."]], [["This was the young man whom his mother and sister called to their aid to sustain them under the serious trial which they felt they would soon have to endure.", "어머니와 누이가 곧 닥쳐올 거라 느끼는 그 혹독한 시련을 견뎌 낼 힘을 얻고자 불러들인 청년이 바로 이런 사람이었다. 그들은 이 사건의 심각함을 잘못 짚은 것이 아니었다."], ["They had not mistaken the gravity of this event, for the moment after Morrel had entered his private office with Cocles, Julie saw the latter leave it pale, trembling, and his features betraying the utmost consternation.", "모렐이 코클레스와 함께 자기 방으로 들어간 직후, 쥘리는 코클레스가 새파랗게 질려 부들부들 떨며, 얼굴에 더할 나위 없는 경악을 드러낸 채 그 방에서 나오는 것을 보았기 때문이다."], ["She would have questioned him as he passed by her, but the worthy creature hastened down the staircase with unusual precipitation, and only raised his hands to heaven and exclaimed:", "쥘리는 그가 곁을 지날 때 물어보려 했지만, 선량한 이 사람은 평소와 달리 황급히 계단을 내려가며 두 손을 하늘로 치켜들고는 외칠 뿐이었다."]], [["“Oh, mademoiselle, mademoiselle, what a dreadful misfortune!", "\"아아, 아가씨, 아가씨, 이런 끔찍한 변고가 있나요!"], ["Who could ever have believed it!”", "누가 이런 일을 믿을 수 있었겠습니까!\""]], [["A moment afterwards Julie saw him go upstairs carrying two or three heavy ledgers, a portfolio, and a bag of money.", "잠시 후 쥘리는 그가 묵직한 장부 두세 권과 서류철 하나, 돈자루 하나를 들고 다시 올라가는 것을 보았다."]], [["Morrel examined the ledgers, opened the portfolio, and counted the money.", "모렐은 장부를 살펴보고, 서류철을 펼치고, 돈을 세었다."], ["All his funds amounted to 6,000 or 8,000 francs, his bills receivable up to the 5th to 4,000 or 5,000, which, making the best of everything, gave him 14,000 francs to meet debts amounting to 287,500 francs. He had not even the means for making a possible settlement on account.", "그의 자금을 모두 합치면 6천에서 8천 프랑, 5일까지 받을 어음이 4천에서 5천 프랑이었으니, 아무리 좋게 쳐도 28만 7천 5백 프랑에 이르는 빚을 갚는 데 1만 4천 프랑이 전부였다. 일부라도 변제할 만한 가능성조차 없었다."]], [["However, when Morrel went down to his dinner, he appeared very calm.", "그런데도 모렐은 저녁을 먹으러 내려올 때 무척 차분해 보였다."], ["This calmness was more alarming to the two women than the deepest dejection would have been.", "이 차분함이 두 여인에게는 더없이 깊은 절망보다 더 불안하게 다가왔다."], ["After dinner Morrel usually went out and used to take his coffee at the club of the Phocéens, and read the _Semaphore_; this day he did not leave the house, but returned to his office.", "저녁 식사 후면 모렐은 으레 밖으로 나가 포세앵 클럽에서 커피를 마시며 「세마포르」를 읽곤 했다. 그런데 이날은 집을 나서지 않고 다시 자기 방으로 돌아갔다."]], [["As to Cocles, he seemed completely bewildered.", "코클레스로 말하자면, 완전히 넋이 나간 듯했다."], ["For part of the day he went into the courtyard, seated himself on a stone with his head bare and exposed to the blazing sun.", "그는 한낮의 한동안을 안마당으로 나가 맨머리로 이글거리는 햇볕 아래 돌 위에 앉아 있었다."], ["Emmanuel tried to comfort the women, but his eloquence faltered.", "에마뉘엘은 두 여인을 위로하려 했지만 그의 말은 자꾸 막혔다."], ["The young man was too well acquainted with the business of the house, not to feel that a great catastrophe hung over the Morrel family.", "청년은 상회의 사정을 너무나 잘 알았기에, 모렐 가문에 큰 재앙이 드리워져 있음을 느끼지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Night came, the two women had watched, hoping that when he left his room Morrel would come to them, but they heard him pass before their door, and trying to conceal the noise of his footsteps.", "밤이 왔고, 두 여인은 모렐이 방에서 나오면 자기들에게 와 주리라 기대하며 깨어 있었다. 그러나 그들은 그가 발소리를 죽이려 애쓰며 자기들 방문 앞을 지나가는 소리를 들었을 뿐이었다."], ["They listened; he went into his sleeping-room, and fastened the door inside.", "두 사람은 귀를 기울였다. 그는 침실로 들어가 안에서 문을 잠갔다."], ["Madame Morrel sent her daughter to bed, and half an hour after Julie had retired, she rose, took off her shoes, and went stealthily along the passage, to see through the keyhole what her husband was doing.", "모렐 부인은 딸을 잠자리에 들게 했고, 쥘리가 물러간 지 삼십 분쯤 지나자 자리에서 일어나 신발을 벗고 살금살금 복도를 따라가, 열쇠 구멍으로 남편이 무엇을 하고 있는지 들여다보려 했다."]], [["In the passage she saw a retreating shadow; it was Julie, who, uneasy herself, had anticipated her mother.", "복도에서 부인은 물러나는 그림자 하나를 보았다. 쥘리였다. 자신도 불안했던 쥘리가 어머니보다 먼저 와 있었던 것이다."], ["The young lady went towards Madame Morrel.", "딸이 모렐 부인 쪽으로 다가왔다."]], [["“He is writing,”", "\"아버지가 글을 쓰고 계세요.\""], ["she said.", "쥘리가 말했다."]], [["They had understood each other without speaking.", "두 사람은 말없이도 서로의 마음을 알아차렸다."], ["Madame Morrel looked again through the keyhole, Morrel was writing; but Madame Morrel remarked, what her daughter had not observed, that her husband was writing on stamped paper.", "모렐 부인이 다시 열쇠 구멍으로 들여다보았다. 모렐은 글을 쓰고 있었다. 그러나 모렐 부인은 딸이 알아채지 못한 것을 알아보았다."], ["The terrible idea that he was writing his will flashed across her; she shuddered, and yet had not strength to utter a word.", "남편이 인지가 찍힌 종이에 글을 쓰고 있었던 것이다. 그가 유언장을 쓰고 있다는 끔찍한 생각이 머릿속을 스쳤다. 부인은 몸서리를 쳤지만, 차마 한마디도 입 밖에 낼 힘이 없었다."]], [["Next day M. Morrel seemed as calm as ever, went into his office as usual, came to his breakfast punctually, and then, after dinner, he placed his daughter beside him, took her head in his arms, and held her for a long time against his bosom.", "이튿날 모렐 씨는 여느 때처럼 차분해 보였고, 평소대로 사무실에 들어갔으며, 정확한 시간에 아침을 들었다. 그러고는 저녁 식사 후 딸을 곁에 앉히고 두 팔로 딸의 머리를 감싸 안아 오래도록 가슴에 꼭 끌어안았다."], ["In the evening, Julie told her mother, that although he was apparently so calm, she had noticed that her father’s heart beat violently.", "그날 저녁 쥘리는 어머니에게, 아버지가 겉으로는 그토록 차분해 보여도 가슴이 거세게 뛰는 것을 느꼈다고 말했다."]], [["The next two days passed in much the same way.", "그다음 이틀도 거의 같은 식으로 지나갔다."], ["On the evening of the 4th of September, M. Morrel asked his daughter for the key of his study.", "9월 4일 저녁, 모렐 씨는 딸에게 서재 열쇠를 달라고 했다. 쥘리는 이 부탁에 몸을 떨었다."], ["Julie trembled at this request, which seemed to her of bad omen.", "불길한 징조처럼 느껴졌던 것이다."], ["Why did her father ask for this key which she always kept, and which was only taken from her in childhood as a punishment?", "늘 자신이 간직하고 있던, 어린 시절 벌을 받을 때나 빼앗기던 이 열쇠를 어째서 아버지가 달라고 하는 걸까?"], ["The young girl looked at Morrel.", "쥘리는 모렐을 바라보았다."]], [["“What have I done wrong, father,”", "\"제가 무슨 잘못을 했기에,\""], ["she said, “that you should take this key from me?”", "그녀가 말했다. \"아버지가 이 열쇠를 가져가시려는 거예요?\""]], [["“Nothing, my dear,”", "\"아무 잘못도 없단다, 얘야.\""], ["replied the unhappy man, the tears starting to his eyes at this simple question,—“nothing, only I want it.”", "이 단순한 물음에 눈에 눈물이 핑 돌며 불행한 사내가 대답했다. \"아무 잘못도 없어. 다만 그게 필요할 뿐이야.\""]], [["Julie made a pretence to feel for the key.", "쥘리는 열쇠를 찾는 척했다."], ["“I must have left it in my room,” she said.", "\"방에 두고 온 모양이에요.\" 그녀가 말했다."]], [["And she went out, but instead of going to her apartment she hastened to consult Emmanuel.", "그러고는 밖으로 나갔지만, 자기 방으로 가는 대신 에마뉘엘에게 의논하러 서둘러 갔다."]], [["“Do not give this key to your father,”", "\"그 열쇠를 아버님께 드리지 마세요.\""], ["said he, “and tomorrow morning, if possible, do not quit him for a moment.”", "그가 말했다. \"그리고 내일 아침에는, 되도록 한순간도 아버님 곁을 떠나지 마시고요.\""]], [["She questioned Emmanuel, but he knew nothing, or would not say what he knew.", "그녀는 에마뉘엘에게 캐물었지만, 그는 아무것도 몰랐거나, 아니면 아는 것을 말하려 하지 않았다."]], [["During the night, between the 4th and 5th of September, Madame Morrel remained listening for every sound, and, until three o’clock in the morning, she heard her husband pacing the room in great agitation.", "9월 4일에서 5일로 넘어가는 밤, 모렐 부인은 작은 소리 하나에도 귀를 곤두세운 채 깨어 있었고, 새벽 세 시까지 남편이 몹시 흥분한 채 방 안을 서성이는 소리를 들었다."], ["It was three o’clock when he threw himself on the bed.", "그가 침대에 몸을 던진 것은 세 시였다."], ["The mother and daughter passed the night together.", "어머니와 딸은 함께 밤을 지새웠다."], ["They had expected Maximilian since the previous evening.", "그들은 전날 저녁부터 막시밀리앙을 기다리고 있었다."], ["At eight o’clock in the morning Morrel entered their chamber.", "아침 여덟 시에 모렐이 두 사람의 방으로 들어왔다."], ["He was calm; but the agitation of the night was legible in his pale and careworn visage.", "그는 차분했다. 그러나 간밤의 동요가 그 창백하고 수척한 얼굴에 또렷이 드러나 있었다."], ["They did not dare to ask him how he had slept. Morrel was kinder to his wife, more affectionate to his daughter, than he had ever been.", "두 사람은 차마 그에게 잠은 잘 잤느냐고 묻지 못했다. 모렐은 그 어느 때보다 아내에게 다정했고, 딸에게 살가웠다."], ["He could not cease gazing at and kissing the sweet girl.", "그는 사랑스러운 딸을 바라보고 입맞추기를 멈추지 못했다."], ["Julie, mindful of Emmanuel’s request, was following her father when he quitted the room, but he said to her quickly:", "쥘리는 에마뉘엘의 부탁을 떠올리며, 아버지가 방을 나설 때 뒤따르려 했다. 그러나 그가 황급히 말했다."]], [["“Remain with your mother, dearest.”", "\"어머니 곁에 있거라, 얘야.\""], ["Julie wished to accompany him. “I wish you to do so,”", "쥘리는 따라가려 했다. \"그렇게 해 주었으면 좋겠구나.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["This was the first time Morrel had ever so spoken, but he said it in a tone of paternal kindness, and Julie did not dare to disobey.", "모렐이 이런 식으로 말한 것은 처음이었지만, 그 어조에는 아버지다운 다정함이 배어 있었기에 쥘리는 차마 거역하지 못했다."], ["She remained at the same spot standing mute and motionless.", "그녀는 그 자리에 선 채 말없이 꼼짝 않고 서 있었다."], ["An instant afterwards the door opened, she felt two arms encircle her, and a mouth pressed her forehead.", "잠시 후 문이 열리더니, 두 팔이 자신을 감싸안고 어떤 입술이 이마에 닿는 것이 느껴졌다."], ["She looked up and uttered an exclamation of joy. 20053m", "그녀는 고개를 들고 기쁨의 탄성을 질렀다."]], [["“Maximilian, my dearest brother!” she cried.", "\"막시밀리앙, 사랑하는 오빠!\" 그녀가 외쳤다."]], [["At these words Madame Morrel rose, and threw herself into her son’s arms.", "이 말에 모렐 부인이 일어나 아들의 품으로 뛰어들었다."]], [["“Mother,”", "\"어머니,\""], ["said the young man, looking alternately at Madame Morrel and her daughter, “what has occurred—what has happened?", "청년이 모렐 부인과 누이를 번갈아 보며 말했다. \"무슨 일이에요? 무슨 일이 일어난 거예요?"], ["Your letter has frightened me, and I have come hither with all speed.”", "누이 편지에 깜짝 놀라서 한달음에 달려왔어요.\""]], [["“Julie,”", "\"쥘리,\""], ["said Madame Morrel, making a sign to the young man, “go and tell your father that Maximilian has just arrived.”", "모렐 부인이 아들에게 눈짓을 하며 말했다. \"가서 막시밀리앙이 막 도착했다고 아버지께 전하렴.\""]], [["The young lady rushed out of the apartment, but on the first step of the staircase she found a man holding a letter in his hand.", "쥘리는 방에서 뛰쳐나갔지만, 계단 첫 단에서 편지 한 통을 손에 든 한 남자와 마주쳤다."]], [["“Are you not Mademoiselle Julie Morrel?”", "\"쥘리 모렐 아가씨 아니신가요?\""], ["inquired the man, with a strong Italian accent.", "남자가 강한 이탈리아 억양으로 물었다."]], [["“Yes, sir,”", "\"네, 그런데요.\""], ["replied Julie with hesitation; “what is your pleasure?", "쥘리가 머뭇거리며 대답했다. \"무슨 일이시죠?"], ["I do not know you.”", "저는 당신을 모르는데요.\""]], [["“Read this letter,”", "\"이 편지를 읽어 보십시오.\""], ["he said, handing it to her.", "그가 편지를 건네며 말했다."], ["Julie hesitated.", "쥘리는 머뭇거렸다."], ["“It concerns the best interests of your father,”", "\"아가씨 아버님의 가장 중대한 이익에 관한 일입니다.\""], ["said the messenger.", "심부름꾼이 말했다."]], [["The young girl hastily took the letter from him.", "쥘리는 황급히 그에게서 편지를 받아 들었다."], ["She opened it quickly and read:", "얼른 봉투를 뜯어 읽어 내려갔다."]], [["“Go this moment to the Allées de Meilhan, enter the house No. 15, ask the porter for the key of the room on the fifth floor, enter the apartment, take from the corner of the mantelpiece a purse netted in red silk, and give it to your father.", "\"지금 당장 메이앙 가로수길로 가서 15번지 집으로 들어가, 문지기에게 육층 방 열쇠를 청하시오. 그 방에 들어가 벽난로 선반 모퉁이에서 붉은 비단으로 짠 지갑 하나를 집어 아버님께 드리시오."], ["It is important that he should receive it before eleven o’clock.", "아버님이 그것을 열한 시 전에 받는 것이 중요하오."], ["You promised to obey me implicitly. Remember your oath.", "당신은 내게 무조건 따르겠다고 약속했소. 그 맹세를 잊지 마시오."]], [["“Sinbad the Sailor.”", "뱃사람 신드바드.\""]], [["The young girl uttered a joyful cry, raised her eyes, looked round to question the messenger, but he had disappeared.", "쥘리는 기쁨의 탄성을 지르고 눈을 들어 심부름꾼에게 물으려고 주위를 둘러보았다. 그러나 그는 사라지고 없었다."], ["She cast her eyes again over the note to peruse it a second time, and saw there was a postscript. She read:", "그녀는 다시 한번 읽어 보려고 쪽지로 눈을 돌렸다가, 추신이 있는 것을 보았다. 그녀는 읽었다."]], [["“It is important that you should fulfil this mission in person and alone.", "\"이 일은 반드시 당신이 직접, 혼자 해내야 하오."], ["If you go accompanied by any other person, or should anyone else go in your place, the porter will reply that he does not know anything about it.”", "다른 사람과 함께 가거나, 당신 대신 다른 누가 간다면, 문지기는 그런 일은 전혀 모른다고 답할 것이오.\""]], [["This postscript decreased greatly the young girl’s happiness.", "이 추신은 쥘리의 기쁨을 크게 줄여 놓았다."], ["Was there nothing to fear?", "정말 두려워할 것이 없을까?"], ["was there not some snare laid for her?", "자신에게 무슨 덫을 놓은 것은 아닐까?"], ["Her innocence had kept her in ignorance of the dangers that might assail a young girl of her age.", "그녀는 순진했던 탓에, 자기 또래 처녀에게 닥칠 수 있는 위험을 알지 못했다."], ["But there is no need to know danger in order to fear it; indeed, it may be observed, that it is usually unknown perils that inspire the greatest terror.", "그러나 위험을 두려워하는 데 굳이 그것을 알 필요는 없는 법이다. 오히려 가장 큰 공포를 불러일으키는 것은 대개 알 수 없는 위험이라는 점을 짚어 둘 만하다."]], [["Julie hesitated, and resolved to take counsel.", "쥘리는 머뭇거리다가 누군가에게 의논하기로 마음먹었다."], ["Yet, through a singular impulse, it was neither to her mother nor her brother that she applied, but to Emmanuel.", "그런데 묘한 충동이 일어, 그녀가 의논하러 간 것은 어머니도 오빠도 아닌 에마뉘엘이었다."], ["She hastened down and told him what had occurred on the day when the agent of Thomson & French had come to her father’s, related the scene on the staircase, repeated the promise she had made, and showed him the letter.", "그녀는 서둘러 내려가, 톰슨앤드프렌치 상회의 대리인이 아버지를 찾아왔던 날 있었던 일을 그에게 들려주고, 계단에서 있었던 일을 이야기하고, 자기가 한 약속을 되뇌고는, 편지를 보여 주었다."]], [["“You must go, then, mademoiselle,”", "\"그렇다면 가셔야 합니다, 아가씨.\""], ["said Emmanuel.", "에마뉘엘이 말했다."]], [["“Go there?”", "\"거길 가라고요?\""], ["murmured Julie.", "쥘리가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Yes; I will accompany you.”", "\"네. 제가 함께 가 드리지요.\""]], [["“But did you not read that I must be alone?”", "\"하지만 저 혼자 가야 한다고 쓰여 있는 걸 못 읽으셨어요?\""], ["said Julie.", "쥘리가 말했다."]], [["“And you shall be alone,”", "\"혼자 가시게 될 겁니다.\""], ["replied the young man.", "청년이 대답했다."], ["“I will await you at the corner of the Rue du Musée, and if you are so long absent as to make me uneasy, I will hasten to rejoin you, and woe to him of whom you shall have cause to complain to me!”", "\"저는 뮈제 거리 모퉁이에서 기다리겠습니다. 그리고 아가씨가 너무 오래 돌아오지 않아 제가 불안해지면, 한달음에 달려가 다시 아가씨 곁에 서겠습니다. 아가씨가 제게 하소연할 일을 만든 자가 있다면, 그자에게 화가 미칠 겁니다!\""]], [["“Then, Emmanuel?”", "\"그럼, 에마뉘엘,\""], ["said the young girl with hesitation, “it is your opinion that I should obey this invitation?”", "쥘리가 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"이 청에 따르는 게 좋겠다는 말씀이시죠?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["Did not the messenger say your father’s safety depended upon it?”", "아가씨 아버님의 안위가 거기 달려 있다고 심부름꾼이 말하지 않았습니까?\""]], [["“But what danger threatens him, then, Emmanuel?”", "\"하지만 도대체 무슨 위험이 아버지를 위협하고 있는 거죠, 에마뉘엘?\""], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["Emmanuel hesitated a moment, but his desire to make Julie decide immediately made him reply.", "에마뉘엘은 잠시 망설였지만, 쥘리를 당장 결심하게 만들고 싶은 마음에 이렇게 대답했다."]], [["“Listen,”", "\"들어 보세요.\""], ["he said; “today is the 5th of September, is it not?”", "그가 말했다. \"오늘이 9월 5일 맞지요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Today, then, at eleven o’clock, your father has nearly three hundred thousand francs to pay?”", "\"그럼 오늘 열한 시에, 아버님이 갚으셔야 할 돈이 거의 30만 프랑입니다.\""]], [["“Yes, we know that.”", "\"네, 그건 우리도 알아요.\""]], [["“Well, then,”", "\"그런데,\""], ["continued Emmanuel, “we have not fifteen thousand francs in the house.”", "에마뉘엘이 말을 이었다. \"이 집에는 1만 5천 프랑도 없습니다.\""]], [["“What will happen then?”", "\"그럼 어떻게 되는 거죠?\""]], [["“Why, if today before eleven o’clock your father has not found someone who will come to his aid, he will be compelled at twelve o’clock to declare himself a bankrupt.”", "\"오늘 열한 시 전에 아버님을 도와줄 사람을 찾지 못하면, 정오에는 파산을 선언할 수밖에 없게 됩니다.\""]], [["“Oh, come, then, come!” cried she, hastening away with the young man.", "\"아, 그럼 어서, 어서 가요!\" 그녀가 외치며 청년과 함께 서둘러 떠났다."]], [["During this time, Madame Morrel had told her son everything.", "그동안 모렐 부인은 아들에게 모든 것을 이야기했다."], ["The young man knew quite well that, after the succession of misfortunes which had befallen his father, great changes had taken place in the style of living and housekeeping; but he did not know that matters had reached such a point.", "청년은 아버지에게 연이어 닥친 불운 끝에 생활과 살림의 방식이 크게 달라졌다는 것을 잘 알고 있었지만, 사태가 이 지경에 이른 줄은 몰랐다. 그는 벼락을 맞은 듯했다."], ["He was thunderstruck. Then, rushing hastily out of the apartment, he ran upstairs, expecting to find his father in his study, but he rapped there in vain.", "그러더니 황급히 방을 뛰쳐나가 위층으로 달려갔다. 아버지가 서재에 있으리라 여기고 문을 두드렸지만 헛일이었다."]], [["While he was yet at the door of the study he heard the bedroom door open, turned, and saw his father.", "서재 문 앞에 서 있는데 침실 문이 열리는 소리가 들렸다. 그가 돌아보니 아버지였다."], ["Instead of going direct to his study, M. Morrel had returned to his bedchamber, which he was only this moment quitting.", "모렐 씨는 서재로 곧장 가는 대신 침실로 돌아갔다가, 이제 막 거기서 나오는 참이었다."], ["Morrel uttered a cry of surprise at the sight of his son, of whose arrival he was ignorant.", "모렐은 도착한 줄도 몰랐던 아들을 보고 놀라움의 외마디를 질렀다."], ["He remained motionless on the spot, pressing with his left hand something he had concealed under his coat.", "그는 그 자리에 꼼짝 않고 선 채, 왼손으로 윗옷 안에 감춘 무언가를 누르고 있었다."], ["Maximilian sprang down the staircase, and threw his arms round his father’s neck; but suddenly he recoiled, and placed his right hand on Morrel’s breast.", "막시밀리앙은 계단을 뛰어 내려와 아버지의 목을 두 팔로 끌어안았다. 그러나 그는 별안간 움찔 물러서며 오른손을 모렐의 가슴에 갖다 댔다."]], [["“Father,”", "\"아버지,\""], ["he exclaimed, turning pale as death, “what are you going to do with that brace of pistols under your coat?”", "그가 죽은 사람처럼 새파랗게 질려 외쳤다. \"윗옷 안의 그 권총 두 자루로 뭘 하시려는 거예요?\""]], [["“Oh, this is what I feared!”", "\"오, 내가 두려워하던 게 바로 이거였다!\""], ["said Morrel.", "모렐이 말했다."]], [["“Father, father, in Heaven’s name,”", "\"아버지, 아버지, 제발,\""], ["exclaimed the young man, “what are these weapons for?”", "청년이 외쳤다. \"이 총들은 뭐에 쓰시려는 거예요?\""]], [["“Maximilian,”", "\"막시밀리앙,\""], ["replied Morrel, looking fixedly at his son, “you are a man, and a man of honor.", "모렐이 아들을 똑바로 바라보며 대답했다. \"너는 사내이고, 명예를 아는 사내다."], ["Come, and I will explain to you.”", "따라오너라, 설명해 주마.\""]], [["And with a firm step Morrel went up to his study, while Maximilian followed him, trembling as he went.", "그러고는 모렐이 단호한 걸음으로 서재로 올라갔고, 막시밀리앙은 떨면서 그 뒤를 따랐다."], ["Morrel opened the door, and closed it behind his son; then, crossing the anteroom, went to his desk on which he placed the pistols, and pointed with his finger to an open ledger.", "모렐은 문을 열고 아들을 들인 뒤 문을 닫았다. 그러고는 대기실을 가로질러 책상으로 가서 권총을 내려놓고, 손가락으로 펼쳐진 장부 하나를 가리켰다."], ["In this ledger was made out an exact balance-sheet of his affairs.", "이 장부에는 그의 사업 형편을 정확히 적은 대차대조표가 작성되어 있었다."], ["Morrel had to pay, within half an hour, 287,500 francs.", "모렐은 삼십 분 안에 28만 7천 5백 프랑을 갚아야 했다."], ["All he possessed was 15,257 francs.", "그가 가진 전부는 1만 5천 2백 57프랑이었다."]], [["“Read!” said Morrel.", "\"읽어 보아라!\" 모렐이 말했다."]], [["The young man was overwhelmed as he read.", "청년은 그것을 읽으며 망연자실했다."], ["Morrel said not a word.", "모렐은 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["What could he say?", "무슨 말을 할 수 있었겠는가?"], ["What need he add to such a desperate proof in figures?", "이토록 절망적인 숫자의 증거에 무슨 말을 보탤 수 있었겠는가?"]], [["“And have you done all that is possible, father, to meet this disastrous result?”", "\"이 참혹한 결과를 막으려고 할 수 있는 일은 다 해 보셨나요, 아버지?\""], ["asked the young man, after a moment’s pause.", "잠시 침묵하던 청년이 물었다."]], [["“I have,” replied Morrel.", "\"다 해 보았다.\" 모렐이 대답했다."]], [["“You have no money coming in on which you can rely?”", "\"기댈 만한 수입이 들어올 데도 없고요?\""]], [["“None.”", "\"없다.\""]], [["“You have exhausted every resource?”", "\"방편이란 방편은 다 써 보셨고요?\""]], [["“All.”", "\"전부.\""]], [["“And in half an hour,”", "\"그럼 삼십 분 후에는,\""], ["said Maximilian in a gloomy voice, “our name is dishonored!”", "막시밀리앙이 침울한 목소리로 말했다. \"우리 가문의 이름이 더럽혀지는군요!\""]], [["“Blood washes out dishonor,”", "\"피는 불명예를 씻어 낸다.\""], ["said Morrel.", "모렐이 말했다."]], [["“You are right, father; I understand you.”", "\"옳으신 말씀이에요, 아버지. 무슨 뜻인지 알겠어요.\""], ["Then extending his hand towards one of the pistols, he said, “There is one for you and one for me—thanks!”", "그러고는 권총 하나를 향해 손을 뻗으며 말했다. \"하나는 아버지 것, 하나는 제 것이네요. 고맙습니다!\""]], [["Morrel caught his hand.", "모렐이 그의 손을 붙잡았다."], ["“Your mother—your sister!", "\"네 어머니는! 네 누이는!"], ["Who will support them?”", "누가 그들을 돌보겠느냐?\""]], [["A shudder ran through the young man’s frame.", "청년의 온몸에 전율이 일었다. \"아버지,\""], ["“Father,” he said, “do you reflect that you are bidding me to live?”", "그가 말했다. \"지금 제게 살아 있으라고 명하시는 거예요?\""]], [["“Yes, I do so bid you,”", "\"그래, 그렇게 명한다.\""], ["answered Morrel, “it is your duty.", "모렐이 대답했다. \"그게 네 의무다."], ["You have a calm, strong mind, Maximilian.", "너는 차분하고 굳센 정신을 지녔다, 막시밀리앙."], ["Maximilian, you are no ordinary man.", "막시밀리앙, 너는 예사 사내가 아니다."], ["I make no requests or commands; I only ask you to examine my position as if it were your own, and then judge for yourself.”", "나는 부탁도 명령도 하지 않으마. 다만 내 처지를 네 처지인 것처럼 헤아려 보고, 스스로 판단해 보라는 것뿐이다.\""]], [["The young man reflected for a moment, then an expression of sublime resignation appeared in his eyes, and with a slow and sad gesture he took off his two epaulets, the insignia of his rank.", "청년은 잠시 곰곰이 생각했다. 그러자 그의 눈에 숭고한 체념의 빛이 떠올랐고, 그는 느리고 서글픈 몸짓으로 자기 계급의 표장인 견장 두 개를 떼어 냈다."]], [["“Be it so, then, my father,”", "\"그럼 그렇게 하겠습니다, 아버지.\""], ["he said, extending his hand to Morrel, “die in peace, my father; I will live.”", "그가 모렐에게 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"편히 눈감으세요, 아버지. 저는 살겠습니다.\""]], [["Morrel was about to cast himself on his knees before his son, but Maximilian caught him in his arms, and those two noble hearts were pressed against each other for a moment.", "모렐이 아들 앞에 무릎을 꿇으려 했지만, 막시밀리앙이 그를 두 팔로 붙들었고, 그 고결한 두 마음은 한순간 서로를 부둥켜안았다."]], [["“You know it is not my fault,”", "\"이게 내 잘못이 아니라는 걸 너도 알지.\""], ["said Morrel. 20057m", "모렐이 말했다."]], [["Maximilian smiled.", "막시밀리앙이 미소 지었다."], ["“I know, father, you are the most honorable man I have ever known.”", "\"알아요, 아버지. 제가 아는 가장 명예로운 분이세요.\""]], [["“Good, my son.", "\"착하구나, 아들아."], ["And now there is no more to be said; go and rejoin your mother and sister.”", "이제 더 할 말은 없다. 어머니와 누이에게 가 보아라.\""]], [["“My father,”", "\"아버지,\""], ["said the young man, bending his knee, “bless me!”", "청년이 한쪽 무릎을 꿇으며 말했다."], ["Morrel took the head of his son between his two hands, drew him forward, and kissing his forehead several times said:", "\"축복해 주세요!\" 모렐은 두 손으로 아들의 머리를 감싸 끌어당기더니, 이마에 여러 번 입맞추며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, yes, yes, I bless you in my own name, and in the name of three generations of irreproachable men, who say through me, ‘The edifice which misfortune has destroyed, Providence may build up again.’ On seeing me die such a death, the most inexorable will have pity on you.", "\"오, 그래, 그래, 내 이름으로, 그리고 나를 통해 이렇게 말하는 흠 없는 삼대 조상의 이름으로 너를 축복한다. '불운이 무너뜨린 집을, 섭리는 다시 세울 수 있으리라.' 내가 이런 죽음을 맞는 것을 보면, 아무리 가차 없는 자라도 너를 가엾이 여길 것이다."], ["To you, perhaps, they will accord the time they have refused to me.", "그들은 어쩌면 내게 거절했던 시간을 네게는 허락해 줄지도 모른다."], ["Then do your best to keep our name free from dishonor.", "그러니 우리 이름을 불명예에서 지켜 내도록 최선을 다하거라."], ["Go to work, labor, young man, struggle ardently and courageously; live, yourself, your mother and sister, with the most rigid economy, so that from day to day the property of those whom I leave in your hands may augment and fructify.", "일하고, 애쓰고, 뜨겁고 용감하게 분투하거라. 너 자신도, 네 어머니도, 네 누이도 더없이 엄격하게 절약하며 살아서, 내가 네 손에 맡기고 가는 이들의 재산이 날마다 불어나고 열매 맺게 하거라. 완전히 회복되는 그날이 얼마나 영광스럽고, 얼마나 위대하고, 얼마나 엄숙할지 헤아려 보아라."], ["Reflect how glorious a day it will be, how grand, how solemn, that day of complete restoration, on which you will say in this very office, ‘My father died because he could not do what I have this day done; but he died calmly and peaceably, because in dying he knew what I should do.’”", "바로 이 사무실에서 네가 이렇게 말할 그날 말이다. '내 아버지는 오늘 내가 해낸 일을 하지 못해 돌아가셨다. 그러나 돌아가시면서 내가 무엇을 하리라는 것을 아셨기에, 차분하고 평온하게 눈감으셨다.'\""]], [["“My father, my father!”", "\"아버지, 아버지!\""], ["cried the young man, “why should you not live?”", "청년이 외쳤다. \"어째서 살아 계시면 안 되는 거예요?\""]], [["“If I live, all would be changed; if I live, interest would be converted into doubt, pity into hostility; if I live I am only a man who has broken his word, failed in his engagements—in fact, only a bankrupt.", "\"내가 살아 있으면 모든 게 달라진다. 내가 살아 있으면 호의는 의심으로, 동정은 적의로 변할 거다. 내가 살아 있으면 나는 그저 약속을 어기고 의무를 저버린 사내, 한낱 파산자일 뿐이다."], ["If, on the contrary, I die, remember, Maximilian, my corpse is that of an honest but unfortunate man.", "그러나 반대로 내가 죽으면, 기억해 두어라, 막시밀리앙, 내 주검은 정직하지만 불운했던 사내의 주검이 되는 거다."], ["Living, my best friends would avoid my house; dead, all Marseilles will follow me in tears to my last home.", "살아 있으면 내 가장 가까운 친구들도 내 집을 피할 테지만, 죽으면 온 마르세유가 눈물을 흘리며 나를 마지막 안식처까지 배웅할 거다."], ["Living, you would feel shame at my name; dead, you may raise your head and say, ‘I am the son of him you killed, because, for the first time, he has been compelled to break his word.’”", "살아 있으면 너는 내 이름을 부끄러워하겠지만, 죽으면 너는 고개를 들고 이렇게 말할 수 있을 거다. '나는 당신들이 죽인 사람의 아들이오. 그는 난생처음으로 약속을 어길 수밖에 없었기에 그리된 것이오.'\""]], [["The young man uttered a groan, but appeared resigned.", "청년은 신음을 토했지만, 체념한 듯했다."]], [["“And now,”", "\"이제,\""], ["said Morrel, “leave me alone, and endeavor to keep your mother and sister away.”", "모렐이 말했다. \"나를 혼자 있게 해 다오. 그리고 네 어머니와 누이를 떼어 놓도록 애써 보아라.\""]], [["“Will you not see my sister once more?”", "\"누이를 한 번 더 보지 않으시겠어요?\""], ["asked Maximilian.", "막시밀리앙이 물었다."], ["A last but final hope was concealed by the young man in the effect of this interview, and therefore he had suggested it.", "청년은 이 만남이 가져올 효과에 마지막 희망을, 그러나 최후의 희망을 걸고 그렇게 제안한 것이었다."], ["Morrel shook his head.", "모렐이 고개를 저었다."], ["“I saw her this morning, and bade her adieu.”", "\"오늘 아침에 그 애를 보았고, 작별 인사도 했다.\""]], [["“Have you no particular commands to leave with me, my father?”", "\"제게 따로 남기실 분부는 없으세요, 아버지?\""], ["inquired Maximilian in a faltering voice.", "막시밀리앙이 떨리는 목소리로 물었다."]], [["“Yes; my son, and a sacred command.”", "\"있다, 아들아. 그것도 신성한 분부다.\""]], [["“Say it, my father.”", "\"말씀해 주세요, 아버지.\""]], [["“The house of Thomson & French is the only one who, from humanity, or, it may be, selfishness—it is not for me to read men’s hearts—has had any pity for me.", "\"톰슨앤드프렌치 상회는, 인정에서였든 아니면 타산에서였든 — 사람의 마음속을 읽는 것은 내가 할 일이 아니다만 — 유일하게 나를 가엾이 여겨 준 곳이다."], ["Its agent, who will in ten minutes present himself to receive the amount of a bill of 287,500 francs, I will not say granted, but offered me three months.", "그 대리인이 십 분 후에 28만 7천 5백 프랑짜리 어음의 대금을 받으러 올 텐데, 그가 내게 석 달을 내주었다고는 하지 않으마, 다만 먼저 제안했다고 해 두자."], ["Let this house be the first repaid, my son, and respect this man.”", "그 상회에 가장 먼저 빚을 갚아라, 아들아. 그리고 그 사람을 공경하거라.\""]], [["“Father, I will,”", "\"아버지, 그렇게 하겠습니다.\""], ["said Maximilian.", "막시밀리앙이 말했다."]], [["“And now, once more, adieu,”", "\"이제 다시 한번, 잘 있거라.\""], ["said Morrel.", "모렐이 말했다."], ["“Go, leave me; I would be alone.", "\"가거라, 나를 두고 가거라."], ["You will find my will in the secretaire in my bedroom.”", "혼자 있고 싶구나. 내 유언장은 침실 책상 서랍에 있을 거다.\""]], [["The young man remained standing and motionless, having but the force of will and not the power of execution.", "청년은 의지의 힘은 있으되 그것을 실행할 힘은 없는 채로, 선 자리에 꼼짝 않고 서 있었다."]], [["“Hear me, Maximilian,”", "\"내 말을 들어라, 막시밀리앙.\" 아버지가 말했다."], ["said his father.", "\"내가 너처럼 군인이고, 어떤 보루를 점령하라는 명령을 받았다고 치자."], ["“Suppose I were a soldier like you, and ordered to carry a certain redoubt, and you knew I must be killed in the assault, would you not say to me, as you said just now, ‘Go, father; for you are dishonored by delay, and death is preferable to shame!’”", "그리고 네가 내가 그 공격에서 죽으리라는 걸 안다면, 너는 방금 했던 대로 내게 이렇게 말하지 않겠느냐. '가세요, 아버지. 지체하면 아버지는 불명예를 입게 되고, 죽음이 치욕보다 나으니까요!'\""]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"네, 네.\""], ["said the young man, “yes;”", "청년이 말했다. \"네.\""], ["and once again embracing his father with convulsive pressure, he said, “Be it so, my father.”", "그러고는 다시 한번 발작하듯 아버지를 으스러져라 끌어안으며 말했다. \"그럼 그렇게 하세요, 아버지.\""]], [["And he rushed out of the study.", "그러고는 서재를 뛰쳐나갔다."], ["When his son had left him, Morrel remained an instant standing with his eyes fixed on the door; then putting forth his arm, he pulled the bell.", "아들이 떠나자 모렐은 한순간 눈을 문에 고정한 채 서 있었다. 그러고는 팔을 뻗어 종을 당겼다."], ["After a moment’s interval, Cocles appeared.", "잠시 후 코클레스가 나타났다."]], [["It was no longer the same man—the fearful revelations of the three last days had crushed him.", "이제 그는 더 이상 예전의 그 사람이 아니었다. 지난 사흘간의 끔찍한 진상이 그를 짓이겨 놓았다."], ["This thought—the house of Morrel is about to stop payment—bent him to the earth more than twenty years would otherwise have done.", "모렐 상회가 곧 지불을 멈추리라는 이 생각이, 이십 년 세월이 했을 것보다 더 그를 땅바닥으로 짓눌렀다."]], [["“My worthy Cocles,”", "\"착한 코클레스,\""], ["said Morrel in a tone impossible to describe, “do you remain in the antechamber.", "모렐이 형언할 수 없는 어조로 말했다. \"대기실에 있게나."], ["When the gentleman who came three months ago—the agent of Thomson & French—arrives, announce his arrival to me.”", "석 달 전에 왔던 그 양반, 톰슨앤드프렌치 상회 대리인이 오거든, 도착했다고 내게 알려 주게.\""]], [["Cocles made no reply; he made a sign with his head, went into the anteroom, and seated himself.", "코클레스는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다. 그는 고개로만 알았다는 시늉을 하고는 대기실로 들어가 앉았다."], ["Morrel fell back in his chair, his eyes fixed on the clock; there were seven minutes left, that was all.", "모렐은 의자에 등을 기댄 채 눈을 시계에 고정했다. 남은 시간은 칠 분, 그게 전부였다."], ["The hand moved on with incredible rapidity, he seemed to see its motion.", "시곗바늘은 믿기 힘든 속도로 움직였고, 그는 그 움직임이 눈에 보이는 것만 같았다."]], [["What passed in the mind of this man at the supreme moment of his agony cannot be told in words.", "극도의 고뇌가 정점에 이른 그 순간 이 사내의 마음속에 무엇이 스쳐 갔는지는 말로 다 할 수 없다."], ["He was still comparatively young, he was surrounded by the loving care of a devoted family, but he had convinced himself by a course of reasoning, illogical perhaps, yet certainly plausible, that he must separate himself from all he held dear in the world, even life itself.", "그는 아직 비교적 젊었고, 헌신적인 가족의 사랑 어린 보살핌에 둘러싸여 있었다. 그러나 그는 어쩌면 비논리적일지언정 분명히 그럴듯한 일련의 추론을 통해, 이 세상에서 자신이 아끼는 모든 것과, 심지어 목숨까지도 떼어 내야 한다고 스스로를 납득시켜 놓은 터였다."], ["To form the slightest idea of his feelings, one must have seen his face with its expression of enforced resignation and its tear-moistened eyes raised to heaven.", "그의 심정을 조금이나마 헤아리려면, 억지로 짜낸 체념의 표정과 눈물에 젖어 하늘을 향해 들린 그 눈을 띤 얼굴을 보았어야만 한다."], ["The minute hand moved on.", "분침이 나아갔다."], ["The pistols were loaded; he stretched forth his hand, took one up, and murmured his daughter’s name.", "권총에는 장전이 되어 있었다. 그는 손을 뻗어 하나를 집어 들고 딸의 이름을 중얼거렸다."], ["Then he laid it down, seized his pen, and wrote a few words.", "그러고는 그것을 내려놓고 펜을 쥐어 몇 마디를 적었다."], ["It seemed to him as if he had not taken a sufficient farewell of his beloved daughter.", "사랑하는 딸에게 충분히 작별을 고하지 못한 것 같았던 것이다."], ["Then he turned again to the clock, counting time now not by minutes, but by seconds.", "그러고는 다시 시계로 눈을 돌려, 이제는 분이 아니라 초로 시간을 헤아렸다."]], [["He took up the deadly weapon again, his lips parted and his eyes fixed on the clock, and then shuddered at the click of the trigger as he cocked the pistol.", "그는 다시 그 죽음의 무기를 집어 들었다. 입술은 벌어졌고 눈은 시계에 고정되어 있었다. 그러고는 공이치기를 당기자 방아쇠가 철컥하는 소리에 몸서리쳤다."], ["At this moment of mortal anguish the cold sweat came forth upon his brow, a pang stronger than death clutched at his heart-strings.", "죽음 같은 이 고뇌의 순간, 식은땀이 이마에 배어 나왔고, 죽음보다 더 모진 통증이 그의 심장의 끈을 움켜쥐었다."], ["He heard the door of the staircase creak on its hinges—the clock gave its warning to strike eleven—the door of his study opened.", "그는 계단 쪽 문이 경첩에서 삐걱이는 소리를 들었다. 시계가 열한 시를 알리는 예고음을 냈다. 서재 문이 열렸다."], ["Morrel did not turn round—he expected these words of Cocles, “The agent of Thomson & French.”", "모렐은 돌아보지 않았다. 그는 코클레스의 이 말을 기다리고 있었다. \"톰슨앤드프렌치 상회 대리인입니다.\""]], [["He placed the muzzle of the pistol between his teeth.", "그는 권총 총구를 이 사이에 물었다."], ["Suddenly he heard a cry—it was his daughter’s voice.", "그때 갑자기 외침이 들렸다. 딸의 목소리였다."], ["He turned and saw Julie.", "그가 돌아보니 쥘리였다."], ["The pistol fell from his hands.", "권총이 손에서 떨어졌다."]], [["“My father!”", "\"아버지!\""], ["cried the young girl, out of breath, and half dead with joy—“saved, you are saved!”", "처녀가 숨을 헐떡이며, 기쁨에 거의 넋이 나간 채 외쳤다. \"살았어요, 아버지가 살았어요!\""], ["And she threw herself into his arms, holding in her extended hand a red, netted silk purse.", "그러고는 붉은 그물 비단 지갑 하나를 뻗은 손에 든 채 아버지의 품으로 뛰어들었다."]], [["20061m\n“Saved, my child!”", "\"살았다니, 얘야?\""], ["said Morrel; “what do you mean?”", "모렐이 말했다. \"그게 무슨 소리냐?\""]], [["“Yes, saved—saved!", "\"네, 살았어요, 살았다고요!"], ["See, see!” said the young girl.", "보세요, 보세요!\" 처녀가 말했다."]], [["Morrel took the purse, and started as he did so, for a vague remembrance reminded him that it once belonged to himself.", "모렐은 지갑을 받아 들었고, 그 순간 흠칫했다. 어렴풋한 기억이, 그것이 한때 자기 것이었음을 일깨워 주었기 때문이다."], ["At one end was the receipted bill for the 287,000 francs, and at the other was a diamond as large as a hazel-nut, with these words on a small slip of parchment: _Julie’s Dowry_.", "한쪽 끝에는 28만 7천 프랑짜리 어음의 영수증이 들어 있었고, 다른 쪽 끝에는 개암만 한 크기의 다이아몬드 하나가, 작은 양피지 조각에 이런 글귀와 함께 들어 있었다. \"쥘리의 지참금.\""]], [["Morrel passed his hand over his brow; it seemed to him a dream.", "모렐은 손으로 이마를 훔쳤다. 꿈만 같았다."], ["At this moment the clock struck eleven.", "그 순간 시계가 열한 시를 쳤다."], ["He felt as if each stroke of the hammer fell upon his heart.", "그는 망치질하는 소리 하나하나가 자기 심장 위로 떨어지는 것만 같았다."]], [["“Explain, my child,” he said, “Explain, my child,”", "\"설명해 다오, 얘야.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said, “explain—where did you find this purse?”", "\"설명해 다오, 얘야. 이 지갑을 어디서 찾았느냐?\""]], [["“In a house in the Allées de Meilhan, No. 15, on the corner of a mantelpiece in a small room on the fifth floor.”", "\"메이앙 가로수길 15번지 집, 오층 작은 방의 벽난로 선반 모퉁이에서요.\""]], [["“But,” cried Morrel, “this purse is not yours!”", "\"하지만,\" 모렐이 외쳤다."], ["Julie handed to her father the letter she had received in the morning.", "\"이 지갑은 네 것이 아니잖느냐!\" 쥘리가 아침에 받은 편지를 아버지에게 건넸다."]], [["“And did you go alone?”", "\"그럼 혼자 갔단 말이냐?\""], ["asked Morrel, after he had read it.", "편지를 읽고 난 모렐이 물었다."]], [["“Emmanuel accompanied me, father.", "\"에마뉘엘이 함께 갔어요, 아버지."], ["He was to have waited for me at the corner of the Rue du Musée, but, strange to say, he was not there when I returned.”", "뮈제 거리 모퉁이에서 저를 기다리기로 했는데, 이상하게도 제가 돌아왔을 땐 거기 없었어요.\""]], [["“Monsieur Morrel!”", "\"모렐 씨!\""], ["exclaimed a voice on the stairs; “Monsieur Morrel!”", "계단에서 어떤 목소리가 외쳤다. \"모렐 씨!\""]], [["“It is his voice!”", "\"저 사람 목소리예요!\""], ["said Julie.", "쥘리가 말했다."], ["At this moment Emmanuel entered, his countenance full of animation and joy.", "그 순간 에마뉘엘이 들어왔다. 그의 얼굴은 생기와 기쁨으로 가득했다."]], [["“The _Pharaon_!”", "\"파라옹호예요!\""], ["he cried; “the _Pharaon_!”", "그가 외쳤다. \"파라옹호!\""]], [["“What!—what!—the _Pharaon_!", "\"뭐라고! 뭐! 파라옹호라니!"], ["Are you mad, Emmanuel?", "미쳤나, 에마뉘엘?"], ["You know the vessel is lost.”", "그 배가 침몰한 걸 자네도 알잖나.\""]], [["“The _Pharaon_, sir—they signal the _Pharaon_!", "\"파라옹호예요, 선주님. 사람들이 파라옹호 신호를 보내고 있어요!"], ["The _Pharaon_ is entering the harbor!”", "파라옹호가 항구로 들어오고 있다고요!\""]], [["Morrel fell back in his chair, his strength was failing him; his understanding weakened by such events, refused to comprehend such incredible, unheard-of, fabulous facts.", "모렐은 의자에 등을 기댄 채 쓰러지듯 주저앉았다. 기력이 다해 가고 있었다. 이런 사건들로 약해진 그의 머리는, 이토록 믿기 힘들고, 들어 본 적 없고, 허황된 사실을 받아들이기를 거부했다."], ["But his son came in.", "그러나 그의 아들이 들어왔다."]], [["“Father,”", "\"아버지,\""], ["cried Maximilian, “how could you say the _Pharaon_ was lost?", "막시밀리앙이 외쳤다. \"어째서 파라옹호가 침몰했다고 하셨어요?"], ["The lookout has signalled her, and they say she is now coming into port.” 20063m", "망루에서 그 배 신호를 보냈고, 지금 항구로 들어오고 있대요.\""]], [["“My dear friends,”", "\"얘들아,\""], ["said Morrel, “if this be so, it must be a miracle of heaven!", "모렐이 말했다. \"이게 사실이라면, 이건 하늘의 기적일 게다!"], ["Impossible, impossible!”", "그럴 리가 없어, 그럴 리가!\""]], [["But what was real and not less incredible was the purse he held in his hand, the acceptance receipted—the splendid diamond.", "그러나 못지않게 믿기 힘들면서도 분명히 실재하는 것이, 그의 손에 들린 지갑이었다. 영수된 어음과, 그 눈부신 다이아몬드 말이다."]], [["“Ah, sir,”", "\"아, 선주님,\""], ["exclaimed Cocles, “what can it mean?—the _Pharaon_?”", "코클레스가 외쳤다. \"이게 무슨 영문일까요? 파라옹호라니요?\""]], [["“Come, dear ones,”", "\"가자, 얘들아.\""], ["said Morrel, rising from his seat, “let us go and see, and Heaven have pity upon us if it be false intelligence!”", "모렐이 자리에서 일어서며 말했다. \"가서 보자꾸나. 그리고 만약 잘못된 소식이라면, 하늘이 우리를 가엾이 여겨 주시기를!\""]], [["They all went out, and on the stairs met Madame Morrel, who had been afraid to go up into the study.", "그들 모두 밖으로 나갔고, 계단에서 서재로 올라오기를 두려워하던 모렐 부인과 마주쳤다."], ["In a moment they were at the Canebière.", "이내 그들은 카느비에르 거리에 다다랐다."], ["There was a crowd on the pier.", "부두에는 군중이 몰려 있었다."], ["All the crowd gave way before Morrel.", "모든 군중이 모렐 앞에서 길을 비켜 주었다."], ["“The _Pharaon_!", "\"파라옹호다!"], ["the _Pharaon_!”", "파라옹호!\""], ["said every voice.", "사람마다 외쳤다."]], [["And, wonderful to see, in front of the tower of Saint-Jean, was a ship bearing on her stern these words, printed in white letters, “The _Pharaon_, Morrel & Son, of Marseilles.”", "그리고 놀랍게도, 생장 요새 앞에 배 한 척이 있었다. 그 고물에는 흰 글씨로 이런 글귀가 적혀 있었다. \"파라옹호, 마르세유의 모렐 부자 상회.\""], ["She was the exact duplicate of the other _Pharaon_, and loaded, as that had been, with cochineal and indigo.", "그것은 또 다른 파라옹호와 똑같이 빼닮은 배였고, 예전의 그 배가 그랬듯 연지벌레와 쪽으로 짐을 가득 싣고 있었다."], ["She cast anchor, clued up sails, and on the deck was Captain Gaumard giving orders, and good old Penelon making signals to M.", "배는 닻을 내리고 돛을 거두어 올렸으며, 갑판에서는 고마르 선장이 명령을 내리고 있었고, 마음씨 좋은 늙은 페늘롱이 모렐 씨에게 신호를 보내고 있었다."], ["Morrel. To doubt any longer was impossible; there was the evidence of the senses, and ten thousand persons who came to corroborate the testimony.", "더 의심할 수는 없었다. 두 눈으로 보는 증거가 있었고, 만 명에 이르는 사람들이 그 증거를 뒷받침하러 모여들었다."]], [["As Morrel and his son embraced on the pier-head, in the presence and amid the applause of the whole city witnessing this event, a man, with his face half-covered by a black beard, and who, concealed behind the sentry-box, watched the scene with delight, uttered these words in a low tone:", "모렐과 그의 아들이 부두 끝에서, 이 광경을 지켜보는 온 도시 사람들 앞에서, 그 갈채 속에 서로를 끌어안고 있을 때, 검은 수염으로 얼굴을 반쯤 가린 한 남자가 초소 뒤에 몸을 숨긴 채 흐뭇하게 그 광경을 지켜보며 낮은 목소리로 이렇게 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Be happy, noble heart, be blessed for all the good thou hast done and wilt do hereafter, and let my gratitude remain in obscurity like your good deeds.”", "\"행복하시오, 고결한 분이여. 당신이 베푼, 그리고 앞으로 베풀 모든 선행으로 복받으시오. 그리고 내 감사하는 마음은 당신의 선행처럼 어둠 속에 묻어 두겠소.\""]], [["20064m\nAnd with a smile expressive of supreme content, he left his hiding-place, and without being observed, descended one of the flights of steps provided for debarkation, and hailing three times, shouted “Jacopo, Jacopo, Jacopo!”", "그러고는 더없는 만족을 담은 미소를 지으며 숨어 있던 곳을 떠났다. 그는 아무에게도 눈에 띄지 않고 하선용으로 마련된 계단 한쪽을 내려가, 세 번 소리쳐 불렀다. \"자코포, 자코포, 자코포!\""]], [["Then a launch came to shore, took him on board, and conveyed him to a yacht splendidly fitted up, on whose deck he sprung with the activity of a sailor; thence he once again looked towards Morrel, who, weeping with joy, was shaking hands most cordially with all the crowd around him, and thanking with a look the unknown benefactor whom he seemed to be seeking in the skies.", "그러자 거룻배 한 척이 물가로 다가와 그를 태우고는, 호화롭게 꾸민 요트로 데려갔다. 그는 뱃사람다운 날렵함으로 그 갑판에 뛰어올랐다. 그러고는 다시 한번 모렐 쪽을 바라보았다. 모렐은 기쁨에 눈물을 흘리며 주위의 모든 사람과 더없이 다정하게 악수를 나누고 있었고, 눈길로는 하늘에서 찾는 듯한 그 미지의 은인에게 감사를 보내고 있었다."]], [["“And now,”", "\"이제,\""], ["said the unknown, “farewell kindness, humanity, and gratitude!", "그 미지의 사내가 말했다. \"친절이여, 인정이여, 감사하는 마음이여, 잘 있거라!"], ["Farewell to all the feelings that expand the heart!", "마음을 부풀게 하는 그 모든 감정이여, 잘 있거라!"], ["I have been Heaven’s substitute to recompense the good—now the god of vengeance yields to me his power to punish the wicked!”", "나는 선한 자에게 보답하는 하늘의 대리인이었다. 이제는 복수의 신이 악한 자를 벌하는 그 권능을 내게 넘겨주는 것이다!\""]], [["At these words he gave a signal, and, as if only awaiting this signal, the yacht instantly put out to sea.", "이 말과 함께 그가 신호를 보내자, 마치 이 신호만을 기다리고 있었던 듯 요트가 곧장 바다로 나아갔다."]], [["# Chapter 31. Italy: Sinbad the Sailor", "# Chapter 31. Italy: Sinbad the Sailor"]], [["Towards the beginning of the year 1838, two young men belonging to the first society of Paris, the Viscount Albert de Morcerf and the Baron Franz d’Épinay, were at Florence.", "1838년 초, 파리 상류 사회에 속한 두 젊은이가 피렌체에 있었다. 모르세르 자작 알베르와 남작 프란츠 데피네였다."], ["They had agreed to see the Carnival at Rome that year, and that Franz, who for the last three or four years had inhabited Italy, should act as _cicerone_ to Albert.", "두 사람은 그해 로마에서 카니발을 함께 보기로 약속했고, 지난 서너 해 동안 이탈리아에서 지내 온 프란츠가 알베르의 안내자 노릇을 맡기로 했다."]], [["As it is no inconsiderable affair to spend the Carnival at Rome, especially when you have no great desire to sleep on the Piazza del Popolo, or the Campo Vaccino, they wrote to Signor Pastrini, the proprietor of the Hôtel de Londres, Piazza di Spagna, to reserve comfortable apartments for them.", "로마에서 카니발을 보낸다는 것은 결코 만만한 일이 아니었다. 더구나 포폴로 광장이나 캄포 바치노에서 노숙할 마음이 없다면 더욱 그랬다. 그래서 두 사람은 스페인 광장에 있는 런던 호텔의 주인 파스트리니 씨에게 편지를 보내 안락한 방을 예약해 달라고 부탁했다."], ["Signor Pastrini replied that he had only two rooms and a parlor on the third floor, which he offered at the low charge of a louis per diem.", "파스트리니 씨는 4층에 방 두 개와 거실 하나가 있을 뿐이라고 답하며, 하루 1루이라는 싼값에 그것을 내주겠다고 했다."], ["They accepted his offer; but wishing to make the best use of the time that was left, Albert started for Naples.", "두 사람은 그 제안을 받아들였다. 그러나 남은 시간을 알차게 쓰고 싶었던 알베르는 나폴리로 떠났다."], ["As for Franz, he remained at Florence, and after having passed a few days in exploring the paradise of the Cascine, and spending two or three evenings at the houses of the Florentine nobility, he took a fancy into his head (having already visited Corsica, the cradle of Bonaparte) to visit Elba, the waiting-place of Napoleon.", "프란츠는 피렌체에 남아, 카시네의 낙원을 거니는 데 며칠을 보내고 피렌체 귀족들의 저택에서 두세 차례 저녁을 보낸 뒤, 문득 한 가지 생각에 사로잡혔다. 이미 보나파르트의 요람인 코르시카를 다녀온 그는, 나폴레옹이 기다리며 지냈던 곳인 엘바섬을 가 보고 싶어진 것이었다."]], [["One evening he cast off the painter of a sailboat from the iron ring that secured it to the dock at Leghorn, wrapped himself in his coat and lay down, and said to the crew,—“To the Island of Elba!”", "어느 날 저녁, 그는 리보르노 부두의 쇠고리에 매여 있던 돛단배의 밧줄을 풀어내고는, 외투로 몸을 감싼 채 누워 선원들에게 말했다. \"엘바섬으로!\""]], [["The boat shot out of the harbor like a bird and the next morning Franz disembarked at Porto-Ferrajo.", "배는 새처럼 항구를 빠져나갔고, 이튿날 아침 프란츠는 포르토페라요에 내렸다."], ["He traversed the island, after having followed the traces which the footsteps of the giant have left, and re-embarked for Marciana.", "그는 그 거인이 발자취를 남긴 흔적을 따라 섬을 가로지른 뒤, 다시 배에 올라 마르치아나로 향했다."]], [["Two hours after he again landed at Pianosa, where he was assured that red partridges abounded.", "두 시간 뒤 그는 다시 피아노사에 내렸다. 그곳에는 붉은자고새가 득시글하다는 말을 들었던 것이다."], ["The sport was bad; Franz only succeeded in killing a few partridges, and, like every unsuccessful sportsman, he returned to the boat very much out of temper.", "그러나 사냥은 신통치 않았다. 프란츠는 자고새 몇 마리를 잡는 데 그쳤고, 사냥에 실패한 사람이 으레 그렇듯 잔뜩 언짢은 얼굴로 배로 돌아왔다."]], [["“Ah, if your excellency chose,”", "\"아, 나리께서 마음만 있으시다면,\""], ["said the captain, “you might have capital sport.”", "선장이 말했다. \"굉장한 사냥을 즐기실 수 있습니다요.\""]], [["“Where?”", "\"어디서?\""]], [["“Do you see that island?”", "\"저 섬이 보이십니까?\""], ["continued the captain, pointing to a conical pile rising from the indigo sea.", "선장이 쪽빛 바다 위로 솟아오른 원뿔 모양의 돌무더기를 가리키며 말을 이었다."]], [["“Well, what is this island?”", "\"저 섬이 뭔데?\""]], [["“The Island of Monte Cristo.”", "\"몬테크리스토섬입니다.\""]], [["“But I have no permission to shoot over this island.”", "\"하지만 난 저 섬에서 사냥할 허가가 없네.\""]], [["“Your excellency does not require a permit, for the island is uninhabited.”", "\"나리껜 허가가 필요 없습니다. 저 섬에는 사람이 살지 않으니까요.\""]], [["“Ah, indeed!”", "\"아, 그래!\""], ["said the young man.", "젊은이가 말했다."], ["“A desert island in the midst of the Mediterranean must be a curiosity.”", "\"지중해 한복판의 무인도라니, 그것참 구경거리겠군.\""]], [["“It is very natural; this island is a mass of rocks, and does not contain an acre of land capable of cultivation.”", "\"당연한 일입니다요. 저 섬은 바위투성이라, 농사지을 만한 땅이라곤 한 뙈기도 없으니까요.\""]], [["“To whom does this island belong?”", "\"저 섬은 누구 소유인가?\""]], [["“To Tuscany.”", "\"토스카나의 것입니다.\""]], [["“What game shall I find there!”", "\"거기서 무슨 사냥감을 잡을 수 있겠나?\""]], [["“Thousands of wild goats.”", "\"수천 마리의 들염소를 잡으실 수 있습니다요.\""]], [["“Who live upon the stones, I suppose,”", "\"돌이나 뜯어 먹고 사는 모양이지.\""], ["said Franz with an incredulous smile.", "프란츠가 못 믿겠다는 듯 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“No, but by browsing the shrubs and trees that grow out of the crevices of the rocks.”", "\"아닙니다요, 바위틈에서 자라난 덤불과 나무를 뜯어 먹고 삽니다.\""]], [["“Where can I sleep?”", "\"잠은 어디서 자나?\""]], [["“On shore in the grottos, or on board in your cloak; besides, if your excellency pleases, we can leave as soon as you like—we can sail as well by night as by day, and if the wind drops we can use our oars.”", "\"뭍의 동굴에서 주무시거나, 배 위에서 외투를 덮고 주무시면 됩니다. 게다가 나리만 좋으시다면 언제든 떠날 수 있습니다요. 우린 밤에도 낮 못지않게 항해할 수 있고, 바람이 잦아들면 노를 저으면 되니까요.\""]], [["As Franz had sufficient time, and his apartments at Rome were not yet available, he accepted the proposition.", "프란츠는 시간이 넉넉했고, 로마의 숙소도 아직 쓸 수 없는 형편이라 그 제안을 받아들였다."], ["Upon his answer in the affirmative, the sailors exchanged a few words together in a low tone.", "그가 그러자고 대답하자, 선원들이 자기들끼리 낮은 목소리로 몇 마디를 주고받았다. \"왜 그러나?\""], ["“Well,” asked he, “what now?", "그가 물었다. \"이번엔 또 뭔가?"], ["Is there any difficulty in the way?”", "무슨 곤란한 일이라도 있어?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"아닙니다.\""], ["replied the captain, “but we must warn your excellency that the island is an infected port.”", "선장이 대답했다. \"다만 나리께 미리 일러 두어야 할 게 있습니다. 저 섬은 오염된 항구라는 겁니다.\""]], [["“What do you mean?”", "\"그게 무슨 말인가?\""]], [["“Monte Cristo although uninhabited, yet serves occasionally as a refuge for the smugglers and pirates who come from Corsica, Sardinia, and Africa, and if it becomes known that we have been there, we shall have to perform quarantine for six days on our return to Leghorn.”", "\"몬테크리스토는 사람이 살지 않지만, 이따금 코르시카며 사르데냐며 아프리카에서 오는 밀수꾼들과 해적들의 피난처 노릇을 합니다요. 우리가 거기 다녀왔다는 게 알려지면, 리보르노로 돌아와서 엿새 동안 검역을 받아야 합니다.\""]], [["“The deuce!", "\"이런!"], ["That puts a different face on the matter.", "그러면 얘기가 달라지는데. 엿새라니!"], ["Six days! Why, that’s as long as the Almighty took to make the world!", "아니, 그건 전능하신 하느님께서 세상을 만드신 시간만큼이나 길지 않은가!"], ["Too long a wait—too long.”", "너무 길어, 너무 길다고.\""]], [["“But who will say your excellency has been to Monte Cristo?”", "\"하지만 나리께서 몬테크리스토에 다녀왔다는 걸 누가 말하겠습니까요?\""]], [["“Oh, I shall not,”", "\"오, 난 안 하지.\""], ["cried Franz.", "프란츠가 외쳤다."]], [["“Nor I, nor I,” chorused the sailors.", "\"저희도 안 합니다요, 저희도요.\" 선원들이 입을 모았다."]], [["“Then steer for Monte Cristo.”", "\"그럼 몬테크리스토로 키를 잡게.\""]], [["The captain gave his orders, the helm was put up, and the boat was soon sailing in the direction of the island.", "선장이 명령을 내리자 키가 돌아갔고, 배는 이내 섬을 향해 나아갔다. 프란츠는 만사가 정돈되기를 기다렸다."], ["Franz waited until all was in order, and when the sail was filled, and the four sailors had taken their places—three forward, and one at the helm—he resumed the conversation.", "돛이 바람을 머금고, 선원 넷이 제자리를 잡자 — 셋은 뱃머리에, 하나는 키에 — 그는 다시 이야기를 꺼냈다. \"가에타노,\""], ["“Gaetano,” said he to the captain, “you tell me Monte Cristo serves as a refuge for pirates, who are, it seems to me, a very different kind of game from the goats.”", "그가 선장에게 말했다. \"몬테크리스토가 해적들의 피난처라고 했는데, 그자들은 내가 보기에 염소와는 영 다른 종류의 사냥감 같은데.\""]], [["“Yes, your excellency, and it is true.”", "\"네, 나리, 그렇습니다요.\""]], [["“I knew there were smugglers, but I thought that since the capture of Algiers, and the destruction of the regency, pirates existed only in the romances of Cooper and Captain Marryat.”", "\"밀수꾼이 있다는 건 알았지만, 알제가 함락되고 섭정 정권이 무너진 뒤로 해적이란 건 쿠퍼나 매리엇 선장의 소설 속에나 있는 줄 알았네.\""]], [["“Your excellency is mistaken; there are pirates, like the bandits who were believed to have been exterminated by Pope Leo XII., and who yet, every day, rob travellers at the gates of Rome.", "\"나리께서 잘못 아신 겁니다요. 해적은 있습니다. 교황 레오 12세께서 진작에 소탕했다고들 믿는 산적들도 여태 날마다 로마 성문 앞에서 여행자들을 털고 있지요."], ["Has not your excellency heard that the French _chargé d’affaires_ was robbed six months ago within five hundred paces of Velletri?”", "반년 전에 프랑스 대리공사가 벨레트리에서 오백 걸음도 안 되는 데서 강도를 당했다는 얘기, 나리께선 못 들으셨습니까요?\""]], [["“Oh, yes, I heard that.”", "\"오, 그래, 그 얘긴 들었지.\""]], [["“Well, then, if, like us, your excellency lived at Leghorn, you would hear, from time to time, that a little merchant vessel, or an English yacht that was expected at Bastia, at Porto-Ferrajo, or at Civita Vecchia, has not arrived; no one knows what has become of it, but, doubtless, it has struck on a rock and foundered.", "\"음, 그렇다면, 나리께서 저희처럼 리보르노에 사셨다면, 이따금 이런 소문을 들으셨을 겁니다요. 바스티아나 포르토페라요, 치비타베키아에 도착하기로 되어 있던 작은 상선이나 영국 요트가 도착하지 않았다, 아무도 그 배가 어찌 됐는지 모른다, 하지만 분명 암초에 부딪혀 가라앉았을 거다, 하고요."], ["Now this rock it has met has been a long and narrow boat, manned by six or eight men, who have surprised and plundered it, some dark and stormy night, near some desert and gloomy island, as bandits plunder a carriage in the recesses of a forest.”", "그런데 그 배가 부딪힌 암초란 게 바로 여섯이나 여덟 사람이 탄 길고 좁다란 배였던 겁니다. 그자들이 깜깜하고 폭풍우 치는 밤에, 인적 없고 음산한 섬 근처에서 그 배를 덮쳐 약탈한 거지요. 마치 산적들이 숲속 깊은 곳에서 마차를 터는 것처럼요.\""]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["asked Franz, who lay wrapped in his cloak at the bottom of the boat, “why do not those who have been plundered complain to the French, Sardinian, or Tuscan governments?”", "외투로 몸을 감싼 채 배 바닥에 누운 프란츠가 물었다. \"약탈당한 자들은 왜 프랑스나 사르데냐, 토스카나 정부에 하소연하지 않는 건가?\""]], [["“Why?” said Gaetano with a smile.", "\"왜냐고요?\" 가에타노가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, why?”", "\"그래, 왜?\""]], [["“Because, in the first place, they transfer from the vessel to their own boat whatever they think worth taking, then they bind the crew hand and foot, they attach to everyone’s neck a four-and-twenty-pound ball, a large hole is chopped in the vessel’s bottom, and then they leave her.", "\"왜냐하면, 우선 그자들은 배에서 값나가 보이는 건 죄다 자기네 배로 옮깁니다. 그런 다음 선원들의 손발을 묶고, 저마다 목에 24파운드짜리 포탄을 매답니다. 그리고 배 밑바닥에 큰 구멍을 뚫고는 떠나 버리지요."], ["At the end of ten minutes the vessel begins to roll heavily and settle down. First one gun’l goes under, then the other.", "십 분쯤 지나면 배가 심하게 흔들리며 가라앉기 시작합니다. 먼저 한쪽 뱃전이 물에 잠기고, 그다음 반대쪽이 잠기지요."], ["Then they lift and sink again, and both go under at once. All at once there’s a noise like a cannon—that’s the air blowing up the deck.", "그러다 다시 솟구쳤다 가라앉기를 반복하더니, 양쪽이 한꺼번에 물에 잠깁니다. 그러다 별안간 대포 소리 같은 굉음이 납니다 — 그건 갑판을 뚫고 터져 나오는 공기 소리지요."], ["Soon the water rushes out of the scupper-holes like a whale spouting, the vessel gives a last groan, spins round and round, and disappears, forming a vast whirlpool in the ocean, and then all is over, so that in five minutes nothing but the eye of God can see the vessel where she lies at the bottom of the sea.", "곧 배수구로 고래가 물을 뿜듯 물이 쏟아져 나오고, 배는 마지막 신음을 토하며 빙글빙글 돌다가 바다 위에 거대한 소용돌이를 남기고 사라집니다. 그러면 모든 게 끝난 겁니다. 그렇게 오 분이면, 바다 밑바닥에 가라앉은 그 배를 볼 수 있는 건 하느님의 눈밖에 없게 되지요."], ["Do you understand now,”", "이제 아시겠습니까,\""], ["said the captain, “why no complaints are made to the government, and why the vessel never reaches port?”", "선장이 말했다. \"왜 정부에 하소연 따위가 없고, 왜 그 배가 끝내 항구에 닿지 못하는지를요?\""]], [["It is probable that if Gaetano had related this previous to proposing the expedition, Franz would have hesitated, but now that they had started, he thought it would be cowardly to draw back.", "가에타노가 이 이야기를 원정을 제안하기 전에 했더라면, 프란츠는 아마 망설였을 것이다. 그러나 이미 출발한 마당에, 그는 이제 와서 물러서는 것은 비겁한 일이라고 여겼다."], ["He was one of those men who do not rashly court danger, but if danger presents itself, combat it with the most unalterable coolness.", "그는 무모하게 위험을 자초하는 사람은 아니었지만, 위험이 닥치면 흔들림 없는 냉정함으로 맞서는 부류의 사람이었다."], ["Calm and resolute, he treated any peril as he would an adversary in a duel,—calculated its probable method of approach; retreated, if at all, as a point of strategy and not from cowardice; was quick to see an opening for attack, and won victory at a single thrust.", "차분하고 결단력 있는 그는, 어떤 위험이든 결투의 상대를 다루듯 다루었다. 위험이 다가올 법한 방식을 가늠하고, 물러서더라도 그것은 비겁함이 아니라 전략의 일환으로 물러섰으며, 공격할 빈틈을 재빨리 포착해 단번에 찔러 승리를 거두는 사람이었다."]], [["“Bah!”", "\"흥!\""], ["said he, “I have travelled through Sicily and Calabria—I have sailed two months in the Archipelago, and yet I never saw even the shadow of a bandit or a pirate.”", "그가 말했다. \"난 시칠리아며 칼라브리아를 두루 다녔고, 다도해에서 두 달을 항해했지만, 산적이나 해적의 그림자조차 본 적이 없네.\""]], [["“I did not tell your excellency this to deter you from your project,”", "\"제가 이런 얘기를 한 건 나리의 계획을 단념시키려는 게 아닙니다요.\""], ["replied Gaetano, “but you questioned me, and I have answered; that’s all.”", "가에타노가 대답했다. \"나리께서 물으시기에 답한 것뿐입니다, 그게 다입니다.\""]], [["“Yes, and your conversation is most interesting; and as I wish to enjoy it as long as possible, steer for Monte Cristo.”", "\"그래, 자네 얘긴 무척 흥미롭군. 그러니 가능한 한 오래 그 얘길 즐기고 싶으니, 몬테크리스토로 키를 잡게.\""]], [["The wind blew strongly, the boat made six or seven knots an hour, and they were rapidly reaching the end of their voyage.", "바람이 거세게 불어 배는 시속 6노트에서 7노트로 나아갔고, 두 사람은 빠르게 항해의 끝에 다다르고 있었다."], ["As they drew near the island seemed to lift from the sea, and the air was so clear that they could already distinguish the rocks heaped on one another, like cannon balls in an arsenal, with green bushes and trees growing in the crevices.", "가까이 다가가자 섬은 바다에서 솟아오르는 듯 보였고, 공기가 어찌나 맑은지 무기고에 쌓인 포탄처럼 차곡차곡 포개진 바위들과, 그 틈새에서 자라난 푸른 덤불과 나무까지 벌써 또렷이 분간할 수 있었다."], ["As for the sailors, although they appeared perfectly tranquil yet it was evident that they were on the alert, and that they carefully watched the glassy surface over which they were sailing, and on which a few fishing-boats, with their white sails, were alone visible.", "선원들로 말하자면, 겉으로는 더없이 태평해 보였지만, 그들이 바짝 경계하고 있다는 것은 분명했다. 그들은 흰 돛을 단 고깃배 몇 척만이 드문드문 보이는, 거울처럼 잔잔한 수면을 조심스레 살피고 있었다."]], [["They were within fifteen miles of Monte Cristo when the sun began to set behind Corsica, whose mountains appeared against the sky, showing their rugged peaks in bold relief; this mass of rock, like the giant Adamastor, rose dead ahead, a formidable barrier, and intercepting the light that gilded its massive peaks so that the voyagers were in shadow.", "몬테크리스토까지 15마일쯤 남았을 때, 해가 코르시카 너머로 지기 시작했다. 코르시카의 산들은 하늘을 배경으로 거친 봉우리를 또렷이 드러내며 솟아 있었다. 거인 아다마스토르 같은 그 바위 덩어리가 정면으로 우뚝 솟아 무시무시한 장벽을 이루었고, 그 육중한 봉우리를 황금빛으로 물들이던 햇빛을 가로막은 탓에 항해자들은 그늘 속에 들었다."], ["Little by little the shadow rose higher and seemed to drive before it the last rays of the expiring day; at last the reflection rested on the summit of the mountain, where it paused an instant, like the fiery crest of a volcano, then gloom gradually covered the summit as it had covered the base, and the island now only appeared to be a gray mountain that grew continually darker; half an hour after, the night was quite dark.", "그늘은 조금씩 높이 차올라, 마치 저물어 가는 하루의 마지막 햇살을 앞으로 몰아내는 듯했다. 마침내 그 반사광이 산꼭대기에 머물러, 화산의 불타는 봉우리처럼 한순간 멈춰 섰다가, 어둠이 산기슭을 뒤덮었듯 차츰 꼭대기를 뒤덮었다. 이제 섬은 점점 더 어두워져 가는 잿빛 산으로만 보일 뿐이었고, 반 시간 뒤에는 밤이 완전히 깜깜해졌다."]], [["Fortunately, the mariners were used to these latitudes, and knew every rock in the Tuscan Archipelago; for in the midst of this obscurity Franz was not without uneasiness—Corsica had long since disappeared, and Monte Cristo itself was invisible; but the sailors seemed, like the lynx, to see in the dark, and the pilot who steered did not evince the slightest hesitation.", "다행히 뱃사람들은 이 위도에 익숙했고, 토스카나 다도해의 바위란 바위는 죄다 꿰고 있었다. 이 어둠 속에서 프란츠는 불안하지 않을 수 없었다. 코르시카는 이미 사라진 지 오래였고, 몬테크리스토 자체도 보이지 않았다. 그러나 선원들은 스라소니처럼 어둠 속을 꿰뚫어 보는 듯했고, 키를 잡은 도선사는 한 치의 망설임도 보이지 않았다."]], [["An hour had passed since the sun had set, when Franz fancied he saw, at a quarter of a mile to the left, a dark mass, but he could not precisely make out what it was, and fearing to excite the mirth of the sailors by mistaking a floating cloud for land, he remained silent; suddenly a great light appeared on the strand; land might resemble a cloud, but the fire was not a meteor.", "해가 진 지 한 시간이 지났을 무렵, 프란츠는 왼편으로 사분의 일 마일쯤 떨어진 곳에 검은 덩어리가 보이는 것 같았다. 그러나 그게 정확히 무엇인지 분간할 수 없었고, 떠다니는 구름을 뭍으로 잘못 본 것이라며 선원들의 웃음거리가 될까 두려워 잠자코 있었다. 그때 별안간 물가에 커다란 불빛이 나타났다. 뭍이라면 구름으로 보일 수도 있겠지만, 불은 유성이 아니었다."]], [["“What is this light?” asked he.", "\"저 불빛은 뭔가?\" 그가 물었다."]], [["“Hush!”", "\"쉿!\""], ["said the captain; “it is a fire.”", "선장이 말했다. \"불입니다요.\""]], [["“But you told me the island was uninhabited?”", "\"하지만 자넨 저 섬에 사람이 안 산다고 하지 않았나?\""]], [["“I said there were no fixed habitations on it, but I said also that it served sometimes as a harbor for smugglers.”", "\"고정된 거처가 없다고 했지요. 하지만 이따금 밀수꾼들의 항구 노릇을 한다고도 말씀드렸습니다.\" \"해적들의 항구 노릇도 한다고 했지?\""]], [["“And for pirates?” “And for pirates,”", "\"해적들의 항구 노릇도 합니다요.\""], ["returned Gaetano, repeating Franz’s words.", "가에타노가 프란츠의 말을 되받아 말했다."], ["“It is for that reason I have given orders to pass the island, for, as you see, the fire is behind us.”", "\"그래서 제가 섬을 지나쳐 가라고 명령한 겁니다. 보시다시피 불이 우리 뒤편에 있으니까요.\""]], [["“But this fire?” continued Franz.", "\"하지만 저 불 말일세.\""], ["“It seems to me rather reassuring than otherwise; men who did not wish to be seen would not light a fire.”", "프란츠가 말을 이었다. \"내가 보기엔 오히려 안심이 되는데. 들키고 싶지 않은 자들이라면 불을 피우지 않았을 테니까.\""]], [["“Oh, that goes for nothing,”", "\"오, 그건 아무것도 아닙니다요.\""], ["said Gaetano. “If you can guess the position of the island in the darkness, you will see that the fire cannot be seen from the side or from Pianosa, but only from the sea.”", "가에타노가 말했다. \"어둠 속에서도 섬의 위치를 헤아릴 수 있으시다면, 저 불이 옆쪽에서나 피아노사에서는 보이지 않고, 오직 바다에서만 보인다는 걸 아실 겁니다.\""]], [["“You think, then, this fire indicates the presence of unpleasant neighbors?”", "\"그러니까 자네 생각엔, 저 불이 달갑잖은 이웃이 있다는 표시란 말인가?\""]], [["“That is what we must find out,”", "\"그건 알아봐야지요.\""], ["returned Gaetano, fixing his eyes on this terrestrial star.", "가에타노가 그 지상의 별에 눈길을 박은 채 대답했다."]], [["“How can you find out?”", "\"어떻게 알아본단 말인가?\""]], [["“You shall see.”", "\"보시면 압니다요.\""]], [["Gaetano consulted with his companions, and after five minutes’ discussion a manœuvre was executed which caused the vessel to tack about, they returned the way they had come, and in a few minutes the fire disappeared, hidden by an elevation of the land.", "가에타노가 동료들과 의논하더니, 오 분쯤 이야기를 나눈 끝에 배를 돌리는 조작이 이루어졌다. 그들은 왔던 길을 되짚어 갔고, 몇 분 만에 불은 솟아오른 지형에 가려 보이지 않게 되었다."], ["The pilot again changed the course of the boat, which rapidly approached the island, and was soon within fifty paces of it.", "도선사가 다시 배의 항로를 바꾸자 배는 빠르게 섬으로 다가가, 이내 오십 걸음 거리에 이르렀다."], ["Gaetano lowered the sail, and the boat came to rest.", "가에타노가 돛을 내리자 배가 멈춰 섰다."], ["All this was done in silence, and from the moment that their course was changed not a word was spoken.", "이 모든 일이 침묵 속에 이루어졌고, 항로를 바꾼 순간부터는 한마디 말도 오가지 않았다."]], [["Gaetano, who had proposed the expedition, had taken all the responsibility on himself; the four sailors fixed their eyes on him, while they got out their oars and held themselves in readiness to row away, which, thanks to the darkness, would not be difficult.", "원정을 제안한 가에타노는 모든 책임을 자기 어깨에 짊어졌다. 선원 넷은 노를 꺼내 들고 언제든 저어 달아날 채비를 한 채 그에게 눈을 박았다. 어둠 덕분에 달아나기란 어렵지 않을 터였다."], ["As for Franz, he examined his arms with the utmost coolness; he had two double-barrelled guns and a rifle; he loaded them, looked at the priming, and waited quietly.", "프란츠로 말하자면, 더없이 침착하게 자신의 무기를 점검했다. 그에게는 쌍발총 두 자루와 소총 한 자루가 있었다. 그는 총에 탄을 재고 점화약을 살핀 뒤 조용히 기다렸다."]], [["During this time the captain had thrown off his vest and shirt, and secured his trousers round his waist; his feet were naked, so he had no shoes and stockings to take off; after these preparations he placed his finger on his lips, and lowering himself noiselessly into the sea, swam towards the shore with such precaution that it was impossible to hear the slightest sound; he could only be traced by the phosphorescent line in his wake.", "그동안 선장은 조끼와 셔츠를 벗어 던지고 바지를 허리에 단단히 동여맸다. 그의 발은 맨발이었으니 벗어야 할 신발이나 양말도 없었다. 이런 채비를 마친 그는 손가락을 입술에 대고는 소리 없이 바닷속으로 미끄러져 들어가, 작은 소리 하나 들리지 않을 만큼 조심스럽게 물가를 향해 헤엄쳐 갔다."], ["This track soon disappeared; it was evident that he had touched the shore.", "그를 좇을 수 있는 것이라곤 그가 지나간 자리에 남은 인광의 선뿐이었다. 그 자취마저 곧 사라졌다. 그가 물가에 닿은 게 분명했다."]], [["Everyone on board remained motionless for half an hour, when the same luminous track was again observed, and the swimmer was soon on board.", "배에 탄 모두가 반 시간 동안 꼼짝 않고 있었다. 그러자 같은 빛의 자취가 다시 보였고, 헤엄치던 자는 이내 배 위로 돌아왔다."]], [["“Well?” exclaimed Franz and the sailors in unison.", "\"어땠나?\" 프란츠와 선원들이 한목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“They are Spanish smugglers,”", "\"스페인 밀수꾼들입니다요.\""], ["said he; “they have with them two Corsican bandits.”", "그가 말했다. \"코르시카 산적 둘을 데리고 있습니다.\""]], [["“And what are these Corsican bandits doing here with Spanish smugglers?”", "\"그 코르시카 산적들이 스페인 밀수꾼들과 함께 여기서 뭘 하고 있나?\""]], [["“Alas,”", "\"아, 그게 말입니다.\""], ["returned the captain with an accent of the most profound pity, “we ought always to help one another.", "선장이 더없이 깊은 연민이 어린 어조로 대답했다. \"사람이란 늘 서로 도와야 하는 법이지요. 산적들은 헌병이나 카르비니에리에게 바짝 쫓기는 일이 흔합니다."], ["Very often the bandits are hard pressed by gendarmes or carbineers; well, they see a vessel, and good fellows like us on board, they come and demand hospitality of us; you can’t refuse help to a poor hunted devil; we receive them, and for greater security we stand out to sea.", "그러다 배 한 척이 눈에 띄고, 그 위에 저희 같은 좋은 사람들이 타고 있으면 다가와 잠자리를 청하지요. 쫓기는 가엾은 놈을 어찌 안 도와줍니까. 받아 주는 거지요."], ["This costs us nothing, and saves the life, or at least the liberty, of a fellow-creature, who on the first occasion returns the service by pointing out some safe spot where we can land our goods without interruption.”", "그리고 더 안전하라고 먼바다로 나갑니다. 이건 저희한테 손해 볼 것도 없고, 한 동포의 목숨이나 적어도 자유를 구해 주는 일입니다. 그러면 그자도 첫 기회가 오면, 우리가 방해받지 않고 물건을 부릴 만한 안전한 곳을 일러 주며 그 은혜를 갚지요.\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["said Franz, “then you are a smuggler occasionally, Gaetano?”", "프란츠가 말했다. \"그럼 자네도 이따금 밀수꾼 노릇을 하는 거로군, 가에타노?\""]], [["“Your excellency, we must live somehow,”", "\"나리, 사람은 어떻게든 먹고살아야 하지 않습니까요.\""], ["returned the other, smiling impenetrably.", "그 사내가 속내를 알 수 없는 미소를 지으며 대꾸했다."]], [["“Then you know the men who are now on Monte Cristo?”", "\"그럼 지금 몬테크리스토에 있는 자들을 자네가 안다는 말인가?\""]], [["“Oh, yes, we sailors are like freemasons, and recognize each other by signs.”", "\"오, 그럼요, 저희 뱃사람들은 프리메이슨 같은 거라, 서로 신호로 알아봅니다요.\""]], [["“And do you think we have nothing to fear if we land?”", "\"그럼 우리가 뭍에 내려도 두려워할 게 없다고 보나?\""]], [["“Nothing at all; smugglers are not thieves.”", "\"전혀 없습니다요. 밀수꾼은 도둑이 아니니까요.\""]], [["“But these two Corsican bandits?”", "\"하지만 그 코르시카 산적 둘은?\""], ["said Franz, calculating the chances of peril.", "프란츠가 위험의 확률을 가늠하며 말했다."]], [["“It is not their fault that they are bandits, but that of the authorities.”", "\"그자들이 산적이 된 건 그자들 탓이 아니라, 관청 탓입니다요.\" \"어째서 그런가?\""]], [["“How so?” “Because they are pursued for having made a stiff, as if it was not in a Corsican’s nature to revenge himself.”", "\"한 놈 해치웠다고 쫓기는 거니까요. 마치 코르시카 사람의 천성에 복수가 없기라도 한 것처럼 말입니다.\""]], [["“What do you mean by having made a stiff?—having assassinated a man?”", "\"한 놈 해치웠다는 게 무슨 말인가? 사람을 죽였단 말인가?\""], ["said Franz, continuing his investigation.", "프란츠가 캐묻기를 이어 갔다."]], [["“I mean that they have killed an enemy, which is a very different thing,”", "\"적을 하나 죽였다는 겁니다요. 그건 전혀 다른 일이지요.\""], ["returned the captain.", "선장이 대답했다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"좋아.\""], ["said the young man, “let us demand hospitality of these smugglers and bandits.", "젊은이가 말했다. \"그 밀수꾼들과 산적들에게 잠자리를 청해 보세."], ["Do you think they will grant it?”", "그자들이 응할 것 같나?\""]], [["“Without doubt.”", "\"틀림없이 응할 겁니다.\""]], [["“How many are they?”", "\"몇 명이나 되나?\""]], [["“Four, and the two bandits make six.”", "\"넷에다 산적 둘을 더하면 여섯입니다요.\""]], [["“Just our number, so that if they prove troublesome, we shall be able to hold them in check; so, for the last time, steer to Monte Cristo.”", "\"마침 우리 수와 같군. 그러니 그자들이 말썽을 부린다면 우리도 충분히 제압할 수 있어. 그러니 마지막으로 다시 한번, 몬테크리스토로 키를 잡게.\""]], [["“Yes, but your excellency will permit us to take all due precautions.”", "\"네, 다만 나리께서 저희가 마땅한 경계를 다 갖추도록 허락해 주십시오.\""]], [["“By all means, be as wise as Nestor and as prudent as Ulysses; I do more than permit, I exhort you.”", "\"얼마든지. 네스토르처럼 슬기롭고 율리시스처럼 신중하게 하게. 난 허락하는 정도가 아니라, 그러기를 권하는 바일세.\""]], [["“Silence, then!”", "\"그럼 조용히!\""], ["said Gaetano.", "가에타노가 말했다."]], [["Everyone obeyed.", "모두가 따랐다."], ["For a man who, like Franz, viewed his position in its true light, it was a grave one.", "프란츠처럼 자신의 처지를 똑바로 바라보는 사람에게는, 그것은 심각한 상황이었다."], ["He was alone in the darkness with sailors whom he did not know, and who had no reason to be devoted to him; who knew that he had several thousand francs in his belt, and who had often examined his weapons,—which were very beautiful,—if not with envy, at least with curiosity.", "그는 알지도 못하는, 자기에게 충실할 까닭이라곤 없는 선원들과 어둠 속에 단둘이 있었다. 그자들은 그의 허리띠에 수천 프랑이 들어 있다는 것을 알았고, 그의 무기를 — 매우 아름다운 무기를 — 부러움까지는 아니더라도 적어도 호기심 어린 눈으로 여러 번 살펴본 자들이었다."], ["On the other hand, he was about to land, without any other escort than these men, on an island which had, indeed, a very religious name, but which did not seem to Franz likely to afford him much hospitality, thanks to the smugglers and bandits.", "한편 그는 이 사내들 말고는 아무런 호위도 없이 한 섬에 내리려는 참이었다. 그 섬은 과연 더없이 경건한 이름을 지녔으나, 밀수꾼과 산적들 덕분에 프란츠에게 그리 후한 대접을 베풀 것 같지는 않았다."], ["The history of the scuttled vessels, which had appeared improbable during the day, seemed very probable at night; placed as he was between two possible sources of danger, he kept his eye on the crew, and his gun in his hand.", "낮에는 도무지 믿기지 않던 침몰선 이야기가 밤이 되자 무척 그럴듯하게 여겨졌다. 두 가지 위험의 가능성 사이에 놓인 그는, 선원들에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 총을 손에 쥐고 있었다."]], [["The sailors had again hoisted sail, and the vessel was once more cleaving the waves.", "선원들이 다시 돛을 올리자 배는 또 한 번 물결을 갈랐다."], ["Through the darkness Franz, whose eyes were now more accustomed to it, could see the looming shore along which the boat was sailing, and then, as they rounded a rocky point, he saw the fire more brilliant than ever, and about it five or six persons seated.", "어둠에 어느새 눈이 익은 프란츠는, 배가 따라 항해하는 어슴푸레한 물가를 볼 수 있었다. 그러다 바위 곶을 돌아 나오자, 그는 그 어느 때보다 환히 타오르는 불과 그 주위에 둘러앉은 대여섯 사람을 보았다."], ["The blaze illumined the sea for a hundred paces around.", "불길이 사방 백 걸음의 바다를 밝히고 있었다."], ["Gaetano skirted the light, carefully keeping the boat in the shadow; then, when they were opposite the fire, he steered to the centre of the circle, singing a fishing song, of which his companions sung the chorus.", "가에타노는 배를 줄곧 그늘 속에 두며 조심스럽게 불빛을 비껴 갔다. 그러다 불과 정면으로 마주한 지점에서, 그는 뱃노래를 부르며 그 원의 한가운데로 키를 틀었다. 노래의 후렴은 동료들이 받아 불렀다."]], [["At the first words of the song the men seated round the fire arose and approached the landing-place, their eyes fixed on the boat, evidently seeking to know who the new-comers were and what were their intentions.", "노래의 첫 소절이 들리자 불가에 둘러앉아 있던 사내들이 일어나 상륙 지점으로 다가왔다. 그들은 배에 눈을 박은 채, 새로 온 자들이 누구이며 무슨 속셈인지 알아내려는 기색이 역력했다."], ["They soon appeared satisfied and returned (with the exception of one, who remained at the shore) to their fire, at which the carcass of a goat was roasting.", "곧 그들은 만족한 듯 (한 사람만 물가에 남고) 불가로 돌아갔다. 불 위에서는 염소 한 마리가 통째로 구워지고 있었다."], ["When the boat was within twenty paces of the shore, the man on the beach, who carried a carbine, presented arms after the manner of a sentinel, and cried, “Who comes there?”", "배가 물가에서 스무 걸음 거리에 이르자, 카빈총을 든 물가의 사내가 보초처럼 받들어총 자세를 취하며 사르데냐 말로 외쳤다."], ["in Sardinian.", "\"누구냐?\""]], [["Franz coolly cocked both barrels.", "프란츠는 침착하게 두 총신의 공이치기를 당겼다."], ["Gaetano then exchanged a few words with this man which the traveller did not understand, but which evidently concerned him.", "그러자 가에타노가 그 사내와 몇 마디를 주고받았다. 여행자는 그 말을 알아듣지 못했으나, 분명 자기에 관한 이야기였다."]], [["“Will your excellency give your name, or remain _incognito_?”", "\"나리, 성함을 밝히시겠습니까, 아니면 익명으로 남으시겠습니까?\""], ["asked the captain.", "선장이 물었다."]], [["“My name must rest unknown,”", "\"내 이름은 알리지 말게.\""], ["replied Franz; “merely say I am a Frenchman travelling for pleasure.”", "프란츠가 대답했다. \"그저 즐기러 여행하는 프랑스 사람이라고만 하게.\""]], [["As soon as Gaetano had transmitted this answer, the sentinel gave an order to one of the men seated round the fire, who rose and disappeared among the rocks.", "가에타노가 이 대답을 전하자, 보초는 불가에 둘러앉은 자들 중 하나에게 명령을 내렸다. 그 사람은 일어나 바위 사이로 사라졌다."], ["Not a word was spoken, everyone seemed occupied, Franz with his disembarkment, the sailors with their sails, the smugglers with their goat; but in the midst of all this carelessness it was evident that they mutually observed each other.", "아무도 말을 하지 않았고, 저마다 제 일에 몰두한 듯했다. 프란츠는 상륙 채비에, 선원들은 돛에, 밀수꾼들은 염소에. 그러나 이 태연함 속에서도, 그들이 서로를 살피고 있다는 것은 분명했다."]], [["The man who had disappeared returned suddenly on the opposite side to that by which he had left; he made a sign with his head to the sentinel, who, turning to the boat, said, “_S’accommodi_.”", "사라졌던 사내가 떠날 때와는 반대편에서 별안간 돌아왔다. 그가 보초에게 머리로 신호를 보내자, 보초는 배 쪽으로 돌아서서 말했다. \"사코모디.\""], ["The Italian _s’accommodi_ is untranslatable; it means at once, “Come, enter, you are welcome; make yourself at home; you are the master.”", "이탈리아어 사코모디는 번역할 수 없는 말이다. 그것은 \"오시오, 들어오시오, 환영하오, 편히 계시오, 당신이 주인이오\"라는 뜻을 한꺼번에 담고 있다."], ["It is like that Turkish phrase of Molière’s that so astonished the bourgeois gentleman by the number of things implied in its utterance.", "그것은 몰리에르의 작품에 나오는, 그 한마디에 담긴 숱한 의미로 서민 출신 귀족을 어리둥절하게 했던 터키어 문구와도 같다."]], [["The sailors did not wait for a second invitation; four strokes of the oar brought them to land; Gaetano sprang to shore, exchanged a few words with the sentinel, then his comrades disembarked, and lastly came Franz.", "선원들은 두 번 청할 것도 없이, 노를 네 번 저어 뭍에 닿았다. 가에타노가 물가로 뛰어내려 보초와 몇 마디를 나눈 뒤 동료들이 내렸고, 마지막으로 프란츠가 내렸다."], ["One of his guns was swung over his shoulder, Gaetano had the other, and a sailor held his rifle; his dress, half artist, half dandy, did not excite any suspicion, and, consequently, no disquietude.", "그의 총 한 자루는 어깨에 메여 있었고, 다른 한 자루는 가에타노가, 소총은 한 선원이 들고 있었다. 반은 예술가풍, 반은 멋쟁이풍인 그의 옷차림은 아무런 의심도, 따라서 아무런 불안도 자아내지 않았다."], ["The boat was moored to the shore, and they advanced a few paces to find a comfortable bivouac; but, doubtless, the spot they chose did not suit the smuggler who filled the post of sentinel, for he cried out:", "배를 물가에 매어 놓고 그들은 편안한 야영지를 찾아 몇 걸음 나아갔다. 그러나 그들이 고른 자리가 보초 노릇을 하던 밀수꾼의 마음에 들지 않았던 게 분명했다. 그가 외쳤기 때문이다."]], [["“Not that way, if you please.”", "\"미안하지만, 그쪽은 안 됩니다.\""]], [["Gaetano faltered an excuse, and advanced to the opposite side, while two sailors kindled torches at the fire to light them on their way.", "가에타노가 우물쭈물 변명을 늘어놓으며 반대편으로 나아갔고, 그동안 선원 둘이 불에서 횃불을 붙여 길을 밝혔다."]], [["They advanced about thirty paces, and then stopped at a small esplanade surrounded with rocks, in which seats had been cut, not unlike sentry-boxes.", "그들은 서른 걸음쯤 나아가다가, 바위에 둘러싸인 작은 공터에서 멈춰 섰다. 바위에는 보초막과 다름없는 자리들이 깎여 있었다."], ["Around in the crevices of the rocks grew a few dwarf oaks and thick bushes of myrtles.", "그 둘레의 바위틈에는 작달막한 떡갈나무 몇 그루와 무성한 도금양 덤불이 자라 있었다."], ["Franz lowered a torch, and saw by the mass of cinders that had accumulated that he was not the first to discover this retreat, which was, doubtless, one of the halting-places of the wandering visitors of Monte Cristo.", "프란츠가 횃불을 낮춰 보니, 수북이 쌓인 재 더미로 미루어 이 은신처를 발견한 사람이 자기가 처음이 아니라는 것을 알 수 있었다. 이곳은 분명 몬테크리스토를 떠도는 방문객들의 쉼터 가운데 하나였다."]], [["As for his suspicions, once on _terra firma_, once that he had seen the indifferent, if not friendly, appearance of his hosts, his anxiety had quite disappeared, or rather, at sight of the goat, had turned to appetite.", "의심으로 말하자면, 일단 단단한 땅을 밟고, 적대적이지는 않을지언정 무심한 주인들의 태도를 보고 나자, 그의 불안은 깡그리 사라졌다. 아니, 오히려 염소를 보자 식욕으로 바뀌어 버렸다."], ["He mentioned this to Gaetano, who replied that nothing could be more easy than to prepare a supper when they had in their boat, bread, wine, half a dozen partridges, and a good fire to roast them by.", "그가 이를 가에타노에게 말하자, 가에타노는 배 안에 빵과 포도주, 자고새 여섯 마리와 그것을 구울 좋은 불이 있으니 저녁상을 차리는 것쯤은 더없이 쉬운 일이라고 대답했다."]], [["“Besides,”", "\"게다가,\""], ["added he, “if the smell of their roast meat tempts you, I will go and offer them two of our birds for a slice.”", "그가 덧붙였다. \"저들이 굽는 고기 냄새에 군침이 도신다면, 제가 가서 우리 새 두 마리를 줄 테니 한 점 달라고 해 보겠습니다요.\""]], [["“You are a born diplomat,”", "\"자넨 타고난 외교관이로군.\""], ["returned Franz; “go and try.”", "프란츠가 대꾸했다. \"가서 해 보게.\""]], [["Meanwhile the sailors had collected dried sticks and branches with which they made a fire.", "그동안 선원들은 마른 나뭇가지와 잔가지를 모아 불을 피웠다."], ["Franz waited impatiently, inhaling the aroma of the roasted meat, when the captain returned with a mysterious air.", "프란츠는 구운 고기 냄새를 들이마시며 애가 타게 기다렸다. 그때 선장이 묘한 기색으로 돌아왔다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"어떤가,\""], ["said Franz, “anything new?—do they refuse?”", "프란츠가 말했다. \"뭐 새로운 소식이라도? 거절하던가?\""]], [["“On the contrary,”", "\"오히려 그 반대입니다요.\""], ["returned Gaetano, “the chief, who was told you were a young Frenchman, invites you to sup with him.”", "가에타노가 대답했다. \"젊은 프랑스 분이라는 얘기를 들은 두목이, 나리께 함께 저녁을 들자고 청합니다.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"음,\" 프란츠가 말했다."], ["observed Franz, “this chief is very polite, and I see no objection—the more so as I bring my share of the supper.”", "\"그 두목, 무척 예의 바르군. 안 될 것 없지. 더구나 내가 저녁상에 내 몫을 보태는 셈이니까.\""]], [["“Oh, it is not that; he has plenty, and to spare, for supper; but he makes one condition, and rather a peculiar one, before he will receive you at his house.”", "\"오, 그게 아닙니다요. 저녁거리는 그분께 넘치도록 있습니다. 다만 나리를 댁으로 들이기 전에 한 가지 조건을, 그것도 좀 별난 조건을 내거셨습니다.\""]], [["“His house?", "\"댁이라니?"], ["Has he built one here, then?”", "그럼 여기에 집이라도 지었단 말인가?\""]], [["“No, but he has a very comfortable one all the same, so they say.”", "\"아닙니다요. 그래도 무척 안락한 집이 있다고들 합니다.\""]], [["“You know this chief, then?”", "\"그럼 자넨 그 두목을 아는가?\""]], [["“I have heard talk of him.”", "\"소문은 들었습니다요.\""]], [["“Favorably or otherwise?” “Both.”", "\"좋은 소문인가, 나쁜 소문인가?\""]], [["“The deuce!—and what is this condition?”", "\"둘 다입니다요.\" \"이런! 그래, 그 조건이란 게 뭔가?\""]], [["“That you are blindfolded, and do not take off the bandage until he himself bids you.”", "\"눈을 가리시고, 그분이 직접 풀라고 하시기 전까지는 그 붕대를 벗지 않으셔야 한다는 겁니다.\""]], [["Franz looked at Gaetano, to see, if possible, what he thought of this proposal. “Ah,”", "프란츠는 가에타노가 이 제안을 어떻게 생각하는지 알아내려고 그를 바라보았다. \"아,\""], ["replied he, guessing Franz’s thought, “I know this is a serious matter.”", "프란츠의 속내를 짐작한 가에타노가 대답했다. \"저도 이게 심상찮은 일이라는 건 압니다요.\""]], [["“What should you do in my place?”", "\"내 처지라면 자넨 어떻게 하겠나?\""]], [["“I, who have nothing to lose,—I should go.” 20075m", "\"잃을 게 없는 저라면, 가겠습니다요.\""]], [["“You would accept?”", "\"받아들이겠다고?\""]], [["“Yes, were it only out of curiosity.”", "\"네, 호기심 때문에라도요.\""]], [["“There is something very peculiar about this chief, then?”", "\"그럼 그 두목에게 뭔가 무척 별난 구석이 있다는 말인가?\""]], [["“Listen,”", "\"들어 보십시오.\""], ["said Gaetano, lowering his voice, “I do not know if what they say is true”—he stopped to see if anyone was near.", "가에타노가 목소리를 낮추며 말했다. \"그게 사실인지는 저도 모릅니다만……\" 그가 누가 가까이 있는지 살피려고 말을 멈췄다."]], [["“What do they say?”", "\"뭐라고들 하던가?\""]], [["“That this chief inhabits a cavern to which the Pitti Palace is nothing.”", "\"그 두목이 동굴에 산다는데, 피티 궁전도 거기 비하면 아무것도 아니랍니다.\""]], [["“What nonsense!”", "\"무슨 헛소리를!\""], ["said Franz, reseating himself.", "프란츠가 다시 자리에 앉으며 말했다."]], [["“It is no nonsense; it is quite true.", "\"헛소리가 아닙니다요, 정말입니다."], ["Cama, the pilot of the _Saint Ferdinand_, went in once, and he came back amazed, vowing that such treasures were only to be heard of in fairy tales.”", "생페르디낭호의 도선사 카마가 한번 들어가 봤는데, 그런 보물은 동화 속에서나 들을 법한 것이더라며 넋이 나가서 돌아왔습니다요.\""]], [["“Do you know,”", "\"이보게,\""], ["observed Franz, “that with such stories you make me think of Ali Baba’s enchanted cavern?”", "프란츠가 말했다. \"그런 이야기를 들으니 알리바바의 마법 동굴이 떠오르는군.\""]], [["“I tell you what I have been told.”", "\"저야 들은 대로 말씀드리는 겁니다요.\""]], [["“Then you advise me to accept?”", "\"그럼 받아들이라고 권하는 건가?\""]], [["“Oh, I don’t say that; your excellency will do as you please; I should be sorry to advise you in the matter.”", "\"오, 그렇게는 말씀 못 드리지요. 나리 좋으실 대로 하십시오. 이 일에 관해서는 권해 드리기가 송구합니다요.\""]], [["Franz pondered the matter for a few moments, concluded that a man so rich could not have any intention of plundering him of what little he had, and seeing only the prospect of a good supper, accepted.", "프란츠는 잠시 그 일을 곰곰이 따져 보았다. 그러고는 그토록 부유한 사람이 자기 같은 사람의 보잘것없는 재물을 약탈할 마음이야 없으리라 결론짓고, 또 그저 잘 차린 저녁상만 떠올리며 받아들이기로 했다."], ["Gaetano departed with the reply.", "가에타노가 그 대답을 가지고 떠났다."], ["Franz was prudent, and wished to learn all he possibly could concerning his host.", "신중한 프란츠는 주인에 관해 알아낼 수 있는 모든 것을 알아내고 싶었다."], ["He turned towards the sailor, who, during this dialogue, had sat gravely plucking the partridges with the air of a man proud of his office, and asked him how these men had landed, as no vessel of any kind was visible.", "그는 한 선원에게로 몸을 돌렸다. 그 선원은 이 대화가 오가는 동안, 제 일에 자부심을 느끼는 사람 특유의 태도로 진지하게 자고새 털을 뽑으며 앉아 있었다. 프란츠는 그에게 배라곤 어디에도 보이지 않는데 그자들이 어떻게 이 섬에 내렸는지 물었다."]], [["“Never mind that,”", "\"그건 신경 쓰지 마십시오.\""], ["returned the sailor, “I know their vessel.”", "선원이 대꾸했다. \"저는 그자들 배를 압니다요.\""]], [["“Is it a very beautiful vessel?”", "\"무척 아름다운 배인가?\""]], [["“I would not wish for a better to sail round the world.”", "\"세계 일주를 한다 해도 그보다 나은 배는 바라지 않을 겁니다요.\""]], [["“Of what burden is she?”", "\"적재량은 얼마나 되나?\""]], [["“About a hundred tons; but she is built to stand any weather.", "\"백 톤쯤 됩니다요. 하지만 어떤 날씨에도 끄떡없게 지은 배지요."], ["She is what the English call a yacht.”", "영국 사람들이 요트라고 부르는 겁니다.\""]], [["“Where was she built?”", "\"어디서 지은 배인가?\""]], [["“I know not; but my own opinion is she is a Genoese.”", "\"모르겠습니다요. 하지만 제 생각엔 제노바산 같습니다.\""]], [["“And how did a leader of smugglers,”", "\"그런데 어떻게 밀수꾼 두목이,\""], ["continued Franz, “venture to build a vessel designed for such a purpose at Genoa?”", "프란츠가 말을 이었다. \"그런 용도의 배를 감히 제노바에서 짓는단 말인가?\""]], [["“I did not say that the owner was a smuggler,”", "\"제가 언제 그 주인이 밀수꾼이라고 했습니까요.\""], ["replied the sailor.", "선원이 대답했다."]], [["“No; but Gaetano did, I thought.”", "\"아니, 가에타노가 그랬던 것 같은데.\""]], [["“Gaetano had only seen the vessel from a distance, he had not then spoken to anyone.”", "\"가에타노는 그 배를 멀리서 봤을 뿐, 그때는 아무하고도 말을 안 나눴습니다요.\""]], [["“And if this person be not a smuggler, who is he?”", "\"그럼 그 사람이 밀수꾼이 아니라면, 누구란 말인가?\""]], [["“A wealthy signor, who travels for his pleasure.”", "\"즐기러 여행 다니는 부유한 양반이지요.\""]], [["“Come,”", "'이거,'"], ["thought Franz, “he is still more mysterious, since the two accounts do not agree.”", "프란츠는 생각했다. '두 사람 얘기가 안 맞으니, 더욱 수상쩍군.'"]], [["“What is his name?”", "\"그 사람 이름이 뭔가?\""]], [["“If you ask him, he says Sinbad the Sailor; but I doubt if it be his real name.”", "\"물어보시면 신드바드라고 하지만, 그게 진짜 이름인지는 모르겠습니다요.\""]], [["“Sinbad the Sailor?”", "\"뱃사람 신드바드?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“On the sea.”", "\"바다에서 삽니다요.\""]], [["“What country does he come from?”", "\"어느 나라 사람인가?\""]], [["“I do not know.”", "\"모릅니다요.\""]], [["“Have you ever seen him?”", "\"본 적은 있나?\""]], [["“Sometimes.”", "\"이따금요.\""]], [["“What sort of a man is he?”", "\"어떤 사람인가?\""]], [["“Your excellency will judge for yourself.”", "\"나리께서 직접 판단하실 겁니다요.\""]], [["“Where will he receive me?”", "\"날 어디서 맞아 줄 텐가?\""]], [["“No doubt in the subterranean palace Gaetano told you of.”", "\"틀림없이 가에타노가 말씀드린 그 지하 궁전에서겠지요.\""]], [["“Have you never had the curiosity, when you have landed and found this island deserted, to seek for this enchanted palace?”", "\"자넨 뭍에 내려 이 섬이 텅 비어 있는 걸 보고도, 그 마법 궁전을 찾아보고 싶은 호기심이 든 적이 한 번도 없었나?\""]], [["“Oh, yes, more than once, but always in vain; we examined the grotto all over, but we never could find the slightest trace of any opening; they say that the door is not opened by a key, but a magic word.”", "\"오, 있고말고요, 한두 번이 아닙니다요. 하지만 늘 헛수고였지요. 동굴을 샅샅이 살펴봤지만, 입구의 흔적이라곤 조금도 찾을 수 없었습니다. 그 문은 열쇠가 아니라 주문으로 열린다고들 합니다요.\""]], [["“Decidedly,”", "\"이건 영락없이,\""], ["muttered Franz, “this is an Arabian Nights’ adventure.”", "프란츠가 중얼거렸다. \"천일야화의 모험이로군.\""]], [["“His excellency waits for you,”", "\"나리께서 기다리십니다.\""], ["said a voice, which he recognized as that of the sentinel.", "한 목소리가 말했다. 프란츠는 그것이 보초의 목소리임을 알아챘다."], ["He was accompanied by two of the yacht’s crew.", "그는 요트 선원 둘과 함께였다."]], [["Franz drew his handkerchief from his pocket, and presented it to the man who had spoken to him.", "프란츠는 주머니에서 손수건을 꺼내 자기에게 말을 건 사내에게 건넸다."], ["Without uttering a word, they bandaged his eyes with a care that showed their apprehensions of his committing some indiscretion.", "그들은 한마디 말도 없이 그의 눈을 가렸는데, 그가 무슨 경솔한 짓이라도 할까 봐 염려하는 기색이 역력한 정성스러운 손길이었다."], ["Afterwards he was made to promise that he would not make the least attempt to raise the bandage.", "그러고는 그에게 붕대를 들춰 보려는 시도를 조금도 하지 않겠다는 약속을 받아 냈다."], ["He promised.", "그는 약속했다."]], [["Then his two guides took his arms, and he went on, guided by them, and preceded by the sentinel.", "이윽고 두 안내인이 그의 양팔을 붙잡았고, 그는 보초를 앞세우고 그들에게 이끌려 걸어갔다."], ["After going about thirty paces, he smelt the appetizing odor of the kid that was roasting, and knew thus that he was passing the bivouac; they then led him on about fifty paces farther, evidently advancing towards that part of the shore where they would not allow Gaetano to go—a refusal he could now comprehend.", "서른 걸음쯤 가자 구워지는 새끼 염소의 군침 도는 냄새가 풍겨, 그는 자기가 야영지를 지나고 있음을 알았다. 그런 다음 그들은 다시 오십 걸음쯤 더 그를 이끌고 갔는데, 분명 그들이 가에타노에게는 가지 못하게 막았던 그 물가 쪽으로 나아가고 있었다. 이제야 그는 그 거절의 까닭을 헤아릴 수 있었다."]], [["Presently, by a change in the atmosphere, he knew that they were entering a cave; after going on for a few seconds more he heard a crackling, and it seemed to him as though the atmosphere again changed, and became balmy and perfumed.", "이윽고 공기의 변화로, 그는 자기가 동굴에 들어서고 있음을 알았다. 몇 초 더 나아가자 무언가 타닥거리는 소리가 들렸고, 공기가 다시 바뀌어 향긋하고 그윽해지는 것 같았다."], ["At length his feet touched on a thick and soft carpet, and his guides let go their hold of him.", "마침내 그의 발이 두껍고 부드러운 양탄자에 닿았고, 안내인들이 그를 놓아주었다."], ["There was a moment’s silence, and then a voice, in excellent French, although, with a foreign accent, said:", "한순간 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 외국 억양이 섞이긴 했으나 더없이 훌륭한 프랑스어로 한 목소리가 말했다."]], [["“Welcome, sir. I beg you will remove your bandage.”", "\"어서 오십시오, 선생. 부디 붕대를 벗으시지요.\""]], [["It may be supposed, then, Franz did not wait for a repetition of this permission, but took off the handkerchief, and found himself in the presence of a man from thirty-eight to forty years of age, dressed in a Tunisian costume, that is to say, a red cap with a long blue silk tassel, a vest of black cloth embroidered with gold, pantaloons of deep red, large and full gaiters of the same color, embroidered with gold like the vest, and yellow slippers; he had a splendid cashmere round his waist, and a small sharp and crooked cangiar was passed through his girdle.", "말할 것도 없이, 프란츠는 이 허락이 되풀이되기를 기다리지 않고 손수건을 벗었다. 그러자 서른여덟에서 마흔쯤 된 한 사내가 눈앞에 있었다. 사내는 튀니지풍 옷차림이었다. 다시 말해, 긴 푸른 비단 술이 달린 붉은 모자에, 금실 자수를 놓은 검은 천 조끼, 짙은 붉은색 통 넓은 바지, 조끼처럼 금실 자수를 놓은 같은 색의 크고 헐렁한 각반, 그리고 노란 슬리퍼를 신고 있었다. 허리에는 화려한 캐시미어 천을 둘렀고, 허리띠에는 작고 날카로운 곡선 모양의 단검이 꽂혀 있었다."]], [["Although of a paleness that was almost livid, this man had a remarkably handsome face; his eyes were penetrating and sparkling; his nose, quite straight, and projecting direct from the brow, was of the pure Greek type, while his teeth, as white as pearls, were set off to admiration by the black moustache that encircled them.", "거의 송장 같은 창백함을 띠긴 했으나, 이 사내의 얼굴은 놀랍도록 잘생긴 것이었다. 그의 눈은 꿰뚫는 듯 번득였고, 이마에서 곧장 뻗어 나온 반듯한 코는 순수한 그리스풍이었으며, 진주처럼 흰 이는 그것을 두른 검은 콧수염 덕분에 한층 돋보였다."]], [["His pallor was so peculiar, that it seemed to pertain to one who had been long entombed, and who was incapable of resuming the healthy glow and hue of life.", "그의 창백함은 어찌나 유별난지, 마치 오래 무덤에 묻혀 있어 살아 있는 자의 건강한 혈색과 윤기를 되찾을 수 없게 된 사람의 것 같았다."], ["He was not particularly tall, but extremely well made, and, like the men of the South, had small hands and feet.", "그는 키가 특별히 크지는 않았으나 무척 균형 잡힌 몸매였고, 남부 사람들이 그렇듯 손발이 작았다."], ["But what astonished Franz, who had treated Gaetano’s description as a fable, was the splendor of the apartment in which he found himself.", "그러나 가에타노의 묘사를 한낱 꾸며 낸 이야기로 여겼던 프란츠를 놀라게 한 것은, 자신이 들어선 방의 호화로움이었다."]], [["The entire chamber was lined with crimson brocade, worked with flowers of gold.", "방 전체가 황금빛 꽃무늬를 넣어 짠 진홍색 비단으로 둘러져 있었다. 움푹 들어간 곳에는 일종의 긴 의자가 놓여 있었고, 그 위에는 은 칼집에 든 아라비아 검들을 꽂은 받침대가 있었는데, 손잡이마다 보석이 찬란히 빛났다."], ["In a recess was a kind of divan, surmounted with a stand of Arabian swords in silver scabbards, and the handles resplendent with gems; from the ceiling hung a lamp of Venetian glass, of beautiful shape and color, while the feet rested on a Turkey carpet, in which they sunk to the instep; tapestry hung before the door by which Franz had entered, and also in front of another door, leading into a second apartment which seemed to be brilliantly illuminated.", "천장에는 아름다운 모양과 빛깔의 베네치아 유리 등이 매달려 있었고, 발은 튀르키예산 양탄자에 푹 잠겨 발등까지 묻혔다. 프란츠가 들어온 문 앞에는 휘장이 드리워져 있었고, 또 다른 문 앞에도 마찬가지였다. 그 문은 휘황하게 밝혀진 듯한 두 번째 방으로 이어져 있었다."]], [["The host gave Franz time to recover from his surprise, and, moreover, returned look for look, not even taking his eyes off him.", "주인은 프란츠가 놀라움에서 헤어나도록 시간을 주었다. 게다가 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않고, 시선에는 시선으로 응했다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"선생,\" 그가 잠시 뒤에 말했다. \"이리로 모시면서 취한 조심성에 대해 천 번이라도 양해를 구합니다."], ["he said, after a pause, “a thousand excuses for the precaution taken in your introduction hither; but as, during the greater portion of the year, this island is deserted, if the secret of this abode were discovered, I should doubtless, find on my return my temporary retirement in a state of great disorder, which would be exceedingly annoying, not for the loss it occasioned me, but because I should not have the certainty I now possess of separating myself from all the rest of mankind at pleasure.", "하지만 일 년의 대부분 동안 이 섬은 텅 비어 있는지라, 만약 이 거처의 비밀이 새어 나간다면, 돌아왔을 때 제 임시 은신처가 엉망진창이 되어 있는 것을 보게 될 게 분명합니다. 그건 무척 곤혹스러운 일이지요. 그것이 제게 끼치는 손실 때문이 아니라, 제가 지금 누리고 있는 확신, 즉 내키는 대로 다른 모든 인간들로부터 떨어져 지낼 수 있다는 확신을 잃게 되기 때문입니다."], ["Let me now endeavor to make you forget this temporary unpleasantness, and offer you what no doubt you did not expect to find here—that is to say, a tolerable supper and pretty comfortable beds.”", "자, 이제 이 잠깐의 불쾌함을 잊으시도록 애써 보겠습니다. 그리고 선생께서 여기서 찾으리라곤 기대치 못하셨을 것을 대접해 드리지요. 다시 말해, 그럭저럭 먹을 만한 저녁상과 꽤 안락한 잠자리를 말입니다.\""]], [["“_Ma foi_, my dear sir,”", "\"맹세코, 선생,\" 프란츠가 대답했다."], ["replied Franz, “make no apologies.", "\"사과하실 것 없습니다. 마법 궁전에 들어서는 사람들의 눈을 가린다는 건, 저도 늘 보아 온 일입니다. 가령 「위그노 교도들」의 라울 같은 경우 말이지요."], ["I have always observed that they bandage people’s eyes who penetrate enchanted palaces, for instance, those of Raoul in the _Huguenots_, and really I have nothing to complain of, for what I see makes me think of the wonders of the _Arabian Nights_.” 20079m", "정말이지 저는 불평할 게 조금도 없습니다. 지금 제 눈앞의 광경은 「천일야화」의 경이로움을 떠올리게 하니까요.\""]], [["“Alas!", "\"아아!"], ["I may say with Lucullus, if I could have anticipated the honor of your visit, I would have prepared for it.", "루쿨루스의 말마따나, 선생의 방문이라는 영광을 미리 알았더라면 그에 걸맞은 채비를 해 두었을 것을."], ["But such as is my hermitage, it is at your disposal; such as is my supper, it is yours to share, if you will.", "하지만 이 보잘것없는 은둔처나마 선생께 내어 드리겠습니다. 이 변변찮은 저녁상이나마, 원하신다면 함께 드시지요."], ["Ali, is the supper ready?”", "알리, 저녁상은 다 차려졌느냐?\""]], [["At this moment the tapestry moved aside, and a Nubian, black as ebony, and dressed in a plain white tunic, made a sign to his master that all was prepared in the dining-room.", "이 순간 휘장이 옆으로 젖혀지더니, 흑단처럼 검고 수수한 흰 튜닉을 걸친 누비아인이 식당에 모든 준비가 끝났다는 신호를 주인에게 보냈다."]], [["“Now,”", "\"자,\""], ["said the unknown to Franz, “I do not know if you are of my opinion, but I think nothing is more annoying than to remain two or three hours together without knowing by name or appellation how to address one another.", "낯선 사내가 프란츠에게 말했다. \"선생도 같은 생각이신지 모르겠으나, 저는 두세 시간을 함께 있으면서도 서로를 무어라 부를지 이름도 호칭도 모르는 것만큼 거북한 일은 없다고 봅니다."], ["Pray observe, that I too much respect the laws of hospitality to ask your name or title.", "부디 알아주십시오, 저는 손님을 대접하는 법도를 너무도 존중하기에 선생의 성함이나 직함을 묻지 않겠습니다."], ["I only request you to give me one by which I may have the pleasure of addressing you.", "다만 제가 기꺼이 선생을 부를 수 있도록 호칭 하나만 일러 주시기를 청합니다."], ["As for myself, that I may put you at your ease, I tell you that I am generally called ‘Sinbad the Sailor.’”", "제 쪽으로 말하자면, 선생을 편안케 해 드리고자 미리 말씀드리는데, 저는 흔히 '뱃사람 신드바드'라고 불립니다.\""]], [["“And I,”", "\"그럼 저도,\""], ["replied Franz, “will tell you, as I only require his wonderful lamp to make me precisely like Aladdin, that I see no reason why at this moment I should not be called Aladdin.", "프란츠가 대답했다. \"말씀드리지요. 저는 그 놀라운 램프만 있으면 영락없이 알라딘이 될 사람이니, 지금 이 순간 저를 알라딘이라 부르지 못할 까닭이 없겠군요."], ["That will keep us from going away from the East whither I am tempted to think I have been conveyed by some good genius.”", "그러면 어떤 선한 정령에게 실려 온 듯한 이 동방에서 벗어나지 않아도 될 테고요.\""]], [["“Well, then, Signor Aladdin,”", "\"좋습니다, 그럼 알라딘 나리,\""], ["replied the singular Amphitryon, “you heard our repast announced, will you now take the trouble to enter the dining-room, your humble servant going first to show the way?”", "이 별난 주인이 대답했다. \"식사 준비가 됐다는 말을 들으셨으니, 이제 식당으로 드시지요. 보잘것없는 종인 제가 앞장서서 길을 안내하겠습니다.\""]], [["At these words, moving aside the tapestry, Sinbad preceded his guest.", "이 말과 함께 신드바드는 휘장을 옆으로 젖히며 손님을 앞서 갔다."], ["Franz now looked upon another scene of enchantment; the table was splendidly covered, and once convinced of this important point he cast his eyes around him.", "프란츠는 이제 또 다른 마법의 광경을 바라보았다. 식탁은 휘황찬란하게 차려져 있었고, 이 중요한 점을 확인하고 나자 그는 사방을 둘러보았다."], ["The dining-room was scarcely less striking than the room he had just left; it was entirely of marble, with antique bas-reliefs of priceless value; and at the four corners of this apartment, which was oblong, were four magnificent statues, having baskets in their hands.", "식당은 방금 떠나온 방 못지않게 눈부셨다. 온통 대리석으로 되어 있었고, 값을 매길 수 없는 고풍스러운 부조들이 걸려 있었다. 직사각형인 이 방의 네 귀퉁이에는 손에 바구니를 든 웅장한 조각상 넷이 서 있었다."], ["These baskets contained four pyramids of most splendid fruit; there were Sicily pine-apples, pomegranates from Malaga, oranges from the Balearic Isles, peaches from France, and dates from Tunis.", "그 바구니들에는 더없이 화려한 과일이 네 개의 피라미드처럼 담겨 있었다. 시칠리아산 파인애플, 말라가산 석류, 발레아레스제도산 오렌지, 프랑스산 복숭아, 그리고 튀니스산 대추야자였다."]], [["The supper consisted of a roast pheasant garnished with Corsican blackbirds; a boar’s ham with jelly, a quarter of a kid with tartar sauce, a glorious turbot, and a gigantic lobster.", "저녁상에는 코르시카산 검은지빠귀로 곁들인 꿩구이, 젤리를 얹은 멧돼지 다리살, 타르타르소스를 곁들인 새끼 염소 사분지 한 마리, 훌륭한 가자미 한 마리, 그리고 거대한 바닷가재 한 마리가 올랐다."], ["Between these large dishes were smaller ones containing various dainties.", "이 큰 요리들 사이에는 갖가지 별미를 담은 작은 접시들이 놓여 있었다."], ["The dishes were of silver, and the plates of Japanese china.", "그릇은 은제였고, 접시는 일본 도자기였다."]], [["Franz rubbed his eyes in order to assure himself that this was not a dream.", "프란츠는 이것이 꿈이 아닌지 확인하려고 눈을 비볐다."], ["Ali alone was present to wait at table, and acquitted himself so admirably, that the guest complimented his host thereupon.", "식탁 시중은 알리 혼자서 들었는데, 어찌나 훌륭하게 해내는지 손님이 주인에게 그 점을 칭찬할 정도였다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네,\""], ["replied he, while he did the honors of the supper with much ease and grace—“yes, he is a poor devil who is much devoted to me, and does all he can to prove it.", "그가 더없이 편안하고 우아하게 저녁상을 대접하며 대답했다. \"네, 그는 제게 무척 헌신적인 가엾은 친구로, 그것을 증명하려고 할 수 있는 모든 일을 다 합니다."], ["He remembers that I saved his life, and as he has a regard for his head, he feels some gratitude towards me for having kept it on his shoulders.”", "제가 제 목숨을 구해 준 것을 기억하지요. 그리고 자기 머리를 소중히 여기는지라, 그것을 어깨 위에 붙어 있게 해 준 데 대해 제게 얼마간 고마움을 느끼는 겁니다.\""]], [["Ali approached his master, took his hand, and kissed it.", "알리가 주인에게 다가가 그의 손을 잡고 입을 맞췄다."]], [["“Would it be impertinent, Signor Sinbad,”", "\"신드바드 나리,\" 프란츠가 말했다."], ["said Franz, “to ask you the particulars of this kindness?”", "\"이 친절에 얽힌 사연을 여쭤본다면 무례한 일이 될까요?\""]], [["20081m\n“Oh, they are simple enough,”", "\"오, 아주 간단한 이야기입니다.\""], ["replied the host.", "주인이 대답했다."], ["“It seems the fellow had been caught wandering nearer to the harem of the Bey of Tunis than etiquette permits to one of his color, and he was condemned by the Bey to have his tongue cut out, and his hand and head cut off; the tongue the first day, the hand the second, and the head the third.", "\"이 친구가 튀니스 태수의 후궁 근처를, 제 피부색을 가진 사람에게 허락된 예법보다 더 가까이 어슬렁거리다가 붙잡힌 모양입니다. 태수는 그에게 혀를 자르고 손과 머리를 자르라는 형을 내렸지요. 첫날은 혀, 이튿날은 손, 사흗날은 머리를 말입니다."], ["I always had a desire to have a mute in my service, so learning the day his tongue was cut out, I went to the Bey, and proposed to give him for Ali a splendid double-barreled gun, which I knew he was very desirous of having.", "저는 늘 말 못 하는 하인을 두고 싶었던지라, 그의 혀가 잘리는 날을 알아내고는 태수를 찾아가 알리를 주는 대가로 태수가 무척 갖고 싶어 하던 줄 아는 훌륭한 쌍발총을 주겠다고 제안했지요. 태수는 잠시 망설였습니다."], ["He hesitated a moment, he was so very desirous to complete the poor devil’s punishment.", "그 가엾은 친구의 형벌을 끝까지 마치고 싶은 마음이 워낙 컸으니까요."], ["But when I added to the gun an English cutlass with which I had shivered his highness’s yataghan to pieces, the Bey yielded, and agreed to forgive the hand and head, but on condition that the poor fellow never again set foot in Tunis.", "하지만 제가 태수의 언월도를 산산조각 낸 적이 있는 영국제 단검을 총에 얹어 주자, 태수는 마음을 누그러뜨려 손과 머리는 용서하기로 했습니다. 다만 그 가엾은 친구가 두 번 다시 튀니스에 발을 들이지 않는다는 조건이었지요."], ["This was a useless clause in the bargain, for whenever the coward sees the first glimpse of the shores of Africa, he runs down below, and can only be induced to appear again when we are out of sight of that quarter of the globe.”", "사실 그건 거래에서 쓸데없는 조항이었습니다. 그 겁쟁이는 아프리카 해안이 어렴풋이 보이기만 해도 아래로 달려 내려가, 우리가 지구의 그 구역을 벗어나야만 겨우 다시 모습을 드러내니까요.\""]], [["Franz remained a moment silent and pensive, hardly knowing what to think of the half-kindness, half-cruelty, with which his host related the brief narrative.", "프란츠는 잠시 말없이 생각에 잠겨, 주인이 이 짤막한 이야기를 들려준 그 반은 다정하고 반은 잔혹한 태도를 어떻게 받아들여야 할지 알 수 없었다."]], [["“And like the celebrated sailor whose name you have assumed,”", "\"그리고 선생이 그 이름을 빌려 온 저 유명한 뱃사람처럼,\""], ["he said, by way of changing the conversation, “you pass your life in travelling?”", "그가 화제를 바꾸려고 말했다. \"선생도 여행하며 생을 보내시는군요?\""]], [["“Yes. I made a vow at a time when I little thought I should ever be able to accomplish it,”", "\"그렇습니다. 언젠가 그것을 이룰 수 있으리라곤 거의 생각지도 못했던 시절에 저는 한 가지 맹세를 했지요.\""], ["said the unknown with a singular smile; “and I made some others also which I hope I may fulfil in due season.”", "낯선 사내가 묘한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"그리고 다른 몇 가지 맹세도 했는데, 때가 되면 이루어지기를 바라고 있습니다.\""]], [["Although Sinbad pronounced these words with much calmness, his eyes gave forth gleams of extraordinary ferocity.", "신드바드는 이 말을 무척 차분하게 내뱉었으나, 그의 눈에서는 예사롭지 않은 사나움의 섬광이 번득였다."]], [["“You have suffered a great deal, sir?”", "\"선생, 많이 고생하셨군요?\""], ["said Franz inquiringly.", "프란츠가 캐묻듯 말했다."]], [["Sinbad started and looked fixedly at him, as he replied, “What makes you suppose so?”", "신드바드가 움찔하더니 그를 뚫어지게 바라보며 대답했다. \"어찌 그리 생각하십니까?\""]], [["“Everything,”", "\"모든 게 그렇습니다.\""], ["answered Franz,—“your voice, your look, your pallid complexion, and even the life you lead.”", "프란츠가 대답했다. \"선생의 목소리, 눈빛, 창백한 안색, 게다가 선생이 보내는 삶까지도요.\""]], [["“I?—I live the happiest life possible, the real life of a pasha.", "\"제가요? 저는 더없이 행복한 삶을, 진짜 파샤의 삶을 살고 있습니다."], ["I am king of all creation.", "저는 만물의 왕입니다."], ["I am pleased with one place, and stay there; I get tired of it, and leave it; I am free as a bird and have wings like one; my attendants obey my slightest wish.", "한곳이 마음에 들면 거기 머물고, 싫증이 나면 떠나지요. 저는 새처럼 자유롭고, 새처럼 날개도 있습니다. 제 시중꾼들은 제 사소한 바람에도 복종합니다."], ["Sometimes I amuse myself by delivering some bandit or criminal from the bonds of the law.", "이따금 저는 산적이나 죄인을 법의 굴레에서 풀어 주며 즐기기도 하지요."], ["Then I have my mode of dispensing justice, silent and sure, without respite or appeal, which condemns or pardons, and which no one sees.", "그리고 제게는 정의를 베푸는 저만의 방식이 있는데, 그것은 조용하고 확실하며, 유예도 항소도 없이 죄를 묻거나 사면하고, 아무도 그것을 보지 못합니다."], ["Ah, if you had tasted my life, you would not desire any other, and would never return to the world unless you had some great project to accomplish there.”", "아, 선생도 제 삶을 맛보셨더라면, 다른 어떤 삶도 바라지 않으셨을 겁니다. 그리고 그곳에서 이뤄야 할 큰 계획이 있지 않은 한, 결코 세상으로 돌아가지 않으셨을 테고요.\""]], [["“Revenge, for instance!”", "\"이를테면 복수 같은 것 말이지요!\""], ["observed Franz.", "프란츠가 한마디 했다."]], [["The unknown fixed on the young man one of those looks which penetrate into the depth of the heart and thoughts.", "낯선 사내는 마음과 생각의 밑바닥까지 꿰뚫어 보는 듯한 그런 눈길 가운데 하나를 젊은이에게 박았다."], ["“And why revenge?”", "\"한데 왜 복수입니까?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“Because,”", "\"왜냐하면,\""], ["replied Franz, “you seem to me like a man who, persecuted by society, has a fearful account to settle with it.”", "프란츠가 대답했다. \"선생은 제게, 사회에 박해받아 사회와 무시무시한 셈을 치러야 하는 사람처럼 보이니까요.\""]], [["“Ah!” responded Sinbad, laughing with his singular laugh, which displayed his white and sharp teeth.", "\"아!\" 신드바드가 흰 송곳니를 드러내며 그 특유의 미소로 웃으며 대꾸했다."], ["“You have not guessed rightly.", "\"잘못 짚으셨군요."], ["Such as you see me I am, a sort of philosopher, and one day perhaps I shall go to Paris to rival Monsieur Appert, and the man in the little blue cloak.”", "보시다시피 저는 일종의 철학자입니다. 그리고 언젠가는 어쩌면 파리로 가서 아페르 씨와, 그 작은 파란 외투를 걸친 사내와 겨뤄 볼지도 모르지요.\""]], [["“And will that be the first time you ever took that journey?”", "\"그럼 그게 그 여행을 처음 하시는 게 됩니까?\""]], [["“Yes; it will.", "\"네, 그렇습니다."], ["I must seem to you by no means curious, but I assure you that it is not my fault I have delayed it so long—it will happen one day or the other.”", "선생께는 제가 도무지 호기심이 없는 사람처럼 보이겠지요. 하지만 그토록 오래 미뤄 온 게 제 탓이 아니라는 건 분명히 말씀드립니다."], ["20083m", "언젠가는 이뤄질 일입니다.\""]], [["“And do you propose to make this journey very shortly?”", "\"그럼 그 여행을 머지않아 떠나실 생각이십니까?\""]], [["“I do not know; it depends on circumstances which depend on certain arrangements.”", "\"모르겠습니다. 이런저런 조정들에 달린 상황에 따라 정해질 일이지요.\""]], [["“I should like to be there at the time you come, and I will endeavor to repay you, as far as lies in my power, for your liberal hospitality displayed to me at Monte Cristo.”", "\"선생께서 오실 때 저도 그곳에 있고 싶습니다. 몬테크리스토에서 베풀어 주신 후한 환대에, 제 힘닿는 데까지 보답하도록 애써 보겠습니다.\""]], [["“I should avail myself of your offer with pleasure,”", "\"기꺼이 선생의 제안을 받아들이고 싶군요.\""], ["replied the host, “but, unfortunately, if I go there, it will be, in all probability, _incognito_.”", "주인이 대답했다. \"하지만 안타깝게도, 제가 그곳에 간다면 십중팔구 익명으로 갈 겁니다.\""]], [["The supper appeared to have been supplied solely for Franz, for the unknown scarcely touched one or two dishes of the splendid banquet to which his guest did ample justice.", "저녁상은 오로지 프란츠를 위해 차려진 것처럼 보였다. 낯선 사내는 손님이 흠뻑 즐긴 그 호화로운 만찬에서 요리 한두 가지를 겨우 건드릴 뿐이었다."], ["Then Ali brought on the dessert, or rather took the baskets from the hands of the statues and placed them on the table.", "이윽고 알리가 후식을 들여왔다. 아니, 차라리 조각상들의 손에서 바구니를 내려 식탁 위에 놓았다고 해야겠다."], ["Between the two baskets he placed a small silver cup with a silver cover.", "그리고 두 바구니 사이에 은 뚜껑이 덮인 작은 은잔 하나를 놓았다."], ["The care with which Ali placed this cup on the table roused Franz’s curiosity.", "알리가 이 잔을 식탁에 놓는 정성스러운 손길에 프란츠의 호기심이 일었다."], ["He raised the cover and saw a kind of greenish paste, something like preserved angelica, but which was perfectly unknown to him.", "그가 뚜껑을 들춰 보니, 절인 안젤리카 비슷한 푸르스름한 반죽 같은 것이 들어 있었다. 그러나 그로서는 도무지 알 수 없는 것이었다."], ["He replaced the lid, as ignorant of what the cup contained as he was before he had looked at it, and then casting his eyes towards his host he saw him smile at his disappointment.", "그는 들여다보기 전과 마찬가지로 잔 속에 든 것이 무엇인지 모른 채 뚜껑을 다시 덮었다. 그러고는 주인 쪽으로 눈길을 던지니, 주인이 그의 실망스러운 표정을 보고 미소 짓고 있었다."]], [["“You cannot guess,”", "\"맞혀 보실 수 없겠지요,\""], ["said he, “what there is in that small vase, can you?”", "그가 말했다. \"저 작은 단지 안에 무엇이 들었는지 말입니다.\""]], [["“No, I really cannot.”", "\"네, 정말 모르겠습니다.\""]], [["“Well, then, that green preserve is nothing less than the ambrosia which Hebe served at the table of Jupiter.”", "\"음, 그렇다면, 저 초록빛 보존식이 바로 헤베가 유피테르의 식탁에서 시중들던 신들의 음식, 암브로시아랍니다.\""]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\" 프란츠가 대답했다."], ["replied Franz, “this ambrosia, no doubt, in passing through mortal hands has lost its heavenly appellation and assumed a human name; in vulgar phrase, what may you term this composition, for which, to tell the truth, I do not feel any particular desire?”", "\"이 암브로시아라는 것도, 분명 인간의 손을 거치는 동안 그 천상의 이름을 잃고 인간의 이름을 얻었겠지요. 속된 말로, 이 혼합물을 뭐라고 부르십니까? 솔직히 말씀드리면, 그다지 끌리지는 않습니다만.\""]], [["“Ah, thus it is that our material origin is revealed,”", "\"아, 바로 그렇게 우리의 물질적인 뿌리가 드러나는 겁니다.\""], ["cried Sinbad; “we frequently pass so near to happiness without seeing, without regarding it, or if we do see and regard it, yet without recognizing it.", "신드바드가 외쳤다. \"우리는 흔히 행복 곁을 그토록 가까이 지나치면서도 그것을 보지 못하고, 헤아리지 못합니다. 설령 보고 헤아린다 해도 알아보지 못하지요."], ["Are you a man for the substantials, and is gold your god?", "선생은 실속을 따지는 분이십니까, 황금이 선생의 신입니까?"], ["taste this, and the mines of Peru, Guzerat, and Golconda are opened to you.", "이것을 맛보십시오, 그러면 페루와 구자라트와 골콘다의 광맥이 선생께 열립니다."], ["Are you a man of imagination—a poet?", "선생은 상상력의 사람, 시인이십니까?"], ["taste this, and the boundaries of possibility disappear; the fields of infinite space open to you, you advance free in heart, free in mind, into the boundless realms of unfettered reverie.", "이것을 맛보십시오, 그러면 가능성의 경계가 사라지고, 무한한 공간의 들판이 선생께 펼쳐집니다. 선생은 마음도 자유로이, 정신도 자유로이, 거침없는 몽상의 끝없는 영역으로 나아가게 되지요."], ["Are you ambitious, and do you seek after the greatnesses of the earth?", "선생은 야심가이십니까, 이 땅의 위대함을 좇으십니까?"], ["taste this, and in an hour you will be a king, not a king of a petty kingdom hidden in some corner of Europe like France, Spain, or England, but king of the world, king of the universe, king of creation; without bowing at the feet of Satan, you will be king and master of all the kingdoms of the earth.", "이것을 맛보십시오, 그러면 한 시간 안에 선생은 왕이 됩니다. 프랑스나 스페인, 영국처럼 유럽 한구석에 숨은 보잘것없는 왕국의 왕이 아니라, 세상의 왕, 우주의 왕, 만물의 왕이 되는 겁니다. 사탄의 발치에 굽실거리지 않고도, 선생은 이 땅의 모든 왕국의 왕이자 주인이 되는 거지요."], ["Is it not tempting what I offer you, and is it not an easy thing, since it is only to do thus?", "제가 선생께 권하는 것이 솔깃하지 않습니까? 게다가 이렇게 하기만 하면 되니, 쉬운 일이 아닙니까?"], ["look!”", "보십시오!\""]], [["At these words he uncovered the small cup which contained the substance so lauded, took a teaspoonful of the magic sweetmeat, raised it to his lips, and swallowed it slowly with his eyes half shut and his head bent backwards.", "이 말과 함께 그는 그토록 찬양해 마지않던 그 물질이 담긴 작은 잔의 뚜껑을 벗기고, 그 마법의 단것을 한 찻숟갈 떠서 입술로 가져가더니, 눈을 반쯤 감고 고개를 뒤로 젖힌 채 천천히 삼켰다."], ["Franz did not disturb him whilst he absorbed his favorite sweetmeat, but when he had finished, he inquired:", "프란츠는 그가 좋아하는 단것을 음미하는 동안 그를 방해하지 않았으나, 그가 다 삼키고 나자 물었다."]], [["“What, then, is this precious stuff?”", "\"그래, 이 귀한 물건이 대체 무엇입니까?\""]], [["“Did you ever hear,”", "\"혹시 들어 보셨습니까,\""], ["he replied, “of the Old Man of the Mountain, who attempted to assassinate Philippe Auguste?”", "그가 대답했다. \"필리프 오귀스트를 암살하려 했던 산상 노인의 이야기를요?\""]], [["“Of course I have.”", "\"물론 들어 봤지요.\""]], [["“Well, you know he reigned over a rich valley which was overhung by the mountain whence he derived his picturesque name.", "\"음, 아시겠지만 그는 풍요로운 골짜기를 다스렸는데, 그 골짜기는 그에게 그림 같은 이름을 안겨 준 산이 굽어보고 있었지요."], ["In this valley were magnificent gardens planted by Hassen-ben-Sabah, and in these gardens isolated pavilions.", "이 골짜기에는 하산벤사바가 가꾼 웅장한 정원들이 있었고, 그 정원들에는 외딴 누각들이 있었습니다."], ["Into these pavilions he admitted the elect, and there, says Marco Polo, gave them to eat a certain herb, which transported them to Paradise, in the midst of ever-blooming shrubs, ever-ripe fruit, and ever-lovely virgins.", "마르코 폴로의 말에 따르면, 그는 이 누각들로 선택받은 자들을 들여, 거기서 그들에게 어떤 풀을 먹였다고 합니다. 그 풀이 그들을 낙원으로, 영원히 꽃피는 관목과 영원히 익은 과일과 영원히 어여쁜 처녀들 한가운데로 데려다주었다지요."], ["What these happy persons took for reality was but a dream; but it was a dream so soft, so voluptuous, so enthralling, that they sold themselves body and soul to him who gave it to them, and obedient to his orders as to those of a deity, struck down the designated victim, died in torture without a murmur, believing that the death they underwent was but a quick transition to that life of delights of which the holy herb, now before you, had given them a slight foretaste.”", "이 행복한 자들이 현실로 여긴 것은 한낱 꿈이었습니다. 그러나 그것은 어찌나 달콤하고, 어찌나 관능적이며, 어찌나 사람을 사로잡는 꿈이었던지, 그들은 그것을 준 자에게 몸과 영혼을 팔아넘기고, 그의 명령을 신의 명령처럼 받들어, 지목된 희생자를 베어 넘기고, 신음 한마디 없이 고문 속에 죽어 갔습니다. 자기들이 겪는 죽음이란 그저, 지금 선생 앞에 놓인 저 거룩한 풀이 살짝 맛보여 준 그 환락의 삶으로 건너가는 빠른 길목일 뿐이라 믿으면서 말이지요.\""]], [["“Then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["cried Franz, “it is hashish!", "프란츠가 외쳤다. \"이건 해시시로군요!"], ["I know that—by name at least.”", "적어도 이름만은 압니다.\""]], [["“That is it precisely, Signor Aladdin; it is hashish—the purest and most unadulterated hashish of Alexandria,—the hashish of Abou-Gor, the celebrated maker, the only man, the man to whom there should be built a palace, inscribed with these words, _A grateful world to the dealer in happiness_.”", "\"바로 그렇습니다, 알라딘 나리. 이건 해시시입니다. 알렉산드리아의 가장 순수하고 가장 섞임 없는 해시시, 저 유명한 제조가 아부고르의 해시시지요. 그에게는 궁전을 지어 이런 글귀를 새겨 마땅한, 그런 유일한 사람입니다. '행복을 파는 자에게, 고마운 세상이 바친다.'\""]], [["“Do you know,”", "\"이보십시오,\""], ["said Franz, “I have a very great inclination to judge for myself of the truth or exaggeration of your eulogies.”", "프란츠가 말했다. \"선생의 그 찬사가 사실인지 과장인지, 제가 직접 판단해 보고 싶은 마음이 무척 큽니다.\""]], [["“Judge for yourself, Signor Aladdin—judge, but do not confine yourself to one trial.", "\"직접 판단해 보십시오, 알라딘 나리. 판단해 보시되, 한 번의 시도로 그치지는 마십시오."], ["Like everything else, we must habituate the senses to a fresh impression, gentle or violent, sad or joyous.", "다른 모든 것이 그렇듯, 우리는 감각을 새로운 인상에 — 그것이 부드럽든 거칠든, 슬프든 즐겁든 — 길들여야 하니까요."], ["There is a struggle in nature against this divine substance,—in nature which is not made for joy and clings to pain.", "자연 속에는 이 신성한 물질에 맞서는 싸움이 있습니다. 기쁨을 위해 만들어지지 않고 고통에 매달리는 자연 속에 말이지요."], ["Nature subdued must yield in the combat, the dream must succeed to reality, and then the dream reigns supreme, then the dream becomes life, and life becomes the dream.", "굴복한 자연은 그 싸움에서 물러설 수밖에 없고, 꿈이 현실을 대신하게 되며, 그러면 꿈이 더없이 군림합니다. 그러면 꿈이 삶이 되고, 삶이 꿈이 되지요."], ["But what changes occur!", "한데 어떤 변화가 일어나는지!"], ["It is only by comparing the pains of actual being with the joys of the assumed existence, that you would desire to live no longer, but to dream thus forever.", "오로지 실제 존재의 고통과 가상 존재의 기쁨을 견주어 봐야만, 선생은 더는 살고 싶지 않고 오직 영원히 그렇게 꿈꾸기만을 바라게 됩니다."], ["When you return to this mundane sphere from your visionary world, you would seem to leave a Neapolitan spring for a Lapland winter—to quit paradise for earth—heaven for hell!", "그 환상의 세계에서 이 속세로 돌아올 때면, 선생은 마치 나폴리의 봄을 떠나 라플란드의 겨울로, 낙원을 떠나 땅으로, 천국을 떠나 지옥으로 들어서는 듯할 겁니다."], ["Taste the hashish, guest of mine—taste the hashish.”", "해시시를 맛보십시오, 손님이여, 해시시를 맛보십시오.\""]], [["Franz’s only reply was to take a teaspoonful of the marvellous preparation, about as much in quantity as his host had eaten, and lift it to his mouth.", "프란츠의 유일한 대답은, 주인이 먹은 것과 거의 같은 양으로 그 경이로운 조제품을 한 찻숟갈 떠서 입으로 가져가는 것이었다."]], [["“_Diable!_”", "\"제기랄!\""], ["he said, after having swallowed the divine preserve.", "그가 그 신성한 보존식을 삼킨 뒤에 말했다."], ["“I do not know if the result will be as agreeable as you describe, but the thing does not appear to me as palatable as you say.”", "\"결과가 선생 말씀처럼 흡족할지는 모르겠지만, 적어도 맛은 선생 말씀처럼 좋아 보이진 않는군요.\""]], [["“Because your palate his not yet been attuned to the sublimity of the substances it flavors.", "\"선생의 입맛이 아직 그것이 맛을 내는 물질들의 숭고함에 길들지 않아서입니다."], ["Tell me, the first time you tasted oysters, tea, porter, truffles, and sundry other dainties which you now adore, did you like them?", "말씀해 보십시오, 굴이며 차며 흑맥주며 송로버섯이며, 지금은 그토록 좋아하시는 갖가지 별미를 처음 맛보셨을 때, 좋으셨습니까?"], ["Could you comprehend how the Romans stuffed their pheasants with assafœtida, and the Chinese eat swallows’ nests? Eh? no!", "로마인들이 꿩 속에 아위를 채워 넣고, 중국인들이 제비집을 먹는 것을 이해하실 수 있었습니까? 네? 못 하셨겠지요!"], ["Well, it is the same with hashish; only eat for a week, and nothing in the world will seem to you to equal the delicacy of its flavor, which now appears to you flat and distasteful.", "음, 해시시도 마찬가집니다. 일주일만 드셔 보십시오, 그러면 지금은 밋밋하고 역겹게 느껴지는 그 맛의 정묘함에 견줄 만한 것이 세상에 아무것도 없게 됩니다."], ["Let us now go into the adjoining chamber, which is your apartment, and Ali will bring us coffee and pipes.”", "자, 이제 옆방으로 갑시다. 그곳이 선생의 거처입니다. 알리가 커피와 담뱃대를 가져다줄 겁니다.\""]], [["They both arose, and while he who called himself Sinbad—and whom we have occasionally named so, that we might, like his guest, have some title by which to distinguish him—gave some orders to the servant, Franz entered still another apartment.", "두 사람은 일어섰다. 그리고 스스로를 신드바드라 칭한 사내가 — 우리도 그의 손님처럼 그를 구별할 호칭이 있어야 했기에 이따금 그렇게 불러 온 그 사내가 — 하인에게 몇 가지 분부를 내리는 동안, 프란츠는 또 다른 방으로 들어섰다."]], [["It was simply yet richly furnished.", "그곳은 수수하면서도 호화롭게 꾸며져 있었다."], ["It was round, and a large divan completely encircled it.", "둥근 방이었고, 커다란 긴 의자가 빙 둘러 놓여 있었다."], ["Divan, walls, ceiling, floor, were all covered with magnificent skins as soft and downy as the richest carpets; there were heavy-maned lion-skins from Atlas, striped tiger-skins from Bengal; panther-skins from the Cape, spotted beautifully, like those that appeared to Dante; bear-skins from Siberia, fox-skins from Norway, and so on; and all these skins were strewn in profusion one on the other, so that it seemed like walking over the most mossy turf, or reclining on the most luxurious bed.", "긴 의자며 벽이며 천장이며 바닥이 죄다 더없이 값진 양탄자처럼 부드럽고 보드라운 화려한 짐승 가죽으로 덮여 있었다. 아틀라스산 갈기 무성한 사자 가죽, 벵골산 줄무늬 호랑이 가죽, 단테의 눈앞에 나타났던 것처럼 아름다운 반점이 박힌 케이프산 표범 가죽, 시베리아산 곰 가죽, 노르웨이산 여우 가죽, 그 밖에도 갖가지 가죽이 있었다. 이 모든 가죽이 겹겹이 수북하게 깔려 있어, 마치 더없이 이끼 낀 풀밭 위를 걷거나 더없이 호사스러운 침대에 기대 눕는 것 같았다."]], [["Both laid themselves down on the divan; chibouques with jasmine tubes and amber mouthpieces were within reach, and all prepared so that there was no need to smoke the same pipe twice.", "두 사람 모두 긴 의자에 몸을 뉘었다. 손이 닿는 곳에는 재스민 대롱과 호박 물부리가 달린 치부크가 놓여 있었는데, 같은 담뱃대를 두 번 피울 필요가 없도록 모두 준비되어 있었다."], ["Each of them took one, which Ali lighted and then retired to prepare the coffee.", "둘은 저마다 하나씩 집어 들었고, 알리가 불을 붙여 준 뒤 커피를 준비하러 물러갔다."]], [["There was a moment’s silence, during which Sinbad gave himself up to thoughts that seemed to occupy him incessantly, even in the midst of his conversation; and Franz abandoned himself to that mute reverie, into which we always sink when smoking excellent tobacco, which seems to remove with its fume all the troubles of the mind, and to give the smoker in exchange all the visions of the soul.", "한순간 침묵이 흘렀다. 그동안 신드바드는, 이야기를 나누는 와중에도 끊임없이 그를 사로잡는 듯한 상념에 잠겼다. 그리고 프란츠는, 훌륭한 담배를 피울 때면 늘 빠져드는 그 말 없는 몽상에 자신을 내맡겼다."], ["Ali brought in the coffee.", "그 담배는 그 연기로 마음의 온갖 시름을 거두어 가고, 그 대신 영혼의 온갖 환영을 피우는 이에게 건네주는 듯했다. 알리가 커피를 들여왔다."]], [["“How do you take it?”", "\"어떻게 드시겠습니까?\""], ["inquired the unknown; “in the French or Turkish style, strong or weak, sugar or none, cool or boiling?", "낯선 사내가 물었다. \"프랑스식으로, 아니면 터키식으로, 진하게 아니면 묽게, 설탕을 넣을까요 말까요, 식혀서 아니면 끓는 채로?"], ["As you please; it is ready in all ways.”", "좋으실 대로 하십시오. 어떻게든 준비되어 있으니까요.\""]], [["“I will take it in the Turkish style,” replied Franz.", "\"터키식으로 들겠습니다.\" 프란츠가 대답했다."]], [["“And you are right,”", "\"잘 고르셨습니다.\" 주인이 말했다."], ["said his host; “it shows you have a tendency for an Oriental life.", "\"선생께 동방의 삶에 기우는 기질이 있다는 걸 보여 주는군요. 아, 그 동방 사람들이라니. 사는 법을 아는 건 그들뿐입니다."], ["Ah, those Orientals; they are the only men who know how to live. As for me,”", "저로 말하면,\" 그가 젊은이의 눈을 피해 가지 못한 그 묘한 미소를 또 한 번 지으며 덧붙였다."], ["he added, with one of those singular smiles which did not escape the young man, “when I have completed my affairs in Paris, I shall go and die in the East; and should you wish to see me again, you must seek me at Cairo, Bagdad, or Ispahan.” 20087m", "\"파리에서 제 일을 마치고 나면, 동방으로 가서 죽을 겁니다. 그러니 저를 다시 보고 싶으시거든, 카이로나 바그다드나 이스파한에서 찾으셔야 할 겁니다.\""]], [["“_Ma foi_,”", "\"맹세코,\""], ["said Franz, “it would be the easiest thing in the world; for I feel eagle’s wings springing out at my shoulders, and with those wings I could make a tour of the world in four-and-twenty hours.”", "프란츠가 말했다. \"그건 세상에서 가장 쉬운 일이겠군요. 제 어깨에서 독수리의 날개가 돋아나는 것 같으니, 그 날개로 스물네 시간 만에 세계를 한 바퀴 돌 수 있을 테니까요.\""]], [["“Ah, yes, the hashish is beginning its work.", "\"아, 그렇지요, 해시시가 효력을 부리기 시작하는 겁니다."], ["Well, unfurl your wings, and fly into superhuman regions; fear nothing, there is a watch over you; and if your wings, like those of Icarus, melt before the sun, we are here to ease your fall.”", "자, 날개를 펼치고 인간을 넘어선 세계로 날아오르십시오. 아무것도 두려워 마십시오, 선생을 지켜보는 이가 있으니까요. 그리고 선생의 날개가 이카로스의 날개처럼 태양 앞에서 녹아내린다 해도, 우리가 여기서 선생의 추락을 가벼이 받아 드리겠습니다.\""]], [["He then said something in Arabic to Ali, who made a sign of obedience and withdrew, but not to any distance.", "그러고는 그가 알리에게 아라비아어로 무언가를 말했고, 알리는 복종의 표시를 하고 물러갔다. 그러나 멀리 가지는 않았다."]], [["As to Franz a strange transformation had taken place in him. All the bodily fatigue of the day, all the preoccupation of mind which the events of the evening had brought on, disappeared as they do at the first approach of sleep, when we are still sufficiently conscious to be aware of the coming of slumber. His body seemed to acquire an airy lightness, his perception brightened in a remarkable manner, his senses seemed to redouble their power, the horizon continued to expand; but it was not the gloomy horizon of vague alarms, and which he had seen before he slept, but a blue, transparent, unbounded horizon, with all the blue of the ocean, all the spangles of the sun, all the perfumes of the summer breeze; then, in the midst of the songs of his sailors,—songs so clear and sonorous, that they would have made a divine harmony had their notes been taken down,—he saw the Island of Monte Cristo, no longer as a threatening rock in the midst of the waves, but as an oasis in the desert; then, as his boat drew nearer, the songs became louder, for an enchanting and mysterious harmony rose to heaven, as if some Loreley had decreed to attract a soul thither, or Amphion, the enchanter, intended there to build a city.", "프란츠에게는 기이한 변화가 일어났다. 하루 동안의 온갖 육체적 피로와, 저녁의 사건들이 가져온 온갖 마음속 근심이, 잠이 처음 다가올 때처럼 사라져 버렸다. 아직 잠이 오는 것을 알아챌 만큼은 의식이 또렷한 그 순간처럼 말이다. 그의 몸은 공기처럼 가벼워지는 듯했고, 지각은 놀랍도록 또렷해졌으며, 감각은 그 힘이 갑절로 늘어나는 것 같았고, 지평선은 계속 넓어져 갔다. 그러나 그것은 잠들기 전에 보았던, 막연한 두려움으로 가득한 음울한 지평선이 아니었다. 그것은 푸르고 투명하며 끝없는 지평선이었다. 바다의 온갖 푸름과, 태양의 온갖 반짝임과, 여름 산들바람의 온갖 향기를 머금은 지평선이었다. 그러다 자기 선원들의 노랫소리 한가운데서 — 그 음을 받아 적기만 했더라면 신성한 화음이 되었을 만큼 맑고 낭랑한 그 노랫소리 한가운데서 — 그는 몬테크리스토섬을 보았다. 이제는 물결 한가운데 위협적으로 솟은 바위가 아니라, 사막 가운데의 오아시스 같은 섬이었다. 그러다 배가 가까이 다가가자 노랫소리는 더욱 커졌으니, 황홀하고 신비로운 화음이 하늘로 솟아올랐던 것이다. 마치 어떤 로렐라이가 영혼을 그곳으로 끌어들이기로 작정한 듯, 혹은 마법사 암피온이 거기에 도시를 세우려는 듯."]], [["At length the boat touched the shore, but without effort, without shock, as lips touch lips; and he entered the grotto amidst continued strains of most delicious melody. He descended, or rather seemed to descend, several steps, inhaling the fresh and balmy air, like that which may be supposed to reign around the grotto of Circe, formed from such perfumes as set the mind a-dreaming, and such fires as burn the very senses; and he saw again all he had seen before his sleep, from Sinbad, his singular host, to Ali, the mute attendant; then all seemed to fade away and become confused before his eyes, like the last shadows of the magic lantern before it is extinguished, and he was again in the chamber of statues, lighted only by one of those pale and antique lamps which watch in the dead of the night over the sleep of pleasure.", "이윽고 배는 닿았다. 그러나 애쓰지도, 부딪히지도 않고, 마치 입술이 입술에 닿듯 그렇게 닿았다. 그리고 그는 더없이 감미로운 가락이 끊임없이 흐르는 가운데 동굴로 들어섰다. 그는 신선하고 향긋한 공기를 들이마시며 몇 계단을 내려갔다. 아니, 내려가는 것 같았다. 마음을 몽상에 잠기게 하는 향기와 감각마저 태워 버리는 불꽃으로 이루어진, 키르케의 동굴 둘레에 감돌 법한 그런 공기였다. 그리고 그는 잠들기 전에 보았던 모든 것을 다시 보았다. 별난 주인 신드바드부터 말 못 하는 시중꾼 알리까지. 그러다 모든 것이 눈앞에서 차츰 흐려지고 뒤엉키더니, 마치 꺼지기 직전의 환등기에 비친 마지막 그림자들처럼 되었고, 그는 다시 조각상들의 방에 와 있었다. 죽은 듯한 한밤중에 쾌락의 잠을 지키는, 그 창백하고 고풍스러운 등불 가운데 하나만이 방을 밝히고 있었다."]], [["They were the same statues, rich in form, in attraction, and poesy, with eyes of fascination, smiles of love, and bright and flowing hair.", "그것은 같은 조각상들이었다. 형태와 매혹과 시정이 넘치고, 사람을 홀리는 눈과 사랑의 미소와 빛나며 흘러내리는 머리채를 지닌 조각상들."], ["They were Phryne, Cleopatra, Messalina, those three celebrated courtesans.", "그것은 프리네, 클레오파트라, 메살리나, 저 유명한 세 명의 고급 창부였다."], ["Then among them glided like a pure ray, like a Christian angel in the midst of Olympus, one of those chaste figures, those calm shadows, those soft visions, which seemed to veil its virgin brow before these marble wantons.", "그러다 그들 사이로, 한 줄기 순수한 빛처럼, 올림포스 한가운데의 그리스도교 천사처럼, 정결한 자태 하나가 미끄러져 들어왔다. 그 고요한 그림자, 그 부드러운 환영은, 이 대리석 탕녀들 앞에서 제 처녀다운 이마를 가리려는 듯했다."]], [["Then the three statues advanced towards him with looks of love, and approached the couch on which he was reposing, their feet hidden in their long white tunics, their throats bare, hair flowing like waves, and assuming attitudes which the gods could not resist, but which saints withstood, and looks inflexible and ardent like those with which the serpent charms the bird; and then he gave way before looks that held him in a torturing grasp and delighted his senses as with a voluptuous kiss.", "이윽고 세 조각상이 사랑의 눈길을 띠고 그를 향해 다가와, 그가 쉬고 있는 긴 의자로 다가왔다. 발은 긴 흰 튜닉 속에 감추고, 목은 드러내고, 머리채는 물결처럼 흘러내린 채로. 그들은 신들도 거역할 수 없으나 성인들은 견뎌 낸 그런 자태를 취했고, 뱀이 새를 홀릴 때의 그것처럼 굽힐 줄 모르는 뜨거운 눈길을 보냈다. 그러자 그는, 그를 고문하듯 옭아매면서도 관능적인 입맞춤처럼 그의 감각을 황홀케 하는 그 눈길 앞에 무너졌다."]], [["It seemed to Franz that he closed his eyes, and in a last look about him saw the vision of modesty completely veiled; and then followed a dream of passion like that promised by the Prophet to the elect.", "프란츠는 눈을 감은 것 같았고, 마지막으로 사방을 둘러보다가 정결의 환영이 완전히 가려지는 것을 보았다. 그리고 예언자가 선택받은 자들에게 약속한 것 같은 정념의 꿈이 뒤따랐다."], ["Lips of stone turned to flame, breasts of ice became like heated lava, so that to Franz, yielding for the first time to the sway of the drug, love was a sorrow and voluptuousness a torture, as burning mouths were pressed to his thirsty lips, and he was held in cool serpent-like embraces.", "돌의 입술이 불꽃으로 바뀌고, 얼음의 가슴이 뜨거운 용암처럼 되었으니, 처음으로 그 약물의 지배에 굴복한 프란츠에게 사랑은 슬픔이요 관능은 고문이었다. 불타는 입들이 그의 목마른 입술에 짓눌리고, 그는 뱀처럼 서늘한 포옹에 휘감겼다."], ["The more he strove against this unhallowed passion the more his senses yielded to its thrall, and at length, weary of a struggle that taxed his very soul, he gave way and sank back breathless and exhausted beneath the kisses of these marble goddesses, and the enchantment of his marvellous dream.", "이 부정한 정념에 맞서 몸부림칠수록 그의 감각은 더욱 그 굴레에 굴복해 들어갔고, 마침내 제 영혼마저 짜내는 그 싸움에 지친 그는, 무너져 내려, 이 대리석 여신들의 입맞춤과 그 경이로운 꿈의 마법 아래 숨을 헐떡이며 기진맥진한 채 쓰러졌다."]], [["# Chapter 32. The Waking", "# Chapter 32. The Waking"]], [["When Franz returned to himself, he seemed still to be in a dream.", "프란츠가 정신을 차렸을 때, 그는 여전히 꿈속에 있는 듯했다."], ["He thought himself in a sepulchre, into which a ray of sunlight in pity scarcely penetrated.", "자신이 무덤 속에 있다고, 그 무덤에는 햇살 한 줄기가 가엾이 여겨 겨우 스며들 뿐이라고 여겼다."], ["He stretched forth his hand, and touched stone; he rose to his seat, and found himself lying on his bournous in a bed of dry heather, very soft and odoriferous.", "그는 손을 뻗어 돌을 만졌다. 몸을 일으켜 앉으니, 자신이 부르누스를 깔고 마른 히스 풀로 된 잠자리에 누워 있었다. 그 잠자리는 무척 부드럽고 향기로웠다."], ["The vision had fled; and as if the statues had been but shadows from the tomb, they had vanished at his waking.", "환영은 사라져 버렸다. 마치 조각상들이 무덤에서 나온 그림자에 지나지 않았던 듯, 그가 깨어남과 동시에 자취를 감추고 만 것이었다."]], [["He advanced several paces towards the point whence the light came, and to all the excitement of his dream succeeded the calmness of reality.", "그는 빛이 들어오는 쪽으로 몇 걸음 나아갔다. 꿈이 안겨 준 온갖 흥분이 가시고 그 자리에 현실의 고요함이 들어섰다."], ["He found that he was in a grotto, went towards the opening, and through a kind of fanlight saw a blue sea and an azure sky.", "그는 자신이 동굴 안에 있다는 것을 알아차렸다. 입구 쪽으로 다가가니, 부채꼴 채광창 같은 틈으로 푸른 바다와 쪽빛 하늘이 보였다."], ["The air and water were shining in the beams of the morning sun; on the shore the sailors were sitting, chatting and laughing; and at ten yards from them the boat was at anchor, undulating gracefully on the water.", "공기와 물결이 아침 햇살 속에서 반짝였다. 물가에는 선원들이 앉아 잡담을 나누며 웃고 있었고, 그들에게서 열 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳에는 작은 배가 닻을 내린 채 물 위에서 우아하게 일렁이고 있었다."]], [["There for some time he enjoyed the fresh breeze which played on his brow, and listened to the dash of the waves on the beach, that left against the rocks a lace of foam as white as silver.", "그는 한동안 이마를 스치는 산뜻한 바닷바람을 즐겼고, 모래밭에 부딪치는 파도 소리에 귀를 기울였다. 그 파도는 바위에 은처럼 새하얀 거품 레이스를 남겨 놓았다."], ["He was for some time without reflection or thought for the divine charm which is in the things of nature, specially after a fantastic dream; then gradually this view of the outer world, so calm, so pure, so grand, reminded him of the illusiveness of his vision, and once more awakened memory.", "한동안 그는 자연 속에 깃든 신성한 매혹을, 특히 환상적인 꿈을 꾼 뒤의 그 매혹을 아무 생각 없이 느꼈다. 그러다 차츰, 이토록 고요하고 맑고 웅장한 바깥세상의 풍경이 그에게 자신이 본 환영의 헛됨을 일깨웠고, 다시 한번 기억을 되살려 주었다."], ["He recalled his arrival on the island, his presentation to a smuggler chief, a subterranean palace full of splendor, an excellent supper, and a spoonful of hashish.", "그는 이 섬에 도착했던 일, 밀수업자 두목에게 소개되었던 일, 호화로움이 넘치던 지하 궁전, 더없이 훌륭한 만찬, 그리고 한 숟갈의 대마를 떠올렸다."]], [["It seemed, however, even in the very face of open day, that at least a year had elapsed since all these things had passed, so deep was the impression made in his mind by the dream, and so strong a hold had it taken of his imagination.", "그러나 환한 대낮인데도, 이 모든 일이 일어난 지 적어도 일 년은 흐른 것만 같았다. 꿈이 그의 마음에 남긴 인상이 그토록 깊었고, 그의 상상력을 그토록 강하게 사로잡고 있었던 것이다."], ["Thus every now and then he saw in fancy amid the sailors, seated on a rock, or undulating in the vessel, one of the shadows which had shared his dream with looks and kisses.", "그래서 이따금 그는 선원들 사이에서, 바위에 앉아 있는 어떤 형상이나 배 위에서 일렁이는 어떤 그림자에서, 눈길과 입맞춤으로 자신의 꿈을 함께했던 그림자 가운데 하나를 본 듯한 착각에 빠졌다."], ["Otherwise, his head was perfectly clear, and his body refreshed; he was free from the slightest headache; on the contrary, he felt a certain degree of lightness, a faculty for absorbing the pure air, and enjoying the bright sunshine more vividly than ever.", "그 점만 빼면 그의 머리는 더없이 맑았고 몸은 개운했다. 두통이라곤 조금도 없었다. 오히려 어떤 가벼움이, 맑은 공기를 들이마시고 밝은 햇살을 그 어느 때보다 생생하게 즐길 수 있는 능력이 느껴졌다."]], [["He went gayly up to the sailors, who rose as soon as they perceived him; and the patron, accosting him, said:", "그는 활기차게 선원들에게 다가갔다. 선원들은 그를 보자마자 자리에서 일어섰다. 선장이 다가와 말했다."]], [["“The Signor Sinbad has left his compliments for your excellency, and desires us to express the regret he feels at not being able to take his leave in person; but he trusts you will excuse him, as very important business calls him to Malaga.”", "\"신드바드 나리께서 각하께 인사를 남기셨습니다. 직접 작별 인사를 드리지 못하는 것을 몹시 아쉬워하셨지요. 하지만 매우 중요한 용무가 있어 말라가로 가셔야 하니, 너그러이 양해해 주실 거라 믿는다고 하셨습니다.\""]], [["“So, then, Gaetano,”", "\"그렇다면 가에타노,\""], ["said Franz, “this is, then, all reality; there exists a man who has received me in this island, entertained me right royally, and has departed while I was asleep?”", "프란츠가 말했다. \"이게 다 정말 현실이란 말인가? 이 섬에서 나를 맞아 주고, 임금처럼 융숭히 대접해 주고는, 내가 잠든 사이에 떠나 버린 사람이 실제로 있다는 건가?\""]], [["“He exists as certainly as that you may see his small yacht with all her sails spread; and if you will use your glass, you will, in all probability, recognize your host in the midst of his crew.”", "\"각하께서 돛을 모두 펼친 저 작은 요트를 보실 수 있는 것만큼이나 분명히 실재하는 분입니다. 망원경을 써 보시면, 십중팔구 선원들 가운데서 그 주인장을 알아보실 수 있을 겁니다.\""]], [["So saying, Gaetano pointed in a direction in which a small vessel was making sail towards the southern point of Corsica.", "이렇게 말하며 가에타노는 작은 배 한 척이 코르시카 남단을 향해 돛을 올리고 나아가는 쪽을 가리켰다."], ["Franz adjusted his telescope, and directed it towards the yacht.", "프란츠는 망원경의 초점을 맞춰 요트 쪽으로 겨누었다."], ["Gaetano was not mistaken. At the stern the mysterious stranger was standing up looking towards the shore, and holding a spy-glass in his hand.", "가에타노의 말은 틀리지 않았다. 고물에는 그 신비로운 이방인이 일어선 채 물가 쪽을 바라보며 손에 망원경을 들고 있었다."], ["He was attired as he had been on the previous evening, and waved his pocket-handkerchief to his guest in token of adieu.", "그는 전날 저녁과 똑같은 차림이었고, 손수건을 흔들어 손님에게 작별을 알렸다."], ["Franz returned the salute by shaking his handkerchief as an exchange of signals.", "프란츠도 신호를 주고받듯 손수건을 흔들어 답례했다."], ["After a second, a slight cloud of smoke was seen at the stern of the vessel, which rose gracefully as it expanded in the air, and then Franz heard a slight report.", "잠시 후, 배의 고물에서 옅은 연기구름이 일더니 공중으로 퍼지며 우아하게 솟아올랐다. 이어 프란츠는 가벼운 포성을 들었다."]], [["“There, do you hear?”", "\"자, 들리십니까?\""], ["observed Gaetano; “he is bidding you adieu.”", "가에타노가 말했다. \"각하께 작별을 고하시는 겁니다.\""]], [["The young man took his carbine and fired it in the air, but without any idea that the noise could be heard at the distance which separated the yacht from the shore.", "청년은 카빈총을 집어 들어 공중에 발사했다. 요트와 물가를 갈라놓은 거리만큼 멀리까지 그 소리가 들릴 거라는 생각은 없었지만 말이다."]], [["“What are your excellency’s orders?”", "\"각하, 분부하실 일이 있으십니까?\""], ["inquired Gaetano.", "가에타노가 물었다."]], [["“In the first place, light me a torch.”", "\"우선, 횃불을 하나 밝혀 주게.\""]], [["“Ah, yes, I understand,”", "\"아, 그렇지요, 알겠습니다.\""], ["replied the patron, “to find the entrance to the enchanted apartment.", "선장이 대답했다. \"그 마법의 거처로 들어가는 입구를 찾으시려는 거군요."], ["With much pleasure, your excellency, if it would amuse you; and I will get you the torch you ask for.", "재미 삼아 그러시려는 거라면 기꺼이 도와드리겠습니다, 각하. 청하신 횃불을 가져다 드리지요."], ["But I too have had the idea you have, and two or three times the same fancy has come over me; but I have always given it up.", "사실 저도 각하와 똑같은 생각을 한 적이 있습니다. 두세 번쯤 같은 충동에 사로잡혔지요. 하지만 그때마다 단념했습니다."], ["Giovanni, light a torch,”", "조반니, 횃불을 밝혀라.\""], ["he added, “and give it to his excellency.”", "그가 덧붙였다. \"그래서 각하께 드려라.\""]], [["Giovanni obeyed. Franz took the lamp, and entered the subterranean grotto, followed by Gaetano.", "조반니가 시키는 대로 했다. 프란츠는 등불을 받아 들고 지하 동굴로 들어섰고, 가에타노가 뒤를 따랐다."], ["He recognized the place where he had awaked by the bed of heather that was there; but it was in vain that he carried his torch all round the exterior surface of the grotto.", "그는 거기 놓인 히스 풀 잠자리를 보고 자신이 깨어났던 자리를 알아보았다. 하지만 횃불을 들고 동굴 바깥쪽 표면을 아무리 샅샅이 비춰 봐도 헛수고였다."], ["He saw nothing, unless that, by traces of smoke, others had before him attempted the same thing, and, like him, in vain.", "보이는 것이라곤, 연기 그을린 자국으로 미루어 자기보다 앞서 누군가가 같은 시도를 했고 자기처럼 헛수고했다는 사실뿐이었다."], ["Yet he did not leave a foot of this granite wall, as impenetrable as futurity, without strict scrutiny; he did not see a fissure without introducing the blade of his hunting sword into it, or a projecting point on which he did not lean and press in the hopes it would give way.", "그래도 그는 미래만큼이나 뚫고 들 수 없는 그 화강암 벽을, 한 자 한 자 빠짐없이 면밀히 살폈다. 갈라진 틈이 보이면 사냥칼 칼날을 찔러 넣어 보았고, 튀어나온 모서리가 보이면 무너지기를 바라며 기대어 눌러 보았다."], ["All was vain; and he lost two hours in his attempts, which were at last utterly useless.", "모두 헛수고였다. 그는 그렇게 두 시간을 허비했지만, 결국 아무 소용이 없었다."], ["At the end of this time he gave up his search, and Gaetano smiled.", "그 끝에 그는 탐색을 단념했고, 가에타노는 빙그레 웃었다."]], [["When Franz appeared again on the shore, the yacht only seemed like a small white speck on the horizon.", "프란츠가 다시 물가에 모습을 드러냈을 때, 요트는 수평선 위의 작고 하얀 점 하나로밖에 보이지 않았다."], ["He looked again through his glass, but even then he could not distinguish anything.", "그는 다시 망원경으로 들여다보았지만, 그때조차 아무것도 분간할 수 없었다."]], [["Gaetano reminded him that he had come for the purpose of shooting goats, which he had utterly forgotten.", "가에타노는 그가 염소를 사냥하러 왔다는 사실을 일깨워 주었다. 그는 그 일을 까맣게 잊고 있었다."], ["He took his fowling-piece, and began to hunt over the island with the air of a man who is fulfilling a duty, rather than enjoying a pleasure; and at the end of a quarter of an hour he had killed a goat and two kids.", "그는 엽총을 집어 들고, 즐거움을 누리는 사람이라기보다 의무를 다하는 사람 같은 태도로 섬 곳곳을 누비며 사냥에 나섰다. 십오 분쯤 지나자 그는 어미 염소 한 마리와 새끼 두 마리를 잡았다."], ["These animals, though wild and agile as chamois, were too much like domestic goats, and Franz could not consider them as game.", "이 짐승들은 영양처럼 사납고 날쌨지만, 집에서 기르는 염소와 너무 닮아 있어서 프란츠는 그것들을 사냥감으로 여길 수가 없었다."], ["Moreover, other ideas, much more enthralling, occupied his mind.", "게다가 훨씬 더 마음을 사로잡는 다른 생각들이 그의 머릿속을 차지하고 있었다."], ["Since, the evening before, he had really been the hero of one of the tales of the _Thousand and One Nights_, and he was irresistibly attracted towards the grotto.", "전날 저녁 이후로 그는 정말이지 「천일야화」 속 이야기의 주인공이 된 것 같았고, 그 동굴에 거역할 수 없이 이끌렸다."]], [["Then, in spite of the failure of his first search, he began a second, after having told Gaetano to roast one of the two kids.", "그래서 첫 탐색이 실패로 끝났는데도, 그는 가에타노에게 새끼 염소 두 마리 가운데 하나를 구워 달라고 일러두고 두 번째 탐색에 나섰다."], ["The second visit was a long one, and when he returned the kid was roasted and the repast ready.", "이 두 번째 탐색은 꽤 오래 걸렸고, 그가 돌아왔을 때는 새끼 염소가 다 구워져 식사가 차려져 있었다."], ["Franz was sitting on the spot where he was on the previous evening when his mysterious host had invited him to supper; and he saw the little yacht, now like a sea-gull on the wave, continuing her flight towards Corsica.", "프란츠는 전날 저녁, 그 신비로운 주인장이 만찬에 초대했던 바로 그 자리에 앉았다. 그리고 이제 파도 위의 갈매기처럼 보이는 작은 요트가 코르시카를 향해 계속 날아가는 것을 바라보았다."]], [["“Why,”", "\"이보게,\""], ["he remarked to Gaetano, “you told me that Signor Sinbad was going to Malaga, while it seems he is in the direction of Porto-Vecchio.”", "그가 가에타노에게 말했다. \"자네는 신드바드 나리가 말라가로 가신다고 했는데, 보아하니 포르토베키오 쪽으로 가시는 것 같군.\""]], [["“Don’t you remember,”", "\"기억 안 나십니까?\""], ["said the patron, “I told you that among the crew there were two Corsican brigands?”", "선장이 말했다. \"제가 그 선원들 가운데 코르시카 출신 산적이 둘 있다고 말씀드렸잖습니까.\""]], [["“True; and he is going to land them,”", "\"그렇지. 그러니까 그들을 내려 주려는 거로군.\""], ["added Franz.", "프란츠가 덧붙였다."]], [["“Precisely so,”", "\"바로 그렇습니다.\""], ["replied Gaetano.", "가에타노가 대답했다."], ["“Ah, he is one who fears neither God nor Satan, they say, and would at any time run fifty leagues out of his course to do a poor devil a service.”", "\"아, 그분으로 말할 것 같으면 하느님도 악마도 두려워하지 않는 분이라고들 하지요. 가엾은 사람 하나 돕자고 언제든 항로를 오십 리외나 벗어날 분입니다.\""]], [["20093m\n“But such services as these might involve him with the authorities of the country in which he practices this kind of philanthropy,” said Franz.", "\"하지만 그런 식의 선행을 베풀다가는, 그가 그런 박애를 펼치는 나라의 당국과 얽힐 수도 있겠군.\" 프란츠가 말했다."]], [["“And what cares he for that,”", "\"그분이 그런 걸 신경이나 쓰겠습니까?\""], ["replied Gaetano with a laugh, “or any authorities? He smiles at them.", "가에타노가 웃으며 대답했다. \"어느 나라 당국이든 코웃음을 치실 분입니다."], ["Let them try to pursue him!", "잡을 테면 잡아 보라지요!"], ["Why, in the first place, his yacht is not a ship, but a bird, and he would beat any frigate three knots in every nine; and if he were to throw himself on the coast, why, is he not certain of finding friends everywhere?”", "우선 그 요트는 배가 아니라 새거든요. 어떤 군함이든 9노트에 3노트는 따돌려 버릴 겁니다. 게다가 뭍에 올라야 한다 해도, 어디서든 도와줄 친구를 만나는 건 떼어 놓은 당상 아니겠습니까?\""]], [["It was perfectly clear that the Signor Sinbad, Franz’s host, had the honor of being on excellent terms with the smugglers and bandits along the whole coast of the Mediterranean, and so enjoyed exceptional privileges.", "프란츠의 주인장인 신드바드 나리가 지중해 연안 전역의 밀수업자며 산적들과 더없이 좋은 사이라는 영예를 누리고 있고, 그래서 남다른 특권을 누린다는 것은 더없이 분명했다."], ["As to Franz, he had no longer any inducement to remain at Monte Cristo.", "프란츠로 말하자면, 이제 더는 몬테크리스토섬에 머무를 이유가 없었다."], ["He had lost all hope of detecting the secret of the grotto; he consequently despatched his breakfast, and, his boat being ready, he hastened on board, and they were soon under way.", "그는 동굴의 비밀을 밝혀내겠다는 희망을 모조리 잃어버렸다. 그래서 아침 식사를 서둘러 마쳤고, 배가 준비되자 부랴부랴 올라탔다. 이윽고 배가 출발했다."], ["At the moment the boat began her course they lost sight of the yacht, as it disappeared in the gulf of Porto-Vecchio.", "배가 항로에 들어선 순간, 그들은 요트를 시야에서 놓쳤다. 요트가 포르토베키오만으로 사라져 버린 것이다."], ["With it was effaced the last trace of the preceding night; and then supper, Sinbad, hashish, statues,—all became a dream for Franz.", "그와 함께 지난밤의 마지막 흔적도 지워졌다. 그러자 만찬도, 신드바드도, 대마도, 조각상들도, 그 모든 것이 프란츠에게는 한낱 꿈이 되었다."]], [["The boat sailed on all day and all night, and next morning, when the sun rose, they had lost sight of Monte Cristo.", "배는 온종일, 밤새도록 나아갔고, 이튿날 아침 해가 떠올랐을 때는 몬테크리스토섬을 시야에서 놓친 뒤였다."]], [["When Franz had once again set foot on shore, he forgot, for the moment at least, the events which had just passed, while he finished his affairs of pleasure at Florence, and then thought of nothing but how he should rejoin his companion, who was awaiting him at Rome.", "프란츠는 다시 한번 뭍에 발을 디디자, 적어도 그 순간만큼은 방금 겪은 일들을 잊었다. 그는 피렌체에서 즐기던 일들을 마무리지었고, 그런 뒤로는 로마에서 자신을 기다리고 있는 동행과 어떻게 다시 만날까 하는 생각뿐이었다."]], [["He set out, and on the Saturday evening reached the Place de la Douane by the mail-coach.", "그는 길을 나섰고, 토요일 저녁에 우편 마차를 타고 두아나 광장에 도착했다."], ["An apartment, as we have said, had been retained beforehand, and thus he had but to go to Signor Pastrini’s hotel.", "앞서 말했듯이 숙소는 미리 잡아 두었으니, 그는 파스트리니 씨의 호텔로 가기만 하면 되었다. 하지만 그것은 그리 쉬운 일이 아니었다. 거리가 사람들로 북적였고, 로마는 이미 온갖 큰 행사에 앞서 일렁이는 그 낮고 들뜬 웅성거림에 사로잡혀 있었기 때문이다."], ["But this was not so easy a matter, for the streets were thronged with people, and Rome was already a prey to that low and feverish murmur which precedes all great events; and at Rome there are four great events in every year,—the Carnival, Holy Week, Corpus Christi, and the Feast of St. Peter.", "로마에는 해마다 네 가지 큰 행사가 있다. 사육제, 성주간, 성체 축일, 그리고 성 베드로 축일이다."]], [["All the rest of the year the city is in that state of dull apathy, between life and death, which renders it similar to a kind of station between this world and the next—a sublime spot, a resting-place full of poetry and character, and at which Franz had already halted five or six times, and at each time found it more marvellous and striking.", "그 밖의 시기에는 도시가 삶과 죽음 사이의, 저 따분하고 무기력한 상태에 잠겨 있다. 그래서 도시는 이승과 저승 사이의 어떤 정거장 같아 보인다. 시적 정취와 개성이 넘치는 숭고한 곳, 휴식처 같은 곳이다. 프란츠는 이미 그곳에 대여섯 번 머문 적이 있었고, 그때마다 그곳이 더욱 경이롭고 인상 깊게 느껴졌다."]], [["At last he made his way through the mob, which was continually increasing and getting more and more turbulent, and reached the hotel.", "마침내 그는 끊임없이 불어나고 점점 더 소란스러워지는 군중을 헤치고 호텔에 다다랐다."], ["On his first inquiry he was told, with the impertinence peculiar to hired hackney-coachmen and innkeepers with their houses full, that there was no room for him at the Hôtel de Londres.", "처음 물었을 때, 그는 집이 만원인 마차꾼이나 여관 주인 특유의 무례한 태도로, 런던 호텔에는 그가 묵을 방이 없다는 대답을 들었다."], ["Then he sent his card to Signor Pastrini, and asked for Albert de Morcerf.", "그래서 그는 파스트리니 씨에게 명함을 들여보내며 알베르 드 모르세르를 찾았다."], ["This plan succeeded; and Signor Pastrini himself ran to him, excusing himself for having made his excellency wait, scolding the waiters, taking the candlestick from the porter, who was ready to pounce on the traveller and was about to lead him to Albert, when Morcerf himself appeared.", "이 방법이 통했다. 파스트리니 씨가 직접 그에게 달려와, 각하를 기다리게 했다며 양해를 구하고 종업원들을 나무라더니, 손님에게 달려들어 알베르에게 안내하려던 짐꾼에게서 촛대를 빼앗았다. 그때 마침 모르세르가 직접 나타났다."]], [["The apartment consisted of two small rooms and a parlor.", "숙소는 작은 방 두 개와 응접실 하나로 이루어져 있었다."], ["The two rooms looked on to the street—a fact which Signor Pastrini commented upon as an inappreciable advantage.", "두 방은 거리 쪽으로 나 있었는데, 파스트리니 씨는 이를 두고 더없이 값진 이점이라고 떠벌렸다."], ["The rest of the floor was hired by a very rich gentleman who was supposed to be a Sicilian or Maltese; but the host was unable to decide to which of the two nations the traveller belonged.", "그 층의 나머지는 어느 무척 부유한 신사가 빌려 쓰고 있었다. 사람들은 그가 시칠리아 사람이거나 몰타 사람일 거라고 했지만, 호텔 주인은 그 손님이 두 나라 가운데 어느 쪽 사람인지 가려내지 못했다."]], [["“Very good, signor Pastrini,”", "\"아주 좋네, 파스트리니 씨.\""], ["said Franz; “but we must have some supper instantly, and a carriage for tomorrow and the following days.”", "프란츠가 말했다. \"그런데 당장 저녁 식사를 좀 차려 줘야겠고, 내일과 그 뒤 며칠 동안 쓸 마차도 한 대 필요하네.\""]], [["“As to supper,”", "\"저녁 식사라면,\""], ["replied the landlord, “you shall be served immediately; but as for the carriage——”", "주인이 대답했다. \"지금 바로 차려 드리겠습니다. 그런데 마차로 말씀드리자면……\""]], [["“What as to the carriage?”", "\"마차가 어쨌다는 거요?\""], ["exclaimed Albert.", "알베르가 소리쳤다."], ["“Come, come, Signor Pastrini, no joking; we must have a carriage.”", "\"이보시오, 이보시오, 파스트리니 씨, 농담은 그만두시오. 우리는 마차가 꼭 필요하단 말이오.\""]], [["“Sir,”", "\"손님,\""], ["replied the host, “we will do all in our power to procure you one—this is all I can say.”", "주인이 대답했다. \"한 대 구해 드리려고 저희가 할 수 있는 건 다 해 보겠습니다. 제가 드릴 수 있는 말씀은 그게 전부입니다.\""]], [["“And when shall we know?”", "\"그럼 언제쯤 알 수 있겠소?\""], ["inquired Franz.", "프란츠가 물었다."]], [["“Tomorrow morning,”", "\"내일 아침입니다.\""], ["answered the innkeeper.", "여관 주인이 대답했다."]], [["“Oh, the deuce!", "\"오, 제기랄!"], ["then we shall pay the more, that’s all, I see plainly enough.", "그러면 값을 더 치르면 될 일 아닌가, 빤히 보이는군."], ["At Drake’s or Aaron’s one pays twenty-five lire for common days, and thirty or thirty-five lire a day more for Sundays and feast days; add five lire a day more for extras, that will make forty, and there’s an end of it.”", "드레이크네나 아론네서는 평일에 25리라를 받고, 일요일과 축일에는 하루에 30리라나 35리라를 더 받지. 추가 비용으로 하루에 5리라를 더 보태면 40리라가 될 테니, 그걸로 끝나는 거야.\""]], [["“I am afraid if we offer them double that we shall not procure a carriage.”", "\"그 두 배를 부른다 해도 마차를 구하지 못할까 봐 걱정입니다.\""]], [["“Then they must put horses to mine.", "\"그렇다면 내 마차에 말을 매면 되지."], ["It is a little worse for the journey, but that’s no matter.”", "여행에는 좀 못하겠지만, 그쯤이야 상관없네.\""]], [["“There are no horses.”", "\"말이 없습니다.\""]], [["Albert looked at Franz like a man who hears a reply he does not understand.", "알베르는 도무지 알아들을 수 없는 대답을 들은 사람처럼 프란츠를 바라보았다."]], [["“Do you understand that, my dear Franz—no horses?”", "\"이보게, 프란츠, 이 말 알아듣겠나? 말이 없다는군.\""], ["he said, “but can’t we have post-horses?”", "그가 말했다. \"그래도 역마라도 빌릴 수 있지 않겠나?\""]], [["“They have been all hired this fortnight, and there are none left but those absolutely requisite for posting.”", "\"역마는 지난 두 주 동안 죄다 예약되어, 우편 업무에 반드시 필요한 것 말고는 한 마리도 남지 않았습니다.\""]], [["“What are we to say to this?”", "\"이걸 두고 우리가 뭐라고 해야 하나?\""], ["asked Franz.", "프란츠가 물었다."]], [["“I say, that when a thing completely surpasses my comprehension, I am accustomed not to dwell on that thing, but to pass to another.", "\"내 생각은 이러네. 어떤 일이 도무지 내 이해를 넘어설 때면, 나는 그 일에 매달리지 않고 다른 일로 넘어가는 버릇이 있지."], ["Is supper ready, Signor Pastrini?”", "저녁 식사는 준비됐소, 파스트리니 씨?\""]], [["“Yes, your excellency.”", "\"네, 각하.\""]], [["“Well, then, let us sup.”", "\"좋아, 그럼 식사나 합시다.\""]], [["“But the carriage and horses?” said Franz.", "\"하지만 마차와 말은?\" 프란츠가 말했다."]], [["“Be easy, my dear boy; they will come in due season; it is only a question of how much shall be charged for them.”", "\"마음 놓게, 이 친구야. 때가 되면 다 올 걸세. 결국 값을 얼마나 치르느냐의 문제일 뿐이니까.\""], ["Morcerf then, with that delighted philosophy which believes that nothing is impossible to a full purse or well-lined pocketbook, supped, went to bed, slept soundly, and dreamed he was racing all over Rome at Carnival time in a coach with six horses.", "그러고는 모르세르는, 두둑한 지갑이나 빵빵한 지갑에는 불가능한 일이 없다고 믿는 그 흐뭇한 철학으로, 저녁을 먹고 잠자리에 들어 곤히 잠들었다. 그리고 여섯 마리 말이 끄는 마차를 타고 사육제 기간의 로마를 누비며 질주하는 꿈을 꾸었다."]], [["# Chapter 33. Roman Bandits", "# Chapter 33. Roman Bandits"]], [["The next morning Franz woke first, and instantly rang the bell.", "이튿날 아침 프란츠가 먼저 깨어나 곧바로 종을 울렸다."], ["The sound had not yet died away when Signor Pastrini himself entered.", "종소리가 채 잦아들기도 전에 파스트리니 씨가 직접 들어왔다."]], [["“Well, excellency,”", "\"그렇습니다, 나리.\" 여관 주인이 의기양양하게, 프란츠가 묻기를 기다리지도 않고 입을 열었다."], ["said the landlord triumphantly, and without waiting for Franz to question him, “I feared yesterday, when I would not promise you anything, that you were too late—there is not a single carriage to be had—that is, for the three last days”", "\"어제 제가 아무것도 약속드리지 못했을 때, 나리께서 너무 늦으신 게 아닌가 걱정했지요. 마차가 한 대도 남아 있질 않습니다. 그러니까, 마지막 사흘 동안은 말입니다.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇지.\""], ["returned Franz, “for the very three days it is most needed.”", "프란츠가 대답했다. \"하필 가장 필요한 그 사흘이라니.\""]], [["“What is the matter?”", "\"무슨 일인데요?\""], ["said Albert, entering; “no carriage to be had?”", "알베르가 들어오며 물었다. \"마차를 못 구한다고요?\""]], [["“Just so,”", "\"바로 그렇다네.\""], ["returned Franz, “you have guessed it.”", "프란츠가 대답했다. \"자네가 알아맞혔어.\""]], [["“Well, your Eternal City is a nice sort of place.”", "\"이런, 자네의 영원한 도시라는 데가 참 그럴듯한 곳이로군.\""]], [["“That is to say, excellency,”", "\"그러니까 말씀드리자면, 나리.\""], ["replied Pastrini, who was desirous of keeping up the dignity of the capital of the Christian world in the eyes of his guest, “that there are no carriages to be had from Sunday to Tuesday evening, but from now till Sunday you can have fifty if you please.”", "손님 앞에서 그리스도교 세계 수도의 위엄을 지키려는 마음에 파스트리니가 대답했다. \"일요일부터 화요일 저녁까지는 마차를 구할 수 없습니다만, 지금부터 일요일까지라면 원하시는 대로 쉰 대라도 구할 수 있습니다.\""]], [["“Ah, that is something,”", "\"아, 그거 다행이군.\""], ["said Albert; “today is Thursday, and who knows what may arrive between this and Sunday?”", "알베르가 말했다. \"오늘이 목요일이니, 지금부터 일요일 사이에 무슨 일이 생길지 누가 알겠나?\""]], [["“Ten or twelve thousand travellers will arrive,”", "\"여행객이 만 명에서 만 이천 명쯤 도착할 걸세.\""], ["replied Franz, “which will make it still more difficult.”", "프란츠가 대답했다. \"그러면 일이 더 어려워지겠지.\""]], [["“My friend,”", "\"이보게.\""], ["said Morcerf, “let us enjoy the present without gloomy forebodings for the future.”", "모르세르가 말했다. \"미래를 두고 음울하게 점치지 말고 현재나 즐기세.\""]], [["“At least we can have a window?”", "\"적어도 창문 하나쯤은 얻을 수 있겠지?\""]], [["“Where?”", "\"어디에?\""]], [["“In the Corso.”", "\"코르소 거리에.\""]], [["“Ah, a window!”", "\"아, 창문이요!\""], ["exclaimed Signor Pastrini,—“utterly impossible; there was only one left on the fifth floor of the Doria Palace, and that has been let to a Russian prince for twenty sequins a day.”", "파스트리니 씨가 소리쳤다. \"그건 도무지 불가능합니다. 도리아 궁전 오 층에 딱 하나 남아 있었는데, 그것도 어느 러시아 공작에게 하루 이십 제키노에 빌려주었습니다.\""]], [["The two young men looked at each other with an air of stupefaction.", "두 젊은이는 어이없다는 표정으로 서로를 마주 보았다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"이보게.\""], ["said Franz to Albert, “do you know what is the best thing we can do?", "프란츠가 알베르에게 말했다. \"우리가 할 수 있는 최선이 뭔지 아나?"], ["It is to pass the Carnival at Venice; there we are sure of obtaining gondolas if we cannot have carriages.”", "베네치아에서 카니발을 보내는 거야. 거기서는 마차는 못 구해도 곤돌라만큼은 틀림없이 구할 수 있지.\""]], [["“Ah, the devil, no,”", "\"아, 무슨 소리, 안 돼.\""], ["cried Albert; “I came to Rome to see the Carnival, and I will, though I see it on stilts.”", "알베르가 외쳤다. \"나는 카니발을 보려고 로마에 왔어. 죽마를 타고서라도 기어이 보고 말겠네.\""]], [["“Bravo! an excellent idea.", "\"브라보! 훌륭한 생각이야."], ["We will disguise ourselves as monster pulchinellos or shepherds of the Landes, and we shall have complete success.”", "우리가 괴물 같은 풀치넬라나 랑드 지방 양치기로 변장하면 대성공을 거두겠군.\""]], [["“Do your excellencies still wish for a carriage from now to Sunday morning?”", "\"그래서 나리들께서는 지금부터 일요일 아침까지 쓰실 마차를 여전히 원하십니까?\""]], [["“_Parbleu!_”", "\"이런 제기랄!\""], ["said Albert, “do you think we are going to run about on foot in the streets of Rome, like lawyers’ clerks?”", "알베르가 말했다. \"우리가 변호사 사무실 서기처럼 로마 거리를 두 발로 뛰어다닐 줄 알았나?\""]], [["“I hasten to comply with your excellencies’ wishes; only, I tell you beforehand, the carriage will cost you six piastres a day.”", "\"나리들의 분부를 서둘러 받들겠습니다. 다만 미리 말씀드리는데, 마차는 하루에 육 피아스터입니다.\""]], [["“And, as I am not a millionaire, like the gentleman in the next apartments,”", "\"그리고 나는 옆방의 그 신사 양반처럼 백만장자가 아닐세.\""], ["said Franz, “I warn you, that as I have been four times before at Rome, I know the prices of all the carriages; we will give you twelve piastres for today, tomorrow, and the day after, and then you will make a good profit.”", "프란츠가 말했다. \"미리 말해 두는데, 나는 로마에 이번이 네 번째라 마차 값을 죄다 꿰고 있어. 오늘, 내일, 모레, 이렇게 사흘에 십이 피아스터를 줄 테니, 그래도 자네는 충분히 남는 장사일 거야.\""]], [["“But, excellency”—said Pastrini, still striving to gain his point.", "\"하지만 나리……\" 파스트리니가 여전히 제 뜻을 관철하려 애쓰며 말했다."]], [["“Now go,” returned Franz, “or I shall go myself and bargain with your _affettatore_, who is mine also; he is an old friend of mine, who has plundered me pretty well already, and, in the hope of making more out of me, he will take a less price than the one I offer you; you will lose the preference, and that will be your fault.”", "\"이제 가 보게.\" 프란츠가 대답했다. \"안 그러면 내가 직접 자네의 마부에게 가서 흥정하겠어. 그자는 내 마부이기도 하니까. 그는 내 오랜 친구로, 이미 나한테서 톡톡히 등쳐 먹은 자야. 그러니 나한테서 더 뜯어낼 욕심에, 내가 자네에게 제시한 값보다 더 싸게 받을 걸세. 그러면 자네는 우선권을 잃을 텐데, 그건 다 자네 탓일세.\""]], [["“Do not give yourselves the trouble, excellency,”", "\"그렇게 수고하실 것 없습니다, 나리.\""], ["returned Signor Pastrini, with the smile peculiar to the Italian speculator when he confesses defeat; “I will do all I can, and I hope you will be satisfied.”", "파스트리니 씨가 패배를 인정할 때 이탈리아 장사꾼 특유의 미소를 띠며 대답했다. \"제가 할 수 있는 일은 다 하겠습니다. 만족하시리라 믿습니다.\""]], [["“And now we understand each other.”", "\"그럼 이제 서로 이해한 셈이군.\""]], [["“When do you wish the carriage to be here?”", "\"마차가 언제 와 있기를 바라십니까?\""]], [["“In an hour.”", "\"한 시간 안에.\""]], [["“In an hour it will be at the door.”", "\"한 시간 안에 문 앞에 대 놓겠습니다.\""]], [["An hour after the vehicle was at the door; it was a hack conveyance which was elevated to the rank of a private carriage in honor of the occasion, but, in spite of its humble exterior, the young men would have thought themselves happy to have secured it for the last three days of the Carnival.", "한 시간 뒤 마차가 문 앞에 와 있었다. 이번 일을 기리느라 자가용 마차의 반열에 올려놓은 삯마차에 불과했지만, 그 초라한 겉모습에도 불구하고 두 젊은이는 카니발의 마지막 사흘 동안 그것을 확보한 것만으로도 다행이라 여길 만했다."]], [["“Excellency,”", "\"나리.\""], ["cried the _cicerone_, seeing Franz approach the window, “shall I bring the carriage nearer to the palace?”", "프란츠가 창가로 다가오는 것을 보고 안내인이 외쳤다. \"마차를 궁전 가까이로 대 올릴까요?\""]], [["Accustomed as Franz was to the Italian phraseology, his first impulse was to look round him, but these words were addressed to him.", "이탈리아식 말투에 익숙한 프란츠였지만, 처음에는 무심코 주위를 둘러보았다. 그러나 그 말은 자신에게 한 것이었다."], ["Franz was the “excellency,” the vehicle was the “carriage,” and the Hôtel de Londres was the “palace.”", "프란츠가 '나리'였고, 그 탈것이 '마차'였으며, 런던 호텔이 '궁전'이었던 것이다."], ["The genius for laudation characteristic of the race was in that phrase.", "이 종족 특유의 찬사의 재능이 그 한마디에 고스란히 담겨 있었다."]], [["Franz and Albert descended, the carriage approached the palace; their excellencies stretched their legs along the seats; the _cicerone_ sprang into the seat behind.", "프란츠와 알베르가 내려갔고, 마차가 궁전으로 다가왔다. 두 나리는 좌석 위로 다리를 쭉 뻗었고, 안내인은 뒷자리로 훌쩍 올라탔다."]], [["“Where do your excellencies wish to go?” asked he.", "\"나리들께서는 어디로 가시렵니까?\" 그가 물었다."]], [["“To Saint Peter’s first, and then to the Colosseum,”", "\"우선 성 베드로 대성당으로, 그다음에는 콜로세움으로.\""], ["returned Albert.", "알베르가 대답했다."], ["But Albert did not know that it takes a day to see Saint Peter’s, and a month to study it.", "그러나 알베르는 성 베드로 대성당을 둘러보는 데 하루가 걸리고, 제대로 공부하려면 한 달이 걸린다는 것을 몰랐다."], ["The day was passed at Saint Peter’s alone.", "그날 하루는 성 베드로 대성당 한 곳에서 다 지나갔다."]], [["Suddenly the daylight began to fade away; Franz took out his watch—it was half-past four.", "이윽고 햇빛이 사그라지기 시작했다. 프란츠가 시계를 꺼내 보니 네 시 반이었다."], ["They returned to the hotel; at the door Franz ordered the coachman to be ready at eight.", "두 사람은 호텔로 돌아왔다. 문 앞에서 프란츠는 마부에게 여덟 시에 채비를 갖추고 있으라고 일렀다."], ["He wished to show Albert the Colosseum by moonlight, as he had shown him Saint Peter’s by daylight.", "그는 낮의 햇빛 아래 알베르에게 성 베드로 대성당을 보여 주었듯, 달빛 아래 콜로세움을 보여 주고 싶었다."], ["When we show a friend a city one has already visited, we feel the same pride as when we point out a woman whose lover we have been.", "이미 가 본 도시를 친구에게 보여 줄 때면, 우리는 한때 연인이었던 여자를 가리켜 보일 때와 똑같은 자부심을 느낀다."]], [["He was to leave the city by the Porta del Popolo, skirt the outer wall, and re-enter by the Porta San Giovanni; thus they would behold the Colosseum without finding their impressions dulled by first looking on the Capitol, the Forum, the Arch of Septimus Severus, the Temple of Antoninus and Faustina, and the Via Sacra.", "그는 포폴로 문으로 도시를 빠져나가 바깥 성벽을 따라 돌다가 산조반니 문으로 되돌아 들어올 작정이었다. 그러면 카피톨리노 언덕과 포룸, 셉티미우스 세베루스 개선문, 안토니누스와 파우스티나 신전, 그리고 비아 사크라를 먼저 보느라 인상이 무뎌지는 일 없이 곧장 콜로세움을 마주할 수 있을 터였다."]], [["They sat down to dinner. Signor Pastrini had promised them a banquet; he gave them a tolerable repast.", "두 사람은 저녁 식사 자리에 앉았다. 파스트리니 씨는 성대한 잔칫상을 약속했는데, 그런대로 먹을 만한 식사를 차려 냈다."], ["At the end of the dinner he entered in person.", "식사가 끝날 무렵 그가 몸소 들어왔다."], ["Franz thought that he came to hear his dinner praised, and began accordingly, but at the first words he was interrupted.", "프란츠는 음식 칭찬을 들으러 온 줄로 알고 그렇게 입을 떼었으나, 첫마디부터 말을 가로막혔다."]], [["“Excellency,”", "\"나리.\""], ["said Pastrini, “I am delighted to have your approbation, but it was not for that I came.”", "파스트리니가 말했다. \"흡족해하시니 기쁩니다만, 제가 온 것은 그 때문이 아닙니다.\""]], [["“Did you come to tell us you have procured a carriage?” asked Albert, lighting his cigar.", "\"마차를 구했다고 알려 주러 온 건가?\" 알베르가 시가에 불을 붙이며 물었다."]], [["“No; and your excellencies will do well not to think of that any longer; at Rome things can or cannot be done; when you are told anything cannot be done, there is an end of it.”", "\"아닙니다. 그리고 나리들께서는 그 일은 더 이상 생각하지 않으시는 게 좋겠습니다. 로마에서는 되는 일이 있고 안 되는 일이 있는데, 안 된다는 말을 들으면 그걸로 끝입니다.\""]], [["“It is much more convenient at Paris,—when anything cannot be done, you pay double, and it is done directly.”", "\"파리에서는 훨씬 편리하지. 안 되는 일이 있으면 돈을 두 배로 내고, 그러면 곧장 처리되거든.\""]], [["“That is what all the French say,”", "\"프랑스 사람들은 다들 그렇게 말합니다.\""], ["returned Signor Pastrini, somewhat piqued; “for that reason, I do not understand why they travel.”", "파스트리니 씨가 다소 발끈하며 대답했다. \"그러니 도대체 왜 여행을 다니시는지 모르겠습니다.\""]], [["“But,” said Albert, emitting a volume of smoke and balancing his chair on its hind legs, “only madmen, or blockheads like us, ever do travel.", "\"하지만.\" 알베르가 연기를 한 모금 내뿜고 의자를 뒷다리로 까딱거리며 말했다."], ["Men in their senses do not quit their hotel in the Rue du Helder, their walk on the Boulevard de Gand, and the Café de Paris.”", "\"여행이란 건 미치광이나, 아니면 우리 같은 멍청이나 다니는 거지. 제정신인 사람은 엘데르 거리의 호텔과 강 대로의 산책길과 카페 드 파리를 떠나지 않는 법이야.\""]], [["It is of course understood that Albert resided in the aforesaid street, appeared every day on the fashionable walk, and dined frequently at the only restaurant where you can really dine, that is, if you are on good terms with its waiters.", "물론 알베르는 앞서 말한 그 거리에 살았고, 그 멋쟁이들의 산책길에 날마다 모습을 드러냈으며, 정말로 제대로 식사할 수 있는 유일한 식당에서 자주 끼니를 들었다. 단, 그 식당 종업원들과 사이가 좋아야 한다는 조건에서 말이다."]], [["Signor Pastrini remained silent a short time; it was evident that he was musing over this answer, which did not seem very clear.", "파스트리니 씨는 잠시 입을 다물었다. 그다지 분명치 않게 들리는 그 대답을 곰곰이 곱씹는 게 분명했다."]], [["“But,”", "\"그런데.\""], ["said Franz, in his turn interrupting his host’s meditations, “you had some motive for coming here, may I beg to know what it was?” 20099m", "이번엔 프란츠가 여관 주인의 사색을 가로막으며 말했다. \"여기 온 데는 무슨 까닭이 있었을 텐데, 그게 무엇인지 물어봐도 되겠나?\""]], [["“Ah, yes; you have ordered your carriage at eight o’clock precisely?”", "\"아, 그렇지요. 마차를 정확히 여덟 시에 부르셨지요?\""]], [["“I have.”", "\"그랬네.\""]], [["“You intend visiting _Il Colosseo_.”", "\"일 콜로세오를 둘러보실 작정이시고요.\""]], [["“You mean the Colosseum?”", "\"콜로세움 말인가?\""]], [["“It is the same thing.", "\"같은 겁니다."], ["You have told your coachman to leave the city by the Porta del Popolo, to drive round the walls, and re-enter by the Porta San Giovanni?”", "마부에게 포폴로 문으로 도시를 나가 성벽을 빙 돌고 산조반니 문으로 되돌아 들어오라고 이르셨지요?\""]], [["“These are my words exactly.”", "\"내가 한 말 그대로일세.\""]], [["“Well, this route is impossible.”", "\"그런데 그 길은 불가능합니다.\""]], [["“Impossible!”", "\"불가능하다고!\""]], [["“Very dangerous, to say the least.”", "\"적어도 몹시 위험하지요.\""]], [["“Dangerous!—and why?”", "\"위험하다니, 어째서?\""]], [["“On account of the famous Luigi Vampa.”", "\"그 유명한 루이지 밤파 때문입니다.\""]], [["“Pray, who may this famous Luigi Vampa be?”", "\"부디 묻건대, 그 유명한 루이지 밤파가 누구인가?\""], ["inquired Albert; “he may be very famous at Rome, but I can assure you he is quite unknown at Paris.”", "알베르가 물었다. \"로마에서야 아주 유명할지 모르나, 장담하건대 파리에서는 전혀 알려지지 않은 자야.\""]], [["“What! do you not know him?”", "\"뭐라고요! 그를 모르신단 말입니까?\""]], [["“I have not that honor.”", "\"그런 영광은 누리지 못했네.\""]], [["“You have never heard his name?”", "\"그 이름을 한 번도 들어 보신 적이 없다고요?\""]], [["“Never.”", "\"한 번도.\""]], [["“Well, then, he is a bandit, compared to whom the Decesaris and the Gasparones were mere children.”", "\"음, 그렇다면 그는 산적입니다. 그에 비하면 데체사리와 가스파로네는 그저 어린애에 불과했지요.\""]], [["“Now then, Albert,”", "\"자, 알베르.\""], ["cried Franz, “here is a bandit for you at last.”", "프란츠가 외쳤다. \"드디어 자네가 바라던 산적이 나타났군.\""]], [["“I forewarn you, Signor Pastrini, that I shall not believe one word of what you are going to tell us; having told you this, begin.", "\"미리 말해 두겠네, 파스트리니 씨. 자네가 우리에게 들려줄 이야기는 한마디도 믿지 않겠어. 이렇게 말해 두었으니 시작하게."], ["‘Once upon a time——’ Well, go on.”", "'옛날 옛적에……' 자, 어서 해 보게.\""]], [["Signor Pastrini turned toward Franz, who seemed to him the more reasonable of the two; we must do him justice,—he had had a great many Frenchmen in his house, but had never been able to comprehend them.", "파스트리니 씨는 둘 중 더 분별 있어 보이는 프란츠 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다. 공정하게 말하자면, 그는 제 집에 수많은 프랑스 사람을 들였지만 한 번도 그들을 이해해 본 적이 없었다."]], [["“Excellency,”", "\"나리.\""], ["said he gravely, addressing Franz, “if you look upon me as a liar, it is useless for me to say anything; it was for your interest I——”", "그가 프란츠를 향해 진지하게 말했다. \"저를 거짓말쟁이로 여기신다면, 제가 무슨 말을 해도 소용이 없겠지요. 다 나리들을 위해서 드린 말씀인데……\""]], [["“Albert does not say you are a liar, Signor Pastrini,”", "\"알베르는 자네를 거짓말쟁이라고 한 게 아닐세, 파스트리니 씨.\" 프란츠가 말했다."], ["said Franz, “but that he will not believe what you are going to tell us,—but I will believe all you say; so proceed.”", "\"다만 자네가 들려줄 이야기를 믿지 않겠다는 거지. 하지만 나는 자네 말을 죄다 믿겠네. 그러니 계속하게.\""]], [["“But if your excellency doubt my veracity——”", "\"하지만 나리께서 제 진실성을 의심하신다면……\""]], [["“Signor Pastrini,”", "\"파스트리니 씨.\" 프란츠가 대답했다."], ["returned Franz, “you are more susceptible than Cassandra, who was a prophetess, and yet no one believed her; while you, at least, are sure of the credence of half your audience.", "\"자네는 카산드라보다 더 예민하구먼. 그 여자는 예언자였는데도 아무도 그 말을 믿지 않았지. 그런데 자네는 적어도 청중의 절반에게는 믿음을 얻고 있질 않나."], ["Come, sit down, and tell us all about this Signor Vampa.”", "자, 앉아서 그 밤파 씨 이야기를 죄다 들려주게.\""]], [["“I had told your excellency he is the most famous bandit we have had since the days of Mastrilla.”", "\"말씀드렸듯이 그는 마스트릴라 시대 이래 가장 유명한 산적입니다.\""]], [["“Well, what has this bandit to do with the order I have given the coachman to leave the city by the Porta del Popolo, and to re-enter by the Porta San Giovanni?”", "\"좋아, 그래서 그 산적이 내가 마부에게 포폴로 문으로 도시를 나가 산조반니 문으로 되돌아 들어오라고 한 명령과 무슨 상관인가?\""]], [["20101m\n“This,”", "\"이런 상관이 있습니다.\""], ["replied Signor Pastrini, “that you will go out by one, but I very much doubt your returning by the other.”", "파스트리니 씨가 대답했다. \"한쪽 문으로 나가시기는 할 텐데, 다른 쪽 문으로 돌아오실지는 심히 의심스럽습니다.\""]], [["“Why?”", "\"어째서?\""], ["asked Franz.", "프란츠가 물었다."]], [["“Because, after nightfall, you are not safe fifty yards from the gates.”", "\"해가 지고 나면 성문에서 오십 미터만 떨어져도 안전하지 않으니까요.\""]], [["“On your honor, is that true?”", "\"명예를 걸고, 그게 사실인가?\""], ["cried Albert.", "알베르가 외쳤다."]], [["“Count,”", "\"백작 나리.\""], ["returned Signor Pastrini, hurt at Albert’s repeated doubts of the truth of his assertions, “I do not say this to you, but to your companion, who knows Rome, and knows, too, that these things are not to be laughed at.”", "알베르가 거듭 제 말의 진위를 의심하자 마음이 상한 파스트리니 씨가 대답했다. \"이건 나리께 드리는 말씀이 아니라, 로마를 잘 아시고 또 이런 일을 웃어넘길 게 아니라는 것도 아시는 일행분께 드리는 말씀입니다.\""]], [["“My dear fellow,”", "\"이보게.\" 알베르가 프란츠 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다."], ["said Albert, turning to Franz, “here is an admirable adventure; we will fill our carriage with pistols, blunderbusses, and double-barrelled guns.", "\"이거 멋진 모험거리로군. 마차에 권총이며 나팔총이며 쌍열총을 가득 싣는 거야. 루이지 밤파가 우리를 잡으러 오면, 우리가 그를 잡는 거지."], ["Luigi Vampa comes to take us, and we take him—we bring him back to Rome, and present him to his holiness the Pope, who asks how he can repay so great a service; then we merely ask for a carriage and a pair of horses, and we see the Carnival in the carriage, and doubtless the Roman people will crown us at the Capitol, and proclaim us, like Curtius and Horatius Cocles, the preservers of their country.”", "그를 로마로 끌고 와 교황 성하께 바치면, 성하께서 이토록 큰 공을 어떻게 갚으면 좋겠냐고 물으실 테고, 그러면 우리는 그저 마차 한 대와 말 두 필만 청하면 되네. 그러고는 마차를 타고 카니발을 구경하는 거야. 그러면 틀림없이 로마 시민들이 카피톨리노 언덕에서 우리에게 월계관을 씌우고, 쿠르티우스와 호라티우스 코클레스처럼 나라를 구한 영웅이라 칭송하겠지.\""]], [["Whilst Albert proposed this scheme, Signor Pastrini’s face assumed an expression impossible to describe.", "알베르가 이 계획을 늘어놓는 동안, 파스트리니 씨의 얼굴에는 형언할 수 없는 표정이 떠올랐다."]], [["“And pray,”", "\"그래, 한데 묻건대.\""], ["asked Franz, “where are these pistols, blunderbusses, and other deadly weapons with which you intend filling the carriage?”", "프란츠가 물었다. \"자네가 마차에 가득 채우겠다는 그 권총이며 나팔총이며 다른 흉기들은 어디 있나?\""]], [["“Not out of my armory, for at Terracina I was plundered even of my hunting-knife. And you?”", "\"내 무기고에는 없지. 테라치나에서 사냥칼까지 죄다 털렸으니 말이야. 자네는?\""]], [["“I shared the same fate at Aquapendente.”", "\"나도 아콰펜덴테에서 같은 신세였지.\""]], [["“Do you know, Signor Pastrini,”", "\"이보게, 파스트리니 씨.\""], ["said Albert, lighting a second cigar at the first, “that this practice is very convenient for bandits, and that it seems to be due to an arrangement of their own.”", "알베르가 첫 시가의 불에 두 번째 시가를 붙이며 말했다. \"이런 관행은 산적들에게 아주 편리하겠어. 게다가 그자들끼리 짜고 하는 일인 것 같단 말이지.\""]], [["Doubtless Signor Pastrini found this pleasantry compromising, for he only answered half the question, and then he spoke to Franz, as the only one likely to listen with attention.", "파스트리니 씨는 이 농담이 자신을 곤란하게 만든다고 여긴 모양이었다. 질문의 절반에만 답하고는, 그나마 주의 깊게 들어 줄 듯한 프란츠에게 말을 건넸기 때문이다."], ["“Your excellency knows that it is not customary to defend yourself when attacked by bandits.”", "\"나리께서도 아시다시피, 산적들에게 습격당했을 때는 저항하지 않는 것이 관례입니다.\""]], [["“What!”", "\"뭐라고!\""], ["cried Albert, whose courage revolted at the idea of being plundered tamely, “not make any resistance!”", "순순히 털리는 꼴을 생각하니 용기가 끓어오른 알베르가 외쳤다. \"아무 저항도 하지 말라고!\""]], [["“No, for it would be useless.", "\"네, 소용없는 짓이니까요."], ["What could you do against a dozen bandits who spring out of some pit, ruin, or aqueduct, and level their pieces at you?”", "구덩이나 폐허, 혹은 수도교에서 열두 명이나 되는 산적이 튀어나와 총을 겨누면, 무엇을 할 수 있겠습니까?\""]], [["“Eh, _parbleu!_—they should kill me.”", "\"이런 제기랄, 그럼 나를 죽이라고 하지.\""]], [["The innkeeper turned to Franz with an air that seemed to say, “Your friend is decidedly mad.”", "여관 주인은 \"자네 친구는 영락없이 미쳤군\" 하고 말하는 듯한 표정으로 프란츠를 돌아보았다."]], [["“My dear Albert,”", "\"이보게, 알베르.\" 프란츠가 대답했다. \"자네 대답은 숭고하기 그지없어."], ["returned Franz, “your answer is sublime, and worthy the ‘_Let him die_,’ of Corneille, only, when Horace made that answer, the safety of Rome was concerned; but, as for us, it is only to gratify a whim, and it would be ridiculous to risk our lives for so foolish a motive.”", "코르네유의 그 '죽게 내버려 두라'에 견줄 만하군. 다만 호라티우스가 그 대답을 했을 때는 로마의 안위가 걸려 있었지. 하지만 우리로 말하자면 한낱 변덕을 채우자는 것뿐인데, 그런 어리석은 까닭으로 목숨을 거는 건 우스운 일이야.\""]], [["Albert poured himself out a glass of _lacryma Christi_, which he sipped at intervals, muttering some unintelligible words.", "알베르는 라크리마 크리스티 한 잔을 따라 이따금 홀짝거리며 알아들을 수 없는 말을 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Well, Signor Pastrini,”", "\"자, 파스트리니 씨.\""], ["said Franz, “now that my companion is quieted, and you have seen how peaceful my intentions are, tell me who is this Luigi Vampa.", "프란츠가 말했다. \"이제 내 일행도 잠잠해졌고, 내 의도가 얼마나 평화로운지도 보았을 테니, 그 루이지 밤파가 누구인지 말해 주게."], ["Is he a shepherd or a nobleman?—young or old?—tall or short?", "양치기인가, 귀족인가? 젊은가, 늙었나? 키가 큰가, 작은가?"], ["Describe him, in order that, if we meet him by chance, like Jean Sbogar or Lara, we may recognize him.”", "그를 묘사해 주게. 그래야 행여 장 스보가르나 라라처럼 우연히 마주치더라도 알아볼 수 있지 않겠나.\""]], [["“You could not apply to anyone better able to inform you on all these points, for I knew him when he was a child, and one day that I fell into his hands, going from Ferentino to Alatri, he, fortunately for me, recollected me, and set me free, not only without ransom, but made me a present of a very splendid watch, and related his history to me.”", "\"이 모든 점에 관해서라면 저보다 더 잘 알려 드릴 사람을 찾기 어려우실 겁니다. 저는 그가 어릴 적부터 그를 알았고, 한번은 페렌티노에서 알라트리로 가다가 그의 손에 떨어진 적이 있는데, 다행히 그가 저를 기억하고는 풀어 주었지요. 몸값을 받지 않았을뿐더러 아주 훌륭한 시계까지 선물로 주고, 제게 자기 내력을 들려주었답니다.\""]], [["“Let us see the watch,”", "\"그 시계 좀 봅시다.\""], ["said Albert.", "알베르가 말했다."]], [["Signor Pastrini drew from his fob a magnificent Bréguet, bearing the name of its maker, of Parisian manufacture, and a count’s coronet.", "파스트리니 씨가 회중시계 주머니에서 멋들어진 브레게 시계를 꺼냈다. 제작자의 이름이 새겨진 파리제 시계로, 백작의 관(冠) 문장이 박혀 있었다."]], [["“Here it is,”", "\"여기 있습니다.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“_Peste!_”", "\"이런!\" 알베르가 대꾸했다. \"축하하네."], ["returned Albert, “I compliment you on it; I have its fellow”—he took his watch from his waistcoat pocket—“and it cost me 3,000 francs.”", "나도 그것과 짝이 되는 걸 가지고 있지.\" 그는 조끼 주머니에서 제 시계를 꺼냈다. \"이건 삼천 프랑이나 주고 산 거야.\""]], [["“Let us hear the history,”", "\"이야기를 들어 봅시다.\""], ["said Franz, motioning Signor Pastrini to seat himself.", "프란츠가 파스트리니 씨에게 앉으라고 손짓하며 말했다."]], [["“Your excellencies permit it?”", "\"나리들께서 허락하시겠습니까?\""], ["asked the host.", "주인이 물었다."]], [["“_Pardieu!_”", "\"이런 맙소사!\""], ["cried Albert, “you are not a preacher, to remain standing!”", "알베르가 외쳤다. \"자네가 무슨 설교자라고 선 채로 있나!\""]], [["The host sat down, after having made each of them a respectful bow, which meant that he was ready to tell them all they wished to know concerning Luigi Vampa.", "주인은 두 사람에게 각각 공손히 절을 하고서 자리에 앉았다. 그 절은 루이지 밤파에 관해 두 사람이 알고 싶어 하는 것을 죄다 들려줄 채비가 되었다는 뜻이었다."]], [["“You tell me,”", "\"자네 말이.\""], ["said Franz, at the moment Signor Pastrini was about to open his mouth, “that you knew Luigi Vampa when he was a child—he is still a young man, then?”", "파스트리니 씨가 막 입을 열려는 찰나에 프란츠가 말했다. \"루이지 밤파가 어릴 적부터 그를 알았다고 했지. 그럼 그가 아직 젊은이라는 말이군?\""]], [["“A young man?", "\"젊은이냐고요?"], ["he is only two-and-twenty;—he will gain himself a reputation.”", "이제 겨우 스물둘입니다. 앞으로 이름깨나 떨칠 자지요.\""]], [["“What do you think of that, Albert?—at two-and-twenty to be thus famous?”", "\"어떻게 생각하나, 알베르? 스물둘에 그토록 유명하다니?\""]], [["“Yes, and at his age, Alexander, Cæsar, and Napoleon, who have all made some noise in the world, were quite behind him.”", "\"그래, 세상에 적잖이 이름을 떨친 알렉산드로스며 카이사르며 나폴레옹도 그 나이에는 한참 그에게 못 미쳤지.\""]], [["“So,”", "\"그러니까.\""], ["continued Franz, “the hero of this history is only two-and-twenty?”", "프란츠가 말을 이었다. \"이 이야기의 주인공이 겨우 스물둘이란 말이지?\""]], [["“Scarcely so much.”", "\"그것도 채 안 됩니다.\""]], [["“Is he tall or short?”", "\"키가 큰가, 작은가?\""]], [["“Of the middle height—about the same stature as his excellency,”", "\"중키입니다. 저기 나리와 비슷한 키지요.\""], ["returned the host, pointing to Albert.", "주인이 알베르를 가리키며 대답했다."]], [["“Thanks for the comparison,” said Albert, with a bow.", "\"비유해 줘서 고맙군.\" 알베르가 가볍게 인사하며 말했다."]], [["“Go on, Signor Pastrini,”", "\"계속하게, 파스트리니 씨.\""], ["continued Franz, smiling at his friend’s susceptibility.", "친구의 예민함에 미소 지으며 프란츠가 말을 이었다."], ["“To what class of society does he belong?”", "\"그는 어느 계층 출신인가?\""]], [["“He was a shepherd-boy attached to the farm of the Count of San-Felice, situated between Palestrina and the Lake of Gabri; he was born at Pampinara, and entered the count’s service when he was five years old; his father was also a shepherd, who owned a small flock, and lived by the wool and the milk, which he sold at Rome.", "\"그는 산펠리체 백작의 농장에 딸린 양치기 소년이었습니다. 그 농장은 팔레스트리나와 가브리 호수 사이에 있었지요."], ["When quite a child, the little Vampa displayed a most extraordinary precocity.", "그는 팜피나라에서 태어나 다섯 살에 백작의 집에 들어갔습니다."], ["One day, when he was seven years old, he came to the curate of Palestrina, and asked to be taught to read; it was somewhat difficult, for he could not quit his flock; but the good curate went every day to say mass at a little hamlet too poor to pay a priest and which, having no other name, was called Borgo; he told Luigi that he might meet him on his return, and that then he would give him a lesson, warning him that it would be short, and that he must profit as much as possible by it.", "그의 아버지 역시 양치기로, 작은 양 떼를 거느리고 그 양털과 젖을 로마에 내다 팔아 생계를 꾸렸지요. 어린 밤파는 아주 어릴 때부터 비범한 조숙함을 드러냈습니다. 일곱 살 되던 어느 날, 그는 팔레스트리나의 사제를 찾아가 글을 가르쳐 달라고 청했습니다."], ["The child accepted joyfully.", "쉽지 않은 일이었지요."], ["Every day Luigi led his flock to graze on the road that leads from Palestrina to Borgo; every day, at nine o’clock in the morning, the priest and the boy sat down on a bank by the wayside, and the little shepherd took his lesson out of the priest’s breviary.", "양 떼를 떠날 수 없었으니까요. 하지만 그 마음씨 좋은 사제는 사제를 둘 형편이 못 되어 그저 보르고라고만 불리는, 너무 가난해서 다른 이름조차 없는 작은 마을에 날마다 미사를 드리러 갔습니다. 사제는 루이지에게, 자기가 돌아오는 길에 만나면 그때 글을 가르쳐 주겠노라고 했습니다."], ["At the end of three months he had learned to read. This was not enough—he must now learn to write.", "다만 시간이 짧을 테니 되도록 그 시간을 최대한 활용해야 한다고 일러 두었지요. 아이는 기쁘게 받아들였습니다."], ["The priest had a writing teacher at Rome make three alphabets—one large, one middling, and one small; and pointed out to him that by the help of a sharp instrument he could trace the letters on a slate, and thus learn to write.", "날마다 루이지는 팔레스트리나에서 보르고로 가는 길에 양 떼를 풀어놓고 풀을 뜯게 했습니다. 날마다 아침 아홉 시면 사제와 소년은 길가 둑에 앉았고, 어린 양치기는 사제의 성무일도서로 글을 익혔습니다. 석 달이 지나자 그는 글을 읽을 줄 알게 되었지요."], ["The same evening, when the flock was safe at the farm, the little Luigi hastened to the smith at Palestrina, took a large nail, heated and sharpened it, and formed a sort of stylus.", "그것으로는 부족했습니다. 이제 쓰는 법을 배워야 했지요. 사제는 로마의 서예 선생에게 부탁해 큰 것, 중간 것, 작은 것, 이렇게 세 가지 글자판을 만들어 오게 했습니다."], ["The next morning he gathered an armful of pieces of slate and began.", "그리고 끝이 뾰족한 도구로 석판에 글자를 그으면 쓰는 법을 익힐 수 있다고 일러 주었지요."], ["At the end of three months he had learned to write. The curate, astonished at his quickness and intelligence, made him a present of pens, paper, and a penknife.", "그날 저녁, 양 떼를 무사히 농장에 들여놓자마자 어린 루이지는 팔레스트리나의 대장장이에게 달려가 커다란 못을 하나 얻어 와, 그것을 달구고 갈아서 일종의 철필을 만들었습니다. 이튿날 아침 그는 석판 조각을 한 아름 그러모아 시작했지요."], ["This demanded new effort, but nothing compared to the first; at the end of a week he wrote as well with this pen as with the stylus.", "석 달이 지나자 그는 글을 쓸 줄 알게 되었습니다. 그의 명민함과 영리함에 놀란 사제는 그에게 펜과 종이, 그리고 주머니칼을 선물했습니다. 이것은 또 새로운 노력을 요구했지만, 처음에 비할 바는 못 되었지요."], ["The curate related the incident to the Count of San-Felice, who sent for the little shepherd, made him read and write before him, ordered his attendant to let him eat with the domestics, and to give him two piastres a month.", "일주일 만에 그는 철필로 쓸 때만큼 펜으로도 잘 쓰게 되었습니다. 사제가 이 일을 산펠리체 백작에게 들려주자, 백작은 어린 양치기를 불러 자기 앞에서 글을 읽고 쓰게 해 보았습니다. 그러고는 시종에게 일러 그를 하인들과 함께 식사하게 하고, 달마다 두 피아스터를 주도록 했지요."], ["With this, Luigi purchased books and pencils.", "루이지는 그 돈으로 책과 연필을 샀습니다."], ["He applied his imitative powers to everything, and, like Giotto, when young, he drew on his slate sheep, houses, and trees.", "그는 무엇에나 모방의 재능을 발휘했고, 어린 시절의 조토처럼 석판에 양과 집과 나무를 그렸습니다."], ["Then, with his knife, he began to carve all sorts of objects in wood; it was thus that Pinelli, the famous sculptor, had commenced. 20105m", "그러다 칼을 들고 나무에 온갖 물건을 새기기 시작했지요. 그 유명한 조각가 피넬리도 바로 그렇게 시작했답니다."]], [["“A girl of six or seven—that is, a little younger than Vampa—tended sheep on a farm near Palestrina; she was an orphan, born at Valmontone and was named Teresa.", "\"여섯이나 일곱 살쯤 된, 그러니까 밤파보다 조금 어린 소녀가 팔레스트리나 근처 어느 농장에서 양을 치고 있었습니다. 발몬토네에서 태어난 고아였는데, 이름은 테레사였지요."], ["The two children met, sat down near each other, let their flocks mingle together, played, laughed, and conversed together; in the evening they separated the Count of San-Felice’s flock from those of Baron Cervetri, and the children returned to their respective farms, promising to meet the next morning.", "두 아이는 만나서 나란히 앉아 양 떼를 한데 섞이게 두고는 함께 놀고, 함께 웃고, 함께 이야기를 나누었습니다. 저녁이면 산펠리체 백작의 양 떼와 체르베트리 남작의 양 떼를 갈라놓고, 아이들은 이튿날 아침 다시 만나기로 약속하며 각자의 농장으로 돌아갔지요."], ["The next day they kept their word, and thus they grew up together.", "이튿날 아이들은 약속을 지켰고, 그렇게 함께 자랐습니다."], ["Vampa was twelve, and Teresa eleven.", "밤파는 열둘, 테레사는 열한 살이 되었지요."], ["And yet their natural disposition revealed itself.", "그런데 그들의 타고난 기질이 드러나기 시작했습니다."], ["Beside his taste for the fine arts, which Luigi had carried as far as he could in his solitude, he was given to alternating fits of sadness and enthusiasm, was often angry and capricious, and always sarcastic.", "루이지는 고독 속에서 갈 수 있는 데까지 밀고 나간 예술적 취향 외에도, 슬픔과 열정의 발작을 번갈아 겪었고, 자주 화를 냈으며 변덕스럽고 늘 빈정거렸지요."], ["None of the lads of Pampinara, Palestrina, or Valmontone had been able to gain any influence over him or even to become his companion.", "팜피나라며 팔레스트리나, 발몬토네의 어느 사내아이도 그에게 영향을 미치거나 그의 벗이 되지 못했습니다."], ["His disposition (always inclined to exact concessions rather than to make them) kept him aloof from all friendships.", "그의 기질은 (양보를 하기보다는 늘 양보를 받아 내려는 성미였지요) 그를 모든 우정에서 멀어지게 했습니다."], ["Teresa alone ruled by a look, a word, a gesture, this impetuous character, which yielded beneath the hand of a woman, and which beneath the hand of a man might have broken, but could never have been bended.", "오직 테레사만이 눈짓 하나, 말 한마디, 몸짓 하나로 이 격렬한 성격을 다스렸지요. 여자의 손길 아래서는 굽혔지만, 남자의 손길 아래서는 부러질지언정 결코 휘어지지 않을 성격이었습니다."], ["Teresa was lively and gay, but coquettish to excess.", "테레사는 발랄하고 명랑했으나, 지나치게 교태를 부렸습니다."], ["The two piastres that Luigi received every month from the Count of San-Felice’s steward, and the price of all the little carvings in wood he sold at Rome, were expended in ear-rings, necklaces, and gold hairpins.", "루이지가 달마다 산펠리체 백작의 집사에게서 받는 두 피아스터며, 로마에 내다 판 자잘한 나무 조각의 값은 모두 귀고리며 목걸이며 금 머리핀에 쓰였지요."], ["So that, thanks to her friend’s generosity, Teresa was the most beautiful and the best-attired peasant near Rome.", "그리하여 친구의 너그러움 덕분에 테레사는 로마 인근에서 가장 아름답고 가장 잘 차려입은 시골 처녀가 되었습니다."]], [["“The two children grew up together, passing all their time with each other, and giving themselves up to the wild ideas of their different characters.", "\"두 아이는 함께 자라며 모든 시간을 같이 보냈고, 서로 다른 기질에서 비롯된 거친 공상에 빠져들었습니다."], ["Thus, in all their dreams, their wishes, and their conversations, Vampa saw himself the captain of a vessel, general of an army, or governor of a province.", "그리하여 온갖 꿈과 소망과 대화 속에서 밤파는 자신이 배의 선장이나 군대의 장군, 혹은 어느 지방의 총독이 된 모습을 그렸습니다."], ["Teresa saw herself rich, superbly attired, and attended by a train of liveried domestics. Then, when they had thus passed the day in building castles in the air, they separated their flocks, and descended from the elevation of their dreams to the reality of their humble position.", "테레사는 자신이 부유하고 화려하게 차려입은 채, 제복 입은 하인들의 시중을 받는 모습을 그렸지요. 그렇게 하루 종일 허공에 누각을 짓고 나면, 두 사람은 양 떼를 갈라놓고 꿈의 높이에서 내려와 초라한 처지의 현실로 돌아왔습니다."]], [["“One day the young shepherd told the count’s steward that he had seen a wolf come out of the Sabine mountains, and prowl around his flock.", "\"어느 날 어린 양치기는 백작의 집사에게, 사비니 산맥에서 늑대 한 마리가 내려와 양 떼 주위를 어슬렁거리는 것을 보았다고 말했습니다."], ["The steward gave him a gun; this was what Vampa longed for.", "집사가 그에게 총을 한 자루 주었지요. 밤파가 그토록 바라던 것이었습니다."], ["This gun had an excellent barrel, made at Brescia, and carrying a ball with the precision of an English rifle; but one day the count broke the stock, and had then cast the gun aside.", "그 총은 브레시아에서 만든 명품 총신을 지녀, 영국제 소총처럼 정확하게 탄알을 날렸지요. 그런데 어느 날 백작이 개머리판을 부러뜨렸고, 그 뒤로 총을 한쪽에 처박아 두었습니다."], ["This, however, was nothing to a sculptor like Vampa; he examined the broken stock, calculated what change it would require to adapt the gun to his shoulder, and made a fresh stock, so beautifully carved that it would have fetched fifteen or twenty piastres, had he chosen to sell it.", "하지만 밤파 같은 조각가에게 그쯤은 아무것도 아니었지요. 그는 부러진 개머리판을 살펴보고, 총을 제 어깨에 맞추려면 어떤 손질이 필요한지 가늠하고는 새 개머리판을 만들었습니다. 어찌나 아름답게 새겼던지, 마음만 먹고 내다 팔았다면 열다섯이나 스무 피아스터는 받았을 겁니다."], ["But nothing could be farther from his thoughts.", "하지만 그건 그의 생각과는 거리가 멀었지요."]], [["“For a long time a gun had been the young man’s greatest ambition.", "\"오래전부터 총은 그 젊은이의 가장 큰 야망이었습니다."], ["In every country where independence has taken the place of liberty, the first desire of a manly heart is to possess a weapon, which at once renders him capable of defence or attack, and, by rendering its owner terrible, often makes him feared.", "자유 대신 독립이 자리 잡은 모든 나라에서, 사내다운 마음이 처음으로 품는 소망은 무기를 갖는 것입니다. 무기는 그를 단번에 방어할 수 있고 공격할 수도 있게 해 주며, 그 주인을 무섭게 만들어 종종 두려움의 대상이 되게 하니까요. 이때부터 밤파는 모든 여가를 이 귀중한 무기를 능숙하게 다루는 데 바쳤습니다."], ["From this moment Vampa devoted all his leisure time to perfecting himself in the use of his precious weapon; he purchased powder and ball, and everything served him for a mark—the trunk of some old and moss-grown olive-tree, that grew on the Sabine mountains; the fox, as he quitted his earth on some marauding excursion; the eagle that soared above their heads: and thus he soon became so expert, that Teresa overcame the terror she at first felt at the report, and amused herself by watching him direct the ball wherever he pleased, with as much accuracy as if he placed it by hand.", "화약과 탄알을 사들였고, 무엇이든 과녁이 되었지요. 사비니 산맥에 자란, 이끼 낀 늙은 올리브 나무 둥치며, 무언가를 노략질하러 굴을 나서는 여우며, 머리 위로 날아오르는 독수리까지 말입니다. 그리하여 그는 곧 빼어난 솜씨를 갖추게 되었고, 처음에는 총소리에 겁을 먹던 테레사도 그 두려움을 이겨 내고는, 그가 손으로 직접 갖다 놓기라도 한 듯 정확하게 탄알을 원하는 곳에 박아 넣는 모습을 지켜보며 즐거워했습니다."]], [["20107m\n“One evening a wolf emerged from a pine-wood near which they were usually stationed, but the wolf had scarcely advanced ten yards ere he was dead.", "\"어느 날 저녁, 두 사람이 늘 머물던 곳 근처의 소나무 숲에서 늑대 한 마리가 나타났습니다. 그런데 그 늑대는 채 열 걸음도 나오기 전에 죽고 말았지요."], ["Proud of this exploit, Vampa took the dead animal on his shoulders, and carried him to the farm.", "이 위업을 자랑스러워하며 밤파는 죽은 짐승을 어깨에 둘러메고 농장으로 가져갔습니다."], ["These exploits had gained Luigi considerable reputation.", "이런 무용담은 루이지에게 적잖은 명성을 안겨 주었지요."], ["The man of superior abilities always finds admirers, go where he will.", "뛰어난 능력을 지닌 사람은 어디를 가든 늘 찬미하는 이를 얻기 마련입니다."], ["He was spoken of as the most adroit, the strongest, and the most courageous _contadino_ for ten leagues around; and although Teresa was universally allowed to be the most beautiful girl of the Sabines, no one had ever spoken to her of love, because it was known that she was beloved by Vampa.", "사람들은 그를 사방 십 리에서 가장 날쌔고 힘세고 용감한 시골 청년이라고 입에 올렸습니다. 그리고 테레사가 사비니 일대에서 가장 아름다운 처녀라는 데 누구나 동의했지만, 아무도 그녀에게 사랑을 고백하지 않았지요. 그녀가 밤파의 사랑을 받고 있다는 걸 알았으니까요."], ["And yet the two young people had never declared their affection; they had grown together like two trees whose roots are mingled, whose branches intertwined, and whose intermingled perfume rises to the heavens.", "그런데도 두 젊은이는 한 번도 서로의 애정을 말로 드러낸 적이 없었습니다. 두 사람은 뿌리가 한데 얽히고 가지가 서로 휘감기며, 뒤섞인 향기가 하늘로 피어오르는 두 그루 나무처럼 함께 자랐지요."], ["Only their wish to see each other had become a necessity, and they would have preferred death to a day’s separation.", "다만 서로를 보고 싶다는 바람이 어느새 없어서는 안 될 일이 되어, 하루를 떨어져 지내느니 차라리 죽음을 택했을 겁니다."]], [["“Teresa was sixteen, and Vampa seventeen. About this time, a band of brigands that had established itself in the Lepini mountains began to be much spoken of.", "\"테레사는 열여섯, 밤파는 열일곱이 되었습니다. 무렵에 레피니 산맥에 자리 잡은 한 도적 떼의 소문이 자자해지기 시작했지요."], ["The brigands have never been really extirpated from the neighborhood of Rome.", "로마 인근에서 도적은 한 번도 완전히 뿌리 뽑힌 적이 없었습니다."], ["Sometimes a chief is wanted, but when a chief presents himself he rarely has to wait long for a band of followers.", "이따금 두목이 모자랄 뿐, 두목이 나타나기만 하면 추종자 무리를 모으는 데는 좀처럼 오래 걸리지 않았지요."]], [["“The celebrated Cucumetto, pursued in the Abruzzo, driven out of the kingdom of Naples, where he had carried on a regular war, had crossed the Garigliano, like Manfred, and had taken refuge on the banks of the Amasine between Sonnino and Juperno.", "\"그 유명한 쿠쿠메토는 아브루초에서 쫓기고, 정규전을 벌이던 나폴리 왕국에서 쫓겨난 뒤, 만프레디처럼 가릴리아노강을 건너 손니노와 유페르노 사이 아마시네강 기슭으로 피신했습니다."], ["He strove to collect a band of followers, and followed the footsteps of Decesaris and Gasparone, whom he hoped to surpass.", "그는 추종자 무리를 모으려 애쓰며, 자기가 능가하기를 바랐던 데체사리와 가스파로네의 발자취를 좇았지요."], ["Many young men of Palestrina, Frascati, and Pampinara had disappeared.", "팔레스트리나며 프라스카티며 팜피나라의 젊은이들이 여럿 사라졌습니다."], ["Their disappearance at first caused much disquietude; but it was soon known that they had joined Cucumetto.", "처음에는 그 실종이 큰 불안을 일으켰지만, 그들이 쿠쿠메토에게 가담했다는 사실이 곧 알려졌지요."], ["After some time Cucumetto became the object of universal attention; the most extraordinary traits of ferocious daring and brutality were related of him.", "얼마 뒤 쿠쿠메토는 모든 이의 이목을 끄는 인물이 되었습니다. 그의 잔혹하고 대담무쌍한 행적과 야만성에 관한 더없이 기이한 이야기들이 떠돌았지요."]], [["“One day he carried off a young girl, the daughter of a surveyor of Frosinone.", "\"어느 날 그는 프로시노네의 어느 측량 기사의 딸인 젊은 처녀를 납치했습니다."], ["The bandit’s laws are positive; a young girl belongs first to him who carries her off, then the rest draw lots for her, and she is abandoned to their brutality until death relieves her sufferings.", "도적의 율법은 단호하지요. 젊은 처녀는 우선 그녀를 납치한 자의 것이 되고, 그다음에는 나머지가 제비를 뽑아 그녀를 차지하며, 죽음이 그 고통을 끝내 줄 때까지 그들의 잔학함에 내맡겨집니다."], ["When their parents are sufficiently rich to pay a ransom, a messenger is sent to negotiate; the prisoner is hostage for the security of the messenger; should the ransom be refused, the prisoner is irrevocably lost.", "부모가 몸값을 치를 만큼 넉넉하면 흥정하러 사자를 보내는데, 그 사자의 안전을 담보하는 것이 바로 포로지요. 몸값을 거절당하면, 포로는 돌이킬 수 없이 끝장이 납니다."], ["The young girl’s lover was in Cucumetto’s troop; his name was Carlini.", "그 젊은 처녀의 연인이 쿠쿠메토의 무리에 있었습니다. 이름은 카를리니였지요."], ["When she recognized her lover, the poor girl extended her arms to him, and believed herself safe; but Carlini felt his heart sink, for he but too well knew the fate that awaited her.", "가엾은 처녀는 연인을 알아보자 두 팔을 뻗으며 이제 살았다고 믿었습니다. 하지만 카를리니는 가슴이 내려앉았지요. 그녀를 기다리는 운명을 너무도 잘 알았으니까요."], ["However, as he was a favorite with Cucumetto, as he had for three years faithfully served him, and as he had saved his life by shooting a dragoon who was about to cut him down, he hoped the chief would have pity on him.", "그러나 그는 쿠쿠메토의 총애를 받았고, 삼 년 동안 충실히 섬겼으며, 두목을 베려던 기병을 쏘아 그의 목숨을 구한 적도 있었던지라, 두목이 자기를 가엾이 여겨 주기를 바랐습니다."], ["He took Cucumetto one side, while the young girl, seated at the foot of a huge pine that stood in the centre of the forest, made a veil of her picturesque head-dress to hide her face from the lascivious gaze of the bandits.", "그는 쿠쿠메토를 한쪽으로 데려갔습니다. 그동안 젊은 처녀는 숲 한가운데 선 거대한 소나무 밑동에 앉아, 그림처럼 아름다운 머리 장식으로 베일을 만들어 음탕한 도적들의 눈길에서 얼굴을 가렸지요."], ["There he told the chief all—his affection for the prisoner, their promises of mutual fidelity, and how every night, since he had been near, they had met in some neighboring ruins. 20109m", "그곳에서 그는 두목에게 모든 것을 털어놓았습니다. 포로에 대한 자신의 애정, 서로 굳게 맺은 정절의 약속, 그리고 가까이 온 뒤로 밤마다 인근 폐허에서 만나 왔다는 것까지요."]], [["“It so happened that night that Cucumetto had sent Carlini to a village, so that he had been unable to go to the place of meeting.", "\"하필 그날 밤 쿠쿠메토가 카를리니를 어느 마을로 보낸 탓에, 그는 만남의 장소에 나갈 수 없었습니다."], ["Cucumetto had been there, however, by accident, as he said, and had carried the maiden off.", "그런데 쿠쿠메토가 우연히 그곳에 갔다가, 본인 말로는, 그 처녀를 납치해 왔다는 것이었지요."], ["Carlini besought his chief to make an exception in Rita’s favor, as her father was rich, and could pay a large ransom.", "카를리니는 두목에게 리타만은 예외로 해 달라고 간청했습니다. 그녀의 아버지가 부유해서 두둑한 몸값을 치를 수 있다면서요."], ["Cucumetto seemed to yield to his friend’s entreaties, and bade him find a shepherd to send to Rita’s father at Frosinone.", "쿠쿠메토는 친구의 간청에 못 이기는 척하며, 양치기를 하나 구해 프로시노네의 리타 아버지에게 보내라고 일렀습니다."]], [["“Carlini flew joyfully to Rita, telling her she was saved, and bidding her write to her father, to inform him what had occurred, and that her ransom was fixed at three hundred piastres.", "\"카를리니는 기쁨에 차 리타에게 날아가, 이제 살았다고 말하고는 아버지에게 편지를 써서 그동안의 일을 알리고 몸값이 삼백 피아스터로 정해졌다는 것도 전하라고 했습니다."], ["Twelve hours’ delay was all that was granted—that is, until nine the next morning.", "허락된 말미는 단 열두 시간, 그러니까 이튿날 아침 아홉 시까지였지요."], ["The instant the letter was written, Carlini seized it, and hastened to the plain to find a messenger.", "편지가 다 쓰이자마자 카를리니는 그것을 움켜쥐고 사자를 찾으러 들판으로 달려갔습니다."], ["He found a young shepherd watching his flock.", "그는 양 떼를 지키는 젊은 양치기를 발견했지요."], ["The natural messengers of the bandits are the shepherds who live between the city and the mountains, between civilized and savage life.", "도적들의 천연 전령은 바로 도시와 산 사이, 문명과 야만의 삶 사이에 사는 양치기들입니다."], ["The boy undertook the commission, promising to be in Frosinone in less than an hour.", "소년은 그 심부름을 맡아, 한 시간도 안 돼 프로시노네에 닿겠노라고 약속했습니다."], ["Carlini returned, anxious to see his mistress, and announce the joyful intelligence.", "카를리니는 연인을 보고 기쁜 소식을 전하고 싶은 마음에 서둘러 돌아왔지요."], ["He found the troop in the glade, supping off the provisions exacted as contributions from the peasants; but his eye vainly sought Rita and Cucumetto among them.", "그는 무리가 빈터에서, 농부들에게 공물로 뜯어낸 식량으로 저녁을 먹고 있는 것을 보았습니다. 하지만 아무리 둘러보아도 그들 가운데 리타와 쿠쿠메토는 없었지요."]], [["“He inquired where they were, and was answered by a burst of laughter.", "\"그가 두 사람이 어디 있느냐고 묻자, 한바탕 웃음이 답으로 돌아왔습니다."], ["A cold perspiration burst from every pore, and his hair stood on end.", "온몸의 땀구멍에서 식은땀이 솟고, 머리카락이 곤두섰지요."], ["He repeated his question.", "그가 다시 물었습니다."], ["One of the bandits rose, and offered him a glass filled with Orvietto, saying, ‘To the health of the brave Cucumetto and the fair Rita.’ At this moment Carlini heard a woman’s cry; he divined the truth, seized the glass, broke it across the face of him who presented it, and rushed towards the spot whence the cry came.", "도적 하나가 일어나, 오르비에토를 가득 채운 잔을 내밀며 말했습니다. '용감한 쿠쿠메토와 어여쁜 리타의 건강을 위하여.' 그 순간 카를리니는 여자의 비명을 들었습니다."], ["After a hundred yards he turned the corner of the thicket; he found Rita senseless in the arms of Cucumetto.", "그는 진상을 알아챘지요. 잔을 낚아채 그것을 내민 자의 얼굴에 박살을 내고는, 비명이 들려온 쪽으로 내달렸습니다."], ["At the sight of Carlini, Cucumetto rose, a pistol in each hand.", "백 걸음쯤 가서 덤불 모퉁이를 돌자, 쿠쿠메토의 품에 정신을 잃고 늘어진 리타가 보였습니다."], ["The two brigands looked at each other for a moment—the one with a smile of lasciviousness on his lips, the other with the pallor of death on his brow.", "카를리니를 보자 쿠쿠메토는 양손에 권총을 한 자루씩 들고 일어섰지요. 두 도적은 잠시 서로를 노려보았습니다. 한 사람은 입가에 음탕한 미소를 띠고, 다른 한 사람은 이마에 죽음 같은 창백함을 띠고서 말입니다."], ["A terrible battle between the two men seemed imminent; but by degrees Carlini’s features relaxed, his hand, which had grasped one of the pistols in his belt, fell to his side.", "두 사내 사이에 끔찍한 격투가 임박한 듯했지요. 그러나 차츰 카를리니의 표정이 풀어지더니, 허리띠의 권총 한 자루를 움켜쥐었던 손이 옆으로 떨어졌습니다."], ["Rita lay between them. The moon lighted the group.", "리타가 두 사람 사이에 누워 있었습니다. 달빛이 그 무리를 비추었지요."]], [["“‘Well,’ said Cucumetto, ‘have you executed your commission?’", "\"'그래.' 쿠쿠메토가 말했다. '심부름은 마쳤나?'"]], [["“‘Yes, captain,’ returned Carlini.", "\"'네, 두목.' 카를리니가 대답했다."], ["‘At nine o’clock tomorrow Rita’s father will be here with the money.’", "'내일 아홉 시면 리타의 아버지가 돈을 가지고 이리로 올 겁니다.'"]], [["“‘It is well; in the meantime, we will have a merry night; this young girl is charming, and does credit to your taste.", "\"'좋아. 그동안 우리는 즐거운 밤을 보내야지. 이 처녀는 매력적이로군."], ["Now, as I am not egotistical, we will return to our comrades and draw lots for her.’", "자네 취향을 칭찬할 만해. 자, 나는 이기적인 사람이 아니니, 동료들에게 돌아가 이 여자를 두고 제비를 뽑세.'"]], [["“‘You have determined, then, to abandon her to the common law?’ said Carlini.", "\"'그럼 그녀를 공동의 율법에 내맡기기로 하셨군요?' 카를리니가 말했다."]], [["“‘Why should an exception be made in her favor?’", "\"'어째서 그녀에게만 예외를 두어야 하나?'"]], [["“‘I thought that my entreaties——’", "\"'제 간청이라면……'"]], [["“‘What right have you, any more than the rest, to ask for an exception?’", "\"'네가 다른 자들보다 무슨 권리로 예외를 청하나?'"]], [["“‘It is true.’", "\"'옳은 말씀입니다.'"]], [["“‘But never mind,’ continued Cucumetto, laughing, ‘sooner or later your turn will come.’ Carlini’s teeth clenched convulsively.", "\"'하지만 걱정 말게.' 쿠쿠메토가 웃으며 말을 이었다. '머잖아 자네 차례도 올 테니.' 카를리니의 이가 발작하듯 악물렸다."]], [["“‘Now, then,’ said Cucumetto, advancing towards the other bandits, ‘are you coming?’", "\"'자, 그럼.' 쿠쿠메토가 다른 도적들 쪽으로 다가가며 말했다. '오겠나?'"]], [["“‘I follow you.’", "\"'따라가겠습니다.'"]], [["20111m\n“Cucumetto departed, without losing sight of Carlini, for, doubtless, he feared lest he should strike him unawares; but nothing betrayed a hostile design on Carlini’s part.", "\"쿠쿠메토는 카를리니에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 떠났습니다. 분명 그가 불시에 자신을 칠까 두려웠던 게지요. 하지만 카를리니에게서는 적의를 드러내는 기색이라곤 없었습니다."], ["He was standing, his arms folded, near Rita, who was still insensible.", "그는 여전히 정신을 잃은 채 누워 있는 리타 곁에 팔짱을 끼고 서 있었지요."], ["Cucumetto fancied for a moment the young man was about to take her in his arms and fly; but this mattered little to him now Rita had been his; and as for the money, three hundred piastres distributed among the band was so small a sum that he cared little about it.", "쿠쿠메토는 한순간, 저 젊은이가 그녀를 품에 안고 달아나려는 게 아닐까 싶었습니다. 하지만 리타가 이미 자기 것이 된 마당에 그쯤은 이제 그에게 별일도 아니었지요. 돈으로 말하자면, 무리에게 나누어 줄 삼백 피아스터쯤은 하잘것없는 액수라 그는 거의 신경 쓰지 않았습니다."], ["He continued to follow the path to the glade; but, to his great surprise, Carlini arrived almost as soon as himself.", "그는 계속 빈터로 가는 길을 따라갔지요. 그런데 놀랍게도 카를리니가 그와 거의 동시에 도착했습니다."]], [["“‘Let us draw lots!", "\"'제비를 뽑자!"], ["let us draw lots!’ cried all the brigands, when they saw the chief.", "제비를 뽑자!' 두목이 나타나는 것을 보자 도적들이 일제히 외쳤습니다."]], [["“Their demand was fair, and the chief inclined his head in sign of acquiescence.", "\"그들의 요구는 정당했고, 두목은 승낙의 표시로 고개를 끄덕였습니다."], ["The eyes of all shone fiercely as they made their demand, and the red light of the fire made them look like demons.", "모두의 눈이 그 요구를 하며 사납게 번득였고, 모닥불의 붉은 빛이 그들을 악마처럼 보이게 했지요."], ["The names of all, including Carlini, were placed in a hat, and the youngest of the band drew forth a ticket; the ticket bore the name of Diavolaccio.", "카를리니를 포함한 모두의 이름이 모자 속에 담겼고, 무리에서 가장 어린 자가 표를 하나 뽑았습니다. 표에는 디아볼라치오라는 이름이 적혀 있었지요."], ["He was the man who had proposed to Carlini the health of their chief, and to whom Carlini replied by breaking the glass across his face.", "바로 카를리니에게 두목의 건강을 위해 건배하자고 잔을 내밀었다가, 카를리니가 그 잔으로 얼굴을 박살 내며 답했던 그 사내였습니다."], ["A large wound, extending from the temple to the mouth, was bleeding profusely.", "관자놀이에서 입까지 길게 이어진 커다란 상처에서 피가 철철 흐르고 있었지요."], ["Diavolaccio, seeing himself thus favored by fortune, burst into a loud laugh.", "디아볼라치오는 행운이 제게 이렇게 미소 짓는 것을 보고 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸습니다."]], [["“‘Captain,’ said he, ‘just now Carlini would not drink your health when I proposed it to him; propose mine to him, and let us see if he will be more condescending to you than to me.’", "\"'두목.' 그가 말했다. '방금 카를리니는 제가 권하는데도 두목의 건강을 위해 마시지 않았습니다. 그에게 제 건강을 위해 마시라고 권해 보십시오. 제게보다 두목에게는 더 너그러운지 봅시다.'"]], [["“Everyone expected an explosion on Carlini’s part; but to their great surprise, he took a glass in one hand and a flask in the other, and filling it,—", "\"모두가 카를리니의 분노가 터지리라 예상했지요. 하지만 놀랍게도 그는 한 손에 잔을, 다른 손에 술병을 들고 잔을 채웠습니다."]], [["“‘Your health, Diavolaccio,’ said he calmly, and he drank it off, without his hand trembling in the least.", "\"'자네의 건강을 위해, 디아볼라치오.' 그가 침착하게 말하고는, 손을 조금도 떨지 않고 단숨에 들이켰습니다."], ["Then sitting down by the fire, ‘My supper,’ said he; ‘my expedition has given me an appetite.’", "그러고는 모닥불 옆에 앉으며 말했지요. '저녁을 다오. 다녀왔더니 입맛이 도는군.'"]], [["“‘Well done, Carlini!’ cried the brigands; ‘that is acting like a good fellow;’ and they all formed a circle round the fire, while Diavolaccio disappeared.", "\"'잘했어, 카를리니!' 도적들이 외쳤습니다. '사내답게 구는군.' 그러고는 모두 모닥불을 빙 둘러앉았고, 디아볼라치오는 자취를 감추었습니다."]], [["“Carlini ate and drank as if nothing had happened.", "\"카를리니는 아무 일도 없었다는 듯 먹고 마셨습니다."], ["The bandits looked on with astonishment at this singular conduct until they heard footsteps.", "도적들은 그 기이한 행동을 의아하게 바라보다가, 발소리를 들었지요."], ["They turned round, and saw Diavolaccio bearing the young girl in his arms.", "돌아보니 디아볼라치오가 젊은 처녀를 품에 안고 오고 있었습니다."], ["Her head hung back, and her long hair swept the ground.", "그녀의 머리는 뒤로 늘어졌고, 긴 머리카락이 땅을 쓸었지요."], ["As they entered the circle, the bandits could perceive, by the firelight, the unearthly pallor of the young girl and of Diavolaccio.", "그들이 둘러앉은 자리로 들어서자, 도적들은 모닥불 빛에 젊은 처녀와 디아볼라치오의 시신 같은 창백함을 알아볼 수 있었습니다."], ["This apparition was so strange and so solemn, that everyone rose, with the exception of Carlini, who remained seated, and ate and drank calmly.", "이 출현이 어찌나 기이하고 엄숙했던지, 카를리니만 빼고 모두가 일어섰지요. 카를리니는 앉은 채로 태연히 먹고 마실 뿐이었습니다."], ["Diavolaccio advanced amidst the most profound silence, and laid Rita at the captain’s feet.", "디아볼라치오는 더없이 깊은 침묵 속에서 다가와, 리타를 두목의 발치에 내려놓았습니다."], ["Then everyone could understand the cause of the unearthly pallor in the young girl and the bandit.", "그제야 모두가 젊은 처녀와 도적의 시신 같은 창백함의 까닭을 알 수 있었지요."], ["A knife was plunged up to the hilt in Rita’s left breast.", "칼 한 자루가 자루까지 깊이 리타의 왼쪽 가슴에 박혀 있었습니다."], ["Everyone looked at Carlini; the sheath at his belt was empty.", "모두가 카를리니를 바라보았지요. 그의 허리띠에 매인 칼집은 비어 있었습니다."]], [["“‘Ah, ah,’ said the chief, ‘I now understand why Carlini stayed behind.’", "\"'아하, 아하.' 두목이 말했다. '이제 알겠군. 카를리니가 왜 뒤에 남았는지.'"]], [["“All savage natures appreciate a desperate deed.", "\"야만적인 본성을 지닌 자들은 모두 결사적인 행위를 높이 삽니다."], ["No other of the bandits would, perhaps, have done the same; but they all understood what Carlini had done.", "다른 도적이라면 아마 똑같이 하지 못했겠지요. 하지만 그들 모두 카를리니가 한 일을 이해했습니다."]], [["“‘Now, then,’ cried Carlini, rising in his turn, and approaching the corpse, his hand on the butt of one of his pistols, ‘does anyone dispute the possession of this woman with me?’", "\"'자, 그럼.' 카를리니가 이번에는 자기가 일어서서 시신에 다가가며, 권총 한 자루의 손잡이에 손을 얹고 외쳤습니다. '이 여자를 두고 나와 다툴 자가 있나?'"]], [["“‘No,’ returned the chief, ‘she is thine.’", "\"'없다.' 두목이 대답했다. '그 여자는 네 것이다.'"]], [["“Carlini raised her in his arms, and carried her out of the circle of firelight.", "\"카를리니는 그녀를 품에 안고 모닥불 빛이 닿는 원 밖으로 나갔습니다."], ["Cucumetto placed his sentinels for the night, and the bandits wrapped themselves in their cloaks, and lay down before the fire.", "쿠쿠메토는 밤을 위해 보초들을 세웠고, 도적들은 외투를 둘러쓰고 모닥불 앞에 누웠지요."], ["At midnight the sentinel gave the alarm, and in an instant all were on the alert.", "자정 무렵 보초가 경보를 울리자, 순식간에 모두가 경계 태세에 들어갔습니다."], ["It was Rita’s father, who brought his daughter’s ransom in person.", "리타의 아버지가 직접 딸의 몸값을 가지고 온 것이었지요."]], [["“‘Here,’ said he, to Cucumetto, ‘here are three hundred piastres; give me back my child.", "\"'자.' 그가 쿠쿠메토에게 말했다. '여기 삼백 피아스터가 있소. 내 자식을 돌려주시오.'"]], [["“But the chief, without taking the money, made a sign to him to follow.", "\"하지만 두목은 돈을 받지 않고, 그에게 따라오라고 손짓했습니다."], ["The old man obeyed.", "노인은 따랐지요."], ["They both advanced beneath the trees, through whose branches streamed the moonlight.", "두 사람은 가지 사이로 달빛이 쏟아져 내리는 나무들 밑으로 나아갔습니다."], ["Cucumetto stopped at last, and pointed to two persons grouped at the foot of a tree.", "쿠쿠메토는 마침내 걸음을 멈추고, 어느 나무 밑동에 모여 있는 두 사람을 가리켰지요."]], [["“‘There,’ said he, ‘demand thy child of Carlini; he will tell thee what has become of her;’ and he returned to his companions.", "\"'저기.' 그가 말했다. '카를리니에게 자네 딸을 내놓으라고 하게. 그가 딸이 어찌 되었는지 말해 줄 테니.' 그러고는 동료들에게로 돌아갔습니다."]], [["“The old man remained motionless; he felt that some great and unforeseen misfortune hung over his head.", "\"노인은 꼼짝 않고 서 있었습니다. 그는 어떤 크고 예기치 못한 불행이 제 머리 위에 드리워져 있음을 느꼈지요."], ["At length he advanced toward the group, the meaning of which he could not comprehend.", "마침내 그는 그 무리 쪽으로 다가갔습니다. 그 광경의 뜻을 헤아릴 수 없었지요."], ["As he approached, Carlini raised his head, and the forms of two persons became visible to the old man’s eyes.", "그가 다가가자 카를리니가 고개를 들었고, 노인의 눈에 두 사람의 형상이 또렷이 드러났습니다."], ["A woman lay on the ground, her head resting on the knees of a man, who was seated by her; as he raised his head, the woman’s face became visible.", "한 여자가 땅에 누워 있었고, 그 머리는 곁에 앉은 사내의 무릎에 얹혀 있었지요. 사내가 고개를 들자, 여자의 얼굴이 드러났습니다."], ["The old man recognized his child, and Carlini recognized the old man.", "노인은 제 자식을 알아보았고, 카를리니는 노인을 알아보았지요."]], [["“‘I expected thee,’ said the bandit to Rita’s father.", "\"'당신을 기다리고 있었습니다.' 도적이 리타의 아버지에게 말했다."]], [["“‘Wretch!’ returned the old man, ‘what hast thou done?’ and he gazed with terror on Rita, pale and bloody, a knife buried in her bosom.", "\"'이 악당아!' 노인이 대답했다. '네놈이 무슨 짓을 한 거냐?'"], ["A ray of moonlight poured through the trees, and lighted up the face of the dead.", "그러고는 창백하게 피투성이가 된 채 가슴에 칼이 박힌 리타를 공포에 질려 바라보았습니다. 한 줄기 달빛이 나무들 사이로 쏟아져 죽은 자의 얼굴을 비추었지요."]], [["“‘Cucumetto had violated thy daughter,’ said the bandit; ‘I loved her, therefore I slew her; for she would have served as the sport of the whole band.’ The old man spoke not, and grew pale as death. ‘Now,’ continued Carlini, ‘if I have done wrongly, avenge her;’ and withdrawing the knife from the wound in Rita’s bosom, he held it out to the old man with one hand, while with the other he tore open his vest.", "\"'쿠쿠메토가 당신 딸을 욕보였습니다.' 도적이 말했다. '나는 그녀를 사랑했기에 죽였습니다. 안 그랬으면 그녀는 무리 전체의 노리개가 되었을 테니까요.' 노인은 아무 말도 못 한 채 죽음처럼 창백해졌습니다. '자.' 카를리니가 말을 이었다. '내가 잘못했다면, 그녀의 원수를 갚으시오.' 그러고는 리타의 가슴에 박힌 칼을 뽑아, 한 손으로 노인에게 내밀고 다른 손으로는 제 조끼를 활짝 열어젖혔습니다."]], [["“‘Thou hast done well!’ returned the old man in a hoarse voice; ‘embrace me, my son.’", "\"'자네는 옳은 일을 했네!' 노인이 쉰 목소리로 대답했다. '나를 안아 주게, 내 아들아.'"]], [["20115m\nCarlini threw himself, sobbing like a child, into the arms of his mistress’s father.", "\"카를리니는 어린아이처럼 흐느끼며 연인의 아버지의 품에 몸을 던졌습니다."], ["These were the first tears the man of blood had ever wept.", "그것은 그 피의 사내가 난생처음 흘려 본 눈물이었지요."]], [["“‘Now,’ said the old man, ‘aid me to bury my child.’ Carlini fetched two pickaxes; and the father and the lover began to dig at the foot of a huge oak, beneath which the young girl was to repose.", "\"'자.' 노인이 말했다. '내 자식을 묻는 것을 도와주게.'"], ["When the grave was formed, the father embraced her first, and then the lover; afterwards, one taking the head, the other the feet, they placed her in the grave.", "카를리니가 곡괭이 두 자루를 가져왔습니다. 아버지와 연인은 거대한 떡갈나무 밑동을 파기 시작했지요. 그 아래에 젊은 처녀를 뉘기로 한 것입니다."], ["Then they knelt on each side of the grave, and said the prayers of the dead.", "무덤이 다 만들어지자, 아버지가 먼저 그녀를 끌어안았고, 그다음 연인이 끌어안았습니다."], ["Then, when they had finished, they cast the earth over the corpse, until the grave was filled.", "그런 뒤 한 사람은 머리를, 다른 한 사람은 발을 들어 그녀를 무덤에 뉘었지요. 그러고는 무덤 양쪽에 무릎을 꿇고 죽은 이를 위한 기도를 올렸습니다. 기도를 마치자, 무덤이 가득 찰 때까지 시신 위에 흙을 덮었지요."], ["Then, extending his hand, the old man said; ‘I thank you, my son; and now leave me alone.’", "그런 다음 노인은 손을 내밀며 말했습니다. '고맙네, 내 아들아. 이제 나를 혼자 있게 해 주게.'"]], [["“‘Yet——’ replied Carlini.", "\"'하지만……' 카를리니가 대답했다."]], [["“‘Leave me, I command you.’", "\"'나를 두고 가게, 명령일세.'"]], [["“Carlini obeyed, rejoined his comrades, folded himself in his cloak, and soon appeared to sleep as soundly as the rest.", "\"카를리니는 따랐습니다. 동료들에게 돌아가 외투를 둘러쓰고는, 곧 다른 자들처럼 깊이 잠든 듯이 보였지요."], ["It had been resolved the night before to change their encampment.", "전날 밤 그들은 야영지를 옮기기로 정해 둔 터였습니다."], ["An hour before daybreak, Cucumetto aroused his men, and gave the word to march.", "동트기 한 시간 전, 쿠쿠메토는 부하들을 깨우고 행군 명령을 내렸지요."], ["But Carlini would not quit the forest, without knowing what had become of Rita’s father.", "하지만 카를리니는 리타의 아버지가 어찌 되었는지 알지 못한 채로는 숲을 떠나려 하지 않았습니다."], ["He went toward the place where he had left him.", "그는 노인을 두고 온 자리로 갔지요."], ["He found the old man suspended from one of the branches of the oak which shaded his daughter’s grave.", "노인은 제 딸의 무덤에 그늘을 드리운 떡갈나무 가지 하나에 목을 맨 채였습니다."], ["He then took an oath of bitter vengeance over the dead body of the one and the tomb of the other.", "그러자 그는 한 사람의 시신과 다른 한 사람의 무덤 위에서 쓰라린 복수의 맹세를 했지요."], ["But he was unable to complete this oath, for two days afterwards, in an encounter with the Roman carbineers, Carlini was killed.", "하지만 그는 그 맹세를 끝맺지 못했습니다. 이틀 뒤 로마 기병들과의 충돌에서 카를리니가 죽었으니까요."], ["There was some surprise, however, that, as he was with his face to the enemy, he should have received a ball between his shoulders.", "그런데 한 가지 의아한 것은, 그가 적을 향해 얼굴을 두고 있었는데도 어깨 사이에 총알을 맞았다는 점이었습니다."], ["That astonishment ceased when one of the brigands remarked to his comrades that Cucumetto was stationed ten paces in Carlini’s rear when he fell.", "그 놀라움은, 카를리니가 쓰러질 때 쿠쿠메토가 그의 뒤 열 걸음 자리에 있었다고 한 도적이 동료들에게 일러 주자 가셨지요."], ["On the morning of the departure from the forest of Frosinone he had followed Carlini in the darkness, and heard this oath of vengeance, and, like a wise man, anticipated it.", "프로시노네 숲을 떠나던 날 아침, 그는 어둠 속에서 카를리니를 뒤따라가 그 복수의 맹세를 들었고, 영악한 자답게 미리 손을 쓴 것이었습니다."]], [["“They told ten other stories of this bandit chief, each more singular than the other.", "\"사람들은 이 도적 두목에 관해 또 다른 열 가지 이야기를 들려주었는데, 하나같이 더 기이했지요."], ["Thus, from Fondi to Perusia, everyone trembles at the name of Cucumetto.", "그리하여 폰디에서 페루자에 이르기까지 모두가 쿠쿠메토라는 이름에 벌벌 떨었습니다."]], [["“These narratives were frequently the theme of conversation between Luigi and Teresa.", "\"이런 이야기들은 곧잘 루이지와 테레사의 대화 주제가 되었습니다."], ["The young girl trembled very much at hearing the stories; but Vampa reassured her with a smile, tapping the butt of his good fowling-piece, which threw its ball so well; and if that did not restore her courage, he pointed to a crow, perched on some dead branch, took aim, touched the trigger, and the bird fell dead at the foot of the tree.", "젊은 처녀는 그 이야기를 들으며 몹시 떨었지만, 밤파는 탄알을 그토록 잘 날려 보내는 자신의 멋진 사냥총 개머리판을 톡톡 치며 미소로 그녀를 안심시켰지요. 그래도 그녀의 용기가 살아나지 않으면, 그는 죽은 나뭇가지에 앉은 까마귀를 가리켜 겨눈 뒤 방아쇠를 당겼고, 그러면 새는 나무 밑동에 떨어져 죽었습니다."], ["Time passed on, and the two young people had agreed to be married when Vampa should be twenty and Teresa nineteen years of age.", "세월이 흘렀고, 두 젊은이는 밤파가 스무 살, 테레사가 열아홉 살이 되면 결혼하기로 약속했습니다."], ["They were both orphans, and had only their employers’ leave to ask, which had been already sought and obtained.", "둘 다 고아라, 청해야 할 것은 고용주들의 허락뿐이었는데, 그것도 이미 청하여 얻어 둔 터였지요."], ["One day when they were talking over their plans for the future, they heard two or three reports of firearms, and then suddenly a man came out of the wood, near which the two young persons used to graze their flocks, and hurried towards them.", "어느 날 두 사람이 미래의 계획을 의논하고 있는데, 총소리가 두세 발 들리더니 갑자기 한 사내가, 두 젊은이가 늘 양 떼를 풀어놓던 숲 근처에서 튀어나와 그들 쪽으로 달려왔습니다."], ["When he came within hearing, he exclaimed:\n‘I am pursued; can you conceal me?’", "그는 목소리가 닿을 만큼 가까워지자 외쳤지요. '쫓기고 있소. 나를 숨겨 주겠소?'"]], [["“They knew full well that this fugitive must be a bandit; but there is an innate sympathy between the Roman brigand and the Roman peasant and the latter is always ready to aid the former.", "\"두 사람은 이 도망자가 도적임을 잘 알았습니다. 하지만 로마의 산적과 로마의 농민 사이에는 타고난 동질감이 있어, 후자는 늘 전자를 기꺼이 도왔지요."], ["Vampa, without saying a word, hastened to the stone that closed up the entrance to their grotto, drew it away, made a sign to the fugitive to take refuge there, in a retreat unknown to everyone, closed the stone upon him, and then went and resumed his seat by Teresa.", "밤파는 한마디 말도 없이, 자기들 동굴 입구를 막은 돌로 달려가 그것을 치우고는, 도망자에게 아무도 모르는 그 은신처에 몸을 숨기라고 손짓했습니다. 그러고는 다시 돌로 입구를 막고, 가서 테레사 곁에 도로 앉았지요."], ["Instantly afterwards four carbineers, on horseback, appeared on the edge of the wood; three of them appeared to be looking for the fugitive, while the fourth dragged a brigand prisoner by the neck.", "곧이어 말을 탄 기병 넷이 숲 가장자리에 나타났습니다. 그중 셋은 도망자를 찾는 듯했고, 넷째는 도적 하나를 포로로 목덜미를 잡아끌고 있었지요."], ["The three carbineers looked about carefully on every side, saw the young peasants, and galloping up, began to question them. They had seen no one.", "세 기병은 사방을 주의 깊게 둘러보다가 젊은 농민들을 발견하고는, 말을 달려 와 캐묻기 시작했습니다. 두 사람은 아무도 보지 못했다고 했지요."]], [["“‘That is very annoying,’ said the brigadier; for the man we are looking for is the chief.’", "\"'그것 참 곤란하군.' 분대장이 말했다. '우리가 찾는 자가 바로 두목인데.'"]], [["“‘Cucumetto?’ cried Luigi and Teresa at the same moment.", "\"'쿠쿠메토요?' 루이지와 테레사가 동시에 외쳤습니다."]], [["“‘Yes,’ replied the brigadier; ‘and as his head is valued at a thousand Roman crowns, there would have been five hundred for you, if you had helped us to catch him.’ The two young persons exchanged looks.", "\"'그렇다.' 분대장이 대답했다. '그자의 목에 로마 크라운 천 냥이 걸려 있으니, 너희가 그를 잡는 걸 도왔다면 오백 냥은 받았을 텐데.'"], ["The brigadier had a moment’s hope.", "두 젊은이는 서로 눈길을 주고받았습니다."], ["Five hundred Roman crowns are three thousand lire, and three thousand lire are a fortune for two poor orphans who are going to be married.", "분대장은 한순간 희망을 품었지요. 로마 크라운 오백 냥은 삼천 리라이고, 삼천 리라면 결혼을 앞둔 가난한 두 고아에게는 한밑천이었으니까요."]], [["“‘Yes, it is very annoying,’ said Vampa; ‘but we have not seen him.’", "\"'그래요, 정말 곤란하군요.' 밤파가 말했다. '하지만 우리는 그를 보지 못했습니다.'"]], [["“Then the carbineers scoured the country in different directions, but in vain; then, after a time, they disappeared.", "\"그러자 기병들은 사방으로 흩어져 일대를 샅샅이 뒤졌지만 허사였지요. 그러다 얼마 뒤 그들은 사라졌습니다."], ["Vampa then removed the stone, and Cucumetto came out.", "밤파는 그제야 돌을 치웠고, 쿠쿠메토가 나왔지요."], ["Through the crevices in the granite he had seen the two young peasants talking with the carbineers, and guessed the subject of their parley.", "화강암 틈으로 그는 두 젊은 농민이 기병들과 이야기하는 것을 보았고, 그 담판의 내용을 짐작했습니다."], ["He had read in the countenances of Luigi and Teresa their steadfast resolution not to surrender him, and he drew from his pocket a purse full of gold, which he offered to them.", "그는 루이지와 테레사의 얼굴에서 자기를 넘기지 않겠다는 확고한 결의를 읽고는, 주머니에서 금화가 가득 든 지갑을 꺼내 두 사람에게 내밀었습니다."], ["But Vampa raised his head proudly; as to Teresa, her eyes sparkled when she thought of all the fine gowns and gay jewellery she could buy with this purse of gold.", "하지만 밤파는 도도하게 고개를 쳐들었지요. 테레사로 말하자면, 이 금화 지갑으로 살 수 있는 온갖 고운 드레스며 화사한 보석을 떠올리자 두 눈이 반짝였습니다."]], [["“Cucumetto was a cunning fiend, and had assumed the form of a brigand instead of a serpent, and this look from Teresa showed to him that she was a worthy daughter of Eve, and he returned to the forest, pausing several times on his way, under the pretext of saluting his protectors.", "\"쿠쿠메토는 교활한 악마라, 뱀 대신 도적의 모습을 빌려 나타난 자였지요. 테레사의 그 눈빛을 보고 그는 그녀가 이브의 어엿한 딸임을 알아챘습니다. 그는 가는 길에 몇 번이나 걸음을 멈추고는, 자기를 지켜 준 이들에게 인사를 한다는 핑계로 뒤를 돌아보며 숲으로 돌아갔습니다."]], [["“Several days elapsed, and they neither saw nor heard of Cucumetto. The time of the Carnival was at hand.", "\"며칠이 흘렀지만, 두 사람은 쿠쿠메토를 보지도 그 소식을 듣지도 못했습니다. 카니발 철이 다가왔지요."], ["The Count of San-Felice announced a grand masked ball, to which all that were distinguished in Rome were invited.", "산펠리체 백작이 성대한 가면무도회를 연다고 알렸는데, 로마의 내로라하는 인사들이 모두 초대받았습니다."], ["Teresa had a great desire to see this ball.", "테레사는 이 무도회를 몹시 보고 싶어 했지요."], ["Luigi asked permission of his protector, the steward, that she and he might be present amongst the servants of the house.", "루이지는 자기 후견인인 집사에게, 자기와 그녀가 그 집 하인들 틈에 끼어 무도회에 참석해도 되겠냐고 청했습니다."], ["This was granted.", "허락이 떨어졌지요."], ["The ball was given by the Count for the particular pleasure of his daughter Carmela, whom he adored.", "무도회는 백작이 끔찍이 아끼는 딸 카르멜라를 특별히 기쁘게 해 주려고 마련한 것이었습니다."], ["Carmela was precisely the age and figure of Teresa, and Teresa was as handsome as Carmela.", "카르멜라는 나이도 몸매도 테레사와 꼭 같았고, 테레사는 카르멜라만큼이나 아름다웠지요."], ["On the evening of the ball Teresa was attired in her best, her most brilliant ornaments in her hair, and gayest glass beads,—she was in the costume of the women of Frascati.", "무도회가 열리던 날 저녁, 테레사는 가장 좋은 옷을 차려입고, 머리에 가장 화려한 장식과 가장 화사한 유리구슬을 달았습니다. 프라스카티 여인들의 의상이었지요."], ["Luigi wore the very picturesque garb of the Roman peasant at holiday time.", "루이지는 명절 때 입는 로마 농민의 더없이 그림 같은 옷차림이었습니다."], ["They both mingled, as they had leave to do, with the servants and peasants.", "두 사람은 허락받은 대로 하인들과 농민들 틈에 섞여 들었지요."]], [["“The _festa_ was magnificent; not only was the villa brilliantly illuminated, but thousands of colored lanterns were suspended from the trees in the garden; and very soon the palace overflowed to the terraces, and the terraces to the garden-walks.", "\"잔치는 장려했습니다. 별장이 휘황하게 불을 밝혔을 뿐 아니라, 정원의 나무들에는 수천 개의 색등이 매달렸지요. 이내 사람들이 궁전에서 테라스로, 테라스에서 정원의 산책로로 넘쳐흘렀습니다."], ["At each cross-path was an orchestra, and tables spread with refreshments; the guests stopped, formed quadrilles, and danced in any part of the grounds they pleased.", "갈림길마다 악단이 있었고, 다과를 차린 탁자가 놓여 있었지요. 손님들은 걸음을 멈추고 카드리유를 짜서, 마음에 드는 어디서든 춤을 추었습니다."], ["Carmela was attired like a woman of Sonnino.", "카르멜라는 손니노 여인처럼 차려입었지요."], ["Her cap was embroidered with pearls, the pins in her hair were of gold and diamonds, her girdle was of Turkey silk, with large embroidered flowers, her bodice and skirt were of cashmere, her apron of Indian muslin, and the buttons of her corset were of jewels.", "모자는 진주로 수놓아졌고, 머리에 꽂은 핀은 금과 다이아몬드였으며, 허리띠는 큼직한 꽃이 수놓인 터키 비단이었습니다. 보디스와 치마는 캐시미어, 앞치마는 인도산 모슬린이었고, 코르셋 단추는 보석이었지요."], ["Two of her companions were dressed, the one as a woman of Nettuno, and the other as a woman of La Riccia.", "동행한 그녀의 두 동무는 하나는 네투노 여인으로, 다른 하나는 라리차 여인으로 차려입었습니다."], ["Four young men of the richest and noblest families of Rome accompanied them with that Italian freedom which has not its parallel in any other country in the world.", "로마에서 가장 부유하고 가장 고귀한 가문의 젊은이 넷이 그들을 수행했는데, 세계 어느 나라에서도 견줄 데 없는 이탈리아식 자유분방함이 묻어났지요."], ["They were attired as peasants of Albano, Velletri, Civita-Castellana, and Sora.", "그들은 알바노, 벨레트리, 치비타카스텔라나, 소라의 농민으로 차려입었습니다."], ["We need hardly add that these peasant costumes, like those of the young women, were brilliant with gold and jewels.", "굳이 덧붙일 것도 없지만, 이 농민 의상들은 젊은 처녀들의 의상처럼 금과 보석으로 휘황찬란했지요."]], [["“Carmela wished to form a quadrille, but there was one lady wanting.", "\"카르멜라는 카드리유를 한 조 짜고 싶었지만, 여자 한 명이 모자랐습니다."], ["Carmela looked all around her, but not one of the guests had a costume similar to her own, or those of her companions.", "카르멜라는 사방을 둘러보았으나, 손님들 가운데 자기나 자기 동무들과 비슷한 의상을 한 사람은 하나도 없었지요."], ["The Count of San-Felice pointed out Teresa, who was hanging on Luigi’s arm in a group of peasants.", "산펠리체 백작이, 농민 무리 틈에서 루이지의 팔에 매달려 있는 테레사를 가리켰습니다."]], [["“‘Will you allow me, father?’ said Carmela.", "\"'허락해 주시겠어요, 아버지?' 카르멜라가 말했다."]], [["“‘Certainly,’ replied the count, ‘are we not in Carnival time?’", "\"'그럼.' 백작이 대답했다. '지금이 카니발 철이 아니냐?'"]], [["“Carmela turned towards the young man who was talking with her, and saying a few words to him, pointed with her finger to Teresa.", "\"카르멜라는 자기와 이야기하던 젊은이 쪽으로 돌아서서 몇 마디 건네고는, 손가락으로 테레사를 가리켰습니다."], ["The young man looked, bowed in obedience, and then went to Teresa, and invited her to dance in a quadrille directed by the count’s daughter.", "젊은이는 바라보더니 순순히 고개를 숙였고, 테레사에게 가서 백작 딸이 이끄는 카드리유에서 함께 춤추자고 청했지요."], ["Teresa felt a flush pass over her face; she looked at Luigi, who could not refuse his assent.", "테레사는 얼굴이 화끈 달아오르는 것을 느꼈습니다. 그녀는 루이지를 바라보았지만, 그도 동의하지 않을 수 없었지요."], ["Luigi slowly relinquished Teresa’s arm, which he had held beneath his own, and Teresa, accompanied by her elegant cavalier, took her appointed place with much agitation in the aristocratic quadrille.", "루이지는 제 팔 밑에 끼고 있던 테레사의 팔을 천천히 놓아 주었고, 테레사는 그 우아한 기사와 함께 잔뜩 떨리는 마음으로 귀족들의 카드리유에서 정해진 자리에 섰습니다."], ["Certainly, in the eyes of an artist, the exact and strict costume of Teresa had a very different character from that of Carmela and her companions; and Teresa was frivolous and coquettish, and thus the embroidery and muslins, the cashmere waist-girdles, all dazzled her, and the reflection of sapphires and diamonds almost turned her giddy brain.", "분명 예술가의 눈으로 보자면, 정확하고 엄정한 테레사의 의상은 카르멜라며 그 동무들의 의상과는 사뭇 다른 멋을 지녔지요. 하지만 테레사는 경박하고 교태가 심한 처녀라, 그 자수와 모슬린, 캐시미어 허리띠가 그녀의 눈을 죄다 부시게 했고, 사파이어와 다이아몬드의 광채에 가벼운 머리가 거의 어질어질해졌습니다."]], [["“Luigi felt a sensation hitherto unknown arising in his mind. It was like an acute pain which gnawed at his heart, and then thrilled through his whole body.", "\"루이지는 여태껏 알지 못하던 어떤 감각이 마음속에서 솟아오르는 것을 느꼈습니다. 그것은 심장을 갉아먹다가 온몸으로 전율처럼 퍼지는 날카로운 통증 같았지요."], ["He followed with his eye each movement of Teresa and her cavalier; when their hands touched, he felt as though he should swoon; every pulse beat with violence, and it seemed as though a bell were ringing in his ears.", "그는 테레사와 그 기사의 움직임 하나하나를 눈으로 좇았습니다. 두 사람의 손이 닿을 때면 그는 정신을 잃을 것만 같았고, 맥박이 마구 뛰었으며, 귓속에서 종이 울리는 듯했지요."], ["When they spoke, although Teresa listened timidly and with downcast eyes to the conversation of her cavalier, as Luigi could read in the ardent looks of the good-looking young man that his language was that of praise, it seemed as if the whole world was turning round with him, and all the voices of hell were whispering in his ears ideas of murder and assassination.", "두 사람이 이야기를 나눌 때면, 테레사는 그 기사의 말에 수줍게 눈을 내리깔고 귀를 기울였지만, 루이지는 그 잘생긴 젊은이의 뜨거운 눈빛에서 그가 찬사를 늘어놓고 있음을 읽을 수 있었습니다. 그러면 온 세상이 자기와 함께 빙빙 도는 듯했고, 지옥의 온갖 목소리가 살인과 암살의 생각을 귓가에 속삭이는 듯했지요."], ["Then fearing that his paroxysm might get the better of him, he clutched with one hand the branch of a tree against which he was leaning, and with the other convulsively grasped the dagger with a carved handle which was in his belt, and which, unwittingly, he drew from the scabbard from time to time.", "발작이 자신을 집어삼킬까 두려워진 그는 한 손으로 기대고 선 나뭇가지를 움켜쥐고, 다른 손으로는 허리띠에 꽂힌, 손잡이를 새긴 단검을 발작하듯 거머쥐었습니다. 그러고는 자기도 모르게 이따금 그것을 칼집에서 뽑아 들곤 했지요."]], [["“Luigi was jealous!", "\"루이지는 질투에 사로잡혔던 것입니다!"]], [["“He felt that, influenced by her ambitions and coquettish disposition, Teresa might escape him.", "\"그는, 야심과 교태 어린 기질에 휘둘려 테레사가 자기에게서 달아날지도 모른다는 것을 느꼈습니다."]], [["“The young peasant girl, at first timid and scared, soon recovered herself.", "\"처음에는 수줍고 겁먹었던 젊은 시골 처녀는 곧 평정을 되찾았습니다."], ["We have said that Teresa was handsome, but this is not all; Teresa was endowed with all those wild graces which are so much more potent than our affected and studied elegancies.", "앞서 테레사가 아름답다고 했지만, 그게 다가 아니었지요. 테레사는 우리의 꾸미고 다듬은 우아함보다 훨씬 더 강렬한, 온갖 야성적인 매력을 타고났습니다."], ["She had almost all the honors of the quadrille, and if she were envious of the Count of San-Felice’s daughter, we will not undertake to say that Carmela was not jealous of her.", "카드리유의 영예는 거의 그녀의 차지였고, 그녀가 산펠리체 백작 딸을 부러워했다면, 카르멜라 역시 그녀를 질투하지 않았다고는 장담하지 못하겠습니다."], ["And with overpowering compliments her handsome cavalier led her back to the place whence he had taken her, and where Luigi awaited her.", "잘생긴 기사는 넘치도록 찬사를 늘어놓으며 그녀를 데려왔던 자리, 루이지가 기다리고 있는 그곳으로 다시 데려다주었습니다."], ["Twice or thrice during the dance the young girl had glanced at Luigi, and each time she saw that he was pale and that his features were agitated, once even the blade of his knife, half drawn from its sheath, had dazzled her eyes with its sinister glare.", "춤추는 동안 두세 번 젊은 처녀는 루이지를 흘끗 보았는데, 그때마다 그가 창백하고 표정이 일그러져 있는 것을 보았지요. 한번은 칼집에서 반쯤 뽑힌 단검의 날이 불길한 빛으로 그녀의 눈을 부시게 하기도 했습니다."], ["Thus, it was almost tremblingly that she resumed her lover’s arm.", "그리하여 그녀는 거의 떨면서 연인의 팔을 다시 잡았지요."], ["The quadrille had been most perfect, and it was evident there was a great demand for a repetition, Carmela alone objecting to it, but the Count of San-Felice besought his daughter so earnestly, that she acceded.", "카드리유는 더없이 완벽했고, 다시 한번 추자는 요청이 빗발친 게 분명했지만, 카르멜라만은 반대했습니다. 그러나 산펠리체 백작이 어찌나 간곡히 청하던지, 딸은 결국 응했지요."]], [["“One of the cavaliers then hastened to invite Teresa, without whom it was impossible for the quadrille to be formed, but the young girl had disappeared.", "\"그러자 기사 하나가 서둘러 테레사를 청하러 나섰습니다. 그녀가 없으면 카드리유를 짤 수 없었으니까요. 하지만 젊은 처녀는 사라지고 없었습니다."]], [["“The truth was, that Luigi had not felt the strength to support another such trial, and, half by persuasion and half by force, he had removed Teresa toward another part of the garden.", "\"실은 루이지가 그런 시련을 한 번 더 견딜 힘이 없어, 반은 설득으로 반은 완력으로 테레사를 정원의 다른 쪽으로 데려간 것이었습니다."], ["Teresa had yielded in spite of herself, but when she looked at the agitated countenance of the young man, she understood by his silence and trembling voice that something strange was passing within him.", "테레사는 마지못해 따라갔지만, 그 젊은이의 일그러진 얼굴을 보자, 그의 침묵과 떨리는 목소리에서 무언가 심상찮은 일이 그의 안에서 일어나고 있음을 알아챘지요."], ["She herself was not exempt from internal emotion, and without having done anything wrong, yet fully comprehended that Luigi was right in reproaching her.", "그녀 자신도 속으로 동요가 없지 않았고, 아무 잘못도 저지르지 않았으면서도, 루이지가 자기를 나무라는 게 옳다는 것을 충분히 헤아렸습니다."], ["Why, she did not know, but yet she did not the less feel that these reproaches were merited.", "왜인지는 알 수 없었지만, 그래도 그 책망이 마땅하다는 것만은 못지않게 느꼈지요."]], [["“However, to Teresa’s great astonishment, Luigi remained mute, and not a word escaped his lips the rest of the evening.", "\"그런데 테레사로서는 몹시 놀랍게도, 루이지는 입을 다문 채 그날 저녁 내내 한마디도 내뱉지 않았습니다."], ["When the chill of the night had driven away the guests from the gardens, and the gates of the villa were closed on them for the _festa_ in-doors, he took Teresa quite away, and as he left her at her home, he said:", "밤기운이 손님들을 정원에서 몰아내고, 실내의 잔치를 위해 별장의 문이 그들 뒤로 닫히자, 그는 테레사를 멀찍이 데리고 나왔지요. 그리고 그녀를 집에 데려다주며 말했습니다."]], [["“‘Teresa, what were you thinking of as you danced opposite the young Countess of San-Felice?’", "\"'테레사, 너는 젊은 산펠리체 백작 영애와 마주 보고 춤추면서 무슨 생각을 했지?'"]], [["“‘I thought,’ replied the young girl, with all the frankness of her nature, ‘that I would give half my life for a costume such as she wore.’", "\"'생각했어요.' 젊은 처녀가 타고난 솔직함으로 대답했다. '저 사람이 입은 것 같은 옷을 가질 수 있다면 내 목숨의 절반이라도 내놓겠다고요.'"]], [["“‘And what said your cavalier to you?’", "\"'그래, 그 기사는 너한테 뭐라고 했지?'"]], [["“‘He said it only depended on myself to have it, and I had only one word to say.’", "\"'그건 나한테 달린 일이고, 한마디만 하면 된다고 했어요.'"]], [["“‘He was right,’ said Luigi.", "\"'그자 말이 옳았어.' 루이지가 말했다."], ["‘Do you desire it as ardently as you say?’", "'네 말처럼 그렇게 간절히 그걸 원하나?'"]], [["“‘Yes.’", "\"'네.'"]], [["“‘Well, then, you shall have it!’", "\"'좋아, 그럼 너는 그걸 갖게 될 거야!'"]], [["“The young girl, much astonished, raised her head to look at him, but his face was so gloomy and terrible that her words froze to her lips.", "\"젊은 처녀는 몹시 놀라 그를 보려고 고개를 들었지만, 그의 얼굴이 어찌나 음울하고 무섭던지 입가에서 말이 얼어붙었습니다."], ["As Luigi spoke thus, he left her.", "루이지는 그렇게 말하고 그녀를 떠났지요."], ["Teresa followed him with her eyes into the darkness as long as she could, and when he had quite disappeared, she went into the house with a sigh.", "테레사는 어둠 속으로 사라지는 그를 보일 때까지 눈으로 좇았고, 그가 완전히 자취를 감추자 한숨을 내쉬며 집으로 들어갔습니다."]], [["20121m\n“That night a memorable event occurred, due, no doubt, to the imprudence of some servant who had neglected to extinguish the lights.", "\"그날 밤 잊지 못할 사건이 벌어졌습니다. 틀림없이 불을 끄는 것을 소홀히 한 어느 하인의 부주의 탓이었지요."], ["The Villa of San-Felice took fire in the rooms adjoining the very apartment of the lovely Carmela.", "산펠리체 별장은 어여쁜 카르멜라의 방에 바로 잇닿은 방들에서 불이 났습니다."], ["Awakened in the night by the light of the flames, she sprang out of bed, wrapped herself in a dressing-gown, and attempted to escape by the door, but the corridor by which she hoped to fly was already a prey to the flames.", "한밤중 불길의 빛에 잠을 깬 그녀는 침대에서 뛰쳐나와 실내복을 걸치고 문으로 빠져나가려 했지만, 달아나려던 복도는 이미 불길에 휩싸여 있었지요."], ["She then returned to her room, calling for help as loudly as she could, when suddenly her window, which was twenty feet from the ground, was opened, a young peasant jumped into the chamber, seized her in his arms, and with superhuman skill and strength conveyed her to the turf of the grass-plot, where she fainted.", "그녀는 다시 방으로 돌아와 있는 힘껏 도움을 청했습니다. 그때 갑자기 땅에서 이십 자쯤 떨어진 창문이 열리더니, 젊은 농민 하나가 방 안으로 뛰어들어 그녀를 품에 안고는, 초인적인 솜씨와 힘으로 잔디밭 위로 그녀를 옮겼지요. 그곳에서 그녀는 정신을 잃었습니다."], ["When she recovered, her father was by her side.", "정신을 차렸을 때, 곁에는 아버지가 있었지요."], ["All the servants surrounded her, offering her assistance.", "하인들이 모두 그녀를 둘러싸고 거들겠다고 나섰습니다."], ["An entire wing of the villa was burnt down; but what of that, as long as Carmela was safe and uninjured?", "별장의 한쪽 동이 통째로 타 버렸지만, 카르멜라가 무사하고 다치지 않았으니 그쯤이야 무슨 대수겠습니까?"]], [["“Her preserver was everywhere sought for, but he did not appear; he was inquired after, but no one had seen him.", "\"사람들이 사방으로 그녀의 구원자를 찾았지만, 그는 나타나지 않았습니다. 그를 수소문했으나, 아무도 그를 본 사람이 없었지요."], ["Carmela was greatly troubled that she had not recognized him.", "카르멜라는 그를 알아보지 못한 것이 몹시 마음에 걸렸습니다."]], [["“As the count was immensely rich, excepting the danger Carmela had run,—and the marvellous manner in which she had escaped, made that appear to him rather a favor of Providence than a real misfortune,—the loss occasioned by the conflagration was to him but a trifle.", "\"백작은 엄청난 부자라, 카르멜라가 겪은 위험만 빼면, 그것도 그녀가 기적처럼 벗어난 방식 때문에 그에게는 차라리 진짜 불행이라기보다 섭리의 은총처럼 여겨졌으니, 화재로 입은 손실쯤은 그에게 하잘것없었지요."]], [["“The next day, at the usual hour, the two young peasants were on the borders of the forest. Luigi arrived first.", "\"이튿날 여느 때처럼 두 젊은 농민은 숲 어귀에 나왔습니다. 루이지가 먼저 도착했지요."], ["He came toward Teresa in high spirits, and seemed to have completely forgotten the events of the previous evening.", "그는 들뜬 기색으로 테레사에게 다가왔는데, 전날 저녁의 일은 까맣게 잊은 듯했습니다."], ["The young girl was very pensive, but seeing Luigi so cheerful, she on her part assumed a smiling air, which was natural to her when she was not excited or in a passion.", "젊은 처녀는 몹시 시름에 잠겨 있었지만, 루이지가 저토록 쾌활한 것을 보자, 그녀도 흥분하거나 화나지 않을 때면 자연스레 짓던 미소 띤 표정을 지었지요."]], [["“Luigi took her arm beneath his own, and led her to the door of the grotto.", "\"루이지는 그녀의 팔을 제 팔에 끼고 동굴 입구로 이끌었습니다."], ["Then he paused. The young girl, perceiving that there was something extraordinary, looked at him steadfastly.", "그러고는 걸음을 멈추었지요. 무언가 심상찮은 일이 있음을 알아챈 젊은 처녀가 그를 뚫어지게 바라보았습니다."]], [["“‘Teresa,’ said Luigi, ‘yesterday evening you told me you would give all the world to have a costume similar to that of the count’s daughter.’", "\"'테레사.' 루이지가 말했다. '어제저녁 너는 백작 딸이 입은 것 같은 옷을 가질 수만 있다면 세상의 무엇이라도 내놓겠다고 했지.'"]], [["“‘Yes,’ replied Teresa with astonishment; ‘but I was mad to utter such a wish.’", "\"'네.' 테레사가 놀라며 대답했다. '하지만 그런 소원을 입에 담다니, 내가 정신이 나갔던 거예요.'"]], [["“‘And I replied, “Very well, you shall have it.”’", "\"'그래서 나는 대답했어. 「좋아, 너는 그걸 갖게 될 거야」라고.'"]], [["“‘Yes,’ replied the young girl, whose astonishment increased at every word uttered by Luigi, ‘but of course your reply was only to please me.’", "\"'네.' 루이지가 한마디 할 때마다 놀라움이 더해 가는 젊은 처녀가 대답했다. '하지만 물론 당신 대답은 그저 나를 기쁘게 해 주려던 거였잖아요.'"]], [["“‘I have promised no more than I have given you, Teresa,’ said Luigi proudly.", "\"'나는 너에게 준 것 이상으로 약속한 적이 없어, 테레사.' 루이지가 도도하게 말했다."], ["‘Go into the grotto and dress yourself.’", "'동굴 안으로 들어가 옷을 갈아입어.'"]], [["“At these words he drew away the stone, and showed Teresa the grotto, lighted up by two wax lights, which burnt on each side of a splendid mirror; on a rustic table, made by Luigi, were spread out the pearl necklace and the diamond pins, and on a chair at the side was laid the rest of the costume.", "\"이렇게 말하며 그는 돌을 치우고, 테레사에게 동굴을 보여 주었습니다. 동굴은 멋진 거울 양옆에서 타오르는 밀랍 초 두 자루로 환히 밝혀져 있었지요. 루이지가 만든 투박한 탁자 위에는 진주 목걸이와 다이아몬드 핀이 펼쳐져 있었고, 옆 의자에는 나머지 의상이 놓여 있었습니다."]], [["“Teresa uttered a cry of joy, and, without inquiring whence this attire came, or even thanking Luigi, darted into the grotto, transformed into a dressing-room.", "\"테레사는 기쁨의 탄성을 질렀고, 이 차림새가 어디서 났는지 묻지도, 루이지에게 고맙다는 인사조차 하지 않은 채, 탈의실로 변한 동굴 안으로 뛰어들었지요."]], [["“Luigi pushed the stone behind her, for on the crest of a small adjacent hill which cut off the view toward Palestrina, he saw a traveller on horseback, stopping a moment, as if uncertain of his road, and thus presenting against the blue sky that perfect outline which is peculiar to distant objects in southern climes.", "\"루이지는 그녀의 뒤로 돌을 밀어 닫았습니다. 팔레스트리나 쪽 시야를 가로막는 인근의 자그마한 언덕 마루에, 말을 탄 길손 하나가 마치 제 길을 몰라 망설이듯 잠시 멈춰 선 게 보였기 때문이지요. 그렇게 그는 푸른 하늘을 배경으로, 남쪽 지방의 먼 사물 특유의 완벽한 윤곽을 드러내고 있었습니다."], ["When he saw Luigi, he put his horse into a gallop and advanced toward him.", "루이지를 보자 길손은 말에 박차를 가해 그에게 다가왔지요."]], [["“Luigi was not mistaken.", "\"루이지의 짐작은 틀리지 않았습니다."], ["The traveller, who was going from Palestrina to Tivoli, had mistaken his way; the young man directed him; but as at a distance of a quarter of a mile the road again divided into three ways, and on reaching these the traveller might again stray from his route, he begged Luigi to be his guide.", "팔레스트리나에서 티볼리로 가던 길손이 길을 잘못 든 것이었지요. 젊은이가 길을 일러 주었습니다. 하지만 사 분의 일 마일쯤 가면 길이 다시 세 갈래로 갈리는데, 거기에 이르면 길손이 또 길을 잘못 들 수도 있었던지라, 그는 루이지에게 길잡이가 되어 달라고 청했습니다."]], [["“Luigi threw his cloak on the ground, placed his carbine on his shoulder, and freed from his heavy covering, preceded the traveller with the rapid step of a mountaineer, which a horse can scarcely keep up with.", "\"루이지는 외투를 땅에 벗어 던지고 소총을 어깨에 멘 뒤, 무거운 겉옷에서 벗어나 산사람 특유의 날랜 걸음으로 길손을 앞장섰지요. 말이 좀처럼 따라잡기 힘든 걸음이었습니다."], ["In ten minutes Luigi and the traveller reached the cross-roads.", "십 분 만에 루이지와 길손은 갈림길에 닿았지요."], ["On arriving there, with an air as majestic as that of an emperor, he stretched his hand towards that one of the roads which the traveller was to follow.", "거기 이르자 그는 황제처럼 위풍당당한 태도로, 길손이 가야 할 그 길을 향해 손을 뻗었습니다."]], [["“‘That is your road, excellency, and now you cannot again mistake.’", "\"'저것이 나리께서 가실 길입니다. 이제 다시는 잘못 드실 일이 없을 겁니다.'"]], [["“‘And here is your recompense,’ said the traveller, offering the young herdsman some small pieces of money.", "\"'그럼 이건 자네 사례일세.' 길손이 어린 목동에게 잔돈 몇 닢을 내밀며 말했다."]], [["“‘Thank you,’ said Luigi, drawing back his hand; ‘I render a service, I do not sell it.’", "\"'고맙습니다만.' 루이지가 손을 거두며 말했다. '저는 도움을 베푸는 것이지, 팔지는 않습니다.'"]], [["“‘Well,’ replied the traveller, who seemed used to this difference between the servility of a man of the cities and the pride of the mountaineer, ‘if you refuse wages, you will, perhaps, accept a gift.’", "\"'그렇군.' 도시 사람의 비굴함과 산사람의 자존심 사이의 이런 차이에 익숙한 듯한 길손이 대답했다. '품삯은 마다하더라도, 선물이라면 아마 받겠지.'"]], [["“‘Ah, yes, that is another thing.’", "\"'아, 네, 그건 다른 이야기지요.'"]], [["“‘Then,’ said the traveller, ‘take these two Venetian sequins and give them to your bride, to make herself a pair of earrings.’", "\"'그럼.' 길손이 말했다. '이 베네치아 제키노 두 닢을 받아, 신부에게 주어 귀고리 한 쌍을 장만하게 하게.'"]], [["“‘And then do you take this poniard,’ said the young herdsman; ‘you will not find one better carved between Albano and Civita-Castellana.’", "\"'그럼 나리께서는 이 단검을 받으십시오.' 어린 목동이 말했다. '알바노에서 치비타카스텔라나에 이르도록 이보다 더 잘 새긴 것은 찾지 못하실 겁니다.'"]], [["“‘I accept it,’ answered the traveller, ‘but then the obligation will be on my side, for this poniard is worth more than two sequins.’", "\"'받겠네.' 길손이 대답했다. '하지만 그러면 빚은 내 쪽이 지는 셈이군. 이 단검은 제키노 두 닢보다 값이 더 나가니 말일세.'"]], [["“‘For a dealer perhaps; but for me, who engraved it myself, it is hardly worth a piastre.’", "\"'장사꾼한테야 그렇겠지요. 하지만 제 손으로 직접 새긴 저에게는 한 피아스터도 못 되는 물건입니다.'"]], [["“‘What is your name?’ inquired the traveller.", "\"'자네 이름이 무엇인가?' 길손이 물었다."]], [["“‘Luigi Vampa,’ replied the shepherd, with the same air as he would have replied, Alexander, King of Macedon.", "\"'루이지 밤파입니다.' 목동이, 마케도니아 왕 알렉산드로스라고 대답하듯 그런 태도로 대답했다."], ["‘And yours?’", "'나리는요?'"]], [["“‘I,’ said the traveller, ‘am called Sinbad the Sailor.’”", "\"'나는.' 길손이 말했다. '뱃사람 신드바드라 하네.'\""]], [["Franz d’Épinay started with surprise.", "프란츠 데피네가 깜짝 놀라며 움찔했다."]], [["“Sinbad the Sailor?”", "\"뱃사람 신드바드라고?\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["replied the narrator; “that was the name which the traveller gave to Vampa as his own.”", "이야기꾼이 대답했다. \"그것이 길손이 밤파에게 일러 준 자기 이름이었습니다.\""]], [["“Well, and what may you have to say against this name?”", "\"그래, 그 이름에 대해 자네는 무슨 할 말이라도 있나?\""], ["inquired Albert; “it is a very pretty name, and the adventures of the gentleman of that name amused me very much in my youth, I must confess.”", "알베르가 물었다. \"아주 예쁜 이름이군. 솔직히 말해, 그 이름의 신사가 펼친 모험은 내 어린 시절에 나를 무척 즐겁게 해 주었지.\""]], [["Franz said no more. The name of Sinbad the Sailor, as may well be supposed, awakened in him a world of recollections, as had the name of the Count of Monte Cristo on the previous evening.", "프란츠는 더 말하지 않았다. 능히 짐작하겠지만, 뱃사람 신드바드라는 이름은, 전날 저녁의 몬테크리스토 백작이라는 이름이 그러했듯, 그의 마음속에 온갖 기억의 세계를 일깨웠던 것이다."]], [["“Proceed!”", "\"계속하게!\""], ["said he to the host.", "그가 주인에게 말했다."]], [["“Vampa put the two sequins haughtily into his pocket, and slowly returned by the way he had gone.", "\"밤파는 제키노 두 닢을 거만하게 주머니에 넣고, 왔던 길을 천천히 되돌아갔습니다."], ["As he came within two or three hundred paces of the grotto, he thought he heard a cry.", "동굴에서 이삼백 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳에 이르렀을 때, 그는 비명 소리를 들은 듯했지요."], ["He listened to know whence this sound could proceed.", "그 소리가 어디서 나는지 알아내려고 귀를 기울였습니다."], ["A moment afterwards he thought he heard his own name pronounced distinctly.", "잠시 뒤 자기 이름이 또렷이 불리는 것을 들은 듯했지요."]], [["“The cry proceeded from the grotto.", "\"비명은 동굴에서 나고 있었습니다."], ["He bounded like a chamois, cocking his carbine as he went, and in a moment reached the summit of a hill opposite to that on which he had perceived the traveller.", "그는 영양처럼 펄쩍 뛰어, 달려가며 소총의 공이를 당겼고, 순식간에 아까 그 길손을 발견했던 언덕 맞은편 언덕 꼭대기에 이르렀지요."], ["Three cries for help came more distinctly to his ear.", "도와 달라는 세 번의 외침이 더 또렷이 그의 귀에 닿았습니다."], ["He cast his eyes around him and saw a man carrying off Teresa, as Nessus, the centaur, carried Deianira.", "그가 주위를 둘러보자, 한 사내가, 켄타우로스 네소스가 데이아네이라를 채어 가듯 테레사를 채어 가는 것이 보였지요."]], [["“This man, who was hastening towards the wood, was already three-quarters of the way on the road from the grotto to the forest.", "\"숲 쪽으로 서둘러 가는 이 사내는, 동굴에서 숲으로 가는 길의 사 분의 삼쯤을 이미 와 있었습니다."], ["Vampa measured the distance; the man was at least two hundred paces in advance of him, and there was not a chance of overtaking him.", "밤파는 거리를 가늠했지요. 사내는 적어도 이백 걸음은 앞서 있어, 따라잡을 가망이 없었습니다."], ["The young shepherd stopped, as if his feet had been rooted to the ground; then he put the butt of his carbine to his shoulder, took aim at the ravisher, followed him for a second in his track, and then fired.", "어린 양치기는 두 발이 땅에 뿌리박힌 듯 멈춰 섰지요. 그러고는 소총 개머리판을 어깨에 대고 그 납치범을 겨눈 뒤, 일 초 동안 그의 자취를 좇다가 방아쇠를 당겼습니다."]], [["“The ravisher stopped suddenly, his knees bent under him, and he fell with Teresa in his arms.", "\"납치범이 우뚝 멈춰 서더니, 무릎이 꺾이며 테레사를 품에 안은 채 쓰러졌지요."], ["The young girl rose instantly, but the man lay on the earth struggling in the agonies of death.", "젊은 처녀는 곧바로 일어섰지만, 사내는 죽음의 고통에 몸부림치며 땅바닥에 누워 있었습니다."], ["Vampa then rushed towards Teresa; for at ten paces from the dying man her legs had failed her, and she had dropped on her knees, so that the young man feared that the ball that had brought down his enemy, had also wounded his betrothed.", "그러자 밤파는 테레사 쪽으로 내달렸지요. 죽어 가는 사내에게서 열 걸음 떨어진 곳에서 그녀는 다리에 힘이 풀려 무릎을 꿇고 주저앉아 있었고, 젊은이는 두려웠습니다. 그 총탄이 자기 원수를 쓰러뜨렸을 뿐만 아니라 약혼녀까지 다치게 한 것은 아닐까 두려웠던 것이다."]], [["“Fortunately, she was unscathed, and it was fright alone that had overcome Teresa.", "“다행히 그녀는 상처 하나 없었고, 테레사를 쓰러뜨린 것은 오직 공포뿐이었습니다."], ["When Luigi had assured himself that she was safe and unharmed, he turned towards the wounded man.", "그녀가 무사하고 다친 데 없음을 확인한 루이지는 부상당한 사내 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸지요. 그자는 막 숨을 거둔 참이었습니다."], ["He had just expired, with clenched hands, his mouth in a spasm of agony, and his hair on end in the sweat of death.", "두 손은 움켜쥔 채였고, 입은 단말마의 경련으로 일그러졌으며, 머리카락은 죽음의 식은땀에 곤두서 있었지요."], ["His eyes remained open and menacing.", "두 눈은 위협하듯 뜬 채였습니다."], ["Vampa approached the corpse, and recognized Cucumetto.", "밤파가 시체에 다가가 보니, 쿠쿠메토였습니다."]], [["“From the day on which the bandit had been saved by the two young peasants, he had been enamoured of Teresa, and had sworn she should be his.", "“그 산적은 두 젊은 농부에게 목숨을 구원받은 그날부터 테레사에게 반해, 그녀를 제 것으로 만들겠다고 맹세했습니다."], ["From that time he had watched them, and profiting by the moment when her lover had left her alone, had carried her off, and believed he at length had her in his power, when the ball, directed by the unerring skill of the young herdsman, had pierced his heart.", "그때부터 두 사람을 지켜봐 오다가, 연인이 그녀를 혼자 두고 떠난 순간을 노려 그녀를 납치했고, 마침내 손아귀에 넣었다고 믿었습니다. 바로 그 순간, 젊은 목동의 빗나감 없는 솜씨로 날아든 총탄이 그자의 심장을 꿰뚫은 것이지요."], ["Vampa gazed on him for a moment without betraying the slightest emotion; while, on the contrary, Teresa, shuddering in every limb, dared not approach the slain ruffian but by degrees, and threw a hesitating glance at the dead body over the shoulder of her lover.", "밤파는 잠시 동안 추호의 동요도 없이 그를 바라보았습니다. 반면 테레사는 온몸을 떨며 살해된 악당에게 차마 다가가지 못한 채 조금씩 발을 옮겼고, 연인의 어깨 너머로 시신을 향해 머뭇거리는 눈길을 던졌습니다."], ["Suddenly Vampa turned toward his mistress:", "갑자기 밤파가 자기 연인 쪽으로 돌아섰습니다."]], [["“‘Ah,’ said he—‘good, good!", "“‘아,’ 그가 말했습니다."], ["You are dressed; it is now my turn to dress myself.’\n20125m", "‘됐어, 됐어! 너는 옷을 차려입었으니, 이제 내가 차려입을 차례군.’"]], [["“Teresa was clothed from head to foot in the garb of the Count of San-Felice’s daughter.", "“테레사는 머리부터 발끝까지 산펠리체 백작 딸의 차림새를 하고 있었습니다."], ["Vampa took Cucumetto’s body in his arms and conveyed it to the grotto, while in her turn Teresa remained outside.", "밤파는 쿠쿠메토의 시체를 두 팔로 안아 동굴로 옮겼고, 이번에는 테레사가 밖에 남았습니다."], ["If a second traveller had passed, he would have seen a strange thing,—a shepherdess watching her flock, clad in a cashmere grown, with ear-rings and necklace of pearls, diamond pins, and buttons of sapphires, emeralds, and rubies.", "만약 또 다른 길손이 그곳을 지났더라면, 기이한 광경을 보았을 겁니다. 양 떼를 지키는 양치기 처녀가 캐시미어 가운을 걸치고, 진주 귀고리와 목걸이, 다이아몬드 핀, 사파이어와 에메랄드와 루비 단추를 단 모습이었으니까요."], ["He would, no doubt, have believed that he had returned to the times of Florian, and would have declared, on reaching Paris, that he had met an Alpine shepherdess seated at the foot of the Sabine Hill.", "그 길손은 분명 플로리앙의 시대로 되돌아갔다고 믿었을 테고, 파리에 도착해서는 사비니 언덕 기슭에 앉은 알프스의 양치기 처녀를 만났노라 장담했을 겁니다."]], [["“At the end of a quarter of an hour Vampa quitted the grotto; his costume was no less elegant than that of Teresa.", "“십오 분쯤 지나자 밤파가 동굴에서 나왔는데, 그의 차림새도 테레사 못지않게 우아했습니다. 그는 금빛으로 깎아 만든 단추가 달린 석류빛 벨벳 조끼를 입었고, 그 위에 자수를 가득 놓은 비단 조끼를 덧입었습니다."], ["He wore a vest of garnet-colored velvet, with buttons of cut gold; a silk waistcoat covered with embroidery; a Roman scarf tied round his neck; a cartridge-box worked with gold, and red and green silk; sky-blue velvet breeches, fastened above the knee with diamond buckles; garters of deerskin, worked with a thousand arabesques, and a hat whereon hung ribbons of all colors; two watches hung from his girdle, and a splendid poniard was in his belt.", "목에는 로마식 스카프를 둘렀고, 금실과 붉은빛, 초록빛 비단으로 수놓은 탄약통을 찼습니다. 하늘빛 벨벳 반바지는 무릎 위에서 다이아몬드 죔쇠로 여몄고, 사슴 가죽으로 만든 대님에는 수천 가지 당초무늬가 새겨져 있었습니다. 모자에는 온갖 빛깔의 리본이 드리워져 있었고, 허리띠에는 시계 두 개가 매달렸으며, 띠에는 화려한 단검이 꽂혀 있었지요."]], [["“Teresa uttered a cry of admiration.", "“테레사가 감탄의 탄성을 질렀습니다."], ["Vampa in this attire resembled a painting by Léopold Robert or Schnetz.", "그런 차림새의 밤파는 레오폴 로베르나 슈네츠의 그림 속 인물 같았습니다."], ["He had assumed the entire costume of Cucumetto.", "그는 쿠쿠메토의 차림새를 그대로 갖추었던 것이지요."], ["The young man saw the effect produced on his betrothed, and a smile of pride passed over his lips.", "젊은이는 약혼녀에게 자아낸 효과를 알아챘고, 그의 입가에 자랑스러운 미소가 스쳤습니다."]], [["“‘Now,’ he said to Teresa, ‘are you ready to share my fortune, whatever it may be?’", "“‘자,’ 그가 테레사에게 말했습니다. ‘무엇이 되었든 내 운명을 함께할 준비가 되었나?’"]], [["“‘Oh, yes!’ exclaimed the young girl enthusiastically.", "“‘오, 그럼요!’ 처녀가 열렬히 외쳤습니다."]], [["“‘And follow me wherever I go?’", "“‘내가 어디로 가든 따라오겠나?’"]], [["“‘To the world’s end.’", "“‘세상 끝까지라도요.’"]], [["“‘Then take my arm, and let us on; we have no time to lose.’", "“‘그럼 내 팔을 잡고, 가자. 지체할 시간이 없어.’"]], [["“The young girl did so without questioning her lover as to where he was conducting her, for he appeared to her at this moment as handsome, proud, and powerful as a god.", "“처녀는 자기를 어디로 데려가는지 연인에게 묻지도 않고 그대로 따랐습니다. 이 순간 그가 신처럼 잘생기고 당당하며 강인해 보였기 때문이지요."], ["They went towards the forest, and soon entered it.", "두 사람은 숲을 향해 걸었고, 곧 그 안으로 들어섰습니다."]], [["“We need scarcely say that all the paths of the mountain were known to Vampa; he therefore went forward without a moment’s hesitation, although there was no beaten track, but he knew his path by looking at the trees and bushes, and thus they kept on advancing for nearly an hour and a half.", "“밤파가 산속의 모든 길을 훤히 꿰뚫고 있었다는 건 굳이 말할 필요도 없겠지요. 그래서 다져진 길이 전혀 없는데도 한순간의 망설임도 없이 앞으로 나아갔습니다. 그는 나무와 덤불을 보고 길을 알아냈고, 그렇게 두 사람은 거의 한 시간 반 동안 계속 걸었습니다."], ["At the end of this time they had reached the thickest part of the forest.", "그 끝에 이르렀을 때, 그들은 숲에서 가장 우거진 곳에 다다랐습니다."], ["A torrent, whose bed was dry, led into a deep gorge.", "물이 말라 버린 골짜기 하나가 깊은 협곡으로 이어졌지요."], ["Vampa took this wild road, which, enclosed between two ridges, and shadowed by the tufted umbrage of the pines, seemed, but for the difficulties of its descent, that path to Avernus of which Virgil speaks.", "밤파는 이 험한 길로 들어섰습니다. 양쪽 능선 사이에 끼어 있고 빽빽한 소나무 그늘에 덮인 그 길은, 내려가기가 험난하다는 점만 빼면, 베르길리우스가 말한 아베르누스로 가는 길 같았습니다."], ["Teresa had become alarmed at the wild and deserted look of the plain around her, and pressed closely against her guide, not uttering a syllable; but as she saw him advance with even step and composed countenance, she endeavored to repress her emotion.", "테레사는 주위 들판의 거칠고 황량한 모습에 겁을 먹고 길잡이에게 바싹 붙어 한마디도 내지 못했지만, 그가 한결같은 걸음과 차분한 얼굴로 나아가는 것을 보고는 두려움을 억누르려 애썼습니다."]], [["“Suddenly, about ten paces from them, a man advanced from behind a tree and aimed at Vampa.", "“갑자기 그들에게서 열 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳에서 한 사내가 나무 뒤에서 나오더니 밤파를 겨누었습니다."]], [["“‘Not another step,’ he said, ‘or you are a dead man.’", "“‘한 발짝도 더 움직이지 마라.’ 그가 말했습니다. ‘그러지 않으면 죽은 목숨이다.’"]], [["“‘What, then,’ said Vampa, raising his hand with a gesture of disdain, while Teresa, no longer able to restrain her alarm, clung closely to him, ‘do wolves rend each other?’", "“‘이런,’ 밤파가 경멸의 몸짓으로 손을 들어 올리며 말했습니다. 그러는 사이 테레사는 더 이상 두려움을 누르지 못하고 그에게 바싹 매달렸지요. ‘늑대끼리 서로 물어뜯기라도 한다더냐?’"]], [["“‘Who are you?’ inquired the sentinel.", "“‘너는 누구냐?’ 보초가 물었습니다."]], [["“‘I am Luigi Vampa, shepherd of the San-Felice farm.’", "“‘나는 산펠리체 농장의 목동, 루이지 밤파다.’"]], [["“‘What do you want?’", "“‘무엇을 원하느냐?’"]], [["“‘I would speak with your companions who are in the glade at Rocca Bianca.’", "“‘로카비안카 빈터에 있는 네 동료들과 이야기를 나누고 싶다.’"]], [["“‘Follow me, then,’ said the sentinel; ‘or, as you know your way, go first.’", "“‘그렇다면 나를 따라오너라.’ 보초가 말했습니다. ‘아니, 길을 안다면 네가 앞장서거라.’"]], [["“Vampa smiled disdainfully at this precaution on the part of the bandit, went before Teresa, and continued to advance with the same firm and easy step as before.", "“밤파는 산적의 그런 경계심에 경멸하듯 미소 짓고는, 테레사보다 앞서 나가 아까처럼 굳건하고 거침없는 걸음으로 계속 나아갔습니다."], ["At the end of ten minutes the bandit made them a sign to stop.", "십 분쯤 지나자 산적이 멈추라는 신호를 보냈지요."], ["The two young persons obeyed.", "두 젊은이는 그대로 따랐습니다."], ["Then the bandit thrice imitated the cry of a crow; a croak answered this signal.", "그러자 산적이 세 차례 까마귀 울음소리를 흉내 냈고, 그 신호에 까옥 하는 소리가 답했습니다."]], [["“‘Good!’ said the sentry, ‘you may now go on.’", "“‘좋다!’ 보초가 말했습니다. ‘이제 가도 된다.’"]], [["“Luigi and Teresa again set forward; as they went on Teresa clung tremblingly to her lover at the sight of weapons and the glistening of carbines through the trees.", "“루이지와 테레사는 다시 발걸음을 옮겼습니다. 나아가는 동안 테레사는 나무 사이로 무기들과 번뜩이는 기총들을 보고 떨면서 연인에게 매달렸지요."], ["The retreat of Rocca Bianca was at the top of a small mountain, which no doubt in former days had been a volcano—an extinct volcano before the days when Remus and Romulus had deserted Alba to come and found the city of Rome.", "로카비안카의 은신처는 작은 산꼭대기에 있었는데, 그 산은 분명 오래전에는 화산이었을 겁니다. 레무스와 로물루스가 알바를 버리고 로마라는 도시를 세우러 오기 전에 이미 불이 꺼진 화산이었지요."]], [["“Teresa and Luigi reached the summit, and all at once found themselves in the presence of twenty bandits.", "“테레사와 루이지가 정상에 다다르자, 별안간 산적 스무 명과 마주하게 되었습니다."]], [["“‘Here is a young man who seeks and wishes to speak to you,’ said the sentinel.", "“‘여기 당신들을 찾아와 이야기하고 싶다는 젊은이가 있소.’ 보초가 말했습니다."]], [["“‘What has he to say?’ inquired the young man who was in command in the chief’s absence.", "“‘무슨 할 말이 있다는 거냐?’ 두목이 자리를 비운 사이 지휘를 맡고 있던 젊은이가 물었습니다."]], [["“‘I wish to say that I am tired of a shepherd’s life,’ was Vampa’s reply.", "“‘목동의 삶이 지긋지긋해졌다는 말이오.’ 밤파가 대답했습니다."]], [["“‘Ah, I understand,’ said the lieutenant; ‘and you seek admittance into our ranks?’", "“‘아, 알겠군.’ 부두목이 말했습니다. ‘그래서 우리 무리에 들고 싶다는 거냐?’"]], [["“‘Welcome!’ cried several bandits from Ferrusino, Pampinara, and Anagni, who had recognized Luigi Vampa.", "“‘잘 왔네!’ 페루시노와 팜피나라, 아나니 출신의 산적 몇이 루이지 밤파를 알아보고 외쳤습니다."]], [["“‘Yes, but I came to ask something more than to be your companion.’", "“‘그렇소, 하지만 나는 당신들의 동료가 되는 것 말고 더 청할 게 있어 왔소.’"]], [["“‘And what may that be?’ inquired the bandits with astonishment.", "“‘그게 무엇이냐?’ 산적들이 놀라며 물었습니다."]], [["“‘I come to ask to be your captain,’ said the young man.", "“‘나는 당신들의 두목이 되겠다고 청하러 왔소.’ 젊은이가 말했습니다."]], [["“The bandits shouted with laughter.", "“산적들은 폭소를 터뜨렸습니다."]], [["“‘And what have you done to aspire to this honor?’ demanded the lieutenant.", "“‘그런 영예를 바랄 만큼 네가 무슨 일을 했단 말이냐?’ 부두목이 따져 물었습니다."]], [["“‘I have killed your chief, Cucumetto, whose dress I now wear; and I set fire to the villa San-Felice to procure a wedding-dress for my betrothed.’", "“‘나는 당신들의 두목 쿠쿠메토를 죽였고, 지금 그 옷을 입고 있소. 그리고 내 약혼녀에게 줄 혼례복을 마련하려고 산펠리체 별장에 불을 질렀소.’"]], [["“An hour afterwards Luigi Vampa was chosen captain, vice Cucumetto, deceased.”", "“한 시간 뒤, 루이지 밤파는 고인이 된 쿠쿠메토를 대신할 두목으로 뽑혔습니다.”"]], [["20129m\n“Well, my dear Albert,”", "“자, 친애하는 알베르,”"], ["said Franz, turning towards his friend; “what think you of citizen Luigi Vampa?”", "프란츠가 친구 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다. “시민 루이지 밤파에 대해 어떻게 생각하나?”"]], [["“I say he is a myth,”", "“지어낸 이야기라고 봐.”"], ["replied Albert, “and never had an existence.”", "알베르가 대답했다. “애초에 그런 인물은 있지도 않았어.”"]], [["“And what may a myth be?” inquired Pastrini.", "“지어낸 이야기라니, 그게 무슨 뜻입니까?” 파스트리니가 물었다."]], [["“The explanation would be too long, my dear landlord,” replied Franz.", "“설명하자면 너무 길어요, 주인장.” 프란츠가 대답했다."], ["“And you say that Signor Vampa exercises his profession at this moment in the environs of Rome?”", "“그럼 그 밤파 선생이 지금 이 순간에도 로마 근교에서 그 일을 벌이고 있다는 거요?”"]], [["“And with a boldness of which no bandit before him ever gave an example.”", "“그것도 이전의 어떤 산적도 본보기를 보인 적 없는 대담함으로 말입니다.”"]], [["“Then the police have vainly tried to lay hands on him?”", "“그렇다면 경찰이 그를 붙잡으려고 애써 봤지만 헛수고였다는 거요?”"]], [["“Why, you see, he has a good understanding with the shepherds in the plains, the fishermen of the Tiber, and the smugglers of the coast.", "“글쎄요, 보시다시피 그는 들판의 목동들, 티베르강의 어부들, 해안의 밀수꾼들과 두루 통해 있습니다."], ["They seek for him in the mountains, and he is on the waters; they follow him on the waters, and he is on the open sea; then they pursue him, and he has suddenly taken refuge in the islands, at Giglio, Giannutri, or Monte Cristo; and when they hunt for him there, he reappears suddenly at Albano, Tivoli, or La Riccia.”", "산속에서 찾으면 그는 물 위에 있고, 물 위로 쫓으면 먼바다에 나가 있지요. 그래서 추격하면 별안간 섬으로, 질리오나 잔누트리, 혹은 몬테크리스토로 피신해 버립니다. 그리고 거기서 그를 사냥하려 들면, 별안간 알바노나 티볼리, 라리차에 다시 나타나는 거지요.”"]], [["“And how does he behave towards travellers?”", "“그럼 여행자들에게는 어떻게 대하오?”"]], [["“Alas! his plan is very simple.", "“아아! 그 방식은 아주 단순합니다."], ["It depends on the distance he may be from the city, whether he gives eight hours, twelve hours, or a day wherein to pay their ransom; and when that time has elapsed he allows another hour’s grace.", "도시에서 떨어진 거리에 따라, 몸값을 치르도록 여덟 시간이나 열두 시간, 혹은 하루를 주지요. 그 시간이 지나면 한 시간을 더 봐줍니다."], ["At the sixtieth minute of this hour, if the money is not forthcoming, he blows out the prisoner’s brains with a pistol-shot, or plants his dagger in his heart, and that settles the account.”", "그 한 시간의 예순 번째 분이 되도록 돈이 마련되지 않으면, 권총 한 방으로 포로의 머리통을 날려 버리거나 심장에 단검을 꽂아 버립니다. 그것으로 셈이 끝나는 거지요.”"]], [["“Well, Albert,”", "“자, 알베르,”"], ["inquired Franz of his companion, “are you still disposed to go to the Colosseum by the outer wall?”", "프란츠가 친구에게 물었다. “아직도 바깥 성벽을 따라 콜로세움에 갈 생각인가?”"]], [["“Quite so,”", "“물론이지.”"], ["said Albert, “if the way be picturesque.”", "알베르가 말했다. “길이 그림처럼 멋지기만 하다면 말이야.”"]], [["The clock struck nine as the door opened, and a coachman appeared.", "문이 열리며 시계가 아홉 시를 알릴 때, 마부가 나타났다."]], [["“Excellencies,”", "“나리들,”"], ["said he, “the coach is ready.”", "그가 말했다. “마차 준비가 되었습니다.”"]], [["“Well, then,”", "“그럼,”"], ["said Franz, “let us to the Colosseum.”", "프란츠가 말했다. “콜로세움으로 가지.”"]], [["“By the Porta del Popolo or by the streets, your excellencies?”", "“포폴로 문으로 가시겠습니까, 아니면 시가지를 거쳐 가시겠습니까, 나리들?”"]], [["“By the streets, _morbleu!_ by the streets!”", "“시가지로, 빌어먹을! 시가지로 가!”"], ["cried Franz.", "프란츠가 외쳤다."]], [["“Ah, my dear fellow,”", "“이런, 친구,”"], ["said Albert, rising, and lighting his third cigar, “really, I thought you had more courage.”", "알베르가 일어나며 세 번째 시가에 불을 붙이고는 말했다. “솔직히 자네가 좀 더 배짱 있는 사람인 줄 알았는데.”"]], [["So saying, the two young men went down the staircase, and got into the carriage.", "이렇게 말하며 두 젊은이는 계단을 내려가 마차에 올랐다."]], [["# Chapter 34. The Colosseum", "# Chapter 34. The Colosseum"]], [["20131m\nFranz had so managed his route, that during the ride to the Colosseum they passed not a single ancient ruin, so that no preliminary impression interfered to mitigate the colossal proportions of the gigantic building they came to admire.", "프란츠는 콜로세움으로 가는 길을 미리 잘 짚어 둔 덕분에, 마차를 타고 가는 동안 단 하나의 고대 유적도 지나치지 않았다. 그래서 그들이 보러 가는 그 거대한 건축물의 어마어마한 규모를 미리 본 인상이 무디게 만드는 일도 없었다."], ["The road selected was a continuation of the Via Sistina; then by cutting off the right angle of the street in which stands Santa Maria Maggiore and proceeding by the Via Urbana and San Pietro in Vincoli, the travellers would find themselves directly opposite the Colosseum.", "그가 고른 길은 시스티나 거리의 연장선이었다. 그런 다음 산타 마리아 마조레 성당이 서 있는 거리의 직각 모퉁이를 가로질러, 우르바나 거리와 산 피에트로 인 빈콜리를 지나면, 여행자들은 곧장 콜로세움과 마주하게 되는 것이었다."]], [["This itinerary possessed another great advantage,—that of leaving Franz at full liberty to indulge his deep reverie upon the subject of Signor Pastrini’s story, in which his mysterious host of Monte Cristo was so strangely mixed up.", "이 경로에는 또 하나의 큰 이점이 있었다. 파스트리니 씨의 이야기를 마음껏 곱씹을 수 있도록 프란츠를 완전히 자유롭게 내버려 둔다는 점이었다. 그 이야기에는 몬테 크리스토의 수수께끼 같은 그 집주인이 어찌나 기묘하게 얽혀 있었던지."], ["Seated with folded arms in a corner of the carriage, he continued to ponder over the singular history he had so lately listened to, and to ask himself an interminable number of questions touching its various circumstances without, however, arriving at a satisfactory reply to any of them.", "그는 마차 한구석에 팔짱을 끼고 앉아, 조금 전에 들은 그 기이한 이야기를 끊임없이 되새기고, 그 갖가지 정황을 둘러싼 무수한 질문을 스스로에게 던졌으나, 어느 하나에도 만족스러운 답에는 이르지 못했다."]], [["One fact more than the rest brought his friend “Sinbad the Sailor”", "다른 무엇보다도 한 가지 사실이 그의 벗 '뱃사람 신드바드'를 다시 떠올리게 했다."], ["back to his recollection, and that was the mysterious sort of intimacy that seemed to exist between the brigands and the sailors; and Pastrini’s account of Vampa’s having found refuge on board the vessels of smugglers and fishermen, reminded Franz of the two Corsican bandits he had found supping so amicably with the crew of the little yacht, which had even deviated from its course and touched at Porto-Vecchio for the sole purpose of landing them.", "그것은 산적들과 뱃사람들 사이에 존재하는 듯 보이는 그 묘한 친분이었다. 그리고 밤파가 밀수꾼과 어부들의 배에 몸을 숨겼다는 파스트리니의 이야기는, 프란츠에게 작은 요트의 선원들과 그토록 다정하게 저녁을 들던 두 코르시카 산적을 떠올리게 했다. 그 요트는 오직 그들을 내려놓을 목적으로 항로를 벗어나 포르토베키오에 들르기까지 했었다."], ["The very name assumed by his host of Monte Cristo and again repeated by the landlord of the Hôtel de Londres, abundantly proved to him that his island friend was playing his philanthropic part on the shores of Piombino, Civita Vecchia, Ostia, and Gaëta, as on those of Corsica, Tuscany, and Spain; and further, Franz bethought him of having heard his singular entertainer speak both of Tunis and Palermo, proving thereby how largely his circle of acquaintances extended.", "몬테 크리스토의 그 집주인이 스스로 댄 이름, 그리고 런던 호텔 주인이 다시 입에 올린 그 이름은, 그의 섬 친구가 코르시카와 토스카나, 에스파냐의 해안에서뿐 아니라 피옴비노와 치비타 베키아, 오스티아, 가에타의 해안에서도 박애주의자 노릇을 하고 있음을 충분히 입증해 주었다. 게다가 프란츠는 그 기이한 접대자가 튀니스와 팔레르모를 함께 언급하던 것을 떠올렸고, 그것은 그의 교제 범위가 얼마나 넓은지를 증명하는 셈이었다."]], [["But however the mind of the young man might be absorbed in these reflections, they were at once dispersed at the sight of the dark frowning ruins of the stupendous Colosseum, through the various openings of which the pale moonlight played and flickered like the unearthly gleam from the eyes of the wandering dead.", "그러나 젊은이의 마음이 이런 상념에 아무리 깊이 잠겨 있었다 한들, 그 상념은 어마어마한 콜로세움의 어둡고 음울한 폐허가 눈앞에 나타나는 순간 단번에 흩어졌다. 그 여러 틈새로 창백한 달빛이 비쳐 들며 일렁이는 모습은, 마치 떠도는 망자의 눈에서 새어 나오는 이 세상 것 아닌 빛 같았다."], ["The carriage stopped near the Meta Sudans; the door was opened, and the young men, eagerly alighting, found themselves opposite a _cicerone_, who appeared to have sprung up from the ground, so unexpected was his appearance.", "마차가 메타 수단스 근처에서 멈추었다. 문이 열리고, 두 젊은이가 서둘러 내리자, 안내인 하나와 마주쳤다. 그 등장이 어찌나 뜻밖이었던지 땅에서 솟아난 것만 같았다."], ["The usual guide from the hotel having followed them, they had paid two conductors, nor is it possible, at Rome, to avoid this abundant supply of guides; besides the ordinary _cicerone_, who seizes upon you directly you set foot in your hotel, and never quits you while you remain in the city, there is also a special _cicerone_ belonging to each monument—nay, almost to each part of a monument.", "호텔에서 따라온 평소의 안내인까지 뒤에 있었으니, 그들은 두 명의 안내인에게 삯을 치른 셈이었다. 로마에서는 이렇게 넘쳐 나는 안내인들을 피할 도리가 없다. 호텔에 발을 들이는 순간 들러붙어 도시에 머무는 내내 떨어지지 않는 평범한 안내인 말고도, 각 기념물마다, 아니 거의 기념물의 부분 부분마다 따로 딸린 전담 안내인이 있다."], ["It may, therefore, be easily imagined there is no scarcity of guides at the Colosseum, that wonder of all ages, which Martial thus eulogizes:", "그러니 모든 시대의 경이라 불리는 콜로세움에 안내인이 모자랄 리 없다는 것은 쉽게 짐작할 수 있다. 마르티알리스는 그곳을 이렇게 찬미했다."]], [["“Let Memphis cease to boast the barbarous miracles of her pyramids, and the wonders of Babylon be talked of no more among us; all must bow to the superiority of the gigantic labor of the Cæsars, and the many voices of Fame spread far and wide the surpassing merits of this incomparable monument.”", "\"멤피스로 하여금 그 야만스러운 피라미드의 기적을 자랑하기를 멈추게 하라, 바빌론의 경이도 우리 사이에서 더는 입에 오르지 않게 하라. 모두가 카이사르들의 거대한 노역이 지닌 우월함 앞에 머리를 숙여야 하리니, 명성의 숱한 목소리가 이 비할 데 없는 기념물의 빼어난 공덕을 멀리멀리 퍼뜨리리라.\""]], [["As for Albert and Franz, they essayed not to escape from their _ciceronian_ tyrants; and, indeed, it would have been so much the more difficult to break their bondage, as the guides alone are permitted to visit these monuments with torches in their hands.", "알베르와 프란츠로 말하자면, 그들은 안내인 폭군들에게서 달아나려 들지 않았다. 게다가 그 속박을 끊기란 그만큼 더 어려운 일이었으니, 손에 횃불을 들고 이 기념물들을 둘러볼 수 있는 것은 오직 안내인뿐이었던 것이다. 그리하여 두 젊은이는 저항할 엄두도 내지 않고, 그저 무턱대고 믿음직스럽게 안내인들의 보호와 손길에 자신을 내맡겼다."], ["Thus, then, the young men made no attempt at resistance, but blindly and confidingly surrendered themselves into the care and custody of their conductors.", "프란츠는 이미 일고여덟 번이나 콜로세움을 비슷하게 둘러본 적이 있었던 반면, 그보다 운이 덜 따른 그의 동행은 난생처음으로 플라비우스 베스파시아누스의 기념물을 이루는 그 고전의 땅을 밟는 것이었다. 그리고 그를 위해 한마디 해 두자면, 안내인들의 매끄러운 수다 한가운데서도 그의 마음은 눈앞에 펼쳐진 모든 것에 마땅하고도 깊은 경외와 열렬한 감탄으로 물들었다."], ["Franz had already made seven or eight similar excursions to the Colosseum, while his less favored companion trod for the first time in his life the classic ground forming the monument of Flavius Vespasian; and, to his credit be it spoken, his mind, even amid the glib loquacity of the guides, was duly and deeply touched with awe and enthusiastic admiration of all he saw; and certainly no adequate notion of these stupendous ruins can be formed save by such as have visited them, and more especially by moonlight, at which time the vast proportions of the building appear twice as large when viewed by the mysterious beams of a southern moonlit sky, whose rays are sufficiently clear and vivid to light the horizon with a glow equal to the soft twilight of a western clime.", "이 어마어마한 폐허에 대한 합당한 관념은 직접 가 본 사람, 더구나 달빛 아래에서 본 사람이 아니고서는 도저히 가질 수 없는 것이다. 그 시각이면 건물의 광대한 규모는 남국의 달빛 어린 하늘이 뿜는 신비로운 빛줄기 아래에서 두 배는 더 커 보인다. 그 빛은 서녘의 부드러운 황혼에 견줄 만한 빛으로 지평선을 물들일 만큼 맑고 또렷하다."]], [["Scarcely, therefore, had the reflective Franz walked a hundred steps beneath the interior porticoes of the ruin, when, abandoning Albert to the guides (who would by no means yield their prescriptive right of carrying their victims through the routine regularly laid down, and as regularly followed by them, but dragged the unconscious visitor to the various objects with a pertinacity that admitted of no appeal, beginning, as a matter of course, with the “Lions’ Den”, the “Hall of the Gladiators”", "그러니 사색에 잠긴 프란츠가 폐허의 안쪽 주랑 아래로 백 걸음도 채 걷기 전에, 그는 알베르를 안내인들 손에 내맡겨 버렸다. (안내인들은 자기들이 으레 정해 놓고 그대로 따르는 순서대로 희생양을 끌고 다닐 관습적 권리를 결코 양보하려 들지 않았고, 아무것도 모르는 방문객을 항변의 여지조차 없는 끈질김으로 이런저런 볼거리 앞으로 끌고 다녔다."], ["and finishing with “Cæsar’s Podium”), to escape a jargon and mechanical survey of the wonders by which he was surrounded, Franz ascended a half-dilapidated staircase, and, leaving them to follow their monotonous round, seated himself at the foot of a column, and immediately opposite a large aperture, which permitted him to enjoy a full and undisturbed view of the gigantic dimensions of the majestic ruin.", "으레 그렇듯 '사자 우리'에서 시작해 '검투사의 방'을 거쳐 '카이사르의 귀빈석'에서 마무리하는 식이었다.) 자신을 둘러싼 경이들에 대한 그 횡설수설과 기계적인 둘러보기에서 벗어나기 위해, 프란츠는 반쯤 허물어진 계단을 올랐다. 그러고는 안내인들이 그 단조로운 순회를 계속하도록 내버려 둔 채, 기둥 하나의 밑동에 자리 잡고 앉았다. 그 맞은편에는 커다란 틈이 나 있어, 그는 그 장엄한 폐허의 거대한 규모를 온전하고도 거침없이 바라볼 수 있었다."]], [["Franz had remained for nearly a quarter of an hour perfectly hidden by the shadow of the vast column at whose base he had found a resting-place, and from whence his eyes followed the motions of Albert and his guides, who, holding torches in their hands, had emerged from a vomitorium at the opposite extremity of the Colosseum, and then again disappeared down the steps conducting to the seats reserved for the Vestal virgins, resembling, as they glided along, some restless shades following the flickering glare of so many _ignes fatui_.", "프란츠는 자기가 발견한 쉼터인 그 거대한 기둥의 밑동, 그 그늘에 완전히 몸을 숨긴 채 거의 십오 분 가까이 머물러 있었다. 거기서 그의 눈은 알베르와 그 안내인들의 움직임을 좇았다. 그들은 손에 횃불을 들고 콜로세움 반대편 끝의 한 출입구에서 나타났다가, 다시 베스타 처녀들에게 마련되었던 좌석으로 통하는 계단을 따라 사라졌다."], ["All at once his ear caught a sound resembling that of a stone rolling down the staircase opposite the one by which he had himself ascended.", "미끄러지듯 나아가는 그 모습은, 마치 도깨비불의 일렁이는 불빛을 뒤쫓는 안절부절못하는 망령들 같았다. 문득 그의 귀에 어떤 소리가 들렸다."], ["There was nothing remarkable in the circumstance of a fragment of granite giving way and falling heavily below; but it seemed to him that the substance that fell gave way beneath the pressure of a foot, and also that someone, who endeavored as much as possible to prevent his footsteps from being heard, was approaching the spot where he sat.", "그가 올라온 계단의 맞은편 계단을 따라 돌멩이 하나가 굴러떨어지는 듯한 소리였다. 화강암 조각이 떨어져 나가 아래로 무겁게 굴러내리는 일에는 별달리 이상할 게 없었다. 하지만 그에게는 떨어진 그 물체가 어떤 발의 무게에 눌려 굴러떨어진 것처럼 느껴졌고, 또 누군가가 발소리를 들키지 않으려 한껏 애쓰면서 그가 앉아 있는 곳으로 다가오고 있는 것만 같았다."]], [["Conjecture soon became certainty, for the figure of a man was distinctly visible to Franz, gradually emerging from the staircase opposite, upon which the moon was at that moment pouring a full tide of silvery brightness.", "짐작은 곧 확신이 되었다. 한 남자의 형체가 프란츠의 눈에 또렷이 들어왔던 것이다. 맞은편 계단에서 차츰 모습을 드러내는 그 위로, 그 순간 달이 은빛 광채를 가득 쏟아붓고 있었다."]], [["The stranger thus presenting himself was probably a person who, like Franz, preferred the enjoyment of solitude and his own thoughts to the frivolous gabble of the guides.", "이렇게 모습을 드러낸 이방인은 아마도 프란츠처럼, 안내인들의 시시한 수다보다는 고독과 자기 생각을 즐기기를 더 좋아하는 사람인 듯했다."], ["And his appearance had nothing extraordinary in it; but the hesitation with which he proceeded, stopping and listening with anxious attention at every step he took, convinced Franz that he expected the arrival of some person.", "그의 모습에는 별난 데가 없었다. 그러나 한 걸음 한 걸음 내디딜 때마다 멈춰 서서 불안한 주의를 기울여 귀 기울이는 그 망설임으로 미루어, 프란츠는 그가 누군가의 도착을 기다리고 있음을 확신했다."]], [["By a sort of instinctive impulse, Franz withdrew as much as possible behind his pillar.", "본능적인 충동에 이끌려 프란츠는 기둥 뒤로 한껏 몸을 물렸다."]], [["About ten feet from the spot where he and the stranger were, the roof had given way, leaving a large round opening, through which might be seen the blue vault of heaven, thickly studded with stars.", "그와 이방인이 있는 곳에서 열 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳은 지붕이 내려앉아, 둥글고 커다란 구멍이 나 있었다. 그 너머로는 별들이 촘촘히 박힌 푸른 하늘의 천장이 보였다."]], [["Around this opening, which had, possibly, for ages permitted a free entrance to the brilliant moonbeams that now illumined the vast pile, grew a quantity of creeping plants, whose delicate green branches stood out in bold relief against the clear azure of the firmament, while large masses of thick, strong fibrous shoots forced their way through the chasm, and hung floating to and fro, like so many waving strings.", "이 구멍 둘레에는, 어쩌면 오랜 세월 동안 지금 저 거대한 더미를 비추는 찬란한 달빛이 자유로이 들어오도록 길을 내주었을 그 구멍 둘레에는, 덩굴식물이 무성하게 자라 있었다. 그 가녀린 초록 가지들이 맑고 푸른 창공을 배경으로 또렷이 도드라졌고, 한편 굵고 질긴 섬유질 새순 덩어리들이 갈라진 틈을 비집고 나와 이리저리 흔들리며 늘어져, 마치 출렁이는 끈 가닥들 같았다."]], [["The person whose mysterious arrival had attracted the attention of Franz stood in a kind of half-light, that rendered it impossible to distinguish his features, although his dress was easily made out.", "수수께끼 같은 등장으로 프란츠의 주의를 끈 그 사람은 일종의 어스름 속에 서 있어, 옷차림은 쉽게 알아볼 수 있었으나 얼굴은 도무지 분간할 수 없었다."], ["He wore a large brown mantle, one fold of which, thrown over his left shoulder, served likewise to mask the lower part of his countenance, while the upper part was completely hidden by his broad-brimmed hat.", "그는 커다란 갈색 망토를 두르고 있었는데, 그 한 자락을 왼쪽 어깨에 걸쳐 얼굴 아랫부분까지 가리고 있었고, 위쪽은 챙 넓은 모자에 완전히 가려져 있었다."], ["The lower part of his dress was more distinctly visible by the bright rays of the moon, which, entering through the broken ceiling, shed their refulgent beams on feet cased in elegantly made boots of polished leather, over which descended fashionably cut trousers of black cloth. 20135m", "옷차림의 아랫부분은 부서진 천장을 통해 들어온 환한 달빛 덕분에 더 또렷이 보였다. 그 눈부신 빛줄기가 윤이 나는 가죽으로 멋들어지게 지은 장화에 싸인 발을 비추었고, 그 위로는 검은 천으로 유행에 맞게 재단한 바지가 내려와 있었다."]], [["From the imperfect means Franz had of judging, he could only come to one conclusion,—that the person whom he was thus watching certainly belonged to no inferior station of life.", "프란츠가 가진 불완전한 판단의 수단으로는 단 하나의 결론에 이를 수밖에 없었다. 이렇게 지켜보는 인물이 결코 비천한 신분에 속한 사람은 아니라는 것이었다."]], [["Some few minutes had elapsed, and the stranger began to show manifest signs of impatience, when a slight noise was heard outside the aperture in the roof, and almost immediately a dark shadow seemed to obstruct the flood of light that had entered it, and the figure of a man was clearly seen gazing with eager scrutiny on the immense space beneath him; then, as his eye caught sight of him in the mantle, he grasped a floating mass of thickly matted boughs, and glided down by their help to within three or four feet of the ground, and then leaped lightly on his feet.", "몇 분이 흘렀고, 이방인이 뚜렷이 초조한 기색을 보이기 시작할 무렵, 지붕의 그 틈 바깥에서 희미한 소리가 들렸다. 그러더니 거의 곧바로 어두운 그림자 하나가 그리로 들어오던 빛의 물결을 가로막는 듯했고, 한 남자의 형체가 아래의 그 광대한 공간을 열심히 살피며 내려다보는 모습이 또렷이 보였다. 이윽고 그의 눈이 망토를 두른 사내를 알아채자, 그는 빽빽이 엉킨 가지 덩어리를 움켜쥐고 그것을 의지해 땅에서 서너 발쯤 되는 높이까지 미끄러져 내려오더니, 가볍게 발을 디디고 뛰어내렸다."], ["The man who had performed this daring act with so much indifference wore the Transtevere costume.", "이토록 대담한 짓을 그토록 태연하게 해낸 그 남자는 트라스테베레 차림을 하고 있었다."]], [["“I beg your excellency’s pardon for keeping you waiting,”", "\"각하를 기다리시게 해 죄송합니다.\""], ["said the man, in the Roman dialect, “but I don’t think I’m many minutes after my time, ten o’clock has just struck by the clock of Saint John Lateran.”", "사내가 로마 사투리로 말했다. \"그래도 약속 시간에서 몇 분쯤밖에 늦지 않았다고 봅니다. 산 조반니 인 라테라노 종이 방금 열 시를 쳤으니까요.\""]], [["“Say not a word about being late,”", "\"늦었다는 말은 한마디도 마라.\""], ["replied the stranger in purest Tuscan; “’tis I who am too soon.", "이방인이 더없이 순수한 토스카나 말로 대답했다."], ["But even if you had caused me to wait a little while, I should have felt quite sure that the delay was not occasioned by any fault of yours.”", "\"내가 너무 일찍 온 게다. 설령 네가 나를 잠시 기다리게 했다 한들, 그 지체가 네 잘못 때문이 아니라는 것은 충분히 확신했을 게다.\""]], [["“Your excellency is perfectly right in so thinking,”", "\"각하께서 그리 생각하시니 참으로 옳으십니다.\""], ["said the man; “I came here direct from the Castle of St. Angelo, and I had an immense deal of trouble before I could get a chance to speak to Beppo.”", "사내가 말했다. \"저는 산탄젤로성에서 곧장 이리로 왔습니다만, 베포와 말 한마디 나눌 틈을 얻기까지 엄청나게 애를 먹었습니다.\""]], [["“And who is Beppo?”", "\"베포가 누구냐?\""]], [["“Oh, Beppo is employed in the prison, and I give him so much a year to let me know what is going on within his holiness’s castle.”", "\"아, 베포는 감옥에서 일하는 자입니다. 제가 일 년에 얼마씩 쥐여 주고 교황 성하의 성안에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 알려 달라고 합니다.\""]], [["“Indeed!", "\"그래!"], ["You are a provident person, I see.”", "보아하니 너는 앞날을 챙기는 사람이로구나.\""]], [["“Why, you see, no one knows what may happen.", "\"그게, 아시다시피 무슨 일이 닥칠지 누가 알겠습니까."], ["Perhaps some of these days I may be entrapped, like poor Peppino and may be very glad to have some little nibbling mouse to gnaw the meshes of my net, and so help me out of prison.”", "어쩌면 어느 날 가엾은 페피노처럼 저도 덫에 걸릴지 모르고, 그러면 제 그물코를 갉아 저를 감옥에서 꺼내 줄 자그마한 생쥐가 있다는 게 무척 반가울지도 모르지요.\""]], [["“Briefly, what did you learn?”", "\"간단히 말해, 무엇을 알아냈느냐?\""]], [["“That two executions of considerable interest will take place the day after tomorrow at two o’clock, as is customary at Rome at the commencement of all great festivals.", "\"적잖이 흥미로운 처형 두 건이 모레 두 시에 거행된다는 겁니다. 로마에서 큰 축제가 시작될 때면 으레 그러하듯이요."], ["One of the culprits will be _mazzolato_;3 he is an atrocious villain, who murdered the priest who brought him up, and deserves not the smallest pity.", "죄인 하나는 마촐라토●를 당할 겁니다. 그자는 자기를 길러 준 신부를 죽인 흉악한 악당이니 손톱만큼의 동정도 받을 자격이 없지요."], ["The other sufferer is sentenced to be _decapitato_;4 and he, your excellency, is poor Peppino.”", "다른 하나는 데카피타토●●형을 선고받았는데, 각하, 그자가 바로 가엾은 페피노입니다.\""]], [["“The fact is, that you have inspired not only the pontifical government, but also the neighboring states, with such extreme fear, that they are glad of all opportunity of making an example.”", "* 마촐라토: 둔기로 머리를 쳐 죽이는 처형. ●● 데카피타토: 참수형.—옮긴이\n\"사실, 너는 교황청 정부뿐 아니라 인근의 여러 나라에까지 어찌나 극심한 두려움을 심어 놓았는지, 그들은 본보기를 보일 기회라면 무엇이든 반기는 게다.\""]], [["“But Peppino did not even belong to my band; he was merely a poor shepherd, whose only crime consisted in furnishing us with provisions.”", "\"하지만 페피노는 제 패거리에 속하지도 않았습니다. 그저 우리에게 식량을 대 준 것이 유일한 죄인 가난한 양치기였을 뿐이지요.\""]], [["“Which makes him your accomplice to all intents and purposes.", "\"바로 그 점이 어느 모로 보나 그를 네 공범으로 만드는 게다."], ["But mark the distinction with which he is treated; instead of being knocked on the head as you would be if once they caught hold of you, he is simply sentenced to be guillotined, by which means, too, the amusements of the day are diversified, and there is a spectacle to please every spectator.”", "하지만 그가 받는 대접의 차이를 잘 보아라. 그들이 너를 한번 붙잡으면 머리를 쳐 죽일 텐데, 그자는 그저 단두대형을 선고받았을 뿐이다. 게다가 그렇게 함으로써 그날의 구경거리도 다채로워지고, 어느 구경꾼이든 만족시킬 볼거리가 생기는 셈이지.\""]], [["“Without reckoning the wholly unexpected one I am preparing to surprise them with.”", "\"제가 그들을 놀래 주려고 준비하고 있는, 전혀 뜻밖의 구경거리는 셈에 넣지 않으셔도요.\""]], [["“My good friend,”", "\"여보게.\""], ["said the man in the cloak, “excuse me for saying that you seem to me precisely in the mood to commit some wild or extravagant act.”", "망토를 두른 사내가 말했다. \"이런 말 해서 미안하네만, 자네는 지금 무슨 무모하거나 터무니없는 짓을 저지를 기분에 딱 사로잡혀 있는 것 같군.\""]], [["“Perhaps I am; but one thing I have resolved on, and that is, to stop at nothing to restore a poor devil to liberty, who has got into this scrape solely from having served me.", "\"그럴지도 모르지요. 하지만 한 가지 결심한 게 있습니다. 오직 저를 위해 일하다 이 곤경에 빠진 가엾은 자를 풀어 주기 위해서라면 무슨 짓이든 마다하지 않겠다는 것입니다."], ["I should hate and despise myself as a coward did I desert the brave fellow in his present extremity.”", "이 다급한 처지에 놓인 그 용감한 친구를 저버린다면, 저는 비겁자인 제 자신을 미워하고 경멸하게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["“And what do you mean to do?”", "\"그래서 어쩔 작정인가?\""]], [["“To surround the scaffold with twenty of my best men, who, at a signal from me, will rush forward directly Peppino is brought for execution, and, by the assistance of their stilettos, drive back the guard, and carry off the prisoner.”", "\"제 부하 가운데 가장 뛰어난 스무 명으로 처형대를 에워싸려 합니다. 그들은 제 신호에 맞추어, 페피노가 처형장으로 끌려 나오는 즉시 앞으로 달려들어, 단검의 힘을 빌려 호위병들을 밀어내고 죄수를 빼낼 겁니다.\""]], [["“That seems to me as hazardous as uncertain, and convinces me that my scheme is far better than yours.”", "\"내가 보기에 그건 위험천만한 데다 불확실하기까지 해서, 내 계획이 자네 것보다 훨씬 낫다는 확신만 들게 하는군.\""]], [["“And what is your excellency’s project?”", "\"각하의 계획은 무엇입니까?\""]], [["“Just this.", "\"바로 이것이네."], ["I will so advantageously bestow 2,000 piastres, that the person receiving them shall obtain a respite till next year for Peppino; and during that year, another skilfully placed 1,000 piastres will afford him the means of escaping from his prison.”", "내가 2천 피아스트르를 아주 요긴하게 쓰면, 그 돈을 받은 자가 페피노에게 내년까지 형 집행 유예를 얻어 줄 걸세. 그리고 그 한 해 동안, 솜씨 좋게 또 한 번 1천 피아스트르를 쓰면 그가 감옥에서 빠져나올 방도가 마련될 게야.\""]], [["“And do you feel sure of succeeding?”", "\"그래서 성공을 확신하십니까?\""]], [["“_Pardieu!_” exclaimed the man in the cloak, suddenly expressing himself in French.", "\"파르디외!\" 망토를 두른 사내가 별안간 프랑스어로 내뱉었다."]], [["“What did your excellency say?”", "\"각하, 무어라 하셨습니까?\""], ["inquired the other.", "상대가 물었다."]], [["“I said, my good fellow, that I would do more single-handed by the means of gold than you and all your troop could effect with stilettos, pistols, carbines, and blunderbusses included.", "\"이런 말을 했네, 여보게. 자네와 자네의 온 패거리가 단검에 권총, 기병총, 나팔총까지 다 동원해 해낼 수 있는 것보다, 내가 황금의 힘으로 혼자서 더 많은 일을 해내겠다고 말이야."], ["Leave me, then, to act, and have no fears for the result.”", "그러니 일은 내게 맡기고, 결과는 걱정 말게.\""]], [["“At least, there can be no harm in myself and party being in readiness, in case your excellency should fail.”", "\"적어도 각하께서 실패하실 경우에 대비해, 저와 제 패거리가 채비를 갖추고 있는 것은 해될 게 없겠지요.\""]], [["“None whatever.", "\"전혀 해될 게 없네."], ["Take what precautions you please, if it is any satisfaction to you to do so; but rely upon my obtaining the reprieve I seek.”", "그렇게 하는 게 자네 마음이 놓인다면, 좋을 대로 대비책을 마련해 두게. 하지만 내가 구하려는 그 유예는 반드시 얻어 낼 테니 믿게나.\""]], [["“Remember, the execution is fixed for the day after tomorrow, and that you have but one day to work in.”", "\"처형이 모레로 잡혀 있고, 각하께 주어진 시간이 하루뿐이라는 걸 잊지 마십시오.\""]], [["“And what of that?", "\"그게 어떻단 말인가?"], ["Is not a day divided into twenty-four hours, each hour into sixty minutes, and every minute sub-divided into sixty seconds?", "하루는 스물네 시간으로 나뉘고, 한 시간은 육십 분으로, 또 일 분은 육십 초로 잘게 나뉘지 않나?"], ["Now in 86,400 seconds very many things can be done.”", "8만 6천 4백 초면 참으로 숱한 일을 해낼 수 있다네.\""]], [["“And how shall I know whether your excellency has succeeded or not.”", "\"각하께서 성공하셨는지 아닌지는 제가 어떻게 알 수 있습니까?\""]], [["“Oh, that is very easily arranged.", "\"아, 그건 아주 쉽게 일러 둘 수 있네."], ["I have engaged the three lower windows at the Café Rospoli; should I have obtained the requisite pardon for Peppino, the two outside windows will be hung with yellow damasks, and the centre with white, having a large cross in red marked on it.”", "내가 카페 로스폴리의 아래층 창문 셋을 빌려 두었네. 페피노에게 필요한 사면을 얻어 냈다면, 바깥쪽 창 둘에는 노란 다마스크 천을 걸고, 가운데 창에는 흰 천에 커다란 붉은 십자가를 그려서 걸 걸세.\""]], [["“And whom will you employ to carry the reprieve to the officer directing the execution?”", "\"그럼 처형을 지휘하는 관리에게 그 유예장을 누구를 시켜 전하실 작정이십니까?\""]], [["“Send one of your men, disguised as a penitent friar, and I will give it to him.", "\"자네 부하 하나를 참회 수사로 변장시켜 보내게. 그러면 내가 그에게 그것을 건네지. 그 옷차림 덕에 그는 처형대 바로 곁까지 다가갈 수 있을 테고, 공식 명령서를 관리에게 전하면, 관리는 다시 그것을 사형 집행인에게 넘기겠지."], ["His dress will procure him the means of approaching the scaffold itself, and he will deliver the official order to the officer, who, in his turn, will hand it to the executioner; in the meantime, it will be as well to acquaint Peppino with what we have determined on, if it be only to prevent his dying of fear or losing his senses, because in either case a very useless expense will have been incurred.”", "그동안 페피노에게도 우리가 결정한 바를 알려 두는 게 좋겠네. 적어도 그가 겁에 질려 죽거나 정신을 잃지 않게는 해야지. 어느 쪽이든 그렇게 되면 아주 쓸데없는 돈을 쓴 셈이 될 테니까.\""]], [["“Your excellency,”", "\"각하.\" 사내가 말했다."], ["said the man, “you are fully persuaded of my entire devotion to you, are you not?”", "\"제가 각하께 온전히 헌신하고 있다는 것을 충분히 믿으시지요, 그렇지 않습니까?\""]], [["“Nay, I flatter myself that there can be no doubt of it,”", "\"암, 그 점은 의심의 여지가 없다고 자부하네.\""], ["replied the cavalier in the cloak.", "망토를 두른 그 신사가 대답했다."]], [["“Well, then, only fulfil your promise of rescuing Peppino, and henceforward you shall receive not only devotion, but the most absolute obedience from myself and those under me that one human being can render to another.”", "\"그러시다면, 페피노를 구해 주시겠다는 약속만 지켜 주십시오. 그러면 이제부터 각하께서는 저와 제 부하들로부터 헌신뿐만 아니라, 한 인간이 다른 인간에게 바칠 수 있는 가장 절대적인 복종까지 받으실 겁니다.\""]], [["“Have a care how far you pledge yourself, my good friend, for I may remind you of your promise at some, perhaps, not very distant period, when I, in my turn, may require your aid and influence.”", "\"섣불리 약속을 다짐하지 말게, 여보게. 어쩌면 그리 멀지 않은 어느 날, 이번엔 내가 자네의 도움과 영향력을 필요로 할 때, 그 약속을 상기시킬지도 모르니까.\""]], [["“Let that day come sooner or later, your excellency will find me what I have found you in this my heavy trouble; and if from the other end of the world you but write me word to do such or such a thing, you may regard it as done, for done it shall be, on the word and faith of——”", "\"그날이 이르건 늦건, 각하께서는 이 무거운 곤경 속에서 제가 각하를 본 그대로의 각하를 보시게 될 겁니다. 세상 저 끝에서라도 이러저러한 일을 하라고 한마디만 적어 보내시면, 그건 다 된 일로 여기셔도 좋습니다. 반드시 해낼 테니까요. 제 이름과 신의를 걸고——\""]], [["“Hush!”", "\"쉿!\""], ["interrupted the stranger; “I hear a noise.”", "이방인이 말을 가로막았다. \"무슨 소리가 들리는군.\""]], [["“’Tis some travellers, who are visiting the Colosseum by torchlight.”", "\"횃불을 들고 콜로세움을 구경하는 여행객들일 겁니다.\""]], [["“’Twere better we should not be seen together; those guides are nothing but spies, and might possibly recognize you; and, however I may be honored by your friendship, my worthy friend, if once the extent of our intimacy were known, I am sadly afraid both my reputation and credit would suffer thereby.”", "\"우리가 함께 있는 모습을 들키지 않는 게 좋겠네. 저 안내인들은 첩자나 다름없어서, 어쩌면 자네를 알아볼지도 모르니까. 여보게, 자네의 우정으로 내가 아무리 영광스럽다 한들, 우리의 친분이 얼마나 두터운지 한번 알려지는 날이면, 내 평판도 신용도 그로 인해 몹시 상하지 않을까 심히 두렵네.\""]], [["“Well, then, if you obtain the reprieve?”", "\"그럼 유예를 얻어 내시면요?\""]], [["“The middle window at the Café Rospoli will be hung with white damask, bearing a red cross.”", "\"카페 로스폴리의 가운데 창에 붉은 십자가를 새긴 흰 다마스크 천을 걸겠네.\""]], [["“And if you fail?”", "\"실패하시면요?\""]], [["“Then all three windows will have yellow draperies.”", "\"그럼 창 셋 모두에 노란 휘장을 걸지.\""]], [["“And then?”", "\"그러면요?\""]], [["“And then, my good fellow, use your daggers in any way you please, and I further promise you to be there as a spectator of your prowess.”", "\"그러면, 여보게, 자네 좋을 대로 단검을 쓰게. 그리고 한 가지 더 약속하지. 내가 그 자리에 자네의 무용을 지켜보는 구경꾼으로 가 있겠다고 말일세.\""]], [["“We understand each other perfectly, then.", "\"그럼 우리는 서로를 완벽히 이해한 셈이군요."], ["Adieu, your excellency; depend upon me as firmly as I do upon you.”", "안녕히 가십시오, 각하. 제가 각하를 믿는 만큼 굳게 저를 믿어 주십시오.\""]], [["Saying these words, the Transteverin disappeared down the staircase, while his companion, muffling his features more closely than before in the folds of his mantle, passed almost close to Franz, and descended to the arena by an outward flight of steps.", "이 말을 하고서 트라스테베레 사내는 계단 아래로 사라졌고, 그의 동행은 전보다 더 단단히 망토 자락에 얼굴을 파묻고 프란츠의 거의 코앞을 스쳐 지나, 바깥쪽 계단을 따라 원형 경기장으로 내려갔다."], ["The next minute Franz heard himself called by Albert, who made the lofty building re-echo with the sound of his friend’s name.", "다음 순간 프란츠는 알베르가 자기를 부르는 소리를 들었다. 그 드높은 건물이 벗의 이름을 메아리치게 울리고 있었다."], ["Franz, however, did not obey the summons till he had satisfied himself that the two men whose conversation he had overheard were at a sufficient distance to prevent his encountering them in his descent.", "그러나 프란츠는 자기가 엿들은 대화의 두 남자가 내려가는 길에 마주치지 않을 만큼 충분히 멀어졌다고 확신할 때까지 그 부름에 응하지 않았다."], ["In ten minutes after the strangers had departed, Franz was on the road to the Piazza di Spagna, listening with studied indifference to the learned dissertation delivered by Albert, after the manner of Pliny and Calpurnius, touching the iron-pointed nets used to prevent the ferocious beasts from springing on the spectators.", "이방인들이 떠난 지 십 분 뒤, 프란츠는 스페인 광장으로 가는 길에 올라, 알베르가 플리니우스와 칼푸르니우스의 방식을 따라 늘어놓는 학식 어린 강론에 짐짓 무심한 척 귀를 기울이고 있었다. 사나운 짐승들이 구경꾼들에게 달려드는 것을 막으려고 쓰던 쇠창 박힌 그물에 관한 이야기였다."]], [["Franz let him proceed without interruption, and, in fact, did not hear what was said; he longed to be alone, and free to ponder over all that had occurred.", "프란츠는 그가 끊임없이 떠들도록 내버려 두었으나, 실은 그 말을 한마디도 듣고 있지 않았다. 그는 혼자가 되어, 방금 일어난 모든 일을 마음껏 곱씹고 싶었다."], ["One of the two men, whose mysterious meeting in the Colosseum he had so unintentionally witnessed, was an entire stranger to him, but not so the other; and though Franz had been unable to distinguish his features, from his being either wrapped in his mantle or obscured by the shadow, the tones of his voice had made too powerful an impression on him the first time he had heard them for him ever again to forget them, hear them when or where he might.", "콜로세움에서 우연히 목격한 그 수수께끼 같은 만남의 두 남자 가운데 하나는 그에게 전혀 낯선 사람이었으나, 다른 하나는 그렇지 않았다. 비록 프란츠는 그 얼굴을 분간할 수 없었지만—그가 망토에 싸여 있었거나 그늘에 가려 있었던 탓이었다—그 목소리의 음색만큼은 처음 들었을 때부터 그에게 너무도 강렬한 인상을 남긴 터라, 언제 어디서 다시 듣든 결코 잊을 수 없을 것이었다."], ["It was more especially when this man was speaking in a manner half jesting, half bitter, that Franz’s ear recalled most vividly the deep sonorous, yet well-pitched voice that had addressed him in the grotto of Monte Cristo, and which he heard for the second time amid the darkness and ruined grandeur of the Colosseum.", "특히 그 남자가 반쯤은 농담조로, 반쯤은 쓰디쓴 어조로 말할 때면, 프란츠의 귀는 몬테 크리스토의 동굴에서 자기에게 말을 건넸던 그 깊고 낭랑하면서도 잘 다듬어진 목소리를 가장 또렷이 떠올렸다. 그리고 이제 그 목소리를 콜로세움의 어둠과 폐허가 된 장엄함 한가운데서 두 번째로 들은 것이었다."], ["And the more he thought, the more entire was his conviction, that the person who wore the mantle was no other than his former host and entertainer, “Sinbad the Sailor.” 20139m", "그리고 생각하면 할수록, 망토를 두른 그 사람이 다름 아닌 자신의 옛 집주인이자 접대자였던 '뱃사람 신드바드'라는 확신은 더욱 굳어졌다."]], [["Under any other circumstances, Franz would have found it impossible to resist his extreme curiosity to know more of so singular a personage, and with that intent have sought to renew their short acquaintance; but in the present instance, the confidential nature of the conversation he had overheard made him, with propriety, judge that his appearance at such a time would be anything but agreeable.", "다른 어떤 상황에서였다면, 프란츠는 그토록 기이한 인물에 대해 더 알고 싶은 극심한 호기심을 도저히 억누르지 못하고, 그 짧은 친분을 다시 잇고자 했을 것이다. 하지만 지금 이 경우에는, 자기가 엿들은 대화의 은밀한 성격으로 미루어, 이런 때에 자기가 나타나는 것은 결코 달가운 일이 아니리라고 분별 있게 판단했다."], ["As we have seen, therefore, he permitted his former host to retire without attempting a recognition, but fully promising himself a rich indemnity for his present forbearance should chance afford him another opportunity.", "그리하여 앞서 보았듯이, 그는 자기 옛 집주인이 알은체도 하지 않고 물러가도록 내버려 두었으나, 만약 우연히 또 한 번 기회가 주어진다면 지금 이 자제에 대해 톡톡히 보상받으리라고 단단히 다짐했다."]], [["In vain did Franz endeavor to forget the many perplexing thoughts which assailed him; in vain did he court the refreshment of sleep.", "프란츠는 자기를 덮쳐 오는 그 숱한 어지러운 생각들을 떨쳐 내려 했으나 헛수고였고, 잠의 안식을 청해 보았으나 그 또한 부질없었다."], ["Slumber refused to visit his eyelids and the night was passed in feverish contemplation of the chain of circumstances tending to prove the identity of the mysterious visitant to the Colosseum with the inhabitant of the grotto of Monte Cristo; and the more he thought, the firmer grew his opinion on the subject.", "잠은 그의 눈꺼풀을 찾아오기를 마다했고, 밤은 콜로세움의 그 수수께끼 같은 방문객과 몬테 크리스토 동굴의 거주자가 동일 인물임을 입증하는 일련의 정황을 열에 들떠 곱씹는 가운데 흘러갔다. 그리고 생각하면 할수록 그 문제에 대한 그의 의견은 더욱 확고해졌다."]], [["Worn out at length, he fell asleep at daybreak, and did not awake till late.", "마침내 기진맥진한 그는 동틀 무렵에야 잠이 들어, 늦도록 깨어나지 못했다."], ["Like a genuine Frenchman, Albert had employed his time in arranging for the evening’s diversion; he had sent to engage a box at the Teatro Argentina; and Franz, having a number of letters to write, relinquished the carriage to Albert for the whole of the day.", "천생 프랑스 사람인 알베르는 그동안 저녁의 놀이를 마련하느라 시간을 썼다. 그는 사람을 보내 아르젠티나 극장의 특별석을 잡아 두었다. 그리고 프란츠는 써야 할 편지가 여러 통 있던 터라, 하루 종일 마차를 알베르에게 양보했다."]], [["At five o’clock Albert returned, delighted with his day’s work; he had been occupied in leaving his letters of introduction, and had received in return more invitations to balls and routs than it would be possible for him to accept; besides this, he had seen (as he called it) all the remarkable sights at Rome.", "다섯 시에 알베르가 하루 일에 흡족해하며 돌아왔다. 그는 소개장들을 돌리며 시간을 보냈고, 그 답례로 도저히 다 받아들일 수 없을 만큼 많은 무도회와 야회 초대를 받았다. 그뿐 아니라 그는 (그의 표현을 빌리자면) 로마의 볼 만한 명소를 죄다 보고 왔다."], ["Yes, in a single day he had accomplished what his more serious-minded companion would have taken weeks to effect.", "그렇다, 그는 단 하루 만에, 더 진중한 그의 동행이라면 몇 주는 걸렸을 일을 해치운 것이다."], ["Neither had he neglected to ascertain the name of the piece to be played that night at the Teatro Argentina, and also what performers appeared in it.", "또한 그는 그날 밤 아르젠티나 극장에서 공연될 작품의 이름과, 거기 출연하는 배우들이 누구인지 알아내는 것도 빠뜨리지 않았다."], ["The opera of _Parisina_ was announced for representation, and the principal actors were Coselli, Moriani, and La Specchia.", "오페라 〈파리지나〉의 상연이 예고되어 있었고, 주역 배우들은 코셀리와 모리아니, 라 스페키아였다."]], [["The young men, therefore, had reason to consider themselves fortunate in having the opportunity of hearing one of the best works by the composer of _Lucia di Lammermoor_, supported by three of the most renowned vocalists of Italy.", "그러니 두 젊은이는 이탈리아에서 가장 이름난 성악가 셋의 뒷받침을 받아 〈람메르무어의 루치아〉를 작곡한 그 작곡가의 가장 빼어난 작품 가운데 하나를 들을 기회를 얻은 자신들을 운 좋다고 여길 만했다."]], [["Albert had never been able to endure the Italian theatres, with their orchestras from which it is impossible to see, and the absence of balconies, or open boxes; all these defects pressed hard on a man who had had his stall at the Bouffes, and had shared a lower box at the Opera.", "알베르는 도무지 이탈리아 극장을 견뎌 내질 못했다. 무대가 보이지 않는 위치의 관현악단이며, 발코니나 트인 특별석이 없는 것이며—이 모든 결점이, 부프 극장에 자기 좌석을 두고 오페라 극장의 아래층 특별석을 함께 쓰던 사내에게는 견디기 힘들었다."], ["Still, in spite of this, Albert displayed his most dazzling and effective costumes each time he visited the theatres; but, alas, his elegant toilet was wholly thrown away, and one of the most worthy representatives of Parisian fashion had to carry with him the mortifying reflection that he had nearly overrun Italy without meeting with a single adventure.", "그래도 이런데도 불구하고, 알베르는 극장에 갈 때마다 자기의 가장 눈부시고 효과적인 의상을 뽐냈다. 그러나 아아, 그의 우아한 차림새는 온전히 헛수고였고, 파리 멋쟁이의 가장 훌륭한 본보기 가운데 하나는 단 한 번의 모험도 만나지 못한 채 이탈리아를 거의 다 돌아 버렸다는 굴욕적인 상념을 떠안아야 했다."]], [["Sometimes Albert would affect to make a joke of his want of success; but internally he was deeply wounded, and his self-love immensely piqued, to think that Albert de Morcerf, the most admired and most sought after of any young person of his day, should thus be passed over, and merely have his labor for his pains.", "이따금 알베르는 자기의 성공 없음을 짐짓 농담거리로 삼곤 했다. 하지만 속으로는 깊이 상처를 입었고, 당대 어느 젊은이보다 사랑받고 쫓김을 받던 알베르 드 모르세르가 이렇게 외면당하고 그저 헛수고만 했다는 생각에 그의 자존심은 몹시 짓밟혔다."], ["And the thing was so much the more annoying, as, according to the characteristic modesty of a Frenchman, Albert had quitted Paris with the full conviction that he had only to show himself in Italy to carry all before him, and that upon his return he should astonish the Parisian world with the recital of his numerous love-affairs.", "게다가 그 일이 더더욱 분했던 까닭은, 프랑스 사람 특유의 그 겸손함에 따라, 알베르는 이탈리아에서는 자기가 모습을 드러내기만 하면 모든 것을 휩쓸 것이며, 돌아가면 자신의 숱한 연애담을 늘어놓아 파리 사교계를 놀라게 하리라고 굳게 확신한 채 파리를 떠나왔기 때문이었다."]], [["Alas, poor Albert!", "아아, 가엾은 알베르! 그 흥미진진한 모험들 가운데 어느 하나도 그의 앞길에 떨어지지 않았다."], ["None of those interesting adventures fell in his way; the lovely Genoese, Florentines, and Neapolitans were all faithful, if not to their husbands, at least to their lovers, and thought not of changing even for the splendid appearance of Albert de Morcerf; and all he gained was the painful conviction that the ladies of Italy have this advantage over those of France, that they are faithful even in their infidelity.", "어여쁜 제노바와 피렌체, 나폴리의 여인들은, 남편에게는 아닐지언정 적어도 정부에게는 모두 충실했고, 알베르 드 모르세르의 그 화려한 모습을 보고서도 상대를 바꿀 생각은 하지 않았다. 그가 얻은 것이라곤, 이탈리아 여인들이 프랑스 여인들보다 한 가지 점에서 낫다는 쓰라린 확신뿐이었다. 그들은 부정을 저지를 때조차 충실하다는 것이었다."]], [["Yet he could not restrain a hope that in Italy, as elsewhere, there might be an exception to the general rule.", "그래도 그는 이탈리아에서도 다른 곳에서처럼, 그 일반적인 법칙에 예외가 있을지 모른다는 희망만은 억누를 수 없었다."]], [["Albert, besides being an elegant, well-looking young man, was also possessed of considerable talent and ability; moreover, he was a viscount—a recently created one, certainly, but in the present day it is not necessary to go as far back as Noah in tracing a descent, and a genealogical tree is equally estimated, whether dated from 1399 or merely 1815; but to crown all these advantages, Albert de Morcerf commanded an income of 50,000 livres, a more than sufficient sum to render him a personage of considerable importance in Paris.", "알베르는 우아하고 잘생긴 젊은이일 뿐 아니라, 상당한 재능과 능력도 갖추고 있었다. 게다가 그는 자작이었다—물론 최근에 작위를 받은 자작이긴 했지만, 오늘날에는 가문의 내력을 노아까지 거슬러 올라가 따질 필요가 없으며, 가계도란 1399년에서 시작되었건 그저 1815년에서 시작되었건 똑같이 높이 쳐주는 법이다. 그러나 이 모든 이점에 화룡점정을 더하자면, 알베르 드 모르세르는 5만 리브르의 수입을 거느리고 있었으니, 파리에서 그를 상당히 중요한 인물로 만들기에 차고도 넘치는 액수였다."], ["It was therefore no small mortification to him to have visited most of the principal cities in Italy without having excited the most trifling observation.", "그러니 이탈리아의 주요 도시 대부분을 둘러보고도 가장 사소한 눈길조차 끌지 못했다는 것은 그에게 적잖은 굴욕이었다."]], [["Albert, however, hoped to indemnify himself for all these slights and indifferences during the Carnival, knowing full well that among the different states and kingdoms in which this festivity is celebrated, Rome is the spot where even the wisest and gravest throw off the usual rigidity of their lives, and deign to mingle in the follies of this time of liberty and relaxation.", "그러나 알베르는 사육제 동안에 이 모든 무시와 냉대를 보상받으리라 기대하고 있었다. 이 축제가 거행되는 여러 나라와 왕국들 가운데, 가장 지혜롭고 진중한 이들조차 평소의 그 엄격함을 벗어던지고 기꺼이 이 자유와 방종의 시절의 흥청거림에 끼어드는 곳이 바로 로마라는 것을 그는 잘 알고 있었던 것이다."], ["The Carnival was to commence on the morrow; therefore Albert had not an instant to lose in setting forth the programme of his hopes, expectations, and claims to notice.", "사육제는 이튿날 시작될 참이었으므로, 알베르로서는 자신의 희망과 기대, 그리고 주목받을 권리의 계획표를 펼쳐 놓는 데 한순간도 허비할 수 없었다."]], [["With this design he had engaged a box in the most conspicuous part of the theatre, and exerted himself to set off his personal attractions by the aid of the most rich and elaborate toilet.", "이런 속셈으로 그는 극장에서 가장 눈에 잘 띄는 자리에 특별석을 잡았고, 더없이 화려하고 공들인 차림새의 힘을 빌려 자신의 매력을 한껏 돋보이게 하려고 애썼다."], ["The box taken by Albert was in the first circle; although each of the three tiers of boxes is deemed equally aristocratic, and is, for this reason, generally styled the “nobility’s boxes,” and although the box engaged for the two friends was sufficiently capacious to contain at least a dozen persons, it had cost less than would be paid at some of the French theatres for one admitting merely four occupants.", "알베르가 잡은 특별석은 첫째 층에 있었다. 세 층의 특별석 모두가 똑같이 귀족적인 것으로 여겨져, 그 때문에 흔히 '귀족석'이라 불리긴 하지만, 그리고 두 벗을 위해 잡은 그 특별석이 적어도 열두 명은 너끈히 들어갈 만큼 넓었지만, 그 값은 프랑스의 어느 극장에서 고작 네 사람 드는 자리에 치를 값보다도 적게 들었다."]], [["Another motive had influenced Albert’s selection of his seat,—who knew but that, thus advantageously placed, he might not in truth attract the notice of some fair Roman, and an introduction might ensue that would procure him the offer of a seat in a carriage, or a place in a princely balcony, from which he might behold the gayeties of the Carnival?", "알베르가 자리를 고르는 데에는 또 다른 동기도 작용했다. 이렇게 좋은 위치에 자리 잡으면, 어느 어여쁜 로마 여인의 눈길을 정말로 끌게 될지, 그래서 소개로 이어져 마차 한자리나 어느 귀족의 발코니 한 자리를 권유받게 되어, 거기서 사육제의 흥겨운 광경을 굽어보게 될지 누가 알겠는가?"], ["These united considerations made Albert more lively and anxious to please than he had hitherto been.", "이 여러 가지 생각이 한데 어우러져, 알베르는 여태껏보다도 더 활기차고 환심을 사려 안달이 났다."], ["Totally disregarding the business of the stage, he leaned from his box and began attentively scrutinizing the beauty of each pretty woman, aided by a powerful opera-glass; but, alas, this attempt to attract notice wholly failed; not even curiosity had been excited, and it was but too apparent that the lovely creatures, into whose good graces he was desirous of stealing, were all so much engrossed with themselves, their lovers, or their own thoughts, that they had not so much as noticed him or the manipulation of his glass.", "무대 위의 일은 아예 거들떠보지도 않고, 그는 특별석에서 몸을 내밀어 강력한 오페라글라스의 도움을 받아 가며 예쁜 여인 하나하나의 미모를 열심히 뜯어보기 시작했다. 그러나 아아, 눈길을 끌어 보려던 이 시도는 완전히 실패했다. 호기심조차 동하지 않았으니, 그가 그 환심 안으로 슬며시 비집고 들고 싶어 한 그 사랑스러운 여인들은 하나같이 자기 자신이나 자기 정부, 혹은 자기 생각에 어찌나 골몰해 있던지, 그도, 그가 안경을 만지작거리는 모습도 거들떠보지 않았다는 것이 너무도 분명했다."]], [["The truth was, that the anticipated pleasures of the Carnival, with the “Holy Week” that was to succeed it, so filled every fair breast, as to prevent the least attention being bestowed even on the business of the stage.", "사실인즉, 사육제와 그 뒤를 이을 '성주간'의 기대되는 즐거움이 어여쁜 여인들의 가슴마다 어찌나 가득 차 있었던지, 무대 위의 일에조차 조금의 관심도 기울이지 못하게 만든 것이었다."], ["The actors made their entries and exits unobserved or unthought of; at certain conventional moments, the spectators would suddenly cease their conversation, or rouse themselves from their musings, to listen to some brilliant effort of Moriani’s, a well-executed recitative by Coselli, or to join in loud applause at the wonderful powers of La Specchia; but that momentary excitement over, they quickly relapsed into their former state of preoccupation or interesting conversation.", "배우들은 아무도 알아채거나 마음 쓰는 이 없이 등장하고 퇴장했다. 다만 어떤 관습적인 순간이 되면, 관객들은 갑자기 대화를 멈추거나 상념에서 깨어나, 모리아니의 어떤 빼어난 열창이나 코셀리의 잘 다듬어진 서창에 귀를 기울이거나, 라 스페키아의 놀라운 기량에 우레와 같은 갈채를 함께 보내곤 했다. 그러나 그 잠깐의 흥분이 가시면, 그들은 이내 다시금 본래의 그 골몰함이나 흥미로운 대화에 빠져들었다."]], [["Towards the close of the first act, the door of a box which had been hitherto vacant was opened; a lady entered to whom Franz had been introduced in Paris, where indeed, he had imagined she still was.", "1막이 끝나갈 무렵, 여태 비어 있던 한 특별석의 문이 열렸다. 한 부인이 들어왔는데, 프란츠가 파리에서 소개받은 적이 있는 사람이었다. 실은 그는 그녀가 아직 거기 있으리라 여기고 있었다."], ["The quick eye of Albert caught the involuntary start with which his friend beheld the new arrival, and, turning to him, he said hastily:", "알베르의 날카로운 눈은 벗이 그 새 도착한 이를 보고 저도 모르게 흠칫하는 모습을 놓치지 않았다. 그래서 그쪽으로 돌아서며 다급히 말했다."]], [["“Do you know the woman who has just entered that box?”", "\"방금 저 특별석에 들어온 여인을 아나?\""]], [["“Yes; what do you think of her?”", "\"그래. 어떻게 생각하나?\""]], [["“Oh, she is perfectly lovely—what a complexion!", "\"오, 더없이 사랑스럽군—저 피부 좀 보게!"], ["And such magnificent hair!", "게다가 저 멋진 머리카락이라니!"], ["Is she French?”", "프랑스 사람인가?\""]], [["“No; a Venetian.”", "\"아니, 베네치아 사람이야.\""]], [["“And her name is——”", "\"그럼 이름이——\""]], [["“Countess G——.”", "\"G백작 부인이지.\""]], [["“Ah, I know her by name!”", "\"아, 이름은 알고 있네!\""], ["exclaimed Albert; “she is said to possess as much wit and cleverness as beauty.", "알베르가 외쳤다. \"미모만큼이나 재치와 영민함을 갖춘 분이라고들 하더군."], ["I was to have been presented to her when I met her at Madame Villefort’s ball.”", "빌포르 부인의 무도회에서 그분을 만났을 때 소개받기로 되어 있었지.\""]], [["“Shall I assist you in repairing your negligence?”", "\"자네의 그 소홀함을 바로잡아 주는 일을 내가 도와줄까?\""], ["asked Franz.", "프란츠가 물었다."]], [["“My dear fellow, are you really on such good terms with her as to venture to take me to her box?”", "\"이보게, 자네 정말 저분과 그렇게 가까운 사이라서 나를 저분의 특별석으로 데려갈 엄두까지 낼 수 있는 건가?\""]], [["“Why, I have only had the honor of being in her society and conversing with her three or four times in my life; but you know that even such an acquaintance as that might warrant my doing what you ask.”", "\"뭐, 내 평생 저분과 같은 자리에 있으면서 이야기를 나눈 영광을 누린 게 서너 번뿐이긴 하네만, 자네도 알다시피 그 정도 친분이라도 자네가 청하는 일을 할 명분은 될 테지.\""]], [["At that instant, the countess perceived Franz, and graciously waved her hand to him, to which he replied by a respectful inclination of the head.", "바로 그 순간, 백작 부인이 프란츠를 알아보고는 우아하게 손을 흔들어 보였고, 그는 정중하게 고개를 숙여 답했다. \"정말이지,\""], ["“Upon my word,”", "알베르가 말했다."], ["said Albert, “you seem to be on excellent terms with the beautiful countess.”", "\"자네는 저 아름다운 백작 부인과 더없이 좋은 사이인 것 같군.\""]], [["“You are mistaken in thinking so,”", "\"그렇게 생각한다면 자네가 틀린 걸세.\" 프란츠가 차분히 대꾸했다."], ["returned Franz calmly; “but you merely fall into the same error which leads so many of our countrymen to commit the most egregious blunders,—I mean that of judging the habits and customs of Italy and Spain by our Parisian notions; believe me, nothing is more fallacious than to form any estimate of the degree of intimacy you may suppose existing among persons by the familiar terms they seem upon; there is a similarity of feeling at this instant between ourselves and the countess—nothing more.”", "\"자네는 그저 우리 동포들 가운데 그토록 많은 이가 어처구니없는 실수를 저지르게 만드는 그 똑같은 오류에 빠져 있을 뿐이야—이탈리아와 에스파냐의 습속과 관례를 우리 파리식 관념으로 재단하는 오류 말이지. 내 말을 믿게, 사람들이 격의 없는 말투를 주고받는다고 해서 그들 사이에 있으리라 짐작되는 친분의 정도를 가늠하는 것만큼 그릇된 일도 없네. 지금 이 순간 우리와 백작 부인 사이에는 어떤 감정의 닮음이 있을 뿐이야—그 이상은 아무것도 없네.\""]], [["“Is there, indeed, my good fellow?", "\"정말 그런가, 이 사람아?"], ["Pray tell me, is it sympathy of heart?”", "어디 말해 보게, 그게 마음의 공감인가?\""]], [["“No; of taste,”", "\"아니, 취향의 공감이지.\""], ["continued Franz gravely.", "프란츠가 진지하게 말을 이었다."]], [["“And in what manner has this congeniality of mind been evinced?”", "\"한데 그 마음의 어울림이라는 게 어떤 식으로 드러났나?\""]], [["“By the countess’s visiting the Colosseum, as we did last night, by moonlight, and nearly alone.”", "\"백작 부인이 콜로세움을 찾았는데, 우리가 어젯밤 그랬듯이 달빛 아래 거의 혼자서 갔다는 걸세.\""]], [["“You were with her, then?”", "\"그럼 자네가 그분과 함께 있었나?\""]], [["“I was.”", "\"그랬지.\""]], [["“And what did you say to her?”", "\"그래, 그분께 무슨 말을 했나?\""]], [["“Oh, we talked of the illustrious dead of whom that magnificent ruin is a glorious monument!”", "\"오, 그 장엄한 폐허가 영광스러운 기념물로 남은, 그 이름난 망자들에 대해 이야기했지!\""]], [["“Upon my word,”", "\"정말이지,\""], ["cried Albert, “you must have been a very entertaining companion alone, or all but alone, with a beautiful woman in such a place of sentiment as the Colosseum, and yet to find nothing better to talk about than the dead!", "알베르가 외쳤다. \"콜로세움 같은 그토록 감상적인 장소에서 아름다운 여인과 단둘이, 아니 거의 단둘이 있으면서, 고작 망자 이야기 말고는 더 나은 화제를 찾지 못했다니, 자네 참 흥겨운 동행이었겠군!"], ["All I can say is, if ever I should get such a chance, the living should be my theme.”", "내가 할 수 있는 말이라곤, 나라면 만약 그런 기회를 얻는다면, 살아 있는 사람을 화제로 삼았을 거라는 것뿐이야.\""]], [["20143m\n“And you will probably find your theme ill-chosen.”", "\"그리고 자네는 아마 그 화제를 잘못 골랐다는 걸 알게 될 걸세.\""]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["said Albert, breaking in upon his discourse, “never mind the past; let us only remember the present.", "알베르가 그의 말허리를 자르며 말했다. \"지난 일은 신경 쓰지 말고, 현재만 기억하세."], ["Are you not going to keep your promise of introducing me to the fair subject of our remarks?”", "우리가 이야기한 그 어여쁜 여인에게 나를 소개해 주겠다던 약속을 지킬 셈인가, 안인가?\""]], [["“Certainly, directly the curtain falls on the stage.”", "\"물론이지, 무대의 막이 내리는 즉시 말이야.\""]], [["“What a confounded long time this first act lasts.", "\"이 1막은 어찌 이리도 지긋지긋하게 긴지."], ["I believe, on my soul, that they never mean to finish it.”", "정말이지, 끝낼 생각이 도무지 없는 것 같군.\""]], [["“Oh, yes, they will; only listen to that charming finale.", "\"오, 아닐세, 끝낼 거야. 저 매혹적인 피날레나 좀 들어 보게."], ["How exquisitely Coselli sings his part.”", "코셀리가 자기 배역을 얼마나 절묘하게 노래하는지.\""]], [["“But what an awkward, inelegant fellow he is.”", "\"하지만 저자는 어찌 저리 어설프고 볼품없는 사내인지.\""]], [["“Well, then, what do you say to La Specchia?", "\"그래, 그럼 라 스페키아는 어떤가?"], ["Did you ever see anything more perfect than her acting?”", "저보다 더 완벽한 연기를 본 적 있나?\""]], [["“Why, you know, my dear fellow, when one has been accustomed to Malibran and Sontag, such singers as these don’t make the same impression on you they perhaps do on others.”", "\"이보게, 알다시피 한번 말리브랑이나 존타크에 익숙해진 사람한테는, 저런 가수들이 다른 이들에게 줄지 모를 인상을 똑같이 주지는 못하는 법이라네.\""]], [["“At least, you must admire Moriani’s style and execution.”", "\"적어도 모리아니의 양식과 가창만큼은 감탄해야 할 걸세.\""]], [["“I never fancied men of his dark, ponderous appearance singing with a voice like a woman’s.”", "\"저렇게 거무튀튀하고 둔중하게 생긴 사내가 여자 같은 목소리로 노래하는 건 도무지 상상이 안 가서 말이야.\""]], [["“My good friend,”", "\"이 사람아,\" 프란츠가 그에게로 돌아서며 말했다."], ["said Franz, turning to him, while Albert continued to point his glass at every box in the theatre, “you seem determined not to approve; you are really too difficult to please.”", "그러는 동안에도 알베르는 극장의 특별석마다 자기 안경을 겨누고 있었다. \"자네는 무엇 하나 마음에 들어 하지 않기로 작정한 모양이군. 자네는 정말이지 비위 맞추기가 너무 까다로워.\""]], [["The curtain at length fell on the performances, to the infinite satisfaction of the Viscount of Morcerf, who seized his hat, rapidly passed his fingers through his hair, arranged his cravat and wristbands, and signified to Franz that he was waiting for him to lead the way.", "마침내 공연의 막이 내렸고, 모르세르 자작은 더없이 흡족해했다. 그는 모자를 집어 들고, 머리카락을 손가락으로 잽싸게 쓸어 넘기고, 크라바트와 소맷동을 매만지고는, 프란츠에게 앞장서 줄 것을 기다리고 있다는 뜻을 내비쳤다."]], [["Franz, who had mutely interrogated the countess, and received from her a gracious smile in token that he would be welcome, sought not to retard the gratification of Albert’s eager impatience, but began at once the tour of the house, closely followed by Albert, who availed himself of the few minutes required to reach the opposite side of the theatre to settle the height and smoothness of his collar, and to arrange the lappets of his coat.", "프란츠는 눈짓으로 백작 부인에게 물어 그녀로부터 환영한다는 표시로 우아한 미소를 받은 터라, 알베르의 안달 난 조바심을 채워 주는 일을 미루려 하지 않았다. 그는 곧장 객석을 돌기 시작했고, 알베르가 바짝 뒤따랐다. 알베르는 극장 반대편에 이르기까지의 그 짧은 몇 분을 이용해 옷깃의 높이와 매끄러움을 가다듬고 윗옷 자락을 정돈했다."], ["This important task was just completed as they arrived at the countess’s box.", "백작 부인의 특별석에 다다랐을 때, 이 중대한 작업은 막 끝난 참이었다."]], [["At the knock, the door was immediately opened, and the young man who was seated beside the countess, in obedience to the Italian custom, instantly rose and surrendered his place to the strangers, who, in turn, would be expected to retire upon the arrival of other visitors.", "노크 소리에 문이 곧바로 열렸고, 이탈리아 관습에 따라 백작 부인 곁에 앉아 있던 젊은이가 즉시 일어나 손님들에게 자리를 내주었다. 그 손님들 또한 다른 방문객이 오면 물러가야 할 처지였다."], ["Franz presented Albert as one of the most distinguished young men of the day, both as regarded his position in society and extraordinary talents; nor did he say more than the truth, for in Paris and the circle in which the viscount moved, he was looked upon and cited as a model of perfection.", "프란츠는 알베르를, 사교계에서의 지위로 보나 비범한 재능으로 보나 당대 가장 뛰어난 젊은이의 한 사람으로 소개했다. 그리고 그가 한 말은 사실 이상도 아니었으니, 파리와 자작이 드나드는 그 사교계에서 그는 완벽함의 본보기로 여겨지고 거론되었던 것이다."], ["Franz added that his companion, deeply grieved at having been prevented the honor of being presented to the countess during her sojourn in Paris, was most anxious to make up for it, and had requested him (Franz) to remedy the past misfortune by conducting him to her box, and concluded by asking pardon for his presumption in having taken it upon himself to do so.", "프란츠는 덧붙이기를, 자기 동행이 백작 부인이 파리에 머무는 동안 소개받는 영광을 누리지 못한 것을 몹시 애석해하던 차에, 그것을 만회하고 싶어 안달이 나, 자기(프란츠)에게 그를 부인의 특별석으로 안내함으로써 지난 불운을 바로잡아 달라고 청했노라고 했다. 그러고는 주제넘게 그 일을 떠맡은 데 대해 용서를 구하며 말을 맺었다."]], [["The countess, in reply, bowed gracefully to Albert, and extended her hand with cordial kindness to Franz; then, inviting Albert to take the vacant seat beside her, she recommended Franz to take the next best, if he wished to view the ballet, and pointed to the one behind her own chair.", "백작 부인은 그 답으로 알베르에게 우아하게 고개 숙여 인사하고는, 프란츠에게는 다정한 친절을 담아 손을 내밀었다. 그런 다음 알베르에게 자기 곁의 빈자리에 앉기를 권하고, 프란츠에게는 발레를 보고 싶다면 그다음으로 좋은 자리에 앉으라며 자기 의자 뒤쪽의 자리를 가리켰다."]], [["Albert was soon deeply engrossed in discoursing upon Paris and Parisian matters, speaking to the countess of the various persons they both knew there.", "알베르는 이내 파리와 파리의 이런저런 일들을 논하는 데 깊이 빠져들어, 두 사람이 그곳에서 함께 아는 여러 인물에 대해 백작 부인에게 이야기했다."], ["Franz perceived how completely he was in his element; and, unwilling to interfere with the pleasure he so evidently felt, took up Albert’s glass, and began in his turn to survey the audience.", "프란츠는 그가 얼마나 완전히 제 세상을 만났는지를 알아챘다. 그래서 그가 그토록 분명히 누리는 즐거움을 방해하고 싶지 않아, 알베르의 안경을 집어 들고 이번에는 자기 차례로 관객석을 둘러보기 시작했다."]], [["Sitting alone, in the front of a box immediately opposite, but situated on the third row, was a woman of exquisite beauty, dressed in a Greek costume, which evidently, from the ease and grace with which she wore it, was her national attire.", "바로 맞은편이지만 셋째 줄에 자리 잡은 한 특별석 앞쪽에, 한 여인이 혼자 앉아 있었다. 그리스풍 의상을 차려입은 더없이 아리따운 여인이었는데, 그것을 걸친 자연스러움과 우아함으로 미루어 그 옷이 그녀의 민족 의상임이 분명했다."], ["Behind her, but in deep shadow, was the outline of a masculine figure; but the features of this latter personage it was not possible to distinguish.", "그녀 뒤로, 그러나 짙은 그늘 속에, 어떤 남자의 윤곽이 보였으나, 그 인물의 얼굴은 도무지 분간할 수 없었다."], ["Franz could not forbear breaking in upon the apparently interesting conversation passing between the countess and Albert, to inquire of the former if she knew who was the fair Albanian opposite, since beauty such as hers was well worthy of being observed by either sex.", "프란츠는 백작 부인과 알베르 사이에 한창 오가는 듯한 흥미로운 대화에 끼어들지 않을 수 없었다. 부인에게 저 맞은편의 아름다운 알바니아 여인이 누구인지 아느냐고 물은 것이었다. 그녀 같은 미모라면 남녀를 막론하고 눈여겨볼 만한 가치가 충분했으니 말이다."]], [["“All I can tell about her,”", "\"저 여인에 대해 제가 말씀드릴 수 있는 거라곤,\""], ["replied the countess, “is, that she has been at Rome since the beginning of the season; for I saw her where she now sits the very first night of the season, and since then she has never missed a performance.", "백작 부인이 대답했다. \"철 초부터 로마에 와 있다는 것뿐이에요. 시즌 첫날 밤에 지금 저 앉아 있는 자리에서 저 여인을 보았는데, 그 뒤로 한 번도 공연을 거른 적이 없거든요."], ["Sometimes she is accompanied by the person who is now with her, and at others she is merely attended by a black servant.”", "어떤 때는 지금 함께 있는 저 사람과 같이 오고, 어떤 때는 그저 흑인 하인 하나만 거느리고 오지요.\""]], [["“And what do you think of her personal appearance?”", "\"그럼 저 여인의 용모는 어떻게 생각하십니까?\""]], [["“Oh, I consider her perfectly lovely—she is just my idea of what Medora must have been.”", "\"오, 더없이 사랑스럽다고 봐요—제가 생각하는 메도라의 모습이 꼭 저럴 거예요.\""]], [["Franz and the countess exchanged a smile, and then the latter resumed her conversation with Albert, while Franz returned to his previous survey of the house and company.", "프란츠와 백작 부인은 서로 미소를 주고받았고, 이어 부인은 다시 알베르와의 대화를 이어 갔다. 그동안 프란츠는 다시 아까 하던 대로 객석과 관객들을 둘러보았다. 발레의 막이 올랐는데, 이탈리아 유파의 그 빼어난 본보기 가운데 하나로, 앙리가 훌륭하게 안무하고 무대에 올린 것이었다."], ["The curtain rose on the ballet, which was one of those excellent specimens of the Italian school, admirably arranged and put on the stage by Henri, who has established for himself a great reputation throughout Italy for his taste and skill in the choreographic art—one of those masterly productions of grace, method, and elegance in which the whole _corps de ballet_, from the principal dancers to the humblest supernumerary, are all engaged on the stage at the same time; and a hundred and fifty persons may be seen exhibiting the same attitude, or elevating the same arm or leg with a simultaneous movement, that would lead you to suppose that but one mind, one act of volition, influenced the moving mass.", "앙리는 무용 예술에서의 안목과 솜씨로 이탈리아 전역에 큰 명성을 얻은 인물이었다. 그것은 우아함과 짜임새, 기품이 어우러진 거장다운 작품의 하나로, 주역 무용수에서부터 가장 하찮은 단역에 이르기까지 군무단 전체가 무대 위에 한꺼번에 나서는 그런 작품이었다. 백쉰 명에 이르는 사람들이 같은 자세를 취하거나, 같은 팔이나 다리를 일제히 들어 올리는 광경을 볼 수 있었으니, 그 움직이는 무리 전체를 단 하나의 정신, 단 하나의 의지가 좌우하고 있다고 여기게 할 정도였다."]], [["The ballet was called _Poliska_.", "그 발레의 제목은 〈폴리스카〉였다."]], [["However much the ballet might have claimed his attention, Franz was too deeply occupied with the beautiful Greek to take any note of it; while she seemed to experience an almost childlike delight in watching it, her eager, animated looks contrasting strongly with the utter indifference of her companion, who, during the whole time the piece lasted, never even moved, not even when the furious, crashing din produced by the trumpets, cymbals, and Chinese bells sounded their loudest from the orchestra.", "발레가 아무리 그의 주의를 끌 만했다 한들, 프란츠는 그 아름다운 그리스 여인에게 너무도 깊이 빠져 있어 발레에는 도통 눈길도 주지 않았다. 한편 그녀는 발레를 지켜보며 거의 어린아이 같은 기쁨을 맛보는 듯했고, 그 열렬하고 생기 넘치는 표정은 그녀의 동행이 보이는 그 완전한 무관심과 강렬하게 대비되었다."], ["Of this he took no heed, but was, as far as appearances might be trusted, enjoying soft repose and bright celestial dreams.", "동행은 작품이 이어지는 내내 꼼짝도 하지 않았으니, 관현악단에서 가장 사납고 우레 같은 소리가 터져 나올 때조차 그러했다."], ["The ballet at length came to a close, and the curtain fell amid the loud, unanimous plaudits of an enthusiastic and delighted audience.", "그는 그런 것에는 아랑곳하지 않고, 겉모습을 믿을 수 있다면, 그저 포근한 안식과 환한 천상의 꿈을 누리고 있는 듯했다."]], [["Owing to the very judicious plan of dividing the two acts of the opera with a ballet, the pauses between the performances are very short, the singers in the opera having time to repose themselves and change their costume, when necessary, while the dancers are executing their pirouettes and exhibiting their graceful steps.", "마침내 발레가 끝나고, 열광하며 황홀해하는 관객들의 우레 같은 만장일치의 갈채 속에 막이 내렸다. 오페라의 두 막 사이를 발레로 가르는 그 매우 현명한 방식 덕분에, 공연 사이의 휴식은 매우 짧다. 무용수들이 회전과 우아한 발놀림을 펼치는 동안 오페라의 가수들은 쉬면서 필요하면 의상을 갈아입을 시간을 갖는 것이다."]], [["The overture to the second act began; and, at the first sound of the leader’s bow across his violin, Franz observed the sleeper slowly arise and approach the Greek girl, who turned around to say a few words to him, and then, leaning forward again on the railing of her box, she became as absorbed as before in what was going on.", "2막의 서곡이 시작되었다. 그리고 지휘자의 활이 바이올린 위를 처음 긋는 소리가 나자, 프란츠는 그 잠자던 사내가 천천히 일어나 그리스 여인에게 다가가는 것을 보았다. 여인은 몸을 돌려 그에게 몇 마디 건네고는, 다시 특별석 난간 위로 몸을 기울여 무대에서 벌어지는 일에 아까처럼 빠져들었다."]], [["The countenance of the person who had addressed her remained so completely in the shade, that, though Franz tried his utmost, he could not distinguish a single feature.", "그녀에게 말을 건 그 사람의 얼굴은 너무도 완전히 그늘에 가려 있어, 프란츠가 아무리 애써도 단 하나의 이목구비조차 분간할 수 없었다."], ["The curtain rose, and the attention of Franz was attracted by the actors; and his eyes turned from the box containing the Greek girl and her strange companion to watch the business of the stage.", "막이 올랐고, 프란츠의 주의는 배우들에게 끌렸다. 그의 눈은 그리스 여인과 그 기이한 동행이 있는 특별석에서 벗어나 무대 위의 일을 지켜보았다."]], [["Most of my readers are aware that the second act of _Parisina_ opens with the celebrated and effective duet in which Parisina, while sleeping, betrays to Azzo the secret of her love for Ugo.", "내 독자들 대부분은 〈파리지나〉의 2막이 그 유명하고 인상적인 이중창으로 막을 연다는 것을 알 것이다. 파리지나가 잠결에 우고에 대한 자신의 사랑을 아초에게 무심코 누설하는 장면이다."], ["The injured husband goes through all the emotions of jealousy, until conviction seizes on his mind, and then, in a frenzy of rage and indignation, he awakens his guilty wife to tell her that he knows her guilt and to threaten her with his vengeance.", "모욕당한 남편은 질투의 온갖 감정을 거치다가 마침내 확신에 사로잡히고, 이윽고 분노와 격분에 미쳐 날뛰며 죄지은 아내를 깨워, 그녀의 죄를 안다고 말하며 복수하겠다고 위협한다."]], [["This duet is one of the most beautiful, expressive and terrible conceptions that has ever emanated from the fruitful pen of Donizetti.", "이 이중창은 도니체티의 그 풍부한 펜 끝에서 흘러나온 가장 아름답고 표현력 넘치며 무시무시한 착상 가운데 하나다."], ["Franz now listened to it for the third time; yet its notes, so tenderly expressive and fearfully grand as the wretched husband and wife give vent to their different griefs and passions, thrilled through the soul of Franz with an effect equal to his first emotions upon hearing it.", "프란츠는 이제 그것을 세 번째로 듣는 것이었다. 그런데도 가련한 남편과 아내가 저마다의 슬픔과 격정을 토해 내는 그 음표들, 그토록 애틋하게 표현력 넘치고 무섭도록 장엄한 그 음표들은, 처음 들었을 때의 감동에 못지않은 효과로 프란츠의 영혼을 전율케 했다."], ["Excited beyond his usual calm demeanor, Franz rose with the audience, and was about to join the loud, enthusiastic applause that followed; but suddenly his purpose was arrested, his hands fell by his sides, and the half-uttered “bravos”", "평소의 침착함을 넘어 흥분한 프란츠는 관객들과 함께 일어서서, 뒤따른 그 우레 같고 열광적인 갈채에 막 합세하려던 참이었다. 그러나 별안간 그의 뜻은 가로막혔고, 두 손은 옆구리로 툭 떨어졌으며, 반쯤 내뱉던 \"브라보\""], ["expired on his lips.", "소리가 입술 위에서 사그라졌다."]], [["The occupant of the box in which the Greek girl sat appeared to share the universal admiration that prevailed; for he left his seat to stand up in front, so that, his countenance being fully revealed, Franz had no difficulty in recognizing him as the mysterious inhabitant of Monte Cristo, and the very same person he had encountered the preceding evening in the ruins of the Colosseum, and whose voice and figure had seemed so familiar to him.", "그리스 여인이 앉아 있던 특별석의 그 사내가 그곳에 감도는 만인의 감탄에 함께하는 듯했다. 그가 자리에서 일어나 앞쪽에 섰던 것이다. 그리하여 그의 얼굴이 온전히 드러나자, 프란츠는 그를 몬테 크리스토의 수수께끼 같은 거주자로, 그리고 바로 전날 저녁 콜로세움의 폐허에서 마주쳤던, 그 목소리와 형체가 그토록 낯익게 느껴졌던 바로 그 인물로 어렵잖게 알아보았다."]], [["All doubt of his identity was now at an end; his singular host evidently resided at Rome.", "이제 그의 정체에 대한 모든 의심은 끝이 났다. 그 기이한 집주인은 분명 로마에 살고 있었다."], ["The surprise and agitation occasioned by this full confirmation of Franz’s former suspicion had no doubt imparted a corresponding expression to his features; for the countess, after gazing with a puzzled look at his face, burst into a fit of laughter, and begged to know what had happened.", "프란츠의 앞선 의심이 이렇게 온전히 확인되면서 일어난 놀라움과 동요가 틀림없이 그의 얼굴에도 그에 상응하는 표정을 새겨 놓았던 모양이다. 백작 부인이 어리둥절한 눈길로 그의 얼굴을 한참 들여다보더니 웃음을 터뜨리며 무슨 일이냐고 알려 달라고 청했으니 말이다."]], [["“Countess,”", "\"백작 부인,\" 프란츠가 그녀의 놀림에는 아랑곳없이 대꾸했다."], ["returned Franz, totally unheeding her raillery, “I asked you a short time since if you knew any particulars respecting the Albanian lady opposite; I must now beseech you to inform me who and what is her husband?”", "\"조금 전에 제가 부인께 저 맞은편의 알바니아 여인에 대해 무언가 아시는 게 있느냐고 여쭈었지요. 이제는 부인께 간청드려야겠습니다. 그녀의 남편이 누구이며 어떤 사람인지 좀 알려 주십시오.\""]], [["“Nay,”", "\"이런,\""], ["answered the countess, “I know no more of him than yourself.”", "백작 부인이 대답했다. \"그 사람에 대해서는 저도 당신만큼이나 아는 게 없답니다.\""]], [["“Perhaps you never before noticed him?”", "\"전에 한 번도 그를 눈여겨보신 적이 없는 모양이군요?\""]], [["“What a question—so truly French!", "\"그런 질문을 하다니—참으로 프랑스 사람답네요!"], ["Do you not know that we Italians have eyes only for the man we love?”", "우리 이탈리아 여자들은 오직 사랑하는 남자만 눈에 들어온다는 걸 모르세요?\""]], [["“True,” replied Franz.", "\"맞는 말씀입니다.\" 프란츠가 대답했다."]], [["“All I can say is,”", "\"제가 말씀드릴 수 있는 거라곤,\""], ["continued the countess, taking up the _lorgnette_, and directing it toward the box in question, “that the gentleman, whose history I am unable to furnish, seems to me as though he had just been dug up; he looks more like a corpse permitted by some friendly grave-digger to quit his tomb for a while, and revisit this earth of ours, than anything human.", "백작 부인이 오페라글라스를 집어 들어 문제의 특별석 쪽으로 겨누며 말을 이었다. \"내력을 알려 드릴 수 없는 저 신사가, 제 눈에는 마치 방금 무덤에서 파헤쳐진 사람 같다는 것뿐이에요. 저이는 인간이라기보다는, 어느 마음씨 좋은 무덤지기가 잠시 무덤을 떠나 우리 이 땅을 다시 찾도록 허락해 준 시신 같아 보이는걸요."], ["How ghastly pale he is!”", "저 끔찍하도록 창백한 얼굴 좀 봐요!\""]], [["“Oh, he is always as colorless as you now see him,”", "\"오, 그는 늘 지금 보시는 것처럼 핏기가 없습니다.\""], ["said Franz.", "프란츠가 말했다."]], [["“Then you know him?”", "\"그럼 그를 아세요?\""], ["almost screamed the countess.", "백작 부인이 거의 비명을 지르다시피 했다."], ["“Oh, pray do, for heaven’s sake, tell us all about—is he a vampire, or a resuscitated corpse, or what?”", "\"오, 제발, 부디 저희에게 그에 대해 모두 말씀해 주세요—그는 흡혈귀인가요, 되살아난 시신인가요, 아니면 대체 무엇인가요?\""]], [["“I fancy I have seen him before; and I even think he recognizes me.”", "\"전에 그를 본 적이 있는 것 같습니다. 그리고 그가 저를 알아보는 것 같기까지 하군요.\""]], [["“And I can well understand,”", "\"그리고 충분히 이해가 가요,\""], ["said the countess, shrugging up her beautiful shoulders, as though an involuntary shudder passed through her veins, “that those who have once seen that man will never be likely to forget him.”", "백작 부인이 그 아름다운 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다. 마치 저도 모르게 핏줄을 타고 전율이 스쳐 가기라도 한 듯이. \"저 남자를 한번 본 사람이라면 결코 잊을 수 없으리라는 게 말이에요.\""]], [["The sensation experienced by Franz was evidently not peculiar to himself; another, and wholly uninterested person, felt the same unaccountable awe and misgiving.", "프란츠가 느낀 그 감각은 분명 그만의 것이 아니었다. 그와는 전혀 무관한 다른 한 사람도, 똑같이 까닭 모를 외경과 불안을 느낀 것이다."]], [["“Well.”", "\"그래,\" 프란츠가 물었다."], ["inquired Franz, after the countess had a second time directed her _lorgnette_ at the box, “what do you think of our opposite neighbor?” 20147m", "백작 부인이 또 한 번 오페라글라스를 그 특별석으로 겨눈 뒤였다. \"우리 맞은편 이웃을 어떻게 생각하십니까?\""]], [["“Why, that he is no other than Lord Ruthven himself in a living form.”", "\"글쎄요, 살아 있는 모습을 한 러스벤 경 바로 그 사람이 아닐까 싶네요.\""]], [["This fresh allusion to Byron5 drew a smile to Franz’s countenance; although he could but allow that if anything was likely to induce belief in the existence of vampires, it would be the presence of such a man as the mysterious personage before him.", "바이런을 다시 한번 빗댄 이 말에 프란츠의 얼굴에는 미소가 떠올랐다. 그래도 그는, 만약 흡혈귀의 존재를 믿게 만들 만한 것이 있다면, 그것은 바로 눈앞의 저 수수께끼 같은 인물 같은 사내의 존재이리라는 것만은 인정하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“I must positively find out who and what he is,”", "\"기어이 저자가 누구이며 어떤 사람인지 알아내야겠습니다.\""], ["said Franz, rising from his seat.", "프란츠가 자리에서 일어서며 말했다."]], [["“No, no,” cried the countess; “you must not leave me.", "\"안 돼요, 안 돼요.\" 백작 부인이 외쳤다."], ["I depend upon you to escort me home.", "\"절 떠나시면 안 돼요. 집까지 바래다주실 걸 믿고 있는걸요."], ["Oh, indeed, I cannot permit you to go.”", "오, 정말이지, 가시도록 둘 수 없어요.\""]], [["“Is it possible,”", "\"설마,\""], ["whispered Franz, “that you entertain any fear?”", "프란츠가 속삭였다. \"두려움이라도 품고 계신 겁니까?\""]], [["“I’ll tell you,”", "\"말씀드리지요.\""], ["answered the countess.", "백작 부인이 대답했다."], ["“Byron had the most perfect belief in the existence of vampires, and even assured me that he had seen them.", "\"바이런은 흡혈귀의 존재를 더없이 완벽하게 믿었어요. 심지어 자기가 그것들을 본 적이 있다고 제게 장담하기까지 했지요."], ["The description he gave me perfectly corresponds with the features and character of the man before us.", "그가 제게 들려준 묘사가 눈앞의 저 남자의 이목구비며 성격과 완벽하게 들어맞아요."], ["Oh, he is the exact personification of what I have been led to expect!", "오, 저이는 제가 들어 온 그 모습을 그대로 본떠 놓은 것 같아요!"], ["The coal-black hair, large bright, glittering eyes, in which a wild, unearthly fire seems burning,—the same ghastly paleness.", "새카만 머리카락, 크고 환하게 번득이는 두 눈, 그 속에서는 사납고 이 세상 것 아닌 불길이 타오르는 것 같아요—그 똑같이 끔찍한 창백함이라니."], ["Then observe, too, that the woman with him is altogether unlike all others of her sex. She is a foreigner—a stranger.", "그리고 또 보세요, 저이와 함께 있는 여자도 다른 여느 여인과는 전혀 달라요. 저 여자는 이방인이에요—낯선 사람이지요."], ["Nobody knows who she is, or where she comes from.", "저 여자가 누구인지, 어디서 왔는지 아무도 몰라요."], ["No doubt she belongs to the same horrible race he does, and is, like himself, a dealer in magical arts.", "틀림없이 저이와 똑같은 그 끔찍한 족속에 속한 자일 테고, 저이처럼 마법을 다루는 자일 거예요."], ["I entreat of you not to go near him—at least tonight; and if tomorrow your curiosity still continues as great, pursue your researches if you will; but tonight you neither can nor shall.", "부디 저자 가까이 가지 말아 주세요—적어도 오늘 밤은요. 만약 내일도 당신의 호기심이 여전히 그토록 크다면, 좋으실 대로 탐문해 보세요. 하지만 오늘 밤은 안 되고, 그래서도 안 됩니다."], ["For that purpose I mean to keep you all to myself.”", "그러려고 저는 당신을 온전히 제 곁에 붙잡아 둘 작정이에요.\""]], [["Franz protested he could not defer his pursuit till the following day, for many reasons.", "프란츠는 여러 가지 이유로 그 추적을 이튿날까지 미룰 수 없노라고 항변했다."]], [["“Listen to me,”", "\"제 말을 들어 보세요.\""], ["said the countess, “and do not be so very headstrong.", "백작 부인이 말했다. \"그렇게 고집부리지 마시고요."], ["I am going home.", "저는 집에 갑니다."], ["I have a party at my house tonight, and therefore cannot possibly remain till the end of the opera.", "오늘 밤 저희 집에서 모임이 있어서, 도저히 오페라 끝까지 남아 있을 수가 없어요."], ["Now, I cannot for one instant believe you so devoid of gallantry as to refuse a lady your escort when she even condescends to ask you for it.”", "그런데 한 부인이 이렇게 몸소 청하는데도 호위를 거절할 만큼 당신이 신사도가 없는 사람이라고는 한순간도 믿을 수 없는걸요.\""]], [["There was nothing else left for Franz to do but to take up his hat, open the door of the box, and offer the countess his arm.", "프란츠로서는 모자를 집어 들고 특별석 문을 열어 백작 부인에게 팔을 내미는 것 말고는 달리 할 일이 없었다."], ["It was quite evident, by her manner, that her uneasiness was not feigned; and Franz himself could not resist a feeling of superstitious dread—so much the stronger in him, as it arose from a variety of corroborative recollections, while the terror of the countess sprang from an instinctive belief, originally created in her mind by the wild tales she had listened to till she believed them truths.", "그녀의 태도로 미루어, 그 불안이 거짓이 아님은 너무도 분명했다. 그리고 프란츠 자신도 미신적인 두려움을 떨칠 수 없었다—그에게는 그 두려움이 한층 더 강렬했으니, 그것은 그것을 뒷받침하는 갖가지 기억에서 비롯된 것이었기 때문이다. 반면 백작 부인의 공포는, 본래 들어 온 무시무시한 이야기들을 끝내 진실로 믿게 되면서 그녀의 마음에 처음 생겨난 본능적인 믿음에서 솟아난 것이었다."], ["Franz could even feel her arm tremble as he assisted her into the carriage.", "프란츠는 그녀를 부축해 마차에 태우면서 그녀의 팔이 떨리는 것까지 느낄 수 있었다."], ["Upon arriving at her hotel, Franz perceived that she had deceived him when she spoke of expecting company; on the contrary, her own return before the appointed hour seemed greatly to astonish the servants.", "그녀의 호텔에 다다랐을 때, 프란츠는 그녀가 손님을 기다린다고 한 말이 자기를 속인 것임을 알아차렸다. 오히려 정해진 시각보다 일찍 돌아온 그녀의 모습에 하인들이 몹시 놀라는 듯했으니 말이다."]], [["“Excuse my little subterfuge,”", "\"제 자그마한 핑계를 용서하세요.\""], ["said the countess, in reply to her companion’s half-reproachful observation on the subject; “but that horrid man had made me feel quite uncomfortable, and I longed to be alone, that I might compose my startled mind.”", "백작 부인이 그 일에 대한 동행의 반쯤 나무라는 듯한 지적에 답하여 말했다. \"하지만 그 끔찍한 남자가 저를 어찌나 불편하게 만들던지, 혼자가 되어 놀란 마음을 가라앉히고 싶었어요.\""]], [["Franz essayed to smile.", "프란츠는 미소 지어 보이려 했다."]], [["“Nay,”", "\"이런,\""], ["said she, “do not smile; it ill accords with the expression of your countenance, and I am sure it does not spring from your heart.", "그녀가 말했다. \"웃지 마세요. 그건 당신 얼굴 표정과도 어울리지 않고, 또 분명 당신 마음에서 우러난 것도 아니잖아요."], ["However, promise me one thing.”", "그래도 한 가지만 약속해 주세요.\""]], [["“What is it?”", "\"무엇을요?\""]], [["“Promise me, I say.”", "\"약속해 달라니까요.\""]], [["“I will do anything you desire, except relinquish my determination of finding out who this man is.", "\"이 남자가 누구인지 알아내겠다는 제 결심을 버리는 일만 아니라면, 무엇이든 원하시는 대로 하지요."], ["I have more reasons than you can imagine for desiring to know who he is, from whence he came, and whither he is going.”", "저는 그가 누구인지, 어디서 왔는지, 어디로 가는지 알고 싶은, 부인께서 상상하실 수 있는 것보다 더 많은 이유가 있습니다.\""]], [["“Where he comes from I am ignorant; but I can readily tell you where he is going to, and that is down below, without the least doubt.”", "\"그가 어디서 왔는지는 저도 모르지만, 어디로 가는지는 기꺼이 말씀드릴 수 있어요. 두말할 것 없이 저 아래, 지옥이지요.\""]], [["“Let us only speak of the promise you wished me to make,”", "\"부인께서 제게 청하시려던 그 약속에 대해서만 이야기합시다.\""], ["said Franz.", "프란츠가 말했다."]], [["“Well, then, you must give me your word to return immediately to your hotel, and make no attempt to follow this man tonight.", "\"좋아요, 그럼 곧장 호텔로 돌아가서, 오늘 밤은 이 남자를 뒤쫓으려는 어떤 시도도 하지 않겠다고 약속해 주세요."], ["There are certain affinities between the persons we quit and those we meet afterwards.", "우리가 헤어진 사람들과 그 뒤에 만나는 사람들 사이에는 어떤 끌림 같은 게 있는 법이에요."], ["For heaven’s sake, do not serve as a conductor between that man and me.", "제발, 저 남자와 저 사이의 매개자 노릇은 하지 말아 주세요."], ["Pursue your chase after him tomorrow as eagerly as you please; but never bring him near me, if you would not see me die of terror.", "내일은 마음껏 그를 뒤쫓으세요. 하지만 저를 두려움에 떨다 죽는 꼴로 보고 싶지 않으시거든, 결코 그를 제 가까이로 데려오지는 마세요."], ["And now, good-night; go to your rooms, and try to sleep away all recollections of this evening.", "자, 그럼 안녕히 주무세요. 방으로 가서, 오늘 밤의 기억일랑 모두 잠으로 잊어 버리세요."], ["For my own part, I am quite sure I shall not be able to close my eyes.”", "저로 말하자면, 도무지 눈을 붙일 수 없으리란 걸 잘 알고 있지만요.\""]], [["So saying, the countess quitted Franz, leaving him unable to decide whether she were merely amusing herself at his expense, or whether her fears and agitations were genuine.", "이 말을 하고서 백작 부인은 프란츠를 떠났고, 그는 그녀가 그저 자기를 가지고 놀고 있는 것인지, 아니면 그 두려움과 동요가 진짜인지 도무지 가늠할 수 없었다."]], [["Upon his return to the hotel, Franz found Albert in his dressing-gown and slippers, listlessly extended on a sofa, smoking a cigar.", "호텔로 돌아온 프란츠는 알베르가 실내복에 슬리퍼 차림으로 소파에 늘어진 채 시가를 피우고 있는 것을 발견했다."]], [["“My dear fellow!”", "\"이 사람아!\""], ["cried he, springing up, “is it really you?", "그가 벌떡 일어나며 외쳤다. \"정말 자넨가?"], ["Why, I did not expect to see you before tomorrow.”", "아니, 내일이나 되어야 볼 줄 알았는데.\""]], [["“My dear Albert,”", "\"이보게 알베르,\""], ["replied Franz, “I am glad of this opportunity to tell you, once and forever, that you entertain a most erroneous notion concerning Italian women.", "프란츠가 대답했다. \"이참에 자네에게 단단히, 그리고 영영 일러 두게 되어 기쁘군. 자네는 이탈리아 여인들에 대해 더없이 그릇된 관념을 품고 있어."], ["I should have thought the continual failures you have met with in all your own love affairs might have taught you better by this time.”", "자네가 그동안 자네 연애마다 줄곧 겪어 온 그 실패들이, 이쯤 되면 자네에게 더 나은 걸 가르쳐 줬을 법도 한데 말이지.\""]], [["“Upon my soul, these women would puzzle the very Devil to read them aright.", "\"맹세코, 이 여자들은 악마라도 제대로 읽어 내려면 쩔쩔맬 걸세."], ["Why, here—they give you their hand—they press yours in return—they keep up a whispering conversation—permit you to accompany them home.", "자, 보게나—손을 내밀어 주고—이쪽이 잡으면 마주 쥐어 주고—소곤소곤 대화를 이어 가고—집까지 바래다주게 해 주고."], ["Why, if a Parisian were to indulge in a quarter of these marks of flattering attention, her reputation would be gone forever.”", "자, 파리 여자가 이런 호의의 표시를 사분의 일이라도 누리게 했다가는, 그 평판은 영영 끝장날 걸세.\""]], [["“And the very reason why the women of this fine country, ‘where sounds the _si_,’ as Dante writes, put so little restraint on their words and actions, is because they live so much in public, and have really nothing to conceal.", "\"그리고 단테가 적었듯이 '시(sì) 소리가 울리는' 이 아름다운 나라의 여인들이 자기 말과 행동에 그토록 거리낌이 없는 바로 그 까닭은, 그들이 그만큼 사람들 앞에 드러나 살아가고, 실은 감출 게 아무것도 없기 때문이라네."], ["Besides, you must have perceived that the countess was really alarmed.”", "게다가 자네도 보았겠지, 백작 부인이 정말로 겁에 질려 있던 걸.\""]], [["“At what? At the sight of that respectable gentleman sitting opposite to us in the same box with the lovely Greek girl?", "\"무엇에 말인가? 그 아름다운 그리스 여인과 같은 특별석에 마주 앉아 있던 그 점잖은 신사를 보고서?"], ["Now, for my part, I met them in the lobby after the conclusion of the piece; and hang me, if I can guess where you took your notions of the other world from.", "그런데, 나로 말하자면, 공연이 끝난 뒤 로비에서 그들과 마주쳤네. 그리고 내 목을 걸어도 좋아, 자네가 어디서 그런 저세상 이야기 같은 생각을 끌어왔는지 도무지 모르겠군."], ["I can assure you that this hobgoblin of yours is a deuced fine-looking fellow—admirably dressed.", "단언컨대 자네의 그 도깨비란 자는 빌어먹게 잘생긴 사내인 데다, 옷차림도 감탄스러웠어."], ["Indeed, I feel quite sure, from the cut of his clothes, they are made by a first-rate Paris tailor—probably Blin or Humann.", "사실 그 옷 재단으로 보아, 일류 파리 재단사가—아마 블랭 아니면 위만이—지은 게 틀림없다고 확신하네."], ["He was rather too pale, certainly; but then, you know, paleness is always looked upon as a strong proof of aristocratic descent and distinguished breeding.”", "좀 지나치게 창백하긴 했지, 분명히. 하지만 자네도 알다시피, 창백함이란 늘 고귀한 혈통과 빼어난 가문의 강력한 증거로 여겨지는 법 아닌가.\""]], [["Franz smiled; for he well remembered that Albert particularly prided himself on the entire absence of color in his own complexion.", "프란츠는 미소 지었다. 알베르가 자기 안색에 핏기라곤 도무지 없다는 점을 유난히 자랑스러워한다는 것을 잘 기억하고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["“Well, that tends to confirm my own ideas,”", "\"그래, 그건 내 생각을 뒷받침해 주는군.\""], ["said Franz, “that the countess’s suspicions were destitute alike of sense and reason.", "프란츠가 말했다. \"백작 부인의 의심이라는 게 분별도 이치도 없는 것이라는 내 생각 말일세."], ["Did he speak in your hearing?", "그가 자네가 들을 수 있는 데서 말을 하던가?"], ["and did you catch any of his words?”", "그리고 자네가 그 말을 좀 알아들었나?\""]], [["“I did; but they were uttered in the Romaic dialect.", "\"알아들었네. 하지만 로마이크 방언으로 한 말이었어."], ["I knew that from the mixture of Greek words.", "섞여 있는 그리스어 낱말들로 그걸 알았지."], ["I don’t know whether I ever told you that when I was at college I was rather—rather strong in Greek.”", "내가 대학 시절에 그리스어를 좀—꽤 잘했다는 걸 자네에게 말한 적이 있는지 모르겠군.\""]], [["“He spoke the Romaic language, did he?”", "\"그가 로마이크어로 말했단 말이지?\""]], [["“I think so.”", "\"그런 것 같네.\""]], [["“That settles it,”", "\"그걸로 결판났군.\""], ["murmured Franz.", "프란츠가 중얼거렸다."], ["“’Tis he, past all doubt.”", "\"그자야, 의심할 여지없이.\""]], [["“What do you say?”", "\"무어라 했나?\""]], [["“Nothing, nothing.", "\"아무것도, 아무것도 아닐세."], ["But tell me, what were you thinking about when I came in?”", "그런데 말해 보게, 내가 들어왔을 때 무슨 생각을 하고 있던 건가?\""]], [["“Oh, I was arranging a little surprise for you.”", "\"오, 자네를 위해 자그마한 깜짝 선물을 마련하던 중이었지.\""]], [["“Indeed.", "\"그래?"], ["Of what nature?”", "어떤 종류의?\""]], [["“Why, you know it is quite impossible to procure a carriage.”", "\"그게, 자네도 알다시피 마차를 구하기란 도무지 불가능하잖나.\""]], [["“Certainly; and I also know that we have done all that human means afforded to endeavor to get one.”", "\"물론이지. 게다가 우리가 한 대 구해 보려고 인간으로서 할 수 있는 일은 다 했다는 것도 알고 있네.\""]], [["“Now, then, in this difficulty a bright idea has flashed across my brain.”", "\"그래, 그래서 이 난관에 부닥쳐 내 머리에 번뜩이는 묘안이 하나 떠올랐다네.\""]], [["Franz looked at Albert as though he had not much confidence in the suggestions of his imagination.", "프란츠는 그의 상상력이 내놓는 제안에 그다지 신뢰가 가지 않는다는 듯이 알베르를 바라보았다."]], [["“I tell you what, M. Franz,”", "\"이보게 프란츠 군,\""], ["cried Albert, “you deserve to be called out for such a misgiving and incredulous glance as that you were pleased to bestow on me just now.”", "알베르가 외쳤다. \"자네가 방금 내게 보내 준 그런 미심쩍고 못 믿겠다는 눈초리에는, 결투를 신청받아 마땅하네.\""]], [["“And I promise to give you the satisfaction of a gentleman if your scheme turns out as ingenious as you assert.”", "\"그럼 자네 계획이 자네 장담대로 그렇게 기발하게 드러난다면, 신사로서 자네 분풀이에 응해 주겠다고 약속하지.\""]], [["“Well, then, hearken to me.”", "\"좋아, 그럼 들어 보게.\""]], [["“I listen.”", "\"듣고 있네.\""]], [["“You agree, do you not, that obtaining a carriage is out of the question?” “I do.”", "\"자네도 동의하지, 마차를 구하는 건 어림없는 일이라는 데 말이야?\""]], [["“Neither can we procure horses?”", "\"동의하네.\" \"말도 구할 수 없고?\""]], [["“True; we have offered any sum, but have failed.”", "\"맞아. 얼마든 값을 부르겠다 했지만 실패했지.\""]], [["“Well, now, what do you say to a cart?", "\"그래, 그럼 짐수레는 어떤가?"], ["I dare say such a thing might be had.”", "그런 거라면 구할 수 있을 법한데.\""]], [["“Very possibly.”", "\"충분히 가능하지.\""]], [["“And a pair of oxen?”", "\"그리고 황소 두 마리는?\""]], [["“As easily found as the cart.”", "\"짐수레만큼이나 쉽게 구할 수 있겠지.\""]], [["“Then you see, my good fellow, with a cart and a couple of oxen our business can be managed.", "\"그럼 보게, 이 사람아, 짐수레 한 대와 황소 두 마리만 있으면 우리 일은 해결되네."], ["The cart must be tastefully ornamented; and if you and I dress ourselves as Neapolitan reapers, we may get up a striking tableau, after the manner of that splendid picture by Léopold Robert.", "짐수레는 맵시 있게 꾸미는 거야. 그리고 자네와 내가 나폴리 추수꾼 차림을 하면, 저 레오폴 로베르의 그 멋들어진 그림을 본떠 인상적인 활인화를 꾸밀 수 있지."], ["It would add greatly to the effect if the countess would join us in the costume of a peasant from Puzzoli or Sorrento.", "게다가 백작 부인이 포추올리나 소렌토 농촌 아낙 차림으로 우리와 함께해 준다면 효과가 한결 더할 걸세."], ["Our group would then be quite complete, more especially as the countess is quite beautiful enough to represent a Madonna.”", "그러면 우리 무리는 완전무결해지지. 더욱이 백작 부인은 성모 마리아를 표현하기에 충분할 만큼 아름다우니까.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래,\""], ["said Franz, “this time, M. Albert, I am bound to give you credit for having hit upon a most capital idea.”", "프란츠가 말했다. \"이번만큼은 알베르 군, 자네가 더없이 멋진 묘안을 짚어 냈다고 인정하지 않을 수 없군.\""]], [["“And quite a national one, too,”", "\"게다가 아주 자국(自國)다운 묘안이기도 하지.\""], ["replied Albert with gratified pride.", "알베르가 흡족한 자부심에 차 대답했다."], ["“A mere masque borrowed from our own festivities.", "\"우리네 축제에서 그대로 빌려 온 가장무도회일 뿐이거든."], ["Ha, ha, ye Romans!", "하하, 너희 로마인들아!"], ["you thought to make us, unhappy strangers, trot at the heels of your processions, like so many lazzaroni, because no carriages or horses are to be had in your beggarly city.", "너희는 너희 거지 같은 도시에 마차도 말도 구할 수 없다는 이유로, 우리 가엾은 이방인들을 라차로니●처럼 너희 행렬 꽁무니나 졸졸 따라다니게 만들 작정이었겠지. 하지만 너희는 우리를 모른다."], ["But you don’t know us; when we can’t have one thing we invent another.”", "한 가지를 가질 수 없으면 우리는 다른 걸 지어내는 법이야.\""]], [["“And have you communicated your triumphant idea to anybody?”", "* 라차로니: 나폴리의 빈민·부랑자를 가리키던 말.—옮긴이"]], [["“Only to our host. Upon my return home I sent for him, and I then explained to him what I wished to procure.", "\"그래서 자네의 그 의기양양한 묘안을 누구에게 알리기라도 했나?\" \"우리 주인장한테만 했네."], ["He assured me that nothing would be easier than to furnish all I desired.", "집에 돌아오자마자 그를 불러서, 내가 무엇을 구하고 싶은지 설명했지. 그가 장담하기를, 내가 바라는 걸 다 마련하는 일쯤은 더없이 쉽다더군."], ["One thing I was sorry for; when I bade him have the horns of the oxen gilded, he told me there would not be time, as it would require three days to do that; so you see we must do without this little superfluity.”", "한 가지 아쉬운 게 있었네. 내가 황소 뿔에 금박을 입히라고 했더니, 그러려면 사흘은 걸린다며 그럴 시간이 없다지 뭔가. 그러니 이 자그마한 사치는 없이 해야겠어.\""]], [["“And where is he now?” “Who?”", "\"그럼 그는 지금 어디 있나?\""]], [["“Our host.”", "\"누구 말인가?\""]], [["“Gone out in search of our equipage, by tomorrow it might be too late.”", "\"우리 주인장 말일세.\" \"우리 탈것을 구하러 나갔지. 내일이면 너무 늦을지도 모르니까.\""]], [["“Then he will be able to give us an answer tonight.”", "\"그럼 오늘 밤 우리에게 답을 줄 수 있겠군.\""]], [["“Oh, I expect him every minute.”", "\"오, 곧 돌아올 걸세.\""]], [["At this instant the door opened, and the head of Signor Pastrini appeared. “_Permesso_?”", "바로 그 순간 문이 열리고 파스트리니 씨의 머리가 나타났다. \"들어가도 되겠습니까(페르메소)?\""], ["inquired he.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“Certainly—certainly,”", "\"물론이지요, 물론이지요.\""], ["cried Franz.", "프란츠가 외쳤다."], ["“Come in, my host.”", "\"들어오세요, 주인장.\""]], [["“Now, then,”", "\"자, 그래서,\""], ["asked Albert eagerly, “have you found the desired cart and oxen?”", "알베르가 다급히 물었다. \"그 바라던 짐수레와 황소는 구했나요?\""]], [["“Better than that!” replied Signor Pastrini, with the air of a man perfectly well satisfied with himself.", "\"그보다 더 나은 것을요!\" 파스트리니 씨가 스스로에게 더없이 흡족해하는 사람의 기색으로 대답했다."]], [["“Take care, my worthy host,”", "\"조심하시오, 우리 훌륭한 주인장.\""], ["said Albert, “_better_ is a sure enemy to _well_.”", "알베르가 말했다. \"'더 나은 것'이란 '좋은 것'의 확실한 적이니까.\""]], [["“Let your excellencies only leave the matter to me,”", "\"그저 그 일은 제게 맡겨만 주십시오, 나리들.\""], ["returned Signor Pastrini in a tone indicative of unbounded self-confidence.", "파스트리니 씨가 한없는 자신감을 드러내는 어조로 대꾸했다."]], [["“But what _have_ you done?”", "\"한데 대체 무엇을 했단 말이오?\""], ["asked Franz.", "프란츠가 물었다."], ["“Speak out, there’s a worthy fellow.”", "\"어서 말해 보시오, 우리 양반.\""]], [["“Your excellencies are aware,”", "\"나리들께서도 아시다시피,\""], ["responded the landlord, swelling with importance, “that the Count of Monte Cristo is living on the same floor with yourselves!”", "주인장이 거드름을 부풀리며 대답했다. \"몬테 크리스토 백작께서 나리들과 같은 층에 묵고 계시지 않습니까!\""]], [["“I should think we did know it,”", "\"그야 우리도 잘 알지요.\""], ["exclaimed Albert, “since it is owing to that circumstance that we are packed into these small rooms, like two poor students in the back streets of Paris.”", "알베르가 외쳤다. \"바로 그 사정 때문에 우리가 파리 뒷골목의 가난한 학생 둘처럼 이 비좁은 방에 처박혀 있는 거니까요.\""]], [["“When, then, the Count of Monte Cristo, hearing of the dilemma in which you are placed, has sent to offer you seats in his carriage and two places at his windows in the Palazzo Rospoli.”", "\"그런데 말입니다, 몬테 크리스토 백작께서 나리들이 처하신 그 곤경을 전해 들으시고, 자신의 마차에 자리를 내드리고, 또 로스폴리 궁의 자기 창가에 두 자리를 내드리겠다고 사람을 보내 청하셨습니다.\""], ["The friends looked at each other with unutterable surprise.", "두 벗은 이루 말할 수 없는 놀라움으로 서로를 마주 보았다."]], [["“But do you think,”", "\"하지만 자네 생각엔,\""], ["asked Albert, “that we ought to accept such offers from a perfect stranger?”", "알베르가 물었다. \"우리가 생판 모르는 사람한테서 그런 제안을 받아들여도 되겠나?\""]], [["“What sort of person is this Count of Monte Cristo?”", "\"이 몬테 크리스토 백작이라는 사람은 어떤 인물이오?\""], ["asked Franz of his host.", "프란츠가 주인장에게 물었다."]], [["“A very great nobleman, but whether Maltese or Sicilian I cannot exactly say; but this I know, that he is noble as a Borghese and rich as a gold mine.”", "\"아주 대단한 귀족이십니다만, 몰타 사람인지 시칠리아 사람인지는 정확히 말씀드릴 수 없습니다. 다만 이것만은 압니다. 보르게세 가문 못지않게 고귀하시고, 금광만큼이나 부유하시다는 것을요.\""]], [["“It seems to me,”", "\"내 생각엔,\""], ["said Franz, speaking in an undertone to Albert, “that if this person merited the high panegyrics of our landlord, he would have conveyed his invitation through another channel, and not permitted it to be brought to us in this unceremonious way.", "프란츠가 알베르에게 나직이 말했다. \"만약 이 사람이 우리 주인장의 그 거창한 찬사를 받을 만한 인물이라면, 자기 초대를 다른 경로로 전했을 테고, 이런 격식 없는 방식으로 우리에게 전달되게 두지는 않았을 거야."], ["He would have written—or——”", "글로 써서 보냈거나—아니면——\""]], [["At this instant someone knocked at the door.", "바로 그 순간 누군가 문을 두드렸다."]], [["“Come in,”", "\"들어오시오.\""], ["said Franz.", "프란츠가 말했다."]], [["A servant, wearing a livery of considerable style and richness, appeared at the threshold, and, placing two cards in the landlord’s hands, who forthwith presented them to the two young men, he said:", "상당히 멋스럽고 화려한 제복을 입은 하인이 문지방에 나타났다. 그는 카드 두 장을 주인장의 손에 건넸고, 주인장은 그것을 곧장 두 젊은이에게 내밀었다. 하인이 말했다."]], [["“Please to deliver these, from the Count of Monte Cristo to Vicomte Albert de Morcerf and M. Franz d’Épinay.", "\"몬테 크리스토 백작께서 알베르 드 모르세르 자작님과 프란츠 데피네 님께 이것을 전하라 하셨습니다."], ["The Count of Monte Cristo,”", "몬테 크리스토 백작께서는,\""], ["continued the servant, “begs these gentlemen’s permission to wait upon them as their neighbor, and he will be honored by an intimation of what time they will please to receive him.”", "하인이 말을 이었다. \"이웃으로서 두 분을 찾아뵙고자 허락을 청하시며, 두 분께서 몇 시에 자신을 맞아 주실지 알려 주시면 영광이겠다 하셨습니다.\""]], [["“Faith, Franz,”", "\"이거 참, 프란츠,\""], ["whispered Albert, “there is not much to find fault with here.”", "알베르가 속삭였다. \"여기엔 흠잡을 게 별로 없군.\""]], [["“Tell the count,”", "\"백작께 전하시오,\""], ["replied Franz, “that we will do ourselves the pleasure of calling on him.”", "프란츠가 대답했다. \"우리가 기꺼이 그분을 찾아뵙겠노라고 말이오.\""]], [["The servant bowed and retired.", "하인이 고개를 숙이고 물러갔다."]], [["“That is what I call an elegant mode of attack,”", "\"저게 바로 내가 우아한 공략법이라 부르는 걸세.\""], ["said Albert, “You were quite correct in what you said, Signor Pastrini.", "알베르가 말했다. \"주인장, 당신 말이 아주 옳았소."], ["The Count of Monte Cristo is unquestionably a man of first-rate breeding and knowledge of the world.”", "몬테 크리스토 백작은 의심할 여지없이 일류 가문 출신에 세상 물정에 밝은 분이오.\""]], [["“Then you accept his offer?”", "\"그럼 그분의 제안을 받아들이시는 겁니까?\""], ["said the host.", "주인장이 말했다."]], [["“Of course we do,” replied Albert.", "\"물론 받아들이고말고요.\""], ["“Still, I must own I am sorry to be obliged to give up the cart and the group of reapers—it would have produced such an effect!", "알베르가 대답했다. \"다만 그 짐수레와 추수꾼 무리를 포기해야 하는 건 솔직히 아쉽군요—그게 얼마나 큰 효과를 냈을지!"], ["And were it not for the windows at the Palazzo Rospoli, by way of recompense for the loss of our beautiful scheme, I don’t know but what I should have held on by my original plan. What say you, Franz?”", "그리고 우리의 그 멋진 계획을 잃는 데 대한 보상으로 로스폴리 궁의 창문이 없었다면, 나는 어쩌면 본래 계획을 끝까지 밀고 나갔을지도 모르겠어. 자네 생각은 어떤가, 프란츠?\""]], [["“Oh, I agree with you; the windows in the Palazzo Rospoli alone decided me.”", "\"오, 나도 자네와 같은 생각일세. 로스폴리 궁의 그 창문이야말로 나를 결심하게 한 거였으니까.\""]], [["The truth was, that the mention of two places in the Palazzo Rospoli had recalled to Franz the conversation he had overheard the preceding evening in the ruins of the Colosseum between the mysterious unknown and the Transteverin, in which the stranger in the cloak had undertaken to obtain the freedom of a condemned criminal; and if this muffled-up individual proved (as Franz felt sure he would) the same as the person he had just seen in the Teatro Argentina, then he should be able to establish his identity, and also to prosecute his researches respecting him with perfect facility and freedom.", "사실인즉, 로스폴리 궁의 두 자리 이야기가, 프란츠로 하여금 바로 전날 저녁 콜로세움의 폐허에서 그 수수께끼 같은 낯선 이와 또 다른 자 사이에 오가는 것을 엿들은 그 대화를 떠올리게 했던 것이다\n망토를 두른 낯선 사내가 사형수 한 명을 풀어 주겠노라 장담하던 트라스테베레 사람이 떠올랐다. 그리고 그 얼굴을 감춘 사내가 (프란츠가 확신하듯) 방금 아르헨티나 극장에서 본 그 인물과 같은 사람이라면, 그의 정체를 밝힐 수 있을 뿐 아니라, 그에 관한 조사도 더없이 수월하고 자유롭게 이어 갈 수 있으리라."]], [["Franz passed the night in confused dreams respecting the two meetings he had already had with his mysterious tormentor, and in waking speculations as to what the morrow would produce.", "프란츠는 그 정체 모를 훼방꾼과 이미 두 번이나 마주쳤던 일을 어수선한 꿈속에서 곱씹으며 밤을 보냈고, 잠에서 깨어서는 이튿날이 무엇을 가져다줄지 이런저런 추측에 빠졌다."], ["The next day must clear up every doubt; and unless his near neighbor and would-be friend, the Count of Monte Cristo, possessed the ring of Gyges, and by its power was able to render himself invisible, it was very certain he could not escape this time.", "다음 날이면 모든 의문이 풀릴 터였다. 가까운 이웃이자 친구가 되고 싶어 하는 그 몬테 크리스토 백작이 기게스의 반지를 지녀 그 힘으로 제 몸을 보이지 않게 만들지 않는 한, 이번에는 결코 빠져나가지 못하리라는 것만은 분명했다."]], [["Eight o’clock found Franz up and dressed, while Albert, who had not the same motives for early rising, was still soundly asleep.", "여덟 시가 되자 프란츠는 자리에서 일어나 옷을 갖춰 입었다. 일찍 일어날 까닭이 없는 알베르는 여전히 곤히 잠들어 있었다."], ["The first act of Franz was to summon his landlord, who presented himself with his accustomed obsequiousness.", "프란츠가 가장 먼저 한 일은 여관 주인을 부르는 것이었고, 주인은 여느 때처럼 굽실거리며 나타났다."]], [["“Pray, Signor Pastrini,”", "\"파스트리니 씨,\""], ["asked Franz, “is not some execution appointed to take place today?”", "프란츠가 물었다. \"오늘 사형 집행이 있다던데, 맞소?\""]], [["“Yes, your excellency; but if your reason for inquiry is that you may procure a window to view it from, you are much too late.”", "\"그렇습니다, 나리. 하지만 구경할 창문을 잡으시려고 물으시는 거라면, 이미 너무 늦으셨습니다.\""]], [["“Oh, no,”", "\"아니, 그게 아니오.\""], ["answered Franz, “I had no such intention; and even if I had felt a wish to witness the spectacle, I might have done so from Monte Pincio; could I not?”", "프란츠가 대답했다. \"그럴 생각은 없었소. 설령 그 광경을 보고 싶었다 한들, 핀초 언덕에서도 볼 수 있지 않겠소?\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["exclaimed mine host, “I did not think it likely your excellency would have chosen to mingle with such a rabble as are always collected on that hill, which, indeed, they consider as exclusively belonging to themselves.”", "주인이 외쳤다. \"나리께서 그 언덕에 늘 모여드는 부랑배들 틈에 끼고 싶어 하실 줄은 몰랐습니다. 사실 그자들은 그 언덕을 제 것이라도 되는 양 여기지요.\""]], [["“Very possibly I may not go,”", "\"안 갈 수도 있소.\""], ["answered Franz; “but in case I feel disposed, give me some particulars of today’s executions.”", "프란츠가 대답했다. \"하지만 혹시 마음이 동할 수도 있으니, 오늘 집행에 대해 자세히 좀 일러 주시오.\""]], [["“What particulars would your excellency like to hear?”", "\"어떤 점을 알고 싶으십니까, 나리?\""]], [["“Why, the number of persons condemned to suffer, their names, and description of the death they are to die.”", "\"사형을 당할 사람의 수, 이름, 그리고 어떤 죽음을 맞게 되는지 말이오.\""]], [["“That happens just lucky, your excellency!", "\"마침 운이 좋으십니다, 나리!"], ["Only a few minutes ago they brought me the _tavolettas_.”", "불과 몇 분 전에 타볼레타를 가져다주었거든요.\""]], [["“What are they?”", "\"그게 뭐요?\""]], [["“Sort of wooden tablets hung up at the corners of streets the evening before an execution, on which is pasted up a paper containing the names of the condemned persons, their crimes, and mode of punishment.", "\"사형 집행 전날 저녁에 거리 모퉁이마다 걸어 두는 일종의 나무 판입니다. 거기에 사형수들의 이름과 죄목, 처형 방식이 적힌 종이를 붙여 놓지요."], ["The reason for so publicly announcing all this is, that all good and faithful Catholics may offer up their prayers for the unfortunate culprits, and, above all, beseech of Heaven to grant them a sincere repentance.”", "이렇게 공공연히 알리는 까닭은, 선량하고 독실한 가톨릭 신자들이 그 불운한 죄인들을 위해 기도를 올리고, 무엇보다 하늘에 진심 어린 회개를 내려 주십사 빌 수 있게 하려는 것입니다.\""]], [["“And these tablets are brought to you that you may add your prayers to those of the faithful, are they?”", "\"그래서 그 판을 댁한테 가져다주니, 댁도 신자들과 함께 기도를 올리라는 게요?\""], ["asked Franz somewhat incredulously.", "프란츠가 다소 미심쩍은 듯 물었다."]], [["“Oh, dear, no, your excellency!", "\"오, 천만에요, 나리! 저는 제 일과 귀하신 손님들 일 말고는 남의 일에 신경 쓸 겨를이 없습니다."], ["I have not time for anybody’s affairs but my own and those of my honorable guests; but I make an agreement with the man who pastes up the papers, and he brings them to me as he would the playbills, that in case any person staying at my hotel should like to witness an execution, he may obtain every requisite information concerning the time and place etc.”", "다만 종이를 붙이는 사람과 약조를 해 두었지요. 그자가 연극 전단처럼 그것을 제게 가져다주는 겁니다. 혹시 제 여관에 묵는 손님 중에 사형 집행을 보고 싶어 하는 분이 계시면, 시간이며 장소 같은 데 필요한 정보를 빠짐없이 알려 드릴 수 있도록 말입니다.\""]], [["“Upon my word, that is a most delicate attention on your part, Signor Pastrini,” cried Franz.", "\"이거 참, 자네답게 세심한 배려로군, 파스트리니 씨.\" 프란츠가 외쳤다."]], [["“Why, your excellency,”", "\"아이고, 나리,\""], ["returned the landlord, chuckling and rubbing his hands with infinite complacency, “I think I may take upon myself to say I neglect nothing to deserve the support and patronage of the noble visitors to this poor hotel.”", "주인이 더없이 흡족한 듯 낄낄대며 두 손을 비볐다. \"이 보잘것없는 여관을 찾아 주시는 귀하신 손님들의 후원과 보살핌을 받을 자격을 얻으려고, 소홀히 하는 일이 없다고 감히 말씀드릴 수 있습니다.\""]], [["“I see that plainly enough, my most excellent host, and you may rely upon me to proclaim so striking a proof of your attention to your guests wherever I go.", "\"정말 잘 알겠소, 더없이 훌륭한 주인장. 손님을 향한 이토록 놀라운 정성의 증거를 내가 어디를 가든 널리 알리겠다고 약속하리다."], ["Meanwhile, oblige me by a sight of one of these _tavolettas_.”", "그런데 우선 그 타볼레타라는 것을 한번 보여 주시오.\""]], [["“Nothing can be easier than to comply with your excellency’s wish,”", "\"나리의 분부를 따르는 것보다 쉬운 일은 없지요.\""], ["said the landlord, opening the door of the chamber; “I have caused one to be placed on the landing, close by your apartment.”", "주인이 방문을 열며 말했다. \"마침 한 장을 나리 방 바로 옆 층계참에 걸어 두었습니다.\""]], [["Then, taking the tablet from the wall, he handed it to Franz, who read as follows:", "그러고는 벽에서 그 판을 떼어 프란츠에게 건넸다. 프란츠는 다음과 같이 읽었다."]], [["“‘The public is informed that on Wednesday, February 23rd, being the first day of the Carnival, executions will take place in the Piazza del Popolo, by order of the Tribunal of the Rota, of two persons, named Andrea Rondolo, and Peppino, otherwise called Rocca Priori; the former found guilty of the murder of a venerable and exemplary priest, named Don César Torlini, canon of the church of St.", "\"'2월 23일 수요일, 카니발 첫날에 로타 법정의 명에 따라 포폴로 광장에서 두 사람에 대한 사형이 집행됨을 공지하는 바이다. 그 이름은 안드레아 론돌로와, 일명 로카 프리오리라 불리는 페피노이다. 전자는 산조반니인라테라노 성당의 참사회원이며 덕망 높고 모범적인 사제 돈 체사레 토를리니를 살해한 죄로, 후자는 잔혹하고 피에 굶주린 산적 루이지 밤파와 그 무리의 공범인 죄로 유죄를 선고받았다."], ["John Lateran; and the latter convicted of being an accomplice of the atrocious and sanguinary bandit, Luigi Vampa, and his band. The first-named malefactor will be _mazzolato_, the second culprit _decapitato_.", "첫 번째 죄인은 마촐라토 형에, 두 번째 죄인은 데카피타토 형에 처해질 것이다."]], [["“‘The prayers of all good Christians are entreated for these unfortunate men, that it may please God to awaken them to a sense of their guilt, and to grant them a hearty and sincere repentance for their crimes.’”", "\"'모든 선량한 그리스도교 신자들은 이 불운한 자들을 위해 기도하기를 간청하노니, 하느님께서 부디 그들이 제 죄를 깨닫게 하시고, 그 죄에 대해 진심 어린 참회를 내려 주시기를 바라노라.'\""]], [["This was precisely what Franz had heard the evening before in the ruins of the Colosseum.", "이것은 프란츠가 전날 저녁 콜로세움 폐허에서 들었던 바로 그 내용이었다."], ["No part of the programme differed,—the names of the condemned persons, their crimes, and mode of punishment, all agreed with his previous information.", "어느 한 대목도 다르지 않았다. 사형수들의 이름, 그들의 죄목, 처형 방식, 모든 것이 그가 미리 들었던 정보와 일치했다."], ["In all probability, therefore, the Transteverin was no other than the bandit Luigi Vampa himself, and the man shrouded in the mantle the same he had known as “Sinbad the Sailor,”", "그렇다면 십중팔구 그 트라스테베레 사람은 다름 아닌 산적 루이지 밤파 본인이며, 망토를 두른 사내는 그가 \"뱃사람 신드바드\"로 알고 있던 바로 그 인물일 터였다."], ["but who, no doubt, was still pursuing his philanthropic expedition in Rome, as he had already done at Porto-Vecchio and Tunis.", "그는 포르토베키오와 튀니스에서 이미 그러했듯, 로마에서도 여전히 자선 행각을 벌이고 있는 것이 틀림없었다."]], [["Time was getting on, however, and Franz deemed it advisable to awaken Albert; but at the moment he prepared to proceed to his chamber, his friend entered the room in perfect costume for the day.", "그러나 시간이 흘러가고 있었으므로, 프란츠는 알베르를 깨우는 편이 좋겠다고 여겼다. 그런데 막 친구의 방으로 가려는 순간, 알베르가 그날에 어울리는 복장을 완벽하게 갖춰 입고 방으로 들어섰다."], ["The anticipated delights of the Carnival had so run in his head as to make him leave his pillow long before his usual hour.", "카니발의 즐거움에 대한 기대가 머릿속을 어찌나 가득 채웠던지, 그는 평소보다 훨씬 일찍 잠자리를 박차고 나온 것이었다."]], [["“Now, my excellent Signor Pastrini,”", "\"자, 더없이 훌륭한 파스트리니 씨,\""], ["said Franz, addressing his landlord, “since we are both ready, do you think we may proceed at once to visit the Count of Monte Cristo?”", "프란츠가 주인에게 말했다. \"우리 둘 다 채비가 되었으니, 지금 곧장 몬테 크리스토 백작을 찾아뵈어도 괜찮겠소?\""]], [["“Most assuredly,”", "\"여부가 있겠습니까.\""], ["replied he.", "주인이 대답했다."], ["“The Count of Monte Cristo is always an early riser; and I can answer for his having been up these two hours.”", "\"몬테 크리스토 백작님은 늘 일찍 일어나시지요. 벌써 두 시간 전부터 일어나 계신 것은 제가 보증합니다.\""]], [["“Then you really consider we shall not be intruding if we pay our respects to him directly?” 20155m", "\"그렇다면 지금 바로 인사를 드리러 가도 폐가 되지 않으리라 정말 생각하시오?\""]], [["“Oh, I am quite sure.", "\"오, 확실합니다."], ["I will take all the blame on myself if you find I have led you into an error.”", "만약 제가 나리를 잘못 이끈 것이라면, 그 책임은 모두 제가 지겠습니다.\""]], [["“Well, then, if it be so, are you ready, Albert?”", "\"좋소, 그렇다면, 알베르, 자네 준비됐나?\""]], [["“Perfectly.”", "\"완벽하네.\""]], [["“Let us go and return our best thanks for his courtesy.”", "\"가서 그분의 호의에 진심으로 감사를 드리세.\""]], [["“Yes, let us do so.”", "\"그래, 그러세.\""]], [["The landlord preceded the friends across the landing, which was all that separated them from the apartments of the count, rang at the bell, and, upon the door being opened by a servant, said:", "주인은 두 친구를 앞장서서 층계참을 가로질러 안내했다. 백작의 거처와 그들 사이를 가르는 것이라곤 그 층계참뿐이었다. 그는 초인종을 울렸고, 하인이 문을 열자 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“_I signori Francesi_.”", "\"프랑스 신사 두 분이십니다.\""]], [["The domestic bowed respectfully, and invited them to enter.", "하인이 정중하게 고개를 숙이고는 그들을 안으로 청했다."], ["They passed through two rooms, furnished in a luxurious manner they had not expected to see under the roof of Signor Pastrini, and were shown into an elegantly fitted-up drawing-room.", "두 사람은 파스트리니 씨의 지붕 아래에서 보리라고는 예상치 못한 호화로운 방 두 개를 지나, 우아하게 꾸며진 응접실로 안내되었다."], ["The richest Turkey carpets covered the floor, and the softest and most inviting couches, easy-chairs, and sofas, offered their high-piled and yielding cushions to such as desired repose or refreshment.", "더없이 값진 터키산 양탄자가 바닥을 덮고 있었고, 더없이 푹신하고 안락한 긴 의자와 안락의자, 소파가 휴식이나 다과를 원하는 이들에게 두툼하고 폭신한 방석을 내밀고 있었다."], ["Splendid paintings by the first masters were ranged against the walls, intermingled with magnificent trophies of war, while heavy curtains of costly tapestry were suspended before the different doors of the room.", "일류 거장들이 그린 빼어난 그림들이 벽을 따라 늘어서 있었고, 그 사이사이에 화려한 전리품들이 섞여 있었으며, 방의 여러 문 앞에는 값비싼 태피스트리로 만든 묵직한 휘장이 드리워져 있었다."]], [["“If your excellencies will please to be seated,”", "\"나리들께서 앉아 계시면,\" 하인이 말했다."], ["said the man, “I will let the count know that you are here.”", "\"백작님께 두 분이 와 계신다고 알려 드리겠습니다.\""]], [["And with these words he disappeared behind one of the tapestried _portières_.", "이 말과 함께 그는 태피스트리 휘장 하나 뒤로 사라졌다."], ["As the door opened, the sound of a _guzla_ reached the ears of the young men, but was almost immediately lost, for the rapid closing of the door merely allowed one rich swell of harmony to enter.", "문이 열리는 순간 구즐라 소리가 두 젊은이의 귀에 닿았으나, 곧바로 잦아들고 말았다. 문이 재빨리 닫히는 바람에 풍성한 선율 한 자락만이 흘러들었던 것이다."], ["Franz and Albert looked inquiringly at each other, then at the gorgeous furnishings of the apartment.", "프란츠와 알베르는 서로를 의아한 눈빛으로 바라보다가, 이내 방 안의 화려한 세간으로 눈길을 돌렸다."], ["Everything seemed more magnificent at a second view than it had done at their first rapid survey.", "모든 것이 처음 휙 둘러보았을 때보다 두 번째로 보니 한층 더 웅장해 보였다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래,\""], ["said Franz to his friend, “what think you of all this?”", "프란츠가 친구에게 말했다. \"이 모든 게 어떤가?\""]], [["“Why, upon my soul, my dear fellow, it strikes me that our elegant and attentive neighbor must either be some successful stock-jobber who has speculated in the fall of the Spanish funds, or some prince travelling _incog_.”", "\"이거 참, 솔직히 말하자면, 이보게, 내 보기엔 이 우아하고 세심한 우리 이웃은 에스파냐 공채 하락에 투기를 걸어 한몫 잡은 증권 거간꾼이거나, 아니면 신분을 감추고 여행하는 어느 왕족일 것 같네.\""]], [["“Hush, hush!”", "\"쉿, 쉿!\""], ["replied Franz; “we shall ascertain who and what he is—he comes!”", "프란츠가 대답했다. \"그가 누구이며 어떤 자인지 곧 알게 될 걸세. 오는군!\""]], [["As Franz spoke, he heard the sound of a door turning on its hinges, and almost immediately afterwards the tapestry was drawn aside, and the owner of all these riches stood before the two young men.", "프란츠가 말하는 사이, 문이 경첩을 따라 돌아가는 소리가 들렸고, 거의 곧바로 태피스트리가 옆으로 젖혀지며 이 모든 부의 주인이 두 젊은이 앞에 모습을 드러냈다."], ["Albert instantly rose to meet him, but Franz remained, in a manner, spellbound on his chair; for in the person of him who had just entered he recognized not only the mysterious visitant to the Colosseum, and the occupant of the box at the Teatro Argentina, but also his extraordinary host of Monte Cristo. 20157m", "알베르는 즉시 일어나 그를 맞으러 갔으나, 프란츠는 마치 홀린 듯 의자에 붙박인 채 있었다. 방금 들어선 그 사람에게서, 콜로세움의 정체 모를 방문객이자 아르헨티나 극장 특별석의 주인일 뿐 아니라, 몬테 크리스토에서 만났던 그 비범한 주인의 모습까지 알아보았기 때문이다."]], [["# Chapter 35. La Mazzolata", "# Chapter 35. La Mazzolata"]], [["Gentlemen,”", "“여러분, 이렇게 미리 찾아온 것을 부디 양해해 주십시오.”"], ["said the Count of Monte Cristo as he entered, “I pray you excuse me for suffering my visit to be anticipated; but I feared to disturb you by presenting myself earlier at your apartments; besides, you sent me word that you would come to me, and I have held myself at your disposal.”", "몬테크리스토 백작이 들어서며 말했다. “좀 더 일찍 두 분 방으로 찾아뵈었다가 폐가 될까 염려스러웠습니다. 게다가 저를 보러 오시겠다는 전갈을 주셨기에, 저는 두 분을 기다리며 채비를 하고 있었지요.”"]], [["“Franz and I have to thank you a thousand times, count,”", "“백작님, 프란츠와 저는 천 번이라도 감사드려야 할 처지입니다.”"], ["returned Albert; “you extricated us from a great dilemma, and we were on the point of inventing a very fantastic vehicle when your friendly invitation reached us.”", "알베르가 대꾸했다. “저희를 큰 곤경에서 구해 주셨으니까요. 친절한 초대장을 받았을 때, 저희는 막 기상천외한 탈것을 하나 궁리해 내려던 참이었답니다.”"]], [["“Indeed,” returned the count, motioning the two young men to sit down.", "“그러셨군요.” 백작이 두 젊은이에게 앉으라고 손짓하며 대꾸했다."], ["“It was the fault of that blockhead Pastrini, that I did not sooner assist you in your distress.", "“좀 더 일찍 곤경에 처한 두 분을 돕지 못한 것은 다 그 멍청한 파스트리니 탓입니다."], ["He did not mention a syllable of your embarrassment to me, when he knows that, alone and isolated as I am, I seek every opportunity of making the acquaintance of my neighbors.", "저는 혈혈단신 외톨이로 지내는 처지라 이웃과 사귈 기회라면 무엇이든 마다하지 않는데, 그 사람은 그 사정을 잘 알면서도 두 분이 난처해하신다는 말은 한마디도 꺼내지 않았습니다."], ["As soon as I learned I could in any way assist you, I most eagerly seized the opportunity of offering my services.”", "제가 무슨 도움이라도 드릴 수 있다는 걸 알자마자, 저는 서둘러 그 기회를 붙잡아 제 도움을 제안했던 것이지요.”"]], [["The two young men bowed.", "두 젊은이가 고개를 숙였다."], ["Franz had, as yet, found nothing to say; he had come to no determination, and as nothing in the count’s manner manifested the wish that he should recognize him, he did not know whether to make any allusion to the past, or wait until he had more proof; besides, although sure it was he who had been in the box the previous evening, he could not be equally positive that this was the man he had seen at the Colosseum.", "프란츠는 아직 무슨 말을 해야 할지 찾지 못했다. 마음을 정하지도 못했고, 백작의 태도 어디에도 자신을 알아봐 주기를 바라는 기색이 없었기에, 지난 일을 넌지시 들춰야 할지 아니면 더 확실한 증거가 잡힐 때까지 기다려야 할지 알 수 없었다. 게다가 전날 저녁 칸막이 좌석에 있던 사람이 바로 이 사람이라는 것은 확신했지만, 콜로세움에서 본 그 사내가 맞는지는 그만큼 단언할 수 없었다."], ["He resolved, therefore, to let things take their course without making any direct overture to the count.", "그래서 그는 백작에게 직접 운을 떼지 않고 일이 흘러가는 대로 두기로 마음먹었다. 더구나 그에게는 유리한 점이 하나 있었다."], ["Moreover, he had this advantage, he was master of the count’s secret, while the count had no hold on Franz, who had nothing to conceal.", "자신은 백작의 비밀을 쥐고 있는데, 정작 백작은 숨길 것이 하나도 없는 프란츠에게 어떤 약점도 잡을 수 없다는 것이었다."], ["However, he resolved to lead the conversation to a subject which might possibly clear up his doubts.", "그래도 그는 의혹을 풀어 줄 만한 화제로 대화를 이끌어 보기로 했다."]], [["“Count,”", "“백작님,”"], ["said he, “you have offered us places in your carriage, and at your windows in the Rospoli Palace.", "그가 말했다. “저희에게 마차의 자리와 로스폴리 궁전 창가의 자리를 내주신다고 하셨지요."], ["Can you tell us where we can obtain a sight of the Piazza del Popolo?”", "그런데 포폴로 광장은 어디서 보면 좋을지 알려 주실 수 있겠습니까?”"]], [["“Ah,”", "“아,”"], ["said the count negligently, looking attentively at Morcerf, “is there not something like an execution upon the Piazza del Popolo?”", "백작이 무심한 듯 모르세르를 유심히 바라보며 말했다. “포폴로 광장에서 무슨 처형 같은 게 있지 않습니까?”"]], [["“Yes,”", "“네.”"], ["returned Franz, finding that the count was coming to the point he wished.", "프란츠는 백작이 자신이 바라던 화제로 들어서는 것을 보며 대답했다."]], [["“Stay, I think I told my steward yesterday to attend to this; perhaps I can render you this slight service also.”", "“가만, 어제 집사에게 그 일을 챙기라고 일러 둔 것 같군요. 어쩌면 그 사소한 일도 도와드릴 수 있겠습니다.”"]], [["He extended his hand, and rang the bell thrice.", "그는 손을 뻗어 종을 세 번 울렸다."]], [["“Did you ever occupy yourself,” said he to Franz, “with the employment of time and the means of simplifying the summoning your servants?", "“시간을 효율적으로 쓰고 하인을 부르는 일을 간단하게 만드는 방법을 궁리해 보신 적이 있습니까?” 그가 프란츠에게 말했다."], ["I have.", "“저는 해 봤지요."], ["When I ring once, it is for my valet; twice, for my majordomo; thrice, for my steward,—thus I do not waste a minute or a word.", "한 번 울리면 시종, 두 번 울리면 집사장, 세 번 울리면 집사를 부르는 겁니다. 이렇게 하면 단 일 분도, 단 한마디도 허비하지 않지요."], ["Here he is.”", "자, 왔군요.”"]], [["A man of about forty-five or fifty entered, exactly resembling the smuggler who had introduced Franz into the cavern; but he did not appear to recognize him.", "마흔다섯이나 쉰쯤 되어 보이는 사내가 들어왔다. 프란츠를 동굴로 안내했던 그 밀수꾼과 똑같이 닮은 사람이었다. 그러나 그는 프란츠를 알아보는 기색이 없었다."], ["It was evident he had his orders.", "미리 지시를 받았음이 분명했다."]], [["“Monsieur Bertuccio,”", "“베르투치오,”"], ["said the count, “you have procured me windows looking on the Piazza del Popolo, as I ordered you yesterday.”", "백작이 말했다. “어제 일러 둔 대로 포폴로 광장이 내려다보이는 창을 마련해 두었나?”"]], [["“Yes, excellency,”", "“네, 각하.”"], ["returned the steward; “but it was very late.”", "집사가 대답했다. “하지만 너무 늦은 시각이었습니다.”"]], [["“Did I not tell you I wished for one?”", "“하나 필요하다고 일러 두지 않았나?”"], ["replied the count, frowning.", "백작이 미간을 찌푸리며 대꾸했다."]], [["“And your excellency has one, which was let to Prince Lobanieff; but I was obliged to pay a hundred——”", "“그래서 각하께서는 창을 하나 얻으셨습니다. 로바니에프 공께 빌려 드렸던 자리인데, 백 닢이나 치러야 했고······.”"]], [["“That will do—that will do, Monsieur Bertuccio; spare these gentlemen all such domestic arrangements.", "“됐네, 됐어, 베르투치오. 그런 집안 사정으로 손님들을 번거롭게 하지 말게."], ["You have the window, that is sufficient.", "창이 있다니 그걸로 충분해."], ["Give orders to the coachman; and be in readiness on the stairs to conduct us to it.”", "마부에게 지시를 내리고, 계단에서 채비하고 있다가 우리를 안내하게.”"]], [["The steward bowed, and was about to quit the room.", "집사가 고개를 숙이고 방을 나서려 했다."]], [["“Ah!”", "“아!”"], ["continued the count, “be good enough to ask Pastrini if he has received the _tavoletta_, and if he can send us an account of the execution.”", "백작이 말을 이었다. “파스트리니에게 타볼레타를 받았는지, 처형 안내문을 우리에게 보내 줄 수 있는지 물어봐 주게.”"]], [["“There is no need to do that,”", "“그러실 필요 없습니다.”"], ["said Franz, taking out his tablets; “for I saw the account, and copied it down.”", "프란츠가 수첩을 꺼내며 말했다. “제가 그 안내문을 보고 옮겨 적어 두었거든요.”"]], [["“Very well, you can retire, M. Bertuccio; I need you no longer.", "“좋습니다, 그럼 물러가도 되네, 베르투치오. 더는 자네가 필요 없으니."], ["Let us know when breakfast is ready.", "아침 식사 준비가 되면 알려 주게."], ["These gentlemen,”", "두 분께서는,”"], ["added he, turning to the two friends, “will, I trust, do me the honor to breakfast with me?”", "그가 두 친구에게로 돌아서며 덧붙였다. “저와 함께 아침을 드는 영광을 베풀어 주시겠지요?”"]], [["“But, my dear count,”", "“하지만 백작님,”"], ["said Albert, “we shall abuse your kindness.”", "알베르가 말했다. “그러면 호의에 너무 기대는 셈이 됩니다.”"]], [["“Not at all; on the contrary, you will give me great pleasure.", "“천만에요. 오히려 제게는 큰 기쁨입니다."], ["You will, one or other of you, perhaps both, return it to me at Paris. M.", "두 분 중 한 분이, 어쩌면 두 분 모두, 파리에서 제게 답례해 주시면 되니까요."], ["Bertuccio, lay covers for three.”", "베르투치오, 세 사람 분으로 상을 차리게.”"]], [["He then took Franz’s tablets out of his hand.", "그러고 나서 그는 프란츠의 손에서 수첩을 받아 들었다. “‘알린다.’”"], ["“‘We announce,’ he read, in the same tone with which he would have read a newspaper, ‘that today, the 23rd of February, will be executed Andrea Rondolo, guilty of murder on the person of the respected and venerated Don César Torlini, canon of the church of St.", "그는 마치 신문이라도 읽듯이 같은 어조로 읽어 내려갔다. “‘오늘 2월 23일, 안드레아 론돌로의 처형이 집행된다. 그는 성 요한 라테란 성당의 참사회원이며 존경받고 추앙받던 돈 체사르 토를리니를 살해한 죄를 지었다."], ["John Lateran, and Peppino, called Rocca Priori, convicted of complicity with the detestable bandit Luigi Vampa, and the men of his band.’", "또한 로카 프리오리라 불리는 페피노는 흉악한 산적 루이지 밤파 및 그 일당과 공모한 죄로 유죄가 확정되었다.’"]], [["“Hum!", "흠!"], ["‘The first will be _mazzolato_, the second _decapitato_.’ Yes,”", "‘첫 번째 죄인은 마촐라토 형에, 두 번째 죄인은 데카피타토 형에 처해진다.’ 그래요,”"], ["continued the count, “it was at first arranged in this way; but I think since yesterday some change has taken place in the order of the ceremony.”", "백작이 말을 이었다. “처음에는 이렇게 정해져 있었지요. 하지만 어제부터 의식의 순서에 무슨 변동이 생긴 듯합니다.”"]], [["“Really?”", "“정말입니까?”"], ["said Franz.", "프란츠가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, I passed the evening at the Cardinal Rospigliosi’s, and there mention was made of something like a pardon for one of the two men.”", "“그렇습니다. 어제저녁 로스필리오시 추기경 댁에서 시간을 보냈는데, 거기서 두 사람 중 하나에게 사면 같은 게 내려졌다는 이야기가 나왔거든요.”"]], [["“For Andrea Rondolo?”", "“안드레아 론돌로에게 말입니까?”"], ["asked Franz.", "프란츠가 물었다."]], [["“No,”", "“아니요.”"], ["replied the count, carelessly; “for the other (he glanced at the tablets as if to recall the name), for Peppino, called Rocca Priori.", "백작이 무심히 대답했다. “다른 자에게요.(그는 이름을 떠올리려는 듯 수첩을 흘끗 보았다.) 로카 프리오리라 불리는 페피노에게 말입니다. 그러니 두 분은 단두대에서 목이 떨어지는 사내를 보지는 못하게 되었군요."], ["You are thus deprived of seeing a man guillotined; but the _mazzolata_ still remains, which is a very curious punishment when seen for the first time, and even the second, while the other, as you must know, is very simple.", "하지만 마촐라타 형은 여전히 남아 있습니다. 그건 처음 볼 때, 아니 두 번째로 볼 때까지도 아주 진기한 형벌이지요. 반면 다른 쪽은, 두 분도 아시겠지만 너무나 단순합니다."], ["The _mandaïa_6 never fails, never trembles, never strikes thirty times ineffectually, like the soldier who beheaded the Count of Chalais, and to whose tender mercy Richelieu had doubtless recommended the sufferer. Ah,”", "만다이아는 절대 빗나가지 않고, 떨리지도 않으며, 샬레 백작의 목을 친 그 병사처럼 서른 번을 헛쳐 대는 일도 없습니다. 그 사형수는 리슐리외가 틀림없이 그 병사의 자비로운 솜씨에 맡겨 버렸겠지요. 아,” 백작이 경멸하는 어조로 덧붙였다."], ["added the count, in a contemptuous tone, “do not tell me of European punishments, they are in the infancy, or rather the old age, of cruelty.”", "“유럽의 형벌 이야기는 제게 꺼내지 마십시오. 그건 잔혹함의 유아기, 아니 차라리 노년기에 머물러 있으니까요.” * 만다이아: 로마에서 쓰던 단두대의 일종으로, 칼날이 초승달 모양이다. —옮긴이"]], [["“Really, count,”", "“정말이지 백작님은,”"], ["replied Franz, “one would think that you had studied the different tortures of all the nations of the world.”", "프란츠가 대답했다. “세상 온갖 나라의 갖가지 고문을 연구하기라도 하신 것 같습니다.”"]], [["“There are, at least, few that I have not seen,”", "“적어도 못 본 것이 거의 없기는 합니다.”"], ["said the count coldly.", "백작이 차갑게 말했다."]], [["“And you took pleasure in beholding these dreadful spectacles?”", "“그럼 그런 끔찍한 광경을 보며 즐거워하셨다는 말씀입니까?”"]], [["“My first sentiment was horror, the second indifference, the third curiosity.”", "“처음엔 공포였고, 두 번째엔 무관심이었고, 세 번째엔 호기심이었지요.”"]], [["“Curiosity—that is a terrible word.”", "“호기심이라니, 그건 무서운 말이군요.”"]], [["“Why so?", "“어째서요?"], ["In life, our greatest preoccupation is death; is it not then, curious to study the different ways by which the soul and body can part; and how, according to their different characters, temperaments, and even the different customs of their countries, different persons bear the transition from life to death, from existence to annihilation?", "삶에서 우리의 가장 큰 관심사는 죽음입니다. 그러니 영혼과 육체가 갈라서는 갖가지 방식을 연구하는 것, 또 저마다의 성격과 기질에 따라, 심지어 나라마다 다른 풍습에 따라, 사람들이 삶에서 죽음으로, 존재에서 소멸로 건너가는 그 순간을 어떻게 견뎌 내는지 살펴보는 것이 흥미롭지 않겠습니까? 저로 말하자면, 한 가지는 장담할 수 있습니다."], ["As for myself, I can assure you of one thing,—the more men you see die, the easier it becomes to die yourself; and in my opinion, death may be a torture, but it is not an expiation.”", "사람이 죽는 것을 많이 볼수록, 제 자신이 죽는 일도 한결 쉬워진다는 것입니다. 그리고 제 생각으로는, 죽음이 고문일 수는 있어도 속죄일 수는 없습니다.”"]], [["“I do not quite understand you,”", "“잘 이해가 가지 않습니다.”"], ["replied Franz; “pray explain your meaning, for you excite my curiosity to the highest pitch.”", "프란츠가 대답했다. “부디 그 뜻을 설명해 주십시오. 제 호기심을 더할 나위 없이 자극하시는군요.”"]], [["“Listen,”", "“들어 보십시오.”"], ["said the count, and deep hatred mounted to his face, as the blood would to the face of any other.", "백작이 말했다. 그러자 그의 얼굴 위로 깊은 증오가 솟구쳤다. 다른 사람 같으면 얼굴에 피가 솟구쳤을 그 자리에."], ["“If a man had by unheard-of and excruciating tortures destroyed your father, your mother, your betrothed,—a being who, when torn from you, left a desolation, a wound that never closes, in your breast,—do you think the reparation that society gives you is sufficient when it interposes the knife of the guillotine between the base of the occiput and the trapezal muscles of the murderer, and allows him who has caused us years of moral sufferings to escape with a few moments of physical pain?”", "“만일 어떤 자가 듣도 보도 못한 잔혹한 고문으로 당신의 아버지를, 어머니를, 약혼녀를, 그러니까 당신에게서 떼어 내면 결코 아물지 않는 황폐함과 상처를 가슴에 남길 그런 존재를 짓밟아 버렸다고 합시다. 그런데 사회가 당신에게 해 주는 보상이라는 것이, 그 살인자의 뒤통수와 목덜미 근육 사이에 단두대의 칼날을 들이미는 것뿐이라면, 그리고 우리에게 수년간 정신적 고통을 안긴 자가 단 몇 순간의 육체적 고통으로 빠져나가게 내버려 둔다면, 당신은 그것으로 충분하다고 보십니까?”"]], [["“Yes, I know,”", "“네, 압니다.” 프란츠가 말했다."], ["said Franz, “that human justice is insufficient to console us; she can give blood in return for blood, that is all; but you must demand from her only what it is in her power to grant.”", "“인간의 정의는 우리를 위로하기에는 모자라지요. 피에는 피로 갚아 줄 뿐, 그게 전부니까요. 하지만 그 정의에는 그것이 베풀 수 있는 것만을 요구해야 하지 않겠습니까.”"]], [["“I will put another case to you,”", "“다른 경우를 하나 들어 보지요.”"], ["continued the count; “that where society, attacked by the death of a person, avenges death by death.", "백작이 말을 이었다. “어떤 사람이 죽임을 당해 사회가 침해받았을 때, 사회는 죽음을 죽음으로 갚습니다."], ["But are there not a thousand tortures by which a man may be made to suffer without society taking the least cognizance of them, or offering him even the insufficient means of vengeance, of which we have just spoken?", "하지만 사회가 전혀 인지하지도 못한 채, 방금 우리가 말한 그 불충분한 복수의 수단조차 주지 않은 채, 한 사람이 겪을 수 있는 고문이 수천 가지나 있지 않습니까?"], ["Are there not crimes for which the impalement of the Turks, the augers of the Persians, the stake and the brand of the Iroquois Indians, are inadequate tortures, and which are unpunished by society?", "터키의 말뚝형도, 페르시아의 송곳도, 이로쿼이 인디언의 화형주와 인두도 모자란 그런 죄, 그러면서도 사회는 벌하지 못하는 그런 죄가 있지 않습니까? 대답해 보십시오."], ["Answer me, do not these crimes exist?”", "그런 죄가 존재하지 않습니까?”"]], [["“Yes,”", "“네.”"], ["answered Franz; “and it is to punish them that duelling is tolerated.”", "프란츠가 대답했다. “바로 그런 죄를 벌하려고 결투가 용인되는 것이지요.”"]], [["“Ah, duelling,”", "“아, 결투라!”"], ["cried the count; “a pleasant manner, upon my soul, of arriving at your end when that end is vengeance!", "백작이 외쳤다. “맹세코, 목적이 복수일 때 그 목적에 다다르는 참으로 유쾌한 방법이지요!"], ["A man has carried off your mistress, a man has seduced your wife, a man has dishonored your daughter; he has rendered the whole life of one who had the right to expect from Heaven that portion of happiness God has promised to everyone of his creatures, an existence of misery and infamy; and you think you are avenged because you send a ball through the head, or pass a sword through the breast, of that man who has planted madness in your brain, and despair in your heart.", "어떤 자가 당신의 정부를 빼앗고, 어떤 자가 당신의 아내를 유혹하고, 어떤 자가 당신의 딸을 욕보였다고 합시다. 신께서 모든 피조물에게 약속하신 그 몫의 행복을 하늘에서 받으리라 기대할 권리가 있던 한 사람의 평생을, 그자는 비참과 치욕의 삶으로 만들어 버렸습니다. 그런데 당신의 머릿속에 광기를, 가슴속에 절망을 심어 놓은 그 사내의 머리통에 총알을 박거나 가슴에 칼을 찔러 넣었다고 해서, 당신은 복수를 했다고 여깁니까?"], ["And remember, moreover, that it is often he who comes off victorious from the strife, absolved of all crime in the eyes of the world.", "게다가 그 싸움에서 이겨 세상의 눈에 모든 죄를 사면받고 나오는 쪽이 오히려 그자인 경우도 많다는 것을 기억하십시오. 아닙니다, 아니에요.”"], ["No, no,” continued the count, “had I to avenge myself, it is not thus I would take revenge.”", "백작이 말을 이었다. “제가 복수를 해야 한다면, 저는 그런 식으로 하지는 않을 겁니다.”"]], [["“Then you disapprove of duelling?", "“그럼 결투에는 찬성하지 않으신다는 겁니까?"], ["You would not fight a duel?” asked Albert in his turn, astonished at this strange theory.", "백작님은 결투를 하지 않으시겠다는 말씀이십니까?” 이번에는 알베르가 그 기이한 논리에 놀라 물었다."]], [["“Oh, yes,” replied the count; “understand me, I would fight a duel for a trifle, for an insult, for a blow; and the more so that, thanks to my skill in all bodily exercises, and the indifference to danger I have gradually acquired, I should be almost certain to kill my man. Oh, I would fight for such a cause; but in return for a slow, profound, eternal torture, I would give back the same, were it possible; an eye for an eye, a tooth for a tooth, as the Orientalists say,—our masters in everything,—those favored creatures who have formed for themselves a life of dreams and a paradise of realities.”", "“오, 천만에요.” 백작이 대답했다. “제 말을 오해하지 마십시오. 저는 사소한 일로도, 모욕 한 번으로도, 한 대 맞은 일로도 결투를 할 겁니다. 더구나 온갖 신체 단련에 능한 솜씨와 위험 앞에서 차츰 길러 온 무심함 덕분에, 저는 거의 틀림없이 상대를 죽일 수 있을 테니까요. 오, 그런 일로는 싸우겠습니다. 하지만 더디고, 깊고, 영원한 고문에 대해서라면, 가능한 한 똑같이 되갚아 줄 겁니다. 동방인들이 말하듯, 눈에는 눈, 이에는 이로 말이지요. 그들은 모든 면에서 우리의 스승입니다. 스스로 꿈의 삶과 현실의 낙원을 만들어 낸 그 축복받은 사람들이지요.”"]], [["“But,”", "“하지만 백작님,”"], ["said Franz to the count, “with this theory, which renders you at once judge and executioner of your own cause, it would be difficult to adopt a course that would forever prevent your falling under the power of the law.", "프란츠가 백작에게 말했다. “자신을 자기 사건의 재판관이자 동시에 처형인으로 만드는 그 논리대로라면, 영영 법의 손아귀에 떨어지지 않을 길을 택하기란 어려울 겁니다. 증오는 눈이 멀게 하고, 분노는 사람을 휩쓸어 가지요."], ["Hatred is blind, rage carries you away; and he who pours out vengeance runs the risk of tasting a bitter draught.”", "복수를 쏟아붓는 자는 쓰디쓴 잔을 들이켜게 될 위험을 무릅쓰는 셈입니다.”"]], [["“Yes, if he be poor and inexperienced, not if he be rich and skilful; besides, the worst that could happen to him would be the punishment of which we have already spoken, and which the philanthropic French Revolution has substituted for being torn to pieces by horses or broken on the wheel. What matters this punishment, as long as he is avenged? On my word, I almost regret that in all probability this miserable Peppino will not be beheaded, as you might have had an opportunity then of seeing how short a time the punishment lasts, and whether it is worth even mentioning; but, really this is a most singular conversation for the Carnival, gentlemen; how did it arise? Ah, I recollect, you asked for a place at my window; you shall have it; but let us first sit down to table, for here comes the servant to inform us that breakfast is ready.”", "“가난하고 미숙한 자라면 그렇겠지요. 하지만 부유하고 노련한 자라면 그렇지 않습니다. 게다가 그에게 닥칠 최악의 일이라야 우리가 이미 이야기한 그 형벌, 박애를 내세운 프랑스 혁명이 사지를 말에 묶어 찢거나 수레바퀴에 매달아 부수던 형벌 대신 들여놓은 그 형벌일 뿐입니다. 복수만 이룬다면 그깟 형벌이 무슨 대수겠습니까? 솔직히 말해, 십중팔구 저 가엾은 페피노의 목이 떨어지지 않게 되어 거의 아쉬울 지경입니다. 그랬다면 두 분께서도 그 형벌이 얼마나 짧게 끝나는지, 입에 올릴 가치나 있는지 직접 보실 기회가 있었을 텐데 말이지요. 그건 그렇고, 사육제에 어울리지 않는 참 묘한 대화로군요, 여러분. 어쩌다 이런 이야기가 나왔을까요? 아, 생각났습니다. 두 분께서 제 창가의 자리를 청하셨지요. 자리는 드리겠습니다. 하지만 우선 식탁에 앉읍시다. 마침 아침 식사가 준비됐다고 알리러 하인이 오는군요.”"]], [["As he spoke, a servant opened one of the four doors of the apartment, saying:", "그가 말하는 사이, 하인이 방에 난 네 개의 문 가운데 하나를 열며 말했다."]], [["“_Al suo commodo!_”", "“알 수오 코모도!”"]], [["The two young men arose and entered the breakfast-room.", "두 젊은이가 일어나 식당으로 들어갔다."]], [["During the meal, which was excellent, and admirably served, Franz looked repeatedly at Albert, in order to observe the impressions which he doubted not had been made on him by the words of their entertainer; but whether with his usual carelessness he had paid but little attention to him, whether the explanation of the Count of Monte Cristo with regard to duelling had satisfied him, or whether the events which Franz knew of had had their effect on him alone, he remarked that his companion did not pay the least regard to them, but on the contrary ate like a man who for the last four or five months had been condemned to partake of Italian cookery—that is, the worst in the world.", "훌륭하고 흠잡을 데 없이 차려진 식사 동안, 프란츠는 거듭 알베르를 바라보며 주인의 말이 그에게 어떤 인상을 남겼는지 살피려 했다. 그런 인상을 남기지 않았을 리 없다고 여겼던 것이다. 그러나 알베르가 늘 그렇듯 무심해서 별로 귀담아듣지 않았는지, 결투에 관한 몬테크리스토 백작의 해명에 만족했는지, 아니면 프란츠만이 알고 있는 그 사건들이 프란츠에게만 영향을 미쳤는지는 알 수 없었으나, 어쨌든 그가 보기에 동행은 그 말들에 조금도 마음을 두지 않았다. 오히려 지난 너덧 달 동안 이탈리아 요리를, 그러니까 세상에서 가장 형편없는 요리를 먹으라는 선고라도 받았던 사람처럼 게걸스레 먹어 댔다."]], [["As for the count, he just touched the dishes; he seemed to fulfil the duties of a host by sitting down with his guests, and awaited their departure to be served with some strange or more delicate food.", "백작으로 말하자면, 음식에 거의 손을 대지 않았다. 손님들과 함께 식탁에 앉아 주인의 도리를 다하는 것처럼 보였고, 손님들이 떠난 뒤에야 무언가 색다르거나 더 정교한 음식을 들려는 듯했다."], ["This brought back to Franz, in spite of himself, the recollection of the terror with which the count had inspired the Countess G——, and her firm conviction that the man in the opposite box was a vampire.", "이 광경은 프란츠의 의지와 상관없이, 백작이 G백작 부인에게 불러일으켰던 그 공포, 그리고 맞은편 좌석의 사내가 흡혈귀라던 부인의 확고한 믿음을 다시 떠올리게 했다."]], [["At the end of the breakfast Franz took out his watch.", "식사가 끝나 갈 무렵 프란츠가 시계를 꺼냈다."]], [["“Well,”", "“이런,”"], ["said the count, “what are you doing?”", "백작이 말했다. “뭘 하시는 겁니까?”"]], [["“You must excuse us, count,”", "“양해해 주십시오, 백작님.”"], ["returned Franz, “but we have still much to do.”", "프란츠가 대꾸했다. “하지만 저희는 아직 할 일이 많아서요.”"]], [["“What may that be?”", "“그게 무엇입니까?”"]], [["“We have no masks, and it is absolutely necessary to procure them.”", "“가면이 없습니다. 그것을 반드시 마련해야 하거든요.”"]], [["“Do not concern yourself about that; we have, I think, a private room in the Piazza del Popolo; I will have whatever costumes you choose brought to us, and you can dress there.”", "“그 일이라면 염려 마십시오. 제 생각에 포폴로 광장에 저희가 쓸 별실이 하나 있습니다. 두 분이 고르시는 의상은 무엇이든 그리로 가져다 두게 할 테니, 거기서 갈아입으시면 됩니다.”"]], [["“After the execution?”", "“처형이 끝난 뒤에 말입니까?”"], ["cried Franz.", "프란츠가 외쳤다."]], [["“Before or after, whichever you please.”", "“그 전이든 후든, 원하시는 대로요.”"]], [["“Opposite the scaffold?”", "“처형대를 마주 보고서요?”"]], [["“The scaffold forms part of the _fête_.”", "“처형대도 축제의 한 부분이니까요.”"]], [["“Count, I have reflected on the matter,”", "“백작님, 곰곰이 생각해 보았습니다.”"], ["said Franz, “I thank you for your courtesy, but I shall content myself with accepting a place in your carriage and at your window at the Rospoli Palace, and I leave you at liberty to dispose of my place at the Piazza del Popolo.”", "프란츠가 말했다. “호의는 감사하지만, 저는 마차의 자리와 로스폴리 궁전 창가의 자리만 받아들이는 것으로 만족하겠습니다. 포폴로 광장의 제 자리는 백작님께서 마음대로 처분하셔도 좋습니다.”"]], [["“But I warn you, you will lose a very curious sight,”", "“하지만 미리 말씀드리는데, 아주 진기한 구경거리를 놓치시는 겁니다.”"], ["returned the count.", "백작이 대꾸했다."]], [["“You will describe it to me,”", "“그건 백작님께서 제게 들려주시면 됩니다.”"], ["replied Franz, “and the recital from your lips will make as great an impression on me as if I had witnessed it.", "프란츠가 대답했다. “백작님 입에서 나온 이야기라면, 제가 직접 본 것이나 다름없이 큰 인상을 남길 테니까요."], ["I have more than once intended witnessing an execution, but I have never been able to make up my mind; and you, Albert?”", "저는 한두 번 처형을 직접 볼까 마음먹은 적이 있지만, 끝내 결심이 서지 않았습니다. 자네는 어떤가, 알베르?”"]], [["“I,”", "“나는,” 자작이 대답했다."], ["replied the viscount,—“I saw Castaing executed, but I think I was rather intoxicated that day, for I had quitted college the same morning, and we had passed the previous night at a tavern.”", "“나는 카스탱이 처형되는 걸 봤지. 하지만 그날은 좀 취해 있었던 것 같아. 그날 아침에 막 학교를 나왔고, 전날 밤을 선술집에서 보냈거든.”"]], [["“Besides, it is no reason because you have not seen an execution at Paris, that you should not see one anywhere else; when you travel, it is to see everything.", "“게다가 파리에서 처형을 보지 못했다고 해서 다른 데서도 보지 말란 법은 없지요. 여행이란 모든 것을 보러 다니는 것이니까요."], ["Think what a figure you will make when you are asked, ‘How do they execute at Rome?’ and you reply, ‘I do not know!’ And, besides, they say that the culprit is an infamous scoundrel, who killed with a log of wood a worthy canon who had brought him up like his own son.", "누가 ‘로마에서는 어떻게 처형하던가?’ 하고 물었을 때 ‘모르겠습니다!’ 하고 대답한다면, 그 꼴이 어떻겠습니까. 게다가 듣자 하니 그 죄인은 자기를 친자식처럼 길러 준 훌륭한 참사회원을 장작개비로 때려죽인 파렴치한 악당이랍니다. 빌어먹을!"], ["_Diable!_ when a churchman is killed, it should be with a different weapon than a log, especially when he has behaved like a father.", "성직자를 죽일 거라면 적어도 장작개비가 아닌 다른 무기를 써야지요. 더구나 그자가 아버지처럼 대해 준 분이었다면 말입니다."], ["If you went to Spain, would you not see the bull-fights?", "스페인에 가면 투우를 보지 않겠습니까?"], ["Well, suppose it is a bull-fight you are going to see?", "자, 이것도 투우를 보러 간다고 치십시오."], ["Recollect the ancient Romans of the Circus, and the sports where they killed three hundred lions and a hundred men.", "옛 로마 원형 경기장의 로마인들을, 사자 삼백 마리와 사람 백 명을 죽이던 그 구경거리를 떠올려 보십시오."], ["Think of the eighty thousand applauding spectators, the sage matrons who took their daughters, and the charming Vestals who made with the thumb of their white hands the fatal sign that said, ‘Come, despatch the dying.’”", "환호하던 팔만 명의 관중을, 딸들을 데리고 오던 점잖은 부인들을, 그리고 그 하얀 손의 엄지로 ‘자, 죽어 가는 자를 끝장내라.’라는 치명적인 신호를 보내던 매혹적인 베스타 무녀들을 떠올려 보십시오.”"]], [["“Shall you go, then, Albert?”", "“그럼 자네도 가겠나, 알베르?”"], ["asked Franz.", "프란츠가 물었다."]], [["“_Ma foi_, yes; like you, I hesitated, but the count’s eloquence decides me.”", "“맙소사, 가야지. 나도 자네처럼 망설였지만, 백작님의 능변에 마음을 정했네.”"]], [["“Let us go, then,”", "“그럼 가세.”"], ["said Franz, “since you wish it; but on our way to the Piazza del Popolo, I wish to pass through the Corso.", "프란츠가 말했다. “자네가 원하니 말이야. 하지만 포폴로 광장으로 가는 길에 코르소 거리를 지나고 싶군요."], ["Is this possible, count?”", "그럴 수 있겠습니까, 백작님?”"]], [["“On foot, yes, in a carriage, no.”", "“걸어서라면 됩니다만, 마차로는 안 됩니다.”"]], [["“I will go on foot, then.”", "“그럼 걸어서 가겠습니다.”"]], [["“Is it important that you should go that way?”", "“꼭 그쪽 길로 가셔야 합니까?”"]], [["“Yes, there is something I wish to see.”", "“네, 보고 싶은 게 있어서요.”"]], [["“Well, we will go by the Corso.", "“좋습니다, 그럼 코르소를 거쳐 가지요."], ["We will send the carriage to wait for us on the Piazza del Popolo, by the Via del Babuino, for I shall be glad to pass, myself, through the Corso, to see if some orders I have given have been executed.”", "마차는 바부이노 거리를 통해 포폴로 광장에서 기다리도록 보내 두겠습니다. 저도 제가 내려 둔 몇 가지 지시가 제대로 이행됐는지 보러 코르소를 직접 지나가면 좋겠으니까요.”"]], [["“Excellency,”", "“각하,”"], ["said a servant, opening the door, “a man in the dress of a penitent wishes to speak to you.”", "하인이 문을 열며 말했다. “고행자 옷차림을 한 사내가 각하를 뵙고자 합니다.”"]], [["“Ah! yes,”", "“아! 그래.”"], ["returned the count, “I know who he is, gentlemen; will you return to the salon?", "백작이 대꾸했다. “누군지 알겠군. 여러분, 응접실로 돌아가 계시겠습니까?"], ["you will find good cigars on the centre table.", "가운데 탁자에 좋은 시가가 있을 겁니다."], ["I will be with you directly.”", "곧 가지요.”"]], [["The young men rose and returned into the salon, while the count, again apologizing, left by another door.", "두 젊은이가 일어나 응접실로 돌아갔고, 백작은 다시 한번 양해를 구하고는 다른 문으로 나갔다."], ["Albert, who was a great smoker, and who had considered it no small sacrifice to be deprived of the cigars of the Café de Paris, approached the table, and uttered a cry of joy at perceiving some veritable _puros_.", "대단한 애연가였던 알베르는 파리 카페의 시가를 못 피우는 것을 적잖은 희생으로 여기던 터라, 탁자로 다가갔다가 진짜 푸로스 시가를 발견하고는 기쁨의 탄성을 질렀다."]], [["“Well,”", "“그래,”"], ["asked Franz, “what think you of the Count of Monte Cristo?”", "프란츠가 물었다. “몬테크리스토 백작을 어떻게 생각하나?”"]], [["“What do I think?”", "“어떻게 생각하느냐고?”"], ["said Albert, evidently surprised at such a question from his companion; “I think he is a delightful fellow, who does the honors of his table admirably; who has travelled much, read much, is, like Brutus, of the Stoic school, and moreover,”", "알베르는 동행이 그런 질문을 하는 것에 적잖이 놀란 기색이었다. “더할 나위 없이 유쾌한 사람이라고 생각하네. 식탁의 주인 노릇을 훌륭히 해내고, 여행도 많이 했고, 책도 많이 읽었고, 브루투스처럼 스토아학파에 속한 사람이지. 게다가,”"], ["added he, sending a volume of smoke up towards the ceiling, “that he has excellent cigars.”", "그가 천장을 향해 연기를 한 모금 내뿜으며 덧붙였다. “최고급 시가를 갖고 있고 말일세.”"]], [["Such was Albert’s opinion of the count, and as Franz well knew that Albert professed never to form an opinion except upon long reflection, he made no attempt to change it.", "이것이 백작에 대한 알베르의 견해였다. 알베르가 오랜 숙고 끝에야 비로소 견해를 내놓는다고 공언하는 사람임을 잘 아는 프란츠는, 그 생각을 굳이 바꾸려 들지 않았다."]], [["“But,”", "“그런데,”"], ["said he, “did you observe one very singular thing?”", "그가 말했다. “아주 묘한 점 하나를 눈치챘나?”"]], [["“What?”", "“뭘 말인가?”"]], [["“How attentively he looked at you.”", "“백작이 얼마나 자네를 유심히 보던지.”"]], [["“At me?”", "“나를?”"]], [["“Yes.”", "“그래.”"]], [["Albert reflected. “Ah,”", "알베르가 곰곰이 생각했다. “아,”"], ["replied he, sighing, “that is not very surprising; I have been more than a year absent from Paris, and my clothes are of a most antiquated cut; the count takes me for a provincial.", "그가 한숨을 쉬며 대답했다. “그건 그리 놀랄 일이 아니야. 내가 파리를 떠나온 지 일 년이 넘었으니, 옷차림이 한물간 구식이지 않은가."], ["The first opportunity you have, undeceive him, I beg, and tell him I am nothing of the kind.”", "백작은 나를 시골뜨기로 본 거야. 기회가 닿거든 부디 그 오해를 풀어 주게. 나는 결코 그런 사람이 아니라고 말일세.”"]], [["Franz smiled; an instant after the count entered.", "프란츠가 미소 지었다. 잠시 후 백작이 들어왔다."]], [["“I am now quite at your service, gentlemen,”", "“이제 두 분 분부대로 따르겠습니다, 여러분.”"], ["said he. “The carriage is going one way to the Piazza del Popolo, and we will go another; and, if you please, by the Corso.", "그가 말했다. “마차는 한쪽 길로 포폴로 광장으로 가고, 우리는 다른 길로, 괜찮으시다면 코르소를 거쳐 가지요."], ["Take some more of these cigars, M. de Morcerf.”", "시가를 좀 더 챙기십시오, 모르세르 씨.”"]], [["“With all my heart,”", "“기꺼이 그러지요.”"], ["returned Albert; “Italian cigars are horrible.", "알베르가 대꾸했다. “이탈리아 시가는 끔찍하거든요."], ["When you come to Paris, I will return all this.”", "파리에 오시면 이 모든 것을 갚아 드리겠습니다.”"]], [["“I will not refuse; I intend going there soon, and since you allow me, I will pay you a visit.", "“사양하지 않겠습니다. 곧 그리로 갈 작정이니, 허락해 주신다면 댁으로 찾아뵙지요."], ["Come, we have not any time to lose, it is half-past twelve—let us set off.”", "자, 지체할 시간이 없습니다. 벌써 열두 시 반이군요. 출발합시다.”"]], [["All three descended; the coachman received his master’s orders, and drove down the Via del Babuino.", "세 사람이 모두 아래로 내려갔다. 마부는 주인의 지시를 받고 바부이노 거리로 마차를 몰았다."], ["While the three gentlemen walked along the Piazza di Spagna and the Via Frattina, which led directly between the Fiano and Rospoli palaces, Franz’s attention was directed towards the windows of that last palace, for he had not forgotten the signal agreed upon between the man in the mantle and the Transtevere peasant.", "세 신사가 스페인 광장과 프라티나 거리를 걷는 동안, 그 길은 피아노 궁전과 로스폴리 궁전 사이로 곧장 이어졌는데, 프란츠의 시선은 그 마지막 궁전의 창들로 향했다. 망토를 두른 사내와 트라스테베레의 농부 사이에 정해 두었던 그 신호를 잊지 않고 있었던 것이다."]], [["“Which are your windows?” asked he of the count, with as much indifference as he could assume.", "“백작님 창은 어느 것입니까?” 그가 애써 무심한 척하며 백작에게 물었다."]], [["“The three last,”", "“마지막 세 개입니다.”"], ["returned he, with a negligence evidently unaffected, for he could not imagine with what intention the question was put.", "백작이 대답했다. 무심한 태도는 전혀 꾸민 것이 아니었다. 그 질문에 어떤 속셈이 있는지 짐작조차 할 수 없었으니까."]], [["Franz glanced rapidly towards the three windows.", "프란츠는 재빨리 그 세 창으로 눈길을 던졌다."], ["The side windows were hung with yellow damask, and the centre one with white damask and a red cross.", "양옆 창에는 노란 다마스크 천이 드리워져 있었고, 가운데 창에는 흰 다마스크 천에 붉은 십자가가 새겨져 있었다."], ["The man in the mantle had kept his promise to the Transteverin, and there could now be no doubt that he was the count.", "망토를 두른 사내는 트라스테베레의 농부에게 한 약속을 지켰던 것이다. 이제 그가 백작이라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없었다."]], [["The three windows were still untenanted.", "세 창은 여전히 비어 있었다."], ["Preparations were making on every side; chairs were placed, scaffolds were raised, and windows were hung with flags.", "사방에서 준비가 한창이었다. 의자가 놓이고, 관람대가 세워지고, 창마다 깃발이 드리워졌다."], ["The masks could not appear; the carriages could not move about; but the masks were visible behind the windows, the carriages, and the doors.", "가면을 쓴 사람들은 아직 나설 수 없었고, 마차도 돌아다닐 수 없었다. 그러나 창과 마차와 문 뒤로는 가면을 쓴 사람들이 보였다."]], [["Franz, Albert, and the count continued to descend the Corso.", "프란츠와 알베르, 그리고 백작은 코르소를 따라 계속 내려갔다. 포폴로 광장에 가까워질수록 군중은 더욱 빽빽해졌고, 무리의 머리 위로 두 개의 물체가 보였다."], ["As they approached the Piazza del Popolo, the crowd became more dense, and above the heads of the multitude two objects were visible: the obelisk, surmounted by a cross, which marks the centre of the square, and in front of the obelisk, at the point where the three streets, del Babuino, del Corso, and di Ripetta, meet, the two uprights of the scaffold, between which glittered the curved knife of the _mandaïa_.", "하나는 광장 한복판을 표시하는, 꼭대기에 십자가가 얹힌 오벨리스크였다. 또 하나는 그 오벨리스크 앞, 바부이노와 코르소와 리페타, 세 거리가 만나는 지점에 선 처형대의 두 기둥이었다. 그 사이로 만다이아의 굽은 칼날이 번득였다."]], [["At the corner of the street they met the count’s steward, who was awaiting his master.", "거리 모퉁이에서 그들은 주인을 기다리던 백작의 집사를 만났다."], ["The window, let at an exorbitant price, which the count had doubtless wished to conceal from his guests, was on the second floor of the great palace, situated between the Via del Babuino and the Monte Pincio.", "백작이 분명 손님들에게 숨기고 싶어 했을, 터무니없는 값에 빌린 그 창은 바부이노 거리와 핀초 언덕 사이에 자리한 큰 궁전의 이 층에 있었다."], ["It consisted, as we have said, of a small dressing-room, opening into a bedroom, and, when the door of communication was shut, the inmates were quite alone.", "앞서 말했듯이 그곳은 침실로 통하는 작은 화장실이었고, 사이 문을 닫으면 그 안의 사람들은 완전히 단둘이 있게 되었다."], ["On chairs were laid elegant masquerade costumes of blue and white satin. 20167m", "의자 위에는 파랑과 흰빛 새틴으로 지은 우아한 가장무도회 의상이 놓여 있었다."]], [["“As you left the choice of your costumes to me,”", "“두 분께서 의상 고르는 일을 제게 맡기셨으니,” 백작이 두 친구에게 말했다."], ["said the count to the two friends, “I have had these brought, as they will be the most worn this year; and they are most suitable, on account of the _confetti_ (sweetmeats), as they do not show the flour.”", "“올해 가장 많이들 입을 이걸 가져다 두라고 했습니다. 게다가 콘페티, 그러니까 사탕 알갱이 때문에라도 이게 가장 알맞지요. 밀가루가 묻어도 표가 나지 않으니까요.”"]], [["Franz heard the words of the count but imperfectly, and he perhaps did not fully appreciate this new attention to their wishes; for he was wholly absorbed by the spectacle that the Piazza del Popolo presented, and by the terrible instrument that was in the centre.", "프란츠는 백작의 말을 어렴풋이 들었을 뿐, 자신들의 바람을 또 한 번 헤아려 준 이 배려를 어쩌면 제대로 음미하지 못했다. 그는 포폴로 광장이 펼쳐 보이는 광경과, 그 한복판에 놓인 무시무시한 기계에 온통 사로잡혀 있었다."]], [["It was the first time Franz had ever seen a guillotine,—we say guillotine, because the Roman _mandaïa_ is formed on almost the same model as the French instrument.7 The knife, which is shaped like a crescent, that cuts with the convex side, falls from a less height, and that is all the difference.", "프란츠가 단두대를 본 것은 그때가 처음이었다. 단두대라고 말하는 것은, 로마의 만다이아가 프랑스의 기계와 거의 같은 본을 따 만든 것이기 때문이다. 초승달 모양으로 굽은 칼날이 볼록한 쪽으로 베어 내린다는 점, 그리고 떨어지는 높이가 좀 더 낮다는 점, 차이라고는 그것뿐이었다."]], [["Two men, seated on the movable plank on which the victim is laid, were eating their breakfasts, while waiting for the criminal.", "희생자를 눕히는 그 움직이는 널빤지 위에 두 사내가 걸터앉아 죄인을 기다리며 아침을 먹고 있었다."], ["Their repast consisted apparently of bread and sausages.", "그들의 식사는 보아하니 빵과 소시지였다."], ["One of them lifted the plank, took out a flask of wine, drank some, and then passed it to his companion.", "그중 한 사람이 널빤지를 들어 올려 포도주 병을 꺼내더니 한 모금 마시고는 동료에게 건넸다."], ["These two men were the executioner’s assistants.", "이 두 사람은 처형인의 조수였다."]], [["At this sight Franz felt the perspiration start forth upon his brow.", "이 광경에 프란츠는 이마에 땀이 솟는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["The prisoners, transported the previous evening from the Carceri Nuove to the little church of Santa Maria del Popolo, had passed the night, each accompanied by two priests, in a chapel closed by a grating, before which were two sentinels, who were relieved at intervals.", "전날 저녁 카르체리 누오베에서 산타 마리아 델 포폴로 작은 성당으로 옮겨진 죄수들은, 저마다 사제 두 명을 거느린 채 쇠창살로 닫힌 예배당에서 밤을 보냈다. 그 앞에는 보초 두 사람이 서서 번갈아 교대하고 있었다."], ["A double line of carbineers, placed on each side of the door of the church, reached to the scaffold, and formed a circle around it, leaving a path about ten feet wide, and around the guillotine a space of nearly a hundred feet.", "성당 문 양옆으로 늘어선 두 줄의 기마총병이 처형대까지 이어져 그 둘레를 에워쌌고, 폭 십 피트가량의 길을 터놓았으며, 단두대 둘레로는 백 피트 가까운 공간을 비워 두었다."]], [["All the rest of the square was paved with heads.", "광장의 나머지는 온통 사람 머리로 메워져 있었다."], ["Many women held their infants on their shoulders, and thus the children had the best view.", "많은 여인이 어깨 위에 갓난아기를 얹고 있어서, 아이들이 가장 잘 볼 수 있었다. 핀초 언덕은 구경꾼으로 가득 찬 거대한 원형 경기장 같았다."], ["The Monte Pincio seemed a vast amphitheatre filled with spectators; the balconies of the two churches at the corner of the Via del Babuino and the Via di Ripetta were crammed; the steps even seemed a parti-colored sea, that was impelled towards the portico; every niche in the wall held its living statue.", "바부이노 거리와 리페타 거리 모퉁이에 선 두 성당의 발코니도 꽉 들어찼다. 심지어 계단마저 현관 쪽으로 밀려가는 알록달록한 바다처럼 보였다. 담벼락의 움푹한 곳마다 살아 있는 조각상을 하나씩 품고 있었다."], ["What the count said was true—the most curious spectacle in life is that of death.", "백작의 말은 사실이었다. 삶에서 가장 진기한 구경거리는 죽음의 광경이었다."]], [["And yet, instead of the silence and the solemnity demanded by the occasion, laughter and jests arose from the crowd.", "그런데도 그 자리에 마땅히 깃들어야 할 침묵과 엄숙함 대신, 군중에게서는 웃음과 농담이 터져 나왔다."], ["It was evident that the execution was, in the eyes of the people, only the commencement of the Carnival.", "사람들의 눈에 그 처형이 그저 사육제의 시작에 불과하다는 것이 분명했다."]], [["Suddenly the tumult ceased, as if by magic, and the doors of the church opened.", "별안간, 마법에라도 걸린 듯 소란이 그쳤고, 성당 문이 열렸다."], ["A brotherhood of penitents, clothed from head to foot in robes of gray sackcloth, with holes for the eyes, and holding in their hands lighted tapers, appeared first; the chief marched at the head. 20169m", "머리부터 발끝까지 잿빛 거친 삼베 옷을 두르고 눈 부분에 구멍을 뚫은, 손에는 불 켜진 양초를 든 고행자 단체가 가장 먼저 모습을 드러냈다. 그 우두머리가 맨 앞에서 행진했다."]], [["Behind the penitents came a man of vast stature and proportions.", "고행자들 뒤로 거대한 체구와 풍채를 지닌 사내가 따라 나왔다."], ["He was naked, with the exception of cloth drawers at the left side of which hung a large knife in a sheath, and he bore on his right shoulder a heavy iron sledge-hammer.", "그는 왼쪽에 칼집에 든 큼직한 칼을 매단 천 바지 외에는 아무것도 걸치지 않은 알몸이었고, 오른쪽 어깨에는 묵직한 쇠메를 짊어지고 있었다."]], [["This man was the executioner.", "이 사내가 처형인이었다."]], [["He had, moreover, sandals bound on his feet by cords.", "그는 또 끈으로 동여맨 샌들을 발에 신고 있었다."]], [["Behind the executioner came, in the order in which they were to die, first Peppino and then Andrea.", "처형인 뒤로는 죽을 순서대로, 먼저 페피노가, 그다음에 안드레아가 따라왔다."], ["Each was accompanied by two priests.", "둘 다 사제 두 명을 거느리고 있었다."], ["Neither had his eyes bandaged.", "어느 쪽도 눈을 가리지 않았다."]], [["Peppino walked with a firm step, doubtless aware of what awaited him.", "페피노는 자신을 기다리는 운명을 분명 알고 있는 듯, 흔들림 없는 걸음으로 걸었다."], ["Andrea was supported by two priests.", "안드레아는 사제 두 사람의 부축을 받았다."], ["Each of them, from time to time, kissed the crucifix a confessor held out to them.", "두 죄인은 이따금 고해 신부가 내미는 십자가상에 입을 맞추었다."]], [["At this sight alone Franz felt his legs tremble under him.", "이 광경을 보는 것만으로도 프란츠는 다리가 후들거리는 것을 느꼈다."], ["He looked at Albert—he was as white as his shirt, and mechanically cast away his cigar, although he had not half smoked it.", "그는 알베르를 보았다. 알베르는 셔츠처럼 새하얗게 질려, 채 반도 피우지 않은 시가를 무의식중에 던져 버렸다."], ["The count alone seemed unmoved—nay, more, a slight color seemed striving to rise in his pale cheeks.", "오직 백작만이 동요하지 않은 듯 보였다. 아니, 그뿐 아니라 그 창백한 두 뺨에는 옅은 핏기가 돌려고 안간힘을 쓰는 듯했다."], ["His nostrils dilated like those of a wild beast that scents its prey, and his lips, half opened, disclosed his white teeth, small and sharp like those of a jackal.", "콧구멍은 먹잇감의 냄새를 맡은 야수처럼 벌름거렸고, 반쯤 벌어진 입술 사이로는 자칼의 것처럼 작고 날카로운 흰 이가 드러났다."], ["And yet his features wore an expression of smiling tenderness, such as Franz had never before witnessed in them; his black eyes especially were full of kindness and pity.", "그러면서도 그의 표정에는 프란츠가 일찍이 그에게서 본 적 없는, 미소 띤 다정함이 어려 있었다. 특히 그 검은 두 눈은 친절과 연민으로 가득했다."]], [["However, the two culprits advanced, and as they approached their faces became visible.", "그러는 사이 두 죄인이 다가왔고, 가까워질수록 그 얼굴이 또렷이 보였다."], ["Peppino was a handsome young man of four or five-and-twenty, bronzed by the sun; he carried his head erect, and seemed on the watch to see on which side his liberator would appear.", "페피노는 햇볕에 그을린, 스물네댓 살쯤 된 잘생긴 청년이었다. 그는 고개를 꼿꼿이 쳐들고, 자신을 구해 줄 사람이 어느 쪽에서 나타날지 살피려는 듯 경계하는 눈빛이었다."], ["Andrea was short and fat; his visage, marked with brutal cruelty, did not indicate age; he might be thirty.", "안드레아는 키가 작고 뚱뚱했다. 잔혹함이 짐승처럼 박혀 있는 그 얼굴로는 나이를 가늠할 수 없었다."], ["In prison he had suffered his beard to grow; his head fell on his shoulder, his legs bent beneath him, and his movements were apparently automatic and unconscious.", "서른쯤 되었을까. 감옥에서 수염을 길게 길렀고, 머리는 어깨 위로 떨궈져 있었으며, 두 다리는 휘청거리고, 그 몸짓은 보아하니 의식 없는 자동인형 같았다."]], [["“I thought,”", "“저는,”"], ["said Franz to the count, “that you told me there would be but one execution.”", "프란츠가 백작에게 말했다. “처형이 한 건뿐일 거라고 말씀하셨던 걸로 압니다만.”"]], [["“I told you true,”", "“사실대로 말씀드린 겁니다.”"], ["replied he coldly.", "백작이 차갑게 대답했다."]], [["“And yet here are two culprits.”", "“그런데 죄인이 둘이지 않습니까.”"]], [["“Yes; but only one of these two is about to die; the other has many years to live.”", "“그렇지요. 하지만 이 둘 중 한 사람만 죽게 됩니다. 다른 하나는 앞으로 여러 해를 더 살 거고요.”"]], [["“If the pardon is to come, there is no time to lose.”", "“사면이 내려질 거라면, 지체할 시간이 없겠군요.”"]], [["“And see, here it is,” said the count.", "“보십시오, 저기 오고 있습니다.”"], ["At the moment when Peppino reached the foot of the _mandaïa_, a priest arrived in some haste, forced his way through the soldiers, and, advancing to the chief of the brotherhood, gave him a folded paper.", "백작이 말했다. 페피노가 만다이아 발치에 다다른 바로 그 순간, 한 사제가 서둘러 달려와 병사들 사이를 헤치고 나아가더니, 고행자 단체의 우두머리에게 다가가 접힌 종이 한 장을 건넸다."], ["The piercing eye of Peppino had noticed all.", "페피노의 날카로운 눈은 그 모든 것을 놓치지 않았다."], ["The chief took the paper, unfolded it, and, raising his hand, “Heaven be praised, and his Holiness also,”", "우두머리는 종이를 받아 펼치고는 손을 들어 올리며 큰 소리로 외쳤다. “하늘과 교황 성하께 영광을!"], ["said he in a loud voice; “here is a pardon for one of the prisoners!”", "여기 죄수 한 사람에 대한 사면장이 있노라!”"]], [["“A pardon!”", "“사면이다!”"], ["cried the people with one voice; “a pardon!”", "군중이 한목소리로 외쳤다. “사면이야!”"]], [["At this cry Andrea raised his head.", "이 외침에 안드레아가 고개를 들었다."]], [["“Pardon for whom?”", "“누구에 대한 사면이오?”"], ["cried he.", "그가 외쳤다."]], [["Peppino remained breathless.", "페피노는 숨을 죽인 채 가만히 있었다."]], [["“A pardon for Peppino, called Rocca Priori,”", "“로카 프리오리라 불리는 페피노에 대한 사면이오.”"], ["said the principal friar.", "우두머리 수도사가 말했다."], ["And he passed the paper to the officer commanding the carbineers, who read and returned it to him.", "그러고는 기마총병을 지휘하는 장교에게 종이를 건넸고, 장교는 그것을 읽고 다시 돌려주었다."]], [["“For Peppino!”", "“페피노에게라고!”"], ["cried Andrea, who seemed roused from the torpor in which he had been plunged. “Why for him and not for me?", "빠져 있던 마비에서 깨어난 듯 안드레아가 외쳤다. “어째서 저놈에게는 사면이고 내게는 아니란 말이오?"], ["We ought to die together.", "우리는 함께 죽어야 마땅하오."], ["I was promised he should die with me.", "저놈도 나와 함께 죽기로 약속받았소."], ["You have no right to put me to death alone.", "당신들에겐 나 혼자만 죽일 권리가 없소."], ["I will not die alone—I will not!”", "나 혼자는 죽지 않겠소. 절대 안 돼!”"]], [["And he broke from the priests struggling and raving like a wild beast, and striving desperately to break the cords that bound his hands.", "그러고는 야수처럼 발버둥치고 미쳐 날뛰며 사제들을 뿌리치고, 손을 묶은 밧줄을 끊어 보려고 필사적으로 몸부림쳤다."], ["The executioner made a sign, and his two assistants leaped from the scaffold and seized him.", "처형인이 신호를 보내자 두 조수가 처형대에서 뛰어내려 그를 붙잡았다."]], [["“What is going on?”", "“무슨 일입니까?”"], ["asked Franz of the count; for, as all the talk was in the Roman dialect, he had not perfectly understood it.", "프란츠가 백작에게 물었다. 오가는 말이 모두 로마 사투리였던 탓에 제대로 알아듣지 못했던 것이다."]], [["“Do you not see?”", "“보이지 않으십니까?”"], ["returned the count, “that this human creature who is about to die is furious that his fellow-sufferer does not perish with him?", "백작이 대꾸했다. “죽음을 앞둔 저 인간이, 함께 형을 받던 자가 자기와 같이 죽지 않는다고 길길이 날뛰는 게 말입니다."], ["and, were he able, he would rather tear him to pieces with his teeth and nails than let him enjoy the life he himself is about to be deprived of. Oh, man, man—race of crocodiles,”", "할 수만 있다면 저자는, 제가 곧 잃게 될 그 목숨을 동료가 누리게 두느니 차라리 이빨과 손톱으로 그를 갈가리 찢어 놓고 말 겁니다. 오, 인간이여, 인간이여, 악어의 족속이여.”"], ["cried the count, extending his clenched hands towards the crowd, “how well do I recognize you there, and that at all times you are worthy of yourselves!”", "백작이 군중을 향해 꽉 쥔 두 주먹을 내뻗으며 외쳤다. “나는 저기서 너희를 똑똑히 알아보겠노라. 너희가 언제나 너희다움을 잃지 않음을!”"]], [["Meanwhile Andrea and the two executioners were struggling on the ground, and he kept exclaiming, “He ought to die!—he shall die!—I will not die alone!”", "그러는 동안 안드레아와 두 처형인은 땅바닥에서 뒤엉켜 싸웠고, 그는 연신 부르짖었다. “저놈도 죽어야 해! 저놈도 죽일 거야! 나 혼자는 죽지 않아!”"]], [["“Look, look,”", "“보십시오, 보세요.”"], ["cried the count, seizing the young men’s hands; “look, for on my soul it is curious.", "백작이 두 젊은이의 손을 붙잡으며 외쳤다. “보십시오, 맹세코 흥미로운 일이니까요."], ["Here is a man who had resigned himself to his fate, who was going to the scaffold to die—like a coward, it is true, but he was about to die without resistance.", "여기 자기 운명을 체념하고 받아들였던 사내가, 처형대로 죽으러 가던 사내가 있습니다. 비겁한 꼴이긴 했어도, 어쨌든 저항 없이 죽으려던 참이었지요."], ["Do you know what gave him strength?", "무엇이 그에게 그런 힘을 주었는지 아십니까?"], ["do you know what consoled him?", "무엇이 그를 위로했는지 아십니까?"], ["It was, that another partook of his punishment—that another partook of his anguish—that another was to die before him!", "또 다른 자가 그의 형벌을 함께 나누고, 또 다른 자가 그의 고통을 함께 나누며, 또 다른 자가 그보다 먼저 죽는다는 것이었습니다!"], ["Lead two sheep to the butcher’s, two oxen to the slaughterhouse, and make one of them understand that his companion will not die; the sheep will bleat for pleasure, the ox will bellow with joy.", "양 두 마리를 도살장으로 끌고 가고, 소 두 마리를 도축장으로 끌고 가서, 그중 하나에게 동료는 죽지 않는다고 알아듣게 해 보십시오. 양은 기뻐서 매애 울고, 소는 즐거워서 음매 울 겁니다."], ["But man—man, whom God created in his own image—man, upon whom God has laid his first, his sole commandment, to love his neighbor—man, to whom God has given a voice to express his thoughts—what is his first cry when he hears his fellow-man is saved?", "그러나 인간은, 신이 자신의 형상대로 지으신 인간은, 신이 가장 으뜸가고 유일한 계명, 곧 네 이웃을 사랑하라는 계명을 내리신 그 인간은, 신이 제 생각을 표현하라고 목소리를 주신 그 인간은, 제 동료가 살아났다는 말을 들었을 때 가장 먼저 무엇을 외칩니까?"], ["A blasphemy.", "저주입니다."], ["Honor to man, this masterpiece of nature, this king of the creation!”", "인간에게 영광을, 이 자연의 걸작에게, 이 만물의 왕에게 영광을!”"]], [["And the count burst into a laugh; a terrible laugh, that showed he must have suffered horribly to be able thus to laugh.", "그러더니 백작은 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 끔찍한 웃음이었다. 저토록 웃을 수 있으려면 그가 얼마나 처참하게 고통받았을지를 짐작하게 하는 웃음이었다."]], [["However, the struggle still continued, and it was dreadful to witness.", "그러는 동안에도 싸움은 계속되었고, 차마 지켜보기 끔찍했다."], ["The two assistants carried Andrea up to the scaffold; the people all took part against Andrea, and twenty thousand voices cried, “Put him to death!", "두 조수가 안드레아를 처형대 위로 끌어 올렸다. 사람들은 모두 안드레아에게 등을 돌렸고, 이만 명의 목소리가 외쳤다. “저놈을 죽여라!"], ["put him to death!”", "죽여 버려라!”"]], [["Franz sprang back, but the count seized his arm, and held him before the window.", "프란츠가 흠칫 물러섰으나, 백작이 그의 팔을 붙잡아 창 앞에 붙들어 세웠다."]], [["“What are you doing?”", "“뭘 하시는 겁니까?”"], ["said he. “Do you pity him?", "그가 말했다. “저자가 가엾으십니까?"], ["If you heard the cry of ‘Mad dog!’ you would take your gun—you would unhesitatingly shoot the poor beast, who, after all, was only guilty of having been bitten by another dog.", "‘미친개다!’ 하는 외침을 들으면, 당신은 총을 집어 들 겁니다. 따지고 보면 다른 개에게 물린 죄밖에 없는 그 가엾은 짐승을, 당신은 망설임 없이 쏘아 버리겠지요."], ["And yet you pity a man who, without being bitten by one of his race, has yet murdered his benefactor; and who, now unable to kill anyone, because his hands are bound, wishes to see his companion in captivity perish.", "그러면서 같은 족속에게 물린 적도 없이 제 은인을 죽인 사내를, 이제는 손이 묶여 누구도 죽일 수 없게 되자 옥에 갇힌 동료가 죽기를 바라는 그 사내를 가엾게 여기십니까? 아닙니다, 아니에요."], ["No, no—look, look!” 20172m", "보십시오, 보세요!”"]], [["The recommendation was needless.", "그런 권유는 부질없었다."], ["Franz was fascinated by the horrible spectacle.", "프란츠는 그 끔찍한 광경에 넋이 나가 있었다."]], [["The two assistants had borne Andrea to the scaffold, and there, in spite of his struggles, his bites, and his cries, had forced him to his knees.", "두 조수가 안드레아를 처형대로 끌어다 놓고는, 그의 발버둥과 깨물기와 비명에도 아랑곳없이 무릎을 꿇려 놓았다."], ["During this time the executioner had raised his mace, and signed to them to get out of the way; the criminal strove to rise, but, ere he had time, the mace fell on his left temple.", "그사이 처형인은 쇠메를 들어 올리고는 조수들에게 비키라고 신호했다. 죄인이 일어서려고 안간힘을 썼으나, 미처 그럴 틈도 없이 쇠메가 그의 왼쪽 관자놀이에 떨어졌다."], ["A dull and heavy sound was heard, and the man dropped like an ox on his face, and then turned over on his back.", "둔하고 묵직한 소리가 울렸고, 사내는 소처럼 얼굴부터 고꾸라졌다가 다시 벌렁 나자빠졌다."]], [["The executioner let fall his mace, drew his knife, and with one stroke opened his throat, and mounting on his stomach, stamped violently on it with his feet.", "처형인은 쇠메를 떨구고 칼을 뽑아 단칼에 그의 목을 갈랐다. 그러고는 그의 배 위로 올라타 두 발로 사납게 짓밟았다."], ["At every stroke a jet of blood sprang from the wound.", "짓밟을 때마다 상처에서 핏줄기가 솟구쳤다."]], [["This time Franz could contain himself no longer, but sank, half fainting, into a seat.", "이번에는 프란츠도 더 이상 견디지 못하고, 반쯤 정신을 잃은 채 의자에 주저앉았다."]], [["Albert, with his eyes closed, was standing grasping the window-curtains.", "알베르는 두 눈을 감은 채 창문 커튼을 움켜쥐고 서 있었다."]], [["The count was erect and triumphant, like the Avenging Angel!", "백작은 복수의 천사처럼, 꼿꼿이 서서 의기양양해 있었다!"]], [["# Chapter 36. The Carnival at Rome", "# Chapter 36. The Carnival at Rome"]], [["When Franz recovered his senses, he saw Albert drinking a glass of water, of which, to judge from his pallor, he stood in great need; and the count, who was assuming his masquerade costume.", "프란츠가 정신을 차렸을 때, 알베르는 물 한 잔을 마시고 있었다. 그 창백한 얼굴로 보아 물이 몹시 필요해 보였다. 백작은 가장무도회 의상을 걸치고 있었다."], ["He glanced mechanically towards the piazza—the scene was wholly changed; scaffold, executioners, victims, all had disappeared; only the people remained, full of noise and excitement.", "프란츠는 무심코 광장 쪽을 바라보았다. 풍경은 완전히 달라져 있었다. 처형대도, 사형 집행인들도, 사형수도 모두 사라지고 없었다."], ["The bell of Monte Citorio, which only sounds on the pope’s decease and the opening of the Carnival, was ringing a joyous peal.", "오직 사람들만 남아 떠들썩하게 들끓고 있었다. 교황의 서거와 사육제의 개막 때만 울리는 몬테 치토리오의 종이 흥겨운 가락으로 울려 퍼지고 있었다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래서,\""], ["asked he of the count, “what has, then, happened?”", "그가 백작에게 물었다. \"대체 무슨 일이 있었던 겁니까?\""]], [["“Nothing,”", "\"아무 일도 없었습니다.\""], ["replied the count; “only, as you see, the Carnival has commenced.", "백작이 대답했다. \"보시다시피 사육제가 시작되었을 뿐이지요."], ["Make haste and dress yourself.”", "어서 옷을 갈아입으십시오.\""]], [["“In fact,”", "\"사실,\""], ["said Franz, “this horrible scene has passed away like a dream.”", "프란츠가 말했다. \"그 끔찍한 광경이 꿈처럼 지나가 버렸군요.\""]], [["“It is but a dream, a nightmare, that has disturbed you.”", "\"당신을 어지럽힌 건 한낱 꿈, 악몽일 뿐입니다.\""]], [["“Yes, that I have suffered; but the culprit?”", "\"그렇지요, 제가 시달린 것은요. 그런데 그 죄인은요?\""]], [["“That is a dream also; only he has remained asleep, while you have awakened; and who knows which of you is the most fortunate?”", "\"그것도 꿈입니다. 다만 그는 여전히 잠들어 있고, 당신은 깨어났을 뿐이지요. 둘 중 누가 더 운이 좋은지 누가 알겠습니까?\""]], [["“But Peppino—what has become of him?”", "\"하지만 페피노는요. 그는 어떻게 됐습니까?\""]], [["“Peppino is a lad of sense, who, unlike most men, who are happy in proportion as they are noticed, was delighted to see that the general attention was directed towards his companion.", "\"페피노는 분별 있는 젊은이지요. 대개의 사람들은 주목받는 만큼 행복해하지만, 그는 달랐습니다. 사람들의 관심이 온통 자기 동료에게 쏠리는 걸 보고 기뻐했지요."], ["He profited by this distraction to slip away among the crowd, without even thanking the worthy priests who accompanied him.", "그 틈을 타 군중 속으로 슬쩍 빠져나갔습니다. 그를 따라온 갸륵한 신부들에게 고맙다는 인사조차 없이 말입니다."], ["Decidedly man is an ungrateful and egotistical animal.", "정말이지 인간이란 배은망덕하고 이기적인 동물입니다."], ["But dress yourself; see, M.", "자, 옷을 갈아입으시지요."], ["de Morcerf sets you the example.”", "보십시오, 모르세르 씨가 본을 보이고 있지 않습니까.\""]], [["Albert was drawing on the satin pantaloon over his black trousers and varnished boots.", "알베르는 검정 바지와 에나멜 장화 위로 새틴 판탈롱을 끌어올리고 있었다."]], [["“Well, Albert,”", "\"그래, 알베르.\""], ["said Franz, “do you feel much inclined to join the revels?", "프란츠가 말했다. \"흥겨운 잔치에 끼어들고 싶은 마음이 좀 드나?"], ["Come, answer frankly.”", "어디, 솔직히 말해 보게.\""]], [["“_Ma foi_, no,”", "\"맹세코 그렇지는 않아.\""], ["returned Albert.", "알베르가 대꾸했다."], ["“But I am really glad to have seen such a sight; and I understand what the count said—that when you have once habituated yourself to a similar spectacle, it is the only one that causes you any emotion.”", "\"하지만 그런 광경을 본 건 정말 다행이라고 생각해. 그리고 백작이 한 말이 이제 이해가 가. 그런 구경거리에 한번 익숙해지고 나면, 그것만이 사람을 동요시키는 유일한 광경이 된다는 말 말이야.\""]], [["20175m\n“Without reflecting that this is the only moment in which you can study character,”", "\"이 순간이야말로 인간의 본성을 들여다볼 수 있는 유일한 때라는 건 생각지 못하셨군요.\""], ["said the count; “on the steps of the scaffold death tears off the mask that has been worn through life, and the real visage is disclosed.", "백작이 말했다. \"처형대 계단에서 죽음은 평생 써 온 가면을 벗겨 내고, 진짜 얼굴을 드러냅니다."], ["It must be allowed that Andrea was not very handsome, the hideous scoundrel!", "안드레아가 그리 잘생긴 얼굴이 아니었다는 건 인정해야겠지요, 그 흉측한 악당 말입니다!"], ["Come, dress yourselves, gentlemen, dress yourselves.”", "자, 어서 옷을 입으세요, 여러분, 어서요.\""]], [["Franz felt it would be ridiculous not to follow his two companions’ example.", "프란츠는 두 동행을 따르지 않으면 우스워 보이리라는 것을 깨달았다."], ["He assumed his costume, and fastened on the mask that scarcely equalled the pallor of his own face.", "그는 의상을 걸치고, 자신의 얼굴빛에 거의 미치지 못할 만큼 창백한 가면을 썼다."], ["Their toilet finished, they descended; the carriage awaited them at the door, filled with sweetmeats and bouquets.", "단장을 마치고 그들은 아래로 내려갔다. 마차가 문 앞에서 그들을 기다리고 있었는데, 사탕과 꽃다발로 가득 차 있었다."], ["They fell into the line of carriages.", "그들은 마차들의 행렬 속으로 끼어들었다."]], [["It is difficult to form an idea of the perfect change that had taken place.", "이 완벽한 변화를 머릿속에 그려 보기란 쉽지 않다."], ["Instead of the spectacle of gloomy and silent death, the Piazza del Popolo presented a spectacle of gay and noisy mirth and revelry.", "음울하고 적막한 죽음의 광경 대신, 포폴로 광장은 흥겹고 떠들썩한 환락과 잔치의 무대로 바뀌어 있었다."], ["A crowd of masks flowed in from all sides, emerging from the doors, descending from the windows.", "가면을 쓴 무리가 사방에서 쏟아져 나와, 문을 통해 흘러나오고 창문에서 내려왔다. 거리마다 모퉁이마다 마차들이 달려 나왔다."], ["From every street and every corner drove carriages filled with clowns, harlequins, dominoes, mummers, pantomimists, Transteverins, knights, and peasants, screaming, fighting, gesticulating, throwing eggs filled with flour, confetti, nosegays, attacking, with their sarcasms and their missiles, friends and foes, companions and strangers, indiscriminately, and no one took offence, or did anything but laugh.", "그 안에는 어릿광대, 아를레키노, 도미노 가면을 쓴 자들, 무언극 배우들, 판토마임 연기자들, 트라스테베레 사람들, 기사들, 농부들이 가득 타고 있었다. 그들은 소리를 지르고, 싸우고, 손짓을 해 대며, 밀가루를 채운 달걀과 콘페티, 꽃다발을 던졌다. 친구든 적이든, 동료든 낯선 사람이든 가리지 않고 비아냥과 투척물로 공격했지만, 아무도 화내지 않고 그저 웃기만 했다."]], [["Franz and Albert were like men who, to drive away a violent sorrow, have recourse to wine, and who, as they drink and become intoxicated, feel a thick veil drawn between the past and the present.", "프란츠와 알베르는 격렬한 슬픔을 떨쳐 내려고 술에 기대는 사람들 같았다. 마시고 또 마시며 취해 갈수록, 과거와 현재 사이에 두꺼운 장막이 드리워지는 것을 느끼는 사람들 말이다."], ["They saw, or rather continued to see, the image of what they had witnessed; but little by little the general vertigo seized them, and they felt themselves obliged to take part in the noise and confusion.", "그들은 자신들이 목격한 장면을 보았다, 아니 계속 보고 있었다. 하지만 차츰차츰 전반적인 현기증이 그들을 사로잡았고, 그들은 어쩔 수 없이 그 소란과 혼돈에 끼어들지 않을 수 없게 되었다."]], [["A handful of confetti that came from a neighboring carriage, and which, while it covered Morcerf and his two companions with dust, pricked his neck and that portion of his face uncovered by his mask like a hundred pins, incited him to join in the general combat, in which all the masks around him were engaged.", "옆을 지나던 마차에서 날아온 콘페티 한 줌이 모르세르와 두 동행을 먼지로 뒤덮었다. 그러면서 가면으로 가리지 못한 그의 목과 얼굴 일부를 바늘 백 개로 찌르듯 따끔하게 만들었다. 이에 자극받은 그는 주위의 가면 쓴 사람들이 모두 벌이고 있는 전반적인 싸움에 끼어들었다."], ["He rose in his turn, and seizing handfuls of confetti and sweetmeats, with which the carriage was filled, cast them with all the force and skill he was master of. 20177m", "그도 자리에서 일어나 마차에 가득한 콘페티와 사탕을 한 움큼씩 집어, 자신이 부릴 수 있는 온 힘과 솜씨로 던졌다."]], [["The strife had fairly begun, and the recollection of what they had seen half an hour before was gradually effaced from the young men’s minds, so much were they occupied by the gay and glittering procession they now beheld.", "싸움이 본격적으로 시작되었다. 반 시간 전에 본 광경의 기억은 두 젊은이의 머릿속에서 차츰 지워져 갔다. 지금 눈앞에 펼쳐진 흥겹고 화려한 행렬에 그만큼 정신이 팔려 있었기 때문이다."]], [["As for the Count of Monte Cristo, he had never for an instant shown any appearance of having been moved.", "몬테크리스토 백작으로 말하자면, 그는 단 한순간도 동요한 기색을 내비치지 않았다."], ["Imagine the large and splendid Corso, bordered from one end to the other with lofty palaces, with their balconies hung with carpets, and their windows with flags.", "한쪽 끝에서 다른 끝까지 높다란 궁전들이 늘어선, 크고 화려한 코르소 대로를 상상해 보라. 발코니에는 양탄자가 걸려 있고, 창문에는 깃발이 나부낀다."], ["At these balconies are three hundred thousand spectators—Romans, Italians, strangers from all parts of the world, the united aristocracy of birth, wealth, and genius.", "그 발코니에는 삼십만 명의 구경꾼이 있다. 로마 사람들, 이탈리아 사람들, 세계 각지에서 온 이방인들, 혈통과 부와 재능을 두루 갖춘 귀족들이 한데 모여 있다."], ["Lovely women, yielding to the influence of the scene, bend over their balconies, or lean from their windows, and shower down confetti, which are returned by bouquets; the air seems darkened with the falling confetti and flying flowers.", "사랑스러운 여인들이 그 분위기에 이끌려 발코니 너머로 몸을 기울이거나 창밖으로 상체를 내밀고 콘페티를 흩뿌리면, 꽃다발이 되돌아온다. 떨어지는 콘페티와 날아다니는 꽃으로 하늘이 어두워진 듯하다."], ["In the streets the lively crowd is dressed in the most fantastic costumes—gigantic cabbages walk gravely about, buffaloes’ heads bellow from men’s shoulders, dogs walk on their hind legs; in the midst of all this a mask is lifted, and, as in Callot’s Temptation of St.", "거리에서는 활기찬 군중이 더없이 기상천외한 의상을 입고 있다. 거대한 양배추가 점잖게 걸어 다니고, 물소 머리가 사람들의 어깨 위에서 울부짖으며, 개가 뒷다리로 걷는다. 이 모든 것 한가운데서 가면이 들어 올려지면, 칼로의 「성 안토니우스의 유혹」에서처럼 사랑스러운 얼굴이 드러난다."], ["Anthony, a lovely face is exhibited, which we would fain follow, but from which we are separated by troops of fiends. This will give a faint idea of the Carnival at Rome.", "우리는 그 뒤를 따르고 싶지만, 악귀들의 무리에 가로막혀 떨어지고 만다. 이것으로 로마 사육제의 어렴풋한 인상이나마 짐작할 수 있으리라."]], [["At the second turn, the count stopped the carriage, and requested permission to withdraw, leaving the vehicle at their disposal.", "두 번째 모퉁이에서 백작은 마차를 세우고, 자리를 뜨게 해 달라고 청하면서 마차는 두 사람이 마음대로 쓰라고 했다."], ["Franz looked up—they were opposite the Rospoli Palace.", "프란츠가 고개를 들어 보니, 그들은 로스폴리 궁전 맞은편에 있었다."], ["At the centre window, the one hung with white damask with a red cross, was a blue domino, beneath which Franz’s imagination easily pictured the beautiful Greek of the Argentina.", "한가운데 창문, 붉은 십자가가 그려진 흰색 다마스크 천을 두른 그 창문에 푸른 도미노가 보였다. 프란츠는 그 가면 아래에 아르헨티나 극장의 아름다운 그리스 여인을 손쉽게 떠올렸다."]], [["“Gentlemen,”", "\"여러분,\""], ["said the count, springing out, “when you are tired of being actors, and wish to become spectators of this scene, you know you have places at my windows.", "백작이 마차에서 뛰어내리며 말했다. \"배우 노릇에 지쳐 이 광경의 구경꾼이 되고 싶어지거든, 제 창문에 자리가 마련되어 있다는 걸 기억하십시오."], ["In the meantime, dispose of my coachman, my carriage, and my servants.”", "그동안 제 마부와 마차, 하인들을 마음껏 부리시고요.\""]], [["We have forgotten to mention, that the count’s coachman was attired in a bear-skin, exactly resembling Odry’s in _The Bear and the Pasha_; and the two footmen behind were dressed up as green monkeys, with spring masks, with which they made grimaces at everyone who passed.", "미처 말하지 못했는데, 백작의 마부는 「곰과 파샤」에 나오는 오드리와 똑같이 곰 가죽을 뒤집어쓰고 있었다. 뒤에 선 두 하인은 초록빛 원숭이로 분장하고, 용수철 달린 가면을 쓴 채 지나가는 사람마다 우스꽝스러운 표정을 지어 보였다."]], [["Franz thanked the count for his attention.", "프란츠는 백작의 배려에 고마움을 표했다."], ["As for Albert, he was busily occupied throwing bouquets at a carriage full of Roman peasants that was passing near him.", "알베르는 가까이 지나가는, 로마 농부들로 가득 찬 마차에 꽃다발을 던지느라 여념이 없었다."], ["Unfortunately for him, the line of carriages moved on again, and while he descended the Piazza del Popolo, the other ascended towards the Palazzo di Venezia.", "그에게는 불운하게도, 마차 행렬이 다시 움직이기 시작했다. 그가 포폴로 광장으로 내려가는 동안, 다른 마차는 베네치아 궁전 쪽으로 올라가 버렸다."]], [["“Ah, my dear fellow,”", "\"아, 이보게.\""], ["said he to Franz; “you did not see?”", "그가 프란츠에게 말했다. \"자네 못 봤나?\""]], [["“What?”", "\"뭘?\""]], [["“There,—that calash filled with Roman peasants.” “No.”", "\"저기, 로마 농부들이 가득 탄 저 사륜마차 말이야.\""]], [["“Well, I am convinced they are all charming women.”", "\"못 봤네.\" \"음, 분명히 다들 매력적인 여자들일 거야.\""]], [["“How unfortunate that you were masked, Albert,”", "\"가면을 쓰고 있었으니 안됐군, 알베르.\""], ["said Franz; “here was an opportunity of making up for past disappointments.”", "프란츠가 말했다. \"지난번 실망을 만회할 기회였는데 말이야.\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오.\""], ["replied he, half laughing, half serious; “I hope the Carnival will not pass without some amends in one shape or the other.”", "그가 반은 웃으며 반은 진지하게 대꾸했다. \"이번 사육제가 이런저런 보상 한 번 없이 지나가지는 않을 거라고 믿네.\""]], [["But, in spite of Albert’s hope, the day passed unmarked by any incident, excepting two or three encounters with the carriage full of Roman peasants.", "하지만 알베르의 바람과 달리, 그날은 로마 농부들로 가득 찬 그 마차와 두세 번 마주친 것 말고는 아무 일도 없이 지나갔다."], ["At one of these encounters, accidentally or purposely, Albert’s mask fell off.", "그렇게 마주치던 어느 순간, 우연인지 일부러인지 알베르의 가면이 벗겨졌다."], ["He instantly rose and cast the remainder of the bouquets into the carriage.", "그는 즉시 일어나 남은 꽃다발을 그 마차 안으로 던졌다."], ["Doubtless one of the charming females Albert had detected beneath their coquettish disguise was touched by his gallantry; for, as the carriage of the two friends passed her, she threw a bunch of violets.", "알베르가 그 교태 어린 변장 아래로 알아본 매력적인 여인들 중 하나가 그의 호의에 마음이 움직인 게 분명했다. 두 친구의 마차가 그녀 곁을 지날 때, 그녀가 제비꽃 한 다발을 던졌으니까. 알베르가 그것을 붙잡았다."], ["Albert seized it, and as Franz had no reason to suppose it was meant for him, he suffered Albert to retain it.", "프란츠는 그것이 자기에게 던진 것이라고 여길 이유가 없었으므로, 알베르가 그것을 간직하도록 내버려 두었다."], ["Albert placed it in his button-hole, and the carriage went triumphantly on.", "알베르는 그것을 단춧구멍에 꽂았고, 마차는 의기양양하게 나아갔다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래.\""], ["said Franz to him; “there is the beginning of an adventure.”", "프란츠가 그에게 말했다. \"모험의 시작이로군.\""]], [["“Laugh if you please—I really think so.", "\"비웃고 싶으면 비웃게. 나는 정말 그렇다고 생각하니까."], ["So I will not abandon this bouquet.”", "그러니 이 꽃다발을 버리지 않겠어.\""]], [["“_Pardieu_,”", "\"이런.\""], ["returned Franz, laughing, “in token of your ingratitude.”", "프란츠가 웃으며 대꾸했다. \"자네 배은망덕의 증표로군.\""]], [["The jest, however, soon appeared to become earnest; for when Albert and Franz again encountered the carriage with the _contadini_, the one who had thrown the violets to Albert, clapped her hands when she beheld them in his button-hole.", "그러나 그 농담은 곧 진담이 되는 듯했다. 알베르와 프란츠가 그 콘타디니가 탄 마차와 다시 마주쳤을 때, 알베르에게 제비꽃을 던진 여인이 그의 단춧구멍에 꽂힌 그 꽃을 보고는 손뼉을 쳤기 때문이다."]], [["“Bravo, bravo,”", "\"브라보, 브라보.\""], ["said Franz; “things go wonderfully.", "프란츠가 말했다. \"일이 놀랍게 풀리는군."], ["Shall I leave you? Perhaps you would prefer being alone?”", "내가 자리를 비켜 줄까? 혼자 있는 편이 낫겠나?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니.\""], ["replied he; “I will not be caught like a fool at a first disclosure by a rendezvous under the clock, as they say at the opera-balls.", "그가 대답했다. \"오페라 무도회에서들 하는 말마따나, 첫 만남에 시계탑 아래 밀회 약속에나 넘어가는 바보처럼 굴지는 않겠어."], ["If the fair peasant wishes to carry matters any further, we shall find her, or rather, she will find us tomorrow; then she will give me some sign or other, and I shall know what I have to do.”", "저 어여쁜 농부 아가씨가 일을 더 진전시키고 싶다면, 우리가 그녀를 찾아내겠지. 아니, 내일은 그녀가 우리를 찾아낼 거야. 그러면 그녀가 무슨 신호든 보내올 테고, 나는 내가 뭘 해야 할지 알게 되겠지.\""]], [["“On my word,”", "\"맹세코,\""], ["said Franz, “you are as wise as Nestor and prudent as Ulysses, and your fair Circe must be very skilful or very powerful if she succeed in changing you into a beast of any kind.”", "프란츠가 말했다. \"자네는 네스토르만큼 현명하고 율리시스만큼 신중하군. 자네의 그 어여쁜 키르케가 자네를 무슨 짐승으로든 둔갑시키려면, 어지간히 솜씨가 좋거나 어지간히 위력이 세야 할걸세.\""]], [["Albert was right; the fair unknown had resolved, doubtless, to carry the intrigue no farther; for although the young men made several more turns, they did not again see the calash, which had turned up one of the neighboring streets. Then they returned to the Rospoli Palace; but the count and the blue domino had also disappeared; the two windows, hung with yellow damask, were still occupied by the persons whom the count had invited.", "알베르의 말이 옳았다. 그 어여쁜 미지의 여인은 분명 이 밀회를 더는 진전시키지 않기로 마음먹은 듯했다. 두 젊은이가 몇 바퀴를 더 돌았지만, 그 사륜마차를 다시 보지 못했으니까. 마차는 근처 골목 중 하나로 접어들어 버렸다. 그들은 다시 로스폴리 궁전으로 돌아왔다. 하지만 백작도 푸른 도미노도 사라지고 없었다. 노란 다마스크 천을 두른 두 창문에는 백작이 초대한 사람들이 여전히 자리하고 있었다."]], [["At this moment the same bell that had proclaimed the beginning of the mascherata sounded the retreat.", "이때, 가장행렬의 시작을 알렸던 바로 그 종이 퇴장을 알렸다."], ["The file on the Corso broke the line, and in a second all the carriages had disappeared.", "코르소의 마차 행렬이 대열을 풀었고, 순식간에 모든 마차가 사라졌다."], ["Franz and Albert were opposite the Via delle Muratte; the coachman, without saying a word, drove up it, passed along the Piazza di Spagna and the Rospoli Palace and stopped at the door of the hotel.", "프란츠와 알베르는 무라테 거리 맞은편에 있었다. 마부는 한마디도 없이 그 길을 올라가, 스페인 광장과 로스폴리 궁전을 지나 호텔 문 앞에 멈췄다."], ["Signor Pastrini came to the door to receive his guests.", "파스트리니 씨가 손님들을 맞으러 문으로 나왔다."]], [["Franz hastened to inquire after the count, and to express regret that he had not returned in sufficient time; but Pastrini reassured him by saying that the Count of Monte Cristo had ordered a second carriage for himself, and that it had gone at four o’clock to fetch him from the Rospoli Palace.", "프란츠는 서둘러 백작의 안부를 물으며, 백작이 제때 돌아오지 못한 것을 안타까워했다. 하지만 파스트리니는 몬테크리스토 백작이 자신을 위해 두 번째 마차를 따로 마련해 두었고, 그것이 네 시에 로스폴리 궁전으로 그를 데리러 갔다고 안심시켰다."]], [["The count had, moreover, charged him to offer the two friends the key of his box at the Argentina.", "게다가 백작은 그에게 두 친구에게 아르헨티나 극장의 자기 특별석 열쇠를 건네 달라고 부탁해 두었다."], ["Franz questioned Albert as to his intentions; but Albert had great projects to put into execution before going to the theatre; and instead of making any answer, he inquired if Signor Pastrini could procure him a tailor.", "프란츠가 알베르에게 어쩔 셈이냐고 물었다. 하지만 알베르는 극장에 가기 전에 실행에 옮길 큰 계획이 있었다. 그는 대답 대신, 파스트리니 씨가 재단사를 구해 줄 수 있겠느냐고 물었다."]], [["“A tailor,”", "\"재단사라뇨.\""], ["said the host; “and for what?”", "주인이 말했다. \"무엇 때문에요?\""]], [["“To make us between now and tomorrow two Roman peasant costumes,”", "\"지금부터 내일까지 우리 둘이 입을 로마 농부 의상 두 벌을 만들어야 해서요.\""], ["returned Albert.", "알베르가 대꾸했다."]], [["The host shook his head.", "주인이 고개를 저었다."]], [["“To make you two costumes between now and tomorrow?", "\"지금부터 내일까지 두 벌을 만들라굽쇼?"], ["I ask your excellencies’ pardon, but this is quite a French demand; for the next week you will not find a single tailor who would consent to sew six buttons on a waistcoat if you paid him a crown a piece for each button.”", "나리들, 송구하오나 그건 영락없이 프랑스식 요구이십니다요. 앞으로 일주일간은, 단추 하나에 한 닢씩을 쳐주신대도 조끼에 단추 여섯 개를 달아 주려 할 재단사를 단 한 명도 못 찾으실 겁니다.\""]], [["“Then I must give up the idea?”", "\"그럼 이 생각은 포기해야겠군요?\""]], [["“No; we have them ready-made.", "\"아닙니다요. 이미 다 만들어진 게 있습죠."], ["Leave all to me; and tomorrow, when you awake, you shall find a collection of costumes with which you will be satisfied.”", "다 제게 맡기십시오. 내일 아침 잠에서 깨시면, 만족하실 만한 의상들이 한 벌 갖춰져 있을 겁니다.\""]], [["“My dear Albert,”", "\"이보게 알베르.\" 프란츠가 말했다."], ["said Franz, “leave all to our host; he has already proved himself full of resources; let us dine quietly, and afterwards go and see _l’Italienne à Alger!_", "\"다 주인에게 맡기게. 이 사람은 이미 수완이 좋다는 걸 충분히 증명했으니. 우리는 조용히 저녁이나 들고, 그런 다음 「알제의 이탈리아 여인」을 보러 가세!\""]], [["“Agreed,”", "\"좋아.\""], ["returned Albert; “but remember, Signor Pastrini, that both my friend and myself attach the greatest importance to having tomorrow the costumes we have asked for.”", "알베르가 대꾸했다. \"다만 파스트리니 씨, 내 친구와 나 둘 다 부탁한 의상을 내일 받는 일을 무엇보다 중하게 여긴다는 걸 잊지 마시오.\""]], [["The host again assured them they might rely on him, and that their wishes should be attended to; upon which Franz and Albert mounted to their apartments, and proceeded to disencumber themselves of their costumes.", "주인은 자신을 믿어도 좋으며 두 분의 바람을 꼭 챙기겠다고 거듭 다짐했다. 그 말에 프란츠와 알베르는 각자의 방으로 올라가, 의상을 벗기 시작했다."], ["Albert, as he took off his dress, carefully preserved the bunch of violets; it was his token reserved for the morrow.", "알베르는 옷을 벗으면서 제비꽃 다발을 조심스레 간직했다. 그것은 내일을 위해 남겨 둔 그의 증표였다."]], [["The two friends sat down to table; but they could not refrain from remarking the difference between the Count of Monte Cristo’s table and that of Signor Pastrini.", "두 친구는 식탁에 앉았다. 하지만 몬테크리스토 백작의 식탁과 파스트리니 씨의 식탁 사이의 차이를 언급하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Truth compelled Franz, in spite of the dislike he seemed to have taken to the count, to confess that the advantage was not on Pastrini’s side.", "프란츠는 백작에게 품게 된 듯한 거부감에도 불구하고, 진실 앞에서 파스트리니 쪽이 우세하지 않다는 것을 인정할 수밖에 없었다."], ["During dessert, the servant inquired at what time they wished for the carriage.", "후식을 드는 동안, 하인이 몇 시에 마차를 부르실 거냐고 물었다."], ["Albert and Franz looked at each other, fearing really to abuse the count’s kindness.", "알베르와 프란츠는 백작의 호의를 정말로 남용하는 게 아닐까 염려하며 서로를 바라보았다."], ["The servant understood them.", "하인이 그 마음을 알아챘다."]], [["“His excellency the Count of Monte Cristo had,”", "\"몬테크리스토 백작 각하께서는,\""], ["he said, “given positive orders that the carriage was to remain at their lordships’ orders all day, and they could therefore dispose of it without fear of indiscretion.”", "그가 말했다. \"마차를 종일 두 나리의 분부에 두라고 분명히 명하셨습니다. 그러니 조금도 폐가 될까 염려치 마시고 마음껏 쓰셔도 됩니다.\""]], [["They resolved to profit by the count’s courtesy, and ordered the horses to be harnessed, while they substituted evening dress for that which they had on, and which was somewhat the worse for the numerous combats they had sustained.", "그들은 백작의 호의를 누리기로 마음먹고 말에 마구를 채우라고 일렀다. 그동안 그들은 입고 있던 옷을 벗고 야회복으로 갈아입었다. 입고 있던 옷은 그동안 치른 숱한 싸움으로 다소 망가진 상태였다."]], [["20181m\nThis precaution taken, they went to the theatre, and installed themselves in the count’s box.", "이렇게 채비를 마치고 그들은 극장으로 가, 백작의 특별석에 자리를 잡았다."], ["During the first act, the Countess G—— entered.", "첫 막이 진행되는 동안, G 백작 부인이 들어왔다."], ["Her first look was at the box where she had seen the count the previous evening, so that she perceived Franz and Albert in the place of the very person concerning whom she had expressed so strange an opinion to Franz.", "그녀의 첫 시선은 전날 저녁 백작을 보았던 그 특별석으로 향했다. 그래서 그녀는, 자신이 프란츠에게 그토록 기이한 견해를 밝혔던 바로 그 인물 대신 프란츠와 알베르가 앉아 있는 것을 알아챘다."], ["Her opera-glass was so fixedly directed towards them, that Franz saw it would be cruel not to satisfy her curiosity; and, availing himself of one of the privileges of the spectators of the Italian theatres, who use their boxes to hold receptions, the two friends went to pay their respects to the countess.", "그녀의 오페라 안경이 어찌나 그들 쪽에 고정되어 있던지, 프란츠는 그녀의 호기심을 채워 주지 않으면 매정한 일이 되겠다고 느꼈다. 이탈리아 극장 관객들은 특별석을 응접실 삼아 손님을 맞곤 하는데, 두 친구는 그 특권 중 하나를 빌려 백작 부인에게 인사를 하러 갔다."], ["Scarcely had they entered, when she motioned to Franz to assume the seat of honor.", "그들이 들어서기가 무섭게, 그녀는 프란츠에게 상석에 앉으라고 손짓했다."], ["Albert, in his turn, sat behind.", "알베르는 그 뒤에 앉았다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래.\""], ["said she, hardly giving Franz time to sit down, “it seems you have nothing better to do than to make the acquaintance of this new Lord Ruthven, and you are already the best friends in the world.”", "그녀가 프란츠에게 앉을 새도 거의 주지 않고 말했다. \"이 새로운 러스벤 경과 친분을 트는 일보다 더 나은 일이 없으셨나 봐요. 벌써 둘도 없는 친구 사이가 되셨다지요.\""]], [["“Without being so far advanced as that, my dear countess,”", "\"거기까지 나아간 건 아닙니다만, 친애하는 백작 부인.\""], ["returned Franz, “I cannot deny that we have abused his good nature all day.”", "프란츠가 대꾸했다. \"온종일 그의 호의를 남용한 건 부인할 수 없겠군요.\""]], [["“All day?”", "\"온종일이요?\""]], [["“Yes; this morning we breakfasted with him; we rode in his carriage all day, and now we have taken possession of his box.”", "\"네. 오늘 아침엔 그와 함께 아침을 들었고, 종일 그의 마차를 탔으며, 이제는 그의 특별석까지 차지하고 있으니까요.\""]], [["“You know him, then?”", "\"그럼 그를 아세요?\""]], [["“Yes, and no.” “How so?”", "\"알기도 하고, 모르기도 합니다.\""]], [["“It is a long story.”", "\"그건 긴 이야기입니다.\""]], [["“Tell it to me.”", "\"들려주세요.\""]], [["“It would frighten you too much.”", "\"너무 놀라실 텐데요.\""]], [["“So much the more reason.”", "\"그러니 더욱 듣고 싶군요.\""]], [["“At least wait until the story has a conclusion.”", "\"적어도 이야기가 결말이 날 때까지는 기다리시지요.\""]], [["“Very well; I prefer complete histories; but tell me how you made his acquaintance?", "\"좋아요. 저는 완결된 이야기를 더 좋아하니까요. 하지만 그와 어떻게 알게 되셨는지는 말씀해 주세요."], ["Did anyone introduce you to him?”", "누가 그를 소개해 주던가요?\""]], [["“No; it was he who introduced himself to us.”", "\"아니요. 그가 먼저 우리에게 자신을 소개했습니다.\""]], [["“When?”", "\"언제요?\""]], [["“Last night, after we left you.”", "\"어젯밤, 우리가 부인 곁을 떠난 뒤에요.\""]], [["“Through what medium?”", "\"무슨 인연으로요?\""]], [["“The very prosaic one of our landlord.”", "\"우리 호텔 주인이라는 더없이 평범한 매개를 통해서지요.\""]], [["“He is staying, then, at the Hôtel de Londres with you?”", "\"그럼 그가 당신들과 같은 런던 호텔에 묵고 있나요?\""]], [["“Not only in the same hotel, but on the same floor.”", "\"같은 호텔일 뿐 아니라, 같은 층에 묵고 있습니다.\""]], [["“What is his name; for, of course, you know?”", "\"이름이 뭐죠? 물론 아실 테죠?\""]], [["“The Count of Monte Cristo.”", "\"몬테크리스토 백작입니다.\""]], [["“That is not a family name?”", "\"가문 이름은 아니겠지요?\""]], [["“No, it is the name of the island he has purchased.”", "\"네. 그가 사들인 섬의 이름입니다.\""]], [["“And he is a count?”", "\"그래서 백작이라고요?\""]], [["“A Tuscan count.”", "\"토스카나의 백작이지요.\""]], [["“Well, we must put up with that,”", "\"음, 그건 그러려니 해야겠네요.\""], ["said the countess, who was herself from one of the oldest Venetian families.", "백작 부인이 말했다. 그녀 자신이 베네치아의 가장 오래된 가문 출신이었다."], ["“What sort of a man is he?”", "\"어떤 사람인가요?\""]], [["“Ask the Vicomte de Morcerf.”", "\"모르세르 자작에게 물어보시지요.\""]], [["“You hear, M. de Morcerf, I am referred to you,”", "\"들으셨죠, 모르세르 씨, 저를 당신에게 넘기시는군요.\""], ["said the countess.", "백작 부인이 말했다."]], [["“We should be very hard to please, madam,” returned Albert, “did we not think him delightful.", "\"그를 유쾌한 사람이라 여기지 않는다면 우리가 어지간히 까다로운 사람일 겁니다, 부인.\" 알베르가 대꾸했다."], ["A friend of ten years’ standing could not have done more for us, or with a more perfect courtesy.”", "\"십 년 지기라도 그보다 더, 또 그보다 더 완벽한 예의로 우리에게 잘해 주지는 못했을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Come,”", "\"이런.\""], ["observed the countess, smiling, “I see my vampire is only some millionaire, who has taken the appearance of Lara in order to avoid being confounded with M.", "백작 부인이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"제 흡혈귀가 알고 보니 그저 어느 백만장자였나 보군요. 로스차일드 씨와 혼동되지 않으려고 라라의 모습을 빌린 거겠지요."], ["de Rothschild; and you have seen her?”", "그래, 그녀는 보셨나요?\""]], [["“Her?” 20183m", "\"그녀라뇨?\""]], [["“The beautiful Greek of yesterday.”", "\"어제 그 아름다운 그리스 여인 말이에요.\""]], [["“No; we heard, I think, the sound of her _guzla_, but she remained perfectly invisible.”", "\"아니요. 그녀의 구즐라 소리는 들은 것 같은데, 정작 그녀는 끝내 모습을 보이지 않았습니다.\""]], [["“When you say invisible,”", "\"보이지 않았다고 하는 건,\""], ["interrupted Albert, “it is only to keep up the mystery; for whom do you take the blue domino at the window with the white curtains?”", "알베르가 끼어들었다. \"그저 신비를 지키려는 말일 뿐입니다. 흰 커튼이 드리운 그 창가의 푸른 도미노를 부인은 누구라고 여기시는지요?\""]], [["“Where was this window with white hangings?”", "\"흰 휘장을 두른 그 창문이 어디 있었지요?\""], ["asked the countess.", "백작 부인이 물었다."]], [["“At the Rospoli Palace.”", "\"로스폴리 궁전에요.\""]], [["“The count had three windows at the Rospoli Palace?”", "\"백작이 로스폴리 궁전에 창문을 세 개나 가지고 있었어요?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["Did you pass through the Corso?”", "코르소를 지나오셨습니까?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Well, did you notice two windows hung with yellow damask, and one with white damask with a red cross?", "\"그럼 노란 다마스크 천을 두른 창문 둘과, 붉은 십자가가 그려진 흰색 다마스크 천을 두른 창문 하나를 보셨겠군요?"], ["Those were the count’s windows.”", "그게 백작의 창문이었습니다.\""]], [["“Why, he must be a nabob. Do you know what those three windows were worth?”", "\"아니, 그 사람은 부호임에 틀림없군요. 그 창문 세 개가 얼마짜리인지 아세요?\""]], [["“Two or three hundred Roman crowns?”", "\"로마 크라운으로 이삼백 정도?\""]], [["“Two or three thousand.”", "\"이삼천이에요.\""]], [["“The deuce!”", "\"맙소사!\""]], [["“Does his island produce him such a revenue?”", "\"그의 섬이 그에게 그런 수입을 안겨 주나요?\""]], [["“It does not bring him a bajocco.”", "\"한 푼도 안 가져다줍니다.\""]], [["“Then why did he purchase it?”", "\"그럼 왜 그 섬을 샀대요?\""]], [["“For a whim.”", "\"변덕으로요.\""]], [["“He is an original, then?”", "\"그럼 별난 사람이군요?\""]], [["“In reality,”", "\"사실,\""], ["observed Albert, “he seemed to me somewhat eccentric; were he at Paris, and a frequenter of the theatres, I should say he was a poor devil literally mad.", "알베르가 한마디 했다. \"제가 보기에도 좀 별난 데가 있더군요. 만약 그가 파리에 살면서 극장을 드나드는 사람이라면, 말 그대로 정신 나간 가엾은 작자라고 했을 겁니다."], ["This morning he made two or three exits worthy of Didier or Anthony.”", "오늘 아침에도 디디에나 안토니에게나 어울릴 법한 퇴장을 두세 번이나 해 보였거든요.\""]], [["At this moment a fresh visitor entered, and, according to custom, Franz gave up his seat to him.", "이때 새로운 손님이 들어왔고, 관례에 따라 프란츠는 그에게 자리를 내주었다."], ["This circumstance had, moreover, the effect of changing the conversation; an hour afterwards the two friends returned to their hotel.", "게다가 이 일은 화제를 바꾸는 효과까지 가져왔다. 한 시간 뒤 두 친구는 호텔로 돌아왔다."]], [["Signor Pastrini had already set about procuring their disguises for the morrow; and he assured them that they would be perfectly satisfied.", "파스트리니 씨는 이미 이튿날 쓸 변장 의상을 마련하느라 분주했다. 그는 두 사람이 완전히 만족할 거라고 장담했다."], ["The next morning, at nine o’clock, he entered Franz’s room, followed by a tailor, who had eight or ten Roman peasant costumes on his arm; they selected two exactly alike, and charged the tailor to sew on each of their hats about twenty yards of ribbon, and to procure them two of the long silk sashes of different colors with which the lower orders decorate themselves on fête days.", "이튿날 아침 아홉 시, 그는 로마 농부 의상을 여덟아홉 벌쯤 팔에 걸친 재단사를 데리고 프란츠의 방으로 들어왔다. 두 사람은 똑같은 것으로 두 벌을 골랐다. 그러고는 재단사에게 각자의 모자에 리본을 스무 야드쯤 달고, 서민들이 축제 때 몸을 치장하는 색색의 긴 비단 띠를 두 개 구해 오라고 일렀다."]], [["Albert was impatient to see how he looked in his new dress—a jacket and breeches of blue velvet, silk stockings with clocks, shoes with buckles, and a silk waistcoat.", "알베르는 새 옷을 입은 자기 모습이 어떨지 보고 싶어 안달이었다. 푸른 벨벳 재킷과 반바지, 무늬가 짜인 비단 양말, 버클 달린 구두, 그리고 비단 조끼."], ["This picturesque attire set him off to great advantage; and when he had bound the scarf around his waist, and when his hat, placed coquettishly on one side, let fall on his shoulder a stream of ribbons, Franz was forced to confess that costume has much to do with the physical superiority we accord to certain nations.", "이 그림 같은 차림새는 그를 한층 돋보이게 했다. 허리에 띠를 두르고, 모자를 멋스럽게 한쪽으로 비스듬히 얹어 어깨로 리본 자락을 흘러내리게 하자, 프란츠는 인정하지 않을 수 없었다. 우리가 어떤 민족에게 부여하는 외양의 우월함에 의상이 큰 몫을 한다는 사실을 말이다."], ["The Turks used to be so picturesque with their long and flowing robes, but are they not now hideous with their blue frocks buttoned up to the chin, and their red caps, which make them look like a bottle of wine with a red seal?", "터키 사람들은 길고 펄럭이는 옷을 입었을 때 그토록 그림 같았는데, 지금은 턱까지 단추를 채운 푸른 윗도리에 붉은 모자를 쓰고 있으니, 붉은 봉인이 찍힌 포도주 병처럼 보여 흉하지 않은가?"], ["Franz complimented Albert, who looked at himself in the glass with an unequivocal smile of satisfaction.", "프란츠가 알베르를 칭찬하자, 알베르는 만족스러운 미소를 숨김없이 띠며 거울에 비친 자기 모습을 들여다보았다."], ["They were thus engaged when the Count of Monte Cristo entered. 20185m", "그들이 그러고 있을 때 몬테크리스토 백작이 들어왔다."]], [["“Gentlemen,”", "\"여러분.\""], ["said he, “although a companion is agreeable, perfect freedom is sometimes still more agreeable.", "그가 말했다. \"동행이 즐거운 일이긴 하나, 완전한 자유가 때로는 그보다 더 즐거운 법이지요."], ["I come to say that today, and for the remainder of the Carnival, I leave the carriage entirely at your disposal.", "오늘부터 사육제가 끝날 때까지 마차를 온전히 두 분께 맡기겠다는 말을 하러 왔습니다."], ["The host will tell you I have three or four more, so that you will not inconvenience me in any way.", "주인이 말해 주겠지만, 제게는 마차가 서너 대 더 있으니 조금도 제게 폐가 되지 않습니다."], ["Make use of it, I pray you, for your pleasure or your business.”", "부디 즐기시든 일을 보시든 마음껏 쓰십시오.\""]], [["The young men wished to decline, but they could find no good reason for refusing an offer which was so agreeable to them.", "두 젊은이는 사양하려 했지만, 그토록 흡족한 제안을 거절할 그럴듯한 이유를 찾지 못했다."], ["The Count of Monte Cristo remained a quarter of an hour with them, conversing on all subjects with the greatest ease.", "몬테크리스토 백작은 그들과 십오 분쯤 머물며 온갖 주제에 대해 더없이 편안하게 이야기를 나누었다."], ["He was, as we have already said, perfectly well acquainted with the literature of all countries. A glance at the walls of his salon proved to Franz and Albert that he was a connoisseur of pictures.", "앞서 말했듯 그는 모든 나라의 문학에 통달해 있었다. 그의 살롱 벽을 한번 둘러보는 것만으로도, 프란츠와 알베르는 그가 그림 감식가라는 것을 알 수 있었다."], ["A few words he let fall showed them that he was no stranger to the sciences, and he seemed much occupied with chemistry.", "그가 무심코 던지는 몇 마디에서, 그들은 그가 과학에도 문외한이 아니며, 특히 화학에 깊이 빠져 있는 듯하다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["The two friends did not venture to return the count the breakfast he had given them; it would have been too absurd to offer him in exchange for his excellent table the very inferior one of Signor Pastrini.", "두 친구는 백작이 베푼 아침 식사를 답례로 대접할 엄두를 내지 못했다. 그의 훌륭한 식탁에 견주어 파스트리니 씨의 형편없는 식탁을 내놓는 것은 너무도 우스운 일이었을 테니까."], ["They told him so frankly, and he received their excuses with the air of a man who appreciated their delicacy.", "그들은 그 사정을 솔직히 털어놓았고, 백작은 그들의 섬세함을 헤아리는 사람다운 태도로 그 변명을 받아들였다."], ["Albert was charmed with the count’s manners, and he was only prevented from recognizing him for a perfect gentleman by reason of his varied knowledge.", "알베르는 백작의 태도에 매료되었다. 그가 백작을 완벽한 신사로 인정하는 데 걸림돌이 된 것이 있다면, 오직 그의 다채로운 지식뿐이었다."]], [["The permission to do what he liked with the carriage pleased him above all, for the fair peasants had appeared in a most elegant carriage the preceding evening, and Albert was not sorry to be upon an equal footing with them.", "마차를 마음대로 써도 좋다는 허락이 무엇보다 그를 기쁘게 했다. 전날 저녁 그 어여쁜 농부 아가씨들이 더없이 우아한 마차를 타고 나타났던 터라, 알베르는 그들과 대등한 처지가 되는 것이 싫지 않았다."], ["At half-past one they descended, the coachman and footman had put on their livery over their disguises, which gave them a more ridiculous appearance than ever, and which gained them the applause of Franz and Albert.", "한 시 반에 그들은 아래로 내려갔다. 마부와 하인은 변장 위에 정복을 걸치고 있어, 그 어느 때보다 우스꽝스러워 보였고, 프란츠와 알베르의 갈채를 받았다."], ["Albert had fastened the faded bunch of violets to his button-hole.", "알베르는 시든 제비꽃 다발을 단춧구멍에 꽂았다."], ["At the first sound of the bell they hastened into the Corso by the Via Vittoria.", "종이 처음 울리자 그들은 비토리아 거리를 통해 코르소로 서둘러 나아갔다."]], [["At the second turn, a bunch of fresh violets, thrown from a carriage filled with harlequins, indicated to Albert that, like himself and his friend, the peasants had changed their costume also; and whether it was the result of chance, or whether a similar feeling had possessed them both, while he had donned their costume, they had assumed his.", "두 번째 모퉁이에서, 아를레키노들로 가득 찬 마차에서 던져진 싱싱한 제비꽃 다발이 알베르에게 알려 주었다. 자신과 친구처럼, 그 농부 아가씨들도 의상을 바꿔 입었다는 것을. 우연의 결과였는지, 아니면 두 사람 다 같은 마음에 사로잡힌 탓이었는지, 그가 그들의 의상을 걸치는 동안 그들은 그의 의상을 걸친 것이었다."]], [["Albert placed the fresh bouquet in his button-hole, but he kept the faded one in his hand; and when he again met the calash, he raised it to his lips, an action which seemed greatly to amuse not only the fair lady who had thrown it, but her joyous companions also.", "알베르는 싱싱한 꽃다발을 단춧구멍에 꽂았지만, 시든 것은 손에 쥐고 있었다. 그리고 그 사륜마차를 다시 만났을 때, 그것을 입술로 가져갔다. 그 행동은 꽃을 던진 어여쁜 여인뿐 아니라 그녀의 흥겨운 동행들까지 몹시 즐겁게 한 듯했다."], ["The day was as gay as the preceding one, perhaps even more animated and noisy; the count appeared for an instant at his window, but when they again passed he had disappeared.", "그날은 전날 못지않게 흥겨웠고, 어쩌면 더 활기차고 떠들썩했다. 백작이 잠깐 창가에 모습을 보였지만, 그들이 다시 지나갈 때는 사라지고 없었다."], ["It is almost needless to say that the flirtation between Albert and the fair peasant continued all day.", "알베르와 그 어여쁜 농부 아가씨 사이의 시시덕거림이 온종일 이어졌다는 것은 굳이 말할 필요도 없으리라."]], [["In the evening, on his return, Franz found a letter from the embassy, informing him that he would have the honor of being received by his holiness the next day.", "저녁에 돌아오니, 프란츠는 대사관에서 온 편지 한 통을 발견했다. 이튿날 교황 성하를 알현하는 영광을 누리게 되리라는 내용이었다."], ["At each previous visit he had made to Rome, he had solicited and obtained the same favor; and incited as much by a religious feeling as by gratitude, he was unwilling to quit the capital of the Christian world without laying his respectful homage at the feet of one of St.", "그는 로마를 찾을 때마다 매번 같은 은혜를 청하고 또 누려 왔다. 종교적 감정에서나 감사하는 마음에서나, 그는 온갖 미덕의 보기 드문 본보기를 보여 준 성 베드로의 후계자 한 사람의 발치에 정중한 경의를 바치지 않고서는 그리스도교 세계의 수도를 떠나고 싶지 않았다."], ["Peter’s successors who has set the rare example of all the virtues. He did not then think of the Carnival, for in spite of his condescension and touching kindness, one cannot incline one’s self without awe before the venerable and noble old man called Gregory XVI.", "그러자 사육제 생각은 사라졌다. 그레고리우스 16세라 불리는 그 존엄하고 고결한 노인은, 그 겸허함과 가슴 뭉클한 자애로움에도 불구하고, 누구든 그 앞에서 경외감 없이는 고개를 숙일 수 없는 분이었기 때문이다."]], [["On his return from the Vatican, Franz carefully avoided the Corso; he brought away with him a treasure of pious thoughts, to which the mad gayety of the maskers would have been profanation.", "바티칸에서 돌아오는 길에, 프란츠는 코르소를 일부러 피했다. 그는 경건한 상념의 보물을 가슴에 품고 나왔다. 가면 쓴 자들의 광적인 흥겨움은 그 상념을 모독하는 일이 되었을 테니까."]], [["At ten minutes past five Albert entered overjoyed.", "다섯 시 십 분에 알베르가 몹시 기뻐하며 들어왔다."], ["The harlequin had reassumed her peasant’s costume, and as she passed she raised her mask.", "그 아를레키노가 다시 농부 의상을 입었고, 곁을 지날 때 가면을 들어 올렸다는 것이다."], ["She was charming.", "그녀는 매혹적이었다."], ["Franz congratulated Albert, who received his congratulations with the air of a man conscious that they are merited.", "프란츠가 알베르를 축하했고, 알베르는 마땅히 받을 만한 축하라는 것을 아는 사람다운 태도로 그것을 받았다."], ["He had recognized by certain unmistakable signs, that his fair _incognita_ belonged to the aristocracy.", "그는 몇 가지 틀림없는 징표로, 그 어여쁜 미지의 여인이 귀족 계급에 속한다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["He had made up his mind to write to her the next day.", "그는 이튿날 그녀에게 편지를 쓰기로 마음먹고 있었다."]], [["Franz remarked, while he gave these details, that Albert seemed to have something to ask of him, but that he was unwilling to ask it.", "이 자초지종을 늘어놓는 동안, 프란츠는 알베르가 자기에게 뭔가 청하고 싶어 하면서도 차마 청하지 못하는 듯하다는 것을 눈치챘다."], ["He insisted upon it, declaring beforehand that he was willing to make any sacrifice the other wished.", "그는 어떤 희생이든 알베르가 바라는 대로 치를 용의가 있다고 미리 못 박으며 채근했다."]], [["Albert let himself be pressed just as long as friendship required, and then avowed to Franz that he would do him a great favor by allowing him to occupy the carriage alone the next day.", "알베르는 우정이 요구하는 만큼만 채근을 받다가, 이튿날 마차를 혼자 쓰게 해 준다면 큰 호의를 베푸는 셈이 되겠다고 프란츠에게 털어놓았다."], ["Albert attributed to Franz’s absence the extreme kindness of the fair peasant in raising her mask.", "알베르는 그 어여쁜 농부 아가씨가 가면을 들어 올린 그 각별한 호의를, 프란츠가 자리에 없었던 덕으로 돌렸다."], ["Franz was not sufficiently egotistical to stop Albert in the middle of an adventure that promised to prove so agreeable to his curiosity and so flattering to his vanity.", "프란츠는 친구의 호기심을 그토록 흡족하게 채워 주고 허영심을 그토록 우쭐하게 만들어 줄 모험을 한가운데서 가로막을 만큼 이기적인 사람이 아니었다."], ["He felt assured that the perfect indiscretion of his friend would duly inform him of all that happened; and as, during three years that he had travelled all over Italy, a similar piece of good fortune had never fallen to his share, Franz was by no means sorry to learn how to act on such an occasion.", "그는 친구의 빈틈없는 입싼 성미가 일어난 일을 죄다 일러 주리라 확신했다. 게다가 그는 이탈리아 전역을 떠돈 삼 년 동안 그런 종류의 행운을 단 한 번도 누려 본 적이 없었으니, 그런 상황에서 어떻게 처신하는지 알게 되는 것이 결코 싫지 않았다."], ["He therefore promised Albert that he would content himself the morrow with witnessing the Carnival from the windows of the Rospoli Palace.", "그리하여 그는 이튿날 로스폴리 궁전의 창문에서 사육제를 구경하는 것으로 만족하겠다고 알베르에게 약속했다."]], [["The next morning he saw Albert pass and repass, holding an enormous bouquet, which he doubtless meant to make the bearer of his amorous epistle.", "이튿날 아침, 그는 알베르가 거대한 꽃다발을 들고 오가는 것을 보았다. 그것은 분명 그의 연애편지를 전할 매개로 삼으려는 것이었다."], ["This belief was changed into certainty when Franz saw the bouquet (conspicuous by a circle of white camellias) in the hand of a charming harlequin dressed in rose-colored satin.", "이 짐작은 확신으로 바뀌었다. 프란츠가 그 꽃다발을, 흰 동백꽃 테두리로 눈에 띄던 그것을, 장밋빛 새틴을 차려입은 매혹적인 아를레키노의 손에서 보았을 때 말이다."]], [["The evening was no longer joy, but delirium.", "저녁은 더 이상 흥겨움이 아니라 도취였다."], ["Albert nothing doubted but that the fair unknown would reply in the same manner.", "알베르는 그 어여쁜 미지의 여인이 같은 방식으로 답하리라는 것을 조금도 의심하지 않았다."], ["Franz anticipated his wishes by saying that the noise fatigued him, and that he should pass the next day in writing and looking over his journal.", "프란츠는 소란이 자기를 피곤하게 하니 이튿날은 글을 쓰고 일기를 들여다보며 보내겠다고 말하며 그의 바람을 앞질러 주었다. 알베르는 속지 않았다."], ["Albert was not deceived, for the next evening Franz saw him enter triumphantly shaking a folded paper which he held by one corner.", "이튿날 저녁, 프란츠는 그가 접힌 종이 한 장을 한쪽 귀퉁이로 쥐고 흔들며 의기양양하게 들어오는 것을 보았으니까."]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래.\""], ["said he, “was I mistaken?”", "그가 말했다. \"내 짐작이 틀렸던가?\""]], [["“She has answered you!”", "\"그녀가 답을 보냈군!\""], ["cried Franz. “Read.”", "프란츠가 외쳤다."]], [["This word was pronounced in a manner impossible to describe.", "\"읽어 보게.\" 이 말은 뭐라 형용할 수 없는 어조로 내뱉어졌다."], ["Franz took the letter, and read:", "프란츠는 편지를 받아 들고 읽었다."]], [["“Tuesday evening, at seven o’clock, descend from your carriage opposite the Via dei Pontefici, and follow the Roman peasant who snatches your torch from you.", "\"화요일 저녁 일곱 시, 폰테피치 거리 맞은편에서 마차를 내려, 당신의 횃불을 낚아채는 로마 농부 여인을 따라오세요."], ["When you arrive at the first step of the church of San Giacomo, be sure to fasten a knot of rose-colored ribbons to the shoulder of your harlequin costume, in order that you may be recognized. Until then you will not see me.", "산 자코모 성당의 첫 계단에 이르거든, 알아볼 수 있도록 당신의 아를레키노 의상 어깨에 장밋빛 리본 매듭을 꼭 매어 두세요. 그때까지 당신은 저를 볼 수 없을 거예요."], ["—Constancy and Discretion.”", "─한결같음과 신중함.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래.\""], ["asked he, when Franz had finished, “what do you think of that?”", "프란츠가 다 읽자 그가 물었다. \"어떻게 생각하나?\""]], [["“I think that the adventure is assuming a very agreeable appearance.”", "\"이 모험이 아주 흡족한 모양새를 갖춰 가는 것 같군.\""]], [["“I think so, also,”", "\"나도 그렇게 생각해.\""], ["replied Albert; “and I very much fear you will go alone to the Duke of Bracciano’s ball.”", "알베르가 대답했다. \"그리고 자네가 브라차노 공작의 무도회에 혼자 가게 될까 봐 무척 걱정스럽네.\""]], [["Franz and Albert had received that morning an invitation from the celebrated Roman banker.", "그날 아침 프란츠와 알베르는 그 유명한 로마 은행가에게서 초대를 받았다."]], [["“Take care, Albert,”", "\"조심하게, 알베르.\""], ["said Franz. “All the nobility of Rome will be present, and if your fair _incognita_ belong to the higher class of society, she must go there.”", "프란츠가 말했다. \"로마의 귀족이란 귀족은 다 참석할 텐데, 자네의 그 어여쁜 미지의 여인이 상류 사회에 속한다면 그녀도 거기 가야 할 테니까.\""]], [["“Whether she goes there or not, my opinion is still the same,” returned Albert.", "\"그녀가 가든 안 가든, 내 생각은 변함없네.\" 알베르가 대꾸했다."], ["“You have read the letter?”", "\"그 편지를 읽었지?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"읽었네.\""]], [["“You know how imperfectly the women of the _mezzo cito_ are educated in Italy?”", "\"이탈리아에서 메초 치토 여인들이 얼마나 형편없는 교육을 받는지 아나?\""], ["(This is the name of the lower class.)", "(이것은 하층 계급을 일컫는 이름이다.)"]], [["“Yes.”", "\"알지.\""]], [["“Well, read the letter again.", "\"그럼 그 편지를 다시 읽어 보게."], ["Look at the writing, and find if you can, any blemish in the language or orthography.”", "필체를 보고, 할 수 있다면 그 말투나 철자에서 흠을 하나라도 찾아보게.\""], ["The writing was, in reality, charming, and the orthography irreproachable.", "필체는 정말로 매혹적이었고, 철자는 나무랄 데가 없었다."]], [["“You are born to good fortune,”", "\"자네는 행운을 타고났군.\""], ["said Franz, as he returned the letter.", "프란츠가 편지를 돌려주며 말했다."]], [["“Laugh as much as you will,”", "\"실컷 비웃게.\" 알베르가 대꾸했다."], ["replied Albert, “I am in love.”", "\"나는 사랑에 빠졌으니까.\""]], [["“You alarm me,” cried Franz.", "\"자네 때문에 겁이 나는군.\""], ["“I see that I shall not only go alone to the Duke of Bracciano’s, but also return to Florence alone.”", "프란츠가 외쳤다. \"내가 브라차노 공작의 무도회에 혼자 갈 뿐 아니라, 피렌체로도 혼자 돌아가게 생겼어.\""]], [["“If my unknown be as amiable as she is beautiful,”", "\"내 미지의 여인이 아름다운 만큼 상냥하기까지 하다면,\""], ["said Albert, “I shall fix myself at Rome for six weeks, at least.", "알베르가 말했다. \"나는 적어도 여섯 주는 로마에 눌러앉을 거야."], ["I adore Rome, and I have always had a great taste for archæology.” 20189m", "나는 로마를 흠모하고, 늘 고고학에 큰 취미가 있었으니까.\""]], [["“Come, two or three more such adventures, and I do not despair of seeing you a member of the Academy.”", "\"이런, 그런 모험을 두세 번만 더 하면, 자네가 학사원 회원이 되는 것도 보겠는걸.\""]], [["Doubtless Albert was about to discuss seriously his right to the academic chair when they were informed that dinner was ready.", "알베르가 학사원 의자에 앉을 자기 자격을 진지하게 논하려 들 참인데, 저녁 식사가 준비되었다는 전갈이 왔다."], ["Albert’s love had not taken away his appetite.", "알베르의 사랑도 그의 식욕을 앗아 가지는 못했다."], ["He hastened with Franz to seat himself, free to recommence the discussion after dinner.", "그는 프란츠와 함께 서둘러 자리에 앉았다. 식후에 논의를 다시 이어 가면 될 일이었다."], ["After dinner, the Count of Monte Cristo was announced.", "식사를 마치자 몬테크리스토 백작이 왔다는 전갈이 왔다."], ["They had not seen him for two days.", "그들은 이틀 동안 그를 보지 못한 터였다."], ["Signor Pastrini informed them that business had called him to Civita Vecchia.", "파스트리니 씨는 일 때문에 그가 치비타베키아에 다녀왔다고 알려 주었다."], ["He had started the previous evening, and had only returned an hour since.", "그는 전날 저녁에 떠났다가 한 시간 전에야 막 돌아온 것이었다."], ["He was charming.", "그는 매력적이었다."], ["Whether he kept a watch over himself, or whether by accident he did not sound the acrimonious chords that in other circumstances had been touched, he was tonight like everybody else.", "스스로를 단속한 것인지, 아니면 우연히 다른 상황에서 건드렸던 그 신랄한 가락을 울리지 않은 것인지, 그날 밤 그는 여느 사람과 다를 바 없었다."]], [["The man was an enigma to Franz.", "이 사내는 프란츠에게 수수께끼였다."], ["The count must feel sure that Franz recognized him; and yet he had not let fall a single word indicating any previous acquaintance between them.", "백작은 프란츠가 자신을 알아보았다는 것을 분명 확신하고 있을 텐데, 두 사람 사이에 전부터 안면이 있었음을 내비치는 말은 단 한마디도 흘리지 않았다."], ["On his side, however great Franz’s desire was to allude to their former interview, the fear of being disagreeable to the man who had loaded him and his friend with kindness prevented him from mentioning it.", "한편 프란츠로서는 지난번 만남을 넌지시 언급하고 싶은 마음이 아무리 컸어도, 자신과 친구에게 호의를 잔뜩 베푼 사람의 비위를 거스를까 두려워 그것을 입에 올리지 못했다."]], [["The count had learned that the two friends had sent to secure a box at the Argentina Theatre, and were told they were all let.", "백작은 두 친구가 아르헨티나 극장의 특별석을 잡으려고 사람을 보냈으나 모두 동이 났다는 답을 들었다는 사실을 알게 되었다."], ["In consequence, he brought them the key of his own—at least such was the apparent motive of his visit.", "그래서 그는 자기 특별석 열쇠를 그들에게 가져왔다. 적어도 그것이 그가 찾아온 표면상의 이유였다."], ["Franz and Albert made some difficulty, alleging their fear of depriving him of it; but the count replied that, as he was going to the Palli Theatre, the box at the Argentina Theatre would be lost if they did not profit by it.", "프란츠와 알베르는 그에게서 특별석을 빼앗을까 염려된다며 다소 난색을 표했다. 하지만 백작은 자신은 팔리 극장에 갈 참이라, 두 사람이 쓰지 않으면 아르헨티나 극장의 특별석은 헛것이 될 거라고 대답했다."], ["This assurance determined the two friends to accept it.", "이 말에 두 친구는 그것을 받아들이기로 마음을 정했다."]], [["Franz had by degrees become accustomed to the count’s pallor, which had so forcibly struck him at their first meeting.", "프란츠는 백작의 창백함에 차츰 익숙해져 있었다. 처음 만났을 때 그토록 강렬하게 그를 사로잡았던 그 창백함 말이다."], ["He could not refrain from admiring the severe beauty of his features, the only defect, or rather the principal quality of which was the pallor. Truly, a Byronic hero!", "그는 백작 이목구비의 준엄한 아름다움을 감탄하지 않을 수 없었는데, 그 유일한 결점, 아니 차라리 주된 특질이 바로 그 창백함이었다. 정말이지 바이런풍의 영웅이었다!"], ["Franz could not, we will not say see him, but even think of him without imagining his stern head upon Manfred’s shoulders, or beneath Lara’s helmet.", "프란츠는 그를 본다고는 차마 못 하겠으나, 그를 떠올리는 것만으로도 그 준엄한 머리가 만프레드의 어깨 위에 얹히거나 라라의 투구 아래 놓인 모습을 상상하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["His forehead was marked with the line that indicates the constant presence of bitter thoughts; he had the fiery eyes that seem to penetrate to the very soul, and the haughty and disdainful upper lip that gives to the words it utters a peculiar character that impresses them on the minds of those to whom they are addressed.", "그의 이마에는 쓰디쓴 상념이 끊임없이 자리하고 있음을 알려 주는 선이 새겨져 있었다. 영혼의 밑바닥까지 꿰뚫는 듯한 불타는 눈, 그리고 거기서 나오는 말에 독특한 성격을 부여해 듣는 이의 마음에 그 말을 아로새기는, 오만하고 경멸 어린 윗입술을 그는 지니고 있었다."]], [["The count was no longer young.", "백작은 더 이상 젊지 않았다."], ["He was at least forty; and yet it was easy to understand that he was formed to rule the young men with whom he associated at present.", "적어도 마흔은 되었다. 그런데도 그가 지금 함께 어울리는 젊은이들을 다스리도록 타고난 사람이라는 것은 쉽게 알 수 있었다."], ["And, to complete his resemblance with the fantastic heroes of the English poet, the count seemed to have the power of fascination.", "그리고 그 영국 시인의 환상적인 영웅들과의 닮음을 완성하기라도 하듯, 백작은 사람을 매혹하는 힘을 지닌 듯했다."], ["Albert was constantly expatiating on their good fortune in meeting such a man.", "알베르는 이런 사람을 만난 행운에 대해 끊임없이 떠들어 댔다."], ["Franz was less enthusiastic; but the count exercised over him also the ascendency a strong mind always acquires over a mind less domineering.", "프란츠는 그만큼 열광적이지는 않았다. 하지만 강한 정신이 덜 위압적인 정신에 대해 늘 얻게 마련인 그 우위를, 백작은 그에게도 행사하고 있었다."], ["He thought several times of the project the count had of visiting Paris; and he had no doubt but that, with his eccentric character, his characteristic face, and his colossal fortune, he would produce a great effect there.", "그는 백작이 파리를 방문하려는 계획을 여러 차례 떠올렸다. 그리고 백작의 별난 성격과 독특한 얼굴, 막대한 재산이라면 거기서 큰 반향을 일으키리라는 것을 의심치 않았다."], ["And yet he did not wish to be at Paris when the count was there.", "그러면서도 그는 백작이 파리에 있을 때 자신은 파리에 있고 싶지 않았다."]], [["The evening passed as evenings mostly pass at Italian theatres; that is, not in listening to the music, but in paying visits and conversing.", "저녁은 이탈리아 극장에서 저녁이 으레 흘러가는 대로 흘러갔다. 다시 말해, 음악을 듣는 것이 아니라 인사를 다니고 담소를 나누는 것으로 말이다."], ["The Countess G—— wished to revive the subject of the count, but Franz announced he had something far newer to tell her, and, in spite of Albert’s demonstrations of false modesty, he informed the countess of the great event which had preoccupied them for the last three days.", "G 백작 부인이 백작 이야기를 다시 꺼내고 싶어 했지만, 프란츠는 훨씬 더 새로운 이야깃거리가 있다고 알렸다. 그리고 알베르가 거짓 겸손을 부리는 것에도 아랑곳없이, 지난 사흘간 두 사람의 마음을 사로잡았던 그 큰 사건을 백작 부인에게 들려주었다."], ["As similar intrigues are not uncommon in Italy, if we may credit travellers, the comtess did not manifest the least incredulity, but congratulated Albert on his success.", "여행자들의 말을 믿는다면 이런 밀회는 이탈리아에서 드문 일이 아니었으므로, 백작 부인은 조금도 미심쩍어하는 기색 없이 알베르의 성공을 축하해 주었다."], ["They promised, upon separating, to meet at the Duke of Bracciano’s ball, to which all Rome was invited.", "헤어지면서 그들은 온 로마가 초대받은 브라차노 공작의 무도회에서 만나기로 약속했다."]], [["The heroine of the bouquet kept her word; she gave Albert no sign of her existence the morrow or the day after.", "꽃다발의 여주인공은 약속을 지켰다. 이튿날도, 그다음 날도 그녀는 알베르에게 존재의 기척조차 보이지 않았다."]], [["At length Tuesday came, the last and most tumultuous day of the Carnival.", "마침내 화요일이 왔다. 사육제의 마지막이자 가장 떠들썩한 날이었다."], ["On Tuesday, the theatres open at ten o’clock in the morning, as Lent begins after eight at night.", "화요일이면 극장은 아침 열 시에 문을 연다. 사순절이 밤 여덟 시 이후에 시작되기 때문이다."], ["On Tuesday, all those who through want of money, time, or enthusiasm, have not been to see the Carnival before, mingle in the gayety, and contribute to the noise and excitement.", "화요일이면, 돈이 없어서든 시간이 없어서든 흥이 나지 않아서든 그전에 사육제를 보지 못한 사람들이 모두 그 흥겨움 속에 뒤섞여 소란과 열기에 한몫을 보탠다."], ["From two o’clock till five Franz and Albert followed in the _fête_, exchanging handfuls of _confetti_ with the other carriages and the pedestrians, who crowded amongst the horses’ feet and the carriage wheels without a single accident, a single dispute, or a single fight.", "두 시부터 다섯 시까지 프란츠와 알베르는 축제를 따라다니며, 다른 마차들과 보행자들과 콘페티를 한 줌씩 주고받았다. 보행자들은 말발굽 사이와 마차 바퀴 틈으로 몰려들었지만, 사고도, 다툼도, 싸움도 단 한 건 없었다."]], [["The _fêtes_ are veritable pleasure days to the Italians.", "축제는 이탈리아 사람들에게 진정한 즐거움의 날이다."], ["The author of this history, who has resided five or six years in Italy, does not recollect to have ever seen a ceremony interrupted by one of those events so common in other countries.", "이탈리아에서 대여섯 해를 지낸 이 이야기의 글쓴이는, 다른 나라에서는 그토록 흔한 그런 사건으로 행사가 중단되는 것을 단 한 번도 본 기억이 없다."], ["Albert was triumphant in his harlequin costume.", "알베르는 아를레키노 의상을 입고 의기양양했다."], ["A knot of rose-colored ribbons fell from his shoulder almost to the ground.", "장밋빛 리본 매듭이 그의 어깨에서 거의 땅에 닿을 듯이 늘어져 있었다."], ["In order that there might be no confusion, Franz wore his peasant’s costume.", "혼동이 없도록 프란츠는 농부 의상을 입었다."]], [["As the day advanced, the tumult became greater.", "날이 깊어 갈수록 소란은 더해 갔다."], ["There was not on the pavement, in the carriages, at the windows, a single tongue that was silent, a single arm that did not move.", "포도에서도, 마차에서도, 창문에서도 침묵하는 혀가 하나도 없었고, 움직이지 않는 팔이 하나도 없었다."], ["It was a human storm, made up of a thunder of cries, and a hail of sweetmeats, flowers, eggs, oranges, and nosegays.", "그것은 함성의 천둥과, 사탕과 꽃과 달걀과 오렌지와 꽃다발의 우박으로 이루어진 인간의 폭풍우였다."]], [["At three o’clock the sound of fireworks, let off on the Piazza del Popolo and the Piazza di Venezia (heard with difficulty amid the din and confusion) announced that the races were about to begin.", "세 시에, 포폴로 광장과 베네치아 광장에서 터진 불꽃놀이 소리가 (그 소란과 혼란 속에서는 알아듣기 어려웠지만) 경마가 곧 시작되리라는 것을 알렸다."]], [["The races, like the _moccoli_, are one of the episodes peculiar to the last days of the Carnival.", "경마는, 모콜리와 마찬가지로, 사육제 마지막 날들에 특유한 행사 중 하나다."], ["At the sound of the fireworks the carriages instantly broke ranks, and retired by the adjacent streets.", "불꽃놀이 소리가 나자 마차들은 즉시 대열을 풀고 인근 골목으로 물러났다."], ["All these evolutions are executed with an inconceivable address and marvellous rapidity, without the police interfering in the matter.", "이 모든 동작은 경찰이 끼어들 것도 없이, 상상할 수 없을 만큼 능란하고 놀랍도록 신속하게 이루어진다."], ["The pedestrians ranged themselves against the walls; then the trampling of horses and the clashing of steel were heard.", "보행자들은 담벼락에 바싹 붙어 늘어섰다. 그러자 말발굽 소리와 쇠붙이 부딪치는 소리가 들려왔다."], ["A detachment of carbineers, fifteen abreast, galloped up the Corso in order to clear it for the _barberi_. When the detachment arrived at the Piazza di Venezia, a second volley of fireworks was discharged, to announce that the street was clear.", "카르비니에리 한 무리가 열다섯씩 나란히, 바르베리가 달릴 길을 터 주려고 코르소를 질주해 올라갔다. 그 무리가 베네치아 광장에 이르자, 길이 비었음을 알리는 두 번째 불꽃놀이가 터졌다."]], [["Almost instantly, in the midst of a tremendous and general outcry, seven or eight horses, excited by the shouts of three hundred thousand spectators, passed by like lightning.", "거의 곧바로, 엄청난 함성이 사방에서 터지는 가운데, 삼십만 구경꾼의 고함에 흥분한 말 일고여덟 마리가 번개처럼 지나갔다."], ["Then the Castle of Saint Angelo fired three cannon to indicate that number three had won.", "그러자 산탄젤로 성에서 대포 세 발을 쏘아, 삼번 말이 이겼음을 알렸다."]], [["Immediately, without any other signal, the carriages moved on, flowing on towards the Corso, down all the streets, like torrents pent up for a while, which again flow into the parent river; and the immense stream again continued its course between its two granite banks.", "곧바로, 다른 신호도 없이 마차들이 다시 움직이며 코르소를 향해, 모든 골목을 따라 흘러나왔다. 한동안 갇혀 있다가 다시 본류로 흘러드는 급류처럼 말이다. 그리고 그 거대한 물줄기는 두 화강암 둑 사이로 다시 제 길을 이어 갔다."]], [["A new source of noise and movement was added to the crowd.", "새로운 소음과 움직임의 원천이 군중에 더해졌다."], ["The sellers of _moccoletti_ entered on the scene.", "모콜레티 장수들이 무대에 등장한 것이다."], ["The _moccoli_, or _moccoletti_, are candles which vary in size from the pascal taper to the rushlight, and which give to each actor in the great final scene of the Carnival two very serious problems to grapple with,—first, how to keep his own _moccoletto_ alight; and secondly, how to extinguish the _moccoletti_ of others.", "모콜리, 또는 모콜레티는 부활절 초부터 골풀 심지 양초까지 크기가 제각각인 양초로, 사육제 대단원의 위대한 마지막 장면에 나서는 모든 이에게 두 가지 심각한 과제를 던진다. 첫째는 자기 모콜레토의 불을 어떻게 꺼지지 않게 지키느냐, 둘째는 남들의 모콜레토를 어떻게 끄느냐다."], ["The _moccoletto_ is like life: man has found but one means of transmitting it, and that one comes from God.", "모콜레토는 생명과 같다. 인간은 그것을 전하는 방법을 단 하나만 찾아냈고, 그 하나는 신에게서 나온다."], ["But he has discovered a thousand means of taking it away, and the devil has somewhat aided him.", "하지만 그것을 앗아 가는 방법은 천 가지나 발견해 냈으며, 거기엔 악마도 얼마간 거들었다."], ["The _moccoletto_ is kindled by approaching it to a light.", "모콜레토는 불꽃에 가까이 대어 켠다."], ["But who can describe the thousand means of extinguishing the _moccoletto_?—the gigantic bellows, the monstrous extinguishers, the superhuman fans.", "하지만 모콜레토를 끄는 천 가지 방법을 누가 다 묘사할 수 있으랴? 거대한 풀무, 괴물 같은 소화기, 초인적인 부채들."], ["Everyone hastened to purchase _moccoletti_—Franz and Albert among the rest.", "모두가 다투어 모콜레티를 사들였다. 프란츠와 알베르도 그중에 끼었다."]], [["The night was rapidly approaching; and already, at the cry of “_Moccoletti_!”", "밤이 빠르게 다가오고 있었다. 천 명의 장수들이 째지는 목소리로 거듭 외쳐 대는 \"모콜레티!\""], ["repeated by the shrill voices of a thousand vendors, two or three stars began to burn among the crowd.", "소리에 맞춰, 어느새 군중 사이로 두세 개의 별이 타오르기 시작했다."], ["It was a signal.", "그것은 신호였다."], ["At the end of ten minutes fifty thousand lights glittered, descending from the Palazzo di Venezia to the Piazza del Popolo, and mounting from the Piazza del Popolo to the Palazzo di Venezia.", "십 분이 지나자 오만 개의 불빛이 반짝이며, 베네치아 궁전에서 포폴로 광장으로 내려가고, 포폴로 광장에서 베네치아 궁전으로 올라갔다."], ["It seemed like the _fête_ of Jack-o’-lanterns.", "마치 도깨비불의 축제 같았다."]], [["It is impossible to form any idea of it without having seen it.", "직접 보지 않고서는 그것을 머릿속에 그려 볼 수 없다."], ["Suppose that all the stars had descended from the sky and mingled in a wild dance on the face of the earth; the whole accompanied by cries that were never heard in any other part of the world.", "모든 별이 하늘에서 내려와 지상에서 광란의 춤을 추며 뒤섞이는 광경을 상상해 보라. 세상 그 어느 곳에서도 들어 본 적 없는 함성이 그 모든 것에 곁들여진다."], ["The _facchino_ follows the prince, the Transteverin the citizen, everyone blowing, extinguishing, relighting.", "짐꾼이 귀족을 쫓고, 트라스테베레 사람이 시민을 쫓으며, 저마다 불을 불어 끄고 다시 붙인다."], ["Had old Æolus appeared at this moment, he would have been proclaimed king of the _moccoli_, and Aquilo the heir-presumptive to the throne.", "만약 이 순간 늙은 아이올로스가 나타났더라면, 그는 모콜리의 왕으로 추대되었을 테고, 아퀼로는 왕위 계승자가 되었으리라."]], [["This battle of folly and flame continued for two hours; the Corso was light as day; the features of the spectators on the third and fourth stories were visible.", "이 광기와 불꽃의 싸움은 두 시간이나 이어졌다. 코르소는 대낮처럼 환했다. 삼층, 사층에 있는 구경꾼들의 이목구비까지 보일 정도였다."]], [["Every five minutes Albert took out his watch; at length it pointed to seven.", "알베르는 오 분마다 시계를 꺼내 보았다. 마침내 시곗바늘이 일곱 시를 가리켰다."], ["The two friends were in the Via dei Pontefici.", "두 친구는 폰테피치 거리에 있었다."], ["Albert sprang out, bearing his _moccoletto_ in his hand.", "알베르가 모콜레토를 손에 쥐고 마차에서 뛰어내렸다."], ["Two or three masks strove to knock his _moccoletto_ out of his hand; but Albert, a first-rate pugilist, sent them rolling in the street, one after the other, and continued his course towards the church of San Giacomo.", "가면 쓴 자 두셋이 그의 모콜레토를 손에서 쳐 내려고 덤볐지만, 일류 권투 선수인 알베르는 그들을 하나씩 차례로 길바닥에 나뒹굴게 하고는 산 자코모 성당을 향해 가던 길을 계속 갔다."]], [["The steps were crowded with masks, who strove to snatch each other’s torches.", "계단은 서로의 횃불을 낚아채려는 가면 쓴 자들로 빼곡했다."], ["Franz followed Albert with his eyes, and saw him mount the first step.", "프란츠는 알베르를 눈으로 좇으며, 그가 첫 계단을 오르는 것을 보았다."]], [["Instantly a mask, wearing the well-known costume of a peasant woman, snatched his _moccoletto_ from him without his offering any resistance.", "곧바로, 농부 여인의 그 낯익은 의상을 입은 가면 하나가 알베르의 모콜레토를 낚아챘다. 알베르는 아무런 저항도 하지 않았다."], ["Franz was too far off to hear what they said; but, without doubt, nothing hostile passed, for he saw Albert disappear arm-in-arm with the peasant girl.", "프란츠는 너무 멀리 있어 그들이 무슨 말을 하는지 들을 수 없었다. 하지만 적의가 오간 일은 분명 없었으니, 알베르가 그 농부 아가씨와 팔짱을 끼고 사라지는 것을 보았기 때문이다."], ["He watched them pass through the crowd for some time, but at length he lost sight of them in the Via Macello.", "그는 두 사람이 군중 사이를 헤치고 지나가는 것을 한동안 지켜보았지만, 결국 마첼로 거리에서 그들을 시야에서 놓치고 말았다."]], [["Suddenly the bell that gives the signal for the end of the Carnival sounded, and at the same instant all the _moccoletti_ were extinguished as if by enchantment.", "갑자기 사육제의 끝을 알리는 종이 울렸고, 그와 동시에 마치 마법에라도 걸린 듯 모든 모콜레티가 꺼졌다."], ["It seemed as though one immense blast of the wind had extinguished everyone.", "거대한 한 줄기 바람이 불어 와 모든 불을 꺼 버린 것만 같았다."]], [["Franz found himself in utter darkness.", "프란츠는 칠흑 같은 어둠 속에 홀로 남았다."], ["No sound was audible save that of the carriages that were carrying the maskers home; nothing was visible save a few lights that burnt behind the windows.", "가면 쓴 자들을 집으로 실어 나르는 마차 소리 말고는 아무 소리도 들리지 않았다. 창문 너머로 타오르는 몇 점의 불빛 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않았다."]], [["The Carnival was over.", "사육제는 끝났다."]], [["# Chapter 37. The Catacombs of Saint Sebastian", "# Chapter 37. The Catacombs of Saint Sebastian"]], [["20193m\nIn his whole life, perhaps, Franz had never before experienced so sudden an impression, so rapid a transition from gayety to sadness, as in this moment.", "그날 그 순간만큼, 어쩌면 프란츠는 평생 그토록 갑작스러운 인상을, 들뜬 기분에서 슬픔으로의 그토록 빠른 전환을 느껴 본 적이 없었다."], ["It seemed as though Rome, under the magic breath of some demon of the night, had suddenly changed into a vast tomb.", "마치 로마가 어떤 밤의 악마가 마법의 입김을 불어넣기라도 한 듯, 별안간 거대한 무덤으로 변해 버린 것 같았다."], ["By a chance, which added yet more to the intensity of the darkness, the moon, which was on the wane, did not rise until eleven o’clock, and the streets which the young man traversed were plunged in the deepest obscurity.", "어둠을 한층 더 짙게 만든 우연이 겹쳤다. 이지러져 가던 달은 열한 시가 되어서야 떠올랐고, 그래서 젊은이가 지나는 거리는 칠흑 같은 어둠 속에 잠겨 있었다."]], [["The distance was short, and at the end of ten minutes his carriage, or rather the count’s, stopped before the Hôtel de Londres.", "거리는 짧았다. 십 분쯤 지나자 그의 마차, 아니 정확히는 백작의 마차가 런던 호텔 앞에 멈춰 섰다."]], [["Dinner was waiting, but as Albert had told him that he should not return so soon, Franz sat down without him. Signor Pastrini, who had been accustomed to see them dine together, inquired into the cause of his absence, but Franz merely replied that Albert had received on the previous evening an invitation which he had accepted.", "저녁상이 차려져 있었지만, 알베르가 그렇게 일찍 돌아오지 않을 거라고 말해 두었던 터라 프란츠는 혼자 식탁에 앉았다. 두 사람이 늘 함께 식사하는 데 익숙했던 파스트리니가 알베르가 없는 까닭을 물었으나, 프란츠는 그저 알베르가 전날 저녁 받은 초대에 응했다고만 대답했다."]], [["The sudden extinction of the _moccoletti_, the darkness which had replaced the light, and the silence which had succeeded the turmoil, had left in Franz’s mind a certain depression which was not free from uneasiness.", "모콜레티가 별안간 꺼지고, 빛을 대신해 어둠이 깔리고, 소란을 이어 정적이 들어선 일은 프란츠의 마음에 어떤 침울함을 남겼는데, 그 침울함에는 불안이 섞여 있었다."], ["He therefore dined very silently, in spite of the officious attention of his host, who presented himself two or three times to inquire if he wanted anything.", "그래서 그는 무엇이 필요하지 않으냐고 두세 번이나 와서 묻는 주인의 살뜰한 시중에도 불구하고, 아주 말없이 식사를 마쳤다. * 모콜레티: 로마 사육제 마지막 날 밤, 사람들이 들고 다니던 작은 양초.—옮긴이"]], [["Franz resolved to wait for Albert as late as possible.", "프란츠는 알베르를 되도록 늦게까지 기다려 보기로 했다."], ["He ordered the carriage, therefore, for eleven o’clock, desiring Signor Pastrini to inform him the moment that Albert returned to the hotel.", "그래서 마차를 열한 시에 대기시키도록 일러 두고, 알베르가 호텔에 돌아오는 즉시 알려 달라고 파스트리니에게 부탁했다."]], [["At eleven o’clock Albert had not come back.", "열한 시가 되어도 알베르는 돌아오지 않았다."], ["Franz dressed himself, and went out, telling his host that he was going to pass the night at the Duke of Bracciano’s.", "프란츠는 옷을 갖춰 입고 나서며, 브라차노 공작 댁에서 밤을 보내겠다고 주인에게 일러 두었다."], ["The house of the Duke of Bracciano is one of the most delightful in Rome, the duchess, one of the last heiresses of the Colonnas, does its honors with the most consummate grace, and thus their _fêtes_ have a European celebrity.", "브라차노 공작의 저택은 로마에서 가장 멋진 집 가운데 하나였고, 콜론나 가문의 마지막 상속녀 가운데 한 사람인 공작 부인이 더없이 세련된 품위로 손님을 맞았으니, 그 댁의 연회는 유럽 전역에 이름이 높았다."]], [["Franz and Albert had brought to Rome letters of introduction to them, and their first question on his arrival was to inquire the whereabouts of his travelling companion.", "프란츠와 알베르는 그 댁에 보낼 소개장을 들고 로마에 왔던 터라, 프란츠가 도착하자 사람들이 가장 먼저 물은 것은 그의 여행 동무가 어디에 있느냐는 것이었다."], ["Franz replied that he had left him at the moment they were about to extinguish the _moccoli_, and that he had lost sight of him in the Via Macello.", "프란츠는 모콜리를 끄려던 순간에 그와 헤어졌고, 마첼로 거리에서 그를 놓쳐 버렸다고 대답했다."]], [["“Then he has not returned?”", "\"그래, 아직 돌아오지 않았단 말이오?\""], ["said the duke.", "공작이 물었다."]], [["“I waited for him until this hour,”", "\"이 시각까지 그를 기다렸습니다.\""], ["replied Franz.", "프란츠가 대답했다."]], [["“And do you know whither he went?”", "\"그가 어디로 갔는지는 아시오?\""]], [["“No, not precisely; however, I think it was something very like a rendezvous.”", "\"아니요, 정확히는 모릅니다. 다만 밀회 같은 것이 아니었나 싶습니다.\""]], [["“_Diavolo!_”", "\"이런!\""], ["said the duke, “this is a bad day, or rather a bad night, to be out late; is it not, countess?”", "공작이 말했다. \"오늘은, 아니 오늘 밤은 늦게까지 밖에 나돌기에 나쁜 날이지요. 안 그렇소, 백작 부인?\""]], [["These words were addressed to the Countess G——, who had just arrived, and was leaning on the arm of Signor Torlonia, the duke’s brother.", "이 말은 막 도착해 공작의 동생인 토를로니아의 팔에 기대고 있던 G 백작 부인을 향한 것이었다."]], [["“I think, on the contrary, that it is a charming night,”", "\"제 생각엔 오히려 더없이 아름다운 밤이에요.\""], ["replied the countess, “and those who are here will complain of but one thing, that of its too rapid flight.”", "백작 부인이 대답했다. \"여기 모인 분들이 불평할 거라곤 단 한 가지, 이 밤이 너무 빨리 지나간다는 것뿐일 거예요.\""]], [["“I am not speaking,”", "\"제 말은,\" 공작이 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["said the duke with a smile, “of the persons who are here; the men run no other danger than that of falling in love with you, and the women of falling ill of jealousy at seeing you so lovely; I meant persons who were out in the streets of Rome.”", "\"여기 계신 분들 이야기가 아니랍니다. 남자들이야 부인께 반하는 위험밖에 없고, 여자들이야 그토록 어여쁜 부인을 보며 질투로 병이 날 위험밖에 없지요. 제가 말한 건 로마 거리에 나가 있는 사람들 얘깁니다.\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["asked the countess, “who is out in the streets of Rome at this hour, unless it be to go to a ball?”", "백작 부인이 물었다. \"이 시각에 무도회에 가는 게 아니라면, 대체 누가 로마 거리에 나가 있겠어요?\""]], [["“Our friend, Albert de Morcerf, countess, whom I left in pursuit of his unknown about seven o’clock this evening,”", "\"우리 친구 알베르 드 모르세르입니다, 부인.\" 프란츠가 말했다."], ["said Franz, “and whom I have not seen since.”", "\"오늘 저녁 일곱 시쯤 정체 모를 여인을 쫓아가는 걸 보고는, 그 뒤로 보지 못했지요.\""]], [["“And don’t you know where he is?”", "\"그가 어디 있는지 모르세요?\""]], [["“Not at all.”", "\"전혀 모릅니다.\""]], [["“Is he armed?”", "\"무기는 지녔나요?\""]], [["“He is in masquerade.”", "\"가장무도회 차림입니다.\""]], [["“You should not have allowed him to go,”", "\"그를 보내지 말았어야 했소.\""], ["said the duke to Franz; “you, who know Rome better than he does.”", "공작이 프란츠에게 말했다. \"그대는 그 친구보다 로마를 더 잘 알지 않소.\""]], [["“You might as well have tried to stop number three of the _barberi_, who gained the prize in the race today,”", "\"오늘 경주에서 우승한 바르베리 삼 번 말을 멈춰 세우려는 거나 매한가지였을 겁니다.\" 프란츠가 대답했다."], ["replied Franz; “and then moreover, what could happen to him?”", "\"게다가 그에게 무슨 일이 일어나기야 하겠습니까?\""]], [["“Who can tell?", "\"누가 알겠소?"], ["The night is gloomy, and the Tiber is very near the Via Macello.”", "밤은 음침하고, 티베르강은 마첼로 거리에서 아주 가깝지.\""], ["Franz felt a shudder run through his veins at observing that the feeling of the duke and the countess was so much in unison with his own personal disquietude.", "공작과 백작 부인의 심정이 자신의 불안과 그토록 한 가닥으로 맞물리는 것을 보며, 프란츠는 핏줄을 타고 전율이 스치는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“I informed them at the hotel that I had the honor of passing the night here, duke,”", "\"호텔에는 제가 오늘 밤 여기서 묵는 영광을 누린다고 일러 두었습니다, 공작님.\""], ["said Franz, “and desired them to come and inform me of his return.”", "프란츠가 말했다. \"그가 돌아오면 이리로 와서 알려 달라고도 했고요.\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["replied the duke, “here I think, is one of my servants who is seeking you.”", "공작이 대답했다. \"마침 저기, 그대를 찾는 제 하인 하나가 오는 것 같소.\""]], [["The duke was not mistaken; when he saw Franz, the servant came up to him.", "공작의 짐작은 틀리지 않았다. 하인은 프란츠를 보자 곧장 그에게 다가왔다."]], [["“Your excellency,”", "\"각하,\""], ["he said, “the master of the Hôtel de Londres has sent to let you know that a man is waiting for you with a letter from the Viscount of Morcerf.”", "그가 말했다. \"런던 호텔 주인이 사람을 보내, 모르세르 자작님의 편지를 든 사람이 각하를 기다리고 있다고 알려 왔습니다.\""]], [["“A letter from the viscount!” exclaimed Franz.", "\"자작의 편지라니!\" 프란츠가 외쳤다."]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“And who is the man?”", "\"그 사람은 누구냐?\""]], [["“I do not know.”", "\"모르겠습니다.\""]], [["“Why did he not bring it to me here?”", "\"왜 그 편지를 여기로 가져오지 않았느냐?\""]], [["“The messenger did not say.”", "\"심부름꾼은 그 까닭을 말하지 않았습니다.\""]], [["“And where is the messenger?”", "\"그 심부름꾼은 어디 있느냐?\""]], [["“He went away directly he saw me enter the ball-room to find you.”", "\"제가 각하를 찾으러 무도회장에 들어서는 걸 보더니 곧장 가 버렸습니다.\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오,\""], ["said the countess to Franz, “go with all speed—poor young man!", "백작 부인이 프란츠에게 말했다. \"어서 서둘러 가 보세요."], ["Perhaps some accident has happened to him.”", "가엾은 젊은이! 무슨 사고라도 났는지 몰라요.\""]], [["“I will hasten,”", "\"서두르겠습니다.\""], ["replied Franz.", "프란츠가 대답했다."]], [["“Shall we see you again to give us any information?”", "\"무슨 일인지 알려 주러 다시 와 주시겠어요?\""], ["inquired the countess.", "백작 부인이 물었다."]], [["“Yes, if it is not any serious affair, otherwise I cannot answer as to what I may do myself.”", "\"네, 큰일이 아니라면요. 큰일이라면, 제가 어떻게 될지 장담할 수 없습니다.\""]], [["“Be prudent, in any event,”", "\"어쨌든 조심하세요.\""], ["said the countess.", "백작 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Oh! pray be assured of that.”", "\"오! 그 점은 안심하십시오.\""]], [["Franz took his hat and went away in haste.", "프란츠는 모자를 집어 들고 부랴부랴 떠났다."], ["He had sent away his carriage with orders for it to fetch him at two o’clock; fortunately the Palazzo Bracciano, which is on one side in the Corso, and on the other in the Square of the Holy Apostles, is hardly ten minutes’ walk from the Hôtel de Londres.", "마차는 두 시에 데리러 오라는 분부와 함께 돌려보낸 터였다. 다행히 한쪽은 코르소 거리에, 다른 한쪽은 거룩한 사도 광장에 면한 브라차노 궁은 런던 호텔에서 걸어서 십 분도 채 걸리지 않았다."]], [["As he came near the hotel, Franz saw a man in the middle of the street. He had no doubt that it was the messenger from Albert.", "호텔 가까이 이르렀을 때, 프란츠는 길 한복판에 서 있는 한 사내를 보았다. 알베르가 보낸 심부름꾼이 틀림없었다."], ["The man was wrapped up in a large cloak.", "사내는 커다란 망토로 몸을 휘감고 있었다."], ["He went up to him, but, to his extreme astonishment, the stranger first addressed him.", "프란츠가 다가가자, 놀랍게도 낯선 사내가 먼저 말을 걸어왔다."]], [["“What wants your excellency of me?” inquired the man, retreating a step or two, as if to keep on his guard.", "\"각하께서는 제게 무슨 볼일이 있으십니까?\" 사내가 경계하듯 한두 걸음 물러서며 물었다."]], [["“Are not you the person who brought me a letter,”", "\"모르세르 자작에게서,\" 프란츠가 물었다."], ["inquired Franz, “from the Viscount of Morcerf?”", "\"내게 편지를 가져온 사람이 그대 아니오?\""]], [["“Your excellency lodges at Pastrini’s hotel?”", "\"각하께서는 파스트리니 호텔에 묵고 계십니까?\""]], [["“I do.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["“Your excellency is the travelling companion of the viscount?”", "\"각하께서는 자작님의 여행 동무이십니까?\""]], [["“I am.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["“Your excellency’s name——”", "\"각하의 성함은……\""]], [["“Is the Baron Franz d’Épinay.”", "\"프란츠 데피네 남작이오.\""]], [["20199m\n“Then it is to your excellency that this letter is addressed.”", "\"그렇다면 이 편지는 각하께 보내는 것이군요.\""]], [["“Is there any answer?”", "\"답이 필요하오?\""], ["inquired Franz, taking the letter from him.", "프란츠가 편지를 받아 들며 물었다."]], [["“Yes—your friend at least hopes so.”", "\"네. 적어도 각하의 친구분은 그러길 바라십니다.\""]], [["“Come upstairs with me, and I will give it to you.”", "\"나와 함께 위층으로 올라갑시다. 거기서 답을 주겠소.\""]], [["“I prefer waiting here,” said the messenger, with a smile.", "\"저는 여기서 기다리는 편이 좋습니다.\" 심부름꾼이 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“And why?”", "\"어째서요?\""]], [["“Your excellency will know when you have read the letter.”", "\"편지를 읽으시면 각하께서도 아시게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["“Shall I find you here, then?”", "\"그럼 그대를 여기서 찾을 수 있겠소?\""]], [["“Certainly.”", "\"물론입니다.\""]], [["Franz entered the hotel.", "프란츠는 호텔로 들어섰다."], ["On the staircase he met Signor Pastrini. “Well?”", "계단에서 파스트리니와 마주쳤다. \"어떻습니까?\""], ["said the landlord.", "주인이 물었다."]], [["“Well—what?” responded Franz.", "\"어떻다니, 무슨 말이오?\" 프란츠가 되물었다."]], [["“You have seen the man who desired to speak with you from your friend?” he asked of Franz.", "\"친구분이 보내서 각하를 뵙겠다던 그 사람을 만나셨습니까?\" 그가 프란츠에게 물었다."]], [["“Yes, I have seen him,”", "\"그렇소, 만났소.\""], ["he replied, “and he has handed this letter to me.", "그가 대답했다. \"그리고 이 편지를 내게 건넸지."], ["Light the candles in my apartment, if you please.”", "부탁이니 내 방에 촛불을 좀 켜 주시오.\""]], [["The innkeeper gave orders to a servant to go before Franz with a light.", "여관 주인은 하인에게 불을 들고 프란츠 앞에 서라고 일렀다."], ["The young man had found Signor Pastrini looking very much alarmed, and this had only made him the more anxious to read Albert’s letter; and so he went instantly towards the waxlight, and unfolded it.", "젊은이는 파스트리니가 몹시 겁먹은 얼굴을 하고 있는 것을 보았고, 그 때문에 알베르의 편지를 읽고 싶은 마음이 더욱 다급해졌다. 그래서 그는 곧장 촛불 쪽으로 다가가 편지를 펼쳤다."], ["It was written and signed by Albert.", "알베르가 쓰고 서명한 것이었다."], ["Franz read it twice before he could comprehend what it contained. It was thus worded:", "프란츠는 그 내용을 알아차리기까지 두 번을 읽어야 했다. 편지에는 이렇게 적혀 있었다."]], [["“My dear Fellow,", "\"친애하는 벗에게,"]], [["“The moment you have received this, have the kindness to take the letter of credit from my pocket-book, which you will find in the square drawer of the _secrétaire_; add your own to it, if it be not sufficient.", "이 편지를 받는 즉시, 부디 내 수첩에서 신용장을 꺼내 주게. 그건 책상의 네모난 서랍 안에 있을 거야. 그것으로 부족하면 자네 것도 보태 주고."], ["Run to Torlonia, draw from him instantly four thousand piastres, and give them to the bearer.", "토를로니아에게 달려가서 당장 사천 피아스트르를 받아, 이 편지를 가져간 사람에게 건네주게."], ["It is urgent that I should have this money without delay.", "지체 없이 이 돈을 마련해야 하네. 더는 말하지 않겠네."], ["I do not say more, relying on you as you may rely on me.", "내가 자네를 믿듯, 자네도 나를 믿어 주기를 바라며."]], [["“Your friend,", "자네의 벗,"]], [["“Albert de Morcerf.", "알베르 드 모르세르."]], [["“P.S.—I now believe in Italian _banditti_.”", "추신—이제 나는 이탈리아 산적의 존재를 믿네.\""]], [["Below these lines were written, in a strange hand, the following in Italian:", "이 글 아래로, 낯선 필체로 이탈리아어가 다음과 같이 적혀 있었다."]], [["“_Se alle sei della mattina le quattro mille piastre non sono nelle mie mani, alla sette il Conte Alberto avrà cessato di vivere_.", "\"세 알레 세이 델라 마티나 레 콰트로 밀레 피아스트레 논 소노 넬레 미에 마니, 알라 세테 일 콘테 알베르토 아브라 체사토 디 비베레."]], [["“Luigi Vampa.”", "루이지 밤파.\""]], [["“_If by six in the morning the four thousand piastres are not in my hands, by seven o’clock the Count Albert will have ceased to live_.”", "\"내일 아침 여섯 시까지 사천 피아스트르가 내 손에 들어오지 않으면, 일곱 시에 알베르 백작은 목숨을 잃게 될 것이다.\""]], [["This second signature explained everything to Franz, who now understood the objection of the messenger to coming up into the apartment; the street was safer for him.", "이 두 번째 서명은 프란츠에게 모든 것을 설명해 주었다. 그는 이제 심부름꾼이 방으로 올라오기를 거부한 까닭을 알아차렸다. 그에게는 거리가 더 안전했던 것이다."], ["Albert, then, had fallen into the hands of the famous bandit chief, in whose existence he had for so long a time refused to believe.", "그러니까 알베르는 그 이름난 산적 두목의 손아귀에 떨어진 것이었다. 그 두목의 존재를 그토록 오래 믿으려 들지 않았던 알베르가 말이다."]], [["There was no time to lose.", "지체할 시간이 없었다."], ["He hastened to open the _secrétaire_, and found the pocket-book in the drawer, and in it the letter of credit.", "그는 서둘러 책상을 열고 서랍 안에서 수첩을 찾았으며, 그 속에서 신용장을 발견했다."], ["There were in all six thousand piastres, but of these six thousand Albert had already expended three thousand.", "모두 합쳐 육천 피아스트르였으나, 그 육천 가운데 알베르가 이미 삼천을 써 버린 터였다."]], [["As to Franz, he had no letter of credit, as he lived at Florence, and had only come to Rome to pass seven or eight days; he had brought but a hundred louis, and of these he had not more than fifty left.", "프란츠로 말하자면, 그는 신용장이 없었다. 피렌체에 살고 있었고 로마에는 이레나 여드레쯤 머물 작정으로 왔기에, 가져온 것이라곤 백 루이뿐이었다. 그 가운데 남은 것은 오십 루이도 채 되지 않았다."], ["Thus seven or eight hundred piastres were wanting to them both to make up the sum that Albert required.", "그러니 알베르가 요구한 금액을 채우려면 두 사람 몫을 합쳐도 칠팔백 피아스트르가 모자랐다."], ["True, he might in such a case rely on the kindness of Signor Torlonia.", "물론 그런 경우라면 토를로니아의 호의에 기댈 수도 있었다."], ["He was, therefore, about to return to the Palazzo Bracciano without loss of time, when suddenly a luminous idea crossed his mind.", "그래서 그는 한시도 지체하지 않고 브라차노 궁으로 돌아가려던 참이었는데, 문득 머릿속에 한 줄기 빛 같은 생각이 스쳤다."]], [["He remembered the Count of Monte Cristo.", "그는 몬테크리스토 백작을 떠올렸다."], ["Franz was about to ring for Signor Pastrini, when that worthy presented himself.", "프란츠가 파스트리니를 부르려고 종을 울리려는 찰나, 마침 그 친절한 사내가 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["“My dear sir,”", "\"이보시오,\""], ["he said, hastily, “do you know if the count is within?”", "그가 다급히 말했다. \"백작이 안에 계신지 아시오?\""]], [["“Yes, your excellency; he has this moment returned.”", "\"네, 각하. 방금 막 돌아오셨습니다.\""]], [["“Is he in bed?”", "\"잠자리에 드셨소?\""]], [["“I should say no.”", "\"아닐 겁니다.\""]], [["“Then ring at his door, if you please, and request him to be so kind as to give me an audience.”", "\"그럼 부탁이니 그분 방에 가서 종을 울리고, 부디 나를 좀 만나 주십사 청해 주시오.\""]], [["Signor Pastrini did as he was desired, and returning five minutes after, he said:", "파스트리니는 시키는 대로 했고, 오 분 뒤에 돌아와 말했다."]], [["“The count awaits your excellency.”", "\"백작께서 각하를 기다리고 계십니다.\""]], [["Franz went along the corridor, and a servant introduced him to the count.", "프란츠는 복도를 따라갔고, 하인이 그를 백작에게 안내했다."], ["He was in a small room which Franz had not yet seen, and which was surrounded with divans.", "백작은 프란츠가 아직 본 적 없는 작은 방에 있었는데, 그 방은 빙 둘러 긴 의자가 놓여 있었다."], ["The count came towards him.", "백작이 그를 향해 다가왔다."]], [["“Well, what good wind blows you hither at this hour?”", "\"자, 이 시각에 무슨 좋은 바람이 불어 이리로 오셨소?\""], ["said he; “have you come to sup with me?", "그가 말했다. \"나와 함께 야식을 드시러 오셨소?"], ["It would be very kind of you.”", "그렇다면 참으로 반가운 일이오.\""]], [["“No; I have come to speak to you of a very serious matter.”", "\"아닙니다. 아주 중대한 일로 말씀드리러 왔습니다.\""]], [["“A serious matter,”", "\"중대한 일이라.\""], ["said the count, looking at Franz with the earnestness usual to him; “and what may it be?”", "백작이 늘 그렇듯 진지한 눈빛으로 프란츠를 바라보며 말했다. \"그게 무엇이오?\""]], [["“Are we alone?”", "\"우리 둘뿐입니까?\""]], [["“Yes,” replied the count, going to the door, and returning.", "\"그렇소.\" 백작이 문가로 가서 확인하고 돌아오며 대답했다."], ["Franz gave him Albert’s letter.", "프란츠는 그에게 알베르의 편지를 건넸다."]], [["“Read that,”", "\"이걸 읽어 보십시오.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["The count read it.", "백작이 편지를 읽었다."]], [["“Well, well!”", "\"이런, 이런!\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Did you see the postscript?”", "\"추신을 보셨습니까?\""]], [["“I did, indeed.", "\"보았소, 분명히."]], [["“_‘Se alle sei della mattina le quattro mille piastre non sono nelle mie mani, alla sette il conte Alberto avrà cessato di vivere. _", "'세 알레 세이 델라 마티나 레 콰트로 밀레 피아스트레 논 소노 넬레 미에 마니, 알라 세테 일 콘테 알베르토 아브라 체사토 디 비베레."]], [["“‘Luigi Vampa.’”", "루이지 밤파.'\""]], [["“What think you of that?”", "\"어떻게 생각하십니까?\""], ["inquired Franz.", "프란츠가 물었다."]], [["“Have you the money he demands?”", "\"그가 요구한 돈은 지니고 계시오?\""]], [["“Yes, all but eight hundred piastres.”", "\"네, 팔백 피아스트르만 빼고 다요.\""]], [["The count went to his _secrétaire_, opened it, and pulling out a drawer filled with gold, said to Franz, “I hope you will not offend me by applying to anyone but myself.”", "백작은 책상으로 가서 그것을 열고, 금화가 가득한 서랍을 꺼내며 프란츠에게 말했다. \"나 말고 다른 사람에게 손을 벌려 나를 서운하게 하지는 않으시겠지요.\""]], [["“You see, on the contrary, I come to you first and instantly,”", "\"보시다시피, 오히려 저는 가장 먼저, 곧장 백작께 온 겁니다.\""], ["replied Franz.", "프란츠가 대답했다."]], [["“And I thank you; have what you will;”", "\"고맙소. 원하는 만큼 가져가시오.\""], ["and he made a sign to Franz to take what he pleased.", "그러고는 마음껏 집으라는 손짓을 했다."]], [["“Is it absolutely necessary, then, to send the money to Luigi Vampa?”", "\"그렇다면 정말로 그 돈을 루이지 밤파에게 보내야 하는 걸까요?\""], ["asked the young man, looking fixedly in his turn at the count.", "젊은이가 이번에는 자기 쪽에서 백작을 뚫어지게 바라보며 물었다."]], [["“Judge for yourself,”", "\"직접 판단해 보시오.\""], ["replied he.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“The postscript is explicit.”", "\"추신은 명백하지 않소.\""]], [["“I think that if you would take the trouble of reflecting, you could find a way of simplifying the negotiation,” said Franz.", "\"백작께서 조금만 곰곰이 생각해 주신다면, 이 협상을 더 간단히 풀 길을 찾으실 수 있을 것 같습니다.\" 프란츠가 말했다."]], [["“How so?” returned the count, with surprise.", "\"어떻게 말이오?\" 백작이 놀라며 되물었다."]], [["“If we were to go together to Luigi Vampa, I am sure he would not refuse you Albert’s freedom.”", "\"우리가 함께 루이지 밤파를 찾아간다면, 그가 백작께 알베르를 풀어 주기를 거절하지는 못할 거라고 확신합니다.\""]], [["“What influence can I possibly have over a bandit?”", "\"내가 산적에게 무슨 영향력을 가질 수 있겠소?\""]], [["“Have you not just rendered him a service that can never be forgotten?”", "\"방금 그에게 결코 잊지 못할 은혜를 베풀지 않으셨습니까?\""]], [["“What is that?”", "\"그게 무엇이오?\""]], [["“Have you not saved Peppino’s life?”", "\"페피노의 목숨을 구해 주시지 않았습니까?\""]], [["“Well, well,”", "\"이런, 이런.\""], ["said the count, “who told you that?”", "백작이 말했다. \"그건 누구한테 들으셨소?\""]], [["“No matter; I know it.”", "\"상관없습니다."], ["The count knit his brows, and remained silent an instant.", "저는 알고 있으니까요.\" 백작은 이마를 찌푸리며 잠시 말이 없었다."]], [["“And if I went to seek Vampa, would you accompany me?”", "\"내가 밤파를 찾아간다면, 그대도 함께 가시겠소?\""]], [["“If my society would not be disagreeable.”", "\"제가 폐가 되지 않는다면요.\""]], [["“Be it so.", "\"그럼 그렇게 합시다."], ["It is a lovely night, and a walk without Rome will do us both good.”", "아름다운 밤이오. 로마 바깥을 걷는 것은 우리 둘 모두에게 좋을 거요.\""]], [["“Shall I take any arms?”", "\"무기를 챙겨야 할까요?\""]], [["“For what purpose?”", "\"무엇 하러요?\""]], [["“Any money?”", "\"돈은요?\""]], [["“It is useless.", "\"필요 없소."], ["Where is the man who brought the letter?”", "편지를 가져온 사람은 어디 있소?\""]], [["“In the street.”", "\"거리에요.\""]], [["“He awaits the answer?”", "\"답을 기다리고 있소?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“I must learn where we are going.", "\"우리가 어디로 가야 할지 알아야겠소."], ["I will summon him hither.”", "그를 이리로 부르겠소.\""]], [["“It is useless; he would not come up.”", "\"소용없습니다. 올라오려 하지 않을 겁니다.\""]], [["“To your apartments, perhaps; but he will not make any difficulty at entering mine.”", "\"그대 방에는 그러겠지요. 하지만 내 방에 드는 것은 마다하지 않을 거요.\""]], [["The count went to the window of the apartment that looked on to the street, and whistled in a peculiar manner.", "백작은 거리로 면한 방의 창가로 가서 독특한 방식으로 휘파람을 불었다."], ["The man in the mantle quitted the wall, and advanced into the middle of the street.", "망토를 두른 사내가 벽에서 떨어져 길 한복판으로 나섰다."], ["“_Salite!_” said the count, in the same tone in which he would have given an order to his servant.", "\"살리테!\" 백작이 마치 하인에게 명령을 내리듯 그런 어조로 말했다."], ["The messenger obeyed without the least hesitation, but rather with alacrity, and, mounting the steps at a bound, entered the hotel; five seconds afterwards he was at the door of the room.", "심부름꾼은 조금도 망설이지 않고, 오히려 잽싸게 따랐다. 단숨에 계단을 뛰어올라 호텔로 들어섰고, 오 초 만에 방문 앞에 이르렀다."]], [["“Ah, it is you, Peppino,”", "\"아, 자네였군, 페피노.\""], ["said the count.", "백작이 말했다."], ["But Peppino, instead of answering, threw himself on his knees, seized the count’s hand, and covered it with kisses. “Ah,”", "그러나 페피노는 대답 대신 무릎을 꿇고 백작의 손을 붙잡아 입맞춤을 퍼부었다. \"아,\" 백작이 말했다."], ["said the count, “you have, then, not forgotten that I saved your life; that is strange, for it is a week ago.” 20203m", "\"그러니까 자네는 내가 목숨을 구해 준 것을 잊지 않았군. 일주일이나 지난 일인데, 거참 신기하구먼.\""]], [["“No, excellency; and never shall I forget it,”", "\"네, 각하. 결코 잊지 않겠습니다.\""], ["returned Peppino, with an accent of profound gratitude.", "페피노가 깊은 감사의 어조로 대답했다."]], [["“Never?", "\"결코라니?"], ["That is a long time; but it is something that you believe so.", "그건 긴 시간이지. 하지만 자네가 그렇게 믿는다는 것만으로도 대단한 일일세."], ["Rise and answer.”", "일어나서 대답해 보게.\""]], [["Peppino glanced anxiously at Franz.", "페피노가 불안한 눈길로 프란츠를 흘끗 보았다."]], [["“Oh, you may speak before his excellency,”", "\"오, 이분 앞에서 말해도 되네.\" 백작이 말했다."], ["said he; “he is one of my friends.", "\"이분은 내 벗 가운데 한 사람이니까."], ["You allow me to give you this title?”", "이런 칭호를 드려도 괜찮겠지요?\""], ["continued the count in French, “it is necessary to excite this man’s confidence.”", "백작은 프랑스어로 말을 이었다. \"이 사람의 신뢰를 끌어내려면 그래야 합니다.\""]], [["“You can speak before me,”", "\"내 앞에서 말해도 좋소.\""], ["said Franz; “I am a friend of the count’s.”", "프란츠가 말했다. \"나는 백작의 친구요.\""]], [["“Good!” returned Peppino.", "\"좋습니다!\" 페피노가 대답했다."], ["“I am ready to answer any questions your excellency may address to me.”", "\"각하께서 무엇을 물으시든 답할 준비가 되어 있습니다.\""]], [["“How did the Viscount Albert fall into Luigi’s hands?”", "\"알베르 자작이 어쩌다 루이지의 손에 떨어졌나?\""]], [["“Excellency, the Frenchman’s carriage passed several times the one in which was Teresa.”", "\"각하, 그 프랑스인의 마차가 테레사가 탄 마차를 여러 번 스쳐 지나갔습니다.\""]], [["“The chief’s mistress?”", "\"두목의 정부 말인가?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["The Frenchman threw her a bouquet; Teresa returned it—all this with the consent of the chief, who was in the carriage.”", "그 프랑스인이 테레사에게 꽃다발을 던졌고, 테레사가 그걸 되던졌지요. 이 모든 게 마차에 타고 있던 두목의 허락 아래 이루어진 일입니다.\""]], [["“What?”", "\"뭐라고?\""], ["cried Franz, “was Luigi Vampa in the carriage with the Roman peasants?”", "프란츠가 외쳤다. \"루이지 밤파가 로마 농부 차림을 한 사람들 틈에 마차에 타고 있었단 말인가?\""]], [["“It was he who drove, disguised as the coachman,”", "\"마부로 변장해 마차를 몬 게 바로 그였습니다.\""], ["replied Peppino.", "페피노가 대답했다."]], [["“Well?” said the count.", "\"그래서?\" 백작이 말했다."]], [["“Well, then, the Frenchman took off his mask; Teresa, with the chief’s consent, did the same.", "\"그러니까, 그 프랑스인이 가면을 벗었고, 테레사도 두목의 허락 아래 똑같이 가면을 벗었습니다."], ["The Frenchman asked for a rendezvous; Teresa gave him one—only, instead of Teresa, it was Beppo who was on the steps of the church of San Giacomo.”", "프랑스인이 밀회를 청했고, 테레사가 약속을 잡아 주었지요. 다만, 산자코모 성당 계단에 있던 건 테레사가 아니라 베포였습니다.\""]], [["“What!”", "\"뭐라고!\""], ["exclaimed Franz, “the peasant girl who snatched his _mocoletto_ from him——”", "프란츠가 외쳤다. \"그 사람한테서 모콜레토를 낚아챈 농촌 처녀가……\""]], [["“Was a lad of fifteen,”", "\"열다섯 살 먹은 사내아이였습니다.\""], ["replied Peppino.", "페피노가 대답했다."], ["“But it was no disgrace to your friend to have been deceived; Beppo has taken in plenty of others.”", "\"하지만 속아 넘어갔다 해서 자작님께 부끄러울 일은 없습니다. 베포한테 넘어간 사람이 어디 한둘이어야 말이지요.\""]], [["“And Beppo led him outside the walls?”", "\"그래서 베포가 그를 성벽 밖으로 데려갔나?\""], ["said the count.", "백작이 말했다."]], [["“Exactly so; a carriage was waiting at the end of the Via Macello.", "\"바로 그렇습니다. 마첼로 거리 끝에 마차 한 대가 기다리고 있었지요."], ["Beppo got in, inviting the Frenchman to follow him, and he did not wait to be asked twice.", "베포가 올라타며 프랑스인에게 따라오라고 했고, 그는 두 번 청할 것도 없이 따랐습니다."], ["He gallantly offered the right-hand seat to Beppo, and sat by him.", "그는 정중하게 오른쪽 자리를 베포에게 내주고 그 옆에 앉았지요."], ["Beppo told him he was going to take him to a villa a league from Rome; the Frenchman assured him he would follow him to the end of the world.", "베포가 로마에서 십 리쯤 떨어진 별장으로 데려가겠다고 하자, 프랑스인은 세상 끝까지라도 따라가겠다고 했습니다."], ["The coachman went up the Via di Ripetta and the Porta San Paolo; and when they were two hundred yards outside, as the Frenchman became somewhat too forward, Beppo put a brace of pistols to his head, the coachman pulled up and did the same.", "마부는 리페타 거리를 거쳐 산파올로 성문으로 올라갔습니다. 그리고 성문 밖으로 이백 미터쯤 나왔을 때, 프랑스인이 좀 지나치게 들이대자 베포가 그의 머리에 권총 두 자루를 들이댔고, 마부도 마차를 세우고 똑같이 했습니다."], ["At the same time, four of the band, who were concealed on the banks of the Almo, surrounded the carriage.", "그와 동시에 알모 강둑에 숨어 있던 패거리 네 명이 마차를 에워쌌지요."], ["The Frenchman made some resistance, and nearly strangled Beppo; but he could not resist five armed men, and was forced to yield.", "프랑스인은 얼마간 저항하며 베포의 목을 거의 졸라 죽일 뻔했지만, 무장한 다섯 명을 당해 낼 수는 없어 굴복할 수밖에 없었습니다."], ["They made him get out, walk along the banks of the river, and then brought him to Teresa and Luigi, who were waiting for him in the catacombs of St. Sebastian.”", "그들은 그를 마차에서 끌어내 강둑을 따라 걷게 한 뒤, 성세바스티아노 지하 묘지에서 기다리던 테레사와 루이지에게 데려갔습니다.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"자,\""], ["said the count, turning towards Franz, “it seems to me that this is a very likely story.", "백작이 프란츠를 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"내 보기엔 아주 그럴듯한 이야기 같소."], ["What do you say to it?”", "그대는 어떻게 생각하시오?\""]], [["“Why, that I should think it very amusing,” replied Franz, “if it had happened to anyone but poor Albert.”", "\"글쎄요, 가엾은 알베르가 아닌 다른 사람에게 일어난 일이었다면 무척 재미있었을 거라고 생각합니다.\" 프란츠가 대답했다."]], [["“And, in truth, if you had not found me here,”", "\"사실, 그대가 여기서 나를 만나지 못했더라면,\" 백작이 말했다."], ["said the count, “it might have proved a gallant adventure which would have cost your friend dear; but now, be assured, his alarm will be the only serious consequence.”", "\"그건 그대 친구에게 비싼 대가를 치르게 할 호기로운 모험이 되었을지도 모르오. 하지만 이제 안심하시오. 그가 겪을 유일한 심각한 후유증이라곤 놀란 가슴뿐일 테니.\""]], [["“And shall we go and find him?”", "\"그럼 그를 찾으러 가는 겁니까?\""], ["inquired Franz.", "프란츠가 물었다."]], [["“Oh, decidedly, sir.", "\"오, 물론이오."], ["He is in a very picturesque place—do you know the catacombs of St. Sebastian?”", "그는 아주 그림 같은 곳에 있소. 그대는 성세바스티아노 지하 묘지를 아시오?\""]], [["“I was never in them; but I have often resolved to visit them.”", "\"들어가 본 적은 없습니다만, 가 보리라 마음먹은 적은 여러 번 있었지요.\""]], [["“Well, here is an opportunity made to your hand, and it would be difficult to contrive a better.", "\"자, 그렇다면 손쉽게 굴러든 기회로구려. 이보다 더 좋은 기회를 꾸미기도 어려울 거요."], ["Have you a carriage?”", "마차는 있소?\" \"없습니다.\""]], [["“No.” “That is of no consequence; I always have one ready, day and night.”", "\"그건 상관없소. 나는 늘 한 대를 준비해 두니까, 밤이든 낮이든.\""]], [["“Always ready?”", "\"늘 준비해 두신다고요?\""]], [["“Yes. I am a very capricious being, and I should tell you that sometimes when I rise, or after my dinner, or in the middle of the night, I resolve on starting for some particular point, and away I go.”", "\"그렇소. 나는 변덕이 무척 심한 사람이라, 이런 말을 해 두어야겠소만, 이따금 자리에서 일어났을 때나 식사를 마친 뒤나 한밤중에, 문득 어딘가로 떠나야겠다고 마음먹으면 그대로 떠나 버린다오.\""]], [["The count rang, and a footman appeared.", "백작이 종을 울리자 하인이 나타났다."]], [["“Order out the carriage,”", "\"마차를 대령하라.\""], ["he said, “and remove the pistols which are in the holsters.", "그가 말했다. \"그리고 권총집에 든 권총은 빼 놓아라."], ["You need not awaken the coachman; Ali will drive.”", "마부는 깨울 것 없다. 알리가 몰 테니.\""]], [["In a very short time the noise of wheels was heard, and the carriage stopped at the door.", "이내 바퀴 소리가 들리더니, 마차가 문 앞에 멈춰 섰다."], ["The count took out his watch.", "백작이 회중시계를 꺼냈다."]], [["“Half-past twelve,” he said.", "\"열두 시 반이군.\""], ["“We might start at five o’clock and be in time, but the delay may cause your friend to pass an uneasy night, and therefore we had better go with all speed to extricate him from the hands of the infidels.", "그가 말했다. \"다섯 시에 출발해도 시간은 맞겠지만, 그렇게 미루면 그대 친구가 불안한 밤을 보내게 될 거요. 그러니 서둘러 가서 그를 이교도의 손아귀에서 빼내는 편이 낫겠소."], ["Are you still resolved to accompany me?”", "아직도 나와 함께 갈 작정이오?\""]], [["“More determined than ever.”", "\"그 어느 때보다 굳게요.\""]], [["“Well, then, come along.”", "\"그럼 갑시다.\""]], [["Franz and the count went downstairs, accompanied by Peppino.", "프란츠와 백작은 페피노와 함께 아래층으로 내려갔다."], ["At the door they found the carriage. Ali was on the box, in whom Franz recognized the dumb slave of the grotto of Monte Cristo.", "문 앞에 마차가 있었다. 알리가 마부석에 앉아 있었는데, 프란츠는 그가 몬테크리스토섬 동굴에 있던 그 벙어리 노예임을 알아보았다."], ["Franz and the count got into the carriage.", "프란츠와 백작이 마차에 올랐다."], ["Peppino placed himself beside Ali, and they set off at a rapid pace.", "페피노는 알리 옆에 자리를 잡았고, 마차는 빠른 속도로 출발했다."], ["Ali had received his instructions, and went down the Corso, crossed the Campo Vaccino, went up the Strada San Gregorio, and reached the gates of St. Sebastian.", "미리 지시를 받은 알리는 코르소 거리를 따라 내려가 캄포 바치노를 가로지른 뒤, 산그레고리오 거리를 올라가 성세바스티아노 성문에 이르렀다."], ["Then the porter raised some difficulties, but the Count of Monte Cristo produced a permit from the governor of Rome, allowing him to leave or enter the city at any hour of the day or night; the portcullis was therefore raised, the porter had a louis for his trouble, and they went on their way.", "문지기가 이런저런 트집을 잡았으나, 몬테크리스토 백작이 로마 총독이 발급한 통행증을 내보였다. 밤낮 어느 시각에든 도성을 드나들 수 있게 허락한 증서였다. 그러자 쇠살문이 올라갔고, 문지기는 수고비로 일 루이를 받았으며, 그들은 가던 길을 계속 갔다."]], [["The road which the carriage now traversed was the ancient Appian Way, and bordered with tombs.", "이제 마차가 지나는 길은 옛 아피아 가도였는데, 양옆으로 무덤이 늘어서 있었다."], ["From time to time, by the light of the moon, which began to rise, Franz imagined that he saw something like a sentinel appear at various points among the ruins, and suddenly retreat into the darkness on a signal from Peppino.", "떠오르기 시작한 달빛 아래, 프란츠는 폐허 곳곳에서 보초 같은 무엇이 나타났다가 페피노의 신호에 따라 별안간 어둠 속으로 물러서는 것을 본 듯한 느낌이 이따금 들었다."]], [["A short time before they reached the Baths of Caracalla the carriage stopped, Peppino opened the door, and the count and Franz alighted.", "카라칼라 욕장에 다다르기 조금 전에 마차가 멈췄다. 페피노가 문을 열었고, 백작과 프란츠가 내렸다."]], [["“In ten minutes,”", "\"십 분 뒤면,\""], ["said the count to his companion, “we shall be there.”", "백작이 동행에게 말했다. \"우리는 그곳에 도착할 거요.\""]], [["He then took Peppino aside, gave him an order in a low voice, and Peppino went away, taking with him a torch, brought with them in the carriage.", "그러고는 페피노를 한쪽으로 데려가 낮은 목소리로 무언가를 지시했고, 페피노는 마차에서 가져온 횃불을 들고 떠났다."], ["Five minutes elapsed, during which Franz saw the shepherd going along a narrow path that led over the irregular and broken surface of the Campagna; and finally he disappeared in the midst of the tall red herbage, which seemed like the bristling mane of an enormous lion.", "오 분이 흐르는 동안, 프란츠는 그 양치기가 캄파냐의 울퉁불퉁하고 부서진 지면 위로 난 좁은 길을 따라가는 것을 지켜보았다. 마침내 그는 키 큰 붉은 풀숲 한가운데로 사라졌는데, 그 풀숲은 거대한 사자의 곤두선 갈기처럼 보였다."]], [["“Now,”", "\"자,\""], ["said the count, “let us follow him.”", "백작이 말했다. \"그를 따라갑시다.\""]], [["Franz and the count in their turn then advanced along the same path, which, at the distance of a hundred paces, led them over a declivity to the bottom of a small valley.", "이번에는 프란츠와 백작이 같은 길을 따라 나아갔다. 백 걸음쯤 가니 길은 비탈을 지나 작은 골짜기 바닥으로 이어졌다."], ["They then perceived two men conversing in the obscurity.", "그곳에서 두 사람은 어둠 속에서 이야기를 나누는 두 사내를 보았다."]], [["“Ought we to go on?”", "\"계속 가야 할까요?\""], ["asked Franz of the count; “or should we pause?”", "프란츠가 백작에게 물었다. \"아니면 멈춰야 합니까?\""]], [["“Let us go on; Peppino will have warned the sentry of our coming.”", "\"계속 갑시다. 페피노가 우리가 온다고 보초에게 알려 두었을 거요.\""]], [["One of the two men was Peppino, and the other a bandit on the lookout.", "두 사내 가운데 하나는 페피노였고, 다른 하나는 망을 보던 산적이었다."], ["Franz and the count advanced, and the bandit saluted them.", "프란츠와 백작이 다가가자, 산적이 그들에게 경례했다."]], [["“Your excellency,”", "\"각하,\""], ["said Peppino, addressing the count, “if you will follow me, the opening of the catacombs is close at hand.”", "페피노가 백작을 향해 말했다. \"저를 따라오시면, 지하 묘지 입구가 바로 코앞입니다.\""]], [["“Go on, then,” replied the count.", "\"그럼 앞장서게.\" 백작이 대답했다."], ["They came to an opening behind a clump of bushes and in the midst of a pile of rocks, by which a man could scarcely pass.", "그들은 덤불 더미 뒤, 바위가 쌓인 한가운데에 난 입구에 이르렀는데, 사람이 간신히 지날 만한 틈이었다."], ["Peppino glided first into this crevice; after they got along a few paces the passage widened.", "페피노가 먼저 그 틈으로 미끄러져 들어갔다. 몇 걸음 나아가자 통로가 넓어졌다."], ["Peppino passed, lighted his torch, and turned to see if they came after him.", "페피노는 안으로 들어가 횃불을 켜고는, 그들이 뒤따라오는지 돌아보았다."], ["The count first reached an open space and Franz followed him closely.", "백작이 먼저 트인 공간에 이르렀고, 프란츠가 바짝 뒤따랐다."], ["The passageway sloped in a gentle descent, enlarging as they proceeded; still Franz and the count were compelled to advance in a stooping posture, and were scarcely able to proceed abreast of one another.", "통로는 완만한 내리막으로 기울며 갈수록 넓어졌으나, 그래도 프란츠와 백작은 허리를 굽힌 채 나아가야 했고, 둘이 나란히 걷기도 어려웠다."], ["They went on a hundred and fifty paces in this way, and then were stopped by, “Who comes there?”", "그렇게 백오십 걸음쯤 갔을 때, \"누구냐?\" 하는 소리에 발이 멈췄다."], ["At the same time they saw the reflection of a torch on a carbine barrel.", "그와 동시에 카빈총 총신에 비친 횃불의 반사광이 보였다."]], [["“A friend!”", "\"친구다!\""], ["responded Peppino; and, advancing alone towards the sentry, he said a few words to him in a low tone; and then he, like the first, saluted the nocturnal visitors, making a sign that they might proceed.", "페피노가 대답했다. 그러고는 혼자 보초를 향해 나아가 낮은 목소리로 몇 마디 건넸다. 그러자 보초도 앞서의 산적처럼 한밤의 손님들에게 경례하며, 들어가도 좋다는 신호를 보냈다."]], [["Behind the sentinel was a staircase with twenty steps.", "보초 뒤에는 스무 계단짜리 층계가 있었다."], ["Franz and the count descended these, and found themselves in a mortuary chamber.", "프란츠와 백작이 그것을 내려가자, 시신을 안치하는 방에 이르렀다."], ["Five corridors diverged like the rays of a star, and the walls, dug into niches, which were arranged one above the other in the shape of coffins, showed that they were at last in the catacombs.", "다섯 갈래 복도가 별의 빛살처럼 뻗어 있었고, 벽은 관 모양으로 위아래 차곡차곡 배열된 벽감들이 파여 있어, 마침내 그들이 지하 묘지에 들어섰음을 알려 주었다."], ["Down one of the corridors, whose extent it was impossible to determine, rays of light were visible.", "그 가운데 한 복도, 그 길이를 가늠할 수 없는 복도 끝에서 한 줄기 빛이 보였다."], ["The count laid his hand on Franz’s shoulder.", "백작이 프란츠의 어깨에 손을 얹었다."]], [["“Would you like to see a camp of bandits in repose?”", "\"쉬고 있는 산적들의 진영을 보고 싶소?\""], ["he inquired.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“Exceedingly,”", "\"무척이나요.\""], ["replied Franz.", "프란츠가 대답했다."]], [["“Come with me, then.", "\"그럼 나를 따라오시오."], ["Peppino, put out the torch.”", "페피노, 횃불을 꺼라.\""], ["Peppino obeyed, and Franz and the count were in utter darkness, except that fifty paces in advance of them a reddish glare, more evident since Peppino had put out his torch, was visible along the wall.", "페피노가 시키는 대로 하자, 프란츠와 백작은 칠흑 같은 어둠 속에 잠겼다. 다만 오십 걸음쯤 앞에서 불그스름한 빛이 벽을 따라 보였는데, 페피노가 횃불을 끄자 그 빛은 한층 또렷해졌다."]], [["They advanced silently, the count guiding Franz as if he had the singular faculty of seeing in the dark.", "그들은 말없이 나아갔다. 백작은 어둠 속에서도 볼 줄 아는 기이한 능력이라도 지닌 듯 프란츠를 이끌었다."], ["Franz himself, however, saw his way more plainly in proportion as he went on towards the light, which served in some manner as a guide.", "그러나 프란츠 자신도 길잡이 노릇을 하는 그 빛을 향해 갈수록 길이 점점 또렷이 보였다."], ["Three arcades were before them, and the middle one was used as a door.", "그들 앞에 아치 셋이 있었는데, 가운데 것이 문 노릇을 했다."], ["These arcades opened on one side into the corridor where the count and Franz were, and on the other into a large square chamber, entirely surrounded by niches similar to those of which we have spoken.", "이 아치들은 한쪽으로는 백작과 프란츠가 있는 복도로, 다른 쪽으로는 앞서 말한 것과 똑같은 벽감들로 빙 둘러싸인 커다란 네모진 방으로 열려 있었다."]], [["In the midst of this chamber were four stones, which had formerly served as an altar, as was evident from the cross which still surmounted them.", "이 방 한가운데에 돌 넷이 놓여 있었는데, 그 위에 아직도 십자가가 얹혀 있는 것으로 보아 한때 제단으로 쓰였던 것이 분명했다."], ["A lamp, placed at the base of a pillar, lighted up with its pale and flickering flame the singular scene which presented itself to the eyes of the two visitors concealed in the shadow.", "기둥 밑에 놓인 등잔 하나가 창백하게 일렁이는 불꽃으로, 그늘에 숨은 두 방문객의 눈앞에 펼쳐진 기이한 광경을 비추고 있었다."]], [["A man was seated with his elbow leaning on the column, and was reading with his back turned to the arcades, through the openings of which the new-comers contemplated him.", "한 사내가 기둥에 팔꿈치를 괴고 앉아, 아치들에 등을 돌린 채 책을 읽고 있었다. 새로 들어온 이들은 그 아치 틈으로 그를 바라보았다."], ["This was the chief of the band, Luigi Vampa.", "그가 바로 패거리의 두목, 루이지 밤파였다."], ["Around him, and in groups, according to their fancy, lying in their mantles, or with their backs against a sort of stone bench, which went all round the columbarium, were to be seen twenty brigands or more, each having his carbine within reach.", "그의 주위로는, 저마다 마음 내키는 대로 무리 지어, 망토를 두르고 누워 있거나, 납골당을 빙 두른 일종의 돌 의자에 등을 기댄 채, 스무 명 남짓한 산적들이 보였다. 저마다 손 닿는 곳에 카빈총을 두고 있었다."], ["At the other end, silent, scarcely visible, and like a shadow, was a sentinel, who was walking up and down before a grotto, which was only distinguishable because in that spot the darkness seemed more dense than elsewhere.", "방 저쪽 끝에는, 말없이 거의 보이지 않는 그림자 같은 보초 하나가, 동굴 하나 앞을 오락가락하고 있었다. 그 동굴은 그곳의 어둠이 다른 데보다 한층 짙어 보이는 까닭에만 겨우 분간이 되었다."]], [["When the count thought Franz had gazed sufficiently on this picturesque tableau, he raised his finger to his lips, to warn him to be silent, and, ascending the three steps which led to the corridor of the columbarium, entered the chamber by the middle arcade, and advanced towards Vampa, who was so intent on the book before him that he did not hear the noise of his footsteps.", "백작은 프란츠가 이 그림 같은 광경을 충분히 들여다보았다 싶자, 손가락을 입술에 갖다 대어 조용히 하라는 신호를 보냈다. 그러고는 납골당의 복도로 이어지는 세 계단을 올라 가운데 아치를 통해 방으로 들어가, 밤파를 향해 나아갔다. 밤파는 눈앞의 책에 어찌나 골몰해 있던지 그의 발소리를 듣지 못했다."]], [["“Who comes there?”", "\"누구냐?\""], ["cried the sentinel, who was less abstracted, and who saw by the lamp-light a shadow approaching his chief.", "보초가 외쳤다. 그는 정신이 덜 팔려 있던 터라, 등잔불에 비친 그림자가 두목에게 다가오는 것을 본 것이었다."], ["At this challenge, Vampa rose quickly, drawing at the same moment a pistol from his girdle.", "이 외침에 밤파가 벌떡 일어나, 동시에 허리띠에서 권총을 뽑아 들었다."], ["In a moment all the bandits were on their feet, and twenty carbines were levelled at the count.", "순식간에 산적들이 모두 일어났고, 스무 자루의 카빈총이 백작을 겨누었다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"이런,\""], ["said he in a voice perfectly calm, and no muscle of his countenance disturbed, “well, my dear Vampa, it appears to me that you receive a friend with a great deal of ceremony.”", "그가 더없이 침착한 목소리로, 얼굴 근육 하나 흔들리지 않은 채 말했다. \"이런, 친애하는 밤파, 친구를 맞이하는데 격식이 어지간히도 거창하구먼.\""]], [["20207m\n“Ground arms,”", "\"무기를 내려라!\" 두목이 한 손으로 단호한 손짓을 하며 외쳤다. 다른 손으로는 정중하게 모자를 벗었다."], ["exclaimed the chief, with an imperative sign of the hand, while with the other he took off his hat respectfully; then, turning to the singular personage who had caused this scene, he said, “Your pardon, your excellency, but I was so far from expecting the honor of a visit, that I did not really recognize you.”", "그러고는 이 소동을 일으킨 그 기이한 인물을 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"용서하십시오, 각하. 방문해 주시는 영광을 누릴 줄은 전혀 생각지 못한 터라, 차마 알아뵙지 못했습니다.\""]], [["“It seems that your memory is equally short in everything, Vampa,”", "\"자네는 무엇에든 기억이 한결같이 짧은 모양이군, 밤파.\""], ["said the count, “and that not only do you forget people’s faces, but also the conditions you make with them.”", "백작이 말했다. \"사람들 얼굴만 잊는 게 아니라, 그들과 맺은 약조까지도 잊으니 말이야.\""]], [["“What conditions have I forgotten, your excellency?”", "\"제가 무슨 약조를 잊었습니까, 각하?\""], ["inquired the bandit, with the air of a man who, having committed an error, is anxious to repair it.", "산적이, 잘못을 저지르고 그걸 만회하고 싶어 안달하는 사람의 기색으로 물었다."]], [["“Was it not agreed,” asked the count, “that not only my person, but also that of my friends, should be respected by you?”", "\"내 한 몸뿐 아니라 내 벗들의 몸까지 자네가 존중하기로 하지 않았던가?\" 백작이 물었다."]], [["“And how have I broken that treaty, your excellency?”", "\"제가 그 약조를 어떻게 어겼다는 말씀입니까, 각하?\""]], [["“You have this evening carried off and conveyed hither the Viscount Albert de Morcerf. Well,”", "\"자네는 오늘 저녁 알베르 드 모르세르 자작을 납치해 이리로 끌고 왔어. 그런데,\" 백작이 프란츠를 몸서리치게 하는 어조로 말을 이었다."], ["continued the count, in a tone that made Franz shudder, “this young gentleman is one of _my friends_—this young gentleman lodges in the same hotel as myself—this young gentleman has been up and down the Corso for eight hours in my private carriage, and yet, I repeat to you, you have carried him off, and conveyed him hither, and,”", "\"이 젊은 신사는 내 벗 가운데 하나야. 이 젊은 신사는 나와 같은 호텔에 묵고 있어. 이 젊은 신사는 여덟 시간 동안이나 내 개인 마차를 타고 코르소 거리를 오르내렸지."], ["added the count, taking the letter from his pocket, “you have set a ransom on him, as if he were an utter stranger.”", "그런데도 다시 말하건대, 자네는 그를 납치해 이리로 끌고 왔고,\" 백작이 주머니에서 편지를 꺼내며 덧붙였다. \"마치 생판 모르는 남에게 그러듯 그에게 몸값을 매겼어.\""]], [["“Why did you not tell me all this—you?” inquired the brigand chief, turning towards his men, who all retreated before his look.", "\"왜 너희는 이 모든 걸 나에게 말하지 않았느냐, 너희 말이다?\" 산적 두목이 부하들을 향해 돌아서며 물었고, 그들은 모두 그 눈빛 앞에서 뒷걸음질 쳤다."], ["“Why have you caused me thus to fail in my word towards a gentleman like the count, who has all our lives in his hands?", "\"어찌하여 너희는 우리 모두의 목숨을 손에 쥐신 백작 같은 신사분께 내가 약속을 어기게 만들었느냐?"], ["By heavens! if I thought one of you knew that the young gentleman was the friend of his excellency, I would blow his brains out with my own hand!” 20211m", "맹세코, 너희 가운데 누구라도 그 젊은 신사가 각하의 벗인 줄 알고 있었다면, 내 손으로 그놈의 머리통을 날려 버렸을 것이다!\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"자,\""], ["said the count, turning towards Franz, “I told you there was some mistake in this.”", "백작이 프란츠를 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"내가 여기엔 무슨 착오가 있다고 하지 않았소.\""]], [["“Are you not alone?” asked Vampa with uneasiness.", "\"혼자 오신 게 아닙니까?\" 밤파가 불안한 듯 물었다."]], [["“I am with the person to whom this letter was addressed, and to whom I desired to prove that Luigi Vampa was a man of his word.", "\"이 편지를 받은 사람과 함께 왔지. 그에게 루이지 밤파가 약속을 지키는 사내임을 증명해 보이고 싶었거든."], ["Come, your excellency,”", "자, 각하,\""], ["the count added, turning to Franz, “here is Luigi Vampa, who will himself express to you his deep regret at the mistake he has committed.”", "백작이 프란츠를 돌아보며 덧붙였다. \"여기 루이지 밤파가, 자신이 저지른 착오에 대해 깊은 유감을 직접 표할 거요.\""]], [["Franz approached, the chief advancing several steps to meet him.", "프란츠가 다가서자, 두목이 그를 맞으러 몇 걸음 나아왔다."]], [["“Welcome among us, your excellency,”", "\"환영합니다, 각하.\" 그가 프란츠에게 말했다."], ["he said to him; “you heard what the count just said, and also my reply; let me add that I would not for the four thousand piastres at which I had fixed your friend’s ransom, that this had happened.”", "\"방금 백작께서 하신 말씀과 제 대답을 들으셨겠지요. 한마디만 덧붙이자면, 제가 각하의 친구분 몸값으로 매긴 사천 피아스트르를 준다 해도, 이런 일이 일어나기를 바라지는 않았을 겁니다.\""]], [["“But,”", "\"한데,\""], ["said Franz, looking round him uneasily, “where is the viscount?—I do not see him.”", "프란츠가 불안한 듯 주위를 둘러보며 말했다. \"자작은 어디 있소? 보이지 않는구려.\""]], [["“Nothing has happened to him, I hope,”", "\"그에게 무슨 일이라도 생긴 게 아니길 바라네.\""], ["said the count frowningly.", "백작이 이맛살을 찌푸리며 말했다."]], [["“The prisoner is there,”", "\"포로는 저기 있습니다.\""], ["replied Vampa, pointing to the hollow space in front of which the bandit was on guard, “and I will go myself and tell him he is free.”", "밤파가 산적이 망을 보고 있는 앞쪽의 우묵한 공간을 가리키며 대답했다. \"제가 직접 가서 그가 자유의 몸이 되었다고 일러 주겠습니다.\""]], [["The chief went towards the place he had pointed out as Albert’s prison, and Franz and the count followed him.", "두목은 알베르의 감옥이라고 가리킨 그 자리로 향했고, 프란츠와 백작이 그 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["“What is the prisoner doing?”", "\"포로는 무엇을 하고 있느냐?\""], ["inquired Vampa of the sentinel.", "밤파가 보초에게 물었다."]], [["“_Ma foi_, captain,”", "\"글쎄요, 대장.\""], ["replied the sentry, “I do not know; for the last hour I have not heard him stir.”", "보초가 대답했다. \"모르겠습니다. 지난 한 시간 동안 꼼짝하는 소리도 못 들었으니까요.\""]], [["“Come in, your excellency,”", "\"드시지요, 각하.\""], ["said Vampa. The count and Franz ascended seven or eight steps after the chief, who drew back a bolt and opened a door.", "밤파가 말했다. 백작과 프란츠는 두목을 따라 일고여덟 계단을 올랐고, 두목이 빗장을 빼고 문을 열었다."], ["Then, by the gleam of a lamp, similar to that which lighted the columbarium, Albert was to be seen wrapped up in a cloak which one of the bandits had lent him, lying in a corner in profound slumber.", "그러자 납골당을 밝히던 것과 비슷한 등잔불 아래, 알베르가 산적 하나가 빌려 준 망토를 두른 채 한쪽 구석에 누워 깊이 잠들어 있는 모습이 보였다."]], [["“Come,”", "\"이런,\""], ["said the count, smiling with his own peculiar smile, “not so bad for a man who is to be shot at seven o’clock tomorrow morning.”", "백작이 그 특유의 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"내일 아침 일곱 시에 총살당할 사람치고는 제법인걸.\""]], [["Vampa looked at Albert with a kind of admiration; he was not insensible to such a proof of courage.", "밤파는 일종의 감탄 어린 눈으로 알베르를 바라보았다. 그런 용기의 증거에 무감각할 사람이 아니었던 것이다."]], [["“You are right, your excellency,”", "\"옳은 말씀입니다, 각하.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said; “this must be one of your friends.”", "\"이분은 틀림없이 각하의 벗 가운데 한 분이로군요.\""]], [["Then going to Albert, he touched him on the shoulder, saying, “Will your excellency please to awaken?”", "그러고는 알베르에게 다가가 어깨를 건드리며 말했다. \"각하, 좀 일어나 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["Albert stretched out his arms, rubbed his eyelids, and opened his eyes.", "알베르는 두 팔을 쭉 뻗고 눈꺼풀을 비비더니 눈을 떴다."]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오,\""], ["said he, “is it you, captain?", "그가 말했다. \"당신이오, 대장?"], ["You should have allowed me to sleep.", "그냥 자게 내버려 두지 그랬소."], ["I had such a delightful dream.", "정말 근사한 꿈을 꾸고 있었는데."], ["I was dancing the galop at Torlonia’s with the Countess G——.”", "토를로니아 댁에서 G 백작 부인과 갤럽을 추고 있었거든.\""], ["Then he drew his watch from his pocket, that he might see how time sped.", "그러고는 시간이 얼마나 흘렀는지 보려고 주머니에서 시계를 꺼냈다."]], [["“Half-past one only?”", "\"겨우 한 시 반인데?\""], ["said he. “Why the devil do you rouse me at this hour?”", "그가 말했다. \"대체 이 시각에 왜 나를 깨우는 거요?\""]], [["“To tell you that you are free, your excellency.”", "\"각하께서 자유의 몸이 되셨다는 걸 알려 드리려고요.\""]], [["“My dear fellow,”", "\"이보게,\" 알베르가 더없이 태평한 마음으로 대답했다."], ["replied Albert, with perfect ease of mind, “remember, for the future, Napoleon’s maxim, ‘Never awaken me but for bad news;’ if you had let me sleep on, I should have finished my galop, and have been grateful to you all my life.", "\"앞으로는 나폴레옹의 격언을 잊지 말게. '나쁜 소식이 아니거든 절대 나를 깨우지 마라.' 자게 내버려 두었더라면, 갤럽을 다 추고서 평생 자네에게 고마워했을 텐데."], ["So, then, they have paid my ransom?”", "그래, 그러니까 누가 내 몸값을 치렀단 말인가?\""]], [["“No, your excellency.”", "\"아닙니다, 각하.\""]], [["“Well, then, how am I free?”", "\"그럼 내가 어떻게 풀려난다는 거지?\""]], [["“A person to whom I can refuse nothing has come to demand you.”", "\"제가 무엇도 거절할 수 없는 분이 각하를 데려가겠다고 오셨습니다.\""]], [["“Come hither?”", "\"여기로 오셨다고?\""]], [["“Yes, hither.”", "\"네, 여기로요.\""]], [["“Really?", "\"정말로?"], ["Then that person is a most amiable person.”", "그렇다면 그분은 더없이 사랑스러운 분이로군.\""]], [["Albert looked around and perceived Franz. “What,”", "알베르가 주위를 둘러보다 프란츠를 발견했다. \"아니,\""], ["said he, “is it you, my dear Franz, whose devotion and friendship are thus displayed?”", "그가 말했다. \"자네였군, 친애하는 프란츠. 자네의 헌신과 우정이 이렇게 드러나는구먼.\""]], [["“No, not I,”", "\"아니, 나는 아닐세.\""], ["replied Franz, “but our neighbor, the Count of Monte Cristo.”", "프란츠가 대답했다. \"우리 이웃이신 몬테크리스토 백작이시네.\""]], [["“Oh, my dear count,”", "\"오, 친애하는 백작님.\""], ["said Albert gayly, arranging his cravat and wristbands, “you are really most kind, and I hope you will consider me as under eternal obligations to you, in the first place for the carriage, and in the next for this visit,”", "알베르가 즐겁게 말하며 넥타이와 소맷부리를 매만졌다. \"백작님은 정말이지 더없이 친절한 분이십니다. 부디 제가 영원히 빚을 진 사람으로 여겨 주십시오."], ["and he put out his hand to the count, who shuddered as he gave his own, but who nevertheless did give it.", "우선은 그 마차 때문에, 다음으로는 이 방문 때문에요.\" 그러고는 백작에게 손을 내밀었다. 백작은 자기 손을 내밀며 몸서리를 쳤으나, 그래도 손을 내밀어 주었다."]], [["The bandit gazed on this scene with amazement; he was evidently accustomed to see his prisoners tremble before him, and yet here was one whose gay temperament was not for a moment altered; as for Franz, he was enchanted at the way in which Albert had sustained the national honor in the presence of the bandit.", "산적은 이 광경을 어리둥절한 눈으로 바라보았다. 그는 자기 앞에서 포로들이 벌벌 떠는 것을 보는 데 익숙했던 것이 분명했는데, 여기 그 쾌활한 성정이 한순간도 흐트러지지 않는 자가 있었던 것이다. 프란츠로 말하자면, 알베르가 산적 앞에서 조국의 명예를 지켜 낸 그 태도에 매료되었다."]], [["“My dear Albert,”", "\"이보게, 알베르,\""], ["he said, “if you will make haste, we shall yet have time to finish the night at Torlonia’s.", "그가 말했다. \"자네가 서둘러 준다면, 아직 토를로니아 댁에서 밤을 마저 보낼 시간이 있을 걸세."], ["You may conclude your interrupted galop, so that you will owe no ill-will to Signor Luigi, who has, indeed, throughout this whole affair acted like a gentleman.”", "끊겼던 갤럽을 마저 출 수도 있을 테니, 사실 이번 일 내내 신사답게 처신한 루이지 씨에게 원한을 품을 일도 없을 거고.\""]], [["“You are decidedly right, and we may reach the Palazzo by two o’clock.", "\"자네 말이 전적으로 옳네. 두 시면 궁에 닿을 수 있겠어."], ["Signor Luigi,”", "루이지 씨,\""], ["continued Albert, “is there any formality to fulfil before I take leave of your excellency?”", "알베르가 말을 이었다. \"각하와 작별하기 전에 치러야 할 절차가 있소?\""]], [["“None, sir,”", "\"없습니다, 자작님.\""], ["replied the bandit, “you are as free as air.”", "산적이 대답했다. \"자작님은 바람처럼 자유로운 몸입니다.\""]], [["“Well, then, a happy and merry life to you.", "\"그럼, 행복하고 즐거운 삶을 누리시길."], ["Come, gentlemen, come.”", "자, 여러분, 갑시다.\""]], [["And Albert, followed by Franz and the count, descended the staircase, crossed the square chamber, where stood all the bandits, hat in hand.", "알베르는 프란츠와 백작을 거느리고 층계를 내려가, 산적들이 모두 모자를 손에 든 채 서 있는 네모진 방을 가로질렀다."]], [["“Peppino,”", "\"페피노,\""], ["said the brigand chief, “give me the torch.”", "산적 두목이 말했다. \"횃불을 다오.\""]], [["“What are you going to do?”", "\"무얼 하려고?\""], ["inquired the count.", "백작이 물었다."]], [["“I will show you the way back myself,”", "\"제가 직접 돌아가는 길을 안내해 드리겠습니다.\""], ["said the captain; “that is the least honor that I can render to your excellency.”", "대장이 말했다. \"그게 각하께 바칠 수 있는 최소한의 예의니까요.\""]], [["And taking the lighted torch from the hands of the herdsman, he preceded his guests, not as a servant who performs an act of civility, but like a king who precedes ambassadors.", "그러고는 양치기의 손에서 불붙은 횃불을 받아 들고 손님들 앞에 섰는데, 그 모습은 예를 차리는 하인이 아니라 사절들을 거느리고 앞서는 임금 같았다."], ["On reaching the door, he bowed.", "문에 이르자 그가 허리를 굽혔다."]], [["“And now, your excellency,”", "\"그럼 이제, 각하,\""], ["added he, “allow me to repeat my apologies, and I hope you will not entertain any resentment at what has occurred.”", "그가 덧붙였다. \"다시 한번 제 사과를 받아 주시고, 부디 오늘 벌어진 일에 어떤 앙심도 품지 말아 주시길 바랍니다.\""]], [["“No, my dear Vampa,”", "\"앙심이라니, 친애하는 밤파.\""], ["replied the count; “besides, you compensate for your mistakes in so gentlemanly a way, that one almost feels obliged to you for having committed them.”", "백작이 대답했다. \"게다가 자네가 그토록 신사다운 방식으로 잘못을 갚으니, 차라리 그 잘못을 저질러 준 것이 고마울 지경일세.\""]], [["20214m\n“Gentlemen,”", "\"여러분,\""], ["added the chief, turning towards the young men, “perhaps the offer may not appear very tempting to you; but if you should ever feel inclined to pay me a second visit, wherever I may be, you shall be welcome.”", "두목이 두 젊은이를 향해 돌아서며 덧붙였다. \"이 제안이 그다지 솔깃하게 들리지 않을지도 모릅니다만, 혹여 언제고 다시 한번 저를 찾아 주실 마음이 드신다면, 제가 어디에 있든 여러분을 환영하겠습니다.\""]], [["Franz and Albert bowed.", "프란츠와 알베르가 허리를 굽혀 인사했다."], ["The count went out first, then Albert.", "백작이 먼저 나갔고, 그다음 알베르가 나갔다."], ["Franz paused for a moment.", "프란츠는 잠시 걸음을 멈췄다."]], [["“Has your excellency anything to ask me?”", "\"각하, 제게 물으실 게 있으십니까?\""], ["said Vampa with a smile.", "밤파가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, I have,”", "\"그렇소.\""], ["replied Franz; “I am curious to know what work you were perusing with so much attention as we entered.”", "프란츠가 대답했다. \"우리가 들어왔을 때 당신이 그토록 골똘히 읽고 있던 책이 무엇인지 궁금하구려.\""]], [["“Cæsar’s _Commentaries_,”", "\"카이사르의 『갈리아 전기』입니다.\""], ["said the bandit, “it is my favorite work.”", "산적이 말했다. \"제가 가장 좋아하는 책이지요.\""]], [["“Well, are you coming?”", "\"이보게, 안 오나?\""], ["asked Albert.", "알베르가 물었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"가네.\""], ["replied Franz, “here I am,”", "프란츠가 대답했다. \"여기 가네.\""], ["and he, in his turn, left the caves.", "그러고는 그도 이번에는 동굴을 떠났다."], ["They advanced to the plain.", "그들은 평지로 나아갔다."]], [["“Ah, your pardon,”", "\"아, 실례하오.\""], ["said Albert, turning round; “will you allow me, captain?”", "알베르가 돌아서며 말했다. \"대장, 잠깐 괜찮겠소?\""]], [["And he lighted his cigar at Vampa’s torch.", "그러고는 밤파의 횃불에 자기 시가에 불을 붙였다."]], [["“Now, my dear count,”", "\"자, 친애하는 백작님.\""], ["he said, “let us on with all the speed we may.", "그가 말했다. \"되도록 서둘러 갑시다."], ["I am enormously anxious to finish my night at the Duke of Bracciano’s.”", "브라차노 공작 댁에서 밤을 마저 보내고 싶어 안달이 나서요.\""]], [["They found the carriage where they had left it.", "그들은 마차를 세워 두었던 자리에서 마차를 찾았다."], ["The count said a word in Arabic to Ali, and the horses went on at great speed.", "백작이 알리에게 아랍어로 한마디 건네자, 말들이 쏜살같이 달려 나갔다."]], [["It was just two o’clock by Albert’s watch when the two friends entered into the dancing-room.", "두 친구가 무도회장으로 들어섰을 때, 알베르의 시계로 꼭 두 시였다."], ["Their return was quite an event, but as they entered together, all uneasiness on Albert’s account ceased instantly.", "그들의 귀환은 그야말로 하나의 사건이었으나, 둘이 함께 들어선 덕에 알베르를 둘러싼 모든 불안은 순식간에 가셨다."]], [["“Madame,”", "\"부인,\" 모르세르 자작이 백작 부인을 향해 나아가며 말했다. \"어제 부인께서 황송하게도 제게 갤럽 한 곡을 약속해 주셨지요."], ["said the Viscount of Morcerf, advancing towards the countess, “yesterday you were so condescending as to promise me a galop; I am rather late in claiming this gracious promise, but here is my friend, whose character for veracity you well know, and he will assure you the delay arose from no fault of mine.”", "이 고마운 약속을 청하기에 좀 늦은 감이 있습니다만, 여기 제 친구가 있습니다. 부인께서도 그의 진실됨을 잘 아시지요. 이 지체가 결코 제 탓이 아니었음을 그가 증언해 줄 겁니다.\""]], [["And as at this moment the orchestra gave the signal for the waltz, Albert put his arm round the waist of the countess, and disappeared with her in the whirl of dancers.", "그리고 마침 그때 악단이 왈츠의 신호를 울리자, 알베르는 백작 부인의 허리에 팔을 두르고 춤추는 무리의 소용돌이 속으로 그녀와 함께 사라졌다."]], [["In the meanwhile Franz was considering the singular shudder that had passed over the Count of Monte Cristo at the moment when he had been, in some sort, forced to give his hand to Albert.", "그동안 프란츠는, 어떤 의미로 떠밀리다시피 알베르에게 손을 내밀어야 했던 그 순간 몬테크리스토 백작을 스치고 지나간 그 기이한 전율에 대해 곰곰이 생각하고 있었다."]], [["# Chapter 38. The Rendezvous", "# Chapter 38. The Rendezvous"]], [["The first words that Albert uttered to his friend, on the following morning, contained a request that Franz would accompany him on a visit to the count; true, the young man had warmly and energetically thanked the count on the previous evening; but services such as he had rendered could never be too often acknowledged.", "다음 날 아침, 알베르가 친구에게 건넨 첫마디는 백작을 찾아가는 길에 함께 가 달라는 부탁이었다. 사실 그는 전날 저녁에도 백작에게 따뜻하고 열렬하게 감사를 표한 터였다. 하지만 백작이 베푼 정도의 도움이라면 아무리 거듭 사례해도 지나치지 않았다."], ["Franz, who seemed attracted by some invisible influence towards the count, in which terror was strangely mingled, felt an extreme reluctance to permit his friend to be exposed alone to the singular fascination that this mysterious personage seemed to exercise over him, and therefore made no objection to Albert’s request, but at once accompanied him to the desired spot, and, after a short delay, the count joined them in the salon.", "프란츠는 어떤 보이지 않는 힘에 이끌리듯 백작에게 끌렸는데, 그 끌림에는 묘하게도 공포가 뒤섞여 있었다. 그래서 그는 이 신비로운 인물이 친구에게 행사하는 듯한 야릇한 매혹 앞에 알베르를 홀로 내버려 두는 것이 몹시 꺼림칙했다. 결국 알베르의 부탁에 토를 달지 않고 곧장 약속한 장소로 함께 갔고, 잠시 기다리자 백작이 객실로 나와 그들과 합류했다."]], [["“My dear count,”", "\"친애하는 백작님,\" 알베르가 그를 맞으러 다가서며 말했다. \"어젯밤 드린 변변찮은 감사를 다시 한번 되풀이하게 해 주십시오."], ["said Albert, advancing to meet him, “permit me to repeat the poor thanks I offered last night, and to assure you that the remembrance of all I owe to you will never be effaced from my memory; believe me, as long as I live, I shall never cease to dwell with grateful recollection on the prompt and important service you rendered me; and also to remember that to you I am indebted even for my life.”", "그리고 백작님께 진 빚의 기억은 결코 제 머릿속에서 지워지지 않으리라는 것도 분명히 말씀드리고 싶습니다. 믿어 주십시오, 살아 있는 한 저는 백작님이 베풀어 주신 그 신속하고 막중한 도움을 감사한 마음으로 늘 되새길 것입니다. 게다가 제 목숨까지 백작님께 빚지고 있다는 사실도 잊지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["“My very good friend and excellent neighbor,”", "\"내 더없이 좋은 벗이자 훌륭한 이웃이여,\""], ["replied the count, with a smile, “you really exaggerate my trifling exertions.", "백작이 미소를 지으며 대답했다. \"정말이지 그대는 내 사소한 수고를 너무 부풀려 말하는군요."], ["You owe me nothing but some trifle of 20,000 francs, which you have been saved out of your travelling expenses, so that there is not much of a score between us;—but you must really permit me to congratulate you on the ease and unconcern with which you resigned yourself to your fate, and the perfect indifference you manifested as to the turn events might take.”", "그대가 내게 진 것이라곤 여행 경비에서 아낀 2만 프랑 남짓한 푼돈뿐이니, 우리 사이에 셈할 거리도 그리 많지 않습니다. 다만 그대가 제 운명에 어찌나 태연하고 무심하게 몸을 맡겼는지, 또 사태가 어떻게 흘러가든 더없이 무심한 태도를 보였는지, 그 점만큼은 꼭 축하해 드려야겠습니다.\""]], [["“Upon my word,”", "\"솔직히 말해,\""], ["said Albert, “I deserve no credit for what I could not help, namely, a determination to take everything as I found it, and to let those bandits see, that although men get into troublesome scrapes all over the world, there is no nation but the French that can smile even in the face of grim Death himself.", "알베르가 말했다. \"어쩔 수 없는 일에 대해서는 제가 칭찬받을 까닭이 없지요. 다시 말해 모든 일을 있는 그대로 받아들이기로 마음먹은 것, 그리고 그 도적들에게 보여 주려 한 것뿐입니다."], ["All that, however, has nothing to do with my obligations to you, and I now come to ask you whether, in my own person, my family, or connections, I can in any way serve you?", "세상 어디서든 사람은 곤란한 처지에 빠지게 마련이지만, 음산한 죽음의 신 앞에서조차 미소 지을 수 있는 민족은 프랑스인밖에 없다는 것을요. 하지만 그런 건 백작님께 진 빚과는 아무 상관이 없습니다."], ["My father, the Comte de Morcerf, although of Spanish origin, possesses considerable influence, both at the court of France and Madrid, and I unhesitatingly place the best services of myself, and all to whom my life is dear, at your disposal.”", "이제 여쭙고 싶은 건, 제 한 몸으로든, 제 가문으로든, 제 인맥으로든, 어떤 식으로든 백작님께 도움이 될 길이 있겠느냐는 것입니다. 제 아버지 모르세르 백작께서는 에스파냐 혈통이긴 하지만 프랑스 궁정과 마드리드 궁정 양쪽에 상당한 영향력을 지니고 계십니다. 저 자신은 물론, 제가 아끼는 모든 이의 도움을 망설임 없이 백작님께 내드리겠습니다.\""]], [["“Monsieur de Morcerf,”", "\"모르세르 씨,\" 백작이 대답했다. \"그대의 제안은 나를 놀라게 하기는커녕, 바로 내가 그대에게서 기대하던 바입니다."], ["replied the count, “your offer, far from surprising me, is precisely what I expected from you, and I accept it in the same spirit of hearty sincerity with which it is made;—nay, I will go still further, and say that I had previously made up my mind to ask a great favor at your hands.”", "그러니 그 제안이 베풀어진 것과 똑같이 진심 어린 마음으로 받아들이겠습니다. 아니, 한 걸음 더 나아가 말하지요. 실은 나도 그대에게 큰 부탁 하나를 청하려고 진작부터 마음먹고 있었습니다.\""]], [["“Oh, pray name it.”", "\"오, 부디 말씀하십시오.\""]], [["“I am wholly a stranger to Paris—it is a city I have never yet seen.”", "\"나는 파리에 대해 전혀 모르는 이방인입니다. 아직 한 번도 본 적이 없는 도시지요.\""]], [["“Is it possible,”", "\"그게 가능한 일입니까?\""], ["exclaimed Albert, “that you have reached your present age without visiting the finest capital in the world?", "알베르가 외쳤다. \"이 나이가 되도록 세상에서 가장 아름다운 수도를 한 번도 가 보지 않으셨다니요?"], ["I can scarcely credit it.”", "도무지 믿기지가 않습니다.\""]], [["“Nevertheless, it is quite true; still, I agree with you in thinking that my present ignorance of the first city in Europe is a reproach to me in every way, and calls for immediate correction; but, in all probability, I should have performed so important, so necessary a duty, as that of making myself acquainted with the wonders and beauties of your justly celebrated capital, had I known any person who would have introduced me into the fashionable world, but unfortunately I possessed no acquaintance there, and, of necessity, was compelled to abandon the idea.”", "\"그렇지만 정말 사실입니다. 그래도 유럽 제일의 도시에 대한 이 무지가 어느 모로 보나 내게는 부끄러운 일이며, 당장 바로잡아야 할 문제라는 점에서는 그대와 생각이 같습니다. 하지만 그대들이 자랑할 만한 그 수도의 경이와 아름다움을 익히는 일, 그토록 중대하고 꼭 필요한 의무라면 진작에 해냈을 겁니다. 나를 사교계에 소개해 줄 만한 사람을 한 명이라도 알았다면 말입니다. 그런데 불행히도 그곳에는 아는 이가 하나도 없었고, 어쩔 수 없이 그 생각을 접어야 했지요.\""]], [["“So distinguished an individual as yourself,”", "\"백작님처럼 출중하신 분이라면,\""], ["cried Albert, “could scarcely have required an introduction.”", "알베르가 외쳤다. \"소개 같은 건 거의 필요 없으셨을 텐데요.\""]], [["“You are most kind; but as regards myself, I can find no merit I possess, save that, as a millionaire, I might have become a partner in the speculations of M.", "\"참으로 친절하시군요. 하지만 내게 무슨 내세울 만한 점이 있는지 모르겠습니다. 굳이 꼽자면 백만장자이니 아구아도 씨나 로트실트 씨의 투기 사업에 동업자로 끼어들 수야 있겠지요."], ["Aguado and M. Rothschild; but as my motive in travelling to your capital would not have been for the pleasure of dabbling in stocks, I stayed away till some favorable chance should present itself of carrying my wish into execution.", "하지만 내가 그대들의 수도를 찾는 까닭이 주식 놀음의 재미 때문은 아닐 테니, 내 소망을 이룰 만한 좋은 기회가 나타날 때까지 그저 발길을 미루어 두었던 겁니다. 그런데 그대의 제안이 그 모든 난관을 말끔히 풀어 주는군요."], ["Your offer, however, smooths all difficulties, and I have only to ask you, my dear M.", "그러니 친애하는 모르세르 씨,\" 이 말에는 더없이 묘한 미소가 곁들여졌다."], ["de Morcerf” (these words were accompanied by a most peculiar smile), “whether you undertake, upon my arrival in France, to open to me the doors of that fashionable world of which I know no more than a Huron or a native of Cochin-China?”", "\"이제 한 가지만 묻겠습니다. 내가 프랑스에 닿거든, 내게는 휴런족이나 코친차이나 토박이만큼이나 낯선 그 사교계의 문을 열어 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“Oh, that I do, and with infinite pleasure,”", "\"오, 물론이지요, 그것도 더없이 기쁜 마음으로요.\""], ["answered Albert; “and so much the more readily as a letter received this morning from my father summons me to Paris, in consequence of a treaty of marriage (my dear Franz, do not smile, I beg of you) with a family of high standing, and connected with the very cream of Parisian society.”", "알베르가 대답했다. \"마침 오늘 아침 아버지께서 보낸 편지가 저를 파리로 부르고 있으니 더욱 흔쾌히 응할 수 있습니다. 어느 명문가와의 혼담 때문인데요(친애하는 프란츠, 제발 웃지 좀 말게), 파리 사교계의 최상류층과 연이 닿는 집안입니다.\""]], [["“Connected by marriage, you mean,”", "\"혼인으로 연이 닿는 집안이라는 말이겠지.\""], ["said Franz, laughingly.", "프란츠가 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“Well, never mind how it is,”", "\"뭐, 어떻든 상관없네.\""], ["answered Albert, “it comes to the same thing in the end.", "알베르가 대답했다. \"결국 다 그게 그거니까."], ["Perhaps by the time you return to Paris, I shall be quite a sober, staid father of a family!", "어쩌면 자네가 파리로 돌아올 무렵에는 내가 진중하고 점잖은 한 집안의 가장이 되어 있을지도 모르지!"], ["A most edifying representative I shall make of all the domestic virtues—don’t you think so?", "온갖 가정의 미덕을 더없이 교화적으로 보여 주는 본보기가 되어 있을 거야. 그렇게 생각지 않나?"], ["But as regards your wish to visit our fine city, my dear count, I can only say that you may command me and mine to any extent you please.”", "아무튼 친애하는 백작님, 우리 멋진 도시를 보고 싶으시다는 그 소망에 관해서는 이렇게만 말씀드리겠습니다. 저와 제 식구들을 마음껏 부리셔도 좋습니다.\""]], [["“Then it is settled,”", "\"그럼 정해진 거로군요.\""], ["said the count, “and I give you my solemn assurance that I only waited an opportunity like the present to realize plans that I have long meditated.”", "백작이 말했다. \"내 엄숙히 장담하건대, 오랫동안 곱씹어 온 계획을 실현할 기회로 바로 이런 기회를 기다려 왔습니다.\""]], [["Franz did not doubt that these plans were the same concerning which the count had dropped a few words in the grotto of Monte Cristo, and while the count was speaking the young man watched him closely, hoping to read something of his purpose in his face, but his countenance was inscrutable especially when, as in the present case, it was veiled in a sphinx-like smile.", "프란츠는 이 계획이 백작이 몬테크리스토섬 동굴에서 몇 마디 흘린 적 있던 바로 그 계획이리라는 것을 의심치 않았다. 백작이 말하는 동안 젊은이는 그 얼굴에서 속내를 조금이라도 읽어 내기를 바라며 그를 유심히 살폈다. 하지만 그의 표정은 헤아릴 수 없었다. 특히 지금처럼 스핑크스 같은 미소에 가려져 있을 때는 더욱 그러했다."]], [["“But tell me now, count,”", "\"그런데 말씀해 주십시오, 백작님,\""], ["exclaimed Albert, delighted at the idea of having to chaperon so distinguished a person as Monte Cristo; “tell me truly whether you are in earnest, or if this project of visiting Paris is merely one of the chimerical and uncertain air castles of which we make so many in the course of our lives, but which, like a house built on the sand, is liable to be blown over by the first puff of wind?”", "몬테크리스토처럼 출중한 인물의 안내자 노릇을 하게 되었다는 생각에 들뜬 알베르가 외쳤다. \"정말 진심으로 하시는 말씀인지, 아니면 이 파리 방문 계획이 우리가 살면서 숱하게 짓는 그 허황되고 막연한 공중누각 가운데 하나일 뿐인지, 솔직히 말씀해 주십시오. 모래 위에 지은 집처럼 첫 바람 한 줄기에 날아가 버릴 그런 것 말입니다.\""]], [["“I pledge you my honor,”", "\"내 명예를 걸고 맹세하지요.\""], ["returned the count, “that I mean to do as I have said; both inclination and positive necessity compel me to visit Paris.”", "백작이 대답했다. \"말한 그대로 실행할 작정입니다. 내키는 마음에서나, 분명한 필요에서나, 나는 파리에 가야만 합니다.\""]], [["“When do you propose going thither?”", "\"언제쯤 가실 생각이십니까?\""]], [["“Have you made up your mind when you shall be there yourself?”", "\"그대는 언제 그곳에 가 있을지 마음을 정했습니까?\""]], [["“Certainly I have; in a fortnight or three weeks’ time, that is to say, as fast as I can get there!”", "\"물론 정했지요. 두 주나 세 주 안에요. 다시 말해 갈 수 있는 한 빨리요!\""]], [["“Nay,”", "\"아니지요.\""], ["said the Count; “I will give you three months ere I join you; you see I make an ample allowance for all delays and difficulties.", "백작이 말했다. \"그대와 합류하기까지 석 달의 말미를 두겠습니다. 보다시피 온갖 지체와 난관을 넉넉히 감안해 두는 거지요.\""]], [["“And in three months’ time,”", "\"그럼 석 달 뒤에는,\""], ["said Albert, “you will be at my house?”", "알베르가 말했다. \"제 집으로 오시는 겁니까?\""]], [["“Shall we make a positive appointment for a particular day and hour?”", "\"특정한 날과 시각을 딱 잡아 약속을 정해 둘까요?\""], ["inquired the count; “only let me warn you that I am proverbial for my punctilious exactitude in keeping my engagements.”", "백작이 물었다. \"다만 한 가지 일러 두자면, 나는 약속을 빈틈없이 지키기로 정평이 난 사람입니다.\""]], [["“Day for day, hour for hour,”", "\"날은 날대로, 시각은 시각대로 정하지요.\""], ["said Albert; “that will suit me to a dot.”", "알베르가 말했다. \"그게 제게는 딱 좋습니다.\""]], [["“So be it, then,”", "\"그럼 그렇게 합시다.\""], ["replied the count, and extending his hand towards a calendar, suspended near the chimney-piece, he said, “today is the 21st of February;”", "백작이 대답하며 벽난로 곁에 걸린 달력 쪽으로 손을 뻗었다. \"오늘이 2월 21일이군요.\""], ["and drawing out his watch, added, “it is exactly half-past ten o’clock.", "그러고는 회중시계를 꺼내며 덧붙였다. \"지금이 정확히 열 시 반입니다."], ["Now promise me to remember this, and expect me the 21st of May at the same hour in the forenoon.”", "자, 이걸 기억해 두겠다고 약속하십시오. 5월 21일, 오전 같은 시각에 나를 기다리시면 됩니다.\""]], [["“Capital!”", "\"더할 나위 없군요!\""], ["exclaimed Albert; “your breakfast shall be waiting.”", "알베르가 외쳤다. \"아침 식사를 준비해 두고 기다리겠습니다.\""]], [["“Where do you live?”", "\"어디에 사십니까?\""]], [["“No. 27, Rue du Helder.”", "\"엘데르가 27번지입니다.\""]], [["“Have you bachelor’s apartments there?", "\"거기 독신자용 거처가 따로 있습니까?"], ["I hope my coming will not put you to any inconvenience.”", "제가 가는 것이 그대에게 불편을 끼치지 않았으면 합니다만.\""]], [["“I reside in my father’s house, but occupy a pavilion at the farther side of the courtyard, entirely separated from the main building.”", "\"저는 아버지 댁에서 지내지만, 안마당 저쪽 끝에 본채와 완전히 떨어진 별채를 쓰고 있습니다.\""]], [["“Quite sufficient,” replied the count, as, taking out his tablets, he wrote down “No.", "\"그거면 충분하지요.\" 백작이 대답하며 수첩을 꺼내 적어 넣었다."], ["27, Rue du Helder, 21st May, half-past ten in the morning.”", "\"엘데르가 27번지, 5월 21일, 오전 열 시 반.\""]], [["“Now then,”", "\"자, 그럼,\""], ["said the count, returning his tablets to his pocket, “make yourself perfectly easy; the hand of your time-piece will not be more accurate in marking the time than myself.”", "백작이 수첩을 다시 주머니에 넣으며 말했다. \"마음 푹 놓으십시오. 그대의 시계 바늘도 나만큼 정확하게 시각을 가리키지는 못할 겁니다.\""]], [["“Shall I see you again ere my departure?”", "\"떠나기 전에 다시 뵐 수 있을까요?\""], ["asked Albert.", "알베르가 물었다."]], [["“That depends; when do you leave?”", "\"그건 사정에 따라 다르지요. 언제 떠납니까?\""]], [["“Tomorrow evening, at five o’clock.”", "\"내일 저녁 다섯 시입니다.\""]], [["“In that case I must say adieu to you, as I am compelled to go to Naples, and shall not return hither before Saturday evening or Sunday morning.", "\"그렇다면 작별 인사를 드려야겠군요. 나는 나폴리에 가야 하는데, 토요일 저녁이나 일요일 아침이 되어야 이리로 돌아올 테니까요."], ["And you, baron,”", "그리고 남작,\""], ["pursued the count, addressing Franz, “do you also depart tomorrow?”", "백작이 프란츠에게로 말을 돌리며 이었다. \"그대도 내일 떠납니까?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“For France?”", "\"프랑스로요?\""]], [["“No, for Venice; I shall remain in Italy for another year or two.”", "\"아닙니다, 베네치아로 갑니다. 한두 해는 더 이탈리아에 머물 작정입니다.\""]], [["“Then we shall not meet in Paris?”", "\"그럼 우리는 파리에서 만나지 못하겠군요?\""]], [["“I fear I shall not have that honor.”", "\"안타깝게도 그런 영광은 누리지 못할 듯합니다.\""]], [["“Well, since we must part,”", "\"자, 헤어져야 한다니,\""], ["said the count, holding out a hand to each of the young men, “allow me to wish you both a safe and pleasant journey.”", "백작이 두 젊은이에게 각각 한 손씩을 내밀며 말했다. \"두 분 모두 무사하고 즐거운 여행이 되기를 빌겠습니다.\""]], [["It was the first time the hand of Franz had come in contact with that of the mysterious individual before him, and unconsciously he shuddered at its touch, for it felt cold and icy as that of a corpse.", "프란츠의 손이 눈앞의 이 신비로운 인물의 손에 닿은 것은 그때가 처음이었다. 그는 그 손길에 자기도 모르게 몸을 떨었다. 시신의 손처럼 차갑고 얼음장 같았던 것이다."]], [["20219m\n“Let us understand each other,”", "\"서로 분명히 해 두지요.\""], ["said Albert; “it is agreed—is it not?—that you are to be at No.", "알베르가 말했다. \"정한 거지요?"], ["27, in the Rue du Helder, on the 21st of May, at half-past ten in the morning, and your word of honor passed for your punctuality?”", "5월 21일 오전 열 시 반에 엘데르가 27번지에 와 주시고, 시간을 어기지 않겠다고 명예를 걸어 약속하신 거고요?\""]], [["“The 21st of May, at half-past ten in the morning, Rue du Helder, No. 27,”", "\"5월 21일 오전 열 시 반, 엘데르가 27번지.\""], ["replied the count.", "백작이 대답했다."]], [["The young men then rose, and bowing to the count, quitted the room.", "그러자 두 젊은이는 자리에서 일어나 백작에게 인사하고 방을 나섰다."]], [["“What is the matter?”", "\"무슨 일인가?\""], ["asked Albert of Franz, when they had returned to their own apartments; “you seem more than commonly thoughtful.”", "자기들 거처로 돌아오자 알베르가 프란츠에게 물었다. \"자네 평소보다 생각이 많은 얼굴인데.\""]], [["“I will confess to you, Albert,”", "\"솔직히 말하겠네, 알베르.\""], ["replied Franz, “the count is a very singular person, and the appointment you have made to meet him in Paris fills me with a thousand apprehensions.”", "프란츠가 대답했다. \"백작은 아주 기이한 사람이야. 자네가 파리에서 그를 만나기로 한 그 약속이 나를 천 가지 불안으로 가득 채우는군.\""]], [["“My dear fellow,”", "\"이 사람아,\""], ["exclaimed Albert, “what can there possibly be in that to excite uneasiness?", "알베르가 외쳤다. \"그게 뭐 불안해할 거리라도 된단 말인가?"], ["Why, you must have lost your senses.”", "자네 제정신이 아닌 모양일세.\""]], [["“Whether I am in my senses or not,”", "\"내가 제정신이든 아니든,\""], ["answered Franz, “that is the way I feel.”", "프란츠가 대답했다. \"내 느낌이 그렇다는 거야.\""]], [["“Listen to me, Franz,”", "\"내 말 좀 들어 보게, 프란츠.\" 알베르가 말했다."], ["said Albert; “I am glad that the occasion has presented itself for saying this to you, for I have noticed how cold you are in your bearing towards the count, while he, on the other hand, has always been courtesy itself to us.", "\"마침 이런 말을 할 기회가 생겨서 다행이군. 자네가 백작을 대하는 태도가 어찌나 냉랭한지 진작부터 눈여겨봤거든. 반대로 그쪽은 우리에게 늘 깍듯하기 그지없었는데 말이야."], ["Have you anything particular against him?”", "그에게 무슨 특별히 거슬리는 점이라도 있나?\""]], [["“Possibly.”", "\"어쩌면.\""]], [["“Did you ever meet him previously to coming hither?”", "\"여기 오기 전에 그를 만난 적이 있었나?\""]], [["“I have.”", "\"있어.\""]], [["“And where?”", "\"어디서?\""]], [["“Will you promise me not to repeat a single word of what I am about to tell you?”", "\"내가 지금부터 하는 말을 한마디도 입 밖에 내지 않겠다고 약속하겠나?\""]], [["“I promise.”", "\"약속하지.\""]], [["“Upon your honor?”", "\"명예를 걸고?\""]], [["“Upon my honor.”", "\"명예를 걸고.\""]], [["“Then listen to me.”", "\"그럼 들어 보게.\""]], [["Franz then related to his friend the history of his excursion to the Island of Monte Cristo and of his finding a party of smugglers there, and the two Corsican bandits with them.", "이어서 프란츠는 몬테크리스토섬으로 나들이를 갔던 이야기, 거기서 밀수업자 무리와 그들과 함께 있던 코르시카 도적 둘을 만난 일을 친구에게 들려주었다."], ["He dwelt with considerable force and energy on the almost magical hospitality he had received from the count, and the magnificence of his entertainment in the grotto of the _Thousand and One Nights_.", "그는 백작에게서 받은 거의 마법 같던 환대와, 천일야화의 동굴에서 누린 그 호화로운 접대를 상당한 힘과 열의를 실어 자세히 이야기했다."]], [["He recounted, with circumstantial exactitude, all the particulars of the supper, the hashish, the statues, the dream, and how, at his awakening, there remained no proof or trace of all these events, save the small yacht, seen in the distant horizon driving under full sail toward Porto-Vecchio.", "만찬, 대마초, 조각상들, 꿈, 그리고 눈을 떴을 때 그 모든 일의 증거나 흔적이라곤 멀리 수평선 위에서 돛을 활짝 펴고 포르토베키오 쪽으로 내달리던 작은 요트밖에 남아 있지 않았다는 것까지, 그는 빠짐없이 정확하게 자초지종을 늘어놓았다."]], [["Then he detailed the conversation overheard by him at the Colosseum, between the count and Vampa, in which the count had promised to obtain the release of the bandit Peppino,—an engagement which, as our readers are aware, he most faithfully fulfilled.", "이어 그는 콜로세움에서 우연히 엿들은 백작과 밤파 사이의 대화를 자세히 전했다. 그 대화에서 백작은 도적 페피노를 풀려나게 해 주겠다고 약속했고, 독자들도 알다시피 그 약속을 더없이 충실하게 지켰다."]], [["At last he arrived at the adventure of the preceding night, and the embarrassment in which he found himself placed by not having sufficient cash by six or seven hundred piastres to make up the sum required, and finally of his application to the count and the picturesque and satisfactory result that followed.", "마침내 그는 전날 밤의 일에 다다랐다. 필요한 액수를 채우기에 육칠백 피아스트르가 모자라 현금이 부족했던 곤혹스러운 처지, 그리고 끝내 백작에게 도움을 청했던 일과 그 뒤에 따라온 인상적이고도 흡족한 결말까지 이야기했다."], ["Albert listened with the most profound attention.", "알베르는 더없이 깊은 주의를 기울여 귀를 기울였다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래,\""], ["said he, when Franz had concluded, “what do you find to object to in all you have related?", "프란츠가 이야기를 마치자 그가 말했다. \"자네가 지금까지 늘어놓은 그 모든 이야기에서 대체 무엇이 거슬린다는 건가?"], ["The count is fond of travelling, and, being rich, possesses a vessel of his own.", "백작은 여행을 좋아하고, 부자이니 자기 배도 한 척 가지고 있는 거지."], ["Go but to Portsmouth or Southampton, and you will find the harbors crowded with the yachts belonging to such of the English as can afford the expense, and have the same liking for this amusement.", "포츠머스나 사우샘프턴에 한번 가 보게. 그 비용을 감당할 만하고 이런 유희를 똑같이 즐기는 영국인들의 요트로 항구가 빼곡할 테니까. 그러니까 나들이를 다니는 동안 쉴 곳을 마련하려고, 또 지난 넉 달 동안 나를 어떻게든 독살하려 들던 그 형편없는 요리를 피하려고 말이야."], ["Now, by way of having a resting-place during his excursions, avoiding the wretched cookery—which has been trying its best to poison me during the last four months, while you have manfully resisted its effects for as many years,—and obtaining a bed on which it is possible to slumber, Monte Cristo has furnished for himself a temporary abode where you first found him; but, to prevent the possibility of the Tuscan government taking a fancy to his enchanted palace, and thereby depriving him of the advantages naturally expected from so large an outlay of capital, he has wisely enough purchased the island, and taken its name.", "자네는 그걸 그만큼 여러 해를 사내답게 견뎌 냈지만 말이지. 아무튼 잠이라도 청할 만한 침대를 얻으려고 몬테크리스토는 자네가 그를 처음 발견한 그곳에 임시 거처를 꾸민 거야. 다만 토스카나 정부가 그 마법의 궁전을 탐내서, 그렇게 막대한 자본을 쏟아부은 데서 으레 기대할 만한 이득을 빼앗아 갈 가능성을 막으려고, 그는 아주 현명하게도 그 섬을 사들이고 그 이름까지 취한 거지."], ["Just ask yourself, my good fellow, whether there are not many persons of our acquaintance who assume the names of lands and properties they never in their lives were masters of?”", "자, 곰곰이 생각해 보게, 이 사람아. 우리가 아는 이들 중에도 평생 한 번도 주인이었던 적 없는 땅이며 영지의 이름을 갖다 쓰는 사람이 한둘이 아니지 않나?\""]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["said Franz, “the Corsican bandits that were among the crew of his vessel?”", "프란츠가 말했다. \"그 배의 선원들 틈에 끼어 있던 코르시카 도적들은 어쩌고?\""]], [["“Why, really the thing seems to me simple enough.", "\"아니, 정말이지 그건 내가 보기엔 아주 간단한 일일세. 코르시카의 도적들이 악당이나 도둑이 아니라는 건 누구보다 자네가 잘 알지 않나. 그들은 그저 어떤 불길한 사정에 떠밀려 고향 마을에서 쫓겨난 도망자일 뿐이고, 그들과 어울린다고 해서 망신이나 오명이 따라붙는 것도 아니야."], ["Nobody knows better than yourself that the bandits of Corsica are not rogues or thieves, but purely and simply fugitives, driven by some sinister motive from their native town or village, and that their fellowship involves no disgrace or stigma; for my own part, I protest that, should I ever go to Corsica, my first visit, ere even I presented myself to the mayor or prefect, should be to the bandits of Colomba, if I could only manage to find them; for, on my conscience, they are a race of men I admire greatly.”", "나로 말하자면, 단언컨대 언젠가 코르시카에 가게 된다면 시장이나 도지사를 찾아뵙기에 앞서 제일 먼저 콜롱바의 도적들부터 만나러 갈 걸세. 어떻게든 그들을 찾아낼 수만 있다면 말이야. 정말이지 그들은 내가 크게 흠모하는 부류의 사람들이거든.\""]], [["“Still,”", "\"그래도,\""], ["persisted Franz, “I suppose you will allow that such men as Vampa and his band are regular villains, who have no other motive than plunder when they seize your person.", "프란츠가 굽히지 않고 말했다. \"밤파와 그 패거리 같은 자들은 영락없는 악당들이라는 건 자네도 인정하겠지. 사람을 붙잡아 두는 데 약탈 말고는 다른 동기가 없는 자들이잖나."], ["How do you explain the influence the count evidently possessed over those ruffians?”", "그런데 백작이 그 불한당들에게 분명히 행사한 그 영향력은 자네 대체 어떻게 설명할 텐가?\""]], [["“My good friend, as in all probability I own my present safety to that influence, it would ill become me to search too closely into its source; therefore, instead of condemning him for his intimacy with outlaws, you must give me leave to excuse any little irregularity there may be in such a connection; not altogether for preserving my life, for my own idea was that it never was in much danger, but certainly for saving me 4,000 piastres, which, being translated, means neither more nor less than 24,000 livres of our money—a sum at which, most assuredly, I should never have been estimated in France, proving most indisputably,” added Albert with a laugh, “that no prophet is honored in his own country.”", "\"이 사람아, 십중팔구 내가 지금 무사한 것이 바로 그 영향력 덕분일 텐데, 그 출처를 너무 깊이 캐는 건 나로서는 도리가 아니지. 그러니 그가 무법자들과 가까이 지낸다고 비난할 게 아니라, 그런 교제에 다소 떳떳지 못한 구석이 있더라도 너그러이 봐줄 수 있게 해 주게. 꼭 내 목숨을 건져 줘서만은 아니야. 내 목숨이 그리 큰 위험에 처했던 적은 없었다는 게 내 생각이니까. 하지만 적어도 내게서 4천 피아스트르를 절약해 준 건 분명하지. 그걸 환산하면 우리 돈으로 딱 2만 4천 리브르야. 단언컨대 프랑스에서라면 내 몸값이 결코 그만큼으로 매겨지지는 않았을 테고,\" 알베르가 웃으며 덧붙였다. \"이건 어떤 예언자도 제 고향에서는 대접받지 못한다는 걸 더없이 명백하게 증명해 주는 셈이지.\""]], [["“Talking of countries,”", "\"나라 이야기가 나왔으니 말인데,\""], ["replied Franz, “of what country is the count, what is his native tongue, whence does he derive his immense fortune, and what were those events of his early life—a life as marvellous as unknown—that have tinctured his succeeding years with so dark and gloomy a misanthropy?", "프란츠가 대꾸했다. \"백작은 대체 어느 나라 사람이며, 모국어는 무엇이고, 그 막대한 재산은 어디서 나온 것이며, 그토록 어둡고 음울한 인간 혐오로 이후의 세월을 물들여 놓은 그 젊은 날의 사건들은 무엇이었을까? 알려진 바 없으면서도 경이롭기 그지없는 그 삶 말이야."], ["Certainly these are questions that, in your place, I should like to have answered.”", "정말이지 자네 처지라면 나는 이런 물음들에 답을 듣고 싶을 텐데.\""]], [["“My dear Franz,”", "\"친애하는 프란츠,\" 알베르가 대답했다."], ["replied Albert, “when, upon receipt of my letter, you found the necessity of asking the count’s assistance, you promptly went to him, saying, ‘My friend Albert de Morcerf is in danger; help me to deliver him.’ Was not that nearly what you said?”", "\"내 편지를 받고 백작의 도움을 청할 필요가 있다 싶었을 때, 자네는 곧장 그에게 달려가 이렇게 말했지. '제 친구 알베르 드 모르세르가 위험에 처했습니다. 그를 구해 내도록 도와주십시오.'"], ["20222m", "자네가 한 말이 대충 그런 거였잖나?\""]], [["“It was.”", "\"그랬지.\""]], [["“Well, then, did he ask you, ‘Who is M.", "\"자, 그럼 그가 자네에게 이렇게 물었나?"], ["Albert de Morcerf? how does he come by his name—his fortune?", "'알베르 드 모르세르 씨가 누구요? 그 이름은, 그 재산은 어떻게 얻은 것이오?"], ["what are his means of existence?", "무얼로 먹고사는 사람이오?"], ["what is his birthplace? of what country is he a native?’ Tell me, did he put all these questions to you?”", "태생은 어디고, 어느 나라 사람이오?' 말해 보게, 그가 자네에게 이런 질문들을 죄다 던지던가?\""]], [["“I confess he asked me none.”", "\"고백하건대 아무것도 묻지 않았네.\""]], [["“No; he merely came and freed me from the hands of Signor Vampa, where, I can assure you, in spite of all my outward appearance of ease and unconcern, I did not very particularly care to remain.", "\"그렇지. 그는 그저 와서 나를 밤파 나리의 손아귀에서 풀어 줬을 뿐이야. 장담하지만, 겉으로 아무리 태연하고 무심한 척했어도 나는 거기 더 붙잡혀 있고 싶은 마음이 조금도 없었거든."], ["Now, then, Franz, when, for services so promptly and unhesitatingly rendered, he but asks me in return to do for him what is done daily for any Russian prince or Italian nobleman who may pass through Paris—merely to introduce him into society—would you have me refuse?", "자, 그러니 프란츠, 그토록 신속하고 망설임 없이 도움을 베푼 대가로 그가 내게 청한 거라곤, 파리를 지나는 러시아 공작이나 이탈리아 귀족을 위해 날마다 하는 그 일, 그러니까 그저 사교계에 소개해 달라는 것뿐인데, 자네라면 그걸 거절하겠나?"], ["My good fellow, you must have lost your senses to think it possible I could act with such cold-blooded policy.”", "이 사람아, 내가 그토록 냉혈한 같은 잇속을 차리며 굴 수 있으리라 생각하다니, 자네 제정신이 아닌 게 분명하네.\""]], [["And this time it must be confessed that, contrary to the usual state of affairs in discussions between the young men, the effective arguments were all on Albert’s side.", "그리고 이번만큼은 인정해야 했다. 두 젊은이 사이의 논쟁에서 으레 벌어지던 상황과는 반대로, 설득력 있는 논거는 모조리 알베르 쪽에 있었다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"좋네.\""], ["said Franz with a sigh, “do as you please my dear viscount, for your arguments are beyond my powers of refutation.", "프란츠가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"마음대로 하게, 친애하는 자작. 자네 논거는 내가 반박할 수 있는 범위를 넘어섰으니까."], ["Still, in spite of all, you must admit that this Count of Monte Cristo is a most singular personage.”", "그래도 무엇보다, 이 몬테크리스토 백작이 더없이 기이한 인물이라는 것만큼은 자네도 인정해야 하네.\""]], [["“He is a philanthropist,”", "\"그는 박애주의자야.\""], ["answered the other; “and no doubt his motive in visiting Paris is to compete for the Monthyon prize, given, as you are aware, to whoever shall be proved to have most materially advanced the interests of virtue and humanity.", "상대가 대답했다. \"그가 파리에 가는 동기도 분명 몽티옹 상을 노려서일 테지. 자네도 알다시피 미덕과 인류의 이익을 가장 실질적으로 증진시켰다고 입증된 사람에게 주는 상 말이야."], ["If my vote and interest can obtain it for him, I will readily give him the one and promise the other.", "내 한 표와 영향력으로 그가 그 상을 받을 수 있다면, 나는 기꺼이 한 표를 던지고 영향력도 약속하겠네."], ["And now, my dear Franz, let us talk of something else.", "자, 친애하는 프란츠, 이제 다른 이야기를 하세."], ["Come, shall we take our luncheon, and then pay a last visit to St. Peter’s?”", "어떤가, 점심이나 들고 나서 성 베드로 성당에 마지막으로 한 번 가 볼까?\""]], [["Franz silently assented; and the following afternoon, at half-past five o’clock, the young men parted.", "프란츠는 말없이 동의했다. 그리고 이튿날 오후 다섯 시 반, 두 젊은이는 헤어졌다."], ["Albert de Morcerf to return to Paris, and Franz d’Épinay to pass a fortnight at Venice.", "알베르 드 모르세르는 파리로 돌아가고, 프란츠 데피네는 베네치아에서 두 주를 보내기 위해서였다."]], [["But, ere he entered his travelling carriage, Albert, fearing that his expected guest might forget the engagement he had entered into, placed in the care of a waiter at the hotel a card to be delivered to the Count of Monte Cristo, on which, beneath the name of Viscount Albert de Morcerf, he had written in pencil:", "다만 여행 마차에 오르기 전, 알베르는 자기가 기다릴 손님이 맺어 둔 약속을 잊을까 염려되어, 호텔의 종업원에게 명함 한 장을 맡기며 몬테크리스토 백작에게 전해 달라고 부탁했다. 그 명함에는 알베르 드 모르세르 자작이라는 이름 아래 연필로 이렇게 적혀 있었다."]], [["“27, _Rue du Helder, on the_ 21_st May, half-past ten_ A.M.”", "\"엘데르가 27번지, 5월 21일 오전 열 시 반.\""]], [["# Chapter 39. The Guests", "# Chapter 39. The Guests"]], [["In the house in the Rue du Helder, where Albert had invited the Count of Monte Cristo, everything was being prepared on the morning of the 21st of May to do honor to the occasion.", "알베르가 몬테크리스토 백작을 초대한 엘데르가의 저택에서는, 5월 21일 아침부터 그 자리를 빛내기 위한 만반의 준비가 갖추어지고 있었다."], ["Albert de Morcerf inhabited a pavilion situated at the corner of a large court, and directly opposite another building, in which were the servants’ apartments.", "알베르 드 모르세르는 넓은 안뜰 한 모퉁이에 자리한 별채에 살았는데, 그 맞은편에는 하인들의 거처가 있는 또 다른 건물이 곧장 마주 서 있었다."], ["Two windows only of the pavilion faced the street; three other windows looked into the court, and two at the back into the garden.", "별채의 창문 가운데 거리를 향한 것은 둘뿐이었고, 셋은 안뜰을, 뒤편의 둘은 정원을 내다보고 있었다."]], [["Between the court and the garden, built in the heavy style of the imperial architecture, was the large and fashionable dwelling of the Count and Countess of Morcerf.", "안뜰과 정원 사이에는, 제정 양식의 묵직한 풍취로 지어진 모르세르 백작 부부의 크고 화려한 저택이 서 있었다."]], [["A high wall surrounded the whole of the property, surmounted at intervals by vases filled with flowers, and broken in the centre by a large gate of gilded iron, which served as the carriage entrance.", "높은 담장이 저택 전체를 둘러싸고 있었고, 군데군데 꽃을 가득 담은 화분들이 그 위에 얹혀 있었으며, 한가운데는 금빛 철문이 담을 갈라 마차 출입구 노릇을 했다."], ["A small door, close to the lodge of the _concierge_, gave ingress and egress to the servants and masters when they were on foot.", "문지기 막사 가까이에는 작은 문이 하나 더 있어, 걸어서 드나드는 하인들과 주인들이 그리로 나고 들었다."]], [["It was easy to discover that the delicate care of a mother, unwilling to part from her son, and yet aware that a young man of the viscount’s age required the full exercise of his liberty, had chosen this habitation for Albert.", "이 거처를 알베르에게 골라 준 것은, 아들과 떨어지기를 원치 않으면서도 자작 또래의 젊은이라면 자유를 한껏 누려야 한다는 것을 아는 어머니의 섬세한 배려였음을 어렵지 않게 짐작할 수 있었다."], ["There were not lacking, however, evidences of what we may call the intelligent egoism of a youth who is charmed with the indolent, careless life of an only son, and who lives as it were in a gilded cage.", "그러나 외아들의 나른하고 거리낌 없는 삶에 매료되어, 말하자면 황금 새장 속에서 살아가는 젊은이의 이른바 영리한 이기심을 보여 주는 흔적도 적지 않았다."], ["By means of the two windows looking into the street, Albert could see all that passed; the sight of what is going on is necessary to young men, who always want to see the world traverse their horizon, even if that horizon is only a public thoroughfare.", "거리를 내다보는 두 창문을 통해 알베르는 바깥에서 벌어지는 모든 일을 볼 수 있었다. 무슨 일이 일어나는지 지켜보는 것은 젊은이들에게 꼭 필요한 일이다. 그 지평선이 한낱 공공 대로에 지나지 않더라도, 그들은 늘 세상이 제 시야를 가로질러 가는 모습을 보고 싶어 하니까."], ["Then, should anything appear to merit a more minute examination, Albert de Morcerf could follow up his researches by means of a small gate, similar to that close to the _concierge’s_ door, and which merits a particular description.", "그러다 좀 더 면밀히 살펴볼 만한 것이 나타나면, 알베르 드 모르세르는 문지기 옆문과 비슷한 작은 쪽문을 통해 그 탐색을 이어 갈 수 있었는데, 그 문은 따로 설명해 둘 만하다."]], [["It was a little entrance that seemed never to have been opened since the house was built, so entirely was it covered with dust and dirt; but the well-oiled hinges and locks told quite another story.", "그것은 집이 지어진 이래로 한 번도 열린 적이 없는 듯한 작은 문이었다. 먼지와 때가 어찌나 빈틈없이 뒤덮고 있었던지 말이다. 그러나 기름이 잘 칠해진 경첩과 자물쇠는 전혀 다른 이야기를 들려주었다."], ["This door was a mockery to the _concierge_, from whose vigilance and jurisdiction it was free, and, like that famous portal in the _Arabian Nights_, opening at the “_Sesame_”", "이 문은 문지기를 우롱하는 존재였다. 그의 감시와 관할에서 벗어나 있었으니까. 그리고 알리바바의 \"열려라 참깨\"라는 주문에 열리던 그 유명한 「천일야화」"], ["of Ali Baba, it was wont to swing backward at a cabalistic word or a concerted tap from without from the sweetest voices or whitest fingers in the world.", "속의 문처럼, 세상에서 가장 감미로운 목소리나 가장 새하얀 손가락이 바깥에서 보내는 은밀한 주문이나 약속된 두드림에 맞추어 안쪽으로 스르르 열리곤 했다."]], [["At the end of a long corridor, with which the door communicated, and which formed the antechamber, was, on the right, Albert’s breakfast-room, looking into the court, and on the left the salon, looking into the garden.", "그 문과 통하며 응접실 노릇을 하는 긴 복도 끝에는, 오른쪽으로 안뜰을 내다보는 알베르의 식당이 있었고, 왼쪽으로는 정원을 향한 살롱이 있었다."], ["Shrubs and creeping plants covered the windows, and hid from the garden and court these two apartments, the only rooms into which, as they were on the ground floor, the prying eyes of the curious could penetrate.", "관목과 덩굴식물이 창문을 뒤덮어, 정원과 안뜰로부터 이 두 방을 가려 주었다. 1층에 있는 까닭에 호기심 많은 이들의 엿보는 눈길이 들여다볼 수 있는 방은 오직 이 둘뿐이었다."]], [["On the floor above were similar rooms, with the addition of a third, formed out of the antechamber; these three rooms were a salon, a boudoir, and a bedroom.", "위층에도 비슷한 방들이 있었는데, 거기에 응접실을 터서 만든 세 번째 방이 더해져 있었다. 이 세 방은 살롱과 부두아르와 침실이었다."], ["The salon downstairs was only an Algerian divan, for the use of smokers.", "아래층 살롱은 흡연객들을 위한 알제리식 긴 의자 하나가 전부였다."], ["The boudoir upstairs communicated with the bedchamber by an invisible door on the staircase; it was evident that every precaution had been taken.", "위층 부두아르는 계단 쪽으로 난 보이지 않는 문을 통해 침실과 통했다. 모든 경계가 빈틈없이 갖추어져 있음이 분명했다."], ["Above this floor was a large _atelier_, which had been increased in size by pulling down the partitions—a pandemonium, in which the artist and the dandy strove for pre-eminence.", "이 층 위에는 칸막이를 헐어 넓힌 큰 화실이 있었으니, 예술가와 멋쟁이가 서로 우위를 다투는 아수라장이었다."]], [["There were collected and piled up all Albert’s successive caprices, hunting-horns, bass-viols, flutes—a whole orchestra, for Albert had had not a taste but a fancy for music; easels, palettes, brushes, pencils—for music had been succeeded by painting; foils, boxing-gloves, broadswords, and single-sticks—for, following the example of the fashionable young men of the time, Albert de Morcerf cultivated, with far more perseverance than music and drawing, the three arts that complete a dandy’s education, i.e., fencing, boxing, and single-stick; and it was here that he received Grisier, Cooks, and Charles Leboucher.", "거기에는 알베르의 변덕이 차례로 거쳐 간 물건들이 죄다 모여 쌓여 있었다. 사냥용 뿔피리, 베이스비올, 플루트 등 오케스트라 한 무리가 다 있었으니, 알베르가 음악에 진정한 취미라기보다는 한때의 변덕을 품었던 까닭이었다. 이젤과 팔레트, 붓과 연필도 있었으니, 음악의 뒤를 그림이 이었던 것이다. 펜싱 검과 권투 장갑, 폭이 넓은 검과 단봉도 있었으니, 당대 멋쟁이 젊은이들의 본을 따라 알베르 드 모르세르가 음악이나 그림보다 훨씬 끈기 있게 갈고닦은, 멋쟁이의 교양을 완성하는 세 가지 기예, 곧 펜싱과 권투와 단봉술 때문이었다. 그리고 그가 그리지에, 쿡스, 샤를 르부셰를 맞이하던 곳이 바로 여기였다."]], [["The rest of the furniture of this privileged apartment consisted of old cabinets, filled with Chinese porcelain and Japanese vases, Lucca della Robbia _faïences_, and Palissy platters; of old armchairs, in which perhaps had sat Henry IV. or Sully, Louis XIII.", "이 특별한 방의 나머지 가구로는, 중국 도자기와 일본 화병, 루카 델라 로비아의 도기와 팔리시의 접시로 가득 찬 낡은 장식장들이 있었다. 그리고 어쩌면 앙리 4세나 쉴리, 루이 13세나 리슐리외가 앉았을지도 모를 낡은 안락의자들이 있었다."], ["or Richelieu—for two of these armchairs, adorned with a carved shield, on which were engraved the fleur-de-lis of France on an azure field, evidently came from the Louvre, or, at least, some royal residence.", "그중 두 개는 푸른 바탕에 프랑스 왕가의 백합 문장이 새겨진 방패꼴 장식으로 꾸며져 있었는데, 분명 루브르궁이나 적어도 어느 왕실 거처에서 나온 것이었다."]], [["Over these dark and sombre chairs were thrown splendid stuffs, dyed beneath Persia’s sun, or woven by the fingers of the women of Calcutta or of Chandernagor.", "이 어둡고 침침한 의자들 위에는, 페르시아의 햇살 아래 물들였거나 캘커타나 샹데르나고르 여인들의 손길로 짠 화려한 직물들이 걸쳐져 있었다."], ["What these stuffs did there, it was impossible to say; they awaited, while gratifying the eyes, a destination unknown to their owner himself; in the meantime they filled the place with their golden and silky reflections.", "이 직물들이 거기서 무엇을 하고 있는지는 도무지 알 수 없었다. 그것들은 눈을 즐겁게 하면서도, 주인 자신조차 알지 못하는 용처를 기다리고 있었다. 그러는 동안 그것들은 금빛과 비단빛 광채로 방 안을 가득 채우고 있었다."]], [["In the centre of the room was a Roller and Blanchet “baby grand” piano in rosewood, but holding the potentialities of an orchestra in its narrow and sonorous cavity, and groaning beneath the weight of the _chefs-d’œuvre_ of Beethoven, Weber, Mozart, Haydn, Grétry, and Porpora.", "방 한가운데에는 자단으로 만든 롤러앤블랑셰 사의 작은 그랜드피아노가 놓여 있었다. 비좁으면서도 울림 좋은 그 공동 안에 오케스트라의 모든 가능성을 품은 채, 베토벤과 베버, 모차르트와 하이든, 그레트리와 포르포라의 걸작들 무게에 짓눌려 신음하고 있었다."]], [["On the walls, over the doors, on the ceiling, were swords, daggers, Malay creeses, maces, battle-axes; gilded, damasked, and inlaid suits of armor; dried plants, minerals, and stuffed birds, their flame-colored wings outspread in motionless flight, and their beaks forever open.", "벽에도, 문 위에도, 천장에도 검과 단검, 말레이의 크리스 단도, 철퇴와 전투용 도끼가 걸려 있었다. 금박을 입히고 다마스쿠스식으로 무늬를 새기고 상감 세공을 한 갑옷들이 있었고, 말린 식물과 광물, 그리고 박제된 새들이 있었다. 불꽃 빛깔의 날개를 활짝 편 채 미동 없는 날갯짓을 멈추고, 부리는 영원히 벌린 모습이었다."], ["This was Albert’s favorite lounging place.", "이곳이 알베르가 가장 좋아하는 휴식처였다."]], [["However, the morning of the appointment, the young man had established himself in the small salon downstairs.", "그러나 약속이 있는 그날 아침, 젊은이는 아래층의 작은 살롱에 자리를 잡았다. 그곳에는 탁자 하나가 있었고, 그 둘레로 조금 떨어져 크고 호화로운 긴 의자가 놓여 있었다. 탁자 위에는 알려진 온갖 종류의 담배가, 페테르부르크의 노란 담배에서 시나이의 검은 담배에 이르기까지, 또 메릴랜드와 푸에르토리코를 거쳐 라타키아에 이르기까지 죽 펼쳐져 있었다."], ["There, on a table, surrounded at some distance by a large and luxurious divan, every species of tobacco known,—from the yellow tobacco of Petersburg to the black of Sinai, and so on along the scale from Maryland and Porto Rico, to Latakia,—was exposed in pots of crackled earthenware of which the Dutch are so fond; beside them, in boxes of fragrant wood, were ranged, according to their size and quality, puros, regalias, havanas, and manillas; and, in an open cabinet, a collection of German pipes, of chibouques, with their amber mouth-pieces ornamented with coral, and of narghiles, with their long tubes of morocco, awaiting the caprice or the sympathy of the smokers.", "네덜란드인들이 그토록 좋아하는 잔금 간 도기 항아리들에 담긴 채였다. 그 곁에는 향기로운 나무로 만든 상자들 속에 푸로, 레갈리아, 아바나, 마닐라 시가가 크기와 품질에 따라 가지런히 정리되어 있었다. 그리고 열린 장식장 안에는 독일제 파이프와, 호박 물부리에 산호 장식을 단 치부크, 그리고 모로코가죽으로 만든 긴 관이 달린 물담뱃대들이 한데 모여, 흡연객들의 변덕이나 취향을 기다리고 있었다."]], [["Albert had himself presided at the arrangement, or, rather, the symmetrical derangement, which, after coffee, the guests at a breakfast of modern days love to contemplate through the vapor that escapes from their mouths, and ascends in long and fanciful wreaths to the ceiling.", "알베르는 이 배치, 아니 차라리 대칭을 이룬 흐트러뜨림을 직접 주관했는데, 요즘 사람들은 아침 식사 자리에서 커피를 마신 뒤 이 풍경을, 입에서 새어 나와 길고 환상적인 고리를 그리며 천장으로 피어오르는 연기 사이로 바라보기를 즐겼다."]], [["At a quarter to ten, a valet entered; he composed, with a little groom named John, and who only spoke English, all Albert’s establishment, although the cook of the hotel was always at his service, and on great occasions the count’s _chasseur_ also.", "10시 15분 전, 하인 하나가 들어왔다. 그는 영어밖에 할 줄 모르는 존이라는 어린 마부와 더불어 알베르가 부리는 사람의 전부였다. 물론 호텔 요리사가 늘 그의 시중을 들 채비가 되어 있었고, 큰 행사가 있을 때는 백작의 사냥꾼까지 거들기는 했지만 말이다."], ["This valet, whose name was Germain, and who enjoyed the entire confidence of his young master, held in one hand a number of papers, and in the other a packet of letters, which he gave to Albert.", "제르맹이라는 이름의 이 하인은 젊은 주인의 두터운 신임을 받고 있었는데, 한 손에는 신문 몇 부를, 다른 손에는 편지 한 묶음을 들고 와 알베르에게 건넸다."], ["Albert glanced carelessly at the different missives, selected two written in a small and delicate hand, and enclosed in scented envelopes, opened them and perused their contents with some attention.", "알베르는 여러 통의 서신을 무심히 훑어보다가, 작고 섬세한 필체로 쓰여 향내 나는 봉투에 담긴 두 통을 골라내, 그것들을 뜯어 사뭇 주의 깊게 내용을 읽었다."]], [["“How did these letters come?” said he.", "\"이 편지들은 어떻게 왔지?\" 그가 물었다."]], [["“One by the post, Madame Danglars’ footman left the other.”", "\"한 통은 우편으로 왔고, 다른 한 통은 당글라르 부인의 하인이 두고 갔습니다.\""]], [["“Let Madame Danglars know that I accept the place she offers me in her box.", "\"당글라르 부인께, 마련해 주신 관람석 자리를 받겠다고 전해라."], ["Wait; then, during the day, tell Rosa that when I leave the Opera I will sup with her as she wishes.", "잠깐, 그리고 낮 동안에 로자에게, 오페라가 끝나면 그녀가 바라는 대로 함께 밤참을 들겠다고 일러라."], ["Take her six bottles of different wine—Cyprus, sherry, and Malaga, and a barrel of Ostend oysters; get them at Borel’s, and be sure you say they are for me.”", "그녀에게 서로 다른 포도주 여섯 병을 가져가라. 키프로스, 셰리, 말라가 술로, 그리고 오스텐드 굴 한 통도. 보렐 가게에서 사고, 내 것이라고 꼭 말하도록 해라.\""]], [["“At what o’clock, sir, do you breakfast?” 20227m", "\"식사는 몇 시에 하시겠습니까, 도련님?\""]], [["“What time is it now?”", "\"지금 몇 시지?\""]], [["“A quarter to ten.”", "\"10시 15분 전입니다.\""]], [["“Very well, at half past ten.", "\"좋아, 10시 반에 하겠다."], ["Debray will, perhaps, be obliged to go to the minister—and besides”", "드브레는 어쩌면 장관에게 가 봐야 할지도 모르니까. 게다가,\""], ["(Albert looked at his tablets), “it is the hour I told the count, 21st May, at half past ten; and though I do not much rely upon his promise, I wish to be punctual.", "알베르가 수첩을 들여다보며 말했다. \"내가 백작에게 말해 둔 시각이 5월 21일 10시 반이고, 그의 약속을 그리 믿지는 않지만 나는 시간을 지키고 싶거든."], ["Is the countess up yet?”", "백작 부인은 일어나셨느냐?\""]], [["“If you wish, I will inquire.”", "\"원하신다면 여쭙고 오겠습니다.\""]], [["“Yes, ask her for one of her _liqueur_ cellarets, mine is incomplete; and tell her I shall have the honor of seeing her about three o’clock, and that I request permission to introduce someone to her.”", "\"그래, 그분의 리큐어 술상자 하나를 빌려 달라고 여쭤라. 내 것은 다 갖춰지지 않았으니. 그리고 3시쯤 찾아뵙는 영광을 누리겠다고, 또 어떤 분을 소개해 드리고 싶으니 허락을 구한다고 전해라.\""]], [["The valet left the room.", "하인이 방을 나갔다."], ["Albert threw himself on the divan, tore off the cover of two or three of the papers, looked at the theatre announcements, made a face seeing they gave an opera, and not a ballet; hunted vainly amongst the advertisements for a new tooth-powder of which he had heard, and threw down, one after the other, the three leading papers of Paris, muttering,", "알베르는 긴 의자에 몸을 던지고, 신문 두세 부의 띠지를 뜯어내 극장 광고를 들여다보더니, 발레가 아니라 오페라를 한다는 것을 보고 얼굴을 찡그렸다. 광고 사이를 뒤져 소문으로 들은 새 치약을 찾았으나 헛수고였고, 파리의 주요 신문 세 부를 하나씩 차례로 내던지며 중얼거렸다."]], [["“These papers become more and more stupid every day.”", "\"이 신문들, 날이 갈수록 점점 더 멍청해지는군.\""]], [["A moment after, a carriage stopped before the door, and the servant announced M. Lucien Debray.", "잠시 후 마차 한 대가 문 앞에 멈추었고, 하인이 뤼시앵 드브레 씨가 왔음을 알렸다."], ["A tall young man, with light hair, clear gray eyes, and thin and compressed lips, dressed in a blue coat with beautifully carved gold buttons, a white neckcloth, and a tortoiseshell eye-glass suspended by a silken thread, and which, by an effort of the superciliary and zygomatic muscles, he fixed in his eye, entered, with a half-official air, without smiling or speaking.", "옅은 머리칼에 맑은 잿빛 눈, 얇고 꼭 다문 입술을 한 키 큰 젊은이가 들어왔다. 아름답게 새긴 금단추가 달린 푸른 코트에 흰 넥타이를 매고, 비단실에 매단 거북등딱지 안경을 눈썹과 광대 근육의 힘을 빌려 눈에 끼운 채였다. 그는 반쯤 공무를 보는 듯한 태도로, 웃지도 말하지도 않고 들어섰다."]], [["“Good-morning, Lucien, good-morning,”", "\"안녕한가, 뤼시앵, 안녕.\" 알베르가 말했다."], ["said Albert; “your punctuality really alarms me. What do I say?", "\"자네의 시간 엄수에 정말 깜짝 놀랐네. 무슨 소릴 하는 거지?"], ["punctuality!", "시간 엄수라니!"], ["You, whom I expected last, you arrive at five minutes to ten, when the time fixed was half-past!", "가장 늦게 올 줄 알았던 자네가, 정해진 시각이 10시 반인데 10시 5분 전에 도착하다니!"], ["Has the ministry resigned?”", "내각이 사퇴라도 했나?\""]], [["“No, my dear fellow,”", "\"아닐세, 친구.\" 젊은이가 긴 의자에 앉으며 대답했다."], ["returned the young man, seating himself on the divan; “reassure yourself; we are tottering always, but we never fall, and I begin to believe that we shall pass into a state of immobility, and then the affairs of the Peninsula will completely consolidate us.”", "\"안심하게. 우리는 늘 휘청거리지만 결코 쓰러지지는 않으니까. 나는 이제 우리가 부동의 상태로 접어들 것이고, 그러면 이베리아반도 문제가 우리를 완전히 굳건히 다져 줄 거라고 믿기 시작했네.\""]], [["“Ah, true; you drive Don Carlos out of Spain.”", "\"아, 맞아. 자네들이 돈 카를로스를 에스파냐에서 쫓아내는군.\""]], [["“No, no, my dear fellow, do not confound our plans.", "\"아니, 아니, 친구, 우리 계획을 헷갈리지 말게."], ["We take him to the other side of the French frontier, and offer him hospitality at Bourges.”", "우리는 그를 프랑스 국경 너머로 데려가, 부르주에서 환대를 베풀 거라네.\""]], [["“At Bourges?”", "\"부르주에서?\""]], [["“Yes, he has not much to complain of; Bourges is the capital of Charles VII.", "\"그래, 그는 불평할 게 별로 없어. 부르주는 샤를 7세의 수도였으니까."], ["Do you not know that all Paris knew it yesterday, and the day before it had already transpired on the Bourse, and M. Danglars (I do not know by what means that man contrives to obtain intelligence as soon as we do) made a million!”", "어제 파리 전체가 그 소식을 알았고, 그 전날 이미 증권가에 새어 나갔으며, 당글라르 씨가(그자가 무슨 수로 우리만큼이나 빨리 정보를 손에 넣는지는 모르겠네만) 백만을 벌었다는 걸 자네는 모르나?\""]], [["“And you another order, for I see you have a blue ribbon at your button-hole.”", "\"그리고 자네는 훈장 하나를 더 받았고. 단춧구멍에 푸른 리본을 단 게 보이니 말일세.\""]], [["“Yes; they sent me the order of Charles III.,”", "\"그래, 샤를 3세 훈장을 보내왔더군.\""], ["returned Debray carelessly.", "드브레가 무심하게 대답했다."]], [["“Come, do not affect indifference, but confess you were pleased to have it.”", "\"이봐, 무관심한 척하지 말고, 받아서 기뻤다고 털어놓게.\""]], [["“Oh, it is very well as a finish to the toilet.", "\"오, 차림새의 마무리로는 아주 그만이지."], ["It looks very neat on a black coat buttoned up.”", "단추를 채운 검은 코트에 무척 단정하게 어울리거든.\""]], [["“And makes you resemble the Prince of Wales or the Duke of Reichstadt.”", "\"그리고 자네를 웨일스 공이나 라이히슈타트 공작처럼 보이게 해 주고.\""]], [["“It is for that reason you see me so early.”", "\"내가 이렇게 일찍 온 게 바로 그 때문일세.\""]], [["“Because you have the order of Charles III., and you wish to announce the good news to me?”", "\"샤를 3세 훈장을 받았으니, 내게 그 기쁜 소식을 알리고 싶어서?\""]], [["“No, because I passed the night writing letters,—five-and-twenty despatches.", "\"아니, 밤새도록 편지를 쓰느라 그랬네. 전보를 스물다섯 통이나 보냈지."], ["I returned home at daybreak, and strove to sleep; but my head ached and I got up to have a ride for an hour.", "동틀 녘에 집에 돌아와 자려 했지만 머리가 지끈거려서, 일어나 한 시간쯤 말을 타러 나갔네."], ["At the Bois de Boulogne, _ennui_ and hunger attacked me at once,—two enemies who rarely accompany each other, and who are yet leagued against me, a sort of Carlo-republican alliance.", "불로뉴 숲에서 권태와 허기가 한꺼번에 나를 덮쳤지. 좀처럼 함께 다니지 않는 두 적이건만, 나를 상대로 손을 잡았으니, 일종의 카를로스파와 공화파의 동맹이라고나 할까."], ["I then recollected you gave a breakfast this morning, and here I am.", "그러다 자네가 오늘 아침 식사를 차린다는 게 떠올라 이렇게 온 걸세."], ["I am hungry, feed me; I am bored, amuse me.”", "나는 배고프니 먹여 주게. 나는 따분하니 즐겁게 해 주고.\""]], [["“It is my duty as your host,”", "\"주인 된 도리지.\""], ["returned Albert, ringing the bell, while Lucien turned over, with his gold-mounted cane, the papers that lay on the table.", "알베르가 종을 울리며 대답했다. 그동안 뤼시앵은 금 손잡이 단장으로 탁자 위에 놓인 신문들을 뒤적였다."], ["“Germain, a glass of sherry and a biscuit.", "\"제르맹, 셰리 한 잔과 비스킷 좀 가져오너라."], ["In the meantime, my dear Lucien, here are cigars—contraband, of course—try them, and persuade the minister to sell us such instead of poisoning us with cabbage leaves.”", "그동안, 친애하는 뤼시앵, 여기 시가가 있네. 물론 밀수품이지. 한번 피워 보고, 우리를 양배추 잎으로 중독시키는 대신 이런 걸 팔도록 장관을 설득해 보게.\""]], [["“_Peste!_ I will do nothing of the kind; the moment they come from government you would find them execrable.", "\"맙소사! 그런 짓은 절대 안 할 걸세. 정부에서 나오는 순간 자네는 그것들이 형편없다고 느낄 테니까."], ["Besides, that does not concern the home but the financial department.", "게다가 그건 내무부가 아니라 재무부 소관일세."], ["Address yourself to M. Humann, section of the indirect contributions, corridor A., No. 26.”", "위만 씨에게 가서 말하게. 간접세 부서, A 복도 26호일세.\""]], [["“On my word,”", "\"이런,\""], ["said Albert, “you astonish me by the extent of your knowledge.", "알베르가 말했다. \"자네 박식함에 놀랐네."], ["Take a cigar.”", "시가나 한 대 들게.\""]], [["“Really, my dear Albert,”", "\"정말이지, 친애하는 알베르,\""], ["replied Lucien, lighting a manilla at a rose-colored taper that burnt in a beautifully enamelled stand—“how happy you are to have nothing to do.", "뤼시앵이 아름다운 법랑 받침대에서 타오르는 장밋빛 양초로 마닐라 시가에 불을 붙이며 대답했다. \"할 일이 없다는 게 자네는 얼마나 행복한지 모를 걸세."], ["You do not know your own good fortune!”", "자네는 제 행운을 모르고 있어!\""]], [["“And what would you do, my dear diplomatist,”", "\"그럼 자네는 어쩔 텐가, 친애하는 외교관 양반,\""], ["replied Morcerf, with a slight degree of irony in his voice, “if you did nothing? What?", "모르세르가 목소리에 약간의 비꼼을 담아 대꾸했다. \"할 일이 없다면 말일세. 뭐라고?"], ["private secretary to a minister, plunged at once into European cabals and Parisian intrigues; having kings, and, better still, queens, to protect, parties to unite, elections to direct; making more use of your cabinet with your pen and your telegraph than Napoleon did of his battle-fields with his sword and his victories; possessing five-and-twenty thousand francs a year, besides your place; a horse, for which Château-Renaud offered you four hundred louis, and which you would not part with; a tailor who never disappoints you; with the opera, the jockey-club, and other diversions, can you not amuse yourself? Well, I will amuse you.”", "장관의 개인 비서로 유럽의 음모와 파리의 술책에 단번에 뛰어들고, 왕들을, 아니 그보다 더한 왕비들을 비호하고, 당파를 결속시키고, 선거를 지휘하고, 펜과 전보로 집무실에서 부려 먹는 것이 나폴레옹이 검과 승리로 전장에서 부린 것보다 더하며, 봉급 말고도 연 2만 5천 프랑을 가졌고, 샤토르노가 4백 루이를 주겠다고 했어도 내놓지 않은 말을 가졌고, 자네를 결코 실망시키지 않는 재단사를 두었고, 오페라와 자키 클럽과 그 밖의 오락거리까지 있는데, 자네는 즐길 줄을 모른단 말인가? 좋아, 내가 즐겁게 해 주지.\""]], [["“How?”", "\"어떻게?\""]], [["“By introducing to you a new acquaintance.”", "\"새로운 사람을 한 명 소개해 줌으로써 말이야.\""]], [["“A man or a woman?”", "\"남자인가, 여자인가?\""]], [["“A man.”", "\"남자일세.\""]], [["“I know so many men already.”", "\"남자라면 이미 너무 많이 알고 있는데.\""]], [["“But you do not know this man.”", "\"하지만 이 사람은 모를 걸세.\""]], [["“Where does he come from—the end of the world?”", "\"그자는 어디서 왔나? 세상 끝에서?\""]], [["“Farther still, perhaps.”", "\"어쩌면 그보다 더 먼 곳에서.\""]], [["“The deuce! I hope he does not bring our breakfast with him.”", "\"이런! 그가 우리 아침거리를 함께 가져오지는 않았으면 좋겠군.\""]], [["“Oh, no; our breakfast comes from my father’s kitchen.", "\"오, 아닐세. 우리 아침은 아버지 댁 주방에서 오니까."], ["Are you hungry?”", "시장한가?\""]], [["“Humiliating as such a confession is, I am.", "\"이런 고백을 하자니 부끄럽네만, 그래."], ["But I dined at M. de Villefort’s, and lawyers always give you very bad dinners.", "어제 빌포르 씨 댁에서 저녁을 먹었는데, 법조인들은 늘 형편없는 만찬을 차리거든."], ["You would think they felt some remorse; did you ever remark that?”", "마치 무슨 가책이라도 느끼는가 싶어. 자네도 그런 걸 눈치챈 적 있나?\""]], [["“Ah, depreciate other persons’ dinners; you ministers give such splendid ones.”", "\"아, 남의 만찬을 깎아내리는군. 자네들 장관 양반들은 그토록 훌륭한 걸 차린다 이거지.\""]], [["“Yes; but we do not invite people of fashion.", "\"그래, 하지만 우리는 멋쟁이들을 초대하지는 않네."], ["If we were not forced to entertain a parcel of country boobies because they think and vote with us, we should never dream of dining at home, I assure you.”", "시골뜨기 한 무리가 우리와 같은 생각으로 우리에게 표를 준다는 이유로 그들을 대접해야 하는 처지만 아니라면, 집에서 만찬을 차릴 생각은 꿈에도 안 할 걸세, 정말이야.\""]], [["“Well, take another glass of sherry and another biscuit.”", "\"자, 셰리 한 잔 더, 비스킷도 하나 더 들게.\""]], [["“Willingly.", "\"기꺼이."], ["Your Spanish wine is excellent.", "자네의 에스파냐 포도주는 일품이군."], ["You see we were quite right to pacify that country.”", "보게, 우리가 그 나라를 평정한 게 아주 옳은 일이었다니까.\""]], [["“Yes; but Don Carlos?”", "\"그래, 그런데 돈 카를로스는?\""]], [["“Well, Don Carlos will drink Bordeaux, and in ten years we will marry his son to the little queen.”", "\"흠, 돈 카를로스는 보르도 술을 마실 테고, 십 년 뒤에는 그의 아들을 어린 왕비와 결혼시킬 걸세.\""]], [["“You will then obtain the Golden Fleece, if you are still in the ministry.”", "\"그러면 자네가 아직 내각에 있다면 황금양모 훈장을 받겠군.\""]], [["“I think, Albert, you have adopted the system of feeding me on smoke this morning.”", "\"알베르, 자네 오늘 아침 나를 연기로 배 채워 주기로 작정한 모양이군.\""]], [["“Well, you must allow it is the best thing for the stomach; but I hear Beauchamp in the next room; you can dispute together, and that will pass away the time.”", "\"뭐, 그게 위장에는 제일 좋은 거라고 인정하게. 그건 그렇고, 옆방에서 보샹 목소리가 들리는군. 자네들끼리 논쟁이나 하게. 그러면 시간이 잘 갈 테니.\""]], [["“About what?”", "\"무엇에 대해서?\""]], [["“About the papers.”", "\"신문에 대해서지.\""]], [["“My dear friend,”", "\"이보게,\""], ["said Lucien with an air of sovereign contempt, “do I ever read the papers?”", "뤼시앵이 더없이 도도한 경멸의 기색으로 말했다. \"내가 신문 따위를 읽을 성싶은가?\""]], [["“Then you will dispute the more.”", "\"그렇다면 더더욱 논쟁이 붙겠군.\""]], [["“M. Beauchamp,”", "\"보샹 씨 오셨습니다.\""], ["announced the servant.", "하인이 알렸다."], ["“Come in, come in,”", "\"들어오게, 들어와.\""], ["said Albert, rising and advancing to meet the young man.", "알베르가 일어나 젊은이를 맞으러 나아가며 말했다. \"여기 드브레가 있네."], ["“Here is Debray, who detests you without reading you, so he says.”", "읽지도 않으면서 자네를 몹시 싫어한다는군. 본인 말로는 말이야.\""]], [["“He is quite right,”", "\"지당한 말일세.\""], ["returned Beauchamp; “for I criticise him without knowing what he does.", "보샹이 대답했다. \"나도 그가 무슨 일을 하는지 모르면서 그를 비판하니까."], ["Good-day, commander!”", "안녕하신가, 사령관!\""]], [["“Ah, you know that already,” said the private secretary, smiling and shaking hands with him.", "\"아, 자네 벌써 알았군.\" 개인 비서가 미소를 지으며 그와 악수했다."]], [["“_Pardieu!_”", "\"당연하지!\""]], [["“And what do they say of it in the world?”", "\"그래, 세상에선 그걸 두고 뭐라던가?\""]], [["“In which world?", "\"어느 세상 말인가?"], ["we have so many worlds in the year of grace 1838.”", "1838년이라는 해에 우리에겐 세상이 어찌나 많은지.\""]], [["“In the entire political world, of which you are one of the leaders.”", "\"자네가 지도자 중 하나인 정계 전체 말일세.\""]], [["“They say that it is quite fair, and that sowing so much red, you ought to reap a little blue.”", "\"아주 공정한 일이라더군. 그렇게 붉은 것을 잔뜩 뿌렸으니, 푸른 것을 조금쯤 거둘 만하다고 말이야.\""]], [["“Come, come, that is not bad!”", "\"이런, 이런, 제법인걸!\""], ["said Lucien. “Why do you not join our party, my dear Beauchamp?", "뤼시앵이 말했다. \"어째서 우리 당에 들어오지 않나, 친애하는 보샹?"], ["With your talents you would make your fortune in three or four years.”", "자네의 재능이라면 삼사 년 안에 한밑천 잡을 텐데.\""]], [["“I only await one thing before following your advice; that is, a minister who will hold office for six months.", "\"자네 충고를 따르기 전에 딱 한 가지만 기다리고 있다네. 바로 여섯 달은 자리를 지킬 장관일세. 친애하는 알베르, 한마디만 하지."], ["My dear Albert, one word, for I must give poor Lucien a respite.", "가엾은 뤼시앵을 좀 쉬게 해 줘야겠으니. 우리 아침을 먹나, 저녁을 먹나?"], ["Do we breakfast or dine?", "나는 의회에 가 봐야 하네."], ["I must go to the Chamber, for our life is not an idle one.”", "우리 삶이라는 게 한가하지가 않아서 말일세.\""]], [["“You only breakfast; I await two persons, and the instant they arrive we shall sit down to table.”", "\"자네는 아침만 들게. 두 사람을 더 기다리는 중인데, 그들이 도착하는 즉시 식탁에 앉을 걸세.\""]], [["# Chapter 40. The Breakfast", "# Chapter 40. The Breakfast"]], [["And what sort of persons do you expect to breakfast?”", "\"그래, 어떤 사람들이 아침을 먹으러 온다는 건가?\""], ["said Beauchamp.", "보샹이 물었다."]], [["“A gentleman, and a diplomatist.”", "\"신사 한 사람과 외교관 한 사람일세.\""]], [["“Then we shall have to wait two hours for the gentleman, and three for the diplomatist.", "\"그렇다면 신사는 두 시간, 외교관은 세 시간을 기다려야겠군."], ["I shall come back to dessert; keep me some strawberries, coffee, and cigars.", "나는 디저트 시간에 다시 오겠네. 딸기와 커피, 시가를 좀 남겨 두게."], ["I shall take a cutlet on my way to the Chamber.”", "의회로 가는 길에 커틀릿이나 하나 먹고 가지.\""]], [["“Do not do anything of the sort; for were the gentleman a Montmorency, and the diplomatist a Metternich, we will breakfast at eleven; in the meantime, follow Debray’s example, and take a glass of sherry and a biscuit.”", "\"그러지 말게. 신사가 몽모랑시 가문 사람이고 외교관이 메테르니히라 해도, 우리는 열한 시에는 식사를 할 테니까. 그때까지는 드브레를 본받아서 셰리주 한 잔에 비스킷이나 들게나.\""]], [["“Be it so; I will stay; I must do something to distract my thoughts.”", "\"좋아, 그럼 남겠네. 머릿속 생각을 떨쳐 낼 일이라도 좀 해야지.\""]], [["“You are like Debray, and yet it seems to me that when the minister is out of spirits, the opposition ought to be joyous.”", "\"자네도 드브레와 똑같군. 그런데 내가 보기엔, 장관이 풀이 죽어 있을 때면 야당은 기뻐해야 마땅한 거 아닌가.\""]], [["“Ah, you do not know with what I am threatened.", "\"아, 자네는 내가 어떤 협박에 시달리는지 모르는군."], ["I shall hear this morning that M. Danglars make a speech at the Chamber of Deputies, and at his wife’s this evening I shall hear the tragedy of a peer of France.", "오늘 아침에는 당글라르 씨가 하원에서 연설을 한다는 얘기를 들어야 하고, 저녁에는 그의 부인 댁에서 프랑스 상원 의원의 비극을 들어야 하네."], ["The devil take the constitutional government, and since we had our choice, as they say, at least, how could we choose that?”", "입헌 정부라는 게 악마한테나 잡혀가라지. 그리고 말마따나 우리가 직접 골랐다는데, 어떻게 그런 걸 고를 수 있었던 건지 원.\""]], [["“I understand; you must lay in a stock of hilarity.”", "\"무슨 말인지 알겠네. 자네는 웃을 거리를 잔뜩 비축해 둬야겠어.\""]], [["“Do not run down M. Danglars’ speeches,”", "\"당글라르 씨의 연설을 깎아내리지 말게.\""], ["said Debray; “he votes for you, for he belongs to the opposition.”", "드브레가 말했다. \"그는 자네 편에 표를 던지지. 야당 소속이니까.\""]], [["“_Pardieu_, that is exactly the worst of all.", "\"제기랄, 그게 바로 무엇보다 고약한 점이라네."], ["I am waiting until you send him to speak at the Luxembourg, to laugh at my ease.”", "마음 편히 웃으려면, 자네가 그를 뤽상부르 궁으로 보내 연설하게 만들 때까지 기다려야 한다는 게.\""]], [["“My dear friend,”", "\"이보게,\""], ["said Albert to Beauchamp, “it is plain that the affairs of Spain are settled, for you are most desperately out of humor this morning.", "알베르가 보샹에게 말했다. \"에스파냐 문제가 정리됐다는 게 분명하군. 오늘 아침 자네 기분이 그렇게 험악한 걸 보면 말일세."], ["Recollect that Parisian gossip has spoken of a marriage between myself and Mlle.", "파리의 입방아꾼들이 나와 외제니 당글라르 양의 결혼 이야기를 떠들었다는 걸 잊지 말게."], ["Eugénie Danglars; I cannot in conscience, therefore, let you run down the speeches of a man who will one day say to me, ‘Vicomte, you know I give my daughter two millions.’”", "그러니 양심상 나는 자네가 어느 날 내게 '자작, 자네도 알다시피 내 딸에게 2백만을 주겠네'라고 말할 사람의 연설을 깎아내리도록 내버려 둘 수가 없네.\""]], [["“Ah, this marriage will never take place,”", "\"아, 그 결혼은 결코 성사되지 않을 거야.\""], ["said Beauchamp.", "보샹이 말했다."], ["“The king has made him a baron, and can make him a peer, but he cannot make him a gentleman, and the Count of Morcerf is too aristocratic to consent, for the paltry sum of two million francs, to a _mésalliance_.", "\"왕은 그를 남작으로 만들었고, 상원 의원으로도 만들 수 있겠지. 하지만 신사로 만들 수는 없네. 그리고 모르세르 백작은 너무 귀족적이라 고작 2백만 프랑이라는 하찮은 돈 때문에 격에 안 맞는 결혼에 동의하지 않을 걸세."], ["The Viscount of Morcerf can only wed a marchioness.”", "모르세르 자작은 후작 부인급하고나 결혼할 수 있어.\""]], [["“But two million francs make a nice little sum,”", "\"하지만 2백만 프랑이면 제법 쏠쏠한 액수지.\""], ["replied Morcerf.", "모르세르가 대꾸했다."]], [["“It is the social capital of a theatre on the boulevard, or a railroad from the Jardin des Plantes to La Râpée.”", "\"그건 큰길가에 극장 하나를 세울 자본금이거나, 식물원에서 라라페까지 철도를 놓을 돈이야.\""]], [["“Never mind what he says, Morcerf,”", "\"저 친구 말은 신경 쓰지 말게, 모르세르.\""], ["said Debray, “do you marry her.", "드브레가 말했다. \"그냥 그녀와 결혼하게."], ["You marry a money-bag label, it is true; well, but what does that matter?", "돈주머니에 붙은 상표와 결혼하는 셈인 건 사실이지. 하지만 그게 무슨 상관인가?"], ["It is better to have a blazon less and a figure more on it.", "문장(紋章) 하나가 줄고 그 위에 숫자 하나가 느는 게 더 낫지."], ["You have seven martlets on your arms; give three to your wife, and you will still have four; that is one more than M.", "자네 가문 문장에는 제비가 일곱 마리 있잖나. 그중 셋을 아내에게 주게. 그래도 자네에겐 넷이 남아."], ["de Guise had, who so nearly became King of France, and whose cousin was Emperor of Germany.”", "그건 기즈 공보다 하나 많은 거야. 그는 하마터면 프랑스 왕이 될 뻔했고, 그 사촌은 독일 황제였지.\""]], [["“On my word, I think you are right, Lucien,”", "\"이런, 자네 말이 맞는 것 같군, 뤼시앵.\""], ["said Albert absently.", "알베르가 멍하니 말했다."]], [["“To be sure; besides, every millionaire is as noble as a bastard—that is, he can be.”", "\"물론이지. 게다가 백만장자라면 누구나 사생아만큼이나 고귀하지. 그러니까, 고귀해질 수 있다는 말일세.\""]], [["“Do not say that, Debray,”", "\"그런 말은 하지 말게, 드브레.\""], ["returned Beauchamp, laughing, “for here is Château-Renaud, who, to cure you of your mania for paradoxes, will pass the sword of Renaud de Montauban, his ancestor, through your body.”", "보샹이 웃으며 대꾸했다. \"여기 샤토르노가 있으니, 자네의 그 역설 중독을 고쳐 주려고 제 조상 르노 드 몽토방의 검으로 자네 몸을 꿰뚫을 테니까.\""]], [["“He will sully it then,”", "\"그러면 그 검을 더럽히는 셈이지.\""], ["returned Lucien; “for I am low—very low.”", "뤼시앵이 대꾸했다. \"나는 비천한 몸이니까. 아주 비천하지.\""]], [["“Oh, heavens,”", "\"이런, 맙소사.\""], ["cried Beauchamp, “the minister quotes Béranger, what shall we come to next?”", "보샹이 외쳤다. \"장관이 베랑제를 인용하다니, 다음엔 또 무슨 일이 벌어질까?\""]], [["“M. de Château-Renaud—M. Maximilian Morrel,”", "\"샤토르노 백작님, 그리고 막시밀리앙 모렐 대위님이십니다.\""], ["said the servant, announcing two fresh guests.", "하인이 새 손님 두 사람을 알리며 말했다."]], [["“Now, then, to breakfast,”", "\"자, 그럼 이제 아침을 들지.\""], ["said Beauchamp; “for, if I remember, you told me you only expected two persons, Albert.”", "보샹이 말했다. \"내 기억이 맞다면, 알베르, 자네는 손님이 둘뿐이라고 했잖나.\""]], [["“Morrel,”", "\"모렐이라.\""], ["muttered Albert—“Morrel—who is he?”", "알베르가 중얼거렸다. \"모렐이라니, 그게 누구지?\""]], [["But before he had finished, M. de Château-Renaud, a handsome young man of thirty, gentleman all over,—that is, with the figure of a Guiche and the wit of a Mortemart,—took Albert’s hand.", "그러나 그가 말을 채 끝내기도 전에, 서른 살가량의 잘생긴 청년이자 머리끝부터 발끝까지 신사인 샤토르노가, 그러니까 기슈의 풍채에 모르트마르의 재치를 지닌 사내가 알베르의 손을 잡았다."]], [["“My dear Albert,”", "\"친애하는 알베르,\""], ["said he, “let me introduce to you M.", "그가 말했다. \"막시밀리앙 모렐 대위를 소개하지."], ["Maximilian Morrel, captain of Spahis, my friend; and what is more—however the man speaks for himself—my preserver.", "스파이 기병 대위이자 내 친구일세. 그리고 그 이상이지. 본인 입으로 직접 말하긴 뭣하지만, 내 생명의 은인이라네."], ["Salute my hero, viscount.”", "내 영웅에게 인사하게, 자작.\""]], [["And he stepped on one side to give place to a young man of refined and dignified bearing, with large and open brow, piercing eyes, and black moustache, whom our readers have already seen at Marseilles, under circumstances sufficiently dramatic not to be forgotten.", "그러고는 한쪽으로 비켜서며 한 청년에게 자리를 내주었다. 세련되고 위엄 있는 자태에, 넓고 트인 이마와 꿰뚫는 듯한 눈, 검은 콧수염을 지닌 사내였다. 우리 독자들은 마르세유에서, 잊을 수 없을 만큼 극적인 상황 속에서 이미 그를 본 적이 있다."], ["A rich uniform, half French, half Oriental, set off his graceful and stalwart figure, and his broad chest was decorated with the order of the Legion of Honor.", "반은 프랑스풍, 반은 동방풍의 화려한 제복이 그의 우아하고 다부진 몸매를 돋보이게 했고, 떡 벌어진 가슴에는 레지옹 도뇌르 훈장이 달려 있었다."], ["The young officer bowed with easy and elegant politeness.", "젊은 장교는 편안하면서도 우아한 예의로 고개를 숙였다."]], [["“Monsieur,”", "\"선생,\""], ["said Albert with affectionate courtesy, “the count of Château-Renaud knew how much pleasure this introduction would give me; you are his friend, be ours also.”", "알베르가 다정한 정중함을 담아 말했다. \"샤토르노 백작은 이 소개가 제게 얼마나 큰 기쁨을 줄지 잘 알고 있었습니다. 당신은 그의 친구이니, 우리의 친구도 되어 주십시오.\""]], [["“Well said,”", "\"옳은 말이야.\""], ["interrupted Château-Renaud; “and pray that, if you should ever be in a similar predicament, he may do as much for you as he did for me.”", "샤토르노가 끼어들었다. \"그리고 혹시 자네가 언젠가 비슷한 곤경에 처한다면, 그가 내게 해 준 만큼 자네에게도 해 주기를 기도하게.\""]], [["“What has he done?”", "\"그가 무슨 일을 했나?\""], ["asked Albert.", "알베르가 물었다."]], [["“Oh, nothing worth speaking of,”", "\"오, 입에 올릴 만한 일도 아닙니다.\""], ["said Morrel; “M.", "모렐이 말했다."], ["de Château-Renaud exaggerates.”", "\"샤토르노 씨가 과장하는 겁니다.\""]], [["“Not worth speaking of?”", "\"입에 올릴 만한 일이 아니라고?\""], ["cried Château-Renaud; “life is not worth speaking of!—that is rather too philosophical, on my word, Morrel.", "샤토르노가 외쳤다. \"사람 목숨이 입에 올릴 만한 일이 아니라니! 정말이지 그건 너무 철학적인 소리야, 모렐."], ["It is very well for you, who risk your life every day, but for me, who only did so once——”", "날마다 목숨을 거는 자네한테야 괜찮은 말이겠지만, 평생에 단 한 번 그랬던 나로서는……\""]], [["“We gather from all this, baron, that Captain Morrel saved your life.”", "\"이 모든 얘기로 미루어 보면, 남작, 모렐 대위가 자네 목숨을 구했다는 거군.\""]], [["“Exactly so.”", "\"바로 그렇다네.\""]], [["“On what occasion?”", "\"어떤 상황에서?\""], ["asked Beauchamp.", "보샹이 물었다."]], [["“Beauchamp, my good fellow, you know I am starving,”", "\"보샹, 이 사람아, 내가 배가 고파 죽을 지경인 거 자네도 알잖나.\""], ["said Debray: “do not set him off on some long story.”", "드브레가 말했다. \"긴 이야기를 늘어놓게 만들지 말게.\""]], [["“Well, I do not prevent your sitting down to table,”", "\"음, 식탁에 앉는 걸 막진 않겠네.\" 보샹이 대꾸했다."], ["replied Beauchamp, “Château-Renaud can tell us while we eat our breakfast.”", "\"샤토르노가 아침을 먹는 동안 이야기해 줄 수 있을 테니까.\""]], [["“Gentlemen,”", "\"여러분,\""], ["said Morcerf, “it is only a quarter past ten, and I expect someone else.”", "모르세르가 말했다. \"이제 겨우 열 시 십오 분입니다. 한 사람을 더 기다리고 있어요.\""]], [["“Ah, true, a diplomatist!”", "\"아, 그렇지, 외교관!\""], ["observed Debray.", "드브레가 한마디 했다."]], [["“Diplomat or not, I don’t know; I only know that he charged himself on my account with a mission, which he terminated so entirely to my satisfaction, that had I been king, I should have instantly created him knight of all my orders, even had I been able to offer him the Golden Fleece and the Garter.”", "\"외교관인지 아닌지는 모르겠네. 다만 내가 아는 건, 그가 나를 위해 어떤 임무를 떠맡았고, 그걸 어찌나 만족스럽게 끝내 주었는지, 내가 왕이었다면 그 자리에서 내 모든 훈장의 기사로 임명했을 거라는 점이야. 황금양모 훈장이든 가터 훈장이든 줄 수만 있었다면 다 줬을 걸세.\""]], [["“Well, since we are not to sit down to table,”", "\"음, 어차피 식탁에 앉지 않을 거라면,\""], ["said Debray, “take a glass of sherry, and tell us all about it.”", "드브레가 말했다. \"셰리주나 한 잔 들면서 전부 얘기해 보게.\""]], [["“You all know that I had the fancy of going to Africa.”", "\"내가 아프리카에 가고 싶다는 변덕이 들었다는 건 다들 알고 있겠지.\""]], [["“It is a road your ancestors have traced for you,”", "\"그건 자네 조상들이 자네를 위해 닦아 놓은 길이지.\""], ["said Albert gallantly.", "알베르가 정중하게 말했다."]], [["“Yes? but I doubt that your object was like theirs—to rescue the Holy Sepulchre.”", "\"그런가? 하지만 자네 목적이 그들의 목적, 그러니까 성묘를 구하는 것과 같았는지는 의심스럽군.\""]], [["“You are quite right, Beauchamp,”", "\"자네 말이 옳네, 보샹.\""], ["observed the young aristocrat.", "젊은 귀족이 한마디 했다."], ["“It was only to fight as an amateur.", "\"그저 아마추어로서 싸워 보려는 것뿐이었어."], ["I cannot bear duelling ever since two seconds, whom I had chosen to arrange an affair, forced me to break the arm of one of my best friends, one whom you all know—poor Franz d’Épinay.”", "나는 결투를 견딜 수가 없게 됐거든. 언젠가 어떤 일을 정리하려고 입회인 둘을 뽑았는데, 그들이 내게 내 가장 친한 친구 중 하나의 팔을 부러뜨리게 했지. 자네들도 다 아는 친구, 가엾은 프란츠 데피네 말일세.\""]], [["“Ah, true,”", "\"아, 그렇지.\""], ["said Debray, “you did fight some time ago; about what?”", "드브레가 말했다. \"자네가 얼마 전에 결투를 했었지."], ["20235m", "무슨 일로?\""]], [["“The devil take me, if I remember,”", "\"빌어먹을, 기억이 나면 다행이게.\""], ["returned Château-Renaud.", "샤토르노가 대꾸했다."], ["“But I recollect perfectly one thing, that, being unwilling to let such talents as mine sleep, I wished to try upon the Arabs the new pistols that had been given to me.", "\"하지만 한 가지는 똑똑히 기억하네. 나 같은 재능을 썩혀 두기 싫어서, 누가 내게 선물한 새 권총들을 아랍인들에게 시험해 보고 싶었다는 거야."], ["In consequence I embarked for Oran, and went from thence to Constantine, where I arrived just in time to witness the raising of the siege.", "그래서 오랑으로 떠나, 거기서 콩스탕틴으로 갔지. 마침 포위가 풀리는 걸 목격할 만한 때에 도착했어."], ["I retreated with the rest, for eight-and-forty hours.", "나는 다른 이들과 함께 마흔여덟 시간 동안 퇴각했네."], ["I endured the rain during the day, and the cold during the night tolerably well, but the third morning my horse died of cold.", "낮에는 비를, 밤에는 추위를 그럭저럭 견뎌 냈지만, 사흘째 아침에 내 말이 추위로 죽고 말았네. 가엾은 짐승 같으니."], ["Poor brute—accustomed to be covered up and to have a stove in the stable, the Arabian finds himself unable to bear ten degrees of cold in Arabia.”", "늘 덮개를 두르고 마구간에 난로를 둔 채 지내던 아라비아산 말이라, 정작 아라비아에서 10도의 추위도 견디지 못한 거지.\""]], [["“That’s why you want to purchase my English horse,”", "\"그래서 자네가 내 영국산 말을 사고 싶어 하는 거군.\""], ["said Debray, “you think he will bear the cold better.”", "드브레가 말했다. \"그놈은 추위를 더 잘 견디리라 생각하는 거야.\""]], [["“You are mistaken, for I have made a vow never to return to Africa.”", "\"오해일세. 나는 두 번 다시 아프리카에 가지 않기로 맹세했으니까.\""]], [["“You were very much frightened, then?”", "\"그럼 몹시 겁을 먹었던 모양이군?\""], ["asked Beauchamp.", "보샹이 물었다."]], [["“Well, yes, and I had good reason to be so,”", "\"음, 그래. 그럴 만한 이유가 충분했지.\" 샤토르노가 대꾸했다."], ["replied Château-Renaud. “I was retreating on foot, for my horse was dead.", "\"말이 죽어서 나는 걸어서 퇴각하고 있었네. 그때 아랍인 여섯 명이 내 목을 베려고 전속력으로 달려왔지."], ["Six Arabs came up, full gallop, to cut off my head.", "쌍총신 총으로 둘을 쏘고, 권총으로 둘을 더 쐈네."], ["I shot two with my double-barrelled gun, and two more with my pistols, but I was then disarmed, and two were still left; one seized me by the hair (that is why I now wear it so short, for no one knows what may happen), the other swung a yataghan, and I already felt the cold steel on my neck, when this gentleman whom you see here charged them, shot the one who held me by the hair, and cleft the skull of the other with his sabre.", "하지만 그러고 나니 무기가 다 떨어졌는데, 아직 둘이 남아 있었어. 하나는 내 머리채를 움켜잡았고—그래서 지금 내가 머리를 이렇게 짧게 깎고 다니는 거야, 무슨 일이 생길지 모르니까—다른 하나는 언월도를 휘둘렀지. 차가운 칼날이 이미 내 목에 느껴지던 그 순간, 여기 보이는 이분이 그들에게 돌격해, 내 머리채를 잡은 자를 쏘아 죽이고, 다른 자의 두개골을 검으로 쪼개 버렸네."], ["He had assigned himself the task of saving a man’s life that day; chance caused that man to be myself.", "그분은 그날 한 사람의 목숨을 구하기로 스스로 정해 두었던 거야. 우연히 그 사람이 바로 나였던 거지."], ["When I am rich I will order a statue of Chance from Klagmann or Marochetti.”", "내가 부자가 되면 클라그만이나 마로케티한테 '우연'의 동상을 주문할 걸세.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said Morrel, smiling, “it was the 5th of September, the anniversary of the day on which my father was miraculously preserved; therefore, as far as it lies in my power, I endeavor to celebrate it by some——”", "모렐이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그날은 9월 5일, 제 아버지가 기적처럼 목숨을 구하신 날의 기념일이었습니다. 그래서 저는 제 힘이 닿는 한, 그날을 어떤……으로 기리려 애쓰지요.\""]], [["20237m\n“Heroic action,”", "\"영웅적인 행동으로.\""], ["interrupted Château-Renaud. “I was chosen.", "샤토르노가 말을 가로챘다. \"그래서 내가 선택받은 거야."], ["But that is not all—after rescuing me from the sword, he rescued me from the cold, not by sharing his cloak with me, like St.", "하지만 그게 다가 아니라네. 검에서 나를 구해 준 뒤, 그는 추위에서도 나를 구해 줬어. 성 마르탱처럼 외투를 반으로 나눠 준 게 아니라, 통째로 줬지."], ["Martin, but by giving me the whole; then from hunger by sharing with me—guess what?”", "그다음엔 굶주림에서 구해 줬는데, 그러니까 나와 함께 나눠 먹은 게—무엇이었을 것 같나?\""]], [["“A Strasbourg pie?”", "\"스트라스부르 파이?\""], ["asked Beauchamp.", "보샹이 물었다."]], [["“No, his horse; of which we each of us ate a slice with a hearty appetite.", "\"아니, 자기 말이었지. 우리 둘이서 한 점씩 아주 맛있게 먹었어."], ["It was very hard.”", "굉장히 질겼지만.\""]], [["“The horse?”", "\"말이?\""], ["said Morcerf, laughing.", "모르세르가 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“No, the sacrifice,”", "\"아니, 희생이.\""], ["returned Château-Renaud; “ask Debray if he would sacrifice his English steed for a stranger?”", "샤토르노가 대꾸했다. \"드브레한테 물어보게. 낯선 사람을 위해 자기 영국산 말을 희생할 수 있겠는지?\""]], [["“Not for a stranger,”", "\"낯선 사람을 위해서는 안 되지.\""], ["said Debray, “but for a friend I might, perhaps.”", "드브레가 말했다. \"하지만 친구를 위해서라면, 어쩌면 그럴지도.\""]], [["“I divined that you would become mine, count,”", "\"저는 당신이 제 친구가 되리라는 걸 짐작했습니다, 백작님.\""], ["replied Morrel; “besides, as I had the honor to tell you, heroism or not, sacrifice or not, that day I owed an offering to bad fortune in recompense for the favors good fortune had on other days granted to us.”", "모렐이 대꾸했다. \"게다가, 영광스럽게도 말씀드린 대로, 영웅적이든 아니든, 희생이든 아니든, 그날 저는 다른 날들에 행운이 베풀어 준 호의에 대한 보답으로 불운에 제물 하나를 바쳐야 할 빚이 있었던 겁니다.\""]], [["“The history to which M. Morrel alludes,”", "\"모렐 씨가 넌지시 말하는 사연은,\""], ["continued Château-Renaud, “is an admirable one, which he will tell you some day when you are better acquainted with him; today let us fill our stomachs, and not our memories.", "샤토르노가 말을 이었다. \"참으로 감탄할 만한 이야기로, 여러분이 그와 좀 더 가까워지면 언젠가 그가 들려줄 겁니다. 오늘은 기억이 아니라 배를 채웁시다."], ["What time do you breakfast, Albert?”", "알베르, 자네는 몇 시에 아침을 드나?\""]], [["“At half-past ten.”", "\"열 시 반에.\""]], [["“Precisely?”", "\"정확히?\""], ["asked Debray, taking out his watch.", "드브레가 시계를 꺼내며 물었다."]], [["“Oh, you will give me five minutes’ grace,”", "\"오, 자네는 내게 5분쯤 너그러이 봐줄 거잖나.\""], ["replied Morcerf, “for I also expect a preserver.”", "모르세르가 대꾸했다. \"나 역시 생명의 은인 한 사람을 기다리고 있거든.\""]], [["“Of whom?”", "\"누구의?\""]], [["“Of myself,”", "\"내 생명의 은인이지.\""], ["cried Morcerf; “_parbleu!_ do you think I cannot be saved as well as anyone else, and that there are only Arabs who cut off heads?", "모르세르가 외쳤다. \"제기랄! 자네들은 내가 남들처럼 목숨을 구원받을 수 없다고 생각하나? 목을 베는 게 아랍인들밖에 없다고 여기는 건가?"], ["Our breakfast is a philanthropic one, and we shall have at table—at least, I hope so—two benefactors of humanity.”", "우리의 아침 식사는 박애주의적인 자리야. 식탁에—적어도 그러길 바라는데—인류의 은인 두 사람을 모시게 될 거라네.\""]], [["“What shall we do?”", "\"그럼 우린 어쩌지?\""], ["said Debray; “we have only one Monthyon prize.”", "드브레가 말했다. \"몽티옹 상은 하나뿐인데.\""]], [["“Well, it will be given to someone who has done nothing to deserve it,”", "\"음, 그건 그걸 받을 만한 일을 전혀 하지 않은 누군가에게 돌아가겠지.\""], ["said Beauchamp; “that is the way the Academy mostly escapes from the dilemma.”", "보샹이 말했다. \"아카데미가 그 딜레마에서 벗어나는 방식이 대개 그렇잖나.\""]], [["“And where does he come from?” asked Debray.", "\"그래서 그는 어디서 오는 건가?\""], ["“You have already answered the question once, but so vaguely that I venture to put it a second time.”", "드브레가 물었다. \"이미 한 번 답했지만 어찌나 막연하던지, 한 번 더 물어보려는 거야.\""]], [["“Really,”", "\"솔직히,\" 알베르가 말했다."], ["said Albert, “I do not know; when I invited him three months ago, he was then at Rome, but since that time who knows where he may have gone?”", "\"나도 모르네. 석 달 전에 그를 초대했을 때는 로마에 있었지. 하지만 그 뒤로 어디로 갔는지 누가 알겠나?\""]], [["“And you think him capable of being exact?”", "\"그가 시간을 정확히 지킬 만한 사람이라고 생각하나?\""], ["demanded Debray.", "드브레가 따져 물었다."]], [["“I think him capable of everything.”", "\"나는 그가 무슨 일이든 할 수 있는 사람이라고 생각하네.\""]], [["“Well, with the five minutes’ grace, we have only ten left.”", "\"음, 5분 너그러이 봐주기로 한 시간 중에 이제 10분밖에 안 남았군.\""]], [["“I will profit by them to tell you something about my guest.”", "\"그 시간을 이용해서 내 손님 이야기를 좀 해 주지.\""]], [["“I beg pardon,”", "\"미안하지만 묻겠네.\""], ["interrupted Beauchamp; “are there any materials for an article in what you are going to tell us?”", "보샹이 끼어들었다. \"지금 자네가 들려줄 이야기 속에 기삿거리가 될 만한 재료가 있나?\""]], [["“Yes, and for a most curious one.”", "\"그래, 그것도 아주 진기한 기삿거리지.\""]], [["“Go on, then, for I see I shall not get to the Chamber this morning, and I must make up for it.”", "\"그럼 어서 말해 보게. 오늘 아침엔 의회에 못 갈 것 같으니, 그걸 벌충해야겠어.\""]], [["“I was at Rome during the last Carnival.”", "\"나는 지난 사육제 동안 로마에 있었네.\""]], [["“We know that,” said Beauchamp.", "\"그건 우리도 알아.\" 보샹이 말했다."]], [["“Yes, but what you do not know is that I was carried off by bandits.”", "\"그래, 하지만 자네들이 모르는 건, 내가 산적들에게 납치당했다는 거야.\""]], [["“There are no bandits,”", "\"산적이 어디 있나.\""], ["cried Debray.", "드브레가 외쳤다."]], [["“Yes there are, and most hideous, or rather most admirable ones, for I found them ugly enough to frighten me.”", "\"있다네. 그것도 아주 흉측한, 아니, 아주 감탄스러운 자들이지. 나는 그들이 나를 겁먹게 할 만큼 흉측하다는 걸 알았으니까.\""]], [["“Come, my dear Albert,”", "\"이보게, 친애하는 알베르.\""], ["said Debray, “confess that your cook is behindhand, that the oysters have not arrived from Ostend or Marennes, and that, like Madame de Maintenon, you are going to replace the dish by a story.", "드브레가 말했다. \"솔직히 인정하게. 자네 요리사가 일이 늦었고, 굴이 오스텐데나 마렌에서 도착하지 않았고, 그래서 맹트농 부인처럼 요리 대신 이야기로 때우려는 거라고."], ["Say so at once; we are sufficiently well-bred to excuse you, and to listen to your history, fabulous as it promises to be.”", "어서 그렇게 말하게. 우리는 충분히 교양 있는 사람들이니 자네를 너그러이 봐주고, 황당무계할 게 뻔한 자네 이야기를 들어 주겠네.\""]], [["“And I say to you, fabulous as it may seem, I tell it as a true one from beginning to end. The brigands had carried me off, and conducted me to a gloomy spot, called the Catacombs of Saint Sebastian.”", "\"그리고 자네에게 말하는데, 황당무계해 보일지언정, 나는 처음부터 끝까지 진실로서 이야기하는 거야. 도적들이 나를 납치해, 성 세바스티아노 카타콤이라는 음침한 곳으로 끌고 갔네.\""]], [["“I know it,”", "\"나도 거기를 아네.\""], ["said Château-Renaud; “I narrowly escaped catching a fever there.”", "샤토르노가 말했다. \"거기서 하마터면 열병에 걸릴 뻔했지.\""]], [["“And I did more than that,”", "\"나는 그보다 더했네.\""], ["replied Morcerf, “for I caught one.", "모르세르가 대꾸했다. \"정말로 열병에 걸렸으니까."], ["I was informed that I was prisoner until I paid the sum of 4,000 Roman crowns—about 24,000 francs.", "나는 로마 크라운으로 4천 닢, 그러니까 2만 4천 프랑쯤 되는 돈을 낼 때까지 갇혀 있어야 한다는 통고를 받았어."], ["Unfortunately, I had not above 1,500.", "안타깝게도 내겐 1천 5백 닢밖에 없었네."], ["I was at the end of my journey and of my credit.", "여행도 끝물이었고 신용도 바닥이었지."], ["I wrote to Franz—and were he here he would confirm every word—I wrote then to Franz that if he did not come with the four thousand crowns before six, at ten minutes past I should have gone to join the blessed saints and glorious martyrs in whose company I had the honor of being; and Signor Luigi Vampa, such was the name of the chief of these bandits, would have scrupulously kept his word.”", "그래서 프란츠에게 편지를 썼네—그가 여기 있다면 한마디 한마디 다 확인해 줄 거야—프란츠에게 편지를 써서, 만약 여섯 시 전에 4천 크라운을 가지고 오지 않으면, 십 분 후에는 내가 영광스럽게도 함께 지내고 있던 그 복된 성인들과 영광스러운 순교자들의 무리에 끼게 될 거라고 적었지. 그리고 루이지 밤파라는 자가—그게 이 도적들 두목의 이름이었네—틀림없이 약속을 지켰을 걸세.\""]], [["“But Franz did come with the four thousand crowns,”", "\"하지만 프란츠가 4천 크라운을 가지고 오긴 했군.\""], ["said Château-Renaud.", "샤토르노가 말했다."], ["“A man whose name is Franz d’Épinay or Albert de Morcerf has not much difficulty in procuring them.”", "\"프란츠 데피네나 알베르 드 모르세르 같은 이름을 가진 사람이라면 그 정도 마련하는 게 그리 어렵지 않을 테니까.\""]], [["“No, he arrived accompanied simply by the guest I am going to present to you.”", "\"아니, 그는 그저 내가 지금 자네들에게 소개하려는 손님만 데리고 왔네.\""]], [["“Ah, this gentleman is a Hercules killing Cacus, a Perseus freeing Andromeda.”", "\"아, 그럼 이분은 카쿠스를 죽인 헤라클레스, 안드로메다를 구한 페르세우스 같은 분이군.\""]], [["“No, he is a man about my own size.”", "\"아니, 나하고 키가 엇비슷한 사람이야.\""]], [["“Armed to the teeth?”", "\"이로 무장하고 있었나?\""]], [["“He had not even a knitting-needle.”", "\"뜨개바늘 하나도 없었네.\""]], [["“But he paid your ransom?”", "\"하지만 자네 몸값을 치렀잖나?\""]], [["“He said two words to the chief and I was free.”", "\"그는 두목에게 두 마디를 했고, 그러자 나는 풀려났네.\""]], [["“And they apologized to him for having carried you off?”", "\"그리고 그들이 자네를 납치한 것에 대해 그에게 사과까지 했나?\""], ["said Beauchamp.", "보샹이 물었다."]], [["“Just so.”", "\"바로 그랬네.\""]], [["“Why, he is a second Ariosto.”", "\"이런, 그는 제2의 아리오스토로군.\""]], [["“No, his name is the Count of Monte Cristo.”", "\"아니, 그의 이름은 몬테크리스토 백작이야.\""]], [["“There is no Count of Monte Cristo”", "\"몬테크리스토 백작이라는 사람은 없네.\""], ["said Debray.", "드브레가 말했다."]], [["“I do not think so,” added Château-Renaud, with the air of a man who knows the whole of the European nobility perfectly.", "\"나도 그렇게 생각하네.\" 유럽 귀족 전체를 완벽하게 꿰뚫고 있는 사람 같은 태도로 샤토르노가 덧붙였다."]], [["“Does anyone know anything of a Count of Monte Cristo?”", "\"누구든 몬테크리스토 백작에 대해 뭔가 아는 사람 있나?\""]], [["“He comes possibly from the Holy Land, and one of his ancestors possessed Calvary, as the Mortemarts did the Dead Sea.”", "\"어쩌면 성지에서 온 사람일지도 모르지. 모르트마르 가문이 사해를 소유했듯, 그의 조상 중 하나가 갈보리를 소유했을 수도 있고.\""]], [["“I think I can assist your researches,”", "\"내가 자네들 조사에 도움이 될 것 같네.\""], ["said Maximilian.", "막시밀리앙이 말했다."], ["“Monte Cristo is a little island I have often heard spoken of by the old sailors my father employed—a grain of sand in the centre of the Mediterranean, an atom in the infinite.”", "\"몬테크리스토는 작은 섬으로, 아버지가 고용했던 늙은 선원들에게서 여러 번 들었습니다. 지중해 한복판의 모래알 하나, 무한 속의 티끌 하나지요.\""]], [["“Precisely!”", "\"바로 그거야!\""], ["cried Albert.", "알베르가 외쳤다."], ["“Well, he of whom I speak is the lord and master of this grain of sand, of this atom; he has purchased the title of count somewhere in Tuscany.”", "\"음, 내가 말하는 그 사람은 바로 그 모래알, 그 티끌의 영주이자 주인이라네. 그는 토스카나 어딘가에서 백작 작위를 사들였지.\""]], [["“He is rich, then?”", "\"그럼 부자인가?\""]], [["“I believe so.”", "\"그렇다고 보네.\""]], [["“But that ought to be visible.”", "\"하지만 그건 눈에 보여야 하지 않나.\""]], [["“That is what deceives you, Debray.”", "\"바로 그 점이 자네를 속이는 걸세, 드브레.\""]], [["“I do not understand you.”", "\"무슨 말인지 모르겠군.\""]], [["“Have you read the _Arabian Nights_?”", "\"자네 『아라비안나이트』를 읽어 봤나?\""]], [["“What a question!”", "\"별걸 다 묻는군!\""]], [["“Well, do you know if the persons you see there are rich or poor, if their sacks of wheat are not rubies or diamonds?", "\"음, 거기 나오는 사람들이 부자인지 가난한지 자네가 알겠나? 그들의 밀자루가 루비나 다이아몬드가 아니라고 어떻게 알겠어?"], ["They seem like poor fishermen, and suddenly they open some mysterious cavern filled with the wealth of the Indies.”", "그들은 가난한 어부처럼 보이다가, 갑자기 인도의 부가 가득한 어떤 신비로운 동굴을 열어젖히지.\""]], [["“Which means?”", "\"그게 무슨 뜻인가?\""]], [["“Which means that my Count of Monte Cristo is one of those fishermen.", "\"내 몬테크리스토 백작이 바로 그런 어부 중 하나라는 뜻이야."], ["He has even a name taken from the book, since he calls himself Sinbad the Sailor, and has a cave filled with gold.”", "심지어 그는 그 책에서 따온 이름까지 갖고 있어. 자기를 뱃사람 신드바드라 부르고, 황금으로 가득한 동굴을 가지고 있거든.\""]], [["“And you have seen this cavern, Morcerf?”", "\"그래서 자네가 그 동굴을 봤나, 모르세르?\""], ["asked Beauchamp.", "보샹이 물었다."]], [["“No, but Franz has; for heaven’s sake, not a word of this before him.", "\"아니, 하지만 프란츠는 봤네. 제발 부탁이니, 그 앞에서는 이 얘기를 한마디도 하지 말게."], ["Franz went in with his eyes blindfolded, and was waited on by mutes and by women to whom Cleopatra was a painted strumpet.", "프란츠는 눈을 가린 채 들어갔고, 벙어리들과, 클레오파트라쯤은 칠해 놓은 매춘부로 보일 만큼 아름다운 여인들의 시중을 받았지."], ["Only he is not quite sure about the women, for they did not come in until after he had taken hashish, so that what he took for women might have been simply a row of statues.”", "다만 그 여인들에 대해서는 그도 확신하지 못해. 그가 해시시를 복용한 후에야 들어왔거든. 그래서 그가 여인들이라 여긴 것이 실은 한 줄로 늘어선 조각상들이었을 수도 있다는 거야.\""]], [["The two young men looked at Morcerf as if to say,—“Are you mad, or are you laughing at us?”", "두 젊은이는 마치 \"자네 미친 건가, 아니면 우리를 놀리는 건가?\"라고 묻는 듯한 눈으로 모르세르를 바라보았다."]], [["“And I also,”", "\"저 역시,\""], ["said Morrel thoughtfully, “have heard something like this from an old sailor named Penelon.”", "모렐이 생각에 잠긴 채 말했다. \"이와 비슷한 이야기를 페늘롱이라는 늙은 선원에게서 들은 적이 있습니다.\""]], [["“Ah,” cried Albert, “it is very lucky that M.", "\"아!\" 알베르가 외쳤다."], ["Morrel comes to aid me; you are vexed, are you not, that he thus gives a clew to the labyrinth?”", "\"모렐 씨가 나를 도우러 와 주다니 정말 다행이군. 그가 이렇게 미궁의 실마리를 던져 주니 자네들 속이 상하지, 안 그래?\""]], [["“My dear Albert,”", "\"이보게 알베르,\""], ["said Debray, “what you tell us is so extraordinary.”", "드브레가 말했다. \"자네가 들려주는 이야기는 너무나 터무니없어.\""]], [["“Ah, because your ambassadors and your consuls do not tell you of them—they have no time.", "\"아, 그건 자네네 대사들과 영사들이 자네들에게 그런 얘기를 안 해 줘서야."], ["They are too much taken up with interfering in the affairs of their countrymen who travel.”", "그들은 그럴 시간이 없거든. 여행 다니는 제 나라 사람들 일에 간섭하느라 너무 바쁘니까.\""]], [["“Now you get angry, and attack our poor agents.", "\"이제 자네가 화를 내며 우리 가엾은 관리들을 공격하는군."], ["How will you have them protect you?", "그들이 어떻게 자네를 지켜 줄 수 있겠나?"], ["The Chamber cuts down their salaries every day, so that now they have scarcely any.", "의회가 날마다 그들의 봉급을 깎아 대니, 지금은 거의 한 푼도 못 받을 지경이야."], ["Will you be ambassador, Albert?", "자네가 대사가 되겠나, 알베르?"], ["I will send you to Constantinople.”", "내가 자네를 콘스탄티노플로 보내 주지.\""]], [["“No, lest on the first demonstration I make in favor of Mehemet Ali, the Sultan send me the bowstring, and make my secretaries strangle me.”", "\"싫네. 메헤메트 알리를 옹호하는 시위를 처음 벌이는 순간, 술탄이 내게 활시위를 보내 내 비서들 손에 나를 목 졸라 죽이게 할 테니까.\""]], [["“You say very true,”", "\"지당한 말이군.\""], ["responded Debray.", "드브레가 응수했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["said Albert, “but this has nothing to do with the existence of the Count of Monte Cristo.”", "알베르가 말했다. \"하지만 이건 몬테크리스토 백작의 존재와는 아무 상관이 없어.\""]], [["“_Pardieu!_ everyone exists.”", "\"제기랄! 누구나 다 존재하잖나.\""]], [["“Doubtless, but not in the same way; everyone has not black slaves, a princely retinue, an arsenal of weapons that would do credit to an Arabian fortress, horses that cost six thousand francs apiece, and Greek mistresses.”", "\"틀림없이 그렇지. 하지만 같은 방식으로는 아니야. 누구나 다 흑인 노예들과 군주 같은 수행단을 거느리고, 아라비아 요새도 부끄럽지 않을 무기고를 갖추고, 한 마리에 6천 프랑짜리 말들과 그리스인 정부를 두지는 않으니까.\""]], [["“Have you seen the Greek mistress?”", "\"자네 그 그리스인 정부를 본 적 있나?\""]], [["“I have both seen and heard her.", "\"보기도 했고 목소리도 들었네."], ["I saw her at the theatre, and heard her one morning when I breakfasted with the count.”", "극장에서 그녀를 봤고, 백작과 아침을 먹던 어느 날 아침에 목소리를 들었지.\""]], [["“He eats, then?” “Yes; but so little, it can hardly be called eating.”", "\"그래. 하지만 어찌나 조금 먹는지, 먹는다고 하기도 어려울 지경이야.\""]], [["“He must be a vampire.”", "\"그자는 흡혈귀가 틀림없군.\""]], [["“Laugh, if you will; the Countess G——, who knew Lord Ruthven, declared that the count was a vampire.”", "\"웃고 싶으면 웃게. 러스벤 경을 알았던 G 백작 부인이, 그 백작은 흡혈귀라고 단언했으니까.\""]], [["“Ah, capital,”", "\"아, 멋지군.\""], ["said Beauchamp.", "보샹이 말했다."], ["“For a man not connected with newspapers, here is the pendant to the famous sea-serpent of the _Constitutionnel_.”", "\"신문 일과 무관한 사람이 했다기엔, 이건 『콩스티튀시오넬』지의 그 유명한 바다뱀에 견줄 만한 짝패로군.\""]], [["“Wild eyes, the iris of which contracts or dilates at pleasure,”", "\"마음대로 수축하고 팽창하는 홍채를 가진 사나운 눈.\""], ["said Debray; “facial angle strongly developed, magnificent forehead, livid complexion, black beard, sharp and white teeth, politeness unexceptionable.”", "드브레가 말했다. \"강하게 발달한 안면각, 멋들어진 이마, 납빛 안색, 검은 수염, 날카롭고 새하얀 이, 흠잡을 데 없는 정중함.\""]], [["“Just so, Lucien,”", "\"바로 그래, 뤼시앵.\""], ["returned Morcerf; “you have described him feature for feature.", "모르세르가 대꾸했다. \"자네가 그를 이목구비 하나하나 그대로 묘사했어."], ["Yes, keen and cutting politeness.", "그래, 날카롭고 베일 듯한 정중함."], ["This man has often made me shudder; and one day when we were viewing an execution, I thought I should faint, more from hearing the cold and calm manner in which he spoke of every description of torture, than from the sight of the executioner and the culprit.”", "그 사람 때문에 나는 종종 몸서리를 쳤네. 그리고 우리가 함께 사형 집행을 구경하던 어느 날엔, 나는 까무러칠 것만 같았어. 사형 집행인과 죄인의 모습보다도, 그가 온갖 종류의 고문에 대해 이야기하던 그 차갑고 침착한 말투를 듣고 말이야.\""]], [["“Did he not conduct you to the ruins of the Colosseum and suck your blood?”", "\"그가 자네를 콜로세움 폐허로 데려가 자네 피를 빨아먹지는 않았나?\""], ["asked Beauchamp.", "보샹이 물었다."]], [["“Or, having delivered you, make you sign a flaming parchment, surrendering your soul to him as Esau did his birth-right?”", "\"아니면 자네를 풀어 준 뒤, 에서가 장자권을 넘기듯 자네 영혼을 그에게 넘긴다는 불타는 양피지에 서명하게 만들지는 않았고?\""]], [["“Rail on, rail on at your ease, gentlemen,” said Morcerf, somewhat piqued.", "\"마음껏 비웃게, 여러분.\" 모르세르가 다소 발끈해서 말했다."], ["“When I look at you Parisians, idlers on the Boulevard de Gand or the Bois de Boulogne, and think of this man, it seems to me we are not of the same race.”", "\"강 거리나 불로뉴 숲을 어슬렁대는 자네들 파리지앵을 보고 이 사람을 떠올리면, 우리가 도무지 같은 종족이 아닌 것 같단 말이야.\""]], [["“I am highly flattered,”", "\"대단히 영광이군.\""], ["returned Beauchamp.", "보샹이 응수했다."]], [["“At the same time,”", "\"그렇다 해도,\""], ["added Château-Renaud, “your Count of Monte Cristo is a very fine fellow, always excepting his little arrangements with the Italian banditti.”", "샤토르노가 덧붙였다. \"자네의 그 몬테크리스토 백작은 아주 훌륭한 친구야. 이탈리아 산적들과의 그 사소한 거래만 빼면 말이지.\""]], [["“There are no Italian banditti,” said Debray.", "\"이탈리아 산적은 없네.\" 드브레가 말했다."]], [["“No vampire,” cried Beauchamp.", "\"흡혈귀도 없고.\" 보샹이 외쳤다."]], [["“No Count of Monte Cristo” added Debray.", "\"몬테크리스토 백작도 없지.\""], ["“There is half-past ten striking, Albert.” 20243m", "드브레가 덧붙였다. \"열 시 반을 치는군, 알베르.\""]], [["“Confess you have dreamed this, and let us sit down to breakfast,”", "\"이건 자네가 꿈에서 본 거라고 인정하고, 어서 아침 식탁에 앉자고.\""], ["continued Beauchamp.", "보샹이 말을 이었다."]], [["But the sound of the clock had not died away when Germain announced, “His excellency the Count of Monte Cristo.”", "그러나 시계 소리가 채 잦아들기도 전에 제르맹이 알렸다. \"몬테크리스토 백작 각하이십니다.\""], ["The involuntary start everyone gave proved how much Morcerf’s narrative had impressed them, and Albert himself could not wholly refrain from manifesting sudden emotion.", "다들 저도 모르게 흠칫하는 모습이, 모르세르의 이야기가 그들에게 얼마나 깊은 인상을 남겼는지를 증명했다. 알베르 자신도 갑작스러운 동요를 완전히 감추지는 못했다."], ["He had not heard a carriage stop in the street, or steps in the antechamber; the door had itself opened noiselessly.", "그는 거리에서 마차가 멈추는 소리도, 대기실에서 발걸음 소리도 듣지 못했다. 문은 소리 없이 저절로 열렸다."], ["The count appeared, dressed with the greatest simplicity, but the most fastidious dandy could have found nothing to cavil at in his toilet.", "백작이 나타났는데, 더할 나위 없이 소박한 차림이었지만, 아무리 까다로운 멋쟁이라도 그의 옷차림에서 트집 잡을 거리를 찾지 못했을 것이다."], ["Every article of dress—hat, coat, gloves, and boots—was from the first makers.", "모자, 외투, 장갑, 부츠 등 의복의 모든 품목이 일류 제작자의 솜씨였다."], ["He seemed scarcely five-and-thirty.", "그는 서른다섯 살이 채 안 되어 보였다."], ["But what struck everybody was his extreme resemblance to the portrait Debray had drawn.", "그러나 모두를 놀라게 한 것은, 그가 드브레가 묘사한 초상과 극도로 닮았다는 점이었다."], ["The count advanced, smiling, into the centre of the room, and approached Albert, who hastened towards him holding out his hand in a ceremonial manner.", "백작은 미소를 지으며 방 한가운데로 나아가 알베르에게 다가갔고, 알베르는 격식을 갖춘 태도로 손을 내밀며 그에게 서둘러 다가갔다."]], [["“Punctuality,”", "\"시간을 지키는 것은,\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “is the politeness of kings, according to one of your sovereigns, I think; but it is not the same with travellers.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"내가 알기로 당신네 어느 군주가 말했듯, 왕의 예의라지요. 하지만 여행자에게는 사정이 다릅니다."], ["However, I hope you will excuse the two or three seconds I am behindhand; five hundred leagues are not to be accomplished without some trouble, and especially in France, where, it seems, it is forbidden to beat the postilions.”", "그래도 두세 초 늦은 것을 양해해 주시리라 믿습니다. 5백 리외를 별 탈 없이 주파한다는 건 어려운 일이거든요. 더구나 프랑스에서는, 듣자 하니 역마차 마부를 채찍질하는 게 금지되어 있다더군요.\""]], [["“My dear count,”", "\"친애하는 백작님,\""], ["replied Albert, “I was announcing your visit to some of my friends, whom I had invited in consequence of the promise you did me the honor to make, and whom I now present to you.", "알베르가 대답했다. \"마침 친구 몇 사람에게 당신의 방문을 알리던 참이었습니다. 영광스럽게도 당신이 제게 해 주신 약속에 따라 초대한 이들이지요."], ["They are the Count of Château-Renaud, whose nobility goes back to the twelve peers, and whose ancestors had a place at the Round Table; M.", "이제 그들을 소개하겠습니다. 이쪽은 샤토르노 백작으로, 그의 가문은 열두 중신(重臣)까지 거슬러 올라가고, 그 조상들은 원탁에 자리를 차지했습니다."], ["Lucien Debray, private secretary to the minister of the interior; M.", "이쪽은 뤼시앵 드브레 씨로, 내무부 장관의 개인 비서지요."], ["Beauchamp, an editor of a paper, and the terror of the French government, but of whom, in spite of his national celebrity, you perhaps have not heard in Italy, since his paper is prohibited there; and M.", "이쪽은 보샹 씨로, 신문 편집인이자 프랑스 정부의 공포의 대상입니다. 다만 국내에서는 그토록 유명한데도, 당신은 이탈리아에서는 그에 대해 못 들으셨을 겁니다. 그의 신문은 거기서 금지되어 있으니까요."], ["Maximilian Morrel, captain of Spahis.”", "그리고 이쪽은 막시밀리앙 모렐 씨로, 스파이 기병 대위입니다.\""]], [["At this name the count, who had hitherto saluted everyone with courtesy, but at the same time with coldness and formality, stepped a pace forward, and a slight tinge of red colored his pale cheeks.", "이 이름이 나오자, 그때까지 모두에게 정중하면서도 차갑고 격식 차린 태도로 인사하던 백작이 한 발짝 앞으로 나섰고, 그의 창백한 뺨에 옅은 홍조가 스쳤다."]], [["“You wear the uniform of the new French conquerors, monsieur,”", "\"당신은 새로운 프랑스 정복자들의 제복을 입고 계시는군요.\""], ["said he; “it is a handsome uniform.”", "그가 말했다. \"멋진 제복입니다.\""]], [["No one could have said what caused the count’s voice to vibrate so deeply, and what made his eye flash, which was in general so clear, lustrous, and limpid when he pleased.", "무엇이 백작의 목소리를 그토록 깊이 떨리게 했는지, 또 무엇이, 그가 원하기만 하면 대개 그토록 맑고 빛나며 투명한 그의 눈을 그렇게 번득이게 했는지는 아무도 알 수 없었다."]], [["“You have never seen our Africans, count?”", "\"백작님은 저희 아프리카 부대원을 본 적이 없으십니까?\""], ["said Albert.", "알베르가 물었다."]], [["“Never,” replied the count, who was by this time perfectly master of himself again.", "\"한 번도 없습니다.\" 이때쯤에는 다시 완벽하게 평정을 되찾은 백작이 대답했다."]], [["“Well, beneath this uniform beats one of the bravest and noblest hearts in the whole army.”", "\"음, 이 제복 아래에는 군 전체에서 가장 용감하고 가장 고귀한 심장 중 하나가 뛰고 있습니다.\""]], [["“Oh, M. de Morcerf,”", "\"오, 모르세르 씨.\""], ["interrupted Morrel.", "모렐이 끼어들었다."]], [["“Let me go on, captain. And we have just heard,”", "\"말을 마저 하게 해 주십시오, 대위.\" 알베르가 말을 이었다."], ["continued Albert, “of a new deed of his, and so heroic a one, that, although I have seen him today for the first time, I request you to allow me to introduce him as my friend.”", "\"그리고 우리는 방금, 그의 새로운 무용담을 들었습니다. 어찌나 영웅적인지, 비록 오늘 처음 그를 만났지만, 저는 그를 제 친구로 소개하도록 허락해 주시길 청합니다.\""]], [["At these words it was still possible to observe in Monte Cristo the concentrated look, changing color, and slight trembling of the eyelid that show emotion.", "이 말에, 몬테크리스토에게서는 여전히 응축된 시선과 변하는 안색, 감정을 드러내는 눈꺼풀의 가벼운 떨림을 관찰할 수 있었다."]], [["“Ah, you have a noble heart,”", "\"아, 당신은 고귀한 마음을 가지셨군요.\""], ["said the count; “so much the better.”", "백작이 말했다. \"그렇다면 더욱 좋습니다.\""]], [["This exclamation, which corresponded to the count’s own thought rather than to what Albert was saying, surprised everybody, and especially Morrel, who looked at Monte Cristo with wonder.", "알베르가 한 말보다는 백작 자신의 생각에 들어맞는 이 외침이 모두를, 특히 모렐을 놀라게 했고, 그는 경이로운 눈으로 몬테크리스토를 바라보았다."], ["But, at the same time, the intonation was so soft that, however strange the speech might seem, it was impossible to be offended at it. 20245m", "그러나 동시에 그 억양이 어찌나 부드러웠던지, 그 말이 아무리 이상하게 들릴지라도 거기에 기분이 상한다는 것은 불가능했다."]], [["“Why should he doubt it?”", "\"그가 왜 그걸 의심하겠나?\""], ["said Beauchamp to Château-Renaud.", "보샹이 샤토르노에게 말했다."]], [["“In reality,”", "\"사실,\" 후자가 대꾸했다."], ["replied the latter, who, with his aristocratic glance and his knowledge of the world, had penetrated at once all that was penetrable in Monte Cristo, “Albert has not deceived us, for the count is a most singular being.", "그는 귀족다운 안목과 세상에 대한 식견으로 몬테크리스토에게서 간파할 수 있는 모든 것을 단번에 꿰뚫어 본 터였다. \"알베르가 우리를 속인 게 아니었어. 백작은 참으로 독특한 존재로군."], ["What say you, Morrel!”", "자네 생각은 어떤가, 모렐!\""]], [["“_Ma foi_, he has an open look about him that pleases me, in spite of the singular remark he has made about me.”", "\"솔직히, 저에 대해 그런 독특한 말을 하긴 했지만, 그분에게는 마음에 드는 솔직한 구석이 있습니다.\""]], [["“Gentlemen,”", "\"여러분,\""], ["said Albert, “Germain informs me that breakfast is ready.", "알베르가 말했다. \"제르맹이 아침 식사가 준비됐다고 알리는군요."], ["My dear count, allow me to show you the way.”", "친애하는 백작님, 길을 안내하도록 허락해 주십시오.\""], ["They passed silently into the breakfast-room, and everyone took his place.", "그들은 말없이 식당으로 들어가, 저마다 자리에 앉았다."]], [["“Gentlemen,”", "\"여러분,\""], ["said the count, seating himself, “permit me to make a confession which must form my excuse for any improprieties I may commit.", "백작이 자리에 앉으며 말했다. \"제가 저지를지도 모를 어떤 결례에 대한 변명이 될 고백 하나를 하도록 허락해 주십시오."], ["I am a stranger, and a stranger to such a degree, that this is the first time I have ever been at Paris.", "저는 이방인입니다. 그것도 어찌나 낯선 이방인인지, 파리에 와 본 것이 이번이 처음입니다."], ["The French way of living is utterly unknown to me, and up to the present time I have followed the Eastern customs, which are entirely in contrast to the Parisian.", "프랑스식 생활 방식을 저는 전혀 모르고, 지금까지는 줄곧 동방의 관습을 따라왔습니다. 그건 파리의 관습과는 완전히 상반되지요."], ["I beg you, therefore, to excuse if you find anything in me too Turkish, too Italian, or too Arabian.", "그러니 제게서 너무 튀르크적이거나, 너무 이탈리아적이거나, 너무 아라비아적인 면을 발견하시더라도 너그러이 봐주시길 청합니다."], ["Now, then, let us breakfast.”", "자, 그럼, 아침을 듭시다.\""]], [["“With what an air he says all this,”", "\"이 모든 말을 어찌나 멋진 태도로 하는지.\""], ["muttered Beauchamp; “decidedly he is a great man.”", "보샹이 중얼거렸다. \"확실히 대단한 인물이야.\""]], [["“A great man in his own country,”", "\"제 나라에서나 대단한 인물이겠지.\""], ["added Debray.", "드브레가 덧붙였다."]], [["“A great man in every country, M. Debray,”", "\"어느 나라에서든 대단한 인물입니다, 드브레 씨.\""], ["said Château-Renaud.", "샤토르노가 말했다."]], [["The count was, it may be remembered, a most temperate guest.", "기억하다시피 백작은 더없이 소식하는 손님이었다."], ["Albert remarked this, expressing his fears lest, at the outset, the Parisian mode of life should displease the traveller in the most essential point.", "알베르는 이를 알아채고는, 처음부터 파리의 생활 방식이 가장 본질적인 점에서 이 여행자의 마음에 들지 않을까 봐 걱정스럽다고 말했다."]], [["“My dear count,”", "\"친애하는 백작님,\""], ["said he, “I fear one thing, and that is, that the fare of the Rue du Helder is not so much to your taste as that of the Piazza di Spagna.", "그가 말했다. \"한 가지 두려운 게 있습니다. 엘데르 거리의 음식이 에스파냐 광장의 음식만큼 입맛에 맞지 않을까 봐서요."], ["I ought to have consulted you on the point, and have had some dishes prepared expressly.”", "그 점에 대해 미리 여쭤보고, 특별히 몇 가지 요리를 준비했어야 했는데 말입니다.\""]], [["“Did you know me better,”", "\"저를 좀 더 잘 아신다면,\" 백작이 미소 지으며 대꾸했다."], ["returned the count, smiling, “you would not give one thought of such a thing for a traveller like myself, who has successively lived on macaroni at Naples, polenta at Milan, olla podrida at Valencia, pilau at Constantinople, curry in India, and swallows’ nests in China.", "\"저 같은 여행자에 대해서는 그런 걸 조금도 신경 쓰지 않으실 겁니다. 저는 나폴리에서 마카로니를, 밀라노에서 폴렌타를, 발렌시아에서 올라 포드리다를, 콘스탄티노플에서 필라프를, 인도에서 카레를, 중국에서 제비집을 차례로 먹으며 살아온 사람이니까요. 저는 어디서든, 무엇이든 먹습니다."], ["I eat everywhere, and of everything, only I eat but little; and today, that you reproach me with my want of appetite, is my day of appetite, for I have not eaten since yesterday morning.”", "다만 아주 조금 먹을 뿐이지요. 그리고 당신이 제 입맛 없는 걸 나무라는 오늘이, 사실은 제 식욕이 도는 날입니다. 어제 아침부터 아무것도 먹지 않았거든요.\""]], [["“What,”", "\"뭐라고요!\""], ["cried all the guests, “you have not eaten for four-and-twenty hours?”", "손님들이 일제히 외쳤다. \"스물네 시간 동안 아무것도 안 드셨다고요?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"네.\""], ["replied the count; “I was forced to go out of my road to obtain some information near Nîmes, so that I was somewhat late, and therefore I did not choose to stop.”", "백작이 대답했다. \"님 근처에서 무슨 정보를 얻느라 길을 벗어나야 했고, 그래서 좀 늦은 탓에 멈추지 않기로 한 겁니다.\""]], [["“And you ate in your carriage?”", "\"그럼 마차 안에서 드신 겁니까?\""], ["asked Morcerf.", "모르세르가 물었다."]], [["“No, I slept, as I generally do when I am weary without having the courage to amuse myself, or when I am hungry without feeling inclined to eat.”", "\"아니요, 잤습니다. 굳이 즐길 마음이 없는데 지칠 때나, 먹고 싶은 마음이 없는데 배가 고플 때면 대개 그러지요.\""]], [["“But you can sleep when you please, monsieur?”", "\"하지만 원하실 때 잠을 잘 수 있으십니까, 선생?\""], ["said Morrel.", "모렐이 물었다."]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“You have a recipe for it?”", "\"무슨 비법이라도 있으신가요?\""]], [["“An infallible one.”", "\"실패하는 법이 없는 비법이지요.\""]], [["“That would be invaluable to us in Africa, who have not always any food to eat, and rarely anything to drink.”", "\"그건 우리 아프리카 사람들에게 더없이 값진 것이겠군요. 우리는 늘 먹을 게 있는 것도 아니고, 마실 건 좀처럼 없거든요.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “but, unfortunately, a recipe excellent for a man like myself would be very dangerous applied to an army, which might not awake when it was needed.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"하지만 안타깝게도, 저 같은 사람에게는 훌륭한 비법이 군대에 적용되면 매우 위험할 겁니다. 필요할 때 깨어나지 못할 수도 있으니까요.\""]], [["“May we inquire what is this recipe?”", "\"그 비법이 무엇인지 여쭤봐도 되겠습니까?\""], ["asked Debray.", "드브레가 물었다."]], [["“Oh, yes,” returned Monte Cristo; “I make no secret of it.", "\"오, 물론이지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 대꾸했다."], ["It is a mixture of excellent opium, which I fetched myself from Canton in order to have it pure, and the best hashish which grows in the East—that is, between the Tigris and the Euphrates.", "\"비밀로 할 것도 없습니다. 그건 순수하게 얻으려고 제가 직접 광둥에서 가져온 최상급 아편과, 동방에서 자라는 최고급 해시시, 그러니까 티그리스강과 유프라테스강 사이에서 나는 것을 섞은 겁니다."], ["These two ingredients are mixed in equal proportions, and formed into pills.", "이 두 재료를 같은 비율로 섞어 알약으로 빚지요."], ["Ten minutes after one is taken, the effect is produced.", "하나를 복용하고 십 분이 지나면 효과가 나타납니다."], ["Ask Baron Franz d’Épinay; I think he tasted them one day.”", "프란츠 데피네 남작에게 물어보십시오. 언젠가 그가 맛본 것 같으니까요.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["replied Morcerf, “he said something about it to me.”", "모르세르가 대꾸했다. \"그가 제게 그 얘기를 했었지요.\""]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["said Beauchamp, who, as became a journalist, was very incredulous, “you always carry this drug about you?”", "언론인답게 몹시 의심이 많은 보샹이 말했다. \"그 약을 늘 몸에 지니고 다니십니까?\" \"늘 지니고 다닙니다.\""]], [["“Always.” “Would it be an indiscretion to ask to see those precious pills?”", "\"그 귀한 알약을 좀 보여 달라고 청하면 무례한 일일까요?\""], ["continued Beauchamp, hoping to take him at a disadvantage.", "보샹이 그를 곤란하게 만들 수 있기를 바라며 말을 이었다."]], [["“No, monsieur,”", "\"아니요, 선생.\""], ["returned the count; and he drew from his pocket a marvellous casket, formed out of a single emerald and closed by a golden lid which unscrewed and gave passage to a small greenish colored pellet about the size of a pea.", "백작이 대꾸했다. 그러고는 주머니에서 놀라운 작은 함을 꺼냈다. 에메랄드 하나를 통째로 깎아 만든 것으로, 돌려서 여는 황금 뚜껑이 닫혀 있었다."], ["This ball had an acrid and penetrating odor. There were four or five more in the emerald, which would contain about a dozen.", "뚜껑을 열자 완두콩만 한 크기의 초록빛 작은 알갱이가 나왔다. 그 알약에서는 톡 쏘는 듯한 향이 코를 찔렀다."], ["The casket passed around the table, but it was more to examine the admirable emerald than to see the pills that it passed from hand to hand.", "에메랄드 안에는 네다섯 알이 더 들어 있었는데, 열두 알쯤은 담길 만했다. 함이 식탁을 돌았지만, 그것이 손에서 손으로 건네진 것은 알약을 보려는 것보다는 그 감탄스러운 에메랄드를 살펴보려는 마음에서였다."]], [["“And is it your cook who prepares these pills?”", "\"그럼 이 알약을 만드는 사람이 백작님의 요리사입니까?\""], ["asked Beauchamp.", "보샹이 물었다."]], [["“Oh, no, monsieur,”", "\"오, 아닙니다, 선생.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo; “I do not thus betray my enjoyments to the vulgar.", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"저는 제 즐거움을 그렇게 천박한 자들에게 내맡기지 않습니다."], ["I am a tolerable chemist, and prepare my pills myself.”", "저는 그럭저럭 화학을 좀 하는 사람이라, 제 알약은 직접 만들지요.\""]], [["“This is a magnificent emerald, and the largest I have ever seen,”", "\"이건 굉장한 에메랄드로군요. 제가 본 것 중에 제일 큽니다.\" 샤토르노가 말했다."], ["said Château-Renaud, “although my mother has some remarkable family jewels.”", "\"저희 어머니께서 가문 대대로 내려오는 보석을 몇 개 가지고 계시긴 합니다만.\""]], [["“I had three similar ones,”", "\"제겐 이와 비슷한 것이 세 개 있었지요.\""], ["returned Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 대꾸했다."], ["“I gave one to the Sultan, who mounted it in his sabre; another to our holy father the Pope, who had it set in his tiara, opposite to one nearly as large, though not so fine, given by the Emperor Napoleon to his predecessor, Pius VII.", "\"하나는 술탄에게 드렸는데, 그가 자기 검에 박았습니다. 또 하나는 우리의 성부(聖父)이신 교황께 드렸는데, 그분이 삼중관에 박으셨지요. 그 맞은편에는 거의 같은 크기의, 그렇게 곱지는 않은 에메랄드가 박혀 있었습니다."], ["I kept the third for myself, and I had it hollowed out, which reduced its value, but rendered it more commodious for the purpose I intended.”", "나폴레옹 황제가 선임 교황 비오 7세께 드린 것이었지요. 세 번째는 제가 가졌는데, 속을 파내게 했습니다. 그래서 값어치는 떨어졌지만, 제가 의도한 용도에는 더 편리해졌지요.\""]], [["Everyone looked at Monte Cristo with astonishment; he spoke with so much simplicity that it was evident he spoke the truth, or that he was mad.", "모두가 놀라움에 차서 몬테크리스토를 바라보았다. 그는 어찌나 담담하게 말하던지, 그가 진실을 말하고 있거나, 아니면 미친 것이 분명해 보였다."], ["However, the sight of the emerald made them naturally incline to the former belief.", "그러나 에메랄드를 본 터라 자연히 전자의 믿음 쪽으로 기울었다."]], [["“And what did these two sovereigns give you in exchange for these magnificent presents?”", "\"그래서 그 두 군주는 이 훌륭한 선물에 대한 답례로 백작님께 무엇을 주었습니까?\""], ["asked Debray.", "드브레가 물었다."]], [["“The Sultan, the liberty of a woman,”", "\"술탄은 한 여인의 자유를.\""], ["replied the Count; “the Pope, the life of a man; so that once in my life I have been as powerful as if heaven had brought me into the world on the steps of a throne.”", "백작이 대답했다. \"교황은 한 남자의 목숨을 주었지요. 그래서 제 생애에 단 한 번, 마치 하늘이 저를 옥좌의 계단 위에 태어나게 한 것처럼 강력한 권세를 누려 본 셈입니다.\""]], [["“And it was Peppino you saved, was it not?”", "\"그럼 백작님이 구하신 게 페피노였군요, 그렇지 않습니까?\""], ["cried Morcerf; “it was for him that you obtained pardon?”", "모르세르가 외쳤다. \"백작님이 그를 위해 사면을 얻어 내신 거지요?\""]], [["“Perhaps,”", "\"어쩌면요.\""], ["returned the count, smiling.", "백작이 미소 지으며 대꾸했다."]], [["“My dear count, you have no idea what pleasure it gives me to hear you speak thus,” said Morcerf.", "\"친애하는 백작님, 이렇게 말씀하시는 걸 들으니 제가 얼마나 기쁜지 모르실 겁니다.\""], ["“I had announced you beforehand to my friends as an enchanter of the _Arabian Nights_, a wizard of the Middle Ages; but the Parisians are so subtle in paradoxes that they mistake for caprices of the imagination the most incontestable truths, when these truths do not form a part of their daily existence.", "모르세르가 말했다. \"저는 미리 친구들에게 당신을 『아라비안나이트』의 마법사, 중세의 마술사라고 소개했습니다. 그런데 파리지앵들은 역설에 어찌나 능한지, 의심할 여지 없는 진실이라도 그것이 자기들 일상의 일부가 아니면 그저 상상의 변덕으로 치부해 버리지요."], ["For example, here is Debray who reads, and Beauchamp who prints, every day, ‘A member of the Jockey Club has been stopped and robbed on the Boulevard;’ ‘four persons have been assassinated in the Rue St. Denis’ or ‘the Faubourg St.", "예를 들어, 여기 드브레는 읽고 보샹은 인쇄하는데, 날마다 '조키 클럽 회원 한 명이 큰길에서 붙잡혀 강도를 당했다', '생드니 거리'나 '생제르맹 교외에서 네 사람이 살해됐다', '탕플 큰길의 어느 카페나 쥘리앵 욕장에서 도둑 열, 열다섯, 스무 명이 체포됐다'고 하지요."], ["Germain;’ ‘ten, fifteen, or twenty thieves, have been arrested in a _café_ on the Boulevard du Temple, or in the Thermes de Julien,’—and yet these same men deny the existence of the bandits in the Maremma, the Campagna di Romana, or the Pontine Marshes.", "그러면서도 이 같은 사람들이 마렘마나 로마 평원, 폰티노 습지에 산적들이 존재한다는 건 부정합니다."], ["Tell them yourself that I was taken by bandits, and that without your generous intercession I should now have been sleeping in the Catacombs of St. Sebastian, instead of receiving them in my humble abode in the Rue du Helder.”", "제가 산적들에게 붙잡혔다는 것, 그리고 당신의 너그러운 중재가 없었다면 지금쯤 엘데르 거리의 누추한 제 거처에서 이들을 맞이하는 대신 성 세바스티아노 카타콤에서 잠들어 있었으리라는 걸, 부디 직접 말씀해 주십시오.\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아.\""], ["said Monte Cristo “you promised me never to mention that circumstance.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"당신은 그 일을 절대 입에 올리지 않겠다고 제게 약속했습니다.\""]], [["“It was not I who made that promise,”", "\"그런 약속을 한 건 제가 아닙니다.\""], ["cried Morcerf; “it must have been someone else whom you have rescued in the same manner, and whom you have forgotten.", "모르세르가 외쳤다. \"같은 방식으로 구해 주셨다가 잊어버리신 다른 누군가였겠지요. 부디 그 얘기를 해 주십시오."], ["Pray speak of it, for I shall not only, I trust, relate the little I do know, but also a great deal I do not know.”", "제가 아는 것 몇 가지뿐 아니라, 제가 모르는 것도 잔뜩 이야기해 드릴 테니까요.\""]], [["“It seems to me,”", "\"제가 보기엔,\""], ["returned the count, smiling, “that you played a sufficiently important part to know as well as myself what happened.”", "백작이 미소 지으며 대꾸했다. \"당신이 꽤 중요한 역할을 했으니, 무슨 일이 있었는지 저만큼이나 잘 알고 계실 텐데요.\""]], [["20249m\n“Well, you promise me, if I tell all I know, to relate, in your turn, all that I do not know?”", "\"좋습니다, 제가 아는 걸 전부 이야기하면, 당신도 이번엔 제가 모르는 걸 전부 이야기해 주시겠다고 약속하시는 겁니까?\""]], [["“That is but fair,”", "\"그거야 공평한 일이지요.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"좋습니다.\""], ["said Morcerf, “for three days I believed myself the object of the attentions of a masque, whom I took for a descendant of Tullia or Poppæa, while I was simply the object of the attentions of a _contadina_, and I say _contadina_ to avoid saying peasant girl.", "모르세르가 말했다. \"사흘 동안 저는 어떤 가면을 쓴 여인이 제게 마음을 두고 있다고 믿었습니다. 저는 그녀를 툴리아나 포파이아의 후손쯤으로 여겼는데, 실은 그저 콘타디나가 제게 마음을 둔 것이었지요."], ["What I know is, that, like a fool, a greater fool than he of whom I spoke just now, I mistook for this peasant girl a young bandit of fifteen or sixteen, with a beardless chin and slim waist, and who, just as I was about to imprint a chaste salute on his lips, placed a pistol to my head, and, aided by seven or eight others, led, or rather dragged me, to the Catacombs of St.", "시골 처녀라는 말을 피하려고 콘타디나라고 하겠습니다. 제가 아는 거라곤, 제가 바보같이, 그것도 방금 말한 그 친구보다 더한 바보같이, 이 시골 처녀를 열대여섯 살쯤 된 어린 산적으로 잘못 봤다는 겁니다. 턱에는 수염도 안 났고 허리는 호리호리한 녀석이었지요."], ["Sebastian, where I found a highly educated brigand chief perusing Cæsar’s _Commentaries_, and who deigned to leave off reading to inform me, that unless the next morning, before six o’clock, four thousand piastres were paid into his account at his banker’s, at a quarter past six I should have ceased to exist.", "제가 그 입술에 정숙한 입맞춤을 막 새기려는 찰나, 그가 제 머리에 권총을 들이대더니, 일고여덟 명의 패거리와 함께 저를 성 세바스티아노 카타콤으로 끌고, 아니 질질 끌고 갔습니다. 거기서 저는 카이사르의 『갈리아 전기』를 정독하고 있는, 대단히 교양 있는 산적 두목을 만났지요. 그는 황송하게도 책 읽기를 멈추고 제게 알려 주더군요."], ["The letter is still to be seen, for it is in Franz d’Épinay’s possession, signed by me, and with a postscript of M. Luigi Vampa.", "이튿날 아침 여섯 시 전까지 4천 피아스트르가 그의 은행 계좌로 입금되지 않으면, 여섯 시 십오 분에는 제가 존재하기를 그칠 거라고요. 그 편지는 지금도 볼 수 있습니다."], ["This is all I know, but I know not, count, how you contrived to inspire so much respect in the bandits of Rome who ordinarily have so little respect for anything.", "프란츠 데피네가 가지고 있는데, 제 서명이 들어 있고 루이지 밤파 씨의 추신이 달려 있지요. 제가 아는 건 이게 전부입니다. 다만 백작님, 평소 무엇 하나 좀처럼 존중하는 법이 없는 로마의 산적들에게 어떻게 그토록 큰 존경심을 불어넣으셨는지는 모르겠습니다."], ["I assure you, Franz and I were lost in admiration.”", "정말이지 프란츠와 저는 감탄을 금치 못했습니다.\""]], [["“Nothing more simple,”", "\"더없이 간단한 일입니다.\""], ["returned the count.", "백작이 대꾸했다."], ["“I had known the famous Vampa for more than ten years.", "\"저는 그 유명한 밤파를 십 년도 더 전부터 알고 있었습니다."], ["When he was quite a child, and only a shepherd, I gave him a few gold pieces for showing me my way, and he, in order to repay me, gave me a poniard, the hilt of which he had carved with his own hand, and which you may have seen in my collection of arms.", "그가 아직 어린아이로 양치기에 불과했을 때, 길을 가르쳐 준 대가로 제가 금화 몇 닢을 주었지요. 그러자 그는 그 보답으로 제게 단검 하나를 주었는데, 손잡이를 제 손으로—아니, 자기 손으로 직접 새긴 것이었습니다. 제 무기 수집품 중에서 보셨을지도 모르겠군요."], ["In after years, whether he had forgotten this interchange of presents, which ought to have cemented our friendship, or whether he did not recollect me, he sought to take me, but, on the contrary, it was I who captured him and a dozen of his band.", "세월이 흐른 뒤, 우리 우정을 굳혔어야 할 그 선물 교환을 잊었는지, 아니면 저를 기억하지 못했는지, 그가 저를 붙잡으려 했지요. 그런데 오히려 제가 그와 그의 패거리 열두 명을 사로잡았습니다. 저는 그를 로마 사법 당국에 넘길 수도 있었지요."], ["I might have handed him over to Roman justice, which is somewhat expeditious, and which would have been particularly so with him; but I did nothing of the sort—I suffered him and his band to depart.”", "그쪽은 일 처리가 꽤 신속하고, 특히 그에게라면 더더욱 그랬을 겁니다. 하지만 저는 그러지 않았습니다. 그와 그의 패거리가 떠나도록 내버려 두었지요.\""]], [["“With the condition that they should sin no more,”", "\"두 번 다시 죄를 짓지 않는다는 조건으로 말이지요.\""], ["said Beauchamp, laughing. “I see they kept their promise.”", "보샹이 웃으며 말했다. \"보아하니 그들이 약속을 지킨 모양이군요.\""]], [["“No, monsieur,”", "\"아닙니다, 선생.\""], ["returned Monte Cristo “upon the simple condition that they should respect myself and my friends.", "몬테크리스토가 대꾸했다. \"그저 저와 제 친구들을 존중한다는 단순한 조건이었습니다. 어쩌면 제가 지금 하려는 말이, 박애를 떠벌리고 이웃에 대한 의무를 자랑하는 사회주의자인 여러분에게는 이상하게 들릴지도 모르겠습니다."], ["Perhaps what I am about to say may seem strange to you, who are socialists, and vaunt humanity and your duty to your neighbor, but I never seek to protect a society which does not protect me, and which I will even say, generally occupies itself about me only to injure me; and thus by giving them a low place in my esteem, and preserving a neutrality towards them, it is society and my neighbor who are indebted to me.”", "하지만 저는 저를 보호해 주지 않는 사회를 결코 보호하려 들지 않습니다. 게다가 그 사회는, 굳이 말하자면, 대체로 저를 해치기 위해서만 저에게 신경을 쓰지요. 그래서 그 사회를 제 평가에서 낮은 자리에 두고 그것에 중립을 지킴으로써, 오히려 사회와 제 이웃이 제게 빚을 지는 셈입니다.\""]], [["“Bravo,”", "\"브라보!\""], ["cried Château-Renaud; “you are the first man I ever met sufficiently courageous to preach egotism.", "샤토르노가 외쳤다. \"이기주의를 설파할 만큼 용감한 사람은 제 평생 처음 봅니다."], ["Bravo, count, bravo!”", "브라보, 백작님, 브라보!\""]], [["“It is frank, at least,”", "\"적어도 솔직하긴 합니다.\""], ["said Morrel.", "모렐이 말했다."], ["“But I am sure that the count does not regret having once deviated from the principles he has so boldly avowed.”", "\"하지만 백작님이 그토록 대담하게 천명하신 원칙에서 한 번 벗어난 것을 후회하지 않으시리라 확신합니다.\""]], [["“How have I deviated from those principles, monsieur?”", "\"제가 그 원칙에서 어떻게 벗어났다는 겁니까, 선생?\""], ["asked Monte Cristo, who could not help looking at Morrel with so much intensity, that two or three times the young man had been unable to sustain that clear and piercing glance.", "몬테크리스토가 물었다. 그는 모렐을 어찌나 강렬하게 바라보던지, 그 청년이 두세 번이나 그 맑고 꿰뚫는 듯한 시선을 견디지 못할 정도였다."]], [["“Why, it seems to me,”", "\"그게, 제가 보기엔,\""], ["replied Morrel, “that in delivering M. de Morcerf, whom you did not know, you did good to your neighbor and to society.”", "모렐이 대답했다. \"알지도 못하는 모르세르 씨를 구해 주심으로써, 당신은 이웃과 사회에 선행을 베푸신 거니까요.\""]], [["“Of which he is the brightest ornament,” said Beauchamp, drinking off a glass of champagne.", "\"그가 바로 그 사회의 가장 빛나는 장식이지요.\" 보샹이 샴페인 한 잔을 들이켜며 말했다."]], [["“My dear count,”", "\"친애하는 백작님,\" 모르세르가 외쳤다."], ["cried Morcerf, “you are at fault—you, one of the most formidable logicians I know—and you must see it clearly proved that instead of being an egotist, you are a philanthropist.", "\"당신이 틀렸습니다. 제가 아는 가장 가공할 논리가 중 한 분이신 당신이 말입니다. 그리고 당신은 이기주의자가 아니라 박애주의자라는 게 명백히 입증되는 걸 똑똑히 보셔야 합니다."], ["Ah, you call yourself Oriental, a Levantine, Maltese, Indian, Chinese; your family name is Monte Cristo; Sinbad the Sailor is your baptismal appellation, and yet the first day you set foot in Paris you instinctively display the greatest virtue, or rather the chief defect, of us eccentric Parisians,—that is, you assume the vices you have not, and conceal the virtues you possess.”", "아, 당신은 자신을 동방인이니 레반트인이니 몰타인이니 인도인이니 중국인이니 하시고, 성(姓)은 몬테크리스토에, 세례명은 뱃사람 신드바드라 하십니다. 그러면서도 파리에 발을 들인 첫날부터, 우리 별난 파리지앵들의 가장 큰 미덕, 아니 가장 큰 결점을 본능적으로 드러내시는군요. 그러니까 갖지도 않은 악덕은 짐짓 꾸미고, 지닌 미덕은 감추시는 것 말입니다.\""]], [["“My dear vicomte,”", "\"친애하는 자작,\""], ["returned Monte Cristo, “I do not see, in all I have done, anything that merits, either from you or these gentlemen, the pretended eulogies I have received.", "몬테크리스토가 대꾸했다. \"제가 한 모든 일에서, 당신이나 이분들에게서 받은 그 거창한 찬사를 받을 만한 점은 하나도 보이지 않습니다. 당신은 제게 낯선 사람이 아니었지요."], ["You were no stranger to me, for I knew you from the time I gave up two rooms to you, invited you to breakfast with me, lent you one of my carriages, witnessed the Carnival in your company, and saw with you from a window in the Piazza del Popolo the execution that affected you so much that you nearly fainted.", "저는 당신에게 방 두 개를 내주고, 함께 아침을 들자고 청하고, 제 마차 한 대를 빌려주고, 당신과 함께 사육제를 구경하고, 포폴로 광장의 어느 창가에서 당신과 함께 그 사형 집행을 보았던 그때부터 당신을 알았으니까요. 당신은 그 광경에 어찌나 마음이 흔들렸던지 거의 까무러칠 뻔했지요."], ["I will appeal to any of these gentlemen, could I leave my guest in the hands of a hideous bandit, as you term him?", "이분들 중 누구에게라도 여쭤보겠습니다. 제가 손님을, 당신 표현대로 그 흉측한 산적의 손에 내버려 둘 수 있었겠습니까?"], ["Besides, you know, I had the idea that you could introduce me into some of the Paris salons when I came to France.", "게다가 아시다시피, 저는 프랑스에 오면 당신이 저를 파리의 살롱 몇 군데에 소개해 줄 수 있으리라는 생각을 품고 있었습니다."], ["You might some time ago have looked upon this resolution as a vague project, but today you see it was a reality, and you must submit to it under penalty of breaking your word.”", "얼마 전만 해도 당신은 이 결심을 막연한 계획쯤으로 여겼을지 모르지만, 오늘 보시다시피 그것은 현실이었지요. 그러니 약속을 어겼다는 벌을 받지 않으려면 당신은 이에 응하셔야 합니다.\""]], [["“I will keep it,”", "\"지키겠습니다.\""], ["returned Morcerf; “but I fear that you will be much disappointed, accustomed as you are to picturesque events and fantastic horizons.", "모르세르가 대꾸했다. \"다만 그림 같은 사건들과 환상적인 지평선에 익숙하신 당신이 크게 실망하실까 봐 걱정입니다."], ["Amongst us you will not meet with any of those episodes with which your adventurous existence has so familiarized you; our Chimborazo is Mortmartre, our Himalaya is Mount Valérien, our Great Desert is the plain of Grenelle, where they are now boring an artesian well to water the caravans.", "우리 사이에서는 당신의 모험적인 삶이 그토록 친숙하게 만들어 놓은 그런 일화들을 하나도 만나지 못하실 겁니다. 우리의 침보라소는 몽마르트르고, 우리의 히말라야는 발레리앵산이며, 우리의 대사막은 그르넬 평원입니다. 거기서는 지금 대상(隊商)들에게 물을 대려고 자분정을 파고 있지요."], ["We have plenty of thieves, though not so many as is said; but these thieves stand in far more dread of a policeman than a lord.", "도둑이야 넘쳐 나지만, 말들 만큼 많지는 않습니다. 그리고 이 도둑들은 영주보다 경찰관을 훨씬 더 무서워하지요."], ["France is so prosaic, and Paris so civilized a city, that you will not find in its eighty-five departments—I say eighty-five, because I do not include Corsica—you will not find, then, in these eighty-five departments a single hill on which there is not a telegraph, or a grotto in which the commissary of police has not put up a gaslamp.", "프랑스는 너무나 산문적이고, 파리는 너무나 문명화된 도시라, 그 여든다섯 개 주(州)에서—코르시카는 빼니까 여든다섯이라고 하는 겁니다—그러니까 이 여든다섯 개 주에서, 전신주가 없는 언덕 하나, 경찰서장이 가스등을 달아 놓지 않은 동굴 하나 찾지 못하실 겁니다. 제가 당신께 해 드릴 수 있는 봉사는 단 하나뿐이고, 그 일이라면 전적으로 당신 분부를 따르겠습니다."], ["There is but one service I can render you, and for that I place myself entirely at your orders, that is, to present, or make my friends present, you everywhere; besides, you have no need of anyone to introduce you—with your name, and your fortune, and your talent”", "그러니까 어디서든 당신을 소개하거나, 제 친구들이 소개하도록 하는 것이지요. 사실 당신은 누가 소개해 줄 필요도 없습니다. 당신의 이름과 재산과 재능이라면\""], ["(Monte Cristo bowed with a somewhat ironical smile) “you can present yourself everywhere, and be well received.", "(몬테크리스토가 다소 빈정거리는 미소를 띠며 고개를 숙였다) \"어디든 스스로 나설 수 있고, 환대받으실 테니까요."], ["I can be useful in one way only—if knowledge of Parisian habits, of the means of rendering yourself comfortable, or of the bazaars, can assist, you may depend upon me to find you a fitting dwelling here.", "제가 쓸모 있는 길은 한 가지뿐입니다. 파리의 관습이나, 편히 지내는 방법이나, 시장에 관한 지식이 도움이 된다면, 여기서 당신께 알맞은 거처를 찾아 드리는 일은 저를 믿으셔도 됩니다. 제가 로마에서 당신의 방을 함께 썼던 것처럼 감히 제 거처를 함께 쓰자고 청하지는 못하겠습니다."], ["I do not dare offer to share my apartments with you, as I shared yours at Rome—I, who do not profess egotism, but am yet egotist _par excellence_; for, except myself, these rooms would not hold a shadow more, unless that shadow were feminine.”", "이기주의를 표방하지는 않지만, 그럼에도 더없는 이기주의자인 제가 말입니다. 저 말고는, 그 방에 그림자 하나 더 들어설 자리가 없거든요. 그 그림자가 여성의 것이 아니라면요.\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아.\""], ["said the count, “that is a most conjugal reservation; I recollect that at Rome you said something of a projected marriage.", "백작이 말했다. \"그건 참으로 부부다운 단서로군요. 로마에서 당신이 결혼 계획 같은 걸 말씀하셨던 게 기억납니다."], ["May I congratulate you?”", "축하드려도 될까요?\""]], [["“The affair is still in projection.”", "\"아직 계획 단계입니다.\""]], [["“And he who says in ‘projection,’ means already decided,”", "\"그리고 '계획 단계'라고 말하는 사람은 이미 결정됐다는 뜻이지요.\""], ["said Debray.", "드브레가 말했다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니요.\""], ["replied Morcerf, “my father is most anxious about it; and I hope, ere long, to introduce you, if not to my wife, at least to my betrothed—Mademoiselle Eugénie Danglars.”", "모르세르가 대꾸했다. \"아버지가 그 일에 무척 신경을 쓰고 계시지요. 머지않아 당신께, 제 아내까지는 아니어도 적어도 약혼녀인 외제니 당글라르 양을 소개할 수 있기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["“Eugénie Danglars,”", "\"외제니 당글라르라.\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “tell me, is not her father Baron Danglars?”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"말해 보십시오, 그녀의 아버지가 당글라르 남작 아닙니까?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"맞습니다.\""], ["returned Morcerf, “a baron of a new creation.”", "모르세르가 대꾸했다. \"새로 작위를 받은 남작이지요.\""]], [["“What matter,”", "\"그게 무슨 상관입니까,\""], ["said Monte Cristo “if he has rendered the State services which merit this distinction?”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그가 이 영예를 받을 만한 봉사를 국가에 했다면요?\""]], [["“Enormous ones,” answered Beauchamp.", "\"엄청난 봉사를 했지요.\" 보샹이 대답했다."], ["“Although in reality a Liberal, he negotiated a loan of six millions for Charles X., in 1829, who made him a baron and chevalier of the Legion of Honor; so that he wears the ribbon, not, as you would think, in his waistcoat-pocket, but at his button-hole.”", "\"사실은 자유주의자면서도, 1829년에 샤를 10세를 위해 6백만 차관을 협상해 냈거든요. 그 덕에 그를 남작이자 레지옹 도뇌르 훈장 기사로 만들어 주었지요. 그래서 그는 그 훈장 리본을, 당신이 생각하듯 조끼 주머니에 넣고 다니는 게 아니라, 단춧구멍에 달고 다닙니다.\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아.\""], ["interrupted Morcerf, laughing, “Beauchamp, Beauchamp, keep that for the _Corsaire_ or the _Charivari_, but spare my future father-in-law before me.”", "모르세르가 웃으며 끼어들었다. \"보샹, 보샹, 그런 얘기는 『코르세르』나 『샤리바리』에나 써먹고, 내 앞에서 내 장래의 장인은 좀 봐주게.\""], ["Then, turning to Monte Cristo, “You just now spoke his name as if you knew the baron?”", "그러고는 몬테크리스토를 돌아보며 말했다. \"방금 그 이름을 마치 남작을 아시는 것처럼 말씀하시던데요?\""]], [["“I do not know him,”", "\"그를 알지는 못합니다.\""], ["returned Monte Cristo; “but I shall probably soon make his acquaintance, for I have a credit opened with him by the house of Richard & Blount, of London, Arstein & Eskeles of Vienna, and Thomson & French at Rome.”", "몬테크리스토가 대꾸했다. \"하지만 머지않아 알게 될 것 같군요. 런던의 리처드 앤드 블런트 상회, 빈의 아르슈타인 앤드 에스켈레스 상회, 그리고 로마의 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회를 통해 그에게 신용 거래가 개설되어 있으니까요.\""], ["As he pronounced the two last names, the count glanced at Maximilian Morrel.", "마지막 두 이름을 발음할 때, 백작은 막시밀리앙 모렐을 흘끗 보았다."], ["If the stranger expected to produce an effect on Morrel, he was not mistaken—Maximilian started as if he had been electrified.", "이방인이 모렐에게 어떤 반응을 끌어내려 했다면, 그는 빗나가지 않았다. 막시밀리앙이 마치 전기에 감전된 듯 흠칫했다."]], [["“Thomson & French,”", "\"톰슨 앤드 프렌치라.\""], ["said he; “do you know this house, monsieur?”", "그가 말했다. \"이 상회를 아십니까, 선생?\""]], [["20253m\n“They are my bankers in the capital of the Christian world,”", "\"그들은 기독교 세계의 수도에 있는 제 거래 은행입니다.\""], ["returned the count quietly.", "백작이 차분히 대꾸했다."], ["“Can my influence with them be of any service to you?”", "\"그들에 대한 제 영향력이 당신께 어떤 도움이 될 수 있을까요?\""]], [["“Oh, count, you could assist me perhaps in researches which have been, up to the present, fruitless.", "\"오, 백작님, 어쩌면 지금까지 헛수고로 끝났던 어떤 조사에 도움을 주실 수 있을지도 모르겠습니다."], ["This house, in past years, did ours a great service, and has, I know not for what reason, always denied having rendered us this service.”", "그 상회는 지난날 저희 상회에 큰 도움을 베풀었는데, 무슨 까닭에서인지 그 도움을 베풀었다는 사실을 줄곧 부인해 왔거든요.\""]], [["“I shall be at your orders,” said Monte Cristo bowing.", "\"분부대로 하겠습니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 고개를 숙이며 말했다."]], [["“But,”", "\"그런데,\""], ["continued Morcerf, “_à propos_ of Danglars,—we have strangely wandered from the subject.", "모르세르가 말을 이었다. \"당글라르 얘기가 나온 김에 말하자면, 우리가 주제에서 참 멀리도 벗어났군요."], ["We were speaking of a suitable habitation for the Count of Monte Cristo.", "우리는 몬테크리스토 백작께 알맞은 거처를 이야기하던 중이었지요."], ["Come, gentlemen, let us all propose some place.", "자, 여러분, 다들 어디 한 군데씩 제안해 봅시다."], ["Where shall we lodge this new guest in our great capital?”", "이 새 손님을 우리 대도시 어디에 모실까요?\""]], [["“Faubourg Saint-Germain,”", "\"생제르맹 교외.\""], ["said Château-Renaud.", "샤토르노가 말했다."], ["“The count will find there a charming hotel, with a court and garden.”", "\"백작께서는 거기서 안뜰과 정원이 딸린 멋진 저택을 찾으실 겁니다.\""]], [["“Bah!", "\"흥!"], ["Château-Renaud,”", "샤토르노,\" 드브레가 대꾸했다. \"자네는 그 따분하고 음침한 생제르맹 교외밖에 모르지."], ["returned Debray, “you only know your dull and gloomy Faubourg Saint-Germain; do not pay any attention to him, count—live in the Chaussée d’Antin, that’s the real centre of Paris.”", "백작님, 저 친구 말엔 신경 쓰지 마십시오. 쇼세 당탱에 사십시오. 거기가 진짜 파리의 중심입니다.\""]], [["“Boulevard de l’Opéra,”", "\"오페라 큰길.\""], ["said Beauchamp; “the second floor—a house with a balcony.", "보샹이 말했다. \"발코니가 딸린 집의 2층이지요."], ["The count will have his cushions of silver cloth brought there, and as he smokes his chibouque, see all Paris pass before him.”", "백작께서는 거기로 은직물 방석을 가져다 놓으시고, 치부크를 피우며 온 파리가 눈앞으로 지나가는 걸 보실 수 있을 겁니다.\""]], [["“You have no idea, then, Morrel?”", "\"그럼 자네는 생각이 없나, 모렐?\""], ["asked Château-Renaud; “you do not propose anything.”", "샤토르노가 물었다. \"아무것도 제안하지 않는군.\""]], [["“Oh, yes,”", "\"오, 아닙니다.\" 청년이 미소 지으며 대꾸했다. \"오히려 하나 있습니다."], ["returned the young man, smiling; “on the contrary, I have one, but I expected the count would be tempted by one of the brilliant proposals made him, yet as he has not replied to any of them, I will venture to offer him a suite of apartments in a charming hotel, in the Pompadour style, that my sister has inhabited for a year, in the Rue Meslay.”", "다만 백작께서 다른 분들의 그 멋진 제안 중 하나에 마음이 끌리시리라 생각해서요. 그런데 어느 것에도 답하지 않으시니, 감히 한 가지 제안을 드려 보겠습니다. 멜레 거리에 있는 매력적인 저택의 폼파두르 양식 방들인데, 제 누이가 일 년째 살고 있는 곳이지요.\""]], [["“You have a sister?”", "\"누이가 있으십니까?\""], ["asked the count.", "백작이 물었다."]], [["“Yes, monsieur, a most excellent sister.”", "\"네, 선생, 더없이 훌륭한 누이지요.\""]], [["“Married?”", "\"결혼은 하셨고요?\""]], [["“Nearly nine years.”", "\"거의 구 년째입니다.\""]], [["“Happy?” asked the count again.", "\"행복하신가요?\" 백작이 다시 물었다."]], [["“As happy as it is permitted to a human creature to be,”", "\"인간이라는 존재에게 허락된 만큼 행복하지요.\""], ["replied Maximilian.", "막시밀리앙이 대답했다."], ["“She married the man she loved, who remained faithful to us in our fallen fortunes—Emmanuel Herbaut.”", "\"누이는 사랑하는 사람과 결혼했습니다. 저희 집안이 몰락했을 때도 끝까지 우리에게 의리를 지켜 준 사람, 에마뉘엘 에르보 말입니다.\""]], [["Monte Cristo smiled imperceptibly.", "몬테크리스토가 알아챌 듯 말 듯 미소 지었다."]], [["“I live there during my leave of absence,”", "\"저는 휴가 중에 거기서 지냅니다.\""], ["continued Maximilian; “and I shall be, together with my brother-in-law Emmanuel, at the disposition of the Count, whenever he thinks fit to honor us.”", "막시밀리앙이 말을 이었다. \"그리고 백작께서 언제든 저희를 찾아 주신다면, 매부 에마뉘엘과 함께 백작님의 분부를 따르겠습니다.\""]], [["“One minute,”", "\"잠깐만.\""], ["cried Albert, without giving Monte Cristo the time to reply.", "몬테크리스토에게 대답할 틈을 주지 않고 알베르가 외쳤다. \"조심하게."], ["“Take care, you are going to immure a traveller, Sinbad the Sailor, a man who comes to see Paris; you are going to make a patriarch of him.” 20255m", "자네는 여행자를, 뱃사람 신드바드를, 파리를 구경하러 온 사람을 가둬 두려는 거야. 그를 가부장 같은 사람으로 만들 셈인가.\""]], [["“Oh, no,”", "\"오, 아닐세.\""], ["said Morrel; “my sister is five-and-twenty, my brother-in-law is thirty, they are gay, young, and happy.", "모렐이 말했다. \"제 누이는 스물다섯이고 매부는 서른인데, 둘 다 명랑하고 젊고 행복하다네."], ["Besides, the count will be in his own house, and only see them when he thinks fit to do so.”", "게다가 백작께서는 자기 집에 계시는 셈이라, 마음 내키실 때만 그들을 보시면 되지.\""]], [["“Thanks, monsieur,”", "\"고맙습니다, 선생.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["said Monte Cristo; “I shall content myself with being presented to your sister and her husband, if you will do me the honor to introduce me; but I cannot accept the offer of anyone of these gentlemen, since my habitation is already prepared.”", "\"당신이 영광스럽게도 소개해 주신다면, 저는 당신의 누이와 그 남편께 인사드리는 것으로 만족하겠습니다. 하지만 이분들 중 누구의 제안도 받아들일 수가 없군요. 제 거처는 이미 마련되어 있으니까요.\""]], [["“What,”", "\"뭐라고요!\""], ["cried Morcerf; “you are, then, going to a hotel—that will be very dull for you.”", "모르세르가 외쳤다. \"그럼 호텔로 가시는 겁니까? 그건 무척 따분하실 텐데요.\""]], [["“Was I so badly lodged at Rome?”", "\"제가 로마에서 그렇게 형편없이 묵었던가요?\""], ["said Monte Cristo smiling.", "몬테크리스토가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“_Parbleu!_ at Rome you spent fifty thousand piastres in furnishing your apartments, but I presume that you are not disposed to spend a similar sum every day.”", "\"제기랄! 로마에서야 방을 꾸미는 데 5만 피아스트르를 쓰셨지요. 하지만 날마다 그만한 돈을 쓸 마음은 없으시겠지요.\""]], [["“It is not that which deterred me,”", "\"저를 망설이게 한 건 그게 아닙니다.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo; “but as I determined to have a house to myself, I sent on my valet de chambre, and he ought by this time to have bought the house and furnished it.”", "몬테크리스토가 대꾸했다. \"다만 저는 제 집을 따로 갖기로 정하고, 제 시종을 먼저 보냈습니다. 그라면 지금쯤 집을 사서 가구까지 들여놓았을 겁니다.\""]], [["“But you have, then, a valet de chambre who knows Paris?”", "\"그럼 파리를 아는 시종이 있으시군요?\""], ["said Beauchamp.", "보샹이 말했다."]], [["“It is the first time he has ever been in Paris.", "\"그가 파리에 와 본 건 이번이 처음입니다."], ["He is black, and cannot speak,”", "그는 흑인이고, 말을 못 하지요.\""], ["returned Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 대꾸했다."]], [["“It is Ali!” cried Albert, in the midst of the general surprise.", "\"알리로군요!\" 일동의 놀라움 속에서 알베르가 외쳤다."]], [["“Yes, Ali himself, my Nubian mute, whom you saw, I think, at Rome.”", "\"네, 바로 그 알리, 제 누비아인 벙어리지요. 로마에서 보셨을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Certainly,”", "\"물론입니다.\""], ["said Morcerf; “I recollect him perfectly.", "모르세르가 말했다. \"그를 똑똑히 기억합니다."], ["But how could you charge a Nubian to purchase a house, and a mute to furnish it?—he will do everything wrong.”", "하지만 어떻게 누비아인에게 집을 사오라 맡기고, 벙어리에게 가구를 들이라 맡기실 수 있습니까? 모든 걸 엉망으로 해 놓을 텐데요.\""]], [["“Undeceive yourself, monsieur,”", "\"오해십니다, 선생.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo; “I am quite sure, that, on the contrary, he will choose everything as I wish.", "몬테크리스토가 대꾸했다. \"저는 오히려, 그가 모든 걸 제 뜻대로 골라 놓으리라 확신합니다."], ["He knows my tastes, my caprices, my wants.", "그는 제 취향과 변덕과 필요를 다 알지요."], ["He has been here a week, with the instinct of a hound, hunting by himself.", "그는 일주일 전부터 여기 와서, 사냥개 같은 본능으로 혼자 돌아다니며 찾아냈습니다."], ["He will arrange everything for me. He knew, that I should arrive today at ten o’clock; he was waiting for me at nine at the Barrière de Fontainebleau.", "그가 저를 위해 모든 걸 정돈해 놓을 겁니다. 제가 오늘 열 시에 도착하리라는 걸 알고, 아홉 시에 퐁텐블로 관문에서 저를 기다리고 있었지요."], ["He gave me this paper; it contains the number of my new abode; read it yourself,”", "그가 제게 이 종이를 건넸습니다. 제 새 거처의 번지가 적혀 있는데, 직접 읽어 보십시오.\""], ["and Monte Cristo passed a paper to Albert.", "그러고는 몬테크리스토가 종이 한 장을 알베르에게 건넸다."]], [["“Ah, that is really original,”", "\"아, 정말 독창적이군요.\""], ["said Beauchamp.", "보샹이 말했다."]], [["“And very princely,”", "\"그리고 매우 군주답고요.\""], ["added Château-Renaud.", "샤토르노가 덧붙였다."]], [["“What, do you not know your house?”", "\"뭐라고요, 당신 집을 모르신단 말입니까?\""], ["asked Debray.", "드브레가 물었다."]], [["“No,”", "\"모릅니다.\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “I told you I did not wish to be behind my time; I dressed myself in the carriage, and descended at the viscount’s door.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"말씀드렸다시피, 저는 늦고 싶지 않았습니다. 마차 안에서 옷을 차려입고, 자작의 문 앞에서 내렸지요.\""], ["The young men looked at each other; they did not know if it was a comedy Monte Cristo was playing, but every word he uttered had such an air of simplicity, that it was impossible to suppose what he said was false—besides, why should he tell a falsehood?", "젊은이들은 서로를 마주 보았다. 그들은 몬테크리스토가 한 편의 희극을 연기하고 있는 것인지 알 수 없었지만, 그가 내뱉는 말마다 어찌나 소박한 기운이 감돌던지, 그의 말이 거짓이라고는 도무지 생각할 수 없었다. 게다가 그가 무엇 때문에 거짓말을 하겠는가?"]], [["“We must content ourselves, then,”", "\"그렇다면 우리는,\" 보샹이 말했다."], ["said Beauchamp, “with rendering the count all the little services in our power.", "\"우리 힘이 닿는 자잘한 도움이나마 백작께 다 해 드리는 것으로 만족해야겠군요."], ["I, in my quality of journalist, open all the theatres to him.”", "저는 언론인의 자격으로, 모든 극장을 백작께 열어 드리겠습니다.\""]], [["“Thanks, monsieur,”", "\"고맙습니다, 선생.\""], ["returned Monte Cristo, “my steward has orders to take a box at each theatre.”", "몬테크리스토가 대꾸했다. \"제 집사가 극장마다 특별석을 하나씩 잡아 두라는 지시를 받았습니다.\""]], [["“Is your steward also a Nubian?” asked Debray.", "\"집사도 누비아인입니까?\" 드브레가 물었다."]], [["“No, he is a countryman of yours, if a Corsican is a countryman of anyone’s.", "\"아니요, 그는 당신네 동포입니다. 코르시카 사람이 누군가의 동포일 수 있다면 말이지요."], ["But you know him, M. de Morcerf.”", "그런데 당신은 그를 아십니다, 모르세르 씨.\""]], [["“Is it that excellent M. Bertuccio, who understands hiring windows so well?”", "\"창문 빌리는 일을 그토록 잘 아는, 그 훌륭한 베르투치오 씨 말씀입니까?\""]], [["“Yes, you saw him the day I had the honor of receiving you; he has been a soldier, a smuggler—in fact, everything.", "\"네, 제가 영광스럽게도 당신을 맞이하던 날 보셨지요. 그는 군인이었고, 밀수꾼이었고, 사실상 안 해 본 게 없는 사람입니다."], ["I would not be quite sure that he has not been mixed up with the police for some trifle—a stab with a knife, for instance.”", "어쩌면 사소한 일로—이를테면 칼부림 같은 것으로 경찰과 얽힌 적이 없다고는 장담 못 하겠군요.\""]], [["“And you have chosen this honest citizen for your steward,” said Debray.", "\"그런 정직한 시민을 집사로 고르셨군요.\" 드브레가 말했다."], ["“Of how much does he rob you every year?”", "\"해마다 당신에게서 얼마나 훔쳐 갑니까?\""]], [["“On my word,”", "\"솔직히,\""], ["replied the count, “not more than another.", "백작이 대꾸했다. \"다른 자들보다 더 많이는 아닙니다."], ["I am sure he answers my purpose, knows no impossibility, and so I keep him.”", "그가 제 목적에 맞고, 불가능이란 걸 모른다고 확신하기에, 그래서 데리고 있는 거지요.\""]], [["“Then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["continued Château-Renaud, “since you have an establishment, a steward, and a hotel in the Champs-Élysées, you only want a mistress.”", "샤토르노가 말을 이었다. \"이미 살림과 집사와 샹젤리제의 저택까지 갖추셨으니, 이제 정부(情婦)만 있으면 되겠군요.\""], ["Albert smiled. He thought of the fair Greek he had seen in the count’s box at the Argentina and Valle theatres.", "알베르가 미소 지었다. 그는 아르헨티나 극장과 발레 극장의 백작 특별석에서 보았던 그 아름다운 그리스 여인을 떠올렸다."]], [["“I have something better than that,”", "\"저는 그보다 더 나은 걸 가지고 있습니다.\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “I have a slave.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"저에겐 노예가 있지요."], ["You procure your mistresses from the opera, the Vaudeville, or the Variétés; I purchased mine at Constantinople; it cost me more, but I have nothing to fear.”", "당신들은 오페라극장이나 보드빌극장, 바리에테극장에서 정부를 구하지만, 저는 콘스탄티노플에서 제 노예를 샀습니다. 값은 더 비쌌지만, 저는 두려워할 게 없지요.\""]], [["“But you forget,”", "\"하지만 잊으셨군요.\""], ["replied Debray, laughing, “that we are Franks by name and franks by nature, as King Charles said, and that the moment she puts her foot in France your slave becomes free.”", "드브레가 웃으며 대꾸했다. \"샤를 왕이 말했듯이 우리는 이름으로도 프랑크인이고 천성으로도 자유로운 사람들이라, 당신의 노예가 프랑스 땅에 발을 들이는 순간 자유의 몸이 된다는 걸요.\""]], [["“Who will tell her?”", "\"누가 그녀에게 그걸 일러 주겠습니까?\""]], [["“The first person who sees her.”", "\"그녀를 보는 첫 번째 사람이지요.\""]], [["“She only speaks Romaic.”", "\"그녀는 로마이크어밖에 못 합니다.\""]], [["“That is different.”", "\"그렇다면 얘기가 다르군요.\""]], [["“But at least we shall see her,”", "\"하지만 적어도 우리가 그녀를 보긴 보겠지요?\""], ["said Beauchamp, “or do you keep eunuchs as well as mutes?”", "보샹이 말했다. \"아니면 벙어리뿐 아니라 환관들도 두십니까?\""]], [["“Oh, no,” replied Monte Cristo; “I do not carry brutalism so far.", "\"오, 아닙니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 대꾸했다."], ["Everyone who surrounds me is free to quit me, and when they leave me will no longer have any need of me or anyone else; it is for that reason, perhaps, that they do not quit me.”", "\"저는 야만을 그 정도까지 밀고 가지는 않습니다. 제 주위를 둘러싼 이들은 누구나 저를 떠날 자유가 있고, 떠나고 나면 저나 다른 누구도 더는 필요로 하지 않게 되지요. 어쩌면 그래서 그들이 저를 떠나지 않는 건지도 모르겠습니다.\""]], [["They had long since passed to dessert and cigars.", "그들은 진작에 디저트와 시가로 넘어가 있었다."]], [["“My dear Albert,”", "\"친애하는 알베르,\""], ["said Debray, rising, “it is half-past two.", "드브레가 일어서며 말했다. \"두 시 반일세."], ["Your guest is charming, but you leave the best company to go into the worst sometimes.", "자네 손님은 매력적이지만, 가끔은 가장 좋은 자리를 떠나 가장 형편없는 자리로 가야 할 때가 있는 법이지."], ["I must return to the minister’s.", "나는 장관께 돌아가야겠네."], ["I will tell him of the count, and we shall soon know who he is.”", "백작에 대해 말씀드리면, 우리도 곧 그가 누구인지 알게 될 걸세.\""]], [["“Take care,”", "\"조심하게.\""], ["returned Albert; “no one has been able to accomplish that.”", "알베르가 대꾸했다. \"그 일을 해낸 사람은 아무도 없었으니까.\""]], [["“Oh, we have three millions for our police; it is true they are almost always spent beforehand, but, no matter, we shall still have fifty thousand francs to spend for this purpose.”", "\"오, 우리 경찰에는 3백만이 있거든. 늘 미리 다 써 버리는 게 사실이긴 하지만, 그래도 상관없어. 이 일에 쓸 5만 프랑쯤은 여전히 남아 있을 테니까.\""]], [["“And when you know, will you tell me?”", "\"그럼 알게 되면 내게 말해 주겠나?\""]], [["“I promise you.", "\"약속하지."], ["_Au revoir_, Albert.", "잘 있게, 알베르."], ["Gentlemen, good morning.”", "여러분, 안녕히.\""]], [["As he left the room, Debray called out loudly, “My carriage.”", "방을 나서며 드브레가 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"내 마차!\""]], [["“Bravo,”", "\"브라보.\" 보샹이 알베르에게 말했다."], ["said Beauchamp to Albert; “I shall not go to the Chamber, but I have something better to offer my readers than a speech of M. Danglars.”", "\"나는 의회엔 안 가겠네. 하지만 당글라르 씨의 연설보다 내 독자들에게 내놓을 더 나은 거리가 생겼으니까.\""]], [["“For heaven’s sake, Beauchamp,”", "\"제발 부탁이네, 보샹.\""], ["returned Morcerf, “do not deprive me of the merit of introducing him everywhere.", "모르세르가 대꾸했다. \"그를 어디든 소개하는 공로를 내게서 빼앗지는 말아 주게."], ["Is he not peculiar?”", "그가 별난 사람 아닌가?\""]], [["“He is more than that,”", "\"별난 정도가 아니지.\""], ["replied Château-Renaud; “he is one of the most extraordinary men I ever saw in my life.", "샤토르노가 대꾸했다. \"내 평생 본 사람 중에 가장 비범한 인물 중 하나야."], ["Are you coming, Morrel?”", "자네 가려나, 모렐?\""]], [["“Directly I have given my card to the count, who has promised to pay us a visit at Rue Meslay, No. 14.”", "\"백작께 명함을 드리고 곧 가겠습니다. 멜레 거리 14번지로 저희를 찾아오시겠다고 약속하셨거든요.\""]], [["“Be sure I shall not fail to do so,” returned the count, bowing.", "\"꼭 들르겠습니다.\" 백작이 고개를 숙이며 대꾸했다."]], [["And Maximilian Morrel left the room with the Baron de Château-Renaud, leaving Monte Cristo alone with Morcerf.", "그리하여 막시밀리앙 모렐은 샤토르노 남작과 함께 방을 나섰고, 몬테크리스토는 모르세르와 단둘이 남았다."]], [["# Chapter 41. The Presentation", "# Chapter 41. The Presentation"]], [["When Albert found himself alone with Monte Cristo, “My dear count,”", "알베르가 몬테크리스토 백작과 단둘이 남게 되자 입을 열었다. \"백작님,\""], ["said he, “allow me to commence my services as _cicerone_ by showing you a specimen of a bachelor’s apartment.", "그가 말했다. \"안내자로서 제 임무를 시작하도록 허락해 주십시오. 우선 독신 남자의 거처가 어떤 것인지 보여 드리지요."], ["You, who are accustomed to the palaces of Italy, can amuse yourself by calculating in how many square feet a young man who is not the worst lodged in Paris can live.", "이탈리아의 궁전들에 익숙하신 분이니, 파리에서 가장 못한 축에 들지는 않는 한 젊은이가 대체 몇 평방피트 안에서 살아가는지 가늠해 보시는 것도 재미있으실 겁니다."], ["As we pass from one room to another, I will open the windows to let you breathe.”", "방에서 방으로 옮겨 갈 때마다 창문을 열어 숨 좀 돌리시게 해 드리겠습니다.\""]], [["Monte Cristo had already seen the breakfast-room and the salon on the ground floor.", "몬테크리스토는 이미 일층의 식당과 응접실을 보았다. 알베르는 먼저 그를 자신의 화실로 데려갔다."], ["Albert led him first to his _atelier_, which was, as we have said, his favorite apartment.", "앞서 말했듯이 그가 가장 아끼는 방이었다. 몬테크리스토는 알베르가 이곳에 모아 둔 것들을 단숨에 알아보았다."], ["Monte Cristo quickly appreciated all that Albert had collected here—old cabinets, Japanese porcelain, Oriental stuffs, Venetian glass, arms from all parts of the world—everything was familiar to him; and at the first glance he recognized their date, their country, and their origin.", "오래된 장식장, 일본 자기, 동방의 직물, 베네치아 유리, 세계 곳곳에서 가져온 무기들. 모든 것이 그에게 익숙했다. 한눈에 그것들의 연대와 출신지와 내력을 알아맞혔다."]], [["Morcerf had expected he should be the guide; on the contrary, it was he who, under the count’s guidance, followed a course of archæology, mineralogy, and natural history.", "모르세르는 자신이 안내자 노릇을 하리라 기대했다. 그런데 오히려 그 자신이 백작의 안내를 받으며 고고학과 광물학, 박물학 강의를 듣는 처지가 되었다."]], [["They descended to the first floor; Albert led his guest into the salon. The salon was filled with the works of modern artists; there were landscapes by Dupré, with their long reeds and tall trees, their lowing oxen and marvellous skies; Delacroix’s Arabian cavaliers, with their long white burnouses, their shining belts, their damasked arms, their horses, who tore each other with their teeth while their riders contended fiercely with their maces; _aquarelles_ of Boulanger, representing Notre Dame de Paris with that vigor that makes the artist the rival of the poet; there were paintings by Diaz, who makes his flowers more beautiful than flowers, his suns more brilliant than the sun; designs by Decamp, as vividly colored as those of Salvator Rosa, but more poetic; _pastels_ by Giraud and Müller, representing children like angels and women with the features of a virgin; sketches torn from the album of Dauzats’ “Travels in the East,” that had been made in a few seconds on the saddle of a camel, or beneath the dome of a mosque—in a word, all that modern art can give in exchange and as recompense for the art lost and gone with ages long since past.", "두 사람은 이층으로 내려갔다. 알베르는 손님을 응접실로 이끌었다. 응접실은 근대 화가들의 작품으로 가득했다. 뒤프레의 풍경화에는 긴 갈대와 키 큰 나무들, 음매 우는 소들, 그리고 경이로운 하늘이 담겨 있었다. 들라크루아의 아라비아 기병들은 길고 흰 부르누스를 걸치고 번쩍이는 허리띠를 두르고 다마스 무늬가 새겨진 무기를 들었으며, 그 말들은 서로 이빨로 물어뜯고 기수들은 철퇴를 휘두르며 맹렬히 싸우고 있었다. 불랑제의 수채화는 파리 노트르담을 화가로 하여금 시인의 맞수가 되게 할 만한 박력으로 그려 냈다. 디아스의 그림들은 꽃을 실제 꽃보다 더 아름답게, 태양을 실제 태양보다 더 찬란하게 그렸다. 드캉의 데생은 살바토르 로사의 그것만큼이나 색채가 선명하면서도 한결 시적이었다. 지로와 뮐러의 파스텔화는 천사 같은 아이들과 성모의 이목구비를 지닌 여인들을 담았다. 도자의 화집 『동방 여행』에서 뜯어낸 스케치들은 낙타의 안장 위에서, 혹은 모스크의 둥근 지붕 아래에서 단 몇 초 만에 그려진 것이었다. 한마디로 그것은 오래전 시대와 더불어 사라져 버린 옛 예술을 대신하고 보상하기 위해 근대 예술이 내놓을 수 있는 모든 것이었다."]], [["Albert expected to have something new this time to show to the traveller, but, to his great surprise, the latter, without seeking for the signatures, many of which, indeed, were only initials, named instantly the author of every picture in such a manner that it was easy to see that each name was not only known to him, but that each style associated with it had been appreciated and studied by him.", "알베르는 이번만큼은 이 여행자에게 새로운 무언가를 보여 줄 수 있으리라 기대했다. 그러나 놀랍게도 백작은 서명을 찾아보지도 않은 채—사실 그 서명들 가운데 다수는 머리글자뿐이었다—그림마다 화가의 이름을 곧바로 댔다. 그 솜씨를 보면 이름 하나하나를 알고 있을 뿐 아니라 거기 결부된 화풍을 두루 음미하고 연구해 왔음이 쉽게 드러났다."], ["From the salon they passed into the bedchamber; it was a model of taste and simple elegance.", "응접실에서 두 사람은 침실로 옮겨 갔다. 침실은 고상한 취향과 소박한 우아함의 본보기였다."], ["A single portrait, signed by Léopold Robert, shone in its carved and gilded frame.", "레오폴 로베르의 서명이 들어간 초상화 한 점이 정교하게 조각하고 금박을 입힌 액자 속에서 빛나고 있었다."], ["This portrait attracted the Count of Monte Cristo’s attention, for he made three rapid steps in the chamber, and stopped suddenly before it.", "그 초상화가 몬테크리스토 백작의 시선을 끌었다. 그는 방 안으로 빠르게 세 걸음을 내딛더니, 그림 앞에서 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."]], [["It was the portrait of a young woman of five or six-and-twenty, with a dark complexion, and light and lustrous eyes, veiled beneath long lashes.", "스물대여섯 살쯤 된 젊은 여인의 초상이었다. 가무잡잡한 피부에, 길고 짙은 속눈썹 아래 맑고 윤기 어린 눈동자가 자리하고 있었다."], ["She wore the picturesque costume of the Catalan fisherwomen, a red and black bodice, and golden pins in her hair.", "여인은 카탈루냐 어부 아낙네들의 그림 같은 옷차림을 하고 있었다. 붉은빛과 검은빛이 어우러진 보디스에, 머리에는 황금 핀을 꽂았다."], ["She was looking at the sea, and her form was outlined on the blue ocean and sky. The light was so faint in the room that Albert did not perceive the pallor that spread itself over the count’s visage, or the nervous heaving of his chest and shoulders.", "여인은 바다를 바라보고 있었고, 그 자태가 푸른 대양과 하늘을 배경으로 또렷이 떠올라 있었다. 방 안이 어찌나 어두침침했던지 알베르는 백작의 얼굴에 퍼지는 창백함도, 가슴과 어깨가 신경질적으로 들썩이는 것도 알아채지 못했다."], ["Silence prevailed for an instant, during which Monte Cristo gazed intently on the picture.", "한순간 침묵이 흘렀고, 그동안 몬테크리스토는 그림을 뚫어지게 응시했다."]], [["“You have there a most charming mistress, viscount,”", "\"참으로 매력적인 연인을 두셨군요, 자작님.\""], ["said the count in a perfectly calm tone; “and this costume—a ball costume, doubtless—becomes her admirably.”", "백작이 더없이 차분한 어조로 말했다. \"그리고 이 옷차림은—틀림없이 무도회 의상일 텐데—썩 잘 어울립니다.\""]], [["“Ah, monsieur,”", "\"아, 백작님.\""], ["returned Albert, “I would never forgive you this mistake if you had seen another picture beside this.", "알베르가 대꾸했다. \"이 곁에 다른 그림이 있는 것을 보셨다면 그 오해를 결코 용서하지 않았을 겁니다."], ["You do not know my mother; she it is whom you see here.", "제 어머니를 모르시는군요. 여기 보시는 분이 바로 제 어머니입니다."], ["She had her portrait painted thus six or eight years ago.", "육칠 년쯤 전에 이렇게 초상을 그리게 하셨지요."], ["This costume is a fancy one, it appears, and the resemblance is so great that I think I still see my mother the same as she was in 1830.", "이 의상은 그저 멋을 부린 것인 듯합니다만, 어찌나 닮았는지 1830년 무렵의 제 어머니 모습을 지금도 보고 있는 것만 같습니다."], ["The countess had this portrait painted during the count’s absence.", "백작께서 자리를 비우신 동안 어머니가 이 초상을 그리게 하셨습니다."], ["She doubtless intended giving him an agreeable surprise; but, strange to say, this portrait seemed to displease my father, and the value of the picture, which is, as you see, one of the best works of Léopold Robert, could not overcome his dislike to it.", "분명 아버지께 기쁜 놀라움을 안겨 드리려는 뜻이었겠지요. 그런데 이상하게도 이 초상은 아버지의 마음에 들지 않는 듯했습니다. 보시다시피 레오폴 로베르의 가장 뛰어난 작품 가운데 하나라는 그림의 값어치로도 아버지의 거부감을 누그러뜨리지는 못했지요."], ["It is true, between ourselves, that M. de Morcerf is one of the most assiduous peers at the Luxembourg, a general renowned for theory, but a most mediocre amateur of art.", "사실 우리끼리 하는 말이지만, 모르세르 백작께서는 뤽상부르에서 가장 부지런한 귀족원 의원 가운데 한 분이고 이론으로 이름난 장군이시지만, 예술 애호가로서는 더없이 평범한 분입니다. 어머니는 다릅니다."], ["It is different with my mother, who paints exceedingly well, and who, unwilling to part with so valuable a picture, gave it to me to put here, where it would be less likely to displease M.", "그림 솜씨가 매우 빼어나시지요. 그래서 이토록 값진 그림을 손에서 놓고 싶지 않으셨던 어머니가, 그것을 여기 걸어 두라고 제게 주셨습니다. 그래야 모르세르 백작의 심기를 덜 거스를 테니까요."], ["de Morcerf, whose portrait, by Gros, I will also show you.", "아버지의 초상은 그로가 그린 것인데, 그것도 보여 드리겠습니다."], ["Excuse my talking of family matters, but as I shall have the honor of introducing you to the count, I tell you this to prevent you making any allusions to this picture.", "집안일을 늘어놓아 죄송합니다만, 제가 백작님을 아버지께 소개하는 영광을 누릴 터라, 혹여 이 그림에 대해 어떤 말씀이라도 꺼내실까 미리 말씀드리는 겁니다."], ["The picture seems to have a malign influence, for my mother rarely comes here without looking at it, and still more rarely does she look at it without weeping.", "이 그림에는 무언가 해로운 기운이 깃든 듯합니다. 어머니는 이곳에 오시면 거의 빠짐없이 이 그림을 바라보시고, 바라보실 때면 거의 빠짐없이 눈물을 흘리시니까요."], ["This disagreement is the only one that has ever taken place between the count and countess, who are still as much united, although married more than twenty years, as on the first day of their wedding.” 20261m", "이 불화는 백작과 백작 부인 사이에 일어난 유일한 다툼입니다. 결혼하신 지 이십 년이 넘었지만, 두 분은 혼례를 올린 첫날과 다름없이 여전히 깊이 맺어져 계십니다.\""]], [["Monte Cristo glanced rapidly at Albert, as if to seek a hidden meaning in his words, but it was evident the young man uttered them in the simplicity of his heart.", "몬테크리스토는 그 말 속에 숨은 뜻이라도 찾으려는 듯 알베르를 흘끗 보았다. 그러나 젊은이가 그 말을 진심에서 우러난 순박함으로 내뱉었다는 것은 분명했다."]], [["“Now,”", "\"자,\""], ["said Albert, “that you have seen all my treasures, allow me to offer them to you, unworthy as they are.", "알베르가 말했다. \"이제 제 보물을 다 보셨으니, 변변찮은 것들이나마 백작님께 바치도록 허락해 주십시오."], ["Consider yourself as in your own house, and to put yourself still more at your ease, pray accompany me to the apartments of M.", "부디 내 집처럼 편히 여기십시오. 그리고 더 마음 편히 계시도록, 모르세르 백작의 거처로 저와 함께 가 주시지요."], ["de Morcerf, he whom I wrote from Rome an account of the services you rendered me, and to whom I announced your promised visit, and I may say that both the count and countess anxiously desire to thank you in person.", "로마에서 백작님이 제게 베풀어 주신 도움을 편지로 아버지께 알리고, 약속하신 방문도 미리 전해 두었습니다. 분명히 말씀드리지만, 아버지도 어머니도 직접 감사를 드리고 싶어 애태우고 계십니다."], ["You are somewhat _blasé_ I know, and family scenes have not much effect on Sinbad the Sailor, who has seen so many others.", "백작님이 어지간한 일에는 시들해지신 분이라는 걸 압니다. 숱한 가족 상봉의 장면을 보아 온 신드바드에게 이런 광경이 무슨 큰 감흥이 있겠습니까."], ["However, accept what I propose to you as an initiation into Parisian life—a life of politeness, visiting, and introductions.”", "그래도 제가 권하는 이 일을 파리 생활로 들어서는 입문 의식으로 여기고 받아들여 주십시오. 예의와 방문과 소개로 이루어진 생활 말입니다.\""]], [["Monte Cristo bowed without making any answer; he accepted the offer without enthusiasm and without regret, as one of those conventions of society which every gentleman looks upon as a duty. Albert summoned his servant, and ordered him to acquaint M.", "몬테크리스토는 아무 대답 없이 고개를 숙였다. 그는 신사라면 누구나 의무로 여기는 사교계의 관례 가운데 하나로서, 그 제안을 열의도 없이, 그렇다고 마지못한 기색도 없이 받아들였다."], ["and Madame de Morcerf of the arrival of the Count of Monte Cristo. Albert followed him with the count.", "알베르는 하인을 불러, 모르세르 백작 부부에게 몬테크리스토 백작이 도착했음을 알리라고 일렀다."], ["When they arrived at the antechamber, above the door was visible a shield, which, by its rich ornaments and its harmony with the rest of the furniture, indicated the importance the owner attached to this blazon.", "알베르는 백작과 함께 하인의 뒤를 따랐다. 대기실에 이르자 문 위에 방패꼴 문장이 보였다. 그 화려한 장식과 나머지 가구들과의 조화로 보아, 집주인이 이 문장에 얼마나 큰 의미를 두는지 알 수 있었다."], ["Monte Cristo stopped and examined it attentively.", "몬테크리스토는 걸음을 멈추고 그것을 유심히 살폈다."]], [["“Azure seven merlets, or, placed bender,”", "\"하늘빛 바탕에 금빛 제비 일곱 마리를 비스듬히 배치했군요.\""], ["said he. “These are, doubtless, your family arms?", "그가 말했다. \"이것이 댁의 가문 문장이겠지요?"], ["Except the knowledge of blazons, that enables me to decipher them, I am very ignorant of heraldry—I, a count of a fresh creation, fabricated in Tuscany by the aid of a commandery of St.", "문장학 지식이 있어 이걸 풀이할 수 있다는 것 말고는, 저는 문장학에 대해 아주 무지합니다. 저야 새로 만들어진 백작, 그것도 성 스테파노 기사단의 도움을 빌려 토스카나에서 꾸며 낸 백작에 지나지 않으니까요."], ["Stephen, and who would not have taken the trouble had I not been told that when you travel much it is necessary.", "여행을 많이 다니려면 그런 게 필요하다는 말을 듣지 않았더라면, 그런 수고조차 하지 않았을 겁니다."], ["Besides, you must have something on the panels of your carriage, to escape being searched by the custom-house officers.", "게다가 세관원에게 검문당하지 않으려면 마차 옆판에 무언가 새겨 두어야 하지요."], ["Excuse my putting such a question to you.”", "이런 질문을 드리는 걸 양해해 주십시오.\""]], [["“It is not indiscreet,”", "\"무례한 질문이 아닙니다.\""], ["returned Morcerf, with the simplicity of conviction.", "모르세르가 확신에 찬 순박함으로 대답했다. \"정확히 알아맞히셨습니다."], ["“You have guessed rightly.", "이것이 우리 가문의 문장입니다."], ["These are our arms, that is, those of my father, but they are, as you see, joined to another shield, which has gules, a silver tower, which are my mother’s.", "정확히는 아버지 가문의 것이지요. 그런데 보시다시피 다른 방패꼴 문장과 합쳐져 있습니다. 붉은 바탕에 은빛 탑이 있는 것이 어머니 가문의 문장입니다."], ["By her side I am Spanish, but the family of Morcerf is French, and, I have heard, one of the oldest of the south of France.”", "어머니 쪽으로는 제가 에스파냐 사람이지만, 모르세르 가문은 프랑스 가문이고, 듣자 하니 프랑스 남부에서 가장 오래된 가문 가운데 하나라고 합니다.\""]], [["“Yes,” replied Monte Cristo “these blazons prove that.", "\"그렇습니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다."], ["Almost all the armed pilgrims that went to the Holy Land took for their arms either a cross, in honor of their mission, or birds of passage, in sign of the long voyage they were about to undertake, and which they hoped to accomplish on the wings of faith.", "\"이 문장들이 그것을 증명하는군요. 성지로 떠난 무장 순례자들은 거의 모두 자기 사명을 기리는 뜻으로 십자가를, 아니면 앞으로 떠날 머나먼 여정을 나타내는 뜻으로 철새를 문장에 새겼습니다. 믿음의 날개를 타고 그 여정을 이루기를 바라면서요."], ["One of your ancestors had joined the Crusades, and supposing it to be only that of St. Louis, that makes you mount to the thirteenth century, which is tolerably ancient.”", "댁의 선조 가운데 한 분이 십자군에 참여하셨던 거지요. 설령 그것이 성왕 루이의 십자군에 지나지 않았다 해도, 그것만으로 13세기까지 거슬러 올라가는 셈이니 제법 오래된 가문입니다.\""]], [["“It is possible,”", "\"그럴 수도 있겠지요.\""], ["said Morcerf; “my father has in his study a genealogical tree which will tell you all that, and on which I made commentaries that would have greatly edified d’Hozier and Jaucourt.", "모르세르가 말했다. \"아버지 서재에 가계도가 하나 있는데, 그것을 보면 다 알 수 있을 겁니다. 제가 거기에 주석을 달아 두었는데, 도지에와 조쿠르●도 크게 감탄했을 정도지요."], ["At present I no longer think of it, and yet I must tell you that we are beginning to occupy ourselves greatly with these things under our popular government.”", "요즘은 더 이상 그런 것에 마음을 두지 않습니다. 그래도 말씀드려야겠는데, 우리는 이 민중의 정부 아래에서 다시금 그런 것들에 부쩍 관심을 쏟기 시작했습니다.\" * 도지에와 조쿠르: 둘 다 프랑스의 이름난 가문·문장 연구가다. —옮긴이"]], [["“Well, then, your government would do well to choose from the past something better than the things that I have noticed on your monuments, and which have no heraldic meaning whatever.", "\"그렇다면 댁의 정부도 과거에서 무언가 골라 쓰려거든, 제가 댁의 기념물들에서 본 것들보다는 더 나은 것을 고르는 편이 좋겠군요. 그것들은 문장학적으로 아무 의미도 없으니 말입니다."], ["As for you, viscount,”", "그런데 자작님은,\""], ["continued Monte Cristo to Morcerf, “you are more fortunate than the government, for your arms are really beautiful, and speak to the imagination.", "몬테크리스토가 모르세르를 향해 말을 이었다. \"정부보다 운이 좋으십니다. 자작님의 문장은 정말로 아름답고, 상상력을 자극하니까요."], ["Yes, you are at once from Provence and Spain; that explains, if the portrait you showed me be like, the dark hue I so much admired on the visage of the noble Catalan.”", "그렇지요, 자작님은 프로방스와 에스파냐 양쪽에서 비롯된 분입니다. 아까 보여 주신 초상이 실물을 닮은 것이라면, 그 고귀한 카탈루냐 여인의 얼굴에서 제가 그토록 감탄했던 가무잡잡한 빛깔도 그래서 설명이 되는군요.\""]], [["It would have required the penetration of Œdipus or the Sphinx to have divined the irony the count concealed beneath these words, apparently uttered with the greatest politeness.", "겉으로는 더없이 정중하게 내뱉은 이 말 속에 백작이 감추어 둔 빈정거림을 알아채려면 오이디푸스나 스핑크스의 통찰력이 있어야 했을 것이다. 모르세르는 미소로 고마움을 표하고, 자기 가문의 문장이 걸린 문을 밀어 열었다."], ["Morcerf thanked him with a smile, and pushed open the door above which were his arms, and which, as we have said, opened into the salon.", "앞서 말했듯이 그 문은 응접실로 통했다. 응접실에서 가장 눈에 잘 띄는 자리에 또 다른 초상화가 걸려 있었다."], ["In the most conspicuous part of the salon was another portrait.", "서른대여섯에서 서른여덟쯤 된 남자의 초상이었다."], ["It was that of a man, from five to eight-and-thirty, in the uniform of a general officer, wearing the double epaulet of heavy bullion, that indicates superior rank, the ribbon of the Legion of Honor around his neck, which showed he was a commander, and on the right breast, the star of a grand officer of the order of the Saviour, and on the left that of the grand cross of Charles III., which proved that the person represented by the picture had served in the wars of Greece and Spain, or, what was just the same thing as regarded decorations, had fulfilled some diplomatic mission in the two countries.", "그는 장군 정복 차림으로, 높은 지위를 나타내는 굵은 금실 견장을 양어깨에 달고 있었다. 목에는 레지옹 도뇌르 훈장 끈을 둘러 그가 사령관급임을 드러냈고, 오른쪽 가슴에는 구세주 기사단 일등 훈장의 별이, 왼쪽에는 카를로스 3세 대십자 훈장의 별이 달려 있었다. 이는 초상화 속 인물이 그리스와 에스파냐의 전쟁에 종군했거나, 훈장에 관한 한 마찬가지인 일이지만 두 나라에서 어떤 외교 임무를 수행했음을 입증해 주었다."]], [["Monte Cristo was engaged in examining this portrait with no less care than he had bestowed upon the other, when another door opened, and he found himself opposite to the Count of Morcerf in person.", "몬테크리스토는 앞서 다른 초상화에 쏟았던 것 못지않은 주의를 기울여 이 초상화를 살피고 있었다. 그때 또 다른 문이 열렸고, 그는 모르세르 백작 본인과 마주 서게 되었다."]], [["He was a man of forty to forty-five years, but he seemed at least fifty, and his black moustache and eyebrows contrasted strangely with his almost white hair, which was cut short, in the military fashion.", "마흔에서 마흔다섯 살쯤 된 남자였으나, 적어도 쉰은 되어 보였다. 검은 콧수염과 눈썹이 군인답게 짧게 깎은 거의 새하얀 머리카락과 기묘한 대조를 이루고 있었다."], ["He was dressed in plain clothes, and wore at his button-hole the ribbons of the different orders to which he belonged.", "그는 평복 차림이었고, 단춧구멍에 자신이 속한 여러 훈장의 약장을 달고 있었다."]], [["He entered with a tolerably dignified step, and some little haste.", "그는 제법 위엄 있는 걸음걸이로, 약간 서두르는 기색으로 들어왔다."], ["Monte Cristo saw him advance towards him without making a single step. It seemed as if his feet were rooted to the ground, and his eyes on the Count of Morcerf.", "몬테크리스토는 한 걸음도 떼지 않은 채 그가 자기 쪽으로 다가오는 것을 바라보았다. 두 발이 땅에 박힌 듯, 두 눈은 모르세르 백작에게 못 박힌 듯했다."]], [["“Father,”", "\"아버지.\""], ["said the young man, “I have the honor of presenting to you the Count of Monte Cristo, the generous friend whom I had the good fortune to meet in the critical situation of which I have told you.”", "젊은이가 말했다. \"몬테크리스토 백작님을 소개하는 영광을 누리게 되었습니다. 제가 말씀드렸던 그 위급한 처지에서 다행히 만나게 된, 너그러우신 친구분입니다.\""]], [["“You are most welcome, monsieur,”", "\"참으로 환영합니다, 백작.\""], ["said the Count of Morcerf, saluting Monte Cristo with a smile, “and monsieur has rendered our house, in preserving its only heir, a service which insures him our eternal gratitude.”", "모르세르 백작이 미소를 지으며 몬테크리스토에게 인사를 건넸다. \"백작께서는 우리 집안의 하나뿐인 후계자를 지켜 주심으로써, 우리가 영원히 감사할 수밖에 없는 은혜를 베풀어 주셨습니다.\""]], [["As he said these words, the count of Morcerf pointed to a chair, while he seated himself in another opposite the window.", "이렇게 말하면서 모르세르 백작은 의자 하나를 가리켰고, 자신은 창 맞은편의 다른 의자에 앉았다."]], [["Monte Cristo, in taking the seat Morcerf offered him, placed himself in such a manner as to remain concealed in the shadow of the large velvet curtains, and read on the careworn and livid features of the count a whole history of secret griefs written in each wrinkle time had planted there.", "몬테크리스토는 모르세르가 권한 자리에 앉으면서, 커다란 벨벳 커튼의 그늘에 몸이 가려지도록 자세를 잡았다. 그러고는 백작의 근심에 찌든 핏기 없는 얼굴에서, 세월이 새겨 놓은 주름 하나하나에 적힌 은밀한 슬픔의 내력을 통째로 읽어 냈다."]], [["“The countess,”", "\"백작 부인은,\""], ["said Morcerf, “was at her toilet when she was informed of the visit she was about to receive.", "모르세르가 말했다. \"맞이할 손님이 온다는 전갈을 받았을 때 마침 몸단장을 하고 있었습니다."], ["She will, however, be in the salon in ten minutes.”", "그래도 십 분이면 응접실로 나올 겁니다.\""]], [["“It is a great honor to me,”", "\"제게는 더없는 영광입니다.\""], ["returned Monte Cristo, “to be thus, on the first day of my arrival in Paris, brought in contact with a man whose merit equals his reputation, and to whom fortune has for once been equitable, but has she not still on the plains of Mitidja, or in the mountains of Atlas, a marshal’s staff to offer you?”", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"파리에 도착한 첫날에, 명성에 걸맞은 공적을 지니신 분, 운명이 모처럼 공정하게 대해 준 분과 이렇게 마주하게 되었으니 말입니다. 그런데 운명은 미티자 평원이나 아틀라스 산맥에서 아직 원수의 지휘봉 하나쯤 더 내어 줄 여지가 남아 있지 않습니까?\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오.\""], ["replied Morcerf, reddening slightly, “I have left the service, monsieur.", "모르세르가 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했다. \"저는 군에서 물러났습니다, 백작."], ["Made a peer at the Restoration, I served through the first campaign under the orders of Marshal Bourmont.", "왕정복고 때 귀족원 의원이 되었고, 첫 원정에서는 부르몽 원수의 지휘 아래 복무했지요."], ["I could, therefore, expect a higher rank, and who knows what might have happened had the elder branch remained on the throne?", "그러니 더 높은 지위를 바랄 수도 있었고, 만약 부르봉 본가가 왕좌에 그대로 남아 있었다면 무슨 일이 일어났을지 누가 알겠습니까?"], ["But the Revolution of July was, it seems, sufficiently glorious to allow itself to be ungrateful, and it was so for all services that did not date from the imperial period.", "그러나 7월 혁명은 보아하니 배은망덕해도 될 만큼 충분히 영광스러웠던 모양이고, 제정 시대까지 거슬러 올라가지 않는 모든 공적에 대해서는 실제로 그렇게 굴었습니다."], ["I tendered my resignation, for when you have gained your epaulets on the battle-field, you do not know how to manœuvre on the slippery grounds of the salons.", "저는 사직서를 냈습니다. 전쟁터에서 견장을 따낸 사람은 응접실의 미끄러운 바닥에서 어찌 처신해야 할지 모르는 법이니까요."], ["I have hung up my sword, and cast myself into politics.", "저는 칼을 걸어 두고 정치에 뛰어들었습니다."], ["I have devoted myself to industry; I study the useful arts.", "산업에 몸을 바쳤지요. 실용적인 기술을 연구합니다."], ["During the twenty years I served, I often wished to do so, but I had not the time.”", "복무한 이십 년 동안 줄곧 그러고 싶었지만, 시간이 없었습니다.\""]], [["“These are the ideas that render your nation superior to any other,”", "\"바로 그런 생각이 귀국을 다른 어느 나라보다 뛰어나게 만드는 것이지요.\""], ["returned Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다."], ["“A gentleman of high birth, possessor of an ample fortune, you have consented to gain your promotion as an obscure soldier, step by step—this is uncommon; then become general, peer of France, commander of the Legion of Honor, you consent to again commence a second apprenticeship, without any other hope or any other desire than that of one day becoming useful to your fellow-creatures; this, indeed, is praiseworthy,—nay, more, it is sublime.”", "\"높은 가문에서 태어나 넉넉한 재산을 지닌 분이, 이름 없는 병사처럼 한 계단 한 계단 승진을 일구는 데 동의하셨다니, 이는 흔치 않은 일입니다. 그러고는 장군이 되고, 프랑스 귀족원 의원이 되고, 레지옹 도뇌르 사령관이 되신 분이, 언젠가 동포에게 쓸모 있는 사람이 되리라는 것 외에 다른 어떤 희망도 욕심도 없이 또다시 두 번째 수련을 시작하기로 하셨군요. 이는 참으로 칭송할 만한 일입니다."], ["20265m", "아니, 그 이상이지요. 숭고한 일입니다.\""]], [["Albert looked on and listened with astonishment; he was not used to see Monte Cristo give vent to such bursts of enthusiasm.", "알베르는 놀란 얼굴로 지켜보며 귀를 기울였다. 그는 몬테크리스토가 이렇게 열정을 터뜨리는 모습을 본 적이 없었다."]], [["“Alas,”", "\"아아.\" 이방인이 말을 이었다."], ["continued the stranger, doubtless to dispel the slight cloud that covered Morcerf’s brow, “we do not act thus in Italy; we grow according to our race and our species, and we pursue the same lines, and often the same uselessness, all our lives.”", "분명 모르세르의 이마에 드리운 옅은 그늘을 걷어 내려는 뜻이었으리라. \"이탈리아에서는 우리가 이렇게 행동하지 않습니다. 우리는 혈통과 종에 따라 자라고, 평생토록 같은 길을, 흔히 같은 무용함을 좇을 따름이지요.\""]], [["“But, monsieur,”", "\"하지만 백작,\""], ["said the Count of Morcerf, “for a man of your merit, Italy is not a country, and France opens her arms to receive you; respond to her call.", "모르세르 백작이 말했다. \"백작 같은 공적을 지닌 분에게 이탈리아는 나라라고 할 수 없습니다. 프랑스는 두 팔을 벌려 백작을 맞이할 겁니다."], ["France will not, perhaps, be always ungrateful.", "그 부름에 응해 주십시오. 프랑스가 언제까지나 배은망덕하지는 않을 겁니다."], ["She treats her children ill, but she always welcomes strangers.”", "자기 자식들은 박대하지만, 이방인은 늘 환대하는 나라니까요.\""]], [["“Ah, father,”", "\"아, 아버지.\""], ["said Albert with a smile, “it is evident you do not know the Count of Monte Cristo; he despises all honors, and contents himself with those written on his passport.”", "알베르가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"아버지가 몬테크리스토 백작님을 모르신다는 게 분명하군요. 백작님은 온갖 명예를 경멸하시고, 여권에 적힌 명예 하나로 만족하시는 분입니다.\""]], [["“That is the most just remark,”", "\"제 자신에 관해 들어 본 말 가운데,\""], ["replied the stranger, “I ever heard made concerning myself.”", "이방인이 대답했다. \"가장 옳은 평이군요.\""]], [["“You have been free to choose your career,”", "\"백작께서는 자유로이 길을 선택하실 수 있었군요.\""], ["observed the Count of Morcerf, with a sigh; “and you have chosen the path strewed with flowers.”", "모르세르 백작이 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"그리고 꽃이 흩뿌려진 길을 고르셨고요.\""]], [["“Precisely, monsieur,”", "\"바로 그렇습니다, 백작.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo with one of those smiles that a painter could never represent or a physiologist analyze.", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. 화가도 결코 그려 낼 수 없고 생리학자도 분석해 낼 수 없을 그런 미소를 띤 채였다."]], [["“If I did not fear to fatigue you,”", "\"피곤하게 해 드릴까 염려되지 않는다면,\" 장군이 말했다."], ["said the general, evidently charmed with the count’s manners, “I would have taken you to the Chamber; there is a debate very curious to those who are strangers to our modern senators.”", "백작의 처신에 분명 매료된 기색이었다. \"의사당으로 모시고 싶습니다. 오늘 우리 근대 상원 의원들을 낯설어하는 분들에게는 무척 흥미로운 토론이 있거든요.\""]], [["“I shall be most grateful, monsieur, if you will, at some future time, renew your offer, but I have been flattered with the hope of being introduced to the countess, and I will therefore wait.”", "\"훗날 언젠가 그 제안을 다시 해 주신다면 더없이 감사하겠습니다, 백작. 하지만 저는 백작 부인께 소개받으리라는 기대에 마음이 부풀어 있으니, 그때까지 기다리겠습니다.\""]], [["“Ah, here is my mother,”", "\"아, 어머니가 오셨군요.\""], ["cried the viscount.", "자작이 외쳤다."]], [["Monte Cristo, turned round hastily, and saw Madame de Morcerf at the entrance of the salon, at the door opposite to that by which her husband had entered, pale and motionless; when Monte Cristo turned round, she let fall her arm, which for some unknown reason had been resting on the gilded door-post.", "몬테크리스토가 황급히 돌아보니, 남편이 들어온 문 맞은편 문, 응접실 입구에 모르세르 부인이 창백하게 굳은 채 서 있었다. 몬테크리스토가 돌아보자, 그녀는 까닭 모를 이유로 금박 입힌 문기둥에 기대고 있던 팔을 떨구었다."], ["She had been there some moments, and had heard the last words of the visitor.", "그녀는 얼마간 그곳에 서서, 손님이 한 마지막 말을 듣고 있었던 것이다."], ["The latter rose and bowed to the countess, who inclined herself without speaking.", "백작이 일어나 부인에게 인사를 건넸고, 부인은 아무 말 없이 고개를 숙였다."]], [["“Ah! good heavens, madame,”", "\"아! 이런, 부인.\""], ["said the count, “are you ill, or is it the heat of the room that affects you?”", "백작이 말했다. \"어디 편찮으신지요, 아니면 방 안의 열기 때문에 그러신지요?\""]], [["“Are you ill, mother?”", "\"편찮으세요, 어머니?\""], ["cried the viscount, springing towards her.", "자작이 어머니에게 달려가며 외쳤다."]], [["She thanked them both with a smile.", "그녀는 미소로 두 사람에게 고마움을 표했다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니다.\""], ["returned she, “but I feel some emotion on seeing, for the first time, the man without whose intervention we should have been in tears and desolation.", "그녀가 대답했다. \"다만 당신의 도움이 없었더라면 우리가 눈물과 비탄에 잠겼을, 그분을 처음으로 뵙게 되니 마음이 좀 벅차오르는구나. 백작님.\""], ["Monsieur,” continued the countess, advancing with the majesty of a queen, “I owe to you the life of my son, and for this I bless you.", "부인이 여왕 같은 위엄으로 다가서며 말을 이었다. \"저는 백작님께 제 아들의 목숨을 빚졌고, 그래서 백작님께 축복을 빕니다."], ["Now, I thank you for the pleasure you give me in thus affording me the opportunity of thanking you as I have blessed you, from the bottom of my heart.”", "이제 마음 깊은 곳에서부터, 축복을 빌었던 것처럼 감사를 드릴 기회를 이렇게 마련해 주시니, 그 기쁨에 감사드립니다.\""]], [["The count bowed again, but lower than before; he was even paler than Mercédès.", "백작이 다시 고개를 숙였다. 이번에는 아까보다 더 깊이 숙였다. 그의 얼굴은 메르세데스보다도 더 창백했다."]], [["“Madame,”", "\"부인.\""], ["said he, “the count and yourself recompense too generously a simple action.", "그가 말했다. \"백작님과 부인께서는 보잘것없는 행동에 너무도 후한 보답을 해 주십니다."], ["To save a man, to spare a father’s feelings, or a mother’s sensibility, is not to do a good action, but a simple deed of humanity.”", "한 사람의 목숨을 구하고, 한 아버지의 마음과 한 어머니의 애틋함을 지켜 드린 일은, 선행이라 할 것도 못 되고 그저 인간으로서의 도리일 뿐입니다.\""]], [["At these words, uttered with the most exquisite sweetness and politeness, Madame de Morcerf replied:", "더없이 그윽한 다정함과 정중함으로 내뱉은 이 말에, 모르세르 부인이 대답했다."]], [["“It is very fortunate for my son, monsieur, that he found such a friend, and I thank God that things are thus.”", "\"제 아들이 그런 친구분을 만났다니 참으로 다행입니다, 백작님. 일이 이렇게 되도록 해 주신 하느님께 감사드립니다.\""]], [["And Mercédès raised her fine eyes to heaven with so fervent an expression of gratitude, that the count fancied he saw tears in them. M.", "그러면서 메르세데스는 어찌나 간절한 감사의 표정으로 아름다운 눈을 하늘로 들어 올렸던지, 백작은 그 눈에 눈물이 어린 것을 본 듯했다."], ["de Morcerf approached her.", "모르세르 백작이 부인에게 다가갔다."]], [["“Madame,”", "\"부인.\""], ["said he. “I have already made my excuses to the count for quitting him, and I pray you to do so also.", "그가 말했다. \"백작께 자리를 떠야 한다고 이미 양해를 구했으니, 당신도 부디 양해해 주오."], ["The sitting commences at two; it is now three, and I am to speak.”", "회의가 두 시에 시작되는데 지금 세 시요. 게다가 내가 발언을 해야 하오.\""]], [["“Go, then, and monsieur and I will strive our best to forget your absence,”", "\"그럼 다녀오세요. 백작님과 저는 당신이 없는 빈자리를 잊으려 애써 보겠어요.\""], ["replied the countess, with the same tone of deep feeling.", "부인이 여전히 깊은 감정이 어린 어조로 대답했다."], ["“Monsieur,”", "\"백작님.\""], ["continued she, turning to Monte Cristo, “will you do us the honor of passing the rest of the day with us?”", "그녀가 몬테크리스토를 향해 말을 이었다. \"남은 하루를 저희와 함께 보내 주시는 영광을 베풀어 주시겠어요?\""]], [["“Believe me, madame, I feel most grateful for your kindness, but I got out of my travelling carriage at your door this morning, and I am ignorant how I am installed in Paris, which I scarcely know; this is but a trifling inquietude, I know, but one that may be appreciated.”", "\"믿어 주십시오, 부인. 그 친절에 더없이 감사드립니다. 하지만 오늘 아침 댁의 문 앞에서 여행 마차에서 내린 터라, 거의 알지도 못하는 이 파리에서 제가 어떻게 자리를 잡았는지조차 모릅니다. 사소한 걱정거리라는 건 압니다만, 헤아려 주실 만한 일이기도 하지요.\""]], [["“We shall have the pleasure another time,”", "\"그럼 다음 기회에 그 기쁨을 누리겠습니다.\""], ["said the countess; “you promise that?”", "부인이 말했다. \"약속해 주시는 거지요?\""]], [["Monte Cristo inclined himself without answering, but the gesture might pass for assent.", "몬테크리스토는 대답 없이 고개를 숙였으나, 그 몸짓은 승낙으로 받아들일 만했다."]], [["“I will not detain you, monsieur,”", "\"붙들지 않겠습니다, 백작님.\""], ["continued the countess; “I would not have our gratitude become indiscreet or importunate.”", "부인이 말을 이었다. \"저희의 감사가 무례하거나 성가신 것이 되기를 바라지 않으니까요.\""]], [["“My dear Count,”", "\"백작님.\""], ["said Albert, “I will endeavor to return your politeness at Rome, and place my coupé at your disposal until your own be ready.”", "알베르가 말했다. \"로마에서 받은 친절에 보답하고자, 백작님의 마차가 마련될 때까지 제 이륜마차를 마음껏 쓰시도록 내어 드리겠습니다.\""]], [["“A thousand thanks for your kindness, viscount,”", "\"친절은 정말 고맙습니다, 자작님.\""], ["returned the Count of Monte Cristo “but I suppose that M. Bertuccio has suitably employed the four hours and a half I have given him, and that I shall find a carriage of some sort ready at the door.”", "몬테크리스토 백작이 대답했다. \"하지만 베르투치오가 제가 준 네 시간 반을 알맞게 썼을 테니, 문 앞에 마차 한 대쯤은 채비되어 있으리라 봅니다.\""]], [["Albert was used to the count’s manner of proceeding; he knew that, like Nero, he was in search of the impossible, and nothing astonished him, but wishing to judge with his own eyes how far the count’s orders had been executed, he accompanied him to the door of the house.", "알베르는 백작의 일 처리 방식에 익숙했다. 그는 백작이 네로처럼 불가능한 것을 찾아 나서는 사람임을 알았기에 무엇에도 놀라지 않았다. 다만 백작의 지시가 얼마나 이행되었는지 제 눈으로 가늠해 보고 싶어, 집 문간까지 백작을 배웅했다."], ["Monte Cristo was not deceived.", "몬테크리스토의 짐작은 빗나가지 않았다."], ["As soon as he appeared in the Count of Morcerf’s antechamber, a footman, the same who at Rome had brought the count’s card to the two young men, and announced his visit, sprang into the vestibule, and when he arrived at the door the illustrious traveller found his carriage awaiting him.", "그가 모르세르 백작의 대기실에 모습을 드러내자마자, 로마에서 두 젊은이에게 백작의 명함을 가져다주며 그의 방문을 알렸던 바로 그 하인이 현관으로 달려 나갔다. 그리하여 이 저명한 여행자가 문에 이르렀을 때, 마차가 그를 기다리고 있었다."], ["It was a _coupé_ of Koller’s building, and with horses and harness for which Drake had, to the knowledge of all the lions of Paris, refused on the previous day seven hundred guineas.", "콜러가 만든 이륜마차였고, 거기 매인 말과 마구로 말하자면, 어제 드레이크가 칠백 기니를 거절했다는 사실을 파리의 멋쟁이라면 누구나 알고 있는 것이었다."]], [["“Monsieur,”", "\"자작님.\""], ["said the count to Albert, “I do not ask you to accompany me to my house, as I can only show you a habitation fitted up in a hurry, and I have, as you know, a reputation to keep up as regards not being taken by surprise.", "백작이 알베르에게 말했다. \"제 집까지 함께 가자고 청하지는 않겠습니다. 급하게 꾸민 거처밖에 보여 드릴 게 없으니까요."], ["Give me, therefore, one more day before I invite you; I shall then be certain not to fail in my hospitality.”", "게다가 아시다시피 저는 누구에게도 허를 잡히지 않는다는 평판을 지켜야 하는 몸입니다. 그러니 자작님을 초대하기 전에 하루만 더 주십시오. 그러면 손님 대접에 빈틈이 없으리라 확신할 수 있을 테니까요.\""]], [["“If you ask me for a day, count, I know what to anticipate; it will not be a house I shall see, but a palace.", "\"하루를 청하신다면, 백작님, 무엇을 기대해야 할지 알 만합니다. 제가 보게 될 것은 집이 아니라 궁전이겠지요."], ["You have decidedly some genius at your control.”", "백작님은 분명 마음대로 부리는 무슨 천재라도 거느리고 계신 모양입니다.\""]], [["“_Ma foi_, spread that idea,”", "\"맙소사, 그 소문이나 좀 퍼뜨려 주시지요.\""], ["replied the Count of Monte Cristo, putting his foot on the velvet-lined steps of his splendid carriage, “and that will be worth something to me among the ladies.”", "몬테크리스토 백작이 화려한 마차의 벨벳을 댄 발판에 발을 올리며 대답했다. \"그러면 부인네들 사이에서 제법 득이 될 테니까요.\""]], [["As he spoke, he sprang into the vehicle, the door was closed, but not so rapidly that Monte Cristo failed to perceive the almost imperceptible movement which stirred the curtains of the apartment in which he had left Madame de Morcerf.", "이렇게 말하면서 그는 마차에 훌쩍 올랐고, 문이 닫혔다. 그러나 그리 빠르지는 않아서, 몬테크리스토는 자신이 모르세르 부인을 남겨 두고 온 그 방의 커튼이 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 미세하게 흔들리는 것을 놓치지 않았다."]], [["When Albert returned to his mother, he found her in the boudoir reclining in a large velvet armchair, the whole room so obscure that only the shining spangle, fastened here and there to the drapery, and the angles of the gilded frames of the pictures, showed with some degree of brightness in the gloom.", "알베르가 어머니에게 돌아가 보니, 그녀는 규방의 커다란 벨벳 안락의자에 기대어 있었다. 방 안이 어찌나 어두침침했던지, 휘장 여기저기에 박힌 반짝이 장식과 그림의 금박 액자 모서리만이 어둠 속에서 어느 정도 빛을 내고 있을 뿐이었다."], ["Albert could not see the face of the countess, as it was covered with a thin veil she had put on her head, and which fell over her features in misty folds, but it seemed to him as though her voice had altered.", "알베르는 어머니의 얼굴을 볼 수 없었다. 머리에 쓴 얇은 베일이 안개처럼 주름지며 이목구비를 덮고 있었기 때문이다. 그래도 어머니의 목소리가 달라진 것만은 느껴졌다."], ["He could distinguish amid the perfumes of the roses and heliotropes in the flower-stands, the sharp and fragrant odor of volatile salts, and he noticed in one of the chased cups on the mantle-piece the countess’s smelling-bottle, taken from its shagreen case, and exclaimed in a tone of uneasiness, as he entered:", "그는 화분대에 놓인 장미와 헬리오트로프의 향내 가운데서 휘발성 소금의 톡 쏘는 향긋한 냄새를 가려낼 수 있었다. 또 벽난로 위에 놓인 무늬 새긴 잔 하나에, 가죽 상자에서 꺼낸 어머니의 각성제 병이 담겨 있는 것을 알아챘다. 그는 방에 들어서며 불안한 어조로 외쳤다."]], [["“My dear mother, have you been ill during my absence?”", "\"어머니, 제가 없는 동안 편찮으셨어요?\""]], [["“No, no, Albert, but you know these roses, tuberoses, and orange-flowers throw out at first, before one is used to them, such violent perfumes.”", "\"아니다, 아니야, 알베르. 하지만 이 장미며 월하향이며 오렌지꽃이라는 게, 익숙해지기 전에는 처음엔 향이 어찌나 독한지 알잖니.\""]], [["“Then, my dear mother,”", "\"그렇다면, 어머니.\""], ["said Albert, putting his hand to the bell, “they must be taken into the antechamber.", "알베르가 종에 손을 가져가며 말했다. \"이것들을 대기실로 내가야겠어요."], ["You are really ill, and just now were so pale as you came into the room——”", "정말로 편찮으신 거예요. 방금 들어오실 때도 그렇게 창백하셨으니까요.\""]], [["“Was I pale, Albert?”", "\"내가 창백했니, 알베르?\""]], [["“Yes; a pallor that suits you admirably, mother, but which did not the less alarm my father and myself.”", "\"네. 어머니께 더없이 잘 어울리는 창백함이었지만, 그래도 아버지와 저를 적잖이 놀라게 한 창백함이었어요.\""]], [["“Did your father speak of it?”", "\"네 아버지가 그 얘기를 하시더냐?\""], ["inquired Mercédès eagerly.", "메르세데스가 다급히 물었다."]], [["“No, madame; but do you not remember that he spoke of the fact to you?” 20269m", "\"아니요, 어머니. 그래도 아버지가 어머니께 그 얘기를 하셨던 걸 기억하지 못하세요?\""]], [["“Yes, I do remember,”", "\"그래, 기억나는구나.\""], ["replied the countess.", "부인이 대답했다."]], [["A servant entered, summoned by Albert’s ring of the bell.", "알베르가 종을 울리자 하인이 들어왔다."]], [["“Take these flowers into the anteroom or dressing-room,”", "\"이 꽃들을 대기실이나 화장실로 옮기게.\" 자작이 말했다."], ["said the viscount; “they make the countess ill.”", "\"이것 때문에 백작 부인이 편찮으시니.\""]], [["The footman obeyed his orders.", "하인이 분부에 따랐다."], ["A long pause ensued, which lasted until all the flowers were removed.", "꽃이 모두 치워질 때까지 긴 침묵이 이어졌다."]], [["“What is this name of Monte Cristo?”", "\"몬테크리스토라는 이름은 무슨 뜻이니?\""], ["inquired the countess, when the servant had taken away the last vase of flowers, “is it a family name, or the name of the estate, or a simple title?”", "하인이 마지막 꽃병을 들고 나가자 부인이 물었다. \"가문의 성이냐, 아니면 영지의 이름이냐, 그도 아니면 그저 칭호일 뿐이냐?\""]], [["“I believe, mother, it is merely a title.", "\"제 생각엔, 어머니, 그저 칭호인 듯합니다."], ["The count purchased an island in the Tuscan archipelago, and, as he told you today, has founded a commandery.", "백작은 토스카나 군도의 섬 하나를 사들였고, 오늘 어머니께도 말씀드렸듯이 기사단을 하나 세웠지요."], ["You know the same thing was done for Saint Stephen of Florence, Saint George Constantinian of Parma, and even for the Order of Malta.", "피렌체의 성 스테파노 기사단이나, 파르마의 성 게오르기우스 콘스탄티누스 기사단, 심지어 몰타 기사단을 위해서도 같은 일이 이루어졌다는 걸 아실 겁니다."], ["Except this, he has no pretension to nobility, and calls himself a chance count, although the general opinion at Rome is that the count is a man of very high distinction.”", "이것 말고는 귀족 행세를 할 만한 게 전혀 없어서, 스스로를 우연히 된 백작이라고 부른답니다. 그래도 로마에서는 백작이 대단히 지체 높은 인물이라는 게 중론이지요.\""]], [["“His manners are admirable,”", "\"몸가짐이 훌륭하더구나.\""], ["said the countess, “at least, as far as I could judge in the few minutes he remained here.”", "부인이 말했다. \"적어도 잠깐 머무는 동안 내가 본 바로는 말이다.\""]], [["“They are perfect mother, so perfect, that they surpass by far all I have known in the leading aristocracy of the three proudest nobilities of Europe—the English, the Spanish, and the German.”", "\"완벽하지요, 어머니. 어찌나 완벽한지, 유럽에서 가장 자존심 강한 세 귀족 집단—영국과 에스파냐와 독일 귀족—의 최상류층에서 제가 보아 온 누구도 그를 한참 따라가지 못할 정도입니다.\""]], [["The countess paused a moment; then, after a slight hesitation, she resumed.", "부인은 잠시 말을 멈췄다. 그러더니 약간 머뭇거린 뒤 다시 입을 열었다."]], [["“You have seen, my dear Albert—I ask the question as a mother—you have seen M. de Monte Cristo in his house, you are quicksighted, have much knowledge of the world, more tact than is usual at your age, do you think the count is really what he appears to be?”", "\"알베르, 네가 봤잖니—어미로서 묻는 거란다—너는 몬테크리스토 백작의 집에서 그를 봤고, 너는 눈썰미가 좋고 세상 물정에도 밝으며, 나이에 비해 보기 드물게 분별이 있는 아이지. 너는 그 백작이 정말로 겉보기 그대로의 사람이라고 생각하니?\""]], [["“What does he appear to be?”", "\"겉보기에는 어떤 사람인데요?\""]], [["“Why, you have just said,—a man of high distinction.”", "\"아니, 방금 네가 말하지 않았니. 지체 높은 인물이라고.\""]], [["“I told you, my dear mother, he was esteemed such.”", "\"그렇게 평가받는다고 말씀드린 거지요, 어머니.\""]], [["“But what is your own opinion, Albert?”", "\"그럼 네 생각은 어떠니, 알베르?\""]], [["“I must tell you that I have not come to any decided opinion respecting him, but I think him a Maltese.”", "\"솔직히 말씀드리면 그에 관해서는 아직 확실한 생각을 정하지 못했습니다. 다만 몰타 사람이 아닐까 합니다.\""]], [["“I do not ask you of his origin but what he is.”", "\"출신을 묻는 게 아니라, 어떤 사람이냐고 묻는 거란다.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["what he is; that is quite another thing.", "어떤 사람이냐, 그건 또 전혀 다른 이야기지요. 그에게서 워낙 비범한 점을 많이 보아서, 제 생각을 정말로 말씀드리자면, 저는 그가 바이런이 그린 영웅들 가운데 하나라고 봅니다."], ["I have seen so many remarkable things in him, that if you would have me really say what I think, I shall reply that I really do look upon him as one of Byron’s heroes, whom misery has marked with a fatal brand; some Manfred, some Lara, some Werner, one of those wrecks, as it were, of some ancient family, who, disinherited of their patrimony, have achieved one by the force of their adventurous genius, which has placed them above the laws of society.”", "불운이 치명적인 낙인을 찍어 놓은 인물 말입니다. 어떤 만프레드, 어떤 라라, 어떤 베르너 같은. 말하자면 어느 오래된 가문의 잔해 같은 사람이지요."], ["“You say——”", "물려받을 유산을 빼앗겼지만, 모험을 좇는 천재성의 힘으로 스스로 유산을 일구어 내어 사회의 법 위에 올라선 그런 사람 말입니다.\""]], [["“I say that Monte Cristo is an island in the midst of the Mediterranean, without inhabitants or garrison, the resort of smugglers of all nations, and pirates of every flag.", "\"그러니까 네 말은—\" \"제 말은, 몬테크리스토가 지중해 한복판에 있는 섬이라는 겁니다. 주민도 수비대도 없고, 온갖 나라의 밀수꾼과 온갖 깃발을 단 해적들이 드나드는 곳이지요."], ["Who knows whether or not these industrious worthies do not pay to their feudal lord some dues for his protection?”", "그 부지런한 작자들이 자기들을 지켜 주는 대가로 봉건 영주에게 무슨 세금이라도 바치고 있는지 누가 알겠습니까?\""]], [["“That is possible,”", "\"그럴 수도 있겠구나.\""], ["said the countess, reflecting.", "부인이 생각에 잠겨 말했다."]], [["“Never mind,”", "\"아무튼.\""], ["continued the young man, “smuggler or not, you must agree, mother dear, as you have seen him, that the Count of Monte Cristo is a remarkable man, who will have the greatest success in the salons of Paris.", "젊은이가 말을 이었다. \"밀수꾼이든 아니든, 직접 보셨으니 어머니도 인정하실 겁니다. 몬테크리스토 백작은 비범한 사람이고, 파리 사교계에서 큰 성공을 거둘 거예요."], ["Why, this very morning, in my rooms, he made his _entrée_ amongst us by striking every man of us with amazement, not even excepting Château-Renaud.”", "아니, 바로 오늘 아침에도 제 방에서, 그가 우리 사이에 등장하면서 사람들을 하나같이 경탄케 했지요. 샤토르노조차 예외가 아니었습니다.\""]], [["“And what do you suppose is the count’s age?”", "\"그래, 백작의 나이가 몇쯤이라고 생각하니?\""], ["inquired Mercédès, evidently attaching great importance to this question.", "메르세데스가 물었다. 이 질문에 큰 비중을 두는 것이 분명했다."]], [["“Thirty-five or thirty-six, mother.”", "\"서른다섯이나 서른여섯쯤요, 어머니.\""]], [["“So young,—it is impossible,”", "\"그렇게 젊다니—그럴 리가 없다.\""], ["said Mercédès, replying at the same time to what Albert said as well as to her own private reflection.", "메르세데스가 말했다. 알베르의 말에 대한 대답이면서 동시에 자신의 속생각에 대한 대답이기도 했다."]], [["“It is the truth, however.", "\"하지만 사실인걸요. 그가 서너 번이나 제게 말했어요."], ["Three or four times he has said to me, and certainly without the slightest premeditation, ‘at such a period I was five years old, at another ten years old, at another twelve,’ and I, induced by curiosity, which kept me alive to these details, have compared the dates, and never found him inaccurate.", "분명 아무 꿍꿍이 없이요. '그 무렵 나는 다섯 살이었지', '또 그 무렵엔 열 살', '또 그 무렵엔 열두 살'이라고요. 저는 그런 사소한 것에 호기심이 일어 자꾸 마음이 쏠렸고, 그 날짜들을 맞춰 보았는데 한 번도 어긋난 적이 없었습니다."], ["The age of this singular man, who is of no age, is then, I am certain, thirty-five.", "나이를 가늠할 수 없는 이 묘한 사람의 나이는, 그러니까 저는 서른다섯이 틀림없다고 봅니다."], ["Besides, mother, remark how vivid his eye, how raven-black his hair, and his brow, though so pale, is free from wrinkles,—he is not only vigorous, but also young.”", "게다가 어머니, 그 눈이 얼마나 형형한지, 머리카락이 얼마나 까마귀처럼 새카만지 보세요. 그토록 창백한데도 이마에는 주름 하나 없으니, 정정할 뿐 아니라 젊기까지 한 사람입니다.\""]], [["The countess bent her head, as if beneath a heavy wave of bitter thoughts.", "부인은 쓰디쓴 상념의 물결에 짓눌리기라도 한 듯 고개를 떨구었다."]], [["“And has this man displayed a friendship for you, Albert?”", "\"그래서 그 사람이 네게 우정을 보이더냐, 알베르?\""], ["she asked with a nervous shudder.", "그녀가 신경질적으로 몸을 떨며 물었다."]], [["“I am inclined to think so.”", "\"그런 것 같습니다.\""]], [["“And—do—you—like—him?”", "\"그럼—너는—그를—좋아하니?\""]], [["“Why, he pleases me in spite of Franz d’Épinay, who tries to convince me that he is a being returned from the other world.”", "\"네, 그가 마음에 듭니다. 그를 저세상에서 돌아온 존재라고 저더러 믿게 하려는 프란츠 데피네에도 불구하고 말이지요.\""]], [["The countess shuddered.", "부인이 흠칫 몸을 떨었다."]], [["“Albert,”", "\"알베르.\""], ["she said, in a voice which was altered by emotion, “I have always put you on your guard against new acquaintances.", "그녀가 감정에 겨워 달라진 목소리로 말했다. \"나는 늘 너에게 새로 사귄 사람을 조심하라고 일러 왔다."], ["Now you are a man, and are able to give me advice; yet I repeat to you, Albert, be prudent.”", "이제 너는 어른이 되었고 도리어 나에게 조언을 해 줄 수 있는 나이지. 그래도 다시 한번 당부하마, 알베르. 신중해야 한다.\""]], [["“Why, my dear mother, it is necessary, in order to make your advice turn to account, that I should know beforehand what I have to distrust.", "\"하지만 어머니, 어머니의 조언이 쓸모가 있으려면, 제가 무엇을 경계해야 하는지를 미리 알아야 하지 않겠습니까."], ["The count never plays, he only drinks pure water tinged with a little sherry, and is so rich that he cannot, without intending to laugh at me, try to borrow money.", "백작은 노름을 전혀 하지 않고, 셰리주를 살짝 탄 맹물만 마시며, 어찌나 부자인지 저를 비웃을 작정이 아니고서야 제게 돈을 빌리려 들 리도 없습니다."], ["What, then, have I to fear from him?”", "그러니 제가 그에게서 무엇을 두려워해야 하지요?\""]], [["“You are right,”", "\"네 말이 옳다.\""], ["said the countess, “and my fears are weakness, especially when directed against a man who has saved your life.", "부인이 말했다. \"내 두려움은 부질없는 약한 마음일 뿐이지. 더구나 네 목숨을 구해 준 사람을 두고 그러다니."], ["How did your father receive him, Albert?", "알베르, 네 아버지는 그를 어떻게 맞으셨니?"], ["It is necessary that we should be more than complaisant to the count. M.", "우리는 백작에게 그저 정중한 것 이상으로 대해야 한다."], ["de Morcerf is sometimes occupied, his business makes him reflective, and he might, without intending it——”", "모르세르 백작은 가끔 일에 골몰하시고, 사업 때문에 생각에 잠기곤 하시니, 본의 아니게—\""]], [["“Nothing could be in better taste than my father’s demeanor, madame,”", "\"아버지의 처신은 더없이 흠잡을 데 없었습니다, 어머니.\" 알베르가 말했다."], ["said Albert; “nay, more, he seemed greatly flattered at two or three compliments which the count very skilfully and agreeably paid him with as much ease as if he had known him these thirty years.", "\"아니, 그 이상이었지요. 백작이 아주 능숙하고 기분 좋게 두세 마디 칭찬을 건넸는데, 마치 삼십 년이나 알고 지낸 사이처럼 거침이 없더군요. 아버지가 무척 우쭐해하시는 듯했습니다."], ["Each of these little tickling arrows must have pleased my father,”", "그 간질간질한 작은 화살들이 하나하나 아버지의 마음을 흡족하게 했을 거예요.\""], ["added Albert with a laugh.", "알베르가 웃으며 덧붙였다."], ["“And thus they parted the best possible friends, and M. de Morcerf even wished to take him to the Chamber to hear the speakers.”", "\"그렇게 두 분은 더없이 좋은 친구가 되어 헤어졌고, 모르세르 백작은 연설을 들으러 의사당으로 그를 데려가려고까지 하셨답니다.\""]], [["The countess made no reply.", "부인은 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["She fell into so deep a reverie that her eyes gradually closed.", "그녀는 어찌나 깊은 상념에 빠져들었던지 눈이 차츰 감겼다."], ["The young man, standing up before her, gazed upon her with that filial affection which is so tender and endearing with children whose mothers are still young and handsome.", "젊은이는 어머니 앞에 선 채로, 아직 젊고 아름다운 어머니를 둔 자식만이 품는 그 애틋하고 다정한 효심으로 어머니를 바라보았다."], ["Then, after seeing her eyes closed, and hearing her breathe gently, he believed she had dropped asleep, and left the apartment on tiptoe, closing the door after him with the utmost precaution.", "그러고는 어머니의 눈이 감기고 숨소리가 잔잔해지는 것을 보고 잠이 든 줄로 여겨, 발끝으로 살그머니 방을 나섰다. 그러고는 더없이 조심스럽게 문을 닫았다."]], [["“This devil of a fellow,”", "\"이 빌어먹을 사내 같으니.\""], ["he muttered, shaking his head; “I said at the time he would create a sensation here, and I measure his effect by an infallible thermometer.", "그가 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 중얼거렸다. \"그때 내가 말했지, 그가 여기서 돌풍을 일으킬 거라고. 그 효과를 나는 어김없는 온도계로 가늠하는 거야."], ["My mother has noticed him, and he must therefore, perforce, be remarkable.”", "어머니가 그를 주목하셨으니, 그는 어쩔 수 없이 비범한 사람일 수밖에 없지.\""]], [["He went down to the stables, not without some slight annoyance, when he remembered that the Count of Monte Cristo had laid his hands on a “turnout” which sent his bays down to second place in the opinion of connoisseurs.", "그는 마구간으로 내려갔다. 몬테크리스토 백작이 손에 넣은 그 마차 한 벌 때문에, 감식가들의 평가에서 자신의 밤색 말들이 이류로 밀려났다는 사실이 떠올라 약간의 언짢음이 없지 않았다."]], [["“Most decidedly,”", "\"확실히,\""], ["said he, “men are not equal, and I must beg my father to develop this theorem in the Chamber of Peers.”", "그가 말했다. \"사람은 평등하지 않아. 아버지께 이 정리를 귀족원에서 한번 펼쳐 보시라고 청해야겠군.\""]], [["# Chapter 42. Monsieur Bertuccio", "# Chapter 42. Monsieur Bertuccio"]], [["Meanwhile the count had arrived at his house; it had taken him six minutes to perform the distance, but these six minutes were sufficient to induce twenty young men who knew the price of the equipage they had been unable to purchase themselves, to put their horses in a gallop in order to see the rich foreigner who could afford to give 20,000 francs apiece for his horses.", "그사이 백작은 자기 집에 도착했다. 거리를 지나는 데 6분이 걸렸지만, 그 6분만으로도 직접 마차를 사들일 형편이 못 되었던 젊은이 스무 명이 말을 전속력으로 몰아, 한 필에 2만 프랑씩 주고 말을 살 수 있는 그 부유한 이방인을 보려고 따라붙기에 충분했다."]], [["The house Ali had chosen, and which was to serve as a town residence to Monte Cristo, was situated on the right hand as you ascend the Champs-Élysées.", "알리가 골라, 몬테크리스토의 시내 거처가 될 그 집은 샹젤리제를 올라가다 보면 오른편에 자리하고 있었다."], ["A thick clump of trees and shrubs rose in the centre, and masked a portion of the front; around this shrubbery two alleys, like two arms, extended right and left, and formed a carriage-drive from the iron gates to a double portico, on every step of which stood a porcelain vase, filled with flowers.", "한가운데에는 나무와 관목이 빽빽이 우거져 정면의 일부를 가렸다. 이 수풀을 둘러싸고 두 갈래 길이 마치 두 팔처럼 좌우로 뻗어, 철문에서부터 이중 현관에 이르는 마찻길을 이루었으며, 그 계단마다 꽃이 가득 담긴 도자기 화병이 놓여 있었다."], ["This house, isolated from the rest, had, besides the main entrance, another in the Rue de Ponthieu.", "다른 집들과 동떨어진 이 저택은 정문 외에도 퐁티외 거리 쪽으로 또 하나의 출입구가 있었다."], ["Even before the coachman had hailed the _concierge_, the massy gates rolled on their hinges—they had seen the Count coming, and at Paris, as everywhere else, he was served with the rapidity of lightning.", "마부가 문지기를 부르기도 전에 육중한 대문이 경첩을 돌며 활짝 열렸다. 백작이 오는 것을 미리 본 것이었다. 파리에서도, 다른 어디서나 그렇듯, 그는 번개 같은 신속함으로 시중을 받았다."], ["The coachman entered and traversed the half-circle without slackening his speed, and the gates were closed ere the wheels had ceased to sound on the gravel.", "마부는 속도를 늦추지 않고 반원형 진입로를 가로질러 들어갔고, 바퀴가 자갈 위에서 멎기도 전에 대문이 닫혔다."], ["The carriage stopped at the left side of the portico, two men presented themselves at the carriage-window; the one was Ali, who, smiling with an expression of the most sincere joy, seemed amply repaid by a mere look from Monte Cristo.", "마차가 현관 왼편에 멈추자 두 사람이 마차 창가로 다가왔다. 한 사람은 알리였다. 그는 더없이 진심 어린 기쁨이 어린 미소를 지었는데, 몬테크리스토가 한번 눈길을 던지는 것만으로도 충분히 보상받은 듯했다."], ["The other bowed respectfully, and offered his arm to assist the count in descending.", "다른 한 사람은 공손히 허리를 숙이고는 백작이 내리는 것을 돕기 위해 팔을 내밀었다."]], [["“Thanks, M. Bertuccio,”", "\"고맙네, 베르투치오 씨.\""], ["said the count, springing lightly up the three steps of the portico; “and the notary?”", "백작이 현관 계단 세 개를 가볍게 뛰어오르며 말했다. \"공증인은?\""]], [["“He is in the small salon, excellency,”", "\"작은 응접실에 계십니다, 각하.\""], ["returned Bertuccio.", "베르투치오가 대답했다."]], [["“And the cards I ordered to be engraved as soon as you knew the number of the house?”", "\"집 번지가 정해지는 대로 새기라고 일러 둔 명함은?\""]], [["“Your excellency, it is done already.", "\"각하, 이미 다 되었습니다."], ["I have been myself to the best engraver of the Palais Royal, who did the plate in my presence.", "제가 직접 팔레루아얄 최고의 각인사를 찾아가, 제 눈앞에서 동판을 새기게 했습니다."], ["The first card struck off was taken, according to your orders, to the Baron Danglars, Rue de la Chaussée d’Antin, No. 7; the others are on the mantle-piece of your excellency’s bedroom.”", "분부하신 대로 가장 먼저 찍어 낸 명함은 쇼세당탱 거리 7번지의 당글라르 남작께 보냈고, 나머지는 각하의 침실 벽난로 위에 놓여 있습니다.\""]], [["“Good; what o’clock is it?”", "\"좋아. 지금 몇 시인가?\""]], [["“Four o’clock.”", "\"네 시입니다.\""]], [["Monte Cristo gave his hat, cane, and gloves to the same French footman who had called his carriage at the Count of Morcerf’s, and then he passed into the small salon, preceded by Bertuccio, who showed him the way.", "몬테크리스토는 모르세르 백작 댁에서 마차를 불러 주었던 그 프랑스인 하인에게 모자와 지팡이, 장갑을 건네고는, 길을 안내하는 베르투치오를 앞세워 작은 응접실로 들어갔다."]], [["“These are but indifferent marbles in this antechamber,” said Monte Cristo.", "\"이 대기실의 대리석은 영 변변치가 않군.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["“I trust all this will soon be taken away.”", "\"이것들도 곧 다 치웠으면 좋겠네.\""]], [["Bertuccio bowed. As the steward had said, the notary awaited him in the small salon.", "베르투치오가 허리를 숙였다. 집사가 말한 대로 공증인은 작은 응접실에서 그를 기다리고 있었다."], ["He was a simple-looking lawyer’s clerk, elevated to the extraordinary dignity of a provincial scrivener.", "그는 시골 대서인이라는 분에 넘치는 자리에까지 오른, 어디서나 볼 법한 평범한 법률 사무원이었다."]], [["“You are the notary empowered to sell the country house that I wish to purchase, monsieur?” asked Monte Cristo.", "\"내가 사려는 시골집을 매각하도록 위임받은 공증인이 그대인가?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, count,” returned the notary.", "\"그렇습니다, 백작님.\" 공증인이 대답했다."]], [["“Is the deed of sale ready?”", "\"매매 증서는 준비됐나?\""]], [["“Yes, count.”", "\"네, 백작님.\""]], [["“Have you brought it?”", "\"가져왔나?\""]], [["“Here it is.”", "\"여기 있습니다.\""]], [["“Very well; and where is this house that I purchase?”", "\"좋아. 그래, 내가 사는 그 집이 어디에 있지?\""], ["asked the count carelessly, addressing himself half to Bertuccio, half to the notary.", "백작이 무심하게 물었다. 절반은 베르투치오에게, 절반은 공증인에게 던지는 말이었다."], ["The steward made a gesture that signified, “I do not know.”", "집사는 \"저는 모릅니다\"라는 뜻의 몸짓을 했다."], ["The notary looked at the count with astonishment.", "공증인이 놀란 눈으로 백작을 바라보았다."]], [["“What!”", "\"아니!\""], ["said he, “does not the count know where the house he purchases is situated?”", "그가 말했다. \"백작님은 사들이시는 집이 어디 있는지도 모르신단 말입니까?\""]], [["“No,” returned the count.", "\"모르네.\" 백작이 대답했다."]], [["“The count does not know?”", "\"백작님이 모르신다고요?\""]], [["“How should I know?", "\"내가 어찌 알겠나?"], ["I have arrived from Cadiz this morning.", "오늘 아침에 카디스에서 막 왔네."], ["I have never before been at Paris, and it is the first time I have ever even set my foot in France.”", "파리에는 처음이고, 프랑스 땅에 발을 디딘 것조차 이번이 처음일세.\""]], [["“Ah, that is different; the house you purchase is at Auteuil.”", "\"아, 그러시다면 사정이 다르군요. 사들이시는 집은 오퇴유에 있습니다.\""]], [["At these words Bertuccio turned pale.", "그 말에 베르투치오의 얼굴이 새파랗게 질렸다."]], [["“And where is Auteuil?”", "\"오퇴유는 어디 있나?\""], ["asked the count.", "백작이 물었다."]], [["“Close by here, monsieur,”", "\"바로 이 근처입니다, 백작님.\""], ["replied the notary—“a little beyond Passy; a charming situation, in the heart of the Bois de Boulogne.”", "공증인이 대답했다. \"파시를 조금 지나서, 불로뉴 숲 한복판에 자리한 더없이 운치 있는 곳입니다.\""]], [["“So near as that?”", "\"그렇게 가까운가?\""], ["said the Count; “but that is not in the country.", "백작이 말했다. \"그럼 시골이라고 할 수 없지 않나."], ["What made you choose a house at the gates of Paris, M. Bertuccio?”", "무슨 생각으로 파리 코앞에 있는 집을 고른 건가, 베르투치오 씨?\""]], [["“I,” cried the steward with a strange expression.", "\"제가요.\" 집사가 묘한 표정으로 외쳤다."], ["“His excellency did not charge me to purchase this house.", "\"이 집을 사라고 분부하신 적은 없으십니다."], ["If his excellency will recollect—if he will think——”", "각하께서 떠올려 보시면, 곰곰이 생각해 보시면……\""]], [["“Ah, true,” observed Monte Cristo; “I recollect now.", "\"아, 그렇지.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["I read the advertisement in one of the papers, and was tempted by the false title, ‘a country house.’”", "\"이제 기억나는군. 어느 신문에서 광고를 읽었는데, '시골집'이라는 그릇된 표제에 끌렸던 거야.\""]], [["“It is not yet too late,”", "\"아직 늦지 않았습니다.\""], ["cried Bertuccio, eagerly; “and if your excellency will intrust me with the commission, I will find you a better at Enghien, at Fontenay-aux-Roses, or at Bellevue.”", "베르투치오가 간절하게 외쳤다. \"각하께서 제게 일을 맡겨 주신다면, 앙기앵이나 퐁트네오로즈, 벨뷔에서 더 나은 집을 찾아 드리겠습니다.\""]], [["“Oh, no,”", "\"아니, 됐네.\""], ["returned Monte Cristo negligently; “since I have this, I will keep it.”", "몬테크리스토가 대수롭지 않게 대꾸했다. \"이미 이걸 샀으니, 그냥 갖겠네.\""]], [["“And you are quite right,”", "\"옳은 말씀이십니다.\""], ["said the notary, who feared to lose his fee.", "수수료를 잃을까 봐 마음을 졸이던 공증인이 말했다. \"샘물이 풍부하고 나무도 훌륭한, 더없이 운치 있는 곳입니다."], ["“It is a charming place, well supplied with spring-water and fine trees; a comfortable habitation, although abandoned for a long time, without reckoning the furniture, which, although old, is yet valuable, now that old things are so much sought after.", "오래 비워 두긴 했어도 살기에 편안한 집이지요. 게다가 가구도 따로 셈해야 합니다. 오래된 것들이긴 하나, 요즘처럼 골동품을 그토록 찾는 시절에는 값진 물건들이지요."], ["I suppose the count has the tastes of the day?”", "백작님께서도 요즘 취향을 가지고 계시리라 봅니다만?\""]], [["“To be sure,”", "\"물론이지.\""], ["returned Monte Cristo; “it is very convenient, then?”", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"그래, 살기에 아주 편한가?\""]], [["“It is more—it is magnificent.”", "\"편한 정도가 아니라, 더없이 훌륭합니다.\""]], [["“_Peste!_ let us not lose such an opportunity,”", "\"이런! 그런 기회를 놓칠 수야 없지.\""], ["returned Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 대꾸했다."], ["“The deed, if you please, Mr. Notary.”", "\"증서를 주게, 공증인.\""]], [["And he signed it rapidly, after having first run his eye over that part of the deed in which were specified the situation of the house and the names of the proprietors.", "그러고는 집의 위치와 소유주들의 이름이 적힌 부분을 먼저 한번 훑어본 뒤, 빠르게 서명했다."]], [["“Bertuccio,”", "\"베르투치오.\""], ["said he, “give fifty-five thousand francs to monsieur.”", "그가 말했다. \"이분께 5만 5천 프랑을 드리게.\""]], [["The steward left the room with a faltering step, and returned with a bundle of bank-notes, which the notary counted like a man who never gives a receipt for money until after he is sure it is all there.", "집사는 비틀거리는 걸음으로 방을 나갔다가 지폐 한 다발을 들고 돌아왔고, 공증인은 돈이 모두 거기 있다는 걸 확인하기 전에는 결코 영수증을 써 주지 않는 사람답게 그것을 세었다."]], [["“And now,”", "\"자, 이제.\""], ["demanded the count, “are all the forms complied with?”", "백작이 물었다. \"모든 절차가 다 끝났나?\""]], [["“All, sir.”", "\"전부 끝났습니다.\""]], [["“Have you the keys?”", "\"열쇠는 가지고 있나?\""]], [["“They are in the hands of the concierge, who takes care of the house, but here is the order I have given him to install the count in his new possessions.”", "\"집을 관리하는 문지기가 가지고 있습니다만, 여기 백작님을 새 거처에 맞아들이도록 그에게 내린 지시서가 있습니다.\""]], [["“Very well;”", "\"좋아.\""], ["and Monte Cristo made a sign with his hand to the notary, which said, “I have no further need of you; you may go.”", "그러고는 몬테크리스토가 공증인에게 손짓을 했는데, \"이제 그대에게 볼일이 없으니 가도 좋다\"는 뜻이었다."]], [["“But,”", "\"그런데,\""], ["observed the honest notary, “the count is, I think, mistaken; it is only fifty thousand francs, everything included.”", "정직한 공증인이 말했다. \"백작님께서 착각하신 듯합니다. 모든 걸 포함해 5만 프랑입니다.\""]], [["“And your fee?”", "\"그럼 그대의 수수료는?\""]], [["“Is included in this sum.”", "\"그 금액에 포함되어 있습니다.\""]], [["“But have you not come from Auteuil here?”", "\"하지만 그대가 오퇴유에서 여기까지 오지 않았나?\""]], [["“Yes, certainly.”", "\"네, 그렇습니다.\""]], [["“Well, then, it is but fair that you should be paid for your loss of time and trouble,”", "\"그렇다면 시간을 들이고 수고한 값을 받는 것이 마땅하지.\""], ["said the count; and he made a gesture of polite dismissal.", "백작이 말하고는, 정중하게 물러가라는 몸짓을 했다."]], [["The notary left the room backwards, and bowing down to the ground; it was the first time he had ever met a similar client.", "공증인은 뒷걸음질로, 땅에 닿을 듯 허리를 숙이며 방을 나갔다. 이런 손님을 만난 것은 그로서도 난생처음이었다."]], [["“See this gentleman out,”", "\"이분을 배웅해 드리게.\""], ["said the count to Bertuccio.", "백작이 베르투치오에게 말했다."], ["And the steward followed the notary out of the room.", "그러자 집사가 공증인을 따라 방을 나갔다."]], [["Scarcely was the count alone, when he drew from his pocket a book closed with a lock, and opened it with a key which he wore round his neck, and which never left him.", "혼자 남게 되자마자 백작은 자물쇠로 잠긴 수첩을 호주머니에서 꺼내, 목에 걸고 다니며 한시도 몸에서 떼어 놓지 않는 열쇠로 그것을 열었다."], ["After having sought for a few minutes, he stopped at a leaf which had several notes, and compared them with the deed of sale, which lay on the table, and recalling his _souvenirs_—", "잠시 뒤적이던 그는 여러 메모가 적힌 한 장에서 멈추어, 탁자 위에 놓인 매매 증서와 그것을 견주어 보며 옛 기억을 더듬었다."]], [["“‘Auteuil, Rue de la Fontaine, No.", "\"'오퇴유, 라퐁텐 거리 28번지.'"], ["28;’ it is indeed the same,”", "과연 같은 곳이군.\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he; “and now, am I to rely upon an avowal extorted by religious or physical terror?", "\"그런데 종교적 두려움이나 육체적 공포 때문에 토해 낸 고백을 과연 믿어도 될까?"], ["However, in an hour I shall know all.", "어쨌든 한 시간 안에 모든 걸 알게 되겠지."], ["Bertuccio!”", "베르투치오!\""], ["cried he, striking a light hammer with a pliant handle on a small gong.", "그가 외치며, 작은 징을 낭창낭창한 자루가 달린 가벼운 망치로 두드렸다."], ["“Bertuccio!”", "\"베르투치오!\""]], [["The steward appeared at the door.", "집사가 문가에 나타났다."]], [["“Monsieur Bertuccio,”", "\"베르투치오 씨.\""], ["said the count, “did you never tell me that you had travelled in France?”", "백작이 말했다. \"전에 프랑스를 여행한 적이 있다고 하지 않았나?\""]], [["“In some parts of France—yes, excellency.”", "\"프랑스 몇몇 지방은요, 네, 각하.\""]], [["“You know the environs of Paris, then?”", "\"그렇다면 파리 근교도 알겠군?\""]], [["“No, excellency, no,”", "\"아닙니다, 각하, 모릅니다.\""], ["returned the steward, with a sort of nervous trembling, which Monte Cristo, a connoisseur in all emotions, rightly attributed to great disquietude.", "집사가 일종의 신경질적인 떨림과 함께 대답했다. 온갖 감정을 꿰뚫어 보는 데 일가견이 있는 몬테크리스토는 그것이 깊은 불안에서 나온 것임을 정확히 알아챘다."]], [["“It is unfortunate,”", "\"안타깝군.\""], ["returned he, “that you have never visited the environs, for I wish to see my new property this evening, and had you gone with me, you could have given me some useful information.”", "그가 대꾸했다. \"자네가 근교에 가 본 적이 없다니 말이야. 오늘 저녁에 내 새 영지를 보러 갈 참인데, 자네가 함께 갔더라면 쓸 만한 정보를 좀 일러 줄 수 있었을 텐데.\""]], [["“To Auteuil!”", "\"오퇴유라니요!\""], ["cried Bertuccio, whose copper complexion became livid—“I go to Auteuil?”", "베르투치오가 외쳤다. 그 구릿빛 얼굴이 납빛으로 질렸다. \"제가 오퇴유에 간다고요?\""]], [["“Well, what is there surprising in that?", "\"그게 뭐가 그리 놀랄 일인가?"], ["When I live at Auteuil, you must come there, as you belong to my service.”", "내가 오퇴유에 살게 되면, 자네는 내 밑에서 일하는 사람이니 그곳에 와야지.\""]], [["Bertuccio hung down his head before the imperious look of his master, and remained motionless, without making any answer.", "베르투치오는 주인의 위압적인 눈빛 앞에서 고개를 떨군 채, 아무 대답도 없이 꼼짝 않고 서 있었다."]], [["“Why, what has happened to you?—are you going to make me ring a second time for the carriage?”", "\"왜 그러나, 무슨 일이 있나? 내가 마차를 두 번 부르게 만들 작정인가?\""], ["asked Monte Cristo, in the same tone that Louis XIV. pronounced the famous, “I have been almost obliged to wait.”", "몬테크리스토가 물었다. 루이 14세가 그 유명한 \"나는 하마터면 기다릴 뻔했다\"라는 말을 내뱉던 바로 그런 어조였다."], ["Bertuccio made but one bound to the antechamber, and cried in a hoarse voice:", "베르투치오는 단숨에 대기실로 뛰쳐나가 쉰 목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“His excellency’s horses!”", "\"각하의 말들을 대령하라!\""]], [["Monte Cristo wrote two or three notes, and, as he sealed the last, the steward appeared.", "몬테크리스토가 편지 두세 통을 쓰고 마지막 편지를 봉인하고 있을 때, 집사가 다시 나타났다."]], [["“Your excellency’s carriage is at the door,” said he.", "\"각하의 마차가 문 앞에 대령했습니다.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["“Well, take your hat and gloves,”", "\"좋아, 그럼 모자와 장갑을 챙기게.\""], ["returned Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 대꾸했다."]], [["“Am I to accompany you, your excellency?”", "\"제가 모시고 가야 합니까, 각하?\""], ["cried Bertuccio.", "베르투치오가 외쳤다."]], [["“Certainly, you must give the orders, for I intend residing at the house.”", "\"물론이지, 자네가 지시를 내려야 하지 않나. 나는 그 집에서 지낼 작정이니까.\""]], [["20277m\nIt was unexampled for a servant of the count’s to dare to dispute an order of his, so the steward, without saying a word, followed his master, who got into the carriage, and signed to him to follow, which he did, taking his place respectfully on the front seat.", "백작의 명을 감히 거역하는 하인이란 일찍이 없던 일이었다. 그래서 집사는 한마디도 없이 주인을 따랐다. 주인은 마차에 올라 그에게 따라오라고 손짓했고, 그는 공손히 앞쪽 자리에 올라앉았다."]], [["# Chapter 43. The House at Auteuil", "# Chapter 43. The House at Auteuil"]], [["Monte Cristo noticed, as they descended the staircase, that Bertuccio signed himself in the Corsican manner; that is, had formed the sign of the cross in the air with his thumb, and as he seated himself in the carriage, muttered a short prayer.", "몬테크리스토는 계단을 내려가면서 베르투치오가 코르시카식으로 성호를 긋는 것을 알아챘다. 엄지손가락으로 허공에 십자를 그리는 그 방식으로, 마차에 올라앉으면서도 짤막한 기도를 중얼거렸다."], ["Anyone but a man of exhaustless thirst for knowledge would have had pity on seeing the steward’s extraordinary repugnance for the count’s projected drive without the walls; but the count was too curious to let Bertuccio off from this little journey.", "끝없는 지식욕에 사로잡힌 사람이 아니었다면, 백작이 성 밖으로 마차를 몰겠다는 계획에 집사가 보이는 유별난 거부감을 보고 측은한 마음이 들었을 것이다. 그러나 백작은 너무도 호기심이 많아 이 자그마한 여정에서 베르투치오를 놓아줄 생각이 없었다."], ["In twenty minutes they were at Auteuil; the steward’s emotion had continued to augment as they entered the village.", "이십 분 만에 그들은 오퇴유에 도착했다. 마을로 들어서는 동안 집사의 동요는 점점 더 커져 갔다."], ["Bertuccio, crouched in the corner of the carriage, began to examine with a feverish anxiety every house they passed.", "마차 구석에 웅크린 베르투치오는 지나치는 집들을 하나하나 열에 들뜬 불안으로 살피기 시작했다."]], [["“Tell them to stop at Rue de la Fontaine, No. 28,”", "\"퐁텐가 28번지에 세우라고 일러라.\""], ["said the count, fixing his eyes on the steward, to whom he gave this order.", "백작이 집사에게 시선을 고정한 채 이렇게 명령했다."]], [["Bertuccio’s forehead was covered with perspiration; however, he obeyed, and, leaning out of the window, he cried to the coachman,—“Rue de la Fontaine, No. 28.” No.", "베르투치오의 이마는 땀으로 뒤덮였다. 그래도 그는 명령에 따라 창밖으로 몸을 내밀고 마부에게 외쳤다. \"퐁텐가 28번지로!\""], ["28 was situated at the extremity of the village; during the drive night had set in, and darkness gave the surroundings the artificial appearance of a scene on the stage.", "28번지는 마을 끝자락에 자리하고 있었다. 마차를 달리는 동안 밤이 내려앉았고, 어둠은 주변 풍경에 무대 위 한 장면 같은 인위적인 모습을 입혔다."], ["The carriage stopped, the footman sprang off the box and opened the door.", "마차가 멈추자 하인이 마부석에서 뛰어내려 문을 열었다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"이런,\""], ["said the count, “you do not get out, M.", "백작이 말했다. \"내리지 않는 건가, 베르투치오 씨."], ["Bertuccio—you are going to stay in the carriage, then?", "그래, 마차 안에 그대로 있을 셈인가?"], ["What are you thinking of this evening?”", "오늘 저녁 대체 무슨 생각을 하고 있는 거지?\""]], [["Bertuccio sprang out, and offered his shoulder to the count, who, this time, leaned upon it as he descended the three steps of the carriage.", "베르투치오는 뛰어내려 백작에게 어깨를 내밀었고, 이번에는 백작이 그 어깨에 기대어 마차의 세 계단을 내려왔다."]], [["“Knock,”", "\"문을 두드리고,\""], ["said the count, “and announce me.”", "백작이 말했다. \"내가 왔다고 알려라.\""]], [["Bertuccio knocked, the door opened, and the concierge appeared.", "베르투치오가 문을 두드리자 문이 열리고 문지기가 나타났다."]], [["“What is it?”", "\"무슨 일이오?\""], ["asked he.", "문지기가 물었다."]], [["“It is your new master, my good fellow,”", "\"당신의 새 주인이시오, 친구.\""], ["said the footman.", "하인이 말했다."], ["And he held out to the concierge the notary’s order.", "그러고는 공증인의 서류를 문지기에게 내밀었다."]], [["“The house is sold, then?”", "\"그럼 집이 팔렸단 말이오?\""], ["demanded the concierge; “and this gentleman is coming to live here?”", "문지기가 물었다. \"그리고 이 양반이 여기 와서 사신다는 거고?\""]], [["“Yes, my friend,”", "\"그렇다네, 친구.\""], ["returned the count; “and I will endeavor to give you no cause to regret your old master.”", "백작이 대답했다. \"옛 주인을 그리워할 일이 없도록 애써 보겠네.\""]], [["“Oh, monsieur,”", "\"아, 나리,\" 문지기가 말했다. \"그분을 그리워할 일은 별로 없을 겁니다."], ["said the concierge, “I shall not have much cause to regret him, for he came here but seldom; it is five years since he was here last, and he did well to sell the house, for it did not bring him in anything at all.”", "워낙 이곳에 드물게 오셨으니까요. 마지막으로 오신 지가 오 년이나 됐는데, 집을 파신 건 잘하신 일이지요. 아무런 수입도 나지 않는 집이었으니까요.\""]], [["“What was the name of your old master?” said Monte Cristo.", "\"옛 주인의 이름이 뭐였나?\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“The Marquis of Saint-Méran.", "\"생메랑 후작이십니다."], ["Ah, I am sure he has not sold the house for what he gave for it.”", "아, 사신 값보다 못 받고 파셨을 게 분명합니다.\""]], [["“The Marquis of Saint-Méran!”", "\"생메랑 후작이라!\""], ["returned the count.", "백작이 대꾸했다."], ["“The name is not unknown to me; the Marquis of Saint-Méran!”", "\"내가 모르는 이름이 아니로군. 생메랑 후작!\""], ["and he appeared to meditate.", "그러고는 무언가 생각에 잠긴 듯했다."]], [["“An old gentleman,”", "\"연로하신 어른이셨지요.\""], ["continued the concierge, “a staunch follower of the Bourbons; he had an only daughter, who married M.", "문지기가 말을 이었다. \"부르봉 왕가의 충직한 지지자셨고요. 외동딸이 하나 있었는데, 빌포르 씨와 결혼했습니다."], ["de Villefort, who had been the king’s attorney at Nîmes, and afterwards at Versailles.”", "님에서, 나중에는 베르사유에서 검사를 지내신 분이지요.\""]], [["Monte Cristo glanced at Bertuccio, who became whiter than the wall against which he leaned to prevent himself from falling.", "몬테크리스토는 베르투치오를 흘끗 보았다. 그는 쓰러지지 않으려 벽에 기대고 있었는데, 그 벽보다 더 하얗게 질려 있었다."]], [["“And is not this daughter dead?”", "\"그 딸은 이미 죽지 않았나?\""], ["demanded Monte Cristo; “I fancy I have heard so.”", "몬테크리스토가 물었다. \"그렇게 들은 것 같은데.\""]], [["“Yes, monsieur, one-and-twenty years ago; and since then we have not seen the poor marquis three times.”", "\"그렇습니다, 나리. 이십일 년 전에요. 그 후로는 가엾은 후작님을 세 번도 채 뵙지 못했습니다.\""]], [["“Thanks, thanks,”", "\"고맙네, 고마워.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, judging from the steward’s utter prostration that he could not stretch the cord further without danger of breaking it.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. 집사가 완전히 기진맥진한 것을 보고, 줄을 더 당겼다가는 끊어질 위험이 있다고 판단한 것이다."], ["“Give me a light.”", "\"불을 좀 밝혀 주게.\""]], [["“Shall I accompany you, monsieur?”", "\"제가 모셔다 드릴까요, 나리?\""]], [["“No, it is unnecessary; Bertuccio will show me a light.”", "\"아니, 그럴 것 없네. 베르투치오가 불을 비춰 줄 테니.\""]], [["And Monte Cristo accompanied these words by the gift of two gold pieces, which produced a torrent of thanks and blessings from the concierge.", "그러면서 몬테크리스토는 이 말에 금화 두 닢을 곁들였고, 그것은 문지기에게서 감사와 축복의 말을 줄줄이 쏟아져 나오게 했다."]], [["“Ah, monsieur,”", "\"아, 나리,\""], ["said he, after having vainly searched on the mantle-piece and the shelves, “I have not got any candles.”", "문지기가 벽난로 선반과 시렁을 헛되이 뒤져 본 뒤에 말했다. \"초가 한 자루도 없습니다.\""]], [["“Take one of the carriage-lamps, Bertuccio,”", "\"마차 등을 하나 들게, 베르투치오.\""], ["said the count, “and show me the apartments.”", "백작이 말했다. \"그리고 방들을 안내해라.\""]], [["The steward obeyed in silence, but it was easy to see, from the manner in which the hand that held the light trembled, how much it cost him to obey.", "집사는 말없이 명령에 따랐다. 그러나 불을 든 손이 떨리는 모습만 보아도 그가 얼마나 큰 대가를 치르며 복종하는지 쉽게 알 수 있었다."], ["They went over a tolerably large ground floor; a first floor consisted of a salon, a bathroom, and two bedrooms; near one of the bedrooms they came to a winding staircase that led down to the garden.", "그들은 제법 넓은 일 층을 둘러보았다. 이 층에는 살롱 하나와 욕실 하나, 침실 둘이 있었다. 침실 하나 가까이에 정원으로 내려가는 나선형 계단이 보였다."]], [["“Ah, here is a private staircase,”", "\"아, 여기 따로 계단이 있군.\""], ["said the count; “that is convenient.", "백작이 말했다. \"편리하겠어."], ["Light me, M. Bertuccio, and go first; we will see where it leads to.”", "불을 비춰라, 베르투치오 씨, 그리고 앞장서게. 어디로 통하는지 보세.\""]], [["“Monsieur,”", "\"나리,\""], ["replied Bertuccio, “it leads to the garden.”", "베르투치오가 대답했다. \"정원으로 통합니다.\""]], [["“And, pray, how do you know that?”", "\"한데 그걸 어떻게 아나?\""]], [["“It ought to do so, at least.”", "\"적어도 그래야 마땅하니까요.\""]], [["“Well, let us be sure of that.”", "\"좋아, 그럼 확인해 보세.\""]], [["Bertuccio sighed, and went on first; the stairs did, indeed, lead to the garden.", "베르투치오가 한숨을 내쉬며 앞장섰다. 과연 계단은 정원으로 이어졌다."], ["At the outer door the steward paused.", "바깥문 앞에서 집사가 걸음을 멈추었다."]], [["“Go on, Monsieur Bertuccio,”", "\"계속 가게, 베르투치오 씨.\""], ["said the count.", "백작이 말했다."]], [["But he who was addressed stood there, stupefied, bewildered, stunned; his haggard eyes glanced around, as if in search of the traces of some terrible event, and with his clenched hands he seemed striving to shut out horrible recollections.", "그러나 그 말을 들은 사내는 그 자리에 우두커니 서 있었다. 망연자실하여 넋이 나간 채였다. 초췌한 두 눈은 끔찍한 사건의 흔적이라도 찾는 듯 주위를 두리번거렸고, 움켜쥔 두 손으로는 무시무시한 기억을 떨쳐 내려 안간힘을 쓰는 듯했다."]], [["“Well!” insisted the Count.", "\"왜 그러나!\" 백작이 재촉했다."]], [["“No, no,” cried Bertuccio, setting down the lantern at the angle of the interior wall.", "\"아니, 안 됩니다.\" 베르투치오가 안쪽 벽 모퉁이에 등을 내려놓으며 외쳤다."], ["“No, monsieur, it is impossible; I can go no farther.”", "\"안 됩니다, 나리, 이건 불가능합니다. 더는 못 가겠습니다.\""]], [["“What does this mean?”", "\"이게 무슨 뜻이지?\""], ["demanded the irresistible voice of Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토의 거역할 수 없는 목소리가 물었다."]], [["“Why, you must see, your excellency,”", "\"이걸 보십시오, 나리.\""], ["cried the steward, “that this is not natural; that, having a house to purchase, you purchase it exactly at Auteuil, and that, purchasing it at Auteuil, this house should be No.", "집사가 외쳤다. \"이건 예사로운 일이 아닙니다. 집을 사시는데 하필 오퇴유에서 사시고, 오퇴유에서 사시는데 그 집이 하필 퐁텐가 28번지라니요."], ["28, Rue de la Fontaine. Oh, why did I not tell you all?", "아, 어째서 제가 다 말씀드리지 않았을까요?"], ["I am sure you would not have forced me to come.", "그랬다면 절 억지로 여기 오게 하지는 않으셨을 텐데."], ["I hoped your house would have been some other one than this; as if there was not another house at Auteuil than that of the assassination!”", "저는 나리의 집이 이 집이 아닌 다른 집이기를 바랐습니다. 마치 오퇴유에 그 살인이 벌어진 집 말고는 다른 집이 없기라도 한 것처럼요!\""]], [["“What, what!”", "\"뭐라고, 뭐?\""], ["cried Monte Cristo, stopping suddenly, “what words do you utter?", "몬테크리스토가 갑자기 멈춰 서며 외쳤다. \"지금 무슨 말을 하는 건가?"], ["Devil of a man, Corsican that you are—always mysteries or superstitions.", "별난 인간이로군. 역시 코르시카 사람이라 이건가. 늘 알 수 없는 일 아니면 미신 타령이지."], ["Come, take the lantern, and let us visit the garden; you are not afraid of ghosts with me, I hope?”", "자, 등을 들게, 정원을 둘러보세. 나와 함께라면 유령 따위는 두렵지 않겠지?\""]], [["Bertuccio raised the lantern, and obeyed.", "베르투치오는 등을 들어 올리고 명령에 따랐다."], ["The door, as it opened, disclosed a gloomy sky, in which the moon strove vainly to struggle through a sea of clouds that covered her with billows of vapor which she illumined for an instant, only to sink into obscurity.", "문이 열리자 음울한 하늘이 드러났다. 달은 구름의 바다를 뚫고 나오려 헛되이 애쓰고 있었다. 구름은 안개의 물결로 달을 뒤덮었고, 달은 한순간 그것을 비추다가 다시 어둠 속으로 가라앉을 뿐이었다."], ["The steward wished to turn to the left.", "집사가 왼쪽으로 방향을 틀려 했다."]], [["“No, no, monsieur,” said Monte Cristo.", "\"아니, 아니야, 베르투치오.\""], ["“What is the use of following the alleys?", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"오솔길을 따라가서 뭐 하겠나?"], ["Here is a beautiful lawn; let us go on straight forwards.”", "여기 아름다운 잔디밭이 있으니 곧장 앞으로 가세.\""]], [["Bertuccio wiped the perspiration from his brow, but obeyed; however, he continued to take the left hand.", "베르투치오는 이마의 땀을 닦으면서도 명령에 따랐다. 그러나 그는 여전히 왼쪽 길을 택했다."], ["Monte Cristo, on the contrary, took the right hand; arrived near a clump of trees, he stopped.", "반면 몬테크리스토는 오른쪽 길로 들어섰고, 한 무리의 나무들 가까이에 이르러 걸음을 멈추었다."], ["The steward could not restrain himself.", "집사는 더는 자제하지 못했다."]], [["“Move, monsieur—move away, I entreat you; you are exactly in the spot!”", "\"비켜 주십시오, 나리. 거기서 물러나 주십시오, 간청드립니다."], ["“What spot?”", "나리는 바로 그 자리에 서 계십니다!\""]], [["“Where he fell.” 20281m", "\"무슨 자리 말인가?\""]], [["“My dear Monsieur Bertuccio,”", "\"그자가 쓰러진 자리요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, laughing, “control yourself; we are not at Sartène or at Corte.", "\"이보게, 베르투치오 씨.\" 몬테크리스토가 웃으며 말했다. \"정신 좀 차리게."], ["This is not a Corsican _maquis_ but an English garden; badly kept, I own, but still you must not calumniate it for that.”", "여기는 사르테네도 코르테도 아니야. 코르시카의 덤불숲이 아니라 영국식 정원이라고. 손질이 엉망인 건 인정하네만, 그렇다고 험담할 것까지는 없지.\""]], [["“Monsieur, I implore you do not stay there!”", "\"나리, 제발 거기 서 계시지 마십시오!\""]], [["“I think you are going mad, Bertuccio,”", "\"내 보기엔 자네가 미쳐 가는 것 같군, 베르투치오.\""], ["said the count coldly.", "백작이 차갑게 말했다."], ["“If that is the case, I warn you, I shall have you put in a lunatic asylum.”", "\"정말 그렇다면 미리 일러두는데, 자네를 정신병원에 처넣을 걸세.\""]], [["“Alas! excellency,”", "\"아아! 나리.\""], ["returned Bertuccio, joining his hands, and shaking his head in a manner that would have excited the count’s laughter, had not thoughts of a superior interest occupied him, and rendered him attentive to the least revelation of this timorous conscience.", "베르투치오가 두 손을 맞잡고 머리를 흔들며 대답했다. 그 모습은 백작의 웃음을 자아냈을 법했으나, 백작의 머릿속은 더 큰 관심사로 가득 차 있어, 이 겁먹은 양심이 흘리는 사소한 단서 하나에도 귀를 곤두세우고 있었다. \"아아!"], ["“Alas! excellency, the evil has arrived!”", "나리, 기어이 화가 닥치고 말았습니다!\""]], [["“M. Bertuccio,”", "\"베르투치오 씨.\" 백작이 말했다."], ["said the count, “I am very glad to tell you, that while you gesticulate, you wring your hands and roll your eyes like a man possessed by a devil who will not leave him; and I have always observed, that the devil most obstinate to be expelled is a secret.", "\"기꺼이 말해 주지. 자네는 손짓 발짓을 하며 두 손을 비틀고 눈을 굴리는 꼴이 마치 떨어지려 하지 않는 악마에 들린 사람 같아. 그리고 내가 늘 지켜본 바로는, 가장 끈질기게 떨어지지 않는 악마가 바로 비밀이라네."], ["I knew you were a Corsican.", "나는 자네가 코르시카 사람인 걸 알았어."], ["I knew you were gloomy, and always brooding over some old history of the vendetta; and I overlooked that in Italy, because in Italy those things are thought nothing of.", "음울하고 늘 무슨 해묵은 복수극 따위를 곱씹고 있다는 것도 알았지. 하지만 이탈리아에서는 그런 걸 눈감아 주었어. 이탈리아에서는 그런 일을 대수롭지 않게 여기니까."], ["But in France they are considered in very bad taste; there are gendarmes who occupy themselves with such affairs, judges who condemn, and scaffolds which avenge.”", "그러나 프랑스에서는 아주 천박한 취미로 본다네. 그런 일에 매달리는 헌병들이 있고, 단죄하는 판사들이 있고, 복수해 주는 단두대가 있지.\""]], [["Bertuccio clasped his hands, and as, in all these evolutions, he did not let fall the lantern, the light showed his pale and altered countenance.", "베르투치오는 두 손을 움켜쥐었다. 그 모든 몸부림 속에서도 등은 떨어뜨리지 않았기에, 그 불빛이 창백하게 일그러진 그의 얼굴을 드러냈다."], ["Monte Cristo examined him with the same look that, at Rome, he had bent upon the execution of Andrea, and then, in a tone that made a shudder pass through the veins of the poor steward—", "몬테크리스토는 로마에서 안드레아의 처형을 지켜보던 바로 그 눈빛으로 그를 살폈고, 이윽고 가엾은 집사의 핏줄에 전율이 흐르게 하는 어조로 입을 열었다."]], [["“The Abbé Busoni, then told me an untruth,”", "\"그렇다면 부소니 신부가 내게 거짓을 말한 셈이군.\""], ["said he, “when, after his journey in France, in 1829, he sent you to me, with a letter of recommendation, in which he enumerated all your valuable qualities.", "그가 말했다. \"1829년에 프랑스를 여행한 뒤, 자네의 온갖 훌륭한 자질을 늘어놓은 추천서와 함께 자네를 내게 보냈을 때 말이야."], ["Well, I shall write to the abbé; I shall hold him responsible for his _protégé’s_ misconduct, and I shall soon know all about this assassination.", "좋아, 신부에게 편지를 쓰겠네. 자기가 천거한 자의 비행에 대해 책임을 지우고, 이 살인 사건의 전말을 곧 다 알아내겠어."], ["Only I warn you, that when I reside in a country, I conform to all its code, and I have no wish to put myself within the compass of the French laws for your sake.”", "다만 미리 일러두는데, 나는 어떤 나라에 머물면 그 나라의 모든 법을 따르네. 자네 때문에 프랑스 법의 손아귀에 내 몸을 들이밀 생각은 추호도 없어.\""]], [["“Oh, do not do that, excellency; I have always served you faithfully,”", "\"오, 그러지 마십시오, 나리. 저는 늘 충실히 모셔 왔습니다.\""], ["cried Bertuccio, in despair.", "베르투치오가 절망하여 외쳤다."], ["“I have always been an honest man, and, as far as lay in my power, I have done good.”", "\"저는 늘 정직한 사람이었고, 제 힘이 닿는 한 선을 행해 왔습니다.\""]], [["“I do not deny it,”", "\"부정하지는 않겠네.\""], ["returned the count; “but why are you thus agitated.", "백작이 대답했다. \"하지만 어째서 이리 동요하는 건가?"], ["It is a bad sign; a quiet conscience does not occasion such paleness in the cheeks, and such fever in the hands of a man.”", "좋지 않은 징조야. 떳떳한 양심이라면 사람의 뺨을 이토록 창백하게 만들지도, 두 손을 이토록 열에 들뜨게 하지도 않는 법이지.\""]], [["“But, your excellency,”", "\"하지만, 나리,\""], ["replied Bertuccio hesitatingly, “did not the Abbé Busoni, who heard my confession in the prison at Nîmes, tell you that I had a heavy burden upon my conscience?”", "베르투치오가 머뭇거리며 대답했다. \"님 감옥에서 제 고해를 들으셨던 부소니 신부님이, 제 양심에 무거운 짐이 얹혀 있다고 말씀드리지 않았습니까?\""]], [["“Yes; but as he said you would make an excellent steward, I concluded you had stolen—that was all.”", "\"그래. 하지만 신부가 자네를 훌륭한 집사가 될 사람이라고 했기에, 나는 자네가 도둑질을 한 모양이라고만 여겼지. 그게 다였어.\""]], [["“Oh, your excellency!” returned Bertuccio in deep contempt.", "\"오, 나리!\" 베르투치오가 깊은 모멸감을 드러내며 대답했다."]], [["“Or, as you are a Corsican, that you had been unable to resist the desire of making a ‘stiff,’ as you call it.”", "\"아니면 자네가 코르시카 사람이니, 자네들 말로 누구 하나 '뻣뻣하게 만들고' 싶은 욕망을 못 참았으려니 했지.\""]], [["“Yes, my good master,”", "\"그렇습니다, 나리.\""], ["cried Bertuccio, casting himself at the count’s feet, “it was simply vengeance—nothing else.”", "베르투치오가 백작의 발치에 몸을 던지며 외쳤다. \"그건 그저 복수였습니다. 그 외에는 아무것도 아니었습니다.\""]], [["“I understand that, but I do not understand what it is that galvanizes you in this manner.”", "\"그건 알겠어. 하지만 무엇이 자네를 이토록 발작하게 하는지는 모르겠군.\""]], [["“But, monsieur, it is very natural,”", "\"하지만 나리, 그건 너무도 당연한 일입니다.\""], ["returned Bertuccio, “since it was in this house that my vengeance was accomplished.”", "베르투치오가 대답했다. \"제 복수가 이루어진 곳이 바로 이 집이니까요.\""]], [["“What!", "\"뭐라고!"], ["my house?”", "내 집이?\""]], [["“Oh, your excellency, it was not yours, then.”", "\"오, 나리, 그땐 나리의 집이 아니었지요.\""]], [["“Whose, then? The Marquis de Saint-Méran, I think, the concierge said.", "\"그럼 누구 집이었나? 문지기 말로는 생메랑 후작의 집이었다던데."], ["What had you to revenge on the Marquis de Saint-Méran?”", "생메랑 후작에게 무슨 복수를 했단 말인가?\""]], [["“Oh, it was not on him, monsieur; it was on another.”", "\"오, 그분에게 한 게 아닙니다, 나리. 다른 사람에게 한 일이었습니다.\""]], [["“This is strange,”", "\"이상한 일이군.\""], ["returned Monte Cristo, seeming to yield to his reflections, “that you should find yourself without any preparation in a house where the event happened that causes you so much remorse.”", "몬테크리스토가 자신의 상념에 잠긴 듯 대꾸했다. \"자네가 그토록 큰 회한을 안겨 준 사건이 벌어진 집에, 아무런 마음의 준비도 없이 들어서게 되다니 말이야.\""]], [["“Monsieur,”", "\"나리,\""], ["said the steward, “it is fatality, I am sure.", "집사가 말했다. \"이건 운명입니다, 분명합니다."], ["First, you purchase a house at Auteuil—this house is the one where I have committed an assassination; you descend to the garden by the same staircase by which he descended; you stop at the spot where he received the blow; and two paces farther is the grave in which he had just buried his child.", "우선 나리는 오퇴유에 집을 사셨는데, 이 집이 바로 제가 살인을 저지른 집입니다. 나리는 그자가 내려간 바로 그 계단으로 정원에 내려오셨고, 그자가 칼을 맞은 바로 그 자리에 멈춰 서셨습니다. 그리고 거기서 두 걸음 떨어진 곳에, 그자가 막 제 아이를 묻은 무덤이 있습니다."], ["This is not chance, for chance, in this case, is too much like Providence.”", "이건 우연이 아닙니다. 이런 경우의 우연이란 섭리와 너무도 닮았으니까요.\""]], [["“Well, amiable Corsican, let us suppose it is Providence.", "\"좋아, 친애하는 코르시카 양반, 그게 섭리라고 치세."], ["I always suppose anything people please, and, besides, you must concede something to diseased minds.", "나는 늘 사람들이 원하는 건 무엇이든 그렇다고 쳐 주거든. 게다가 병든 마음에는 얼마쯤 양보해 줘야 하는 법이지."], ["Come, collect yourself, and tell me all.”", "자, 정신을 차리고, 내게 모두 말해 보게.\""]], [["“I have related it but once, and that was to the Abbé Busoni.", "\"저는 이 이야기를 단 한 번 했을 뿐입니다. 그것도 부소니 신부님께요."], ["Such things,”", "그런 일은,\""], ["continued Bertuccio, shaking his head, “are only related under the seal of confession.”", "베르투치오가 머리를 흔들며 말을 이었다. \"고해의 봉인 아래에서만 털어놓는 법입니다.\""]], [["“Then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["said the count, “I refer you to your confessor.", "백작이 말했다. \"자네를 고해 신부에게 보내 주지."], ["Turn Chartreux or Trappist, and relate your secrets, but, as for me, I do not like anyone who is alarmed by such phantasms, and I do not choose that my servants should be afraid to walk in the garden of an evening.", "카르투지오회나 트라피스트회 수도사가 되어 자네 비밀을 털어놓게나. 하지만 나로 말하자면, 그런 헛것에 겁먹는 자는 좋아하지 않아. 그리고 내 하인들이 저녁에 정원을 거니는 것조차 두려워하는 꼴은 보고 싶지 않네."], ["I confess I am not very desirous of a visit from the commissary of police, for, in Italy, justice is only paid when silent—in France she is paid only when she speaks.", "솔직히 나는 경찰서장의 방문 따위는 별로 반갑지 않아. 이탈리아에서는 정의가 침묵할 때만 값을 치르지만, 프랑스에서는 입을 열 때만 값을 치르는 법이니까. 제기랄!"], ["_Peste!_ I thought you somewhat Corsican, a great deal smuggler, and an excellent steward; but I see you have other strings to your bow.", "나는 자네가 코르시카 사람 기질이 좀 있고, 밀수꾼 솜씨가 뛰어나며, 훌륭한 집사라고만 여겼지. 한데 보아하니 자네 활에는 다른 줄도 걸려 있었군."], ["You are no longer in my service, Monsieur Bertuccio.”", "자네는 이제 내 사람이 아닐세, 베르투치오 씨.\""]], [["“Oh, your excellency, your excellency!”", "\"오, 나리, 나리!\""], ["cried the steward, struck with terror at this threat, “if that is the only reason I cannot remain in your service, I will tell all, for if I quit you, it will only be to go to the scaffold.”", "그 위협에 공포로 얼어붙은 집사가 외쳤다. \"그것만이 제가 나리를 더 모실 수 없는 이유라면, 모두 말씀드리겠습니다. 나리를 떠나는 건 곧 단두대로 가는 길일 뿐이니까요.\""]], [["“That is different,”", "\"그렇다면 얘기가 다르지.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo; “but if you intend to tell an untruth, reflect it were better not to speak at all.”", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"하지만 거짓을 말할 작정이라면, 차라리 입을 다무는 편이 낫다는 걸 명심하게.\""]], [["“No, monsieur, I swear to you, by my hopes of salvation, I will tell you all, for the Abbé Busoni himself only knew a part of my secret; but, I pray you, go away from that plane-tree.", "\"아닙니다, 나리. 구원의 소망을 걸고 맹세하건대, 모두 말씀드리겠습니다. 부소니 신부님조차도 제 비밀의 일부만 아셨으니까요."], ["The moon is just bursting through the clouds, and there, standing where you do, and wrapped in that cloak that conceals your figure, you remind me of M. de Villefort.”", "하지만 부탁드립니다, 저 플라타너스 나무에서 물러나 주십시오. 마침 달이 구름을 뚫고 나오는데, 나리께서 거기 그렇게 서서 몸을 가리는 그 망토를 두르고 계시니, 빌포르 씨가 떠오릅니다.\""]], [["“What!”", "\"뭐라고!\""], ["cried Monte Cristo, “it was M. de Villefort?”", "몬테크리스토가 외쳤다. \"빌포르 씨였단 말인가?\""]], [["“Your excellency knows him?”", "\"나리께서 그분을 아십니까?\""]], [["“The former royal attorney at Nîmes?”", "\"님의 전직 검사 말인가?\" \"그렇습니다.\""]], [["“Who married the Marquis of Saint-Méran’s daughter?”", "\"생메랑 후작의 딸과 결혼한?\" \"그렇습니다.\""]], [["“Yes.” “Who enjoyed the reputation of being the most severe, the most upright, the most rigid magistrate on the bench?”", "\"가장 엄정하고, 가장 강직하며, 가장 추상같은 법관이라는 평판을 누리던 그 사람?\""]], [["“Well, monsieur,”", "\"그렇습니다, 나리.\""], ["said Bertuccio, “this man with this spotless reputation——”", "베르투치오가 말했다. \"그 흠 하나 없는 평판을 지닌 사내가……\""]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서?\""]], [["“Was a villain.”", "\"악당이었습니다.\""]], [["“Bah,”", "\"쳇,\""], ["replied Monte Cristo, “impossible!”", "몬테크리스토가 대꾸했다. \"그럴 리가!\""]], [["“It is as I tell you.”", "\"제 말 그대로입니다.\""]], [["“Ah, really,”", "\"아, 정말로.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["“Have you proof of this?”", "\"그 증거가 있나?\""]], [["“I had it.”", "\"있었습니다.\""]], [["“And you have lost it; how stupid!”", "\"한데 그걸 잃어버렸다고? 어리석기는!\""]], [["“Yes; but by careful search it might be recovered.”", "\"그렇습니다. 하지만 잘 찾아보면 되찾을 수 있을지도 모릅니다.\""]], [["“Really,”", "\"정말이지,\""], ["returned the count, “relate it to me, for it begins to interest me.”", "백작이 대꾸했다. \"내게 들려주게. 슬슬 흥미가 동하기 시작하는군.\""]], [["And the count, humming an air from _Lucia_, went to sit down on a bench, while Bertuccio followed him, collecting his thoughts.", "그러고는 백작이 「람메르무어의 루치아」의 한 가락을 흥얼거리며 벤치에 가서 앉았고, 베르투치오는 생각을 가다듬으며 그를 따랐다."], ["Bertuccio remained standing before him. 20285m", "베르투치오는 그의 앞에 선 채로 있었다."]], [["# Chapter 44. The Vendetta", "# Chapter 44. The Vendetta"]], [["At what point shall I begin my story, your excellency?” asked Bertuccio.", "“이야기를 어디서부터 시작할까요, 백작님?” 베르투치오가 물었다."]], [["“Where you please,”", "“자네가 좋은 데서 시작하게."], ["returned Monte Cristo, “since I know nothing at all of it.”", "난 아무것도 모르니까.” 몬테크리스토가 대답했다."]], [["“I thought the Abbé Busoni had told your excellency.”", "“부소니 신부님께서 백작님께 말씀드린 줄 알았습니다.”"]], [["“Some particulars, doubtless, but that is seven or eight years ago, and I have forgotten them.”", "“몇 가지 사정은 들었지. 하지만 칠팔 년 전 일이라 다 잊어버렸네.”"]], [["“Then I can speak without fear of tiring your excellency.”", "“그렇다면 백작님을 지루하게 할 걱정 없이 말씀드릴 수 있겠군요.”"]], [["“Go on, M. Bertuccio; you will supply the want of the evening papers.”", "“계속하게, 베르투치오. 자네가 저녁 신문을 대신해 주는 셈이지.”"]], [["“The story begins in 1815.”", "“이야기는 1815년에 시작됩니다.”"]], [["“Ah,”", "“아,”"], ["said Monte Cristo, “1815 is not yesterday.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. “1815년이라면 어제오늘 일이 아니로군.”"]], [["“No, monsieur, and yet I recollect all things as clearly as if they had happened but then.", "“네, 백작님. 그런데도 모든 일이 바로 그때 일어난 것처럼 또렷이 기억납니다. 제게는 형이 하나 있었습니다."], ["I had a brother, an elder brother, who was in the service of the emperor; he had become lieutenant in a regiment composed entirely of Corsicans.", "손위 형이었지요. 황제 폐하 밑에서 복무했고, 코르시카 사람들로만 이루어진 연대의 중위가 되어 있었습니다. 그 형은 제 유일한 벗이었습니다."], ["This brother was my only friend; we became orphans—I at five, he at eighteen.", "우리는 고아가 되었는데, 저는 다섯 살, 형은 열여덟 살이었지요."], ["He brought me up as if I had been his son, and in 1814 he married.", "형은 저를 친아들처럼 길러 주었고, 1814년에 결혼했습니다."], ["When the emperor returned from the Island of Elba, my brother instantly joined the army, was slightly wounded at Waterloo, and retired with the army beyond the Loire.”", "황제 폐하가 엘바섬에서 돌아오시자 형은 곧장 군에 합류했고, 워털루에서 가볍게 부상을 입은 뒤 군대를 따라 루아르강 너머로 물러났습니다.”"]], [["“But that is the history of the Hundred Days, M.", "“하지만 그건 백일천하 이야기 아닌가, 베르투치오.”"], ["Bertuccio,”", "백작이 말했다."], ["said the count; “unless I am mistaken, it has been already written.”", "“내가 틀리지 않았다면, 그건 이미 다 기록되어 있는 일일세.”"]], [["“Excuse me, excellency, but these details are necessary, and you promised to be patient.”", "“송구합니다, 백작님. 하지만 이런 자세한 사정이 꼭 필요하고, 또 인내해 주시겠다고 약속하셨지 않습니까.”"]], [["“Go on; I will keep my word.”", "“계속하게. 약속은 지키지.”"]], [["“One day we received a letter. I should tell you that we lived in the little village of Rogliano, at the extremity of Cap Corse.", "“어느 날 우리는 편지 한 통을 받았습니다. 말씀드리자면 우리는 코르시카곶 끝자락에 있는 로글리아노라는 작은 마을에 살고 있었습니다."], ["This letter was from my brother.", "그 편지는 형이 보낸 것이었습니다."], ["He told us that the army was disbanded, and that he should return by Châteauroux, Clermont-Ferrand, Le Puy, and Nîmes; and, if I had any money, he prayed me to leave it for him at Nîmes, with an innkeeper with whom I had dealings.”", "형은 군대가 해산되었으며, 샤토루와 클레르몽페랑, 르퓌, 님을 거쳐 돌아오겠다고 적었습니다. 그리고 만약 제게 돈이 있거든 님에서 제가 거래하던 어느 여관 주인에게 맡겨 두라고 부탁했습니다.”"]], [["“In the smuggling line?”", "“밀수 거래 말인가?”"], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“Eh, your excellency? Everyone must live.”", "“네, 백작님. 사람은 누구나 먹고살아야 하지 않습니까.”"]], [["“Certainly; go on.”", "“그렇지. 계속하게.”"]], [["“I loved my brother tenderly, as I told your excellency, and I resolved not to send the money, but to take it to him myself.", "“말씀드렸듯이 저는 형을 끔찍이 아꼈습니다. 그래서 돈을 부치는 대신 직접 가져다주기로 마음먹었습니다."], ["I possessed a thousand francs.", "제게는 천 프랑이 있었지요."], ["I left five hundred with Assunta, my sister-in-law, and with the other five hundred I set off for Nîmes.", "그중 오백 프랑은 형수 아순타에게 남겨 두고, 나머지 오백 프랑을 들고 님으로 떠났습니다."], ["It was easy to do so, and as I had my boat and a lading to take in at sea, everything favored my project.", "그건 어려운 일이 아니었습니다. 제게는 배가 있었고 바다에서 실어 올 짐도 있었으니, 모든 게 제 계획에 들어맞았지요."], ["But, after we had taken in our cargo, the wind became contrary, so that we were four or five days without being able to enter the Rhône.", "그런데 화물을 다 실은 뒤로 바람이 거꾸로 불어, 나흘이고 닷새고 론강에 들어가지 못했습니다."], ["At last, however, we succeeded, and worked up to Arles.", "하지만 마침내 성공해서 아를까지 거슬러 올라갔습니다."], ["I left the boat between Bellegarde and Beaucaire, and took the road to Nîmes.”", "저는 벨가르드와 보케르 사이에 배를 두고, 님으로 가는 길에 올랐습니다.”"]], [["“We are getting to the story now?”", "“이제 본 이야기로 들어가는 건가?”"]], [["“Yes, your excellency; excuse me, but, as you will see, I only tell you what is absolutely necessary.", "“네, 백작님. 송구합니다만, 보시다시피 저는 꼭 필요한 것만 말씀드리고 있습니다."], ["Just at this time the famous massacres took place in the south of France.", "마침 그 무렵 프랑스 남부에서 그 악명 높은 학살이 벌어졌습니다."], ["Three brigands, called Trestaillon, Truphemy, and Graffan, publicly assassinated everybody whom they suspected of Bonapartism.", "트레스타용, 트뤼페미, 그라팡이라는 세 악당이 보나파르트파라고 의심되는 사람은 누구든 공공연히 죽였습니다."], ["You have doubtless heard of these massacres, your excellency?”", "백작님께서도 분명 그 학살에 대해 들으셨겠지요?”"]], [["“Vaguely; I was far from France at that period.", "“어렴풋이. 그 무렵 나는 프랑스에서 멀리 떨어져 있었네."], ["Go on.”", "계속하게.”"]], [["“As I entered Nîmes, I literally waded in blood; at every step you encountered dead bodies and bands of murderers, who killed, plundered, and burned.", "“님에 들어서자 저는 그야말로 피바다 속을 헤치고 다니는 형국이었습니다. 발을 옮길 때마다 시체와, 죽이고 약탈하고 불태우는 살인자 무리와 마주쳤습니다."], ["At the sight of this slaughter and devastation I became terrified, not for myself—for I, a simple Corsican fisherman, had nothing to fear; on the contrary, that time was most favorable for us smugglers—but for my brother, a soldier of the empire, returning from the army of the Loire, with his uniform and his epaulets, there was everything to apprehend.", "그 살육과 참상을 보며 저는 두려움에 휩싸였습니다. 제 자신을 위해서가 아니었지요. 저야 한낱 코르시카 어부에 불과하니 두려울 게 없었고, 오히려 그런 시절은 우리 밀수꾼들에게는 더없이 유리했으니까요."], ["I hastened to the innkeeper. My misgivings had been but too true.", "하지만 형은 제국의 군인이었습니다. 루아르 군대에서 돌아오는 길이었고, 군복과 견장까지 갖추고 있었으니, 모든 게 염려스러웠습니다."], ["My brother had arrived the previous evening at Nîmes, and, at the very door of the house where he was about to demand hospitality, he had been assassinated.", "저는 서둘러 여관 주인을 찾아갔습니다. 제 불길한 예감은 너무도 정확했습니다. 형은 전날 저녁 님에 도착했고, 묵을 곳을 청하려던 바로 그 집 문 앞에서 살해당했던 것입니다."], ["I did all in my power to discover the murderers, but no one durst tell me their names, so much were they dreaded.", "저는 살인자들을 찾아내려고 온갖 수를 다 썼지만, 그들이 어찌나 두려운 존재였던지 누구도 감히 그 이름을 말해 주지 않았습니다."], ["I then thought of that French justice of which I had heard so much, and which feared nothing, and I went to the king’s attorney.”", "그래서 저는 그토록 많이 들어 왔던, 아무것도 두려워하지 않는다는 그 프랑스 사법을 떠올리고, 검사를 찾아갔습니다.”"]], [["“And this king’s attorney was named Villefort?”", "“그런데 그 검사의 이름이 빌포르였나?”"], ["asked Monte Cristo carelessly.", "몬테크리스토가 무심한 듯 물었다."]], [["“Yes, your excellency; he came from Marseilles, where he had been deputy procureur.", "“네, 백작님. 그는 마르세유 출신으로, 그곳에서 검사 대리를 지냈지요."], ["His zeal had procured him advancement, and he was said to be one of the first who had informed the government of the departure from the Island of Elba.”", "그 열성 덕에 승진했고, 엘바섬에서의 탈출을 정부에 가장 먼저 알린 사람 중 하나라는 말이 있었습니다.”"]], [["“Then,”", "“그래서,”"], ["said Monte Cristo “you went to him?”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. “그를 찾아갔단 말이지?”"]], [["“‘Monsieur,’ I said, ‘my brother was assassinated yesterday in the streets of Nîmes, I know not by whom, but it is your duty to find out.", "“‘검사님, 제 형이 어제 님 거리에서 살해당했습니다. 누구의 손에 죽었는지는 모르지만, 그것을 밝혀내는 것이 당신의 의무입니다."], ["You are the representative of justice here, and it is for justice to avenge those she has been unable to protect.’", "당신은 이곳 정의의 대리자이고, 정의가 미처 지켜 주지 못한 자들의 원수를 갚는 것이 바로 정의의 몫이니까요.’ 제가 말했습니다."]], [["“‘Who was your brother?’ asked he.", "“‘당신 형이 누구였소?’ 그가 물었습니다."]], [["“‘A lieutenant in the Corsican battalion.’", "“‘코르시카 대대의 중위였습니다.’"]], [["“‘A soldier of the usurper, then?’", "“‘그럼 찬탈자의 군인이었군?’"]], [["“‘A soldier of the French army.’", "“‘프랑스 군대의 군인이었습니다.’"]], [["“‘Well,’ replied he, ‘he has smitten with the sword, and he has perished by the sword.’", "“‘흠,’ 그가 대답했습니다. ‘칼로 친 자는 칼로 망하는 법이오.’"]], [["“‘You are mistaken, monsieur,’ I replied; ‘he has perished by the poniard.’", "“‘잘못 아셨습니다, 검사님.’ 제가 대꾸했습니다. ‘제 형은 단검에 찔려 죽었습니다.’"]], [["“‘What do you want me to do?’ asked the magistrate.", "“‘그래서 내가 뭘 해 주길 바라는 거요?’ 그 판사가 물었습니다."]], [["“‘I have already told you—avenge him.’", "“‘이미 말씀드렸습니다. 원수를 갚아 주십시오.’"]], [["“‘On whom?’", "“‘누구에게?’"]], [["“‘On his murderers.’", "“‘형을 죽인 자들에게요.’"]], [["“‘How should I know who they are?’", "“‘그자들이 누군지 내가 어찌 안단 말이오?’"]], [["“‘Order them to be sought for.’", "“‘찾으라고 명령하시면 됩니다.’"]], [["“‘Why, your brother has been involved in a quarrel, and killed in a duel.", "“‘이보시오, 당신 형은 싸움에 휘말려 결투로 죽은 거요."], ["All these old soldiers commit excesses which were tolerated in the time of the emperor, but which are not suffered now, for the people here do not like soldiers of such disorderly conduct.’", "그 늙은 군인들이란 죄다 황제 시절에는 묵인되던 행패를 부리는데, 지금은 그게 용납되지 않소. 이곳 사람들은 그런 난폭한 군인들을 좋아하지 않으니까.’"]], [["“‘Monsieur,’ I replied, ‘it is not for myself that I entreat your interference—I should grieve for him or avenge him, but my poor brother had a wife, and were anything to happen to me, the poor creature would perish from want, for my brother’s pay alone kept her.", "“‘검사님,’ 제가 대답했습니다. ‘제가 개입을 청하는 것은 저 자신을 위해서가 아닙니다. 저야 형을 위해 슬퍼하거나 복수하면 그만이지요."], ["Pray, try and obtain a small government pension for her.’", "하지만 가엾은 제 형에게는 아내가 있고, 만약 제게 무슨 일이 생기면 그 가엾은 여인은 굶어 죽고 말 겁니다. 형의 봉급만으로 살아가던 사람이거든요. 부디 그 여인에게 정부의 작은 연금이라도 받게 해 주십시오.’"]], [["“‘Every revolution has its catastrophes,’ returned M. de Villefort; ‘your brother has been the victim of this.", "“‘혁명에는 으레 참화가 따르는 법이오.’ 빌포르 씨가 대답했습니다. ‘당신 형은 그 희생자가 된 거요."], ["It is a misfortune, and government owes nothing to his family.", "불행한 일이지만, 정부가 그 가족에게 빚진 것은 없소."], ["If we are to judge by all the vengeance that the followers of the usurper exercised on the partisans of the king, when, in their turn, they were in power, your brother would be today, in all probability, condemned to death.", "찬탈자의 추종자들이 권력을 쥐었을 때 왕당파를 향해 휘두른 그 모든 복수를 기준으로 따진다면, 당신 형은 아마 오늘쯤 사형을 선고받았을 거요."], ["What has happened is quite natural, and in conformity with the law of reprisals.’", "일어난 일은 지극히 자연스럽고, 보복의 법칙에도 들어맞소.’"]], [["“‘What,’ cried I, ‘do you, a magistrate, speak thus to me?’", "“‘아니,’ 제가 소리쳤습니다. ‘판사라는 분이 제게 그런 식으로 말씀하십니까?’"]], [["“‘All these Corsicans are mad, on my honor,’ replied M. de Villefort; ‘they fancy that their countryman is still emperor.", "“‘이 코르시카 놈들은 하나같이 미쳤어, 정말이지.’ 빌포르 씨가 대꾸했습니다. ‘제 고향 사람이 아직도 황제인 줄 아는군."], ["You have mistaken the time, you should have told me this two months ago, it is too late now.", "당신은 때를 잘못 짚었소. 이런 얘기는 두 달 전에 했어야지, 이젠 너무 늦었소."], ["Go now, at once, or I shall have you put out.’", "어서 나가시오, 당장. 안 그러면 끌어내게 할 테니.’"]], [["“I looked at him an instant to see if there was anything to hope from further entreaty. But he was a man of stone.", "“저는 더 애원해 봤자 기대할 것이 있는지 가늠하려고 한순간 그를 바라보았습니다. 하지만 그는 돌로 된 사람이었습니다."], ["I approached him, and said in a low voice, ‘Well, since you know the Corsicans so well, you know that they always keep their word.", "저는 그에게 다가가 낮은 목소리로 말했습니다. ‘좋습니다. 코르시카 사람을 그토록 잘 아신다니, 그들이 언제나 한 말은 지킨다는 것도 아시겠지요."], ["You think that it was a good deed to kill my brother, who was a Bonapartist, because you are a royalist.", "당신은 왕당파라서, 보나파르트파였던 제 형을 죽인 것이 잘한 일이라 여기는군요."], ["Well, I, who am a Bonapartist also, declare one thing to you, which is, that I will kill you.", "그렇다면 저 역시 보나파르트파인 사람으로서 한 가지를 분명히 말해 두겠습니다. 저는 당신을 죽일 겁니다."], ["From this moment I declare the vendetta against you, so protect yourself as well as you can, for the next time we meet your last hour has come.’ And before he had recovered from his surprise, I opened the door and left the room.”", "이 순간부터 저는 당신에게 방데타를 선언합니다. 그러니 할 수 있는 한 몸조심하시지요. 다음에 우리가 만날 때가 당신의 마지막 순간이 될 테니까요.’ 그리고 그가 놀라움에서 채 깨어나기도 전에, 저는 문을 열고 방을 나섰습니다.”"]], [["“Well, well,”", "“허허,”"], ["said Monte Cristo, “such an innocent looking person as you are to do those things, M. Bertuccio, and to a king’s attorney at that!", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. “자네처럼 순해 보이는 사람이 그런 일을 저지르다니, 그것도 검사를 상대로 말이야!"], ["But did he know what was meant by the terrible word ‘vendetta’?”", "그런데 그자는 ‘방데타’라는 그 무서운 말이 무슨 뜻인지 알았나?”"]], [["“He knew so well, that from that moment he shut himself in his house, and never went out unattended, seeking me high and low.", "“어찌나 잘 알았던지, 그 순간부터 집 안에 틀어박혀 누군가를 대동하지 않고는 절대 밖에 나가지 않았고, 사방으로 저를 찾아다녔습니다."], ["Fortunately, I was so well concealed that he could not find me.", "다행히 저는 워낙 잘 숨어 있어서 그가 찾아낼 수 없었지요."], ["Then he became alarmed, and dared not stay any longer at Nîmes, so he solicited a change of residence, and, as he was in reality very influential, he was nominated to Versailles.", "그러자 그는 겁을 먹고 더는 님에 머물 엄두를 내지 못했습니다. 그래서 임지를 옮겨 달라고 청원했고, 실제로 영향력이 큰 사람이었던지라 베르사유로 발령받았습니다."], ["But, as you know, a Corsican who has sworn to avenge himself cares not for distance, so his carriage, fast as it went, was never above half a day’s journey before me, who followed him on foot.", "하지만 백작님도 아시다시피, 복수를 맹세한 코르시카 사람에게는 거리 따위는 문제가 되지 않습니다. 그러니 그의 마차가 아무리 빨리 달려도, 걸어서 뒤를 쫓는 저보다 반나절 이상 앞선 적이 없었습니다."], ["The most important thing was, not to kill him only—for I had an opportunity of doing so a hundred times—but to kill him without being discovered—at least, without being arrested.", "가장 중요한 것은 단지 그를 죽이는 것이 아니라 — 죽일 기회야 백 번도 더 있었으니까요 — 들키지 않고, 적어도 붙잡히지 않고 죽이는 것이었습니다."], ["I no longer belonged to myself, for I had my sister-in-law to protect and provide for.", "이제 저는 제 한 몸만의 사람이 아니었으니까요. 제게는 보살피고 부양해야 할 형수가 있었습니다."]], [["“For three months I watched M. de Villefort, for three months he took not a step out-of-doors without my following him.", "“석 달 동안 저는 빌포르 씨를 감시했고, 석 달 동안 그는 한 걸음도 밖으로 내딛지 못한 채 제가 그 뒤를 따랐습니다."], ["At length I discovered that he went mysteriously to Auteuil.", "마침내 저는 그가 은밀히 오퇴유로 드나든다는 것을 알아냈습니다."], ["I followed him thither, and I saw him enter the house where we now are, only, instead of entering by the great door that looks into the street, he came on horseback, or in his carriage, left the one or the other at the little inn, and entered by the gate you see there.”", "저는 그를 그곳까지 따라갔고, 지금 우리가 있는 바로 이 집으로 그가 들어가는 것을 보았습니다. 다만 거리 쪽으로 난 큰 문으로 들어가는 대신, 그는 말을 타거나 마차를 타고 와서는 그 말이나 마차를 작은 여관에 맡기고, 저기 보이는 저 쪽문으로 들어갔습니다.”"]], [["Monte Cristo made a sign with his head to show that he could discern in the darkness the door to which Bertuccio alluded.", "몬테크리스토는 베르투치오가 가리키는 문을 어둠 속에서도 알아볼 수 있다는 뜻으로 고개를 끄덕여 보였다."]], [["“As I had nothing more to do at Versailles, I went to Auteuil, and gained all the information I could.", "“이제 베르사유에서는 더 할 일이 없었으므로, 저는 오퇴유로 가서 알아낼 수 있는 정보를 죄다 캐냈습니다."], ["If I wished to surprise him, it was evident this was the spot to lie in wait for him.", "그를 기습하려면 바로 이곳이 매복하기에 적당한 곳임이 분명했지요."], ["The house belonged, as the concierge informed your excellency, to M. de Saint-Méran, Villefort’s father-in-law. M.", "그 집은, 문지기가 백작님께 말씀드렸듯이, 빌포르의 장인인 생메랑 씨의 소유였습니다."], ["de Saint-Méran lived at Marseilles, so that this country house was useless to him, and it was reported to be let to a young widow, known only by the name of ‘the Baroness.’", "생메랑 씨는 마르세유에 살았으므로 이 시골 별장은 그에게 쓸모가 없었고, 오로지 ‘남작 부인’이라는 이름으로만 알려진 어느 젊은 과부에게 세를 놓았다는 소문이 돌았습니다."]], [["“One evening, as I was looking over the wall, I saw a young and handsome woman who was walking alone in that garden, which was not overlooked by any windows, and I guessed that she was awaiting M. de Villefort.", "“어느 저녁, 제가 담장 너머를 살펴보고 있는데, 젊고 아름다운 여인이 그 정원을 홀로 거니는 것이 보였습니다. 그 정원은 어느 창에서도 내려다보이지 않는 곳이었고, 저는 그 여인이 빌포르 씨를 기다리고 있다고 짐작했습니다."], ["When she was sufficiently near for me to distinguish her features, I saw she was from eighteen to nineteen, tall and very fair.", "여인이 이목구비를 알아볼 수 있을 만큼 가까이 다가왔을 때, 저는 그녀가 열여덟이나 열아홉쯤 되고, 키가 크며 살결이 무척 흰 여인임을 보았습니다."], ["As she had a loose muslin dress on and as nothing concealed her figure, I saw she would ere long become a mother. A few moments after, the little door was opened and a man entered.", "헐렁한 모슬린 드레스를 입고 있어 몸매가 전혀 가려지지 않았던 까닭에, 저는 그녀가 머지않아 어머니가 되리라는 것을 알 수 있었습니다. 잠시 후 쪽문이 열리고 한 남자가 들어왔습니다."], ["The young woman hastened to meet him.", "젊은 여인이 서둘러 그를 맞으러 갔습니다."], ["They threw themselves into each other’s arms, embraced tenderly, and returned together to the house. The man was M.", "두 사람은 서로의 품에 뛰어들어 다정하게 끌어안고는 함께 집 안으로 들어갔습니다. 그 남자가 바로 빌포르 씨였습니다."], ["de Villefort; I fully believed that when he went out in the night he would be forced to traverse the whole of the garden alone.” 20291m", "저는 그가 밤에 나올 때면 그 넓은 정원 전체를 홀로 가로질러야 하리라고 굳게 믿었습니다.”"]], [["“And,”", "“그런데,”"], ["asked the count, “did you ever know the name of this woman?”", "백작이 물었다. “그 여인의 이름은 알아냈나?”"]], [["“No, excellency,”", "“아니요, 백작님.”"], ["returned Bertuccio; “you will see that I had no time to learn it.”", "베르투치오가 대답했다. “보시면 아시겠지만, 그것을 알아낼 겨를이 없었습니다.”"]], [["“Go on.”", "“계속하게.”"]], [["“That evening,”", "“그날 저녁,”"], ["continued Bertuccio, “I could have killed the procureur, but as I was not sufficiently acquainted with the neighborhood, I was fearful of not killing him on the spot, and that if his cries were overheard I might be taken; so I put it off until the next occasion, and in order that nothing should escape me, I took a chamber looking into the street bordered by the wall of the garden.", "베르투치오가 말을 이었다. “저는 그 검사를 죽일 수도 있었습니다. 하지만 그 일대를 충분히 알지 못해, 단번에 죽이지 못할까 봐, 그래서 그의 비명이 누군가에게 들려 제가 붙잡힐까 봐 두려웠습니다."], ["Three days after, about seven o’clock in the evening, I saw a servant on horseback leave the house at full gallop, and take the road to Sèvres.", "그래서 다음 기회로 미루었고, 무엇 하나 놓치지 않으려고 정원 담장이 면한 거리 쪽으로 난 방 하나를 빌렸습니다. 사흘 뒤, 저녁 일곱 시쯤, 하인 하나가 말을 타고 전속력으로 집을 나서서 세브르 쪽으로 길을 잡는 것을 보았습니다."], ["I concluded that he was going to Versailles, and I was not deceived.", "저는 그가 베르사유로 가는 것이라고 짐작했고, 제 짐작은 틀리지 않았습니다."], ["Three hours later, the man returned covered with dust, his errand was performed, and two minutes after, another man on foot, muffled in a mantle, opened the little door of the garden, which he closed after him.", "세 시간 뒤 그 하인은 먼지를 뒤집어쓴 채 돌아왔습니다. 심부름을 마친 것이지요. 그리고 이 분 뒤, 망토를 두른 또 다른 남자가 걸어와 정원 쪽문을 열고 들어선 다음 문을 닫았습니다."], ["I descended rapidly; although I had not seen Villefort’s face, I recognized him by the beating of my heart.", "저는 재빨리 내려갔습니다. 빌포르의 얼굴은 보지 못했지만, 가슴이 뛰는 것으로 그를 알아차렸습니다."], ["I crossed the street, and stopped at a post placed at the angle of the wall, and by means of which I had once before looked into the garden.", "저는 거리를 가로질러, 담장 모퉁이에 세워진 말뚝 곁에 멈춰 섰습니다. 전에도 한 번 그것을 딛고 정원 안을 들여다본 적이 있었지요."]], [["“This time I did not content myself with looking, but I took my knife out of my pocket, felt that the point was sharp, and sprang over the wall.", "“이번에는 그저 들여다보는 것으로 만족하지 않고, 주머니에서 칼을 꺼내 칼끝이 날카로운지 만져 본 다음 담장을 뛰어넘었습니다."], ["My first care was to run to the door; he had left the key in it, taking the simple precaution of turning it twice in the lock.", "제일 먼저 한 일은 쪽문으로 달려가는 것이었습니다. 그는 자물쇠에 열쇠를 그대로 꽂아 둔 채, 그저 두 번 돌려 잠그는 정도의 단순한 조심만 해 두었더군요."], ["Nothing, then, preventing my escape by this means, I examined the grounds.", "그러니 그 문으로 달아나는 데에는 아무 걸림돌이 없는 셈이어서, 저는 정원을 살폈습니다."], ["The garden was long and narrow; a stretch of smooth turf extended down the middle, and at the corners were clumps of trees with thick and massy foliage, that made a background for the shrubs and flowers.", "정원은 길고 좁았습니다. 한가운데로는 매끄러운 잔디밭이 길게 뻗어 있었고, 모퉁이마다 잎이 두껍고 무성한 나무들이 무리 지어 관목과 꽃들의 배경을 이루고 있었습니다."], ["In order to go from the door to the house, or from the house to the door, M. de Villefort would be obliged to pass by one of these clumps of trees.", "쪽문에서 집으로, 또는 집에서 쪽문으로 가려면 빌포르 씨는 이 나무 덤불 가운데 하나를 반드시 지나야 했습니다."]], [["20293m\n“It was the end of September; the wind blew violently.", "“때는 9월 말이었고, 바람이 사납게 불었습니다."], ["The faint glimpses of the pale moon, hidden momentarily by masses of dark clouds that were sweeping across the sky, whitened the gravel walks that led to the house, but were unable to pierce the obscurity of the thick shrubberies, in which a man could conceal himself without any fear of discovery.", "하늘을 휩쓸고 지나가는 검은 구름 떼에 이따금 가려지는 창백한 달의 희미한 빛이 집으로 이어지는 자갈길을 하얗게 비추었지만, 사람이 들킬 염려 없이 몸을 숨길 수 있는 그 무성한 덤불의 어둠만은 꿰뚫지 못했습니다. 저는 빌포르가 반드시 지날 길과 가장 가까운 덤불 속에 몸을 숨겼습니다."], ["I hid myself in the one nearest to the path Villefort must take, and scarcely was I there when, amidst the gusts of wind, I fancied I heard groans; but you know, or rather you do not know, your excellency, that he who is about to commit an assassination fancies that he hears low cries perpetually ringing in his ears.", "그런데 거기 들어서자마자, 휘몰아치는 바람 소리 사이로 신음이 들리는 것 같았습니다. 하지만 백작님께서도 아시겠지요, 아니 오히려 모르시겠지요. 사람을 죽이려는 자에게는 낮은 비명이 줄곧 귓가에 울리는 듯이 느껴지는 법입니다."], ["Two hours passed thus, during which I imagined I heard moans repeatedly.", "그렇게 두 시간이 흘렀고, 그동안 저는 거듭 신음 소리를 듣는 것만 같았습니다."], ["Midnight struck. As the last stroke died away, I saw a faint light shine through the windows of the private staircase by which we have just descended.", "자정을 알리는 종이 울렸습니다. 마지막 종소리가 잦아드는 순간, 우리가 방금 내려온 그 비밀 계단의 창문으로 희미한 불빛이 새어 나오는 것이 보였습니다."], ["The door opened, and the man in the mantle reappeared.", "문이 열리고, 망토를 두른 남자가 다시 모습을 드러냈습니다."]], [["“The terrible moment had come, but I had so long been prepared for it that my heart did not fail in the least.", "“그 끔찍한 순간이 다가왔습니다. 하지만 저는 오래도록 그 순간을 준비해 온 터라 가슴이 조금도 떨리지 않았습니다."], ["I drew my knife from my pocket again, opened it, and made ready to strike.", "저는 다시 주머니에서 칼을 꺼내 펼치고, 찌를 채비를 했습니다."], ["The man in the mantle advanced towards me, but as he drew near I saw that he had a weapon in his hand.", "망토를 두른 남자가 제 쪽으로 다가왔는데, 가까워지면서 보니 그의 손에 무기가 들려 있었습니다. 저는 두려웠습니다."], ["I was afraid, not of a struggle, but of a failure.", "다툼이 두려운 것이 아니라, 실패가 두려웠던 것이지요."], ["When he was only a few paces from me, I saw that what I had taken for a weapon was only a spade.", "그가 제게서 몇 걸음밖에 떨어지지 않았을 때, 제가 무기로 여겼던 것이 실은 삽에 지나지 않음을 알았습니다."], ["I was still unable to divine for what reason M. de Villefort had this spade in his hands, when he stopped close to the thicket where I was, glanced round, and began to dig a hole in the earth.", "빌포르 씨가 왜 그 삽을 손에 들고 있는지 여전히 짐작하지 못하고 있는데, 그가 제가 숨은 덤불 바로 곁에 멈춰 서서 사방을 둘러보더니 땅에 구덩이를 파기 시작했습니다."], ["I then perceived that he was hiding something under his mantle, which he laid on the grass in order to dig more freely.", "그제야 저는 그가 망토 밑에 무언가를 감추고 있다는 것을 알아챘습니다. 그는 더 편히 파려고 그것을 잔디 위에 내려놓았지요."], ["Then, I confess, curiosity mingled with hatred; I wished to see what Villefort was going to do there, and I remained motionless, holding my breath.", "그러자, 고백하건대, 증오에 호기심이 뒤섞였습니다. 빌포르가 거기서 무엇을 하려는지 보고 싶어진 저는 숨을 죽인 채 꼼짝 않고 있었습니다."], ["Then an idea crossed my mind, which was confirmed when I saw the procureur lift from under his mantle a box, two feet long, and six or eight inches deep.", "그때 한 가지 생각이 머릿속을 스쳤고, 그 검사가 망토 밑에서 길이 두 자에 깊이가 예닐곱 치쯤 되는 상자를 들어 올리는 것을 보자 그 생각이 확실해졌습니다."], ["I let him place the box in the hole he had made, then, while he stamped with his feet to remove all traces of his occupation, I rushed on him and plunged my knife into his breast, exclaiming:", "저는 그가 파 놓은 구덩이에 상자를 내려놓도록 내버려 두었습니다. 그런 다음, 그가 제 작업의 흔적을 모조리 없애려고 발로 흙을 다지는 사이, 저는 그에게 달려들어 가슴에 칼을 깊숙이 꽂으며 외쳤습니다."]], [["“‘I am Giovanni Bertuccio; thy death for my brother’s; thy treasure for his widow; thou seest that my vengeance is more complete than I had hoped.’", "“‘나는 조반니 베르투치오다. 내 형의 목숨값으로 네 목숨을, 그의 미망인을 위해 네 보물을 가져가겠다. 보아라, 내 복수는 내가 바라던 것보다도 완벽하구나.’"]], [["“I know not if he heard these words; I think he did not, for he fell without a cry.", "“그가 이 말을 들었는지는 모르겠습니다. 못 들었으리라 생각합니다. 비명 한마디 없이 쓰러졌으니까요."], ["I felt his blood gush over my face, but I was intoxicated, I was delirious, and the blood refreshed, instead of burning me.", "그의 피가 제 얼굴 위로 뿜어져 오르는 것이 느껴졌지만, 저는 취해 있었고, 광란에 빠져 있었으며, 그 피는 저를 태우기는커녕 오히려 시원하게 식혀 주었습니다."], ["In a second I had disinterred the box; then, that it might not be known I had done so, I filled up the hole, threw the spade over the wall, and rushed through the door, which I double-locked, carrying off the key.”", "순식간에 저는 상자를 파냈습니다. 그런 다음 제가 그렇게 한 것을 들키지 않으려고 구덩이를 도로 메우고, 삽을 담장 너머로 던져 버린 뒤, 쪽문으로 빠져나가 이중으로 잠그고 열쇠를 가지고 갔습니다.”"]], [["“Ah,”", "“흠,”"], ["said Monte Cristo “it seems to me this was nothing but murder and robbery.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. “내가 보기엔 살인에 강도질이 더해진 것에 지나지 않는 것 같은데.”"]], [["“No, your excellency,”", "“아닙니다, 백작님.”"], ["returned Bertuccio; “it was a vendetta followed by restitution.”", "베르투치오가 대답했다. “방데타에 뒤이은 반환이었습니다.”"]], [["“And was the sum a large one?”", "“그래, 그 액수가 컸나?”"]], [["“It was not money.”", "“돈이 아니었습니다.”"]], [["“Ah, I recollect,”", "“아, 생각나는군.”"], ["replied the count; “did you not say something of an infant?”", "백작이 대답했다. “자네가 갓난아이에 대해 무슨 말을 하지 않았나?”"]], [["“Yes, excellency; I hastened to the river, sat down on the bank, and with my knife forced open the lock of the box.", "“네, 백작님. 저는 강가로 서둘러 가서 둑에 앉아, 칼로 상자의 자물쇠를 비틀어 열었습니다."], ["In a fine linen cloth was wrapped a new-born child.", "고운 아마포 안에 갓 태어난 아기가 싸여 있었습니다."], ["Its purple visage, and its violet-colored hands showed that it had perished from suffocation, but as it was not yet cold, I hesitated to throw it into the water that ran at my feet.", "시퍼런 얼굴과 보랏빛 손은 그 아기가 질식해 죽었음을 말해 주고 있었지만, 아직 차갑지 않았던 까닭에 저는 발치에 흐르는 물속으로 그것을 던져 버리기를 망설였습니다."], ["After a moment I fancied that I felt a slight pulsation of the heart, and as I had been assistant at the hospital at Bastia, I did what a doctor would have done—I inflated the lungs by blowing air into them, and at the expiration of a quarter of an hour, it began to breathe, and cried feebly.", "잠시 후 저는 심장이 희미하게 뛰는 것을 느낀 것만 같았고, 바스티아 병원에서 조수로 일한 적이 있던 터라 의사라면 했을 법한 일을 했습니다. 입으로 공기를 불어넣어 폐를 부풀린 것이지요. 그러자 십오 분쯤 지나 아기는 숨을 쉬기 시작했고, 가냘프게 울음을 터뜨렸습니다."], ["In my turn I uttered a cry, but a cry of joy.", "이번에는 제가 비명을 질렀습니다. 그러나 기쁨의 비명이었지요."]], [["“‘God has not cursed me then,’ I cried, ‘since he permits me to save the life of a human creature, in exchange for the life I have taken away.’” 20295m", "“‘그렇다면 하느님께서 저를 저주하신 것이 아니로구나.’ 저는 외쳤습니다. ‘제가 빼앗은 목숨 대신 한 인간의 목숨을 구하도록 허락하셨으니.’”"]], [["“And what did you do with the child?”", "“그래, 그 아이는 어떻게 했나?”"], ["asked Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 물었다."], ["“It was an embarrassing load for a man seeking to escape.”", "“도망치려는 사람에게는 거추장스러운 짐이었을 텐데.”"]], [["“I had not for a moment the idea of keeping it, but I knew that at Paris there was an asylum where they receive such creatures.", "“그 아이를 데리고 있을 생각은 한순간도 하지 않았습니다. 다만 파리에 그런 아이들을 받아 주는 보호소가 있다는 것을 알고 있었지요."], ["As I passed the city gates I declared that I had found the child on the road, and I inquired where the asylum was; the box confirmed my statement, the linen proved that the infant belonged to wealthy parents, the blood with which I was covered might have proceeded from the child as well as from anyone else.", "저는 성문을 지나며 길에서 아이를 주웠다고 말하고, 그 보호소가 어디인지 물었습니다. 상자가 제 말을 뒷받침해 주었고, 아마포는 아기가 부유한 부모의 자식임을 입증해 주었으며, 제 몸을 뒤덮은 피는 다른 누구의 것이 아니라 아기의 것일 수도 있었습니다."], ["No objection was raised, but they pointed out the asylum, which was situated at the upper end of the Rue d’Enfer, and after having taken the precaution of cutting the linen in two pieces, so that one of the two letters which marked it was on the piece wrapped around the child, while the other remained in my possession, I rang the bell, and fled with all speed.", "아무도 이의를 달지 않았고, 그저 앙페르가 위쪽 끝에 있는 보호소를 알려 주었습니다. 그리고 저는 아마포를 둘로 잘라, 거기 새겨진 두 글자 중 하나는 아기를 감싼 천에 남고 다른 하나는 제 손에 남도록 미리 조처해 둔 다음, 종을 울리고 있는 힘껏 달아났습니다."], ["A fortnight after I was at Rogliano, and I said to Assunta:", "보름 뒤 저는 로글리아노에 있었고, 아순타에게 말했습니다."]], [["“‘Console thyself, sister; Israel is dead, but he is avenged.’", "“‘마음을 추스르시오, 형수. 이스라엘은 죽었지만, 원수는 갚았소.’"]], [["“She demanded what I meant, and when I had told her all,—‘Giovanni,’ said she, ‘you should have brought this child with you; we would have replaced the parents it has lost, have called it Benedetto, and then, in consequence of this good action, God would have blessed us.’ In reply I gave her the half of the linen I had kept in order to reclaim him if we became rich.”", "“형수가 무슨 뜻이냐고 물었고, 제가 모든 것을 이야기하자, ‘조반니,’ 형수가 말했습니다. ‘그 아이를 데려왔어야지요. 그 아이가 잃은 부모를 우리가 대신해 주고, 베네데토라 이름 짓고, 그 선행 덕분에 하느님께서 우리를 축복해 주셨을 텐데요.’ 저는 대답 대신, 우리가 부유해지면 그 아이를 도로 찾을 수 있도록 간직해 둔 아마포 반쪽을 형수에게 건넸습니다.”"]], [["“What letters were marked on the linen?”", "“그 아마포에는 무슨 글자가 새겨져 있었나?”"], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“An H and an N, surmounted by a baron’s coronet.”", "“H와 N이었습니다. 그 위에 남작의 관(冠)이 얹혀 있었지요.”"]], [["“By heaven, M. Bertuccio, you make use of heraldic terms; where did you study heraldry?”", "“이런, 베르투치오, 자네 문장학 용어를 쓰는군. 문장학은 어디서 배웠나?”"]], [["“In your service, excellency, where everything is learned.”", "“백작님 밑에서 배웠습니다. 거기서는 무엇이든 배우게 되니까요.”"]], [["“Go on, I am curious to know two things.”", "“계속하게. 나는 두 가지가 궁금하군.”"]], [["“What are they, your excellency?”", "“그게 무엇입니까, 백작님?”"]], [["“What became of this little boy?", "“그 사내아이는 어떻게 되었나?"], ["for I think you told me it was a boy, M. Bertuccio.”", "자네가 사내아이라고 말한 것 같은데, 베르투치오.”"]], [["“No excellency, I do not recollect telling you that.”", "“아닙니다, 백작님. 제가 그렇게 말씀드린 기억이 없습니다.”"]], [["“I thought you did; I must have been mistaken.”", "“그런 줄 알았는데. 내가 잘못 알았나 보군.”"]], [["“No, you were not, for it was in reality a little boy.", "“아니요, 잘못 아신 게 아닙니다. 실제로 사내아이였으니까요."], ["But your excellency wished to know two things; what was the second?”", "그런데 백작님께서 두 가지가 궁금하다고 하셨지요. 두 번째는 무엇입니까?”"]], [["“The second was the crime of which you were accused when you asked for a confessor, and the Abbé Busoni came to visit you at your request in the prison at Nîmes.”", "“두 번째는, 자네가 고해 신부를 청해 부소니 신부가 자네의 요청대로 님 감옥으로 자네를 찾아왔을 때, 자네가 받고 있던 그 죄목일세.”"]], [["“The story will be very long, excellency.”", "“그 이야기는 무척 길 것입니다, 백작님.”"]], [["“What matter? you know I take but little sleep, and I do not suppose you are very much inclined for it either.”", "“그게 무슨 상관인가? 알다시피 나는 잠이 별로 없고, 자네도 그다지 잠이 오는 것 같지는 않으니 말일세.”"], ["Bertuccio bowed, and resumed his story.", "베르투치오는 고개를 숙이고 다시 이야기를 시작했다."]], [["“Partly to drown the recollections of the past that haunted me, partly to supply the wants of the poor widow, I eagerly returned to my trade of smuggler, which had become more easy since that relaxation of the laws which always follows a revolution.", "“한편으로는 저를 따라다니는 지난날의 기억을 지우려고, 또 한편으로는 가난한 미망인의 필요를 채우려고, 저는 다시 열심히 밀수업으로 돌아갔습니다. 혁명 뒤에는 으레 법이 느슨해지게 마련이라 그 일도 한결 수월해져 있었지요."], ["The southern districts were ill-watched in particular, in consequence of the disturbances that were perpetually breaking out in Avignon, Nîmes, or Uzès.", "특히 남부 지방은 아비뇽이며 님이며 위제스에서 끊임없이 소요가 터지는 통에 단속이 허술했습니다."], ["We profited by this respite on the part of the government to make friends everywhere.", "우리는 정부의 이 한숨 돌릴 틈을 틈타 곳곳에 친구를 만들어 두었습니다."], ["Since my brother’s assassination in the streets of Nîmes, I had never entered the town; the result was that the innkeeper with whom we were connected, seeing that we would no longer come to him, was forced to come to us, and had established a branch to his inn, on the road from Bellegarde to Beaucaire, at the sign of the Pont du Gard.", "형이 님 거리에서 살해당한 뒤로 저는 한 번도 그 도시에 발을 들이지 않았습니다. 그 결과, 우리와 거래하던 그 여관 주인은 우리가 더는 자기를 찾아오지 않는 것을 보고 어쩔 수 없이 우리 쪽으로 와야 했고, 벨가르드에서 보케르로 가는 길에 ‘퐁뒤가르’라는 간판을 걸고 분점을 차렸습니다."], ["We had thus, at Aigues-Mortes, Martigues, or Bouc, a dozen places where we left our goods, and where, in case of necessity, we concealed ourselves from the gendarmes and custom-house officers.", "그렇게 해서 우리는 에그모르트며 마르티그며 부크에, 물건을 맡겨 두는 곳을, 그리고 필요할 때 헌병이나 세관원들을 피해 숨는 곳을 열두 군데쯤 두게 되었습니다."], ["Smuggling is a profitable trade, when a certain degree of vigor and intelligence is employed; as for myself, brought up in the mountains, I had a double motive for fearing the gendarmes and custom-house officers, as my appearance before the judges would cause an inquiry, and an inquiry always looks back into the past.", "밀수란 어느 정도 담력과 영리함을 발휘하면 짭짤한 장사입니다. 저로 말하자면, 산속에서 자란 몸이라 헌병이며 세관원을 두려워할 이유가 두 배였습니다. 제가 판사 앞에 서게 되면 조사가 시작될 테고, 조사란 언제나 과거를 캐는 법이니까요."], ["And in my past life they might find something far more grave than the selling of smuggled cigars, or barrels of brandy without a permit.", "그리고 제 지난 삶 속에서는 밀수 담배나 허가 없는 브랜디 통을 판 것보다 훨씬 더 무거운 무언가가 드러날 수도 있었습니다."], ["So, preferring death to capture, I accomplished the most astonishing deeds, and which, more than once, showed me that the too great care we take of our bodies is the only obstacle to the success of those projects which require rapid decision, and vigorous and determined execution.", "그래서 저는 붙잡히느니 죽음을 택하기로 하고, 참으로 놀라운 일들을 해냈습니다. 그러면서 한두 번이 아니라 거듭 깨달았지요. 제 몸을 지나치게 아끼는 것이야말로, 빠른 결단과 힘차고 단호한 실행을 요하는 그런 계획들의 성공을 가로막는 유일한 걸림돌이라는 것을요."], ["In reality, when you have once devoted your life to your enterprises, you are no longer the equal of other men, or, rather, other men are no longer your equals, and whosoever has taken this resolution, feels his strength and resources doubled.”", "사실 일단 자기 사업에 목숨을 내건 사람은 더 이상 다른 사람들과 대등하지 않습니다. 아니, 오히려 다른 사람들이 더 이상 그와 대등하지 못하게 되지요. 그런 결심을 한 자는 누구나 자신의 힘과 수완이 두 배로 늘어나는 것을 느끼게 됩니다.”"]], [["“Philosophy, M. Bertuccio,”", "“철학이로군, 베르투치오.”"], ["interrupted the count; “you have done a little of everything in your life.”", "백작이 말을 끊었다. “자네는 살면서 안 해 본 일이 없구먼.”"]], [["“Oh, excellency!”", "“오, 백작님!”"]], [["“No, no; but philosophy at half-past ten at night is somewhat late; yet I have no other observation to make, for what you say is correct, which is more than can be said for all philosophy.”", "“아니, 아닐세. 다만 밤 열 시 반에 철학이라니 좀 늦은 감이 있군. 그래도 달리 할 말은 없네. 자네 말이 옳으니까. 그건 그 어떤 철학에 대해서도 하기 어려운 칭찬이지.”"]], [["“My journeys became more and more extensive and more productive.", "“제 항해는 점점 더 멀어지고 점점 더 짭짤해졌습니다."], ["Assunta took care of all, and our little fortune increased.", "아순타가 모든 것을 챙겼고, 우리의 작은 재산은 불어났습니다."], ["One day as I was setting off on an expedition, ‘Go,’ said she; ‘at your return I will give you a surprise.’ I questioned her, but in vain; she would tell me nothing, and I departed.", "어느 날 제가 한 차례 원정을 떠나려는데, ‘다녀오세요.’ 형수가 말했습니다. ‘돌아오시면 깜짝 놀랄 일을 보여 드릴게요.’ 제가 캐물었지만 소용없었습니다. 형수는 아무것도 말해 주지 않았고, 저는 떠났습니다."], ["Our expedition lasted nearly six weeks; we had been to Lucca to take in oil, to Leghorn for English cottons, and we ran our cargo without opposition, and returned home full of joy.", "그 원정은 거의 여섯 주가 걸렸습니다. 우리는 루카로 가서 기름을 싣고, 리보르노로 가서 영국산 면직물을 실었으며, 아무 방해 없이 화물을 부리고는 기쁨에 차서 집으로 돌아왔습니다."], ["When I entered the house, the first thing I beheld in the middle of Assunta’s chamber was a cradle that might be called sumptuous compared with the rest of the furniture, and in it a baby seven or eight months old.", "집에 들어서자 제일 먼저 눈에 들어온 것은, 아순타의 방 한가운데 놓인 요람이었습니다. 나머지 가구에 견주면 호화롭다 할 만한 요람이었고, 그 안에 일고여덟 달 된 아기가 있었습니다."], ["I uttered a cry of joy; the only moments of sadness I had known since the assassination of the procureur were caused by the recollection that I had abandoned this child.", "저는 기쁨의 탄성을 질렀습니다. 그 검사를 죽인 뒤로 제가 겪은 슬픔의 순간이라고는, 그 아이를 버렸다는 기억에서 비롯된 것뿐이었으니까요."], ["For the assassination itself I had never felt any remorse.", "살인 그 자체에 대해서는 한 번도 가책을 느낀 적이 없었습니다."], ["Poor Assunta had guessed all. She had profited by my absence, and furnished with the half of the linen, and having written down the day and hour at which I had deposited the child at the asylum, had set off for Paris, and had reclaimed it.", "가엾은 아순타는 모든 것을 짐작하고 있었습니다. 형수는 제가 없는 틈을 타, 제가 간직해 둔 아마포 반쪽을 챙기고, 제가 아이를 보호소에 맡긴 날짜와 시각을 적어 둔 다음, 파리로 떠나 그 아이를 도로 찾아온 것이었습니다."], ["No objection was raised, and the infant was given up to her.", "아무도 이의를 달지 않았고, 아기는 형수에게 넘겨졌습니다."], ["Ah, I confess, your excellency, when I saw this poor creature sleeping peacefully in its cradle, I felt my eyes filled with tears.", "아, 고백하건대, 백작님, 그 가엾은 것이 요람 속에서 평화롭게 잠든 것을 보았을 때, 저는 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁 차오르는 것을 느꼈습니다."], ["‘Ah, Assunta,’ cried I, ‘you are an excellent woman, and Heaven will bless you.’”", "‘아, 아순타,’ 저는 외쳤습니다. ‘당신은 훌륭한 여자요. 하늘이 당신을 축복할 거요.’”"]], [["“This,”", "“그건,”"], ["said Monte Cristo, “is less correct than your philosophy,—it is only faith.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. “자네 철학보다 덜 정확하군. 그저 믿음일 뿐이야.”"]], [["“Alas, your excellency is right,”", "“아아, 백작님 말씀이 옳습니다.”"], ["replied Bertuccio, “and God made this infant the instrument of our punishment.", "베르투치오가 대답했다. “하느님은 그 아이를 우리를 벌하는 도구로 삼으셨으니까요."], ["Never did a perverse nature declare itself more prematurely, and yet it was not owing to any fault in his bringing up.", "그토록 일찍부터 비뚤어진 천성을 드러낸 아이는 일찍이 없었습니다. 그렇다고 양육에 무슨 잘못이 있었던 것도 아니었지요."], ["He was a most lovely child, with large blue eyes, of that deep color that harmonizes so well with the blond complexion; only his hair, which was too light, gave his face a most singular expression, and added to the vivacity of his look, and the malice of his smile.", "그 아이는 더없이 사랑스러운 아이였습니다. 큰 푸른 눈은 금발의 살결과 그렇게도 잘 어울리는 깊은 빛깔이었습니다. 다만 머리카락만은 지나치게 옅어서, 그 얼굴에 무척이나 묘한 인상을 더해 주었고, 눈빛의 생기와 미소에 깃든 악의를 한층 돋보이게 했습니다."]], [["“Unfortunately, there is a proverb which says that ‘red is either altogether good or altogether bad.’ The proverb was but too correct as regarded Benedetto, and even in his infancy he manifested the worst disposition.", "“불행히도, ‘붉은 것은 더없이 좋거나 더없이 나쁘거나 둘 중 하나다’라는 속담이 있습니다. 그 속담은 베네데토에게는 너무도 잘 들어맞았고, 어린 시절부터 그 아이는 가장 고약한 성품을 드러냈습니다."], ["It is true that the indulgence of his foster-mother encouraged him.", "물론 양어머니의 응석받이가 그것을 부추긴 것은 사실입니다."], ["This child, for whom my poor sister would go to the town, five or six leagues off, to purchase the earliest fruits and the most tempting sweetmeats, preferred to Palma grapes or Genoese preserves, the chestnuts stolen from a neighbor’s orchard, or the dried apples in his loft, when he could eat as well of the nuts and apples that grew in my garden.", "제 가엾은 형수가 그 아이를 위해 대여섯 리외나 떨어진 도시까지 가서 가장 이른 과일과 가장 군침 도는 사탕을 사다 주었건만, 그 아이는 팔마 포도나 제노바산 설탕절임보다도 이웃집 과수원에서 훔친 밤이나 다락의 말린 사과를 더 좋아했습니다. 제 정원에서 자란 호두며 사과를 얼마든지 먹을 수 있었는데도 말이지요."]], [["“One day, when Benedetto was about five or six, our neighbor Wasilio, who, according to the custom of the country, never locked up his purse or his valuables—for, as your excellency knows, there are no thieves in Corsica—complained that he had lost a louis out of his purse; we thought he must have made a mistake in counting his money, but he persisted in the accuracy of his statement.", "“베네데토가 대여섯 살쯤 되던 어느 날, 이웃 와실리오가 — 그는 그 고장 풍습대로 지갑이나 귀중품을 결코 잠가 두는 법이 없었습니다. 백작님께서도 아시다시피 코르시카에는 도둑이 없으니까요 — 지갑에서 1루이가 없어졌다고 하소연했습니다. 우리는 그가 돈을 세다가 착각한 것이려니 했지만, 그는 자기 말이 정확하다고 우겼습니다."], ["One day, Benedetto, who had been gone from the house since morning, to our great anxiety, did not return until late in the evening, dragging a monkey after him, which he said he had found chained to the foot of a tree.", "어느 날, 아침부터 집을 나가 우리를 몹시 애태우던 베네데토가 저녁 늦게야 돌아왔는데, 원숭이 한 마리를 끌고 왔습니다. 나무 밑동에 사슬로 묶여 있던 것을 발견했다는 것이었습니다."], ["For more than a month past, the mischievous child, who knew not what to wish for, had taken it into his head to have a monkey.", "한 달이 넘도록 그 개구쟁이는 무엇을 갖고 싶은지도 모르면서 원숭이를 갖고 싶다는 생각에 사로잡혀 있었습니다."], ["A boatman, who had passed by Rogliano, and who had several of these animals, whose tricks had greatly diverted him, had, doubtless, suggested this idea to him.", "로글리아노를 지나가던 어느 뱃사람이 그런 짐승을 여러 마리 데리고 있었는데, 그 재주가 아이를 무척 즐겁게 해 준 모양이었으니, 분명 거기서 그런 생각이 싹텄을 것입니다."], ["‘Monkeys are not found in our woods chained to trees,’ said I; ‘confess how you obtained this animal.’ Benedetto maintained the truth of what he had said, and accompanied it with details that did more honor to his imagination than to his veracity.", "‘우리 숲에는 나무에 사슬로 묶인 원숭이 따위는 없다.’ 제가 말했습니다. ‘이 짐승을 어떻게 손에 넣었는지 바른대로 말해 봐라.’ 베네데토는 자기 말이 사실이라고 우기며, 그 진실성보다는 상상력에 더 어울리는 자질구레한 이야기를 덧붙였습니다. 저는 화가 났습니다."], ["I became angry; he began to laugh, I threatened to strike him, and he made two steps backwards.", "그러자 아이가 웃기 시작했고, 제가 때리겠다고 으름장을 놓자 두 걸음 뒤로 물러섰습니다."], ["‘You cannot beat me,’ said he; ‘you have no right, for you are not my father.’\n20299m", "‘날 때릴 수는 없어.’ 아이가 말했습니다. ‘그럴 권리가 없잖아. 당신은 내 아버지가 아니니까.’"]], [["“We never knew who had revealed this fatal secret, which we had so carefully concealed from him; however, it was this answer, in which the child’s whole character revealed itself, that almost terrified me, and my arm fell without touching him.", "“우리가 그토록 조심스레 감춰 온 그 끔찍한 비밀을 누가 그 아이에게 일러 주었는지는 끝내 알 수 없었습니다. 어쨌든, 그 아이의 본성이 통째로 드러난 이 대꾸가 저를 거의 질리게 만들었고, 제 팔은 그를 건드리지도 못한 채 툭 떨어지고 말았습니다."]], [["“The boy triumphed, and this victory rendered him so audacious, that all the money of Assunta, whose affection for him seemed to increase as he became more unworthy of it, was spent in caprices she knew not how to contend against, and follies she had not the courage to prevent.", "“아이가 의기양양해졌고, 이 승리가 그를 어찌나 대담하게 만들었던지, 그 아이가 그럴 자격이 없어질수록 그를 향한 애정은 오히려 깊어만 가는 듯하던 아순타의 돈은, 그녀가 도무지 맞설 줄 모르는 변덕과, 막을 용기를 내지 못하는 어리석은 짓들에 죄다 탕진되었습니다."], ["When I was at Rogliano everything went on properly, but no sooner was my back turned than Benedetto became master, and everything went ill.", "제가 로글리아노에 있을 때는 모든 것이 제대로 굴러갔지만, 제가 등을 돌리기가 무섭게 베네데토가 주인 노릇을 하며 모든 것이 엉망이 되었습니다."], ["When he was only eleven, he chose his companions from among the young men of eighteen or twenty, the worst characters in Bastia, or, indeed, in Corsica, and they had already, for some mischievous pranks, been several times threatened with a prosecution.", "겨우 열한 살 때 그 아이는 열여덟이나 스무 살 된 젊은 패거리 중에서 동무를 골랐는데, 바스티아에서, 아니 코르시카 전체에서 가장 악질인 자들이었습니다. 그들은 벌써 몇몇 못된 장난 때문에 여러 차례 기소 위협을 받은 처지였지요."], ["I became alarmed, as any prosecution might be attended with serious consequences.", "저는 겁이 났습니다. 어떤 기소든 심각한 결과를 불러올 수 있었으니까요."], ["I was compelled, at this period, to leave Corsica on an important expedition; I reflected for a long time, and with the hope of averting some impending misfortune, I resolved that Benedetto should accompany me.", "마침 그 무렵 저는 중요한 원정 때문에 코르시카를 떠나야 했습니다. 저는 오랫동안 곰곰이 생각한 끝에, 닥쳐오는 어떤 불행을 막아 보려는 바람으로, 베네데토를 데려가기로 마음먹었습니다."]], [["“I hoped that the active and laborious life of a smuggler, with the severe discipline on board, would have a salutary effect on his character, which was now well-nigh, if not quite, corrupt.", "“저는 밀수꾼의 활기차고 고된 생활과 배 위의 엄격한 규율이, 이제 거의 — 완전히는 아니더라도 — 망가져 가는 그 아이의 성품에 좋은 영향을 주리라 기대했습니다."], ["I spoke to Benedetto alone, and proposed to him to accompany me, endeavoring to tempt him by all the promises most likely to dazzle the imagination of a child of twelve.", "저는 베네데토와 단둘이 이야기하며 함께 가자고 권했고, 열두 살 아이의 상상력을 현혹할 만한 온갖 약속으로 그를 꾀어 보려 했습니다."], ["He heard me patiently, and when I had finished, burst out laughing.", "아이는 참을성 있게 듣더니, 제 말이 끝나자 웃음을 터뜨렸습니다."]], [["“‘Are you mad, uncle?’ (he called me by this name when he was in good humor); ‘do you think I am going to change the life I lead for your mode of existence—my agreeable indolence for the hard and precarious toil you impose on yourself, exposed to the bitter frost at night, and the scorching heat by day, compelled to conceal yourself, and when you are perceived, receive a volley of bullets, all to earn a paltry sum?", "“‘삼촌, 미쳤어요?’ (기분이 좋을 때면 그 아이는 저를 이렇게 불렀습니다.) ‘내가 지금 누리는 삶을 삼촌의 삶과 바꿀 줄 알아요? 이 편안한 게으름을, 밤이면 살을 에는 서리에, 낮이면 타는 듯한 더위에 시달리고, 몸을 숨겨야 하고, 들키면 총알 세례를 받아 가며, 그 보잘것없는 푼돈을 벌려고 삼촌이 스스로에게 지우는 그 고되고 위태로운 노역과요?"], ["Why, I have as much money as I want; mother Assunta always furnishes me when I ask for it!", "아니, 나는 갖고 싶은 만큼 돈이 있는걸요. 아순타 엄마는 내가 달라고 하면 언제든 줘요!"], ["You see that I should be a fool to accept your offer.’", "보세요, 삼촌 제안을 받아들이면 내가 바보지.’"]], [["“The arguments, and his audacity, perfectly stupefied me.", "“그 말들이, 그리고 그 뻔뻔함이 저를 완전히 얼어붙게 했습니다."], ["Benedetto rejoined his associates, and I saw him from a distance point me out to them as a fool.”", "베네데토는 제 패거리에게 돌아갔고, 저는 그가 멀리서 저를 바보라고 가리켜 보이는 것을 보았습니다.”"]], [["“Sweet child,” murmured Monte Cristo.", "“참으로 귀여운 아이로군.” 몬테크리스토가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Oh, had he been my own son,”", "“오, 그 아이가 제 친아들이었거나,”"], ["replied Bertuccio, “or even my nephew, I would have brought him back to the right road, for the knowledge that you are doing your duty gives you strength, but the idea that I was striking a child whose father I had killed, made it impossible for me to punish him.", "베르투치오가 대답했다. “하다못해 친조카이기만 했어도, 저는 그를 바른길로 되돌려 놓았을 겁니다. 자기가 마땅한 도리를 다하고 있다는 자각은 사람에게 힘을 주니까요."], ["I gave my sister, who constantly defended the unfortunate boy, good advice, and as she confessed that she had several times missed money to a considerable amount, I showed her a safe place in which to conceal our little treasure for the future.", "하지만 제가 그 아비를 죽인 아이를 때리고 있다는 생각이, 그를 도무지 벌할 수 없게 만들었습니다. 저는 그 가엾은 아이를 줄곧 감싸고도는 형수에게 좋은 충고를 해 주었습니다. 그리고 형수가 여러 번 적잖은 돈이 없어진 적이 있다고 털어놓기에, 앞으로 우리의 작은 재산을 숨겨 둘 안전한 곳을 일러 주었습니다."], ["My mind was already made up.", "제 마음은 이미 정해져 있었습니다."], ["Benedetto could read, write, and cipher perfectly, for when the fit seized him, he learned more in a day than others in a week.", "베네데토는 읽기와 쓰기, 셈을 완벽하게 할 줄 알았습니다. 마음이 동할 때면 남들이 일주일에 배울 것을 하루에 익히는 아이였으니까요."], ["My intention was to enter him as a clerk in some ship, and without letting him know anything of my plan, to convey him some morning on board; by this means his future treatment would depend upon his own conduct. I set off for France, after having fixed upon the plan.", "제 생각은 그를 어느 배에 사환으로 들여보내고, 제 계획은 전혀 눈치채지 못하게 한 채 어느 날 아침 배에 태워 버리는 것이었습니다. 그렇게 하면 앞으로 그가 어떤 대접을 받느냐는 오로지 제 행실에 달리게 되는 셈이었지요."], ["Our cargo was to be landed in the Gulf of Lyons, and this was a difficult thing to do because it was then the year 1829.", "저는 그 계획을 굳히고 나서 프랑스로 떠났습니다. 우리 화물은 리옹만에 부려야 했는데, 그것은 쉽지 않은 일이었습니다."], ["The most perfect tranquillity was restored, and the vigilance of the custom-house officers was redoubled, and their strictness was increased at this time, in consequence of the fair at Beaucaire. 20301m", "때가 1829년이었기 때문이지요. 더없는 평온이 회복되어 세관원들의 경계가 두 배로 강화되어 있었고, 마침 보케르 장이 열리는 통에 단속도 한층 엄해져 있었습니다."]], [["“Our expedition made a favorable beginning.", "“우리 원정은 순조롭게 출발했습니다."], ["We anchored our vessel—which had a double hold, where our goods were concealed—amidst a number of other vessels that bordered the banks of the Rhône from Beaucaire to Arles.", "우리는 물건을 숨겨 둔 이중 선창이 달린 배를, 보케르에서 아를까지 론강 기슭을 메운 수많은 다른 배들 틈에 정박시켰습니다."], ["On our arrival we began to discharge our cargo in the night, and to convey it into the town, by the help of the innkeeper with whom we were connected.", "도착하자 우리는 밤을 틈타 화물을 내리고, 우리와 거래하던 그 여관 주인의 도움을 받아 그것을 시내로 날랐습니다."]], [["“Whether success rendered us imprudent, or whether we were betrayed, I know not; but one evening, about five o’clock, our little cabin-boy came breathlessly, to inform us that he had seen a detachment of custom-house officers advancing in our direction.", "“성공에 우리가 방심한 탓인지, 아니면 누가 우리를 밀고한 탓인지 모르겠습니다만, 어느 저녁 다섯 시쯤, 어린 사환이 숨을 헐떡이며 달려와 세관원 일대(一隊)가 우리 쪽으로 다가오고 있다고 알렸습니다. 우리를 불안하게 한 것은 그들이 가까이 왔다는 사실이 아니었습니다."], ["It was not their proximity that alarmed us, for detachments were constantly patrolling along the banks of the Rhône, but the care, according to the boy’s account, that they took to avoid being seen.", "론강 기슭에는 늘 무리 지어 순찰을 도는 자들이 있었으니까요. 다만 사환의 말로는, 그들이 눈에 띄지 않으려고 무척 조심하더라는 것이었습니다. 순식간에 우리는 경계 태세에 들어갔지만, 이미 늦었습니다."], ["In an instant we were on the alert, but it was too late; our vessel was surrounded, and amongst the custom-house officers I observed several gendarmes, and, as terrified at the sight of their uniforms as I was brave at the sight of any other, I sprang into the hold, opened a port, and dropped into the river, dived, and only rose at intervals to breathe, until I reached a ditch that had recently been made from the Rhône to the canal that runs from Beaucaire to Aigues-Mortes.", "우리 배는 포위되었고, 세관원들 가운데 헌병도 몇 보였습니다. 다른 무엇 앞에서는 그토록 용감하던 제가 그 제복을 보자 겁에 질려, 저는 선창으로 뛰어들어 현창(舷窓)을 열고 강물 속으로 떨어졌습니다. 물속으로 잠수해, 숨을 쉬려고 이따금씩만 떠오르면서, 얼마 전 론강에서 보케르와 에그모르트를 잇는 운하로 새로 판 도랑에 이를 때까지 헤엄쳤습니다."], ["I was now safe, for I could swim along the ditch without being seen, and I reached the canal in safety.", "이제 저는 안전했습니다. 그 도랑을 따라가면 들키지 않고 헤엄칠 수 있었으니까요."], ["I had designedly taken this direction.", "저는 무사히 운하에 다다랐습니다."], ["I have already told your excellency of an innkeeper from Nîmes who had set up a little tavern on the road from Bellegarde to Beaucaire.”", "저는 일부러 그쪽 방향을 택한 것이었습니다. 제가 벨가르드에서 보케르로 가는 길에 작은 선술집을 차린 님 출신 여관 주인 이야기를 이미 백작님께 말씀드렸지요.”"]], [["“Yes,”", "“그래,”"], ["said Monte Cristo “I perfectly recollect him; I think he was your colleague.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. “그 사람은 똑똑히 기억나는군. 자네 동료였던 것 같은데.”"]], [["“Precisely,”", "“바로 그렇습니다.”"], ["answered Bertuccio; “but he had, seven or eight years before this period, sold his establishment to a tailor at Marseilles, who, having almost ruined himself in his old trade, wished to make his fortune in another.", "베르투치오가 대답했다. “하지만 그는 이 무렵보다 칠팔 년 전에 자기 가게를 마르세유의 어느 재단사에게 팔았습니다. 그 재단사는 본래 하던 일로 거의 빈털터리가 된 터라, 다른 일로 한밑천 잡아 보려던 사람이었지요."], ["Of course, we made the same arrangements with the new landlord that we had with the old; and it was of this man that I intended to ask shelter.”", "물론 우리는 새 주인과도 옛 주인과 맺었던 것과 똑같은 약조를 맺었습니다. 그리고 제가 몸을 숨길 곳을 청하려던 상대가 바로 이 사람이었습니다.”"]], [["“What was his name?”", "“그자의 이름이 무엇이었나?”"], ["inquired the count, who seemed to become somewhat interested in Bertuccio’s story.", "백작이 물었다. 그는 베르투치오의 이야기에 다소 흥미가 동한 듯했다."]], [["“Gaspard Caderousse; he had married a woman from the village of Carconte, and whom we did not know by any other name than that of her village.", "“가스파르 카드루스였습니다. 그는 카르콩트 마을 출신 여자와 결혼했는데, 우리는 그 여자를 그 마을 이름 말고는 달리 부를 줄을 몰랐습니다."], ["She was suffering from malarial fever, and seemed dying by inches.", "그 여자는 학질에 시달리며 조금씩 죽어 가는 듯했습니다."], ["As for her husband, he was a strapping fellow of forty, or five-and-forty, who had more than once, in time of danger, given ample proof of his presence of mind and courage.”", "남편으로 말하면, 마흔이나 마흔다섯쯤 된 건장한 사내로, 위험한 순간이면 한두 번이 아니라 거듭 침착함과 용기를 충분히 보여 준 자였습니다.”"]], [["“And you say,”", "“그래서 자네 말은,”"], ["interrupted Monte Cristo “that this took place towards the year——”", "몬테크리스토가 말을 끊었다. “이 일이 몇 년쯤에 일어났다고 했지?”"]], [["“1829, your excellency.”", "“1829년입니다, 백작님.”"]], [["“In what month?”", "“몇 월에?”"]], [["“The beginning or the end?”", "“초인가, 말인가?”"]], [["“The evening of the 3rd.”", "“3일 저녁이었습니다.”"]], [["20303m\n“Ah,”", "“아,”"], ["said Monte Cristo “the evening of the 3rd of June, 1829.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. “1829년 6월 3일 저녁이라."], ["Go on.”", "계속하게.”"]], [["“It was from Caderousse that I intended demanding shelter, and, as we never entered by the door that opened onto the road, I resolved not to break through the rule, so climbing over the garden-hedge, I crept amongst the olive and wild fig trees, and fearing that Caderousse might have some guest, I entered a kind of shed in which I had often passed the night, and which was only separated from the inn by a partition, in which holes had been made in order to enable us to watch an opportunity of announcing our presence.", "“제가 몸을 숨길 곳을 청하려던 상대가 바로 카드루스였습니다. 우리는 길로 난 문으로는 결코 들어가는 법이 없었으므로, 저는 그 규칙을 깨지 않기로 하고, 정원 울타리를 넘어 올리브나무와 야생 무화과나무 사이로 기어들었습니다. 그리고 카드루스에게 손님이 있을지도 모른다는 생각에, 제가 곧잘 밤을 지새우던 일종의 헛간으로 들어갔습니다. 그곳은 칸막이 하나로만 여관과 나뉘어 있었는데, 그 칸막이에는 우리가 안을 살피며 기척을 알릴 틈을 노릴 수 있도록 구멍이 뚫려 있었습니다."]], [["“My intention was, if Caderousse was alone, to acquaint him with my presence, finish the meal the custom-house officers had interrupted, and profit by the threatened storm to return to the Rhône, and ascertain the state of our vessel and its crew.", "“제 생각은, 카드루스가 혼자 있거든 그에게 제가 온 것을 알리고, 세관원들 때문에 중단됐던 식사를 마저 한 다음, 막 몰아칠 듯한 폭풍우를 틈타 론강으로 돌아가 우리 배와 선원들의 형편을 알아보자는 것이었습니다."], ["I stepped into the shed, and it was fortunate I did so, for at that moment Caderousse entered with a stranger.", "저는 헛간으로 들어섰는데, 마침 그러기를 잘한 것이, 바로 그 순간 카드루스가 웬 낯선 사람과 함께 들어왔기 때문입니다."]], [["“I waited patiently, not to overhear what they said, but because I could do nothing else; besides, the same thing had occurred often before.", "“저는 그들이 하는 말을 엿들으려고가 아니라, 달리 어쩔 도리가 없어서 참을성 있게 기다렸습니다. 게다가 그런 일은 전에도 자주 있었으니까요. 카드루스와 함께 온 사내는 분명 프랑스 남부 사람이 아니었습니다."], ["The man who was with Caderousse was evidently a stranger to the South of France; he was one of those merchants who come to sell jewellery at the Beaucaire fair, and who during the month the fair lasts, and during which there is so great an influx of merchants and customers from all parts of Europe, often have dealings to the amount of 100,000 to 150,000 francs.", "그는 보케르 장에 보석을 팔러 오는 상인들 가운데 하나로, 장이 서는 한 달 동안, 유럽 각지에서 상인과 손님이 그토록 밀려드는 그동안에, 흔히 십만에서 십오만 프랑어치 거래를 하는 자들이었습니다. 카드루스가 급히 들어왔습니다. 그러고는 방이 여느 때처럼 비어 있고 개 한 마리만 지키고 있는 것을 보자, 아내를 불렀습니다."], ["Caderousse entered hastily.", "‘여보, 카르콩트,’ 그가 말했습니다."], ["Then, seeing that the room was, as usual, empty, and only guarded by the dog, he called to his wife, ‘Hello, Carconte,’ said he, ‘the worthy priest has not deceived us; the diamond is real.’", "‘그 양반 신부님 말이 거짓이 아니었어. 그 다이아몬드는 진짜였다고.’"]], [["“An exclamation of joy was heard, and the staircase creaked beneath a feeble step. ‘What do you say?’ asked his wife, pale as death.", "“기쁨의 탄성이 들리더니, 계단이 가냘픈 발걸음 아래서 삐걱거렸습니다. ‘무슨 말이에요?’ 그의 아내가 죽은 사람처럼 창백한 얼굴로 물었습니다."]], [["“‘I say that the diamond is real, and that this gentleman, one of the first jewellers of Paris, will give us 50,000 francs for it.", "“‘이 다이아몬드가 진짜라고. 그리고 파리 일류 보석상인 이분이 이걸 5만 프랑에 사 주시겠다는 거야."], ["Only, in order to satisfy himself that it really belongs to us, he wishes you to relate to him, as I have done already, the miraculous manner in which the diamond came into our possession.", "다만, 이게 정말로 우리 것이 맞는지 확인하시려고, 이 다이아몬드가 어떻게 우리 손에 들어오게 됐는지 그 기적 같은 사연을, 내가 이미 말씀드린 대로, 당신도 들려 드렸으면 하셔."], ["In the meantime please to sit down, monsieur, and I will fetch you some refreshment.’", "그러니 그동안, 선생님, 부디 앉으시지요. 제가 마실 것 좀 가져오겠습니다.’"]], [["“The jeweller examined attentively the interior of the inn and the apparent poverty of the persons who were about to sell him a diamond that seemed to have come from the casket of a prince.", "“보석상은 여관 내부와, 왕가의 보석함에서나 나왔을 법한 다이아몬드를 팔려는 이 사람들의 한눈에 보이는 가난을 유심히 살펴보았습니다."]], [["“‘Relate your story, madame,’ said he, wishing, no doubt, to profit by the absence of the husband, so that the latter could not influence the wife’s story, to see if the two recitals tallied.", "“‘부인, 사연을 들려주시지요.’ 그가 말했습니다. 분명 남편이 없는 틈을 타, 남편이 아내의 이야기에 영향을 주지 못하게 해서, 두 사람의 이야기가 들어맞는지 보려는 속셈이었지요."]], [["“‘Oh,’ returned she, ‘it was a gift of heaven.", "“‘오,’ 여자가 대답했습니다. ‘그건 하늘이 내린 선물이었답니다."], ["My husband was a great friend, in 1814 or 1815, of a sailor named Edmond Dantès.", "제 남편은 1814년인가 1815년인가, 에드몽 당테스라는 뱃사람과 둘도 없는 친구였어요."], ["This poor fellow, whom Caderousse had forgotten, had not forgotten him, and at his death he bequeathed this diamond to him.’", "카드루스가 까맣게 잊고 지내던 그 가엾은 사람은 정작 남편을 잊지 않았고, 죽으면서 이 다이아몬드를 남편에게 남겼지요.’"]], [["“‘But how did he obtain it?’ asked the jeweller; ‘had he it before he was imprisoned?’", "“‘그런데 그 사람은 이걸 어떻게 손에 넣었답니까?’ 보석상이 물었습니다. ‘투옥되기 전부터 가지고 있었나요?’"]], [["“‘No, monsieur; but it appears that in prison he made the acquaintance of a rich Englishman, and as in prison he fell sick, and Dantès took the same care of him as if he had been his brother, the Englishman, when he was set free, gave this stone to Dantès, who, less fortunate, died, and, in his turn, left it to us, and charged the excellent abbé, who was here this morning, to deliver it.’", "“‘아니요, 선생님. 하지만 감옥에서 어느 부유한 영국 사람과 알게 된 모양이에요. 거기서 그 영국 사람이 병이 들었는데, 당테스가 친형제처럼 간호해 주었답니다. 그래서 그 영국 사람이 풀려나면서 이 보석을 당테스에게 주었고, 운이 덜 좋았던 당테스는 죽으면서 다시 우리에게 남기고, 오늘 아침 여기 왔던 그 훌륭한 신부님께 전해 달라고 부탁한 거지요.’"]], [["“‘The same story,’ muttered the jeweller; ‘and improbable as it seemed at first, it may be true.", "“‘똑같은 이야기로군.’ 보석상이 중얼거렸습니다. ‘처음엔 그럴듯하지 않게 들렸지만, 사실일 수도 있겠어."], ["There’s only the price we are not agreed about.’", "다만 값만큼은 우리가 아직 합의를 못 봤지.’"]], [["“‘How not agreed about?’ said Caderousse.", "“‘합의를 못 보다니요?’ 카드루스가 말했습니다."], ["‘I thought we agreed for the price I asked.’", "‘내가 부른 값에 합의한 줄 알았는데요.’"]], [["“‘That is,’ replied the jeweller, ‘I offered 40,000 francs.’", "“‘그러니까,’ 보석상이 대답했습니다. ‘나는 4만 프랑을 불렀던 거요.’"]], [["‘Forty thousand,’ cried La Carconte; ‘we will not part with it for that sum.", "“‘4만이라니,’ 카르콩트가 소리쳤습니다. ‘그 값에는 못 내놔요."], ["The abbé told us it was worth 50,000 without the setting.’", "신부님이 그러는데, 받침을 빼고도 5만 프랑은 나간다고 했단 말이에요.’"]], [["“‘What was the abbé’s name?’ asked the indefatigable questioner.", "“‘그 신부 이름이 무엇이었소?’ 지칠 줄 모르는 그 질문꾼이 물었습니다."]], [["“‘The Abbé Busoni,’ said La Carconte.", "“‘부소니 신부님이오.’ 카르콩트가 말했습니다."]], [["“‘He was a foreigner?’", "“‘외국 사람이었나요?’"]], [["“‘An Italian from the neighborhood of Mantua, I believe.’", "“‘만토바 근방 출신 이탈리아 사람인 것 같았어요.’"]], [["“‘Let me see this diamond again,’ replied the jeweller; ‘the first time you are often mistaken as to the value of a stone.’", "“‘이 다이아몬드를 다시 한번 봅시다.’ 보석상이 말했습니다. ‘처음에는 돌의 값어치를 잘못 보는 일이 흔하니까요.’"]], [["“Caderousse took from his pocket a small case of black shagreen, opened, and gave it to the jeweller.", "“카드루스가 주머니에서 검은 상어가죽 작은 갑을 꺼내 열어, 보석상에게 건넸습니다."], ["At the sight of the diamond, which was as large as a hazel-nut, La Carconte’s eyes sparkled with cupidity.”", "개암만큼 큰 그 다이아몬드를 보자, 카르콩트의 눈이 탐욕으로 번쩍였습니다.”"]], [["“And what did you think of this fine story, eavesdropper?”", "“그래, 자넨 그 그럴듯한 이야기를 어떻게 생각했나, 이 엿듣던 양반아?”"], ["said Monte Cristo; “did you credit it?”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. “그 말을 믿었나?”"]], [["“Yes, your excellency.", "“네, 백작님."], ["I did not look on Caderousse as a bad man, and I thought him incapable of committing a crime, or even a theft.”", "저는 카드루스를 나쁜 사람으로 보지 않았고, 그가 범죄는커녕 도둑질조차 저지를 위인이 못 된다고 여겼습니다.”"]], [["“That did more honor to your heart than to your experience, M.", "“그건 자네 경험보다는 자네 마음을 돋보이게 하는 말이로군, 베르투치오."], ["Bertuccio. Had you known this Edmond Dantès, of whom they spoke?”", "그들이 입에 올린 그 에드몽 당테스라는 자를 자넨 알고 있었나?”"]], [["“No, your excellency, I had never heard of him before, and never but once afterwards, and that was from the Abbé Busoni himself, when I saw him in the prison at Nîmes.”", "“아니요, 백작님. 그전에는 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없었고, 그 뒤로도 딱 한 번 들었을 뿐인데, 그것도 부소니 신부님께 직접 들은 것입니다. 님 감옥에서 그분을 뵈었을 때였지요.”"]], [["“Go on.”", "“계속하게.”"]], [["“The jeweller took the ring, and drawing from his pocket a pair of steel pliers and a small set of copper scales, he took the stone out of its setting, and weighed it carefully.", "“보석상은 반지를 집어 들고, 주머니에서 강철 집게와 작은 구리 저울 한 벌을 꺼내, 받침에서 돌을 빼내 정성스레 무게를 달았습니다."]], [["“‘I will give you 45,000,’ said he, ‘but not a sou more; besides, as that is the exact value of the stone, I brought just that sum with me.’", "“‘4만 5천을 드리리다.’ 그가 말했습니다. ‘하지만 한 푼도 더는 안 됩니다. 게다가 그게 이 돌의 정확한 값이라, 마침 딱 그만큼만 가지고 왔거든요.’"]], [["“‘Oh, that’s no matter,’ replied Caderousse, ‘I will go back with you to fetch the other 5,000 francs.’", "“‘오, 그건 문제없습니다.’ 카드루스가 대답했습니다. ‘나머지 5천 프랑은 내가 당신과 함께 가서 받아 오면 되니까요.’"]], [["“‘No,’ returned the jeweller, giving back the diamond and the ring to Caderousse, ‘no, it is worth no more, and I am sorry I offered so much, for the stone has a flaw in it, which I had not seen.", "“‘아니요.’ 보석상이 다이아몬드와 반지를 카드루스에게 돌려주며 대답했습니다. ‘아니, 이건 그 이상은 못 됩니다. 내가 그렇게 많이 부른 게 후회될 지경이오."], ["However, I will not go back on my word, and I will give 45,000.’", "이 돌에는 미처 못 봤던 흠이 있어요. 그래도 한 말을 되돌리지는 않겠소. 4만 5천을 드리리다.’"]], [["“‘At least, replace the diamond in the ring,’ said La Carconte sharply.", "“‘적어도 돌은 반지에 도로 끼워 주세요.’ 카르콩트가 쏘아붙였습니다."]], [["“‘Ah, true,’ replied the jeweller, and he reset the stone.", "“‘아, 그렇지.’ 보석상이 대답하고는 돌을 도로 끼웠습니다."]], [["“‘No matter,’ observed Caderousse, replacing the box in his pocket, ‘someone else will purchase it.’", "“‘상관없소.’ 카드루스가 갑을 다시 주머니에 넣으며 말했습니다. ‘다른 사람이 사겠지요.’"]], [["“‘Yes,’ continued the jeweller; ‘but someone else will not be so easy as I am, or content himself with the same story.", "“‘그렇겠지요.’ 보석상이 말을 이었습니다. ‘하지만 다른 사람은 나만큼 너그럽지 않을 거고, 그 똑같은 이야기로 만족하지도 않을 거요."], ["It is not natural that a man like you should possess such a diamond.", "당신 같은 사람이 이런 다이아몬드를 가지고 있다는 게 당연한 일이 아니거든."], ["He will inform against you.", "그자는 당신을 고발할 거요."], ["You will have to find the Abbé Busoni; and abbés who give diamonds worth two thousand louis are rare.", "그러면 당신은 부소니 신부를 찾아내야 할 텐데, 2천 루이짜리 다이아몬드를 거저 주는 신부가 흔하겠소?"], ["The law would seize it, and put you in prison; if at the end of three or four months you are set at liberty, the ring will be lost, or a false stone, worth three francs, will be given you, instead of a diamond worth 50,000 or perhaps 55,000 francs; from which you must allow that one runs considerable risk in purchasing.’", "법이 그걸 압수해서 당신을 감옥에 처넣을 거고, 서너 달 뒤에 풀려난다 해도, 반지는 사라지고 없거나, 5만, 어쩌면 5만 5천 프랑짜리 다이아몬드 대신 3프랑짜리 가짜 돌이 손에 쥐어질 거요. 그러니 이걸 사는 데에도 상당한 위험이 따른다는 걸 인정하셔야지.’"]], [["“Caderousse and his wife looked eagerly at each other.", "“카드루스와 그의 아내는 서로를 간절히 마주 보았습니다."]], [["“‘No,’ said Caderousse, ‘we are not rich enough to lose 5,000 francs.’", "“‘아니요.’ 카드루스가 말했습니다. ‘우린 5천 프랑을 잃을 만큼 넉넉한 형편이 못 됩니다.’"]], [["“‘As you please, my dear sir,’ said the jeweller; ‘I had, however, as you see, brought you the money in bright coin.’ And he drew from his pocket a handful of gold, and held it sparkling before the dazzled eyes of the innkeeper, and in the other hand he held a packet of bank-notes.", "“‘좋으실 대로 하시지요, 선생.’ 보석상이 말했습니다. ‘하지만 보다시피, 나는 번쩍이는 돈으로 값을 가져왔소이다.’ 그러고는 주머니에서 금화 한 줌을 꺼내, 눈이 휘둥그레진 여관 주인 앞에 반짝이게 펼쳐 보였고, 다른 한 손에는 지폐 한 뭉치를 들고 있었습니다."]], [["“There was evidently a severe struggle in the mind of Caderousse; it was plain that the small shagreen case, which he turned over and over in his hand, did not seem to him commensurate in value to the enormous sum which fascinated his gaze.", "“카드루스의 마음속에서 격렬한 갈등이 이는 것이 분명했습니다. 그가 손에서 이리저리 굴리는 그 작은 상어가죽 갑이, 그의 시선을 사로잡은 그 엄청난 액수에 견줄 만한 값어치로는 여겨지지 않는 게 뻔했지요."], ["He turned towards his wife.", "그는 아내 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸습니다."]], [["“‘What do you think of this?’ he asked in a low voice.", "“‘당신 생각은 어때?’ 그가 낮은 목소리로 물었습니다."]], [["“‘Let him have it—let him have it,’ she said.", "“‘넘겨요, 넘기라고요.’ 여자가 말했습니다."], ["‘If he returns to Beaucaire without the diamond, he will inform against us, and, as he says, who knows if we shall ever again see the Abbé Busoni?—in all probability we shall never see him.’", "‘저 사람이 다이아몬드 없이 보케르로 돌아가면 우릴 고발할 거예요. 게다가 저 사람 말처럼, 우리가 부소니 신부님을 다시 볼 수 있을지 누가 알아요? 십중팔구 다시는 못 볼 거라고요.’"]], [["“‘Well, then, so I will!’ said Caderousse; ‘so you may have the diamond for 45,000 francs.", "“‘좋아, 그럼 그렇게 하지!’ 카드루스가 말했습니다. ‘이 다이아몬드를 4만 5천 프랑에 가져가시오."], ["But my wife wants a gold chain, and I want a pair of silver buckles.’", "다만 내 아내는 금목걸이를 갖고 싶어 하고, 나는 은 버클 한 쌍이 갖고 싶소.’"]], [["“The jeweller drew from his pocket a long flat box, which contained several samples of the articles demanded.", "“보석상이 주머니에서 길쭉하고 납작한 갑을 꺼냈는데, 그 안에는 요구받은 물건들의 견본이 여럿 들어 있었습니다."], ["‘Here,’ he said, ‘I am very straightforward in my dealings—take your choice.’", "‘자,’ 그가 말했습니다. ‘나는 거래가 아주 깔끔한 사람이오. 마음에 드는 걸 고르시구려.’"]], [["“The woman selected a gold chain worth about five louis, and the husband a pair of buckles, worth perhaps fifteen francs.", "“여자는 5루이쯤 나가는 금목걸이를 골랐고, 남편은 어쩌면 15프랑쯤 할 버클 한 쌍을 골랐습니다."]], [["“‘I hope you will not complain now?’ said the jeweller.", "“‘이제는 불평 없으시겠지요?’ 보석상이 말했습니다."]], [["“‘The abbé told me it was worth 50,000 francs,’ muttered Caderousse.", "“‘신부님은 이게 5만 프랑짜리라고 했단 말이오.’ 카드루스가 중얼거렸습니다."]], [["“‘Come, come—give it to me!", "“‘이런, 이런, 어서 이리 주시오!"], ["What a strange fellow you are,’ said the jeweller, taking the diamond from his hand.", "별난 양반일세.’ 보석상이 그의 손에서 다이아몬드를 받으며 말했습니다."], ["‘I give you 45,000 francs—that is, 2,500 livres of income,—a fortune such as I wish I had myself, and you are not satisfied!’", "‘내가 4만 5천 프랑을, 그러니까 연수입 2천 5백 리브르를 드리는 거요. 나라도 갖고 싶은 그런 재산을 말이오. 그런데도 만족을 못 하시다니!’"]], [["“‘And the five-and-forty thousand francs,’ inquired Caderousse in a hoarse voice, ‘where are they?", "“‘그래서 그 4만 5천 프랑은,’ 카드루스가 쉰 목소리로 물었습니다. ‘어디 있소?"], ["Come—let us see them.’", "자, 어디 좀 봅시다.’"]], [["“‘Here they are,’ replied the jeweller, and he counted out upon the table 15,000 francs in gold, and 30,000 francs in bank-notes.", "“‘여기 있소.’ 보석상이 대답하고는 탁자 위에 금화로 1만 5천 프랑, 지폐로 3만 프랑을 세어 놓았습니다."]], [["“‘Wait whilst I light the lamp,’ said La Carconte; ‘it is growing dark, and there may be some mistake.’ In fact, night had come on during this conversation, and with night the storm which had been threatening for the last half-hour.", "“‘등불을 켤 테니 기다려요.’ 카르콩트가 말했습니다. ‘날이 어두워지고 있어서, 잘못 셀 수도 있으니까요.’ 사실 이 대화가 오가는 동안 밤이 깃들었고, 밤과 함께 반 시간 전부터 몰아칠 듯하던 폭풍우도 닥쳐왔습니다."], ["The thunder growled in the distance; but it was apparently not heard by the jeweller, Caderousse, or La Carconte, absorbed as they were all three with the demon of gain.", "천둥이 멀리서 으르렁거렸지만, 보석상도 카드루스도 카르콩트도 셋 다 탐욕의 악마에 사로잡혀 있던 터라 그 소리를 듣지 못한 모양이었습니다."], ["I myself felt a strange kind of fascination at the sight of all this gold and all these bank-notes; it seemed to me that I was in a dream, and, as it always happens in a dream, I felt myself riveted to the spot.", "저 역시 그 모든 금화와 지폐를 보고 있노라니 묘한 황홀감에 빠져들었습니다. 마치 꿈을 꾸는 것만 같았고, 꿈에서 으레 그렇듯, 저는 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 꼼짝할 수 없었습니다."], ["Caderousse counted and again counted the gold and the notes, then handed them to his wife, who counted and counted them again in her turn.", "카드루스가 금화와 지폐를 세고 또 세더니, 그것을 아내에게 건넸고, 아내가 이번에는 또다시 세고 또 셌습니다."], ["During this time, the jeweller made the diamond play and sparkle in the lamplight, and the gem threw out jets of light which made him unmindful of those which—precursors of the storm—began to play in at the windows.", "그동안 보석상은 등불 빛에 다이아몬드를 이리저리 비춰 반짝이게 했고, 그 보석이 뿜어내는 빛줄기에 정신이 팔려, 폭풍우의 전조로 창문 너머에서 번쩍이기 시작한 또 다른 빛은 알아채지 못했습니다."]], [["“‘Well,’ inquired the jeweller, ‘is the cash all right?’", "“‘자,’ 보석상이 물었습니다. ‘돈은 다 맞소?’"]], [["“‘Yes,’ said Caderousse.", "“‘맞아요.’ 카드루스가 말했습니다."], ["‘Give me the pocket-book, La Carconte, and find a bag somewhere.’", "‘지갑 좀 줘, 카르콩트. 그리고 어디 자루도 하나 찾아봐.’"]], [["“La Carconte went to a cupboard, and returned with an old leathern pocket-book and a bag.", "“카르콩트가 찬장으로 가더니 낡은 가죽 지갑과 자루 하나를 가지고 돌아왔습니다."], ["From the former she took some greasy letters, and put in their place the bank-notes, and from the bag took two or three crowns of six livres each, which, in all probability, formed the entire fortune of the miserable couple.", "지갑에서는 기름때 묻은 편지 몇 장을 꺼내고 그 자리에 지폐를 넣었으며, 자루에서는 각각 6리브르짜리 은화 두세 닢을 꺼냈는데, 그것이 십중팔구 이 가련한 부부의 전 재산이었을 것입니다."]], [["“‘There,’ said Caderousse; ‘and now, although you have wronged us of perhaps 10,000 francs, will you have your supper with us?", "“‘자,’ 카드루스가 말했습니다. ‘이제, 비록 당신이 우리한테서 1만 프랑쯤 우려내긴 했지만, 우리와 함께 저녁이나 드시지 않겠소?"], ["I invite you with good-will.’", "진심으로 청하는 거요.’"]], [["“‘Thank you,’ replied the jeweller, ‘it must be getting late, and I must return to Beaucaire—my wife will be getting uneasy.’ He drew out his watch, and exclaimed, ‘_Morbleu!_ nearly nine o’clock—why, I shall not get back to Beaucaire before midnight!", "“‘고맙소.’ 보석상이 대답했습니다. ‘이제 시간이 꽤 늦은 모양이니, 보케르로 돌아가야겠소. 안 그러면 아내가 걱정할 테니.’ 그는 회중시계를 꺼내 들고 외쳤습니다."], ["Good-night, my friends.", "‘이런, 거의 아홉 시로군."], ["If the Abbé Busoni should by any accident return, think of me.’", "자정 전에는 보케르에 못 닿겠어! 잘들 지내시오, 친구들. 혹시라도 부소니 신부가 돌아오거든, 나를 떠올려 주시오.’"]], [["“‘In another week you will have left Beaucaire,’ remarked Caderousse, ‘for the fair ends in a few days.’", "“‘일주일이면 당신도 보케르를 떠나 있을 텐데요.’ 카드루스가 말했습니다. ‘며칠 안에 장이 끝나니까요.’"]], [["“‘True, but that makes no difference.", "“‘그렇긴 하지만, 그건 상관없소."], ["Write to me at Paris, to M.", "파리로 편지를 보내시오."], ["Joannes, in the Palais Royal, arcade Pierre, No. 45.", "팔레루아얄, 피에르 회랑 45번지, 조아네스 앞으로."], ["I will make the journey on purpose to see him, if it is worth while.’", "그럴 만한 값어치가 있다면, 일부러라도 그를 보러 한걸음에 달려오겠소.’"]], [["“At this moment there was a tremendous clap of thunder, accompanied by a flash of lightning so vivid, that it quite eclipsed the light of the lamp. 20307m", "“바로 그때 어마어마한 천둥소리가 울리고, 등불 빛을 무색하게 할 만큼 눈부신 번개가 번쩍였습니다."]], [["“‘See here,’ exclaimed Caderousse. ‘You cannot think of going out in such weather as this.’", "“‘이것 좀 보세요.’ 카드루스가 외쳤습니다. ‘이런 날씨에 나가실 생각은 마세요.’"]], [["“‘Oh, I am not afraid of thunder,’ said the jeweller.", "“‘오, 나는 천둥 따위는 무섭지 않소.’ 보석상이 말했습니다."]], [["“‘And then there are robbers,’ said La Carconte.", "“‘게다가 도둑들도 있고요.’ 카르콩트가 말했습니다."], ["‘The road is never very safe during fair time.’", "‘장이 서는 동안에는 길이 도통 안전하질 않거든요.’"]], [["“‘Oh, as to the robbers,’ said Joannes, ‘here is something for them,’ and he drew from his pocket a pair of small pistols, loaded to the muzzle.", "“‘오, 도둑이라면,’ 조아네스가 말했습니다. ‘여기 그들을 위한 게 있소.’ 그러고는 주머니에서 총구까지 가득 장전된 작은 권총 한 쌍을 꺼냈습니다."], ["‘Here,’ said he, ‘are dogs who bark and bite at the same time, they are for the two first who shall have a longing for your diamond, Friend Caderousse.’", "‘자,’ 그가 말했습니다. ‘이놈들은 짖으면서 동시에 무는 개들이오. 당신네 다이아몬드를 탐내는 첫 두 놈을 위한 거지요, 카드루스 친구.’"]], [["“Caderousse and his wife again interchanged a meaning look. It seemed as though they were both inspired at the same time with some horrible thought.", "“카드루스와 그의 아내가 다시 의미심장한 눈빛을 주고받았습니다. 마치 두 사람이 동시에 어떤 끔찍한 생각에 사로잡힌 듯했습니다."], ["‘Well, then, a good journey to you,’ said Caderousse.", "‘그럼, 잘 가시오.’ 카드루스가 말했습니다."]], [["“‘Thanks,’ replied the jeweller.", "“‘고맙소.’ 보석상이 대답했습니다."], ["He then took his cane, which he had placed against an old cupboard, and went out.", "그러고는 낡은 찬장에 세워 두었던 지팡이를 집어 들고 밖으로 나갔습니다."], ["At the moment when he opened the door, such a gust of wind came in that the lamp was nearly extinguished.", "그가 문을 여는 순간, 어찌나 거센 바람이 불어들었던지 등불이 꺼질 뻔했습니다. ‘오,’ 그가 말했습니다."], ["‘Oh,’ said he, ‘this is very nice weather, and two leagues to go in such a storm.’", "‘날씨 한번 고약하구먼. 이런 폭풍 속을 두 리외나 가야 하다니.’"]], [["“‘Remain,’ said Caderousse.", "“‘여기 머무시오.’ 카드루스가 말했습니다."], ["‘You can sleep here.’", "‘여기서 주무시면 되잖소.’"]], [["“‘Yes; do stay,’ added La Carconte in a tremulous voice; ‘we will take every care of you.’", "“‘네, 머무세요.’ 카르콩트가 떨리는 목소리로 거들었습니다. ‘저희가 정성껏 모시겠어요.’"]], [["“‘No; I must sleep at Beaucaire.", "“‘아니, 나는 보케르에서 자야 하오."], ["So, once more, good-night.’ Caderousse followed him slowly to the threshold.", "그러니, 다시 한번, 잘들 지내시오.’ 카드루스가 천천히 문턱까지 그를 따라갔습니다."], ["‘I can see neither heaven nor earth,’ said the jeweller, who was outside the door.", "‘하늘도 땅도 분간이 안 되는군.’ 문밖으로 나선 보석상이 말했습니다."], ["‘Do I turn to the right, or to the left hand?’", "‘오른쪽으로 가야 하오, 왼쪽으로 가야 하오?’"]], [["“‘To the right,’ said Caderousse.", "“‘오른쪽이오.’ 카드루스가 말했습니다."], ["‘You cannot go wrong—the road is bordered by trees on both sides.’", "‘길 양쪽으로 나무가 늘어서 있으니 잘못 들 일은 없을 거요.’"]], [["“‘Good—all right,’ said a voice almost lost in the distance.", "“‘좋소, 알았소.’ 거의 멀리서 사라지는 목소리가 들렸습니다."]], [["“‘Close the door,’ said La Carconte; ‘I do not like open doors when it thunders.’", "“‘문 닫아요.’ 카르콩트가 말했습니다. ‘난 천둥 칠 때 문 열려 있는 게 싫어요.’"]], [["“‘Particularly when there is money in the house, eh?’ answered Caderousse, double-locking the door.", "“‘집 안에 돈이 있을 때는 더더욱 그렇겠지, 안 그래?’ 카드루스가 문을 이중으로 잠그며 대꾸했습니다."]], [["20311m\n“He came into the room, went to the cupboard, took out the bag and pocket-book, and both began, for the third time, to count their gold and bank-notes.", "“그는 방으로 들어와 찬장으로 가서 자루와 지갑을 꺼냈고, 두 사람은 세 번째로 금화와 지폐를 세기 시작했습니다."], ["I never saw such an expression of cupidity as the flickering lamp revealed in those two countenances.", "깜박이는 등불이 그 두 얼굴에 드러내는 그런 탐욕의 표정은 저도 일찍이 본 적이 없었습니다."], ["The woman, especially, was hideous; her usual feverish tremulousness was intensified, her countenance had become livid, and her eyes resembled burning coals.", "특히 여자가 흉측했습니다. 평소의 열에 들뜬 떨림이 더 심해졌고, 낯빛은 납빛이 되었으며, 두 눈은 타오르는 숯덩이 같았습니다."]], [["“‘Why,’ she inquired in a hoarse voice, ‘did you invite him to sleep here tonight?’", "“‘그런데 왜,’ 여자가 쉰 목소리로 물었습니다. ‘오늘 밤 저자더러 여기서 자라고 권한 거예요?’"]], [["“‘Why?’ said Caderousse with a shudder; ‘why, that he might not have the trouble of returning to Beaucaire.’", "“‘왜냐고?’ 카드루스가 몸서리치며 말했습니다. ‘저 사람이 보케르까지 돌아가는 수고를 안 해도 되라고 그런 거지.’"]], [["“‘Ah,’ responded the woman, with an expression impossible to describe; ‘I thought it was for something else.’", "“‘아,’ 여자가 뭐라 형용할 수 없는 표정으로 대꾸했습니다. ‘난 또 다른 이유가 있는 줄 알았네.’"]], [["“‘Woman, woman—why do you have such ideas?’ cried Caderousse; ‘or, if you have them, why don’t you keep them to yourself?’", "“‘이 사람아, 이 사람아, 어쩌자고 그런 생각을 하는 거야?’ 카드루스가 소리쳤습니다. ‘설령 그런 생각이 든다 해도, 왜 혼자 속으로만 담아 두지 못하는 거냐고?’"]], [["“‘Well,’ said La Carconte, after a moment’s pause, ‘you are not a man.’", "“‘흥,’ 카르콩트가 잠시 뜸을 들이고 말했습니다. ‘당신은 사내도 아니야.’"]], [["“‘What do you mean?’ added Caderousse.", "“‘그게 무슨 소리야?’ 카드루스가 받아쳤습니다."]], [["“‘If you had been a man, you would not have let him go from here.’", "“‘당신이 사내였다면, 저자를 여기서 그냥 보내지 않았을 거란 말이지.’"]], [["“‘Woman!’", "“‘이 사람이!’"]], [["“‘Or else he should not have reached Beaucaire.’", "“‘아니면 적어도 저자가 보케르까지 닿지는 못했을 거고.’"]], [["“‘Woman!’", "“‘이 사람이!’"]], [["“‘The road takes a turn—he is obliged to follow it—while alongside of the canal there is a shorter road.’", "“‘길은 굽이를 도니까 저자는 어쩔 수 없이 그 길을 따라가야 해. 그런데 운하를 끼고 가면 더 가까운 지름길이 있단 말이야.’"]], [["“‘Woman!—you offend the good God.", "“‘이 사람아! 하느님께 죄짓는 소리야."], ["There—listen!’", "저것 봐, 들어 봐!’"]], [["And at this moment there was a tremendous peal of thunder, while the livid lightning illumined the room, and the thunder, rolling away in the distance, seemed to withdraw unwillingly from the cursed abode.", "“바로 그 순간 어마어마한 천둥소리가 울리고, 납빛 번개가 방 안을 환히 밝혔습니다. 그리고 천둥은 멀리 굴러가며, 이 저주받은 거처에서 마지못해 물러나는 것만 같았습니다."], ["‘Mercy!’ said Caderousse, crossing himself. 20312m", "‘주여, 자비를!’ 카드루스가 성호를 그으며 말했습니다."]], [["“At the same moment, and in the midst of the terrifying silence which usually follows a clap of thunder, they heard a knocking at the door.", "“바로 그 순간, 천둥소리 뒤에 으레 따르는 그 무시무시한 정적 한가운데서, 두 사람은 문 두드리는 소리를 들었습니다."], ["Caderousse and his wife started and looked aghast at each other.", "카드루스와 그의 아내는 흠칫 놀라 넋이 나간 얼굴로 서로를 바라보았습니다."]], [["“‘Who’s there?’ cried Caderousse, rising, and drawing up in a heap the gold and notes scattered over the table, and which he covered with his two hands.", "“‘누구요?’ 카드루스가 자리에서 일어나며 외쳤습니다. 그러고는 탁자 위에 흩어진 금화와 지폐를 한데 그러모아 두 손으로 덮었습니다."]], [["“‘It is I,’ shouted a voice.", "“‘나요.’ 한 목소리가 소리쳤습니다."]], [["“‘And who are you?’", "“‘당신이 누구요?’"]], [["“‘Eh, _pardieu!_ Joannes, the jeweller.’", "“‘에이, 이런! 보석상 조아네스요.’"]], [["“‘Well, and you said I offended the good God,’ said La Carconte with a horrid smile.", "“‘봐요, 당신이 나더러 하느님께 죄짓는 소리라더니.’ 카르콩트가 끔찍한 미소를 지으며 말했습니다."], ["‘Why, the good God sends him back again.’ Caderousse sank pale and breathless into his chair.", "‘하느님이 저자를 도로 보내 주셨잖아요.’ 카드루스는 창백해진 채 숨도 못 쉬고 의자에 털썩 주저앉았습니다."]], [["“La Carconte, on the contrary, rose, and going with a firm step towards the door, opened it, saying, as she did so:", "“반대로 카르콩트는 일어나, 단호한 걸음으로 문으로 다가가 그것을 열며 말했습니다."]], [["“‘Come in, dear M. Joannes.’", "“‘어서 오세요, 조아네스 선생님.’"]], [["“‘_Ma foi_,’ said the jeweller, drenched with rain, ‘I am not destined to return to Beaucaire tonight.", "“‘이런,’ 비에 흠뻑 젖은 보석상이 말했습니다. ‘오늘 밤은 보케르로 돌아갈 팔자가 아닌가 보오."], ["The shortest follies are best, my dear Caderousse.", "어리석은 짓도 짧은 게 상책이라 하지 않소, 카드루스 친구."], ["You offered me hospitality, and I accept it, and have returned to sleep beneath your friendly roof.’", "당신이 묵어 가라고 청했으니, 그 호의를 받아들여 이 정다운 지붕 아래서 자려고 돌아왔소이다.’"]], [["“Caderousse stammered out something, while he wiped away the sweat that started to his brow. La Carconte double-locked the door behind the jeweller.”", "“카드루스는 이마에 솟은 땀을 닦으며 무어라 더듬거렸습니다. 카르콩트는 보석상의 등 뒤로 문을 이중으로 잠갔습니다.”"]], [["# Chapter 45. The Rain of Blood", "# Chapter 45. The Rain of Blood"]], [["As the jeweller returned to the apartment, he cast around him a scrutinizing glance—but there was nothing to excite suspicion, if it did not exist, or to confirm it, if it were already awakened.", "보석상이 방으로 돌아오면서 주위를 날카롭게 훑어보았다. 그러나 의심을 불러일으킬 만한 것도, 이미 깨어난 의심을 굳혀 줄 만한 것도 없었다."], ["Caderousse’s hands still grasped the gold and bank-notes, and La Carconte called up her sweetest smiles while welcoming the reappearance of their guest.", "카드루스의 두 손은 여전히 금화와 지폐를 움켜쥐고 있었고, 카르콩트는 다시 나타난 손님을 맞으며 더없이 다정한 미소를 지어 보였다."]], [["“‘Well, well,’ said the jeweller, ‘you seem, my good friends, to have had some fears respecting the accuracy of your money, by counting it over so carefully directly I was gone.’", "\"이런, 이런.\" 보석상이 말했다. \"두 분, 제가 자리를 비우자마자 그렇게 꼼꼼하게 돈을 세시는 걸 보니, 받으신 돈이 정확한지 영 미덥지 않으셨던 모양입니다.\""]], [["“‘Oh, no,’ answered Caderousse, ‘that was not my reason, I can assure you; but the circumstances by which we have become possessed of this wealth are so unexpected, as to make us scarcely credit our good fortune, and it is only by placing the actual proof of our riches before our eyes that we can persuade ourselves that the whole affair is not a dream.’", "\"아, 그게 아닙니다.\" 카드루스가 대답했다. \"분명히 말씀드리지만 그런 이유가 아닙니다. 이 재물이 손에 들어온 사정이 너무도 뜻밖이라 우리 행운을 도무지 믿을 수가 없어서, 이렇게 재물을 눈앞에 두고 직접 확인해야만 이 모든 일이 꿈이 아니라고 스스로를 납득시킬 수 있는 겁니다.\""]], [["“The jeweller smiled.", "보석상이 미소 지었다."], ["‘Have you any other guests in your house?’ inquired he.", "\"이 집에 다른 손님은 없습니까?\" 그가 물었다."]], [["“‘Nobody but ourselves,’ replied Caderousse; ‘the fact is, we do not lodge travellers—indeed, our tavern is so near the town, that nobody would think of stopping here.’", "\"우리 둘뿐입니다.\" 카드루스가 대답했다. \"사실 우리는 나그네를 묵게 하지 않습니다. 게다가 이 술집이 마을과 너무 가까워서, 여기서 묵을 생각을 하는 사람은 아무도 없지요.\""]], [["“‘Then I am afraid I shall very much inconvenience you.’", "\"그렇다면 제가 무척 폐를 끼치게 되는 게 아닐까 걱정입니다.\""]], [["“‘Inconvenience us?", "\"폐라니요?"], ["Not at all, my dear sir,’ said La Carconte in her most gracious manner.", "천만의 말씀입니다, 손님.\" 카르콩트가 더없이 상냥하게 말했다."], ["‘Not at all, I assure you.’", "\"조금도 폐가 되지 않으니 안심하세요.\""]], [["“‘But where will you manage to stow me?’", "\"하지만 저를 어디에 재우시려고요?\""]], [["“‘In the chamber overhead.’", "\"위층 방에요.\""]], [["“‘Surely that is where you yourselves sleep?’", "\"거긴 두 분이 주무시는 곳이 아닙니까?\""]], [["“‘Never mind that; we have a second bed in the adjoining room.’", "\"신경 쓰지 마세요. 옆방에 침대가 하나 더 있으니까요.\""]], [["“Caderousse stared at his wife with much astonishment.", "카드루스가 깜짝 놀라 아내를 빤히 쳐다보았다."]], [["“The jeweller, meanwhile, was humming a song as he stood warming his back at the fire La Carconte had kindled to dry the wet garments of her guest; and this done, she next occupied herself in arranging his supper, by spreading a napkin at the end of the table, and placing on it the slender remains of their dinner, to which she added three or four fresh-laid eggs.", "그러는 동안 보석상은 노래를 흥얼거리며 카르콩트가 손님의 젖은 옷을 말리려고 피워 놓은 불에 등을 쬐고 있었다. 카르콩트는 그러고 나서 손님의 저녁상을 차리는 데 매달렸다. 식탁 끝에 냅킨을 펴고 점심에 남은 변변찮은 음식을 차린 다음, 갓 낳은 달걀 서너 개를 더했다."], ["Caderousse had once more parted with his treasure—the banknotes were replaced in the pocket-book, the gold put back into the bag, and the whole carefully locked in the cupboard.", "카드루스는 또 한 번 자기 보물과 헤어졌다. 지폐는 다시 지갑에 넣고, 금화는 자루에 도로 담아, 모두 찬장에 조심스레 넣고 잠갔다."], ["He then began pacing the room with a pensive and gloomy air, glancing from time to time at the jeweller, who stood reeking with the steam from his wet clothes, and merely changing his place on the warm hearth, to enable the whole of his garments to be dried.", "그러고는 생각에 잠긴 침울한 얼굴로 방 안을 거닐기 시작했다. 이따금 보석상 쪽을 흘끔거렸는데, 보석상은 젖은 옷에서 김을 무럭무럭 피워 올리며 옷이 골고루 마르도록 따뜻한 화롯가에서 자리만 옮겨 가며 서 있었다."]], [["“‘There,’ said La Carconte, as she placed a bottle of wine on the table, ‘supper is ready whenever you are.’", "\"자.\" 카르콩트가 식탁에 포도주 한 병을 놓으며 말했다. \"준비되시면 언제든 드세요.\""]], [["“‘And you?’ asked Joannes.", "\"두 분은요?\" 조아네스가 물었다."]], [["“‘I don’t want any supper,’ said Caderousse.", "\"난 저녁 생각 없소.\" 카드루스가 말했다."]], [["“‘We dined so very late,’ hastily interposed La Carconte.", "\"우리는 점심을 아주 늦게 먹었거든요.\" 카르콩트가 황급히 끼어들었다."]], [["“‘Then it seems I am to eat alone,’ remarked the jeweller.", "\"그럼 저 혼자 먹어야 하는 모양이군요.\" 보석상이 말했다."]], [["“‘Oh, we shall have the pleasure of waiting upon you,’ answered La Carconte, with an eager attention she was not accustomed to manifest even to guests who paid for what they took.", "\"오, 곁에서 시중드는 즐거움을 누리겠습니다.\" 카르콩트가 대답했다. 돈을 치르는 손님에게조차 좀처럼 보이지 않던 살뜰한 정성이었다."]], [["“From time to time Caderousse darted on his wife keen, searching glances, but rapid as the lightning flash.", "카드루스는 이따금 아내에게 날카롭게 캐묻는 듯한 눈길을 던졌으나, 그것은 번갯불처럼 순식간이었다."], ["The storm still continued.", "폭풍은 여전히 계속되고 있었다."]], [["“‘There, there,’ said La Carconte; ‘do you hear that?", "\"저것 봐요, 저것.\" 카르콩트가 말했다. \"저 소리 들리세요?"], ["upon my word, you did well to come back.’", "정말이지 돌아오시길 잘했어요.\""]], [["“‘Nevertheless,’ replied the jeweller, ‘if by the time I have finished my supper the tempest has at all abated, I shall make another start.’", "\"그래도,\" 보석상이 대답했다. \"저녁을 다 먹을 때쯤 폭풍이 좀 잦아들면, 다시 길을 나설 생각입니다.\""]], [["“‘It’s the mistral,’ said Caderousse, ‘and it will be sure to last till tomorrow morning.’ He sighed heavily.", "\"미스트랄이오.\" 카드루스가 말했다. \"내일 아침까지는 틀림없이 그칠 줄 모를 거요.\" 그가 무겁게 한숨을 내쉬었다."]], [["“‘Well,’ said the jeweller, as he placed himself at table, ‘all I can say is, so much the worse for those who are abroad.’", "\"뭐,\" 보석상이 식탁에 앉으며 말했다. \"이런 날 바깥에 나도는 사람들은 더 딱하게 됐다, 그 말밖에 더 하겠습니까.\""]], [["“‘Yes,’ chimed in La Carconte, ‘they will have a wretched night of it.’", "\"그러게요.\" 카르콩트가 맞장구쳤다. \"그 사람들은 끔찍한 밤을 보내겠지요.\""]], [["“The jeweller began eating his supper, and the woman, who was ordinarily so querulous and indifferent to all who approached her, was suddenly transformed into the most smiling and attentive hostess.", "보석상이 저녁을 먹기 시작했다. 평소에는 다가오는 누구에게나 투덜대고 무심하던 그 여자가 별안간 더없이 환한 미소를 띤 살가운 안주인으로 탈바꿈했다."], ["Had the unhappy man on whom she lavished her assiduities been previously acquainted with her, so sudden an alteration might well have excited suspicion in his mind, or at least have greatly astonished him.", "그녀가 정성을 쏟아붓는 불행한 사내가 만일 그녀를 전부터 알았더라면, 그토록 갑작스러운 변화에 의심을 품었거나, 적어도 크게 놀랐을 것이다."], ["Caderousse, meanwhile, continued to pace the room in gloomy silence, sedulously avoiding the sight of his guest; but as soon as the stranger had completed his repast, the agitated innkeeper went eagerly to the door and opened it.", "그러는 동안 카드루스는 침울하게 입을 다문 채 계속 방 안을 거닐며, 손님의 모습을 한사코 외면했다. 그러나 낯선 손님이 식사를 마치자마자, 동요하던 주막 주인은 부리나케 문으로 가서 문을 열었다."]], [["“‘I believe the storm is over,’ said he.", "\"폭풍이 그친 것 같소.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["“But as if to contradict his statement, at that instant a violent clap of thunder seemed to shake the house to its very foundation, while a sudden gust of wind, mingled with rain, extinguished the lamp he held in his hand.", "그러나 그 말을 반박하기라도 하듯, 바로 그 순간 사나운 천둥소리가 집 밑동까지 뒤흔드는 듯했고, 비를 머금은 돌풍이 별안간 몰아쳐 그가 손에 든 등불을 꺼뜨렸다."]], [["“Trembling and awe-struck, Caderousse hastily shut the door and returned to his guest, while La Carconte lighted a candle by the smouldering ashes that glimmered on the hearth.", "카드루스는 벌벌 떨며 겁에 질려 황급히 문을 닫고 손님에게로 돌아왔고, 카르콩트는 화롯가에 가물거리는 잿불에 초에 불을 붙였다."]], [["“‘You must be tired,’ said she to the jeweller; ‘I have spread a pair of white sheets on your bed; go up when you are ready, and sleep well.’", "\"피곤하시겠어요.\" 카르콩트가 보석상에게 말했다. \"침대에 흰 시트 한 벌을 깔아 두었으니, 준비되시면 올라가셔서 푹 주무세요.\""]], [["“Joannes stayed for a while to see whether the storm seemed to abate in its fury, but a brief space of time sufficed to assure him that, instead of diminishing, the violence of the rain and thunder momentarily increased; resigning himself, therefore, to what seemed inevitable, he bade his host good-night, and mounted the stairs.", "조아네스는 잠시 머무르며 폭풍의 기세가 잦아드는지 살폈으나, 비와 천둥이 누그러지기는커녕 순간순간 거세지고 있다는 것을 깨닫는 데에는 잠깐이면 충분했다. 그래서 그는 피할 수 없는 일에 체념한 채 주인에게 잘 자라는 인사를 하고 계단을 올라갔다."], ["He passed over my head and I heard the flooring creak beneath his footsteps.", "그가 내 머리 위를 지나가자, 발걸음에 마룻바닥이 삐걱대는 소리가 들렸다."], ["The quick, eager glance of La Carconte followed him as he ascended, while Caderousse, on the contrary, turned his back, and seemed most anxiously to avoid even glancing at him.", "그가 올라가는 동안 카르콩트의 날쌔고 탐욕스러운 눈길이 그를 좇았으나, 카드루스는 오히려 등을 돌린 채 그를 흘끗 쳐다보는 것조차 애써 피하는 듯했다."]], [["“All these circumstances did not strike me as painfully at the time as they have since done; in fact, all that had happened (with the exception of the story of the diamond, which certainly did wear an air of improbability), appeared natural enough, and called for neither apprehension nor mistrust; but, worn out as I was with fatigue, and fully purposing to proceed onwards directly the tempest abated, I determined to obtain a few hours’ sleep.", "이 모든 정황이 그때는 나중만큼 마음을 아프게 짓누르지는 않았다. 사실 그때까지 벌어진 일이라면(분명 그럴듯하지 않은 구석이 있던 다이아몬드 이야기만 빼면) 더없이 자연스러워 보였고, 두려워하거나 미심쩍어할 까닭이 없었다. 다만 나는 피로에 지칠 대로 지쳐 있었고, 폭풍이 잦아들면 곧장 길을 떠날 작정이었으므로, 몇 시간만이라도 눈을 붙이기로 했다."], ["Overhead I could accurately distinguish every movement of the jeweller, who, after making the best arrangements in his power for passing a comfortable night, threw himself on his bed, and I could hear it creak and groan beneath his weight.", "머리 위에서는 보석상의 움직임을 하나하나 또렷이 분간할 수 있었다. 그는 편한 밤을 보내려고 나름대로 잘 채비를 해 두고는 침대에 몸을 던졌고, 나는 그 무게에 침대가 삐걱대고 끙끙대는 소리를 들을 수 있었다."]], [["“Insensibly my eyelids grew heavy, deep sleep stole over me, and having no suspicion of anything wrong, I sought not to shake it off.", "나도 모르게 눈꺼풀이 무거워지고, 깊은 잠이 슬며시 밀려왔다. 무엇 하나 잘못됐으리라고는 의심하지 않았으므로, 나는 그 잠을 떨쳐 내려 하지 않았다."], ["I looked into the kitchen once more and saw Caderousse sitting by the side of a long table upon one of the low wooden stools which in country places are frequently used instead of chairs; his back was turned towards me, so that I could not see the expression of his countenance—neither should I have been able to do so had he been placed differently, as his head was buried between his two hands.", "한 번 더 부엌을 들여다보니, 카드루스가 긴 식탁 옆 나지막한 나무 걸상에 앉아 있는 것이 보였다. 시골에서 흔히 의자 대신 쓰는 그런 걸상이었다. 그가 내게 등을 돌리고 있어서 표정은 볼 수 없었는데, 설령 다르게 앉아 있었더라도 마찬가지였을 것이다."], ["La Carconte continued to gaze on him for some time, then shrugging her shoulders, she took her seat immediately opposite to him.", "두 손에 머리를 파묻고 있었으니까. 카르콩트는 한동안 그를 바라보다가 어깨를 으쓱하고는 그 맞은편에 가서 앉았다."]], [["“At this moment the expiring embers threw up a fresh flame from the kindling of a piece of wood that lay near, and a bright light flashed over the room.", "그 순간 가까이 놓인 장작 한 토막에 불이 붙으면서 꺼져 가던 잿불이 다시 화르르 타올랐고, 밝은 빛이 방 안을 환히 비추었다."], ["La Carconte still kept her eyes fixed on her husband, but as he made no sign of changing his position, she extended her hard, bony hand, and touched him on the forehead.", "카르콩트는 여전히 남편에게서 눈을 떼지 않았는데, 그가 도무지 자세를 바꿀 기미를 보이지 않자 굳고 앙상한 손을 뻗어 그의 이마를 건드렸다."]], [["20317m\n“Caderousse shuddered.", "카드루스가 흠칫 몸을 떨었다."], ["The woman’s lips seemed to move, as though she were talking; but because she merely spoke in an undertone, or my senses were dulled by sleep, I did not catch a word she uttered.", "여자의 입술이 무슨 말을 하려는 듯 달싹이는 것 같았으나, 그저 낮은 목소리로 말했거나 졸음에 감각이 무뎌진 탓에, 나는 그녀가 내뱉는 말을 한마디도 알아듣지 못했다."], ["Confused sights and sounds seemed to float before me, and gradually I fell into a deep, heavy slumber.", "흐릿한 영상과 소리가 눈앞을 떠다니는 듯하더니, 나는 차츰 깊고 무거운 잠에 빠져들었다."], ["How long I had been in this unconscious state I know not, when I was suddenly aroused by the report of a pistol, followed by a fearful cry.", "그 정신 없는 상태로 얼마나 있었는지 알 수 없으나, 별안간 권총 소리에 이어 무시무시한 비명이 울리며 나는 화들짝 깨어났다."], ["Weak and tottering footsteps resounded across the chamber above me, and the next instant a dull, heavy weight seemed to fall powerless on the staircase.", "위층 방을 가로지르는 비틀거리는 힘없는 발소리가 울려 퍼졌고, 다음 순간 둔하고 무거운 무엇인가가 맥없이 계단으로 떨어지는 듯했다."], ["I had not yet fully recovered consciousness, when again I heard groans, mingled with half-stifled cries, as if from persons engaged in a deadly struggle.", "미처 정신을 다 차리기도 전에, 다시 신음 소리가 들렸다. 반쯤 짓눌린 비명이 뒤섞인 것이, 마치 목숨을 걸고 격투를 벌이는 사람들이 내는 소리 같았다."], ["A cry more prolonged than the others and ending in a series of groans effectually roused me from my drowsy lethargy.", "다른 것들보다 길게 이어지다가 거푸 신음으로 잦아드는 비명이 결국 내 졸음의 무력함을 떨쳐 냈다."], ["Hastily raising myself on one arm, I looked around, but all was dark; and it seemed to me as if the rain must have penetrated through the flooring of the room above, for some kind of moisture appeared to fall, drop by drop, upon my forehead, and when I passed my hand across my brow, I felt that it was wet and clammy.", "나는 황급히 한쪽 팔을 짚고 몸을 일으켜 주위를 둘러보았으나 사방이 캄캄했다. 위층 방 마루를 뚫고 비가 새어 든 게 틀림없는 듯했다. 무슨 물기가 한 방울 한 방울 내 이마에 떨어지는 듯했고, 손으로 이마를 훔쳐 보니 축축하고 끈적했다."]], [["“To the fearful noises that had awakened me had succeeded the most perfect silence—unbroken, save by the footsteps of a man walking about in the chamber above.", "나를 깨운 그 무서운 소리들 뒤에는 더없이 완벽한 정적이 이어졌다. 위층 방에서 한 사내가 서성이는 발소리만이 그 정적을 깨뜨렸다."], ["The staircase creaked, he descended into the room below, approached the fire and lit a candle.", "계단이 삐걱대더니, 그가 아래층 방으로 내려와 불 쪽으로 다가가 초에 불을 붙였다."]], [["“The man was Caderousse—he was pale and his shirt was all bloody.", "그 사내는 카드루스였다. 그는 핏기가 가셨고, 셔츠는 온통 피투성이였다."], ["Having obtained the light, he hurried upstairs again, and once more I heard his rapid and uneasy footsteps.", "불을 밝힌 그는 다시 황급히 위층으로 올라갔고, 나는 또 한 번 그의 빠르고 불안한 발소리를 들었다."]], [["“A moment later he came down again, holding in his hand the small shagreen case, which he opened, to assure himself it contained the diamond,—seemed to hesitate as to which pocket he should put it in, then, as if dissatisfied with the security of either pocket, he deposited it in his red handkerchief, which he carefully rolled round his head.", "잠시 뒤 그가 다시 내려왔다. 손에 작은 가죽 상자를 들고 있었는데, 그것을 열어 다이아몬드가 들어 있는지 확인했다. 그러고는 어느 주머니에 넣을지 망설이는 듯하더니, 어느 쪽 주머니도 미덥지 않은 듯 빨간 손수건에 싸서 머리에 조심스레 둘둘 동여맸다."]], [["“After this he took from his cupboard the bank-notes and gold he had put there, thrust the one into the pocket of his trousers, and the other into that of his waistcoat, hastily tied up a small bundle of linen, and rushing towards the door, disappeared in the darkness of the night.", "그러고 나서 그는 찬장에 넣어 두었던 지폐와 금화를 꺼내, 한쪽은 바지 주머니에, 다른 한쪽은 조끼 주머니에 쑤셔 넣고, 황급히 옷가지 한 보따리를 묶고는 문으로 달려가 어두운 밤 속으로 사라졌다."]], [["“Then all became clear and manifest to me, and I reproached myself with what had happened, as though I myself had done the guilty deed.", "그러자 모든 것이 또렷하고 분명해졌고, 나는 마치 그 죄를 내 손으로 저지른 것처럼 벌어진 일을 자책했다."], ["I fancied that I still heard faint moans, and imagining that the unfortunate jeweller might not be quite dead, I determined to go to his relief, by way of atoning in some slight degree, not for the crime I had committed, but for that which I had not endeavored to prevent.", "아직도 희미한 신음 소리가 들리는 듯했고, 불운한 보석상이 아주 죽지는 않았을지도 모른다는 생각에, 그를 구하러 가기로 마음먹었다. 내가 저지른 죄가 아니라, 막으려 애쓰지 않은 그 죄를 조금이나마 속죄하기 위해서였다."], ["For this purpose I applied all the strength I possessed to force an entrance from the cramped spot in which I lay to the adjoining room.", "그러기 위해 나는 가진 힘을 다 짜내, 내가 웅크리고 있던 비좁은 자리에서 옆방으로 비집고 들어갔다."], ["The poorly fastened boards which alone divided me from it yielded to my efforts, and I found myself in the house.", "나와 옆방을 가르던 허술하게 박힌 판자가 내 힘에 못 이겨 떨어져 나갔고, 나는 집 안으로 들어섰다."], ["Hastily snatching up the lighted candle, I hurried to the staircase; about midway a body was lying quite across the stairs.", "황급히 켜져 있는 초를 집어 들고 계단으로 달려갔다. 계단 중간쯤에 시신 하나가 계단을 가로질러 누워 있었다."], ["It was that of La Carconte.", "카르콩트의 시신이었다."], ["The pistol I had heard had doubtless been fired at her.", "내가 들었던 그 권총은 분명 그녀를 향해 발사된 것이었다."], ["The shot had frightfully lacerated her throat, leaving two gaping wounds from which, as well as the mouth, the blood was pouring in floods. She was stone dead.", "총탄에 목이 끔찍하게 찢겨, 입에서뿐 아니라 쩍 벌어진 두 군데 상처에서도 피가 콸콸 쏟아지고 있었다. 그녀는 완전히 숨이 끊겨 있었다."], ["I strode past her, and ascended to the sleeping chamber, which presented an appearance of the wildest disorder.", "나는 그녀를 성큼 넘어 침실로 올라갔다. 그곳은 더없이 어지럽게 난장판이 되어 있었다."], ["The furniture had been knocked over in the deadly struggle that had taken place there, and the sheets, to which the unfortunate jeweller had doubtless clung, were dragged across the room.", "거기서 벌어진 사투에 가구가 나뒹굴었고, 불운한 보석상이 분명 매달렸을 시트는 방을 가로질러 끌려가 있었다."], ["The murdered man lay on the floor, his head leaning against the wall, and about him was a pool of blood which poured forth from three large wounds in his breast; there was a fourth gash, in which a long table knife was plunged up to the handle.", "살해된 사내는 마룻바닥에 머리를 벽에 기댄 채 쓰러져 있었고, 그 주위로는 가슴의 커다란 상처 세 군데에서 쏟아진 피가 흥건히 고여 있었다. 게다가 네 번째 자상이 있었는데, 거기에는 긴 식칼이 손잡이까지 박혀 있었다."]], [["“I stumbled over some object; I stooped to examine—it was the second pistol, which had not gone off, probably from the powder being wet.", "나는 무언가에 발이 걸려 휘청했다. 몸을 굽혀 살펴보니 두 번째 권총이었다. 발사되지 않은 것을 보면, 아마 화약이 젖은 탓인 듯했다."], ["I approached the jeweller, who was not quite dead, and at the sound of my footsteps and the creaking of the floor, he opened his eyes, fixed them on me with an anxious and inquiring gaze, moved his lips as though trying to speak, then, overcome by the effort, fell back and expired.", "나는 보석상에게 다가갔다. 그는 아주 죽지는 않았는데, 내 발소리와 마루 삐걱대는 소리에 눈을 떠 불안하게 캐묻는 눈길로 나를 응시하더니, 입술을 달싹여 무슨 말을 하려다 그 힘에 부쳐 도로 쓰러져 숨을 거두었다."]], [["“This appalling sight almost bereft me of my senses, and finding that I could no longer be of service to anyone in the house, my only desire was to fly.", "이 소름 끼치는 광경에 나는 거의 제정신을 잃었고, 더는 이 집의 누구에게도 도움이 될 수 없음을 깨닫자, 오직 달아나고 싶은 마음뿐이었다."], ["I rushed towards the staircase, clutching my hair, and uttering a groan of horror.", "나는 머리를 쥐어뜯으며 공포의 신음을 내뱉고 계단으로 달려갔다."]], [["“Upon reaching the room below, I found five or six custom-house officers, and two or three gendarmes—all heavily armed.", "아래층 방에 이르렀을 때, 거기에는 세관원이 대여섯 명, 헌병이 두세 명 있었다. 모두 단단히 무장한 상태였다."], ["They threw themselves upon me.", "그들이 내게 달려들었다."], ["I made no resistance; I was no longer master of my senses.", "나는 저항하지 않았다. 더는 제정신이 아니었다."], ["When I strove to speak, a few inarticulate sounds alone escaped my lips.", "말을 하려 해도 입에서 알아들을 수 없는 소리 몇 마디만 새어 나올 뿐이었다."]], [["“As I noticed the significant manner in which the whole party pointed to my blood-stained garments, I involuntarily surveyed myself, and then I discovered that the thick warm drops that had so bedewed me as I lay beneath the staircase must have been the blood of La Carconte.", "그들 모두가 내 피투성이 옷을 의미심장하게 가리키는 것을 알아채고, 나는 무심결에 내 몸을 살펴보았다. 그러고는 계단 밑에 누워 있을 때 나를 그토록 적셨던 그 따뜻하고 짙은 핏방울이 카르콩트의 피였음을 깨달았다."], ["I pointed to the spot where I had concealed myself.", "나는 내가 숨어 있던 자리를 가리켰다."]], [["“‘What does he mean?’ asked a gendarme.", "\"저자가 무슨 말을 하려는 거지?\" 한 헌병이 물었다."]], [["“One of the officers went to the place I directed.", "세관원 하나가 내가 가리킨 곳으로 갔다."]], [["“‘He means,’ replied the man upon his return, ‘that he got in that way;’ and he showed the hole I had made when I broke through.", "\"저자 말은,\" 돌아온 그가 대답했다. \"이리로 들어왔다는 겁니다.\" 그러면서 내가 뚫고 들어오느라 낸 구멍을 보여 주었다."]], [["“Then I saw that they took me for the assassin. I recovered force and energy enough to free myself from the hands of those who held me, while I managed to stammer forth:", "그제야 나는 그들이 나를 살인자로 여긴다는 것을 알았다. 나는 나를 붙든 자들의 손에서 벗어날 만큼의 기운과 힘을 되찾으며, 더듬더듬 외쳤다."]], [["“‘I did not do it!", "\"내가 한 게 아니오!"], ["Indeed, indeed I did not!’", "정말로, 정말로 내가 한 게 아니란 말이오!\""]], [["“A couple of gendarmes held the muzzles of their carbines against my breast.", "헌병 둘이 기총 총구를 내 가슴에 들이댔다."]], [["“‘Stir but a step,’ said they, ‘and you are a dead man.’", "\"한 발짝이라도 움직이면,\" 그들이 말했다. \"넌 죽은 목숨이다.\""]], [["“‘Why should you threaten me with death,’ cried I, ‘when I have already declared my innocence?’", "\"이미 결백을 밝혔는데,\" 내가 외쳤다. \"왜 나를 죽이겠다고 으름장을 놓는 거요?\""]], [["“‘Tush, tush,’ cried the men; ‘keep your innocent stories to tell to the judge at Nîmes.", "\"쳇, 쳇.\" 사내들이 외쳤다. \"그 결백 타령은 님 재판관 앞에 가서나 늘어놓아라."], ["Meanwhile, come along with us; and the best advice we can give you is to do so unresistingly.’", "그때까진 순순히 우리를 따라오는 게 네게 해 줄 수 있는 최선의 충고다.\""]], [["“Alas, resistance was far from my thoughts.", "아아, 저항이라니 나로서는 꿈도 못 꿀 일이었다."], ["I was utterly overpowered by surprise and terror; and without a word I suffered myself to be handcuffed and tied to a horse’s tail, and thus they took me to Nîmes.", "나는 놀라움과 공포에 완전히 짓눌려, 군말 없이 수갑을 차고 말 꼬리에 묶여 끌려갔다. 그렇게 그들은 나를 님으로 데려갔다."]], [["“I had been tracked by a customs-officer, who had lost sight of me near the tavern; feeling certain that I intended to pass the night there, he had returned to summon his comrades, who just arrived in time to hear the report of the pistol, and to take me in the midst of such circumstantial proofs of my guilt as rendered all hopes of proving my innocence utterly futile.", "한 세관원이 주막 근처에서 나를 놓치고는 내 뒤를 쫓고 있었던 것이다. 내가 거기서 하룻밤 묵으려 한다고 확신한 그는 동료들을 부르러 돌아갔고, 그들은 마침 권총 소리를 듣기에 딱 맞춰 도착했다. 그렇게 그들은 내 죄를 입증하는 정황 증거가 빼곡한 한복판에서 나를 붙잡았으니, 결백을 증명할 희망 따위는 죄다 부질없었다."], ["One only chance was left me, that of beseeching the magistrate before whom I was taken to cause every inquiry to be made for the Abbé Busoni, who had stopped at the inn of the Pont du Gard on that morning.", "내게 남은 단 한 가닥 기회는, 끌려간 곳의 치안판사에게 부소니 사제를 백방으로 수소문해 달라고 애원하는 것뿐이었다. 그 사제는 그날 아침 퐁뒤가르의 여관에 들렀던 사람이었다."]], [["“If Caderousse had invented the story relative to the diamond, and there existed no such person as the Abbé Busoni, then, indeed, I was lost past redemption, or, at least, my life hung upon the feeble chance of Caderousse himself being apprehended and confessing the whole truth.", "만일 카드루스가 다이아몬드에 얽힌 이야기를 지어낸 것이고, 부소니 사제라는 사람이 애당초 존재하지 않는다면, 나는 정말로 돌이킬 수 없이 끝장난 셈이었다. 아니면 적어도 내 목숨은 카드루스 본인이 붙잡혀 모든 진실을 자백하리라는 가냘픈 가능성에 매달려 있는 셈이었다."]], [["“Two months passed away in hopeless expectation on my part, while I must do the magistrate the justice to say that he used every means to obtain information of the person I declared could exculpate me if he would.", "내 쪽에서는 가망 없는 기다림 속에 두 달이 흘렀다. 그래도 치안판사를 두고 공정하게 말하자면, 그는 내가 결백을 밝혀 줄 수 있다고 주장한 그 사람의 행방을 알아내려고 온갖 수단을 동원했다."], ["Caderousse still evaded all pursuit, and I had resigned myself to what seemed my inevitable fate.", "카드루스는 여전히 추적을 따돌렸고, 나는 피할 수 없어 보이는 운명에 체념해 있었다. 내 재판은 다가오는 순회재판에서 열릴 예정이었다."], ["My trial was to come on at the approaching assizes; when, on the 8th of September—that is to say, precisely three months and five days after the events which had perilled my life—the Abbé Busoni, whom I never ventured to believe I should see, presented himself at the prison doors, saying he understood one of the prisoners wished to speak to him; he added, that having learned at Marseilles the particulars of my imprisonment, he hastened to comply with my desire.", "그런데 9월 8일, 그러니까 내 목숨을 위태롭게 한 사건이 있은 지 정확히 석 달 닷새째 되던 날, 다시 보리라고는 감히 믿지도 못했던 부소니 사제가 감옥 문 앞에 나타났다. 그는 죄수 가운데 자기와 이야기하고 싶어 하는 사람이 있다고 들었다고 했다. 그러면서, 마르세유에서 내 투옥에 얽힌 자초지종을 알게 되어 내 바람에 응하려고 서둘러 왔다고 덧붙였다."]], [["“You may easily imagine with what eagerness I welcomed him, and how minutely I related the whole of what I had seen and heard.", "내가 얼마나 간절히 그를 맞았는지, 그리고 내가 보고 들은 모든 것을 얼마나 낱낱이 들려주었는지는 쉬이 짐작하실 겁니다."], ["I felt some degree of nervousness as I entered upon the history of the diamond, but, to my inexpressible astonishment, he confirmed it in every particular, and to my equal surprise, he seemed to place entire belief in all I said.", "다이아몬드 이야기를 꺼낼 때는 다소 마음이 떨렸으나, 이루 말할 수 없이 놀랍게도 그는 그 이야기를 하나하나 다 사실로 확인해 주었다. 마찬가지로 놀랍게도, 그는 내가 한 모든 말을 온전히 믿는 듯했다."]], [["“And then it was that, won by his mild charity, seeing that he was acquainted with all the habits and customs of my own country, and considering also that pardon for the only crime of which I was really guilty might come with a double power from lips so benevolent and kind, I besought him to receive my confession, under the seal of which I recounted the Auteuil affair in all its details, as well as every other transaction of my life.", "그러자 나는 그의 온화한 자비에 마음이 끌렸고, 그가 내 고향의 온갖 풍습과 관습을 훤히 알고 있음을 보았으며, 또 내가 진정 죄를 지은 단 하나의 범행에 대한 용서가 그토록 자애롭고 다정한 입술에서라면 두 배의 힘으로 내려질 수 있으리라 여겼다. 그래서 나는 그에게 내 고해를 받아 달라고 간청했고, 고해의 봉인 아래 오퇴유 사건의 자초지종은 물론, 내 삶의 다른 모든 일까지 낱낱이 털어놓았다."], ["That which I had done by the impulse of my best feelings produced the same effect as though it had been the result of calculation.", "가장 선한 마음에 이끌려 한 일이, 마치 계산속에서 나온 일과 똑같은 결과를 낳았다."], ["My voluntary confession of the assassination at Auteuil proved to him that I had not committed that of which I stood accused.", "내가 오퇴유의 살인을 자진해 고백한 것이, 정작 내가 고발당한 범행은 저지르지 않았다는 증거가 된 것이다."], ["When he quitted me, he bade me be of good courage, and to rely upon his doing all in his power to convince my judges of my innocence.", "그는 나를 떠나며 부디 용기를 내라고, 그리고 재판관들에게 내 결백을 납득시키기 위해 자기가 할 수 있는 모든 일을 하리라 믿어도 된다고 일렀다."]], [["“I had speedy proofs that the excellent abbé was engaged in my behalf, for the rigors of my imprisonment were alleviated by many trifling though acceptable indulgences, and I was told that my trial was to be postponed to the assizes following those now being held.", "이 훌륭한 사제가 나를 위해 애쓰고 있다는 증거는 곧바로 나타났다. 옥살이의 가혹함이 사소하나마 고마운 여러 배려로 누그러졌고, 내 재판이 지금 열리고 있는 순회재판 다음 회기로 미뤄졌다는 말을 들었으니 말이다."]], [["“In the interim it pleased Providence to cause the apprehension of Caderousse, who was discovered in some distant country, and brought back to France, where he made a full confession, refusing to make the fact of his wife’s having suggested and arranged the murder any excuse for his own guilt.", "그사이 신의 섭리는 카드루스를 붙잡게 했다. 그는 멀리 떨어진 어느 나라에서 발각되어 프랑스로 압송되었고, 거기서 모든 것을 자백했다. 그는 아내가 살인을 부추기고 꾸몄다는 사실을 제 죄의 핑계로 삼기를 한사코 거부했다."], ["The wretched man was sentenced to the galleys for life, and I was immediately set at liberty.”", "그 가련한 사내는 무기 도형을 선고받았고, 나는 곧바로 풀려났다.\""]], [["“And then it was, I presume,”", "\"그래서 그때,\""], ["said Monte Cristo “that you came to me as the bearer of a letter from the Abbé Busoni?”", "몬테 크리스토가 말했다. \"자네가 부소니 사제의 편지를 들고 나를 찾아온 것이로군?\""]], [["“It was, your excellency; the benevolent abbé took an evident interest in all that concerned me.", "\"그렇습니다, 나리. 그 자애로운 사제는 저와 관련된 모든 일에 분명한 관심을 보였습니다."]], [["“‘Your mode of life as a smuggler,’ said he to me one day, ‘will be the ruin of you; if you get out, don’t take it up again.’", "'밀수꾼으로 살아가는 자네의 생활은,' 어느 날 그가 제게 말했습니다. '결국 자네를 파멸시킬 걸세. 풀려나거든 다시는 그 일에 손대지 말게.'"]], [["“‘But how,’ inquired I, ‘am I to maintain myself and my poor sister?’", "'하지만 어떻게,' 제가 물었습니다. '저 자신과 가엾은 누이를 건사하란 말씀이십니까?'"]], [["“‘A person, whose confessor I am,’ replied he, ‘and who entertains a high regard for me, applied to me a short time since to procure him a confidential servant.", "'내가 고해를 맡고 있고,' 그가 대답했습니다. '나를 무척 존중하는 어떤 사람이 얼마 전 내게 믿을 만한 하인 하나를 구해 달라고 부탁했네."], ["Would you like such a post?", "그런 자리라면 마음에 드는가?"], ["If so, I will give you a letter of introduction to him.’", "그렇다면 그에게 보낼 소개장을 써 주겠네.'"]], [["“‘Oh, father,’ I exclaimed, ‘you are very good.’", "'오, 신부님,' 제가 외쳤습니다. '참으로 인자하십니다.'"]], [["“‘But you must swear solemnly that I shall never have reason to repent my recommendation.’", "'하지만 내 추천을 후회할 일은 결코 없으리라고 엄숙히 맹세해야 하네.'"]], [["“I extended my hand, and was about to pledge myself by any promise he would dictate, but he stopped me.", "저는 손을 내밀어 그가 부르는 대로 무슨 약속이든 하려 했으나, 그가 저를 막았습니다."]], [["“‘It is unnecessary for you to bind yourself by any vow,’ said he; ‘I know and admire the Corsican nature too well to fear you. Here, take this,’ continued he, after rapidly writing the few lines I brought to your excellency, and upon receipt of which you deigned to receive me into your service, and proudly I ask whether your excellency has ever had cause to repent having done so?”", "'무슨 맹세로 스스로를 옭아맬 것 없네,' 그가 말했습니다. '나는 코르시카 사람의 천성을 너무도 잘 알고 또 높이 사고 있으니 자네를 두려워하지 않네. 자, 이걸 받게.' 그러고는 제가 나리께 가져다드린 그 몇 줄을 재빨리 써 주었습니다. 그 편지를 받으시고 나리께서는 저를 거두어 일을 맡겨 주셨지요. 그래서 떳떳이 여쭙건대, 나리께서는 그렇게 하신 것을 후회하실 일이 한 번이라도 있으셨습니까?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"없네.\""], ["replied the count; “I take pleasure in saying that you have served me faithfully, Bertuccio; but you might have shown more confidence in me.”", "백작이 대답했다. \"자네가 나를 충직하게 섬겼다는 말은 기꺼이 해 주지, 베르투치오. 다만 자네는 내게 좀 더 마음을 터놓을 수도 있었을 거야.\""]], [["“I, your excellency?”", "\"제가요, 나리?\""]], [["“Yes; you. How comes it, that having both a sister and an adopted son, you have never spoken to me of either?”", "\"그래, 자네 말일세. 누이와 양아들이 둘 다 있으면서, 어찌 그 어느 쪽 이야기도 내게 한 번도 하지 않았나?\""]], [["20323m\n“Alas, I have still to recount the most distressing period of my life.", "\"아아, 제 삶에서 가장 비통한 시기를 아직 들려드려야겠군요."], ["Anxious as you may suppose I was to behold and comfort my dear sister, I lost no time in hastening to Corsica, but when I arrived at Rogliano I found a house of mourning, the consequences of a scene so horrible that the neighbors remember and speak of it to this day.", "짐작하시겠지만, 저는 사랑하는 누이를 만나 위로하고 싶은 마음에 한시도 지체 않고 코르시카로 달려갔습니다. 그러나 롤리아노에 다다랐을 때 저는 초상집을 보았습니다. 어찌나 끔찍한 일이 벌어졌던지, 동네 사람들이 오늘까지도 기억하며 입에 올리는 그런 참극의 결과였습니다."], ["Acting by my advice, my poor sister had refused to comply with the unreasonable demands of Benedetto, who was continually tormenting her for money, as long as he believed there was a sou left in her possession.", "제 충고에 따라 가엾은 누이는 베네데토의 터무니없는 요구를 들어주기를 거절했습니다. 그놈은 누이에게 한 푼이라도 남아 있다고 여기는 한 끊임없이 돈을 뜯어내려 들들 볶았으니까요."], ["One morning he threatened her with the severest consequences if she did not supply him with what he desired, and disappeared and remained away all day, leaving the kind-hearted Assunta, who loved him as if he were her own child, to weep over his conduct and bewail his absence.", "어느 날 아침 그놈은 자기가 원하는 것을 내놓지 않으면 가장 끔찍한 꼴을 보게 될 거라고 누이를 협박하고는 자취를 감추어 온종일 돌아오지 않았습니다. 그놈을 제 자식처럼 사랑하던 마음씨 고운 아순타는 그 행실을 두고 눈물 흘리며 부재를 슬퍼할 뿐이었지요."], ["Evening came, and still, with all the patient solicitude of a mother, she watched for his return.", "저녁이 되었어도, 누이는 어머니다운 끈질긴 애정으로 그놈이 돌아오기를 기다리고 있었습니다."]], [["“As the eleventh hour struck, he entered with a swaggering air, attended by two of the most dissolute and reckless of his boon companions.", "밤 열한 시를 알리는 종이 울리자, 그놈이 거들먹거리며 들어왔습니다. 가장 타락하고 무모한 술친구 둘을 데리고서요."], ["She stretched out her arms to him, but they seized hold of her, and one of the three—none other than the accursed Benedetto exclaimed:", "누이가 그놈에게 두 팔을 벌렸으나, 그들이 누이를 붙들었고, 셋 중 하나가, 다름 아닌 그 저주받을 베네데토가 외쳤습니다."]], [["“‘Put her to torture and she’ll soon tell us where her money is.’", "'이 여자를 고문해라. 그럼 돈을 어디 숨겼는지 곧 불 거다.'"]], [["“It unfortunately happened that our neighbor, Wasilio, was at Bastia, leaving no person in his house but his wife; no human creature beside could hear or see anything that took place within our dwelling.", "공교롭게도 그때 이웃인 바실리오가 바스티아에 가 있어서, 그 집에는 그의 아내 말고는 아무도 없었습니다. 우리 집에서 벌어지는 일을 듣거나 볼 수 있는 사람은 달리 아무도 없었지요."], ["Two held poor Assunta, who, unable to conceive that any harm was intended to her, smiled in the face of those who were soon to become her executioners.", "둘이서 가엾은 아순타를 붙들었습니다. 누이는 자기에게 해를 끼치려 한다고는 상상조차 못 하고, 머잖아 제 처형인이 될 자들의 얼굴에 대고 미소 지었습니다."], ["The third proceeded to barricade the doors and windows, then returned, and the three united in stifling the cries of terror incited by the sight of these preparations, and then dragged Assunta feet foremost towards the brazier, expecting to wring from her an avowal of where her supposed treasure was secreted.", "세 번째 놈은 문과 창문을 막아 버리고 돌아왔습니다. 그러고는 셋이 함께, 그 채비를 보고 겁에 질려 터져 나오는 누이의 비명을 틀어막고는, 아순타의 발을 먼저 끌어 화로 쪽으로 잡아당겼습니다. 숨겨 두었으리라 여긴 보물이 어디 있는지 실토하게 하려는 속셈이었지요."], ["In the struggle her clothes caught fire, and they were obliged to let go their hold in order to preserve themselves from sharing the same fate.", "그 와중에 누이의 옷에 불이 붙었고, 그들은 같은 꼴을 당하지 않으려고 손을 놓을 수밖에 없었습니다. 불길에 휩싸인 아순타는 미친 듯이 문으로 달려갔으나 문은 잠겨 있었습니다."], ["Covered with flames, Assunta rushed wildly to the door, but it was fastened; she flew to the windows, but they were also secured; then the neighbors heard frightful shrieks; it was Assunta calling for help.", "창문으로 날아갔으나 그것 또한 막혀 있었지요. 그제야 이웃들은 무시무시한 비명을 들었습니다. 도와 달라고 외치는 아순타의 소리였습니다."], ["The cries died away in groans, and next morning, as soon as Wasilio’s wife could muster up courage to venture abroad, she caused the door of our dwelling to be opened by the public authorities, when Assunta, although dreadfully burnt, was found still breathing; every drawer and closet in the house had been forced open, and the money stolen.", "그 외침은 신음 속에 잦아들었고, 이튿날 아침 바실리오의 아내가 겨우 용기를 내어 밖으로 나설 수 있게 되자, 그녀는 관청에 알려 우리 집 문을 열게 했습니다. 그때 아순타는 끔찍하게 불에 탔으나 아직 숨이 붙어 있는 채로 발견되었습니다. 집 안의 서랍과 벽장이란 벽장은 모조리 부서져 열려 있었고, 돈은 도둑맞았습니다."], ["Benedetto never again appeared at Rogliano, neither have I since that day either seen or heard anything concerning him.", "베네데토는 그 뒤로 다시는 롤리아노에 나타나지 않았고, 저도 그날 이후로 그놈에 대해 보거나 들은 것이 전혀 없습니다."]], [["“It was subsequently to these dreadful events that I waited on your excellency, to whom it would have been folly to have mentioned Benedetto, since all trace of him seemed entirely lost; or of my sister, since she was dead.”", "제가 나리를 모시게 된 것은 이 끔찍한 일들이 있은 뒤였습니다. 나리께 베네데토 이야기를 꺼내 봐야 어리석은 짓이었지요. 그놈의 자취는 완전히 끊겨 버린 듯했으니까요. 또 누이 이야기를 꺼낼 것도 없었습니다. 누이는 이미 죽었으니까요.\""]], [["“And in what light did you view the occurrence?”", "\"그래, 그 일을 자네는 어떻게 보았나?\""], ["inquired Monte Cristo.", "몬테 크리스토가 물었다."]], [["“As a punishment for the crime I had committed,”", "\"제가 저지른 죄에 대한 벌이라고요.\""], ["answered Bertuccio.", "베르투치오가 대답했다."], ["“Oh, those Villeforts are an accursed race!”", "\"아, 그 빌포르 가문은 저주받은 종자들입니다!\""]], [["“Truly they are,”", "\"정말 그렇지.\""], ["murmured the count in a lugubrious tone.", "백작이 음울한 어조로 중얼거렸다."]], [["“And now,”", "\"그러니,\""], ["resumed Bertuccio, “your excellency may, perhaps, be able to comprehend that this place, which I revisit for the first time—this garden, the actual scene of my crime—must have given rise to reflections of no very agreeable nature, and produced that gloom and depression of spirits which excited the notice of your excellency, who was pleased to express a desire to know the cause.", "베르투치오가 말을 이었다. \"나리께서는 이제 어쩌면 헤아리실 수 있을 겁니다. 제가 처음으로 다시 찾은 이곳, 바로 제 범행의 현장인 이 정원이 어찌하여 그리 유쾌하지 못한 생각들을 불러일으켰는지, 그리고 어찌하여 나리께서 알아채시고 그 까닭을 알고 싶다 하셨던 그 침울함과 의기소침을 자아냈는지를요."], ["At this instant a shudder passes over me as I reflect that possibly I am now standing on the very grave in which lies M.", "이 순간에도 저는 몸서리가 칩니다. 어쩌면 제가 지금 빌포르 씨가 누워 있는 바로 그 무덤 위에 서 있을지도 모른다는 생각에 말입니다."], ["de Villefort, by whose hand the ground was dug to receive the corpse of his child.”", "자기 자식의 시신을 묻으려고 손수 땅을 팠던 바로 그 사내 말입니다.\""]], [["“Everything is possible,”", "\"무슨 일이든 있을 수 있지.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, rising from the bench on which he had been sitting; “even,”", "몬테 크리스토가 앉아 있던 벤치에서 일어서며 말했다. \"심지어,\""], ["he added in an inaudible voice, “even that the procureur be not dead.", "그가 들릴 듯 말 듯한 목소리로 덧붙였다. \"그 검사가 죽지 않았다는 것조차도."], ["The Abbé Busoni did right to send you to me,”", "부소니 사제는 자네를 내게 보낸 게 옳았어.\""], ["he went on in his ordinary tone, “and you have done well in relating to me the whole of your history, as it will prevent my forming any erroneous opinions concerning you in future.", "그가 다시 평소의 어조로 말을 이었다. \"그리고 자네는 자기 이야기를 전부 들려준 게 잘한 일이야. 앞으로 자네에 대해 내가 그릇된 판단을 내리지 않게 해 줄 테니까."], ["As for that Benedetto, who so grossly belied his name, have you never made any effort to trace out whither he has gone, or what has become of him?”", "그 이름을 그토록 욕보인 베네데토 말인데, 그놈이 어디로 갔는지, 어찌 되었는지 알아내려고 애써 본 적은 한 번도 없나?\""]], [["“No; far from wishing to learn whither he has betaken himself, I should shun the possibility of meeting him as I would a wild beast.", "\"없습니다. 그놈이 어디로 갔는지 알고 싶기는커녕, 저는 맹수를 피하듯 그놈과 마주칠 가능성마저 피하려 들 겁니다."], ["Thank God, I have never heard his name mentioned by any person, and I hope and believe he is dead.”", "다행히 저는 누구한테서도 그놈의 이름이 입에 오르는 것을 들은 적이 없고, 그놈이 죽었기를 바라며 또 그렇게 믿습니다.\""]], [["“Do not think so, Bertuccio,”", "\"그렇게 생각지 말게, 베르투치오.\""], ["replied the count; “for the wicked are not so easily disposed of, for God seems to have them under his special watch-care to make of them instruments of his vengeance.”", "백작이 대답했다. \"악인이란 그리 쉽사리 없어지지 않는 법이니까. 신께서는 그들을 당신 복수의 도구로 쓰시려고 특별히 굽어살피시는 듯하거든.\""]], [["“So be it,”", "\"그렇다면 그런 거겠지요.\""], ["responded Bertuccio, “all I ask of heaven is that I may never see him again.", "베르투치오가 대답했다. \"제가 하늘에 바라는 것이라곤 다시는 그놈을 보지 않는 것뿐입니다. 그리고 이제 나리,\""], ["And now, your excellency,”", "그가 고개를 숙이며 덧붙였다."], ["he added, bowing his head, “you know everything—you are my judge on earth, as the Almighty is in heaven; have you for me no words of consolation?”", "\"나리께서는 모든 것을 아십니다. 전능하신 분이 하늘에서 그러하시듯, 나리는 이 땅에서 저를 심판하시는 분입니다. 저를 위한 위로의 말씀은 없으신지요?\""]], [["“My good friend, I can only repeat the words addressed to you by the Abbé Busoni.", "\"이보게, 나는 부소니 사제가 자네에게 한 말을 되풀이할 수 있을 뿐이네."], ["Villefort merited punishment for what he had done to you, and, perhaps, to others.", "빌포르는 자네에게, 그리고 어쩌면 다른 이들에게도 저지른 일로 마땅히 벌을 받아야 했지."], ["Benedetto, if still living, will become the instrument of divine retribution in some way or other, and then be duly punished in his turn.", "베네데토는 아직 살아 있다면 어떤 식으로든 신의 응징의 도구가 될 것이고, 그런 다음 제 차례가 되어 응당한 벌을 받을 걸세."], ["As far as you yourself are concerned, I see but one point in which you are really guilty.", "자네 자신으로 말하자면, 자네가 정말로 죄를 지은 대목은 단 하나밖에 보이지 않네."], ["Ask yourself, wherefore, after rescuing the infant from its living grave, you did not restore it to its mother?", "스스로에게 물어보게. 그 갓난아이를 산 무덤에서 구해 낸 뒤, 어찌하여 그 어미에게 돌려주지 않았는가를."], ["There was the crime, Bertuccio—that was where you became really culpable.”", "거기에 죄가 있었네, 베르투치오. 바로 거기서 자네는 정말로 죄인이 된 거야.\""]], [["“True, excellency, that was the crime, the real crime, for in that I acted like a coward.", "\"옳은 말씀입니다, 나리. 그것이 죄였습니다. 진짜 죄였지요."], ["My first duty, directly I had succeeded in recalling the babe to life, was to restore it to its mother; but, in order to do so, I must have made close and careful inquiry, which would, in all probability, have led to my own apprehension; and I clung to life, partly on my sister’s account, and partly from that feeling of pride inborn in our hearts of desiring to come off untouched and victorious in the execution of our vengeance.", "거기서 저는 비겁자처럼 굴었으니까요. 갓난아이를 되살려 내는 데 성공한 그 즉시, 제 첫 번째 의무는 그 아이를 어미에게 돌려주는 것이었습니다. 그러나 그러자면 면밀하고 조심스러운 수소문을 거쳐야 했을 테고, 그러다 보면 십중팔구 제가 붙잡히는 결과로 이어졌을 겁니다."], ["Perhaps, too, the natural and instinctive love of life made me wish to avoid endangering my own.", "게다가 저는 목숨에 매달려 있었습니다. 한편으로는 누이 때문이었고, 또 한편으로는 우리 마음속에 타고난 그 자존심 때문이었지요. 복수를 마치고도 털끝 하나 다치지 않은 채 의기양양하게 살아남고 싶다는 그 마음 말입니다."], ["And then, again, I am not as brave and courageous as was my poor brother.”", "또 어쩌면 누구에게나 타고난 본능적인 삶의 애착이 제 목숨을 위태롭게 하기를 꺼리게 했는지도 모릅니다. 그리고 거기다, 저는 제 가엾은 형만큼 용감하고 담대하지도 못했고요.\""]], [["Bertuccio hid his face in his hands as he uttered these words, while Monte Cristo fixed on him a look of inscrutable meaning.", "베르투치오는 이 말을 내뱉으며 두 손에 얼굴을 묻었고, 몬테 크리스토는 헤아릴 수 없는 의미를 담은 눈길로 그를 응시했다."], ["After a brief silence, rendered still more solemn by the time and place, the count said, in a tone of melancholy wholly unlike his usual manner:", "시각과 장소 탓에 한층 엄숙해진 짧은 침묵 뒤에, 백작이 평소와는 사뭇 다른 우수에 잠긴 어조로 말했다."]], [["“In order to bring this conversation to a fitting termination (the last we shall ever hold upon this subject), I will repeat to you some words I have heard from the lips of the Abbé Busoni.", "\"이 대화를 알맞게 매듭짓기 위해(이 일을 두고 나누는 마지막 대화가 될 테니), 부소니 사제의 입에서 들은 몇 마디를 자네에게 되풀이해 주겠네."], ["For all evils there are two remedies—time and silence.", "모든 불행에는 두 가지 약이 있다. 시간과 침묵이지."], ["And now leave me, Monsieur Bertuccio, to walk alone here in the garden.", "자, 베르투치오 씨, 이제 나 혼자 이 정원을 거닐게 두고 물러가게."], ["The very circumstances which inflict on you, as a principal in the tragic scene enacted here, such painful emotions, are to me, on the contrary, a source of something like contentment, and serve but to enhance the value of this dwelling in my estimation.", "여기서 벌어진 비극의 장본인인 자네에게 그토록 고통스러운 감정을 안기는 바로 그 정황들이, 내게는 도리어 일종의 만족과도 같은 것의 원천이 되고, 이 집의 가치를 내 눈에 더욱 높여 줄 뿐일세."], ["The chief beauty of trees consists in the deep shadow of their umbrageous boughs, while fancy pictures a moving multitude of shapes and forms flitting and passing beneath that shade.", "나무의 으뜸가는 아름다움은 그 우거진 가지가 드리우는 짙은 그늘에 있는 법이고, 그러는 동안 상상은 그 그늘 아래로 어른거리며 스쳐 지나가는 갖가지 형상과 모습의 무리를 그려 내는 법이지."], ["Here I have a garden laid out in such a way as to afford the fullest scope for the imagination, and furnished with thickly grown trees, beneath whose leafy screen a visionary like myself may conjure up phantoms at will.", "여기 내게는 상상이 한껏 펼쳐질 수 있도록 꾸며진 정원이 있고, 빽빽이 우거진 나무들이 들어찼으니, 나 같은 몽상가는 그 잎의 장막 아래서 마음껏 환영을 불러낼 수 있다네."], ["This to me, who expected but to find a blank enclosure surrounded by a straight wall, is, I assure you, a most agreeable surprise.", "곧은 담장으로 둘러싸인 텅 빈 마당이나 보게 되리라 예상했던 내게는, 분명히 말하지만 더없이 흐뭇한 뜻밖의 선물일세."], ["I have no fear of ghosts, and I have never heard it said that so much harm had been done by the dead during six thousand years as is wrought by the living in a single day.", "나는 유령을 두려워하지 않거니와, 죽은 자들이 육천 년 동안 끼친 해악이 산 자들이 단 하루에 저지르는 해악만큼 크다는 말은 들어 본 적이 없네."], ["Retire within, Bertuccio, and tranquillize your mind.", "안으로 들어가게, 베르투치오, 그리고 마음을 가라앉히게."], ["Should your confessor be less indulgent to you in your dying moments than you found the Abbé Busoni, send for me, if I am still on earth, and I will soothe your ears with words that shall effectually calm and soothe your parting soul ere it goes forth to traverse the ocean called eternity.”", "임종의 순간에 자네의 고해 신부가 부소니 사제만큼 자네에게 너그럽지 못하거든, 내가 아직 이 땅에 있거든 나를 부르게. 그러면 자네의 떠나는 영혼이 영원이라는 대양을 가로질러 나아가기에 앞서, 그것을 어김없이 잠재우고 달래 줄 말로 자네의 귀를 어루만져 주겠네.\""]], [["Bertuccio bowed respectfully, and turned away, sighing heavily.", "베르투치오는 공손히 절하고는 무겁게 한숨지으며 돌아섰다."], ["Monte Cristo, left alone, took three or four steps onwards, and murmured:", "홀로 남은 몬테 크리스토는 서너 걸음 앞으로 나아가며 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Here, beneath this plane-tree, must have been where the infant’s grave was dug.", "\"여기, 이 플라타너스 아래가 그 갓난아이의 무덤을 팠던 자리가 틀림없어."], ["There is the little door opening into the garden.", "저기 정원으로 통하는 작은 문이 있고."], ["At this corner is the private staircase communicating with the sleeping apartment.", "이 모퉁이에는 침실로 이어지는 은밀한 계단이 있지."], ["There will be no necessity for me to make a note of these particulars, for there, before my eyes, beneath my feet, all around me, I have the plan sketched with all the living reality of truth.”", "이런 자질구레한 것들을 굳이 적어 둘 필요는 없겠군. 저기, 내 눈앞에, 내 발밑에, 내 주위 사방에, 그 도면이 살아 있는 진실 그대로 그려져 있으니까.\""]], [["After making the tour of the garden a second time, the count re-entered his carriage, while Bertuccio, who perceived the thoughtful expression of his master’s features, took his seat beside the driver without uttering a word.", "정원을 한 차례 더 돌아본 뒤, 백작은 다시 마차에 올랐다. 주인의 표정에 어린 깊은 상념을 알아챈 베르투치오는 한마디도 하지 않고 마부 옆자리에 앉았다."], ["The carriage proceeded rapidly towards Paris.", "마차는 파리를 향해 빠르게 나아갔다."]], [["That same evening, upon reaching his abode in the Champs-Élysées, the Count of Monte Cristo went over the whole building with the air of one long acquainted with each nook or corner.", "그날 저녁, 샹젤리제의 거처에 다다른 몬테 크리스토 백작은 마치 구석구석을 오래전부터 훤히 알고 있는 사람의 태도로 건물 전체를 둘러보았다."], ["Nor, although preceding the party, did he once mistake one door for another, or commit the smallest error when choosing any particular corridor or staircase to conduct him to a place or suite of rooms he desired to visit.", "일행보다 앞장서 걸으면서도, 그는 한 번도 문을 헷갈리거나, 가고자 하는 방이나 방들이 늘어선 곳으로 데려다줄 복도며 계단을 고를 때 사소한 실수 하나 저지르지 않았다."], ["Ali was his principal attendant during this nocturnal survey.", "이 야간 점검 동안 그의 곁을 시중든 것은 주로 알리였다."], ["Having given various orders to Bertuccio relative to the improvements and alterations he desired to make in the house, the Count, drawing out his watch, said to the attentive Nubian:", "백작은 집에 하고 싶은 개수와 변경에 관해 베르투치오에게 이런저런 지시를 내린 다음, 회중시계를 꺼내 들고 곁에서 대기 중인 누비아인에게 말했다."]], [["“It is half-past eleven o’clock; Haydée will soon be here.", "\"열한 시 반이로군. 하이데가 곧 도착할 게다."], ["Have the French attendants been summoned to await her coming?”", "프랑스인 시중꾼들에게 마중 나와 기다리라고 일렀느냐?\""]], [["Ali extended his hands towards the apartments destined for the fair Greek, which were so effectually concealed by means of a tapestried entrance, that it would have puzzled the most curious to have divined their existence.", "알리가 아름다운 그리스 여인을 위해 마련된 방들 쪽으로 두 손을 뻗었다. 그 방들은 융단을 드리운 입구로 어찌나 감쪽같이 가려져 있던지, 아무리 호기심 많은 사람이라도 그 존재를 알아채기 어려웠을 것이다."], ["Ali, having pointed to the apartments, held up three fingers of his right hand, and then, placing it beneath his head, shut his eyes, and feigned to sleep.", "그 방들을 가리킨 알리는 오른손 손가락 셋을 펴 보이고는, 그 손을 머리 밑에 받친 채 눈을 감고 자는 시늉을 했다."]], [["“I understand,”", "\"알겠다.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, well acquainted with Ali’s pantomime; “you mean to tell me that three female attendants await their new mistress in her sleeping-chamber.”", "알리의 손짓에 익숙한 몬테 크리스토가 말했다. \"시중드는 여인 셋이 침실에서 새 안주인을 기다리고 있다는 말이구나.\""]], [["Ali, with considerable animation, made a sign in the affirmative.", "알리가 자못 활기차게 그렇다는 시늉을 했다."]], [["“Madame will be tired tonight,”", "\"마님은 오늘 밤 피곤할 게다.\""], ["continued Monte Cristo, “and will, no doubt, wish to rest.", "몬테 크리스토가 말을 이었다. \"분명 쉬고 싶어 할 테고."], ["Desire the French attendants not to weary her with questions, but merely to pay their respectful duty and retire.", "프랑스인 시중꾼들에게 질문 따위로 마님을 성가시게 하지 말고, 그저 공손히 예를 갖춘 다음 물러가라고 일러라."], ["You will also see that the Greek servants hold no communication with those of this country.”", "그리고 그리스인 하인들이 이 나라 하인들과는 일절 왕래하지 않도록 살피거라.\""]], [["He bowed. Just at that moment voices were heard hailing the concierge.", "알리가 절을 했다. 바로 그때 문지기를 부르는 목소리가 들렸다."], ["The gate opened, a carriage rolled down the avenue, and stopped at the steps.", "대문이 열리고, 마차 한 대가 가로수 길을 굴러 내려와 계단 앞에 멈춰 섰다."], ["The count hastily descended, presented himself at the already opened carriage door, and held out his hand to a young woman, completely enveloped in a green silk mantle heavily embroidered with gold.", "백작이 황급히 내려가, 이미 열린 마차 문 앞에 나서서 한 젊은 여인에게 손을 내밀었다. 그녀는 금실로 화려하게 수놓은 초록빛 비단 외투에 온몸을 폭 감싸고 있었다."], ["She raised the hand extended towards her to her lips, and kissed it with a mixture of love and respect.", "그녀는 자기 쪽으로 내밀어진 손을 들어 입술로 가져가, 사랑과 존경이 뒤섞인 입맞춤을 했다."], ["Some few words passed between them in that sonorous language in which Homer makes his gods converse.", "호메로스가 신들에게 나누게 한 그 낭랑한 언어로 두 사람 사이에 몇 마디가 오갔다."], ["The young woman spoke with an expression of deep tenderness, while the count replied with an air of gentle gravity.", "젊은 여인은 깊은 다정함이 어린 어조로 말했고, 백작은 부드럽되 진중한 태도로 대답했다."]], [["Preceded by Ali, who carried a rose-colored flambeau in his hand, the young lady, who was no other than the lovely Greek who had been Monte Cristo’s companion in Italy, was conducted to her apartments, while the count retired to the pavilion reserved for himself.", "장밋빛 횃불을 손에 든 알리를 앞세우고, 그 젊은 여인은 방으로 안내되었다. 그녀는 다름 아닌, 이탈리아에서 몬테 크리스토와 함께 다니던 그 사랑스러운 그리스 여인이었다. 백작은 자기를 위해 마련된 별채로 물러갔다."], ["In another hour every light in the house was extinguished, and it might have been thought that all its inmates slept.", "한 시간 뒤에는 집 안의 모든 불이 꺼졌고, 그 안의 모든 사람이 잠든 것이라 여겨도 좋았을 것이다."]], [["# Chapter 46. Unlimited Credit", "# Chapter 46. Unlimited Credit"]], [["About two o’clock the following day a calash, drawn by a pair of magnificent English horses, stopped at the door of Monte Cristo and a person, dressed in a blue coat, with buttons of a similar color, a white waistcoat, over which was displayed a massive gold chain, brown trousers, and a quantity of black hair descending so low over his eyebrows as to leave it doubtful whether it were not artificial so little did its jetty glossiness assimilate with the deep wrinkles stamped on his features—a person, in a word, who, although evidently past fifty, desired to be taken for not more than forty, bent forwards from the carriage door, on the panels of which were emblazoned the armorial bearings of a baron, and directed his groom to inquire at the porter’s lodge whether the Count of Monte Cristo resided there, and if he were within.", "이튿날 두 시쯤, 멋진 영국산 말 두 필이 끄는 사륜마차 한 대가 몬테크리스토 저택 문 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 마차에서 한 사내가 마차 문 너머로 몸을 내밀었다. 그는 같은 색 단추가 달린 파란 외투를 걸쳤고, 흰 조끼 위로 묵직한 금사슬을 늘어뜨렸으며, 갈색 바지를 입고 있었다. 검은 머리카락이 눈썹 위까지 어찌나 낮게 내려와 있는지, 그 칠흑 같은 윤기가 얼굴에 깊이 패인 주름과 너무도 어울리지 않아 가발이 아닐까 싶을 정도였다. 한마디로, 분명 쉰은 넘었으면서도 마흔밖에 안 돼 보이기를 바라는 사내였다. 마차 문짝에는 남작의 문장이 새겨져 있었다. 그는 마부에게, 수위실에 들러 몬테크리스토 백작이 여기 사는지, 또 지금 안에 있는지 물어보라고 일렀다."]], [["While waiting, the occupant of the carriage surveyed the house, the garden as far as he could distinguish it, and the livery of servants who passed to and fro, with an attention so close as to be somewhat impertinent. His glance was keen but showed cunning rather than intelligence; his lips were straight, and so thin that, as they closed, they were drawn in over the teeth; his cheek-bones were broad and projecting, a never-failing proof of audacity and craftiness; while the flatness of his forehead, and the enlargement of the back of his skull, which rose much higher than his large and coarsely shaped ears, combined to form a physiognomy anything but prepossessing, save in the eyes of such as considered that the owner of so splendid an equipage must needs be all that was admirable and enviable, more especially when they gazed on the enormous diamond that glittered in his shirt, and the red ribbon that depended from his button-hole.", "기다리는 동안 마차 안의 사내는 저택과, 눈에 들어오는 데까지의 정원과, 이리저리 오가는 하인들의 제복을 다소 무례하다 싶을 만큼 빈틈없이 살펴보았다. 그의 눈길은 날카로웠으나 영민함보다는 교활함이 묻어났다. 입술은 일자로 가늘어, 다물면 이 위로 말려 들어갔다. 광대뼈는 넓고 튀어나와 있었는데, 이는 대담함과 음흉함의 어김없는 증표였다. 게다가 이마는 납작하고 뒤통수는 불룩 솟아, 크고 거칠게 생긴 귀보다도 훨씬 높았다. 이 모든 것이 어우러져, 도무지 호감이 가지 않는 인상을 이루었다. 다만 이렇게 으리으리한 마차를 가진 사람이라면 무엇이든 감탄스럽고 부러울 만한 인물이 틀림없다고 여기는 부류의 눈에는 달랐다. 더구나 그의 셔츠에서 번쩍이는 거대한 다이아몬드와 단춧구멍에 늘어뜨린 붉은 리본을 보면 더욱 그러했다."]], [["The groom, in obedience to his orders, tapped at the window of the porter’s lodge, saying:", "마부는 지시에 따라 수위실 창을 두드리며 말했다."]], [["“Pray, does not the Count of Monte Cristo live here?”", "\"실례합니다, 여기가 몬테크리스토 백작님 댁입니까?\""]], [["“His excellency does reside here,”", "\"각하께서는 여기 사십니다.\""], ["replied the concierge; “but——”", "수위가 대답했다. \"하지만……\""], ["added he, glancing an inquiring look at Ali.", "그러고는 알리에게 묻는 듯한 눈길을 던졌다."], ["Ali returned a sign in the negative.", "알리는 안 된다는 표시로 고개를 저었다."]], [["“But what?” asked the groom.", "\"하지만 뭡니까?\" 마부가 물었다."]], [["“His excellency does not receive visitors today.”", "\"각하께서는 오늘 손님을 받지 않으십니다.\""]], [["“Then here is my master’s card, the Baron Danglars.", "\"그렇다면 여기 제 주인님 명함을 두고 가겠습니다."], ["You will take it to the count, and say that, although in haste to attend the Chamber, my master came out of his way to have the honor of calling upon him.”", "당글라르 남작이십니다. 백작님께 이 명함을 전하면서, 주인님이 의회에 가시는 길에도 일부러 들러 백작님을 뵙는 영광을 누리고자 하셨다고 전해 주십시오.\""]], [["“I never speak to his excellency,”", "\"저는 각하께 직접 말씀을 올리지 않습니다.\""], ["replied the concierge; “the valet de chambre will carry your message.”", "수위가 대답했다. \"시종이 전갈을 전할 겁니다.\""]], [["“Well?”", "“그래서?”"], ["asked Danglars.", "당글라르가 물었다."]], [["The man, somewhat crest-fallen by the rebuke he had received, repeated what the concierge had said.", "\"어떻게 됐나?\" 당글라르가 물었다. 핀잔을 들어 다소 풀이 죽은 사내는 수위가 한 말을 그대로 옮겼다."]], [["“Bless me,”", "\"이런.\""], ["murmured Baron Danglars, “this must surely be a prince instead of a count by their styling him ‘excellency,’ and only venturing to address him by the medium of his valet de chambre.", "당글라르 남작이 중얼거렸다. \"백작을 두고 '각하'라 부르고, 감히 시종을 통해서만 말을 전할 수 있다니, 이건 백작이 아니라 분명 무슨 왕족이 틀림없군."], ["However, it does not signify; he has a letter of credit on me, so I must see him when he requires his money.”", "하지만 상관없어. 그는 내게 신용장을 가지고 있으니, 돈이 필요하면 결국 나를 만나야 할 테지.\""]], [["Then, throwing himself back in his carriage, Danglars called out to his coachman, in a voice that might be heard across the road, “To the Chamber of Deputies.”", "그러고는 마차에 몸을 깊이 기대며, 길 건너까지 들릴 만한 소리로 마부에게 외쳤다. \"하원으로.\""]], [["Apprised in time of the visit paid him, Monte Cristo had, from behind the blinds of his pavilion, as minutely observed the baron, by means of an excellent lorgnette, as Danglars himself had scrutinized the house, garden, and servants.", "자신을 찾아온 손님을 미리 전해 들은 몬테크리스토는 별채의 덧창 뒤에서 훌륭한 오페라글라스로 남작을 샅샅이 살펴보았다. 당글라르가 저택과 정원과 하인들을 뜯어본 것과 꼭 마찬가지였다."]], [["“That fellow has a decidedly bad countenance,”", "\"저 작자, 얼굴이 영 좋지 않군.\""], ["said the count in a tone of disgust, as he shut up his glass into its ivory case.", "백작이 오페라글라스를 상아 갑에 도로 넣으며 역겹다는 듯이 말했다."], ["“How comes it that all do not retreat in aversion at sight of that flat, receding, serpent-like forehead, round, vulture-shaped head, and sharp-hooked nose, like the beak of a buzzard? Ali,”", "\"저 납작하게 들어간 뱀 같은 이마, 둥글둥글한 독수리 머리통, 매부리처럼 끝이 뾰족하게 휘어진 코를 보고도 다들 질겁해 달아나지 않다니 어찌 된 노릇인가? 알리.\""], ["cried he, striking at the same time on the brazen gong.", "그러면서 동시에 놋쇠 징을 쳤다. 알리가 나타났다."], ["Ali appeared. “Summon Bertuccio,”", "\"베르투치오를 불러라.\""], ["said the count.", "백작이 말했다."], ["Almost immediately Bertuccio entered the apartment.", "거의 곧바로 베르투치오가 방으로 들어왔다."]], [["“Did your excellency desire to see me?” inquired he.", "\"부르셨습니까, 각하?\" 그가 물었다."]], [["“I did,” replied the count.", "\"불렀네.\" 백작이 대답했다."], ["“You no doubt observed the horses standing a few minutes since at the door?”", "\"조금 전 문 앞에 서 있던 말들을 보았겠지?\""]], [["“Certainly, your excellency.", "\"물론입니다, 각하."], ["I noticed them for their remarkable beauty.”", "워낙 빼어나게 아름다워 눈여겨보았습니다.\""]], [["“Then how comes it,”", "\"그렇다면 어찌 된 일인가.\""], ["said Monte Cristo with a frown, “that, when I desired you to purchase for me the finest pair of horses to be found in Paris, there is another pair, fully as fine as mine, not in my stables?”", "몬테크리스토가 이맛살을 찌푸리며 말했다. \"파리에서 구할 수 있는 가장 훌륭한 한 쌍을 사 오라고 일렀건만, 내 것 못지않게 빼어난 또 한 쌍이 내 마구간에 없으니 말일세?\""]], [["At the look of displeasure, added to the angry tone in which the count spoke, Ali turned pale and held down his head.", "백작의 노기 띤 말투에 더해 못마땅해하는 기색이 드러나자, 알리는 얼굴이 새파래져 고개를 떨구었다."]], [["“It is not your fault, my good Ali,”", "\"네 잘못이 아니다, 착한 알리야.\""], ["said the count in the Arabic language, and with a gentleness none would have thought him capable of showing, either in voice or face—“it is not your fault.", "백작이 아랍어로, 그 목소리나 표정에서 누구도 그가 보일 수 있으리라 짐작 못 할 다정함을 담아 말했다. \"네 잘못이 아니야."], ["You do not understand the points of English horses.”", "너는 영국 말을 보는 안목이 없으니까.\""]], [["The countenance of poor Ali recovered its serenity.", "가엾은 알리의 얼굴에 다시 평온이 돌아왔다."]], [["“Permit me to assure your excellency,”", "\"각하께 한 말씀 올리겠습니다.\""], ["said Bertuccio, “that the horses you speak of were not to be sold when I purchased yours.”", "베르투치오가 말했다. \"제가 각하의 말을 살 때, 말씀하신 그 말들은 팔려고 내놓은 것이 아니었습니다.\""]], [["Monte Cristo shrugged his shoulders.", "몬테크리스토는 어깨를 으쓱했다."], ["“It seems, sir steward,”", "\"이보게 집사.\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “that you have yet to learn that all things are to be sold to such as care to pay the price.”", "\"값을 치를 마음만 있다면 세상 모든 것은 살 수 있는 법인데, 자네는 아직 그걸 모르는 모양이군.\""]], [["“His excellency is not, perhaps, aware that M. Danglars gave 16,000 francs for his horses?”", "\"혹시 당글라르 씨가 그 말들에 1만 6천 프랑을 치른 것을 각하께서 모르시는지요?\""]], [["“Very well.", "\"좋아."], ["Then offer him double that sum; a banker never loses an opportunity of doubling his capital.”", "그럼 그 두 배를 부르게. 은행가란 제 자본을 곱절로 불릴 기회를 결코 놓치지 않는 법이지.\""]], [["“Is your excellency really in earnest?”", "\"각하, 정말로 진심이십니까?\""], ["inquired the steward.", "집사가 물었다."]], [["Monte Cristo regarded the person who durst presume to doubt his words with the look of one equally surprised and displeased.", "몬테크리스토는 감히 제 말을 의심하려 드는 자를, 놀라움과 불쾌함이 뒤섞인 눈빛으로 바라보았다."]], [["“I have to pay a visit this evening,”", "\"오늘 저녁에 방문할 데가 있네.\""], ["replied he. “I desire that these horses, with completely new harness, may be at the door with my carriage.”", "그가 대답했다. \"그 말들에 완전히 새 마구를 채워, 내 마차와 함께 문 앞에 대 놓도록 하게.\""]], [["Bertuccio bowed, and was about to retire; but when he reached the door, he paused, and then said, “At what o’clock does your excellency wish the carriage and horses to be ready?”", "베르투치오는 절을 하고 물러가려 했다. 그러나 문에 이르자 걸음을 멈추더니 말했다. \"마차와 말은 몇 시까지 채비를 해 두면 되겠습니까, 각하?\""]], [["“At five o’clock,”", "\"다섯 시.\""], ["replied the count.", "백작이 대답했다."]], [["“I beg your excellency’s pardon,”", "\"송구하오나, 각하.\""], ["interposed the steward in a deprecating manner, “for venturing to observe that it is already two o’clock.”", "집사가 조심스러운 투로 끼어들었다. \"감히 말씀드리자면, 지금 벌써 두 시입니다.\""]], [["“I am perfectly aware of that fact,”", "\"나도 그쯤은 잘 알고 있네.\""], ["answered Monte Cristo calmly.", "몬테크리스토가 차분히 대답했다."], ["Then, turning towards Ali, he said, “Let all the horses in my stables be led before the windows of your young lady, that she may select those she prefers for her carriage.", "그러고는 알리 쪽으로 돌아서서 말했다. \"마구간에 있는 말을 모조리 그 아가씨의 창 앞으로 끌고 가, 마차에 매고 싶은 말을 직접 고르게 하라."], ["Request her also to oblige me by saying whether it is her pleasure to dine with me; if so, let dinner be served in her apartments.", "또 나와 함께 저녁을 들겠는지도 물어봐 다오. 그러겠다면 그녀의 방으로 저녁상을 들이고."], ["Now, leave me, and desire my valet de chambre to come hither.”", "자, 이제 물러가고, 내 시종을 이리로 들라 일러라.\""]], [["Scarcely had Ali disappeared when the valet entered the chamber.", "알리가 사라지기 무섭게 시종이 방으로 들어왔다."]], [["“Monsieur Baptistin,”", "\"바티스탱 군.\""], ["said the count, “you have been in my service one year, the time I generally give myself to judge of the merits or demerits of those about me.", "백작이 말했다. \"자네가 내 밑에서 일한 지 일 년이 되었네. 내가 주위 사람의 됨됨이를 가늠하는 데 으레 두는 기간이지."], ["You suit me very well.”", "자네는 내 마음에 썩 든다네.\""]], [["Baptistin bowed low.", "바티스탱이 깊이 절했다."]], [["“It only remains for me to know whether I also suit you?”", "\"이제 남은 건, 나 또한 자네 마음에 드는지 아는 것뿐이군.\""]], [["“Oh, your excellency!”", "\"오, 각하!\""], ["exclaimed Baptistin eagerly.", "바티스탱이 다급히 외쳤다."]], [["“Listen, if you please, till I have finished speaking,”", "\"부디 내 말이 끝날 때까지 들어 주게.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다."], ["“You receive 1,500 francs per annum for your services here—more than many a brave subaltern, who continually risks his life for his country, obtains.", "\"자네는 여기서 일한 대가로 연 1천5백 프랑을 받지. 조국을 위해 끊임없이 목숨을 거는 어지간한 용감한 하급 장교가 받는 것보다도 많은 액수일세."], ["You live in a manner far superior to many clerks who work ten times harder than you do for their money.", "자네는 자네보다 열 배는 고되게 일하고도 그만한 돈을 받는 숱한 사무원들보다 훨씬 낫게 살고 있어."], ["Then, though yourself a servant, you have other servants to wait upon you, take care of your clothes, and see that your linen is duly prepared for you.", "게다가 자네 자신이 하인이면서도, 시중을 들고 옷을 챙기며 자네 속옷을 제때 마련해 주는 다른 하인들까지 거느리고 있지."], ["Again, you make a profit upon each article you purchase for my toilet, amounting in the course of a year to a sum equalling your wages.”", "또한 자네는 내 몸치장에 쓸 물건을 사 올 때마다 이문을 남기는데, 일 년이면 자네 봉급에 맞먹는 액수가 되더군.\""]], [["“Nay, indeed, your excellency.”", "\"아니, 정말로, 각하……\""]], [["“I am not condemning you for this, Monsieur Baptistin; but let your profits end here.", "\"내가 그 일로 자네를 나무라는 건 아니네, 바티스탱 군."], ["It would be long indeed ere you would find so lucrative a post as that you have now the good fortune to fill.", "다만 이문은 거기서 그치게. 자네가 지금 누리는 이 자리만큼 짭짤한 일자리는 좀처럼 다시 못 찾을 테니까."], ["I neither ill-use nor ill-treat my servants by word or action.", "나는 말로든 행동으로든 하인을 함부로 대하거나 학대하지 않아."], ["An error I readily forgive, but wilful negligence or forgetfulness, never.", "실수는 기꺼이 용서하지만, 고의로 게으름을 부리거나 잊어버리는 것은 결코 용서치 않네."], ["My commands are ordinarily short, clear, and precise; and I would rather be obliged to repeat my words twice, or even three times, than they should be misunderstood.", "내 명령은 으레 짧고 분명하며 정확하지. 그리고 내 말이 잘못 전해지느니, 차라리 두 번, 세 번 거듭 말하는 편이 낫다고 여긴다네."], ["I am rich enough to know whatever I desire to know, and I can promise you I am not wanting in curiosity.", "나는 알고자 하는 것은 무엇이든 알아낼 만큼 부유하고, 호기심도 적잖다는 걸 약속하지."], ["If, then, I should learn that you had taken upon yourself to speak of me to anyone favorably or unfavorably, to comment on my actions, or watch my conduct, that very instant you would quit my service.", "그러니 만약 자네가 나를 두고 누구에게든 좋게든 나쁘게든 떠벌리거나, 내 행동을 입에 올리거나, 내 처신을 엿보았다는 걸 알게 되면, 그 즉시 자네는 내 곁을 떠나게 될 걸세."], ["You may now retire.", "이제 물러가도 좋네."], ["I never caution my servants a second time—remember that.”", "나는 하인에게 두 번 다시 경고하지 않으니, 그 점을 명심하게.\""]], [["Baptistin bowed, and was proceeding towards the door.", "바티스탱이 절을 하고 문 쪽으로 걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["“I forgot to mention to you,”", "\"한 가지 일러두는 걸 깜빡했군.\" 백작이 말했다."], ["said the count, “that I lay yearly aside a certain sum for each servant in my establishment; those whom I am compelled to dismiss lose (as a matter of course) all participation in this money, while their portion goes to the fund accumulating for those domestics who remain with me, and among whom it will be divided at my death.", "\"나는 해마다 내 집의 하인 하나하나를 위해 일정한 돈을 따로 떼어 두네. 부득이 내보내야 하는 자는 당연히 그 돈에 대한 몫을 모두 잃고, 그 몫은 내 곁에 남는 하인들을 위해 쌓이는 기금으로 들어가지. 그래서 내가 죽으면 그들 사이에 나뉠 걸세."], ["You have been in my service a year, your fund has already begun to accumulate—let it continue to do so.”", "자네는 내 밑에서 일한 지 일 년이 되어 자네 몫의 기금이 이미 쌓이기 시작했으니, 그대로 계속 불려 가게나.\""]], [["This address, delivered in the presence of Ali, who, not understanding one word of the language in which it was spoken, stood wholly unmoved, produced an effect on M.", "이 말은 알리가 보는 앞에서 이루어졌다. 알리는 그 말이 오간 언어를 한마디도 알아듣지 못해 미동도 없이 서 있었다."], ["Baptistin only to be conceived by such as have occasion to study the character and disposition of French domestics.", "그러나 바티스탱에게는, 프랑스 하인의 성정과 기질을 살펴본 적 있는 자라야 짐작할 만한 효과를 일으켰다."]], [["“I assure your excellency,”", "\"각하께 약속드리겠습니다.\""], ["said he, “that at least it shall be my study to merit your approbation in all things, and I will take M. Ali as my model.”", "그가 말했다. \"적어도 매사에 각하의 인정을 받도록 힘쓰겠으며, 알리 씨를 본보기로 삼겠습니다.\""]], [["“By no means,”", "\"천만에.\""], ["replied the count in the most frigid tones; “Ali has many faults mixed with most excellent qualities.", "백작이 더없이 차가운 어조로 대답했다. \"알리는 더없이 빼어난 자질에 못지않게 결점도 많은 자일세. 자네에게 처신의 본보기가 될 수 없지."], ["He cannot possibly serve you as a pattern for your conduct, not being, as you are, a paid servant, but a mere slave—a dog, who, should he fail in his duty towards me, I should not discharge from my service, but kill.”", "그는 자네처럼 봉급을 받는 하인이 아니라 한낱 노예니까. 한 마리 개일세. 만약 그가 내게 주어진 임무를 저버린다면, 나는 그를 내쫓는 게 아니라 죽일 걸세.\""]], [["Baptistin opened his eyes with astonishment.", "바티스탱은 놀라 눈을 휘둥그레 떴다."]], [["“You seem incredulous,”", "\"못 믿겠다는 얼굴이군.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, who repeated to Ali in the Arabic language what he had just been saying to Baptistin in French.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. 그러고는 방금 프랑스어로 바티스탱에게 한 말을 알리에게 아랍어로 되풀이해 일러 주었다."]], [["The Nubian smiled assentingly to his master’s words, then, kneeling on one knee, respectfully kissed the hand of the count.", "누비아인은 주인의 말에 동의하듯 미소 지었다. 그러더니 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고 백작의 손에 공손히 입을 맞추었다."], ["This corroboration of the lesson he had just received put the finishing stroke to the wonder and stupefaction of M.", "방금 들은 그 말을 이렇게 뒷받침하는 광경은, 바티스탱의 경악과 어리둥절함에 마지막 쐐기를 박았다."], ["Baptistin. The count then motioned the valet de chambre to retire, and to Ali to follow to his study, where they conversed long and earnestly together.", "백작은 시종에게 물러가라는 손짓을 하고, 알리에게는 서재로 따라오라 일렀다. 두 사람은 그곳에서 오래도록 진지하게 이야기를 나누었다."], ["As the hand of the clock pointed to five the count struck thrice upon his gong.", "시곗바늘이 다섯 시를 가리키자 백작은 징을 세 번 쳤다."], ["When Ali was wanted one stroke was given, two summoned Baptistin, and three Bertuccio.", "알리를 부를 때는 한 번, 바티스탱은 두 번, 베르투치오는 세 번 치는 것이었다."], ["The steward entered.", "집사가 들어왔다."]], [["“My horses,”", "\"내 말은.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“They are at the door harnessed to the carriage as your excellency desired.", "\"각하께서 분부하신 대로 마차에 매여 문 앞에 대 놓았습니다."], ["Does your excellency wish me to accompany him?”", "제가 모시고 갈까요, 각하?\""]], [["“No, the coachman, Ali, and Baptistin will go.”", "\"아니, 마부와 알리, 바티스탱이 갈 걸세.\""]], [["The count descended to the door of his mansion, and beheld his carriage drawn by the very pair of horses he had so much admired in the morning as the property of Danglars. As he passed them he said:", "백작은 저택 문 앞으로 내려가, 아침에 당글라르의 것이라며 그토록 감탄해 마지않던 바로 그 한 쌍의 말이 끄는 마차를 보았다. 그 곁을 지나며 그가 말했다."]], [["“They are extremely handsome certainly, and you have done well to purchase them, although you were somewhat remiss not to have procured them sooner.”", "\"확실히 더없이 잘생긴 놈들이군. 자네가 잘 사 왔어. 다만 좀 더 일찍 손에 넣지 못한 것은 다소 굼떴다 하겠네.\""]], [["“Indeed, your excellency, I had very considerable difficulty in obtaining them, and, as it is, they have cost an enormous price.”", "\"실은, 각하, 이 말들을 손에 넣느라 적잖이 애를 먹었습니다. 그러고도 엄청난 값을 치렀고요.\""]], [["“Does the sum you gave for them make the animals less beautiful,”", "\"자네가 치른 그 값 때문에 이 짐승들이 덜 아름다워지기라도 하나?\""], ["inquired the count, shrugging his shoulders.", "백작이 어깨를 으쓱하며 물었다."]], [["“Nay, if your excellency is satisfied, it is all that I could wish.", "\"아닙니다. 각하께서 흡족하시다면 더 바랄 게 없습니다."], ["Whither does your excellency desire to be driven?”", "어디로 모실까요, 각하?\""]], [["“To the residence of Baron Danglars, Rue de la Chaussée d’Antin.”", "\"당글라르 남작 댁으로. 쇼세당탱가일세.\""]], [["This conversation had passed as they stood upon the terrace, from which a flight of stone steps led to the carriage-drive.", "이 대화는 두 사람이 테라스에 서 있는 동안 오간 것이었다. 테라스에서 돌계단을 내려가면 마찻길이 나 있었다."], ["As Bertuccio, with a respectful bow, was moving away, the count called him back.", "베르투치오가 공손히 절하고 물러서려는데, 백작이 그를 다시 불렀다."]], [["“I have another commission for you, M. Bertuccio,”", "\"자네에게 맡길 일이 또 하나 있네, 베르투치오.\""], ["said he; “I am desirous of having an estate by the seaside in Normandy—for instance, between Le Havre and Boulogne.", "그가 말했다. \"노르망디 바닷가에 영지를 하나 가지고 싶군. 이를테면 르아브르와 불로뉴 사이쯤이면 좋겠어."], ["You see I give you a wide range.", "보다시피 자네에게 폭넓게 맡기는 거야."], ["It will be absolutely necessary that the place you may select have a small harbor, creek, or bay, into which my corvette can enter and remain at anchor. She draws only fifteen feet.", "다만 자네가 고르는 곳에는 반드시 작은 항구나 후미, 만이 있어서, 내 코르벳함이 들어와 닻을 내릴 수 있어야 하네. 흘수가 15피트밖에 안 되는 배일세."], ["She must be kept in constant readiness to sail immediately I think proper to give the signal.", "내가 적당하다 싶어 신호를 보내면 곧장 출항할 수 있도록 늘 채비를 갖춰 두어야 해."], ["Make the requisite inquiries for a place of this description, and when you have met with an eligible spot, visit it, and if it possess the advantages desired, purchase it at once in your own name.", "그런 곳을 찾도록 필요한 데를 두루 알아보고, 마땅한 자리를 만나거든 직접 가 보게. 바라던 이점이 갖춰져 있거든 곧바로 자네 이름으로 사들이게나."], ["The corvette must now, I think, be on her way to Fécamp, must she not?” 20333m", "코르벳함은 지금쯤 페캉으로 가고 있을 텐데, 그렇지 않나?\""]], [["“Certainly, your excellency; I saw her put to sea the same evening we quitted Marseilles.”", "\"그렇습니다, 각하. 우리가 마르세유를 떠난 바로 그날 저녁 배가 바다로 나가는 걸 제가 보았습니다.\""]], [["“And the yacht.”", "\"그럼 요트는?\""]], [["“Was ordered to remain at Martigues.”", "\"마르티그에 머물도록 일러 두었습니다.\""]], [["“’Tis well.", "\"잘했네."], ["I wish you to write from time to time to the captains in charge of the two vessels so as to keep them on the alert.”", "자네가 이따금 그 두 배의 선장에게 편지를 보내, 늘 긴장을 풀지 않게 해 주었으면 하네.\""]], [["“And the steamboat?”", "\"증기선은요?\""]], [["“She is at Châlons?”", "\"샬롱에 있나?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“The same orders for her as for the two sailing vessels.”", "\"그 배에도 두 범선과 같은 지시를 내리게.\""]], [["“Very good.”", "\"알겠습니다.\""]], [["“When you have purchased the estate I desire, I want constant relays of horses at ten leagues apart along the northern and southern road.”", "\"내가 바라는 영지를 사들이거든, 북부와 남부 도로를 따라 10리외마다 갈아탈 말을 늘 마련해 두게.\""]], [["“Your excellency may depend upon me.”", "\"각하, 제게 믿고 맡기십시오.\""]], [["The Count made a gesture of satisfaction, descended the terrace steps, and sprang into his carriage, which was whirled along swiftly to the banker’s house.", "백작은 흡족하다는 몸짓을 하고는 테라스 계단을 내려가 마차에 뛰어올랐다. 마차는 은행가의 집을 향해 쏜살같이 내달렸다."]], [["Danglars was engaged at that moment, presiding over a railroad committee.", "당글라르는 그때 마침 철도 위원회를 주재하고 있었다."], ["But the meeting was nearly concluded when the name of his visitor was announced.", "그러나 손님의 이름이 알려졌을 때 회의는 거의 끝나 가던 참이었다."], ["As the count’s title sounded on his ear he rose, and addressing his colleagues, who were members of one or the other Chamber, he said:", "백작의 칭호가 귀에 들리자 그는 자리에서 일어나, 이 의회 저 의회의 의원인 동료들을 향해 말했다."]], [["“Gentlemen, pardon me for leaving you so abruptly; but a most ridiculous circumstance has occurred, which is this,—Thomson & French, the Roman bankers, have sent to me a certain person calling himself the Count of Monte Cristo, and have given him an unlimited credit with me.", "\"여러분, 이렇게 불쑥 자리를 뜨는 걸 용서하시오. 한데 우스꽝스럽기 짝이 없는 일이 생겨서 말이오. 로마의 은행 톰슨 앤드 프렌치가 몬테크리스토 백작이라는 자를 내게 보내, 우리 은행에서 무제한 신용 거래를 할 수 있게 해 주었지 뭡니까."], ["I confess this is the drollest thing I have ever met with in the course of my extensive foreign transactions, and you may readily suppose it has greatly roused my curiosity.", "솔직히 내가 폭넓게 해외 거래를 해 오면서 이렇게 익살맞은 일은 처음이라, 여러분도 짐작하시겠지만 호기심이 잔뜩 동했소이다. 오늘 아침 일부러 그 자칭 백작을 찾아갔지요."], ["I took the trouble this morning to call on the pretended count—if he were a real count he wouldn’t be so rich.", "정말 백작이라면 그렇게 부유할 리가 없을 텐데 말이오. 그런데 글쎄, 믿어지십니까, '손님을 받지 않는다'더군요."], ["But, would you believe it, ‘He was not receiving.’ So the master of Monte Cristo gives himself airs befitting a great millionaire or a capricious beauty.", "그러니 이 몬테크리스토의 주인이라는 자는 대단한 백만장자나 변덕스러운 미인이나 부릴 법한 거드름을 피우는 셈이오."], ["I made inquiries, and found that the house in the Champs-Élysées is his own property, and certainly it was very decently kept up. But,”", "알아보니 샹젤리제의 그 저택은 그자 소유고, 분명 꽤 그럴듯하게 관리되고 있더군요. 하지만,\""], ["pursued Danglars with one of his sinister smiles, “an order for unlimited credit calls for something like caution on the part of the banker to whom that order is given.", "당글라르가 음흉한 미소를 띠며 말을 이었다. \"무제한 신용 거래를 허락하는 지시서라는 건, 그걸 받은 은행가로서는 어느 정도 조심하지 않을 수 없는 일이지요."], ["I am very anxious to see this man.", "나는 이 사내를 몹시 보고 싶소이다."], ["I suspect a hoax is intended, but the instigators of it little knew whom they had to deal with. ‘They laugh best who laugh last!’”", "무슨 속임수를 꾸미는 게 아닌가 싶지만, 그걸 꾸민 자들은 자기네가 누굴 상대하는지 까맣게 몰랐던 게요. '마지막에 웃는 자가 진짜 웃는 자'라 하지 않습니까!\""]], [["Having delivered himself of this pompous address, uttered with a degree of energy that left the baron almost out of breath, he bowed to the assembled party and withdrew to his drawing-room, whose sumptuous furnishings of white and gold had caused a great sensation in the Chaussée d’Antin.", "남작은 거의 숨이 찰 만큼 기세 좋게 이 거창한 연설을 늘어놓고는, 모인 이들에게 절하고 응접실로 물러났다. 흰색과 금색으로 호화롭게 꾸민 그 응접실은 쇼세당탱에서 한바탕 화제가 되었던 곳이다."], ["It was to this apartment he had desired his guest to be shown, with the purpose of overwhelming him at the sight of so much luxury.", "그가 손님을 이 방으로 들이라 한 것은, 그 으리으리한 사치에 압도시킬 셈이었다."], ["He found the count standing before some copies of Albano and Fattore that had been passed off to the banker as originals; but which, mere copies as they were, seemed to feel their degradation in being brought into juxtaposition with the gaudy colors that covered the ceiling.", "와서 보니 백작은 알바노와 파토레의 모사화 몇 점 앞에 서 있었다. 그것들은 원작이라며 은행가에게 떠넘겨진 것이었지만, 한낱 모사화일 뿐이어서, 천장을 뒤덮은 야한 색채와 나란히 놓이자 제 신세의 초라함을 느끼는 듯했다."]], [["The count turned round as he heard the entrance of Danglars into the room.", "백작은 당글라르가 방으로 들어오는 소리를 듣고 돌아섰다."], ["With a slight inclination of the head, Danglars signed to the count to be seated, pointing significantly to a gilded armchair, covered with white satin embroidered with gold.", "당글라르는 고개를 살짝 숙이며 백작에게 앉으라는 표시를 했고, 금실로 수놓은 흰 공단을 씌운 도금 안락의자를 의미심장하게 가리켰다."], ["The count sat down. 20335m", "백작이 앉았다."]], [["“I have the honor, I presume, of addressing M. de Monte Cristo.”", "\"이렇게 뵙는 영광을 누리는 분이 몬테크리스토 백작님이시겠지요.\""]], [["The count bowed.", "백작이 절했다."]], [["“And I of speaking to Baron Danglars, chevalier of the Legion of Honor, and member of the Chamber of Deputies?”", "\"그리고 제가 말씀을 나누는 분은 레지옹 도뇌르 슈발리에이자 하원 의원이신 당글라르 남작이시고요?\""]], [["Monte Cristo repeated all the titles he had read on the baron’s card.", "몬테크리스토는 남작의 명함에서 읽은 칭호를 죄다 되풀이했다."]], [["Danglars felt the irony and compressed his lips.", "당글라르는 그 비꼼을 알아채고 입술을 꾹 다물었다."]], [["“You will, I trust, excuse me, monsieur, for not calling you by your title when I first addressed you,”", "\"처음 말을 건넬 때 칭호로 부르지 못한 것을 너그러이 봐주시기 바랍니다.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said, “but you are aware that we are living under a popular form of government, and that I am myself a representative of the liberties of the people.”", "\"하지만 아시다시피 우리는 민중을 위한 정부 아래 살고 있고, 저 자신이 인민의 자유를 대변하는 사람인지라 말입니다.\""]], [["“So much so,”", "\"그렇군요.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo, “that while you call yourself baron you are not willing to call anybody else count.”", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"그래서 자신은 남작이라 부르면서도, 남은 백작이라 불러 주기를 꺼리시는 거로군요.\""]], [["“Upon my word, monsieur,”", "\"이거 참, 백작님.\" 당글라르가 짐짓 무심한 척하며 말했다."], ["said Danglars with affected carelessness, “I attach no sort of value to such empty distinctions; but the fact is, I was made baron, and also chevalier of the Legion of Honor, in return for services rendered, but——”", "\"저는 그런 헛된 칭호 따위에는 아무런 가치도 두지 않습니다. 다만 사실인즉, 저는 공로를 인정받아 남작이 되었고 레지옹 도뇌르 슈발리에도 되었지요. 하지만……\""]], [["“But you have discarded your titles after the example set you by Messrs. de Montmorency and Lafayette?", "\"하지만 몽모랑시 씨나 라파예트 씨가 보인 본을 좇아 칭호를 버리셨다, 그 말씀이군요?"], ["That was a noble example to follow, monsieur.”", "따를 만한 고귀한 본보기였지요.\""]], [["“Why,”", "\"글쎄요.\" 당글라르가 대답했다."], ["replied Danglars, “not entirely so; with the servants,—you understand.”", "\"꼭 그렇지만도 않습니다. 하인들 앞에서는…… 무슨 말인지 아시겠지요.\""]], [["“I see; to your domestics you are ‘my lord,’ the journalists style you ‘monsieur,’ while your constituents call you ‘citizen.’ These are distinctions very suitable under a constitutional government.", "\"알겠습니다. 하인들에게는 '나리'이시고, 기자들은 '므슈'라 부르며, 유권자들은 '시민'이라 일컫는 거지요. 입헌 정부 아래서는 더없이 어울리는 구별입니다."], ["I understand perfectly.”", "잘 알겠습니다.\""]], [["Again Danglars bit his lips; he saw that he was no match for Monte Cristo in an argument of this sort, and he therefore hastened to turn to subjects more congenial.", "당글라르는 또 한 번 입술을 깨물었다. 이런 식의 말씨름으로는 도저히 몬테크리스토의 적수가 못 된다는 걸 알아챈 그는, 서둘러 좀 더 자기 입맛에 맞는 화제로 옮겨 갔다."]], [["“Permit me to inform you, Count,”", "\"한 가지 알려 드리지요, 백작님.\""], ["said he, bowing, “that I have received a letter of advice from Thomson & French, of Rome.”", "그가 절하며 말했다. \"로마의 톰슨 앤드 프렌치로부터 통지 서신을 받았습니다.\""]], [["“I am glad to hear it, baron,—for I must claim the privilege of addressing you after the manner of your servants.", "\"반가운 소식이군요, 남작. 저야 당신 하인들 방식대로 당신을 부르는 특권을 누려야겠지만요."], ["I have acquired the bad habit of calling persons by their titles from living in a country where barons are still barons by right of birth.", "저는 출생만으로도 여전히 남작이 남작인 나라에서 살다 보니, 사람들을 칭호로 부르는 못된 버릇이 들었거든요."], ["But as regards the letter of advice, I am charmed to find that it has reached you; that will spare me the troublesome and disagreeable task of coming to you for money myself.", "어쨌든 그 통지 서신이 당신께 닿았다니 더없이 기쁩니다. 덕분에 제가 직접 돈을 받으러 당신을 찾아오는, 번거롭고 떨떠름한 수고를 덜게 됐으니까요."], ["You have received a regular letter of advice?”", "정식 통지 서신을 받으셨군요?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said Danglars, “but I confess I didn’t quite comprehend its meaning.”", "당글라르가 말했다. \"하지만 솔직히 그 뜻을 잘 모르겠더군요.\""]], [["“Indeed?”", "\"그래요?\""]], [["“And for that reason I did myself the honor of calling upon you, in order to beg for an explanation.”", "\"그래서 제가 직접 찾아뵙는 영광을 누린 겁니다. 설명을 좀 부탁드리려고요.\""]], [["“Go on, monsieur.", "\"말씀하시지요."], ["Here I am, ready to give you any explanation you desire.”", "자, 여기 있으니 무엇이든 원하는 대로 설명해 드리겠습니다.\""]], [["“Why,”", "\"그게 말입니다.\""], ["said Danglars, “in the letter—I believe I have it about me”—here he felt in his breast-pocket—“yes, here it is.", "당글라르가 말했다. \"그 서신에…… 제가 지금 가지고 있을 텐데요.\""], ["Well, this letter gives the Count of Monte Cristo unlimited credit on our house.”", "그러면서 그는 가슴 주머니를 더듬었다. \"그래, 여기 있군요. 자, 이 서신에는 몬테크리스토 백작께서 우리 은행에서 무제한으로 신용 거래를 할 수 있다고 적혀 있습니다.\""]], [["“Well, baron, what is there difficult to understand about that?”", "\"그게 어디 이해하기 어려운 대목입니까, 남작?\""]], [["“Merely the term _unlimited_—nothing else, certainly.”", "\"그저 무제한이라는 말, 그것뿐입니다, 확실히.\""]], [["“Is not that word known in France?", "\"프랑스에서는 그 말을 모릅니까?"], ["The people who wrote are Anglo-Germans, you know.”", "아시다시피 그걸 쓴 사람들은 영국계 독일인이라서요.\""]], [["“Oh, as for the composition of the letter, there is nothing to be said; but as regards the competency of the document, I certainly have doubts.”", "\"오, 서신의 문장이야 흠잡을 데 없습니다. 다만 그 문서가 효력을 지니는지에 대해서는 분명 의문이 듭니다.\""]], [["“Is it possible?”", "\"그럴 수가.\""], ["asked the count, assuming all air and tone of the utmost simplicity and candor.", "백작이 더없이 천진하고 솔직한 기색과 어조를 꾸미며 말했다."], ["“Is it possible that Thomson & French are not looked upon as safe and solvent bankers?", "\"톰슨 앤드 프렌치가 안전하고 지급 능력 있는 은행으로 여겨지지 않는다는 말씀입니까?"], ["Pray tell me what you think, baron, for I feel uneasy, I can assure you, having some considerable property in their hands.”", "어떻게 생각하시는지 말씀해 주십시오, 남작. 솔직히 저도 그쪽에 적잖은 재산을 맡겨 둔 터라 마음이 편치 않으니 말입니다.\""]], [["“Thomson & French are perfectly solvent,”", "\"톰슨 앤드 프렌치야 더할 나위 없이 지급 능력이 있지요.\""], ["replied Danglars, with an almost mocking smile; “but the word _unlimited_, in financial affairs, is so extremely vague.”", "당글라르가 거의 비웃는 듯한 미소를 띠며 대답했다. \"다만 무제한이라는 말은, 금융 거래에서는 너무도 모호합니다.\""]], [["“Is, in fact, unlimited,”", "\"사실 무제한이지요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“Precisely what I was about to say,”", "\"바로 제가 하려던 말씀이 그겁니다.\""], ["cried Danglars.", "당글라르가 외쳤다."], ["“Now what is vague is doubtful; and it was a wise man who said, ‘when in doubt, keep out.’”", "\"한데 모호한 것은 미심쩍은 법이지요. 그래서 어느 현명한 사람이 '의심스럽거든 발을 들이지 마라'고 했고요.\""]], [["“Meaning to say,”", "\"그러니까,\""], ["rejoined Monte Cristo, “that however Thomson & French may be inclined to commit acts of imprudence and folly, the Baron Danglars is not disposed to follow their example.”", "몬테크리스토가 받았다. \"톰슨 앤드 프렌치가 아무리 경솔하고 어리석은 짓을 저지를 작정이라 해도, 당글라르 남작께서는 그 본을 따를 마음이 없으시다, 그 말씀이군요.\""]], [["“Not at all.”", "\"그렇지요.\""]], [["“Plainly enough; Messrs. Thomson & French set no bounds to their engagements while those of M.", "\"분명하군요. 톰슨 앤드 프렌치는 자기네 약정에 한계를 두지 않는 반면, 당글라르 씨의 약정에는 한계가 있다."], ["Danglars have their limits; he is a wise man, according to his own showing.”", "자기 말마따나 그분은 현명한 사람이다, 이거로군요.\""]], [["“Monsieur,”", "\"백작님.\""], ["replied the banker, drawing himself up with a haughty air, “the extent of my resources has never yet been questioned.”", "은행가가 거만한 기색으로 몸을 곧추세우며 대답했다. \"제 자산의 규모는 여태껏 단 한 번도 의심받은 적이 없습니다.\""]], [["“It seems, then, reserved for me,” said Monte Cristo coldly, “to be the first to do so.”", "\"그렇다면 제가 그걸 처음으로 의심하는 사람이 되도록 정해진 모양이군요.\" 몬테크리스토가 차갑게 말했다."]], [["“By what right, sir?”", "\"무슨 권리로요, 백작?\""]], [["“By right of the objections you have raised, and the explanations you have demanded, which certainly must have some motive.”", "\"당신이 제기한 이의와, 당신이 요구한 설명을 권리 삼아서지요. 그런 데에는 분명 무슨 까닭이 있을 테니까요.\""]], [["Once more Danglars bit his lips.", "당글라르는 다시 한 번 입술을 깨물었다."], ["It was the second time he had been worsted, and this time on his own ground.", "그가 밀린 것은 이번이 두 번째였고, 이번에는 자기 안방에서였다."], ["His forced politeness sat awkwardly upon him, and approached almost to impertinence.", "억지로 차린 정중함이 어색하게 들러붙어, 거의 무례함에 가까워질 지경이었다."], ["Monte Cristo on the contrary, preserved a graceful suavity of demeanor, aided by a certain degree of simplicity he could assume at pleasure, and thus possessed the advantage.", "반면 몬테크리스토는 우아하고 부드러운 태도를 잃지 않았고, 마음만 먹으면 꾸며 낼 수 있는 어떤 천진함까지 곁들여 우위를 점했다."]], [["“Well, sir,”", "\"그럼, 백작님.\" 당글라르가 잠시 침묵하다 다시 입을 열었다."], ["resumed Danglars, after a brief silence, “I will endeavor to make myself understood, by requesting you to inform me for what sum you propose to draw upon me?”", "\"제 뜻이 통하도록 해 보겠습니다. 한 가지 여쭙지요. 제게 얼마나 되는 금액을 인출하실 작정이십니까?\""]], [["“Why, truly,”", "\"실은 말입니다.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo, determined not to lose an inch of the ground he had gained, “my reason for desiring an ‘unlimited’ credit was precisely because I did not know how much money I might need.”", "몬테크리스토가 자기가 차지한 자리를 한 치도 내주지 않으려 작정하고 대답했다. \"제가 무제한 신용을 바란 까닭이 바로, 제가 돈이 얼마나 필요할지 몰랐기 때문입니다.\""]], [["The banker thought the time had come for him to take the upper hand.", "은행가는 이제 자기가 주도권을 쥘 때가 왔다고 여겼다."], ["So throwing himself back in his armchair, he said, with an arrogant and purse-proud air:", "그래서 안락의자에 몸을 기대며, 오만하고 돈푼깨나 있다는 기색으로 말했다."]], [["“Let me beg of you not to hesitate in naming your wishes; you will then be convinced that the resources of the house of Danglars, however limited, are still equal to meeting the largest demands; and were you even to require a million——”", "\"부디 바라시는 바를 말씀하시는 데 망설이지 마십시오. 그러면 아무리 한정되어 있다 해도 당글라르 은행의 자산이 가장 큰 요구에도 능히 응할 수 있다는 걸 아시게 될 겁니다. 설령 백작께서 백만 프랑을 요구하신다 해도……\""]], [["“I beg your pardon,”", "\"실례합니다.\""], ["interposed Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 끼어들었다."]], [["“I said a million,”", "\"백만이라 했습니다.\""], ["replied Danglars, with the confidence of ignorance.", "당글라르가 무지에서 비롯된 자신감으로 대답했다."]], [["“But could I do with a million?”", "\"제가 백만으로 뭘 한단 말입니까?\""], ["retorted the count.", "백작이 되받았다."], ["“My dear sir, if a trifle like that could suffice me, I should never have given myself the trouble of opening an account. A million?", "\"이보십시오, 그깟 푼돈으로 충분할 정도라면, 애초에 계좌를 트는 수고 따위 하지도 않았겠지요. 백만이라고요?"], ["Excuse my smiling when you speak of a sum I am in the habit of carrying in my pocket-book or dressing-case.”", "제가 으레 수첩이나 화장 상자에 넣고 다니는 액수를 두고 말씀하시니, 웃음이 나는 걸 용서하십시오.\""]], [["And with these words Monte Cristo took from his pocket a small case containing his visiting-cards, and drew forth two orders on the treasury for 500,000 francs each, payable at sight to the bearer.", "이렇게 말하며 몬테크리스토는 호주머니에서 명함을 넣어 두는 작은 갑을 꺼내, 각각 50만 프랑짜리 국고 어음 두 장을 뽑아 들었다. 소지자에게 일람불로 지급되는 어음이었다."], ["A man like Danglars was wholly inaccessible to any gentler method of correction.", "당글라르 같은 사내에게는 그보다 온화한 방식의 교정이란 전혀 통하지 않는 법이다."], ["The effect of the present revelation was stunning; he trembled and was on the verge of apoplexy.", "지금 눈앞에 드러난 사실의 효과는 가히 충격적이었다. 그는 부들부들 떨었고, 거의 졸중을 일으킬 지경이었다."], ["The pupils of his eyes, as he gazed at Monte Cristo dilated horribly.", "몬테크리스토를 바라보는 그의 눈동자가 끔찍하게 커졌다."]], [["“Come, come,”", "\"자, 자.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “confess honestly that you have not perfect confidence in Thomson & French.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"솔직히 인정하시지요. 당신은 톰슨 앤드 프렌치를 온전히 믿지 못하고 있는 겁니다."], ["I understand, and foreseeing that such might be the case, I took, in spite of my ignorance of affairs, certain precautions.", "이해합니다. 저는 그럴 수도 있으리라 미리 헤아려, 거래에 어두운 사람이긴 해도 나름의 대비를 해 두었지요."], ["See, here are two similar letters to that you have yourself received; one from the house of Arstein & Eskeles of Vienna, to Baron Rothschild, the other drawn by Baring of London, upon M. Lafitte.", "보십시오, 당신이 직접 받은 것과 똑같은 서신이 여기 두 통 더 있습니다. 하나는 빈의 아른슈타인 앤드 에스켈레스 은행이 로스차일드 남작에게 보낸 것이고, 다른 하나는 런던의 베어링이 라피트 씨 앞으로 발행한 것입니다."], ["Now, sir, you have but to say the word, and I will spare you all uneasiness by presenting my letter of credit to one or other of these two firms.”", "자, 한마디만 하시면 됩니다. 그러면 제가 이 신용장을 이 두 은행 중 한 곳에 제시해, 당신의 모든 근심을 덜어 드리지요.\""]], [["The blow had struck home, and Danglars was entirely vanquished; with a trembling hand he took the two letters from the count, who held them carelessly between finger and thumb, and proceeded to scrutinize the signatures, with a minuteness that the count might have regarded as insulting, had it not suited his present purpose to mislead the banker.", "일격이 정통으로 들어맞았고, 당글라르는 완전히 무릎을 꿇었다. 그는 떨리는 손으로 백작에게서 두 통의 편지를 받아 들었다. 백작은 그것을 엄지와 검지 사이에 무심히 끼우고 있었다. 그러고는 서명을 어찌나 꼼꼼히 뜯어보는지, 만약 지금 은행가를 속이는 것이 백작의 목적에 맞지 않았더라면 모욕으로 여겼을 법했다."]], [["“Oh, sir,”", "\"오, 백작님.\""], ["said Danglars, after he had convinced himself of the authenticity of the documents he held, and rising as if to salute the power of gold personified in the man before him,—“three letters of unlimited credit!", "손에 든 문서가 진짜임을 확인한 당글라르가 말했다. 그러고는 눈앞의 사내로 구현된 황금의 위력에 경의를 표하듯 자리에서 일어섰다. \"무제한 신용장이 세 통이라니!"], ["I can be no longer mistrustful, but you must pardon me, my dear count, for confessing to some degree of astonishment.”", "더는 의심할 수가 없군요. 하지만 친애하는 백작님, 제가 어느 정도 놀란 것은 부디 너그러이 봐주십시오.\""]], [["“Nay,”", "\"천만에요.\""], ["answered Monte Cristo, with the most gentlemanly air, “’tis not for such trifling sums as these that your banking house is to be incommoded.", "몬테크리스토가 더없이 신사다운 태도로 대답했다. \"이까짓 하찮은 액수로 당신의 은행을 번거롭게 할 일은 아니지요."], ["Then, you can let me have some money, can you not?”", "자, 그럼 제게 돈을 좀 내주실 수 있겠지요?\""]], [["“Whatever you say, my dear count; I am at your orders.”", "\"말씀만 하십시오, 친애하는 백작님. 분부대로 따르겠습니다.\""]], [["“Why,”", "\"그럼,\""], ["replied Monte Cristo, “since we mutually understand each other—for such I presume is the case?”", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"우리가 서로 이해하게 되었으니 말입니다. 그런 줄로 알겠습니다만?\""], ["Danglars bowed assentingly.", "당글라르가 동의하듯 절했다."], ["“You are quite sure that not a lurking doubt or suspicion lingers in your mind?”", "\"마음속에 도사린 의심이나 미심쩍음이 한 점도 남지 않은 게 확실하지요?\""]], [["“Oh, my dear count,”", "\"오, 친애하는 백작님.\""], ["exclaimed Danglars, “I never for an instant entertained such a feeling towards you.”", "당글라르가 외쳤다. \"저는 단 한순간도 당신께 그런 감정을 품은 적이 없습니다.\""]], [["“No, you merely wished to be convinced, nothing more; but now that we have come to so clear an understanding, and that all distrust and suspicion are laid at rest, we may as well fix a sum as the probable expenditure of the first year, suppose we say six millions to——”", "\"그래요, 당신은 그저 확인하고 싶었을 뿐이지요, 그뿐입니다. 한데 이제 이렇게 분명히 서로를 이해하게 되었고, 온갖 불신과 의심도 가라앉았으니, 첫해의 예상 지출액을 어림잡아 정해 두는 것도 좋겠군요. 가령 6백만 프랑쯤이라 하면……\""]], [["“Six millions!”", "\"6백만이라고요!\""], ["gasped Danglars—“so be it.”", "당글라르가 숨을 헐떡였다. \"그렇게 하시지요.\""]], [["“Then, if I should require more,”", "\"그러다 더 필요하면,\""], ["continued Monte Cristo in a careless manner, “why, of course, I should draw upon you; but my present intention is not to remain in France more than a year, and during that period I scarcely think I shall exceed the sum I mentioned.", "몬테크리스토가 무심한 투로 말을 이었다. \"물론 당신께 더 인출하겠지요. 하지만 지금 제 생각으로는 프랑스에 일 년 넘게 머물 작정은 없고, 그동안 방금 말한 액수를 넘기는 일도 거의 없을 듯합니다."], ["However, we shall see.", "어쨌든 두고 보지요."], ["Be kind enough, then, to send me 500,000 francs tomorrow.", "그럼 친절을 베푸시어, 내일 제게 50만 프랑을 보내 주십시오."], ["I shall be at home till midday, or if not, I will leave a receipt with my steward.”", "정오까지는 집에 있을 테고, 혹 없으면 영수증을 집사에게 맡겨 두겠습니다.\""]], [["“The money you desire shall be at your house by ten o’clock tomorrow morning, my dear count,”", "\"바라시는 돈은 내일 아침 열 시까지 댁으로 보내 드리겠습니다, 친애하는 백작님.\""], ["replied Danglars.", "당글라르가 대답했다."], ["“How would you like to have it? in gold, silver, or notes?”", "\"어떻게 받으시겠습니까? 금화로, 은화로, 아니면 지폐로요?\""]], [["“Half in gold, and the other half in bank-notes, if you please,”", "\"절반은 금화로, 나머지 절반은 은행권으로 부탁드립니다.\""], ["said the count, rising from his seat.", "백작이 자리에서 일어서며 말했다."]], [["“I must confess to you, count,”", "\"솔직히 말씀드리지요, 백작님.\""], ["said Danglars, “that I have hitherto imagined myself acquainted with the degree of all the great fortunes of Europe, and still wealth such as yours has been wholly unknown to me.", "당글라르가 말했다. \"저는 여태껏 유럽의 큰 재산이라면 그 규모를 죄다 꿰고 있다고 자부해 왔는데, 백작님 같은 재산은 도무지 들어 본 적이 없습니다."], ["May I presume to ask whether you have long possessed it?”", "외람되오나, 그 재산을 가지신 지 오래되셨는지 여쭈어도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["“It has been in the family a very long while,”", "\"우리 집안에 아주 오랫동안 내려온 것입니다.\""], ["returned Monte Cristo, “a sort of treasure expressly forbidden to be touched for a certain period of years, during which the accumulated interest has doubled the capital.", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"일정한 햇수 동안은 절대 손대지 못하도록 정해진 일종의 보물이었지요. 그동안 쌓인 이자가 원금을 곱절로 불려 놓았고요."], ["The period appointed by the testator for the disposal of these riches occurred only a short time ago, and they have only been employed by me within the last few years.", "유언자가 이 재산을 처분하도록 정한 시기가 온 게 바로 얼마 전이라, 저도 최근 몇 해 사이에야 비로소 쓰기 시작했습니다."], ["Your ignorance on the subject, therefore, is easily accounted for.", "그러니 당신이 그걸 모르셨던 것도 무리는 아니지요."], ["However, you will be better informed as to me and my possessions ere long.”", "어쨌든 머지않아 저와 제 재산에 대해 더 잘 아시게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["And the count, while pronouncing these latter words, accompanied them with one of those ghastly smiles that used to strike terror into poor Franz d’Épinay.", "이 마지막 말을 하면서 백작은, 가엾은 프란츠 데피네의 가슴에 늘 공포를 박아 넣곤 하던 그 섬뜩한 미소 가운데 하나를 곁들였다."]], [["“With your tastes, and means of gratifying them,”", "\"백작님의 취향과, 그걸 만족시킬 재력이라면,\""], ["continued Danglars, “you will exhibit a splendor that must effectually put us poor miserable millionaires quite in the shade.", "당글라르가 말을 이었다. \"우리 같은 가련한 백만장자 따위는 완전히 그늘에 가려 버릴 만큼 화려함을 뽐내시겠지요."], ["If I mistake not you are an admirer of paintings, at least I judged so from the attention you appeared to be bestowing on mine when I entered the room.", "제가 잘못 본 게 아니라면 백작님은 그림 애호가이신 듯합니다. 적어도 제가 방에 들어왔을 때 제 그림에 쏟으시던 눈길을 보고 그리 짐작했지요."], ["If you will permit me, I shall be happy to show you my picture gallery, composed entirely of works by the ancient masters—warranted as such.", "괜찮으시다면 제 화랑을 기꺼이 보여 드리고 싶군요. 죄다 옛 거장들의 작품으로만 이루어져 있고, 그건 보증할 수 있습니다."], ["Not a modern picture among them.", "그중에 현대 그림은 한 점도 없습니다."], ["I cannot endure the modern school of painting.”", "저는 현대 화풍을 도무지 견딜 수가 없어서요.\""]], [["“You are perfectly right in objecting to them, for this one great fault—that they have not yet had time to become old.”", "\"그것들을 못마땅해하시는 건 지극히 옳습니다. 그것들에게는 큰 흠이 하나 있으니까요. 아직 오래될 시간을 갖지 못했다는 흠 말입니다.\""]], [["“Or will you allow me to show you several fine statues by Thorwaldsen, Bartoloni, and Canova?—all foreign artists, for, as you may perceive, I think but very indifferently of our French sculptors.”", "\"아니면 토르발센과 바르톨리니, 카노바의 빼어난 조각상 몇 점을 보여 드릴까요? 모두 외국 예술가들이지요. 보시다시피 저는 우리 프랑스 조각가들은 영 대수롭잖게 여기거든요.\""]], [["“You have a right to be unjust to them, monsieur; they are your compatriots.”", "\"그들에게 박하게 구실 권리가 있으시지요, 므슈. 당신 동포들이니까요.\""]], [["“But all this may come later, when we shall be better known to each other.", "\"하지만 이런 건 다 나중에, 우리가 서로를 더 잘 알게 되었을 때 해도 될 일이지요."], ["For the present, I will confine myself (if perfectly agreeable to you) to introducing you to the Baroness Danglars—excuse my impatience, my dear count, but a client like you is almost like a member of the family.”", "지금은, 괜찮으시다면, 당글라르 남작 부인을 소개하는 것으로 그치겠습니다. 제 조급함을 용서하십시오, 친애하는 백작님. 하지만 당신 같은 고객은 거의 한 식구나 다름없으니까요.\""]], [["Monte Cristo bowed, in sign that he accepted the proffered honor; Danglars rang and was answered by a servant in a showy livery.", "몬테크리스토는 베푸는 영광을 받아들이겠다는 표시로 절했다. 당글라르가 종을 울리자, 화려한 제복 차림의 하인이 응답했다."]], [["“Is the baroness at home?”", "\"마님은 댁에 계신가?\""], ["inquired Danglars.", "당글라르가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, my lord,”", "\"네, 나리.\""], ["answered the man.", "하인이 대답했다."]], [["“And alone?”", "\"혼자 계시고?\""]], [["“No, my lord, madame has visitors.”", "\"아닙니다, 나리. 마님께 손님이 와 계십니다.\""]], [["“Have you any objection to meet any persons who may be with madame, or do you desire to preserve a strict _incognito_?”", "\"마님과 함께 있는 손님을 만나도 괜찮으시겠습니까, 아니면 끝까지 신분을 숨기고 싶으십니까?\""]], [["“No, indeed,”", "\"천만에요.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo with a smile, “I do not arrogate to myself the right of so doing.”", "몬테크리스토가 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"제게 그럴 권리가 있다고 우길 생각은 없습니다.\""]], [["“And who is with madame?—M. Debray?”", "\"마님과 함께 계신 분이 누구냐?"], ["inquired Danglars, with an air of indulgence and good-nature that made Monte Cristo smile, acquainted as he was with the secrets of the banker’s domestic life.", "드브레 씨인가?\" 당글라르가 물었다. 그 너그럽고 선량한 기색에, 은행가의 집안 사정을 속속들이 아는 몬테크리스토는 빙긋 웃음이 났다."]], [["“Yes, my lord,”", "\"네, 나리.\""], ["replied the servant, “M. Debray is with madame.”", "하인이 대답했다. \"드브레 씨가 마님과 함께 계십니다.\""]], [["Danglars nodded his head; then, turning to Monte Cristo, said, “M.", "당글라르가 고개를 끄덕였다. 그러고는 몬테크리스토를 돌아보며 말했다."], ["Lucien Debray is an old friend of ours, and private secretary to the Minister of the Interior.", "\"뤼시앵 드브레 씨는 우리의 오랜 벗이자 내무부 장관의 비서관입니다."], ["As for my wife, I must tell you, she lowered herself by marrying me, for she belongs to one of the most ancient families in France.", "제 아내로 말하자면, 솔직히 말해 저와 결혼함으로써 신분을 낮춘 셈이지요. 프랑스에서 가장 유서 깊은 가문 중 하나 출신이거든요."], ["Her maiden name was De Servières, and her first husband was Colonel the Marquis of Nargonne.”", "처녀 적 이름이 드세르비에르였고, 첫 남편은 나르곤 후작인 대령이었습니다.\""]], [["“I have not the honor of knowing Madame Danglars; but I have already met M. Lucien Debray.”", "\"당글라르 부인을 알고 지내는 영광은 없습니다만, 뤼시앵 드브레 씨라면 이미 만난 적이 있습니다.\""]], [["“Ah, indeed?”", "\"아, 그래요?\""], ["said Danglars; “and where was that?”", "당글라르가 말했다. \"어디서 말입니까?\""]], [["“At the house of M. de Morcerf.”", "\"모르세르 씨 댁에서요.\""]], [["“Ah! you are acquainted with the young viscount, are you?”", "\"아! 그럼 그 젊은 자작과 아는 사이로군요?\""]], [["“We were together a good deal during the Carnival at Rome.”", "\"로마에서 카니발 기간에 꽤 자주 어울렸지요.\""]], [["“True, true,”", "\"그렇지, 그렇지.\""], ["cried Danglars.", "당글라르가 외쳤다."], ["“Let me see; have I not heard talk of some strange adventure with bandits or thieves hid in ruins, and of his having had a miraculous escape?", "\"가만, 폐허에 숨어 있던 산적이며 도둑들과 얽힌 무슨 기이한 모험 이야기를, 그리고 그가 기적처럼 빠져나왔다는 이야기를 들은 적이 있지 않던가요?"], ["I forget how, but I know he used to amuse my wife and daughter by telling them about it after his return from Italy.”", "어떻게였는지는 잊었지만, 그가 이탈리아에서 돌아온 뒤 제 아내와 딸에게 그 이야기를 들려주며 곧잘 즐겁게 해 주곤 했지요.\""]], [["“Her ladyship is waiting to receive you, gentlemen,” said the servant, who had gone to inquire the pleasure of his mistress.", "\"마님께서 두 분을 맞이하려고 기다리고 계십니다.\" 안주인의 뜻을 여쭈러 갔던 하인이 말했다."]], [["“With your permission,”", "\"실례지만,\""], ["said Danglars, bowing, “I will precede you, to show you the way.”", "당글라르가 절하며 말했다. \"제가 앞장서서 길을 안내하겠습니다.\""]], [["“By all means,”", "\"그러시지요.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo; “I follow you.”", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"뒤따르겠습니다.\""]], [["# Chapter 47. The Dappled Grays", "# Chapter 47. The Dappled Grays"]], [["The baron, followed by the count, traversed a long series of apartments, in which the prevailing characteristics were heavy magnificence and the gaudiness of ostentatious wealth, until he reached the boudoir of Madame Danglars—a small octagonal-shaped room, hung with pink satin, covered with white Indian muslin.", "남작은 백작을 뒤에 거느리고 여러 개의 방을 차례로 지나갔다. 그 방들을 지배하는 분위기는 묵직한 호화로움과 부를 과시하는 요란함이었다. 마침내 그는 당글라르 부인의 규방에 이르렀다."], ["The chairs were of ancient workmanship and materials; over the doors were painted sketches of shepherds and shepherdesses, after the style and manner of Boucher; and at each side pretty medallions in crayons, harmonizing well with the furnishings of this charming apartment, the only one throughout the great mansion in which any distinctive taste prevailed.", "작은 팔각형의 방으로, 벽에는 분홍빛 새틴이 드리워지고 그 위에 흰 인도산 모슬린이 덮여 있었다. 의자들은 고풍스러운 솜씨와 재료로 만든 것이었고, 문 위에는 부셰의 화풍을 본떠 그린 목동과 양치기 처녀의 스케치가 걸려 있었으며, 양옆에는 크레용으로 그린 예쁜 원형 장식이 이 매혹적인 방의 가구들과 잘 어우러졌다. 이 큰 저택을 통틀어 독자적인 취향이 살아 있는 곳은 이 방뿐이었다."], ["The truth was, it had been entirely overlooked in the plan arranged and followed out by M.", "사실 이 방은 당글라르 씨와 그의 건축가가 짜고 따른 설계도에서 완전히 빠져 있었다."], ["Danglars and his architect, who had been selected to aid the baron in the great work of improvement solely because he was the most fashionable and celebrated decorator of the day.", "그 건축가는 당대 가장 유행을 타고 가장 이름난 실내장식가라는 이유 하나만으로 남작의 대대적인 개조 작업을 돕도록 뽑힌 사람이었다."], ["The decorations of the boudoir had then been left entirely to Madame Danglars and Lucien Debray. M.", "그래서 규방의 장식은 전적으로 당글라르 부인과 뤼시앵 드브레의 손에 맡겨졌다."], ["Danglars, however, while possessing a great admiration for the antique, as it was understood during the time of the Directory, entertained the most sovereign contempt for the simple elegance of his wife’s favorite sitting-room, where, by the way, he was never permitted to intrude, unless, indeed, he excused his own appearance by ushering in some more agreeable visitor than himself; and even then he had rather the air and manner of a person who was himself introduced, than that of being the presenter of another, his reception being cordial or frigid, in proportion as the person who accompanied him chanced to please or displease the baroness.", "당글라르 씨는 총재정부 시대에 이해되던 식의 고가구를 무척 흠모했으나, 아내가 가장 아끼는 이 거실의 소박한 우아함에는 더없는 경멸을 품고 있었다. 덧붙이자면 그는 이 방에 들어오는 것을 결코 허락받지 못했고, 다만 자신보다 더 반가운 손님을 안내해 들일 때만 자신의 출현이 양해되었다. 그런 때에도 그는 다른 사람을 소개하는 사람이라기보다 자신이 소개받는 사람 같은 분위기와 태도를 풍겼으며, 그가 데려온 사람이 남작 부인의 마음에 드느냐 들지 않느냐에 따라 그 환대는 따뜻하기도 하고 싸늘하기도 했다."]], [["Madame Danglars (who, although past the first bloom of youth, was still strikingly handsome) was now seated at the piano, a most elaborate piece of cabinet and inlaid work, while Lucien Debray, standing before a small work-table, was turning over the pages of an album.", "당글라르 부인은 (비록 젊음의 첫 만개는 지났으나 여전히 눈에 띄게 아름다웠다) 지금 피아노 앞에 앉아 있었다. 무척 정교한 세공과 상감 솜씨가 들어간 악기였다. 한편 뤼시앵 드브레는 작은 작업대 앞에 서서 화첩의 책장을 넘기고 있었다."]], [["Lucien had found time, preparatory to the count’s arrival, to relate many particulars respecting him to Madame Danglars.", "뤼시앵은 백작이 도착하기 전에 그에 관한 많은 사정을 당글라르 부인에게 이야기해 둘 시간이 있었다."], ["It will be remembered that Monte Cristo had made a lively impression on the minds of all the party assembled at the breakfast given by Albert de Morcerf; and although Debray was not in the habit of yielding to such feelings, he had never been able to shake off the powerful influence excited in his mind by the impressive look and manner of the count, consequently the description given by Lucien to the baroness bore the highly-colored tinge of his own heated imagination.", "알베르 드 모르세르가 베푼 아침 식사 자리에 모인 모든 사람의 마음에 몬테크리스토가 강렬한 인상을 남겼다는 점을 기억할 것이다. 드브레는 그런 감정에 좀처럼 휘둘리는 사람이 아니었는데도, 백작의 인상적인 눈빛과 태도가 그의 마음에 불러일으킨 강한 영향을 끝내 떨쳐 내지 못했다. 그러니 뤼시앵이 남작 부인에게 들려준 묘사가 그 자신의 달아오른 상상력으로 한껏 윤색되어 있었던 것도 당연했다."], ["Already excited by the wonderful stories related of the count by de Morcerf, it is no wonder that Madame Danglars eagerly listened to, and fully credited, all the additional circumstances detailed by Debray.", "모르세르가 들려준 백작에 관한 놀라운 이야기들로 이미 들떠 있던 당글라르 부인이 드브레가 덧붙여 늘어놓는 온갖 사정에 열심히 귀 기울이고 또 그것을 고스란히 믿어 버린 것도 이상한 일이 아니었다."], ["This posing at the piano and over the album was only a little ruse adopted by way of precaution.", "피아노 앞에서 자세를 잡고 화첩을 들여다보던 것은 만일을 위한 작은 술책에 지나지 않았다."], ["A most gracious welcome and unusual smile were bestowed on M. Danglars; the count, in return for his gentlemanly bow, received a formal though graceful courtesy, while Lucien exchanged with the count a sort of distant recognition, and with Danglars a free and easy nod.", "당글라르 씨는 더없이 정중한 환영과 평소답지 않은 미소를 받았고, 백작은 신사다운 인사에 대한 답례로 격식을 갖춘 우아한 목례를 받았다. 한편 뤼시앵은 백작과 어딘가 서먹한 알은체를 주고받았고, 당글라르와는 허물없고 가벼운 끄덕임을 나누었다."]], [["“Baroness,”", "\"부인,\""], ["said Danglars, “give me leave to present to you the Count of Monte Cristo, who has been most warmly recommended to me by my correspondents at Rome.", "당글라르가 말했다. \"몬테크리스토 백작을 소개해 드리겠습니다. 로마에 있는 제 거래처에서 더없이 따뜻하게 추천해 준 분이지요."], ["I need but mention one fact to make all the ladies in Paris court his notice, and that is, that he has come to take up his abode in Paris for a year, during which brief period he proposes to spend six millions of money.", "파리의 모든 부인이 그분의 눈길을 끌려고 다툴 만한 사실 하나만 말씀드리겠습니다. 그분은 일 년 동안 파리에 머무를 작정으로 오셨는데, 그 짧은 동안에 무려 육백만 프랑을 쓰실 계획이라는 겁니다."], ["That means balls, dinners, and lawn parties without end, in all of which I trust the count will remember us, as he may depend upon it we shall him, in our own humble entertainments.”", "그건 곧 끝없는 무도회와 만찬, 정원 잔치를 뜻하지요. 그 모든 자리에서 백작께서 저희를 잊지 않으시길 바랍니다. 물론 저희도 변변치 않은 자리에나마 백작을 꼭 모실 테니까요.\""]], [["In spite of the gross flattery and coarseness of this address, Madame Danglars could not forbear gazing with considerable interest on a man capable of expending six millions in twelve months, and who had selected Paris for the scene of his princely extravagance.", "이 노골적인 아첨과 거친 말투에도 불구하고, 당글라르 부인은 열두 달에 육백만 프랑을 쓸 수 있는 데다 그 호사스러운 낭비의 무대로 파리를 골랐다는 남자를 적잖은 흥미로 바라보지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“And when did you arrive here?”", "\"언제 이곳에 도착하셨나요?\""], ["inquired she.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“Yesterday morning, madame.”", "\"어제 아침입니다, 부인.\""]], [["“Coming, as usual, I presume, from the extreme end of the globe? Pardon me—at least, such I have heard is your custom.”", "\"여느 때처럼 지구 끝에서 오셨겠지요? 실례합니다만, 적어도 그게 백작님의 습관이라고 들었거든요.\""]], [["“Nay, madame.", "\"아닙니다, 부인."], ["This time I have merely come from Cadiz.”", "이번에는 그저 카디스에서 왔을 뿐입니다.\""]], [["“You have selected a most unfavorable moment for your first visit.", "\"첫 방문으로는 더없이 나쁜 때를 고르셨군요."], ["Paris is a horrible place in summer.", "파리는 여름이면 끔찍한 곳이랍니다."], ["Balls, parties, and _fêtes_ are over; the Italian opera is in London; the French opera everywhere except in Paris.", "무도회도 모임도 축제도 다 끝났고, 이탈리아 오페라단은 런던에 가 있고, 프랑스 오페라단은 파리만 빼고 어디에나 있지요."], ["As for the Théatre Français, you know, of course, that it is nowhere.", "프랑세 극장으로 말하자면, 백작님도 물론 아시겠지만 있으나 마나 한 형편이고요."], ["The only amusements left us are the indifferent races at the Champ-de-Mars and Satory.", "저희에게 남은 유일한 즐길 거리라고는 샹드마르스와 사토리에서 열리는 시들한 경마뿐입니다."], ["Do you propose entering any horses at either of these races, count?”", "백작님께서는 그 경마 중 어느 한쪽에라도 말을 출전시키실 생각이 있으신가요?\""]], [["“I shall do whatever they do at Paris, madame, if I have the good fortune to find someone who will initiate me into the prevalent ideas of amusement.”", "\"파리에서 사람들이 하는 일이라면 저도 무엇이든 하겠습니다, 부인. 여기서 유행하는 오락의 관념을 제게 일러 줄 사람을 다행히 만나기만 한다면 말이지요.\""]], [["“Are you fond of horses, count?”", "\"백작님은 말을 좋아하시나요?\""]], [["“I have passed a considerable part of my life in the East, madame, and you are doubtless aware that the Orientals value only two things—the fine breeding of their horses and the beauty of their women.”", "\"제 삶의 상당 부분을 동방에서 보냈습니다, 부인. 그리고 동방 사람들이 단 두 가지만을 귀하게 여긴다는 것은 부인께서도 분명 아실 겁니다. 좋은 혈통의 말과 여인의 아름다움이지요.\""]], [["“Nay, count,”", "\"어머, 백작님,\""], ["said the baroness, “it would have been somewhat more gallant to have placed the ladies first.”", "남작 부인이 말했다. \"여인을 먼저 두셨더라면 좀 더 기사다웠을 텐데요.\""]], [["“You see, madame, how rightly I spoke when I said I required a preceptor to guide me in all my sayings and doings here.”", "\"보십시오, 부인. 이곳에서의 제 모든 말과 행동을 이끌어 줄 선생이 필요하다고 한 제 말이 얼마나 옳았는지 말입니다.\""]], [["At this instant the favorite attendant of Madame Danglars entered the boudoir; approaching her mistress, she spoke some words in an undertone.", "이때 당글라르 부인이 총애하는 시녀가 규방으로 들어왔다. 시녀는 안주인에게 다가가 낮은 목소리로 몇 마디를 속삭였다."], ["Madame Danglars turned very pale, then exclaimed:", "당글라르 부인의 얼굴이 새파랗게 질리더니, 이내 외쳤다."]], [["“I cannot believe it; the thing is impossible.”", "\"믿을 수 없구나. 그런 일이 있을 리 없어.\""]], [["“I assure you, madame,”", "\"정말입니다, 마님.\""], ["replied the woman, “it is as I have said.”", "시녀가 대답했다. \"제가 말씀드린 그대로입니다.\""]], [["Turning impatiently towards her husband, Madame Danglars demanded, “Is this true?”", "당글라르 부인은 조바심을 내며 남편 쪽으로 돌아서더니 다그쳤다. \"이게 사실인가요?\""]], [["“Is what true, madame?” inquired Danglars, visibly agitated.", "\"무엇이 사실이냐는 말이오, 부인?\" 당글라르가 눈에 띄게 동요하며 물었다."]], [["“What my maid tells me.”", "\"제 하녀가 방금 한 말이요.\""]], [["“But what does she tell you?”", "\"그런데 하녀가 무슨 말을 했소?\""]], [["“That when my coachman was about to harness the horses to my carriage, he discovered that they had been removed from the stables without his knowledge.", "\"마부가 제 마차에 말을 매려고 했더니, 자기도 모르는 새 말들이 마구간에서 사라졌더라는군요."], ["I desire to know what is the meaning of this?”", "이게 대체 무슨 영문인지 알고 싶어요.\""]], [["“Be kind enough, madame, to listen to me,”", "\"부인, 부디 내 말을 좀 들어 주시오.\""], ["said Danglars.", "당글라르가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, yes; I will listen, monsieur, for I am most curious to hear what explanation you will give.", "\"오, 그럼요. 듣고말고요. 무슨 변명을 늘어놓으실지 무척이나 궁금하니까요."], ["These two gentlemen shall decide between us; but, first, I will state the case to them. Gentlemen,”", "이 두 분이 우리 사이를 판가름해 주실 거예요. 다만 먼저 제가 두 분께 사정을 말씀드리지요. 여러분,\""], ["continued the baroness, “among the ten horses in the stables of Baron Danglars, are two that belong exclusively to me—a pair of the handsomest and most spirited creatures to be found in Paris.", "남작 부인이 말을 이었다. \"당글라르 남작의 마구간에 있는 열 마리 말 가운데 두 마리는 오로지 제 것이랍니다. 파리에서 찾아볼 수 있는 가장 아름답고 가장 기운 넘치는 한 쌍이지요."], ["But to you, at least, M. Debray, I need not give a further description, because to you my beautiful pair of dappled grays were well known.", "하지만 적어도 드브레 씨께는 더 설명을 드릴 필요가 없겠네요. 제 아름다운 얼룩 회색 말 한 쌍을 잘 아실 테니까요."], ["Well, I had promised Madame de Villefort the loan of my carriage to drive tomorrow to the Bois; but when my coachman goes to fetch the grays from the stables they are gone—positively gone.", "그런데 제가 빌포르 부인께 내일 불로뉴 숲으로 나들이를 가시도록 제 마차를 빌려 드리기로 약속했답니다. 그런데 마부가 마구간으로 그 회색 말들을 데리러 가니, 글쎄 사라지고 없더라는 거예요."], ["No doubt M. Danglars has sacrificed them to the selfish consideration of gaining some thousands of paltry francs.", "감쪽같이 사라진 거지요. 당글라르 씨가 보잘것없는 몇천 프랑을 벌겠다는 이기적인 속셈으로 그 말들을 팔아 치운 게 틀림없어요."], ["Oh, what a detestable crew they are, these mercenary speculators!”", "오, 정말 가증스러운 무리들이라니까요. 돈에 환장한 이 투기꾼들 말이에요!\""]], [["“Madame,”", "\"부인,\""], ["replied Danglars, “the horses were not sufficiently quiet for you; they were scarcely four years old, and they made me extremely uneasy on your account.”", "당글라르가 대답했다. \"그 말들은 당신이 타기에는 그리 얌전하지가 못했소. 겨우 네 살배기라, 당신을 생각하면 나는 마음이 몹시 불안했단 말이오.\""]], [["“Nonsense,”", "\"말도 안 되는 소리예요.\""], ["retorted the baroness; “you could not have entertained any alarm on the subject, because you are perfectly well aware that I have had for a month in my service the very best coachman in Paris.", "남작 부인이 쏘아붙였다. \"그 일로 불안해하셨을 리가 없잖아요. 제가 한 달 전부터 파리 최고의 마부를 부리고 있다는 걸 누구보다 잘 아시면서요."], ["But, perhaps, you have disposed of the coachman as well as the horses?”", "아니면 혹시 말들과 함께 그 마부까지 처분하셨나요?\""]], [["“My dear love, pray do not say any more about them, and I promise you another pair exactly like them in appearance, only more quiet and steady.”", "\"여보, 제발 그 말들 얘기는 그만합시다. 겉모습이 똑같으면서도 더 얌전하고 차분한 놈들로 한 쌍 구해 주리다.\""]], [["The baroness shrugged her shoulders with an air of ineffable contempt, while her husband, affecting not to observe this unconjugal gesture, turned towards Monte Cristo and said,—“Upon my word, count, I am quite sorry not to have met you sooner.", "남작 부인은 이루 말할 수 없는 경멸의 기색으로 어깨를 으쓱했다. 그러는 동안 남편은 이 부부답지 않은 몸짓을 못 본 척하며 몬테크리스토 쪽으로 돌아서서 말했다. \"정말이지 백작님, 좀 더 일찍 뵙지 못한 게 무척이나 아쉽군요."], ["You are setting up an establishment, of course?”", "백작님께서는 물론 살림을 꾸리고 계시겠지요?\""]], [["“Why, yes,” replied the count.", "\"네, 그렇습니다.\" 백작이 대답했다."]], [["“I should have liked to have made you the offer of these horses.", "\"그 말들을 백작님께 권해 드릴 수 있었을 텐데요."], ["I have almost given them away, as it is; but, as I before said, I was anxious to get rid of them upon any terms.", "이미 거저 주다시피 한 셈이지만요. 그래도 앞서 말씀드렸듯이, 저는 어떤 조건으로든 그놈들을 처분하고 싶었습니다."], ["They were only fit for a young man.”", "젊은이에게나 어울리는 말들이었거든요.\""]], [["“I am much obliged by your kind intentions towards me,”", "\"제게 보여 주신 호의에 깊이 감사드립니다.\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “but this morning I purchased a very excellent pair of carriage-horses, and I do not think they were dear.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"하지만 오늘 아침에 아주 훌륭한 마차용 말 한 쌍을 샀습니다. 그리 비싸지도 않았던 것 같고요."], ["There they are.", "저기 있군요."], ["Come, M. Debray, you are a connoisseur, I believe, let me have your opinion upon them.”", "자, 드브레 씨, 듣자 하니 안목이 높으시다던데, 저 말들에 대한 의견을 좀 들려주시지요.\""]], [["As Debray walked towards the window, Danglars approached his wife.", "드브레가 창가로 걸어가는 동안, 당글라르가 아내에게 다가갔다."]], [["“I could not tell you before others,”", "\"다른 사람들 앞에서는 말할 수 없었지만,\""], ["said he in a low tone, “the reason of my parting with the horses; but a most enormous price was offered me this morning for them.", "그가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"내가 그 말들을 처분한 이유가 있소. 오늘 아침에 누가 엄청난 값을 부르더란 말이오."], ["Some madman or fool, bent upon ruining himself as fast as he can, actually sent his steward to me to purchase them at any cost; and the fact is, I have gained 16,000 francs by the sale of them.", "제 몸 망치기를 어떻게든 서두르려 드는 어떤 미치광이인지 멍청이인지가, 실제로 제 집사를 보내 값이 얼마든 좋으니 그 말들을 사 오라고 한 거요. 그래서 사실 나는 그 거래로 만 육천 프랑을 벌었소."], ["Come, don’t look so angry, and you shall have 4,000 francs of the money to do what you like with, and Eugénie shall have 2,000.", "자, 그렇게 화난 얼굴 좀 풀어요. 그 돈에서 사천 프랑을 줄 테니 당신 마음대로 쓰고, 외제니에게는 이천 프랑을 줍시다."], ["There, what do you think now of the affair? Wasn’t I right to part with the horses?”", "자, 이제 어떻소? 내가 그 말들을 처분한 게 옳지 않았소?\""]], [["Madame Danglars surveyed her husband with a look of withering contempt.", "당글라르 부인은 몸서리쳐지는 경멸의 눈길로 남편을 훑어보았다."]], [["“Great heavens?”", "\"맙소사?\""], ["suddenly exclaimed Debray.", "드브레가 갑자기 외쳤다."]], [["“What is it?”", "\"무슨 일이에요?\""], ["asked the baroness.", "남작 부인이 물었다."]], [["“I cannot be mistaken; there are your horses!", "\"제가 잘못 봤을 리 없습니다. 부인의 말들입니다!"], ["The very animals we were speaking of, harnessed to the count’s carriage!”", "우리가 방금 이야기하던 바로 그 말들이, 백작님의 마차에 매여 있어요!\""]], [["“My dappled grays?” demanded the baroness, springing to the window.", "\"제 얼룩 회색 말들이라고요?\" 남작 부인이 창가로 달려가며 다그쳤다."], ["“’Tis indeed they!”", "\"정말 그 애들이네요!\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["Danglars looked absolutely stupefied.", "당글라르는 완전히 얼이 빠진 얼굴이었다."]], [["“How very singular,”", "\"이거 참 묘한 일이군요.\""], ["cried Monte Cristo with well-feigned astonishment.", "몬테크리스토가 그럴듯하게 꾸민 놀라움으로 외쳤다."]], [["“I cannot believe it,”", "\"믿을 수가 없군.\""], ["murmured the banker.", "은행가가 중얼거렸다."], ["Madame Danglars whispered a few words in the ear of Debray, who approached Monte Cristo, saying, “The baroness wishes to know what you paid her husband for the horses.”", "당글라르 부인이 드브레의 귀에 몇 마디를 속삭였고, 드브레가 몬테크리스토에게 다가가 말했다. \"부인께서 백작님이 저 말들을 자기 남편에게서 얼마에 사셨는지 알고 싶어 하십니다.\""]], [["“I scarcely know,”", "\"글쎄, 잘 모르겠습니다.\""], ["replied the count; “it was a little surprise prepared for me by my steward, and cost me—well, somewhere about 30,000 francs.”", "백작이 대답했다. \"제 집사가 저를 깜짝 놀라게 해 주려고 마련한 일이라서요. 든 값은, 글쎄요, 삼만 프랑쯤 됐을 겁니다.\""]], [["Debray conveyed the count’s reply to the baroness.", "드브레가 백작의 대답을 남작 부인에게 전했다."], ["Poor Danglars looked so crest-fallen and discomfited that Monte Cristo assumed a pitying air towards him.", "가엾은 당글라르는 어찌나 풀이 죽고 낭패한 얼굴인지, 몬테크리스토는 그에게 측은해하는 기색을 지어 보였다."]], [["“See,” said the count, “how very ungrateful women are.", "\"보십시오.\" 백작이 말했다."], ["Your kind attention, in providing for the safety of the baroness by disposing of the horses, does not seem to have made the least impression on her.", "\"여인이란 얼마나 은혜를 모르는지요. 그 말들을 처분해 남작 부인의 안전을 지켜 드리려 한 당신의 친절한 배려가 부인께는 조금도 와닿지 않은 모양입니다."], ["But so it is; a woman will often, from mere wilfulness, prefer that which is dangerous to that which is safe.", "하지만 으레 그런 법이지요. 여자란 흔히 한갓 고집 때문에 안전한 것보다 위험한 것을 더 좋아하니까요."], ["Therefore, in my opinion, my dear baron, the best and easiest way is to leave them to their fancies, and allow them to act as they please, and then, if any mischief follows, why, at least, they have no one to blame but themselves.”", "그러니 제 생각에는, 친애하는 남작님, 가장 좋고 가장 손쉬운 길은 그저 여자들의 변덕에 맡겨 두고 제멋대로 하게 내버려 두는 겁니다. 그러다가 무슨 탈이 나면, 적어도 탓할 사람이 자기 자신밖에 없을 테니까요.\""]], [["Danglars made no reply; he was occupied in anticipations of the coming scene between himself and the baroness, whose frowning brow, like that of Olympic Jove, predicted a storm.", "당글라르는 아무런 대꾸도 하지 않았다. 그는 자신과 남작 부인 사이에 곧 벌어질 장면을 머릿속으로 그려 보느라 정신이 없었다."], ["Debray, who perceived the gathering clouds, and felt no desire to witness the explosion of Madame Danglars’ rage, suddenly recollected an appointment, which compelled him to take his leave; while Monte Cristo, unwilling by prolonging his stay to destroy the advantages he hoped to obtain, made a farewell bow and departed, leaving Danglars to endure the angry reproaches of his wife.", "잔뜩 찌푸린 부인의 이마는 올림포스의 제우스처럼 폭풍을 예고하고 있었다. 먹구름이 몰려오는 것을 알아챈 드브레는 당글라르 부인의 분노가 터지는 광경을 지켜볼 마음이 없어, 문득 약속이 있는 것을 떠올리고는 자리를 떴다. 한편 몬테크리스토는 머무름을 길게 끌어 얻고자 하는 이점을 그르치고 싶지 않아, 작별 인사로 목례를 하고 떠났다."], ["20347m", "당글라르는 아내의 성난 질책을 고스란히 견뎌야 할 처지로 남겨졌다."]], [["“Excellent,”", "\"훌륭하군.\""], ["murmured Monte Cristo to himself, as he came away.", "몬테크리스토는 그곳을 나오며 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다."], ["“All has gone according to my wishes.", "\"모든 일이 내 바람대로 흘러갔어."], ["The domestic peace of this family is henceforth in my hands.", "이제 이 집안의 가정 평화는 내 손안에 있는 셈이지."], ["Now, then, to play another master-stroke, by which I shall gain the heart of both husband and wife—delightful! Still,”", "자, 그럼 이번에는 또 다른 묘수를 써서 남편과 아내 양쪽의 마음을 다 사 보자고. 정말 즐거운 일이야! 그래도,\""], ["added he, “amid all this, I have not yet been presented to Mademoiselle Eugénie Danglars, whose acquaintance I should have been glad to make. But,”", "그가 덧붙였다. \"이 와중에 아직 외제니 당글라르 양에게는 소개를 받지 못했군. 기꺼이 알고 지내고 싶은 사람인데. 하지만,\""], ["he went on with his peculiar smile, “I am here in Paris, and have plenty of time before me—by and by will do for that.”", "그는 특유의 미소를 지으며 말을 이었다. \"나는 지금 파리에 있고, 시간은 넉넉하니까. 그 일이야 차차 하면 되지.\""]], [["With these reflections he entered his carriage and returned home.", "이런 생각을 하며 그는 마차에 올라 집으로 돌아갔다."], ["Two hours afterwards, Madame Danglars received a most flattering epistle from the count, in which he entreated her to receive back her favorite “dappled grays,” protesting that he could not endure the idea of making his entry into the Parisian world of fashion with the knowledge that his splendid equipage had been obtained at the price of a lovely woman’s regrets.", "두 시간 뒤, 당글라르 부인은 백작에게서 더없이 찬사가 넘치는 편지 한 통을 받았다. 편지에서 백작은 그녀가 가장 아끼는 \"얼룩 회색 말들\"을 도로 받아 달라고 간청하며, 그 멋진 마차가 한 사랑스러운 여인의 아쉬움을 대가로 얻어진 것임을 알면서 파리 사교계에 발을 들인다는 생각은 도저히 견딜 수 없노라고 호소했다."], ["The horses were sent back wearing the same harness she had seen on them in the morning; only, by the count’s orders, in the centre of each rosette that adorned either side of their heads, had been fastened a large diamond.", "말들은 그녀가 아침에 보았던 것과 똑같은 마구를 차고 돌려보내졌다. 다만 백작의 명령으로, 말들의 머리 양쪽을 장식한 매듭 장미 한가운데마다 커다란 다이아몬드 하나씩이 박혀 있었다."]], [["To Danglars Monte Cristo also wrote, requesting him to excuse the whimsical gift of a capricious millionaire, and to beg the baroness to pardon the Eastern fashion adopted in the return of the horses.", "몬테크리스토는 당글라르에게도 편지를 써서, 변덕스러운 백만장자의 별난 선물을 너그러이 받아 주기를 청하고, 말들을 돌려보내며 취한 동방식 방식을 남작 부인께서 용서해 주시도록 부탁드린다고 했다."]], [["During the evening, Monte Cristo quitted Paris for Auteuil, accompanied by Ali.", "저녁 무렵, 몬테크리스토는 알리를 데리고 파리를 떠나 오퇴유로 향했다."], ["The following day, about three o’clock, a single blow struck on the gong summoned Ali to the presence of the count.", "이튿날 세 시쯤, 징을 한 번 울리는 소리가 알리를 백작 앞으로 불러들였다."]], [["“Ali,”", "\"알리,\""], ["observed his master, as the Nubian entered the chamber, “you have frequently explained to me how more than commonly skilful you are in throwing the lasso, have you not?”", "누비아인이 방으로 들어서자 주인이 말을 꺼냈다. \"너는 올가미를 던지는 데 남달리 능숙하다고 내게 여러 번 말한 적이 있지, 그렇지 않으냐?\""]], [["Ali drew himself up proudly, and then returned a sign in the affirmative.", "알리는 자랑스럽게 몸을 곧추세우더니, 그렇다는 표시를 해 보였다."]], [["“I thought I did not mistake.", "\"내가 잘못 안 게 아니라고 생각했다."], ["With your lasso you could stop an ox?”", "그 올가미로 황소를 멈춰 세울 수 있겠느냐?\""]], [["Again Ali repeated his affirmative gesture.", "알리는 다시 한번 그렇다는 몸짓을 되풀이했다."]], [["“Or a tiger?”", "\"호랑이는 어떠냐?\""]], [["Ali bowed his head in token of assent.", "알리는 고개를 숙여 그렇다는 뜻을 보였다."]], [["“A lion even?”", "\"사자까지도?\""]], [["Ali sprung forwards, imitating the action of one throwing the lasso, then of a strangled lion.", "알리는 앞으로 뛰쳐나가더니, 올가미를 던지는 동작에 이어 목 졸린 사자의 시늉을 해 보였다."]], [["“I understand,”", "\"알겠다.\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “you wish to tell me you have hunted the lion?”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"사자를 사냥한 적이 있다는 말이로구나?\""]], [["Ali smiled with triumphant pride as he signified that he had indeed both chased and captured many lions.", "알리는 사자를 여러 마리 뒤쫓아 사로잡아 보았노라는 뜻을 비치며, 의기양양한 자부심으로 미소 지었다."]], [["“But do you believe you could arrest the progress of two horses rushing forwards with ungovernable fury?”", "\"하지만 미친 듯이 날뛰며 앞으로 내달리는 말 두 마리를 멈춰 세울 수 있겠느냐?\""]], [["The Nubian smiled.", "누비아인이 빙긋 웃었다."]], [["“It is well,” said Monte Cristo.", "\"좋다.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["“Then listen to me.", "\"그럼 내 말을 잘 들어라."], ["Ere long a carriage will dash past here, drawn by the pair of dappled gray horses you saw me with yesterday; now, at the risk of your own life, you must manage to stop those horses before my door.”", "머지않아 마차 한 대가 이 앞을 쏜살같이 지나갈 것이다. 어제 나와 함께 있던 그 얼룩 회색 말 한 쌍이 끄는 마차다. 자, 네 목숨을 걸고서라도 그 말들을 내 집 문 앞에서 멈춰 세워야 한다.\""]], [["Ali descended to the street, and marked a straight line on the pavement immediately at the entrance of the house, and then pointed out the line he had traced to the count, who was watching him.", "알리는 거리로 내려가, 집 입구 바로 앞 포장도로에 곧게 선 하나를 그었다. 그러고는 자기를 지켜보던 백작에게 그어 놓은 선을 가리켜 보였다. 백작은 알리의 어깨를 가볍게 두드렸다."], ["The count patted him gently on the shoulder, his usual mode of praising Ali, who, pleased and gratified with the commission assigned him, walked calmly towards a projecting stone forming the angle of the street and house, and, seating himself thereon, began to smoke his chibouque, while Monte Cristo re-entered his dwelling, perfectly assured of the success of his plan.", "알리를 칭찬하는 그의 늘 하는 방식이었다. 맡은 임무가 흡족하고 기뻤던 알리는 거리와 집의 모서리를 이루는 튀어나온 돌 쪽으로 차분히 걸어가, 그 위에 앉아 물담뱃대를 피우기 시작했다. 한편 몬테크리스토는 자기 계획의 성공을 굳게 확신하며 집 안으로 다시 들어갔다."]], [["Still, as five o’clock approached, and the carriage was momentarily expected by the count, the indication of more than common impatience and uneasiness might be observed in his manner.", "그래도 다섯 시가 가까워지고, 마차가 곧 들이닥치리라 백작이 시시각각 기다리는 동안, 그의 태도에서는 예사롭지 않은 조바심과 불안의 기색이 엿보였다."], ["He stationed himself in a room commanding a view of the street, pacing the chamber with restless steps, stopping merely to listen from time to time for the sound of approaching wheels, then to cast an anxious glance on Ali; but the regularity with which the Nubian puffed forth the smoke of his chibouque proved that he at least was wholly absorbed in the enjoyment of his favorite occupation.", "그는 거리가 내려다보이는 방에 자리를 잡고, 잠시도 가만있지 못하는 발걸음으로 방 안을 서성였다. 이따금 다가오는 바퀴 소리에 귀를 기울이느라 멈춰 섰다가, 다시 알리에게 초조한 눈길을 던졌다. 그러나 누비아인이 한결같은 박자로 물담뱃대 연기를 뿜어 대는 것으로 보아, 적어도 그만은 가장 좋아하는 일에 흠뻑 빠져 있는 게 분명했다."]], [["Suddenly a distant sound of rapidly advancing wheels was heard, and almost immediately a carriage appeared, drawn by a pair of wild, ungovernable horses, while the terrified coachman strove in vain to restrain their furious speed.", "문득 멀리서 바퀴가 빠르게 굴러오는 소리가 들리더니, 거의 곧바로 마차 한 대가 나타났다. 사납고 걷잡을 수 없는 말 한 쌍이 끄는 마차였다. 겁에 질린 마부는 그 미친 듯한 속도를 억누르려 애썼으나 헛일이었다."]], [["In the vehicle was a young woman and a child of about seven or eight clasped in each other’s arms.", "마차 안에는 젊은 여인과 일고여덟 살쯤 된 아이가 서로를 끌어안고 있었다."], ["Terror seemed to have deprived them even of the power of uttering a cry.", "공포가 그들에게서 비명을 지를 힘마저 앗아 간 듯했다."], ["The carriage creaked and rattled as it flew over the rough stones, and the slightest obstacle under the wheels would have caused disaster; but it kept on in the middle of the road, and those who saw it pass uttered cries of terror.", "마차는 우툴두툴한 돌길 위를 내달리며 삐걱대고 덜컹거렸고, 바퀴 밑에 자그마한 장애물 하나만 걸려도 큰일이 날 판이었다. 그러나 마차는 길 한복판을 따라 계속 달려갔고, 그것이 지나가는 것을 본 사람들은 공포에 찬 비명을 질렀다."]], [["Ali suddenly cast aside his chibouque, drew the lasso from his pocket, threw it so skilfully as to catch the forelegs of the near horse in its triple fold, and suffered himself to be dragged on for a few steps by the violence of the shock, then the animal fell over on the pole, which snapped, and therefore prevented the other horse from pursuing its way.", "알리는 별안간 물담뱃대를 내던지고 주머니에서 올가미를 꺼내, 가까운 쪽 말의 앞다리를 세 겹으로 옭아맬 만큼 능숙하게 던졌다. 그러고는 충격의 거센 힘에 몇 걸음 끌려가도록 몸을 내맡겼다. 그러자 그 말이 끌채 위로 고꾸라지면서 끌채가 부러졌고, 그 바람에 다른 말도 더는 내달리지 못하게 되었다."], ["Gladly availing himself of this opportunity, the coachman leaped from his box; but Ali had promptly seized the nostrils of the second horse, and held them in his iron grasp, till the beast, snorting with pain, sunk beside his companion.", "마부는 이 틈을 반갑게 이용해 마부석에서 뛰어내렸다. 그러나 알리는 이미 두 번째 말의 콧구멍을 잽싸게 움켜쥐고 쇠집게 같은 손아귀에 단단히 붙들고 있었고, 마침내 그 짐승은 고통으로 콧김을 내뿜으며 동료 곁에 주저앉고 말았다."]], [["All this was achieved in much less time than is occupied in the recital.", "이 모든 일은 이야기로 옮기는 데 걸리는 시간보다 훨씬 짧은 동안에 이루어졌다."], ["The brief space had, however, been sufficient for a man, followed by a number of servants, to rush from the house before which the accident had occurred, and, as the coachman opened the door of the carriage, to take from it a lady who was convulsively grasping the cushions with one hand, while with the other she pressed to her bosom the young boy, who had lost consciousness.", "그러나 그 짧은 틈은, 사고가 일어난 집에서 한 남자가 하인 여럿을 거느리고 뛰쳐나오기에 충분했다. 마부가 마차 문을 열자, 그는 한 손으로 방석을 발작적으로 움켜쥐고 다른 손으로는 정신을 잃은 어린 사내아이를 가슴에 끌어안은 부인을 마차에서 안아 내렸다."], ["Monte Cristo carried them both to the salon, and deposited them on a sofa.", "몬테크리스토는 두 사람을 살롱으로 안아 들고 가, 소파에 뉘었다."]], [["“Compose yourself, madame,”", "\"마음을 가라앉히십시오, 부인.\""], ["said he; “all danger is over.”", "그가 말했다. \"위험은 다 지나갔습니다.\""], ["The woman looked up at these words, and, with a glance far more expressive than any entreaties could have been, pointed to her child, who still continued insensible.", "그 말에 부인이 고개를 들었다. 그러고는 어떤 간청보다 훨씬 더 절절한 눈길로, 여전히 의식이 없는 자기 아이를 가리켰다. \"부인의 두려움이 어떤 것인지 잘 압니다.\""], ["“I understand the nature of your alarms, madame,”", "백작이 아이를 조심스레 살피며 말했다."], ["said the count, carefully examining the child, “but I assure you there is not the slightest occasion for uneasiness; your little charge has not received the least injury; his insensibility is merely the effects of terror, and will soon pass.”", "\"하지만 불안해하실 까닭이 조금도 없다고 장담합니다. 이 아이는 어디 하나 다친 데가 없습니다. 정신을 잃은 것은 그저 공포 때문이니, 곧 깨어날 겁니다.\""]], [["“Are you quite sure you do not say so to tranquillize my fears? See how deadly pale he is!", "\"제 두려움을 달래시려고 하시는 말씀은 아니신가요? 아이가 어찌나 새파랗게 질렸는지 보세요!"], ["My child, my darling Edward; speak to your mother—open your dear eyes and look on me once again!", "내 아가, 사랑하는 에두아르, 엄마한테 말 좀 해 보렴. 그 예쁜 눈 좀 뜨고 한 번만 더 엄마를 봐 다오!"], ["Oh, sir, in pity send for a physician; my whole fortune shall not be thought too much for the recovery of my boy.”", "오, 선생님, 제발 의사를 좀 불러 주세요. 제 아이만 살릴 수 있다면 제 전 재산도 아깝지 않아요.\""]], [["With a calm smile and a gentle wave of the hand, Monte Cristo signed to the distracted mother to lay aside her apprehensions; then, opening a casket that stood near, he drew forth a phial of Bohemian glass incrusted with gold, containing a liquid of the color of blood, of which he let fall a single drop on the child’s lips.", "몬테크리스토는 차분한 미소와 부드러운 손짓으로, 정신없이 애태우는 어머니에게 걱정을 내려놓으라는 뜻을 비쳤다. 그러고는 곁에 놓인 작은 함을 열어, 금으로 상감 세공을 한 보헤미아 유리병 하나를 꺼냈다. 피처럼 붉은 빛깔의 액체가 담긴 병이었다."], ["Scarcely had it reached them, ere the boy, though still pale as marble, opened his eyes, and eagerly gazed around him.", "그는 그 액체를 아이의 입술에 딱 한 방울 떨어뜨렸다. 그것이 입술에 닿기가 무섭게, 아이는 여전히 대리석처럼 창백한 채로 눈을 뜨더니 주위를 두리번거렸다."], ["At this, the delight of the mother was almost frantic.", "그 모습에 어머니의 기쁨은 거의 광란에 가까울 지경이었다."]], [["“Where am I?”", "\"제가 어디 있는 거죠?\""], ["exclaimed she; “and to whom am I indebted for so happy a termination to my late dreadful alarm?”", "그녀가 외쳤다. \"그리고 그토록 끔찍했던 두려움이 이렇게 다행스럽게 끝난 것은 누구 덕분인가요?\""]], [["“Madame,”", "\"부인,\""], ["answered the count, “you are under the roof of one who esteems himself most fortunate in having been able to save you from a further continuance of your sufferings.”", "백작이 대답했다. \"부인을 더 큰 고통에서 구해 드릴 수 있었던 것을 더없는 행운으로 여기는 사람의 지붕 아래 계십니다.\""]], [["“My wretched curiosity has brought all this about,”", "\"이 모든 일이 다 제 못된 호기심 탓이에요.\""], ["pursued the lady.", "부인이 말을 이었다."], ["“All Paris rung with the praises of Madame Danglars’ beautiful horses, and I had the folly to desire to know whether they really merited the high praise given to them.”", "\"온 파리가 당글라르 부인의 아름다운 말들을 입에 침이 마르도록 칭찬하기에, 그 말들이 정말로 그런 극찬을 받을 만한지 알고 싶다는 어리석은 생각을 했던 거지요.\""]], [["“Is it possible,”", "\"그게 가능한 일입니까?\""], ["exclaimed the count with well-feigned astonishment, “that these horses belong to the baroness?”", "백작이 그럴듯하게 꾸민 놀라움으로 외쳤다. \"그 말들이 남작 부인의 것이라니요?\""]], [["“They do, indeed.", "\"정말 그렇답니다."], ["May I inquire if you are acquainted with Madame Danglars?”", "그런데 혹시 당글라르 부인을 아시는지 여쭤봐도 될까요?\""]], [["20351m\n“I have that honor; and my happiness at your escape from the danger that threatened you is redoubled by the consciousness that I have been the unwilling and the unintentional cause of all the peril you have incurred.", "\"그분을 아는 영광을 누리고 있습니다. 부인을 위협한 위험에서 부인이 벗어나신 데 대한 제 기쁨은, 부인이 겪으신 그 모든 위험의 본의 아닌, 또 의도치 않은 원인이 바로 저였다는 것을 깨닫고 나니 곱절이 됩니다."], ["I yesterday purchased these horses of the baron; but as the baroness evidently regretted parting with them, I ventured to send them back to her, with a request that she would gratify me by accepting them from my hands.”", "어제 제가 남작에게서 그 말들을 샀는데, 남작 부인이 말들과 헤어지기를 못내 아쉬워하시는 게 분명하기에, 제 손에서 그것들을 받아 주십사 청하며 도로 보내 드렸던 겁니다.\""]], [["“You are, then, doubtless, the Count of Monte Cristo, of whom Hermine has talked to me so much?”", "\"그렇다면 분명 에르민이 제게 그토록 많은 이야기를 해 준 그 몬테크리스토 백작님이시군요?\""]], [["“You have rightly guessed, madame,”", "\"바로 맞히셨습니다, 부인.\""], ["replied the count.", "백작이 대답했다."]], [["“And I am Madame Héloïse de Villefort.”", "\"저는 엘로이즈 드 빌포르랍니다.\""]], [["The count bowed with the air of a person who hears a name for the first time.", "백작은 처음 듣는 이름이라는 듯한 태도로 고개를 숙였다."]], [["“How grateful will M. de Villefort be for all your goodness; how thankfully will he acknowledge that to you alone he owes the existence of his wife and child!", "\"빌포르 씨께서 백작님의 그 모든 친절에 얼마나 감사해하실까요. 자신의 아내와 아이가 살아 있는 것이 오직 백작님 덕분임을 얼마나 고마운 마음으로 인정하실까요!"], ["Most certainly, but for the prompt assistance of your intrepid servant, this dear child and myself must both have perished.”", "백작님의 용감한 하인이 그토록 재빨리 도와주지 않았더라면, 이 가엾은 아이와 저는 분명 둘 다 목숨을 잃었을 거예요.\""]], [["“Indeed, I still shudder at the fearful danger you were placed in.”", "\"정말이지, 부인이 처하셨던 그 무시무시한 위험을 생각하면 저도 여태 몸서리가 쳐집니다.\""]], [["“I trust you will allow me to recompense worthily the devotion of your man.”", "\"백작님의 하인이 보여 준 그 헌신에 마땅한 보답을 할 수 있도록 허락해 주시기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["“I beseech you, madame,”", "\"간청드립니다, 부인.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo “not to spoil Ali, either by too great praise or rewards.", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"지나친 칭찬이나 보상으로 알리를 버릇 들이지 말아 주십시오."], ["I cannot allow him to acquire the habit of expecting to be recompensed for every trifling service he may render. Ali is my slave, and in saving your life he was but discharging his duty to me.”", "사소한 일을 할 때마다 보답을 바라는 버릇을 들이게 둘 수는 없으니까요. 알리는 제 노예이고, 부인의 목숨을 구한 것은 제게 대한 자기 의무를 다한 것에 지나지 않습니다.\""]], [["“Nay,”", "\"아니에요,\""], ["interposed Madame de Villefort, on whom the authoritative style adopted by the count made a deep impression, “nay, but consider that to preserve my life he has risked his own.”", "백작이 취한 위엄 어린 말투에 깊은 인상을 받은 빌포르 부인이 끼어들었다. \"아니에요, 그래도 제 목숨을 구하려고 자기 목숨을 걸었다는 걸 생각해 보세요.\""]], [["“His life, madame, belongs not to him; it is mine, in return for my having myself saved him from death.”", "\"그의 목숨은 그의 것이 아닙니다, 부인. 제가 그를 죽음에서 구해 준 대가로 받은 제 것이지요.\""]], [["Madame de Villefort made no further reply; her mind was utterly absorbed in the contemplation of the person who, from the first instant she saw him, had made so powerful an impression on her.", "빌포르 부인은 더는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다. 그녀의 마음은 처음 본 순간부터 그토록 강렬한 인상을 안겨 준 그 사람을 가만히 바라보는 데 온통 빠져 있었다."]], [["During the evident preoccupation of Madame de Villefort, Monte Cristo scrutinized the features and appearance of the boy she kept folded in her arms, lavishing on him the most tender endearments.", "빌포르 부인이 이렇게 넋을 놓고 있는 동안, 몬테크리스토는 그녀가 두 팔로 끌어안고 더없이 다정한 애무를 퍼붓는 아이의 이목구비와 생김새를 찬찬히 뜯어보았다."], ["The child was small for his age, and unnaturally pale.", "아이는 나이에 비해 몸집이 작고, 부자연스러울 만큼 창백했다."], ["A mass of straight black hair, defying all attempts to train or curl it, fell over his projecting forehead, and hung down to his shoulders, giving increased vivacity to eyes already sparkling with a youthful love of mischief and fondness for every forbidden enjoyment.", "빗질이나 곱슬로 다듬으려는 온갖 시도를 거부하는 뻣뻣한 검은 머리카락이 툭 튀어나온 이마 위로 흘러내려 어깨까지 닿았고, 그 때문에 이미 짓궂은 장난을 좋아하는 어린아이다운 기운과 온갖 금지된 즐거움을 탐하는 빛으로 반짝이던 두 눈이 한층 더 생기를 띠었다."], ["His mouth was large, and the lips, which had not yet regained their color, were particularly thin; in fact, the deep and crafty look, giving a predominant expression to the child’s face, belonged rather to a boy of twelve or fourteen than to one so young.", "입은 컸고, 아직 핏기가 돌아오지 않은 입술은 유난히 얇았다. 사실 그 아이의 얼굴을 지배하는 깊고 교활한 표정은, 그렇게 어린아이라기보다 차라리 열두어 살이나 열네 살쯤 된 소년에게나 어울릴 법한 것이었다."], ["His first movement was to free himself by a violent push from the encircling arms of his mother, and to rush forward to the casket from whence the count had taken the phial of elixir; then, without asking permission of anyone, he proceeded, in all the wilfulness of a spoiled child unaccustomed to restrain either whims or caprices, to pull the corks out of all the bottles.", "아이가 맨 처음 한 짓은 자기를 감싼 어머니의 두 팔을 사납게 밀쳐 내고 빠져나가, 백작이 영약이 든 병을 꺼냈던 그 함으로 달려가는 것이었다. 그러고는 누구의 허락도 구하지 않고, 변덕이든 어리광이든 도무지 억제할 줄 모르는 응석받이 아이 특유의 고집으로, 모든 병의 마개를 죄다 뽑아내기 시작했다."]], [["“Touch nothing, my little friend,”", "\"아무것도 만지지 마라, 꼬마 친구야.\""], ["cried the count eagerly; “some of those liquids are not only dangerous to taste, but even to inhale.”", "백작이 다급히 외쳤다. \"그 액체들 중에는 맛보는 것은 물론이고 들이마시기만 해도 위험한 것이 있단다.\""]], [["Madame de Villefort became very pale, and, seizing her son’s arm, drew him anxiously toward her; but, once satisfied of his safety, she also cast a brief but expressive glance on the casket, which was not lost upon the count.", "빌포르 부인이 새파랗게 질려 아들의 팔을 붙잡고 안타까이 제 쪽으로 끌어당겼다. 그러나 아이가 무사한 것을 확인하고 나자, 그녀는 그 함에도 짧지만 의미심장한 눈길을 던졌고, 그것은 백작의 눈을 비껴가지 못했다."], ["At this moment Ali entered.", "이때 알리가 들어왔다."], ["At sight of him Madame de Villefort uttered an expression of pleasure, and, holding the child still closer towards her, she said:", "그를 보자 빌포르 부인은 반가운 소리를 내며 아이를 한층 더 가까이 끌어안고 말했다."]], [["“Edward, dearest, do you see that good man?", "\"에두아르, 우리 아가, 저 착한 분이 보이니?"], ["He has shown very great courage and resolution, for he exposed his own life to stop the horses that were running away with us, and would certainly have dashed the carriage to pieces.", "저분은 아주 큰 용기와 결단을 보여 주셨단다. 우리를 태우고 달아나던 말들을, 분명 마차를 산산조각 냈을 그 말들을 멈춰 세우려고 자기 목숨까지 거셨거든."], ["Thank him, then, my child, in your very best manner; for, had he not come to our aid, neither you nor I would have been alive to speak our thanks.”", "그러니 저분께 감사드리렴, 우리 아가, 네가 할 수 있는 가장 예쁜 말로 말이야. 저분이 우리를 도우러 와 주시지 않았다면, 너도 나도 이렇게 살아서 감사 인사를 드리지 못했을 테니까.\""]], [["The child stuck out his lips and turned away his head in a disdainful manner, saying, “He’s too ugly.”", "아이는 입을 삐죽 내밀고 멸시하듯 고개를 돌리며 말했다. \"너무 못생겼는걸요.\""]], [["20353m\nThe count smiled as if the child bade fair to realize his hopes, while Madame de Villefort reprimanded her son with a gentleness and moderation very far from conveying the least idea of a fault having been committed.", "백작은 그 아이가 자신의 기대를 그대로 이루어 줄 것 같다는 듯 미소 지었다. 한편 빌포르 부인은 무슨 잘못을 저질렀다는 생각이라고는 조금도 들지 않을 만큼 부드럽고 누그러진 태도로 아들을 나무랐다."]], [["“This lady,”", "\"이 부인께서는,\""], ["said the Count, speaking to Ali in the Arabic language, “is desirous that her son should thank you for saving both their lives; but the boy refuses, saying you are too ugly.”", "백작이 아랍어로 알리에게 말했다. \"아들이 너에게 두 사람의 목숨을 구해 준 데 대해 감사하기를 바라신다. 그런데 아이는 네가 너무 못생겼다며 거절하는구나.\""]], [["Ali turned his intelligent countenance towards the boy, on whom he gazed without any apparent emotion; but the spasmodic working of the nostrils showed to the practiced eye of Monte Cristo that the Arab had been wounded to the heart.", "알리는 영민한 얼굴을 아이 쪽으로 돌려, 겉으로는 아무런 감정도 없이 아이를 바라보았다. 그러나 콧구멍이 경련하듯 실룩이는 것을 보고, 몬테크리스토의 노련한 눈은 그 아랍인이 가슴 깊이 상처받았음을 알아챘다."]], [["“Will you permit me to inquire,”", "\"여쭤봐도 될까요?\""], ["said Madame de Villefort, as she arose to take her leave, “whether you usually reside here?”", "빌포르 부인이 작별을 고하려 일어서며 말했다. \"백작님은 보통 이곳에 머무시나요?\""]], [["“No, I do not,”", "\"아닙니다.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo; “it is a small place I have purchased quite lately.", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"이건 제가 아주 최근에 사들인 작은 곳입니다."], ["My place of abode is No. 30, Avenue des Champs-Élysées; but I see you have quite recovered from your fright, and are, no doubt, desirous of returning home.", "제가 사는 곳은 샹젤리제 거리 30번지지요. 그런데 부인께서는 놀라셨던 것에서 완전히 회복하신 모양이고, 분명 댁으로 돌아가고 싶으시겠지요."], ["Anticipating your wishes, I have desired the same horses you came with to be put to one of my carriages, and Ali, he whom you think so very ugly,”", "부인의 뜻을 미리 헤아려, 부인이 타고 오셨던 그 말들을 제 마차 한 대에 매어 두라고 일러 두었습니다. 그리고 부인께서 그토록 못생겼다고 여기시는 알리가,\""], ["continued he, addressing the boy with a smiling air, “will have the honor of driving you home, while your coachman remains here to attend to the necessary repairs of your calash.", "그는 미소 띤 얼굴로 아이에게 말을 건네며 말을 이었다. \"부인을 댁까지 모셔다 드리는 영광을 누릴 겁니다. 부인의 마부는 여기 남아 마차의 필요한 수리를 맡고요."], ["As soon as that important business is concluded, I will have a pair of my own horses harnessed to convey it direct to Madame Danglars.”", "그 중요한 일이 끝나는 대로, 제 말 한 쌍을 매어 그 마차를 곧장 당글라르 부인께 보내 드리겠습니다.\""]], [["“I dare not return with those dreadful horses,”", "\"그 끔찍한 말들을 타고 돌아갈 엄두가 나지 않아요.\""], ["said Madame de Villefort.", "빌포르 부인이 말했다."]], [["“You will see,”", "\"보시면 아실 겁니다.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo, “that they will be as different as possible in the hands of Ali.", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"알리의 손에 맡겨지면 그 말들이 더없이 딴판이 된다는 것을요."], ["With him they will be gentle and docile as lambs.”", "그의 손에서는 양처럼 순하고 고분고분해질 테니까요.\""]], [["Ali had, indeed, given proof of this; for, approaching the animals, who had been got upon their legs with considerable difficulty, he rubbed their foreheads and nostrils with a sponge soaked in aromatic vinegar, and wiped off the sweat and foam that covered their mouths.", "과연 알리는 그것을 증명해 보였다. 그는 가까스로 일으켜 세운 말들에게 다가가, 향긋한 식초를 적신 해면으로 말들의 이마와 콧구멍을 문지르고, 입을 뒤덮은 땀과 거품을 닦아 냈다."], ["Then, commencing a loud whistling noise, he rubbed them well all over their bodies for several minutes; then, undisturbed by the noisy crowd collected round the broken carriage, Ali quietly harnessed the pacified animals to the count’s chariot, took the reins in his hands, and mounted the box, when to the utter astonishment of those who had witnessed the ungovernable spirit and maddened speed of the same horses, he was actually compelled to apply his whip in no very gentle manner before he could induce them to start; and even then all that could be obtained from the celebrated “dappled grays,”", "그런 다음 큰 소리로 휘파람을 불기 시작하면서, 몇 분 동안 말들의 온몸을 구석구석 잘 문질러 주었다. 그러고는 부서진 마차 둘레에 모여든 소란스러운 군중에도 아랑곳없이, 알리는 잠잠해진 말들을 백작의 마차에 차분히 매고 고삐를 손에 쥔 채 마부석에 올랐다. 그러자 바로 그 말들의 걷잡을 수 없는 사나움과 미친 듯한 질주를 목격했던 사람들이 깜짝 놀라게도, 그는 채찍을 자못 매섭게 휘두른 뒤에야 비로소 말들을 움직이게 할 수 있었다."], ["now changed into a couple of dull, sluggish, stupid brutes, was a slow, pottering pace, kept up with so much difficulty that Madame de Villefort was more than two hours returning to her residence in the Faubourg Saint-Honoré. 20355m", "게다가 그러고도 이제 둔하고 굼뜨고 멍청한 두 마리 짐승으로 변해 버린 그 이름난 \"얼룩 회색 말들\"에게서 끌어낼 수 있었던 것이라고는, 어찌나 힘겹게 유지되는지 느릿느릿 굼뜬 걸음뿐이었다. 그 탓에 빌포르 부인은 생토노레 거리의 자기 집으로 돌아가는 데 두 시간이 넘게 걸렸다."]], [["Scarcely had the first congratulations upon her marvellous escape been gone through when she wrote the following letter to Madame Danglars:—", "기적 같은 탈출에 대한 첫 축하 인사가 채 끝나기도 전에, 그녀는 당글라르 부인에게 다음과 같은 편지를 썼다."]], [["“Dear Hermine,—I have just had a wonderful escape from the most imminent danger, and I owe my safety to the very Count of Monte Cristo we were talking about yesterday, but whom I little expected to see today.", "\"친애하는 에르민, 저는 방금 더없이 급박한 위험에서 놀랍게 벗어났답니다. 그리고 제 무사함은 바로 어제 우리가 이야기하던 그 몬테크리스토 백작님 덕분이지요. 오늘 그분을 뵙게 되리라고는 전혀 생각지 못했는데 말이에요."], ["I remember how unmercifully I laughed at what I considered your eulogistic and exaggerated praises of him; but I have now ample cause to admit that your enthusiastic description of this wonderful man fell far short of his merits.", "제가 당신의 그 칭찬을 과장된 미사여구라 여기고 얼마나 매정하게 비웃었는지 기억나요. 하지만 이제 저는 그 놀라운 분에 대한 당신의 열렬한 묘사가 그분의 진가에 한참 못 미쳤음을 인정할 충분한 이유가 생겼답니다."], ["Your horses got as far as Ranelagh, when they darted forward like mad things, and galloped away at so fearful a rate, that there seemed no other prospect for myself and my poor Edward but that of being dashed to pieces against the first object that impeded their progress, when a strange-looking man,—an Arab, a negro, or a Nubian, at least a black of some nation or other—at a signal from the count, whose domestic he is, suddenly seized and stopped the infuriated animals, even at the risk of being trampled to death himself; and certainly he must have had a most wonderful escape.", "당신의 말들이 라넬라까지 갔을 때, 미친 것들처럼 앞으로 내달리더니 어찌나 무시무시한 속도로 질주하던지, 저와 가엾은 에두아르에게는 앞을 가로막는 첫 번째 물체에 부딪혀 산산조각 나는 것 말고는 다른 앞날이 없어 보였어요. 바로 그때 이상하게 생긴 한 남자가, 아랍인인지 흑인인지 누비아인인지, 적어도 어느 나라 사람이든 검은 피부의 사내가, 자기 주인인 백작의 신호 한 번에 자칫 짓밟혀 죽을 위험까지 무릅쓰고 별안간 그 광란하는 짐승들을 붙들어 멈춰 세웠답니다. 그 사내야말로 정말 기적적으로 목숨을 건진 게 틀림없어요."], ["The count then hastened to us, and took us into his house, where he speedily recalled my poor Edward to life. He sent us home in his own carriage.", "그러자 백작님이 우리에게 황급히 달려와 우리를 집 안으로 데려가셨고, 거기서 가엾은 에두아르를 금세 되살려 내셨지요. 그분은 자신의 마차로 우리를 집까지 보내 주셨어요."], ["Yours will be returned to you tomorrow.", "당신의 마차는 내일 돌아갈 거예요."], ["You will find your horses in bad condition, from the results of this accident; they seem thoroughly stupefied, as if sulky and vexed at having been conquered by man.", "당신의 말들은 이 사고의 여파로 상태가 말이 아닐 거예요. 사람에게 정복당한 게 분하고 토라진 듯, 완전히 얼이 빠진 모양이더군요."], ["The count, however, has commissioned me to assure you that two or three days’ rest, with plenty of barley for their sole food during that time, will bring them back to as fine, that is as terrifying, a condition as they were in yesterday. 20356m", "그래도 백작님이 제게 단단히 일러 주시기를, 이삼일만 쉬게 하면서 그동안 보리만 잔뜩 먹이면 어제처럼 멋진, 그러니까 어제처럼 무시무시한 상태로 돌아갈 거라고 하셨답니다."]], [["Adieu!", "그럼 이만! 어제의 나들이에 대해서는 그다지 감사를 전할 수가 없네요. 하지만 따지고 보면, 당신 말들의 못된 짓을 당신 탓으로 돌릴 수는 없겠지요."], ["I cannot return you many thanks for the drive of yesterday; but, after all, I ought not to blame you for the misconduct of your horses, more especially as it procured me the pleasure of an introduction to the Count of Monte Cristo,—and certainly that illustrious personage, apart from the millions he is said to be so very anxious to dispose of, seemed to me one of those curiously interesting problems I, for one, delight in solving at any risk, even if it were to necessitate another drive to the Bois behind your horses.", "더구나 그 덕분에 몬테크리스토 백작님을 소개받는 기쁨까지 얻었으니까요. 그리고 정말이지 그 명망 높은 분은, 그토록 처분하고 싶어 안달이라는 그 수백만 프랑을 차치하고라도, 제가 어떤 위험을 무릅쓰고서라도 풀어 보기를 즐기는 그 묘하게 흥미로운 수수께끼 같은 분들 중 하나로 보였어요. 설령 당신 말들 뒤에 매여 불로뉴 숲으로 또 한 번 나들이를 가야 한다 해도 말이지요."]], [["Edward endured the accident with miraculous courage—he did not utter a single cry, but fell lifeless into my arms; nor did a tear fall from his eyes after it was over.", "에두아르는 그 사고를 기적 같은 용기로 견뎌 냈답니다. 비명 한 번 지르지 않고 정신을 잃은 채 제 품으로 쓰러졌고, 일이 다 끝난 뒤에도 눈물 한 방울 흘리지 않았어요."], ["I doubt not you will consider these praises the result of blind maternal affection, but there is a soul of iron in that delicate, fragile body.", "당신은 이게 다 눈먼 모정에서 나온 칭찬이라 여기시겠지만, 그 가냘프고 여린 몸 안에는 쇠 같은 영혼이 깃들어 있답니다."], ["Valentine sends many affectionate remembrances to your dear Eugénie.", "발랑틴이 당신의 사랑스러운 외제니에게 다정한 안부를 전해요."], ["I embrace you with all my heart.", "온 마음을 다해 당신을 껴안습니다."]], [["Héloïse de Villefort.", "엘로이즈 드 빌포르."]], [["P.S.—Do pray contrive some means for me to meet the Count of Monte Cristo at your house.", "추신. 부디 제가 당신 댁에서 몬테크리스토 백작님을 만날 방도를 좀 마련해 주세요."], ["I must and will see him again. I have just made M.", "저는 그분을 꼭 다시 봐야겠고, 또 보고야 말 거예요."], ["de Villefort promise to call on him, and I hope the visit will be returned.", "방금 빌포르 씨에게 그분을 찾아뵙겠다는 약속을 받아 두었으니, 그 답방도 있기를 바라고 있어요.\""]], [["That night the adventure at Auteuil was talked of everywhere.", "그날 밤, 오퇴유에서 일어난 그 사건은 어디서나 화젯거리가 되었다."], ["Albert related it to his mother; Château-Renaud recounted it at the Jockey Club, and Debray detailed it at length in the salons of the minister; even Beauchamp accorded twenty lines in his journal to the relation of the count’s courage and gallantry, thereby celebrating him as the greatest hero of the day in the eyes of all the feminine members of the aristocracy.", "알베르는 그것을 어머니에게 이야기했고, 샤토르노는 조키 클럽에서 그 일을 떠벌렸으며, 드브레는 장관의 살롱에서 그것을 길게 늘어놓았다. 보샹조차 백작의 용기와 호기로움을 다룬 이야기에 자기 신문의 스무 줄을 할애해, 귀족 사회의 모든 여성 독자들 눈에 그를 그날의 가장 위대한 영웅으로 기렸다."]], [["Vast was the crowd of visitors and inquiring friends who left their names at the residence of Madame de Villefort, with the design of renewing their visit at the right moment, of hearing from her lips all the interesting circumstances of this most romantic adventure.", "빌포르 부인의 집에는 방문객과 궁금증에 찬 벗들이 떼를 지어 몰려와 이름을 남기고 갔다. 적당한 때를 골라 다시 찾아와, 이 더없이 낭만적인 사건의 흥미로운 사정을 부인의 입을 통해 직접 들으려는 속셈이었다."]], [["As for M. de Villefort, he fulfilled the predictions of Héloïse to the letter,—donned his dress suit, drew on a pair of white gloves, ordered the servants to attend the carriage dressed in their full livery, and drove that same night to No.", "빌포르 씨로 말하자면, 그는 엘로이즈의 예언을 한 자 한 자 그대로 이루어 냈다."], ["30 in the Avenue des Champs-Élysées. 30009m", "예복을 차려입고 흰 장갑을 끼고, 하인들에게는 정장 제복을 입고 마차를 수행하라 이른 뒤, 바로 그날 밤 샹젤리제 거리 30번지로 마차를 몰았다."]], [["# Chapter 48. Ideology", "# Chapter 48. Ideology"]], [["30011m\nIf the Count of Monte Cristo had been for a long time familiar with the ways of Parisian society, he would have appreciated better the significance of the step which M. de Villefort had taken.", "몬테크리스토 백작이 파리 사교계의 방식에 오랫동안 익숙했더라면, 빌포르 씨가 취한 행동의 의미를 더 잘 헤아렸을 것이다. 군주가 왕가의 큰집 출신이든 작은집 출신이든, 정부가 교조적 자유주의이든 보수주의이든, 그는 늘 궁정에서 입지가 탄탄했다."], ["Standing well at court, whether the king regnant was of the older or younger branch, whether the government was doctrinaire liberal, or conservative; looked upon by all as a man of talent, since those who have never experienced a political check are generally so regarded; hated by many, but warmly supported by others, without being really liked by anybody, M.", "정치적 좌절을 한 번도 겪어 보지 못한 사람은 으레 유능하다고 여겨지는 법이니, 그 역시 누구에게나 재능 있는 인물로 통했다. 많은 이의 미움을 사면서도 또 어떤 이들로부터는 열렬한 지지를 받았으나, 정작 누구에게도 진심으로 사랑받지는 못했다."], ["de Villefort held a high position in the magistracy, and maintained his eminence like a Harlay or a Molé.", "그런 빌포르 씨는 사법계에서 높은 자리를 차지하고서 아를레나 몰레처럼 그 위엄을 지켰다."], ["His drawing-room, under the regenerating influence of a young wife and a daughter by his first marriage, scarcely eighteen, was still one of the well-regulated Paris salons where the worship of traditional customs and the observance of rigid etiquette were carefully maintained.", "젊은 아내와 첫 결혼에서 얻은 이제 겨우 열여덟 살 난 딸의 생기 있는 손길 덕분에, 그의 응접실은 여전히 파리에서 손꼽히는 절도 있는 살롱 가운데 하나였고, 그곳에서는 전통 관습에 대한 숭배와 엄격한 예법의 준수가 정성스레 지켜졌다."], ["A freezing politeness, a strict fidelity to government principles, a profound contempt for theories and theorists, a deep-seated hatred of ideality,—these were the elements of private and public life displayed by M. de Villefort.", "얼음장 같은 정중함, 정부 원칙에 대한 철저한 충실, 이론과 이론가를 향한 깊은 경멸, 이상주의에 대한 뿌리 깊은 증오, 이런 것들이 빌포르 씨가 사생활과 공적 생활에서 드러내 보이는 요소들이었다."]], [["30023m\nM. de Villefort was not only a magistrate, he was almost a diplomatist.", "빌포르 씨는 사법관일 뿐 아니라 거의 외교관이기도 했다."], ["His relations with the former court, of which he always spoke with dignity and respect, made him respected by the new one, and he knew so many things, that not only was he always carefully considered, but sometimes consulted.", "그가 언제나 위엄과 경의를 담아 이야기하던 옛 궁정과의 관계 덕분에 그는 새 궁정에서도 존경을 받았다. 그는 너무도 많은 것을 알고 있어서, 늘 신중한 대우를 받을 뿐 아니라 때로는 자문을 구하는 대상이 되기도 했다."], ["Perhaps this would not have been so had it been possible to get rid of M.", "빌포르 씨를 제거할 수만 있었다면 사정은 달랐을지도 모른다."], ["de Villefort; but, like the feudal barons who rebelled against their sovereign, he dwelt in an impregnable fortress.", "그러나 군주에게 반기를 든 봉건 영주들처럼, 그는 난공불락의 요새 안에 들어앉아 있었다."], ["This fortress was his post as king’s attorney, all the advantages of which he exploited with marvellous skill, and which he would not have resigned but to be made deputy, and thus to replace neutrality by opposition.", "그 요새란 검사라는 자리였고, 그는 그 자리의 온갖 이점을 놀라운 솜씨로 활용했다. 그가 그 자리를 내놓을 일이라곤, 대의원이 되어 중립을 야당의 위치로 바꾸는 경우뿐이었다."]], [["Ordinarily M. de Villefort made and returned very few visits.", "빌포르 씨는 평소 거의 방문을 하지도, 받지도 않았다."], ["His wife visited for him, and this was the received thing in the world, where the weighty and multifarious occupations of the magistrate were accepted as an excuse for what was really only calculated pride, a manifestation of professed superiority—in fact, the application of the axiom, _Pretend to think well of yourself, and the world will think well of you_, an axiom a hundred times more useful in society nowadays than that of the Greeks, “Know thyself,”", "그의 아내가 그를 대신해 방문을 다녔고, 사교계에서는 이것이 당연한 일로 받아들여졌다. 사람들은 사법관의 무겁고도 잡다한 업무를 핑계로 인정해 주었으나, 그것은 사실 계산된 자존심에 지나지 않았으며, 공언된 우월감의 표현이었다. 결국 \"스스로를 높이 여기는 척하라, 그러면 세상이 그대를 높이 여길 것이다\"라는 격언을 실천하는 셈이었다."], ["a knowledge for which, in our days, we have substituted the less difficult and more advantageous science of _knowing others_.", "이 격언은 오늘날 사교계에서 그리스인들의 \"너 자신을 알라\"보다 백 배는 더 쓸모가 있다. 우리 시대에 와서 그 자기 인식은 더 손쉽고 더 이로운 학문, 곧 \"남을 아는\" 기술로 대체되었으니 말이다."]], [["To his friends M. de Villefort was a powerful protector; to his enemies, he was a silent, but bitter opponent; for those who were neither the one nor the other, he was a statue of the law-made man.", "빌포르 씨는 친구들에게는 든든한 보호자였고, 적들에게는 말이 없으나 모진 적수였다. 둘 중 어느 쪽도 아닌 사람들에게 그는 법으로 빚어진 인간의 조각상이었다."], ["He had a haughty bearing, a look either steady and impenetrable or insolently piercing and inquisitorial.", "그는 거만한 태도에, 한결같고 속을 알 수 없거나 아니면 무례할 만큼 꿰뚫어 보며 캐묻는 듯한 눈빛을 지녔다."], ["Four successive revolutions had built and cemented the pedestal upon which his fortune was based.", "네 차례에 걸친 혁명이 그의 출세가 딛고 선 받침대를 쌓아 올리고 굳혀 주었다."]], [["M. de Villefort had the reputation of being the least curious and the least wearisome man in France.", "빌포르 씨는 프랑스에서 가장 호기심 없고 가장 따분한 사람이라는 평판을 얻고 있었다."], ["He gave a ball every year, at which he appeared for a quarter of an hour only,—that is to say, five-and-forty minutes less than the king is visible at his balls.", "그는 해마다 무도회를 한 번씩 열었으나, 그 자리에 십오 분만 모습을 드러낼 뿐이었다. 다시 말해 왕이 무도회에서 모습을 보이는 시간보다 사십오 분이나 짧았다."], ["He was never seen at the theatres, at concerts, or in any place of public resort.", "그는 극장에서도, 음악회에서도, 사람들이 모이는 그 어떤 자리에서도 결코 눈에 띄지 않았다."], ["Occasionally, but seldom, he played at whist, and then care was taken to select partners worthy of him—sometimes they were ambassadors, sometimes archbishops, or sometimes a prince, or a president, or some dowager duchess.", "어쩌다, 그것도 아주 드물게 휘스트 놀이를 했는데, 그럴 때면 그에 걸맞은 상대를 고르도록 세심히 마음을 썼다. 그 상대는 때로 대사들이었고, 때로 대주교들이었으며, 때로는 어느 공작이거나 의장이거나 미망인이 된 공작 부인이었다."]], [["Such was the man whose carriage had just now stopped before the Count of Monte Cristo’s door.", "방금 몬테크리스토 백작의 저택 문 앞에 마차를 세운 사람이 바로 그런 인물이었다."], ["The valet de chambre announced M. de Villefort at the moment when the count, leaning over a large table, was tracing on a map the route from St. Petersburg to China.", "백작이 커다란 탁자 위로 몸을 숙인 채 지도에 상트페테르부르크에서 중국에 이르는 경로를 그려 넣고 있을 때, 시종이 빌포르 씨의 방문을 알렸다."]], [["The procureur entered with the same grave and measured step he would have employed in entering a court of justice.", "검사는 법정에 들어설 때와 똑같이 근엄하고 절도 있는 걸음걸이로 들어왔다."], ["He was the same man, or rather the development of the same man, whom we have heretofore seen as assistant attorney at Marseilles.", "그는 우리가 앞서 마르세유에서 검사보로 보았던 바로 그 인물이었다. 아니, 그 인물이 발전한 모습이라 해야 옳았다."], ["Nature, according to her way, had made no deviation in the path he had marked out for himself.", "자연은 늘 그렇듯, 그가 스스로 그어 놓은 길에서 한 치도 벗어나게 하지 않았다."], ["From being slender he had now become meagre; once pale, he was now yellow; his deep-set eyes were hollow, and the gold spectacles shielding his eyes seemed to be an integral portion of his face.", "호리호리하던 몸은 이제 비쩍 말랐고, 한때 창백하던 낯빛은 이제 누렇게 떴다. 깊이 박힌 두 눈은 움푹 꺼졌고, 그 눈을 가린 금테 안경은 이제 그의 얼굴에서 떼어 낼 수 없는 일부처럼 보였다."], ["He dressed entirely in black, with the exception of his white tie, and his funeral appearance was only mitigated by the slight line of red ribbon which passed almost imperceptibly through his button-hole, and appeared like a streak of blood traced with a delicate brush.", "그는 흰 넥타이만 빼면 온통 검은 옷차림이었다. 단춧구멍을 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 가느다랗게 가로지른 붉은 리본만이 그 장례식 같은 모습을 조금 누그러뜨릴 뿐이었는데, 그 리본은 마치 섬세한 붓끝으로 그어 놓은 한 줄기 핏자국 같았다."]], [["Although master of himself, Monte Cristo, scrutinized with irrepressible curiosity the magistrate whose salute he returned, and who, distrustful by habit, and especially incredulous as to social prodigies, was much more despised to look upon “the noble stranger,”", "몬테크리스토는 스스로를 다스리는 사람이었으나, 인사를 받으며 답례한 그 사법관을 억누를 수 없는 호기심으로 뜯어보았다. 빌포르는 천성이 의심 많은 데다, 특히 사교계의 진귀한 인물에 대해서는 좀처럼 믿으려 들지 않았다."], ["as Monte Cristo was already called, as an adventurer in search of new fields, or an escaped criminal, rather than as a prince of the Holy See, or a sultan of the _Thousand and One Nights_.", "그래서 그는 이미 \"고귀한 이방인\"이라 불리던 몬테크리스토를, 교황청의 왕자나 천일야화 속 술탄이라기보다는 새로운 사냥터를 찾아 헤매는 모험가, 아니면 탈옥한 죄인쯤으로 여겨 더욱 깔보는 눈으로 바라보았다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"백작님,\""], ["said Villefort, in the squeaky tone assumed by magistrates in their oratorical periods, and of which they cannot, or will not, divest themselves in society, “sir, the signal service which you yesterday rendered to my wife and son has made it a duty for me to offer you my thanks.", "빌포르가 입을 열었다. 사법관들이 연설조로 말할 때 곧잘 짓는, 사교 자리에서도 떨쳐 내지 못하거나 떨쳐 내려 하지 않는 그 째지는 듯한 어조였다. \"백작님, 어제 제 아내와 아들에게 베풀어 주신 더없이 귀한 도움 때문에, 저로서는 마땅히 감사의 말씀을 드려야 할 의무가 생겼습니다."], ["I have come, therefore, to discharge this duty, and to express to you my overwhelming gratitude.”", "그래서 그 의무를 다하고, 제 벅찬 고마움을 전해 드리고자 이렇게 찾아왔습니다.\""]], [["And as he said this, the “eye severe” of the magistrate had lost nothing of its habitual arrogance.", "이렇게 말하는 동안에도 사법관의 \"준엄한 눈\"은 여느 때의 오만함을 조금도 잃지 않았다."], ["He spoke in a voice of the procureur-general, with the rigid inflexibility of neck and shoulders which caused his flatterers to say (as we have before observed) that he was the living statue of the law.", "그는 검사장의 목소리로, 목과 어깨를 뻣뻣하게 굳힌 채 말했는데, 바로 그 모습 때문에 그의 아첨꾼들이 (앞서 보았듯이) 그를 살아 있는 법의 조각상이라 일컫는 것이었다."]], [["“Monsieur,”", "\"검사님,\" 백작이 싸늘한 기색으로 대답했다."], ["replied the count, with a chilling air, “I am very happy to have been the means of preserving a son to his mother, for they say that the sentiment of maternity is the most holy of all; and the good fortune which occurred to me, monsieur, might have enabled you to dispense with a duty which, in its discharge, confers an undoubtedly great honor; for I am aware that M.", "\"어머니에게 아들을 지켜 드리는 도구가 되었다니 저로서는 더없이 기쁩니다. 모성애야말로 세상에서 가장 거룩한 감정이라고들 하니까요. 그런데 검사님, 제게 찾아온 그 행운 덕분에 검사님께서는 굳이 이런 의무를 치르지 않으셔도 되었을 겁니다."], ["de Villefort is not usually lavish of the favor which he now bestows on me,—a favor which, however estimable, is unequal to the satisfaction which I have in my own consciousness.”", "물론 그 의무를 다하시는 일이 제게 더없이 큰 영광을 안겨 주기는 합니다만 말입니다. 빌포르 씨께서 지금 제게 베풀어 주시는 이런 호의를 평소에는 좀처럼 후하게 내주지 않으신다는 것을 저도 알고 있으니까요. 하지만 그 호의가 아무리 값진 것이라 해도, 제 양심 속에서 느끼는 만족에는 미치지 못합니다.\""]], [["Villefort, astonished at this reply, which he by no means expected, started like a soldier who feels the blow levelled at him over the armor he wears, and a curl of his disdainful lip indicated that from that moment he noted in the tablets of his brain that the Count of Monte Cristo was by no means a highly bred gentleman.", "빌포르는 전혀 예상하지 못한 이 대답에 깜짝 놀라, 갑옷 위로 날아드는 일격을 느낀 병사처럼 움찔했다. 그리고 멸시하듯 입술 한쪽을 비트는 그 모습은, 바로 그 순간부터 그가 머릿속 수첩에 몬테크리스토 백작은 결코 교양 있는 신사가 아니라고 적어 넣었음을 드러내고 있었다."]], [["He glanced around, in order to seize on something on which the conversation might turn, and seemed to fall easily on a topic.", "그는 대화를 이어 갈 만한 무언가를 붙잡으려고 주위를 둘러보았고, 이내 손쉽게 화제를 찾은 듯했다."], ["He saw the map which Monte Cristo had been examining when he entered, and said:", "그는 자기가 들어왔을 때 몬테크리스토가 들여다보고 있던 지도를 보고는 말했다."]], [["“You seem geographically engaged, sir?", "\"지리에 몰두하고 계신 모양이군요, 백작님?"], ["It is a rich study for you, who, as I learn, have seen as many lands as are delineated on this map.”", "듣자 하니 이 지도에 그려진 만큼이나 많은 나라를 두루 보셨다는 백작님께는 풍성한 공부거리이겠습니다.\""]], [["“Yes, sir,”", "\"그렇습니다, 검사님.\""], ["replied the count; “I have sought to make of the human race, taken in the mass, what you practice every day on individuals—a physiological study.", "백작이 대답했다. \"저는 인류 전체를 한 덩어리로 놓고, 검사님께서 날마다 개개인을 두고 하시는 일, 곧 생리학적 연구를 해 보려 했습니다."], ["I have believed it was much easier to descend from the whole to a part than to ascend from a part to the whole.", "부분에서 전체로 올라가기보다 전체에서 부분으로 내려가는 편이 훨씬 쉽다고 믿었지요."], ["It is an algebraic axiom, which makes us proceed from a known to an unknown quantity, and not from an unknown to a known; but sit down, sir, I beg of you.”", "이는 미지수에서 기지수로가 아니라 기지수에서 미지수로 나아가게 하는 대수학의 공리이기도 합니다. 그건 그렇고, 앉으시지요, 검사님. 부탁드립니다.\""]], [["Monte Cristo pointed to a chair, which the procureur was obliged to take the trouble to move forwards himself, while the count merely fell back into his own, on which he had been kneeling when M. Villefort entered.", "몬테크리스토가 의자를 가리켰는데, 검사는 손수 그 의자를 앞으로 끌어와야 하는 수고를 치러야 했다. 반면 백작은 빌포르 씨가 들어왔을 때 무릎을 꿇고 기대 있던 제 의자에 그저 털썩 몸을 묻을 뿐이었다."], ["Thus the count was halfway turned towards his visitor, having his back towards the window, his elbow resting on the geographical chart which furnished the theme of conversation for the moment,—a conversation which assumed, as in the case of the interviews with Danglars and Morcerf, a turn analogous to the persons, if not to the situation.", "그리하여 백작은 창을 등진 채 방문객 쪽으로 반쯤 돌아앉아, 한쪽 팔꿈치를 잠시나마 대화의 주제가 되어 준 지도에 괴고 있었다. 그리고 그 대화는, 당글라르나 모르세르와의 면담 때처럼, 상황까지는 아니더라도 사람에 어울리는 방향으로 흘러갔다."]], [["“Ah, you philosophize,”", "\"아, 철학을 하시는군요.\""], ["replied Villefort, after a moment’s silence, during which, like a wrestler who encounters a powerful opponent, he took breath; “well, sir, really, if, like you, I had nothing else to do, I should seek a more amusing occupation.”", "빌포르가 잠시 침묵한 뒤에 대답했다. 그 침묵 동안 그는 강력한 적수와 맞붙은 씨름꾼처럼 숨을 골랐다. \"그런데 백작님, 정말이지 저도 백작님처럼 달리 할 일이 없다면, 좀 더 재미있는 소일거리를 찾아볼 것 같군요.\""]], [["“Why, in truth, sir,”", "\"실은요, 검사님,\""], ["was Monte Cristo’s reply, “man is but an ugly caterpillar for him who studies him through a solar microscope; but you said, I think, that I had nothing else to do.", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"태양 현미경으로 인간을 들여다보는 사람에게 인간이란 그저 흉측한 애벌레에 지나지 않습니다. 그런데 제게 달리 할 일이 없다고 말씀하신 것 같군요."], ["Now, really, let me ask, sir, have you?—do you believe you have anything to do?", "그렇다면 제가 한번 여쭙겠습니다, 검사님. 검사님께서는 하실 일이 있으십니까? 하실 일이 있다고 정말 믿으십니까?"], ["or to speak in plain terms, do you really think that what you do deserves being called anything?”", "더 노골적으로 말해, 검사님께서 하시는 일이 정말 무어라 불릴 만한 가치가 있다고 생각하십니까?\""]], [["Villefort’s astonishment redoubled at this second thrust so forcibly made by his strange adversary.", "이 기이한 적수가 이토록 힘차게 찔러 넣은 두 번째 일격에 빌포르의 놀라움은 곱절이 되었다."], ["It was a long time since the magistrate had heard a paradox so strong, or rather, to say the truth more exactly, it was the first time he had ever heard of it.", "이 사법관이 이토록 강력한 역설을 들어 본 것은 참으로 오랜만이었다. 아니, 더 정확히 말하면, 그가 그런 말을 들어 본 것은 난생처음이었다."], ["The procureur exerted himself to reply.", "검사는 안간힘을 다해 대꾸했다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"백작님,\""], ["he responded, “you are a stranger, and I believe you say yourself that a portion of your life has been spent in Oriental countries, so you are not aware how human justice, so expeditious in barbarous countries, takes with us a prudent and well-studied course.”", "그가 응수했다. \"백작님은 이방인이시고, 또 본인 입으로 인생의 한 시절을 동방의 여러 나라에서 보내셨다 하셨으니, 야만의 나라에서는 그토록 신속한 인간의 정의가 우리나라에서는 얼마나 신중하고 면밀하게 따져진 절차를 밟는지 모르실 겁니다.\""]], [["“Oh, yes—yes, I do, sir; it is the _pede claudo_ of the ancients.", "\"아, 압니다, 알고말고요, 검사님. 그건 옛사람들이 말하던 '페데 클라우도', 곧 절뚝거리는 걸음이지요. 저는 그 모든 것을 압니다."], ["I know all that, for it is with the justice of all countries especially that I have occupied myself—it is with the criminal procedure of all nations that I have compared natural justice, and I must say, sir, that it is the law of primitive nations, that is, the law of retaliation, that I have most frequently found to be according to the law of God.”", "제가 특히 몰두해 온 것이 바로 모든 나라의 정의였으니까요. 저는 자연의 정의를 모든 민족의 형사 절차와 견주어 왔습니다. 그리고 말씀드리건대, 검사님, 제가 하느님의 법에 가장 자주 부합한다고 본 것은 원시 민족들의 법, 곧 보복의 법이었습니다.\""]], [["“If this law were adopted, sir,”", "\"그 법이 채택된다면, 백작님,\""], ["said the procureur, “it would greatly simplify our legal codes, and in that case the magistrates would not (as you just observed) have much to do.”", "검사가 말했다. \"우리 법전이 무척 간단해질 테고, 그렇게 되면 사법관들은 (방금 백작님이 지적하신 대로) 할 일이 그리 많지 않겠지요.\""]], [["“It may, perhaps, come to this in time,”", "\"어쩌면 시간이 흐르면 그렇게 될지도 모릅니다.\""], ["observed Monte Cristo; “you know that human inventions march from the complex to the simple, and simplicity is always perfection.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"인간의 발명이란 복잡한 데서 단순한 데로 나아가는 법이고, 단순함이란 언제나 완성이니까요.\""]], [["“In the meanwhile,”", "\"그러나 그동안에는,\" 사법관이 말을 이었다."], ["continued the magistrate, “our codes are in full force, with all their contradictory enactments derived from Gallic customs, Roman laws, and Frank usages; the knowledge of all which, you will agree, is not to be acquired without extended labor; it needs tedious study to acquire this knowledge, and, when acquired, a strong power of brain to retain it.”", "\"우리 법전은 갈리아의 관습과 로마의 법, 프랑크족의 관례에서 비롯된 온갖 모순된 조항을 안은 채 그대로 효력을 발휘하고 있습니다. 백작님도 동의하시겠지만, 그 모든 것을 익히려면 오랜 수고가 필요합니다. 이 지식을 얻으려면 지루한 공부가 필요하고, 일단 얻고 나면 그것을 머릿속에 붙들어 둘 강한 기억력이 필요하지요.\""]], [["“I agree with you entirely, sir; but all that even you know with respect to the French code, I know, not only in reference to that code, but as regards the codes of all nations.", "\"전적으로 동의합니다, 검사님. 그러나 검사님께서 프랑스 법전에 관해 아시는 그 모든 것을, 저는 그 법전뿐 아니라 모든 나라의 법전에 관해서도 알고 있습니다. 영국의 법, 터키의 법, 일본의 법, 힌두의 법이 제게는 프랑스 법만큼이나 익숙합니다."], ["The English, Turkish, Japanese, Hindu laws, are as familiar to me as the French laws, and thus I was right, when I said to you, that relatively (you know that everything is relative, sir)—that relatively to what I have done, you have very little to do; but that relatively to all I have learned, you have yet a great deal to learn.”", "그러니 제가 검사님께 상대적으로 (검사님도 아시다시피, 만물은 상대적이지요) 말씀드린 것이 옳았던 셈입니다. 즉 제가 해 온 일에 견주면 검사님께서 하실 일은 아주 적습니다. 그러나 제가 배운 모든 것에 견주면, 검사님께서는 아직 배우셔야 할 것이 무척 많습니다.\""]], [["“But with what motive have you learned all this?”", "\"그런데 무슨 동기로 그 모든 것을 배우셨습니까?\""], ["inquired Villefort, in astonishment.", "빌포르가 놀라워하며 물었다."]], [["Monte Cristo smiled.", "몬테크리스토가 미소 지었다."]], [["“Really, sir,”", "\"정말이지, 검사님,\""], ["he observed, “I see that in spite of the reputation which you have acquired as a superior man, you look at everything from the material and vulgar view of society, beginning with man, and ending with man—that is to say, in the most restricted, most narrow view which it is possible for human understanding to embrace.”", "그가 말했다. \"남달리 뛰어난 분이라는 평판을 얻으셨는데도, 검사님께서는 모든 것을 사회의 물질적이고 속된 관점에서, 곧 인간에서 시작해 인간에서 끝나는 관점에서 바라보시는군요. 다시 말해 인간의 지성이 품을 수 있는 가장 제한되고 가장 좁은 시야로 말입니다.\""]], [["“Pray, sir, explain yourself,”", "\"제발, 백작님, 좀 더 풀어서 설명해 주십시오.\""], ["said Villefort, more and more astonished, “I really do—not—understand you—perfectly.”", "빌포르가 점점 더 놀라워하며 말했다. \"저는 정말 백작님 말씀을 온전히 이해하지 못하겠습니다.\""]], [["“I say, sir, that with the eyes fixed on the social organization of nations, you see only the springs of the machine, and lose sight of the sublime workman who makes them act; I say that you do not recognize before you and around you any but those office-holders whose commissions have been signed by a minister or king; and that the men whom God has put above those office-holders, ministers, and kings, by giving them a mission to follow out, instead of a post to fill—I say that they escape your narrow, limited field of observation.", "\"제 말은 이렇습니다, 검사님. 검사님께서는 여러 나라의 사회 조직에 눈을 고정한 채, 기계의 태엽만 볼 뿐 그것을 움직이게 하는 숭고한 장인은 보지 못하신다는 겁니다. 또 검사님 앞과 주위에서, 장관이나 왕이 임명장에 서명해 준 관리들 말고는 아무도 알아보지 못하신다는 겁니다."], ["It is thus that human weakness fails, from its debilitated and imperfect organs.", "하느님께서 그 관리들과 장관들, 왕들 위에 두신 사람들, 곧 채워야 할 자리가 아니라 완수해야 할 사명을 부여받은 사람들은 검사님의 좁고 한정된 관찰의 영역에서 빠져나가 버립니다. 인간의 나약함이 이렇게 실패하는 것은, 그 기관이 쇠약하고 불완전하기 때문이지요. 토비트는 자신에게 빛을 되찾아 준 천사를 평범한 젊은이로 알았습니다."], ["Tobias took the angel who restored him to light for an ordinary young man.", "여러 민족은 자기들을 멸망시킬 운명을 타고난 아틸라를 여느 정복자와 다름없는 정복자로 알았지요."], ["The nations took Attila, who was doomed to destroy them, for a conqueror similar to other conquerors, and it was necessary for both to reveal their missions, that they might be known and acknowledged; one was compelled to say, ‘I am the angel of the Lord’; and the other, ‘I am the hammer of God,’ in order that the divine essence in both might be revealed.”", "그래서 둘 다 자기 사명을 밝혀야만, 비로소 알려지고 인정받을 수 있었습니다. 한쪽은 '나는 주님의 천사다'라고 말해야 했고, 다른 쪽은 '나는 하느님의 망치다'라고 말해야 했습니다. 그래야 그 둘 안에 깃든 신성한 본질이 드러날 수 있었던 것입니다.\""]], [["“Then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["said Villefort, more and more amazed, and really supposing he was speaking to a mystic or a madman, “you consider yourself as one of those extraordinary beings whom you have mentioned?”", "빌포르가 점점 더 어리둥절해하며, 자기가 신비주의자나 미치광이와 이야기를 나누고 있다고 정말로 여기며 말했다. \"백작님께서는 스스로를 방금 말씀하신 그 비범한 존재들 가운데 하나로 여기시는 겁니까?\""]], [["“And why not?”", "\"못 될 것도 없지요?\""], ["said Monte Cristo coldly.", "몬테크리스토가 싸늘하게 말했다."]], [["“Your pardon, sir,”", "\"용서하십시오, 백작님.\""], ["replied Villefort, quite astounded, “but you will excuse me if, when I presented myself to you, I was unaware that I should meet with a person whose knowledge and understanding so far surpass the usual knowledge and understanding of men.", "빌포르가 완전히 어안이 벙벙해져서 대답했다. \"백작님을 찾아뵈었을 때, 보통 사람들의 지식과 이해를 이토록 멀리 뛰어넘는 지식과 이해를 갖춘 분을 만나리라고는 미처 몰랐던 점을 너그러이 봐주십시오."], ["It is not usual with us corrupted wretches of civilization to find gentlemen like yourself, possessors, as you are, of immense fortune—at least, so it is said—and I beg you to observe that I do not inquire, I merely repeat;—it is not usual, I say, for such privileged and wealthy beings to waste their time in speculations on the state of society, in philosophical reveries, intended at best to console those whom fate has disinherited from the goods of this world.”", "우리처럼 문명에 찌든 가련한 자들 사이에서는, 백작님같이 막대한 재산을 지니신 (적어도 그렇다고들 합니다만) 신사를 만나는 일이 흔치 않습니다. 그리고 부디 알아주십시오, 저는 캐묻는 것이 아니라 그저 들은 말을 옮길 뿐입니다. 말씀드리거니와, 그런 특권을 누리는 부유한 분들이 사회의 상태를 두고 사색에 잠기거나, 기껏해야 운명에게 이 세상의 재물을 빼앗긴 자들이나 위로할 만한 철학적 공상에 시간을 허비하는 일은 흔치 않습니다.\""]], [["“Really, sir,”", "\"정말이지, 검사님,\""], ["retorted the count, “have you attained the eminent situation in which you are, without having admitted, or even without having met with exceptions?", "백작이 응수했다. \"검사님께서는 지금의 그 높은 지위에 오르시면서, 예외라는 것을 한 번도 인정하지 않으시거나, 단 한 번도 마주치지 않으셨단 말입니까?"], ["and do you never use your eyes, which must have acquired so much _finesse_ and certainty, to divine, at a glance, the kind of man by whom you are confronted?", "그토록 섬세하고 확실해졌을 그 두 눈을, 검사님은 한 번도 쓰지 않으십니까? 마주한 상대가 어떤 부류의 인간인지 한눈에 꿰뚫어 보는 데 말입니다."], ["Should not a magistrate be not merely the best administrator of the law, but the most crafty expounder of the chicanery of his profession, a steel probe to search hearts, a touchstone to try the gold which in each soul is mingled with more or less of alloy?”", "사법관이라면 마땅히 법을 가장 잘 집행하는 사람일 뿐 아니라, 그 직업의 술수를 가장 교묘하게 풀어내는 사람이어야 하지 않겠습니까? 마음을 더듬는 강철 탐침, 영혼마다 많든 적든 불순물이 섞여 있는 그 금을 시험하는 시금석이어야 하지 않겠습니까?\""]], [["“Sir,”", "\"백작님,\""], ["said Villefort, “upon my word, you overcome me.", "빌포르가 말했다. \"맹세코, 백작님께 손을 들었습니다."], ["I really never heard a person speak as you do.”", "저는 정말로 백작님처럼 말하는 사람을 한 번도 만난 적이 없습니다.\""]], [["“Because you remain eternally encircled in a round of general conditions, and have never dared to raise your wings into those upper spheres which God has peopled with invisible or exceptional beings.”", "\"그건 검사님께서 늘 평범한 조건들의 테두리 안에 갇혀 계실 뿐, 하느님께서 보이지 않거나 예외적인 존재들로 채워 놓으신 저 높은 영역으로 날개를 펼칠 엄두를 한 번도 내 보지 않으셨기 때문이지요.\""]], [["“And you allow then, sir, that spheres exist, and that these marked and invisible beings mingle amongst us?”", "\"그렇다면 백작님, 그런 영역이 존재하고, 그 표지를 지닌 보이지 않는 존재들이 우리 사이에 섞여 있다고 인정하시는 겁니까?\""]], [["“Why should they not?", "\"못 될 것도 없지요?"], ["Can you see the air you breathe, and yet without which you could not for a moment exist?”", "검사님은 지금 숨 쉬는 공기가 보이십니까? 그 공기가 없으면 한순간도 살 수 없는데도 말입니다.\""]], [["“Then we do not see those beings to whom you allude?”", "\"그럼 우리는 백작님께서 말씀하시는 그 존재들을 보지 못하는 것이군요?\""]], [["“Yes, we do; you see them whenever God pleases to allow them to assume a material form.", "\"아니요, 우리는 그들을 봅니다. 하느님께서 그들에게 물질의 형태를 입도록 허락하실 때면 언제든 검사님은 그들을 보게 됩니다."], ["You touch them, come in contact with them, speak to them, and they reply to you.”", "검사님은 그들을 만지고, 그들과 부딪치고, 그들에게 말을 걸고, 그러면 그들도 검사님께 대답하지요.\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["said Villefort, smiling, “I confess I should like to be warned when one of these beings is in contact with me.”", "빌포르가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"솔직히 그런 존재 가운데 하나가 제 곁에 닿아 있을 때라면 미리 귀띔이라도 받고 싶군요.\""]], [["“You have been served as you desire, monsieur, for you were warned just now, and I now again warn you.”", "\"바라시는 대로 이미 받으셨습니다, 검사님. 방금 귀띔을 받으셨고, 지금 제가 다시 한번 귀띔을 드리니까요.\""]], [["“Then you yourself are one of these marked beings?”", "\"그렇다면 백작님 자신이 그 표지를 지닌 존재들 가운데 하나라는 말씀입니까?\""]], [["“Yes, monsieur, I believe so; for until now, no man has found himself in a position similar to mine.", "\"그렇습니다, 검사님. 저는 그렇게 믿습니다. 지금까지 그 누구도 저와 같은 처지에 놓인 적이 없으니까요."], ["The dominions of kings are limited either by mountains or rivers, or a change of manners, or an alteration of language.", "왕들의 영토는 산이나 강으로, 또는 풍속의 변화나 언어의 차이로 경계가 그어집니다. 그러나 제 왕국의 경계는 오직 이 세계뿐입니다."], ["My kingdom is bounded only by the world, for I am not an Italian, or a Frenchman, or a Hindu, or an American, or a Spaniard—I am a cosmopolite.", "저는 이탈리아인도, 프랑스인도, 힌두인도, 미국인도, 에스파냐인도 아니기 때문입니다. 저는 세계인입니다. 그 어느 나라도 제가 태어나는 것을 보았다고 말할 수 없습니다."], ["No country can say it saw my birth. God alone knows what country will see me die.", "제가 어느 나라에서 죽을지는 오직 하느님만이 아십니다."], ["I adopt all customs, speak all languages.", "저는 모든 관습을 받아들이고, 모든 언어를 구사합니다."], ["You believe me to be a Frenchman, for I speak French with the same facility and purity as yourself.", "검사님은 제가 프랑스어를 검사님만큼이나 막힘없이 또 정확하게 구사하니 저를 프랑스인이라 믿으십니다."], ["Well, Ali, my Nubian, believes me to be an Arab; Bertuccio, my steward, takes me for a Roman; Haydée, my slave, thinks me a Greek.", "그런데 제 누비아인 알리는 저를 아랍인으로 믿고, 제 집사 베르투치오는 저를 로마인으로 여기며, 제 노예 하이데는 저를 그리스인이라 생각합니다."], ["You may, therefore, comprehend, that being of no country, asking no protection from any government, acknowledging no man as my brother, not one of the scruples that arrest the powerful, or the obstacles which paralyze the weak, paralyzes or arrests me.", "그러니 검사님도 헤아리실 겁니다. 어느 나라에도 속하지 않고, 어느 정부에도 보호를 청하지 않으며, 그 누구도 형제로 인정하지 않는 저는, 강한 자들을 붙드는 거리낌도, 약한 자들을 마비시키는 장애도, 그 어느 것 하나 저를 마비시키거나 붙들지 못합니다. 제게는 적이 둘뿐입니다."], ["I have only two adversaries—I will not say two conquerors, for with perseverance I subdue even them,—they are time and distance.", "정복자라고는 하지 않겠습니다. 끈기를 가지면 그 둘마저 굴복시키니까요. 그 둘이란 바로 시간과 거리입니다."], ["There is a third, and the most terrible—that is my condition as a mortal being.", "그리고 세 번째이자 가장 무서운 적이 있으니, 그것은 죽음을 면치 못하는 인간이라는 제 처지입니다."], ["This alone can stop me in my onward career, before I have attained the goal at which I aim, for all the rest I have reduced to mathematical terms.", "제가 겨누는 목표에 이르기도 전에 저를 가로막을 수 있는 것은 오직 이것뿐입니다. 나머지는 모두 수학의 항으로 환원해 놓았으니까요."], ["What men call the chances of fate—namely, ruin, change, circumstances—I have fully anticipated, and if any of these should overtake me, yet it will not overwhelm me.", "사람들이 운명의 우연이라 부르는 것, 곧 파멸이며 변화며 정황이며 하는 것들을 저는 모조리 예견해 두었습니다. 설령 그중 어느 하나가 저를 덮친다 해도, 저를 짓누르지는 못할 겁니다."], ["Unless I die, I shall always be what I am, and therefore it is that I utter the things you have never heard, even from the mouths of kings—for kings have need, and other persons have fear of you.", "죽지 않는 한, 저는 언제나 지금의 저일 것입니다. 그러기에 저는 검사님께서 한 번도 들어 보지 못한 것들을, 왕들의 입에서조차 들어 보지 못한 것들을 말씀드리는 겁니다."], ["For who is there who does not say to himself, in a society as incongruously organized as ours, ‘Perhaps some day I shall have to do with the king’s attorney’?”", "왕들은 검사님을 필요로 하고, 그 밖의 사람들은 검사님을 두려워하니까요. 우리처럼 어처구니없게 짜인 사회에서 '언젠가 나도 검사와 얽히게 될지 모른다'고 속으로 되뇌지 않는 자가 어디 있겠습니까?\""]], [["“But can you not say that, sir? The moment you become an inhabitant of France, you are naturally subjected to the French law.”", "\"그러나 백작님께서도 그렇게 말씀하실 수 있지 않습니까? 프랑스의 주민이 되시는 순간, 백작님께서도 당연히 프랑스 법의 지배를 받게 되시니까요.\""]], [["“I know it sir,”", "\"압니다, 검사님.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo; “but when I visit a country I begin to study, by all the means which are available, the men from whom I may have anything to hope or to fear, till I know them as well as, perhaps better than, they know themselves.", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"그러나 저는 어느 나라를 방문하면, 동원할 수 있는 모든 수단을 써서, 제가 무언가를 기대하거나 두려워할 만한 사람들을 연구하기 시작합니다. 그들이 스스로를 아는 것만큼, 어쩌면 그 이상으로 그들을 알게 될 때까지 말입니다."], ["It follows from this, that the king’s attorney, be he who he may, with whom I should have to deal, would assuredly be more embarrassed than I should.”", "그러니 제가 상대해야 할 검사가, 그가 누구이든 간에, 저보다 훨씬 더 난처해지리라는 것은 자명한 일이지요.\""]], [["“That is to say,”", "\"그 말씀인즉,\""], ["replied Villefort with hesitation, “that human nature being weak, every man, according to your creed, has committed faults.”", "빌포르가 머뭇거리며 대답했다. \"인간의 본성이 나약하니, 백작님의 신조대로라면 사람은 누구나 잘못을 저질렀다는 것이군요.\""]], [["“Faults or crimes,” responded Monte Cristo with a negligent air.", "\"잘못이든 죄든 말입니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 무심한 기색으로 응수했다."]], [["“And that you alone, amongst the men whom you do not recognize as your brothers—for you have said so,”", "\"그리고 형제로 인정하지 않는다고 손수 말씀하신 그 사람들 가운데, 오직 백작님 한 분만이,\""], ["observed Villefort in a tone that faltered somewhat—“you alone are perfect.”", "빌포르가 다소 떨리는 어조로 말했다. \"백작님 한 분만이 완벽하시다는 것이군요.\""]], [["“No, not perfect,”", "\"아니요, 완벽한 것은 아닙니다.\""], ["was the count’s reply; “only impenetrable, that’s all.", "백작이 대답했다. \"그저 속을 알 수 없을 뿐이지요. 그게 다입니다."], ["But let us leave off this strain, sir, if the tone of it is displeasing to you; I am no more disturbed by your justice than are you by my second-sight.”", "그러나 이런 가락이 검사님께 거슬리신다면, 이쯤에서 그만두시지요. 저는 검사님의 정의에 동요하지 않습니다. 검사님께서 제 천리안에 동요하지 않으시는 것처럼 말입니다.\""]], [["“No, no,—by no means,”", "\"아니요, 아닙니다, 결코요.\""], ["said Villefort, who was afraid of seeming to abandon his ground.", "자기 입지를 포기하는 것처럼 보일까 두려워하며 빌포르가 말했다. \"아닙니다. 백작님의 눈부시고 거의 숭고하기까지 한 대화로, 백작님은 저를 평범한 차원 위로 끌어올려 주셨습니다."], ["“No; by your brilliant and almost sublime conversation you have elevated me above the ordinary level; we no longer talk, we rise to dissertation.", "우리는 이제 한낱 이야기를 나누는 것이 아니라 논설의 차원으로 올라선 셈입니다. 그러나 검사님도 아시다시피, 강단에 선 신학자들과 논쟁에 빠진 철학자들은 이따금 잔혹한 진실을 말하지요. 그러니 잠시 우리가 사교적인 방식으로, 아니 철학적인 방식으로 신학을 논하고 있다 칩시다."], ["But you know how the theologians in their collegiate chairs, and philosophers in their controversies, occasionally say cruel truths; let us suppose for the moment that we are theologizing in a social way, or even philosophically, and I will say to you, rude as it may seem, ‘My brother, you sacrifice greatly to pride; you may be above others, but above you there is God.’”", "그러면 제가 무례하게 들릴지언정 백작님께 이렇게 말씀드리겠습니다. '형제여, 그대는 자존심에 너무 많은 것을 바치고 있소. 그대가 남들 위에 있을지는 모르나, 그대 위에는 하느님이 계시오.'\""]], [["30029m\n“Above us all, sir,”", "\"우리 모두의 위에 계시지요, 검사님.\""], ["was Monte Cristo’s response, in a tone and with an emphasis so deep that Villefort involuntarily shuddered.", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. 그 어조와 강조가 어찌나 깊었던지, 빌포르는 자기도 모르게 몸서리를 쳤다."], ["“I have my pride for men—serpents always ready to threaten everyone who would pass without crushing them under foot.", "\"저는 사람들에 대해서는 자존심을 품습니다. 사람들이란 짓밟지 않고 지나가려는 이라면 누구든 위협할 채비가 되어 있는 뱀들이니까요."], ["But I lay aside that pride before God, who has taken me from nothing to make me what I am.”", "그러나 하느님 앞에서는 그 자존심을 내려놓습니다. 하느님께서 저를 무에서 끌어내어 지금의 저로 만들어 주셨으니까요.\""]], [["“Then, count, I admire you,”", "\"그렇다면, 백작님, 저는 백작님을 존경합니다.\""], ["said Villefort, who, for the first time in this strange conversation, used the aristocratic form to the unknown personage, whom, until now, he had only called monsieur.", "빌포르가 말했다. 이 기이한 대화에서 처음으로 그는, 지금까지 그저 '검사님'이라고만 불러 온 이 낯선 인물에게 귀족을 부르는 호칭을 썼다. \"그렇습니다, 그리고 백작님께 말씀드리겠습니다."], ["“Yes, and I say to you, if you are really strong, really superior, really pious, or impenetrable, which you were right in saying amounts to the same thing—then be proud, sir, for that is the characteristic of predominance.", "백작님이 정말로 강하고, 정말로 우월하며, 정말로 경건하거나 속을 알 수 없으시다면, 또 그 둘이 같은 것이라 하신 백작님 말씀이 옳다면, 그렇다면 자긍심을 품으십시오, 백작님. 그것이야말로 우월함의 특징이니까요."], ["Yet you have unquestionably some ambition.”", "그런데 백작님께서는 분명 어떤 야심을 품고 계시겠지요.\""]], [["“I have, sir.”", "\"품고 있습니다, 검사님.\""]], [["“And what may it be?”", "\"그게 무엇입니까?\""]], [["“I too, as happens to every man once in his life, have been taken by Satan into the highest mountain in the earth, and when there he showed me all the kingdoms of the world, and as he said before, so said he to me, ‘Child of earth, what wouldst thou have to make thee adore me?’ I reflected long, for a gnawing ambition had long preyed upon me, and then I replied, ‘Listen,—I have always heard of Providence, and yet I have never seen him, or anything that resembles him, or which can make me believe that he exists.", "\"저 역시 사람이라면 누구나 일생에 한 번은 겪는 일을 겪었습니다. 사탄이 저를 이 땅에서 가장 높은 산으로 데려가더니, 그곳에서 세상의 모든 왕국을 보여 주었지요. 그리고 예전에 그가 말했던 것처럼, 제게도 이렇게 말했습니다."], ["I wish to be Providence myself, for I feel that the most beautiful, noblest, most sublime thing in the world, is to recompense and punish.’ Satan bowed his head, and groaned.", "'땅의 자식이여, 나를 숭배하려면 무엇을 갖고 싶으냐?' 저는 오래 생각했습니다. 갉아먹는 듯한 야심이 오랫동안 저를 괴롭혀 왔으니까요."], ["‘You mistake,’ he said, ‘Providence does exist, only you have never seen him, because the child of God is as invisible as the parent.", "그러고는 이렇게 대답했습니다. '들어라. 나는 늘 섭리에 관한 이야기를 들어 왔으나, 한 번도 그를 본 적이 없다. 그와 닮은 것도, 그가 존재한다고 믿게 할 만한 그 무엇도 본 적이 없다."], ["You have seen nothing that resembles him, because he works by secret springs, and moves by hidden ways.", "나는 나 자신이 섭리가 되고 싶다. 세상에서 가장 아름답고 가장 고귀하며 가장 숭고한 일은 상을 주고 벌을 내리는 것이라고 느끼니까.' 사탄은 고개를 숙이며 신음했습니다."], ["All I can do for you is to make you one of the agents of that Providence.’ The bargain was concluded.", "'네가 잘못 알고 있구나.' 그가 말했습니다. '섭리는 분명 존재한다."], ["I may sacrifice my soul, but what matters it?”", "다만 네가 한 번도 본 적이 없을 뿐이지. 하느님의 자식은 그 아버지만큼이나 보이지 않으니까. 네가 그와 닮은 것을 본 적이 없는 까닭은, 그가 은밀한 태엽으로 일을 하고, 숨겨진 길로 움직이기 때문이다."], ["added Monte Cristo.", "내가 너를 위해 해 줄 수 있는 것이라곤, 너를 그 섭리의 대행자 가운데 하나로 만들어 주는 것뿐이다.' 그렇게 거래가 맺어졌습니다. 어쩌면 제 영혼을 바쳐야 할지도 모르지요."], ["“If the thing were to do again, I would again do it.”", "그러나 그게 무슨 대수겠습니까?\" 몬테크리스토가 덧붙였다. \"다시 그 일을 하게 된다 해도, 저는 또다시 그렇게 할 것입니다.\""]], [["Villefort looked at Monte Cristo with extreme amazement.", "빌포르는 더없이 놀라워하며 몬테크리스토를 바라보았다."]], [["“Count,”", "\"백작님,\""], ["he inquired, “have you any relations?”", "그가 물었다. \"친척이 있으십니까?\""]], [["“No, sir, I am alone in the world.”", "\"없습니다, 검사님. 저는 이 세상에 홀로입니다.\""]], [["“So much the worse.”", "\"그것참 안된 일이군요.\""]], [["“Why?” asked Monte Cristo.", "\"어째서요?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다."]], [["“Because then you might witness a spectacle calculated to break down your pride.", "\"그렇지 않다면, 백작님의 그 자긍심을 무너뜨리고도 남을 광경을 보실 수도 있었을 테니까요."], ["You say you fear nothing but death?”", "백작님은 죽음 말고는 아무것도 두렵지 않다고 하셨지요?\""]], [["“I did not say that I feared it; I only said that death alone could check the execution of my plans.”", "\"두렵다고는 하지 않았습니다. 그저 죽음만이 제 계획의 실행을 가로막을 수 있다고 말했을 뿐입니다.\""]], [["“And old age?”", "\"노년은요?\""]], [["“My end will be achieved before I grow old.”", "\"저는 늙기 전에 제 목적을 이룰 것입니다.\""]], [["“And madness?”", "\"광기는요?\""]], [["“I have been nearly mad; and you know the axiom,—_non bis in idem_.", "\"저는 거의 미칠 뻔한 적이 있습니다. 그리고 검사님도 '논 비스 인 이뎀'이라는 격언을 아시지요."], ["It is an axiom of criminal law, and, consequently, you understand its full application.” 30031m", "같은 일로 두 번 벌하지 않는다는 형법의 격언이니, 검사님께서는 그 뜻을 온전히 헤아리실 겁니다.\""]], [["“Sir,”", "\"백작님,\""], ["continued Villefort, “there is something to fear besides death, old age, and madness.", "빌포르가 말을 이었다. \"죽음과 노년과 광기 말고도 두려워할 것이 있습니다."], ["For instance, there is apoplexy—that lightning-stroke which strikes but does not destroy you, and yet which brings everything to an end.", "이를테면 졸중이 있지요. 그 벼락 같은 일격은 사람을 내리치되 죽이지는 않으면서도, 모든 것을 끝장내 버립니다."], ["You are still yourself as now, and yet you are yourself no longer; you who, like Ariel, verge on the angelic, are but an inert mass, which, like Caliban, verges on the brutal; and this is called in human tongues, as I tell you, neither more nor less than apoplexy.", "백작님은 지금처럼 여전히 백작님 자신이면서도, 더는 백작님 자신이 아니게 됩니다. 아리엘처럼 천사에 가깝던 백작님이, 캘리밴처럼 짐승에 가까운 한낱 무력한 덩어리가 되어 버리는 겁니다. 그리고 인간의 말로 이것을 부르는 이름은, 말씀드렸다시피, 다름 아닌 졸중입니다."], ["Come, if so you will, count, and continue this conversation at my house, any day you may be willing to see an adversary capable of understanding and anxious to refute you, and I will show you my father, M.", "자, 백작님께서 원하신다면, 언제든 저희 집에 오셔서 이 대화를 이어 가시지요. 백작님을 이해할 줄도 알고 또 기꺼이 반박하고 싶어 하는 적수를 만나고 싶으신 날이라면 언제든 말입니다."], ["Noirtier de Villefort, one of the most fiery Jacobins of the French Revolution; that is to say, he had the most remarkable audacity, seconded by a most powerful organization—a man who has not, perhaps, like yourself seen all the kingdoms of the earth, but who has helped to overturn one of the greatest; in fact, a man who believed himself, like you, one of the envoys, not of God, but of a supreme being; not of Providence, but of fate.", "그러면 제 아버지 누아르티에 드 빌포르 씨를 보여 드리겠습니다. 프랑스 혁명 때 가장 격렬했던 자코뱅 당원 가운데 한 분이지요. 다시 말해, 더없이 강인한 체질을 바탕으로 더없이 놀라운 대담함을 지녔던 분입니다."], ["Well, sir, the rupture of a blood-vessel on the lobe of the brain has destroyed all this, not in a day, not in an hour, but in a second. M.", "어쩌면 백작님처럼 이 땅의 모든 왕국을 다 보지는 못하셨겠지만, 그 가운데 가장 위대한 나라 하나를 뒤엎는 데 손을 보탠 분입니다."], ["Noirtier, who, on the previous night, was the old Jacobin, the old senator, the old Carbonaro, laughing at the guillotine, the cannon, and the dagger—M.", "실로 백작님처럼 자신을, 하느님이 아니라 어느 지고한 존재의 사절로, 섭리가 아니라 운명의 사절로 믿었던 분입니다."], ["Noirtier, playing with revolutions—M. Noirtier, for whom France was a vast chess-board, from which pawns, rooks, knights, and queens were to disappear, so that the king was checkmated—M.", "그런데 검사님, 뇌의 한 부분에서 혈관 하나가 터지자 이 모든 것이 무너지고 말았습니다. 하루 만에도, 한 시간 만에도 아닌, 단 일 초 만에 말입니다."], ["Noirtier, the redoubtable, was the next morning _poor M. Noirtier_, the helpless old man, at the tender mercies of the weakest creature in the household, that is, his grandchild, Valentine; a dumb and frozen carcass, in fact, living painlessly on, that time may be given for his frame to decompose without his consciousness of its decay.”", "전날 밤만 해도 단두대와 대포와 비수를 비웃던 늙은 자코뱅, 늙은 원로원 의원, 늙은 카르보나리 당원이던 누아르티에 씨, 혁명을 가지고 놀던 누아르티에 씨, 프랑스를 하나의 거대한 장기판으로 여겨, 그 위에서 졸과 차와 마와 비숍과 퀸을 차례로 사라지게 하여 끝내 왕을 외통수에 몰아넣으려던 누아르티에 씨, 그토록 가공할 누아르티에 씨가, 이튿날 아침에는 '가엾은 누아르티에 씨', 곧 집안에서 가장 약한 존재인 손녀 발랑틴의 자비에 매달리는 무력한 노인이 되어 버렸습니다. 사실상 말 못 하고 얼어붙은 송장이 되어, 자기 몸이 썩어 가는 것조차 의식하지 못한 채, 그 몸이 무너져 갈 시간을 벌기 위해 고통 없이 목숨만 이어 가고 있는 것입니다.\""]], [["“Alas, sir,”", "\"아아, 검사님,\""], ["said Monte Cristo “this spectacle is neither strange to my eye nor my thought.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그 광경은 제 눈에도, 제 생각에도 낯선 것이 아닙니다."], ["I am something of a physician, and have, like my fellows, sought more than once for the soul in living and in dead matter; yet, like Providence, it has remained invisible to my eyes, although present to my heart.", "저도 의술을 조금 아는 사람이라, 제 동료들처럼 살아 있는 물질에서도, 죽은 물질에서도 영혼을 한두 번 찾아보았습니다. 그러나 섭리와 마찬가지로, 영혼은 제 마음에는 깃들어 있으면서도 제 눈에는 보이지 않은 채로 남았지요."], ["A hundred writers since Socrates, Seneca, St. Augustine, and Gall, have made, in verse and prose, the comparison you have made, and yet I can well understand that a father’s sufferings may effect great changes in the mind of a son.", "소크라테스와 세네카, 성 아우구스티누스, 그리고 갈 이래로 수백 명의 저술가가 운문으로, 또 산문으로 검사님이 방금 하신 그 비유를 해 왔습니다. 그렇지만 아버지의 고통이 아들의 마음에 큰 변화를 일으킬 수 있다는 것을, 저도 충분히 이해합니다."], ["I will call on you, sir, since you bid me contemplate, for the advantage of my pride, this terrible spectacle, which must have been so great a source of sorrow to your family.”", "검사님, 찾아뵙겠습니다. 검사님께서 제 자긍심을 위해 그 끔찍한 광경을 한번 보라고 권하시니까요. 그것은 검사님 가족에게 틀림없이 크나큰 슬픔의 원천이었을 겁니다.\""]], [["“It would have been so unquestionably, had not God given me so large a compensation.", "\"하느님께서 제게 그토록 큰 보상을 내려 주시지 않았더라면, 의심할 바 없이 그러했겠지요."], ["In contrast with the old man, who is dragging his way to the tomb, are two children just entering into life—Valentine, the daughter by my first wife—Mademoiselle Renée de Saint-Méran—and Edward, the boy whose life you have this day saved.”", "무덤을 향해 발을 끌며 나아가는 그 노인과는 대조적으로, 이제 막 인생에 들어선 두 아이가 있으니까요. 제 첫 아내, 르네 드 생메랑 양에게서 얻은 딸 발랑틴과, 오늘 백작님께서 목숨을 구해 주신 사내아이 에두아르 말입니다.\""]], [["“And what is your deduction from this compensation, sir?”", "\"그 보상에서 검사님은 어떤 결론을 끌어내십니까, 검사님?\""], ["inquired Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 물었다."]], [["“My deduction is,”", "\"제가 끌어내는 결론은 이렇습니다.\""], ["replied Villefort, “that my father, led away by his passions, has committed some fault unknown to human justice, but marked by the justice of God.", "빌포르가 대답했다. \"제 아버지는 정념에 휩쓸려, 인간의 정의는 알지 못하나 하느님의 정의가 똑똑히 새겨 둔 어떤 잘못을 저지르셨다는 겁니다."], ["That God, desirous in his mercy to punish but one person, has visited this justice on him alone.”", "그 하느님께서 자비로이 단 한 사람만을 벌하고자 하시어, 이 정의를 아버지 한 분께만 내리셨다는 것이지요.\""]], [["Monte Cristo with a smile on his lips, uttered in the depths of his soul a groan which would have made Villefort fly had he but heard it.", "몬테크리스토는 입가에 미소를 띤 채, 빌포르가 들었더라면 혼비백산하여 달아났을 신음을 영혼 깊은 곳에서 토해 냈다."]], [["“Adieu, sir,”", "\"그럼 이만 가 보겠습니다, 백작님.\""], ["said the magistrate, who had risen from his seat; “I leave you, bearing a remembrance of you—a remembrance of esteem, which I hope will not be disagreeable to you when you know me better; for I am not a man to bore my friends, as you will learn.", "자리에서 일어선 사법관이 말했다. \"백작님에 대한 기억을, 존경의 기억을 안고 떠나겠습니다. 저를 더 잘 알게 되시면, 그 기억이 백작님께 불쾌하지 않으시기를 바랍니다."], ["Besides, you have made an eternal friend of Madame de Villefort.”", "저는 친구를 따분하게 만드는 사람이 아니니까요. 차차 아시게 될 겁니다. 게다가 백작님께서는 빌포르 부인을 영원한 벗으로 얻으셨습니다.\""]], [["The count bowed, and contented himself with seeing Villefort to the door of his cabinet, the procureur being escorted to his carriage by two footmen, who, on a signal from their master, followed him with every mark of attention.", "백작은 가볍게 고개를 숙였고, 빌포르를 서재 문까지만 배웅하는 데 그쳤다. 검사는 두 하인의 호위를 받으며 마차로 향했는데, 하인들은 주인의 신호 한 번에 더없이 깍듯한 태도로 그를 뒤따랐다."], ["When he had gone, Monte Cristo breathed a profound sigh, and said:", "빌포르가 떠나자 몬테크리스토는 깊은 한숨을 내쉬고는 말했다."]], [["“Enough of this poison, let me now seek the antidote.”", "\"이 독은 이쯤 마셨으니, 이제 해독제를 찾아봐야겠군.\""]], [["Then sounding his bell, he said to Ali, who entered:", "그러고는 종을 울려, 들어온 알리에게 말했다."]], [["“I am going to madame’s chamber—have the carriage ready at one o’clock.”", "\"부인의 방으로 갈 것이다. 한 시에 마차를 대기시켜 두어라.\""]], [["# Chapter 49. Haydée", "# Chapter 49. Haydée"]], [["It will be recollected that the new, or rather old, acquaintances of the Count of Monte Cristo, residing in the Rue Meslay, were no other than Maximilian, Julie, and Emmanuel.", "몬테크리스토 백작의 새로운, 아니 차라리 오래된 지인이라 해야 할 사람들, 메슬레 거리에 사는 그 사람들이 다름 아닌 막시밀리앙과 쥘리, 그리고 에마뉘엘이라는 것을 독자들은 기억할 것이다."]], [["The very anticipations of delight to be enjoyed in his forthcoming visits—the bright, pure gleam of heavenly happiness it diffused over the almost deadly warfare in which he had voluntarily engaged, illumined his whole countenance with a look of ineffable joy and calmness, as, immediately after Villefort’s departure, his thoughts flew back to the cheering prospect before him, of tasting, at least, a brief respite from the fierce and stormy passions of his mind.", "곧 다가올 방문에서 누릴 기쁨에 대한 예감, 스스로 뛰어든 그 거의 목숨을 건 싸움 위로 천상의 행복이 던지는 밝고 순수한 광채가, 빌포르가 떠난 직후 그의 마음을 비추었다. 적어도 사납고 폭풍 같은 마음의 격정에서 잠시나마 벗어나 쉴 수 있으리라는 위안의 전망으로 그의 생각이 되돌아가자, 그의 얼굴 전체가 형언할 수 없는 기쁨과 평온의 빛으로 환해졌다."], ["Even Ali, who had hastened to obey the Count’s summons, went forth from his master’s presence in charmed amazement at the unusual animation and pleasure depicted on features ordinarily so stern and cold; while, as though dreading to put to flight the agreeable ideas hovering over his patron’s meditations, whatever they were, the faithful Nubian walked on tiptoe towards the door, holding his breath, lest its faintest sound should dissipate his master’s happy reverie.", "백작의 부름에 서둘러 달려왔던 알리조차도, 평소 그토록 엄하고 차가운 얼굴에 드물게 떠오른 생기와 즐거움에 매혹된 듯 놀라며 주인 앞을 물러났다. 그리고 충직한 누비아인은 그것이 무엇이든 간에 주인의 사색 위를 맴도는 유쾌한 상념을 쫓아낼까 두려워하는 듯, 그 행복한 몽상을 흩어 버릴 미세한 소리조차 내지 않으려 숨을 죽인 채 발끝으로 문을 향해 걸어갔다."]], [["It was noon, and Monte Cristo had set apart one hour to be passed in the apartments of Haydée, as though his oppressed spirit could not all at once admit the feeling of pure and unmixed joy, but required a gradual succession of calm and gentle emotions to prepare his mind to receive full and perfect happiness, in the same manner as ordinary natures demand to be inured by degrees to the reception of strong or violent sensations.", "정오였다. 몬테크리스토는 한 시간을 따로 떼어 두어 하이데의 거처에서 보내기로 했다. 마치 짓눌린 그의 영혼이 순수하고 섞임 없는 기쁨의 감정을 단번에 받아들이지는 못하고, 완전하고 온전한 행복을 받아들일 마음의 준비를 위해 잔잔하고 부드러운 감정의 점진적 흐름을 필요로 하는 것 같았다. 평범한 본성을 지닌 이들이 강하거나 격렬한 감각을 받아들이려면 차츰차츰 길들여져야 하듯이 말이다."]], [["The young Greek, as we have already said, occupied apartments wholly unconnected with those of the count.", "이미 말했듯이 그 젊은 그리스 여인은 백작의 거처와는 완전히 동떨어진 방들을 쓰고 있었다."], ["The rooms had been fitted up in strict accordance with Oriental ideas; the floors were covered with the richest carpets Turkey could produce; the walls hung with brocaded silk of the most magnificent designs and texture; while around each chamber luxurious divans were placed, with piles of soft and yielding cushions, that needed only to be arranged at the pleasure or convenience of such as sought repose.", "방들은 동방의 취향에 철저히 맞추어 꾸며져 있었다. 바닥에는 터키가 만들어 낼 수 있는 가장 값진 양탄자가 깔려 있었고, 벽에는 가장 화려한 무늬와 질감의 비단 비단 직물이 드리워져 있었다. 방마다 둘레에는 호화로운 긴 의자가 놓이고 부드럽게 푹 꺼지는 방석이 높이 쌓여 있어, 쉬려는 이의 기분이나 편의에 맞추어 정돈하기만 하면 되었다."]], [["Haydée had three French maids, and one who was a Greek.", "하이데에게는 프랑스인 하녀 셋과 그리스인 하녀 하나가 있었다."], ["The first three remained constantly in a small waiting-room, ready to obey the summons of a small golden bell, or to receive the orders of the Romaic slave, who knew just enough French to be able to transmit her mistress’s wishes to the three other waiting-women; the latter had received most peremptory instructions from Monte Cristo to treat Haydée with all the deference they would observe to a queen.", "프랑스인 하녀 셋은 늘 작은 대기실에 머물면서, 작은 금종이 울리거나 로마이크어를 쓰는 노예가 명을 전하면 곧장 따를 채비를 하고 있었다. 그 그리스인 노예는 자기 안주인의 뜻을 다른 세 시녀에게 전달할 만큼의 프랑스어는 알고 있었다. 그 세 시녀는 하이데를 여왕에게 보일 법한 예우를 다해 모시라는 몬테크리스토의 지극히 엄한 지시를 받은 터였다."]], [["The young girl herself generally passed her time in the chamber at the farther end of her apartments.", "그 젊은 처녀 자신은 대개 거처의 맨 안쪽 방에서 시간을 보냈다."], ["This was a sort of boudoir, circular, and lighted only from the roof, which consisted of rose-colored glass.", "그곳은 일종의 규방으로, 원형이었고 오로지 천장으로만 빛이 들었는데, 천장은 장밋빛 유리로 되어 있었다."], ["Haydée was reclining upon soft downy cushions, covered with blue satin spotted with silver; her head, supported by one of her exquisitely moulded arms, rested on the divan immediately behind her, while the other was employed in adjusting to her lips the coral tube of a rich narghile, through whose flexible pipe she drew the smoke fragrant by its passage through perfumed water.", "하이데는 은빛 점이 박힌 푸른 새틴으로 덮인 부드러운 솜털 방석에 기대 누워 있었다. 더없이 곱게 빚어진 한쪽 팔로 머리를 받쳐 바로 뒤의 긴 의자에 기댄 채, 다른 손으로는 값진 물담뱃대의 산호 물부리를 입술에 가져다 대고 있었다. 향수를 푼 물을 지나며 향기를 머금은 연기가 그 잘 휘는 관을 통해 빨려 들었다."], ["Her attitude, though perfectly natural for an Eastern woman would, in a European, have been deemed too full of coquettish straining after effect.", "동방의 여인에게는 더없이 자연스러운 그 자세도, 유럽인이었다면 효과를 노린 지나친 교태로 여겨졌을 것이다."]], [["Her dress, which was that of the women of Epirus, consisted of a pair of white satin trousers, embroidered with pink roses, displaying feet so exquisitely formed and so delicately fair, that they might well have been taken for Parian marble, had not the eye been undeceived by their movements as they constantly shifted in and out of a pair of little slippers with upturned toes, beautifully ornamented with gold and pearls.", "에피루스 여인들의 차림인 그녀의 옷은, 분홍 장미가 수놓인 흰 새틴 바지로 이루어져 있었다. 그 사이로 드러난 발은 어찌나 곱게 생기고 어찌나 희고 섬세한지, 끝이 위로 들린 작은 슬리퍼를 들락거리며 끊임없이 움직이는 모습이 눈을 일깨워 주지 않았더라면 파로스 대리석으로 착각했을 만했다. 슬리퍼는 금과 진주로 아름답게 장식되어 있었다."], ["She wore a blue and white-striped vest, with long open sleeves, trimmed with silver loops and buttons of pearls, and a sort of bodice, which, closing only from the centre to the waist, exhibited the whole of the ivory throat and upper part of the bosom; it was fastened with three magnificent diamond clasps.", "그녀는 파란색과 흰색 줄무늬의 조끼를 입었는데, 길게 트인 소매에는 은빛 고리와 진주 단추가 달려 있었다. 그리고 일종의 보디스를 걸쳤는데, 가운데에서 허리까지만 여며져 있어 상아 같은 목 전체와 가슴 윗부분이 드러났다. 그것은 세 개의 화려한 다이아몬드 걸쇠로 채워져 있었다."], ["The junction of the bodice and drawers was entirely concealed by one of the many-colored scarves, whose brilliant hues and rich silken fringe have rendered them so precious in the eyes of Parisian belles.", "보디스와 바지가 만나는 부분은 갖가지 빛깔의 스카프 하나로 완전히 가려져 있었는데, 그 찬란한 색조와 풍성한 비단 술 장식은 파리 미인들의 눈에 그토록 귀하게 비치는 것이었다."]], [["Tilted on one side of her head she had a small cap of gold-colored silk, embroidered with pearls; while on the other a purple rose mingled its glowing colors with the luxuriant masses of her hair, of which the blackness was so intense that it was tinged with blue.", "머리 한쪽에 비스듬히, 진주가 수놓인 황금빛 비단의 작은 모자를 얹었고, 다른 한쪽에는 자줏빛 장미가 그 타오르는 빛깔을 풍성한 머릿단과 뒤섞고 있었다. 머리카락은 어찌나 새카만지 푸른빛이 감돌 정도였다."]], [["The extreme beauty of the countenance, that shone forth in loveliness that mocked the vain attempts of dress to augment it, was peculiarly and purely Grecian; there were the large, dark, melting eyes, the finely formed nose, the coral lips, and pearly teeth, that belonged to her race and country.", "옷이 더 보태려는 부질없는 시도를 비웃듯 사랑스럽게 빛나는 그 얼굴의 극치의 아름다움은, 유난히도 순수한 그리스의 것이었다. 그녀의 종족과 나라가 지닌 크고 검고 그윽한 눈, 곱게 빚어진 코, 산호 같은 입술, 진주 같은 이가 거기 있었다."]], [["And, to complete the whole, Haydée was in the very springtide and fulness of youthful charms—she had not yet numbered more than nineteen or twenty summers.", "그리고 모든 것을 완성하듯, 하이데는 젊음의 매력이 한창 피어나는 봄날에 있었다. 그녀는 아직 열아홉이나 스무 번의 여름밖에 헤아리지 못했다."]], [["Monte Cristo summoned the Greek attendant, and bade her inquire whether it would be agreeable to her mistress to receive his visit.", "몬테크리스토는 그리스인 시녀를 불러, 안주인이 자신의 방문을 받아 줄 만한지 여쭈어 보라고 일렀다."], ["Haydée’s only reply was to direct her servant by a sign to withdraw the tapestried curtain that hung before the door of her boudoir, the framework of the opening thus made serving as a sort of border to the graceful tableau presented by the young girl’s picturesque attitude and appearance.", "하이데의 유일한 대답은, 손짓으로 시녀에게 규방 문 앞에 드리운 융단 휘장을 걷으라고 이른 것이었다. 그렇게 열린 문틀이 일종의 테두리가 되어, 젊은 처녀의 그림 같은 자세와 자태가 빚어내는 우아한 정경을 감싸 안았다."]], [["As Monte Cristo approached, she leaned upon the elbow of the arm that held the narghile, and extending to him her other hand, said, with a smile of captivating sweetness, in the sonorous language spoken by the women of Athens and Sparta:", "몬테크리스토가 다가가자 그녀는 물담뱃대를 든 팔의 팔꿈치에 몸을 기대고, 다른 손을 그에게 내밀며, 아테네와 스파르타의 여인들이 쓰는 낭랑한 언어로, 사람을 사로잡는 달콤한 미소를 띤 채 말했다."]], [["“Why demand permission ere you enter?", "\"들어오시기 전에 어찌 허락을 구하세요?"], ["Are you no longer my master, or have I ceased to be your slave?”", "더는 제 주인이 아니신가요, 아니면 제가 더는 당신의 노예가 아닌가요?\""]], [["Monte Cristo returned her smile.", "몬테크리스토도 미소로 답했다."]], [["“Haydée,”", "\"하이데,\""], ["said he, “you well know.”", "그가 말했다. \"너도 잘 알지 않느냐.\""]], [["“Why do you address me so coldly—so distantly?”", "\"어찌 그리 차갑게, 그리 멀게 제게 말씀하세요?\""], ["asked the young Greek.", "젊은 그리스 여인이 물었다."], ["“Have I by any means displeased you?", "\"제가 무슨 일로든 당신을 언짢게 했나요?"], ["Oh, if so, punish me as you will; but do not—do not speak to me in tones and manner so formal and constrained.”", "오, 그렇다면 마음대로 벌하셔요. 하지만 부디, 부디 그렇게 격식 차리고 거북한 말투와 태도로 제게 말하지는 마셔요.\""]], [["“Haydée,”", "\"하이데,\""], ["replied the count, “you know that you are now in France, and are free.”", "백작이 대답했다. \"너는 지금 프랑스에 있고, 자유의 몸이라는 걸 알지 않느냐.\""]], [["“Free to do what?” asked the young girl.", "\"무엇을 할 자유 말씀이세요?\" 젊은 처녀가 물었다."]], [["“Free to leave me.”", "\"나를 떠날 자유 말이다.\""]], [["“Leave you?", "\"당신을 떠난다고요?"], ["Why should I leave you?”", "제가 왜 당신을 떠나야 하나요?\""]], [["“That is not for me to say; but we are now about to mix in society—to visit and be visited.”", "\"그건 내가 말할 일이 아니다. 하지만 우리는 이제 사교계에 섞여, 남을 찾아가고 또 찾아오는 손님을 맞이하게 될 것이다.\""]], [["“I don’t wish to see anybody but you.”", "\"저는 당신 말고는 누구도 보고 싶지 않아요.\""]], [["“And should you see one whom you could prefer, I would not be so unjust——”", "\"그러다 네가 더 마음에 두게 될 사람을 만나거든, 나도 그렇게 부당하게 굴지는……\""]], [["“I have never seen anyone I preferred to you, and I have never loved anyone but you and my father.”", "\"저는 당신보다 더 마음에 두는 사람을 본 적이 없고, 당신과 제 아버지 말고는 누구도 사랑한 적이 없어요.\""]], [["“My poor child,”", "\"가엾은 아이로구나,\""], ["replied Monte Cristo, “that is merely because your father and myself are the only men who have ever talked to you.”", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"그건 그저 네 아버지와 나만이 너에게 말을 건넨 유일한 남자들이기 때문이란다.\""]], [["“I don’t want anybody else to talk to me.", "\"저는 다른 누구도 제게 말 거는 걸 원치 않아요."], ["My father said I was his ‘joy’—you style me your ‘love,’—and both of you have called me ‘my child.’”", "제 아버지는 저를 당신의 '기쁨'이라 부르셨고, 당신은 저를 당신의 '사랑'이라 부르시지요. 그리고 두 분 다 저를 '내 아이'라 부르셨어요.\""]], [["“Do you remember your father, Haydée?”", "\"네 아버지를 기억하느냐, 하이데?\""]], [["The young Greek smiled.", "젊은 그리스 여인이 미소 지었다."]], [["“He is here, and here,” said she, touching her eyes and her heart.", "\"아버지는 여기, 그리고 여기에 계세요.\" 그녀가 두 눈과 가슴을 가리키며 말했다."]], [["“And where am I?” inquired Monte Cristo laughingly.", "\"그럼 나는 어디에 있느냐?\" 몬테크리스토가 웃으며 물었다."]], [["“You?” cried she, with tones of thrilling tenderness, “you are everywhere!”", "\"당신이요?\" 그녀가 가슴 떨리는 다정함이 어린 목소리로 외쳤다."], ["Monte Cristo took the delicate hand of the young girl in his, and was about to raise it to his lips, when the simple child of nature hastily withdrew it, and presented her cheek.", "\"당신은 어디에나 계세요!\" 몬테크리스토는 젊은 처녀의 가녀린 손을 자기 손에 쥐고 입술로 가져가려 했으나, 천진한 자연의 아이는 황급히 손을 거두고 뺨을 내밀었다."]], [["“You now understand, Haydée,”", "\"이제 알겠지, 하이데,\""], ["said the count, “that from this moment you are absolutely free; that here you exercise unlimited sway, and are at liberty to lay aside or continue the costume of your country, as it may suit your inclination.", "백작이 말했다. \"이 순간부터 너는 완전히 자유의 몸이며, 여기서는 무엇이든 마음껏 할 수 있고, 네 나라의 옷차림을 벗어 두든 그대로 두든 네 마음대로 할 수 있다."], ["Within this mansion you are absolute mistress of your actions, and may go abroad or remain in your apartments as may seem most agreeable to you.", "이 저택 안에서 너는 네 행동의 절대적인 주인이며, 가장 마음에 드는 대로 바깥출입을 하든 네 방에 머물든 자유다."], ["A carriage waits your orders, and Ali and Myrtho will accompany you whithersoever you desire to go.", "마차는 네 명령을 기다리고 있고, 알리와 미르토가 네가 가고자 하는 어디든 함께 갈 것이다."], ["There is but one favor I would entreat of you.”", "다만 너에게 한 가지 청하고 싶은 것이 있다.\""]], [["“Speak.”", "\"말씀하셔요.\""]], [["“Guard carefully the secret of your birth.", "\"네 출생의 비밀을 부디 잘 지켜다오."], ["Make no allusion to the past; nor upon any occasion be induced to pronounce the names of your illustrious father or ill-fated mother.”", "지난날에 대해서는 한마디도 비치지 말고, 어떤 경우에도 너의 고귀한 아버지나 불운한 어머니의 이름을 입에 올리는 일이 없도록 해라.\""]], [["“I have already told you, my lord, that I shall see no one.”", "\"이미 말씀드렸듯이, 주인님, 저는 아무도 만나지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“It is possible, Haydée, that so perfect a seclusion, though conformable with the habits and customs of the East, may not be practicable in Paris.", "\"하이데, 그렇게 완벽한 칩거는 동방의 습속과 관습에는 맞을지 모르나, 파리에서는 가능하지 않을 수도 있다."], ["Endeavor, then, to accustom yourself to our manner of living in these northern climes as you did to those of Rome, Florence, Milan, and Madrid; it may be useful to you one of these days, whether you remain here or return to the East.”", "그러니 로마와 피렌체, 밀라노, 마드리드의 방식에 익숙해졌듯이, 이 북녘 땅에서의 우리 생활 방식에도 익숙해지도록 애써 보아라. 네가 여기 머물든 동방으로 돌아가든, 언젠가는 그것이 너에게 쓸모가 있을 것이다.\""]], [["The young girl raised her tearful eyes towards Monte Cristo as she said with touching earnestness, “Whether _we_ return to the East, you mean to say, my lord, do you not?”", "젊은 처녀는 눈물 어린 눈을 몬테크리스토에게로 들며, 가슴 저미는 간절함으로 말했다. \"우리가 동방으로 돌아갈 때 말씀이시지요, 주인님, 그렇지 않나요?\""]], [["“My child,”", "\"내 아이야,\""], ["returned Monte Cristo “you know full well that whenever we part, it will be no fault or wish of mine; the tree forsakes not the flower—the flower falls from the tree.”", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"우리가 헤어지게 된다면 그건 결코 내 잘못도 내 바람도 아니라는 것을 너는 잘 알지 않느냐. 나무가 꽃을 버리는 것이 아니라, 꽃이 나무에서 떨어지는 것이란다.\""]], [["“My lord,”", "\"주인님,\""], ["replied Haydée, “I never will leave you, for I am sure I could not exist without you.”", "하이데가 대답했다. \"저는 결코 당신을 떠나지 않을 거예요. 당신 없이는 살아갈 수 없으리라는 걸 잘 아니까요.\""]], [["“My poor girl, in ten years I shall be old, and you will be still young.”", "\"가엾은 아이야, 십 년 뒤면 나는 늙어 있을 테고, 너는 여전히 젊을 것이다.\""]], [["“My father had a long white beard, but I loved him; he was sixty years old, but to me he was handsomer than all the fine youths I saw.”", "\"제 아버지는 길고 흰 수염을 기르셨지만, 저는 아버지를 사랑했어요. 아버지는 예순이셨지만, 제게는 제가 본 그 어떤 잘생긴 젊은이들보다 더 멋지셨어요.\""]], [["“Then tell me, Haydée, do you believe you shall be able to accustom yourself to our present mode of life?”", "\"그렇다면 말해 보아라, 하이데. 우리의 지금 생활 방식에 익숙해질 수 있을 것 같으냐?\""]], [["“Shall I see you?”", "\"당신을 뵐 수 있나요?\""]], [["“Every day.”", "\"날마다.\""]], [["“Then what do you fear, my lord?”", "\"그럼 무엇을 두려워하시나요, 주인님?\""]], [["“You might find it dull.”", "\"네가 지루해할까 봐 그런다.\""]], [["“No, my lord.", "\"아니에요, 주인님."], ["In the morning, I shall rejoice in the prospect of your coming, and in the evening dwell with delight on the happiness I have enjoyed in your presence; then too, when alone, I can call forth mighty pictures of the past, see vast horizons bounded only by the towering mountains of Pindus and Olympus.", "아침이면 당신이 오시리라는 기대로 기뻐할 것이고, 저녁이면 당신 곁에서 누린 행복을 떠올리며 즐거워할 거예요. 게다가 혼자 있을 때면 지난날의 웅대한 그림들을 불러낼 수 있고, 핀두스와 올림푸스의 우뚝 솟은 산봉우리로만 가로막힌 드넓은 지평선을 볼 수 있어요. 오, 믿어 주세요."], ["Oh, believe me, that when three great passions, such as sorrow, love, and gratitude fill the heart, _ennui_ can find no place.”", "슬픔과 사랑과 감사라는 세 가지 큰 격정이 가슴을 가득 채우고 있을 때, 권태가 들어설 자리는 없답니다.\""]], [["“You are a worthy daughter of Epirus, Haydée, and your charming and poetical ideas prove well your descent from that race of goddesses who claim your country as their birthplace.", "\"너는 에피루스의 자랑스러운 딸이로구나, 하이데. 너의 매혹적이고 시적인 생각은, 네 나라를 고향이라 일컫는 그 여신들의 혈통에서 네가 나왔음을 잘 보여 준다."], ["Depend on my care to see that your youth is not blighted, or suffered to pass away in ungenial solitude; and of this be well assured, that if you love me as a father, I love you as a child.”", "너의 젊음이 시들거나 쓸쓸한 고독 속에 흘러가 버리지 않도록 내가 보살피마, 그것은 믿어도 좋다. 그리고 이것만은 분명히 알아 두어라. 네가 나를 아버지처럼 사랑한다면, 나는 너를 자식처럼 사랑한다는 것을.\""]], [["“You are wrong, my lord.", "\"틀리셨어요, 주인님."], ["The love I have for you is very different from the love I had for my father.", "제가 당신께 품은 사랑은 제 아버지께 품었던 사랑과는 아주 달라요."], ["My father died, but I did not die.", "제 아버지는 돌아가셨지만, 저는 죽지 않았어요."], ["If you were to die, I should die too.”", "하지만 당신이 돌아가신다면, 저도 함께 죽을 거예요.\""]], [["The count, with a smile of profound tenderness, extended his hand, and she carried it to her lips.", "백작은 깊은 다정함이 어린 미소를 띠고 손을 내밀었고, 그녀는 그 손을 입술로 가져갔다."]], [["Monte Cristo, thus attuned to the interview he proposed to hold with Morrel and his family, departed, murmuring as he went these lines of Pindar, “Youth is a flower of which love is the fruit; happy is he who, after having watched its silent growth, is permitted to gather and call it his own.”", "이렇게 모렐과 그 가족과 나누려던 만남에 어울리는 마음 상태가 된 몬테크리스토는, 핀다로스의 이런 시구를 읊조리며 걸어 나갔다. \"젊음은 꽃이요 사랑은 그 열매라. 그 고요한 자람을 지켜본 끝에 그것을 거두어 제 것이라 부를 수 있는 자는 복되도다.\""], ["The carriage was prepared according to orders, and stepping lightly into it, the count drove off at his usual rapid pace.", "마차는 명령대로 준비되어 있었고, 백작은 가볍게 올라타 여느 때처럼 빠른 속도로 달려갔다."]], [["# Chapter 50. The Morrel Family", "# Chapter 50. The Morrel Family"]], [["In a very few minutes the count reached No. 7 in the Rue Meslay.", "몇 분도 채 지나지 않아 백작은 멜레가 7번지에 다다랐다."], ["The house was of white stone, and in a small court before it were two small beds full of beautiful flowers.", "흰 돌로 지은 집이었고, 그 앞 작은 안뜰에는 아름다운 꽃이 가득한 작은 화단 두 개가 놓여 있었다."], ["In the concierge that opened the gate the count recognized Cocles; but as he had but one eye, and that eye had become somewhat dim in the course of nine years, Cocles did not recognize the count.", "문을 열어 준 문지기에게서 백작은 코클레스를 알아보았다. 그러나 코클레스는 한쪽 눈만 남은 데다, 그 한 눈마저 구 년 세월에 다소 흐릿해진 탓에 백작을 알아보지 못했다."]], [["The carriages that drove up to the door were compelled to turn, to avoid a fountain that played in a basin of rockwork,—an ornament that had excited the jealousy of the whole quarter, and had gained for the place the appellation of _The Little Versailles_.", "현관 앞까지 들어온 마차들은 바위로 꾸민 수반에서 솟구치는 분수를 피하느라 방향을 틀어야 했다. 이 장식은 동네 전체의 부러움을 사, 이곳에 \"작은 베르사유\"라는 별명을 안겨 준 터였다."], ["It is needless to add that there were gold and silver fish in the basin.", "수반 안에 금붕어와 은붕어가 헤엄치고 있었음은 굳이 덧붙일 필요도 없으리라."], ["The house, with kitchens and cellars below, had above the ground floor, two stories and attics.", "집은 아래에 부엌과 지하 저장고를 두고, 일층 위로 두 개 층과 다락이 더 있었다."], ["The whole of the property, consisting of an immense workshop, two pavilions at the bottom of the garden, and the garden itself, had been purchased by Emmanuel, who had seen at a glance that he could make of it a profitable speculation.", "거대한 작업장과 정원 끝의 별채 두 채, 그리고 정원 자체로 이루어진 이 부지 전체를 에마뉘엘이 사들였는데, 그는 한눈에 이것을 수지맞는 거래로 만들 수 있음을 알아보았다."], ["He had reserved the house and half the garden, and building a wall between the garden and the workshops, had let them upon lease with the pavilions at the bottom of the garden.", "그는 집과 정원의 절반을 자기 몫으로 남기고, 정원과 작업장 사이에 담을 쌓은 뒤 정원 끝 별채와 함께 임대했다."], ["So that for a trifling sum he was as well lodged, and as perfectly shut out from observation, as the inhabitants of the finest mansion in the Faubourg St. Germain.", "그리하여 그는 푼돈으로도, 생제르맹 거리의 가장 훌륭한 저택에 사는 이들 못지않게 안락하고 또 남의 눈에 띄지 않는 거처를 누렸다."]], [["The breakfast-room was finished in oak; the salon in mahogany, and the furnishings were of blue velvet; the bedroom was in citronwood and green damask.", "아침 식당은 떡갈나무로, 응접실은 마호가니로 마감했고, 가구는 푸른 벨벳이었다. 침실은 시트론 나무와 초록빛 다마스크로 꾸며져 있었다."], ["There was a study for Emmanuel, who never studied, and a music-room for Julie, who never played.", "한 번도 공부한 적 없는 에마뉘엘에게는 서재가, 한 번도 연주한 적 없는 쥘리에게는 음악실이 있었다."], ["The whole of the second story was set apart for Maximilian; it was precisely similar to his sister’s apartments, except that for the breakfast-parlor he had a billiard-room, where he received his friends.", "이층 전체는 막시밀리앙의 차지였다. 그의 거처는 누이의 것과 거의 똑같았으나, 아침 식당 대신 친구들을 맞이하는 당구실이 있다는 점만 달랐다."], ["He was superintending the grooming of his horse, and smoking his cigar at the entrance of the garden, when the count’s carriage stopped at the gate.", "백작의 마차가 대문 앞에 멈춰 섰을 때, 그는 정원 입구에서 시가를 피우며 자기 말을 손질하는 모습을 지켜보고 있었다."]], [["Cocles opened the gate, and Baptistin, springing from the box, inquired whether Monsieur and Madame Herbault and Monsieur Maximilian Morrel would see his excellency the Count of Monte Cristo.", "코클레스가 대문을 열자, 마부석에서 뛰어내린 바티스탱이 에르보 부부와 막시밀리앙 모렐 씨가 몬테크리스토 백작 각하를 만나 주실 수 있겠느냐고 물었다."]], [["“The Count of Monte Cristo?”", "\"몬테크리스토 백작이라고?\""], ["cried Morrel, throwing away his cigar and hastening to the carriage; “I should think we would see him.", "모렐이 시가를 던지고 마차 쪽으로 달려오며 외쳤다. \"당연히 뵙고말고요."], ["Ah, a thousand thanks, count, for not having forgotten your promise.”", "아, 정말 고맙습니다, 백작님. 약속을 잊지 않고 와 주셨군요.\""]], [["And the young officer shook the count’s hand so warmly, that Monte Cristo could not be mistaken as to the sincerity of his joy, and he saw that he had been expected with impatience, and was received with pleasure.", "그러고서 젊은 장교는 어찌나 뜨겁게 백작의 손을 잡고 흔들었던지, 몬테크리스토는 그 기쁨이 진심임을 의심할 수 없었다. 자신이 애타게 기다려졌고 또 반갑게 맞아들여지고 있음을 그는 알아차렸다."]], [["“Come, come,”", "\"자, 자,\""], ["said Maximilian, “I will serve as your guide; such a man as you are ought not to be introduced by a servant.", "막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"제가 길잡이가 되어 드리지요. 백작님 같은 분을 하인 손에 맡길 수야 없지요."], ["My sister is in the garden plucking the dead roses; my brother is reading his two papers, _la Presse_ and _les Débats_, within six steps of her; for wherever you see Madame Herbault, you have only to look within a circle of four yards and you will find M.", "누이는 정원에서 시든 장미를 따고 있고, 매형은 그녀에게서 여섯 걸음도 안 되는 곳에서 신문 두 가지, 라 프레스와 레 데바를 읽고 있을 겁니다. 에르보 부인이 보이는 곳이라면, 반경 사 미터 안을 살피기만 하면 에마뉘엘 씨가 있거든요."], ["Emmanuel, and ‘reciprocally,’ as they say at the Polytechnic School.”", "또 그 '역도 성립한다'고, 폴리테크니크 학교에서들 말하듯 말이지요.\""]], [["At the sound of their steps a young woman of twenty to five-and-twenty, dressed in a silk morning gown, and busily engaged in plucking the dead leaves off a noisette rose-tree, raised her head.", "발소리에 스물에서 스물다섯 살쯤 된 젊은 여인이 고개를 들었다. 비단 실내복 차림으로 노아제트 장미나무의 시든 잎을 부지런히 떼어 내던 참이었다."], ["This was Julie, who had become, as the clerk of the house of Thomson & French had predicted, Madame Emmanuel Herbault.", "톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회 직원이 예언한 그대로, 에마뉘엘 에르보 부인이 된 쥘리였다."], ["She uttered a cry of surprise at the sight of a stranger, and Maximilian began to laugh.", "그녀는 낯선 사람을 보고 놀란 소리를 냈고, 막시밀리앙은 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“Don’t disturb yourself, Julie,” said he.", "\"신경 쓰지 마, 쥘리.\" 그가 말했다."], ["“The count has only been two or three days in Paris, but he already knows what a fashionable woman of the Marais is, and if he does not, you will show him.”", "\"백작님은 파리에 오신 지 이삼 일밖에 안 됐지만, 마레의 멋쟁이 부인이 어떤지는 벌써 다 알고 계셔. 혹 모르신다면, 네가 직접 보여 드리면 되고.\""]], [["“Ah, monsieur,”", "\"아, 백작님,\""], ["returned Julie, “it is treason in my brother to bring you thus, but he never has any regard for his poor sister.", "쥘리가 대답했다. \"이렇게 백작님을 모시고 오다니, 제 동생의 횡포랍니다. 이 애는 가엾은 누이를 한 번도 배려한 적이 없어요."], ["Penelon, Penelon!”", "페늘롱, 페늘롱!\""]], [["An old man, who was digging busily at one of the beds, stuck his spade in the earth, and approached, cap in hand, striving to conceal a quid of tobacco he had just thrust into his cheek.", "화단 한 곳을 부지런히 파고 있던 노인이 삽을 흙에 꽂아 두고 다가왔다. 모자를 손에 쥐고서, 방금 뺨 안쪽에 밀어 넣은 씹는담배를 감추려 애쓰고 있었다."], ["A few locks of gray mingled with his hair, which was still thick and matted, while his bronzed features and determined glance well suited an old sailor who had braved the heat of the equator and the storms of the tropics.", "여전히 숱 많고 헝클어진 머리칼에 희끗한 가닥이 섞여 있었고, 햇볕에 그을린 얼굴과 단호한 눈빛은 적도의 열기와 열대의 폭풍을 견뎌 온 늙은 선원에게 잘 어울렸다."]], [["“I think you hailed me, Mademoiselle Julie?” said he.", "\"부르셨습니까, 쥘리 아가씨?\" 그가 말했다."]], [["Penelon had still preserved the habit of calling his master’s daughter “Mademoiselle Julie,” and had never been able to change the name to Madame Herbault.", "페늘롱은 여전히 주인의 딸을 \"쥘리 아가씨\"라고 부르는 버릇을 간직하고 있었고, 그 호칭을 에르보 부인으로 바꾸지 못했다."]], [["“Penelon,”", "\"페늘롱,\""], ["replied Julie, “go and inform M.", "쥘리가 대답했다."], ["Emmanuel of this gentleman’s visit, and Maximilian will conduct him to the salon.”", "\"에마뉘엘 씨께 이분이 오셨다고 알려 드려요. 그리고 막시밀리앙, 백작님을 응접실로 모셔 드려.\""]], [["Then, turning to Monte Cristo,—“I hope you will permit me to leave you for a few minutes,”", "그러고는 몬테크리스토를 돌아보며 말을 이었다."], ["continued she; and without awaiting any reply, disappeared behind a clump of trees, and escaped to the house by a lateral alley. 30041m", "\"잠시 자리를 비우는 것을 양해해 주시기 바랍니다.\" 그녀는 대답도 기다리지 않고 나무 덤불 뒤로 사라져, 옆길로 집 안으로 들어가 버렸다."]], [["“I am sorry to see,” observed Monte Cristo to Morrel, “that I cause no small disturbance in your house.”", "\"제가 댁에 적잖은 소란을 일으킨 모양입니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 모렐에게 말했다."]], [["“Look there,”", "\"저기 보십시오.\""], ["said Maximilian, laughing; “there is her husband changing his jacket for a coat.", "막시밀리앙이 웃으며 말했다. \"매형이 작업복을 정장으로 갈아입고 있군요."], ["I assure you, you are well known in the Rue Meslay.”", "정말이지, 멜레가에서 백작님은 모르는 사람이 없답니다.\""]], [["“Your family appears to be a very happy one,” said the count, as if speaking to himself.", "\"댁의 가족은 무척 행복한 분들 같군요.\" 백작이 혼잣말하듯 말했다."]], [["“Oh, yes, I assure you, count, they want nothing that can render them happy; they are young and cheerful, they are tenderly attached to each other, and with twenty-five thousand francs a year they fancy themselves as rich as Rothschild.”", "\"오, 그럼요, 백작님. 행복에 필요한 것은 아무것도 부족하지 않습니다. 두 사람은 젊고 쾌활하며 서로 깊이 사랑하지요. 연 이만 오천 프랑이면 자기들이 로스차일드만큼이나 부유하다고 여긴답니다.\""]], [["“Five-and-twenty thousand francs is not a large sum, however,”", "\"하지만 이만 오천 프랑은 큰돈이 아닌데요.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다."], ["replied Monte Cristo, with a tone so sweet and gentle, that it went to Maximilian’s heart like the voice of a father; “but they will not be content with that.", "어찌나 부드럽고 다정한 목소리였던지, 막시밀리앙의 가슴에는 마치 아버지의 음성처럼 와닿았다. \"하지만 두 분은 그것으로 만족하지 못할 겁니다."], ["Your brother-in-law is a barrister?", "매형 되시는 분은 변호사입니까?"], ["a doctor?”", "의사입니까?\""]], [["“He was a merchant, monsieur, and had succeeded to the business of my poor father. M.", "\"상인이었습니다, 백작님. 제 가엾은 아버지의 사업을 이어받았지요."], ["Morrel, at his death, left 500,000 francs, which were divided between my sister and myself, for we were his only children.", "모렐 씨는 돌아가시면서 오십만 프랑을 남기셨고, 그것이 누이와 제게 나뉘었습니다. 우리가 유일한 자식이었으니까요."], ["Her husband, who, when he married her, had no other patrimony than his noble probity, his first-rate ability, and his spotless reputation, wished to possess as much as his wife.", "매형은 누이와 결혼할 때 고결한 정직함과 일류의 능력, 흠 없는 평판 말고는 아무 재산도 없었지만, 아내만큼의 재산을 갖고 싶어 했습니다."], ["He labored and toiled until he had amassed 250,000 francs; six years sufficed to achieve this object.", "그는 일하고 또 애써, 마침내 이십오만 프랑을 모았지요. 이 목표를 이루는 데 육 년이면 충분했습니다."], ["Oh, I assure you, sir, it was a touching spectacle to see these young creatures, destined by their talents for higher stations, toiling together, and through their unwillingness to change any of the customs of their paternal house, taking six years to accomplish what less scrupulous people would have effected in two or three.", "오, 정말이지, 백작님, 재능으로 보아 더 높은 자리에 오를 운명이었던 이 젊은 부부가 함께 애쓰는 모습은 가슴 뭉클한 광경이었습니다. 두 사람은 아버지 집안의 관습을 조금도 바꾸려 하지 않았던 탓에, 양심이 무딘 사람들이라면 두세 해면 이뤘을 일을 육 년 걸려 이뤄 냈지요."], ["Marseilles resounded with their well-earned praises.", "마르세유는 두 사람의 정당한 칭송으로 떠들썩했습니다."], ["At last, one day, Emmanuel came to his wife, who had just finished making up the accounts.", "마침내 어느 날, 에마뉘엘이 막 장부 정리를 마친 아내에게 찾아왔습니다."]], [["“‘Julie,’ said he to her, ‘Cocles has just given me the last rouleau of a hundred francs; that completes the 250,000 francs we had fixed as the limits of our gains.", "\"'쥘리,' 그가 그녀에게 말했습니다. '방금 코클레스가 마지막 백 프랑짜리 다발을 내게 가져왔소."], ["Can you content yourself with the small fortune which we shall possess for the future?", "이것으로 우리가 한도로 정해 둔 이십오만 프랑이 다 채워졌소."], ["Listen to me. Our house transacts business to the amount of a million a year, from which we derive an income of 40,000 francs.", "앞으로 우리가 가지게 될 이 소박한 재산에 당신은 만족할 수 있겠소? 내 말을 들어 보오."], ["We can dispose of the business, if we please, in an hour, for I have received a letter from M.", "우리 상회는 연간 백만 프랑어치 거래를 하고, 거기서 사만 프랑의 수입을 얻고 있소."], ["Delaunay, in which he offers to purchase the good-will of the house, to unite with his own, for 300,000 francs.", "원한다면 한 시간 안에 사업을 처분할 수도 있소. 들로네 씨에게서 편지를 받았는데, 우리 상회의 영업권을 자기 것과 합치려고 삼십만 프랑에 사겠다고 했거든."], ["Advise me what I had better do.’", "어떻게 하면 좋을지 조언해 주오.'"]], [["“‘Emmanuel,’ returned my sister, ‘the house of Morrel can only be carried on by a Morrel.", "\"'에마뉘엘,' 누이가 대답했습니다. '모렐 상회는 모렐 사람만이 이어 갈 수 있어요."], ["Is it not worth 300,000 francs to save our father’s name from the chances of evil fortune and failure?’", "아버지의 이름을 불운과 파산의 위험에서 지켜 내는 일이 삼십만 프랑의 값어치가 없겠어요?'"]], [["“‘I thought so,’ replied Emmanuel; ‘but I wished to have your advice.’", "\"'나도 그렇게 생각했소,' 에마뉘엘이 대답했습니다. '하지만 당신 의견을 듣고 싶었소.'"]], [["“‘This is my counsel:—Our accounts are made up and our bills paid; all we have to do is to stop the issue of any more, and close our office.’", "\"'내 생각은 이래요. 우리 장부는 정리됐고 어음도 다 갚았어요. 이제 더 이상 어음을 발행하지 말고, 사무소를 닫기만 하면 돼요.'"]], [["“This was done instantly.", "\"이 일은 즉시 실행되었습니다."], ["It was three o’clock; at a quarter past, a merchant presented himself to insure two ships; it was a clear profit of 15,000 francs.", "세 시였지요. 십오 분 뒤, 한 상인이 배 두 척의 보험을 들러 찾아왔습니다. 만 오천 프랑의 순이익이 걸린 일이었지요."]], [["“‘Monsieur,’ said Emmanuel, ‘have the goodness to address yourself to M.", "\"'선생,' 에마뉘엘이 말했습니다. '들로네 씨를 찾아가 주시지요."], ["Delaunay. We have quitted business.’", "우리는 사업을 그만두었습니다.'"]], [["“‘How long?’ inquired the astonished merchant.", "\"'언제부터요?' 놀란 상인이 물었습니다."]], [["30043m\n“‘A quarter of an hour,’ was the reply.", "\"'십오 분 전부터요.' 이것이 그 대답이었습니다."]], [["“And this is the reason, monsieur,”", "\"그래서, 백작님,\" 막시밀리앙이 말을 이었다."], ["continued Maximilian, “of my sister and brother-in-law having only 25,000 francs a year.”", "\"누이와 매형이 연 이만 오천 프랑밖에 갖지 못한 까닭이 바로 이것입니다.\""]], [["Maximilian had scarcely finished his story, during which the count’s heart had swelled within him, when Emmanuel entered wearing a hat and coat.", "막시밀리앙이 이야기를 막 끝냈을 때, 그 이야기를 듣는 동안 백작의 가슴은 부풀어 올랐는데, 모자를 쓰고 정장을 차려입은 에마뉘엘이 들어왔다."], ["He saluted the count with the air of a man who is aware of the rank of his guest; then, after having led Monte Cristo around the little garden, he returned to the house.", "그는 손님의 지체를 아는 사람다운 태도로 백작에게 인사한 뒤, 작은 정원을 한 바퀴 안내하고는 집 안으로 돌아왔다."]], [["A large vase of Japan porcelain, filled with flowers that loaded the air with their perfume, stood in the salon.", "향기로 공기를 가득 채운 꽃이 꽂힌 커다란 일본 자기 화병이 응접실에 놓여 있었다."], ["Julie, suitably dressed, and her hair arranged (she had accomplished this feat in less than ten minutes), received the count on his entrance.", "단정히 차려입고 머리를 매만진 쥘리가, 이 모든 일을 십 분도 안 되어 해치우고서, 백작이 들어오자 그를 맞이했다."], ["The songs of the birds were heard in an aviary hard by, and the branches of laburnums and rose acacias formed an exquisite framework to the blue velvet curtains.", "바로 옆 새장에서 새들의 노래가 들려왔고, 금사슬나무와 분홍 아카시아 가지가 푸른 벨벳 커튼을 더없이 아름답게 둘러쌌다."], ["Everything in this charming retreat, from the warble of the birds to the smile of the mistress, breathed tranquillity and repose.", "새들의 지저귐부터 안주인의 미소에 이르기까지, 이 매혹적인 안식처의 모든 것이 평온과 휴식을 내뿜고 있었다."]], [["The count had felt the influence of this happiness from the moment he entered the house, and he remained silent and pensive, forgetting that he was expected to renew the conversation, which had ceased after the first salutations had been exchanged.", "백작은 집에 들어선 순간부터 이 행복의 기운을 느꼈다. 그래서 첫인사를 주고받은 뒤 끊긴 대화를 다시 이어 가야 한다는 것도 잊은 채 말없이 생각에 잠겨 있었다."], ["The silence became almost painful when, by a violent effort, tearing himself from his pleasing reverie:", "침묵이 거의 고통스러워질 무렵, 그는 안간힘을 써서 그 흐뭇한 몽상에서 자신을 떼어 냈다."]], [["“Madame,”", "\"부인,\""], ["said he at length, “I pray you to excuse my emotion, which must astonish you who are only accustomed to the happiness I meet here; but contentment is so new a sight to me, that I could never be weary of looking at yourself and your husband.”", "그가 마침내 입을 열었다. \"제 감정을 용서하시기 바랍니다. 여기서 늘 누리는 행복에만 익숙하신 분께는 분명 놀라우시겠지만, 제게는 이런 만족스러운 광경이 너무도 새로워서, 부인과 부군을 바라보는 데 도무지 싫증이 나질 않는군요.\""]], [["“We are very happy, monsieur,”", "\"저희는 무척 행복합니다, 백작님.\""], ["replied Julie; “but we have also known unhappiness, and few have ever undergone more bitter sufferings than ourselves.”", "쥘리가 대답했다. \"하지만 저희도 불행을 겪어 보았답니다. 저희보다 더 쓰라린 고통을 겪은 사람은 드물 거예요.\""]], [["The count’s features displayed an expression of the most intense curiosity.", "백작의 얼굴에 더없이 강렬한 호기심의 빛이 떠올랐다."]], [["“Oh, all this is a family history, as Château-Renaud told you the other day,”", "\"오, 이건 다 집안 내력이에요. 며칠 전 샤토 르노가 백작님께 말씀드렸듯이요.\""], ["observed Maximilian.", "막시밀리앙이 말했다."], ["“This humble picture would have but little interest for you, accustomed as you are to behold the pleasures and the misfortunes of the wealthy and industrious; but such as we are, we have experienced bitter sorrows.”", "\"부유하고 부지런한 사람들의 기쁨과 불행을 보는 데 익숙하신 백작님께야 이 보잘것없는 그림이 별 흥미가 없으시겠지만, 그래도 보시다시피 저희도 쓰라린 슬픔을 겪었답니다.\""]], [["“And God has poured balm into your wounds, as he does into those of all who are in affliction?”", "\"그리고 신께서는 모든 괴로워하는 이들에게 그러시듯, 두 분의 상처에도 향유를 부어 주셨나요?\""], ["said Monte Cristo inquiringly.", "몬테크리스토가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, count,”", "\"네, 백작님.\" 쥘리가 대답했다."], ["returned Julie, “we may indeed say he has, for he has done for us what he grants only to his chosen; he sent us one of his angels.”", "\"정말 그렇게 말할 수 있어요. 신께서는 오직 택하신 이들에게만 베푸시는 일을 저희에게 해 주셨거든요. 저희에게 천사 하나를 보내 주셨답니다.\""]], [["The count’s cheeks became scarlet, and he coughed, in order to have an excuse for putting his handkerchief to his mouth.", "백작의 두 뺨이 새빨갛게 물들었다. 그는 손수건을 입에 댈 구실을 만들려고 헛기침을 했다."]], [["“Those born to wealth, and who have the means of gratifying every wish,”", "\"부유하게 태어나 무엇이든 바라는 대로 채울 수 있는 사람들은,\""], ["said Emmanuel, “know not what is the real happiness of life, just as those who have been tossed on the stormy waters of the ocean on a few frail planks can alone realize the blessings of fair weather.”", "에마뉘엘이 말했다. \"삶의 진정한 행복이 무엇인지 알지 못하지요. 마치 몇 장의 부서지기 쉬운 널빤지에 의지해 사나운 바다 위를 떠다녀 본 사람만이 맑은 날씨의 축복을 깨닫는 것처럼 말입니다.\""]], [["Monte Cristo rose, and without making any answer (for the tremulousness of his voice would have betrayed his emotion) walked up and down the apartment with a slow step.", "몬테크리스토는 자리에서 일어나, 아무 대답도 하지 않고서—목소리가 떨려 감정을 들킬 것 같았으므로—느린 걸음으로 방 안을 거닐었다."]], [["“Our magnificence makes you smile, count,” said Maximilian, who had followed him with his eyes.", "\"저희의 호사스러움이 우습게 보이시는군요, 백작님.\" 눈으로 그를 좇던 막시밀리앙이 말했다."]], [["“No, no,”", "\"아니, 아닙니다.\""], ["returned Monte Cristo, pale as death, pressing one hand on his heart to still its throbbings, while with the other he pointed to a crystal cover, beneath which a silken purse lay on a black velvet cushion.", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. 죽은 사람처럼 창백해진 그는 한 손을 가슴에 얹어 두근거림을 가라앉히면서, 다른 손으로는 수정 덮개를 가리켰다. 그 아래 검은 벨벳 방석 위에 비단 지갑 하나가 놓여 있었다."], ["“I was wondering what could be the significance of this purse, with the paper at one end and the large diamond at the other.”", "\"저 지갑에 무슨 사연이 있나 궁금했을 뿐입니다. 한쪽 끝에는 종이가, 다른 쪽 끝에는 커다란 다이아몬드가 들어 있어서요.\""]], [["“Count,”", "\"백작님,\""], ["replied Maximilian, with an air of gravity, “those are our most precious family treasures.”", "막시밀리앙이 엄숙한 태도로 대답했다. \"저것이 저희 집안의 가장 소중한 보물입니다.\""]], [["“The stone seems very brilliant,”", "\"보석이 무척 영롱하군요.\""], ["answered the count.", "백작이 대답했다."]], [["“Oh, my brother does not allude to its value, although it has been estimated at 100,000 francs; he means, that the articles contained in this purse are the relics of the angel I spoke of just now.”", "\"오, 제 동생은 그 값어치를 말하는 게 아니랍니다. 십만 프랑으로 매겨지긴 했지만요. 동생 말은, 이 지갑 안에 든 물건들이 방금 말씀드린 그 천사의 유물이라는 뜻이에요.\""]], [["“This I do not comprehend; and yet I may not ask for an explanation, madame,”", "\"무슨 말씀이신지 모르겠습니다. 그렇다고 설명을 청할 수도 없겠지요, 부인.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo bowing.", "몬테크리스토가 고개를 숙이며 대답했다."], ["“Pardon me, I had no intention of committing an indiscretion.”", "\"용서하십시오. 결례를 범할 뜻은 없었습니다.\""]], [["“Indiscretion,—oh, you make us happy by giving us an excuse for expatiating on this subject.", "\"결례라니요. 오히려 이 이야기를 늘어놓을 구실을 주시니 저희가 기쁜걸요."], ["If we wanted to conceal the noble action this purse commemorates, we should not expose it thus to view.", "이 지갑이 기리는 고귀한 행동을 감추고 싶었다면, 이렇게 내놓고 보여 드리지도 않았겠지요."], ["Oh, would we could relate it everywhere, and to everyone, so that the emotion of our unknown benefactor might reveal his presence.”", "오, 저희는 이 이야기를 어디서든, 누구에게든 들려주고 싶답니다. 그러면 저희의 알려지지 않은 은인이 마음이 동해 그 모습을 드러낼지도 모르니까요.\""]], [["“Ah, really,”", "\"아, 정말이지.\""], ["said Monte Cristo in a half-stifled voice.", "몬테크리스토가 반쯤 막힌 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Monsieur,”", "\"백작님,\" 막시밀리앙이 유리 덮개를 들어 올리고 비단 지갑에 정중히 입을 맞추며 대답했다."], ["returned Maximilian, raising the glass cover, and respectfully kissing the silken purse, “this has touched the hand of a man who saved my father from suicide, us from ruin, and our name from shame and disgrace,—a man by whose matchless benevolence we poor children, doomed to want and wretchedness, can at present hear everyone envying our happy lot.", "\"이 지갑은 한 사람의 손을 거친 것입니다. 제 아버지를 자살에서, 저희를 파멸에서, 저희 가문을 수치와 불명예에서 구해 주신 분이지요. 그분의 견줄 데 없는 자비 덕분에, 궁핍과 비참에 빠질 운명이었던 가엾은 저희 남매가 지금은 누구나 부러워하는 행복을 누리게 되었습니다."], ["This letter”", "이 편지는\""], ["(as he spoke, Maximilian drew a letter from the purse and gave it to the count)—“this letter was written by him the day that my father had taken a desperate resolution, and this diamond was given by the generous unknown to my sister as her dowry.”", "그렇게 말하며 막시밀리앙은 지갑에서 편지 한 통을 꺼내 백작에게 건넸다. \"아버지가 절망에 빠져 끔찍한 결심을 하셨던 바로 그날, 그분이 쓰신 것입니다. 그리고 이 다이아몬드는 그 너그러운 미지의 분이 제 누이에게 지참금으로 주신 것이고요.\""]], [["Monte Cristo opened the letter, and read it with an indescribable feeling of delight.", "몬테크리스토는 편지를 펼쳐 형언할 수 없는 기쁨에 젖어 읽어 내려갔다."], ["It was the letter written (as our readers know) to Julie, and signed “Sinbad the Sailor.”", "그것은 (독자들이 아시다시피) 쥘리에게 쓰여 \"뱃사람 신드바드\"라고 서명된 바로 그 편지였다."]], [["“Unknown you say, is the man who rendered you this service—unknown to you?”", "\"이 일을 베풀어 주신 분이 알려지지 않은 사람이라고요? 두 분께 알려지지 않은?\""]], [["“Yes; we have never had the happiness of pressing his hand,”", "\"네. 저희는 그분의 손을 잡아 보는 행복을 누리지 못했답니다.\""], ["continued Maximilian.", "막시밀리앙이 말을 이었다."], ["“We have supplicated Heaven in vain to grant us this favor, but the whole affair has had a mysterious meaning that we cannot comprehend—we have been guided by an invisible hand,—a hand as powerful as that of an enchanter.”", "\"그 은혜를 베풀어 달라고 하늘에 빌고 또 빌었지만 헛수고였지요. 이 모든 일에는 저희가 헤아릴 수 없는 신비로운 의미가 깃들어 있었습니다. 저희는 보이지 않는 손에, 마법사의 손처럼 강력한 손에 이끌려 왔어요.\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오,\""], ["cried Julie, “I have not lost all hope of some day kissing that hand, as I now kiss the purse which he has touched.", "쥘리가 외쳤다. \"저는 언젠가 그 손에 입 맞출 희망을 버리지 않았어요. 지금 그분이 만지셨던 이 지갑에 입 맞추듯이요."], ["Four years ago, Penelon was at Trieste—Penelon, count, is the old sailor you saw in the garden, and who, from quartermaster, has become gardener—Penelon, when he was at Trieste, saw on the quay an Englishman, who was on the point of embarking on board a yacht, and he recognized him as the person who called on my father the fifth of June, 1829, and who wrote me this letter on the fifth of September.", "사 년 전, 페늘롱이 트리에스테에 있었어요. 페늘롱은, 백작님, 정원에서 보신 그 늙은 선원이랍니다. 갑판장에서 정원사가 된 사람이지요."], ["He felt convinced of his identity, but he did not venture to address him.”", "페늘롱이 트리에스테에 있을 때, 부두에서 막 요트에 오르려던 영국인 한 사람을 보았어요. 그런데 그가 1829년 6월 5일에 제 아버지를 찾아왔고, 또 9월 5일에 제게 이 편지를 써 준 바로 그 사람임을 알아본 거예요. 같은 사람이라고 확신했지만, 차마 말을 걸지는 못했답니다.\""]], [["“An Englishman,”", "\"영국인이라고요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, who grew uneasy at the attention with which Julie looked at him.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. 쥘리가 자신을 뚫어지게 바라보는 통에 불안해진 것이었다."], ["“An Englishman you say?”", "\"영국인이라고 하셨습니까?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["replied Maximilian, “an Englishman, who represented himself as the confidential clerk of the house of Thomson & French, at Rome.", "막시밀리앙이 대답했다. \"영국인이었습니다. 로마에 있는 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회의 신임받는 직원이라고 자기를 소개했지요."], ["It was this that made me start when you said the other day, at M. de Morcerf’s, that Messrs. Thomson & French were your bankers.", "그래서 며칠 전 모르세르 씨 댁에서 백작님이 톰슨 앤드 프렌치가 백작님의 거래 은행이라고 하셨을 때 제가 깜짝 놀랐던 겁니다."], ["That happened, as I told you, in 1829.", "말씀드린 대로 그 일은 1829년에 있었지요."], ["For God’s sake, tell me, did you know this Englishman?”", "제발 말씀해 주십시오. 백작님은 그 영국인을 아셨습니까?\""]], [["“But you tell me, also, that the house of Thomson & French have constantly denied having rendered you this service?”", "\"하지만 두 분 말씀으로는, 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회가 이 은혜를 베푼 적이 없다고 줄곧 부인해 왔다고 하셨지요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Then is it not probable that this Englishman may be someone who, grateful for a kindness your father had shown him, and which he himself had forgotten, has taken this method of requiting the obligation?”", "\"그렇다면 이 영국인이, 두 분의 아버님께서 베푸셨던, 그러나 아버님 자신은 잊고 계셨던 어떤 친절에 감사하는 마음으로, 그 신세를 갚으려고 이런 방법을 택한 누군가일 수도 있지 않겠습니까?\""]], [["“Everything is possible in this affair, even a miracle.”", "\"이 일에서는 무엇이든 가능하지요. 기적까지도요.\""]], [["“What was his name?” asked Monte Cristo.", "\"그 사람의 이름이 무엇이었습니까?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다."]], [["“He gave no other name,”", "\"그는 다른 이름은 대지 않았어요.\""], ["answered Julie, looking earnestly at the count, “than that at the end of his letter—‘Sinbad the Sailor.’”", "쥘리가 백작을 진지하게 바라보며 대답했다. \"편지 끝에 적힌 이름, '뱃사람 신드바드' 말고는요.\""]], [["“Which is evidently not his real name, but a fictitious one.”", "\"그건 분명 본명이 아니라 꾸며 낸 이름이겠지요.\""]], [["Then, noticing that Julie was struck with the sound of his voice:", "그러고는 쥘리가 자기 목소리에 흠칫하는 것을 알아채고 말을 이었다."]], [["“Tell me,”", "\"말씀해 보십시오.\""], ["continued he, “was he not about my height, perhaps a little taller, with his chin imprisoned, as it were, in a high cravat; his coat closely buttoned up, and constantly taking out his pencil?”", "그가 계속했다. \"그 사람이 제 키쯤, 어쩌면 좀 더 컸고, 턱이 높은 크라바트에 파묻히다시피 했으며, 외투 단추를 빈틈없이 채우고, 끊임없이 연필을 꺼내 들지 않았습니까?\""]], [["“Oh, do you then know him?”", "\"오, 그럼 그분을 아세요?\""], ["cried Julie, whose eyes sparkled with joy.", "쥘리가 외쳤다. 그녀의 눈이 기쁨으로 반짝였다."]], [["“No,” returned Monte Cristo “I only guessed.", "\"아닙니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다."], ["I knew a Lord Wilmore, who was constantly doing actions of this kind.”", "\"그저 짐작해 본 것뿐입니다. 제가 윌모어 경이라는 사람을 알았는데, 늘 이런 종류의 일을 하던 분이었지요.\""]], [["“Without revealing himself?”", "\"자기 정체를 밝히지 않고요?\""]], [["“He was an eccentric being, and did not believe in the existence of gratitude.”", "\"괴팍한 사람이었습니다. 감사라는 게 존재한다고 믿지 않았지요.\""]], [["“Oh, Heaven,”", "\"오, 맙소사,\""], ["exclaimed Julie, clasping her hands, “in what did he believe, then?”", "쥘리가 두 손을 맞잡으며 외쳤다. \"그럼 그분은 무엇을 믿으셨나요?\""]], [["30047m\n“He did not credit it at the period which I knew him,”", "\"제가 그를 알던 무렵에는 감사를 믿지 않았습니다.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, touched to the heart by the accents of Julie’s voice; “but, perhaps, since then he has had proofs that gratitude does exist.”", "몬테크리스토가 쥘리의 음성에 가슴이 뭉클해져 말했다. \"하지만 어쩌면, 그 뒤로는 감사라는 게 정말 존재한다는 증거를 얻었을지도 모르지요.\""]], [["“And do you know this gentleman, monsieur?”", "\"그럼 그분을 아세요, 백작님?\""], ["inquired Emmanuel.", "에마뉘엘이 물었다."]], [["“Oh, if you do know him,”", "\"오, 정말 그분을 아신다면,\""], ["cried Julie, “can you tell us where he is—where we can find him?", "쥘리가 외쳤다. \"어디 계신지, 어디서 찾을 수 있는지 말씀해 주실 수 있나요?"], ["Maximilian—Emmanuel—if we do but discover him, he must believe in the gratitude of the heart!”", "막시밀리앙, 에마뉘엘, 우리가 그분을 찾아내기만 하면, 그분도 마음에서 우러난 감사가 있다는 걸 믿게 되실 거예요!\""]], [["Monte Cristo felt tears start into his eyes, and he again walked hastily up and down the room.", "몬테크리스토는 눈에 눈물이 차오르는 것을 느끼고, 다시 황급히 방 안을 거닐었다."]], [["“In the name of Heaven,”", "\"제발 부탁드립니다.\""], ["said Maximilian, “if you know anything of him, tell us what it is.”", "막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"그분에 대해 무엇이든 아시는 게 있다면, 그것이 무엇인지 말씀해 주십시오.\""]], [["“Alas,”", "\"아아,\""], ["cried Monte Cristo, striving to repress his emotion, “if Lord Wilmore was your unknown benefactor, I fear you will never see him again.", "몬테크리스토가 감정을 누르려 애쓰며 외쳤다. \"윌모어 경이 두 분의 알려지지 않은 은인이라면, 다시는 그를 만나지 못하실 것 같아 두렵습니다."], ["I parted from him two years ago at Palermo, and he was then on the point of setting out for the most remote regions; so that I fear he will never return.”", "저는 이 년 전 팔레르모에서 그와 헤어졌는데, 그때 그는 가장 먼 지방으로 떠나려던 참이었습니다. 그러니 다시는 돌아오지 않을까 걱정스럽군요.\""]], [["“Oh, monsieur, this is cruel of you,”", "\"오, 백작님, 너무 잔인하세요.\""], ["said Julie, much affected; and the young lady’s eyes swam with tears.", "쥘리가 깊이 마음 상해 말했다. 젊은 부인의 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁했다."]], [["“Madame,”", "\"부인,\""], ["replied Monte Cristo gravely, and gazing earnestly on the two liquid pearls that trickled down Julie’s cheeks, “had Lord Wilmore seen what I now see, he would become attached to life, for the tears you shed would reconcile him to mankind;”", "몬테크리스토가 엄숙하게, 쥘리의 뺨을 타고 흐르는 두 알의 영롱한 눈물방울을 진지하게 바라보며 대답했다. \"윌모어 경이 지금 제가 보는 것을 보았더라면, 그는 삶에 애착을 느꼈을 겁니다. 부인이 흘리시는 눈물이 그를 인류와 화해시켜 주었을 테니까요.\""], ["and he held out his hand to Julie, who gave him hers, carried away by the look and accent of the count.", "그는 쥘리에게 손을 내밀었고, 백작의 눈빛과 말투에 사로잡힌 그녀는 그에게 자기 손을 맡겼다."]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["continued she, “Lord Wilmore had a family or friends, he must have known someone, can we not——”", "그녀가 말을 이었다. \"윌모어 경에게도 가족이나 친구가 있었을 테고, 누군가는 알았을 텐데요. 저희가 혹시……\""]], [["“Oh, it is useless to inquire,”", "\"오, 알아보셔야 소용없습니다.\""], ["returned the count; “perhaps, after all, he was not the man you seek for.", "백작이 대답했다. \"어쩌면 결국 그는 두 분이 찾으시는 사람이 아닐지도 모릅니다."], ["He was my friend: he had no secrets from me, and if this had been so he would have confided in me.”", "그는 제 친구였고, 제게 아무것도 숨기지 않았으니, 만약 그런 일이 있었다면 제게 털어놓았을 겁니다.\""]], [["“And he told you nothing?”", "\"그래서 그가 당신에게 아무 말도 하지 않았다고요?\""]], [["“Nothing that would lead you to suppose?”", "\"짐작이라도 할 만한 어떤 것도요?\""]], [["“Nothing.”", "\"아무것도요.\""]], [["“And yet you spoke of him at once.”", "\"그런데도 곧바로 그분 이야기를 꺼내셨군요.\""]], [["“Ah, in such a case one supposes——”", "\"아, 그런 경우에는 누구라도 짐작을 하는 법이지요……\""]], [["“Sister, sister,”", "\"누나, 누나,\""], ["said Maximilian, coming to the count’s aid, “monsieur is quite right.", "막시밀리앙이 백작을 거들며 말했다. \"백작님 말씀이 옳아요."], ["Recollect what our excellent father so often told us, ‘It was no Englishman that thus saved us.’”", "우리의 훌륭하신 아버지가 그토록 자주 하시던 말씀을 떠올려 봐요. '우리를 그렇게 구해 준 사람은 영국인이 아니었다'고 하셨잖아요.\""]], [["Monte Cristo started.", "몬테크리스토가 흠칫했다."], ["“What did your father tell you, M. Morrel?”", "\"아버님이 뭐라고 하셨다고요, 모렐 씨?\""], ["said he eagerly.", "그가 다급히 물었다."]], [["“My father thought that this action had been miraculously performed—he believed that a benefactor had arisen from the grave to save us.", "\"제 아버지는 그 일이 기적처럼 이루어졌다고 생각하셨습니다. 어떤 은인이 무덤에서 일어나 저희를 구했다고 믿으셨지요. 오, 그건 가슴 뭉클한 미신이었습니다, 백작님."], ["Oh, it was a touching superstition, monsieur, and although I did not myself believe it, I would not for the world have destroyed my father’s faith.", "저 자신은 그것을 믿지 않았지만, 무슨 일이 있어도 아버지의 믿음만은 깨뜨리고 싶지 않았어요. 아버지가 얼마나 자주 그 일을 곱씹으며, 어느 소중한 친구의 이름을, 영영 잃어버린 친구의 이름을 부르셨던지요."], ["How often did he muse over it and pronounce the name of a dear friend—a friend lost to him forever; and on his death-bed, when the near approach of eternity seemed to have illumined his mind with supernatural light, this thought, which had until then been but a doubt, became a conviction, and his last words were, ‘Maximilian, it was Edmond Dantès!’”", "그리고 임종 자리에서, 영원이 가까이 다가오며 그분의 마음을 초자연적인 빛으로 밝혀 준 듯했을 때, 그때까지 의심에 불과했던 그 생각이 확신으로 굳어졌습니다. 아버지의 마지막 말씀은 이러했지요. '막시밀리앙, 그건 에드몽 당테스였다!'\""]], [["At these words the count’s paleness, which had for some time been increasing, became alarming; he could not speak; he looked at his watch like a man who has forgotten the hour, said a few hurried words to Madame Herbault, and pressing the hands of Emmanuel and Maximilian,—“Madame,”", "이 말에, 한동안 점점 더해 가던 백작의 창백함이 놀라울 지경에 이르렀다. 그는 말을 잇지 못했다. 시각을 잊은 사람처럼 시계를 들여다보고는, 에르보 부인에게 몇 마디 황급한 말을 건넸다."], ["said he, “I trust you will allow me to visit you occasionally; I value your friendship, and feel grateful to you for your welcome, for this is the first time for many years that I have thus yielded to my feelings;”", "그리고 에마뉘엘과 막시밀리앙의 손을 잡으며 말했다. \"부인, 부디 가끔 찾아뵙는 것을 허락해 주시기 바랍니다. 두 분의 우정을 소중히 여기며, 따뜻하게 맞아 주셔서 감사드립니다."], ["and he hastily quitted the apartment.", "여러 해 만에 처음으로 이렇게 제 감정에 굴복해 본 것이니까요.\" 그러고서 그는 황급히 방을 나섰다."]], [["“This Count of Monte Cristo is a strange man,”", "\"이 몬테크리스토 백작은 참 별난 사람이군.\""], ["said Emmanuel.", "에마뉘엘이 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래요.\""], ["answered Maximilian, “but I feel sure he has an excellent heart, and that he likes us.”", "막시밀리앙이 대답했다. \"하지만 저분은 더없이 훌륭한 마음을 지녔고, 우리를 좋아한다는 게 확실히 느껴져요.\""]], [["“His voice went to my heart,”", "\"그분 목소리가 제 가슴에 와닿았어요.\""], ["observed Julie; “and two or three times I fancied that I had heard it before.”", "쥘리가 말했다. \"그리고 두세 번쯤은, 전에 들어 본 목소리 같다는 생각이 들었답니다.\""]], [["# Chapter 51. Pyramus and Thisbe", "# Chapter 51. Pyramus and Thisbe"]], [["About two-thirds of the way along the Faubourg Saint-Honoré, and in the rear of one of the most imposing mansions in this rich neighborhood, where the various houses vie with each other for elegance of design and magnificence of construction, extended a large garden, where the wide-spreading chestnut-trees raised their heads high above the walls in a solid rampart, and with the coming of every spring scattered a shower of delicate pink and white blossoms into the large stone vases that stood upon the two square pilasters of a curiously wrought iron gate, that dated from the time of Louis XIII.", "피라무스와 티스베●\n생토노레 거리를 따라 삼분의 이쯤 걸어 들어간 곳, 집집마다 설계의 우아함과 건축의 화려함을 다투는 그 부유한 동네에서도 가장 위풍당당한 저택 가운데 하나의 뒤편으로, 넓은 정원 하나가 펼쳐져 있었다. 그 정원에는 가지를 넓게 뻗은 밤나무들이 단단한 성벽처럼 담장 위로 높이 머리를 치켜들고 있었고, 봄이 올 때마다 섬세한 분홍빛과 흰빛 꽃잎을 비처럼 흩뿌렸다. 그 꽃잎들은 루이 13세 시절로 거슬러 올라가는, 기묘하게 세공된 철문의 네모진 두 기둥 위에 놓인 커다란 돌 화병 속으로 떨어져 내렸다."]], [["This noble entrance, however, in spite of its striking appearance and the graceful effect of the geraniums planted in the two vases, as they waved their variegated leaves in the wind and charmed the eye with their scarlet bloom, had fallen into utter disuse.", "그러나 이 고귀한 출입구는, 그 인상적인 외관에도 불구하고, 또 두 화병에 심긴 제라늄들이 얼룩덜룩한 잎사귀를 바람에 흔들며 진홍빛 꽃으로 눈을 사로잡는 우아한 정취에도 불구하고, 완전히 쓰이지 않은 채 버려져 있었다."], ["The proprietors of the mansion had many years before thought it best to confine themselves to the possession of the house itself, with its thickly planted courtyard, opening into the Faubourg Saint-Honoré, and to the garden shut in by this gate, which formerly communicated with a fine kitchen-garden of about an acre.", "저택의 주인들은 오래전부터 저택 본채만 지니는 편이 낫다고 여겼다. 본채는 나무가 빽빽이 심긴 안뜰과 함께 생토노레 거리로 통했고, 정원은 이 철문으로 가로막혀 있었는데, 그 철문은 예전에 한 에이커쯤 되는 훌륭한 채마밭으로 이어져 있었다."], ["For the demon of speculation drew a line, or in other words projected a street, at the farther side of the kitchen-garden.", "투기의 악마가 그 채마밭 저편에 선을 하나 그었기 때문이다. 다시 말해, 거리 하나를 설계해 놓았던 것이다."], ["The street was laid out, a name was chosen and posted up on an iron plate, but before construction was begun, it occurred to the possessor of the property that a handsome sum might be obtained for the ground then devoted to fruits and vegetables, by building along the line of the proposed street, and so making it a branch of communication with the Faubourg Saint-Honoré itself, one of the most important thoroughfares in the city of Paris.", "거리가 측량되고, 이름이 정해져 철판에 새겨 붙여졌다. 그러나 공사가 시작되기도 전에, 그 땅의 소유주는 한 가지 생각이 떠올랐다. 지금 과일과 채소에 바쳐진 이 땅을 따라 건물을 지어 올리면, 그래서 그 길을 파리에서 가장 중요한 대로 가운데 하나인 생토노레 거리 자체와 통하는 지선으로 만들면, 제법 두둑한 돈을 얻을 수 있으리라는 것이었다."]], [["In matters of speculation, however, though “man proposes,” yet “money disposes.”", "그러나 투기라는 일에서는, \"사람이 꾀해도\" \"돈이 처리하는\" 법이다."], ["From some such difficulty the newly named street died almost in birth, and the purchaser of the kitchen-garden, having paid a high price for it, and being quite unable to find anyone willing to take his bargain off his hands without a considerable loss, yet still clinging to the belief that at some future day he should obtain a sum for it that would repay him, not only for his past outlay, but also the interest upon the capital locked up in his new acquisition, contented himself with letting the ground temporarily to some market-gardeners, at a yearly rental of 500 francs.", "그런 비슷한 난관 탓에, 새로 이름까지 얻은 그 거리는 태어나자마자 죽고 말았다. 그리고 채마밭을 사들인 자는, 비싼 값을 치른 데다, 상당한 손해를 보지 않고서는 그 매물을 떠맡아 줄 사람을 도무지 찾을 수 없었다. 그러면서도 언젠가는 그 땅으로, 지난날의 지출은 물론이고 새로 사들인 자산에 묶인 자본의 이자까지 갚아 줄 만한 돈을 손에 넣게 되리라는 믿음을 여전히 버리지 못한 채, 결국 그 땅을 몇몇 채소 장수에게 한 해 500프랑의 세를 받고 임시로 빌려주는 것으로 만족했다."]], [["And so, as we have said, the iron gate leading into the kitchen-garden had been closed up and left to the rust, which bade fair before long to eat off its hinges, while to prevent the ignoble glances of the diggers and delvers of the ground from presuming to sully the aristocratic enclosure belonging to the mansion, the gate had been boarded up to a height of six feet.", "그리하여, 앞서 말했듯, 채마밭으로 통하는 철문은 닫힌 채 녹슬도록 방치되었고, 머지않아 그 녹이 경첩을 갉아먹어 버릴 듯했다. 한편 땅을 파고 일구는 자들의 천한 시선이 감히 저택에 딸린 귀족적인 울타리 안을 더럽히지 못하도록, 철문은 6피트 높이까지 널빤지로 막혀 있었다."], ["True, the planks were not so closely adjusted but that a hasty peep might be obtained through their interstices; but the strict decorum and rigid propriety of the inhabitants of the house left no grounds for apprehending that advantage would be taken of that circumstance.", "사실 그 널빤지들이 그리 빈틈없이 맞물려 있지는 않아서, 그 틈새로 황급히 안을 엿볼 수는 있었다. 그러나 저택에 사는 사람들의 엄격한 예법과 빈틈없는 품위로 보아, 누가 그런 틈을 악용하리라고 염려할 까닭은 전혀 없었다."]], [["Horticulture seemed, however, to have been abandoned in the deserted kitchen-garden; and where cabbages, carrots, radishes, peas, and melons had once flourished, a scanty crop of lucern alone bore evidence of its being deemed worthy of cultivation.", "그러나 버려진 채마밭에서는 원예가 손을 놓아 버린 듯했다. 한때 양배추며 당근, 무, 완두콩, 멜론이 무성하던 자리에는, 이제 듬성듬성한 자주개자리 한 무더기만이 그나마 가꿀 만한 가치가 있다고 여겨졌음을 증언하고 있었다."], ["A small, low door gave egress from the walled space we have been describing into the projected street, the ground having been abandoned as unproductive by its various renters, and had now fallen so completely in general estimation as to return not even the one-half per cent it had originally paid.", "우리가 지금껏 묘사해 온 담장으로 둘러싸인 공간에서 그 설계된 거리로 빠져나가는, 낮고 작은 문이 하나 있었다. 그 땅은 여러 소작인이 소출이 없다고 버린 곳이라, 이제는 세간의 평가가 완전히 땅에 떨어져, 처음 내던 임차료의 절반의 절반도 거두어들이지 못했다."], ["Towards the house the chestnut-trees we have before mentioned rose high above the wall, without in any way affecting the growth of other luxuriant shrubs and flowers that eagerly dressed forward to fill up the vacant spaces, as though asserting their right to enjoy the boon of light and air.", "저택 쪽으로는 앞서 말한 밤나무들이 담장 위로 높이 솟아 있었지만, 그렇다고 다른 무성한 관목이며 꽃들이 자라는 것을 조금도 막지는 못했다. 그 식물들은 마치 빛과 공기라는 은혜를 누릴 권리가 제게 있다고 주장하듯, 빈자리를 메우려 앞다투어 몸을 내밀었다."], ["At one corner, where the foliage became so thick as almost to shut out day, a large stone bench and sundry rustic seats indicated that this sheltered spot was either in general favor or particular use by some inhabitant of the house, which was faintly discernible through the dense mass of verdure that partially concealed it, though situated but a hundred paces off.", "한쪽 구석에서는 잎이 어찌나 무성한지 거의 햇빛마저 가릴 지경이었는데, 그곳에 놓인 커다란 돌 의자와 몇몇 소박한 걸상들은, 이 아늑한 자리가 저택에 사는 누군가에게 두루 사랑받거나 특별히 쓰이는 곳임을 말해 주었다. 저택은 그곳을 일부 가리고 있는 빽빽한 초록빛 더미 사이로 어렴풋이 보였는데, 백 걸음밖에 떨어져 있지 않았는데도 그러했다."]], [["Whoever had selected this retired portion of the grounds as the boundary of a walk, or as a place for meditation, was abundantly justified in the choice by the absence of all glare, the cool, refreshing shade, the screen it afforded from the scorching rays of the sun, that found no entrance there even during the burning days of hottest summer, the incessant and melodious warbling of birds, and the entire removal from either the noise of the street or the bustle of the mansion.", "이 외진 정원 한 귀퉁이를 산책의 끝자락으로, 혹은 명상의 자리로 고른 사람이 누구였든, 그 선택은 충분히 정당했다. 눈부신 빛이라곤 없었고, 서늘하고 상쾌한 그늘이 있었으며, 한여름 가장 무더운 날에도 이곳에는 발을 들이지 못하는 작열하는 햇살을 막아 주는 차양이 있었고, 끊임없이 들려오는 새들의 고운 지저귐이 있었으며, 거리의 소음에서도 저택의 부산함에서도 완전히 벗어나 있었기 때문이다. 봄이 파리 사람들에게 베푼 가장 따뜻한 날들 가운데 어느 하루의 저녁, 돌 의자 위에는 책 한 권과 양산 하나, 그리고 일감 바구니 하나가 아무렇게나 놓여 있는 것이 보였다."], ["On the evening of one of the warmest days spring had yet bestowed on the inhabitants of Paris, might be seen negligently thrown upon the stone bench, a book, a parasol, and a work-basket, from which hung a partly embroidered cambric handkerchief, while at a little distance from these articles was a young woman, standing close to the iron gate, endeavoring to discern something on the other side by means of the openings in the planks,—the earnestness of her attitude and the fixed gaze with which she seemed to seek the object of her wishes, proving how much her feelings were interested in the matter.", "바구니에서는 절반쯤 수놓다 만 고운 무명 손수건이 늘어져 있었다. 그 물건들에서 조금 떨어진 곳에 한 젊은 여인이 철문 가까이 바짝 붙어 서서, 널빤지 틈으로 건너편의 무언가를 알아보려 애쓰고 있었다. 그 간절한 자세와, 바라던 대상을 찾으려는 듯한 고정된 눈길은, 그녀의 마음이 이 일에 얼마나 깊이 쏠려 있는지를 잘 드러내고 있었다."]], [["At that instant the little side-gate leading from the waste ground to the street was noiselessly opened, and a tall, powerful young man appeared.", "바로 그 순간, 버려진 땅에서 거리로 통하는 작은 옆문이 소리 없이 열리며, 키가 크고 다부진 젊은 남자가 나타났다."], ["He was dressed in a common gray blouse and velvet cap, but his carefully arranged hair, beard and moustache, all of the richest and glossiest black, ill accorded with his plebeian attire.", "그는 흔해 빠진 회색 작업복에 벨벳 모자 차림이었지만, 더없이 풍성하고 윤기 흐르는 검은빛으로 정성껏 다듬은 머리와 턱수염, 콧수염은 그 천한 옷차림과 어울리지 않았다."], ["After casting a rapid glance around him, in order to assure himself that he was unobserved, he entered by the small gate, and, carefully closing and securing it after him, proceeded with a hurried step towards the barrier.", "그는 누가 보지 않는지 확인하려고 재빨리 주위를 한번 둘러본 뒤, 작은 문으로 들어와 조심스레 문을 닫아 잠그고는, 황급한 걸음으로 울타리를 향해 나아갔다."]], [["At the sight of him she expected, though probably not in such a costume, the young woman started in terror, and was about to make a hasty retreat.", "그녀가 기다리던 사람이긴 했으나 아마도 그런 차림은 아니었던 터라, 그를 보고 젊은 여인은 깜짝 놀라 황급히 물러서려 했다."], ["But the eye of love had already seen, even through the narrow chinks of the wooden palisades, the movement of the white robe, and observed the fluttering of the blue sash.", "그러나 사랑에 빠진 눈은 이미, 좁은 나무 울타리 틈새로도, 하얀 옷자락이 움직이는 것을 보았고, 파란 띠가 나부끼는 것을 알아챘다."], ["Pressing his lips close to the planks, he exclaimed:", "그는 널빤지에 입술을 바짝 대고 외쳤다."]], [["“Don’t be alarmed, Valentine—it is I!”", "\"놀라지 마요, 발랑틴. 나예요!\""]], [["Again the timid girl found courage to return to the gate, saying, as she did so:", "겁먹은 처녀는 다시 용기를 내어 철문으로 돌아왔고, 그러면서 말했다."]], [["“And why do you come so late today?", "\"그런데 오늘은 왜 이렇게 늦게 오셨어요?"], ["It is almost dinner-time, and I had to use no little diplomacy to get rid of my watchful stepmother, my too-devoted maid, and my troublesome brother, who is always teasing me about coming to work at my embroidery, which I am in a fair way never to get done.", "곧 저녁 식사 시간인걸요. 게다가 저는 늘 저를 감시하는 새어머니와, 지나치게 헌신적인 하녀와, 늘 저를 못살게 구는 성가신 동생을 떼어 내느라 적잖이 머리를 써야 했어요. 동생은 제가 수놓으러 온다고 놀려 대는데, 그 수는 영영 끝내지 못할 것 같아요."], ["So pray excuse yourself as well as you can for having made me wait, and, after that, tell me why I see you in a dress so singular that at first I did not recognize you.”", "그러니 저를 기다리게 한 일은 어떻게든 변명을 잘해 보시고, 그다음엔 어째서 제가 처음엔 못 알아볼 만큼 이상한 옷차림을 하고 계신지 말씀해 주세요.\""]], [["“Dearest Valentine,”", "\"사랑하는 발랑틴,\""], ["said the young man, “the difference between our respective stations makes me fear to offend you by speaking of my love, but yet I cannot find myself in your presence without longing to pour forth my soul, and tell you how fondly I adore you.", "젊은 남자가 말했다. \"우리 두 사람의 신분 차이 때문에, 내 사랑을 입에 담으면 당신의 기분을 상하게 할까 두렵소. 하지만 당신 앞에 서면, 내 영혼을 다 쏟아 내어, 내가 얼마나 애틋이 당신을 흠모하는지 말하고 싶은 마음을 도무지 억누를 수가 없구려."], ["If it be but to carry away with me the recollection of such sweet moments, I could even thank you for chiding me, for it leaves me a gleam of hope, that if you did not expect me (and that indeed would be worse than vanity to suppose), at least I was in your thoughts.", "그저 이런 달콤한 순간의 기억을 가슴에 품고 돌아가기 위해서라면, 나는 당신이 나를 나무란 것조차 고마워할 수 있소. 그 꾸지람은 내게 한 가닥 희망의 빛을 남겨 주니까. 비록 당신이 나를 기다린 게 아니었다 해도 (그렇게 여기는 건 정말이지 자만을 넘어선 일이겠지만), 적어도 내가 당신의 생각 속에는 있었다는 희망 말이오."], ["You asked me the cause of my being late, and why I come disguised.", "당신은 내가 왜 늦었는지, 또 왜 변장을 하고 왔는지 물었지요."], ["I will candidly explain the reason of both, and I trust to your goodness to pardon me.", "두 가지 다 솔직히 까닭을 밝히겠소. 그러고 나면 당신이 너그러이 나를 용서해 주리라 믿소."], ["I have chosen a trade.”", "나는 직업을 하나 골랐소.\""]], [["“A trade?", "\"직업이요?"], ["Oh, Maximilian, how can you jest at a time when we have such deep cause for uneasiness?”", "아, 막시밀리앙, 우리에게 이토록 깊은 근심거리가 있는 때에 어떻게 농담을 하실 수 있어요?\""]], [["“Heaven keep me from jesting with that which is far dearer to me than life itself!", "\"내 목숨보다도 훨씬 더 소중한 것을 두고 농담을 하다니, 하늘이 막아 주시기를!"], ["But listen to me, Valentine, and I will tell you all about it.", "하지만 발랑틴, 내 말을 들어 보시오. 자초지종을 다 말해 주리다."], ["I became weary of ranging fields and scaling walls, and seriously alarmed at the idea suggested by you, that if caught hovering about here your father would very likely have me sent to prison as a thief.", "나는 들판을 헤매고 담을 기어오르는 일에 진력이 났소. 게다가 당신이 일러 준 생각, 그러니까 내가 이 근처를 어슬렁대다 들키면 당신 아버지가 나를 도둑으로 몰아 감옥에 처넣을지도 모른다는 생각에 진지하게 겁이 났소."], ["That would compromise the honor of the French army, to say nothing of the fact that the continual presence of a captain of Spahis in a place where no warlike projects could be supposed to account for it might well create surprise; so I have become a gardener, and, consequently, adopted the costume of my calling.”", "그건 프랑스군의 명예를 더럽히는 일일 테고, 또 전쟁 계획이 있을 리 없는 곳에 스파이 기병대 대위가 줄곧 나타난다면 사람들이 의아해할 게 뻔하다는 점은 말할 것도 없소. 그래서 나는 정원사가 되었고, 그 결과로 이 직업에 맞는 옷차림을 한 것이오.\""]], [["“What excessive nonsense you talk, Maximilian!”", "\"무슨 그런 터무니없는 말씀을 하세요, 막시밀리앙!\""]], [["“Nonsense? Pray do not call what I consider the wisest action of my life by such a name.", "\"터무니없다니? 부디 내 평생 가장 현명한 행동이라 여기는 일을 그런 식으로 부르지 마시오."], ["Consider, by becoming a gardener I effectually screen our meetings from all suspicion or danger.” 30053m", "생각해 보시오. 정원사가 됨으로써 나는 우리의 만남을 온갖 의심이나 위험에서 완벽하게 가려 주는 거요.\""]], [["“I beseech of you, Maximilian, to cease trifling, and tell me what you really mean.”", "\"제발 부탁이에요, 막시밀리앙, 농담은 그만하시고, 정말 무슨 말씀이신지 알려 주세요.\""]], [["“Simply, that having ascertained that the piece of ground on which I stand was to let, I made application for it, was readily accepted by the proprietor, and am now master of this fine crop of lucern.", "\"간단하오. 내가 지금 서 있는 이 땅뙈기가 세를 놓는다는 걸 알아내고, 그걸 빌리겠다고 신청했더니, 주인이 선뜻 받아 주었고, 그래서 나는 이제 이 훌륭한 자주개자리 밭의 주인이 된 거요."], ["Think of that, Valentine!", "생각해 보시오, 발랑틴!"], ["There is nothing now to prevent my building myself a little hut on my plantation, and residing not twenty yards from you.", "이제 내가 내 농장에 작은 오두막을 한 채 짓고, 당신에게서 스무 걸음도 떨어지지 않은 곳에 사는 것을 막을 것이 아무것도 없소."], ["Only imagine what happiness that would afford me. I can scarcely contain myself at the bare idea.", "그게 내게 얼마나 큰 행복을 안겨 줄지 한번 상상이나 해 보시오."], ["Such felicity seems above all price—as a thing impossible and unattainable.", "그 생각만으로도 나는 가슴이 벅차오르는 것을 가눌 수가 없소."], ["But would you believe that I purchase all this delight, joy, and happiness, for which I would cheerfully have surrendered ten years of my life, at the small cost of 500 francs per annum, paid quarterly? Henceforth we have nothing to fear.", "그런 더없는 기쁨은 그 무엇으로도 값을 매길 수 없는, 불가능하고 손에 닿지 않는 일처럼만 보였소. 그런데 내 목숨에서 십 년쯤은 기꺼이 내놓았을 이 모든 기쁨과 환희와 행복을, 한 해 500프랑이라는 적은 값으로, 그것도 분기마다 나누어 치르고 사들였다면 믿겠소?"], ["I am on my own ground, and have an undoubted right to place a ladder against the wall, and to look over when I please, without having any apprehensions of being taken off by the police as a suspicious character.", "이제부터 우리는 두려워할 게 아무것도 없소. 나는 내 땅 위에 있으니, 담에 사다리를 세우고 마음 내킬 때 너머를 들여다볼 분명한 권리가 있고, 수상한 자로 몰려 경찰에 끌려갈 걱정도 없소."], ["I may also enjoy the precious privilege of assuring you of my fond, faithful, and unalterable affection, whenever you visit your favorite bower, unless, indeed, it offends your pride to listen to professions of love from the lips of a poor workingman, clad in a blouse and cap.”", "또한 당신이 그 좋아하는 정자에 들를 때마다, 내 다정하고 한결같으며 변치 않는 사랑을 당신에게 다짐할 귀한 특권도 누릴 수 있소. 작업복에 모자를 쓴 가난한 일꾼의 입에서 나오는 사랑 고백을 듣는 게 당신의 자존심을 상하게 하지만 않는다면 말이오.\""]], [["A faint cry of mingled pleasure and surprise escaped from the lips of Valentine, who almost instantly said, in a saddened tone, as though some envious cloud darkened the joy which illumined her heart:", "기쁨과 놀라움이 뒤섞인 가느다란 탄성이 발랑틴의 입술에서 새어 나왔다. 그러나 그녀는 거의 곧바로, 마치 어떤 질투 어린 구름이 그녀의 가슴을 밝히던 기쁨을 어둡게 가리기라도 한 듯, 서글픈 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Alas, no, Maximilian, this must not be, for many reasons.", "\"아, 아니에요, 막시밀리앙, 이건 안 돼요. 여러 가지 이유에서요."], ["We should presume too much on our own strength, and, like others, perhaps, be led astray by our blind confidence in each other’s prudence.”", "우리는 우리 자신의 힘을 너무 믿어서는 안 돼요. 어쩌면 다른 사람들처럼, 서로의 신중함을 맹목적으로 믿다가 그릇된 길로 빠질지도 몰라요.\""]], [["“How can you for an instant entertain so unworthy a thought, dear Valentine?", "\"어떻게 한순간이라도 그런 부당한 생각을 품을 수 있소, 사랑하는 발랑틴?"], ["Have I not, from the first blessed hour of our acquaintance, schooled all my words and actions to your sentiments and ideas?", "우리가 알게 된 그 축복받은 첫 순간부터, 나는 내 말과 행동을 모두 당신의 마음과 생각에 맞추어 다스려 오지 않았소?"], ["And you have, I am sure, the fullest confidence in my honor.", "그리고 당신도 분명 내 명예를 더없이 믿고 있을 거요."], ["When you spoke to me of experiencing a vague and indefinite sense of coming danger, I placed myself blindly and devotedly at your service, asking no other reward than the pleasure of being useful to you; and have I ever since, by word or look, given you cause of regret for having selected me from the numbers that would willingly have sacrificed their lives for you?", "당신이 어렴풋하고 막연한, 다가올 위험의 예감이 든다고 말했을 때, 나는 아무런 보상도 바라지 않고 그저 당신에게 도움이 될 수 있다는 기쁨만을 바라며, 맹목적이고 헌신적으로 당신을 위해 몸을 바쳤소. 그 뒤로 내가 단 한 번이라도, 말로든 눈빛으로든, 당신을 위해 기꺼이 목숨을 내놓았을 그 숱한 사내들 가운데 나를 골라 준 것을 후회하게 만든 적이 있었소?"], ["You told me, my dear Valentine, that you were engaged to M. d’Épinay, and that your father was resolved upon completing the match, and that from his will there was no appeal, as M. de Villefort was never known to change a determination once formed.", "사랑하는 발랑틴, 당신은 내게 데피네 씨와 약혼한 사이라고, 당신 아버지가 그 혼사를 끝내 성사시키기로 굳게 마음먹었다고, 그리고 한번 정한 결심을 바꾼 적이 없는 빌포르 씨인지라 그 뜻에는 거역할 길이 없다고 말했소."], ["I kept in the background, as you wished, and waited, not for the decision of your heart or my own, but hoping that Providence would graciously interpose in our behalf, and order events in our favor.", "나는 당신이 원하는 대로 뒤로 물러나, 당신 마음의 결정도, 내 마음의 결정도 아닌, 다만 섭리가 자비로이 우리를 위해 끼어들어 일을 우리에게 유리하게 이끌어 주기를 바라며 기다렸소."], ["But what cared I for delays or difficulties, Valentine, as long as you confessed that you loved me, and took pity on me?", "하지만 발랑틴, 당신이 나를 사랑한다고, 나를 가엾이 여긴다고 고백해 준 이상, 내가 지체나 어려움 따위를 무엇 때문에 마음에 두겠소?"], ["If you will only repeat that avowal now and then, I can endure anything.”", "가끔이라도 그 고백을 되풀이해 준다면, 나는 무엇이든 견딜 수 있소.\""]], [["“Ah, Maximilian, that is the very thing that makes you so bold, and which renders me at once so happy and unhappy, that I frequently ask myself whether it is better for me to endure the harshness of my stepmother, and her blind preference for her own child, or to be, as I now am, insensible to any pleasure save such as I find in these meetings, so fraught with danger to both.”", "\"아, 막시밀리앙, 바로 그 점이 당신을 그토록 대담하게 만들고, 또 저를 동시에 그토록 행복하면서도 불행하게 만드는 거예요. 그래서 저는 자주 스스로에게 물어요. 새어머니의 모진 대접과 제 자식만 눈먼 듯 편애하는 꼴을 견디는 편이 나을지, 아니면 지금처럼 양쪽 모두에게 위험천만한 이 만남에서 얻는 것 말고는 어떤 기쁨에도 무감각해진 채 사는 편이 나을지요.\""]], [["“I will not admit that word,”", "\"그 말은 받아들일 수 없소.\""], ["returned the young man; “it is at once cruel and unjust.", "젊은 남자가 대꾸했다. \"그건 잔인하면서도 부당한 말이오."], ["Is it possible to find a more submissive slave than myself?", "나만큼 고분고분한 노예를 또 찾을 수 있겠소?"], ["You have permitted me to converse with you from time to time, Valentine, but forbidden my ever following you in your walks or elsewhere—have I not obeyed?", "발랑틴, 당신은 내게 가끔 당신과 이야기 나누는 것은 허락했지만, 산책길에서든 어디서든 당신을 따라다니는 것은 금했소. 내가 그 말을 따르지 않은 적이 있소?"], ["And since I found means to enter this enclosure to exchange a few words with you through this gate—to be close to you without really seeing you—have I ever asked so much as to touch the hem of your gown or tried to pass this barrier which is but a trifle to one of my youth and strength?", "그리고 이 울타리 안으로 들어와 이 철문 너머로 당신과 몇 마디 주고받을 방도를 찾아낸 뒤로도, 정작 당신을 제대로 보지도 못한 채 곁에 있을 뿐인데도, 내가 당신 옷자락 끝이라도 만지게 해 달라고 청한 적이 있소? 아니면 나 같은 젊음과 힘을 가진 자에게는 하찮기 그지없는 이 울타리를 넘으려 한 적이 있소?"], ["Never has a complaint or a murmur escaped me.", "단 한 번도 불평이나 투정을 입에 담은 적이 없소."], ["I have been bound by my promises as rigidly as any knight of olden times.", "나는 옛 기사들 못지않게 엄격히 내 약속에 매여 있었소."], ["Come, come, dearest Valentine, confess that what I say is true, lest I be tempted to call you unjust.”", "자, 자, 사랑하는 발랑틴, 내 말이 옳다고 인정해 주시오. 그러지 않으면 나는 당신을 부당한 사람이라 부르고 싶어질 테니.\""]], [["30055m\n“It is true,”", "\"맞아요.\""], ["said Valentine, as she passed the end of her slender fingers through a small opening in the planks, and permitted Maximilian to press his lips to them, “and you are a true and faithful friend; but still you acted from motives of self-interest, my dear Maximilian, for you well knew that from the moment in which you had manifested an opposite spirit all would have been ended between us.", "발랑틴이 가느다란 손가락 끝을 널빤지의 작은 틈으로 밀어 넣어, 막시밀리앙이 거기에 입술을 갖다 대도록 허락하며 말했다. \"당신은 진실하고 한결같은 벗이에요. 하지만 사랑하는 막시밀리앙, 당신은 어쨌든 자기 잇속을 따져 그렇게 행동한 거예요."], ["You promised to bestow on me the friendly affection of a brother.", "당신이 그와 반대되는 마음을 드러내는 순간 우리 사이는 모두 끝이라는 걸 잘 알고 있었으니까요. 당신은 제게 오빠 같은 다정한 애정을 베풀겠다고 약속했어요. 이 세상에 제 벗이라곤 당신뿐이니까요."], ["For I have no friend but yourself upon earth, who am neglected and forgotten by my father, harassed and persecuted by my stepmother, and left to the sole companionship of a paralyzed and speechless old man, whose withered hand can no longer press mine, and who can speak to me with the eye alone, although there still lingers in his heart the warmest tenderness for his poor grandchild.", "아버지에게는 등한시되고 잊힌 채, 새어머니에게는 들볶이고 박해받으며, 말 못 하고 몸이 마비된 노인 한 분만을 유일한 길벗으로 둔 저예요. 그 시든 손은 더 이상 제 손을 잡아 주지도 못하고, 오직 눈으로만 제게 말을 건넬 수 있을 뿐이지요. 그래도 그분 마음속에는 아직 가엾은 손녀를 향한 더없이 따뜻한 정이 남아 있어요."], ["Oh, how bitter a fate is mine, to serve either as a victim or an enemy to all who are stronger than myself, while my only friend and supporter is a living corpse!", "아, 제 운명은 얼마나 쓰라린지요. 저보다 힘센 모든 이에게 제물이 되거나 적이 되어야 하는데, 제 유일한 벗이자 의지가 되는 분은 살아 있는 시체나 다름없으니!"], ["Indeed, indeed, Maximilian, I am very miserable, and if you love me it must be out of pity.”", "정말로, 정말로, 막시밀리앙, 저는 너무나 비참해요. 만약 당신이 저를 사랑한다면, 그건 가엾이 여기는 마음에서일 거예요.\""]], [["“Valentine,”", "\"발랑틴,\""], ["replied the young man, deeply affected, “I will not say you are all I love in the world, for I dearly prize my sister and brother-in-law; but my affection for them is calm and tranquil, in no manner resembling what I feel for you.", "깊이 감동한 젊은 남자가 대답했다. \"당신이 이 세상에서 내가 사랑하는 전부라고는 말하지 않겠소. 나는 내 누이와 매형을 무척 아끼니까."], ["When I think of you my heart beats fast, the blood burns in my veins, and I can hardly breathe; but I solemnly promise you to restrain all this ardor, this fervor and intensity of feeling, until you yourself shall require me to render them available in serving or assisting you. M.", "하지만 그들을 향한 내 애정은 잔잔하고 평온해서, 당신에게 느끼는 것과는 도무지 닮은 데가 없소. 당신을 떠올리면 내 심장은 빠르게 뛰고, 피가 핏줄 속에서 타오르며, 숨조차 제대로 쉴 수가 없소. 하지만 나는 엄숙히 약속하겠소."], ["Franz is not expected to return home for a year to come, I am told; in that time many favorable and unforeseen chances may befriend us.", "당신 자신이 나더러 이 모든 것을 당신을 돕거나 섬기는 데 쓰라고 요구할 그날까지, 이 모든 열정과, 이 뜨겁고 격렬한 감정을 억누르겠다고 말이오. 듣자 하니 데피네 씨는 앞으로 일 년은 지나야 돌아온다더군."], ["Let us, then, hope for the best; hope is so sweet a comforter.", "그동안 뜻밖의 좋은 기회들이 우리 편을 들어 줄지도 모르오."], ["Meanwhile, Valentine, while reproaching me with selfishness, think a little what you have been to me—the beautiful but cold resemblance of a marble Venus.", "그러니 우리 잘되기를 바랍시다. 희망이란 참으로 달콤한 위안이니까. 그런데 발랑틴, 당신은 나를 이기적이라 나무라면서도, 정작 당신이 내게 어떤 존재였는지 한번 생각해 보시오."], ["What promise of future reward have you made me for all the submission and obedience I have evinced?—none whatever.", "아름답지만 차가운, 대리석 비너스를 빼닮은 모습이었소. 내가 그토록 순종하고 복종해 보였건만, 당신은 내게 어떤 보답의 약속을 해 주었소? 아무것도 없었소."], ["What granted me?—scarcely more.", "내게 무엇을 허락해 주었소?"], ["You tell me of M.", "그조차 거의 없었소."], ["Franz d’Épinay, your betrothed lover, and you shrink from the idea of being his wife; but tell me, Valentine, is there no other sorrow in your heart?", "당신은 약혼자인 데피네 씨 이야기를 하면서, 그의 아내가 된다는 생각에 몸서리를 치오. 하지만 말해 보시오, 발랑틴, 당신 마음속에 다른 슬픔은 없소? 당신은 내가 몸도 마음도 당신에게 바친 것을 보고 있소."], ["You see me devoted to you, body and soul, my life and each warm drop that circles round my heart are consecrated to your service; you know full well that my existence is bound up in yours—that were I to lose you I would not outlive the hour of such crushing misery; yet you speak with calmness of the prospect of your being the wife of another!", "내 목숨과, 내 심장을 도는 따뜻한 핏방울 하나하나가 당신을 섬기는 데 바쳐졌소. 당신은 내 존재가 당신의 존재에 묶여 있음을, 만약 당신을 잃는다면 그 짓누르는 비참함의 순간을 넘기고서는 살아남지 못하리라는 것을 너무도 잘 알고 있소. 그런데도 당신은 다른 사람의 아내가 될 거라는 앞날을 침착하게 입에 담는구려!"], ["Oh, Valentine, were I in your place, and did I feel conscious, as you do, of being worshipped, adored, with such a love as mine, a hundred times at least should I have passed my hand between these iron bars, and said, ‘Take this hand, dearest Maximilian, and believe that, living or dead, I am yours—yours only, and forever!’”", "아, 발랑틴, 내가 당신 처지였다면, 그리고 당신처럼 내 사랑 같은 사랑으로 흠모받고 떠받들어지고 있음을 느꼈다면, 적어도 백 번은 이 철창 사이로 손을 내밀어 이렇게 말했을 거요. '이 손을 잡아요, 사랑하는 막시밀리앙. 그리고 믿어요, 살아서든 죽어서든 나는 당신의 것, 오직 당신만의 것, 영원히 당신의 것이라는 걸!'\""]], [["The poor girl made no reply, but her lover could plainly hear her sobs and tears. A rapid change took place in the young man’s feelings.", "가엾은 처녀는 아무 대답도 하지 못했지만, 연인은 그녀의 흐느낌과 눈물 소리를 또렷이 들을 수 있었다. 젊은 남자의 마음에 급격한 변화가 일었다."]], [["“Dearest, dearest Valentine,”", "\"사랑하는, 사랑하는 발랑틴,\""], ["exclaimed he, “forgive me if I have offended you, and forget the words I spoke if they have unwittingly caused you pain.”", "그가 외쳤다. \"내가 당신의 기분을 상하게 했다면 용서해 주시오. 내 말이 본의 아니게 당신을 아프게 했다면 그 말은 잊어버려요.\""]], [["“No, Maximilian, I am not offended,”", "\"아니에요, 막시밀리앙, 기분이 상한 게 아니에요.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"하지만 제가 얼마나 가엾고 무력한 존재인지 모르세요?"], ["answered she, “but do you not see what a poor, helpless being I am, almost a stranger and an outcast in my father’s house, where even he is seldom seen; whose will has been thwarted, and spirits broken, from the age of ten years, beneath the iron rod so sternly held over me; oppressed, mortified, and persecuted, day by day, hour by hour, minute by minute, no person has cared for, even observed my sufferings, nor have I ever breathed one word on the subject save to yourself.", "아버지 집에서조차 거의 남이나 다름없고 버림받은 신세인걸요. 그 집에선 아버지마저 좀처럼 얼굴을 볼 수 없어요. 열 살 때부터 제 위에 그토록 모질게 드리워진 쇠막대 아래서 제 뜻은 꺾이고 기개는 부러져 버렸어요."], ["Outwardly and in the eyes of the world, I am surrounded by kindness and affection; but the reverse is the case.", "날마다, 시간마다, 분마다, 순간마다 억눌리고 모욕당하고 박해받아 왔지만, 누구 하나 제 고통을 보살펴 주기는커녕 눈여겨봐 준 적도 없어요. 그리고 저는 이 일을 두고 당신 말고는 누구에게도 단 한마디 입 밖에 낸 적이 없어요. 겉으로 보기에, 또 세상 사람들 눈에는, 제가 온갖 친절과 애정에 둘러싸여 있는 것 같겠지요."], ["The general remark is, ‘Oh, it cannot be expected that one of so stern a character as M.", "하지만 실상은 정반대예요. 사람들은 흔히 이렇게 말해요. '아, 빌포르 씨처럼 그토록 엄격한 성품을 지닌 분이, 어떤 아버지들이 딸에게 쏟는 그런 다정함을 베풀기를 바랄 수야 없지."], ["Villefort could lavish the tenderness some fathers do on their daughters.", "어린 나이에 친어머니를 잃긴 했어도, 빌포르 부인이라는 새어머니를 얻는 행복을 누렸으니 다행이지 뭐.'"], ["What though she has lost her own mother at a tender age, she has had the happiness to find a second mother in Madame de Villefort.’ The world, however, is mistaken; my father abandons me from utter indifference, while my stepmother detests me with a hatred so much the more terrible because it is veiled beneath a continual smile.”", "하지만 세상은 잘못 알고 있어요. 아버지는 철저한 무관심으로 저를 버려두었고, 새어머니는 저를 미워해요. 그것도 끊임없는 미소 뒤에 가려져 있어서 더욱더 무서운 증오로요.\""]], [["“Hate you, sweet Valentine,”", "\"당신을 미워한다니, 사랑스러운 발랑틴,\""], ["exclaimed the young man; “how is it possible for anyone to do that?”", "젊은 남자가 외쳤다. \"어떻게 누가 그럴 수 있단 말이오?\""]], [["“Alas,”", "\"아아,\" 울고 있던 처녀가 대답했다."], ["replied the weeping girl, “I am obliged to own that my stepmother’s aversion to me arises from a very natural source—her overweening love for her own child, my brother Edward.”", "\"인정할 수밖에 없네요. 새어머니가 저를 싫어하는 건 아주 자연스러운 데서 비롯된 거예요. 바로 제 자식, 그러니까 제 동생 에두아르를 향한 지나친 사랑 말이에요.\""]], [["“But why should it?”", "\"그렇다고 왜 그래야 하오?\""]], [["“I do not know; but, though unwilling to introduce money matters into our present conversation, I will just say this much—that her extreme dislike to me has its origin there; and I much fear she envies me the fortune I enjoy in right of my mother, and which will be more than doubled at the death of M.", "\"저도 몰라요. 하지만 지금 우리 이야기에 돈 문제를 끌어들이고 싶지는 않지만, 이 정도만은 말해 둘게요. 저를 향한 그 극심한 미움은 거기서 비롯된 거예요."], ["and Mme. de Saint-Méran, whose sole heiress I am.", "새어머니는, 제가 어머니에게서 물려받은 권리로 누리는 재산을 시샘하는 것 같아요."], ["Madame de Villefort has nothing of her own, and hates me for being so richly endowed.", "게다가 그 재산은 생메랑 후작 부부께서 돌아가시면 갑절이 넘게 늘어날 텐데, 그 두 분의 유일한 상속녀가 바로 저거든요."], ["Alas, how gladly would I exchange the half of this wealth for the happiness of at least sharing my father’s love.", "빌포르 부인은 자기 재산이라곤 한 푼도 없어서, 제가 그토록 풍족하게 물려받았다는 이유로 저를 미워하는 거예요."], ["God knows, I would prefer sacrificing the whole, so that it would obtain me a happy and affectionate home.”", "아아, 적어도 아버지의 사랑이라도 나눠 가지는 행복을 얻을 수만 있다면, 저는 이 재산의 절반쯤은 기꺼이 내놓을 텐데요. 하늘이 아시지만, 행복하고 다정한 가정을 얻을 수만 있다면 전부라도 내던지고 싶어요.\""]], [["“Poor Valentine!”", "\"가엾은 발랑틴!\""]], [["“I seem to myself as though living a life of bondage, yet at the same time am so conscious of my own weakness that I fear to break the restraint in which I am held, lest I fall utterly helpless.", "\"저는 마치 속박의 삶을 사는 것 같아요. 그러면서도 동시에 제 자신의 나약함을 너무도 잘 알고 있어서, 지금 매여 있는 이 굴레를 깨뜨렸다가 완전히 무너져 버릴까 봐 두려워요."], ["Then, too, my father is not a person whose orders may be infringed with impunity; protected as he is by his high position and firmly established reputation for talent and unswerving integrity, no one could oppose him; he is all-powerful even with the king; he would crush you at a word.", "게다가 아버지는 그 명령을 거역하고도 무사할 수 있는 분이 아니에요. 높은 지위와, 재능과 흔들림 없는 청렴함으로 굳건히 다져진 명성에 둘러싸여 있어서, 아무도 그분을 거스를 수 없어요. 아버지는 왕 앞에서도 무소불위의 힘을 지녔어요."], ["Dear Maximilian, believe me when I assure you that if I do not attempt to resist my father’s commands it is more on your account than my own.”", "말 한마디로 당신을 짓밟아 버릴 거예요. 사랑하는 막시밀리앙, 제 말을 믿어 주세요. 제가 아버지의 명령에 맞서려 들지 않는 건, 저 자신보다도 당신을 위해서랍니다.\""]], [["“But why, Valentine, do you persist in anticipating the worst,—why picture so gloomy a future?”", "\"하지만 발랑틴, 어째서 당신은 자꾸 최악을 미리 점치는 거요? 어째서 그토록 암울한 앞날을 그리는 거요?\""]], [["“Because I judge it from the past.”", "\"지나간 일로 미루어 판단하니까요.\""]], [["“Still, consider that although I may not be, strictly speaking, what is termed an illustrious match for you, I am, for many reasons, not altogether so much beneath your alliance.", "\"그래도 생각해 보시오. 비록 내가 엄밀히 말해 당신에게 걸맞은 빛나는 혼처는 아닐지 몰라도, 여러 이유로 당신과 맺어지기에 그리 못 미치는 사람은 아니오."], ["The days when such distinctions were so nicely weighed and considered no longer exist in France, and the first families of the monarchy have intermarried with those of the empire.", "그런 신분 차이를 그토록 까다롭게 따지고 저울질하던 시절은 프랑스에서 이제 사라졌소. 왕정의 으뜸가는 가문들이 제국의 가문들과 혼인을 맺어 왔소."], ["The aristocracy of the lance has allied itself with the nobility of the cannon.", "창의 귀족이 대포의 귀족과 손을 잡은 거요."], ["Now I belong to this last-named class; and certainly my prospects of military preferment are most encouraging as well as certain.", "그런데 나는 바로 그 후자에 속하오. 그리고 분명히, 군에서 승진할 내 앞날은 더없이 밝고 확실하오."], ["My fortune, though small, is free and unfettered, and the memory of my late father is respected in our country, Valentine, as that of the most upright and honorable merchant of the city; I say our country, because you were born not far from Marseilles.”", "내 재산은, 비록 많지는 않아도, 빚 한 푼 없이 온전한 내 것이고, 돌아가신 내 아버지에 대한 기억은, 발랑틴, 우리 고장에서 그 도시의 가장 정직하고 명예로운 상인으로 존경받고 있소. 우리 고장이라 말한 건, 당신도 마르세유에서 그리 멀지 않은 곳에서 태어났기 때문이오.\""]], [["“Don’t speak of Marseilles, I beg of you, Maximilian; that one word brings back my mother to my recollection—my angel mother, who died too soon for myself and all who knew her; but who, after watching over her child during the brief period allotted to her in this world, now, I fondly hope, watches from her home in heaven.", "\"마르세유 이야기는 하지 말아 주세요, 부탁이에요, 막시밀리앙. 그 한마디가 제 어머니를 기억 속에 되살려 놓아요. 천사 같은 제 어머니, 저에게도, 또 어머니를 아는 모든 이에게도 너무 일찍 세상을 떠나 버린 분."], ["Oh, if my mother were still living, there would be nothing to fear, Maximilian, for I would tell her that I loved you, and she would protect us.”", "하지만 이 세상에서 허락된 그 짧은 동안 당신의 아이를 지켜봐 주신 뒤로, 지금은, 제 간절한 바람이지만, 하늘의 거처에서 저를 지켜보고 계실 거예요. 아, 제 어머니가 아직 살아 계셨다면, 막시밀리앙, 두려울 게 아무것도 없었을 거예요. 어머니께 당신을 사랑한다고 말씀드렸을 테고, 어머니가 우리를 지켜 주셨을 테니까요.\""]], [["“I fear, Valentine,”", "\"그게 두렵소, 발랑틴.\""], ["replied the lover, “that were she living I should never have had the happiness of knowing you; you would then have been too happy to have stooped from your grandeur to bestow a thought on me.”", "연인이 대답했다. \"어머니가 살아 계셨다면 나는 당신을 알게 되는 행복을 결코 누리지 못했을 거요. 그랬다면 당신은 너무도 행복해서, 그 고귀한 자리에서 몸을 굽혀 나 같은 사람에게 생각 한 자락 베풀어 줄 일이 없었을 테니까.\""]], [["“Now it is you who are unjust, Maximilian,”", "\"이번엔 당신이 부당한 사람이군요, 막시밀리앙.\""], ["cried Valentine; “but there is one thing I wish to know.”", "발랑틴이 외쳤다. \"하지만 한 가지 알고 싶은 게 있어요.\""]], [["“And what is that?”", "\"그게 뭐요?\""], ["inquired the young man, perceiving that Valentine hesitated.", "젊은 남자가 발랑틴이 머뭇거리는 것을 알아채고 물었다."]], [["“Tell me truly, Maximilian, whether in former days, when our fathers dwelt at Marseilles, there was ever any misunderstanding between them?”", "\"솔직히 말해 주세요, 막시밀리앙. 지난날 우리 아버지들이 마르세유에 살던 시절, 두 분 사이에 무슨 불화 같은 게 있었나요?\""]], [["“Not that I am aware of,”", "\"내가 아는 한은 없소.\" 젊은 남자가 대답했다."], ["replied the young man, “unless, indeed, any ill-feeling might have arisen from their being of opposite parties—your father was, as you know, a zealous partisan of the Bourbons, while mine was wholly devoted to the emperor; there could not possibly be any other difference between them.", "\"다만, 두 분이 서로 다른 당파에 속해 있던 데서 어떤 앙금이 생겼을 수는 있겠소. 당신 아버지는 알다시피 부르봉 왕가의 열렬한 지지자였고, 내 아버지는 황제께 온전히 헌신한 분이었으니. 그 밖에 두 분 사이에 다른 차이가 있었을 리는 없소."], ["But why do you ask?”", "그런데 왜 묻는 거요?\""]], [["“I will tell you,”", "\"말씀드릴게요.\""], ["replied the young girl, “for it is but right you should know.", "처녀가 대답했다. \"당신이 알아 두는 게 마땅하니까요."], ["Well, on the day when your appointment as an officer of the Legion of Honor was announced in the papers, we were all sitting with my grandfather, M.", "그러니까, 당신이 레지옹 도뇌르 훈장의 오피시에로 임명되었다는 소식이 신문에 실린 날이었어요. 우리는 모두 할아버지 누아르티에 씨와 함께 앉아 있었지요. 당글라르 씨도 거기 있었어요."], ["Noirtier; M. Danglars was there also—you recollect M.", "막시밀리앙, 당글라르 씨를 기억하지요? 그 은행가 말이에요."], ["Danglars, do you not, Maximilian, the banker, whose horses ran away with my stepmother and little brother, and very nearly killed them?", "그 사람 말들이 날뛰는 바람에 새어머니와 어린 동생이 거의 목숨을 잃을 뻔했잖아요. 다른 사람들이 당글라르 양의 다가오는 결혼 이야기를 하는 동안, 저는 할아버지께 신문을 읽어 드리고 있었어요."], ["While the rest of the company were discussing the approaching marriage of Mademoiselle Danglars, I was reading the paper to my grandfather; but when I came to the paragraph about you, although I had done nothing else but read it over to myself all the morning (you know you had told me all about it the previous evening), I felt so happy, and yet so nervous, at the idea of speaking your name aloud, and before so many people, that I really think I should have passed it over, but for the fear that my doing so might create suspicions as to the cause of my silence; so I summoned up all my courage, and read it as firmly and as steadily as I could.” 30059m", "그런데 당신에 관한 대목에 이르렀을 때, 저는 아침 내내 그 부분을 혼자 읽고 또 읽기만 했으면서도 (당신이 그 전날 저녁에 다 이야기해 주었던 거 알지요), 그 많은 사람 앞에서 당신 이름을 소리 내어 말한다는 생각에 어찌나 행복하면서도 떨리던지, 정말이지 그 대목을 그냥 건너뛰어 버렸을 거예요. 그러다 제가 입을 다물면 그 까닭을 두고 의심을 살까 두려워서, 저는 온 용기를 그러모아, 할 수 있는 한 또박또박 침착하게 읽었어요.\""]], [["“Dear Valentine!”", "\"사랑하는 발랑틴!\""]], [["“Well, would you believe it?", "\"그런데, 믿어지세요? 아버지는 당신 이름을 듣자마자 무척 황급히 고개를 돌리셨어요."], ["directly my father caught the sound of your name he turned round quite hastily, and, like a poor silly thing, I was so persuaded that everyone must be as much affected as myself by the utterance of your name, that I was not surprised to see my father start, and almost tremble; but I even thought (though that surely must have been a mistake) that M. Danglars trembled too.”", "그리고 저는, 어리석은 바보처럼, 당신 이름을 입에 올리면 다들 저만큼이나 마음이 흔들릴 거라고 굳게 믿고 있었던 터라, 아버지가 흠칫 놀라며 거의 떠는 모습을 보고도 놀라지 않았어요. 하지만 (그건 분명 제 착각이었겠지만) 당글라르 씨까지 떠는 것 같았어요.\""]], [["“‘Morrel, Morrel,’ cried my father, ‘stop a bit;’ then knitting his brows into a deep frown, he added, ‘surely this cannot be one of the Morrel family who lived at Marseilles, and gave us so much trouble from their violent Bonapartism—I mean about the year 1815.’", "\"'모렐, 모렐,' 아버지가 외쳤어요. '잠깐.' 그러더니 미간을 깊이 찌푸리며 덧붙이셨어요. '설마 이게 마르세유에 살면서 그 격렬한 보나파르트주의로 우리에게 그토록 골치를 썩이던 그 모렐 가문 사람은 아니겠지. 1815년 무렵 말이야.'"]], [["“‘Yes,’ replied M.", "\"'네,'"], ["Danglars, ‘I believe he is the son of the old shipowner.’”", "당글라르 씨가 대답했어요. '제 생각엔 그 늙은 선주의 아들인 듯합니다.'\""]], [["“Indeed,”", "\"그래서,\""], ["answered Maximilian; “and what did your father say then, Valentine?”", "막시밀리앙이 대꾸했다. \"그다음 당신 아버지는 뭐라고 하셨소, 발랑틴?\""]], [["“Oh, such a dreadful thing, that I don’t dare to tell you.”", "\"아, 어찌나 끔찍한 말씀이던지, 차마 말씀드리기가 두려워요.\""]], [["“Always tell me everything,”", "\"내겐 늘 무엇이든 다 말해 주시오.\""], ["said Maximilian with a smile.", "막시밀리앙이 미소를 띠며 말했다."]], [["“‘Ah,’ continued my father, still frowning, ‘their idolized emperor treated these madmen as they deserved; he called them ‘food for cannon,’ which was precisely all they were good for; and I am delighted to see that the present government have adopted this salutary principle with all its pristine vigor; if Algiers were good for nothing but to furnish the means of carrying so admirable an idea into practice, it would be an acquisition well worthy of struggling to obtain. Though it certainly does cost France somewhat dear to assert her rights in that uncivilized country.’”", "\"'아,' 아버지가 여전히 얼굴을 찌푸린 채 말을 이으셨어요. '그자들이 떠받들던 황제가 그 미치광이들을 마땅히 받아야 할 대로 다루어 주었지. 그들을 '대포밥'이라 불렀으니, 그게 바로 그자들이 쓸모 있는 전부였어. 그리고 나는 지금 정부가 그 유익한 원칙을 본래의 기개 그대로 받아들인 것을 보니 더없이 기쁘구나. 알제리가 그토록 훌륭한 생각을 실행에 옮길 수단을 대는 것 말고는 아무 쓸모가 없다 해도, 그것만으로도 기를 쓰고 손에 넣을 가치가 충분한 땅이지. 프랑스가 그 미개한 나라에서 제 권리를 주장하는 데 적잖은 비용이 드는 건 사실이다만.'\""]], [["“Brutal politics, I must confess.”", "\"솔직히 말해 야만스러운 정치관이오.\""], ["said Maximilian; “but don’t attach any serious importance, dear, to what your father said.", "막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"하지만 사랑하는 발랑틴, 당신 아버지가 한 말에 너무 깊이 마음 두지 마시오."], ["My father was not a bit behind yours in that sort of talk.", "그런 식의 말이라면 내 아버지도 당신 아버지에 조금도 뒤지지 않았으니까."], ["‘Why,’ said he, ‘does not the emperor, who has devised so many clever and efficient modes of improving the art of war, organize a regiment of lawyers, judges and legal practitioners, sending them in the hottest fire the enemy could maintain, and using them to save better men?’ You see, my dear, that for picturesque expression and generosity of spirit there is not much to choose between the language of either party.", "아버지는 이러셨소. '전쟁 기술을 개선할 영리하고 효율적인 방법을 그토록 많이 고안해 낸 황제께서, 어째서 변호사며 판사며 법률가들로 연대를 하나 꾸려, 적이 퍼부을 수 있는 가장 거센 포화 속으로 들여보내, 그자들을 더 나은 사람들을 살리는 데 써먹지 않으시는 거지?' 보다시피, 사랑하는 발랑틴, 그림처럼 생생한 표현이나 통 큰 기개로 따지자면 어느 당파의 말이든 별반 고를 게 없소."], ["But what did M. Danglars say to this outburst on the part of the procureur?”", "그런데 검사장의 그런 열변에 당글라르 씨는 뭐라 했소?\""]], [["“Oh, he laughed, and in that singular manner so peculiar to himself—half-malicious, half-ferocious; he almost immediately got up and took his leave; then, for the first time, I observed the agitation of my grandfather, and I must tell you, Maximilian, that I am the only person capable of discerning emotion in his paralyzed frame. And I suspected that the conversation that had been carried on in his presence (for they always say and do what they like before the dear old man, without the smallest regard for his feelings) had made a strong impression on his mind; for, naturally enough, it must have pained him to hear the emperor he so devotedly loved and served spoken of in that depreciating manner.”", "\"아, 그 사람은 웃었어요. 그것도 그 사람 특유의 묘한 방식으로요. 반쯤은 악의적이고 반쯤은 사나운 그런 웃음 말이에요. 그러더니 거의 곧바로 일어나 작별을 고했지요. 그제야 처음으로, 저는 할아버지가 동요하시는 걸 알아챘어요. 그리고 막시밀리앙, 말해 두지만, 그 마비된 몸에서 감정을 읽어 낼 수 있는 사람은 저뿐이에요. 저는 그분 앞에서 오간 대화가 (사람들은 늘 그 정다운 노인의 심정 따위는 조금도 헤아리지 않고, 그분 앞에서 하고 싶은 말과 행동을 다 하거든요) 그분 마음에 강한 인상을 남겼다고 짐작했어요. 그도 그럴 것이, 그토록 헌신적으로 사랑하고 섬긴 황제가 그렇게 깎아내려지는 말을 듣는 건 분명 그분께 고통스러운 일이었을 테니까요.\""]], [["“The name of M. Noirtier,”", "\"누아르티에 씨라는 이름은,\" 막시밀리앙이 끼어들었다."], ["interposed Maximilian, “is celebrated throughout Europe; he was a statesman of high standing, and you may or may not know, Valentine, that he took a leading part in every Bonapartist conspiracy set on foot during the restoration of the Bourbons.”", "\"유럽 전역에 널리 알려져 있소. 그분은 높은 명망을 지닌 정치가였소. 그리고 당신이 아는지 모르는지 모르겠지만, 발랑틴, 그분은 부르봉 왕가가 복위한 동안 일어난 모든 보나파르트파 음모에서 주도적인 역할을 하셨소.\""]], [["“Oh, I have often heard whispers of things that seem to me most strange—the father a Bonapartist, the son a Royalist; what can have been the reason of so singular a difference in parties and politics?", "\"아, 저도 더없이 기이하게 들리는 일들을 종종 수군대는 소리로 들었어요. 아버지는 보나파르트파, 아들은 왕당파라니. 당파와 정치 성향이 그토록 유별나게 갈린 까닭이 대체 무엇이었을까요?"], ["But to resume my story; I turned towards my grandfather, as though to question him as to the cause of his emotion; he looked expressively at the newspaper I had been reading.", "그건 그렇고, 다시 제 이야기로 돌아갈게요. 저는 마치 할아버지께 동요하시는 까닭을 여쭙기라도 하려는 듯 그분 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸어요."], ["‘What is the matter, dear grandfather?’ said I, ‘are you pleased?’ He gave me a sign in the affirmative.", "할아버지는 제가 읽고 있던 신문을 의미심장하게 바라보셨어요. '왜 그러세요, 할아버지?' 제가 여쭈었지요."], ["‘With what my father said just now?’ He returned a sign in the negative.", "'기쁘세요?' 할아버지가 그렇다는 신호를 보내셨어요. '방금 아버지가 하신 말씀에요?'"], ["‘Perhaps you liked what M.", "그러자 아니라는 신호를 보내셨어요."], ["Danglars said?’ Another sign in the negative. ‘Oh, then, you were glad to hear that M.", "'그럼 혹시 당글라르 씨가 한 말이 마음에 드셨어요?' 또 아니라는 신호였어요."], ["Morrel (I didn’t dare to say Maximilian) had been made an officer of the Legion of Honor?’ He signified assent; only think of the poor old man’s being so pleased to think that you, who were a perfect stranger to him, had been made an officer of the Legion of Honor!", "'아, 그럼 모렐 씨가 (저는 차마 막시밀리앙이라고는 말하지 못했어요) 레지옹 도뇌르 훈장의 오피시에가 되었다는 소식이 기쁘셨군요?' 할아버지가 그렇다는 뜻을 보이셨어요. 생각해 보세요, 그 가엾은 노인이, 당신처럼 그분께는 생판 남인 사람이 레지옹 도뇌르 훈장의 오피시에가 되었다는 것을 그토록 기뻐하시다니요!"], ["Perhaps it was a mere whim on his part, for he is falling, they say, into second childhood, but I love him for showing so much interest in you.”", "어쩌면 그저 변덕이었는지도 몰라요. 사람들 말로는 그분이 다시 어린아이처럼 되어 가신다니까요. 하지만 저는 그분이 당신에게 그토록 관심을 보여 주신 게 사랑스러워요.\""]], [["“How singular,”", "\"참 묘한 일이오.\""], ["murmured Maximilian; “your father hates me, while your grandfather, on the contrary—What strange feelings are aroused by politics.”", "막시밀리앙이 중얼거렸다. \"당신 아버지는 나를 미워하는데, 반대로 당신 할아버지는…… 정치가 불러일으키는 감정이란 참으로 야릇하구려.\""]], [["“Hush,”", "\"쉿,\""], ["cried Valentine, suddenly; “someone is coming!”", "발랑틴이 별안간 외쳤다. \"누가 와요!\""], ["Maximilian leaped at one bound into his crop of lucern, which he began to pull up in the most ruthless way, under the pretext of being occupied in weeding it.", "막시밀리앙은 한달음에 제 자주개자리 밭으로 뛰어들더니, 잡초를 뽑는 척하며 더없이 사정없이 그것들을 뽑아 대기 시작했다."]], [["“Mademoiselle, mademoiselle!”", "\"아가씨, 아가씨!\""], ["exclaimed a voice from behind the trees.", "나무들 뒤편에서 한 목소리가 외쳤다."], ["“Madame is searching for you everywhere; there is a visitor in the drawing-room.”", "\"마님께서 사방으로 아가씨를 찾고 계세요. 응접실에 손님이 와 계시거든요.\""]], [["“A visitor?”", "\"손님이?\""], ["inquired Valentine, much agitated; “who is it?”", "발랑틴이 몹시 동요하며 물었다. \"누구신데?\""]], [["“Some grand personage—a prince I believe they said—the Count of Monte Cristo.”", "\"어느 대단한 분이세요. 왕자님이라던가 그러던데요. 몬테크리스토 백작이라는 분이세요.\""]], [["“I will come directly,” cried Valentine aloud.", "\"곧 갈게요.\" 발랑틴이 큰 소리로 외쳤다."]], [["The name of Monte Cristo sent an electric shock through the young man on the other side of the iron gate, to whom Valentine’s _“I am coming”_ was the customary signal of farewell.", "몬테크리스토라는 이름은 철문 건너편의 젊은 남자에게 전기 충격 같은 것을 흘려보냈다. 그에게 발랑틴의 \"곧 갈게요\"라는 말은 으레 작별을 알리는 신호였다."]], [["“Now, then,”", "\"자, 그렇다면,\""], ["said Maximilian, leaning on the handle of his spade, “I would give a good deal to know how it comes about that the Count of Monte Cristo is acquainted with M. de Villefort.”", "막시밀리앙이 삽자루에 몸을 기대며 말했다. \"몬테크리스토 백작이 빌포르 씨와 어떻게 아는 사이가 됐는지, 그걸 알아낼 수만 있다면 무엇이든 톡톡히 내놓겠는데.\""]], [["# Chapter 52. Toxicology", "# Chapter 52. Toxicology"]], [["It was really the Count of Monte Cristo who had just arrived at Madame de Villefort’s for the purpose of returning the procureur’s visit, and at his name, as may be easily imagined, the whole house was in confusion.", "바로 그 몬테크리스토 백작이 검사장의 답방을 하기 위해 빌포르 부인의 집에 막 도착한 것이었다. 그리고 쉽게 짐작할 수 있듯이, 그의 이름이 전해지자 온 집안이 술렁였다."]], [["Madame de Villefort, who was alone in her drawing-room when the count was announced, desired that her son might be brought thither instantly to renew his thanks to the count; and Edward, who heard this great personage talked of for two whole days, made all possible haste to come to him, not from obedience to his mother, or out of any feeling of gratitude to the count, but from sheer curiosity, and that some chance remark might give him the opportunity for making one of the impertinent speeches which made his mother say:", "빌포르 검사장의 답방을 위해 빌포르 부인의 집을 막 찾아온 사람은 다름 아닌 몬테크리스토 백작이었다. 능히 짐작할 수 있듯, 그의 이름이 전해지자 온 집안이 술렁였다. 백작의 방문이 알려졌을 때 응접실에 혼자 있던 빌포르 부인은, 백작에게 다시 한번 감사 인사를 올리도록 아들을 당장 데려오게 했다. 에두아르는 이 대단한 인물에 관한 이야기를 꼬박 이틀 동안 들어 온 터라 부리나케 달려왔다. 어머니의 분부를 따라서도 아니고 백작에게 고마운 마음이 있어서도 아니었다. 그저 호기심 때문이었고, 어쩌다 한마디 던질 기회가 생기면 무례한 말을 내뱉어 어머니로 하여금 이렇게 말하게 하려는 속셈이었다."]], [["“Oh, that naughty child!", "\"오, 저런 짓궂은 녀석!"], ["But I can’t be severe with him, he is really _so_ bright.”", "하지만 저 애한테는 엄하게 굴 수가 없어요. 정말 어찌나 영리한지.\""]], [["After the usual civilities, the count inquired after M. de Villefort.", "여느 인사치레가 오간 뒤, 백작은 빌포르 씨의 안부를 물었다."]], [["“My husband dines with the chancellor,”", "\"제 남편은 법무대신과 만찬을 하러 갔답니다.\""], ["replied the young lady; “he has just gone, and I am sure he’ll be exceedingly sorry not to have had the pleasure of seeing you before he went.”", "젊은 부인이 대답했다. \"방금 나갔어요. 떠나기 전에 백작님을 뵙는 기쁨을 누리지 못해 무척 안타까워할 거예요.\""]], [["Two visitors who were there when the count arrived, having gazed at him with all their eyes, retired after that reasonable delay which politeness admits and curiosity requires.", "백작이 도착했을 때 응접실에 있던 두 손님은 눈이 빠져라 그를 바라보더니, 예의가 허락하고 호기심이 요구하는 만큼의 적당한 시간이 흐른 뒤 물러갔다."]], [["“What is your sister Valentine doing?”", "\"네 누나 발랑틴은 뭘 하고 있니?\""], ["inquired Madame de Villefort of Edward; “tell someone to bid her come here, that I may have the honor of introducing her to the count.”", "빌포르 부인이 에두아르에게 물었다. \"누구를 시켜서 이리로 오라고 일러라. 백작님께 소개하는 영광을 누리고 싶구나.\""]], [["“You have a daughter, then, madame?”", "\"그럼 따님이 계신 겁니까, 부인?\""], ["inquired the count; “very young, I presume?”", "백작이 물었다. \"아주 어린 분이겠지요?\""]], [["“The daughter of M. de Villefort by his first marriage,”", "\"빌포르 씨가 첫 결혼에서 얻은 딸이에요.\""], ["replied the young wife, “a fine well-grown girl.”", "젊은 아내가 대답했다. \"다 자란 예쁜 아이지요.\""]], [["“But melancholy,” interrupted Master Edward, snatching the feathers out of the tail of a splendid paroquet that was screaming on its gilded perch, in order to make a plume for his hat.", "\"하지만 우울하죠.\" 에두아르가 끼어들며, 도금한 횃대 위에서 꽥꽥거리는 화려한 앵무새의 꽁지 깃털을 뽑아 제 모자에 꽂을 깃 장식을 만들었다."]], [["Madame de Villefort merely cried, “Be still, Edward!”", "빌포르 부인은 그저 \"가만있어, 에두아르!\" 하고 외쳤다. 그러고는 덧붙였다."], ["She then added, “This young madcap is, however, very nearly right, and merely re-echoes what he has heard me say with pain a hundred times; for Mademoiselle de Villefort is, in spite of all we can do to rouse her, of a melancholy disposition and taciturn habit, which frequently injure the effect of her beauty.", "\"그래도 이 어린 말썽꾸러기가 거의 맞는 말을 했답니다. 제가 가슴 아파하며 백 번도 더 한 말을 그대로 따라 하는 것뿐이에요. 우리가 아무리 기운을 북돋우려 해도, 빌포르 양은 천성이 우울하고 말수가 적어서, 그게 자주 그 아이의 아름다움을 가린답니다."], ["But what detains her? Go, Edward, and see.”", "그런데 왜 안 오는 걸까? 가 봐라, 에두아르, 가서 봐.\""]], [["“Because they are looking for her where she is not to be found.”", "\"누나가 없는 데서 찾고 있으니까 그렇죠.\""]], [["“And where are they looking for her?”", "\"그럼 어디서 찾고 있는데?\""]], [["“With grandpapa Noirtier.”", "\"노르티에 할아버지 방에서요.\""]], [["“And do you think she is not there?”", "\"누나가 거기 없다는 거니?\""]], [["“No, no, no, no, no, she is not there,”", "\"아니, 아니, 아니, 아니, 아니, 없어요.\""], ["replied Edward, singing his words.", "에두아르가 노래하듯 말꼬리를 늘이며 대답했다."]], [["“And where is she, then?", "\"그럼 어디 있니?"], ["If you know, why don’t you tell?”", "알면 왜 말을 안 해?\""]], [["“She is under the big chestnut-tree,”", "\"커다란 밤나무 아래 있어요.\""], ["replied the spoiled brat, as he gave, in spite of his mother’s commands, live flies to the parrot, which seemed keenly to relish such fare.", "응석받이 녀석이 대답하며, 어머니의 명령에도 아랑곳없이 산 파리들을 앵무새에게 주었다. 앵무새는 그런 먹이를 무척 좋아하는 듯했다."]], [["Madame de Villefort stretched out her hand to ring, intending to direct her waiting-maid to the spot where she would find Valentine, when the young lady herself entered the apartment.", "빌포르 부인이 손을 뻗어 종을 울리려 했다. 하녀에게 발랑틴이 있을 곳을 일러 데려오게 할 참이었다. 그때 발랑틴이 직접 응접실로 들어왔다."], ["She appeared much dejected; and any person who considered her attentively might have observed the traces of recent tears in her eyes.", "그녀는 몹시 풀이 죽어 보였다. 그녀를 유심히 살펴보는 사람이라면 누구든 그 눈가에 방금 흘린 눈물 자국이 남아 있는 것을 알아챘을 터였다."]], [["Valentine, whom we have in the rapid march of our narrative presented to our readers without formally introducing her, was a tall and graceful girl of nineteen, with bright chestnut hair, deep blue eyes, and that reposeful air of quiet distinction which characterized her mother.", "발랑틴은 우리가 빠른 서사의 흐름 속에서 정식으로 소개하지 못한 채 독자들에게 내보인 인물이다. 그녀는 키가 크고 우아한 열아홉 살 처녀로, 밝은 밤색 머리에 깊은 푸른 눈을 지녔으며, 어머니에게서 물려받은 차분하고 고요한 기품이 감돌았다."], ["Her white and slender fingers, her pearly neck, her cheeks tinted with varying hues reminded one of the lovely Englishwomen who have been so poetically compared in their manner to the gracefulness of a swan.", "희고 가느다란 손가락, 진주 같은 목덜미, 색조가 변하는 두 뺨은, 그 자태가 백조의 우아함에 곧잘 시적으로 비유되곤 하던 사랑스러운 영국 여인들을 떠올리게 했다."]], [["She entered the apartment, and seeing near her stepmother the stranger of whom she had already heard so much, saluted him without any girlish awkwardness, or even lowering her eyes, and with an elegance that redoubled the count’s attention.", "그녀는 응접실로 들어와, 이미 그토록 많은 이야기를 들은 낯선 손님이 계모 곁에 있는 것을 보고 처녀다운 어색함도 없이, 심지어 눈을 내리깔지도 않은 채 그에게 인사했다. 그 우아함이 백작의 주의를 한층 끌었다."]], [["He rose to return the salutation.", "그는 인사에 답하려고 자리에서 일어섰다."]], [["“Mademoiselle de Villefort, my step-daughter,”", "\"제 의붓딸 빌포르 양이에요.\""], ["said Madame de Villefort to Monte Cristo, leaning back on her sofa and motioning towards Valentine with her hand.", "빌포르 부인이 소파에 등을 기댄 채 손으로 발랑틴 쪽을 가리키며 몬테크리스토에게 말했다."]], [["“And M. de Monte Cristo, King of China, Emperor of Cochin-China,”", "\"그리고 이쪽은 몬테크리스토 백작님, 중국의 왕이자 코친차이나의 황제.\""], ["said the young imp, looking slyly towards his sister.", "어린 악동이 누나 쪽을 슬쩍 곁눈질하며 말했다."]], [["Madame de Villefort at this really did turn pale, and was very nearly angry with this household plague, who answered to the name of Edward; but the count, on the contrary, smiled, and appeared to look at the boy complacently, which caused the maternal heart to bound again with joy and enthusiasm.", "이 말에 빌포르 부인은 정말로 새파랗게 질렸고, 에두아르라는 이름으로 불리는 이 집안의 골칫거리에게 막 화를 낼 뻔했다. 하지만 백작은 도리어 미소를 지으며 만족스러운 눈으로 아이를 바라보는 듯했고, 그 모습에 어머니의 마음은 다시금 기쁨과 감격으로 뛰었다."]], [["“But, madame,”", "\"그런데, 부인.\""], ["replied the count, continuing the conversation, and looking by turns at Madame de Villefort and Valentine, “have I not already had the honor of meeting yourself and mademoiselle before?", "백작이 대화를 이어 가며 빌포르 부인과 발랑틴을 번갈아 바라보고 말했다. \"제가 전에 부인과 따님을 뵙는 영광을 누린 적이 있지 않습니까?"], ["I could not help thinking so just now; the idea came over my mind, and as mademoiselle entered the sight of her was an additional ray of light thrown on a confused remembrance; excuse the remark.”", "조금 전부터 그런 생각이 자꾸 들더군요. 문득 그런 느낌이 떠올랐고, 따님이 들어오시는 순간 그 모습이 어렴풋한 기억 위에 한 줄기 빛을 더 비춰 주었습니다. 무례를 용서하십시오.\""]], [["“I do not think it likely, sir; Mademoiselle de Villefort is not very fond of society, and we very seldom go out,”", "\"그럴 리는 없을 것 같은데요. 빌포르 양은 사교를 그리 좋아하지 않고, 저희도 좀처럼 외출을 하지 않거든요.\""], ["said the young lady.", "젊은 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Then it was not in society that I met with mademoiselle or yourself, madame, or this charming little merry boy.", "\"그렇다면 제가 따님이나 부인을, 또 이 귀엽고 명랑한 꼬마 도련님을 뵌 것은 사교 모임에서는 아니었군요."], ["Besides, the Parisian world is entirely unknown to me, for, as I believe I told you, I have been in Paris but very few days.", "게다가 파리의 사교계는 제게 전혀 낯선 곳입니다. 말씀드렸다시피, 저는 파리에 온 지 며칠 되지 않았으니까요."], ["No,—but, perhaps, you will permit me to call to mind—stay!”", "아니, 하지만, 어쩌면 떠올려 보아도 괜찮으시다면 — 잠깐만요!\""]], [["The Count placed his hand on his brow as if to collect his thoughts.", "백작은 생각을 가다듬으려는 듯 이마에 손을 얹었다."]], [["“No—it was somewhere—away from here—it was—I do not know—but it appears that this recollection is connected with a lovely sky and some religious _fête_; mademoiselle was holding flowers in her hand, the interesting boy was chasing a beautiful peacock in a garden, and you, madame, were under the trellis of some arbor.", "\"아닙니다 — 어딘가였어요 — 여기서 멀리 떨어진 — 그게 — 잘 모르겠지만 — 그 기억은 아름다운 하늘과 어떤 종교 축제와 이어져 있는 것 같습니다. 따님은 손에 꽃을 들고 계셨고, 이 흥미로운 도련님은 정원에서 아름다운 공작새를 쫓고 있었지요. 그리고 부인, 당신은 어느 정자의 격자 시렁 아래 계셨습니다."], ["Pray come to my aid, madame; do not these circumstances appeal to your memory?”", "부디 좀 거들어 주십시오, 부인. 이런 정황들이 기억나지 않으십니까?\""]], [["“No, indeed,”", "\"아니요, 정말로요.\""], ["replied Madame de Villefort; “and yet it appears to me, sir, that if I had met you anywhere, the recollection of you must have been imprinted on my memory.”", "빌포르 부인이 대답했다. \"그런데도 제 생각엔, 만약 어디서든 백작님을 뵈었다면, 그 기억이 제 머릿속에 새겨져 있었을 텐데요.\""]], [["“Perhaps the count saw us in Italy,”", "\"백작님이 이탈리아에서 저희를 보셨는지도 몰라요.\""], ["said Valentine timidly.", "발랑틴이 수줍게 말했다."]], [["“Yes, in Italy; it was in Italy most probably,”", "\"그래요, 이탈리아입니다. 아마도 이탈리아였을 겁니다.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo; “you have travelled then in Italy, mademoiselle?”", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"그럼 따님은 이탈리아를 여행하셨군요?\""]], [["“Yes; madame and I were there two years ago.", "\"네, 어머니와 저는 이 년 전에 그곳에 있었어요."], ["The doctors, anxious for my lungs, had prescribed the air of Naples.", "제 폐를 염려한 의사들이 나폴리의 공기를 권했거든요."], ["We went by Bologna, Perugia, and Rome.”", "저희는 볼로냐와 페루자, 로마를 거쳐 갔어요.\""]], [["“Ah, yes—true, mademoiselle,” exclaimed Monte Cristo as if this simple explanation was sufficient to revive the recollection he sought.", "\"아, 그래요 — 맞습니다, 따님.\" 몬테크리스토가, 마치 이 간단한 설명만으로 찾던 기억이 되살아나기에 충분하다는 듯 외쳤다."], ["“It was at Perugia on Corpus Christi Day, in the garden of the Hôtel des Postes, when chance brought us together; you, Madame de Villefort, and her son; I now remember having had the honor of meeting you.”", "\"성체 축일에 페루자, 우편 호텔 정원에서, 우연이 우리를 만나게 했지요. 부인, 당신과 아드님 말입니다. 이제 당신을 뵙는 영광을 누렸던 게 떠오릅니다.\""]], [["“I perfectly well remember Perugia, sir, and the Hôtel des Postes, and the festival of which you speak,”", "\"페루자는 똑똑히 기억해요. 우편 호텔도, 말씀하신 그 축제도요.\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다."], ["said Madame de Villefort, “but in vain do I tax my memory, of whose treachery I am ashamed, for I really do not recall to mind that I ever had the pleasure of seeing you before.”", "\"하지만 아무리 기억을 더듬어 봐도 — 제 기억력이 이렇게 변변치 못한 게 부끄럽지만 — 전에 백작님을 뵙는 기쁨을 누린 적은 정말이지 떠오르지 않네요.\""]], [["“It is strange, but neither do I recollect meeting with you,”", "\"이상하네요. 저도 백작님을 뵌 기억이 없어요.\""], ["observed Valentine, raising her beautiful eyes to the count. 30065m", "발랑틴이 아름다운 두 눈을 들어 백작을 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“But I remember it perfectly,”", "\"하지만 저는 똑똑히 기억해요.\""], ["interposed the darling Edward.", "사랑스러운 에두아르가 끼어들었다."]], [["“I will assist your memory, madame,”", "\"제가 기억을 도와드리지요, 부인.\""], ["continued the count; “the day had been burning hot; you were waiting for horses, which were delayed in consequence of the festival.", "백작이 말을 이었다. \"그날은 타는 듯이 더웠습니다. 부인은 말을 기다리고 계셨는데, 축제 탓에 말이 늦어지고 있었지요."], ["Mademoiselle was walking in the shade of the garden, and your son disappeared in pursuit of the peacock.”", "따님은 정원 그늘에서 거닐고 있었고, 아드님은 공작새를 쫓아 사라졌습니다.\""]], [["“And I caught it, mamma, don’t you remember?”", "\"그리고 내가 그놈을 잡았잖아요, 엄마, 기억 안 나요?\""], ["interposed Edward, “and I pulled three such beautiful feathers out of his tail.”", "에두아르가 끼어들었다. \"그래서 그 예쁜 깃털을 세 개나 꽁지에서 뽑았다고요.\""]], [["“You, madame, remained under the arbor; do you not remember, that while you were seated on a stone bench, and while, as I told you, Mademoiselle de Villefort and your young son were absent, you conversed for a considerable time with somebody?”", "\"부인, 당신은 정자 아래 남아 계셨습니다. 기억나지 않으십니까? 돌 벤치에 앉아 계셨고, 방금 말씀드린 대로 빌포르 양과 어린 아드님이 자리를 비운 사이, 당신은 누군가와 꽤 오랫동안 이야기를 나누셨지요.\""]], [["“Yes, in truth, yes,”", "\"네, 정말, 그래요.\""], ["answered the young lady, turning very red, “I do remember conversing with a person wrapped in a long woollen mantle; he was a medical man, I think.”", "젊은 부인이 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했다. \"긴 모직 망토를 두른 사람과 이야기했던 게 기억나요. 제 생각엔 의사였던 것 같아요.\""]], [["“Precisely so, madame; this man was myself; for a fortnight I had been at that hotel, during which period I had cured my valet de chambre of a fever, and my landlord of the jaundice, so that I really acquired a reputation as a skilful physician.", "\"바로 그렇습니다, 부인. 그 사람이 저였습니다. 저는 그 호텔에 보름쯤 묵었는데, 그동안 제 시종의 열병을 고쳐 주고 주인의 황달도 낫게 해 주어, 정말이지 솜씨 좋은 의사라는 평판을 얻었지요."], ["We discoursed a long time, madame, on different subjects; of Perugino, of Raphael, of manners, customs, of the famous _aqua Tofana_, of which they had told you, I think you said, that certain individuals in Perugia had preserved the secret.”", "우리는 여러 가지 화제로 오랫동안 이야기를 나누었습니다, 부인. 페루지노며, 라파엘로며, 풍속과 관습이며, 그 유명한 아쿠아 토파나며 말이지요. 부인은, 페루자의 어떤 사람들이 그 비법을 간직하고 있다는 말을 들으셨다고 하셨던 것 같습니다.\""]], [["“Yes, true,”", "\"네, 맞아요.\""], ["replied Madame de Villefort, somewhat uneasily, “I remember now.”", "빌포르 부인이 다소 불안한 기색으로 대답했다. \"이제 기억나네요.\""]], [["“I do not recollect now all the various subjects of which we discoursed, madame,”", "\"우리가 나눈 그 여러 화제를 지금 다 떠올리지는 못합니다, 부인.\""], ["continued the count with perfect calmness; “but I perfectly remember that, falling into the error which others had entertained respecting me, you consulted me as to the health of Mademoiselle de Villefort.”", "백작이 더없이 침착하게 말을 이었다. \"하지만, 남들이 저를 두고 품었던 오해에 부인도 빠지셔서, 빌포르 양의 건강에 관해 제게 상담하셨던 건 똑똑히 기억합니다.\""]], [["“Yes, really, sir, you were in fact a medical man,”", "\"네, 정말, 백작님은 사실상 의사셨던 거죠.\""], ["said Madame de Villefort, “since you had cured the sick.”", "빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"병자들을 고치셨으니까요.\""]], [["“Molière or Beaumarchais would reply to you, madame, that it was precisely because I was not, that I had cured my patients; for myself, I am content to say to you that I have studied chemistry and the natural sciences somewhat deeply, but still only as an amateur, you understand.”", "\"몰리에르나 보마르셰라면 이렇게 대답하겠지요, 부인. 바로 제가 의사가 아니었기에 환자들을 고친 거라고요. 저로서는, 화학과 자연과학을 다소 깊이 공부했다는 정도로 말씀드리는 걸로 만족하겠습니다. 그래도 어디까지나 아마추어로서요, 아시겠지요.\""]], [["At this moment the clock struck six.", "이때 시계가 여섯 시를 알렸다."]], [["“It is six o’clock,”", "\"여섯 시네요.\""], ["said Madame de Villefort, evidently agitated.", "빌포르 부인이 눈에 띄게 동요하며 말했다."], ["“Valentine, will you not go and see if your grandpapa will have his dinner?”", "\"발랑틴, 가서 할아버지가 식사를 하실지 좀 보고 오겠니?\""]], [["Valentine rose, and saluting the count, left the apartment without speaking.", "발랑틴이 일어나 백작에게 인사하고는 아무 말 없이 응접실을 나갔다."]], [["“Oh, madame,”", "\"오, 부인.\""], ["said the count, when Valentine had left the room, “was it on my account that you sent Mademoiselle de Villefort away?”", "발랑틴이 방을 나가자 백작이 말했다. \"혹시 저 때문에 빌포르 양을 내보내신 건가요?\""]], [["“By no means,”", "\"천만에요.\""], ["replied the young lady quickly; “but this is the hour when we usually give M. Noirtier the unwelcome meal that sustains his pitiful existence.", "젊은 부인이 얼른 대답했다. \"지금이 노르티에 씨께 그분의 가련한 목숨을 이어 주는, 달갑지 않은 식사를 드리는 시간이거든요."], ["You are aware, sir, of the deplorable condition of my husband’s father?”", "백작님도 제 시아버님의 딱한 처지를 아시지요?\""]], [["“Yes, madame, M.", "\"네, 부인."], ["de Villefort spoke of it to me—a paralysis, I think.”", "빌포르 씨가 제게 말씀하셨습니다 — 마비, 였던 것 같은데요.\""]], [["“Alas, yes; the poor old gentleman is entirely helpless; the mind alone is still active in this human machine, and that is faint and flickering, like the light of a lamp about to expire.", "\"아, 그래요. 가엾은 노인은 완전히 손쓸 수 없는 상태랍니다. 이 인간이라는 기계에서 이제 정신만이 여전히 살아 있는데, 그마저도 꺼져 가는 등불처럼 희미하게 깜박이지요."], ["But excuse me, sir, for talking of our domestic misfortunes; I interrupted you at the moment when you were telling me that you were a skilful chemist.”", "하지만 집안의 불행을 늘어놓아 죄송해요, 백작님. 백작님이 솜씨 좋은 화학자라고 말씀하시던 참에 제가 끊어 버렸군요.\""]], [["“No, madame, I did not say as much as that,”", "\"아닙니다, 부인. 그렇게까지 말하지는 않았습니다.\""], ["replied the count with a smile; “quite the contrary.", "백작이 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"오히려 그 반대지요."], ["I have studied chemistry because, having determined to live in eastern climates I have been desirous of following the example of King Mithridates.”", "제가 화학을 공부한 건, 동방에서 살기로 마음먹은 뒤 미트리다테스 왕의 본을 따르고 싶었기 때문입니다.\""]], [["“_Mithridates, rex Ponticus_,”", "\"미트리다테스, 폰토스의 왕.\""], ["said the young scamp, as he tore some beautiful portraits out of a splendid album, “the individual who took cream in his cup of poison every morning at breakfast.”", "어린 장난꾸러기가, 멋진 화첩에서 아름다운 초상화 몇 장을 찢어 내며 말했다. \"매일 아침 식사 때 독이 든 잔에 크림을 타서 마셨다는 그 사람이지.\""]], [["“Edward, you naughty boy,”", "\"에두아르, 이 못된 녀석.\" 빌포르 부인이 망가진 책을 개구쟁이의 손에서 낚아채며 외쳤다."], ["exclaimed Madame de Villefort, snatching the mutilated book from the urchin’s grasp, “you are positively past bearing; you really disturb the conversation; go, leave us, and join your sister Valentine in dear grandpapa Noirtier’s room.”", "\"정말이지 더는 못 참겠구나. 너 때문에 대화가 다 끊기잖니. 가, 우리를 두고 가서, 노르티에 할아버지 방에서 누나 발랑틴이랑 같이 있어.\""]], [["“The album,” said Edward sulkily.", "\"화첩.\" 에두아르가 뾰로통하게 말했다."]], [["“What do you mean?—the album!”", "\"무슨 소리야 — 화첩이라니!\""]], [["“I want the album.”", "\"화첩 줘요.\""]], [["“How dare you tear out the drawings?”", "\"감히 그림을 찢어 놓고선?\""]], [["“Oh, it amuses me.”", "\"아, 재밌으니까요.\""]], [["“Go—go at once.”", "\"가 — 당장 가.\""]], [["“I won’t go unless you give me the album,”", "\"화첩 안 주면 안 갈 거예요.\""], ["said the boy, seating himself doggedly in an armchair, according to his habit of never giving way.", "아이가, 한 번도 물러서지 않는 제 버릇대로 고집스레 안락의자에 주저앉으며 말했다."]], [["“Take it, then, and pray disturb us no longer,”", "\"그럼 가져가, 그리고 제발 더는 방해하지 마.\""], ["said Madame de Villefort, giving the album to Edward, who then went towards the door, led by his mother.", "빌포르 부인이 에두아르에게 화첩을 건네며 말했다. 그러자 아이는 어머니의 손에 이끌려 문 쪽으로 갔다."], ["The count followed her with his eyes.", "백작이 눈으로 그녀를 좇았다."]], [["“Let us see if she shuts the door after him,”", "\"저 여자가 아이를 내보낸 뒤 문을 닫는지 보자.\""], ["he muttered.", "그가 중얼거렸다."]], [["Madame de Villefort closed the door carefully after the child, the count appearing not to notice her; then casting a scrutinizing glance around the chamber, the young wife returned to her chair, in which she seated herself.", "빌포르 부인은 아이를 내보낸 뒤 조심스레 문을 닫았다. 백작은 그것을 눈치채지 못한 척했다. 그러고 나서 젊은 아내는 방 안을 살피는 눈길로 한 바퀴 둘러보고는 제 의자로 돌아와 앉았다."]], [["“Allow me to observe, madame,”", "\"한마디 드려도 될까요, 부인.\""], ["said the count, with that kind tone he could assume so well, “you are really very severe with that dear clever child.”", "백작이 그토록 능숙하게 지어낼 줄 아는 그 다정한 어조로 말했다. \"저 사랑스럽고 영리한 아이에게 정말이지 무척 엄하시군요.\""]], [["“Oh, sometimes severity is quite necessary,”", "\"오, 때로는 엄격함도 꼭 필요한 법이지요.\""], ["replied Madame de Villefort, with all a mother’s real firmness.", "빌포르 부인이 어머니다운 진정한 단호함으로 대답했다."]], [["“It was his Cornelius Nepos that Master Edward was repeating when he referred to King Mithridates,”", "\"에두아르 도련님이 미트리다테스 왕을 언급한 건 코르넬리우스 네포스를 외고 있었던 거였지요.\" 백작이 말을 이었다."], ["continued the count, “and you interrupted him in a quotation which proves that his tutor has by no means neglected him, for your son is really advanced for his years.”", "\"그런데 부인이 그 인용을 끊으셨군요. 그건 가정교사가 결코 도련님을 소홀히 가르치지 않았다는 증거입니다. 아드님은 정말 나이에 비해 앞서 있으니까요.\""]], [["“The fact is, count,”", "\"실은요, 백작님.\""], ["answered the mother, agreeably flattered, “he has great aptitude, and learns all that is set before him.", "어머니가 흐뭇하게 으쓱해하며 대답했다. \"저 애는 재주가 아주 뛰어나서, 앞에 놓인 건 뭐든 다 익힌답니다."], ["He has but one fault, he is somewhat wilful; but really, on referring for the moment to what he said, do you truly believe that Mithridates used these precautions, and that these precautions were efficacious?”", "흠이라면 단 하나, 좀 제멋대로라는 거지요. 그런데 정말, 잠깐 저 애가 한 말로 돌아가서요, 미트리다테스가 정말 그런 예방책을 썼고, 그게 효험이 있었다고 보세요?\""]], [["“I think so, madame, because I myself have made use of them, that I might not be poisoned at Naples, at Palermo, and at Smyrna—that is to say, on three several occasions when, but for these precautions, I must have lost my life.”", "\"그렇게 봅니다, 부인. 저 자신이 그 방법을 써 왔으니까요. 나폴리에서, 팔레르모에서, 스미르나에서 독살당하지 않으려고 말입니다 — 다시 말해, 그런 예방책이 없었더라면 목숨을 잃었을 게 분명한 세 차례의 일에서요.\""]], [["“And your precautions were successful?”", "\"그래서 그 예방책이 효험이 있었나요?\""]], [["“Completely so.”", "\"완벽했습니다.\""]], [["“Yes, I remember now your mentioning to me at Perugia something of this sort.”", "\"네, 이제 기억나요. 페루자에서 제게 그 비슷한 이야기를 하셨던 게요.\""]], [["“Indeed?”", "\"그랬습니까?\""], ["said the count with an air of surprise, remarkably well counterfeited; “I really did not remember.”", "백작이, 놀랍도록 그럴듯하게 꾸며 낸 의외라는 표정으로 말했다. \"정말이지 기억이 안 나는군요.\""]], [["“I inquired of you if poisons acted equally, and with the same effect, on men of the North as on men of the South; and you answered me that the cold and sluggish habits of the North did not present the same aptitude as the rich and energetic temperaments of the natives of the South.”", "\"제가 백작님께, 독이 남방 사람들에게 그러듯이 북방 사람들에게도 똑같이, 같은 효력으로 작용하느냐고 여쭈었지요. 그러자 백작님은 차고 굼뜬 북방의 기질은 풍부하고 활기찬 남방 토박이들의 기질만 한 감수성을 보이지 않는다고 답하셨고요.\""]], [["“And that is the case,”", "\"맞는 말입니다.\""], ["observed Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["“I have seen Russians devour, without being visibly inconvenienced, vegetable substances which would infallibly have killed a Neapolitan or an Arab.”", "\"저는 러시아인들이, 나폴리 사람이나 아랍인이라면 틀림없이 죽었을 식물성 물질을 눈에 띄는 탈도 없이 먹어 치우는 걸 본 적이 있습니다.\""]], [["“And you really believe the result would be still more sure with us than in the East, and in the midst of our fogs and rains a man would habituate himself more easily than in a warm latitude to this progressive absorption of poison?”", "\"그럼 그 결과가 동방에서보다 우리 쪽에서 한층 더 확실하리라고 정말 믿으세요? 안개와 비 속에서 사는 사람이 따뜻한 위도에서보다 이렇게 점진적으로 독을 흡수하는 데 더 쉽게 익숙해진다고요?\""]], [["“Certainly; it being at the same time perfectly understood that he should have been duly fortified against the poison to which he had not been accustomed.”", "\"물론입니다. 다만 그가 익숙하지 않던 독에 대해 충분히 단련되어 있어야 한다는 게 전제이지요.\""]], [["“Yes, I understand that; and how would you habituate yourself, for instance, or rather, how did you habituate yourself to it?”", "\"네, 그건 알겠어요. 그럼 어떻게 자신을, 이를테면, 아니, 백작님은 어떻게 그 독에 익숙해지셨나요?\""]], [["“Oh, very easily.", "\"오, 아주 쉽습니다."], ["Suppose you knew beforehand the poison that would be made use of against you; suppose the poison was, for instance, brucine——”", "당신을 해칠 독이 무엇인지 미리 안다고 가정해 봅시다. 그 독이, 이를테면, 브루신이라고 한다면 —\""]], [["“Brucine is extracted from the false angostura8 is it not?”", "\"브루신은 가짜 앙고스투라에서 추출하는 거 아닌가요?\""], ["inquired Madame de Villefort.", "빌포르 부인이 물었다."]], [["“Precisely, madame,”", "\"바로 그렇습니다, 부인.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo; “but I perceive I have not much to teach you.", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"이거 제가 부인께 가르쳐 드릴 게 별로 없겠군요."], ["Allow me to compliment you on your knowledge; such learning is very rare among ladies.”", "부인의 박식함에 경의를 표합니다. 그런 지식은 여성에게선 아주 드무니까요.\""]], [["“Oh, I am aware of that,”", "\"오, 저도 알아요.\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"하지만 저는 신비학에 열정이 있어서요."], ["said Madame de Villefort; “but I have a passion for the occult sciences, which speak to the imagination like poetry, and are reducible to figures, like an algebraic equation; but go on, I beg of you; what you say interests me to the greatest degree.”", "그건 시처럼 상상력에 말을 걸고, 대수 방정식처럼 숫자로 환원되거든요. 그런데 계속해 주세요, 부디. 백작님 말씀이 더없이 흥미로우니까요.\""]], [["30069m\n“Well,”", "\"좋습니다.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo “suppose, then, that this poison was brucine, and you were to take a milligramme the first day, two milligrammes the second day, and so on.", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"그럼 이 독이 브루신이라고 하고, 부인이 첫날에는 1밀리그램, 둘째 날에는 2밀리그램, 이런 식으로 복용한다고 합시다."], ["Well, at the end of ten days you would have taken a centigramme, at the end of twenty days, increasing another milligramme, you would have taken three hundred centigrammes; that is to say, a dose which you would support without inconvenience, and which would be very dangerous for any other person who had not taken the same precautions as yourself.", "자, 열흘이 지나면 1센티그램을 복용한 셈이 되고, 스무 날이 지나면 매번 1밀리그램씩 늘려 300센티그램을 복용하게 됩니다. 다시 말해, 부인은 아무 탈 없이 견디지만, 부인과 같은 예방책을 쓰지 않은 다른 사람에게는 몹시 위험할 분량이지요."], ["Well, then, at the end of a month, when drinking water from the same carafe, you would kill the person who drank with you, without your perceiving, otherwise than from slight inconvenience, that there was any poisonous substance mingled with this water.”", "그렇게 한 달이 지나면, 같은 물병의 물을 마실 때, 부인은 함께 마신 사람을 죽이게 됩니다. 그런데도 부인 자신은, 가벼운 불편함 말고는, 그 물에 독성 물질이 섞여 있었다는 걸 느끼지도 못하는 거지요.\""]], [["“Do you know any other counter-poisons?”", "\"다른 해독제도 알고 계세요?\""]], [["“I do not.”", "\"모릅니다.\""]], [["“I have often read, and read again, the history of Mithridates,”", "\"미트리다테스 이야기를 여러 번 읽고 또 읽었어요.\""], ["said Madame de Villefort in a tone of reflection, “and had always considered it a fable.”", "빌포르 부인이 곰곰이 생각에 잠긴 어조로 말했다. \"그러면서도 늘 꾸며 낸 이야기라고만 여겼지요.\""]], [["“No, madame, contrary to most history, it is true; but what you tell me, madame, what you inquire of me, is not the result of a chance query, for two years ago you asked me the same questions, and said then, that for a very long time this history of Mithridates had occupied your mind.”", "\"아닙니다, 부인. 대부분의 역사와 달리, 그건 사실입니다. 그런데 부인이 제게 하시는 말씀, 제게 물으시는 것은, 우연한 물음에서 나온 게 아니로군요. 이 년 전에도 부인은 제게 똑같은 질문을 하셨고, 그때 이 미트리다테스 이야기가 아주 오랫동안 머릿속을 떠나지 않았다고 하셨으니까요.\""]], [["“True, sir.", "\"맞아요, 백작님."], ["The two favorite studies of my youth were botany and mineralogy, and subsequently, when I learned that the use of simples frequently explained the whole history of a people, and the entire life of individuals in the East, as flowers betoken and symbolize a love affair, I have regretted that I was not a man, that I might have been a Flamel, a Fontana, or a Cabanis.”", "젊은 시절 제가 가장 좋아한 두 가지 공부가 식물학과 광물학이었어요. 그 뒤에 약초의 쓰임이 동방에서 한 민족의 역사 전체를, 또 개인들의 삶 전체를 자주 풀이해 준다는 걸, 마치 꽃이 연애 사건을 알리고 상징하듯이 말이에요, 그걸 알고 나서는, 제가 남자로 태어나지 못한 게 한스러웠답니다. 그랬다면 플라멜이나 폰타나, 혹은 카바니스 같은 사람이 될 수도 있었을 텐데요.\""]], [["“And the more, madame,”", "\"더욱이, 부인.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “as the Orientals do not confine themselves, as did Mithridates, to make a cuirass of his poisons, but they also made them a dagger.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"동방 사람들은 미트리다테스처럼 독으로 갑옷을 만드는 데 그치지 않고, 그것으로 단검까지 만들었으니까요."], ["Science becomes, in their hands, not only a defensive weapon, but still more frequently an offensive one; the one serves against all their physical sufferings, the other against all their enemies.", "그들의 손에서 학문은 방어 무기일 뿐 아니라, 그보다 더 자주 공격 무기가 됩니다. 하나는 온갖 육체적 고통에 맞서 쓰이고, 다른 하나는 모든 적에 맞서 쓰이지요."], ["With opium, belladonna, brucea, snake-wood, and the cherry-laurel, they put to sleep all who stand in their way.", "아편과 벨라도나, 브루세아, 사목, 그리고 월계수벚나무로 그들은 길을 막는 자는 누구든 잠재웁니다."], ["There is not one of those women, Egyptian, Turkish, or Greek, whom here you call ‘good women,’ who do not know how, by means of chemistry, to stupefy a doctor, and in psychology to amaze a confessor.”", "이집트 여인이든 터키 여인이든 그리스 여인이든, 여기서 '착한 여인들'이라 부르는 그들 가운데, 화학으로 의사를 멍하게 만들고 심리술로 고해 신부를 경악하게 할 줄 모르는 사람은 하나도 없답니다.\""]], [["“Really,” said Madame de Villefort, whose eyes sparkled with strange fire at this conversation.", "\"정말요.\" 이 대화에 두 눈이 묘한 불꽃으로 반짝이며 빌포르 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Oh, yes, indeed, madame,”", "\"오, 그럼요, 정말입니다, 부인.\""], ["continued Monte Cristo, “the secret dramas of the East begin with a love philtre and end with a death potion—begin with paradise and end with—hell.", "몬테크리스토가 말을 이었다. \"동방의 은밀한 드라마는 사랑의 미약으로 시작해 죽음의 묘약으로 끝나지요 — 천국에서 시작해 — 지옥에서 끝나는 겁니다."], ["There are as many elixirs of every kind as there are caprices and peculiarities in the physical and moral nature of humanity; and I will say further—the art of these chemists is capable with the utmost precision to accommodate and proportion the remedy and the bane to yearnings for love or desires for vengeance.”", "인간의 육체적, 정신적 본성에 변덕과 별난 점이 있는 만큼이나, 온갖 종류의 영약이 존재합니다. 한 가지 더 말씀드리자면 — 그 화학자들의 기술은 더없이 정밀하게, 약과 독을 사랑의 갈망이나 복수의 욕망에 맞게 조절하고 배합할 수 있지요.\""]], [["“But, sir,”", "\"하지만, 백작님.\""], ["remarked the young woman, “these Eastern societies, in the midst of which you have passed a portion of your existence, are as fantastic as the tales that come from their strange land.", "젊은 부인이 말했다. \"백작님이 생애의 한 시절을 보내신 그 동방 사회는, 그 낯선 땅에서 전해 오는 이야기들만큼이나 환상적이군요."], ["A man can easily be put out of the way there, then; it is, indeed, the Bagdad and Bassora of the _Thousand and One Nights_.", "그럼 거기서는 사람 하나쯤 쉽게 치워 버릴 수 있겠어요. 정말이지 「천일야화」에 나오는 바그다드와 바스라로군요."], ["The sultans and viziers who rule over society there, and who constitute what in France we call the government, are really Haroun-al-Raschids and Giaffars, who not only pardon a poisoner, but even make him a prime minister, if his crime has been an ingenious one, and who, under such circumstances, have the whole story written in letters of gold, to divert their hours of idleness and _ennui_.”", "그곳 사회를 다스리는, 프랑스에서 우리가 정부라고 부르는 것을 이루는 술탄과 재상들은, 실은 하룬 알 라시드나 자파르 같은 자들이어서, 독살자를 용서할 뿐 아니라, 그 범죄가 교묘했다면 그를 재상으로 삼기까지 하고, 그런 경우에는 한가하고 권태로운 시간을 달래려고 그 이야기를 통째로 금박 글씨로 적게 한다는 거지요.\""]], [["“By no means, madame; the fanciful exists no longer in the East.", "\"천만에요, 부인. 동방에 이제 그런 환상 같은 건 더 없습니다."], ["There, disguised under other names, and concealed under other costumes, are police agents, magistrates, attorneys-general, and bailiffs.", "그곳에도, 다른 이름으로 위장하고 다른 복색으로 감춘 경찰관과 치안판사, 검사장, 집행관들이 있지요."], ["They hang, behead, and impale their criminals in the most agreeable possible manner; but some of these, like clever rogues, have contrived to escape human justice, and succeed in their fraudulent enterprises by cunning stratagems.", "그들은 더없이 보기 좋은 방식으로 죄인을 목매달고, 목 베고, 꼬챙이에 꿰지요. 다만 그중 몇몇은, 영리한 악당답게, 인간의 정의를 빠져나갈 궁리를 해내고, 교활한 술책으로 사기 행각에 성공한답니다."], ["Amongst us a simpleton, possessed by the demon of hate or cupidity, who has an enemy to destroy, or some near relation to dispose of, goes straight to the grocer’s or druggist’s, gives a false name, which leads more easily to his detection than his real one, and under the pretext that the rats prevent him from sleeping, purchases five or six grammes of arsenic—if he is really a cunning fellow, he goes to five or six different druggists or grocers, and thereby becomes only five or six times more easily traced;—then, when he has acquired his specific, he administers duly to his enemy, or near kinsman, a dose of arsenic which would make a mammoth or mastodon burst, and which, without rhyme or reason, makes his victim utter groans which alarm the entire neighborhood.", "우리 쪽에서는, 증오나 탐욕의 악마에 사로잡힌 어리석은 자가, 없애 버릴 원수나 처치할 가까운 친척이 있으면, 곧장 식료품상이나 약종상에게 가서, 진짜 이름보다 발각되기 더 쉬운 가짜 이름을 대고는, 쥐 때문에 잠을 못 잔다는 핑계로 비소 대여섯 그램을 삽니다 — 정말로 교활한 작자라면, 식료품상이나 약종상 대여섯 군데를 돌지요. 그래서 추적당하기가 그저 대여섯 배 더 쉬워질 뿐입니다. 그런 다음, 그 약을 손에 넣으면, 원수나 가까운 친척에게 매머드나 마스토돈도 터져 죽을 만큼의 비소를 정성껏 먹이고, 그러면 까닭도 영문도 없이 그 희생자가 온 동네를 놀라게 하는 신음을 토해 내지요."], ["Then arrive a crowd of policemen and constables.", "그러면 경찰관과 순경들이 떼로 몰려옵니다."], ["They fetch a doctor, who opens the dead body, and collects from the entrails and stomach a quantity of arsenic in a spoon.", "의사를 불러오고, 의사는 시신을 갈라, 내장과 위에서 비소를 한 숟갈 그득 긁어냅니다."], ["Next day a hundred newspapers relate the fact, with the names of the victim and the murderer.", "이튿날 신문 백 군데가, 희생자와 살인자의 이름까지 들어 그 사실을 보도하지요."], ["The same evening the grocer or grocers, druggist or druggists, come and say, ‘It was I who sold the arsenic to the gentleman;’ and rather than not recognize the guilty purchaser, they will recognize twenty.", "그날 저녁이면 그 식료품상이나 약종상들이 와서 말합니다. '그 신사분에게 비소를 판 게 바로 접니다.' 그러고는 죄지은 손님을 못 알아보느니, 차라리 스무 명이라도 알아보겠다고 나서지요."], ["Then the foolish criminal is taken, imprisoned, interrogated, confronted, confounded, condemned, and cut off by hemp or steel; or if she be a woman of any consideration, they lock her up for life.", "그래서 그 어리석은 범인은 붙잡혀 투옥되고, 심문당하고, 대질되고, 궁지에 몰리고, 유죄를 선고받고, 삼이나 쇠로 목숨을 끊깁니다. 혹은 어느 정도 신분 있는 여인이라면, 평생 가둬 두지요."], ["This is the way in which you Northerns understand chemistry, madame.", "부인, 이게 바로 북방 사람들이 화학을 이해하는 방식입니다."], ["Desrues was, however, I must confess, more skilful.”", "그래도 데뤼는, 솔직히 말씀드려, 좀 더 솜씨가 좋긴 했지요.\""]], [["“What would you have, sir?”", "\"어쩌겠어요, 백작님?\""], ["said the lady, laughing; “we do what we can.", "부인이 웃으며 말했다. \"우리야 할 수 있는 걸 할 뿐이죠."], ["All the world has not the secret of the Medicis or the Borgias.”", "세상 모두가 메디치나 보르자 가문의 비법을 아는 건 아니니까요.\""]], [["“Now,” replied the count, shrugging his shoulders, “shall I tell you the cause of all these stupidities?", "\"자, 이제.\" 백작이 어깨를 으쓱하며 대답했다."], ["It is because, at your theatres, by what at least I could judge by reading the pieces they play, they see persons swallow the contents of a phial, or suck the button of a ring, and fall dead instantly.", "\"이 모든 어리석은 짓의 원인을 말씀드릴까요? 그건 부인들이 가는 극장에서, 적어도 상연되는 작품들을 읽어 본 제가 판단하기로는, 사람들이 약병의 내용물을 삼키거나 반지의 단추를 빨고는 그 자리에서 즉사하는 걸 보기 때문입니다. 오 분 뒤면 막이 내리고, 관객들은 떠나지요."], ["Five minutes afterwards the curtain falls, and the spectators depart.", "그들은 살인의 뒷일을 모릅니다. 직무 휘장을 단 경찰서장도, 부하 넷을 거느린 하사도 보지 못하지요."], ["They are ignorant of the consequences of the murder; they see neither the police commissary with his badge of office, nor the corporal with his four men; and so the poor fools believe that the whole thing is as easy as lying.", "그래서 그 가엾은 바보들은 그 모든 일이 거짓말처럼 쉬운 거라고 믿습니다. 하지만 프랑스에서 좀 벗어나 보세요 — 알레포나 카이로로 가든, 그저 나폴리나 로마로만 가도 — 거리에서 당신 곁을 스쳐 가는 사람들이 보일 겁니다. 꼿꼿이 서서, 미소 띠고, 혈색 좋은 사람들 말이지요."], ["But go a little way from France—go either to Aleppo or Cairo, or only to Naples or Rome, and you will see people passing by you in the streets—people erect, smiling, and fresh-colored, of whom Asmodeus, if you were holding on by the skirt of his mantle, would say, ‘That man was poisoned three weeks ago; he will be a dead man in a month.’”", "만약 당신이 아스모데우스의 망토 자락을 붙들고 있다면, 그가 이렇게 말할 사람들입니다. '저자는 삼 주 전에 독을 먹었지. 한 달 안에 죽은 목숨이오.'\""]], [["“Then,”", "\"그럼.\""], ["remarked Madame de Villefort, “they have again discovered the secret of the famous _aqua Tofana_ that they said was lost at Perugia.”", "빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"페루자에서 사라졌다던 그 유명한 아쿠아 토파나의 비법을 사람들이 다시 알아낸 거로군요.\""]], [["“Ah, but madame, does mankind ever lose anything?", "\"아, 하지만 부인, 인류가 무언가를 영영 잃는 법이 있던가요? 기술이란 자리를 바꿔 가며 세상을 한 바퀴 돌지요. 사물은 다른 이름을 얻고, 보통 사람들은 그걸 좇지 못하는 겁니다 — 그게 전부예요."], ["The arts change about and make a tour of the world; things take a different name, and the vulgar do not follow them—that is all; but there is always the same result.", "하지만 결과는 언제나 똑같지요. 독은 특히 이런저런 기관에 작용합니다 — 어떤 건 위에, 어떤 건 뇌에, 어떤 건 창자에요. 자, 독이 기침을 일으키고, 기침이 폐렴이나 학문의 책에 분류된 다른 병을 일으키지요."], ["Poisons act particularly on some organ or another—one on the stomach, another on the brain, another on the intestines.", "그렇다고 그게 결코 치명적이지 않은 게 되는 건 아닙니다. 또 설사 치명적이지 않더라도, 어리석은 의사들이 — 대개 형편없는 화학자이지요 — 쓰는 치료약 덕분에 틀림없이 치명적인 게 됩니다. 그 약은 부인 뜻대로, 병에 유리하게도 불리하게도 작용하지요."], ["Well, the poison brings on a cough, the cough an inflammation of the lungs, or some other complaint catalogued in the book of science, which, however, by no means precludes it from being decidedly mortal; and if it were not, would be sure to become so, thanks to the remedies applied by foolish doctors, who are generally bad chemists, and which will act in favor of or against the malady, as you please; and then there is a human being killed according to all the rules of art and skill, and of whom justice learns nothing, as was said by a terrible chemist of my acquaintance, the worthy Abbé Adelmonte of Taormina, in Sicily, who has studied these national phenomena very profoundly.”", "그렇게 해서 한 인간이 온갖 기술과 솜씨의 규칙에 따라 죽임을 당하는데, 정의는 그것에 대해 아무것도 알아내지 못합니다. 제가 아는 어느 무시무시한 화학자가 한 말이지요. 시칠리아 타오르미나의 훌륭한 아델몬테 신부인데, 이 민족 특유의 현상들을 아주 깊이 연구한 분입니다.\""]], [["“It is quite frightful, but deeply interesting,”", "\"정말 끔찍하지만, 더없이 흥미롭네요.\""], ["said the young lady, motionless with attention.", "젊은 부인이 넋을 잃고 꼼짝 않은 채 말했다."], ["“I thought, I must confess, that these tales, were inventions of the Middle Ages.”", "\"솔직히 저는 그런 이야기들이 중세의 꾸며 낸 것인 줄로만 알았어요.\""]], [["“Yes, no doubt, but improved upon by ours.", "\"네, 물론입니다. 하지만 우리 시대가 그걸 더 개량했지요."], ["What is the use of time, rewards of merit, medals, crosses, Monthyon prizes, if they do not lead society towards more complete perfection?", "사회를 더 완전한 경지로 이끌지 못한다면, 시간이며 공로 표창이며 메달이며 십자 훈장이며 몽티옹 상이 다 무슨 소용이겠습니까?"], ["Yet man will never be perfect until he learns to create and destroy; he does know how to destroy, and that is half the battle.”", "그렇지만 인간은 창조하고 파괴하는 법을 배우기 전에는 결코 완전해질 수 없을 겁니다. 인간은 파괴하는 법은 알지요. 그러니 절반은 해낸 셈입니다.\""]], [["“So,”", "\"그럼.\""], ["added Madame de Villefort, constantly returning to her object, “the poisons of the Borgias, the Medicis, the Renées, the Ruggieris, and later, probably, that of Baron de Trenck, whose story has been so misused by modern drama and romance——”", "빌포르 부인이, 줄곧 제 관심사로 되돌아오며 덧붙였다. \"보르자며 메디치며 르네며 루지에리 가문의 독, 그리고 그 뒤로는, 아마도 현대 연극과 소설이 그토록 함부로 써먹은 트렁크 남작의 독까지 —\""]], [["“Were objects of art, madame, and nothing more,”", "\"그건 예술 작품이었을 뿐입니다, 부인."], ["replied the count.", "그 이상은 아니에요.\""], ["“Do you suppose that the real _savant_ addresses himself stupidly to the mere individual? By no means.", "백작이 대답했다. \"진정한 학자가 한낱 개인 따위를 어리석게 상대하리라 보십니까? 천만에요."], ["Science loves eccentricities, leaps and bounds, trials of strength, fancies, if I may be allowed so to term them.", "학문은 별난 것, 도약과 비약, 힘의 시험, 환상 — 그렇게 불러도 된다면요 — 을 사랑합니다."], ["Thus, for instance, the excellent Abbé Adelmonte, of whom I spoke just now, made in this way some marvellous experiments.”", "그러니, 이를테면, 방금 말씀드린 그 훌륭한 아델몬테 신부는 이런 식으로 몇 가지 놀라운 실험을 했지요.\""]], [["“Really?”", "\"정말요?\""]], [["“Yes; I will mention one to you.", "\"네. 한 가지 말씀드리지요."], ["He had a remarkably fine garden, full of vegetables, flowers, and fruit.", "그분은 채소며 꽃이며 과일이 가득한, 유난히 멋진 정원을 가지고 있었습니다."], ["From amongst these vegetables he selected the most simple—a cabbage, for instance.", "그 채소들 가운데서 가장 평범한 것 — 이를테면 양배추를 골랐지요."], ["For three days he watered this cabbage with a distillation of arsenic; on the third, the cabbage began to droop and turn yellow.", "사흘 동안 그 양배추에 비소 증류액을 뿌렸습니다. 사흘째가 되자 양배추는 시들며 누렇게 변하기 시작했지요."], ["At that moment he cut it. In the eyes of everybody it seemed fit for table, and preserved its wholesome appearance.", "바로 그 순간 그분은 그걸 베었습니다. 누가 봐도 식탁에 올릴 만했고, 멀쩡해 보이는 모습 그대로였지요."], ["It was only poisoned to the Abbé Adelmonte.", "오직 아델몬테 신부에게만 그건 독이 든 양배추였습니다."], ["He then took the cabbage to the room where he had rabbits—for the Abbé Adelmonte had a collection of rabbits, cats, and guinea-pigs, fully as fine as his collection of vegetables, flowers, and fruit.", "그러고 나서 그분은 그 양배추를 토끼가 있는 방으로 가져갔지요 — 아델몬테 신부는 채소, 꽃, 과일 못지않게 훌륭한 토끼와 고양이, 기니피그 수집품을 가지고 있었으니까요."], ["Well, the Abbé Adelmonte took a rabbit, and made it eat a leaf of the cabbage.", "자, 아델몬테 신부는 토끼 한 마리를 잡아 그 양배추 잎을 먹였습니다."], ["The rabbit died.", "토끼는 죽었지요."], ["What magistrate would find, or even venture to insinuate, anything against this? What procureur has ever ventured to draw up an accusation against M.", "어느 치안판사가 이걸 두고 무얼 찾아내겠으며, 감히 무얼 넌지시 비추기라도 하겠습니까?"], ["Magendie or M. Flourens, in consequence of the rabbits, cats, and guinea-pigs they have killed?—not one.", "어떤 검사장이, 마장디 씨나 플루랑스 씨가 죽인 토끼며 고양이며 기니피그를 이유로 감히 그들을 고발한 적이 있던가요? 단 한 사람도 없습니다."], ["So, then, the rabbit dies, and justice takes no notice.", "그러니 토끼가 죽어도 정의는 거들떠보지 않지요."], ["This rabbit dead, the Abbé Adelmonte has its entrails taken out by his cook and thrown on the dunghill; on this dunghill is a hen, who, pecking these intestines, is in her turn taken ill, and dies next day.", "토끼가 죽으면, 아델몬테 신부는 요리사에게 그 내장을 끄집어내 거름 더미에 던지게 합니다. 그 거름 더미에는 암탉이 한 마리 있는데, 그 창자를 쪼아 먹고는 이번엔 그 암탉이 병들어 이튿날 죽지요."], ["At the moment when she is struggling in the convulsions of death, a vulture is flying by (there are a good many vultures in Adelmonte’s country); this bird darts on the dead fowl, and carries it away to a rock, where it dines off its prey.", "암탉이 죽음의 경련에 몸부림치는 바로 그 순간, 독수리 한 마리가 날아갑니다 — 아델몬테의 고장에는 독수리가 꽤 많거든요. 이 새는 죽은 닭에게 달려들어 바위로 물고 가, 그 먹이로 식사를 하지요."], ["Three days afterwards, this poor vulture, which has been very much indisposed since that dinner, suddenly feels very giddy while flying aloft in the clouds, and falls heavily into a fish-pond. The pike, eels, and carp eat greedily always, as everybody knows—well, they feast on the vulture.", "사흘 뒤, 그 식사 이후 줄곧 몹시 시름시름 앓던 이 가엾은 독수리는, 구름 높이 날다가 갑자기 어지럼을 느끼고 못으로 무겁게 떨어집니다. 강꼬치며 뱀장어며 잉어는, 누구나 알다시피, 늘 게걸스레 먹어 치우지요 — 자, 그것들이 독수리를 포식합니다."], ["Now suppose that next day, one of these eels, or pike, or carp, poisoned at the fourth remove, is served up at your table.", "그런데 이튿날, 네 단계나 건너 독에 물든 그 뱀장어나 강꼬치, 잉어 한 마리가 부인의 식탁에 오른다고 합시다."], ["Well, then, your guest will be poisoned at the fifth remove, and die, at the end of eight or ten days, of pains in the intestines, sickness, or abscess of the pylorus.", "그러면 부인의 손님은 다섯 단계째 독에 중독되어, 여드레나 열흘 뒤에 창자 통증이며 구토며 유문 농양으로 죽지요."], ["The doctors open the body and say with an air of profound learning, ‘The subject has died of a tumor on the liver, or of typhoid fever!’”", "의사들은 시신을 갈라 보고는 더없이 박식한 척 말합니다. '이 사람은 간 종양으로, 혹은 장티푸스로 죽었군요!'\""]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만.\""], ["remarked Madame de Villefort, “all these circumstances which you link thus to one another may be broken by the least accident; the vulture may not see the fowl, or may fall a hundred yards from the fish-pond.”", "빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"백작님이 그렇게 하나하나 이어 붙이신 그 모든 정황은, 사소한 우연 하나로도 깨질 수 있잖아요. 독수리가 닭을 못 볼 수도 있고, 못에서 백 미터쯤 떨어진 데 떨어질 수도 있고요.\""]], [["“Ah, that is where the art comes in.", "\"아, 바로 거기에 기술이 들어가는 겁니다."], ["To be a great chemist in the East, one must direct chance; and this is to be achieved.”", "동방에서 위대한 화학자가 되려면, 우연을 마음대로 다스려야 하지요. 그리고 그건 이뤄 낼 수 있는 일입니다.\""]], [["Madame de Villefort was in deep thought, yet listened attentively.", "빌포르 부인은 깊은 생각에 잠겼으면서도 귀를 기울여 듣고 있었다."]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만.\""], ["she exclaimed, suddenly, “arsenic is indelible, indestructible; in whatsoever way it is absorbed, it will be found again in the body of the victim from the moment when it has been taken in sufficient quantity to cause death.”", "그녀가 불쑥 외쳤다. \"비소는 지워지지도 파괴되지도 않잖아요. 어떤 식으로 흡수되든, 죽음을 일으킬 만큼 충분히 섭취된 순간부터는, 희생자의 몸에서 다시 발견되고요.\""]], [["“Precisely so,”", "\"바로 그렇습니다.\""], ["cried Monte Cristo—“precisely so; and this is what I said to my worthy Adelmonte.", "몬테크리스토가 외쳤다. \"바로 그래요. 저도 그 점을 제 훌륭한 아델몬테에게 말했지요."], ["He reflected, smiled, and replied to me by a Sicilian proverb, which I believe is also a French proverb, ‘My son, the world was not made in a day—but in seven.", "그분은 곰곰이 생각하더니, 미소를 지으며 시칠리아 속담으로 답하더군요. 제 생각엔 프랑스 속담이기도 한 것 같은데요. '얘야, 세상은 하루 만에 만들어진 게 아니라 — 이레에 걸쳐 만들어졌단다."], ["Return on Sunday.’ On the Sunday following I did return to him.", "일요일에 다시 오너라.' 그다음 일요일에 저는 그분께 다시 갔지요."], ["Instead of having watered his cabbage with arsenic, he had watered it this time with a solution of salts, having their basis in strychnine, _strychnos colubrina_, as the learned term it.", "이번엔 양배추에 비소를 뿌리는 대신, 학자들이 스트리크노스 콜루브리나라 부르는, 스트리크닌을 기본으로 한 염류 용액을 뿌려 두었더군요."], ["Now, the cabbage had not the slightest appearance of disease in the world, and the rabbit had not the smallest distrust; yet, five minutes afterwards, the rabbit was dead.", "자, 그 양배추는 세상 어디에도 병든 기색이라곤 없었고, 토끼도 조금도 의심하지 않았습니다. 그런데도 오 분 뒤에 토끼는 죽었지요."], ["The fowl pecked at the rabbit, and the next day was a dead hen.", "닭이 그 토끼를 쪼아 먹었고, 이튿날엔 죽은 암탉이 되었습니다."], ["This time we were the vultures; so we opened the bird, and this time all special symptoms had disappeared, there were only general symptoms.", "이번엔 우리가 독수리였지요. 그래서 우리는 그 새를 갈라 보았는데, 이번엔 특별한 증상이라곤 다 사라지고 일반적인 증상밖에 없더군요."], ["There was no peculiar indication in any organ—an excitement of the nervous system—that was it; a case of cerebral congestion—nothing more.", "어느 기관에도 특이한 징후는 없었습니다 — 신경계의 흥분 — 그게 다였지요. 뇌충혈 사례 — 그뿐이었습니다."], ["The fowl had not been poisoned—she had died of apoplexy.", "닭은 독에 죽은 게 아니었어요 — 졸중으로 죽은 거였지요."], ["Apoplexy is a rare disease among fowls, I believe, but very common among men.”", "졸중은 닭에게는 드문 병이라고 알고 있습니다만, 사람에게는 아주 흔하지요.\""]], [["Madame de Villefort appeared more and more thoughtful.", "빌포르 부인은 점점 더 깊은 생각에 잠긴 듯 보였다."]], [["“It is very fortunate,”", "\"참 다행이에요.\""], ["she observed, “that such substances could only be prepared by chemists; otherwise, all the world would be poisoning each other.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"그런 물질들은 화학자만이 만들 수 있다는 게요. 안 그랬다면 온 세상이 서로 독살하고 있었을 테니까요.\""]], [["“By chemists and persons who have a taste for chemistry,”", "\"화학자와, 화학에 취미가 있는 사람들이지요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo carelessly.", "몬테크리스토가 무심히 말했다."]], [["“And then,”", "\"그리고요.\""], ["said Madame de Villefort, endeavoring by a struggle, and with effort, to get away from her thoughts, “however skilfully it is prepared, crime is always crime, and if it avoid human scrutiny, it does not escape the eye of God.", "빌포르 부인이, 안간힘을 쓰고 애를 써서 제 생각에서 벗어나려 하며 말했다. \"아무리 솜씨 좋게 만든다 해도, 범죄는 늘 범죄이고, 인간의 눈은 피한다 해도 신의 눈은 벗어나지 못하지요."], ["The Orientals are stronger than we are in cases of conscience, and, very prudently, have no hell—that is the point.”", "동방 사람들은 양심 문제에서 우리보다 강해서, 아주 신중하게도, 지옥이라는 걸 두지 않아요 — 바로 그 점이에요.\""]], [["30075m\n“Really, madame, this is a scruple which naturally must occur to a pure mind like yours, but which would easily yield before sound reasoning.", "\"정말이지, 부인, 그건 부인처럼 순수한 마음에는 당연히 떠오를 망설임이지요. 하지만 건전한 추론 앞에서는 쉽사리 수그러들 겁니다."], ["The bad side of human thought will always be defined by the paradox of Jean Jacques Rousseau,—you remember,—the mandarin who is killed five hundred leagues off by raising the tip of the finger.", "인간 사고의 나쁜 면은 늘 장 자크 루소의 역설로 정의되지요 — 기억나시지요 — 손가락 끝을 까딱하는 것만으로 오백 리 밖에서 죽임을 당하는 그 만다린 말입니다."], ["Man’s whole life passes in doing these things, and his intellect is exhausted by reflecting on them.", "인간의 일생은 그런 짓을 하며 흘러가고, 그 지성은 그런 것을 곱씹느라 소진되지요."], ["You will find very few persons who will go and brutally thrust a knife in the heart of a fellow-creature, or will administer to him, in order to remove him from the surface of the globe on which we move with life and animation, that quantity of arsenic of which we just now talked.", "동료 인간의 심장에 잔인하게 칼을 꽂거나, 우리가 생기 있게 살아 움직이는 이 지구 표면에서 그자를 없애려고 아까 이야기한 그만큼의 비소를 먹일 사람은 아주 드물 겁니다. 그런 짓은 정말이지 정도에서 벗어난 일이지요 — 별나거나 어리석은 짓입니다."], ["Such a thing is really out of rule—eccentric or stupid. To attain such a point, the blood must be heated to thirty-six degrees, the pulse be, at least, at ninety, and the feelings excited beyond the ordinary limit.", "그 지경에 이르려면, 피가 36도까지 끓어올라야 하고, 맥박이 적어도 90은 되어야 하며, 감정이 보통의 한계를 넘어 격해져야 하니까요. 하지만 어학에서 허용되듯, 그 말 자체에서 좀 더 부드러운 동의어로 옮겨 간다고 합시다."], ["But suppose one pass, as is permissible in philology, from the word itself to its softened synonym, then, instead of committing an ignoble assassination you make an ‘elimination;’ you merely and simply remove from your path the individual who is in your way, and that without shock or violence, without the display of the sufferings which, in the case of becoming a punishment, make a martyr of the victim, and a butcher, in every sense of the word, of him who inflicts them.", "그러면 비열한 암살을 저지르는 대신 '제거'를 하는 셈이 되지요. 길을 막는 자를 그저 단순히 길에서 치워 버리는 겁니다. 그것도 충격이나 폭력 없이, 또 형벌이 되었을 때 희생자를 순교자로 만들고 그것을 가하는 자를 어느 모로 보나 백정으로 만드는 그 고통의 광경을 드러내지 않고서 말이지요."], ["Then there will be no blood, no groans, no convulsions, and above all, no consciousness of that horrid and compromising moment of accomplishing the act,—then one escapes the clutch of the human law, which says, ‘Do not disturb society!’ This is the mode in which they manage these things, and succeed in Eastern climes, where there are grave and phlegmatic persons who care very little for the questions of time in conjunctures of importance.”", "그러면 피도 없고, 신음도 없고, 경련도 없고, 무엇보다 그 끔찍하고 위태로운, 행위를 완수하는 순간의 의식도 없습니다 — 그렇게 해서 '사회를 어지럽히지 말라!'고 외치는 인간 법의 손아귀를 벗어나는 거지요. 이게 바로 동방의 풍토에서 그런 일을 처리하고 성공시키는 방식입니다. 그곳에는, 중대한 고비에서 시간 따위는 거의 개의치 않는, 점잖고 냉담한 사람들이 있으니까요.\""]], [["“Yet conscience remains,”", "\"그래도 양심은 남잖아요.\""], ["remarked Madame de Villefort in an agitated voice, and with a stifled sigh.", "빌포르 부인이 동요한 목소리로, 억눌린 한숨과 함께 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["answered Monte Cristo “happily, yes, conscience does remain; and if it did not, how wretched we should be!", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"다행히도, 그래요, 양심은 남지요. 그게 없다면 우리는 얼마나 비참하겠습니까!"], ["After every action requiring exertion, it is conscience that saves us, for it supplies us with a thousand good excuses, of which we alone are judges; and these reasons, howsoever excellent in producing sleep, would avail us but very little before a tribunal, when we were tried for our lives.", "애쓰지 않으면 안 되는 모든 행동 뒤에, 우리를 구하는 건 양심입니다. 양심은 우리에게 천 가지 그럴듯한 핑계를 대 주는데, 그걸 판단하는 건 오직 우리 자신뿐이지요. 그런 핑계들은, 잠을 이루는 데는 더없이 훌륭하지만, 우리가 목숨을 걸고 재판받는 법정 앞에서는 거의 도움이 안 될 겁니다."], ["Thus Richard III., for instance, was marvellously served by his conscience after the putting away of the two children of Edward IV.; in fact, he could say, ‘These two children of a cruel and persecuting king, who have inherited the vices of their father, which I alone could perceive in their juvenile propensities—these two children are impediments in my way of promoting the happiness of the English people, whose unhappiness they (the children) would infallibly have caused.’ Thus was Lady Macbeth served by her conscience, when she sought to give her son, and not her husband (whatever Shakespeare may say), a throne.", "가령 리처드 3세는, 에드워드 4세의 두 아이를 치워 버린 뒤 양심의 놀라운 도움을 받았지요. 실제로 그는 이렇게 말할 수 있었으니까요. '잔인하고 학정을 일삼던 왕의 이 두 아이는, 그 어린 성향에서 나만이 알아볼 수 있었던 아비의 악덕을 물려받았다 — 이 두 아이는 영국 백성의 행복을 도모하려는 내 길의 걸림돌이며, 그들이(이 아이들이) 백성에게 틀림없이 불행을 안겼을 것이다.'"], ["Ah, maternal love is a great virtue, a powerful motive—so powerful that it excuses a multitude of things, even if, after Duncan’s death, Lady Macbeth had been at all pricked by her conscience.”", "맥베스 부인도 양심의 도움을 받았지요. 그녀가 — 셰익스피어가 뭐라 하든 — 남편이 아니라 제 아들에게 왕좌를 주려 했을 때 말입니다. 아, 모성애는 위대한 미덕이고, 강력한 동기이지요 — 어찌나 강력한지, 던컨이 죽은 뒤 맥베스 부인이 양심에 조금이나마 찔렸다 하더라도, 수많은 것을 변명해 주니까요.\""]], [["Madame de Villefort listened with avidity to these appalling maxims and horrible paradoxes, delivered by the count with that ironical simplicity which was peculiar to him.", "빌포르 부인은, 백작 특유의 그 빈정대는 듯한 천연덕스러움으로 풀어내는 이 소름 끼치는 격언과 끔찍한 역설들을 게걸스레 귀 기울여 들었다."]], [["After a moment’s silence, the lady inquired:", "잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 부인이 물었다."]], [["“Do you know, my dear count,”", "\"있잖아요, 백작님.\""], ["she said, “that you are a very terrible reasoner, and that you look at the world through a somewhat distempered medium?", "그녀가 말했다. \"백작님은 정말 무시무시한 논객이세요. 게다가 다소 병든 매개를 통해 세상을 바라보시는 것 같고요."], ["Have you really measured the world by scrutinies, or through alembics and crucibles?", "정말로 세심한 관찰로, 아니면 증류기와 도가니를 통해 세상을 재 보셨나요?"], ["For you must indeed be a great chemist, and the elixir you administered to my son, which recalled him to life almost instantaneously——”", "정말 백작님은 대단한 화학자임에 틀림없어요. 제 아들에게 먹여, 거의 순식간에 그 애를 되살려 낸 그 영약만 봐도 —\""]], [["30077m\n“Oh, do not place any reliance on that, madame; _one_ drop of that elixir sufficed to recall life to a dying child, but three drops would have impelled the blood into his lungs in such a way as to have produced most violent palpitations; six would have suspended his respiration, and caused syncope more serious than that in which he was; ten would have destroyed him.", "\"오, 그걸 너무 믿진 마십시오, 부인. 그 영약 한 방울이면 죽어 가는 아이를 되살리기에 충분했지만, 세 방울이었다면 피를 폐로 몰아붙여 더없이 격렬한 심계항진을 일으켰을 겁니다. 여섯 방울이면 호흡을 멎게 해, 그 애가 빠졌던 것보다 더 위중한 실신을 불렀을 테고요."], ["You know, madame, how suddenly I snatched him from those phials which he so imprudently touched?”", "열 방울이면 그 애를 죽였을 겁니다. 부인도 아시지요, 그 애가 그토록 경솔하게 만진 약병들에서 제가 얼마나 갑작스레 그 애를 낚아챘는지요?\""]], [["“Is it then so terrible a poison?”", "\"그럼 그렇게나 무서운 독인가요?\""]], [["“Oh, no!", "\"오, 아닙니다!"], ["In the first place, let us agree that the word poison does not exist, because in medicine use is made of the most violent poisons, which become, according as they are employed, most salutary remedies.”", "우선, 독이라는 말은 존재하지 않는다는 데 동의하기로 하지요. 의학에서는 가장 격렬한 독들을 쓰는데, 그것이 쓰임새에 따라 더없이 이로운 치료약이 되니까요.\""]], [["“What, then, is it?”", "\"그럼 그게 대체 뭐죠?\""]], [["“A skilful preparation of my friend’s the worthy Abbé Adelmonte, who taught me the use of it.”", "\"제 친구인 그 훌륭한 아델몬테 신부의 솜씨 좋은 조제약입니다. 그분이 제게 그 쓰임새를 가르쳐 주셨지요.\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오.\""], ["observed Madame de Villefort, “it must be an admirable anti-spasmodic.”", "빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"그거 틀림없이 훌륭한 진경제겠군요.\""]], [["“Perfect, madame, as you have seen,”", "\"완벽하지요, 부인. 보셨다시피요.\""], ["replied the count; “and I frequently make use of it—with all possible prudence though, be it observed,”", "백작이 대답했다. \"저도 자주 쓴답니다 — 다만, 말이 나왔으니, 가능한 한 신중을 다해서요.\""], ["he added with a smile of intelligence.", "그가 의미심장한 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다."]], [["“Most assuredly,” responded Madame de Villefort in the same tone.", "\"그야 물론이지요.\" 빌포르 부인이 같은 어조로 응했다."], ["“As for me, so nervous, and so subject to fainting fits, I should require a Doctor Adelmonte to invent for me some means of breathing freely and tranquillizing my mind, in the fear I have of dying some fine day of suffocation.", "\"저는 신경이 어찌나 예민하고 또 까무러치는 일이 잦은지, 어느 좋은 날 질식해 죽을까 봐 두려운 마음에, 숨을 편히 쉬고 마음을 가라앉힐 무슨 방법을 발명해 줄 아델몬테 박사 같은 분이 필요할 지경이에요."], ["In the meanwhile, as the thing is difficult to find in France, and your abbé is not probably disposed to make a journey to Paris on my account, I must continue to use Monsieur Planche’s anti-spasmodics; and mint and Hoffman’s drops are among my favorite remedies.", "그런데 그런 걸 프랑스에서 찾기는 어렵고, 백작님의 그 신부님도 저 때문에 파리까지 걸음하실 마음은 없으실 테니, 저는 계속 플랑슈 씨의 진경제를 써야겠지요. 박하랑 호프만 물약이 제가 즐겨 쓰는 약 중에 들어요."], ["Here are some lozenges which I have made up on purpose; they are compounded doubly strong.”", "여기 일부러 만들어 둔 정제가 좀 있는데, 약효를 두 배로 진하게 조제한 것이랍니다.\""]], [["Monte Cristo opened the tortoise-shell box, which the lady presented to him, and inhaled the odor of the lozenges with the air of an amateur who thoroughly appreciated their composition.", "몬테크리스토는 부인이 건넨 거북 등딱지 갑을 열고, 그 조제법을 속속들이 음미하는 애호가의 표정으로 정제의 냄새를 들이마셨다."]], [["“They are indeed exquisite,”", "\"정말 절묘하군요.\""], ["he said; “but as they are necessarily submitted to the process of deglutition—a function which it is frequently impossible for a fainting person to accomplish—I prefer my own specific.”", "그가 말했다. \"다만 이건 삼키는 과정을 반드시 거쳐야 하는데 — 까무러친 사람으로서는 그 일을 해내기가 흔히 불가능하지요 — 그래서 저는 제 약을 더 좋아한답니다.\""]], [["“Undoubtedly, and so should I prefer it, after the effects I have seen produced; but of course it is a secret, and I am not so indiscreet as to ask it of you.”", "\"틀림없이 그렇겠죠. 저도 그 효력을 직접 본 터라 그쪽을 더 좋아하겠어요. 하지만 물론 그건 비밀이고, 저도 그걸 백작님께 여쭐 만큼 경솔하진 않아요.\""]], [["“But I,”", "\"하지만 저는.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, rising as he spoke—“I am gallant enough to offer it you.”", "몬테크리스토가 말하며 자리에서 일어섰다. \"기꺼이 그걸 부인께 드릴 만큼 너그러운 사람이지요.\""]], [["30079m\n“How kind you are.”", "\"어쩜 이리 친절하실까요.\""]], [["“Only remember one thing—a small dose is a remedy, a large one is poison.", "\"한 가지만 기억하십시오 — 적은 양은 약이고, 많은 양은 독입니다."], ["One drop will restore life, as you have seen; five or six will inevitably kill, and in a way the more terrible inasmuch as, poured into a glass of wine, it would not in the slightest degree affect its flavor.", "보셨다시피 한 방울이면 생명을 되살리지요. 다섯이나 여섯 방울이면 어김없이 죽이는데, 더더욱 무서운 건, 포도주 잔에 부어도 그 맛을 조금도 바꾸지 않는다는 점입니다. 하지만 더는 말하지 않겠습니다, 부인."], ["But I say no more, madame; it is really as if I were prescribing for you.”", "이러다간 정말 제가 부인께 처방을 내리는 꼴이 되겠군요.\""]], [["The clock struck half-past six, and a lady was announced, a friend of Madame de Villefort, who came to dine with her.", "시계가 여섯 시 반을 알렸고, 한 부인의 방문이 알려졌다. 빌포르 부인의 벗으로, 함께 만찬을 들러 온 사람이었다."]], [["“If I had had the honor of seeing you for the third or fourth time, count, instead of only for the second,”", "\"제가 백작님을 두 번째가 아니라 세 번째나 네 번째로 뵙는 영광을 누렸더라면요, 백작님.\""], ["said Madame de Villefort; “if I had had the honor of being your friend, instead of only having the happiness of being under an obligation to you, I should insist on detaining you to dinner, and not allow myself to be daunted by a first refusal.”", "빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"그저 신세를 진 행복만이 아니라 백작님의 벗이 되는 영광을 누렸더라면, 만찬에 모시겠다고 우겨, 첫 거절쯤에 주눅 들지 않았을 거예요.\""]], [["“A thousand thanks, madame,”", "\"천 번 감사드립니다, 부인.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo “but I have an engagement which I cannot break.", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"하지만 어길 수 없는 약속이 있어서요."], ["I have promised to escort to the Académie a Greek princess of my acquaintance who has never seen your grand opera, and who relies on me to conduct her thither.”", "우리 그랜드 오페라를 한 번도 본 적 없는, 제 지인인 어느 그리스 공주를 아카데미로 모셔다 드리기로 했거든요. 거기로 안내해 달라고 제게 의지하고 있답니다.\""]], [["“Adieu, then, sir, and do not forget the prescription.”", "\"그럼 안녕히 가세요, 백작님. 처방은 잊지 마시고요.\""]], [["“Ah, in truth, madame, to do that I must forget the hour’s conversation I have had with you, which is indeed impossible.”", "\"아, 정말이지, 부인. 그러려면 부인과 나눈 한 시간의 대화를 잊어야 할 텐데, 그건 도저히 불가능한 일이지요.\""]], [["Monte Cristo bowed, and left the house.", "몬테크리스토는 인사하고 집을 나섰다."], ["Madame de Villefort remained immersed in thought.", "빌포르 부인은 생각에 잠긴 채 남아 있었다."]], [["“He is a very strange man,”", "\"참 별난 사람이야.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, “and in my opinion is himself the Adelmonte he talks about.”", "\"내가 보기엔, 저 사람이야말로 입에 올리는 그 아델몬테 본인인 것 같아.\""]], [["As to Monte Cristo the result had surpassed his utmost expectations.", "몬테크리스토로 말하자면, 결과는 그의 더없이 큰 기대마저 넘어선 것이었다."]], [["“Good,”", "\"좋아.\""], ["said he, as he went away; “this is a fruitful soil, and I feel certain that the seed sown will not be cast on barren ground.”", "그가 떠나며 말했다. \"여긴 비옥한 땅이야. 뿌린 씨앗이 메마른 땅에 떨어지진 않으리라 확신한다.\""]], [["Next morning, faithful to his promise, he sent the prescription requested.", "이튿날 아침, 그는 약속을 지켜, 부인이 청한 처방을 보냈다."]], [["# Chapter 53. Robert le Diable", "# Chapter 53. Robert le Diable"]], [["The pretext of an opera engagement was so much the more feasible, as there chanced to be on that very night a more than ordinary attraction at the Académie Royale.", "오페라를 핑계 삼는 것은 한결 그럴듯한 일이었다. 마침 그날 밤 왕립 음악원에서는 평소보다 더한 볼거리가 펼쳐질 예정이었기 때문이다."], ["Levasseur, who had been suffering under severe illness, made his reappearance in the character of _Bertram_, and, as usual, the announcement of the most admired production of the favorite composer of the day had attracted a brilliant and fashionable audience.", "중병을 앓던 르바쇠르가 베르트랑 역으로 다시 무대에 오르는 데다, 늘 그렇듯 당대 최고 인기 작곡가의 가장 사랑받는 작품을 상연한다는 소식이 화려하고 세련된 관객들을 불러 모았다."], ["Morcerf, like most other young men of rank and fortune, had his orchestra stall, with the certainty of always finding a seat in at least a dozen of the principal boxes occupied by persons of his acquaintance; he had, moreover, his right of entry into the omnibus box.", "모르세르는 지체와 재산을 갖춘 여느 젊은이들이 그렇듯 일층 정면석을 가지고 있었고, 적어도 열두 개쯤 되는 주요 칸막이석 가운데 아는 사람들이 차지한 자리는 언제든 들어갈 수 있었다. 게다가 그는 합승석에 들어갈 권리도 있었다."], ["Château-Renaud rented a stall beside his own, while Beauchamp, as a journalist, had unlimited range all over the theatre.", "샤토르노는 그의 옆자리 정면석을 빌렸고, 신문 기자인 보샹은 극장 어디든 무제한으로 드나들 수 있었다."], ["It happened that on this particular night the minister’s box was placed at the disposal of Lucien Debray, who offered it to the Comte de Morcerf, who again, upon his rejection of it by Mercédès, sent it to Danglars, with an intimation that he should probably do himself the honor of joining the baroness and her daughter during the evening, in the event of their accepting the box in question.", "그날 밤은 마침 장관의 칸막이석이 뤼시앵 드브레의 손에 맡겨졌는데, 드브레는 그것을 모르세르 백작에게 권했다. 모르세르 백작은 메르세데스가 거절하자 다시 그것을 당글라르에게 보냈고, 그 칸막이석을 받아들인다면 저녁 동안 남작 부인과 그 딸과 합석하는 영광을 누리게 될지도 모른다는 뜻을 넌지시 비쳤다."], ["The ladies received the offer with too much pleasure to dream of a refusal.", "부인들은 그 제안을 거절은 꿈도 꾸지 않을 만큼 기쁘게 받아들였다."], ["To no class of persons is the presentation of a gratuitous opera-box more acceptable than to the wealthy millionaire, who still hugs economy while boasting of carrying a king’s ransom in his waistcoat pocket.", "어떤 부류의 사람에게도, 조끼 주머니에 임금의 몸값을 넣고 다닌다고 뽐내면서도 여전히 절약에 매달리는 부유한 백만장자에게만큼 공짜 오페라 칸막이석이 반가운 일은 없는 법이다."]], [["Danglars had, however, protested against showing himself in a ministerial box, declaring that his political principles, and his parliamentary position as member of the opposition party would not permit him so to commit himself; the baroness had, therefore, despatched a note to Lucien Debray, bidding him call for them, it being wholly impossible for her to go alone with Eugénie to the opera.", "그러나 당글라르는 장관의 칸막이석에 모습을 드러내기를 거부했다. 자신의 정치적 신념과 야당 의원이라는 의회 내 위치가 그렇게 처신하도록 허락하지 않는다는 것이었다. 그래서 남작 부인은 뤼시앵 드브레에게 자신들을 데리러 와 달라는 쪽지를 보냈다. 외제니와 단둘이 오페라에 간다는 것은 도저히 있을 수 없는 일이었기 때문이다."]], [["There is no gainsaying the fact that a very unfavorable construction would have been put upon the circumstance if the two women had gone without escort, while the addition of a third, in the person of her mother’s admitted lover, enabled Mademoiselle Danglars to defy malice and ill-nature.", "두 여자가 동반자도 없이 갔더라면 그 처사가 매우 좋지 않게 받아들여졌으리라는 것은 부인할 수 없는 사실이었다. 반면 어머니가 공공연히 인정한 연인이라는 세 번째 인물이 더해지자, 당글라르 양은 악의와 험담을 너끈히 비웃을 수 있었다."], ["One must take the world as one finds it.", "세상은 있는 그대로 받아들여야 하는 법이다."]], [["30083m\nThe curtain rose, as usual, to an almost empty house, it being one of the absurdities of Parisian fashion never to appear at the opera until after the beginning of the performance, so that the first act is generally played without the slightest attention being paid to it, that part of the audience already assembled being too much occupied in observing the fresh arrivals, while nothing is heard but the noise of opening and shutting doors, and the buzz of conversation.", "막이 오를 때면 으레 그렇듯 객석은 거의 비어 있었다. 공연이 시작된 뒤에야 오페라에 모습을 드러내는 것이 파리 사교계의 어처구니없는 관습이었기에, 1막은 대개 아무런 주목도 받지 못한 채 흘러갔다. 이미 자리에 든 관객들은 새로 들어오는 사람들을 관찰하느라 여념이 없었고, 들리는 것이라고는 문을 여닫는 소리와 잡담 소리뿐이었다."]], [["“Surely,”", "\"틀림없어.\""], ["said Albert, as the door of a box on the first circle opened, “that must be the Countess G——.”", "일층 첫째 줄 칸막이석 문이 열리자 알베르가 말했다. \"저분은 G 백작 부인이 분명해.\""]], [["“And who is the Countess G——?”", "\"G 백작 부인이 누군가?\""], ["inquired Château-Renaud.", "샤토르노가 물었다."]], [["“What a question!", "\"그런 걸 묻다니!"], ["Now, do you know, baron, I have a great mind to pick a quarrel with you for asking it; as if all the world did not know who the Countess G—— was.”", "남작, 그런 질문을 한다면 내가 정말 싸움이라도 걸고 싶어지네. 마치 온 세상이 G 백작 부인이 누군지 모른다는 듯이 말이야.\""]], [["“Ah, to be sure,”", "\"아, 그렇군.\""], ["replied Château-Renaud; “the lovely Venetian, is it not?”", "샤토르노가 대답했다. \"그 어여쁜 베네치아 여인 말이지?\""]], [["“Herself.” At this moment the countess perceived Albert, and returned his salutation with a smile.", "\"바로 그분일세.\" 그 순간 백작 부인이 알베르를 알아보고 미소로 인사를 받았다."]], [["“You know her, it seems?”", "\"아는 사이인 모양이군?\""], ["said Château-Renaud.", "샤토르노가 말했다."]], [["“Franz introduced me to her at Rome,”", "\"로마에서 프란츠가 소개해 줬네.\""], ["replied Albert.", "알베르가 대답했다."]], [["“Well, then, will you do as much for me in Paris as Franz did for you in Rome?”", "\"그럼 프란츠가 로마에서 자네에게 해 준 만큼, 파리에서 나를 위해 해 줄 수 있겠나?\""]], [["“With pleasure.”", "\"기꺼이.\""]], [["There was a cry of “Shut up!”", "관객석에서 \"조용히 해!\""], ["from the audience. This manifestation on the part of the spectators of their wish to be allowed to hear the music, produced not the slightest effect on the two young men, who continued their conversation.", "하는 소리가 터져 나왔다. 음악을 듣고 싶다는 관객들의 이런 의사 표시는 두 젊은이에게 조금도 영향을 미치지 못했고, 두 사람은 계속 이야기를 나누었다."]], [["“The countess was present at the races in the Champ-de-Mars,”", "\"백작 부인은 샹드마르스 경마장에도 와 있었네.\" 샤토르노가 말했다."], ["said Château-Renaud.", "\"오늘 말인가?\""]], [["“Today?”", "\"그래.\""]], [["“Yes.” “Bless me, I quite forgot the races.", "\"이런, 경마를 까맣게 잊고 있었군."], ["Did you bet?”", "자넨 돈을 걸었나?\""]], [["“Oh, merely a paltry fifty louis.” “And who was the winner?”", "\"아, 그저 루이 금화 쉰 닢이라는 보잘것없는 돈을 걸었을 뿐일세.\""]], [["“Nautilus. I staked on him.”", "\"그래, 누가 이겼나?\""]], [["“But there were three races, were there not?”", "\"노틸러스. 내가 그놈한테 걸었지.\""]], [["“Yes; there was the prize given by the Jockey Club—a gold cup, you know—and a very singular circumstance occurred about that race.”", "\"하지만 경주가 셋이었지, 안 그런가?\" \"그래. 자키 클럽이 내건 상이 있었네. 알다시피 금잔이지. 그런데 그 경주에서 아주 묘한 일이 벌어졌어.\""]], [["“What was it?”", "\"무슨 일이었나?\""]], [["“Oh, shut up!” again interposed some of the audience.", "\"어이, 조용히 좀 해!\" 또다시 관객 몇몇이 끼어들었다."]], [["“Why, it was won by a horse and rider utterly unknown on the course.”", "\"경주장에서는 전혀 알려지지 않은 말과 기수가 우승했지 뭔가.\""]], [["“Is that possible?”", "\"그게 가능한 일인가?\""]], [["“True as day.", "\"대낮처럼 분명한 사실일세. 사실은 이래."], ["The fact was, nobody had observed a horse entered by the name of Vampa, or that of a jockey styled Job, when, at the last moment, a splendid roan, mounted by a jockey about as big as your fist, presented themselves at the starting-post.", "밤파라는 이름으로 출전 등록된 말이나 조브라는 이름의 기수를 아무도 눈여겨보지 않았는데, 막판에 가서 멋진 적갈색 말이 나타났네. 자네 주먹만 한 기수가 올라타고 말이야. 출발선에 모습을 드러냈지."], ["They were obliged to stuff at least twenty pounds weight of shot in the small rider’s pockets, to make him weight; but with all that he outstripped Ariel and Barbare, against whom he ran, by at least three whole lengths.”", "그 작은 기수의 주머니에 적어도 이십 파운드는 되는 탄환을 채워 넣어야 했네. 무게를 맞추려고 말이야. 그런데도 그 말은 함께 달린 아리엘과 바르바르를 적어도 세 마신 차이로 따돌렸어.\""]], [["“And was it not found out at last to whom the horse and jockey belonged?” “No.”", "\"그래서 결국 말과 기수가 누구 것인지 밝혀지지 않았나?\""]], [["“You say that the horse was entered under the name of Vampa?”", "\"못 밝혔지.\" \"그 말이 밤파라는 이름으로 등록됐다고 했나?\""]], [["“Exactly; that was the title.”", "\"바로 그렇네. 그게 이름이었지.\""]], [["“Then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["answered Albert, “I am better informed than you are, and know who the owner of that horse was.”", "알베르가 대답했다. \"내가 자네보다 더 잘 아는군. 그 말 주인이 누군지 알겠어.\""]], [["“Shut up, there!”", "\"거기, 조용히 해!\""], ["cried the pit in chorus. And this time the tone and manner in which the command was given, betokened such growing hostility that the two young men perceived, for the first time, that the mandate was addressed to them.", "일층 입석에서 합창하듯 외쳤다. 이번에는 그 명령을 던지는 어조와 태도에 점점 짙어지는 적의가 배어 있어서, 두 젊은이는 비로소 그 명령이 자기들을 향한 것임을 깨달았다."], ["Leisurely turning round, they calmly scrutinized the various countenances around them, as though demanding some one person who would take upon himself the responsibility of what they deemed excessive impertinence; but as no one responded to the challenge, the friends turned again to the front of the theatre, and affected to busy themselves with the stage.", "두 사람은 느긋하게 몸을 돌려, 주위의 여러 얼굴을 차분히 훑어보았다. 자기들이 보기에 지나친 무례라 여겨지는 그 일에 책임을 지겠다고 나설 자가 누군지 찾는 듯한 태도였다. 그러나 아무도 그 도전에 응하지 않자, 두 친구는 다시 무대 쪽으로 돌아앉아 공연에 열중하는 척했다."], ["At this moment the door of the minister’s box opened, and Madame Danglars, accompanied by her daughter, entered, escorted by Lucien Debray, who assiduously conducted them to their seats.", "그 순간 장관의 칸막이석 문이 열리며 당글라르 부인이 딸과 함께 들어왔고, 뤼시앵 드브레가 부지런히 두 사람을 자리로 안내하며 동행했다."]], [["“Ha, ha,”", "\"하하,\""], ["said Château-Renaud, “here come some friends of yours, viscount!", "샤토르노가 말했다. \"자작, 자네 친구분들이 오시네!"], ["What are you looking at there?", "거기서 뭘 보고 있나?"], ["don’t you see they are trying to catch your eye?”", "저분들이 자네와 눈을 맞추려 하는 게 안 보이나?\""]], [["Albert turned round, just in time to receive a gracious wave of the fan from the baroness; as for Mademoiselle Eugénie, she scarcely vouchsafed to waste the glances of her large black eyes even upon the business of the stage.", "알베르가 돌아보았다. 마침 남작 부인이 우아하게 부채를 흔들어 보내는 인사를 받기에 딱 알맞은 때였다. 외제니 양으로 말하자면, 그 커다란 검은 눈의 시선을 무대 위 일에조차 좀처럼 던지려 하지 않았다."]], [["“I tell you what, my dear fellow,”", "\"이보게,\" 샤토르노가 말했다."], ["said Château-Renaud, “I cannot imagine what objection you can possibly have to Mademoiselle Danglars—that is, setting aside her want of ancestry and somewhat inferior rank, which by the way I don’t think you care very much about.", "\"자네가 당글라르 양한테 대체 무슨 불만을 품고 있는지 도무지 모르겠네. 가문이 변변치 않고 신분도 다소 낮다는 점이야 그렇다 쳐도, 사실 그런 거야 자네가 그리 마음 쓰는 것 같지도 않고 말이야."], ["Now, barring all that, I mean to say she is a deuced fine girl!”", "그런 걸 다 제쳐 두면, 어쨌든 굉장히 멋진 아가씨라고 봐야지!\""]], [["“Handsome, certainly,”", "\"아름답긴 하지.\""], ["replied Albert, “but not to my taste, which I confess, inclines to something softer, gentler, and more feminine.”", "알베르가 대답했다. \"하지만 내 취향은 아닐세. 솔직히 말해 나는 좀 더 부드럽고 온화하고 여성스러운 쪽으로 마음이 기우는 편이라서.\""]], [["“Ah, well,”", "\"아, 그렇군.\""], ["exclaimed Château-Renaud, who because he had seen his thirtieth summer fancied himself duly warranted in assuming a sort of paternal air with his more youthful friend, “you young people are never satisfied; why, what would you have more?", "샤토르노가 외쳤다. 그는 서른 번째 여름을 넘겼다는 이유만으로 더 젊은 친구 앞에서 아비 같은 태도를 취할 자격이 충분하다고 여기는 사람이었다. \"자네 같은 젊은이들은 도무지 만족할 줄을 몰라."], ["your parents have chosen you a bride built on the model of Diana, the huntress, and yet you are not content.”", "대체 더 뭘 바라나? 자네 부모님이 사냥의 여신 디아나를 본떠 만든 듯한 신붓감을 골라 주셨는데도 흡족하지 않다니.\""]], [["“No, for that very resemblance affrights me; I should have liked something more in the manner of the Venus of Milo or Capua; but this chase-loving Diana, continually surrounded by her nymphs, gives me a sort of alarm lest she should some day bring on me the fate of Actæon.”", "\"그래, 바로 그 닮은 점이 나는 두렵단 말일세. 나는 차라리 밀로나 카푸아의 비너스 같은 쪽이 좋았을 거야. 그런데 이 사냥을 즐기는 디아나는 늘 요정들에게 둘러싸여 있으니, 언젠가 나한테 악타이온 같은 운명을 안겨 줄까 봐 어쩐지 겁이 난단 말이지.\""]], [["And, indeed, it required but one glance at Mademoiselle Danglars to comprehend the justness of Morcerf’s remark.", "과연 당글라르 양을 한 번 보기만 해도 모르세르의 말이 옳다는 것을 알 수 있었다."], ["She was beautiful, but her beauty was of too marked and decided a character to please a fastidious taste; her hair was raven black, but its natural waves seemed somewhat rebellious; her eyes, of the same color as her hair, were surmounted by well-arched brows, whose great defect, however, consisted in an almost habitual frown, while her whole physiognomy wore that expression of firmness and decision so little in accordance with the gentler attributes of her sex—her nose was precisely what a sculptor would have chosen for a chiselled Juno.", "그녀는 아름다웠지만, 그 아름다움은 까다로운 취향을 만족시키기에는 지나치게 두드러지고 단호한 성격을 띠고 있었다. 머리카락은 까마귀처럼 검었으나 타고난 곱슬기가 어쩐지 다루기 힘들어 보였다. 머리카락과 같은 빛깔의 두 눈 위로는 멋지게 둥근 눈썹이 솟아 있었는데, 그 가장 큰 흠은 거의 습관처럼 찌푸려져 있다는 점이었다."], ["Her mouth, which might have been found fault with as too large, displayed teeth of pearly whiteness, rendered still more conspicuous by the brilliant carmine of her lips, contrasting vividly with her naturally pale complexion.", "게다가 그녀의 얼굴 전체에는 여성의 부드러운 속성과는 도무지 어울리지 않는 굳건함과 단호함의 표정이 깃들어 있었다. 코는 조각가가 유노 흉상을 깎을 때 골랐을 법한 바로 그 모양이었다. 입은 너무 크다고 흠잡을 만했으나, 진주처럼 흰 이가 드러나 있었고, 입술의 선명한 진홍빛이 그것을 한층 두드러지게 했다."], ["But that which completed the almost masculine look Morcerf found so little to his taste, was a dark mole, of much larger dimensions than these freaks of nature generally are, placed just at the corner of her mouth; and the effect tended to increase the expression of self-dependence that characterized her countenance.", "그 진홍빛은 본래 창백한 피부와 또렷이 대비되었다. 그런데 모르세르가 그토록 마음에 들어 하지 않던 그 거의 남성적인 인상을 완성하는 것은, 자연의 변덕이 빚어내는 것치고는 훨씬 큰 검은 점이었다. 그것은 바로 입가에 자리하고 있었고, 그 효과는 그녀의 얼굴을 특징짓는 자립심의 표정을 한층 짙게 만들었다."]], [["The rest of Mademoiselle Eugénie’s person was in perfect keeping with the head just described; she, indeed, reminded one of Diana, as Château-Renaud observed, but her bearing was more haughty and resolute.", "외제니 양의 나머지 모습도 방금 묘사한 얼굴과 완벽하게 어우러졌다. 과연 그녀는 샤토르노의 말마따나 디아나를 떠올리게 했으나, 자태는 한층 도도하고 단호했다."]], [["As regarded her attainments, the only fault to be found with them was the same that a fastidious connoisseur might have found with her beauty, that they were somewhat too erudite and masculine for so young a person.", "교양으로 말하자면, 그것에서 흠잡을 점이라고는 까다로운 감식가가 그녀의 아름다움에서 찾아낼 만한 것과 같은 한 가지뿐이었다. 즉 그토록 어린 나이의 사람치고는 다소 지나치게 박식하고 남성적이라는 점이었다."], ["She was a perfect linguist, a first-rate artist, wrote poetry, and composed music; to the study of the latter she professed to be entirely devoted, following it with an indefatigable perseverance, assisted by a schoolfellow,—a young woman without fortune whose talent promised to develop into remarkable powers as a singer.", "그녀는 완벽한 어학가였고, 일류 화가였으며, 시를 쓰고 곡을 지었다. 특히 음악 공부에 온전히 몰두한다고 공언하며, 지칠 줄 모르는 끈기로 그것을 파고들었다. 학교 친구 한 명이 곁에서 도왔는데, 그 친구는 재산은 없으나 그 재능이 장차 가수로서 비범한 역량으로 꽃필 만한 젊은 여인이었다."], ["It was rumored that she was an object of almost paternal interest to one of the principal composers of the day, who excited her to spare no pains in the cultivation of her voice, which might hereafter prove a source of wealth and independence.", "소문에 따르면, 그녀는 당대 주요 작곡가 가운데 한 사람에게서 거의 아버지 같은 관심을 받고 있었다. 그 작곡가는 그녀에게 목소리를 갈고닦는 데 온 힘을 쏟으라고 부추겼으니, 그 목소리가 훗날 부와 독립의 원천이 될 수 있다는 것이었다."], ["But this counsel effectually decided Mademoiselle Danglars never to commit herself by being seen in public with one destined for a theatrical life; and acting upon this principle, the banker’s daughter, though perfectly willing to allow Mademoiselle Louise d’Armilly (that was the name of the young virtuosa) to practice with her through the day, took especial care not to be seen in her company.", "그러나 이 충고 때문에 당글라르 양은 장차 무대 인생을 살아갈 사람과 사람들 앞에 함께 나서서 처신을 그르치는 일은 결코 하지 않겠다고 단단히 마음먹었다. 그래서 그 원칙에 따라, 은행가의 딸은 낮 동안 루이즈 다르밀리 양과 — 그것이 그 젊은 명연주자의 이름이었다 — 함께 연습하는 것은 얼마든지 좋아하면서도, 그녀와 함께 있는 모습이 남의 눈에 띄지 않도록 각별히 조심했다."], ["Still, though not actually received at the Hôtel Danglars in the light of an acknowledged friend, Louise was treated with far more kindness and consideration than is usually bestowed on a governess.", "그래도 루이즈는 당글라르 저택에서 공인된 친구로 대접받지는 못했지만, 보통 가정 교사에게 베푸는 것보다는 훨씬 다정하고 정중한 대우를 받았다."]], [["The curtain fell almost immediately after the entrance of Madame Danglars into her box, the band quitted the orchestra for the accustomed half-hour’s interval allowed between the acts, and the audience were left at liberty to promenade the salon or lobbies, or to pay and receive visits in their respective boxes.", "당글라르 부인이 칸막이석에 들어서기가 무섭게 막이 내렸고, 악단은 막간에 으레 주어지는 삼십 분의 휴식을 위해 오케스트라석을 떠났다. 관객들은 자유로이 살롱이나 복도를 거닐거나, 저마다의 칸막이석에서 방문을 주고받을 수 있었다."]], [["Morcerf and Château-Renaud were amongst the first to avail themselves of this permission.", "모르세르와 샤토르노는 이 허락을 가장 먼저 누린 축에 들었다."], ["For an instant the idea struck Madame Danglars that this eagerness on the part of the young viscount arose from his impatience to join her party, and she whispered her expectations to her daughter, that Albert was hurrying to pay his respects to them.", "한순간 당글라르 부인은 젊은 자작의 이런 서두름이 자기네 자리에 합석하고 싶은 조바심에서 비롯된 것이라는 생각이 들었고, 알베르가 자기들에게 인사하러 서둘러 오는 것이라는 기대를 딸에게 속삭였다. 그러나 외제니 양은 그저 못마땅하다는 듯 고개를 가로저을 뿐이었다."], ["Mademoiselle Eugénie, however, merely returned a dissenting movement of the head, while, with a cold smile, she directed the attention of her mother to an opposite box on the first circle, in which sat the Countess G——, and where Morcerf had just made his appearance. 30087m", "그러고는 차가운 미소를 띠며, 일층 첫째 줄 맞은편 칸막이석으로 어머니의 주의를 돌렸다. 그곳에는 G 백작 부인이 앉아 있었고, 모르세르가 막 그곳에 모습을 드러낸 참이었다."]], [["“So we meet again, my travelling friend, do we?”", "\"또 만났군요, 나의 여행 동무님.\" 백작 부인이 외쳤다."], ["cried the countess, extending her hand to him with all the warmth and cordiality of an old acquaintance; “it was really very good of you to recognize me so quickly, and still more so to bestow your first visit on me.”", "그녀는 오랜 친구다운 온기와 다정함을 담뿍 담아 그에게 손을 내밀었다. \"이렇게 빨리 저를 알아봐 주시다니 정말 고마운 일이에요. 게다가 첫 방문을 저에게 베풀어 주시니 더욱더 그렇고요.\""]], [["“Be assured,”", "\"분명히 말씀드리지만,\""], ["replied Albert, “that if I had been aware of your arrival in Paris, and had known your address, I should have paid my respects to you before this.", "알베르가 대답했다. \"부인께서 파리에 오신 걸 알았고 주소를 알았더라면, 진작에 찾아뵙고 인사드렸을 겁니다."], ["Allow me to introduce my friend, Baron de Château-Renaud, one of the few true gentlemen now to be found in France, and from whom I have just learned that you were a spectator of the races in the Champ-de-Mars, yesterday.”", "제 친구 샤토르노 남작을 소개하지요. 오늘날 프랑스에서 찾아보기 드문 진정한 신사 가운데 하나입니다. 어제 부인께서 샹드마르스 경마를 구경하셨다는 것도 방금 이 친구한테서 들었습니다.\""]], [["Château-Renaud bowed to the countess.", "샤토르노가 백작 부인에게 인사했다."]], [["“So you were at the races, baron?”", "\"그래, 남작님도 경마장에 계셨나요?\""], ["inquired the countess eagerly.", "백작 부인이 다급히 물었다."]], [["“Yes, madame.”", "\"네, 부인.\""]], [["“Well, then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["pursued Madame G—— with considerable animation, “you can probably tell me who won the Jockey Club stakes?”", "G 부인이 상당히 활기를 띠며 말을 이었다. \"혹시 자키 클럽 상을 누가 탔는지 말씀해 주실 수 있나요?\""]], [["“I am sorry to say I cannot,”", "\"유감스럽게도 그럴 수가 없습니다.\""], ["replied the baron; “and I was just asking the same question of Albert.”", "남작이 대답했다. \"마침 저도 알베르에게 똑같은 걸 묻던 참이었거든요.\""]], [["“Are you very anxious to know, countess?”", "\"꼭 알고 싶으십니까, 백작 부인?\""], ["asked Albert.", "알베르가 물었다."]], [["“To know what?”", "\"무엇을요?\""]], [["“The name of the owner of the winning horse?”", "\"우승마 주인의 이름을요.\""]], [["“Excessively; only imagine—but do tell me, viscount, whether you really are acquainted with it or no?”", "\"무척이나요. 글쎄, 한번 상상해 보세요. 아니, 그보다 말씀해 주세요, 자작님. 정말로 그걸 아세요, 모르세요?\""]], [["“I beg your pardon, madame, but you were about to relate some story, were you not?", "\"실례지만, 부인.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"무슨 이야기를 들려주시려던 참 아니었습니까?"], ["You said, ‘only imagine,’—and then paused.", "'한번 상상해 보세요'라고 하시고는 말을 멈추셨잖아요."], ["Pray continue.”", "부디 계속하시지요.\""]], [["“Well, then, listen.", "\"그럼 들어 보세요."], ["You must know I felt so interested in the splendid roan horse, with his elegant little rider, so tastefully dressed in a pink satin jacket and cap, that I could not help praying for their success with as much earnestness as though the half of my fortune were at stake; and when I saw them outstrip all the others, and come to the winning-post in such gallant style, I actually clapped my hands with joy.", "저는 그 멋진 적갈색 말과, 분홍 새틴 윗옷에 모자를 멋스럽게 차려입은 자그마한 기수에게 어찌나 마음이 끌렸던지, 마치 제 재산의 절반이 걸리기라도 한 듯 간절히 그들의 승리를 빌지 않을 수 없었답니다. 그리고 그들이 다른 말들을 모두 따돌리고 그토록 늠름하게 결승점에 들어오는 걸 봤을 땐, 정말이지 기뻐서 박수를 쳤지 뭐예요."], ["Imagine my surprise, when, upon returning home, the first object I met on the staircase was the identical jockey in the pink jacket!", "그런데 집으로 돌아왔을 때 제가 얼마나 놀랐는지 상상해 보세요. 계단에서 제일 먼저 마주친 게 바로 그 분홍 윗옷을 입은 기수였으니까요!"], ["I concluded that, by some singular chance, the owner of the winning horse must live in the same hotel as myself; but, as I entered my apartments, I beheld the very gold cup awarded as a prize to the unknown horse and rider.", "저는 무슨 묘한 우연으로 우승마 주인이 저와 같은 호텔에 묵나 보다 했어요. 그런데 제 방에 들어서니, 그 미지의 말과 기수에게 상으로 주어진 바로 그 금잔이 놓여 있는 게 아니겠어요."], ["Inside the cup was a small piece of paper, on which were written these words—‘From Lord Ruthven to Countess G——.’”", "잔 안에는 작은 종이쪽지가 들어 있었고, 거기에 이런 말이 적혀 있었답니다. '러스벤 경이 G 백작 부인께.'\""]], [["“Precisely; I was sure of it,”", "\"바로 그겁니다. 제 짐작이 맞았어요.\""], ["said Morcerf.", "모르세르가 말했다."]], [["“Sure of what?”", "\"뭐가 맞았다는 거죠?\""]], [["“That the owner of the horse was Lord Ruthven himself.”", "\"그 말 주인이 바로 러스벤 경이라는 거요.\""]], [["“What Lord Ruthven do you mean?”", "\"러스벤 경이라니, 무슨 말씀이신지?\""]], [["“Why, our Lord Ruthven—the Vampire of the Salle Argentina!”", "\"왜요, 우리의 그 러스벤 경 말입니다. 아르헨티나 극장의 흡혈귀요!\""]], [["“Is it possible?”", "\"그게 가능한 일인가요?\""], ["exclaimed the countess; “is he here in Paris?”", "백작 부인이 외쳤다. \"그분이 파리에 와 있다고요?\""]], [["“To be sure,—why not?”", "\"그럼요. 안 될 게 뭐 있습니까?\""]], [["“And you visit him?—meet him at your own house and elsewhere?”", "\"그래서 그분 댁을 찾아가시고요? 자기 댁에서도, 다른 데서도 만나시고요?\""]], [["“I assure you he is my most intimate friend, and M.", "\"분명히 말씀드리는데, 그분은 제 가장 막역한 친구입니다."], ["de Château-Renaud has also the honor of his acquaintance.”", "샤토르노 씨도 그분과 알고 지내는 영광을 누리고 있고요.\""]], [["“But why are you so sure of his being the winner of the Jockey Club prize?”", "\"그런데 그분이 자키 클럽 상의 우승자라는 걸 어떻게 그리 확신하시죠?\""]], [["“Was not the winning horse entered by the name of Vampa?”", "\"우승마가 밤파라는 이름으로 등록되지 않았습니까?\""]], [["“What of that?”", "\"그게 어쨌다는 거죠?\""]], [["“Why, do you not recollect the name of the celebrated bandit by whom I was made prisoner?”", "\"왜요, 저를 사로잡았던 그 이름난 산적의 이름이 기억나지 않으십니까?\""]], [["“Oh, yes.”", "\"아, 그래요.\""]], [["“And from whose hands the count extricated me in so wonderful a manner?”", "\"그리고 백작님이 저를 그자의 손아귀에서 그토록 놀랍게 구해 내셨잖아요?\""]], [["“To be sure, I remember it all now.”", "\"맞아요, 이제 다 기억나는군요.\""]], [["“He called himself Vampa.", "\"그자가 자기를 밤파라고 했지요."], ["You see, it’s evident where the count got the name.”", "보세요, 백작님이 그 이름을 어디서 따왔는지 뻔하지 않습니까.\""]], [["“But what could have been his motive for sending the cup to me?”", "\"하지만 그분이 무슨 까닭으로 그 잔을 저에게 보냈을까요?\""]], [["“In the first place, because I had spoken much of you to him, as you may believe; and in the second, because he delighted to see a countrywoman take so lively an interest in his success.”", "\"첫째로는, 짐작하시겠지만, 제가 그분에게 부인 얘기를 많이 했기 때문이지요. 둘째로는, 동향 사람이 자신의 승리에 그토록 생생한 관심을 보이는 걸 흐뭇해했기 때문이고요.\""]], [["“I trust and hope you never repeated to the count all the foolish remarks we used to make about him?”", "\"우리가 그분에 대해 늘 떠들던 그 어리석은 소리들을 백작님께 다 옮기지는 않으셨겠지요?\""]], [["“I should not like to affirm upon oath that I have not.", "\"맹세코 안 그랬다고 단언하기는 어렵겠는데요."], ["Besides, his presenting you the cup under the name of Lord Ruthven——”", "게다가 그분이 러스벤 경이라는 이름으로 부인께 잔을 보낸 걸 보면……\""]], [["“Oh, but that is dreadful!", "\"어머, 그건 너무하잖아요!"], ["Why, the man must owe me a fearful grudge.”", "그렇다면 그분은 저한테 무시무시한 원한을 품은 게 분명해요.\""]], [["“Does his action appear like that of an enemy?”", "\"그분의 행동이 적의 행동 같습니까?\""]], [["“No; certainly not.”", "\"아니요, 그럴 리는 없죠.\""]], [["“Well, then——”", "\"그럼……\""]], [["“And so he is in Paris?”", "\"그래서 그분이 파리에 있다는 거죠?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“And what effect does he produce?”", "\"그래서 어떤 반응을 일으키고 있나요?\""]], [["“Why,”", "\"글쎄요,\" 알베르가 말했다."], ["said Albert, “he was talked about for a week; then the coronation of the queen of England took place, followed by the theft of Mademoiselle Mars’s diamonds; and so people talked of something else.”", "\"일주일쯤은 사람들 입에 오르내렸지요. 그러다 영국 여왕의 대관식이 있었고, 뒤이어 마르스 양의 다이아몬드 도난 사건이 터졌어요. 그래서 사람들은 다른 얘기를 하게 됐고요.\""]], [["“My good fellow,”", "\"여보게,\""], ["said Château-Renaud, “the count is your friend and you treat him accordingly.", "샤토르노가 말했다. \"백작은 자네 친구이니 자네가 두둔하는 거겠지."], ["Do not believe what Albert is telling you, countess; so far from the sensation excited in the Parisian circles by the appearance of the Count of Monte Cristo having abated, I take upon myself to declare that it is as strong as ever.", "백작 부인, 알베르 말은 믿지 마십시오. 몬테크리스토 백작의 등장이 파리 사교계에 불러일으킨 화제가 가라앉기는커녕, 제가 장담컨대 여전히 처음처럼 뜨겁습니다."], ["His first astounding act upon coming amongst us was to present a pair of horses, worth 32,000 francs, to Madame Danglars; his second, the almost miraculous preservation of Madame de Villefort’s life; now it seems that he has carried off the prize awarded by the Jockey Club.", "그분이 우리 사이에 나타나 처음 한 놀라운 일은 당글라르 부인께 삼만 이천 프랑짜리 말 두 필을 선사한 것이었지요. 두 번째는 빌포르 부인의 목숨을 거의 기적처럼 구한 일이었고요. 그런데 이제는 자키 클럽이 내건 상까지 거머쥔 모양입니다."], ["I therefore maintain, in spite of Morcerf, that not only is the count the object of interest at this present moment, but also that he will continue to be so for a month longer if he pleases to exhibit an eccentricity of conduct which, after all, may be his ordinary mode of existence.”", "그러니 저는 모르세르가 뭐라 해도 이렇게 주장하겠습니다. 백작은 지금 이 순간 관심의 대상일 뿐 아니라, 그가 보여 주는 — 어쩌면 그에게는 그저 일상적인 삶의 방식일지도 모르는 — 그 별난 행동을 계속 즐겨 보이는 한, 앞으로 한 달은 더 그러할 거라고요.\""]], [["“Perhaps you are right,”", "\"자네 말이 옳을지도 모르지.\""], ["said Morcerf; “meanwhile, who is in the Russian ambassador’s box?”", "모르세르가 말했다. \"그건 그렇고, 러시아 대사 칸막이석에는 누가 있나?\""]], [["“Which box do you mean?”", "\"어느 칸막이석을 말하는 건가?\""], ["asked the countess.", "백작 부인이 물었다."]], [["“The one between the pillars on the first tier—it seems to have been fitted up entirely afresh.”", "\"일층 기둥과 기둥 사이에 있는 자리요. 전부 새로 꾸민 것 같던데요.\""]], [["“Did you observe anyone during the first act?”", "\"1막 동안 거기서 누굴 보셨나요?\" 샤토르노가 물었다."], ["asked Château-Renaud.", "\"어디서요?\""]], [["“Where?” “In that box.”", "\"그 칸막이석에서요.\""]], [["“No,” replied the countess, “it was certainly empty during the first act;”", "\"아니요,\" 백작 부인이 대답했다."], ["then, resuming the subject of their previous conversation, she said, “And so you really believe it was your mysterious Count of Monte Cristo that gained the prize?”", "\"1막 동안엔 분명 비어 있었어요.\" 그러고는 앞서 나누던 화제로 돌아가 말했다. \"그래, 그 수수께끼 같은 몬테크리스토 백작이 정말로 상을 탔다고 믿으시는 거예요?\""]], [["“I am sure of it.”", "\"확신합니다.\""]], [["“And who afterwards sent the cup to me?”", "\"그리고 그 뒤에 저에게 잔을 보냈고요?\""]], [["“Undoubtedly.”", "\"틀림없이요.\""]], [["“But I don’t know him,”", "\"하지만 저는 그분을 모르는걸요.\""], ["said the countess; “I have a great mind to return it.”", "백작 부인이 말했다. \"잔을 돌려보낼까 봐요.\""]], [["“Do no such thing, I beg of you; he would only send you another, formed of a magnificent sapphire, or hollowed out of a gigantic ruby.", "\"제발 그러지 마십시오, 부탁드립니다. 그분은 또 다른 잔을 보낼 겁니다. 이번엔 근사한 사파이어로 만든 것이거나, 어마어마한 루비를 파서 만든 것이거나요."], ["It is his way, and you must take him as you find him.”", "그게 그분 방식이니, 그분은 있는 그대로 받아들이셔야 합니다.\""]], [["At this moment the bell rang to announce the drawing up of the curtain for the second act. Albert rose to return to his place.", "그 순간 2막의 막이 오른다는 종이 울렸다. 알베르가 제자리로 돌아가려고 일어섰다."]], [["“Shall I see you again?”", "\"또 뵐 수 있을까요?\""], ["asked the countess.", "백작 부인이 물었다."]], [["“At the end of the next act, with your permission, I will come and inquire whether there is anything I can do for you in Paris?”", "\"다음 막이 끝나면, 허락하신다면 다시 와서 파리에서 제가 부인을 위해 해 드릴 일이 없는지 여쭙겠습니다.\""]], [["“Pray take notice,”", "\"기억해 두세요.\""], ["said the countess, “that my present residence is 22 Rue de Rivoli, and that I am at home to my friends every Saturday evening.", "백작 부인이 말했다. \"제가 지금 머무는 곳은 리볼리 거리 22번지이고, 토요일 저녁마다 친구분들을 맞이한답니다."], ["So now, you are both forewarned.”", "자, 이제 두 분 다 미리 알려 드렸어요.\""]], [["The young men bowed, and quitted the box. Upon reaching their stalls, they found the whole of the audience in the parterre standing up and directing their gaze towards the box formerly possessed by the Russian ambassador.", "두 젊은이는 인사를 하고 칸막이석을 나섰다. 자기들 자리에 이르자, 일층 일반석 관객 전체가 일어서서 예전에 러시아 대사가 쓰던 칸막이석 쪽으로 시선을 던지고 있었다."], ["A man of from thirty-five to forty years of age, dressed in deep black, had just entered, accompanied by a young woman dressed after the Eastern style.", "서른다섯에서 마흔쯤 되어 보이는, 새까만 옷을 차려입은 남자가 막 들어선 참이었고, 동방식으로 차려입은 젊은 여인이 함께였다."], ["The lady was surpassingly beautiful, while the rich magnificence of her attire drew all eyes upon her.", "그 여인은 빼어나게 아름다웠고, 화려하고 호사스러운 옷차림이 모든 이의 시선을 끌었다."]], [["“Hullo,”", "\"이런,\""], ["said Albert; “it is Monte Cristo and his Greek!”", "알베르가 말했다. \"몬테크리스토와 그의 그리스 여인이로군!\""]], [["The strangers were, indeed, no other than the count and Haydée.", "그 낯선 두 사람은 과연 백작과 하이데였다."], ["In a few moments the young girl had attracted the attention of the whole house, and even the occupants of the boxes leaned forward to scrutinize her magnificent diamonds.", "잠시 후 젊은 처녀는 극장 전체의 주목을 끌었고, 칸막이석에 든 사람들조차 몸을 앞으로 기울여 그녀의 화려한 다이아몬드를 살펴보았다."]], [["The second act passed away during one continued buzz of voices—one deep whisper—intimating that some great and universally interesting event had occurred; all eyes, all thoughts, were occupied with the young and beautiful woman, whose gorgeous apparel and splendid jewels made a most extraordinary spectacle.", "2막은 끊임없는 웅성거림 속에서 흘러갔다. 무언가 거대하고 만인의 관심을 끄는 사건이 벌어졌음을 알리는, 나직하게 깔린 속삭임이었다. 모든 눈, 모든 생각이 그 젊고 아름다운 여인에게 쏠려 있었다. 그녀의 눈부신 의상과 찬란한 보석은 더없이 비범한 볼거리였다."]], [["Upon this occasion an unmistakable sign from Madame Danglars intimated her desire to see Albert in her box directly the curtain fell on the second act, and neither the politeness nor good taste of Morcerf would permit his neglecting an invitation so unequivocally given.", "이 무렵 당글라르 부인은 2막이 끝나 막이 내리는 즉시 알베르를 자기 칸막이석에서 보고 싶다는 뜻을 분명한 신호로 전했다. 그토록 분명하게 건네진 초대를 모르세르의 예의나 분별이 차마 외면하게 두지는 않았다."], ["At the close of the act he therefore went to the baroness.", "그래서 그는 막이 끝나자 남작 부인에게로 갔다."]], [["Having bowed to the two ladies, he extended his hand to Debray.", "두 부인에게 인사를 한 뒤, 그는 드브레에게 손을 내밀었다."], ["By the baroness he was most graciously welcomed, while Eugénie received him with her accustomed coldness.", "남작 부인은 더없이 다정하게 그를 맞이했고, 외제니는 늘 그렇듯 차갑게 그를 대했다."]], [["“My dear fellow,”", "\"여보게,\""], ["said Debray, “you have come in the nick of time.", "드브레가 말했다. \"마침 딱 좋은 때에 왔네. 여기 부인께서 백작에 관한 질문으로 나를 들볶고 계시지 뭔가."], ["There is madame overwhelming me with questions respecting the count; she insists upon it that I can tell her his birth, education, and parentage, where he came from, and whither he is going.", "부인은 내가 그자의 출생, 학력, 가문, 어디서 왔고 어디로 가는지까지 다 말해 줄 수 있다고 우기신다네. 나는 칼리오스트로의 제자도 아니니 도무지 그럴 수가 없었지. 그래서 곤경에서 빠져나오려고 이렇게 말했네."], ["Being no disciple of Cagliostro, I was wholly unable to do this; so, by way of getting out of the scrape, I said, ‘Ask Morcerf; he has got the whole history of his beloved Monte Cristo at his fingers’ ends;’ whereupon the baroness signified her desire to see you.”", "'모르세르에게 물어보십시오. 그자는 자기가 사랑하는 몬테크리스토 이야기라면 손바닥 들여다보듯 훤히 꿰고 있으니까요.' 그랬더니 부인께서 자네를 보고 싶다고 하셨네.\""]], [["“Is it not almost incredible,”", "\"거의 믿기지 않는 일 아닌가요.\""], ["said Madame Danglars, “that a person having at least half a million of secret-service money at his command, should possess so little information?”", "당글라르 부인이 말했다. \"적어도 오십만 프랑이나 되는 비밀 정보비를 마음대로 주무르는 분이 그토록 아는 게 없다니 말이에요.\""]], [["“Let me assure you, madame,”", "\"분명히 말씀드리는데요, 부인.\""], ["said Lucien, “that had I really the sum you mention at my disposal, I would employ it more profitably than in troubling myself to obtain particulars respecting the Count of Monte Cristo, whose only merit in my eyes consists in his being twice as rich as a nabob.", "뤼시앵이 말했다. \"제가 정말로 그만한 돈을 손에 쥐고 있다면, 몬테크리스토 백작에 관한 시시콜콜한 정보를 캐내느라 애쓰는 것보다는 더 보람 있게 쓸 겁니다. 제 눈에 그자의 유일한 장점이라곤 나바브의 두 배쯤 부자라는 것뿐이거든요."], ["However, I have turned the business over to Morcerf, so pray settle it with him as may be most agreeable to you; for my own part, I care nothing about the count or his mysterious doings.” 30093m", "어쨌든 그 일은 모르세르에게 넘겼으니, 부디 그와 마음껏 정리하시지요. 저로서는 백작이나 그의 알 수 없는 짓거리 따위엔 도통 관심이 없으니까요.\""]], [["“I am very sure no nabob would have sent me a pair of horses worth 32,000 francs, wearing on their heads four diamonds valued at 5,000 francs each.”", "\"제게 머리마다 오천 프랑짜리 다이아몬드를 넷씩 단 삼만 이천 프랑짜리 말 두 필을 보내 줄 나바브는 분명 없었을 거예요.\""]], [["“He seems to have a mania for diamonds,”", "\"그분은 다이아몬드에 미친 모양입니다.\""], ["said Morcerf, smiling, “and I verily believe that, like Potemkin, he keeps his pockets filled, for the sake of strewing them along the road, as Tom Thumb did his flint stones.”", "모르세르가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"정말이지 제 생각엔, 그분은 포템킨처럼 주머니마다 다이아몬드를 채워 두는 것 같아요. 엄지 동자가 부싯돌을 흘리듯, 길에 뿌리고 다니려고 말입니다.\""]], [["“Perhaps he has discovered some mine,”", "\"어쩌면 어디서 광산이라도 찾아냈는지 모르죠.\""], ["said Madame Danglars.", "당글라르 부인이 말했다."], ["“I suppose you know he has an order for unlimited credit on the baron’s banking establishment?”", "\"그분이 남작의 은행에 무제한 신용 거래 명령서를 가지고 있다는 거, 아세요?\""]], [["“I was not aware of it,”", "\"몰랐습니다.\" 알베르가 대답했다."], ["replied Albert, “but I can readily believe it.”", "\"하지만 충분히 믿을 만한 얘기로군요.\""]], [["“And, further, that he stated to M. Danglars his intention of only staying a year in Paris, during which time he proposed to spend six millions.", "\"게다가, 당글라르 씨에게 파리엔 일 년만 머물 작정이라고 했답니다. 그동안 육백만 프랑을 쓸 생각이라면서요.\""]], [["“He must be the Shah of Persia, travelling _incog_.”", "\"그분은 신분을 감추고 여행하는 페르시아의 샤가 분명하군요.\""]], [["“Have you noticed the remarkable beauty of the young woman, M. Lucien?”", "\"저 젊은 여인의 빼어난 아름다움을 눈여겨보셨어요, 뤼시앵 씨?\""], ["inquired Eugénie.", "외제니가 물었다."]], [["“I really never met with one woman so ready to do justice to the charms of another as yourself,”", "\"정말이지 저는 다른 여인의 아름다움을 아가씨만큼 기꺼이 인정하는 여인을 만나 본 적이 없습니다.\""], ["responded Lucien, raising his lorgnette to his eye.", "뤼시앵이 오페라글라스를 눈에 가져다 대며 대답했다."], ["“A most lovely creature, upon my soul!”", "\"맙소사, 더없이 사랑스러운 여인이군요!\""], ["was his verdict.", "그가 내린 평이었다."]], [["“Who is this young person, M. de Morcerf?”", "\"저 젊은 여인이 누구죠, 모르세르 씨?\""], ["inquired Eugénie; “does anybody know?”", "외제니가 물었다. \"누구 아는 사람 없나요?\""]], [["“Mademoiselle,”", "\"아가씨,\""], ["said Albert, replying to this direct appeal, “I can give you very exact information on that subject, as well as on most points relative to the mysterious person of whom we are now conversing—the young woman is a Greek.”", "알베르가 이 직접적인 물음에 답하며 말했다. \"그 문제에 관해서라면, 또 지금 우리가 화제로 삼고 있는 그 수수께끼 같은 인물에 관한 대부분의 점에 관해서라면, 제가 아주 정확한 정보를 드릴 수 있습니다. 저 젊은 여인은 그리스 사람입니다.\""]], [["“So I should suppose by her dress; if you know no more than that, everyone here is as well-informed as yourself.”", "\"옷차림을 보고 저도 그쯤은 짐작했어요. 그것밖에 모르신다면, 여기 있는 누구나 자작님만큼은 알고 있겠는걸요.\""]], [["“I am extremely sorry you find me so ignorant a _cicerone_,” replied Morcerf, “but I am reluctantly obliged to confess, I have nothing further to communicate—yes, stay, I do know one thing more, namely, that she is a musician, for one day when I chanced to be breakfasting with the count, I heard the sound of a guzla—it is impossible that it could have been touched by any other finger than her own.”", "\"제가 그토록 무지한 안내자라니 정말 죄송하군요.\" 모르세르가 대답했다. \"하지만 유감스럽게도 더 전해 드릴 게 없다고 고백할 수밖에요. 아, 잠깐만요, 한 가지 더 아는 게 있습니다. 그녀가 음악가라는 사실이죠. 언젠가 백작과 함께 아침을 들던 날, 구즐라 소리를 들은 적이 있거든요. 그 여인의 손가락이 아니고서야 누가 켰을 리 없는 소리였지요.\""]], [["“Then your count entertains visitors, does he?”", "\"그럼 백작이 손님을 맞이하기도 하나요?\""], ["asked Madame Danglars.", "당글라르 부인이 물었다."]], [["“Indeed he does, and in a most lavish manner, I can assure you.”", "\"물론입니다. 그것도 더없이 후하게 맞이하지요. 장담합니다.\""]], [["“I must try and persuade M. Danglars to invite him to a ball or dinner, or something of the sort, that he may be compelled to ask us in return.”", "\"당글라르 씨를 설득해서 그분을 무도회나 만찬 같은 데 초대해야겠어요. 그래야 그분도 답례로 우리를 부르지 않을 수 없을 테니까요.\""]], [["“What,”", "\"뭐라고요?\""], ["said Debray, laughing; “do you really mean you would go to his house?”", "드브레가 웃으며 말했다. \"정말로 그분 댁에 가시겠다는 겁니까?\""]], [["“Why not? my husband could accompany me.”", "\"안 될 게 뭐예요? 남편이 함께 가면 되지요.\""]], [["“But do you know this mysterious count is a bachelor?”", "\"하지만 그 수수께끼 같은 백작이 독신이라는 거, 아십니까?\""]], [["“You have ample proof to the contrary, if you look opposite,”", "\"맞은편을 보시면 그렇지 않다는 충분한 증거가 있지요.\""], ["said the baroness, as she laughingly pointed to the beautiful Greek.", "남작 부인이 웃으며 그 아름다운 그리스 여인을 가리켰다."]], [["“No, no!”", "\"아니, 아닙니다!\""], ["exclaimed Debray; “that girl is not his wife: he told us himself she was his slave.", "드브레가 외쳤다. \"저 여인은 그의 아내가 아니에요. 그자가 직접 우리에게 자기 노예라고 했지요."], ["Do you not recollect, Morcerf, his telling us so at your breakfast?”", "모르세르, 자네 집에서 아침 들 때 그가 그렇게 말한 거 기억 안 나나?\""]], [["“Well, then,”", "\"그래요,\""], ["said the baroness, “if slave she be, she has all the air and manner of a princess.”", "남작 부인이 말했다. \"노예라 해도, 저 여인은 영락없이 공주 같은 분위기와 자태를 지니고 있군요.\""]], [["“Of the ‘Arabian Nights’.”", "\"『아라비안나이트』에 나올 법한 공주죠.\""]], [["“If you like; but tell me, my dear Lucien, what it is that constitutes a princess.", "\"좋으실 대로요. 하지만 말해 보세요, 뤼시앵. 공주를 공주답게 만드는 게 뭐죠?"], ["Why, diamonds—and she is covered with them.”", "바로 다이아몬드예요. 그런데 저 여인은 온통 다이아몬드로 덮여 있잖아요.\""]], [["“To me she seems overloaded,”", "\"제 눈엔 좀 과해 보이는데요.\""], ["observed Eugénie; “she would look far better if she wore fewer, and we should then be able to see her finely formed throat and wrists.”", "외제니가 한마디 했다. \"좀 덜 걸쳤더라면 훨씬 보기 좋았을 거예요. 그러면 곱게 빠진 목과 손목도 볼 수 있었을 텐데요.\""]], [["“See how the artist peeps out!” exclaimed Madame Danglars.", "\"저것 좀 봐, 예술가 기질이 다 드러나는구나!\" 당글라르 부인이 외쳤다."], ["“My poor Eugénie, you must conceal your passion for the fine arts.”", "\"가엾은 우리 외제니, 너는 예술을 향한 그 열정을 좀 감추어야 한단다.\""]], [["“I admire all that is beautiful,”", "\"저는 아름다운 건 뭐든 찬미해요.\""], ["returned the young lady.", "젊은 아가씨가 대꾸했다."]], [["“What do you think of the count?”", "\"백작은 어떻게 보십니까?\""], ["inquired Debray; “he is not much amiss, according to my ideas of good looks.”", "드브레가 물었다. \"제 잘생김의 기준으로 보면 그리 나쁘진 않던데요.\""]], [["“The count,”", "\"백작이라.\""], ["repeated Eugénie, as though it had not occurred to her to observe him sooner; “the count?—oh, he is so dreadfully pale.”", "외제니가 마치 그를 더 일찍 살펴볼 생각은 하지도 못했다는 듯 되뇌었다. \"백작이요? 아, 그분은 너무 끔찍하게 창백하던데요.\""]], [["“I quite agree with you,”", "\"저도 같은 생각입니다.\""], ["said Morcerf; “and the secret of that very pallor is what we want to find out.", "모르세르가 말했다. \"그리고 바로 그 창백함의 비밀이야말로 우리가 알아내고 싶은 거지요."], ["The Countess G—— insists upon it that he is a vampire.”", "G 백작 부인은 그분이 흡혈귀라고 우기던걸요.\""]], [["“Then the Countess G—— has returned to Paris, has she?”", "\"그럼 G 백작 부인이 파리로 돌아왔단 말인가요?\""], ["inquired the baroness.", "남작 부인이 물었다."]], [["“Is that she, mamma?”", "\"엄마, 저 사람이에요?\""], ["asked Eugénie; “almost opposite to us, with that profusion of beautiful light hair?”", "외제니가 물었다. \"우리 거의 맞은편에, 곱고 풍성한 금발을 늘어뜨린 저분이요?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["said Madame Danglars, “that is she.", "당글라르 부인이 말했다. \"저 사람이 바로 그 부인이야."], ["Shall I tell you what you ought to do, Morcerf?”", "모르세르, 자네가 어떻게 하면 좋을지 말해 줄까?\""]], [["“Command me, madame.”", "\"분부만 하십시오, 부인.\""]], [["“Well, then, you should go and bring your Count of Monte Cristo to us.”", "\"그럼 가서 자네의 그 몬테크리스토 백작을 우리에게 데려오게.\""]], [["“What for?”", "\"무엇 하러요?\""], ["asked Eugénie.", "외제니가 물었다."]], [["“What for? Why, to converse with him, of course.", "\"무엇 하러라니? 당연히 그분과 이야기를 나누려고지."], ["Have you really no desire to meet him?”", "너는 정말 그분을 만나고 싶지 않니?\""]], [["“None whatever,”", "\"조금도요.\""], ["replied Eugénie.", "외제니가 대답했다."]], [["“Strange child,”", "\"별난 아이로구나.\""], ["murmured the baroness.", "남작 부인이 중얼거렸다."]], [["“He will very probably come of his own accord,” said Morcerf.", "\"아마 그분은 제 발로 올 겁니다.\" 모르세르가 말했다."], ["“There; do you see, madame, he recognizes you, and bows.”", "\"저것 보세요, 부인. 그분이 부인을 알아보고 인사하는군요.\""]], [["The baroness returned the salute in the most smiling and graceful manner.", "남작 부인이 더없이 환하고 우아한 태도로 인사를 받았다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"좋습니다.\""], ["said Morcerf, “I may as well be magnanimous, and tear myself away to forward your wishes.", "모르세르가 말했다. \"기왕이면 제가 너그러운 마음으로, 부인의 뜻을 받들기 위해 자리를 박차고 나서지요."], ["Adieu; I will go and try if there are any means of speaking to him.”", "그럼 이만. 가서 그분과 이야기를 나눌 방법이 있는지 알아보겠습니다.\""]], [["“Go straight to his box; that will be the simplest plan.”", "\"곧장 그분 칸막이석으로 가게. 그게 가장 간단한 방법일 테니.\""]], [["“But I have never been presented.”", "\"하지만 저는 인사를 나눈 적이 없는데요.\""]], [["“Presented to whom?”", "\"누구한테 인사를 나눠?\""]], [["“To the beautiful Greek.”", "\"그 아름다운 그리스 여인한테요.\""]], [["“You say she is only a slave?”", "\"그 여인은 노예일 뿐이라면서요?\""]], [["“While you assert that she is a queen, or at least a princess.", "\"그건 부인이 그 여인을 여왕, 적어도 공주라고 우기시기 때문이죠."], ["No; I hope that when he sees me leave you, he will come out.”", "아니요, 제가 부인 곁을 떠나는 걸 그분이 보면, 직접 나오리라 기대합니다.\""]], [["“That is possible—go.”", "\"그럴 수도 있겠지. 가 보게.\""]], [["“I am going,”", "\"가 봅니다.\""], ["said Albert, as he made his parting bow.", "알베르가 작별 인사를 하며 말했다."]], [["Just as he was passing the count’s box, the door opened, and Monte Cristo came forth.", "그가 백작의 칸막이석을 막 지나치려는데 문이 열리며 몬테크리스토가 밖으로 나왔다."], ["After giving some directions to Ali, who stood in the lobby, the count took Albert’s arm.", "복도에 서 있던 알리에게 몇 가지 지시를 내린 뒤, 백작은 알베르의 팔을 잡았다."], ["Carefully closing the box door, Ali placed himself before it, while a crowd of spectators assembled round the Nubian.", "알리는 칸막이석 문을 조심스레 닫고 그 앞을 막아섰고, 그 사이 구경꾼들 한 무리가 그 누비아인 주위로 몰려들었다."]], [["“Upon my word,”", "\"정말이지,\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “Paris is a strange city, and the Parisians a very singular people.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"파리는 묘한 도시이고, 파리 사람들은 아주 별난 족속이군요."], ["See that cluster of persons collected around poor Ali, who is as much astonished as themselves; really one might suppose he was the only Nubian they had ever beheld.", "가엾은 알리 주위에 몰려든 저 사람들 좀 보십시오. 알리도 저들 못지않게 어리둥절해하고 있지요. 정말이지 알리가 저들이 여태껏 본 유일한 누비아인이라고 여길 만하군요."], ["Now I can promise you, that a Frenchman might show himself in public, either in Tunis, Constantinople, Bagdad, or Cairo, without being treated in that way.”", "그런데 장담컨대, 프랑스인이 튀니스든 콘스탄티노플이든 바그다드든 카이로든 사람들 앞에 나선다 해도 저런 대접은 받지 않을 겁니다.\""]], [["“That shows that the Eastern nations have too much good sense to waste their time and attention on objects undeserving of either.", "\"그건 동방 사람들이 분별이 너무 깊어서, 그럴 가치도 없는 것에 시간과 관심을 허비하지 않기 때문이지요."], ["However, as far as Ali is concerned, I can assure you, the interest he excites is merely from the circumstance of his being your attendant—you, who are at this moment the most celebrated and fashionable person in Paris.”", "하지만 알리에 관해서라면, 그가 끄는 관심은 순전히 그가 백작님의 시종이기 때문이라는 걸 장담할 수 있습니다. 백작님이야말로 지금 이 순간 파리에서 가장 유명하고 가장 유행을 타는 인물이시니까요.\""]], [["“Really? and what has procured me so flattering a distinction?”", "\"정말입니까? 그래, 무엇이 제게 그토록 우쭐한 영예를 안겨 주었답니까?\""]], [["“What? why, yourself, to be sure!", "\"무엇이라니요? 바로 백작님 자신이지요!"], ["You give away horses worth a thousand louis; you save the lives of ladies of high rank and beauty; under the name of Major Black you run thoroughbreds ridden by tiny urchins not larger than marmots; then, when you have carried off the golden trophy of victory, instead of setting any value on it, you give it to the first handsome woman you think of!”", "천 루이짜리 말을 거저 주시고, 지체 높고 아름다운 부인들의 목숨을 구하시고, 블랙 소령이라는 이름으로 마멋만큼이나 자그마한 꼬마들이 모는 순종마를 출전시키시고, 게다가 그 황금 우승 트로피를 거머쥐고도 거기에 아무 값어치도 두지 않으시고는 제일 먼저 떠오른 미인에게 줘 버리시고!\""]], [["“And who has filled your head with all this nonsense?”", "\"대체 누가 자네 머릿속에 그런 허튼소리를 잔뜩 채워 넣었답니까?\""]], [["“Why, in the first place, I heard it from Madame Danglars, who, by the by, is dying to see you in her box, or to have you seen there by others; secondly, I learned it from Beauchamp’s journal; and thirdly, from my own imagination.", "\"우선은 당글라르 부인한테서 들었습니다. 그분은, 그러고 보니, 백작님을 자기 칸막이석에서 보거나, 아니면 백작님이 거기 계신 모습을 남들에게 보여 주고 싶어 안달이 나 있지요. 둘째로는 보샹의 신문에서 알았고, 셋째로는 제 상상에서요."], ["Why, if you sought concealment, did you call your horse Vampa?”", "아니, 정체를 숨기고 싶으셨다면 어째서 말 이름을 밤파라고 지으셨습니까?\""]], [["“That was an oversight, certainly,”", "\"그건 분명 부주의한 짓이었지요.\""], ["replied the count; “but tell me, does the Count of Morcerf never visit the Opera?", "백작이 대답했다. \"그런데 말씀해 보십시오, 모르세르 백작은 오페라에 통 안 오십니까?"], ["I have been looking for him, but without success.”", "한참 찾아봤는데 보이질 않는군요.\""]], [["“He will be here tonight.”", "\"오늘 밤 오실 겁니다.\""]], [["“In what part of the house?”", "\"극장 어느 자리에요?\""]], [["“In the baroness’s box, I believe.”", "\"남작 부인의 칸막이석일 겁니다.\""]], [["“That charming young woman with her is her daughter?”", "\"부인과 함께 있는 그 매력적인 젊은 여인이 따님인가요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“I congratulate you.”", "\"축하드립니다.\""]], [["Morcerf smiled.", "모르세르가 미소 지었다."]], [["“We will discuss that subject at length some future time,”", "\"그 얘기는 언제 따로 길게 나누지요.\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he. “But what do you think of the music?”", "\"그건 그렇고, 음악은 어떻게 들으셨습니까?\""]], [["“What music?”", "\"무슨 음악이요?\""]], [["“Why, the music you have been listening to.”", "\"왜요, 방금까지 듣고 계시던 음악 말입니다.\""]], [["“Oh, it is well enough as the production of a human composer, sung by featherless bipeds, to quote the late Diogenes.”", "\"아, 인간 작곡가가 만들고, 작고한 디오게네스의 말을 빌리자면 깃털 없는 두 발 짐승들이 부르는 작품치고는 그런대로 괜찮더군요.\""]], [["“From which it would seem, my dear count, that you can at pleasure enjoy the seraphic strains that proceed from the seven choirs of paradise?” 30097m", "\"그렇다면, 백작님께서는 마음만 먹으면 천국의 일곱 합창대가 들려주는 천사 같은 선율도 즐기실 수 있다는 말씀처럼 들리는군요?\""]], [["“You are right, in some degree; when I wish to listen to sounds more exquisitely attuned to melody than mortal ear ever yet listened to, I go to sleep.”", "\"어느 정도는 옳은 말입니다. 죽을 운명의 귀로는 여태껏 들어 본 적 없는, 가락에 더없이 절묘하게 맞추어진 소리를 듣고 싶을 땐, 저는 잠을 잡니다.\""]], [["“Then sleep here, my dear count.", "\"그럼 여기서 주무십시오, 백작님."], ["The conditions are favorable; what else was opera invented for?”", "조건이 안성맞춤이지 않습니까. 오페라가 달리 무엇 하러 생겨났겠습니까?\""]], [["“No, thank you.", "\"아니요, 됐습니다."], ["Your orchestra is too noisy.", "댁의 오케스트라는 너무 시끄럽군요."], ["To sleep after the manner I speak of, absolute calm and silence are necessary, and then a certain preparation——”", "제가 말한 식으로 잠들려면 완벽한 고요와 정적이 필요하고, 그다음엔 어떤 준비가 필요하지요……\""]], [["“I know—the famous hashish!”", "\"알겠습니다. 그 유명한 해시시 말씀이군요!\""]], [["“Precisely. So, my dear viscount, whenever you wish to be regaled with music come and sup with me.”", "\"바로 그겁니다. 그러니 자작, 음악으로 흥을 돋우고 싶으실 때면 언제든 저와 함께 밤참을 드시지요.\""]], [["“I have already enjoyed that treat when breakfasting with you,” said Morcerf.", "\"이미 백작님과 아침을 들 때 그 호사를 누려 봤는걸요.\" 모르세르가 말했다."]], [["“Do you mean at Rome?”", "\"로마에서 말입니까?\""]], [["“I do.”", "\"그렇습니다.\""]], [["“Ah, then, I suppose you heard Haydée’s guzla; the poor exile frequently beguiles a weary hour in playing over to me the airs of her native land.”", "\"아, 그렇다면 하이데의 구즐라를 들으셨겠군요. 그 가엾은 망명자는 곧잘 고향의 가락을 제게 켜 주며 따분한 시간을 달래곤 하지요.\""]], [["Morcerf did not pursue the subject, and Monte Cristo himself fell into a silent reverie.", "모르세르는 그 화제를 더 이어 가지 않았고, 몬테크리스토도 말없는 상념에 잠겼다."]], [["The bell rang at this moment for the rising of the curtain.", "그 순간 막이 오른다는 종이 울렸다."]], [["“You will excuse my leaving you,”", "\"이만 자리를 비켜 줘야겠습니다.\""], ["said the count, turning in the direction of his box.", "백작이 자기 칸막이석 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며 말했다."]], [["“What?", "\"네?"], ["Are you going?”", "가시려고요?\""]], [["“Pray, say everything that is kind to Countess G—— on the part of her friend the vampire.”", "\"부디 G 백작 부인께, 그 친구인 흡혈귀가 보내는 온갖 다정한 인사를 전해 주십시오.\""]], [["“And what message shall I convey to the baroness!”", "\"그럼 남작 부인께는 무슨 전갈을 전할까요!\""]], [["“That, with her permission, I shall do myself the honor of paying my respects in the course of the evening.”", "\"허락하신다면, 저녁 동안 찾아뵙고 인사드리는 영광을 누리겠다고요.\""]], [["The third act had begun; and during its progress the Count of Morcerf, according to his promise, made his appearance in the box of Madame Danglars.", "3막이 시작되었다. 그 막이 진행되는 동안 모르세르 백작은 약속대로 당글라르 부인의 칸막이석에 모습을 드러냈다."], ["The Count of Morcerf was not a person to excite either interest or curiosity in a place of public amusement; his presence, therefore, was wholly unnoticed, save by the occupants of the box in which he had just seated himself.", "모르세르 백작은 공공의 오락장에서 관심이나 호기심을 불러일으킬 만한 인물이 아니었다. 그래서 그의 등장은 방금 자리에 든 그 칸막이석 사람들 말고는 아무도 눈여겨보지 않았다."]], [["The quick eye of Monte Cristo however, marked his coming; and a slight though meaning smile passed over his lips.", "그러나 몬테크리스토의 날카로운 눈은 그가 오는 것을 놓치지 않았고, 의미심장하면서도 옅은 미소가 그의 입가를 스쳤다."], ["Haydée, whose soul seemed centred in the business of the stage, like all unsophisticated natures, delighted in whatever addressed itself to the eye or ear.", "하이데는 영혼이 온통 무대 위 일에 쏠려 있는 듯했다. 꾸밈없는 모든 천성이 그러하듯, 그녀는 눈과 귀를 즐겁게 하는 것이라면 무엇에나 빠져들었다."]], [["30099m\nThe third act passed off as usual.", "3막은 여느 때처럼 흘러갔다."], ["Mesdemoiselles Noblet, Julia, and Leroux executed the customary pirouettes; Robert duly challenged the Prince of Granada; and the royal father of the princess Isabella, taking his daughter by the hand, swept round the stage with majestic strides, the better to display the rich folds of his velvet robe and mantle.", "노블레 양, 쥘리아 양, 르루 양이 으레 추는 피루엣을 선보였고, 로베르는 어김없이 그라나다 왕자에게 결투를 신청했으며, 이사벨라 공주의 부왕은 딸의 손을 잡고 위풍당당한 걸음으로 무대를 누비며 벨벳 가운과 망토의 풍성한 주름을 한껏 뽐냈다."], ["After which the curtain again fell, and the spectators poured forth from the theatre into the lobbies and salon.", "그런 뒤 다시 막이 내렸고, 관객들은 극장에서 복도와 살롱으로 쏟아져 나왔다."]], [["The count left his box, and a moment later was saluting the Baronne Danglars, who could not restrain a cry of mingled pleasure and surprise.", "백작은 자기 칸막이석을 나섰고, 잠시 후 당글라르 남작 부인에게 인사를 건넸다. 부인은 반가움과 놀라움이 뒤섞인 탄성을 억누르지 못했다."]], [["“You are welcome, count!”", "\"어서 오세요, 백작님!\""], ["she exclaimed, as he entered.", "그가 들어서자 부인이 외쳤다."], ["“I have been most anxious to see you, that I might repeat orally the thanks writing can so ill express.”", "\"정말이지 백작님을 뵙고 싶어 안달이 났답니다. 글로는 도무지 다 표현하지 못한 감사의 말을 직접 입으로 전하고 싶어서요.\""]], [["“Surely so trifling a circumstance cannot deserve a place in your remembrance.", "\"그토록 사소한 일이 부인의 기억에 자리를 차지할 가치는 없을 텐데요."], ["Believe me, madame, I had entirely forgotten it.”", "믿어 주십시오, 부인, 저는 그 일을 까맣게 잊고 있었습니다.\""]], [["“But it is not so easy to forget, monsieur, that the very next day after your princely gift you saved the life of my dear friend, Madame de Villefort, which was endangered by the very animals your generosity restored to me.” 30101m", "\"하지만, 백작님, 그 어진 선물을 주신 바로 다음 날, 제 소중한 벗 빌포르 부인의 목숨을 구해 주셨다는 건 그렇게 쉽게 잊히는 일이 아니랍니다. 게다가 그 부인을 위태롭게 한 게 바로 백작님의 너그러움으로 제게 돌아온 그 말들이었으니까요.\""]], [["“This time, at least, I do not deserve your thanks.", "\"적어도 이번만큼은 부인의 감사를 받을 자격이 없습니다."], ["It was Ali, my Nubian slave, who rendered this service to Madame de Villefort.”", "빌포르 부인께 그런 도움을 드린 건 제 누비아인 노예 알리였으니까요.\""]], [["“Was it Ali,”", "\"제 아들을 산적들의 손에서 구해 준 게,\""], ["asked the Count of Morcerf, “who rescued my son from the hands of bandits?”", "모르세르 백작이 물었다. \"그 알리였습니까?\""]], [["“No, count,”", "\"아닙니다, 백작님.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo taking the hand held out to him by the general; “in this instance I may fairly and freely accept your thanks; but you have already tendered them, and fully discharged your debt—if indeed there existed one—and I feel almost mortified to find you still reverting to the subject.", "몬테크리스토가 장군이 내민 손을 잡으며 대답했다. \"그 일에 관해서라면 저는 마땅히 거리낌 없이 감사를 받겠습니다. 하지만 이미 감사의 뜻을 전하셨고, 빚이 — 정말 빚이라는 게 있었다면 — 다 갚으셨지요."], ["May I beg of you, baroness, to honor me with an introduction to your daughter?”", "그런데도 백작님께서 자꾸 그 일로 돌아오시니 저로서는 거의 민망할 지경입니다. 남작 부인, 따님을 제게 소개해 주시는 영광을 베풀어 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“Oh, you are no stranger—at least not by name,”", "\"아, 백작님은 낯선 분이 아니랍니다. 적어도 함자만큼은요.\""], ["replied Madame Danglars, “and the last two or three days we have really talked of nothing but you. Eugénie,”", "당글라르 부인이 대답했다. \"지난 이삼 일 동안 우리가 백작님 얘기밖에 안 했거든요. 외제니,\""], ["continued the baroness, turning towards her daughter, “this is the Count of Monte Cristo.”", "남작 부인이 딸 쪽으로 돌아서며 말을 이었다. \"이분이 몬테크리스토 백작님이시다.\""]], [["The count bowed, while Mademoiselle Danglars bent her head slightly.", "백작이 인사하자, 당글라르 양은 살짝 고개를 숙였다."]], [["“You have a charming young person with you tonight, count,”", "\"오늘 밤 백작님과 함께 온 젊은 여인이 매력적이던데요.\""], ["said Eugénie. “Is she your daughter?”", "외제니가 말했다. \"따님이신가요?\""]], [["“No, mademoiselle,”", "\"아닙니다, 아가씨.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, astonished at the coolness and freedom of the question.", "몬테크리스토가 그 질문의 거리낌 없고 당돌함에 놀라며 말했다."], ["“She is a poor unfortunate Greek left under my care.”", "\"제가 맡아 돌보는 가엾고 불행한 그리스 여인입니다.\""]], [["“And what is her name?”", "\"이름이 뭐죠?\""]], [["“Haydée,” replied Monte Cristo.", "\"하이데입니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다."]], [["“A Greek?” murmured the Count of Morcerf.", "\"그리스 여인이라.\" 모르세르 백작이 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Yes, indeed, count,”", "\"그럼요, 백작님.\""], ["said Madame Danglars; “and tell me, did you ever see at the court of Ali Tepelini, whom you so gloriously and valiantly served, a more exquisite beauty or richer costume?”", "당글라르 부인이 말했다. \"그런데 말씀해 보세요. 백작님이 그토록 영광스럽고 용감하게 섬기셨다는 알리 테펠리니의 궁정에서, 저보다 더 빼어난 미인이나 더 화려한 의상을 보신 적이 있으신가요?\""]], [["“Did I hear rightly, monsieur,”", "\"제가 제대로 들었는지요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo “that you served at Yanina?”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"야니나에서 복무하셨다고요?\""]], [["“I was inspector-general of the pasha’s troops,”", "\"파샤 군대의 총감독관이었습니다.\""], ["replied Morcerf; “and it is no secret that I owe my fortune, such as it is, to the liberality of the illustrious Albanese chief.”", "모르세르가 대답했다. \"그리고 변변찮으나마 제 재산이 그 이름난 알바니아 수장의 후한 인심 덕이라는 건 비밀도 아니지요.\""]], [["“But look!” exclaimed Madame Danglars.", "\"하지만 저것 좀 보세요!\" 당글라르 부인이 외쳤다."]], [["“Where?” stammered Morcerf.", "\"어디 말입니까?\" 모르세르가 더듬거렸다."]], [["“There,”", "\"저기요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo placing his arms around the count, and leaning with him over the front of the box, just as Haydée, whose eyes were occupied in examining the theatre in search of her guardian, perceived his pale features close to Morcerf’s face.", "몬테크리스토가 백작의 어깨에 팔을 두르고 함께 칸막이석 앞으로 몸을 기울이며 말했다. 마침 하이데가 보호자를 찾아 눈으로 극장을 살피다가, 모르세르의 얼굴 가까이 있는 그의 창백한 이목구비를 알아본 순간이었다."], ["It was as if the young girl beheld the head of Medusa.", "마치 젊은 처녀가 메두사의 머리를 본 것 같았다."], ["She bent forwards as though to assure herself of the reality of what she saw, then, uttering a faint cry, threw herself back in her seat.", "그녀는 자기가 본 것이 진짜인지 확인하려는 듯 몸을 앞으로 기울였다가, 희미한 비명을 지르며 자리에 몸을 던지듯 기댔다."], ["The sound was heard by the people about Ali, who instantly opened the box-door.", "그 소리를 알리 곁에 있던 사람들이 들었고, 알리는 즉시 칸막이석 문을 열었다."]], [["“Why, count,”", "\"아니, 백작님.\""], ["exclaimed Eugénie, “what has happened to your ward?", "외제니가 외쳤다. \"보호하시는 분께 무슨 일이 생겼나요?"], ["she seems to have been taken suddenly ill.", "갑자기 몸이 안 좋아진 것 같은데요.\""]], [["“Very probably,”", "\"아마 그럴 겁니다.\""], ["answered the count.", "백작이 대답했다."], ["“But do not be alarmed on her account.", "\"하지만 저 아이 때문에 놀라지는 마십시오."], ["Haydée’s nervous system is delicately organized, and she is peculiarly susceptible to the odors even of flowers—nay, there are some which cause her to faint if brought into her presence.", "하이데는 신경이 섬세하게 짜여 있어서, 꽃향기에도 유난히 예민하답니다. 아니, 어떤 꽃은 곁에 가져오기만 해도 까무러치게 만들지요. 하지만,\""], ["However,” continued Monte Cristo, drawing a small phial from his pocket, “I have an infallible remedy.”", "몬테크리스토가 주머니에서 작은 약병을 꺼내며 말을 이었다. \"저에게는 틀림없는 약이 있습니다.\""]], [["So saying, he bowed to the baroness and her daughter, exchanged a parting shake of the hand with Debray and the count, and left Madame Danglars’ box.", "그렇게 말하며 그는 남작 부인과 그 딸에게 인사하고, 드브레와 백작과 작별의 악수를 나눈 뒤 당글라르 부인의 칸막이석을 떠났다."], ["Upon his return to Haydée he found her still very pale.", "하이데에게 돌아와 보니 그녀는 여전히 몹시 창백했다."], ["As soon as she saw him she seized his hand; her own hands were moist and icy cold.", "그녀는 그를 보자마자 그의 손을 움켜쥐었다. 그녀의 두 손은 축축하고 얼음장처럼 차가웠다."]], [["“Who was it you were talking with over there?” she asked.", "\"저기서 누구와 이야기를 나누고 계셨어요?\" 그녀가 물었다."]], [["“With the Count of Morcerf,”", "\"모르세르 백작과.\""], ["answered Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다."], ["“He tells me he served your illustrious father, and that he owes his fortune to him.”", "\"네 위대하신 아버지를 섬겼고, 그분 덕에 재산을 얻었다는구나.\""]], [["“Wretch!”", "\"비열한 자!\""], ["exclaimed Haydée, her eyes flashing with rage; “he sold my father to the Turks, and the fortune he boasts of was the price of his treachery!", "하이데가 분노로 눈을 번뜩이며 외쳤다. \"그자가 제 아버지를 터키인들에게 팔아넘겼고, 그자가 뽐내는 재산은 그 배신의 대가였어요!"], ["Did not you know that, my dear lord?”", "모르고 계셨나요, 주인님?\""]], [["“Something of this I heard in Epirus,”", "\"그 비슷한 이야기를 에피루스에서 들은 적은 있다.\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “but the particulars are still unknown to me.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"하지만 자세한 사정은 아직 모르지."], ["You shall relate them to me, my child.", "네가 내게 들려주려무나, 얘야."], ["They are, no doubt, both curious and interesting.”", "분명 기이하면서도 흥미로운 이야기일 테니.\""]], [["“Yes, yes; but let us go.", "\"네, 네. 하지만 이만 가요."], ["I feel as though it would kill me to remain long near that dreadful man.”", "저 끔찍한 자 곁에 오래 있다가는 죽을 것만 같아요.\""]], [["So saying, Haydée arose, and wrapping herself in her burnouse of white cashmere embroidered with pearls and coral, she hastily quitted the box at the moment when the curtain was rising upon the fourth act.", "그렇게 말하며 하이데는 일어나, 진주와 산호로 수놓은 흰 캐시미어 부르누스를 둘러쓰고는, 4막의 막이 오르는 순간 서둘러 칸막이석을 떠났다."]], [["“Do you observe,”", "\"보셨어요?\" G 백작 부인이 자기 곁으로 돌아온 알베르에게 말했다."], ["said the Countess G—— to Albert, who had returned to her side, “that man does nothing like other people; he listens most devoutly to the third act of _Robert le Diable_, and when the fourth begins, takes his departure.”", "\"저 남자는 하나같이 남들과 다르게 행동하네요. 「로베르 르 디아블」 3막은 그토록 경건하게 듣더니, 4막이 시작되자 자리를 떠 버리잖아요.\""]], [["# Chapter 54. A Flurry in Stocks", "# Chapter 54. A Flurry in Stocks"]], [["Some days after this meeting, Albert de Morcerf visited the Count of Monte Cristo at his house in the Champs-Élysées, which had already assumed that palace-like appearance which the count’s princely fortune enabled him to give even to his most temporary residences.", "이 만남이 있고 며칠 뒤, 알베르 드 모르세르는 샹젤리제에 있는 몬테크리스토 백작의 저택을 찾아갔다. 그 저택은 이미 궁전 같은 면모를 갖추고 있었는데, 백작은 왕후 같은 재력으로 아무리 잠시 머무는 거처라 해도 그런 풍모를 부여할 수 있는 사람이었다."], ["He came to renew the thanks of Madame Danglars which had been already conveyed to the count through the medium of a letter, signed “Baronne Danglars, _née_ Hermine de Servieux.”", "알베르는 당글라르 부인이 백작에게 전하는 감사 인사를 다시 한번 전하러 온 것이었다. 그 인사는 이미 \"당글라르 남작 부인, 본명 에르민 드 세르비외\"라고 서명된 편지로 백작에게 전달된 바 있었다."]], [["Albert was accompanied by Lucien Debray, who, joining in his friend’s conversation, added some passing compliments, the source of which the count’s talent for finesse easily enabled him to guess.", "알베르는 뤼시앵 드브레와 함께였다. 뤼시앵은 친구의 대화에 끼어들어 가볍게 몇 마디 칭찬을 보탰는데, 백작은 워낙 사람의 속을 꿰뚫어 보는 재주가 있어 그 말의 출처를 쉽게 짐작했다."], ["He was convinced that Lucien’s visit was due to a double feeling of curiosity, the larger half of which sentiment emanated from the Rue de la Chaussée d’Antin.", "백작은 뤼시앵의 방문이 두 겹의 호기심에서 비롯되었으며, 그 절반 이상이 쇼세당탱 거리에서 나온 것임을 확신했다."], ["In short, Madame Danglars, not being able personally to examine in detail the domestic economy and household arrangements of a man who gave away horses worth 30,000 francs and who went to the opera with a Greek slave wearing diamonds to the amount of a million of money, had deputed those eyes, by which she was accustomed to see, to give her a faithful account of the mode of life of this incomprehensible person.", "요컨대 당글라르 부인은 3만 프랑짜리 말을 선뜻 내주고, 백만 프랑어치 다이아몬드를 두른 그리스 노예를 데리고 오페라에 가는 사내의 살림살이와 집안 형편을 직접 세세히 살펴볼 수 없었으므로, 늘 자기 대신 눈 노릇을 해 주던 사람을 보내 그 헤아릴 수 없는 인물의 생활상을 충실히 전해 듣고자 한 것이었다."], ["But the count did not appear to suspect that there could be the slightest connection between Lucien’s visit and the curiosity of the baroness.", "그러나 백작은 뤼시앵의 방문과 남작 부인의 호기심 사이에 조금이라도 연관이 있으리라고는 전혀 눈치채지 못한 듯 보였다."]], [["“You are in constant communication with the Baron Danglars?”", "\"당글라르 남작과 계속 연락을 주고받고 계십니까?\""], ["the count inquired of Albert de Morcerf.", "백작이 알베르 드 모르세르에게 물었다."]], [["“Yes, count, you know what I told you?”", "\"네, 백작님. 제가 말씀드린 거 기억하시지요?\""]], [["“All remains the same, then, in that quarter?”", "\"그럼 그쪽 일은 여전히 그대로입니까?\""]], [["“It is more than ever a settled thing,”", "\"그 어느 때보다 확정된 일이지요.\""], ["said Lucien,—and, considering that this remark was all that he was at that time called upon to make, he adjusted the glass to his eye, and biting the top of his gold headed cane, began to make the tour of the apartment, examining the arms and the pictures.", "뤼시앵이 말했다. 그러고는 지금 자기가 해야 할 말은 그게 전부라고 여겼는지, 외알 안경을 눈에 맞춰 끼우고 금장식 지팡이 끝을 깨물며 방 안을 한 바퀴 돌기 시작했다. 무기며 그림들을 살펴보면서였다."]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["said Monte Cristo “I did not expect that the affair would be so promptly concluded.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그 일이 그렇게 빨리 매듭지어지리라곤 생각지 못했군요.\""]], [["“Oh, things take their course without our assistance.", "\"오, 일이란 우리가 거들지 않아도 제 갈 길을 가는 법이지요."], ["While we are forgetting them, they are falling into their appointed order; and when, again, our attention is directed to them, we are surprised at the progress they have made towards the proposed end. My father and M.", "우리가 그 일을 잊고 있는 동안에도 일은 정해진 순서대로 흘러가고, 다시 우리가 거기에 눈을 돌렸을 때는 목표를 향해 얼마나 나아갔는지 놀라게 되는 겁니다. 제 아버지와 당글라르 씨는 스페인에서 함께 복무하셨습니다."], ["Danglars served together in Spain, my father in the army and M.", "아버지는 군에 계셨고 당글라르 씨는 병참부에 계셨지요."], ["Danglars in the commissariat department. It was there that my father, ruined by the revolution, and M. Danglars, who never had possessed any patrimony, both laid the foundations of their different fortunes.”", "혁명으로 몰락한 제 아버지와, 애초에 물려받은 재산이라곤 한 푼도 없던 당글라르 씨, 두 분이 각자 다른 재산의 토대를 닦은 곳이 바로 거기였습니다.\""]], [["“Yes,” said Monte Cristo “I think M.", "\"그렇군요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["Danglars mentioned that in a visit which I paid him; and,”", "\"그건 제가 찾아갔을 때 당글라르 씨가 이야기해 준 것 같습니다. 그리고,\""], ["continued he, casting a side-glance at Lucien, who was turning over the leaves of an album, “Mademoiselle Eugénie is pretty—I think I remember that to be her name.”", "그는 화첩을 넘기고 있는 뤼시앵에게 곁눈질을 보내며 말을 이었다. \"외제니 양은 어여쁘더군요. 그 이름이 맞지요, 아마?\""]], [["“Very pretty, or rather, very beautiful,”", "\"무척 어여쁘지요. 아니, 차라리 무척 아름답다고 해야겠지요.\""], ["replied Albert, “but of that style of beauty which I do not appreciate; I am an ungrateful fellow.”", "알베르가 대답했다. \"하지만 제가 알아보지 못하는 부류의 아름다움입니다. 제가 배은망덕한 놈이지요.\""]], [["“You speak as if you were already her husband.”", "\"마치 벌써 그 아가씨의 남편이라도 된 것처럼 말씀하시는군요.\""]], [["“Ah,” returned Albert, in his turn looking around to see what Lucien was doing.", "\"아.\" 알베르가 이번에는 자기가 주위를 둘러보며 뤼시앵이 뭘 하고 있나 살폈다."]], [["“Really,”", "\"솔직히,\""], ["said Monte Cristo, lowering his voice, “you do not appear to me to be very enthusiastic on the subject of this marriage.”", "몬테크리스토가 목소리를 낮추며 말했다. \"제가 보기엔 이 결혼에 그리 열의가 있어 보이지 않습니다.\""]], [["“Mademoiselle Danglars is too rich for me,”", "\"당글라르 양은 제게 너무 부유합니다.\""], ["replied Morcerf, “and that frightens me.”", "모르세르가 대답했다. \"그게 저를 겁나게 하지요.\""]], [["“Bah,”", "\"허, 거참,\""], ["exclaimed Monte Cristo, “that’s a fine reason to give.", "몬테크리스토가 소리쳤다. \"그거 참 그럴듯한 이유로군요."], ["Are you not rich yourself?”", "당신도 부유하지 않습니까?\""]], [["“My father’s income is about 50,000 francs per annum; and he will give me, perhaps, ten or twelve thousand when I marry.”", "\"제 아버지의 수입이 연 5만 프랑쯤 됩니다. 제가 결혼하면 만 프랑이나 만이천 프랑쯤 주실 테고요.\""]], [["“That, perhaps, might not be considered a large sum, in Paris especially,”", "\"그게, 특히 파리에서는, 큰돈으로 여겨지지 않을지도 모르지요.\" 백작이 말했다."], ["said the count; “but everything does not depend on wealth, and it is a fine thing to have a good name, and to occupy a high station in society.", "\"하지만 모든 것이 재산에 달린 건 아닙니다. 좋은 가문의 이름을 지니고 사회에서 높은 지위를 차지하는 것도 훌륭한 일이지요. 당신의 이름은 명망 높고, 당신의 지위는 더없이 훌륭합니다."], ["Your name is celebrated, your position magnificent; and then the Comte de Morcerf is a soldier, and it is pleasing to see the integrity of a Bayard united to the poverty of a Duguesclin; disinterestedness is the brightest ray in which a noble sword can shine.", "게다가 모르세르 백작님은 군인이시지요. 바야르의 청렴함이 뒤게클랭의 가난함과 하나로 어우러진 모습을 보는 건 흐뭇한 일입니다. 사욕 없음이야말로 고결한 검이 빛날 수 있는 가장 밝은 광채니까요."], ["As for me, I consider the union with Mademoiselle Danglars a most suitable one; she will enrich you, and you will ennoble her.”", "저로서는 당글라르 양과의 결합이 더없이 적절하다고 봅니다. 그녀는 당신을 부유하게 해 줄 것이고, 당신은 그녀를 고귀하게 해 줄 테니까요.\""]], [["Albert shook his head, and looked thoughtful.", "알베르는 고개를 저으며 생각에 잠긴 표정을 지었다."]], [["“There is still something else,” said he.", "\"아직 다른 무언가가 있습니다.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["“I confess,”", "\"솔직히,\""], ["observed Monte Cristo, “that I have some difficulty in comprehending your objection to a young lady who is both rich and beautiful.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"부유하면서도 아름다운 아가씨를 마다하시는 이유를 저는 잘 이해하기가 어렵군요.\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오,\""], ["said Morcerf, “this repugnance, if repugnance it may be called, is not all on my side.”", "모르세르가 말했다. \"이 거부감이, 거부감이라 부를 수 있다면 말이지요, 제 쪽에만 있는 게 아닙니다.\""]], [["“Whence can it arise, then?", "\"그럼 어디서 비롯된 거지요?"], ["for you told me your father desired the marriage.”", "아버님이 이 결혼을 바라신다고 하셨으니 말입니다.\""]], [["“It is my mother who dissents; she has a clear and penetrating judgment, and does not smile on the proposed union.", "\"반대하시는 분은 제 어머니입니다. 어머니는 명료하고 예리한 판단력을 지니신 분인데, 이 혼사를 달가워하지 않으십니다."], ["I cannot account for it, but she seems to entertain some prejudice against the Danglars.”", "까닭은 잘 모르겠지만, 당글라르 가문에 어떤 편견을 품고 계신 듯합니다.\""]], [["30107m\n“Ah,”", "\"아,\" 백작이 다소 억지스러운 어조로 말했다."], ["said the count, in a somewhat forced tone, “that may be easily explained; the Comtesse de Morcerf, who is aristocracy and refinement itself, does not relish the idea of being allied by your marriage with one of ignoble birth; that is natural enough.”", "\"그건 쉽게 설명할 수 있겠군요. 귀족적 품위와 세련됨 그 자체이신 모르세르 백작 부인께서, 당신의 결혼으로 비천한 출신과 인척이 되는 걸 탐탁지 않게 여기시는 거지요. 충분히 자연스러운 일입니다.\""]], [["“I do not know if that is her reason,”", "\"그게 어머니의 이유인지는 모르겠습니다.\""], ["said Albert, “but one thing I do know, that if this marriage be consummated, it will render her quite miserable.", "알베르가 말했다. \"하지만 한 가지는 확실히 압니다. 이 결혼이 성사되면 어머니가 무척 비참해지시리라는 것이지요."], ["There was to have been a meeting six weeks ago in order to talk over and settle the affair; but I had such a sudden attack of indisposition——”", "여섯 주 전에 이 일을 의논하고 매듭짓기 위한 모임이 있을 예정이었습니다. 그런데 제가 갑자기 몸이 좋지 않아서요……\""]], [["“Real?” interrupted the count, smiling.", "\"진짜로요?\" 백작이 미소 지으며 말을 가로챘다."]], [["“Oh, real enough, from anxiety doubtless,—at any rate they postponed the matter for two months.", "\"오, 진짜였습니다, 의심할 바 없이 근심 때문에요. 어쨌든 그 일은 두 달 미뤄졌습니다."], ["There is no hurry, you know.", "서두를 일은 없으니까요, 아시다시피."], ["I am not yet twenty-one, and Eugénie is only seventeen; but the two months expire next week.", "저는 아직 스물한 살도 안 됐고, 외제니는 겨우 열일곱이니까요. 그런데 그 두 달이 다음 주면 끝납니다."], ["It must be done.", "해치워야 하는 거지요."], ["My dear count, you cannot imagine how my mind is harassed.", "백작님, 제 마음이 얼마나 시달리는지 상상도 못 하실 겁니다."], ["How happy you are in being exempt from all this!”", "이 모든 것에서 벗어나 계신 백작님은 얼마나 행복하신지요!\""]], [["“Well, and why should not you be free, too?", "\"그럼, 당신은 왜 자유로워질 수 없는 겁니까?"], ["What prevents you from being so?”", "무엇이 당신을 가로막고 있지요?\""]], [["“Oh, it will be too great a disappointment to my father if I do not marry Mademoiselle Danglars.”", "\"오, 제가 당글라르 양과 결혼하지 않으면 아버지께서 너무 크게 실망하실 겁니다.\""]], [["“Marry her then,” said the count, with a significant shrug of the shoulders.", "\"그럼 결혼하시지요.\" 백작이 의미심장하게 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["replied Morcerf, “but that will plunge my mother into positive grief.”", "모르세르가 대답했다. \"하지만 그러면 어머니가 진짜 슬픔에 빠지실 겁니다.\""]], [["“Then do not marry her,”", "\"그럼 결혼하지 마시지요.\""], ["said the count.", "백작이 말했다."]], [["“Well, I shall see.", "\"음, 두고 보겠습니다."], ["I will try and think over what is the best thing to be done; you will give me your advice, will you not, and if possible extricate me from my unpleasant position?", "어떻게 하는 게 가장 좋을지 곰곰이 생각해 봐야지요. 조언을 해 주시겠지요, 그렇지요? 가능하다면 이 거북한 처지에서 저를 빼내 주시고요."], ["I think, rather than give pain to my dear mother, I would run the risk of offending the count.”", "사랑하는 어머니를 괴롭히느니, 차라리 백작님 노여움을 살 위험을 무릅쓰겠습니다.\""]], [["Monte Cristo turned away; he seemed moved by this last remark.", "몬테크리스토가 고개를 돌렸다. 이 마지막 말에 마음이 움직인 듯 보였다."]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아.\""], ["said he to Debray, who had thrown himself into an easy-chair at the farthest extremity of the salon, and who held a pencil in his right hand and an account book in his left, “what are you doing there?", "그가 드브레에게 말을 건넸다. 드브레는 살롱의 가장 안쪽 끝에 놓인 안락의자에 몸을 파묻은 채, 오른손에는 연필을, 왼손에는 장부를 들고 있었다. \"거기서 뭘 하고 계십니까?"], ["Are you making a sketch after Poussin?”", "푸생을 본떠 스케치라도 하시는 건가요?\""]], [["“Oh, no,”", "\"오, 아닙니다.\""], ["was the tranquil response; “I am too fond of art to attempt anything of that sort.", "차분한 대답이 돌아왔다. \"저는 예술을 너무 사랑해서 그런 건 감히 시도하지 못합니다."], ["I am doing a little sum in arithmetic.”", "자그마한 셈을 좀 하고 있었지요.\""]], [["“In arithmetic?”", "\"셈을요?\""]], [["“Yes; I am calculating—by the way, Morcerf, that indirectly concerns you—I am calculating what the house of Danglars must have gained by the last rise in Haiti bonds; from 206 they have risen to 409 in three days, and the prudent banker had purchased at 206; therefore he must have made 300,000 livres.”", "\"네. 계산을 하고 있었습니다. 그런데, 모르세르, 이건 자네하고도 간접적으로 관련이 있는 일이야. 당글라르 상회가 지난번 아이티 국채 폭등으로 얼마나 벌었을지 계산하고 있었지요. 사흘 만에 206에서 409로 올랐는데, 그 신중한 은행가는 206에 사 뒀거든요. 그러니 30만 리브르는 벌었을 겁니다.\""]], [["“That is not his biggest scoop,”", "\"그건 그 사람 최대 수확도 아니야.\""], ["said Morcerf; “did he not make a million in Spaniards this last year?”", "모르세르가 말했다. \"작년에 스페인 채권으로 백만을 벌지 않았나?\""]], [["“My dear fellow,”", "\"이보게,\""], ["said Lucien, “here is the Count of Monte Cristo, who will say to you, as the Italians do,—", "뤼시앵이 말했다. \"여기 몬테크리스토 백작님이 계시니, 이탈리아 사람들 말마따나 이렇게 말씀하실 걸세."]], [["“‘Denaro e santità,\nMetà della metà.’9", "'돈과 거룩함, 그 절반의 절반.'"]], [["“When they tell me such things, I only shrug my shoulders and say nothing.”", "저는 누가 그런 이야기를 하면 그저 어깨를 으쓱하고 아무 말도 하지 않습니다.\""]], [["“But you were speaking of Haitians?”", "\"그런데 아이티 채권 얘기를 하고 계셨지요?\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“Ah, Haitians,—that is quite another thing!", "\"아, 아이티 채권이요. 그건 또 전혀 다른 얘기지요!"], ["Haitians are the _écarté_ of French stock-jobbing.", "아이티 채권은 프랑스 증권 투기의 에카르테 같은 겁니다."], ["We may like bouillotte, delight in whist, be enraptured with boston, and yet grow tired of them all; but we always come back to _écarté_—it is not only a game, it is a _hors-d’œuvre_! M.", "부요트를 좋아할 수도, 휘스트에 빠질 수도, 보스턴에 황홀해할 수도 있지만, 결국엔 다 싫증이 나는 법이지요. 그런데 우리는 언제나 에카르테로 돌아옵니다. 그건 단순한 노름이 아니라 전채 요리거든요!"], ["Danglars sold yesterday at 405, and pockets 300,000 francs.", "당글라르 씨는 어제 405에 팔아서 30만 프랑을 챙겼습니다."], ["Had he but waited till today, the price would have fallen to 205, and instead of gaining 300,000 francs, he would have lost 20 or 25,000.”", "만약 오늘까지 기다렸더라면 시세가 205로 떨어졌을 테고, 30만 프랑을 버는 대신 2만이나 2만5천 프랑을 잃었을 겁니다.\""]], [["“And what has caused the sudden fall from 409 to 206?”", "\"그런데 무엇 때문에 409에서 206으로 갑자기 떨어진 건가요?\""], ["asked Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 물었다."], ["“I am profoundly ignorant of all these stock-jobbing intrigues.”", "\"저는 이런 증권 투기의 속내라곤 도무지 모르는 사람이라서요.\""]], [["“Because,”", "\"왜냐하면,\""], ["said Albert, laughing, “one piece of news follows another, and there is often great dissimilarity between them.”", "알베르가 웃으며 말했다. \"한 가지 소식 뒤에 또 다른 소식이 잇따르는데, 그 둘이 종종 크게 어긋나기 때문이지요.\""]], [["“Ah,” said the count, “I see that M.", "\"아,\" 백작이 말했다."], ["Danglars is accustomed to play at gaining or losing 300,000 francs in a day; he must be enormously rich.”", "\"보아하니 당글라르 씨는 하루에 30만 프랑을 벌거나 잃는 노름에 익숙한 모양이군요. 엄청나게 부유한 사람임이 틀림없습니다.\""]], [["“It is not he who plays!”", "\"노름을 하는 건 그 사람이 아닙니다!\""], ["exclaimed Lucien; “it is Madame Danglars; she is indeed daring.”", "뤼시앵이 소리쳤다. \"당글라르 부인이지요. 정말 대담한 분입니다.\""]], [["“But you who are a reasonable being, Lucien, and who knows how little dependence is to be placed on the news, since you are at the fountain-head, surely you ought to prevent it,”", "\"하지만 이성적인 사람인 자네, 게다가 그런 소식이 얼마나 믿을 게 못 되는지 잘 아는 자네가, 소식의 원천에 있는 자네가, 당연히 그걸 막아야 하지 않나.\""], ["said Morcerf, with a smile.", "모르세르가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“How can I, if her husband fails in controlling her?”", "\"남편도 못 막는 걸 내가 어떻게 막겠나?\" 뤼시앵이 물었다."], ["asked Lucien; “you know the character of the baroness—no one has any influence with her, and she does precisely what she pleases.”", "\"남작 부인의 성격을 알지 않나. 아무도 그분을 좌우하지 못해. 자기 하고 싶은 대로 하시는 분이지.\""]], [["“Ah, if I were in your place——”", "\"아, 내가 자네 처지라면……\""], ["said Albert.", "알베르가 말했다."]], [["“Well?”", "\"그러면?\""]], [["“I would reform her; it would be rendering a service to her future son-in-law.”", "\"내가 그분을 바로잡아 드리겠네. 장차 사위 될 사람에게 봉사하는 셈이 될 테니.\""]], [["“How would you set about it?”", "\"어떻게 그러겠다는 건가?\""]], [["“Ah, that would be easy enough—I would give her a lesson.”", "\"아, 그거야 아주 쉽지. 본때를 보여 드리는 거야.\""]], [["“A lesson?”", "\"본때를?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래."], ["Your position as secretary to the minister renders your authority great on the subject of political news; you never open your mouth but the stockbrokers immediately stenograph your words.", "장관 비서라는 자네 지위 덕에 정치 소식에 관해서는 자네 말이 큰 권위를 갖지 않나. 자네가 입을 열기만 하면 증권 중개인들이 즉각 그 말을 속기로 받아 적으니까."], ["Cause her to lose a hundred thousand francs, and that would teach her prudence.”", "그분께 십만 프랑을 잃게 해 드리면 신중함을 배우실 거야.\""]], [["“I do not understand,”", "\"무슨 말인지 모르겠는데.\""], ["stammered Lucien.", "뤼시앵이 더듬거렸다."]], [["“It is very clear, notwithstanding,”", "\"그래도 아주 분명한 얘기야.\""], ["replied the young man, with an artlessness wholly free from affectation; “tell her some fine morning an unheard-of piece of intelligence—some telegraphic despatch, of which you alone are in possession; for instance, that Henri IV.", "젊은이가 조금도 꾸밈없는 천진함으로 대답했다. \"어느 화창한 아침에 그분께 전대미문의 소식을, 자네만 손에 쥐고 있는 어떤 전보 같은 걸 흘려 주는 거야. 예를 들어 어제 앙리 4세가 가브리엘의 집에서 목격됐다는 식으로 말이지."], ["was seen yesterday at Gabrielle’s.", "그러면 시세가 치솟을 테고, 그분은 잔뜩 사들이실 거야."], ["That would boom the market; she will buy heavily, and she will certainly lose when Beauchamp announces the following day, in his gazette, ‘The report circulated by some usually well-informed persons that the king was seen yesterday at Gabrielle’s house, is totally without foundation. We can positively assert that his majesty did not quit the Pont-Neuf.’”", "그러고는 이튿날 보샹이 자기 신문에 '평소 정통한 소식통이라는 일부 인사들이 어제 국왕께서 가브리엘의 집에서 목격되었다고 퍼뜨린 소문은 전혀 근거가 없다. 우리는 폐하께서 퐁뇌프를 한 발짝도 벗어나지 않으셨음을 단언할 수 있다'라고 발표하면 그분은 틀림없이 잃으시겠지.\""]], [["Lucien half smiled. Monte Cristo, although apparently indifferent, had not lost one word of this conversation, and his penetrating eye had even read a hidden secret in the embarrassed manner of the secretary.", "뤼시앵이 살짝 미소를 지었다. 몬테크리스토는 겉으로는 무심한 척하면서도 이 대화의 한 마디도 놓치지 않았고, 그 꿰뚫는 눈으로 비서의 당황한 태도에서 감춰진 비밀까지 읽어 냈다."], ["This embarrassment had completely escaped Albert, but it caused Lucien to shorten his visit; he was evidently ill at ease.", "이 당황함을 알베르는 전혀 알아채지 못했지만, 뤼시앵은 그 때문에 방문을 서둘러 끝냈다. 그는 분명 마음이 편치 않았다."], ["The count, in taking leave of him, said something in a low voice, to which he answered, “Willingly, count; I accept.”", "백작은 그와 작별하면서 낮은 목소리로 뭐라 말을 건넸고, 그는 \"기꺼이요, 백작님. 받아들이겠습니다\"라고 대답했다."], ["The count returned to young Morcerf.", "백작은 젊은 모르세르에게로 돌아왔다."]], [["“Do you not think, on reflection,”", "\"가만히 생각해 보니,\""], ["said he to him, “that you have done wrong in thus speaking of your mother-in-law in the presence of M. Debray?”", "백작이 그에게 말했다. \"드브레 씨가 있는 자리에서 장모님 이야기를 그렇게 한 건 잘못한 일이 아니었나요?\""]], [["“My dear count,”", "\"백작님,\""], ["said Morcerf, “I beg of you not to apply that title so prematurely.”", "모르세르가 말했다. \"그 호칭을 그리 성급하게 갖다 붙이지 말아 주시기를 부탁드립니다.\""]], [["“Now, speaking without any exaggeration, is your mother really so very much averse to this marriage?”", "\"자, 조금도 과장 없이 말씀해 보십시오. 어머님이 정말로 이 결혼을 그토록 싫어하십니까?\""]], [["“So much so that the baroness very rarely comes to the house, and my mother, has not, I think, visited Madame Danglars twice in her whole life.”", "\"어찌나 싫어하시는지 남작 부인은 저희 집에 좀처럼 오시지 않고, 제 어머니도 평생 당글라르 부인을 두 번도 채 찾아가신 적이 없을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["said the count, “I am emboldened to speak openly to you. M.", "백작이 말했다. \"제가 솔직하게 말씀드릴 용기가 나는군요."], ["Danglars is my banker; M. de Villefort has overwhelmed me with politeness in return for a service which a casual piece of good fortune enabled me to render him.", "당글라르 씨는 제 거래 은행가입니다. 그리고 빌포르 씨는, 우연한 행운 덕에 제가 베풀게 된 어떤 도움에 대한 답례로 저를 친절로 넘치게 대해 주었지요."], ["I predict from all this an avalanche of dinners and routs.", "이 모든 것으로 미루어 저는 만찬과 연회의 눈사태를 예감합니다."], ["Now, in order not to presume on this, and also to be beforehand with them, I have, if agreeable to you, thought of inviting M. and Madame Danglars, and M. and Madame de Villefort, to my country-house at Auteuil.", "그래서 이걸 당연하게 여기지 않으려고, 또 그들보다 한발 앞서고자, 당신만 괜찮다면 당글라르 부부와 빌포르 부부를 오퇴유에 있는 제 시골집으로 초대할 생각을 했습니다."], ["If I were to invite you and the Count and Countess of Morcerf to this dinner, I should give it the appearance of being a matrimonial meeting, or at least Madame de Morcerf would look upon the affair in that light, especially if Baron Danglars did me the honor to bring his daughter.", "그런데 만약 이 만찬에 당신과 모르세르 백작 부부까지 초대한다면, 마치 혼담을 위한 모임처럼 보이게 만드는 셈이 되겠지요. 적어도 모르세르 백작 부인께서는 그 일을 그렇게 보실 겁니다. 더구나 당글라르 남작이 영광스럽게도 따님을 데려온다면 더욱 그럴 테고요."], ["In that case your mother would hold me in aversion, and I do not at all wish that; on the contrary, I desire to stand high in her esteem.”", "그렇게 되면 어머님이 저를 미워하실 텐데, 저는 결코 그걸 바라지 않습니다. 오히려 그분의 존경을 받고 싶지요.\""]], [["“Indeed, count,”", "\"정말이지, 백작님,\""], ["said Morcerf, “I thank you sincerely for having used so much candor towards me, and I gratefully accept the exclusion which you propose.", "모르세르가 말했다. \"저에게 이토록 솔직하게 대해 주셔서 진심으로 감사드립니다. 제안하신 대로 저를 빼 주시는 것을 고맙게 받아들이겠습니다."], ["You say you desire my mother’s good opinion; I assure you it is already yours to a very unusual extent.”", "어머니의 호감을 바라신다고 하셨는데, 분명히 말씀드리지만 그 호감은 이미 더없이 각별하게 백작님의 것입니다.\""]], [["30111m\n“Do you think so?”", "\"그렇게 생각하십니까?\""], ["said Monte Cristo, with interest.", "몬테크리스토가 관심을 보이며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, I am sure of it; we talked of you an hour after you left us the other day. But to return to what we were saying.", "\"오, 확신합니다. 지난번에 백작님이 자리를 뜨신 지 한 시간 동안 저희가 백작님 이야기를 했거든요."], ["If my mother could know of this attention on your part—and I will venture to tell her—I am sure that she will be most grateful to you; it is true that my father will be equally angry.”", "하지만 하던 이야기로 돌아가지요. 어머니가 백작님의 이 배려를 아신다면, 제가 감히 말씀드리겠지만, 분명 백작님께 무척 감사해하실 겁니다. 물론 그만큼 제 아버지는 화를 내시겠지요.\""], ["The count laughed.", "백작이 웃었다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"음,\""], ["said he to Morcerf, “but I think your father will not be the only angry one; M.", "그가 모르세르에게 말했다. \"하지만 화내실 분이 아버님 한 분만은 아닐 것 같군요."], ["and Madame Danglars will think me a very ill-mannered person.", "당글라르 부부는 저를 아주 무례한 사람으로 여길 겁니다."], ["They know that I am intimate with you—that you are, in fact; one of the oldest of my Parisian acquaintances—and they will not find you at my house; they will certainly ask me why I did not invite you.", "제가 당신과 친하다는 걸, 사실 당신이 제 파리 지인 가운데 가장 오래된 분이라는 걸 그들도 아는데, 제 집에서 당신을 보지 못하면 분명 왜 당신을 초대하지 않았느냐고 물을 테니까요."], ["Be sure to provide yourself with some previous engagement which shall have a semblance of probability, and communicate the fact to me by a line in writing.", "그러니 그럴듯해 보이는 선약을 꼭 하나 마련해 두었다가, 그 사실을 글로 한 줄 적어 제게 알려 주십시오."], ["You know that with bankers nothing but a written document will be valid.”", "은행가들에게는 글로 된 문서가 아니면 아무것도 유효하지 않다는 걸 아시지요.\""]], [["“I will do better than that,”", "\"그보다 더 좋은 방법이 있습니다.\""], ["said Albert; “my mother is wishing to go to the sea-side—what day is fixed for your dinner?”", "알베르가 말했다. \"제 어머니가 바닷가에 가고 싶어 하시거든요. 만찬은 며칠로 정하셨습니까?\""]], [["“Saturday.”", "\"토요일입니다.\""]], [["“This is Tuesday—well, tomorrow evening we leave, and the day after we shall be at Tréport.", "\"오늘이 화요일이군요. 그럼 내일 저녁에 떠나서, 그다음 날이면 트레포르에 가 있겠습니다."], ["Really, count, you have a delightful way of setting people at their ease.”", "정말이지, 백작님은 사람을 편안하게 해 주는 멋진 재주가 있으십니다.\""]], [["“Indeed, you give me more credit than I deserve; I only wish to do what will be agreeable to you, that is all.”", "\"과분한 평가입니다. 저는 그저 당신이 흡족할 만한 일을 하고 싶을 뿐, 그게 전부입니다.\""]], [["“When shall you send your invitations?”", "\"초대장은 언제 보내실 겁니까?\""]], [["“This very day.”", "\"바로 오늘이요.\""]], [["“Well, I will immediately call on M. Danglars, and tell him that my mother and myself must leave Paris tomorrow.", "\"좋습니다. 그럼 곧장 당글라르 씨를 찾아가서, 어머니와 제가 내일 파리를 떠나야 한다고 말하겠습니다."], ["I have not seen you, consequently I know nothing of your dinner.”", "저는 백작님을 뵙지 못했으니, 만찬에 대해서는 아무것도 모르는 거지요.\""]], [["“How foolish you are!", "\"어리석은 분이로군요!"], ["Have you forgotten that M. Debray has just seen you at my house?”", "드브레 씨가 방금 제 집에서 당신을 봤다는 걸 잊으셨습니까?\""]], [["“Ah, true.”", "\"아, 그렇군요.\""]], [["“Fix it this way. I have seen you, and invited you without any ceremony, when you instantly answered that it would be impossible for you to accept, as you were going to Tréport.”", "\"이렇게 정합시다. 제가 당신을 만나 격식 없이 초대했는데, 당신이 곧바로 트레포르에 가야 해서 받아들일 수 없겠다고 대답한 걸로요.\""]], [["“Well, then, that is settled; but you will come and call on my mother before tomorrow?”", "\"그럼 그렇게 정리하지요. 그런데 내일 전에 제 어머니를 찾아뵈러 오시겠습니까?\""]], [["“Before tomorrow?—that will be a difficult matter to arrange, besides, I shall just be in the way of all the preparations for departure.”", "\"내일 전에요? 그건 마련하기 어려운 일이겠는데요. 게다가 저는 출발 준비로 한창 정신이 없을 테니까요.\""]], [["“Well, you can do better.", "\"음, 더 좋은 방법이 있습니다."], ["You were only a charming man before, but, if you accede to my proposal, you will be adorable.”", "백작님은 지금까지는 매력적인 분이셨을 뿐이지만, 제 제안을 받아들이시면 흠모할 만한 분이 되실 겁니다.\""]], [["“What must I do to attain such sublimity?”", "\"그런 숭고한 경지에 이르려면 제가 무얼 해야 합니까?\""]], [["“You are today free as air—come and dine with me; we shall be a small party—only yourself, my mother, and I.", "\"오늘 백작님은 바람처럼 자유로우시지요. 저와 함께 저녁을 드시지요. 단출한 자리가 될 겁니다."], ["You have scarcely seen my mother; you shall have an opportunity of observing her more closely.", "백작님과 제 어머니, 그리고 저뿐이니까요. 백작님은 제 어머니를 거의 못 보셨으니, 더 가까이서 살펴보실 기회가 될 겁니다."], ["She is a remarkable woman, and I only regret that there does not exist another like her, about twenty years younger; in that case, I assure you, there would very soon be a Countess and Viscountess of Morcerf.", "어머니는 비범한 분이지요. 다만 한 가지 아쉬운 건, 어머니와 똑같으면서 스무 살쯤 더 젊은 분이 또 없다는 거예요. 그런 분이 있었다면, 정말이지 머지않아 모르세르 백작 부인과 자작 부인이 함께 생겼을 텐데 말입니다."], ["As to my father, you will not see him; he is officially engaged, and dines with the chief referendary.", "아버지로 말하자면, 보지 못하실 겁니다. 공무가 있어서 수석 청원위원과 식사하시거든요. 저희는 여행 이야기를 나눌 겁니다."], ["We will talk over our travels; and you, who have seen the whole world, will relate your adventures—you shall tell us the history of the beautiful Greek who was with you the other night at the Opera, and whom you call your slave, and yet treat like a princess.", "그리고 온 세상을 두루 다니신 백작님은 모험담을 들려주시는 거지요. 지난번 오페라에서 백작님과 함께 있던, 백작님이 노예라고 부르면서도 공주처럼 대하는 그 아름다운 그리스 여인의 내력도 들려주십시오. 이탈리아어로도, 스페인어로도 이야기합시다."], ["We will talk Italian and Spanish.", "자, 제 초대를 받아 주십시오."], ["Come, accept my invitation, and my mother will thank you.”", "그러면 어머니가 백작님께 감사할 겁니다.\""]], [["“A thousand thanks,”", "\"천번 감사드립니다.\""], ["said the count, “your invitation is most gracious, and I regret exceedingly that it is not in my power to accept it.", "백작이 말했다. \"참으로 고마운 초대입니다만, 받아들일 수 없는 처지라 몹시 유감입니다."], ["I am not so much at liberty as you suppose; on the contrary, I have a most important engagement.”", "저는 당신이 생각하시는 만큼 그렇게 자유롭지가 않아요. 오히려 더없이 중요한 약속이 있답니다.\""]], [["“Ah, take care, you were teaching me just now how, in case of an invitation to dinner, one might creditably make an excuse.", "\"아, 조심하십시오. 방금 백작님께서 저에게, 만찬 초대를 받았을 때 어떻게 그럴듯하게 핑계를 댈 수 있는지 가르쳐 주셨잖습니까."], ["I require the proof of a pre-engagement.", "저는 선약의 증거를 요구합니다."], ["I am not a banker, like M. Danglars, but I am quite as incredulous as he is.”", "저는 당글라르 씨 같은 은행가는 아니지만, 그 사람만큼이나 의심이 많거든요.\""]], [["“I am going to give you a proof,”", "\"증거를 보여 드리지요.\""], ["replied the count, and he rang the bell.", "백작이 대답하며 종을 울렸다."]], [["“Humph,”", "\"흠,\""], ["said Morcerf, “this is the second time you have refused to dine with my mother; it is evident that you wish to avoid her.”", "모르세르가 말했다. \"이걸로 제 어머니와 식사하기를 거절하신 게 두 번째로군요. 어머니를 피하고 싶으신 게 분명합니다.\""]], [["Monte Cristo started.", "몬테크리스토가 흠칫했다."], ["“Oh, you do not mean that,”", "\"오, 그런 뜻으로 하신 말씀은 아니시겠지요.\""], ["said he; “besides, here comes the confirmation of my assertion.”", "그가 말했다. \"게다가, 마침 제 말을 뒷받침해 줄 사람이 오는군요.\""]], [["Baptistin entered, and remained standing at the door.", "밥티스탱이 들어와 문가에 선 채로 있었다."]], [["“I had no previous knowledge of your visit, had I?”", "\"제가 당신의 방문을 미리 알지 못했지요, 그렇지 않습니까?\""]], [["“Indeed, you are such an extraordinary person, that I would not answer for it.”", "\"솔직히, 백작님은 워낙 비범한 분이라, 그것까지는 장담 못 하겠는데요.\""]], [["“At all events, I could not guess that you would invite me to dinner.”", "\"어쨌든 당신이 저를 저녁 식사에 초대하리라곤 짐작할 수 없었습니다.\""]], [["“Probably not.”", "\"아마 그러셨겠지요.\""]], [["“Well, listen, Baptistin, what did I tell you this morning when I called you into my laboratory?”", "\"자, 들어 보십시오. 밥티스탱, 오늘 아침 내가 실험실로 자네를 불렀을 때 뭐라고 했지?\""]], [["“To close the door against visitors as soon as the clock struck five,”", "\"다섯 시 종이 치는 즉시 방문객을 받지 말고 문을 닫으라 하셨습니다.\""], ["replied the valet.", "하인이 대답했다."]], [["“What then?”", "\"그다음은?\""]], [["“Ah, my dear count,” said Albert.", "\"아, 백작님.\" 알베르가 말했다."]], [["“No, no, I wish to do away with that mysterious reputation that you have given me, my dear viscount; it is tiresome to be always acting Manfred.", "\"아니, 아니, 저는 당신이 제게 씌워 준 그 신비로운 평판을 떨쳐 버리고 싶습니다, 자작. 늘 만프레드 노릇을 하는 건 지겨운 일이에요."], ["I wish my life to be free and open.", "저는 제 삶이 자유롭고 떳떳하기를 바랍니다."], ["Go on, Baptistin.”", "계속하게, 밥티스탱.\""]], [["“Then to admit no one except Major Bartolomeo Cavalcanti and his son.”", "\"그다음엔 바르톨로메오 카발칸티 소령과 그 아드님 외에는 아무도 들이지 말라 하셨습니다.\""]], [["“You hear—Major Bartolomeo Cavalcanti—a man who ranks amongst the most ancient nobility of Italy, whose name Dante has celebrated in the tenth canto of _The Inferno_, you remember it, do you not?", "\"들으셨지요. 바르톨로메오 카발칸티 소령, 이탈리아에서 가장 유서 깊은 귀족 가문에 드는 분이지요. 단테가 『지옥편』 제10곡에서 그 이름을 기린 가문 말입니다, 기억나시지요?"], ["Then there is his son, Andrea, a charming young man, about your own age, viscount, bearing the same title as yourself, and who is making his entry into the Parisian world, aided by his father’s millions.", "그리고 그 아드님 안드레아가 있는데, 매력적인 청년이고 자작과 나이도 엇비슷하지요. 당신과 같은 작위를 지녔고, 아버지의 막대한 재산에 힘입어 파리 사교계에 막 발을 들이려는 참입니다."], ["The major will bring his son with him this evening, the _contino_, as we say in Italy; he confides him to my care. If he proves himself worthy of it, I will do what I can to advance his interests.", "오늘 저녁 소령이 아드님을, 이탈리아 말로 콘티노를 데려올 텐데, 그를 제게 맡기려는 거지요. 그 청년이 그럴 만한 자격이 있음을 보여 준다면, 저는 그의 앞날을 위해 할 수 있는 일을 다 할 생각입니다."], ["You will assist me in the work, will you not?”", "당신도 이 일을 거들어 주시겠지요, 그렇지 않습니까?\""]], [["“Most undoubtedly.", "\"물론입니다."], ["This Major Cavalcanti is an old friend of yours, then?”", "그럼 이 카발칸티 소령은 백작님의 오랜 벗이신가요?\""]], [["“By no means.", "\"전혀 아닙니다."], ["He is a perfect nobleman, very polite, modest, and agreeable, such as may be found constantly in Italy, descendants of very ancient families.", "그는 완벽한 귀족이에요. 무척 정중하고 겸손하며 호감 가는 인물이지요. 아주 오래된 가문의 후손으로 이탈리아에서 늘 볼 수 있는 그런 사람입니다."], ["I have met him several times at Florence, Bologna and Lucca, and he has now communicated to me the fact of his arrival in Paris.", "저는 피렌체와 볼로냐, 루카에서 그를 여러 번 만났는데, 이번에 파리에 도착했다고 제게 알려 왔어요."], ["The acquaintances one makes in travelling have a sort of claim on one; they everywhere expect to receive the same attention which you once paid them by chance, as though the civilities of a passing hour were likely to awaken any lasting interest in favor of the man in whose society you may happen to be thrown in the course of your journey.", "여행길에서 사귄 사람들은 일종의 권리를 주장하는 법이지요. 그들은 어디서든, 당신이 한때 우연히 베풀었던 것과 똑같은 관심을 받기를 기대합니다. 마치 스쳐 가는 한때의 친절이, 여행 중에 우연히 어울리게 된 사람을 향한 지속적인 관심을 불러일으킬 수 있기라도 한 것처럼 말이지요."], ["This good Major Cavalcanti is come to take a second view of Paris, which he only saw in passing through in the time of the Empire, when he was on his way to Moscow.", "이 선량한 카발칸티 소령은 파리를 다시 한번 보러 온 겁니다. 제정 시절 모스크바로 향하던 길에 그저 지나치며 본 게 전부였으니까요."], ["I shall give him a good dinner, he will confide his son to my care, I will promise to watch over him, I shall let him follow in whatever path his folly may lead him, and then I shall have done my part.”", "저는 그에게 훌륭한 만찬을 대접할 테고, 그는 제게 아들을 맡길 것이며, 저는 그를 보살피겠다고 약속할 겁니다. 그러고는 그의 어리석음이 어떤 길로 이끌든 내버려 두는 거지요. 그러면 제 몫은 다한 셈입니다.\""]], [["“Certainly; I see you are a model Mentor,”", "\"분명히요. 백작님은 본보기가 될 만한 멘토시군요.\""], ["said Albert “Good-bye, we shall return on Sunday.", "알베르가 말했다. \"그럼 안녕히 계십시오. 저희는 일요일에 돌아올 겁니다."], ["By the way, I have received news of Franz.”", "그런데 프란츠한테서 소식을 받았습니다.\""]], [["“Have you?", "\"그래요?"], ["Is he still amusing himself in Italy?”", "아직도 이탈리아에서 즐겁게 지내고 있나요?\""]], [["“I believe so; however, he regrets your absence extremely.", "\"그런 것 같습니다. 다만 백작님이 안 계셔서 무척 아쉬워하더군요."], ["He says you were the sun of Rome, and that without you all appears dark and cloudy; I do not know if he does not even go so far as to say that it rains.”", "백작님이 로마의 태양이셨다며, 백작님이 안 계시니 모든 게 어둡고 흐려 보인다고요. 비까지 온다고 한 건 아닌지 모르겠네요.\""]], [["“His opinion of me is altered for the better, then?”", "\"그럼 저에 대한 그의 평가가 좋게 바뀐 모양이군요?\""]], [["“No, he still persists in looking upon you as the most incomprehensible and mysterious of beings.”", "\"아니요. 여전히 백작님을 세상에서 가장 헤아릴 수 없고 신비로운 존재로 여기는 데는 변함이 없습니다.\""]], [["“He is a charming young man,”", "\"매력적인 청년이지요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo “and I felt a lively interest in him the very first evening of my introduction, when I met him in search of a supper, and prevailed upon him to accept a portion of mine.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"처음 만나 소개받던 그날 저녁부터 저는 그에게 깊은 관심을 느꼈습니다. 저녁거리를 찾아 헤매던 그를 만나, 제 것을 좀 나누자고 권했었지요."], ["He is, I think, the son of General d’Épinay?”", "그가, 제 생각엔, 데피네 장군의 아들이지요?\""]], [["“He is.”", "\"맞습니다.\""]], [["“The same who was so shamefully assassinated in 1815?”", "\"1815년에 그토록 수치스럽게 암살당한 그분 말이지요?\""]], [["“By the Bonapartists.”", "\"보나파르트파에게요.\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래요."], ["Really I like him extremely; is there not also a matrimonial engagement contemplated for him?”", "정말이지 저는 그가 무척 마음에 듭니다. 그에게도 혼담이 오가고 있지 않나요?\""]], [["“Yes, he is to marry Mademoiselle de Villefort.”", "\"네, 빌포르 양과 결혼하게 되어 있지요.\""]], [["“Indeed?”", "\"그래요?\""]], [["30115m\n“And you know I am to marry Mademoiselle Danglars,”", "\"그리고 백작님도 아시다시피 저는 당글라르 양과 결혼하게 되어 있고요.\""], ["said Albert, laughing.", "알베르가 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“You smile.”", "\"웃으시는군요.\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Why do you do so?”", "\"왜 그러시지요?\""]], [["“I smile because there appears to me to be about as much inclination for the consummation of the engagement in question as there is for my own.", "\"제가 웃는 건, 그 혼약을 성사시키려는 마음이 제 혼사를 성사시키려는 마음만큼이나 있는 듯 보여서입니다."], ["But really, my dear count, we are talking as much of women as they do of us; it is unpardonable.”", "그런데 정말이지, 백작님, 우리도 여자들이 우리 얘기를 하는 만큼 여자들 얘기를 하고 있군요. 용서받지 못할 일입니다.\""]], [["Albert rose.", "알베르가 일어섰다."]], [["“Are you going?”", "\"가시려고요?\""]], [["“Really, that is a good idea!—two hours have I been boring you to death with my company, and then you, with the greatest politeness, ask me if I am going.", "\"이거 참 좋은 말씀이네요! 제가 두 시간이나 옆에 붙어서 백작님을 지루해 죽게 만들어 놓고는, 백작님은 더없이 정중하게 제가 가는지 물으시다니요."], ["Indeed, count, you are the most polished man in the world.", "정말이지, 백작님은 세상에서 가장 세련된 분이십니다."], ["And your servants, too, how very well behaved they are; there is quite a style about them.", "백작님 하인들도 어찌나 예의 바른지요. 다들 품위가 있어요. 특히 밥티스탱 씨가요."], ["Monsieur Baptistin especially; I could never get such a man as that.", "저는 도무지 그런 사람을 구할 수가 없습니다."], ["My servants seem to imitate those you sometimes see in a play, who, because they have only a word or two to say, aquit themselves in the most awkward manner possible.", "제 하인들은 연극에서 가끔 보는 그런 자들을 닮았어요. 대사라곤 한두 마디뿐인데도 더없이 어색하게 해치우는 그런 자들 말입니다."], ["Therefore, if you part with M. Baptistin, give me the refusal of him.”", "그러니 혹시 밥티스탱 씨를 내보내시거든, 제게 먼저 거절할 기회를 주십시오.\""]], [["“By all means.”", "\"얼마든지요.\""]], [["“That is not all; give my compliments to your illustrious Luccanese, Cavalcante of the Cavalcanti; and if by any chance he should be wishing to establish his son, find him a wife very rich, very noble on her mother’s side at least, and a baroness in right of her father, I will help you in the search.”", "\"그게 다가 아닙니다. 그 명망 높은 루카 분, 카발칸티 가문의 카발칸테 씨에게 안부를 전해 주십시오. 혹시라도 그분이 아드님을 짝지어 주고 싶어 하신다면, 적어도 어머니 쪽으로는 무척 부유하고 무척 고귀하며, 아버지 덕분에 남작 작위까지 지닌 신붓감을 찾아 드리세요. 저도 함께 찾아 드리겠습니다.\""]], [["“Ah, ha; you will do as much as that, will you?”", "\"하, 하. 그렇게까지 해 주시겠다는 겁니까?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Well, really, nothing is certain in this world.”", "\"음, 정말이지, 세상에 확실한 건 없군요.\""]], [["“Oh, count, what a service you might render me! I should like you a hundred times better if, by your intervention, I could manage to remain a bachelor, even were it only for ten years.”", "\"오, 백작님, 저에게 얼마나 큰 도움을 베푸실 수 있는지요! 백작님께서 나서 주신 덕에 단 십 년만이라도 제가 독신으로 남을 수 있다면, 백작님을 백배는 더 좋아하게 될 텐데요.\""]], [["“Nothing is impossible,”", "\"불가능한 건 없지요.\""], ["gravely replied Monte Cristo; and taking leave of Albert, he returned into the house, and struck the gong three times.", "몬테크리스토가 진지하게 대답했다. 그리고 알베르와 작별한 뒤 집 안으로 돌아와 징을 세 번 울렸다."], ["Bertuccio appeared.", "베르투치오가 나타났다."]], [["“Monsieur Bertuccio, you understand that I intend entertaining company on Saturday at Auteuil.”", "\"베르투치오, 토요일에 오퇴유에서 손님을 대접할 생각이라는 걸 알아 두게.\""], ["Bertuccio slightly started.", "베르투치오가 살짝 흠칫했다."], ["“I shall require your services to see that all be properly arranged.", "\"모든 게 제대로 갖춰지도록 자네 손을 좀 빌려야겠어."], ["It is a beautiful house, or at all events may be made so.”", "아름다운 집이지, 적어도 그렇게 꾸밀 수 있는 집이고.\""]], [["“There must be a good deal done before it can deserve that title, your excellency, for the tapestried hangings are very old.”", "\"그런 이름값을 하려면 손볼 데가 많을 겁니다, 각하. 벽걸이 융단이 무척 낡았거든요.\""]], [["“Let them all be taken away and changed, then, with the exception of the sleeping-chamber which is hung with red damask; you will leave that exactly as it is.”", "\"그럼 붉은 다마스크 천을 두른 침실만 빼고 전부 떼어 내서 바꾸게. 그 방은 지금 그대로 두고.\" 베르투치오가 고개를 숙였다."], ["Bertuccio bowed.", "\"정원도 손대지 말게."], ["“You will not touch the garden either; as to the yard, you may do what you please with it; I should prefer that being altered beyond all recognition.”", "안마당은 자네 마음대로 해도 좋아. 알아볼 수 없을 만큼 완전히 바꿔 놓았으면 싶군.\""]], [["“I will do everything in my power to carry out your wishes, your excellency.", "\"각하의 뜻을 받들어 제 힘닿는 데까지 다 하겠습니다."], ["I should be glad, however, to receive your excellency’s commands concerning the dinner.”", "다만 만찬에 관해서는 각하의 분부를 받았으면 합니다.\""]], [["“Really, my dear M. Bertuccio,”", "\"이런, 베르투치오,\""], ["said the count, “since you have been in Paris, you have become quite nervous, and apparently out of your element; you no longer seem to understand me.”", "백작이 말했다. \"파리에 온 뒤로 자네가 아주 신경이 예민해지고, 도무지 제 물을 만난 것 같지가 않아. 더는 내 말을 못 알아듣는군.\""]], [["“But surely your excellency will be so good as to inform me whom you are expecting to receive?”", "\"하지만 각하께서 누구를 맞으실 생각이신지 알려 주시면 좋겠습니다만.\""]], [["“I do not yet know myself, neither is it necessary that you should do so.", "\"나도 아직 모르고, 자네가 알 필요도 없어."], ["‘Lucullus dines with Lucullus,’ that is quite sufficient.”", "'루쿨루스가 루쿨루스와 식사한다.' 그걸로 충분하네.\""]], [["Bertuccio bowed, and left the room.", "베르투치오가 고개를 숙이고 방을 나섰다."]], [["# Chapter 55. Major Cavalcanti", "# Chapter 55. Major Cavalcanti"]], [["Both the count and Baptistin had told the truth when they announced to Morcerf the proposed visit of the major, which had served Monte Cristo as a pretext for declining Albert’s invitation.", "백작과 바티스탱이 모르세르에게 소령의 방문이 예정되어 있다고 알렸을 때, 두 사람 모두 사실을 말한 것이었다. 그 방문은 몬테크리스토가 알베르의 초대를 거절하는 구실이 되었다."], ["Seven o’clock had just struck, and M.", "일곱 시를 막 알리는 종이 울렸다."], ["Bertuccio, according to the command which had been given him, had two hours before left for Auteuil, when a cab stopped at the door, and after depositing its occupant at the gate, immediately hurried away, as if ashamed of its employment.", "베르투치오 씨는 미리 받은 명령에 따라 두 시간 전에 오퇴유로 떠난 뒤였다. 그때 삯마차 한 대가 문 앞에 멈춰 서더니, 손님을 대문 앞에 내려놓고는 마치 그 일이 부끄럽기라도 한 듯 황급히 떠나 버렸다."], ["The visitor was about fifty-two years of age, dressed in one of the green surtouts, ornamented with black frogs, which have so long maintained their popularity all over Europe.", "손님은 쉰두 살쯤 된 사람으로, 오랫동안 유럽 전역에서 인기를 누려 온, 검은 장식끈이 달린 초록색 프록코트를 입고 있었다."], ["He wore trousers of blue cloth, boots tolerably clean, but not of the brightest polish, and a little too thick in the soles, buckskin gloves, a hat somewhat resembling in shape those usually worn by the gendarmes, and a black cravat striped with white, which, if the proprietor had not worn it of his own free will, might have passed for a halter, so much did it resemble one.", "푸른 천으로 된 바지에, 그런대로 깨끗하지만 광이 가장 잘 나는 것은 아니고 밑창이 조금 두꺼운 장화를 신었다. 사슴 가죽 장갑을 끼고, 헌병들이 흔히 쓰는 것과 비슷한 모양의 모자를 썼으며, 흰 줄무늬가 있는 검은 넥타이를 맸는데, 그것은 주인이 제 발로 매지 않았더라면 교수형 밧줄로 보였을 만큼 그것과 닮아 있었다."], ["Such was the picturesque costume of the person who rang at the gate, and demanded if it was not at No. 30 in the Avenue des Champs-Élysées that the Count of Monte Cristo lived, and who, being answered by the porter in the affirmative, entered, closed the gate after him, and began to ascend the steps.", "대문에서 초인종을 울리고, 샹젤리제 대로 30번지에 몬테크리스토 백작이 사는 게 맞느냐고 물은 사람의 그림 같은 차림새가 그러했다. 문지기가 그렇다고 대답하자, 그는 안으로 들어와 등 뒤로 문을 닫고는 계단을 오르기 시작했다."]], [["The small and angular head of this man, his white hair and thick gray moustaches, caused him to be easily recognized by Baptistin, who had received an exact description of the expected visitor, and who was awaiting him in the hall.", "이 사내의 작고 모난 머리, 흰 머리카락과 숱 많은 잿빛 콧수염 덕분에, 바티스탱은 그를 쉽게 알아보았다. 그는 예정된 손님에 대해 정확한 인상착의를 미리 들었고, 현관에서 그를 기다리고 있었다."], ["Therefore, scarcely had the stranger time to pronounce his name before the count was apprised of his arrival.", "그래서 낯선 사내가 이름을 댈 겨를도 거의 없이 백작은 그의 도착을 통보받았다."], ["He was ushered into a simple and elegant drawing-room, and the count rose to meet him with a smiling air.", "그는 소박하고 우아한 응접실로 안내되었고, 백작이 미소 띤 얼굴로 일어나 그를 맞이했다."]], [["“Ah, my dear sir, you are most welcome; I was expecting you.”", "\"아, 선생, 잘 오셨습니다. 기다리고 있었지요.\""]], [["“Indeed,”", "\"그렇습니까.\""], ["said the Italian, “was your excellency then aware of my visit?”", "이탈리아인이 말했다. \"그럼 각하께서는 제 방문을 알고 계셨던 겁니까?\""]], [["“Yes; I had been told that I should see you today at seven o’clock.”", "\"그렇습니다. 오늘 일곱 시에 뵙게 될 거라고 들었습니다.\""]], [["“Then you have received full information concerning my arrival?”", "\"그럼 제 도착에 관해 빠짐없이 통보받으셨군요?\""]], [["“Of course.”", "\"물론입니다.\""]], [["“Ah, so much the better, I feared this little precaution might have been forgotten.”", "\"아, 그렇다면 다행입니다. 이 작은 조처가 잊혔을까 봐 걱정했거든요.\""]], [["30119m\n“What precaution?”", "\"무슨 조처 말입니까?\""]], [["“That of informing you beforehand of my coming.”", "\"제가 올 거라고 미리 알려 드리는 것 말입니다.\""]], [["“Oh, no, it has not.”", "\"아니요, 잊히지 않았습니다.\""]], [["“But you are sure you are not mistaken.”", "\"하지만 분명 착오가 아니시지요.\""]], [["“Very sure.”", "\"분명합니다.\""]], [["“It really was I whom your excellency expected at seven o’clock this evening?”", "\"오늘 저녁 일곱 시에 각하께서 기다리신 사람이 정말 저였단 말씀이지요?\""]], [["“I will prove it to you beyond a doubt.”", "\"의심할 여지가 없도록 증명해 드리지요.\""]], [["“Oh, no, never mind that,”", "\"아, 아닙니다, 괘념치 마십시오.\""], ["said the Italian; “it is not worth the trouble.”", "이탈리아인이 말했다. \"그럴 것까진 없습니다.\""]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"네, 네.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["His visitor appeared slightly uneasy. “Let me see,”", "손님은 다소 불안해 보였다. \"어디 봅시다.\""], ["said the count; “are you not the Marquis Bartolomeo Cavalcanti?”", "백작이 말했다. \"바르톨로메오 카발칸티 후작이 아니십니까?\""]], [["“Bartolomeo Cavalcanti,”", "\"바르톨로메오 카발칸티.\""], ["joyfully replied the Italian; “yes, I am really he.”", "이탈리아인이 기쁘게 대답했다. \"그렇습니다, 제가 바로 그 사람입니다.\""]], [["“Ex-major in the Austrian service?”", "\"오스트리아군에서 복무하신 전직 소령이시지요?\""]], [["“Was I a major?” timidly asked the old soldier.", "\"제가 소령이었습니까?\" 노병이 조심스럽게 물었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇습니다.\""], ["said Monte Cristo “you were a major; that is the title the French give to the post which you filled in Italy.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"소령이셨지요. 이탈리아에서 맡으셨던 직책을 프랑스인들은 그렇게 부릅니다.\""]], [["“Very good,”", "\"좋습니다.\""], ["said the major, “I do not demand more, you understand——”", "소령이 말했다. \"더 바랄 게 없지요, 무슨 말씀인지 아시겠지만……\""]], [["“Your visit here today is not of your own suggestion, is it?”", "\"오늘 이곳을 방문하신 것은 선생 스스로의 생각이 아니지요?\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“No, certainly not.”", "\"네, 분명 아닙니다.\""]], [["“You were sent by some other person?”", "\"누군가 다른 사람이 보낸 겁니까?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“By the excellent Abbé Busoni?”", "\"그 훌륭한 부소니 신부가요?\""]], [["“Exactly so,” said the delighted major.", "\"바로 그렇습니다.\" 소령이 반색하며 말했다."]], [["“And you have a letter?”", "\"편지를 가지고 계십니까?\""]], [["“Yes, there it is.”", "\"네, 여기 있습니다.\""]], [["“Give it to me, then.” And Monte Cristo took the letter, which he opened and read.", "\"그럼 제게 주십시오.\" 몬테크리스토는 편지를 받아 펼쳐 읽었다."], ["The major looked at the count with his large staring eyes, and then took a survey of the apartment, but his gaze almost immediately reverted to the proprietor of the room.", "소령은 크고 휑한 눈으로 백작을 바라보다가 방 안을 둘러보았으나, 그 시선은 곧바로 다시 방 주인에게로 돌아왔다."]], [["“Yes, yes, I see. ‘Major Cavalcanti, a worthy patrician of Lucca, a descendant of the Cavalcanti of Florence,’”", "\"그렇군요, 그래요, 알겠습니다. '루카의 훌륭한 귀족이며 피렌체 카발칸티 가문의 후손인 카발칸티 소령은,'\""], ["continued Monte Cristo, reading aloud, “‘possessing an income of half a million.’”", "몬테크리스토가 소리 내어 읽기를 이어 갔다. \"'오십만의 수입을 가진 분으로.'\""]], [["Monte Cristo raised his eyes from the paper, and bowed.", "몬테크리스토는 종이에서 눈을 들어 고개를 숙였다."]], [["“Half a million,”", "\"오십만이라니.\""], ["said he, “magnificent!”", "그가 말했다. \"대단하십니다!\""]], [["“Half a million, is it?”", "\"오십만이라고요?\""], ["said the major.", "소령이 말했다."]], [["“Yes, in so many words; and it must be so, for the abbé knows correctly the amount of all the largest fortunes in Europe.”", "\"네, 글자 그대로요. 또 분명 그러할 겁니다. 신부님은 유럽의 큰 재산들의 액수를 모두 정확히 알고 계시니까요.\""]], [["“Be it half a million, then; but on my word of honor, I had no idea that it was so much.”", "\"오십만이라 칩시다. 하지만 맹세코, 그렇게나 많은 줄은 몰랐습니다.\""]], [["“Because you are robbed by your steward. You must make some reformation in that quarter.”", "\"집사에게 도둑맞고 계시기 때문이지요. 그쪽을 좀 손보셔야겠습니다.\""]], [["“You have opened my eyes,”", "\"눈을 뜨게 해 주셨군요.\""], ["said the Italian gravely; “I will show the gentlemen the door.”", "이탈리아인이 근엄하게 말했다. \"그자들을 내쫓겠습니다.\""]], [["Monte Cristo resumed the perusal of the letter:", "몬테크리스토는 다시 편지를 읽어 내려갔다."]], [["“‘And who only needs one thing more to make him happy.’”", "\"'그리고 그를 행복하게 하는 데에는 단 한 가지가 더 필요할 뿐이니.'\""]], [["“Yes, indeed but one!” said the major with a sigh.", "\"그렇습니다, 정말이지 단 한 가지뿐이지요!\" 소령이 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."]], [["“‘Which is to recover a lost and adored son.’”", "\"'그것은 잃어버린, 그토록 사랑하는 아들을 되찾는 일이라.'\""]], [["“A lost and adored son!”", "\"잃어버린, 그토록 사랑하는 아들이라니!\""]], [["“‘Stolen away in his infancy, either by an enemy of his noble family or by the gypsies.’”", "\"'그 아이는 어릴 적에 고귀한 가문의 원수에게, 혹은 집시들에게 빼앗겼으니.'\""]], [["“At the age of five years!”", "\"다섯 살 때였습니다!\""], ["said the major with a deep sigh, and raising his eye to heaven.", "소령이 깊은 한숨을 내쉬며 하늘로 눈을 들어 올렸다."]], [["“Unhappy father,”", "\"불행한 아버지로군요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["The count continued:", "백작은 계속 읽어 나갔다."]], [["“‘I have given him renewed life and hope, in the assurance that you have the power of restoring the son whom he has vainly sought for fifteen years.’”", "\"'나는 그에게 새로운 삶과 희망을 주었으니, 그가 십오 년 동안 헛되이 찾아 헤맨 아들을 그대가 되돌려 줄 힘이 있음을 확신하는 까닭이라.'\""]], [["The major looked at the count with an indescribable expression of anxiety.", "소령은 형언할 수 없는 불안의 표정으로 백작을 바라보았다."]], [["“I have the power of so doing,”", "\"제게 그럴 힘이 있지요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo. The major recovered his self-possession.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. 소령은 평정을 되찾았다."]], [["“So, then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["said he, “the letter was true to the end?”", "그가 말했다. \"이 편지는 끝까지 사실이었군요?\""]], [["“Did you doubt it, my dear Monsieur Bartolomeo?”", "\"의심하셨습니까, 친애하는 바르톨로메오 씨?\""]], [["“No, indeed; certainly not; a good man, a man holding religious office, as does the Abbé Busoni, could not condescend to deceive or play off a joke; but your excellency has not read all.”", "\"아니요, 정말 아닙니다. 부소니 신부 같은 선량한 분, 성직을 맡으신 분이 사람을 속이거나 농을 거실 리야 없지요. 하지만 각하께서는 다 읽지 않으셨습니다.\""]], [["“Ah, true,”", "\"아, 그렇군요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo “there is a postscript.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"추신이 있군요.\""]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"네, 그렇습니다.\""], ["repeated the major, “yes—there—is—a—postscript.”", "소령이 되풀이했다. \"네, 추신이, 있습니다.\""]], [["“‘In order to save Major Cavalcanti the trouble of drawing on his banker, I send him a draft for 2,000 francs to defray his travelling expenses, and credit on you for the further sum of 48,000 francs, which you still owe me.’”", "\"'카발칸티 소령이 자기 거래 은행에 손을 댈 수고를 덜어 주고자, 나는 그에게 여비조로 이천 프랑의 환어음을 보내며, 그대에게는 그대가 아직 내게 빚진 사만 팔천 프랑의 추가 금액을 그에게 지급하도록 신용장을 보내노라.'\""]], [["The major awaited the conclusion of the postscript, apparently with great anxiety.", "소령은 추신의 결말을 기다렸는데, 보기에 몹시 불안한 기색이었다."]], [["“Very good,” said the count.", "\"아주 좋습니다.\" 백작이 말했다."]], [["“He said ‘very good,’”", "\"'아주 좋다'고 하셨겠다.\""], ["muttered the major, “then—sir——”", "소령이 중얼거리고는 \"그렇다면, 각하……\""], ["replied he.", "하고 말을 받았다."]], [["“Then what?”", "\"그렇다면 뭡니까?\""], ["asked Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 물었다."]], [["“Then the postscript——”", "\"그렇다면 추신은……\""]], [["“Well; what of the postscript?”", "\"네, 추신이 어떻단 말입니까?\""]], [["“Then the postscript is as favorably received by you as the rest of the letter?”", "\"그렇다면 추신도 편지의 나머지 부분만큼이나 흡족하게 받아들여진 겁니까?\""]], [["“Certainly; the Abbé Busoni and myself have a small account open between us.", "\"물론입니다. 부소니 신부와 저 사이에는 자그마한 외상 거래가 트여 있지요."], ["I do not remember if it is exactly 48,000 francs, which I am still owing him, but I dare say we shall not dispute the difference.", "제가 아직 그분께 빚진 게 정확히 사만 팔천 프랑인지는 기억나지 않습니다만, 그 차액을 두고 다툴 일은 없을 겁니다."], ["You attached great importance, then, to this postscript, my dear Monsieur Cavalcanti?”", "그러니까 이 추신을 무척 중요하게 여기셨군요, 친애하는 카발칸티 씨?\""]], [["“I must explain to you,”", "\"설명을 드려야겠습니다.\""], ["said the major, “that, fully confiding in the signature of the Abbé Busoni, I had not provided myself with any other funds; so that if this resource had failed me, I should have found myself very unpleasantly situated in Paris.”", "소령이 말했다. \"부소니 신부의 서명을 전적으로 믿고서, 저는 다른 자금을 마련해 두지 않았습니다. 그러니 만약 이 자금줄이 끊겼다면, 저는 파리에서 무척 난처한 처지에 놓였을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Is it possible that a man of your standing should be embarrassed anywhere?”", "\"선생 같은 신분의 분이 어디서든 곤경에 처할 수 있단 말입니까?\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“Why, really I know no one,”", "\"실은, 아는 사람이 하나도 없어서요.\""], ["said the major.", "소령이 말했다."]], [["“But then you yourself are known to others?”", "\"하지만 그럼 선생 자신은 다른 사람들에게 알려져 있지 않습니까?\""]], [["“Yes, I am known, so that——”", "\"네, 저는 알려져 있지요, 그러니까……\""]], [["“Proceed, my dear Monsieur Cavalcanti.”", "\"말씀해 보시지요, 친애하는 카발칸티 씨.\""]], [["“So that you will remit to me these 48,000 francs?”", "\"그러니까 제게 그 사만 팔천 프랑을 내주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“Certainly, at your first request.”", "\"물론입니다, 청하시는 즉시 드리지요.\""], ["The major’s eyes dilated with pleasing astonishment. “But sit down,”", "소령의 눈이 흐뭇한 놀라움으로 휘둥그레졌다. \"그건 그렇고 앉으시지요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “really I do not know what I have been thinking of—I have positively kept you standing for the last quarter of an hour.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"정말이지 제가 무슨 생각을 하고 있었는지 모르겠군요. 지난 십오 분 동안 손님을 줄곧 세워 두었다니 말입니다.\""]], [["“Don’t mention it.”", "\"별말씀을요.\""], ["The major drew an armchair towards him, and proceeded to seat himself.", "소령은 안락의자를 끌어당겨 자리에 앉았다."]], [["“Now,”", "\"자,\""], ["said the count, “what will you take—a glass of sherry, port, or Alicante?”", "백작이 말했다. \"무엇을 드시겠습니까. 셰리주 한 잔, 아니면 포르토주, 알리칸테주?\""]], [["“Alicante, if you please; it is my favorite wine.”", "\"괜찮으시다면 알리칸테주로 하지요. 제가 가장 좋아하는 술입니다.\""]], [["“I have some that is very good. You will take a biscuit with it, will you not?”", "\"아주 좋은 게 좀 있지요. 비스킷도 곁들이시겠지요?\""]], [["“Yes, I will take a biscuit, as you are so obliging.”", "\"네, 이렇게 친절을 베푸시니 비스킷도 들겠습니다.\""]], [["Monte Cristo rang; Baptistin appeared.", "몬테크리스토가 종을 울렸다. 바티스탱이 나타났다."], ["The count advanced to meet him.", "백작이 그를 맞으러 다가갔다."]], [["“Well?”", "\"어떤가?\""], ["said he in a low voice.", "그가 낮은 목소리로 물었다."]], [["“The young man is here,” said the valet de chambre in the same tone.", "\"젊은이가 와 있습니다.\" 하인이 같은 어조로 대답했다."]], [["“Into what room did you take him?”", "\"어느 방으로 안내했나?\""]], [["“Into the blue drawing-room, according to your excellency’s orders.”", "\"각하의 분부대로 푸른 응접실로 모셨습니다.\""]], [["“That’s right; now bring the Alicante and some biscuits.”", "\"잘했네. 자, 이제 알리칸테주와 비스킷을 가져오게.\""]], [["Baptistin left the room.", "바티스탱이 방을 나갔다."]], [["“Really,”", "\"정말이지,\""], ["said the major, “I am quite ashamed of the trouble I am giving you.”", "소령이 말했다. \"이렇게 폐를 끼치니 몸 둘 바를 모르겠습니다.\""]], [["“Pray don’t mention such a thing,”", "\"그런 말씀 마십시오.\""], ["said the count.", "백작이 말했다."], ["Baptistin re-entered with glasses, wine, and biscuits.", "바티스탱이 잔과 포도주, 비스킷을 들고 다시 들어왔다."], ["The count filled one glass, but in the other he only poured a few drops of the ruby-colored liquid.", "백작은 한 잔을 가득 채웠으나, 다른 잔에는 루비빛 액체를 몇 방울만 따랐다."], ["The bottle was covered with spiders’ webs, and all the other signs which indicate the age of wine more truly than do wrinkles on a man’s face.", "술병은 거미줄로 뒤덮여 있었고, 사람 얼굴의 주름보다도 더 정확하게 포도주의 연륜을 말해 주는 온갖 흔적이 가득했다."], ["The major made a wise choice; he took the full glass and a biscuit.", "소령은 현명한 선택을 했다. 그는 가득 찬 잔과 비스킷을 집었다."], ["The count told Baptistin to leave the plate within reach of his guest, who began by sipping the Alicante with an expression of great satisfaction, and then delicately steeped his biscuit in the wine. 30123m", "백작은 바티스탱에게 접시를 손님 손이 닿는 곳에 두라고 일렀고, 손님은 우선 알리칸테주를 한 모금 음미하며 무척 흡족한 표정을 짓고는, 비스킷을 포도주에 정갈하게 적셨다."]], [["“So, sir, you lived at Lucca, did you?", "\"그래, 선생은 루카에 사셨다지요?"], ["You were rich, noble, held in great esteem—had all that could render a man happy?”", "부유하고, 고귀하고, 큰 존경을 받으며, 사람을 행복하게 할 모든 것을 가지셨고요?\""]], [["“All,”", "\"전부 다요.\""], ["said the major, hastily swallowing his biscuit, “positively all.”", "소령이 비스킷을 황급히 삼키며 말했다. \"정말이지 전부 다요.\""]], [["“And yet there was one thing wanting in order to complete your happiness?”", "\"그런데도 그 행복을 완성하는 데에 한 가지가 빠져 있었군요?\""]], [["“Only one thing,”", "\"단 한 가지가요.\""], ["said the Italian.", "이탈리아인이 말했다."]], [["“And that one thing, your lost child.”", "\"그리고 그 한 가지가, 바로 잃어버린 아이로군요.\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아.\""], ["said the major, taking a second biscuit, “that consummation of my happiness was indeed wanting.”", "소령이 두 번째 비스킷을 집으며 말했다. \"제 행복을 완성할 그것이 정말이지 빠져 있었지요.\""], ["The worthy major raised his eyes to heaven and sighed.", "갸륵한 소령은 하늘로 눈을 들어 올리며 한숨을 쉬었다."]], [["“Let me hear, then,”", "\"그럼 한번 들려주시지요.\""], ["said the count, “who this deeply regretted son was; for I always understood you were a bachelor.”", "백작이 말했다. \"그토록 애통해하시는 아드님이 누구인지를요. 저는 늘 선생을 독신으로 알고 있었거든요.\""]], [["“That was the general opinion, sir,”", "\"세간의 평이 그러했지요, 각하.\""], ["said the major, “and I——”", "소령이 말했다. \"그리고 저는……\""]], [["“Yes,” replied the count, “and you confirmed the report.", "\"그렇지요.\" 백작이 받았다."], ["A youthful indiscretion, I suppose, which you were anxious to conceal from the world at large?”", "\"선생도 그 소문을 굳히셨고요. 젊은 날의 경솔한 과오를 세상에 감추고 싶으셨던 게로군요?\""]], [["The major recovered himself, and resumed his usual calm manner, at the same time casting his eyes down, either to give himself time to compose his countenance, or to assist his imagination, all the while giving an under-look at the count, the protracted smile on whose lips still announced the same polite curiosity.", "소령은 마음을 추슬러 다시 평소의 차분한 태도로 돌아갔다. 그러면서 눈을 내리깔았는데, 얼굴 표정을 가다듬을 시간을 벌려는 것인지 상상을 짜내려는 것인지 알 수 없었다. 그러는 내내 백작을 슬쩍슬쩍 곁눈질했는데, 백작의 입가에 길게 머무는 미소는 여전히 변함없는 정중한 호기심을 드러내고 있었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇습니다.\""], ["said the major, “I did wish this fault to be hidden from every eye.”", "소령이 말했다. \"저는 그 과오를 모든 이의 눈에서 감추고 싶었지요.\""]], [["“Not on your own account, surely,”", "\"설마 선생 자신을 위해서는 아니겠지요.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo; “for a man is above that sort of thing?”", "몬테크리스토가 받았다. \"사내라면 그런 일쯤은 초연한 법이니까요?\""]], [["“Oh, no, certainly not on my own account,” said the major with a smile and a shake of the head.", "\"아, 아닙니다, 분명 저 자신을 위해서는 아니었지요.\" 소령이 미소 지으며 고개를 저었다."]], [["“But for the sake of the mother?”", "\"그럼 그 어머니를 위해서?\""], ["said the count.", "백작이 말했다."]], [["“Yes, for the mother’s sake—his poor mother!”", "\"그렇습니다, 그 어머니를 위해서지요. 가엾은 그 어미를!\""], ["cried the major, taking a third biscuit.", "소령이 세 번째 비스킷을 집으며 외쳤다."]], [["“Take some more wine, my dear Cavalcanti,”", "\"포도주를 좀 더 드시지요, 친애하는 카발칸티 씨.\""], ["said the count, pouring out for him a second glass of Alicante; “your emotion has quite overcome you.”", "백작이 그에게 알리칸테주를 두 번째 잔으로 따라 주며 말했다. \"감정이 북받쳐 어쩔 줄 모르시는군요.\""]], [["“His poor mother,” murmured the major, trying to get the lachrymal gland in operation, so as to moisten the corner of his eye with a false tear.", "\"그 가엾은 어미를.\" 소령이 중얼거리며, 거짓 눈물로 눈가를 적시려고 눈물샘을 작동시키려 애썼다."]], [["“She belonged to one of the first families in Italy, I think, did she not?”", "\"제 생각에 그분은 이탈리아의 손꼽히는 가문 출신이었던 것 같은데, 그렇지 않습니까?\""]], [["“She was of a noble family of Fiesole, count.”", "\"피에솔레의 고귀한 가문 출신이었습니다, 백작님.\""]], [["“And her name was——”", "\"그리고 그분 성함은……\""]], [["“Do you desire to know her name——?”", "\"그 이름을 알고 싶으십니까……?\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"아.\""], ["said Monte Cristo “it would be quite superfluous for you to tell me, for I already know it.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"굳이 말씀하실 것 없습니다. 제가 이미 알고 있으니까요.\""]], [["“The count knows everything,”", "\"백작님은 모르시는 게 없으시군요.\""], ["said the Italian, bowing.", "이탈리아인이 고개를 숙이며 말했다."]], [["“Oliva Corsinari, was it not?”", "\"올리바 코르시나리, 아니었습니까?\""]], [["“Oliva Corsinari!”", "\"올리바 코르시나리!\""]], [["“A marchioness?”", "\"후작 부인이셨고요?\""]], [["“A marchioness!”", "\"후작 부인이었지요!\""]], [["“And you married her at last, notwithstanding the opposition of her family?”", "\"그래서 가문의 반대에도 불구하고 마침내 그분과 결혼하셨고요?\""]], [["“Yes, that was the way it ended.”", "\"네, 그렇게 매듭지어졌지요.\""]], [["“And you have doubtless brought all your papers with you?” said Monte Cristo.", "\"그럼 분명 서류를 모두 가져오셨겠지요?\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“What papers?”", "\"무슨 서류 말씀입니까?\""]], [["“The certificate of your marriage with Oliva Corsinari, and the register of your child’s birth.”", "\"올리바 코르시나리와의 혼인 증명서, 그리고 자제분의 출생 기록 말입니다.\""]], [["“The register of my child’s birth?”", "\"제 아이의 출생 기록이요?\""]], [["“The register of the birth of Andrea Cavalcanti—of your son; is not his name Andrea?”", "\"안드레아 카발칸티의, 그러니까 아드님의 출생 기록 말입니다. 이름이 안드레아 아닙니까?\""]], [["“I believe so,” said the major.", "\"그런 것 같습니다.\" 소령이 말했다."]], [["“What? You believe so?”", "\"뭐라고요? 그런 것 같다니요?\""]], [["“I dare not positively assert it, as he has been lost for so long a time.”", "\"워낙 오랫동안 잃어버렸던 터라, 확실히 단언하기는 어렵습니다.\""]], [["“Well, then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["said Monte Cristo “you have all the documents with you?”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"서류는 모두 가지고 계시겠지요?\""]], [["“Your excellency, I regret to say that, not knowing it was necessary to come provided with these papers, I neglected to bring them.”", "\"각하, 송구하게도 그 서류들을 갖춰 와야 하는 줄 몰라서, 챙겨 오지 못했습니다.\""]], [["“That is unfortunate,”", "\"그것 참 안타깝군요.\""], ["returned Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 받았다."]], [["“Were they, then, so necessary?”", "\"그게, 그렇게나 필요했습니까?\""]], [["“They were indispensable.”", "\"없어서는 안 될 것이었지요.\""]], [["The major passed his hand across his brow.", "소령은 손으로 이마를 쓸었다."], ["“Ah, _perbacco_, indispensable, were they?”", "\"아, 페르바코, 없어서는 안 된다고요?\""]], [["“Certainly they were; supposing there were to be doubts raised as to the validity of your marriage or the legitimacy of your child?”", "\"물론이지요. 선생의 혼인이 유효한지, 자제분이 적자인지에 대해 의혹이 제기된다고 쳐 보십시오.\""]], [["“True,”", "\"그렇군요.\""], ["said the major, “there might be doubts raised.”", "소령이 말했다. \"의혹이 제기될 수도 있겠지요.\""]], [["“In that case your son would be very unpleasantly situated.”", "\"그렇게 되면 아드님은 무척 난처한 처지에 놓일 겁니다.\""]], [["“It would be fatal to his interests.”", "\"그 애의 이익에 치명적이겠지요.\""]], [["“It might cause him to fail in some desirable matrimonial alliance.”", "\"바라 마지않던 혼인을 그르치게 될 수도 있고요.\""]], [["“_O peccato!_”", "\"오, 페카토!\""]], [["“You must know that in France they are very particular on these points; it is not sufficient, as in Italy, to go to the priest and say, ‘We love each other, and want you to marry us.’ Marriage is a civil affair in France, and in order to marry in an orthodox manner you must have papers which undeniably establish your identity.”", "\"아셔야 할 것이, 프랑스에서는 이런 점에 무척 까다롭습니다. 이탈리아에서처럼 신부님께 가서 '우리는 서로 사랑하니 결혼시켜 주십시오'라고 말하는 것으로는 충분치 않지요. 프랑스에서 결혼은 민사상의 일이라, 격식에 맞게 결혼하려면 신원을 의심할 여지 없이 입증하는 서류가 있어야 합니다.\""]], [["“That is the misfortune!", "\"그게 바로 불운이지요!"], ["You see I have not these necessary papers.”", "보시다시피 저는 그 필요한 서류들이 없으니 말입니다.\""]], [["“Fortunately, I have them, though,”", "\"다행히도 제게는 그것들이 있습니다만.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"각하께서요?\""]], [["“You?”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Yes.” “You have them?”", "\"각하께서 그걸 가지고 계신다고요?\""]], [["“I have them.”", "\"가지고 있습니다.\""]], [["“Ah, indeed?”", "\"아, 정말입니까?\" 소령이 말했다. 그는 서류가 없어 여행의 목적이 좌절될까, 또한 자신의 깜빡함이 사만 팔천 프랑을 둘러싼 곤란을 빚어낼까 두려워하던 참이었다."], ["said the major, who, seeing the object of his journey frustrated by the absence of the papers, feared also that his forgetfulness might give rise to some difficulty concerning the 48,000 francs—“ah, indeed, that is a fortunate circumstance; yes, that really is lucky, for it never occurred to me to bring them.”", "\"아, 정말이지, 그것 참 다행한 일이군요. 네, 정말 운이 좋습니다. 그것들을 가져올 생각은 미처 못 했거든요.\""]], [["“I do not at all wonder at it—one cannot think of everything; but, happily, the Abbé Busoni thought for you.”", "\"조금도 이상하지 않습니다. 모든 걸 일일이 다 생각할 수는 없는 노릇이지요. 하지만 다행히도 부소니 신부가 선생 대신 생각해 두셨습니다.\""]], [["“He is an excellent person.”", "\"훌륭한 분이지요.\""]], [["“He is extremely prudent and thoughtful.”", "\"대단히 신중하고 사려 깊은 분입니다.\""]], [["“He is an admirable man,”", "\"감탄스러운 분이지요.\""], ["said the major; “and he sent them to you?”", "소령이 말했다. \"그분이 그걸 각하께 보내셨군요?\""]], [["“Here they are.”", "\"여기 있습니다.\""]], [["The major clasped his hands in token of admiration.", "소령은 감탄의 표시로 두 손을 맞잡았다."]], [["“You married Oliva Corsinari in the church of San Paolo del Monte-Cattini; here is the priest’s certificate.”", "\"선생은 산파올로델몬테카티니 성당에서 올리바 코르시나리와 결혼하셨지요. 여기 신부님의 증명서가 있습니다.\""]], [["“Yes indeed, there it is truly,”", "\"네, 정말이지 그게 맞습니다, 틀림없어요.\""], ["said the Italian, looking on with astonishment.", "이탈리아인이 놀라움에 차서 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["“And here is Andrea Cavalcanti’s baptismal register, given by the curé of Saravezza.”", "\"그리고 여기 안드레아 카발칸티의 세례 기록이 있습니다. 사라베차의 주임 신부가 작성한 것이지요.\""]], [["“All quite correct.”", "\"전부 아주 정확합니다.\""]], [["“Take these documents, then; they do not concern me.", "\"그럼 이 서류들을 받으십시오."], ["You will give them to your son, who will, of course, take great care of them.”", "제게는 소용없는 것들이니까요. 이걸 아드님께 주십시오. 물론 잘 간수하겠지요.\""]], [["“I should think so, indeed! If he were to lose them——”", "\"그렇고말고요! 만약 이걸 잃어버리기라도 한다면……\""]], [["“Well, and if he were to lose them?”", "\"그래요, 만약 잃어버린다면요?\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“In that case,”", "\"그렇게 되면,\""], ["replied the major, “it would be necessary to write to the curé for duplicates, and it would be some time before they could be obtained.”", "소령이 대답했다. \"주임 신부에게 편지를 써서 사본을 받아야 할 텐데, 손에 넣기까지 한참 걸릴 겁니다.\""]], [["“It would be a difficult matter to arrange,” said Monte Cristo.", "\"수습하기가 까다로운 일이겠군요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“Almost an impossibility,”", "\"거의 불가능한 일이지요.\""], ["replied the major.", "소령이 대답했다."]], [["“I am very glad to see that you understand the value of these papers.”", "\"이 서류들의 가치를 알아주시니 무척 기쁩니다.\""]], [["“I regard them as invaluable.”", "\"더없이 귀한 것으로 여기지요.\""]], [["“Now,”", "\"자, 이제.\""], ["said Monte Cristo “as to the mother of the young man——”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그 젊은이의 어머니에 관해서는……\""]], [["“As to the mother of the young man——”", "\"그 젊은이의 어머니에 관해서는……\""], ["repeated the Italian, with anxiety.", "이탈리아인이 불안하게 되풀이했다."]], [["“As regards the Marchesa Corsinari——”", "\"코르시나리 후작 부인에 대해 말입니다만……\""]], [["“Really,”", "\"정말이지,\""], ["said the major, “difficulties seem to thicken upon us; will she be wanted in any way?”", "소령이 말했다. \"어려움이 점점 쌓여 가는 것 같군요. 그 사람도 어떤 식으로든 필요합니까?\""]], [["“No, sir,”", "\"아니요, 선생.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo; “besides, has she not——”", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"게다가, 그분은 이미……\""]], [["“Yes, sir,”", "\"네, 각하.\""], ["said the major, “she has——” 30127m", "소령이 말했다. \"그 사람은 이미……\""]], [["“Paid the last debt of nature?”", "\"세상을 떠나셨지요?\""]], [["“Alas, yes,” returned the Italian.", "\"아아, 그렇습니다.\" 이탈리아인이 받았다."]], [["“I knew that,”", "\"알고 있었습니다.\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “she has been dead these ten years.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그분은 십 년 전에 돌아가셨지요.\""]], [["“And I am still mourning her loss,” exclaimed the major, drawing from his pocket a checked handkerchief, and alternately wiping first the left and then the right eye.", "\"그리고 저는 아직도 그 사람을 잃은 슬픔에 잠겨 있답니다.\" 소령이 주머니에서 체크무늬 손수건을 꺼내, 왼쪽 눈과 오른쪽 눈을 번갈아 닦으며 외쳤다."]], [["“What would you have?”", "\"어쩌겠습니까?\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “we are all mortal.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["Now, you understand, my dear Monsieur Cavalcanti, that it is useless for you to tell people in France that you have been separated from your son for fifteen years.", "\"우리는 모두 죽게 마련이지요. 자, 친애하는 카발칸티 씨, 이제 아셔야 할 것이, 프랑스 사람들에게 아드님과 십오 년 동안 떨어져 지냈다고 말해 봐야 소용없습니다."], ["Stories of gypsies, who steal children, are not at all in vogue in this part of the world, and would not be believed.", "아이를 훔쳐 가는 집시 이야기 따위는 이 고장에서는 전혀 통하지 않고, 믿어 주지도 않을 테니까요."], ["You sent him for his education to a college in one of the provinces, and now you wish him to complete his education in the Parisian world.", "선생은 아드님을 교육하려고 지방의 어느 학교에 보냈고, 이제는 파리 사교계에서 그 교육을 완성시키고자 하는 겁니다."], ["That is the reason which has induced you to leave Via Reggio, where you have lived since the death of your wife.", "바로 그 때문에 부인이 돌아가신 뒤로 줄곧 살아온 비아레조를 떠나신 거고요."], ["That will be sufficient.”", "그 정도면 충분할 겁니다.\""]], [["“You think so?”", "\"그렇게 생각하십니까?\""]], [["“Certainly.”", "\"물론입니다.\""]], [["“Very well, then.”", "\"좋습니다, 그럼.\""]], [["“If they should hear of the separation——”", "\"혹시 떨어져 지낸 일에 대해 사람들이 듣게 되면……\""]], [["“Ah, yes; what could I say?”", "\"아, 그렇군요. 뭐라고 말하면 됩니까?\""]], [["“That an unfaithful tutor, bought over by the enemies of your family——”", "\"가문의 원수들에게 매수된 부정한 가정교사가……\""]], [["“By the Corsinari?”", "\"코르시나리 가문에게요?\""]], [["“Precisely. Had stolen away this child, in order that your name might become extinct.”", "\"바로 그렇습니다. 선생의 가문을 끊어 버리려고 그 아이를 훔쳐 갔다고 하시면 됩니다.\""]], [["“That is reasonable, since he is an only son.”", "\"그럴듯하군요, 외아들이니 말입니다.\""]], [["“Well, now that all is arranged, do not let these newly awakened remembrances be forgotten.", "\"자, 이제 모든 게 정리됐으니, 이렇게 새로 일깨워진 기억들을 잊지 마십시오."], ["You have, doubtless, already guessed that I was preparing a surprise for you?”", "제가 선생에게 깜짝 놀랄 일을 준비했다는 걸 분명 벌써 눈치채셨겠지요?\""]], [["“An agreeable one?”", "\"기분 좋은 일입니까?\""], ["asked the Italian.", "이탈리아인이 물었다."]], [["“Ah, I see the eye of a father is no more to be deceived than his heart.”", "\"아, 보아하니 아버지의 눈은 그 마음만큼이나 속이기 어렵군요.\""]], [["“Hum!”", "\"흠!\""], ["said the major.", "소령이 말했다."]], [["“Someone has told you the secret; or, perhaps, you guessed that he was here.”", "\"누군가 선생께 비밀을 일러 주었거나, 아니면 그 애가 여기 와 있다는 걸 알아채셨군요.\""]], [["“That who was here?”", "\"누가 여기 와 있다고요?\""]], [["“Your child—your son—your Andrea!”", "\"선생의 아이, 선생의 아드님, 선생의 안드레아 말입니다!\""]], [["“I did guess it,”", "\"짐작하고 있었습니다.\""], ["replied the major with the greatest possible coolness. “Then he is here?”", "소령이 더없이 태연하게 대답했다. \"그래, 그 애가 여기 와 있다고요?\""]], [["“He is,”", "\"와 있습니다.\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “when the valet de chambre came in just now, he told me of his arrival.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"방금 하인이 들어왔을 때, 그 애가 도착했다고 알려 주더군요.\""]], [["“Ah, very well, very well,”", "\"아, 좋습니다, 좋아요.\""], ["said the major, clutching the buttons of his coat at each exclamation.", "소령이 한 마디씩 외칠 때마다 코트 단추를 움켜쥐며 말했다."]], [["“My dear sir,”", "\"선생,\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “I understand your emotion; you must have time to recover yourself.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"감정이 북받치시는 걸 이해합니다. 마음을 추스를 시간이 필요하시겠지요."], ["I will, in the meantime, go and prepare the young man for this much-desired interview, for I presume that he is not less impatient for it than yourself.”", "그동안 저는 가서 이 그토록 바라던 만남에 대비하도록 그 젊은이를 준비시키겠습니다. 그 애도 선생 못지않게 이 만남을 애타게 기다리고 있을 테니까요.\""]], [["“I should quite imagine that to be the case,” said Cavalcanti.", "\"분명 그러리라 짐작됩니다.\" 카발칸티가 말했다."]], [["“Well, in a quarter of an hour he shall be with you.”", "\"그럼 십오 분 뒤에 그 애가 선생께 갈 겁니다.\""]], [["“You will bring him, then?", "\"그럼 직접 데려오시는 겁니까?"], ["You carry your goodness so far as even to present him to me yourself?”", "친히 그 애를 제게 소개해 주시는 데까지 친절을 베푸시는 겁니까?\""]], [["“No; I do not wish to come between a father and son.", "\"아니요. 저는 아버지와 아들 사이에 끼어들고 싶지 않습니다."], ["Your interview will be private.", "두 분의 만남은 단둘이 하셔야지요. 하지만 염려 마십시오."], ["But do not be uneasy; even if the powerful voice of nature should be silent, you cannot well mistake him; he will enter by this door.", "설령 강력한 천륜의 목소리가 침묵하더라도, 그 애를 알아보지 못할 리는 없습니다. 이 문으로 들어올 테니까요. 잘생긴 젊은이입니다."], ["He is a fine young man, of fair complexion—a little too fair, perhaps—pleasing in manners; but you will see and judge for yourself.”", "살결이 희고, 어쩌면 좀 지나치게 흴지도 모르지요. 태도가 상냥하고요. 하지만 직접 보고 판단하시지요.\""]], [["“By the way,”", "\"그런데 말입니다,\""], ["said the major, “you know I have only the 2,000 francs which the Abbé Busoni sent me; this sum I have expended upon travelling expenses, and——”", "소령이 말했다. \"아시다시피 제게는 부소니 신부가 보내 준 이천 프랑밖에 없습니다. 그 돈은 여비로 다 써 버렸고, 그래서……\""]], [["“And you want money; that is a matter of course, my dear M. Cavalcanti.", "\"그래서 돈이 필요하시다, 그야 당연한 일이지요, 친애하는 카발칸티 씨."], ["Well, here are 8,000 francs on account.”", "자, 여기 우선 팔천 프랑입니다.\""]], [["The major’s eyes sparkled brilliantly.", "소령의 눈이 환하게 반짝였다."]], [["“It is 40,000 francs which I now owe you,”", "\"이제 제가 선생께 빚진 게 사만 프랑이군요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“Does your excellency wish for a receipt?”", "\"각하께서는 영수증을 원하십니까?\""], ["said the major, at the same time slipping the money into the inner pocket of his coat.", "소령이 그렇게 말하며 돈을 코트 안주머니에 슬그머니 집어넣었다."]], [["“For what?”", "\"뭣 하러요?\""], ["said the count.", "백작이 말했다."]], [["“I thought you might want it to show the Abbé Busoni.”", "\"부소니 신부께 보여 주려면 필요하실까 해서요.\""]], [["“Well, when you receive the remaining 40,000, you shall give me a receipt in full.", "\"그럼, 나머지 사만 프랑을 받으실 때 전액 영수증을 한 장 써 주십시오."], ["Between honest men such excessive precaution is, I think, quite unnecessary.”", "정직한 사람들 사이에 그런 지나친 조심은, 제 생각에는 전혀 필요 없는 일이지요.\""]], [["“Yes, so it is, between perfectly upright people.”", "\"네, 그렇고말고요, 완벽하게 올곧은 사람들 사이에서야.\""]], [["“One word more,”", "\"한마디만 더 하지요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“Say on.”", "\"말씀하시지요.\""]], [["“You will permit me to make one remark?”", "\"한 가지 짚어 드려도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["“Certainly; pray do so.”", "\"물론입니다. 어서 그러시지요.\""]], [["“Then I should advise you to leave off wearing that style of dress.”", "\"그럼 충고하건대, 그런 차림새는 그만 입으시는 게 좋겠습니다.\""]], [["“Indeed,” said the major, regarding himself with an air of complete satisfaction.", "\"그렇습니까.\" 소령이 더없이 흡족한 표정으로 자신의 차림을 내려다보며 말했다."]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["It may be worn at Via Reggio; but that costume, however elegant in itself, has long been out of fashion in Paris.”", "비아레조에서야 입으셔도 괜찮겠지요. 하지만 그 차림은, 그 자체로는 아무리 우아하다 해도, 파리에서는 한물간 지 오래입니다.\""]], [["“That’s unfortunate.”", "\"그것 참 안타깝군요.\""]], [["“Oh, if you really are attached to your old mode of dress; you can easily resume it when you leave Paris.”", "\"아, 정말로 그 옛 차림새에 애착이 있으시다면, 파리를 떠나실 때 얼마든지 다시 입으시면 됩니다.\""]], [["“But what shall I wear?”", "\"하지만 그럼 무엇을 입어야 합니까?\""]], [["“What you find in your trunks.”", "\"가방 안에 있는 것을 입으시지요.\""]], [["“In my trunks?", "\"제 가방 안에요?"], ["I have but one portmanteau.”", "제게는 여행 가방 하나뿐인데요.\""]], [["“I dare say you have nothing else with you.", "\"그것 말고 다른 건 아무것도 안 가져오셨겠지요."], ["What is the use of boring one’s self with so many things?", "그렇게 많은 짐으로 스스로를 번거롭게 할 필요가 어디 있겠습니까?"], ["Besides an old soldier always likes to march with as little baggage as possible.”", "게다가 노병은 늘 가능한 한 짐을 적게 하고 행군하기를 좋아하는 법이지요.\""]], [["“That is just the case—precisely so.”", "\"바로 그렇습니다, 정확히 그렇지요.\""]], [["“But you are a man of foresight and prudence, therefore you sent your luggage on before you.", "\"하지만 선생은 선견지명과 신중함을 갖춘 분이니, 짐은 미리 부쳐 두셨겠지요."], ["It has arrived at the Hôtel des Princes, Rue de Richelieu.", "그것이 리슐리외 거리의 프랭스 호텔에 도착해 있습니다."], ["It is there you are to take up your quarters.”", "그곳에 거처를 정하시면 됩니다.\""]], [["“Then, in these trunks——”", "\"그럼, 그 가방 안에는……\""]], [["“I presume you have given orders to your valet de chambre to put in all you are likely to need,—your plain clothes and your uniform.", "\"하인에게 분부해서 필요할 만한 것은 다 챙겨 넣게 하셨겠지요. 평상복과 군복 말입니다."], ["On grand occasions you must wear your uniform; that will look very well.", "큰 행사 때에는 군복을 입으셔야 합니다. 그게 아주 보기 좋을 테니까요."], ["Do not forget your crosses.", "훈장도 잊지 마시고요."], ["They still laugh at them in France, and yet always wear them, for all that.”", "프랑스에서는 아직도 그런 걸 보고 비웃지만, 그러면서도 늘 달고 다니지요.\""]], [["“Very well, very well,” said the major, who was in ecstasy at the attention paid him by the count.", "\"좋습니다, 아주 좋아요.\" 소령은 백작이 베푸는 세심한 배려에 황홀해하며 말했다."]], [["“Now,”", "\"자, 이제.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “that you have fortified yourself against all painful excitement, prepare yourself, my dear M. Cavalcanti, to meet your lost Andrea.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"온갖 괴로운 흥분에 단단히 대비하셨으니, 친애하는 카발칸티 씨, 마음의 준비를 하고 잃어버린 안드레아를 맞이하십시오.\""]], [["Saying which Monte Cristo bowed, and disappeared behind the tapestry, leaving the major fascinated beyond expression with the delightful reception which he had received at the hands of the count.", "그렇게 말하며 몬테크리스토는 고개를 숙이고 벽걸이 천 뒤로 사라졌다. 소령은 백작에게서 받은 더없이 흐뭇한 환대에 형언할 수 없을 만큼 매료된 채 남겨졌다."]], [["# Chapter 56. Andrea Cavalcanti", "# Chapter 56. Andrea Cavalcanti"]], [["The Count of Monte Cristo entered the adjoining room, which Baptistin had designated as the drawing-room, and found there a young man, of graceful demeanor and elegant appearance, who had arrived in a cab about half an hour previously.", "몬테크리스토 백작은 바티스탱이 응접실이라 일러 준 옆방으로 들어갔다. 거기에는 우아한 몸가짐과 세련된 풍모의 한 젊은이가 있었는데, 그는 반 시간쯤 전에 삯마차를 타고 도착해 있었다."], ["Baptistin had not found any difficulty in recognizing the person who presented himself at the door for admittance.", "바티스탱은 문 앞에 나타나 들여보내 달라고 청한 사람을 알아보는 데 조금도 어려움이 없었다."], ["He was certainly the tall young man with light hair, red beard, black eyes, and brilliant complexion, whom his master had so particularly described to him.", "키가 크고 머리칼이 옅으며 수염은 붉고 눈은 검은 데다 안색이 환한 그 젊은이는, 주인이 그토록 세세하게 일러 준 바로 그 사람이 틀림없었다."], ["When the count entered the room the young man was carelessly stretched on a sofa, tapping his boot with the gold-headed cane which he held in his hand.", "백작이 방으로 들어섰을 때 젊은이는 소파에 무심히 몸을 길게 뻗은 채, 손에 쥔 금장식 지팡이로 자기 장화를 톡톡 두드리고 있었다."], ["On perceiving the count he rose quickly.", "백작을 알아본 그는 재빨리 일어섰다."]], [["“The Count of Monte Cristo, I believe?” said he.", "\"몬테크리스토 백작이시죠?\" 그가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, sir, and I think I have the honor of addressing Count Andrea Cavalcanti?”", "\"그렇소. 그리고 안드레아 카발칸티 백작을 뵙는 영광을 누리는 듯하군요?\""]], [["“Count Andrea Cavalcanti,” repeated the young man, accompanying his words with a bow.", "\"안드레아 카발칸티 백작입니다.\" 젊은이가 말과 함께 고개를 숙이며 되풀이했다."]], [["“You are charged with a letter of introduction addressed to me, are you not?” said the count.", "\"내게 보내는 소개장을 지니고 계시지요?\" 백작이 말했다."]], [["“I did not mention that, because the signature seemed to me so strange.”", "\"그건 말씀드리지 않았습니다. 서명이 어찌나 기묘하던지요.\""]], [["“The letter signed ‘Sinbad the Sailor,’ is it not?”", "\"'뱃사람 신드바드'라고 서명된 편지 아닙니까?\""]], [["“Exactly so.", "\"바로 그렇습니다."], ["Now, as I have never known any Sinbad, with the exception of the one celebrated in the _Thousand and One Nights_——”", "그런데 저는 신드바드라는 사람을 한 번도 안 적이 없습니다. 「천일야화」에 나오는 그 유명한 인물 말고는요.\""]], [["“Well, it is one of his descendants, and a great friend of mine; he is a very rich Englishman, eccentric almost to insanity, and his real name is Lord Wilmore.”", "\"음, 그가 바로 그 신드바드의 후손 중 하나이고, 내 절친한 벗이지요. 광기에 가까울 만큼 괴팍한 대단한 부자 영국인인데, 본명은 윌모어 경입니다.\""]], [["“Ah, indeed?", "\"아, 정말입니까?"], ["Then that explains everything that is extraordinary,”", "그렇다면 모든 기이한 일이 다 설명되는군요.\" 안드레아가 말했다."], ["said Andrea. “He is, then, the same Englishman whom I met—at—ah—yes, indeed.", "\"그러니까 그분이, 제가 만났던 그 영국인이로군요. 어디였더라, 아, 그래요, 정말 그렇습니다."], ["Well, monsieur, I am at your service.”", "자, 백작님, 분부만 내려 주십시오.\""]], [["“If what you say be true,”", "\"당신 말씀이 사실이라면,\""], ["replied the count, smiling, “perhaps you will be kind enough to give me some account of yourself and your family?”", "백작이 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"당신 자신과 가문에 대해 좀 들려주시는 친절을 베풀어 주시겠지요?\""]], [["“Certainly, I will do so,” said the young man, with a quickness which gave proof of his ready invention.", "\"물론입니다, 그렇게 하지요.\" 젊은이가 막힘없는 임기응변을 증명하는 빠른 말씨로 대답했다."], ["“I am (as you have said) the Count Andrea Cavalcanti, son of Major Bartolomeo Cavalcanti, a descendant of the Cavalcanti whose names are inscribed in the golden book at Florence.", "\"저는 (말씀하신 대로) 안드레아 카발칸티 백작이며, 바르톨로메오 카발칸티 소령의 아들입니다. 피렌체의 황금 명부에 이름이 새겨진 카발칸티 가문의 후손이지요."], ["Our family, although still rich (for my father’s income amounts to half a million), has experienced many misfortunes, and I myself was, at the age of five years, taken away by the treachery of my tutor, so that for fifteen years I have not seen the author of my existence.", "저희 가문은 아직 부유하기는 하나(제 아버지의 수입이 50만에 이르니까요), 숱한 불행을 겪었습니다. 저 자신도 다섯 살 때 가정교사의 배신으로 끌려갔고, 그리하여 십오 년 동안 저를 낳아 주신 분을 뵙지 못했습니다."], ["Since I have arrived at years of discretion and become my own master, I have been constantly seeking him, but all in vain.", "분별이 설 나이에 이르러 제 한 몸의 주인이 된 뒤로, 저는 끊임없이 아버지를 찾아 헤맸으나 모두 헛수고였습니다."], ["At length I received this letter from your friend, which states that my father is in Paris, and authorizes me to address myself to you for information respecting him.”", "그러던 차에 백작님의 벗에게서 이 편지를 받았는데, 제 아버지가 파리에 계시며, 그분에 관한 소식을 얻으려거든 백작님을 찾아뵈라는 내용이었습니다.\""]], [["“Really, all you have related to me is exceedingly interesting,”", "\"정말이지, 당신이 들려준 이야기가 더없이 흥미롭군요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, observing the young man with a gloomy satisfaction; “and you have done well to conform in everything to the wishes of my friend Sinbad; for your father is indeed here, and is seeking you.”", "몬테크리스토가 음울한 만족을 띤 채 젊은이를 살피며 말했다. \"그리고 내 벗 신드바드의 뜻에 일일이 따른 것은 잘하셨소. 당신 아버지가 정말 이곳에 와서 당신을 찾고 계시니까.\""]], [["The count from the moment of first entering the drawing-room, had not once lost sight of the expression of the young man’s countenance; he had admired the assurance of his look and the firmness of his voice; but at these words, so natural in themselves, “Your father is indeed here, and is seeking you,”", "백작은 응접실에 처음 발을 들인 순간부터 젊은이의 표정을 단 한 번도 놓치지 않았다. 그 눈빛의 당당함과 목소리의 확신을 그는 감탄하며 지켜보았다. 그런데 그 자체로는 지극히 자연스러운 \"당신 아버지가 정말 이곳에 와서 당신을 찾고 계시니까.\"라는 말에, 젊은 안드레아는 움찔하며 소리쳤다."], ["young Andrea started, and exclaimed, “My father? Is my father here?”", "\"제 아버지요? 제 아버지가 여기 계신다고요?\""]], [["“Most undoubtedly,”", "\"틀림없소.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo; “your father, Major Bartolomeo Cavalcanti.”", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"당신 아버지, 바르톨로메오 카발칸티 소령 말이오.\""], ["The expression of terror which, for the moment, had overspread the features of the young man, had now disappeared.", "한순간 젊은이의 얼굴을 뒤덮었던 공포의 빛은 이제 사라지고 없었다."]], [["“Ah, yes, that is the name, certainly.", "\"아, 그래요, 분명 그 이름입니다."], ["Major Bartolomeo Cavalcanti.", "바르톨로메오 카발칸티 소령."], ["And you really mean to say; monsieur, that my dear father is here?”", "그래서 정말로, 백작님, 제 사랑하는 아버지가 여기 계시다는 말씀이신지요?\""]], [["“Yes, sir; and I can even add that I have only just left his company.", "\"그렇소. 게다가 방금 그분과 함께 있다가 온 길이라고까지 말할 수 있소."], ["The history which he related to me of his lost son touched me to the quick; indeed, his griefs, hopes, and fears on that subject might furnish material for a most touching and pathetic poem.", "그분이 잃어버린 아들에 관해 들려준 사연이 내 마음을 깊이 울렸지요. 정말이지, 그 문제에 얽힌 그분의 비탄과 희망과 두려움은 더없이 애절하고 가슴 저미는 시 한 편의 소재가 될 만했소."], ["At length, he one day received a letter, stating that the abductors of his son now offered to restore him, or at least to give notice where he might be found, on condition of receiving a large sum of money, by way of ransom.", "그러던 어느 날, 그분은 편지 한 통을 받았소. 아들을 납치한 자들이 이제 그를 돌려주거나, 적어도 어디에서 찾을 수 있는지 알려 주겠노라고, 다만 몸값으로 거액을 받는 조건으로 말이오."], ["Your father did not hesitate an instant, and the sum was sent to the frontier of Piedmont, with a passport signed for Italy.", "당신 아버지는 한순간도 망설이지 않았소. 그래서 그 돈은 이탈리아행 서명이 든 통행증과 함께 피에몬테 국경으로 보내졌소."], ["You were in the south of France, I think?”", "당신은 프랑스 남부에 있었지요, 아마?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["replied Andrea, with an embarrassed air, “I was in the south of France.”", "안드레아가 거북한 기색으로 대답했다. \"프랑스 남부에 있었습니다.\""]], [["“A carriage was to await you at Nice?”", "\"마차 한 대가 니스에서 당신을 기다리기로 되어 있었지요?\""]], [["“Precisely so; and it conveyed me from Nice to Genoa, from Genoa to Turin, from Turin to Chambéry, from Chambéry to Pont-de-Beauvoisin, and from Pont-de-Beauvoisin to Paris.”", "\"바로 그렇습니다. 그 마차가 저를 니스에서 제노바로, 제노바에서 토리노로, 토리노에서 샹베리로, 샹베리에서 퐁드보부아쟁으로, 퐁드보부아쟁에서 파리로 데려다주었습니다.\""]], [["30133m\n“Indeed?", "\"정말이오?"], ["Then your father ought to have met with you on the road, for it is exactly the same route which he himself took, and that is how we have been able to trace your journey to this place.”", "그렇다면 당신 아버지가 도중에 당신과 마주쳤어야 했는데. 그분이 몸소 지나온 길과 정확히 같은 경로니 말이오. 우리가 당신의 여정을 이곳까지 짚어 올 수 있었던 것도 그래서요.\""]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["said Andrea, “if my father had met me, I doubt if he would have recognized me; I must be somewhat altered since he last saw me.”", "안드레아가 말했다. \"아버지가 저와 마주쳤다 해도 저를 알아보셨을지 의문입니다. 마지막으로 보셨을 때와는 제가 꽤 달라졌을 테니까요.\""]], [["“Oh, the voice of nature,”", "\"오, 천륜의 목소리가 있지 않소.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“True,”", "\"맞습니다.\""], ["interrupted the young man, “I had not looked upon it in that light.”", "젊은이가 끼어들며 말했다. \"그렇게는 생각해 보지 못했습니다.\""]], [["“Now,”", "\"자,\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다."], ["replied Monte Cristo “there is only one source of uneasiness left in your father’s mind, which is this—he is anxious to know how you have been employed during your long absence from him, how you have been treated by your persecutors, and if they have conducted themselves towards you with all the deference due to your rank.", "\"이제 당신 아버지 마음에는 한 가지 걱정거리만 남아 있소. 이런 것이오. 그분은 당신이 그 오랜 별리의 세월 동안 무슨 일을 하며 지냈는지, 당신을 박해한 자들에게 어떤 대접을 받았는지, 그들이 당신의 신분에 걸맞은 예우를 다하며 처신했는지를 몹시 알고 싶어 하시오."], ["Finally, he is anxious to see if you have been fortunate enough to escape the bad moral influence to which you have been exposed, and which is infinitely more to be dreaded than any physical suffering; he wishes to discover if the fine abilities with which nature had endowed you have been weakened by want of culture; and, in short, whether you consider yourself capable of resuming and retaining in the world the high position to which your rank entitles you.”", "끝으로 그분은, 당신이 노출되었던 그 나쁜 도덕적 영향, 어떤 육체적 고통보다 한없이 더 두려운 그 영향에서 다행히 벗어났는지를 보고 싶어 하시오. 또 자연이 당신에게 부여한 그 뛰어난 자질이 교양의 결핍으로 무뎌지지는 않았는지 확인하고 싶어 하시고요. 한마디로, 당신이 사교계에서 당신의 신분이 부여하는 그 높은 지위를 되찾아 지켜 나갈 만하다고 스스로 여기는지 알고 싶어 하시는 거요.\""]], [["“Sir!”", "\"백작님!\""], ["exclaimed the young man, quite astounded, “I hope no false report——”", "젊은이가 어안이 벙벙해 소리쳤다. \"부디 거짓된 소문이……\""]], [["“As for myself, I first heard you spoken of by my friend Wilmore, the philanthropist.", "\"나로 말하자면, 자선가인 내 벗 윌모어에게서 당신 이야기를 처음 들었소."], ["I believe he found you in some unpleasant position, but do not know of what nature, for I did not ask, not being inquisitive.", "그가 당신을 어떤 곤란한 처지에서 만난 듯하나, 그게 어떤 성격의 것인지는 모르오. 캐묻기를 좋아하지 않는 터라 묻지 않았으니까."], ["Your misfortunes engaged his sympathies, so you see you must have been interesting.", "당신의 불행이 그의 동정을 샀으니, 보다시피 당신은 흥미로운 사람이었던 셈이오."], ["He told me that he was anxious to restore you to the position which you had lost, and that he would seek your father until he found him.", "그는 당신이 잃어버린 지위를 되찾아 주고 싶다고, 그리고 당신 아버지를 찾아낼 때까지 찾아 헤매겠노라고 내게 말했소."], ["He did seek, and has found him, apparently, since he is here now; and, finally, my friend apprised me of your coming, and gave me a few other instructions relative to your future fortune.", "그는 정말 찾아 나섰고, 보아하니 찾아낸 모양이오. 지금 여기 와 계시니 말이오. 그리고 마침내 내 벗은 당신이 온다는 것을 내게 알리고, 당신의 장래에 관한 몇 가지 다른 지시도 일러 주었소."], ["I am quite aware that my friend Wilmore is peculiar, but he is sincere, and as rich as a gold mine, consequently, he may indulge his eccentricities without any fear of their ruining him, and I have promised to adhere to his instructions.", "내 벗 윌모어가 별난 사람인 건 익히 알지만, 그는 진실한 사람이고 금광만큼이나 부유하오. 따라서 그 기벽들이 자신을 파산시킬 염려 없이 마음껏 누릴 수 있는 사람이지요. 나는 그의 지시를 따르겠노라 약속했소."], ["Now, sir, pray do not be offended at the question I am about to put to you, as it comes in the way of my duty as your patron.", "자, 백작, 이제 내가 당신에게 던질 질문에 부디 언짢아 마시오. 당신의 후견인으로서 마땅히 해야 할 일이라서 그런 것이니."], ["I would wish to know if the misfortunes which have happened to you—misfortunes entirely beyond your control, and which in no degree diminish my regard for you—I would wish to know if they have not, in some measure, contributed to render you a stranger to the world in which your fortune and your name entitle you to make a conspicuous figure?”", "당신에게 닥친 그 불행들이, 전적으로 당신이 어찌할 수 없는 일이었고 내 존경심을 조금도 깎아내리지 않는 그 불행들이, 혹시라도 어느 정도, 당신의 재산과 이름이 마땅히 두드러진 인물이 되게끔 해 주는 그 세계에서 당신을 낯선 이로 만들어 버리지는 않았는지, 그것을 알고 싶소.\""]], [["“Sir,” returned the young man, with a reassurance of manner, “make your mind easy on this score.", "\"백작님,\" 젊은이가 한결 안정된 태도로 대답했다."], ["Those who took me from my father, and who always intended, sooner or later, to sell me again to my original proprietor, as they have now done, calculated that, in order to make the most of their bargain, it would be politic to leave me in possession of all my personal and hereditary worth, and even to increase the value, if possible.", "\"그 점에 관해서는 마음 놓으셔도 됩니다. 저를 아버지에게서 떼어 낸 자들은, 머지않아 저를 원래 주인에게 다시 팔아넘길 작정이었고, 지금 그렇게 한 셈인데, 그 거래에서 가장 큰 이득을 보려면 제 개인적·세습적 가치를 고스란히 지니게 두고, 가능하다면 그 값어치를 더 높여 두는 것이 영리한 처사라고 계산했지요."], ["I have, therefore, received a very good education, and have been treated by these kidnappers very much as the slaves were treated in Asia Minor, whose masters made them grammarians, doctors, and philosophers, in order that they might fetch a higher price in the Roman market.”", "그래서 저는 아주 훌륭한 교육을 받았고, 그 납치범들은 저를 소아시아의 노예처럼 대했습니다. 노예의 주인들이 로마 시장에서 더 비싼 값을 받으려고 그들을 문법학자며 의사며 철학자로 만들었던 것처럼 말입니다.\""]], [["Monte Cristo smiled with satisfaction; it appeared as if he had not expected so much from M. Andrea Cavalcanti.", "몬테크리스토가 흡족한 듯 미소 지었다. 안드레아 카발칸티 씨에게서 이만큼은 기대하지 않았던 듯했다."]], [["“Besides,”", "\"게다가,\""], ["continued the young man, “if there did appear some defect in education, or offence against the established forms of etiquette, I suppose it would be excused, in consideration of the misfortunes which accompanied my birth, and followed me through my youth.”", "젊은이가 말을 이었다. \"혹 교육에 어떤 결함이 보이거나 정해진 예법에 어긋나는 점이 드러난다 해도, 제 출생에 따라붙고 젊은 시절 내내 저를 쫓아다닌 불행들을 헤아려 용서받으리라 생각합니다.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"음,\""], ["said Monte Cristo in an indifferent tone, “you will do as you please, count, for you are the master of your own actions, and are the person most concerned in the matter, but if I were you, I would not divulge a word of these adventures.", "몬테크리스토가 무심한 어조로 말했다. \"백작, 당신 좋을 대로 하시오. 당신은 당신 행동의 주인이고, 이 일에 가장 깊이 얽힌 당사자니까."], ["Your history is quite a romance, and the world, which delights in romances in yellow covers, strangely mistrusts those which are bound in living parchment, even though they be gilded like yourself.", "그러나 내가 당신이라면, 그 모험담은 한마디도 발설하지 않겠소. 당신의 내력은 그야말로 한 편의 소설인데, 노란 표지의 소설을 좋아하는 세상은 정작 살아 있는 양피지에 묶인 소설은, 그게 당신처럼 도금이 되어 있다 해도, 이상하리만치 미심쩍어한단 말이오."], ["This is the kind of difficulty which I wished to represent to you, my dear count.", "이게 바로 내가 당신에게 일러두고 싶은 종류의 곤란함이오, 친애하는 백작."], ["You would hardly have recited your touching history before it would go forth to the world, and be deemed unlikely and unnatural.", "당신이 그 가슴 저미는 내력을 채 다 읊기도 전에, 그것은 세상에 퍼져 나가 있을 법하지 않고 부자연스럽다고 여겨질 거요."], ["You would be no longer a lost child found, but you would be looked upon as an upstart, who had sprung up like a mushroom in the night.", "당신은 더 이상 잃었다가 되찾은 아이가 아니라, 하룻밤 사이 버섯처럼 솟아난 벼락출세자로 취급받게 될 거란 말이오."], ["You might excite a little curiosity, but it is not everyone who likes to be made the centre of observation and the subject of unpleasant remark.”", "약간의 호기심은 불러일으킬지 몰라도, 누구나 다 남들의 이목이 쏠리는 중심이 되고 불쾌한 입방아의 대상이 되기를 좋아하는 건 아니니까.\""]], [["“I agree with you, monsieur,”", "\"동감입니다, 백작님.\""], ["said the young man, turning pale, and, in spite of himself, trembling beneath the scrutinizing look of his companion, “such consequences would be extremely unpleasant.”", "젊은이가 낯빛이 창백해지며, 상대의 꿰뚫는 듯한 눈길 아래 저도 모르게 떨면서 말했다. \"그런 결과는 정말이지 몹시 불쾌하겠지요.\""]], [["“Nevertheless, you must not exaggerate the evil,”", "\"그렇다고 그 폐해를 과장해서도 안 되오.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “for by endeavoring to avoid one fault you will fall into another.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"한 가지 잘못을 피하려다 다른 잘못에 빠질 수 있으니 말이오."], ["You must resolve upon one simple and single line of conduct, and for a man of your intelligence, this plan is as easy as it is necessary; you must form honorable friendships, and by that means counteract the prejudice which may attach to the obscurity of your former life.”", "당신은 단순하고 한결같은 단 하나의 행동 방침을 정해야 하오. 당신만큼 지혜로운 사람에게 이 방책은 필요한 만큼이나 쉬운 일이오. 당신은 명예로운 친분을 쌓아야 하고, 그렇게 함으로써 당신의 흐릿한 지난날에 따라붙을지 모를 편견을 상쇄해야 하오.\""]], [["Andrea visibly changed countenance.", "안드레아의 안색이 눈에 띄게 바뀌었다."]], [["“I would offer myself as your surety and friendly adviser,”", "\"내가 당신의 보증인이자 다정한 조언자로 나서고 싶소만,\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “did I not possess a moral distrust of my best friends, and a sort of inclination to lead others to doubt them too; therefore, in departing from this rule, I should (as the actors say) be playing a part quite out of my line, and should, therefore, run the risk of being hissed, which would be an act of folly.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"내게는 가장 가까운 벗조차 도덕적으로 미덥지 않게 여기는 버릇이 있고, 남들도 그들을 의심하도록 이끌고 싶은 일종의 성향이 있소. 그러니 그 원칙을 벗어난다면 나는 (배우들 말마따나) 내 배역에서 한참 벗어난 연기를 하는 꼴이 될 테고, 그리하여 야유를 받을 위험을 무릅쓰게 될 텐데, 그건 어리석은 짓이지요.\""]], [["“However, your excellency,”", "\"하지만, 각하,\""], ["said Andrea, “in consideration of Lord Wilmore, by whom I was recommended to you——”", "안드레아가 말했다. \"저를 각하께 천거해 주신 윌모어 경을 보아서라도……\""]], [["“Yes, certainly,”", "\"그렇소, 물론이오.\""], ["interrupted Monte Cristo; “but Lord Wilmore did not omit to inform me, my dear M. Andrea, that the season of your youth was rather a stormy one. Ah,”", "몬테크리스토가 가로막았다. \"하지만 윌모어 경은, 친애하는 안드레아 씨, 당신의 젊은 시절이 자못 폭풍 같았다는 것도 빠뜨리지 않고 일러 주었소. 아,\""], ["said the count, watching Andrea’s countenance, “I do not demand any confession from you; it is precisely to avoid that necessity that your father was sent for from Lucca.", "백작이 안드레아의 표정을 살피며 말했다. \"당신에게 어떤 고백을 요구하는 건 아니오. 바로 그런 필요를 면하게 하려고 당신 아버지를 루카에서 모셔 온 것이니까."], ["You shall soon see him.", "곧 그분을 보게 될 거요."], ["He is a little stiff and pompous in his manner, and he is disfigured by his uniform; but when it becomes known that he has been for eighteen years in the Austrian service, all that will be pardoned.", "거동이 좀 뻣뻣하고 거드름을 피우는 데다 군복 탓에 볼품이 없긴 하나, 그분이 십팔 년 동안 오스트리아 군에서 복무했다는 사실이 알려지면 그 모든 게 용서될 거요."], ["We are not generally very severe with the Austrians.", "우리는 대체로 오스트리아 사람들에게 그리 매몰차지 않으니까."], ["In short, you will find your father a very presentable person, I assure you.”", "요컨대, 당신 아버지는 자못 내세울 만한 인물이라는 걸, 내 장담하오.\""]], [["“Ah, sir, you have given me confidence; it is so long since we were separated, that I have not the least remembrance of him, and, besides, you know that in the eyes of the world a large fortune covers all defects.”", "\"아, 백작님, 제게 자신감을 주시는군요. 헤어진 지 워낙 오래라 그분에 대한 기억이라곤 조금도 없습니다. 게다가 백작님도 아시다시피, 세상의 눈에는 막대한 재산이 모든 결함을 덮어 주는 법이니까요.\""]], [["“He is a millionaire—his income is 500,000 francs.”", "\"그분은 백만장자요. 수입이 50만 프랑이지요.\""]], [["“Then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["said the young man, with anxiety, “I shall be sure to be placed in an agreeable position.”", "젊은이가 조바심 어린 어조로 말했다. \"저는 분명 흡족한 처지에 놓이겠군요.\""]], [["“One of the most agreeable possible, my dear sir; he will allow you an income of 50,000 livres per annum during the whole time of your stay in Paris.”", "\"더없이 흡족한 처지 중 하나일 거요, 친애하는 양반. 그분은 당신이 파리에 머무는 동안 내내 연 5만 리브르의 수입을 허락하실 거요.\""]], [["“Then in that case I shall always choose to remain there.”", "\"그렇다면 그 경우, 저는 언제까지나 이곳에 머무는 쪽을 택하겠습니다.\""]], [["“You cannot control circumstances, my dear sir; ‘man proposes, and God disposes.’”", "\"형편이란 마음대로 되는 게 아니오, 친애하는 양반. '계획은 사람이 하고, 결정은 신이 하는 법'이니까.\""], ["Andrea sighed.", "안드레아가 한숨을 내쉬었다."]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["said he, “so long as I do remain in Paris, and nothing forces me to quit it, do you mean to tell me that I may rely on receiving the sum you just now mentioned to me?”", "그가 말했다. \"제가 파리에 정말 머무는 한, 그리고 그곳을 떠나야 할 일이 아무것도 없는 한, 방금 말씀하신 그 액수를 받으리라 믿어도 된다는 말씀이신지요?\""]], [["“You may.”", "\"믿어도 되오.\""]], [["“Shall I receive it from my father?”", "\"그 돈을 제 아버지에게서 받게 됩니까?\""], ["asked Andrea, with some uneasiness.", "안드레아가 다소 불안한 기색으로 물었다."]], [["“Yes, you will receive it from your father personally, but Lord Wilmore will be the security for the money.", "\"그렇소, 당신 아버지에게서 직접 받게 되오. 하지만 그 돈의 보증은 윌모어 경이 설 거요."], ["He has, at the request of your father, opened an account of 5,000 francs a month at M. Danglars’, which is one of the safest banks in Paris.”", "그분은 당신 아버지의 부탁으로 당글라르 씨네에 매달 5천 프랑짜리 계좌를 텄는데, 거기가 파리에서 가장 안전한 은행 중 하나지요.\""]], [["“And does my father mean to remain long in Paris?”", "\"그런데 제 아버지는 파리에 오래 머무실 생각이신가요?\""], ["asked Andrea.", "안드레아가 물었다."]], [["“Only a few days,”", "\"고작 며칠이오.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo. “His service does not allow him to absent himself more than two or three weeks together.”", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"그분의 직무상 한 번에 이삼 주 넘게 자리를 비울 수 없으니까.\""]], [["“Ah, my dear father!”", "\"아, 사랑하는 제 아버지!\""], ["exclaimed Andrea, evidently charmed with the idea of his speedy departure.", "안드레아가 외쳤다. 아버지가 곧 떠난다는 생각에 분명히 마음이 흡족한 듯했다."]], [["“Therefore,”", "\"그러니,\""], ["said Monte Cristo feigning to mistake his meaning—“therefore I will not, for another instant, retard the pleasure of your meeting.", "몬테크리스토가 그 속내를 짐짓 잘못 알아들은 척하며 말했다. \"그러니 나는 두 분이 만나는 기쁨을 한순간도 더 미루지 않겠소."], ["Are you prepared to embrace your worthy father?”", "그 훌륭한 아버지를 끌어안을 준비가 되셨소?\""]], [["“I hope you do not doubt it.”", "\"의심치 않으시길 바랍니다.\""]], [["30137m\n“Go, then, into the drawing-room, my young friend, where you will find your father awaiting you.”", "\"그렇다면, 젊은 벗이여, 저 응접실로 들어가시오. 거기서 아버지가 당신을 기다리고 계실 거요.\""]], [["Andrea made a low bow to the count, and entered the adjoining room.", "안드레아는 백작에게 깊이 고개를 숙여 절하고 옆방으로 들어갔다."], ["Monte Cristo watched him till he disappeared, and then touched a spring in a panel made to look like a picture, which, in sliding partly from the frame, discovered to view a small opening, so cleverly contrived that it revealed all that was passing in the drawing-room now occupied by Cavalcanti and Andrea.", "몬테크리스토는 그가 사라질 때까지 지켜보다가, 그림처럼 보이도록 만든 벽판의 용수철을 눌렀다. 그러자 벽판이 틀에서 일부 미끄러져 나오면서 작은 구멍이 드러났는데, 어찌나 교묘하게 고안되었던지 이제 카발칸티와 안드레아가 들어가 있는 응접실에서 벌어지는 일이 죄다 보였다."], ["The young man closed the door behind him, and advanced towards the major, who had risen when he heard steps approaching him.", "젊은이는 등 뒤로 문을 닫고, 발소리가 다가오는 것을 듣고 일어선 소령 쪽으로 다가갔다."]], [["“Ah, my dear father!”", "\"아, 사랑하는 아버지!\""], ["said Andrea in a loud voice, in order that the count might hear him in the next room, “is it really you?”", "안드레아가 옆방의 백작이 들을 수 있도록 큰 소리로 말했다. \"정말 아버지이십니까?\""]], [["“How do you do, my dear son?”", "\"잘 있었느냐, 사랑하는 아들아?\""], ["said the major gravely.", "소령이 점잖게 말했다."]], [["“After so many years of painful separation,”", "\"이토록 오랜 세월 가슴 아픈 이별을 겪고 나서,\""], ["said Andrea, in the same tone of voice, and glancing towards the door, “what a happiness it is to meet again!”", "안드레아가 같은 어조로, 문 쪽을 흘끔거리며 말했다. \"다시 만나니 이 얼마나 큰 행복인지요!\""]], [["“Indeed it is, after so long a separation.”", "\"정말 그렇구나, 그토록 오랜 이별 끝이니.\""]], [["“Will you not embrace me, sir?”", "\"저를 안아 주지 않으시겠습니까, 아버지?\""], ["said Andrea. 30139m", "안드레아가 말했다."]], [["“If you wish it, my son,”", "\"네가 원한다면, 아들아.\""], ["said the major; and the two men embraced each other after the fashion of actors on the stage; that is to say, each rested his head on the other’s shoulder.", "소령이 말했다. 그러고서 두 사내는 무대 위 배우들이 하듯 서로를 끌어안았다. 다시 말해, 각자 상대의 어깨에 제 머리를 얹었다."]], [["“Then we are once more reunited?”", "\"그럼 우리는 다시 한번 한자리에 모인 거군요?\""], ["said Andrea.", "안드레아가 말했다."]], [["“Once more,” replied the major.", "\"다시 한번이지.\" 소령이 대답했다."]], [["“Never more to be separated?”", "\"이제 다시는 헤어지지 않는 거고요?\""]], [["“Why, as to that—I think, my dear son, you must be by this time so accustomed to France as to look upon it almost as a second country.”", "\"글쎄다, 그 점에 대해서는, 사랑하는 아들아, 너도 이쯤 되면 프랑스에 워낙 익숙해져서 이곳을 거의 제2의 조국처럼 여기게 됐을 듯한데.\""]], [["“The fact is,”", "\"실은,\""], ["said the young man, “that I should be exceedingly grieved to leave it.”", "젊은이가 말했다. \"이곳을 떠난다면 저는 무척이나 서글플 겁니다.\""]], [["“As for me, you must know I cannot possibly live out of Lucca; therefore I shall return to Italy as soon as I can.”", "\"나로 말하자면, 너도 알다시피 루카를 벗어나서는 도무지 살 수가 없단다. 그러니 나는 되도록 빨리 이탈리아로 돌아갈 작정이야.\""]], [["“But before you leave France, my dear father, I hope you will put me in possession of the documents which will be necessary to prove my descent.”", "\"하지만 프랑스를 떠나시기 전에, 사랑하는 아버지, 제 혈통을 입증해 줄 서류들을 제게 넘겨주시길 바랍니다.\""]], [["“Certainly; I am come expressly on that account; it has cost me much trouble to find you, but I had resolved on giving them into your hands, and if I had to recommence my search, it would occupy all the few remaining years of my life.”", "\"물론이지. 바로 그 일로 일부러 온 것이란다. 너를 찾는 데 어지간히 애를 먹었다만, 그것들을 네 손에 쥐여 주리라 마음먹었지. 다시 처음부터 너를 찾아 나서야 한다 해도, 내게 남은 몇 안 되는 세월을 다 바쳤을 게다.\""]], [["“Where are these papers, then?”", "\"그 서류들은 어디 있습니까?\""]], [["“Here they are.”", "\"여기 있다.\""]], [["Andrea seized the certificate of his father’s marriage and his own baptismal register, and after having opened them with all the eagerness which might be expected under the circumstances, he read them with a facility which proved that he was accustomed to similar documents, and with an expression which plainly denoted an unusual interest in the contents.", "안드레아는 아버지의 혼인 증서와 자신의 세례 등록부를 낚아챘다. 그러고는 그런 상황에서 능히 짐작할 만한 열의로 그것을 펼쳐 든 뒤, 비슷한 서류를 다루는 데 익숙한 사람임을 증명하는 능란함으로, 그리고 그 내용에 남다른 관심을 품었음을 또렷이 드러내는 표정으로 읽어 내려갔다."], ["When he had perused the documents, an indefinable expression of pleasure lighted up his countenance, and looking at the major with a most peculiar smile, he said, in very excellent Tuscan:", "서류를 다 훑고 나자 형언할 수 없는 기쁨의 빛이 그의 얼굴을 환히 밝혔고, 그는 자못 묘한 미소를 띤 채 소령을 바라보며 더없이 훌륭한 토스카나 말로 말했다."]], [["“Then there is no longer any such thing, in Italy as being condemned to the galleys?”", "\"그럼 이제 이탈리아에는 갤리선 노역형이라는 게 더는 없는 모양이군요?\""]], [["The major drew himself up to his full height.", "소령이 몸을 한껏 곧추세웠다."]], [["“Why?—what do you mean by that question?”", "\"왜?, 그 질문은 무슨 뜻이냐?\""]], [["“I mean that if there were, it would be impossible to draw up with impunity two such deeds as these.", "\"제 말은, 만약 그게 있다면 이런 증서 두 장을 무사히 꾸며 낸다는 건 불가능했을 거란 뜻입니다."], ["In France, my dear sir, half such a piece of effrontery as that would cause you to be quickly despatched to Toulon for five years, for change of air.”", "프랑스에서라면, 영감님, 이런 뻔뻔한 짓의 절반만 해도 바람이나 쐬라고 5년 동안 툴롱으로 후딱 보내질 텐데요.\""]], [["“Will you be good enough to explain your meaning?”", "\"무슨 말인지 좀 설명해 주겠나?\""], ["said the major, endeavoring as much as possible to assume an air of the greatest majesty.", "소령이 한껏 위엄을 갖춘 태도를 지으려 애쓰며 말했다."]], [["“My dear M. Cavalcanti,”", "\"친애하는 카발칸티 씨,\""], ["said Andrea, taking the major by the arm in a confidential manner, “how much are you paid for being my father?”", "안드레아가 은밀하게 소령의 팔을 붙잡으며 말했다. \"제 아버지 노릇을 하는 대가로 얼마를 받으셨습니까?\""]], [["The major was about to speak, when Andrea continued, in a low voice:", "소령이 막 입을 열려는데, 안드레아가 낮은 목소리로 말을 이었다."]], [["“Nonsense, I am going to set you an example of confidence, they give me 50,000 francs a year to be your son; consequently, you can understand that it is not at all likely I shall ever deny my parent.”", "\"에이, 그러지 마시고요. 제가 먼저 솔직한 본을 보이지요. 저는 당신 아들 노릇을 하는 대가로 일 년에 5만 프랑을 받습니다. 그러니 제가 부모를 부인할 리는 결코 없으리란 걸 아실 만하지요.\""]], [["The major looked anxiously around him.", "소령이 불안한 듯 주위를 둘러보았다."]], [["“Make yourself easy, we are quite alone,”", "\"마음 놓으세요, 우리 둘뿐입니다.\""], ["said Andrea; “besides, we are conversing in Italian.”", "안드레아가 말했다. \"게다가 우리는 이탈리아 말로 얘기하고 있고요.\""]], [["“Well, then,”", "\"음, 그렇다면,\""], ["replied the major, “they paid me 50,000 francs down.”", "소령이 대답했다. \"나는 일시불로 5만 프랑을 받았네.\""]], [["“Monsieur Cavalcanti,”", "\"카발칸티 씨,\""], ["said Andrea, “do you believe in fairy tales?”", "안드레아가 말했다. \"당신은 동화를 믿으십니까?\""]], [["“I used not to do so, but I really feel now almost obliged to have faith in them.”", "\"예전엔 안 믿었네만, 이젠 정말이지 거의 믿지 않을 수 없게 됐어.\""]], [["“You have, then, been induced to alter your opinion; you have had some proofs of their truth?”", "\"그럼 생각을 바꾸도록 이끌릴 만한 일이 있었던 거군요. 그게 사실이라는 무슨 증거라도 보셨습니까?\""], ["The major drew from his pocket a handful of gold.", "소령이 주머니에서 금화 한 줌을 꺼냈다."]], [["“Most palpable proofs,”", "\"더없이 또렷한 증거지.\""], ["said he, “as you may perceive.”", "그가 말했다. \"보다시피 말이야.\""]], [["“You think, then, that I may rely on the count’s promises?”", "\"그럼 백작의 약속을 믿어도 되겠다고 생각하시는 거지요?\""]], [["“Certainly I do.”", "\"물론이지.\""]], [["“You are sure he will keep his word with me?”", "\"그가 제게 한 말을 꼭 지키리라 확신하시고요?\""]], [["“To the letter, but at the same time, remember, we must continue to play our respective parts.", "\"한 치 어김없이 지킬 거야. 다만 동시에, 잊지 말게나, 우리는 각자 제 배역을 계속 연기해야 해."], ["I, as a tender father——”", "나는 자애로운 아버지로서……\""]], [["“And I as a dutiful son, as they choose that I shall be descended from you.”", "\"그리고 저는 효성스러운 아들로서요, 그들이 제가 당신의 후손이기를 바라니까요.\""]], [["“Whom do you mean by they?”", "\"그들이라니, 누구를 말하는 건가?\""]], [["“_Ma foi_, I can hardly tell, but I was alluding to those who wrote the letter; you received one, did you not?”", "\"글쎄요, 저도 딱히 말하기 어렵습니다만, 그 편지를 쓴 자들을 가리킨 거였습니다. 당신도 한 통 받으셨지요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"받았네.\""]], [["“From whom?”", "\"누구한테서요?\""]], [["“From a certain Abbé Busoni.”", "\"부소니 신부라는 자한테서.\""]], [["“Have you any knowledge of him?”", "\"그자를 좀 아십니까?\""]], [["“No, I have never seen him.”", "\"아니, 한 번도 본 적 없어.\""]], [["“What did he say in the letter?”", "\"편지에 뭐라고 쓰여 있던가요?\""]], [["“You will promise not to betray me?”", "\"나를 발설하지 않겠다고 약속하겠나?\""]], [["“Rest assured of that; you well know that our interests are the same.”", "\"그건 안심하셔도 됩니다. 우리 둘의 이해가 똑같다는 건 잘 아시잖습니까.\""]], [["“Then read for yourself;”", "\"그렇다면 직접 읽어 보게.\""], ["and the major gave a letter into the young man’s hand.", "소령이 젊은이의 손에 편지 한 통을 건넸다."], ["Andrea read in a low voice:", "안드레아가 낮은 목소리로 읽었다."]], [["“‘You are poor; a miserable old age awaits you. Would you like to become rich, or at least independent?", "\"'그대는 가난하고, 비참한 노년이 그대를 기다리고 있소. 부유해지고 싶지 않소, 아니면 적어도 남에게 매이지 않고 살고 싶지 않소?"], ["Set out immediately for Paris, and demand of the Count of Monte Cristo, Avenue des Champs-Élysées, No. 30, the son whom you had by the Marchesa Corsinari, and who was taken from you at five years of age.", "즉시 파리로 떠나, 샹젤리제 거리 30번지의 몬테크리스토 백작에게 가서, 그대가 코르시나리 후작 부인에게서 얻었다가 다섯 살 때 빼앗긴 아들을 내놓으라 요구하시오."], ["This son is named Andrea Cavalcanti.", "그 아들의 이름은 안드레아 카발칸티요."], ["In order that you may not doubt the kind intention of the writer of this letter, you will find enclosed an order for 2,400 francs, payable in Florence, at Signor Gozzi’s; also a letter of introduction to the Count of Monte Cristo, on whom I give you a draft of 48,000 francs.", "이 편지를 쓰는 이의 호의를 의심치 않도록, 피렌체의 고치 씨에게서 지급되는 2,400프랑짜리 어음을 동봉하오. 아울러 몬테크리스토 백작에게 보내는 소개장도 넣었으니, 그 백작 앞으로 4만 8천 프랑짜리 어음을 발행해 두었소."], ["Remember to go to the count on the 26th May at seven o’clock in the evening.", "5월 26일 저녁 일곱 시에 백작을 찾아갈 것을 잊지 마시오."]], [["“(Signed) ‘Abbé Busoni.’”", "(서명) 부소니 신부.'\""]], [["“It is the same.”", "\"똑같군요.\""]], [["“What do you mean?”", "\"무슨 소린가?\""], ["said the major.", "소령이 말했다."]], [["“I was going to say that I received a letter almost to the same effect.”", "\"저도 거의 똑같은 취지의 편지를 받았다는 말입니다.\""]], [["“You?”", "\"자네가?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“From the Abbé Busoni?”", "\"부소니 신부한테서?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"아니요.\""]], [["“From whom, then?”", "\"그럼 누구한테서?\""]], [["“From an Englishman, called Lord Wilmore, who takes the name of Sinbad the Sailor.”", "\"뱃사람 신드바드라는 이름을 쓰는 윌모어 경이라는 영국인한테서요.\""]], [["“And of whom you have no more knowledge than I of the Abbé Busoni?”", "\"내가 부소니 신부를 모르는 것만큼이나 자네도 그자를 모르는 거고?\""]], [["“You are mistaken; there I am ahead of you.”", "\"틀리셨습니다. 그 점에선 제가 영감님보다 한 수 위지요.\""]], [["“You have seen him, then?”", "\"그럼 자네는 그자를 본 적이 있단 말인가?\""]], [["“Yes, once.”", "\"네, 한 번요.\""]], [["“Where?”", "\"어디서?\""]], [["“Ah, that is just what I cannot tell you; if I did, I should make you as wise as myself, which it is not my intention to do.”", "\"아, 그건 제가 말씀드릴 수 없는 부분입니다. 말씀드리면 영감님을 저만큼 똑똑하게 만들어 드리는 셈인데, 저는 그럴 생각이 없거든요.\""]], [["“And what did the letter contain?”", "\"그래, 그 편지에는 뭐라고 쓰여 있던가?\""]], [["“Read it.”", "\"읽어 보세요.\""]], [["“‘You are poor, and your future prospects are dark and gloomy.", "\"'그대는 가난하고, 그대의 앞날은 어둡고 음울하오."], ["Do you wish for a name?", "이름을 얻고 싶소?"], ["should you like to be rich, and your own master?’”", "부유해지고, 제 한 몸의 주인이 되고 싶소?'\""]], [["“_Parbleu!_”", "\"세상에!\""], ["said the young man; “was it possible there could be two answers to such a question?”", "젊은이가 말했다. \"그런 물음에 대답이 둘일 수가 있겠습니까?\""]], [["“‘Take the post-chaise which you will find waiting at the Porte de Gênes, as you enter Nice; pass through Turin, Chambéry, and Pont-de-Beauvoisin.", "\"'니스로 들어서면서 포르트 드 젠 앞에 대기 중인 역마차를 타시오. 토리노, 샹베리, 퐁드보부아쟁을 거치시오."], ["Go to the Count of Monte Cristo, Avenue des Champs-Élysées, on the 26th of May, at seven o’clock in the evening, and demand of him your father.", "5월 26일 저녁 일곱 시에 샹젤리제 거리의 몬테크리스토 백작에게 가서, 그에게 그대의 아버지를 내놓으라 요구하시오."], ["You are the son of the Marchese Cavalcanti and the Marchesa Oliva Corsinari.", "그대는 카발칸티 후작과 올리바 코르시나리 후작 부인의 아들이오."], ["The marquis will give you some papers which will certify this fact, and authorize you to appear under that name in the Parisian world.", "후작이 이 사실을 증명하고 그대가 그 이름으로 파리 사교계에 나설 수 있도록 해 줄 서류 몇 가지를 줄 것이오."], ["As to your rank, an annual income of 50,000 livres will enable you to support it admirably.", "그대의 신분으로 말하자면, 연 5만 리브르의 수입이면 그 신분을 훌륭히 떠받쳐 줄 것이오."], ["I enclose a draft for 5,000 livres, payable on M. Ferrea, banker at Nice, and also a letter of introduction to the Count of Monte Cristo, whom I have directed to supply all your wants.", "니스의 은행가 페레아 씨에게서 지급되는 5천 리브르짜리 어음을 동봉하며, 몬테크리스토 백작에게 보내는 소개장도 넣었소. 그 백작에게 그대의 필요를 모두 채워 주도록 일러 두었소."]], [["“‘Sinbad the Sailor.’”", "뱃사람 신드바드.'\""]], [["“Humph,”", "\"흠,\""], ["said the major; “very good.", "소령이 말했다. \"아주 좋군."], ["You have seen the count, you say?”", "그래, 백작을 만났다고 했지?\""]], [["“I have only just left him.”", "\"방금 그한테서 나오는 길입니다.\""]], [["“And has he conformed to all that the letter specified?”", "\"그래서 편지에 명시된 그대로 다 응해 주던가?\""]], [["“He has.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Do you understand it?”", "\"무슨 영문인지 알겠나?\""]], [["“Not in the least.”", "\"전혀요.\""]], [["“There is a dupe somewhere.”", "\"어딘가에 호구가 하나 있는 거야.\""]], [["“At all events, it is neither you nor I.”", "\"어쨌든 그게 영감님이나 저는 아니지요.\""]], [["“Certainly not.”", "\"물론 아니지.\""]], [["“Well, then——”", "\"그럼 뭐……\""]], [["“Why, it does not much concern us, do you think it does?”", "\"그게 우리하고는 별 상관없는 일 아니냐, 안 그러냐?\""]], [["“No; I agree with you there.", "\"그렇지요. 그 점은 저도 동감입니다."], ["We must play the game to the end, and consent to be blindfolded.”", "우리는 이 판을 끝까지 끌고 가야 하고, 눈가림을 당하는 데 기꺼이 응해야지요.\""]], [["“Ah, you shall see; I promise you I will sustain my part to admiration.”", "\"아, 두고 보세요. 제가 제 배역을 기막히게 해낼 테니 약속드립니다.\""]], [["“I never once doubted your doing so.” Monte Cristo chose this moment for re-entering the drawing-room.", "\"자네가 그러리라는 건 한 번도 의심한 적 없네.\" 몬테크리스토는 바로 이 순간을 골라 응접실로 다시 들어섰다."], ["On hearing the sound of his footsteps, the two men threw themselves in each other’s arms, and while they were in the midst of this embrace, the count entered.", "그의 발소리가 들리자 두 사내는 서로의 품에 와락 안겼고, 그렇게 한창 끌어안고 있는 와중에 백작이 들어섰다."]], [["“Well, marquis,”", "\"자, 후작,\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “you appear to be in no way disappointed in the son whom your good fortune has restored to you.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"행운이 되돌려 준 그 아들에게 조금도 실망하지 않으신 모양이군요.\""]], [["“Ah, your excellency, I am overwhelmed with delight.”", "\"아, 각하, 기쁨에 겨워 어찌할 바를 모르겠습니다.\""]], [["“And what are your feelings?”", "\"그럼 당신의 심정은 어떻소?\""], ["said Monte Cristo, turning to the young man.", "몬테크리스토가 젊은이를 돌아보며 말했다."]], [["“As for me, my heart is overflowing with happiness.”", "\"저로 말하면, 가슴이 행복으로 벅차오릅니다.\""]], [["“Happy father, happy son!”", "\"행복한 아버지, 행복한 아들이로군!\""], ["said the count.", "백작이 말했다."]], [["“There is only one thing which grieves me,”", "\"한 가지만이 저를 슬프게 합니다.\""], ["observed the major, “and that is the necessity for my leaving Paris so soon.”", "소령이 말했다. \"그건 제가 이토록 빨리 파리를 떠나야만 한다는 것이지요.\""]], [["“Ah, my dear M. Cavalcanti, I trust you will not leave before I have had the honor of presenting you to some of my friends.”", "\"아, 친애하는 카발칸티 씨, 부디 내 벗 몇몇에게 당신을 소개하는 영광을 누리기 전에는 떠나지 말아 주시오.\""]], [["“I am at your service, sir,”", "\"분부대로 하지요.\""], ["replied the major.", "소령이 대답했다."]], [["“Now, sir,”", "\"자, 그럼,\""], ["said Monte Cristo, addressing Andrea, “make your confession.”", "몬테크리스토가 안드레아를 향해 말했다. \"고백을 좀 해 보시지요.\""]], [["“To whom?”", "\"누구에게요?\""]], [["“Tell M. Cavalcanti something of the state of your finances.”", "\"당신 형편이 어떤지 카발칸티 씨에게 좀 털어놓으시오.\""]], [["“_Ma foi!_ monsieur, you have touched upon a tender chord.”", "\"이런, 백작님, 아픈 데를 건드리시는군요.\""]], [["“Do you hear what he says, major?”", "\"이 아이가 하는 말 들으셨소, 소령?\""]], [["“Certainly I do.”", "\"물론 들었습니다.\""]], [["“But do you understand?” “I do.”", "\"한데 무슨 뜻인지 알아들으셨소?\""]], [["“Your son says he requires money.”", "\"알아들었습니다.\" \"당신 아들이 돈이 필요하다는군요.\""]], [["“Well, what would you have me do?”", "\"음, 그럼 저더러 어쩌라는 말씀이십니까?\""], ["said the major.", "소령이 말했다."]], [["“You should furnish him with some of course,”", "\"당연히 좀 마련해 주셔야지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다."], ["replied Monte Cristo.", "\"제가요?\""]], [["“I?” “Yes, you,”", "\"그렇소, 당신이요.\""], ["said the count, at the same time advancing towards Andrea, and slipping a packet of bank-notes into the young man’s hand.", "백작이 말하며 동시에 안드레아 쪽으로 다가가, 젊은이의 손에 지폐 한 다발을 슬며시 쥐여 주었다."]], [["“What is this?”", "\"이게 뭡니까?\""]], [["“It is from your father.”", "\"당신 아버지가 주시는 거요.\""]], [["“From my father?”", "\"제 아버지가요?\""]], [["“Yes; did you not tell him just now that you wanted money?", "\"그렇소. 방금 그분께 돈이 필요하다 말하지 않았소?"], ["Well, then, he deputes me to give you this.”", "자, 그래서 그분이 이걸 당신에게 주라고 내게 맡기신 거요.\""]], [["“Am I to consider this as part of my income on account?”", "\"이걸 제 수입의 일부를 미리 받는 걸로 여기면 됩니까?\""]], [["“No, it is for the first expenses of your settling in Paris.”", "\"아니, 이건 당신이 파리에 자리 잡는 데 드는 첫 비용이오.\""]], [["“Ah, how good my dear father is!”", "\"아, 사랑하는 제 아버지는 어쩌면 이리도 좋으신지요!\""]], [["“Silence,”", "\"쉿,\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “he does not wish you to know that it comes from him.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그분은 이게 자신에게서 나온 것임을 당신이 알기를 바라지 않으시오.\""]], [["“I fully appreciate his delicacy,”", "\"그 세심한 마음 씀씀이를 충분히 헤아립니다.\""], ["said Andrea, cramming the notes hastily into his pocket.", "안드레아가 지폐를 황급히 주머니에 쑤셔 넣으며 말했다."]], [["“And now, gentlemen, I wish you good-morning,” said Monte Cristo.", "\"자, 그럼 두 분, 안녕히 가시오.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“And when shall we have the honor of seeing you again, your excellency?”", "\"한데 각하, 언제 다시 뵙는 영광을 누릴 수 있겠습니까?\""], ["asked Cavalcanti.", "카발칸티가 물었다."]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["said Andrea, “when may we hope for that pleasure?”", "안드레아가 말했다. \"언제 그 기쁨을 기대해도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["“On Saturday, if you will—Yes.—Let me see—Saturday—I am to dine at my country house, at Auteuil, on that day, Rue de la Fontaine, No. 28.", "\"좋으시다면 토요일에. 그래요. 가만있자, 토요일이라. 그날 나는 오퇴유에 있는 시골 별장에서 만찬을 들 예정이오."], ["Several persons are invited, and among others, M. Danglars, your banker.", "퐁텐 거리 28번지지요. 여러 사람이 초대받았는데, 그중에 당신의 은행가인 당글라르 씨도 있소."], ["I will introduce you to him, for it will be necessary he should know you, as he is to pay your money.”", "그에게 당신을 소개하리다. 그가 당신 돈을 지급할 사람이니, 당신을 알아둘 필요가 있을 테니까.\""]], [["“Full dress?” said the major, half aloud.", "\"정장을 해야 합니까?\" 소령이 반쯤 소리 내어 물었다."]], [["“Oh, yes, certainly,”", "\"오, 그렇소, 물론이오.\""], ["said the count; “uniform, cross, knee-breeches.”", "백작이 말했다. \"군복에, 훈장에, 반바지까지요.\""]], [["“And how shall I be dressed?”", "\"그럼 저는 어떻게 차려입어야 합니까?\""], ["demanded Andrea. 30145m", "안드레아가 물었다."]], [["“Oh, very simply; black trousers, patent leather boots, white waistcoat, either a black or blue coat, and a long cravat.", "\"오, 아주 간소하게. 검은 바지에, 에나멜 가죽 장화, 흰 조끼, 검정이나 파랑 상의, 그리고 긴 넥타이면 되오."], ["Go to Blin or Véronique for your clothes.", "옷은 블랭이나 베로니크에 가서 맞추시오."], ["Baptistin will tell you where, if you do not know their address.", "주소를 모르거든 바티스탱이 어디인지 일러 줄 거요."], ["The less pretension there is in your attire, the better will be the effect, as you are a rich man.", "차림새에 허세가 적을수록 효과는 더 좋을 거요. 당신은 부자니까 말이오."], ["If you mean to buy any horses, get them of Devedeux, and if you purchase a phaeton, go to Baptiste for it.”", "말을 좀 사려거든 드브되에서 구하고, 사륜마차를 살 거면 바티스트에게 가시오.\""]], [["“At what hour shall we come?”", "\"몇 시에 가면 됩니까?\""], ["asked the young man.", "젊은이가 물었다."]], [["“About half-past six.”", "\"여섯 시 반쯤에.\""]], [["“We will be with you at that time,”", "\"그 시각에 찾아뵙겠습니다.\""], ["said the major.", "소령이 말했다."], ["The two Cavalcanti bowed to the count, and left the house.", "두 카발칸티는 백작에게 절하고 집을 나섰다."], ["Monte Cristo went to the window, and saw them crossing the street, arm in arm.", "몬테크리스토는 창가로 가, 그들이 팔짱을 낀 채 거리를 가로질러 가는 모습을 바라보았다."]], [["“There go two miscreants;”", "\"저기 악당 둘이 가는군.\""], ["said he, “it is a pity they are not really related!”", "그가 말했다. \"정말로 부자지간이 아닌 게 안타깝구나!\""], ["Then, after an instant of gloomy reflection, “Come, I will go to see the Morrels,”", "그러고는 잠시 음울한 상념에 잠겼다가, 입을 열었다. \"자, 모렐 가족을 보러 가야겠다."], ["said he; “I think that disgust is even more sickening than hatred.”", "혐오감이란 증오보다 한층 더 속을 메스껍게 하는 것이니.\""]], [["# Chapter 57. In the Lucern Patch", "# Chapter 57. In the Lucern Patch"]], [["30147m\nOur readers must now allow us to transport them again to the enclosure surrounding M.", "이제 독자들을 다시 빌포르 씨 집을 에워싼 울타리 안으로 데려가야겠다."], ["de Villefort’s house, and, behind the gate, half screened from view by the large chestnut-trees, which on all sides spread their luxuriant branches, we shall find some people of our acquaintance. This time Maximilian was the first to arrive.", "사방으로 무성한 가지를 뻗은 커다란 밤나무들에 절반쯤 가려진 철문 뒤에서, 우리는 낯익은 사람 몇몇을 다시 만나게 될 것이다. 이번에는 막시밀리앙이 가장 먼저 도착해 있었다."], ["He was intently watching for a shadow to appear among the trees, and awaiting with anxiety the sound of a light step on the gravel walk.", "그는 나무들 사이로 그림자 하나가 나타나기를 골똘히 살피며, 자갈길 위로 가벼운 발소리가 들려오기를 애타게 기다리고 있었다."]], [["At length, the long-desired sound was heard, and instead of one figure, as he had expected, he perceived that two were approaching him.", "마침내 그토록 기다리던 소리가 들려왔다. 하지만 그가 예상했던 한 사람이 아니라, 두 사람이 다가오는 것이 보였다."], ["The delay had been occasioned by a visit from Madame Danglars and Eugénie, which had been prolonged beyond the time at which Valentine was expected.", "늦어진 것은 당글라르 부인과 외제니가 찾아왔기 때문이었는데, 그 방문이 발랑틴이 약속한 시간을 넘기도록 길어진 탓이었다."], ["That she might not appear to fail in her promise to Maximilian, she proposed to Mademoiselle Danglars that they should take a walk in the garden, being anxious to show that the delay, which was doubtless a cause of vexation to him, was not occasioned by any neglect on her part.", "막시밀리앙과의 약속을 어긴 것처럼 보이지 않으려고, 발랑틴은 당글라르 양에게 정원을 거닐자고 제안했다. 분명 그를 속상하게 했을 그 지체가 자신의 소홀함 때문이 아니라는 것을 보여 주고 싶은 마음에서였다. 사랑에 빠진 사람 특유의 직감으로, 청년은 그녀가 어쩔 수 없이 처한 상황을 곧바로 알아차렸고 마음이 놓였다."], ["The young man, with the intuitive perception of a lover, quickly understood the circumstances in which she was involuntarily placed, and he was comforted.", "게다가 발랑틴은 말을 건넬 수 있을 만큼 가까이 다가오지는 않으면서도, 막시밀리앙이 자신이 오가는 모습을 볼 수 있게 했다."], ["Besides, although she avoided coming within speaking distance, Valentine arranged so that Maximilian could see her pass and repass, and each time she went by, she managed, unperceived by her companion, to cast an expressive look at the young man, which seemed to say, “Have patience!", "그리고 그 곁을 지날 때마다, 동행이 눈치채지 못하게 청년에게 의미심장한 눈길을 던졌다. 그 눈길은 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"조금만 참아요!"], ["You see it is not my fault.”", "제 잘못이 아니라는 거 아시잖아요.\""]], [["And Maximilian was patient, and employed himself in mentally contrasting the two girls,—one fair, with soft languishing eyes, a figure gracefully bending like a weeping willow; the other a brunette, with a fierce and haughty expression, and as straight as a poplar.", "그래서 막시밀리앙은 참고 기다리며, 마음속으로 두 아가씨를 견주어 보았다. 한 사람은 살결이 희고 부드럽고 그윽한 눈매에, 능수버들처럼 우아하게 휘어진 자태였다. 다른 한 사람은 머리가 검고 사납고 거만한 표정에, 미루나무처럼 꼿꼿했다."], ["It is unnecessary to state that, in the eyes of the young man, Valentine did not suffer by the contrast.", "청년의 눈에 발랑틴이 그 비교에서 조금도 뒤지지 않았다는 것은 새삼 말할 필요도 없다."], ["In about half an hour the girls went away, and Maximilian understood that Mademoiselle Danglars’ visit had at last come to an end.", "반 시간쯤 지나 두 아가씨가 자리를 떴고, 막시밀리앙은 당글라르 양의 방문이 마침내 끝났음을 알아차렸다."], ["In a few minutes Valentine re-entered the garden alone.", "잠시 후 발랑틴이 혼자서 정원으로 다시 들어왔다."], ["For fear that anyone should be observing her return, she walked slowly; and instead of immediately directing her steps towards the gate, she seated herself on a bench, and, carefully casting her eyes around, to convince herself that she was not watched, she presently arose, and proceeded quickly to join Maximilian.", "돌아오는 모습을 누가 볼까 봐 두려워 그녀는 천천히 걸었다. 그러고는 곧장 철문 쪽으로 가지 않고 벤치에 앉아, 자신을 지켜보는 사람이 없는지 확인하려고 조심스럽게 주위를 둘러보았다. 그런 다음에야 일어나 빠른 걸음으로 막시밀리앙에게 다가갔다."]], [["“Good-evening, Valentine,”", "\"안녕하세요, 발랑틴.\""], ["said a well-known voice.", "귀에 익은 목소리가 말했다."]], [["“Good-evening, Maximilian; I know I have kept you waiting, but you saw the cause of my delay.”", "\"안녕하세요, 막시밀리앙. 오래 기다리게 한 거 알아요. 하지만 제가 왜 늦었는지 보셨잖아요.\""]], [["“Yes, I recognized Mademoiselle Danglars.", "\"네, 당글라르 양인 걸 알아봤어요."], ["I was not aware that you were so intimate with her.”", "그렇게 가까운 사이인 줄은 몰랐네요.\""]], [["“Who told you we were intimate, Maximilian?”", "\"우리가 가까운 사이라고 누가 그러던가요, 막시밀리앙?\""]], [["“No one, but you appeared to be so.", "\"아무도요."], ["From the manner in which you walked and talked together, one would have thought you were two school-girls telling your secrets to each other.”", "하지만 그렇게 보이던걸요. 둘이서 걸으며 이야기 나누는 모습이, 마치 비밀을 털어놓는 두 여학생 같았어요.\""]], [["“We were having a confidential conversation,” returned Valentine; “she was owning to me her repugnance to the marriage with M.", "\"우리는 은밀한 이야기를 나누고 있었어요.\" 발랑틴이 대답했다."], ["de Morcerf; and I, on the other hand, was confessing to her how wretched it made me to think of marrying M. d’Épinay.”", "\"그 애는 모르세르 씨와의 결혼이 싫다고 제게 털어놓았고, 저는 저대로 데피네 씨와 결혼한다는 생각만으로도 얼마나 비참한지 고백하던 참이었어요.\""]], [["“Dear Valentine!”", "\"발랑틴!\""]], [["“That will account to you for the unreserved manner which you observed between me and Eugénie, as in speaking of the man whom I could not love, my thoughts involuntarily reverted to him on whom my affections were fixed.”", "\"그러니 외제니와 제가 그렇게 거리낌 없이 이야기 나눈 까닭을 아시겠죠. 사랑할 수 없는 남자 이야기를 하다 보니, 제 마음이 어느새 사랑하는 사람에게로 돌아갔던 거예요.\""]], [["“Ah, how good you are to say so, Valentine!", "\"아, 그렇게 말해 주다니 정말 다정하군요, 발랑틴!"], ["You possess a quality which can never belong to Mademoiselle Danglars.", "당신에게는 당글라르 양은 결코 가질 수 없는 무언가가 있어요."], ["It is that indefinable charm which is to a woman what perfume is to the flower and flavor to the fruit, for the beauty of either is not the only quality we seek.”", "그건 형언할 수 없는 매력이에요. 꽃에게 향기와 같고, 열매에게 풍미와 같은 것이지요. 꽃이든 열매든, 우리가 찾는 게 아름다움 하나만은 아니니까요.\""]], [["“It is your love which makes you look upon everything in that light.”", "\"그건 당신이 사랑에 빠져서 모든 걸 그렇게 보는 거예요.\""]], [["“No, Valentine, I assure you such is not the case.", "\"아니에요, 발랑틴. 정말 그렇지 않아요."], ["I was observing you both when you were walking in the garden, and, on my honor, without at all wishing to depreciate the beauty of Mademoiselle Danglars, I cannot understand how any man can really love her.”", "두 분이 정원을 거닐 때 제가 유심히 보았는데, 맹세코 당글라르 양의 아름다움을 깎아내릴 생각은 조금도 없지만, 어떤 남자가 진심으로 그녀를 사랑할 수 있을지 도무지 모르겠어요.\""]], [["“The fact is, Maximilian, that I was there, and my presence had the effect of rendering you unjust in your comparison.”", "\"실은요, 막시밀리앙, 그 자리에 제가 있었으니, 제가 있어서 당신이 그 비교에서 공정하지 못했던 거예요.\""]], [["“No; but tell me—it is a question of simple curiosity, and which was suggested by certain ideas passing in my mind relative to Mademoiselle Danglars——”", "\"아니에요. 그런데 한 가지 물어봐도 될까요. 그저 호기심에서 묻는 건데, 당글라르 양과 관련해서 제 마음속에 떠오른 어떤 생각 때문에요……\""]], [["“I dare say it is something disparaging which you are going to say. It only proves how little indulgence we may expect from your sex,”", "\"분명 무슨 헐뜯는 말을 하려는 거겠죠. 당신네 남자들에게서 너그러움이라곤 거의 기대할 수 없다는 걸 보여 주는 셈이네요.\""], ["interrupted Valentine.", "발랑틴이 말을 가로막았다."]], [["“You cannot, at least, deny that you are very harsh judges of each other.”", "\"적어도 여자들끼리 서로 아주 가혹하게 평가한다는 건 부인 못 하시겠죠.\""]], [["“If we are so, it is because we generally judge under the influence of excitement.", "\"우리가 그런다면, 대개 흥분한 상태에서 판단하기 때문이에요."], ["But return to your question.” 30151m", "하지만 질문으로 돌아가세요.\""]], [["“Does Mademoiselle Danglars object to this marriage with M. de Morcerf on account of loving another?”", "\"당글라르 양이 모르세르 씨와의 결혼을 마다하는 게, 다른 사람을 사랑하기 때문인가요?\""]], [["“I told you I was not on terms of strict intimacy with Eugénie.”", "\"외제니와 그렇게 막역한 사이는 아니라고 했잖아요.\""]], [["“Yes, but girls tell each other secrets without being particularly intimate; own, now, that you did question her on the subject.", "\"네, 하지만 여자들은 특별히 친하지 않아도 비밀을 주고받잖아요. 자, 솔직히 말해 봐요. 그 일을 캐물었죠?"], ["Ah, I see you are smiling.”", "아, 웃는 걸 보니 알겠네요.\""]], [["“If you are already aware of the conversation that passed, the wooden partition which interposed between us and you has proved but a slight security.”", "\"우리가 나눈 이야기를 벌써 다 알고 있다니, 우리와 당신 사이를 가로막은 나무 칸막이가 그리 단단한 차단막은 아니었나 봐요.\""]], [["“Come, what did she say?”", "\"자, 그 애가 뭐라던가요?\""]], [["“She told me that she loved no one,”", "\"누구도 사랑하지 않는다고 했어요.\""], ["said Valentine; “that she disliked the idea of being married; that she would infinitely prefer leading an independent and unfettered life; and that she almost wished her father might lose his fortune, that she might become an artist, like her friend, Mademoiselle Louise d’Armilly.”", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"결혼한다는 생각 자체가 싫고, 자유롭고 얽매이지 않는 삶이 훨씬 좋다고요. 그리고 차라리 아버지가 재산을 다 잃었으면 좋겠다고, 그러면 친구인 루이즈 다르밀리 양처럼 예술가가 될 수 있을 거라고 했어요.\""]], [["“Ah, you see——”", "\"아, 그것 봐요……\""]], [["“Well, what does that prove?”", "\"그래서, 그게 뭘 증명하나요?\""], ["asked Valentine.", "발랑틴이 물었다."]], [["“Nothing,”", "\"아무것도요.\""], ["replied Maximilian.", "막시밀리앙이 대답했다."]], [["“Then why did you smile?”", "\"그럼 왜 웃었어요?\""]], [["“Why, you know very well that you are reflecting on yourself, Valentine.”", "\"아니, 발랑틴, 당신도 잘 알잖아요. 지금 당신 자신을 두고 하는 말이라는 걸요.\""]], [["“Do you want me to go away?”", "\"제가 가 버리길 바라나요?\""]], [["“Ah, no, no.", "\"아, 아니에요, 아니에요."], ["But do not let us lose time; you are the subject on which I wish to speak.”", "하지만 시간을 허비하지 맙시다. 제가 이야기하고 싶은 건 바로 당신이니까요.\""]], [["“True, we must be quick, for we have scarcely ten minutes more to pass together.”", "\"맞아요, 서둘러야 해요. 함께 있을 시간이 채 십 분도 남지 않았어요.\""]], [["“_Ma foi!_”", "\"이런!\""], ["said Maximilian, in consternation.", "막시밀리앙이 낙담하여 말했다."]], [["“Yes, you are right; I am but a poor friend to you.", "\"네, 당신 말이 맞아요. 저는 당신에게 부족한 사람이에요."], ["What a life I cause you to lead, poor Maximilian, you who are formed for happiness!", "제가 당신을 어떤 삶으로 끌어들이고 있는지, 가엾은 막시밀리앙, 행복하게 살아야 할 당신을요!"], ["I bitterly reproach myself, I assure you.”", "정말이지 저 자신을 모질게 책망하고 있어요.\""]], [["“Well, what does it signify, Valentine, so long as I am satisfied, and feel that even this long and painful suspense is amply repaid by five minutes of your society, or two words from your lips?", "\"그게 무슨 상관이에요, 발랑틴, 제가 만족하는데요. 이 길고 괴로운 기다림도 당신과 함께하는 오 분, 당신 입에서 나오는 두 마디 말이면 충분히 보상받는걸요."], ["And I have also a deep conviction that heaven would not have created two hearts, harmonizing as ours do, and almost miraculously brought us together, to separate us at last.”", "게다가 저는 굳게 믿어요. 하늘이 우리처럼 이토록 어우러지는 두 마음을 만들어 거의 기적처럼 맺어 주고서, 끝내 갈라놓지는 않으리라고요.\""]], [["“Those are kind and cheering words.", "\"다정하고 힘이 나는 말이네요."], ["You must hope for us both, Maximilian; that will make me at least partly happy.”", "우리 둘 몫의 희망을 당신이 품어 주세요, 막시밀리앙. 그러면 저도 적어도 절반쯤은 행복할 거예요.\""]], [["“But why must you leave me so soon?”", "\"그런데 왜 이렇게 빨리 가 봐야 하나요?\""]], [["“I do not know particulars.", "\"자세한 건 몰라요."], ["I can only tell you that Madame de Villefort sent to request my presence, as she had a communication to make on which a part of my fortune depended.", "빌포르 부인이 저를 부르러 사람을 보냈다는 것밖에는요. 제 재산의 일부가 걸린 일로 할 말이 있다고요."], ["Let them take my fortune, I am already too rich; and, perhaps, when they have taken it, they will leave me in peace and quietness.", "재산이야 가져가라죠, 저는 이미 너무 부유하니까요. 어쩌면 다 가져가고 나면 저를 평화롭고 조용하게 내버려 둘지도 모르죠."], ["You would love me as much if I were poor, would you not, Maximilian?”", "제가 가난해도 지금처럼 사랑해 주시겠죠, 막시밀리앙?\""]], [["“Oh, I shall always love you. What should I care for either riches or poverty, if my Valentine was near me, and I felt certain that no one could deprive me of her?", "\"아, 저는 언제까지나 당신을 사랑할 거예요. 제 발랑틴이 곁에 있고, 누구도 당신을 제게서 앗아 갈 수 없다는 확신만 있다면, 부유함이든 가난함이든 제게 무슨 상관이겠어요?"], ["But do you not fear that this communication may relate to your marriage?”", "그런데 혹시 그 할 말이라는 게 당신 결혼에 관한 건 아닐까 걱정되지 않나요?\""]], [["“I do not think that is the case.”", "\"그건 아닐 거예요.\""]], [["“However it may be, Valentine, you must not be alarmed. I assure you that, as long as I live, I shall never love anyone else!”", "\"어찌 됐든, 발랑틴, 놀라지 말아요. 맹세컨대, 제가 살아 있는 한 결코 다른 사람을 사랑하지 않을 테니까요!\""]], [["“Do you think to reassure me when you say that, Maximilian?”", "\"그렇게 말하면 제가 안심할 거라고 생각하세요, 막시밀리앙?\""]], [["“Pardon me, you are right.", "\"미안해요, 당신 말이 맞아요."], ["I am a brute.", "제가 짐승 같았네요."], ["But I was going to tell you that I met M. de Morcerf the other day.” “Well?”", "하지만 사실은 며칠 전에 모르세르 씨를 만났다는 얘기를 하려던 거였어요.\""]], [["“Monsieur Franz is his friend, you know.”", "\"그래서요?\" \"프란츠 씨가 그의 친구잖아요.\""]], [["“What then?”", "\"그런데요?\""]], [["“Monsieur de Morcerf has received a letter from Franz, announcing his immediate return.”", "\"모르세르 씨가 프란츠한테서 편지를 받았는데, 곧 돌아오겠다는 거였어요.\""], ["Valentine turned pale, and leaned her hand against the gate.", "발랑틴의 얼굴이 창백해지더니, 그녀는 철문에 손을 기댔다."]], [["“Ah heavens, if it were that!", "\"아, 맙소사, 만약 그 일이라면!"], ["But no, the communication would not come through Madame de Villefort.”", "하지만 아니에요, 그런 거라면 빌포르 부인을 통해 전해질 리가 없어요.\""]], [["“Why not?”", "\"왜 아니죠?\""]], [["“Because—I scarcely know why—but it has appeared as if Madame de Villefort secretly objected to the marriage, although she did not choose openly to oppose it.”", "\"왜냐하면…… 저도 잘은 모르지만…… 빌포르 부인이 드러내 놓고 반대하지는 않으면서도, 속으로는 그 결혼을 마뜩잖아하는 것 같았거든요.\""]], [["“Is it so?", "\"그래요?"], ["Then I feel as if I could adore Madame de Villefort.”", "그렇다면 빌포르 부인을 흠모할 수도 있을 것 같은데요.\""]], [["“Do not be in such a hurry to do that,”", "\"그렇게 서둘러 흠모하지는 말아요.\""], ["said Valentine, with a sad smile.", "발랑틴이 서글픈 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["“If she objects to your marrying M. d’Épinay, she would be all the more likely to listen to any other proposition.”", "\"당신이 데피네 씨와 결혼하는 걸 반대한다면, 다른 청혼이라면 더 귀 기울여 들어 줄 가능성이 높잖아요.\""]], [["“No, Maximilian, it is not suitors to which Madame de Villefort objects, it is marriage itself.”", "\"아니에요, 막시밀리앙. 빌포르 부인이 반대하는 건 구혼자가 아니라 결혼 그 자체예요.\""]], [["“Marriage?", "\"결혼이요?"], ["If she dislikes that so much, why did she ever marry herself?”", "결혼을 그토록 싫어한다면, 부인은 왜 결혼했답니까?\""]], [["“You do not understand me, Maximilian.", "\"제 말을 못 알아들으시네요, 막시밀리앙."], ["About a year ago, I talked of retiring to a convent.", "일 년쯤 전에 제가 수녀원에 들어가겠다고 했어요."], ["Madame de Villefort, in spite of all the remarks which she considered it her duty to make, secretly approved of the proposition, my father consented to it at her instigation, and it was only on account of my poor grandfather that I finally abandoned the project.", "빌포르 부인은 의무라고 여겨 이런저런 말을 늘어놓기는 했지만, 속으로는 그 제안을 반겼지요. 부인이 부추기는 바람에 아버지도 동의했고요. 결국 제가 그 계획을 접은 건 오로지 가엾은 할아버지 때문이었어요."], ["You can form no idea of the expression of that old man’s eye when he looks at me, the only person in the world whom he loves, and, I had almost said, by whom he is beloved in return.", "그분이 저를 바라보실 때의 그 눈빛을, 당신은 상상도 못 할 거예요. 세상에서 그분이 사랑하는 단 한 사람이 저거든요. 그리고, 거의 단언컨대, 그분도 저에게 사랑받는 단 한 사람이고요."], ["When he learned my resolution, I shall never forget the reproachful look which he cast on me, and the tears of utter despair which chased each other down his lifeless cheeks.", "제 결심을 아셨을 때, 그분이 제게 던진 그 책망 어린 눈빛과, 생기 없는 두 뺨을 줄줄이 타고 흐르던 절망의 눈물을 저는 결코 잊지 못할 거예요. 아, 막시밀리앙, 그 순간 저는 제 결심에 어찌나 후회가 밀려오던지, 그분 발치에 몸을 던지며 외쳤어요."], ["Ah, Maximilian, I experienced, at that moment, such remorse for my intention, that, throwing myself at his feet, I exclaimed,—‘Forgive me, pray forgive me, my dear grandfather; they may do what they will with me, I will never leave you.’ When I had ceased speaking, he thankfully raised his eyes to heaven, but without uttering a word.", "'용서하세요, 부디 용서해 주세요, 할아버지. 저들이 저를 어떻게 하든, 저는 절대 할아버지를 떠나지 않을게요.' 제 말이 끝나자 그분은 고마워하며 하늘로 눈을 들어 올리셨지만, 한마디도 하지 않으셨어요."], ["Ah, Maximilian, I may have much to suffer, but I feel as if my grandfather’s look at that moment would more than compensate for all.”", "아, 막시밀리앙, 제가 앞으로 많은 고통을 겪을지도 몰라요. 하지만 그 순간 할아버지의 그 눈빛이 그 모든 것을 보상하고도 남으리라는 느낌이 들어요.\""]], [["“Dear Valentine, you are a perfect angel, and I am sure I do not know what I—sabring right and left among the Bedouins—can have done to merit your being revealed to me, unless, indeed, Heaven took into consideration the fact that the victims of my sword were infidels.", "\"발랑틴, 당신은 완벽한 천사예요. 그런데 제가, 베두인족 사이를 누비며 좌우로 칼을 휘둘러 온 제가, 당신 같은 사람을 만날 자격이 있다고 한 일이 뭐가 있는지 정말 모르겠어요. 굳이 따지자면, 하늘이 제 칼에 쓰러진 자들이 이교도였다는 사실을 참작해 주신 거겠지요."], ["But tell me what interest Madame de Villefort can have in your remaining unmarried?”", "그런데 빌포르 부인이 당신이 결혼하지 않는 데서 무슨 이득을 본다는 거예요?\""]], [["“Did I not tell you just now that I was rich, Maximilian—too rich?", "\"제가 방금 부유하다고, 너무 부유하다고 하지 않았나요, 막시밀리앙?"], ["I possess nearly 50,000 livres in right of my mother; my grandfather and my grandmother, the Marquis and Marquise de Saint-Méran, will leave me as much, and M.", "어머니 쪽으로 5만 리브르 가까이 되는 재산이 제게 있어요. 외할아버지와 외할머니, 생메랑 후작 부부도 그만큼을 제게 남겨 주실 거고요."], ["Noirtier evidently intends making me his heir. My brother Edward, who inherits nothing from his mother, will, therefore, be poor in comparison with me.", "누아르티에 할아버지도 분명 저를 상속인으로 삼으실 작정이세요. 어머니에게서 아무것도 물려받지 못하는 남동생 에두아르는 그러니 저에 비하면 가난할 거예요."], ["Now, if I had taken the veil, all this fortune would have descended to my father, and, in reversion, to his son.”", "그런데 제가 수녀가 되었더라면, 이 모든 재산이 아버지에게로, 그리고 다시 그 아들에게로 넘어갔겠지요.\""]], [["“Ah, how strange it seems that such a young and beautiful woman should be so avaricious.”", "\"아, 저토록 젊고 아름다운 여인이 그렇게 탐욕스러울 수 있다니 참 이상하네요.\""]], [["“It is not for herself that she is so, but for her son, and what you regard as a vice becomes almost a virtue when looked at in the light of maternal love.”", "\"자기 자신을 위한 게 아니라 아들을 위한 거예요. 당신이 악덕이라 여기는 것도 모성애의 빛으로 비추어 보면 거의 미덕에 가까워지지요.\""]], [["“But could you not compromise matters, and give up a portion of your fortune to her son?”", "\"하지만 절충할 수는 없나요? 당신 재산의 일부를 그 아들에게 떼어 주는 거예요.\""]], [["“How could I make such a proposition, especially to a woman who always professes to be so entirely disinterested?”", "\"제가 어떻게 그런 제안을 하겠어요? 더구나 늘 자기는 사심이 전혀 없다고 공언하는 여자한테요.\""]], [["“Valentine, I have always regarded our love in the light of something sacred; consequently, I have covered it with the veil of respect, and hid it in the innermost recesses of my soul.", "\"발랑틴, 저는 늘 우리의 사랑을 신성한 무언가로 여겨 왔어요. 그래서 그 사랑을 존중의 베일로 감싸, 제 영혼 가장 깊은 곳에 감추어 두었지요."], ["No human being, not even my sister, is aware of its existence.", "어떤 사람도, 제 누이조차도 그 존재를 몰라요."], ["Valentine, will you permit me to make a confidant of a friend and reveal to him the love I bear you?”", "발랑틴, 친구 한 사람을 믿고, 그에게 당신을 향한 제 사랑을 털어놓아도 될까요?\""]], [["Valentine started.", "발랑틴이 흠칫했다."], ["“A friend, Maximilian; and who is this friend?", "\"친구라니요, 막시밀리앙. 그 친구가 누구죠?"], ["I tremble to give my permission.”", "허락하기가 떨려요.\""]], [["“Listen, Valentine.", "\"들어 봐요, 발랑틴."], ["Have you never experienced for anyone that sudden and irresistible sympathy which made you feel as if the object of it had been your old and familiar friend, though, in reality, it was the first time you had ever met?", "누군가에게 갑작스럽고 거부할 수 없는 호감을 느낀 적 없나요? 실은 처음 만난 사람인데도, 마치 오래전부터 알고 지낸 친한 벗처럼 느껴지는 그런 감정 말이에요."], ["Nay, further, have you never endeavored to recall the time, place, and circumstances of your former intercourse, and failing in this attempt, have almost believed that your spirits must have held converse with each other in some state of being anterior to the present, and that you are only now occupied in a reminiscence of the past?”", "아니, 한 걸음 더 나아가, 그 사람과 예전에 어울렸던 때며 장소며 상황을 떠올려 보려 애쓰다가, 끝내 떠올리지 못하고는, 지금에 앞선 어떤 세상에서 우리 영혼들이 서로 이야기를 나누었던 게 틀림없으며, 지금은 그저 그 옛 기억을 더듬고 있을 뿐이라고 믿게 된 적은요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Well, that is precisely the feeling which I experienced when I first saw that extraordinary man.”", "\"바로 그게 제가 그 비범한 사람을 처음 보았을 때 느낀 감정이에요.\""]], [["“Extraordinary, did you say?”", "\"비범하다고요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“You have known him for some time, then?”", "\"그럼 안 지 꽤 됐나요?\""]], [["“Scarcely longer than eight or ten days.”", "\"기껏해야 여드레나 열흘쯤 됐어요.\""]], [["“And do you call a man your friend whom you have only known for eight or ten days?", "\"안 지 여드레나 열흘밖에 안 된 사람을 친구라고 부르는 거예요?"], ["Ah, Maximilian, I had hoped you set a higher value on the title of friend.”", "아, 막시밀리앙, 저는 당신이 친구라는 이름에 더 높은 값을 매기는 줄 알았어요.\""]], [["“Your logic is most powerful, Valentine, but say what you will, I can never renounce the sentiment which has instinctively taken possession of my mind.", "\"당신 논리가 더없이 강력하군요, 발랑틴. 하지만 뭐라 하셔도, 본능적으로 제 마음을 사로잡은 이 감정을 저는 결코 버릴 수 없어요."], ["I feel as if it were ordained that this man should be associated with all the good which the future may have in store for me, and sometimes it really seems as if his eye was able to see what was to come, and his hand endowed with the power of directing events according to his own will.”", "마치 미래가 제게 마련해 둔 온갖 좋은 일이 이 사람과 이어지도록 정해져 있는 것만 같아요. 그리고 가끔은 정말이지, 그의 눈이 다가올 일을 꿰뚫어 보고, 그의 손이 사건을 제 뜻대로 좌우하는 힘을 지닌 듯해요.\""]], [["“He must be a prophet, then,”", "\"그럼 예언자인가 보네요.\""], ["said Valentine, smiling.", "발랑틴이 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Indeed,”", "\"정말이지,\""], ["said Maximilian, “I have often been almost tempted to attribute to him the gift of prophecy; at all events, he has a wonderful power of foretelling any future good.”", "막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"저는 종종 그에게 예언의 능력이 있다고 여기고 싶은 충동마저 느꼈어요. 어쨌든 그는 앞으로 닥칠 좋은 일을 내다보는 놀라운 능력을 지녔어요.\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["said Valentine in a mournful tone, “do let me see this man, Maximilian; he may tell me whether I shall ever be loved sufficiently to make amends for all I have suffered.”", "발랑틴이 구슬픈 목소리로 말했다. \"그 사람을 한번 만나게 해 주세요, 막시밀리앙. 제가 그동안 겪은 모든 고통을 보상받을 만큼 충분히 사랑받게 될지, 그가 말해 줄지도 모르잖아요.\""]], [["“My poor girl, you know him already.”", "\"가엾은 발랑틴, 당신은 이미 그를 알고 있어요.\""]], [["“I know him?”", "\"제가 그를 안다고요?\""]], [["“Yes; it was he who saved the life of your step-mother and her son.”", "\"네. 당신 계모와 그 아들의 목숨을 구해 준 사람이 바로 그 사람이에요.\""]], [["“The Count of Monte Cristo?”", "\"몬테크리스토 백작이요?\""]], [["“The same.”", "\"바로 그 사람이에요.\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["cried Valentine, “he is too much the friend of Madame de Villefort ever to be mine.”", "발랑틴이 외쳤다. \"그 사람은 빌포르 부인과 너무 가까운 친구라, 제 친구는 결코 될 수 없어요.\""]], [["“The friend of Madame de Villefort!", "\"빌포르 부인의 친구라니!"], ["It cannot be; surely, Valentine, you are mistaken?”", "그럴 리가요. 발랑틴, 분명 잘못 아신 거예요.\""]], [["“No, indeed, I am not; for I assure you, his power over our household is almost unlimited.", "\"아니에요, 정말 그렇지 않아요. 맹세코, 우리 집안에서 그 사람의 영향력은 거의 무한해요."], ["Courted by my step-mother, who regards him as the epitome of human wisdom; admired by my father, who says he has never before heard such sublime ideas so eloquently expressed; idolized by Edward, who, notwithstanding his fear of the count’s large black eyes, runs to meet him the moment he arrives, and opens his hand, in which he is sure to find some delightful present,—M.", "그를 인간 지혜의 정수라 여기며 떠받드는 계모, 그렇게 숭고한 생각을 그토록 유려하게 풀어내는 걸 처음 들어 본다고 감탄하는 아버지, 백작의 커다란 검은 눈을 무서워하면서도 그가 도착하는 순간 달려가 손을 펴면 어김없이 근사한 선물이 쥐어지기에 그를 우상처럼 떠받드는 에두아르까지."], ["de Monte Cristo appears to exert a mysterious and almost uncontrollable influence over all the members of our family.”", "몬테크리스토 백작은 우리 집안 식구 모두에게 알 수 없는, 거의 거역할 수 없는 영향력을 행사하는 것 같아요.\""]], [["“If such be the case, my dear Valentine, you must yourself have felt, or at all events will soon feel, the effects of his presence.", "\"그렇다면, 발랑틴, 당신 자신도 이미 그 존재의 영향을 느꼈거나, 아니면 머지않아 느끼게 될 거예요."], ["He meets Albert de Morcerf in Italy—it is to rescue him from the hands of the banditti; he introduces himself to Madame Danglars—it is that he may give her a royal present; your step-mother and her son pass before his door—it is that his Nubian may save them from destruction.", "그가 이탈리아에서 알베르 드 모르세르를 만난 건 산적들 손아귀에서 그를 구해 내기 위해서였고, 당글라르 부인에게 자기를 소개한 건 그녀에게 왕에게나 어울릴 선물을 주기 위해서였어요. 당신 계모와 그 아들이 그의 집 앞을 지난 것도, 그의 누비아인이 그들을 죽음에서 구하게 하기 위해서였지요."], ["This man evidently possesses the power of influencing events, both as regards men and things. I never saw more simple tastes united to greater magnificence.", "이 사람은 분명 사람이든 사물이든, 사건에 영향을 미치는 힘을 지녔어요. 그토록 소박한 취향과 그토록 대단한 호화로움이 한데 어우러진 사람을 저는 본 적이 없어요."], ["His smile is so sweet when he addresses me, that I forget it ever can be bitter to others.", "그가 제게 말을 건넬 때면 그 미소가 어찌나 다정한지, 그 미소가 다른 이들에게는 쓰디쓸 수도 있다는 걸 잊어버릴 정도예요."], ["Ah, Valentine, tell me, if he ever looked on you with one of those sweet smiles?", "아, 발랑틴, 말해 봐요. 그가 그 다정한 미소 중 하나로 당신을 바라본 적이 있나요?"], ["if so, depend on it, you will be happy.”", "그렇다면 분명, 당신은 행복해질 거예요.\""]], [["“Me?”", "\"저요?\""], ["said the young girl, “he never even glances at me; on the contrary, if I accidentally cross his path, he appears rather to avoid me.", "아가씨가 말했다. \"그는 저를 거들떠보지도 않아요. 오히려 제가 어쩌다 그의 앞을 지나가면, 차라리 저를 피하는 것 같던걸요."], ["Ah, he is not generous, neither does he possess that supernatural penetration which you attribute to him, for if he did, he would have perceived that I was unhappy; and if he had been generous, seeing me sad and solitary, he would have used his influence to my advantage, and since, as you say, he resembles the sun, he would have warmed my heart with one of his life-giving rays.", "아, 그는 너그럽지도 않고, 당신이 말하는 그 초자연적 통찰력을 지니지도 않았어요. 그게 있었다면 제가 불행하다는 걸 알아챘을 테니까요. 또 너그러웠다면, 슬프고 외로운 저를 보고 자기 영향력을 제게 유리하게 써 주었겠지요."], ["You say he loves you, Maximilian; how do you know that he does?", "당신 말마따나 그가 태양을 닮았다면, 그 생명을 주는 빛줄기 하나로 제 마음을 데워 주었을 테고요."], ["All would pay deference to an officer like you, with a fierce moustache and a long sabre, but they think they may crush a poor weeping girl with impunity.”", "막시밀리앙, 그가 당신을 사랑한다고 하셨죠? 그걸 어떻게 알아요? 다들 당신처럼 사나운 콧수염에 긴 군도를 찬 장교에게는 깍듯이 대하지만, 가엾게 흐느끼는 아가씨 하나쯤은 마음 놓고 짓밟아도 된다고 여기는 법이에요.\""]], [["“Ah, Valentine, I assure you you are mistaken.”", "\"아, 발랑틴, 정말 잘못 아신 거예요.\""]], [["“If it were otherwise—if he treated me diplomatically—that is to say, like a man who wishes, by some means or other, to obtain a footing in the house, so that he may ultimately gain the power of dictating to its occupants—he would, if it had been but once, have honored me with the smile which you extol so loudly; but no, he saw that I was unhappy, he understood that I could be of no use to him, and therefore paid no attention to me whatever.", "\"만약 그렇지 않다면, 다시 말해 그가 저를 노련하게 대했다면, 그러니까 어떻게든 이 집안에 발판을 마련해 끝내 식구들을 마음대로 좌지우지할 힘을 얻으려는 사람처럼 굴었다면, 단 한 번이라도 당신이 그토록 칭송하는 그 미소로 저를 대접했을 거예요. 하지만 아니에요, 그는 제가 불행하다는 걸 보았고, 제가 자기에게 아무 쓸모도 없다는 걸 알아챈 거예요. 그래서 저에게는 도무지 관심을 두지 않은 거고요."], ["Who knows but that, in order to please Madame de Villefort and my father, he may not persecute me by every means in his power?", "누가 알겠어요, 빌포르 부인과 아버지의 비위를 맞추려고, 온갖 수단을 동원해 저를 괴롭힐지도요?"], ["It is not just that he should despise me so, without any reason.", "아무 이유도 없이 저를 그토록 업신여기는 건 옳지 않아요."], ["Ah, forgive me,”", "아, 미안해요.\""], ["said Valentine, perceiving the effect which her words were producing on Maximilian: “I have done wrong, for I have given utterance to thoughts concerning that man which I did not even know existed in my heart.", "자기 말이 막시밀리앙에게 미치는 영향을 알아채고 발랑틴이 말했다. \"제가 잘못했어요. 제 마음속에 있는 줄도 몰랐던, 그 사람에 대한 생각을 내뱉었으니까요."], ["I do not deny the influence of which you speak, or that I have not myself experienced it, but with me it has been productive of evil rather than good.”", "당신이 말하는 그 영향력을 부인하는 것도, 저 자신이 그걸 느껴 보지 못했다는 것도 아니에요. 다만 저에게는 그게 좋은 쪽보다 나쁜 쪽으로 작용했어요.\""]], [["“Well, Valentine,”", "\"좋아요, 발랑틴.\""], ["said Morrel with a sigh, “we will not discuss the matter further.", "모렐이 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"이 문제는 더 이상 따지지 맙시다."], ["I will not make a confidant of him.”", "그를 믿고 털어놓지 않을게요.\""]], [["“Alas!”", "\"아아!\""], ["said Valentine, “I see that I have given you pain.", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"제가 당신을 아프게 한 모양이네요."], ["I can only say how sincerely I ask pardon for having grieved you.", "당신을 슬프게 한 것을, 얼마나 진심으로 용서를 구하는지 말씀드릴 수밖에 없네요."], ["But, indeed, I am not prejudiced beyond the power of conviction.", "하지만 정말, 저는 설득당하지 못할 만큼 고집스럽지는 않아요."], ["Tell me what this Count of Monte Cristo has done for you.”", "그 몬테크리스토 백작이 당신에게 무엇을 해 주었는지 말해 봐요.\""]], [["30157m\n“I own that your question embarrasses me, Valentine, for I cannot say that the count has rendered me any ostensible service.", "\"솔직히 그 질문에는 곤란하군요, 발랑틴. 백작이 제게 눈에 보이는 도움을 주었다고는 말할 수 없으니까요."], ["Still, as I have already told you, I have an instinctive affection for him, the source of which I cannot explain to you.", "그래도 앞서 말했듯이, 저는 그에게 본능적인 애정을 느껴요. 그 근원이 무엇인지는 당신에게 설명할 수 없지만요."], ["Has the sun done anything for me?", "태양이 제게 무얼 해 주었나요? 아니죠."], ["No; he warms me with his rays, and it is by his light that I see you—nothing more.", "그 빛줄기로 저를 데워 주고, 그 빛 덕분에 제가 당신을 보지요. 그뿐이에요."], ["Has such and such a perfume done anything for me?", "어떤 향기가 제게 무얼 해 주었나요? 아니죠."], ["No; its odor charms one of my senses—that is all I can say when I am asked why I praise it.", "그 냄새가 제 감각 하나를 매혹할 뿐, 왜 그걸 좋아하느냐고 물으면 그렇게밖에 대답할 수 없어요."], ["My friendship for him is as strange and unaccountable as his for me.", "그를 향한 제 우정은, 저를 향한 그의 우정만큼이나 기이하고 설명할 길이 없어요."], ["A secret voice seems to whisper to me that there must be something more than chance in this unexpected reciprocity of friendship.", "어떤 은밀한 목소리가 제게 속삭이는 것 같아요. 이 뜻밖의 서로 통하는 우정에는 우연 이상의 무언가가 있을 거라고요."], ["In his most simple actions, as well as in his most secret thoughts, I find a relation to my own.", "그의 더없이 소박한 행동에서도, 더없이 은밀한 생각에서도, 저는 제 자신과의 어떤 연관을 발견해요."], ["You will perhaps smile at me when I tell you that, ever since I have known this man, I have involuntarily entertained the idea that all the good fortune which has befallen me originated from him.", "이런 말을 하면 비웃으실지도 모르지만, 이 사람을 알게 된 뒤로 저는 제게 닥친 온갖 행운이 그에게서 비롯되었다는 생각을 저도 모르게 품게 됐어요."], ["However, I have managed to live thirty years without this protection, you will say; but I will endeavor a little to illustrate my meaning.", "하기야 이 보호 없이도 삼십 년을 살아오지 않았느냐고 하시겠죠. 하지만 제 말이 무슨 뜻인지 조금 풀어 볼게요."], ["He invited me to dine with him on Saturday, which was a very natural thing for him to do. Well, what have I learned since?", "그가 토요일에 함께 저녁을 들자고 저를 초대했는데, 그건 그로서는 아주 자연스러운 일이었어요. 그런데 그 뒤에 제가 뭘 알게 됐는지 아세요?"], ["That your mother and M. de Villefort are both coming to this dinner.", "당신 어머니와 빌포르 씨도 그 저녁 식사에 온다는 거예요."], ["I shall meet them there, and who knows what future advantages may result from the interview?", "저는 거기서 그분들을 만날 테고, 그 만남에서 앞으로 어떤 이득이 생길지 누가 알겠어요?"], ["This may appear to you to be no unusual combination of circumstances; nevertheless, I perceive some hidden plot in the arrangement—something, in fact, more than is apparent on a casual view of the subject.", "당신에게는 별스러울 것 없는 상황의 우연쯤으로 보일지 몰라요. 그래도 저는 이 자리에 어떤 숨은 계략이, 사실 무심코 보아서는 드러나지 않는 무언가가 있다고 느껴요."], ["I believe that this singular man, who appears to fathom the motives of everyone, has purposely arranged for me to meet M.", "누구의 속내든 꿰뚫어 보는 듯한 이 기이한 사람이, 제가 빌포르 부부를 만나도록 일부러 자리를 마련했다고 저는 믿어요."], ["and Madame de Villefort, and sometimes, I confess, I have gone so far as to try to read in his eyes whether he was in possession of the secret of our love.”", "그리고 고백하건대, 가끔은 그가 우리 사랑의 비밀을 알고 있는지 그의 눈에서 읽어 내려고까지 했어요.\""]], [["“My good friend,”", "\"막시밀리앙.\""], ["said Valentine, “I should take you for a visionary, and should tremble for your reason, if I were always to hear you talk in a strain similar to this.", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"당신이 늘 이런 식으로 이야기하는 걸 들어야 한다면, 저는 당신을 몽상가로 여기고 당신의 분별력을 걱정할 거예요."], ["Is it possible that you can see anything more than the merest chance in this meeting?", "이 만남에서 그저 단순한 우연 이상의 무언가를 본다는 게 말이 되나요?"], ["Pray reflect a little.", "부디 조금만 생각해 봐요."], ["My father, who never goes out, has several times been on the point of refusing this invitation; Madame de Villefort, on the contrary, is burning with the desire of seeing this extraordinary nabob in his own house, therefore, she has with great difficulty prevailed on my father to accompany her.", "도무지 외출하는 법이 없는 아버지는 이 초대를 거절할 뻔한 적이 여러 번이었어요. 반면 빌포르 부인은 이 비범한 부자 나리를 그의 집에서 보고 싶어 안달이 나서, 갖은 애를 써서 아버지를 동행하게 만든 거고요. 아니에요, 아니에요."], ["No, no; it is as I have said, Maximilian,—there is no one in the world of whom I can ask help but yourself and my grandfather, who is little better than a corpse—no support to cling to but my mother in heaven!”", "제 말대로예요, 막시밀리앙. 이 세상에 제가 도움을 청할 수 있는 사람은 당신과, 송장이나 다름없는 할아버지뿐이에요. 매달릴 버팀목이라곤 하늘에 계신 어머니뿐이고요!\""]], [["“I see that you are right, logically speaking,”", "\"논리로 따지면 당신 말이 옳다는 걸 알겠어요.\""], ["said Maximilian; “but the gentle voice which usually has such power over me fails to convince me today.”", "막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"하지만 평소에는 제게 그토록 큰 힘을 발휘하던 그 다정한 목소리도, 오늘은 저를 설득하지 못하는군요.\""]], [["“I feel the same as regards yourself.”", "\"저도 당신에 대해 똑같이 느껴요.\""], ["said Valentine; “and I own that, if you have no stronger proof to give me——”", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"솔직히, 더 강력한 증거를 내놓지 못한다면……\""]], [["“I have another,”", "\"또 하나 있어요.\""], ["replied Maximilian; “but I fear you will deem it even more absurd than the first.”", "막시밀리앙이 대답했다. \"하지만 첫 번째보다 더 터무니없다고 여기실까 봐 걱정이네요.\""]], [["“So much the worse,”", "\"그렇다면 더 안됐네요.\""], ["said Valentine, smiling.", "발랑틴이 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“It is, nevertheless, conclusive to my mind.", "\"그래도 제게는 결정적인 증거예요."], ["My ten years of service have also confirmed my ideas on the subject of sudden inspirations, for I have several times owed my life to a mysterious impulse which directed me to move at once either to the right or to the left, in order to escape the ball which killed the comrade fighting by my side, while it left me unharmed.”", "십 년 복무하는 동안에도 저는 갑작스러운 영감에 대한 제 생각을 굳혔어요. 어떤 신비로운 충동에 이끌려 단번에 오른쪽이나 왼쪽으로 몸을 움직인 덕분에, 곁에서 싸우던 전우를 쓰러뜨린 총탄을 피하고 저는 멀쩡했던 적이 여러 번이거든요.\""]], [["“Dear Maximilian, why not attribute your escape to my constant prayers for your safety?", "\"막시밀리앙, 그 위기를 모면한 게 당신의 안전을 비는 제 한결같은 기도 덕분이라고 생각하면 안 될까요?"], ["When you are away, I no longer pray for myself, but for you.” 30159m", "당신이 떠나 있을 때, 저는 더 이상 저를 위해 기도하지 않고 당신을 위해 기도해요.\""]], [["“Yes, since you have known me,”", "\"그렇죠, 당신이 저를 알게 된 뒤로는요.\""], ["said Morrel, smiling; “but that cannot apply to the time previous to our acquaintance, Valentine.”", "모렐이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"하지만 우리가 만나기 전에는 그 말이 들어맞지 않잖아요, 발랑틴.\""]], [["“You are very provoking, and will not give me credit for anything; but let me hear this second proof, which you yourself own to be absurd.”", "\"당신은 정말 사람을 약 올리네요. 제게 무얼 하나도 인정해 주질 않아요. 그래도 그 두 번째 증거를 들어 볼게요. 당신 스스로도 터무니없다고 인정한 거요.\""]], [["“Well, look through this opening, and you will see the beautiful new horse which I rode here.”", "\"자, 이 틈으로 들여다봐요. 제가 타고 온 아름다운 새 말이 보일 거예요.\""]], [["“Ah! what a beautiful creature!”", "\"어머! 정말 멋진 녀석이네요!\""], ["cried Valentine; “why did you not bring him close to the gate, so that I could talk to him and pat him?”", "발랑틴이 외쳤다. \"왜 철문 가까이 데려오지 않았어요? 그러면 제가 말도 걸고 쓰다듬어 줄 수 있었을 텐데.\""]], [["“He is, as you see, a very valuable animal,”", "\"보다시피 아주 값진 녀석이에요.\""], ["said Maximilian. “You know that my means are limited, and that I am what would be designated a man of moderate pretensions.", "막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"제 형편이 빠듯하다는 거, 제가 이른바 분수에 맞게 사는 사람이라는 거 아시잖아요."], ["Well, I went to a horse dealer’s, where I saw this magnificent horse, which I have named Médéah.", "그런데 말 장수에게 갔다가 이 근사한 말을 봤어요. 제가 메데아라고 이름 붙였지요."], ["I asked the price; they told me it was 4,500 francs.", "값을 물었더니 4천5백 프랑이라더군요."], ["I was, therefore, obliged to give it up, as you may imagine, but I own I went away with rather a heavy heart, for the horse had looked at me affectionately, had rubbed his head against me and, when I mounted him, had pranced in the most delightful way imaginable, so that I was altogether fascinated with him.", "그러니 짐작하시겠지만 어쩔 수 없이 포기해야 했어요. 그래도 솔직히 꽤 무거운 마음으로 돌아섰어요. 그 말이 저를 다정하게 바라보더니, 제게 머리를 비비고, 제가 올라타자 더없이 사랑스럽게 껑충껑충 뛰어서, 그만 완전히 반해 버렸거든요."], ["The same evening some friends of mine visited me,—M.", "그날 저녁 친구 몇몇이 저를 찾아왔어요."], ["de Château-Renaud, M. Debray, and five or six other choice spirits, whom you do not know, even by name.", "샤토르노 씨, 드브레 씨, 그리고 당신은 이름조차 모르는 대여섯 명의 멋쟁이들이었지요."], ["They proposed a game of _bouillotte_.", "그들이 부요트 한 판 하자고 했어요."], ["I never play, for I am not rich enough to afford to lose, or sufficiently poor to desire to gain.", "저는 노름을 안 해요. 잃을 만큼 부유하지도, 따고 싶을 만큼 가난하지도 않으니까요."], ["But I was at my own house, you understand, so there was nothing to be done but to send for the cards, which I did.", "하지만 제 집이었으니, 카드를 가져오라고 사람을 보내는 수밖에요. 그래서 그렇게 했지요.\""]], [["“Just as they were sitting down to table, M. de Monte Cristo arrived.", "\"막 자리에 앉으려는데 몬테크리스토 백작이 도착했어요."], ["He took his seat amongst them; they played, and I won.", "그도 자리에 끼었고, 다들 노름을 했는데 제가 땄어요."], ["I am almost ashamed to say that my gains amounted to 5,000 francs.", "말하기 거의 부끄러울 지경인데, 제가 딴 돈이 5천 프랑이나 됐어요."], ["We separated at midnight.", "우리는 자정에 헤어졌지요."], ["I could not defer my pleasure, so I took a cabriolet and drove to the horse dealer’s.", "저는 그 기쁨을 미룰 수가 없어서, 이륜마차를 잡아타고 말 장수에게로 달려갔어요."], ["Feverish and excited, I rang at the door. The person who opened it must have taken me for a madman, for I rushed at once to the stable.", "들뜨고 흥분한 채로 문 앞에서 초인종을 울렸지요. 문을 연 사람은 저를 미친 사람으로 여겼을 거예요."], ["Médéah was standing at the rack, eating his hay.", "제가 곧장 마구간으로 달려갔으니까요."], ["I immediately put on the saddle and bridle, to which operation he lent himself with the best grace possible; then, putting the 4,500 francs into the hands of the astonished dealer, I proceeded to fulfil my intention of passing the night in riding in the Champs-Élysées.", "메데아는 여물통 앞에 서서 건초를 먹고 있었어요. 저는 곧바로 안장과 굴레를 채웠는데, 녀석은 더없이 얌전하게 응해 주었지요. 그러고는 어리둥절해하는 말 장수에게 4천5백 프랑을 쥐여 주고, 밤새 샹젤리제를 달리겠다는 생각을 실행에 옮겼어요."], ["As I rode by the count’s house I perceived a light in one of the windows, and fancied I saw the shadow of his figure moving behind the curtain.", "백작의 집 앞을 지나는데, 창문 하나에 불빛이 보였고, 커튼 뒤로 그의 모습이 어른거리는 것 같았어요. 자, 발랑틴, 저는 굳게 믿어요."], ["Now, Valentine, I firmly believe that he knew of my wish to possess this horse, and that he lost expressly to give me the means of procuring him.”", "그는 제가 이 말을 갖고 싶어 한다는 걸 알았고, 제게 그 말을 살 수단을 마련해 주려고 일부러 노름에서 져 준 거라고요.\""]], [["“My dear Maximilian, you are really too fanciful; you will not love even me long.", "\"막시밀리앙, 당신은 정말 너무 공상이 심해요. 그러다간 저조차도 오래 사랑하지 못할 거예요."], ["A man who accustoms himself to live in such a world of poetry and imagination must find far too little excitement in a common, every-day sort of attachment such as ours.", "그렇게 시와 상상의 세계에서 사는 데 익숙해진 남자는, 우리 같은 평범하고 일상적인 사랑에서는 너무나 흥미를 못 느낄 테니까요."], ["But they are calling me.", "그런데 저를 부르고 있어요."], ["Do you hear?”", "들리세요?\""]], [["“Ah, Valentine,”", "\"아, 발랑틴.\" 막시밀리앙이 말했다."], ["said Maximilian, “give me but one finger through this opening in the grating, one finger, the littlest finger of all, that I may have the happiness of kissing it.”", "\"이 격자 틈으로 손가락 하나만, 손가락 하나만 내밀어 줘요. 가장 작은 새끼손가락이라도요. 거기에 입 맞추는 행복을 누리게요.\""]], [["“Maximilian, we said we would be to each other as two voices, two shadows.”", "\"막시밀리앙, 우리는 서로에게 두 개의 목소리, 두 개의 그림자로만 있기로 했잖아요.\""]], [["“As you will, Valentine.”", "\"좋을 대로 해요, 발랑틴.\""]], [["“Shall you be happy if I do what you wish?”", "\"제가 당신 바람대로 하면 행복하겠어요?\""]], [["“Oh, yes!”", "\"아, 그럼요!\""]], [["Valentine mounted on a bench, and passed not only her finger but her whole hand through the opening.", "발랑틴이 벤치에 올라서서, 손가락뿐 아니라 손 전체를 그 틈으로 내밀었다."], ["Maximilian uttered a cry of delight, and, springing forwards, seized the hand extended towards him, and imprinted on it a fervent and impassioned kiss.", "막시밀리앙은 환희의 탄성을 지르며 앞으로 뛰어들어, 자기에게 뻗어 온 손을 붙잡고는 그 위에 뜨겁고 정열적인 입맞춤을 찍었다."], ["The little hand was then immediately withdrawn, and the young man saw Valentine hurrying towards the house, as though she were almost terrified at her own sensations.", "그러자 작은 손이 곧바로 거두어졌고, 청년은 발랑틴이 집을 향해 황급히 달려가는 모습을 보았다. 마치 자기 자신의 감정에 깜짝 놀라기라도 한 듯이."]], [["# Chapter 58. M. Noirtier de Villefort", "# Chapter 58. M. Noirtier de Villefort"]], [["We will now relate what was passing in the house of the king’s attorney after the departure of Madame Danglars and her daughter, and during the time of the conversation between Maximilian and Valentine, which we have just detailed.", "당글라르 부인과 그 딸이 떠난 뒤, 그리고 방금 우리가 자세히 전한 막시밀리앙과 발랑틴의 대화가 오가던 동안, 검사장의 집에서 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있었는지를 이제 이야기하려 한다."]], [["M. de Villefort entered his father’s room, followed by Madame de Villefort.", "빌포르 씨가 아버지의 방으로 들어섰고, 빌포르 부인이 그 뒤를 따랐다."], ["Both of the visitors, after saluting the old man and speaking to Barrois, a faithful servant, who had been twenty-five years in his service, took their places on either side of the paralytic.", "두 사람은 노인에게 인사하고, 그의 시중을 든 지 이십오 년 된 충직한 하인 바루아에게 몇 마디 건넨 뒤, 반신불수 노인의 양옆에 자리를 잡았다."]], [["M. Noirtier was sitting in an armchair, which moved upon casters, in which he was wheeled into the room in the morning, and in the same way drawn out again at night.", "누아르티에 씨는 바퀴 달린 안락의자에 앉아 있었다. 아침이면 그 의자에 실려 방으로 들어왔고, 밤이면 같은 방식으로 다시 밖으로 끌려 나갔다."], ["He was placed before a large glass, which reflected the whole apartment, and so, without any attempt to move, which would have been impossible, he could see all who entered the room and everything which was going on around him. M.", "그는 방 전체를 비추는 커다란 거울 앞에 놓여 있었다. 그래서 그는 몸을 움직이려는 시도—그것은 애초에 불가능한 일이었다—를 하지 않고도, 방에 들어오는 모든 이와 주위에서 일어나는 모든 일을 볼 수 있었다."], ["Noirtier, although almost as immovable as a corpse, looked at the new-comers with a quick and intelligent expression, perceiving at once, by their ceremonious courtesy, that they were come on business of an unexpected and official character.", "누아르티에 씨는 시신이나 다름없이 거의 꼼짝하지 못했지만, 그 두 방문객을 기민하고 총명한 눈빛으로 바라보았다. 그들의 격식 차린 정중함을 보고, 그는 뜻밖의 공적인 용건으로 찾아왔음을 단번에 알아챘다."]], [["Sight and hearing were the only senses remaining, and they, like two solitary sparks, remained to animate the miserable body which seemed fit for nothing but the grave; it was only, however, by means of one of these senses that he could reveal the thoughts and feelings that still occupied his mind, and the look by which he gave expression to his inner life was like the distant gleam of a candle which a traveller sees by night across some desert place, and knows that a living being dwells beyond the silence and obscurity.", "이제 그에게 남은 감각은 시각과 청각뿐이었고, 그 둘은 외로운 두 불꽃처럼 남아, 무덤 말고는 아무 데도 쓸모없어 보이는 가련한 육신에 생기를 불어넣고 있었다. 그러나 그가 아직 마음속을 채우고 있는 생각과 감정을 드러낼 수 있는 길은 이 두 감각 중 하나뿐이었다. 그가 자신의 내면을 표현하는 눈빛은, 밤길의 나그네가 황량한 들판 너머로 보는 멀리 가물거리는 촛불과도 같았다. 그 침묵과 어둠 너머에 살아 있는 존재가 깃들어 있음을 알려 주는 불빛이었다."]], [["Noirtier’s hair was long and white, and flowed over his shoulders; while in his eyes, shaded by thick black lashes, was concentrated, as it often happens with an organ which is used to the exclusion of the others, all the activity, address, force, and intelligence which were formerly diffused over his whole body; and so although the movement of the arm, the sound of the voice, and the agility of the body, were wanting, the speaking eye sufficed for all.", "누아르티에의 머리카락은 길고 희어 어깨 위로 흘러내렸다. 짙고 검은 속눈썹이 드리운 그의 눈에는, 한 기관이 다른 기관들의 몫까지 떠맡을 때 흔히 그러하듯, 한때 온몸에 퍼져 있던 모든 활기와 솜씨와 힘과 지성이 응축되어 있었다. 그래서 팔의 움직임도, 목소리도, 몸의 민첩함도 없었지만, 말하는 눈 하나로 모든 것이 충분했다."], ["He commanded with it; it was the medium through which his thanks were conveyed.", "그는 그 눈으로 명령했고, 그 눈을 통해 고마움을 전했다."], ["In short, his whole appearance produced on the mind the impression of a corpse with living eyes, and nothing could be more startling than to observe the expression of anger or joy suddenly lighting up these organs, while the rest of the rigid and marble-like features were utterly deprived of the power of participation.", "요컨대 그의 모습 전체는 살아 있는 눈을 가진 시신이라는 인상을 주었다. 그리고 분노나 기쁨의 표정이 그 눈에 별안간 타오르는 한편, 굳어 버린 대리석 같은 나머지 얼굴은 거기에 함께할 힘을 완전히 잃은 채로 있는 광경만큼 놀라운 것은 없었다."], ["Three persons only could understand this language of the poor paralytic; these were Villefort, Valentine, and the old servant of whom we have already spoken.", "이 가엾은 반신불수 노인의 언어를 이해할 수 있는 사람은 단 셋뿐이었다. 빌포르와 발랑틴, 그리고 앞서 말한 늙은 하인이었다."], ["But as Villefort saw his father but seldom, and then only when absolutely obliged, and as he never took any pains to please or gratify him when he was there, all the old man’s happiness was centred in his granddaughter.", "그러나 빌포르는 어쩔 수 없을 때만, 그것도 드물게 아버지를 찾았고, 와서도 그를 기쁘게 하거나 흡족하게 해 주려는 수고를 결코 들이지 않았다. 그래서 노인의 모든 행복은 손녀에게 쏠려 있었다."], ["Valentine, by means of her love, her patience, and her devotion, had learned to read in Noirtier’s look all the varied feelings which were passing in his mind.", "발랑틴은 사랑과 인내와 헌신으로, 누아르티에의 눈빛에서 그의 마음을 스쳐 가는 온갖 감정을 읽어 내는 법을 익혔다."], ["To this dumb language, which was so unintelligible to others, she answered by throwing her whole soul into the expression of her countenance, and in this manner were the conversations sustained between the blooming girl and the helpless invalid, whose body could scarcely be called a living one, but who, nevertheless, possessed a fund of knowledge and penetration, united with a will as powerful as ever although clogged by a body rendered utterly incapable of obeying its impulses.", "남들은 도무지 알아들을 수 없는 이 말 없는 언어에, 그녀는 자신의 온 영혼을 얼굴 표정에 담아 답했다. 이렇게 해서 꽃다운 처녀와 무력한 환자 사이에 대화가 이어졌다. 그 환자의 육신은 살아 있다고 말하기조차 어려웠으나, 그럼에도 그 안에는 풍부한 지식과 통찰이 깃들어 있었고, 충동에 따를 능력을 완전히 잃은 육신에 갇혀 있을지언정 여전히 예전 못지않게 강한 의지가 함께 자리하고 있었다."]], [["Valentine had solved the problem, and was able easily to understand his thoughts, and to convey her own in return, and, through her untiring and devoted assiduity, it was seldom that, in the ordinary transactions of every-day life, she failed to anticipate the wishes of the living, thinking mind, or the wants of the almost inanimate body.", "발랑틴은 그 수수께끼를 풀어내, 그의 생각을 쉽게 알아채고 자신의 생각도 되돌려 전할 수 있었다. 지칠 줄 모르는 헌신적인 정성 덕분에, 일상의 자질구레한 일들에서 살아 있는 그 정신의 바람이나 거의 죽은 듯한 육신의 필요를 그녀가 미리 헤아리지 못하는 일은 좀처럼 없었다."]], [["As to the servant, he had, as we have said, been with his master for five-and-twenty years, therefore he knew all his habits, and it was seldom that Noirtier found it necessary to ask for anything, so prompt was he in administering to all the necessities of the invalid.", "하인으로 말하자면, 앞서 말했듯이 주인을 이십오 년이나 모셔 온 터라, 그의 모든 습관을 알고 있었다. 그래서 누아르티에가 무언가를 청해야 할 일이 좀처럼 없었으니, 환자의 온갖 필요를 그토록 재빨리 채워 주었던 것이다."]], [["Villefort did not need the help of either Valentine or the domestic in order to carry on with his father the strange conversation which he was about to begin.", "빌포르는 이제 막 시작하려는 아버지와의 기묘한 대화를 이어 가는 데 발랑틴의 도움도, 하인의 도움도 필요하지 않았다."], ["As we have said, he perfectly understood the old man’s vocabulary, and if he did not use it more often, it was only indifference and _ennui_ which prevented him from so doing.", "앞서 말했듯이 그는 노인의 어휘를 완벽하게 이해하고 있었고, 그것을 더 자주 쓰지 않은 까닭은 오로지 무관심과 권태 때문이었다."], ["He therefore allowed Valentine to go into the garden, sent away Barrois, and after having seated himself at his father’s right hand, while Madame de Villefort placed herself on the left, he addressed him thus:", "그래서 그는 발랑틴을 정원으로 내보내고 바루아를 물러가게 한 뒤, 아버지의 오른쪽에 앉았다. 빌포르 부인은 왼쪽에 자리를 잡았다. 그러고는 그가 이렇게 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["“I trust you will not be displeased, sir, that Valentine has not come with us, or that I dismissed Barrois, for our conference will be one which could not with propriety be carried on in the presence of either.", "\"불쾌하게 여기지 않으시기를 바랍니다, 아버지. 발랑틴이 함께 오지 않았고, 제가 바루아를 물린 것 말입니다. 우리가 나눌 이야기는 그 둘이 있는 자리에서 격식에 맞게 할 수 있는 것이 아니라서요."], ["Madame de Villefort and I have a communication to make to you.”", "빌포르 부인과 제가 아버지께 전할 말씀이 있습니다.\""]], [["Noirtier’s face remained perfectly passive during this long preamble, while, on the contrary, Villefort’s eye was endeavoring to penetrate into the inmost recesses of the old man’s heart.", "누아르티에의 얼굴은 이 긴 서두가 이어지는 동안 더없이 무표정했다. 반면 빌포르의 눈은 노인의 마음속 가장 깊은 구석까지 꿰뚫어 보려 애쓰고 있었다."]], [["“This communication,”", "\"이 말씀은,\""], ["continued the procureur, in that cold and decisive tone which seemed at once to preclude all discussion, “will, we are sure, meet with your approbation.”", "검사장이 일체의 논의를 단번에 가로막을 듯한 차갑고 단호한 어조로 말을 이었다. \"분명 아버지의 찬성을 얻으리라 믿습니다.\""]], [["The eye of the invalid still retained that vacancy of expression which prevented his son from obtaining any knowledge of the feelings which were passing in his mind; he listened, nothing more.", "환자의 눈은 여전히 텅 빈 표정을 띠고 있어, 아들은 그 마음속을 스치는 감정을 도무지 알아챌 수 없었다. 그는 듣기만 했다, 그뿐이었다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"아버지,\""], ["resumed Villefort, “we are thinking of marrying Valentine.”", "빌포르가 다시 입을 열었다. \"저희는 발랑틴을 시집보낼 생각입니다.\""], ["Had the old man’s face been moulded in wax it could not have shown less emotion at this news than was now to be traced there.", "노인의 얼굴이 밀랍으로 빚어진 것이었다 해도, 이 소식에 지금 그 얼굴에 떠오른 것보다 더 적은 감정을 드러내지는 못했을 것이다."], ["“The marriage will take place in less than three months,”", "\"결혼식은 석 달 안에 치러질 겁니다.\""], ["said Villefort.", "빌포르가 말했다."]], [["Noirtier’s eye still retained its inanimate expression.", "누아르티에의 눈은 여전히 생기 없는 표정을 띠고 있었다."]], [["Madame de Villefort now took her part in the conversation and added:", "이번에는 빌포르 부인이 대화에 끼어들어 이렇게 덧붙였다."]], [["“We thought this news would possess an interest for you, sir, who have always entertained a great affection for Valentine; it therefore only now remains for us to tell you the name of the young man for whom she is destined.", "\"늘 발랑틴을 그토록 아껴 오신 아버님이시니, 이 소식이 마음에 드시리라 생각했습니다. 그러니 이제 그 아이의 짝이 될 청년의 이름만 말씀드리면 되겠지요."], ["It is one of the most desirable connections which could possibly be formed; he possesses fortune, a high rank in society, and every personal qualification likely to render Valentine supremely happy,—his name, moreover, cannot be wholly unknown to you.", "더없이 바람직한 혼처랍니다. 재산도 있고, 사회적 지위도 높으며, 발랑틴을 더없이 행복하게 해 줄 만한 인품도 두루 갖추었지요. 게다가 그 이름이 아버님께 아주 낯설지만은 않을 거예요."], ["It is M. Franz de Quesnel, Baron d’Épinay.”", "프란츠 드 케넬, 데피네 남작이랍니다.\""]], [["While his wife was speaking, Villefort had narrowly watched the old man’s countenance.", "아내가 말하는 동안, 빌포르는 노인의 얼굴을 유심히 살피고 있었다."], ["When Madame de Villefort pronounced the name of Franz, the pupil of M. Noirtier’s eye began to dilate, and his eyelids trembled with the same movement that may be perceived on the lips of an individual about to speak, and he darted a lightning glance at Madame de Villefort and his son.", "빌포르 부인이 프란츠라는 이름을 입에 올리자, 누아르티에 씨의 눈동자가 커지기 시작했고, 막 말을 하려는 사람의 입술에서 볼 법한 떨림이 그 눈꺼풀에 일었다. 그러고는 그가 빌포르 부인과 아들에게 번개 같은 눈길을 던졌다."], ["The procureur, who knew the political hatred which had formerly existed between M. Noirtier and the elder d’Épinay, well understood the agitation and anger which the announcement had produced; but, feigning not to perceive either, he immediately resumed the narrative begun by his wife.", "누아르티에 씨와 데피네 노인 사이에 한때 정치적 증오가 있었음을 아는 검사장은, 그 통고가 불러일으킨 동요와 분노를 잘 알아챘다. 그러나 그는 둘 다 못 본 척하며, 곧바로 아내가 꺼낸 이야기를 이어 갔다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"아버지,\" 그가 말했다. \"발랑틴이 이제 열아홉 살로 접어든다는 걸 아시지요."], ["said he, “you are aware that Valentine is about to enter her nineteenth year, which renders it important that she should lose no time in forming a suitable alliance.", "그러니 알맞은 혼처를 맺는 데 한시도 지체해서는 안 됩니다. 그렇다고 저희 계획에서 아버지를 잊은 것은 아닙니다. 미리 충분히 확인해 두었지요."], ["Nevertheless, you have not been forgotten in our plans, and we have fully ascertained beforehand that Valentine’s future husband will consent, not to live in this house, for that might not be pleasant for the young people, but that you should live with them; so that you and Valentine, who are so attached to each other, would not be separated, and you would be able to pursue exactly the same course of life which you have hitherto done, and thus, instead of losing, you will be a gainer by the change, as it will secure to you two children instead of one, to watch over and comfort you.”", "발랑틴의 장래 남편은—젊은 부부에게는 그게 달갑지 않을 수 있으니 이 집에서 함께 사는 것은 아니되—아버지께서 그들과 함께 지내시는 데 흔쾌히 동의할 거라고요. 그러면 그토록 서로를 아끼는 아버지와 발랑틴이 떨어질 일도 없을 테고, 지금껏 해 오신 것과 똑같은 생활을 그대로 이어 가실 수 있을 겁니다. 그러니 이번 일로 잃는 게 아니라 오히려 얻는 셈이지요."], ["30165m", "아버지를 돌보고 위로해 줄 자식이 하나가 아니라 둘로 늘어날 테니까요.\""]], [["Noirtier’s look was furious; it was very evident that something desperate was passing in the old man’s mind, for a cry of anger and grief rose in his throat, and not being able to find vent in utterance, appeared almost to choke him, for his face and lips turned quite purple with the struggle.", "누아르티에의 눈빛이 사나워졌다. 노인의 마음속에 무언가 절박한 것이 휘몰아치고 있음이 또렷이 보였다. 분노와 비탄의 외침이 목구멍까지 치밀어 올랐으나 말로 터져 나올 길을 찾지 못해, 그를 거의 질식시킬 듯했다."], ["Villefort quietly opened a window, saying, “It is very warm, and the heat affects M.", "그 몸부림에 얼굴과 입술이 시퍼렇게 변했다. 빌포르는 조용히 창문을 열며 말했다. \"몹시 덥군요."], ["Noirtier.” He then returned to his place, but did not sit down.", "이 더위가 아버지께 무리가 되나 봅니다.\" 그러고는 제자리로 돌아왔으나 앉지는 않았다."]], [["“This marriage,”", "\"이 결혼은,\""], ["added Madame de Villefort, “is quite agreeable to the wishes of M.", "빌포르 부인이 덧붙였다. \"데피네 씨와 그 집안의 뜻에도 더없이 합당하답니다."], ["d’Épinay and his family; besides, he had no relations nearer than an uncle and aunt, his mother having died at his birth, and his father having been assassinated in 1815, that is to say, when he was but two years old; it naturally followed that the child was permitted to choose his own pursuits, and he has, therefore, seldom acknowledged any other authority but that of his own will.”", "게다가 그분에게는 숙부와 숙모 말고는 가까운 친척이 없어요. 어머니는 그가 태어날 때 돌아가셨고, 아버지는 1815년에, 그러니까 그가 겨우 두 살이었을 때 암살당하셨으니까요. 그래서 자연히 그 아이는 제 갈 길을 스스로 고르도록 맡겨졌고, 그러니 제 의지 말고는 어떤 권위도 좀처럼 인정한 적이 없답니다.\""]], [["“That assassination was a mysterious affair,”", "\"그 암살은 알 수 없는 사건이었습니다.\""], ["said Villefort, “and the perpetrators have hitherto escaped detection, although suspicion has fallen on the head of more than one person.”", "빌포르가 말했다. \"여러 사람에게 혐의가 쏠리긴 했지만, 범인들은 지금껏 잡히지 않았지요.\""]], [["Noirtier made such an effort that his lips expanded into a smile.", "누아르티에는 어찌나 안간힘을 썼던지, 입술이 벌어져 미소를 지었다."]], [["“Now,”", "\"한데,\""], ["continued Villefort, “those to whom the guilt really belongs, by whom the crime was committed, on whose heads the justice of man may probably descend here, and the certain judgment of God hereafter, would rejoice in the opportunity thus afforded of bestowing such a peace-offering as Valentine on the son of him whose life they so ruthlessly destroyed.”", "빌포르가 말을 이었다. \"정말로 죄를 진 자들, 그 범행을 저지른 자들, 이승에서는 인간의 정의가, 저승에서는 하느님의 어김없는 심판이 그 머리 위에 내릴 자들이라면, 자기들이 그토록 무자비하게 목숨을 앗은 사람의 아들에게 발랑틴을 화해의 제물로 바칠 기회가 주어진 것을 기뻐할 겁니다.\""], ["Noirtier had succeeded in mastering his emotion more than could have been deemed possible with such an enfeebled and shattered frame.", "누아르티에는, 그토록 쇠약하고 부서진 몸으로는 도저히 가능하리라 여겨지지 않을 만큼, 감정을 다스리는 데 성공했다."]], [["“Yes, I understand,”", "\"그래, 알겠다.\""], ["was the reply contained in his look; and this look expressed a feeling of strong indignation, mixed with profound contempt.", "그의 눈빛에 담긴 대답은 그런 것이었다. 그리고 그 눈빛은 강한 분노에 깊은 경멸이 뒤섞인 감정을 드러냈다."], ["Villefort fully understood his father’s meaning, and answered by a slight shrug of his shoulders.", "빌포르는 아버지의 뜻을 똑똑히 이해하고는, 어깨를 가볍게 으쓱하며 답했다."], ["He then motioned to his wife to take leave.", "그러고는 아내에게 물러가자는 신호를 보냈다."]], [["“Now sir,” said Madame de Villefort, “I must bid you farewell.", "\"그럼 아버님,\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다."], ["Would you like me to send Edward to you for a short time?”", "\"이만 물러가겠습니다. 에두아르를 잠깐 아버님께 보내 드릴까요?\""]], [["It had been agreed that the old man should express his approbation by closing his eyes, his refusal by winking them several times, and if he had some desire or feeling to express, he raised them to heaven.", "미리 정해 둔 약속이 있었다. 노인은 찬성하면 눈을 감고, 거절하면 여러 번 눈을 깜박이며, 바라거나 표현하고 싶은 것이 있으면 눈을 하늘로 치켜뜨기로 했다."], ["If he wanted Valentine, he closed his right eye only, and if Barrois, the left.", "발랑틴을 원하면 오른쪽 눈만 감고, 바루아를 원하면 왼쪽 눈만 감았다."], ["At Madame de Villefort’s proposition he instantly winked his eyes.", "빌포르 부인의 제안에 그는 곧장 눈을 깜박였다."]], [["Provoked by a complete refusal, she bit her lip and said, “Then shall I send Valentine to you?”", "단호한 거절에 발끈한 그녀는 입술을 깨물며 말했다. \"그럼 발랑틴을 보내 드릴까요?\""], ["The old man closed his eyes eagerly, thereby intimating that such was his wish.", "노인은 그러기를 바란다는 뜻으로 얼른 눈을 감았다."]], [["M. and Madame de Villefort bowed and left the room, giving orders that Valentine should be summoned to her grandfather’s presence, and feeling sure that she would have much to do to restore calmness to the perturbed spirit of the invalid.", "빌포르 부부는 인사하고 방을 나서며, 발랑틴을 할아버지에게 들도록 하라고 일렀다. 환자의 들끓는 마음을 가라앉히려면 그 아이가 무던히 애를 써야 하리라 확신하면서였다."], ["Valentine, with a color still heightened by emotion, entered the room just after her parents had quitted it.", "부모가 막 방을 나서자마자, 발랑틴이 격한 감정으로 여전히 발그레해진 얼굴로 들어왔다."], ["One look was sufficient to tell her that her grandfather was suffering, and that there was much on his mind which he was wishing to communicate to her.", "그녀는 한눈에 할아버지가 괴로워하고 있음을, 그리고 자신에게 전하고 싶은 무언가가 마음속에 잔뜩 쌓여 있음을 알아챘다."]], [["“Dear grandpapa,”", "\"할아버지,\""], ["cried she, “what has happened?", "그녀가 외쳤다. \"무슨 일이세요?"], ["They have vexed you, and you are angry?”", "누가 할아버지를 속상하게 했군요, 화가 나셨죠?\""]], [["The paralytic closed his eyes in token of assent.", "반신불수 노인이 그렇다는 표시로 눈을 감았다."]], [["“Who has displeased you? Is it my father?” “No.”", "\"누가 할아버지를 언짢게 했어요? 우리 아버지인가요?\""]], [["“Madame de Villefort?”", "\"아니다.\" \"빌포르 부인인가요?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"아니다.\""]], [["“Me?” The former sign was repeated.", "\"저예요?\" 앞서의 신호가 되풀이되었다."]], [["“Are you displeased with me?”", "\"저한테 화가 나신 거예요?\""], ["cried Valentine in astonishment. M. Noirtier again closed his eyes.", "발랑틴이 놀라 외쳤다. 누아르티에 씨가 다시 눈을 감았다."]], [["“And what have I done, dear grandpapa, that you should be angry with me?”", "\"제가 무얼 했기에 저한테 화를 내세요, 할아버지?\""], ["cried Valentine.", "발랑틴이 외쳤다."]], [["There was no answer, and she continued:", "아무 대답이 없었고, 그녀는 말을 이었다."]], [["“I have not seen you all day.", "\"하루 종일 할아버지를 못 뵈었는걸요."], ["Has anyone been speaking to you against me?”", "누가 할아버지께 제 험담이라도 했나요?\""]], [["“Yes,” said the old man’s look, with eagerness.", "\"그렇다.\" 노인의 눈빛이 다급하게 말했다."]], [["“Let me think a moment.", "\"잠깐 생각해 볼게요."], ["I do assure you, grandpapa—Ah—M. and Madame de Villefort have just left this room, have they not?”", "정말이지 할아버지—아, 아버지와 빌포르 부인이 방금 이 방에서 나가셨죠, 그렇죠?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇다.\""]], [["“And it was they who told you something which made you angry?", "\"그분들이 할아버지께 무슨 말을 해서 화가 나신 거군요?"], ["What was it then?", "그게 뭐였나요?"], ["May I go and ask them, that I may have the opportunity of making my peace with you?”", "가서 여쭤보고 와도 될까요? 그래야 할아버지와 화해할 길이 생길 테니까요.\""]], [["“No, no,” said Noirtier’s look.", "\"아니다, 아니다.\" 누아르티에의 눈빛이 말했다."]], [["“Ah, you frighten me. What can they have said?”", "\"아, 무서워요. 도대체 무슨 말을 한 걸까요?\""], ["and she again tried to think what it could be.", "그녀는 다시 그게 무엇일지 생각해 보려 애썼다."]], [["“Ah, I know,” said she, lowering her voice and going close to the old man.", "\"아, 알겠어요.\" 그녀가 목소리를 낮추며 노인에게 바짝 다가가 말했다."], ["“They have been speaking of my marriage,—have they not?”", "\"제 결혼 이야기를 하셨군요. 그렇죠?\""]], [["“Yes,” replied the angry look.", "\"그렇다.\" 화난 눈빛이 답했다."]], [["“I understand; you are displeased at the silence I have preserved on the subject.", "\"알겠어요. 제가 그 일을 입 다물고 있던 게 언짢으셨군요."], ["The reason of it was, that they had insisted on my keeping the matter a secret, and begged me not to tell you anything of it.", "그건 그분들이 비밀로 하라고 신신당부하면서, 할아버지께 아무것도 말씀드리지 말라고 부탁했기 때문이에요."], ["They did not even acquaint me with their intentions, and I only discovered them by chance, that is why I have been so reserved with you, dear grandpapa.", "제게는 그분들 속셈조차 알려 주지 않아서, 우연히 알아챈 거랍니다. 그래서 할아버지께 그토록 입을 다물고 있었던 거예요."], ["Pray forgive me.”", "부디 용서해 주세요.\""]], [["But there was no look calculated to reassure her; all it seemed to say was, “It is not only your reserve which afflicts me.”", "그러나 그녀를 안심시킬 만한 눈빛은 보이지 않았다. 그 눈빛이 말하는 것은 오직 이것뿐인 듯했다. \"나를 괴롭히는 건 네가 입을 다물었던 것만이 아니다.\""]], [["“What is it, then?”", "\"그럼 뭐예요?\""], ["asked the young girl.", "처녀가 물었다."], ["“Perhaps you think I shall abandon you, dear grandpapa, and that I shall forget you when I am married?” “No.”", "\"할아버지, 혹시 제가 결혼하면 할아버지를 버리고 잊어버릴 거라고 생각하시는 거예요?\""]], [["“They told you, then, that M. d’Épinay consented to our all living together?”", "\"아니다.\" \"그럼 데피네 씨가 우리 모두 함께 사는 데 동의했다는 말을 들으셨군요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇다.\""]], [["“Then why are you still vexed and grieved?”", "\"그런데 왜 아직도 속상해하고 슬퍼하세요?\""], ["The old man’s eyes beamed with an expression of gentle affection.", "노인의 눈이 부드러운 애정의 표정으로 빛났다."]], [["“Yes, I understand,”", "\"알겠어요.\""], ["said Valentine; “it is because you love me.”", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"저를 사랑하셔서 그런 거죠.\""], ["The old man assented.", "노인이 그렇다고 했다."]], [["“And you are afraid I shall be unhappy?”", "\"그리고 제가 불행해질까 봐 두려우신 거고요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇다.\""]], [["“You do not like M. Franz?”", "\"프란츠 씨가 마음에 안 드시는 거예요?\""], ["The eyes repeated several times, “No, no, no.”", "눈이 여러 번 되풀이했다. \"아니다, 아니다, 아니다.\""]], [["“Then you are vexed with the engagement?”", "\"그럼 이 약혼이 언짢으신 거군요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇다.\""]], [["“Well, listen,”", "\"그럼 들어 보세요.\""], ["said Valentine, throwing herself on her knees, and putting her arm round her grandfather’s neck, “I am vexed, too, for I do not love M.", "발랑틴이 무릎을 꿇고 할아버지의 목에 팔을 두르며 말했다. \"저도 언짢아요."], ["Franz d’Épinay.”", "저도 프란츠 데피네 씨를 사랑하지 않으니까요.\""]], [["An expression of intense joy illumined the old man’s eyes.", "노인의 눈에 더없는 기쁨의 표정이 환히 떠올랐다."]], [["“When I wished to retire into a convent, you remember how angry you were with me?”", "\"제가 수녀원에 들어가려 했을 때, 할아버지가 저한테 얼마나 화를 내셨는지 기억하시죠?\""], ["A tear trembled in the eye of the invalid. “Well,”", "환자의 눈에 눈물이 어른거렸다. \"실은요,\""], ["continued Valentine, “the reason of my proposing it was that I might escape this hateful marriage, which drives me to despair.”", "발랑틴이 말을 이었다. \"제가 그러자고 한 까닭은 이 끔찍한 결혼을 피하고 싶어서였어요. 그 생각만 하면 절망에 빠지거든요.\""], ["Noirtier’s breathing came thick and short.", "누아르티에의 숨이 가쁘고 짧아졌다."]], [["“Then the idea of this marriage really grieves you too?", "\"그럼 이 결혼이 정말 할아버지도 슬프게 하는군요?"], ["Ah, if you could but help me—if we could both together defeat their plan!", "아, 할아버지가 저를 도울 수만 있다면—우리 둘이 함께 그분들 계획을 무너뜨릴 수만 있다면!"], ["But you are unable to oppose them,—you, whose mind is so quick, and whose will is so firm are nevertheless, as weak and unequal to the contest as I am myself.", "하지만 할아버지는 그분들에게 맞서실 수가 없잖아요. 그토록 명민한 정신과 그토록 굳센 의지를 지니셨는데도, 할아버지 역시 저처럼 이 싸움에 무력하고 부치는 처지인걸요."], ["Alas, you, who would have been such a powerful protector to me in the days of your health and strength, can now only sympathize in my joys and sorrows, without being able to take any active part in them.", "아, 건강하고 정정하시던 시절이었다면 제게 그토록 든든한 보호자가 되어 주셨을 할아버지가, 이제는 제 기쁨과 슬픔에 함께 마음 아파하실 뿐, 거기에 적극적으로 나서지는 못하시는군요."], ["However, this is much, and calls for gratitude and Heaven has not taken away all my blessings when it leaves me your sympathy and kindness.”", "그래도 이것만으로도 큰일이고 고마운 일이에요. 할아버지의 공감과 다정함을 제게 남겨 주셨으니, 하늘이 제 모든 복을 앗아 간 건 아니랍니다.\""]], [["At these words there appeared in Noirtier’s eye an expression of such deep meaning that the young girl thought she could read these words there: “You are mistaken; I can still do much for you.”", "이 말에 누아르티에의 눈에 어찌나 깊은 뜻이 담긴 표정이 떠올랐던지, 처녀는 거기서 이런 말을 읽을 수 있을 것만 같았다. \"네가 잘못 알고 있다. 나는 아직 너를 위해 많은 일을 할 수 있다.\""]], [["“Do you think you can help me, dear grandpapa?”", "\"할아버지, 저를 도와주실 수 있다고 생각하세요?\""], ["said Valentine.", "발랑틴이 말했다."]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇다.\""], ["Noirtier raised his eyes, it was the sign agreed on between him and Valentine when he wanted anything.", "누아르티에가 눈을 치켜떴다. 무언가를 원할 때 그와 발랑틴 사이에 정해 둔 신호였다."]], [["“What is it you want, dear grandpapa?”", "\"무얼 원하세요, 할아버지?\""], ["said Valentine, and she endeavored to recall to mind all the things which he would be likely to need; and as the ideas presented themselves to her mind, she repeated them aloud, then,—finding that all her efforts elicited nothing but a constant _“No,”_—she said, “Come, since this plan does not answer, I will have recourse to another.”", "발랑틴이 말하며, 그가 필요로 할 만한 모든 것을 떠올려 보려 애썼다. 생각이 떠오르는 대로 소리 내어 읊었으나, 그 모든 노력에 돌아오는 것이라곤 끊임없는 \"아니다\"뿐임을 알고는 이렇게 말했다. \"자, 이 방법이 안 통하니, 다른 방법을 써 볼게요.\""]], [["She then recited all the letters of the alphabet from A down to N.", "그러고는 알파벳 A부터 N까지 죄다 읊었다."], ["When she arrived at that letter the paralytic made her understand that she had spoken the initial letter of the thing he wanted.", "N에 이르자 반신불수 노인은, 그녀가 자신이 원하는 것의 첫 글자를 말했음을 알려 주었다."]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["said Valentine, “the thing you desire begins with the letter N; it is with N that we have to do, then.", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"원하시는 게 N으로 시작하는군요. 그럼 N인 거네요."], ["Well, let me see, what can you want that begins with N? Na—Ne—Ni—No——”", "자, 어디 볼까요, N으로 시작하는 무얼 원하실까? 나—느—니—노……\""]], [["“Yes, yes, yes,” said the old man’s eye.", "\"그래, 그래, 그래.\" 노인의 눈이 말했다."]], [["“Ah, it is No, then?”", "\"아, 그럼 '노'예요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇다.\""]], [["Valentine fetched a dictionary, which she placed on a desk before Noirtier; she opened it, and, seeing that the old man’s eye was thoroughly fixed on its pages, she ran her finger quickly up and down the columns.", "발랑틴은 사전을 가져와 누아르티에 앞 책상에 펼쳐 놓았다. 노인의 눈이 그 책장에 단단히 못 박힌 것을 보고, 그녀는 손가락으로 단을 따라 위아래로 재빨리 훑어 내려갔다."], ["During the six years which had passed since Noirtier first fell into this sad state, Valentine’s powers of invention had been too often put to the test not to render her expert in devising expedients for gaining a knowledge of his wishes, and the constant practice had so perfected her in the art that she guessed the old man’s meaning as quickly as if he himself had been able to seek for what he wanted.", "누아르티에가 처음 이 가련한 처지에 빠진 뒤로 흐른 육 년 동안, 발랑틴의 기지는 하도 여러 번 시험대에 올랐던 터라, 그의 바람을 알아내는 방편을 짜내는 데 능숙하지 않을 수 없었다. 그 끊임없는 연습이 그녀를 이 기술에 완벽하게 단련시켜, 노인이 직접 원하는 것을 찾아낼 수 있었더라도 그만큼 빠를까 싶을 만큼, 그녀는 노인의 뜻을 단박에 알아맞혔다."], ["At the word _Notary_, Noirtier made a sign to her to stop. 30169m", "'공증인'이라는 낱말에 이르자, 누아르티에가 그녀에게 멈추라는 신호를 보냈다."]], [["“Notary,”", "\"공증인이요.\""], ["said she, “do you want a notary, dear grandpapa?”", "그녀가 말했다. \"공증인을 원하세요, 할아버지?\""], ["The old man again signified that it was a notary he desired.", "노인은 자신이 원하는 것이 공증인임을 다시 한번 알렸다."]], [["“You would wish a notary to be sent for then?”", "\"그럼 공증인을 불러오기를 바라시는 거예요?\""], ["said Valentine.", "발랑틴이 말했다."]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇다.\""]], [["“Shall my father be informed of your wish?”", "\"이 일을 아버지께 알려도 될까요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇다.\""]], [["“Do you wish the notary to be sent for immediately?”", "\"공증인을 당장 불러오기를 바라세요?\" \"그렇다.\""]], [["“Yes.” “Then they shall go for him directly, dear grandpapa.", "\"그럼 지금 바로 사람을 보내 모셔 오게 할게요, 할아버지."], ["Is that all you want?”", "원하시는 게 그게 다예요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇다.\""], ["Valentine rang the bell, and ordered the servant to tell Monsieur or Madame de Villefort that they were requested to come to M. Noirtier’s room.", "발랑틴은 종을 울려, 빌포르 씨나 부인께 누아르티에 씨 방으로 와 주십사 청한다고 하인에게 전하라 일렀다."]], [["“Are you satisfied now?”", "\"이제 흡족하세요?\""], ["inquired Valentine.", "발랑틴이 물었다. \"그렇다.\""]], [["“Yes.” “I am sure you are; it is not very difficult to discover that.”", "\"그러실 줄 알았어요. 그건 알아채기가 그리 어렵지 않은걸요.\""], ["And the young girl smiled on her grandfather, as if he had been a child. M.", "그러고는 처녀가 마치 어린아이를 대하듯 할아버지에게 미소 지었다."], ["de Villefort entered, followed by Barrois.", "빌포르 씨가 들어왔고, 바루아가 그 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["“What do you want me for, sir?”", "\"무슨 일로 저를 부르셨습니까, 아버지?\""], ["demanded he of the paralytic.", "그가 반신불수 노인에게 물었다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"아버지,\""], ["said Valentine, “my grandfather wishes for a notary.”", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"할아버지가 공증인을 원하세요.\""], ["At this strange and unexpected demand M. de Villefort and his father exchanged looks.", "이 기이하고 뜻밖의 요구에, 빌포르 씨와 그의 아버지가 서로 눈길을 주고받았다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇다.\""], ["motioned the latter, with a firmness which seemed to declare that with the help of Valentine and his old servant, who both knew what his wishes were, he was quite prepared to maintain the contest.", "노인이 신호했다. 자신의 바람을 다 아는 발랑틴과 늙은 하인의 도움이 있으니 이 싸움을 끝까지 밀고 나갈 채비가 되어 있다고 선언하는 듯한 단호함이었다."]], [["“Do you wish for a notary?”", "\"공증인을 원하십니까?\" 빌포르가 물었다."], ["asked Villefort.", "\"그렇다.\""]], [["“Yes.” “What to do?”", "\"무엇 하시려고요?\""]], [["Noirtier made no answer.", "누아르티에는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."]], [["“What do you want with a notary?”", "\"공증인을 불러 무얼 하시려는 겁니까?\""], ["again repeated Villefort.", "빌포르가 다시 되물었다."], ["The invalid’s eye remained fixed, by which expression he intended to intimate that his resolution was unalterable.", "환자의 눈은 못 박힌 듯 움직이지 않았다. 그 표정으로 자신의 결심은 바꿀 수 없다는 뜻을 알리려는 것이었다."]], [["“Is it to do us some ill turn?", "\"우리에게 해코지라도 하시려고요?"], ["Do you think it is worth while?”", "그럴 만한 가치가 있다고 보십니까?\""], ["said Villefort.", "빌포르가 말했다."]], [["“Still,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["said Barrois, with the freedom and fidelity of an old servant, “if M.", "늙은 하인다운 거리낌 없는 충직함으로 바루아가 말했다."], ["Noirtier asks for a notary, I suppose he really wishes for a notary; therefore I shall go at once and fetch one.”", "\"누아르티에 어른께서 공증인을 청하신다면, 정말로 공증인을 원하시는 거겠지요. 그러니 제가 당장 가서 한 분 모셔 오겠습니다.\""], ["Barrois acknowledged no master but Noirtier, and never allowed his desires in any way to be contradicted. 30171m", "바루아는 누아르티에 말고는 어떤 주인도 인정하지 않았고, 그의 바람이 어떤 식으로든 거스러지는 것을 결코 용납하지 않았다."]], [["“Yes, I do want a notary,”", "\"그래, 나는 공증인을 원한다.\""], ["motioned the old man, shutting his eyes with a look of defiance, which seemed to say, “and I should like to see the person who dares to refuse my request.”", "노인이 도전적인 눈빛으로 눈을 감으며 신호했다. 그 눈빛은 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"내 청을 감히 거절하는 자가 있다면 어디 한번 보고 싶구나.\""]], [["“You shall have a notary, as you absolutely wish for one, sir,”", "\"정 그토록 원하신다니 공증인을 들이겠습니다, 아버지.\" 빌포르가 말했다."], ["said Villefort; “but I shall explain to him your state of health, and make excuses for you, for the scene cannot fail of being a most ridiculous one.”", "\"다만 제가 그 사람에게 아버지의 건강 상태를 설명하고, 아버지를 위해 양해를 구해야겠지요. 이 일은 더없이 우스꽝스러운 광경이 될 게 뻔하니까요.\""]], [["“Never mind that,”", "\"그건 상관없습니다.\""], ["said Barrois; “I shall go and fetch a notary, nevertheless.”", "바루아가 말했다. \"어쨌든 저는 가서 공증인을 모셔 오겠습니다.\""], ["And the old servant departed triumphantly on his mission.", "그러고는 늙은 하인이 의기양양하게 제 임무를 띠고 떠났다."]], [["# Chapter 59. The Will", "# Chapter 59. The Will"]], [["As soon as Barrois had left the room, Noirtier looked at Valentine with a malicious expression that said many things.", "바루아가 방을 나서자마자 누아르티에는 발랑틴을 바라보았다. 그 눈빛에는 많은 것을 담은 짓궂은 기색이 어려 있었다."], ["The young girl perfectly understood the look, and so did Villefort, for his countenance became clouded, and he knitted his eyebrows angrily.", "처녀는 그 눈빛을 완벽히 알아들었고, 빌포르 역시 마찬가지였다. 그의 얼굴이 흐려지고, 화가 난 듯 눈썹이 찌푸려졌다."], ["He took a seat, and quietly awaited the arrival of the notary.", "그는 자리에 앉아 공증인이 오기를 조용히 기다렸다."], ["Noirtier saw him seat himself with an appearance of perfect indifference, at the same time giving a side look at Valentine, which made her understand that she also was to remain in the room.", "누아르티에는 그가 더없이 무심한 태도로 앉는 모습을 지켜보았고, 동시에 발랑틴에게 곁눈질을 보내 그녀 또한 방에 남아 있어야 한다는 것을 알렸다."], ["Three-quarters of an hour after, Barrois returned, bringing the notary with him.", "사십오 분 뒤, 바루아가 공증인을 데리고 돌아왔다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"선생,\""], ["said Villefort, after the first salutations were over, “you were sent for by M. Noirtier, whom you see here.", "첫인사를 나눈 뒤 빌포르가 말했다. \"여기 보시는 누아르티에 씨께서 선생을 모셔 오라 하셨습니다."], ["All his limbs have become completely paralysed, he has lost his voice also, and we ourselves find much trouble in endeavoring to catch some fragments of his meaning.”", "사지가 완전히 마비되셨고, 목소리마저 잃으셨지요. 그래서 저희도 그 뜻을 단편적으로나마 알아내려고 무던히 애를 쓰고 있습니다.\""]], [["Noirtier cast an appealing look on Valentine, which look was at once so earnest and imperative, that she answered immediately.", "누아르티에가 발랑틴에게 간곡한 눈빛을 던졌다. 그 눈빛이 어찌나 진지하고도 위엄에 차 있었던지, 그녀는 곧바로 대답했다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"선생님,\""], ["said she, “I perfectly understand my grandfather’s meaning at all times.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"저는 언제든 할아버지의 뜻을 완벽하게 이해합니다.\""]], [["“That is quite true,”", "\"정말 그렇습니다.\""], ["said Barrois; “and that is what I told the gentleman as we walked along.”", "바루아가 말했다. \"오는 길에도 제가 이 선생께 그 말씀을 드렸습지요.\""]], [["“Permit me,”", "\"잠시만요.\""], ["said the notary, turning first to Villefort and then to Valentine—“permit me to state that the case in question is just one of those in which a public officer like myself cannot proceed to act without thereby incurring a dangerous responsibility.", "공증인이 빌포르를 향했다가 다시 발랑틴을 향하며 말했다. \"이 사안은, 저 같은 공직자가 자칫 위험한 책임을 떠안지 않고서는 함부로 처리할 수 없는 부류의 일임을 말씀드려야겠습니다."], ["The first thing necessary to render an act valid is, that the notary should be thoroughly convinced that he has faithfully interpreted the will and wishes of the person dictating the act.", "어떤 행위를 유효하게 만들려면, 무엇보다 공증인이 그 행위를 구술하는 사람의 의지와 소망을 충실하게 옮겼다는 확신을 가져야 합니다."], ["Now I cannot be sure of the approbation or disapprobation of a client who cannot speak, and as the object of his desire or his repugnance cannot be clearly proved to me, on account of his want of speech, my services here would be quite useless, and cannot be legally exercised.”", "그런데 저로서는 말을 못 하는 의뢰인의 동의나 부동의를 확신할 수가 없습니다. 말을 못 하시니, 그분이 무엇을 바라거나 꺼리는지 제게 명확히 입증될 수가 없습니다. 그러니 이곳에서 제가 할 일은 전혀 쓸모가 없고, 법적으로도 행사될 수 없습니다.\""]], [["The notary then prepared to retire.", "그러고서 공증인은 물러갈 채비를 했다."], ["An imperceptible smile of triumph was expressed on the lips of the procureur.", "검사의 입가에 알아챌 듯 말 듯한 승리의 미소가 떠올랐다."], ["Noirtier looked at Valentine with an expression so full of grief, that she arrested the departure of the notary.", "누아르티에는 어찌나 슬픔이 가득한 표정으로 발랑틴을 바라보았던지, 그녀는 떠나려는 공증인을 붙들었다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"선생님,\""], ["said she, “the language which I speak with my grandfather may be easily learnt, and I can teach you in a few minutes, to understand it almost as well as I can myself.", "그녀가 말했다. \"제가 할아버지와 나누는 말은 쉽게 배우실 수 있습니다. 몇 분이면 선생님께서도 저만큼 이해하시도록 가르쳐 드릴 수 있어요."], ["Will you tell me what you require, in order to set your conscience quite at ease on the subject?”", "이 일에 대해 양심을 완전히 놓으시려면 무엇이 필요한지 말씀해 주시겠어요?\""]], [["“In order to render an act valid, I must be certain of the approbation or disapprobation of my client.", "\"어떤 행위를 유효하게 하려면, 의뢰인의 동의나 부동의를 확신해야 합니다."], ["Illness of body would not affect the validity of the deed, but sanity of mind is absolutely requisite.”", "몸이 병들었다고 해서 증서의 효력이 손상되지는 않습니다만, 정신의 온전함은 절대적으로 필요한 조건이지요.\""]], [["“Well, sir, by the help of two signs, with which I will acquaint you presently, you may ascertain with perfect certainty that my grandfather is still in the full possession of all his mental faculties. M.", "\"그럼 선생님, 제가 지금 알려 드릴 두 가지 신호의 도움을 받으시면, 제 할아버지께서 여전히 모든 정신적 능력을 온전히 갖추고 계시다는 것을 완벽하게 확인하실 수 있습니다."], ["Noirtier, being deprived of voice and motion, is accustomed to convey his meaning by closing his eyes when he wishes to signify ‘yes,’ and to wink when he means ‘no.’ You now know quite enough to enable you to converse with M.", "누아르티에 씨는 목소리도 움직임도 잃으신 터라, '예'라고 하실 때는 눈을 감으시고, '아니오'라고 하실 때는 눈을 깜박이셔서 뜻을 전하십니다. 이제 누아르티에 씨와 대화하시기에 충분히 아셨을 거예요."], ["Noirtier;—try.”", "한번 해 보세요.\""]], [["Noirtier gave Valentine such a look of tenderness and gratitude that it was comprehended even by the notary himself.", "누아르티에가 발랑틴에게 어찌나 다정하고 고마운 눈빛을 보냈던지, 공증인마저 그 뜻을 알아차렸다."]], [["“You have heard and understood what your granddaughter has been saying, sir, have you?”", "\"손녀분께서 하신 말씀을 들으시고 이해하셨습니까, 선생님?\""], ["asked the notary.", "공증인이 물었다."], ["Noirtier closed his eyes.", "누아르티에가 눈을 감았다."]], [["“And you approve of what she said—that is to say, you declare that the signs which she mentioned are really those by means of which you are accustomed to convey your thoughts?”", "\"그리고 손녀분의 말씀에 동의하십니까? 다시 말해, 손녀분이 말씀하신 그 신호가 정녕 선생님께서 평소에 생각을 전하시는 신호가 맞다고 선언하시는 겁니까?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"예.\""]], [["“It was you who sent for me?”", "\"저를 부르신 게 선생님이십니까?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"예.\""]], [["“To make your will?”", "\"유언장을 작성하시려고요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"예.\""]], [["“And you do not wish me to go away without fulfilling your original intentions?”", "\"그러니 제가 본래의 뜻을 이행하지 않고 떠나는 것을 바라지 않으시는 겁니까?\""], ["The old man winked violently.", "노인이 세차게 눈을 깜박였다."]], [["“Well, sir,”", "\"자, 선생님,\""], ["said the young girl, “do you understand now, and is your conscience perfectly at rest on the subject?”", "처녀가 말했다. \"이제 이해가 되셨나요? 이 일에 대해 양심이 완전히 편안해지셨고요?\""]], [["But before the notary could answer, Villefort had drawn him aside.", "그러나 공증인이 대답하기도 전에, 빌포르가 그를 한쪽으로 끌어당겼다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"선생,\""], ["said he, “do you suppose for a moment that a man can sustain a physical shock, such as M. Noirtier has received, without any detriment to his mental faculties?”", "그가 말했다. \"누아르티에 씨가 입은 것과 같은 육체적 충격을 받은 사람이, 정신적 능력에 아무런 손상 없이 그것을 견뎌 낼 수 있다고 잠시라도 생각하십니까?\""]], [["“It is not exactly that, sir,”", "\"제 불안의 까닭은 정확히 그것이 아닙니다, 선생.\""], ["said the notary, “which makes me uneasy, but the difficulty will be in wording his thoughts and intentions, so as to be able to get his answers.”", "공증인이 말했다. \"다만 어려움은, 답변을 얻어 낼 수 있도록 그분의 생각과 의도를 말로 옮기는 데 있겠지요.\""]], [["“You must see that to be an utter impossibility,”", "\"그게 전혀 불가능한 일이라는 걸 아셔야 합니다.\""], ["said Villefort.", "빌포르가 말했다."], ["Valentine and the old man heard this conversation, and Noirtier fixed his eye so earnestly on Valentine that she felt bound to answer to the look.", "발랑틴과 노인은 이 대화를 들었고, 누아르티에가 어찌나 간절히 발랑틴을 응시했던지 그녀는 그 눈빛에 응답하지 않을 수 없다고 느꼈다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"선생님,\""], ["said she, “that need not make you uneasy, however difficult it may at first sight appear to be.", "그녀가 말했다. \"그 점은 언뜻 아무리 어려워 보이더라도 불안해하실 필요가 없습니다."], ["I can discover and explain to you my grandfather’s thoughts, so as to put an end to all your doubts and fears on the subject.", "제가 할아버지의 생각을 알아내어 설명해 드릴 수 있으니, 이 일에 대한 모든 의심과 두려움을 끝낼 수 있을 거예요."], ["I have now been six years with M. Noirtier, and let him tell you if ever once, during that time, he has entertained a thought which he was unable to make me understand.”", "저는 누아르티에 씨와 함께 지낸 지 벌써 육 년이 되었어요. 그동안 단 한 번이라도 제게 이해시키지 못한 생각을 품으신 적이 있는지, 할아버지께 여쭤보세요.\""]], [["“No,” signed the old man.", "\"없다.\" 노인이 신호했다."]], [["“Let us try what we can do, then,”", "\"그렇다면 어디 한번 해 봅시다.\""], ["said the notary.", "공증인이 말했다."], ["“You accept this young lady as your interpreter, M. Noirtier?”", "\"이 아가씨를 통역자로 받아들이시겠습니까, 누아르티에 씨?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"예.\""]], [["“Well, sir, what do you require of me, and what document is it that you wish to be drawn up?”", "\"좋습니다, 선생님. 제게 무엇을 바라시며, 어떤 문서를 작성하길 원하십니까?\""]], [["Valentine named all the letters of the alphabet until she came to W.", "발랑틴이 알파벳의 모든 글자를 차례로 불렀다."], ["At this letter the eloquent eye of Noirtier gave her notice that she was to stop.", "W에 이르렀을 때, 누아르티에의 말 많은 눈이 멈추라는 신호를 보냈다."]], [["“It is very evident that it is the letter W which M. Noirtier wants,”", "\"누아르티에 씨가 원하시는 게 W라는 글자임은 아주 분명하군요.\""], ["said the notary.", "공증인이 말했다."]], [["“Wait,” said Valentine; and, turning to her grandfather, she repeated, “Wa—We—Wi——”", "\"기다려 보세요.\" 발랑틴이 말하고는 할아버지를 향해 되풀이했다. \"Wa…… We……"], ["The old man stopped her at the last syllable.", "Wi…….\" 노인이 마지막 음절에서 그녀를 멈춰 세웠다."], ["Valentine then took the dictionary, and the notary watched her while she turned over the pages.", "그러자 발랑틴은 사전을 집어 들었고, 공증인은 그녀가 책장을 넘기는 모습을 지켜보았다."]], [["She passed her finger slowly down the columns, and when she came to the word “Will,” M. Noirtier’s eye bade her stop.", "그녀가 손가락을 단(段)을 따라 천천히 내려가다가 'Will(유언)'이라는 단어에 이르자, 누아르티에의 눈이 멈추라고 일렀다."]], [["“Will,”", "\"유언.\""], ["said the notary; “it is very evident that M. Noirtier is desirous of making his will.”", "공증인이 말했다. \"누아르티에 씨가 유언장을 작성하고 싶어 하시는 게 아주 분명하군요.\""]], [["“Yes, yes, yes,”", "\"예, 예, 예.\""], ["motioned the invalid.", "환자가 신호했다."]], [["“Really, sir, you must allow that this is most extraordinary,”", "\"정말이지, 선생, 이건 더없이 비범한 일이라는 걸 인정하셔야겠습니다.\""], ["said the astonished notary, turning to M. de Villefort.", "놀란 공증인이 빌포르 씨를 향해 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇군요.\""], ["said the procureur, “and I think the will promises to be yet more extraordinary, for I cannot see how it is to be drawn up without the intervention of Valentine, and she may, perhaps, be considered as too much interested in its contents to allow of her being a suitable interpreter of the obscure and ill-defined wishes of her grandfather.”", "검사가 말했다. \"그리고 이 유언장은 앞으로 한층 더 비범해질 것 같습니다. 발랑틴이 끼어들지 않고서는 도무지 작성될 수가 없는데, 그 아이는 어쩌면 유언 내용에 너무 깊이 얽힌 당사자라, 모호하고 불분명한 할아버지의 뜻을 통역하기에 적합한 사람이라고 보기 어려울 테니까요.\""]], [["“No, no, no,” replied the eye of the paralytic.", "\"아니, 아니, 아니.\" 중풍 환자의 눈이 대답했다."]], [["“What?”", "\"뭐라고요?\""], ["said Villefort, “do you mean to say that Valentine is not interested in your will?”", "빌포르가 말했다. \"발랑틴이 아버지의 유언과 아무 이해관계가 없다는 말씀이십니까?\" \"아니다.\""]], [["“No.” “Sir,”", "\"선생님,\""], ["said the notary, whose interest had been greatly excited, and who had resolved on publishing far and wide the account of this extraordinary and picturesque scene, “what appeared so impossible to me an hour ago, has now become quite easy and practicable, and this may be a perfectly valid will, provided it be read in the presence of seven witnesses, approved by the testator, and sealed by the notary in the presence of the witnesses.", "공증인이 말했다. 그는 흥미가 잔뜩 동했고, 이 비범하고도 그림 같은 장면을 널리 알리리라 마음먹은 터였다. \"한 시간 전만 해도 제게 그토록 불가능해 보이던 일이, 이제는 아주 쉽고 실행 가능한 일이 되었군요."], ["As to the time, it will not require very much more than the generality of wills. There are certain forms necessary to be gone through, and which are always the same.", "그리고 이 유언장은 일곱 명의 증인 앞에서 낭독되고, 유언자의 승인을 받고, 증인들이 보는 앞에서 공증인이 봉인한다면 완벽하게 유효한 유언장이 될 수 있습니다. 시간으로 말하면, 보통의 유언장보다 그리 더 걸리지도 않을 겁니다."], ["As to the details, the greater part will be furnished afterwards by the state in which we find the affairs of the testator, and by yourself, who, having had the management of them, can doubtless give full information on the subject.", "반드시 거쳐야 하는 형식이 몇 가지 있는데, 그건 늘 똑같지요. 세부 사항으로 말하면, 그 대부분은 유언자의 재산 상태를 통해, 또 그 재산을 관리해 오신 만큼 분명 충분한 정보를 주실 수 있는 선생을 통해 나중에 채워질 겁니다."], ["But besides all this, in order that the instrument may not be contested, I am anxious to give it the greatest possible authenticity, therefore, one of my colleagues will help me, and, contrary to custom, will assist in the dictation of the testament.", "다만 이 모든 것에 더해, 이 증서가 이의 제기를 받지 않도록 저는 가능한 한 최대의 진정성을 부여하고 싶습니다. 그러니 제 동료 한 사람이 저를 도울 것이고, 관례와는 달리 유언 구술에도 참여할 것입니다."], ["Are you satisfied, sir?”", "만족하십니까, 선생님?\""], ["continued the notary, addressing the old man.", "공증인이 노인을 향해 말을 이었다."]], [["30175m\n“Yes,”", "\"예.\""], ["looked the invalid, his eye beaming with delight at the ready interpretation of his meaning.", "환자의 눈빛이 말했다. 자신의 뜻이 척척 통역되는 것에 그의 눈이 기쁨으로 빛났다."]], [["“What is he going to do?”", "'아버지가 무슨 일을 하려는 거지?'"], ["thought Villefort, whose position demanded much reserve, but who was longing to know what his father’s intentions were.", "빌포르는 생각했다. 그의 처지로서는 신중을 지켜야 했지만, 아버지의 의도가 무엇인지 몹시 알고 싶었다."], ["He left the room to give orders for another notary to be sent, but Barrois, who had heard all that passed, had guessed his master’s wishes, and had already gone to fetch one.", "그는 또 다른 공증인을 부르라는 지시를 내리러 방을 나섰다. 그러나 그동안의 일을 모두 들은 바루아는 주인의 뜻을 짐작하고 이미 한 사람을 데리러 떠난 뒤였다."], ["The procureur then told his wife to come up.", "그러자 검사는 아내에게 올라오라고 일렀다."], ["In the course of a quarter of an hour everyone had assembled in the chamber of the paralytic; the second notary had also arrived.", "십오 분쯤 지나자 모두가 중풍 환자의 방에 모였고, 두 번째 공증인도 도착했다."]], [["A few words sufficed for a mutual understanding between the two officers of the law.", "두 법률가가 서로 뜻을 맞추는 데는 몇 마디면 충분했다."], ["They read to Noirtier the formal copy of a will, in order to give him an idea of the terms in which such documents are generally couched; then, in order to test the capacity of the testator, the first notary said, turning towards him:", "그들은 그런 문서가 일반적으로 어떤 문구로 작성되는지 누아르티에에게 보여 주려고 유언장의 정식 사본 한 부를 읽어 주었다. 그런 다음, 유언자의 능력을 시험하려고 첫 번째 공증인이 그를 향해 돌아서며 말했다."]], [["“When an individual makes his will, it is generally in favor or in prejudice of some person.”", "\"사람이 유언을 할 때는, 대개 누군가에게 유리하게 하거나 불리하게 하기 마련이지요.\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"예.\""]], [["“Have you an exact idea of the amount of your fortune?”", "\"선생님께서는 재산의 액수를 정확히 알고 계십니까?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"예.\""]], [["“I will name to you several sums which will increase by gradation; you will stop me when I reach the one representing the amount of your own possessions?”", "\"제가 차츰 액수를 늘려 가며 여러 금액을 부르겠습니다. 선생님 재산의 총액에 해당하는 금액에 이르면 저를 멈추십시오.\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"예.\""]], [["There was a kind of solemnity in this interrogation.", "이 질문에는 일종의 엄숙함이 깃들어 있었다."], ["Never had the struggle between mind and matter been more apparent than now, and if it was not a sublime, it was, at least, a curious spectacle.", "정신과 물질의 싸움이 지금보다 더 또렷이 드러난 적은 없었으니, 숭고하지는 않을지언정 적어도 진기한 광경이었다."], ["They had formed a circle round the invalid; the second notary was sitting at a table, prepared for writing, and his colleague was standing before the testator in the act of interrogating him on the subject to which we have alluded.", "사람들이 환자를 둘러싸고 원을 이루었다. 두 번째 공증인은 글을 쓰려고 채비한 채 탁자에 앉았고, 그의 동료는 유언자 앞에 서서 앞서 말한 사안에 관해 그에게 질문을 던지고 있었다."]], [["“Your fortune exceeds 300,000 francs, does it not?” asked he.", "\"선생님 재산은 30만 프랑을 넘지요?\" 그가 물었다."], ["Noirtier made a sign that it did.", "누아르티에가 그렇다는 신호를 보냈다."]], [["“Do you possess 400,000 francs?”", "\"40만 프랑을 가지고 계십니까?\""], ["inquired the notary.", "공증인이 물었다."], ["Noirtier’s eye remained immovable.", "누아르티에의 눈은 움직이지 않았다."]], [["“500,000?” The same expression continued.", "\"50만 프랑은요?\" 같은 표정이 계속되었다."]], [["“600,000—700,000—800,000—900,000?”", "\"60만…… 70만…… 80만…… 90만?\""]], [["Noirtier stopped him at the last-named sum.", "누아르티에가 마지막으로 부른 금액에서 그를 멈추었다."]], [["“You are then in possession of 900,000 francs?”", "\"그러면 선생님께서는 90만 프랑을 가지고 계신 겁니까?\""], ["asked the notary.", "공증인이 물었다."]], [["“Yes.”", "\"예.\""]], [["“In landed property?”", "\"부동산으로요?\" \"아니오.\""]], [["“No.” “In stock?”", "\"증권으로요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"예.\""]], [["“The stock is in your own hands?”", "\"그 증권을 직접 보관하고 계십니까?\""]], [["The look which M. Noirtier cast on Barrois showed that there was something wanting which he knew where to find.", "누아르티에가 바루아에게 던진 눈빛은, 자신이 어디에 있는지 아는 무언가가 빠져 있다는 것을 보여 주었다."], ["The old servant left the room, and presently returned, bringing with him a small casket.", "늙은 하인이 방을 나갔다가 곧 작은 함을 들고 돌아왔다."]], [["“Do you permit us to open this casket?”", "\"이 함을 열어도 되겠습니까?\""], ["asked the notary.", "공증인이 물었다."], ["Noirtier gave his assent.", "누아르티에가 동의했다."]], [["They opened it, and found 900,000 francs in bank scrip. The first notary handed over each note, as he examined it, to his colleague.", "그들이 함을 열자 90만 프랑어치의 은행 증권이 들어 있었다. 첫 번째 공증인은 한 장씩 살펴본 뒤 동료에게 건넸다."]], [["The total amount was found to be as M. Noirtier had stated.", "총액은 누아르티에가 말한 그대로였다."]], [["“It is all as he has said; it is very evident that the mind still retains its full force and vigor.”", "\"모든 게 말씀하신 대로군요. 정신이 여전히 온전한 힘과 활기를 지니고 있음이 아주 분명합니다.\""], ["Then, turning towards the paralytic, he said, “You possess, then, 900,000 francs of capital, which, according to the manner in which you have invested it, ought to bring in an income of about 40,000 livres?”", "그러고는 중풍 환자를 향해 말했다. \"그러니까 선생님께서는 90만 프랑의 자본을 가지고 계시고, 투자 방식에 따라 연 4만 리브르가량의 수입을 가져올 텐데요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"예.\""]], [["“To whom do you desire to leave this fortune?”", "\"이 재산을 누구에게 남기고 싶으십니까?\""]], [["“Oh!”", "\"오!\""], ["said Madame de Villefort, “there is not much doubt on that subject. M.", "빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"그 점은 별로 의심할 게 없지요."], ["Noirtier tenderly loves his granddaughter, Mademoiselle de Villefort; it is she who has nursed and tended him for six years, and has, by her devoted attention, fully secured the affection, I had almost said the gratitude, of her grandfather, and it is but just that she should reap the fruit of her devotion.”", "누아르티에 씨는 손녀이신 빌포르 양을 끔찍이 사랑하십니다. 육 년 동안 그분을 간호하고 보살핀 사람이 바로 그 아이고, 그 헌신적인 정성으로 할아버지의 애정을, 거의 감사라고 할 만한 마음까지 온전히 얻었으니, 그 헌신의 열매를 거두는 것이 마땅하지요.\""]], [["The eye of Noirtier clearly showed by its expression that he was not deceived by the false assent given by Madame de Villefort’s words and manner to the motives which she supposed him to entertain.", "누아르티에의 눈은, 자신이 품었으리라고 빌포르 부인이 짐작한 동기에 대해 그녀의 말과 태도가 보낸 거짓 동의에 자신이 속지 않았음을 표정으로 분명히 드러냈다."]], [["“Is it, then, to Mademoiselle Valentine de Villefort that you leave these 900,000 francs?”", "\"그러면 이 90만 프랑을 발랑틴 드 빌포르 양에게 남기시는 겁니까?\""], ["demanded the notary, thinking he had only to insert this clause, but waiting first for the assent of Noirtier, which it was necessary should be given before all the witnesses of this singular scene.", "공증인이 물었다. 그는 이 조항만 써넣으면 되리라 여겼지만, 우선 누아르티에의 동의를 기다렸다. 그 동의는 이 기이한 장면의 모든 증인 앞에서 반드시 표해져야 했다."]], [["Valentine, when her name was made the subject of discussion, had stepped back, to escape unpleasant observation; her eyes were cast down, and she was crying.", "발랑틴은 자기 이름이 논의의 대상이 되자, 불편한 시선을 피하려고 뒤로 물러서 있었다. 두 눈을 내리깐 채 울고 있었다."], ["The old man looked at her for an instant with an expression of the deepest tenderness, then, turning towards the notary, he significantly winked his eye in token of dissent.", "노인이 한순간 깊디깊은 다정함이 어린 표정으로 그녀를 바라보더니, 공증인을 향해 돌아서서 반대의 표시로 의미심장하게 눈을 깜박였다."]], [["“What,”", "\"뭐라고요.\""], ["said the notary, “do you not intend making Mademoiselle Valentine de Villefort your residuary legatee?”", "공증인이 말했다. \"발랑틴 드 빌포르 양을 잔여 재산 수령인으로 삼지 않으시겠다는 겁니까?\" \"아니오.\""]], [["“No.” “You are not making any mistake, are you?”", "\"착오는 없으신 거지요?\" 공증인이 말했다."], ["said the notary; “you really mean to declare that such is not your intention?”", "\"정말로 그것이 선생님의 뜻이 아니라고 선언하시는 겁니까?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니오.\""], ["repeated Noirtier; “No.”", "누아르티에가 되풀이했다. \"아니오.\""]], [["Valentine raised her head, struck dumb with astonishment.", "발랑틴이 고개를 들었다. 놀라움에 말문이 막혔다."], ["It was not so much the conviction that she was disinherited that caused her grief, but her total inability to account for the feelings which had provoked her grandfather to such an act.", "그녀를 슬프게 한 것은 상속에서 제외되었다는 사실 자체가 아니라, 할아버지를 그런 행동으로 몰아간 감정을 도무지 헤아릴 수 없다는 점이었다."], ["But Noirtier looked at her with so much affectionate tenderness that she exclaimed:", "그러나 누아르티에가 어찌나 애정 어린 다정함으로 그녀를 바라보았던지, 그녀는 소리쳤다."]], [["“Oh, grandpapa, I see now that it is only your fortune of which you deprive me; you still leave me the love which I have always enjoyed.”", "\"오, 할아버지, 이제 알겠어요. 제게서 거두시는 건 오직 재산뿐이군요. 제가 늘 누려 온 사랑은 여전히 남겨 주시는 거예요.\""]], [["“Ah, yes, most assuredly,”", "\"아, 그렇다, 정녕 그렇다.\""], ["said the eyes of the paralytic, for he closed them with an expression which Valentine could not mistake.", "중풍 환자의 눈이 말했다. 그가 발랑틴이 오해할 수 없는 표정으로 눈을 감았기 때문이다."]], [["“Thank you, thank you,” murmured she.", "\"고맙습니다, 고맙습니다.\""], ["The old man’s declaration that Valentine was not the destined inheritor of his fortune had excited the hopes of Madame de Villefort; she gradually approached the invalid, and said:", "그녀가 속삭였다. 발랑틴이 자기 재산의 정해진 상속인이 아니라는 노인의 선언은 빌포르 부인의 기대를 부추겼다. 그녀는 슬며시 환자에게 다가가 말했다."]], [["“Then, doubtless, dear M. Noirtier, you intend leaving your fortune to your grandson, Edward de Villefort?”", "\"그렇다면, 분명, 친애하는 누아르티에 씨, 재산을 손자이신 에두아르 드 빌포르에게 남기실 생각이시군요?\""]], [["The winking of the eyes which answered this speech was most decided and terrible, and expressed a feeling almost amounting to hatred.", "이 말에 응답한 눈 깜박임은 더없이 단호하고도 끔찍했으며, 거의 증오에 가까운 감정을 드러냈다."]], [["“No?”", "\"아니라고요?\""], ["said the notary; “then, perhaps, it is to your son, M. de Villefort?”", "공증인이 말했다. \"그러면, 어쩌면 아드님이신 빌포르 씨에게요?\""]], [["“No.” The two notaries looked at each other in mute astonishment and inquiry as to what were the real intentions of the testator.", "\"아니오.\" 두 공증인이 유언자의 진짜 의도가 무엇인지 묻듯, 말없는 놀라움 속에 서로를 마주 보았다."], ["Villefort and his wife both grew red, one from shame, the other from anger.", "빌포르와 그의 아내는 둘 다 얼굴이 붉어졌다. 한쪽은 수치심으로, 다른 한쪽은 분노로."]], [["“What have we all done, then, dear grandpapa?”", "\"그럼 저희 모두가 무슨 잘못을 한 건가요, 할아버지?\""], ["said Valentine; “you no longer seem to love any of us?”", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"이제 저희 누구도 사랑하지 않으시는 것 같아요.\""]], [["The old man’s eyes passed rapidly from Villefort and his wife, and rested on Valentine with a look of unutterable fondness.", "노인의 눈이 빌포르와 그의 아내를 빠르게 훑고 지나가더니, 형언할 수 없는 애정의 눈빛으로 발랑틴에게 머물렀다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"그럼,\""], ["said she; “if you love me, grandpapa, try and bring that love to bear upon your actions at this present moment.", "그녀가 말했다. \"할아버지, 저를 사랑하신다면, 지금 이 순간 그 사랑을 행동에 옮겨 보세요."], ["You know me well enough to be quite sure that I have never thought of your fortune; besides, they say I am already rich in right of my mother—too rich, even.", "제가 할아버지 재산을 한 번도 생각해 본 적 없다는 건 잘 아시잖아요. 게다가 사람들은 제가 어머니 쪽으로 이미 부자라고, 지나치게 부자라고까지 하는걸요."], ["Explain yourself, then.”", "그러니 뜻을 밝혀 주세요.\""]], [["Noirtier fixed his intelligent eyes on Valentine’s hand.", "누아르티에가 영민한 눈을 발랑틴의 손에 고정했다."]], [["“My hand?”", "\"제 손이요?\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Yes.”", "\"예.\""]], [["“Her hand!” exclaimed everyone.", "\"손이라니!\" 모두가 외쳤다."]], [["“Oh, gentlemen, you see it is all useless, and that my father’s mind is really impaired,”", "\"오, 여러분, 보시다시피 다 소용없는 일이고, 제 아버지의 정신이 정말로 손상된 겁니다.\""], ["said Villefort.", "빌포르가 말했다."]], [["“Ah,” cried Valentine suddenly, “I understand.", "\"아.\" 발랑틴이 불쑥 외쳤다."], ["It is my marriage you mean, is it not, dear grandpapa?”", "\"알겠어요. 제 결혼을 말씀하시는 거죠, 할아버지?\""]], [["“Yes, yes, yes,”", "\"예, 예, 예.\""], ["signed the paralytic, casting on Valentine a look of joyful gratitude for having guessed his meaning. 30179m", "중풍 환자가 신호했다. 자기 뜻을 알아맞힌 발랑틴에게 기쁘고 고마운 눈빛을 던지며."]], [["“You are angry with us all on account of this marriage, are you not?”", "\"이 결혼 때문에 저희 모두에게 화가 나신 거죠, 그렇죠?\""]], [["“Yes?”", "\"예.\""]], [["“Really, this is too absurd,”", "\"정말이지, 이건 너무 터무니없군요.\""], ["said Villefort.", "빌포르가 말했다."]], [["“Excuse me, sir,”", "\"실례합니다, 선생.\""], ["replied the notary; “on the contrary, the meaning of M.", "공증인이 대꾸했다. \"오히려 누아르티에 씨의 뜻은 제게 아주 분명합니다."], ["Noirtier is quite evident to me, and I can quite easily connect the train of ideas passing in his mind.”", "그분 머릿속을 스쳐 가는 생각의 흐름을 저는 어렵지 않게 이을 수 있습니다.\""]], [["“You do not wish me to marry M. Franz d’Épinay?”", "\"제가 프란츠 데피네 씨와 결혼하길 원치 않으시는 거죠?\""], ["observed Valentine.", "발랑틴이 말했다."]], [["“I do not wish it,”", "\"원치 않는다.\""], ["said the eye of her grandfather.", "할아버지의 눈이 말했다."]], [["“And you disinherit your granddaughter,”", "\"그래서 손녀분의 상속권을 박탈하시는 겁니까?\""], ["continued the notary, “because she has contracted an engagement contrary to your wishes?”", "공증인이 말을 이었다. \"선생님의 뜻에 어긋나는 혼약을 맺었다는 이유로요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"예.\""]], [["“So that, but for this marriage, she would have been your heir?”", "\"그러니까, 이 결혼만 아니었다면 손녀분이 상속인이 되었을 거란 말씀이군요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"예.\""]], [["There was a profound silence.", "깊은 침묵이 흘렀다."], ["The two notaries were holding a consultation as to the best means of proceeding with the affair.", "두 공증인은 이 일을 어떻게 진행하는 게 최선일지 의논하고 있었다."], ["Valentine was looking at her grandfather with a smile of intense gratitude, and Villefort was biting his lips with vexation, while Madame de Villefort could not succeed in repressing an inward feeling of joy, which, in spite of herself, appeared in her whole countenance.", "발랑틴은 더없이 고마운 미소를 띠고 할아버지를 바라보았고, 빌포르는 분에 받쳐 입술을 깨물고 있었으며, 빌포르 부인은 자기도 모르게 온 얼굴에 드러나는 내심의 기쁨을 억누르지 못하고 있었다."]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["said Villefort, who was the first to break the silence, “I consider that I am the best judge of the propriety of the marriage in question.", "침묵을 가장 먼저 깬 빌포르가 말했다. \"이 결혼의 타당성을 가장 잘 판단할 사람은 저라고 봅니다."], ["I am the only person possessing the right to dispose of my daughter’s hand.", "제 딸의 혼사를 결정할 권리를 가진 사람은 오직 저뿐입니다."], ["It is my wish that she should marry M. Franz d’Épinay—and she shall marry him.”", "그 아이가 프란츠 데피네 씨와 결혼하기를 저는 바라며, 그 아이는 그와 결혼할 것입니다.\""]], [["Valentine sank weeping into a chair.", "발랑틴이 흐느끼며 의자에 주저앉았다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"선생,\""], ["said the notary, “how do you intend disposing of your fortune in case Mademoiselle de Villefort still determines on marrying M.", "공증인이 말했다. \"빌포르 양이 그래도 프란츠 씨와의 결혼을 고집한다면, 재산을 어떻게 처분하실 작정이십니까?\""], ["Franz?” The old man gave no answer.", "노인은 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."]], [["“You will, of course, dispose of it in some way or other?”", "\"물론, 어떤 식으로든 처분하시겠지요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"예.\""]], [["“In favor of some member of your family?”", "\"가족 중 누군가에게 유리하게요?\" \"아니오.\""]], [["“No.” “Do you intend devoting it to charitable purposes, then?”", "\"그렇다면 자선 목적에 바치실 생각이십니까?\""], ["pursued the notary.", "공증인이 계속 물었다."]], [["“Yes.”", "\"예.\""]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["said the notary, “you are aware that the law does not allow a son to be entirely deprived of his patrimony?”", "공증인이 말했다. \"법이 아들에게서 상속분을 통째로 박탈하는 것은 허용하지 않는다는 걸 아십니까?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"예.\""]], [["“You only intend, then, to dispose of that part of your fortune which the law allows you to subtract from the inheritance of your son?” Noirtier made no answer.", "\"그러면 법이 아드님의 상속분에서 제하도록 허용하는 부분만 처분하실 생각이십니까?\" 누아르티에는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Do you still wish to dispose of all?”", "\"그래도 전부 다 처분하길 원하십니까?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"예.\""]], [["“But they will contest the will after your death?” “No.”", "\"하지만 선생님이 돌아가신 뒤에 유언에 이의를 제기할 텐데요?\" \"아니오.\""]], [["“My father knows me,”", "\"제 아버지는 저를 아십니다.\""], ["replied Villefort; “he is quite sure that his wishes will be held sacred by me; besides, he understands that in my position I cannot plead against the poor.”", "빌포르가 말했다. \"아버지의 뜻을 제가 신성하게 받들리라 확신하고 계시지요. 게다가 제 처지로는 가난한 이들을 상대로 소송할 수 없다는 것도 아십니다.\""], ["The eye of Noirtier beamed with triumph.", "누아르티에의 눈이 승리감으로 빛났다."]], [["“What do you decide on, sir?”", "\"선생은 어떻게 결정하시겠습니까?\""], ["asked the notary of Villefort.", "공증인이 빌포르에게 물었다."]], [["“Nothing, sir; it is a resolution which my father has taken and I know he never alters his mind. I am quite resigned.", "\"아무것도 없습니다, 선생. 제 아버지가 내리신 결심이고, 한번 정한 마음을 결코 바꾸지 않으신다는 걸 저는 압니다."], ["These 900,000 francs will go out of the family in order to enrich some hospital; but it is ridiculous thus to yield to the caprices of an old man, and I shall, therefore, act according to my conscience.”", "저는 완전히 체념했습니다. 이 90만 프랑은 어느 병원을 살찌우려고 집안을 떠나겠지요. 하지만 늙은이의 변덕에 이렇게 굴복하는 건 우스운 일이니, 저는 제 양심에 따라 처신하겠습니다.\""]], [["Having said this, Villefort quitted the room with his wife, leaving his father at liberty to do as he pleased.", "이렇게 말하고 빌포르는 아내와 함께 방을 나갔고, 아버지가 마음대로 하도록 내버려 두었다."], ["The same day the will was made, the witnesses were brought, it was approved by the old man, sealed in the presence of all and given in charge to M. Deschamps, the family notary.", "그날로 유언장이 작성되었고, 증인들이 불려 왔으며, 노인의 승인을 받아 모두가 보는 앞에서 봉인된 뒤, 집안의 공증인인 데샹 씨에게 맡겨졌다."]], [["# Chapter 60. The Telegraph", "# Chapter 60. The Telegraph"]], [["M. and Madame de Villefort found on their return that the Count of Monte Cristo, who had come to visit them in their absence, had been ushered into the drawing-room, and was still awaiting them there.", "빌포르 부부가 돌아와 보니, 두 사람이 없는 사이에 찾아온 몬테크리스토 백작이 응접실로 안내되어 그곳에서 아직 그들을 기다리고 있었다."], ["Madame de Villefort, who had not yet sufficiently recovered from her late emotion to allow of her entertaining visitors so immediately, retired to her bedroom, while the procureur, who could better depend upon himself, proceeded at once to the salon.", "빌포르 부인은 조금 전의 격한 감정에서 아직 충분히 회복되지 못해 손님을 곧바로 맞을 수 없었으므로 침실로 물러났고, 좀 더 자신을 다잡을 수 있는 검사는 곧장 응접실로 향했다."]], [["Although M. de Villefort flattered himself that, to all outward view, he had completely masked the feelings which were passing in his mind, he did not know that the cloud was still lowering on his brow, so much so that the count, whose smile was radiant, immediately noticed his sombre and thoughtful air.", "빌포르는 겉으로 보기에는 마음속을 스치는 감정을 완전히 감추었다고 자부했지만, 자신의 이마에 아직도 먹구름이 드리워 있다는 것은 알지 못했다. 그것이 어찌나 또렷했던지, 환한 미소를 짓고 있던 백작은 그의 어둡고 골똘한 기색을 곧바로 알아챘다."]], [["“_Ma foi!_”", "\"이런!\""], ["said Monte Cristo, after the first compliments were over, “what is the matter with you, M. de Villefort?", "첫인사가 끝난 뒤 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"무슨 일이십니까, 빌포르 씨?"], ["Have I arrived at the moment when you were drawing up an indictment for a capital crime?”", "혹시 사형에 해당하는 죄의 기소장을 작성하시던 참에 제가 들이닥친 건가요?\""]], [["Villefort tried to smile.", "빌포르가 억지로 웃어 보였다."]], [["“No, count,”", "\"아닙니다, 백작.\""], ["he replied, “I am the only victim in this case.", "그가 대답했다. \"이번 일의 희생자는 오직 저 하나뿐이지요."], ["It is I who lose my cause, and it is ill-luck, obstinacy, and folly which have caused it to be decided against me.”", "송사에서 진 사람도 저이고, 그 일이 제게 불리하게 결판난 것도 불운과 고집과 어리석음 탓이니까요.\""]], [["“To what do you refer?”", "\"무슨 말씀이십니까?\""], ["said Monte Cristo with well-feigned interest.", "몬테크리스토가 그럴듯하게 꾸민 관심을 보이며 말했다."], ["“Have you really met with some great misfortune?”", "\"정말로 무슨 큰 불행이라도 겪으신 겁니까?\""]], [["“Oh, no, monsieur,”", "\"아, 아닙니다, 백작.\""], ["said Villefort with a bitter smile; “it is only a loss of money which I have sustained—nothing worth mentioning, I assure you.”", "빌포르가 쓴웃음을 지으며 말했다. \"그저 돈을 좀 잃은 것뿐입니다. 입에 올릴 가치도 없는 일이지요, 정말입니다.\""]], [["“True,”", "\"그렇군요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “the loss of a sum of money becomes almost immaterial with a fortune such as you possess, and to one of your philosophic spirit.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"당신처럼 큰 재산을 지닌 데다 그토록 달관한 정신을 가진 분에게는, 돈 얼마쯤 잃는 일은 거의 대수롭지 않은 것이겠지요.\""]], [["“It is not so much the loss of the money that vexes me,”", "\"저를 괴롭히는 건 돈을 잃었다는 사실 자체가 아닙니다.\" 빌포르가 말했다."], ["said Villefort, “though, after all, 900,000 francs are worth regretting; but I am the more annoyed with this fate, chance, or whatever you please to call the power which has destroyed my hopes and my fortune, and may blast the prospects of my child also, as it is all occasioned by an old man relapsed into second childhood.” 30183m", "\"물론 90만 프랑이면 아쉬워할 만한 액수이긴 하지요. 하지만 제 희망과 재산을 무너뜨리고, 어쩌면 제 자식의 앞날까지 망쳐 놓을지 모르는 이 운명, 이 우연, 아니 뭐라 부르시든 그 힘에 더 화가 나는 것은, 이 모든 것이 다시 노망이 든 늙은이 하나 때문에 벌어진 일이라는 점입니다.\""]], [["“What do you say?”", "\"뭐라고 하셨습니까?\""], ["said the count; “900,000 francs?", "백작이 말했다. \"90만 프랑이라고요?"], ["It is indeed a sum which might be regretted even by a philosopher.", "그건 정말이지 철학자라도 아쉬워할 만한 액수로군요."], ["And who is the cause of all this annoyance?”", "그런데 이 모든 골칫거리의 원인이 대체 누구입니까?\""]], [["“My father, as I told you.”", "\"제 아버지입니다, 말씀드렸다시피요.\""]], [["“M. Noirtier?", "\"누아르티에 씨 말입니까?"], ["But I thought you told me he had become entirely paralyzed, and that all his faculties were completely destroyed?”", "하지만 제 기억으로는, 그분이 완전히 마비되어 모든 기능을 잃으셨다고 하지 않으셨던가요?\""]], [["“Yes, his bodily faculties, for he can neither move nor speak, nevertheless he thinks, acts, and wills in the manner I have described.", "\"네, 신체 기능은요. 움직이지도 말하지도 못하시니까요. 그런데도 제가 말씀드린 식으로 생각하고, 행동하고, 뜻을 관철하십니다."], ["I left him about five minutes ago, and he is now occupied in dictating his will to two notaries.”", "한 오 분 전에 그 방에서 나왔는데, 지금 공증인 두 사람을 앞에 두고 유언장을 받아쓰게 하고 계십니다.\""]], [["“But to do this he must have spoken?”", "\"하지만 그러려면 말씀을 하셔야 할 텐데요?\""]], [["“He has done better than that—he has made himself understood.”", "\"그보다 더한 일을 하셨지요. 자기 뜻을 전달하셨습니다.\""]], [["“How was such a thing possible?”", "\"그런 일이 어떻게 가능합니까?\""]], [["“By the help of his eyes, which are still full of life, and, as you perceive, possess the power of inflicting mortal injury.”", "\"아직 생기로 가득한 두 눈의 힘을 빌려서지요. 보시다시피, 그 눈은 치명상을 입힐 힘까지 지니고 있습니다.\""]], [["“My dear,”", "\"여보,\""], ["said Madame de Villefort, who had just entered the room, “perhaps you exaggerate the evil.”", "막 방으로 들어선 빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"어쩌면 당신이 일을 너무 과장하는지도 몰라요.\""]], [["“Good-morning, madame,”", "\"안녕하십니까, 부인.\""], ["said the count, bowing.", "백작이 인사하며 말했다."]], [["Madame de Villefort acknowledged the salutation with one of her most gracious smiles.", "빌포르 부인이 더없이 우아한 미소로 인사를 받았다."]], [["“What is this that M. de Villefort has been telling me?”", "\"빌포르 씨가 제게 들려주신 이야기가 대체 무엇입니까?\""], ["demanded Monte Cristo “and what incomprehensible misfortune——”", "몬테크리스토가 물었다. \"그 도무지 알 수 없는 불행이라는 게……\""]], [["“Incomprehensible is the word!”", "\"알 수 없다는 말이 딱 맞지요!\""], ["interrupted the procureur, shrugging his shoulders.", "검사가 어깨를 으쓱하며 말을 가로챘다."], ["“It is an old man’s caprice!”", "\"한 노인네의 변덕일 뿐입니다!\""]], [["“And is there no means of making him revoke his decision?”", "\"그분의 결정을 번복하게 할 방법은 없습니까?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"있어요.\""], ["said Madame de Villefort; “and it is still entirely in the power of my husband to cause the will, which is now in prejudice of Valentine, to be altered in her favor.”", "빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"지금 발랑틴에게 불리하게 작성되고 있는 그 유언장을, 발랑틴에게 유리하도록 바꾸게 하는 것은 여전히 전적으로 제 남편의 손에 달려 있답니다.\""]], [["The count, who perceived that M. and Madame de Villefort were beginning to speak in parables, appeared to pay no attention to the conversation, and feigned to be busily engaged in watching Edward, who was mischievously pouring some ink into the bird’s water-glass.", "빌포르 부부가 수수께끼 같은 말투로 이야기를 시작하는 것을 알아챈 백작은, 대화에 전혀 관심이 없는 척하며 에두아르를 지켜보는 데 열중한 양 꾸몄다. 에두아르는 장난스럽게 새의 물그릇에 잉크를 따르고 있었다."]], [["“My dear,”", "\"여보,\""], ["said Villefort, in answer to his wife, “you know I have never been accustomed to play the patriarch in my family, nor have I ever considered that the fate of a universe was to be decided by my nod.", "빌포르가 아내에게 대답했다. \"내가 우리 집안에서 가장 노릇을 한 적도 없고, 내 고갯짓 하나로 온 세상의 운명이 결판난다고 여긴 적도 없다는 걸 당신도 알지 않소."], ["Nevertheless, it is necessary that my will should be respected in my family, and that the folly of an old man and the caprice of a child should not be allowed to overturn a project which I have entertained for so many years.", "그렇지만 우리 집안에서 내 뜻만큼은 존중받아야 하고, 늙은이의 망령과 아이의 변덕이 내가 그토록 오랜 세월 품어 온 계획을 뒤엎도록 내버려 둘 수는 없소."], ["The Baron d’Épinay was my friend, as you know, and an alliance with his son is the most suitable thing that could possibly be arranged.”", "데피네 남작은, 당신도 알다시피, 내 친구였소. 그의 아들과 사돈을 맺는 것은 더없이 합당한 일이오.\""]], [["“Do you think,”", "\"당신은,\""], ["said Madame de Villefort, “that Valentine is in league with him?", "빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"발랑틴이 그분과 한패라고 생각하지 않으세요?"], ["She has always been opposed to this marriage, and I should not be at all surprised if what we have just seen and heard is nothing but the execution of a plan concerted between them.”", "그 애는 늘 이 결혼을 반대해 왔잖아요. 방금 우리가 보고 들은 일이 그 둘이 짜고 꾸민 계략을 실행에 옮긴 것뿐이라 해도 저는 조금도 놀라지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“Madame,”", "\"부인,\""], ["said Villefort, “believe me, a fortune of 900,000 francs is not so easily renounced.”", "빌포르가 말했다. \"내 말을 믿으시오. 90만 프랑의 재산을 그렇게 쉽게 포기할 수는 없는 법이오.\""]], [["“She could, nevertheless, make up her mind to renounce the world, sir, since it is only about a year ago that she herself proposed entering a convent.”", "\"그래도 그 애는 세상을 등질 결심을 할 수도 있어요. 바로 일 년쯤 전에 그 애 스스로 수도원에 들어가겠다고 했었으니까요.\""]], [["“Never mind,”", "\"상관없소.\""], ["replied Villefort; “I say that this marriage _shall_ be consummated.”", "빌포르가 대답했다. \"이 결혼은 반드시 성사될 것이라고 나는 말하는 거요.\""]], [["“Notwithstanding your father’s wishes to the contrary?”", "\"당신 아버님의 반대 뜻에도 불구하고요?\""], ["said Madame de Villefort, selecting a new point of attack.", "빌포르 부인이 새로운 공격 지점을 골라잡으며 말했다."], ["“That is a serious thing.”", "\"그건 중대한 일이에요.\""]], [["Monte Cristo, who pretended not to be listening, heard however, every word that was said.", "귀를 기울이지 않는 척하던 몬테크리스토는, 그러나 오가는 말을 한마디도 빠짐없이 듣고 있었다."]], [["30185m\n“Madame,”", "\"부인,\""], ["replied Villefort “I can truly say that I have always entertained a high respect for my father, because, to the natural feeling of relationship was added the consciousness of his moral superiority.", "빌포르가 대답했다. \"나는 정말이지 늘 아버지를 깊이 존경해 왔다고 떳떳이 말할 수 있소. 혈육의 정이라는 본능적인 감정에, 그분의 도덕적 우월함에 대한 자각까지 더해졌기 때문이오."], ["The name of father is sacred in two senses; he should be reverenced as the author of our being and as a master whom we ought to obey.", "아버지라는 이름은 두 가지 의미에서 신성하오. 우리를 존재하게 한 분으로서, 또 우리가 마땅히 따라야 할 주인으로서 받들어져야 하니까."], ["But, under the present circumstances, I am justified in doubting the wisdom of an old man who, because he hated the father, vents his anger on the son.", "하지만 지금 같은 상황이라면, 아버지를 미워했다는 이유로 그 아들에게 분풀이를 하는 한 노인의 분별을 의심하는 것도 정당한 일이오."], ["It would be ridiculous in me to regulate my conduct by such caprices.", "그런 변덕에 맞춰 내 행동을 조절한다면 우스운 노릇이지."], ["I shall still continue to preserve the same respect toward M.", "나는 앞으로도 누아르티에 씨께 변함없는 존경을 지킬 것이오."], ["Noirtier; I will suffer, without complaint, the pecuniary deprivation to which he has subjected me; but I shall remain firm in my determination, and the world shall see which party has reason on his side.", "그분이 내게 안긴 금전적 손실도 불평 없이 견디겠소. 그러나 내 결심만큼은 흔들리지 않을 것이고, 세상은 어느 쪽이 옳은지 보게 될 거요."], ["Consequently I shall marry my daughter to the Baron Franz d’Épinay, because I consider it would be a proper and eligible match for her to make, and, in short, because I choose to bestow my daughter’s hand on whomever I please.”", "그러니 나는 내 딸을 프란츠 데피네 남작에게 시집보내겠소. 그것이 그 애에게 마땅하고 어울리는 혼처라고 여기기 때문이고, 한마디로 내 딸의 손을 누구에게 내어 줄지는 내 마음이기 때문이오.\""]], [["“What?”", "\"뭐라고요?\""], ["said the count, the approbation of whose eye Villefort had frequently solicited during this speech. “What?", "백작이 말했다. 빌포르는 이 일장연설을 하는 내내 백작의 눈에서 동의의 빛을 거듭 구했었다."], ["Do you say that M.", "\"뭐라고요?"], ["Noirtier disinherits Mademoiselle de Villefort because she is going to marry M. le Baron Franz d’Épinay?”", "누아르티에 씨가 따님이 프란츠 데피네 남작과 결혼하려 한다는 이유로 빌포르 양의 상속권을 박탈하신다는 말씀입니까?\""]], [["“Yes, sir, that is the reason,”", "\"그렇습니다. 그게 이유지요.\""], ["said Villefort, shrugging his shoulders.", "빌포르가 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다."]], [["“The apparent reason, at least,”", "\"적어도 겉으로 내세운 이유는 그래요.\""], ["said Madame de Villefort.", "빌포르 부인이 말했다."]], [["“The _real_ reason, madame, I can assure you; I know my father.”", "\"진짜 이유입니다, 부인. 분명히 말씀드릴 수 있어요. 저는 제 아버지를 아니까요.\""]], [["“But I want to know in what way M. d’Épinay can have displeased your father more than any other person?”", "\"하지만 저는 데피네 씨가 어떤 점에서 다른 누구보다도 아버님의 비위를 거슬렀는지 알고 싶군요.\""]], [["“I believe I know M. Franz d’Épinay,”", "\"제가 프란츠 데피네 씨를 아는 것 같습니다.\""], ["said the count; “is he not the son of General de Quesnel, who was created Baron d’Épinay by Charles X.?”", "백작이 말했다. \"샤를 10세에 의해 데피네 남작 작위를 받은 케넬 장군의 아들이 아닙니까?\""]], [["“The same,” said Villefort.", "\"바로 그렇습니다.\" 빌포르가 말했다."]], [["“Well, but he is a charming young man, according to my ideas.”", "\"음, 하지만 제가 보기에 그는 매력적인 청년인데요.\""]], [["“He is, which makes me believe that it is only an excuse of M.", "\"그렇지요. 그러니 그건 누아르티에 씨가 손녀의 결혼을 막으려고 둘러댄 핑계에 불과하다고 저는 믿는 거예요."], ["Noirtier to prevent his granddaughter marrying; old men are always so selfish in their affection,”", "늙은이들의 애정이란 늘 그렇게 이기적이니까요.\""], ["said Madame de Villefort.", "빌포르 부인이 말했다."]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["said Monte Cristo “do you not know any cause for this hatred?”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그 증오의 원인을 전혀 모르신단 말입니까?\""]], [["“Ah, _ma foi!_ who is to know?”", "\"아, 이런! 그걸 누가 알겠어요?\""]], [["“Perhaps it is some political difference?”", "\"혹시 어떤 정치적 견해 차이 때문은 아닐까요?\""]], [["“My father and the Baron d’Épinay lived in the stormy times of which I only saw the ending,”", "\"제 아버지와 데피네 남작은 제가 그 끝자락만 본 그 격동의 시대를 사신 분들이지요.\""], ["said Villefort.", "빌포르가 말했다."]], [["“Was not your father a Bonapartist?”", "\"아버님이 보나파르트파 아니셨습니까?\""], ["asked Monte Cristo; “I think I remember that you told me something of that kind.”", "몬테크리스토가 물었다. \"그런 비슷한 말씀을 제게 하셨던 것 같은데요.\""]], [["“My father has been a Jacobin more than anything else,”", "\"제 아버지는 무엇보다도 자코뱅파였습니다.\""], ["said Villefort, carried by his emotion beyond the bounds of prudence; “and the senator’s robe, which Napoleon cast on his shoulders, only served to disguise the old man without in any degree changing him.", "빌포르가 감정에 휩쓸려 신중함의 선을 넘어 버린 채 말했다. \"나폴레옹이 그분의 어깨에 걸쳐 준 원로원 의원의 예복은, 그 노인을 위장해 주었을 뿐 조금도 바꾸어 놓지는 못했지요."], ["When my father conspired, it was not for the emperor, it was against the Bourbons; for M.", "제 아버지가 음모를 꾸민 것은 황제를 위해서가 아니라 부르봉 왕가에 맞서기 위해서였습니다. 누아르티에 씨에게는 이런 특이한 점이 있었거든요."], ["Noirtier possessed this peculiarity, he never projected any Utopian schemes which could never be realized, but strove for possibilities, and he applied to the realization of these possibilities the terrible theories of The Mountain,—theories that never shrank from any means that were deemed necessary to bring about the desired result.”", "그분은 결코 실현될 수 없는 몽상적인 계획 따위는 꾀하지 않으셨고, 오직 가능한 일을 위해 싸우셨습니다. 그리고 그 가능한 일을 실현하기 위해, 산악파의 그 무시무시한 이론을 적용하셨지요. 원하는 결과를 얻기 위해 필요하다 싶은 수단이라면 어떤 것 앞에서도 결코 물러서지 않는 이론을 말입니다.\""]], [["“Well,” said Monte Cristo, “it is just as I thought; it was politics which brought Noirtier and M.", "* 산악파: 프랑스 혁명기 국민공회의 급진 좌파. —옮긴이\n\"음,\""], ["d’Épinay into personal contact.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["Although General d’Épinay served under Napoleon, did he not still retain royalist sentiments?", "\"제가 생각한 그대로군요. 누아르티에 씨와 데피네 씨를 개인적으로 얽히게 한 것은 정치였습니다."], ["And was he not the person who was assassinated one evening on leaving a Bonapartist meeting to which he had been invited on the supposition that he favored the cause of the emperor?”", "데피네 장군은 나폴레옹 밑에서 복무했으면서도 여전히 왕당파의 신념을 간직하고 있지 않았습니까? 그리고 황제의 대의를 지지한다고 여겨져 초대받은 보나파르트파 모임에서 나오던 어느 날 저녁, 암살당한 바로 그 인물이 아닙니까?\""]], [["Villefort looked at the count almost with terror.", "빌포르가 거의 공포에 질린 얼굴로 백작을 바라보았다."]], [["“Am I mistaken, then?”", "\"그럼 제가 잘못 알고 있는 겁니까?\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“No, sir, the facts were precisely what you have stated,”", "\"아니에요, 백작. 사실은 정확히 말씀하신 그대로랍니다.\""], ["said Madame de Villefort; “and it was to prevent the renewal of old feuds that M. de Villefort formed the idea of uniting in the bonds of affection the two children of these inveterate enemies.”", "빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"그리고 그 해묵은 원한이 되살아나는 것을 막으려고, 빌포르 씨가 그 철천지원수였던 두 사람의 자식들을 애정의 끈으로 맺어 주자는 생각을 하게 된 거예요.\""]], [["“It was a sublime and charitable thought,”", "\"숭고하고 자비로운 생각이로군요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “and the whole world should applaud it.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"온 세상이 박수를 보낼 만한 일입니다."], ["It would be noble to see Mademoiselle Noirtier de Villefort assuming the title of Madame Franz d’Épinay.”", "누아르티에 드 빌포르 양이 프란츠 데피네 부인이라는 칭호를 얻는 모습을 보게 된다면, 그야말로 고결한 일이지요.\""]], [["Villefort shuddered and looked at Monte Cristo as if he wished to read in his countenance the real feelings which had dictated the words he had just uttered.", "빌포르가 몸서리를 치며, 방금 자신이 내뱉은 말을 짓게 한 진짜 속내를 그 표정에서 읽어 내려는 듯 몬테크리스토를 바라보았다."], ["But the count completely baffled the procureur, and prevented him from discovering anything beneath the never-varying smile he was so constantly in the habit of assuming.", "그러나 백작은 검사를 완전히 따돌렸고, 늘 한결같이 짓고 있는 그 변함없는 미소 아래에서 무엇 하나 알아챌 수 없게 만들었다."]], [["“Although,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["said Villefort, “it will be a serious thing for Valentine to lose her grandfather’s fortune, I do not think that M. d’Épinay will be frightened at this pecuniary loss.", "빌포르가 말했다. \"발랑틴이 할아버지의 재산을 잃는다는 것은 중대한 일이긴 해도, 데피네 씨가 이 금전적 손실에 겁을 먹으리라고는 생각지 않습니다."], ["He will, perhaps, hold me in greater esteem than the money itself, seeing that I sacrifice everything in order to keep my word with him.", "그는 어쩌면 그 돈 자체보다 저를 더 높이 평가할 겁니다. 제가 그와의 약속을 지키기 위해 모든 것을 희생하는 것을 보았으니까요."], ["Besides, he knows that Valentine is rich in right of her mother, and that she will, in all probability, inherit the fortune of M. and Madame de Saint-Méran, her mother’s parents, who both love her tenderly.”", "게다가 그는 발랑틴이 어머니 쪽 권리로 부유하다는 것도, 그 애를 끔찍이 아끼는 외조부모인 생메랑 후작 부부의 재산을 십중팔구 물려받으리라는 것도 알고 있습니다.\""]], [["“And who are fully as well worth loving and tending as M. Noirtier,”", "\"그분들로 말하자면 누아르티에 씨 못지않게 사랑받고 보살핌을 받으실 만한 분들이지요.\""], ["said Madame de Villefort; “besides, they are to come to Paris in about a month, and Valentine, after the affront she has received, need not consider it necessary to continue to bury herself alive by being shut up with M. Noirtier.”", "빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"게다가 한 달쯤 뒤면 파리로 오실 거고, 발랑틴도 이런 모욕을 당한 마당에 누아르티에 씨에게 갇혀 산 채로 파묻혀 지낼 필요가 더는 없게 될 거예요.\""]], [["The count listened with satisfaction to this tale of wounded self-love and defeated ambition.", "백작은 이 상처 입은 자존심과 좌절된 야심의 사연을 흡족하게 들었다."]], [["“But it seems to me,”", "\"하지만 제가 보기에는,\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “and I must begin by asking your pardon for what I am about to say, that if M. Noirtier disinherits Mademoiselle de Villefort because she is going to marry a man whose father he detested, he cannot have the same cause of complaint against this dear Edward.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그리고 이제부터 드릴 말씀에 대해 먼저 양해를 구해야겠습니다만, 만약 누아르티에 씨가 빌포르 양이 자신이 증오하던 사람의 아들과 결혼하려 한다는 이유로 그녀의 상속권을 박탈하시는 것이라면, 이 사랑스러운 에두아르에 대해서는 똑같은 불만을 품으실 까닭이 없지 않습니까.\""]], [["“True,”", "\"맞아요.\""], ["said Madame de Villefort, with an intonation of voice which it is impossible to describe; “is it not unjust—shamefully unjust?", "빌포르 부인이 뭐라 형언할 수 없는 어조로 말했다. \"이건 부당한 일 아닌가요? 부끄러울 만큼 부당한 일이지요."], ["Poor Edward is as much M. Noirtier’s grandchild as Valentine, and yet, if she had not been going to marry M. Franz, M.", "가엾은 에두아르도 발랑틴과 똑같이 누아르티에 씨의 손주인데, 만약 그 애가 프란츠 씨와 결혼하려 하지 않았다면 누아르티에 씨는 전 재산을 그 애에게 물려주었을 거예요."], ["Noirtier would have left her all his money; and supposing Valentine to be disinherited by her grandfather, she will still be three times richer than he.”", "게다가 발랑틴이 할아버지에게서 상속권을 박탈당한다 해도, 그 애는 여전히 에두아르보다 세 배는 더 부유할 테고요.\""]], [["The count listened and said no more.", "백작은 듣기만 할 뿐 더는 말이 없었다."]], [["“Count,”", "\"백작,\""], ["said Villefort, “we will not entertain you any longer with our family misfortunes.", "빌포르가 말했다. \"저희 집안의 불행으로 더는 당신을 붙들어 두지 않겠습니다."], ["It is true that my patrimony will go to endow charitable institutions, and my father will have deprived me of my lawful inheritance without any reason for doing so, but I shall have the satisfaction of knowing that I have acted like a man of sense and feeling. M.", "사실 제 유산은 자선 단체로 넘어갈 것이고, 아버지는 아무런 까닭도 없이 제게서 정당한 상속분을 빼앗으신 셈이지요. 하지만 저는 분별 있고 정 있는 사람답게 처신했다는 것을 안다는 만족감만은 얻을 겁니다."], ["d’Épinay, to whom I had promised the interest of this sum, shall receive it, even if I endure the most cruel privations.”", "제가 그 돈의 이자를 약속했던 데피네 씨는, 설령 제가 더없이 가혹한 궁핍을 견뎌야 한다 해도 그것을 받게 될 것입니다.\""]], [["“However,”", "\"그렇지만,\""], ["said Madame de Villefort, returning to the one idea which incessantly occupied her mind, “perhaps it would be better to explain this unlucky affair to M.", "빌포르 부인이 끊임없이 자기 머릿속을 맴도는 그 한 가지 생각으로 되돌아오며 말했다. \"어쩌면 이 불운한 일을 데피네 씨에게 설명해 드리는 편이 나을지도 몰라요."], ["d’Épinay, in order to give him the opportunity of himself renouncing his claim to the hand of Mademoiselle de Villefort.”", "그분 스스로 빌포르 양에 대한 청혼을 거두어들일 기회를 드리도록 말이에요.\""]], [["“Ah, that would be a great pity,”", "\"아, 그건 참으로 안타까운 일이지요.\""], ["said Villefort.", "빌포르가 말했다."]], [["“A great pity,” said Monte Cristo.", "\"참으로 안타까운 일이고말고요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“Undoubtedly,”", "\"틀림없습니다.\""], ["said Villefort, moderating the tones of his voice, “a marriage once concerted and then broken off, throws a sort of discredit on a young lady; then again, the old reports, which I was so anxious to put an end to, will instantly gain ground.", "빌포르가 목소리를 누그러뜨리며 말했다. \"한번 맺어졌다가 깨진 혼담은 처녀에게 일종의 불명예를 안기는 법이지요. 게다가 제가 그토록 끝내고 싶어 했던 그 해묵은 소문들이 당장 다시 고개를 들 겁니다."], ["No, it will all go well; M.", "아니, 모든 게 잘될 거예요."], ["d’Épinay, if he is an honorable man, will consider himself more than ever pledged to Mademoiselle de Villefort, unless he were actuated by a decided feeling of avarice, but that is impossible.”", "데피네 씨가 명예를 아는 사람이라면, 자신이 그 어느 때보다도 더 빌포르 양에게 묶여 있다고 여길 겁니다. 노골적인 탐욕에 휘둘리는 자가 아니라면 말이지요. 하지만 그건 있을 수 없는 일이고요.\""]], [["“I agree with M. de Villefort,”", "\"저도 빌포르 씨의 의견에 동의합니다.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, fixing his eyes on Madame de Villefort; “and if I were sufficiently intimate with him to allow of giving my advice, I would persuade him, since I have been told M. d’Épinay is coming back, to settle this affair at once beyond all possibility of revocation.", "몬테크리스토가 빌포르 부인에게 시선을 고정한 채 말했다. \"그리고 만약 제가 충고를 드려도 될 만큼 그와 가까운 사이라면, 데피네 씨가 돌아오는 중이라고 들었으니, 이 일을 당장, 결코 번복할 수 없도록 매듭지으라고 권하겠습니다."], ["I will answer for the success of a project which will reflect so much honor on M. de Villefort.”", "빌포르 씨에게 그토록 큰 명예가 될 그 계획의 성공을 제가 보증하지요.\""]], [["The procureur arose, delighted with the proposition, but his wife slightly changed color.", "검사가 그 제안에 기뻐하며 자리에서 일어섰지만, 그의 아내는 안색이 살짝 변했다."]], [["“Well, that is all that I wanted, and I will be guided by a counsellor such as you are,”", "\"좋습니다, 제가 바라던 게 바로 그것이고, 당신 같은 조언자의 인도를 따르겠습니다.\""], ["said he, extending his hand to Monte Cristo.", "빌포르가 몬테크리스토에게 손을 내밀며 말했다."], ["“Therefore let everyone here look upon what has passed today as if it had not happened, and as though we had never thought of such a thing as a change in our original plans.”", "\"그러니 여기 있는 모두는 오늘 있었던 일을 아예 없었던 일로 여기고, 우리가 애초의 계획을 바꾸는 일 따위는 생각조차 한 적 없는 것으로 합시다.\""]], [["“Sir,” said the count, “the world, unjust as it is, will be pleased with your resolution; your friends will be proud of you, and M.", "\"빌포르 씨,\" 백작이 말했다."], ["d’Épinay, even if he took Mademoiselle de Villefort without any dowry, which he will not do, would be delighted with the idea of entering a family which could make such sacrifices in order to keep a promise and fulfil a duty.”", "\"세상은 부당하긴 해도 당신의 결단을 흡족히 여길 겁니다. 당신의 친구들은 당신을 자랑스러워할 것이고, 데피네 씨로 말하자면, 설령 그가 빌포르 양을 지참금 한 푼 없이 맞아들인다 해도, 물론 그러지는 않겠지만, 약속을 지키고 의무를 다하기 위해 그런 희생도 마다하지 않는 집안에 들어가게 되었다는 생각에 더없이 기뻐할 겁니다.\""]], [["At the conclusion of these words, the count rose to depart.", "이 말을 마치자 백작이 떠나려고 자리에서 일어섰다."]], [["“Are you going to leave us, count?”", "\"가시려는 거예요, 백작?\""], ["said Madame de Villefort.", "빌포르 부인이 말했다."]], [["“I am sorry to say I must do so, madame, I only came to remind you of your promise for Saturday.”", "\"유감스럽게도 그래야겠습니다, 부인. 토요일 약속을 다시 한번 일러 드리려고 들렀을 뿐이니까요.\""]], [["“Did you fear that we should forget it?”", "\"저희가 그걸 잊을까 봐 걱정하셨나요?\""]], [["“You are very good, madame, but M. de Villefort has so many important and urgent occupations.”", "\"부인은 참 친절하십니다만, 빌포르 씨는 워낙 중요하고 급한 일이 많으셔서요.\""]], [["“My husband has given me his word, sir,”", "\"제 남편이 약속을 했답니다.\""], ["said Madame de Villefort; “you have just seen him resolve to keep it when he has everything to lose, and surely there is more reason for his doing so where he has everything to gain.”", "빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"방금 모든 것을 잃을 처지에서도 약속을 지키기로 결심하는 모습을 보셨잖아요. 모든 것을 얻을 일이라면 더더욱 그렇게 할 까닭이 있겠지요.\""]], [["“And,”", "\"그런데,\""], ["said Villefort, “is it at your house in the Champs-Élysées that you receive your visitors?”", "빌포르가 말했다. \"손님을 맞이하시는 곳이 샹젤리제의 댁입니까?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아닙니다.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “which is precisely the reason which renders your kindness more meritorious,—it is in the country.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"바로 그 점이 두 분의 호의를 한층 더 값지게 하는 까닭이지요. 시골에 있는 집이거든요.\""]], [["“In the country?”", "\"시골이요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Where is it, then?", "\"그럼 어디입니까?"], ["Near Paris, is it not?”", "파리 근교 아닌가요?\""]], [["“Very near, only half a league from the Barriers,—it is at Auteuil.”", "\"아주 가깝습니다. 시문에서 반 리외밖에 안 되는 오퇴유랍니다.\""]], [["“At Auteuil?”", "\"오퇴유라고요?\""], ["said Villefort; “true, Madame de Villefort told me you lived at Auteuil, since it was to your house that she was taken.", "빌포르가 말했다. \"참, 빌포르 부인이 당신께서 오퇴유에 사신다고 하더군요. 부인이 실려 간 곳이 당신 댁이었으니까요."], ["And in what part of Auteuil do you reside?”", "그런데 오퇴유 어느 쪽에 사십니까?\""]], [["“Rue de la Fontaine.”", "\"퐁텐 거리입니다.\""]], [["“Rue de la Fontaine!”", "\"퐁텐 거리라고요!\""], ["exclaimed Villefort in an agitated tone; “at what number?”", "빌포르가 동요한 어조로 외쳤다. \"몇 번지입니까?\" \"28번지입니다.\""]], [["“No. 28.” “Then,”", "\"그럼,\""], ["cried Villefort, “was it you who bought M. de Saint-Méran’s house!”", "빌포르가 소리쳤다. \"생메랑 씨의 집을 사신 게 바로 당신이었군요!\""]], [["“Did it belong to M. de Saint-Méran?”", "\"그게 생메랑 씨의 집이었습니까?\""], ["demanded Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 물었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["replied Madame de Villefort; “and, would you believe it, count——”", "빌포르 부인이 대답했다. \"그런데, 백작, 믿어지세요……\""]], [["“Believe what?”", "\"무엇을 말입니까?\""]], [["“You think this house pretty, do you not?”", "\"그 집이 예쁘다고 생각하시죠, 그렇죠?\""]], [["“I think it charming.”", "\"아주 매력적이라고 생각합니다.\""]], [["“Well, my husband would never live in it.”", "\"그런데 제 남편은 그 집에서는 도무지 살려고 하질 않았답니다.\""]], [["“Indeed?”", "\"그래요?\""], ["returned Monte Cristo, “that is a prejudice on your part, M. de Villefort, for which I am quite at a loss to account.”", "몬테크리스토가 받아 말했다. \"그건 빌포르 씨의 편견인데, 저로서는 도무지 그 까닭을 알 수가 없군요.\""]], [["“I do not like Auteuil, sir,”", "\"저는 오퇴유가 싫습니다.\""], ["said the procureur, making an evident effort to appear calm.", "검사가 애써 태연한 척하려고 안간힘을 쓰며 말했다."]], [["“But I hope you will not carry your antipathy so far as to deprive me of the pleasure of your company, sir,” said Monte Cristo.", "\"하지만 그 반감이 지나쳐서 제게서 당신과 함께할 기쁨까지 앗아 가시지는 않겠지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“No, count,—I hope—I assure you I shall do my best,”", "\"아닙니다, 백작. 그러길 바라고, 정말이지 최선을 다하겠습니다.\""], ["stammered Villefort.", "빌포르가 더듬거리며 말했다."]], [["“Oh,”", "\"아,\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “I allow of no excuse.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"어떤 핑계도 받아들이지 않겠습니다."], ["On Saturday, at six o’clock.", "토요일 여섯 시입니다. 기다리고 있겠습니다."], ["I shall be expecting you, and if you fail to come, I shall think—for how do I know to the contrary?—that this house, which has remained uninhabited for twenty years, must have some gloomy tradition or dreadful legend connected with it.”", "만약 오시지 않으면 저는 이렇게 생각할 겁니다. 달리 알 길이 없으니 말이지요. 이십 년이나 사람이 살지 않은 그 집에 무슨 음울한 내력이나 끔찍한 전설이라도 얽혀 있는 게 틀림없다고요.\""]], [["“I will come, count,—I will be sure to come,”", "\"가겠습니다, 백작. 꼭 가지요.\""], ["said Villefort eagerly.", "빌포르가 다급히 말했다."]], [["“Thank you,”", "\"고맙습니다.\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “now you must permit me to take my leave of you.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그럼 이제 작별을 고하게 해 주십시오.\""]], [["“You said before that you were obliged to leave us, monsieur,”", "\"아까 가 보셔야 한다고 하셨지요.\""], ["said Madame de Villefort, “and you were about to tell us why when your attention was called to some other subject.”", "빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"그 까닭을 말씀하시려던 참에 다른 일로 주의를 돌리셨고요.\""]], [["“Indeed madame,”", "\"실은, 부인,\""], ["said Monte Cristo: “I scarcely know if I dare tell you where I am going.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"제가 어디로 가는지 감히 말씀드려도 될지 모르겠군요.\""]], [["“Nonsense; say on.”", "\"별말씀을. 어서 말씀하세요.\""]], [["“Well, then, it is to see a thing on which I have sometimes mused for hours together.”", "\"그럼 말씀드리지요. 제가 때때로 몇 시간이고 골똘히 생각에 잠기곤 했던 어떤 것을 보러 가는 길입니다.\""]], [["“What is it?”", "\"그게 뭔가요?\""]], [["“A telegraph.", "\"전신기입니다."], ["So now I have told my secret.”", "자, 이제 제 비밀을 털어놓았군요.\""]], [["“A telegraph?”", "\"전신기요?\""], ["repeated Madame de Villefort.", "빌포르 부인이 되물었다."]], [["“Yes, a telegraph.", "\"네, 전신기입니다."], ["I had often seen one placed at the end of a road on a hillock, and in the light of the sun its black arms, bending in every direction, always reminded me of the claws of an immense beetle, and I assure you it was never without emotion that I gazed on it, for I could not help thinking how wonderful it was that these various signs should be made to cleave the air with such precision as to convey to the distance of three hundred leagues the ideas and wishes of a man sitting at a table at one end of the line to another man similarly placed at the opposite extremity, and all this effected by a simple act of volition on the part of the sender of the message.", "길 끝 언덕배기에 세워진 그것을 저는 종종 보아 왔지요. 햇빛 속에서 사방으로 꺾이는 그 검은 팔들은 늘 거대한 딱정벌레의 발톱을 떠올리게 했습니다. 그리고 정말이지, 저는 그것을 바라볼 때마다 늘 가슴이 설레었답니다."], ["I began to think of genii, sylphs, gnomes, in short, of all the ministers of the occult sciences, until I laughed aloud at the freaks of my own imagination.", "이 갖가지 신호들이 그토록 정확하게 허공을 가르며, 선의 한쪽 끝 탁자 앞에 앉은 사람의 생각과 바람을 삼백 리외나 떨어진 반대편 끝의 또 다른 사람에게, 그것도 메시지를 보내는 이의 단순한 의지 행위 하나만으로 전달한다는 것이 얼마나 경이로운 일인가 생각하지 않을 수 없었으니까요."], ["Now, it never occurred to me to wish for a nearer inspection of these large insects, with their long black claws, for I always feared to find under their stone wings some little human genius fagged to death with cabals, factions, and government intrigues.", "저는 정령이며 공기의 요정이며 땅의 요정, 한마디로 비밀스러운 학문을 관장하는 온갖 존재를 떠올리기 시작했고, 마침내 제 상상의 별난 장난에 소리 내어 웃고 말았지요. 그런데 길고 검은 발톱을 지닌 이 커다란 곤충들을 더 가까이 들여다보고 싶다는 생각은 도무지 들지 않았습니다."], ["But one fine day I learned that the mover of this telegraph was only a poor wretch, hired for twelve hundred francs a year, and employed all day, not in studying the heavens like an astronomer, or in gazing on the water like an angler, or even in enjoying the privilege of observing the country around him, but all his monotonous life was passed in watching his white-bellied, black-clawed fellow insect, four or five leagues distant from him.", "그 돌로 된 날개 밑에서, 음모와 파벌과 정부의 책략에 시달려 죽을 지경이 된 어떤 자그마한 인간 정령을 발견하게 될까 봐 늘 두려웠거든요. 그런데 어느 화창한 날, 저는 이 전신기를 움직이는 자가 일 년에 천이백 프랑을 받고 고용된 가련한 신세에 불과하다는 것을 알게 되었습니다. 그는 천문학자처럼 하늘을 살피는 것도, 낚시꾼처럼 물을 바라보는 것도, 심지어 주위의 풍경을 둘러보는 특권을 누리는 것도 아니라, 하루 종일 단조로운 일생을 자기에게서 네댓 리외 떨어진, 흰 배에 검은 발톱을 가진 동료 곤충을 지켜보는 데 다 보낸다더군요."], ["At length I felt a desire to study this living chrysalis more closely, and to endeavor to understand the secret part played by these insect-actors when they occupy themselves simply with pulling different pieces of string.” 30191m", "마침내 저는 이 살아 있는 번데기를 더 가까이 들여다보고 싶은, 그리고 이 곤충 배우들이 그저 갖가지 줄을 잡아당기는 일에만 매달릴 때 그들이 맡은 그 은밀한 역할을 이해해 보고 싶은 욕구를 느꼈습니다.\""]], [["“And are you going there?” “I am.”", "\"그래서 거기로 가시는 거군요?\""]], [["“What telegraph do you intend visiting?", "\"그렇습니다.\" \"어느 전신기를 찾아가실 작정인가요?"], ["that of the home department, or of the observatory?”", "내무부 것인가요, 아니면 천문대 것인가요?\""]], [["“Oh, no; I should find there people who would force me to understand things of which I would prefer to remain ignorant, and who would try to explain to me, in spite of myself, a mystery which even they do not understand.", "\"아, 아닙니다. 거기 가면 제가 차라리 모르고 싶은 것들을 억지로 이해시키려 드는 사람들, 그리고 자기들조차 모르는 그 신비를 제 뜻과 상관없이 설명하려 드는 사람들을 만나게 될 테니까요. 이런!"], ["_Ma foi!_ I should wish to keep my illusions concerning insects unimpaired; it is quite enough to have those dissipated which I had formed of my fellow-creatures.", "저는 곤충에 대한 환상만큼은 고이 간직하고 싶습니다. 같은 인간에 대해 품었던 환상이 깨진 것만으로도 충분하니까요."], ["I shall, therefore, not visit either of these telegraphs, but one in the open country where I shall find a good-natured simpleton, who knows no more than the machine he is employed to work.”", "그러니 저는 그 어느 전신기도 찾지 않고, 탁 트인 시골에 있는 것을 찾아가렵니다. 거기서라면 자기가 다루는 기계 이상은 아무것도 모르는, 사람 좋은 얼뜨기 하나를 만날 수 있겠지요.\""]], [["“You are a singular man,”", "\"당신은 별난 분이로군요.\""], ["said Villefort.", "빌포르가 말했다."]], [["“What line would you advise me to study?”", "\"어느 노선을 살펴보는 게 좋겠습니까?\""]], [["“The one that is most in use just at this time.”", "\"지금 한창 가장 많이 쓰이는 노선이지요.\""]], [["“The Spanish one, you mean, I suppose?”", "\"에스파냐 노선을 말씀하시는 거겠지요?\""]], [["“Yes; should you like a letter to the minister that they might explain to you——”", "\"그렇습니다. 장관에게 보내는 소개장이 필요하시면, 그쪽에서 당신에게 설명을……\""]], [["“No,” said Monte Cristo; “since, as I told you before, I do not wish to comprehend it.", "\"아닙니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["The moment I understand it there will no longer exist a telegraph for me; it will be nothing more than a sign from M. Duchâtel, or from M.", "\"아까도 말씀드렸듯이, 저는 그것을 이해하고 싶지 않으니까요. 제가 그것을 이해하는 순간, 제게 전신기란 더는 존재하지 않게 될 겁니다."], ["Montalivet, transmitted to the prefect of Bayonne, mystified by two Greek words, _têle_, _graphein_. It is the insect with black claws, and the awful word which I wish to retain in my imagination in all its purity and all its importance.”", "그건 그저 뒤샤텔 씨나 몽탈리베 씨가 바욘의 도지사에게 보내는 신호, 그리스어 두 단어 '텔레'와 '그라페인'으로 그럴싸하게 꾸민 것에 지나지 않게 되겠지요. 제가 상상 속에 온전하고 중요한 모습 그대로 간직하고 싶은 것은, 바로 그 검은 발톱을 지닌 곤충과 그 경이로운 단어랍니다.\""]], [["“Go then; for in the course of two hours it will be dark, and you will not be able to see anything.”", "\"그럼 가 보세요. 두 시간만 지나면 어두워져서 아무것도 못 보실 테니까요.\""]], [["“_Ma foi!_ you frighten me.", "\"이런! 겁을 주시는군요."], ["Which is the nearest way? Bayonne?”", "가장 가까운 길이 어느 쪽입니까? 바욘 쪽인가요?\""]], [["“Yes; the road to Bayonne.”", "\"그렇습니다. 바욘으로 가는 길이지요.\""]], [["“And afterwards the road to Châtillon?” “Yes.”", "\"그다음엔 샤티용으로 가는 길이고요?\""]], [["“By the tower of Montlhéry, you mean?”", "\"그렇습니다.\" \"몽레리 탑을 지나서 말이지요?\""]], [["“Thank you.", "\"그렇습니다.\""], ["Good-bye.", "\"고맙습니다."], ["On Saturday I will tell you my impressions concerning the telegraph.”", "안녕히 계십시오. 토요일에 전신기에 대한 제 소감을 들려드리겠습니다.\""]], [["At the door the count was met by the two notaries, who had just completed the act which was to disinherit Valentine, and who were leaving under the conviction of having done a thing which could not fail of redounding considerably to their credit.", "문간에서 백작은 공증인 두 사람과 마주쳤다. 그들은 발랑틴의 상속권을 박탈하는 증서를 막 완성한 참이었고, 자기들의 평판에 적잖이 보탬이 될 일을 해냈다는 확신에 차서 떠나는 길이었다."]], [["# Chapter 61. How a Gardener May Get Rid of the Dormice that Eat His Peaches", "# Chapter 61. How a Gardener May Get Rid of the Dormice that Eat His Peaches"]], [["Not on the same night as he had stated, but the next morning, the Count of Monte Cristo went out by the Barrière d’Enfer, taking the road to Orléans.", "자기가 말한 그날 밤이 아니라 이튿날 아침, 몬테크리스토 백작은 앙페르 관문을 빠져나가 오를레앙으로 가는 길로 접어들었다."], ["Leaving the village of Linas, without stopping at the telegraph, which flourished its great bony arms as he passed, the count reached the tower of Montlhéry, situated, as everyone knows, upon the highest point of the plain of that name.", "리나스 마을을 지날 때 전신소가 거대한 뼈만 남은 팔을 휘둘렀지만, 백작은 멈추지 않고 지나쳐 그 유명한 몽틀레리 평원의 가장 높은 곳에 자리한 몽틀레리 탑에 다다랐다."], ["At the foot of the hill the count dismounted and began to ascend by a little winding path, about eighteen inches wide; when he reached the summit he found himself stopped by a hedge, upon which green fruit had succeeded to red and white flowers.", "언덕 기슭에서 백작은 말에서 내려 폭이 한 자 반쯤 되는 구불구불한 작은 오솔길을 따라 올라가기 시작했다. 꼭대기에 이르자 산울타리가 길을 막았는데, 그 위에는 붉고 흰 꽃 자리에 풋과일이 맺혀 있었다."]], [["Monte Cristo looked for the entrance to the enclosure, and was not long in finding a little wooden gate, working on willow hinges, and fastened with a nail and string.", "몬테크리스토는 울타리 안으로 들어가는 입구를 찾았고, 오래지 않아 버드나무 가지로 만든 경첩이 달리고 못과 끈으로 잠긴 작은 나무 쪽문을 발견했다."], ["The count soon mastered the mechanism, the gate opened, and he then found himself in a little garden, about twenty feet long by twelve wide, bounded on one side by part of the hedge, which contained the ingenious contrivance we have called a gate, and on the other by the old tower, covered with ivy and studded with wall-flowers.", "백작은 곧 그 장치를 풀어냈고, 문이 열리자 길이 스무 자, 너비 열두 자쯤 되는 작은 정원에 들어섰다. 한쪽은 우리가 문이라 부른 그 기발한 장치가 달린 산울타리의 일부로, 다른 한쪽은 담쟁이덩굴로 뒤덮이고 갈란투스가 점점이 박힌 오래된 탑으로 막혀 있었다."]], [["No one would have thought in looking at this old, weather-beaten, floral-decked tower (which might be likened to an elderly dame dressed up to receive her grandchildren at a birthday feast) that it would have been capable of telling strange things, if,—in addition to the menacing ears which the proverb says all walls are provided with,—it had also a voice.", "낡고 비바람에 시달리고 꽃으로 단장한 이 탑을 보고서는—생일 잔치에 손주들을 맞으려고 곱게 차려입은 노부인에 비길 만한 그 탑이—이상한 이야기들을 들려줄 수 있으리라고는 아무도 생각하지 못했을 것이다. 속담에 모든 벽에는 위협적인 귀가 달려 있다지만, 만약 그 탑에 귀뿐 아니라 목소리까지 있었다면 말이다."]], [["The garden was crossed by a path of red gravel, edged by a border of thick box, of many years’ growth, and of a tone and color that would have delighted the heart of Delacroix, our modern Rubens.", "정원에는 붉은 자갈을 깐 길이 가로질러 나 있었고, 그 가장자리에는 여러 해 자란 두툼한 회양목이 띠를 두르고 있었는데, 그 빛깔과 색조가 우리 시대의 루벤스라 할 들라크루아의 마음마저 흐뭇하게 했을 법했다."], ["This path was formed in the shape of the figure of 8, thus, in its windings, making a walk of sixty feet in a garden of only twenty.", "이 길은 숫자 8 자 모양으로 나 있어, 구불구불 휘어지며 스무 자밖에 안 되는 정원에 예순 자나 되는 산책로를 만들어 냈다."]], [["Never had Flora, the fresh and smiling goddess of gardeners, been honored with a purer or more scrupulous worship than that which was paid to her in this little enclosure.", "정원사들의 싱그럽고 환히 웃는 여신 플로라가, 이 작은 울타리 안에서 바쳐진 것보다 더 순수하고 더 정성스러운 숭배를 받은 적은 일찍이 없었다."], ["In fact, of the twenty rose-trees which formed the _parterre_, not one bore the mark of the slug, nor were there evidences anywhere of the clustering aphis which is so destructive to plants growing in a damp soil.", "실제로 화단을 이룬 스무 그루의 장미나무 가운데 민달팽이가 갉아먹은 자국이 있는 것은 하나도 없었고, 습한 흙에서 자라는 식물을 그토록 망쳐 놓는 진딧물 떼의 흔적도 어디에서도 찾아볼 수 없었다."], ["And yet it was not because the damp had been excluded from the garden; the earth, black as soot, the thick foliage of the trees betrayed its presence; besides, had natural humidity been wanting, it could have been immediately supplied by artificial means, thanks to a tank of water, sunk in one of the corners of the garden, and upon which were stationed a frog and a toad, who, from antipathy, no doubt, always remained on the two opposite sides of the basin.", "그렇다고 정원에서 습기를 몰아낸 것도 아니었다. 검댕처럼 까만 흙과 나무들의 무성한 잎이 습기의 존재를 일러 주었다. 게다가 자연의 습기가 모자랐다 해도, 정원 한구석에 묻어 둔 물탱크 덕분에 인공으로 곧장 채워 줄 수 있었으니, 그 물탱크 위에는 개구리 한 마리와 두꺼비 한 마리가 자리를 잡고 있었는데, 둘은 서로 미워해서인지 늘 수반의 양쪽 끝에 떨어져 있었다."], ["There was not a blade of grass to be seen in the paths, or a weed in the flower-beds; no fine lady ever trained and watered her geraniums, her cacti, and her rhododendrons, in her porcelain _jardinière_ with more pains than this hitherto unseen gardener bestowed upon his little enclosure.", "길에는 풀잎 하나 보이지 않았고, 꽃밭에는 잡초 한 포기 없었다. 어떤 귀부인도 도자기 화분에 심은 제라늄과 선인장, 진달래를 이 여태껏 본 적 없는 정원사가 자기 작은 울타리에 쏟은 만큼의 정성으로 가꾸고 물을 준 적은 없었다."]], [["Monte Cristo stopped after having closed the gate and fastened the string to the nail, and cast a look around.", "몬테크리스토는 문을 닫고 끈을 못에 걸어 잠근 뒤 걸음을 멈추고 주위를 둘러보았다."]], [["“The man at the telegraph,”", "\"이 전신소의 사람은,\""], ["said he, “must either engage a gardener or devote himself passionately to agriculture.”", "그가 말했다. \"정원사를 고용했거나, 아니면 농사일에 푹 빠져 사는 게 틀림없군.\""]], [["Suddenly he struck against something crouching behind a wheelbarrow filled with leaves; the something rose, uttering an exclamation of astonishment, and Monte Cristo found himself facing a man about fifty years old, who was plucking strawberries, which he was placing upon grape leaves.", "문득 그는 잎이 가득 담긴 손수레 뒤에 웅크리고 있던 무언가에 부딪혔다. 그 무언가가 놀라 소리를 지르며 일어섰고, 몬테크리스토는 쉰 살쯤 된 사내와 마주 섰다. 사내는 딸기를 따서 포도 잎사귀 위에 올려놓고 있었다."], ["He had twelve leaves and about as many strawberries, which, on rising suddenly, he let fall from his hand.", "잎사귀 열두 장에 딸기도 그만큼 있었는데, 느닷없이 일어서느라 손에 들고 있던 것을 떨어뜨리고 말았다."]], [["“You are gathering your crop, sir?”", "\"수확하시는 중이군요, 선생?\""], ["said Monte Cristo, smiling.", "몬테크리스토가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["30193m\n“Excuse me, sir,”", "\"실례했습니다, 선생.\""], ["replied the man, raising his hand to his cap; “I am not up there, I know, but I have only just come down.”", "사내가 모자에 손을 올리며 대답했다. \"저 위에 있어야 하는데, 잠깐 내려온 참입니다.\""]], [["“Do not let me interfere with you in anything, my friend,”", "\"내 일이 자네를 방해하게 두지 말게, 친구.\""], ["said the count; “gather your strawberries, if, indeed, there are any left.”", "백작이 말했다. \"딸기를 따게나, 정말 남은 게 있다면 말이야.\""]], [["“I have ten left,”", "\"열 개 남았습니다.\""], ["said the man, “for here are eleven, and I had twenty-one, five more than last year.", "사내가 말했다. \"여기 열한 개가 있고, 원래 스물한 개였으니까요. 작년보다 다섯 개 더 많지요."], ["But I am not surprised; the spring has been warm this year, and strawberries require heat, sir.", "하지만 놀랄 일은 아닙니다. 올해는 봄이 따뜻했는데, 딸기는 더위를 좋아하거든요, 선생."], ["This is the reason that, instead of the sixteen I had last year, I have this year, you see, eleven, already plucked—twelve, thirteen, fourteen, fifteen, sixteen, seventeen, eighteen.", "그래서 작년에 열여섯 개였던 게 올해는, 보십시오, 벌써 열한 개—열두, 열세, 열넷, 열다섯, 열여섯, 열일곱, 열여덟 개나 땄지요."], ["Ah, I miss three, they were here last night, sir—I am sure they were here—I counted them.", "아, 세 개가 모자라네. 어젯밤엔 여기 있었는데, 선생—분명히 있었어요—세어 봤거든요."], ["It must be the son of Mère Simon who has stolen them; I saw him strolling about here this morning.", "시몬 아주머니네 아들놈이 훔쳐 간 게 분명합니다. 오늘 아침에 그놈이 여기 어슬렁거리는 걸 봤거든요."], ["Ah, the young rascal—stealing in a garden—he does not know where that may lead him to.”", "아, 어린 망나니 같으니—남의 정원에서 도둑질을 하다니—그게 어디까지 갈지 모르는 게지요.\""]], [["“Certainly, it is wrong,”", "\"물론, 그건 나쁜 짓이지.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “but you should take into consideration the youth and greediness of the delinquent.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"하지만 그 죄지은 녀석이 어리고 식탐이 많다는 점도 헤아려 줘야지.\""]], [["“Of course,”", "\"그야 그렇지요.\""], ["said the gardener, “but that does not make it the less unpleasant.", "정원사가 말했다. \"하지만 그렇다고 덜 불쾌한 건 아닙니다."], ["But, sir, once more I beg pardon; perhaps you are an officer that I am detaining here.”", "그런데, 선생, 다시 한번 용서를 빕니다. 혹시 제가 여기 붙잡아 두고 있는 분이 관리이신지요.\""], ["And he glanced timidly at the count’s blue coat.", "그러고는 백작의 푸른 외투를 머뭇머뭇 흘끔거렸다."]], [["30197m\n“Calm yourself, my friend,”", "\"안심하게, 친구.\" 백작이 말했다."], ["said the count, with the smile which he made at will either terrible or benevolent, and which now expressed only the kindliest feeling; “I am not an inspector, but a traveller, brought here by a curiosity he half repents of, since he causes you to lose your time.”", "그는 마음먹은 대로 무섭게도, 다정하게도 지을 수 있는 그 미소를 띠었는데, 지금은 더없이 인자한 마음만을 드러내고 있었다. \"나는 감독관이 아니라 여행자라네. 절반쯤 후회되는 호기심에 이끌려 여기까지 왔지, 자네 시간을 빼앗고 있으니 말이야.\""]], [["“Ah, my time is not valuable,”", "\"아, 제 시간은 값나가는 게 아닙니다.\""], ["replied the man with a melancholy smile.", "사내가 우울한 미소를 지으며 대답했다."], ["“Still it belongs to government, and I ought not to waste it; but, having received the signal that I might rest for an hour”", "\"그래도 나라에 속한 시간이니 허비해서는 안 되지요. 하지만 한 시간 쉬어도 좋다는 신호를 받은 데다\"—여기서 그는 해시계를 흘끗 보았다."], ["(here he glanced at the sun-dial, for there was everything in the enclosure of Montlhéry, even a sun-dial), “and having ten minutes before me, and my strawberries being ripe, when a day longer—by-the-by, sir, do you think dormice eat them?”", "몽틀레리의 이 울타리 안에는 해시계까지, 없는 게 없었다—\"십 분쯤 여유가 있고, 딸기도 익었으니 말입니다. 하루만 더 있으면—그러고 보니, 선생, 겨울잠쥐가 딸기를 먹는다고 보십니까?\""]], [["“Indeed, I should think not,”", "\"글쎄, 그렇진 않을 듯한데.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo; “dormice are bad neighbors for us who do not eat them preserved, as the Romans did.”", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"겨울잠쥐는 로마인들처럼 그놈을 절여 먹지 않는 우리에게는 골치 아픈 이웃이지.\""]], [["“What?", "\"네?"], ["Did the Romans eat them?”", "로마인들이 그걸 먹었다고요?\""], ["said the gardener—“ate dormice?”", "정원사가 말했다. \"겨울잠쥐를 먹었다는 겁니까?\""]], [["“I have read so in Petronius,”", "\"페트로니우스에서 그렇게 읽었네.\""], ["said the count.", "백작이 말했다."]], [["“Really?", "\"정말입니까?"], ["They can’t be nice, though they do say ‘as fat as a dormouse.’ It is not a wonder they are fat, sleeping all day, and only waking to eat all night.", "'겨울잠쥐처럼 살쪘다'는 말이야 있다지만, 맛이 좋을 리가 없는데요. 하루 종일 자다가 밤새 먹으려고만 깨어나니 살이 안 찔 수가 없지요."], ["Listen.", "들어 보십시오."], ["Last year I had four apricots—they stole one, I had one nectarine, only one—well, sir, they ate half of it on the wall; a splendid nectarine—I never ate a better.”", "작년에 살구가 네 개 있었는데—한 개를 훔쳐 갔습니다. 천도복숭아는 딱 하나 있었는데—글쎄, 선생, 그놈들이 담벼락에서 반을 먹어 치웠지 뭡니까. 끝내주는 천도복숭아였는데—그렇게 맛있는 건 먹어 본 적이 없습니다.\""]], [["“You ate it?”", "\"자네가 먹었나?\""]], [["“That is to say, the half that was left—you understand; it was exquisite, sir.", "\"그러니까, 남은 반쪽 말입니다—아시잖습니까. 정말 기막혔지요, 선생."], ["Ah, those gentlemen never choose the worst morsels; like Mère Simon’s son, who has not chosen the worst strawberries.", "아, 그 양반들이 제일 못한 놈을 고르는 법이 없어요. 제일 못한 딸기를 고르지 않은 시몬 아주머니네 아들놈처럼 말입니다."], ["But this year,”", "하지만 올해는,\""], ["continued the horticulturist, “I’ll take care it shall not happen, even if I should be forced to sit by the whole night to watch when the strawberries are ripe.”", "원예가가 말을 이었다. \"그런 일이 없도록 단단히 지킬 겁니다. 딸기가 익으면 밤새 옆에 앉아 망을 봐야 한다 해도 말이지요.\""]], [["Monte Cristo had seen enough.", "몬테크리스토는 충분히 보았다."], ["Every man has a devouring passion in his heart, as every fruit has its worm; that of the telegraph man was horticulture.", "과일마다 벌레가 있듯, 사람마다 가슴속에 집어삼키는 열정이 하나씩 있는 법이다. 이 전신소 사내의 열정은 원예였다."], ["He began gathering the grape-leaves which screened the sun from the grapes, and won the heart of the gardener.", "백작은 포도에 닿는 햇볕을 가리던 포도 잎사귀를 따 주기 시작했고, 그렇게 정원사의 마음을 사로잡았다."]], [["“Did you come here, sir, to see the telegraph?” he said.", "\"전신기를 보러 오셨습니까, 선생?\" 그가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, if it isn’t contrary to the rules.”", "\"그래, 규칙에 어긋나지 않는다면 말일세.\""]], [["“Oh, no,”", "\"오, 아니지요.\""], ["said the gardener; “not in the least, since there is no danger that anyone can possibly understand what we are saying.”", "정원사가 말했다. \"조금도 어긋나지 않습니다. 우리가 무슨 말을 하는지 알아들을 사람이 있을 리 없으니까요.\""]], [["“I have been told,”", "\"내가 듣기로는,\""], ["said the count, “that you do not always yourselves understand the signals you repeat.”", "백작이 말했다. \"자네들 자신도 옮겨 보내는 신호를 늘 알아듣는 건 아니라던데.\""]], [["“That is true, sir, and that is what I like best,”", "\"맞습니다, 선생. 바로 그 점이 제일 마음에 들지요.\""], ["said the man, smiling.", "사내가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Why do you like that best?”", "\"어째서 그 점이 제일 마음에 드나?\""]], [["“Because then I have no responsibility.", "\"그러면 제겐 아무 책임이 없으니까요."], ["I am a machine then, and nothing else, and so long as I work, nothing more is required of me.”", "그럴 때 저는 기계일 뿐, 그 이상도 아니지요. 그러니 제 일만 하면 그 이상은 아무것도 요구받지 않습니다.\""]], [["“Is it possible,” said Monte Cristo to himself, “that I can have met with a man that has no ambition?", "'야망이라곤 없는 사람을 만나다니, 이런 일이 있을 수 있나?' 몬테크리스토가 속으로 생각했다."], ["That would spoil my plans.”", "'그렇다면 내 계획이 어그러질 텐데.'"]], [["“Sir,”", "\"선생,\""], ["said the gardener, glancing at the sun-dial, “the ten minutes are almost up; I must return to my post.", "정원사가 해시계를 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"십 분이 거의 다 됐습니다. 제자리로 돌아가야겠습니다."], ["Will you go up with me?”", "저와 함께 올라가시겠습니까?\""]], [["“I follow you.”", "\"따라가겠네.\""]], [["Monte Cristo entered the tower, which was divided into three stories.", "몬테크리스토는 삼 층으로 나뉜 탑 안으로 들어갔다."], ["The tower contained implements, such as spades, rakes, watering-pots, hung against the wall; this was all the furniture.", "탑에는 삽, 갈퀴, 물뿌리개 같은 연장들이 벽에 걸려 있었는데, 그게 가구의 전부였다. 둘째 층은 사내가 늘 지내는 거처, 정확히는 잠자는 곳이었다."], ["The second was the man’s conventional abode, or rather sleeping-place; it contained a few poor articles of household furniture—a bed, a table, two chairs, a stone pitcher—and some dry herbs, hung up to the ceiling, which the count recognized as sweet peas, and of which the good man was preserving the seeds; he had labelled them with as much care as if he had been master botanist in the Jardin des Plantes.", "변변찮은 살림살이 몇 가지가 있었다—침대 하나, 탁자 하나, 의자 둘, 돌 항아리 하나—그리고 천장에 매달린 마른 풀 몇 다발이 있었는데, 백작은 그것이 스위트피임을 알아보았다. 사내는 그 씨를 받아 두고 있었다. 마치 식물원의 수석 식물학자라도 되는 양 정성껏 이름표까지 붙여 두었다."]], [["“Does it require much study to learn the art of telegraphing?” asked Monte Cristo.", "\"전신 기술을 익히려면 공부를 많이 해야 하나?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다."]], [["“The study does not take long; it was acting as a supernumerary that was so tedious.”", "\"공부야 오래 걸리지 않습니다. 견습 시절이 그렇게 지루했지요.\""]], [["“And what is the pay?”", "\"보수는 얼마나 받나?\""]], [["“A thousand francs, sir.”", "\"천 프랑입니다, 선생.\""]], [["“It is nothing.”", "\"별것 아니군.\""]], [["“No; but then we are lodged, as you perceive.”", "\"네, 하지만 보시다시피 거처는 마련해 주니까요.\""]], [["Monte Cristo looked at the room.", "몬테크리스토는 방을 둘러보았다."], ["They passed to the third story; it was the telegraph room.", "두 사람은 셋째 층으로 올라갔다. 전신실이었다."], ["Monte Cristo looked in turn at the two iron handles by which the machine was worked.", "몬테크리스토는 기계를 작동시키는 두 개의 쇠손잡이를 번갈아 살펴보았다."], ["“It is very interesting,”", "\"퍽 흥미롭군.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said, “but it must be very tedious for a lifetime.”", "\"하지만 평생 이 일을 하려면 무척 지루하겠어.\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["At first my neck was cramped with looking at it, but at the end of a year I became used to it; and then we have our hours of recreation, and our holidays.”", "처음에는 이걸 들여다보느라 목이 뻣뻣해졌지만, 한 해가 지나니 익숙해지더군요. 게다가 쉬는 시간도 있고, 휴일도 있고요.\""]], [["“Holidays?”", "\"휴일이?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“When?”", "\"언제?\""]], [["“When we have a fog.”", "\"안개가 낄 때요.\""]], [["“Ah, to be sure.”", "\"아, 그렇겠군.\""]], [["“Those are indeed holidays to me; I go into the garden, I plant, I prune, I trim, I kill the insects all day long.”", "\"그게 제겐 정말 휴일이지요. 정원에 나가서 씨를 심고, 가지를 치고, 다듬고, 하루 종일 벌레를 잡습니다.\""]], [["“How long have you been here?”", "\"여기 온 지는 얼마나 됐나?\""]], [["“Ten years, and five as a supernumerary make fifteen.”", "\"십 년에다, 견습으로 오 년을 더하면 십오 년입니다.\""]], [["“You are——”", "\"나이는——\""]], [["“Fifty-five years old.”", "\"쉰다섯입니다.\""]], [["“How long must you have served to claim the pension?”", "\"연금을 받으려면 얼마나 더 일해야 하나?\""]], [["“Oh, sir, twenty-five years.”", "\"아, 선생, 이십오 년이지요.\""]], [["“And how much is the pension?”", "\"연금은 얼마나 되고?\""]], [["“A hundred crowns.”", "\"백 에퀴입니다.\""]], [["“Poor humanity!”", "\"가엾은 인생이로군!\""], ["murmured Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“What did you say, sir?”", "\"뭐라고 하셨습니까, 선생?\""], ["asked the man.", "사내가 물었다."]], [["“I was saying it was very interesting.”", "\"무척 흥미롭다고 했네.\""]], [["“What was?” “All you were showing me.", "\"자네가 보여 준 이 모든 게 말이야."], ["And you really understand none of these signals?”", "그래서 자네는 이 신호들을 정말 하나도 모른단 말인가?\""]], [["“None at all.”", "\"전혀 모릅니다.\""]], [["“And have you never tried to understand them?”", "\"알아보려고 해 본 적도 없고?\""]], [["“Never.", "\"한 번도요."], ["Why should I?”", "그럴 까닭이 있겠습니까?\""]], [["“But still there are some signals only addressed to you.”", "\"하지만 그래도 자네한테만 보내는 신호가 더러 있을 텐데.\""]], [["“Certainly.”", "\"그야 있지요.\""]], [["“And do you understand them?”", "\"그건 알아듣고?\""]], [["“They are always the same.”", "\"늘 같은 것들이니까요.\""]], [["“And they mean——”", "\"그게 뜻하는 건——\""]], [["“‘_Nothing new; You have an hour;_’ or ‘_Tomorrow_.’”", "\"'새로운 것 없음. 한 시간 쉬어도 좋음.' 아니면 '내일.'이지요.\""]], [["“This is simple enough,”", "\"꽤 단순하군.\""], ["said the count; “but look, is not your correspondent putting itself in motion?”", "백작이 말했다. \"그런데 보게, 자네 상대편 기계가 움직이기 시작한 게 아닌가?\""]], [["“Ah, yes; thank you, sir.”", "\"아, 그렇군요. 고맙습니다, 선생.\""]], [["“And what is it saying—anything you understand?”", "\"무어라고 하는 건가—알아들을 수 있는 건가?\""]], [["“Yes; it asks if I am ready.”", "\"네. 준비됐느냐고 묻고 있습니다.\""]], [["“And you reply?”", "\"그럼 자네는 어떻게 답하나?\""]], [["“By the same sign, which, at the same time, tells my right-hand correspondent that I am ready, while it gives notice to my left-hand correspondent to prepare in his turn.”", "\"같은 신호로요. 그 신호는 동시에 오른쪽 상대편에게는 제가 준비됐다고 알리고, 왼쪽 상대편에게는 이번엔 그쪽이 준비하라고 일러 줍니다.\""]], [["“It is very ingenious,”", "\"아주 기발하군.\""], ["said the count.", "백작이 말했다."]], [["“You will see,”", "\"보십시오.\""], ["said the man proudly; “in five minutes he will speak.”", "사내가 자랑스럽게 말했다. \"오 분 뒤면 저쪽이 말을 할 겁니다.\""]], [["“I have, then, five minutes,”", "'그렇다면 내겐 오 분이 있는 셈이군.'"], ["said Monte Cristo to himself; “it is more time than I require.", "몬테크리스토가 속으로 생각했다. '필요한 것보다 넉넉한 시간이야.'"], ["My dear sir, will you allow me to ask you a question?”", "\"이보게, 선생, 한 가지 물어봐도 되겠나?\""]], [["“What is it, sir?”", "\"무엇입니까, 선생?\""]], [["“You are fond of gardening?”", "\"자네는 정원 가꾸는 걸 좋아하지?\""]], [["“Passionately.”", "\"끔찍이 좋아하지요.\""]], [["“And you would be pleased to have, instead of this terrace of twenty feet, an enclosure of two acres?”", "\"그럼 이 스무 자짜리 좁은 터 대신, 두 에이커짜리 울타리를 갖게 된다면 기쁘겠나?\""]], [["“Sir, I should make a terrestrial paradise of it.”", "\"선생, 그렇다면 지상 낙원을 만들어 보일 겁니다.\""]], [["“You live badly on your thousand francs?”", "\"천 프랑으로는 살림이 옹색하지?\""]], [["“Badly enough; but yet I do live.”", "\"꽤 옹색하지요. 그래도 그럭저럭 살아갑니다.\""]], [["“Yes; but you have a wretchedly small garden.”", "\"그래, 하지만 자네 정원은 형편없이 작잖나.\""]], [["“True, the garden is not large.”", "\"맞습니다, 정원이 크진 않지요.\""]], [["“And, then, such as it is, it is filled with dormice, who eat everything.”", "\"게다가 그나마도 모든 걸 갉아먹는 겨울잠쥐로 가득하고.\""]], [["“Ah, they are my scourges.”", "\"아, 그놈들이 제 재앙이지요.\""]], [["“Tell me, should you have the misfortune to turn your head while your right-hand correspondent was telegraphing——”", "\"한 가지 묻겠네, 만일 운 나쁘게도 오른쪽 상대편이 신호를 보내는 동안 자네가 고개를 돌렸다면——\""]], [["“I should not see him.”", "\"못 보겠지요.\""]], [["“Then what would happen?”", "\"그럼 어떻게 되나?\""]], [["“I could not repeat the signals.”", "\"신호를 옮길 수 없게 됩니다.\""]], [["“And then?”", "\"그러면?\""]], [["“Not having repeated them, through negligence, I should be fined.”", "\"신호를 옮기지 않으면, 부주의로 벌금을 물게 되지요.\""]], [["“How much?”", "\"얼마나?\""]], [["“A hundred francs.”", "\"백 프랑입니다.\""]], [["“The tenth of your income—that would be fine work.”", "\"수입의 십분의 일이라—그것 참 대단한 일이로군.\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["said the man.", "사내가 말했다."]], [["“Has it ever happened to you?”", "\"자네한테 그런 일이 있었던 적이 있나?\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“Once, sir, when I was grafting a rose-tree.”", "\"한 번 있었습니다, 선생. 장미나무를 접붙이고 있을 때였지요.\""]], [["“Well, suppose you were to alter a signal, and substitute another?”", "\"자, 그렇다면 만일 신호 하나를 바꿔서 다른 걸로 대신 보낸다면 어떻게 되나?\""]], [["“Ah, that is another case; I should be turned off, and lose my pension.”", "\"아, 그건 다른 얘기지요. 쫓겨나고 연금도 잃습니다.\""]], [["“Three hundred francs?”", "\"삼백 프랑을?\""]], [["“A hundred crowns, yes, sir; so you see that I am not likely to do any of these things.”", "\"백 에퀴지요, 선생. 그러니 보시다시피 저는 그런 짓을 할 사람이 아닙니다.\""]], [["“Not even for fifteen years’ wages?", "\"십오 년치 봉급을 준대도 안 한단 말인가?"], ["Come, it is worth thinking about?”", "자, 한번 생각해 볼 만하지 않나?\""]], [["“For fifteen thousand francs?”", "\"만 오천 프랑을요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["“Sir, you alarm me.”", "\"선생, 저를 놀라게 하시는군요.\""], ["“Nonsense.”", "\"별소릴 다 하는군.\""]], [["“Sir, you are tempting me?”", "\"선생, 지금 저를 떠보시는 겁니까?\""]], [["“Just so; fifteen thousand francs, do you understand?”", "\"바로 그렇지. 만 오천 프랑이야, 알아듣겠나?\""]], [["“Sir, let me see my right-hand correspondent.”", "\"선생, 오른쪽 상대편 신호 좀 보게 해 주십시오.\""]], [["“On the contrary, do not look at him, but at this.”", "\"그 반대일세, 그쪽은 보지 말고 이걸 보게.\""]], [["“What is it?”", "\"이게 뭡니까?\""]], [["“What? Do you not know these bits of paper?”", "\"뭐냐고? 자네 이 종잇장들을 모르나?\""]], [["“Bank-notes!”", "\"지폐로군요!\""]], [["“Exactly; there are fifteen of them.”", "\"바로 그렇지. 열다섯 장이 있네.\""]], [["“And whose are they?”", "\"이게 누구 겁니까?\""]], [["“Yours, if you like.”", "\"원한다면 자네 거지.\""]], [["“Mine?” exclaimed the man, half-suffocated.", "\"제 거라고요?\" 사내가 숨이 막힐 듯 외쳤다."]], [["“Yes; yours—your own property.”", "\"그래, 자네 거—자네 소유 말일세.\""]], [["“Sir, my right-hand correspondent is signalling.”", "\"선생, 오른쪽 상대편이 신호를 보내고 있습니다.\""]], [["“Let him signal.”", "\"보내라고 두게.\""]], [["“Sir, you have distracted me; I shall be fined.”", "\"선생, 정신을 빼놓으시는군요. 저 벌금 물게 생겼습니다.\""]], [["“That will cost you a hundred francs; you see it is your interest to take my bank-notes.”", "\"그래 봐야 백 프랑이지. 보게나, 내 지폐를 받는 게 자네한테 이득이라니까.\""]], [["“Sir, my right-hand correspondent redoubles his signals; he is impatient.”", "\"선생, 오른쪽 상대편이 신호를 더 빨리 보냅니다. 안달이 났어요.\""]], [["“Never mind—take these;”", "\"신경 쓰지 말고—이걸 받게.\""], ["and the count placed the packet in the man’s hands.", "백작이 사내의 손에 지폐 뭉치를 쥐여 주었다."], ["“Now this is not all,”", "\"자, 이게 다가 아니야.\""], ["he said; “you cannot live upon your fifteen thousand francs.”", "그가 말했다. \"만 오천 프랑으로는 살아갈 수 없을 테니.\""]], [["“I shall still have my place.”", "\"제 자리는 그대로 있지 않습니까.\""]], [["“No, you will lose it, for you are going to alter your correspondent’s message.”", "\"아니, 잃게 될 걸세. 자네가 상대편의 전언을 바꿀 테니까.\""]], [["“Oh, sir, what are you proposing?”", "\"오, 선생, 무슨 말씀을 하시는 겁니까?\""]], [["“A jest.”", "\"장난일세.\""]], [["“Sir, unless you force me——”", "\"선생, 억지로 시키시지 않는 한——\""]], [["“I think I can effectually force you;”", "\"내가 자네를 단단히 시킬 수 있을 것 같은데.\""], ["and Monte Cristo drew another packet from his pocket.", "그러면서 몬테크리스토는 주머니에서 또 다른 뭉치를 꺼냈다."], ["“Here are ten thousand more francs,”", "\"여기 만 프랑이 더 있네.\""], ["he said, “with the fifteen thousand already in your pocket, they will make twenty-five thousand.", "그가 말했다. \"이미 자네 주머니에 든 만 오천 프랑과 합치면 이만 오천 프랑이 되지."], ["With five thousand you can buy a pretty little house with two acres of land; the remaining twenty thousand will bring you in a thousand francs a year.”", "오천이면 두 에이커짜리 땅이 딸린 예쁜 작은 집을 살 수 있고, 남은 이만 프랑은 자네에게 해마다 천 프랑을 안겨 줄 걸세.\""]], [["“A garden with two acres of land!”", "\"두 에이커짜리 땅이 딸린 정원이라!\""]], [["“And a thousand francs a year.”", "\"게다가 해마다 천 프랑씩.\""]], [["“Oh, heavens!”", "\"오, 하느님!\""]], [["“Come, take them,”", "\"자, 받게.\""], ["and Monte Cristo forced the bank-notes into his hand.", "몬테크리스토가 지폐를 사내의 손에 억지로 쥐여 주었다."]], [["“What am I to do?”", "\"제가 뭘 해야 합니까?\""]], [["“Nothing very difficult.”", "\"그리 어려울 것 없네.\""]], [["“But what is it?”", "\"하지만 뭡니까?\""]], [["“To repeat these signs.”", "\"이 신호들을 옮기는 거지.\""], ["Monte Cristo took a paper from his pocket, upon which were drawn three signs, with numbers to indicate the order in which they were to be worked.", "몬테크리스토가 주머니에서 종이 한 장을 꺼냈는데, 거기에는 세 가지 신호가 그려져 있고, 작동시킬 순서를 가리키는 번호가 적혀 있었다."]], [["“There, you see it will not take long.”", "\"보게, 오래 걸리지 않을 걸세.\""]], [["“Yes; but——”", "\"네, 하지만——\""]], [["“Do this, and you will have nectarines and all the rest.”", "\"이대로만 하게, 그러면 천도복숭아도, 나머지 다른 것도 다 갖게 될 테니.\""]], [["The shot told; red with fever, while the large drops fell from his brow, the man executed, one after the other, the three signs given by the count, in spite of the frightful contortions of the right-hand correspondent, who, not understanding the change, began to think the gardener had gone mad.", "화살이 적중했다. 사내는 열에 들떠 얼굴이 벌게진 채, 이마에서 굵은 땀방울을 뚝뚝 흘리며 백작이 일러 준 세 가지 신호를 차례차례 작동시켰다. 그 와중에 오른쪽 상대편은 영문을 모른 채 끔찍하게 몸을 비틀어 댔으니, 그는 정원사가 미치기라도 한 줄 알기 시작했다."], ["As to the left-hand one, he conscientiously repeated the same signals, which were finally transmitted to the Minister of the Interior.", "한편 왼쪽 상대편은 그 신호들을 충실히 그대로 옮겼고, 신호는 마침내 내무부 장관에게까지 전해졌다."]], [["“Now you are rich,”", "\"이제 자네는 부자일세.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["replied the man, “but at what a price!”", "사내가 대답했다. \"하지만 무슨 대가를 치르고서요!\""]], [["“Listen, friend,”", "\"잘 듣게, 친구.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["“I do not wish to cause you any remorse; believe me, then, when I swear to you that you have wronged no man, but on the contrary have benefited mankind.”", "\"나는 자네가 양심의 가책을 느끼게 할 생각이 없네. 그러니 내 맹세를 믿게. 자네는 아무에게도 해를 끼치지 않았고, 오히려 인류에게 이로운 일을 한 거라네.\""]], [["The man looked at the bank-notes, felt them, counted them, turned pale, then red, then rushed into his room to drink a glass of water, but he had no time to reach the water-jug, and fainted in the midst of his dried herbs.", "사내는 지폐를 바라보고, 만져 보고, 세어 보더니, 새파래졌다가 새빨개졌고, 이내 물 한 잔을 마시러 제 방으로 달려갔다. 그러나 물 항아리에 미처 닿기도 전에 마른 풀 다발들 한가운데서 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌다."], ["Five minutes after the new telegram reached the minister, Debray had the horses put to his carriage, and drove to Danglars’ house.", "새 전보가 장관에게 닿고 오 분 뒤, 드브레는 마차에 말을 매게 하고는 당글라르의 집으로 달려갔다."]], [["“Has your husband any Spanish bonds?”", "\"부군께서 에스파냐 채권을 갖고 계십니까?\""], ["he asked of the baroness.", "그가 남작 부인에게 물었다."]], [["“I think so, indeed! He has six millions’ worth.”", "\"네, 그럼요! 육백만 프랑어치나 갖고 계신걸요.\""]], [["“He must sell them at whatever price.”", "\"무슨 값에라도 당장 파셔야 합니다.\""]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜요?\""]], [["“Because Don Carlos has fled from Bourges, and has returned to Spain.” 30203m", "\"돈 카를로스가 부르주에서 달아나 에스파냐로 돌아갔거든요.\""]], [["“How do you know?” Debray shrugged his shoulders.", "\"그걸 어떻게 아세요?\" 드브레가 어깨를 으쓱했다."]], [["“The idea of asking how I hear the news,”", "\"내가 그 소식을 어떻게 듣는지 물으시다니요.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["The baroness did not wait for a repetition; she ran to her husband, who immediately hastened to his agent, and ordered him to sell at any price.", "남작 부인은 두 번 말하길 기다리지 않았다. 그녀는 남편에게로 달려갔고, 남편은 곧장 중개인에게 달려가 값을 가리지 말고 팔라고 지시했다."], ["When it was seen that Danglars sold, the Spanish funds fell directly.", "당글라르가 판다는 사실이 알려지자 에스파냐 공채는 곧바로 떨어졌다."], ["Danglars lost five hundred thousand francs; but he rid himself of all his Spanish shares.", "당글라르는 오십만 프랑을 잃었지만, 가진 에스파냐 주식을 모조리 처분했다."], ["The same evening the following was read in _Le Messager_:", "그날 저녁 르 메사제에는 다음과 같은 기사가 실렸다."]], [["“[By telegraph.] The king, Don Carlos, has escaped the vigilance of his guardians at Bourges, and has returned to Spain by the Catalonian frontier.", "\"[전신 발신] 국왕 돈 카를로스가 부르주에서 감시자들의 눈을 피해 빠져나가, 카탈루냐 국경을 통해 에스파냐로 돌아갔다."], ["Barcelona has risen in his favor.”", "바르셀로나가 그를 지지하며 봉기했다.\""]], [["All that evening nothing was spoken of but the foresight of Danglars, who had sold his shares, and of the luck of the stock-jobber, who only lost five hundred thousand francs by such a blow.", "그날 저녁 내내 사람들은 주식을 미리 팔아 치운 당글라르의 선견지명과, 그런 일격을 받고도 고작 오십만 프랑밖에 잃지 않은 그 증권 투기꾼의 행운에 대해서만 떠들어 댔다."], ["Those who had kept their shares, or bought those of Danglars, looked upon themselves as ruined, and passed a very bad night.", "주식을 그대로 쥐고 있던 사람들이나, 당글라르의 주식을 사들인 사람들은 스스로 파산했다고 여기며 몹시 괴로운 밤을 보냈다."], ["Next morning _Le Moniteur_ contained the following:", "이튿날 아침 르 모니퇴르에는 다음과 같은 기사가 실렸다."]], [["“It was without any foundation that _Le Messager_ yesterday announced the flight of Don Carlos and the revolt of Barcelona.", "\"어제 르 메사제가 보도한 돈 카를로스의 도주와 바르셀로나의 봉기는 아무런 근거가 없는 것이었다."], ["The king (Don Carlos) has not left Bourges, and the peninsula is in the enjoyment of profound peace.", "국왕(돈 카를로스)은 부르주를 떠나지 않았으며, 반도는 깊은 평화를 누리고 있다."], ["A telegraphic signal, improperly interpreted, owing to the fog, was the cause of this error.”", "안개 탓에 잘못 해석된 전신 신호 하나가 이 오보의 원인이었다.\""]], [["The funds rose one per cent higher than before they had fallen.", "공채는 떨어지기 전보다 일 퍼센트나 더 올랐다."], ["This, reckoning his loss, and what he had missed gaining, made the difference of a million to Danglars.", "손실에다 놓친 이익까지 합치면, 이는 당글라르에게 백만 프랑의 차이가 났다."]], [["“Good,”", "\"좋아.\""], ["said Monte Cristo to Morrel, who was at his house when the news arrived of the strange reverse of fortune of which Danglars had been the victim, “I have just made a discovery for twenty-five thousand francs, for which I would have paid a hundred thousand.”", "당글라르가 희생양이 된 이 기묘한 운명의 역전 소식이 전해졌을 때 자기 집에 와 있던 모렐에게 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"방금 이만 오천 프랑으로 한 가지 발견을 했는데, 십만 프랑을 치르고서라도 알아낼 만한 거였지.\""]], [["“What have you discovered?”", "\"무엇을 발견하셨는데요?\""], ["asked Morrel.", "모렐이 물었다."]], [["“I have just discovered how a gardener may get rid of the dormice that eat his peaches.”", "\"정원사가 복숭아를 갉아먹는 겨울잠쥐를 쫓아내는 법을 방금 발견했다네.\""]], [["# Chapter 62. Ghosts", "# Chapter 62. Ghosts"]], [["At first sight, the exterior of the house at Auteuil gave no indications of splendor, nothing one would expect from the destined residence of the magnificent Count of Monte Cristo; but this simplicity was according to the will of its master, who positively ordered nothing to be altered outside.", "처음 보기에 오퇴유 저택의 외관은 화려함의 기색이라곤 없었다. 위풍당당한 몬테크리스토 백작의 거처로 점지된 곳이라면 누구나 기대할 법한 그 무엇도 없었다. 그러나 이 소박함은 집주인의 뜻이었으니, 그는 바깥은 무엇 하나 손대지 말라고 단단히 일러두었던 것이다."], ["The splendor was within.", "화려함은 안에 있었다."], ["Indeed, almost before the door opened, the scene changed.", "정말이지, 문이 채 열리기도 전에 풍경은 바뀌었다."]], [["M. Bertuccio had outdone himself in the taste displayed in furnishing, and in the rapidity with which it was executed.", "베르투치오는 집을 꾸미는 안목에서나, 그 일을 해치우는 속도에서나 자기 자신을 능가했다."], ["It is told that the Duc d’Antin removed in a single night a whole avenue of trees that annoyed Louis XIV.; in three days M.", "당탱 공작이 루이 14세의 심기를 거스르던 가로수길 전체를 단 하룻밤 사이에 옮겨 놓았다는 이야기가 전해 온다."], ["Bertuccio planted an entirely bare court with poplars, large spreading sycamores to shade the different parts of the house, and in the foreground, instead of the usual paving-stones, half hidden by the grass, there extended a lawn but that morning laid down, and upon which the water was yet glistening.", "베르투치오는 사흘 만에 휑하던 안마당에 포플러를 심고, 집의 이곳저곳을 그늘지게 할 가지 넓게 뻗은 플라타너스를 들였다. 그리고 앞뜰에는 흔히 보이는, 풀에 반쯤 가린 포석 대신 그날 아침에야 깐 잔디밭이 펼쳐져 있었고, 그 위에는 아직 물기가 반짝였다."], ["For the rest, the orders had been issued by the count; he himself had given a plan to Bertuccio, marking the spot where each tree was to be planted, and the shape and extent of the lawn which was to take the place of the paving-stones.", "나머지 지시는 모두 백작이 직접 내린 것이었다. 그는 직접 도면을 그려 베르투치오에게 건넸고, 나무를 한 그루씩 어디에 심을지, 포석을 대신할 잔디밭의 모양과 넓이는 어떠해야 할지를 표시해 두었다."]], [["Thus the house had become unrecognizable, and Bertuccio himself declared that he scarcely knew it, encircled as it was by a framework of trees.", "이리하여 집은 알아볼 수 없게 변했고, 베르투치오 자신조차 나무 틀에 둘러싸인 그 집을 거의 알아보지 못하겠다고 말했다."], ["The overseer would not have objected, while he was about it, to have made some improvements in the garden, but the count had positively forbidden it to be touched.", "집사는 이왕 손을 댄 김에 정원에도 몇 가지 손질을 더하고 싶었으나, 백작은 정원만은 절대 건드리지 말라고 단단히 못 박았다."], ["Bertuccio made amends, however, by loading the antechambers, staircases, and mantle-pieces with flowers.", "대신 베르투치오는 대기실과 계단, 벽난로 선반마다 꽃을 가득 채우는 것으로 아쉬움을 달랬다."]], [["What, above all, manifested the shrewdness of the steward, and the profound science of the master, the one in carrying out the ideas of the other, was that this house which appeared only the night before so sad and gloomy, impregnated with that sickly smell one can almost fancy to be the smell of time, had in a single day acquired the aspect of life, was scented with its master’s favorite perfumes, and had the very light regulated according to his wish.", "무엇보다도 집사의 영민함과 주인의 깊은 안목을, 하나가 다른 하나의 뜻을 구현해 내는 솜씨를 여실히 드러낸 것은, 바로 전날 밤만 해도 그토록 음울하고 침침하던 이 집이, 거의 세월의 냄새라 여길 만한 그 병적인 냄새에 절어 있던 이 집이, 단 하루 만에 생기를 띤 모습으로 바뀌어, 주인이 좋아하는 향기를 풍기고, 빛마저 그의 뜻에 맞게 조절되어 있다는 점이었다. 백작이 도착하자, 손이 닿는 곳에 그의 책과 무기가 놓여 있었고, 눈길은 그가 아끼는 그림에 머물렀다."], ["When the count arrived, he had under his touch his books and arms, his eyes rested upon his favorite pictures; his dogs, whose caresses he loved, welcomed him in the antechamber; the birds, whose songs delighted him, cheered him with their music; and the house, awakened from its long sleep, like the sleeping beauty in the wood, lived, sang, and bloomed like the houses we have long cherished, and in which, when we are forced to leave them, we leave a part of our souls.", "그가 쓰다듬기를 즐기던 개들이 대기실에서 그를 반겼고, 그가 노래를 사랑하던 새들이 그 음악으로 그를 즐겁게 해 주었다. 그리고 이 집은 긴 잠에서 깨어난 숲속의 잠자는 미녀처럼 살아 숨 쉬고, 노래하고, 꽃피웠다. 오래도록 정들었던, 그래서 떠나야 할 때면 영혼의 한 조각을 두고 가게 되는 그런 집들처럼."]], [["The servants passed gayly along the fine courtyard; some, belonging to the kitchens, gliding down the stairs, restored but the previous day, as if they had always inhabited the house; others filling the coach-houses, where the equipages, encased and numbered, appeared to have been installed for the last fifty years; and in the stables the horses replied with neighs to the grooms, who spoke to them with much more respect than many servants pay their masters.", "하인들은 멋진 안마당을 따라 흥겹게 오갔다. 주방에 속한 이들은 바로 전날 새로 손본 계단을 미끄러지듯 내려갔다. 마치 늘 그 집에서 살아온 것 같았다. 다른 이들은 마차고를 채웠는데, 덮개를 씌우고 번호를 매긴 마차들은 마치 지난 오십 년 동안 그 자리에 있어 온 듯했다. 마구간에서는 말들이 마부의 말에 울음소리로 답했고, 그 마부들은 어지간한 하인들이 주인을 대하는 것보다 훨씬 더 정중하게 말들에게 말을 걸었다."]], [["The library was divided into two parts on either side of the wall, and contained upwards of two thousand volumes; one division was entirely devoted to novels, and even the volume which had been published but the day before was to be seen in its place in all the dignity of its red and gold binding.", "서재는 벽을 사이에 두고 양쪽으로 나뉘어 있었고, 이천 권이 넘는 책을 갖추고 있었다. 한쪽은 통째로 소설에 바쳐져 있었는데, 바로 전날 출간된 책마저도 붉은빛과 금빛 장정의 위엄을 온전히 갖춘 채 제자리에 꽂혀 있었다."]], [["On the other side of the house, to match with the library, was the conservatory, ornamented with rare flowers, that bloomed in china jars; and in the midst of the greenhouse, marvellous alike to sight and smell, was a billiard-table which looked as if it had been abandoned during the past hour by players who had left the balls on the cloth.", "집의 반대편에는 서재와 짝을 이루듯 온실이 있었고, 그곳에는 도자기 항아리에 핀 진귀한 꽃들이 장식되어 있었다. 보기에도 향기에도 경탄스러운 그 온실 한가운데에는 당구대가 놓여 있었는데, 마치 지난 한 시간 전쯤 천 위에 공을 놓아둔 채 떠나 버린 사람들의 흔적인 양 보였다."]], [["One chamber alone had been respected by the magnificent Bertuccio.", "오직 한 방만은 그 위풍당당한 베르투치오의 손길을 면했다."], ["Before this room, to which you could ascend by the grand, and go out by the back staircase, the servants passed with curiosity, and Bertuccio with terror.", "큰 계단으로 올라가 뒷계단으로 나오게 되어 있는 이 방 앞을, 하인들은 호기심에 차서 지나갔고, 베르투치오는 공포에 질려 지나갔다."]], [["At five o’clock precisely, the count arrived before the house at Auteuil, followed by Ali.", "정각 다섯 시에 백작이 알리를 데리고 오퇴유 저택 앞에 도착했다."], ["Bertuccio was awaiting this arrival with impatience, mingled with uneasiness; he hoped for some compliments, while, at the same time, he feared to have frowns.", "베르투치오는 불안이 뒤섞인 초조함으로 그의 도착을 기다리고 있었다. 그는 칭찬을 바라면서도, 동시에 찌푸린 얼굴을 마주할까 두려워했다."], ["Monte Cristo descended into the courtyard, walked all over the house, without giving any sign of approbation or pleasure, until he entered his bedroom, situated on the opposite side to the closed room; then he approached a little piece of furniture, made of rosewood, which he had noticed at a previous visit.", "몬테크리스토는 안마당으로 내려와 흡족하다거나 즐겁다는 기색 한 번 보이지 않은 채 집 안을 두루 돌아보았다. 그러다 닫힌 방의 맞은편에 있는 자신의 침실로 들어서서는, 지난번 방문 때 눈여겨봐 두었던 자단목으로 만든 작은 가구 하나로 다가갔다."]], [["“That can only be to hold gloves,”", "\"이건 장갑을 넣어 두는 것밖에 안 되겠군.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Will your excellency deign to open it?”", "\"각하, 한번 열어 보시지요.\""], ["said the delighted Bertuccio, “and you will find gloves in it.”", "기뻐 어쩔 줄 모르며 베르투치오가 말했다. \"장갑이 들어 있을 겁니다.\""]], [["Elsewhere the count found everything he required—smelling-bottles, cigars, knick-knacks.", "다른 곳에서도 백작은 필요한 것을 모두 발견했다. 향수병, 시가, 자질구레한 장식품까지."]], [["30207m\n“Good,”", "\"좋아.\""], ["he said; and M.", "그가 말했다."], ["Bertuccio left enraptured, so great, so powerful, and real was the influence exercised by this man over all who surrounded him.", "그러자 베르투치오는 황홀해하며 물러갔다. 이 사람이 주위의 모든 이에게 미치는 영향력은 그토록 크고, 그토록 강하며, 그토록 실재하는 것이었다."]], [["At precisely six o’clock the clatter of horses’ hoofs was heard at the entrance door; it was our captain of Spahis, who had arrived on Médéah.", "정각 여섯 시에 현관 앞에서 말발굽 소리가 요란하게 들려왔다. 메데아를 타고 도착한, 우리의 스파히 대위였다. \"내가 제일 먼저 온 게 분명하군요.\""], ["“I am sure I am the first,”", "모렐이 외쳤다."], ["cried Morrel; “I did it on purpose to have you a minute to myself, before everyone came.", "\"다들 오기 전에 백작님과 단둘이 잠깐 있고 싶어서 일부러 그랬습니다."], ["Julie and Emmanuel have a thousand things to tell you.", "쥘리와 에마뉘엘이 백작님께 할 이야기가 산더미예요."], ["Ah, really this is magnificent!", "아, 정말이지 굉장하군요!"], ["But tell me, count, will your people take care of my horse?”", "그런데 백작님, 댁의 사람들이 제 말을 돌봐 줄까요?\""]], [["“Do not alarm yourself, my dear Maximilian—they understand.”", "\"걱정 말게, 막시밀리앙. 다들 알아서 할 걸세.\""]], [["“I mean, because he wants petting.", "\"녀석이 보살핌을 받아야 해서요."], ["If you had seen at what a pace he came—like the wind!”", "어찌나 빨리 달려왔는지 보셨어야 하는데요. 바람 같았답니다!\""]], [["“I should think so,—a horse that cost 5,000 francs!”", "\"그럴 만하지. 오천 프랑이나 한 말 아닌가!\""], ["said Monte Cristo, in the tone which a father would use towards a son.", "몬테크리스토가 아버지가 아들에게 쓰는 듯한 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Do you regret them?”", "\"그게 아깝습니까?\""], ["asked Morrel, with his open laugh.", "모렐이 환하게 웃으며 물었다."]], [["“I? Certainly not,”", "\"내가? 천만에.\""], ["replied the count. “No; I should only regret if the horse had not proved good.”", "백작이 대답했다. \"아니지. 그 말이 좋은 말이 아니었다면 아까웠겠지만 말일세.\""]], [["“It is so good, that I have distanced M. de Château-Renaud, one of the best riders in France, and M.", "\"어찌나 좋은 말인지, 프랑스 최고의 기수 중 하나인 샤토르노 씨와 데브레 씨를 따돌렸답니다."], ["Debray, who both mount the minister’s Arabians; and close on their heels are the horses of Madame Danglars, who always go at six leagues an hour.”", "둘 다 장관의 아라비아 말을 탔는데 말이지요. 그 뒤를 바짝 쫓아온 게 당글라르 부인의 말들인데, 늘 시속 육 리외로 달리는 녀석들이지요.\""]], [["“Then they follow you?”", "\"그럼 자네를 따라온 건가?\""], ["asked Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 물었다."]], [["“See, they are here.”", "\"보십시오, 도착했군요.\""], ["And at the same minute a carriage with smoking horses, accompanied by two mounted gentlemen, arrived at the gate, which opened before them.", "바로 그 순간, 김이 무럭무럭 나는 말들이 끄는 마차 한 대가, 말을 탄 두 신사를 거느린 채 정문 앞에 다다랐고, 문이 그들 앞에서 열렸다."], ["The carriage drove round, and stopped at the steps, followed by the horsemen.", "마차는 빙 돌아와 계단 앞에 멈춰 섰고, 말 탄 이들이 그 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["The instant Debray had touched the ground, he was at the carriage-door.", "데브레는 땅에 발을 딛는 순간 마차 문 앞에 가 있었다."], ["He offered his hand to the baroness, who, descending, took it with a peculiarity of manner imperceptible to everyone but Monte Cristo.", "그가 남작 부인에게 손을 내밀자, 부인은 마차에서 내리며 그 손을 잡았다. 그 잡는 태도에는 몬테크리스토 외에는 누구도 알아채지 못할 어떤 미묘함이 있었다."], ["But nothing escaped the count’s notice, and he observed a little note, passed with the facility that indicates frequent practice, from the hand of Madame Danglars to that of the minister’s secretary.", "그러나 백작의 눈을 벗어나는 것은 아무것도 없었다. 그는 잦은 연습에서 비롯된 듯한 능숙한 손놀림으로, 당글라르 부인의 손에서 장관 비서의 손으로 작은 쪽지 하나가 건네지는 것을 보았다."]], [["After his wife the banker descended, as pale as though he had issued from his tomb instead of his carriage.", "아내에 이어 은행가가 내렸는데, 마차가 아니라 무덤에서 나오기라도 한 듯 핼쑥한 얼굴이었다."]], [["Madame Danglars threw a rapid and inquiring glance which could only be interpreted by Monte Cristo, around the courtyard, over the peristyle, and across the front of the house, then, repressing a slight emotion, which must have been seen on her countenance if she had not kept her color, she ascended the steps, saying to Morrel:", "당글라르 부인은 몬테크리스토만이 해석할 수 있을 빠르고 캐묻는 듯한 시선을 안마당에, 열주 회랑에, 그리고 집의 정면에 던졌다. 그러고는 가벼운 동요를 억눌렀는데, 만약 그녀가 안색을 잃지 않으려고 애쓰지 않았더라면 그 동요는 얼굴에 드러나고 말았을 것이다. 그녀는 계단을 오르며 모렐에게 말했다."]], [["“Sir, if you were a friend of mine, I should ask you if you would sell your horse.”", "\"선생님, 제 친구라면 그 말을 파실 의향이 있는지 여쭤보고 싶군요.\""]], [["Morrel smiled with an expression very like a grimace, and then turned round to Monte Cristo, as if to ask him to extricate him from his embarrassment.", "모렐은 찡그림에 가까운 표정으로 미소 지었고, 곤란한 처지에서 빼내 달라는 듯 몬테크리스토 쪽으로 돌아섰다."], ["The count understood him.", "백작은 그 뜻을 알아챘다."]], [["“Ah, madame,”", "\"아, 부인.\""], ["he said, “why did you not make that request of me?”", "그가 말했다. \"어찌하여 제게 그 청을 하지 않으셨습니까?\""]], [["“With you, sir,”", "\"백작님께는요.\""], ["replied the baroness, “one can wish for nothing, one is so sure to obtain it.", "남작 부인이 대답했다. \"무엇 하나 바랄 수가 없답니다. 너무도 확실히 얻게 되니까요."], ["If it were so with M. Morrel——”", "모렐 씨도 그랬더라면……\""]], [["“Unfortunately,”", "\"안타깝게도,\""], ["replied the count, “I am witness that M.", "백작이 대답했다."], ["Morrel cannot give up his horse, his honor being engaged in keeping it.”", "\"모렐 씨가 그 말을 내줄 수 없다는 건 제가 증인입니다. 그 말을 지키는 데 그의 명예가 걸려 있으니까요.\""]], [["“How so?”", "\"어째서요?\""]], [["“He laid a wager he would tame Médéah in the space of six months.", "\"그는 육 개월 안에 메데아를 길들이겠다고 내기를 걸었습니다. 그러니 이제 아시겠지요, 정해진 기한 전에 그 짐승을 처분한다면 내기에 질 뿐만 아니라, 사람들이 그가 겁을 먹었다고 수군댈 겁니다."], ["You understand now that if he were to get rid of the animal before the time named, he would not only lose his bet, but people would say he was afraid; and a brave captain of Spahis cannot risk this, even to gratify a pretty woman, which is, in my opinion, one of the most sacred obligations in the world.”", "용감한 스파히 대위는 그런 위험을 무릅쓸 수 없지요. 어여쁜 여인을 기쁘게 해 드리기 위해서라도 말입니다. 제 생각에 그건 세상에서 가장 신성한 의무 가운데 하나입니다만.\""]], [["“You see my position, madame,”", "\"제 입장을 아시겠지요, 부인.\""], ["said Morrel, bestowing a grateful smile on Monte Cristo.", "모렐이 몬테크리스토에게 고마운 미소를 보내며 말했다."]], [["“It seems to me,”", "\"내가 보기엔,\""], ["said Danglars, in his coarse tone, ill-concealed by a forced smile, “that you have already got horses enough.”", "당글라르가 억지웃음으로도 잘 가려지지 않는 거친 어조로 말했다. \"당신은 이미 말이 충분히 많지 않소.\""]], [["Madame Danglars seldom allowed remarks of this kind to pass unnoticed, but, to the surprise of the young people, she pretended not to hear it, and said nothing.", "당글라르 부인은 이런 종류의 말을 그냥 넘기는 일이 좀처럼 없었지만, 젊은이들이 놀랍게도 그녀는 못 들은 척하며 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["Monte Cristo smiled at her unusual humility, and showed her two immense porcelain jars, over which wound marine plants, of a size and delicacy that nature alone could produce.", "몬테크리스토는 그녀의 보기 드문 겸손에 미소 지었고, 두 개의 거대한 자기 항아리를 보여 주었다. 그 위로는 자연만이 빚어낼 수 있을 크기와 섬세함을 지닌 해초가 휘감겨 있었다."], ["The baroness was astonished.", "남작 부인은 깜짝 놀랐다."]], [["“Why,”", "\"세상에.\""], ["said she, “you could plant one of the chestnut-trees in the Tuileries inside!", "그녀가 말했다. \"튈르리 정원의 밤나무 한 그루를 안에 심을 수도 있겠어요!"], ["How can such enormous jars have been manufactured?”", "이렇게 거대한 항아리를 어떻게 만들 수 있었을까요?\""]], [["“Ah! madame,”", "\"아! 부인.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo, “you must not ask of us, the manufacturers of fine porcelain, such a question.", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"고급 자기를 만드는 우리에게 그런 질문을 하시면 안 됩니다."], ["It is the work of another age, constructed by the genii of earth and water.”", "이건 다른 시대의 작품으로, 흙과 물의 정령들이 빚은 것이니까요.\""]], [["“How so?—at what period can that have been?”", "\"어째서요? 대체 어느 시대에 만들어졌단 말입니까?\""]], [["“I do not know; I have only heard that an emperor of China had an oven built expressly, and that in this oven twelve jars like this were successively baked.", "\"저도 모릅니다. 다만 중국의 어느 황제가 특별히 가마를 짓게 했고, 그 가마에서 이런 항아리 열두 개를 차례로 구워 냈다는 이야기만 들었습니다."], ["Two broke, from the heat of the fire; the other ten were sunk three hundred fathoms deep into the sea.", "그중 둘은 불의 열기로 깨졌고, 나머지 열은 바다 속 삼백 길 깊이에 가라앉혔답니다."], ["The sea, knowing what was required of her, threw over them her weeds, encircled them with coral, and encrusted them with shells; the whole was cemented by two hundred years beneath these almost impervious depths, for a revolution carried away the emperor who wished to make the trial, and only left the documents proving the manufacture of the jars and their descent into the sea.", "바다는 자기에게 무엇이 요구되는지 알았던지, 그 위에 해초를 덮고, 산호로 둘러싸고, 조개껍데기로 입혀 주었습니다. 그 모든 것은 거의 빛도 들지 않는 그 깊은 곳에서 이백 년 세월에 의해 굳어졌지요. 이 시도를 해 보고자 했던 황제를 혁명이 휩쓸어 가는 바람에, 남은 것이라곤 항아리를 만들었다는 사실과 그것을 바다에 가라앉혔다는 사실을 증명하는 문서뿐이었답니다."], ["At the end of two hundred years the documents were found, and they thought of bringing up the jars.", "이백 년이 지나 그 문서가 발견되었고, 사람들은 항아리를 끌어올릴 생각을 했지요."], ["Divers descended in machines, made expressly on the discovery, into the bay where they were thrown; but of ten three only remained, the rest having been broken by the waves.", "잠수부들이 그 발견에 맞춰 특별히 만든 기계를 타고, 항아리를 던져 넣은 만으로 내려갔습니다. 그러나 열 개 중 셋만 남아 있었고, 나머지는 파도에 깨져 버렸지요."], ["I am fond of these jars, upon which, perhaps, misshapen, frightful monsters have fixed their cold, dull eyes, and in which myriads of small fish have slept, seeking a refuge from the pursuit of their enemies.”", "저는 이 항아리들을 무척 좋아합니다. 어쩌면 흉측하고 끔찍한 괴물들이 그 차갑고 흐릿한 눈을 이 위에 박았을 테고, 또 무수히 많은 작은 물고기들이 적의 추격을 피해 숨어들어 그 안에서 잠들었을 테니까요.\""]], [["Meanwhile, Danglars, who had cared little for curiosities, was mechanically tearing off the blossoms of a splendid orange-tree, one after another.", "그러는 동안 골동품에는 별 관심이 없던 당글라르는, 멋들어진 오렌지나무의 꽃송이를 하나씩 기계적으로 뜯어내고 있었다."], ["When he had finished with the orange-tree, he began at the cactus; but this, not being so easily plucked as the orange-tree, pricked him dreadfully.", "오렌지나무를 다 뜯고 나자, 그는 선인장에 손을 댔다. 그러나 이것은 오렌지나무처럼 쉽게 뜯기지 않았고, 그를 끔찍하게 찔러 댔다."], ["He shuddered, and rubbed his eyes as though awaking from a dream.", "그는 움찔하더니 꿈에서 깨어난 듯 눈을 비볐다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"선생.\""], ["said Monte Cristo to him, “I do not recommend my pictures to you, who possess such splendid paintings; but, nevertheless, here are two by Hobbema, a Paul Potter, a Mieris, two by Gerard Douw, a Raphael, a Van Dyck, a Zurbaran, and two or three by Murillo, worth looking at.”", "몬테크리스토가 그에게 말했다. \"그토록 훌륭한 회화를 소장하신 당신께 제 그림을 권하지는 않겠습니다. 그래도 여기 호베마 두 점, 파울 포터 한 점, 미리스 한 점, 헤라르트 도우 두 점, 라파엘로 한 점, 반 다이크 한 점, 수르바란 한 점, 그리고 무리요 두세 점 정도는 볼 만하실 겁니다.\""]], [["“Stay,”", "\"잠깐.\""], ["said Debray; “I recognize this Hobbema.”", "데브레가 말했다. \"이 호베마는 알아보겠는데요.\""]], [["“Ah, indeed!”", "\"아, 정말입니까!\""]], [["“Yes; it was proposed for the Museum.”", "\"네, 미술관에 들이려고 제안되었던 작품이지요.\""]], [["“Which, I believe, does not contain one?”", "\"제 생각엔, 미술관에 한 점도 없지 않습니까?\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“No; and yet they refused to buy it.”", "\"없지요. 그런데도 사들이기를 거부했답니다.\""]], [["“Why?” said Château-Renaud.", "\"왜요?\" 샤토르노가 물었다."]], [["“You pretend not to know,—because government was not rich enough.”", "\"모르는 척하시는군요. 정부에 돈이 넉넉지 않았기 때문이지요.\""]], [["“Ah, pardon me,”", "\"아, 미안하지만.\""], ["said Château-Renaud; “I have heard of these things every day during the last eight years, and I cannot understand them yet.”", "샤토르노가 말했다. \"지난 팔 년 동안 매일같이 그런 이야기를 들어 왔는데도 아직 이해가 안 됩니다.\""]], [["“You will, by and by,”", "\"머잖아 이해하게 될 겁니다.\""], ["said Debray.", "데브레가 말했다."]], [["“I think not,” replied Château-Renaud.", "\"그럴 것 같지 않은데요.\" 샤토르노가 대답했다."]], [["“Major Bartolomeo Cavalcanti and Count Andrea Cavalcanti,”", "\"바르톨로메오 카발칸티 소령님, 그리고 안드레아 카발칸티 백작님 드십니다.\""], ["announced Baptistin.", "바티스탱이 알렸다."]], [["A black satin stock, fresh from the maker’s hands, gray moustaches, a bold eye, a major’s uniform, ornamented with three medals and five crosses—in fact, the thorough bearing of an old soldier—such was the appearance of Major Bartolomeo Cavalcanti, that tender father with whom we are already acquainted.", "장인의 손길에서 막 나온 듯한 검은 새틴 스톡, 희끗한 콧수염, 대담한 눈매, 훈장 셋과 십자장 다섯으로 장식된 소령 군복, 한마디로 노련한 군인의 영락없는 풍모, 이것이 우리가 이미 아는 그 다정한 아버지, 바르톨로메오 카발칸티 소령의 모습이었다."], ["Close to him, dressed in entirely new clothes, advanced smilingly Count Andrea Cavalcanti, the dutiful son, whom we also know.", "그 곁에는 온통 새 옷을 차려입은 안드레아 카발칸티 백작이 미소를 띤 채 다가왔다. 우리가 역시 아는 그 효성스러운 아들이었다."], ["The three young people were talking together.", "세 젊은이가 함께 이야기를 나누고 있었다."], ["On the entrance of the new-comers, their eyes glanced from father to son, and then, naturally enough, rested on the latter, whom they began criticising.", "새로 온 이들이 들어서자, 그들의 시선은 아버지에게서 아들에게로 옮겨 갔고, 그러다 자연스레 아들에게 머물러 그를 품평하기 시작했다."]], [["“Cavalcanti!”", "\"카발칸티라!\""], ["said Debray.", "데브레가 말했다."]], [["“A fine name,”", "\"멋진 이름이군.\""], ["said Morrel.", "모렐이 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["said Château-Renaud, “these Italians are well named and badly dressed.”", "샤토르노가 말했다. \"저 이탈리아 사람들은 이름은 그럴듯한데 옷은 형편없이 입었어.\""]], [["“You are fastidious, Château-Renaud,”", "\"까다롭군, 샤토르노.\" 데브레가 대꾸했다."], ["replied Debray; “those clothes are well cut and quite new.”", "\"저 옷은 재단도 잘됐고 아주 새 옷인걸.\""]], [["“That is just what I find fault with.", "\"바로 그게 흠이라는 거지."], ["That gentleman appears to be well dressed for the first time in his life.”", "저 신사는 난생처음 옷을 잘 차려입은 사람 같단 말이야.\""]], [["“Who are those gentlemen?”", "\"저 신사분들은 누구입니까?\""], ["asked Danglars of Monte Cristo.", "당글라르가 몬테크리스토에게 물었다."]], [["“You heard—Cavalcanti.”", "\"들으셨잖습니까. 카발칸티 부자입니다.\""]], [["“That tells me their name, and nothing else.”", "\"이름만 들었지, 그 외엔 아무것도 모릅니다.\""]], [["“Ah! true.", "\"아! 그렇군요."], ["You do not know the Italian nobility; the Cavalcanti are all descended from princes.”", "이탈리아 귀족을 모르시는군요. 카발칸티 가문은 모두 군주의 후예입니다.\""]], [["“Have they any fortune?”", "\"재산은 좀 있습니까?\""]], [["“An enormous one.”", "\"막대하지요.\""]], [["“What do they do?”", "\"무슨 일을 합니까?\""]], [["“Try to spend it all. They have some business with you, I think, from what they told me the day before yesterday.", "\"그걸 다 쓰려고 애쓰는 일이지요. 그저께 들은 바로는, 당신과 무슨 거래가 있는 모양이더군요."], ["I, indeed, invited them here today on your account. I will introduce you to them.”", "사실 오늘 그분들을 이리로 초대한 것도 당신을 위해서랍니다. 소개해 드리지요.\""]], [["“But they appear to speak French with a very pure accent,”", "\"그런데 프랑스어를 아주 순수한 억양으로 구사하는 것 같던데요.\""], ["said Danglars.", "당글라르가 말했다."]], [["“The son has been educated in a college in the south; I believe near Marseilles.", "\"아들은 남부의 한 대학에서 교육을 받았답니다. 아마 마르세유 근처였을 겁니다."], ["You will find him quite enthusiastic.”", "아주 열정적인 사람이라는 걸 알게 되실 겁니다.\""]], [["“Upon what subject?”", "\"무슨 주제에 대해서요?\""], ["asked Madame Danglars.", "당글라르 부인이 물었다."]], [["“The French ladies, madame.", "\"프랑스 여인들 말입니다, 부인."], ["He has made up his mind to take a wife from Paris.”", "파리에서 아내를 맞기로 마음을 굳혔답니다.\""]], [["“A fine idea that of his,”", "\"별 좋은 생각도 다 하는군.\""], ["said Danglars, shrugging his shoulders.", "당글라르가 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다."], ["Madame Danglars looked at her husband with an expression which, at any other time, would have indicated a storm, but for the second time she controlled herself.", "당글라르 부인은 다른 때 같았으면 한바탕 폭풍을 예고했을 표정으로 남편을 바라보았지만, 이번에도 두 번째로 자신을 억눌렀다."]], [["“The baron appears thoughtful today,”", "\"남작께서 오늘은 생각에 잠겨 계시는 듯하군요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo to her; “are they going to put him in the ministry?”", "몬테크리스토가 그녀에게 말했다. \"혹시 입각이라도 시키려는 겁니까?\""]], [["“Not yet, I think.", "\"아직은 아닌 듯해요."], ["More likely he has been speculating on the Bourse, and has lost money.”", "그보다는 증권 거래소에서 투기를 하다가 돈을 잃은 게지요.\""]], [["“M. and Madame de Villefort,”", "\"빌포르 씨 부부 드십니다.\""], ["cried Baptistin.", "바티스탱이 외쳤다."]], [["They entered. M.", "두 사람이 들어왔다."], ["de Villefort, notwithstanding his self-control, was visibly affected, and when Monte Cristo touched his hand, he felt it tremble.", "빌포르 씨는 그 자제력에도 불구하고 눈에 띄게 동요했고, 몬테크리스토가 그의 손을 잡았을 때 그 손이 떨리는 것이 느껴졌다."]], [["“Certainly, women alone know how to dissimulate,”", "'역시 여자들만이 속내를 감출 줄 아는군.'"], ["said Monte Cristo to himself, glancing at Madame Danglars, who was smiling on the procureur, and embracing his wife.", "몬테크리스토는 속으로 중얼거리며, 검사에게 미소 지으며 그의 아내를 끌어안는 당글라르 부인을 흘끗 보았다."]], [["After a short time, the count saw Bertuccio, who, until then, had been occupied on the other side of the house, glide into an adjoining room. He went to him.", "잠시 후, 백작은 그때까지 집의 반대편에서 일을 보고 있던 베르투치오가 옆방으로 슬그머니 들어가는 것을 보았다. 그는 그에게로 갔다."]], [["“What do you want, M. Bertuccio?”", "\"무슨 일인가, 베르투치오?\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Your excellency has not stated the number of guests.”", "\"각하께서 손님이 몇 분이신지 말씀해 주지 않으셔서요.\""]], [["“Ah, true.”", "\"아, 그렇지.\""]], [["“How many covers?”", "\"몇 사람 분으로 차릴까요?\""]], [["“Count for yourself.”", "\"자네가 세어 보게.\""]], [["“Is everyone here, your excellency?”", "\"다들 오신 겁니까, 각하?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["Bertuccio glanced through the door, which was ajar.", "베르투치오는 빠끔히 열린 문틈으로 안을 들여다보았다."], ["The count watched him.", "백작은 그를 지켜보았다."], ["“Good heavens!”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["he exclaimed.", "그가 소리쳤다."]], [["“What is the matter?”", "\"무슨 일인가?\""], ["said the count.", "백작이 말했다."]], [["“That woman—that woman!”", "\"저 여자, 저 여자가!\""]], [["“Which?”", "\"누구 말인가?\""]], [["“The one with a white dress and so many diamonds—the fair one.”", "\"흰 드레스에 다이아몬드를 잔뜩 단, 저 금발 여자 말입니다.\""]], [["“Madame Danglars?”", "\"당글라르 부인 말인가?\""]], [["“I do not know her name; but it is she, sir, it is she!”", "\"이름은 모릅니다. 하지만 저 여자입니다, 나리, 저 여자예요!\""]], [["“Whom do you mean?”", "\"누구 말인가?\""]], [["“The woman of the garden!—she that was _enceinte_—she who was walking while she waited for——”", "\"그 정원의 여자입니다! 아이를 밴 채로, 누군가를 기다리며 거닐던 그…… 그 여자……\""]], [["Bertuccio stood at the open door, with his eyes starting and his hair on end.", "베르투치오는 열린 문 앞에 선 채, 눈이 튀어나올 듯하고 머리카락이 곤두서 있었다."]], [["“Waiting for whom?” Bertuccio, without answering, pointed to Villefort with something of the gesture Macbeth uses to point out Banquo.", "\"누구를 기다렸다는 건가?\" 베르투치오는 대답 없이, 마치 맥베스가 뱅쿠오를 가리키던 그 몸짓으로 빌포르를 가리켰다."]], [["“Oh, oh!”", "\"오, 오!\""], ["he at length muttered, “do you see?”", "그가 마침내 중얼거렸다. \"보이십니까?\""]], [["“What? Who?” “Him!”", "\"무엇이? 누가?\""]], [["“Him!—M.", "\"저 사람이요!\""], ["de Villefort, the king’s attorney?", "\"저 사람? 검사 빌포르 씨 말인가?"], ["Certainly I see him.”", "물론 보이지.\""]], [["“Then I did not kill him?”", "\"그럼 제가 그를 죽인 게 아니란 말입니까?\""]], [["“Really, I think you are going mad, good Bertuccio,”", "\"정말이지 자네 미쳐 가는 것 같군, 착한 베르투치오.\""], ["said the count.", "백작이 말했다."]], [["“Then he is not dead?” 30213m", "\"그럼 그가 죽지 않았단 말입니까?\""]], [["“No; you see plainly he is not dead.", "\"그래. 보다시피 죽지 않았네."], ["Instead of striking between the sixth and seventh left ribs, as your countrymen do, you must have struck higher or lower, and life is very tenacious in these lawyers, or rather there is no truth in anything you have told me—it was a fright of the imagination, a dream of your fancy.", "자네 나라 사람들이 흔히 하듯 왼쪽 여섯 번째와 일곱 번째 갈비뼈 사이를 찔렀어야 했는데, 분명 더 위나 아래를 찔렀던 게지. 게다가 이 변호사들이란 목숨이 어찌나 질긴지. 아니, 그게 아니라면 자네가 내게 한 이야기에는 진실이라곤 없는 거야."], ["You went to sleep full of thoughts of vengeance; they weighed heavily upon your stomach; you had the nightmare—that’s all. Come, calm yourself, and reckon them up—M.", "그건 상상이 빚은 공포, 환상이 만든 꿈이었던 게지. 자네는 복수 생각에 가득 차서 잠이 들었고, 그 생각이 위장을 무겁게 짓눌렀고, 그래서 악몽을 꾼 걸세."], ["and Madame de Villefort, two; M.", "그게 다야. 자, 진정하고 손님 수나 세어 보게."], ["and Madame Danglars, four; M.", "빌포르 씨 부부, 둘."], ["de Château-Renaud, M.", "당글라르 씨 부부, 넷."], ["Debray, M. Morrel, seven; Major Bartolomeo Cavalcanti, eight.”", "샤토르노 씨, 데브레 씨, 모렐 씨, 일곱. 바르톨로메오 카발칸티 소령, 여덟.\""]], [["“Eight!”", "\"여덟!\""], ["repeated Bertuccio.", "베르투치오가 따라 말했다."]], [["“Stop!", "\"잠깐!"], ["You are in a shocking hurry to be off—you forget one of my guests.", "어지간히 서둘러 가려는군. 손님 한 분을 빠뜨렸네. 왼쪽으로 조금 기대 보게. 자!"], ["Lean a little to the left. Stay!", "안드레아 카발칸티 씨를 보게."], ["look at M. Andrea Cavalcanti, the young man in a black coat, looking at Murillo’s ‘Madonna’; now he is turning.”", "무리요의 '성모'를 바라보고 있는, 검은 외투 차림의 저 젊은이 말일세. 자, 이제 돌아서는군.\""]], [["This time Bertuccio would have uttered an exclamation, had not a look from Monte Cristo silenced him.", "이번에는 베르투치오가 외마디 소리를 지를 뻔했으나, 몬테크리스토의 눈짓이 그를 침묵하게 했다."]], [["“Benedetto?”", "\"베네데토?\""], ["he muttered; “fatality!”", "그가 중얼거렸다. \"운명의 장난이로구나!\""]], [["“Half-past six o’clock has just struck, M.", "\"방금 여섯 시 반을 쳤네, 베르투치오.\""], ["Bertuccio,” said the count severely; “I ordered dinner at that hour, and I do not like to wait;”", "백작이 엄하게 말했다. \"내가 저녁을 그 시각에 들이라고 일렀고, 나는 기다리는 걸 좋아하지 않아.\""], ["and he returned to his guests, while Bertuccio, leaning against the wall, succeeded in reaching the dining-room.", "그러고는 손님들에게로 돌아갔다. 베르투치오는 벽에 기댄 채로 간신히 식당까지 다다랐다."], ["Five minutes afterwards the doors of the drawing-room were thrown open, and Bertuccio appearing said, with a violent effort, “The dinner waits.”", "오 분 뒤 응접실 문이 활짝 열렸고, 베르투치오가 나타나 안간힘을 다해 말했다. \"식사 준비가 되었습니다.\""]], [["The Count of Monte Cristo offered his arm to Madame de Villefort. “M.", "몬테크리스토 백작은 빌포르 부인에게 팔을 내밀었다. \"빌포르 씨.\""], ["de Villefort,”", "그가 말했다."], ["he said, “will you conduct the Baroness Danglars?”", "\"당글라르 남작 부인을 모셔 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["Villefort complied, and they passed on to the dining-room.", "빌포르가 그 청에 따랐고, 사람들은 식당으로 향했다."]], [["# Chapter 63. The Dinner", "# Chapter 63. The Dinner"]], [["It was evident that one sentiment affected all the guests on entering the dining-room.", "식당으로 들어서는 손님들 모두가 한 가지 같은 감정에 사로잡혀 있는 것이 분명했다."], ["Each one asked what strange influence had brought them to this house, and yet astonished, even uneasy though they were, they still felt that they would not like to be absent.", "저마다 무슨 묘한 힘에 이끌려 이 집까지 오게 되었는지 자문했지만, 놀라고 또 불안하면서도 그 자리에 빠지고 싶지는 않다는 마음이 들었다."], ["The recent events, the solitary and eccentric position of the count, his enormous, nay, almost incredible fortune, should have made men cautious, and have altogether prevented ladies visiting a house where there was no one of their own sex to receive them; and yet curiosity had been enough to lead them to overleap the bounds of prudence and decorum.", "최근에 일어난 일들, 백작의 고독하고 별난 처지, 그의 막대한, 아니 거의 믿기 어려울 만큼의 재산, 이런 것들은 마땅히 사람들을 신중하게 만들고, 자신들을 맞아 줄 같은 성별의 사람이 하나도 없는 집에 부인들이 발길을 들이지 못하게 막았어야 했다. 그런데도 호기심이 신중함과 예법의 경계를 뛰어넘게 만들기에 충분했다."]], [["And all present, even including Cavalcanti and his son, notwithstanding the stiffness of the one and the carelessness of the other, were thoughtful, on finding themselves assembled at the house of this incomprehensible man.", "그 자리에 모인 모든 이들, 심지어 카발칸티 부자까지도, 한쪽은 뻣뻣하고 다른 한쪽은 무심했음에도, 이 헤아릴 수 없는 인물의 집에 모여 있다는 사실에 생각에 잠겨 있었다."], ["Madame Danglars had started when Villefort, on the count’s invitation, offered his arm; and Villefort felt that his glance was uneasy beneath his gold spectacles, when he felt the arm of the baroness press upon his own.", "백작의 권유로 빌포르가 팔을 내밀자 당글라르 부인은 흠칫 놀랐다. 그리고 빌포르는 남작 부인의 팔이 자기 팔에 닿는 순간, 금테 안경 아래의 자기 눈길이 불안하게 흔들리는 것을 느꼈다."], ["None of this had escaped the count, and even by this mere contact of individuals the scene had already acquired considerable interest for an observer.", "이 모든 것이 백작의 눈을 피해 가지 못했다. 단지 이렇게 사람들이 서로 닿는 것만으로도, 그 광경은 관찰하는 이에게 이미 상당한 흥미를 자아내고 있었다."]], [["M. de Villefort had on the right hand Madame Danglars, on his left Morrel.", "빌포르 씨의 오른편에는 당글라르 부인이, 왼편에는 모렐이 앉았다."], ["The count was seated between Madame de Villefort and Danglars; the other seats were filled by Debray, who was placed between the two Cavalcanti, and by Château-Renaud, seated between Madame de Villefort and Morrel.", "백작은 빌포르 부인과 당글라르 사이에 앉았다. 나머지 자리는 카발칸티 부자 사이에 앉은 드브레와, 빌포르 부인과 모렐 사이에 앉은 샤토르노가 채웠다."]], [["The repast was magnificent; Monte Cristo had endeavored completely to overturn the Parisian ideas, and to feed the curiosity as much as the appetite of his guests.", "식사는 더없이 화려했다. 몬테크리스토는 파리 사람들의 통념을 완전히 뒤엎고, 손님들의 식욕만큼이나 호기심도 채워 주려고 애를 썼다. 그가 차려 놓은 것은 동방의 잔치였으나, 아라비아의 요정들이 마련했을 법한 그런 종류의 잔치였다."], ["It was an Oriental feast that he offered to them, but of such a kind as the Arabian fairies might be supposed to prepare.", "지구의 네 모퉁이에서 구할 수 있는 온갖 진귀한 과일이 중국 도자기 항아리와 일본 단지에 수북이 담겨 있었다."], ["Every delicious fruit that the four quarters of the globe could provide was heaped in vases from China and jars from Japan.", "가장 화려한 깃털을 그대로 두른 진귀한 새들, 묵직한 은쟁반에 펼쳐진 거대한 물고기들, 거기에 군도(群島)와 소아시아, 희망봉에서 나는 온갖 포도주가 있었다."], ["Rare birds, retaining their most brilliant plumage, enormous fish, spread upon massive silver dishes, together with every wine produced in the Archipelago, Asia Minor, or the Cape, sparkling in bottles, whose grotesque shape seemed to give an additional flavor to the draught,—all these, like one of the displays with which Apicius of old gratified his guests, passed in review before the eyes of the astonished Parisians, who understood that it was possible to expend a thousand louis upon a dinner for ten persons, but only on the condition of eating pearls, like Cleopatra, or drinking refined gold, like Lorenzo de’ Medici.", "그 기괴한 모양만으로도 술맛을 한층 더해 주는 듯한 병들 속에서 포도주가 반짝였다. 이 모든 것이, 옛날 아피키우스가 손님들을 흡족하게 했던 진수성찬처럼, 놀란 파리 사람들의 눈앞에 차례로 펼쳐졌다. 그들은 열 사람 저녁상에 천 루이를 쓰는 것이 가능하다는 것을 깨달았지만, 그것은 클레오파트라처럼 진주를 먹거나 로렌초 데 메디치처럼 정제한 황금을 마실 때에나 가능한 일이었다."]], [["Monte Cristo noticed the general astonishment, and began laughing and joking about it.", "몬테크리스토는 모두가 놀라워하는 것을 알아채고는 웃으며 농담을 던지기 시작했다."]], [["“Gentlemen,”", "\"여러분,\""], ["he said, “you will admit that, when arrived at a certain degree of fortune, the superfluities of life are all that can be desired; and the ladies will allow that, after having risen to a certain eminence of position, the ideal alone can be more exalted.", "그가 말했다. \"어느 정도의 재산에 이르고 나면 인생에서 바랄 수 있는 것은 군더더기뿐이라는 데 동의하실 겁니다."], ["Now, to follow out this reasoning, what is the marvellous?—that which we do not understand. What is it that we really desire?—that which we cannot obtain.", "그리고 부인들께서는, 어느 정도의 지위에 오르고 나면 오직 이상만이 더 높이 고양될 수 있다는 데 동의하실 겁니다. 자, 이 논리를 끝까지 밀고 나가 봅시다."], ["Now, to see things which I cannot understand, to procure impossibilities, these are the study of my life.", "경이로운 것이란 무엇입니까? 우리가 이해하지 못하는 것이지요. 우리가 진정으로 갈망하는 것은 무엇입니까?"], ["I gratify my wishes by two means—my will and my money. I take as much interest in the pursuit of some whim as you do, M.", "우리가 손에 넣을 수 없는 것입니다. 그러니 이해할 수 없는 것을 보고, 불가능한 것을 손에 넣는 것, 이것이 제 삶의 연구입니다."], ["Danglars, in promoting a new railway line; you, M.", "저는 두 가지 수단으로 제 소망을 채웁니다. 의지와 돈이지요."], ["de Villefort, in condemning a culprit to death; you, M. Debray, in pacifying a kingdom; you, M. de Château-Renaud, in pleasing a woman; and you, Morrel, in breaking a horse that no one can ride.", "저는 어떤 변덕을 좇는 데서, 당글라르 씨가 새 철도 노선을 추진하는 데서 느끼는 것만큼, 빌포르 씨가 죄인에게 사형을 선고하는 데서, 드브레 씨가 한 왕국을 평정하는 데서, 샤토르노 씨가 여인의 마음을 사는 데서, 그리고 모렐 씨가 아무도 못 타는 말을 길들이는 데서 느끼는 것만큼의 흥미를 느낍니다."], ["For example, you see these two fish; one brought from fifty leagues beyond St. Petersburg, the other five leagues from Naples.", "예를 들어, 이 물고기 두 마리를 보십시오. 하나는 상트페테르부르크에서 오십 리외 너머에서 가져온 것이고, 다른 하나는 나폴리에서 오 리외 떨어진 곳에서 가져온 것입니다."], ["Is it not amusing to see them both on the same table?”", "둘이 같은 식탁에 올라 있는 걸 보는 게 재미있지 않습니까?\""]], [["“What are the two fish?”", "\"그 두 물고기가 무엇입니까?\""], ["asked Danglars.", "당글라르가 물었다."]], [["“M. Château-Renaud, who has lived in Russia, will tell you the name of one, and Major Cavalcanti, who is an Italian, will tell you the name of the other.”", "\"러시아에서 사신 적이 있는 샤토르노 씨가 한 마리의 이름을 말씀해 주실 테고, 이탈리아인이신 카발칸티 소령이 다른 한 마리의 이름을 말씀해 주실 겁니다.\""]], [["“This one is, I think, a sterlet,”", "\"이건 제 생각에 철갑상어인 것 같군요.\""], ["said Château-Renaud.", "샤토르노가 말했다."]], [["“And that one, if I mistake not, a lamprey.”", "\"그리고 저건 제가 틀리지 않았다면 칠성장어입니다.\""]], [["“Just so. Now, M.", "\"바로 그렇습니다."], ["Danglars, ask these gentlemen where they are caught.”", "자, 당글라르 씨, 이분들께 이것들이 어디서 잡히는지 물어보십시오.\""]], [["“Sterlets,”", "\"철갑상어는,\""], ["said Château-Renaud, “are only found in the Volga.”", "샤토르노가 말했다. \"오직 볼가강에서만 잡힙니다.\""]], [["“And,”", "\"그리고,\""], ["said Cavalcanti, “I know that Lake Fusaro alone supplies lampreys of that size.”", "카발칸티가 말했다. \"그만한 크기의 칠성장어는 푸사로 호수에서만 난다는 걸 압니다.\""]], [["“Exactly; one comes from the Volga, and the other from Lake Fusaro.”", "\"정확합니다. 하나는 볼가강에서, 다른 하나는 푸사로 호수에서 왔지요.\""]], [["“Impossible!” cried all the guests simultaneously.", "\"그럴 리가요!\" 손님들이 일제히 외쳤다."]], [["“Well, this is just what amuses me,”", "\"바로 그것이 저를 즐겁게 하는 겁니다.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["“I am like Nero—_cupitor impossibilium_; and that is what is amusing you at this moment.", "\"저는 네로와 같습니다. 불가능한 것을 갈망하는 자(cupitor impossibilium) 말이지요."], ["This fish, which seems so exquisite to you, is very likely no better than perch or salmon; but it seemed impossible to procure it, and here it is.”", "바로 그것이 지금 여러분을 즐겁게 하고 있는 겁니다. 여러분께 이토록 진귀해 보이는 이 물고기도, 십중팔구 농어나 연어보다 나을 게 없을 겁니다. 다만 그것을 구하는 일이 불가능해 보였고, 이렇게 여기 있는 것이지요.\""]], [["“But how could you have these fish brought to France?”", "\"하지만 어떻게 이 물고기들을 프랑스까지 가져올 수 있었습니까?\""]], [["“Oh, nothing more easy.", "\"오, 그보다 쉬운 일은 없습니다. 물고기마다 통 하나에 담겨 운반되었지요."], ["Each fish was brought over in a cask—one filled with river herbs and weeds, the other with rushes and lake plants; they were placed in a wagon built on purpose, and thus the sterlet lived twelve days, the lamprey eight, and both were alive when my cook seized them, killing one with milk and the other with wine.", "하나는 강에서 나는 풀과 수초로, 다른 하나는 골풀과 호숫가 식물로 채운 통이었습니다. 그것들을 일부러 만든 짐수레에 실었더니, 철갑상어는 열이틀을 살았고 칠성장어는 여드레를 살았습니다. 둘 다 제 요리사가 손에 넣을 때까지 살아 있었고, 그는 한 마리는 우유로, 다른 한 마리는 포도주로 죽였습니다."], ["You do not believe me, M. Danglars!”", "제 말을 안 믿으시는군요, 당글라르 씨!\""]], [["“I cannot help doubting,”", "\"의심하지 않을 수가 없군요.\""], ["answered Danglars with his stupid smile.", "당글라르가 멍청한 미소를 띠며 대답했다."]], [["“Baptistin,”", "\"바티스탱,\""], ["said the count, “have the other fish brought in—the sterlet and the lamprey which came in the other casks, and which are yet alive.”", "백작이 말했다. \"다른 물고기들을 가져오너라. 다른 통에 담겨 와서 아직 살아 있는 철갑상어와 칠성장어 말이다.\""]], [["Danglars opened his bewildered eyes; the company clapped their hands.", "당글라르는 어리둥절한 눈을 크게 떴다. 좌중은 손뼉을 쳤다."], ["Four servants carried in two casks covered with aquatic plants, and in each of which was breathing a fish similar to those on the table.", "하인 넷이 수초로 덮인 통 두 개를 들고 들어왔는데, 그 안에는 식탁에 오른 것과 똑같은 물고기가 한 마리씩 숨 쉬고 있었다."]], [["“But why have two of each sort?”", "\"하지만 왜 종류마다 두 마리씩 있는 겁니까?\""], ["asked Danglars.", "당글라르가 물었다."]], [["“Merely because one might have died,”", "\"그저 한 마리가 죽을 수도 있었으니까요.\""], ["carelessly answered Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 무심하게 대답했다."]], [["“You are certainly an extraordinary man,”", "\"당신은 정말이지 비범한 분이십니다.\""], ["said Danglars; “and philosophers may well say it is a fine thing to be rich.”", "당글라르가 말했다. \"철학자들이 부자가 되는 건 멋진 일이라고 말할 만하군요.\""]], [["“And to have ideas,”", "\"게다가 발상이 있어야지요.\""], ["added Madame Danglars.", "당글라르 부인이 덧붙였다."]], [["“Oh, do not give me credit for this, madame; it was done by the Romans, who much esteemed them, and Pliny relates that they sent slaves from Ostia to Rome, who carried on their heads fish which he calls the _mulus_, and which, from the description, must probably be the goldfish.", "\"오, 이걸 제 공으로 돌리지는 마십시오, 부인. 이건 로마인들이 하던 일입니다. 그들은 이런 것들을 무척 귀하게 여겼지요."], ["It was also considered a luxury to have them alive, it being an amusing sight to see them die, for, when dying, they change color three or four times, and like the rainbow when it disappears, pass through all the prismatic shades, after which they were sent to the kitchen.", "플리니우스의 기록에 따르면, 그들은 오스티아에서 로마로 노예들을 보냈는데, 그 노예들이 머리에 이고 온 물고기를 '물루스(mulus)'라 불렀고, 묘사로 보아 아마도 그건 금붕어였을 겁니다. 또 그것들을 산 채로 두는 것도 사치로 여겨졌는데, 죽어 가는 모습을 보는 것이 흥미로운 구경거리였기 때문입니다. 죽어 갈 때 그것들은 서너 번 빛깔이 바뀌었고, 스러지는 무지개처럼 프리즘의 온갖 색조를 거쳐 갔으며, 그런 뒤에야 주방으로 보내졌지요."], ["Their agony formed part of their merit—if they were not seen alive, they were despised when dead.”", "그 단말마의 고통이 그것들 가치의 일부였습니다. 산 모습을 보지 못하면, 죽은 것은 멸시받았으니까요.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네,\""], ["said Debray, “but then Ostia is only a few leagues from Rome.”", "드브레가 말했다. \"하지만 오스티아는 로마에서 겨우 몇 리외밖에 떨어져 있지 않잖습니까.\""]], [["“True,”", "\"맞습니다.\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “but what would be the use of living eighteen hundred years after Lucullus, if we can do no better than he could?”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"하지만 루쿨루스보다 천팔백 년 뒤에 살면서, 그가 했던 것보다 더 나을 게 없다면 그 삶이 무슨 소용이 있겠습니까?\""]], [["The two Cavalcanti opened their enormous eyes, but had the good sense not to say anything.", "카발칸티 부자는 그 커다란 눈을 휘둥그레 떴지만, 아무 말도 하지 않을 만한 분별은 있었다."]], [["“All this is very extraordinary,”", "\"이 모든 게 참으로 비범하군요.\""], ["said Château-Renaud; “still, what I admire the most, I confess, is the marvellous promptitude with which your orders are executed.", "샤토르노가 말했다. \"그래도 솔직히 제가 가장 감탄하는 건, 당신의 분부가 실로 놀랍도록 신속하게 이행된다는 점입니다."], ["Is it not true that you only bought this house five or six days ago?”", "이 집을 산 게 닷새인가 엿새 전이라는 게 사실 아닙니까?\""]], [["“Certainly not longer.”", "\"분명 그보다 더 오래되지는 않았지요.\""]], [["“Well, I am sure it is quite transformed since last week.", "\"그런데 지난주 이후로 완전히 달라진 게 분명합니다."], ["If I remember rightly, it had another entrance, and the courtyard was paved and empty; while today we have a splendid lawn, bordered by trees which appear to be a hundred years old.”", "제 기억이 맞다면, 입구가 다른 쪽에 있었고 안마당은 포장된 채 텅 비어 있었지요. 그런데 오늘은 백 년은 됐을 법한 나무들로 둘러싸인 멋진 잔디밭이 있군요.\""]], [["“Why not? I am fond of grass and shade,”", "\"안 될 게 뭐 있겠습니까? 저는 풀과 그늘을 좋아하니까요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네,\""], ["said Madame de Villefort, “the door was towards the road before, and on the day of my miraculous escape you brought me into the house from the road, I remember.”", "빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"전에는 문이 길 쪽으로 나 있었지요. 제가 기적적으로 살아난 그날, 당신이 길에서 저를 집 안으로 데려다 주셨던 게 기억나요.\""]], [["“Yes, madame,”", "\"그렇습니다, 부인.\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “but I preferred having an entrance which would allow me to see the Bois de Boulogne over my gate.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"하지만 저는 대문 너머로 불로뉴 숲이 보이는 입구가 더 마음에 들었습니다.\""]], [["“In four days,”", "\"나흘 만에 말이지요.\""], ["said Morrel; “it is extraordinary!”", "모렐이 말했다. \"정말 놀랍습니다!\""]], [["“Indeed,”", "\"정말이지,\""], ["said Château-Renaud, “it seems quite miraculous to make a new house out of an old one; for it was very old, and dull too.", "샤토르노가 말했다. \"낡은 집을 새 집으로 탈바꿈시키다니 거의 기적 같군요. 무척 낡았고 또 음침했으니까요."], ["I recollect coming for my mother to look at it when M. de Saint-Méran advertised it for sale two or three years ago.”", "이삼 년 전에 생메랑 씨가 이 집을 매물로 내놓았을 때, 어머니를 모시고 보러 왔던 게 기억납니다.\""]], [["“M. de Saint-Méran?”", "\"생메랑 씨라고요?\""], ["said Madame de Villefort; “then this house belonged to M. de Saint-Méran before you bought it?”", "빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"그럼 이 집은 당신이 사기 전에 생메랑 씨 소유였단 말인가요?\""]], [["“It appears so,”", "\"그런 모양이더군요.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다."]], [["“Is it possible that you do not know of whom you purchased it?”", "\"이 집을 누구한테 사셨는지도 모르신단 말입니까?\""]], [["“Quite so; my steward transacts all this business for me.”", "\"그렇습니다. 제 집사가 그런 일을 다 처리하니까요.\""]], [["“It is certainly ten years since the house had been occupied,”", "\"분명 십 년은 사람이 살지 않았던 집입니다.\""], ["said Château-Renaud, “and it was quite melancholy to look at it, with the blinds closed, the doors locked, and the weeds in the court.", "샤토르노가 말했다. \"덧창은 닫히고 문은 잠긴 데다 안마당엔 잡초가 무성해서, 바라보기만 해도 참 울적했지요."], ["Really, if the house had not belonged to the father-in-law of the procureur, one might have thought it some accursed place where a horrible crime had been committed.”", "사실 이 집이 검사장의 장인 소유가 아니었다면, 끔찍한 범죄가 저질러진 저주받은 장소쯤으로 여겼을지도 모릅니다.\""]], [["Villefort, who had hitherto not tasted the three or four glasses of rare wine which were placed before him, here took one, and drank it off.", "이제껏 앞에 놓인 진귀한 포도주 서너 잔에 입도 대지 않았던 빌포르가, 여기서 한 잔을 들어 단숨에 비웠다."], ["Monte Cristo allowed a short time to elapse, and then said:", "몬테크리스토는 잠시 시간이 흐르도록 두었다가 말했다."]], [["“It is singular, baron, but the same idea came across me the first time I came here; it looked so gloomy I should never have bought it if my steward had not taken the matter into his own hands.", "\"이상하지만, 남작, 저도 이 집에 처음 왔을 때 똑같은 생각이 들었습니다. 어찌나 음침해 보이던지, 제 집사가 그 일을 직접 떠맡지 않았더라면 결코 사지 않았을 겁니다."], ["Perhaps the fellow had been bribed by the notary.”", "어쩌면 그 친구가 공증인한테 매수당했는지도 모르지요.\""]], [["“It is probable,”", "\"그럴 법한 일입니다.\""], ["stammered out Villefort, trying to smile; “but I can assure you that I had nothing to do with any such proceeding.", "빌포르가 억지로 미소 지으려 애쓰며 더듬더듬 말했다. \"하지만 분명히 말씀드리건대, 저는 그런 거래와는 아무 상관이 없습니다."], ["This house is part of Valentine’s marriage-portion, and M. de Saint-Méran wished to sell it; for if it had remained another year or two uninhabited it would have fallen to ruin.”", "이 집은 발랑틴의 혼인 지참금의 일부고, 생메랑 씨가 팔고 싶어 했지요. 일이 년만 더 사람이 살지 않았다면 폐허가 되었을 테니까요.\""]], [["It was Morrel’s turn to become pale.", "이번에는 모렐의 얼굴이 창백해질 차례였다."]], [["“There was, above all, one room,”", "\"무엇보다도 방이 하나 있었지요.\""], ["continued Monte Cristo, “very plain in appearance, hung with red damask, which, I know not why, appeared to me quite dramatic.”", "몬테크리스토가 말을 이었다. \"겉보기엔 아주 수수하고 붉은 다마스크 천이 드리워진 방인데, 왜인지 모르겠지만 제겐 무척 극적으로 보였습니다.\""]], [["“Why so?”", "\"왜 그렇습니까?\""], ["said Danglars; “why dramatic?”", "당글라르가 말했다. \"왜 극적이라는 거지요?\""]], [["“Can we account for instinct?”", "\"본능을 설명할 수 있겠습니까?\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["“Are there not some places where we seem to breathe sadness?—why, we cannot tell.", "\"왠지 슬픔이 숨 쉬는 듯한 장소들이 있지 않습니까? 그 까닭은 알 수 없습니다."], ["It is a chain of recollections—an idea which carries you back to other times, to other places—which, very likely, have no connection with the present time and place.", "그건 한 줄로 이어진 기억들이지요. 다른 시절, 다른 장소로 우리를 데려가는 어떤 관념인데, 십중팔구 지금 이 시간, 이 장소와는 아무 관련이 없는 것들 말입니다."], ["And there is something in this room which reminds me forcibly of the chamber of the Marquise de Ganges10 or Desdemona.", "그리고 이 방에는 강가드 후작 부인이나 데스데모나의 침실을 강하게 떠올리게 하는 무언가가 있습니다."], ["Stay, since we have finished dinner, I will show it to you, and then we will take coffee in the garden.", "자, 저녁 식사도 끝났으니, 그 방을 보여 드리지요. 그런 다음 정원에서 커피를 들도록 합시다."], ["After dinner, the play.”", "식사 뒤에는 연극이 있어야지요.\""]], [["Monte Cristo looked inquiringly at his guests.", "몬테크리스토는 묻는 듯한 눈길로 손님들을 바라보았다."], ["Madame de Villefort rose, Monte Cristo did the same, and the rest followed their example.", "빌포르 부인이 일어섰고, 몬테크리스토도 따라 일어섰으며, 나머지 사람들도 그들을 따랐다."], ["Villefort and Madame Danglars remained for a moment, as if rooted to their seats; they questioned each other with vague and stupid glances.", "빌포르와 당글라르 부인만은 마치 자리에 뿌리내린 듯 잠시 그대로 있었다. 두 사람은 멍하고 막연한 눈길로 서로에게 물었다."]], [["“Did you hear?”", "\"들으셨어요?\""], ["said Madame Danglars.", "당글라르 부인이 말했다."]], [["“We must go,”", "\"가야지요.\""], ["replied Villefort, offering his arm.", "빌포르가 팔을 내밀며 대답했다."]], [["The others, attracted by curiosity, were already scattered in different parts of the house; for they thought the visit would not be limited to the one room, and that, at the same time, they would obtain a view of the rest of the building, of which Monte Cristo had created a palace.", "다른 이들은 호기심에 이끌려 이미 집 곳곳으로 흩어져 있었다. 그 구경이 방 하나에 그치지 않을 것이고, 동시에 몬테크리스토가 궁전처럼 만들어 놓은 건물의 나머지 부분도 볼 수 있으리라 여겼기 때문이다."], ["Each one went out by the open doors.", "저마다 열려 있는 문으로 나갔다."], ["Monte Cristo waited for the two who remained; then, when they had passed, he brought up the rear, and on his face was a smile, which, if they could have understood it, would have alarmed them much more than a visit to the room they were about to enter.", "몬테크리스토는 남아 있는 두 사람을 기다렸다. 그러고는 두 사람이 지나가자 맨 뒤를 따랐다. 그의 얼굴에는 미소가 떠올라 있었는데, 만약 그들이 그 의미를 알아챘다면 곧 들어설 그 방을 구경하는 것보다 훨씬 더 섬뜩했을 미소였다."], ["They began by walking through the apartments, many of which were fitted up in the Eastern style, with cushions and divans instead of beds, and pipes instead of furniture.", "그들은 먼저 여러 방을 거쳐 걸었는데, 그중 많은 방이 동방식으로 꾸며져 있었다. 침대 대신 방석과 긴 의자가, 가구 대신 담뱃대가 놓여 있었다."], ["The drawing-rooms were decorated with the rarest pictures by the old masters, the boudoirs hung with draperies from China, of fanciful colors, fantastic design, and wonderful texture.", "응접실들은 옛 거장들의 더없이 진귀한 그림들로 장식되어 있었고, 규방들에는 기이한 빛깔과 환상적인 문양, 놀라운 질감의 중국산 휘장이 드리워져 있었다."], ["At length they arrived at the famous room.", "마침내 그들은 그 유명한 방에 다다랐다."], ["There was nothing particular about it, excepting that, although daylight had disappeared, it was not lighted, and everything in it was old-fashioned, while the rest of the rooms had been redecorated. These two causes were enough to give it a gloomy aspect.", "특별할 것은 없었으나, 다만 햇빛이 사라졌는데도 불이 켜져 있지 않았고, 다른 방들은 새로 단장된 반면 그 방의 모든 것은 옛날식 그대로였다. 이 두 가지만으로도 방에 음침한 분위기를 자아내기에 충분했다."]], [["“Oh.”", "\"어머.\""], ["cried Madame de Villefort, “it is really frightful.”", "빌포르 부인이 외쳤다. \"정말 무시무시하네요.\""]], [["Madame Danglars tried to utter a few words, but was not heard.", "당글라르 부인은 몇 마디 하려 했으나 아무도 듣지 못했다."], ["Many observations were made, the import of which was a unanimous opinion that there was something sinister about the room.", "여러 말이 오갔는데, 그 요지는 그 방에 무언가 불길한 데가 있다는 한결같은 의견이었다."]], [["30221m\n“Is it not so?”", "\"그렇지 않습니까?\""], ["asked Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 물었다."], ["“Look at that large clumsy bed, hung with such gloomy, blood-colored drapery!", "\"저 크고 투박한 침대를 보십시오. 저토록 음침한 핏빛 휘장이 드리워져 있지요!"], ["And those two crayon portraits, that have faded from the dampness; do they not seem to say, with their pale lips and staring eyes, ‘We have seen’?”", "그리고 습기에 빛바랜 저 크레용 초상화 두 점. 핏기 없는 입술과 부릅뜬 눈으로 '우리는 보았노라'라고 말하는 것 같지 않습니까?\""]], [["Villefort became livid; Madame Danglars fell into a long seat placed near the chimney.", "빌포르는 새파랗게 질렸다. 당글라르 부인은 벽난로 가까이 놓인 긴 의자에 주저앉았다."]], [["“Oh,”", "\"어머,\""], ["said Madame de Villefort, smiling, “are you courageous enough to sit down upon the very seat perhaps upon which the crime was committed?”", "빌포르 부인이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"어쩌면 바로 그 위에서 범죄가 저질러졌을지도 모르는 그 의자에 앉으시다니, 정말 용감하시네요?\""]], [["Madame Danglars rose suddenly.", "당글라르 부인이 벌떡 일어났다."]], [["“And then,”", "\"그리고,\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “this is not all.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"이게 다가 아닙니다.\""]], [["“What is there more?”", "\"또 뭐가 있습니까?\""], ["said Debray, who had not failed to notice the agitation of Madame Danglars.", "드브레가 말했다. 그는 당글라르 부인의 동요를 놓치지 않고 있었다."]], [["“Ah, what else is there?”", "\"아, 또 뭐가 있다는 겁니까?\""], ["said Danglars; “for, at present, I cannot say that I have seen anything extraordinary.", "당글라르가 말했다. \"지금까지 저는 별다른 걸 본 것 같지 않은데요."], ["What do you say, M. Cavalcanti?”", "어떻게 생각하십니까, 카발칸티 씨?\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["said he, “we have at Pisa, Ugolino’s tower; at Ferrara, Tasso’s prison; at Rimini, the room of Francesca and Paolo.”", "그가 말했다. \"우리한테는 피사에 우골리노의 탑이 있고, 페라라에 타소의 감옥이 있고, 리미니에 프란체스카와 파올로의 방이 있지요.\""]], [["“Yes, but you have not this little staircase,”", "\"네, 하지만 여러분에게는 이 작은 계단은 없지요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, opening a door concealed by the drapery.", "몬테크리스토가 휘장에 가려진 문을 열며 말했다."], ["“Look at it, and tell me what you think of it.”", "\"이걸 보시고, 어떻게 생각하시는지 말씀해 주십시오.\""]], [["“What a wicked-looking, crooked staircase,”", "\"참 음흉하게 생긴 비뚤어진 계단이군요.\""], ["said Château-Renaud with a smile.", "샤토르노가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“I do not know whether the wine of Chios produces melancholy, but certainly everything appears to me black in this house,” said Debray.", "\"키오스산 포도주가 우울을 자아내는지는 모르겠습니다만, 분명 이 집에서는 모든 게 시커멓게 보이는군요.\" 드브레가 말했다."]], [["Ever since Valentine’s dowry had been mentioned, Morrel had been silent and sad.", "발랑틴의 지참금 이야기가 나온 뒤로, 모렐은 줄곧 말이 없고 침울했다."]], [["“Can you imagine,”", "\"상상해 보십시오.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “some Othello or Abbé de Ganges, one stormy, dark night, descending these stairs step by step, carrying a load, which he wishes to hide from the sight of man, if not from God?”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"어떤 오셀로나 강가드 신부가, 폭풍이 몰아치는 어느 캄캄한 밤에, 신에게는 몰라도 사람의 눈에서는 감추고 싶은 짐을 안고서 이 계단을 한 칸 한 칸 내려가는 모습을 말입니다.\""]], [["Madame Danglars half fainted on the arm of Villefort, who was obliged to support himself against the wall.", "당글라르 부인은 빌포르의 팔에 반쯤 기절하며 쓰러졌고, 빌포르는 벽에 몸을 기대지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“Ah, madame,”", "\"아, 부인,\""], ["cried Debray, “what is the matter with you?", "드브레가 외쳤다. \"왜 그러십니까?"], ["how pale you look!”", "어쩌면 그렇게 창백하세요!\""]], [["“It is very evident what is the matter with her,”", "\"무엇 때문인지는 뻔하지요.\" 빌포르 부인이 말했다."], ["said Madame de Villefort; “M. de Monte Cristo is relating horrible stories to us, doubtless intending to frighten us to death.”", "\"몬테크리스토 씨가 우리한테 끔찍한 이야기를 들려주고 계시잖아요. 분명 우리를 겁에 질려 죽게 만들 작정이신 거예요.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇습니다.\""], ["said Villefort, “really, count, you frighten the ladies.”", "빌포르가 말했다. \"정말이지 백작, 부인들을 겁주고 계시는군요.\""]], [["“What is the matter?”", "\"무슨 일이십니까?\""], ["asked Debray, in a whisper, of Madame Danglars.", "드브레가 당글라르 부인에게 속삭이며 물었다."]], [["“Nothing,” she replied with a violent effort.", "\"아무것도 아니에요.\" 그녀가 안간힘을 쓰며 대답했다."], ["“I want air, that is all.”", "\"그저 바람을 좀 쐬고 싶을 뿐이에요.\""]], [["“Will you come into the garden?”", "\"정원으로 나가시겠습니까?\""], ["said Debray, advancing towards the back staircase.", "드브레가 뒤쪽 계단으로 다가서며 말했다."]], [["“No, no,”", "\"아니, 아니에요.\""], ["she answered, “I would rather remain here.”", "그녀가 대답했다. \"차라리 여기 있는 게 낫겠어요.\""]], [["“Are you really frightened, madame?”", "\"정말로 겁이 나십니까, 부인?\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, no, sir,”", "\"오, 아니에요, 백작님.\""], ["said Madame Danglars; “but you suppose scenes in a manner which gives them the appearance of reality.” 30223m", "당글라르 부인이 말했다. \"다만 당신은 장면들을 떠올리는 솜씨가 어찌나 실감 나는지, 마치 진짜처럼 보여서요.\""]], [["“Ah, yes,”", "\"아, 그렇지요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo smiling; “it is all a matter of imagination.", "몬테크리스토가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"다 상상하기 나름이지요."], ["Why should we not imagine this the apartment of an honest mother?", "어째서 이 방을 어느 정숙한 어머니의 방으로 상상하면 안 되겠습니까?"], ["And this bed with red hangings, a bed visited by the goddess Lucina?", "그리고 붉은 휘장이 드리워진 이 침대를, 출산의 여신 루키나가 찾아든 침대로요."], ["And that mysterious staircase, the passage through which, not to disturb their sleep, the doctor and nurse pass, or even the father carrying the sleeping child?”", "그리고 저 신비로운 계단을, 잠을 깨우지 않으려고 의사와 유모가, 혹은 잠든 아이를 안은 아버지가 지나다니는 통로로 말입니다.\""]], [["Here Madame Danglars, instead of being calmed by the soft picture, uttered a groan and fainted.", "이때 당글라르 부인은 그 다정한 그림에 진정되기는커녕, 신음을 토하며 기절해 버렸다."]], [["“Madame Danglars is ill,”", "\"당글라르 부인이 편찮으시군요.\""], ["said Villefort; “it would be better to take her to her carriage.”", "빌포르가 말했다. \"마차로 모셔 가는 게 낫겠습니다.\""]], [["“Oh, _mon Dieu!_”", "\"오, 이런(mon Dieu)!\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “and I have forgotten my smelling-bottle!”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"제가 각성제 병을 잊고 있었군요!\""]], [["“I have mine,”", "\"제게 있어요.\""], ["said Madame de Villefort; and she passed over to Monte Cristo a bottle full of the same kind of red liquid whose good properties the count had tested on Edward.", "빌포르 부인이 말했다. 그러고는 백작이 에두아르에게 그 효능을 시험해 본 적이 있는 것과 똑같은 종류의 붉은 액체가 가득 든 병을 몬테크리스토에게 건넸다."]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, taking it from her hand.", "몬테크리스토가 그녀의 손에서 그것을 받아 들며 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네,\""], ["she said, “at your advice I have made the trial.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"당신 조언대로 시험해 보았어요.\""]], [["“And have you succeeded?”", "\"그래서 성공하셨습니까?\""]], [["“I think so.”", "\"그런 것 같아요.\""]], [["Madame Danglars was carried into the adjoining room; Monte Cristo dropped a very small portion of the red liquid upon her lips; she returned to consciousness.", "당글라르 부인은 옆방으로 옮겨졌다. 몬테크리스토는 그 붉은 액체를 아주 조금 그녀의 입술에 떨어뜨렸고, 그녀는 의식을 되찾았다."]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["she cried, “what a frightful dream!”", "그녀가 외쳤다. \"정말 끔찍한 꿈이었어요!\""]], [["Villefort pressed her hand to let her know it was not a dream. They looked for M.", "빌포르는 그것이 꿈이 아니었다는 것을 알리려고 그녀의 손을 꽉 눌렀다."], ["Danglars, but, as he was not especially interested in poetical ideas, he had gone into the garden, and was talking with Major Cavalcanti on the projected railway from Leghorn to Florence.", "사람들이 당글라르 씨를 찾았지만, 그는 시적인 발상에 별다른 흥미가 없던 터라 정원으로 나가 카발칸티 소령과 함께 리보르노에서 피렌체에 이르는 철도 계획에 관해 이야기를 나누고 있었다."], ["Monte Cristo seemed in despair.", "몬테크리스토는 절망한 듯한 기색이었다."], ["He took the arm of Madame Danglars, and conducted her into the garden, where they found Danglars taking coffee between the Cavalcanti.", "그는 당글라르 부인의 팔을 잡고 정원으로 데려갔는데, 거기서 카발칸티 부자 사이에 끼어 커피를 마시고 있는 당글라르를 만났다."]], [["“Really, madame,”", "\"정말이지, 부인,\""], ["he said, “did I alarm you much?”", "그가 말했다. \"제가 부인을 그렇게나 놀라게 했습니까?\""]], [["“Oh, no, sir,”", "\"오, 아니에요, 백작님.\""], ["she answered; “but you know, things impress us differently, according to the mood of our minds.”", "그녀가 대답했다. \"하지만 아시다시피, 마음 상태에 따라 같은 것도 다르게 와닿는 법이잖아요.\""], ["Villefort forced a laugh.", "빌포르가 억지웃음을 지었다."]], [["“And then, you know,”", "\"게다가, 아시다시피,\""], ["he said, “an idea, a supposition, is sufficient.”", "그가 말했다. \"관념 하나, 가정 하나로도 충분하지요.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"음,\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “you may believe me if you like, but it is my opinion that a crime has been committed in this house.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"믿거나 말거나 좋으실 대로 하십시오만, 제 생각엔 이 집에서 범죄가 저질러진 것 같습니다.\""]], [["“Take care,”", "\"조심하세요.\""], ["said Madame de Villefort, “the king’s attorney is here.”", "빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"여기 검사장님이 계시잖아요.\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["replied Monte Cristo, “since that is the case, I will take advantage of his presence to make my declaration.”", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"그렇다면 그분이 계신 김에 제 신고를 하도록 하지요.\""]], [["“Your declaration?”", "\"신고라니요?\""], ["said Villefort.", "빌포르가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, before witnesses.”", "\"네, 증인들 앞에서요.\""]], [["“Oh, this is very interesting,”", "\"오, 이거 아주 흥미롭군요.\""], ["said Debray; “if there really has been a crime, we will investigate it.”", "드브레가 말했다. \"정말로 범죄가 있었다면, 우리가 조사해 봐야지요.\""]], [["“There has been a crime,”", "\"범죄가 있었습니다.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["“Come this way, gentlemen; come, M.", "\"이쪽으로 오시지요, 여러분."], ["Villefort, for a declaration to be available, should be made before the competent authorities.”", "오십시오, 빌포르 씨. 신고가 효력을 가지려면 마땅한 권한을 가진 분 앞에서 해야 하니까요.\""]], [["He then took Villefort’s arm, and, at the same time, holding that of Madame Danglars under his own, he dragged the procureur to the plantain-tree, where the shade was thickest.", "그러고는 빌포르의 팔을 잡고, 동시에 당글라르 부인의 팔도 자기 팔에 끼운 채, 그늘이 가장 짙은 플라타너스 나무 쪽으로 검사장을 끌고 갔다."], ["All the other guests followed.", "다른 손님들도 모두 뒤따랐다."]], [["“Stay,”", "\"보십시오.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “here, in this very spot”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"바로 여기, 이 자리에서\" 하고 그가 땅을 발로 굴렀다."], ["(and he stamped upon the ground), “I had the earth dug up and fresh mould put in, to refresh these old trees; well, my man, digging, found a box, or rather, the iron-work of a box, in the midst of which was the skeleton of a newly born infant.” 30225m", "\"저는 이 오래된 나무들에 기운을 북돋우려고 흙을 파내고 새 거름흙을 넣게 했습니다. 그런데 제 일꾼이 땅을 파다가 상자 하나를, 아니 정확히는 상자의 쇠붙이 부분을 찾아냈는데, 그 한가운데 갓 태어난 아기의 해골이 들어 있었지요.\""]], [["Monte Cristo felt the arm of Madame Danglars stiffen, while that of Villefort trembled.", "몬테크리스토는 당글라르 부인의 팔이 뻣뻣하게 굳고, 빌포르의 팔이 떨리는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“A newly born infant,”", "\"갓 태어난 아기라.\""], ["repeated Debray; “this affair becomes serious!”", "드브레가 되뇌었다. \"이거 일이 심각해지는군요!\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"거 보십시오.\""], ["said Château-Renaud, “I was not wrong just now then, when I said that houses had souls and faces like men, and that their exteriors carried the impress of their characters.", "샤토르노가 말했다. \"그러니까 아까 제가 틀린 말을 한 게 아니었군요. 집에도 사람처럼 영혼과 얼굴이 있고, 그 외양이 그 성격의 흔적을 지니고 있다고 한 말 말입니다."], ["This house was gloomy because it was remorseful: it was remorseful because it concealed a crime.”", "이 집이 음침했던 건 회한에 차 있었기 때문입니다. 회한에 차 있었던 건 범죄를 감추고 있었기 때문이고요.\""]], [["“Who said it was a crime?”", "\"누가 그게 범죄라고 했습니까?\""], ["asked Villefort, with a last effort.", "빌포르가 마지막 안간힘을 다해 물었다."]], [["“How? is it not a crime to bury a living child in a garden?”", "\"뭐라고요? 정원에 산 아이를 묻는 게 범죄가 아니란 말입니까?\""], ["cried Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 외쳤다."], ["“And pray what do you call such an action?”", "\"대체 그런 짓을 뭐라고 부르시겠습니까?\""]], [["“But who said it was buried alive?”", "\"하지만 누가 그 아이를 산 채로 묻었다고 했습니까?\""]], [["“Why bury it there if it were dead?", "\"죽은 아이라면 왜 거기 묻겠습니까?"], ["This garden has never been a cemetery.”", "이 정원은 한 번도 묘지였던 적이 없는데요.\""]], [["“What is done to infanticides in this country?”", "\"이 나라에서는 영아 살해범을 어떻게 처벌합니까?\""], ["asked Major Cavalcanti innocently.", "카발칸티 소령이 천연덕스럽게 물었다."]], [["“Oh, their heads are soon cut off,”", "\"오, 그런 자들은 곧바로 목이 잘리지요.\""], ["said Danglars.", "당글라르가 말했다."]], [["“Ah, indeed?”", "\"아, 그렇습니까?\""], ["said Cavalcanti.", "카발칸티가 말했다."]], [["“I think so; am I not right, M. de Villefort?”", "\"그럴 겁니다. 안 그렇습니까, 빌포르 씨?\""], ["asked Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, count,”", "\"그렇습니다, 백작.\""], ["replied Villefort, in a voice now scarcely human.", "빌포르가 이제는 거의 사람의 것이 아닌 듯한 목소리로 대답했다."]], [["Monte Cristo, seeing that the two persons for whom he had prepared this scene could scarcely endure it, and not wishing to carry it too far, said:", "몬테크리스토는 이 장면을 위해 준비한 두 사람이 거의 견디지 못하는 것을 보고, 또 너무 지나치게 몰고 가고 싶지는 않아서 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“Come, gentlemen,—some coffee, we seem to have forgotten it,”", "\"자, 여러분, 커피를 좀 들지요. 깜빡 잊고 있었군요.\""], ["and he conducted the guests back to the table on the lawn.", "그러고는 손님들을 잔디밭의 식탁으로 다시 데려갔다."]], [["“Indeed, count,”", "\"정말이지, 백작님,\""], ["said Madame Danglars, “I am ashamed to own it, but all your frightful stories have so upset me, that I must beg you to let me sit down;”", "당글라르 부인이 말했다. \"부끄럽지만 솔직히 말씀드리자면, 그 끔찍한 이야기들에 어찌나 마음이 어지러운지, 좀 앉게 해 주셔야겠어요.\""], ["and she fell into a chair.", "그러고는 의자에 주저앉았다."]], [["Monte Cristo bowed, and went to Madame de Villefort.", "몬테크리스토는 고개를 숙여 인사하고는 빌포르 부인에게 갔다."]], [["“I think Madame Danglars again requires your bottle,” he said.", "\"당글라르 부인께 다시 그 병이 필요한 듯합니다.\" 그가 말했다."], ["But before Madame de Villefort could reach her friend, the procureur had found time to whisper to Madame Danglars, “I must speak to you.” “When?”", "그러나 빌포르 부인이 친구에게 다가가기도 전에, 검사장은 당글라르 부인에게 속삭일 틈을 찾아냈다. \"당신과 이야기를 나눠야겠소.\""]], [["“Tomorrow.”", "\"내일.\""]], [["“Where?”", "\"어디서?\""]], [["“In my office, or in the court, if you like,—that is the surest place.”", "\"어디서요?\" \"내 사무실에서, 아니면 원한다면 법원에서. 거기가 가장 안전한 곳이오.\""]], [["“I will be there.”", "\"그리로 가지요.\""]], [["At this moment Madame de Villefort approached.", "이때 빌포르 부인이 다가왔다."]], [["“Thanks, my dear friend,”", "\"고마워요, 친애하는 벗이여.\""], ["said Madame Danglars, trying to smile; “it is over now, and I am much better.”", "당글라르 부인이 미소 지으려 애쓰며 말했다. \"이제 다 지나갔어요. 한결 나아졌답니다.\""]], [["# Chapter 64. The Beggar", "# Chapter 64. The Beggar"]], [["The evening passed on; Madame de Villefort expressed a desire to return to Paris, which Madame Danglars had not dared to do, notwithstanding the uneasiness she experienced.", "저녁 시간이 흘러갔다. 빌포르 부인은 파리로 돌아가고 싶다는 뜻을 비쳤다. 당글라르 부인은 불안한 마음에도 불구하고 차마 그러지 못하던 참이었다."], ["On his wife’s request, M. de Villefort was the first to give the signal of departure.", "아내의 청에 따라 빌포르 씨가 가장 먼저 출발 신호를 보냈다."], ["He offered a seat in his landau to Madame Danglars, that she might be under the care of his wife. As for M.", "그는 당글라르 부인이 자기 아내의 보살핌을 받을 수 있도록 자신의 사륜마차에 한 자리를 권했다."], ["Danglars, absorbed in an interesting conversation with M. Cavalcanti, he paid no attention to anything that was passing.", "당글라르 씨로 말하자면, 카발칸티 씨와 흥미로운 대화에 빠져 주위에서 벌어지는 일에는 도무지 관심을 두지 않았다."], ["While Monte Cristo had begged the smelling-bottle of Madame de Villefort, he had noticed the approach of Villefort to Madame Danglars, and he soon guessed all that had passed between them, though the words had been uttered in so low a voice as hardly to be heard by Madame Danglars.", "몬테크리스토는 빌포르 부인에게 향수병을 청하던 와중에 빌포르가 당글라르 부인에게 다가가는 것을 눈여겨보았고, 두 사람 사이에 오간 모든 이야기를 곧 짐작해 냈다. 비록 그 말이 당글라르 부인조차 거의 알아듣지 못할 만큼 낮은 목소리로 오갔는데도 말이다."], ["Without opposing their arrangements, he allowed Morrel, Château-Renaud, and Debray to leave on horseback, and the ladies in M. de Villefort’s carriage.", "그는 그들의 계획에 반대하지 않고, 모렐과 샤토르노, 데브레는 말을 타고 떠나도록, 부인들은 빌포르 씨의 마차로 떠나도록 내버려 두었다."], ["Danglars, more and more delighted with Major Cavalcanti, had offered him a seat in his carriage.", "카발칸티 소령에게 점점 더 매료된 당글라르는 그에게 자기 마차의 자리를 권했다."], ["Andrea Cavalcanti found his tilbury waiting at the door; the groom, in every respect a caricature of the English fashion, was standing on tiptoe to hold a large iron-gray horse.", "안드레아 카발칸티는 자신의 틸버리가 문 앞에서 기다리는 것을 발견했다. 모든 면에서 영국식 유행을 어설프게 흉내 낸 마부가 발끝으로 서서 커다란 철회색 말을 붙잡고 있었다."]], [["Andrea had spoken very little during dinner; he was an intelligent lad, and he feared to utter some absurdity before so many grand people, amongst whom, with dilating eyes, he saw the king’s attorney.", "안드레아는 만찬 내내 거의 말을 하지 않았다. 그는 영리한 청년이라, 그토록 쟁쟁한 사람들 앞에서, 게다가 그 가운데에는 눈을 휘둥그렇게 뜨고 바라본 검사까지 있었으니, 행여 어리석은 말을 입에 담을까 두려웠던 것이다."], ["Then he had been seized upon by Danglars, who, with a rapid glance at the stiff-necked old major and his modest son, and taking into consideration the hospitality of the count, made up his mind that he was in the society of some nabob come to Paris to finish the worldly education of his heir.", "그러다 당글라르에게 붙들렸는데, 당글라르는 뻣뻣한 목을 한 늙은 소령과 그 겸손한 아들을 재빠르게 훑어보고, 백작의 환대까지 헤아린 끝에, 자기가 지금 상속자의 사교 교육을 마무리하려고 파리에 온 어느 갑부와 어울리고 있다고 마음속으로 단정했다."], ["He contemplated with unspeakable delight the large diamond which shone on the major’s little finger; for the major, like a prudent man, in case of any accident happening to his bank-notes, had immediately converted them into an available asset.", "그는 소령의 새끼손가락에서 빛나는 커다란 다이아몬드를 형언할 수 없는 기쁨으로 바라보았다. 소령은 신중한 사람답게, 지폐에 무슨 일이라도 생길 경우를 대비해 그것을 즉시 쓸 수 있는 자산으로 바꿔 두었던 것이다."], ["Then, after dinner, on the pretext of business, he questioned the father and son upon their mode of living; and the father and son, previously informed that it was through Danglars the one was to receive his 48,000 francs and the other 50,000 livres annually, were so full of affability that they would have shaken hands even with the banker’s servants, so much did their gratitude need an object to expend itself upon.", "그러다 만찬 뒤에는 사업을 핑계로 부자에게 그들의 생활 방식을 캐물었다. 한 사람은 해마다 4만 8천 프랑을, 다른 한 사람은 5만 리브르를 당글라르를 통해 받게 된다는 것을 미리 들어 알고 있던 부자는 어찌나 살갑게 굴었던지, 그 고마움을 쏟아부을 대상이 어찌나 필요했던지, 은행가의 하인들과도 기꺼이 악수를 나누었을 정도였다."]], [["One thing above all the rest heightened the respect, nay almost the veneration, of Danglars for Cavalcanti.", "무엇보다도 당글라르가 카발칸티에게 품은 존경, 아니 거의 숭배에 가까운 마음을 한층 드높인 일이 하나 있었다."], ["The latter, faithful to the principle of Horace, _nil admirari_, had contented himself with showing his knowledge by declaring in what lake the best lampreys were caught.", "카발칸티는 \"아무것도 경탄하지 말라\"는 호라티우스의 원칙에 충실하여, 어느 호수에서 최상의 칠성장어가 잡히는지 밝히는 정도로 자신의 식견을 드러내는 데 만족했다."], ["Then he had eaten some without saying a word more; Danglars, therefore, concluded that such luxuries were common at the table of the illustrious descendant of the Cavalcanti, who most likely in Lucca fed upon trout brought from Switzerland, and lobsters sent from England, by the same means used by the count to bring the lampreys from Lake Fusaro, and the sterlet from the Volga.", "그러고는 더 말을 보태지 않고 그것을 몇 점 먹었다. 그리하여 당글라르는 이런 진미가 카발칸티의 그 빛나는 후예의 식탁에서는 흔한 것이려니 결론지었다. 아마도 그는 루카에서, 백작이 푸사로 호수에서 칠성장어를, 볼가강에서 철갑상어를 가져오는 것과 똑같은 방법으로, 스위스에서 가져온 송어와 영국에서 보내온 바닷가재를 먹고 살 터였다."], ["Thus it was with much politeness of manner that he heard Cavalcanti pronounce these words:", "그리하여 그는 카발칸티가 다음과 같은 말을 꺼냈을 때 매우 정중한 태도로 귀를 기울였다."]], [["“Tomorrow, sir, I shall have the honor of waiting upon you on business.”", "\"내일, 선생, 사업차 찾아뵙는 영광을 누리겠습니다.\""]], [["“And I, sir,”", "\"저 또한, 선생,\""], ["said Danglars, “shall be most happy to receive you.”", "당글라르가 말했다. \"기꺼이 맞이하겠습니다.\""]], [["Upon which he offered to take Cavalcanti in his carriage to the Hôtel des Princes, if it would not be depriving him of the company of his son.", "그러면서 그는, 아들과 동행하는 즐거움을 빼앗는 것이 아니라면, 자기 마차로 카발칸티를 프랭스 호텔까지 모셔다드리겠노라 청했다."], ["To this Cavalcanti replied by saying that for some time past his son had lived independently of him, that he had his own horses and carriages, and that not having come together, it would not be difficult for them to leave separately.", "이에 카발칸티는, 얼마 전부터 아들이 자기와 따로 살며 제 말과 마차를 두고 있고, 함께 오지 않았으니 따로 떠나는 것도 어렵지 않을 거라고 답했다. 그리하여 소령은 당글라르 곁에 자리를 잡았다."], ["The major seated himself, therefore, by the side of Danglars, who was more and more charmed with the ideas of order and economy which ruled this man, and yet who, being able to allow his son 60,000 francs a year, might be supposed to possess a fortune of 500,000 or 600,000 livres.", "당글라르는 이 사내를 지배하는 질서와 절약의 관념에 점점 더 매료되었다. 더구나 아들에게 한 해 6만 프랑을 줄 수 있는 사람이라면 응당 50만 내지 60만 리브르의 재산을 가졌으리라 짐작되었다."]], [["As for Andrea, he began, by way of showing off, to scold his groom, who, instead of bringing the tilbury to the steps of the house, had taken it to the outer door, thus giving him the trouble of walking thirty steps to reach it.", "안드레아로 말하자면, 자신을 과시할 요량으로 마부를 꾸짖기 시작했다. 마부가 틸버리를 집의 현관 계단 앞으로 가져오지 않고 바깥문 쪽에 세워 두는 바람에, 거기까지 서른 걸음이나 걸어가는 수고를 끼쳤다는 것이었다."], ["The groom heard him with humility, took the bit of the impatient animal with his left hand, and with the right held out the reins to Andrea, who, taking them from him, rested his polished boot lightly on the step.", "마부는 공손히 그 꾸지람을 듣고서, 왼손으로 안달하는 말의 재갈을 잡고 오른손으로 안드레아에게 고삐를 내밀었다. 안드레아는 그 고삐를 받아 들고는 윤이 나는 장화를 발판에 가볍게 올렸다."]], [["At that moment a hand touched his shoulder.", "그 순간 누군가의 손이 그의 어깨를 건드렸다."], ["The young man turned round, thinking that Danglars or Monte Cristo had forgotten something they wished to tell him, and had returned just as they were starting.", "청년은 당글라르나 몬테크리스토가 무언가 할 말을 잊고서 막 출발하려던 차에 되돌아온 것이려니 여기며 몸을 돌렸다. 그러나 그 둘 가운데 누구도 아니라, 낯선 얼굴 하나가 보일 뿐이었다."], ["But instead of either of these, he saw nothing but a strange face, sunburnt, and encircled by a beard, with eyes brilliant as carbuncles, and a smile upon the mouth which displayed a perfect set of white teeth, pointed and sharp as the wolf’s or jackal’s.", "햇볕에 그을린 데다 수염으로 둘러싸인 얼굴, 석류석처럼 번득이는 눈, 그리고 입가에는 늑대나 자칼의 것처럼 뾰족하고 날카로운 새하얀 이를 고스란히 드러낸 미소가 걸려 있었다. 붉은 손수건이 그 잿빛 머리를 동여매고 있었다."], ["A red handkerchief encircled his gray head; torn and filthy garments covered his large bony limbs, which seemed as though, like those of a skeleton, they would rattle as he walked; and the hand with which he leaned upon the young man’s shoulder, and which was the first thing Andrea saw, seemed of gigantic size. 30229m", "찢기고 더러운 옷가지가 그 크고 앙상한 사지를 덮고 있었는데, 마치 해골의 뼈처럼 걸을 때마다 달그락거릴 것만 같았다. 그가 청년의 어깨를 짚고 있던 손, 안드레아가 가장 먼저 본 그 손은 거인의 것처럼 커 보였다."]], [["Did the young man recognize that face by the light of the lantern in his tilbury, or was he merely struck with the horrible appearance of his interrogator?", "청년은 틸버리의 등불 빛으로 그 얼굴을 알아본 것일까, 아니면 그저 질문을 던지는 그자의 끔찍한 몰골에 놀란 것일까? 우리로서는 알 수 없다."], ["We cannot say; but only relate the fact that he shuddered and stepped back suddenly.", "다만 그가 몸서리치며 별안간 뒷걸음질 쳤다는 사실만 전할 따름이다."]], [["“What do you want of me?”", "\"나한테 뭘 원하는 거요?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“Pardon me, my friend, if I disturb you,”", "\"이보게, 친구, 방해해서 미안하네,\""], ["said the man with the red handkerchief, “but I want to speak to you.”", "붉은 손수건을 두른 사내가 말했다. \"하지만 자네와 이야기를 좀 나누고 싶어서 말이야.\""]], [["“You have no right to beg at night,”", "\"밤에 구걸할 권리는 없소.\""], ["said the groom, endeavoring to rid his master of the troublesome intruder.", "마부가 거추장스러운 침입자로부터 주인을 떼어 놓으려 애쓰며 말했다."]], [["“I am not begging, my fine fellow,”", "\"구걸하는 게 아니다, 이 잘난 친구야.\" 낯선 사내가 하인을 향해 말했다."], ["said the unknown to the servant, with so ironical an expression of the eye, and so frightful a smile, that he withdrew; “I only wish to say two or three words to your master, who gave me a commission to execute about a fortnight ago.”", "그 눈빛이 어찌나 비웃는 듯하고 미소가 어찌나 섬뜩했던지 하인은 물러서고 말았다. \"그저 자네 주인한테 두세 마디 하려는 것뿐이야. 보름쯤 전에 나한테 일을 하나 맡긴 분이거든.\""]], [["“Come,”", "\"이리 와.\""], ["said Andrea, with sufficient nerve for his servant not to perceive his agitation, “what do you want?", "안드레아가, 하인이 자신의 동요를 알아채지 못할 만큼 충분한 침착함을 짜내며 말했다. \"원하는 게 뭐요?"], ["Speak quickly, friend.”", "빨리 말해 보시오, 친구.\""]], [["The man said, in a low voice: “I wish—I wish you to spare me the walk back to Paris.", "사내가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"내가 바라는 건, 파리까지 걸어 돌아가는 수고를 덜어 줬으면 하는 거야."], ["I am very tired, and as I have not eaten so good a dinner as you, I can scarcely stand.”", "어찌나 지쳤는지, 자네만큼 잘 차려진 저녁도 못 먹은 터라 서 있기도 힘들거든.\""]], [["The young man shuddered at this strange familiarity.", "청년은 이 기묘한 허물없음에 몸서리쳤다."]], [["“Tell me,”", "\"말해 보시오.\""], ["he said—“tell me what you want?”", "그가 말했다. \"원하는 게 뭔지 말해 보란 말이오.\""]], [["“Well, then, I want you to take me up in your fine carriage, and carry me back.” Andrea turned pale, but said nothing.", "\"그러니까, 자네의 그 멋진 마차에 나를 태워 데려다 줬으면 한다는 거야.\" 안드레아의 얼굴이 창백해졌으나, 그는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["said the man, thrusting his hands into his pockets, and looking impudently at the youth; “I have taken the whim into my head; do you understand, Master Benedetto?”", "사내가 주머니에 두 손을 찔러 넣고 뻔뻔스럽게 청년을 바라보며 말했다. \"이런 변덕이 머릿속에 들어차 버렸지 뭐냐. 무슨 말인지 알겠나, 베네데토 도련님?\""]], [["At this name, no doubt, the young man reflected a little, for he went towards his groom, saying:", "이 이름에, 청년은 분명 잠시 생각에 잠겼다. 그가 마부에게로 다가가며 말했던 것이다."]], [["“This man is right; I did indeed charge him with a commission, the result of which he must tell me; walk to the barrier, there take a cab, that you may not be too late.”", "\"이 사람 말이 맞아. 내가 정말로 이 사람한테 일을 하나 맡겼는데, 그 결과를 들어야겠어. 자네는 검문소까지 걸어가서 거기서 합승 마차를 잡게. 너무 늦지 않도록 말이야.\""]], [["The surprised groom retired.", "놀란 마부가 물러갔다."]], [["“Let me at least reach a shady spot,”", "\"적어도 그늘진 데까지는 가게 해 주시오.\""], ["said Andrea.", "안드레아가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, as for that, I’ll take you to a splendid place,”", "\"오, 그쯤이야, 근사한 데로 데려다주지.\""], ["said the man with the handkerchief; and taking the horse’s bit he led the tilbury where it was certainly impossible for anyone to witness the honor that Andrea conferred upon him.", "손수건을 두른 사내가 말했다. 그러고는 말의 재갈을 잡고 틸버리를 끌고 갔다. 그곳은 분명, 안드레아가 그에게 베푸는 영광을 그 누구도 목격할 수 없을 만한 자리였다."]], [["“Don’t think I want the glory of riding in your fine carriage,”", "\"내가 자네 멋진 마차를 타는 영예를 바란다고는 생각지 말게.\""], ["said he; “oh, no, it’s only because I am tired, and also because I have a little business to talk over with you.”", "그가 말했다. \"오, 천만에, 그저 지친 데다, 자네와 의논할 자그마한 일이 좀 있어서 그러는 거야.\""]], [["“Come, step in,” said the young man.", "\"자, 올라타시오.\" 청년이 말했다."], ["It was a pity this scene had not occurred in daylight, for it was curious to see this rascal throwing himself heavily down on the cushion beside the young and elegant driver of the tilbury.", "이 장면이 대낮에 벌어지지 않은 것은 안타까운 일이었다. 이 악당이 젊고 우아한 틸버리 운전자 곁의 방석 위로 육중하게 몸을 던지는 광경은 꽤나 볼만했을 테니 말이다."], ["Andrea drove past the last house in the village without saying a word to his companion, who smiled complacently, as though well-pleased to find himself travelling in so comfortable a vehicle.", "안드레아는 마을의 마지막 집을 지날 때까지 일행에게 한마디도 하지 않았고, 사내는 이토록 안락한 탈것으로 여행하게 된 것이 흡족하기 그지없다는 듯 만족스럽게 미소 지었다."], ["Once out of Auteuil, Andrea looked around, in order to assure himself that he could neither be seen nor heard, and then, stopping the horse and crossing his arms before the man, he asked:", "오퇴유를 벗어나자 안드레아는 보는 이도 듣는 이도 없음을 확인하려고 주위를 둘러보았다. 그러고는 말을 세우고 사내 앞에서 두 팔을 가로지른 채 물었다."]], [["“Now, tell me why you come to disturb my tranquillity?”", "\"자, 무슨 일로 내 평온을 어지럽히러 온 건지 말해 보시오.\""]], [["“Let me ask you why you deceived me?”", "\"자네야말로 왜 나를 속였는지 물어봐도 되겠나?\""]], [["“How have I deceived you?”", "\"내가 어떻게 당신을 속였다는 거요?\""]], [["“‘How,’ do you ask?", "\"'어떻게'라니?"], ["When we parted at the Pont du Var, you told me you were going to travel through Piedmont and Tuscany; but instead of that, you come to Paris.”", "우리가 바르 다리에서 헤어질 때, 자넨 피에몬테와 토스카나를 거쳐 여행하겠다고 했지. 그런데 그러기는커녕 파리로 왔지 않나.\""]], [["“How does that annoy you?”", "\"그게 당신을 언짢게 할 일이오?\""]], [["“It does not; on the contrary, I think it will answer my purpose.”", "\"그렇지 않아. 오히려 내 목적에 들어맞을 것 같은걸.\""]], [["“So,”", "\"그러니까,\""], ["said Andrea, “you are speculating upon me?”", "안드레아가 말했다. \"나를 두고 한몫 잡아 보겠다는 거요?\""]], [["“What fine words he uses!”", "\"말도 참 그럴듯하게 하는구나!\""]], [["“I warn you, Master Caderousse, that you are mistaken.”", "\"미리 경고하는데, 카드루스 양반, 당신이 잘못 짚었소.\""]], [["“Well, well, don’t be angry, my boy; you know well enough what it is to be unfortunate; and misfortunes make us jealous.", "\"자, 자, 화내지 마, 이 친구야. 불행이 어떤 건지는 자네도 잘 알 텐데. 불행은 사람을 시샘하게 만드는 법이지."], ["I thought you were earning a living in Tuscany or Piedmont by acting as _facchino_ or _cicerone_, and I pitied you sincerely, as I would a child of my own.", "난 자네가 토스카나나 피에몬테에서 짐꾼이나 길잡이 노릇을 하며 입에 풀칠이나 하는 줄 알았어. 그래서 내 자식이라도 되는 양 진심으로 가엾게 여겼지."], ["You know I always did call you my child.”", "알다시피 난 늘 자넬 내 자식이라 불렀잖나.\""]], [["“Come, come, what then?”", "\"좋아, 좋아, 그래서?\""]], [["“Patience—patience!”", "\"진득하게, 진득하게!\""]], [["“I am patient, but go on.”", "\"진득하게 듣고 있으니, 어서 계속하시오.\""]], [["“All at once I see you pass through the barrier with a groom, a tilbury, and fine new clothes.", "\"그러던 어느 날 갑자기, 마부에 틸버리에 새 옷까지 멋들어지게 차려입고 검문소를 지나는 자네를 보게 된 거야."], ["You must have discovered a mine, or else become a stockbroker.”", "광맥이라도 발견했거나, 아니면 주식 중개인이라도 됐겠지.\""]], [["“So that, as you confess, you are jealous?”", "\"그래서, 당신 입으로 인정하듯, 시샘이 난다는 거요?\""]], [["“No, I am pleased—so pleased that I wished to congratulate you; but as I am not quite properly dressed, I chose my opportunity, that I might not compromise you.”", "\"아니, 난 기쁘다네. 어찌나 기쁜지 축하를 해 주고 싶을 정도였지. 다만 내 행색이 영 변변치 못해서, 자네를 곤란하게 하지 않으려고 때를 골랐던 거야.\""]], [["“Yes, and a fine opportunity you have chosen!”", "\"그래, 참 근사한 때를 골랐구려!\""], ["exclaimed Andrea; “you speak to me before my servant.”", "안드레아가 소리쳤다. \"내 하인 앞에서 나한테 말을 걸다니.\""]], [["“How can I help that, my boy?", "\"낸들 어쩌겠나, 이 친구야?"], ["I speak to you when I can catch you.", "잡을 수 있을 때 말을 걸 수밖에."], ["You have a quick horse, a light tilbury, you are naturally as slippery as an eel; if I had missed you tonight, I might not have had another chance.”", "자넨 빠른 말에, 가벼운 틸버리에, 본래 뱀장어처럼 미끄러운 사람이잖나. 오늘 밤 자넬 놓쳤다면, 다시는 기회가 없었을지도 모르지.\""]], [["“You see, I do not conceal myself.”", "\"보다시피, 난 몸을 숨기지 않소.\""]], [["“You are lucky; I wish I could say as much, for I do conceal myself; and then I was afraid you would not recognize me, but you did,”", "\"운이 좋군. 나도 그렇게 말할 수 있다면 좋으련만, 난 몸을 숨기거든. 게다가 자네가 날 못 알아볼까 봐 걱정했는데, 알아보더군.\""], ["added Caderousse with his unpleasant smile.", "카드루스가 그 불쾌한 미소를 지으며 덧붙였다."], ["“It was very polite of you.”", "\"참 예의 바른 일이었어.\""]], [["“Come,”", "\"자,\""], ["said Andrea, “what do you want?”", "안드레아가 말했다. \"원하는 게 뭐요?\""]], [["“You do not speak affectionately to me, Benedetto, my old friend, that is not right—take care, or I may become troublesome.”", "\"베네데토, 내 오랜 친구, 나한테 살갑게 말하지 않는구나. 그건 옳지 않아. 조심하게, 안 그러면 내가 성가신 사람이 될 수도 있으니.\""]], [["This menace smothered the young man’s passion. He urged the horse again into a trot.", "이 위협에 청년의 격정이 사그라들었다. 그는 다시 말을 몰아 빠른 걸음으로 나아가게 했다."]], [["“You should not speak so to an old friend like me, Caderousse, as you said just now; you are a native of Marseilles, I am——”", "\"방금 자네 입으로 말했듯, 나 같은 오랜 친구한테 그렇게 말하면 못쓰지, 카드루스. 자넨 마르세유 태생이고, 난——\""]], [["“Do you know then now what you are?”", "\"그러니까 자넨 이제 자기가 뭔지 알기는 하나?\""]], [["“No, but I was brought up in Corsica; you are old and obstinate, I am young and wilful.", "\"아니, 하지만 난 코르시카에서 자랐소. 당신은 늙고 고집스럽고, 난 젊고 제멋대로요."], ["Between people like us threats are out of place, everything should be amicably arranged.", "우리 같은 사람들 사이에 위협은 어울리지 않소. 모든 건 사이좋게 풀어야지."], ["Is it my fault if fortune, which has frowned on you, has been kind to me?”", "운명이 당신한테는 눈살을 찌푸리고 나한테는 너그러웠던 게 내 탓이오?\""]], [["“Fortune has been kind to you, then?", "\"그래, 운명이 자네한테는 너그러웠다?"], ["Your tilbury, your groom, your clothes, are not then hired?", "그럼 자네 틸버리며, 마부며, 옷가지가 다 빌린 게 아니라는 거지?"], ["Good, so much the better,”", "좋아, 그렇다면 더 잘됐군.\""], ["said Caderousse, his eyes sparkling with avarice.", "카드루스가 탐욕으로 눈을 번득이며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, you knew that well enough before speaking to me,”", "\"오, 그건 나한테 말을 걸기 전에 이미 잘 알고 있었잖소.\""], ["said Andrea, becoming more and more excited.", "안드레아가 점점 더 흥분하며 말했다."], ["“If I had been wearing a handkerchief like yours on my head, rags on my back, and worn-out shoes on my feet, you would not have known me.”", "\"내가 당신처럼 머리에 손수건을 두르고, 등에는 누더기를 걸치고, 발에는 다 해진 신발을 신고 있었다면, 당신은 날 알아보지도 않았을 거요.\""]], [["“You wrong me, my boy; now I have found you, nothing prevents my being as well-dressed as anyone, knowing, as I do, the goodness of your heart.", "\"그건 날 오해하는 거다, 이 친구야. 이제 자넬 찾았으니, 자네 마음씨가 얼마나 고운지 잘 아는 마당에, 나라고 누구 못지않게 잘 차려입지 못할 까닭이 없지."], ["If you have two coats you will give me one of them.", "자네한테 외투가 두 벌 있으면 그중 한 벌을 내게 줄 테고."], ["I used to divide my soup and beans with you when you were hungry.”", "자네가 굶주릴 때, 난 내 수프와 콩을 자네와 나눠 먹곤 했잖나.\""]], [["“True,”", "\"맞소.\""], ["said Andrea.", "안드레아가 말했다."]], [["“What an appetite you used to have!", "\"자네 그 식욕이란 게 어찌나 대단했던지!"], ["Is it as good now?”", "지금도 여전한가?\""]], [["“Oh, yes,” replied Andrea, laughing.", "\"오, 그럼요.\" 안드레아가 웃으며 대답했다."]], [["“How did you come to be dining with that prince whose house you have just left?”", "\"방금 자네가 나온 그 저택의 공작과는 어쩌다 함께 식사를 하게 된 거지?\""]], [["“He is not a prince; simply a count.”", "\"공작이 아니오. 그저 백작이지.\""]], [["“A count, and a rich one too, eh?”", "\"백작이라, 게다가 돈 많은 백작이렷다, 응?\""]], [["“Yes; but you had better not have anything to say to him, for he is not a very good-tempered gentleman.”", "\"그렇소. 하지만 당신은 그 사람한테 무슨 볼일도 두지 않는 게 좋을 거요. 그리 너그러운 양반이 아니거든.\""]], [["“Oh, be easy!", "\"오, 안심하게!"], ["I have no design upon your count, and you shall have him all to yourself. But,”", "자네의 그 백작한테 무슨 꿍꿍이도 없으니, 그자는 통째로 자네 차지로 두지. 다만,\""], ["said Caderousse, again smiling with the disagreeable expression he had before assumed, “you must pay for it—you understand?”", "카드루스가 또다시 아까의 그 못마땅한 표정으로 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그 대가는 치러야겠어. 무슨 말인지 알겠지?\""]], [["“Well, what do you want?”", "\"좋소, 원하는 게 뭐요?\""]], [["“I think that with a hundred francs a month——”", "\"내 생각엔, 한 달에 백 프랑이면——\" \"그러면?\""]], [["“Well?” “I could live——”", "\"먹고살 수 있을 것 같은데——\""]], [["“Upon a hundred francs!”", "\"백 프랑으로!\""]], [["“Come—you understand me; but that with——”", "\"자, 내 말을 알아듣겠지. 다만 거기에——\""]], [["“With?”", "\"거기에?\""]], [["“With a hundred and fifty francs I should be quite happy.”", "\"백오십 프랑이면 아주 흡족하겠어.\""]], [["“Here are two hundred,”", "\"여기 이백 프랑이 있소.\""], ["said Andrea; and he placed ten gold louis in the hand of Caderousse. 30233m", "안드레아가 말했다. 그러고는 카드루스의 손에 금화 열 닢을 쥐여 주었다."]], [["“Good!”", "\"좋아!\""], ["said Caderousse.", "카드루스가 말했다."]], [["“Apply to the steward on the first day of every month, and you will receive the same sum.”", "\"매달 초하루에 집사한테 가면, 같은 액수를 받을 거요.\""]], [["“There now, again you degrade me.”", "\"이런, 또 날 모욕하는군.\""]], [["“How so?”", "\"어째서?\""]], [["“By making me apply to the servants, when I want to transact business with you alone.”", "\"자네하고만 거래하고 싶은데, 하인들한테 가게 만들다니.\""]], [["“Well, be it so, then.", "\"좋소, 정 그렇다면."], ["Take it from me then, and so long at least as I receive my income, you shall be paid yours.”", "그럼 나한테서 직접 받으시오. 적어도 내가 내 수입을 받는 한, 당신도 당신 몫을 받게 될 거요.\""]], [["“Come, come; I always said you were a fine fellow, and it is a blessing when good fortune happens to such as you.", "\"자, 자. 난 늘 자네가 멋진 친구라고 말했지. 자네 같은 사람한테 행운이 찾아오는 건 축복이야."], ["But tell me all about it?”", "그런데 그 자초지종을 다 말해 주겠나?\""]], [["“Why do you wish to know?”", "\"왜 알고 싶은 거요?\""], ["asked Cavalcanti.", "카발칸티가 물었다."]], [["“What?", "\"뭐?"], ["do you again defy me?”", "또 날 거역하는 거냐?\""]], [["“No; the fact is, I have found my father.”", "\"아니오. 실은 내가 아버지를 찾았소.\""]], [["“What?", "\"뭐?"], ["a real father?”", "진짜 아버지를?\""]], [["“Yes, so long as he pays me——”", "\"그렇소, 그자가 돈을 주는 한은——\""]], [["“You’ll honor and believe him—that’s right.", "\"그러면 그를 떠받들고 믿어야지, 그게 맞아."], ["What is his name?”", "이름이 뭔가?\""]], [["“Major Cavalcanti.”", "\"카발칸티 소령이오.\""]], [["“Is he pleased with you?”", "\"그자가 자네한테 만족하던가?\""]], [["“So far I have appeared to answer his purpose.”", "\"지금까지는 내가 그 사람 목적에 맞아떨어진 듯하오.\""]], [["“And who found this father for you?”", "\"그런데 누가 자네한테 그런 아버지를 찾아 줬지?\""]], [["“The Count of Monte Cristo.”", "\"몬테크리스토 백작이오.\""]], [["“The man whose house you have just left?”", "\"방금 자네가 나온 그 저택의 사람 말인가?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["“I wish you would try and find me a situation with him as grandfather, since he holds the money-chest!”", "\"돈궤를 쥐고 있는 사람이라니, 자네가 나한테도 그자의 할아버지 자리라도 하나 찾아 줬으면 좋겠군!\""]], [["“Well, I will mention you to him.", "\"좋소, 그 사람한테 당신 얘기를 해 두지."], ["Meanwhile, what are you going to do?” “I?”", "그건 그렇고, 당신은 앞으로 뭘 할 작정이오?\""]], [["“Yes, you.”", "\"내가?\""]], [["“It is very kind of you to trouble yourself about me.”", "\"그렇소, 당신 말이오.\" \"날 그렇게 걱정해 주다니 참 고맙구나.\""]], [["“Since you interest yourself in my affairs, I think it is now my turn to ask you some questions.”", "\"당신이 내 일에 관심을 가지니, 이제 내가 당신한테 몇 가지 물어볼 차례인 것 같소만.\""]], [["“Ah, true. Well; I shall rent a room in some respectable house, wear a decent coat, shave every day, and go and read the papers in a café.", "\"아, 그렇지. 그럼, 어디 점잖은 집에 방을 하나 빌려서, 번듯한 외투를 걸치고, 매일 면도를 하고, 카페에 가서 신문이나 읽을 거야."], ["Then, in the evening, I shall go to the theatre; I shall look like some retired baker.", "그러고는 저녁이면 극장에 가야지. 은퇴한 빵집 주인쯤으로 보이겠지."], ["That is what I want.”", "내가 원하는 게 그거야.\""]], [["“Come, if you will only put this scheme into execution, and be steady, nothing could be better.”", "\"좋소, 그 계획대로만 하고 진득하게 처신한다면, 더없이 좋겠소.\""]], [["“Do you think so, M. Bossuet?", "\"그렇게 생각하나, 보쉬에 선생?"], ["And you—what will you become?", "그럼 자넨——자넨 뭐가 될 텐가?"], ["A peer of France?”", "프랑스의 귀족?\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["said Andrea, “who knows?”", "안드레아가 말했다. \"누가 알겠소?\""]], [["“Major Cavalcanti is already one, perhaps; but then, hereditary rank is abolished.”", "\"카발칸티 소령은 이미 귀족인지도 모르지. 하지만 세습 작위는 폐지됐으니 말이야.\""]], [["“No politics, Caderousse. And now that you have all you want, and that we understand each other, jump down from the tilbury and disappear.”", "\"정치 얘기는 그만둡시다, 카드루스. 자, 이제 원하는 건 다 손에 넣었고, 우리도 서로 뜻이 통했으니, 틸버리에서 내려서 사라져 주시오.\""]], [["“Not at all, my good friend.”", "\"천만에, 이 친구야.\""]], [["“How?", "\"뭐요?"], ["Not at all?”", "천만에라니?\""]], [["“Why, just think for a moment; with this red handkerchief on my head, with scarcely any shoes, no papers, and ten gold napoleons in my pocket, without reckoning what was there before—making in all about two hundred francs,—why, I should certainly be arrested at the barriers.", "\"아니, 잠깐만 생각해 보게. 이렇게 머리에 붉은 손수건을 두르고, 신발이라고 할 것도 없는 데다, 신분증도 없고, 주머니에는 금화 나폴레옹 열 닢에다, 거기에 본래 있던 것까지 합치면 대략 이백 프랑이 들었으니——나야 검문소에서 영락없이 붙들리고 말 거야."], ["Then, to justify myself, I should say that you gave me the money; this would cause inquiries, it would be found that I left Toulon without giving due notice, and I should then be escorted back to the shores of the Mediterranean.", "그러면 변명을 하느라 자네가 준 돈이라고 말할 테고, 그러면 조사가 시작될 테지. 그러다 보면 내가 정당한 신고도 없이 툴롱을 떠난 게 들통날 테고, 결국 난 지중해 연안으로 도로 호송될 거야. 그러면 난 그저 죄수 106호가 되고 마는 거지."], ["Then I should become simply No. 106, and good-bye to my dream of resembling the retired baker!", "은퇴한 빵집 주인을 닮아 보겠다는 내 꿈이여, 잘 가거라! 안 돼, 안 돼, 이 친구야."], ["No, no, my boy; I prefer remaining honorably in the capital.”", "난 수도에서 떳떳하게 지내는 편이 좋다네.\""]], [["Andrea scowled. Certainly, as he had himself owned, the reputed son of Major Cavalcanti was a wilful fellow.", "안드레아가 인상을 찌푸렸다. 분명, 그 자신도 인정했듯, 카발칸티 소령의 양아들은 제멋대로인 위인이었다."], ["He drew up for a minute, threw a rapid glance around him, and then his hand fell instantly into his pocket, where it began playing with a pistol.", "그는 잠시 말을 세우고 주위를 재빠르게 훑어보았다. 그러더니 그의 손이 곧장 주머니로 떨어져, 안에서 권총을 만지작거리기 시작했다."], ["But, meanwhile, Caderousse, who had never taken his eyes off his companion, passed his hand behind his back, and opened a long Spanish knife, which he always carried with him, to be ready in case of need.", "그러나 그사이, 한순간도 일행에게서 눈을 떼지 않던 카드루스는 손을 등 뒤로 돌려, 만일에 대비해 늘 지니고 다니는 기다란 스페인식 칼을 폈다."], ["The two friends, as we see, were worthy of and understood one another.", "보다시피, 이 두 친구는 서로 어울리는 데다 서로의 속을 훤히 꿰뚫고 있었다."], ["Andrea’s hand left his pocket inoffensively, and was carried up to the red moustache, which it played with for some time.", "안드레아의 손은 아무 해 끼치지 않고 주머니에서 빠져나와, 붉은 콧수염으로 올라가더니 한동안 그것을 만지작거렸다."]], [["“Good Caderousse,”", "\"착한 카드루스,\""], ["he said, “how happy you will be.”", "그가 말했다. \"당신은 얼마나 행복할까.\""]], [["“I will do my best,”", "\"최선을 다해 보지.\""], ["said the innkeeper of the Pont du Gard, shutting up his knife.", "가르 다리 여인숙 주인이 칼을 도로 접으며 말했다."]], [["“Well, then, we will go into Paris.", "\"자, 그럼 파리로 갑시다."], ["But how will you pass through the barrier without exciting suspicion?", "그런데 의심을 사지 않고 검문소를 어떻게 지날 셈이오?"], ["It seems to me that you are in more danger riding than on foot.”", "내가 보기엔 걸어가는 것보다 마차를 타는 게 더 위험할 것 같은데.\""]], [["“Wait,” said Caderousse, “we shall see.”", "\"기다려 보게.\" 카드루스가 말했다."], ["He then took the greatcoat with the large collar, which the groom had left behind in the tilbury, and put it on his back; then he took off Cavalcanti’s hat, which he placed upon his own head, and finally he assumed the careless attitude of a servant whose master drives himself.", "\"어디 두고 보자고.\" 그러고는 마부가 틸버리에 두고 간 큼직한 깃의 외투를 집어 등에 걸쳤다. 이어 카발칸티의 모자를 벗겨 자기 머리에 얹고는, 마침내 주인이 직접 마차를 모는 하인의 무심한 자세를 취했다."]], [["“But, tell me,”", "\"그런데 말이오,\""], ["said Andrea, “am I to remain bareheaded?”", "안드레아가 말했다. \"난 그럼 맨머리로 있어야 하오?\""]], [["“Pooh,”", "\"흥,\""], ["said Caderousse; “it is so windy that your hat can easily appear to have blown off.”", "카드루스가 말했다. \"바람이 어찌나 거센지, 자네 모자가 날아가 버렸다고 해도 다들 곧이듣겠어.\""]], [["“Come, come; enough of this,”", "\"자, 자, 그만하면 됐소.\""], ["said Cavalcanti.", "카발칸티가 말했다."]], [["“What are you waiting for?”", "\"뭘 기다리는 건가?\""], ["said Caderousse.", "카드루스가 말했다."], ["“I hope I am not the cause.”", "\"설마 나 때문은 아니겠지.\""]], [["“Hush,”", "\"쉿.\""], ["said Andrea. They passed the barrier without accident.", "안드레아가 말했다. 두 사람은 무사히 검문소를 지났다."], ["At the first cross street Andrea stopped his horse, and Caderousse leaped out.", "첫 번째 교차로에서 안드레아는 말을 세웠고, 카드루스가 훌쩍 뛰어내렸다."]], [["“Well!”", "\"이봐!\""], ["said Andrea,—“my servant’s coat and my hat?”", "안드레아가 말했다. \"내 하인의 외투랑 내 모자는?\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["said Caderousse, “you would not like me to risk taking cold?”", "카드루스가 말했다. \"설마 나더러 감기에 걸릴 위험을 무릅쓰라는 건 아니겠지?\""]], [["“But what am I to do?”", "\"하지만 난 어쩌란 말이오?\""]], [["“You?", "\"자네?"], ["Oh, you are young while I am beginning to get old.", "오, 자넨 젊지 않나. 난 슬슬 늙어 가는 몸이고."], ["_Au revoir_, Benedetto;”", "또 보세, 베네데토.\""], ["and running into a court, he disappeared.", "그러고는 어느 안마당으로 뛰어들어 자취를 감추었다."]], [["“Alas,”", "\"아아,\""], ["said Andrea, sighing, “one cannot be completely happy in this world!”", "안드레아가 한숨지으며 말했다. \"이 세상에서 완전히 행복할 수는 없는 노릇이로군!\""]], [["# Chapter 65. A Conjugal Scene", "# Chapter 65. A Conjugal Scene"]], [["At the Place Louis XV. the three young people separated—that is to say, Morrel went to the Boulevards, Château-Renaud to the Pont de la Révolution, and Debray to the Quai.", "루이 15세 광장에서 세 젊은이는 헤어졌다. 다시 말해, 모렐은 큰길 쪽으로, 샤토르노는 레볼뤼시옹 다리 쪽으로, 드브레는 강변 쪽으로 갔다."], ["Most probably Morrel and Château-Renaud returned to their “domestic hearths,” as they say in the gallery of the Chamber in well-turned speeches, and in the theatre of the Rue Richelieu in well-written pieces; but it was not the case with Debray.", "모렐과 샤토르노는 십중팔구 의회 회랑의 다듬어진 연설이나 리슐리외 거리 극장의 잘 쓰인 희곡에서 흔히 말하듯 자신들의 '가정의 화롯가'로 돌아갔을 것이다. 그러나 드브레는 그렇지 않았다."], ["When he reached the wicket of the Louvre, he turned to the left, galloped across the Carrousel, passed through the Rue Saint-Roch, and, issuing from the Rue de la Michodière, he arrived at M. Danglars’ door just at the same time that Villefort’s landau, after having deposited him and his wife at the Faubourg Saint-Honoré, stopped to leave the baroness at her own house.", "그는 루브르의 작은 문에 이르자 왼쪽으로 꺾어 카루젤 광장을 가로질러 달렸고, 생로크 거리를 지나 미쇼디에르 거리에서 빠져나와, 마침 빌포르의 사륜마차가 빌포르 부부를 포부르생토노레에 내려준 뒤 남작 부인을 자기 집에 내려주려고 멈추는 바로 그 순간 당글라르의 집 문 앞에 도착했다."]], [["Debray, with the air of a man familiar with the house, entered first into the court, threw his bridle into the hands of a footman, and returned to the door to receive Madame Danglars, to whom he offered his arm, to conduct her to her apartments.", "이 집에 익숙한 사람다운 태도로 드브레는 먼저 안마당으로 들어가 하인에게 말고삐를 던지고는 다시 문가로 돌아와 당글라르 부인을 맞이했다. 그는 부인에게 팔을 내밀어 그녀의 방까지 데려다주었다."], ["The gate once closed, and Debray and the baroness alone in the court, he asked:", "대문이 닫히고 안마당에 드브레와 남작 부인 단둘만 남자, 그가 물었다."]], [["“What was the matter with you, Hermine?", "\"무슨 일이 있었던 거요, 에르민?"], ["and why were you so affected at that story, or rather fable, which the count related?”", "어째서 백작이 들려준 그 이야기, 아니 그 우화 같은 것에 그렇게 마음이 흔들렸소?\""]], [["“Because I have been in such shocking spirits all the evening, my friend,” said the baroness.", "\"오늘 저녁 내내 기분이 너무 끔찍했거든요, 여보.\" 남작 부인이 말했다."]], [["“No, Hermine,”", "\"아니오, 에르민.\""], ["replied Debray; “you cannot make me believe that; on the contrary, you were in excellent spirits when you arrived at the count’s. M.", "드브레가 대답했다. \"그런 말로는 날 못 속여요. 오히려 당신은 백작의 집에 도착했을 때만 해도 기분이 더없이 좋았소."], ["Danglars was disagreeable, certainly, but I know how much you care for his ill-humor.", "당글라르 씨가 불쾌하게 굴긴 했지만, 당신이 그 사람 심사 따위 얼마나 마음에 두지 않는지 내가 잘 알지."], ["Someone has vexed you; I will allow no one to annoy you.”", "누군가 당신을 거슬리게 한 거요. 누구도 당신을 괴롭히게 두지 않겠소.\""]], [["“You are deceived, Lucien, I assure you,”", "\"오해예요, 뤼시앵. 정말이에요.\""], ["replied Madame Danglars; “and what I have told you is really the case, added to the ill-humor you remarked, but which I did not think it worth while to allude to.”", "당글라르 부인이 대답했다. \"내가 말한 게 정말 사실이에요. 거기에 당신이 알아챈 그 언짢은 기분이 더해졌을 뿐, 그건 굳이 입에 올릴 가치도 없다고 생각했어요.\""]], [["It was evident that Madame Danglars was suffering from that nervous irritability which women frequently cannot account for even to themselves; or that, as Debray had guessed, she had experienced some secret agitation that she would not acknowledge to anyone.", "당글라르 부인이 여자들이 흔히 스스로도 설명하지 못하는 그 신경질적 흥분에 시달리고 있거나, 아니면 드브레가 짐작한 대로 누구에게도 털어놓고 싶지 않은 어떤 은밀한 동요를 겪은 것이 분명했다."], ["Being a man who knew that the former of these symptoms was one of the inherent penalties of womanhood, he did not then press his inquiries, but waited for a more appropriate opportunity when he should again interrogate her, or receive an avowal _proprio motu_.", "앞의 증상이 여자라는 존재에 따라붙는 천성적 형벌 중 하나임을 아는 남자였기에, 그는 그 자리에서 캐묻기를 밀어붙이지 않고 다시 물어보거나 부인 스스로 고백할 더 적절한 기회를 기다렸다."]], [["At the door of her apartment the baroness met Mademoiselle Cornélie, her confidential maid.", "방문 앞에서 남작 부인은 자신의 측근 하녀인 코르넬리 양과 마주쳤다."]], [["“What is my daughter doing?”", "\"내 딸은 무얼 하고 있느냐?\""], ["asked Madame Danglars.", "당글라르 부인이 물었다."]], [["“She practiced all the evening, and then went to bed,”", "\"저녁 내내 연습을 하시다가 잠자리에 드셨습니다.\""], ["replied Mademoiselle Cornélie.", "코르넬리 양이 대답했다."]], [["“Yet I think I hear her piano.”", "\"그런데 피아노 소리가 들리는 것 같은데.\""]], [["“It is Mademoiselle Louise d’Armilly, who is playing while Mademoiselle Danglars is in bed.”", "\"당글라르 아가씨가 잠자리에 드신 동안 루이즈 다르미이 양이 치고 계십니다.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"좋아.\""], ["said Madame Danglars, “come and undress me.”", "당글라르 부인이 말했다. \"이리 와서 옷 벗는 걸 좀 도와다오.\""]], [["They entered the bedroom.", "그들은 침실로 들어갔다."], ["Debray stretched himself upon a large couch, and Madame Danglars passed into her dressing-room with Mademoiselle Cornélie.", "드브레는 커다란 긴 의자에 몸을 뉘었고, 당글라르 부인은 코르넬리 양과 함께 화장실로 들어갔다."]], [["“My dear M. Lucien,”", "\"내 사랑 뤼시앵 씨.\""], ["said Madame Danglars through the door, “you are always complaining that Eugénie will not address a word to you.”", "당글라르 부인이 문 너머로 말했다. \"당신은 늘 외제니가 당신한테 한마디도 건네지 않는다고 불평하지요.\""]], [["“Madame,”", "\"부인.\""], ["said Lucien, playing with a little dog, who, recognizing him as a friend of the house, expected to be caressed, “I am not the only one who makes similar complaints, I think I heard Morcerf say that he could not extract a word from his betrothed.”", "뤼시앵이, 자신을 이 집의 친구로 알아보고 쓰다듬어 주기를 기대하며 다가오는 작은 개와 장난을 치면서 말했다. \"그런 불평을 하는 게 나만은 아닐 거요. 모르세르가 자기 약혼녀한테서도 말 한마디 끌어낼 수가 없다고 하는 걸 들은 것 같으니까.\""]], [["“True,”", "\"맞아요.\""], ["said Madame Danglars; “yet I think this will all pass off, and that you will one day see her enter your study.”", "당글라르 부인이 말했다. \"하지만 다 지나갈 거예요. 언젠가는 그 애가 당신 집무실로 들어서는 걸 보게 될 테니까요.\""]], [["“My study?”", "\"내 집무실로?\""]], [["“At least that of the minister.”", "\"적어도 장관 집무실로요.\""]], [["“Why so!”", "\"어째서요!\""]], [["“To ask for an engagement at the Opera.", "\"오페라극장 계약을 청하러요."], ["Really, I never saw such an infatuation for music; it is quite ridiculous for a young lady of fashion.”", "정말이지, 그런 음악에 대한 열중은 본 적이 없어요. 상류 사회 아가씨로서는 우스꽝스럽기 짝이 없지요.\""]], [["Debray smiled.", "드브레가 미소 지었다. \"그렇다면,\""], ["“Well,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “let her come, with your consent and that of the baron, and we will try and give her an engagement, though we are very poor to pay such talent as hers.”", "\"당신과 남작의 승낙을 받아 그 아이를 보내요. 우리가 계약을 맺어 보도록 하지요. 비록 그 아이만 한 재능에 보수를 주기엔 우리 형편이 무척 빈약하지만 말이오.\""]], [["“Go, Cornélie,”", "\"가 보거라, 코르넬리.\""], ["said Madame Danglars, “I do not require you any longer.”", "당글라르 부인이 말했다. \"이제 너는 필요 없다.\""]], [["Cornélie obeyed, and the next minute Madame Danglars left her room in a charming loose dress, and came and sat down close to Debray.", "코르넬리가 시키는 대로 물러갔고, 다음 순간 당글라르 부인이 매혹적인 헐렁한 실내복 차림으로 방에서 나와 드브레 곁에 바싹 다가앉았다."], ["Then she began thoughtfully to caress the little spaniel.", "그러고는 생각에 잠긴 채 작은 스패니얼을 쓰다듬기 시작했다."], ["Lucien looked at her for a moment in silence.", "뤼시앵은 잠시 말없이 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["“Come, Hermine,”", "\"자, 에르민.\" 그가 잠시 후 말했다."], ["he said, after a short time, “answer candidly,—something vexes you—is it not so?”", "\"솔직하게 대답해 봐요. 뭔가 당신을 거슬리게 하고 있어. 그렇지 않소?\""]], [["30239m\n“Nothing,”", "\"아무것도 아니에요.\""], ["answered the baroness.", "남작 부인이 대답했다."]], [["And yet, as she could scarcely breathe, she rose and went towards a looking-glass.", "그러면서도 그녀는 숨을 제대로 쉬지 못해 자리에서 일어나 거울 쪽으로 걸어갔다."], ["“I am frightful tonight,” she said.", "\"오늘 밤 나는 끔찍한 몰골이군요.\""], ["Debray rose, smiling, and was about to contradict the baroness upon this latter point, when the door opened suddenly. M.", "그녀가 말했다. 드브레가 미소 지으며 일어나, 이 마지막 말만큼은 반박하려는 참인데, 문이 갑자기 열렸다."], ["Danglars appeared; Debray reseated himself.", "당글라르 씨가 나타났다."], ["At the noise of the door Madame Danglars turned round, and looked upon her husband with an astonishment she took no trouble to conceal.", "드브레는 다시 자리에 앉았다. 문소리에 당글라르 부인이 돌아서서, 감추려는 기색도 없이 놀란 눈으로 남편을 바라보았다."]], [["“Good-evening, madame,”", "\"안녕하시오, 부인.\""], ["said the banker; “good-evening, M. Debray.”", "은행가가 말했다. \"안녕하시오, 드브레 씨.\""]], [["Probably the baroness thought this unexpected visit signified a desire to make up for the sharp words he had uttered during the day.", "남작 부인은 아마도 이 뜻밖의 방문이 낮 동안 그가 내뱉은 날카로운 말들을 만회하려는 뜻이라고 생각했을 것이다."], ["Assuming a dignified air, she turned round to Debray, without answering her husband.", "위엄 있는 태도를 취하며 그녀는 남편에게는 대답도 하지 않고 드브레 쪽으로 돌아앉았다."]], [["“Read me something, M. Debray,” she said.", "\"뭔가 좀 읽어 주세요, 드브레 씨.\""], ["Debray, who was slightly disturbed at this visit, recovered himself when he saw the calmness of the baroness, and took up a book marked by a mother-of-pearl knife inlaid with gold.", "그녀가 말했다. 이 방문에 약간 마음이 어수선했던 드브레는 남작 부인의 침착함을 보고 마음을 가다듬어, 금을 박은 자개 칼이 끼워져 있는 책 한 권을 집어 들었다."]], [["“Excuse me,”", "\"실례지만,\""], ["said the banker, “but you will tire yourself, baroness, by such late hours, and M.", "은행가가 말했다. \"이렇게 늦은 시각까지 깨어 있으면 지치실 거요, 부인."], ["Debray lives some distance from here.”", "게다가 드브레 씨는 여기서 좀 떨어진 곳에 사시니 말이오.\""]], [["Debray was petrified, not only to hear Danglars speak so calmly and politely, but because it was apparent that beneath outward politeness there really lurked a determined spirit of opposition to anything his wife might wish to do.", "드브레는 굳어 버렸다. 당글라르가 그토록 침착하고 정중하게 말하는 것을 들어서만이 아니라, 그 겉치레 정중함 아래 아내가 무얼 하려 하든 단호히 막겠다는 의지가 실제로 도사리고 있음이 분명했기 때문이다."], ["The baroness was also surprised, and showed her astonishment by a look which would doubtless have had some effect upon her husband if he had not been intently occupied with the paper, where he was looking to see the closing stock quotations.", "남작 부인 또한 놀라, 만일 남편이 폐장 시세를 확인하려고 신문에 골몰해 있지 않았더라면 틀림없이 어떤 효과를 발휘했을 눈길로 그 놀라움을 드러냈다."], ["The result was, that the proud look entirely failed of its purpose.", "그 결과, 그 도도한 눈길은 목적을 완전히 놓치고 말았다."]], [["“M. Lucien,”", "\"뤼시앵 씨.\""], ["said the baroness, “I assure you I have no desire to sleep, and that I have a thousand things to tell you this evening, which you must listen to, even though you slept while hearing me.”", "남작 부인이 말했다. \"정말이지 나는 잠잘 마음이 조금도 없고, 오늘 저녁 당신한테 들려줄 이야기가 천 가지나 있어요. 비록 듣는 동안 당신이 잠들어 버린다 해도 들어 주셔야 해요.\""]], [["“I am at your service, madame,”", "\"분부대로 하겠습니다, 부인.\""], ["replied Lucien coldly.", "뤼시앵이 차갑게 대답했다."]], [["“My dear M.", "\"드브레 씨.\" 은행가가 말했다."], ["Debray,”", "\"오늘 밤 당글라르 부인의 어리석은 이야기를 듣느라 목숨을 깎지 마시오."], ["said the banker, “do not kill yourself tonight listening to the follies of Madame Danglars, for you can hear them as well tomorrow; but I claim tonight and will devote it, if you will allow me, to talk over some serious matters with my wife.”", "그런 이야기야 내일 들어도 되는 거 아니오. 허나 오늘 밤만은 내가 차지하겠소. 허락해 주신다면, 아내와 진지한 문제 몇 가지를 의논하는 데 쓰겠소.\""]], [["This time the blow was so well aimed, and hit so directly, that Lucien and the baroness were staggered, and they interrogated each other with their eyes, as if to seek help against this aggression, but the irresistible will of the master of the house prevailed, and the husband was victorious.", "이번에는 그 일격이 워낙 정확히 겨누어져 곧장 명중한 터라, 뤼시앵과 남작 부인은 휘청거리며 마치 이 공격에 맞설 도움을 구하듯 서로 눈으로 물었다. 그러나 이 집 주인의 거역할 수 없는 의지가 우위를 점했고, 남편이 승리를 거두었다."]], [["“Do not think I wish to turn you out, my dear Debray,”", "\"당신을 내쫓으려는 거라고 생각진 마시오, 드브레 씨.\""], ["continued Danglars; “oh, no, not at all.", "당글라르가 말을 이었다. \"오, 아니, 결코 그렇지 않소."], ["An unexpected occurrence forces me to ask my wife to have a little conversation with me; it is so rarely I make such a request, I am sure you cannot grudge it to me.”", "뜻밖의 일이 생겨서 아내와 잠깐 이야기를 나눠야 할 형편이 됐을 뿐이오. 내가 이런 청을 하는 일이야 워낙 드무니, 설마 그걸 아까워하시진 않겠지요.\""]], [["Debray muttered something, bowed and went out, knocking himself against the edge of the door, like Nathan in _Athalie_.", "드브레는 뭐라고 중얼거리며 고개 숙여 인사하고 나갔는데, 「아탈리」에 나오는 나탕처럼 문 모서리에 부딪혔다."]], [["“It is extraordinary,”", "\"참 기이한 일이야.\""], ["he said, when the door was closed behind him, “how easily these husbands, whom we ridicule, gain an advantage over us.” 30241m", "등 뒤로 문이 닫히자 그가 말했다. \"우리가 비웃던 그 남편이라는 자들이 어쩌면 이렇게 손쉽게 우리를 누르는지.\""]], [["Lucien having left, Danglars took his place on the sofa, closed the open book, and placing himself in a dreadfully dictatorial attitude, he began playing with the dog; but the animal, not liking him as well as Debray, and attempting to bite him, Danglars seized him by the skin of his neck and threw him upon a couch on the other side of the room.", "* 「아탈리」: 라신의 비극. 나탕은 그 등장인물. — 옮긴이\n뤼시앵이 떠나자 당글라르가 긴 의자의 그 자리에 앉아, 펼쳐진 책을 덮고는 무섭도록 위압적인 자세를 취하며 개와 장난을 치기 시작했다."], ["The animal uttered a cry during the transit, but, arrived at its destination, it crouched behind the cushions, and stupefied at such unusual treatment remained silent and motionless.", "그러나 그 짐승은 드브레만큼 그를 좋아하지 않아 물려고 들었고, 당글라르는 목덜미 가죽을 움켜쥐어 방 건너편 긴 의자 위로 던져 버렸다. 짐승은 날아가는 동안 비명을 질렀으나, 떨어진 자리에 닿자 쿠션 뒤에 웅크려 그런 유별난 대접에 얼이 빠진 채 잠자코 꼼짝도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Do you know, sir,” asked the baroness, “that you are improving?", "\"당신, 점점 나아지고 있다는 걸 아세요?\" 남작 부인이 물었다."], ["Generally you are only rude, but tonight you are brutal.”", "\"평소엔 무례하기만 하더니, 오늘 밤은 잔인하기까지 하군요.\""]], [["“It is because I am in a worse humor than usual,”", "\"평소보다 기분이 더 나쁘기 때문이오.\""], ["replied Danglars.", "당글라르가 대답했다."], ["Hermine looked at the banker with supreme disdain.", "에르민은 더없는 경멸의 눈으로 은행가를 바라보았다."], ["These glances frequently exasperated the pride of Danglars, but this evening he took no notice of them.", "그런 눈길은 자주 당글라르의 자존심을 격분시키곤 했으나, 이날 저녁만은 그가 거들떠보지도 않았다."]], [["“And what have I to do with your ill-humor?”", "\"그런데 당신 언짢은 기분이 나와 무슨 상관이죠?\""], ["said the baroness, irritated at the impassibility of her husband; “do these things concern me?", "남편의 무감각함에 화가 난 남작 부인이 말했다. \"그런 일이 나와 관계라도 있나요?"], ["Keep your ill-humor at home in your money boxes, or, since you have clerks whom you pay, vent it upon them.”", "언짢은 기분은 집에 있는 당신 금고에나 간직하든가, 아니면 봉급 주는 사무원들이 있으니 그자들한테나 풀어요.\""]], [["“Not so,”", "\"그렇지 않소.\""], ["replied Danglars; “your advice is wrong, so I shall not follow it.", "당글라르가 대답했다. \"당신 충고는 틀렸으니 따르지 않겠소."], ["My money boxes are my Pactolus, as, I think, M. Demoustier says, and I will not retard its course, or disturb its calm.", "내 금고는, 데무스티에 씨가 한 말이었던가, 내 황금강이오. 그 흐름을 막지도, 그 고요를 흩뜨리지도 않겠소."], ["My clerks are honest men, who earn my fortune, whom I pay much below their deserts, if I may value them according to what they bring in; therefore I shall not get into a passion with them; those with whom I will be in a passion are those who eat my dinners, mount my horses, and exhaust my fortune.”", "내 사무원들은 정직한 사람들이고, 내 재산을 벌어다 주지. 그들이 벌어들이는 것에 견주어 값을 매긴다면 받아 마땅한 것보다 훨씬 적은 보수를 주는 셈이니, 그들에게 성을 낼 까닭이 없소. 내가 성을 낼 자들은 내 밥을 먹고, 내 말을 타고, 내 재산을 거덜 내는 자들이오.\""]], [["“And pray who are the persons who exhaust your fortune?", "\"그래, 부디, 당신 재산을 거덜 내는 자들이 누구죠?"], ["Explain yourself more clearly, I beg, sir.”", "더 분명히 말해 봐요, 부탁이에요.\""]], [["“Oh, make yourself easy!—I am not speaking riddles, and you will soon know what I mean.", "\"오, 마음 놓으시오! 수수께끼를 내는 게 아니니, 곧 무슨 뜻인지 알게 될 거요."], ["The people who exhaust my fortune are those who draw out 700,000 francs in the course of an hour.”", "내 재산을 거덜 내는 자들은 한 시간 만에 70만 프랑을 빼내 가는 자들이오.\""]], [["“I do not understand you, sir,”", "\"무슨 말인지 모르겠군요.\""], ["said the baroness, trying to disguise the agitation of her voice and the flush of her face.", "남작 부인이 떨리는 목소리와 붉어진 얼굴을 감추려 애쓰며 말했다."]], [["“You understand me perfectly, on the contrary,”", "\"천만에, 당신은 내 말을 완벽하게 알아듣고 있소.\""], ["said Danglars: “but, if you will persist, I will tell you that I have just lost 700,000 francs upon the Spanish loan.”", "당글라르가 말했다. \"하지만 정 우기겠다면, 내가 방금 스페인 공채로 70만 프랑을 잃었다고 말해 주리다.\""]], [["“And pray,”", "\"그래서요, 부디 묻건대,\""], ["asked the baroness, “am I responsible for this loss?” “Why not?”", "남작 부인이 말했다. \"그 손실에 내가 책임이라도 있다는 건가요?\""]], [["“Is it my fault you have lost 700,000 francs?”", "\"아니라는 법이 있소?\" \"당신이 70만 프랑을 잃은 게 내 잘못이라고요?\""]], [["“Certainly it is not mine.”", "\"분명 내 잘못은 아니지.\""]], [["“Once for all, sir,”", "\"단번에 말해 두겠어요.\""], ["replied the baroness sharply, “I tell you I will not hear cash named; it is a style of language I never heard in the house of my parents or in that of my first husband.”", "남작 부인이 매섭게 대꾸했다. \"나는 돈 이야기를 입에 올리는 걸 듣지 않겠어요. 부모님 집에서도, 첫 남편의 집에서도 들어 본 적 없는 말투니까요.\""]], [["“Oh, I can well believe that, for neither of them was worth a penny.”", "\"오, 그야 그럴 만하지. 두 집 다 한 푼어치 값도 없었으니까.\""]], [["“The better reason for my not being conversant with the slang of the bank, which is here dinning in my ears from morning to night; that noise of jingling crowns, which are constantly being counted and re-counted, is odious to me.", "\"그렇다면 더더욱 내가 은행 은어 따위에 익숙할 까닭이 없지요. 아침부터 밤까지 여기서 내 귀를 울려 대는 그 은어 말이에요. 끊임없이 세고 또 세는 그 동전 짤랑거리는 소리가 나는 지긋지긋해요."], ["I only know one thing I dislike more, which is the sound of your voice.”", "그보다 더 싫은 게 딱 하나 있는데, 바로 당신 목소리지요.\""]], [["“Really?”", "\"정말이오?\""], ["said Danglars.", "당글라르가 말했다."], ["“Well, this surprises me, for I thought you took the liveliest interest in all my affairs!” 30243m", "\"허, 그것 참 놀랍군. 나는 당신이 내 사업 전부에 더없이 활기찬 관심을 두는 줄 알았는데 말이오!\""]], [["“I?", "\"내가요?"], ["What could put such an idea into your head?” “Yourself.”", "누가 당신 머릿속에 그런 생각을 집어넣던가요?\""]], [["“Ah?—what next?”", "\"당신 자신이오.\""]], [["“Most assuredly.”", "\"아? 그래서 또?\""]], [["“I should like to know upon what occasion?”", "\"틀림없는 사실이오.\" \"어떤 경우였는지 알고 싶군요.\""]], [["“Oh, _mon Dieu!_ that is very easily done.", "\"오, 맙소사! 그야 아주 간단하지."], ["Last February you were the first who told me of the Haitian funds.", "지난 2월에 나한테 아이티 공채 이야기를 처음 꺼낸 게 바로 당신이었소."], ["You had dreamed that a ship had entered the harbor at Le Havre, that this ship brought news that a payment we had looked upon as lost was going to be made.", "당신은 르아브르 항구에 배 한 척이 들어오는 꿈을 꿨고, 그 배가 우리가 떼인 줄로 여겼던 대금이 곧 지불될 거라는 소식을 가져왔다고 했지."], ["I know how clear-sighted your dreams are; I therefore purchased immediately as many shares as I could of the Haitian debt, and I gained 400,000 francs by it, of which 100,000 have been honestly paid to you.", "당신 꿈이 얼마나 들어맞는지 내가 잘 아니까, 곧장 아이티 공채를 살 수 있는 만큼 사들였고, 그걸로 40만 프랑을 벌었소. 그중 10만 프랑은 정직하게 당신한테 건넸지."], ["You spent it as you pleased; that was your business.", "당신은 그걸 마음대로 썼고, 그건 당신 일이오."], ["In March there was a question about a grant to a railway.", "3월에는 어느 철도 부설권 문제가 있었지."], ["Three companies presented themselves, each offering equal securities.", "세 회사가 나섰는데, 저마다 똑같은 담보를 내걸었소."], ["You told me that your instinct,—and although you pretend to know nothing about speculations, I think on the contrary, that your comprehension is very clear upon certain affairs,—well, you told me that your instinct led you to believe the grant would be given to the company called the Southern.", "당신은 자기 직감이—당신은 투기에 대해 아무것도 모르는 척하지만, 나는 오히려 어떤 일들에 대해선 당신 머리가 아주 명석하다고 보오—그래, 당신은 자기 직감으로 보아 그 부설권이 남부라는 회사에 떨어질 것 같다고 했지."], ["I bought two thirds of the shares of that company; as you had foreseen, the shares trebled in value, and I picked up a million, from which 250,000 francs were paid to you for pin-money.", "나는 그 회사 주식의 삼분의 이를 사들였고, 당신이 내다본 대로 주가가 세 배로 뛰어 나는 백만 프랑을 거머쥐었소. 그중 25만 프랑을 당신 용돈으로 건넸지."], ["How have you spent this 250,000 francs?—it is no business of mine.”", "그 25만 프랑을 어떻게 썼는지는—내 알 바 아니오.\""]], [["“When are you coming to the point?”", "\"대체 언제 본론으로 들어올 거죠?\""], ["cried the baroness, shivering with anger and impatience.", "분노와 조바심에 떨며 남작 부인이 외쳤다."]], [["“Patience, madame, I am coming to it.”", "\"인내심을 가지시오, 부인. 이제 본론으로 가는 중이오.\""]], [["“That’s fortunate.”", "\"그거 다행이군요.\""]], [["“In April you went to dine at the minister’s.", "\"4월에 당신은 장관 댁 만찬에 갔지."], ["You heard a private conversation respecting Spanish affairs—on the expulsion of Don Carlos.", "거기서 스페인 정세에 관한, 돈 카를로스의 추방에 관한 은밀한 대화를 들었소."], ["I bought some Spanish shares.", "나는 스페인 주식을 좀 사들였지."], ["The expulsion took place and I pocketed 600,000 francs the day Charles V. repassed the Bidassoa.", "추방이 일어났고, 카를 5세가 비다소아강을 다시 건너던 날 나는 60만 프랑을 챙겼소."], ["Of these 600,000 francs you took 50,000 crowns.", "그 60만 프랑 중에서 당신은 5만 에퀴를 가져갔지."], ["They were yours, you disposed of them according to your fancy, and I asked no questions; but it is not the less true that you have this year received 500,000 livres.”", "그건 당신 것이었고, 당신 좋을 대로 처분했으며, 나는 아무것도 묻지 않았소. 그래도 당신이 올해 50만 리브르를 받았다는 사실엔 변함이 없지.\""]], [["“Well, sir, and what then?”", "\"좋아요, 그래서요, 그게 어쨌다는 거죠?\""]], [["“Ah, yes, it was just after this that you spoiled everything.”", "\"아, 그렇지, 바로 그다음에 당신이 모든 걸 망쳐 놓았소.\""]], [["“Really, your manner of speaking——”", "\"정말이지, 당신 말투란——\""]], [["“It expresses my meaning, and that is all I want.", "\"내 뜻을 그대로 드러낼 뿐이오. 그거면 됐소."], ["Well, three days after that you talked politics with M. Debray, and you fancied from his words that Don Carlos had returned to Spain.", "자, 그 일이 있고 사흘 뒤에 당신은 드브레 씨와 정치 이야기를 나눴고, 그 사람 말을 듣고는 돈 카를로스가 스페인으로 돌아갔다고 넘겨짚었지."], ["Well, I sold my shares, the news got out, and I no longer sold—I gave them away, next day I find the news was false, and by this false report I have lost 700,000 francs.”", "그래서 나는 내 주식을 팔았소. 소문이 퍼졌고, 더는 팔 것도 없어 거저 줘 버리다시피 했지. 그런데 이튿날 그 소식이 거짓임이 드러났고, 그 거짓 보고 때문에 나는 70만 프랑을 잃었소.\""]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서요?\""]], [["“Well, since I gave you a fourth of my gains, I think you owe me a fourth of my losses; the fourth of 700,000 francs is 175,000 francs.”", "\"그래서 말인데, 내가 이익의 사분의 일을 당신한테 주었으니, 당신도 내 손실의 사분의 일을 갚아야 마땅하다고 보오. 70만 프랑의 사분의 일은 17만 5천 프랑이지.\""]], [["“What you say is absurd, and I cannot see why M. Debray’s name is mixed up in this affair.”", "\"말도 안 되는 소리예요. 게다가 이 일에 드브레 씨 이름이 왜 끼어드는지 모르겠군요.\""]], [["“Because if you do not possess the 175,000 francs I reclaim, you must have lent them to your friends, and M. Debray is one of your friends.”", "\"내가 돌려받겠다는 그 17만 5천 프랑이 당신한테 없다면, 당신 친구들한테 빌려준 게 분명하고, 드브레 씨가 바로 당신 친구 중 하나니까.\""]], [["30245m\n“For shame!”", "\"부끄러운 줄 아세요!\""], ["exclaimed the baroness.", "남작 부인이 외쳤다."]], [["“Oh, let us have no gestures, no screams, no modern drama, or you will oblige me to tell you that I see Debray leave here, pocketing the whole of the 500,000 livres you have handed over to him this year, while he smiles to himself, saying that he has found what the most skilful players have never discovered—that is, a roulette where he wins without playing, and is no loser when he loses.”", "\"오, 손짓도, 비명도, 요즘 유행하는 연극도 그만둡시다. 안 그러면 당신더러 듣게 만들 거요. 드브레가 올해 당신한테서 넘겨받은 50만 리브르를 죄다 주머니에 챙겨 여기를 나서면서, 가장 노련한 노름꾼도 끝내 찾아내지 못한 것을 자기는 찾아냈다고 혼자 빙긋 웃는 꼴을 내가 본다고 말이오. 노름을 하지 않고도 따는, 잃어도 손해 볼 게 없는 룰렛판 말이오.\""]], [["The baroness became enraged.", "남작 부인은 격분했다."]], [["“Wretch!”", "\"이 비열한 인간!\""], ["she cried, “will you dare to tell me you did not know what you now reproach me with?”", "그녀가 외쳤다. \"지금 나를 책망하는 그 일을 당신이 몰랐다고 감히 말할 셈인가요?\""]], [["“I do not say that I did know it, and I do not say that I did not know it.", "\"내가 알았다고도, 몰랐다고도 말하지 않겠소."], ["I merely tell you to look into my conduct during the last four years that we have ceased to be husband and wife, and see whether it has not always been consistent.", "다만 우리가 부부이기를 그만둔 지난 사 년 동안 내 행실을 들여다보고, 그게 한결같지 않았는지 살펴보라고 할 뿐이오."], ["Some time after our rupture, you wished to study music, under the celebrated baritone who made such a successful appearance at the Théâtre Italien; at the same time I felt inclined to learn dancing of the _danseuse_ who acquired such a reputation in London.", "우리가 갈라선 얼마 뒤, 당신은 이탈리아 극장에서 그토록 성공적으로 등장한 그 유명한 바리톤에게 음악을 배우고 싶어 했지. 그 무렵 나는 런던에서 그토록 명성을 얻은 그 무희에게 춤을 배우고 싶은 마음이 들었고."], ["This cost me, on your account and mine, 100,000 francs.", "그게 당신 몫 내 몫 합쳐 10만 프랑이 들었소."], ["I said nothing, for we must have peace in the house; and 100,000 francs for a lady and gentleman to be properly instructed in music and dancing are not too much.", "나는 아무 말도 하지 않았지. 집안의 평화는 지켜야 하니까. 그리고 신사 숙녀가 음악과 춤을 제대로 배우는 데 10만 프랑이면 과하지도 않고."], ["Well, you soon become tired of singing, and you take a fancy to study diplomacy with the minister’s secretary.", "그런데 당신은 이내 노래에 싫증을 내더니, 이번엔 장관 비서한테 외교술을 배울 마음이 동했지."], ["You understand, it signifies nothing to me so long as you pay for your lessons out of your own cash box.", "알겠소만, 당신이 제 금고에서 수업료를 치르는 한 나야 아무래도 상관없소."], ["But today I find you are drawing on mine, and that your apprenticeship may cost me 700,000 francs per month.", "그런데 오늘 보니 당신은 내 금고를 끌어다 쓰고 있고, 그 수업 비용이 한 달에 70만 프랑씩 들 판이오. 거기서 멈추시오, 부인."], ["Stop there, madame, for this cannot last.", "이건 더 이상 계속될 수 없소."], ["Either the diplomatist must give his lessons gratis, and I will tolerate him, or he must never set his foot again in my house;—do you understand, madame?”", "그 외교가가 수업을 거저 해 주든가—그렇다면 나도 눈감아 주겠소—아니면 다시는 우리 집에 발을 들이지 말든가 해야 하오. 알아듣겠소, 부인?\""]], [["“Oh, this is too much,”", "\"오, 이건 너무하군요.\""], ["cried Hermine, choking, “you are worse than despicable.”", "에르민이 숨이 막혀 외쳤다. \"당신은 비열함을 넘어선 사람이에요.\""]], [["“But,”", "\"그런데,\""], ["continued Danglars, “I find you did not even pause there——”", "당글라르가 말을 이었다. \"보아하니 당신은 거기서 멈추지도 않았더군——\""]], [["“Insults!”", "\"모욕이야!\""]], [["“You are right; let us leave these facts alone, and reason coolly.", "\"옳은 말이오. 그런 일들은 그냥 두고, 냉정하게 따져 봅시다."], ["I have never interfered in your affairs excepting for your good; treat me in the same way.", "나는 당신을 위해서가 아니라면 당신 일에 한 번도 끼어든 적이 없소. 당신도 나를 그렇게 대해 주시오."], ["You say you have nothing to do with my cash box.", "당신은 내 금고와 아무 상관 없다고 했지. 좋소."], ["Be it so. Do as you like with your own, but do not fill or empty mine.", "당신 것은 마음대로 하되, 내 것은 채우지도 비우지도 마시오."], ["Besides, how do I know that this was not a political trick, that the minister enraged at seeing me in the opposition, and jealous of the popular sympathy I excite, has not concerted with M. Debray to ruin me?”", "게다가 이게 정치적 술책이 아니라고 내가 어찌 장담하겠소? 내가 야당에 선 꼴을 보고 격분한, 또 내가 불러일으키는 대중의 호감을 시샘하는 장관이 드브레 씨와 짜고 나를 파멸시키려 한 게 아니라고 말이오?\""]], [["“A probable thing!”", "\"그럴듯도 하군요!\""]], [["“Why not?", "\"아니라는 법이 있소?"], ["Who ever heard of such an occurrence as this?—a false telegraphic despatch—it is almost impossible for wrong signals to be made as they were in the last two telegrams.", "이런 일이 일어났다는 말을 들어 본 적이나 있소? 거짓 전신 급보라니. 지난 두 전신처럼 잘못된 신호가 보내지는 건 거의 불가능한 일이오."], ["It was done on purpose for me—I am sure of it.”", "일부러 나를 노리고 한 짓이오—나는 확신하오.\""]], [["“Sir,”", "\"여보.\""], ["said the baroness humbly, “are you not aware that the man employed there was dismissed, that they talked of going to law with him, that orders were issued to arrest him and that this order would have been put into execution if he had not escaped by flight, which proves that he was either mad or guilty?", "남작 부인이 풀죽은 목소리로 말했다. \"그곳에 고용된 자가 해고되었고, 그자를 법정에 세우자는 말까지 나왔으며, 체포하라는 명령이 내려졌고, 그가 달아나 도망치지 않았더라면 그 명령이 집행되었으리라는 걸 모르세요? 그건 그자가 미쳤거나 죄가 있다는 증거예요."], ["It was a mistake.”", "그저 실수였다고요.\""]], [["“Yes, which made fools laugh, which caused the minister to have a sleepless night, which has caused the minister’s secretaries to blacken several sheets of paper, but which has cost me 700,000 francs.”", "\"그렇지, 그 실수가 어리석은 자들을 웃게 만들었고, 장관에게 하룻밤 잠을 설치게 했고, 장관 비서들에게 종이 몇 장을 검게 채우게 했지. 하지만 내게는 70만 프랑을 치르게 한 실수요.\""]], [["“But, sir,”", "\"하지만 여보.\""], ["said Hermine suddenly, “if all this is, as you say, caused by M. Debray, why, instead of going direct to him, do you come and tell me of it?", "에르민이 불쑥 말했다. \"이 모든 게, 당신 말대로 드브레 씨 탓이라면, 어째서 그 사람한테 곧장 가지 않고 나한테 와서 이런 이야기를 하는 거죠?"], ["Why, to accuse the man, do you address the woman?”", "어째서 남자를 탓하면서 여자한테 따지는 거예요?\""]], [["“Do I know M.", "\"내가 드브레 씨를 알기나 하오?"], ["Debray?—do I wish to know him?—do I wish to know that he gives advice?—do I wish to follow it?—do I speculate?", "내가 그를 알고 싶기나 하오? 그가 충고를 한다는 걸 내가 알고 싶기나 하오? 내가 그 충고를 따르고 싶기나 하오?"], ["No; you do all this, not I.”", "내가 투기를 하오? 아니지. 그 모든 걸 하는 건 나 아닌 당신이오.\""]], [["“Still it seems to me, that as you profit by it——”", "\"그래도 당신이 그 덕을 보고 있으니——\""]], [["Danglars shrugged his shoulders. “Foolish creature,”", "당글라르가 어깨를 으쓱했다. \"어리석은 사람 같으니.\""], ["he exclaimed.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["“Women fancy they have talent because they have managed two or three intrigues without being the talk of Paris!", "\"여자들은 파리의 입방아에 오르지 않고 두세 건의 정사를 꾸며 냈다고 자기한테 재주가 있는 줄 안다니까!"], ["But know that if you had even hidden your irregularities from your husband, who has but the commencement of the art—for generally husbands _will_ not see—you would then have been but a faint imitation of most of your friends among the women of the world.", "하지만 알아 두시오. 설령 당신이 그 외도를—그 기술의 초보 단계에나 와 있는—남편한테 감췄다 한들, 워낙 남편이란 대개 보려 들지 않으니, 당신은 사교계 여자 친구들 대부분의 어설픈 흉내쟁이에 지나지 않았을 거요."], ["But it has not been so with me,—I see, and always have seen, during the last sixteen years.", "그런데 나는 그렇지 않았소. 나는 지난 십육 년 동안 보아 왔고, 늘 보고 있었소."], ["You may, perhaps, have hidden a thought; but not a step, not an action, not a fault, has escaped me, while you flattered yourself upon your address, and firmly believed you had deceived me.", "당신은 어쩌면 생각 하나쯤 숨겼을지 모르지만, 걸음 하나, 행동 하나, 잘못 하나 내 눈을 벗어나지 못했소. 그러는 동안 당신은 제 수완에 우쭐대며, 나를 감쪽같이 속였다고 굳게 믿었지."], ["What has been the result?—that, thanks to my pretended ignorance, there is none of your friends, from M. de Villefort to M.", "그 결과가 무엇이오? 내가 모르는 척해 준 덕분에, 빌포르 씨에서 드브레 씨에 이르기까지 당신 친구 중에 내 앞에서 떨지 않은 자가 하나도 없소."], ["Debray, who has not trembled before me. There is not one who has not treated me as the master of the house,—the only title I desire with respect to you; there is not one, in fact, who would have dared to speak of me as I have spoken of them this day.", "나를 이 집 주인으로 대하지 않은 자가 하나도 없지—당신에 관한 한 내가 바라는 칭호는 그것뿐이오. 사실, 오늘 내가 그들을 두고 말한 대로 감히 나를 두고 말할 수 있었을 자도 하나 없소."], ["I will allow you to make me hateful, but I will prevent your rendering me ridiculous, and, above all, I forbid you to ruin me.”", "나를 미워하게 만드는 건 허락하겠소만, 나를 우스꽝스럽게 만드는 건 막겠소. 무엇보다도, 나를 파멸시키는 건 금하오.\""]], [["The baroness had been tolerably composed until the name of Villefort had been pronounced; but then she became pale, and, rising, as if touched by a spring, she stretched out her hands as though conjuring an apparition; she then took two or three steps towards her husband, as though to tear the secret from him, of which he was ignorant, or which he withheld from some odious calculation,—odious, as all his calculations were.", "남작 부인은 빌포르라는 이름이 입에 오르기 전까지는 그런대로 평정을 지키고 있었다. 그러나 그 순간 그녀는 새파랗게 질려, 용수철에 튕긴 듯 벌떡 일어나, 마치 망령을 물리치려는 듯 두 손을 뻗었다. 그러고는 남편이 모르고 있거나, 아니면 어떤 흉악한 속셈에서—그의 속셈이란 다 그렇듯 흉악한—감추고 있는 그 비밀을 캐내려는 듯 그를 향해 두세 걸음 다가갔다."]], [["“M. de Villefort!—What do you mean?”", "\"빌포르 씨라니!—무슨 뜻이죠?\""]], [["“I mean that M. de Nargonne, your first husband, being neither a philosopher nor a banker, or perhaps being both, and seeing there was nothing to be got out of a king’s attorney, died of grief or anger at finding, after an absence of nine months, that you had been _enceinte_ six.", "\"당신 첫 남편 나르곤 씨가 철학자도 은행가도 아니었거나, 어쩌면 둘 다였거나 간에, 왕실 검사한테서는 얻어 낼 게 없다는 걸 알고, 아홉 달을 비웠다 돌아와 보니 당신이 여섯 달째 임신해 있는 걸 알고는 비탄인지 분노인지로 죽어 버렸다는 뜻이오."], ["I am brutal,—I not only allow it, but boast of it; it is one of the reasons of my success in commercial business.", "나는 잔인하오—그걸 인정할 뿐 아니라 자랑으로 여기지. 그게 내가 상업에서 성공한 까닭 중 하나요."], ["Why did he kill himself instead of you?", "그 사람은 어째서 당신 대신 제 목숨을 끊었겠소?"], ["Because he had no cash to save.", "구해 낼 돈이 없었으니까."], ["My life belongs to my cash. M.", "내 목숨은 내 돈에 달려 있소."], ["Debray has made me lose 700,000 francs; let him bear his share of the loss, and we will go on as before; if not, let him become bankrupt for the 250,000 livres, and do as all bankrupts do—disappear.", "드브레 씨가 내게 70만 프랑을 잃게 했으니, 제 몫의 손실을 짊어지게 하시오. 그러면 우리는 전처럼 지내겠소. 그게 싫다면, 그 25만 리브르 때문에 파산하든가, 모든 파산자들이 하는 대로 하든가—자취를 감추든가 하라지."], ["He is a charming fellow, I allow, when his news is correct; but when it is not, there are fifty others in the world who would do better than he.”", "인정하건대, 소식이 정확할 때야 그자도 매력적인 친구지. 하지만 그렇지 못할 때, 그보다 더 잘해 낼 자가 세상에 쉰 명은 있소.\""]], [["Madame Danglars was rooted to the spot; she made a violent effort to reply to this last attack, but she fell upon a chair thinking of Villefort, of the dinner scene, of the strange series of misfortunes which had taken place in her house during the last few days, and changed the usual calm of her establishment to a scene of scandalous debate.", "당글라르 부인은 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 굳어 버렸다. 이 마지막 공격에 대꾸하려고 안간힘을 썼으나, 그녀는 의자에 주저앉아 빌포르를, 그 만찬 자리의 광경을, 지난 며칠 사이 자기 집에서 벌어져 이 집의 평소 평온을 추문 가득한 언쟁의 무대로 바꿔 놓은 그 기이한 불행의 연속을 떠올렸다."]], [["Danglars did not even look at her, though she did her best to faint.", "당글라르는 그녀가 기절하려고 갖은 애를 쓰는데도 거들떠보지도 않았다."], ["He shut the bedroom door after him, without adding another word, and returned to his apartments; and when Madame Danglars recovered from her half-fainting condition, she could almost believe that she had had a disagreeable dream.", "그는 더는 한마디도 보태지 않고 등 뒤로 침실 문을 닫고 자기 방으로 돌아갔다. 그리고 당글라르 부인이 반쯤 기절했던 상태에서 깨어났을 때, 그녀는 자신이 불쾌한 꿈을 꾸었다고 거의 믿을 뻔했다."]], [["# Chapter 66. Matrimonial Projects", "# Chapter 66. Matrimonial Projects"]], [["The day following this scene, at the hour Debray usually chose to pay a visit to Madame Danglars on his way to his office, his _coupé_ did not appear.", "이 일이 있은 다음 날, 평소 드브레가 출근길에 당글라르 부인을 찾아오던 시각이 되었는데도 그의 마차는 나타나지 않았다."], ["At this time, that is, about half-past twelve, Madame Danglars ordered her carriage, and went out.", "이 무렵, 그러니까 열두 시 반쯤 당글라르 부인은 마차를 대령하라 이르고는 외출했다."], ["Danglars, hidden behind a curtain, watched the departure he had been waiting for.", "당글라르는 커튼 뒤에 몸을 숨긴 채, 자신이 기다려 온 부인의 외출을 지켜보았다."], ["He gave orders that he should be informed as soon as Madame Danglars appeared; but at two o’clock she had not returned.", "그는 부인이 돌아오면 곧바로 알리라고 일러 두었다. 그러나 두 시가 되어도 부인은 돌아오지 않았다."], ["He then called for his horses, drove to the Chamber, and inscribed his name to speak against the budget.", "그러자 그는 말을 대령하라 하여 의회로 가, 예산안에 반대하는 발언자 명단에 자기 이름을 올렸다."], ["From twelve to two o’clock Danglars had remained in his study, unsealing his dispatches, and becoming more and more sad every minute, heaping figure upon figure, and receiving, among other visits, one from Major Cavalcanti, who, as stiff and exact as ever, presented himself precisely at the hour named the night before, to terminate his business with the banker.", "열두 시부터 두 시까지 당글라르는 서재에 틀어박혀 들어온 전보들의 봉인을 뜯으며 시시각각 우울해져 갔다. 숫자 위에 숫자를 쌓아 올리던 그는 여러 방문객 가운데 카발칸티 소령의 방문도 받았다. 소령은 여느 때처럼 뻣뻣하고 빈틈없는 태도로, 전날 밤에 정해 둔 시각에 정확히 맞춰 와서 은행가와의 거래를 마무리 지었다."]], [["On leaving the Chamber, Danglars, who had shown violent marks of agitation during the sitting, and been more bitter than ever against the ministry, re-entered his carriage, and told the coachman to drive to the Avenue des Champs-Élysées, No. 30.", "회의가 진행되는 동안 격한 동요의 기색을 드러냈고 그 어느 때보다 신랄하게 내각을 공격했던 당글라르는, 의회를 나서며 다시 마차에 올라 마부에게 샹젤리제 가 30번지로 가라고 일렀다."]], [["Monte Cristo was at home; only he was engaged with someone and begged Danglars to wait for a moment in the drawing-room.", "몬테크리스토는 집에 있었다. 다만 누군가와 면담 중이라, 잠시 응접실에서 기다려 달라고 당글라르에게 청했다."], ["While the banker was waiting in the anteroom, the door opened, and a man dressed as an abbé and doubtless more familiar with the house than he was, came in and instead of waiting, merely bowed, passed on to the farther apartments, and disappeared.", "은행가가 대기실에서 기다리는 동안 문이 열리더니, 사제 옷차림을 한 남자가 들어왔다. 그는 분명 당글라르보다 그 집을 잘 아는 듯, 기다리지도 않고 그저 가볍게 고개만 숙이고는 안쪽 방으로 지나가 사라졌다."]], [["A minute after the door by which the priest had entered reopened, and Monte Cristo appeared.", "사제가 들어갔던 그 문이 잠시 후 다시 열리며 몬테크리스토가 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["“Pardon me,”", "\"용서하시오.\" 그가 말했다. \"친애하는 남작, 내 친구 부소니 신부가 방금 파리에 도착했소."], ["said he, “my dear baron, but one of my friends, the Abbé Busoni, whom you perhaps saw pass by, has just arrived in Paris; not having seen him for a long time, I could not make up my mind to leave him sooner, so I hope this will be sufficient reason for my having made you wait.”", "아마 지나가는 걸 보셨을 거요. 오랫동안 보지 못한 터라, 그렇게 일찍 그를 두고 나올 마음이 들지 않더군요. 기다리시게 한 사정을 이만하면 양해해 주시리라 믿소.\""]], [["“Nay,”", "\"천만에요.\""], ["said Danglars, “it is my fault; I have chosen my visit at a wrong time, and will retire.”", "당글라르가 말했다. \"제 잘못입니다. 찾아온 때가 좋지 않았으니, 물러가겠습니다.\""]], [["“Not at all; on the contrary, be seated; but what is the matter with you?", "\"전혀 그렇지 않소. 오히려 앉으시오. 그런데 무슨 일이라도 있소?"], ["You look careworn; really, you alarm me.", "근심에 잠긴 얼굴이구려."], ["Melancholy in a capitalist, like the appearance of a comet, presages some misfortune to the world.”", "정말이지 걱정이 되는군요. 자본가의 우울이란, 혜성의 출현처럼 세상에 닥칠 불행을 예고하는 법이거든.\""]], [["“I have been in ill-luck for several days,”", "\"며칠째 운이 좋지 않습니다.\""], ["said Danglars, “and I have heard nothing but bad news.”", "당글라르가 말했다. \"나쁜 소식밖에 듣지 못했지요.\""]], [["“Ah, indeed?”", "\"아, 그렇소?\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["“Have you had another fall at the Bourse?”", "\"증권 거래소에서 또 한 번 손실을 보셨소?\""]], [["“No; I am safe for a few days at least.", "\"아닙니다. 적어도 며칠은 안전합니다."], ["I am only annoyed about a bankrupt of Trieste.”", "트리에스테에서 파산한 자 때문에 골치가 아플 뿐이지요.\""]], [["“Really? Does it happen to be Jacopo Manfredi?”", "\"정말이오? 혹시 그게 야코포 만프레디요?\""]], [["“Exactly so.", "\"바로 그렇습니다."], ["Imagine a man who has transacted business with me for I don’t know how long, to the amount of 800,000 or 900,000 francs during the year.", "한번 생각해 보십시오. 그자는 얼마나 오래되었는지도 모를 만큼 오랫동안 저와 거래해 왔고, 한 해에 80만, 90만 프랑에 이르는 거래를 했습니다."], ["Never a mistake or delay—a fellow who paid like a prince.", "단 한 번도 실수나 지체가 없었지요. 왕족처럼 척척 지불하던 자였습니다."], ["Well, I was a million in advance with him, and now my fine Jacopo Manfredi suspends payment!”", "그런데 제가 그자에게 백만 프랑을 미리 내준 참에, 이 훌륭하신 야코포 만프레디가 지불을 정지해 버린 겁니다!\""]], [["“Really?”", "\"정말이오?\""]], [["“It is an unheard-of fatality.", "\"전례 없는 불운입니다."], ["I draw upon him for 600,000 francs, my bills are returned unpaid, and, more than that, I hold bills of exchange signed by him to the value of 400,000 francs, payable at his correspondent’s in Paris at the end of this month.", "제가 그자 앞으로 60만 프랑짜리 어음을 발행했더니 지불 거절로 되돌아왔습니다. 게다가 그것뿐만이 아니라, 이달 말 파리의 거래처에서 지불하기로 그자가 서명한 40만 프랑짜리 환어음까지 제가 들고 있지요."], ["Today is the 30th.", "오늘이 30일입니다."], ["I present them; but my correspondent has disappeared.", "그걸 제시하러 갔더니, 거래처가 사라져 버렸지 뭡니까."], ["This, with my Spanish affairs, made a pretty end to the month.”", "이게 스페인 일까지 겹쳐서, 이번 달 끝이 참으로 볼만하게 되었습니다.\""]], [["“Then you really lost by that affair in Spain?”", "\"그럼 스페인 그 일로 정말 손해를 보신 게요?\""]], [["“Yes; only 700,000 francs out of my cash box—nothing more!”", "\"네. 제 금고에서 고작 70만 프랑이 나갔을 뿐이지요. 그것뿐입니다!\""]], [["“Why, how could you make such a mistake—such an old stager?”", "\"아니, 어쩌다 그런 실수를 하셨소? 노련한 분께서?\""]], [["“Oh, it is all my wife’s fault.", "\"오, 다 제 아내 탓입니다."], ["She dreamed Don Carlos had returned to Spain; she believes in dreams.", "돈 카를로스가 스페인으로 돌아오는 꿈을 꾸었다지 뭡니까."], ["It is magnetism, she says, and when she dreams a thing it is sure to happen, she assures me.", "아내는 꿈을 믿지요. 그게 자력 작용이라나요, 자기가 꿈에 본 것은 반드시 일어난다고 우기더군요."], ["On this conviction I allow her to speculate, she having her bank and her stockbroker; she speculated and lost.", "그 확신을 믿고 저는 아내가 투기를 하도록 내버려 둡니다. 아내에게는 따로 거래 은행과 증권 중개인이 있거든요."], ["It is true she speculates with her own money, not mine; nevertheless, you can understand that when 700,000 francs leave the wife’s pocket, the husband always finds it out.", "아내는 투기를 했고, 잃었습니다. 물론 제 돈이 아니라 자기 돈으로 한 일이긴 합니다. 그렇지만 아시다시피 아내 주머니에서 70만 프랑이 빠져나가면, 남편이 모를 리가 없지요."], ["But do you mean to say you have not heard of this?", "그런데 정말 이 일을 못 들으셨다는 겁니까?"], ["Why, the thing has made a tremendous noise.”", "어찌나 큰 소동이 났는데요.\""]], [["“Yes, I heard it spoken of, but I did not know the details, and then no one can be more ignorant than I am of the affairs in the Bourse.”", "\"그래요, 이야기는 들었소만 자세한 사정은 몰랐소. 게다가 거래소 일에 관해서라면 나만큼 무지한 사람도 없을 거요.\""]], [["30251m\n“Then you do not speculate?”", "\"그럼 백작께서는 투기를 안 하십니까?\""]], [["“I?—How could I speculate when I already have so much trouble in regulating my income?", "\"내가요? 가진 수입을 관리하는 것만도 골치가 아픈데 어떻게 투기를 하겠소?"], ["I should be obliged, besides my steward, to keep a clerk and a boy.", "그랬다가는 집사 말고도 서기 한 명에 사환 하나를 더 두어야 할 거요."], ["But touching these Spanish affairs, I think that the baroness did not dream the whole of the Don Carlos matter.", "그런데 이 스페인 일을 두고 하는 말인데, 내 생각에는 남작 부인이 돈 카를로스 일을 통째로 꿈에서 본 건 아닐 거요."], ["The papers said something about it, did they not?”", "신문에 그 비슷한 이야기가 실렸지 않소?\""]], [["“Then you believe the papers?”", "\"그럼 백작께서는 신문을 믿으십니까?\""]], [["“I?—not the least in the world; only I fancied that the honest _Messager_ was an exception to the rule, and that it only announced telegraphic despatches.”", "\"내가요? 천만에요. 다만 그 정직한 메사제만큼은 예외라서, 전신으로 들어온 급보만 싣는 줄 알았소.\""]], [["“Well, that’s what puzzles me,”", "\"바로 그게 제가 알 수 없는 대목입니다.\""], ["replied Danglars; “the news of the return of Don Carlos was brought by telegraph.”", "당글라르가 대답했다. \"돈 카를로스가 돌아왔다는 소식은 전신으로 들어왔거든요.\""]], [["“So that,”", "\"그러니까,\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “you have lost nearly 1,700,000 francs this month.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"이달에 거의 백칠십만 프랑을 잃으셨다는 말이군요.\""]], [["“Not nearly, indeed; that is exactly my loss.”", "\"거의가 아니라, 딱 그만큼이 제 손실입니다.\""]], [["“_Diable!_”", "\"저런!\""], ["said Monte Cristo compassionately, “it is a hard blow for a third-rate fortune.”", "몬테크리스토가 동정 어린 어조로 말했다. \"삼류 재산에는 호된 타격이겠구려.\""]], [["“Third-rate,”", "\"삼류라니요.\""], ["said Danglars, rather humble, “what do you mean by that?”", "당글라르가 다소 풀이 죽어 말했다. \"그게 무슨 뜻입니까?\""]], [["“Certainly,”", "\"이렇게 보시오.\" 몬테크리스토가 말을 이었다."], ["continued Monte Cristo, “I make three assortments in fortune—first-rate, second-rate, and third-rate fortunes.", "\"나는 재산을 셋으로 나누지요. 일류, 이류, 삼류 재산으로 말이오. 일류라 함은, 광산이나 토지, 프랑스나 오스트리아, 영국 같은 나라의 공채처럼 손안에 쥐고 있는 보물로 이루어진 재산을 말하오."], ["I call those first-rate which are composed of treasures one possesses under one’s hand, such as mines, lands, and funded property, in such states as France, Austria, and England, provided these treasures and property form a total of about a hundred millions; I call those second-rate fortunes, that are gained by manufacturing enterprises, joint-stock companies, viceroyalties, and principalities, not drawing more than 1,500,000 francs, the whole forming a capital of about fifty millions; finally, I call those third-rate fortunes, which are composed of a fluctuating capital, dependent upon the will of others, or upon chances which a bankruptcy involves or a false telegram shakes, such as banks, speculations of the day—in fact, all operations under the influence of greater or less mischances, the whole bringing in a real or fictitious capital of about fifteen millions.", "단, 이 보물과 자산이 합쳐 약 일억 프랑쯤은 되어야 하지요. 이류 재산이라 함은, 제조업이나 주식회사, 부왕령이나 공국에서 얻는 것으로, 그 수입이 백오십만 프랑을 넘지 않으며 전체 자본이 오천만 프랑쯤 되는 재산을 말하오. 끝으로 삼류 재산이라 함은, 남의 의지나 우연에 좌우되는 변동 자본으로 이루어진 것이오."], ["I think this is about your position, is it not?”", "파산이 휘말려 들거나 거짓 전보 하나에도 흔들리는, 이를테면 은행이나 그날그날의 투기 같은 것 말이오. 요컨대 크고 작은 불운에 좌우되는 온갖 거래로, 그 전체가 실재든 허구든 약 천오백만 프랑의 자본을 가져다주는 재산이지요. 내 생각에는 이게 대략 남작의 처지 같소만, 아니오?\""]], [["“Confound it, yes!”", "\"제기랄, 맞습니다!\""], ["replied Danglars.", "당글라르가 대답했다."]], [["“The result, then, of six more such months as this would be to reduce the third-rate house to despair.”", "\"그렇다면 이런 달이 여섯 번만 더 이어진다면, 삼류 가문은 절망에 빠지고 말겠구려.\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오,\""], ["said Danglars, becoming very pale, how you are running on!”", "당글라르가 새파랗게 질려 말했다. \"어찌 그리 말씀을 함부로 하십니까!\""]], [["“Let us imagine seven such months,”", "\"이런 달이 일곱 번 이어진다고 쳐 봅시다.\" 몬테크리스토가 같은 어조로 말을 이었다."], ["continued Monte Cristo, in the same tone.", "\"말해 보시오, 백칠십만 프랑의 일곱 배가 거의 천이백만 프랑이라는 걸 생각해 본 적 있소? 없겠지요. 음, 그게 옳소. 그런 생각에 빠졌다가는 결코 원금을 걸지 못할 테니까."], ["“Tell me, have you ever thought that seven times 1,700,000 francs make nearly twelve millions?", "투기꾼에게 원금이란, 문명인에게 살갗과도 같은 것이오. 우리에게는 옷이 있고, 그중에는 더 화려한 것도 있지요. 그게 바로 신용이오."], ["No, you have not;—well, you are right, for if you indulged in such reflections, you would never risk your principal, which is to the speculator what the skin is to civilized man.", "하지만 사람이 죽으면 남는 건 살갗뿐이오. 마찬가지로, 사업에서 손을 떼면 남는 건 실제 원금뿐인데, 그래 봐야 기껏 오백만이나 육백만 프랑이오. 삼류 재산이란 겉보기의 사분의 일을 넘는 법이 없으니까."], ["We have our clothes, some more splendid than others,—this is our credit; but when a man dies he has only his skin; in the same way, on retiring from business, you have nothing but your real principal of about five or six millions, at the most; for third-rate fortunes are never more than a fourth of what they appear to be, like the locomotive on a railway, the size of which is magnified by the smoke and steam surrounding it.", "철로 위의 기관차가 둘러싼 연기와 증기 때문에 실제보다 커 보이는 것과 같지요. 자, 남작의 실제 자본을 이루는 오백만이나 육백만 프랑 가운데, 방금 거의 이백만 프랑을 잃으셨소. 그만큼 당연히 남작의 신용과 허구의 재산도 줄어들 수밖에 없지요."], ["Well, out of the five or six millions which form your real capital, you have just lost nearly two millions, which must, of course, in the same degree diminish your credit and fictitious fortune; to follow out my simile, your skin has been opened by bleeding, and this if repeated three or four times will cause death—so pay attention to it, my dear Monsieur Danglars.", "내 비유를 끝까지 따라가 보면, 남작의 살갗은 출혈로 갈라진 셈이오. 이게 서너 번 거듭되면 죽음에 이르고 말지요. 그러니 부디 조심하시오, 친애하는 당글라르 씨."], ["Do you want money?", "돈이 필요하시오?"], ["Do you wish me to lend you some?” 30253m", "내가 좀 빌려 드릴까요?\""]], [["“What a bad calculator you are!” exclaimed Danglars, calling to his assistance all his philosophy and dissimulation.", "\"백작께서는 참 셈이 서투르시군요!\" 당글라르가 자신의 온갖 철학과 위장술을 끌어모아 외쳤다."], ["“I have made money at the same time by speculations which have succeeded.", "\"저는 같은 시기에 성공한 투기로 돈도 벌었습니다."], ["I have made up the loss of blood by nutrition.", "빠져나간 피를 영양으로 보충했지요."], ["I lost a battle in Spain, I have been defeated in Trieste, but my naval army in India will have taken some galleons, and my Mexican pioneers will have discovered some mine.”", "스페인에서 한 전투를 졌고 트리에스테에서 패했지만, 인도에 있는 제 함대가 갈레온선 몇 척쯤은 나포했을 테고, 멕시코의 제 광산 개척자들이 광맥을 발견했을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Very good, very good!", "\"좋소, 아주 좋소!"], ["But the wound remains and will reopen at the first loss.”", "하지만 상처는 그대로 남아, 다음 손실이 닥치는 순간 다시 벌어질 거요.\""]], [["“No, for I am only embarked in certainties,”", "\"아닙니다. 저는 확실한 것에만 발을 들이거든요.\""], ["replied Danglars, with the air of a mountebank sounding his own praises; “to involve me, three governments must crumble to dust.”", "당글라르가 제 자랑을 늘어놓는 약장수 같은 태도로 대답했다. \"저를 무너뜨리려면 세 나라 정부가 가루가 되도록 무너져야 할 겁니다.\""]], [["“Well, such things have been.”", "\"음, 그런 일도 있었던 법이오.\""]], [["“That there should be a famine!”", "\"기근이라도 들어야 한단 말이지요!\""]], [["“Recollect the seven fat and the seven lean kine.”", "\"살진 소 일곱 마리와 여윈 소 일곱 마리를 떠올려 보시오.\""]], [["“Or, that the sea should become dry, as in the days of Pharaoh, and even then my vessels would become caravans.”", "\"아니면 파라오 시절처럼 바다가 말라붙어야 하겠지요. 그래 봐야 그땐 제 배들이 대상(隊商)으로 바뀔 테지만요.\""]], [["“So much the better.", "\"그렇다면 더없이 좋은 일이오."], ["I congratulate you, my dear M. Danglars,”", "축하하오, 친애하는 당글라르 씨.\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “I see I was deceived, and that you belong to the class of second-rate fortunes.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"내가 잘못 봤구려. 남작은 이류 재산에 속하는 분이군요.\""]], [["“I think I may aspire to that honor,”", "\"저도 그 영광은 넘볼 만하지 않나 합니다.\""], ["said Danglars with a smile, which reminded Monte Cristo of the sickly moons which bad artists are so fond of daubing into their pictures of ruins.", "당글라르가 미소 지으며 말했다. 그 미소를 보고 몬테크리스토는, 서투른 화가들이 폐허 그림에 즐겨 처발라 넣는 병색 짙은 달을 떠올렸다."], ["“But, while we are speaking of business,”", "\"그런데 사업 이야기가 나온 김에,\""], ["Danglars added, pleased to find an opportunity of changing the subject, “tell me what I am to do for M. Cavalcanti.”", "당글라르가 화제를 돌릴 기회를 잡아 흐뭇해하며 덧붙였다. \"카발칸티 씨 건은 어떻게 하면 좋을지 일러 주시지요.\""]], [["“Give him money, if he is recommended to you, and the recommendation seems good.”", "\"그에게 돈을 주시오. 추천을 받았고, 그 추천이 믿을 만해 보인다면 말이오.\""]], [["“Excellent; he presented himself this morning with a bond of 40,000 francs, payable at sight, on you, signed by Busoni, and returned by you to me, with your endorsement—of course, I immediately counted him over the forty bank-notes.”", "\"더할 나위 없지요. 오늘 아침에 그가 일람불 채권 4만 프랑짜리를 들고 찾아왔습니다. 백작 앞으로 발행된 것이고, 부소니가 서명해서 백작께서 배서하여 제게 돌려보내신 것이었지요. 물론 저는 곧바로 지폐 마흔 장을 세어 주었습니다.\""]], [["Monte Cristo nodded his head in token of assent.", "몬테크리스토는 동의의 표시로 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["“But that is not all,”", "\"하지만 그것뿐만이 아닙니다.\""], ["continued Danglars; “he has opened an account with my house for his son.”", "당글라르가 말을 이었다. \"그가 자기 아들 명의로 제 은행에 계좌를 열었거든요.\""]], [["“May I ask how much he allows the young man?”", "\"그 젊은이에게 얼마나 내준다는지 여쭤도 되겠소?\""]], [["“Five thousand francs per month.”", "\"한 달에 오천 프랑입니다.\""]], [["“Sixty thousand francs per year.", "\"일 년에 육만 프랑이라."], ["I thought I was right in believing that Cavalcanti to be a stingy fellow. How can a young man live upon 5,000 francs a month?”", "카발칸티가 인색한 자라고 본 게 옳았던 모양이오. 젊은이가 한 달에 오천 프랑으로 어찌 살아간단 말이오?\""]], [["“But you understand that if the young man should want a few thousands more——”", "\"하지만 아시다시피, 그 젊은이가 몇천 프랑쯤 더 필요로 한다면……\""]], [["“Do not advance it; the father will never repay it.", "\"미리 내주지 마시오. 아비가 결코 갚지 않을 테니까."], ["You do not know these ultramontane millionaires; they are regular misers.", "남작은 알프스 너머 그 백만장자들을 모르시는군요. 하나같이 지독한 구두쇠들이오."], ["And by whom were they recommended to you?”", "그런데 그들을 누가 추천했소?\""]], [["“Oh, by the house of Fenzi, one of the best in Florence.”", "\"오, 펜치 상회입니다. 피렌체에서 손꼽히는 곳이지요.\""]], [["“I do not mean to say you will lose, but, nevertheless, mind you hold to the terms of the agreement.”", "\"손해 본다는 말은 아니오. 그래도 부디 계약 조건에서 벗어나지 않도록 단단히 지키시오.\""]], [["“Would you not trust the Cavalcanti?”", "\"카발칸티를 못 믿으신단 말씀입니까?\""]], [["“I? oh, I would advance ten millions on his signature.", "\"내가요? 오, 나라면 그자의 서명만 보고도 천만 프랑은 내줄 거요."], ["I was only speaking in reference to the second-rate fortunes we were mentioning just now.”", "그저 방금 우리가 이야기한 이류 재산을 두고 한 말일 뿐이오.\""]], [["“And with all this, how unassuming he is!", "\"그러면서도 어찌나 겸손한지요!"], ["I should never have taken him for anything more than a mere major.”", "저는 그를 한낱 소령 이상으로는 보지 않았을 겁니다.\""]], [["“And you would have flattered him, for certainly, as you say, he has no manner.", "\"그렇다면 그를 한껏 추켜세운 셈이오. 정말이지 남작 말대로, 그는 통 품위라고는 없으니까."], ["The first time I saw him he appeared to me like an old lieutenant who had grown mouldy under his epaulets.", "처음 봤을 때 나는 견장 밑에서 곰팡내가 날 만큼 늙어 버린 퇴역 중위 같다고 생각했소."], ["But all the Italians are the same; they are like old Jews when they are not glittering in Oriental splendor.”", "하지만 이탈리아인이란 다 그렇지요. 동방의 화려함으로 번쩍이지 않을 때면, 늙은 유대인 같다오.\""]], [["“The young man is better,”", "\"아들은 그보다 낫더군요.\""], ["said Danglars.", "당글라르가 말했다."]], [["“Yes; a little nervous, perhaps, but, upon the whole, he appeared tolerable.", "\"그렇소. 어쩌면 좀 신경질적이긴 해도, 대체로 봐줄 만했소."], ["I was uneasy about him.”", "그 청년 때문에 마음이 좀 불안했지요.\""]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜요?\""]], [["“Because you met him at my house, just after his introduction into the world, as they told me.", "\"남작이 우리 집에서 그를 만난 게, 사람들 말로는 막 사교계에 발을 들인 직후였으니 말이오."], ["He has been travelling with a very severe tutor, and had never been to Paris before.”", "그는 아주 엄한 가정교사와 함께 여행을 다녔고, 파리에는 한 번도 온 적이 없었다오.\""]], [["“Ah, I believe noblemen marry amongst themselves, do they not?”", "\"아, 귀족들은 자기들끼리 결혼하지 않습니까?\""], ["asked Danglars carelessly; “they like to unite their fortunes.”", "당글라르가 무심한 듯 물었다. \"재산을 합치고 싶어 하지요.\""]], [["“It is usual, certainly; but Cavalcanti is an original who does nothing like other people.", "\"물론 흔한 일이오. 하지만 카발칸티는 남들과는 다른 별난 사람이오."], ["I cannot help thinking that he has brought his son to France to choose a wife.”", "아무래도 며느릿감을 고르려고 아들을 프랑스로 데려온 것 같단 말이지요.\""]], [["“Do you think so?”", "\"그렇게 생각하십니까?\""]], [["“I am sure of it.”", "\"확신하오.\""]], [["“And you have heard his fortune mentioned?”", "\"그의 재산에 대해서는 무슨 말을 들으셨습니까?\""]], [["“Nothing else was talked of; only some said he was worth millions, and others that he did not possess a farthing.”", "\"오로지 그 이야기뿐이었소. 다만 누구는 그가 수백만 프랑을 가졌다 하고, 누구는 한 푼도 없다고 하더군요.\""]], [["“And what is your opinion?”", "\"백작의 생각은 어떠십니까?\""]], [["“I ought not to influence you, because it is only my own personal impression.”", "\"내 의견에 남작이 휘둘려서는 안 되오. 그저 순전히 내 개인적인 인상일 뿐이니까.\""]], [["“Well, and it is that——”", "\"그래도, 그게 어떻다는……\""]], [["“My opinion is, that all these old _podestàs_, these ancient _condottieri_,—for the Cavalcanti have commanded armies and governed provinces,—my opinion, I say, is, that they have buried their millions in corners, the secret of which they have transmitted only to their eldest sons, who have done the same from generation to generation; and the proof of this is seen in their yellow and dry appearance, like the florins of the republic, which, from being constantly gazed upon, have become reflected in them.”", "\"내 생각은 이렇소. 이 늙은 포데스타들, 이 옛 콘도티에리들은 — 카발칸티 가문은 군대를 지휘하고 지방을 다스린 집안이니까 — 내 생각에는 말이오, 그들이 수백만 프랑을 구석구석 파묻어 두고, 그 비밀을 오직 맏아들에게만 물려주고, 그 맏아들이 또 대를 이어 같은 짓을 해 온 거요. 그 증거가 바로 그들의 누렇고 메마른 얼굴이지요. 공화국의 플로린 금화를 하도 들여다본 나머지, 그 빛깔이 얼굴에 비친 거라오.\""]], [["“Certainly,”", "\"확실히 그렇습니다.\""], ["said Danglars, “and this is further supported by the fact of their not possessing an inch of land.”", "당글라르가 말했다. \"땅 한 뼘 가진 게 없다는 사실도 그걸 뒷받침하지요.\""]], [["“Very little, at least; I know of none which Cavalcanti possesses, excepting his palace in Lucca.”", "\"적어도 거의 없지요. 카발칸티가 가진 땅이라고는 루카에 있는 저택 말고는 들어 본 게 없소.\""]], [["“Ah, he has a palace?”", "\"아, 저택이 있긴 합니까?\""], ["said Danglars, laughing; “come, that is something.”", "당글라르가 웃으며 말했다. \"이런, 그래도 그건 뭔가 되는군요.\""]], [["“Yes; and more than that, he lets it to the Minister of Finance while he lives in a simple house.", "\"그렇소. 게다가 그는 그 저택을 재무 장관에게 세놓고 자기는 수수한 집에 산다오."], ["Oh, as I told you before, I think the old fellow is very close.”", "오, 앞서 말했듯이 그 영감은 아주 인색한 사람 같소.\""]], [["“Come, you do not flatter him.”", "\"이런, 그를 추켜세우진 않으시는군요.\""]], [["“I scarcely know him; I think I have seen him three times in my life; all I know relating to him is through Busoni and himself.", "\"나는 그를 잘 모르오. 평생 세 번쯤 본 것 같소. 그에 관해 아는 건 다 부소니와 그자 본인을 통해 들은 거요."], ["He was telling me this morning that, tired of letting his property lie dormant in Italy, which is a dead nation, he wished to find a method, either in France or England, of multiplying his millions, but remember, that though I place great confidence in Busoni, I am not responsible for this.”", "오늘 아침에 그가 하는 말이, 죽은 나라나 다름없는 이탈리아에 자기 재산을 묵혀 두기가 지겨워서, 프랑스든 영국이든 어디서든 수백만 프랑을 불릴 방도를 찾고 싶다더군요. 하지만 명심하시오, 내가 부소니는 무척 신뢰하지만 이 일까지 책임지는 건 아니라오.\""]], [["“Never mind; accept my thanks for the client you have sent me.", "\"괜찮습니다. 좋은 고객을 보내 주셔서 감사할 따름이지요."], ["It is a fine name to inscribe on my ledgers, and my cashier was quite proud of it when I explained to him who the Cavalcanti were.", "제 장부에 올리기에 훌륭한 이름이고, 카발칸티 가문이 어떤 집안인지 설명해 주었더니 제 회계 담당도 무척 자랑스러워하더군요."], ["By the way, this is merely a simple question, when this sort of people marry their sons, do they give them any fortune?”", "그건 그렇고, 이건 그저 단순한 질문입니다만, 이런 사람들은 아들을 결혼시킬 때 재산을 좀 떼어 줍니까?\""]], [["“Oh, that depends upon circumstances.", "\"오, 그건 사정에 따라 다르오."], ["I know an Italian prince, rich as a gold mine, one of the noblest families in Tuscany, who, when his sons married according to his wish, gave them millions; and when they married against his consent, merely allowed them thirty crowns a month.", "내가 아는 어느 이탈리아 공작은 금광처럼 부유하고 토스카나에서도 손꼽히는 명문 출신인데, 아들들이 제 뜻에 맞게 결혼하면 수백만 프랑을 주었지만, 제 뜻을 거슬러 결혼하면 한 달에 고작 삼십 크라운만 내주었다오."], ["Should Andrea marry according to his father’s views, he will, perhaps, give him one, two, or three millions.", "안드레아도 제 아비의 뜻에 맞게 결혼한다면, 아마 백만이나 이백만, 삼백만 프랑쯤은 줄 거요."], ["For example, supposing it were the daughter of a banker, he might take an interest in the house of the father-in-law of his son; then again, if he disliked his choice, the major takes the key, double-locks his coffer, and Master Andrea would be obliged to live like the sons of a Parisian family, by shuffling cards or rattling the dice.”", "가령 상대가 은행가의 딸이라면, 아들의 장인이 될 사람의 은행에 출자할 수도 있겠지요. 하지만 또 그 선택이 마음에 들지 않으면, 소령은 열쇠를 챙겨 금고를 이중으로 잠가 버릴 테고, 그러면 안드레아 도련님은 파리 가문 자제들처럼 카드를 섞거나 주사위를 굴려 가며 먹고살아야 할 거요.\""]], [["“Ah, that boy will find out some Bavarian or Peruvian princess; he will want a crown, an El Dorado, and Potosí.”", "\"아, 그 녀석은 바이에른이나 페루의 공주라도 찾아내려 할 겁니다. 왕관과 엘도라도와 포토시를 원하겠지요.\""]], [["“No; these grand lords on the other side of the Alps frequently marry into plain families; like Jupiter, they like to cross the race.", "\"아니오. 알프스 저편의 이 대귀족들은 흔히 평범한 가문과도 결혼하지요. 유피테르처럼, 혈통을 섞는 걸 좋아한단 말이오."], ["But do you wish to marry Andrea, my dear M. Danglars, that you are asking so many questions?”", "그런데 친애하는 당글라르 씨, 그렇게 질문이 많은 걸 보니 안드레아를 사위로 삼고 싶으신 거요?\""]], [["“_Ma foi_,”", "\"맹세코,\""], ["said Danglars, “it would not be a bad speculation, I fancy, and you know I am a speculator.”", "당글라르가 말했다. \"나쁘지 않은 투기일 것 같습니다. 아시다시피 저는 투기꾼 아닙니까.\""]], [["“You are not thinking of Mademoiselle Danglars, I hope; you would not like poor Andrea to have his throat cut by Albert?”", "\"설마 당글라르 양을 두고 하는 말은 아니길 바라오. 가엾은 안드레아가 알베르에게 목이 베이는 꼴을 보고 싶진 않으실 텐데?\""]], [["“Albert,”", "\"알베르라.\""], ["repeated Danglars, shrugging his shoulders; “ah, well; he would care very little about it, I think.”", "당글라르가 어깨를 으쓱하며 되뇌었다. \"아, 글쎄요. 그 친구야 별로 개의치 않을 것 같습니다.\""]], [["“But he is betrothed to your daughter, I believe?”", "\"하지만 그가 따님과 약혼한 사이 아니오?\""]], [["“Well, M. de Morcerf and I have talked about this marriage, but Madame de Morcerf and Albert——”", "\"음, 모르세르 씨와 저는 그 혼사를 의논한 적이 있습니다. 하지만 모르세르 부인과 알베르는……\""]], [["“You do not mean to say that it would not be a good match?”", "\"그게 좋은 혼처가 아니라는 말씀은 아니겠지요?\""]], [["“Indeed, I imagine that Mademoiselle Danglars is as good as M. de Morcerf.”", "\"솔직히, 제 생각에 당글라르 양은 모르세르 씨에게 조금도 모자라지 않습니다.\""]], [["“Mademoiselle Danglars’ fortune will be great, no doubt, especially if the telegraph should not make any more mistakes.”", "\"당글라르 양의 재산이야 대단하겠지요. 특히 전신이 더는 실수를 저지르지 않는다면 말이오.\""]], [["“Oh, I do not mean her fortune only; but tell me——”", "\"오, 재산만 두고 하는 말이 아닙니다. 그런데 말씀해 보시지요……\""]], [["“What?”", "\"무엇을?\""]], [["“Why did you not invite M. and Madame de Morcerf to your dinner?”", "\"왜 모르세르 씨 부부를 만찬에 초대하지 않으셨습니까?\""]], [["“I did so, but he excused himself on account of Madame de Morcerf being obliged to go to Dieppe for the benefit of sea air.”", "\"초대했소. 하지만 그는 모르세르 부인이 바다 공기를 쐬러 디에프에 가야 한다며 사양하더군요.\""]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"그래요, 그렇겠지요.\""], ["said Danglars, laughing, “it would do her a great deal of good.”", "당글라르가 웃으며 말했다. \"부인에게는 더없이 좋은 일이겠지요.\""]], [["“Why so?”", "\"어째서요?\""]], [["“Because it is the air she always breathed in her youth.”", "\"젊은 시절 늘 들이마시던 공기니까요.\""]], [["Monte Cristo took no notice of this ill-natured remark.", "몬테크리스토는 이 짓궂은 말에 아무 내색도 하지 않았다."]], [["“But still, if Albert be not so rich as Mademoiselle Danglars,”", "\"하지만 그래도, 알베르가 당글라르 양만큼 부유하지 않다 해도,\""], ["said the count, “you must allow that he has a fine name?”", "백작이 말했다. \"훌륭한 이름을 가졌다는 건 인정하셔야 할 거요.\""]], [["“So he has; but I like mine as well.”", "\"그야 그렇지요. 하지만 제 이름도 그에 못지않게 좋습니다.\""]], [["“Certainly; your name is popular, and does honor to the title they have adorned it with; but you are too intelligent not to know that according to a prejudice, too firmly rooted to be exterminated, a nobility which dates back five centuries is worth more than one that can only reckon twenty years.”", "\"물론이오. 남작의 이름은 널리 알려졌고, 그 이름을 빛내 주는 작위에도 명예를 더하지요. 하지만 남작은 워낙 영민한 분이니, 좀처럼 뿌리 뽑기 힘들 만큼 굳어진 편견에 따라, 오백 년을 거슬러 올라가는 귀족 가문이 고작 이십 년밖에 헤아릴 수 없는 가문보다 가치 있게 여겨진다는 걸 모르지 않으실 거요.\""]], [["“And for this very reason,”", "\"바로 그 이유 때문에,\""], ["said Danglars with a smile, which he tried to make sardonic, “I prefer M.", "당글라르가 비꼬는 투를 내려 애쓰며 미소 띤 채 말했다."], ["Andrea Cavalcanti to M. Albert de Morcerf.”", "\"저는 알베르 드 모르세르 씨보다 안드레아 카발칸티 씨가 더 마음에 듭니다.\""]], [["“Still, I should not think the Morcerfs would yield to the Cavalcanti?”", "\"그래도 모르세르 가문이 카발칸티 가문에 밀린다고는 생각하기 어려운데요?\""]], [["“The Morcerfs!—Stay, my dear count,”", "\"모르세르 가문이라니요! 잠깐, 친애하는 백작.\""], ["said Danglars; “you are a man of the world, are you not?”", "당글라르가 말했다. \"백작께서는 세상 물정에 밝은 분이시지요, 안 그렇습니까?\""]], [["“I think so.”", "\"그런 것 같소.\""]], [["“And you understand heraldry?”", "\"그럼 문장학(紋章學)도 좀 아십니까?\""]], [["“A little.”", "\"조금은요.\""]], [["“Well, look at my coat-of-arms, it is worth more than Morcerf’s.”", "\"그럼 제 가문 문장을 보십시오. 모르세르의 것보다 더 값나갑니다.\""]], [["“Why so?”", "\"어째서요?\""]], [["“Because, though I am not a baron by birth, my real name is, at least, Danglars.”", "\"저는 비록 태어날 때부터 남작은 아니었어도, 적어도 제 진짜 이름은 당글라르거든요.\""]], [["“Well, what then?”", "\"그래서, 그게 어떻다는 거요?\""]], [["“While his name is not Morcerf.”", "\"하지만 그의 이름은 모르세르가 아닙니다.\""]], [["“How?—not Morcerf?”", "\"뭐라고요? 모르세르가 아니라니?\""]], [["“Not the least in the world.”", "\"전혀 아닙니다.\" \"계속해 보시오.\""]], [["“Go on.” “I have been made a baron, so that I actually am one; he made himself a count, so that he is not one at all.”", "\"저는 남작으로 만들어졌으니, 실제로 남작입니다. 그는 스스로를 백작으로 만들었으니, 사실 백작이 전혀 아니지요.\""]], [["“Impossible!”", "\"그럴 리가!\""]], [["“Listen my dear count; M.", "\"들어 보십시오, 친애하는 백작."], ["de Morcerf has been my friend, or rather my acquaintance, during the last thirty years.", "모르세르 씨는 지난 삼십 년간 제 친구였습니다. 아니, 정확히는 아는 사이였지요."], ["You know I have made the most of my arms, though I never forgot my origin.”", "아시다시피 저는 제 문장을 한껏 활용해 왔습니다만, 제 출신을 결코 잊은 적은 없습니다.\""]], [["“A proof of great humility or great pride,”", "\"대단한 겸손이거나, 대단한 자만의 증거구려.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“Well, when I was a clerk, Morcerf was a mere fisherman.”", "\"제가 서기였을 때, 모르세르는 한낱 어부였습니다.\""]], [["“And then he was called——”", "\"그럼 그때 그의 이름은……\""]], [["“Fernand.”", "\"페르낭이었지요.\""]], [["“Only Fernand?”", "\"그냥 페르낭이오?\""]], [["“Fernand Mondego.”", "\"페르낭 몽데고입니다.\""]], [["“You are sure?”", "\"확실하오?\""]], [["“_Pardieu!_ I have bought enough fish of him to know his name.”", "\"맙소사! 그자한테서 생선을 사도 어지간히 샀으니 이름쯤은 알지요.\""]], [["“Then, why did you think of giving your daughter to him?”", "\"그런데 어째서 따님을 그에게 주려고 생각하셨소?\""]], [["“Because Fernand and Danglars, being both parvenus, both having become noble, both rich, are about equal in worth, excepting that there have been certain things mentioned of him that were never said of me.”", "\"페르낭과 당글라르가 둘 다 벼락출세한 자들이고, 둘 다 귀족이 되었고, 둘 다 부자이니, 그 값어치가 엇비슷하기 때문이지요. 다만 그자에 관해서는 저한테는 한 번도 나온 적 없는 어떤 말들이 떠돌았다는 점만 빼면요.\" \"무슨 말이오?\""]], [["“What?” “Oh, nothing!”", "\"오, 아무것도 아닙니다!\""]], [["“Ah, yes; what you tell me recalls to mind something about the name of Fernand Mondego.", "\"아, 그렇군. 남작 말을 들으니 페르낭 몽데고라는 이름에 얽힌 무언가가 떠오르는구려."], ["I have heard that name in Greece.”", "그리스에서 그 이름을 들은 적이 있소.\""]], [["“In conjunction with the affairs of Ali Pasha?”", "\"알리 파샤 사건과 얽혀서요?\""]], [["“Exactly so.”", "\"바로 그렇소.\""]], [["“This is the mystery,”", "\"바로 그게 수수께끼입니다.\""], ["said Danglars.", "당글라르가 말했다."], ["“I acknowledge I would have given anything to find it out.”", "\"솔직히 그걸 알아낼 수만 있다면 무엇이든 내놓았을 겁니다.\""]], [["“It would be very easy if you much wished it?”", "\"정말로 알고 싶다면 아주 쉬운 일일 텐데요?\""]], [["“How so?”", "\"어떻게요?\""]], [["“Probably you have some correspondent in Greece?”", "\"그리스에 거래처가 있을 것 아니오?\""]], [["“I should think so.”", "\"있을 겁니다.\""]], [["“At Yanina?”", "\"야니나에도요?\""]], [["“Everywhere.”", "\"어디에든 있지요.\""]], [["“Well, write to your correspondent in Yanina, and ask him what part was played by a Frenchman named Fernand Mondego in the catastrophe of Ali Tepelini.”", "\"그럼 야니나의 거래처에 편지를 써서, 알리 테펠리니의 참변에서 페르낭 몽데고라는 프랑스인이 어떤 역할을 했는지 물어보시오.\""]], [["“You are right,”", "\"옳은 말씀입니다.\""], ["exclaimed Danglars, rising quickly, “I will write today.”", "당글라르가 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다. \"오늘 당장 편지를 쓰겠습니다.\""]], [["“Do so.”", "\"그러시오.\""]], [["“I will.” 30259m", "\"그러겠습니다.\""]], [["“And if you should hear of anything very scandalous——”", "\"그리고 혹시 아주 추잡한 이야기라도 듣게 되거든……\""]], [["“I will communicate it to you.”", "\"백작께 알려 드리지요.\""]], [["“You will oblige me.”", "\"고맙겠소.\""]], [["Danglars rushed out of the room, and made but one leap into his _coupé_.", "당글라르는 방을 뛰쳐나가, 단숨에 자기 마차로 뛰어올랐다."]], [["# Chapter 67. The Office of the King’s Attorney", "# Chapter 67. The Office of the King’s Attorney"]], [["Let us leave the banker driving his horses at their fullest speed, and follow Madame Danglars in her morning excursion.", "은행가가 말들을 전속력으로 몰아대도록 내버려 두고, 우리는 당글라르 부인의 아침 나들이를 뒤따라가 보자."], ["We have said that at half-past twelve o’clock Madame Danglars had ordered her horses, and had left home in the carriage.", "앞서 말한 대로, 열두 시 반에 당글라르 부인은 말을 준비하라 일러두고 마차에 올라 집을 나섰다."], ["She directed her course towards the Faubourg Saint Germain, went down the Rue Mazarine, and stopped at the Passage du Pont-Neuf.", "그녀는 생제르맹 구역 쪽으로 방향을 잡아 마자린가를 따라 내려가더니, 퐁뇌프 통로에서 멈추었다."], ["She descended, and went through the passage.", "그녀는 마차에서 내려 통로를 가로질러 갔다."], ["She was very plainly dressed, as would be the case with a woman of taste walking in the morning.", "옷차림은 아주 수수했는데, 아침나절에 산책하는 안목 있는 여인이라면 으레 그러할 법한 차림이었다."], ["At the Rue Guénégaud she called a cab, and directed the driver to go to the Rue de Harlay.", "그녀는 게네고가에서 삯마차를 불러, 마부에게 아를레가로 가자고 일렀다."], ["As soon as she was seated in the vehicle, she drew from her pocket a very thick black veil, which she tied on to her straw bonnet.", "차에 앉자마자 그녀는 주머니에서 아주 두꺼운 검은 베일을 꺼내 밀짚 보닛에 둘러 묶었다."], ["She then replaced the bonnet, and saw with pleasure, in a little pocket-mirror, that her white complexion and brilliant eyes were alone visible.", "그러고는 보닛을 다시 쓰고, 작은 손거울에 비친 자신의 모습을 보며 흐뭇해했다. 흰 살결과 빛나는 두 눈만 드러나 있었던 것이다."], ["The cab crossed the Pont-Neuf and entered the Rue de Harlay by the Place Dauphine; the driver was paid as the door opened, and stepping lightly up the stairs Madame Danglars soon reached the Salle des Pas-Perdus.", "삯마차는 퐁뇌프를 건너 도핀 광장을 지나 아를레가로 들어섰다. 마차 문이 열리자 마부에게 삯이 치러졌고, 당글라르 부인은 가뿐히 계단을 올라 곧 '잃어버린 발걸음의 방'에 다다랐다."]], [["There was a great deal going on that morning, and many business-like persons at the Palais; business-like persons pay very little attention to women, and Madame Danglars crossed the hall without exciting any more attention than any other woman calling upon her lawyer.", "그날 아침 법원에서는 무척 분주한 일들이 벌어지고 있었고, 일에 바쁜 사람들이 많았다. 일에 바쁜 사람들은 여인에게 거의 눈길을 주지 않는 법이라, 당글라르 부인은 변호사를 찾아온 여느 여인이나 다름없이 아무런 주목도 끌지 않고 홀을 가로질렀다."]], [["There was a great press of people in M. de Villefort’s antechamber, but Madame Danglars had no occasion even to pronounce her name.", "빌포르 씨의 대기실에는 사람들이 잔뜩 밀려 있었지만, 당글라르 부인은 이름조차 밝힐 필요가 없었다."], ["The instant she appeared the door-keeper rose, came to her, and asked her whether she was not the person with whom the procureur had made an appointment; and on her affirmative answer being given, he conducted her by a private passage to M. de Villefort’s office.", "그녀가 모습을 드러내자마자 문지기가 일어나 그녀에게 다가오더니, 검사장과 약속한 분이 아니냐고 물었다. 그녀가 그렇다고 대답하자, 그는 별도의 통로로 그녀를 안내해 빌포르 씨의 집무실로 데려갔다."]], [["The magistrate was seated in an armchair, writing, with his back towards the door; he did not move as he heard it open, and the door-keeper pronounce the words, “Walk in, madame,”", "검사는 안락의자에 앉아 글을 쓰고 있었는데, 문을 등진 자세였다. 그는 문이 열리는 소리와 문지기가 \"들어가십시오, 부인\"이라고 하는 말, 그리고 다시 문이 닫히는 소리를 들으면서도 꼼짝하지 않았다."], ["and then reclose it; but no sooner had the man’s footsteps ceased, than he started up, drew the bolts, closed the curtains, and examined every corner of the room.", "그러나 문지기의 발소리가 잦아들기 무섭게 그는 벌떡 일어나 빗장을 지르고, 커튼을 치고, 방 안 구석구석을 살폈다."], ["Then, when he had assured himself that he could neither be seen nor heard, and was consequently relieved of doubts, he said:", "그렇게 누구에게도 보이거나 들릴 염려가 없음을 확인하고 의혹에서 벗어나자, 그가 입을 열었다."]], [["“Thanks, madame,—thanks for your punctuality;”", "\"고맙습니다, 부인. 시간을 지켜 와 주셔서요.\""], ["and he offered a chair to Madame Danglars, which she accepted, for her heart beat so violently that she felt nearly suffocated.", "그는 당글라르 부인에게 의자를 권했고, 그녀는 그것을 받아들였다. 가슴이 어찌나 세차게 뛰던지 숨이 막힐 지경이었기 때문이다."]], [["30261m\n“It is a long time, madame,”", "\"참으로 오랜만입니다, 부인.\""], ["said the procureur, describing a half-circle with his chair, so as to place himself exactly opposite to Madame Danglars,—“it is a long time since I had the pleasure of speaking alone with you, and I regret that we have only now met to enter upon a painful conversation.”", "검사는 의자를 반원을 그리며 돌려, 당글라르 부인과 정면으로 마주 앉았다. \"부인과 단둘이 이야기를 나누는 기쁨을 누린 지가 참으로 오래되었습니다. 그런데 이렇게 다시 만난 자리가 하필 괴로운 이야기를 나누어야 하는 자리라니, 유감스럽습니다.\""]], [["“Nevertheless, sir, you see I have answered your first appeal, although certainly the conversation must be much more painful for me than for you.”", "\"그렇긴 하지만, 검사님, 보시다시피 저는 처음 부르심에 응해 이렇게 왔습니다. 비록 이 이야기가 검사님보다는 저에게 훨씬 더 괴로운 것임이 분명하지만요.\""], ["Villefort smiled bitterly.", "빌포르가 씁쓸하게 미소 지었다."]], [["“It is true, then,”", "\"그렇다면 사실이군요.\""], ["he said, rather uttering his thoughts aloud than addressing his companion,—“it is true, then, that all our actions leave their traces—some sad, others bright—on our paths; it is true that every step in our lives is like the course of an insect on the sands;—it leaves its track!", "그는 상대에게 건네는 말이라기보다 제 생각을 소리 내어 읊조리듯 말했다. \"우리의 모든 행위가 우리가 걸어온 길 위에 흔적을 남긴다는 것이 사실이군요. 어떤 것은 슬프고, 어떤 것은 밝은 흔적을요."], ["Alas, to many the path is traced by tears.”", "우리 삶의 한 걸음 한 걸음이 모래 위를 기어가는 벌레의 자취와도 같다는 것이 사실이군요. 자취를 남기지요! 아아, 많은 이들에게 그 길은 눈물로 그려집니다.\""]], [["“Sir,”", "\"검사님,\""], ["said Madame Danglars, “you can feel for my emotion, can you not?", "당글라르 부인이 말했다. \"제 심정을 헤아려 주실 수 있겠지요?"], ["Spare me, then, I beseech you.", "그러니 부디 저를 가엾이 여겨 주십시오."], ["When I look at this room,—whence so many guilty creatures have departed, trembling and ashamed, when I look at that chair before which I now sit trembling and ashamed,—oh, it requires all my reason to convince me that I am not a very guilty woman and you a menacing judge.”", "이 방을 둘러보노라면 — 수많은 죄인들이 떨면서 부끄러워하며 떠나간 이 방을, 지금 제가 떨면서 부끄러워하며 앉아 있는 이 의자를 바라보노라면 — 아, 제가 죄 많은 여인이 아니고 검사님이 위협하는 재판관이 아님을 스스로에게 납득시키려면 온 이성을 다 끌어모아야 한답니다.\""]], [["Villefort dropped his head and sighed.", "빌포르가 고개를 떨구고 한숨을 쉬었다."]], [["“And I,”", "\"그리고 저는,\""], ["he said, “I feel that my place is not in the judge’s seat, but on the prisoner’s bench.” 30263m", "그가 말했다. \"제 자리가 재판관석이 아니라 피고석에 있다고 느낍니다.\""]], [["“You?”", "\"검사님께서요?\""], ["said Madame Danglars. “Yes, I.”", "당글라르 부인이 말했다."]], [["“I think, sir, you exaggerate your situation,”", "\"그렇습니다, 제가요.\""], ["said Madame Danglars, whose beautiful eyes sparkled for a moment.", "\"제 생각에는, 검사님, 처지를 너무 과장하시는 것 같군요.\" 당글라르 부인이 말했다."], ["“The paths of which you were just speaking have been traced by all young men of ardent imaginations.", "그녀의 아름다운 두 눈이 한순간 반짝였다. \"방금 말씀하신 그런 길은 정열적인 상상력을 지닌 젊은이라면 누구나 걸어온 길이지요."], ["Besides the pleasure, there is always remorse from the indulgence of our passions, and, after all, what have you men to fear from all this?", "게다가 정념에 탐닉하면 쾌락 뒤에는 늘 회한이 따르는 법이고요. 그렇다 한들 검사님 같은 남자분들이 이런 일로 무엇을 두려워하시겠습니까?"], ["the world excuses, and notoriety ennobles you.”", "세상은 너그러이 봐주고, 평판은 오히려 남자를 높여 주는걸요.\""]], [["“Madame,”", "\"부인,\""], ["replied Villefort, “you know that I am no hypocrite, or, at least, that I never deceive without a reason.", "빌포르가 대답했다. \"제가 위선자가 아니라는 것을, 적어도 까닭 없이 남을 속이는 사람이 아니라는 것을 부인도 아실 겁니다."], ["If my brow be severe, it is because many misfortunes have clouded it; if my heart be petrified, it is that it might sustain the blows it has received.", "제 이마가 험상궂다면, 그것은 수많은 불행이 그 위에 그늘을 드리웠기 때문입니다. 제 심장이 돌처럼 굳어 있다면, 그것은 받은 타격들을 견뎌 내기 위함이었습니다."], ["I was not so in my youth, I was not so on the night of the betrothal, when we were all seated around a table in the Rue du Cours at Marseilles.", "젊은 시절의 저는 그렇지 않았습니다. 약혼하던 그날 밤, 우리 모두가 마르세유의 쿠르가에서 한 식탁에 둘러앉아 있던 그날의 저는 그렇지 않았습니다."], ["But since then everything has changed in and about me; I am accustomed to brave difficulties, and, in the conflict to crush those who, by their own free will, or by chance, voluntarily or involuntarily, interfere with me in my career.", "그러나 그 이후로 제 안의 모든 것, 제 둘레의 모든 것이 변했습니다. 저는 어려움에 맞서는 데 익숙해졌고, 그 싸움 속에서 제 길을 가로막는 자들을, 그들이 제 발로든 우연이든, 의도했든 아니든, 짓밟는 데 익숙해졌습니다. 우리가 가장 간절히 바라는 것일수록, 그것을 얻으려는 자들이나 우리가 빼앗으려는 자들에 의해 그만큼 간절히 가로막히는 것이 보통입니다."], ["It is generally the case that what we most ardently desire is as ardently withheld from us by those who wish to obtain it, or from whom we attempt to snatch it.", "그리하여 인간이 저지르는 잘못의 대부분은 필연이라는 그럴듯한 모습으로 위장한 채 우리 앞에 나타납니다. 그러다 흥분과 광란과 두려움의 한순간에 잘못을 저지르고 나면, 그제야 우리는 그것을 피하고 벗어날 수 있었음을 깨닫습니다."], ["Thus, the greater number of a man’s errors come before him disguised under the specious form of necessity; then, after error has been committed in a moment of excitement, of delirium, or of fear, we see that we might have avoided and escaped it.", "눈먼 우리가 그때는 보지 못했던, 쓸 수도 있었던 방법들이 그제야 단순하고 쉬워 보이지요. 그러면 우리는 말합니다. '왜 나는 저렇게 하지 않고 이렇게 했던가?'"], ["The means we might have used, which we in our blindness could not see, then seem simple and easy, and we say, ‘Why did I not do this, instead of that?’ Women, on the contrary, are rarely tormented with remorse; for the decision does not come from you,—your misfortunes are generally imposed upon you, and your faults the results of others’ crimes.”", "반면에 여인들은 회한에 시달리는 일이 드뭅니다. 결정이 부인들에게서 나온 것이 아니니까요. 부인들의 불행은 대개 떠안겨진 것이고, 부인들의 잘못은 남들이 저지른 죄의 결과일 뿐이지요.\""]], [["“In any case, sir, you will allow,”", "\"어찌 됐든, 검사님, 인정하셔야 할 것이 있습니다.\""], ["replied Madame Danglars, “that, even if the fault were alone mine, I last night received a severe punishment for it.”", "당글라르 부인이 대답했다. \"설령 그 잘못이 오롯이 저만의 것이었다 해도, 저는 어젯밤 그 죄에 대해 혹독한 벌을 받았으니까요.\""]], [["“Poor thing,”", "\"가엾은 분.\" 빌포르가 그녀의 손을 잡으며 말했다."], ["said Villefort, pressing her hand, “it was too severe for your strength, for you were twice overwhelmed, and yet——”", "\"부인의 기력으로 견디기에는 너무도 혹독했지요. 두 번이나 압도당하셨으니. 하지만 그래도——\""]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래도요?\""]], [["“Well, I must tell you.", "\"그래도, 말씀드려야겠습니다."], ["Collect all your courage, for you have not yet heard all.”", "용기를 다 끌어모으십시오. 아직 다 들으신 게 아니니까요.\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["exclaimed Madame Danglars, alarmed, “what is there more to hear?”", "당글라르 부인이 겁에 질려 외쳤다. \"더 들어야 할 게 있단 말씀인가요?\""]], [["“You only look back to the past, and it is, indeed, bad enough.", "\"부인은 과거만 돌아보고 계시지요. 사실 그것만으로도 충분히 끔찍합니다."], ["Well, picture to yourself a future more gloomy still—certainly frightful, perhaps sanguinary!”", "하지만 그보다 더 음울한 미래를 그려 보십시오. 분명 무시무시하고, 어쩌면 피로 물든 미래를요!\""]], [["The baroness knew how calm Villefort naturally was, and his present excitement frightened her so much that she opened her mouth to scream, but the sound died in her throat.", "남작 부인은 빌포르가 본래 얼마나 침착한 사람인지 알고 있었기에, 지금의 이 흥분이 그녀를 너무도 두렵게 하여 비명을 지르려 입을 벌렸으나, 소리는 목구멍에서 잦아들고 말았다."]], [["“How has this terrible past been recalled?”", "\"이 끔찍한 과거가 어떻게 다시 불려 나왔을까요?\""], ["cried Villefort; “how is it that it has escaped from the depths of the tomb and the recesses of our hearts, where it was buried, to visit us now, like a phantom, whitening our cheeks and flushing our brows with shame?”", "빌포르가 외쳤다. \"그것이 어떻게 무덤 깊은 곳과 우리 마음속 후미진 자리에서, 그토록 깊이 묻어 두었던 그곳에서 빠져나와, 이제 와 유령처럼 우리를 찾아와 우리 두 뺨을 핏기 없이 만들고 우리 이마를 부끄러움으로 물들이는 걸까요?\""]], [["“Alas,”", "\"아아,\""], ["said Hermine, “doubtless it is chance.”", "에르민이 말했다. \"필시 우연이겠지요.\""]], [["“Chance?”", "\"우연이라고요?\""], ["replied Villefort; “No, no, madame, there is no such thing as chance.”", "빌포르가 대답했다. \"아니요, 아닙니다, 부인. 우연 같은 것은 없습니다.\""]], [["“Oh, yes; has not a fatal chance revealed all this?", "\"아니에요, 우연이 맞아요. 운명적인 우연이 이 모든 걸 드러낸 게 아닌가요?"], ["Was it not by chance the Count of Monte Cristo bought that house?", "몬테크리스토 백작이 그 집을 산 것이 우연이 아니던가요?"], ["Was it not by chance he caused the earth to be dug up?", "그가 땅을 파게 한 것이 우연이 아니던가요?"], ["Is it not by chance that the unfortunate child was disinterred under the trees?—that poor innocent offspring of mine, which I never even kissed, but for whom I wept many, many tears.", "그 불행한 아이가 나무들 밑에서 파헤쳐진 것이 우연이 아니던가요? 입 한번 맞춰 보지도 못한, 그러나 그토록 많고 많은 눈물을 흘리며 슬퍼했던 가엾고 죄 없는 제 핏줄이 말이에요."], ["Ah, my heart clung to the count when he mentioned the dear spoil found beneath the flowers.”", "아, 백작이 꽃 밑에서 발견된 그 가여운 잔해 이야기를 꺼냈을 때, 제 가슴이 그에게로 매달렸답니다.\""]], [["“Well, no, madame,—this is the terrible news I have to tell you,”", "\"아니요, 부인, 그렇지 않습니다. 바로 이것이 제가 부인께 전해야 할 끔찍한 소식입니다.\""], ["said Villefort in a hollow voice—“no, nothing was found beneath the flowers; there was no child disinterred—no.", "빌포르가 공허한 목소리로 말했다. \"아닙니다, 꽃 밑에서는 아무것도 발견되지 않았습니다. 파헤쳐진 아이 같은 것은 없었습니다. 없었어요."], ["You must not weep, no, you must not groan, you must tremble!”", "부인은 울어서는 안 됩니다, 아니, 신음해서도 안 됩니다, 부인은 떨어야 합니다!\""]], [["“What can you mean?”", "\"그게 무슨 말씀이세요?\""], ["asked Madame Danglars, shuddering.", "당글라르 부인이 몸서리치며 물었다."]], [["“I mean that M. de Monte Cristo, digging underneath these trees, found neither skeleton nor chest, because neither of them was there!”", "\"몬테크리스토 씨가 그 나무들 밑을 파헤쳤을 때, 해골도 상자도 찾지 못했다는 뜻입니다. 둘 다 거기 없었으니까요!\""]], [["“Neither of them there?”", "\"둘 다 거기 없었다고요?\""], ["repeated Madame Danglars, her staring, wide-open eyes expressing her alarm.", "당글라르 부인이 따라 말했다. 부릅뜬 채 휘둥그레진 두 눈이 그녀의 경악을 드러내고 있었다."], ["“Neither of them there!”", "\"둘 다 거기 없었다니!\""], ["she again said, as though striving to impress herself with the meaning of the words which escaped her.", "그녀는 자신의 입에서 새어 나온 그 말의 뜻을 스스로에게 새기려 애쓰듯 다시 한번 말했다."]], [["“No,”", "\"없었습니다.\""], ["said Villefort, burying his face in his hands, “no, a hundred times no!”", "빌포르가 두 손에 얼굴을 파묻으며 말했다. \"없었어요, 백 번이라도 없었다 말하겠습니다!\""]], [["“Then you did not bury the poor child there, sir?", "\"그렇다면 검사님이 그 가엾은 아이를 거기 묻으신 게 아니었나요?"], ["Why did you deceive me?", "어째서 저를 속이셨어요?"], ["Where did you place it?", "그 아이를 어디다 두셨어요?"], ["tell me—where?”", "말해 주세요. 어디에요?\""]], [["“There!", "\"거기였습니다! 하지만 제 말을 들어 주십시오."], ["But listen to me—listen—and you will pity me who has for twenty years alone borne the heavy burden of grief I am about to reveal, without casting the least portion upon you.”", "들어 주세요. 그러면 부인도 저를 가엾이 여기실 겁니다. 이십 년 동안 홀로, 부인께는 그 짐의 티끌만큼도 지우지 않고서, 이제 털어놓으려는 이 무거운 슬픔의 짐을 짊어져 온 저를 말입니다.\""]], [["“Oh, you frighten me!", "\"아, 검사님은 저를 무섭게 하시는군요!"], ["But speak; I will listen.”", "하지만 말씀하세요. 듣겠습니다.\""]], [["“You recollect that sad night, when you were half-expiring on that bed in the red damask room, while I, scarcely less agitated than you, awaited your delivery.", "\"부인도 그 슬픈 밤을 기억하실 겁니다. 부인은 붉은 다마스크 천이 깔린 방의 그 침상에서 반쯤 숨이 넘어가고 있었고, 저는 부인 못지않게 마음을 졸이며 해산을 기다리고 있었지요."], ["The child was born, was given to me—motionless, breathless, voiceless; we thought it dead.”", "아이가 태어났고, 제게 건네졌습니다. 미동도, 숨결도, 울음소리도 없었습니다. 우리는 아이가 죽은 줄 알았습니다.\""]], [["Madame Danglars moved rapidly, as though she would spring from her chair, but Villefort stopped, and clasped his hands as if to implore her attention.", "당글라르 부인이 의자에서 벌떡 일어서려는 듯 황급히 몸을 움직였으나, 빌포르가 멈춰 세우고는 그녀의 주의를 간청하듯 두 손을 맞잡았다."]], [["“We thought it dead,”", "\"우리는 아이가 죽은 줄 알았습니다.\""], ["he repeated; “I placed it in the chest, which was to take the place of a coffin; I descended to the garden, I dug a hole, and then flung it down in haste.", "그가 거듭 말했다. \"저는 그 아이를 관 대신 쓸 상자에 넣었습니다. 정원으로 내려가 구덩이를 파고는 서둘러 던져 넣었지요."], ["Scarcely had I covered it with earth, when the arm of the Corsican was stretched towards me; I saw a shadow rise, and, at the same time, a flash of light.", "흙으로 막 덮었을까 말까 했을 때, 코르시카인의 팔이 제 쪽으로 뻗어 왔습니다. 그림자 하나가 솟아오르는 것을 보았고, 동시에 번쩍하는 불빛이 일었습니다."], ["I felt pain; I wished to cry out, but an icy shiver ran through my veins and stifled my voice; I fell lifeless, and fancied myself killed.", "통증을 느꼈고, 소리를 지르려 했지만, 얼음장 같은 전율이 핏줄을 타고 흘러 제 목소리를 틀어막았습니다. 저는 숨이 끊긴 듯 쓰러졌고, 죽었다고 여겼습니다."], ["Never shall I forget your sublime courage, when, having returned to consciousness, I dragged myself to the foot of the stairs, and you, almost dying yourself, came to meet me.", "부인의 그 숭고한 용기를 저는 결코 잊지 못할 겁니다. 의식을 되찾은 제가 계단 아래까지 몸을 끌고 갔을 때, 부인은 거의 죽어 가는 몸으로 저를 맞으러 나오셨지요."], ["We were obliged to keep silent upon the dreadful catastrophe.", "우리는 그 끔찍한 참사에 대해 입을 다물 수밖에 없었습니다."], ["You had the fortitude to regain the house, assisted by your nurse.", "부인은 유모의 부축을 받아 집으로 돌아갈 만큼의 굳센 마음을 지니고 계셨고요."], ["A duel was the pretext for my wound.", "결투를 제 상처의 핑계로 둘러댔습니다."], ["Though we scarcely expected it, our secret remained in our own keeping alone.", "우리로서는 거의 기대도 못 했지만, 우리의 비밀은 우리만의 것으로 남았습니다."], ["I was taken to Versailles; for three months I struggled with death; at last, as I seemed to cling to life, I was ordered to the South.", "저는 베르사유로 옮겨졌고, 석 달 동안 죽음과 사투를 벌였습니다. 마침내 제가 목숨에 매달리는 듯 보이자, 남쪽으로 가라는 처방이 내려졌지요."], ["Four men carried me from Paris to Châlons, walking six leagues a day; Madame de Villefort followed the litter in her carriage.", "네 사람이 저를 파리에서 샬롱까지 떠메고, 하루에 6리외씩 걸어 옮겼습니다. 빌포르 부인은 자기 마차로 들것 뒤를 따랐고요."], ["At Châlons I was put upon the Saône, thence I passed on to the Rhône, whence I descended, merely with the current, to Arles; at Arles I was again placed on my litter, and continued my journey to Marseilles.", "샬롱에서 저는 손강에 배로 실렸고, 거기서 론강으로 옮겨 탄 뒤, 그저 물길을 따라 아를까지 내려갔습니다. 아를에서 다시 들것에 실려 마르세유까지 여정을 이어 갔지요."], ["My recovery lasted six months.", "회복하는 데 여섯 달이 걸렸습니다."], ["I never heard you mentioned, and I did not dare inquire for you.", "부인 이야기는 한마디도 듣지 못했고, 감히 부인의 소식을 물을 엄두도 내지 못했습니다."], ["When I returned to Paris, I learned that you, the widow of M. de Nargonne, had married M. Danglars.", "파리로 돌아왔을 때, 저는 나르곤 씨의 미망인이 된 부인이 당글라르 씨와 결혼했다는 것을 알게 되었습니다."]], [["“What was the subject of my thoughts from the time consciousness returned to me?", "\"의식을 되찾은 이래로 제 생각을 사로잡은 것이 무엇이었겠습니까? 언제나 똑같았습니다."], ["Always the same—always the child’s corpse, coming every night in my dreams, rising from the earth, and hovering over the grave with menacing look and gesture.", "언제나 그 아이의 시신이었지요. 밤마다 꿈속에 나타나 땅에서 솟아올라, 위협하는 눈빛과 몸짓으로 무덤 위를 맴돌았습니다. 파리로 돌아오자마자 저는 수소문했습니다."], ["I inquired immediately on my return to Paris; the house had not been inhabited since we left it, but it had just been let for nine years.", "그 집은 우리가 떠난 뒤로 사람이 살지 않았는데, 마침 구 년 계약으로 막 세를 놓은 참이었습니다. 저는 세입자를 찾아갔습니다."], ["I found the tenant. I pretended that I disliked the idea that a house belonging to my wife’s father and mother should pass into the hands of strangers.", "제 장인 장모의 소유였던 집이 낯선 이들의 손에 넘어가는 것이 못마땅하다는 척했지요. 계약을 해지해 주면 보상을 하겠다고 제안했습니다."], ["I offered to pay them for cancelling the lease; they demanded 6,000 francs.", "그들은 6천 프랑을 요구하더군요. 저는 만 프랑이라도 줄 작정이었습니다."], ["I would have given 10,000—I would have given 20,000.", "2만 프랑이라도 줄 참이었지요."], ["I had the money with me; I made the tenant sign the deed of resilition, and when I had obtained what I so much wanted, I galloped to Auteuil.", "마침 돈을 지니고 있었기에, 세입자에게 해지 증서에 서명을 받았고, 그토록 원하던 것을 손에 넣자마자 오퇴유로 말을 몰았습니다."], ["No one had entered the house since I had left it.", "제가 떠난 뒤로 그 집에 발을 들인 사람은 아무도 없었습니다."]], [["“It was five o’clock in the afternoon; I ascended into the red room, and waited for night.", "\"오후 다섯 시였습니다. 저는 붉은 방으로 올라가 밤을 기다렸지요."], ["There all the thoughts which had disturbed me during my year of constant agony came back with double force.", "그곳에서, 일 년 내내 끊임없는 고뇌로 저를 어지럽혔던 온갖 생각들이 곱절의 힘으로 되살아났습니다."], ["The Corsican, who had declared the vendetta against me, who had followed me from Nîmes to Paris, who had hid himself in the garden, who had struck me, had seen me dig the grave, had seen me inter the child,—he might become acquainted with your person,—nay, he might even then have known it.", "저를 상대로 복수를 선포하고, 님에서 파리까지 저를 뒤쫓아 와, 정원에 숨어 저를 찌른 그 코르시카인 — 제가 구덩이를 파는 것을 보고, 제가 아이를 묻는 것을 본 그 사내가 — 부인의 신원을 알아낼 수도 있었습니다. 아니, 어쩌면 이미 알고 있었는지도 모릅니다."], ["Would he not one day make you pay for keeping this terrible secret?", "그가 언젠가는 부인에게 이 끔찍한 비밀을 지킨 대가를 치르게 하지 않겠습니까?"], ["Would it not be a sweet revenge for him when he found that I had not died from the blow of his dagger?", "제가 그의 단검에 죽지 않았음을 알게 된다면, 그에게는 그것이 달콤한 복수가 되지 않겠습니까?"], ["It was therefore necessary, before everything else, and at all risks, that I should cause all traces of the past to disappear—that I should destroy every material vestige; too much reality would always remain in my recollection.", "그러므로 무엇보다 먼저, 어떤 위험을 무릅쓰고라도, 저는 과거의 모든 흔적을 없애야 했습니다. 물질적인 자취란 자취는 죄다 없애야 했지요. 어차피 제 기억 속에는 너무도 많은 현실이 영영 남아 있을 테니까요."], ["It was for this I had annulled the lease—it was for this I had come—it was for this I was waiting.", "제가 계약을 해지한 것도, 제가 찾아온 것도, 제가 기다리고 있던 것도 모두 그 때문이었습니다."]], [["“Night arrived; I allowed it to become quite dark.", "\"밤이 왔습니다. 저는 사위가 완전히 어두워지도록 내버려 두었습니다."], ["I was without a light in that room; when the wind shook all the doors, behind which I continually expected to see some spy concealed, I trembled.", "그 방에서 저는 불도 켜지 않았습니다. 바람이 모든 문을 흔들어 댈 때면, 그 문 뒤마다 첩자가 숨어 있을 것만 같아 끊임없이 떨었습니다."], ["I seemed everywhere to hear your moans behind me in the bed, and I dared not turn around.", "사방에서 침상에 누운 부인의 신음이 제 등 뒤로 들려오는 것만 같아, 감히 뒤돌아볼 수가 없었습니다."], ["My heart beat so violently that I feared my wound would open.", "심장이 어찌나 세차게 뛰던지 상처가 터질까 두려웠습니다."], ["At length, one by one, all the noises in the neighborhood ceased.", "마침내 동네의 온갖 소음이 하나둘 그쳤습니다."], ["I understood that I had nothing to fear, that I should neither be seen nor heard, so I decided upon descending to the garden.", "두려워할 것이 없음을, 누구에게도 보이거나 들리지 않으리라는 것을 깨닫고, 저는 정원으로 내려가기로 마음먹었습니다."]], [["“Listen, Hermine; I consider myself as brave as most men, but when I drew from my breast the little key of the staircase, which I had found in my coat—that little key we both used to cherish so much, which you wished to have fastened to a golden ring—when I opened the door, and saw the pale moon shedding a long stream of white light on the spiral staircase like a spectre, I leaned against the wall, and nearly shrieked.", "\"들어 보세요, 에르민. 저는 제가 웬만한 사내들만큼은 용감하다고 자부합니다. 하지만 외투 속에서 찾아낸 그 계단의 작은 열쇠를 — 우리 둘이 그토록 소중히 여겼던, 부인이 황금 고리에 매달아 두고 싶어 했던 그 작은 열쇠를 — 품에서 꺼냈을 때, 문을 열고서 창백한 달이 나선 계단 위로 유령처럼 길게 흰 빛줄기를 드리우는 것을 보았을 때, 저는 벽에 기대선 채 하마터면 비명을 지를 뻔했습니다."], ["I seemed to be going mad.", "미쳐 가는 것만 같았지요."], ["At last I mastered my agitation.", "마침내 저는 동요를 다스렸습니다."], ["I descended the staircase step by step; the only thing I could not conquer was a strange trembling in my knees.", "한 계단 한 계단 내려갔지요. 도무지 어찌할 수 없었던 단 한 가지는 무릎이 묘하게 떨리는 것이었습니다."], ["I grasped the railings; if I had relaxed my hold for a moment, I should have fallen.", "저는 난간을 움켜쥐었습니다. 한순간이라도 손을 놓았더라면 그대로 굴러떨어졌을 겁니다."], ["I reached the lower door.", "저는 아래쪽 문에 다다랐습니다."], ["Outside this door a spade was placed against the wall; I took it, and advanced towards the thicket.", "그 문 밖 벽에 삽 하나가 세워져 있더군요. 저는 그것을 집어 들고 덤불 쪽으로 나아갔습니다."], ["I had provided myself with a dark lantern.", "어둠 가리개가 달린 등불을 챙겨 둔 터였지요."], ["In the middle of the lawn I stopped to light it, then I continued my path.", "잔디밭 한가운데서 멈춰 그 불을 밝히고, 다시 가던 길을 이었습니다."]], [["“It was the end of November, all the verdure of the garden had disappeared, the trees were nothing more than skeletons with their long bony arms, and the dead leaves sounded on the gravel under my feet.", "\"십일월 말이라, 정원의 푸르름은 죄다 사라지고 없었습니다. 나무들은 길고 앙상한 팔뚝만 남은 해골에 지나지 않았고, 발밑 자갈 위로 마른 잎들이 바스락거렸습니다."], ["My terror overcame me to such a degree as I approached the thicket, that I took a pistol from my pocket and armed myself.", "덤불에 다가갈수록 공포가 어찌나 저를 짓누르던지, 저는 주머니에서 권총을 꺼내 무장했습니다."], ["I fancied continually that I saw the figure of the Corsican between the branches.", "가지들 사이로 끊임없이 코르시카인의 형상이 보이는 것만 같았지요."], ["I examined the thicket with my dark lantern; it was empty.", "저는 어둠 가리개 등불로 덤불을 살폈습니다. 비어 있더군요."], ["I looked carefully around; I was indeed alone,—no noise disturbed the silence but the owl, whose piercing cry seemed to be calling up the phantoms of the night.", "주위를 찬찬히 둘러보았습니다. 정말로 저 혼자였습니다. 정적을 깨뜨리는 것이라곤 올빼미뿐이었는데, 그 날카로운 울음소리가 밤의 유령들을 불러내는 듯했습니다."], ["I tied my lantern to a forked branch I had noticed a year before at the precise spot where I stopped to dig the hole.", "일 년 전, 구덩이를 파려고 멈춰 섰던 바로 그 자리에서 봐 두었던 갈라진 나뭇가지에 저는 등불을 매달았습니다."]], [["“The grass had grown very thickly there during the summer, and when autumn arrived no one had been there to mow it.", "\"여름내 그곳에는 풀이 아주 무성하게 자라 있었고, 가을이 와도 그것을 베러 온 사람은 없었습니다."], ["Still one place where the grass was thin attracted my attention; it evidently was there I had turned up the ground. I went to work.", "그래도 풀이 듬성한 한 곳이 제 눈길을 끌었지요. 분명 제가 흙을 파헤쳐 놓았던 자리였습니다."], ["The hour, then, for which I had been waiting during the last year had at length arrived.", "저는 일을 시작했습니다. 지난 일 년 내내 기다려 온 그 시간이 마침내 온 것이었지요."], ["How I worked, how I hoped, how I struck every piece of turf, thinking to find some resistance to my spade!", "제가 얼마나 파헤쳤는지, 얼마나 기대했는지, 삽에 무언가 걸리기를 바라며 잔디 한 조각 한 조각을 얼마나 내리쳤는지!"], ["But no, I found nothing, though I had made a hole twice as large as the first.", "그러나 아니었습니다. 처음 것보다 두 배는 큰 구덩이를 팠는데도 아무것도 나오지 않았습니다."], ["I thought I had been deceived—had mistaken the spot.", "제가 속았구나, 자리를 잘못 짚었구나 싶었습니다."], ["I turned around, I looked at the trees, I tried to recall the details which had struck me at the time.", "저는 몸을 돌려 나무들을 바라보며, 그때 제 마음에 새겨졌던 세세한 것들을 떠올리려 애썼습니다."], ["A cold, sharp wind whistled through the leafless branches, and yet the drops fell from my forehead.", "잎 떨어진 가지 사이로 차고 매서운 바람이 휘파람을 불었지만, 그래도 제 이마에서는 땀방울이 떨어졌습니다. 구덩이를 메우려 흙을 밟아 다지던 바로 그 순간에 칼을 맞았던 것이 떠올랐습니다."], ["I recollected that I was stabbed just as I was trampling the ground to fill up the hole; while doing so I had leaned against a laburnum; behind me was an artificial rockery, intended to serve as a resting-place for persons walking in the garden; in falling, my hand, relaxing its hold of the laburnum, felt the coldness of the stone.", "그때 저는 금사슬나무에 몸을 기대고 있었고, 제 뒤로는 정원을 거니는 사람들이 쉬어 가도록 만든 인공 석가산이 있었지요. 쓰러지면서, 금사슬나무를 잡고 있던 손이 잡은 것을 놓치며 돌의 차가움을 느꼈습니다. 제 오른쪽에 나무가 보였고, 제 뒤에는 바위가 있었습니다."], ["On my right I saw the tree, behind me the rock.", "저는 그때와 똑같은 자세로 서서 몸을 던졌지요."], ["I stood in the same attitude, and threw myself down.", "그러고는 일어나 다시 구덩이를 파고 넓히기 시작했습니다."], ["I rose, and again began digging and enlarging the hole; still I found nothing, nothing—the chest was no longer there!” 30269m", "그래도 아무것도, 아무것도 나오지 않았습니다. 상자는 더는 거기 없었던 겁니다!\""]], [["“The chest no longer there?”", "\"상자가 더는 거기 없었다고요?\""], ["murmured Madame Danglars, choking with fear.", "당글라르 부인이 두려움에 목이 메어 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Think not I contented myself with this one effort,” continued Villefort.", "\"제가 그 한 번의 시도로 만족했으리라고는 생각지 마십시오.\""], ["“No; I searched the whole thicket.", "빌포르가 말을 이었다. \"아닙니다."], ["I thought the assassin, having discovered the chest, and supposing it to be a treasure, had intended carrying it off, but, perceiving his error, had dug another hole, and deposited it there; but I could find nothing.", "저는 덤불 전체를 뒤졌습니다. 그 살인자가 상자를 발견하고는 보물인 줄 알고 가져가려다가, 제 착각임을 깨닫고 다른 구덩이를 파 거기 묻어 둔 것이라고 생각했지요. 하지만 아무것도 찾지 못했습니다."], ["Then the idea struck me that he had not taken these precautions, and had simply thrown it in a corner.", "그러다 문득, 그가 그런 수고는 하지 않고 그저 구석에다 던져 두었으리라는 생각이 들었습니다."], ["In the last case I must wait for daylight to renew my search.", "그렇다면 날이 밝기를 기다려 다시 찾아봐야 했지요."], ["I remained in the room and waited.”", "저는 방에 남아 기다렸습니다.\""]], [["“Oh, Heaven!”", "\"오, 하느님!\""]], [["30271m\nWhen daylight dawned I went down again.", "\"날이 밝자 저는 다시 내려갔습니다."], ["My first visit was to the thicket.", "맨 먼저 덤불로 갔지요."], ["I hoped to find some traces which had escaped me in the darkness.", "어둠 속에서 놓쳤던 흔적이라도 찾을 수 있기를 바랐습니다."], ["I had turned up the earth over a surface of more than twenty feet square, and a depth of two feet.", "저는 가로세로 스무 자가 넘는 너비에 두 자 깊이로 땅을 파헤쳐 놓은 터였습니다."], ["A laborer would not have done in a day what occupied me an hour.", "인부라도 하루에 못 했을 일을 저는 한 시간 만에 해치운 것이지요."], ["But I could find nothing—absolutely nothing.", "하지만 아무것도, 정말 아무것도 찾을 수 없었습니다."], ["Then I renewed the search.", "그래서 저는 다시 수색에 나섰습니다."], ["Supposing it had been thrown aside, it would probably be on the path which led to the little gate; but this examination was as useless as the first, and with a bursting heart I returned to the thicket, which now contained no hope for me.”", "한쪽으로 던져졌다면 아마도 작은 쪽문으로 이어지는 길 위에 있으리라 싶었지요. 하지만 이 조사도 처음 것만큼이나 헛수고였고, 저는 터질 듯한 가슴을 안고 덤불로 돌아왔습니다. 이제 그곳에는 제게 아무런 희망도 남아 있지 않았습니다.\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오,\""], ["cried Madame Danglars, “it was enough to drive you mad!”", "당글라르 부인이 외쳤다. \"그건 사람을 미치게 하기에 충분했겠어요!\""]], [["“I hoped for a moment that it might,”", "\"한순간 정말 미쳐 버렸으면 싶었습니다.\""], ["said Villefort; “but that happiness was denied me.", "빌포르가 말했다. \"하지만 제게는 그런 행운조차 허락되지 않았지요."], ["However, recovering my strength and my ideas, ‘Why,’ said I, ‘should that man have carried away the corpse?’”", "그래도 기력과 생각을 추스르고는, '대체 왜 그자가 시신을 가져갔겠는가?' 하고 자문했습니다.\""]], [["“But you said,”", "\"하지만 검사님은,\""], ["replied Madame Danglars, “he would require it as a proof.”", "당글라르 부인이 대답했다. \"그자가 그걸 증거로 쓰려 했을 거라고 하셨잖아요.\""]], [["“Ah, no, madame, that could not be.", "\"아, 아닙니다, 부인. 그럴 리 없습니다."], ["Dead bodies are not kept a year; they are shown to a magistrate, and the evidence is taken.", "시신을 일 년씩 보관하지는 않으니까요. 시신은 판사에게 제시되고 증거 조사가 이루어지는 법입니다."], ["Now, nothing of the kind has happened.”", "그런데 그런 일은 전혀 일어나지 않았습니다.\""]], [["“What then?” asked Hermine, trembling violently.", "\"그렇다면 뭐란 말인가요?\" 에르민이 격하게 떨며 물었다."]], [["“Something more terrible, more fatal, more alarming for us—the child was, perhaps, alive, and the assassin may have saved it!”", "\"더 끔찍하고, 더 치명적이며, 우리에게 더 두려운 일입니다. 그 아이가 어쩌면 살아 있었고, 그 살인자가 아이를 구했을지도 모른다는 것이지요!\""]], [["Madame Danglars uttered a piercing cry, and, seizing Villefort’s hands, exclaimed, “My child was alive?”", "당글라르 부인이 찢어지는 비명을 지르더니, 빌포르의 두 손을 부여잡고 외쳤다. \"제 아이가 살아 있었다고요?\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she; “you buried my child alive?", "\"제 아이를 산 채로 묻으셨단 말인가요?"], ["You were not certain my child was dead, and you buried it? Ah——”", "제 아이가 죽었는지도 확실하지 않은데 묻어 버리셨다고요? 아아——\""]], [["Madame Danglars had risen, and stood before the procureur, whose hands she wrung in her feeble grasp.", "당글라르 부인은 자리에서 일어나 검사 앞에 마주 서서, 힘없는 손으로 그의 두 손을 비틀어 쥐고 있었다."]], [["“I know not; I merely suppose so, as I might suppose anything else,”", "\"저도 모릅니다. 다른 무엇이든 추측하듯, 그저 그렇게 추측할 뿐입니다.\""], ["replied Villefort with a look so fixed, it indicated that his powerful mind was on the verge of despair and madness.", "빌포르가 대답했다. 그 눈길이 어찌나 한곳에 못 박혀 있던지, 그의 강인한 정신이 절망과 광기의 벼랑에 서 있음을 보여 주었다."]], [["“Ah, my child, my poor child!” cried the baroness, falling on her chair, and stifling her sobs in her handkerchief.", "\"아, 내 아이, 가엾은 내 아이!\" 남작 부인이 의자에 쓰러지며 손수건에 흐느낌을 틀어막고 외쳤다."], ["Villefort, becoming somewhat reassured, perceived that to avert the maternal storm gathering over his head, he must inspire Madame Danglars with the terror he felt.", "다소 마음을 추스른 빌포르는, 제 머리 위로 몰려오는 어미의 폭풍을 잠재우려면 자기가 느끼는 공포를 당글라르 부인에게 심어 주어야 한다는 것을 알아차렸다."]], [["“You understand, then, that if it were so,”", "\"그러니 부인도 아시겠지요, 만약 그렇다면,\""], ["said he, rising in his turn, and approaching the baroness, to speak to her in a lower tone, “we are lost.", "그가 이번에는 자기가 일어나 남작 부인에게 다가서며, 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"우리는 끝장입니다."], ["This child lives, and someone knows it lives—someone is in possession of our secret; and since Monte Cristo speaks before us of a child disinterred, when that child could not be found, it is he who is in possession of our secret.”", "그 아이가 살아 있고, 누군가 그 아이가 살아 있음을 알고 있습니다. 누군가 우리의 비밀을 쥐고 있는 겁니다. 그리고 몬테크리스토가 우리 앞에서 파헤쳐진 아이 이야기를 했는데, 정작 그 아이를 찾을 수 없었으니, 우리의 비밀을 쥐고 있는 자는 바로 그입니다.\""]], [["“Just God, avenging God!”", "\"의로우신 하느님, 복수하시는 하느님!\""], ["murmured Madame Danglars.", "당글라르 부인이 중얼거렸다."]], [["Villefort’s only answer was a stifled groan.", "빌포르의 대답은 틀어막은 신음 하나뿐이었다."]], [["“But the child—the child, sir?” repeated the agitated mother.", "\"하지만 그 아이는, 그 아이는 어찌 됐나요, 검사님?\" 동요한 어미가 거듭 물었다."]], [["“How I have searched for him,”", "\"제가 그 아이를 얼마나 찾아 헤맸는지요.\""], ["replied Villefort, wringing his hands; “how I have called him in my long sleepless nights; how I have longed for royal wealth to purchase a million of secrets from a million of men, and to find mine among them!", "빌포르가 두 손을 비틀며 대답했다. \"잠 못 이루는 긴긴밤마다 그 아이를 얼마나 불러 댔는지요. 백만 명의 사람에게서 백만 가지 비밀을 사들여 그 가운데서 제 비밀을 찾아낼 수 있도록, 왕의 재물을 얼마나 갈망했는지요!"], ["At last, one day, when for the hundredth time I took up my spade, I asked myself again and again what the Corsican could have done with the child.", "마침내 어느 날, 백 번째로 삽을 집어 들었을 때, 저는 그 코르시카인이 아이를 어떻게 했을지 거듭거듭 자문했습니다."], ["A child encumbers a fugitive; perhaps, on perceiving it was still alive, he had thrown it into the river.”", "아이는 도망자에게 짐이 됩니다. 어쩌면 아이가 아직 살아 있음을 알아채고는 강물에 던져 버렸을지도 모르지요.\""]], [["“Impossible!”", "\"그럴 리 없어요!\""], ["cried Madame Danglars: “a man may murder another out of revenge, but he would not deliberately drown a child.”", "당글라르 부인이 외쳤다. \"복수심에 다른 사람을 죽일 수는 있어도, 아이를 작정하고 물에 빠뜨려 죽이지는 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“Perhaps,”", "\"어쩌면,\""], ["continued Villefort, “he had put it in the foundling hospital.”", "빌포르가 말을 이었다. \"그자가 아이를 기아 보호원에 맡겼을지도 모릅니다.\""]], [["“Oh, yes, yes,”", "\"오, 그래요, 그래요.\""], ["cried the baroness; “my child is there!”", "남작 부인이 외쳤다. \"내 아이가 거기 있군요!\""]], [["“I ran to the hospital, and learned that the same night—the night of the 20th of September—a child had been brought there, wrapped in part of a fine linen napkin, purposely torn in half.", "\"저는 보호원으로 달려갔고, 바로 그날 밤 — 구월 이십일 밤에 — 한 아이가 그곳에 맡겨졌다는 것을 알게 되었습니다. 일부러 반으로 찢은 고운 아마포 냅킨 조각에 싸여서요."], ["This portion of the napkin was marked with half a baron’s crown, and the letter H.”", "그 냅킨 조각에는 남작 관(冠)의 절반과 H라는 글자가 새겨져 있었습니다.\""]], [["“Truly, truly,”", "\"정말, 정말이에요.\""], ["said Madame Danglars, “all my linen is marked thus; Monsieur de Nargonne was a baron, and my name is Hermine.", "당글라르 부인이 말했다. \"제 아마포에는 죄다 그렇게 새겨져 있어요. 나르곤 씨는 남작이었고, 제 이름은 에르민이니까요."], ["Thank God, my child was not then dead!”", "오, 하느님, 그렇다면 제 아이가 죽지 않았던 거군요!\""]], [["“No, it was not dead.”", "\"그렇습니다, 죽지 않았습니다.\""]], [["“And you can tell me so without fearing to make me die of joy?", "\"그러면서도 제가 기뻐서 죽을까 두려워하지도 않고 그 말씀을 하실 수 있나요?"], ["Where is the child?”", "그 아이는 어디 있나요?\""]], [["Villefort shrugged his shoulders.", "빌포르가 어깨를 으쓱했다."]], [["“Do I know?”", "\"제가 그걸 알겠습니까?\""], ["said he; “and do you believe that if I knew I would relate to you all its trials and all its adventures as would a dramatist or a novel writer?", "그가 말했다. \"제가 안다면, 극작가나 소설가처럼 그 아이가 겪은 온갖 시련과 모험을 부인께 늘어놓을 것 같습니까?"], ["Alas, no, I know not. A woman, about six months after, came to claim it with the other half of the napkin.", "아아, 아닙니다, 저는 모릅니다. 여섯 달쯤 뒤에 한 여인이 나머지 반쪽 냅킨을 들고 그 아이를 찾으러 왔습니다."], ["This woman gave all the requisite particulars, and it was intrusted to her.”", "그 여인은 필요한 사항을 죄다 댔고, 아이는 그 여인에게 맡겨졌지요.\""]], [["“But you should have inquired for the woman; you should have traced her.”", "\"하지만 그 여인을 수소문하셨어야지요. 그 여인의 뒤를 쫓으셨어야지요.\""]], [["“And what do you think I did?", "\"제가 어떻게 했을 것 같습니까?"], ["I feigned a criminal process, and employed all the most acute bloodhounds and skilful agents in search of her.", "저는 형사 사건을 꾸며 내, 가장 예리한 추적견 같은 자들과 가장 노련한 요원들을 죄다 동원해 그 여인을 찾았습니다."], ["They traced her to Châlons, and there they lost her.”", "그들은 샬롱까지 그 여인을 추적했지만, 거기서 놓치고 말았습니다.\""]], [["“They lost her?”", "\"놓쳤다고요?\""]], [["“Yes, forever.”", "\"네, 영영요.\""]], [["Madame Danglars had listened to this recital with a sigh, a tear, or a shriek for every detail.", "당글라르 부인은 이 이야기를 들으며 대목마다 한숨을, 눈물을, 비명을 쏟아 냈다."], ["“And this is all?”", "\"그게 다인가요?\""], ["said she; “and you stopped there?”", "그녀가 말했다. \"거기서 멈추신 거예요?\""]], [["“Oh, no,” said Villefort; “I never ceased to search and to inquire.", "\"오, 아닙니다.\" 빌포르가 말했다."], ["However, the last two or three years I had allowed myself some respite.", "\"저는 찾고 수소문하기를 결코 멈추지 않았습니다. 다만 지난 이삼 년은 제게 다소 휴식을 허락했지요."], ["But now I will begin with more perseverance and fury than ever, since fear urges me, not my conscience.”", "하지만 이제는 그 어느 때보다 끈질기고 맹렬하게 다시 시작할 겁니다. 양심이 아니라 두려움이 저를 내모니까요.\""]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["replied Madame Danglars, “the Count of Monte Cristo can know nothing, or he would not seek our society as he does.”", "당글라르 부인이 대답했다. \"몬테크리스토 백작은 아무것도 모를 거예요. 그렇지 않다면 저렇게 우리와 어울리려 들지 않았겠지요.\""]], [["“Oh, the wickedness of man is very great,”", "\"오, 인간의 사악함은 참으로 큽니다.\""], ["said Villefort, “since it surpasses the goodness of God.", "빌포르가 말했다. \"하느님의 선하심을 능가할 만큼요."], ["Did you observe that man’s eyes while he was speaking to us?”", "그자가 우리에게 말할 때 그 눈을 살펴보셨습니까?\" \"아니요.\""]], [["“No.” “But have you ever watched him carefully?”", "\"하지만 그자를 유심히 지켜본 적이 있으십니까?\""]], [["“Doubtless he is capricious, but that is all; one thing alone struck me,—of all the exquisite things he placed before us, he touched nothing.", "\"변덕스러운 사람인 건 분명하지만, 그게 다예요. 한 가지만은 마음에 걸리더군요. 우리 앞에 차려 낸 그 진귀한 음식들 가운데 어느 것에도 그자가 손을 대지 않았다는 것이요."], ["I might have suspected he was poisoning us.”", "우리를 독살하려는 게 아닐까 의심이 들 정도였지요.\""]], [["“And you see you would have been deceived.”", "\"그러니 부인이 속으셨을 뻔한 거지요.\""]], [["“Yes, doubtless.”", "\"네, 분명히요.\""]], [["“But believe me, that man has other projects.", "\"하지만 제 말을 믿으십시오. 그자에게는 다른 속셈이 있습니다."], ["For that reason I wished to see you, to speak to you, to warn you against everyone, but especially against him. Tell me,”", "바로 그 때문에 제가 부인을 뵙고, 부인과 이야기를 나누고, 모든 사람을, 특히 그자를 경계하시라 일러 드리고 싶었던 겁니다."], ["cried Villefort, fixing his eyes more steadfastly on her than he had ever done before, “did you ever reveal to anyone our connection?”", "말씀해 보십시오.\" 빌포르가 그 어느 때보다 더 굳게 그녀에게 시선을 못 박으며 외쳤다. \"누군가에게 우리 사이를 발설하신 적이 있습니까?\""]], [["“Never, to anyone.”", "\"아무에게도, 한 번도요.\""]], [["“You understand me,”", "\"제 말뜻을 헤아려 주십시오.\""], ["replied Villefort, affectionately; “when I say anyone,—pardon my urgency,—to anyone living I mean?”", "빌포르가 다정하게 말했다. \"제가 누군가라고 할 때는 — 제 다그침을 용서하십시오 — 살아 있는 그 누구에게도 말입니다.\""]], [["“Yes, yes, I understand very well,”", "\"네, 네, 잘 알아들었어요.\""], ["ejaculated the baroness; “never, I swear to you.”", "남작 부인이 내뱉었다. \"한 번도 없어요, 맹세코요.\""]], [["“Were you ever in the habit of writing in the evening what had transpired in the morning?", "\"아침에 있었던 일을 저녁에 적어 두는 버릇이 있으셨습니까?"], ["Do you keep a journal?”", "일기를 쓰십니까?\""]], [["“No, my life has been passed in frivolity; I wish to forget it myself.”", "\"아니요, 제 삶은 덧없는 일들로 흘러갔는걸요. 저 스스로도 그걸 잊고 싶답니다.\""]], [["“Do you talk in your sleep?”", "\"잠꼬대를 하십니까?\""]], [["30275m\n“I sleep soundly, like a child; do you not remember?”", "\"저는 아이처럼 곤히 잠든답니다. 기억 안 나세요?\""]], [["The color mounted to the baroness’s face, and Villefort turned awfully pale.", "남작 부인의 얼굴에 붉은빛이 차올랐고, 빌포르는 무섭도록 창백해졌다."]], [["“It is true,”", "\"그렇지요.\""], ["said he, in so low a tone that he could hardly be heard.", "그가 거의 들리지 않을 만큼 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Well?” said the baroness.", "\"그래서요?\" 남작 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Well, I understand what I now have to do,”", "\"그래서, 이제 제가 무엇을 해야 할지 알겠습니다.\""], ["replied Villefort.", "빌포르가 대답했다."], ["“In less than one week from this time I will ascertain who this M.", "\"지금부터 일주일도 안 되어 저는 알아낼 겁니다."], ["de Monte Cristo is, whence he comes, where he goes, and why he speaks in our presence of children that have been disinterred in a garden.”", "이 몬테크리스토라는 자가 누구인지, 어디서 왔는지, 어디로 가는지, 그리고 어째서 우리 앞에서 정원에 파묻혔던 아이 이야기를 떠벌리는지를요.\""]], [["Villefort pronounced these words with an accent which would have made the count shudder had he heard him.", "빌포르는 이 말들을 어떤 어조로 내뱉었는데, 백작이 그 말을 들었더라면 몸서리를 쳤을 법한 어조였다."], ["Then he pressed the hand the baroness reluctantly gave him, and led her respectfully back to the door.", "그러고는 남작 부인이 마지못해 내미는 손을 잡고, 정중하게 그녀를 문까지 바래다주었다."], ["Madame Danglars returned in another cab to the passage, on the other side of which she found her carriage, and her coachman sleeping peacefully on his box while waiting for her.", "당글라르 부인은 다른 삯마차를 타고 그 통로로 돌아왔고, 통로 반대편에서는 그녀의 마차와, 그녀를 기다리며 마부석에서 평화로이 잠든 마부가 그녀를 맞았다."]], [["# Chapter 68. A Summer Ball", "# Chapter 68. A Summer Ball"]], [["The same day during the interview between Madame Danglars and the procureur, a travelling-carriage entered the Rue du Helder, passed through the gateway of No. 27, and stopped in the yard.", "당글라르 부인과 검사가 만나 이야기를 나누던 바로 그날, 여행용 마차 한 대가 엘데르 거리로 들어와 27번지 대문을 지나 안마당에 멈춰 섰다."], ["In a moment the door was opened, and Madame de Morcerf alighted, leaning on her son’s arm.", "곧 문이 열리더니, 모르세르 부인이 아들의 팔에 기대어 내렸다."], ["Albert soon left her, ordered his horses, and having arranged his toilet, drove to the Champs-Élysées, to the house of Monte Cristo.", "알베르는 이내 어머니 곁을 떠나 말을 준비시키게 하고, 몸단장을 마친 뒤 샹젤리제에 있는 몬테크리스토의 저택으로 마차를 몰았다."]], [["The count received him with his habitual smile.", "백작은 여느 때처럼 미소를 띠고 그를 맞이했다."], ["It was a strange thing that no one ever appeared to advance a step in that man’s favor.", "이상한 일이지만, 그 누구도 이 사내의 호감 속으로 한 걸음도 더 들어서지 못하는 듯했다."], ["Those who would, as it were, force a passage to his heart, found an impassable barrier.", "그의 마음으로 통하는 길을 억지로라도 열려는 사람들은 도무지 넘을 수 없는 장벽에 부딪히곤 했다."], ["Morcerf, who ran towards him with open arms, was chilled as he drew near, in spite of the friendly smile, and simply held out his hand.", "두 팔을 벌리고 달려가던 모르세르도 다정한 미소에도 불구하고 다가갈수록 마음이 식어, 결국은 그저 손을 내밀 뿐이었다."], ["Monte Cristo shook it coldly, according to his invariable practice.", "몬테크리스토는 변함없는 습관대로 그 손을 차갑게 쥐었다."]], [["“Here I am, dear count.”", "\"왔습니다, 친애하는 백작님.\""]], [["“Welcome home again.”", "\"무사히 돌아오셨군요.\""]], [["“I arrived an hour since.”", "\"한 시간 전에 도착했습니다.\""]], [["“From Dieppe?”", "\"디에프에서요?\""]], [["“No, from Tréport.”", "\"아닙니다, 트레포르에서요.\""]], [["“Indeed?”", "\"그렇습니까?\""]], [["“And I have come at once to see you.”", "\"그래서 곧장 백작님을 뵈러 온 겁니다.\""]], [["“That is extremely kind of you,”", "\"정말 친절하시군요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo with a tone of perfect indifference.", "몬테크리스토가 더없이 무심한 어조로 말했다."]], [["“And what is the news?”", "\"무슨 소식이라도 있습니까?\""]], [["“You should not ask a stranger, a foreigner, for news.”", "\"이방인에게, 그것도 외국인에게 소식을 물으시면 안 되지요.\""]], [["“I know it, but in asking for news, I mean, have you done anything for me?”", "\"압니다, 하지만 소식을 묻는다는 건, 그러니까 저를 위해 뭔가 해 주신 게 있느냐는 뜻입니다.\""]], [["“Had you commissioned me?”", "\"제게 뭔가 맡기신 게 있던가요?\""], ["said Monte Cristo, feigning uneasiness.", "몬테크리스토가 곤란한 척하며 말했다."]], [["“Come, come,”", "\"자, 자,\""], ["said Albert, “do not assume so much indifference.", "알베르가 말했다. \"그렇게 시치미 떼지 마십시오."], ["It is said, sympathy travels rapidly, and when at Tréport, I felt the electric shock; you have either been working for me or thinking of me.”", "사람들이 그러더군요, 마음이 통하는 데에는 빠른 법이라고요. 트레포르에 있을 때 저는 전기에 닿은 듯한 충격을 느꼈습니다. 백작님이 저를 위해 일하고 계셨거나, 아니면 저를 생각하고 계셨던 거지요.\""]], [["“Possibly,”", "\"그럴지도 모르지요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “I have indeed thought of you, but the magnetic wire I was guiding acted, indeed, without my knowledge.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"사실 당신을 생각하긴 했습니다. 하지만 제가 다루던 그 자기를 띤 전선은, 정말이지 제가 모르는 사이에 작동했답니다.\""]], [["30277m\n“Indeed! Pray tell me how it happened.”", "\"정말요? 어떻게 된 일인지 어서 말씀해 주십시오.\""]], [["“Willingly. M.", "\"기꺼이 말씀드리지요."], ["Danglars dined with me.”", "당글라르 씨가 저와 함께 저녁을 들었습니다.\""]], [["“I know it; to avoid meeting him, my mother and I left town.”", "\"압니다. 그 사람과 마주치지 않으려고 어머니와 제가 도시를 떠났던 거니까요.\""]], [["“But he met here M. Andrea Cavalcanti.”", "\"그런데 그가 여기서 안드레아 카발칸티 씨와 만났습니다.\""]], [["“Your Italian prince?”", "\"그 이탈리아 왕자 말입니까?\""]], [["“Not so fast; M.", "\"그렇게 서두르지 마십시오."], ["Andrea only calls himself count.”", "안드레아 씨는 그저 자신을 백작이라 칭할 뿐입니다.\""]], [["“Calls himself, do you say?”", "\"칭한다고요?\""]], [["“Yes, calls himself.”", "\"네, 칭한다는 말입니다.\""]], [["“Is he not a count?”", "\"백작이 아니란 말입니까?\""]], [["“What can I know of him?", "\"제가 그에 대해 무얼 알겠습니까?"], ["He calls himself so. I, of course, give him the same title, and everyone else does likewise.”", "자신이 그렇게 부르니, 저도 당연히 같은 칭호로 부르고, 다른 사람들도 다 그렇게 하지요.\""]], [["“What a strange man you are! What next?", "\"참 이상한 분이시군요! 그래서 다음은요?"], ["You say M. Danglars dined here?”", "당글라르 씨가 여기서 저녁을 들었다고요?\""]], [["“Yes, with Count Cavalcanti, the marquis his father, Madame Danglars, M. and Madame de Villefort,—charming people,—M. Debray, Maximilian Morrel, and M. de Château-Renaud.”", "\"네, 카발칸티 백작과 그 아버지인 후작, 당글라르 부인, 빌포르 씨 부부 — 매력적인 분들이지요 — 그리고 드브레 씨, 막시밀리앙 모렐, 샤토르노 씨와 함께요.\""]], [["“Did they speak of me?”", "\"제 이야기를 했습니까?\""]], [["“Not a word.”", "\"한마디도 안 했습니다.\""]], [["“So much the worse.” “Why so?", "\"그럼 더 나쁘군요.\" \"왜 그렇습니까?"], ["I thought you wished them to forget you?”", "저는 그 사람들이 당신을 잊어 주기를 바라는 줄 알았는데요?\""]], [["“If they did not speak of me, I am sure they thought about me, and I am in despair.”", "\"그들이 제 이야기를 하지 않았다면, 분명 속으로는 저를 생각했을 테니, 절망스럽지요.\""]], [["“How will that affect you, since Mademoiselle Danglars was not among the number here who thought of you? Truly, she might have thought of you at home.”", "\"당글라르 양은 당신을 생각한 사람들 축에 끼지도 않았는데, 그게 무슨 상관입니까? 하기야, 그 아가씨라면 집에서 당신을 생각했을지도 모르겠군요.\""]], [["“I have no fear of that; or, if she did, it was only in the same way in which I think of her.”", "\"그런 걱정은 안 합니다. 설령 그랬다 해도, 제가 그녀를 생각하는 딱 그 정도였겠지요.\""]], [["“Touching sympathy!", "\"가슴 뭉클한 공감이군요!"], ["So you hate each other?” said the count.", "그러니까 두 분이 서로 미워한다는 말씀입니까?\" 백작이 말했다."]], [["“Listen,”", "\"들어 보십시오.\""], ["said Morcerf—“if Mademoiselle Danglars were disposed to take pity on my supposed martyrdom on her account, and would dispense with all matrimonial formalities between our two families, I am ready to agree to the arrangement.", "모르세르가 말했다. \"만일 당글라르 양이 저 때문에 순교라도 하는 양 여겨지는 제 처지를 가엾이 여겨, 우리 두 집안 사이의 혼인 절차를 죄다 없던 일로 해 준다면, 저는 기꺼이 그 합의에 응하겠습니다."], ["In a word, Mademoiselle Danglars would make a charming mistress—but a wife—_diable!_”", "한마디로, 당글라르 양은 매혹적인 정부는 될 수 있어도, 아내라니 — 천만에요!\""]], [["“And this,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “is your opinion of your intended spouse?”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그게 당신의 정혼자에 대한 생각이란 말입니까?\""]], [["“Yes; it is rather unkind, I acknowledge, but it is true.", "\"네. 좀 야박한 말이라는 건 인정합니다만, 사실인걸요."], ["But as this dream cannot be realized, since Mademoiselle Danglars must become my lawful wife, live perpetually with me, sing to me, compose verses and music within ten paces of me, and that for my whole life, it frightens me.", "그러나 이 꿈은 이루어질 수 없으니, 당글라르 양은 제 정식 아내가 되어, 저와 평생을 함께 살며, 제게 노래를 들려주고, 제게서 열 걸음 떨어진 곳에서 시를 짓고 곡을 붙이겠지요. 그것도 제 평생토록요. 그러니 두렵습니다."], ["One may forsake a mistress, but a wife,—good heavens!", "정부야 버릴 수 있지만, 아내라니 — 맙소사!"], ["There she must always be; and to marry Mademoiselle Danglars would be awful.”", "그 여자는 늘 곁에 있어야 하는 겁니다. 당글라르 양과 결혼한다는 건 끔찍한 일이지요.\""]], [["“You are difficult to please, viscount.”", "\"여간 까다로운 분이 아니시군요, 자작.\""]], [["“Yes, for I often wish for what is impossible.”", "\"네, 저는 곧잘 불가능한 것을 바라니까요.\""]], [["“What is that?”", "\"그게 무엇입니까?\""]], [["“To find such a wife as my father found.”", "\"제 아버지가 찾으셨던 것 같은 아내를 만나는 일이지요.\""]], [["Monte Cristo turned pale, and looked at Albert, while playing with some magnificent pistols.", "몬테크리스토는 얼굴이 창백해지더니, 멋진 권총 몇 자루를 만지작거리며 알베르를 바라보았다."]], [["“Your father was fortunate, then?”", "\"그럼 당신 아버님은 운이 좋으셨군요?\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“You know my opinion of my mother, count; look at her,—still beautiful, witty, more charming than ever.", "\"제가 어머니를 어떻게 생각하는지 아시지 않습니까, 백작님. 어머니를 보십시오."], ["For any other son to have stayed with his mother for four days at Tréport, it would have been a condescension or a martyrdom, while I return, more contented, more peaceful—shall I say more poetic!—than if I had taken Queen Mab or Titania as my companion.”", "여전히 아름답고, 재치 있고, 그 어느 때보다 매력적이지요. 다른 아들이라면 트레포르에서 나흘이나 어머니와 함께 지내는 게 큰 양보거나 고역이었을 텐데, 저는 더 만족스럽고 더 평온한 마음으로 — 더 시적인 기분이라고 해야 할까요! — 돌아왔습니다."], ["30279m", "마치 마브 여왕이나 티타니아를 길동무로 삼았던 것보다 더요.\""]], [["“That is an overwhelming demonstration, and you would make everyone vow to live a single life.”", "\"참으로 압도적인 증명이군요. 그렇게 말씀하시면 누구나 독신으로 살겠노라 맹세하겠습니다.\""]], [["“Such are my reasons for not liking to marry Mademoiselle Danglars.", "\"제가 당글라르 양과 결혼하기 싫은 까닭이 바로 그겁니다."], ["Have you ever noticed how much a thing is heightened in value when we obtain possession of it?", "무언가를 손에 넣는 순간 그 값어치가 얼마나 높아지는지 눈여겨보신 적 있습니까?"], ["The diamond which glittered in the window at Marlé’s or Fossin’s shines with more splendor when it is our own; but if we are compelled to acknowledge the superiority of another, and still must retain the one that is inferior, do you not know what we have to endure?”", "마를레나 포생의 진열창에서 반짝이던 다이아몬드도 막상 제 것이 되면 훨씬 더 찬란하게 빛나는 법이지요. 그러나 다른 것이 더 낫다는 걸 인정할 수밖에 없으면서도, 더 못한 쪽을 계속 지녀야 한다면, 그게 얼마나 견디기 힘든 일인지 아십니까?\""]], [["“Worldling,”", "\"속물이로군.\""], ["murmured the count.", "백작이 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Thus I shall rejoice when Mademoiselle Eugénie perceives I am but a pitiful atom, with scarcely as many hundred thousand francs as she has millions.”", "\"그러니 외제니 양이 저를 그저 보잘것없는 티끌만 한 존재로, 그녀가 가진 수백만 프랑에 견주면 수십만 프랑도 못 가진 자로 여기게 되는 날이 오면, 저는 오히려 기뻐할 겁니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 미소 지었다."], ["Monte Cristo smiled. “One plan occurred to me,”", "\"한 가지 계획이 떠올랐지요.\" 알베르가 말을 이었다."], ["continued Albert; “Franz likes all that is eccentric; I tried to make him fall in love with Mademoiselle Danglars; but in spite of four letters, written in the most alluring style, he invariably answered: ‘My eccentricity may be great, but it will not make me break my promise.’”", "\"프란츠는 별난 것이라면 무엇이든 좋아하니까, 그를 당글라르 양과 사랑에 빠지게 만들어 보려 했습니다. 그래서 더없이 매혹적인 문체로 편지를 네 통이나 보냈는데, 그는 한결같이 이렇게 답하더군요. '내 별난 취향이 아무리 대단해도, 그것 때문에 약속을 깨지는 않겠네.'\""]], [["“That is what I call devoted friendship, to recommend to another one whom you would not marry yourself.”", "\"그게 바로 제가 말하는 헌신적인 우정이지요. 자기는 결혼하기 싫은 여자를 남에게 권하는 것 말입니다.\""], ["Albert smiled.", "알베르가 미소 지었다."]], [["“Apropos,”", "\"그건 그렇고,\""], ["continued he, “Franz is coming soon, but it will not interest you; you dislike him, I think?”", "그가 말을 이었다. \"프란츠가 곧 옵니다만, 당신에겐 관심 없는 일이겠지요. 백작님은 그를 싫어하시는 것 같으니까요?\""]], [["“I?”", "\"제가요?\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “my dear viscount, how have you discovered that I did not like M.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"친애하는 자작, 제가 프란츠 씨를 싫어한다는 걸 어떻게 알아내셨습니까?"], ["Franz! I like everyone.”", "저는 누구나 다 좋아합니다.\""]], [["“And you include me in the expression everyone—many thanks!”", "\"그 '누구나'에 저도 들어가는 거지요 — 정말 감사하군요!\""]], [["“Let us not mistake,”", "\"오해는 마십시오.\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “I love everyone as God commands us to love our neighbor, as Christians; but I thoroughly hate but a few.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"저는 하느님께서 명하신 대로, 그리스도인으로서 이웃을 사랑하듯 모두를 사랑합니다. 하지만 진심으로 미워하는 사람도 몇은 있지요."], ["Let us return to M. Franz d’Épinay.", "다시 프란츠 데피네 씨 이야기로 돌아갑시다."], ["Did you say he was coming?”", "그가 온다고 하셨나요?\""]], [["“Yes; summoned by M.", "\"네. 빌포르 씨가 불렀습니다."], ["de Villefort, who is apparently as anxious to get Mademoiselle Valentine married as M. Danglars is to see Mademoiselle Eugénie settled.", "당글라르 씨가 외제니 양을 시집보내고 싶어 안달하는 만큼이나, 그분도 발랑틴 양을 결혼시키고 싶어 안달이 난 모양입니다."], ["It must be a very irksome office to be the father of a grown-up daughter; it seems to make one feverish, and to raise one’s pulse to ninety beats a minute until the deed is done.”", "다 큰 딸을 둔 아버지 노릇이란 참 성가신 일인가 봅니다. 그 일을 마칠 때까지는 열에 들뜬 사람처럼 안절부절못하고, 맥박이 분당 아흔까지 뛰는 듯하니 말입니다.\""]], [["“But M. d’Épinay, unlike you, bears his misfortune patiently.”", "\"하지만 데피네 씨는 당신과 달리 그 불운을 묵묵히 견디는군요.\""]], [["“Still more, he talks seriously about the matter, puts on a white tie, and speaks of his family.", "\"그뿐이 아닙니다. 그는 그 일을 진지하게 이야기하고, 흰 넥타이를 매고, 자기 집안에 대해 늘어놓지요."], ["He entertains a very high opinion of M. and Madame de Villefort.”", "빌포르 씨 부부를 아주 높이 평가하고 있습니다.\""]], [["“Which they deserve, do they not?”", "\"그분들이 그럴 만하니까 그렇겠지요, 안 그렇습니까?\""]], [["“I believe they do. M.", "\"그럴 만하다고 봅니다."], ["de Villefort has always passed for a severe but a just man.”", "빌포르 씨는 늘 엄격하지만 공정한 사람으로 통해 왔으니까요.\""]], [["“There is, then, one,”", "\"그렇다면,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["said Monte Cristo, “whom you do not condemn like poor Danglars?”", "\"가엾은 당글라르처럼 단죄하지 않는 사람도 하나쯤은 있는 셈이군요?\""]], [["“Because I am not compelled to marry his daughter perhaps,”", "\"아마 제가 그분 딸과 결혼할 처지가 아니라서겠지요.\""], ["replied Albert, laughing.", "알베르가 웃으며 대답했다."]], [["“Indeed, my dear sir,”", "\"이런, 자작,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["said Monte Cristo, “you are revoltingly foppish.”", "\"정말이지 역겨울 만큼 멋이나 부리는 한량이시군요.\""]], [["“I foppish?", "\"제가 한량이라고요?"], ["how do you mean?”", "그게 무슨 말씀입니까?\""]], [["“Yes; pray take a cigar, and cease to defend yourself, and to struggle to escape marrying Mademoiselle Danglars.", "\"그렇습니다. 자, 시가나 한 대 피우시고, 그만 변명을 늘어놓으며 당글라르 양과의 결혼에서 벗어나려고 발버둥 치는 것도 그치시지요."], ["Let things take their course; perhaps you may not have to retract.”", "흘러가는 대로 두십시오. 어쩌면 그 말을 다시 주워 담을 필요가 없어질지도 모르니까요.\""]], [["“Bah!”", "\"천만에요!\""], ["said Albert, staring.", "알베르가 눈을 동그랗게 뜨고 말했다."]], [["“Doubtless, my dear viscount, you will not be taken by force; and seriously, do you wish to break off your engagement?”", "\"틀림없이, 친애하는 자작, 당신을 억지로 끌고 가지는 않을 겁니다. 진지하게 묻는데, 그 약혼을 깨고 싶으십니까?\""]], [["“I would give a hundred thousand francs to be able to do so.”", "\"그럴 수만 있다면 십만 프랑이라도 내놓겠습니다.\""]], [["“Then make yourself quite easy. M.", "\"그렇다면 마음 푹 놓으십시오."], ["Danglars would give double that sum to attain the same end.”", "당글라르 씨는 같은 목적을 이루기 위해 그 곱절이라도 내놓을 테니까요.\""]], [["“Am I, indeed, so happy?”", "\"제가 정말 그렇게 운이 좋단 말입니까?\""], ["said Albert, who still could not prevent an almost imperceptible cloud passing across his brow.", "알베르가 말했다. 그러면서도 거의 알아챌 수 없는 그늘이 그의 이마를 스치는 것은 어쩔 수 없었다."], ["“But, my dear count, has M. Danglars any reason?”", "\"하지만 친애하는 백작님, 당글라르 씨에게 무슨 까닭이라도 있습니까?\""]], [["“Ah! there is your proud and selfish nature.", "\"아! 그게 바로 당신의 오만하고 이기적인 천성이지요."], ["You would expose the self-love of another with a hatchet, but you shrink if your own is attacked with a needle.”", "남의 자존심은 도끼로 내려쳐 까발리면서, 정작 당신 것이 바늘로 살짝만 찔려도 움츠러드는군요.\""]], [["“But yet, M. Danglars appeared——”", "\"하지만 그래도 당글라르 씨는……\""]], [["“Delighted with you, was he not?", "\"당신을 마음에 들어 했다, 그 말이지요?"], ["Well, he is a man of bad taste, and is still more enchanted with another.", "글쎄요, 그 사람은 취향이 형편없는 자라, 이제는 다른 사람에게 더 홀딱 빠져 있답니다."], ["I know not whom; look and judge for yourself.”", "누구인지는 저도 모릅니다. 직접 보시고 판단하시지요.\""]], [["“Thank you, I understand.", "\"고맙습니다, 알겠습니다."], ["But my mother—no, not my mother; I mistake—my father intends giving a ball.”", "그런데 제 어머니가 — 아니, 어머니가 아니라, 제가 잘못 말했군요 — 아버지가 무도회를 여실 작정입니다.\""]], [["“A ball at this season?”", "\"이런 철에 무도회를요?\""]], [["“Summer balls are fashionable.”", "\"여름 무도회가 유행이거든요.\""]], [["“If they were not, the countess has only to wish it, and they would become so.”", "\"설령 유행이 아니더라도, 백작 부인께서 바라기만 하시면 곧 유행이 되겠지요.\""]], [["“You are right; You know they are select affairs; those who remain in Paris in July must be true Parisians.", "\"옳은 말씀입니다. 아시다시피 이건 가려 뽑힌 사람들만 모이는 자리지요. 칠월에 파리에 남아 있는 사람이라면 진짜 파리지앵일 테니까요."], ["Will you take charge of our invitation to Messieurs Cavalcanti?”", "카발칸티 부자에게 보낼 초대를 맡아 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“When will it take place?”", "\"언제 열립니까?\""]], [["“On Saturday.”", "\"토요일입니다.\""]], [["“M. Cavalcanti’s father will be gone.”", "\"카발칸티 씨의 아버지는 그때 떠나고 없을 겁니다.\""]], [["“But the son will be here; will you invite young M. Cavalcanti?”", "\"하지만 아들은 여기 있겠지요. 젊은 카발칸티 씨를 초대해 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“I do not know him, viscount.”", "\"저는 그를 모릅니다, 자작.\""]], [["“You do not know him?”", "\"모르신다고요?\""]], [["“No, I never saw him until a few days since, and am not responsible for him.”", "\"네, 며칠 전에야 처음 봤을 뿐이고, 그에 대해 책임질 수 있는 입장도 아닙니다.\""]], [["“But you receive him at your house?”", "\"하지만 댁에서 그를 맞이하시지 않습니까?\""]], [["“That is another thing: he was recommended to me by a good abbé, who may be deceived.", "\"그건 또 다른 문제입니다. 어느 훌륭한 신부님이 제게 추천하신 사람인데, 그 신부님도 속았을 수 있지요."], ["Give him a direct invitation, but do not ask me to present him.", "직접 초대장을 보내시되, 제게 그를 소개해 달라고는 마십시오."], ["If he were afterwards to marry Mademoiselle Danglars, you would accuse me of intrigue, and would be challenging me,—besides, I may not be there myself.”", "만일 나중에 그가 당글라르 양과 결혼이라도 하게 되면, 당신은 제가 농간을 부렸다고 책망하며 결투를 청할 테니까요. 게다가 저는 거기 가지 못할 수도 있고요.\""]], [["“Where?”", "\"어디서요?\""], ["“At your ball.”", "\"당신의 무도회 말입니다.\""]], [["“Why should you not be there?”", "\"왜 못 오신다는 겁니까?\""]], [["“Because you have not yet invited me.”", "\"아직 저를 초대하지 않으셨으니까요.\""]], [["“But I come expressly for that purpose.”", "\"바로 그 일로 일부러 온 겁니다.\""]], [["“You are very kind, but I may be prevented.”", "\"참 친절하시군요. 하지만 사정이 생길지도 모르지요.\""]], [["“If I tell you one thing, you will be so amiable as to set aside all impediments.”", "\"한 가지만 말씀드리면, 어떤 핑계든 다 제쳐 두실 만큼 너그러워지실 텐데요.\""]], [["“Tell me what it is.”", "\"그게 무엇인지 말씀해 보십시오.\""]], [["“My mother begs you to come.”", "\"어머니께서 꼭 와 주십사 청하셨습니다.\""]], [["“The Comtesse de Morcerf?”", "\"모르세르 백작 부인께서요?\""], ["said Monte Cristo, starting.", "몬테크리스토가 흠칫하며 말했다."]], [["“Ah, count,”", "\"아, 백작님,\" 알베르가 말했다."], ["said Albert, “I assure you Madame de Morcerf speaks freely to me, and if you have not felt those sympathetic fibres of which I spoke just now thrill within you, you must be entirely devoid of them, for during the last four days we have spoken of no one else.”", "\"정말이지 어머니는 제게 거리낌 없이 속을 털어놓으시는 분입니다. 만일 백작님이 아까 제가 말씀드린 그 공감의 가느다란 실이 마음속에서 떨리는 걸 느끼지 못하셨다면, 백작님께는 그런 게 아예 없는 거나 마찬가지일 겁니다. 지난 나흘 동안 우리는 오로지 백작님 이야기만 했으니까요.\""]], [["“You have talked of me?”", "\"제 이야기를 하셨다고요?\""]], [["“Yes, that is the penalty of being a living puzzle!”", "\"네, 그게 살아 있는 수수께끼가 치러야 할 대가지요!\""]], [["“Then I am also a puzzle to your mother?", "\"그럼 제가 당신 어머님께도 수수께끼란 말입니까?"], ["I should have thought her too reasonable to be led by imagination.”", "저는 그분이 너무도 이성적인 분이라 상상에 휘둘리지 않으실 줄 알았는데요.\""]], [["“A problem, my dear count, for everyone—for my mother as well as others; much studied, but not solved, you still remain an enigma, do not fear.", "\"수수께끼지요, 친애하는 백작님. 누구에게나요 — 제 어머니에게도, 다른 이들에게도 마찬가지입니다. 다들 골똘히 연구해도 풀지 못하니, 당신은 여전히 풀리지 않은 수수께끼로 남아 있습니다."], ["My mother is only astonished that you remain so long unsolved.", "걱정 마십시오. 어머니는 그저 당신이 이토록 오래 풀리지 않는다는 데 놀라실 뿐입니다."], ["I believe, while the Countess G—— takes you for Lord Ruthven, my mother imagines you to be Cagliostro or the Count Saint-Germain.", "G 백작 부인은 당신을 루스벤 경으로 여기지만, 어머니는 당신을 칼리오스트로나 생제르맹 백작쯤으로 상상하시는 것 같습니다."], ["The first opportunity you have, confirm her in her opinion; it will be easy for you, as you have the philosophy of the one and the wit of the other.”", "기회가 닿거든 그 생각을 굳혀 드리십시오. 당신에게는 그쯤은 식은 죽 먹기일 겁니다. 한 사람의 철학과 다른 사람의 재치를 두루 갖추셨으니까요.\""]], [["“I thank you for the warning,”", "\"일러 주셔서 감사합니다.\""], ["said the count; “I shall endeavor to be prepared for all suppositions.”", "백작이 말했다. \"어떤 추측에든 대비하도록 애써 보지요.\""]], [["“You will, then, come on Saturday?”", "\"그럼 토요일에 오시는 거지요?\""]], [["“Yes, since Madame de Morcerf invites me.”", "\"네, 모르세르 부인께서 초대하셨으니까요.\""]], [["“You are very kind.”", "\"참 친절하시군요.\""]], [["“Will M. Danglars be there?”", "\"당글라르 씨도 옵니까?\""]], [["“He has already been invited by my father.", "\"아버지가 벌써 초대하셨습니다."], ["We shall try to persuade the great d’Aguesseau,11 M. de Villefort, to come, but have not much hope of seeing him.”", "그 위대한 다게소 같은 빌포르 씨도 모셔 보려 애쓸 작정입니다만, 와 주실지는 별로 기대하지 않습니다.\""]], [["“‘Never despair of anything,’ says the proverb.”", "\"'무슨 일이든 결코 단념하지 말라'는 속담도 있지 않습니까.\""]], [["“Do you dance, count?”", "\"백작님은 춤추십니까?\""]], [["“I dance?”", "\"제가 춤을요?\""]], [["“Yes, you; it would not be astonishing.”", "\"네, 백작님 말입니다. 그래도 이상할 거야 없지요.\""]], [["“That is very well before one is over forty.", "\"마흔이 넘기 전이라면 모를까요."], ["No, I do not dance, but I like to see others do so.", "아니요, 저는 춤을 추지 않습니다. 다만 남이 추는 걸 보는 건 좋아하지요."], ["Does Madame de Morcerf dance?”", "모르세르 부인은 춤을 추십니까?\""]], [["“Never; you can talk to her, she so delights in your conversation.”", "\"전혀 안 추십니다. 어머니와 이야기를 나누시면 됩니다. 어머니는 백작님과의 대화를 무척 즐기시니까요.\""]], [["“Indeed?” 30283m", "\"정말입니까?\""]], [["“Yes, truly; and I assure you.", "\"네, 정말입니다. 장담하지요."], ["You are the only man of whom I have heard her speak with interest.”", "어머니가 그토록 관심을 가지고 이야기하시는 분은 백작님 말고는 없습니다.\""], ["Albert rose and took his hat; the count conducted him to the door.", "알베르가 일어나 모자를 집어 들자, 백작은 그를 문까지 배웅했다."]], [["“I have one thing to reproach myself with,”", "\"한 가지 제 자신을 나무랄 일이 있군요.\""], ["said he, stopping Albert on the steps.", "백작이 계단에서 알베르를 붙잡으며 말했다."], ["“What is it?”", "\"그게 뭡니까?\""]], [["“I have spoken to you indiscreetly about Danglars.”", "\"제가 당글라르에 대해 경솔하게 말했군요.\""]], [["“On the contrary, speak to me always in the same strain about him.”", "\"천만에요, 그에 대해서라면 늘 그런 식으로 말씀해 주십시오.\""]], [["“I am glad to be reassured on that point.", "\"그 점에 대해 안심하게 되어 기쁩니다."], ["Apropos, when do you aspect M. d’Épinay?”", "그건 그렇고, 데피네 씨는 언제쯤 오실 것 같습니까?\""]], [["“Five or six days hence at the latest.”", "\"늦어도 닷새나 엿새 뒤일 겁니다.\""]], [["“And when is he to be married?”", "\"결혼은 언제 합니까?\""]], [["“Immediately on the arrival of M. and Madame de Saint-Méran.”", "\"생메랑 씨 부부가 도착하는 대로 곧바로요.\""]], [["“Bring him to see me.", "\"제게 그를 데려와 주십시오."], ["Although you say I do not like him, I assure you I shall be happy to see him.”", "제가 그를 좋아하지 않는다고 하셨지만, 정말이지 그를 만나면 반가울 겁니다.\""]], [["“I will obey your orders, my lord.”", "\"분부대로 하겠습니다, 백작님.\""]], [["“Until Saturday, when I may expect you, may I not?”", "\"그럼 안녕히 가십시오.\" \"토요일에 뵙는 거지요? 기다려도 되겠지요?\""]], [["“Yes, I promised you.”", "\"네, 약속했으니까요.\""], ["The Count watched Albert, waving his hand to him.", "백작은 손을 흔드는 알베르를 지켜보았다."], ["When he had mounted his phaeton, Monte Cristo turned, and seeing Bertuccio, “What news?” said he.", "알베르가 사륜마차에 오르자, 몬테크리스토는 몸을 돌리다 베르투치오를 보고 말했다. \"무슨 소식이라도?\""]], [["“She went to the Palais,” replied the steward.", "\"부인께서 사법청에 가셨습니다.\" 집사가 대답했다."]], [["“Did she stay long there?”", "\"오래 머물렀나?\" \"한 시간 반쯤요.\""]], [["“An hour and a half.”", "\"집으로 돌아갔고?\""]], [["“Did she return home?”", "\"곧장 돌아가셨습니다.\""]], [["“Directly.” “Well, my dear Bertuccio,”", "\"좋아, 친애하는 베르투치오,\""], ["said the count, “I now advise you to go in quest of the little estate I spoke to you of in Normandy.”", "백작이 말했다. \"이제 자네에게 일러두는데, 전에 말했던 노르망디의 그 작은 영지를 알아보러 가게.\""]], [["Bertuccio bowed, and as his wishes were in perfect harmony with the order he had received, he started the same evening.", "베르투치오는 고개를 숙였다. 받은 명령이 자기 바람과 더없이 잘 맞아떨어졌으므로, 그는 그날 저녁으로 곧장 길을 떠났다."]], [["# Chapter 69. The Inquiry", "# Chapter 69. The Inquiry"]], [["M. de Villefort kept the promise he had made to Madame Danglars, to endeavor to find out how the Count of Monte Cristo had discovered the history of the house at Auteuil.", "빌포르 씨는 당글라르 부인에게 한 약속, 곧 몬테크리스토 백작이 어떻게 오퇴유 저택의 내력을 알아냈는지 밝혀 보겠다는 약속을 지켰다."], ["He wrote the same day for the required information to M.", "그는 그날로 필요한 정보를 얻기 위해 보빌 씨에게 편지를 썼다."], ["de Boville, who, from having been an inspector of prisons, was promoted to a high office in the police; and the latter begged for two days time to ascertain exactly who would be most likely to give him full particulars.", "보빌 씨는 형무소 감독관이었다가 경찰의 고위직으로 승진한 인물이었다. 보빌 씨는 누가 가장 자세한 사정을 알려 줄 만한지 정확히 알아보겠다며 이틀의 여유를 청했다."], ["At the end of the second day M. de Villefort received the following note:", "이틀이 지났을 때 빌포르 씨는 다음과 같은 쪽지를 받았다."]], [["“The person called the Count of Monte Cristo is an intimate acquaintance of Lord Wilmore, a rich foreigner, who is sometimes seen in Paris and who is there at this moment; he is also known to the Abbé Busoni, a Sicilian priest, of high repute in the East, where he has done much good.”", "\"몬테크리스토 백작이라 불리는 인물은 윌모어 경과 막역한 사이입니다. 윌모어 경은 부유한 외국인으로 이따금 파리에서 모습을 보이는데, 지금도 파리에 있습니다. 그는 또 부소니 신부와도 아는 사이입니다. 부소니 신부는 시칠리아 출신 사제로, 동방에서 많은 선행을 베풀어 명망이 높습니다.\""]], [["M. de Villefort replied by ordering the strictest inquiries to be made respecting these two persons; his orders were executed, and the following evening he received these details:", "빌포르 씨는 이 두 사람에 관해 가장 철저한 조사를 하라는 지시로 답했다. 명령은 그대로 이행되었고, 이튿날 저녁 그는 다음과 같은 상세한 보고를 받았다."]], [["“The abbé, who was in Paris only for a month, inhabited a small two-storied house behind Saint-Sulpice; there were two rooms on each floor and he was the only tenant.", "\"신부는 파리에 머문 지 한 달밖에 되지 않았으며, 생쉴피스 성당 뒤편의 작은 이층집에 살고 있습니다. 층마다 방이 둘씩 있는데, 세입자는 그 한 사람뿐입니다."], ["The two lower rooms consisted of a dining-room, with a table, chairs, and side-board of walnut, and a wainscoted parlor, without ornaments, carpet, or timepiece.", "아래층 두 방은 호두나무로 만든 식탁과 의자, 찬장을 갖춘 식당과, 장식도 양탄자도 시계도 없는 징두리널을 두른 응접실로 이루어져 있습니다."], ["It was evident that the abbé limited himself to objects of strict necessity.", "신부가 꼭 필요한 물건에만 자신을 한정하고 있음이 분명합니다."], ["He preferred to use the sitting-room upstairs, which was more library than parlor, and was furnished with theological books and parchments, in which he delighted to bury himself for months at a time, according to his valet de chambre.", "그는 위층의 거실을 즐겨 쓰는데, 그곳은 응접실이라기보다 서재에 가까웠습니다. 신학 서적과 양피지 문서들로 채워져 있었고, 시종의 말로는 신부가 몇 달씩 그 안에 파묻혀 지내기를 즐긴다고 합니다."], ["His valet looked at the visitors through a sort of wicket; and if their faces were unknown to him or displeased him, he replied that the abbé was not in Paris, an answer which satisfied most persons, because the abbé was known to be a great traveller.", "시종은 일종의 쪽문을 통해 방문객을 살피는데, 낯이 익지 않거나 마음에 들지 않는 얼굴이면 신부는 파리에 없다고 답합니다. 신부가 대단한 여행가로 알려진 까닭에, 그 대답에 대부분의 사람들이 수긍합니다."], ["Besides, whether at home or not, whether in Paris or Cairo, the abbé always left something to give away, which the valet distributed through this wicket in his master’s name.", "게다가 집에 있든 없든, 파리에 있든 카이로에 있든, 신부는 늘 나누어 줄 무언가를 남겨 두며, 시종이 그 쪽문을 통해 주인의 이름으로 그것을 나누어 줍니다."], ["The other room near the library was a bedroom.", "서재 옆의 다른 방은 침실입니다."], ["A bed without curtains, four armchairs, and a couch, covered with yellow Utrecht velvet, composed, with a _prie-Dieu_, all its furniture.", "휘장 없는 침대 하나와 안락의자 넷, 노란 위트레흐트 벨벳을 씌운 긴 의자 하나가 기도대 하나와 더불어 가구의 전부였습니다."]], [["“Lord Wilmore resided in Rue Fontaine-Saint-Georges.", "\"윌모어 경은 퐁텐생조르주 거리에 거주합니다."], ["He was one of those English tourists who consume a large fortune in travelling.", "그는 막대한 재산을 여행으로 탕진하는 영국 관광객 가운데 하나입니다."], ["He hired the apartment in which he lived furnished, passed only a few hours in the day there, and rarely slept there.", "그가 사는 집은 가구가 딸린 채로 빌린 것이며, 하루 중 몇 시간만 그곳에서 보낼 뿐 거기서 잠을 자는 일은 드뭅니다."], ["One of his peculiarities was never to speak a word of French, which he however wrote with great facility.”", "그의 특이한 버릇 하나는 프랑스어를 단 한마디도 입에 올리지 않는 것입니다. 다만 글로는 아주 유창하게 씁니다.\""]], [["The day after this important information had been given to the king’s attorney, a man alighted from a carriage at the corner of the Rue Férou, and rapping at an olive-green door, asked if the Abbé Busoni were within.", "이 중요한 정보가 검사에게 전해진 이튿날, 한 사내가 페루 거리 모퉁이에서 마차에서 내렸다. 그는 올리브색 문을 두드리며 부소니 신부가 안에 있느냐고 물었다."]], [["“No, he went out early this morning,”", "\"안 계십니다. 오늘 아침 일찍 나가셨습니다.\""], ["replied the valet.", "시종이 대답했다."]], [["“I might not always be content with that answer,”", "\"나라면 늘 그 대답으로 만족할 수는 없을 텐데.\""], ["replied the visitor, “for I come from one to whom everyone must be at home.", "방문객이 대답했다. \"나는 누구든 집에 있어 주어야 마땅한 분의 명을 받고 왔으니 말이오."], ["But have the kindness to give the Abbé Busoni——”", "그러니 수고스럽겠지만 부소니 신부께 전해——\""]], [["“I told you he was not at home,”", "\"신부님은 안 계시다고 말씀드렸습니다.\""], ["repeated the valet.", "시종이 거듭 말했다."]], [["“Then on his return give him that card and this sealed paper.", "\"그럼 돌아오시거든 이 명함과 봉인된 서류를 전해 주시오."], ["Will he be at home at eight o’clock this evening?”", "오늘 저녁 여덟 시에는 댁에 계시겠소?\""]], [["“Doubtless, unless he is at work, which is the same as if he were out.”", "\"틀림없이 계실 겁니다. 일하고 계신 게 아니라면요. 일하실 때는 안 계신 것이나 마찬가지입니다만.\""]], [["“I will come again at that time,”", "\"그 시각에 다시 오겠소.\""], ["replied the visitor, who then retired.", "방문객은 이렇게 대답하고 물러갔다."]], [["At the appointed hour the same man returned in the same carriage, which, instead of stopping this time at the end of the Rue Férou, drove up to the green door.", "약속한 시각에 같은 사내가 같은 마차를 타고 돌아왔다. 그런데 이번에는 마차가 페루 거리 끝에 서지 않고 곧장 초록색 문 앞까지 다가갔다."], ["He knocked, and it opened immediately to admit him.", "그가 문을 두드리자 문이 곧바로 열리며 그를 들였다."], ["From the signs of respect the valet paid him, he saw that his note had produced a good effect.", "시종이 보이는 공손한 태도로 미루어, 그는 자신의 쪽지가 효과를 거두었음을 알아챘다."]], [["“Is the abbé at home?”", "\"신부님은 댁에 계시오?\""], ["asked he.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“Yes; he is at work in his library, but he expects you, sir,”", "\"네, 서재에서 일하고 계십니다만, 손님을 기다리고 계십니다.\" 시종이 대답했다."], ["replied the valet.", "낯선 사내는 투박한 계단을 올라갔다."], ["The stranger ascended a rough staircase, and before a table, illumined by a lamp whose light was concentrated by a large shade while the rest of the apartment was in partial darkness, he perceived the abbé in a monk’s dress, with a cowl on his head such as was used by learned men of the Middle Ages.", "큼직한 갓이 빛을 한곳으로 모아 비추는 등불 아래 탁자 하나가 놓여 있었고, 방의 나머지는 어슴푸레한 어둠에 잠겨 있었다. 그 탁자 앞에서 그는 수도사 옷차림을 한 신부를 보았다. 신부는 중세의 학자들이 쓰던 것 같은 두건을 머리에 쓰고 있었다."]], [["“Have I the honor of addressing the Abbé Busoni?”", "\"부소니 신부님을 뵙는 영광을 누리는 것입니까?\""], ["asked the visitor.", "방문객이 물었다."]], [["“Yes, sir,”", "\"그렇습니다.\""], ["replied the abbé; “and you are the person whom M. de Boville, formerly an inspector of prisons, sends to me from the prefect of police?”", "신부가 대답했다. \"그리고 당신은 전 형무소 감독관 보빌 씨가 경시총감의 뜻을 받들어 제게 보낸 분이로군요?\""]], [["“Exactly, sir.”", "\"바로 그렇습니다.\""]], [["“One of the agents appointed to secure the safety of Paris?”", "\"파리의 치안을 지키는 임무를 맡은 요원들 가운데 한 분이시고요?\""]], [["“Yes, sir” replied the stranger with a slight hesitation, and blushing.", "\"그렇습니다.\" 낯선 사내가 살짝 머뭇거리며, 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했다."]], [["The abbé replaced the large spectacles, which covered not only his eyes but his temples, and sitting down motioned to his visitor to do the same.", "신부는 눈만이 아니라 관자놀이까지 덮는 커다란 안경을 다시 고쳐 썼다. 그러고는 자리에 앉으며 방문객에게도 앉으라는 몸짓을 했다."], ["“I am at your service, sir,”", "\"무슨 일이신지 말씀하시지요.\""], ["said the abbé, with a marked Italian accent.", "신부가 뚜렷한 이탈리아 억양으로 말했다."]], [["“The mission with which I am charged, sir,”", "\"제게 맡겨진 임무는 말입니다.\""], ["replied the visitor, speaking with hesitation, “is a confidential one on the part of him who fulfils it, and him by whom he is employed.”", "방문객이 머뭇거리며 말을 이었다. \"그것을 수행하는 자에게나, 그를 부린 분에게나 다 같이 은밀한 성격의 일입니다.\""], ["The abbé bowed. “Your probity,”", "신부가 고개를 숙였다."], ["replied the stranger, “is so well known to the prefect that he wishes as a magistrate to ascertain from you some particulars connected with the public safety, to ascertain which I am deputed to see you.", "\"신부님의 강직함은.\" 낯선 사내가 말을 이었다. \"경시총감께서도 익히 아시는 바라, 그분은 사법관으로서 공공의 안전과 관련된 몇 가지 사정을 신부님께 확인하고자 하십니다."], ["It is hoped that no ties of friendship or humane consideration will induce you to conceal the truth.”", "제가 그 일로 신부님을 뵙도록 파견된 것입니다. 부디 우정이나 인정에 매여 진실을 감추는 일이 없으시길 바랍니다.\""]], [["“Provided, sir, the particulars you wish for do not interfere with my scruples or my conscience.", "\"단, 알고자 하시는 일이 제 양심이나 신념에 어긋나지 않는다면 말입니다."], ["I am a priest, sir, and the secrets of confession, for instance, must remain between me and God, and not between me and human justice.”", "저는 사제입니다. 이를테면 고해의 비밀은 저와 하느님 사이에 머물러야지, 저와 인간의 정의 사이에 놓일 수 없는 법이지요.\""]], [["“Do not alarm yourself, monsieur, we will duly respect your conscience.”", "\"걱정 마십시오, 신부님. 신부님의 양심은 마땅히 존중하겠습니다.\""]], [["At this moment the abbé pressed down his side of the shade and so raised it on the other, throwing a bright light on the stranger’s face, while his own remained obscured.", "이때 신부가 갓의 한쪽을 눌러 반대편을 들어 올리니, 환한 빛이 낯선 사내의 얼굴로 쏟아지고 정작 신부 자신의 얼굴은 어둠에 잠겼다."]], [["“Excuse me, abbé,”", "\"실례합니다, 신부님.\""], ["said the envoy of the prefect of the police, “but the light tries my eyes very much.”", "경시총감의 사절이 말했다. \"빛이 눈을 너무 아프게 합니다.\""], ["The abbé lowered the shade.", "신부가 갓을 다시 내렸다."]], [["“Now, sir, I am listening—go on.”", "\"자, 이제 듣고 있습니다. 말씀하시지요.\""]], [["“I will come at once to the point.", "\"곧장 본론으로 들어가겠습니다."], ["Do you know the Count of Monte Cristo?”", "몬테크리스토 백작을 아십니까?\""]], [["“You mean Monsieur Zaccone, I presume?”", "\"자코네 씨를 말씀하시는 거겠지요?\""]], [["“Zaccone?—is not his name Monte Cristo?”", "\"자코네라뇨? 그 사람 이름이 몬테크리스토가 아닙니까?\""]], [["“Monte Cristo is the name of an estate, or, rather, of a rock, and not a family name.”", "\"몬테크리스토는 영지의 이름, 아니 좀 더 정확히는 바위의 이름이지, 가문의 성이 아닙니다.\""]], [["“Well, be it so—let us not dispute about words; and since M.", "\"좋습니다, 그렇다 칩시다. 말꼬리를 잡고 다툴 일은 아니지요."], ["de Monte Cristo and M. Zaccone are the same——”", "몬테크리스토 씨와 자코네 씨가 같은 사람이라면——\""]], [["“Absolutely the same.”", "\"완전히 같은 사람입니다.\""]], [["“Let us speak of M. Zaccone.”", "\"그럼 자코네 씨에 관해 이야기하지요.\""]], [["“Agreed.”", "\"좋습니다.\""]], [["“I asked you if you knew him?”", "\"그를 아시느냐고 여쭈었습니다.\""]], [["“Extremely well.”", "\"아주 잘 압니다.\""]], [["“Who is he?”", "\"그가 누구입니까?\""]], [["“The son of a rich shipbuilder in Malta.”", "\"몰타의 부유한 조선업자의 아들입니다.\""]], [["“I know that is the report; but, as you are aware, the police does not content itself with vague reports.”", "\"그렇게들 말한다는 건 압니다. 하지만 아시다시피 경찰은 막연한 소문으로는 만족하지 않습니다.\""]], [["“However,”", "\"그렇지만.\""], ["replied the abbé, with an affable smile, “when that report is in accordance with the truth, everybody must believe it, the police as well as all the rest.”", "신부가 상냥한 미소를 띠며 대답했다. \"그 소문이 진실과 일치한다면, 경찰이든 누구든 다들 믿을 수밖에 없는 법이지요.\""]], [["“Are you sure of what you assert?”", "\"확신하시는 바입니까?\""]], [["“What do you mean by that question?”", "\"그 물음은 무슨 뜻입니까?\""]], [["“Understand, sir, I do not in the least suspect your veracity; I ask if you are certain of it?”", "\"이해해 주십시오, 신부님. 신부님의 진실됨을 조금도 의심하는 게 아닙니다. 다만 그것을 확신하시느냐고 여쭙는 겁니다.\""]], [["“I knew his father, M. Zaccone.”", "\"저는 그의 아버지 자코네 씨를 알았습니다.\""]], [["“Ah, indeed?”", "\"아, 그렇습니까?\""]], [["“And when a child I often played with the son in the timber-yards.”", "\"그리고 어렸을 적에 목재 야적장에서 그 아들과 자주 함께 놀았지요.\""]], [["“But whence does he derive the title of count?”", "\"하지만 백작이라는 작위는 어디서 얻은 것입니까?\""]], [["“You are aware that may be bought.” “In Italy?”", "\"그런 건 살 수 있다는 걸 아실 텐데요.\""]], [["“Everywhere.”", "\"이탈리아에서요?\""]], [["“And his immense riches, whence does he procure them?”", "\"어디서나 그렇습니다.\""]], [["“They may not be so very great.”", "\"그럼 그 막대한 재산은 어디서 났습니까?\""]], [["“How much do you suppose he possesses?”", "\"그리 대단한 재산은 아닐지도 모르지요.\""]], [["“From one hundred and fifty to two hundred thousand livres per annum.”", "\"그가 얼마나 가졌다고 보십니까?\" \"연 십오만에서 이십만 리브르쯤 될 겁니다.\""]], [["“That is reasonable,”", "\"그건 그럴듯하군요.\""], ["said the visitor; “I have heard he had three or four millions.”", "방문객이 말했다. \"저는 그가 삼사백만은 가졌다고 들었습니다.\""]], [["“Two hundred thousand per annum would make four millions of capital.”", "\"연 이십만이면 자본으로 따져 사백만은 되는 셈입니다.\""]], [["“But I was told he had four millions per annum.”", "\"하지만 저는 그가 연 사백만의 수입을 올린다고 들었습니다.\""]], [["“That is not probable.”", "\"그건 있을 법하지 않은 일입니다.\""]], [["“Do you know this Island of Monte Cristo?”", "\"몬테크리스토섬이라는 곳을 아십니까?\""]], [["“Certainly, everyone who has come from Palermo, Naples, or Rome to France by sea must know it, since he has passed close to it and must have seen it.”", "\"물론입니다. 팔레르모나 나폴리, 로마에서 배로 프랑스에 온 사람이라면 누구나 알지요. 그 곁을 바짝 지나며 보았을 테니까요.\""]], [["“I am told it is a delightful place?”", "\"듣자 하니 아주 멋진 곳이라던데요?\""]], [["“It is a rock.”", "\"바위덩어리입니다.\""]], [["“And why has the count bought a rock?”", "\"그런데 백작은 어찌하여 바위를 샀을까요?\""]], [["“For the sake of being a count.", "\"백작이 되기 위해서지요."], ["In Italy one must have territorial possessions to be a count.”", "이탈리아에서는 백작이 되려면 영지를 가져야 하니까요.\""]], [["“You have, doubtless, heard the adventures of M. Zaccone’s youth?”", "\"자코네 씨가 젊은 시절에 겪은 일들에 대해서는 분명 들으셨겠지요?\""]], [["“The father’s?”", "\"아버지 말입니까?\""]], [["“No, the son’s.”", "\"아니요, 아들 말입니다.\""]], [["“I know nothing certain; at that period of his life, I lost sight of my young comrade.”", "\"확실한 건 아무것도 모릅니다. 그 무렵 저는 어린 시절의 동무를 시야에서 놓쳤으니까요.\""]], [["“Was he in the wars?”", "\"그는 전쟁에 나갔습니까?\""]], [["“I think he entered the service.”", "\"군에 들어간 걸로 압니다.\""]], [["“In what branch?”", "\"어느 병과에요?\""]], [["“In the navy.”", "\"해군이었습니다.\""]], [["“Are you not his confessor?”", "\"신부님은 그의 고해 신부가 아니십니까?\""]], [["“No, sir; I believe he is a Lutheran.”", "\"아닙니다. 제가 알기로 그는 루터교도입니다.\""]], [["“A Lutheran?”", "\"루터교도라고요?\""]], [["“I say, I believe such is the case, I do not affirm it; besides, liberty of conscience is established in France.”", "\"그런 것 같다는 말이지, 단언하는 건 아닙니다. 게다가 프랑스에서는 신앙의 자유가 보장되어 있지 않습니까.\""]], [["“Doubtless, and we are not now inquiring into his creed, but his actions; in the name of the prefect of police, I ask you what you know of him.", "\"물론입니다. 지금 우리가 캐묻는 건 그의 신앙이 아니라 그의 행적이지요. 경시총감의 이름으로 여쭙겠습니다. 그에 대해 무엇을 알고 계십니까?\""]], [["“He passes for a very charitable man.", "\"그는 대단히 자선심이 깊은 사람으로 통합니다."], ["Our holy father, the pope, has made him a knight of Jesus Christ for the services he rendered to the Christians in the East; he has five or six rings as testimonials from Eastern monarchs of his services.”", "우리의 성부이신 교황께서는 그가 동방에서 기독교도들에게 베푼 공로를 기려 그를 그리스도 기사로 봉하셨습니다. 또 동방의 군주들에게서 공로의 증표로 받은 반지가 대여섯 개나 됩니다.\""]], [["“Does he wear them?”", "\"그것들을 끼고 다닙니까?\""]], [["“No, but he is proud of them; he is better pleased with rewards given to the benefactors of man than to his destroyers.”", "\"아니요. 하지만 그것들을 자랑스러워하지요. 그는 인류를 해친 자에게 주는 상보다 인류에게 은혜를 베푼 자에게 주는 상을 더 기뻐합니다.\""]], [["“He is a Quaker then?”", "\"그렇다면 퀘이커교도입니까?\""]], [["“Exactly, he is a Quaker, with the exception of the peculiar dress.”", "\"바로 그렇습니다. 독특한 복장만 빼면 그는 퀘이커교도지요.\""]], [["“Has he any friends?”", "\"그에게 벗이 있습니까?\""]], [["“Yes, everyone who knows him is his friend.”", "\"네, 그를 아는 사람은 누구나 그의 벗입니다.\""]], [["“But has he any enemies?”", "\"하지만 적도 있습니까?\""]], [["“One only.”", "\"단 하나 있습니다.\""]], [["“What is his name?”", "\"이름이 무엇입니까?\""]], [["“Lord Wilmore.”", "\"윌모어 경입니다.\""]], [["“Where is he?”", "\"그는 어디에 있습니까?\""]], [["“He is in Paris just now.”", "\"지금 파리에 있습니다.\""]], [["“Can he give me any particulars?”", "\"제게 자세한 사정을 알려 줄 수 있을까요?\""]], [["“Important ones; he was in India with Zaccone.”", "\"중요한 사정을요. 그는 자코네와 함께 인도에 있었으니까요.\""]], [["“Do you know his abode?”", "\"그가 사는 곳을 아십니까?\""]], [["“It’s somewhere in the Chaussée d’Antin; but I know neither the street nor the number.” 30289m", "\"쇼세당탱 어디쯤입니다. 다만 거리도 번지도 모릅니다.\""]], [["“Are you at variance with the Englishman?”", "\"그 영국인과 사이가 좋지 않으십니까?\""]], [["“I love Zaccone, and he hates him; we are consequently not friends.”", "\"저는 자코네를 사랑하고, 그는 자코네를 미워합니다. 그러니 우리는 벗이 아니지요.\""]], [["“Do you think the Count of Monte Cristo had ever been in France before he made this visit to Paris?”", "\"몬테크리스토 백작이 이번에 파리를 찾기 전에 프랑스에 온 적이 있다고 보십니까?\""]], [["“To that question I can answer positively; no, sir, he had not, because he applied to me six months ago for the particulars he required, and as I did not know when I might again come to Paris, I recommended M.", "\"그 물음에는 분명히 답할 수 있습니다. 아니요, 온 적이 없습니다. 반년 전에 그가 필요한 정보를 얻으려고 제게 청해 왔는데, 제가 언제 다시 파리에 올지 알 수 없어 카발칸티 씨를 그에게 소개해 주었으니까요.\""], ["Cavalcanti to him.”", "\"안드레아 말입니까?\""]], [["“Andrea?” “No, Bartolomeo, his father.”", "\"안드레아 말입니까?\" \"아니요, 그의 아버지 바르톨로메오입니다.\""]], [["“Now, sir, I have but one question more to ask, and I charge you, in the name of honor, of humanity, and of religion, to answer me candidly.”", "\"자, 신부님, 이제 한 가지만 더 여쭙겠습니다. 명예와 인정과 종교의 이름으로 청하건대, 솔직히 답해 주십시오.\""]], [["“What is it, sir?”", "\"무엇입니까?\""]], [["“Do you know with what design M. de Monte Cristo purchased a house at Auteuil?”", "\"몬테크리스토 씨가 무슨 의도로 오퇴유에 저택을 샀는지 아십니까?\""]], [["“Certainly, for he told me.”", "\"물론입니다. 그가 제게 말해 주었으니까요.\""]], [["“What is it, sir?”", "\"무엇입니까?\""]], [["“To make a lunatic asylum of it, similar to that founded by the Count of Pisani at Palermo.", "\"그것을 정신병자 요양원으로 만들겠다고 했습니다. 피사니 백작이 팔레르모에 세운 것과 같은 곳으로 말이지요."], ["Do you know about that institution?”", "그 시설에 대해 아십니까?\""]], [["“I have heard of it.”", "\"들어 본 적이 있습니다.\""]], [["“It is a magnificent charity.”", "\"훌륭한 자선 사업이지요.\""], ["Having said this, the abbé bowed to imply he wished to pursue his studies.", "이렇게 말한 뒤 신부는 다시 학문에 몰두하고 싶다는 뜻으로 고개를 숙였다."]], [["The visitor either understood the abbé’s meaning, or had no more questions to ask; he arose, and the abbé accompanied him to the door.", "방문객은 신부의 뜻을 알아챘는지, 아니면 더 물을 것이 없었는지, 자리에서 일어섰다. 신부가 그를 문까지 배웅했다."]], [["“You are a great almsgiver,”", "\"신부님은 대단한 자선가이십니다.\""], ["said the visitor, “and although you are said to be rich, I will venture to offer you something for your poor people; will you accept my offering?”", "방문객이 말했다. \"부유하시다고들 하지만, 그래도 신부님의 가난한 이들을 위해 제가 무언가를 드려도 될지요. 제 성의를 받아 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“I thank you, sir; I am only jealous in one thing, and that is that the relief I give should be entirely from my own resources.”", "\"고맙습니다. 다만 저는 한 가지에 대해서만큼은 욕심이 있습니다. 제가 베푸는 구호는 오롯이 제 재산에서만 나와야 한다는 것이지요.\""]], [["“However——”", "\"그렇지만——\""]], [["“My resolution, sir, is unchangeable, but you have only to search for yourself and you will find, alas, but too many objects upon whom to exercise your benevolence.”", "\"제 결심은 변함이 없습니다. 다만 직접 찾아보시기만 하면, 안타깝게도 선행을 베풀 대상은 차고 넘치도록 많을 겁니다.\""]], [["The abbé once more bowed as he opened the door, the stranger bowed and took his leave, and the carriage conveyed him straight to the house of M. de Villefort.", "신부가 문을 열며 다시 한번 고개를 숙였고, 낯선 사내도 고개를 숙이며 작별을 고했다. 마차는 그를 곧장 빌포르 씨의 저택으로 데려갔다."], ["An hour afterwards the carriage was again ordered, and this time it went to the Rue Fontaine-Saint-Georges, and stopped at No.", "한 시간 뒤 마차가 다시 불려 나왔고, 이번에는 퐁텐생조르주 거리로 가서 윌모어 경이 사는 5번지 앞에 멈춰 섰다."], ["5, where Lord Wilmore lived. The stranger had written to Lord Wilmore, requesting an interview, which the latter had fixed for ten o’clock.", "낯선 사내는 윌모어 경에게 면담을 청하는 편지를 미리 보냈고, 윌모어 경은 열 시로 시각을 정해 두었다."], ["As the envoy of the prefect of police arrived ten minutes before ten, he was told that Lord Wilmore, who was precision and punctuality personified, was not yet come in, but that he would be sure to return as the clock struck.", "경시총감의 사절이 열 시 십 분 전에 도착하자, 정확함과 시간 엄수의 화신과도 같은 윌모어 경이 아직 돌아오지 않았으나 시계가 정각을 칠 때면 틀림없이 돌아올 것이라는 말을 들었다."]], [["The visitor was introduced into the drawing-room, which was like all other furnished drawing-rooms.", "방문객은 응접실로 안내되었다. 그곳은 여느 가구 딸린 응접실과 다를 바 없었다."], ["A mantle-piece, with two modern Sèvres vases, a timepiece representing Cupid with his bent bow, a mirror with an engraving on each side—one representing Homer carrying his guide, the other, Belisarius begging—a grayish paper; red and black tapestry—such was the appearance of Lord Wilmore’s drawing-room.", "현대식 세브르 도자기 꽃병 둘이 놓인 벽난로 선반, 활을 당긴 큐피드를 본뜬 시계, 양옆에 판화가 하나씩 걸린 거울——하나는 길잡이를 업은 호메로스를, 다른 하나는 구걸하는 벨리사리우스를 그린 것이었다——거무스름한 회색 벽지, 붉고 검은 벽걸이 천. 윌모어 경의 응접실은 그런 모습이었다."]], [["It was illuminated by lamps with ground-glass shades which gave only a feeble light, as if out of consideration for the envoy’s weak sight.", "응접실은 간유리 갓을 씌운 등불로 밝혀져 있었는데, 마치 사절의 약한 시력을 배려하기라도 한 듯 희미한 빛만을 내고 있었다."], ["After ten minutes’ expectation the clock struck ten; at the fifth stroke the door opened and Lord Wilmore appeared.", "십 분쯤 기다리자 시계가 열 시를 쳤다. 다섯 번째 종소리에 문이 열리며 윌모어 경이 나타났다."], ["He was rather above the middle height, with thin reddish whiskers, light complexion and light hair, turning rather gray.", "그는 중키를 조금 넘는 키에, 불그스름한 구레나룻이 성기게 났고, 살갗이 희며 머리칼은 옅은 빛깔로 희끗희끗 세어 가고 있었다."], ["He was dressed with all the English peculiarity, namely, in a blue coat, with gilt buttons and high collar, in the fashion of 1811, a white kerseymere waistcoat, and nankeen pantaloons, three inches too short, but which were prevented by straps from slipping up to the knee.", "그는 영국풍 특유의 차림을 빠짐없이 갖추고 있었다. 곧 도금 단추가 달리고 깃을 높이 세운 1811년식 파란 코트에, 흰색 커지미어 조끼, 그리고 세 치쯤 짧은 난징 무명 바지를 입었는데, 바지는 멜빵끈 덕분에 무릎까지 말려 올라가지 않았다."], ["His first remark on entering was:", "그가 들어서며 던진 첫마디는 이러했다."]], [["“You know, sir, I do not speak French?”", "\"아시겠지만, 나는 프랑스어를 하지 않소.\""]], [["“I know you do not like to converse in our language,”", "\"우리 말로 대화하시는 걸 좋아하지 않으신다는 건 압니다.\""], ["replied the envoy.", "사절이 대답했다."]], [["“But you may use it,”", "\"하지만 당신은 써도 좋소.\""], ["replied Lord Wilmore; “I understand it.”", "윌모어 경이 대답했다. \"나는 알아듣소.\""]], [["“And I,”", "\"저도.\""], ["replied the visitor, changing his idiom, “know enough of English to keep up the conversation.", "방문객이 말을 바꾸며 대답했다. \"대화를 이어 갈 만큼은 영어를 압니다."], ["Do not put yourself to the slightest inconvenience.”", "조금도 불편하실 것 없습니다.\""]], [["“Aw?” said Lord Wilmore, with that tone which is only known to natives of Great Britain.", "\"어?\" 윌모어 경이 영국 토박이들만이 낼 수 있는 그 어조로 말했다."]], [["The envoy presented his letter of introduction, which the latter read with English coolness, and having finished:", "사절이 소개장을 내밀자, 윌모어 경은 영국인다운 냉정함으로 그것을 읽고 나서 말했다."]], [["“I understand,”", "\"잘 알겠소.\""], ["said he, “perfectly.” 30293m", "그가 말했다. \"완벽하게 알겠소.\""]], [["Then began the questions, which were similar to those which had been addressed to the Abbé Busoni.", "그러고 나서 질문이 시작되었는데, 부소니 신부에게 던졌던 것과 비슷했다."], ["But as Lord Wilmore, in the character of the count’s enemy, was less restrained in his answers, they were more numerous; he described the youth of Monte Cristo, who he said, at ten years of age, entered the service of one of the petty sovereigns of India who make war on the English.", "다만 윌모어 경은 백작의 적이라는 처지였으므로 답하는 데 거리낌이 덜했고, 그래서 질문도 더 많아졌다. 그는 몬테크리스토의 젊은 시절을 들려주었다. 그의 말로는, 자코네는 열 살에 영국과 전쟁을 벌이던 인도의 어느 소군주를 섬기기 시작했다고 했다."], ["It was there Wilmore had first met him and fought against him; and in that war Zaccone had been taken prisoner, sent to England, and consigned to the hulks, whence he had escaped by swimming.", "윌모어가 그를 처음 만나 맞서 싸운 곳이 바로 거기였다. 그 전쟁에서 자코네는 포로로 잡혀 영국으로 보내져 죄수선에 갇혔으나, 헤엄을 쳐서 탈출했다."], ["Then began his travels, his duels, his caprices; then the insurrection in Greece broke out, and he had served in the Grecian ranks.", "그리하여 그의 떠돌이 생활과 결투와 변덕스러운 행각이 시작되었다. 그러다 그리스에서 봉기가 일어나자 그는 그리스 군대에 들어가 싸웠다."], ["While in that service he had discovered a silver mine in the mountains of Thessaly, but he had been careful to conceal it from everyone.", "그곳에서 복무하는 동안 그는 테살리아의 산속에서 은광을 발견했으나, 그것을 누구에게도 알리지 않으려 조심했다."], ["After the battle of Navarino, when the Greek government was consolidated, he asked of King Otho a mining grant for that district, which was given him.", "나바리노 해전 뒤 그리스 정부가 안정되자, 그는 오톤 왕에게 그 지역의 광산 채굴권을 청해 받아 냈다."], ["Hence that immense fortune, which, in Lord Wilmore’s opinion, possibly amounted to one or two millions per annum,—a precarious fortune, which might be momentarily lost by the failure of the mine.", "바로 거기서 그 막대한 재산이 나온 것이었다. 윌모어 경의 생각으로는 어쩌면 연 백만에서 이백만에 이를 수도 있겠으나, 광산이 바닥나면 한순간에 잃을 수도 있는 불안정한 재산이었다."]], [["“But,”", "\"그런데.\""], ["asked the visitor, “do you know why he came to France?”", "방문객이 물었다. \"그가 프랑스에 온 까닭을 아십니까?\""]], [["“He is speculating in railways,”", "\"철도에 투기하고 있소.\""], ["said Lord Wilmore, “and as he is an expert chemist and physicist, he has invented a new system of telegraphy, which he is seeking to bring to perfection.”", "윌모어 경이 말했다. \"게다가 그는 뛰어난 화학자이자 물리학자라, 새로운 전신 체계를 발명해 그것을 완성해 보려 애쓰고 있소.\""]], [["“How much does he spend yearly?”", "\"해마다 얼마나 씁니까?\""], ["asked the prefect.", "총감이 물었다."]], [["“Not more than five or six hundred thousand francs,”", "\"기껏해야 오륙십만 프랑이오.\" 윌모어 경이 말했다."], ["said Lord Wilmore; “he is a miser.”", "\"그는 구두쇠요.\""], ["Hatred evidently inspired the Englishman, who, knowing no other reproach to bring on the count, accused him of avarice.", "이 영국인은 분명 증오에 차 있었다. 백작에게 달리 씌울 흠을 찾지 못하자 인색하다고 몰아붙이는 것이었다."]], [["“Do you know his house at Auteuil?”", "\"오퇴유에 있는 그의 집을 아십니까?\""]], [["“Certainly.”", "\"물론이오.\""]], [["“What do you know respecting it?”", "\"그 집에 대해 무엇을 알고 계십니까?\""]], [["“Do you wish to know why he bought it?”", "\"그가 왜 그 집을 샀는지 알고 싶소?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“The count is a speculator, who will certainly ruin himself in experiments.", "\"백작은 투기꾼이라, 틀림없이 이런저런 실험으로 제 몸을 망칠 거요."], ["He supposes there is in the neighborhood of the house he has bought a mineral spring equal to those at Bagnères, Luchon, and Cauterets. He is going to turn his house into a _Badhaus_, as the Germans term it.", "그는 자기가 산 집 근처에 바녜르나 뤼숑, 코트레의 것에 견줄 만한 광천이 있다고 믿고 있소. 그래서 그 집을 독일 사람들이 말하는 이른바 '바트하우스', 곧 온천장으로 바꾸려는 거요."], ["He has already dug up all the garden two or three times to find the famous spring, and, being unsuccessful, he will soon purchase all the contiguous houses.", "그는 그 유명한 샘을 찾겠다고 벌써 정원을 두세 번이나 죄다 파헤쳤는데, 끝내 찾지 못했으니 머지않아 이웃한 집들까지 모조리 사들일 거요."], ["Now, as I dislike him, and hope his railway, his electric telegraph, or his search for baths, will ruin him, I am watching for his discomfiture, which must soon take place.”", "한데 나는 그를 싫어하는지라, 그의 철도든, 전신이든, 온천 찾기든 그를 파멸시키기를 바라며 그 낭패를 지켜보고 있소. 그런 날이 곧 닥칠 게요.\""]], [["“What was the cause of your quarrel?”", "\"두 분이 다투신 까닭은 무엇입니까?\""]], [["“When he was in England he seduced the wife of one of my friends.”", "\"그가 영국에 있을 때 내 벗 가운데 한 사람의 아내를 유혹했소.\""]], [["“Why do you not seek revenge?”", "\"어찌하여 복수하지 않으십니까?\""]], [["“I have already fought three duels with him,”", "\"이미 그와 결투를 세 번이나 했소.\""], ["said the Englishman, “the first with the pistol, the second with the sword, and the third with the sabre.”", "영국인이 말했다. \"처음에는 권총으로, 두 번째는 검으로, 세 번째는 사브르로.\""]], [["“And what was the result of those duels?”", "\"그 결투들의 결과는 어떻게 되었습니까?\""]], [["“The first time, he broke my arm; the second, he wounded me in the breast; and the third time, made this large wound.”", "\"처음에는 그가 내 팔을 부러뜨렸고, 두 번째는 가슴에 상처를 입혔으며, 세 번째에는 이렇게 큰 상처를 냈소.\""], ["The Englishman turned down his shirt-collar, and showed a scar, whose redness proved it to be a recent one.", "영국인이 셔츠 깃을 내려 흉터를 보였는데, 그 붉은빛으로 보아 최근에 생긴 것임을 알 수 있었다."], ["“So that, you see, there is a deadly feud between us.”", "\"그러니 보다시피 우리 사이에는 목숨을 건 원한이 있는 거요.\""]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만.\""], ["said the envoy, “you do not go about it in the right way to kill him, if I understand you correctly.”", "사절이 말했다. \"제가 제대로 이해했다면, 그를 죽이려는 방식치고는 영 옳은 길이 아니신 듯합니다.\""]], [["“Aw?”", "\"어?\""], ["said the Englishman, “I practice shooting every day, and every other day Grisier comes to my house.”", "영국인이 말했다. \"나는 날마다 사격을 연습하고, 이틀에 한 번꼴로 그리지에가 우리 집에 와 주오.\""]], [["This was all the visitor wished to ascertain, or, rather, all the Englishman appeared to know.", "이것이 방문객이 알아내고자 한 전부였다. 아니, 좀 더 정확히 말하면 영국인이 아는 것의 전부인 듯했다."], ["The agent arose, and having bowed to Lord Wilmore, who returned his salutation with the stiff politeness of the English, he retired.", "요원이 자리에서 일어나 윌모어 경에게 고개를 숙이자, 윌모어 경도 영국인다운 뻣뻣한 정중함으로 인사를 받았고, 요원은 물러갔다."], ["Lord Wilmore, having heard the door close after him, returned to his bedroom, where with one hand he pulled off his light hair, his red whiskers, his false jaw, and his wound, to resume the black hair, dark complexion, and pearly teeth of the Count of Monte Cristo.", "윌모어 경은 등 뒤로 문이 닫히는 소리를 듣고는 침실로 돌아갔다. 그러고는 한 손으로 옅은 빛깔의 머리칼과 붉은 구레나룻, 가짜 턱과 상처를 떼어 내고, 다시 몬테크리스토 백작의 검은 머리와 가무잡잡한 살갗, 진주 같은 이를 되찾았다."]], [["It was M. de Villefort, and not the prefect, who returned to the house of M. de Villefort.", "빌포르 씨의 저택으로 돌아온 것은 경시총감의 사절이 아니라 빌포르 씨 자신이었다."], ["The procureur felt more at ease, although he had learned nothing really satisfactory, and, for the first time since the dinner-party at Auteuil, he slept soundly.", "검사는 정작 만족스러운 것은 아무것도 알아내지 못했으면서도 한결 마음이 놓였고, 오퇴유의 만찬 이래 처음으로 깊은 잠을 잤다."]], [["# Chapter 70. The Ball", "# Chapter 70. The Ball"]], [["It was in the warmest days of July, when in due course of time the Saturday arrived upon which the ball was to take place at M. de Morcerf’s.", "칠월의 가장 무더운 날들이었다. 그리고 때가 무르익어, 모르세르 씨 댁에서 무도회가 열리기로 한 토요일이 찾아왔다."], ["It was ten o’clock at night; the branches of the great trees in the garden of the count’s house stood out boldly against the azure canopy of heaven, which was studded with golden stars, but where the last fleeting clouds of a vanishing storm yet lingered.", "밤 열 시였다. 백작 저택의 정원에 선 큰 나무들의 가지가, 황금빛 별들이 점점이 박힌 푸른 하늘의 천장을 배경으로 또렷이 솟아 있었다. 그 하늘에는 잦아드는 폭풍의 마지막 구름 조각들이 아직 머뭇거리고 있었다."]], [["From the apartments on the ground floor might be heard the sound of music, with the whirl of the waltz and galop, while brilliant streams of light shone through the openings of the Venetian blinds.", "일층의 방들에서는 음악 소리가, 왈츠와 갈로프의 소용돌이와 함께 들려왔고, 베네치아풍 덧창의 틈새로 눈부신 빛줄기가 새어 나왔다."], ["At this moment the garden was only occupied by about ten servants, who had just received orders from their mistress to prepare the supper, the serenity of the weather continuing to increase.", "그 순간 정원에는 하인 열 명쯤만 나와 있었다. 그들은 방금 안주인에게서 저녁 식사를 준비하라는 분부를 받은 참이었다. 날씨가 점점 더 화창해지고 있었다."], ["Until now, it had been undecided whether the supper should take place in the dining-room, or under a long tent erected on the lawn, but the beautiful blue sky, studded with stars, had settled the question in favor of the lawn.", "지금까지도 저녁 식사를 식당에서 차릴지, 잔디밭에 세운 긴 천막 아래서 차릴지 정하지 못한 상태였다. 그러나 별이 점점이 박힌 아름다운 푸른 하늘이 잔디밭 쪽으로 결판을 내 주었다."]], [["The gardens were illuminated with colored lanterns, according to the Italian custom, and, as is usual in countries where the luxuries of the table—the rarest of all luxuries in their complete form—are well understood, the supper-table was loaded with wax-lights and flowers.", "정원은 이탈리아 풍습에 따라 색색의 등불로 환히 밝혀졌고, 식탁의 사치 — 온전한 형태를 갖추기로는 모든 사치 가운데 가장 드문 그 사치 — 가 잘 이해되는 나라들에서 으레 그렇듯, 만찬 식탁은 밀랍 촛불과 꽃들로 가득했다."]], [["30297m\nAt the time the Countess of Morcerf returned to the rooms, after giving her orders, many guests were arriving, more attracted by the charming hospitality of the countess than by the distinguished position of the count; for, owing to the good taste of Mercédès, one was sure of finding some devices at her entertainment worthy of describing, or even copying in case of need.", "분부를 마치고 모르세르 백작 부인이 방으로 돌아왔을 때, 손님들이 많이 도착하고 있었다. 그들은 백작의 높은 지위보다 백작 부인의 매혹적인 환대에 더 이끌린 사람들이었다. 메르세데스의 빼어난 취향 덕분에, 그녀의 연회에서는 묘사할 만한, 필요하다면 본떠 볼 만한 무언가를 반드시 발견하게 마련이었으니 말이다."]], [["Madame Danglars, in whom the events we have related had caused deep anxiety, had hesitated about going to Madame de Morcerf’s, when during the morning her carriage happened to meet that of Villefort.", "앞서 우리가 이야기한 사건들로 깊은 불안에 빠져 있던 당글라르 부인은 모르세르 부인 댁에 갈지 망설이고 있었는데, 그날 아침 그녀의 마차가 우연히 빌포르의 마차와 마주쳤다."], ["The latter made a sign, and when the carriages had drawn close together, said:", "빌포르가 신호를 보냈고, 두 마차가 바짝 다가서자 그가 말했다."]], [["“You are going to Madame de Morcerf’s, are you not?”", "\"모르세르 부인 댁에 가시는 길이지요, 그렇지 않습니까?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니요.\""], ["replied Madame Danglars, “I am too ill.”", "당글라르 부인이 대답했다. \"몸이 너무 안 좋아서요.\""]], [["“You are wrong,”", "\"그건 잘못된 생각입니다.\""], ["replied Villefort, significantly; “it is important that you should be seen there.”", "빌포르가 의미심장하게 대답했다. \"거기서 부인의 모습을 보이는 것이 중요합니다.\""]], [["“Do you think so?”", "\"그렇게 생각하세요?\""], ["asked the baroness.", "남작 부인이 물었다."]], [["“I do.”", "\"그렇습니다.\""]], [["“In that case I will go.”", "\"그렇다면 가야겠군요.\""]], [["And the two carriages passed on towards their different destinations.", "그리고 두 마차는 각자의 목적지를 향해 지나갔다."], ["Madame Danglars therefore came, not only beautiful in person, but radiant with splendor; she entered by one door at the time when Mercédès appeared at the door.", "그리하여 당글라르 부인은 아름다운 자태로, 게다가 화려함으로 눈부시게 빛나며 도착했다. 메르세데스가 한쪽 문에 나타나는 바로 그 순간, 그녀는 다른 문으로 들어섰다."], ["The countess took Albert to meet Madame Danglars.", "백작 부인은 알베르를 데리고 당글라르 부인을 맞으러 갔다."], ["He approached, paid her some well merited compliments on her toilet, and offered his arm to conduct her to a seat.", "알베르가 다가가 부인의 차림새에 마땅한 찬사를 몇 마디 건넨 뒤, 자리로 안내하려고 팔을 내밀었다."], ["Albert looked around him.", "알베르가 주위를 둘러보았다."]], [["“You are looking for my daughter?” said the baroness, smiling.", "\"제 딸을 찾으시나요?\" 남작 부인이 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“I confess it,”", "\"솔직히 그렇습니다.\""], ["replied Albert.", "알베르가 대답했다."], ["“Could you have been so cruel as not to bring her?”", "\"설마 따님을 데려오지 않으시는 모진 일을 하셨을 리가요?\""]], [["“Calm yourself.", "\"안심하세요."], ["She has met Mademoiselle de Villefort, and has taken her arm; see, they are following us, both in white dresses, one with a bouquet of camellias, the other with one of myosotis.", "빌포르 양과 마주쳐서 그 아이 팔을 잡고 있답니다. 보세요, 우리 뒤를 따라오고 있어요. 둘 다 흰 드레스를 입었는데, 하나는 동백꽃 다발을, 다른 하나는 물망초 다발을 들었지요."], ["But tell me——”", "그런데 말해 봐요……\""]], [["“Well, what do you wish to know?”", "\"네, 무엇을 알고 싶으신지요?\""]], [["“Will not the Count of Monte Cristo be here tonight?”", "\"오늘 밤 몬테 크리스토 백작이 여기 오시나요?\""]], [["“Seventeen!”", "\"열일곱!\""], ["replied Albert.", "알베르가 대답했다."]], [["“What do you mean?”", "\"무슨 말씀이세요?\""]], [["“I only mean that the count seems the rage,”", "\"백작이 굉장한 화제인 모양이라는 말입니다.\""], ["replied the viscount, smiling, “and that you are the seventeenth person that has asked me the same question.", "자작이 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"그리고 부인이 제게 같은 질문을 한 열일곱 번째 분이라는 말이고요."], ["The count is in fashion; I congratulate him upon it.”", "백작은 유행이지요. 그 점은 그에게 축하를 보냅니다.\""]], [["“And have you replied to everyone as you have to me?”", "\"그래서 모두에게 제게 한 것처럼 대답하셨나요?\""]], [["“Ah, to be sure, I have not answered you; be satisfied, we shall have this ‘lion’; we are among the privileged ones.”", "\"아, 그러고 보니 부인께는 대답을 안 했군요. 안심하세요, 우리는 이 '명사'를 모실 겁니다. 우리는 특권을 누리는 사람들 축에 들거든요.\""]], [["“Were you at the Opera yesterday?”", "\"어제 오페라에 가셨어요?\" \"아니요.\""]], [["“No.” “He was there.”", "\"그분이 거기 계셨어요.\""]], [["“Ah, indeed?", "\"아, 정말요?"], ["And did the eccentric person commit any new originality?”", "그래서 그 별난 양반이 또 무슨 새로운 기행을 벌이던가요?\""]], [["“Can he be seen without doing so?", "\"그분이 그런 일 없이 사람들 눈에 띌 수가 있나요?"], ["Elssler was dancing in _Le Diable boiteux_; the Greek princess was in ecstasies.", "엘슬레르가 '절름발이 악마'를 추고 있었고, 그리스 공주는 황홀경에 빠져 있었지요."], ["After the cachucha he placed a magnificent ring on the stem of a bouquet, and threw it to the charming danseuse, who, in the third act, to do honor to the gift, reappeared with it on her finger.", "카추차가 끝난 뒤, 그분이 화려한 반지 하나를 꽃다발 줄기에 끼워 그 매혹적인 무희에게 던졌어요. 그러자 무희가 삼막에서 그 선물에 경의를 표하느라 손가락에 반지를 끼고 다시 나왔답니다."], ["And the Greek princess,—will she be here?”", "그런데 그리스 공주는 — 오늘 여기 오나요?\""]], [["“No, you will be deprived of that pleasure; her position in the count’s establishment is not sufficiently understood.”", "\"아니요, 그 즐거움은 누리지 못하실 겁니다. 백작 집안에서 그녀의 위치가 충분히 알려지지 않았거든요.\""]], [["“Wait; leave me here, and go and speak to Madame de Villefort, who is trying to attract your attention.”", "\"잠깐만요, 저는 여기 두고 빌포르 부인께 가서 말씀을 나누세요. 당신 시선을 끌려고 하고 계시잖아요.\""]], [["Albert bowed to Madame Danglars, and advanced towards Madame de Villefort, whose lips opened as he approached.", "알베르는 당글라르 부인에게 고개 숙여 인사한 뒤 빌포르 부인 쪽으로 다가갔다. 그가 다가가자 부인이 입을 열었다."]], [["“I wager anything,”", "\"무엇이든 걸겠습니다.\""], ["said Albert, interrupting her, “that I know what you were about to say.”", "알베르가 부인의 말을 가로막으며 말했다. \"방금 무슨 말씀을 하시려던 건지 제가 압니다.\""]], [["“Well, what is it?”", "\"그래요, 그게 뭔가요?\""]], [["“If I guess rightly, will you confess it?”", "\"제가 옳게 맞히면, 인정하시겠습니까?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“On your honor?”", "\"명예를 걸고요?\""]], [["“On my honor.”", "\"명예를 걸고요.\""]], [["“You were going to ask me if the Count of Monte Cristo had arrived, or was expected.”", "\"몬테 크리스토 백작이 도착했는지, 아니면 올 예정인지 물으시려던 거지요.\""]], [["“Not at all.", "\"전혀 아니에요."], ["It is not of him that I am now thinking.", "지금 제가 생각하는 건 그분이 아니랍니다."], ["I was going to ask you if you had received any news of Monsieur Franz.”", "프란츠 씨에게서 무슨 소식이라도 받으셨는지 물으려던 거예요.\""]], [["“Yes,—yesterday.”", "\"네, 어제 받았습니다.\""]], [["“What did he tell you?”", "\"뭐라고 하던가요?\""]], [["“That he was leaving at the same time as his letter.”", "\"편지와 같은 시기에 떠난다고요.\""]], [["“Well, now then, the count?”", "\"그럼, 이제 백작은요?\""]], [["“The count will come, of that you may be satisfied.”", "\"백작은 올 겁니다. 그 점은 안심하셔도 됩니다.\""]], [["“You know that he has another name besides Monte Cristo?”", "\"그분에게 몬테 크리스토 말고 다른 이름이 있는 거 아세요?\""]], [["“No, I did not know it.”", "\"아니요, 몰랐습니다.\""]], [["“Monte Cristo is the name of an island, and he has a family name.”", "\"몬테 크리스토는 섬 이름이고, 그분에겐 따로 성이 있어요.\""]], [["“I never heard it.”", "\"전혀 들어 본 적이 없습니다.\""]], [["“Well, then, I am better informed than you; his name is Zaccone.”", "\"그렇다면 제가 당신보다 더 소식에 밝네요. 그분 이름은 자코네예요.\""]], [["“It is possible.”", "\"그럴 수도 있겠지요.\""]], [["“He is a Maltese.”", "\"몰타 사람이고요.\""]], [["“That is also possible.", "\"그것도 그럴 수 있고요.\""]], [["“The son of a shipowner.”", "\"어느 선주의 아들이래요.\""]], [["“Really, you should relate all this aloud, you would have the greatest success.”", "\"정말이지 그걸 다 큰 소리로 이야기하셔야겠습니다. 대단한 성공을 거두실 텐데요.\""]], [["“He served in India, discovered a mine in Thessaly, and comes to Paris to establish a mineral water-cure at Auteuil.”", "\"인도에서 복무했고, 테살리아에서 광산 하나를 발견했으며, 오퇴유에 광천 요양소를 세우려고 파리에 온 거래요.\""]], [["“Well, I’m sure,”", "\"이거 참,\""], ["said Morcerf, “this is indeed news!", "모르세르가 말했다. \"정말 새로운 소식이군요!"], ["Am I allowed to repeat it?”", "제가 이걸 옮겨도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["“Yes, but cautiously, tell one thing at a time, and do not say I told you.” “Why so?”", "\"네, 하지만 조심해서요. 한 번에 한 가지씩만 말하고, 제가 했다는 말은 하지 마세요.\""]], [["“Because it is a secret just discovered.”", "\"왜 그러시지요?\" \"막 알려진 비밀이라서요.\""]], [["“By whom?”", "\"누가요?\""]], [["“The police.”", "\"경찰이요.\""]], [["“Then the news originated——”", "\"그럼 그 소식이 나온 곳은……\""]], [["“At the prefect’s last night.", "\"어젯밤 경시청장 댁이요."], ["Paris, you can understand, is astonished at the sight of such unusual splendor, and the police have made inquiries.”", "파리가, 아시다시피, 그런 보기 드문 화려함을 보고 깜짝 놀라서 경찰이 조사를 한 거예요.\""]], [["“Well, well! Nothing more is wanting than to arrest the count as a vagabond, on the pretext of his being too rich.”", "\"이런, 이런! 너무 부자라는 구실로 그 백작을 부랑자로 체포하는 일만 남았군요.\""]], [["“Indeed, that doubtless would have happened if his credentials had not been so favorable.”", "\"실은, 만약 신원 증명이 그렇게 유리하지 않았다면 틀림없이 그렇게 됐을 거예요.\""]], [["“Poor count!", "\"가엾은 백작!"], ["And is he aware of the danger he has been in?”", "그분은 자기가 어떤 위험에 처했었는지 알고 계실까요?\""]], [["“I think not.”", "\"모를 것 같아요.\""]], [["“Then it will be but charitable to inform him.", "\"그렇다면 알려 드리는 것이 자비로운 일이겠군요."], ["When he arrives, I will not fail to do so.”", "그분이 도착하면 꼭 그렇게 하겠습니다.\""]], [["Just then, a handsome young man, with bright eyes, black hair, and glossy moustache, respectfully bowed to Madame de Villefort.", "바로 그때, 빛나는 눈과 검은 머리, 윤기 흐르는 콧수염을 한 잘생긴 청년이 빌포르 부인에게 정중하게 고개를 숙였다."], ["Albert extended his hand.", "알베르가 손을 내밀었다."]], [["“Madame,”", "\"부인,\""], ["said Albert, “allow me to present to you M.", "알베르가 말했다. \"막시밀리앙 모렐 씨를 소개해 드리겠습니다."], ["Maximilian Morrel, captain of Spahis, one of our best, and, above all, of our bravest officers.”", "스파히 기병대의 대위이자, 우리 군에서 가장 훌륭하고, 무엇보다 가장 용감한 장교 가운데 한 사람입니다.\""]], [["“I have already had the pleasure of meeting this gentleman at Auteuil, at the house of the Count of Monte Cristo,”", "\"이미 오퇴유에서, 몬테 크리스토 백작 댁에서 이분을 만나는 기쁨을 누렸답니다.\""], ["replied Madame de Villefort, turning away with marked coldness of manner.", "빌포르 부인이 눈에 띄게 쌀쌀한 태도로 고개를 돌리며 대답했다."]], [["This answer, and especially the tone in which it was uttered, chilled the heart of poor Morrel.", "이 대답, 그리고 특히 그 말투가 가엾은 모렐의 가슴을 얼어붙게 했다."], ["But a recompense was in store for him; turning around, he saw near the door a beautiful fair face, whose large blue eyes were, without any marked expression, fixed upon him, while the bouquet of myosotis was gently raised to her lips.", "그러나 그에게는 보상이 마련되어 있었다. 몸을 돌리자 문 가까이에서 아름다운 금발의 얼굴이 보였다. 커다란 파란 눈이, 별다른 표정 없이 그에게 고정되어 있었고, 그 사이 물망초 다발이 가만히 입술로 들어 올려졌다."]], [["The salutation was so well understood that Morrel, with the same expression in his eyes, placed his handkerchief to his mouth; and these two living statues, whose hearts beat so violently under their marble aspect, separated from each other by the whole length of the room, forgot themselves for a moment, or rather forgot the world in their mutual contemplation.", "그 인사가 어찌나 잘 통했던지, 모렐도 눈에 같은 표정을 띤 채 손수건을 입가로 가져갔다. 그리하여 대리석 같은 겉모습 아래 그토록 격렬하게 뛰는 두 살아 있는 조각상은, 방 전체 길이만큼 서로 떨어진 채로 한순간 자신들을 잊었다. 아니, 차라리 서로를 응시하느라 온 세상을 잊었다고 해야 할 것이다."], ["They might have remained much longer lost in one another, without anyone noticing their abstraction.", "누구도 그들의 넋 나간 모습을 알아채지 못한 채, 둘은 훨씬 더 오래 서로에게 빠져 있을 수도 있었을 것이다."], ["The Count of Monte Cristo had just entered.", "몬테 크리스토 백작이 막 들어선 참이었다."]], [["We have already said that there was something in the count which attracted universal attention wherever he appeared.", "우리는 백작에게는, 그가 어디에 나타나든 모든 사람의 시선을 끄는 무언가가 있다고 이미 말한 바 있다. 그것은 재단에 흠잡을 데 없으면서도 단순하고 장식 없는 그의 코트가 아니었다. 그것은 수수한 흰 조끼도 아니었다."], ["It was not the coat, unexceptional in its cut, though simple and unornamented; it was not the plain white waistcoat; it was not the trousers, that displayed the foot so perfectly formed—it was none of these things that attracted the attention,—it was his pale complexion, his waving black hair, his calm and serene expression, his dark and melancholy eye, his mouth, chiselled with such marvellous delicacy, which so easily expressed such high disdain,—these were what fixed the attention of all upon him.", "그것은 더없이 잘 빠진 발을 드러내는 바지도 아니었다. 시선을 끈 것은 그런 것들이 아니었다. 그의 창백한 안색, 물결치는 검은 머리카락, 차분하고 평온한 표정, 어둡고 우수에 잠긴 눈, 그토록 높은 경멸을 그토록 쉽게 드러내며 놀랍도록 섬세하게 깎아 놓은 듯한 입 — 모든 이의 시선을 그에게 못 박은 것은 바로 이런 것들이었다."]], [["Many men might have been handsomer, but certainly there could be none whose appearance was more _significant_, if the expression may be used.", "그보다 더 잘생긴 사내는 많았을 것이다. 그러나 — 이런 표현을 써도 된다면 — 그의 모습보다 더 의미심장한 사람은 분명 없었을 것이다."], ["Everything about the count seemed to have its meaning, for the constant habit of thought which he had acquired had given an ease and vigor to the expression of his face, and even to the most trifling gesture, scarcely to be understood.", "백작의 모든 것이 저마다 뜻을 지닌 듯했다. 그가 몸에 익힌 끊임없는 사색의 습관이 그의 얼굴 표정에, 심지어 가장 사소한 몸짓에까지, 좀처럼 헤아리기 힘든 편안함과 활력을 부여했기 때문이다."], ["Yet the Parisian world is so strange, that even all this might not have won attention had there not been connected with it a mysterious story gilded by an immense fortune.", "그렇지만 파리의 사교계란 워낙 기묘한 곳이라, 막대한 재산으로 덧칠된 어떤 신비로운 이야기가 거기에 얽혀 있지 않았더라면, 이 모든 것조차 사람들의 관심을 끌지 못했을지도 모른다."]], [["30301m\nMeanwhile he advanced through the assemblage of guests under a battery of curious glances towards Madame de Morcerf, who, standing before a mantle-piece ornamented with flowers, had seen his entrance in a looking-glass placed opposite the door, and was prepared to receive him.", "그러는 동안 백작은 호기심 어린 시선들의 포화를 받으며 손님들 무리 사이를 헤치고 모르세르 부인 쪽으로 나아갔다. 꽃으로 장식된 벽난로 앞에 서 있던 부인은, 문 맞은편에 놓인 거울로 그가 들어서는 것을 보고 그를 맞을 채비를 하고 있었다."], ["She turned towards him with a serene smile just at the moment he was bowing to her.", "그가 막 고개를 숙이는 순간, 부인이 평온한 미소를 띠며 그를 향해 돌아섰다."], ["No doubt she fancied the count would speak to her, while on his side the count thought she was about to address him; but both remained silent, and after a mere bow, Monte Cristo directed his steps to Albert, who received him cordially.", "부인은 분명 백작이 자기에게 말을 걸리라 여겼고, 백작 쪽에서도 부인이 막 말을 건네려는 줄 알았다. 그러나 둘 다 입을 다물었고, 그저 가벼운 인사만 나눈 뒤, 몬테 크리스토는 알베르 쪽으로 걸음을 옮겼다. 알베르가 그를 다정하게 맞았다."]], [["“Have you seen my mother?”", "\"제 어머니를 뵈셨습니까?\""], ["asked Albert.", "알베르가 물었다."]], [["“I have just had the pleasure,”", "\"방금 그 기쁨을 누렸습니다.\""], ["replied the count; “but I have not seen your father.”", "백작이 대답했다. \"하지만 자작의 아버님은 뵙지 못했군요.\""]], [["“See, he is down there, talking politics with that little group of great geniuses.”", "\"보세요, 저기 계십니다. 저 위대한 천재들의 작은 무리와 정치 이야기를 나누고 계시지요.\""]], [["“Indeed?”", "\"그래요?\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “and so those gentlemen down there are men of great talent.", "몬테 크리스토가 말했다. \"그러니까 저기 저 신사분들이 대단한 재능을 지닌 분들이란 말이군요."], ["I should not have guessed it.", "짐작도 못 했습니다."], ["And for what kind of talent are they celebrated?", "그런데 어떤 종류의 재능으로 유명하신가요?"], ["You know there are different sorts.”", "아시다시피 재능에도 여러 가지가 있으니까요.\""]], [["“That tall, harsh-looking man is very learned, he discovered, in the neighborhood of Rome, a kind of lizard with a vertebra more than lizards usually have, and he immediately laid his discovery before the Institute.", "\"키가 크고 험상궂게 생긴 저분은 아주 박식한 분입니다. 로마 인근에서, 도마뱀류가 보통 가진 것보다 척추뼈가 하나 더 많은 도마뱀을 발견하고는, 곧장 그 발견을 학사원에 제출했지요."], ["The thing was discussed for a long time, but finally decided in his favor.", "그 일이 오랫동안 논의되었지만, 결국 그분에게 유리하게 결론이 났습니다."], ["I can assure you the vertebra made a great noise in the learned world, and the gentleman, who was only a knight of the Legion of Honor, was made an officer.”", "장담하건대 그 척추뼈가 학계에 큰 파문을 일으켰고, 레지옹 도뇌르 슈발리에에 불과했던 그분이 오피시에로 승급했답니다.\""]], [["“Come,”", "\"이런,\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “this cross seems to me to be wisely awarded.", "몬테 크리스토가 말했다. \"그 훈장은 현명하게 수여된 것 같군요."], ["I suppose, had he found another additional vertebra, they would have made him a commander.”", "척추뼈를 하나 더 발견했다면, 코망되르로 만들어 주었겠는데요.\""]], [["“Very likely,”", "\"그럴 법하지요.\""], ["said Albert.", "알베르가 말했다."]], [["“And who can that person be who has taken it into his head to wrap himself up in a blue coat embroidered with green?”", "\"그리고 초록색으로 수놓은 파란 코트로 몸을 감쌀 생각을 한 저 사람은 누구입니까?\""]], [["“Oh, that coat is not his own idea; it is the Republic’s, which deputed David12 to devise a uniform for the Academicians.”", "\"아, 저 코트는 저분 생각이 아닙니다. 공화국의 작품이지요. 학사원 회원들의 제복을 고안하라고 다비드에게 위임했거든요.\""]], [["“Indeed?”", "\"그래요?\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “so this gentleman is an Academician?”", "몬테 크리스토가 말했다. \"그럼 저 신사분은 학사원 회원이군요?\""]], [["“Within the last week he has been made one of the learned assembly.”", "\"지난주에 그 학식 높은 모임의 일원이 되었습니다.\""]], [["“And what is his especial talent?”", "\"그럼 저분의 특별한 재능은 무엇입니까?\""]], [["“His talent? I believe he thrusts pins through the heads of rabbits, he makes fowls eat madder, and punches the spinal marrow out of dogs with whalebone.”", "\"저분의 재능이요? 토끼 머리에 핀을 꽂고, 닭에게 꼭두서니를 먹이고, 고래수염으로 개의 척수를 빼내는 것으로 압니다.\""]], [["“And he is made a member of the Academy of Sciences for this?”", "\"그런 일로 과학 학사원 회원이 되었단 말입니까?\""]], [["“No; of the French Academy.”", "\"아니요, 프랑스 학사원입니다.\""]], [["“But what has the French Academy to do with all this?”", "\"그런데 프랑스 학사원이 그런 일과 무슨 상관이 있나요?\""]], [["“I was going to tell you.", "\"제가 막 말씀드리려던 참입니다."], ["It seems——”", "듣자 하니……\""]], [["“That his experiments have very considerably advanced the cause of science, doubtless?”", "\"그분의 실험이 과학의 대의를 상당히 진전시켰겠지요, 틀림없이?\""]], [["“No; that his style of writing is very good.”", "\"아니요, 그분의 글솜씨가 아주 뛰어나서랍니다.\""]], [["“This must be very flattering to the feelings of the rabbits into whose heads he has thrust pins, to the fowls whose bones he has dyed red, and to the dogs whose spinal marrow he has punched out?”", "\"머리에 핀이 꽂힌 토끼들, 뼈가 붉게 물든 닭들, 척수가 뽑힌 개들로서는 무척이나 우쭐한 일이겠군요?\""]], [["Albert laughed.", "알베르가 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“And the other one?” demanded the count.", "\"그럼 저 다른 분은요?\" 백작이 물었다."]], [["“That one?”", "\"저분 말입니까?\""]], [["“Yes, the third.”", "\"네, 세 번째 사람이요.\""]], [["“The one in the dark blue coat?”", "\"짙은 파란색 코트를 입은 분이요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“He is a colleague of the count, and one of the most active opponents to the idea of providing the Chamber of Peers with a uniform.", "\"저분은 가친의 동료로, 귀족원에 제복을 도입하자는 안에 가장 적극적으로 반대한 사람 가운데 하나입니다."], ["He was very successful upon that question.", "그 문제에서 큰 성공을 거두었지요."], ["He stood badly with the Liberal papers, but his noble opposition to the wishes of the court is now getting him into favor with the journalists.", "진보 성향 신문들과는 사이가 나빴지만, 궁정의 뜻에 맞선 그 고결한 반대 덕분에 지금은 언론인들의 호감을 사고 있습니다."], ["They talk of making him an ambassador.” 30303m", "그분을 대사로 앉히자는 말까지 나오고 있어요.\""]], [["“And what are his claims to the peerage?”", "\"그분이 귀족원에 들어갈 자격은 무엇입니까?\""]], [["“He has composed two or three comic operas, written four or five articles in the _Siècle_, and voted five or six years on the ministerial side.”", "\"희가극을 두세 편 작곡했고, '시에클'지에 네다섯 편의 글을 썼으며, 대여섯 해 동안 내각 편에 표를 던졌습니다.\""]], [["“Bravo, viscount,”", "\"브라보, 자작.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, smiling; “you are a delightful _cicerone_.", "몬테 크리스토가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"당신은 더없이 즐거운 안내인이군요."], ["And now you will do me a favor, will you not?”", "그런데 이제 제 부탁 하나 들어주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“What is it?”", "\"무엇입니까?\""]], [["“Do not introduce me to any of these gentlemen; and should they wish it, you will warn me.”", "\"저 신사분들 가운데 누구에게도 저를 소개하지 말아 주세요. 그리고 혹시 그분들이 소개를 원하면 제게 미리 귀띔해 주시고요.\""], ["Just then the count felt his arm pressed.", "바로 그때 백작은 누군가 자기 팔을 잡는 것을 느꼈다."], ["He turned round; it was Danglars.", "돌아보니 당글라르였다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["is it you, baron?”", "당신이군요, 남작?\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Why do you call me baron?”", "\"왜 저를 남작이라고 부르십니까?\""], ["said Danglars; “you know that I care nothing for my title.", "당글라르가 말했다. \"제가 작위 따위는 전혀 개의치 않는다는 걸 아시잖습니까."], ["I am not like you, viscount; you like your title, do you not?”", "저는 자작과는 다릅니다. 자작은 자기 작위를 좋아하지 않습니까?\""]], [["“Certainly,”", "\"물론이지요.\""], ["replied Albert, “seeing that without my title I should be nothing; while you, sacrificing the baron, would still remain the millionaire.”", "알베르가 대답했다. \"작위가 없으면 저는 아무것도 아닐 테니까요. 반면에 당신은 남작 자리를 내놓아도 여전히 백만장자로 남을 테고요.\""]], [["“Which seems to me the finest title under the royalty of July,”", "\"그게 7월 왕정 아래서는 가장 훌륭한 작위인 듯합니다만.\""], ["replied Danglars.", "당글라르가 대답했다."]], [["“Unfortunately,”", "\"안타깝게도,\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “one’s title to a millionaire does not last for life, like that of baron, peer of France, or academician; for example, the millionaires Franck & Poulmann, of Frankfurt, who have just become bankrupts.”", "몬테 크리스토가 말했다. \"백만장자라는 칭호는 남작이나 프랑스 귀족, 학사원 회원 칭호와 달리 평생 가는 게 아니지요. 이를테면 프랑크푸르트의 백만장자 프랑크 운트 포울만 상회가 방금 파산했답니다.\""]], [["“Indeed?”", "\"정말입니까?\""], ["said Danglars, becoming pale.", "당글라르가 안색이 창백해지며 말했다."]], [["“Yes; I received the news this evening by a courier.", "\"네, 오늘 저녁 급사 편으로 그 소식을 받았습니다."], ["I had about a million in their hands, but, warned in time, I withdrew it a month ago.”", "그들에게 백만쯤 맡겨 두었었는데, 제때 경고를 받아 한 달 전에 빼냈지요.\""]], [["“Ah, _mon Dieu!_”", "\"아, 맙소사!\""], ["exclaimed Danglars, “they have drawn on me for 200,000 francs!”", "당글라르가 외쳤다. \"그들이 제 앞으로 20만 프랑짜리 어음을 발행했단 말입니다!\""]], [["“Well, you can throw out the draft; their signature is worth five per cent.”", "\"그렇다면 그 어음은 던져 버리셔도 됩니다. 그들의 서명은 5퍼센트 값어치밖에 안 되니까요.\""]], [["“Yes, but it is too late,”", "\"그렇긴 합니다만, 너무 늦었습니다.\""], ["said Danglars, “I have honored their bills.”", "당글라르가 말했다. \"제가 그 어음을 결제해 버렸거든요.\""]], [["“Then,”", "\"그럼,\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “here are 200,000 francs gone after——”", "몬테 크리스토가 말했다. \"여기 20만 프랑이 또 날아가 버린 셈이군요. 앞서 날린 돈에 더해서……\""]], [["“Hush, do not mention these things,”", "\"쉿, 그런 이야기는 꺼내지 마십시오.\""], ["said Danglars; then, approaching Monte Cristo, he added, “especially before young M.", "당글라르가 말했다. 그러고는 몬테 크리스토에게 다가서며 덧붙였다."], ["Cavalcanti;” after which he smiled, and turned towards the young man in question.", "\"특히 젊은 카발칸티 씨 앞에서는요.\" 그러더니 미소를 지으며 그 청년 쪽으로 돌아섰다."]], [["Albert had left the count to speak to his mother, Danglars to converse with young Cavalcanti; Monte Cristo was for an instant alone.", "알베르는 어머니와 이야기하려고 백작 곁을 떠났고, 당글라르는 젊은 카발칸티와 대화하려고 자리를 옮겼다. 몬테 크리스토는 한순간 혼자가 되었다."], ["Meanwhile the heat became excessive. The footmen were hastening through the rooms with waiters loaded with ices.", "그러는 사이 더위가 견디기 힘들 만큼 심해졌다. 하인들이 얼음과자를 가득 실은 쟁반을 들고 방마다 분주히 오갔다."], ["Monte Cristo wiped the perspiration from his forehead, but drew back when the waiter was presented to him; he took no refreshment.", "몬테 크리스토는 이마의 땀을 닦았지만, 하인이 쟁반을 내밀자 뒤로 물러섰다. 그는 아무것도 들지 않았다."], ["Madame de Morcerf did not lose sight of Monte Cristo; she saw that he took nothing, and even noticed his gesture of refusal.", "모르세르 부인은 몬테 크리스토에게서 눈을 떼지 않고 있었다. 그가 아무것도 들지 않는 것을, 심지어 사양하는 그 몸짓까지 보았다."]], [["“Albert,”", "\"알베르,\""], ["she asked, “did you notice that?”", "부인이 물었다. \"저것 봤니?\""]], [["“What, mother?”", "\"무엇을요, 어머니?\""]], [["“That the count has never been willing to partake of food under the roof of M. de Morcerf.”", "\"백작이 모르세르 씨 댁 지붕 아래서는 결코 음식을 들려 하지 않는다는 거 말이다.\""]], [["“Yes; but then he breakfasted with me—indeed, he made his first appearance in the world on that occasion.”", "\"네, 하지만 그분은 저와 아침을 함께 들었는걸요. 사실 그분이 사교계에 처음 모습을 드러낸 것이 바로 그 자리였고요.\""]], [["“But your house is not M. de Morcerf’s,”", "\"하지만 네 집은 모르세르 씨 댁이 아니잖니.\""], ["murmured Mercédès; “and since he has been here I have watched him.”", "메르세데스가 중얼거렸다. \"여기 온 뒤로 줄곧 그분을 지켜봤단다.\" \"그래서요?\""]], [["“Well?” “Well, he has taken nothing yet.”", "\"그래서 말이다, 아직 아무것도 들지 않으셨어.\""]], [["“The count is very temperate.”", "\"백작은 무척 절제하는 분이세요.\""]], [["Mercédès smiled sadly.", "메르세데스가 서글프게 미소 지었다."]], [["“Approach him,”", "\"그분에게 다가가렴.\""], ["said she, “and when the next waiter passes, insist upon his taking something.”", "부인이 말했다. \"그리고 다음번에 하인이 지나갈 때, 뭐라도 꼭 드시도록 권해 보아라.\""]], [["“But why, mother?”", "\"하지만 왜요, 어머니?\""]], [["“Just to please me, Albert,”", "\"그저 나를 기쁘게 해 다오, 알베르.\""], ["said Mercédès.", "메르세데스가 말했다."], ["Albert kissed his mother’s hand, and drew near the count.", "알베르는 어머니의 손에 입을 맞춘 뒤 백작에게 다가갔다."], ["Another salver passed, loaded like the preceding ones; she saw Albert attempt to persuade the count, but he obstinately refused.", "앞서와 똑같이 가득 채워진 또 다른 쟁반이 지나갔다. 부인은 알베르가 백작을 설득하려 애쓰는 것을 보았지만, 백작은 완강히 거절했다."], ["Albert rejoined his mother; she was very pale.", "알베르가 어머니에게로 돌아왔다. 부인은 몹시 창백했다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"보았지?\""], ["said she, “you see he refuses?”", "부인이 말했다. \"거절하지 않니?\""]], [["“Yes; but why need this annoy you?”", "\"네, 그런데 그게 왜 어머니를 언짢게 하나요?\""]], [["“You know, Albert, women are singular creatures.", "\"알베르, 너도 알다시피 여자들이란 묘한 존재란다."], ["I should like to have seen the count take something in my house, if only an ice.", "백작이 우리 집에서 뭐라도, 얼음과자라도 드시는 모습을 보고 싶었어."], ["Perhaps he cannot reconcile himself to the French style of living, and might prefer something else.”", "어쩌면 프랑스식 생활에 익숙해지지 못해서 다른 걸 더 좋아할지도 모르지.\""]], [["“Oh, no; I have seen him eat of everything in Italy; no doubt he does not feel inclined this evening.”", "\"오, 아니에요. 이탈리아에서 그분이 무엇이든 잘 드시는 걸 봤는걸요. 틀림없이 오늘 저녁엔 그저 내키지 않으신 거예요.\""]], [["“And besides,”", "\"게다가,\""], ["said the countess, “accustomed as he is to burning climates, possibly he does not feel the heat as we do.”", "백작 부인이 말했다. \"그분은 뜨거운 기후에 익숙하니, 어쩌면 우리만큼 더위를 느끼지 않는 걸지도 모르겠구나.\""]], [["“I do not think that, for he has complained of feeling almost suffocated, and asked why the Venetian blinds were not opened as well as the windows.”", "\"그건 아닐 거예요. 거의 숨이 막힐 것 같다고 하시면서, 창문뿐 아니라 베네치아풍 덧창도 열지 않는 이유가 뭐냐고 물으셨거든요.\""]], [["“In a word,”", "\"한마디로,\""], ["said Mercédès, “it was a way of assuring me that his abstinence was intended.”", "메르세데스가 말했다. \"그분의 절식은 일부러 한 일이라고 내게 확실히 알려 준 셈이로구나.\""]], [["And she left the room.", "그러고는 방을 나섰다."]], [["A minute afterwards the blinds were thrown open, and through the jessamine and clematis that overhung the window one could see the garden ornamented with lanterns, and the supper laid under the tent.", "잠시 후 덧창이 활짝 열렸고, 창가에 늘어진 재스민과 클레마티스 사이로, 등불로 장식된 정원과 천막 아래 차려진 만찬이 보였다."], ["Dancers, players, talkers, all uttered an exclamation of joy—everyone inhaled with delight the breeze that floated in.", "춤추던 이들, 카드놀이를 하던 이들, 담소를 나누던 이들 모두가 기쁨의 탄성을 질렀다. 다들 안으로 흘러드는 산들바람을 황홀하게 들이마셨다."], ["At the same time Mercédès reappeared, paler than before, but with that imperturbable expression of countenance which she sometimes wore.", "동시에 메르세데스가 다시 나타났다. 아까보다 더 창백했지만, 이따금 짓곤 하는 그 흔들림 없는 표정을 띠고 있었다."], ["She went straight to the group of which her husband formed the centre.", "부인은 남편이 중심을 이루고 있는 무리 쪽으로 곧장 걸어갔다."]], [["“Do not detain those gentlemen here, count,”", "\"백작, 저분들을 여기 붙잡아 두지 마세요.\""], ["she said; “they would prefer, I should think, to breathe in the garden rather than suffocate here, since they are not playing.”", "부인이 말했다. \"카드놀이를 하는 것도 아니니, 여기서 숨 막혀 하느니 정원에서 바람을 쐬고 싶어 하실 거예요.\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["said a gallant old general, who, in 1809, had sung _Partant pour la Syrie_,—“we will not go alone to the garden.”", "1809년에 '시리아로 떠나며'를 불렀던 한 멋스러운 노장군이 말했다. \"우리끼리만 정원에 가지는 않겠습니다.\""]], [["“Then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["said Mercédès, “I will lead the way.”", "메르세데스가 말했다. \"제가 앞장서지요.\""]], [["Turning towards Monte Cristo, she added, “count, will you oblige me with your arm?”", "그러고는 몬테 크리스토를 향해 돌아서며 덧붙였다. \"백작, 팔을 좀 빌려주시겠어요?\""]], [["The count almost staggered at these simple words; then he fixed his eyes on Mercédès.", "이 단순한 말에 백작은 거의 비틀거릴 뻔했다. 그러고는 메르세데스에게 눈길을 고정했다."], ["It was only a momentary glance, but it seemed to the countess to have lasted for a century, so much was expressed in that one look.", "그저 한순간의 시선이었지만, 백작 부인에게는 한 세기만큼이나 길게 느껴졌다. 그 한 번의 눈길에 그토록 많은 것이 담겨 있었던 것이다. 백작이 백작 부인에게 팔을 내밀었다."], ["He offered his arm to the countess; she took it, or rather just touched it with her little hand, and they together descended the steps, lined with rhododendrons and camellias.", "부인이 그 팔을 잡았다. 아니, 차라리 작은 손으로 살짝 닿았을 뿐이었다. 그렇게 두 사람은 진달래와 동백꽃이 늘어선 계단을 함께 내려갔다."], ["Behind them, by another outlet, a group of about twenty persons rushed into the garden with loud exclamations of delight.", "그들 뒤로, 다른 출구를 통해 스무 명쯤 되는 무리가 환호성을 지르며 정원으로 쏟아져 나왔다."]], [["# Chapter 71. Bread and Salt", "# 제70장. 무도회"]], [["Madame de Morcerf entered an archway of trees with her companion.", "모르세르 부인은 일행과 함께 나무 아치 아래로 들어섰다."], ["It led through a grove of lindens to a conservatory.", "그 길은 보리수 숲을 지나 온실로 이어졌다."]], [["“It was too warm in the room, was it not, count?” she asked.", "\"방 안이 너무 더웠지요, 백작님?\" 그녀가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, madame; and it was an excellent idea of yours to open the doors and the blinds.”", "\"네, 부인. 문과 덧창을 여시다니 참 좋은 생각이셨습니다.\""], ["As he ceased speaking, the count felt the hand of Mercédès tremble. “But you,”", "말을 마치며 백작은 메르세데스의 손이 떨리는 것을 느꼈다. \"하지만 부인은,\""], ["he said, “with that light dress, and without anything to cover you but that gauze scarf, perhaps you feel cold?”", "그가 말했다. \"그렇게 얇은 옷에다 몸에 두른 것이라곤 망사 숄뿐이니, 어쩌면 추우신 게 아닙니까?\""]], [["“Do you know where I am leading you?”", "\"제가 백작님을 어디로 모시고 가는지 아세요?\""], ["said the countess, without replying to the question.", "백작 부인이 그 질문에는 대답하지 않은 채 말했다."]], [["“No, madame,”", "\"모릅니다, 부인.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo; “but you see I make no resistance.”", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"하지만 보시다시피 저는 거역하지 않습니다.\""]], [["“We are going to the greenhouse that you see at the other end of the grove.”", "\"저 숲 끝에 보이는 온실로 가는 길이에요.\""]], [["The count looked at Mercédès as if to interrogate her, but she continued to walk on in silence, and he refrained from speaking.", "백작은 묻기라도 하듯 메르세데스를 바라보았다. 그러나 그녀는 말없이 계속 걸었고, 그도 입을 다물었다."], ["They reached the building, ornamented with magnificent fruits, which ripen at the beginning of July in the artificial temperature which takes the place of the sun, so frequently absent in our climate.", "두 사람은 그 건물에 다다랐다. 그곳은 우리네 기후에서 그토록 자주 모습을 감추는 태양을 대신하는 인공의 온도로 7월 초면 익는 화려한 과일들로 장식되어 있었다."], ["The countess left the arm of Monte Cristo, and gathered a bunch of Muscatel grapes.", "백작 부인은 몬테크리스토의 팔에서 손을 떼고, 무스카텔 포도 한 송이를 땄다."]], [["“See, count,”", "\"보세요, 백작님.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, with a smile so sad in its expression that one could almost detect the tears on her eyelids—“see, our French grapes are not to be compared, I know, with yours of Sicily and Cyprus, but you will make allowance for our northern sun.”", "그 미소가 어찌나 슬퍼 보였던지, 눈꺼풀에 맺힌 눈물마저 알아챌 수 있을 듯했다. \"우리 프랑스 포도가 시칠리아나 키프로스의 백작님 포도에 견줄 수 없다는 건 저도 압니다. 하지만 우리 북쪽 나라의 태양을 헤아려 주시겠지요.\""], ["The count bowed, but stepped back.", "백작은 고개를 숙였으나, 한 걸음 물러섰다."]], [["“Do you refuse?”", "\"거절하시는 건가요?\""], ["said Mercédès, in a tremulous voice.", "메르세데스가 떨리는 목소리로 물었다."]], [["“Pray excuse me, madame,”", "\"부디 양해해 주십시오, 부인.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo, “but I never eat Muscatel grapes.”", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"저는 무스카텔 포도를 결코 먹지 않습니다.\""]], [["Mercédès let them fall, and sighed. A magnificent peach was hanging against an adjoining wall, ripened by the same artificial heat.", "메르세데스는 포도를 떨어뜨리며 한숨을 쉬었다. 곁에 있는 벽에 같은 인공의 열로 익은 탐스러운 복숭아 하나가 매달려 있었다."], ["Mercédès drew near, and plucked the fruit.", "메르세데스가 다가가 그 열매를 땄다."]], [["“Take this peach, then,”", "\"그럼 이 복숭아를 드세요.\""], ["she said. The count again refused.", "그녀가 말했다. 백작은 또다시 거절했다."], ["“What, again?”", "\"아니, 또요?\""], ["she exclaimed, in so plaintive an accent that it seemed to stifle a sob; “really, you pain me.”", "그녀가 외쳤다. 그 애처로운 목소리는 흐느낌을 억누르는 듯했다. \"정말이지, 저를 아프게 하시는군요.\""]], [["A long silence followed; the peach, like the grapes, fell to the ground.", "긴 침묵이 이어졌다. 포도가 그랬듯, 복숭아도 땅에 떨어졌다."]], [["“Count,”", "\"백작님.\""], ["added Mercédès with a supplicating glance, “there is a beautiful Arabian custom, which makes eternal friends of those who have together eaten bread and salt under the same roof.”", "메르세데스가 애원하는 눈빛으로 덧붙였다. \"아라비아에는 아름다운 풍습이 하나 있어요. 한 지붕 아래에서 빵과 소금을 함께 나눈 사람들은 영원한 벗이 된다는 풍습이지요.\""]], [["“I know it, madame,”", "\"저도 압니다, 부인.\""], ["replied the count; “but we are in France, and not in Arabia, and in France eternal friendships are as rare as the custom of dividing bread and salt with one another.”", "백작이 대답했다. \"하지만 여기는 프랑스이지 아라비아가 아닙니다. 그리고 프랑스에서는 영원한 우정이란, 빵과 소금을 서로 나누는 풍습만큼이나 드문 것이지요.\""]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["said the countess, breathlessly, with her eyes fixed on Monte Cristo, whose arm she convulsively pressed with both hands, “we are friends, are we not?”", "백작 부인이 숨을 몰아쉬며 말했다. 그녀는 몬테크리스토에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채, 두 손으로 그의 팔을 발작하듯 움켜쥐고 있었다. \"우리는 벗이지요, 그렇지 않나요?\""]], [["The count became pale as death, the blood rushed to his heart, and then again rising, dyed his cheeks with crimson; his eyes swam like those of a man suddenly dazzled.", "백작은 죽은 사람처럼 새파래졌다. 피가 심장으로 몰렸다가 다시 솟구쳐 그의 두 뺨을 진홍빛으로 물들였다. 그의 두 눈은 갑자기 눈이 부신 사람처럼 흔들렸다."]], [["“Certainly, we are friends,”", "\"물론 우리는 벗입니다.\" 그가 대답했다."], ["he replied; “why should we not be?”", "\"그렇지 않을 이유가 어디 있겠습니까?\""]], [["The answer was so little like the one Mercédès desired, that she turned away to give vent to a sigh, which sounded more like a groan.", "그 대답은 메르세데스가 바라던 것과는 너무도 동떨어진 것이어서, 그녀는 신음에 가까운 한숨을 토해 내려고 고개를 돌렸다. \"고맙습니다.\""], ["“Thank you,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["she said. And they walked on again.", "그리고 두 사람은 다시 걸음을 옮겼다."], ["They went the whole length of the garden without uttering a word.", "그들은 한마디 말도 없이 정원을 끝까지 가로질렀다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"백작님.\""], ["suddenly exclaimed the countess, after their walk had continued ten minutes in silence, “is it true that you have seen so much, travelled so far, and suffered so deeply?”", "침묵 속에 십 분쯤 걷고 난 뒤, 백작 부인이 불쑥 물었다. \"그토록 많은 것을 보고, 그토록 멀리 다니고, 그토록 깊이 고통받으셨다는 게 사실인가요?\""]], [["“I have suffered deeply, madame,”", "\"저는 깊이 고통받았습니다, 부인.\""], ["answered Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다."]], [["“But now you are happy?”", "\"하지만 지금은 행복하시지요?\""]], [["“Doubtless,”", "\"틀림없이 그렇습니다.\""], ["replied the count, “since no one hears me complain.”", "백작이 대답했다. \"제가 불평하는 걸 듣는 사람이 아무도 없으니까요.\""]], [["“And your present happiness, has it softened your heart?”", "\"그럼 지금의 그 행복이 백작님의 마음을 누그러뜨렸나요?\""]], [["“My present happiness equals my past misery,”", "\"지금의 제 행복은 지난날의 비참함과 맞먹습니다.\""], ["said the count.", "백작이 말했다."]], [["“Are you not married?”", "\"결혼은 하지 않으셨나요?\""], ["asked the countess.", "백작 부인이 물었다."]], [["“I, married?”", "\"제가, 결혼이요?\""], ["exclaimed Monte Cristo, shuddering; “who could have told you so?”", "몬테크리스토가 몸서리치며 외쳤다. \"누가 그런 말을 했습니까?\""]], [["“No one told me you were, but you have frequently been seen at the Opera with a young and lovely woman.”", "\"결혼하셨다고 말한 사람은 없어요. 하지만 백작님이 젊고 사랑스러운 여인과 함께 오페라에 계신 모습을 사람들이 여러 번 보았으니까요.\""]], [["“She is a slave whom I bought at Constantinople, madame, the daughter of a prince.", "\"그 여인은 제가 콘스탄티노플에서 산 노예입니다, 부인. 어느 왕자의 딸이지요."], ["I have adopted her as my daughter, having no one else to love in the world.”", "이 세상에 달리 사랑할 사람이 없어서, 제 딸로 거두었습니다.\""]], [["“You live alone, then?”", "\"그럼 혼자 사시나요?\""]], [["“I do.”", "\"그렇습니다.\""]], [["“You have no sister—no son—no father?”", "\"누이도, 아들도, 아버지도 없으시고요?\""]], [["“I have no one.”", "\"아무도 없습니다.\""]], [["“How can you exist thus without anyone to attach you to life?”", "\"당신을 삶에 붙들어 매어 줄 사람이 아무도 없이, 어떻게 그렇게 사실 수가 있나요?\""]], [["“It is not my fault, madame.", "\"제 탓이 아닙니다, 부인."], ["At Malta, I loved a young girl, was on the point of marrying her, when war came and carried me away.", "몰타에서 저는 한 처녀를 사랑했고, 그녀와 막 결혼하려던 참에 전쟁이 일어나 저를 끌고 갔습니다."], ["I thought she loved me well enough to wait for me, and even to remain faithful to my memory.", "저는 그녀가 저를 기다려 줄 만큼, 또 제 기억에 변함없이 충실할 만큼 저를 사랑한다고 믿었습니다."], ["When I returned she was married. This is the history of most men who have passed twenty years of age.", "그런데 돌아와 보니 그녀는 결혼한 뒤였지요. 스무 살을 넘긴 남자라면 대개 겪는 이야기입니다."], ["Perhaps my heart was weaker than the hearts of most men, and I suffered more than they would have done in my place; that is all.”", "어쩌면 제 마음이 보통 사람들의 마음보다 약했는지도 모르지요. 그래서 제 처지였다면 남들이 겪었을 것보다 더 많이 괴로워했던 것이고요. 그게 다입니다.\""]], [["The countess stopped for a moment, as if gasping for breath.", "백작 부인은 숨이 막히는 듯 잠시 멈춰 섰다. \"그래요.\""], ["“Yes,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, “and you have still preserved this love in your heart—one can only love once—and did you ever see her again?”", "\"그래서 그 사랑을 아직도 마음속에 간직하고 계시는군요. 사람은 단 한 번만 사랑할 수 있는 법이니까요. 그래서 그녀를 다시 본 적이 있으신가요?\""]], [["30309m\n“Never.”", "\"한 번도 없습니다.\""]], [["“Never?”", "\"한 번도요?\""]], [["“I never returned to the country where she lived.”", "\"저는 그녀가 살던 나라로 다시 돌아가지 않았습니다.\""]], [["“To Malta?”", "\"몰타로요?\""]], [["“Yes; Malta.”", "\"네, 몰타입니다.\""]], [["“She is, then, now at Malta?”", "\"그럼 그녀는 지금도 몰타에 있나요?\""]], [["“I think so.”", "\"그렇겠지요.\""]], [["“And have you forgiven her for all she has made you suffer?”", "\"그리고 그녀가 당신에게 안긴 그 모든 고통을, 다 용서하셨나요?\" \"그녀는, 용서했습니다.\""]], [["“Her,—yes.” “But only her; do you then still hate those who separated you?”", "\"하지만 그녀만이군요. 그럼 당신은 두 사람을 갈라놓은 자들을 아직도 미워하시나요?\""]], [["“I hate them? Not at all; why should I?”", "\"제가 그들을 미워한다고요? 천만에요."], ["The countess placed herself before Monte Cristo, still holding in her hand a portion of the perfumed grapes.", "무엇 때문에 그러겠습니까?\" 백작 부인은 향기로운 포도 한 자락을 여전히 손에 든 채, 몬테크리스토 앞을 가로막았다."]], [["“Take some,”", "\"좀 드세요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Madame, I never eat Muscatel grapes,”", "\"부인, 저는 무스카텔 포도를 결코 먹지 않습니다.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo, as if the subject had not been mentioned before.", "몬테크리스토가, 그 이야기가 앞서 한 번도 나오지 않았던 것처럼 대답했다."], ["The countess dashed the grapes into the nearest thicket, with a gesture of despair.", "백작 부인은 절망에 찬 몸짓으로 포도를 가장 가까운 덤불 속으로 내던졌다."]], [["“Inflexible man!”", "\"매정하신 분!\""], ["she murmured.", "그녀가 중얼거렸다."], ["Monte Cristo remained as unmoved as if the reproach had not been addressed to him.", "몬테크리스토는 그 책망이 자신을 향한 것이 아니기라도 한 듯, 조금도 동요하지 않은 채 서 있었다."]], [["Albert at this moment ran in.", "바로 그때 알베르가 달려 들어왔다."], ["“Oh, mother,”", "\"아, 어머니.\""], ["he exclaimed, “such a misfortune has happened!”", "그가 외쳤다. \"큰 변고가 생겼어요!\""]], [["“What? What has happened?”", "\"무슨 일이냐?"], ["asked the countess, as though awakening from a sleep to the realities of life; “did you say a misfortune?", "무슨 일이 있었어?\" 백작 부인이 잠에서 깨어나 현실로 돌아온 사람처럼 물었다. \"변고라고 했느냐?"], ["Indeed, I should expect misfortunes.”", "정말이지, 변고가 닥칠 만도 하지.\""]], [["“M. de Villefort is here.”", "\"빌포르 씨가 와 계세요.\""]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서?\""]], [["“He comes to fetch his wife and daughter.”", "\"부인과 따님을 데리러 오셨어요.\""]], [["“Why so?”", "\"어째서?\""]], [["“Because Madame de Saint-Méran is just arrived in Paris, bringing the news of M. de Saint-Méran’s death, which took place on the first stage after he left Marseilles.", "\"생메랑 부인이 막 파리에 도착하셨는데, 생메랑 씨가 돌아가셨다는 소식을 가지고 오셨거든요. 마르세유를 떠나 첫 역참에 다다랐을 때 일어난 일이래요."], ["Madame de Villefort, who was in very good spirits, would neither believe nor think of the misfortune, but Mademoiselle Valentine, at the first words, guessed the whole truth, notwithstanding all the precautions of her father; the blow struck her like a thunderbolt, and she fell senseless.”", "빌포르 부인은 아주 기분이 좋으셨던 터라, 그 변고를 믿으려고도, 떠올리려고도 하지 않으셨어요. 하지만 발랑틴 양은 아버지가 그토록 조심했는데도 첫마디에 모든 진실을 알아채고 말았어요. 그 충격이 벼락처럼 그녀를 내리쳤고, 그녀는 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌어요.\""]], [["“And how was M. de Saint-Méran related to Mademoiselle de Villefort?”", "\"그런데 생메랑 씨는 빌포르 양과 어떤 관계요?\""], ["said the count.", "백작이 물었다."]], [["“He was her grandfather on the mother’s side. He was coming here to hasten her marriage with Franz.”", "\"외할아버지세요. 발랑틴 양과 프란츠의 결혼을 서두르려고 이리로 오시던 길이셨고요.\""]], [["“Ah, indeed!”", "\"아, 그렇군!\""]], [["“So Franz must wait. Why was not M.", "\"그러니 프란츠는 기다려야겠지요."], ["de Saint-Méran also grandfather to Mademoiselle Danglars?”", "어째서 생메랑 씨는 당글라르 양의 외할아버지는 못 되셨을까요?\""]], [["“Albert, Albert,”", "\"알베르, 알베르.\""], ["said Madame de Morcerf, in a tone of mild reproof, “what are you saying?", "모르세르 부인이 부드럽게 나무라는 어조로 말했다. \"그게 무슨 말이냐?"], ["Ah, count, he esteems you so highly, tell him that he has spoken amiss.”", "아, 백작님, 이 아이가 백작님을 무척 존경하니, 말을 함부로 했다고 좀 일러 주세요.\""]], [["And she took two or three steps forward.", "그러고는 두세 걸음 앞으로 나아갔다."], ["Monte Cristo watched her with an air so thoughtful, and so full of affectionate admiration, that she turned back and grasped his hand; at the same time she seized that of her son, and joined them together.", "몬테크리스토가 어찌나 깊은 생각에 잠긴, 또 다정한 흠모로 가득 찬 표정으로 그녀를 바라보았던지, 그녀는 돌아서서 그의 손을 잡았다. 동시에 아들의 손을 잡아, 두 손을 맞잡게 했다."]], [["“We are friends; are we not?”", "\"우리는 벗이지요, 그렇지 않나요?\""], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“Oh, madame, I do not presume to call myself your friend, but at all times I am your most respectful servant.”", "\"아, 부인, 저는 감히 저를 부인의 벗이라 일컫지는 못합니다. 그러나 저는 언제나 부인의 가장 공손한 종입니다.\""], ["The countess left with an indescribable pang in her heart, and before she had taken ten steps the count saw her raise her handkerchief to her eyes.", "백작 부인은 마음속에 형언할 수 없는 아픔을 품은 채 자리를 떴고, 채 열 걸음도 떼기 전에 손수건을 들어 눈가로 가져가는 모습을 백작은 보았다."]], [["“Do not my mother and you agree?”", "\"어머니와 백작님이 뜻이 안 맞으시나요?\""], ["asked Albert, astonished.", "알베르가 놀라서 물었다."]], [["“On the contrary,”", "\"오히려 그 반대일세.\""], ["replied the count, “did you not hear her declare that we were friends?”", "백작이 대답했다. \"우리가 벗이라고 어머님께서 말씀하시는 걸 자네도 듣지 않았나?\""]], [["They re-entered the drawing-room, which Valentine and Madame de Villefort had just quitted. It is perhaps needless to add that Morrel departed almost at the same time.", "그들은 발랑틴과 빌포르 부인이 막 떠난 응접실로 다시 들어섰다. 모렐도 거의 같은 때에 자리를 떴다는 말은, 굳이 덧붙일 필요가 없을지도 모르겠다."]], [["# Chapter 72. Madame de Saint-Méran", "# Chapter 72. Madame de Saint-Méran"]], [["A gloomy scene had indeed just passed at the house of M. de Villefort.", "빌포르 씨의 집에서도 마침 음울한 일이 벌어진 참이었다. 부인들이 무도회로 떠난 뒤였다."], ["After the ladies had departed for the ball, whither all the entreaties of Madame de Villefort had failed in persuading him to accompany them, the procureur had shut himself up in his study, according to his custom, with a heap of papers calculated to alarm anyone else, but which generally scarcely satisfied his inordinate desires.", "빌포르 부인이 아무리 간청해도 그를 함께 데려가지는 못했다. 검사는 늘 그러듯 서재에 틀어박혔다. 다른 사람이라면 누구나 질겁할 만큼 산더미처럼 쌓인 서류들과 함께였지만, 그것들조차 그의 끝없는 욕망을 거의 채워 주지 못하곤 했다."]], [["But this time the papers were a mere matter of form.", "그러나 이번에는 서류가 한낱 구실에 지나지 않았다."], ["Villefort had secluded himself, not to study, but to reflect; and with the door locked and orders given that he should not be disturbed excepting for important business, he sat down in his armchair and began to ponder over the events, the remembrance of which had during the last eight days filled his mind with so many gloomy thoughts and bitter recollections.", "빌포르가 틀어박힌 것은 일을 하기 위해서가 아니라 생각에 잠기기 위해서였다. 문을 잠그고, 중요한 용무가 아니면 방해하지 말라고 일러둔 뒤, 그는 안락의자에 앉아 지난 여드레 동안 그토록 많은 음울한 상념과 쓰라린 기억으로 마음을 가득 채워 온 일들을 곱씹기 시작했다."]], [["Then, instead of plunging into the mass of documents piled before him, he opened the drawer of his desk, touched a spring, and drew out a parcel of cherished memoranda, amongst which he had carefully arranged, in characters only known to himself, the names of all those who, either in his political career, in money matters, at the bar, or in his mysterious love affairs, had become his enemies.", "그러고는 눈앞에 쌓인 서류 더미에 파고드는 대신, 책상 서랍을 열고 용수철을 눌러 소중히 간직해 온 비망록 꾸러미를 꺼냈다. 거기에는 그의 정치 인생에서든, 금전 문제에서든, 법정에서든, 아니면 은밀한 연애에서든 그의 적이 된 모든 자들의 이름이 오직 자신만이 아는 기호로 정성껏 정리되어 있었다."]], [["Their number was formidable, now that he had begun to fear, and yet these names, powerful though they were, had often caused him to smile with the same kind of satisfaction experienced by a traveller who from the summit of a mountain beholds at his feet the craggy eminences, the almost impassable paths, and the fearful chasms, through which he has so perilously climbed.", "이제 그가 두려움을 품기 시작한 마당이라, 그 수효는 만만치 않았다. 그러나 그 이름들은, 비록 막강하긴 했어도, 산 정상에서 발아래로 험준한 봉우리와 거의 지날 수 없는 길과 무시무시한 골짜기를 굽어보는 나그네의 만족감 같은 것을 그에게 안겨 주며 곧잘 미소 짓게 했다. 그가 그 길들을 그토록 위태롭게 기어올라 왔던 것이다."], ["When he had run over all these names in his memory, again read and studied them, commenting meanwhile upon his lists, he shook his head.", "그 모든 이름을 기억 속에서 훑어보고, 다시 읽고 살피며 목록에 주석을 달던 그는 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니야.\""], ["he murmured, “none of my enemies would have waited so patiently and laboriously for so long a space of time, that they might now come and crush me with this secret.", "그가 중얼거렸다. \"내 적 가운데 누구도 그토록 오랜 세월을 참을성 있게, 그토록 공들여 기다렸다가 이제 와서 이 비밀로 나를 짓밟으러 올 리는 없어."], ["Sometimes, as Hamlet says:", "햄릿의 말처럼, 이따금,"]], [["‘Foul deeds will rise,", "'추악한 행실은 떠오르는 법,"]], [["Though all the earth o’erwhelm them, to men’s eyes;’", "온 땅이 그것을 덮어 묻어도 사람들의 눈앞에.'"]], [["but, like a phosphoric light, they rise but to mislead.", "하지만 그것들은 도깨비불처럼, 길을 잘못 들게 하려고 떠오를 뿐이다."], ["The story has been told by the Corsican to some priest, who in his turn has repeated it. M.", "그 이야기는 코르시카 사람이 어느 사제에게 했고, 그 사제가 다시 옮긴 게야."], ["de Monte Cristo may have heard it, and to enlighten himself——", "몬테 크리스토 씨가 그 얘기를 들었을 수도 있고, 진상을 알아보려고—"]], [["“But why should he wish to enlighten himself upon the subject?” asked Villefort, after a moment’s reflection, “what interest can this M.", "\"하지만 그가 그 일에 관해 진상을 알아보려 할 까닭이 무엇이란 말인가?\" 빌포르가 잠시 생각한 끝에 자문했다."], ["de Monte Cristo or M. Zaccone,—son of a shipowner of Malta, discoverer of a mine in Thessaly, now visiting Paris for the first time,—what interest, I say, can he take in discovering a gloomy, mysterious, and useless fact like this?", "\"이 몬테 크리스토 씨, 아니 자코네 씨—몰타 선주의 아들이자 테살리아에서 광산을 발견하고 이제 처음으로 파리를 찾은 그자가, 이 음울하고 알 수 없고 쓸모없는 사실 하나를 캐내는 데 대체 무슨 이해관계가 있단 말인가?"], ["However, among all the incoherent details given to me by the Abbé Busoni and by Lord Wilmore, by that friend and that enemy, one thing appears certain and clear in my opinion—that in no period, in no case, in no circumstance, could there have been any contact between him and me.”", "어쨌든 부소니 신부와 윌모어 경, 그 벗과 그 적이 내게 들려준 그 모든 두서없는 이야기 가운데서도, 내 생각에 한 가지만은 분명하고 명백하다. 어느 시기에도, 어떤 경우에도, 어떤 상황에서도, 그와 나 사이에 접점이 있었을 리 없다는 것.\""]], [["But Villefort uttered words which even he himself did not believe.", "그러나 빌포르가 입에 올린 말은 그 자신조차 믿지 않는 말이었다. 그가 두려워한 것은 폭로 자체가 아니었다."], ["He dreaded not so much the revelation, for he could reply to or deny its truth;—he cared little for that _mene, mene, tekel upharsin_, which appeared suddenly in letters of blood upon the wall;—but what he was really anxious for was to discover whose hand had traced them.", "그것이라면 반박하거나 부인할 수 있었으니까. 벽에 핏빛 글자로 느닷없이 나타난 그 '메네, 메네, 테켈, 우파르신' 따위는 별로 걱정되지 않았다."], ["While he was endeavoring to calm his fears,—and instead of dwelling upon the political future that had so often been the subject of his ambitious dreams, was imagining a future limited to the enjoyments of home, in fear of awakening the enemy that had so long slept,—the noise of a carriage sounded in the yard, then he heard the steps of an aged person ascending the stairs, followed by tears and lamentations, such as servants always give vent to when they wish to appear interested in their master’s grief.", "정작 그가 애타게 알고 싶었던 것은 그 글자를 그어 놓은 손이 누구의 것이냐 하는 것이었다. 그가 두려움을 가라앉히려 애쓰며—그토록 자주 야심 찬 꿈의 주제가 되었던 정치적 미래를 곱씹는 대신, 그토록 오래 잠들어 있던 적을 깨울까 두려워하며 가정의 즐거움에 한정된 미래를 그려 보고 있을 때—마당에서 마차 소리가 울렸다. 이어 나이 든 사람이 계단을 오르는 발소리가 들렸고, 하인들이 주인의 슬픔에 관심 있는 척하려 할 때 으레 터뜨리는 울음과 탄식이 뒤따랐다."]], [["He drew back the bolt of his door, and almost directly an old lady entered, unannounced, carrying her shawl on her arm, and her bonnet in her hand.", "그가 문의 빗장을 풀자, 거의 곧바로 한 노부인이 통보도 없이 들어섰다. 숄은 팔에 걸치고 모자는 손에 쥔 채였다."], ["The white hair was thrown back from her yellow forehead, and her eyes, already sunken by the furrows of age, now almost disappeared beneath the eyelids swollen with grief.", "흰머리가 누런 이마에서 뒤로 넘겨져 있었고, 이미 세월의 주름으로 움푹 들어간 두 눈은 슬픔으로 부어오른 눈꺼풀에 가려 거의 보이지 않았다."]], [["“Oh, sir,”", "\"오, 자네.\""], ["she said; “oh, sir, what a misfortune!", "그녀가 말했다. \"오, 자네, 이 무슨 변고인가!"], ["I shall die of it; oh, yes, I shall certainly die of it!”", "나는 이 일로 죽고 말 걸세. 오, 그래, 분명히 이 일로 죽고 말 게야!\""]], [["And then, falling upon the chair nearest the door, she burst into a paroxysm of sobs.", "그러고는 문에서 가장 가까운 의자에 풀썩 주저앉아 격렬하게 흐느끼기 시작했다."], ["The servants, standing in the doorway, not daring to approach nearer, were looking at Noirtier’s old servant, who had heard the noise from his master’s room, and run there also, remaining behind the others.", "문간에 선 하인들은 감히 더 다가서지 못한 채, 누아르티에의 늙은 하인을 바라보고 있었다. 그자도 주인의 방에서 소리를 듣고 달려와 다른 이들 뒤에 서 있었다. 빌포르는 일어나 장모에게로 달려갔다."], ["Villefort rose, and ran towards his mother-in-law, for it was she.", "그 노부인이 바로 그의 장모였던 것이다."]], [["“Why, what can have happened?”", "\"아니, 무슨 일이 있으셨습니까?\""], ["he exclaimed, “what has thus disturbed you? Is M.", "그가 외쳤다. \"어쩌다 이렇게 심란해지셨습니까?"], ["de Saint-Méran with you?”", "생메랑 후작께서는 함께 계신지요?\""]], [["“M. de Saint-Méran is dead,”", "\"생메랑 후작은 죽었네.\""], ["answered the old marchioness, without preface and without expression; she appeared to be stupefied.", "늙은 후작 부인이 서두도 없이, 아무런 감정도 없이 대답했다. 그녀는 넋이 나간 듯했다."], ["Villefort drew back, and clasping his hands together, exclaimed:", "빌포르가 뒷걸음질 치며 두 손을 맞잡고 외쳤다."]], [["“Dead!—so suddenly?”", "\"돌아가셨다고요? 그렇게 갑자기요?\""]], [["“A week ago,”", "\"일주일 전이었네.\""], ["continued Madame de Saint-Méran, “we went out together in the carriage after dinner. M.", "생메랑 부인이 말을 이었다. \"우리는 저녁을 든 뒤 함께 마차를 타고 나섰지."], ["de Saint-Méran had been unwell for some days; still, the idea of seeing our dear Valentine again inspired him with courage, and notwithstanding his illness he would leave.", "생메랑 후작은 며칠 전부터 몸이 좋지 않았어. 그래도 사랑하는 발랑틴을 다시 본다는 생각에 용기를 냈고, 몸이 아픈데도 기어이 떠나려 했네."], ["At six leagues from Marseilles, after having eaten some of the lozenges he is accustomed to take, he fell into such a deep sleep, that it appeared to me unnatural; still I hesitated to wake him, although I fancied that his face was flushed, and that the veins of his temples throbbed more violently than usual.", "마르세유에서 6리외쯤 갔을 때, 늘 드시던 알약을 몇 알 드신 후작이 어찌나 깊이 잠드는지, 내겐 그게 예사롭지 않아 보였어. 그래도 얼굴이 붉어진 듯하고 관자놀이의 핏줄이 평소보다 거세게 뛰는 것 같다고 여기면서도, 차마 깨우기를 망설였지."], ["However, as it became dark, and I could no longer see, I fell asleep; I was soon aroused by a piercing shriek, as from a person suffering in his dreams, and he suddenly threw his head back violently.", "하지만 날이 어두워져 더는 보이지 않게 되자 나도 잠이 들었네. 곧 꿈속에서 괴로워하는 사람이 내지르는 듯한 날카로운 비명에 깜짝 놀라 깼는데, 후작이 별안간 머리를 거세게 뒤로 젖히지 뭔가."], ["I called the valet, I stopped the postilion, I spoke to M. de Saint-Méran, I applied my smelling-salts; but all was over, and I arrived at Aix by the side of a corpse.”", "시종을 부르고, 마부를 멈춰 세우고, 생메랑 후작에게 말을 걸고, 각성제를 갖다 대었지만, 모든 게 이미 끝나 있었네. 나는 송장 곁에 앉은 채로 엑스에 도착했지.\""]], [["Villefort stood with his mouth half open, quite stupefied.", "빌포르는 입을 반쯤 벌린 채, 완전히 넋이 나가 서 있었다."]], [["“Of course you sent for a doctor?”", "\"물론 의사를 부르셨겠지요?\""]], [["“Immediately; but, as I have told you, it was too late.”", "\"곧바로 불렀지. 하지만 말했듯이 너무 늦었더군.\""]], [["“Yes; but then he could tell of what complaint the poor marquis had died.”", "\"네, 하지만 그래도 의사가 가엾은 후작께서 무슨 병으로 돌아가셨는지는 말해 줄 수 있었을 텐데요.\""]], [["“Oh, yes, sir, he told me; it appears to have been an apoplectic stroke.”", "\"오, 그래, 자네, 말해 주더군. 졸중 발작이었던 듯하다고.\""]], [["“And what did you do then?”", "\"그래서 어떻게 하셨습니까?\""]], [["“M. de Saint-Méran had always expressed a desire, in case his death happened during his absence from Paris, that his body might be brought to the family vault.", "\"생메랑 후작은 늘, 파리를 떠나 있는 동안 죽음이 닥칠 경우 시신을 가문의 납골당으로 옮겨 달라고 했네."], ["I had him put into a leaden coffin, and I am preceding him by a few days.”", "그래서 납으로 만든 관에 넣게 하고, 나는 며칠 앞서 온 것일세.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["my poor mother!”", "가엾은 어머님!\""], ["said Villefort, “to have such duties to perform at your age after such a blow!”", "빌포르가 말했다. \"그 연세에, 그런 충격을 겪으신 뒤에 그런 일까지 치르셔야 하다니요!\""]], [["“God has supported me through all; and then, my dear marquis, he would certainly have done everything for me that I performed for him.", "\"하느님께서 그 모든 일을 견디게 해 주셨네. 게다가, 사랑하는 후작도 내가 그를 위해 한 일을 분명 나를 위해 다 해 주었을 게야."], ["It is true that since I left him, I seem to have lost my senses.", "사실 그이 곁을 떠난 뒤로 나는 제정신이 아닌 것 같아."], ["I cannot cry; at my age they say that we have no more tears,—still I think that when one is in trouble one should have the power of weeping.", "울 수도 없네. 내 나이가 되면 더는 흘릴 눈물도 없다고들 하지만, 그래도 괴로울 때는 울 수 있어야 마땅하다고 나는 생각하네."], ["Where is Valentine, sir?", "발랑틴은 어디 있나, 자네?"], ["It is on her account I am here; I wish to see Valentine.” 30315m", "그 아이 때문에 내가 여기 온 게야. 발랑틴이 보고 싶네.\""]], [["Villefort thought it would be terrible to reply that Valentine was at a ball; so he only said that she had gone out with her step-mother, and that she should be fetched.", "발랑틴이 무도회에 가 있다고 대답하기가 끔찍하게 여겨진 빌포르는, 그저 그 애가 새어머니와 함께 외출했으며 곧 데려오겠다고만 말했다."], ["“This instant, sir—this instant, I beseech you!”", "\"지금 당장, 자네, 지금 당장 부탁하네!\""], ["said the old lady.", "노부인이 말했다."], ["Villefort placed the arm of Madame de Saint-Méran within his own, and conducted her to his apartment.", "빌포르는 생메랑 부인의 팔을 자신의 팔에 끼고 자기 방으로 모셨다."]], [["“Rest yourself, mother,” he said.", "\"좀 쉬십시오, 어머님.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["The marchioness raised her head at this word, and beholding the man who so forcibly reminded her of her deeply-regretted child, who still lived for her in Valentine, she felt touched at the name of mother, and bursting into tears, she fell on her knees before an armchair, where she buried her venerable head.", "이 말에 후작 부인이 고개를 들었다. 그토록 절절히 그리워하는 딸, 발랑틴 안에 아직 살아 있는 그 딸을 강렬하게 떠올리게 하는 사내를 바라보던 그녀는, 어머님이라는 말에 마음이 뭉클해져 눈물을 쏟으며 안락의자 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 그 위에 존엄한 머리를 묻었다."], ["Villefort left her to the care of the women, while old Barrois ran, half-scared, to his master; for nothing frightens old people so much as when death relaxes its vigilance over them for a moment in order to strike some other old person.", "빌포르는 그녀를 여인들의 보살핌에 맡겼고, 늙은 바루아는 반쯤 겁에 질려 제 주인에게 달려갔다. 죽음이 한 노인을 치려고 잠시 다른 노인에게서 경계의 눈길을 거둘 때보다 노인들을 더 두렵게 하는 것은 없으니까."], ["Then, while Madame de Saint-Méran remained on her knees, praying fervently, Villefort sent for a cab, and went himself to fetch his wife and daughter from Madame de Morcerf’s.", "그런 뒤, 생메랑 부인이 무릎을 꿇은 채 간절히 기도를 올리는 동안, 빌포르는 삯마차를 불러 오게 하고는 몸소 모르세르 부인의 집으로 아내와 딸을 데리러 갔다."], ["He was so pale when he appeared at the door of the ball-room, that Valentine ran to him, saying:", "그가 무도회장 입구에 나타났을 때 어찌나 창백했던지, 발랑틴이 그에게 달려오며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, father, some misfortune has happened!”", "\"오, 아버지, 무슨 변고가 생겼군요!\""]], [["“Your grandmamma has just arrived, Valentine,”", "\"네 할머님께서 막 도착하셨다, 발랑틴.\""], ["said M. de Villefort.", "빌포르 씨가 말했다."]], [["“And grandpapa?” inquired the young girl, trembling with apprehension. M.", "\"할아버님은요?\" 어린 아가씨가 불안에 떨며 물었다."], ["de Villefort only replied by offering his arm to his daughter.", "빌포르 씨는 딸에게 팔을 내미는 것으로만 대답했다."], ["It was just in time, for Valentine’s head swam, and she staggered; Madame de Villefort instantly hastened to her assistance, and aided her husband in dragging her to the carriage, saying:", "마침맞은 일이었다. 발랑틴이 어지러워 비틀거렸던 것이다. 빌포르 부인이 곧장 달려와 거들며 남편이 딸을 마차로 끌고 가는 것을 도왔고, 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“What a singular event!", "\"이 무슨 기막힌 일인지!"], ["Who could have thought it?", "누가 짐작이나 했겠어요?"], ["Ah, yes, it is indeed strange!”", "아, 그래요, 정말이지 이상한 일이에요!\""]], [["And the wretched family departed, leaving a cloud of sadness hanging over the rest of the evening.", "그렇게 가엾은 가족은 떠나갔고, 남은 저녁 시간 위로 슬픔의 구름이 드리웠다."], ["At the foot of the stairs, Valentine found Barrois awaiting her.", "계단 아래에서 발랑틴은 바루아가 자신을 기다리고 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["“M. Noirtier wishes to see you tonight, he said, in an undertone.", "\"누아르티에 어르신께서 오늘 밤 아가씨를 뵙고자 하십니다.\" 그가 낮은 소리로 말했다."]], [["“Tell him I will come when I leave my dear grandmamma,”", "\"사랑하는 할머님 곁을 떠나거든 가겠다고 전해 주세요.\""], ["she replied, feeling, with true delicacy, that the person to whom she could be of the most service just then was Madame de Saint-Méran.", "그녀가 대답했다. 지금 자신이 가장 도움이 될 수 있는 사람은 생메랑 부인이라고, 그녀는 참된 섬세함으로 느꼈던 것이다."]], [["Valentine found her grandmother in bed; silent caresses, heartwrung sobs, broken sighs, burning tears, were all that passed in this sad interview, while Madame de Villefort, leaning on her husband’s arm, maintained all outward forms of respect, at least towards the poor widow.", "발랑틴은 할머니가 침대에 누워 있는 것을 보았다. 말없는 어루만짐, 가슴을 쥐어짜는 흐느낌, 끊어지는 한숨, 뜨거운 눈물, 이 슬픈 만남에서 오간 것은 그게 전부였다. 그동안 빌포르 부인은 남편의 팔에 기댄 채, 적어도 그 가엾은 미망인에게만은 겉으로나마 온갖 예를 갖추었다."], ["She soon whispered to her husband:", "이윽고 그녀가 남편에게 속삭였다."]], [["“I think it would be better for me to retire, with your permission, for the sight of me appears still to afflict your mother-in-law.”", "\"제가 자리를 비키는 편이 나을 것 같아요, 허락해 주신다면요. 제 모습이 아직도 어머님을 괴롭게 하는 것 같으니까요.\""], ["Madame de Saint-Méran heard her.", "생메랑 부인이 그 말을 들었다."]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"그래, 그래.\""], ["she said softly to Valentine, “let her leave; but do you stay.”", "그녀가 발랑틴에게 나직이 말했다. \"저 사람은 가게 해라. 하지만 너는 남아 있거라.\""]], [["Madame de Villefort left, and Valentine remained alone beside the bed, for the procureur, overcome with astonishment at the unexpected death, had followed his wife.", "빌포르 부인이 나갔고, 발랑틴은 침대 곁에 홀로 남았다. 뜻밖의 죽음에 놀라움을 이기지 못한 검사가 아내를 따라 나갔기 때문이다."], ["Meanwhile, Barrois had returned for the first time to old Noirtier, who having heard the noise in the house, had, as we have said, sent his old servant to inquire the cause; on his return, his quick intelligent eye interrogated the messenger.", "한편 바루아는 처음으로 늙은 누아르티에에게 돌아갔다. 앞서 말했듯, 집 안의 소란을 듣고 그 까닭을 알아보라며 늙은 하인을 보냈던 그였다. 하인이 돌아오자, 그의 빠르고 영민한 눈이 심부름꾼에게 물음을 던졌다."]], [["“Alas, sir,”", "\"아아, 어르신.\""], ["exclaimed Barrois, “a great misfortune has happened.", "바루아가 외쳤다. \"큰 변고가 생겼습니다."], ["Madame de Saint-Méran has arrived, and her husband is dead!”", "생메랑 부인께서 오셨는데, 부군께서 돌아가셨답니다!\""]], [["M. de Saint-Méran and Noirtier had never been on strict terms of friendship; still, the death of one old man always considerably affects another.", "생메랑 후작과 누아르티에는 결코 두터운 우정으로 맺어진 사이가 아니었다. 그래도 한 노인의 죽음은 언제나 다른 노인에게 적잖은 충격을 주는 법이다."], ["Noirtier let his head fall upon his chest, apparently overwhelmed and thoughtful; then he closed one eye, in token of inquiry.", "누아르티에는 압도되어 깊은 생각에 잠긴 듯 고개를 가슴께로 떨구었다. 그러더니 묻겠다는 표시로 한쪽 눈을 감았다."]], [["Barrois asked, “Mademoiselle Valentine?”", "바루아가 물었다. \"발랑틴 아가씨 말씀이십니까?\""]], [["Noirtier nodded his head.", "누아르티에가 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["“She is at the ball, as you know, since she came to say good-bye to you in full dress.”", "\"아시다시피 아가씨는 무도회에 가 계십니다. 정장 차림으로 어르신께 작별 인사를 드리러 오셨으니까요.\""], ["Noirtier again closed his left eye.", "누아르티에가 다시 왼쪽 눈을 감았다."]], [["“Do you wish to see her?”", "\"아가씨를 보고 싶으신 겁니까?\""], ["Noirtier again made an affirmative sign.", "누아르티에가 또 한 번 그렇다는 표시를 했다."]], [["“Well, they have gone to fetch her, no doubt, from Madame de Morcerf’s; I will await her return, and beg her to come up here.", "\"그럼 틀림없이 모르세르 부인 댁으로 아가씨를 데리러 갔을 테니, 아가씨가 돌아오시기를 기다렸다가 이리로 올라오시도록 청하겠습니다."], ["Is that what you wish for?”", "그렇게 하길 바라십니까?\""]], [["“Yes,” replied the invalid.", "\"그래.\" 환자가 대답했다."]], [["Barrois, therefore, as we have seen, watched for Valentine, and informed her of her grandfather’s wish.", "그리하여 앞서 보았듯, 바루아는 발랑틴을 기다렸다가 할아버지의 뜻을 알렸다."], ["Consequently, Valentine came up to Noirtier, on leaving Madame de Saint-Méran, who in the midst of her grief had at last yielded to fatigue and fallen into a feverish sleep.", "그래서 발랑틴은 생메랑 부인 곁을 떠나 누아르티에에게로 올라갔다. 부인은 슬픔에 잠긴 끝에 마침내 피로에 굴복하여 열에 들뜬 잠에 빠져 있었다."], ["Within reach of her hand they placed a small table upon which stood a bottle of orangeade, her usual beverage, and a glass.", "손이 닿는 곳에 작은 탁자를 놓아 두었는데, 그 위에는 그녀가 늘 마시는 오렌지 음료 한 병과 잔이 놓여 있었다."], ["Then, as we have said, the young girl left the bedside to see M. Noirtier.", "그런 뒤, 앞서 말했듯 어린 아가씨는 누아르티에 씨를 보러 침대 곁을 떠났다."]], [["Valentine kissed the old man, who looked at her with such tenderness that her eyes again filled with tears, whose sources he thought must be exhausted.", "발랑틴은 노인에게 입을 맞추었다. 노인이 어찌나 다정한 눈길로 바라보던지, 이제 샘이 마른 줄로만 여겼던 그녀의 눈에 다시 눈물이 그렁그렁 차올랐다."], ["The old gentleman continued to dwell upon her with the same expression.", "노신사는 변함없는 표정으로 그녀를 계속 바라보았다."]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"네, 네.\""], ["said Valentine, “you mean that I have yet a kind grandfather left, do you not.”", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"제겐 아직 다정한 할아버지가 남아 계시다는 말씀이시죠, 그렇지요?\""], ["The old man intimated that such was his meaning. “Ah, yes, happily I have,”", "노인이 바로 그런 뜻이라고 알렸다. \"아, 그래요, 다행히 그렇지요.\""], ["replied Valentine.", "발랑틴이 대답했다."], ["“Without that, what would become of me?”", "\"그게 아니라면 저는 어떻게 되겠어요?\""]], [["It was one o’clock in the morning. Barrois, who wished to go to bed himself, observed that after such sad events everyone stood in need of rest.", "새벽 한 시였다. 자신도 잠자리에 들고 싶었던 바루아는, 이런 슬픈 일을 겪은 뒤에는 누구나 휴식이 필요하다고 일렀다."], ["Noirtier would not say that the only rest he needed was to see his child, but wished her good-night, for grief and fatigue had made her appear quite ill.", "누아르티에는 자신에게 필요한 단 하나의 휴식은 손녀를 보는 것뿐이라고 말하지는 못한 채, 손녀에게 잘 자라고 인사를 건넸다. 슬픔과 피로로 그녀가 몹시 아파 보였던 것이다."]], [["The next morning she found her grandmother in bed; the fever had not abated, on the contrary her eyes glistened and she appeared to be suffering from violent nervous irritability.", "이튿날 아침, 그녀는 할머니가 침대에 누워 있는 것을 보았다. 열은 가라앉기는커녕 오히려 두 눈이 번들거렸고, 격렬한 신경의 동요로 괴로워하는 듯했다."]], [["“Oh, dear grandmamma, are you worse?” exclaimed Valentine, perceiving all these signs of agitation.", "\"오, 사랑하는 할머님, 더 안 좋으신 거예요?\" 이 모든 동요의 기색을 알아챈 발랑틴이 외쳤다."]], [["“No, my child, no,”", "\"아니다, 얘야, 아니야.\""], ["said Madame de Saint-Méran; “but I was impatiently waiting for your arrival, that I might send for your father.”", "생메랑 부인이 말했다. \"그저 네 아버지를 부르고 싶어서 네가 오기를 애타게 기다리고 있었단다.\""]], [["“My father?” inquired Valentine, uneasily.", "\"제 아버지를요?\" 발랑틴이 불안하게 물었다."]], [["“Yes, I wish to speak to him.”", "\"그래, 그이와 이야기하고 싶구나.\""]], [["Valentine durst not oppose her grandmother’s wish, the cause of which she did not know, and an instant afterwards Villefort entered.", "발랑틴은 까닭도 모른 채 할머니의 뜻을 거스를 엄두를 내지 못했고, 잠시 뒤 빌포르가 들어왔다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"여보게.\""], ["said Madame de Saint-Méran, without using any circumlocution, and as if fearing she had no time to lose, “you wrote to me concerning the marriage of this child?”", "생메랑 부인이 에두르는 법 없이, 마치 지체할 시간이 없는 사람처럼 말했다. \"이 아이의 혼사에 관해 내게 편지를 보냈었지?\""]], [["“Yes, madame,”", "\"네, 부인.\""], ["replied Villefort, “it is not only projected but arranged.”", "빌포르가 대답했다. \"그저 계획만 한 게 아니라 다 정해진 일입니다.\""]], [["“Your intended son-in-law is named M. Franz d’Épinay?”", "\"사위 될 사람이 프란츠 데피네 씨라고 했던가?\""]], [["“Yes, madame.”", "\"네, 부인.\""]], [["“Is he not the son of General d’Épinay who was on our side, and who was assassinated some days before the usurper returned from the Island of Elba?” “The same.”", "\"우리 편이었다가 찬탈자가 엘바섬에서 돌아오기 며칠 전에 암살된 그 데피네 장군의 아들 아닌가?\""]], [["“Does he not dislike the idea of marrying the granddaughter of a Jacobin?”", "\"바로 그 사람입니다.\" \"그 사람은 자코뱅의 손녀와 혼인하는 걸 꺼리지 않는다던가?\""]], [["“Our civil dissensions are now happily extinguished, mother,”", "\"우리네 내전은 이제 다행히 사그라들었습니다, 어머님.\""], ["said Villefort; “M.", "빌포르가 말했다."], ["d’Épinay was quite a child when his father died, he knows very little of M. Noirtier, and will meet him, if not with pleasure, at least with indifference.”", "\"데피네 씨는 아버지가 죽었을 때 아직 어린아이였고, 누아르티에 씨에 대해 아는 게 거의 없습니다. 기껍지는 않더라도, 적어도 무심하게는 그분을 대할 겁니다.\""]], [["“Is it a suitable match?”", "\"걸맞은 혼처인가?\""]], [["“In every respect.”", "\"모든 면에서요.\""]], [["“And the young man?”", "\"그 젊은이는?\""]], [["“Is regarded with universal esteem.”", "\"누구에게나 존경을 받지요.\""]], [["“You approve of him?”", "\"자네는 그를 인정하는가?\""]], [["“He is one of the most well-bred young men I know.”", "\"제가 아는 가장 예의 바른 젊은이 가운데 하나입니다.\""]], [["During the whole of this conversation Valentine had remained silent.", "이 모든 대화 내내 발랑틴은 말없이 있었다."]], [["“Well, sir,”", "\"좋네, 여보게.\""], ["said Madame de Saint-Méran, after a few minutes’ reflection, “I must hasten the marriage, for I have but a short time to live.”", "생메랑 부인이 잠시 생각에 잠긴 뒤 말했다. \"내 혼사를 서둘러야겠어. 내게 남은 시간이 얼마 없으니까.\""]], [["“You, madame?”", "\"부인께서요?\""], ["“You, dear mamma?”", "\"할머님께서요?\""], ["exclaimed M. de Villefort and Valentine at the same time.", "빌포르 씨와 발랑틴이 동시에 외쳤다."]], [["“I know what I am saying,”", "\"내가 무슨 말을 하는지 나는 안다.\""], ["continued the marchioness; “I must hurry you, so that, as she has no mother, she may at least have a grandmother to bless her marriage.", "후작 부인이 말을 이었다. \"내 자네를 재촉해야겠어. 어미가 없는 아이니, 적어도 혼인을 축복해 줄 할미라도 있어야 하지 않겠나."], ["I am all that is left to her belonging to my poor Renée, whom you have so soon forgotten, sir.”", "자네가 그토록 빨리 잊어버린 내 가엾은 르네에게서 그 아이에게 남은 사람은 나 하나뿐일세.\""]], [["“Ah, madame,”", "\"아, 부인.\""], ["said Villefort, “you forget that I was obliged to give a mother to my child.”", "빌포르가 말했다. \"제가 제 아이에게 어머니를 새로 마련해 줘야 했다는 걸 잊으셨군요.\""]], [["“A stepmother is never a mother, sir.", "\"새어머니는 결코 어머니가 아닐세, 여보게."], ["But this is not to the purpose,—our business concerns Valentine, let us leave the dead in peace.”", "하지만 그건 지금 논할 바가 아니야. 우리 일은 발랑틴에 관한 것이니, 죽은 이는 편히 쉬게 두세.\""]], [["All this was said with such exceeding rapidity, that there was something in the conversation that seemed like the beginning of delirium.", "이 모든 말이 어찌나 빠르게 쏟아졌던지, 그 대화에는 헛소리의 시작 같은 무언가가 어려 있었다."]], [["“It shall be as you wish, madame,”", "\"부인 뜻대로 하겠습니다.\""], ["said Villefort; “more especially since your wishes coincide with mine, and as soon as M.", "빌포르가 말했다. \"더구나 부인의 바람이 제 바람과 일치하니까요."], ["d’Épinay arrives in Paris——”", "데피네 씨가 파리에 도착하는 대로—\""]], [["30319m\n“My dear grandmother,”", "\"사랑하는 할머님.\" 발랑틴이 말을 가로막았다."], ["interrupted Valentine, “consider decorum—the recent death.", "\"예법을 생각하셔야지요. 이렇게 막 초상을 치른 마당에요."], ["You would not have me marry under such sad auspices?”", "이런 슬픈 정황 속에서 제가 혼인하기를 바라지는 않으시겠지요?\""]], [["“My child,”", "\"얘야.\""], ["exclaimed the old lady sharply, “let us hear none of the conventional objections that deter weak minds from preparing for the future.", "노부인이 매섭게 외쳤다. \"미래를 대비하는 것을 마음 약한 자들이나 주저하게 만드는 그런 관습 따위의 핑계는 듣고 싶지 않다."], ["I also was married at the death-bed of my mother, and certainly I have not been less happy on that account.”", "나도 어머니의 임종 자리에서 혼례를 올렸지만, 그렇다고 덜 행복했던 것도 결코 아니란다.\""]], [["“Still that idea of death, madame,”", "\"그래도 죽음을 입에 올리시다니요, 부인.\""], ["said Villefort.", "빌포르가 말했다."]], [["“Still?—Always!", "\"그래도라니?"], ["I tell you I am going to die—do you understand?", "늘 그렇지! 내 말하지 않았나, 나는 죽을 것이라고. 알겠나?"], ["Well, before dying, I wish to see my son-in-law.", "그러니 죽기 전에 사위를 보고 싶어."], ["I wish to tell him to make my child happy; I wish to read in his eyes whether he intends to obey me;—in fact, I will know him—I will!”", "내 아이를 행복하게 해 달라고 그에게 이르고 싶고, 그가 내 말을 따를 작정인지 그 눈에서 읽어 내고 싶네. 한마디로, 나는 그를 알아 둘 것이야. 기어이!\""], ["continued the old lady, with a fearful expression, “that I may rise from the depths of my grave to find him, if he should not fulfil his duty!”", "노부인이 무시무시한 표정으로 말을 이었다. \"그가 제 도리를 다하지 않는다면, 내 무덤 깊은 곳에서라도 일어나 그를 찾아낼 수 있도록 말일세!\""]], [["“Madame,”", "\"부인.\""], ["said Villefort, “you must lay aside these exalted ideas, which almost assume the appearance of madness.", "빌포르가 말했다. \"거의 광기처럼 보이는 그런 격앙된 생각은 떨쳐 버리셔야 합니다."], ["The dead, once buried in their graves, rise no more.”", "죽은 자는 일단 무덤에 묻히면 다시는 일어나지 못합니다.\""]], [["“And I tell you, sir, that you are mistaken.", "\"그러면 내 말하건대, 여보게, 자네가 틀렸네."], ["This night I have had a fearful sleep.", "간밤에 나는 무서운 잠을 잤어."], ["It seemed as though my soul were already hovering over my body, my eyes, which I tried to open, closed against my will, and what will appear impossible above all to you, sir, I saw, with my eyes shut, in the spot where you are now standing, issuing from that corner where there is a door leading into Madame Villefort’s dressing-room—I saw, I tell you, silently enter, a white figure.”", "마치 내 영혼이 이미 내 몸 위를 떠도는 것 같았지. 뜨려고 애써 본 두 눈은 내 뜻과 달리 감겼는데, 무엇보다도 자네에게는 도무지 믿기지 않을 일이겠지만, 나는 눈을 감은 채로 보았네. 지금 자네가 서 있는 바로 그 자리에서, 빌포르 부인의 화장실로 통하는 문이 있는 저 구석에서—내 말하건대, 나는 보았네, 소리 없이 들어서는 하얀 형체를 말일세.\""]], [["Valentine screamed.", "발랑틴이 비명을 질렀다."]], [["“It was the fever that disturbed you, madame,”", "\"열 때문에 심란해지신 겁니다, 부인.\""], ["said Villefort.", "빌포르가 말했다."]], [["30321m\n“Doubt, if you please, but I am sure of what I say.", "\"믿고 싶지 않거든 그러게나. 하지만 나는 내가 한 말을 확신하네."], ["I saw a white figure, and as if to prevent my discrediting the testimony of only one of my senses, I heard my glass removed—the same which is there now on the table.”", "나는 하얀 형체를 보았어. 그리고 마치 내가 한 가지 감각의 증언만으로는 못 미더워할까 봐 그러는 듯, 내 잔이 치워지는 소리도 들었네. 지금 저 탁자 위에 있는 바로 그 잔 말일세.\""]], [["“Oh, dear mother, it was a dream.”", "\"오, 사랑하는 할머님, 그건 꿈이었어요.\""]], [["“So little was it a dream, that I stretched my hand towards the bell; but when I did so, the shade disappeared; my maid then entered with a light.”", "\"꿈이 아니었기에, 나는 종을 향해 손을 뻗었지. 하지만 그러자 그 그림자가 사라졌고, 곧 하녀가 불을 들고 들어왔어.\""]], [["“But she saw no one?”", "\"하지만 하녀는 아무도 못 봤지요?\""]], [["“Phantoms are visible to those only who ought to see them.", "\"유령은 마땅히 보아야 할 사람의 눈에만 보이는 법이다."], ["It was the soul of my husband!—Well, if my husband’s soul can come to me, why should not my soul reappear to guard my granddaughter?", "그것은 내 남편의 영혼이었어! 그래, 내 남편의 영혼이 내게 올 수 있다면, 어찌하여 내 영혼은 손녀를 지키러 다시 나타나지 못하겠느냐?"], ["the tie is even more direct, it seems to me.”", "그 인연이 내가 보기엔 한층 더 직접적이지 않으냐.\""]], [["“Oh, madame,”", "\"오, 부인.\" 빌포르가 자신도 모르게 깊이 마음이 흔들려 말했다."], ["said Villefort, deeply affected, in spite of himself, “do not yield to those gloomy thoughts; you will long live with us, happy, loved, and honored, and we will make you forget——”", "\"그런 음울한 생각에 휘둘리지 마십시오. 부인께서는 오래도록 저희와 함께, 행복하게, 사랑받고 존경받으며 사실 겁니다. 그리고 저희가 부인으로 하여금 잊게 해 드리겠습니다—\""]], [["“Never, never, never,”", "\"결코, 결코, 결코 아닐세.\""], ["said the marchioness.", "후작 부인이 말했다."], ["“When does M. d’Épinay return?”", "\"데피네 씨는 언제 돌아오나?\""]], [["“We expect him every moment.”", "\"머지않아 올 겁니다.\""]], [["“It is well.", "\"잘됐군."], ["As soon as he arrives inform me.", "그가 도착하는 대로 내게 알리게."], ["We must be expeditious.", "우리는 서둘러야 해."], ["And then I also wish to see a notary, that I may be assured that all our property returns to Valentine.”", "그리고 나는 공증인도 보고 싶네. 우리 재산이 모두 발랑틴에게 돌아가도록 확실히 해 두고 싶으니까.\""]], [["“Ah, grandmamma,”", "\"아, 할머님.\""], ["murmured Valentine, pressing her lips on the burning brow, “do you wish to kill me?", "발랑틴이 그 뜨거운 이마에 입술을 대며 속삭였다. \"저를 죽이실 작정이세요?"], ["Oh, how feverish you are; we must not send for a notary, but for a doctor!”", "오, 이렇게 열이 나시는데. 공증인이 아니라 의사를 불러야 해요!\""]], [["“A doctor?”", "\"의사라고?\""], ["said she, shrugging her shoulders, “I am not ill; I am thirsty—that is all.”", "그녀가 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다. \"나는 병난 게 아니다."], ["30323m", "그저 목이 마를 뿐이야.\""]], [["“What are you drinking, dear grandmamma?”", "\"무얼 드시겠어요, 사랑하는 할머님?\""]], [["“The same as usual, my dear, my glass is there on the table—give it to me, Valentine.”", "\"늘 마시던 거다, 얘야. 내 잔이 저기 탁자 위에 있으니, 그걸 다오, 발랑틴.\""], ["Valentine poured the orangeade into a glass and gave it to her grandmother with a certain degree of dread, for it was the same glass she fancied that had been touched by the spectre.", "발랑틴은 오렌지 음료를 잔에 따라 할머니에게 건넸지만, 어쩐지 두려운 마음이 들었다. 유령이 손을 댔다고 할머니가 여긴 바로 그 잔이었기 때문이다."]], [["The marchioness drained the glass at a single draught, and then turned on her pillow, repeating,", "후작 부인은 단숨에 잔을 비우고는 베개 위로 돌아누우며 되뇌었다."]], [["“The notary, the notary!”", "\"공증인을, 공증인을!\""]], [["M. de Villefort left the room, and Valentine seated herself at the bedside of her grandmother.", "빌포르 씨가 방을 나갔고, 발랑틴은 할머니의 침대 곁에 앉았다."], ["The poor child appeared herself to require the doctor she had recommended to her aged relative.", "가엾은 아이는 자신이야말로, 늙은 친척에게 권했던 그 의사가 필요한 듯했다."], ["A bright spot burned in either cheek, her respiration was short and difficult, and her pulse beat with feverish excitement.", "양 볼에는 붉은 반점이 타올랐고, 호흡은 짧고 가빴으며, 맥박은 열에 들떠 뛰었다."], ["She was thinking of the despair of Maximilian, when he should be informed that Madame de Saint-Méran, instead of being an ally, was unconsciously acting as his enemy.", "그녀는 막시밀리앙의 절망을 떠올리고 있었다. 생메랑 부인이 자기편이기는커녕 자기도 모르게 그의 적 노릇을 하고 있다는 사실을 그가 알게 되면 어떻게 될까."]], [["More than once she thought of revealing all to her grandmother, and she would not have hesitated a moment, if Maximilian Morrel had been named Albert de Morcerf or Raoul de Château-Renaud; but Morrel was of plebeian extraction, and Valentine knew how the haughty Marquise de Saint-Méran despised all who were not noble.", "여러 번 그녀는 할머니에게 모든 것을 털어놓을까 생각했고, 막시밀리앙 모렐의 이름이 알베르 드 모르세르나 라울 드 샤토르노였다면 한순간도 망설이지 않았을 것이다. 그러나 모렐은 평민 출신이었고, 도도한 생메랑 후작 부인이 귀족이 아닌 모든 자를 얼마나 멸시하는지 발랑틴은 잘 알고 있었다."], ["Her secret had each time been repressed when she was about to reveal it, by the sad conviction that it would be useless to do so; for, were it once discovered by her father and mother, all would be lost.", "막 비밀을 털어놓으려 할 때마다, 그래 봐야 소용없으리라는 서글픈 확신에 그녀의 비밀은 번번이 억눌렸다. 일단 아버지와 어머니에게 발각되는 날이면, 모든 것이 끝장날 테니까."]], [["Two hours passed thus; Madame de Saint-Méran was in a feverish sleep, and the notary had arrived.", "그렇게 두 시간이 흘렀다. 생메랑 부인은 열에 들뜬 잠에 빠져 있었고, 공증인이 도착했다."], ["Though his coming was announced in a very low tone, Madame de Saint-Méran arose from her pillow.", "아주 낮은 소리로 그가 왔음을 알렸는데도, 생메랑 부인은 베개에서 몸을 일으켰다."]], [["“The notary!”", "\"공증인이!\""], ["she exclaimed, “let him come in.”", "그녀가 외쳤다. \"들어오라 하게.\""]], [["The notary, who was at the door, immediately entered. “Go, Valentine,”", "문 앞에 있던 공증인이 곧바로 들어왔다. \"가거라, 발랑틴.\""], ["said Madame de Saint-Méran, “and leave me with this gentleman.”", "생메랑 부인이 말했다. \"이분과 나만 있게 해다오.\""]], [["“But, grandmamma——”", "\"하지만, 할머님—\""]], [["“Leave me—go!”", "\"나가거라. 가!\""]], [["The young girl kissed her grandmother, and left with her handkerchief to her eyes; at the door she found the valet de chambre, who told her that the doctor was waiting in the dining-room.", "어린 아가씨는 할머니에게 입을 맞추고 손수건을 눈에 댄 채 나갔다. 문간에서 그녀는 시종을 만났는데, 그가 의사가 식당에서 기다리고 있다고 일러 주었다."], ["Valentine instantly ran down.", "발랑틴은 곧장 달려 내려갔다."], ["The doctor was a friend of the family, and at the same time one of the cleverest men of the day, and very fond of Valentine, whose birth he had witnessed.", "의사는 그 집안의 벗이자 당대의 가장 명민한 사람 가운데 하나였고, 자신이 태어나는 것을 지켜본 발랑틴을 무척 아꼈다."], ["He had himself a daughter about her age, but whose life was one continued source of anxiety and fear to him from her mother having been consumptive.", "그에게도 발랑틴 또래의 딸이 있었지만, 그 어미가 폐병을 앓았던 탓에 그 아이의 삶은 그에게 끊임없는 근심과 두려움의 원천이었다."]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오.\""], ["said Valentine, “we have been waiting for you with such impatience, dear M. d’Avrigny.", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"얼마나 애타게 선생님을 기다렸는지 몰라요, 다브리니 선생님."], ["But, first of all, how are Madeleine and Antoinette?”", "그런데 우선, 마들렌과 앙투아네트는 잘 지내나요?\""]], [["Madeleine was the daughter of M. d’Avrigny, and Antoinette his niece. M.", "마들렌은 다브리니 씨의 딸이었고, 앙투아네트는 그의 조카딸이었다."], ["d’Avrigny smiled sadly.", "다브리니 씨가 서글피 미소 지었다."]], [["“Antoinette is very well,”", "\"앙투아네트는 아주 잘 지내고.\""], ["he said, “and Madeleine tolerably so.", "그가 말했다. \"마들렌도 그럭저럭 지낸단다."], ["But you sent for me, my dear child.", "그런데 네가 나를 불렀지, 얘야."], ["It is not your father or Madame de Villefort who is ill.", "아버지나 빌포르 부인이 편찮으신 게 아니고."], ["As for you, although we doctors cannot divest our patients of nerves, I fancy you have no further need of me than to recommend you not to allow your imagination to take too wide a field.”", "너로 말하자면, 우리 의사들이 환자에게서 신경을 떼어 낼 수야 없다만, 내가 보기엔 네게 더 필요한 건, 공상이 너무 넓게 뻗어 나가도록 두지 말라는 당부 정도일 뿐인 것 같구나.\""]], [["Valentine colored. M.", "발랑틴의 얼굴이 붉어졌다."], ["d’Avrigny carried the science of divination almost to a miraculous extent, for he was one of the physicians who always work upon the body through the mind. 30325m", "다브리니 씨는 거의 기적에 가까울 만큼 꿰뚫어 보는 재주를 지녔으니, 늘 마음을 통해 몸을 다스리는 의사들 가운데 하나였던 것이다."]], [["“No,” she replied, “it is for my poor grandmother.", "\"아니에요.\" 그녀가 대답했다."], ["You know the calamity that has happened to us, do you not?”", "\"제 가엾은 할머니 때문이에요. 저희에게 닥친 불행을 아시지요, 그렇지 않나요?\""]], [["“I know nothing.”", "\"나는 아무것도 모른단다.\""], ["said M. d’Avrigny.", "다브리니 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Alas,”", "\"아아.\""], ["said Valentine, restraining her tears, “my grandfather is dead.”", "발랑틴이 눈물을 참으며 말했다. \"제 할아버지가 돌아가셨어요.\""]], [["“M. de Saint-Méran?”", "\"생메랑 후작께서?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Suddenly?”", "\"갑자기?\""]], [["“From an apoplectic stroke.”", "\"졸중 발작으로요.\""]], [["“An apoplectic stroke?”", "\"졸중 발작이라고?\""], ["repeated the doctor.", "의사가 되뇌었다."]], [["“Yes, and my poor grandmother fancies that her husband, whom she never left, has called her, and that she must go and join him.", "\"네, 그래서 가엾은 제 할머니는, 한시도 곁을 떠나지 않았던 남편이 자기를 불렀다고, 그래서 자기도 가서 함께해야 한다고 여기세요."], ["Oh, M. d’Avrigny, I beseech you, do something for her!”", "오, 다브리니 선생님, 부탁이에요, 할머니를 위해 무언가 해 주세요!\""]], [["“Where is she?”", "\"할머니는 어디 계시냐?\""]], [["“In her room with the notary.”", "\"공증인과 함께 방에 계세요.\""]], [["“And M. Noirtier?”", "\"누아르티에 씨는?\""]], [["“Just as he was, his mind perfectly clear, but the same incapability of moving or speaking.”", "\"여전하세요. 정신은 더없이 또렷하시지만, 움직이지도 말하지도 못하시는 것도 그대로고요.\""]], [["“And the same love for you—eh, my dear child?”", "\"그리고 너를 향한 그 사랑도 여전하시고, 응, 얘야?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said Valentine, “he was very fond of me.”", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"할아버지는 저를 무척 아끼셨어요.\""]], [["“Who does not love you?”", "\"너를 사랑하지 않을 사람이 어디 있겠니?\""], ["Valentine smiled sadly.", "발랑틴이 서글피 미소 지었다."], ["“What are your grandmother’s symptoms?”", "\"할머니의 증세는 어떤 것이냐?\""]], [["“An extreme nervous excitement and a strangely agitated sleep; she fancied this morning in her sleep that her soul was hovering above her body, which she at the same time watched.", "\"극심한 신경의 흥분과 이상하리만치 동요된 잠이에요. 오늘 아침에는 주무시면서 자기 영혼이 몸 위를 떠돌고, 동시에 그 몸을 내려다보고 있다고 여기셨어요."], ["It must have been delirium; she fancies, too, that she saw a phantom enter her chamber and even heard the noise it made on touching her glass.”", "분명 헛소리였을 거예요. 또 어떤 유령이 방으로 들어오는 걸 보았고, 그것이 잔을 만질 때 나는 소리까지 들었다고 하시고요.\""]], [["“It is singular,”", "\"기이한 일이로구나.\""], ["said the doctor; “I was not aware that Madame de Saint-Méran was subject to such hallucinations.”", "의사가 말했다. \"생메랑 부인께서 그런 환각에 시달리시는 줄은 몰랐는데.\""]], [["“It is the first time I ever saw her in this condition,”", "\"제가 할머니를 이런 상태로 본 건 처음이에요.\""], ["said Valentine; “and this morning she frightened me so that I thought her mad; and my father, who you know is a strong-minded man, himself appeared deeply impressed.”", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"오늘 아침엔 어찌나 저를 놀라게 하시던지, 미치신 줄로만 알았어요. 그리고 아시다시피 의지가 강한 분인 제 아버지조차 깊이 충격을 받은 듯 보이셨고요.\""]], [["“We will go and see,”", "\"가서 보자꾸나.\""], ["said the doctor; “what you tell me seems very strange.”", "의사가 말했다. \"네 말이 무척 이상하게 들리는구나.\""], ["The notary here descended, and Valentine was informed that her grandmother was alone.", "그때 공증인이 내려왔고, 발랑틴은 할머니가 혼자 계시다는 말을 들었다."]], [["“Go upstairs,”", "\"올라가 보세요.\""], ["she said to the doctor.", "그녀가 의사에게 말했다."]], [["“And you?”", "\"너는?\""]], [["“Oh, I dare not—she forbade my sending for you; and, as you say, I am myself agitated, feverish and out of sorts.", "\"오, 저는 엄두가 나질 않아요. 할머니가 선생님을 부르지 못하게 하셨거든요. 그리고 선생님 말씀대로, 저 자신이 동요되고 열이 나고 몸이 좋지 않아요."], ["I will go and take a turn in the garden to recover myself.”", "정원에 나가 한 바퀴 돌면서 마음을 추슬러야겠어요.\""]], [["The doctor pressed Valentine’s hand, and while he visited her grandmother, she descended the steps.", "의사는 발랑틴의 손을 꼭 잡고는 할머니를 보러 갔고, 그녀는 계단을 내려갔다."], ["We need not say which portion of the garden was her favorite walk.", "정원의 어느 쪽이 그녀가 가장 좋아하는 산책길인지는 굳이 말할 필요가 없으리라."], ["After remaining for a short time in the parterre surrounding the house, and gathering a rose to place in her waist or hair, she turned into the dark avenue which led to the bench; then from the bench she went to the gate.", "집을 둘러싼 화단에서 잠시 머물며 허리춤이나 머리에 꽂을 장미 한 송이를 꺾은 뒤, 그녀는 벤치로 이어지는 어두운 오솔길로 접어들었다. 그러고는 벤치에서 다시 철문 쪽으로 걸어갔다."], ["As usual, Valentine strolled for a short time among her flowers, but without gathering them.", "여느 때처럼 발랑틴은 잠시 꽃들 사이를 거닐었지만, 꽃을 꺾지는 않았다."], ["The mourning in her heart forbade her assuming this simple ornament, though she had not yet had time to put on the outward semblance of woe. 30327m", "마음속의 슬픔이 이 소박한 장식조차 허락하지 않았던 것이다. 아직 겉으로 상복을 갖춰 입을 겨를은 없었지만 말이다."]], [["She then turned towards the avenue.", "이윽고 그녀는 오솔길 쪽으로 발길을 돌렸다."], ["As she advanced she fancied she heard a voice speaking her name.", "걸음을 옮기던 그녀는 자신의 이름을 부르는 목소리가 들리는 듯했다."], ["She stopped astonished, then the voice reached her ear more distinctly, and she recognized it to be that of Maximilian.", "그녀는 놀라 멈춰 섰다. 그러자 그 목소리가 더 또렷이 귓가에 닿았고, 그녀는 그것이 막시밀리앙의 목소리임을 알아차렸다."]], [["# Chapter 73. The Promise", "# Chapter 73. The Promise"]], [["It was indeed Maximilian Morrel, who had passed a wretched existence since the previous day.", "막시밀리앙 모렐이었다. 그는 전날부터 비참한 시간을 보내고 있었다."], ["With the instinct peculiar to lovers he had anticipated after the return of Madame de Saint-Méran and the death of the marquis, that something would occur at M. de Villefort’s in connection with his attachment for Valentine.", "연인 특유의 직감으로, 생메랑 부인이 돌아오고 후작이 세상을 떠난 뒤 빌포르 씨 집안에 발랑틴을 향한 자신의 사랑과 관련된 무슨 일이 벌어지리라는 것을 그는 예감하고 있었다."], ["His presentiments were realized, as we shall see, and his uneasy forebodings had goaded him pale and trembling to the gate under the chestnut-trees.", "앞으로 보게 되겠지만 그 예감은 현실이 되었고, 불안한 예감에 떠밀린 그는 창백하게 떨면서 밤나무 아래의 철문 앞까지 와 있었다."]], [["Valentine was ignorant of the cause of this sorrow and anxiety, and as it was not his accustomed hour for visiting her, she had gone to the spot simply by accident or perhaps through sympathy.", "발랑틴은 이 슬픔과 불안의 까닭을 알지 못했다. 평소 그가 찾아오는 시간도 아니었기에, 그녀는 그저 우연히, 아니 어쩌면 마음이 통한 탓에 그곳으로 나왔다."], ["Morrel called her, and she ran to the gate.", "모렐이 부르자 그녀가 철문으로 달려왔다."]], [["“You here at this hour?” said she.", "\"이 시간에 여기에?\" 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, my poor girl,”", "\"그래, 가엾은 사람.\""], ["replied Morrel; “I come to bring and to hear bad tidings.”", "모렐이 대답했다. \"나쁜 소식을 전하러, 또 들으러 왔어.\""]], [["“This is, indeed, a house of mourning,”", "\"정말이지 이 집은 초상집이에요.\""], ["said Valentine; “speak, Maximilian, although the cup of sorrow seems already full.”", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"말해 주세요, 막시밀리앙. 슬픔의 잔이 이미 가득 찬 것 같지만요.\""]], [["“Dear Valentine,”", "\"사랑하는 발랑틴.\""], ["said Morrel, endeavoring to conceal his own emotion, “listen, I entreat you; what I am about to say is very serious.", "모렐이 자신의 동요를 감추려 애쓰며 말했다. \"부탁이니 들어 줘. 지금부터 하려는 이야기는 아주 심각한 거야."], ["When are you to be married?”", "결혼은 언제 하게 되는 거지?\""]], [["“I will tell you all,”", "\"전부 말씀드릴게요.\""], ["said Valentine; “from you I have nothing to conceal.", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"당신한테 숨길 건 아무것도 없으니까요."], ["This morning the subject was introduced, and my dear grandmother, on whom I depended as my only support, not only declared herself favorable to it, but is so anxious for it, that they only await the arrival of M.", "오늘 아침에 그 이야기가 나왔어요. 제 유일한 의지처라 믿었던 할머니께서 결혼에 찬성하셨을 뿐 아니라 너무도 간절히 바라고 계세요."], ["d’Épinay, and the following day the contract will be signed.”", "그래서 데피네 씨가 도착하기만 기다리고 있고, 그다음 날 계약서에 서명하기로 했어요.\""]], [["A deep sigh escaped the young man, who gazed long and mournfully at her he loved.", "청년의 입에서 깊은 한숨이 새어 나왔다. 그는 사랑하는 여인을 오래도록 슬프게 바라보았다."]], [["“Alas,”", "\"아아.\""], ["replied he, “it is dreadful thus to hear my condemnation from your own lips.", "그가 대답했다. \"이렇게 당신 입으로 직접 내 사형선고를 듣다니 끔찍하군."], ["The sentence is passed, and, in a few hours, will be executed; it must be so, and I will not endeavor to prevent it.", "판결은 내려졌고, 몇 시간 뒤면 집행될 거야. 그렇게 될 수밖에 없겠지. 나도 막으려 하지는 않겠어."], ["But, since you say nothing remains but for M. d’Épinay to arrive that the contract may be signed, and the following day you will be his, tomorrow you will be engaged to M.", "하지만 데피네 씨가 도착하기만 하면 계약서에 서명하고, 그다음 날 당신이 그의 사람이 된다고 했지. 그럼 내일이면 당신은 데피네 씨와 약혼하게 되는 거야."], ["d’Épinay, for he came this morning to Paris.” Valentine uttered a cry.", "그가 오늘 아침에 파리에 왔으니까.\" 발랑틴이 외마디 비명을 질렀다."]], [["“I was at the house of Monte Cristo an hour since,”", "\"한 시간 전에 나는 몬테크리스토의 집에 있었어.\""], ["said Morrel; “we were speaking, he of the sorrow your family had experienced, and I of your grief, when a carriage rolled into the courtyard.", "모렐이 말했다. \"그가 당신 가족이 겪은 슬픔에 대해, 내가 당신의 비탄에 대해 이야기를 나누고 있는데, 마차 한 대가 안마당으로 굴러 들어왔지. 그때까지 나는 예감 따위를 믿어 본 적이 없었어."], ["Never, till then, had I placed any confidence in presentiments, but now I cannot help believing them, Valentine.", "하지만 이제는 믿지 않을 수 없게 됐어, 발랑틴. 그 마차 소리에 나는 몸서리쳤어. 곧 계단을 오르는 발소리가 들렸는데, 그 발소리가 마치 돈 후안에게 들이닥친 기사단장의 발소리처럼 나를 공포에 떨게 했지."], ["At the sound of that carriage I shuddered; soon I heard steps on the staircase, which terrified me as much as the footsteps of the commander did Don Juan.", "마침내 문이 열렸어. 알베르 드 모르세르가 먼저 들어왔지. 나는 내 두려움이 헛것이었기를 바라기 시작했어."], ["The door at last opened; Albert de Morcerf entered first, and I began to hope my fears were vain, when, after him, another young man advanced, and the count exclaimed: ‘Ah, here is the Baron Franz d’Épinay!’ I summoned all my strength and courage to my support.", "그런데 그 뒤로 다른 젊은이가 들어섰고, 백작이 외쳤지. '아, 여기 프란츠 데피네 남작이 오셨군!' 나는 온 힘과 용기를 끌어모아 버텼어."], ["Perhaps I turned pale and trembled, but certainly I smiled; and five minutes after I left, without having heard one word that had passed.”", "아마 창백해지고 몸을 떨었겠지만, 분명히 미소는 지었지. 그리고 오 분 뒤에 그 자리를 떴어. 오간 말은 한마디도 듣지 못한 채로.\""]], [["“Poor Maximilian!”", "\"가엾은 막시밀리앙!\""], ["murmured Valentine.", "발랑틴이 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Valentine, the time has arrived when you must answer me.", "\"발랑틴, 이제 당신이 내게 답을 줘야 할 때야."], ["And remember my life depends on your answer.", "그리고 내 목숨이 당신 대답에 달려 있다는 걸 잊지 마."], ["What do you intend doing?”", "어떻게 할 생각이지?\""], ["Valentine held down her head; she was overwhelmed.", "발랑틴이 고개를 떨구었다. 그녀는 짓눌려 있었다."]], [["“Listen,”", "\"들어 봐.\""], ["said Morrel; “it is not the first time you have contemplated our present position, which is a serious and urgent one; I do not think it is a moment to give way to useless sorrow; leave that for those who like to suffer at their leisure and indulge their grief in secret.", "모렐이 말했다. \"당신이 지금 우리 처지를 헤아려 본 게 이번이 처음은 아니잖아. 우리 처지는 심각하고 급박해."], ["There are such in the world, and God will doubtless reward them in heaven for their resignation on earth, but those who mean to contend must not lose one precious moment, but must return immediately the blow which fortune strikes.", "지금은 쓸데없는 슬픔에 빠질 때가 아니라고 생각해. 그런 건 한가로이 고통받기를 즐기고 남몰래 비탄에 빠지기를 바라는 사람들 몫으로 남겨 두자. 세상에는 그런 사람들도 있고, 하느님께서도 분명 이 땅에서의 그 체념을 천국에서 보상해 주시겠지."], ["Do you intend to struggle against our ill-fortune?", "하지만 맞서 싸우려는 사람은 단 한순간의 귀한 시간도 잃어서는 안 돼. 운명이 가하는 일격을 그 즉시 되돌려쳐야 해."], ["Tell me, Valentine for it is that I came to know.”", "우리에게 닥친 불운에 맞서 싸울 생각이 있어? 말해 줘, 발랑틴. 그걸 알고 싶어서 온 거니까.\""]], [["Valentine trembled, and looked at him with amazement.", "발랑틴이 떨면서 놀란 눈으로 그를 바라보았다."], ["The idea of resisting her father, her grandmother, and all the family, had never occurred to her.", "아버지와 할머니, 온 집안에 저항한다는 생각은 그녀에게 한 번도 떠오른 적이 없었다."]], [["“What do you say, Maximilian?”", "\"무슨 말씀이세요, 막시밀리앙?\""], ["asked Valentine.", "발랑틴이 물었다."], ["“What do you mean by a struggle?", "\"싸운다는 게 무슨 뜻이죠?"], ["Oh, it would be a sacrilege. What?", "오, 그건 신성모독이에요. 뭐라고요?"], ["I resist my father’s order, and my dying grandmother’s wish?", "제가 아버지의 명령에, 돌아가시는 할머니의 소원에 거역하라고요?"], ["Impossible!”", "불가능해요!\""]], [["Morrel started.", "모렐이 흠칫했다."]], [["“You are too noble not to understand me, and you understand me so well that you already yield, dear Maximilian.", "\"당신은 워낙 고결해서 내 말을 이해 못 할 리가 없어. 너무도 잘 이해하기에 이미 굽히고 있는 거지, 사랑하는 막시밀리앙."], ["No, no; I shall need all my strength to struggle with myself and support my grief in secret, as you say.", "아니요, 아니요. 저 자신과 싸우고, 당신 말처럼 남몰래 슬픔을 견디려면 제 모든 힘이 필요할 거예요."], ["But to grieve my father—to disturb my grandmother’s last moments—never!”", "하지만 아버지를 슬프게 하고, 할머니의 마지막 순간을 어지럽히는 일은, 절대로 못 해요!\""]], [["“You are right,”", "\"당신 말이 맞아.\""], ["said Morrel, calmly.", "모렐이 차분하게 말했다."]], [["“In what a tone you speak!”", "\"어쩌면 그런 말투로 말하세요!\""], ["cried Valentine.", "발랑틴이 외쳤다."]], [["“I speak as one who admires you, mademoiselle.”", "\"당신을 흠모하는 사람으로서 하는 말입니다, 마드무아젤.\""]], [["“Mademoiselle,” cried Valentine; “mademoiselle!", "\"마드무아젤이라뇨.\" 발랑틴이 외쳤다."], ["Oh, selfish man!", "\"마드무아젤이라뇨!"], ["he sees me in despair, and pretends he cannot understand me!”", "오, 이기적인 사람! 절망에 빠진 저를 보면서 못 알아듣는 척하다니!\""]], [["“You mistake—I understand you perfectly.", "\"오해야. 나는 당신을 완벽하게 이해해."], ["You will not oppose M. Villefort, you will not displease the marchioness, and tomorrow you will sign the contract which will bind you to your husband.”", "당신은 빌포르 씨에게 맞서지 않을 거고, 후작 부인의 비위를 거스르지 않을 거야. 그리고 내일이면 당신을 남편에게 묶어 둘 계약서에 서명하겠지.\""]], [["“But, _mon Dieu!_ tell me, how can I do otherwise?”", "\"하지만, 맙소사! 말해 봐요, 제가 달리 어떻게 할 수 있겠어요?\""]], [["“Do not appeal to me, mademoiselle; I shall be a bad judge in such a case; my selfishness will blind me,”", "\"내게 묻지 마, 마드무아젤. 이런 일에서 나는 형편없는 판관일 거야. 내 이기심이 눈을 멀게 할 테니까.\""], ["replied Morrel, whose low voice and clenched hands announced his growing desperation.", "모렐이 대답했다. 그의 낮아진 목소리와 꽉 쥔 두 주먹이 점점 커지는 절망을 드러내고 있었다."]], [["“What would you have proposed, Maximilian, had you found me willing to accede?”", "\"제가 받아들일 마음이 있다는 걸 알았다면, 당신은 뭐라고 제안하셨을까요, 막시밀리앙?\""]], [["“It is not for me to say.”", "\"그건 내가 할 말이 아니야.\""]], [["“You are wrong; you must advise me what to do.”", "\"틀렸어요. 어떻게 해야 할지 당신이 조언해 주셔야 해요.\""]], [["“Do you seriously ask my advice, Valentine?”", "\"진심으로 내 조언을 구하는 거야, 발랑틴?\""]], [["“Certainly, dear Maximilian, for if it is good, I will follow it; you know my devotion to you.”", "\"물론이죠, 사랑하는 막시밀리앙. 좋은 조언이라면 따를 거예요. 제가 당신에게 얼마나 헌신적인지 알잖아요.\""]], [["“Valentine,”", "\"발랑틴.\""], ["said Morrel pushing aside a loose plank, “give me your hand in token of forgiveness of my anger; my senses are confused, and during the last hour the most extravagant thoughts have passed through my brain.", "모렐이 헐거워진 널빤지 하나를 밀어내며 말했다. \"내가 화낸 걸 용서한다는 표시로 손을 줘. 정신이 혼란스러워서, 지난 한 시간 동안 더없이 터무니없는 생각들이 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔어."], ["Oh, if you refuse my advice——”", "오, 당신이 내 조언을 거절한다면……\""]], [["“What do you advise?”", "\"무슨 조언인데요?\""], ["said Valentine, raising her eyes to heaven and sighing.", "발랑틴이 눈을 들어 하늘을 보며 한숨을 쉬었다."]], [["“I am free,” replied Maximilian, “and rich enough to support you.", "\"나는 자유로운 몸이야.\" 막시밀리앙이 대답했다."], ["I swear to make you my lawful wife before my lips even shall have approached your forehead.”", "\"당신을 부양할 만큼 넉넉하기도 하고. 맹세코, 내 입술이 당신 이마에 닿기도 전에 당신을 정식 아내로 맞이하겠어.\""]], [["“You make me tremble!” said the young girl.", "\"당신은 저를 떨게 하는군요!\" 처녀가 말했다."]], [["“Follow me,”", "\"나를 따라와.\""], ["said Morrel; “I will take you to my sister, who is worthy also to be yours.", "모렐이 말했다. \"내 누이에게 데려갈게. 그녀도 당신의 누이가 될 자격이 있는 사람이야."], ["We will embark for Algiers, for England, for America, or, if you prefer it, retire to the country and only return to Paris when our friends have reconciled your family.”", "우리는 알제로, 영국으로, 미국으로 배를 타고 떠나는 거야. 아니면 당신이 원한다면 시골에 들어가 살다가, 친구들이 당신 가족을 화해시킨 뒤에야 파리로 돌아오면 돼.\""]], [["Valentine shook her head.", "발랑틴이 고개를 저었다."]], [["“I feared it, Maximilian,”", "\"그럴 줄 알았어요, 막시밀리앙.\""], ["said she; “it is the counsel of a madman, and I should be more mad than you, did I not stop you at once with the word ‘Impossible, Morrel, impossible!’”", "그녀가 말했다. \"그건 미친 사람의 조언이에요. 제가 그 자리에서 '불가능해요, 모렐, 불가능해요!'라는 말로 당신을 막지 않는다면, 저는 당신보다 더 미친 사람일 거예요.\""]], [["“You will then submit to what fate decrees for you without even attempting to contend with it?”", "\"그럼 당신은 운명이 정해 준 것에, 맞서 싸워 보려는 시도조차 없이 순순히 따르겠다는 거야?\""], ["said Morrel sorrowfully.", "모렐이 슬프게 말했다."]], [["“Yes,—if I die!”", "\"네, 죽는 한이 있더라도요!\""]], [["“Well, Valentine,”", "\"좋아, 발랑틴.\""], ["resumed Maximilian, “I can only say again that you are right.", "막시밀리앙이 다시 말했다. \"그럼 나는 당신이 옳다는 말밖에는 할 수가 없군."], ["Truly, it is I who am mad, and you prove to me that passion blinds the most well-meaning.", "정말이지 미친 사람은 나야. 더없이 선의를 가진 사람조차 격정에 눈이 먼다는 걸 당신이 내게 증명해 보이는군."], ["I appreciate your calm reasoning.", "나는 당신의 차분한 분별력을 높이 평가해."], ["It is then understood that tomorrow you will be irrevocably promised to M.", "그러니까 정리하자면, 내일 당신은 프란츠 데피네 씨와 돌이킬 수 없이 약혼하게 되는 거야."], ["Franz d’Épinay, not only by that theatrical formality invented to heighten the effect of a comedy called the signature of the contract, but your own will?”", "계약서 서명이라 부르는, 희극의 효과를 높이려고 고안된 그 연극 같은 형식 절차에 의해서뿐 아니라, 당신 자신의 의지로 말이야?\""]], [["“Again you drive me to despair, Maximilian,”", "\"또 저를 절망으로 몰아넣는군요, 막시밀리앙.\""], ["said Valentine, “again you plunge the dagger into the wound!", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"또 상처에 비수를 꽂는군요!"], ["What would you do, tell me, if your sister listened to such a proposition?”", "말해 봐요, 당신 누이가 이런 제안을 받는다면 당신은 어떻게 하겠어요?\""]], [["“Mademoiselle,”", "\"마드무아젤.\""], ["replied Morrel with a bitter smile, “I am selfish—you have already said so—and as a selfish man I think not of what others would do in my situation, but of what I intend doing myself.", "모렐이 쓰디쓴 미소를 지으며 대답했다. \"나는 이기적인 사람이야. 당신이 이미 그렇게 말했지."], ["I think only that I have known you not a whole year. From the day I first saw you, all my hopes of happiness have been in securing your affection.", "그리고 이기적인 사람으로서, 내 처지에 놓인다면 남들이 어떻게 할지는 생각하지 않고, 나 자신이 무엇을 할 작정인지만 생각해. 나는 그저 당신을 안 지 채 일 년도 되지 않았다는 것만 생각해."], ["One day you acknowledged that you loved me, and since that day my hope of future happiness has rested on obtaining you, for to gain you would be life to me.", "당신을 처음 본 그날부터 내 모든 행복의 희망은 당신의 애정을 얻는 데 걸려 있었어. 어느 날 당신은 나를 사랑한다고 고백했고, 그날 이후로 내 미래의 행복에 대한 희망은 당신을 얻는 데 달려 있게 됐지."], ["Now, I think no more; I say only that fortune has turned against me—I had thought to gain heaven, and now I have lost it.", "당신을 얻는 것이 내게는 곧 삶이니까. 이제 나는 더는 아무것도 생각하지 않아. 다만 운명이 내게 등을 돌렸다는 것만 말할 뿐이야."], ["It is an every-day occurrence for a gambler to lose not only what he possesses but also what he has not.”", "천국을 얻을 줄 알았는데 이제 그걸 잃었지. 도박꾼이 가진 것뿐 아니라 가지지도 않은 것까지 잃는 건 날마다 일어나는 흔한 일이야.\""]], [["Morrel pronounced these words with perfect calmness; Valentine looked at him a moment with her large, scrutinizing eyes, endeavoring not to let Morrel discover the grief which struggled in her heart.", "모렐은 이 말을 완벽하게 차분한 어조로 했다. 발랑틴은 크고 살피는 듯한 두 눈으로 잠시 그를 바라보며, 가슴속에서 몸부림치는 슬픔을 모렐이 알아채지 못하도록 애썼다."]], [["“But, in a word, what are you going to do?”", "\"하지만, 한마디로 말해서, 당신은 어떻게 할 작정인가요?\""], ["asked she.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“I am going to have the honor of taking my leave of you, mademoiselle, solemnly assuring you that I wish your life may be so calm, so happy, and so fully occupied, that there may be no place for me even in your memory.”", "\"마드무아젤, 영광스럽게도 당신께 작별 인사를 드릴 작정입니다. 그리고 당신의 삶이 너무도 평온하고, 너무도 행복하고, 너무도 충만하게 채워져, 당신의 기억 속에조차 내가 들어설 자리가 없기를 진심으로 바란다고 엄숙히 말씀드릴 작정입니다.\""]], [["“Oh!”", "\"오!\""], ["murmured Valentine.", "발랑틴이 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Adieu, Valentine, adieu!” said Morrel, bowing.", "\"안녕, 발랑틴, 안녕!\" 모렐이 고개를 숙이며 말했다."]], [["“Where are you going?”", "\"어디로 가는 거예요?\" 처녀가 외쳤다."], ["cried the young girl, extending her hand through the opening, and seizing Maximilian by his coat, for she understood from her own agitated feelings that her lover’s calmness could not be real; “where are you going?”", "그녀는 틈새로 손을 뻗어 막시밀리앙의 외투를 붙잡았다. 자신의 격앙된 감정으로 미루어, 연인의 차분함이 진짜일 리 없다는 것을 알아챈 것이다. \"어디로 가는 거예요?\""]], [["“I am going, that I may not bring fresh trouble into your family: and to set an example which every honest and devoted man, situated as I am, may follow.”", "\"가는 거야. 당신 가족에게 새로운 분란을 가져오지 않으려고. 그리고 나와 같은 처지에 놓인 모든 정직하고 헌신적인 사람이 따를 만한 본보기를 남기려고.\""]], [["“Before you leave me, tell me what you are going to do, Maximilian.”", "\"나를 떠나기 전에, 무엇을 할 작정인지 말해 줘요, 막시밀리앙.\""], ["The young man smiled sorrowfully.", "청년이 슬프게 미소 지었다."]], [["“Speak, speak!”", "\"말해 줘요, 말해 줘요!\""], ["said Valentine; “I entreat you.”", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"제발 부탁이에요.\""]], [["“Has your resolution changed, Valentine?”", "\"당신의 결심이 바뀌었어, 발랑틴?\""]], [["“It cannot change, unhappy man; you know it must not!”", "\"바뀔 수 없어요, 불행한 사람아. 바뀌어선 안 된다는 걸 당신도 알잖아요!\""], ["cried the young girl.", "처녀가 외쳤다."]], [["“Then adieu, Valentine!”", "\"그럼 안녕, 발랑틴!\""]], [["Valentine shook the gate with a strength of which she could not have been supposed to be possessed, as Morrel was going away, and passing both her hands through the opening, she clasped and wrung them.", "모렐이 떠나려 하자, 발랑틴은 도저히 가졌으리라 짐작할 수 없는 힘으로 철문을 흔들었다. 그리고 두 손을 틈새로 내밀어 그의 손을 맞잡고는 비틀듯 꼭 쥐었다."], ["“I must know what you mean to do!” said she.", "\"당신이 무엇을 할 작정인지 알아야겠어요!\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["“Where are you going?”", "\"어디로 가는 거예요?\""]], [["“Oh, fear not,”", "\"오, 두려워 마.\""], ["said Maximilian, stopping at a short distance, “I do not intend to render another man responsible for the rigorous fate reserved for me.", "막시밀리앙이 조금 떨어진 곳에서 멈추며 말했다. \"내게 예비된 가혹한 운명을 다른 사람에게 떠넘길 생각은 없어."], ["Another might threaten to seek M. Franz, to provoke him, and to fight with him; all that would be folly.", "다른 사람 같으면 프란츠 씨를 찾아가, 그를 자극해, 결투를 벌이겠다고 위협할지도 모르지. 하지만 그건 다 어리석은 짓이야."], ["What has M. Franz to do with it?", "프란츠 씨가 이 일과 무슨 상관이 있겠어?"], ["He saw me this morning for the first time, and has already forgotten he has seen me.", "그는 오늘 아침에 나를 처음 봤고, 봤다는 사실조차 벌써 잊었을 거야."], ["He did not even know I existed when it was arranged by your two families that you should be united.", "당신네 두 집안이 당신들의 결합을 정했을 때, 그는 내가 존재하는지조차 몰랐어."], ["I have no enmity against M. Franz, and promise you the punishment shall not fall on him.”", "나는 프란츠 씨에게 아무런 원한이 없고, 그에게 벌이 떨어지지는 않을 거라고 약속할게.\""]], [["“On whom, then!—on me?”", "\"그럼 누구에게요? 저에게요?\""]], [["“On you? Valentine!", "\"당신에게? 발랑틴!"], ["Oh, Heaven forbid!", "오, 천만에!"], ["Woman is sacred; the woman one loves is holy.”", "여자는 신성해. 사랑하는 여자는 거룩한 존재야.\""]], [["“On yourself, then, unhappy man; on yourself?”", "\"그럼 당신 자신에게요, 불행한 사람아. 당신 자신에게요?\""]], [["“I am the only guilty person, am I not?”", "\"죄가 있는 사람은 나 하나뿐이잖아, 안 그래?\""], ["said Maximilian.", "막시밀리앙이 말했다."]], [["“Maximilian!”", "\"막시밀리앙!\""], ["said Valentine, “Maximilian, come back, I entreat you!”", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"막시밀리앙, 돌아와요, 제발 부탁이에요!\""]], [["He drew near with his sweet smile, and but for his paleness one might have thought him in his usual happy mood.", "그가 다정한 미소를 지으며 다가왔다. 창백한 얼굴만 아니라면 평소의 행복한 기분에 잠긴 사람으로 보일 만했다."]], [["“Listen, my dear, my adored Valentine,”", "\"들어 봐, 내 사랑, 내가 흠모하는 발랑틴.\""], ["said he in his melodious and grave tone; “those who, like us, have never had a thought for which we need blush before the world, such may read each other’s hearts.", "그가 음악처럼 낭랑하면서도 진중한 어조로 말했다. \"우리처럼, 세상 앞에 부끄러워할 만한 생각을 단 한 번도 품어 본 적 없는 사람들은 서로의 마음을 읽을 수 있어. 나는 한 번도 낭만적인 사람이었던 적이 없고, 우울한 영웅도 아니야."], ["I never was romantic, and am no melancholy hero.", "만프레드도 앤서니도 흉내 내지 않아."], ["I imitate neither Manfred nor Anthony; but without words, protestations, or vows, my life has entwined itself with yours; you leave me, and you are right in doing so,—I repeat it, you are right; but in losing you, I lose my life.", "하지만 말도, 항변도, 맹세도 없이, 내 삶은 당신의 삶과 얽혀 버렸어. 당신은 나를 떠나고, 그렇게 하는 게 옳아. 다시 말하지만 당신이 옳아."], ["The moment you leave me, Valentine, I am alone in the world.", "하지만 당신을 잃으면, 나는 내 삶을 잃는 거야. 당신이 나를 떠나는 그 순간, 발랑틴, 나는 이 세상에 홀로 남아."], ["My sister is happily married; her husband is only my brother-in-law, that is, a man whom the ties of social life alone attach to me; no one then longer needs my useless life.", "내 누이는 다행히 결혼해서 잘 살고, 매부는 그저 매부일 뿐, 다시 말해 사회생활의 끈으로만 내게 묶여 있는 사람이지. 그러니 내 쓸모없는 목숨을 더는 누구도 필요로 하지 않아. 내가 하려는 일은 이거야."], ["This is what I shall do; I will wait until the very moment you are married, for I will not lose the shadow of one of those unexpected chances which are sometimes reserved for us, since M.", "나는 당신이 결혼하는 바로 그 순간까지 기다릴 거야. 우리에게 때로 남겨지는 그 뜻밖의 기회들 가운데 그 그림자만큼이라도 놓치고 싶지 않으니까. 어쩌면 프란츠 씨가 결국 그전에 죽을 수도 있고, 당신이 제단에 다가서는 순간 벼락이 떨어질 수도 있어."], ["Franz may, after all, die before that time, a thunderbolt may fall even on the altar as you approach it,—nothing appears impossible to one condemned to die, and miracles appear quite reasonable when his escape from death is concerned.", "죽음을 선고받은 사람에게는 불가능해 보이는 게 아무것도 없고, 죽음에서 벗어나는 일이라면 기적도 지극히 그럴듯해 보이는 법이지. 그러니 마지막 순간까지 기다릴 거야. 그리고 내 불행이 확실해지고, 돌이킬 수 없고, 희망이 없어지면, 매부에게 한 통, 경찰청장에게 또 한 통, 내 결심을 알리는 비밀 편지를 쓸 거야."], ["I will, then, wait until the last moment, and when my misery is certain, irremediable, hopeless, I will write a confidential letter to my brother-in-law, another to the prefect of police, to acquaint them with my intention, and at the corner of some wood, on the brink of some abyss, on the bank of some river, I will put an end to my existence, as certainly as I am the son of the most honest man who ever lived in France.” 30333m", "그리고 어느 숲 모퉁이에서, 어느 심연의 가장자리에서, 어느 강가에서, 내 목숨을 끝낼 거야. 내가 프랑스에서 살았던 가장 정직한 사람의 아들이라는 사실만큼이나 분명하게.\""]], [["Valentine trembled convulsively; she loosened her hold of the gate, her arms fell by her side, and two large tears rolled down her cheeks.", "발랑틴이 발작하듯 떨었다. 철문을 잡았던 손이 풀리고, 두 팔이 옆구리로 떨어졌으며, 굵은 눈물 두 줄기가 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸다."], ["The young man stood before her, sorrowful and resolute.", "청년은 슬프지만 단호한 모습으로 그녀 앞에 서 있었다."]], [["“Oh, for pity’s sake,”", "\"오, 제발 자비를 베풀어요.\""], ["said she, “you will live, will you not?”", "그녀가 말했다. \"당신, 살아 줄 거죠, 그렇죠?\""]], [["“No, on my honor,”", "\"아니, 내 명예를 걸고 말하지만.\""], ["said Maximilian; “but that will not affect you.", "막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"하지만 그건 당신과 상관없는 일이야."], ["You have done your duty, and your conscience will be at rest.”", "당신은 당신의 도리를 다했고, 양심에 거리낄 게 없을 테니까.\""]], [["Valentine fell on her knees, and pressed her almost bursting heart.", "발랑틴이 무릎을 꿇고, 터질 듯한 가슴을 부여잡았다. \"막시밀리앙.\""], ["“Maximilian,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “Maximilian, my friend, my brother on earth, my true husband in heaven, I entreat you, do as I do, live in suffering; perhaps we may one day be united.”", "\"막시밀리앙, 내 친구, 이 땅에서의 내 오빠, 하늘에서의 내 진정한 남편이여, 제발 부탁이니, 나처럼 해 줘요. 고통 속에서라도 살아 줘요. 어쩌면 언젠가 우리가 맺어질 수도 있잖아요.\""]], [["“Adieu, Valentine,”", "\"안녕, 발랑틴.\""], ["repeated Morrel.", "모렐이 되풀이했다."]], [["“My God,”", "\"오, 하느님.\""], ["said Valentine, raising both her hands to heaven with a sublime expression, “I have done my utmost to remain a submissive daughter; I have begged, entreated, implored; he has regarded neither my prayers, my entreaties, nor my tears.", "발랑틴이 숭고한 표정으로 두 손을 하늘로 들어 올리며 말했다. \"저는 순종하는 딸로 남으려고 할 수 있는 모든 것을 다 했어요. 빌고, 애원하고, 매달렸어요."], ["It is done,” cried she, wiping away her tears, and resuming her firmness, “I am resolved not to die of remorse, but rather of shame.", "그런데 그는 제 기도도, 제 애원도, 제 눈물도 거들떠보지 않았어요. 됐어요.\" 그녀가 눈물을 닦으며 다시 단호함을 되찾아 외쳤다."], ["Live, Maximilian, and I will be yours.", "\"저는 회한으로 죽기보다는 차라리 수치로 죽기로 결심했어요. 살아 줘요, 막시밀리앙. 저는 당신 사람이 되겠어요."], ["Say when shall it be?", "말해 봐요, 언제로 할까요?"], ["Speak, command, I will obey.”", "말해 줘요, 명령해 줘요, 따를게요.\""]], [["Morrel, who had already gone some few steps away, again returned, and pale with joy extended both hands towards Valentine through the opening.", "이미 몇 걸음 멀어졌던 모렐이 다시 돌아왔다. 그리고 기쁨에 창백해진 얼굴로 틈새 너머 발랑틴을 향해 두 손을 내밀었다."]], [["“Valentine,”", "\"발랑틴.\""], ["said he, “dear Valentine, you must not speak thus—rather let me die.", "그가 말했다. \"사랑하는 발랑틴, 그렇게 말하면 안 돼. 차라리 나를 죽게 해 줘."], ["Why should I obtain you by violence, if our love is mutual?", "우리 사랑이 서로를 향한 것이라면, 내가 어찌 강압으로 당신을 얻겠어?"], ["Is it from mere humanity you bid me live? I would then rather die.”", "단지 인정 때문에 내게 살라고 하는 거야? 그렇다면 나는 차라리 죽는 편이 나아.\""]], [["“Truly,”", "\"정말로.\""], ["murmured Valentine, “who on this earth cares for me, if he does not?", "발랑틴이 중얼거렸다. \"이 세상에서 그이 말고 누가 나를 아끼겠어?"], ["Who has consoled me in my sorrow but he?", "내 슬픔 속에서 그이 말고 누가 나를 위로해 줬지?"], ["On whom do my hopes rest?", "내 희망은 누구에게 걸려 있지?"], ["On whom does my bleeding heart repose?", "피 흘리는 내 마음은 누구에게 기대지?"], ["On him, on him, always on him! Yes, you are right, Maximilian, I will follow you.", "그이에게, 그이에게, 언제나 그이에게! 그래요, 당신이 옳아요, 막시밀리앙."], ["I will leave the paternal home, I will give up all.", "당신을 따르겠어요. 친정을 떠나겠어요, 전부 버리겠어요."], ["Oh, ungrateful girl that I am,”", "오, 배은망덕한 계집인 나는.\""], ["cried Valentine, sobbing, “I will give up all, even my dear old grandfather, whom I had nearly forgotten.”", "발랑틴이 흐느끼며 외쳤다. \"전부 버리겠어요, 하마터면 잊을 뻔한 사랑하는 할아버지까지도요.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니야.\""], ["said Maximilian, “you shall not leave him. M.", "막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"할아버지를 두고 떠나서는 안 돼."], ["Noirtier has evinced, you say, a kind feeling towards me.", "당신 말로는 누아르티에 씨가 내게 호의를 보이셨다고 했지."], ["Well, before you leave, tell him all; his consent would be your justification in God’s sight.", "좋아, 떠나기 전에 할아버지께 전부 말씀드려. 할아버지의 동의가 하느님 눈앞에서 당신의 정당함이 되어 줄 거야."], ["As soon as we are married, he shall come and live with us, instead of one child, he shall have two.", "우리가 결혼하면, 할아버지께서 우리와 함께 사시게 하자. 자식이 하나가 아니라 둘이 생기는 거지. 당신이 할아버지와 어떻게 대화하고 할아버지가 어떻게 대답하는지 내게 말해 줬잖아."], ["You have told me how you talk to him and how he answers you; I shall very soon learn that language by signs, Valentine, and I promise you solemnly, that instead of despair, it is happiness that awaits us.”", "나도 그 신호 언어를 곧 익히겠어, 발랑틴. 그리고 엄숙히 약속할게. 우리를 기다리는 건 절망이 아니라 행복이라고.\""]], [["“Oh, see, Maximilian, see the power you have over me, you almost make me believe you; and yet, what you tell me is madness, for my father will curse me—he is inflexible—he will never pardon me.", "\"오, 보세요, 막시밀리앙, 당신이 제게 얼마나 큰 힘을 발휘하는지 보세요. 하마터면 당신 말을 믿을 뻔했어요. 그렇지만 당신 말은 미친 소리예요."], ["Now listen to me, Maximilian; if by artifice, by entreaty, by accident—in short, if by any means I can delay this marriage, will you wait?”", "아버지는 저를 저주하실 거고, 워낙 완고한 분이라 절대로 용서하지 않으실 거니까요. 자, 들어 보세요, 막시밀리앙. 제가 꾀로든, 애원으로든, 우연으로든, 한마디로 무슨 수를 써서든 이 결혼을 미룰 수 있다면, 당신은 기다려 줄 건가요?\""]], [["“Yes, I promise you, as faithfully as you have promised me that this horrible marriage shall not take place, and that if you are dragged before a magistrate or a priest, you will refuse.”", "\"그래, 약속할게. 이 끔찍한 결혼이 성사되지 않으리라는 것, 그리고 당신이 판사나 사제 앞에 끌려가더라도 거부하리라는 것을 당신이 내게 충실히 약속한 만큼이나 충실하게.\""]], [["“I promise you by all that is most sacred to me in the world, namely, by my mother.”", "\"이 세상에서 제게 가장 신성한 것을 걸고, 다시 말해 제 어머니를 걸고 약속할게요.\""]], [["“We will wait, then,”", "\"그럼 우리는 기다리는 거야.\""], ["said Morrel.", "모렐이 말했다."]], [["“Yes, we will wait,”", "\"네, 우리는 기다려요.\""], ["replied Valentine, who revived at these words; “there are so many things which may save unhappy beings such as we are.”", "그 말에 되살아난 발랑틴이 대답했다. \"우리 같은 불행한 사람들을 구해 줄 수 있는 일들이 아주 많으니까요.\""]], [["“I rely on you, Valentine,”", "\"나는 당신을 믿어, 발랑틴.\" 모렐이 말했다."], ["said Morrel; “all you do will be well done; only if they disregard your prayers, if your father and Madame de Saint-Méran insist that M. d’Épinay should be called tomorrow to sign the contract——”", "\"당신이 하는 일은 다 잘될 거야. 다만 그들이 당신의 간청을 무시하고, 당신 아버지와 생메랑 부인이 내일 데피네 씨를 불러 계약서에 서명하라고 고집한다면……\""]], [["“Then you have my promise, Maximilian.”", "\"그땐 당신께 한 제 약속이 있잖아요, 막시밀리앙.\""]], [["“Instead of signing——”", "\"서명하는 대신에……\""]], [["“I will go to you, and we will fly; but from this moment until then, let us not tempt Providence, let us not see each other.", "\"당신께 가겠어요. 그리고 우리는 달아나는 거예요. 하지만 지금부터 그때까지는, 하늘을 시험하지 말아요, 서로 만나지 말아요."], ["It is a miracle, it is a providence that we have not been discovered.", "우리가 들키지 않은 건 기적이고, 하늘의 섭리예요."], ["If we were surprised, if it were known that we met thus, we should have no further resource.”", "들키기라도 하면, 우리가 이렇게 만난다는 게 알려지기라도 하면, 우리에겐 더 이상 방도가 없어요.\""]], [["“You are right, Valentine; but how shall I ascertain?”", "\"당신 말이 맞아, 발랑틴. 그런데 어떻게 알아내지?\""]], [["“From the notary, M. Deschamps.”", "\"공증인 데샹 씨에게서요.\""]], [["“I know him.”", "\"그분이라면 나도 알아.\""]], [["“And for myself—I will write to you, depend on me.", "\"그리고 제 쪽에서도 편지를 쓸게요. 저를 믿어요."], ["I dread this marriage, Maximilian, as much as you.”", "저도 당신만큼이나 이 결혼이 두려워요, 막시밀리앙.\""]], [["“Thank you, my adored Valentine, thank you; that is enough. When once I know the hour, I will hasten to this spot, you can easily get over this fence with my assistance, a carriage will await us at the gate, in which you will accompany me to my sister’s; there living, retired or mingling in society, as you wish, we shall be enabled to use our power to resist oppression, and not suffer ourselves to be put to death like sheep, which only defend themselves by sighs.”", "\"고마워, 내가 흠모하는 발랑틴, 고마워. 그거면 충분해. 시간을 알게 되면, 곧장 이곳으로 달려올게. 당신은 내 도움으로 이 울타리를 쉽게 넘을 수 있을 거고, 철문 앞에 마차가 기다리고 있을 거야. 그 마차로 당신은 나와 함께 내 누이 집으로 가는 거지. 거기서 당신이 원하는 대로 숨어 살든, 사람들과 어울려 살든, 우리는 우리의 힘을 써서 억압에 맞설 수 있을 거야. 한숨으로밖에 자신을 지키지 못하는 양처럼 죽임을 당하지는 않을 거야.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said Valentine, “I will now acknowledge you are right, Maximilian; and now are you satisfied with your betrothal?”", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"이제 당신이 옳다는 걸 인정하겠어요, 막시밀리앙. 그래, 이제 당신의 약혼이 만족스러우세요?\""], ["said the young girl sorrowfully.", "처녀가 슬프게 말했다."]], [["“My adored Valentine, words cannot express one half of my satisfaction.”", "\"내가 흠모하는 발랑틴, 내 만족의 절반도 말로는 다 표현할 수 없어.\""]], [["Valentine had approached, or rather, had placed her lips so near the fence, that they nearly touched those of Morrel, which were pressed against the other side of the cold and inexorable barrier.", "발랑틴이 다가왔다. 아니, 정확히는 울타리에 너무도 가까이 입술을 갖다 대어, 차갑고 무정한 그 장벽 반대편에 눌린 모렐의 입술과 거의 닿을 듯했다."]], [["“Adieu, then, till we meet again,”", "\"그럼, 다시 만날 때까지 안녕히.\""], ["said Valentine, tearing herself away.", "발랑틴이 몸을 떼어 내며 말했다."], ["“I shall hear from you?”", "\"소식 주실 거죠?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["“Thanks, thanks, dear love, adieu!”", "\"고마워요, 고마워요, 내 사랑, 안녕히!\""]], [["The sound of a kiss was heard, and Valentine fled through the avenue.", "입맞춤 소리가 들렸고, 발랑틴은 가로수 길을 따라 달아났다."], ["Morrel listened to catch the last sound of her dress brushing the branches, and of her footstep on the gravel, then raised his eyes with an ineffable smile of thankfulness to heaven for being permitted to be thus loved, and then also disappeared.", "모렐은 그녀의 옷자락이 나뭇가지를 스치는 마지막 소리와 자갈을 밟는 발소리를 잡으려 귀 기울였다. 그러고는 이렇게 사랑받도록 허락받은 데 대한 형언할 수 없는 감사의 미소를 띠고 하늘로 눈을 들었다가, 그 역시 자취를 감추었다."]], [["The young man returned home and waited all the evening and all the next day without getting any message.", "청년은 집으로 돌아와 그날 저녁 내내, 그리고 이튿날 온종일 아무 전갈도 받지 못한 채 기다렸다."], ["It was only on the following day, at about ten o’clock in the morning, as he was starting to call on M. Deschamps, the notary, that he received from the postman a small billet, which he knew to be from Valentine, although he had not before seen her writing.", "그다음 날 아침 열 시쯤, 공증인 데샹 씨를 찾아가려고 막 나서던 참에야, 그는 우체부에게서 작은 쪽지 한 장을 받았다. 전에 그녀의 필체를 본 적은 없었지만, 그는 그것이 발랑틴이 보낸 것임을 단번에 알았다."], ["It was to this effect:", "내용은 이러했다."]], [["“Tears, entreaties, prayers, have availed me nothing.", "\"눈물도, 간청도, 기도도 제게 아무 소용이 없었어요."], ["Yesterday, for two hours, I was at the church of Saint-Philippe-du-Roule, and for two hours I prayed most fervently.", "어제 저는 두 시간 동안 생필리프뒤룰 성당에 있으면서, 두 시간 내내 더없이 간절하게 기도했어요."], ["Heaven is as inflexible as man, and the signature of the contract is fixed for this evening at nine o’clock.", "하늘은 사람만큼이나 완고했고, 계약서 서명은 오늘 저녁 아홉 시로 정해졌어요."], ["I have but one promise and but one heart to give; that promise is pledged to you, that heart is also yours.", "제게는 줄 약속이 하나뿐이고, 줄 마음도 하나뿐이에요. 그 약속은 당신께 바쳤고, 그 마음도 당신 것이에요."], ["This evening, then, at a quarter to nine at the gate.", "그러니 오늘 저녁 아홉 시 십오 분 전에 철문 앞에서."]], [["“Your betrothed,", "당신의 약혼녀,"]], [["“Valentine de Villefort.”", "발랑틴 드 빌포르.\""]], [["“P.S.—My poor grandmother gets worse and worse; yesterday her fever amounted to delirium; today her delirium is almost madness.", "\"추신. 가엾은 할머니는 점점 더 나빠지세요. 어제는 열이 올라 헛소리를 하셨고, 오늘은 그 헛소리가 거의 광증에 가까워요."], ["You will be very kind to me, will you not, Morrel, to make me forget my sorrow in leaving her thus?", "이렇게 할머니를 두고 떠나는 제 슬픔을 잊게 해 주시려고 제게 무척 다정하게 대해 주실 거죠, 그렇죠, 모렐?"], ["I think it is kept a secret from grandpapa Noirtier, that the contract is to be signed this evening.”", "계약서가 오늘 저녁 서명된다는 사실은 누아르티에 할아버지께는 비밀에 부쳐진 것 같아요.\""]], [["Morrel went also to the notary, who confirmed the news that the contract was to be signed that evening.", "모렐은 공증인에게도 들렀고, 공증인은 계약서가 그날 저녁 서명될 예정이라는 소식을 확인해 주었다."], ["Then he went to call on Monte Cristo and heard still more.", "그런 다음 그는 몬테크리스토를 찾아가서 더 많은 이야기를 들었다."], ["Franz had been to announce the ceremony, and Madame de Villefort had also written to beg the count to excuse her not inviting him; the death of M.", "프란츠가 예식을 알리러 왔었고, 빌포르 부인도 그를 초대하지 못하는 것을 양해해 달라고 백작에게 편지를 보내왔다는 것이었다."], ["de Saint-Méran and the dangerous illness of his widow would cast a gloom over the meeting which she would regret should be shared by the count whom she wished every happiness.", "생메랑 씨의 죽음과 그 미망인의 위중한 병환이 모임에 어두운 그림자를 드리울 텐데, 온갖 행복을 빌어 마지않는 백작이 그런 자리를 함께하게 되는 것은 부인으로서 안타까운 일이라는 이유였다."]], [["The day before Franz had been presented to Madame de Saint-Méran, who had left her bed to receive him, but had been obliged to return to it immediately after.", "전날 프란츠는 생메랑 부인에게 인사를 올렸다. 부인은 그를 맞으려고 자리에서 일어났으나, 곧바로 다시 누울 수밖에 없었다."]], [["It is easy to suppose that Morrel’s agitation would not escape the count’s penetrating eye.", "모렐의 동요가 백작의 꿰뚫는 눈을 벗어나지 못했으리라는 것은 쉽게 짐작할 수 있다."], ["Monte Cristo was more affectionate than ever,—indeed, his manner was so kind that several times Morrel was on the point of telling him all.", "몬테크리스토는 그 어느 때보다 다정했다. 정말이지 그의 태도가 어찌나 친절했던지, 모렐은 몇 번이나 모든 것을 털어놓을 뻔했다."], ["But he recalled the promise he had made to Valentine, and kept his secret.", "하지만 발랑틴에게 한 약속을 떠올리고는 비밀을 지켰다."]], [["The young man read Valentine’s letter twenty times in the course of the day. It was her first, and on what an occasion!", "청년은 그날 하루 동안 발랑틴의 편지를 스무 번이나 읽었다. 그녀의 첫 편지였고, 게다가 그런 상황에서의 편지였다!"], ["Each time he read it he renewed his vow to make her happy. How great is the power of a woman who has made so courageous a resolution!", "읽을 때마다 그는 그녀를 행복하게 해 주겠다는 맹세를 새로이 다졌다. 이토록 용감한 결심을 한 여인의 힘은 얼마나 위대한가!"], ["What devotion does she deserve from him for whom she has sacrificed everything!", "자신을 위해 모든 것을 희생한 여인에게 그는 얼마나 헌신해야 마땅한가!"], ["How ought she really to be supremely loved!", "그녀는 정말이지 더없이 지극하게 사랑받아 마땅했다!"], ["She becomes at once a queen and a wife, and it is impossible to thank and love her sufficiently.", "그녀는 단번에 여왕이자 아내가 되었으니, 아무리 감사하고 사랑해도 부족할 따름이었다."]], [["Morrel longed intensely for the moment when he should hear Valentine say, “Here I am, Maximilian; come and help me.”", "모렐은 발랑틴이 \"저 여기 있어요, 막시밀리앙. 와서 도와줘요.\" 하고 말하는 순간을 애타게 기다렸다."], ["He had arranged everything for her escape; two ladders were hidden in the clover-field; a cabriolet was ordered for Maximilian alone, without a servant, without lights; at the turning of the first street they would light the lamps, as it would be foolish to attract the notice of the police by too many precautions.", "그는 그녀의 탈출을 위해 모든 것을 준비해 두었다. 사다리 두 개를 클로버 밭에 숨겨 두었고, 막시밀리앙 혼자만 탈 이륜마차를 하인도 등불도 없이 불러 두었다. 첫 번째 거리 모퉁이를 돌면 등에 불을 밝힐 작정이었다."], ["Occasionally he shuddered; he thought of the moment when, from the top of that wall, he should protect the descent of his dear Valentine, pressing in his arms for the first time her of whom he had yet only kissed the delicate hand.", "너무 많은 조심을 부려 경찰의 눈길을 끄는 것은 어리석은 짓일 테니까. 이따금 그는 몸서리쳤다. 저 담장 꼭대기에서 사랑하는 발랑틴이 내려오는 것을 지켜 주고, 아직 그 가녀린 손에만 입 맞춰 보았던 그녀를 처음으로 두 팔에 안을 그 순간을 떠올렸던 것이다."]], [["When the afternoon arrived and he felt that the hour was drawing near, he wished for solitude, his agitation was extreme; a simple question from a friend would have irritated him.", "오후가 되어 그 시간이 다가오고 있음을 느끼자, 그는 혼자 있고 싶어졌다. 동요가 극에 달해, 친구의 사소한 질문 하나에도 짜증이 날 지경이었다."], ["He shut himself in his room, and tried to read, but his eye glanced over the page without understanding a word, and he threw away the book, and for the second time sat down to sketch his plan, the ladders and the fence.", "그는 방에 틀어박혀 책을 읽으려 했지만, 눈길은 한 단어도 이해하지 못한 채 책장 위를 미끄러져 갈 뿐이었다. 그는 책을 내던지고, 두 번째로 자리에 앉아 사다리와 울타리가 그려진 자신의 계획을 다시 그려 보았다."]], [["At length the hour drew near.", "마침내 그 시간이 가까워졌다."], ["Never did a man deeply in love allow the clocks to go on peacefully.", "깊이 사랑에 빠진 남자가 시계를 평화롭게 가도록 내버려 둔 적은 없는 법이다."], ["Morrel tormented his so effectually that they struck eight at half-past six.", "모렐은 어찌나 시계를 들볶았던지, 시계는 여섯 시 반에 여덟 번을 쳤다."], ["He then said, “It is time to start; the signature was indeed fixed to take place at nine o’clock, but perhaps Valentine will not wait for that.”", "그러자 그가 말했다. \"이제 출발할 시간이야. 서명은 분명 아홉 시로 정해졌지만, 어쩌면 발랑틴이 그때까지 기다리지 않을지도 몰라.\""], ["Consequently, Morrel, having left the Rue Meslay at half-past eight by his timepiece, entered the clover-field while the clock of Saint-Philippe-du-Roule was striking eight.", "그리하여 모렐은 자기 시계로 여덟 시 반에 멜레 거리를 나섰고, 생필리프뒤룰 성당의 종이 여덟 번을 칠 때 클로버 밭으로 들어섰다."], ["The horse and cabriolet were concealed behind a small ruin, where Morrel had often waited.", "말과 이륜마차는 작은 폐허 뒤에 숨겨 두었는데, 모렐이 자주 기다리던 곳이었다."]], [["The night gradually drew on, and the foliage in the garden assumed a deeper hue.", "밤이 차츰 깊어 갔고, 정원의 나뭇잎들은 한층 짙은 빛을 띠었다."], ["Then Morrel came out from his hiding-place with a beating heart, and looked through the small opening in the gate; there was yet no one to be seen.", "그러자 모렐은 두근거리는 가슴으로 숨어 있던 곳에서 나와, 철문의 작은 틈으로 안을 들여다보았다. 아직 아무도 보이지 않았다."]], [["The clock struck half-past eight, and still another half-hour was passed in waiting, while Morrel walked to and fro, and gazed more and more frequently through the opening.", "종이 여덟 시 반을 쳤고, 또 한 시간의 절반이 기다림 속에 지나갔다. 그동안 모렐은 이리저리 거닐며 점점 더 자주 틈새로 안을 들여다보았다."], ["The garden became darker still, but in the darkness he looked in vain for the white dress, and in the silence he vainly listened for the sound of footsteps.", "정원은 더욱 어두워졌지만, 그 어둠 속에서 그가 찾는 흰 드레스는 보이지 않았고, 그 정적 속에서 그가 기다리는 발소리도 헛되이 귀를 기울일 뿐이었다."], ["The house, which was discernible through the trees, remained in darkness, and gave no indication that so important an event as the signature of a marriage-contract was going on.", "나무들 사이로 어렴풋이 보이는 집은 여전히 어둠에 잠겨, 혼인 계약서 서명 같은 중대한 일이 진행되고 있다는 기미는 조금도 비치지 않았다."], ["Morrel looked at his watch, which wanted a quarter to ten; but soon the same clock he had already heard strike two or three times rectified the error by striking half-past nine.", "모렐은 회중시계를 들여다보았다. 열 시 십오 분 전을 가리키고 있었다. 그러나 곧, 이미 두세 번 치는 소리를 들었던 그 시계가 아홉 시 반을 쳐서 그 오차를 바로잡았다."]], [["This was already half an hour past the time Valentine had fixed.", "이미 발랑틴이 정한 시간보다 삼십 분이 지난 셈이었다."], ["It was a terrible moment for the young man.", "청년에게는 끔찍한 순간이었다."], ["The slightest rustling of the foliage, the least whistling of the wind, attracted his attention, and drew the perspiration to his brow; then he tremblingly fixed his ladder, and, not to lose a moment, placed his foot on the first step.", "나뭇잎이 사부작거리는 가장 미세한 소리에도, 바람이 휘파람처럼 스치는 가장 작은 소리에도 그의 신경이 곤두섰고, 이마에는 땀이 배어 나왔다. 그러면 그는 떨면서 사다리를 세웠고, 한순간도 허비하지 않으려고 첫 번째 가로대에 발을 올렸다."], ["Amidst all these alternations of hope and fear, the clock struck ten.", "이 모든 희망과 두려움의 교차 속에서 종이 열 시를 쳤다."], ["“It is impossible,”", "\"불가능해.\""], ["said Maximilian, “that the signing of a contract should occupy so long a time without unexpected interruptions.", "막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"계약서 서명이 뜻밖의 방해 없이 이렇게 오래 걸릴 리가 없어."], ["I have weighed all the chances, calculated the time required for all the forms; something must have happened.”", "나는 온갖 경우의 수를 따져 봤고, 모든 절차에 드는 시간을 계산해 봤어. 무슨 일이 생긴 게 분명해.\""]], [["And then he walked rapidly to and fro, and pressed his burning forehead against the fence.", "그러고는 빠르게 이리저리 거닐며, 불타는 이마를 울타리에 갖다 댔다."], ["Had Valentine fainted?", "발랑틴이 기절했을까?"], ["or had she been discovered and stopped in her flight?", "아니면 들켜서 도망치다 붙잡혔을까?"], ["These were the only obstacles which appeared possible to the young man.", "청년에게 떠오를 수 있는 장애는 이 두 가지뿐이었다."]], [["The idea that her strength had failed her in attempting to escape, and that she had fainted in one of the paths, was the one that most impressed itself upon his mind.", "도망치려다 힘이 빠져, 어느 길목에서 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌으리라는 생각이 그의 마음을 가장 짓눌렀다. \"그렇다면.\""], ["“In that case,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “I should lose her, and by my own fault.”", "\"나는 그녀를 잃게 되겠지. 그것도 내 잘못으로.\""], ["He dwelt on this idea for a moment, then it appeared reality.", "그는 잠시 이 생각에 매달렸고, 이내 그것이 현실처럼 여겨졌다."], ["He even thought he could perceive something on the ground at a distance; he ventured to call, and it seemed to him that the wind wafted back an almost inarticulate sigh.", "멀리 땅바닥에 무언가가 있는 것처럼 보이기까지 했다. 그는 용기를 내어 불러 보았는데, 바람이 거의 알아들을 수 없는 한숨 소리를 실어 돌려보내는 듯했다."]], [["At last the half-hour struck.", "마침내 종이 삼십 분을 쳤다."], ["It was impossible to wait longer, his temples throbbed violently, his eyes were growing dim; he passed one leg over the wall, and in a moment leaped down on the other side.", "더는 기다릴 수 없었다. 관자놀이가 격렬하게 뛰었고, 눈앞이 흐려졌다. 그는 한쪽 다리를 담장 위로 넘기더니, 순식간에 반대편으로 뛰어내렸다."], ["He was on Villefort’s premises—had arrived there by scaling the wall.", "그는 빌포르의 부지 안에 있었다. 담을 넘어 들어온 것이었다."], ["What might be the consequences?", "그 결과가 어떨까?"], ["However, he had not ventured thus far to draw back.", "그러나 여기까지 와서 물러설 그가 아니었다."], ["He followed a short distance close under the wall, then crossed a path, hid entered a clump of trees.", "그는 담장에 바짝 붙어 잠시 걸은 다음, 길을 가로질러 나무 덤불 속으로 들어갔다."], ["In a moment he had passed through them, and could see the house distinctly.", "순식간에 그것을 통과하자, 집이 또렷이 보였다."]], [["30341m\nThen Morrel saw that he had been right in believing that the house was not illuminated.", "그제야 모렐은 집에 불이 켜져 있지 않으리라는 자신의 짐작이 옳았음을 알았다."], ["Instead of lights at every window, as is customary on days of ceremony, he saw only a gray mass, which was veiled also by a cloud, which at that moment obscured the moon’s feeble light.", "예식이 있는 날 으레 그렇듯 창마다 불이 켜져 있는 대신, 그는 잿빛 덩어리만을 보았는데, 그마저도 그 순간 달의 희미한 빛을 가린 구름에 뒤덮여 있었다."], ["A light moved rapidly from time to time past three windows of the second floor.", "이따금 불빛 하나가 이 층의 세 창문 앞을 빠르게 스쳐 지나갔다."], ["These three windows were in Madame de Saint-Méran’s room.", "그 세 창은 생메랑 부인의 방에 있는 것이었다."], ["Another remained motionless behind some red curtains which were in Madame de Villefort’s bedroom.", "또 다른 불빛 하나는 빌포르 부인의 침실에 드리운 붉은 커튼 뒤에서 움직임 없이 머물러 있었다."], ["Morrel guessed all this.", "모렐은 이 모든 것을 알아챘다."], ["So many times, in order to follow Valentine in thought at every hour in the day, had he made her describe the whole house, that without having seen it he knew it all.", "하루의 매 순간마다 발랑틴을 생각 속에서 좇기 위해, 그는 수도 없이 그녀에게 집 전체를 묘사하게 했었다. 그래서 직접 본 적은 없어도 그는 그 모든 것을 알고 있었다."]], [["This darkness and silence alarmed Morrel still more than Valentine’s absence had done.", "이 어둠과 정적은 발랑틴이 나타나지 않은 것보다 더욱 모렐을 불안하게 했다."], ["Almost mad with grief, and determined to venture everything in order to see Valentine once more, and be certain of the misfortune he feared, Morrel gained the edge of the clump of trees, and was going to pass as quickly as possible through the flower-garden, when the sound of a voice, still at some distance, but which was borne upon the wind, reached him.", "슬픔에 거의 미칠 지경이 되어, 발랑틴을 한 번만 더 보고, 자신이 두려워하는 불행을 확인하기 위해서라면 무슨 일이든 무릅쓰기로 결심한 모렐은 나무 덤불 가장자리에 이르렀고, 되도록 빨리 화원을 가로지르려던 참이었다. 그때 아직 멀었지만 바람을 타고 실려 온 목소리 하나가 그의 귀에 닿았다."], ["At this sound, as he was already partially exposed to view, he stepped back and concealed himself completely, remaining perfectly motionless.", "그 소리에, 이미 어느 정도 모습이 드러나 있던 그는 뒷걸음질 쳐 완전히 몸을 숨기고, 미동도 없이 가만히 있었다."]], [["He had formed his resolution.", "그는 결심을 굳혔다."], ["If it was Valentine alone, he would speak as she passed; if she was accompanied, and he could not speak, still he should see her, and know that she was safe; if they were strangers, he would listen to their conversation, and might understand something of this hitherto incomprehensible mystery.", "발랑틴 혼자라면, 그녀가 지나갈 때 말을 걸 것이다. 누군가와 함께 있어 말을 걸 수 없더라도, 그래도 그녀를 보고, 그녀가 무사하다는 것을 알 수는 있을 것이다. 낯선 사람들이라면, 그들의 대화에 귀 기울여 여태 알 수 없던 이 수수께끼의 무언가를 알아낼 수 있을지도 몰랐다."]], [["The moon had just then escaped from behind the cloud which had concealed it, and Morrel saw Villefort come out upon the steps, followed by a gentleman in black.", "마침 그때 달이 자신을 가리고 있던 구름 뒤에서 빠져나왔고, 모렐은 빌포르가 계단으로 나오는 것을 보았다. 그 뒤로 검은 옷을 입은 신사가 따라 나왔다."], ["They descended, and advanced towards the clump of trees, and Morrel soon recognized the other gentleman as Doctor d’Avrigny. 30339m", "그들은 계단을 내려와 나무 덤불 쪽으로 다가왔고, 모렐은 곧 그 다른 신사가 다브리니 의사임을 알아보았다."]], [["The young man, seeing them approach, drew back mechanically, until he found himself stopped by a sycamore-tree in the centre of the clump; there he was compelled to remain.", "청년은 그들이 다가오는 것을 보고 기계적으로 뒷걸음질 쳤다. 그러다 덤불 한가운데 있는 플라타너스 나무에 막혀 멈춰 섰고, 그곳에 머무를 수밖에 없었다."], ["Soon the two gentlemen stopped also.", "곧 두 신사도 걸음을 멈췄다."]], [["“Ah, my dear doctor,”", "\"아, 의사 양반.\""], ["said the procureur, “Heaven declares itself against my house!", "검사가 말했다. \"하늘이 우리 집안에 등을 돌렸소!"], ["What a dreadful death—what a blow!", "이 무슨 끔찍한 죽음이며, 이 무슨 타격이오!"], ["Seek not to console me; alas, nothing can alleviate so great a sorrow—the wound is too deep and too fresh!", "나를 위로하려 들지 마시오. 아, 이토록 큰 슬픔은 무엇으로도 누그러뜨릴 수 없소. 상처가 너무 깊고 너무 생생하오!"], ["Dead, dead!”", "돌아가시다니, 돌아가시다니!\""]], [["The cold sweat sprang to the young man’s brow, and his teeth chattered.", "청년의 이마에 식은땀이 솟았고, 이가 딱딱 부딪쳤다."], ["Who could be dead in that house, which Villefort himself had called accursed?", "빌포르 자신이 저주받았다고 부른 저 집에서 누가 죽었단 말인가?"]], [["“My dear M. de Villefort,”", "\"친애하는 빌포르 씨.\""], ["replied the doctor, with a tone which redoubled the terror of the young man, “I have not led you here to console you; on the contrary——”", "의사가 청년의 공포를 곱절로 키우는 어조로 대답했다. \"내가 당신을 이리로 데려온 것은 위로하기 위해서가 아니오. 오히려 그 반대요……\""]], [["“What can you mean?”", "\"무슨 말씀이오?\""], ["asked the procureur, alarmed.", "검사가 놀라 물었다."]], [["“I mean that behind the misfortune which has just happened to you, there is another, perhaps, still greater.”", "\"방금 당신에게 닥친 불행 뒤에, 어쩌면 그보다 더 큰 또 다른 불행이 있다는 말이오.\""]], [["“Can it be possible?”", "\"그럴 수가 있단 말이오?\""], ["murmured Villefort, clasping his hands.", "빌포르가 두 손을 맞잡으며 중얼거렸다."], ["“What are you going to tell me?”", "\"내게 무슨 말을 하려는 거요?\""]], [["“Are we quite alone, my friend?”", "\"우리 정말 단둘뿐이오, 여보시오?\""]], [["“Yes, quite; but why all these precautions?”", "\"그렇소, 단둘뿐이오. 하지만 어째서 이렇게까지 조심하는 거요?\""]], [["“Because I have a terrible secret to communicate to you,”", "\"당신에게 끔찍한 비밀을 전해야 하기 때문이오.\""], ["said the doctor.", "의사가 말했다."], ["“Let us sit down.”", "\"앉읍시다.\""]], [["Villefort fell, rather than seated himself.", "빌포르는 앉았다기보다 주저앉았다."], ["The doctor stood before him, with one hand placed on his shoulder.", "의사는 그 앞에 서서 한 손을 그의 어깨에 얹었다."], ["Morrel, horrified, supported his head with one hand, and with the other pressed his heart, lest its beatings should be heard.", "공포에 질린 모렐은 한 손으로 머리를 받치고, 다른 손으로는 가슴을 눌렀다. 심장 뛰는 소리가 들릴까 봐서였다. \"돌아가셨어, 돌아가셨어!\""], ["“Dead, dead!” repeated he within himself; and he felt as if he were also dying.", "그는 속으로 되뇌었다. 자기 자신마저 죽어 가는 듯한 기분이었다."]], [["“Speak, doctor—I am listening,”", "\"말하시오, 의사 양반. 듣고 있소.\""], ["said Villefort; “strike—I am prepared for everything!”", "빌포르가 말했다. \"내리치시오. 무엇이든 각오가 되어 있으니!\""]], [["“Madame de Saint-Méran was, doubtless, advancing in years, but she enjoyed excellent health.”", "\"생메랑 부인은 분명 연세가 많으셨소. 하지만 더없이 건강하셨소.\""], ["Morrel began again to breathe freely, which he had not done during the last ten minutes.", "모렐은 다시 자유롭게 숨을 쉬기 시작했다. 지난 십 분 동안 하지 못했던 일이었다."]], [["“Grief has consumed her,”", "\"슬픔이 그분을 갉아먹은 거요.\""], ["said Villefort—“yes, grief, doctor!", "빌포르가 말했다. \"그렇소, 슬픔이오, 의사 양반!"], ["After living forty years with the marquis——”", "후작과 사십 년을 함께 사신 분이니……\""]], [["“It is not grief, my dear Villefort,”", "\"슬픔이 아니오, 친애하는 빌포르 씨.\" 의사가 말했다."], ["said the doctor; “grief may kill, although it rarely does, and never in a day, never in an hour, never in ten minutes.”", "\"슬픔이 사람을 죽일 수도 있소. 드물긴 하지만 말이오. 그러나 하루 만에, 한 시간 만에, 십 분 만에 죽이는 법은 결코 없소.\""], ["Villefort answered nothing, he simply raised his head, which had been cast down before, and looked at the doctor with amazement.", "빌포르는 아무 대답도 하지 못하고, 그저 떨구었던 고개를 들어 놀란 눈으로 의사를 바라보았다."]], [["“Were you present during the last struggle?”", "\"마지막 사투의 순간에 곁에 계셨소?\""], ["asked M. d’Avrigny.", "다브리니 씨가 물었다."]], [["“I was,”", "\"있었소.\""], ["replied the procureur; “you begged me not to leave.”", "검사가 대답했다. \"당신이 떠나지 말아 달라고 했잖소.\""]], [["“Did you notice the symptoms of the disease to which Madame de Saint-Méran has fallen a victim?”", "\"생메랑 부인을 쓰러뜨린 병의 증상을 눈여겨보셨소?\""]], [["“I did.", "\"그렇소."], ["Madame de Saint-Méran had three successive attacks, at intervals of some minutes, each one more serious than the former.", "생메랑 부인은 몇 분 간격을 두고 세 차례 잇따라 발작을 일으키셨는데, 한 번 한 번이 그 전보다 더 심했소."], ["When you arrived, Madame de Saint-Méran had already been panting for breath some minutes; she then had a fit, which I took to be simply a nervous attack, and it was only when I saw her raise herself in the bed, and her limbs and neck appear stiffened, that I became really alarmed.", "당신이 도착했을 때, 생메랑 부인은 이미 몇 분 동안 가쁜 숨을 몰아쉬고 계셨소. 그러다 발작을 일으키셨는데, 나는 그저 신경성 발작이려니 했소. 그런데 부인이 침대에서 몸을 일으키시고, 팔다리와 목이 뻣뻣해지는 것을 보고서야 정말로 겁이 났소."], ["Then I understood from your countenance there was more to fear than I had thought.", "그때 당신 얼굴빛을 보고, 내가 생각했던 것보다 두려워할 일이 더 있다는 걸 알았소."], ["This crisis past, I endeavored to catch your eye, but could not.", "이 고비가 지나고 나서 당신과 눈을 맞추려 했지만 그러지 못했소."], ["You held her hand—you were feeling her pulse—and the second fit came on before you had turned towards me.", "당신은 부인의 손을 잡고 맥을 짚고 계셨는데, 당신이 내 쪽으로 돌아서기도 전에 두 번째 발작이 닥쳤소."], ["This was more terrible than the first; the same nervous movements were repeated, and the mouth contracted and turned purple.”", "이건 처음보다 더 끔찍했소. 똑같은 신경성 경련이 되풀이됐고, 입은 일그러지며 보랏빛으로 변했소.\""]], [["“And at the third she expired.”", "\"그리고 세 번째 발작에 숨을 거두셨지요.\""]], [["“At the end of the first attack I discovered symptoms of tetanus; you confirmed my opinion.”", "\"첫 번째 발작이 끝났을 때 나는 파상풍의 증상을 알아챘소. 당신도 내 견해에 동의했고.\""]], [["“Yes, before others,”", "\"그렇소, 다른 사람들 앞에서는.\""], ["replied the doctor; “but now we are alone——”", "의사가 대답했다. \"하지만 지금은 우리 둘뿐이니……\""]], [["“What are you going to say? Oh, spare me!”", "\"무슨 말을 하려는 거요? 오, 제발 봐주시오!\""]], [["“That the symptoms of tetanus and poisoning by vegetable substances are the same.”", "\"파상풍의 증상과 식물성 물질에 의한 중독의 증상은 똑같다는 말이오.\""]], [["M. de Villefort started from his seat, then in a moment fell down again, silent and motionless.", "빌포르 씨가 자리에서 벌떡 일어섰다가, 이내 다시 주저앉았다. 말없이, 미동도 없이."], ["Morrel knew not if he were dreaming or awake.", "모렐은 자신이 꿈을 꾸는 건지 깨어 있는 건지 분간할 수 없었다."]], [["“Listen,”", "\"들으시오.\""], ["said the doctor; “I know the full importance of the statement I have just made, and the disposition of the man to whom I have made it.”", "의사가 말했다. \"나는 방금 내가 한 말이 얼마나 중대한지, 또 그 말을 들은 사람이 어떤 사람인지 잘 알고 있소.\""]], [["“Do you speak to me as a magistrate or as a friend?”", "\"내게 판사로서 말하는 거요, 친구로서 말하는 거요?\""], ["asked Villefort.", "빌포르가 물었다."]], [["“As a friend, and only as a friend, at this moment.", "\"친구로서, 지금 이 순간은 오로지 친구로서 하는 말이오."], ["The similarity in the symptoms of tetanus and poisoning by vegetable substances is so great, that were I obliged to affirm by oath what I have now stated, I should hesitate; I therefore repeat to you, I speak not to a magistrate, but to a friend.", "파상풍과 식물성 물질에 의한 중독의 증상은 너무도 비슷해서, 내가 방금 한 말을 선서하고 단언해야 한다면 망설여질 거요. 그러니 다시 말하건대, 나는 판사가 아니라 친구에게 말하는 거요."], ["And to that friend I say, ‘During the three-quarters of an hour that the struggle continued, I watched the convulsions and the death of Madame de Saint-Méran, and am thoroughly convinced that not only did her death proceed from poison, but I could also specify the poison.’”", "그리고 그 친구에게 말하건대, '한 시간 사십오 분 동안 사투가 이어지는 동안, 나는 생메랑 부인의 경련과 죽음을 지켜보았고, 그분의 죽음이 독에서 비롯되었을 뿐 아니라 그 독이 무엇인지까지 명시할 수 있다고 확신하오.'\""]], [["“Can it be possible?”", "\"그럴 수가 있단 말이오?\""]], [["“The symptoms are marked, do you see?—sleep broken by nervous spasms, excitation of the brain, torpor of the nerve centres.", "\"증상이 뚜렷하오, 알겠소? 신경성 경련으로 끊기는 잠, 뇌의 흥분, 신경 중추의 마비."], ["Madame de Saint-Méran succumbed to a powerful dose of brucine or of strychnine, which by some mistake, perhaps, has been given to her.”", "생메랑 부인은 어쩌면 어떤 착오로 투여된 브루신이나 스트리크닌의 강한 약량에 쓰러지신 거요.\""]], [["Villefort seized the doctor’s hand.", "빌포르가 의사의 손을 붙잡았다."]], [["“Oh, it is impossible,”", "\"오, 그럴 리 없소.\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “I must be dreaming!", "\"내가 꿈을 꾸고 있는 게 분명하오!"], ["It is frightful to hear such things from such a man as you!", "당신 같은 사람한테서 이런 말을 듣다니 끔찍하오!"], ["Tell me, I entreat you, my dear doctor, that you may be deceived.”", "말해 주시오, 부탁이오, 친애하는 의사 양반, 당신이 잘못 보았을 수도 있다고 말이오.\""]], [["“Doubtless I may, but——”", "\"물론 그럴 수도 있소. 하지만……\""]], [["“But?” 30345m", "\"하지만?\""]], [["“But I do not think so.”", "\"하지만 나는 그렇게 생각하지 않소.\""]], [["“Have pity on me doctor! So many dreadful things have happened to me lately that I am on the verge of madness.”", "\"제발 나를 가엾이 여겨 주시오, 의사 양반! 요즘 너무도 끔찍한 일들이 내게 일어나서, 나는 미칠 지경이오.\""]], [["“Has anyone besides me seen Madame de Saint-Méran?”", "\"나 말고 생메랑 부인을 본 사람이 또 있소?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"없소.\""]], [["“Has anything been sent for from a chemist’s that I have not examined?”", "\"내가 살펴보지 않은 약을 약방에서 사 온 게 있소?\""]], [["“Nothing.”", "\"없소.\""]], [["“Had Madame de Saint-Méran any enemies?”", "\"생메랑 부인에게 적이라도 있었소?\""]], [["“Not to my knowledge.”", "\"내가 아는 한 없소.\""]], [["“Would her death affect anyone’s interest?”", "\"부인의 죽음으로 누군가의 이익이 걸리는 일은 없소?\""]], [["“It could not indeed, my daughter is her only heiress—Valentine alone.", "\"있을 리가 없소. 내 딸이 유일한 상속녀요. 발랑틴뿐이오."], ["Oh, if such a thought could present itself, I would stab myself to punish my heart for having for one instant harbored it.”", "오, 그런 생각이 떠오를 수 있다면, 단 한순간이라도 그런 생각을 품은 내 마음을 벌하기 위해 나 자신을 칼로 찌르겠소.\""]], [["“Indeed, my dear friend,”", "\"정말이지, 친애하는 친구.\" 다브리니 씨가 말했다. \"나는 누구도 고발하려는 게 아니오."], ["said M.", "나는 그저 사고에 대해, 알겠소?"], ["d’Avrigny, “I would not accuse anyone; I speak only of an accident, you understand,—of a mistake,—but whether accident or mistake, the fact is there; it is on my conscience and compels me to speak aloud to you.", "착오에 대해 말하는 거요. 하지만 사고든 착오든, 사실은 거기 있소. 그것이 내 양심에 걸려, 당신에게 소리 높여 말하지 않을 수 없게 하는 거요."], ["Make inquiry.”", "알아보시오.\""]], [["“Of whom?—how?—of what?”", "\"누구를? 어떻게? 무엇을 말이오?\""]], [["“May not Barrois, the old servant, have made a mistake, and have given Madame de Saint-Méran a dose prepared for his master?”", "\"늙은 하인 바루아가 착오를 일으켜, 주인 몫으로 준비한 약을 생메랑 부인에게 드린 게 아니겠소?\" \"내 아버지 몫이라는 거요?\""]], [["“For my father?”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["“Yes.” “But how could a dose prepared for M. Noirtier poison Madame de Saint-Méran?”", "\"하지만 누아르티에 씨 몫으로 준비한 약이 어떻게 생메랑 부인을 중독시킬 수 있단 말이오?\""]], [["“Nothing is more simple.", "\"그보다 간단한 일도 없소."], ["You know poisons become remedies in certain diseases, of which paralysis is one.", "어떤 병에서는 독이 약이 되는 법이오. 마비도 그중 하나요."], ["For instance, having tried every other remedy to restore movement and speech to M. Noirtier, I resolved to try one last means, and for three months I have been giving him brucine; so that in the last dose I ordered for him there were six grains.", "가령 누아르티에 씨에게 운동과 언어 능력을 되찾아 주려고 온갖 다른 약을 다 써 본 끝에, 나는 마지막 수단 하나를 시도하기로 했소. 그래서 석 달 동안 그분에게 브루신을 투여해 왔소."], ["This quantity, which is perfectly safe to administer to the paralyzed frame of M. Noirtier, which has become gradually accustomed to it, would be sufficient to kill another person.”", "그러니 내가 마지막으로 처방한 약량에는 6그레인이 들어 있었소. 점차 브루신에 익숙해진 누아르티에 씨의 마비된 신체에는 더없이 안전하게 투여할 수 있는 이 양이, 다른 사람을 죽이기에는 충분한 양이오.\""]], [["“My dear doctor, there is no communication between M. Noirtier’s apartment and that of Madame de Saint-Méran, and Barrois never entered my mother-in-law’s room.", "\"친애하는 의사 양반, 누아르티에 씨의 방과 생메랑 부인의 방 사이에는 오갈 통로가 없소. 그리고 바루아는 한 번도 내 장모님의 방에 들어간 적이 없소."], ["In short, doctor although I know you to be the most conscientious man in the world, and although I place the utmost reliance in you, I want, notwithstanding my conviction, to believe this axiom, _errare humanum est_.”", "한마디로 의사 양반, 나는 당신이 세상에서 가장 양심적인 사람이라는 걸 알고, 당신을 더없이 신뢰하지만, 내 확신에도 불구하고 이 격언을 믿고 싶소. '에라레 후마눔 에스트'.\""]], [["“Is there one of my brethren in whom you have equal confidence with myself?”", "\"내 동료 의사들 가운데 나만큼 당신이 신뢰하는 사람이 있소?\""]], [["“Why do you ask me that?—what do you wish?”", "\"어째서 그런 걸 묻는 거요? 무엇을 바라는 거요?\""]], [["“Send for him; I will tell him what I have seen, and we will consult together, and examine the body.”", "\"그를 부르시오. 내가 본 것을 그에게 말하고, 함께 상의하여 시신을 검사하겠소.\""]], [["“And you will find traces of poison?”", "\"그러면 독의 흔적을 찾아내겠다는 거요?\""]], [["“No, I did not say of poison, but we can prove what was the state of the body; we shall discover the cause of her sudden death, and we shall say, ‘Dear Villefort, if this thing has been caused by negligence, watch over your servants; if from hatred, watch your enemies.’”", "\"아니, 나는 독이라고 하지 않았소. 다만 신체가 어떤 상태였는지는 증명할 수 있소. 우리는 부인의 급작스러운 죽음의 원인을 밝혀낼 것이고, 그러면 이렇게 말하겠소. '친애하는 빌포르, 이 일이 부주의로 인한 것이라면 하인들을 단속하시오. 증오로 인한 것이라면 당신의 적들을 경계하시오.'\""]], [["“What do you propose to me, d’Avrigny?”", "\"내게 무엇을 제안하는 거요, 다브리니?\""], ["said Villefort in despair; “so soon as another is admitted into our secret, an inquest will become necessary; and an inquest in my house—impossible! Still,”", "빌포르가 절망하여 말했다. \"다른 사람이 우리 비밀을 알게 되는 순간 검시가 불가피해지고, 내 집에서 검시라니, 그건 불가능하오! 그래도,\""], ["continued the procureur, looking at the doctor with uneasiness, “if you wish it—if you demand it, why then it shall be done.", "검사가 불안한 눈으로 의사를 바라보며 말을 이었다. \"당신이 원한다면, 당신이 요구한다면, 그럼 그렇게 하겠소."], ["But, doctor, you see me already so grieved—how can I introduce into my house so much scandal, after so much sorrow?", "하지만 의사 양반, 보다시피 나는 이미 이토록 비탄에 빠져 있소. 이만한 슬픔을 겪은 뒤에 어떻게 내 집에 그토록 큰 추문을 끌어들일 수 있겠소?"], ["My wife and my daughter would die of it!", "내 아내와 딸은 그 때문에 죽고 말 거요!"], ["And I, doctor—you know a man does not arrive at the post I occupy—one has not been king’s attorney twenty-five years without having amassed a tolerable number of enemies; mine are numerous.", "그리고 나는, 의사 양반, 당신도 알다시피 내가 지금 차지한 자리에 그저 닿을 수 있는 게 아니오. 이십오 년 동안 검사로 지내며 어지간한 수의 적을 쌓지 않을 수가 없는 법이오."], ["Let this affair be talked of, it will be a triumph for them, which will make them rejoice, and cover me with shame.", "내 적들은 많소. 이 일이 입에 오르내리면, 그건 그들에게 승리가 되어 그들을 기뻐 날뛰게 하고 나를 수치로 뒤덮을 거요."], ["Pardon me, doctor, these worldly ideas; were you a priest I should not dare tell you that, but you are a man, and you know mankind.", "이런 세속적인 생각을 용서하시오, 의사 양반. 당신이 사제라면 감히 이런 말을 못 하겠지만, 당신은 인간이고, 인간을 잘 알지 않소."], ["Doctor, pray recall your words; you have said nothing, have you?”", "의사 양반, 부디 당신 말을 거두어 주시오. 당신은 아무 말도 안 한 거요, 그렇지 않소?\""]], [["“My dear M. de Villefort,”", "\"친애하는 빌포르 씨.\""], ["replied the doctor, “my first duty is to humanity.", "의사가 대답했다. \"내 첫 번째 의무는 인류를 향한 것이오."], ["I would have saved Madame de Saint-Méran, if science could have done it; but she is dead and my duty regards the living.", "과학으로 할 수 있는 일이었다면 나는 생메랑 부인을 살렸을 거요. 하지만 부인은 돌아가셨고, 내 의무는 산 자들을 향한 것이오."], ["Let us bury this terrible secret in the deepest recesses of our hearts; I am willing, if anyone should suspect this, that my silence on the subject should be imputed to my ignorance.", "이 끔찍한 비밀을 우리 마음속 가장 깊은 곳에 묻어 둡시다. 누군가 이 일을 의심한다면, 이 일에 대한 내 침묵이 내 무지의 탓으로 돌려진다 해도 나는 기꺼이 받아들이겠소."], ["Meanwhile, sir, watch always—watch carefully, for perhaps the evil may not stop here.", "그러는 동안, 검사, 늘 경계하시오. 면밀히 경계하시오. 어쩌면 이 악행이 여기서 멈추지 않을지도 모르니."], ["And when you have found the culprit, if you find him, I will say to you, ‘You are a magistrate, do as you will!’”", "그리고 범인을 찾아낸다면, 찾아낸다면 말이오, 내가 당신에게 말하겠소. '당신은 판사요, 마음대로 하시오!'\""]], [["“I thank you, doctor,”", "\"고맙소, 의사 양반.\""], ["said Villefort with indescribable joy; “I never had a better friend than you.”", "빌포르가 형언할 수 없는 기쁨에 차 말했다. \"당신보다 나은 친구는 가져 본 적이 없소.\""], ["And, as if he feared Doctor d’Avrigny would recall his promise, he hurried him towards the house.", "그리고 다브리니 의사가 자신의 약속을 거둘까 두렵기라도 한 듯, 그는 의사를 서둘러 집 쪽으로 이끌었다."]], [["When they were gone, Morrel ventured out from under the trees, and the moon shone upon his face, which was so pale it might have been taken for that of a ghost.", "그들이 떠나자, 모렐은 나무 아래에서 조심스레 나왔고, 달빛이 그의 얼굴을 비추었다. 어찌나 창백한지 유령의 얼굴로 보일 만했다."]], [["“I am manifestly protected in a most wonderful, but most terrible manner,”", "\"나는 분명 더없이 놀랍지만 더없이 끔찍한 방식으로 보호받고 있구나.\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he; “but Valentine, poor girl, how will she bear so much sorrow?”", "\"하지만 발랑틴, 가엾은 사람, 이토록 큰 슬픔을 어떻게 견뎌 낼까?\""]], [["As he thought thus, he looked alternately at the window with red curtains and the three windows with white curtains.", "이렇게 생각하면서 그는 붉은 커튼이 드리운 창과 흰 커튼이 드리운 세 창을 번갈아 바라보았다."], ["The light had almost disappeared from the former; doubtless Madame de Villefort had just put out her lamp, and the nightlamp alone reflected its dull light on the window.", "앞쪽 창에서는 불빛이 거의 사라진 뒤였다. 빌포르 부인이 막 등불을 끈 것이 분명했고, 머리맡 등불만이 그 흐릿한 빛을 창에 비추고 있었다."], ["At the extremity of the building, on the contrary, he saw one of the three windows open.", "그런데 반대로 건물 맨 끝에서, 그는 세 창 가운데 하나가 열리는 것을 보았다."], ["A wax-light placed on the mantle-piece threw some of its pale rays without, and a shadow was seen for one moment on the balcony.", "벽난로 선반에 놓인 밀초 한 자루가 창백한 빛 몇 줄기를 밖으로 던지고 있었고, 발코니 위로 한순간 그림자 하나가 보였다. 모렐이 몸서리쳤다."], ["Morrel shuddered; he thought he heard a sob.", "흐느낌 소리가 들리는 것 같았던 것이다."]], [["It cannot be wondered at that his mind, generally so courageous, but now disturbed by the two strongest human passions, love and fear, was weakened even to the indulgence of superstitious thoughts.", "평소 그토록 용감하던 그의 마음이, 지금은 인간의 가장 강렬한 두 가지 격정인 사랑과 두려움에 흔들려, 미신적인 생각에 빠질 만큼 약해진 것도 무리는 아니었다. 이렇게 몸을 숨긴 자신을 발랑틴이 볼 수 있을 리 없는데도, 그는 창가의 그 그림자가 자신을 부르는 소리를 들은 것 같았다. 그의 어지러운 마음이 그렇게 일러 주었던 것이다."], ["Although it was impossible that Valentine should see him, hidden as he was, he thought he heard the shadow at the window call him; his disturbed mind told him so.", "이 이중의 착각은 거역할 수 없는 현실이 되었고, 청년 특유의 알 수 없는 충동에 휩싸여, 그는 숨어 있던 곳에서 뛰쳐나왔다. 들킬 위험을, 발랑틴을 놀라게 할 위험을, 처녀의 입에서 새어 나올지 모를 어떤 외마디 소리로 발각될 위험을 무릅쓰고, 그는 단 두 걸음에 화원을 가로질렀다."], ["This double error became an irresistible reality, and by one of the incomprehensible transports of youth, he bounded from his hiding-place, and with two strides, at the risk of being seen, at the risk of alarming Valentine, at the risk of being discovered by some exclamation which might escape the young girl, he crossed the flower-garden, which by the light of the moon resembled a large white lake, and having passed the rows of orange-trees which extended in front of the house, he reached the step, ran quickly up and pushed the door, which opened without offering any resistance.", "달빛 아래 화원은 커다란 흰 호수처럼 보였다. 그리고 집 앞에 늘어선 오렌지나무 줄을 지나, 그는 계단에 이르렀다. 그것을 재빨리 뛰어 올라 문을 밀자, 문은 아무런 저항 없이 열렸다."]], [["Valentine had not seen him.", "발랑틴은 그를 보지 못했다."], ["Her eyes, raised towards heaven, were watching a silvery cloud gliding over the azure, its form that of a shadow mounting towards heaven.", "하늘로 향한 그녀의 두 눈은, 푸른 창공을 미끄러져 가는 은빛 구름 한 점을 지켜보고 있었다. 그 구름의 모양은 하늘로 오르는 그림자 같았다."], ["Her poetic and excited mind pictured it as the soul of her grandmother.", "시적이고 격앙된 그녀의 마음은 그것을 할머니의 영혼이라 그려 보았다."]], [["Meanwhile, Morrel had traversed the anteroom and found the staircase, which, being carpeted, prevented his approach being heard, and he had regained that degree of confidence that the presence of M.", "그러는 동안 모렐은 대기실을 지나 계단을 찾아냈다. 계단에는 양탄자가 깔려 있어 다가오는 그의 발소리가 들리지 않았다. 그는 빌포르 씨가 나타나더라도 놀라지 않을 만큼 마음을 다잡고 있었다."], ["de Villefort even would not have alarmed him. He was quite prepared for any such encounter.", "그런 마주침에 대해서도 단단히 각오가 되어 있었다."], ["He would at once approach Valentine’s father and acknowledge all, begging Villefort to pardon and sanction the love which united two fond and loving hearts. Morrel was mad.", "그는 곧장 발랑틴의 아버지에게 다가가 모든 것을 털어놓고, 다정하게 서로를 사랑하는 두 마음을 맺어 준 그 사랑을 용서하고 인정해 달라고 빌포르에게 청할 작정이었다. 모렐은 제정신이 아니었다."]], [["Happily he did not meet anyone.", "다행히 그는 아무도 마주치지 않았다."], ["Now, especially, did he find the description Valentine had given of the interior of the house useful to him; he arrived safely at the top of the staircase, and while he was feeling his way, a sob indicated the direction he was to take.", "이제 특히, 발랑틴이 집 안 구조에 대해 해 준 묘사가 그에게 요긴했다. 그는 무사히 계단 꼭대기에 이르렀고, 손으로 더듬어 길을 찾고 있을 때, 흐느낌 소리가 그가 가야 할 방향을 일러 주었다."], ["He turned back, a door partly open enabled him to see his road, and to hear the voice of one in sorrow.", "그는 몸을 돌렸고, 반쯤 열린 문 하나가 그에게 갈 길을 보여 주었으며 슬픔에 잠긴 누군가의 목소리를 들려주었다."], ["He pushed the door open and entered.", "그는 문을 밀고 들어갔다."], ["At the other end of the room, under a white sheet which covered it, lay the corpse, still more alarming to Morrel since the account he had so unexpectedly overheard.", "방 저편에는, 그것을 덮은 흰 천 아래 시신이 누워 있었다. 그토록 뜻밖에 엿들은 이야기를 떠올린 모렐에게는 더욱 섬뜩했다."], ["By its side, on her knees, and with her head buried in the cushion of an easy-chair, was Valentine, trembling and sobbing, her hands extended above her head, clasped and stiff.", "그 곁에는 발랑틴이 무릎을 꿇고, 안락의자의 쿠션에 머리를 파묻은 채, 두 손을 머리 위로 뻗어 맞잡고 뻣뻣하게 굳힌 채 떨며 흐느끼고 있었다."], ["She had turned from the window, which remained open, and was praying in accents that would have affected the most unfeeling; her words were rapid, incoherent, unintelligible, for the burning weight of grief almost stopped her utterance.", "그녀는 열린 채로 둔 창에서 등을 돌리고, 더없이 무정한 사람조차 마음을 움직였을 어조로 기도하고 있었다. 그녀의 말은 빠르고, 두서없고, 알아들을 수 없었다. 타는 듯한 슬픔의 무게가 그녀의 말을 거의 막아 버린 탓이었다."]], [["The moon shining through the open blinds made the lamp appear to burn paler, and cast a sepulchral hue over the whole scene.", "열린 덧창 사이로 비쳐 드는 달빛에 등불은 더욱 창백하게 타오르는 듯 보였고, 장면 전체에 무덤 같은 빛깔을 드리웠다."], ["Morrel could not resist this; he was not exemplary for piety, he was not easily impressed, but Valentine suffering, weeping, wringing her hands before him, was more than he could bear in silence.", "모렐은 이를 견딜 수 없었다. 그는 신앙심이 깊은 사람도 아니었고, 쉽게 감동받는 사람도 아니었다. 그러나 자기 앞에서 고통받으며 울고, 두 손을 비틀어 짜는 발랑틴은, 그가 침묵 속에서 견디기에는 너무도 벅찼다."], ["He sighed, and whispered a name, and the head bathed in tears and pressed on the velvet cushion of the chair—a head like that of a Magdalen by Correggio—was raised and turned towards him.", "그는 한숨을 쉬며 어떤 이름을 속삭였다. 그러자 눈물에 젖어 의자의 벨벳 쿠션에 눌려 있던 머리, 코레조가 그린 막달레나 같은 그 머리가 들리며 그를 향해 돌아왔다."], ["Valentine perceived him without betraying the least surprise.", "발랑틴은 조금도 놀라는 기색 없이 그를 알아보았다."], ["A heart overwhelmed with one great grief is insensible to minor emotions.", "하나의 커다란 슬픔에 짓눌린 마음은 자잘한 감정에는 무뎌지는 법이다."], ["Morrel held out his hand to her.", "모렐이 그녀에게 손을 내밀었다."], ["Valentine, as her only apology for not having met him, pointed to the corpse under the sheet, and began to sob again.", "발랑틴은 그를 맞으러 나가지 못한 데 대한 유일한 변명으로, 천 아래의 시신을 가리키고는 다시 흐느끼기 시작했다."]], [["Neither dared for some time to speak in that room.", "한동안 둘 다 그 방에서 감히 입을 떼지 못했다."], ["They hesitated to break the silence which death seemed to impose; at length Valentine ventured.", "죽음이 강요하는 듯한 침묵을 깨기를 망설였던 것이다. 마침내 발랑틴이 용기를 냈다."]], [["“My friend,”", "\"내 친구.\""], ["said she, “how came you here?", "그녀가 말했다. \"어떻게 여기까지 오셨어요?"], ["Alas, I would say you are welcome, had not death opened the way for you into this house.”", "아아, 죽음이 당신에게 이 집으로 드는 길을 열어 준 게 아니었다면, 잘 오셨다고 말했을 텐데요.\""]], [["“Valentine,”", "\"발랑틴.\" 모렐이 떨리는 목소리로 말했다."], ["said Morrel with a trembling voice, “I had waited since half-past eight, and did not see you come; I became uneasy, leaped the wall, found my way through the garden, when voices conversing about the fatal event——”", "\"여덟 시 반부터 기다렸는데 당신이 오는 게 보이지 않았어. 불안해진 나는 담을 넘어 정원을 가로질러 왔지. 그러다 그 끔찍한 일에 대해 이야기하는 목소리들이……\""]], [["“What voices?” asked Valentine.", "\"무슨 목소리요?\" 발랑틴이 물었다."], ["Morrel shuddered as he thought of the conversation of the doctor and M.", "모렐은 의사와 빌포르 씨의 대화를 떠올리며 몸서리쳤다."], ["de Villefort, and he thought he could see through the sheet the extended hands, the stiff neck, and the purple lips.", "천 너머로 뻗은 두 손과 뻣뻣한 목, 보랏빛 입술이 보이는 듯했다."]], [["“Your servants,”", "\"당신네 하인들이.\""], ["said he, “who were repeating the whole of the sorrowful story; from them I learned it all.”", "그가 말했다. \"그 슬픈 이야기를 전부 되풀이하고 있었어. 그들한테서 다 들었지.\""]], [["“But it was risking the failure of our plan to come up here, love.”", "\"하지만 여기까지 올라온 건 우리 계획을 그르칠 위험을 무릅쓴 거예요, 내 사랑.\""]], [["“Forgive me,”", "\"용서해.\""], ["replied Morrel; “I will go away.”", "모렐이 대답했다. \"가 볼게.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니요.\""], ["said Valentine, “you might meet someone; stay.”", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"누군가와 마주칠지도 몰라요. 여기 있어요.\""]], [["“But if anyone should come here——”", "\"하지만 누가 이리로 온다면……\""]], [["The young girl shook her head.", "처녀가 고개를 저었다."], ["“No one will come,”", "\"아무도 안 와요.\""], ["said she; “do not fear, there is our safeguard,”", "그녀가 말했다. \"걱정 말아요, 저기 우리의 방패가 있으니까요.\""], ["pointing to the bed.", "침상을 가리키며 말했다."]], [["“But what has become of M. d’Épinay?”", "\"그런데 데피네 씨는 어떻게 됐어?\""], ["replied Morrel.", "모렐이 물었다."]], [["30349m\n“M. Franz arrived to sign the contract just as my dear grandmother was dying.”", "\"프란츠 씨는 제 할머니가 막 돌아가시려는 바로 그때 계약서에 서명하러 도착했어요.\""]], [["“Alas,”", "\"아아.\""], ["said Morrel with a feeling of selfish joy; for he thought this death would cause the wedding to be postponed indefinitely.", "모렐이 이기적인 기쁨을 느끼며 말했다. 이 죽음으로 결혼이 무기한 미뤄지리라 생각했던 것이다."]], [["“But what redoubles my sorrow,”", "\"하지만 제 슬픔을 곱절로 키우는 건,\""], ["continued the young girl, as if this feeling was to receive its immediate punishment, “is that the poor old lady, on her death-bed, requested that the marriage might take place as soon as possible; she also, thinking to protect me, was acting against me.”", "처녀가, 마치 그 감정이 곧바로 벌을 받기라도 하듯 말을 이었다. \"그 가엾은 노부인이 임종의 자리에서, 결혼을 되도록 빨리 치르게 해 달라고 부탁하셨다는 거예요. 할머니도 저를 지켜 주려는 생각으로, 오히려 제게 거역하는 일을 하신 거죠.\""]], [["“Hark!”", "\"쉿!\""], ["said Morrel.", "모렐이 말했다."], ["They both listened; steps were distinctly heard in the corridor and on the stairs.", "둘은 귀를 기울였다. 복도와 계단에서 발소리가 또렷하게 들렸다."]], [["“It is my father, who has just left his study.”", "\"제 아버지예요. 방금 서재에서 나오신 거예요.\""]], [["“To accompany the doctor to the door,” added Morrel.", "\"의사를 문까지 배웅하려고.\" 모렐이 덧붙였다."]], [["“How do you know it is the doctor?”", "\"그게 의사인 줄 어떻게 아세요?\""], ["asked Valentine, astonished.", "발랑틴이 놀라 물었다."]], [["“I imagined it must be,”", "\"그럴 거라고 짐작했어.\""], ["said Morrel.", "모렐이 말했다."]], [["Valentine looked at the young man; they heard the street door close, then M.", "발랑틴이 청년을 바라보았다. 둘은 거리 쪽 문이 닫히는 소리를 들었고, 이어 빌포르 씨가 정원 문을 잠그고 다시 위층으로 올라왔다. 그는 대기실에서 잠시 멈춰 섰다."], ["de Villefort locked the garden door, and returned upstairs.", "자기 방으로 갈지 생메랑 부인의 방으로 갈지 망설이는 듯했다."], ["He stopped a moment in the anteroom, as if hesitating whether to turn to his own apartment or into Madame de Saint-Méran’s; Morrel concealed himself behind a door; Valentine remained motionless, grief seeming to deprive her of all fear. M.", "모렐은 문 뒤에 몸을 숨겼다. 발랑틴은 미동도 없었다. 슬픔이 그녀에게서 모든 두려움을 앗아 간 듯했다."], ["de Villefort passed on to his own room.", "빌포르 씨는 자기 방으로 지나갔다."]], [["“Now,”", "\"이제.\""], ["said Valentine, “you can neither go out by the front door nor by the garden.”", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"당신은 정문으로도, 정원으로도 나갈 수 없어요.\""]], [["Morrel looked at her with astonishment.", "모렐이 놀란 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["“There is but one way left you that is safe,”", "\"당신에게 남은 안전한 길은 하나뿐이에요.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she; “it is through my grandfather’s room.” She rose.", "\"제 할아버지 방을 지나는 거죠.\" 그녀가 일어섰다. \"가요.\""], ["“Come,” she added.", "그녀가 덧붙였다."]], [["“Where?”", "\"어디로?\""], ["asked Maximilian.", "막시밀리앙이 물었다."]], [["“To my grandfather’s room.”", "\"제 할아버지 방으로요.\""]], [["“I in M. Noirtier’s apartment?”", "\"내가 누아르티에 씨의 방에?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Can you mean it, Valentine?”", "\"진심이야, 발랑틴?\""]], [["“I have long wished it; he is my only remaining friend and we both need his help,—come.”", "\"오래전부터 바라던 일이에요. 그분은 제게 남은 유일한 친구이고, 우리 둘 다 그분의 도움이 필요해요. 가요.\""]], [["“Be careful, Valentine,”", "\"조심해, 발랑틴.\""], ["said Morrel, hesitating to comply with the young girl’s wishes; “I now see my error—I acted like a madman in coming in here.", "모렐이 처녀의 뜻을 따르기를 망설이며 말했다. \"이제야 내 잘못이 보여. 여기 들어온 건 미친 사람이나 할 짓이었어."], ["Are you sure you are more reasonable?”", "당신은 정말 나보다 분별이 있다고 확신할 수 있어?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said Valentine; “and I have but one scruple,—that of leaving my dear grandmother’s remains, which I had undertaken to watch.”", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"그리고 제게 망설여지는 건 한 가지뿐이에요. 지켜 드리기로 한 사랑하는 할머니의 유해를 두고 떠난다는 것.\""]], [["“Valentine,”", "\"발랑틴.\""], ["said Morrel, “death is in itself sacred.”", "모렐이 말했다. \"죽음은 그 자체로 신성한 거야.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said Valentine; “besides, it will not be for long.”", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"게다가 오래 걸리지도 않을 거예요.\""]], [["She then crossed the corridor, and led the way down a narrow staircase to M.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 복도를 가로질러, 좁은 계단을 따라 누아르티에 씨의 방으로 앞장서 갔다."], ["Noirtier’s room; Morrel followed her on tiptoe; at the door they found the old servant.", "모렐은 발끝으로 그녀를 따랐다. 문 앞에서 그들은 늙은 하인과 마주쳤다."]], [["“Barrois,”", "\"바루아.\""], ["said Valentine, “shut the door, and let no one come in.”", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"문을 닫고, 아무도 들이지 마세요.\""]], [["She passed first.", "그녀가 먼저 들어갔다."]], [["Noirtier, seated in his chair, and listening to every sound, was watching the door; he saw Valentine, and his eye brightened.", "의자에 앉아 온갖 소리에 귀 기울이고 있던 누아르티에는 문을 지켜보고 있었다. 그는 발랑틴을 보았고, 그의 눈이 환해졌다."], ["There was something grave and solemn in the approach of the young girl which struck the old man, and immediately his bright eye began to interrogate.", "처녀가 다가오는 모습에는 무언가 무겁고 엄숙한 데가 있어 노인을 사로잡았고, 그의 빛나는 눈이 곧바로 묻기 시작했다."]], [["“Dear grandfather.”", "\"사랑하는 할아버지.\""], ["said she hurriedly, “you know poor grandmamma died an hour since, and now I have no friend in the world but you.”", "그녀가 다급히 말했다. \"할아버지도 아시겠지만 가엾은 할머니가 한 시간 전에 돌아가셨어요. 이제 이 세상에서 저한테는 할아버지 말고는 친구가 없어요.\""]], [["His expressive eyes evinced the greatest tenderness.", "그의 표정 풍부한 눈이 더없이 큰 다정함을 드러냈다."]], [["“To you alone, then, may I confide my sorrows and my hopes?”", "\"그러니 오직 할아버지께만 제 슬픔과 제 희망을 털어놓을 수 있겠죠?\""]], [["The paralytic motioned “Yes.”", "마비된 노인이 \"그렇다\"라고 신호했다."]], [["Valentine took Maximilian’s hand.", "발랑틴이 막시밀리앙의 손을 잡았다."]], [["“Look attentively, then, at this gentleman.”", "\"그럼 이 신사분을 주의 깊게 봐 주세요.\""]], [["The old man fixed his scrutinizing gaze with slight astonishment on Morrel.", "노인이 살피는 듯한 시선을, 약간 놀란 빛을 띠고 모렐에게 고정했다."]], [["“It is M. Maximilian Morrel,”", "\"이분은 막시밀리앙 모렐 씨예요.\""], ["said she; “the son of that good merchant of Marseilles, whom you doubtless recollect.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"할아버지도 분명 기억하실 마르세유의 그 선량한 상인의 아드님이에요.\""]], [["“Yes,” said the old man.", "\"그렇구나.\" 노인이 말했다."]], [["“He brings an irreproachable name, which Maximilian is likely to render glorious, since at thirty years of age he is a captain, an officer of the Legion of Honor.”", "\"흠잡을 데 없는 가문의 이름을 가졌고, 막시밀리앙은 그 이름을 더욱 빛낼 사람이에요. 서른 살에 벌써 대위이자 레지옹 도뇌르 훈장을 받은 장교거든요.\""]], [["The old man signified that he recollected him.", "노인은 그를 기억한다는 표시를 했다."]], [["“Well, grandpapa,”", "\"할아버지.\""], ["said Valentine, kneeling before him, and pointing to Maximilian, “I love him, and will be only his; were I compelled to marry another, I would destroy myself.”", "발랑틴이 그 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 막시밀리앙을 가리키며 말했다. \"저는 이분을 사랑하고, 오직 이분의 사람이 될 거예요. 다른 사람과 결혼하라고 강요당한다면 저는 스스로 목숨을 끊을 거예요.\""]], [["The eyes of the paralytic expressed a multitude of tumultuous thoughts.", "마비된 노인의 눈이 격동하는 수많은 생각을 드러냈다."]], [["“You like M. Maximilian Morrel, do you not, grandpapa?”", "\"할아버지는 막시밀리앙 모렐 씨가 마음에 드시죠, 그렇죠?\""], ["asked Valentine.", "발랑틴이 물었다."]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇다.\""]], [["“And you will protect us, who are your children, against the will of my father?”", "\"그리고 할아버지의 자식인 저희를, 제 아버지의 뜻에 맞서 지켜 주실 거죠?\""]], [["Noirtier cast an intelligent glance at Morrel, as if to say, “perhaps I may.”", "누아르티에가 모렐에게 총명한 눈길을 던졌다. 마치 \"어쩌면 그럴 수도 있지\"라고 말하는 듯했다."]], [["Maximilian understood him.", "막시밀리앙은 그 뜻을 알아들었다."]], [["“Mademoiselle,”", "\"마드무아젤.\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “you have a sacred duty to fulfil in your deceased grandmother’s room, will you allow me the honor of a few minutes’ conversation with M. Noirtier?”", "\"당신은 돌아가신 할머니의 방에서 다해야 할 신성한 의무가 있습니다. 제가 누아르티에 씨와 몇 분 동안 이야기를 나누는 영광을 허락해 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“That is it,”", "\"바로 그거다.\""], ["said the old man’s eye.", "노인의 눈이 말했다."], ["Then he looked anxiously at Valentine.", "이어 그는 발랑틴을 근심스레 바라보았다."]], [["“Do you fear he will not understand?”", "\"이분이 못 알아들을까 봐 걱정하세요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇다.\""]], [["“Oh, we have so often spoken of you, that he knows exactly how I talk to you.”", "\"오, 우리가 할아버지 이야기를 워낙 자주 했기 때문에, 이분은 제가 할아버지와 어떻게 대화하는지 정확히 알고 있어요.\""], ["Then turning to Maximilian, with an adorable smile; although shaded by sorrow,—“He knows everything I know,”", "그러고는 막시밀리앙을 향해, 슬픔에 가려지긴 했어도 사랑스러운 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"이분은 제가 아는 걸 다 알아요.\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["Valentine arose, placed a chair for Morrel, requested Barrois not to admit anyone, and having tenderly embraced her grandfather, and sorrowfully taken leave of Morrel, she went away.", "발랑틴이 일어나, 모렐에게 의자를 놓아 주고, 바루아에게 아무도 들이지 말라고 당부했다. 그러고는 할아버지를 다정하게 끌어안고, 모렐에게 슬프게 작별을 고한 뒤 자리를 떴다."], ["To prove to Noirtier that he was in Valentine’s confidence and knew all their secrets, Morrel took the dictionary, a pen, and some paper, and placed them all on a table where there was a light.", "자신이 발랑틴의 신뢰를 받고 있으며 그들의 비밀을 다 알고 있음을 누아르티에에게 증명하기 위해, 모렐은 사전과 펜, 종이를 집어 들어 등불이 놓인 탁자 위에 모두 놓았다."]], [["“But first,”", "\"하지만 먼저,\""], ["said Morrel, “allow me, sir, to tell you who I am, how much I love Mademoiselle Valentine, and what are my designs respecting her.”", "모렐이 말했다. \"제가 누구인지, 제가 발랑틴 양을 얼마나 사랑하는지, 그녀에 대한 제 계획이 무엇인지 말씀드리도록 허락해 주십시오.\""]], [["Noirtier made a sign that he would listen.", "누아르티에가 듣겠다는 신호를 했다."]], [["It was an imposing sight to witness this old man, apparently a mere useless burden, becoming the sole protector, support, and adviser of the lovers who were both young, beautiful, and strong.", "겉보기에는 그저 쓸모없는 짐 같던 이 노인이, 둘 다 젊고 아름답고 강건한 연인들의 유일한 보호자이자 의지처, 조언자가 되는 광경은 자못 위엄이 있었다."], ["His remarkably noble and austere expression struck Morrel, who began his story with trembling.", "그의 유난히 고결하고 준엄한 표정이 모렐을 사로잡았고, 모렐은 떨면서 이야기를 시작했다."], ["He related the manner in which he had become acquainted with Valentine, and how he had loved her, and that Valentine, in her solitude and her misfortune, had accepted the offer of his devotion.", "그는 발랑틴을 어떻게 알게 되었는지, 어떻게 그녀를 사랑하게 되었는지, 그리고 고독과 불행 속에 있던 발랑틴이 자신의 헌신을 받아들였다는 것을 이야기했다."], ["He told him his birth, his position, his fortune, and more than once, when he consulted the look of the paralytic, that look answered, “That is good, proceed.”", "그는 자신의 출생과 처지, 재산을 말했고, 마비된 노인의 표정을 살필 때마다 그 표정은 이렇게 답했다. \"좋다, 계속하거라.\""]], [["“And now,”", "\"그리고 이제,\""], ["said Morrel, when he had finished the first part of his recital, “now I have told you of my love and my hopes, may I inform you of my intentions?”", "첫 부분의 이야기를 마친 모렐이 말했다. \"이제 제 사랑과 제 희망을 말씀드렸으니, 제 계획을 알려 드려도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["“Yes,” signified the old man.", "\"그렇다.\" 노인이 신호했다."]], [["“This was our resolution; a cabriolet was in waiting at the gate, in which I intended to carry off Valentine to my sister’s house, to marry her, and to wait respectfully M.", "\"저희의 결심은 이러했습니다. 이륜마차 한 대가 철문 앞에서 기다리고, 그 마차로 발랑틴을 제 누이 집으로 데려가 그녀와 결혼한 다음, 빌포르 씨의 용서를 정중히 기다리려 했습니다.\" \"안 된다.\""], ["de Villefort’s pardon.”", "누아르티에가 말했다."]], [["“No,” said Noirtier.", "\"그렇게 해서는 안 됩니까?\""]], [["“We must not do so?” “No.”", "\"안 된다.\""]], [["“You do not sanction our project?” “No.”", "\"저희 계획을 인정하지 않으시는군요?\""]], [["“There is another way,”", "\"안 된다.\" \"다른 방법이 있습니다.\""], ["said Morrel.", "모렐이 말했다."], ["The old man’s interrogative eye said, “Which?”", "노인의 묻는 듯한 눈이 말했다. \"어떤 방법이냐?\""]], [["“I will go,” continued Maximilian, “I will seek M.", "\"제가 가겠습니다.\" 막시밀리앙이 말을 이었다."], ["Franz d’Épinay—I am happy to be able to mention this in Mademoiselle de Villefort’s absence—and will conduct myself toward him so as to compel him to challenge me.”", "\"프란츠 데피네 씨를 찾아가겠습니다. 빌포르 양이 없는 자리에서 이 말씀을 드릴 수 있어 다행입니다. 그가 저에게 결투를 신청할 수밖에 없도록 처신하겠습니다.\""], ["Noirtier’s look continued to interrogate.", "누아르티에의 눈길이 계속 묻고 있었다."]], [["“You wish to know what I will do?”", "\"제가 무엇을 할지 알고 싶으십니까?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇다.\""]], [["“I will find him, as I told you.", "\"말씀드린 대로 그를 찾아가겠습니다. 저와 발랑틴 양을 묶어 주는 인연을 그에게 말하겠습니다. 그가 분별 있는 사람이라면, 제 발로 약혼녀의 손을 단념함으로써 그 분별을 증명하고, 죽을 때까지 제 우정과 사랑을 얻을 것입니다."], ["I will tell him the ties which bind me to Mademoiselle Valentine; if he be a sensible man, he will prove it by renouncing of his own accord the hand of his betrothed, and will secure my friendship, and love until death; if he refuse, either through interest or ridiculous pride, after I have proved to him that he would be forcing my wife from me, that Valentine loves me, and will have no other, I will fight with him, give him every advantage, and I shall kill him, or he will kill me; if I am victorious, he will not marry Valentine, and if I die, I am very sure Valentine will not marry him.”", "만약 거절한다면, 이해관계 때문이든 우스꽝스러운 자존심 때문이든, 그가 제 아내를 제게서 강제로 빼앗는 것이며 발랑틴이 저를 사랑하고 다른 누구도 원하지 않는다는 것을 증명한 뒤에, 저는 그와 결투할 것입니다. 모든 이점을 그에게 주고, 제가 그를 죽이거나 그가 저를 죽이게 될 겁니다. 제가 이긴다면 그는 발랑틴과 결혼하지 못할 것이고, 제가 죽는다면 발랑틴이 그와 결혼하지 않을 것을 저는 확신합니다.\""]], [["Noirtier watched, with indescribable pleasure, this noble and sincere countenance, on which every sentiment his tongue uttered was depicted, adding by the expression of his fine features all that coloring adds to a sound and faithful drawing.", "누아르티에는 이 고결하고 진실한 얼굴을 형언할 수 없는 기쁨으로 지켜보았다. 그 얼굴에는 그의 혀가 내뱉는 모든 감정이 그려져 있었고, 그 잘생긴 이목구비의 표정이, 건실하고 충실한 그림에 색채가 더하는 모든 것을 더해 주고 있었다."]], [["Still, when Morrel had finished, he shut his eyes several times, which was his manner of saying “No.”", "그래도 모렐이 말을 마치자, 노인은 여러 번 눈을 감았다. 그것이 그가 \"안 된다\"라고 말하는 방식이었다."]], [["“No?”", "\"안 된다고요?\""], ["said Morrel; “you disapprove of this second project, as you did of the first?”", "모렐이 말했다. \"이 두 번째 계획도 첫 번째 계획처럼 못마땅하십니까?\""]], [["“I do,” signified the old man.", "\"그렇다.\" 노인이 신호했다."]], [["“But what then must be done?”", "\"그럼 대체 어떻게 해야 합니까?\""], ["asked Morrel.", "모렐이 물었다."], ["“Madame de Saint-Méran’s last request was, that the marriage might not be delayed; must I let things take their course?”", "\"생메랑 부인의 마지막 부탁은 결혼을 미루지 말라는 것이었습니다. 일이 그대로 흘러가게 두어야 합니까?\" 누아르티에는 미동도 하지 않았다."], ["Noirtier did not move.", "\"알겠습니다.\""], ["“I understand,”", "모렐이 말했다."], ["said Morrel; “I am to wait.”", "\"저더러 기다리라는 말씀이군요.\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇다.\""]], [["“But delay may ruin our plan, sir,” replied the young man.", "\"하지만 지체하면 저희 계획이 무너질 수 있습니다.\" 청년이 대답했다."], ["“Alone, Valentine has no power; she will be compelled to submit.", "\"혼자서는 발랑틴에게 아무 힘이 없습니다. 그녀는 따를 수밖에 없게 될 겁니다."], ["I am here almost miraculously, and can scarcely hope for so good an opportunity to occur again.", "제가 여기 온 것도 거의 기적이고, 이만한 기회가 다시 올 거라고는 기대하기 어렵습니다."], ["Believe me, there are only the two plans I have proposed to you; forgive my vanity, and tell me which you prefer.", "믿어 주십시오, 제가 말씀드린 두 가지 계획밖에 없습니다. 제 주제넘음을 용서하시고, 어느 쪽을 택하시는지 말씀해 주십시오."], ["Do you authorize Mademoiselle Valentine to intrust herself to my honor?”", "발랑틴 양이 제 명예에 자신을 맡기는 것을 허락하시겠습니까?\" \"안 된다.\""]], [["“No.” “Do you prefer I should seek M. d’Épinay?”", "\"제가 데피네 씨를 찾아가는 편이 낫겠습니까?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"안 된다.\""]], [["“Whence then will come the help we need—from chance?”", "\"그럼 저희에게 필요한 도움은 어디서 옵니까? 우연에서요?\" 모렐이 다시 물었다."], ["resumed Morrel.", "\"안 된다.\""]], [["“No.” “From you?”", "\"할아버지께로부터요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇다.\""]], [["“You thoroughly understand me, sir?", "\"제 말을 정확히 이해하시는 거지요?"], ["Pardon my eagerness, for my life depends on your answer.", "제 조급함을 용서해 주십시오, 제 목숨이 할아버지의 대답에 달려 있으니까요."], ["Will our help come from you?” “Yes.”", "저희의 도움이 할아버지께로부터 오는 겁니까?\""]], [["“You are sure of it?”", "\"그렇다.\" \"확신하십니까?\""]], [["“Yes.” There was so much firmness in the look which gave this answer, no one could, at any rate, doubt his will, if they did his power.", "\"그렇다.\" 이 대답을 보낸 눈빛에는 너무도 굳건함이 서려 있어, 적어도 그 의지만큼은, 설령 능력은 의심할지언정 누구도 의심할 수 없었다."]], [["“Oh, thank you a thousand times!", "\"오, 천 번이라도 감사드립니다!"], ["But how, unless a miracle should restore your speech, your gesture, your movement, how can you, chained to that armchair, dumb and motionless, oppose this marriage?”", "하지만 어떻게요? 기적이 일어나 할아버지의 말과 몸짓과 움직임을 되돌려 주지 않는 한, 그 안락의자에 묶여 말 못 하고 꼼짝 못 하는 할아버지께서 어떻게 이 결혼을 막으실 수 있단 말입니까?\""], ["A smile lit up the old man’s face, a strange smile of the eyes in a paralyzed face.", "미소 하나가 노인의 얼굴을 밝혔다. 마비된 얼굴에 떠오른, 눈으로 짓는 기이한 미소였다."]], [["“Then I must wait?” asked the young man.", "\"그럼 저는 기다려야 합니까?\" 청년이 물었다."]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇다.\""]], [["“But the contract?”", "\"하지만 계약서는요?\""], ["The same smile returned.", "똑같은 미소가 되돌아왔다."], ["“Will you assure me it shall not be signed?”", "\"그것이 서명되지 않으리라고 장담하실 수 있습니까?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇다.\""], ["said Noirtier.", "누아르티에가 말했다."]], [["“The contract shall not be signed!”", "\"계약서는 서명되지 않을 거다!\""], ["cried Morrel.", "모렐이 외쳤다."], ["“Oh, pardon me, sir; I can scarcely realize so great a happiness.", "\"오, 용서하십시오, 할아버지. 이토록 큰 행복을 도무지 실감할 수가 없습니다."], ["Will they not sign it?”", "정말 그들이 서명하지 않을까요?\""]], [["“No,” said the paralytic.", "\"안 한다.\" 마비된 노인이 말했다."], ["Notwithstanding that assurance, Morrel still hesitated.", "그 장담에도 불구하고 모렐은 여전히 망설였다."], ["This promise of an impotent old man was so strange that, instead of being the result of the power of his will, it might emanate from enfeebled organs.", "무력한 노인의 이 약속이 너무도 기이해서, 그것이 그의 의지의 힘에서 나온 것이 아니라 쇠약해진 신체 기관에서 비롯된 것일지도 모른다는 생각이 들었다."], ["Is it not natural that the madman, ignorant of his folly, should attempt things beyond his power?", "자신의 어리석음을 모르는 광인이 제 힘에 부치는 일을 시도하는 것이 자연스러운 일이 아니던가?"], ["The weak man talks of burdens he can raise, the timid of giants he can confront, the poor of treasures he spends, the most humble peasant, in the height of his pride, calls himself Jupiter.", "약한 자는 자기가 들 수 있는 짐을 떠벌리고, 겁쟁이는 자기가 맞설 수 있는 거인을 떠벌리며, 가난뱅이는 자기가 쓸 보물을 떠벌리고, 더없이 비천한 농부도 오만함이 극에 달하면 자신을 유피테르라 부른다."], ["Whether Noirtier understood the young man’s indecision, or whether he had not full confidence in his docility, he looked uneasily at him.", "누아르티에가 청년의 망설임을 알아챘는지, 아니면 그의 순순함을 온전히 믿지 못했는지, 그는 불안한 눈으로 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“What do you wish, sir?”", "\"무엇을 바라십니까, 할아버지?\""], ["asked Morrel; “that I should renew my promise of remaining tranquil?”", "모렐이 물었다. \"제가 잠자코 있겠다는 약속을 다시 하기를 바라십니까?\""], ["Noirtier’s eye remained fixed and firm, as if to imply that a promise did not suffice; then it passed from his face to his hands.", "누아르티에의 눈은 고정된 채 굳건히 머물러 있었다. 약속만으로는 충분치 않다는 듯이. 이윽고 그 눈길이 그의 얼굴에서 두 손으로 옮겨 갔다."]], [["“Shall I swear to you, sir?”", "\"맹세할까요, 할아버지?\""], ["asked Maximilian.", "막시밀리앙이 물었다."]], [["“Yes,” said the paralytic with the same solemnity.", "\"그렇다.\" 마비된 노인이 똑같은 엄숙함으로 말했다."], ["Morrel understood that the old man attached great importance to an oath.", "모렐은 노인이 맹세에 큰 의미를 둔다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["He extended his hand.", "그는 손을 내밀었다."]], [["“I swear to you, on my honor,”", "\"제 명예를 걸고 맹세합니다.\""], ["said he, “to await your decision respecting the course I am to pursue with M. d’Épinay.”", "그가 말했다. \"데피네 씨에 대해 제가 취할 행동에 관해 할아버지의 결정을 기다리겠다고.\""]], [["“That is right,” said the old man.", "\"옳다.\" 노인이 말했다."]], [["“Now,”", "\"이제.\""], ["said Morrel, “do you wish me to retire?” “Yes.”", "모렐이 말했다. \"제가 물러가기를 바라십니까?\""]], [["“Without seeing Mademoiselle Valentine?”", "\"그렇다.\" \"발랑틴 양을 보지 않고요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇다.\""]], [["Morrel made a sign that he was ready to obey.", "모렐은 따르겠다는 신호를 했다. \"하지만,\""], ["“But,” said he, “first allow me to embrace you as your daughter did just now.”", "그가 말했다. \"먼저 방금 따님이 그러셨듯이 할아버지를 안아 드리도록 허락해 주십시오.\""], ["Noirtier’s expression could not be understood.", "누아르티에의 표정은 알 수 없었다."], ["The young man pressed his lips on the same spot, on the old man’s forehead, where Valentine’s had been. Then he bowed a second time and retired.", "청년은 발랑틴이 입맞춤했던 바로 그 자리, 노인의 이마에 입술을 눌렀다. 그러고 나서 다시 한번 고개를 숙이고 물러갔다."]], [["He found outside the door the old servant, to whom Valentine had given directions.", "문밖에서 그는 발랑틴이 지시를 일러 둔 늙은 하인을 만났다."], ["Morrel was conducted along a dark passage, which led to a little door opening on the garden, soon found the spot where he had entered, with the assistance of the shrubs gained the top of the wall, and by his ladder was in an instant in the clover-field where his cabriolet was still waiting for him.", "모렐은 어두운 통로를 따라 안내받았고, 그 통로는 정원으로 통하는 작은 문으로 이어졌다. 그는 곧 자신이 들어왔던 자리를 찾아냈고, 관목들의 도움을 받아 담장 꼭대기에 올랐으며, 사다리를 타고 순식간에 이륜마차가 여전히 그를 기다리고 있는 클로버 밭에 내려섰다."], ["He got in it, and thoroughly wearied by so many emotions, arrived about midnight in the Rue Meslay, threw himself on his bed and slept soundly. 40010m", "그는 마차에 올랐다. 그토록 많은 감정에 완전히 지친 그는 자정 무렵 멜레 거리에 도착했고, 침대에 몸을 던져 깊이 잠들었다."]], [["40020m", "넷째 권"]], [["# Chapter 74. The Villefort Family Vault", "# Chapter 74. The Villefort Family Vault"]], [["Two days after, a considerable crowd was assembled, towards ten o’clock in the morning, around the door of M. de Villefort’s house, and a long file of mourning-coaches and private carriages extended along the Faubourg Saint-Honoré and the Rue de la Pépinière.", "74장. 빌포르 가문의 납골당\n이틀 뒤, 오전 열 시 무렵 빌포르 씨 저택의 문 앞에는 상당한 인파가 모여들었고, 영구 마차와 자가용 마차가 길게 줄지어 포부르생토노레 거리와 페피니에르 거리를 따라 늘어섰다."], ["Among them was one of a very singular form, which appeared to have come from a distance.", "그 가운데 멀리서 온 듯한, 몹시 기이한 모양의 마차가 하나 있었다."], ["It was a kind of covered wagon, painted black, and was one of the first to arrive.", "검게 칠한 일종의 포장마차였는데, 가장 먼저 도착한 축에 들었다."], ["Inquiry was made, and it was ascertained that, by a strange coincidence, this carriage contained the corpse of the Marquis de Saint-Méran, and that those who had come thinking to attend one funeral would follow two.", "사람들이 까닭을 묻자, 기이한 우연으로 그 마차에 생메랑 후작의 유해가 실려 있다는 사실이 밝혀졌다. 한 번의 장례를 따르려고 온 이들이 두 번의 장례를 따르게 된 셈이었다."], ["Their number was great.", "그 수는 많았다."], ["The Marquis de Saint-Méran, one of the most zealous and faithful dignitaries of Louis XVIII. and King Charles X., had preserved a great number of friends, and these, added to the personages whom the usages of society gave Villefort a claim on, formed a considerable body.", "루이 18세와 샤를 10세를 가장 열성적이고 충실하게 섬긴 고관 중 한 사람이었던 생메랑 후작은 많은 벗을 남겼고, 거기에 사교계의 관례상 빌포르와 인연이 있는 인사들까지 더해지니 상당한 무리를 이루었다."]], [["Due information was given to the authorities, and permission obtained that the two funerals should take place at the same time.", "당국에 정식으로 통보가 들어갔고, 두 장례를 동시에 치러도 좋다는 허가가 떨어졌다."], ["A second hearse, decked with the same funereal pomp, was brought to M. de Villefort’s door, and the coffin removed into it from the post-wagon.", "똑같은 장례 의장으로 꾸민 두 번째 영구차가 빌포르 씨의 문 앞으로 옮겨졌고, 관이 포장마차에서 그리로 옮겨졌다."], ["The two bodies were to be interred in the cemetery of Père-Lachaise, where M.", "두 유해는 페르라셰즈 묘지에 안장될 예정이었다."], ["de Villefort had long since had a tomb prepared for the reception of his family.", "빌포르 씨가 오래전에 가족을 위해 마련해 둔 무덤이 그곳에 있었다."], ["The remains of poor Renée were already deposited there, and now, after ten years of separation, her father and mother were to be reunited with her.", "가엾은 르네의 유해는 이미 그 안에 안치되어 있었고, 이제 십 년간의 이별 끝에 그녀의 아버지와 어머니가 그녀와 다시 만나게 되는 것이었다."]], [["The Parisians, always curious, always affected by funereal display, looked on with religious silence while the splendid procession accompanied to their last abode two of the number of the old aristocracy—the greatest protectors of commerce and sincere devotees to their principles.", "언제나 호기심 많고, 언제나 장례 행렬에 마음이 흔들리는 파리 시민들은, 그 호화로운 행렬이 옛 귀족 가운데 두 사람을 마지막 거처로 배웅하는 광경을 경건한 침묵 속에 지켜보았다. 상업의 가장 든든한 보호자이자 자신들의 신념에 충실했던 사람들이었다."]], [["In one of the mourning-coaches Beauchamp, Debray, and Château-Renaud were talking of the very sudden death of the marchioness.", "영구 마차 중 한 대 안에서는 보샹과 드브레, 샤토르노가 후작 부인의 너무도 갑작스러운 죽음에 관해 이야기를 나누고 있었다."]], [["“I saw Madame de Saint-Méran only last year at Marseilles, when I was coming back from Algiers,”", "\"작년에 알제에서 돌아오는 길에 마르세유에서 생메랑 부인을 뵈었는데,\" 샤토르노가 말했다."], ["said Château-Renaud; “she looked like a woman destined to live to be a hundred years old, from her apparent sound health and great activity of mind and body. How old was she?”", "\"겉으로 보기에 건강도 좋으시고 정신도 몸도 정정하셔서, 백 살까지 사실 분 같았소. 연세가 어찌 되셨던가요?\""]], [["“Franz assured me,”", "\"프란츠가 그러는데,\""], ["replied Albert, “that she was sixty-six years old.", "알베르가 대답했다. \"예순여섯이셨답니다."], ["But she has not died of old age, but of grief; it appears that since the death of the marquis, which affected her very deeply, she has not completely recovered her reason.”", "하지만 노환으로 돌아가신 게 아니라 슬픔으로 돌아가셨소. 후작님이 돌아가신 뒤로 그 충격이 워낙 컸던지, 부인은 끝내 온전히 정신을 추스르지 못하셨다고 하더군요.\""]], [["“But of what disease, then, did she die?”", "\"그렇다면 무슨 병으로 돌아가셨소?\""], ["asked Debray.", "드브레가 물었다."]], [["“It is said to have been a congestion of the brain, or apoplexy, which is the same thing, is it not?”", "\"뇌충혈, 아니면 뇌졸중이라던데, 그게 그거 아니오?\""]], [["“Nearly.”", "\"비슷하지요.\""]], [["“It is difficult to believe that it was apoplexy,”", "\"뇌졸중이라니 믿기 어렵소.\""], ["said Beauchamp.", "보샹이 말했다."], ["“Madame de Saint-Méran, whom I once saw, was short, of slender form, and of a much more nervous than sanguine temperament; grief could hardly produce apoplexy in such a constitution as that of Madame de Saint-Méran.”", "\"내가 한 번 뵌 적 있는 생메랑 부인은 키가 작고 몸매가 가냘프며, 다혈질이라기보다는 훨씬 신경질적인 체질이셨소. 그런 체질에 슬픔이 뇌졸중을 일으키기는 어려울 텐데.\""]], [["“At any rate,”", "\"어쨌든,\""], ["said Albert, “whatever disease or doctor may have killed her, M.", "알베르가 말했다."], ["de Villefort, or rather, Mademoiselle Valentine,—or, still rather, our friend Franz, inherits a magnificent fortune, amounting, I believe, to 80,000 livres per annum.”", "\"어떤 병이, 또는 어떤 의사가 부인을 죽음에 이르게 했든 간에, 빌포르 씨가, 아니 정확히는 발랑틴 양이, 더 정확히는 우리 친구 프란츠가 막대한 재산을 물려받게 됐소. 내가 알기로 연 8만 리브르에 달한다더군요.\""]], [["“And this fortune will be doubled at the death of the old Jacobin, Noirtier.”", "\"게다가 그 늙은 자코뱅, 누아르티에가 죽으면 그 재산이 두 배로 불어날 거요.\""]], [["“That is a tenacious old grandfather,”", "\"끈질긴 노인장이지요.\" 보샹이 말했다."], ["said Beauchamp. “_Tenacem propositi virum_.", "\"테나켐 프로포시티 비룸, '제 뜻을 굳게 지키는 사람'이라더니."], ["I think he must have made an agreement with death to outlive all his heirs, and he appears likely to succeed.", "내 생각에 그 노인은 모든 상속인보다 오래 살겠다고 죽음과 약조라도 맺은 것 같고, 또 그 약조를 지킬 모양이오."], ["He resembles the old Conventionalist of ’93, who said to Napoleon, in 1814, ‘You bend because your empire is a young stem, weakened by rapid growth.", "1814년에 나폴레옹에게 이렇게 말한 93년의 그 늙은 국민공회 의원을 닮았소. '폐하의 제국은 빠른 성장으로 약해진 어린 줄기이기에 휘는 것입니다."], ["Take the Republic for a tutor; let us return with renewed strength to the battle-field, and I promise you 500,000 soldiers, another Marengo, and a second Austerlitz.", "공화국을 스승으로 삼으십시오. 새로운 힘으로 다시 전장으로 나아갑시다. 그러면 약속드리건대 오십만 군사와 또 한 번의 마렝고, 두 번째 아우스터리츠를 안겨 드리겠습니다."], ["Ideas do not become extinct, sire; they slumber sometimes, but only revive the stronger before they sleep entirely.’”", "사상은 결코 소멸하지 않습니다, 폐하. 때로 잠들기는 하나, 완전히 잠들기 전에 더욱 강하게 되살아날 뿐입니다.'\""]], [["“Ideas and men appeared the same to him,”", "\"그 노인에게는 사상이나 사람이나 매한가지였던 모양이오.\""], ["said Albert.", "알베르가 말했다."], ["“One thing only puzzles me, namely, how Franz d’Épinay will like a grandfather who cannot be separated from his wife.", "\"한 가지만 궁금하군요. 프란츠 데피네가, 아내와 떼어 놓을 수 없는 그런 할아버지를 어떻게 받아들일지 말이오."], ["But where is Franz?”", "그런데 프란츠는 어디 있소?\""]], [["“In the first carriage, with M. de Villefort, who considers him already as one of the family.”", "\"맨 앞 마차에 빌포르 씨와 함께 타고 있소. 빌포르 씨는 벌써 그를 한 식구로 여기고 있더군요.\""]], [["40024m\nSuch was the conversation in almost all the carriages; these two sudden deaths, so quickly following each other, astonished everyone, but no one suspected the terrible secret which M. d’Avrigny had communicated, in his nocturnal walk to M.", "거의 모든 마차 안에서 이런 대화가 오갔다. 이처럼 잇따라 빠르게 일어난 두 죽음에 모두가 놀랐지만, 다브리니 씨가 빌포르 씨에게 한밤의 산책 길에 털어놓았던 그 끔찍한 비밀을 짐작하는 이는 아무도 없었다."], ["de Villefort. They arrived in about an hour at the cemetery; the weather was mild, but dull, and in harmony with the funeral ceremony.", "한 시간쯤 지나 일행은 묘지에 닿았다. 날씨는 온화하나 흐려서 장례식과 잘 어울렸다."], ["Among the groups which flocked towards the family vault, Château-Renaud recognized Morrel, who had come alone in a cabriolet, and walked silently along the path bordered with yew-trees.", "가족 납골당으로 몰려가는 무리 가운데, 샤토르노는 모렐을 알아보았다. 모렐은 이륜마차를 타고 홀로 와서, 주목으로 가장자리를 두른 길을 따라 말없이 걷고 있었다."]], [["“You here?” said Château-Renaud, passing his arms through the young captain’s; “are you a friend of Villefort’s?", "\"자네가 여기 웬일인가?\" 샤토르노가 젊은 대위의 팔에 자기 팔을 끼며 말했다."], ["How is it that I have never met you at his house?”", "\"빌포르의 친구인가? 어째서 그 집에서는 한 번도 자네를 만나지 못했지?\""]], [["“I am no acquaintance of M. de Villefort’s,”", "\"나는 빌포르 씨와는 아는 사이가 아닐세.\""], ["answered Morrel, “but I was of Madame de Saint-Méran.”", "모렐이 대답했다. \"생메랑 부인과 아는 사이였지.\""], ["Albert came up to them at this moment with Franz.", "그때 알베르가 프란츠와 함께 그들에게 다가왔다."]], [["“The time and place are but ill-suited for an introduction.”", "\"소개를 나누기에는 때도 장소도 마땅찮네만,\" 알베르가 말했다."], ["said Albert; “but we are not superstitious. M.", "\"우리야 미신을 따지는 사람들도 아니니."], ["Morrel, allow me to present to you M.", "모렐 씨, 프란츠 데피네 씨를 소개하지요."], ["Franz d’Épinay, a delightful travelling companion, with whom I made the tour of Italy. My dear Franz, M.", "함께 이탈리아를 두루 돌아본, 더없이 유쾌한 길동무였습니다. 그리고 프란츠, 이쪽은 막시밀리앙 모렐 씨일세."], ["Maximilian Morrel, an excellent friend I have acquired in your absence, and whose name you will hear me mention every time I make any allusion to affection, wit, or amiability.”", "자네가 없는 동안 사귄 더없이 훌륭한 벗으로, 내가 우정이니 재치니 다정함이니 하는 말을 꺼낼 때마다 그 이름을 입에 올리는 걸 자네도 듣게 될 걸세.\""]], [["Morrel hesitated for a moment; he feared it would be hypocritical to accost in a friendly manner the man whom he was tacitly opposing, but his oath and the gravity of the circumstances recurred to his memory; he struggled to conceal his emotion and bowed to Franz.", "모렐은 잠시 머뭇거렸다. 그는 자신이 은밀히 적으로 맞서고 있는 사내에게 다정하게 인사를 건네는 것이 위선처럼 느껴질까 두려웠다. 그러나 자신의 맹세와 사태의 엄중함이 다시 떠올랐다. 그는 애써 감정을 감추며 프란츠에게 고개를 숙였다."]], [["“Mademoiselle de Villefort is in deep sorrow, is she not?” said Debray to Franz.", "\"빌포르 양은 몹시 슬퍼하고 있지요?\" 드브레가 프란츠에게 물었다."]], [["“Extremely,”", "\"이루 말할 수 없을 만큼요.\""], ["replied he; “she looked so pale this morning, I scarcely knew her.”", "그가 대답했다. \"오늘 아침에는 어찌나 창백하던지, 거의 알아보지 못할 뻔했습니다.\""]], [["These apparently simple words pierced Morrel to the heart.", "겉으로는 단순해 보이는 이 말이 모렐의 가슴을 후벼 팠다."], ["This man had seen Valentine, and spoken to her!", "이 사내가 발랑틴을 보았고, 또 그녀와 이야기를 나눴다니!"], ["The young and high-spirited officer required all his strength of mind to resist breaking his oath.", "젊고 혈기 넘치는 그 장교는 맹세를 깨뜨리지 않으려고 온 힘을 다해 마음을 다잡아야 했다."], ["He took the arm of Château-Renaud, and turned towards the vault, where the attendants had already placed the two coffins.", "그는 샤토르노의 팔을 잡고, 인부들이 이미 두 관을 놓아둔 납골당 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["“This is a magnificent habitation,”", "\"참으로 으리으리한 거처로군.\""], ["said Beauchamp, looking towards the mausoleum; “a summer and winter palace.", "보샹이 영묘 쪽을 바라보며 말했다. \"여름 궁전이자 겨울 궁전이라."], ["You will, in turn, enter it, my dear d’Épinay, for you will soon be numbered as one of the family.", "데피네, 자네도 머지않아 이 가문의 일원으로 꼽힐 테니, 차례가 되면 들어가게 되겠지."], ["I, as a philosopher, should like a little country-house, a cottage down there under the trees, without so many free-stones over my poor body.", "나로 말하자면, 철학자로서 차라리 시골의 작은 집, 저 나무들 아래 오두막 한 채가 좋겠소. 이 가엾은 몸뚱이 위에 그리 많은 다듬돌을 얹지 않은 곳 말이오. 죽을 때면 나는 곁의 사람들에게 볼테르가 피롱에게 써 보낸 말을 하겠소."], ["In dying, I will say to those around me what Voltaire wrote to Piron: ‘_Eo rus_, and all will be over.’ But come, Franz, take courage, your wife is an heiress.”", "'에오 루스', 그러면 만사가 끝나는 거지. 하지만 자, 프란츠, 기운을 내게. 자네 아내가 상속녀가 아닌가.\""]], [["“Indeed, Beauchamp, you are unbearable.", "\"이보게 보샹, 정말 못 말리겠군."], ["Politics has made you laugh at everything, and political men have made you disbelieve everything.", "정치가 자네를 매사에 웃게 만들었고, 정치하는 자들이 자네를 매사에 못 믿게 만들었어."], ["But when you have the honor of associating with ordinary men, and the pleasure of leaving politics for a moment, try to find your affectionate heart, which you leave with your stick when you go to the Chamber.”", "하지만 평범한 사람들과 어울리는 영광을 누리고, 잠시나마 정치를 내려놓는 즐거움을 맛볼 때에는, 자네가 의회에 갈 때 지팡이와 함께 놓고 가는 그 다정한 마음을 좀 되찾아 보게.\""]], [["“But tell me,”", "\"그렇지만 말해 보게.\""], ["said Beauchamp, “what is life?", "보샹이 말했다. \"인생이란 무엇인가?"], ["Is it not a halt in Death’s anteroom?” 40026m", "죽음의 대기실에서 잠시 멈춰 서는 것이 아닌가?\""]], [["“I am prejudiced against Beauchamp,”", "\"나는 보샹한테는 손사래를 치겠네.\""], ["said Albert, drawing Franz away, and leaving the former to finish his philosophical dissertation with Debray.", "알베르가 프란츠를 끌고 가며 말했다. 그러고는 보샹이 드브레와 더불어 철학적 논설을 마저 풀어 가도록 내버려 두었다."]], [["The Villefort vault formed a square of white stones, about twenty feet high; an interior partition separated the two families, and each apartment had its entrance door.", "빌포르 가문의 납골당은 높이 스무 자가량의 흰 돌로 쌓은 정방형 건물이었다. 안쪽 칸막이가 두 가문을 갈라놓았고, 각 칸에는 저마다 출입문이 나 있었다."], ["Here were not, as in other tombs, ignoble drawers, one above another, where thrift bestows its dead and labels them like specimens in a museum; all that was visible within the bronze gates was a gloomy-looking room, separated by a wall from the vault itself.", "이곳에는 다른 무덤들처럼, 알뜰함이 죽은 이들을 차곡차곡 쌓아 놓고 박물관의 표본처럼 이름표를 붙여 둔 옹색한 서랍 따위는 없었다. 청동 문 안으로 보이는 것이라곤, 납골당 본채와 벽으로 가로막힌 음침한 방 하나뿐이었다."], ["The two doors before mentioned were in the middle of this wall, and enclosed the Villefort and Saint-Méran coffins.", "앞서 말한 두 문이 그 벽 한가운데 나 있어, 빌포르 가문과 생메랑 가문의 관을 각기 품고 있었다."], ["There grief might freely expend itself without being disturbed by the trifling loungers who came from a picnic party to visit Père-Lachaise, or by lovers who make it their rendezvous.", "그곳에서라면, 소풍을 나왔다가 페르라셰즈를 구경하러 들른 하찮은 한량들이나 이곳을 밀회 장소로 삼는 연인들에게 방해받지 않고 마음껏 슬픔을 쏟아 낼 수 있었다."]], [["The two coffins were placed on trestles previously prepared for their reception in the right-hand crypt belonging to the Saint-Méran family.", "두 관은 미리 마련해 둔 받침대 위에 놓였다. 생메랑 가문에 속한 오른쪽 지하 묘실이었다."], ["Villefort, Franz, and a few near relatives alone entered the sanctuary.", "빌포르와 프란츠, 그리고 몇몇 가까운 친척만이 그 성소 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["As the religious ceremonies had all been performed at the door, and there was no address given, the party all separated; Château-Renaud, Albert, and Morrel, went one way, and Debray and Beauchamp the other. Franz remained with M.", "종교 의례는 모두 문 앞에서 치러졌고 따로 추도사도 없었으므로, 일행은 모두 흩어졌다. 샤토르노와 알베르, 모렐은 한쪽으로, 드브레와 보샹은 다른 쪽으로 갔다."], ["de Villefort; at the gate of the cemetery Morrel made an excuse to wait; he saw Franz and M.", "프란츠는 빌포르 씨와 함께 남았다. 모렐은 묘지 정문에서 핑계를 대고 기다렸다."], ["de Villefort get into the same mourning-coach, and thought this meeting forboded evil.", "그는 프란츠와 빌포르 씨가 같은 영구 마차에 오르는 것을 보았고, 그 만남이 흉조라고 생각했다."], ["He then returned to Paris, and although in the same carriage with Château-Renaud and Albert, he did not hear one word of their conversation.", "그런 뒤 파리로 돌아왔는데, 샤토르노와 알베르와 같은 마차에 탔으면서도 그들의 대화는 한마디도 귀에 들어오지 않았다."]], [["As Franz was about to take leave of M. de Villefort, “When shall I see you again?”", "프란츠가 빌포르 씨에게 작별을 고하려 할 때, 빌포르가 말했다."], ["said the latter.", "\"언제 다시 뵐 수 있을까요?\""]], [["“At what time you please, sir,”", "\"원하시는 시간에 언제든지요.\""], ["replied Franz.", "프란츠가 대답했다."]], [["“As soon as possible.”", "\"되도록 빨리.\""]], [["“I am at your command, sir; shall we return together?”", "\"분부대로 하겠습니다. 함께 돌아갈까요?\""]], [["“If not unpleasant to you.”", "\"불편하지 않으시다면.\""]], [["“On the contrary, I shall feel much pleasure.”", "\"불편하기는요, 오히려 기쁩니다.\""]], [["Thus, the future father and son-in-law stepped into the same carriage, and Morrel, seeing them pass, became uneasy.", "이리하여 장차 장인과 사위가 될 두 사람은 같은 마차에 올랐고, 그들이 지나가는 것을 본 모렐은 마음이 불안해졌다."], ["Villefort and Franz returned to the Faubourg Saint-Honoré.", "빌포르와 프란츠는 포부르생토노레로 돌아갔다."], ["The procureur, without going to see either his wife or his daughter, went at once to his study, and, offering the young man a chair:", "검사는 아내도 딸도 보러 가지 않고 곧장 자기 서재로 가, 젊은이에게 의자를 권하며 말했다."]], [["“M. d’Épinay,”", "\"데피네 씨,\""], ["said he, “allow me to remind you at this moment,—which is perhaps not so ill-chosen as at first sight may appear, for obedience to the wishes of the departed is the first offering which should be made at their tomb,—allow me then to remind you of the wish expressed by Madame de Saint-Méran on her death-bed, that Valentine’s wedding might not be deferred.", "그가 말했다. \"이 순간, 얼핏 보기에는 부적절해 보일지 모르나 실은 그리 잘못 고른 때가 아닐 듯싶은 이 순간에, 한 가지를 일깨워 드릴까 합니다. 고인의 뜻을 따르는 것이야말로 그 무덤에 바칠 첫 번째 공물이니까요."], ["You know the affairs of the deceased are in perfect order, and her will bequeaths to Valentine the entire property of the Saint-Méran family; the notary showed me the documents yesterday, which will enable us to draw up the contract immediately.", "그러니 생메랑 부인이 임종 자리에서 표하신 소망, 발랑틴의 혼례를 미루지 말라던 그 소망을 일깨워 드리고자 합니다. 아시다시피 고인의 재산은 완벽하게 정리되어 있고, 유언에 따라 생메랑 가문의 재산 전부가 발랑틴에게 상속됩니다. 어제 공증인이 그 서류들을 제게 보여 주었으니, 곧바로 계약서를 작성할 수 있습니다."], ["You may call on the notary, M. Deschamps, Place Beauveau, Faubourg Saint-Honoré, and you have my authority to inspect those deeds.”", "포부르생토노레의 보보 광장에 있는 공증인 데샹 씨를 찾아가시면 됩니다. 그 증서들을 살펴보셔도 좋다는 제 권한을 드리지요.\""]], [["“Sir,” replied M.", "\"각하,\""], ["d’Épinay, “it is not, perhaps, the moment for Mademoiselle Valentine, who is in deep distress, to think of a husband; indeed, I fear——”", "데피네 씨가 대답했다. \"지금은 깊은 슬픔에 잠긴 발랑틴 양이 남편을 떠올릴 만한 때가 아닐지도 모릅니다. 사실 저는 염려스럽습니다……\""]], [["40028m\n“Valentine will have no greater pleasure than that of fulfilling her grandmother’s last injunctions; there will be no obstacle from that quarter, I assure you.”", "\"발랑틴에게는 할머니의 마지막 당부를 받드는 것보다 더 큰 기쁨이 없을 겁니다. 그쪽으로는 아무 걸림돌도 없을 테니, 안심하셔도 좋습니다.\""]], [["“In that case,”", "\"그러시다면,\""], ["replied Franz, “as I shall raise none, you may make arrangements when you please; I have pledged my word, and shall feel pleasure and happiness in adhering to it.”", "프란츠가 대답했다. \"저로서도 걸림돌이 될 일은 없으니, 언제든 좋으실 때 절차를 진행하십시오. 저는 이미 약속을 드렸고, 그 약속을 지키는 일이 기쁘고도 행복합니다.\""]], [["“Then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["said Villefort, “nothing further is required.", "빌포르가 말했다. \"더 필요한 것은 없군요."], ["The contract was to have been signed three days since; we shall find it all ready, and can sign it today.”", "계약서는 사흘 전에 서명되었어야 할 것이었으니, 모든 게 준비되어 있을 겁니다. 오늘 서명할 수 있습니다.\""]], [["“But the mourning?”", "\"하지만 상중인데요?\""], ["said Franz, hesitating.", "프란츠가 망설이며 말했다."]], [["“Don’t be uneasy on that score,”", "\"그 점은 염려 마십시오.\""], ["replied Villefort; “no ceremony will be neglected in my house.", "빌포르가 대답했다. \"내 집에서는 어떤 격식도 소홀히 하지 않을 테니까요."], ["Mademoiselle de Villefort may retire during the prescribed three months to her estate of Saint-Méran; I say hers, for she inherits it today.", "빌포르 양은 정해진 석 달 동안 자기 영지인 생메랑으로 물러나 있을 수 있습니다. '자기 영지'라고 한 것은, 오늘부터 그 애가 그것을 상속받기 때문이지요."], ["There, after a few days, if you like, the civil marriage shall be celebrated without pomp or ceremony.", "그곳에서 며칠 지난 뒤, 원하신다면 화려한 의식도 격식도 없이 민사 혼인을 올리면 됩니다."], ["Madame de Saint-Méran wished her daughter should be married there.", "생메랑 부인도 딸이 그곳에서 혼례를 치르기를 바라셨으니까요."], ["When that is over, you, sir, can return to Paris, while your wife passes the time of her mourning with her mother-in-law.”", "그 일이 끝나면, 데피네 씨는 파리로 돌아오시고, 부인은 시어머니와 함께 상중의 기간을 보내면 됩니다.\""]], [["“As you please, sir,”", "\"좋으실 대로 하십시오, 각하.\""], ["said Franz.", "프란츠가 말했다."]], [["“Then,” replied M.", "\"그럼,\""], ["de Villefort, “have the kindness to wait half an hour; Valentine shall come down into the drawing-room.", "빌포르 씨가 대답했다. \"삼십 분만 기다려 주시겠습니까. 발랑틴이 응접실로 내려올 겁니다."], ["I will send for M.", "데샹 씨를 부르겠습니다."], ["Deschamps; we will read and sign the contract before we separate, and this evening Madame de Villefort shall accompany Valentine to her estate, where we will rejoin them in a week.”", "헤어지기 전에 계약서를 읽고 서명하지요. 그리고 오늘 저녁에 빌포르 부인이 발랑틴을 영지까지 데려다줄 것이고, 우리는 일주일 뒤에 그곳에서 합류하면 됩니다.\""]], [["“Sir,”", "\"각하,\""], ["said Franz, “I have one request to make.”", "프란츠가 말했다. \"한 가지 청이 있습니다.\""]], [["“What is it?”", "\"무엇입니까?\""]], [["“I wish Albert de Morcerf and Raoul de Château-Renaud to be present at this signature; you know they are my witnesses.”", "\"이 서명 자리에 알베르 드 모르세르와 라울 드 샤토르노가 함께했으면 합니다. 아시다시피 제 증인들이니까요.\""]], [["“Half an hour will suffice to apprise them; will you go for them yourself, or shall you send?”", "\"삼십 분이면 그들에게 알리기에 충분하겠지요. 직접 다녀오시겠습니까, 아니면 사람을 보내시겠습니까?\""]], [["“I prefer going, sir.”", "\"제가 직접 가는 편이 좋겠습니다.\""]], [["“I shall expect you, then, in half an hour, baron, and Valentine will be ready.”", "\"그럼 삼십 분 뒤에 기다리겠습니다, 남작. 그때 발랑틴도 준비되어 있을 겁니다.\""]], [["Franz bowed and left the room.", "프란츠는 고개를 숙이고 방을 나섰다."], ["Scarcely had the door closed, when M. de Villefort sent to tell Valentine to be ready in the drawing-room in half an hour, as he expected the notary and M.", "문이 채 닫히기도 전에, 빌포르 씨는 발랑틴에게 삼십 분 뒤 응접실로 내려와 채비하고 있으라고 일렀다. 공증인과 데피네 씨, 그리고 그의 증인들을 기다리고 있다는 것이었다."], ["d’Épinay and his witnesses. The news caused a great sensation throughout the house; Madame de Villefort would not believe it, and Valentine was thunderstruck.", "이 소식이 온 집안을 발칵 뒤집어 놓았다. 빌포르 부인은 그 말을 믿으려 들지 않았고, 발랑틴은 벼락이라도 맞은 듯했다."], ["She looked around for help, and would have gone down to her grandfather’s room, but on the stairs she met M.", "그녀는 도움을 청하려 주위를 둘러보았고, 할아버지 방으로 내려가려 했으나, 계단에서 빌포르 씨와 마주쳤다."], ["de Villefort, who took her arm and led her into the drawing-room.", "그는 그녀의 팔을 잡아 응접실로 데려갔다."], ["In the anteroom, Valentine met Barrois, and looked despairingly at the old servant.", "대기실에서 발랑틴은 바루아와 마주쳤고, 절망에 찬 눈으로 그 늙은 하인을 바라보았다."], ["A moment later, Madame de Villefort entered the drawing-room with her little Edward.", "잠시 뒤, 빌포르 부인이 어린 에두아르를 데리고 응접실로 들어왔다."], ["It was evident that she had shared the grief of the family, for she was pale and looked fatigued.", "그녀도 가족의 슬픔을 함께 나눈 것이 분명했으니, 안색이 창백하고 지쳐 보였다."], ["She sat down, took Edward on her knees, and from time to time pressed this child, on whom her affections appeared centred, almost convulsively to her bosom.", "그녀는 자리에 앉아 에두아르를 무릎에 앉혔고, 이따금 자신의 애정이 온통 쏠려 있는 듯한 이 아이를, 거의 발작하듯 가슴에 끌어안았다."]], [["Two carriages were soon heard to enter the courtyard.", "이윽고 마차 두 대가 안마당으로 들어서는 소리가 들렸다."], ["One was the notary’s; the other, that of Franz and his friends. In a moment the whole party was assembled.", "한 대는 공증인의 것이었고, 다른 한 대는 프란츠와 그 친구들의 것이었다. 순식간에 일행이 모두 모였다."], ["Valentine was so pale one might trace the blue veins from her temples, round her eyes and down her cheeks. Franz was deeply affected.", "발랑틴은 어찌나 창백한지, 관자놀이에서 두 눈 둘레와 뺨을 타고 내려가는 푸른 핏줄까지 들여다보일 지경이었다."], ["Château-Renaud and Albert looked at each other with amazement; the ceremony which was just concluded had not appeared more sorrowful than did that which was about to begin.", "프란츠는 깊이 마음이 흔들렸다. 샤토르노와 알베르는 놀란 눈빛으로 서로를 마주보았다. 방금 끝난 의식보다 이제 막 시작되려는 이 일이 더 침울해 보였던 것이다."], ["Madame de Villefort had placed herself in the shadow behind a velvet curtain, and as she constantly bent over her child, it was difficult to read the expression of her face. M.", "빌포르 부인은 벨벳 휘장 뒤 그늘 속에 자리를 잡았고, 줄곧 아이 위로 몸을 굽히고 있어 그 얼굴 표정을 읽기란 어려웠다."], ["de Villefort was, as usual, unmoved.", "빌포르 씨는 여느 때처럼 미동도 없었다."]], [["The notary, after having, according to the customary method, arranged the papers on the table, taken his place in an armchair, and raised his spectacles, turned towards Franz:", "공증인은 관례대로 탁자 위에 서류를 정리하고, 안락의자에 자리를 잡고, 안경을 추켜올린 뒤 프란츠를 향해 돌아앉았다."]], [["“Are you M. Franz de Quesnel, baron d’Épinay?”", "\"당신이 프란츠 드 케넬, 데피네 남작이십니까?\""], ["asked he, although he knew it perfectly.", "그가 물었다. 뻔히 알고 있으면서도 던진 물음이었다."]], [["“Yes, sir,”", "\"그렇습니다.\""], ["replied Franz.", "프란츠가 대답했다."], ["The notary bowed.", "공증인이 고개를 숙였다."]], [["“I have, then, to inform you, sir, at the request of M.", "\"그렇다면 알려 드릴 것이 있습니다."], ["de Villefort, that your projected marriage with Mademoiselle de Villefort has changed the feeling of M. Noirtier towards his grandchild, and that he disinherits her entirely of the fortune he would have left her.", "빌포르 씨의 요청에 따라 말씀드립니다만, 당신이 빌포르 양과 혼인하기로 한 일이 누아르티에 씨가 손녀에게 품은 마음을 바꾸어 놓았고, 그리하여 그분이 본래 손녀에게 물려주려던 재산에서 그녀를 완전히 폐적하셨습니다. 다만 서둘러 덧붙이자면,\""], ["Let me hasten to add,”", "그가 말을 이었다."], ["continued he, “that the testator, having only the right to alienate a part of his fortune, and having alienated it all, the will will not bear scrutiny, and is declared null and void.”", "\"유언자는 재산의 일부만 처분할 권리가 있는데도 전부를 처분하셨으므로, 이 유언은 효력을 인정받지 못하고 무효로 선언됩니다.\""]], [["“Yes.” said Villefort; “but I warn M.", "\"그렇습니다.\" 빌포르가 말했다."], ["d’Épinay, that during my life-time my father’s will shall never be questioned, my position forbidding any doubt to be entertained.” 40032m", "\"하지만 데피네 씨에게 미리 일러두건대, 내가 살아 있는 동안 내 아버지의 유언이 의심받는 일은 결코 없을 겁니다. 내 지위가 어떠한 의혹도 허용하지 않으니까요.\""]], [["“Sir,”", "\"각하,\""], ["said Franz, “I regret much that such a question has been raised in the presence of Mademoiselle Valentine; I have never inquired the amount of her fortune, which, however limited it may be, exceeds mine.", "프란츠가 말했다. \"발랑틴 양이 계신 자리에서 이런 문제가 거론되어 무척 유감스럽습니다. 저는 그녀의 재산이 얼마인지 한 번도 물은 적이 없습니다."], ["My family has sought consideration in this alliance with M. de Villefort; all I seek is happiness.”", "아무리 적다 한들 제 것보다는 많겠지요. 제 집안은 빌포르 씨와 맺는 이 혼사에서 명망을 구했지만, 제가 구하는 것은 오직 행복뿐입니다.\""]], [["Valentine imperceptibly thanked him, while two silent tears rolled down her cheeks.", "발랑틴은 눈에 띄지 않게 그에게 고마움을 표했고, 그러는 동안 소리 없는 눈물 두 줄기가 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸다."]], [["“Besides, sir,”", "\"게다가,\""], ["said Villefort, addressing himself to his future son-in-law, “excepting the loss of a portion of your hopes, this unexpected will need not personally wound you; M.", "빌포르가 장차 사위가 될 사람을 향해 말했다. \"기대했던 몫의 일부를 잃은 것을 빼면, 이 뜻밖의 유언이 자네 개인을 욕보일 일은 없네."], ["Noirtier’s weakness of mind sufficiently explains it. It is not because Mademoiselle Valentine is going to marry you that he is angry, but because she will marry, a union with any other would have caused him the same sorrow.", "누아르티에 씨의 정신이 쇠약해진 탓이라고 보면 충분히 설명되니까. 그분이 노여워하는 건 발랑틴 양이 자네와 결혼하기 때문이 아니라, 결혼을 한다는 사실 그 자체 때문일세."], ["Old age is selfish, sir, and Mademoiselle de Villefort has been a faithful companion to M.", "다른 누구와 맺어진다 해도 그분께 똑같은 슬픔을 안겼을 거야."], ["Noirtier, which she cannot be when she becomes the Baroness d’Épinay.", "늙으면 이기적이 되는 법이지."], ["My father’s melancholy state prevents our speaking to him on any subjects, which the weakness of his mind would incapacitate him from understanding, and I am perfectly convinced that at the present time, although, he knows that his granddaughter is going to be married, M.", "발랑틴 양은 누아르티에 씨에게 충실한 길동무였는데, 데피네 남작 부인이 되고 나면 더는 그럴 수 없을 테니까. 내 아버지는 우울한 상태라 무슨 이야기든 나눌 수가 없네. 정신이 쇠약해져 알아듣지 못하시거든."], ["Noirtier has even forgotten the name of his intended grandson.” M. de Villefort had scarcely said this, when the door opened, and Barrois appeared.", "또 나는 확신하건대, 지금 그분은 손녀가 곧 결혼한다는 것은 아시지만, 손주사위가 될 사람의 이름조차 잊으셨을 거야.\" 빌포르 씨가 이 말을 채 끝내기도 전에 문이 열리고 바루아가 나타났다."]], [["“Gentlemen,”", "\"여러분,\""], ["said he, in a tone strangely firm for a servant speaking to his masters under such solemn circumstances,—“gentlemen, M.", "그가 말했다. 이런 엄숙한 자리에서 주인들에게 말을 건네는 하인치고는 묘하게 단호한 어조였다."], ["Noirtier de Villefort wishes to speak immediately to M. Franz de Quesnel, baron d’Épinay.”", "\"여러분, 누아르티에 드 빌포르 어르신께서 프란츠 드 케넬, 데피네 남작님과 지금 당장 이야기를 나누고 싶어 하십니다.\""], ["He, as well as the notary, that there might be no mistake in the person, gave all his titles to the bridegroom elect.", "그 역시 공증인과 마찬가지로, 인물을 혼동하지 않도록 신랑 후보의 모든 직함을 빠짐없이 붙였다."]], [["Villefort started, Madame de Villefort let her son slip from her knees, Valentine rose, pale and dumb as a statue.", "빌포르가 흠칫했고, 빌포르 부인은 무릎에 앉혔던 아들을 미끄러지듯 내려놓았으며, 발랑틴은 조각상처럼 창백하고 말없이 일어섰다."], ["Albert and Château-Renaud exchanged a second look, more full of amazement than the first.", "알베르와 샤토르노는 처음보다 더 놀란 눈빛으로 두 번째 시선을 주고받았다."], ["The notary looked at Villefort.", "공증인은 빌포르를 바라보았다."]], [["“It is impossible,” said the procureur. “M.", "\"그건 불가능하오.\" 검사가 말했다."], ["d’Épinay cannot leave the drawing-room at present.”", "\"데피네 씨는 지금 응접실을 떠날 수 없소.\""]], [["“It is at this moment,”", "\"바로 지금,\""], ["replied Barrois with the same firmness, “that M.", "바루아가 변함없이 단호하게 대답했다."], ["Noirtier, my master, wishes to speak on important subjects to M. Franz d’Épinay.”", "\"제 주인이신 누아르티에 어르신께서 프란츠 데피네 남작님과 중대한 일을 의논하고자 하십니다.\""]], [["“Grandpapa Noirtier can speak now, then,”", "\"그럼 누아르티에 할아버지가 이제 말을 할 수 있다는 거예요?\""], ["said Edward, with his habitual quickness.", "에두아르가 평소의 그 잽싼 말투로 끼어들었다."], ["However, his remark did not make Madame de Villefort even smile, so much was every mind engaged, and so solemn was the situation.", "그러나 그 말에 빌포르 부인조차 미소를 짓지 않았다. 모두의 마음이 그만큼 어딘가에 쏠려 있었고, 상황이 그만큼 엄숙했던 것이다."]], [["“Tell M. Nortier,”", "\"누아르티에 씨께 전하게.\""], ["resumed Villefort, “that what he demands is impossible.”", "빌포르가 다시 입을 열었다. \"그분이 청하시는 일은 불가능하다고.\""]], [["“Then, M. Nortier gives notice to these gentlemen,”", "\"그렇다면, 누아르티에 어르신께서 여러분께 일러두라 하십니다.\""], ["replied Barrois, “that he will give orders to be carried to the drawing-room.”", "바루아가 대답했다. \"사람을 시켜 당신을 응접실로 모셔 오게 하시겠다고요.\""]], [["Astonishment was at its height.", "놀라움은 극에 달했다."], ["Something like a smile was perceptible on Madame de Villefort’s countenance. Valentine instinctively raised her eyes, as if to thank heaven.", "빌포르 부인의 얼굴에는 미소 비슷한 것이 어렸다. 발랑틴은 하늘에 감사라도 드리려는 듯 저도 모르게 눈을 들어 올렸다."]], [["“Pray go, Valentine,”", "\"발랑틴, 가 보아라.\""], ["said; M. de Villefort, “and see what this new fancy of your grandfather’s is.”", "빌포르 씨가 말했다. \"할아버지의 이 새로운 변덕이 대체 무엇인지 알아보거라.\""], ["Valentine rose quickly, and was hastening joyfully towards the door, when M. de Villefort altered his intention.", "발랑틴이 얼른 일어나 기쁜 마음으로 문 쪽으로 서둘러 가려는데, 빌포르 씨가 마음을 바꾸었다."]], [["“Stop,”", "\"멈추거라.\""], ["said he; “I will go with you.”", "그가 말했다. \"나도 함께 가마.\""]], [["“Excuse me, sir,”", "\"실례합니다, 각하.\""], ["said Franz, “since M.", "프란츠가 말했다."], ["Noirtier sent for me, I am ready to attend to his wish; besides, I shall be happy to pay my respects to him, not having yet had the honor of doing so.”", "\"누아르티에 씨가 저를 부르셨으니, 저는 그분의 뜻에 따를 준비가 되어 있습니다. 더구나 아직 인사를 올리는 영광을 누리지 못했으니, 기꺼이 문안을 드리고 싶습니다.\""]], [["“Pray, sir,”", "\"부디, 데피네 씨.\""], ["said Villefort with marked uneasiness, “do not disturb yourself.” 40032m", "빌포르가 역력한 불안을 드러내며 말했다. \"수고로이 나서지 마시오.\""]], [["“Forgive me, sir,”", "\"용서하십시오, 각하.\""], ["said Franz in a resolute tone.", "프란츠가 단호한 어조로 말했다."], ["“I would not lose this opportunity of proving to M. Noirtier how wrong it would be of him to encourage feelings of dislike to me, which I am determined to conquer, whatever they may be, by my devotion.”", "\"저는 이 기회를 놓치고 싶지 않습니다. 누아르티에 씨께서 저에게 품으신 반감이 어떤 것이든, 제 헌신으로 반드시 누그러뜨리겠다고 마음먹은 터이니, 그런 마음을 키우시는 것이 얼마나 잘못된 일인지 그분께 보여 드리고 싶습니다.\""]], [["And without listening to Villefort he arose, and followed Valentine, who was running downstairs with the joy of a shipwrecked mariner who finds a rock to cling to. M.", "그러고는 빌포르의 말은 듣지도 않고 자리에서 일어나, 매달릴 바위를 찾은 난파 선원처럼 기쁨에 차 계단을 뛰어 내려가는 발랑틴을 뒤따랐다."], ["de Villefort followed them.", "빌포르 씨도 그 뒤를 따랐다."], ["Château-Renaud and Morcerf exchanged a third look of still increasing wonder.", "샤토르노와 모르세르는 더욱 커진 경이의 눈빛으로 세 번째 시선을 주고받았다."]], [["# Chapter 75. A Signed Statement", "# Chapter 75. A Signed Statement"]], [["Noirtier was prepared to receive them, dressed in black, and installed in his armchair.", "누아르티에는 그들을 맞을 채비를 하고 있었다. 검은 옷을 차려입고 안락의자에 자리 잡은 모습이었다."], ["When the three persons he expected had entered, he looked at the door, which his valet immediately closed.", "기다리던 세 사람이 들어서자, 그는 문 쪽을 바라보았고 하인이 곧바로 문을 닫았다."]], [["“Listen,” whispered Villefort to Valentine, who could not conceal her joy; “if M.", "\"잘 듣거라.\" 빌포르가 기쁨을 감추지 못하는 발랑틴에게 속삭였다."], ["Noirtier wishes to communicate anything which would delay your marriage, I forbid you to understand him.”", "\"만일 누아르티에 어른께서 네 결혼을 미루게 할 무언가를 전하려 하시거든, 그 뜻을 알아듣는 것을 금하겠다.\""]], [["Valentine blushed, but did not answer.", "발랑틴은 얼굴을 붉혔으나 대답하지 않았다."], ["Villefort, approached Noirtier.", "빌포르가 누아르티에에게 다가갔다."]], [["“Here is M. Franz d’Épinay,”", "\"프란츠 데피네 군입니다.\""], ["said he; “you requested to see him.", "그가 말했다. \"보고 싶다 하셨지요."], ["We have all wished for this interview, and I trust it will convince you how ill-formed are your objections to Valentine’s marriage.”", "저희 모두 이 만남을 바라 왔습니다. 발랑틴의 결혼에 대한 아버님의 반대가 얼마나 근거 없는 것인지 이 자리에서 깨닫게 되시리라 믿습니다.\""]], [["Noirtier answered only by a look which made Villefort’s blood run cold.", "누아르티에는 빌포르의 피를 얼어붙게 하는 눈빛으로만 대답했다."], ["He motioned to Valentine to approach. In a moment, thanks to her habit of conversing with her grandfather, she understood that he asked for a key.", "그는 발랑틴에게 가까이 오라는 몸짓을 했다. 할아버지와 대화하는 데 익숙한 발랑틴은 이내 그가 열쇠를 찾고 있다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["Then his eye was fixed on the drawer of a small chest between the windows.", "이어 그의 눈길이 두 창문 사이에 놓인 작은 궤의 서랍에 고정되었다."], ["She opened the drawer, and found a key; and, understanding that was what he wanted, again watched his eyes, which turned toward an old secretaire which had been neglected for many years and was supposed to contain nothing but useless documents.", "그녀가 서랍을 열어 열쇠 하나를 찾아냈다. 그가 원하는 것임을 알아채고는 다시 그의 눈을 살폈다. 그 눈은 여러 해 동안 손도 대지 않아 쓸모없는 서류밖에 들어 있지 않으리라 여겨지던 낡은 책상 쪽을 향하고 있었다."]], [["“Shall I open the secretaire?”", "\"이 책상을 열까요?\""], ["asked Valentine.", "발랑틴이 물었다."]], [["“Yes,” said the old man.", "\"그래.\" 노인이 말했다."]], [["“And the drawers?”", "\"서랍들도요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["“Those at the side?”", "\"옆쪽 서랍들이요?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"아니.\""]], [["“The middle one?”", "\"가운데 서랍이요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["Valentine opened it and drew out a bundle of papers.", "발랑틴이 그것을 열고 서류 한 묶음을 꺼냈다."], ["“Is that what you wish for?”", "\"이것을 원하시는 거예요?\""], ["asked she.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“No.”", "\"아니.\""]], [["She took successively all the other papers out till the drawer was empty.", "그녀는 서랍이 빌 때까지 나머지 서류들을 차례로 다 꺼냈다."], ["“But there are no more,”", "\"하지만 더는 없는걸요.\""], ["said she. Noirtier’s eye was fixed on the dictionary.", "그녀가 말했다. 누아르티에의 눈은 사전에 고정되어 있었다."]], [["“Yes, I understand, grandfather,”", "\"네, 알겠어요, 할아버지.\""], ["said the young girl.", "처녀가 말했다."]], [["40036m\nShe pointed to each letter of the alphabet.", "그녀는 알파벳 글자를 하나하나 짚어 갔다."], ["At the letter S the old man stopped her.", "S에 이르자 노인이 그녀를 멈추게 했다."], ["She opened, and found the word “secret.”", "그녀가 사전을 펼쳐 '비밀'이라는 단어를 찾아냈다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["is there a secret spring?”", "비밀 용수철이 있는 거예요?\""], ["said Valentine.", "발랑틴이 물었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["said Noirtier.", "누아르티에가 말했다."]], [["“And who knows it?” Noirtier looked at the door where the servant had gone out.", "\"누가 그걸 알고 있죠?\" 누아르티에는 하인이 나간 문 쪽을 바라보았다."]], [["“Barrois?”", "\"바루아요?\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["“Shall I call him?”", "\"그를 부를까요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["Valentine went to the door, and called Barrois.", "발랑틴이 문가로 가서 바루아를 불렀다."], ["Villefort’s impatience during this scene made the perspiration roll from his forehead, and Franz was stupefied.", "이 장면이 이어지는 동안 빌포르의 초조함은 이마에 땀이 흘러내릴 지경이었고, 프란츠는 얼이 빠져 있었다."], ["The old servant came.", "늙은 하인이 들어왔다."]], [["“Barrois,”", "\"바루아,\""], ["said Valentine, “my grandfather has told me to open that drawer in the secretaire, but there is a secret spring in it, which you know—will you open it?”", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"할아버지께서 책상의 저 서랍을 열라고 하셨는데, 그 안에 비밀 용수철이 있고 자네가 그걸 안다는구나. 좀 열어 주겠나?\""]], [["Barrois looked at the old man. “Obey,”", "바루아가 노인을 바라보았다. \"따르거라.\""], ["said Noirtier’s intelligent eye.", "누아르티에의 영민한 눈이 말했다."], ["Barrois touched a spring, the false bottom came out, and they saw a bundle of papers tied with a black string.", "바루아가 용수철 하나를 건드리자 거짓 바닥이 빠져나왔고, 검은 끈으로 묶인 서류 한 묶음이 드러났다."]], [["“Is that what you wish for?” said Barrois.", "\"이것을 원하십니까?\" 바루아가 말했다."]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["“Shall I give these papers to M. de Villefort?” “No.”", "\"이 서류를 빌포르 씨께 드릴까요?\" \"아니.\""]], [["“To Mademoiselle Valentine?”", "\"발랑틴 아가씨께요?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"아니.\""]], [["“To M. Franz d’Épinay?”", "\"프란츠 데피네 씨께요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["Franz, astonished, advanced a step.", "프란츠가 놀라 한 걸음 다가섰다."], ["“To me, sir?” said he.", "\"제게요?\" 그가 말했다."]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["Franz took them from Barrois and casting a glance at the cover, read:", "프란츠는 바루아에게서 그것을 받아 들고 겉장을 흘끗 보더니 읽었다."]], [["“‘To be given, after my death, to General Durand, who shall bequeath the packet to his son, with an injunction to preserve it as containing an important document.’", "\"'내가 죽은 뒤 뒤랑 장군에게 전할 것. 장군은 이 꾸러미를 아들에게 물려주되, 중요한 문서가 들었으니 잘 보관하라 일러 둘 것.'"]], [["“Well, sir,”", "\"자,\""], ["asked Franz, “what do you wish me to do with this paper?”", "프란츠가 물었다. \"이 서류로 제가 무엇을 하길 바라시는지요?\""]], [["“To preserve it, sealed up as it is, doubtless,”", "\"분명, 봉인된 그대로 보관해 두기를 바라시는 게지요.\""], ["said the procureur.", "검사가 말했다."]], [["“No,” replied Noirtier eagerly.", "\"아니.\" 누아르티에가 다급히 답했다."]], [["“Do you wish him to read it?”", "\"그가 이것을 읽기를 바라세요?\""], ["said Valentine.", "발랑틴이 물었다."]], [["“Yes,” replied the old man.", "\"그래.\" 노인이 답했다."]], [["“You understand, baron, my grandfather wishes you to read this paper,”", "\"남작님, 할아버지께서 이 서류를 읽어 주시기를 바라십니다.\""], ["said Valentine.", "발랑틴이 말했다."]], [["“Then let us sit down,”", "\"그럼 앉도록 하지.\""], ["said Villefort impatiently, “for it will take some time.”", "빌포르가 초조하게 말했다. \"시간이 좀 걸릴 테니.\""]], [["“Sit down,”", "\"앉거라.\""], ["said the old man.", "노인이 말했다."], ["Villefort took a chair, but Valentine remained standing by her father’s side, and Franz before him, holding the mysterious paper in his hand.", "빌포르는 의자를 끌어다 앉았지만, 발랑틴은 아버지 곁에 선 채로 있었고, 프란츠는 그 수수께끼 같은 서류를 손에 든 채 노인 앞에 서 있었다."], ["“Read,” said the old man.", "\"읽거라.\" 노인이 말했다."], ["Franz untied it, and in the midst of the most profound silence read:\n40038m", "프란츠가 끈을 풀었고, 더없이 깊은 침묵 속에서 읽어 내려갔다."]], [["“‘_Extract of the report of a meeting of the Bonapartist Club in the Rue Saint-Jacques, held February 5th, 1815_.’”", "\"'1815년 2월 5일, 생자크 거리에서 열린 보나파르트파 클럽 회합의 기록 발췌.'\""]], [["Franz stopped.", "프란츠가 멈추었다."], ["“February 5th, 1815!”", "\"1815년 2월 5일이라니!\""], ["said he; “it is the day my father was murdered.”", "그가 말했다. \"제 아버지가 살해되신 날입니다.\""], ["Valentine and Villefort were dumb; the eye of the old man alone seemed to say clearly, “Go on.”", "발랑틴과 빌포르는 입을 다물었다. 오직 노인의 눈만이 또렷이 말하는 듯했다. \"계속하거라.\""]], [["“But it was on leaving this club,”", "\"하지만 아버지가 사라지신 것은,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “my father disappeared.”", "\"바로 이 클럽을 나서면서였습니다.\""], ["Noirtier’s eye continued to say, “Read.” He resumed:—", "누아르티에의 눈은 여전히 \"읽거라.\"라고 말하고 있었다. 그가 다시 읽기 시작했다."]], [["“‘The undersigned Louis-Jacques Beaurepaire, lieutenant-colonel of artillery, Étienne Duchampy, general of brigade, and Claude Lecharpal, keeper of woods and forests, declare, that on the 4th of February, a letter arrived from the Island of Elba, recommending to the kindness and the confidence of the Bonapartist Club, General Flavien de Quesnel, who having served the emperor from 1804 to 1814 was supposed to be devoted to the interests of the Napoleon dynasty, notwithstanding the title of baron which Louis XVIII.", "\"'아래 서명한 포병 중령 루이자크 보르파르, 여단장 에티엔 뒤샹피, 그리고 산림 관리관 클로드 르샤르팔은 다음과 같이 진술한다. 2월 4일, 엘바섬에서 한 통의 편지가 도착하여, 플라비앵 드 케넬 장군을 보나파르트파 클럽의 호의와 신뢰에 부탁하였다."], ["had just granted to him with his estate of Épinay.", "그는 1804년부터 1814년까지 황제를 섬겼으므로, 루이 18세가 막 그에게 데피네 영지와 함께 남작 칭호를 내렸음에도, 나폴레옹 왕조의 이익에 충실하리라 여겨졌다."]], [["400340m\n“‘A note was in consequence addressed to General de Quesnel, begging him to be present at the meeting next day, the 5th.", "\"'이에 따라 케넬 장군에게 한 통의 쪽지가 보내져, 이튿날인 5일 회합에 참석해 달라고 청하였다."], ["The note indicated neither the street nor the number of the house where the meeting was to be held; it bore no signature, but it announced to the general that someone would call for him if he would be ready at nine o’clock.", "쪽지에는 회합이 열리는 거리도 집 번지도 적혀 있지 않았고, 서명도 없었으나, 아홉 시에 채비를 갖추고 있으면 누군가 모시러 오리라고 장군에게 알렸다."], ["The meetings were always held from that time till midnight.", "회합은 늘 그 시각부터 자정까지 열렸다."], ["At nine o’clock the president of the club presented himself; the general was ready, the president informed him that one of the conditions of his introduction was that he should be eternally ignorant of the place of meeting, and that he would allow his eyes to be bandaged, swearing that he would not endeavor to take off the bandage.", "아홉 시에 클럽의 의장이 나타났고, 장군은 채비를 마친 상태였다. 의장은 그를 안내하는 조건 가운데 하나가 회합 장소를 영영 모르는 것이며, 따라서 눈을 가리게 두되 가리개를 벗으려 하지 않겠다고 맹세해야 한다고 일렀다."], ["General de Quesnel accepted the condition, and promised on his honor not to seek to discover the road they took.", "케넬 장군은 그 조건을 받아들이고, 자신들이 지나는 길을 알아내려 하지 않겠다고 명예를 걸고 약속했다."], ["The general’s carriage was ready, but the president told him it was impossible for him to use it, since it was useless to blindfold the master if the coachman knew through what streets he went.", "장군의 마차가 준비되어 있었으나, 의장은 그것을 쓸 수 없다고 했다. 주인의 눈을 가린들 마부가 어느 거리를 지나는지 안다면 소용없는 일이기 때문이었다. \"그럼 어찌해야 하오?\""], ["“What must be done then?”", "장군이 물었다."], ["asked the general.—“I have my carriage here,”", "\"제 마차가 여기 있습니다.\""], ["said the president.", "의장이 말했다."]], [["“‘“Have you, then, so much confidence in your servant that you can intrust him with a secret you will not allow me to know?”", "\"'\"그럼 당신은 하인을 어찌나 믿기에, 내게는 알리지 않으려는 비밀을 하인에게는 맡긴단 말이오?\""]], [["“‘“Our coachman is a member of the club,”", "\"'\"우리 마부도 클럽의 일원입니다.\""], ["said the president; “we shall be driven by a State-Councillor.”", "의장이 말했다. \"한 국참사관이 우리를 모실 겁니다.\""]], [["“‘“Then we run another risk,” said the general, laughing, “that of being upset.”", "\"'\"그렇다면 또 다른 위험을 무릅쓰는 셈이군.\" 장군이 웃으며 말했다."], ["We insert this joke to prove that the general was not in the least compelled to attend the meeting, but that he came willingly.", "\"마차가 뒤집힐 위험 말이오.\" 우리는 이 농담을 기록하여, 장군이 조금도 강요받아 회합에 나온 것이 아니라 스스로 기꺼이 왔음을 증명하고자 한다."], ["When they were seated in the carriage the president reminded the general of his promise to allow his eyes to be bandaged, to which he made no opposition.", "두 사람이 마차에 앉자, 의장은 눈을 가리겠다는 장군의 약속을 상기시켰고, 장군은 이에 반대하지 않았다."], ["On the road the president thought he saw the general make an attempt to remove the handkerchief, and reminded him of his oath.", "길을 가던 중 의장은 장군이 가리개를 벗으려 하는 것을 본 듯하여 맹세를 일깨웠다."], ["“Sure enough,”", "\"그렇지.\""], ["said the general.", "장군이 말했다."], ["The carriage stopped at an alley leading out of the Rue Saint-Jacques.", "마차는 생자크 거리에서 빠져나가는 어느 골목 어귀에 멈췄다."], ["The general alighted, leaning on the arm of the president, of whose dignity he was not aware, considering him simply as a member of the club; they went through the alley, mounted a flight of stairs, and entered the assembly-room.", "장군은 의장의 팔에 기대어 내렸는데, 그의 지위는 알지 못한 채 그저 클럽의 일원으로만 여겼다. 두 사람은 골목을 지나 층계를 올라 회의실로 들어섰다."]], [["“‘The deliberations had already begun.", "\"'심의는 이미 시작되어 있었다."], ["The members, apprised of the sort of presentation which was to be made that evening, were all in attendance.", "그날 저녁 어떤 소개가 이루어지리라는 것을 통보받은 회원들은 모두 자리에 있었다."], ["When in the middle of the room the general was invited to remove his bandage, he did so immediately, and was surprised to see so many well-known faces in a society of whose existence he had till then been ignorant.", "방 한가운데에서 가리개를 벗으라는 권유를 받자 장군은 곧바로 그렇게 했고, 그때껏 존재조차 모르던 모임에서 그토록 잘 아는 얼굴들을 그토록 많이 보고는 놀랐다."], ["They questioned him as to his sentiments, but he contented himself with answering, that the letters from the Island of Elba ought to have informed them——’”", "사람들이 그의 견해를 묻자, 그는 엘바섬에서 온 편지가 이미 그것을 알려 주었을 것이라고만 대답하였다……'\""]], [["Franz interrupted himself by saying, “My father was a royalist; they need not have asked his sentiments, which were well known.”", "프란츠가 읽기를 멈추고 말했다. \"제 아버지는 왕당파였습니다. 누구나 다 아는 견해를 물을 필요도 없었지요.\""]], [["“And hence,”", "\"그래서,\""], ["said Villefort, “arose my affection for your father, my dear M. Franz.", "빌포르가 말했다. \"내가 자네 아버지에게 애정을 품게 된 걸세, 친애하는 프란츠."], ["Opinions held in common are a ready bond of union.”", "같은 생각을 품으면 손쉽게 결속의 끈이 되는 법이니까.\""]], [["“Read again,”", "\"다시 읽거라.\""], ["said the old man.", "노인이 말했다."]], [["Franz continued:", "프란츠가 이어 읽었다."]], [["“‘The president then sought to make him speak more explicitly, but M. de Quesnel replied that he wished first to know what they wanted with him.", "\"'그러자 의장은 그가 더 분명히 말하도록 청했으나, 케넬 씨는 먼저 자신에게 무엇을 원하는지 알고 싶다고 답했다."], ["He was then informed of the contents of the letter from the Island of Elba, in which he was recommended to the club as a man who would be likely to advance the interests of their party.", "이에 엘바섬에서 온 편지의 내용을 그에게 알려 주었는데, 거기서 그는 자기네 당의 이익을 증진시킬 만한 인물로 클럽에 천거되어 있었다."], ["One paragraph spoke of the return of Bonaparte and promised another letter and further details, on the arrival of the _Pharaon_ belonging to the shipbuilder Morrel, of Marseilles, whose captain was entirely devoted to the emperor.", "한 대목은 보나파르트의 귀환을 언급하며, 마르세유의 조선업자 모렐의 소유인 파라옹호가 도착하는 대로 또 한 통의 편지와 더 자세한 정황을 알려 주겠다고 약속하고 있었다. 그 배의 선장은 황제에게 온전히 충성하는 자였다."], ["During all this time, the general, on whom they thought to have relied as on a brother, manifested evidently signs of discontent and repugnance.", "이 모든 이야기가 오가는 동안, 형제처럼 믿을 수 있으리라 여겼던 장군은 분명 불만과 거부의 기색을 드러냈다."], ["When the reading was finished, he remained silent, with knitted brows.", "낭독이 끝나자 그는 미간을 찌푸린 채 말이 없었다."]], [["“‘“Well,”", "\"'\"자,\""], ["asked the president, “what do you say to this letter, general?”", "의장이 물었다. \"이 편지를 어찌 생각하십니까, 장군?\""]], [["“‘“I say that it is too soon after declaring myself for Louis XVIII. to break my vow in behalf of the ex-emperor.”", "\"'\"내가 루이 18세를 지지한다고 선언한 지 얼마 되지도 않았는데, 폐위된 황제를 위해 그 맹세를 깨기에는 너무 이르다고 생각하오.\""], ["This answer was too clear to permit of any mistake as to his sentiments. “General,”", "이 대답은 그의 견해를 오해할 여지가 없을 만큼 분명했다. \"장군,\" 의장이 말했다."], ["said the president, “we acknowledge no King Louis XVIII., or an ex-emperor, but his majesty the emperor and king, driven from France, which is his kingdom, by violence and treason.”", "\"우리는 루이 18세 왕도, 폐위된 황제도 인정하지 않습니다. 오직 폭력과 반역으로 자신의 왕국인 프랑스에서 쫓겨난 황제 폐하만을 인정할 뿐입니다.\""]], [["“‘“Excuse me, gentlemen,”", "\"'\"용서하시오, 여러분.\" 장군이 말했다."], ["said the general; “you may not acknowledge Louis XVIII., but I do, as he has made me a baron and a field-marshal, and I shall never forget that for these two titles I am indebted to his happy return to France.”", "\"당신들은 루이 18세를 인정하지 않을지 모르나, 나는 인정하오. 그분이 나를 남작과 원수로 삼아 주셨으니까. 그리고 그 두 칭호가 그분의 다행스러운 프랑스 귀환 덕분임을 결코 잊지 않을 것이오.\""]], [["“‘“Sir,”", "\"'\"선생,\""], ["said the president, rising with gravity, “be careful what you say; your words clearly show us that they are deceived concerning you in the Island of Elba, and have deceived us!", "의장이 엄숙히 일어서며 말했다. \"말을 조심하시오. 당신의 말은 엘바섬에서 당신에 대해 잘못 알았으며, 그래서 우리까지 속았음을 분명히 보여 주는군요!"], ["The communication has been made to you in consequence of the confidence placed in you, and which does you honor.", "당신에게 신뢰를 두었기에 이 사실을 전한 것이고, 그것은 당신에게 명예로운 일이오."], ["Now we discover our error; a title and promotion attach you to the government we wish to overturn.", "그런데 이제 우리의 잘못을 깨달았소. 칭호와 승진이 당신을, 우리가 무너뜨리려는 정부에 묶어 두고 있구려."], ["We will not constrain you to help us; we enroll no one against his conscience, but we will compel you to act generously, even if you are not disposed to do so.”", "우리는 당신에게 협력을 강요하지 않겠소. 양심에 어긋나게 누구를 끌어들이지는 않으니까. 그러나 당신이 내키지 않더라도, 신사답게 처신하도록 강제하겠소.\""]], [["“‘“You would call acting generously, knowing your conspiracy and not informing against you, that is what I should call becoming your accomplice.", "\"'\"당신들의 음모를 알면서도 고발하지 않는 것을 신사다운 처신이라 부른다면, 나라면 그것을 공범이 되는 것이라 부르겠소."], ["You see I am more candid than you.”’”", "보다시피 나는 당신들보다 솔직하오.\"'\""]], [["“Ah, my father!”", "\"아, 아버지!\""], ["said Franz, interrupting himself.", "프란츠가 읽기를 멈추고 말했다."], ["“I understand now why they murdered him.”", "\"이제야 그들이 왜 아버지를 죽였는지 알겠습니다.\""], ["Valentine could not help casting one glance towards the young man, whose filial enthusiasm it was delightful to behold.", "발랑틴은 저도 모르게 그 젊은이를 향해 한 번 눈길을 던졌다. 효심으로 가득 찬 그의 모습은 바라보기에 흐뭇했다."], ["Villefort walked to and fro behind them.", "빌포르는 그들 뒤에서 이리저리 서성였다."], ["Noirtier watched the expression of each one, and preserved his dignified and commanding attitude.", "누아르티에는 저마다의 표정을 살피며 위엄 있고 당당한 자세를 그대로 지켰다."], ["Franz returned to the manuscript, and continued:", "프란츠가 다시 원고로 돌아가 읽어 갔다."]], [["“‘“Sir,”", "\"'\"선생,\""], ["said the president, “you have been invited to join this assembly—you were not forced here; it was proposed to you to come blindfolded—you accepted.", "의장이 말했다. \"당신은 이 모임에 초대받은 것이지 강제로 끌려온 게 아니오. 눈을 가리고 오라는 청을 받았고, 당신은 받아들였소."], ["When you complied with this twofold request you well knew we did not wish to secure the throne of Louis XVIII., or we should not take so much care to avoid the vigilance of the police.", "그 두 가지 청에 응했을 때, 당신은 우리가 루이 18세의 왕좌를 지키려는 게 아님을 잘 알았을 거요. 그게 아니라면 경찰의 감시를 피하려 이토록 애쓸 까닭이 없으니까."], ["It would be conceding too much to allow you to put on a mask to aid you in the discovery of our secret, and then to remove it that you may ruin those who have confided in you.", "당신이 가면을 써서 우리의 비밀을 알아낸 뒤, 다시 그것을 벗어 우리를 믿은 자들을 파멸시키도록 두는 것은 지나친 양보일 것이오."], ["No, no, you must first say if you declare yourself for the king of a day who now reigns, or for his majesty the emperor.”", "안 되오, 안 돼. 먼저 말하시오. 지금 다스리는 하루짜리 왕을 지지하는지, 아니면 황제 폐하를 지지하는지.\""]], [["“‘“I am a royalist,”", "\"'\"나는 왕당파요.\""], ["replied the general; “I have taken the oath of allegiance to Louis XVIII., and I will adhere to it.”", "장군이 답했다. \"루이 18세께 충성의 맹세를 했고, 그것을 지킬 것이오.\""], ["These words were followed by a general murmur, and it was evident that several of the members were discussing the propriety of making the general repent of his rashness.", "이 말에 좌중에 웅성거림이 일었고, 여러 회원이 장군에게 그 경솔함을 후회하게 만드는 것이 옳은지 의논하는 기색이 역력했다."]], [["“‘The president again arose, and having imposed silence, said,—“Sir, you are too serious and too sensible a man not to understand the consequences of our present situation, and your candor has already dictated to us the conditions which remain for us to offer you.”", "\"'의장이 다시 일어나 침묵을 명한 뒤 말했다. \"선생, 당신은 너무도 진중하고 분별 있는 분이라, 지금 우리 처지가 어떤 결과를 부를지 모를 리 없을 거요. 게다가 당신의 솔직함이 우리가 당신에게 제시할 조건을 이미 정해 주었구려.\""], ["The general, putting his hand on his sword, exclaimed,—“If you talk of honor, do not begin by disavowing its laws, and impose nothing by violence.”", "장군은 검에 손을 얹으며 외쳤다. \"명예를 논하려거든, 먼저 그 법도를 저버리지 마시오. 그리고 어떤 것도 폭력으로 강요하지 마시오.\""]], [["“‘“And you, sir,”", "\"'\"그리고 당신은, 선생.\""], ["continued the president, with a calmness still more terrible than the general’s anger, “I advise you not to touch your sword.”", "의장이 장군의 분노보다 더 무서운 침착함으로 말을 이었다. \"검에 손대지 않는 게 좋을 거요.\""], ["The general looked around him with slight uneasiness; however he did not yield, but calling up all his fortitude, said,—“I will not swear.”", "장군은 가벼운 불안을 느끼며 주위를 둘러보았다. 그러나 굴하지 않고 온 용기를 끌어모아 말했다. \"맹세하지 않겠소.\""]], [["“‘“Then you must die,” replied the president calmly. M.", "\"'\"그렇다면 죽어야 하오.\" 의장이 차분히 답했다."], ["d’Épinay became very pale; he looked round him a second time, several members of the club were whispering, and getting their arms from under their cloaks.", "데피네 씨는 새파랗게 질렸다. 그가 다시 한 번 주위를 둘러보니, 여러 회원이 수군대며 망토 밑에서 무기를 꺼내고 있었다. \"장군,\""], ["“General,”", "의장이 말했다."], ["said the president, “do not alarm yourself; you are among men of honor who will use every means to convince you before resorting to the last extremity, but as you have said, you are among conspirators, you are in possession of our secret, and you must restore it to us.”", "\"겁먹을 것 없소. 당신은 명예로운 자들 가운데 있고, 그들은 마지막 수단에 이르기 전에 온갖 방법으로 당신을 설득하려 할 거요. 그러나 당신 말대로, 당신은 음모자들 가운데 있고, 우리의 비밀을 손에 쥐었으니, 그것을 우리에게 돌려주어야 하오.\""], ["A significant silence followed these words, and as the general did not reply,—“Close the doors,”", "이 말 뒤에 의미심장한 침묵이 흘렀고, 장군이 대답하지 않자 의장이 문지기에게 일렀다."], ["said the president to the door-keeper. 40042m", "\"문을 닫으시오.\""]], [["“‘The same deadly silence succeeded these words.", "\"'같은 죽음 같은 침묵이 이 말 뒤에 이어졌다."], ["Then the general advanced, and making a violent effort to control his feelings,—“I have a son,”", "그러자 장군이 앞으로 나서며, 감정을 억누르려 격렬히 애쓰면서 말했다. \"내게는 아들이 있소."], ["said he, “and I ought to think of him, finding myself among assassins.”", "살인자들 틈에 끼인 처지이니, 그 아이를 생각하지 않을 수 없구려.\""]], [["“‘“General,”", "\"'\"장군,\""], ["said the chief of the assembly, “one man may insult fifty—it is the privilege of weakness.", "모임의 우두머리가 말했다. \"한 사람이 쉰 사람을 모욕할 수도 있소. 그것은 약자의 특권이니까."], ["But he does wrong to use his privilege.", "그러나 그 특권을 함부로 쓰는 것은 잘못이오."], ["Follow my advice, swear, and do not insult.”", "내 충고를 따라 맹세하고, 모욕하지 마시오.\""], ["The general, again daunted by the superiority of the chief, hesitated a moment; then advancing to the president’s desk,—“What is the form, said he.", "장군은 우두머리의 위세에 다시 기가 꺾여 잠시 망설였다. 그러다 의장의 책상 앞으로 나아가 말했다. \"그 문구가 무엇이오?\""]], [["“‘“It is this:—‘I swear by my honor not to reveal to anyone what I have seen and heard on the 5th of February, 1815, between nine and ten o’clock in the evening; and I plead guilty of death should I ever violate this oath.’”", "\"'\"이러하오. '나는 1815년 2월 5일 저녁 아홉 시에서 열 시 사이에 보고 들은 것을 누구에게도 결코 발설하지 않을 것을 명예를 걸고 맹세한다. 만일 이 맹세를 어긴다면 죽어 마땅함을 인정한다.'\""], ["The general appeared to be affected by a nervous tremor, which prevented his answering for some moments; then, overcoming his manifest repugnance, he pronounced the required oath, but in so low a tone as to be scarcely audible to the majority of the members, who insisted on his repeating it clearly and distinctly, which he did.", "장군은 신경의 떨림에 사로잡힌 듯, 한동안 대답하지 못했다. 그러다 역력한 거부감을 누르고 요구받은 맹세를 했으나, 그 목소리가 너무 낮아 대다수 회원에게는 거의 들리지 않았다. 회원들이 또렷하고 분명하게 다시 말하라고 다그치자, 그가 그렇게 했다."]], [["“‘“Now am I at liberty to retire?”", "\"'\"이제 물러가도 되겠소?\""], ["said the general.", "장군이 말했다."], ["The president rose, appointed three members to accompany him, and got into the carriage with the general after bandaging his eyes.", "의장이 일어나 세 명의 회원을 지명해 그를 따르게 하고는, 장군의 눈을 다시 가린 뒤 함께 마차에 올랐다."], ["One of those three members was the coachman who had driven them there.", "그 세 사람 가운데 하나는 그들을 그곳까지 몰고 온 마부였다."], ["The other members silently dispersed.", "나머지 회원들은 말없이 흩어졌다."], ["“Where do you wish to be taken?”", "\"어디로 모셔다 드릴까요?\""], ["asked the president.—“Anywhere out of your presence,” replied M.", "의장이 물었다. \"당신 눈앞에서 벗어날 수만 있다면 어디든 좋소.\""], ["d’Épinay. “Beware, sir,”", "데피네 씨가 답했다."], ["replied the president, “you are no longer in the assembly, and have only to do with individuals; do not insult them unless you wish to be held responsible.”", "\"조심하시오, 선생.\" 의장이 답했다. \"당신은 이제 모임에 있는 게 아니라 한 개인을 상대하는 것이오."], ["But instead of listening, M.", "책임지고 싶지 않거든 그를 모욕하지 마시오.\""], ["d’Épinay went on,—“You are still as brave in your carriage as in your assembly because you are still four against one.”", "그러나 데피네 씨는 듣지 않고 말을 이었다. \"당신은 모임에서나 마차에서나 여전히 용감하구려. 아직도 넷이서 하나를 상대하니까.\""], ["The president stopped the coach.", "의장이 마차를 세웠다."], ["They were at that part of the Quai des Ormes where the steps lead down to the river.", "그들은 강으로 내려가는 층계가 있는 오름 강둑의 어느 지점에 있었다."], ["“Why do you stop here?”", "\"왜 여기서 멈추는 거요?\""], ["asked d’Épinay.", "데피네가 물었다."]], [["“‘“Because, sir,”", "\"'\"선생, 당신이 한 사람을 모욕했고,\""], ["said the president, “you have insulted a man, and that man will not go one step farther without demanding honorable reparation.”", "의장이 말했다. \"그 사람은 명예로운 보상을 요구하기 전에는 한 걸음도 더 나아가지 않을 것이기 때문이오.\""]], [["“‘“Another method of assassination?”", "\"'\"또 다른 암살 방식이오?\""], ["said the general, shrugging his shoulders.", "장군이 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다."]], [["“‘“Make no noise, sir, unless you wish me to consider you as one of the men of whom you spoke just now as cowards, who take their weakness for a shield.", "\"'\"소란 피우지 마시오, 선생. 방금 당신이 비겁자라 일컬은 자들, 제 약함을 방패 삼는 그런 자들과 똑같이 여겨지고 싶지 않다면 말이오."], ["You are alone, one alone shall answer you; you have a sword by your side, I have one in my cane; you have no witness, one of these gentlemen will serve you.", "당신은 혼자고, 혼자인 한 사람이 당신을 상대할 거요. 당신은 옆구리에 검을 찼고, 나는 지팡이 속에 한 자루가 있소. 당신에게는 입회인이 없으니, 이분들 중 하나가 그 역할을 맡을 거요."], ["Now, if you please, remove your bandage.”", "자, 괜찮다면 가리개를 벗으시오.\""], ["The general tore the handkerchief from his eyes.", "장군이 눈에서 가리개를 잡아 뜯었다. \"마침내,\""], ["“At last,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “I shall know with whom I have to do.”", "\"내가 누구를 상대하는지 알게 되겠군.\""], ["They opened the door and the four men alighted.’”", "문이 열리고 네 사람이 내렸다.'\""]], [["Franz again interrupted himself, and wiped the cold drops from his brow; there was something awful in hearing the son read aloud in trembling pallor these details of his father’s death, which had hitherto been a mystery.", "프란츠가 다시 읽기를 멈추고 이마의 식은땀을 닦았다. 그때껏 수수께끼였던 아버지의 죽음에 관한 이 세세한 이야기를, 아들이 떨리는 창백한 얼굴로 소리 내어 읽는 것을 듣는 데에는 무언가 끔찍한 데가 있었다."], ["Valentine clasped her hands as if in prayer.", "발랑틴은 기도하듯 두 손을 맞잡았다."], ["Noirtier looked at Villefort with an almost sublime expression of contempt and pride.", "누아르티에는 거의 숭고하다 할 경멸과 자부심의 표정으로 빌포르를 바라보았다."]], [["Franz continued:", "프란츠가 이어 읽었다."]], [["“‘It was, as we said, the fifth of February.", "\"'앞서 말했듯이 때는 2월 5일이었다."], ["For three days the mercury had been five or six degrees below freezing and the steps were covered with ice.", "사흘째 수은주가 영하 오륙 도를 가리켰고, 층계는 얼음으로 덮여 있었다."], ["The general was stout and tall, the president offered him the side of the railing to assist him in getting down.", "장군은 몸집이 크고 키가 컸으므로, 의장은 그가 내려가는 것을 돕고자 난간 쪽을 양보했다."], ["The two witnesses followed.", "두 입회인이 뒤따랐다."], ["It was a dark night.", "어두운 밤이었다."], ["The ground from the steps to the river was covered with snow and hoarfrost, the water of the river looked black and deep.", "층계에서 강에 이르는 땅바닥은 눈과 서리로 덮여 있었고, 강물은 검고 깊어 보였다."], ["One of the seconds went for a lantern in a coal-barge near, and by its light they examined the weapons.", "입회인 하나가 가까운 석탄 거룻배로 가서 등롱을 가져왔고, 그 불빛으로 무기를 살폈다."], ["The president’s sword, which was simply, as he had said, one he carried in his cane, was five inches shorter than the general’s, and had no guard.", "의장의 검은 그가 말한 대로 그저 지팡이 속에 지니고 다니던 것으로, 장군의 검보다 다섯 인치 짧고 손잡이 보호대도 없었다."], ["The general proposed to cast lots for the swords, but the president said it was he who had given the provocation, and when he had given it he had supposed each would use his own arms.", "장군은 검을 제비뽑기로 정하자고 했으나, 의장은 도발한 사람이 자기이며, 도발할 때 저마다 제 무기를 쓰리라 여겼다고 말했다."], ["The witnesses endeavored to insist, but the president bade them be silent.", "입회인들이 우겨 보려 했지만, 의장은 그들에게 잠자코 있으라 했다."], ["The lantern was placed on the ground, the two adversaries took their stations, and the duel began.", "등롱이 땅에 놓이고 두 적수가 자리를 잡자, 결투가 시작되었다."], ["The light made the two swords appear like flashes of lightning; as for the men, they were scarcely perceptible, the darkness was so great. 40044m", "불빛 속에서 두 자루의 검은 번갯불처럼 번뜩였다. 사람의 모습은 어둠이 너무 짙어 거의 분간할 수 없었다."]], [["“‘General d’Épinay passed for one of the best swordsmen in the army, but he was pressed so closely in the onset that he missed his aim and fell.", "\"'데피네 장군은 군에서 손꼽히는 검객으로 통했으나, 첫 공세에서 너무 거세게 몰려 겨냥을 놓치고 쓰러졌다."], ["The witnesses thought he was dead, but his adversary, who knew he had not struck him, offered him the assistance of his hand to rise.", "입회인들은 그가 죽은 줄 알았으나, 자신이 그를 찌르지 않았음을 아는 적수는 그가 일어서도록 손을 내밀어 주었다."], ["The circumstance irritated instead of calming the general, and he rushed on his adversary.", "그 처사는 장군을 진정시키기는커녕 오히려 화나게 했고, 그는 적수에게 달려들었다."], ["But his opponent did not allow his guard to be broken.", "그러나 상대는 자신의 방어를 뚫리도록 두지 않았다."], ["He received him on his sword and three times the general drew back on finding himself too closely engaged, and then returned to the charge.", "그는 장군을 검으로 받아 냈고, 장군은 너무 바싹 붙은 것을 깨닫고 세 번이나 물러섰다가 다시 덤벼들었다."], ["At the third he fell again.", "세 번째에 그가 또 쓰러졌다."], ["They thought he slipped, as at first, and the witnesses, seeing he did not move, approached and endeavored to raise him, but the one who passed his arm around the body found it was moistened with blood.", "사람들은 처음처럼 그가 미끄러진 줄 알았으나, 그가 움직이지 않는 것을 보고 입회인들이 다가가 일으키려 했다. 그런데 그 몸에 팔을 두른 자가 거기 피가 흥건한 것을 알아챘다."], ["The general, who had almost fainted, revived. “Ah,”", "거의 정신을 잃었던 장군이 깨어났다. \"아,\""], ["said he, “they have sent some fencing-master to fight with me.”", "그가 말했다. \"검술 사범을 보내 나와 싸우게 했군.\""], ["The president, without answering, approached the witness who held the lantern, and raising his sleeve, showed him two wounds he had received in his arm; then opening his coat, and unbuttoning his waistcoat, displayed his side, pierced with a third wound.", "의장은 대꾸 없이 등롱을 든 입회인에게 다가가, 소매를 걷어 팔에 입은 두 군데 상처를 보였다. 이어 외투를 열고 조끼 단추를 풀어, 세 번째 상처로 꿰뚫린 옆구리를 드러냈다."], ["Still he had not even uttered a sigh.", "그러면서도 그는 신음 한 번 내지 않았다."], ["General d’Épinay died five minutes after.’”", "데피네 장군은 오 분 뒤에 숨을 거두었다.'\""]], [["Franz read these last words in a voice so choked that they were hardly audible, and then stopped, passing his hand over his eyes as if to dispel a cloud; but after a moment’s silence, he continued:", "프란츠는 이 마지막 구절을 너무 목멘 소리로 읽어 거의 알아들을 수 없을 지경이었다. 그러고는 멈추어, 구름이라도 걷어 내려는 듯 손으로 눈을 훔쳤다. 그러나 잠시 침묵한 뒤 다시 읽어 갔다."]], [["“‘The president went up the steps, after pushing his sword into his cane; a track of blood on the snow marked his course.", "\"'의장은 검을 지팡이 속에 도로 밀어 넣고 층계를 올라갔다. 눈 위에 남은 핏자국이 그의 자취를 새겼다."], ["He had scarcely arrived at the top when he heard a heavy splash in the water—it was the general’s body, which the witnesses had just thrown into the river after ascertaining that he was dead.", "그가 꼭대기에 다다르자마자, 물에서 무언가 무겁게 첨벙 빠지는 소리가 들렸다. 그것은 장군의 시신으로, 입회인들이 그가 죽었음을 확인한 뒤 막 강에 던져 넣은 것이었다."], ["The general fell, then, in a loyal duel, and not in ambush as it might have been reported.", "그러므로 장군은 보도될 수도 있었던 매복이 아니라, 정정당당한 결투에서 쓰러진 것이다."], ["In proof of this we have signed this paper to establish the truth of the facts, lest the moment should arrive when either of the actors in this terrible scene should be accused of premeditated murder or of infringement of the laws of honor.", "이를 증명하기 위해, 이 끔찍한 장면의 어느 당사자든 언젠가 계획적 살인이나 명예의 법도를 어겼다는 혐의를 받게 될 경우에 대비하여, 우리는 사실의 진상을 밝히고자 이 문서에 서명한다."]], [["“‘Signed, Beaurepaire, Duchampy, and Lecharpal.’”", "\"'서명, 보르파르, 뒤샹피, 르샤르팔.'\""]], [["When Franz had finished reading this account, so dreadful for a son; when Valentine, pale with emotion, had wiped away a tear; when Villefort, trembling, and crouched in a corner, had endeavored to lessen the storm by supplicating glances at the implacable old man,—", "아들에게는 그토록 끔찍한 이 기록을 프란츠가 다 읽었을 때, 감정에 북받쳐 창백해진 발랑틴이 눈물을 훔쳤을 때, 떨면서 한구석에 웅크린 빌포르가 무자비한 노인에게 애원하는 눈길을 보내 그 폭풍을 누그러뜨리려 애썼을 때―"]], [["“Sir,”", "\"어르신,\" 데피네가 누아르티에에게 말했다."], ["said d’Épinay to Noirtier, “since you are well acquainted with all these details, which are attested by honorable signatures,—since you appear to take some interest in me, although you have only manifested it hitherto by causing me sorrow, refuse me not one final satisfaction—tell me the name of the president of the club, that I may at least know who killed my father.”", "\"어르신께서는 명예로운 서명으로 입증된 이 모든 세세한 정황을 잘 아시고, 비록 지금껏 제게 슬픔을 안기는 것으로만 드러내셨을지언정 저에게 어떤 관심을 두고 계신 듯하니, 제게 마지막 한 가지 만족만은 거절하지 말아 주십시오. 그 클럽 의장의 이름을 알려 주십시오. 적어도 누가 제 아버지를 죽였는지는 알 수 있도록 말입니다.\""]], [["Villefort mechanically felt for the handle of the door; Valentine, who understood sooner than anyone her grandfather’s answer, and who had often seen two scars upon his right arm, drew back a few steps.", "빌포르는 무심결에 문손잡이를 더듬었다. 누구보다 먼저 할아버지의 대답을 알아챈 발랑틴은, 그의 오른팔에 난 두 개의 흉터를 여러 번 보아 온 터라, 몇 걸음 뒤로 물러섰다."]], [["“Mademoiselle,”", "\"아가씨,\""], ["said Franz, turning towards Valentine, “unite your efforts with mine to find out the name of the man who made me an orphan at two years of age.”", "프란츠가 발랑틴을 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"제가 두 살에 고아가 되게 만든 그 사람의 이름을 밝히는 데 부디 힘을 보태 주십시오.\""], ["Valentine remained dumb and motionless.", "발랑틴은 말없이 꼼짝도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Hold, sir,”", "\"그만하시오, 선생.\""], ["said Villefort, “do not prolong this dreadful scene.", "빌포르가 말했다. \"이 끔찍한 장면을 더 끌지 마시오."], ["The names have been purposely concealed; my father himself does not know who this president was, and if he knows, he cannot tell you; proper names are not in the dictionary.”", "이름은 일부러 감춰진 것이오. 내 아버지조차 그 의장이 누구였는지 모르고, 설령 안다 해도 당신에게 말해 줄 수 없소. 고유명사는 사전에 없으니까.\""]], [["“Oh, misery,”", "\"오, 비참한 일이군요.\""], ["cried Franz: “the only hope which sustained me and enabled me to read to the end was that of knowing, at least, the name of him who killed my father!", "프란츠가 외쳤다. \"제가 끝까지 읽어 낼 수 있도록 저를 지탱해 준 단 하나의 희망은, 적어도 제 아버지를 죽인 자의 이름을 아는 것이었습니다!"], ["Sir, sir,”", "어르신, 어르신,\""], ["cried he, turning to Noirtier, “do what you can—make me understand in some way!”", "그가 누아르티에를 향해 돌아서며 외쳤다. \"하실 수 있는 대로 해 주십시오. 어떻게든 제가 알아듣게 해 주십시오!\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["replied Noirtier.", "누아르티에가 답했다."]], [["“Oh, mademoiselle, mademoiselle!”", "\"오, 아가씨, 아가씨!\""], ["cried Franz, “your grandfather says he can indicate the person.", "프란츠가 외쳤다. \"할아버님께서 그 사람을 가리킬 수 있다고 하십니다."], ["Help me,—lend me your assistance!”", "저를 도와주십시오. 부디 도와주십시오!\""]], [["Noirtier looked at the dictionary.", "누아르티에가 사전을 바라보았다."], ["Franz took it with a nervous trembling, and repeated the letters of the alphabet successively, until he came to M.", "프란츠는 신경질적으로 떨며 그것을 집어 들고, 알파벳 글자를 차례로 짚어 가다가 M에 이르렀다."], ["At that letter the old man signified “Yes.”", "그 글자에서 노인이 \"그래\"라는 뜻을 표했다."]], [["“M,” repeated Franz.", "\"M.\" 프란츠가 되뇌었다."], ["The young man’s finger, glided over the words, but at each one Noirtier answered by a negative sign.", "젊은이의 손가락이 단어들 위로 미끄러졌으나, 단어마다 누아르티에는 아니라는 신호로 답했다."], ["Valentine hid her head between her hands.", "발랑틴은 두 손에 얼굴을 묻었다."], ["At length, Franz arrived at the word MYSELF.", "마침내 프란츠가 '나 자신'이라는 단어에 이르렀다."]], [["40046m", "\"그래!\""]], [["“Yes!” “You!”", "\"당신이!\" 프란츠가 외쳤다."], ["cried Franz, whose hair stood on end; “you, M. Noirtier—you killed my father?”", "그의 머리카락이 곤두섰다. \"당신, 누아르티에 어르신이―당신이 제 아버지를 죽였습니까?\""]], [["“Yes!” replied Noirtier, fixing a majestic look on the young man.", "\"그래!\" 누아르티에가 젊은이에게 위엄 어린 시선을 고정한 채 답했다."], ["Franz fell powerless on a chair; Villefort opened the door and escaped, for the idea had entered his mind to stifle the little remaining life in the heart of this terrible old man.", "프란츠는 맥없이 의자에 쓰러졌다. 빌포르는 문을 열고 달아났다. 이 무서운 노인의 가슴에 남은 얼마 안 되는 목숨을 짓눌러 꺼 버리고 싶다는 생각이 머릿속에 떠올랐던 것이다."]], [["# Chapter 76. Progress of Cavalcanti the Younger", "# Chapter 76. Progress of Cavalcanti the Younger"]], [["Meanwhile M. Cavalcanti the elder had returned to his service, not in the army of his majesty the Emperor of Austria, but at the gaming-table of the baths of Lucca, of which he was one of the most assiduous courtiers.", "그동안 카발칸티 노인은 다시 본업으로 돌아가 있었다. 그가 봉직하는 곳은 오스트리아 황제 폐하의 군대가 아니라, 루카 온천장의 도박판이었다."], ["He had spent every farthing that had been allowed for his journey as a reward for the majestic and solemn manner in which he had maintained his assumed character of father.", "그는 그곳을 가장 부지런히 드나드는 단골 중 하나였다. 아버지라는 가짜 역할을 그토록 위엄 있고 근엄하게 해낸 보상으로 여행 경비로 받은 돈을 한 푼도 남김없이 거기서 탕진해 버렸다."]], [["M. Andrea at his departure inherited all the papers which proved that he had indeed the honor of being the son of the Marquis Bartolomeo and the Marchioness Oliva Corsinari.", "안드레아 군은 그가 떠날 때, 자신이 바르톨로메오 후작과 올리바 코르시나리 후작 부인의 아들이라는 영광을 누리고 있음을 증명하는 모든 서류를 물려받았다."], ["He was now fairly launched in that Parisian society which gives such ready access to foreigners, and treats them, not as they really are, but as they wish to be considered.", "그는 이제 파리 사교계에 제법 그럴듯하게 발을 들였다. 파리 사교계는 외국인을 손쉽게 받아들이고, 그들을 실제 모습 그대로가 아니라 그들이 보이고 싶어 하는 대로 대접해 주는 곳이었다."], ["Besides, what is required of a young man in Paris?", "게다가 파리에서 젊은이에게 요구되는 것이 무엇이겠는가?"], ["To speak its language tolerably, to make a good appearance, to be a good gamester, and to pay in cash.", "프랑스어를 그런대로 구사하고, 외모가 번듯하며, 노름을 잘하고, 현금으로 셈을 치르는 것뿐이었다."], ["They are certainly less particular with a foreigner than with a Frenchman.", "외국인에게는 프랑스인에게보다 분명 덜 까다로운 법이었다."], ["Andrea had, then, in a fortnight, attained a very fair position.", "그리하여 안드레아는 보름 만에 꽤 그럴듯한 지위를 얻었다."], ["He was called count, he was said to possess 50,000 livres per annum; and his father’s immense riches, buried in the quarries of Saravezza, were a constant theme.", "사람들은 그를 백작이라 불렀고, 한 해에 5만 리브르를 거둬들인다고 수군거렸으며, 사라베차 채석장에 묻혀 있다는 그 아버지의 막대한 재산이 늘 화젯거리가 되었다."], ["A learned man, before whom the last circumstance was mentioned as a fact, declared he had seen the quarries in question, which gave great weight to assertions hitherto somewhat doubtful, but which now assumed the garb of reality.", "한 학식 있는 사람은 이 마지막 사실이 정말인 양 언급되는 자리에서, 자신이 바로 그 채석장을 직접 보았노라고 단언했다. 그러자 그때까지 다소 미심쩍던 이야기들이 단번에 무게를 얻으며 사실의 옷을 걸치게 되었다."]], [["Such was the state of society in Paris at the period we bring before our readers, when Monte Cristo went one evening to pay M. Danglars a visit. M.", "지금 우리가 독자들 앞에 펼쳐 보이는 시기의 파리 사교계 형편이 그러했다. 그러던 어느 날 저녁, 몬테크리스토가 당글라르 씨를 찾아갔다."], ["Danglars was out, but the count was asked to go and see the baroness, and he accepted the invitation.", "당글라르 씨는 외출 중이었으나, 백작은 남작 부인을 뵙고 가시라는 청을 받았고, 그 청을 받아들였다."], ["It was never without a nervous shudder, since the dinner at Auteuil, and the events which followed it, that Madame Danglars heard Monte Cristo’s name announced.", "오퇴유 만찬과 그 뒤에 이어진 일들이 있은 뒤로, 당글라르 부인은 몬테크리스토라는 이름이 알려질 때마다 신경질적인 전율을 느끼지 않을 수 없었다."], ["If he did not come, the painful sensation became most intense; if, on the contrary, he appeared, his noble countenance, his brilliant eyes, his amiability, his polite attention even towards Madame Danglars, soon dispelled every impression of fear.", "그가 오지 않으면 그 괴로운 감정이 극도로 심해졌다. 반대로 그가 나타나면, 그 고결한 용모와 빛나는 눈, 상냥함, 당글라르 부인을 향한 정중한 배려가 이내 두려움의 인상을 말끔히 흩어 버렸다. 그토록 더없이 유쾌하고 기분 좋은 태도를 지닌 사람이 자신을 해칠 흉계를 품고 있으리라고는 남작 부인으로서는 도무지 믿기지 않았다."], ["It appeared impossible to the baroness that a man of such delightfully pleasing manners should entertain evil designs against her; besides, the most corrupt minds only suspect evil when it would answer some interested end—useless injury is repugnant to every mind. 40048m", "게다가 아무리 타락한 마음이라 해도, 무언가 이득이 걸려 있을 때에만 악을 의심하는 법이었다. 아무 이득도 없는 해코지란 어떤 마음에게도 거슬리는 것이었다."]], [["When Monte Cristo entered the boudoir, to which we have already once introduced our readers, and where the baroness was examining some drawings, which her daughter passed to her after having looked at them with M.", "몬테크리스토가 규방으로 들어섰을 때 — 우리는 이미 한 번 독자들을 이곳으로 안내한 바 있다 — 남작 부인은 그림 몇 점을 들여다보고 있었다. 그 그림들은 딸이 카발칸티 군과 함께 본 다음 어머니에게 건넨 것이었다."], ["Cavalcanti, his presence soon produced its usual effect, and it was with smiles that the baroness received the count, although she had been a little disconcerted at the announcement of his name.", "백작이 나타나자 이내 늘 그렇던 효과가 나타났다. 남작 부인은 그의 이름이 알려졌을 때 조금 당황했으면서도, 미소를 지으며 백작을 맞이했다."], ["The latter took in the whole scene at a glance.", "백작은 한눈에 그 자리의 광경을 전부 알아차렸다."]], [["The baroness was partially reclining on a sofa, Eugénie sat near her, and Cavalcanti was standing.", "남작 부인은 소파에 반쯤 기대 누워 있었고, 외제니가 그 곁에 앉아 있었으며, 카발칸티는 서 있었다."], ["Cavalcanti, dressed in black, like one of Goethe’s heroes, with varnished shoes and white silk open-worked stockings, passed a white and tolerably nice-looking hand through his light hair, and so displayed a sparkling diamond, that in spite of Monte Cristo’s advice the vain young man had been unable to resist putting on his little finger.", "카발칸티는 괴테의 주인공들처럼 검은 옷차림에, 칠피 구두를 신고, 흰 비단 망사 양말을 신고 있었다. 그는 희고 제법 보기 좋은 손으로 옅은 빛깔의 머리카락을 쓸어 넘겼는데, 그러면서 반짝이는 다이아몬드를 드러내 보였다. 몬테크리스토의 충고에도 불구하고 그 허영심 많은 젊은이는 새끼손가락에 그 반지를 끼고 싶은 유혹을 끝내 이기지 못했던 것이다."], ["This movement was accompanied by killing glances at Mademoiselle Danglars, and by sighs launched in the same direction.", "이 동작에는 당글라르 양을 향한 애를 태우는 듯한 눈길과, 같은 방향으로 내쉬는 한숨이 곁들여졌다."]], [["Mademoiselle Danglars was still the same—cold, beautiful, and satirical.", "당글라르 양은 여전히 그대로였다. 차갑고, 아름답고, 빈정대는 듯했다."], ["Not one of these glances, nor one sigh, was lost on her; they might have been said to fall on the shield of Minerva, which some philosophers assert protected sometimes the breast of Sappho.", "그 눈길도, 그 한숨도 그녀에게는 단 하나도 통하지 않았다. 마치 미네르바의 방패에 부딪혀 떨어지는 것 같았다."], ["Eugénie bowed coldly to the count, and availed herself of the first moment when the conversation became earnest to escape to her study, whence very soon two cheerful and noisy voices being heard in connection with occasional notes of the piano assured Monte Cristo that Mademoiselle Danglars preferred to his society and to that of M.", "어떤 철학자들은 그 방패가 때로 사포의 가슴을 지켜 주었다고 주장하기도 한다. 외제니는 백작에게 차갑게 인사하고는, 대화가 진지해지는 첫 순간을 틈타 자신의 작업실로 빠져나갔다. 곧 그곳에서 명랑하고 떠들썩한 두 목소리가 이따금 피아노 가락과 어우러져 들려왔다."], ["Cavalcanti the company of Mademoiselle Louise d’Armilly, her singing teacher.", "당글라르 양이 백작과 카발칸티 군의 자리보다 노래 선생인 루이즈 다르밀리 양과 함께 있는 편을 더 좋아한다는 것을 몬테크리스토는 분명히 알 수 있었다."]], [["It was then, especially while conversing with Madame Danglars, and apparently absorbed by the charm of the conversation, that the count noticed M.", "그러는 동안, 특히 당글라르 부인과 대화를 나누며 겉으로는 대화의 매력에 푹 빠진 듯하면서도, 백작은 안드레아 카발칸티 군의 안절부절못하는 모습을 눈여겨보았다."], ["Andrea Cavalcanti’s solicitude, his manner of listening to the music at the door he dared not pass, and of manifesting his admiration.", "그가 감히 넘어서지 못하는 문 앞에서 음악에 귀 기울이는 모양과, 그 감탄을 드러내는 태도를 말이다."]], [["The banker soon returned.", "이윽고 은행가가 돌아왔다."], ["His first look was certainly directed towards Monte Cristo, but the second was for Andrea.", "그의 첫 시선은 분명 몬테크리스토를 향했으나, 두 번째 시선은 안드레아에게 갔다."], ["As for his wife, he bowed to her, as some husbands do to their wives, but in a way that bachelors will never comprehend, until a very extensive code is published on conjugal life.", "아내에게는 어떤 남편들이 아내에게 하듯 인사를 건넸는데, 그 방식은 부부 생활에 관한 아주 방대한 법전이 출간되기 전까지는 독신자들로서는 결코 이해하지 못할 그런 것이었다."]], [["“Have not the ladies invited you to join them at the piano?”", "\"부인들께서 자네에게 피아노 곁으로 와 함께하자고 청하지 않던가?\""], ["said Danglars to Andrea.", "당글라르가 안드레아에게 말했다."]], [["“Alas, no, sir,” replied Andrea with a sigh, still more remarkable than the former ones.", "\"안타깝게도, 그렇지 않았습니다.\" 안드레아가 앞서보다 한층 더 두드러진 한숨과 함께 대답했다."], ["Danglars immediately advanced towards the door and opened it.", "당글라르는 곧장 문 쪽으로 다가가 문을 열었다."]], [["40050m\nThe two young ladies were seen seated on the same chair, at the piano, accompanying themselves, each with one hand, a fancy to which they had accustomed themselves, and performed admirably.", "두 젊은 아가씨가 피아노 앞 같은 의자에 나란히 앉아 있는 것이 보였다. 두 사람은 각자 한 손씩으로 스스로 반주를 맞추고 있었는데, 그것은 그들이 익혀 온 취미였고 솜씨도 훌륭했다."], ["Mademoiselle d’Armilly, whom they then perceived through the open doorway, formed with Eugénie one of the _tableaux vivants_ of which the Germans are so fond.", "그때 열린 문틈으로 보이는 다르밀리 양은 외제니와 더불어 독일인들이 그토록 좋아하는 활인화 한 폭을 이루고 있었다."], ["She was somewhat beautiful, and exquisitely formed—a little fairy-like figure, with large curls falling on her neck, which was rather too long, as Perugino sometimes makes his Virgins, and her eyes dull from fatigue.", "그녀는 제법 아름답고 더없이 우아한 몸매를 지닌, 작고 요정 같은 모습이었다. 굵은 곱슬머리가 목덜미로 흘러내렸는데, 그 목이 다소 길어, 페루지노가 이따금 성모를 그릴 때처럼 보였고, 두 눈은 피로로 흐릿했다."], ["She was said to have a weak chest, and like Antonia in the _Cremona Violin_, she would die one day while singing.", "가슴이 약하다는 소문이 있었고, 〈크레모나의 바이올린〉에 나오는 안토니아처럼 언젠가 노래를 부르다 죽으리라는 말도 돌았다."]], [["Monte Cristo cast one rapid and curious glance round this sanctum; it was the first time he had ever seen Mademoiselle d’Armilly, of whom he had heard much.", "몬테크리스토는 이 작은 성역을 한차례 빠르고도 호기심 어린 눈길로 둘러보았다. 그가 다르밀리 양을 직접 본 것은 이번이 처음이었다. 그녀에 관한 이야기는 익히 들어 왔던 터였다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래서,\""], ["said the banker to his daughter, “are we then all to be excluded?”", "은행가가 딸에게 말했다. \"그럼 우리 모두는 빼놓겠다는 게냐?\""]], [["He then led the young man into the study, and either by chance or manœuvre the door was partially closed after Andrea, so that from the place where they sat neither the Count nor the baroness could see anything; but as the banker had accompanied Andrea, Madame Danglars appeared to take no notice of it.", "그러고는 젊은이를 작업실로 데리고 들어갔다. 우연이었는지 술수였는지, 안드레아가 들어간 뒤 문이 반쯤 닫혀, 두 사람이 앉은 자리에서는 백작도 남작 부인도 안을 들여다볼 수 없게 되었다. 그러나 은행가가 안드레아를 따라 들어갔으므로, 당글라르 부인은 그 일을 모르는 체하는 듯했다."]], [["The count soon heard Andrea’s voice, singing a Corsican song, accompanied by the piano.", "이윽고 백작은 피아노 반주에 맞춰 코르시카 노래를 부르는 안드레아의 목소리를 들었다. 백작은 그 노래를 들으며 미소를 지었다."], ["While the count smiled at hearing this song, which made him lose sight of Andrea in the recollection of Benedetto, Madame Danglars was boasting to Monte Cristo of her husband’s strength of mind, who that very morning had lost three or four hundred thousand francs by a failure at Milan.", "그 노래 때문에 안드레아의 모습이 베네데토에 대한 기억 속으로 사라져 버렸기 때문이다. 그동안 당글라르 부인은 몬테크리스토에게 남편의 굳센 마음을 자랑하고 있었다. 바로 그날 아침에도 그는 밀라노에서 일어난 파산으로 삼사십만 프랑을 잃었다는 것이었다."], ["The praise was well deserved, for had not the count heard it from the baroness, or by one of those means by which he knew everything, the baron’s countenance would not have led him to suspect it.", "그 칭찬은 마땅한 것이었다. 백작이 남작 부인에게서, 혹은 그가 무엇이든 알아내는 그 여러 방편 가운데 하나로 듣지 않았더라면, 남작의 표정만 보고서는 그런 일을 짐작조차 하지 못했을 테니 말이다."]], [["“Hem,”", "'흠.'"], ["thought Monte Cristo, “he begins to conceal his losses; a month since he boasted of them.”", "몬테크리스토는 생각했다. '이제 손실을 감추기 시작하는군. 한 달 전만 해도 그걸 자랑하더니.'"]], [["Then aloud,—“Oh, madame, M.", "그러고는 소리 내어 말했다."], ["Danglars is so skilful, he will soon regain at the Bourse what he loses elsewhere.”", "\"오, 부인, 당글라르 씨는 워낙 수완이 좋으신 분이니, 다른 데서 잃은 것쯤은 곧 증권 거래소에서 되찾으실 겁니다.\""]], [["“I see that you participate in a prevalent error,”", "\"백작님께서도 흔한 오해에 빠져 계시는군요.\""], ["said Madame Danglars.", "당글라르 부인이 말했다."]], [["“What is it?” said Monte Cristo.", "\"무슨 오해 말씀입니까?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다."]], [["“That M. Danglars speculates, whereas he never does.”", "\"당글라르 씨가 투기를 한다는 오해 말이에요. 그이는 절대 투기를 하지 않는답니다.\""]], [["“Truly, madame, I recollect M. Debray told me——apropos, what has become of him?", "\"정말이지, 부인, 드브레 씨가 제게 말하기를…… 아 참, 그분은 요즘 어찌 지내십니까?"], ["I have seen nothing of him the last three or four days.”", "사나흘 동안 통 뵙지를 못했네요.\""]], [["“Nor I,”", "\"저도 그래요.\""], ["said Madame Danglars; “but you began a sentence, sir, and did not finish.”", "당글라르 부인이 말했다. \"그런데 무슨 말씀을 시작하시고는 끝맺지 않으셨어요.\" \"무슨 말 말입니까?\""]], [["“Which?” “M. Debray had told you——”", "\"드브레 씨가 백작님께 말씀하셨다는……\""]], [["“Ah, yes; he told me it was you who sacrificed to the demon of speculation.”", "\"아, 그렇지요. 투기의 악마에게 몸을 바치는 분이 바로 부인이라고 하더군요.\""]], [["“I was once very fond of it, but I do not indulge now.”", "\"한때는 무척 좋아했지요. 하지만 이제는 손을 대지 않아요.\""]], [["“Then you are wrong, madame.", "\"그렇다면 잘못하시는 겁니다, 부인."], ["Fortune is precarious; and if I were a woman and fate had made me a banker’s wife, whatever might be my confidence in my husband’s good fortune, still in speculation you know there is great risk.", "재산이란 위태로운 것이지요. 만일 제가 여자이고 운명이 저를 은행가의 아내로 만들었다면, 남편의 좋은 운수를 아무리 믿는다 해도, 아시다시피 투기에는 큰 위험이 따르는 법이니까요."], ["Well, I would secure for myself a fortune independent of him, even if I acquired it by placing my interests in hands unknown to him.”", "그러니 저라면 남편과는 별개로 제 몫의 재산을 마련해 둘 겁니다. 설령 남편이 모르는 손에 제 이익을 맡겨서 얻은 것이라 할지라도 말이지요.\""], ["Madame Danglars blushed, in spite of all her efforts.", "당글라르 부인은 아무리 애를 써도 얼굴이 붉어지는 것을 어쩔 수 없었다."]], [["“Stay,”", "\"참,\""], ["said Monte Cristo, as though he had not observed her confusion, “I have heard of a lucky hit that was made yesterday on the Neapolitan bonds.”", "몬테크리스토가 마치 그녀의 당혹스러움을 알아채지 못한 듯 말했다. \"어제 나폴리 채권에서 한몫 톡톡히 챙긴 일이 있었다고 들었습니다.\""]], [["“I have none—nor have I ever possessed any; but really we have talked long enough of money, count, we are like two stockbrokers; have you heard how fate is persecuting the poor Villeforts?”", "\"저는 그런 채권을 가지고 있지 않아요. 가져 본 적도 없고요. 한데 백작님, 우리가 돈 이야기를 너무 오래 했네요. 꼭 주식 중개인 둘이 마주 앉은 꼴이에요. 그건 그렇고, 운명이 가엾은 빌포르 집안을 어떻게 괴롭히고 있는지 들으셨어요?\""]], [["“What has happened?”", "\"무슨 일이 있었습니까?\""], ["said the count, simulating total ignorance.", "백작이 아무것도 모르는 척하며 물었다."]], [["“You know the Marquis of Saint-Méran died a few days after he had set out on his journey to Paris, and the marchioness a few days after her arrival?”", "\"생메랑 후작이 파리로 가는 여행길에 오른 지 며칠 만에 돌아가셨고, 후작 부인은 도착하신 지 며칠 만에 돌아가셨다는 거 아세요?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["said Monte Cristo, “I have heard that; but, as Claudius said to Hamlet, ‘it is a law of nature; their fathers died before them, and they mourned their loss; they will die before their children, who will, in their turn, grieve for them.’”", "\"그 얘기는 들었습니다. 하지만 클라우디우스가 햄릿에게 말했듯이, '그것은 자연의 법칙이지요. 그들의 아버지가 그들보다 먼저 죽었고 그들이 그 죽음을 애도했듯, 그들도 자식들보다 먼저 죽을 것이고, 자식들이 또 그들을 슬퍼할 테니까요.'\""]], [["“But that is not all.”", "\"하지만 그게 다가 아니에요.\""]], [["“Not all!”", "\"다가 아니라고요!\""]], [["“No; they were going to marry their daughter——”", "\"네. 그 집에서 딸을 시집보내려던 참이었거든요……\""]], [["“To M. Franz d’Épinay.", "\"프란츠 데피네 씨에게 말이지요."], ["Is it broken off?”", "그 혼사가 깨졌습니까?\""]], [["“Yesterday morning, it appears, Franz declined the honor.”", "\"어제 아침에, 프란츠가 그 영예를 사양했다나 봐요.\""]], [["“Indeed? And is the reason known?”", "\"정말입니까? 그 까닭이 알려졌나요?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"아니요.\""]], [["“How extraordinary!", "\"참으로 별일이군요!"], ["And how does M. de Villefort bear it?”", "그래, 빌포르 씨는 그 일을 어떻게 받아들이던가요?\""]], [["“As usual. Like a philosopher.”", "\"여느 때처럼요. 철학자처럼 말이에요.\""]], [["Danglars returned at this moment alone.", "바로 그때 당글라르가 혼자 돌아왔다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래,\""], ["said the baroness, “do you leave M. Cavalcanti with your daughter?”", "남작 부인이 말했다. \"카발칸티 씨를 딸아이와 단둘이 두고 오셨어요?\""]], [["“And Mademoiselle d’Armilly,”", "\"다르밀리 양도 있잖소.\""], ["said the banker; “do you consider her no one?”", "은행가가 말했다. \"그 아가씨는 사람으로 안 친단 말이오?\""], ["Then, turning to Monte Cristo, he said, “Prince Cavalcanti is a charming young man, is he not?", "그러고는 몬테크리스토를 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"카발칸티 공자, 매력적인 젊은이지요, 안 그렇습니까?"], ["But is he really a prince?”", "그런데 정말로 공자가 맞기는 한 겁니까?\""]], [["“I will not answer for it,”", "\"그건 제가 장담할 수 없습니다.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["“His father was introduced to me as a marquis, so he ought to be a count; but I do not think he has much claim to that title.”", "\"그 부친이 제게 소개될 때 후작이라 하였으니, 그렇다면 아들은 백작쯤 되어야겠지요. 하지만 그가 그 작위를 내세울 만한 근거가 그리 있어 보이지는 않습니다.\""]], [["“Why?” said the banker.", "\"어째서요?\" 은행가가 말했다."], ["“If he is a prince, he is wrong not to maintain his rank; I do not like anyone to deny his origin.”", "\"공자라면, 제 신분을 지키지 않는 건 잘못이지요. 나는 누가 제 출신을 부인하는 걸 좋아하지 않습니다.\""]], [["“Oh, you are a thorough democrat,”", "\"오, 당신은 철저한 민주주의자시군요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, smiling.", "몬테크리스토가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“But do you see to what you are exposing yourself?”", "\"하지만 당신이 어떤 처지에 놓이는지 아세요?\""], ["said the baroness.", "남작 부인이 말했다."], ["“If, perchance, M. de Morcerf came, he would find M. Cavalcanti in that room, where he, the betrothed of Eugénie, has never been admitted.”", "\"혹시라도 모르세르 씨가 온다면, 외제니의 약혼자인 그가 한 번도 들어가 본 적 없는 저 방에서 카발칸티 씨와 마주치게 될 거예요.\""]], [["“You may well say, perchance,”", "\"혹시라도, 라는 말이 딱 맞소.\""], ["replied the banker; “for he comes so seldom, it would seem only chance that brings him.”", "은행가가 대꾸했다. \"그 사람이 워낙 드물게 오니, 어쩌다 오는 게 순전히 우연처럼 보일 지경이지.\""]], [["“But should he come and find that young man with your daughter, he might be displeased.”", "\"하지만 그가 와서 저 젊은이가 딸과 함께 있는 걸 본다면, 기분이 상할지도 몰라요.\""]], [["“He? You are mistaken. M.", "\"그 사람이? 당신이 잘못 봤소."], ["Albert would not do us the honor to be jealous; he does not like Eugénie sufficiently.", "알베르 군은 우리한테 질투해 주는 영예를 베풀 위인이 아니야. 외제니를 그만큼 좋아하지도 않고."], ["Besides, I care not for his displeasure.”", "게다가 나는 그가 기분 상하든 말든 상관없소.\""]], [["“Still, situated as we are——”", "\"그래도, 우리 처지가 처지인 만큼……\""]], [["“Yes, do you know how we are situated?", "\"그래, 우리 처지가 어떤지 아시오?"], ["At his mother’s ball he danced once with Eugénie, and M. Cavalcanti three times, and he took no notice of it.”", "제 어머니의 무도회에서 그는 외제니와 딱 한 번 춤을 추었고, 카발칸티 씨는 세 번이나 추었는데, 그는 아무렇지도 않게 여기더군.\""]], [["The valet announced the Vicomte Albert de Morcerf.", "하인이 알베르 드 모르세르 자작을 알렸다."], ["The baroness rose hastily, and was going into the study, when Danglars stopped her.", "남작 부인이 황급히 일어나 작업실로 들어가려 하자, 당글라르가 그녀를 막았다."]], [["“Let her alone,” said he.", "\"그냥 두시오.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["She looked at him in amazement.", "그녀는 놀란 눈으로 그를 바라보았다."], ["Monte Cristo appeared to be unconscious of what passed.", "몬테크리스토는 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 모르는 듯했다."], ["Albert entered, looking very handsome and in high spirits.", "알베르가 들어왔는데, 매우 잘생긴 모습에 기분이 한껏 좋아 보였다."], ["He bowed politely to the baroness, familiarly to Danglars, and affectionately to Monte Cristo.", "그는 남작 부인에게 정중하게, 당글라르에게 허물없이, 몬테크리스토에게 다정하게 인사했다."], ["Then turning to the baroness: “May I ask how Mademoiselle Danglars is?” said he.", "그러고는 남작 부인을 향해 돌아서서 말했다. \"당글라르 양은 잘 지내는지 여쭈어도 될까요?\""]], [["“She is quite well,”", "\"아주 잘 지내네.\""], ["replied Danglars quickly; “she is at the piano with M. Cavalcanti.”", "당글라르가 얼른 대답했다. \"지금 카발칸티 씨와 피아노 앞에 있다네.\""]], [["Albert retained his calm and indifferent manner; he might feel perhaps annoyed, but he knew Monte Cristo’s eye was on him. “M.", "알베르는 차분하고 무심한 태도를 잃지 않았다. 어쩌면 속으로는 언짢았을지도 모르나, 몬테크리스토의 눈이 자신을 향해 있다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["Cavalcanti has a fine tenor voice,”", "\"카발칸티 씨는 훌륭한 테너 목소리를 지녔지요.\""], ["said he, “and Mademoiselle Eugénie a splendid soprano, and then she plays the piano like Thalberg.", "그가 말했다. \"외제니 양은 멋진 소프라노이고, 게다가 탈베르크처럼 피아노를 치니까요."], ["The concert must be a delightful one.”", "그 연주회는 분명 즐거운 것이겠습니다.\""]], [["“They suit each other remarkably well,” said Danglars.", "\"둘이 아주 잘 어울리지.\" 당글라르가 말했다."], ["Albert appeared not to notice this remark, which was, however, so rude that Madame Danglars blushed.", "알베르는 이 말을 못 들은 척했으나, 그 말이 어찌나 무례했던지 당글라르 부인이 얼굴을 붉혔다."]], [["“I, too,”", "\"저도,\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"음악을 합니다."], ["said the young man, “am a musician—at least, my masters used to tell me so; but it is strange that my voice never would suit any other, and a soprano less than any.”", "적어도 제 선생님들은 그렇게 말씀하시곤 했지요. 하지만 이상하게도 제 목소리는 다른 어떤 목소리와도 도무지 어울리질 않았어요. 그중에서도 소프라노와는 가장 안 맞았고요.\""]], [["Danglars smiled, and seemed to say, “It is of no consequence.”", "당글라르는 미소를 지었는데, 마치 \"그건 아무래도 상관없네.\""], ["Then, hoping doubtless to effect his purpose, he said,—“The prince and my daughter were universally admired yesterday.", "하고 말하는 듯했다. 그러고는 분명 제 속셈을 이루려는 듯 말했다. \"공자와 내 딸이 어제 모두에게 큰 찬사를 받았다네."], ["You were not of the party, M. de Morcerf?”", "자네는 그 자리에 없었지, 모르세르 군?\""]], [["“What prince?”", "\"무슨 공자 말씀입니까?\""], ["asked Albert.", "알베르가 물었다."]], [["“Prince Cavalcanti,” said Danglars, who persisted in giving the young man that title.", "\"카발칸티 공자 말일세.\" 당글라르는 끝끝내 그 젊은이에게 그 작위를 붙여 불렀다."]], [["“Pardon me,”", "\"실례지만,\""], ["said Albert, “I was not aware that he was a prince.", "알베르가 말했다. \"그가 공자인 줄은 몰랐습니다."], ["And Prince Cavalcanti sang with Mademoiselle Eugénie yesterday?", "그래, 카발칸티 공자가 어제 외제니 양과 함께 노래를 불렀다고요?"], ["It must have been charming, indeed. I regret not having heard them.", "정말 멋진 자리였겠습니다. 그 노래를 듣지 못한 게 유감이군요."], ["But I was unable to accept your invitation, having promised to accompany my mother to a German concert given by the Baroness of Château-Renaud.”", "하지만 어제는 어머니를 모시고 샤토르노 남작 부인이 여신 독일 음악회에 가기로 약속한 터라, 초대에 응할 수가 없었습니다.\""]], [["This was followed by rather an awkward silence.", "이어 다소 어색한 침묵이 흘렀다."]], [["“May I also be allowed,” said Morcerf, “to pay my respects to Mademoiselle Danglars?”", "\"저도 당글라르 양께 인사를 드려도 될까요?\" 모르세르가 말했다."]], [["“Wait a moment,”", "\"잠깐만.\""], ["said the banker, stopping the young man; “do you hear that delightful cavatina?", "은행가가 젊은이를 붙들며 말했다. \"저 멋진 카바티나가 들리나?"], ["Ta, ta, ta, ti, ta, ti, ta, ta; it is charming, let them finish—one moment.", "타, 타, 타, 티, 타, 티, 타, 타. 정말 근사하군. 끝까지 들어 보세. 잠깐만."], ["Bravo, bravi, brava!” The banker was enthusiastic in his applause.", "브라보, 브라비, 브라바!\" 은행가는 열렬히 박수를 쳤다."]], [["40054m\n“Indeed,”", "\"정말이지,\""], ["said Albert, “it is exquisite; it is impossible to understand the music of his country better than Prince Cavalcanti does.", "알베르가 말했다. \"더없이 훌륭하군요. 카발칸티 공자만큼 제 나라 음악을 잘 이해하기란 불가능한 일이지요."], ["You said prince, did you not?", "공자라고 하셨지요?"], ["But he can easily become one, if he is not already; it is no uncommon thing in Italy.", "하지만 아직 아니라 해도 얼마든지 공자가 될 수 있을 겁니다. 이탈리아에서는 드문 일도 아니니까요."], ["But to return to the charming musicians—you should give us a treat, Danglars, without telling them there is a stranger.", "한데 그 멋진 음악가들 이야기로 돌아가자면 — 당글라르 씨, 저들에게 손님이 와 있다는 말은 하지 말고 우리에게 한 곡 더 들려주시지요."], ["Ask them to sing one more song; it is so delightful to hear music in the distance, when the musicians are unrestrained by observation.”", "부탁드립니다. 음악가들이 남의 눈을 의식하지 않을 때, 멀리서 음악을 듣는 건 참으로 즐거운 일이니까요.\""]], [["Danglars was quite annoyed by the young man’s indifference. He took Monte Cristo aside.", "당글라르는 젊은이의 무심함에 적잖이 언짢았다. 그는 몬테크리스토를 한쪽으로 데리고 갔다."]], [["“What do you think of our lover?” said he.", "\"우리 구혼자를 어떻게 보십니까?\" 그가 말했다."]], [["“He appears cool.", "\"냉랭해 보이는군요."], ["But, then your word is given.”", "하지만 어쨌든 당신은 약속하신 거 아닙니까.\""]], [["“Yes, doubtless I have promised to give my daughter to a man who loves her, but not to one who does not.", "\"그렇지요. 분명 내 딸을, 그 애를 사랑하는 사람에게 주겠다고 약속했지, 사랑하지 않는 사람에게 주겠다고는 안 했습니다."], ["See him there, cold as marble and proud like his father.", "저기 저 사람을 보시오. 대리석처럼 차갑고 제 아비처럼 거만하지 않습니까."], ["If he were rich, if he had Cavalcanti’s fortune, that might be pardoned.", "부자라면, 카발칸티만큼 재산이라도 있다면야 봐줄 수도 있겠지요."], ["_Ma foi_, I haven’t consulted my daughter; but if she has good taste——”", "정말이지, 내 딸에게 물어보지는 않았소만, 그 애가 안목이 있다면……\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["said Monte Cristo, “my fondness may blind me, but I assure you I consider Morcerf a charming young man who will render your daughter happy and will sooner or later attain a certain amount of distinction, and his father’s position is good.”", "\"제가 그를 아끼는 마음에 눈이 멀었는지는 모르겠으나, 분명히 말씀드리건대 저는 모르세르를 매력적인 젊은이로 봅니다. 그는 따님을 행복하게 해 줄 것이고, 머잖아 어느 정도 명성도 얻을 겁니다. 게다가 부친의 지위도 든든하지요.\""]], [["“Hem,”", "\"흠.\""], ["said Danglars.", "당글라르가 말했다."]], [["“Why do you doubt?”", "\"어째서 의심하십니까?\""]], [["“The past—that obscurity on the past.”", "\"과거 말이오. 그 과거에 드리운 그늘 말입니다.\""]], [["“But that does not affect the son.”", "\"하지만 그게 아들에게 영향을 미치지는 않지요.\""]], [["“Very true.”", "\"지당한 말씀입니다.\""]], [["“Now, I beg of you, don’t go off your head.", "\"자, 부탁이니 부디 흥분하지 마십시오. 당신이 이 혼사를 생각해 온 지 벌써 한 달이오."], ["It’s a month now that you have been thinking of this marriage, and you must see that it throws some responsibility on me, for it was at my house you met this young Cavalcanti, whom I do not really know at all.”", "그러니 이 일이 내게도 어느 정도 책임을 지운다는 걸 아셔야 합니다. 당신이 이 젊은 카발칸티를 만난 게 바로 우리 집에서였으니 말이오. 나로서는 그를 사실 전혀 모르는데 말입니다.\""]], [["“But I do.”", "\"하지만 나는 압니다.\""]], [["“Have you made inquiry?”", "\"알아보기라도 하셨소?\""]], [["“Is there any need of that!", "\"그럴 필요가 어디 있겠습니까!"], ["Does not his appearance speak for him?", "그 풍채가 그를 대변해 주지 않습니까?"], ["And he is very rich.”", "게다가 그는 대단한 부자고요.\""]], [["“I am not so sure of that.”", "\"그건 나도 그리 확신이 안 섭니다.\""]], [["“And yet you said he had money.”", "\"그래도 돈이 있다고 하셨잖습니까.\""]], [["“Fifty thousand livres—a mere trifle.”", "\"5만 리브르 — 그까짓 푼돈이지요.\""]], [["“He is well educated.”", "\"교양도 갖추었고요.\""]], [["“Hem,”", "\"흠.\""], ["said Monte Cristo in his turn.", "이번에는 몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“He is a musician.”", "\"음악가이기도 하고요.\""]], [["“So are all Italians.”", "\"이탈리아 사람이라면 다 그렇지요.\""]], [["“Come, count, you do not do that young man justice.”", "\"이거 참, 백작님, 그 젊은이를 너무 박하게 보시는군요.\""]], [["“Well, I acknowledge it annoys me, knowing your connection with the Morcerf family, to see him throw himself in the way.”", "\"글쎄요, 인정합니다만, 당신이 모르세르 집안과 맺은 인연을 아는 터라, 그가 끼어드는 걸 보면 마음이 거슬리는군요.\""], ["Danglars burst out laughing.", "당글라르가 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“What a Puritan you are!”", "\"어쩜 그리 청교도시오!\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he; “that happens every day.”", "\"그런 일은 날마다 일어나는 일인걸요.\""]], [["“But you cannot break it off in this way; the Morcerfs are depending on this union.”", "\"하지만 이런 식으로 깨 버릴 수는 없는 노릇이지요. 모르세르 집안은 이 혼사에 기대를 걸고 있으니까요.\""]], [["“Indeed.”", "\"그렇소?\""]], [["“Positively.”", "\"틀림없습니다.\""]], [["“Then let them explain themselves; you should give the father a hint, you are so intimate with the family.”", "\"그렇다면 그쪽이 분명히 입장을 밝혀야지요. 당신이 그 아버지에게 넌지시 일러 주시구려. 그 집안과 그토록 가까운 사이시니 말이오.\""]], [["“I?—where the devil did you find out that?”", "\"내가요? 대체 어디서 그런 소릴 들으셨습니까?\""]], [["“At their ball; it was apparent enough.", "\"그 댁 무도회에서 말이오."], ["Why, did not the countess, the proud Mercédès, the disdainful Catalane, who will scarcely open her lips to her oldest acquaintances, take your arm, lead you into the garden, into the private walks, and remain there for half an hour?”", "충분히 드러나 보이던걸요. 아니, 그 백작 부인, 그 도도한 메르세데스, 오랜 지기에게도 좀체 입을 열지 않는 그 새침한 카탈루냐 여인이, 당신 팔을 잡고 정원으로, 한적한 산책로로 이끌어 거기서 반 시간이나 머물지 않았습니까?\""]], [["“Ah, baron, baron,”", "\"아, 남작, 남작,\""], ["said Albert, “you are not listening—what barbarism in a megalomaniac like you!”", "알베르가 말했다. \"남작께선 안 듣고 계시는군요 — 당신처럼 과대망상에 빠진 사람한테 이 무슨 야만적인 짓이오!\""]], [["“Oh, don’t worry about me, Sir Mocker,”", "\"오, 내 걱정은 마시오, 비꼬기 선생.\""], ["said Danglars; then turning to Monte Cristo he said:", "당글라르가 말했다. 그러고는 몬테크리스토를 향해 돌아서서 말했다."]], [["“But will you undertake to speak to the father?”", "\"한데 그 아버지한테 얘기를 좀 해 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“Willingly, if you wish it.”", "\"기꺼이 그러지요, 당신이 원하신다면요.\""]], [["“But let it be done explicitly and positively.", "\"하지만 분명하고 확실하게 해 주십시오."], ["If he demands my daughter let him fix the day—declare his conditions; in short, let us either understand each other, or quarrel.", "그가 내 딸을 원한다면 날짜를 잡으라고, 조건을 내걸라고 하시오. 한마디로, 서로 분명히 하든지, 아니면 갈라서든지 합시다."], ["You understand—no more delay.”", "아시겠지요 — 더는 미루지 말자는 겁니다.\""]], [["“Yes, sir, I will give my attention to the subject.”", "\"네, 그러지요. 이 일에 마음을 쓰겠습니다.\""]], [["“I do not say that I await with pleasure his decision, but I do await it.", "\"그의 결정을 즐거이 기다린다고는 못하겠소만, 어쨌든 기다리기는 하겠습니다."], ["A banker must, you know, be a slave to his promise.”", "아시다시피 은행가란 제 약속의 노예가 되어야 하는 법이니까요.\""], ["And Danglars sighed as M. Cavalcanti had done half an hour before.", "그러고서 당글라르는 반 시간 전 카발칸티가 그랬듯 한숨을 내쉬었다."]], [["“Bravi! bravo! brava!”", "\"브라비! 브라보! 브라바!\""], ["cried Morcerf, parodying the banker, as the selection came to an end.", "곡이 끝나자 모르세르가 은행가를 흉내 내며 외쳤다."], ["Danglars began to look suspiciously at Morcerf, when someone came and whispered a few words to him.", "당글라르가 모르세르를 미심쩍은 눈으로 보기 시작할 무렵, 누군가 다가와 그에게 몇 마디를 속삭였다."]], [["“I shall soon return,”", "\"곧 돌아오겠습니다.\""], ["said the banker to Monte Cristo; “wait for me.", "은행가가 몬테크리스토에게 말했다. \"기다려 주십시오."], ["I shall, perhaps, have something to say to you.”", "어쩌면 당신께 드릴 말씀이 있을 것 같으니까요.\""], ["And he went out.", "그러고는 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["The baroness took advantage of her husband’s absence to push open the door of her daughter’s study, and M.", "남작 부인은 남편이 자리를 비운 틈을 타 딸의 작업실 문을 밀어 열었다."], ["Andrea, who was sitting before the piano with Mademoiselle Eugénie, started up like a jack-in-the-box.", "외제니 양과 함께 피아노 앞에 앉아 있던 안드레아 군이 깜짝 상자 인형처럼 벌떡 일어섰다."], ["Albert bowed with a smile to Mademoiselle Danglars, who did not appear in the least disturbed, and returned his bow with her usual coolness.", "알베르가 미소를 띠며 당글라르 양에게 인사했으나, 그녀는 조금도 동요하는 기색 없이 여느 때의 그 차가움으로 답례했다."], ["Cavalcanti was evidently embarrassed; he bowed to Morcerf, who replied with the most impertinent look possible.", "카발칸티는 분명 당황한 기색이었다. 그가 모르세르에게 인사하자, 모르세르는 더없이 무례한 표정으로 답했다."], ["Then Albert launched out in praise of Mademoiselle Danglars’ voice, and on his regret, after what he had just heard, that he had been unable to be present the previous evening.", "그러고 나서 알베르는 당글라르 양의 목소리를 한껏 칭찬하며, 방금 들은 그 노래를 두고 어제저녁 그 자리에 함께하지 못한 것이 못내 아쉽다고 늘어놓았다."]], [["Cavalcanti, being left alone, turned to Monte Cristo.", "홀로 남겨진 카발칸티는 몬테크리스토를 향해 돌아섰다."]], [["“Come,”", "\"자,\""], ["said Madame Danglars, “leave music and compliments, and let us go and take tea.”", "당글라르 부인이 말했다. \"음악이며 인사치레는 그만들 하고, 가서 차나 마십시다.\""]], [["“Come, Louise,” said Mademoiselle Danglars to her friend.", "\"가자, 루이즈.\" 당글라르 양이 친구에게 말했다."]], [["They passed into the next drawing-room, where tea was prepared.", "그들은 차가 준비된 옆 응접실로 자리를 옮겼다."], ["Just as they were beginning, in the English fashion, to leave the spoons in their cups, the door again opened and Danglars entered, visibly agitated.", "영국식으로 찻잔에 숟가락을 그냥 둔 채 막 차를 마시려는 참에, 다시 문이 열리며 당글라르가 들어왔다. 눈에 띄게 동요한 모습이었다."], ["Monte Cristo observed it particularly, and by a look asked the banker for an explanation.", "몬테크리스토가 그것을 유심히 살피고는, 눈짓으로 은행가에게 설명을 청했다."]], [["“I have just received my courier from Greece,”", "\"방금 그리스에서 보낸 전령이 도착했습니다.\""], ["said Danglars.", "당글라르가 말했다."]], [["“Ah, yes,”", "\"아, 그래서,\""], ["said the count; “that was the reason of your running away from us.”", "백작이 말했다. \"우리한테서 황급히 자리를 뜨셨던 거군요.\" \"그렇습니다.\""]], [["“Yes.” “How is King Otho getting on?”", "\"오톤 왕은 어떻게 지내십니까?\""], ["asked Albert in the most sprightly tone.", "알베르가 더없이 쾌활한 어조로 물었다."]], [["Danglars cast another suspicious look towards him without answering, and Monte Cristo turned away to conceal the expression of pity which passed over his features, but which was gone in a moment.", "당글라르는 대꾸도 없이 그에게 또 한 번 미심쩍은 눈길을 던졌고, 몬테크리스토는 제 얼굴에 스치는 연민의 빛을 감추려 고개를 돌렸으나, 그 빛은 한순간에 사라졌다."]], [["“We shall go together, shall we not?”", "\"우리 함께 가는 거지요?\""], ["said Albert to the count.", "알베르가 백작에게 말했다."]], [["“If you like,”", "\"원하신다면요.\""], ["replied the latter.", "백작이 대답했다."]], [["Albert could not understand the banker’s look, and turning to Monte Cristo, who understood it perfectly,—“Did you see,”", "알베르는 은행가의 눈빛을 이해할 수가 없어, 그것을 훤히 알아챈 몬테크리스토를 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"보셨습니까?"], ["said he, “how he looked at me?”", "저 사람이 저를 어떻게 노려보는지요?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said the count; “but did you think there was anything particular in his look?”", "백작이 말했다. \"한데 그 눈빛에 뭔가 특별한 게 있다고 생각하셨습니까?\""]], [["“Indeed, I did; and what does he mean by his news from Greece?”", "\"정말 그랬는걸요. 그리고 그리스에서 온 소식이라는 게 대체 무슨 뜻이랍니까?\""]], [["“How can I tell you?”", "\"그걸 제가 어찌 알겠습니까?\""]], [["“Because I imagine you have correspondents in that country.”", "\"백작께서는 그 나라에 연락하는 사람들을 두고 계실 것 같아서요.\""]], [["Monte Cristo smiled significantly.", "몬테크리스토가 의미심장하게 미소 지었다."]], [["“Stop,”", "\"잠깐,\""], ["said Albert, “here he comes.", "알베르가 말했다. \"그가 이리로 오는군요."], ["I shall compliment Mademoiselle Danglars on her cameo, while the father talks to you.”", "아버지가 백작님과 이야기하시는 동안 저는 당글라르 양에게 카메오를 칭찬해야겠습니다.\""]], [["“If you compliment her at all, let it be on her voice, at least,”", "\"칭찬을 하시려거든, 적어도 그 목소리를 칭찬하시지요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“No, everyone would do that.”", "\"안 됩니다. 그건 누구나 다 할 칭찬이잖습니까.\""]], [["“My dear viscount, you are dreadfully impertinent.”", "\"이보시오, 자작, 당신은 지독히도 무례하군요.\""]], [["Albert advanced towards Eugénie, smiling.", "알베르가 미소를 띠며 외제니에게 다가갔다."]], [["Meanwhile, Danglars, stooping to Monte Cristo’s ear, “Your advice was excellent,”", "그러는 동안 당글라르가 몬테크리스토의 귀에 대고 몸을 숙이며 말했다."], ["said he; “there is a whole history connected with the names Fernand and Yanina.”", "\"당신 충고가 아주 훌륭했습니다. 페르낭과 야니나라는 이름에 얽힌 사연이 통째로 하나 있더군요.\""]], [["“Indeed?”", "\"정말입니까?\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, I will tell you all; but take away the young man; I cannot endure his presence.”", "\"그렇습니다. 다 말씀드리지요. 하지만 저 젊은이를 좀 데려가 주십시오. 그가 곁에 있는 걸 도무지 견딜 수가 없으니.\""]], [["“He is going with me.", "\"그는 저와 함께 갈 겁니다."], ["Shall I send the father to you?”", "그 아버지를 당신께 보내 드릴까요?\""]], [["“Immediately.”", "\"당장 보내 주십시오.\""]], [["“Very well.”", "\"좋습니다.\""], ["The count made a sign to Albert and they bowed to the ladies, and took their leave, Albert perfectly indifferent to Mademoiselle Danglars’ contempt, Monte Cristo reiterating his advice to Madame Danglars on the prudence a banker’s wife should exercise in providing for the future.", "백작이 알베르에게 신호를 보내자, 두 사람은 부인들에게 인사하고 자리를 떴다. 알베르는 당글라르 양의 경멸에도 더없이 무심했고, 몬테크리스토는 은행가의 아내라면 장래를 대비하는 데 마땅히 신중을 기해야 한다고 당글라르 부인에게 거듭 충고했다."]], [["M. Cavalcanti remained master of the field.", "카발칸티 군은 그 자리의 주인으로 남았다."]], [["# Chapter 77. Haydée", "# Chapter 77. Haydée"]], [["Scarcely had the count’s horses cleared the angle of the boulevard, when Albert, turning towards the count, burst into a loud fit of laughter—much too loud in fact not to give the idea of its being rather forced and unnatural.", "백작의 말들이 큰길 모퉁이를 채 벗어나기도 전에, 알베르는 백작을 돌아보며 한바탕 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 사실 그 웃음은 너무 커서, 오히려 억지로 꾸며 낸 부자연스러운 것임을 짐작하게 했다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"자,\""], ["said he, “I will ask you the same question which Charles IX.", "그가 말했다."], ["put to Catherine de’ Medici, after the massacre of Saint Bartholomew: ‘How have I played my little part?’”", "\"샤를 9세가 성 바르톨로메오 축일의 학살이 끝난 뒤 카트린 드 메디시스에게 던졌던 질문을 그대로 백작님께 여쭙고 싶군요. '내가 내 작은 역할을 어떻게 해냈소?'\""]], [["“To what do you allude?” asked Monte Cristo.", "\"무슨 말인가?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다."]], [["“To the installation of my rival at M. Danglars’.”", "\"당글라르 씨 댁에 제 경쟁자를 들여놓은 일 말입니다.\""]], [["“What rival?”", "\"무슨 경쟁자?\""]], [["“_Ma foi!_ what rival?", "\"맙소사! 무슨 경쟁자라뇨?"], ["Why, your protégé, M. Andrea Cavalcanti!”", "백작님이 후원하시는 안드레아 카발칸티 씨 말이지요!\""]], [["“Ah, no joking, viscount, if you please; I do not patronize M.", "\"아, 부디 농담은 그만두시지요, 자작. 나는 안드레아 씨를 후원하지 않습니다."], ["Andrea—at least, not as concerns M. Danglars.”", "적어도 당글라르 씨에 관한 한은요.\""]], [["“And you would be to blame for not assisting him, if the young man really needed your help in that quarter, but, happily for me, he can dispense with it.”", "\"그런데 그 젊은이가 정말로 그 방면에서 백작님의 도움이 필요했다면, 도와주지 않으신 것은 비난받을 일이지요. 하지만 다행히도 제게는, 그가 도움 없이도 잘해 나가고 있습니다.\""]], [["“What, do you think he is paying his addresses?”", "\"그래, 자네는 그가 청혼이라도 하고 있다고 생각하나?\""]], [["“I am certain of it; his languishing looks and modulated tones when addressing Mademoiselle Danglars fully proclaim his intentions.", "\"확신합니다. 당글라르 양에게 말을 걸 때 그 흐느적거리는 눈빛과 곡조 붙인 목소리가 그 속내를 또렷이 드러내거든요."], ["He aspires to the hand of the proud Eugénie.”", "그는 도도한 외제니의 손을 노리고 있습니다.\""]], [["“What does that signify, so long as they favor your suit?”", "\"그게 무슨 상관인가, 정작 자네 구혼은 호의를 얻고 있다면야?\""]], [["“But it is not the case, my dear count: on the contrary.", "\"하지만 그렇지가 않습니다, 백작님. 오히려 반대지요."], ["I am repulsed on all sides.”", "저는 사방에서 퇴짜를 맞고 있습니다.\""]], [["“What!”", "\"뭐라고!\""]], [["“It is so indeed; Mademoiselle Eugénie scarcely answers me, and Mademoiselle d’Armilly, her confidant, does not speak to me at all.”", "\"정말 그렇습니다. 외제니 양은 제게 거의 대꾸도 하지 않고, 그녀의 말벗인 다르밀리 양은 아예 제게 말을 걸지도 않습니다.\""]], [["“But the father has the greatest regard possible for you,”", "\"하지만 아버지는 자네를 더없이 좋게 여기고 있지.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“He?", "\"그가요? 오, 아닙니다."], ["Oh, no, he has plunged a thousand daggers into my heart, tragedy-weapons, I own, which instead of wounding sheathe their points in their own handles, but daggers which he nevertheless believed to be real and deadly.”", "그는 제 가슴에 단검을 천 자루나 꽂았는걸요. 인정하건대, 찌르기는커녕 칼끝이 제 손잡이 속으로 도로 들어가 버리는 연극용 소도구 같은 단검이긴 하지만요. 그래도 그는 그게 진짜이고 사람을 죽일 수 있다고 믿었습니다.\""]], [["“Jealousy indicates affection.”", "\"질투는 애정의 표시일세.\""]], [["“True; but I am not jealous.”", "\"맞습니다. 하지만 저는 질투하지 않아요.\""], ["“He is.”", "\"그가 질투하는 거지.\""]], [["“Of whom?—of Debray?”", "\"누구를? 드브레를?\""]], [["“No, of you.”", "\"아니, 자네를.\""]], [["“Of me? I will engage to say that before a week is past the door will be closed against me.”", "\"아니, 자네를.\" \"저를요? 일주일도 지나기 전에 제 앞에서 문이 닫힐 거라고 장담하지요.\""]], [["“You are mistaken, my dear viscount.”", "\"틀렸네, 자작.\""]], [["“Prove it to me.”", "\"증명해 보십시오.\""]], [["“Do you wish me to do so?”", "\"내가 그러길 바라나?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Well, I am charged with the commission of endeavoring to induce the Comte de Morcerf to make some definite arrangement with the baron.”", "\"실은 내가 모르세르 백작에게 그 남작과 어떤 확실한 합의를 맺도록 설득해 보라는 임무를 맡았다네.\""]], [["“By whom are you charged?”", "\"누구에게서 그런 임무를 맡으셨습니까?\""]], [["“By the baron himself.”", "\"남작 본인에게서.\""]], [["“Oh,” said Albert with all the cajolery of which he was capable.", "\"오,\" 알베르가 부릴 수 있는 온갖 아양을 떨며 말했다."], ["“You surely will not do that, my dear count?”", "\"백작님, 설마 그렇게 하시지는 않겠지요?\""]], [["“Certainly I shall, Albert, as I have promised to do it.”", "\"당연히 할 걸세, 알베르. 그러기로 약속했으니까.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"그렇군요.\""], ["said Albert, with a sigh, “it seems you are determined to marry me.”", "알베르가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"아무래도 백작님은 기어이 저를 결혼시키기로 작정하신 모양입니다.\""]], [["“I am determined to try and be on good terms with everybody, at all events,”", "\"어쨌든 나는 모두와 좋은 사이로 지내려고 애써 보기로 작정했네.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["“But apropos of Debray, how is it that I have not seen him lately at the baron’s house?”", "\"그런데 드브레 이야기가 나왔으니 말인데, 요즘 남작 댁에서 그를 통 못 본 건 어찌 된 일인가?\""]], [["“There has been a misunderstanding.”", "\"오해가 좀 있었거든요.\""]], [["“What, with the baroness?”", "\"뭐, 남작 부인과?\""]], [["“No, with the baron.”", "\"아니요, 남작과요.\""]], [["“Has he perceived anything?”", "\"그가 뭔가 눈치챈 건가?\""]], [["“Ah, that is a good joke!”", "\"아, 그것참 재미있는 농담이군요!\""]], [["“Do you think he suspects?”", "\"그가 의심하고 있다고 생각하나?\""], ["said Monte Cristo with charming artlessness.", "몬테크리스토가 더없이 천연덕스러운 표정으로 물었다."]], [["“Where have you come from, my dear count?”", "\"백작님, 대체 어디서 오신 분입니까?\""], ["said Albert.", "알베르가 말했다."]], [["“From Congo, if you will.”", "\"굳이 따지자면 콩고에서지.\""]], [["“It must be farther off than even that.”", "\"그보다 훨씬 더 먼 데서 오셨겠지요.\""]], [["“But what do I know of your Parisian husbands?”", "\"하지만 내가 파리의 남편들에 대해 뭘 알겠나?\""]], [["“Oh, my dear count, husbands are pretty much the same everywhere; an individual husband of any country is a pretty fair specimen of the whole race.”", "\"오, 백작님, 남편이란 어디서나 거의 똑같습니다. 어느 나라든 남편 하나만 보면 그 종족 전체의 꽤 좋은 표본이 되지요.\""]], [["“But then, what can have led to the quarrel between Danglars and Debray?", "\"하지만 그렇다면, 당글라르와 드브레 사이에 무슨 일로 다툼이 생긴 걸까?"], ["They seemed to understand each other so well,”", "둘은 서로 그렇게나 잘 통하는 것 같던데.\""], ["said Monte Cristo with renewed energy.", "몬테크리스토가 다시금 흥미를 보이며 말했다."]], [["“Ah, now you are trying to penetrate into the mysteries of Isis, in which I am not initiated.", "\"아, 이제 백작님은 제가 입문하지 못한 이시스의 비밀을 캐려 하시는군요."], ["When M. Andrea Cavalcanti has become one of the family, you can ask him that question.”", "안드레아 카발칸티 씨가 그 집안의 일원이 되거든, 그에게 물어보십시오.\""]], [["The carriage stopped.", "마차가 멈췄다."]], [["“Here we are,”", "\"다 왔군.\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “it is only half-past ten o’clock, come in.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"아직 열 시 반밖에 안 됐으니, 들어오게.\""]], [["“Certainly, I will.”", "\"물론, 그러지요.\""]], [["“My carriage shall take you back.”", "\"내 마차로 자네를 데려다주겠네.\""]], [["“No, thank you; I gave orders for my _coupé_ to follow me.”", "\"아니요, 괜찮습니다. 제 쿠페에게 따라오라고 일러두었거든요.\""]], [["“There it is, then,”", "\"저기 있군.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, as he stepped out of the carriage.", "몬테크리스토가 마차에서 내리며 말했다."], ["They both went into the house; the drawing-room was lighted up—they went in there.", "두 사람은 집 안으로 들어갔다. 응접실에 불이 환히 켜져 있어, 그들은 그리로 들어갔다."], ["“You will make tea for us, Baptistin,”", "\"우리에게 차를 내오게, 바티스탱.\""], ["said the count.", "백작이 말했다."], ["Baptistin left the room without waiting to answer, and in two seconds reappeared, bringing on a tray, all that his master had ordered, ready prepared, and appearing to have sprung from the ground, like the repasts which we read of in fairy tales.", "바티스탱은 대답을 기다리지도 않고 방을 나갔다가, 이 초도 안 되어 쟁반을 들고 다시 나타났다. 거기에는 주인이 명한 것이 모두 준비된 채 담겨 있었는데, 마치 동화 속에서나 읽는 그런 잔치처럼 땅에서 솟아난 듯했다."]], [["“Really, my dear count,” said Morcerf, “what I admire in you is, not so much your riches, for perhaps there are people even wealthier than yourself, nor is it only your wit, for Beaumarchais might have possessed as much,—but it is your manner of being served, without any questions, in a moment, in a second; it is as if they guessed what you wanted by your manner of ringing, and made a point of keeping everything you can possibly desire in constant readiness.”", "\"백작님, 정말이지,\" 모르세르가 말했다. \"제가 백작님께 감탄하는 건 그 재산이 아닙니다. 백작님보다 더 부유한 사람도 어쩌면 있을 테니까요. 그렇다고 백작님의 재치만도 아니지요. 보마르셰도 그만큼은 지녔을 테니까요. 제가 감탄하는 건 시중을 받는 방식입니다. 아무것도 묻지 않고, 순식간에, 일 초 만에 말입니다. 마치 백작님이 벨을 울리는 방식만으로 무얼 원하시는지 알아채고, 백작님이 바랄 법한 것은 무엇이든 늘 준비해 두기로 작정한 것 같단 말이지요.\""]], [["“What you say is perhaps true; they know my habits.", "\"자네 말이 어쩌면 맞을 걸세. 그들은 내 습관을 알고 있으니까."], ["For instance, you shall see; how do you wish to occupy yourself during tea-time?”", "예를 들어, 보여 주지. 차를 마시는 동안 무엇을 하며 보내고 싶나?\""]], [["“_Ma foi_, I should like to smoke.”", "\"글쎄요, 담배나 한 대 피우고 싶군요.\""]], [["Monte Cristo took the gong and struck it once.", "몬테크리스토는 징을 들어 한 번 쳤다."], ["In about the space of a second a private door opened, and Ali appeared, bringing two chibouques filled with excellent latakia.", "일 초쯤 지나자 안쪽 문이 열리며 알리가 나타났는데, 최상급 라타키아 담배를 채운 치부크 두 대를 들고 있었다."]], [["“It is quite wonderful,” said Albert.", "\"정말 놀랍군요.\" 알베르가 말했다."]], [["“Oh no, it is as simple as possible,”", "\"오, 아닐세. 더없이 단순한 일이지.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다."], ["replied Monte Cristo.", "\"알리는 내가 보통 차나 커피를 마시면서 담배를 피운다는 걸 알아. 내가 차를 내오라고 명한 것도 들었고, 자네를 집으로 데려온 것도 알지."], ["“Ali knows I generally smoke while I am taking my tea or coffee; he has heard that I ordered tea, and he also knows that I brought you home with me; when I summoned him he naturally guessed the reason of my doing so, and as he comes from a country where hospitality is especially manifested through the medium of smoking, he naturally concludes that we shall smoke in company, and therefore brings two chibouques instead of one—and now the mystery is solved.”", "내가 그를 불렀으니, 그는 자연스레 그 까닭을 짐작했네. 그리고 그는 손님 대접이 특히 흡연을 통해 드러나는 나라에서 왔으니, 우리가 함께 담배를 피우리라고 자연스레 결론지어 한 대가 아니라 두 대의 치부크를 가져온 것이지. 자, 이제 수수께끼가 풀렸군.\""]], [["“Certainly you give a most commonplace air to your explanation, but it is not the less true that you——Ah, but what do I hear?”", "\"분명 백작님은 설명에 더없이 평범한 분위기를 입히시지만, 그렇다고 백작님이……아, 그런데 저게 무슨 소리지요?\""], ["and Morcerf inclined his head towards the door, through which sounds seemed to issue resembling those of a guitar.", "모르세르가 문 쪽으로 고개를 기울였다. 그 문 너머에서 기타 소리 비슷한 음향이 새어 나오는 듯했다."]], [["“_Ma foi_, my dear viscount, you are fated to hear music this evening; you have only escaped from Mademoiselle Danglars’ piano, to be attacked by Haydée’s guzla.”", "\"이런, 자작. 자네는 오늘 저녁 음악을 들을 운명인가 보군. 당글라르 양의 피아노에서 겨우 벗어났더니, 이제 하이데의 구즐라에게 붙들리게 생겼으니 말이야.\""]], [["“Haydée—what an adorable name!", "\"하이데, 정말 사랑스러운 이름이군요!"], ["Are there, then, really women who bear the name of Haydée anywhere but in Byron’s poems?”", "그렇다면 바이런의 시 말고 어딘가에 정말로 하이데라는 이름을 가진 여인들이 있단 말입니까?\""]], [["“Certainly there are.", "\"물론 있지."], ["Haydée is a very uncommon name in France, but is common enough in Albania and Epirus; it is as if you said, for example, Chastity, Modesty, Innocence,—it is a kind of baptismal name, as you Parisians call it.”", "하이데는 프랑스에서는 아주 드문 이름이지만, 알바니아와 에피루스에서는 흔하다네. 이를테면, 자네가 정숙, 겸손, 순결이라고 부르는 것과 같지. 자네 파리 사람들이 말하는 세례명 같은 것일세.\""]], [["“Oh, that is charming,”", "\"오, 그거 멋지군요.\""], ["said Albert, “how I should like to hear my countrywomen called Mademoiselle Goodness, Mademoiselle Silence, Mademoiselle Christian Charity!", "알베르가 말했다. \"제 동포 여인들이 선량 양, 침묵 양, 그리스도교의 자비 양으로 불리는 걸 한번 들어 보고 싶군요!"], ["Only think, then, if Mademoiselle Danglars, instead of being called Claire-Marie-Eugénie, had been named Mademoiselle Chastity-Modesty-Innocence Danglars; what a fine effect that would have produced on the announcement of her marriage!”", "생각해 보십시오, 당글라르 양이 클레르마리외제니가 아니라 정숙겸손순결 당글라르 양이라는 이름이었다면 말입니다. 그녀의 결혼 발표에 얼마나 근사한 효과를 냈겠습니까!\""]], [["“Hush,”", "\"쉿,\""], ["said the count, “do not joke in so loud a tone; Haydée may hear you, perhaps.”", "백작이 말했다. \"그렇게 큰 소리로 농담하지 말게. 하이데가 들을지도 모르니.\""]], [["“And you think she would be angry?”", "\"그러면 그녀가 화를 낼 거라고 생각하십니까?\""]], [["“No, certainly not,” said the count with a haughty expression.", "\"아니, 절대 그렇지 않네.\" 백작이 거만한 표정으로 말했다."]], [["“She is very amiable, then, is she not?”", "\"그렇다면 그녀는 무척 상냥한 모양이군요, 그렇지요?\""], ["said Albert.", "알베르가 말했다."]], [["“It is not to be called amiability, it is her duty; a slave does not dictate to a master.”", "\"그건 상냥함이라고 할 게 아니라, 그녀의 의무일세. 노예는 주인에게 이래라저래라 하지 않으니까.\""]], [["“Come; you are joking yourself now. Are there any more slaves to be had who bear this beautiful name?”", "\"이런, 이제는 백작님이 농담을 하시는군요. 이 아름다운 이름을 가진 노예가 또 있습니까?\""]], [["“Undoubtedly.”", "\"틀림없이 있지.\""]], [["“Really, count, you do nothing, and have nothing like other people.", "\"정말이지, 백작님은 하는 일도, 가진 것도 남들과는 다르군요."], ["The slave of the Count of Monte Cristo!", "몬테크리스토 백작의 노예라!"], ["Why, it is a rank of itself in France, and from the way in which you lavish money, it is a place that must be worth a hundred thousand francs a year.”", "그건 프랑스에서는 그 자체로 하나의 지위입니다. 게다가 백작님이 돈을 뿌리는 걸 보면, 그건 연간 십만 프랑은 나갈 자리일 겁니다.\""]], [["“A hundred thousand francs!", "\"십만 프랑이라!"], ["The poor girl originally possessed much more than that; she was born to treasures in comparison with which those recorded in the _Thousand and One Nights_ would seem but poverty.”", "그 가엾은 처녀는 본디 그보다 훨씬 더 많은 걸 지니고 있었네. 그녀는 보물을 타고난 몸이라, 천일야화에 기록된 보물도 그에 비하면 가난해 보일 정도였지.\""]], [["“She must be a princess then.”", "\"그렇다면 공주임이 분명하군요.\""]], [["“You are right; and she is one of the greatest in her country too.”", "\"맞네. 게다가 그녀는 제 나라에서 가장 지체 높은 사람들 중 하나였지.\""]], [["“I thought so. But how did it happen that such a great princess became a slave?”", "\"그럴 줄 알았습니다. 하지만 어쩌다 그런 위대한 공주가 노예가 되었을까요?\""]], [["“How was it that Dionysius the Tyrant became a schoolmaster?", "\"폭군 디오니시우스가 어쩌다 교사가 되었겠나?"], ["The fortune of war, my dear viscount,—the caprice of fortune; that is the way in which these things are to be accounted for.”", "전쟁의 운명일세, 자작. 운명의 변덕이지. 이런 일들은 그렇게밖에 설명할 수 없다네.\""]], [["“And is her name a secret?”", "\"그녀의 이름은 비밀입니까?\""]], [["“As regards the generality of mankind it is; but not for you, my dear viscount, who are one of my most intimate friends, and on whose silence I feel I may rely, if I consider it necessary to enjoin it—may I not do so?”", "\"보통 사람들에게는 비밀이지. 하지만 자네에게는 아닐세, 자작. 자네는 내 가장 가까운 벗 중 하나이고, 내가 필요하다고 여겨 입단속을 당부하면 자네의 침묵을 믿을 수 있다고 생각하니까. 그래도 되겠지?\""]], [["“Certainly; on my word of honor.”", "\"물론입니다. 제 명예를 걸고요.\""]], [["“You know the history of the Pasha of Yanina, do you not?”", "\"야니나의 파샤 이야기를 알고 있겠지?\""]], [["“Of Ali Tepelini?13 Oh, yes; it was in his service that my father made his fortune.”", "\"알리 테펠레니 말입니까? 오, 그럼요. 제 아버지가 재산을 일군 것도 그를 섬기면서였습니다.\""]], [["“True, I had forgotten that.”", "\"맞아, 내가 그걸 잊고 있었군.\""]], [["40062m\n“Well, what is Haydée to Ali Tepelini?”", "\"그런데 하이데가 알리 테펠레니와 무슨 관계입니까?\""]], [["“Merely his daughter.”", "\"그저 그의 딸일세.\""]], [["“What? the daughter of Ali Pasha?”", "\"뭐라고요? 알리 파샤의 딸이라고요?\""]], [["“Of Ali Pasha and the beautiful Vasiliki.”", "\"알리 파샤와 아름다운 바실리키의 딸이지.\""]], [["“And your slave?”", "\"그런데 백작님의 노예라고요?\""]], [["“_Ma foi_, yes.”", "\"그렇다네.\""]], [["“But how did she become so?”", "\"하지만 어쩌다 그렇게 된 겁니까?\""]], [["“Why, simply from the circumstance of my having bought her one day, as I was passing through the market at Constantinople.”", "\"뭐, 그저 어느 날 콘스탄티노플 시장을 지나다가 내가 그녀를 사들였다는 사정 때문이지.\""]], [["“Wonderful!", "\"놀랍군요!"], ["Really, my dear count, you seem to throw a sort of magic influence over all in which you are concerned; when I listen to you, existence no longer seems reality, but a waking dream.", "정말이지 백작님은 관련된 모든 일에 일종의 마법 같은 영향을 던지시는 것 같습니다. 백작님 말씀을 듣고 있노라면, 존재라는 게 더는 현실이 아니라 깨어 있는 채로 꾸는 꿈처럼 느껴집니다."], ["Now, I am perhaps going to make an imprudent and thoughtless request, but——”", "자, 이제 제가 어쩌면 경솔하고 생각 없는 부탁을 드리려는 참인데요, 하지만……\""]], [["“Say on.”", "\"말해 보게.\""]], [["“But, since you go out with Haydée, and sometimes even take her to the Opera——”", "\"하지만, 백작님은 하이데와 함께 외출하시고, 때로는 오페라에까지 데려가신다고 하니……\""]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서?\""]], [["“I think I may venture to ask you this favor.”", "\"감히 이 청을 드려도 될 것 같습니다.\""]], [["“You may venture to ask me anything.”", "\"내게는 무엇이든 감히 청해도 되네.\""]], [["“Well then, my dear count, present me to your princess.”", "\"그렇다면, 백작님, 백작님의 공주님께 저를 소개해 주십시오.\""]], [["“I will do so; but on two conditions.”", "\"그러지. 하지만 두 가지 조건이 있네.\""]], [["“I accept them at once.”", "\"당장 받아들이겠습니다.\""]], [["“The first is, that you will never tell anyone that I have granted the interview.”", "\"첫째는, 내가 그 자리를 허락했다는 걸 누구에게도 절대 말하지 않는 것일세.\""]], [["“Very well,”", "\"좋습니다.\""], ["said Albert, extending his hand; “I swear I will not.”", "알베르가 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"그러지 않겠다고 맹세합니다.\""]], [["“The second is, that you will not tell her that your father ever served hers.”", "\"둘째는, 자네 아버지가 그녀의 아버지를 섬긴 적이 있다는 걸 그녀에게 말하지 않는 것이네.\""]], [["“I give you my oath that I will not.”", "\"그러지 않겠다고 맹세코 약속드립니다.\""]], [["“Enough, viscount; you will remember those two vows, will you not?", "\"됐네, 자작. 그 두 가지 맹세를 기억하겠지?"], ["But I know you to be a man of honor.”", "하지만 나는 자네가 명예를 아는 사람임을 알고 있네.\""]], [["The count again struck the gong.", "백작이 다시 징을 쳤다. 알리가 나타났다."], ["Ali reappeared.", "\"하이데에게 전하라.\""], ["“Tell Haydée,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “that I will take coffee with her, and give her to understand that I desire permission to present one of my friends to her.”", "\"내가 그녀와 커피를 들겠다고, 그리고 내 벗 한 사람을 그녀에게 소개하고 싶으니 허락을 청한다고 일러 두어라.\""]], [["Ali bowed and left the room.", "알리가 고개를 숙이고 방을 나갔다."]], [["“Now, understand me,”", "\"자, 내 말을 명심하게.\" 백작이 말했다."], ["said the count, “no direct questions, my dear Morcerf; if you wish to know anything, tell me, and I will ask her.”", "\"직접 묻는 일은 없도록 하게, 모르세르. 무언가 알고 싶거든 내게 말하게. 그러면 내가 그녀에게 물어보겠네.\""]], [["“Agreed.”", "\"알겠습니다.\""]], [["Ali reappeared for the third time, and drew back the tapestried hanging which concealed the door, to signify to his master and Albert that they were at liberty to pass on.", "알리가 세 번째로 나타나, 문을 가린 융단 휘장을 젖혀 주인과 알베르가 안으로 들어가도 좋다는 뜻을 알렸다."]], [["“Let us go in,”", "\"들어가세.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["Albert passed his hand through his hair, and curled his moustache, then, having satisfied himself as to his personal appearance, followed the count into the room, the latter having previously resumed his hat and gloves.", "알베르는 머리카락을 손으로 쓸어 넘기고 콧수염을 매만진 다음, 제 차림새에 만족하고 나서 백작을 따라 방으로 들어갔다. 백작은 앞서 모자와 장갑을 다시 갖춘 뒤였다."], ["Ali was stationed as a kind of advanced guard, and the door was kept by the three French attendants, commanded by Myrtho.", "알리는 일종의 전위 보초처럼 자리를 지켰고, 문은 미르토가 지휘하는 세 명의 프랑스인 시종이 지키고 있었다."]], [["Haydée was awaiting her visitors in the first room of her apartments, which was the drawing-room.", "하이데는 자기 거처의 첫 번째 방, 곧 응접실에서 방문객들을 기다리고 있었다."], ["Her large eyes were dilated with surprise and expectation, for it was the first time that any man, except Monte Cristo, had been accorded an entrance into her presence.", "그녀의 커다란 눈은 놀라움과 기대로 한껏 커져 있었다. 몬테크리스토를 제외한 어떤 남자가 그녀 앞에 들어오도록 허락된 것은 이번이 처음이었기 때문이다."], ["She was sitting on a sofa placed in an angle of the room, with her legs crossed under her in the Eastern fashion, and seemed to have made for herself, as it were, a kind of nest in the rich Indian silks which enveloped her.", "그녀는 방 한쪽 모서리에 놓인 소파에 앉아, 동방식으로 두 다리를 몸 아래로 포갠 채였다. 마치 자신을 감싼 화려한 인도산 비단 속에 일종의 둥지를 틀어 놓은 듯했다."], ["Near her was the instrument on which she had just been playing; it was elegantly fashioned, and worthy of its mistress.", "그녀 곁에는 방금 연주하던 악기가 놓여 있었는데, 우아하게 만들어진 것이 그 주인에게 어울렸다."], ["On perceiving Monte Cristo, she arose and welcomed him with a smile peculiar to herself, expressive at once of the most implicit obedience and also of the deepest love.", "몬테크리스토를 알아보자 그녀는 일어서서, 자기만의 독특한 미소로 그를 맞이했다. 더없는 순종과 더없이 깊은 사랑을 동시에 드러내는 미소였다."], ["Monte Cristo advanced towards her and extended his hand, which she as usual raised to her lips. 40064m", "몬테크리스토는 그녀에게 다가가 손을 내밀었고, 그녀는 늘 그렇듯 그 손을 들어 입술에 가져갔다."]], [["Albert had proceeded no farther than the door, where he remained rooted to the spot, being completely fascinated by the sight of such surpassing beauty, beheld as it was for the first time, and of which an inhabitant of more northern climes could form no adequate idea.", "알베르는 문 앞에서 더 나아가지 못한 채, 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 서 있었다. 처음 보는, 그토록 빼어난 아름다움에 완전히 매혹된 것이었다. 더 북쪽 지방의 사람으로서는 도저히 제대로 상상할 수 없는 아름다움이었다."]], [["“Whom do you bring?”", "\"누구를 데려오셨어요?\""], ["asked the young girl in Romaic, of Monte Cristo; “is it a friend, a brother, a simple acquaintance, or an enemy.”", "처녀가 로마이크어로 몬테크리스토에게 물었다. \"벗인가요, 형제인가요, 그저 아는 사이인가요, 아니면 적인가요?\""]], [["“A friend,”", "\"벗이란다.\""], ["said Monte Cristo in the same language.", "몬테크리스토가 같은 언어로 대답했다."]], [["“What is his name?”", "\"이름이 무엇인가요?\""]], [["“Count Albert; it is the same man whom I rescued from the hands of the banditti at Rome.”", "\"알베르 백작. 내가 로마에서 산적들의 손아귀에서 구해 낸 바로 그 사람이지.\""]], [["“In what language would you like me to converse with him?”", "\"어떤 언어로 그와 이야기를 나누면 좋을까요?\""]], [["Monte Cristo turned to Albert.", "몬테크리스토가 알베르를 돌아보았다."], ["“Do you know modern Greek,”", "\"현대 그리스어를 아나?\""], ["asked he.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“Alas! no,”", "\"아아! 모릅니다.\""], ["said Albert; “nor even ancient Greek, my dear count; never had Homer or Plato a more unworthy scholar than myself.”", "알베르가 말했다. \"고대 그리스어조차 모르지요, 백작님. 호메로스나 플라톤도 저보다 더 변변찮은 학생은 둔 적이 없을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["said Haydée, proving by her remark that she had quite understood Monte Cristo’s question and Albert’s answer, “then I will speak either in French or Italian, if my lord so wills it.”", "하이데가 말했다. 그 말로 보아 그녀는 몬테크리스토의 질문과 알베르의 대답을 완전히 알아들은 것이 분명했다. \"그렇다면 주인님이 그리 원하신다면, 저는 프랑스어나 이탈리아어로 말하겠어요.\""]], [["Monte Cristo reflected one instant.", "몬테크리스토가 잠시 생각에 잠겼다."], ["“You will speak in Italian,” said he.", "\"이탈리아어로 말하렴.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["Then, turning towards Albert,—“It is a pity you do not understand either ancient or modern Greek, both of which Haydée speaks so fluently; the poor child will be obliged to talk to you in Italian, which will give you but a very false idea of her powers of conversation.”", "그러고는 알베르를 돌아보며 말했다. \"안됐네. 자네가 고대 그리스어도 현대 그리스어도 모르다니. 하이데는 둘 다 아주 유창하게 구사하거든. 가엾게도 저 아이는 자네에게 이탈리아어로 말해야 할 텐데, 그러면 자네는 그 애의 대화 솜씨에 대해 아주 잘못된 인상만 갖게 될 걸세.\""]], [["The count made a sign to Haydée to address his visitor. “Sir,”", "백작이 하이데에게 손님에게 말을 걸라는 신호를 보냈다. \"선생님,\""], ["she said to Morcerf, “you are most welcome as the friend of my lord and master.”", "그녀가 모르세르에게 말했다. \"제 주인이신 분의 벗으로서 진심으로 환영합니다.\""], ["This was said in excellent Tuscan, and with that soft Roman accent which makes the language of Dante as sonorous as that of Homer.", "이 말은 더없이 훌륭한 토스카나어로, 단테의 언어를 호메로스의 언어만큼이나 낭랑하게 울리게 하는 그 부드러운 로마식 억양으로 전해졌다."], ["Then, turning to Ali, she directed him to bring coffee and pipes, and when he had left the room to execute the orders of his young mistress she beckoned Albert to approach nearer to her.", "그러고는 알리를 돌아보며 커피와 파이프를 가져오라고 일렀고, 그가 어린 여주인의 명을 따르러 방을 나가자 알베르에게 더 가까이 오라고 손짓했다."], ["Monte Cristo and Morcerf drew their seats towards a small table, on which were arranged music, drawings, and vases of flowers.", "몬테크리스토와 모르세르는 악보와 그림과 꽃병들이 놓인 작은 탁자 쪽으로 의자를 끌어당겼다."], ["Ali then entered bringing coffee and chibouques; as to M.", "이윽고 알리가 커피와 치부크를 들고 들어왔다."], ["Baptistin, this portion of the building was interdicted to him.", "바티스탱 씨로 말하자면, 건물의 이 구역은 그에게 출입이 금지되어 있었다."], ["Albert refused the pipe which the Nubian offered him.", "알베르는 누비아인이 건네는 파이프를 사양했다."]], [["“Oh, take it—take it,”", "\"오, 받게나, 받아.\" 백작이 말했다."], ["said the count; “Haydée is almost as civilized as a Parisian; the smell of a Havana is disagreeable to her, but the tobacco of the East is a most delicious perfume, you know.”", "\"하이데는 거의 파리 사람만큼이나 문명화되어 있다네. 하바나 담배 냄새는 그 애에게 거슬리지만, 동방의 담배는 더없이 향긋한 향이거든. 알지 않나.\""]], [["Ali left the room.", "알리가 방을 나갔다."], ["The cups of coffee were all prepared, with the addition of sugar, which had been brought for Albert.", "커피잔은 모두 준비되어 있었고, 알베르를 위해 따로 가져온 설탕도 곁들여졌다."], ["Monte Cristo and Haydée took the beverage in the original Arabian manner, that is to say, without sugar.", "몬테크리스토와 하이데는 본래의 아라비아식대로, 곧 설탕 없이 그 음료를 들었다."], ["Haydée took the porcelain cup in her little slender fingers and conveyed it to her mouth with all the innocent artlessness of a child when eating or drinking something which it likes.", "하이데는 가느다란 작은 손가락으로 도자기 잔을 들어 입으로 가져갔는데, 좋아하는 것을 먹거나 마시는 아이처럼 천진하고 꾸밈없는 모습이었다."], ["At this moment two women entered, bringing salvers filled with ices and sherbet, which they placed on two small tables appropriated to that purpose.", "이 순간 두 여인이 들어와, 얼음과 셔벗이 가득 담긴 쟁반을 들고 와서 그 용도로 마련된 작은 탁자 두 개에 내려놓았다."]], [["“My dear host, and you, signora,”", "\"친애하는 주인님, 그리고 부인,\""], ["said Albert, in Italian, “excuse my apparent stupidity.", "알베르가 이탈리아어로 말했다. \"겉으로 드러나는 제 어리석음을 용서해 주십시오."], ["I am quite bewildered, and it is natural that it should be so.", "저는 완전히 얼이 빠졌는데, 그도 그럴 만합니다."], ["Here I am in the heart of Paris; but a moment ago I heard the rumbling of the omnibuses and the tinkling of the bells of the lemonade-sellers, and now I feel as if I were suddenly transported to the East; not such as I have seen it, but such as my dreams have painted it.", "저는 지금 파리 한복판에 있습니다. 불과 조금 전만 해도 합승마차가 덜컹거리는 소리와 레모네이드 장수들의 방울이 딸랑거리는 소리를 들었는데, 지금은 마치 갑자기 동방으로 옮겨진 듯합니다. 제가 직접 본 동방이 아니라, 제 꿈이 그려 낸 동방으로 말이지요."], ["Oh, signora, if I could but speak Greek, your conversation, added to the fairy-scene which surrounds me, would furnish an evening of such delight as it would be impossible for me ever to forget.”", "오, 부인, 제가 그리스어를 할 수만 있었다면, 부인의 대화에 저를 둘러싼 이 동화 같은 풍경이 더해져, 결코 잊을 수 없을 만큼 황홀한 저녁을 선사했을 겁니다.\""]], [["“I speak sufficient Italian to enable me to converse with you, sir,”", "\"저는 선생님과 대화를 나눌 만큼은 이탈리아어를 합니다.\""], ["said Haydée quietly; “and if you like what is Eastern, I will do my best to secure the gratification of your tastes while you are here.”", "하이데가 조용히 말했다. \"그리고 동방의 것을 좋아하신다면, 여기 계시는 동안 그 취향을 채워 드리도록 최선을 다하겠습니다.\""]], [["“On what subject shall I converse with her?”", "\"그녀와 무슨 주제로 이야기를 나누면 좋을까요?\""], ["said Albert, in a low tone to Monte Cristo.", "알베르가 낮은 목소리로 몬테크리스토에게 말했다."]], [["“Just what you please; you may speak of her country and of her youthful reminiscences, or if you like it better you can talk of Rome, Naples, or Florence.”", "\"무엇이든 좋을 대로 하게. 그녀의 나라나 어린 시절의 추억을 이야기해도 좋고, 더 마음에 든다면 로마나 나폴리, 피렌체 이야기를 해도 되고.\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오,\""], ["said Albert, “it is of no use to be in the company of a Greek if one converses just in the same style as with a Parisian; let me speak to her of the East.”", "알베르가 말했다. \"그리스 여인과 함께 있으면서 파리 사람과 똑같은 식으로 이야기를 나누는 건 아무 소용이 없지요. 그녀에게 동방 이야기를 하게 해 주십시오.\""]], [["“Do so then, for of all themes which you could choose that will be the most agreeable to her taste.”", "\"그러게나. 자네가 고를 수 있는 모든 주제 중에서 그게 그녀의 취향에 가장 맞을 테니.\""]], [["Albert turned towards Haydée.", "알베르가 하이데를 돌아보았다."], ["“At what age did you leave Greece, signora?” asked he.", "\"부인, 몇 살에 그리스를 떠나셨습니까?\" 그가 물었다."]], [["“I left it when I was but five years old,” replied Haydée.", "\"겨우 다섯 살에 떠났어요.\" 하이데가 대답했다."]], [["“And have you any recollection of your country?”", "\"그럼 고향에 대한 기억이 좀 있으신지요?\""]], [["“When I shut my eyes and think, I seem to see it all again.", "\"눈을 감고 생각하면, 그 모든 것이 다시 보이는 것 같아요."], ["The mind can see as well as the body.", "마음은 몸만큼이나 잘 볼 수 있거든요."], ["The body forgets sometimes; but the mind always remembers.”", "몸은 때때로 잊지만, 마음은 언제나 기억하니까요.\""]], [["“And how far back into the past do your recollections extend?”", "\"그럼 과거의 어디까지 기억이 미치십니까?\""]], [["“I could scarcely walk when my mother, who was called Vasiliki, which means royal,” said the young girl, tossing her head proudly, “took me by the hand, and after putting in our purse all the money we possessed, we went out, both covered with veils, to solicit alms for the prisoners, saying, ‘He who giveth to the poor lendeth to the Lord.’ Then when our purse was full we returned to the palace, and without saying a word to my father, we sent it to the convent, where it was divided amongst the prisoners.”", "\"제가 겨우 걸음마를 하던 때예요. 어머니는 — 왕족이라는 뜻을 지닌 바실리키라는 이름이셨지요.\" 처녀가 자랑스럽게 고개를 쳐들며 말했다. \"어머니가 제 손을 잡으시고, 우리가 가진 돈을 모두 지갑에 넣은 다음, 둘 다 베일을 쓰고 나가서 죄수들을 위해 적선을 청하셨어요. '가난한 자에게 베푸는 것은 주께 꾸어 드리는 것이니라'라고 말씀하시면서요. 그러다 지갑이 가득 차면 우리는 궁으로 돌아왔고, 아버지께는 한마디도 하지 않고 그 돈을 수도원으로 보냈어요. 거기서 죄수들에게 나누어 주었지요.\""]], [["“And how old were you at that time?”", "\"그때 몇 살이셨습니까?\""]], [["“I was three years old,” said Haydée.", "\"세 살이었어요.\" 하이데가 말했다."]], [["“Then you remember everything that went on about you from the time when you were three years old?” said Albert.", "\"그럼 세 살 때부터 주변에서 일어난 모든 일을 기억하신단 말입니까?\" 알베르가 말했다."]], [["“Everything.”", "\"전부 다요.\""]], [["“Count,”", "\"백작님,\""], ["said Albert, in a low tone to Monte Cristo, “do allow the signora to tell me something of her history.", "알베르가 낮은 목소리로 몬테크리스토에게 말했다. \"부디 부인이 자신의 내력을 좀 들려주게 해 주십시오."], ["You prohibited my mentioning my father’s name to her, but perhaps she will allude to him of her own accord in the course of the recital, and you have no idea how delighted I should be to hear our name pronounced by such beautiful lips.”", "그녀 앞에서 제 아버지 이름을 입에 올리는 걸 금하셨지만, 어쩌면 이야기를 하다가 그녀 스스로 아버지를 언급할지도 모릅니다. 그토록 아름다운 입술로 우리 가문의 이름이 불리는 걸 듣는다면 제가 얼마나 기쁠지, 백작님은 모르실 겁니다.\""]], [["Monte Cristo turned to Haydée, and with an expression of countenance which commanded her to pay the most implicit attention to his words, he said in Greek, “Πατρὸς μὲν ἄτην μήζε τὸ ὄνομα προδότου καὶ προδοσίαν εἰπὲ ἡμῖν,”—that is, “Tell us the fate of your father; but neither the name of the traitor nor the treason.”", "몬테크리스토가 하이데를 돌아보았다. 그리고 자기 말에 더없이 빈틈없이 주의를 기울이라고 명하는 표정으로, 그리스어로 말했다. \"Πατρὸς μὲν ἄτην μήζε τὸ ὄνομα προδότου καὶ προδοσίαν εἰπὲ ἡμῖν.\""], ["Haydée sighed deeply, and a shade of sadness clouded her beautiful brow.", "곧 \"네 아버지의 운명을 우리에게 들려다오. 다만 배신자의 이름도, 그 배신도 말하지 말아라\"라는 뜻이었다. 하이데가 깊은 한숨을 쉬었고, 그녀의 아름다운 이마에 슬픔의 그늘이 드리웠다."]], [["“What are you saying to her?”", "\"그녀에게 무슨 말씀을 하셨습니까?\""], ["said Morcerf in an undertone.", "모르세르가 나직이 물었다."]], [["“I again reminded her that you were a friend, and that she need not conceal anything from you.”", "\"자네가 벗이니 자네에게는 아무것도 숨길 필요가 없다고 다시 일러 주었네.\""]], [["“Then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["said Albert, “this pious pilgrimage in behalf of the prisoners was your first remembrance; what is the next?”", "알베르가 말했다. \"죄수들을 위한 그 경건한 순례가 부인의 첫 기억이로군요. 그다음은 무엇입니까?\""]], [["“Oh, then I remember as if it were but yesterday sitting under the shade of some sycamore-trees, on the borders of a lake, in the waters of which the trembling foliage was reflected as in a mirror.", "\"오, 그러고 나서 떠오르는 건 마치 어제 일처럼 또렷한데요, 호숫가에서 무화과나무 그늘 아래 앉아 있던 일이에요. 호수 물 위로는 흔들리는 잎새들이 거울처럼 비쳤지요."], ["Under the oldest and thickest of these trees, reclining on cushions, sat my father; my mother was at his feet, and I, childlike, amused myself by playing with his long white beard which descended to his girdle, or with the diamond-hilt of the scimitar attached to his girdle.", "그 나무들 중에서도 가장 늙고 가장 우거진 나무 아래, 쿠션에 기대어 아버지가 앉아 계셨어요. 어머니는 그 발치에 계셨고, 저는 아이답게 허리띠까지 늘어진 아버지의 길고 흰 수염을 가지고 놀거나, 허리띠에 매단 언월도의 다이아몬드 손잡이를 가지고 놀며 시간을 보냈지요."], ["Then from time to time there came to him an Albanian who said something to which I paid no attention, but which he always answered in the same tone of voice, either ‘Kill,’ or ‘Pardon.’”", "그러다 이따금 알바니아 사람 하나가 아버지께 와서 무어라 말을 했는데, 저는 귀담아듣지 않았지만 아버지는 늘 같은 어조로 '죽여라' 아니면 '용서하라'라고 대답하셨어요.\""]], [["“It is very strange,”", "\"참으로 이상한 일입니다.\""], ["said Albert, “to hear such words proceed from the mouth of anyone but an actress on the stage, and one needs constantly to be saying to one’s self, ‘This is no fiction, it is all reality,’ in order to believe it.", "알베르가 말했다. \"무대 위 여배우가 아닌 다른 누군가의 입에서 그런 말이 나오는 걸 들으니 말입니다. 그래서 그게 사실임을 믿으려면 끊임없이 스스로에게 되뇌어야 하는군요."], ["And how does France appear in your eyes, accustomed as they have been to gaze on such enchanted scenes?”", "'이건 꾸며 낸 이야기가 아니다, 전부 현실이다'라고요. 그런데 그토록 황홀한 풍경을 보는 데 익숙한 부인의 눈에 프랑스는 어떻게 비치는지요?\""]], [["“I think it is a fine country,”", "\"좋은 나라라고 생각해요.\" 하이데가 말했다. \"하지만 저는 프랑스를 있는 그대로 보지요."], ["said Haydée, “but I see France as it really is, because I look on it with the eyes of a woman; whereas my own country, which I can only judge of from the impression produced on my childish mind, always seems enveloped in a vague atmosphere, which is luminous or otherwise, according as my remembrances of it are sad or joyous.”", "한 여인의 눈으로 바라보니까요. 반면 제 고향은 어린아이의 마음에 남은 인상으로밖에 판단할 수 없으니, 늘 어렴풋한 안개에 싸여 있는 것처럼 보여요. 그에 대한 제 기억이 슬프냐 즐겁냐에 따라 그 안개는 빛나기도 하고 그렇지 않기도 하지요.\""]], [["“So young,”", "\"그토록 어린 나이에,\""], ["said Albert, forgetting at the moment the Count’s command that he should ask no questions of the slave herself, “is it possible that you can have known what suffering is except by name?”", "알베르가, 노예 본인에게는 어떤 질문도 하지 말라던 백작의 분부를 그 순간 잊은 채 말했다. \"고통이라는 게 무엇인지 이름으로밖에 알지 못하실 줄 알았는데, 그렇지 않으셨다는 말씀입니까?\""]], [["Haydée turned her eyes towards Monte Cristo, who, making at the same time some imperceptible sign, murmured:", "하이데가 몬테크리스토 쪽으로 눈길을 돌렸다. 그는 동시에 알아챌 수 없는 신호를 보내며 나직이 말했다."]], [["“Εἰπέ—speak.”", "\"Εἰπέ — 말하렴.\""]], [["“Nothing is ever so firmly impressed on the mind as the memory of our early childhood, and with the exception of the two scenes I have just described to you, all my earliest reminiscences are fraught with deepest sadness.”", "\"우리 마음에 어린 시절의 기억만큼 단단히 새겨지는 것은 없어요. 그리고 방금 제가 들려드린 두 장면을 빼면, 가장 이른 제 기억들은 모두 더없이 깊은 슬픔으로 가득 차 있답니다.\""]], [["40068m\n“Speak, speak, signora,”", "\"말씀하십시오, 말씀하세요, 부인.\""], ["said Albert, “I am listening with the most intense delight and interest to all you say.”", "알베르가 말했다. \"저는 부인이 하시는 모든 말씀을 더없이 강렬한 기쁨과 관심으로 듣고 있습니다.\""]], [["Haydée answered his remark with a melancholy smile.", "하이데는 그의 말에 우울한 미소로 답했다."], ["“You wish me, then, to relate the history of my past sorrows?” said she.", "\"그렇다면 지난날 제 슬픔의 내력을 들려드리길 바라시나요?\" 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“I beg you to do so,”", "\"부디 그래 주십시오.\""], ["replied Albert.", "알베르가 대답했다."]], [["“Well, I was but four years old when one night I was suddenly awakened by my mother.", "\"그래요, 제가 겨우 네 살이던 어느 날 밤, 저는 어머니에 의해 느닷없이 잠에서 깼어요."], ["We were in the palace of Yanina; she snatched me from the cushions on which I was sleeping, and on opening my eyes I saw hers filled with tears.", "우리는 야니나의 궁에 있었지요. 어머니는 제가 잠들어 있던 쿠션에서 저를 낚아채셨고, 제가 눈을 떠 보니 어머니의 눈에 눈물이 가득했어요."], ["She took me away without speaking.", "어머니는 아무 말 없이 저를 데려가셨지요."], ["When I saw her weeping I began to cry too. ‘Hush, child!’ said she.", "어머니가 우시는 걸 보고 저도 따라 울기 시작했어요. '쉿, 얘야!'"], ["At other times in spite of maternal endearments or threats, I had with a child’s caprice been accustomed to indulge my feelings of sorrow or anger by crying as much as I felt inclined; but on this occasion there was an intonation of such extreme terror in my mother’s voice when she enjoined me to silence, that I ceased crying as soon as her command was given.", "어머니가 말씀하셨어요. 다른 때 같으면 저는 어머니가 아무리 어르거나 으르거나 해도, 아이답게 변덕을 부리며 슬프거나 화나는 마음을 실컷 울어서 풀곤 했어요. 그런데 그날은 어머니가 조용히 하라고 이르시는 목소리에 어찌나 극심한 공포가 배어 있었는지, 어머니의 명령이 떨어지자마자 저는 울음을 그쳤어요."], ["She bore me rapidly away.", "어머니는 저를 안고 서둘러 데려가셨지요."]], [["“I saw then that we were descending a large staircase; around us were all my mother’s servants carrying trunks, bags, ornaments, jewels, purses of gold, with which they were hurrying away in the greatest distraction.", "그때 보니 우리는 커다란 계단을 내려가고 있었어요. 우리 주위에는 어머니의 하인들이 모두 짐궤와 자루와 장신구와 보석과 금화 지갑을 나르며, 더없이 정신없이 서둘러 달아나고 있었지요."]], [["“Behind the women came a guard of twenty men armed with long guns and pistols, and dressed in the costume which the Greeks have assumed since they have again become a nation.", "여인들 뒤로는 긴 총과 권총으로 무장하고, 그리스인들이 다시 한 민족이 된 이래로 입게 된 복장을 한 스무 명의 호위병이 따랐어요."], ["You may imagine there was something startling and ominous,” said Haydée, shaking her head and turning pale at the mere remembrance of the scene, “in this long file of slaves and women only half-aroused from sleep, or at least so they appeared to me, who was myself scarcely awake.", "짐작이 가시겠지만, 잠에서 절반밖에 깨지 못한, 아니 적어도 제게는 그렇게 보였던 — 저 자신도 채 깨지 못했으니까요 — 노예와 여인들의 그 긴 행렬에는 무언가 섬뜩하고 불길한 데가 있었어요.\" 하이데가 그 장면을 떠올리는 것만으로도 고개를 젓고 창백해지며 말했다."], ["Here and there on the walls of the staircase, were reflected gigantic shadows, which trembled in the flickering light of the pine-torches till they seemed to reach to the vaulted roof above.", "\"계단 벽 여기저기에는 거대한 그림자들이 비쳐, 소나무 횃불의 일렁이는 불빛 속에서 떨리며 마침내 머리 위 둥근 천장에까지 닿는 듯했어요."]], [["“‘Quick!’ said a voice at the end of the gallery.", "'서둘러라!' 회랑 끝에서 한 목소리가 외쳤어요."], ["This voice made everyone bow before it, resembling in its effect the wind passing over a field of wheat, by its superior strength forcing every ear to yield obeisance. As for me, it made me tremble.", "이 목소리에 모두가 그 앞에 머리를 조아렸는데, 그 위력이란 마치 밀밭 위를 지나는 바람이 그 우월한 힘으로 모든 이삭을 굴복시켜 절하게 하는 것 같았지요. 저로 말하면, 그 목소리에 몸이 떨렸어요."], ["This voice was that of my father.", "그것은 제 아버지의 목소리였어요."], ["He came last, clothed in his splendid robes and holding in his hand the carbine which your emperor presented him.", "아버지는 화려한 예복을 입고, 손에는 당신네 황제가 하사한 카빈총을 든 채 맨 마지막에 오셨어요."], ["He was leaning on the shoulder of his favorite Selim, and he drove us all before him, as a shepherd would his straggling flock.", "총애하던 셀림의 어깨에 기대신 채, 목동이 흩어진 양 떼를 몰듯 우리 모두를 앞세워 모셨지요. 제 아버지는,\""], ["My father,” said Haydée, raising her head, “was that illustrious man known in Europe under the name of Ali Tepelini, pasha of Yanina, and before whom Turkey trembled.”", "하이데가 고개를 쳐들며 말했다. \"유럽에 야니나의 파샤 알리 테펠레니라는 이름으로 알려진, 터키마저 그 앞에서 벌벌 떨던 그 이름난 분이셨어요.\""]], [["Albert, without knowing why, started on hearing these words pronounced with such a haughty and dignified accent; it appeared to him as if there was something supernaturally gloomy and terrible in the expression which gleamed from the brilliant eyes of Haydée at this moment; she appeared like a Pythoness evoking a spectre, as she recalled to his mind the remembrance of the fearful death of this man, to the news of which all Europe had listened with horror.", "알베르는 영문도 모른 채, 그토록 거만하고 위엄 있는 어조로 발해진 이 말을 듣고 흠칫했다. 그에게는 이 순간 하이데의 빛나는 두 눈에서 번뜩이는 표정에 무언가 초자연적으로 음울하고 무시무시한 것이 깃든 듯이 보였다. 온 유럽이 공포에 떨며 들었던 그 사내의 끔찍한 죽음에 대한 기억을 알베르의 마음속에 불러일으키는 그녀는, 마치 망령을 불러내는 무녀와도 같았다."]], [["“Soon,”", "\"이윽고,\""], ["said Haydée, “we halted on our march, and found ourselves on the borders of a lake.", "하이데가 말했다. \"우리는 가던 길에서 멈춰, 호숫가에 다다랐어요."], ["My mother pressed me to her throbbing heart, and at the distance of a few paces I saw my father, who was glancing anxiously around.", "어머니는 두근거리는 가슴에 저를 끌어안으셨고, 몇 걸음 떨어진 곳에서 저는 불안하게 주위를 살피는 아버지를 보았지요."], ["Four marble steps led down to the water’s edge, and below them was a boat floating on the tide.", "대리석 계단 네 개가 물가로 내려가 있었고, 그 아래에는 물결 위로 배 한 척이 떠 있었어요."]], [["40070m\n“From where we stood I could see in the middle of the lake a large blank mass; it was the kiosk to which we were going.", "우리가 선 곳에서 저는 호수 한가운데 커다란 어둑한 덩어리가 보였어요. 그건 우리가 가려는 정자였지요."], ["This kiosk appeared to me to be at a considerable distance, perhaps on account of the darkness of the night, which prevented any object from being more than partially discerned.", "그 정자는 제게 꽤 멀리 떨어진 듯 보였는데, 아마도 어떤 사물이든 일부밖에 분간할 수 없게 하던 밤의 어둠 탓이었을 거예요."], ["We stepped into the boat.", "우리는 배에 올랐어요."], ["I remember well that the oars made no noise whatever in striking the water, and when I leaned over to ascertain the cause I saw that they were muffled with the sashes of our Palikares.14 Besides the rowers, the boat contained only the women, my father, mother, Selim, and myself.", "노가 물을 칠 때 아무런 소리도 나지 않던 게 똑똑히 기억나요. 까닭을 알아보려고 몸을 기울여 보니, 노에 우리 팔리카르들의 허리띠가 둘둘 감겨 있더군요. 노 젓는 이들 말고 배에는 여인들과 아버지, 어머니, 셀림, 그리고 저뿐이었어요."], ["The Palikares had remained on the shore of the lake, ready to cover our retreat; they were kneeling on the lowest of the marble steps, and in that manner intended making a rampart of the three others, in case of pursuit.", "팔리카르들은 우리의 퇴로를 엄호할 채비를 하고 호숫가에 남아 있었지요. 그들은 대리석 계단의 맨 아래 단에 무릎을 꿇고, 추격이 있을 경우 나머지 세 단을 방벽 삼으려는 것이었어요. 우리 배는 바람보다 빠르게 나아갔어요."], ["Our bark flew before the wind. ‘Why does the boat go so fast?’ asked I of my mother.", "'왜 배가 이렇게 빨리 가요?' 제가 어머니께 여쭈었어요."]], [["“‘Silence, child!", "'조용히 해, 얘야!"], ["Hush, we are flying!’ I did not understand.", "쉿, 우리는 달아나는 중이야!' 저는 이해할 수가 없었어요."], ["Why should my father fly?—he, the all-powerful—he, before whom others were accustomed to fly—he, who had taken for his device,\n‘They hate me; then they fear me!’", "어째서 아버지가 달아나야 하지? 모든 것을 거머쥔 분, 다른 이들이 그 앞에서 으레 달아나던 분, '저들이 나를 미워한다, 그러니 나를 두려워한다!'를 좌우명으로 삼으신 그분이 말이에요."]], [["“It was, indeed, a flight which my father was trying to effect.", "정말로 아버지가 이루려던 것은 도주였어요."], ["I have been told since that the garrison of the castle of Yanina, fatigued with long service——”", "나중에 들은 이야기로는, 야니나성의 수비대가 오랜 복무에 지쳐서……\""]], [["Here Haydée cast a significant glance at Monte Cristo, whose eyes had been riveted on her countenance during the whole course of her narrative.", "여기서 하이데가 몬테크리스토에게 의미심장한 눈길을 던졌다. 백작의 눈은 그녀의 이야기가 이어지는 내내 그녀의 얼굴에 못 박혀 있었다."], ["The young girl then continued, speaking slowly, like a person who is either inventing or suppressing some feature of the history which he is relating.", "이윽고 처녀는, 들려주는 이야기의 어떤 대목을 지어내거나 감추는 사람처럼 천천히 말을 이었다."]], [["“You were saying, signora,”", "\"방금 말씀하시기를, 부인,\""], ["said Albert, who was paying the most implicit attention to the recital, “that the garrison of Yanina, fatigued with long service——”", "알베르가, 더없이 빈틈없이 이야기에 귀 기울이며 말했다. \"야니나의 수비대가 오랜 복무에 지쳐서……\""]], [["“Had treated with the Seraskier15 Kourchid, who had been sent by the sultan to gain possession of the person of my father; it was then that Ali Tepelini—after having sent to the sultan a French officer in whom he reposed great confidence—resolved to retire to the asylum which he had long before prepared for himself, and which he called _kataphygion_, or the refuge.”", "\"술탄이 제 아버지의 신병을 확보하라고 보낸 세라스키에르 쿠르시드와 협상을 맺었어요. 바로 그때 알리 테펠레니는 — 자신이 크게 신뢰하던 프랑스 장교 한 사람을 술탄에게 보낸 뒤 — 오래전부터 자신을 위해 마련해 둔 은신처로 물러나기로 결심하셨어요. 그곳을 카타피기온, 곧 피난처라고 부르셨지요.\""]], [["“And this officer,”", "\"그 장교 말입니다만,\""], ["asked Albert, “do you remember his name, signora?”", "알베르가 물었다. \"그 이름을 기억하십니까, 부인?\""]], [["Monte Cristo exchanged a rapid glance with the young girl, which was quite unperceived by Albert.", "몬테크리스토가 처녀와 재빠른 눈길을 주고받았는데, 알베르는 전혀 알아채지 못했다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니요,\""], ["said she, “I do not remember it just at this moment; but if it should occur to me presently, I will tell you.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"지금 당장은 기억나지 않아요. 하지만 곧 떠오르면 말씀드릴게요.\""]], [["Albert was on the point of pronouncing his father’s name, when Monte Cristo gently held up his finger in token of reproach; the young man recollected his promise, and was silent.", "알베르가 막 제 아버지의 이름을 입에 올리려는 찰나, 몬테크리스토가 나무라는 뜻으로 손가락을 가만히 들어 올렸다. 젊은이는 자기 약속을 떠올리고 입을 다물었다."]], [["“It was towards this kiosk that we were rowing.", "\"우리가 노 저어 가던 곳이 바로 그 정자였어요."], ["A ground floor, ornamented with arabesques, bathing its terraces in the water, and another floor, looking on the lake, was all which was visible to the eye.", "아라베스크 무늬로 장식되고 테라스가 물에 잠겨 있는 일층과, 호수를 바라보는 또 한 층, 그게 눈에 보이는 전부였지요."], ["But beneath the ground floor, stretching out into the island, was a large subterranean cavern, to which my mother, myself, and the women were conducted.", "하지만 일층 아래, 섬 안쪽으로 뻗어 있는 곳에는 커다란 지하 동굴이 있었고, 어머니와 저와 여인들이 그리로 인도되었어요."], ["In this place were together 60,000 pouches and 200 barrels; the pouches contained 25,000,000 of money in gold, and the barrels were filled with 30,000 pounds of gunpowder.", "그곳에는 가죽 자루 육만 개와 통 이백 개가 함께 놓여 있었어요. 자루에는 금화로 이천오백만 냥이 들어 있었고, 통에는 화약 삼만 파운드가 가득 차 있었지요."]], [["“Near the barrels stood Selim, my father’s favorite, whom I mentioned to you just now.", "그 통들 곁에는 제가 방금 말씀드린, 아버지가 총애하던 셀림이 서 있었어요."], ["He stood watch day and night with a lance provided with a lighted slowmatch in his hand, and he had orders to blow up everything—kiosk, guards, women, gold, and Ali Tepelini himself—at the first signal given by my father.", "그는 불붙인 화승을 매단 창을 손에 들고 밤낮으로 보초를 섰지요. 아버지의 첫 신호가 떨어지면 모든 것을 — 정자, 호위병, 여인들, 금, 그리고 알리 테펠레니 자신까지 — 폭파하라는 명을 받은 거였어요."], ["I remember well that the slaves, convinced of the precarious tenure on which they held their lives, passed whole days and nights in praying, crying, and groaning.", "노예들이, 제 목숨이 얼마나 위태로운 줄에 매달려 있는지 알고서 밤낮으로 기도하고 울고 신음하며 지내던 게 똑똑히 기억나요."], ["As for me, I can never forget the pale complexion and black eyes of the young soldier, and whenever the angel of death summons me to another world, I am quite sure I shall recognize Selim.", "저로 말하면, 그 젊은 병사의 창백한 얼굴빛과 검은 눈을 결코 잊을 수 없어요. 그리고 언젠가 죽음의 천사가 저를 저세상으로 부르더라도, 저는 분명 셀림을 알아볼 거예요."], ["I cannot tell you how long we remained in this state; at that period I did not even know what time meant.", "우리가 그 상태로 얼마나 오래 머물렀는지는 말씀드릴 수 없어요. 그 시절에는 시간이라는 게 무엇인지조차 몰랐으니까요."], ["Sometimes, but very rarely, my father summoned me and my mother to the terrace of the palace; these were hours of recreation for me, as I never saw anything in the dismal cavern but the gloomy countenances of the slaves and Selim’s fiery lance.", "이따금, 아주 드물게 아버지가 어머니와 저를 궁의 테라스로 부르셨어요. 그게 제게는 즐거운 시간이었지요. 음울한 동굴 속에서는 노예들의 침울한 얼굴과 셀림의 불타는 창 말고는 아무것도 볼 수 없었으니까요."], ["My father was endeavoring to pierce with his eager looks the remotest verge of the horizon, examining attentively every black speck which appeared on the lake, while my mother, reclining by his side, rested her head on his shoulder, and I played at his feet, admiring everything I saw with that unsophisticated innocence of childhood which throws a charm round objects insignificant in themselves, but which in its eyes are invested with the greatest importance.", "아버지는 간절한 눈길로 지평선의 가장 먼 끝까지 꿰뚫어 보려 하셨고, 호수 위에 나타나는 검은 점 하나하나를 유심히 살피셨어요. 그동안 어머니는 그 곁에 기대어 아버지 어깨에 머리를 얹으셨고, 저는 그 발치에서 놀며 보이는 모든 것에 감탄했지요. 그 자체로는 보잘것없으나 아이의 눈에는 더없이 중대하게 비치는 것들에 매혹을 입히는, 그 꾸밈없는 어린 시절의 천진함으로 말이에요."], ["The heights of Pindus towered above us; the castle of Yanina rose white and angular from the blue waters of the lake, and the immense masses of black vegetation which, viewed in the distance, gave the idea of lichens clinging to the rocks, were in reality gigantic fir-trees and myrtles.", "핀두스산의 봉우리들이 우리 위로 우뚝 솟아 있었고, 야니나성은 호수의 푸른 물에서 희고 모난 모습으로 솟아 있었어요. 멀리서 보면 바위에 들러붙은 이끼처럼 보이던 그 검은 초목의 거대한 덩어리들은, 실은 거대한 전나무와 도금양 나무였지요."]], [["“One morning my father sent for us; my mother had been crying all the night, and was very wretched; we found the pasha calm, but paler than usual.", "어느 날 아침 아버지가 우리를 부르셨어요. 어머니는 밤새 우셨고 몹시 비참한 모습이셨지요. 파샤이신 아버지는 차분하셨지만 여느 때보다 창백하셨어요."], ["‘Take courage, Vasiliki,’ said he; ‘today arrives the firman of the master, and my fate will be decided.", "'용기를 내거라, 바실리키.' 아버지가 말씀하셨어요. '오늘 주군의 칙서가 도착하면 내 운명이 결정될 거요."], ["If my pardon be complete, we shall return triumphant to Yanina; if the news be inauspicious, we must fly this night.’—‘But supposing our enemy should not allow us to do so?’ said my mother.", "내가 온전히 사면된다면 우리는 의기양양하게 야니나로 돌아갈 것이고, 소식이 불길하다면 우리는 오늘 밤 달아나야 하오.' — '하지만 적이 우리가 달아나도록 내버려두지 않으면요?' 어머니가 말씀하셨어요."], ["‘Oh, make yourself easy on that head,’ said Ali, smiling; ‘Selim and his flaming lance will settle that matter.", "'오, 그 점은 마음을 놓으시오.' 알리가 미소 지으며 말씀하셨어요. '셀림과 그의 불타는 창이 그 문제를 해결할 거요."], ["They would be glad to see me dead, but they would not like themselves to die with me.’", "저들은 내가 죽기를 바라겠지만, 자기들이 나와 함께 죽기는 싫을 테니까.'"]], [["“My mother only answered by sighs to consolations which she knew did not come from my father’s heart.", "어머니는 아버지의 진심에서 나온 게 아님을 아는 그 위안의 말에 한숨으로만 답하셨어요. 어머니는 아버지가 늘 마시던 얼음물을 준비하셨어요."], ["She prepared the iced water which he was in the habit of constantly drinking,—for since his sojourn at the kiosk he had been parched by the most violent fever,—after which she anointed his white beard with perfumed oil, and lighted his chibouque, which he sometimes smoked for hours together, quietly watching the wreaths of vapor that ascended in spiral clouds and gradually melted away in the surrounding atmosphere.", "정자에 머무신 뒤로 아버지는 극심한 열로 입이 바싹 타셨거든요. 그러고는 향유를 발라 흰 수염을 매만지시고, 치부크에 불을 붙여 드렸어요. 아버지는 때때로 그것을 몇 시간씩 피우며, 나선을 그리며 피어오르다가 주위 공기 속으로 차츰 녹아드는 연기 자락을 가만히 바라보곤 하셨지요."], ["Presently he made such a sudden movement that I was paralyzed with fear. Then, without taking his eyes from the object which had first attracted his attention, he asked for his telescope.", "그러다 아버지가 어찌나 갑작스럽게 몸을 움직이셨는지, 저는 겁에 질려 옴짝달싹할 수가 없었어요. 그러더니 처음 눈길을 끈 그것에서 눈을 떼지 않으신 채 망원경을 달라고 하셨어요."], ["My mother gave it him, and as she did so, looked whiter than the marble against which she leaned.", "어머니가 건네드렸는데, 그러면서 기대고 있던 대리석보다도 더 하얗게 질려 보였어요. 저는 아버지의 손이 떨리는 걸 보았지요. '배다! 두 척! 세 척!'"], ["I saw my father’s hand tremble.", "아버지가 나직이 중얼거리셨어요. '네 척!'"], ["‘A boat!—two!—three!’ murmured my, father;—‘four!’ He then arose, seizing his arms and priming his pistols.", "그러고는 일어서서 무기를 잡고 권총에 화약을 재셨어요. '바실리키,' 아버지가 어머니께, 눈에 띄게 떨면서 말씀하셨어요."], ["‘Vasiliki,’ said he to my mother, trembling perceptibly, ‘the instant approaches which will decide everything.", "'모든 것을 결정할 순간이 다가오고 있소. 반 시간 안에 우리는 황제의 답을 알게 될 거요. 하이데를 데리고 동굴로 가시오.'"], ["In the space of half an hour we shall know the emperor’s answer.", "— '저는 당신 곁을 떠나지 않겠어요.' 바실리키가 말씀하셨어요."], ["Go into the cavern with Haydée.’—‘I will not quit you,’ said Vasiliki; ‘if you die, my lord, I will die with you.’—‘Go to Selim!’ cried my father.", "'당신이 죽으면, 주인님, 저도 당신과 함께 죽겠어요.' — '셀림에게 가시오!' 아버지가 외치셨어요."], ["‘Adieu, my lord,’ murmured my mother, determining quietly to await the approach of death.", "'안녕히, 주인님.' 어머니가 죽음이 다가오기를 조용히 기다리기로 결심한 듯 나직이 말씀하셨어요."], ["‘Take away Vasiliki!’ said my father to his Palikares.", "'바실리키를 데려가라!' 아버지가 팔리카르들에게 말씀하셨어요."]], [["“As for me, I had been forgotten in the general confusion; I ran toward Ali Tepelini; he saw me hold out my arms to him, and he stooped down and pressed my forehead with his lips.", "저로 말하면, 그 난리통에 잊혀 있었어요. 저는 알리 테펠레니께로 달려갔지요. 아버지는 제가 그분께 두 팔을 내미는 걸 보시더니, 몸을 숙여 제 이마에 입을 맞추셨어요."], ["Oh, how distinctly I remember that kiss!—it was the last he ever gave me, and I feel as if it were still warm on my forehead.", "오, 그 입맞춤이 얼마나 또렷이 기억나는지요! 그건 아버지가 제게 주신 마지막 입맞춤이었고, 저는 그게 아직도 제 이마에 따뜻하게 남아 있는 것만 같아요."], ["On descending, we saw through the lattice-work several boats which were gradually becoming more distinct to our view.", "내려가면서 우리는 격자창 너머로 차츰 또렷해지는 배 여러 척을 보았어요."], ["At first they appeared like black specks, and now they looked like birds skimming the surface of the waves.", "처음에는 검은 점들 같더니, 이제는 물결 위를 스치는 새들처럼 보이더군요."], ["During this time, in the kiosk at my father’s feet, were seated twenty Palikares, concealed from view by an angle of the wall and watching with eager eyes the arrival of the boats.", "그동안 정자에서는 아버지 발치에 팔리카르 스무 명이, 벽 모서리에 가려진 채 간절한 눈으로 배들이 다가오는 걸 지켜보며 앉아 있었어요."], ["They were armed with their long guns inlaid with mother-of-pearl and silver, and cartridges in great numbers were lying scattered on the floor.", "그들은 자개와 은으로 상감된 긴 총으로 무장하고 있었고, 탄약통이 수없이 바닥에 흩어져 있었지요."], ["My father looked at his watch, and paced up and down with a countenance expressive of the greatest anguish.", "아버지는 시계를 보시며, 더없이 깊은 고뇌가 어린 얼굴로 이리저리 거니셨어요."], ["This was the scene which presented itself to my view as I quitted my father after that last kiss.", "그 마지막 입맞춤 뒤 아버지 곁을 떠날 때 제 눈에 비친 광경이 바로 이런 것이었어요."]], [["“My mother and I traversed the gloomy passage leading to the cavern. Selim was still at his post, and smiled sadly on us as we entered.", "어머니와 저는 동굴로 이어진 음울한 통로를 지났어요. 셀림은 여전히 제자리를 지키고 있었고, 우리가 들어서자 슬프게 미소 지었지요."], ["We fetched our cushions from the other end of the cavern, and sat down by Selim.", "우리는 동굴 저쪽 끝에서 쿠션을 가져다가 셀림 곁에 앉았어요."], ["In great dangers the devoted ones cling to each other; and, young as I was, I quite understood that some imminent danger was hanging over our heads.”", "큰 위험 앞에서는 헌신적인 사람들끼리 서로 매달리는 법이지요. 그리고 저는 비록 어렸지만, 어떤 절박한 위험이 우리 머리 위에 드리워 있다는 걸 똑똑히 알았어요.\""]], [["Albert had often heard—not from his father, for he never spoke on the subject, but from strangers—the description of the last moments of the vizier of Yanina; he had read different accounts of his death, but the story seemed to acquire fresh meaning from the voice and expression of the young girl, and her sympathetic accent and the melancholy expression of her countenance at once charmed and horrified him.", "알베르는 야니나 재상의 마지막 순간에 대한 묘사를 — 아버지에게서는 아니었다. 그는 그 일에 대해 결코 입을 열지 않았으니까. 다만 다른 사람들에게서 — 자주 들어 왔다. 그는 그의 죽음에 관한 여러 기록을 읽은 적도 있었다. 하지만 이 이야기는 처녀의 목소리와 표정을 통해 새로운 의미를 띠는 듯했고, 그녀의 가슴 저미는 어조와 얼굴에 어린 우울한 표정은 그를 동시에 매혹하고 또 전율케 했다."]], [["As to Haydée, these terrible reminiscences seemed to have overpowered her for a moment, for she ceased speaking, her head leaning on her hand like a beautiful flower bowing beneath the violence of the storm; and her eyes gazing on vacancy indicated that she was mentally contemplating the green summit of the Pindus and the blue waters of the lake of Yanina, which, like a magic mirror, seemed to reflect the sombre picture which she sketched.", "하이데로 말하면, 이 끔찍한 기억들이 그녀를 잠시 압도한 듯, 그녀는 말을 멈추었다. 폭풍의 거센 기세에 고개 숙인 아름다운 꽃처럼 머리를 손에 기댄 채, 허공을 응시하는 그녀의 두 눈은 그녀가 마음속으로 핀두스산의 푸른 봉우리와 야니나 호수의 푸른 물을 그리고 있음을 말해 주었다. 그것들은 마법의 거울처럼, 그녀가 그려 낸 음울한 그림을 비추는 듯했다."], ["Monte Cristo looked at her with an indescribable expression of interest and pity.", "몬테크리스토는 형언할 수 없는 관심과 연민의 표정으로 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["“Go on, my child,” said the count in the Romaic language.", "\"계속하렴, 얘야.\" 백작이 로마이크어로 말했다."]], [["40074m\nHaydée looked up abruptly, as if the sonorous tones of Monte Cristo’s voice had awakened her from a dream; and she resumed her narrative.", "하이데가, 몬테크리스토의 낭랑한 목소리가 자기를 꿈에서 깨우기라도 한 듯 불쑥 눈을 들었다. 그러고는 다시 이야기를 이어 갔다."]], [["“It was about four o’clock in the afternoon, and although the day was brilliant out-of-doors, we were enveloped in the gloomy darkness of the cavern.", "\"오후 네 시쯤이었어요. 바깥은 눈부신 대낮이었지만, 우리는 동굴의 음울한 어둠에 싸여 있었지요."], ["One single, solitary light was burning there, and it appeared like a star set in a heaven of blackness; it was Selim’s flaming lance.", "그곳에는 단 하나의 외로운 불빛만이 타고 있었는데, 어둠의 하늘에 박힌 별처럼 보였어요. 그건 셀림의 불타는 창이었지요."], ["My mother was a Christian, and she prayed.", "어머니는 그리스도교도셨고, 기도를 올리셨어요."], ["Selim repeated from time to time the sacred words: ‘God is great!’ However, my mother had still some hope.", "셀림은 이따금 거룩한 말을 되풀이했지요. '신은 위대하시다!' 그래도 어머니에게는 아직 한 가닥 희망이 있었어요."], ["As she was coming down, she thought she recognized the French officer who had been sent to Constantinople, and in whom my father placed so much confidence; for he knew that all the soldiers of the French emperor were naturally noble and generous.", "내려오시면서 어머니는, 콘스탄티노플로 보냈던 그 프랑스 장교를, 아버지가 그토록 신뢰하던 그 사람을 알아본 것 같다고 여기셨거든요. 아버지는 프랑스 황제의 병사들은 천성이 고귀하고 너그럽다는 걸 아셨으니까요."], ["She advanced some steps towards the staircase, and listened.", "어머니는 계단 쪽으로 몇 걸음 다가가 귀를 기울이셨어요."], ["‘They are approaching,’ said she; ‘perhaps they bring us peace and liberty!’", "'저들이 다가오고 있어.' 어머니가 말씀하셨어요. '어쩌면 우리에게 평화와 자유를 가져오는지도 몰라!'"]], [["“‘What do you fear, Vasiliki?’ said Selim, in a voice at once so gentle and yet so proud.", "'무엇이 두려우십니까, 바실리키?' 셀림이, 한없이 부드럽지만 또한 한없이 당당한 목소리로 말했어요. '저들이 우리에게 평화를 가져오지 않으면, 우리가 저들에게 전쟁을 안기겠습니다."], ["‘If they do not bring us peace, we will give them war; if they do not bring life, we will give them death.’ And he renewed the flame of his lance with a gesture which made one think of Dionysus of old Crete.16 But I, being only a little child, was terrified by this undaunted courage, which appeared to me both ferocious and senseless, and I recoiled with horror from the idea of the frightful death amidst fire and flames which probably awaited us.", "저들이 생명을 가져오지 않으면, 우리가 저들에게 죽음을 안기겠습니다.' 그러고는 옛 크레타의 디오니소스를 떠올리게 하는 몸짓으로 창의 불꽃을 다시 돋우었어요. 하지만 저는 그저 어린아이였던지라, 제게는 사납고 무모해 보이기만 하던 그 두려움 없는 용기에 겁을 먹었고, 어쩌면 우리를 기다릴지도 모를 불과 화염 속의 끔찍한 죽음을 떠올리며 진저리를 치며 물러섰지요."]], [["“My mother experienced the same sensations, for I felt her tremble. ‘Mamma, mamma,’ said I, ‘are we really to be killed?’ And at the sound of my voice the slaves redoubled their cries and prayers and lamentations. ‘My child,’ said Vasiliki, ‘may God preserve you from ever wishing for that death which today you so much dread!’ Then, whispering to Selim, she asked what were her master’s orders. ‘If he send me his poniard, it will signify that the emperor’s intentions are not favorable, and I am to set fire to the powder; if, on the contrary, he send me his ring, it will be a sign that the emperor pardons him, and I am to extinguish the match and leave the magazine untouched.’—‘My friend,’ said my mother, ‘when your master’s orders arrive, if it is the poniard which he sends, instead of despatching us by that horrible death which we both so much dread, you will mercifully kill us with this same poniard, will you not?’—‘Yes, Vasiliki,’ replied Selim tranquilly.", "어머니도 같은 마음이셨어요. 저는 어머니가 떠는 걸 느꼈으니까요. '엄마, 엄마,' 제가 말했어요. '우리 정말 죽는 거예요?' 그리고 제 목소리가 들리자 노예들은 울부짖음과 기도와 통곡을 더욱 거세게 쏟아 냈어요. '얘야,' 바실리키가 말씀하셨어요. '신께서 너를 지키시어, 네가 오늘 그토록 두려워하는 그 죽음을 언젠가 바라게 되는 일이 결코 없기를!' 그러고는 셀림에게 속삭이며, 주인의 명령이 무엇이냐고 물으셨어요. '주인께서 제게 단검을 보내시면, 그건 황제의 뜻이 호의적이지 않다는 뜻이고, 저는 화약에 불을 붙여야 합니다. 반대로 반지를 보내시면, 그건 황제가 그분을 사면했다는 표시이고, 저는 화승을 끄고 화약고는 손대지 않은 채 두어야 합니다.' — '셀림,' 어머니가 말씀하셨어요. '주인의 명령이 도착했을 때, 만일 보내신 게 단검이라면, 우리 둘 다 그토록 두려워하는 그 끔찍한 죽음으로 우리를 보내는 대신, 자비를 베풀어 바로 그 단검으로 우리를 죽여 주겠는가?' — '그러지요, 바실리키.' 셀림이 담담하게 대답했어요."]], [["“Suddenly we heard loud cries; and, listening, discerned that they were cries of joy.", "갑자기 우리는 요란한 외침을 들었어요. 그리고 귀를 기울여 보니, 그것이 기쁨의 외침임을 알았지요."], ["The name of the French officer who had been sent to Constantinople resounded on all sides amongst our Palikares; it was evident that he brought the answer of the emperor, and that it was favorable.”", "콘스탄티노플로 보냈던 그 프랑스 장교의 이름이 우리 팔리카르들 사이에서 사방으로 울려 퍼졌어요. 그가 황제의 답을 가져왔고, 그게 호의적이라는 게 분명했지요.\""]], [["“And do you not remember the Frenchman’s name?”", "\"그 프랑스인의 이름이 기억나지 않으십니까?\""], ["said Morcerf, quite ready to aid the memory of the narrator.", "모르세르가, 이야기하는 이의 기억을 거들어 줄 채비를 단단히 하고 말했다."], ["Monte Cristo made a sign to him to be silent.", "몬테크리스토가 그에게 잠자코 있으라는 신호를 보냈다."]], [["“I do not recollect it,” said Haydée.", "\"기억나지 않아요.\" 하이데가 말했다."]], [["“The noise increased; steps were heard approaching nearer and nearer; they were descending the steps leading to the cavern.", "\"소란이 커졌어요. 발걸음 소리가 점점 더 가까이 다가오는 게 들렸지요. 동굴로 이어진 계단을 내려오고 있었어요."], ["Selim made ready his lance.", "셀림이 창을 준비했어요."], ["Soon a figure appeared in the gray twilight at the entrance of the cave, formed by the reflection of the few rays of daylight which had found their way into this gloomy retreat.", "이내 잿빛 어스름 속, 동굴 입구에 한 형상이 나타났지요. 그 어스름은 이 음울한 은신처로 새어 든 몇 가닥 햇빛이 반사되어 생긴 거였어요."], ["‘Who are you?’ cried Selim.", "'누구냐?' 셀림이 외쳤어요."], ["‘But whoever you may be, I charge you not to advance another step.’—‘Long live the emperor!’ said the figure.", "'하지만 네가 누구든, 한 걸음도 더 다가오지 말라고 명한다.' — '황제 폐하 만세!' 그 형상이 말했어요."], ["‘He grants a full pardon to the Vizier Ali, and not only gives him his life, but restores to him his fortune and his possessions.’ My mother uttered a cry of joy, and clasped me to her bosom.", "'폐하께서 재상 알리에게 온전한 사면을 베푸시어, 그 목숨을 살려 주실 뿐 아니라 재산과 영지까지 돌려주신다!' 어머니는 기쁨의 외침을 지르며 저를 가슴에 끌어안으셨어요. '멈추세요.'"], ["‘Stop,’ said Selim, seeing that she was about to go out; ‘you see I have not yet received the ring,’—‘True,’ said my mother.", "셀림이, 어머니가 막 나가려는 걸 보고 말했어요. '아직 반지를 받지 못한 게 보이시지요.' — '그렇구나.'"], ["And she fell on her knees, at the same time holding me up towards heaven, as if she desired, while praying to God in my behalf, to raise me actually to his presence.”", "어머니가 말씀하셨어요. 그러고는 무릎을 꿇으시며, 동시에 저를 하늘을 향해 들어 올리셨어요. 마치 저를 위해 신께 기도하면서, 저를 실제로 그분 앞으로 들어 올리고 싶어 하시는 것처럼요.\""]], [["And for the second time Haydée stopped, overcome by such violent emotion that the perspiration stood upon her pale brow, and her stifled voice seemed hardly able to find utterance, so parched and dry were her throat and lips.", "그리고 두 번째로 하이데는 말을 멈추었다. 어찌나 격렬한 감정에 사로잡혔는지, 그녀의 창백한 이마에 땀이 맺혔고, 막힌 목소리는 좀처럼 말이 되어 나오지 못했다."], ["40076m", "목과 입술이 그토록 바싹 말라 있었던 것이다."]], [["Monte Cristo poured a little iced water into a glass, and presented it to her, saying with a mildness in which was also a shade of command,—“Courage.”", "몬테크리스토가 유리잔에 얼음물을 조금 따라 그녀에게 건네며, 부드러우면서도 명령의 기색이 어린 어조로 말했다. \"용기를 내렴.\""]], [["Haydée dried her eyes, and continued:", "하이데가 눈물을 닦고 말을 이었다."]], [["“By this time our eyes, habituated to the darkness, had recognized the messenger of the pasha,—it was a friend. Selim had also recognized him, but the brave young man only acknowledged one duty, which was to obey. ‘In whose name do you come?’ said he to him. ‘I come in the name of our master, Ali Tepelini.’—‘If you come from Ali himself,’ said Selim, ‘you know what you were charged to remit to me?’—‘Yes,’ said the messenger, ‘and I bring you his ring.’ At these words he raised his hand above his head, to show the token; but it was too far off, and there was not light enough to enable Selim, where he was standing, to distinguish and recognize the object presented to his view. ‘I do not see what you have in your hand,’ said Selim. ‘Approach then,’ said the messenger, ‘or I will come nearer to you, if you prefer it.’—‘I will agree to neither one nor the other,’ replied the young soldier; ‘place the object which I desire to see in the ray of light which shines there, and retire while I examine it.’—‘Be it so,’ said the envoy; and he retired, after having first deposited the token agreed on in the place pointed out to him by Selim.", "\"이때쯤엔 어둠에 익숙해진 우리 눈이 그 파샤의 전령을 알아보았어요. 그는 벗이었지요. 셀림도 그를 알아보았지만, 그 용감한 젊은이는 단 하나의 의무만을 인정했어요. 곧 복종이지요. '누구의 이름으로 왔는가?' 셀림이 그에게 물었어요. '우리 주군 알리 테펠레니의 이름으로 왔소.' — '알리 본인에게서 왔다면,' 셀림이 말했어요. '내게 무엇을 전하라고 맡겨졌는지 알 텐데?' — '그렇소.' 전령이 말했어요. '내가 그분의 반지를 가져왔소.' 이 말과 함께 그는 그 표를 보이려고 손을 머리 위로 들어 올렸어요. 하지만 너무 멀었고, 셀림이 서 있던 자리에서 눈앞에 내밀린 그 물건을 분간해 알아보기에는 빛이 충분하지 않았지요. '네 손에 무엇이 있는지 보이지 않는다.' 셀림이 말했어요. '그러면 가까이 오시오.' 전령이 말했어요. '아니면 원한다면 내가 그대에게 더 다가가겠소.' — '둘 다 안 된다.' 젊은 병사가 대답했어요. '내가 보고자 하는 그 물건을 저기 비치는 빛줄기 속에 놓고, 내가 살펴보는 동안 물러서 있어라.' — '그러지요.' 사절이 말했어요. 그러고는 셀림이 가리킨 자리에 약속된 그 표를 먼저 내려놓은 뒤 물러섰어요."]], [["“Oh, how our hearts palpitated; for it did, indeed, seem to be a ring which was placed there.", "오, 우리 가슴이 얼마나 두근거렸는지요. 거기 놓인 건 정말로 반지처럼 보였으니까요."], ["But was it my father’s ring?", "하지만 그게 제 아버지의 반지일까?"], ["that was the question.", "그게 문제였지요."], ["Selim, still holding in his hand the lighted match, walked towards the opening in the cavern, and, aided by the faint light which streamed in through the mouth of the cave, picked up the token.", "셀림은 여전히 불붙은 화승을 손에 든 채 동굴의 입구 쪽으로 걸어갔고, 동굴 어귀로 스며드는 희미한 빛에 의지해 그 표를 집어 들었어요."]], [["“‘It is well,’ said he, kissing it; ‘it is my master’s ring!’ And throwing the match on the ground, he trampled on it and extinguished it.", "'됐다.' 그가 거기에 입 맞추며 말했어요. '내 주인의 반지다!'"], ["The messenger uttered a cry of joy and clapped his hands.", "그러고는 화승을 바닥에 던져 짓밟아 꺼 버렸지요."], ["At this signal four soldiers of the Seraskier Kourchid suddenly appeared, and Selim fell, pierced by five blows.", "전령은 기쁨의 외침을 지르며 손뼉을 쳤어요. 이 신호에 세라스키에르 쿠르시드의 병사 넷이 별안간 나타났고, 셀림은 다섯 군데를 찔린 채 쓰러졌어요."], ["Each man had stabbed him separately, and, intoxicated by their crime, though still pale with fear, they sought all over the cavern to discover if there was any fear of fire, after which they amused themselves by rolling on the bags of gold.", "한 사람씩 따로따로 그를 찔렀던 거예요. 그러고는 제 범죄에 도취해, 여전히 두려움에 창백한 채로, 불이 날 위험은 없는지 동굴 곳곳을 뒤졌어요. 그런 다음 금화 자루 위에서 뒹굴며 흥겨워했지요."], ["At this moment my mother seized me in her arms, and hurrying noiselessly along numerous turnings and windings known only to ourselves, she arrived at a private staircase of the kiosk, where was a scene of frightful tumult and confusion.", "이 순간 어머니가 저를 두 팔에 끌어안으시고, 우리만 아는 숱한 굽이와 모퉁이를 소리 없이 서둘러 지나, 정자의 비밀 계단에 다다르셨어요. 거기는 끔찍한 소란과 혼란의 현장이었지요."], ["The lower rooms were entirely filled with Kourchid’s troops; that is to say, with our enemies.", "아래층 방들은 쿠르시드의 군대로, 곧 우리의 적들로 빼곡히 차 있었어요."], ["Just as my mother was on the point of pushing open a small door, we heard the voice of the pasha sounding in a loud and threatening tone.", "어머니가 막 작은 문 하나를 밀어 열려는 찰나, 우리는 파샤의 목소리가 우렁차고 위협적인 어조로 울리는 걸 들었어요."], ["My mother applied her eye to the crack between the boards; I luckily found a small opening which afforded me a view of the apartment and what was passing within.", "어머니는 판자 틈새에 눈을 갖다 대셨고, 저는 운 좋게도 방 안과 그 안에서 벌어지는 일을 들여다볼 수 있는 작은 구멍을 찾아냈지요. '무엇을 원하느냐?'"], ["‘What do you want?’ said my father to some people who were holding a paper inscribed with characters of gold.", "아버지가, 금빛 글자가 적힌 종이를 든 몇 사람에게 말씀하셨어요. '저희가 원하는 것은,' 한 사람이 대답했어요."], ["‘What we want,’ replied one, ‘is to communicate to you the will of his highness.", "'전하의 뜻을 그대에게 전하는 것이오. 이 칙서가 보이오?' — '보인다.'"], ["Do you see this firman?’—‘I do,’ said my father.", "아버지가 말씀하셨어요. '그렇다면 읽으시오."], ["‘Well, read it; he demands your head.’\n40078m", "전하께서 그대의 목을 요구하시오.'"]], [["“My father answered with a loud laugh, which was more frightful than even threats would have been, and he had not ceased when two reports of a pistol were heard; he had fired them himself, and had killed two men.", "아버지는 큰 웃음으로 답하셨는데, 그건 어떤 위협보다도 더 무시무시했어요. 그리고 그 웃음이 채 끝나기도 전에 권총 소리가 두 발 울렸지요. 아버지가 직접 쏘아, 두 사람을 죽이신 거예요."], ["The Palikares, who were prostrated at my father’s feet, now sprang up and fired, and the room was filled with fire and smoke.", "아버지 발치에 엎드려 있던 팔리카르들이 벌떡 일어나 총을 쏘았고, 방은 불과 연기로 가득 찼어요."], ["At the same instant the firing began on the other side, and the balls penetrated the boards all round us.", "같은 순간 반대편에서도 총격이 시작되어, 총알이 우리 사방의 판자를 꿰뚫었지요."], ["Oh, how noble did the grand vizier my father look at that moment, in the midst of the flying bullets, his scimitar in his hand, and his face blackened with the powder of his enemies!", "오, 그 순간 대재상이신 제 아버지가 날아드는 총탄 한가운데서 손에 언월도를 든 채, 적들의 화약으로 얼굴이 시커메진 모습으로 얼마나 고귀해 보였는지요! 그리고 그런 와중에도 아버지가 그들을 얼마나 두렵게 하고, 자기 앞에서 달아나게 만드셨는지! '셀림, 셀림!'"], ["and how he terrified them, even then, and made them fly before him!", "아버지가 외치셨어요. '불의 수호자여, 네 의무를 다하라!' — '셀림은 죽었습니다.'"], ["‘Selim, Selim!’ cried he, ‘guardian of the fire, do your duty!’—‘Selim is dead,’ replied a voice which seemed to come from the depths of the earth, ‘and you are lost, Ali!’ At the same moment an explosion was heard, and the flooring of the room in which my father was sitting was suddenly torn up and shivered to atoms—the troops were firing from underneath.", "땅속 깊은 데서 나오는 듯한 목소리가 대답했어요. '그리고 당신은 끝장났소, 알리!' 같은 순간 폭발음이 들렸고, 아버지가 앉아 계시던 방의 바닥이 별안간 뜯겨 산산조각이 났어요."], ["Three or four Palikares fell with their bodies literally ploughed with wounds.", "군대가 아래에서 쏘아 댄 거예요. 팔리카르 서넛이 온몸이 그야말로 상처로 갈가리 파헤쳐진 채 쓰러졌지요."]], [["“My father howled aloud, plunged his fingers into the holes which the balls had made, and tore up one of the planks entire.", "아버지는 크게 울부짖으며, 총알이 뚫어 놓은 구멍에 손가락을 찔러 넣어 널빤지 하나를 통째로 뜯어내셨어요."], ["But immediately through this opening twenty more shots were fired, and the flame, rushing up like fire from the crater of a volcano, soon reached the tapestry, which it quickly devoured.", "하지만 곧 그 구멍을 통해 스무 발이 더 쏟아졌고, 화염이 화산 분화구에서 솟는 불처럼 치솟아 이내 융단에 닿더니 순식간에 삼켜 버렸어요."], ["In the midst of all this frightful tumult and these terrific cries, two reports, fearfully distinct, followed by two shrieks more heartrending than all, froze me with terror.", "이 모든 끔찍한 소란과 무시무시한 외침 한가운데서, 소름 끼치도록 또렷한 두 발의 총성에 이어 그 무엇보다 가슴 미어지는 두 마디 비명이 들려와, 저는 공포로 얼어붙었어요."], ["These two shots had mortally wounded my father, and it was he who had given utterance to these frightful cries.", "그 두 발이 아버지에게 치명상을 입혔고, 그 끔찍한 비명을 지르신 분이 바로 아버지였지요."], ["However, he remained standing, clinging to a window.", "그래도 아버지는 창문에 매달린 채 서 계셨어요."], ["My mother tried to force the door, that she might go and die with him, but it was fastened on the inside.", "어머니는 가서 아버지와 함께 죽으려고 문을 밀어 열려 하셨지만, 문은 안쪽에서 잠겨 있었어요."], ["All around him were lying the Palikares, writhing in convulsive agonies, while two or three who were only slightly wounded were trying to escape by springing from the windows.", "아버지 주위에는 팔리카르들이 발작하듯 몸부림치며 쓰러져 있었고, 가벼운 상처만 입은 두셋은 창문에서 뛰어내려 달아나려 하고 있었지요. 이 위기의 순간, 바닥 전체가 별안간 무너져 내렸고, 아버지는 한쪽 무릎을 꿇으셨어요."], ["At this crisis the whole flooring suddenly gave way, my father fell on one knee, and at the same moment twenty hands were thrust forth, armed with sabres, pistols, and poniards—twenty blows were instantaneously directed against one man, and my father disappeared in a whirlwind of fire and smoke kindled by these demons, and which seemed like hell itself opening beneath his feet.", "그와 동시에 스무 개의 손이 군도와 권총과 단검으로 무장한 채 쑥 뻗어 나왔고, 한 사람을 향해 스무 번의 일격이 일제히 가해졌어요. 그리고 아버지는 그 악마들이 일으킨 불과 연기의 소용돌이 속으로 사라지셨지요. 마치 그분 발밑에서 지옥 자체가 열린 것 같았어요."], ["I felt myself fall to the ground, my mother had fainted.”", "저는 제가 바닥에 쓰러지는 걸 느꼈어요. 어머니는 정신을 잃으셨고요.\""]], [["Haydée’s arms fell by her side, and she uttered a deep groan, at the same time looking towards the count as if to ask if he were satisfied with her obedience to his commands.", "하이데의 두 팔이 옆으로 툭 떨어졌고, 그녀는 깊은 신음을 토했다. 동시에 그녀는 백작 쪽을 바라보았다. 자신이 그의 명령에 잘 따랐는지 묻기라도 하는 듯이."]], [["Monte Cristo arose and approached her, took her hand, and said to her in Romaic:", "몬테크리스토가 일어나 그녀에게 다가가, 그녀의 손을 잡고 로마이크어로 말했다."]], [["“Calm yourself, my dear child, and take courage in remembering that there is a God who will punish traitors.”", "\"진정하렴, 얘야. 배신자들을 벌하실 신이 계심을 떠올리며 용기를 내거라.\""]], [["“It is a frightful story, count,”", "\"끔찍한 이야기로군요, 백작님.\""], ["said Albert, terrified at the paleness of Haydée’s countenance, “and I reproach myself now for having been so cruel and thoughtless in my request.”", "알베르가 하이데의 창백한 얼굴빛에 질겁하며 말했다. \"그토록 잔인하고 생각 없는 부탁을 드린 제 자신이 이제 원망스럽습니다.\""]], [["“Oh, it is nothing,”", "\"오, 괜찮네.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["Then, patting the young girl on the head, he continued, “Haydée is very courageous, and she sometimes even finds consolation in the recital of her misfortunes.”", "그러고는 처녀의 머리를 토닥이며 말을 이었다. \"하이데는 무척 용감하고, 때로는 자신의 불행을 이야기하면서 오히려 위안을 얻기도 한다네.\""]], [["“Because, my lord,”", "\"왜냐하면, 주인님,\""], ["said Haydée eagerly, “my miseries recall to me the remembrance of your goodness.”", "하이데가 간절하게 말했다. \"제 비참함은 제게 주인님의 자비를 떠올리게 하니까요.\""]], [["Albert looked at her with curiosity, for she had not yet related what he most desired to know,—how she had become the slave of the count.", "알베르는 호기심 어린 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았다. 그녀가 그가 가장 알고 싶어 하는 것을 — 어떻게 백작의 노예가 되었는지를 — 아직 이야기하지 않았기 때문이다."], ["Haydée saw at a glance the same expression pervading the countenances of her two auditors; she continued:", "하이데는 두 청자의 얼굴에 똑같은 표정이 어린 것을 한눈에 알아채고 말을 이었다."]], [["“When my mother recovered her senses we were before the seraskier.", "\"어머니가 정신을 차렸을 때, 우리는 세라스키에르 앞에 있었어요. '죽이세요.' 어머니가 말씀하셨어요."], ["‘Kill,’ said she, ‘but spare the honor of the widow of Ali.’—‘It is not to me to whom you must address yourself,’ said Kourchid.", "'하지만 알리의 미망인의 명예는 지켜 주세요.' — '그건 내게 청할 일이 아니오.' 쿠르시드가 말했어요."]], [["“‘To whom, then?’—‘To your new master.’", "'그럼 누구에게 청해야 하나요?' — '그대의 새 주인에게.'"]], [["“‘Who and where is he?’—‘He is here.’", "'그가 누구이고 어디 있나요?' — '여기 있소.'"]], [["“And Kourchid pointed out one who had more than any contributed to the death of my father,”", "그러고서 쿠르시드는 누구보다도 제 아버지의 죽음에 크게 한몫한 자를 가리켰어요.\""], ["said Haydée, in a tone of chastened anger.", "하이데가 억눌린 분노의 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["said Albert, “you became the property of this man?” 40080m", "알베르가 말했다. \"부인은 그 사람의 소유가 되었던 겁니까?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니요.\""], ["replied Haydée, “he did not dare to keep us, so we were sold to some slave-merchants who were going to Constantinople.", "하이데가 대답했다. \"그는 감히 우리를 데리고 있지 못했어요. 그래서 우리는 콘스탄티노플로 가던 노예 상인들에게 팔렸지요."], ["We traversed Greece, and arrived half dead at the imperial gates.", "우리는 그리스를 가로질러, 반쯤 죽은 채로 궁성 문에 다다랐어요. 그 문은 사람들 무리에 둘러싸여 있었는데, 그들이 우리가 지나가도록 길을 터 주었어요."], ["They were surrounded by a crowd of people, who opened a way for us to pass, when suddenly my mother, having looked closely at an object which was attracting their attention, uttered a piercing cry and fell to the ground, pointing as she did so to a head which was placed over the gates, and beneath which were inscribed these words:", "그런데 갑자기 어머니가, 그들의 눈길을 끌던 어떤 것을 가까이 들여다보시더니 찢어지는 비명을 지르며 땅에 쓰러지셨어요. 그러면서 문 위에 걸린 머리 하나를 가리키셨지요. 그 아래에는 이런 말이 적혀 있었어요."]], [["‘_This is the head of Ali Tepelini, Pasha of Yanina._’", "'이것은 야니나의 파샤 알리 테펠레니의 머리이다.'"]], [["“I cried bitterly, and tried to raise my mother from the earth, but she was dead!", "저는 비통하게 울며 어머니를 땅에서 일으키려 했지만, 어머니는 이미 돌아가신 뒤였어요!"], ["I was taken to the slave-market, and was purchased by a rich Armenian.", "저는 노예 시장으로 끌려가, 어느 부유한 아르메니아인에게 팔렸어요."], ["He caused me to be instructed, gave me masters, and when I was thirteen years of age he sold me to the Sultan Mahmoud.”", "그는 저를 가르치게 하고 스승들을 붙여 주었어요. 그리고 제가 열세 살이 되자 술탄 마흐무드에게 저를 팔았지요.\""]], [["“Of whom I bought her,”", "\"내가 그에게서 그녀를 샀다네.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “as I told you, Albert, with the emerald which formed a match to the one I had made into a box for the purpose of holding my hashish pills.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"알베르, 자네에게 말했듯이, 내 해시시 알약을 담을 상자로 만들었던 에메랄드와 짝을 이루던 또 하나의 에메랄드로 말이야.\""]], [["“Oh, you are good, you are great, my lord!”", "\"오, 주인님은 선하시고 위대하세요!\""], ["said Haydée, kissing the count’s hand, “and I am very fortunate in belonging to such a master!”", "하이데가 백작의 손에 입 맞추며 말했다. \"그리고 저는 그런 주인님께 속해 있으니 참으로 행복해요!\""]], [["Albert remained quite bewildered with all that he had seen and heard.", "알베르는 자신이 보고 들은 모든 것에 완전히 얼이 빠진 채로 있었다."]], [["“Come, finish your cup of coffee,”", "\"자, 커피를 마저 들게.\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “the history is ended.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"이야기는 끝났으니까.\""]], [["# Chapter 78. We hear From Yanina", "# Chapter 78. We hear From Yanina"]], [["If Valentine could have seen the trembling step and agitated countenance of Franz when he quitted the chamber of M. Noirtier, even she would have been constrained to pity him.", "발랑틴이 노아르티에 씨의 방을 나서는 프란츠의 떨리는 걸음과 흐트러진 얼굴을 보았더라면, 아무리 그녀라도 그를 가엾게 여기지 않을 수 없었을 것이다."], ["Villefort had only just given utterance to a few incoherent sentences, and then retired to his study, where he received about two hours afterwards the following letter:", "빌포르는 두서없는 말 몇 마디를 겨우 내뱉고는 서재로 물러갔고, 두어 시간쯤 뒤에 그곳에서 다음과 같은 편지를 받았다."]], [["“After all the disclosures which were made this morning, M. Noirtier de Villefort must see the utter impossibility of any alliance being formed between his family and that of M. Franz d’Épinay. M.", "“오늘 아침에 밝혀진 모든 사실에 비추어, 노아르티에 드 빌포르 씨께서는 댁의 가문과 프란츠 데피네 씨의 가문 사이에 어떤 혼인도 맺어질 수 없음을 분명히 아셨으리라 생각합니다."], ["d’Épinay must say that he is shocked and astonished that M. de Villefort, who appeared to be aware of all the circumstances detailed this morning, should not have anticipated him in this announcement.”", "데피네는, 오늘 아침에 낱낱이 드러난 모든 정황을 이미 알고 계셨던 듯한 빌포르 씨께서 이 통고를 먼저 해 주시지 않은 데 대해 놀랍고도 충격을 받았다는 점을 밝히지 않을 수 없습니다.”"]], [["No one who had seen the magistrate at this moment, so thoroughly unnerved by the recent inauspicious combination of circumstances, would have supposed for an instant that he had anticipated the annoyance; although it certainly never had occurred to him that his father would carry candor, or rather rudeness, so far as to relate such a history.", "이 순간 검사를 본 사람이라면, 최근의 불길한 사정들이 한꺼번에 겹쳐 완전히 넋이 나간 그의 모습을 보고서, 그가 이런 곤욕을 미리 내다보고 있었다고는 한순간도 생각하지 못했을 것이다. 물론 그의 아버지가 솔직함을, 아니 차라리 무례함을 그토록 멀리까지 밀고 나가 그런 이야기를 다 털어놓으리라고는 빌포르도 전혀 예상하지 못했다."], ["And in justice to Villefort, it must be understood that M. Noirtier, who never cared for the opinion of his son on any subject, had always omitted to explain the affair to Villefort, so that he had all his life entertained the belief that General de Quesnel, or the Baron d’Épinay, as he was alternately styled, according as the speaker wished to identify him by his own family name, or by the title which had been conferred on him, fell the victim of assassination, and not that he was killed fairly in a duel.", "그리고 빌포르를 위해 한마디 해 두자면, 무슨 일에서든 아들의 의견 따위는 안중에 없던 노아르티에 씨가 그 사건을 빌포르에게 한 번도 설명해 주지 않았다는 점을 알아 둘 필요가 있다. 그래서 빌포르는 평생, 케넬 장군이, 또는 말하는 사람이 그를 본래의 성으로 부르려 하느냐 아니면 그에게 주어진 작위로 부르려 하느냐에 따라 데피네 남작이라고도 불리던 그 인물이, 정정당당한 결투에서 쓰러진 것이 아니라 암살의 희생자가 된 것이라 믿어 왔다."], ["This harsh letter, coming as it did from a man generally so polite and respectful, struck a mortal blow at the pride of Villefort.", "평소 그토록 정중하고 깍듯한 사람한테서 날아온 이 가혹한 편지는 빌포르의 자존심에 치명적인 일격을 가했다."]], [["Hardly had he read the letter, when his wife entered.", "그가 편지를 채 다 읽기도 전에 그의 아내가 들어왔다."], ["The sudden departure of Franz, after being summoned by M. Noirtier, had so much astonished everyone, that the position of Madame de Villefort, left alone with the notary and the witnesses, became every moment more embarrassing.", "노아르티에 씨의 부름을 받고 나간 프란츠가 갑자기 사라져 버린 데에 모두가 어찌나 놀랐던지, 공증인과 증인들과 함께 홀로 남겨진 빌포르 부인의 처지는 시간이 갈수록 점점 더 거북해졌다."], ["Determined to bear it no longer, she arose and left the room; saying she would go and make some inquiries into the cause of his sudden disappearance.", "더는 견딜 수 없다고 마음먹은 그녀는 자리에서 일어나 방을 나갔다. 그가 갑작스레 자취를 감춘 까닭을 알아보러 가겠다고 말하면서."]], [["M. de Villefort’s communications on the subject were very limited and concise; he told her, in fact, that an explanation had taken place between M.", "이 일에 관한 빌포르 씨의 설명은 지극히 짧고 간략했다."], ["Noirtier, M. d’Épinay, and himself, and that the marriage of Valentine and Franz would consequently be broken off.", "그는 사실, 노아르티에 씨와 데피네 씨와 자신 사이에 해명이 오갔으며, 그 결과 발랑틴과 프란츠의 혼인은 깨질 것이라고만 말했다."], ["This was an awkward and unpleasant thing to have to report to those who were waiting.", "이는 기다리고 있는 사람들에게 전하기에 난처하고 불쾌한 소식이었다."], ["She therefore contented herself with saying that M. Noirtier having at the commencement of the discussion been attacked by a sort of apoplectic fit, the affair would necessarily be deferred for some days longer.", "그래서 그녀는, 논의가 시작될 무렵 노아르티에 씨가 일종의 졸중 발작을 일으켜 그 일이 어쩔 수 없이 며칠 더 미루어지게 되었다고만 말하는 데 그쳤다."], ["This news, false as it was following so singularly in the train of the two similar misfortunes which had so recently occurred, evidently astonished the auditors, and they retired without a word.", "거짓이긴 했으나 최근 잇따라 일어난 두 차례의 비슷한 불행의 뒤를 묘하게 잇는 이 소식에 듣는 이들은 분명 놀란 기색이었고, 그들은 한마디 말도 없이 물러갔다."]], [["During this time Valentine, at once terrified and happy, after having embraced and thanked the feeble old man for thus breaking with a single blow the chain which she had been accustomed to consider as irrefragable, asked leave to retire to her own room, in order to recover her composure.", "그동안 발랑틴은 겁에 질리는 동시에 행복한 마음으로, 그녀가 끊을 수 없다고만 여겨 온 그 사슬을 단 한 번에 끊어 준 그 가냘픈 노인을 끌어안고 감사를 표한 뒤, 마음을 가라앉히게 제 방으로 물러가도 좋겠느냐고 청했다."], ["Noirtier looked the permission which she solicited. But instead of going to her own room, Valentine, having once gained her liberty, entered the gallery, and, opening a small door at the end of it, found herself at once in the garden.", "노아르티에는 그녀가 청한 허락을 눈빛으로 보내 주었다. 그러나 일단 자유를 얻은 발랑틴은 제 방으로 가는 대신 회랑으로 들어섰고, 그 끝에 있는 작은 문을 열자 곧장 정원에 다다랐다."]], [["In the midst of all the strange events which had crowded one on the other, an indefinable sentiment of dread had taken possession of Valentine’s mind.", "꼬리에 꼬리를 물고 밀어닥친 온갖 기이한 사건들 한가운데서, 형언할 수 없는 두려움이 발랑틴의 마음을 사로잡고 있었다."], ["She expected every moment that she should see Morrel appear, pale and trembling, to forbid the signing of the contract, like the Laird of Ravenswood in _The Bride of Lammermoor_.", "그녀는 순간순간, 마치 「래머무어의 신부」 속 레이븐스우드의 영주처럼, 모렐이 창백하게 떨며 나타나 혼인 계약서에 서명하지 못하게 막는 장면을 보게 될 것만 같았다."]], [["It was high time for her to make her appearance at the gate, for Maximilian had long awaited her coming.", "그녀가 쪽문에 모습을 드러낼 때가 마침맞았다. 막시밀리앙이 오래전부터 그녀를 기다리고 있었기 때문이다."], ["He had half guessed what was going on when he saw Franz quit the cemetery with M. de Villefort.", "그는 프란츠가 빌포르 씨와 함께 묘지를 떠나는 것을 보았을 때 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 절반쯤 짐작했다."], ["He followed M. d’Épinay, saw him enter, afterwards go out, and then re-enter with Albert and Château-Renaud.", "그는 데피네 씨를 따라가, 그가 들어가는 것을, 이어서 나오는 것을, 그러고는 알베르, 샤토르노와 함께 다시 들어가는 것을 보았다."], ["He had no longer any doubts as to the nature of the conference; he therefore quickly went to the gate in the clover-patch, prepared to hear the result of the proceedings, and very certain that Valentine would hasten to him the first moment she should be set at liberty.", "그 회담의 성격에 대해 더는 의심할 여지가 없었다. 그래서 그는 토끼풀밭의 쪽문으로 서둘러 가, 일이 어떻게 되었는지 들을 채비를 하고서, 발랑틴이 풀려나는 순간 곧장 자기에게 달려오리라 확신했다."], ["He was not mistaken; peering through the crevices of the wooden partition, he soon discovered the young girl, who cast aside all her usual precautions and walked at once to the barrier.", "그의 짐작은 틀리지 않았다. 나무 칸막이의 틈새로 내다보던 그는 곧 그 처녀를 발견했는데, 그녀는 평소의 온갖 조심성을 다 내던지고 곧장 울타리로 걸어왔다."], ["The first glance which Maximilian directed towards her entirely reassured him, and the first words she spoke made his heart bound with delight.", "막시밀리앙이 그녀에게 던진 첫 눈길에 그는 완전히 안심했고, 그녀가 입을 열어 처음 내뱉은 말에 그의 가슴은 기쁨으로 뛰었다."]], [["“We are saved!”", "\"우린 살았어요!\""], ["said Valentine.", "발랑틴이 말했다."]], [["“Saved?”", "\"살았다고요?\""], ["repeated Morrel, not being able to conceive such intense happiness; “by whom?”", "모렐이 그토록 벅찬 행복을 도무지 믿을 수 없다는 듯 되물었다. \"누구 덕분에요?\""]], [["“By my grandfather.", "\"제 할아버지 덕분이에요."], ["Oh, Morrel, pray love him for all his goodness to us!”", "아, 모렐, 우리에게 베풀어 주신 그 모든 은혜를 생각해서 부디 그분을 사랑해 주세요!\""]], [["Morrel swore to love him with all his soul; and at that moment he could safely promise to do so, for he felt as though it were not enough to love him merely as a friend or even as a father, he worshiped him as a god.", "모렐은 온 영혼을 다해 그분을 사랑하겠노라 맹세했다. 그리고 그 순간 그는 그 맹세를 거리낌 없이 할 수 있었다. 단지 친구로서, 아니 아버지로서 사랑하는 것만으로는 부족하게 느껴져, 그를 신처럼 우러러 받들었기 때문이다."]], [["“But tell me, Valentine, how has it all been effected?", "\"하지만 말해 줘요, 발랑틴, 어떻게 이 모든 일이 이루어진 거죠?"], ["What strange means has he used to compass this blessed end?”", "이 복된 결말을 끌어내려고 그분이 어떤 기이한 방법을 쓰신 거예요?\""]], [["Valentine was on the point of relating all that had passed, but she suddenly remembered that in doing so she must reveal a terrible secret which concerned others as well as her grandfather, and she said:", "발랑틴은 그동안 있었던 일을 다 이야기하려다, 그러자면 할아버지뿐 아니라 다른 사람들과도 얽힌 끔찍한 비밀을 드러내야 한다는 데에 문득 생각이 미쳤고, 그래서 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“At some future time I will tell you all about it.”", "\"언젠가 다 이야기해 줄게요.\""]], [["“But when will that be?”", "\"하지만 그게 언제죠?\""]], [["“When I am your wife.”", "\"제가 당신의 아내가 되었을 때요.\""]], [["The conversation had now turned upon a topic so pleasing to Morrel, that he was ready to accede to anything that Valentine thought fit to propose, and he likewise felt that a piece of intelligence such as he just heard ought to be more than sufficient to content him for one day.", "이제 대화는 모렐에게 더없이 흐뭇한 화제로 옮겨 갔고, 그는 발랑틴이 어떤 제안을 하든 다 받아들일 마음이 되었다. 게다가 방금 들은 정도의 소식이라면 하루치 만족으로는 차고도 넘친다는 생각이 들었다."], ["However, he would not leave without the promise of seeing Valentine again the next night.", "그래도 그는 다음 날 밤 다시 발랑틴을 만나겠다는 약속을 받지 않고는 자리를 뜨려 하지 않았다."], ["Valentine promised all that Morrel required of her, and certainly it was less difficult now for her to believe that she should marry Maximilian than it was an hour ago to assure herself that she should not marry Franz.", "발랑틴은 모렐이 요구하는 것을 다 약속해 주었다. 그리고 한 시간 전만 해도 자신이 프란츠와 결혼하지 않으리라고 스스로를 안심시키는 일이 무척 어려웠던 데 비해, 막시밀리앙과 결혼하게 되리라 믿는 일은 분명 이제 훨씬 덜 어려웠다."]], [["During the time occupied by the interview we have just detailed, Madame de Villefort had gone to visit M. Noirtier.", "방금 이야기한 그 만남이 이루어지는 동안, 빌포르 부인은 노아르티에 씨를 찾아가 있었다."], ["The old man looked at her with that stern and forbidding expression with which he was accustomed to receive her.", "노인은 늘 그녀를 맞을 때 짓던 그 엄격하고 쌀쌀맞은 표정으로 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"어르신,\""], ["said she, “it is superfluous for me to tell you that Valentine’s marriage is broken off, since it was here that the affair was concluded.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"발랑틴의 혼인이 깨졌다는 말씀을 새삼 드릴 것도 없겠지요. 그 일이 매듭지어진 게 바로 이곳이니까요.\""]], [["Noirtier’s countenance remained immovable.", "노아르티에의 얼굴은 미동도 없었다."]], [["“But one thing I can tell you, of which I do not think you are aware; that is, that I have always been opposed to this marriage, and that the contract was entered into entirely without my consent or approbation.”", "\"하지만 한 가지는 말씀드릴 수 있습니다. 어르신께서는 모르고 계시리라 생각되는 일이지요. 저는 늘 이 혼인에 반대해 왔고, 그 계약은 전적으로 제 동의나 찬성 없이 맺어진 것이었다는 점입니다.\""]], [["Noirtier regarded his daughter-in-law with the look of a man desiring an explanation.", "노아르티에는 설명을 바라는 사람의 눈빛으로 며느리를 바라보았다."]], [["“Now that this marriage, which I know you so much disliked, is done away with, I come to you on an errand which neither M. de Villefort nor Valentine could consistently undertake.”", "\"어르신께서 그토록 싫어하셨던 이 혼인이 이제 깨졌으니, 저는 빌포르 씨도 발랑틴도 차마 떳떳이 맡을 수 없는 한 가지 부탁을 들고 어르신을 찾아왔습니다.\""]], [["Noirtier’s eyes demanded the nature of her mission.", "노아르티에의 눈은 그녀의 용건이 무엇이냐고 묻고 있었다."]], [["“I come to entreat you, sir,”", "\"제가 어르신께 간청드리러 온 것은,\" 빌포르 부인이 말을 이었다."], ["continued Madame de Villefort, “as the only one who has the right of doing so, inasmuch as I am the only one who will receive no personal benefit from the transaction,—I come to entreat you to restore, not your love, for that she has always possessed, but to restore your fortune to your granddaughter.”", "\"오직 저만이 그럴 자격이 있기 때문입니다. 이 일에서 아무런 개인적 이익도 얻지 않을 사람은 저뿐이니까요. 어르신, 부디 손녀에게 사랑을, 그건 그 아이가 늘 받아 온 것이니 그게 아니라, 어르신의 재산을 돌려주시기를 간청드립니다.\""]], [["There was a doubtful expression in Noirtier’s eyes; he was evidently trying to discover the motive of this proceeding, and he could not succeed in doing so.", "노아르티에의 눈에는 미심쩍어하는 빛이 어렸다. 그는 이 행동의 속내를 알아내려 애쓰는 게 분명했으나, 끝내 알아낼 수 없었다."]], [["“May I hope, sir,”", "\"어르신,\""], ["said Madame de Villefort, “that your intentions accord with my request?”", "빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"어르신의 뜻이 제 부탁과 같으리라고 기대해도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["Noirtier made a sign that they did.", "노아르티에는 그렇다는 신호를 보냈다."]], [["“In that case, sir,”", "\"그렇다면 어르신,\""], ["rejoined Madame de Villefort, “I will leave you overwhelmed with gratitude and happiness at your prompt acquiescence to my wishes.”", "빌포르 부인이 말을 받았다. \"제 바람을 이토록 선뜻 받아들여 주신 데에 감사와 기쁨이 벅차오르는 마음으로 물러가겠습니다.\""], ["She then bowed to M. Noirtier and retired.", "그러고는 노아르티에 씨에게 절을 올린 뒤 물러갔다."]], [["The next day M. Noirtier sent for the notary; the first will was torn up and a second made, in which he left the whole of his fortune to Valentine, on condition that she should never be separated from him.", "이튿날 노아르티에 씨는 공증인을 불렀다. 첫 번째 유언장은 찢겨 나가고 두 번째 유언장이 작성되었는데, 거기서 그는 자기 재산 전부를 발랑틴에게 남기되 그 아이가 결코 자기 곁을 떠나지 않는다는 조건을 달았다."], ["It was then generally reported that Mademoiselle de Villefort, the heiress of the marquis and marchioness of Saint-Méran, had regained the good graces of her grandfather, and that she would ultimately be in possession of an income of 300,000 livres.", "그러자 곧 사람들 사이에 소문이 돌았다. 생메랑 후작과 후작 부인의 상속인인 빌포르 양이 할아버지의 총애를 되찾았으며, 머지않아 30만 리브르의 수입을 손에 쥐게 되리라는 소문이었다."]], [["While all the proceedings relative to the dissolution of the marriage-contract were being carried on at the house of M. de Villefort, Monte Cristo had paid his visit to the Count of Morcerf, who, in order to lose no time in responding to M.", "혼인 계약의 파기와 관련된 모든 일이 빌포르 씨 댁에서 진행되는 동안, 몬테크리스토는 모르세르 백작을 찾아가 있었다."], ["Danglars’ wishes, and at the same time to pay all due deference to his position in society, donned his uniform of lieutenant-general, which he ornamented with all his crosses, and thus attired, ordered his finest horses and drove to the Rue de la Chaussée d’Antin.", "백작은 당글라르의 뜻에 한시도 지체 없이 응하는 한편 자신의 사회적 지위에 걸맞은 예를 다하려고, 중장 군복을 차려입고 거기에 받은 훈장을 모조리 단 차림으로, 가장 좋은 말들을 대령시켜 쇼세 당탱 거리로 향했다."]], [["Danglars was balancing his monthly accounts, and it was perhaps not the most favorable moment for finding him in his best humor.", "당글라르는 한 달치 장부를 결산하고 있었으니, 그가 더없이 좋은 기분에 잠겨 있을 만한 때는 아마 아니었다."], ["At the first sight of his old friend, Danglars assumed his majestic air, and settled himself in his easy-chair.", "옛 친구를 처음 본 순간 당글라르는 거드름 피우는 태도를 취하며 안락의자에 깊숙이 몸을 묻었다."]], [["Morcerf, usually so stiff and formal, accosted the banker in an affable and smiling manner, and, feeling sure that the overture he was about to make would be well received, he did not consider it necessary to adopt any manœuvres in order to gain his end, but went at once straight to the point. 40086m", "평소 그토록 뻣뻣하고 격식 차리던 모르세르가 살갑고 웃음 띤 태도로 은행가에게 말을 건넸다. 그리고 자기가 꺼내려는 이야기가 좋게 받아들여지리라 확신했기에, 목적을 이루려고 무슨 술수를 부릴 필요도 없다고 여겨 곧장 본론으로 들어갔다."]], [["“Well, baron,”", "\"자, 남작,\""], ["said he, “here I am at last; some time has elapsed since our plans were formed, and they are not yet executed.”", "그가 말했다. \"드디어 내가 왔소. 우리가 계획을 세운 지도 꽤 시간이 흘렀건만, 아직 실행에 옮기지 못했구려.\""]], [["Morcerf paused at these words, quietly waiting till the cloud should have dispersed which had gathered on the brow of Danglars, and which he attributed to his silence; but, on the contrary, to his great surprise, it grew darker and darker.", "모르세르는 이 말을 끝으로 잠시 멈추고, 당글라르의 이마에 드리운 구름이 걷히기를 조용히 기다렸다. 그는 그 구름이 자기가 그동안 잠자코 있었던 탓이려니 여겼다. 그러나 뜻밖에도, 그를 몹시 놀라게도, 그 구름은 점점 더 짙어져 갔다."]], [["“To what do you allude, monsieur?”", "\"무슨 말씀이신지요?\""], ["said Danglars; as if he were trying in vain to guess at the possible meaning of the general’s words.", "당글라르가 말했다. 마치 장군의 말이 무슨 뜻인지 도무지 헤아릴 수 없다는 듯이."]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["said Morcerf, “I see you are a stickler for forms, my dear sir, and you would remind me that the ceremonial rites should not be omitted.", "모르세르가 말했다. \"이런, 격식을 따지시는구려. 의례적인 절차를 빠뜨려선 안 된다고 일깨우시려는 게로군요."], ["_Ma foi_, I beg your pardon, but as I have but one son, and it is the first time I have ever thought of marrying him, I am still serving my apprenticeship, you know; come, I will reform.”", "이런, 미안하게 됐소. 한데 아들이 하나뿐인 데다 그 아이 혼인을 생각해 보는 것도 처음이라, 아직 견습 중이라 그렇소이다. 자, 고치리다.\""]], [["And Morcerf with a forced smile arose, and, making a low bow to M. Danglars, said:", "그러고는 모르세르가 억지웃음을 지으며 자리에서 일어나, 당글라르 씨에게 정중히 절하며 말했다."]], [["“Baron, I have the honor of asking of you the hand of Mademoiselle Eugénie Danglars for my son, the Vicomte Albert de Morcerf.”", "\"남작, 내 아들 알베르 드 모르세르 자작을 위해 외제니 당글라르 양의 손을 청하는 영광을 누리고자 하오.\""]], [["But Danglars, instead of receiving this address in the favorable manner which Morcerf had expected, knit his brow, and without inviting the count, who was still standing, to take a seat, he said:", "그러나 당글라르는 모르세르가 기대한 대로 이 청을 호의적으로 받아들이기는커녕 이맛살을 찌푸렸고, 여태 서 있는 백작에게 자리를 권하지도 않은 채 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“Monsieur, it will be necessary to reflect before I give you an answer.”", "\"백작, 답을 드리기 전에 좀 생각해 봐야겠습니다.\""]], [["“To reflect?”", "\"생각을 한다고?\""], ["said Morcerf, more and more astonished; “have you not had enough time for reflection during the eight years which have elapsed since this marriage was first discussed between us?”", "모르세르가 점점 더 놀라며 말했다. \"이 혼담이 우리 사이에서 처음 거론된 지 여덟 해가 지났는데, 그동안 생각할 시간이 모자랐단 말이오?\""]], [["“Count,”", "\"백작,\""], ["said the banker, “things are constantly occurring in the world to induce us to lay aside our most established opinions, or at all events to cause us to remodel them according to the change of circumstances, which may have placed affairs in a totally different light to that in which we at first viewed them.”", "은행가가 말했다. \"세상에는 우리가 가장 굳게 세워 둔 견해마저 내려놓게 만드는, 혹은 적어도 정황의 변화에 따라 그 견해를 다시 빚게 만드는 일들이 끊임없이 일어나는 법입니다. 그 정황이라는 것이, 처음 우리가 보던 것과는 사뭇 다른 빛 아래 일을 옮겨 놓을 수도 있고 말입니다.\""]], [["“I do not understand you, baron,”", "\"무슨 말씀인지 모르겠소, 남작.\""], ["said Morcerf.", "모르세르가 말했다."]], [["“What I mean to say is this, sir,—that during the last fortnight unforeseen circumstances have occurred——”", "\"제 말씀은 이렇습니다. 지난 보름 사이에 예기치 못한 일들이 생겼다는 것이지요.\""]], [["“Excuse me,”", "\"실례지만,\""], ["said Morcerf, “but is it a play we are acting?”", "모르세르가 말했다. \"우리가 지금 연극이라도 하고 있는 거요?\""]], [["“A play?”", "\"연극이라뇨?\""]], [["“Yes, for it is like one; pray let us come more to the point, and endeavor thoroughly to understand each other.”", "\"그렇소, 꼭 연극 같으니 말이오. 자, 좀 더 본론으로 들어가서 서로 속을 터놓읍시다.\""]], [["“That is quite my desire.”", "\"저도 바로 그러길 바랍니다.\""]], [["“You have seen M. de Monte Cristo have you not?”", "\"몬테크리스토 씨를 만난 적이 있으시지요?\""]], [["“I see him very often,”", "\"아주 자주 봅니다.\""], ["said Danglars, drawing himself up; “he is a particular friend of mine.”", "당글라르가 몸을 곧추세우며 말했다. \"각별한 벗이지요.\""]], [["“Well, in one of your late conversations with him, you said that I appeared to be forgetful and irresolute concerning this marriage, did you not?”", "\"한데 그와 최근에 나눈 대화 가운데서, 내가 이 혼사에 대해 잊은 듯, 마음을 정하지 못한 듯 보인다고 말씀하셨다지요?\""]], [["“I did say so.”", "\"그렇게 말했습니다.\""]], [["“Well, here I am, proving at once that I am really neither the one nor the other, by entreating you to keep your promise on that score.”", "\"자, 그래서 내가 이렇게 와서, 그 점에 대해 한 약속을 지켜 달라 청함으로써, 내가 그 둘 중 어느 쪽도 아님을 단번에 증명해 보이려는 거요.\""]], [["Danglars did not answer.", "당글라르는 대답하지 않았다."]], [["“Have you so soon changed your mind,”", "\"이렇게 빨리 마음이 바뀌신 거요,\""], ["added Morcerf, “or have you only provoked my request that you may have the pleasure of seeing me humbled?”", "모르세르가 덧붙였다. \"아니면 그저 내가 굽히는 꼴을 보는 재미로 이런 청을 하게끔 부추기신 거요?\""]], [["Danglars, seeing that if he continued the conversation in the same tone in which he had begun it, the whole thing might turn out to his own disadvantage, turned to Morcerf, and said:", "당글라르는, 처음 시작한 그 어조로 계속 대화를 끌고 가다가는 자칫 모든 게 자기에게 불리하게 돌아갈 수 있겠다는 것을 깨닫고, 모르세르를 돌아보며 말했다."]], [["“Count, you must doubtless be surprised at my reserve, and I assure you it costs me much to act in such a manner towards you; but, believe me when I say that imperative necessity has imposed the painful task upon me.”", "\"백작, 제가 이렇게 말을 아끼는 데에 분명 놀라셨을 겁니다. 백작께 이런 식으로 대하는 것이 제게도 무척 괴로운 일임을 분명히 말씀드립니다. 그러나 어쩔 수 없는 사정이 제게 이 괴로운 일을 떠맡겼다는 제 말을 믿어 주십시오.\""]], [["“These are all so many empty words, my dear sir,”", "\"그건 다 빈말일 뿐이오.\" 모르세르가 말했다."], ["said Morcerf: “they might satisfy a new acquaintance, but the Comte de Morcerf does not rank in that list; and when a man like him comes to another, recalls to him his plighted word, and this man fails to redeem the pledge, he has at least a right to exact from him a good reason for so doing.”", "\"새로 알게 된 사람이라면 그런 말에 만족할지 모르나, 모르세르 백작은 그런 부류에 들지 않소. 그리고 이런 사람이 저런 사람을 찾아가, 그가 맺은 언약을 상기시켰는데도 그 사람이 약조를 지키지 않는다면, 적어도 그렇게 하는 까닭이라도 떳떳이 들을 권리는 있는 법이오.\""]], [["Danglars was a coward, but did not wish to appear so; he was piqued at the tone which Morcerf had just assumed.", "당글라르는 겁쟁이였으나 그렇게 비치고 싶지는 않았다. 방금 모르세르가 취한 어조에 그는 발끈했다."]], [["“I am not without a good reason for my conduct,”", "\"제 행동에 그럴 만한 까닭이 없는 게 아닙니다.\""], ["replied the banker.", "은행가가 대답했다."]], [["“What do you mean to say?”", "\"무슨 말씀이시오?\""]], [["“I mean to say that I have a good reason, but that it is difficult to explain.”", "\"제게 그럴 만한 까닭이 있긴 하나, 설명하기가 어렵다는 말씀입니다.\""]], [["“You must be aware, at all events, that it is impossible for me to understand motives before they are explained to me; but one thing at least is clear, which is, that you decline allying yourself with my family.”", "\"적어도 이것만은 아셔야 하오. 그 동기가 내게 설명되기 전에는 내가 그걸 헤아릴 길이 없다는 것 말이오. 그러나 한 가지만은 분명하구려. 당신이 내 가문과 인척을 맺기를 거절한다는 것 말이오.\""]], [["“No, sir,”", "\"아닙니다, 백작.\""], ["said Danglars; “I merely suspend my decision, that is all.”", "당글라르가 말했다. \"그저 결정을 미루는 것뿐입니다.\""]], [["“And do you really flatter yourself that I shall yield to all your caprices, and quietly and humbly await the time of again being received into your good graces?”", "\"그래, 내가 당신의 온갖 변덕에 굴복해서, 다시 당신의 총애를 받게 될 날을 잠자코 머리를 조아리며 기다릴 거라고 정말로 우쭐대시는 거요?\""]], [["“Then, count, if you will not wait, we must look upon these projects as if they had never been entertained.”", "\"그럼 백작, 기다리지 않으시겠다면, 이 혼담은 아예 없던 일로 여길 수밖에요.\""]], [["The count bit his lips till the blood almost started, to prevent the ebullition of anger which his proud and irritable temper scarcely allowed him to restrain; understanding, however, that in the present state of things the laugh would decidedly be against him, he turned from the door, towards which he had been directing his steps, and again confronted the banker.", "백작은 거의 피가 솟구칠 만큼 입술을 깨물어, 거만하고 발끈하기 쉬운 그의 성미로는 좀처럼 누르기 어려운 분노가 끓어 넘치는 것을 가까스로 참았다. 그러나 지금 같은 상황에서는 웃음거리가 될 쪽이 분명 자기라는 것을 깨닫고, 그는 발걸음을 옮기던 문 쪽에서 돌아서서 다시 은행가와 마주 섰다."], ["A cloud settled on his brow, evincing decided anxiety and uneasiness, instead of the expression of offended pride which had lately reigned there.", "방금까지 그의 이마를 채우던 모욕당한 자존심의 표정 대신, 이제는 분명한 불안과 초조함을 드러내는 구름이 거기 내려앉았다."]], [["“My dear Danglars,”", "\"이보시오, 당글라르,\""], ["said Morcerf, “we have been acquainted for many years, and consequently we ought to make some allowance for each other’s failings.", "모르세르가 말했다. \"우리가 알고 지낸 지도 여러 해이니, 마땅히 서로의 허물쯤은 좀 너그러이 봐줘야 하지 않겠소."], ["You owe me an explanation, and really it is but fair that I should know what circumstance has occurred to deprive my son of your favor.”", "당신은 내게 해명할 의무가 있고, 어떤 사정이 생겨 내 아들이 당신의 호의를 잃게 되었는지 내가 아는 것이 도리에 맞는 일이오.\""]], [["“It is from no personal ill-feeling towards the viscount, that is all I can say, sir,”", "\"자작에게 무슨 개인적인 악감정이 있어서가 아닙니다. 제가 드릴 수 있는 말은 그것뿐입니다.\""], ["replied Danglars, who resumed his insolent manner as soon as he perceived that Morcerf was a little softened and calmed down.", "당글라르가 대답했다. 그는 모르세르가 조금 누그러져 가라앉았음을 알아채자마자 다시 거만한 태도로 돌아갔다."]], [["“And towards whom do you bear this personal ill-feeling, then?”", "\"그렇다면 대체 누구에게 그런 개인적인 악감정을 품고 있단 말이오?\""], ["said Morcerf, turning pale with anger.", "모르세르가 분노로 창백해지며 말했다."], ["The expression of the count’s face had not remained unperceived by the banker; he fixed on him a look of greater assurance than before, and said:", "백작의 얼굴 표정이 은행가의 눈을 피해 가지 못했다. 그는 전보다 한층 자신만만한 눈길을 그에게 못박으며 말했다."]], [["“You may, perhaps, be better satisfied that I should not go farther into particulars.”", "\"제가 더 자세히 캐고 들지 않는 편이 백작께도 아마 더 흡족하실 겁니다.\""]], [["A tremor of suppressed rage shook the whole frame of the count, and making a violent effort over himself, he said: “I have a right to insist on your giving me an explanation.", "억눌린 분노에 백작의 온몸이 부르르 떨렸다. 그는 안간힘을 써 자신을 다스리며 말했다. \"내겐 당신이 해명하도록 요구할 권리가 있소."], ["Is it Madame de Morcerf who has displeased you?", "모르세르 부인이 당신의 비위를 거슬렀단 말이오?"], ["Is it my fortune which you find insufficient?", "내 재산이 당신 보기에 변변치 않다는 거요?"], ["Is it because my opinions differ from yours?”", "아니면 내 생각이 당신과 다르다는 거요?\""]], [["“Nothing of the kind, sir,”", "\"그런 것은 전혀 아닙니다.\""], ["replied Danglars: “if such had been the case, I only should have been to blame, inasmuch as I was aware of all these things when I made the engagement.", "당글라르가 대답했다. \"만약 그랬다면 잘못은 오직 제게 있겠지요. 약조를 맺을 당시에 그 모든 것을 이미 알고 있었으니까요."], ["No, do not seek any longer to discover the reason.", "아닙니다, 그 까닭을 더 캐려 하지 마십시오."], ["I really am quite ashamed to have been the cause of your undergoing such severe self-examination; let us drop the subject, and adopt the middle course of delay, which implies neither a rupture nor an engagement.", "백작께서 이토록 혹독하게 스스로를 돌아보시게 한 장본인이 되었다는 게 정말이지 부끄럽습니다. 이 이야기는 그만 접고, 절교도 약혼도 아닌 중간의 길, 곧 미루는 쪽을 택하기로 하지요."], ["_Ma foi_, there is no hurry.", "정말이지, 서두를 것 없습니다."], ["My daughter is only seventeen years old, and your son twenty-one.", "제 딸은 이제 겨우 열일곱이고, 백작의 아드님은 스물하나입니다."], ["While we wait, time will be progressing, events will succeed each other; things which in the evening look dark and obscure, appear but too clearly in the light of morning, and sometimes the utterance of one word, or the lapse of a single day, will reveal the most cruel calumnies.”", "우리가 기다리는 동안 세월은 흘러갈 것이고, 사건이 사건을 잇따라 부를 것입니다. 저녁에는 어둡고 흐릿해 보이던 일들이 아침 햇살 아래서는 더없이 또렷이 드러나기도 하고, 때로는 말 한마디나 단 하루의 흐름이 가장 잔인한 중상을 밝혀내기도 하는 법이니까요.\""]], [["“Calumnies, did you say, sir?”", "\"중상이라 하셨소?\""], ["cried Morcerf, turning livid with rage.", "모르세르가 분노로 새파랗게 질려 외쳤다."], ["“Does anyone dare to slander me?”", "\"누가 감히 나를 모함한단 말이오?\""]], [["“Monsieur, I told you that I considered it best to avoid all explanation.”", "\"백작, 저는 모든 해명을 피하는 편이 낫다고 말씀드렸습니다.\""]], [["“Then, sir, I am patiently to submit to your refusal?”", "\"그럼 나더러 당신의 거절을 잠자코 받아들이라는 거요?\""]], [["“Yes, sir, although I assure you the refusal is as painful for me to give as it is for you to receive, for I had reckoned on the honor of your alliance, and the breaking off of a marriage contract always injures the lady more than the gentleman.”", "\"그렇습니다, 백작. 다만 그 거절이 백작께 받아들이시기 괴로운 만큼이나 제게도 드리기 괴로운 일임을 분명히 말씀드립니다. 저는 백작과 인척을 맺는 영광을 기대하고 있었으니까요. 게다가 혼인 계약이 깨지면 늘 손해를 보는 쪽은 남자보다 여자입니다.\""]], [["“Enough, sir,”", "\"됐소이다.\""], ["said Morcerf, “we will speak no more on the subject.”", "모르세르가 말했다. \"이 일에 대해서는 더 말하지 맙시다.\""]], [["And clutching his gloves in anger, he left the apartment.", "그러고는 분에 못 이겨 장갑을 움켜쥐고 방을 나섰다."], ["Danglars observed that during the whole conversation Morcerf had never once dared to ask if it was on his own account that Danglars recalled his word.", "당글라르는, 대화가 오가는 내내 모르세르가 자신을 두고 약속을 거둔 것이냐고 단 한 번도 감히 묻지 못했다는 사실을 눈여겨보았다."]], [["That evening he had a long conference with several friends; and M.", "그날 저녁 그는 여러 벗과 오랫동안 회담을 나누었다."], ["Cavalcanti, who had remained in the drawing-room with the ladies, was the last to leave the banker’s house.", "그리고 응접실에 부인들과 함께 남아 있던 카발칸티 씨가 은행가의 집을 마지막으로 떠났다."]], [["The next morning, as soon as he awoke, Danglars asked for the newspapers; they were brought to him; he laid aside three or four, and at last fixed on _l’Impartial_, the paper of which Beauchamp was the chief editor.", "이튿날 아침 잠에서 깨자마자 당글라르는 신문을 가져오라고 했다. 신문이 그에게 들어왔다. 그는 서너 부를 옆으로 밀쳐 두고, 마침내 보샹이 편집장으로 있는 「랭파르시알」을 집어 들었다."], ["He hastily tore off the cover, opened the journal with nervous precipitation, passed contemptuously over the Paris jottings, and arriving at the miscellaneous intelligence, stopped with a malicious smile, at a paragraph headed", "그는 황급히 띠지를 뜯고 신경질적인 조급함으로 신문을 펼치더니, 파리 단신 난을 경멸하듯 훑어 넘기고, 잡보 면에 이르러 심술궂은 미소를 띠며 다음과 같은 제목이 붙은 기사 앞에 멈춰 섰다."]], [["_We hear from Yanina._", "야니나로부터의 소식."]], [["“Very good,”", "\"아주 좋아.\""], ["observed Danglars, after having read the paragraph; “here is a little article on Colonel Fernand, which, if I am not mistaken, would render the explanation which the Comte de Morcerf required of me perfectly unnecessary.”", "기사를 다 읽고 나서 당글라르가 중얼거렸다. \"여기 페르낭 대령에 관한 짤막한 기사가 있군. 내가 잘못 본 게 아니라면, 모르세르 백작이 내게 요구하던 해명 따위를 완전히 쓸데없는 것으로 만들어 줄 기사야.\""]], [["At the same moment, that is, at nine o’clock in the morning, Albert de Morcerf, dressed in a black coat buttoned up to his chin, might have been seen walking with a quick and agitated step in the direction of Monte Cristo’s house in the Champs-Élysées.", "바로 그 순간, 그러니까 아침 아홉 시에, 검은 코트를 턱밑까지 단추로 채워 입은 알베르 드 모르세르가 빠르고 흔들리는 걸음으로 샹젤리제에 있는 몬테크리스토의 저택 쪽으로 걸어가고 있는 모습이 보였을 것이다."], ["When he presented himself at the gate the porter informed him that the Count had gone out about half an hour previously.", "그가 대문 앞에 모습을 드러내자 문지기는 백작이 삼십 분쯤 전에 외출했다고 알려 주었다."]], [["“Did he take Baptistin with him?”", "\"바티스탱도 데리고 갔소?\""]], [["“No, my lord.”", "\"아닙니다, 자작님.\""]], [["“Call him, then; I wish to speak to him.”", "\"그럼 그를 불러 주시오. 그에게 할 말이 있소.\""]], [["The concierge went to seek the valet de chambre, and returned with him in an instant.", "문지기는 시종을 부르러 가더니 곧 그와 함께 돌아왔다."]], [["“My good friend,”", "\"이보게,\""], ["said Albert, “I beg pardon for my intrusion, but I was anxious to know from your own mouth if your master was really out or not.”", "알베르가 말했다. \"불쑥 찾아와 미안하네만, 자네 주인이 정말 외출하셨는지 자네 입으로 직접 듣고 싶어서 말일세.\""]], [["“He is really out, sir,”", "\"정말 외출하셨습니다, 자작님.\""], ["replied Baptistin.", "바티스탱이 대답했다."]], [["“Out, even to me?”", "\"나한테까지 안 계신다는 건가?\""]], [["“I know how happy my master always is to receive the vicomte,”", "\"제 주인께서 자작님을 맞으실 때면 늘 얼마나 기뻐하시는지 잘 압니다.\""], ["said Baptistin; “and I should therefore never think of including him in any general order.”", "바티스탱이 말했다. \"그러니 어찌 자작님을 흔한 부재중 명령에 넣을 생각이나 하겠습니까.\""]], [["“You are right; and now I wish to see him on an affair of great importance.", "\"옳은 말이네. 그런데 지금 아주 중요한 일로 그분을 뵈어야 하네."], ["Do you think it will be long before he comes in?”", "곧 들어오실 것 같은가?\""]], [["“No, I think not, for he ordered his breakfast at ten o’clock.”", "\"네, 그러실 겁니다. 열 시에 아침 식사를 들이라 분부하셨으니까요.\""]], [["“Well, I will go and take a turn in the Champs-Élysées, and at ten o’clock I will return here; meanwhile, if the count should come in, will you beg him not to go out again without seeing me?”", "\"그럼 나는 샹젤리제를 좀 거닐다가 열 시에 다시 이리로 오겠네. 그동안 백작께서 들어오시거든, 나를 보지 않고는 다시 나가지 마시라고 청해 주겠나?\""]], [["“You may depend on my doing so, sir,”", "\"믿고 맡겨 주십시오, 자작님.\""], ["said Baptistin.", "바티스탱이 말했다."]], [["Albert left the cab in which he had come at the count’s door, intending to take a turn on foot.", "알베르는 타고 온 마차를 백작의 문 앞에 세워 둔 채 걸어서 한 바퀴 돌 작정이었다."], ["As he was passing the Allée des Veuves, he thought he saw the count’s horses standing at Gosset’s shooting-gallery; he approached, and soon recognized the coachman.", "그는 뵈브 가로수길을 지나다, 백작의 말들이 고세 사격장 앞에 서 있는 것을 본 것 같았다. 다가가 보니 곧 마부를 알아볼 수 있었다."]], [["“Is the count shooting in the gallery?”", "\"백작께서 사격장에서 사격을 하고 계신가?\""], ["said Morcerf.", "모르세르가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, sir,” replied the coachman.", "\"네, 자작님.\" 마부가 대답했다."], ["While he was speaking, Albert had heard the report of two or three pistol-shots. He entered, and on his way met the waiter.", "그가 말하는 동안 알베르는 두세 발의 권총 소리를 들었다. 안으로 들어선 그는 가는 길에 사환과 마주쳤다."]], [["“Excuse me, my lord,”", "\"실례합니다, 자작님.\""], ["said the lad; “but will you have the kindness to wait a moment?”", "사환이 말했다. \"잠시 기다려 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“What for, Philip?” asked Albert, who, being a constant visitor there, did not understand this opposition to his entrance.", "\"무엇 때문에 그러나, 필리프?\" 늘 드나들던 단골이라 자기를 막아서는 까닭을 알 수 없어 알베르가 물었다."]], [["“Because the person who is now in the gallery prefers being alone, and never practices in the presence of anyone.”", "\"지금 사격장에 계신 분이 혼자 있기를 원하셔서, 누구 앞에서도 절대 연습을 하지 않으시거든요.\""]], [["“Not even before you, Philip?", "\"자네 앞에서도 말인가, 필리프?"], ["Then who loads his pistol?”", "그럼 누가 권총에 탄을 재나?\""]], [["“His servant.”", "\"그분의 하인입니다.\""]], [["“A Nubian?”", "\"누비아 사람인가?\""]], [["“A negro.”", "\"흑인입니다.\""]], [["“It is he, then.”", "\"그럼 그분이로군.\""]], [["“Do you know this gentleman?”", "\"이 신사분을 아십니까?\""]], [["“Yes, and I am come to look for him; he is a friend of mine.”", "\"알지. 바로 그분을 찾아온 걸세. 내 벗이라네.\""]], [["“Oh, that is quite another thing, then.", "\"아, 그렇다면 사정이 다르지요."], ["I will go immediately and inform him of your arrival.”", "곧 가서 자작님이 오셨다고 알려 드리겠습니다.\""]], [["And Philip, urged by his own curiosity, entered the gallery; a second afterwards, Monte Cristo appeared on the threshold.", "그러고는 호기심에 못 이긴 필리프가 사격장 안으로 들어갔다. 잠시 뒤 몬테크리스토가 문턱에 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["“I ask your pardon, my dear count,”", "\"용서하시오, 백작.\""], ["said Albert, “for following you here, and I must first tell you that it was not the fault of your servants that I did so; I alone am to blame for the indiscretion.", "알베르가 말했다. \"이렇게 여기까지 백작을 쫓아온 것을 말이오. 우선 이게 댁 하인들의 잘못이 아님을 밝혀 두어야겠소."], ["I went to your house, and they told me you were out, but that they expected you home at ten o’clock to breakfast.", "그 무례는 오로지 내 탓이오. 댁에 갔더니 외출하셨다며, 열 시에 아침 식사를 하러 돌아오실 거라더군요."], ["I was walking about in order to pass away the time till ten o’clock, when I caught sight of your carriage and horses.”", "열 시까지 시간을 죽이려고 거닐다가 백작의 마차와 말들이 눈에 띈 거요.\""]], [["“What you have just said induces me to hope that you intend breakfasting with me.”", "\"방금 하신 말씀을 들으니, 나와 함께 아침을 들 생각이 있으신가 보군요.\""]], [["40092m\n“No, thank you, I am thinking of other things besides breakfast just now; perhaps we may take that meal at a later hour and in worse company.”", "\"아니, 고맙소만, 지금은 아침 식사 말고 다른 일을 생각하고 있소. 어쩌면 그 식사는 더 늦은 시각에, 더 변변찮은 동석자와 함께하게 될지도 모르겠소.\""]], [["“What on earth are you talking of?”", "\"대체 무슨 말씀을 하시는 거요?\""]], [["“I am to fight today.”", "\"오늘 결투를 하게 됐소.\""]], [["“For what?”", "\"무엇 때문에요?\""]], [["“For the sake of fighting!”", "\"결투를 하기 위해서지!\""]], [["“Yes, I understand that, but what is the quarrel?", "\"그건 알겠소만, 무슨 다툼이오?"], ["People fight for all sorts of reasons, you know.”", "사람들은 온갖 이유로 결투를 하니 말이오.\""]], [["“I fight in the cause of honor.”", "\"명예를 위해 싸우는 거요.\""]], [["“Ah, that is something serious.”", "\"아, 그렇다면 심각한 일이군요.\""]], [["“So serious, that I come to beg you to render me a service.”", "\"어찌나 심각한지, 백작께 한 가지 도움을 청하러 왔소.\""]], [["“What is it?”", "\"무엇이오?\""]], [["“To be my second.”", "\"내 입회인이 되어 주는 거요.\""]], [["“That is a serious matter, and we will not discuss it here; let us speak of nothing till we get home.", "\"그건 중대한 문제니, 여기서 의논할 일은 아니오. 집에 갈 때까지 아무 말도 맙시다."], ["Ali, bring me some water.”", "알리, 물 좀 가져오게.\""]], [["The count turned up his sleeves, and passed into the little vestibule where the gentlemen were accustomed to wash their hands after shooting.", "백작은 소매를 걷어 올리고, 사격을 마친 신사들이 손을 씻곤 하는 작은 현관방으로 들어갔다."]], [["“Come in, my lord,” said Philip in a low tone, “and I will show you something droll.”", "\"이리 와 보십시오, 자작님.\" 필리프가 낮은 목소리로 말했다."], ["Morcerf entered, and in place of the usual target, he saw some playing-cards fixed against the wall.", "\"재미난 것을 보여 드리겠습니다.\" 모르세르가 들어가 보니, 늘 있던 과녁 대신 벽에 트럼프 카드들이 붙어 있었다."], ["At a distance Albert thought it was a complete suit, for he counted from the ace to the ten.", "멀찍이서 알베르는 그것이 한 벌 전부인 줄 알았다. 에이스에서 십까지를 세었기 때문이다."]], [["“Ah, ha,”", "\"아하,\""], ["said Albert, “I see you were preparing for a game of cards.”", "알베르가 말했다. \"카드놀이라도 채비하고 계셨던 모양이군요.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니오,\""], ["said the count, “I was making a suit.”", "백작이 말했다. \"한 벌을 만들고 있었소.\""]], [["“How?” said Albert.", "\"어떻게요?\" 알베르가 물었다."]], [["“Those are really aces and twos which you see, but my shots have turned them into threes, fives, sevens, eights, nines, and tens.”", "\"보시는 저것들은 사실 에이스와 둘이었는데, 내 사격으로 그게 셋, 다섯, 일곱, 여덟, 아홉, 십이 된 거요.\""]], [["Albert approached.", "알베르가 다가갔다."], ["In fact, the bullets had actually pierced the cards in the exact places which the painted signs would otherwise have occupied, the lines and distances being as regularly kept as if they had been ruled with pencil.", "정말로 총알들은, 그려진 무늬가 있어야 할 바로 그 자리를 정확히 꿰뚫어 놓았고, 선과 간격은 마치 연필과 자로 그은 듯 한 치의 어김 없이 지켜져 있었다."], ["In going up to the target Morcerf picked up two or three swallows that had been rash enough to come within the range of the count’s pistol.", "과녁 쪽으로 올라가던 모르세르는 백작의 권총 사정거리 안에 겁 없이 날아들었다가 떨어진 제비 두세 마리를 주워 들었다."]], [["“_Diable!_” said Morcerf.", "\"이런!\" 모르세르가 말했다."]], [["“What would you have, my dear viscount?”", "\"어쩌겠소, 자작?\""], ["said Monte Cristo, wiping his hands on the towel which Ali had brought him; “I must occupy my leisure moments in some way or other.", "몬테크리스토가 알리가 가져다준 수건에 손을 닦으며 말했다. \"한가한 시간을 이런저런 방식으로라도 채워야 하지 않겠소."], ["But come, I am waiting for you.”", "자, 가십시다. 기다리고 있겠소.\""]], [["Both men entered Monte Cristo’s carriage, which in the course of a few minutes deposited them safely at No. 30.", "두 사람은 몬테크리스토의 마차에 올랐고, 마차는 몇 분 만에 그들을 30번지에 무사히 내려놓았다."], ["Monte Cristo took Albert into his study, and pointing to a seat, placed another for himself.", "몬테크리스토는 알베르를 서재로 데리고 들어가, 한 자리를 가리키고는 자기 자리도 따로 끌어다 놓았다."], ["“Now let us talk the matter over quietly,”", "\"자, 이제 차분히 이 일을 따져 봅시다.\""], ["said the count.", "백작이 말했다."]], [["“You see I am perfectly composed,”", "\"보시다시피 나는 아주 침착하오.\""], ["said Albert.", "알베르가 말했다."]], [["“With whom are you going to fight?”", "\"누구와 싸우려는 거요?\""]], [["“With Beauchamp.”", "\"보샹과요.\""]], [["“One of your friends!”", "\"당신 친구 아니오!\""]], [["“Of course; it is always with friends that one fights.”", "\"물론이지. 결투란 늘 친구와 하는 법이니까.\""]], [["“I suppose you have some cause of quarrel?”", "\"무슨 다툼의 빌미가 있을 테지요?\""]], [["“I have.”", "\"있소.\""]], [["40094m\n“What has he done to you?”", "\"그가 당신에게 무슨 짓을 했소?\""]], [["“There appeared in his journal last night—but wait, and read for yourself.”", "\"어젯밤 그의 신문에 이런 게 실렸소. 자, 직접 읽어 보시오.\""], ["And Albert handed over the paper to the count, who read as follows:", "그러고는 알베르가 신문을 백작에게 건넸고, 백작은 다음과 같이 읽었다."]], [["“A correspondent at Yanina informs us of a fact of which until now we had remained in ignorance.", "\"야니나 주재 통신원이 지금껏 우리가 모르고 있던 한 가지 사실을 알려 왔다."], ["The castle which formed the protection of the town was given up to the Turks by a French officer named Fernand, in whom the grand vizier, Ali Tepelini, had reposed the greatest confidence.”", "그 도시를 지켜 주던 성채는 페르낭이라는 이름의 한 프랑스 장교가 튀르크 측에 넘겨주었는데, 대재상 알리 테펠리니는 그에게 더없는 신임을 두고 있었다.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래서,\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “what do you see in that to annoy you?”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"여기서 무엇이 당신을 불쾌하게 한다는 거요?\""]], [["“What do I see in it?”", "\"여기서 무엇이냐고요?\""]], [["“Yes; what does it signify to you if the castle of Yanina was given up by a French officer?”", "\"그렇소. 야니나의 성채를 한 프랑스 장교가 넘겨준 것이 당신과 무슨 상관이오?\""]], [["“It signifies to my father, the Count of Morcerf, whose Christian name is Fernand!”", "\"내 아버지, 모르세르 백작과 상관이 있소. 그분의 세례명이 페르낭이거든!\""]], [["“Did your father serve under Ali Pasha?”", "\"당신 부친이 알리 파샤 휘하에서 복무하셨소?\""]], [["“Yes; that is to say, he fought for the independence of the Greeks, and hence arises the calumny.”", "\"그렇소. 그러니까, 그리스의 독립을 위해 싸우셨고, 바로 거기서 이 중상이 비롯된 거요.\""]], [["“Oh, my dear viscount, do talk reason!”", "\"오, 자작, 제발 사리에 맞게 말합시다!\""]], [["“I do not desire to do otherwise.”", "\"나도 그러지 않을 생각은 없소.\""]], [["“Now, just tell me who the devil should know in France that the officer Fernand and the Count of Morcerf are one and the same person?", "\"자, 한번 말해 보시오. 대체 프랑스에서 누가 페르낭이라는 장교와 모르세르 백작이 같은 사람이라는 걸 알겠소?"], ["and who cares now about Yanina, which was taken as long ago as the year 1822 or 1823?”", "그리고 1822년인가 1823년인가, 그 옛날에 함락된 야니나 따위를 지금 누가 마음에 두겠소?\""]], [["“That just shows the meanness of this slander. They have allowed all this time to elapse, and then all of a sudden rake up events which have been forgotten to furnish materials for scandal, in order to tarnish the lustre of our high position.", "\"그게 바로 이 중상의 비열함을 보여 주는 거요. 이만큼이나 세월을 흘려보내 놓고, 갑자기 잊힌 사건을 들춰내 추문의 거리로 삼아, 우리의 높은 지위에 서린 광채를 더럽히려는 거요."], ["I inherit my father’s name, and I do not choose that the shadow of disgrace should darken it.", "나는 아버지의 이름을 물려받았고, 그 이름에 치욕의 그림자가 드리우는 것을 두고 볼 수 없소."], ["I am going to Beauchamp, in whose journal this paragraph appears, and I shall insist on his retracting the assertion before two witnesses.”", "이 기사가 실린 신문의 보샹에게 가서, 두 입회인 앞에서 그 주장을 철회하라고 요구할 작정이오.\""]], [["“Beauchamp will never retract.”", "\"보샹은 결코 철회하지 않을 거요.\""]], [["“Then we must fight.”", "\"그럼 우리는 싸워야겠지요.\""]], [["“No you will not, for he will tell you, what is very true, that perhaps there were fifty officers in the Greek army bearing the same name.”", "\"아니, 그러지 않을 거요. 그가 당신에게, 더없이 옳은 말이지만, 그리스 군에 같은 이름을 가진 장교가 쉰 명은 있었을지도 모른다고 할 테니까.\""]], [["“We will fight, nevertheless.", "\"그래도 우리는 싸울 거요."], ["I will efface that blot on my father’s character.", "나는 아버지의 명예에 묻은 그 오점을 지워 버리겠소."], ["My father, who was such a brave soldier, whose career was so brilliant——”", "그토록 용감한 군인이었고, 그토록 빛나는 이력을 쌓으신 내 아버지를……\""]], [["“Oh, well, he will add, ‘We are warranted in believing that this Fernand is not the illustrious Count of Morcerf, who also bears the same Christian name.’”", "\"오, 그렇다면 그는 이렇게 덧붙이겠지요. '우리는 이 페르낭이 같은 세례명을 가진, 저 명망 높은 모르세르 백작과는 다른 인물이라고 믿을 만한 근거가 있다.'\""]], [["“I am determined not to be content with anything short of an entire retractation.”", "\"나는 완전한 철회가 아니고는 그 무엇에도 만족하지 않기로 마음먹었소.\""]], [["“And you intend to make him do it in the presence of two witnesses, do you?”", "\"그러니까 두 입회인 앞에서 그가 그렇게 하게 만들 작정이란 말이지요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["“You do wrong.”", "\"잘못 생각하셨소.\""]], [["“Which means, I suppose, that you refuse the service which I asked of you?”", "\"그건 결국, 내가 청한 도움을 거절하시겠다는 뜻이겠지요?\""]], [["“You know my theory regarding duels; I told you my opinion on that subject, if you remember, when we were at Rome.”", "\"결투에 대한 내 지론을 알고 계시잖소. 기억하신다면, 우리가 로마에 있을 때 그 문제에 대한 내 의견을 말씀드렸지요.\""]], [["“Nevertheless, my dear count, I found you this morning engaged in an occupation but little consistent with the notions you profess to entertain.”", "\"그렇지만 백작, 오늘 아침 나는, 평소 입에 올리시는 그 신조와는 영 어울리지 않는 일에 몰두해 계신 모습을 보았소.\""]], [["“Because, my dear fellow, you understand one must never be eccentric.", "\"그건, 이보시오, 결코 별나게 굴어서는 안 된다는 걸 아셔야 하기 때문이오."], ["If one’s lot is cast among fools, it is necessary to study folly.", "어리석은 자들 틈에 운명이 던져졌다면, 어리석음을 익혀 둘 필요가 있는 법이오."], ["I shall perhaps find myself one day called out by some harebrained scamp, who has no more real cause of quarrel with me than you have with Beauchamp; he may take me to task for some foolish trifle or other, he will bring his witnesses, or will insult me in some public place, and I am expected to kill him for all that.”", "나도 언젠가, 당신이 보샹에게 품은 것 이상으로 나와 다툴 진짜 빌미라고는 없는 어떤 머리 빈 망나니한테 결투를 신청받을지도 모르오. 그자가 무슨 하찮은 트집을 잡아 나를 몰아붙이고, 입회인들을 데려오거나, 어느 사람 많은 자리에서 나를 모욕할지도 모르오. 그래도 나는 그 때문에 그자를 죽여야 하는 거요.\""]], [["“You admit that you would fight, then?", "\"그럼 백작도 싸우겠다고 인정하시는 거군요?"], ["Well, if so, why do you object to my doing so?”", "그렇다면 왜 내가 싸우는 것에는 반대하시오?\""]], [["“I do not say that you ought not to fight, I only say that a duel is a serious thing, and ought not to be undertaken without due reflection.”", "\"당신이 싸워서는 안 된다는 게 아니오. 다만 결투란 중대한 일이니, 충분히 숙고하지 않고 뛰어들어서는 안 된다는 거요.\""]], [["“Did he reflect before he insulted my father?”", "\"그자는 내 아버지를 모욕하기 전에 숙고하기라도 했답니까?\""]], [["“If he spoke hastily, and owns that he did so, you ought to be satisfied.”", "\"만약 그가 성급하게 말한 거고, 자기가 그랬노라 인정한다면, 당신도 만족해야 하오.\""]], [["“Ah, my dear count, you are far too indulgent.”", "\"아, 백작, 당신은 너무 너그럽소.\""]], [["“And you are far too exacting.", "\"그리고 당신은 너무 까다롭소."], ["Supposing, for instance, and do not be angry at what I am going to say——”", "가령 말이오, 내가 지금부터 할 말에 화내지 마시오……\" \"말씀하시오.\""]], [["“Well.” “Supposing the assertion to be really true?”", "\"가령 그 주장이 정말로 사실이라면 어떻겠소?\""]], [["“A son ought not to submit to such a stain on his father’s honor.”", "\"아들이라면 아버지의 명예에 묻은 그런 오점을 두고 볼 수 없는 법이오.\""]], [["“_Ma foi!_ we live in times when there is much to which we must submit.”", "\"이런! 우리는 받아들일 수밖에 없는 일이 많은 시대를 살고 있소.\""]], [["“That is precisely the fault of the age.”", "\"그게 바로 이 시대의 잘못이오.\""]], [["“And do you undertake to reform it?”", "\"그래서 당신이 그걸 바로잡겠다는 거요?\""]], [["“Yes, as far as I am personally concerned.”", "\"그렇소. 적어도 나 자신에 관한 한은.\""]], [["“Well, you are indeed exacting, my dear fellow!”", "\"이런, 정말이지 까다로운 분이시군요, 이보시오!\""]], [["“Yes, I own it.”", "\"그렇소, 인정하오.\""]], [["“Are you quite impervious to good advice?”", "\"좋은 충고에는 아주 귀를 닫으시는 거요?\""]], [["“Not when it comes from a friend.”", "\"벗이 하는 충고라면 그렇지 않소.\""]], [["“And do you account me that title?”", "\"나를 그 벗으로 쳐 주시는 거요?\""]], [["“Certainly I do.”", "\"물론 그렇소.\""]], [["“Well, then, before going to Beauchamp with your witnesses, seek further information on the subject.”", "\"그렇다면 입회인들을 데리고 보샹에게 가기 전에, 그 일에 대해 좀 더 알아보시오.\""]], [["“From whom?”", "\"누구한테서요?\""]], [["“From Haydée.”", "\"하이데한테서.\""]], [["“Why, what can be the use of mixing a woman up in the affair?—what can she do in it?”", "\"이런, 이 일에 여자를 끌어들여서 무슨 소용이 있겠소? 그녀가 여기서 무얼 할 수 있단 말이오?\""]], [["“She can declare to you, for example, that your father had no hand whatever in the defeat and death of the vizier; or if by chance he had, indeed, the misfortune to——”", "\"예를 들어, 당신 부친이 그 재상의 패망과 죽음에 아무런 손도 대지 않았다고 당신에게 밝혀 줄 수 있소. 혹 만에 하나라도 정말 불행히 그런 일에……\""]], [["“I have told you, my dear count, that I would not for one moment admit of such a proposition.”", "\"백작, 나는 그런 가정 따위는 한순간도 받아들이지 않겠다고 말씀드렸소.\""]], [["“You reject this means of information, then?”", "\"그럼 이 정보의 길마저 마다하시는 거요?\""]], [["“I do—most decidedly.”", "\"그렇소. 단호히 그렇소.\""]], [["“Then let me offer one more word of advice.”", "\"그렇다면 한마디만 더 충고하리다.\""]], [["“Do so, then, but let it be the last.”", "\"하시오. 다만 그게 마지막이게 해 주오.\""]], [["“You do not wish to hear it, perhaps?”", "\"어쩌면 듣고 싶지 않으실지도 모르겠소만?\""]], [["“On the contrary, I request it.”", "\"천만에, 청하는 바요.\""]], [["“Do not take any witnesses with you when you go to Beauchamp—visit him alone.”", "\"보샹에게 갈 때 입회인을 데려가지 마시오. 혼자 찾아가시오.\""]], [["“That would be contrary to all custom.”", "\"그건 모든 관례에 어긋나오.\""]], [["“Your case is not an ordinary one.”", "\"당신의 경우는 예사로운 경우가 아니오.\""]], [["“And what is your reason for advising me to go alone?”", "\"혼자 가라고 권하시는 이유는 무엇이오?\""]], [["“Because then the affair will rest between you and Beauchamp.”", "\"그래야 이 일이 당신과 보샹 사이에만 머물 테니까.\""]], [["“Explain yourself.”", "\"좀 더 설명해 보시오.\""]], [["“I will do so.", "\"그러리다."], ["If Beauchamp be disposed to retract, you ought at least to give him the opportunity of doing it of his own free will,—the satisfaction to you will be the same.", "만약 보샹이 철회할 마음이 있다면, 적어도 그가 제 발로 그렇게 할 기회를 주어야 하오. 그래도 당신이 얻는 만족은 똑같소."], ["If, on the contrary, he refuses to do so, it will then be quite time enough to admit two strangers into your secret.”", "반대로 그가 거절한다면, 그때 가서 두 낯선 사람을 당신의 비밀에 끌어들여도 늦지 않소.\""]], [["“They will not be strangers, they will be friends.”", "\"그들은 낯선 사람이 아니라 벗들이오.\""]], [["“Ah, but the friends of today are the enemies of tomorrow; Beauchamp, for instance.”", "\"아, 하지만 오늘의 벗이 내일의 적이 되는 법이오. 보샹만 해도 그렇소.\""]], [["“So you recommend——”", "\"그럼 권하시는 바는……\""]], [["“I recommend you to be prudent.”", "\"신중하시라는 거요.\""]], [["“Then you advise me to go alone to Beauchamp?”", "\"그럼 보샹에게 혼자 가라는 충고로군요?\""]], [["“I do, and I will tell you why.", "\"그렇소. 그 이유를 말씀드리리다."], ["When you wish to obtain some concession from a man’s self-love, you must avoid even the appearance of wishing to wound it.”", "어떤 사람의 자존심에서 무언가 양보를 얻어 내고자 한다면, 그 자존심을 건드리려는 기색조차 비쳐서는 안 되는 법이오.\""]], [["“I believe you are right.”", "\"백작 말씀이 옳은 것 같소.\""]], [["“I am glad of it.”", "\"그렇다니 기쁘오.\""]], [["“Then I will go alone.”", "\"그럼 혼자 가겠소.\""]], [["“Go; but you would do better still by not going at all.”", "\"가시오. 하지만 아예 안 가는 편이 더 나을 거요.\""]], [["“That is impossible.”", "\"그건 불가능하오.\""]], [["“Do so, then; it will be a wiser plan than the first which you proposed.”", "\"그렇다면 그렇게라도 하시오. 처음에 내놓으신 방안보다는 더 현명한 계책이오.\""]], [["“But if, in spite of all my precautions, I am at last obliged to fight, will you not be my second?”", "\"하지만 그 모든 조심에도 불구하고 끝내 싸워야만 한다면, 내 입회인이 되어 주지 않으시겠소?\""]], [["“My dear viscount,”", "\"이보시오, 자작,\" 몬테크리스토가 엄숙하게 말했다. \"당신은 오늘 이전에도 익히 보셨을 거요."], ["said Monte Cristo gravely, “you must have seen before today that at all times and in all places I have been at your disposal, but the service which you have just demanded of me is one which it is out of my power to render you.” “Why?”", "어느 때, 어느 곳에서든 나는 당신을 위해 늘 마음을 다해 왔다는 걸. 그러나 방금 내게 청하신 그 일만큼은, 내 힘으로는 들어드릴 수 없는 일이오.\""]], [["“Perhaps you may know at some future period, and in the mean time I request you to excuse my declining to put you in possession of my reasons.”", "\"어째서요?\" \"어쩌면 언젠가 그 까닭을 알게 되실지도 모르오. 그동안은, 내가 그 이유를 당신께 밝히기를 마다하는 것을 부디 양해해 주시오.\""]], [["“Well, I will have Franz and Château-Renaud; they will be the very men for it.”", "\"좋소, 그럼 프란츠와 샤토르노를 데리고 가겠소. 그들이라면 그 일에 딱 맞는 사람들이지.\""]], [["“Do so, then.”", "\"그렇게 하시오.\""]], [["“But if I do fight, you will surely not object to giving me a lesson or two in shooting and fencing?”", "\"하지만 정말 싸우게 된다면, 내게 사격과 검술을 한두 차례 가르쳐 주는 것쯤은 마다하지 않으시겠지요?\""]], [["“That, too, is impossible.”", "\"그것 역시 불가능하오.\""]], [["“What a singular being you are!—you will not interfere in anything.”", "\"참 별난 분이시군요! 어떤 일에도 끼어들지 않으시려 하니.\""]], [["“You are right—that is the principle on which I wish to act.”", "\"옳은 말씀이오. 그게 바로 내가 따르고자 하는 원칙이오.\""]], [["“We will say no more about it, then.", "\"그럼 그 일에 대해서는 더 말하지 맙시다."], ["Good-bye, count.”", "잘 있으시오, 백작.\""]], [["Morcerf took his hat, and left the room.", "모르세르는 모자를 집어 들고 방을 나섰다."], ["He found his carriage at the door, and doing his utmost to restrain his anger he went at once to find Beauchamp, who was in his office.", "문 앞에서 마차를 발견한 그는 끓어오르는 분노를 한껏 억누르며 곧장 보샹을 찾아갔는데, 그는 사무실에 있었다."], ["It was a gloomy, dusty-looking apartment, such as journalists’ offices have always been from time immemorial.", "그곳은 음침하고 먼지투성이로 보이는 방으로, 기자들의 사무실이 까마득한 옛날부터 늘 그래 왔듯한 모습이었다."], ["The servant announced M. Albert de Morcerf.", "하인이 알베르 드 모르세르 씨가 왔다고 알렸다."], ["Beauchamp repeated the name to himself, as though he could scarcely believe that he had heard aright, and then gave orders for him to be admitted.", "보샹은 제대로 들은 것이 맞는지 도무지 믿을 수 없다는 듯 그 이름을 혼자 되뇌더니, 그를 들여보내라 일렀다."], ["Albert entered.", "알베르가 들어섰다."]], [["Beauchamp uttered an exclamation of surprise on seeing his friend leap over and trample under foot all the newspapers which were strewed about the room.", "보샹은 자기 친구가 방 안 여기저기 흩어진 신문들을 모조리 뛰어넘고 발로 짓밟는 것을 보고 놀라움의 탄성을 질렀다."]], [["“This way, this way, my dear Albert!”", "\"이리로, 이리로 오게, 알베르!\""], ["said he, holding out his hand to the young man.", "그가 청년에게 손을 내밀며 말했다."], ["“Are you out of your senses, or do you come peaceably to take breakfast with me?", "\"정신이 나간 건가, 아니면 점잖게 나와 아침이라도 들러 온 건가?"], ["Try and find a seat—there is one by that geranium, which is the only thing in the room to remind me that there are other leaves in the world besides leaves of paper.”", "자리를 좀 찾아 앉게. 저 제라늄 옆에 하나 있군. 이 방에서 세상에는 종이 잎사귀 말고도 다른 잎사귀가 있다는 걸 일깨워 주는 거라곤 저것뿐이라네.\""]], [["“Beauchamp,”", "\"보샹,\""], ["said Albert, “it is of your journal that I come to speak.”", "알베르가 말했다. \"내가 이야기하러 온 건 자네 신문에 관한 일일세.\""]], [["“Indeed?", "\"그래?"], ["What do you wish to say about it?”", "그에 대해 무슨 할 말이 있나?\""]], [["“I desire that a statement contained in it should be rectified.”", "\"거기 실린 어떤 기사를 바로잡아 주었으면 하네.\""]], [["“To what do you refer?", "\"무얼 두고 하는 말인가?"], ["But pray sit down.”", "한데 우선 좀 앉게.\""]], [["“Thank you,”", "\"고맙네.\""], ["said Albert, with a cold and formal bow.", "알베르가 차갑고 격식 차린 인사를 건네며 말했다."]], [["“Will you now have the kindness to explain the nature of the statement which has displeased you?”", "\"그럼 이제 자네 비위를 거스른 그 기사가 어떤 것인지 좀 설명해 주겠나?\""]], [["“An announcement has been made which implicates the honor of a member of my family.”", "\"내 가문의 한 사람의 명예에 흠집을 내는 기사가 실렸네.\""]], [["“What is it?”", "\"그게 뭔가?\""], ["said Beauchamp, much surprised; “surely you must be mistaken.”", "보샹이 몹시 놀라며 말했다. \"분명 자네가 뭔가 잘못 안 거겠지.\""]], [["“The story sent you from Yanina.”", "\"야니나에서 자네에게 보내온 그 기사 말일세.\""]], [["“Yanina?”", "\"야니나?\""]], [["“Yes; really you appear to be totally ignorant of the cause which brings me here.”", "\"그래. 정말이지 자네는 내가 여기 온 까닭을 전혀 모르는 모양이군.\""]], [["“Such is really the case, I assure you, upon my honor! Baptiste, give me yesterday’s paper,”", "\"맹세코 정말 그렇다네! 바티스트, 어제 신문 좀 가져오게.\""], ["cried Beauchamp.", "보샹이 소리쳤다."]], [["“Here, I have brought mine with me,” replied Albert.", "\"여기, 내 것을 가져왔네.\" 알베르가 대답했다."]], [["Beauchamp took the paper, and read the article to which Albert pointed in an undertone.", "보샹은 신문을 받아 들고, 알베르가 가리킨 기사를 낮은 목소리로 읽었다."]], [["“You see it is a serious annoyance,”", "\"보다시피 이건 심각한 모욕일세.\""], ["said Morcerf, when Beauchamp had finished the perusal of the paragraph.", "보샹이 그 기사를 다 읽고 났을 때 모르세르가 말했다."]], [["“Is the officer referred to a relation of yours, then?” demanded the journalist.", "\"그럼 여기 언급된 그 장교가 자네 친척이란 말인가?\" 기자가 물었다."]], [["“Yes,” said Albert, blushing.", "\"그렇다네.\" 알베르가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."]], [["“Well, what do you wish me to do for you?”", "\"그래, 내가 자네를 위해 무얼 해 주면 좋겠나?\""], ["said Beauchamp mildly.", "보샹이 부드럽게 말했다."]], [["“My dear Beauchamp, I wish you to contradict this statement.”", "\"보샹, 이 기사를 부인해 주었으면 하네.\""], ["Beauchamp looked at Albert with a benevolent expression.", "보샹은 인자한 표정으로 알베르를 바라보았다."]], [["“Come,”", "\"이보게,\""], ["said he, “this matter will want a good deal of talking over; a retractation is always a serious thing, you know.", "그가 말했다. \"이건 한참 이야기를 나눠 봐야 할 문제일세. 알다시피 철회란 늘 중대한 일이지."], ["Sit down, and I will read it again.”", "앉게, 내가 한 번 더 읽어 보겠네.\""]], [["Albert resumed his seat, and Beauchamp read, with more attention than at first, the lines denounced by his friend.", "알베르는 다시 자리에 앉았고, 보샹은 친구가 규탄한 그 글줄을 처음보다 더 주의 깊게 읽었다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"자,\""], ["said Albert in a determined tone, “you see that your paper has insulted a member of my family, and I insist on a retractation being made.”", "알베르가 단호한 어조로 말했다. \"보다시피 자네 신문이 내 가문의 한 사람을 모욕했네. 나는 철회를 요구하네.\""]], [["“You insist?”", "\"요구한다고?\""]], [["“Yes, I insist.”", "\"그래, 요구하네.\""]], [["“Permit me to remind you that you are not in the Chamber, my dear viscount.”", "\"여긴 의회가 아니라는 걸 일깨워 줘도 되겠나, 자작.\""]], [["“Nor do I wish to be there,”", "\"의회에 있고 싶은 마음도 없네.\""], ["replied the young man, rising.", "청년이 자리에서 일어서며 대답했다."], ["“I repeat that I am determined to have the announcement of yesterday contradicted. You have known me long enough,”", "\"다시 말하지만, 나는 어제의 기사가 부인되게 하기로 마음먹었네. 자네는 나를 충분히 오래 알아 왔고,\""], ["continued Albert, biting his lips convulsively, for he saw that Beauchamp’s anger was beginning to rise,—“you have been my friend, and therefore sufficiently intimate with me to be aware that I am likely to maintain my resolution on this point.”", "알베르가 입술을 발작하듯 깨물며 말을 이었다. 보샹의 노여움이 일기 시작하는 것을 보았기 때문이다. \"자네는 내 벗이었고, 그러니 내가 이 점에 대한 결심을 끝내 지킬 사람이라는 걸 알 만큼 충분히 가까운 사이지.\""]], [["“If I have been your friend, Morcerf, your present manner of speaking would almost lead me to forget that I ever bore that title.", "\"내가 자네 벗이었다면, 모르세르, 지금 자네의 그 말투를 들으니 내가 그 이름으로 불린 적이 있다는 사실마저 잊어버릴 지경일세. 하지만 잠깐만, 우리 화내지는 마세."], ["But wait a moment, do not let us get angry, or at least not yet.", "적어도 아직은 말이야. 자네는 잔뜩 약이 오르고 속이 상했군."], ["You are irritated and vexed—tell me how this Fernand is related to you?”", "말해 보게, 이 페르낭이라는 사람이 자네와 어떻게 되는 사이인가?\""]], [["“He is merely my father,”", "\"그저 내 아버지일 뿐이지.\""], ["said Albert—“M.", "알베르가 말했다."], ["Fernand Mondego, Count of Morcerf, an old soldier who has fought in twenty battles and whose honorable scars they would denounce as badges of disgrace.”", "\"페르낭 몽데고 씨, 모르세르 백작, 스무 차례나 전장에서 싸우신 노병이지. 그분의 명예로운 흉터를 두고 저들은 치욕의 낙인이라 떠벌이는 걸세.\""]], [["“Is it your father?”", "\"자네 아버지라고?\""], ["said Beauchamp; “that is quite another thing.", "보샹이 말했다. \"그렇다면 이야기가 전혀 다르지."], ["Then I can well understand your indignation, my dear Albert.", "그렇다면 자네의 분노도 능히 이해가 가네, 알베르."], ["I will look at it again;”", "다시 한번 보겠네.\""], ["and he read the paragraph for the third time, laying a stress on each word as he proceeded.", "그러고는 한 마디 한 마디에 힘을 주어 가며 그 기사를 세 번째로 읽었다."], ["“But the paper nowhere identifies this Fernand with your father.” 40100m", "\"하지만 신문은 어디에서도 이 페르낭을 자네 아버지와 같은 사람으로 못박지 않았네.\""]], [["“No; but the connection will be seen by others, and therefore I will have the article contradicted.”", "\"그렇지. 하지만 다른 사람들은 그 연결을 알아챌 걸세. 그러니 나는 이 기사가 부인되게 하겠네.\""]], [["At the words _I will_, Beauchamp steadily raised his eyes to Albert’s countenance, and then as gradually lowering them, he remained thoughtful for a few moments.", "'하겠네'라는 말에 보샹은 알베르의 얼굴로 눈을 천천히 들어 올렸다가, 다시 그만큼 천천히 내리깔고는, 잠시 생각에 잠긴 채 잠자코 있었다."]], [["“You will retract this assertion, will you not, Beauchamp?” said Albert with increased though stifled anger.", "\"이 주장을 철회해 주겠지, 보샹?\" 알베르가 한층 커진, 그러나 억눌린 분노로 말했다."]], [["“Yes,” replied Beauchamp.", "\"그러지.\" 보샹이 대답했다."]], [["“Immediately?” said Albert.", "\"당장?\" 알베르가 말했다."]], [["“When I am convinced that the statement is false.”", "\"그 주장이 거짓이라는 확신이 서면.\""]], [["“What?”", "\"뭐라고?\""]], [["“The thing is worth looking into, and I will take pains to investigate the matter thoroughly.”", "\"이건 들여다볼 가치가 있는 일이니, 내가 공을 들여 철저히 알아보겠네.\""]], [["“But what is there to investigate, sir?”", "\"하지만 알아볼 게 뭐가 있단 말인가?\""], ["said Albert, enraged beyond measure at Beauchamp’s last remark.", "보샹의 마지막 말에 분노가 끝 간 데까지 치민 알베르가 말했다."], ["“If you do not believe that it is my father, say so immediately; and if, on the contrary, you believe it to be him, state your reasons for doing so.”", "\"그가 내 아버지가 아니라고 믿는다면 당장 그렇다고 말하게. 반대로 그가 내 아버지라고 믿는다면, 그렇게 믿는 까닭을 대게.\""]], [["Beauchamp looked at Albert with the smile which was so peculiar to him, and which in its numerous modifications served to express every varied emotion of his mind.", "보샹은 그 특유의 미소를 띠며 알베르를 바라보았다. 그 미소는 갖가지로 변주되며 그의 온갖 다양한 감정을 다 드러내 보이곤 했다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"이보게,\""], ["replied he, “if you came to me with the idea of demanding satisfaction, you should have gone at once to the point, and not have entertained me with the idle conversation to which I have been patiently listening for the last half hour.", "그가 대답했다. \"결투를 청할 생각으로 내게 온 거라면, 곧장 본론으로 들어왔어야지, 내가 지난 반 시간 동안 잠자코 들어 준 그 부질없는 이야기로 나를 붙들어 두지는 말았어야 했네."], ["Am I to put this construction on your visit?”", "자네 방문을 그런 뜻으로 새겨도 되겠나?\""]], [["“Yes, if you will not consent to retract that infamous calumny.”", "\"그래. 자네가 그 파렴치한 중상을 철회하는 데 동의하지 않는다면.\""]], [["“Wait a moment—no threats, if you please, M.", "\"잠깐만. 협박은 사양하네, 페르낭 몽데고 씨, 모르세르 자작."], ["Fernand Mondego, Vicomte de Morcerf; I never allow them from my enemies, and therefore shall not put up with them from my friends.", "나는 적들한테서도 협박을 용납한 적이 없으니, 벗한테서라고 참아 넘기지는 않겠네."], ["You insist on my contradicting the article relating to General Fernand, an article with which, I assure you on my word of honor, I had nothing whatever to do?”", "자네는 페르낭 장군에 관한 기사를, 명예를 걸고 맹세하건대 내가 전혀 손대지 않은 그 기사를, 내가 부인하라고 요구하는 거지?\""]], [["“Yes, I insist on it,”", "\"그래, 요구하네.\""], ["said Albert, whose mind was beginning to get bewildered with the excitement of his feelings.", "알베르가 말했다. 그의 마음은 격해진 감정에 점점 갈피를 잃어 가고 있었다."]], [["“And if I refuse to retract, you wish to fight, do you?”", "\"그래서 내가 철회를 거절하면, 자네는 싸우겠다는 거지?\""], ["said Beauchamp in a calm tone.", "보샹이 차분한 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Yes,” replied Albert, raising his voice.", "\"그래.\" 알베르가 목소리를 높이며 대답했다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"좋네,\""], ["said Beauchamp, “here is my answer, my dear sir.", "보샹이 말했다. \"그럼 이게 내 대답일세, 이보게."], ["The article was not inserted by me—I was not even aware of it; but you have, by the step you have taken, called my attention to the paragraph in question, and it will remain until it shall be either contradicted or confirmed by someone who has a right to do so.”", "그 기사는 내가 실은 게 아니네. 나는 그게 실린 줄도 몰랐어. 하지만 자네가 취한 그 행동 때문에 내 주의가 그 기사로 쏠렸으니, 그것은 그럴 자격이 있는 누군가가 부인하거나 확인해 줄 때까지 그대로 남아 있을 걸세.\""]], [["“Sir,”", "\"이보게,\""], ["said Albert, rising, “I will do myself the honor of sending my seconds to you, and you will be kind enough to arrange with them the place of meeting and the weapons.”", "알베르가 일어서며 말했다. \"내 입회인들을 자네에게 보내는 영광을 누리겠네. 자네는 그들과 만남의 장소와 무기를 정해 주면 되겠네.\""]], [["“Certainly, my dear sir.”", "\"물론이지, 이보게.\""]], [["“And this evening, if you please, or tomorrow at the latest, we will meet.”", "\"오늘 저녁이라도, 괜찮다면, 늦어도 내일은 만나세.\""]], [["“No, no, I will be on the ground at the proper time; but in my opinion (and I have a right to dictate the preliminaries, as it is I who have received the provocation)—in my opinion the time ought not to be yet.", "\"아니, 아니야. 나는 정해진 때에 그 자리에 나가겠네. 하지만 내 생각에는 — 도발을 받은 쪽이 나이니 사전 조건을 정할 권리는 내게 있네 — 내 생각에는 아직 그때가 아닐세."], ["I know you to be well skilled in the management of the sword, while I am only moderately so; I know, too, that you are a good marksman—there we are about equal.", "나는 자네가 검술에 능하다는 걸 알고, 나는 그저 어지간한 정도지. 자네가 명사수라는 것도 알아. 그건 우리가 엇비슷하군."], ["I know that a duel between us two would be a serious affair, because you are brave, and I am brave also.", "우리 둘 사이의 결투가 중대한 일이 되리라는 것도 알지. 자네가 용감하고, 나 또한 용감하니까."], ["I do not therefore wish either to kill you, or to be killed myself without a cause.", "그러니 나는 까닭 없이 자네를 죽이고 싶지도, 까닭 없이 내가 죽고 싶지도 않네."], ["Now, I am going to put a question to you, and one very much to the purpose too.", "자, 이제 자네에게 한 가지 묻겠네. 아주 요긴한 물음이지."], ["Do you insist on this retractation so far as to kill me if I do not make it, although I have repeated more than once, and affirmed on my honor, that I was ignorant of the thing with which you charge me, and although I still declare that it is impossible for anyone but you to recognize the Count of Morcerf under the name of Fernand?”", "자네는, 내가 그 일에 대해 아무것도 몰랐다고 한 번 이상 거듭 말하고 명예를 걸어 단언했는데도, 또 자네 말고는 누구도 페르낭이라는 이름 아래 모르세르 백작을 알아볼 수 없다고 내가 여전히 분명히 말하는데도, 내가 철회하지 않으면 나를 죽이겠다고까지 이 철회를 고집하는 건가?\""]], [["“I maintain my original resolution.”", "\"나는 처음 먹은 결심을 굽히지 않겠네.\""]], [["“Very well, my dear sir; then I consent to cut throats with you.", "\"좋네, 이보게. 그럼 자네와 목을 베고 베이는 데 동의하지."], ["But I require three weeks’ preparation; at the end of that time I shall come and say to you, ‘The assertion is false, and I retract it,’ or ‘The assertion is true,’ when I shall immediately draw the sword from its sheath, or the pistols from the case, whichever you please.”", "하지만 나는 삼 주의 준비 기간이 필요하네. 그 기간이 끝나면 자네에게 와서 '그 주장은 거짓이며, 나는 그것을 철회한다'고 하거나, '그 주장은 사실이다'라고 하겠네. 후자라면 그 즉시 칼집에서 검을, 아니면 상자에서 권총을, 자네가 원하는 대로 뽑겠네.\""]], [["“Three weeks!”", "\"삼 주라!\""], ["cried Albert; “they will pass as slowly as three centuries when I am all the time suffering dishonor.”", "알베르가 외쳤다. \"내가 그동안 내내 치욕을 견디는데, 그 삼 주는 삼백 년처럼 더디게 흐를 걸세.\""]], [["“Had you continued to remain on amicable terms with me, I should have said, ‘Patience, my friend;’ but you have constituted yourself my enemy, therefore I say, ‘What does that signify to me, sir?’”", "\"자네가 나와 우호적인 사이로 남아 있었다면, 나는 '참게, 친구' 하고 말했을 걸세. 하지만 자네 스스로 나를 적으로 만들었으니, 나는 이렇게 말하겠네. '그게 나와 무슨 상관인가?'\""]], [["“Well, let it be three weeks then,”", "\"좋네, 그럼 삼 주로 하지.\""], ["said Morcerf; “but remember, at the expiration of that time no delay or subterfuge will justify you in——”", "모르세르가 말했다. \"하지만 명심하게. 그 기한이 다하면 어떤 지체나 핑계로도 자네는……\""]], [["“M. Albert de Morcerf,”", "\"알베르 드 모르세르 씨,\""], ["said Beauchamp, rising in his turn, “I cannot throw you out of window for three weeks—that is to say, for twenty-four days to come—nor have you any right to split my skull open till that time has elapsed.", "보샹이 이번에는 자기가 일어서며 말했다. \"나는 삼 주 동안은 — 그러니까 앞으로 스물나흘 동안은 — 자네를 창밖으로 내던질 수 없고, 자네 또한 그때가 지나기 전에는 내 머리통을 쪼갤 권리가 없네."], ["Today is the 29th of August; the 21st of September will, therefore, be the conclusion of the term agreed on, and till that time arrives—and it is the advice of a gentleman which I am about to give you—till then we will refrain from growling and barking like two dogs chained within sight of each other.”", "오늘이 8월 29일이니, 9월 21일이 우리가 합의한 기한의 끝이 되겠군. 그때가 오기까지는 — 그리고 지금부터 내가 자네에게 하는 충고는 신사로서 하는 충고일세 — 그때까지 우리, 서로 빤히 보이는 곳에 묶인 두 마리 개처럼 으르렁거리고 짖어 대는 짓은 삼가세.\""]], [["When he had concluded his speech, Beauchamp bowed coldly to Albert, turned his back upon him, and went to the press-room.", "말을 마친 보샹은 알베르에게 쌀쌀맞게 절하고는 등을 돌려 인쇄실로 가 버렸다."], ["Albert vented his anger on a pile of newspapers, which he sent flying all over the office by switching them violently with his stick; after which ebullition he departed—not, however, without walking several times to the door of the press-room, as if he had half a mind to enter.", "알베르는 신문 더미에 분풀이를 했는데, 지팡이로 사정없이 후려쳐 사무실 곳곳으로 날려 버렸다. 그러고 나서야 그는 떠났다. 그러나 마치 들어갈까 말까 망설이듯, 인쇄실 문 앞을 몇 번이나 오가고 나서였다."]], [["40104m\nWhile Albert was lashing the front of his carriage in the same manner that he had the newspapers which were the innocent agents of his discomfiture, as he was crossing the barrier he perceived Morrel, who was walking with a quick step and a bright eye.", "알베르가, 자기 낭패의 죄 없는 빌미였던 신문들을 후려쳤던 그 방식 그대로 마차 앞부분을 후려치며, 시 경계를 지날 무렵, 그는 빠른 걸음에 눈을 반짝이며 걸어가는 모렐을 알아보았다."], ["He was passing the Chinese Baths, and appeared to have come from the direction of the Porte Saint-Martin, and to be going towards the Madeleine.", "그는 중국식 목욕탕을 지나는 중이었는데, 생마르탱 문 쪽에서 와 마들렌 쪽으로 가는 듯했다."]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["said Morcerf, “there goes a happy man!”", "모르세르가 말했다. \"저기 행복한 사람이 가는군!\""], ["And it so happened Albert was not mistaken in his opinion.", "그리고 공교롭게도 알베르의 그 짐작은 틀리지 않았다."]], [["# Chapter 79. The Lemonade", "# Chapter 79. The Lemonade"]], [["Morrel was, in fact, very happy. M.", "사실 모렐은 무척 기뻐하고 있었다."], ["Noirtier had just sent for him, and he was in such haste to know the reason of his doing so that he had not stopped to take a cab, placing infinitely more dependence on his own two legs than on the four legs of a cab-horse.", "노르티에 씨가 방금 그를 부른 참이었고, 그 까닭이 어찌나 궁금했던지 그는 삯마차를 잡으려고 멈춰 서지도 않았다. 삯마차를 끄는 말 네 다리보다 제 두 다리를 한없이 더 믿었기 때문이다."], ["He had therefore set off at a furious rate from the Rue Meslay, and was hastening with rapid strides in the direction of the Faubourg Saint-Honoré.", "그래서 그는 멜레가에서부터 맹렬한 속도로 출발해, 포부르생토노레 방면으로 빠른 걸음을 재촉했다."]], [["Morrel advanced with a firm, manly tread, and poor Barrois followed him as he best might.", "모렐은 단단하고 사내다운 걸음으로 앞서 나아갔고, 가엾은 바루아는 그 뒤를 힘닿는 대로 따랐다."], ["Morrel was only thirty-one, Barrois was sixty years of age; Morrel was deeply in love, and Barrois was dying with heat and exertion.", "모렐은 겨우 서른한 살이었고, 바루아는 예순이었다. 모렐은 깊은 사랑에 빠져 있었고, 바루아는 더위와 무리한 힘 때문에 죽을 지경이었다."], ["These two men, thus opposed in age and interests, resembled two parts of a triangle, presenting the extremes of separation, yet nevertheless possessing their point of union.", "나이도 관심사도 이렇게 동떨어진 두 사람은 마치 삼각형의 두 변과 같아서, 서로 가장 멀리 떨어진 양 끝을 이루면서도 그럼에도 만나는 한 점을 지니고 있었다."], ["This point of union was Noirtier, and it was he who had just sent for Morrel, with the request that the latter would lose no time in coming to him—a command which Morrel obeyed to the letter, to the great discomfiture of Barrois.", "그 만나는 점이 바로 노르티에였다. 방금 모렐을 부른 사람이 바로 그였고, 지체 없이 와 달라는 청이었다. 모렐은 그 청을 글자 그대로 따랐으니, 바루아로서는 여간 곤혹스러운 일이 아니었다."], ["On arriving at the house, Morrel was not even out of breath, for love lends wings to our desires; but Barrois, who had long forgotten what it was to love, was sorely fatigued by the expedition he had been constrained to use.", "집에 다다랐을 때 모렐은 숨조차 차지 않았다. 사랑은 우리 욕망에 날개를 달아 주는 법이니까. 그러나 사랑이 무엇인지 잊은 지 오래인 바루아는, 어쩔 수 없이 따라야 했던 그 강행군 탓에 몹시 지쳐 있었다."]], [["The old servant introduced Morrel by a private entrance, closed the door of the study, and soon the rustling of a dress announced the arrival of Valentine.", "늙은 하인은 모렐을 비밀 출입구로 안내한 뒤 서재 문을 닫았다. 곧이어 옷자락 스치는 소리가 발랑틴이 왔음을 알렸다."], ["She looked marvellously beautiful in her deep mourning dress, and Morrel experienced such intense delight in gazing upon her that he felt as if he could almost have dispensed with the conversation of her grandfather.", "짙은 상복 차림의 그녀는 놀랍도록 아름다웠고, 모렐은 그녀를 바라보는 것만으로도 어찌나 강렬한 기쁨을 느꼈던지, 할아버지와의 대화 따위는 없어도 좋을 것만 같았다."]], [["But the easy-chair of the old man was heard rolling along the floor, and he soon made his appearance in the room.", "그러나 노인의 안락의자가 마룻바닥을 구르는 소리가 들리더니, 곧 그가 방 안에 모습을 드러냈다."], ["Noirtier acknowledged by a look of extreme kindness and benevolence the thanks which Morrel lavished on him for his timely intervention on behalf of Valentine and himself—an intervention which had saved them from despair.", "노르티에는 더없이 자상하고 다정한 눈빛으로, 발랑틴과 자신을 위해 때맞춰 나서 준 데에 모렐이 쏟아 낸 감사 인사를 받아들였다. 그 개입이 두 사람을 절망에서 건져 주었던 것이다."], ["Morrel then cast on the invalid an interrogative look as to the new favor which he designed to bestow on him.", "이어서 모렐은 노인이 이번에는 또 어떤 은혜를 베풀려 하는지 묻는 눈빛을 던졌다."], ["Valentine was sitting at a little distance from them, timidly awaiting the moment when she should be obliged to speak.", "발랑틴은 두 사람에게서 조금 떨어진 자리에 앉아, 말을 꺼내야 할 순간을 머뭇거리며 기다리고 있었다."], ["Noirtier fixed his eyes on her.", "노르티에가 그녀에게 시선을 고정했다."]], [["“Am I to say what you told me?”", "\"할아버지가 제게 하신 말씀을 그대로 전할까요?\""], ["asked Valentine.", "발랑틴이 물었다."], ["Noirtier made a sign that she was to do so.", "노르티에가 그렇게 하라는 신호를 보냈다."]], [["“Monsieur Morrel,”", "\"모렐 씨,\""], ["said Valentine to the young man, who was regarding her with the most intense interest, “my grandfather, M.", "발랑틴이 더없이 진지한 관심으로 자기를 바라보는 젊은이에게 말했다."], ["Noirtier, had a thousand things to say, which he told me three days ago; and now, he has sent for you, that I may repeat them to you.", "\"제 할아버지 노르티에 씨께서 사흘 전에 제게 하실 말씀이 아주 많으셨어요. 그래서 이제 당신을 부르신 거예요. 제가 그 말씀을 당신께 옮겨 드리도록요."], ["I will repeat them, then; and since he has chosen me as his interpreter, I will be faithful to the trust, and will not alter a word of his intentions.”", "그러니 이제 전해 드리겠어요. 할아버지께서 저를 통역으로 택하셨으니, 저는 그 신뢰에 충실하게, 그분의 뜻을 한마디도 바꾸지 않고 그대로 전하겠어요.\""]], [["“Oh, I am listening with the greatest impatience,”", "\"아, 더없이 애가 타도록 듣고 있습니다.\""], ["replied the young man; “speak, I beg of you.”", "젊은이가 대답했다. \"부디 말씀해 주십시오.\""]], [["Valentine cast down her eyes; this was a good omen for Morrel, for he knew that nothing but happiness could have the power of thus overcoming Valentine.", "발랑틴이 눈을 내리깔았다. 그것은 모렐에게 좋은 징조였다. 발랑틴을 그렇게 만들 수 있는 것은 오직 행복뿐임을 그는 알고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["“My grandfather intends leaving this house,”", "\"할아버지께서는 이 집을 떠나실 작정이세요.\""], ["said she, “and Barrois is looking out for suitable apartments for him in another.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"바루아가 다른 집에 적당한 거처를 알아보고 있고요.\""]], [["“But you, Mademoiselle de Villefort,—you, who are necessary to M. Noirtier’s happiness——”", "\"하지만 당신은요, 빌포르 양, 노르티에 씨의 행복에 없어서는 안 될 당신은……\""]], [["“I?”", "\"저요?\""], ["interrupted Valentine; “I shall not leave my grandfather,—that is an understood thing between us.", "발랑틴이 말을 가로챘다. \"저는 할아버지 곁을 떠나지 않을 거예요. 그건 저희끼리 이미 정해진 일이에요."], ["My apartment will be close to his. Now, M.", "제 방은 할아버지 방 바로 옆에 둘 거고요."], ["de Villefort must either give his consent to this plan or his refusal; in the first case, I shall leave directly, and in the second, I shall wait till I am of age, which will be in about ten months.", "이제 빌포르 씨가 이 계획에 찬성하시든 반대하시든 둘 중 하나예요. 찬성하시면 저는 곧바로 떠날 거고, 반대하시면 성년이 될 때까지 기다릴 거예요. 그게 한 열 달쯤 남았어요."], ["Then I shall be free, I shall have an independent fortune, and”—", "그러면 저는 자유로운 몸이 되고, 제 몫의 재산도 갖게 되고, 그리고……\""]], [["“And what?” demanded Morrel.", "\"그리고 어떻게 됩니까?\" 모렐이 물었다."]], [["“And with my grandfather’s consent I shall fulfil the promise which I have made you.”", "\"그리고 할아버지의 허락을 받아, 당신께 드린 약속을 지킬 거예요.\""]], [["Valentine pronounced these last few words in such a low tone, that nothing but Morrel’s intense interest in what she was saying could have enabled him to hear them.", "발랑틴은 이 마지막 몇 마디를 어찌나 나직하게 내뱉었던지, 그녀의 말에 온 신경을 기울이고 있던 모렐이 아니었다면 도저히 알아듣지 못했을 정도였다."]], [["“Have I not explained your wishes, grandpapa?”", "\"제가 할아버지의 뜻을 제대로 전했나요, 할아버지?\""], ["said Valentine, addressing Noirtier.", "발랑틴이 노르티에를 향해 물었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇다.\""], ["looked the old man.", "노인의 눈빛이 그렇게 말했다."]], [["“Once under my grandfather’s roof, M. Morrel can visit me in the presence of my good and worthy protector, if we still feel that the union we contemplated will be likely to insure our future comfort and happiness; in that case I shall expect M.", "\"일단 할아버지 댁에 살게 되면, 우리가 마음에 그리던 결합이 앞으로의 평안과 행복을 보장해 주리라는 믿음이 여전히 남아 있을 경우, 모렐 씨는 선량하고 훌륭하신 제 보호자가 계신 자리에서 저를 찾아오실 수 있어요. 그렇게 되면 저는 모렐 씨가 직접 제 손을 청하러 오시기를 기다리겠어요."], ["Morrel to come and claim me at my own hands. But, alas, I have heard it said that hearts inflamed by obstacles to their desire grew cold in time of security; I trust we shall never find it so in our experience!”", "하지만, 아아, 욕망을 가로막는 장애물로 타오르던 마음도 안전해지면 시간이 지나 식어 버린다는 말을 들은 적이 있어요. 부디 우리는 결코 그런 일을 겪지 않기를 바라요!\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"아,\""], ["cried Morrel, almost tempted to throw himself on his knees before Noirtier and Valentine, and to adore them as two superior beings, “what have I ever done in my life to merit such unbounded happiness?”", "모렐은 하마터면 노르티에와 발랑틴 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 그 두 사람을 두 분의 더 높은 존재로 우러러볼 뻔했다. \"제가 평생 무슨 일을 했기에 이토록 끝없는 행복을 누릴 자격이 있단 말입니까?\""]], [["“Until that time,”", "\"그때까지,\" 젊은 아가씨가 차분하고 침착한 목소리로 말을 이었다."], ["continued the young girl in a calm and self-possessed tone of voice, “we will conform to circumstances, and be guided by the wishes of our friends, so long as those wishes do not tend finally to separate us; in a word, and I repeat it, because it expresses all I wish to convey,—we will wait.”", "\"우리는 형편에 따르고, 친지들의 뜻에 이끌릴 거예요. 다만 그 뜻이 끝내 우리를 갈라놓으려 하지 않는 한에서요. 한마디로, 제가 전하고 싶은 말을 다 담고 있기에 다시 한번 말씀드리자면, 우리는 기다릴 거예요.\""]], [["“And I swear to make all the sacrifices which this word imposes, sir,”", "\"그 말이 지우는 모든 희생을, 저는 기꺼이 치르겠다고 맹세합니다.\""], ["said Morrel, “not only with resignation, but with cheerfulness.”", "모렐이 말했다. \"체념해서가 아니라, 즐거운 마음으로요.\""]], [["“Therefore,”", "\"그러니,\" 발랑틴이 막시밀리앙을 장난스럽게 바라보며 말을 이었다."], ["continued Valentine, looking playfully at Maximilian, “no more inconsiderate actions—no more rash projects; for you surely would not wish to compromise one who from this day regards herself as destined, honorably and happily, to bear your name?”", "\"더 이상 경솔한 행동은 안 돼요. 무모한 계획도 안 되고요. 오늘부터 당신의 이름을 떳떳하고 행복하게 지니도록 정해진 사람이라 스스로 여기는 이를, 당신은 설마 위태롭게 하고 싶진 않으시겠죠?\""]], [["Morrel looked obedience to her commands.", "모렐은 그 명령에 순종하는 눈빛을 보냈다."], ["Noirtier regarded the lovers with a look of ineffable tenderness, while Barrois, who had remained in the room in the character of a man privileged to know everything that passed, smiled on the youthful couple as he wiped the perspiration from his bald forehead.", "노르티에는 이루 말할 수 없는 다정한 눈길로 두 연인을 바라보았고, 그동안 이 방에서 벌어지는 모든 일을 알 자격을 지닌 사람의 자격으로 방에 남아 있던 바루아는, 벗어진 이마에서 땀을 닦으며 젊은 두 사람을 보고 미소 지었다."]], [["“How hot you look, my good Barrois,”", "\"몹시 더워 보이네요, 우리 바루아.\""], ["said Valentine.", "발랑틴이 말했다."]], [["“Ah, I have been running very fast, mademoiselle, but I must do M.", "\"아, 무척 빨리 달려왔거든요, 아가씨. 하지만 모렐 씨 얘기를 정직하게 해 드려야겠네요."], ["Morrel the justice to say that he ran still faster.”", "그분은 저보다 더 빨리 달리셨답니다.\""]], [["Noirtier directed their attention to a waiter, on which was placed a decanter containing lemonade and a glass.", "노르티에는 두 사람의 주의를 쟁반 쪽으로 돌렸다. 그 위에는 레모네이드가 담긴 유리병 하나와 잔 하나가 놓여 있었다."], ["The decanter was nearly full, with the exception of a little, which had been already drunk by M. Noirtier.", "병은 노르티에 씨가 이미 조금 마신 것을 빼고는 거의 가득 차 있었다."]], [["“Come, Barrois,”", "\"자, 바루아,\""], ["said the young girl, “take some of this lemonade; I see you are coveting a good draught of it.”", "젊은 아가씨가 말했다. \"이 레모네이드 좀 마셔요. 한 모금 들이켜고 싶어서 군침을 흘리고 있잖아요.\""]], [["“The fact is, mademoiselle,”", "\"실은요, 아가씨,\""], ["said Barrois, “I am dying with thirst, and since you are so kind as to offer it me, I cannot say I should at all object to drinking your health in a glass of it.”", "바루아가 말했다. \"목이 타서 죽을 지경이었어요. 이렇게 친절히 권해 주시니, 한 잔 들이켜며 아가씨의 건강을 빌어도 좋을 것 같습니다.\""]], [["“Take some, then, and come back immediately.”", "\"그럼 좀 마시고, 곧장 돌아와요.\""]], [["Barrois took away the waiter, and hardly was he outside the door, which in his haste he forgot to shut, than they saw him throw back his head and empty to the very dregs the glass which Valentine had filled.", "바루아는 쟁반을 들고 나갔다. 그가 서두르느라 미처 닫지 못한 문 밖으로 나서기가 무섭게, 두 사람은 그가 고개를 뒤로 젖히고 발랑틴이 채워 준 잔을 마지막 한 방울까지 비우는 모습을 보았다."], ["Valentine and Morrel were exchanging their adieux in the presence of Noirtier when a ring was heard at the door-bell.", "발랑틴과 모렐이 노르티에 앞에서 작별 인사를 나누고 있을 때, 현관 초인종 소리가 들렸다."], ["It was the signal of a visit.", "손님이 왔다는 신호였다."], ["Valentine looked at her watch.", "발랑틴이 시계를 보았다."]], [["“It is past noon,”", "\"정오가 지났네요.\""], ["said she, “and today is Saturday; I dare say it is the doctor, grandpapa.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"오늘은 토요일이고요. 의사 선생님이실 거예요, 할아버지.\""]], [["Noirtier looked his conviction that she was right in her supposition.", "노르티에는 그녀의 짐작이 옳다는 확신을 눈빛으로 드러냈다."]], [["“He will come in here, and M. Morrel had better go,—do you not think so, grandpapa?”", "\"선생님이 이리로 오실 테니, 모렐 씨는 그만 가시는 게 좋겠어요. 그렇지 않나요, 할아버지?\""]], [["“Yes,” signed the old man.", "\"그렇다.\" 노인이 신호를 보냈다."]], [["“Barrois,”", "\"바루아,\""], ["cried Valentine, “Barrois!”", "발랑틴이 불렀다. \"바루아!\""]], [["“I am coming, mademoiselle,” replied he.", "\"갑니다, 아가씨.\" 그가 대답했다."]], [["“Barrois will open the door for you,”", "\"바루아가 문을 열어 드릴 거예요.\""], ["said Valentine, addressing Morrel.", "발랑틴이 모렐에게 말했다."], ["“And now remember one thing, Monsieur Officer, that my grandfather commands you not to take any rash or ill-advised step which would be likely to compromise our happiness.”", "\"그리고 한 가지 잊지 마세요, 장교님. 할아버지께서는 우리의 행복을 위태롭게 할 만한 무모하거나 경솔한 짓을 절대 하지 말라고 당신께 명하셨어요.\""]], [["40108m\n“I promised him to wait,”", "\"기다리겠다고 그분께 약속했습니다.\""], ["replied Morrel; “and I will wait.”", "모렐이 대답했다. \"그러니 기다리겠습니다.\""]], [["At this moment Barrois entered. “Who rang?”", "바로 그때 바루아가 들어왔다. \"누가 초인종을 울렸지?\""], ["asked Valentine.", "발랑틴이 물었다."]], [["“Doctor d’Avrigny,” said Barrois, staggering as if he would fall.", "\"다브리니 선생님이십니다.\" 바루아가 쓰러질 듯 비틀거리며 말했다."]], [["“What is the matter, Barrois?”", "\"무슨 일이에요, 바루아?\""], ["said Valentine.", "발랑틴이 물었다."], ["The old man did not answer, but looked at his master with wild staring eyes, while with his cramped hand he grasped a piece of furniture to enable him to stand upright.", "늙은 하인은 대답하지 않고, 사납게 부릅뜬 눈으로 주인을 바라보았다. 한편 경련이 인 손으로는 똑바로 서 있으려고 가구 한 귀퉁이를 붙잡았다."]], [["“He is going to fall!” cried Morrel.", "\"쓰러지겠어요!\" 모렐이 외쳤다."]], [["The rigors which had attacked Barrois gradually increased, the features of the face became quite altered, and the convulsive movement of the muscles appeared to indicate the approach of a most serious nervous disorder.", "바루아를 덮친 떨림은 차츰 심해졌고, 얼굴 생김새가 완전히 일그러졌으며, 근육의 경련은 가장 심각한 신경 발작이 다가오고 있음을 알리는 듯했다."], ["Noirtier, seeing Barrois in this pitiable condition, showed by his looks all the various emotions of sorrow and sympathy which can animate the heart of man.", "노르티에는 바루아의 그 처참한 꼴을 보며, 인간의 마음을 움직일 수 있는 온갖 슬픔과 연민의 감정을 눈빛으로 모두 드러냈다."], ["Barrois made some steps towards his master.", "바루아가 주인을 향해 몇 걸음 내디뎠다."]], [["“Ah, sir,”", "\"아, 주인님,\""], ["said he, “tell me what is the matter with me. I am suffering—I cannot see.", "그가 말했다. \"제게 무슨 일이 일어난 건지 말씀해 주세요. 괴로워요."], ["A thousand fiery darts are piercing my brain.", "앞이 안 보여요. 천 개의 불화살이 제 머리를 꿰뚫고 있어요."], ["Ah, don’t touch me, pray don’t.”", "아, 절 건드리지 마세요, 제발요.\""]], [["By this time his haggard eyes had the appearance of being ready to start from their sockets; his head fell back, and the lower extremities of the body began to stiffen.", "이때 그의 퀭한 두 눈은 금방이라도 눈구멍에서 튀어나올 것만 같았다. 머리가 뒤로 넘어갔고, 몸 아래쪽이 뻣뻣하게 굳기 시작했다. 발랑틴이 공포에 찬 비명을 질렀다."], ["Valentine uttered a cry of horror; Morrel took her in his arms, as if to defend her from some unknown danger.", "모렐은 알 수 없는 위험으로부터 그녀를 지키려는 듯 그녀를 두 팔로 끌어안았다."]], [["“M. d’Avrigny, M. d’Avrigny,”", "\"다브리니 선생님, 다브리니 선생님.\""], ["cried she, in a stifled voice. “Help, help!”", "그녀가 숨 막힌 목소리로 외쳤다. \"도와주세요, 도와주세요!\""]], [["Barrois turned round and with a great effort stumbled a few steps, then fell at the feet of Noirtier, and resting his hand on the knee of the invalid, exclaimed:", "바루아가 몸을 돌려 안간힘을 다해 몇 걸음 비틀거리더니, 노르티에의 발치에 쓰러져 노인의 무릎에 손을 얹고 외쳤다."]], [["“My master, my good master!”", "\"주인님, 인자하신 주인님!\""]], [["At this moment M. de Villefort, attracted by the noise, appeared on the threshold.", "그 순간 소리에 이끌린 빌포르 씨가 문지방에 나타났다."], ["Morrel relaxed his hold of Valentine, and retreating to a distant corner of the room remained half hidden behind a curtain.", "모렐은 발랑틴을 안았던 팔을 풀고, 방 안 먼 구석으로 물러나 커튼 뒤에 반쯤 몸을 숨긴 채로 있었다."], ["Pale as if he had been gazing on a serpent, he fixed his terrified eye on the agonized sufferer.", "마치 뱀이라도 본 듯 창백해진 그는, 겁에 질린 눈을 고통에 몸부림치는 가엾은 자에게 고정했다."]], [["Noirtier, burning with impatience and terror, was in despair at his utter inability to help his old domestic, whom he regarded more in the light of a friend than a servant.", "노르티에는 초조함과 공포로 들끓으면서도, 하인이라기보다 벗에 가깝게 여기던 늙은 시종을 도무지 도울 수 없는 자신의 무력함에 절망하고 있었다."], ["One might by the fearful swelling of the veins of his forehead and the contraction of the muscles round the eye, trace the terrible conflict which was going on between the living energetic mind and the inanimate and helpless body.", "이마에 무섭게 부풀어 오른 핏줄과 눈가 근육의 경련을 보면, 살아 활기 넘치는 정신과 움직이지 못하는 무력한 몸뚱이 사이에서 벌어지는 끔찍한 싸움을 읽어 낼 수 있었다."]], [["Barrois, his features convulsed, his eyes suffused with blood, and his head thrown back, was lying at full length, beating the floor with his hands, while his legs had become so stiff, that they looked as if they would break rather than bend.", "바루아는 얼굴이 경련으로 일그러지고, 두 눈은 피로 충혈되고, 머리는 뒤로 젖혀진 채 길게 뻗어 누워, 두 손으로 마룻바닥을 두드리고 있었다. 한편 두 다리는 어찌나 뻣뻣하게 굳었는지, 구부러지느니 차라리 부러질 것만 같았다."], ["A slight appearance of foam was visible around the mouth, and he breathed painfully, and with extreme difficulty.", "입가에는 희미하게 거품이 비쳤고, 그는 괴롭게, 더없이 힘겹게 숨을 쉬었다."]], [["Villefort seemed stupefied with astonishment, and remained gazing intently on the scene before him without uttering a word. He had not seen Morrel.", "빌포르는 경악에 넋이 나간 듯, 눈앞의 광경을 한마디 말도 없이 뚫어지게 바라보고만 있었다. 그는 모렐을 보지 못했다."], ["After a moment of dumb contemplation, during which his face became pale and his hair seemed to stand on end, he sprang towards the door, crying out:", "얼굴이 창백해지고 머리카락이 곤두서는 듯한 그 말없는 응시의 한순간이 지나고, 그는 문을 향해 달려가며 외쳤다."]], [["“Doctor, doctor!", "\"의사 선생, 의사 선생!"], ["come instantly, pray come!”", "당장 와 주세요, 제발 와 주세요!\""]], [["“Madame, madame!”", "\"부인, 부인!\""], ["cried Valentine, calling her step-mother, and running upstairs to meet her; “come quick, quick!—and bring your bottle of smelling-salts with you.”", "발랑틴이 계모를 부르며 그녀를 맞으러 위층으로 뛰어 올라가며 외쳤다. \"빨리, 빨리 오세요! 그리고 각성제 병도 가져오세요.\""]], [["“What is the matter?”", "\"무슨 일이냐?\""], ["said Madame de Villefort in a harsh and constrained tone.", "빌포르 부인이 거칠고 딱딱한 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Oh! come! come!”", "\"아! 어서요! 어서요!\""]], [["“But where is the doctor?”", "\"그런데 의사는 어디 있소?\""], ["exclaimed Villefort; “where is he?”", "빌포르가 외쳤다. \"어디 있냐고?\""]], [["Madame de Villefort now deliberately descended the staircase.", "빌포르 부인이 이제 천천히 계단을 내려왔다."], ["In one hand she held her handkerchief, with which she appeared to be wiping her face, and in the other a bottle of English smelling-salts.", "한 손에는 얼굴을 닦는 듯 손수건을 들고 있었고, 다른 손에는 영국제 각성제 병을 들고 있었다."], ["Her first look on entering the room was at Noirtier, whose face, independent of the emotion which such a scene could not fail of producing, proclaimed him to be in possession of his usual health; her second glance was at the dying man.", "그녀가 방에 들어서며 처음 던진 시선은 노르티에를 향했다. 이런 광경이 불러일으킬 수밖에 없는 동요를 빼면, 그의 얼굴은 여느 때처럼 멀쩡하다는 것을 알려 주고 있었다. 두 번째 시선은 죽어 가는 자를 향했다."], ["She turned pale, and her eye passed quickly from the servant and rested on the master.", "그녀는 창백해졌고, 그녀의 눈은 하인에게서 재빨리 옮겨 가 주인에게 머물렀다."]], [["“In the name of heaven, madame,”", "\"제발, 부인,\" 빌포르가 말했다."], ["said Villefort, “where is the doctor? He was with you just now.", "\"의사는 어디 있소? 방금 당신과 함께 있었잖소."], ["You see this is a fit of apoplexy, and he might be saved if he could but be bled!”", "보다시피 이건 졸중 발작이오. 피를 뽑아 줄 수만 있다면 살릴 수 있을지도 모르오!\""]], [["“Has he eaten anything lately?”", "\"이 사람이 최근에 뭘 먹었나요?\""], ["asked Madame de Villefort, eluding her husband’s question.", "빌포르 부인이 남편의 질문을 슬쩍 피하며 물었다."]], [["“Madame,”", "\"부인,\""], ["replied Valentine, “he has not even breakfasted.", "발랑틴이 대답했다. \"이 사람은 아침조차 거른걸요."], ["He has been running very fast on an errand with which my grandfather charged him, and when he returned, took nothing but a glass of lemonade.”", "할아버지가 시키신 심부름으로 무척 빨리 달려갔다 왔고, 돌아와서는 레모네이드 한 잔밖에 마시지 않았어요.\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"어머,\""], ["said Madame de Villefort, “why did he not take wine?", "빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"왜 포도주를 마시지 않았을까?"], ["Lemonade was a very bad thing for him.”", "레모네이드는 저 사람한테 아주 나쁜 건데.\""]], [["“Grandpapa’s bottle of lemonade was standing just by his side; poor Barrois was very thirsty, and was thankful to drink anything he could find.”", "\"할아버지의 레모네이드 병이 바로 옆에 놓여 있었거든요. 가엾은 바루아는 몹시 목이 말라서, 눈에 띄는 거라면 무엇이든 고맙게 마셨던 거예요.\""]], [["Madame de Villefort started.", "빌포르 부인이 흠칫했다."], ["Noirtier looked at her with a glance of the most profound scrutiny.", "노르티에는 더없이 깊이 캐묻는 눈빛으로 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["“He has such a short neck,”", "\"저 사람은 목이 워낙 짧아서요.\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Madame,”", "\"부인,\""], ["said Villefort, “I ask where is M. d’Avrigny?", "빌포르가 말했다. \"다브리니 선생이 어디 있느냐고 묻고 있잖소."], ["In God’s name answer me!”", "제발 대답 좀 해 주시오!\""]], [["“He is with Edward, who is not quite well,”", "\"에두아르와 함께 있어요."], ["replied Madame de Villefort, no longer being able to avoid answering.", "그 애가 몸이 좀 안 좋아서요.\" 빌포르 부인이 더는 대답을 피할 수 없어 말했다."]], [["Villefort rushed upstairs to fetch him.", "빌포르가 그를 데리러 위층으로 달려갔다."]], [["“Take this,” said Madame de Villefort, giving her smelling-bottle to Valentine.", "\"이걸 받아라.\" 빌포르 부인이 각성제 병을 발랑틴에게 건네며 말했다."], ["“They will, no doubt, bleed him; therefore I will retire, for I cannot endure the sight of blood;”", "\"틀림없이 이 사람의 피를 뽑을 테니, 나는 물러가마. 피를 보는 건 도저히 견딜 수가 없구나.\""], ["and she followed her husband upstairs.", "그러고는 남편을 따라 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["Morrel now emerged from his hiding-place, where he had remained quite unperceived, so great had been the general confusion.", "이제 모렐이 숨어 있던 자리에서 나왔다. 워낙 큰 혼란이 벌어진 터라 그가 거기 있는 줄은 아무도 알아채지 못했다."]], [["“Go away as quick as you can, Maximilian,”", "\"되도록 빨리 가세요, 막시밀리앙.\" 발랑틴이 말했다."], ["said Valentine, “and stay till I send for you. Go.”", "\"그리고 제가 사람을 보낼 때까지 기다리세요. 어서 가요.\""]], [["Morrel looked towards Noirtier for permission to retire.", "모렐은 물러가도 좋은지 허락을 구하려 노르티에를 바라보았다."], ["The old man, who had preserved all his usual coolness, made a sign to him to do so.", "여전히 평소의 침착함을 잃지 않은 노인이 그렇게 하라는 신호를 보냈다."], ["The young man pressed Valentine’s hand to his lips, and then left the house by a back staircase.", "젊은이는 발랑틴의 손을 입술에 가져다 댄 뒤, 뒷계단으로 집을 빠져나갔다."]], [["At the same moment that he quitted the room, Villefort and the doctor entered by an opposite door.", "그가 방을 떠나는 바로 그 순간, 빌포르와 의사가 맞은편 문으로 들어왔다."], ["Barrois was now showing signs of returning consciousness.", "바루아는 이제 의식이 돌아오는 기미를 보이고 있었다."], ["The crisis seemed past, a low moaning was heard, and he raised himself on one knee.", "고비는 지난 듯, 나직한 신음 소리가 들리더니 그가 한쪽 무릎을 짚고 몸을 일으켰다."], ["D’Avrigny and Villefort laid him on a couch.", "다브리니와 빌포르가 그를 긴 의자에 눕혔다."]], [["“What do you prescribe, doctor?”", "\"무엇을 처방하시겠소, 선생?\""], ["demanded Villefort.", "빌포르가 물었다."]], [["“Give me some water and ether. You have some in the house, have you not?”", "\"물과 에테르를 좀 주시오. 집에 있겠지요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"있소.\""]], [["“Send for some oil of turpentine and tartar emetic.”", "\"테레빈유와 토주석도 가져오라 이르시오.\""]], [["Villefort immediately despatched a messenger. “And now let everyone retire.”", "빌포르는 곧바로 심부름꾼을 보냈다. \"그리고 이제 모두 물러가게 해 주시오.\""]], [["“Must I go too?”", "\"저도 가야 하나요?\""], ["asked Valentine timidly.", "발랑틴이 머뭇거리며 물었다."]], [["“Yes, mademoiselle, you especially,”", "\"네, 아가씨, 특히 아가씨가요.\""], ["replied the doctor abruptly.", "의사가 퉁명스럽게 대답했다."]], [["Valentine looked at M. d’Avrigny with astonishment, kissed her grandfather on the forehead, and left the room.", "발랑틴은 놀란 얼굴로 다브리니 씨를 바라보더니, 할아버지의 이마에 입을 맞추고 방을 나갔다."], ["The doctor closed the door after her with a gloomy air.", "의사는 침울한 표정으로 그녀의 뒤에서 문을 닫았다."]], [["“Look, look, doctor,”", "\"보세요, 보세요, 선생.\""], ["said Villefort, “he is quite coming round again; I really do not think, after all, it is anything of consequence.”", "빌포르가 말했다. \"이 사람이 다시 정신을 차리고 있잖소. 정말이지, 결국 별일 아닌 것 같소.\""]], [["M. d’Avrigny answered by a melancholy smile.", "다브리니 씨는 서글픈 미소로 대답을 대신했다."]], [["“How do you feel, Barrois?”", "\"기분이 어떤가, 바루아?\""], ["asked he.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“A little better, sir.”", "\"좀 나아졌습니다, 선생님.\""]], [["“Will you drink some of this ether and water?”", "\"이 에테르와 물을 좀 마시겠나?\""]], [["“I will try; but don’t touch me.”", "\"마셔 보겠습니다. 하지만 절 건드리지 마세요.\""]], [["“Why not?”", "\"왜 그러나?\""]], [["“Because I feel that if you were only to touch me with the tip of your finger the fit would return.”", "\"손끝으로라도 절 건드리시면 발작이 다시 도질 것 같아서요.\""]], [["“Drink.”", "\"마시게.\""]], [["Barrois took the glass, and, raising it to his purple lips, took about half of the liquid offered him.", "바루아는 잔을 받아 들고 시퍼렇게 질린 입술로 가져가, 건네받은 액체의 절반쯤을 마셨다."]], [["“Where do you suffer?”", "\"어디가 아픈가?\""], ["asked the doctor.", "의사가 물었다."]], [["“Everywhere.", "\"온몸이요."], ["I feel cramps over my whole body.”", "몸 전체에 경련이 일어요.\""]], [["“Do you find any dazzling sensation before the eyes?”", "\"눈앞에 뭔가 번쩍이는 느낌이 드나?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Any noise in the ears?”", "\"귀에서 무슨 소리가 들리나?\""]], [["“Frightful.” 40112m", "\"끔찍할 만큼요.\""]], [["“When did you first feel that?”", "\"그게 처음 느껴진 게 언제였나?\""]], [["“Just now.”", "\"방금이요.\""]], [["“Suddenly?”", "\"갑자기?\""]], [["“Yes, like a clap of thunder.”", "\"네, 벼락이 치듯이요.\""]], [["“Did you feel nothing of it yesterday or the day before?”", "\"어제나 그저께는 아무렇지도 않았나?\" \"아무렇지도 않았습니다.\""]], [["“No drowsiness?”", "\"졸음도 없었고?\""]], [["“None.”", "\"전혀요.\""]], [["“What have you eaten today?”", "\"오늘은 무얼 먹었나?\""]], [["“I have eaten nothing; I only drank a glass of my master’s lemonade—that’s all.”", "\"아무것도 안 먹었습니다. 주인님의 레모네이드 한 잔만 마셨을 뿐이에요. 그게 전부입니다.\""], ["And Barrois turned towards Noirtier, who, immovably fixed in his armchair, was contemplating this terrible scene without allowing a word or a movement to escape him.", "그러고서 바루아는 노르티에 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다. 노인은 안락의자에 꼼짝 않고 붙박인 채, 한마디 말도 한 번의 몸짓도 흘리지 않고 이 끔찍한 광경을 바라보고 있었다."]], [["“Where is this lemonade?”", "\"그 레모네이드가 어디 있나?\""], ["asked the doctor eagerly.", "의사가 다급히 물었다."]], [["“Downstairs in the decanter.”", "\"아래층 유리병에요.\""]], [["“Whereabouts downstairs?”", "\"아래층 어디?\""]], [["“In the kitchen.”", "\"부엌에요.\""]], [["“Shall I go and fetch it, doctor?”", "\"제가 가서 가져올까요, 선생?\""], ["inquired Villefort.", "빌포르가 물었다."]], [["“No, stay here and try to make Barrois drink the rest of this glass of ether and water.", "\"아니오, 여기 남아서 바루아에게 이 에테르와 물을 마저 마시게 해 보시오."], ["I will go myself and fetch the lemonade.”", "레모네이드는 내가 직접 가서 가져오겠소.\""]], [["D’Avrigny bounded towards the door, flew down the back staircase, and almost knocked down Madame de Villefort, in his haste, who was herself going down to the kitchen.", "다브리니는 문 쪽으로 몸을 날려 뒷계단을 단숨에 뛰어 내려갔고, 서두르다가 마침 부엌으로 내려가던 빌포르 부인을 하마터면 쓰러뜨릴 뻔했다."], ["She cried out, but d’Avrigny paid no attention to her; possessed with but one idea, he cleared the last four steps with a bound, and rushed into the kitchen, where he saw the decanter about three parts empty still standing on the waiter, where it had been left.", "그녀가 비명을 질렀지만 다브리니는 아랑곳하지 않았다. 오직 한 가지 생각에 사로잡힌 그는 마지막 네 계단을 한걸음에 뛰어넘어 부엌으로 뛰어들었고, 거기서 쟁반 위에 놓아둔 그대로 세 부분쯤 비어 있는 유리병을 발견했다."], ["He darted upon it as an eagle would seize upon its prey.", "그는 독수리가 먹잇감을 낚아채듯 병을 덮쳤다."], ["Panting with loss of breath, he returned to the room he had just left.", "숨이 차 헐떡이며 그는 방금 나온 방으로 돌아왔다."], ["Madame de Villefort was slowly ascending the steps which led to her room.", "빌포르 부인은 자기 방으로 이어지는 계단을 천천히 올라가고 있었다."]], [["“Is this the decanter you spoke of?”", "\"부인이 말씀하신 그 유리병이 이것이오?\""], ["asked d’Avrigny.", "다브리니가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, doctor.”", "\"네, 선생.\""]], [["“Is this the same lemonade of which you partook?”", "\"부인이 드셨다는 그 레모네이드와 같은 것이오?\""]], [["“I believe so.”", "\"그런 것 같아요.\""]], [["“What did it taste like?”", "\"맛이 어땠소?\""]], [["“It had a bitter taste.”", "\"쓴맛이 났어요.\""]], [["The doctor poured some drops of the lemonade into the palm of his hand, put his lips to it, and after having rinsed his mouth as a man does when he is tasting wine, he spat the liquor into the fireplace.", "의사는 레모네이드 몇 방울을 손바닥에 따라 입술을 갖다 댔고, 포도주를 음미하는 사람처럼 입안을 헹구더니 그 액체를 벽난로에 뱉었다."]], [["“It is no doubt the same,” said he.", "\"틀림없이 같은 것이군요.\" 그가 말했다."], ["“Did you drink some too, M. Noirtier?”", "\"노르티에 씨, 당신도 좀 드셨습니까?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇다.\""]], [["“And did you also discover a bitter taste?”", "\"당신도 쓴맛을 느끼셨습니까?\" \"그렇다.\""]], [["“Yes.” “Oh, doctor,”", "\"아, 선생님,\""], ["cried Barrois, “the fit is coming on again.", "바루아가 외쳤다. \"발작이 다시 도지고 있어요."], ["Oh, do something for me.” The doctor flew to his patient.", "아, 제발 저를 좀 어떻게 해 주세요.\" 의사가 환자에게로 달려갔다."]], [["“That emetic, Villefort—see if it is coming.”", "\"그 토주석, 빌포르 씨, 오는지 좀 보시오.\""]], [["Villefort sprang into the passage, exclaiming, “The emetic!", "빌포르가 복도로 뛰어나가며 외쳤다. \"토주석! 토주석!"], ["the emetic!—is it come yet?” No one answered.", "아직 안 왔나?\" 아무도 대답하지 않았다."], ["The most profound terror reigned throughout the house.", "집 안 전체에 더없이 깊은 공포가 감돌았다."]], [["“If I had anything by means of which I could inflate the lungs,”", "\"폐에 바람을 불어넣을 만한 게 있다면,\""], ["said d’Avrigny, looking around him, “perhaps I might prevent suffocation.", "다브리니가 주위를 둘러보며 말했다. \"질식만은 막을 수 있을지도 모르는데."], ["But there is nothing which would do!—nothing!”", "하지만 쓸 만한 게 아무것도 없군! 아무것도!\""]], [["40114m\n“Oh, sir,”", "\"아, 선생님,\""], ["cried Barrois, “are you going to let me die without help?", "바루아가 외쳤다. \"저를 이대로 도움도 없이 죽게 내버려 두실 겁니까?"], ["Oh, I am dying! Oh, save me!”", "아, 죽어요! 아, 살려 주세요!\""]], [["“A pen, a pen!”", "\"펜, 펜을!\""], ["said the doctor.", "의사가 말했다."], ["There was one lying on the table; he endeavored to introduce it into the mouth of the patient, who, in the midst of his convulsions, was making vain attempts to vomit; but the jaws were so clenched that the pen could not pass them.", "탁자 위에 펜 하나가 놓여 있었다. 그는 그것을 환자의 입에 밀어 넣으려 했다. 환자는 경련에 휩싸인 와중에도 헛되이 토하려고 안간힘을 쓰고 있었다."], ["This second attack was much more violent than the first, and he had slipped from the couch to the ground, where he was writhing in agony.", "그러나 턱이 어찌나 꽉 다물려 있는지 펜이 그 사이를 지날 수가 없었다. 이 두 번째 발작은 첫 번째보다 훨씬 격렬했고, 그는 긴 의자에서 바닥으로 미끄러져 고통 속에 몸부림쳤다."], ["The doctor left him in this paroxysm, knowing that he could do nothing to alleviate it, and, going up to Noirtier, said abruptly:", "의사는 그것을 가라앉히기 위해 자신이 할 수 있는 일이 없음을 알기에 그를 그 발작 속에 내버려 두고, 노르티에에게 다가가 불쑥 말했다."]], [["“How do you find yourself?—well?”", "\"기분이 어떠십니까? 괜찮으십니까?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇다.\""]], [["“Have you any weight on the chest; or does your stomach feel light and comfortable—eh?”", "\"가슴이 답답하십니까? 아니면 속이 가볍고 편안하십니까, 예?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇다.\""]], [["“Then you feel pretty much as you generally do after you have had the dose which I am accustomed to give you every Sunday?”", "\"그렇다면 제가 일요일마다 드리던 그 약을 드신 뒤와 거의 같은 느낌이시군요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇다.\""]], [["“Did Barrois make your lemonade?”", "\"레모네이드는 바루아가 만들었습니까?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇다.\""]], [["“Was it you who asked him to drink some of it?” “No.”", "\"그에게 그것을 좀 마시라고 권하신 게 당신입니까?\" \"아니다.\""]], [["“Was it M. de Villefort?”", "\"빌포르 씨입니까?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"아니다.\""]], [["“Madame?” “No.”", "\"부인입니까?\" \"아니다.\""]], [["“It was your granddaughter, then, was it not?”", "\"그렇다면 손녀분이군요, 그렇지요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇다.\""]], [["A groan from Barrois, accompanied by a yawn which seemed to crack the very jawbones, attracted the attention of M.", "바루아의 신음 소리가, 턱뼈가 으스러질 듯한 하품과 함께 다브리니 씨의 주의를 끌었다."], ["d’Avrigny; he left M. Noirtier, and returned to the sick man.", "그는 노르티에 씨를 두고 병자에게로 돌아갔다."]], [["“Barrois,”", "\"바루아,\""], ["said the doctor, “can you speak?”", "의사가 말했다. \"말할 수 있겠나?\""], ["Barrois muttered a few unintelligible words.", "바루아가 알아들을 수 없는 말 몇 마디를 웅얼거렸다."], ["“Try and make an effort to do so, my good man.”", "\"어떻게든 애를 써서 말해 보게, 이 사람아.\""], ["said d’Avrigny.", "다브리니가 말했다."], ["Barrois reopened his bloodshot eyes.", "바루아가 충혈된 두 눈을 다시 떴다."]], [["“Who made the lemonade?”", "\"레모네이드는 누가 만들었나?\""]], [["“I did.”", "\"제가요.\""]], [["“Did you bring it to your master directly it was made?” “No.”", "\"만든 즉시 주인께 가져갔나?\""]], [["“You left it somewhere, then, in the meantime?”", "\"아니요.\" \"그럼 그사이에 어딘가에 두었군?\""]], [["“Yes; I left it in the pantry, because I was called away.”", "\"네. 식료품 저장실에 두었어요. 불려 나가는 바람에요.\""]], [["“Who brought it into this room, then?”", "\"그럼 이 방에 그걸 가져온 사람은 누군가?\""]], [["“Mademoiselle Valentine.”", "\"발랑틴 아가씨요.\""], ["D’Avrigny struck his forehead with his hand.", "다브리니가 손으로 이마를 쳤다."]], [["“Gracious heaven,” exclaimed he.", "\"맙소사.\" 그가 외쳤다."]], [["“Doctor, doctor!”", "\"선생님, 선생님!\""], ["cried Barrois, who felt another fit coming.", "또 한 번의 발작이 오는 것을 느낀 바루아가 외쳤다."]], [["“Will they never bring that emetic?”", "\"그 토주석은 끝내 안 가져오는 건가?\""], ["asked the doctor.", "의사가 물었다."]], [["“Here is a glass with one already prepared,”", "\"여기 미리 준비된 게 한 잔 있소.\""], ["said Villefort, entering the room.", "빌포르가 방으로 들어서며 말했다."]], [["“Who prepared it?”", "\"누가 준비했소?\""]], [["“The chemist who came here with me.” 40116m", "\"나와 함께 온 약제사가 했소.\""]], [["“Drink it,”", "\"마시게.\""], ["said the doctor to Barrois.", "의사가 바루아에게 말했다."]], [["“Impossible, doctor; it is too late; my throat is closing up.", "\"안 됩니다, 선생님. 너무 늦었어요. 목이 막혀 와요."], ["I am choking!", "숨이 막혀요!"], ["Oh, my heart!", "아, 제 심장!"], ["Ah, my head!—Oh, what agony!—Shall I suffer like this long?”", "아, 제 머리! 아, 이 고통! 이렇게 오래 괴로워해야 합니까?\""]], [["“No, no, friend,”", "\"아니, 아닐세, 이 사람아.\""], ["replied the doctor, “you will soon cease to suffer.”", "의사가 대답했다. \"곧 괴로움이 그칠 걸세.\""]], [["“Ah, I understand you,”", "\"아, 무슨 말씀인지 알겠습니다.\""], ["said the unhappy man.", "가엾은 사내가 말했다."], ["“My God, have mercy upon me!”", "\"하느님, 저를 불쌍히 여기소서!\""], ["and, uttering a fearful cry, Barrois fell back as if he had been struck by lightning.", "그러고서 무시무시한 비명을 지르며, 바루아는 벼락에 맞은 듯 뒤로 쓰러졌다."], ["D’Avrigny put his hand to his heart, and placed a glass before his lips.", "다브리니는 그의 심장에 손을 대 보고, 그의 입술 앞에 유리잔을 갖다 댔다."]], [["“Well?”", "\"어떻소?\""], ["said Villefort.", "빌포르가 말했다."]], [["“Go to the kitchen and get me some syrup of violets.”", "\"부엌에 가서 제비꽃 시럽을 좀 가져다주시오.\""]], [["Villefort went immediately.", "빌포르가 곧바로 갔다."]], [["“Do not be alarmed, M. Noirtier,”", "\"놀라지 마십시오, 노르티에 씨.\""], ["said d’Avrigny; “I am going to take my patient into the next room to bleed him; this sort of attack is very frightful to witness.”", "다브리니가 말했다. \"환자를 옆방으로 옮겨 피를 뽑으려는 겁니다. 이런 종류의 발작은 지켜보기에 몹시 끔찍하니까요.\""]], [["And taking Barrois under the arms, he dragged him into an adjoining room; but almost immediately he returned to fetch the lemonade.", "그러고서 그는 바루아의 양 겨드랑이를 붙잡아 옆방으로 끌고 갔다. 그러나 거의 곧바로 레모네이드를 가지러 돌아왔다."], ["Noirtier closed his right eye.", "노르티에가 오른쪽 눈을 감았다."]], [["“You want Valentine, do you not?", "\"발랑틴을 원하시는 거지요?"], ["I will tell them to send her to you.”", "사람을 시켜 아가씨를 보내 드리라 하겠습니다.\""]], [["Villefort returned, and d’Avrigny met him in the passage.", "빌포르가 돌아왔고, 다브리니가 복도에서 그를 맞았다."]], [["“Well, how is he now?”", "\"그래, 이제 좀 어떻소?\""], ["asked he.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“Come in here,” said d’Avrigny, and he took him into the chamber where the sick man lay.", "\"이리로 들어오시오.\" 다브리니가 말하며 그를 병자가 누운 방으로 데려갔다."]], [["“Is he still in a fit?”", "\"아직 발작 중이오?\""], ["said the procureur.", "검사가 말했다."]], [["“He is dead.”", "\"죽었소.\""]], [["Villefort drew back a few steps, and, clasping his hands, exclaimed, with real amazement and sympathy, “Dead?—and so soon too!”", "빌포르가 몇 걸음 물러서며 두 손을 맞잡고, 진정한 경악과 연민에 잠겨 외쳤다. \"죽었다고? 그것도 이렇게 빨리?\""]], [["“Yes, it is very soon,”", "\"그렇소, 아주 빠르군요.\""], ["said the doctor, looking at the corpse before him; “but that ought not to astonish you; Monsieur and Madame de Saint-Méran died as soon.", "의사가 눈앞의 시신을 바라보며 말했다. \"하지만 그게 놀랄 일은 아니지요. 생메랑 부부도 이만큼 빨리 죽었으니까요."], ["People die very suddenly in your house, M. de Villefort.”", "댁에서는 사람들이 참 갑작스레 죽는군요, 빌포르 씨.\""]], [["“What?”", "\"뭐라고요?\""], ["cried the magistrate, with an accent of horror and consternation, “are you still harping on that terrible idea?”", "검사가 공포와 경악이 서린 어조로 외쳤다. \"여전히 그 끔찍한 생각을 곱씹고 계시는 거요?\""]], [["“Still, sir; and I shall always do so,”", "\"여전히 그렇소. 그리고 앞으로도 줄곧 그럴 거요.\" 다브리니가 대답했다."], ["replied d’Avrigny, “for it has never for one instant ceased to retain possession of my mind; and that you may be quite sure I am not mistaken this time, listen well to what I am going to say, M. de Villefort.”", "\"그 생각이 단 한순간도 내 머릿속을 떠난 적이 없으니까. 그리고 이번에는 내가 결코 틀리지 않았음을 분명히 알 수 있도록, 내가 지금부터 하는 말을 잘 들으시오, 빌포르 씨.\""]], [["The magistrate trembled convulsively.", "검사가 발작하듯 떨었다."]], [["“There is a poison which destroys life almost without leaving any perceptible traces.", "\"이렇다 할 흔적을 거의 남기지 않고 생명을 앗아 가는 독이 하나 있소."], ["I know it well; I have studied it in all its forms and in the effects which it produces.", "나는 그것을 잘 아오. 온갖 형태로, 또 그것이 일으키는 온갖 작용으로 연구했으니까."], ["I recognized the presence of this poison in the case of poor Barrois as well as in that of Madame de Saint-Méran.", "나는 가엾은 바루아의 경우에서도, 생메랑 부인의 경우에서와 마찬가지로 이 독의 존재를 알아보았소."], ["There is a way of detecting its presence.", "그것을 검출하는 방법이 있소."], ["It restores the blue color of litmus-paper reddened by an acid, and it turns syrup of violets green.", "그 독은 산에 의해 붉게 변한 리트머스 종이를 다시 푸르게 되돌리고, 제비꽃 시럽을 초록으로 바꾸어 놓소."], ["We have no litmus-paper, but, see, here they come with the syrup of violets.”", "우리에게 리트머스 종이는 없지만, 보시오, 마침 제비꽃 시럽을 가져오는군요.\""]], [["The doctor was right; steps were heard in the passage. M.", "의사의 말이 옳았다. 복도에서 발소리가 들렸다."], ["d’Avrigny opened the door, and took from the hands of the chambermaid a cup which contained two or three spoonfuls of the syrup, he then carefully closed the door.", "다브리니 씨는 문을 열고 하녀의 손에서 시럽 두세 숟가락이 담긴 잔을 받아 든 뒤, 조심스럽게 문을 닫았다."]], [["“Look,”", "\"보시오,\""], ["said he to the procureur, whose heart beat so loudly that it might almost be heard, “here is in this cup some syrup of violets, and this decanter contains the remainder of the lemonade of which M.", "그가 검사에게 말했다. 검사의 심장은 거의 들릴 만큼 요란하게 뛰고 있었다. \"이 잔에 제비꽃 시럽이 좀 담겨 있고, 이 유리병에는 노르티에 씨와 바루아가 마신 레모네이드의 나머지가 들어 있소."], ["Noirtier and Barrois partook. If the lemonade be pure and inoffensive, the syrup will retain its color; if, on the contrary, the lemonade be drugged with poison, the syrup will become green.", "만약 레모네이드가 깨끗하고 무해한 것이라면 시럽은 제 빛깔을 유지할 거요. 하지만 반대로 레모네이드에 독이 섞여 있다면 시럽은 초록으로 변할 거요."], ["Look closely!”", "똑똑히 보시오!\""]], [["The doctor then slowly poured some drops of the lemonade from the decanter into the cup, and in an instant a light cloudy sediment began to form at the bottom of the cup; this sediment first took a blue shade, then from the color of sapphire it passed to that of opal, and from opal to emerald.", "그러고서 의사는 유리병에서 레모네이드 몇 방울을 천천히 잔에 따랐다. 그러자 순식간에 잔 바닥에 옅고 뿌연 침전물이 생기기 시작했다. 그 침전물은 처음에 푸른빛을 띠더니, 사파이어 빛깔에서 오팔 빛깔로, 다시 오팔에서 에메랄드로 옮겨 갔다."], ["Arrived at this last hue, it changed no more.", "이 마지막 빛깔에 이르자 더는 변하지 않았다."], ["The result of the experiment left no doubt whatever on the mind.", "실험의 결과는 어떤 의심의 여지도 남기지 않았다."]], [["“The unfortunate Barrois has been poisoned,”", "\"가엾은 바루아는 독살된 거요.\" 다브리니가 말했다."], ["said d’Avrigny, “and I will maintain this assertion before God and man.”", "\"그리고 나는 이 단언을 하느님 앞에서도 사람 앞에서도 굽히지 않을 거요.\""]], [["Villefort said nothing, but he clasped his hands, opened his haggard eyes, and, overcome with his emotion, sank into a chair.", "빌포르는 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 두 손을 맞잡고 퀭한 눈을 부릅떴다. 그러고는 복받치는 감정에 못 이겨 의자에 풀썩 주저앉았다."]], [["# Chapter 80. The Accusation", "# 제79장 레모네이드"]], [["M. d’Avrigny soon restored the magistrate to consciousness, who had looked like a second corpse in that chamber of death.", "다브리니 씨는 곧 검사를 의식으로 되돌려 놓았다. 그는 그 죽음의 방 안에서 또 하나의 시체처럼 보였다."]], [["“Oh, death is in my house!”", "\"오, 우리 집에 죽음이 들어왔어!\""], ["cried Villefort.", "빌포르가 외쳤다."]], [["“Say, rather, crime!” replied the doctor.", "\"차라리 범죄라고 하시지요!\" 의사가 받았다."]], [["“M. d’Avrigny,”", "\"다브리니 씨,\""], ["cried Villefort, “I cannot tell you all I feel at this moment,—terror, grief, madness.”", "빌포르가 외쳤다. \"지금 이 순간 제가 느끼는 것을 다 말씀드릴 수가 없습니다. 공포, 비탄, 광기…….\""]], [["“Yes,” said M.", "\"그렇겠지요.\""], ["d’Avrigny, with an imposing calmness, “but I think it is now time to act.", "다브리니 씨가 위엄 어린 침착함으로 말했다. \"하지만 이제는 행동할 때라고 봅니다."], ["I think it is time to stop this torrent of mortality.", "이 죽음의 격류를 멈춰야 할 때라고 봅니다."], ["I can no longer bear to be in possession of these secrets without the hope of seeing the victims and society generally revenged.”", "저는 더 이상 이 비밀을 품고만 있을 수가 없습니다. 희생자들과 사회 전체의 원한이 풀리는 것을 보고 싶습니다.\""]], [["Villefort cast a gloomy look around him.", "빌포르가 음울한 눈길로 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["“In my house,”", "\"우리 집에서,\""], ["murmured he, “in my house!”", "그가 중얼거렸다. \"우리 집에서!\""]], [["“Come, magistrate,” said M.", "\"자, 검사님.\""], ["d’Avrigny, “show yourself a man; as an interpreter of the law, do honor to your profession by sacrificing your selfish interests to it.”", "다브리니 씨가 말했다. \"사내답게 처신하십시오. 법을 해석하는 사람으로서, 사사로운 이익을 직무에 바쳐 그 직분에 명예를 더하십시오.\""]], [["“You make me shudder, doctor.", "\"의사 선생, 소름이 끼치는군요."], ["Do you talk of a sacrifice?”", "희생을 말씀하시는 겁니까?\" \"그렇습니다.\""]], [["“I do.” “Do you then suspect anyone?”", "\"그럼 누군가를 의심하고 계신 겁니까?\""]], [["“I suspect no one; death raps at your door—it enters—it goes, not blindfolded, but circumspectly, from room to room. Well, I follow its course, I track its passage; I adopt the wisdom of the ancients, and feel my way, for my friendship for your family and my respect for you are as a twofold bandage over my eyes; well——”", "\"저는 아무도 의심하지 않습니다. 죽음이 댁의 문을 두드립니다. 들어옵니다. 그리고 눈을 가린 채가 아니라 신중하게, 방에서 방으로 옮겨 다닙니다. 자, 저는 그 행로를 따라가고, 그 자취를 좇습니다. 옛사람들의 지혜를 빌려, 더듬어 길을 찾습니다. 댁의 가족에 대한 제 우정과 검사님에 대한 제 존경이 제 눈을 가린 두 겹의 띠와도 같으니 말입니다. 자…….\""]], [["“Oh, speak, speak, doctor; I shall have courage.”", "\"오, 말씀하십시오, 말씀하십시오, 의사 선생. 견뎌 내겠습니다.\""]], [["“Well, sir, you have in your establishment, or in your family, perhaps, one of the frightful monstrosities of which each century produces only one.", "\"자, 그럼 말씀드리지요. 검사님은 댁의 살림 안에, 혹은 어쩌면 가족 안에, 한 세기에 단 하나만 태어나는 끔찍한 괴물 가운데 하나를 두고 계십니다."], ["Locusta and Agrippina, living at the same time, were an exception, and proved the determination of Providence to effect the entire ruin of the Roman empire, sullied by so many crimes.", "같은 시대에 살았던 로쿠스타와 아그리피나는 예외였고, 그토록 많은 범죄로 더럽혀진 로마 제국을 완전히 멸망시키려는 섭리의 결단을 증명한 존재였지요."], ["Brunhilda and Fredegund were the results of the painful struggle of civilization in its infancy, when man was learning to control mind, were it even by an emissary from the realms of darkness. All these women had been, or were, beautiful.", "브룬힐데와 프레데군트는 문명이 그 유년기에 겪은 고통스러운 투쟁의 산물이었습니다. 어둠의 나라에서 온 사자(使者)의 손을 빌려서라도 인간이 정신을 다스리는 법을 배우려 하던 시절 말입니다."], ["The same flower of innocence had flourished, or was still flourishing, on their brow, that is seen on the brow of the culprit in your house.” 40120m", "이 여인들은 모두 아름다웠거나, 아름다운 이들이었습니다. 댁의 그 범인의 이마에 보이는 것과 똑같은 순결의 꽃이 그들의 이마에도 피었거나, 여전히 피어 있었지요.\""]], [["Villefort shrieked, clasped his hands, and looked at the doctor with a supplicating air.", "빌포르가 비명을 지르며 두 손을 마주 잡고 애원하는 표정으로 의사를 바라보았다."], ["But the latter went on without pity:", "그러나 의사는 가차 없이 말을 이었다."]], [["“‘Seek whom the crime will profit,’ says an axiom of jurisprudence.”", "\"'범죄로 이득을 보는 자를 찾으라.' 법학의 한 격언입니다.\""]], [["“Doctor,”", "\"의사 선생,\""], ["cried Villefort, “alas, doctor, how often has man’s justice been deceived by those fatal words.", "빌포르가 외쳤다. \"아아, 의사 선생, 그 숙명적인 말 때문에 인간의 정의가 얼마나 자주 속아 넘어갔습니까."], ["I know not why, but I feel that this crime——”", "왜인지는 모르겠지만, 저는 이 범죄가…….\""]], [["“You acknowledge, then, the existence of the crime?”", "\"그럼 범죄가 있다는 것은 인정하시는군요?\""]], [["“Yes, I see too plainly that it does exist. But it seems that it is intended to affect me personally.", "\"네, 너무도 분명하게 그것이 존재한다는 걸 알겠습니다. 하지만 그것이 저 개인을 노린 것 같습니다."], ["I fear an attack myself, after all these disasters.”", "이 모든 참화 끝에, 저 자신이 공격당할까 두렵습니다.\""]], [["“Oh, man!”", "\"오, 인간이여!\""], ["murmured d’Avrigny, “the most selfish of all animals, the most personal of all creatures, who believes the earth turns, the sun shines, and death strikes for him alone,—an ant cursing God from the top of a blade of grass!", "다브리니가 중얼거렸다. \"모든 짐승 가운데 가장 이기적이고, 모든 피조물 가운데 가장 자기중심적이며, 땅이 도는 것도, 해가 비치는 것도, 죽음이 내리치는 것도 오직 자기를 위한 일이라 믿는 존재여. 풀잎 끝에 올라앉아 신을 저주하는 개미로다!"], ["And have those who have lost their lives lost nothing?—M.", "그럼 목숨을 잃은 이들은 아무것도 잃지 않았단 말입니까?"], ["de Saint-Méran, Madame de Saint-Méran, M. Noirtier——”", "생메랑 씨, 생메랑 부인, 누아르티에 씨께서는…….\""]], [["“How? M. Noirtier?”", "\"뭐라고요? 누아르티에 씨라고요?\""]], [["“Yes; think you it was the poor servant’s life was coveted?", "\"그렇습니다. 그 가엾은 하인의 목숨을 노렸다고 생각하십니까? 아니지요, 아닙니다."], ["No, no; like Shakespeare’s Polonius, he died for another.", "셰익스피어의 폴로니우스처럼, 그는 다른 사람 대신 죽은 겁니다."], ["It was Noirtier the lemonade was intended for—it is Noirtier, logically speaking, who drank it.", "레모네이드는 누아르티에 씨를 노린 것이었습니다. 논리적으로 따지면 그것을 마셨어야 할 사람은 누아르티에 씨였지요."], ["The other drank it only by accident, and, although Barrois is dead, it was Noirtier whose death was wished for.”", "다른 사람은 그저 우연히 마셨을 뿐입니다. 바루아가 죽긴 했어도, 죽기를 바란 대상은 누아르티에 씨였던 겁니다.\""]], [["“But why did it not kill my father?”", "\"그런데 어째서 제 아버지는 죽지 않았습니까?\""]], [["“I told you one evening in the garden after Madame de Saint-Méran’s death—because his system is accustomed to that very poison, and the dose was trifling to him, which would be fatal to another; because no one knows, not even the assassin, that, for the last twelve months, I have given M.", "\"생메랑 부인이 돌아가신 뒤 어느 날 저녁, 정원에서 제가 말씀드렸지요. 그분의 몸이 바로 그 독에 익숙해져 있기 때문이라고요. 다른 사람에게는 치명적일 양이 그분께는 대수롭지 않았던 겁니다."], ["Noirtier brucine for his paralytic affection, while the assassin is not ignorant, for he has proved that brucine is a violent poison.”", "누아르티에 씨께서 마비 증세를 앓고 계셔서, 제가 지난 열두 달 동안 브루신을 처방해 왔다는 사실을 아무도, 심지어 살인자조차 모르기 때문입니다. 하지만 그 살인자는 브루신이 맹독이라는 사실을 모르지 않습니다. 직접 증명해 보였으니까요.\""]], [["“Oh, have pity—have pity!”", "\"오, 자비를, 제발 자비를!\""], ["murmured Villefort, wringing his hands.", "빌포르가 두 손을 비틀며 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Follow the culprit’s steps; he first kills M. de Saint-Méran——”", "\"범인의 발자취를 따라가 봅시다. 그자는 먼저 생메랑 씨를 죽였습니다…….\""]], [["“Oh, doctor!”", "\"오, 의사 선생!\""]], [["“I would swear to it; what I heard of his symptoms agrees too well with what I have seen in the other cases.”", "\"맹세할 수 있습니다. 제가 전해 들은 그분의 증세가 제가 다른 사례들에서 직접 본 것과 너무도 잘 들어맞으니까요.\""], ["Villefort ceased to contend; he only groaned.", "빌포르는 더 이상 항변하지 않았다. 그저 신음할 뿐이었다."], ["“He first kills M. de Saint-Méran,”", "\"그자는 먼저 생메랑 씨를 죽였습니다.\""], ["repeated the doctor, “then Madame de Saint-Méran,—a double fortune to inherit.”", "의사가 되풀이했다. \"그다음에 생메랑 부인을요. 물려받을 재산이 두 몫이지요.\""], ["Villefort wiped the perspiration from his forehead.", "빌포르가 이마에 맺힌 땀을 닦았다."], ["“Listen attentively.”", "\"주의 깊게 들으십시오.\""]], [["“Alas,”", "\"아아,\""], ["stammered Villefort, “I do not lose a single word.”", "빌포르가 더듬거렸다. \"한마디도 놓치지 않고 있습니다.\""]], [["“M. Noirtier,” resumed M.", "\"누아르티에 씨께서는,\""], ["d’Avrigny in the same pitiless tone,—“M.", "다브리니 씨가 똑같은 무자비한 어조로 말을 이었다."], ["Noirtier had once made a will against you—against your family—in favor of the poor, in fact; M.", "\"누아르티에 씨께서는 한때 검사님과 검사님 가족에게 불리하게, 사실상 가난한 이들에게 유리하게 유언장을 작성하셨지요."], ["Noirtier is spared, because nothing is expected from him.", "누아르티에 씨가 화를 면한 것은 그분에게서 아무것도 기대할 게 없었기 때문입니다."], ["But he has no sooner destroyed his first will and made a second, than, for fear he should make a third, he is struck down.", "그런데 그분이 첫 번째 유언장을 없애고 두 번째 유언장을 작성하시자마자, 세 번째 유언장을 작성하실까 두려워 누군가 그분을 쓰러뜨리려 든 겁니다."], ["The will was made the day before yesterday, I believe; you see there has been no time lost.”", "유언장은 그저께 작성된 것으로 압니다. 보십시오, 한순간도 허비하지 않았지요.\""]], [["“Oh, mercy, M. d’Avrigny!”", "\"오, 자비를, 다브리니 씨!\""]], [["“No mercy, sir!", "\"자비는 없습니다, 검사님!"], ["The physician has a sacred mission on earth; and to fulfil it he begins at the source of life, and goes down to the mysterious darkness of the tomb.", "의사에게는 이 땅에서 신성한 사명이 있습니다. 그 사명을 다하기 위해 그는 생명의 근원에서 시작하여 무덤의 신비로운 어둠에 이르기까지 내려갑니다."], ["When crime has been committed, and God, doubtless in anger, turns away his face, it is for the physician to bring the culprit to justice.”", "범죄가 저질러지고, 신이 필시 진노하여 얼굴을 돌리실 때, 범인을 정의 앞에 세우는 것은 바로 의사의 몫입니다.\""]], [["40122m\n“Have mercy on my child, sir,”", "\"제 아이에게 자비를 베풀어 주십시오, 선생.\""], ["murmured Villefort.", "빌포르가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“You see it is yourself who have first named her—you, her father.”", "\"보십시오, 그 아이의 이름을 먼저 부른 것은 바로 검사님 자신입니다. 그 아이의 아버지인 검사님이요.\""]], [["“Have pity on Valentine!", "\"발랑틴을 가엾이 여겨 주십시오!"], ["Listen, it is impossible. I would as willingly accuse myself!", "들어 보세요, 그건 있을 수 없는 일입니다. 차라리 제 자신을 고발하겠습니다!"], ["Valentine, whose heart is pure as a diamond or a lily!”", "다이아몬드처럼, 백합처럼 순결한 마음을 지닌 발랑틴을요!\""]], [["“No pity, procureur; the crime is fragrant.", "\"자비는 없습니다, 검사님."], ["Mademoiselle herself packed all the medicines which were sent to M. de Saint-Méran; and M.", "범죄의 냄새가 진동합니다. 생메랑 씨에게 보낸 약은 모두 그 아가씨가 손수 챙겼습니다."], ["de Saint-Méran is dead.", "그리고 생메랑 씨는 죽었지요."], ["Mademoiselle de Villefort prepared all the cooling draughts which Madame de Saint-Méran took, and Madame de Saint-Méran is dead.", "생메랑 부인이 드신 진정제도 모두 빌포르 양이 조제했습니다. 그리고 생메랑 부인은 죽었지요."], ["Mademoiselle de Villefort took from the hands of Barrois, who was sent out, the lemonade which M. Noirtier had every morning, and he has escaped by a miracle.", "누아르티에 씨가 매일 아침 드시던 레모네이드를, 심부름을 나가던 바루아의 손에서 받아 든 사람도 빌포르 양입니다. 그리고 그분은 기적적으로 화를 면했습니다."], ["Mademoiselle de Villefort is the culprit—she is the poisoner!", "빌포르 양이 범인입니다. 그 아가씨가 독살범입니다!"], ["To you, as the king’s attorney, I denounce Mademoiselle de Villefort, do your duty.”", "검사님께, 국왕의 검사이신 검사님께, 저는 빌포르 양을 고발합니다. 검사님의 직무를 다하십시오.\""]], [["“Doctor, I resist no longer—I can no longer defend myself—I believe you; but, for pity’s sake, spare my life, my honor!”", "\"의사 선생, 더 이상 버티지 않겠습니다. 더 이상 저 자신을 변호할 수 없습니다. 선생 말씀을 믿습니다. 하지만 제발, 제 목숨을, 제 명예를 살려 주십시오!\""]], [["“M. de Villefort,”", "\"빌포르 씨.\""], ["replied the doctor, with increased vehemence, “there are occasions when I dispense with all foolish human circumspection.", "의사가 한층 격렬하게 받았다. \"어리석은 인간적 신중함을 모두 내던지는 때가 있는 법입니다. 따님이 범죄를 단 한 번만 저질렀고, 또 한 번을 꾸미는 것을 제가 보았다면, 저는 이렇게 말했을 겁니다."], ["If your daughter had committed only one crime, and I saw her meditating another, I would say ‘Warn her, punish her, let her pass the remainder of her life in a convent, weeping and praying.’ If she had committed two crimes, I would say, ‘Here, M.", "'그 아이에게 경고하시오, 벌하시오, 수녀원에서 남은 생을 울며 기도하며 보내게 하시오.' 두 번의 범죄를 저질렀다면, 저는 이렇게 말했을 겁니다. '빌포르 씨, 여기 그 죄수가 모르는 독이 있소."], ["de Villefort, is a poison that the prisoner is not acquainted with,—one that has no known antidote, quick as thought, rapid as lightning, mortal as the thunderbolt; give her that poison, recommending her soul to God, and save your honor and your life, for it is yours she aims at; and I can picture her approaching your pillow with her hypocritical smiles and her sweet exhortations.", "알려진 해독제가 없고, 생각만큼 빠르며, 번개처럼 신속하고, 벼락처럼 치명적인 독이오. 그 독을 따님에게 주고, 그 영혼을 신께 맡기시오. 그렇게 당신의 명예와 목숨을 지키시오."], ["Woe to you, M.", "그 아이가 노리는 것은 바로 당신의 목숨이니."], ["de Villefort, if you do not strike first!’ This is what I would say had she only killed two persons but she has seen three deaths,—has contemplated three murdered persons,—has knelt by three corpses!", "위선적인 미소와 달콤한 권유로 당신의 베갯머리로 다가서는 그 아이의 모습이 눈에 선하오. 빌포르 씨, 당신이 먼저 치지 않으면 화가 미칠 것이오!' 따님이 두 사람만 죽였다면 저는 이렇게 말했을 겁니다."], ["To the scaffold with the poisoner—to the scaffold!", "하지만 그 아이는 세 번의 죽음을 보았습니다. 살해된 세 사람을 지켜보았습니다. 세 구의 시체 곁에 무릎 꿇었습니다!"], ["Do you talk of your honor?", "독살범을 처형대로 보내시오, 처형대로!"], ["Do what I tell you, and immortality awaits you!”", "명예를 말씀하십니까? 제가 시키는 대로 하십시오. 그러면 불멸의 명성이 당신을 기다릴 것입니다!\""]], [["Villefort fell on his knees.", "빌포르가 무릎을 꿇었다."]], [["“Listen,”", "\"들어 보세요.\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he; “I have not the strength of mind you have, or rather that which you would not have, if instead of my daughter Valentine your daughter Madeleine were concerned.”", "\"저에게는 선생 같은 정신력이 없습니다. 아니, 차라리 선생도 갖지 못할 정신력이지요. 만일 제 딸 발랑틴이 아니라 선생의 딸 마들렌의 일이었다면 말입니다.\""], ["The doctor turned pale.", "의사의 얼굴이 창백해졌다."], ["“Doctor, every son of woman is born to suffer and to die; I am content to suffer and to await death.”", "\"의사 선생, 여인의 몸에서 태어난 자는 누구나 고통받고 죽도록 정해져 있습니다. 저는 고통받는 것도, 죽음을 기다리는 것도 달게 받겠습니다.\""]], [["“Beware,” said M.", "\"조심하십시오.\""], ["d’Avrigny, “it may come slowly; you will see it approach after having struck your father, your wife, perhaps your son.”", "다브리니 씨가 말했다. \"죽음은 더디게 올지도 모릅니다. 당신의 아버지를, 당신의 아내를, 어쩌면 당신의 아들을 친 뒤에야 다가오는 것을 보게 될지도 모릅니다.\""]], [["Villefort, suffocating, pressed the doctor’s arm.", "빌포르는 숨이 막힌 듯 의사의 팔을 움켜쥐었다."]], [["40124m\n“Listen,”", "\"들어 보세요.\""], ["cried he; “pity me—help me!", "그가 외쳤다. \"저를 가엾이 여겨 주십시오."], ["No, my daughter is not guilty.", "저를 도와주십시오!"], ["If you drag us both before a tribunal I will still say, ‘No, my daughter is not guilty;—there is no crime in my house.", "아니, 제 딸은 죄가 없습니다. 선생이 우리 둘을 법정으로 끌고 간다 해도, 저는 여전히 이렇게 말하겠습니다. '아니오, 내 딸은 죄가 없소."], ["I will not acknowledge a crime in my house; for when crime enters a dwelling, it is like death—it does not come alone.’ Listen.", "우리 집에는 범죄가 없소. 나는 우리 집의 범죄를 인정하지 않겠소. 범죄가 한 집에 들어올 때는 죽음과도 같아서, 결코 혼자 오지 않으니까.'"], ["What does it signify to you if I am murdered?", "들어 보세요. 제가 살해당한들 선생에게 무슨 상관입니까?"], ["Are you my friend?", "선생은 제 친구입니까?"], ["Are you a man?", "선생은 사람입니까?"], ["Have you a heart?", "선생에게 심장이 있습니까?"], ["No, you are a physician!", "아니지요, 선생은 의사일 뿐입니다!"], ["Well, I tell you I will not drag my daughter before a tribunal, and give her up to the executioner!", "자, 분명히 말씀드립니다. 저는 제 딸을 법정으로 끌고 가 사형 집행인에게 넘기지 않겠습니다! 그런 생각만으로도 저는 죽을 것 같습니다."], ["The bare idea would kill me—would drive me like a madman to dig my heart out with my finger-nails!", "미친 사람처럼 손톱으로 제 심장을 후벼 파게 될 겁니다! 그리고 만일 선생이 틀렸다면, 의사 선생, 만일 제 딸이 아니라면, 만일 어느 날 제가 유령처럼 창백한 얼굴로 선생을 찾아가 '살인자, 당신이 내 아이를 죽였소!'"], ["And if you were mistaken, doctor—if it were not my daughter—if I should come one day, pale as a spectre, and say to you, ‘Assassin, you have killed my child!’—hold—if that should happen, although I am a Christian, M. d’Avrigny, I should kill myself.”", "하고 말하게 된다면…… 멈추세요, 만일 그런 일이 일어난다면, 저는 기독교인이지만, 다브리니 씨, 저는 스스로 목숨을 끊을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"좋습니다.\""], ["said the doctor, after a moment’s silence, “I will wait.”", "잠시 침묵한 뒤 의사가 말했다. \"기다리지요.\""]], [["Villefort looked at him as if he had doubted his words.", "빌포르가 의사의 말을 의심하기라도 하는 듯 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“Only,”", "\"다만,\""], ["continued M. d’Avrigny, with a slow and solemn tone, “if anyone falls ill in your house, if you feel yourself attacked, do not send for me, for I will come no more.", "다브리니 씨가 느리고 엄숙한 어조로 말을 이었다. \"댁에서 누군가 병이 들거나, 검사님 자신이 공격당했다고 느끼시더라도, 저를 부르지 마십시오."], ["I will consent to share this dreadful secret with you, but I will not allow shame and remorse to grow and increase in my conscience, as crime and misery will in your house.”", "다시는 오지 않을 테니까요. 이 끔찍한 비밀을 검사님과 함께 나누는 것은 받아들이겠습니다. 하지만 댁에서 범죄와 불행이 자라나듯, 제 양심 속에서 수치와 회한이 자라나 커지도록 두지는 않겠습니다.\""]], [["“Then you abandon me, doctor?”", "\"그럼 저를 버리시는 겁니까, 의사 선생?\""]], [["“Yes, for I can follow you no farther, and I only stop at the foot of the scaffold.", "\"그렇습니다. 저는 더 이상 검사님을 따라갈 수 없고, 처형대 발치에서 멈출 뿐입니다."], ["Some further discovery will be made, which will bring this dreadful tragedy to a close. Adieu.”", "어떤 새로운 발견이 이루어져, 이 끔찍한 비극이 막을 내리게 될 겁니다. 그럼 안녕히 계십시오.\""]], [["“I entreat you, doctor!”", "\"부탁입니다, 의사 선생!\""]], [["“All the horrors that disturb my thoughts make your house odious and fatal. Adieu, sir.”", "\"제 머릿속을 어지럽히는 그 모든 참혹함이 댁을 끔찍하고 흉한 집으로 만듭니다. 안녕히 계십시오, 검사님.\""]], [["“One word—one single word more, doctor!", "\"한마디만, 한마디만 더요, 의사 선생!"], ["You go, leaving me in all the horror of my situation, after increasing it by what you have revealed to me.", "선생은 떠나면서, 제게 밝혀 주신 사실로 제 처지의 공포를 더해 놓고는, 저를 그 온갖 공포 속에 남겨 두고 가십니다."], ["But what will be reported of the sudden death of the poor old servant?”", "그런데 그 가엾은 늙은 하인의 갑작스러운 죽음은 어떻게 알릴까요?\""]], [["“True,” said M.", "\"옳은 말씀입니다.\""], ["d’Avrigny; “we will return.”", "다브리니 씨가 말했다. \"다시 안으로 들어갑시다.\""]], [["The doctor went out first, followed by M. de Villefort.", "의사가 앞서 나갔고, 빌포르 씨가 뒤따랐다."], ["The terrified servants were on the stairs and in the passage where the doctor would pass.", "겁에 질린 하인들이 의사가 지나갈 계단과 복도에 모여 있었다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"여러분,\""], ["said d’Avrigny to Villefort, so loud that all might hear, “poor Barrois has led too sedentary a life of late; accustomed formerly to ride on horseback, or in the carriage, to the four corners of Europe, the monotonous walk around that armchair has killed him—his blood has thickened.", "다브리니가 모두 들을 수 있도록 큰 소리로 빌포르에게 말했다. \"가엾은 바루아는 요즘 너무 앉아서만 지냈습니다. 예전에는 말을 타거나 마차로 유럽 구석구석을 누비던 사람인데, 그 안락의자 주위만 단조롭게 맴돌다 보니 목숨을 잃은 겁니다."], ["He was stout, had a short, thick neck; he was attacked with apoplexy, and I was called in too late.", "피가 걸쭉해진 거지요. 몸이 비대했고, 목이 짧고 굵었습니다. 뇌졸중이 그를 덮쳤는데, 제가 너무 늦게 불려 왔습니다. 그런데,\""], ["By the way,” added he in a low tone, “take care to throw away that cup of syrup of violets in the ashes.”", "그가 나직한 목소리로 덧붙였다. \"그 제비꽃 시럽 잔은 잿더미 속에 버려 두도록 하십시오.\""]], [["The doctor, without shaking hands with Villefort, without adding a word to what he had said, went out, amid the tears and lamentations of the whole household.", "의사는 빌포르와 악수도 나누지 않고, 방금 한 말에 한마디도 보태지 않은 채, 온 집안의 눈물과 통곡 속을 지나 밖으로 나갔다."], ["The same evening all Villefort’s servants, who had assembled in the kitchen, and had a long consultation, came to tell Madame de Villefort that they wished to leave.", "그날 저녁, 부엌에 모여 오랫동안 의논을 나눈 빌포르의 하인들이 모두 빌포르 부인에게 와서 떠나고 싶다고 말했다. 어떤 간청도, 봉급을 올려 주겠다는 제안도 그들을 붙잡아 둘 수 없었다."], ["No entreaty, no proposition of increased wages, could induce them to remain; to every argument they replied, “We must go, for death is in this house.”", "무슨 말을 해도 그들은 이렇게 대답할 뿐이었다. \"저희는 떠나야 합니다. 이 집에는 죽음이 있으니까요.\""]], [["They all left, in spite of prayers and entreaties, testifying their regret at leaving so good a master and mistress, and especially Mademoiselle Valentine, so good, so kind, and so gentle.", "그들은 그토록 좋은 주인 내외를, 특히 그토록 착하고 다정하고 상냥한 발랑틴 아가씨를 두고 떠나는 것을 못내 아쉬워하면서도, 기도와 간청에도 아랑곳없이 모두 떠나갔다."]], [["Villefort looked at Valentine as they said this.", "하인들이 그렇게 말하는 동안 빌포르는 발랑틴을 바라보았다. 그 아이는 눈물을 흘리고 있었다."], ["She was in tears, and, strange as it was, in spite of the emotions he felt at the sight of these tears, he looked also at Madame de Villefort, and it appeared to him as if a slight gloomy smile had passed over her thin lips, like a meteor seen passing inauspiciously between two clouds in a stormy sky.", "그런데 이상하게도, 그 눈물을 보며 가슴이 미어지는 와중에도, 그는 빌포르 부인 쪽으로도 눈길을 돌렸다. 그러자 그 여인의 얇은 입술 위로 음울한 미소가 살짝 스쳐 지나간 것만 같았다. 폭풍우 치는 하늘에서 두 구름 사이로 불길하게 지나가는 유성처럼."]], [["# Chapter 81. The Room of the Retired Baker", "# Chapter 81. The Room of the Retired Baker"]], [["The evening of the day on which the Count of Morcerf had left Danglars’ house with feelings of shame and anger at the rejection of the projected alliance, M. Andrea Cavalcanti, with curled hair, moustaches in perfect order, and white gloves which fitted admirably, had entered the courtyard of the banker’s house in Rue de la Chaussée d’Antin.", "모르세르 백작이 약혼 계획이 거절당한 데 수치와 분노를 느끼며 당글라르의 집을 떠난 그날 저녁, 안드레아 카발칸티는 곱슬머리를 매만지고 콧수염을 흠잡을 데 없이 다듬고 손에 꼭 맞는 흰 장갑을 낀 차림으로 쇼세당탱 거리에 있는 은행가의 저택 안뜰로 들어섰다."], ["He had not been more than ten minutes in the drawing-room before he drew Danglars aside into the recess of a bow-window, and, after an ingenious preamble, related to him all his anxieties and cares since his noble father’s departure.", "응접실에 들어선 지 십 분도 채 되지 않아 그는 당글라르를 활 모양 창의 움푹한 곳으로 끌고 가, 교묘한 서두를 늘어놓은 뒤 고귀한 아버지가 떠난 이후 자신이 겪은 온갖 근심과 걱정을 털어놓았다."], ["He acknowledged the extreme kindness which had been shown him by the banker’s family, in which he had been received as a son, and where, besides, his warmest affections had found an object on which to centre in Mademoiselle Danglars.", "그는 은행가의 가족이 자신을 아들처럼 받아 준 그 지극한 친절에 감사를 표했고, 게다가 당글라르 양에게서 가장 뜨거운 애정을 쏟을 대상을 찾았노라고 고백했다."]], [["Danglars listened with the most profound attention; he had expected this declaration for the last two or three days, and when at last it came his eyes glistened as much as they had lowered on listening to Morcerf.", "당글라르는 더없이 깊은 주의를 기울여 들었다. 그는 지난 이삼 일 동안 이 고백을 기다려 온 터였고, 마침내 그 말이 나오자 모르세르의 말을 들을 때 내리깔았던 그 눈만큼이나 두 눈이 반짝였다."], ["He would not, however, yield immediately to the young man’s request, but made a few conscientious objections.", "그러나 그는 젊은이의 청에 곧바로 응하지는 않고, 양심적인 척 몇 가지 이의를 제기했다."]], [["“Are you not rather young, M. Andrea, to think of marrying?”", "\"안드레아 군, 결혼을 생각하기엔 좀 젊지 않은가?\""]], [["“I think not, sir,”", "\"그렇지 않다고 봅니다.\""], ["replied M. Cavalcanti; “in Italy the nobility generally marry young.", "카발칸티가 대답했다. \"이탈리아에서는 귀족들이 대개 일찍 결혼합니다."], ["Life is so uncertain, that we ought to secure happiness while it is within our reach.”", "인생이란 워낙 불확실한 것이니, 행복이 손닿는 곳에 있을 때 붙잡아야 하지요.\""]], [["“Well, sir,”", "\"그렇군.\""], ["said Danglars, “in case your proposals, which do me honor, are accepted by my wife and daughter, by whom shall the preliminary arrangements be settled?", "당글라르가 말했다. \"내게는 영광스러운 자네의 청혼이 만약 아내와 딸에게 받아들여진다면, 사전 절차는 누가 처리하게 되는 건가?"], ["So important a negotiation should, I think, be conducted by the respective fathers of the young people.”", "이렇게 중대한 협상은 내 생각에 두 집안의 아버지들이 직접 진행하는 것이 마땅할 텐데.\""]], [["“Sir, my father is a man of great foresight and prudence.", "\"제 아버지는 대단히 멀리 내다보고 신중하신 분입니다."], ["Thinking that I might wish to settle in France, he left me at his departure, together with the papers establishing my identity, a letter promising, if he approved of my choice, 150,000 livres per annum from the day I was married.", "제가 프랑스에 정착하고 싶어 할지도 모른다고 생각하셔서, 떠나실 때 제 신분을 증명하는 서류들과 함께 편지 한 통을 남겨 주셨습니다. 제 선택을 승낙하신다면 결혼하는 날부터 해마다 십오만 리브르를 주겠다고 약속하는 편지였지요."], ["So far as I can judge, I suppose this to be a quarter of my father’s revenue.”", "제가 판단하기로는, 그것이 아버지 수입의 사분의 일쯤 되리라 짐작합니다.\""]], [["“I,”", "\"나는,\""], ["said Danglars, “have always intended giving my daughter 500,000 francs as her dowry; she is, besides, my sole heiress.”", "당글라르가 말했다. \"늘 딸에게 지참금으로 오십만 프랑을 줄 작정이었네. 게다가 그 아이는 내 유일한 상속자이고 말일세.\""]], [["“All would then be easily arranged if the baroness and her daughter are willing.", "\"그렇다면 남작 부인과 따님만 동의하신다면 모든 일이 수월하게 풀리겠군요."], ["We should command an annuity of 175,000 livres.", "우리는 해마다 십칠만 오천 리브르의 수입을 가지게 됩니다."], ["Supposing, also, I should persuade the marquis to give me my capital, which is not likely, but still is possible, we would place these two or three millions in your hands, whose talent might make it realize ten per cent.”", "게다가 만약 제가 후작께 제 자본을 직접 내어 달라고 설득할 수 있다면 — 그럴 가능성은 낮지만 아예 불가능한 것은 아니지요 — 그 이삼백만 프랑을 당글라르 씨 손에 맡길 수 있을 겁니다. 당글라르 씨의 수완이라면 그것으로 십 퍼센트의 수익을 낼 수도 있겠지요.\""]], [["“I never give more than four per cent, and generally only three and a half; but to my son-in-law I would give five, and we would share the profits.”", "\"나는 사 퍼센트 이상은 절대 주지 않고, 대개 삼 퍼센트 반밖에 안 주네. 하지만 사위에게라면 오 퍼센트를 주고, 수익은 나눠 갖도록 하지.\""]], [["“Very good, father-in-law,”", "\"아주 좋습니다, 장인어른.\""], ["said Cavalcanti, yielding to his low-born nature, which would escape sometimes through the aristocratic gloss with which he sought to conceal it.", "카발칸티가 말했다. 그는 귀족적인 광택으로 감추려 애쓰던 천한 본성이 이따금 새어 나오는 것을 막지 못했다. 그는 곧바로 말을 바로잡았다."], ["Correcting himself immediately, he said, “Excuse me, sir; hope alone makes me almost mad,—what will not reality do?”", "\"실례했습니다. 그저 희망만으로도 제정신이 아닐 지경인데, 현실이 되면 어떻겠습니까?\""]], [["“But,”", "\"그런데,\""], ["said Danglars, who, on his part, did not perceive how soon the conversation, which was at first disinterested, was turning to a business transaction, “there is, doubtless, a part of your fortune your father could not refuse you?”", "당글라르가 말했다. 그 또한 처음에는 사심 없이 시작된 대화가 어느새 사업 거래로 바뀌고 있다는 것을 알아차리지 못했다. \"분명 자네 재산 가운데 아버지가 거절할 수 없는 몫이 있을 텐데?\""]], [["“Which?” asked the young man.", "\"어떤 것 말씀이십니까?\" 젊은이가 물었다."]], [["“That you inherit from your mother.”", "\"어머니에게서 물려받은 것 말일세.\""]], [["“Truly, from my mother, Leonora Corsinari.” “How much may it amount to?”", "\"그렇군요, 제 어머니 레오노라 코르시나리에게서 물려받은 것이지요.\""]], [["“Indeed, sir,”", "\"얼마나 되는가?\" \"실은,\""], ["said Andrea, “I assure you I have never given the subject a thought, but I suppose it must have been at least two millions.”", "안드레아가 말했다. \"그 문제는 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없습니다만, 적어도 이백만 프랑은 되리라 짐작합니다.\""]], [["Danglars felt as much overcome with joy as the miser who finds a lost treasure, or as the shipwrecked mariner who feels himself on solid ground instead of in the abyss which he expected would swallow him up.", "당글라르는 잃어버린 보물을 찾은 구두쇠처럼, 또는 자신을 삼키리라 여겼던 심연 대신 단단한 땅을 디딘 난파 선원처럼 기쁨에 휩싸였다."]], [["“Well, sir,”", "\"자, 그렇다면,\""], ["said Andrea, bowing to the banker respectfully, “may I hope?”", "안드레아가 은행가에게 정중히 고개 숙이며 말했다. \"희망을 품어도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["“You may not only hope,”", "\"희망을 품어도 좋을 뿐 아니라,\""], ["said Danglars, “but consider it a settled thing, if no obstacle arises on your part.”", "당글라르가 말했다. \"자네 쪽에서 아무런 장애만 생기지 않는다면, 다 정해진 일로 여겨도 좋네.\""]], [["“I am, indeed, rejoiced,” said Andrea.", "\"정말로 기쁩니다.\" 안드레아가 말했다."]], [["“But,”", "\"그런데,\""], ["said Danglars thoughtfully, “how is it that your patron, M. de Monte Cristo, did not make his proposal for you?”", "당글라르가 생각에 잠겨 말했다. \"자네 후원자인 몬테크리스토 백작이 자네를 위해 직접 청혼하지 않은 것은 어찌 된 일인가?\""]], [["Andrea blushed imperceptibly.", "안드레아의 얼굴이 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 살짝 붉어졌다."]], [["“I have just left the count, sir,”", "\"방금 백작을 뵙고 오는 길입니다.\""], ["said he; “he is, doubtless, a delightful man but inconceivably peculiar in his ideas. He esteems me highly.", "그가 말했다. \"참으로 유쾌한 분이지만 생각하는 방식이 도무지 이해할 수 없을 만큼 별난 분이지요."], ["He even told me he had not the slightest doubt that my father would give me the capital instead of the interest of my property.", "저를 무척 높이 평가하십니다. 제 아버지가 재산의 이자가 아니라 자본 자체를 제게 내어 주리라는 데 추호도 의심이 없다고까지 말씀하셨습니다."], ["He has promised to use his influence to obtain it for me; but he also declared that he never had taken on himself the responsibility of making proposals for another, and he never would.", "그것을 얻을 수 있도록 영향력을 써 주겠노라 약속하셨지요. 하지만 남을 위해 청혼하는 책임은 한 번도 떠맡은 적이 없고 앞으로도 결코 그러지 않겠다고 분명히 말씀하셨습니다."], ["I must, however, do him the justice to add that he assured me if ever he had regretted the repugnance he felt to such a step it was on this occasion, because he thought the projected union would be a happy and suitable one.", "그래도 공정하게 한마디 덧붙이자면, 그런 일을 꺼리는 마음을 후회한 적이 있다면 바로 이번이라고 하셨습니다. 이 혼사가 행복하고 어울리는 결합이 되리라 여기셨기 때문이지요."], ["Besides, if he will do nothing officially, he will answer any questions you propose to him. And now,”", "게다가 공식적으로는 아무것도 하지 않으시겠지만, 당글라르 씨가 물어보시는 것에는 무엇이든 답해 주실 겁니다."], ["continued he, with one of his most charming smiles, “having finished talking to the father-in-law, I must address myself to the banker.”", "자, 그럼 이제,\" 그가 더없이 매혹적인 미소를 띠며 말을 이었다. \"장인어른과의 이야기는 끝났으니, 은행가께 말씀드려야겠군요.\""]], [["“And what may you have to say to him?”", "\"그래, 그 은행가에게 무슨 할 말이 있는가?\""], ["said Danglars, laughing in his turn.", "당글라르가 이번에는 자기가 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“That the day after tomorrow I shall have to draw upon you for about four thousand francs; but the count, expecting my bachelor’s revenue could not suffice for the coming month’s outlay, has offered me a draft for twenty thousand francs.", "\"모레쯤 약 사천 프랑을 인출해야 할 것 같습니다. 다만 백작께서 제 독신자 수입으로는 다음 달 지출을 감당하기 어려우리라 보시고, 이만 프랑짜리 어음을 주셨습니다."], ["It bears his signature, as you see, which is all-sufficient.”", "보시다시피 백작의 서명이 있으니, 그것으로 충분하지요.\""]], [["“Bring me a million such as that,”", "\"이런 어음을 백만 프랑어치 가져오게.\""], ["said Danglars, “I shall be well pleased,”", "당글라르가 어음을 주머니에 넣으며 말했다."], ["putting the draft in his pocket.", "\"그러면 나야 기쁠 따름이지."], ["“Fix your own hour for tomorrow, and my cashier shall call on you with a check for eighty thousand francs.”", "내일 편한 시각을 정하게. 우리 회계원이 팔만 프랑짜리 수표를 들고 자네를 찾아갈 테니.\""]], [["“At ten o’clock then, if you please; I should like it early, as I am going into the country tomorrow.”", "\"그럼 괜찮으시다면 열 시로 하지요. 내일 시골에 다녀올 참이라 이른 시각이 좋겠습니다.\""]], [["“Very well, at ten o’clock; you are still at the Hôtel des Princes?”", "\"좋아, 열 시로 하지. 아직 프랭스 호텔에 묵고 있나?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["The following morning, with the banker’s usual punctuality, the eighty thousand francs were placed in the young man’s hands, as he was on the point of starting, after having left two hundred francs for Caderousse.", "이튿날 아침, 은행가는 늘 그렇듯 정확히 약속을 지켜 팔만 프랑을 젊은이의 손에 건넸다. 안드레아는 카드루스 앞으로 이백 프랑을 남겨 둔 뒤 막 출발하려던 참이었다."], ["He went out chiefly to avoid this dangerous enemy, and returned as late as possible in the evening.", "그는 주로 이 위험한 적을 피하려고 집을 나섰고, 저녁에도 되도록 늦게 돌아왔다."]], [["But scarcely had he stepped out of his carriage when the porter met him with a parcel in his hand.", "그러나 마차에서 내리기가 무섭게 수위가 손에 꾸러미 하나를 들고 그를 맞았다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"나리,\""], ["said he, “that man has been here.”", "그가 말했다. \"그 사람이 다녀갔습니다.\""]], [["“What man?”", "\"무슨 사람 말인가?\""], ["said Andrea carelessly, apparently forgetting him whom he but too well recollected.", "안드레아가 무심하게 말했다. 너무도 잘 기억하고 있는 자를 까맣게 잊은 척하면서."]], [["“Him to whom your excellency pays that little annuity.”", "\"나리께서 그 적은 연금을 보내 주시는 사람 말입니다.\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"아,\""], ["said Andrea, “my father’s old servant.", "안드레아가 말했다. \"내 아버지의 옛 하인 말이군."], ["Well, you gave him the two hundred francs I had left for him?”", "그래, 내가 남겨 둔 이백 프랑은 그에게 주었나?\""]], [["“Yes, your excellency.”", "\"네, 각하.\""], ["Andrea had expressed a wish to be thus addressed. “But,”", "안드레아는 그렇게 불러 달라고 일러두었던 것이다. \"그런데,\""], ["continued the porter, “he would not take them.”", "수위가 말을 이었다. \"그가 받으려 하질 않았습니다.\""]], [["Andrea turned pale, but as it was dark his pallor was not perceptible. “What?", "안드레아의 얼굴이 창백해졌으나, 어두운 탓에 그 핏기 가신 얼굴은 눈에 띄지 않았다. \"뭐라고?"], ["he would not take them?”", "받으려 하지 않았다고?\""], ["said he with slight emotion. 40130m", "그가 살짝 동요하며 말했다."]], [["“No, he wished to speak to your excellency; I told him you were gone out, and after some dispute he believed me and gave me this letter, which he had brought with him already sealed.”", "\"네, 각하와 직접 이야기하고 싶다고 했습니다. 제가 외출하셨다고 말하니, 한참 실랑이를 벌인 끝에야 제 말을 믿고는 이미 봉인해서 가져온 이 편지를 제게 맡기더군요.\""]], [["“Give it me,”", "\"이리 주게.\""], ["said Andrea, and he read by the light of his carriage-lamp:", "안드레아가 말하고는 마차 등불에 비추어 읽었다."]], [["“‘You know where I live; I expect you tomorrow morning at nine o’clock.’”", "\"'자네는 내가 사는 곳을 알지. 내일 아침 아홉 시에 자네를 기다리겠네.'\""]], [["Andrea examined it carefully, to ascertain if the letter had been opened, or if any indiscreet eyes had seen its contents; but it was so carefully folded, that no one could have read it, and the seal was perfect.", "안드레아는 편지가 뜯긴 흔적이 있는지, 혹시 누군가의 경솔한 눈이 그 내용을 들여다본 것은 아닌지 확인하려고 꼼꼼히 살폈다. 그러나 어찌나 정성스럽게 접혀 있던지 아무도 읽었을 리 없었고, 봉인도 멀쩡했다."]], [["“Very well,” said he.", "\"좋아.\" 그가 말했다."], ["“Poor man, he is a worthy creature.”", "\"가엾은 사람, 참 갸륵한 자로군.\""], ["He left the porter to ponder on these words, not knowing which most to admire, the master or the servant.", "그는 이 말을 곱씹도록 수위를 남겨 두고 떠났고, 수위는 주인과 하인 중 누구를 더 우러러봐야 할지 알 수 없었다."]], [["“Take out the horses quickly, and come up to me,”", "\"어서 말들을 풀어 놓고 내 방으로 올라오게.\""], ["said Andrea to his groom.", "안드레아가 마부에게 말했다."], ["In two seconds the young man had reached his room and burnt Caderousse’s letter.", "젊은이는 눈 깜짝할 새에 자기 방에 이르러 카드루스의 편지를 태웠다."], ["The servant entered just as he had finished.", "그 일을 막 끝냈을 때 하인이 들어왔다."]], [["“You are about my height, Pierre,”", "\"피에르, 자네 키가 나와 비슷하지.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“I have that honor, your excellency.”", "\"영광입니다, 각하.\""]], [["“You had a new livery yesterday?”", "\"어제 새 제복을 받았지?\""]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "\"네, 나리.\""]], [["“I have an engagement with a pretty little girl for this evening, and do not wish to be known; lend me your livery till tomorrow.", "\"오늘 저녁에 예쁜 아가씨와 약속이 있는데, 누군지 들키고 싶지 않네. 내일까지 자네 제복을 좀 빌려주게."], ["I may sleep, perhaps, at an inn.”", "어쩌면 여관에서 자게 될지도 모르겠어.\""]], [["Pierre obeyed. Five minutes after, Andrea left the hotel, completely disguised, took a cabriolet, and ordered the driver to take him to the Cheval Rouge, at Picpus.", "피에르는 시키는 대로 했다. 오 분 뒤, 안드레아는 완전히 변장한 채로 호텔을 나서 이륜마차를 잡아타고는 마부에게 픽퓌스에 있는 슈발루주로 가자고 일렀다."], ["The next morning he left that inn as he had left the Hôtel des Princes, without being noticed, walked down the Faubourg Saint-Antoine, along the boulevard to Rue Ménilmontant, and stopping at the door of the third house on the left looked for someone of whom to make inquiry in the porter’s absence.", "이튿날 아침, 그는 프랭스 호텔에서 그랬듯 아무에게도 들키지 않고 그 여관을 나서, 생탕투안 변두리를 따라 내려가 큰길을 거쳐 메닐몽탕 거리로 접어들었다. 그러고는 왼편 세 번째 집 문 앞에 멈춰, 수위가 없는 터라 물어볼 사람을 찾았다."]], [["“For whom are you looking, my fine fellow?”", "\"누굴 찾으세요, 젊은이?\""], ["asked the fruiteress on the opposite side.", "길 건너편의 과일 장수 여자가 물었다."]], [["“Monsieur Pailletin, if you please, my good woman,” replied Andrea.", "\"파일레탱 씨를 찾는데요, 부인.\" 안드레아가 대답했다."]], [["“A retired baker?”", "\"퇴직한 빵집 주인 말인가요?\""], ["asked the fruiteress. “Exactly.”", "과일 장수가 물었다."]], [["“He lives at the end of the yard, on the left, on the third story.”", "\"바로 그렇습니다.\" \"안마당 끝, 왼쪽, 사 층에 살아요.\""]], [["40132m\nAndrea went as she directed him, and on the third floor he found a hare’s paw, which, by the hasty ringing of the bell, it was evident he pulled with considerable ill-temper.", "안드레아는 일러 준 대로 갔고, 사 층에서 토끼발 모양의 초인종 손잡이를 발견했다. 그가 그것을 잡아당기는 다급한 소리로 보아, 상당히 언짢은 기분이라는 것이 분명했다."], ["A moment after Caderousse’s face appeared at the grating in the door.", "잠시 후 문에 난 쇠창살 너머로 카드루스의 얼굴이 나타났다."]], [["“Ah! you are punctual,”", "\"아! 시간을 잘 지켰군.\""], ["said he, as he drew back the door.", "그가 문을 열며 말했다."]], [["“Confound you and your punctuality!”", "\"빌어먹을, 자네와 자네의 그 시간 타령 같으니!\""], ["said Andrea, throwing himself into a chair in a manner which implied that he would rather have flung it at the head of his host.", "안드레아가 말하며 의자에 털썩 주저앉았다. 차라리 그 의자를 집주인 머리통에 집어 던지고 싶다는 듯한 태도였다."]], [["“Come, come, my little fellow, don’t be angry.", "\"이봐, 이봐, 친구, 화내지 말게."], ["See, I have thought about you—look at the good breakfast we are going to have; nothing but what you are fond of.”", "보게나, 내가 자네 생각을 했다네. 우리가 먹을 이 근사한 아침을 좀 보라고. 죄다 자네가 좋아하는 것뿐이야.\""]], [["Andrea, indeed, inhaled the scent of something cooking which was not unwelcome to him, hungry as he was; it was that mixture of fat and garlic peculiar to Provençal kitchens of an inferior order, added to that of dried fish, and above all, the pungent smell of musk and cloves.", "과연 안드레아는 무언가 익어 가는 냄새를 맡았고, 시장하던 터라 그것이 싫지만은 않았다. 그것은 하급 프로방스풍 부엌 특유의 기름과 마늘 냄새에 말린 생선 냄새가 더해지고, 무엇보다 사향과 정향의 톡 쏘는 냄새가 어우러진 것이었다."], ["These odors escaped from two deep dishes which were covered and placed on a stove, and from a copper pan placed in an old iron pot.", "그 냄새들은 뚜껑을 덮어 화덕 위에 올려놓은 깊은 접시 두 개와, 낡은 무쇠솥 속에 든 구리 냄비에서 새어 나왔다."], ["In an adjoining room Andrea saw also a tolerably clean table prepared for two, two bottles of wine sealed, the one with green, the other with yellow, a supply of brandy in a decanter, and a measure of fruit in a cabbage-leaf, cleverly arranged on an earthenware plate.", "옆방에는 제법 깨끗한 식탁이 둘을 위해 차려진 것도 보였다. 봉인된 포도주 두 병이 놓여 있었는데 하나는 초록색, 하나는 노란색으로 봉해져 있었고, 유리병에 담긴 브랜디와 양배추 잎에 담은 과일이 질그릇 접시 위에 솜씨 좋게 담겨 있었다."]], [["“What do you think of it, my little fellow?”", "\"어떤가, 친구?\" 카드루스가 말했다."], ["said Caderousse.", "\"응, 냄새 좋지!"], ["“Ay, that smells good! You know I used to be a good cook; do you recollect how you used to lick your fingers?", "내가 한때 솜씨 좋은 요리사였던 거 알잖나. 자네가 손가락을 쪽쪽 빨던 거 기억나나?"], ["You were among the first who tasted any of my dishes, and I think you relished them tolerably.”", "자네는 내 요리를 맨 처음 맛본 축에 들었고, 제법 입에 맞아 했지.\""], ["While speaking, Caderousse went on peeling a fresh supply of onions.", "카드루스는 그렇게 말하면서 양파를 새로 까기 시작했다."]], [["“But,”", "\"그런데,\""], ["said Andrea, ill-temperedly, “by my faith, if it was only to breakfast with you, that you disturbed me, I wish the devil had taken you!”", "안드레아가 언짢게 말했다. \"정말이지, 고작 자네와 아침이나 먹자고 날 불러낸 거라면, 차라리 악마가 자네를 데려갔으면 좋겠군!\""]], [["“My boy,”", "\"이 사람아,\""], ["said Caderousse sententiously, “one can talk while eating.", "카드루스가 점잔 빼며 말했다. \"먹으면서도 얘기는 할 수 있는 법이지."], ["And then, you ungrateful being, you are not pleased to see an old friend?", "그리고, 이 배은망덕한 친구야, 옛 친구를 보는 게 그리 반갑지 않은가?"], ["I am weeping with joy.”", "나는 기뻐서 눈물이 다 나는데.\""]], [["He was truly crying, but it would have been difficult to say whether joy or the onions produced the greatest effect on the lachrymal glands of the old innkeeper of the Pont-du-Gard.", "그는 정말로 울고 있었으나, 퐁뒤가르의 옛 여관 주인의 눈물샘을 가장 크게 자극한 것이 기쁨인지 양파인지는 가려내기 어려웠을 것이다."]], [["“Hold your tongue, hypocrite,”", "\"입 다물게, 위선자 같으니.\""], ["said Andrea; “you love me!”", "안드레아가 말했다. \"자네가 날 사랑한다고?\""]], [["“Yes, I do, or may the devil take me.", "\"그래, 사랑하지. 거짓말이면 악마가 날 잡아가도 좋네."], ["I know it is a weakness,”", "그게 약점인 줄은 알아.\""], ["said Caderousse, “but it overpowers me.”", "카드루스가 말했다. \"하지만 어쩔 수가 없어.\""]], [["“And yet it has not prevented your sending for me to play me some trick.”", "\"그러면서도 날 불러내 무슨 수작을 부리는 건 막지 못했군그래.\""]], [["“Come,”", "\"이봐.\""], ["said Caderousse, wiping his large knife on his apron, “if I did not like you, do you think I should endure the wretched life you lead me?", "카드루스가 앞치마에 큼직한 칼을 닦으며 말했다. \"내가 자넬 좋아하지 않았다면, 자네가 내게 떠안긴 이 비참한 삶을 견뎌 냈겠나?"], ["Think for a moment.", "잠깐 생각해 보게."], ["You have your servant’s clothes on—you therefore keep a servant; I have none, and am obliged to prepare my own meals.", "자네는 하인의 옷을 입었으니, 하인을 두고 있는 거야. 나는 하인이 없어 끼니도 직접 차려야 하지."], ["You abuse my cookery because you dine at the table d’hôte of the Hôtel des Princes, or the Café de Paris.", "자네는 프랭스 호텔이나 카페 드 파리에서 정찬을 드니까 내 요리를 깔보지."], ["Well, I too could keep a servant; I too could have a tilbury; I too could dine where I like; but why do I not?", "그래, 나도 하인을 둘 수 있고, 마차도 부릴 수 있고, 어디서든 마음대로 식사할 수 있어. 그런데 왜 안 그러겠나?"], ["Because I would not annoy my little Benedetto.", "우리 어린 베네데토를 성가시게 하고 싶지 않아서지."], ["Come, just acknowledge that I could, eh?”", "자, 인정하게. 나도 그럴 수 있다는 걸, 응?\""]], [["This address was accompanied by a look which was by no means difficult to understand.", "이 말에는 도무지 알아듣기 어렵지 않은 눈빛이 함께 따라붙었다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"좋아.\""], ["said Andrea, “admitting your love, why do you want me to breakfast with you?”", "안드레아가 말했다. \"자네 애정을 인정한다 치고, 그래서 왜 나더러 같이 아침을 먹자는 거지?\""]], [["“That I may have the pleasure of seeing you, my little fellow.”", "\"자네를 보는 즐거움을 누리고 싶어서지, 친구.\""]], [["“What is the use of seeing me after we have made all our arrangements?”", "\"우리가 할 얘기를 다 끝낸 마당에 날 봐서 뭐 하게?\""]], [["“Eh, dear friend,”", "\"이런, 친구.\""], ["said Caderousse, “are wills ever made without codicils?", "카드루스가 말했다. \"유언장에 부록이 안 붙는 법이 있나?"], ["But you first came to breakfast, did you not?", "어쨌든 자네가 먼저 아침을 먹으러 온 거잖나?"], ["Well, sit down, and let us begin with these pilchards, and this fresh butter; which I have put on some vine-leaves to please you, wicked one.", "자, 앉아서 이 정어리하고 이 신선한 버터부터 들지. 자네 좋으라고 포도 잎 위에 얹어 놓았다고, 못된 친구야."], ["Ah, yes; you look at my room, my four straw chairs, my images, three francs each.", "아, 그래, 자네 내 방을 둘러보는군. 짚의자 네 개에, 한 개에 삼 프랑짜리 그림들이라니."], ["But what do you expect?", "하지만 뭘 기대하나?"], ["This is not the Hôtel des Princes.”", "여긴 프랭스 호텔이 아니야.\""]], [["“Come, you are growing discontented, you are no longer happy; you, who only wish to live like a retired baker.”", "\"이봐, 자네 갈수록 불만이 늘어 가는군. 이제는 행복하지도 않고 말이야. 퇴직한 빵집 주인처럼 사는 게 소원이라더니.\""]], [["Caderousse sighed.", "카드루스가 한숨을 내쉬었다."]], [["“Well, what have you to say?", "\"이런, 무슨 할 말이 있나?"], ["you have seen your dream realized.”", "자네 꿈이 이루어진 걸 두 눈으로 봤잖나.\""]], [["“I can still say it is a dream; a retired baker, my poor Benedetto, is rich—he has an annuity.”", "\"그래도 꿈이라고밖에 못 하겠어. 퇴직한 빵집 주인이라면, 가엾은 베네데토, 부자라야지. 연금이 있어야 한단 말일세.\""]], [["“Well, you have an annuity.”", "\"이런, 자네한테도 연금이 있잖나.\""]], [["“I have?”", "\"내가?\""]], [["40134m\n“Yes, since I bring you your two hundred francs.”", "\"그래, 내가 자네한테 이백 프랑을 가져다주니 말이야.\""]], [["Caderousse shrugged his shoulders.", "카드루스가 어깨를 으쓱했다."]], [["“It is humiliating,”", "\"굴욕적이야.\""], ["said he, “thus to receive money given grudgingly,—an uncertain supply which may soon fail.", "그가 말했다. \"이렇게 마지못해 내주는 돈을, 언제 끊길지 모를 불안한 돈을 받는다는 게 말이야."], ["You see I am obliged to economize, in case your prosperity should cease.", "보다시피 나는 절약을 해야 해. 자네 형편이 기울 경우를 대비해서지."], ["Well, my friend, fortune is inconstant, as the chaplain of the regiment said.", "그래, 친구, 운명이란 변덕스러운 거야, 연대 군목이 말했듯이 말일세."], ["I know your prosperity is great, you rascal; you are to marry the daughter of Danglars.”", "자네 형편이 대단하다는 건 알지, 이 악당아. 자네는 당글라르의 딸과 결혼할 거잖나.\""]], [["“What?", "\"뭐?"], ["of Danglars?”", "당글라르의 딸과?\""]], [["“Yes, to be sure; must I say Baron Danglars?", "\"그래, 그렇고말고. 당글라르 남작이라고 해야 하나?"], ["I might as well say Count Benedetto.", "그럼 나도 베네데토 백작이라고 하지 뭐. 그자는 내 오랜 친구였어."], ["He was an old friend of mine and if he had not so bad a memory he ought to invite me to your wedding, seeing he came to mine.", "기억력이 그렇게 나쁘지만 않다면 자네 결혼식에 날 초대해야 마땅하지. 내 결혼식엔 왔으니까 말이야. 그래, 그래, 내 결혼식에 왔다고."], ["Yes, yes, to mine; gad, he was not so proud then,—he was an under-clerk to the good M. Morrel.", "제기랄, 그때는 그렇게 거만하지 않았어. 그 선량한 모렐 씨 밑에서 일하는 하급 사무원이었으니까."], ["I have dined many times with him and the Count of Morcerf, so you see I have some high connections and were I to cultivate them a little, we might meet in the same drawing-rooms.”", "나는 그자랑 모르세르 백작이랑 여러 번 같이 식사했어. 그러니 보다시피 내게도 지체 높은 인맥이 좀 있는 거지. 내가 그걸 조금만 가꾸면 같은 응접실에서 마주칠 수도 있을걸.\""]], [["“Come, your jealousy represents everything to you in the wrong light.”", "\"이봐, 자네 질투 때문에 모든 게 비뚤어져 보이는 거야.\""]], [["“That is all very fine, Benedetto mio, but I know what I am saying.", "\"말이야 그럴듯하지, 내 사랑 베네데토. 하지만 나는 내가 무슨 말을 하는지 알아."], ["Perhaps I may one day put on my best coat, and presenting myself at the great gate, introduce myself.", "어쩌면 언젠가 내 가장 좋은 코트를 차려입고, 그 큰 대문 앞에 나타나 나 자신을 소개할지도 모르지."], ["Meanwhile let us sit down and eat.”", "그동안은 앉아서 먹기나 하세.\""]], [["Caderousse set the example and attacked the breakfast with good appetite, praising each dish he set before his visitor.", "카드루스는 앞장서서 왕성한 식욕으로 아침을 공략하며, 손님 앞에 내놓는 요리마다 칭찬을 늘어놓았다."], ["The latter seemed to have resigned himself; he drew the corks, and partook largely of the fish with the garlic and fat.", "손님은 체념한 듯 보였다. 그는 마개를 뽑고, 마늘과 기름을 곁들인 생선을 푸짐하게 먹었다."]], [["“Ah, mate,”", "\"아, 친구.\""], ["said Caderousse, “you are getting on better terms with your old landlord!”", "카드루스가 말했다. \"옛 집주인하고 사이가 좀 좋아지는군!\""]], [["“Faith, yes,”", "\"그래, 정말이지.\""], ["replied Andrea, whose hunger prevailed over every other feeling.", "안드레아가 대답했다. 그의 허기가 다른 모든 감정을 눌렀던 것이다."]], [["“So you like it, you rogue?”", "\"맛이 좋단 말이지, 이 악동아?\""]], [["“So much that I wonder how a man who can cook thus can complain of hard living.”", "\"어찌나 좋은지, 이렇게 요리하는 사람이 어떻게 살림살이가 힘들다고 투덜대는지 모르겠어.\""]], [["“Do you see,”", "\"보게나.\""], ["said Caderousse, “all my happiness is marred by one thought?”", "카드루스가 말했다. \"내 행복은 단 한 가지 생각 때문에 다 망쳐진다네.\""]], [["“What is that?”", "\"그게 뭔데?\""]], [["“That I am dependent on another, I who have always gained my own livelihood honestly.”", "\"내가 남에게 기대어 산다는 거지. 평생 정직하게 제 밥벌이를 해 온 이 몸이 말이야.\""]], [["“Do not let that disturb you, I have enough for two.”", "\"그런 걸로 마음 졸이지 말게. 내겐 둘이 먹고도 남을 만큼 있으니까.\""]], [["“No, truly; you may believe me if you will; at the end of every month I am tormented by remorse.”", "\"아니야, 정말이야. 믿거나 말거나, 매달 말이면 나는 양심의 가책에 시달려.\""]], [["“Good Caderousse!”", "\"착한 카드루스 같으니!\""]], [["“So much so, that yesterday I would not take the two hundred francs.”", "\"어찌나 시달리는지, 어제는 그 이백 프랑을 받지 않으려 했다네.\""]], [["“Yes, you wished to speak to me; but was it indeed remorse, tell me?”", "\"그래, 자네 나하고 얘기하고 싶었다고 했지. 한데 정말로 그게 양심의 가책 때문이었나, 말해 보게?\""]], [["“True remorse; and, besides, an idea had struck me.”", "\"진짜 양심의 가책 때문이었어. 게다가, 한 가지 생각이 떠올랐거든.\""]], [["Andrea shuddered; he always did so at Caderousse’s ideas.", "안드레아가 몸을 떨었다. 그는 카드루스의 '생각'이라는 말에 늘 이렇게 떨었다."]], [["“It is miserable—do you see?—always to wait till the end of the month.”", "\"비참한 일이지, 안 그래? 늘 월말까지 기다려야 하다니 말이야.\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"이런.\""], ["said Andrea philosophically, determined to watch his companion narrowly, “does not life pass in waiting?", "안드레아가 짐짓 철학자인 양 말했다. 그는 상대를 면밀히 살피기로 작정한 터였다. \"인생이란 게 기다림 속에 흘러가는 것 아닌가?"], ["Do I, for instance, fare better?", "가령 나라고 형편이 더 낫겠나?"], ["Well, I wait patiently, do I not?”", "그래도 나는 참을성 있게 기다리잖나, 안 그래?\""]], [["“Yes; because instead of expecting two hundred wretched francs, you expect five or six thousand, perhaps ten, perhaps even twelve, for you take care not to let anyone know the utmost.", "\"그래. 하지만 자네는 비참한 이백 프랑을 기다리는 게 아니라 오천이나 육천, 어쩌면 만, 어쩌면 만 이천을 기다리지. 자네는 그 최대치를 누구에게도 알리지 않으려고 조심하니까."], ["Down there, you always had little presents and Christmas-boxes, which you tried to hide from your poor friend Caderousse.", "거기 있을 때도 자네는 늘 자잘한 선물이며 크리스마스 용돈을 받았고, 그걸 가엾은 친구 카드루스한테 숨기려 했지."], ["Fortunately he is a cunning fellow, that friend Caderousse.”", "다행히도 그 친구 카드루스가 약은 사람이라서 말이야.\""]], [["“There you are beginning again to ramble, to talk again and again of the past!", "\"또 시작이군. 또 옛날 얘기를 자꾸자꾸 늘어놓는단 말이야!"], ["But what is the use of teasing me with going all over that again?”", "한데 그 일을 다시 죄다 들먹이며 날 괴롭혀서 뭐 하자는 건가?\""]], [["“Ah, you are only one-and-twenty, and can forget the past; I am fifty, and am obliged to recollect it.", "\"아, 자네는 겨우 스물한 살이니 과거를 잊을 수 있지. 나는 쉰이니 그걸 기억할 수밖에 없어."], ["But let us return to business.”", "하지만 본론으로 돌아가세.\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["“I was going to say, if I were in your place——”", "\"내가 하려던 말은, 내가 자네 처지라면 말이야……\""]], [["“Well.”", "\"그래서?\""]], [["“I would realize——”", "\"현금으로 바꿔 두겠어……\""]], [["“How would you realize?”", "\"어떻게 바꾸겠다는 거지?\""]], [["“I would ask for six months’ in advance, under pretence of being able to purchase a farm, then with my six months I would decamp.”", "\"농장을 하나 살 수 있다는 핑계로 여섯 달치를 미리 받아 내겠어. 그러고는 그 여섯 달치를 챙겨서 줄행랑을 치는 거지.\""]], [["“Well, well,”", "\"그래, 그래.\""], ["said Andrea, “that isn’t a bad idea.”", "안드레아가 말했다. \"나쁜 생각은 아니군.\""]], [["“My dear friend,”", "\"친구.\""], ["said Caderousse, “eat of my bread, and take my advice; you will be none the worse off, physically or morally.”", "카드루스가 말했다. \"내 빵을 먹었으면 내 충고도 받아들이게. 몸으로 보나 마음으로 보나 손해 볼 것 없을 테니.\""]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["said Andrea, “why do you not act on the advice you gave me?", "안드레아가 말했다. \"자네는 왜 자네가 내게 준 그 충고대로 하지 않나?"], ["Why do you not realize a six months’, a year’s advance even, and retire to Brussels?", "자네는 왜 여섯 달치, 아니 일 년 치라도 미리 받아 내서 브뤼셀로 물러나지 않는 거지?"], ["Instead of living the retired baker, you might live as a bankrupt, using his privileges; that would be very good.”", "퇴직한 빵집 주인으로 사느니, 파산자의 특권을 누리며 파산자로 살 수도 있을 텐데. 그게 아주 좋을 것 같은데 말이야.\""]], [["“But how the devil would you have me retire on twelve hundred francs?”", "\"한데 빌어먹을, 천이백 프랑으로 어떻게 물러나라는 건가?\""]], [["“Ah, Caderousse,”", "\"아, 카드루스.\""], ["said Andrea, “how covetous you are!", "안드레아가 말했다. \"자네 욕심도 참 많구먼!"], ["Two months ago you were dying with hunger.”", "두 달 전만 해도 굶어 죽어 가던 사람이.\""]], [["“The appetite grows by what it feeds on,”", "\"식욕이란 먹을수록 자라는 법이야.\""], ["said Caderousse, grinning and showing his teeth, like a monkey laughing or a tiger growling.", "카드루스가 이를 드러내며 씩 웃었다. 웃는 원숭이나 으르렁대는 호랑이 같은 모습이었다. \"그리고,\""], ["“And,” added he, biting off with his large white teeth an enormous mouthful of bread, “I have formed a plan.”", "그가 그 크고 흰 이로 빵 한 덩이를 큼직하게 베어 물며 덧붙였다. \"내가 계획을 하나 세웠다네.\""]], [["Caderousse’s plans alarmed Andrea still more than his ideas; ideas were but the germ, the plan was reality.", "카드루스의 '계획'은 그의 '생각'보다 안드레아를 한층 더 불안하게 했다. 생각이 씨앗에 불과하다면, 계획은 현실이었다."]], [["“Let me see your plan; I dare say it is a pretty one.”", "\"어디 그 계획 좀 들어 보세. 분명 그럴듯한 거겠지.\""]], [["“Why not?", "\"왜 아니겠나?"], ["Who formed the plan by which we left the establishment of M——! eh?", "우리가 ○○ 시설을 빠져나온 그 계획을 누가 세웠더라? 응?"], ["was it not I?", "그게 나 아니었나?"], ["and it was no bad one I believe, since here we are!”", "게다가 우리가 이렇게 여기 멀쩡히 있는 걸 보면 제법 괜찮은 계획이었던 셈이지!\""]], [["“I do not say,”", "\"자네가 좋은 계획을 한 번도 못 세운다는 말은 아니야.\""], ["replied Andrea, “that you never make a good one; but let us see your plan.”", "안드레아가 대답했다. \"어쨌든 그 계획이나 들어 보세.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"좋아.\""], ["pursued Caderousse, “can you without expending one sou, put me in the way of getting fifteen thousand francs?", "카드루스가 말을 이었다. \"자네, 한 푼도 들이지 않고, 내가 만 오천 프랑을 손에 넣을 길을 마련해 줄 수 있겠나?"], ["No, fifteen thousand are not enough,—I cannot again become an honest man with less than thirty thousand francs.”", "아니, 만 오천으로는 모자라. 삼만 프랑은 있어야 다시 정직한 사람이 될 수 있어.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"안 돼.\""], ["replied Andrea, dryly, “no, I cannot.”", "안드레아가 무뚝뚝하게 대답했다. \"안 돼, 못 하겠어.\""]], [["“I do not think you understand me,”", "\"자네가 내 말을 못 알아들은 것 같군.\""], ["replied Caderousse, calmly; “I said without your laying out a sou.”", "카드루스가 차분하게 대답했다. \"자네는 한 푼도 들이지 않아도 된다고 했잖나.\""]], [["“Do you want me to commit a robbery, to spoil all my good fortune—and yours with mine—and both of us to be dragged down there again?”", "\"내가 강도질을 해서, 내 행운을 — 그리고 내 행운과 함께 자네 행운까지 — 다 망쳐 버리고, 우리 둘이 다시 거기로 끌려가기라도 바라나?\""]], [["“It would make very little difference to me,”", "\"나야 다시 잡혀가도 별 상관없어.\""], ["said Caderousse, “if I were retaken, I am a poor creature to live alone, and sometimes pine for my old comrades; not like you, heartless creature, who would be glad never to see them again.”", "카드루스가 말했다. \"나는 혼자 사는 게 영 못 견디는 가엾은 인간이라, 가끔 옛 동료들이 그립거든. 그자들을 다시는 안 보면 좋겠다고 여길 자네 같은 매정한 인간과는 다르지.\""]], [["Andrea did more than tremble this time, he turned pale.", "이번에 안드레아는 떨기만 한 것이 아니라 얼굴까지 새파래졌다."]], [["“Come, Caderousse, no nonsense!”", "\"이봐, 카드루스, 허튼소리 말게!\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Don’t alarm yourself, my little Benedetto, but just point out to me some means of gaining those thirty thousand francs without your assistance, and I will contrive it.”", "\"겁먹지 말게, 내 어린 베네데토. 그저 자네 도움 없이 그 삼만 프랑을 손에 넣을 방법을 좀 일러 주게. 그럼 내가 알아서 꾸려 볼 테니.\""]], [["“Well, I’ll see—I’ll try to contrive some way,”", "\"그래, 어디 보자고. 무슨 수가 없는지 궁리해 보지.\""], ["said Andrea.", "안드레아가 말했다."]], [["“Meanwhile you will raise my monthly allowance to five hundred francs, my little fellow? I have a fancy, and mean to get a housekeeper.”", "\"그동안 내 월 수당은 오백 프랑으로 올려 주겠지, 친구? 한 가지 바람이 생겼는데, 가정부를 하나 두려고.\""]], [["“Well, you shall have your five hundred francs,”", "\"좋아, 오백 프랑을 받게 해 주지.\" 안드레아가 말했다."], ["said Andrea; “but it is very hard for me, my poor Caderousse—you take advantage——”", "\"하지만 나로서는 무척 힘든 일이야, 가엾은 카드루스. 자네 자꾸 약점을 잡아……\""]], [["“Bah,”", "\"흥.\""], ["said Caderousse, “when you have access to countless stores.”", "카드루스가 말했다. \"자네는 셀 수도 없이 많은 곳간에 손을 댈 수 있으면서 뭘.\""]], [["One would have said Andrea anticipated his companion’s words, so did his eye flash like lightning, but it was but for a moment.", "안드레아가 상대의 말을 미리 예상하고 있었다고 해도 좋을 만큼, 그의 눈이 번개처럼 번뜩였다. 그러나 그것도 한순간뿐이었다."]], [["“True,”", "\"맞아.\""], ["he replied, “and my protector is very kind.”", "그가 대답했다. \"내 후원자는 무척 너그러운 분이지.\""]], [["“That dear protector,”", "\"그 인자하신 후원자께서,\""], ["said Caderousse; “and how much does he give you monthly?”", "카드루스가 말했다. \"자네한테 매달 얼마나 주시는데?\""]], [["“Five thousand francs.”", "\"오천 프랑이야.\""]], [["“As many thousands as you give me hundreds! Truly, it is only bastards who are thus fortunate.", "\"자네가 내게 백 프랑 주는 만큼 그분은 자네에게 천 프랑씩 주는군! 정말이지, 이렇게 운 좋은 건 사생아들뿐이라니까."], ["Five thousand francs per month!", "매달 오천 프랑이라!"], ["What the devil can you do with all that?”", "빌어먹을, 그 많은 돈으로 대체 뭘 하나?\""]], [["“Oh, it is no trouble to spend that; and I am like you, I want capital.”", "\"아, 그 정도 쓰는 거야 일도 아니지. 게다가 나도 자네처럼 목돈이 필요하거든.\""]], [["“Capital?—yes—I understand—everyone would like capital.”", "\"목돈이라…… 그래…… 알지…… 누군들 목돈이 갖고 싶지 않겠나.\""]], [["“Well, and I shall get it.”", "\"그래, 그래서 나는 그걸 손에 넣을 거야.\""]], [["“Who will give it to you—your prince?”", "\"누가 자네한테 주는데? 그 자네 영주께서?\""]], [["“Yes, my prince.", "\"그래, 내 영주께서."], ["But unfortunately I must wait.” 40138m", "하지만 안타깝게도 기다려야 해.\""]], [["“You must wait for what?”", "\"무얼 기다린다는 거지?\""], ["asked Caderousse.", "카드루스가 물었다."]], [["“For his death.”", "\"그분이 죽기를.\""]], [["“The death of your prince?”", "\"자네 영주가 죽기를?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["“How so?”", "\"어째서?\""]], [["“Because he has made his will in my favor.”", "\"그분이 나를 수혜자로 유언장을 작성했거든.\""]], [["“Indeed?”", "\"정말로?\""]], [["“On my honor.”", "\"맹세코.\""]], [["“For how much?”", "\"얼마나?\""]], [["“For five hundred thousand.”", "\"오십만이야.\""]], [["“Only that?", "\"그게 다야?"], ["It’s little enough.”", "그건 너무 적은데.\""]], [["“But so it is.”", "\"하지만 그렇다네.\""]], [["“No, it cannot be!”", "\"아니, 그럴 리가!\""]], [["“Are you my friend, Caderousse?”", "\"자네 내 친구지, 카드루스?\""]], [["“Yes, in life or death.”", "\"그럼, 죽으나 사나.\""]], [["“Well, I will tell you a secret.”", "\"그럼 비밀 하나를 말해 주지.\""]], [["“What is it?”", "\"뭔데?\""]], [["“But remember——”", "\"하지만 명심하게……\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["_pardieu!_ mute as a carp.”", "제기랄, 잉어처럼 입 꾹 다물지.\""]], [["“Well, I think——”", "\"그게 말이야, 내 생각엔……\""]], [["Andrea stopped and looked around.", "안드레아가 말을 멈추고 주위를 둘러보았다."]], [["“You think?", "\"생각엔?"], ["Do not fear; _pardieu!_ we are alone.”", "걱정 말게. 제기랄, 우리뿐이잖나.\""]], [["“I think I have discovered my father.”", "\"내 진짜 아버지를 찾아낸 것 같아.\""]], [["“Your true father?”", "\"자네 진짜 아버지를?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["“Not old Cavalcanti?”", "\"늙은 카발칸티 말고?\""]], [["“No, for he has gone again; the true one, as you say.”", "\"아니야, 그자는 또 떠나 버렸으니까. 자네 말마따나, 진짜 아버지 말이야.\""]], [["“And that father is——”", "\"그래서 그 아버지가……\""]], [["“Well, Caderousse, it is Monte Cristo.”", "\"그게 말이야, 카드루스, 몬테크리스토라네.\""]], [["“Bah!”", "\"설마!\""]], [["“Yes, you understand, that explains all.", "\"그래, 알겠나, 그러면 다 설명이 되지."], ["He cannot acknowledge me openly, it appears, but he does it through M. Cavalcanti, and gives him fifty thousand francs for it.”", "보아하니 그분은 날 드러내 놓고 아들로 인정할 수가 없어서, 카발칸티 씨를 내세워 그렇게 하고, 그 대가로 오만 프랑을 주는 거야.\""]], [["“Fifty thousand francs for being your father?", "\"자네 아버지 노릇 하는 값으로 오만 프랑이라고?"], ["I would have done it for half that, for twenty thousand, for fifteen thousand; why did you not think of me, ungrateful man?”", "나라면 그 절반에도, 이만에도, 만 오천에도 했을 텐데. 왜 날 생각하지 않았나, 이 배은망덕한 사람아?\""]], [["“Did I know anything about it, when it was all done when I was down there?”", "\"그게 다 내가 거기 있을 때 끝난 일인데, 내가 어찌 알았겠나?\""]], [["“Ah, truly?", "\"아, 정말로?"], ["And you say that by his will——”", "그래서 그 유언장에 따라……\""]], [["“He leaves me five hundred thousand livres.”", "\"내게 오십만 리브르를 남기시는 거지.\""]], [["“Are you sure of it?”", "\"확실해?\""]], [["“He showed it me; but that is not all—there is a codicil, as I said just now.”", "\"내게 직접 보여 주셨어. 한데 그게 다가 아니야. 아까 말했듯이 부록이 있거든.\""]], [["“Probably.”", "\"그럴 만도 하지.\""]], [["“And in that codicil he acknowledges me.”", "\"그리고 그 부록에서 나를 아들로 인정하셨어.\""]], [["“Oh, the good father, the brave father, the very honest father!”", "\"오, 인자하신 아버지, 갸륵하신 아버지, 더없이 정직하신 아버지로다!\""], ["said Caderousse, twirling a plate in the air between his two hands.", "카드루스가 양손 사이에서 접시를 빙글빙글 돌리며 말했다."]], [["“Now, say if I conceal anything from you?”", "\"자, 내가 자네한테 뭐라도 숨기는 게 있던가?\""]], [["“No, and your confidence makes you honorable in my opinion; and your princely father, is he rich, very rich?”", "\"없어. 그렇게 털어놓으니 내 보기에 자네는 명예로운 사람이야. 그래, 그 영주 아버지께서는 부자신가? 아주 큰 부자야?\""]], [["“Yes, he is that; he does not himself know the amount of his fortune.”", "\"그럼, 그렇고말고. 자기 재산이 얼마나 되는지 본인도 몰라.\""]], [["“Is it possible?”", "\"그게 가능한가?\""]], [["“It is evident enough to me, who am always at his house.", "\"늘 그분 댁에 드나드는 내게는 충분히 분명한 사실이야."], ["The other day a banker’s clerk brought him fifty thousand francs in a portfolio about the size of your plate; yesterday his banker brought him a hundred thousand francs in gold.”", "며칠 전에는 은행 사무원이 자네 접시만 한 서류첩에 오만 프랑을 담아 가져왔어. 어제는 그분 거래 은행가가 금화로 십만 프랑을 가져왔고.\""]], [["Caderousse was filled with wonder; the young man’s words sounded to him like metal, and he thought he could hear the rushing of cascades of louis.", "카드루스는 경탄에 사로잡혔다. 젊은이의 말이 그에게는 쇳소리처럼 들렸고, 루이 금화가 폭포처럼 쏟아져 내리는 소리가 들리는 듯했다."]], [["“And you go into that house?”", "\"그래서 자네가 그 집에 드나든단 말이지?\""], ["cried he briskly.", "그가 잽싸게 외쳤다."]], [["“When I like.”", "\"내키는 대로.\""]], [["Caderousse was thoughtful for a moment.", "카드루스는 잠시 생각에 잠겼다."], ["It was easy to perceive he was revolving some unfortunate idea in his mind.", "그가 머릿속으로 무언가 불길한 궁리를 굴리고 있다는 것은 쉽게 알아챌 수 있었다."], ["Then suddenly,—", "그러더니 불쑥 말했다."]], [["“How I should like to see all that,”", "\"나도 그걸 다 좀 봤으면 좋겠군!\""], ["cried he; “how beautiful it must be!”", "그가 외쳤다. \"얼마나 근사할까!\""]], [["“It is, in fact, magnificent,” said Andrea.", "\"사실 정말 으리으리하지.\" 안드레아가 말했다."]], [["“And does he not live in the Champs-Élysées?”", "\"샹젤리제에 살지 않나?\" \"그래, 30번지야.\""]], [["“Yes, No. 30.” “Ah,”", "\"아.\" 카드루스가 말했다."], ["said Caderousse, “No. 30.”", "\"30번지라.\""]], [["“Yes, a fine house standing alone, between a courtyard and a garden,—you must know it.”", "\"그래, 안뜰과 정원 사이에 외따로 서 있는 멋진 집이야. 자네도 알 만한 곳이지.\""]], [["“Possibly; but it is not the exterior I care for, it is the interior.", "\"그럴지도. 하지만 내가 마음 쓰는 건 겉모습이 아니야, 안이지."], ["What beautiful furniture there must be in it!”", "그 안에 얼마나 멋진 가구들이 있을까!\""]], [["“Have you ever seen the Tuileries?”", "\"자네 튀일리궁을 본 적 있나?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"아니.\""]], [["“Well, it surpasses that.”", "\"음, 그보다 더하다네.\""]], [["“It must be worth one’s while to stoop, Andrea, when that good M. Monte Cristo lets fall his purse.”", "\"그 인자하신 몬테크리스토 씨가 지갑을 떨어뜨리거든, 안드레아, 그걸 주우려고 허리 굽힐 만하겠는걸.\""]], [["“It is not worthwhile to wait for that,”", "\"그걸 기다릴 것까지도 없어.\""], ["said Andrea; “money is as plentiful in that house as fruit in an orchard.”", "안드레아가 말했다. \"그 집에는 과수원의 과일만큼이나 돈이 흔하니까.\""]], [["“But you should take me there one day with you.”", "\"한데 자네 언제 한번 날 거기 데려가 주게.\""]], [["“How can I?", "\"내가 어떻게?"], ["On what plea?”", "무슨 핑계로?\""]], [["“You are right; but you have made my mouth water.", "\"하긴 그렇군. 하지만 자네가 내 군침을 돌게 했어."], ["I must absolutely see it; I shall find a way.”", "무슨 일이 있어도 꼭 봐야겠어. 방법을 찾아내고 말 거야.\""]], [["“No nonsense, Caderousse!”", "\"허튼소리 말게, 카드루스!\""]], [["“I will offer myself as floor-polisher.”", "\"마룻바닥 닦는 일꾼으로 나서지 뭐.\""]], [["“The rooms are all carpeted.”", "\"방마다 죄다 양탄자가 깔려 있어.\""]], [["“Well, then, I must be contented to imagine it.”", "\"그럼 할 수 없지, 상상하는 걸로 만족해야겠군.\""]], [["“That is the best plan, believe me.”", "\"그게 상책이야, 내 말 믿게.\""]], [["“Try, at least, to give me an idea of what it is.”", "\"그럼 적어도 어떤 곳인지 짐작이라도 가게 해 주게.\""]], [["“How can I?”", "\"내가 어떻게?\""]], [["“Nothing is easier. Is it large?”", "\"그보다 쉬운 게 어딨나. 큰가?\""]], [["“Middling.”", "\"그저 그래.\""]], [["“How is it arranged?”", "\"어떻게 배치돼 있지?\""]], [["“Faith, I should require pen, ink, and paper to make a plan.”", "\"이런, 그걸 그리려면 펜이랑 잉크랑 종이가 있어야겠는데.\""]], [["“They are all here,”", "\"여기 다 있어.\""], ["said Caderousse, briskly.", "카드루스가 잽싸게 말했다."], ["He fetched from an old secretaire a sheet of white paper and pen and ink.", "그는 낡은 책상에서 흰 종이 한 장과 펜과 잉크를 꺼내 왔다. \"자.\""], ["“Here,” said Caderousse, “draw me all that on the paper, my boy.”", "카드루스가 말했다. \"이 종이에 그걸 다 그려 보게, 친구.\""]], [["Andrea took the pen with an imperceptible smile and began.", "안드레아는 알아챌 수 없는 미소를 띠며 펜을 들고 그리기 시작했다."]], [["“The house, as I said, is between the court and the garden; in this way, do you see?”", "\"집은 말했다시피 안뜰과 정원 사이에 있어. 이런 식으로, 알겠나?\""], ["Andrea drew the garden, the court and the house. 40142m", "안드레아는 정원과 안뜰, 그리고 집을 그렸다."]], [["“High walls?”", "\"담은 높은가?\""]], [["“Not more than eight or ten feet.”", "\"기껏해야 팔구 피트 정도야.\""]], [["“That is not prudent,”", "\"그건 신중하지 못한걸.\""], ["said Caderousse.", "카드루스가 말했다."]], [["“In the court are orange-trees in pots, turf, and clumps of flowers.”", "\"안뜰에는 화분에 심은 오렌지나무들이며 잔디, 꽃밭이 있어.\""]], [["“And no steel-traps?”", "\"덫 같은 건 없고?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"없어.\""]], [["“The stables?”", "\"마구간은?\""]], [["“Are on either side of the gate, which you see there.” And Andrea continued his plan.", "\"저기 보이는 대문 양옆에 있지.\" 그러면서 안드레아는 도면을 계속 그렸다."]], [["“Let us see the ground floor,” said Caderousse.", "\"일 층을 좀 보세.\" 카드루스가 말했다."]], [["“On the ground floor, dining-room, two drawing-rooms, billiard-room, staircase in the hall, and a little back staircase.”", "\"일 층에는 식당, 응접실 두 개, 당구실, 현관의 계단, 그리고 작은 뒤쪽 계단이 있어.\""]], [["“Windows?”", "\"창문은?\""]], [["“Magnificent windows, so beautiful, so large, that I believe a man of your size should pass through each frame.”", "\"으리으리한 창문들이지. 어찌나 아름답고 큰지, 자네만 한 덩치도 창틀마다 빠져나갈 수 있을 거야.\""]], [["“Why the devil have they any stairs with such windows?”", "\"빌어먹을, 그런 창문이 있는데 계단은 뭐 하러 둔담?\""]], [["“Luxury has everything.”", "\"호사스러운 데는 다 있는 법이지.\""]], [["“But shutters?”", "\"그럼 덧창은?\""]], [["“Yes, but they are never used.", "\"있긴 한데, 한 번도 쓰지 않아."], ["That Count of Monte Cristo is an original, who loves to look at the sky even at night.”", "그 몬테크리스토 백작은 별난 사람이라, 밤에도 하늘 바라보는 걸 좋아하거든.\""]], [["“And where do the servants sleep?”", "\"그럼 하인들은 어디서 자나?\""]], [["“Oh, they have a house to themselves.", "\"아, 그들은 따로 쓰는 건물이 있어."], ["Picture to yourself a pretty coach-house at the right-hand side where the ladders are kept.", "사다리들을 보관하는 오른편에 멋진 마차 차고가 있다고 상상해 보게."], ["Well, over that coach-house are the servants’ rooms, with bells corresponding with the different apartments.”", "그 차고 위가 하인들 방인데, 방마다 다른 방들과 연결된 초인종이 달려 있어.\""]], [["“Ah, _diable!_ bells did you say?”", "\"아, 제기랄! 초인종이라고 했나?\""]], [["“What do you mean?”", "\"무슨 소린가?\""]], [["“Oh, nothing! I only say they cost a load of money to hang, and what is the use of them, I should like to know?”", "\"아, 아무것도 아니야! 그저 그걸 다는 데 돈이 엄청 들 텐데, 대체 무슨 소용이 있나 싶어서 말이지.\""]], [["“There used to be a dog let loose in the yard at night, but it has been taken to the house at Auteuil, to that you went to, you know.”", "\"밤이면 안뜰에 개를 풀어 놓곤 했는데, 그건 오퇴유 집으로 데려갔어. 자네도 가 봤다는 그 집 말이야, 알지.\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"알지.\""]], [["“I was saying to him only yesterday, ‘You are imprudent, Monsieur Count; for when you go to Auteuil and take your servants the house is left unprotected.’ ‘Well,’ said he, ‘what next?’ ‘Well, next, some day you will be robbed.’”", "\"바로 어제 내가 그분께 이렇게 말씀드렸어. '백작님, 경솔하십니다. 오퇴유에 가실 때 하인들까지 데려가시면 이 집이 무방비로 남으니까요.' 그러자 '그래서?' 하시더군. '그러니까 언젠가는 도둑맞으실 겁니다.' 했지.\""]], [["“What did he answer?”", "\"그분이 뭐라 답하던가?\""]], [["“He quietly said, ‘What do I care if I am?’”", "\"태연히 말씀하시더군. '도둑맞은들 내가 무슨 상관인가?'\""]], [["“Andrea, he has some secretaire with a spring.”", "\"안드레아, 그 사람한테 용수철이 달린 책상이 있군그래.\""]], [["“How do you know?”", "\"그걸 자네가 어떻게 알아?\""]], [["“Yes, which catches the thief in a trap and plays a tune.", "\"그래, 도둑을 덫에 가두고 곡조를 울리는 그런 거 말이야."], ["I was told there were such at the last exhibition.”", "지난번 박람회에 그런 게 있었다더군.\""]], [["“He has simply a mahogany secretaire, in which the key is always kept.”", "\"그분께는 그냥 마호가니 책상이 하나 있을 뿐이야. 거기엔 늘 열쇠가 꽂혀 있고.\""]], [["“And he is not robbed?”", "\"그런데도 도둑을 안 맞는다고?\""]], [["“No; his servants are all devoted to him.”", "\"안 맞아. 하인들이 죄다 그분께 충성스러우니까.\""]], [["“There ought to be some money in that secretaire?”", "\"그 책상 안에 돈이 좀 있겠군?\""]], [["“There may be. No one knows what there is.”", "\"있을지도. 안에 뭐가 있는지는 아무도 몰라.\""]], [["“And where is it?”", "\"그게 어디 있는데?\""]], [["“On the first floor.”", "\"이 층에.\""]], [["“Sketch me the plan of that floor, as you have done of the ground floor, my boy.”", "\"일 층을 그렸듯이 그 층 도면도 그려 보게, 친구.\""]], [["“That is very simple.” Andrea took the pen.", "\"그거야 아주 간단하지.\""], ["“On the first story, do you see, there is the anteroom and the drawing-room; to the right of the drawing-room, a library and a study; to the left, a bedroom and a dressing-room.", "안드레아가 펜을 들었다. \"이 층에는, 보다시피, 대기실과 응접실이 있어. 응접실 오른쪽으로는 서재와 서실, 왼쪽으로는 침실과 화장실이 있지."], ["The famous secretaire is in the dressing-room.”", "그 유명한 책상은 화장실에 있어.\""]], [["“Is there a window in the dressing-room?”", "\"화장실에 창문이 있나?\""]], [["“Two,—one here and one there.”", "\"두 개야. 하나는 여기, 하나는 저기.\""], ["Andrea sketched two windows in the room, which formed an angle on the plan, and appeared as a small square added to the rectangle of the bedroom.", "안드레아는 그 방에 창문 두 개를 그려 넣었는데, 도면에서 그것은 각을 이루며 침실 직사각형에 작은 정사각형이 덧붙은 모양으로 나타났다."], ["Caderousse became thoughtful.", "카드루스가 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["“Does he often go to Auteuil?” added he.", "\"그분이 오퇴유엔 자주 가나?\" 그가 덧붙였다."]], [["“Two or three times a week.", "\"일주일에 두세 번."], ["Tomorrow, for instance, he is going to spend the day and night there.”", "가령 내일도 거기서 낮과 밤을 보내실 거야.\""]], [["“Are you sure of it?”", "\"확실해?\""]], [["“He has invited me to dine there.”", "\"나더러 거기 와서 저녁을 들라고 초대하셨으니까.\""]], [["“There’s a life for you,”", "\"이게 바로 사는 거지.\""], ["said Caderousse; “a town house and a country house.”", "카드루스가 말했다. \"시내 저택에 시골 별장이라니.\""]], [["“That is what it is to be rich.”", "\"부자란 그런 거야.\""]], [["“And shall you dine there?”", "\"그래서 자네 거기 가서 저녁을 들 건가?\""]], [["“Probably.”", "\"아마도.\""]], [["“When you dine there, do you sleep there?”", "\"거기서 저녁을 들면 거기서 자나?\""]], [["“If I like; I am at home there.”", "\"내키면. 그 집에선 내 집처럼 지내니까.\""]], [["Caderousse looked at the young man, as if to get at the truth from the bottom of his heart.", "카드루스는 그 마음속 밑바닥에서 진실을 캐내려는 듯 젊은이를 바라보았다."], ["But Andrea drew a cigar-case from his pocket, took a Havana, quietly lit it, and began smoking.", "그러나 안드레아는 주머니에서 담배 상자를 꺼내 하바나산 시가를 한 대 집고는 태연히 불을 붙여 피우기 시작했다."]], [["“When do you want your twelve hundred francs?”", "\"자네 천이백 프랑은 언제 필요한가?\""], ["said he to Caderousse.", "그가 카드루스에게 말했다."]], [["“Now, if you have them.”", "\"지금. 가지고 있으면 말이야.\""], ["Andrea took five-and-twenty louis from his pocket.", "안드레아가 주머니에서 스물다섯 루이를 꺼냈다."]], [["“Yellow boys?”", "\"노란 녀석들이로군?\""], ["said Caderousse; “no, I thank you.”", "카드루스가 말했다. \"아니, 됐네. 사양하지.\""]], [["“Oh, you despise them.”", "\"오, 자네 그걸 업신여기는군.\""]], [["“On the contrary, I esteem them, but will not have them.”", "\"천만에, 귀히 여기지. 그래도 받지는 않겠어.\""]], [["“You can change them, idiot; gold is worth five sous.”", "\"바꾸면 되잖나, 이 멍청아. 금화는 다섯 수* 더 쳐주는데.\""]], [["“Exactly; and he who changes them will follow friend Caderousse, lay hands on him, and demand what farmers pay him their rent in gold.", "\"바로 그거야. 그걸 바꾸는 자가 친구 카드루스를 미행해서 덜미를 잡고는, 어떤 소작인이 금화로 소작료를 내더냐고 캐물을 테니까."], ["No nonsense, my good fellow; silver simply, round coins with the head of some monarch or other on them.", "허튼짓 말게, 친구. 그냥 은화로 주게. 어느 군주의 머리든 새겨진 둥근 동전 말이야."], ["Anybody may possess a five-franc piece.”", "오 프랑짜리야 누구든 가질 수 있는 거니까.\""]], [["“But do you suppose I carry five hundred francs about with me?", "\"그럼 내가 오백 프랑을 몸에 지니고 다닌다고 생각하나?"], ["I should want a porter.”", "그러려면 짐꾼이 필요할걸.\""]], [["“Well, leave them with your porter; he is to be trusted.", "\"그럼 자네 수위한테 맡겨 두게. 믿을 만한 자니까."], ["I will call for them.”", "내가 가지러 갈 테니.\""]], [["“Today?”", "\"오늘?\""]], [["“No, tomorrow; I shall not have time today.”", "\"아니, 내일. 오늘은 시간이 없어.\""]], [["“Well, tomorrow I will leave them when I go to Auteuil.”", "\"그럼 내일 오퇴유로 갈 때 맡겨 두지.\""]], [["“May I depend on it?”", "\"믿어도 되겠지?\""]], [["“Certainly.”", "\"물론이지.\""]], [["“Because I shall secure my housekeeper on the strength of it.”", "\"그래야 그걸 믿고 가정부를 들일 테니까 말이야.\""]], [["“Now see here, will that be all? Eh?", "\"자, 이제 이게 다지? 응?"], ["And will you not torment me any more?”", "그리고 더는 날 괴롭히지 않겠지?\""]], [["“Never.”", "\"결코.\""]], [["Caderousse had become so gloomy that Andrea feared he should be obliged to notice the change.", "카드루스가 어찌나 침울해졌던지, 안드레아는 그 변화를 모른 척할 수 없게 될까 봐 두려웠다."], ["He redoubled his gayety and carelessness.", "그는 한층 더 명랑하고 태평한 척했다."]], [["“How sprightly you are,”", "\"자네 참 활기차군.\""], ["said Caderousse; “One would say you were already in possession of your property.”", "카드루스가 말했다. \"벌써 자네 재산을 손에 넣기라도 한 사람 같아.\""]], [["“No, unfortunately; but when I do obtain it——”", "\"아니, 안타깝게도 아직. 하지만 일단 손에 넣으면……\""]], [["“Well?”", "\"넣으면?\""]], [["“I shall remember old friends, I can tell you that.”", "\"옛 친구들을 잊지 않을 거야. 그것만은 장담하지.\""]], [["“Yes, since you have such a good memory.”", "\"그래, 자네 기억력이 그렇게 좋으니 말이야.\""]], [["“What do you want?", "\"무얼 바라는 거지?"], ["It looks as if you were trying to fleece me?”", "마치 날 등쳐 먹으려는 것 같군.\""]], [["“I? What an idea!", "\"내가? 무슨 그런 생각을!"], ["I, who am going to give you another piece of good advice.”", "자네한테 좋은 충고를 하나 더 해 주려는 이 몸이 말이야.\""]], [["“What is it?”", "\"그게 뭔데?\""]], [["“To leave behind you the diamond you have on your finger.", "\"자네 손가락에 낀 다이아몬드를 두고 가라는 거야."], ["We shall both get into trouble.", "우리 둘 다 곤경에 빠질 테니까."], ["You will ruin both yourself and me by your folly.”", "자네 어리석음 때문에 자네 신세도 내 신세도 망칠 거라고.\""]], [["“How so?”", "\"어째서?\""], ["said Andrea.", "안드레아가 말했다."]], [["“How? You put on a livery, you disguise yourself as a servant, and yet keep a diamond on your finger worth four or five thousand francs.”", "\"어째서라니? 자네는 하인 제복을 걸치고 하인으로 변장한 주제에, 손가락엔 사오천 프랑짜리 다이아몬드를 끼고 있잖나.\""]], [["“You guess well.”", "\"제법 잘 맞히는군.\""]], [["“I know something of diamonds; I have had some.”", "\"내가 다이아몬드는 좀 알지. 가져 본 적도 있고.\""]], [["“You do well to boast of it,”", "\"그걸 자랑이라고 하는군.\""], ["said Andrea, who, without becoming angry, as Caderousse feared, at this new extortion, quietly resigned the ring.", "안드레아가 말했다. 그는 카드루스가 걱정한 것과 달리 이 새로운 갈취에 화내지 않고, 태연히 반지를 내주었다."], ["Caderousse looked so closely at it that Andrea well knew that he was examining to see if all the edges were perfect.", "카드루스가 어찌나 가까이 들여다보던지, 안드레아는 그가 모든 모서리가 흠 없는지 살피고 있다는 것을 똑똑히 알아챘다."]], [["“It is a false diamond,”", "\"이건 가짜 다이아몬드야.\""], ["said Caderousse.", "카드루스가 말했다."]], [["“You are joking now,”", "\"이제 농담까지 하는군.\""], ["replied Andrea.", "안드레아가 대답했다."]], [["“Do not be angry, we can try it.”", "\"화내지 말게, 시험해 보면 돼.\""], ["Caderousse went to the window, touched the glass with it, and found it would cut.", "카드루스는 창가로 가서 그것으로 유리를 그어 보았고, 유리가 베이는 것을 확인했다."]], [["“_Confiteor!_”", "\"콘피테오르*!\" 카드루스가 다이아몬드를 새끼손가락에 끼며 말했다."], ["said Caderousse, putting the diamond on his little finger; “I was mistaken; but those thieves of jewellers imitate so well that it is no longer worthwhile to rob a jeweller’s shop—it is another branch of industry paralyzed.”", "\"내가 잘못 봤군. 한데 그 도둑놈 같은 보석상들이 어찌나 똑같이 흉내를 내는지, 이젠 보석상을 털 가치도 없어졌어. 한 가지 업종이 또 마비된 셈이지.\""]], [["“Have you finished?”", "\"다 됐나?\""], ["said Andrea,—“do you want anything more?—will you have my waistcoat or my hat?", "안드레아가 말했다. \"더 필요한 건 없고? 내 조끼나 모자라도 줄까?"], ["Make free, now you have begun.”", "기왕 시작했으니 마음껏 가져가게나.\""]], [["“No; you are, after all, a good companion; I will not detain you, and will try to cure myself of my ambition.”", "\"아니야. 자네는 어쨌든 좋은 친구야. 더 붙잡진 않겠어. 내 욕심도 좀 다스려 보도록 하지.\""]], [["“But take care the same thing does not happen to you in selling the diamond you feared with the gold.”", "\"하지만 조심하게, 자네가 금화에 대해 걱정한 그 일이 그 다이아몬드를 팔 때 똑같이 일어나지 않도록 말이야.\""]], [["“I shall not sell it—do not fear.”", "\"그건 안 팔 거야. 걱정 말게.\""]], [["“Not at least till the day after tomorrow,” thought the young man.", "'적어도 모레까지는 안 팔겠지.' 젊은이는 속으로 생각했다."]], [["“Happy rogue,”", "\"운 좋은 악당 같으니.\""], ["said Caderousse; “you are going to find your servants, your horses, your carriage, and your betrothed!”", "카드루스가 말했다. \"이제 자네 하인들이며 말들이며 마차며 약혼녀를 만나러 가는군!\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["said Andrea.", "안드레아가 말했다."]], [["“Well, I hope you will make a handsome wedding-present the day you marry Mademoiselle Danglars.”", "\"그럼, 당글라르 양과 결혼하는 날엔 멋진 결혼 선물을 하나 해 주길 바라네.\""]], [["“I have already told you it is a fancy you have taken in your head.”", "\"그건 자네 머릿속에서 멋대로 지어낸 망상이라고 이미 말했잖나.\""]], [["“What fortune has she?”", "\"그 아가씨 재산이 얼만데?\""]], [["“But I tell you——”", "\"하지만 내 말은……\""]], [["“A million?”", "\"백만이야?\""]], [["Andrea shrugged his shoulders.", "안드레아가 어깨를 으쓱했다."]], [["“Let it be a million,”", "\"백만이라 치세.\""], ["said Caderousse; “you can never have so much as I wish you.”", "카드루스가 말했다. \"내가 자네한테 바라는 만큼은 결코 못 가질 테니까.\""]], [["“Thank you,”", "\"고맙군.\""], ["said the young man.", "젊은이가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, I wish it you with all my heart!”", "\"오, 진심으로 바라는 거라네!\""], ["added Caderousse with his hoarse laugh.", "카드루스가 쉰 웃음을 흘리며 덧붙였다."], ["“Stop, let me show you the way.”", "\"잠깐, 내가 길을 안내하지.\""]], [["“It is not worthwhile.”", "\"그럴 것 없어.\""]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜?\""]], [["“Because there is a little secret, a precaution I thought it desirable to take, one of Huret & Fichet’s locks, revised and improved by Gaspard Caderousse; I will manufacture you a similar one when you are a capitalist.”", "\"여기엔 자그마한 비밀이, 내가 해 둘 만하다고 여긴 조심책이 하나 있거든. 위레 앤드 피셰 회사의 자물쇠를 가스파르 카드루스가 손봐서 개량한 거지. 자네가 자본가가 되면 똑같은 걸 하나 만들어 주겠네.\""]], [["“Thank you,”", "\"고맙군.\""], ["said Andrea; “I will let you know a week beforehand.”", "안드레아가 말했다. \"일주일 전에 미리 알려 주지.\""]], [["They parted. Caderousse remained on the landing until he had not only seen Andrea go down the three stories, but also cross the court.", "두 사람은 헤어졌다. 카드루스는 안드레아가 세 층을 다 내려가는 것은 물론, 안뜰을 가로지르는 것까지 지켜볼 때까지 층계참에 머물러 있었다."], ["Then he returned hastily, shut his door carefully, and began to study, like a clever architect, the plan Andrea had left him.", "그러고는 황급히 돌아와 문을 조심스레 닫고는, 영리한 건축가처럼 안드레아가 남기고 간 도면을 연구하기 시작했다."]], [["“Dear Benedetto,”", "\"베네데토, 친구.\""], ["said he, “I think he will not be sorry to inherit his fortune, and he who hastens the day when he can touch his five hundred thousand will not be his worst friend.”", "그가 말했다. \"내 생각엔 저 친구가 제 재산을 물려받는 걸 마다하지 않을 거야. 그러니 저 친구가 오십만 프랑을 손에 쥘 그날을 앞당겨 주는 자라면, 결코 저 친구의 가장 나쁜 친구는 아니겠지.\""]], [["# Chapter 82. The Burglary", "# Chapter 82. The Burglary"]], [["The day following that on which the conversation we have related took place, the Count of Monte Cristo set out for Auteuil, accompanied by Ali and several attendants, and also taking with him some horses whose qualities he was desirous of ascertaining.", "* 수: 옛 프랑스 화폐 단위로 20분의 1프랑.—옮긴이 * 콘피테오르: 라틴어로 '내 잘못을 고백하나이다'라는 뜻.—옮긴이\n우리가 앞서 전한 대화가 오간 그 이튿날, 몬테크리스토 백작은 알리와 하인 몇을 거느리고 오퇴유로 떠났다."], ["He was induced to undertake this journey, of which the day before he had not even thought and which had not occurred to Andrea either, by the arrival of Bertuccio from Normandy with intelligence respecting the house and sloop.", "그러면서 성능을 시험해 보고 싶던 말 몇 마리도 데려갔다. 전날까지만 해도 그는 이 여행을 생각조차 하지 않았고 안드레아 역시 떠올리지 못했지만, 베르투초가 노르망디에서 집과 범선에 관한 소식을 가지고 돌아온 까닭에 그는 길을 나서게 되었다."], ["The house was ready, and the sloop which had arrived a week before lay at anchor in a small creek with her crew of six men, who had observed all the requisite formalities and were ready again to put to sea.", "집은 준비가 끝났고, 일주일 전에 도착한 범선은 작은 만에 닻을 내린 채 정박해 있었다. 여섯 명의 선원은 필요한 모든 절차를 마치고 다시 바다로 나갈 채비가 되어 있었다."]], [["The count praised Bertuccio’s zeal, and ordered him to prepare for a speedy departure, as his stay in France would not be prolonged more than a month.", "백작은 베르투초의 열성을 칭찬하며, 프랑스에 머무는 기간이 한 달을 넘기지 않을 테니 신속히 떠날 준비를 해 두라고 일렀다."]], [["“Now,”", "\"이제,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “I may require to go in one night from Paris to Tréport; let eight fresh horses be in readiness on the road, which will enable me to go fifty leagues in ten hours.”", "\"하룻밤 사이에 파리에서 트레포르까지 가야 할 일이 생길지도 모르네. 길목마다 싱싱한 말 여덟 마리를 대기시켜 두게. 그러면 열 시간에 오십 리외를 갈 수 있을 테니까.\""]], [["“Your highness had already expressed that wish,”", "\"각하께서 이미 그 분부를 내리셨기에,\""], ["said Bertuccio, “and the horses are ready.", "베르투초가 대답했다. \"말은 준비되어 있습니다."], ["I have bought them, and stationed them myself at the most desirable posts, that is, in villages, where no one generally stops.”", "제가 직접 사들여 가장 적합한 곳에, 그러니까 평소 아무도 머물지 않는 마을들에 배치해 두었습니다.\""]], [["“That’s well,”", "\"잘했네.\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “I remain here a day or two—arrange accordingly.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"나는 여기서 하루이틀 머물 테니, 그에 맞춰 준비해 두게.\""]], [["As Bertuccio was leaving the room to give the requisite orders, Baptistin opened the door: he held a letter on a silver waiter.", "베르투초가 필요한 지시를 내리려고 방을 나서려는데, 바티스탱이 문을 열었다. 그는 은쟁반에 편지 한 통을 받쳐 들고 있었다."]], [["“What are you doing here?”", "\"여기서 뭘 하는 건가?\""], ["asked the count, seeing him covered with dust; “I did not send for you, I think?”", "먼지를 뒤집어쓴 그를 보고 백작이 물었다. \"내가 자네를 부른 적은 없는 듯한데?\""]], [["Baptistin, without answering, approached the count, and presented the letter.", "바티스탱은 대답 없이 백작에게 다가가 편지를 내밀었다."], ["“Important and urgent,” said he.", "\"중요하고 급한 일입니다.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["The count opened the letter, and read:", "백작은 편지를 펼쳐 읽었다."]], [["“‘M. de Monte Cristo is apprised that this night a man will enter his house in the Champs-Élysées with the intention of carrying off some papers supposed to be in the secretaire in the dressing-room.", "\"몬테크리스토 백작께 알립니다. 오늘 밤 한 사내가 백작의 집에 들 것이며, 화장실의 비밀 서랍장에 들어 있으리라 짐작되는 서류 몇 점을 훔쳐 갈 작정입니다."], ["The count’s well-known courage will render unnecessary the aid of the police, whose interference might seriously affect him who sends this advice.", "백작의 잘 알려진 용맹함이라면 경찰의 도움은 필요치 않을 것이며, 경찰이 끼어들면 이 충고를 보내는 자에게 심각한 해가 미칠 수도 있습니다."], ["The count, by any opening from the bedroom, or by concealing himself in the dressing-room, would be able to defend his property himself.", "백작은 침실의 어느 틈으로든, 혹은 화장실에 몸을 숨겨 직접 재산을 지킬 수 있을 것입니다."], ["Many attendants or apparent precautions would prevent the villain from the attempt, and M. de Monte Cristo would lose the opportunity of discovering an enemy whom chance has revealed to him who now sends this warning to the count,—a warning he might not be able to send another time, if this first attempt should fail and another be made.’”", "하인을 여럿 두거나 눈에 띄는 경계를 하면 악당이 시도를 단념할 것이고, 그러면 백작은 우연이 드러내 준 적을 알아낼 기회를 잃게 됩니다. 지금 이 경고를 보내는 자는, 만약 이 첫 시도가 실패하고 또 다른 시도가 이어진다면 다음번에는 이 같은 경고를 보내지 못할 수도 있습니다.\""]], [["The count’s first idea was that this was an artifice—a gross deception, to draw his attention from a minor danger in order to expose him to a greater.", "백작이 처음 떠올린 생각은 이것이 술책이라는 것이었다. 사소한 위험으로 주의를 돌려 더 큰 위험에 빠뜨리려는 조잡한 속임수 말이다."], ["He was on the point of sending the letter to the commissary of police, notwithstanding the advice of his anonymous friend, or perhaps because of that advice, when suddenly the idea occurred to him that it might be some personal enemy, whom he alone should recognize and over whom, if such were the case, he alone would gain any advantage, as Fiesco17 had done over the Moor who would have killed him.", "그는 익명의 친구가 보낸 충고에도 불구하고, 어쩌면 바로 그 충고 때문에 이 편지를 경찰서장에게 보낼 참이었다. 그러다 문득, 어쩌면 자기 혼자만이 알아볼 수 있는 사사로운 적일지도 모른다는 생각이 떠올랐다. 만약 그렇다면, 피에스코가 자신을 죽이려던 무어인을 제압했듯, 오직 자기 혼자만이 이득을 얻을 수 있는 일이었다."], ["We know the count’s vigorous and daring mind, denying anything to be impossible, with that energy which marks the great man.", "우리는 위인을 특징짓는 그 기백으로 어떤 일도 불가능하다고 인정하지 않는, 백작의 강인하고 대담한 정신을 알고 있다."]], [["From his past life, from his resolution to shrink from nothing, the count had acquired an inconceivable relish for the contests in which he had engaged, sometimes against nature, that is to say, against God, and sometimes against the world, that is, against the devil.", "지난 삶에서, 어떤 것 앞에서도 물러서지 않겠다는 결심에서, 백작은 자신이 뛰어들었던 싸움에 대해 형언할 수 없는 흥취를 느끼게 되었다. 때로는 자연을, 다시 말해 신을 상대로, 때로는 세상을, 다시 말해 악마를 상대로 한 싸움이었다."]], [["“They do not want my papers,”", "\"저들은 내 서류를 노리는 게 아니야.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “they want to kill me; they are no robbers, but assassins.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"나를 죽이려는 거지. 저들은 도둑이 아니라 살인자야."], ["I will not allow the prefect of police to interfere with my private affairs.", "경찰청장이 내 사사로운 일에 끼어들도록 두지 않겠어."], ["I am rich enough, forsooth, to distribute his authority on this occasion.”", "이번 일에는 그자의 권한쯤 분배할 만큼 나는 부유하니까.\""]], [["The count recalled Baptistin, who had left the room after delivering the letter.", "백작은 편지를 전하고 방을 나갔던 바티스탱을 다시 불렀다."]], [["“Return to Paris,”", "\"파리로 돌아가게.\""], ["said he; “assemble the servants who remain there.", "그가 말했다. \"거기 남아 있는 하인들을 모두 모으게."], ["I want all my household at Auteuil.”", "우리 집 식솔을 전부 오퇴유로 데려오게.\""]], [["“But will no one remain in the house, my lord?”", "\"하지만 집에는 아무도 남지 않습니까, 나리?\""], ["asked Baptistin.", "바티스탱이 물었다."]], [["“Yes, the porter.”", "\"있지, 문지기.\""]], [["“My lord will remember that the lodge is at a distance from the house.”", "\"나리께서도 아시다시피 문지기 방은 본채에서 멀리 떨어져 있습니다.\""]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서?\""]], [["“The house might be stripped without his hearing the least noise.”", "\"문지기가 아무 소리도 듣지 못하는 사이에 집이 통째로 털릴 수도 있습니다.\""]], [["“By whom?” “By thieves.”", "\"누가 턴다는 건가?\""]], [["“You are a fool, M.", "\"도둑들이지요.\""], ["Baptistin. Thieves might strip the house—it would annoy me less than to be disobeyed.”", "\"자네는 어리석군, 바티스탱. 도둑들이 집을 턴다 한들, 내 명을 거역당하는 것보다는 덜 짜증스러울 거야.\""], ["Baptistin bowed.", "바티스탱이 고개를 숙였다."]], [["40150m\n“You understand me?”", "\"내 말 알아듣겠나?\""], ["said the count.", "백작이 말했다."], ["“Bring your comrades here, one and all; but let everything remain as usual, only close the shutters of the ground floor.”", "\"동료들을 여기로 데려오게, 한 사람도 빠짐없이. 다만 모든 것은 평소대로 두고, 1층 덧창만 닫아 두게.\""]], [["“And those of the first floor?”", "\"2층 덧창은요?\""]], [["“You know they are never closed. Go!”", "\"그건 한 번도 닫은 적이 없다는 걸 알지 않나. 가게!\""]], [["The count signified his intention of dining alone, and that no one but Ali should attend him.", "백작은 혼자 저녁을 들 것이며, 알리 말고는 아무도 시중들지 못하게 하라는 뜻을 밝혔다."], ["Having dined with his usual tranquillity and moderation, the count, making a signal to Ali to follow him, went out by the side-gate and on reaching the Bois de Boulogne turned, apparently without design towards Paris and at twilight; found himself opposite his house in the Champs-Élysées.", "평소처럼 차분하고 절제 있게 저녁을 마친 백작은 알리에게 따라오라는 신호를 보내고 옆문으로 나섰다. 불로뉴 숲에 이르자 그는 별다른 의도 없는 듯 파리 쪽으로 방향을 틀었고, 땅거미가 질 무렵 샹젤리제에 있는 자기 집 맞은편에 다다랐다."], ["All was dark; one solitary, feeble light was burning in the porter’s lodge, about forty paces distant from the house, as Baptistin had said.", "사방이 어두웠다. 바티스탱이 말한 대로 본채에서 마흔 걸음쯤 떨어진 문지기 방에 희미한 불빛 하나만 외로이 타고 있었다."]], [["Monte Cristo leaned against a tree, and with that scrutinizing glance which was so rarely deceived, looked up and down the avenue, examined the passers-by, and carefully looked down the neighboring streets, to see that no one was concealed.", "몬테크리스토는 나무에 몸을 기댄 채, 좀처럼 속는 일이 없는 그 날카로운 눈길로 거리를 위아래로 훑었다. 지나는 사람들을 살피고, 누가 숨어 있지는 않은지 이웃한 거리들까지 꼼꼼히 들여다보았다."], ["Ten minutes passed thus, and he was convinced that no one was watching him.", "그렇게 십 분이 지나자 그는 아무도 자신을 감시하지 않는다고 확신했다."], ["He hastened to the side-door with Ali, entered hurriedly, and by the servants’ staircase, of which he had the key, gained his bedroom without opening or disarranging a single curtain, without even the porter having the slightest suspicion that the house, which he supposed empty, contained its chief occupant.", "그는 알리와 함께 옆문으로 서둘러 들어갔다. 자기가 열쇠를 가진 하인용 계단을 통해, 커튼 하나 젖히거나 흐트러뜨리지 않고 침실에 이르렀다. 집이 비어 있다고 여기던 문지기는 그 집에 정작 주인이 들어와 있다는 것을 조금도 의심하지 못했다."]], [["Arrived in his bedroom, the count motioned to Ali to stop; then he passed into the dressing-room, which he examined.", "침실에 이르자 백작은 알리에게 멈추라는 신호를 했다. 그러고는 화장실로 들어가 살펴보았다."], ["Everything appeared as usual—the precious secretaire in its place, and the key in the secretaire.", "모든 것이 평소와 같아 보였다. 값진 비밀 서랍장은 제자리에 있었고, 열쇠도 서랍장에 꽂혀 있었다."], ["He double locked it, took the key, returned to the bedroom door, removed the double staple of the bolt, and went in.", "그는 서랍장을 이중으로 잠그고 열쇠를 뽑은 뒤, 침실 문으로 돌아가 빗장의 이중 고리를 떼어 내고 안으로 들어갔다."], ["Meanwhile Ali had procured the arms the count required—namely, a short carbine and a pair of double-barrelled pistols, with which as sure an aim might be taken as with a single-barrelled one.", "그사이 알리는 백작이 요구한 무기를 가져다 두었다. 짧은 기병총 한 자루와 쌍열 권총 한 쌍이었다. 쌍열 권총은 단열 권총만큼이나 정확히 겨눌 수 있었다."], ["Thus armed, the count held the lives of five men in his hands.", "이렇게 무장한 백작은 다섯 사람의 목숨을 손안에 쥔 셈이었다."], ["It was about half-past nine.", "시각은 아홉 시 반쯤이었다."]], [["The count and Ali ate in haste a crust of bread and drank a glass of Spanish wine; then Monte Cristo slipped aside one of the movable panels, which enabled him to see into the adjoining room.", "백작과 알리는 빵 한 조각을 서둘러 먹고 스페인산 포도주 한 잔을 마셨다. 그러고 나서 몬테크리스토는 옆방을 들여다볼 수 있는 미닫이 칸막이 하나를 살짝 밀어 두었다."], ["He had within his reach his pistols and carbine, and Ali, standing near him, held one of the small Arabian hatchets, whose form has not varied since the Crusades.", "그의 손이 닿는 곳에 권총과 기병총이 있었고, 곁에 선 알리는 작은 아랍식 손도끼 하나를 쥐고 있었다. 그 도끼의 모양은 십자군 시절 이래로 변하지 않은 것이었다."], ["Through one of the windows of the bedroom, on a line with that in the dressing-room, the count could see into the street.", "화장실 창과 일직선에 놓인 침실 창 하나를 통해 백작은 거리를 내다볼 수 있었다."]], [["Two hours passed thus.", "그렇게 두 시간이 지났다."], ["It was intensely dark; still Ali, thanks to his wild nature, and the count, thanks doubtless to his long confinement, could distinguish in the darkness the slightest movement of the trees.", "칠흑같이 어두웠다. 그래도 알리는 그 야성적인 천성 덕분에, 백작은 아마도 오랜 감옥살이 덕분에, 어둠 속에서도 나무들의 미세한 움직임까지 분간할 수 있었다."], ["The little light in the lodge had long been extinct.", "문지기 방의 작은 불빛은 이미 오래전에 꺼져 있었다."], ["It might be expected that the attack, if indeed an attack was projected, would be made from the staircase of the ground floor, and not from a window; in Monte Cristo’s opinion, the villains sought his life, not his money.", "만약 정말로 침입이 계획된 것이라면, 그것은 창문이 아니라 1층 계단 쪽에서 시작되리라 예상할 수 있었다. 몬테크리스토가 보기에 그 악당들이 노리는 것은 그의 돈이 아니라 목숨이었다."], ["It would be his bedroom they would attack, and they must reach it by the back staircase, or by the window in the dressing-room.", "그들이 들이닥칠 곳은 그의 침실일 테고, 거기에 이르려면 뒤쪽 계단이나 화장실 창을 거쳐야 했다."]], [["The clock of the Invalides struck a quarter to twelve; the west wind bore on its moistened gusts the doleful vibration of the three strokes.", "앵발리드의 시계가 열두 시 십오 분 전을 알렸다. 서풍이 축축한 돌풍에 그 세 번의 울림이 빚어내는 음울한 떨림을 실어 날랐다."]], [["40152m\nAs the last stroke died away, the count thought he heard a slight noise in the dressing-room; this first sound, or rather this first grinding, was followed by a second, then a third; at the fourth, the count knew what to expect.", "마지막 울림이 잦아들 무렵, 백작은 화장실에서 희미한 소리를 들은 듯했다. 첫 소리, 아니 첫 긁히는 소리에 이어 두 번째, 그리고 세 번째 소리가 들렸다. 네 번째 소리에 이르자 백작은 무슨 일인지 알아챘다."], ["A firm and well-practised hand was engaged in cutting the four sides of a pane of glass with a diamond.", "단단하고 노련한 손이 다이아몬드로 유리창 한 장의 네 변을 잘라 내고 있었다."], ["The count felt his heart beat more rapidly.", "백작은 심장이 한층 빠르게 뛰는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["Inured as men may be to danger, forewarned as they may be of peril, they understand, by the fluttering of the heart and the shuddering of the frame, the enormous difference between a dream and a reality, between the project and the execution.", "사람이 아무리 위험에 단련되어 있고 아무리 위태로움을 미리 알고 있다 해도, 심장의 떨림과 몸의 전율을 통해 그들은 꿈과 현실의, 계획과 실행의 어마어마한 차이를 깨닫는 법이다."], ["However, Monte Cristo only made a sign to apprise Ali, who, understanding that danger was approaching from the other side, drew nearer to his master.", "그러나 몬테크리스토는 알리에게 신호 하나만 보냈고, 알리는 위험이 반대편에서 다가오고 있음을 알아채고 주인 곁으로 바싹 다가섰다."], ["Monte Cristo was eager to ascertain the strength and number of his enemies.", "몬테크리스토는 적의 힘과 수를 확인하고 싶어 안달이 났다."]], [["The window whence the noise proceeded was opposite the opening by which the count could see into the dressing-room.", "소리가 나는 그 창은 백작이 화장실을 들여다볼 수 있는 틈 맞은편에 있었다. 그는 그 창에 눈을 박았다."], ["He fixed his eyes on that window—he distinguished a shadow in the darkness; then one of the panes became quite opaque, as if a sheet of paper were stuck on the outside, then the square cracked without falling.", "어둠 속에서 그림자 하나가 어른거렸다. 이윽고 유리창 한 장이 바깥에서 종이를 한 장 붙인 것처럼 완전히 불투명해지더니, 그 네모난 유리가 떨어지지는 않은 채 금이 갔다."], ["Through the opening an arm was passed to find the fastening, then a second; the window turned on its hinges, and a man entered. He was alone.", "그 틈으로 팔 하나가 들어와 걸쇠를 더듬었고, 이어 두 번째 팔이 들어왔다. 창이 돌쩌귀를 축 삼아 돌아갔고, 한 사내가 들어왔다. 혼자였다."]], [["“That’s a daring rascal,”", "\"대담한 놈이군.\""], ["whispered the count.", "백작이 속삭였다."]], [["At that moment Ali touched him slightly on the shoulder.", "그 순간 알리가 그의 어깨를 살짝 건드렸다."], ["He turned; Ali pointed to the window of the room in which they were, facing the street.", "백작이 돌아보자, 알리가 거리 쪽으로 난, 그들이 있는 방의 창을 가리켰다."]], [["“I see!”", "\"보인다!\""], ["said he, “there are two of them; one does the work while the other stands guard.”", "백작이 말했다. \"둘이군. 하나가 일을 하는 동안 다른 하나가 망을 보는 거야.\""], ["He made a sign to Ali not to lose sight of the man in the street, and turned to the one in the dressing-room.", "그는 알리에게 거리의 사내에게서 눈을 떼지 말라고 신호를 보내고, 화장실의 사내 쪽으로 돌아섰다."]], [["The glass-cutter had entered, and was feeling his way, his arms stretched out before him.", "유리를 잘라 낸 사내는 안으로 들어와, 두 팔을 앞으로 뻗은 채 손으로 더듬으며 길을 찾고 있었다."], ["At last he appeared to have made himself familiar with his surroundings. There were two doors; he bolted them both.", "마침내 주변 지리를 익힌 듯했다. 문이 둘 있었는데, 사내는 두 문에 모두 빗장을 질렀다."]], [["When he drew near to the bedroom door, Monte Cristo expected that he was coming in, and raised one of his pistols; but he simply heard the sound of the bolts sliding in their copper rings.", "사내가 침실 문 쪽으로 다가오자, 몬테크리스토는 그가 들어오려나 싶어 권총 하나를 들어 올렸다. 그러나 들려온 것은 빗장이 구리 고리 속에서 미끄러지는 소리뿐이었다."], ["It was only a precaution.", "그저 조심하느라 한 짓이었다."], ["The nocturnal visitor, ignorant of the fact that the count had removed the staples, might now think himself at home, and pursue his purpose with full security.", "백작이 고리를 떼어 냈다는 사실을 모르는 이 한밤의 방문객은 이제 제집인 양 마음 놓고 목적을 이룰 수 있다고 여겼을 것이다."], ["Alone and free to act as he wished, the man then drew from his pocket something which the count could not discern, placed it on a stand, then went straight to the secretaire, felt the lock, and contrary to his expectation found that the key was missing.", "홀로 마음껏 움직일 수 있게 되자, 사내는 주머니에서 백작이 분간할 수 없는 무언가를 꺼내 받침대 위에 올려놓았다. 그러고는 곧장 비밀 서랍장으로 가 자물쇠를 더듬더니, 뜻밖에도 열쇠가 없다는 것을 알았다."], ["But the glass-cutter was a prudent man who had provided for all emergencies.", "그러나 유리를 잘라 낸 그 사내는 온갖 비상사태에 대비해 둔 용의주도한 자였다."], ["The count soon heard the rattling of a bunch of skeleton keys, such as the locksmith brings when called to force a lock, and which thieves call nightingales, doubtless from the music of their nightly song when they grind against the bolt.", "곧 백작은 한 묶음의 곁쇠가 짤랑거리는 소리를 들었다. 자물쇠 장이가 자물쇠를 따 달라는 부름을 받고 가져오는 그런 것으로, 도둑들은 그것을 꾀꼬리라 불렀다. 빗장에 갈릴 때 나는 밤의 노랫소리 때문이리라."]], [["“Ah, ha,”", "\"하, 이런.\""], ["whispered Monte Cristo with a smile of disappointment, “he is only a thief.”", "몬테크리스토가 실망 어린 미소를 띠며 속삭였다. \"그저 도둑일 뿐이군.\""]], [["But the man in the dark could not find the right key.", "그러나 어둠 속의 사내는 맞는 열쇠를 찾지 못했다."], ["He reached the instrument he had placed on the stand, touched a spring, and immediately a pale light, just bright enough to render objects distinct, was reflected on his hands and countenance.", "그는 받침대에 올려 둔 도구로 손을 뻗어 용수철을 건드렸다. 그러자 이내 창백한 빛이, 물체를 또렷이 분간할 만큼만 밝게, 그의 손과 얼굴에 비쳤다."]], [["“By heavens,”", "\"맙소사.\""], ["exclaimed Monte Cristo, starting back, “it is——”", "몬테크리스토가 뒷걸음치며 외쳤다. \"저자는……\""]], [["Ali raised his hatchet.", "알리가 손도끼를 들어 올렸다."]], [["“Don’t stir,”", "\"움직이지 마라.\""], ["whispered Monte Cristo, “and put down your hatchet; we shall require no arms.”", "몬테크리스토가 속삭였다. \"도끼는 내려놓고. 무기는 필요 없을 테니.\""]], [["40154m\nThen he added some words in a low tone, for the exclamation which surprise had drawn from the count, faint as it had been, had startled the man who remained in the pose of the old knife-grinder.", "그러고는 낮은 목소리로 몇 마디 덧붙였다. 놀라움이 백작의 입에서 끌어낸 그 짧은 외침이, 아무리 희미했다 해도, 옛 칼갈이 같은 자세로 그대로 있던 사내를 흠칫하게 했던 것이다."]], [["It was an order the count had just given, for immediately Ali went noiselessly, and returned, bearing a black dress and a three-cornered hat.", "백작이 방금 내린 것은 명령이었다. 알리가 곧장 소리 없이 나갔다가, 검은 옷 한 벌과 삼각모 하나를 들고 돌아왔다."], ["Meanwhile Monte Cristo had rapidly taken off his greatcoat, waistcoat, and shirt, and one might distinguish by the glimmering through the open panel that he wore a pliant tunic of steel mail, of which the last in France, where daggers are no longer dreaded, was worn by King Louis XVI., who feared the dagger at his breast, and whose head was cleft with a hatchet.", "그사이 몬테크리스토는 외투와 조끼와 셔츠를 재빨리 벗었는데, 열린 칸막이 사이로 새어 드는 불빛에 그가 부드러운 강철 사슬 갑옷을 받쳐 입은 것이 분간되었다. 단검이 더는 두렵지 않은 프랑스에서 그런 갑옷을 마지막으로 입은 이는 루이 16세였다. 그는 가슴에 닿을 단검을 두려워했으나, 정작 그의 머리는 도끼에 쪼개졌다."], ["The tunic soon disappeared under a long cassock, as did his hair under a priest’s wig; the three-cornered hat over this effectually transformed the count into an abbé.", "그 갑옷은 이내 긴 사제복 아래로 사라졌고, 머리카락도 사제의 가발 아래로 가려졌다. 그 위에 삼각모를 쓰자 백작은 영락없는 신부로 변모했다."]], [["The man, hearing nothing more, stood erect, and while Monte Cristo was completing his disguise had advanced straight to the secretaire, whose lock was beginning to crack under his nightingale.", "더는 아무 소리도 들리지 않자, 사내는 몸을 곧추세웠다. 몬테크리스토가 변장을 마무리하는 동안 그는 곧장 비밀 서랍장으로 다가갔고, 자물쇠가 그의 꾀꼬리 아래에서 깨지기 시작했다."]], [["“Try again,”", "\"다시 해 봐라.\""], ["whispered the count, who depended on the secret spring, which was unknown to the picklock, clever as he might be—“try again, you have a few minutes’ work there.”", "백작이 속삭였다. 그는 비밀 용수철을 믿고 있었는데, 아무리 솜씨 좋은 자물쇠 따는 자라도 그것은 알지 못했다. \"다시 해 봐, 거기 몇 분은 더 매달려야 할 테니.\""]], [["And he advanced to the window.", "그러고는 창으로 다가갔다."], ["The man whom he had seen seated on a fence had got down, and was still pacing the street; but, strange as it appeared, he cared not for those who might pass from the avenue of the Champs-Élysées or by the Faubourg Saint-Honoré; his attention was engrossed with what was passing at the count’s, and his only aim appeared to be to discern every movement in the dressing-room.", "울타리에 걸터앉아 있던 사내는 어느새 내려와 여전히 거리를 서성이고 있었다. 그런데 이상하게도, 그는 샹젤리제 대로나 포부르생토노레 쪽에서 지나갈지 모를 사람들에게는 신경 쓰지 않았다. 온 정신이 백작의 집에서 벌어지는 일에 쏠려 있었고, 오직 화장실에서 일어나는 움직임 하나하나를 살피는 것만이 그의 목적인 듯했다."]], [["Monte Cristo suddenly struck his finger on his forehead and a smile passed over his lips; then drawing near to Ali, he whispered:", "몬테크리스토는 문득 손가락으로 이마를 쳤고, 입가에 미소가 스쳤다. 그러고는 알리에게 다가가 속삭였다."]], [["“Remain here, concealed in the dark, and whatever noise you hear, whatever passes, only come in or show yourself if I call you.”", "\"여기 어둠 속에 숨어 있어라. 무슨 소리가 들리든, 무슨 일이 벌어지든, 내가 부를 때만 들어오거나 모습을 드러내라.\""]], [["Ali bowed in token of strict obedience.", "알리는 절대복종의 표시로 고개를 숙였다."], ["Monte Cristo then drew a lighted taper from a closet, and when the thief was deeply engaged with his lock, silently opened the door, taking care that the light should shine directly on his face.", "이어 몬테크리스토는 벽장에서 불 켜진 양초 하나를 꺼냈다. 도둑이 자물쇠에 깊이 몰두해 있을 때, 그는 불빛이 곧장 제 얼굴에 비치도록 신경 쓰며 소리 없이 문을 열었다."], ["The door opened so quietly that the thief heard no sound; but, to his astonishment, the room was suddenly illuminated.", "문이 어찌나 조용히 열렸던지 도둑은 아무 소리도 듣지 못했다. 그러나 놀랍게도 방이 갑자기 환해졌다."], ["He turned.", "그가 돌아보았다."]], [["“Ah, good-evening, my dear M. Caderousse,”", "\"아, 안녕하시오, 친애하는 카드루스 씨.\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “what are you doing here, at such an hour?”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"이런 시각에 여기서 무얼 하고 계시오?\""]], [["40156m\n“The Abbé Busoni!”", "\"부소니 신부님!\""], ["exclaimed Caderousse; and, not knowing how this strange apparition could have entered when he had bolted the doors, he let fall his bunch of keys, and remained motionless and stupefied.", "카드루스가 외쳤다. 문에 빗장을 질러 두었는데 이 기이한 환영이 어떻게 들어왔는지 알 수 없어, 그는 열쇠 묶음을 떨어뜨린 채 꼼짝도 못 하고 넋이 나가 버렸다."], ["The count placed himself between Caderousse and the window, thus cutting off from the thief his only chance of retreat.", "백작은 카드루스와 창 사이에 자리를 잡아, 도둑이 달아날 단 하나의 길마저 끊어 버렸다."]], [["“The Abbé Busoni!”", "\"부소니 신부님!\""], ["repeated Caderousse, fixing his haggard gaze on the count.", "카드루스가 핼쑥한 눈길을 백작에게 박은 채 되뇌었다."]], [["“Yes, undoubtedly, the Abbé Busoni himself,”", "\"그렇소, 틀림없이 부소니 신부 본인이오.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다."], ["“And I am very glad you recognize me, dear M.", "\"그리고 나를 알아봐 주니 참으로 기쁘구려, 친애하는 카드루스 씨."], ["Caderousse; it proves you have a good memory, for it must be about ten years since we last met.”", "기억력이 좋다는 증거 아니겠소. 우리가 마지막으로 만난 게 십 년쯤 전일 테니 말이오.\""]], [["This calmness of Busoni, combined with his irony and boldness, staggered Caderousse.", "부소니의 이 침착함이, 그 빈정거림과 대담함과 어우러져 카드루스를 휘청이게 했다."]], [["“The abbé, the abbé!”", "\"신부, 신부……\""], ["murmured he, clenching his fists, and his teeth chattering.", "그가 주먹을 움켜쥐며, 이를 딱딱 부딪치며 중얼거렸다."]], [["“So you would rob the Count of Monte Cristo?”", "\"그래서, 몬테크리스토 백작의 집을 털려는 거였소?\""], ["continued the false abbé.", "가짜 신부가 말을 이었다."]], [["“Reverend sir,”", "\"신부님,\""], ["murmured Caderousse, seeking to regain the window, which the count pitilessly blocked—“reverend sir, I don’t know—believe me—I take my oath——”", "카드루스가 백작이 무자비하게 막고 선 창을 다시 차지하려 애쓰며 중얼거렸다. \"신부님, 저는 모릅니다, 믿어 주십시오, 맹세컨대……\""]], [["“A pane of glass out,”", "\"유리창 한 장이 떨어졌고,\""], ["continued the count, “a dark lantern, a bunch of false keys, a secretaire half forced—it is tolerably evident——”", "백작이 말을 이었다. \"각등에, 곁쇠 한 묶음에, 반쯤 뜯긴 비밀 서랍장까지. 이만하면 꽤 분명하지 않소……\""]], [["Caderousse was choking; he looked around for some corner to hide in, some way of escape.", "카드루스는 숨이 막혔다. 그는 몸을 숨길 구석을, 빠져나갈 길을 찾아 사방을 두리번거렸다."]], [["“Come, come,”", "\"이런, 이런.\""], ["continued the count, “I see you are still the same,—an assassin.”", "백작이 말을 이었다. \"당신은 여전히 똑같은 사람이군. 살인자 말이오.\""]], [["“Reverend sir, since you know everything, you know it was not I—it was La Carconte; that was proved at the trial, since I was only condemned to the galleys.”", "\"신부님, 모든 걸 아시니 그것이 제가 아니었다는 것도 아시겠지요. 라 카르콩트였습니다. 재판에서도 그게 입증되었으니, 저는 갤리선 형만 받았던 겁니다.\""]], [["“Is your time, then, expired, since I find you in a fair way to return there?”", "\"그럼 형기가 끝난 거요? 마침 다시 그리로 돌아가기 딱 좋은 길에 들어선 당신을 만났으니 말이오.\""]], [["“No, reverend sir; I have been liberated by someone.”", "\"아닙니다, 신부님. 누군가가 저를 풀어 주었습니다.\""]], [["“That someone has done society a great kindness.”", "\"그 누군가는 사회에 크나큰 선행을 베푼 셈이군.\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["said Caderousse, “I had promised——”", "카드루스가 말했다. \"제가 약속을 했는데……\""]], [["“And you are breaking your promise!”", "\"그런데 그 약속을 깨고 있는 거지!\""], ["interrupted Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말을 잘랐다."]], [["“Alas, yes!” said Caderousse very uneasily.", "\"아아, 그렇습니다!\" 카드루스가 몹시 불안해하며 말했다."]], [["“A bad relapse, that will lead you, if I mistake not, to the Place de Grève.", "\"고약한 재범이로군. 내 짐작이 틀리지 않다면, 그건 당신을 그레브 광장으로 끌고 갈 거요."], ["So much the worse, so much the worse—_diavolo!_ as they say in my country.”", "더더욱 안됐군, 더더욱 안됐어. 내 고향 말로 디아볼로●로다.\""]], [["“Reverend sir, I am impelled——”", "\"신부님, 저는 떠밀려서……\""]], [["“Every criminal says the same thing.”", "\"죄인은 누구나 똑같은 소리를 하지.\""]], [["“Poverty——”", "\"가난이……\""]], [["“Pshaw!”", "\"흥!\""], ["said Busoni disdainfully; “poverty may make a man beg, steal a loaf of bread at a baker’s door, but not cause him to open a secretaire in a house supposed to be inhabited.", "부소니가 경멸하듯 말했다. \"가난은 사람을 구걸하게 하거나, 빵집 문 앞에서 빵 한 덩이를 훔치게는 할지언정, 사람이 산다고 여겨지는 집의 비밀 서랍장을 열게 하지는 않는 법이오."], ["And when the jeweller Johannes had just paid you 45,000 francs for the diamond I had given you, and you killed him to get the diamond and the money both, was that also poverty?”", "그리고 보석상 요하네스가 내가 당신에게 준 다이아몬드 값으로 4만 5천 프랑을 막 치렀을 때, 당신이 다이아몬드와 돈을 둘 다 차지하려고 그를 죽였소. 그것도 가난 탓이었소?\""]], [["“Pardon, reverend sir,”", "\"용서해 주십시오, 신부님.\""], ["said Caderousse; “you have saved my life once, save me again!”", "카드루스가 말했다. \"신부님께서는 한 번 제 목숨을 구해 주셨습니다. 다시 한 번 구해 주십시오!\""]], [["“That is but poor encouragement.”", "\"그것참 변변찮은 부추김이로군.\""]], [["“Are you alone, reverend sir, or have you there soldiers ready to seize me?”", "\"신부님 혼자 오셨습니까, 아니면 저를 붙잡으려고 거기 군인들이라도 대기시켜 두셨습니까?\""]], [["“I am alone,”", "\"나 혼자요.\""], ["said the abbé, “and I will again have pity on you, and will let you escape, at the risk of the fresh miseries my weakness may lead to, if you tell me the truth.”", "신부가 말했다. \"그리고 다시 한 번 당신을 가엾이 여겨, 내 약한 마음이 어떤 새로운 화를 부르든 그 위험을 무릅쓰고 도망치게 해 주리다. 만약 당신이 사실을 말한다면 말이오.\""]], [["“Ah, reverend sir,”", "\"아, 신부님.\""], ["cried Caderousse, clasping his hands, and drawing nearer to Monte Cristo, “I may indeed say you are my deliverer!”", "카드루스가 두 손을 모으고 몬테크리스토에게 다가서며 외쳤다. \"신부님이야말로 제 구원자이십니다!\""]], [["“You mean to say you have been freed from confinement?”", "\"그러니까 감옥에서 풀려났단 말이지?\""]], [["“Yes, that is true, reverend sir.”", "\"네, 그건 사실입니다, 신부님.\""]], [["“Who was your liberator?”", "\"누가 당신을 해방시켰소?\""]], [["“An Englishman.”", "\"영국인이었소.\""]], [["“What was his name?”", "\"이름이 뭐였소?\""]], [["“Lord Wilmore.”", "\"윌모어 경입니다.\""], ["“I know him; I shall know if you lie.”", "\"내가 아는 사람이지. 당신이 거짓말을 하면 알아챌 거요.\""]], [["“Ah, reverend sir, I tell you the simple truth.”", "\"아, 신부님, 그저 있는 그대로의 진실을 말씀드리는 겁니다.\""]], [["“Was this Englishman protecting you?”", "\"그 영국인이 당신을 비호하고 있었소?\""]], [["“No, not me, but a young Corsican, my companion.”", "\"아닙니다, 저는 아니고, 제 동료였던 젊은 코르시카 사람을요.\""]], [["“What was this young Corsican’s name?”", "\"그 젊은 코르시카 사람 이름이 뭐였소?\""]], [["“Benedetto.”", "\"베네데토입니다.\""]], [["“Is that his Christian name?”", "\"그게 세례명이오?\""]], [["“He had no other; he was a foundling.”", "\"다른 이름은 없었습니다. 버려진 아이였으니까요.\""]], [["“Then this young man escaped with you?”", "\"그럼 그 젊은이가 당신과 함께 탈출한 거요?\""]], [["“He did.”", "\"그렇습니다.\""]], [["“In what way?”", "\"어떻게?\""]], [["“We were working at Saint-Mandrier, near Toulon.", "\"우리는 툴롱 근처 생망드리에에서 일하고 있었습니다."], ["Do you know Saint-Mandrier?”", "생망드리에를 아십니까?\""]], [["“I do.”", "\"알지.\""]], [["“In the hour of rest, between noon and one o’clock——”", "\"정오와 한 시 사이, 쉬는 시간에……\""]], [["“Galley-slaves having a nap after dinner!", "\"갤리선 죄수들이 식사 뒤에 낮잠을 잔다니!"], ["We may well pity the poor fellows!”", "그 가엾은 자들이 참으로 딱하구려!\""], ["said the abbé.", "신부가 말했다."]], [["“Nay,”", "\"아니,\""], ["said Caderousse, “one can’t always work—one is not a dog.”", "카드루스가 말했다. \"사람이 늘 일만 할 수는 없지 않습니까. 개도 아니고요.\""]], [["“So much the better for the dogs,”", "\"그럼 개들한테는 더없이 좋은 일이로군.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“While the rest slept, then, we went away a short distance; we severed our fetters with a file the Englishman had given us, and swam away.”", "\"아무튼 나머지가 잠든 사이에 우리는 좀 떨어진 곳으로 갔습니다. 영국인이 준 줄칼로 족쇄를 끊고 헤엄쳐 달아났지요.\""]], [["“And what is become of this Benedetto?”", "\"그래서 그 베네데토는 어찌 되었소?\""]], [["“I don’t know.”", "\"모릅니다.\""]], [["“You ought to know.”", "\"알아야 할 텐데.\""]], [["“No, in truth; we parted at Hyères.”", "\"정말 모릅니다. 우리는 이에르에서 헤어졌습니다.\""], ["And, to give more weight to his protestation, Caderousse advanced another step towards the abbé, who remained motionless in his place, as calm as ever, and pursuing his interrogation.", "그러고는 자기 항변에 무게를 더하려고 카드루스가 신부 쪽으로 한 걸음 더 다가섰다. 신부는 여전히 그 자리에서 꼼짝 않고, 여느 때처럼 차분하게 심문을 이어 갔다."]], [["“You lie,” said the Abbé Busoni, with a tone of irresistible authority.", "\"거짓말이오.\" 부소니 신부가 거역할 수 없는 위엄이 담긴 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Reverend sir!”", "\"신부님!\""]], [["“You lie!", "\"거짓말이야!"], ["This man is still your friend, and you, perhaps, make use of him as your accomplice.” 40160m", "그 사내는 여전히 당신 친구이고, 당신은 어쩌면 그를 공범으로 부려 먹고 있는 거지.\""]], [["“Oh, reverend sir!”", "\"오, 신부님!\""]], [["“Since you left Toulon what have you lived on? Answer me!”", "\"툴롱을 떠난 뒤로 당신은 무엇으로 먹고살았소? 대답해 보시오!\""]], [["“On what I could get.”", "\"손에 넣을 수 있는 걸로요.\""]], [["“You lie,” repeated the abbé a third time, with a still more imperative tone.", "\"거짓말이야.\" 신부가 한층 더 위압적인 어조로 세 번째로 되뇌었다."], ["Caderousse, terrified, looked at the count.", "카드루스는 겁에 질려 백작을 바라보았다."], ["“You have lived on the money he has given you.”", "\"당신은 그자가 준 돈으로 먹고살았소.\""]], [["“True,”", "\"맞습니다.\""], ["said Caderousse; “Benedetto has become the son of a great lord.”", "카드루스가 말했다. \"베네데토는 한 대귀족의 아들이 되었습니다.\""]], [["“How can he be the son of a great lord?”", "\"어떻게 대귀족의 아들이 될 수 있소?\""]], [["“A natural son.”", "\"사생아지요.\""]], [["“And what is that great lord’s name?”", "\"그 대귀족의 이름이 뭐요?\""]], [["“The Count of Monte Cristo, the very same in whose house we are.”", "\"몬테크리스토 백작, 바로 우리가 지금 들어와 있는 이 집의 주인입니다.\""]], [["“Benedetto the count’s son?”", "\"베네데토가 백작의 아들이라고?\""], ["replied Monte Cristo, astonished in his turn.", "이번에는 몬테크리스토가 놀라며 되물었다."]], [["“Well, I should think so, since the count has found him a false father—since the count gives him four thousand francs a month, and leaves him 500,000 francs in his will.”", "\"그렇겠지요. 백작이 그에게 가짜 아버지까지 붙여 주었으니까요. 백작이 그에게 다달이 4천 프랑을 주고, 유언장에 50만 프랑을 남겨 준다니 말입니다.\""]], [["“Ah, yes,”", "\"아, 그렇군.\""], ["said the factitious abbé, who began to understand; “and what name does the young man bear meanwhile?”", "가짜 신부가 이제야 사정을 알아차리며 말했다. \"그래서 그 젊은이는 지금 무슨 이름을 쓰고 있소?\""]], [["“Andrea Cavalcanti.”", "\"안드레아 카발칸티입니다.\""]], [["“Is it, then, that young man whom my friend the Count of Monte Cristo has received into his house, and who is going to marry Mademoiselle Danglars?”", "\"그렇다면 내 친구 몬테크리스토 백작이 자기 집에 받아들였고, 당글라르 양과 혼인하려는 그 젊은이가 바로 그자란 말이오?\" \"바로 그렇습니다.\""]], [["“Exactly.” “And you suffer that, you wretch!—you, who know his life and his crime?”", "\"그런데 당신은 그걸 보고만 있단 말이오, 이 가증스러운 인간아! 그자의 삶도, 그자의 죄도 다 아는 당신이!\""]], [["“Why should I stand in a comrade’s way?”", "\"제가 왜 동료의 앞길을 막겠습니까?\""], ["said Caderousse.", "카드루스가 말했다."]], [["“You are right; it is not you who should apprise M. Danglars, it is I.”", "\"옳은 말이군. 당글라르 씨에게 알릴 사람은 당신이 아니라 바로 나야.\""]], [["“Do not do so, reverend sir.”", "\"그러지 마십시오, 신부님.\""]], [["“Why not?”", "\"왜 안 되오?\""]], [["“Because you would bring us to ruin.”", "\"우리를 파멸로 몰아넣으실 테니까요.\""]], [["“And you think that to save such villains as you I will become an abettor of their plot, an accomplice in their crimes?”", "\"그래서 당신 같은 악당들을 구해 주려고 내가 그 음모의 부추김꾼이, 그 범죄의 공범이 되어 주리라 생각하는 거요?\""]], [["“Reverend sir,”", "\"신부님.\""], ["said Caderousse, drawing still nearer.", "카드루스가 더욱 다가서며 말했다."]], [["“I will expose all.”", "\"나는 모두 폭로하겠소.\""]], [["“To whom?”", "\"누구에게요?\""]], [["“To M. Danglars.”", "\"당글라르 씨에게.\""]], [["“By Heaven!”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["cried Caderousse, drawing from his waistcoat an open knife, and striking the count in the breast, “you shall disclose nothing, reverend sir!”", "카드루스가 조끼에서 칼날을 펼친 칼을 빼 들고 백작의 가슴을 찌르며 외쳤다. \"신부님, 당신은 아무것도 폭로하지 못하오!\""]], [["To Caderousse’s great astonishment, the knife, instead of piercing the count’s breast, flew back blunted.", "카드루스가 소스라치게 놀랍게도, 칼은 백작의 가슴을 꿰뚫기는커녕 무디게 튕겨 나왔다."], ["At the same moment the count seized with his left hand the assassin’s wrist, and wrung it with such strength that the knife fell from his stiffened fingers, and Caderousse uttered a cry of pain.", "동시에 백작은 왼손으로 살인자의 손목을 붙잡아, 굳어 버린 손가락에서 칼이 떨어져 나갈 만큼 세게 비틀었다. 카드루스가 고통에 찬 비명을 질렀다."], ["But the count, disregarding his cry, continued to wring the bandit’s wrist, until, his arm being dislocated, he fell first on his knees, then flat on the floor.", "그러나 백작은 그 비명에 아랑곳없이 도적의 손목을 계속 비틀었고, 마침내 팔이 탈구되자 카드루스는 먼저 무릎을 꿇었다가 바닥에 납작 엎어졌다."]], [["The count then placed his foot on his head, saying, “I know not what restrains me from crushing thy skull, rascal.”", "그러자 백작이 그의 머리를 발로 짓누르며 말했다. \"이 악당아, 무엇이 네 두개골을 으깨 버리지 못하게 나를 붙드는지 모르겠구나.\""]], [["“Ah, mercy—mercy!” cried Caderousse.", "\"아, 살려 주십시오, 살려 주십시오!\" 카드루스가 외쳤다."]], [["The count withdrew his foot.", "백작이 발을 거두었다."]], [["40162m\n“Rise!” said he.", "\"일어나라!\" 그가 말했다."], ["Caderousse rose.", "카드루스가 일어났다."]], [["“What a wrist you have, reverend sir!”", "\"신부님 손목이 어쩌면 그리 무섭습니까!\""], ["said Caderousse, stroking his arm, all bruised by the fleshy pincers which had held it; “what a wrist!”", "카드루스가, 그 살집 있는 집게에 붙들려 온통 멍든 팔을 어루만지며 말했다. \"어쩌면 그런 손목이!\""]], [["“Silence!", "\"닥쳐라!"], ["God gives me strength to overcome a wild beast like you; in the name of that God I act,—remember that, wretch,—and to spare thee at this moment is still serving him.”", "신께서 너 같은 들짐승을 제압할 힘을 내게 주시는 것이다. 나는 그 신의 이름으로 행하는 것이니, 그것을 명심해라, 이 가련한 인간아. 지금 이 순간 너를 살려 두는 것도 여전히 그분을 섬기는 일이다.\""]], [["“Oh!” said Caderousse, groaning with pain.", "\"오!\" 카드루스가 고통에 신음하며 말했다."]], [["“Take this pen and paper, and write what I dictate.”", "\"이 펜과 종이를 들고, 내가 부르는 대로 받아써라.\""]], [["“I don’t know how to write, reverend sir.”", "\"저는 글을 쓸 줄 모릅니다, 신부님.\""]], [["“You lie! Take this pen, and write!”", "\"거짓말이야! 이 펜을 들고 써라!\""]], [["Caderousse, awed by the superior power of the abbé, sat down and wrote:", "카드루스는 신부의 압도적인 힘에 기가 눌려 자리에 앉아 받아썼다."]], [["“Sir,—The man whom you are receiving at your house, and to whom you intend to marry your daughter, is a felon who escaped with me from confinement at Toulon. He was No.", "\"선생, 당신이 댁에 받아들였고 따님과 혼인시키려는 그 사내는, 툴롱에서 나와 함께 감옥을 탈출한 중죄인입니다. 그는 59번이었고 나는 58번이었습니다."], ["59, and I No. 58. He was called Benedetto, but he is ignorant of his real name, having never known his parents.”", "그는 베네데토라 불렸으나, 부모를 전혀 알지 못한 터라 제 진짜 이름은 모릅니다.\""]], [["“Sign it!”", "\"서명해라!\""], ["continued the count.", "백작이 말을 이었다."]], [["“But would you ruin me?”", "\"하지만 저를 파멸시키시려는 겁니까?\""]], [["“If I sought your ruin, fool, I should drag you to the first guard-house; besides, when that note is delivered, in all probability you will have no more to fear.", "\"내가 네 파멸을 노렸다면, 이 멍청아, 너를 가까운 초소로 끌고 갔겠지. 게다가 그 쪽지가 전달되고 나면, 십중팔구 너는 더 두려워할 게 없을 거다."], ["Sign it, then!”", "그러니 서명해라!\""]], [["Caderousse signed it.", "카드루스가 서명했다."]], [["“The address, ‘To monsieur the Baron Danglars, banker, Rue de la Chaussée d’Antin.’”", "\"수신인은 '당글라르 남작, 은행가, 쇼세 당탱 거리.'라고 쓰고.\""]], [["Caderousse wrote the address.", "카드루스가 주소를 적었다."], ["The abbé took the note.", "신부가 쪽지를 받아 들었다."]], [["“Now,”", "\"자,\""], ["said he, “that suffices—begone!”", "그가 말했다. \"이걸로 됐다. 어서 꺼져라!\""]], [["“Which way?”", "\"어느 쪽으로요?\""]], [["“The way you came.”", "\"들어온 길로.\""]], [["“You wish me to get out at that window?”", "\"저 창문으로 나가라는 말씀입니까?\""]], [["“You got in very well.”", "\"잘만 들어왔잖나.\""]], [["“Oh, you have some design against me, reverend sir.”", "\"오, 신부님께서 저를 노리는 무슨 꿍꿍이가 있으시군요.\""]], [["“Idiot!", "\"멍청한 놈!"], ["what design can I have?”", "내게 무슨 꿍꿍이가 있단 말이냐?\""]], [["“Why, then, not let me out by the door?”", "\"그럼 어째서 문으로 내보내 주시지 않습니까?\""]], [["“What would be the advantage of waking the porter?”", "\"문지기를 깨워서 좋을 게 뭐가 있겠나?\""]], [["“Ah, reverend sir, tell me, do you wish me dead?”", "\"아, 신부님, 말씀해 주십시오, 제가 죽기를 바라십니까?\""]], [["“I wish what God wills.”", "\"나는 신께서 바라시는 것을 바랄 뿐이다.\""]], [["“But swear that you will not strike me as I go down.”", "\"하지만 제가 내려가는 동안 저를 치지 않겠다고 맹세해 주십시오.\""]], [["“Cowardly fool!”", "\"비겁한 멍청이로군!\""]], [["“What do you intend doing with me?”", "\"저를 어쩌실 작정입니까?\""]], [["“I ask you what can I do?", "\"내가 무엇을 할 수 있겠느냐?"], ["I have tried to make you a happy man, and you have turned out a murderer.”", "나는 너를 행복한 사람으로 만들려고 애썼건만, 너는 살인자가 되어 버렸다.\""]], [["“Oh, monsieur,”", "\"오, 신부님.\""], ["said Caderousse, “make one more attempt—try me once more!”", "카드루스가 말했다. \"한 번만 더 시험해 주십시오. 다시 한 번만 저를 시험해 주십시오!\""]], [["“I will,”", "\"그러마.\""], ["said the count. “Listen—you know if I may be relied on.”", "백작이 말했다. \"들어라. 너는 내가 믿을 만한 사람인지 알 테지.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said Caderousse.", "카드루스가 말했다."]], [["“If you arrive safely at home——”", "\"네가 무사히 집에 닿거든……\""]], [["“What have I to fear, except from you?”", "\"신부님 말고 제가 두려워할 게 뭐가 있겠습니까?\""]], [["“If you reach your home safely, leave Paris, leave France, and wherever you may be, so long as you conduct yourself well, I will send you a small annuity; for, if you return home safely, then——”", "\"네가 무사히 집에 닿거든, 파리를 떠나라, 프랑스를 떠나라. 어디에 있든, 네가 처신을 바르게 하는 한, 내가 너에게 약간의 연금을 보내 주마. 만약 네가 무사히 집에 닿는다면, 그러면……\""]], [["“Then?”", "\"그러면요?\""], ["asked Caderousse, shuddering. 40164m", "카드루스가 몸을 떨며 물었다."]], [["“Then I shall believe God has forgiven you, and I will forgive you too.”", "\"그러면 신께서 너를 용서하셨다고 믿을 테고, 나 또한 너를 용서하마.\""]], [["“As true as I am a Christian,”", "\"제가 그리스도교도라는 것이 진실인 만큼이나 진실로,\""], ["stammered Caderousse, “you will make me die of fright!”", "카드루스가 더듬거렸다. \"신부님은 저를 두려움에 떨다 죽게 만드시는군요!\""]], [["“Now begone,” said the count, pointing to the window.", "\"이제 꺼져라.\" 백작이 창을 가리키며 말했다."]], [["Caderousse, scarcely yet relying on this promise, put his legs out of the window and stood on the ladder.", "카드루스는 그 약속을 아직 믿지 못한 채로 두 다리를 창밖으로 내밀어 사다리에 올라섰다."]], [["“Now go down,” said the abbé, folding his arms.", "\"이제 내려가라.\" 신부가 팔짱을 끼며 말했다."], ["Understanding he had nothing more to fear from him, Caderousse began to go down.", "더는 그에게서 두려워할 게 없음을 깨닫고 카드루스가 내려가기 시작했다."], ["Then the count brought the taper to the window, that it might be seen in the Champs-Élysées that a man was getting out of the window while another held a light.", "그러자 백작은 양초를 창가로 가져갔다. 한 사내가 창문을 빠져나가는 동안 다른 사내가 불을 들고 있는 모습을 샹젤리제에서 볼 수 있도록 하기 위해서였다."]], [["“What are you doing, reverend sir?", "\"신부님, 뭐 하시는 겁니까?"], ["Suppose a watchman should pass?”", "야경꾼이라도 지나가면 어쩌시려고요?\""], ["And he blew out the light. He then descended, but it was only when he felt his foot touch the ground that he was satisfied of his safety.", "그러고는 그가 불을 불어 껐다. 이어 그는 내려갔는데, 발이 땅에 닿는 것을 느끼고서야 비로소 안전하다고 안심했다."]], [["Monte Cristo returned to his bedroom, and, glancing rapidly from the garden to the street, he saw first Caderousse, who after walking to the end of the garden, fixed his ladder against the wall at a different part from where he came in.", "몬테크리스토는 침실로 돌아왔다. 정원에서 거리로 재빨리 눈길을 옮기던 그는 먼저 카드루스를 보았다. 카드루스는 정원 끝까지 걸어가더니, 들어온 곳과는 다른 자리에 사다리를 벽에 기대 세웠다."], ["The count then looking over into the street, saw the man who appeared to be waiting run in the same direction, and place himself against the angle of the wall where Caderousse would come over.", "이어 백작이 거리 쪽을 내려다보니, 기다리고 있던 듯한 그 사내가 같은 방향으로 달려가, 카드루스가 넘어올 벽 모퉁이에 몸을 붙였다."], ["Caderousse climbed the ladder slowly, and looked over the coping to see if the street was quiet.", "카드루스는 천천히 사다리를 올라가, 거리가 조용한지 보려고 담장 위로 고개를 내밀었다."], ["No one could be seen or heard.", "보이는 사람도 들리는 소리도 없었다."], ["The clock of the Invalides struck one.", "앵발리드의 시계가 한 시를 알렸다."], ["Then Caderousse sat astride the coping, and drawing up his ladder passed it over the wall; then he began to descend, or rather to slide down by the two stanchions, which he did with an ease which proved how accustomed he was to the exercise.", "그러자 카드루스가 담장 꼭대기에 걸터앉아 사다리를 끌어 올려 벽 너머로 넘겼다. 이어 그는 내려가기 시작했는데, 아니 두 받침 기둥을 타고 미끄러져 내렸는데, 그 솜씨가 어찌나 익숙한지 그가 그런 짓에 얼마나 단련되어 있는지를 보여 주었다."], ["But, once started, he could not stop.", "그러나 일단 내려가기 시작하자 멈출 수가 없었다."], ["In vain did he see a man start from the shadow when he was halfway down—in vain did he see an arm raised as he touched the ground.", "절반쯤 내려왔을 때 그림자 속에서 한 사내가 튀어나오는 것을 보았으나 소용없었고, 땅에 발이 닿는 순간 팔 하나가 치켜올려지는 것을 보았으나 소용없었다."]], [["Before he could defend himself that arm struck him so violently in the back that he let go the ladder, crying, “Help!”", "미처 몸을 지킬 새도 없이 그 팔이 어찌나 세차게 그의 등을 후려쳤던지, 그는 사다리를 놓치며 \"사람 살려!\" 하고 외쳤다."], ["A second blow struck him almost immediately in the side, and he fell, calling, “Help, murder!”", "곧이어 두 번째 일격이 그의 옆구리를 쳤고, 그는 \"사람 살려, 살인이야!\"라고 외치며 쓰러졌다."], ["Then, as he rolled on the ground, his adversary seized him by the hair, and struck him a third blow in the chest.", "이어 그가 바닥을 뒹구는 사이, 적이 그의 머리채를 움켜쥐고 가슴에 세 번째 일격을 가했다."]], [["This time Caderousse endeavored to call again, but he could only utter a groan, and he shuddered as the blood flowed from his three wounds.", "이번에는 카드루스도 다시 소리치려 했으나 신음 한 가닥밖에 내지 못했고, 세 군데 상처에서 피가 흘러나오자 몸을 부르르 떨었다."], ["The assassin, finding that he no longer cried out, lifted his head up by the hair; his eyes were closed, and the mouth was distorted.", "살인자는 그가 더는 소리치지 않자 머리채를 잡아 그의 머리를 들어 올렸다. 두 눈은 감겨 있었고 입은 일그러져 있었다."], ["The murderer, supposing him dead, let fall his head and disappeared.", "죽었다고 여긴 살인자는 그 머리를 떨어뜨리고 사라졌다."]], [["Then Caderousse, feeling that he was leaving him, raised himself on his elbow, and with a dying voice cried with great effort:", "그러자 카드루스는 그가 떠나는 것을 느끼고 팔꿈치로 몸을 일으켜, 죽어 가는 목소리로 안간힘을 다해 외쳤다."]], [["“Murder!", "\"살인이야!"], ["I am dying! Help, reverend sir,—help!”", "나 죽는다! 살려 주십시오, 신부님, 살려 주십시오!\""]], [["This mournful appeal pierced the darkness.", "이 애처로운 호소가 어둠을 꿰뚫었다."], ["The door of the back-staircase opened, then the side-gate of the garden, and Ali and his master were on the spot with lights.", "뒤쪽 계단의 문이 열리고, 이어 정원의 옆문이 열리더니, 알리와 그 주인이 불을 들고 그 자리에 나타났다. ● 디아볼로: 이탈리아어로 '제기랄' 정도의 뜻. —옮긴이"]], [["# Chapter 83. The Hand of God", "# Chapter 83. The Hand of God"]], [["Caderousse continued to call piteously, “Help, reverend sir, help!”", "카드루스는 애처롭게 계속 울부짖었다. \"사람 살려요, 신부님, 살려 주세요!\""]], [["“What is the matter?”", "\"무슨 일이오?\""], ["asked Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 물었다."]], [["“Help,”", "\"사람 살려.\""], ["cried Caderousse; “I am murdered!”", "카드루스가 외쳤다. \"저는 칼에 찔렸어요!\""]], [["“We are here;—take courage.”", "\"우리가 여기 있소. 기운을 내시오.\""]], [["“Ah, it’s all over!", "\"아, 다 끝났어요!"], ["You are come too late—you are come to see me die.", "너무 늦게 오셨어요. 제가 죽는 걸 보러 오신 거예요."], ["What blows, what blood!”", "이렇게 찔리고, 이렇게 피가……!\""]], [["He fainted. Ali and his master conveyed the wounded man into a room.", "그는 정신을 잃었다. 알리와 그의 주인은 부상자를 방 안으로 옮겼다."], ["Monte Cristo motioned to Ali to undress him, and he then examined his dreadful wounds.", "몬테크리스토가 알리에게 그의 옷을 벗기라고 손짓했고, 그런 뒤 끔찍한 상처들을 살펴보았다."]], [["“My God!”", "\"오, 하느님!\""], ["he exclaimed, “thy vengeance is sometimes delayed, but only that it may fall the more effectually.”", "그가 외쳤다. \"당신의 복수는 때로 늦어지지만, 그것은 더욱 어김없이 떨어지기 위함이로군요.\""], ["Ali looked at his master for further instructions.", "알리가 다음 지시를 기다리며 주인을 바라보았다."], ["“Bring here immediately the king’s attorney, M.", "\"지금 당장 검찰총장 빌포르 씨를 모셔 오너라."], ["de Villefort, who lives in the Faubourg Saint-Honoré.", "포부르생토노레에 사는 분이다."], ["As you pass the lodge, wake the porter, and send him for a surgeon.”", "수위실을 지날 때 수위를 깨워 외과 의사를 부르러 보내고.\""]], [["Ali obeyed, leaving the abbé alone with Caderousse, who had not yet revived.", "알리는 분부를 따랐고, 아직 의식을 되찾지 못한 카드루스와 신부만이 남았다."]], [["When the wretched man again opened his eyes, the count looked at him with a mournful expression of pity, and his lips moved as if in prayer.", "가엾은 사내가 다시 눈을 떴을 때, 백작은 슬픈 연민의 표정으로 그를 바라보았고, 그의 입술은 마치 기도하듯 움직였다."], ["“A surgeon, reverend sir—a surgeon!”", "\"의사를요, 신부님, 의사를!\""], ["said Caderousse.", "카드루스가 말했다."]], [["“I have sent for one,”", "\"사람을 보냈소.\""], ["replied the abbé.", "신부가 대답했다."]], [["“I know he cannot save my life, but he may strengthen me to give my evidence.”", "\"제 목숨을 구할 수 없다는 건 압니다. 하지만 증언할 힘은 채워 줄 수 있을지도 몰라요.\""]], [["“Against whom?”", "\"누구에 대한 증언이오?\""]], [["“Against my murderer.”", "\"저를 죽인 자에 대한 증언이오.\""]], [["“Did you recognize him?”", "\"그자를 알아보았소?\""]], [["“Yes; it was Benedetto.”", "\"네, 베네데토였어요.\""]], [["“The young Corsican?”", "\"그 젊은 코르시카 사람 말이오?\""], ["“Himself.”", "\"바로 그자예요.\""]], [["“Your comrade?”", "\"당신 동료요?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"당신의 동료 아니오?\" \"네."], ["After giving me the plan of this house, doubtless hoping I should kill the count and he thus become his heir, or that the count would kill me and I should be out of his way, he waylaid me, and has murdered me.”", "그자는 이 집 도면을 제게 주었지요. 분명 제가 백작을 죽이면 자기가 백작의 상속자가 되거나, 아니면 백작이 저를 죽여 제가 길에서 사라지기를 바랐던 거예요. 그러더니 길목에서 저를 기다렸다가 칼로 찔렀습니다.\""]], [["“I have also sent for the procureur.”", "\"검사도 불러오게 했소.\""]], [["“He will not come in time; I feel my life fast ebbing.”", "\"제때 오지 못할 거예요. 목숨이 빠르게 빠져나가는 게 느껴져요.\""]], [["“Wait a moment,”", "\"잠깐만 기다리시오.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["He left the room, and returned in five minutes with a phial.", "그는 방을 나갔다가 오 분 뒤 작은 약병을 들고 돌아왔다."], ["The dying man’s eyes were all the time riveted on the door, through which he hoped succor would arrive.", "죽어 가는 사내의 눈은 그동안 내내 문에 못 박혀 있었다. 그 문으로 구원의 손길이 들어오기를 바라고 있었던 것이다."]], [["“Hasten, reverend sir, hasten!", "\"서둘러 주세요, 신부님, 서둘러요!"], ["I shall faint again!” Monte Cristo approached, and dropped on his purple lips three or four drops of the contents of the phial.", "또 정신을 잃을 것 같아요!\" 몬테크리스토가 다가가 그의 보랏빛 입술에 약병 속 내용물을 서너 방울 떨어뜨렸다."], ["Caderousse drew a deep breath. “Oh,”", "카드루스가 깊은 숨을 들이쉬었다. \"오.\""], ["said he, “that is life to me; more, more!”", "그가 말했다. \"이건 제게 생명이에요. 더, 더요!\""]], [["“Two drops more would kill you,”", "\"두 방울만 더 떨어뜨리면 당신은 죽소.\""], ["replied the abbé.", "신부가 대답했다."]], [["“Oh, send for someone to whom I can denounce the wretch!”", "\"오, 그 악당을 고발할 수 있게 누구든 불러 주세요!\""]], [["“Shall I write your deposition?", "\"내가 당신의 진술을 받아 적을까?"], ["You can sign it.”", "서명만 하면 되오.\""]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"네, 네.\""], ["said Caderousse; and his eyes glistened at the thought of this posthumous revenge.", "카드루스가 말했다. 이 죽음 이후의 복수를 생각하니 그의 눈이 번득였다."], ["Monte Cristo wrote:", "몬테크리스토가 받아 적었다."]], [["“I die, murdered by the Corsican Benedetto, my comrade in the galleys at Toulon, No. 59.”", "\"나는 툴롱 감옥의 동료, 59호 코르시카 사람 베네데토에게 살해당해 죽는다.\""]], [["“Quick, quick!”", "\"빨리요, 빨리!\""], ["said Caderousse, “or I shall be unable to sign it.”", "카드루스가 말했다. \"안 그러면 서명할 수 없을 거예요.\""]], [["Monte Cristo gave the pen to Caderousse, who collected all his strength, signed it, and fell back on his bed, saying:", "몬테크리스토가 카드루스에게 펜을 건넸고, 그는 남은 힘을 다 끌어모아 서명을 한 뒤 침대에 다시 쓰러지며 말했다."]], [["“You will relate all the rest, reverend sir; you will say he calls himself Andrea Cavalcanti.", "\"나머지는 다 신부님이 말씀해 주세요. 그자는 스스로 안드레아 카발칸티라고 부른다고요."], ["He lodges at the Hôtel des Princes.", "프랭스 호텔에 묵고 있어요."], ["Oh, I am dying!”", "오, 죽겠어요!\""], ["He again fainted. The abbé made him smell the contents of the phial, and he again opened his eyes.", "그는 다시 정신을 잃었다. 신부가 약병의 내용물을 그에게 맡게 하자, 그가 다시 눈을 떴다."], ["His desire for revenge had not forsaken him.", "복수심은 그를 떠나지 않았다."]], [["“Ah, you will tell all I have said, will you not, reverend sir?”", "\"아, 제가 한 말을 다 전해 주실 거지요, 신부님?\""]], [["“Yes, and much more.”", "\"그러겠소. 그보다 더 많은 것도.\""]], [["“What more will you say?”", "\"무엇을 더 말씀하실 건데요?\""]], [["“I will say he had doubtless given you the plan of this house, in the hope the count would kill you.", "\"그자가 분명 당신에게 이 집 도면을 주었다고 말하겠소. 백작이 당신을 죽이기를 바라면서 말이오."], ["I will say, likewise, he had apprised the count, by a note, of your intention, and, the count being absent, I read the note and sat up to await you.”", "또한 그자가 쪽지로 백작에게 당신의 계획을 미리 알렸으나, 백작이 자리에 없어 내가 그 쪽지를 읽고 밤을 새우며 당신을 기다렸다고 말하겠소.\""]], [["“And he will be guillotined, will he not?”", "\"그러면 그자는 단두대로 가겠지요?\""], ["said Caderousse.", "카드루스가 말했다."], ["“Promise me that, and I will die with that hope.”", "\"그렇게 약속해 주세요. 그러면 그 희망을 품고 죽겠어요.\""]], [["“I will say,”", "\"그자가 내내 당신을 따라다니며 지켜보았고,\""], ["continued the count, “that he followed and watched you the whole time, and when he saw you leave the house, ran to the angle of the wall to conceal himself.”", "백작이 말을 이었다. \"당신이 집에서 나오는 걸 보자 몸을 숨기려고 담 모퉁이로 달려갔다고 말하겠소.\""]], [["“Did you see all that?”", "\"그걸 다 보셨어요?\""]], [["“Remember my words: ‘If you return home safely, I shall believe God has forgiven you, and I will forgive you also.’”", "\"내 말을 기억하시오. '만약 당신이 무사히 집에 돌아간다면, 나는 하느님이 당신을 용서하셨다고 믿겠고, 나 또한 당신을 용서하겠소.'\""]], [["“And you did not warn me!” cried Caderousse, raising himself on his elbows.", "\"그러면서 저한테 경고하지 않으셨군요!\" 카드루스가 팔꿈치로 몸을 일으키며 외쳤다."], ["“You knew I should be killed on leaving this house, and did not warn me!”", "\"이 집에서 나가면 제가 살해당하리라는 걸 알면서도, 경고하지 않으셨어요!\""]], [["40168m\n“No; for I saw God’s justice placed in the hands of Benedetto, and should have thought it sacrilege to oppose the designs of Providence.”", "\"그렇소. 하느님의 정의가 베네데토의 손에 맡겨진 것을 보았으니, 섭리의 뜻을 거스르는 것은 신성모독이라 여겼기 때문이오.\""]], [["“God’s justice!", "\"하느님의 정의라뇨!"], ["Speak not of it, reverend sir. If God were just, you know how many would be punished who now escape.”", "그런 말 마세요, 신부님. 하느님이 정의로우셨다면, 지금 벌을 면하고 있는 자들이 얼마나 많이 벌을 받았을지 신부님도 아실 거예요.\""]], [["“Patience,”", "\"참으시오.\""], ["said the abbé, in a tone which made the dying man shudder; “have patience!”", "신부가 죽어 가는 사내를 몸서리치게 만드는 어조로 말했다. \"참고 견디시오!\""]], [["Caderousse looked at him with amazement.", "카드루스가 놀라움에 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“Besides,”", "\"게다가,\" 신부가 말했다."], ["said the abbé, “God is merciful to all, as he has been to you; he is first a father, then a judge.”", "\"하느님은 모두에게 자비로우시오. 당신에게 그러셨던 것처럼 말이오. 그분은 먼저 아버지이고, 그다음에 심판자이시오.\""]], [["“Do you then believe in God?”", "\"그럼 신부님은 하느님을 믿으세요?\""], ["said Caderousse.", "카드루스가 말했다."]], [["“Had I been so unhappy as not to believe in him until now,”", "\"내가 지금껏 하느님을 믿지 못할 만큼 불행했다 해도,\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “I must believe on seeing you.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"당신을 보면 믿지 않을 수 없을 것이오.\""]], [["Caderousse raised his clenched hands towards heaven.", "카드루스가 움켜쥔 두 손을 하늘로 치켜들었다."]], [["“Listen,”", "\"들으시오.\""], ["said the abbé, extending his hand over the wounded man, as if to command him to believe; “this is what the God in whom, on your death-bed, you refuse to believe, has done for you—he gave you health, strength, regular employment, even friends—a life, in fact, which a man might enjoy with a calm conscience.", "신부가 마치 믿으라 명령하듯 부상자 위로 손을 뻗으며 말했다. \"당신이 죽음의 자리에서 믿기를 거부하는 그 하느님이 당신을 위해 하신 일은 이렇소. 그분은 당신에게 건강과 힘, 변변한 일자리, 심지어 친구까지 주셨소."], ["Instead of improving these gifts, rarely granted so abundantly, this has been your course—you have given yourself up to sloth and drunkenness, and in a fit of intoxication have ruined your best friend.”", "한마디로 사람이 떳떳한 양심으로 누릴 수 있는 삶을 주신 것이오. 그토록 넉넉히 베풀어진 일이 드문 이 선물을 더 키우기는커녕, 당신은 이런 길을 걸었소. 게으름과 술독에 자신을 내맡겼고, 만취한 끝에 가장 가까운 벗을 파멸시켰소.\""]], [["“Help!”", "\"사람 살려!\" 카드루스가 외쳤다. \"저한테 필요한 건 신부가 아니라 의사예요."], ["cried Caderousse; “I require a surgeon, not a priest; perhaps I am not mortally wounded—I may not die; perhaps they can yet save my life.”", "어쩌면 치명상이 아닐지도 몰라요. 죽지 않을 수도 있어요. 아직 제 목숨을 구할 수 있을지도 모른다고요.\""]], [["“Your wounds are so far mortal that, without the three drops I gave you, you would now be dead.", "\"당신의 상처는 치명적이오. 내가 준 세 방울이 없었다면 당신은 지금쯤 죽었을 것이오."], ["Listen, then.”", "그러니 들으시오.\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아.\""], ["murmured Caderousse, “what a strange priest you are; you drive the dying to despair, instead of consoling them.”", "카드루스가 중얼거렸다. \"참 별난 신부도 다 있네. 죽어 가는 사람을 위로하기는커녕 절망으로 몰아넣다니.\""]], [["“Listen,”", "\"들으시오.\""], ["continued the abbé.", "신부가 말을 이었다."], ["“When you had betrayed your friend, God began not to strike, but to warn you.", "\"당신이 벗을 배신했을 때, 하느님은 치시지 않고 먼저 당신에게 경고하셨소."], ["Poverty overtook you.", "가난이 당신에게 닥쳤소."], ["You had already passed half your life in coveting that which you might have honorably acquired; and already you contemplated crime under the excuse of want, when God worked a miracle in your behalf, sending you, by my hands, a fortune—brilliant, indeed, for you, who had never possessed any.", "당신은 이미 정직하게 얻을 수도 있었던 것을 탐내며 인생의 절반을 보냈소. 그리고 궁핍을 핑계 삼아 이미 범죄를 꿈꾸고 있었는데, 그때 하느님이 당신을 위해 기적을 행하셨소. 내 손을 빌려 당신에게 재산을 보내신 것이오."], ["But this unexpected, unhoped-for, unheard-of fortune sufficed you no longer when you once possessed it; you wished to double it, and how?—by a murder!", "한 번도 가져 본 적 없는 당신에게는 참으로 눈부신 재산이었지. 그러나 한때 그토록 뜻밖이고 바라지도 못했으며 들어 본 적도 없던 그 재산은, 일단 손에 넣고 나니 더는 당신을 만족시키지 못했소. 당신은 그것을 두 배로 늘리고 싶어 했소."], ["You succeeded, and then God snatched it from you, and brought you to justice.”", "그런데 어떻게? 살인으로 말이오! 당신은 성공했고, 그러자 하느님이 그것을 당신에게서 빼앗아 당신을 법정에 세우셨소.\""]], [["“It was not I who wished to kill the Jew,”", "\"그 유대인을 죽이려 한 건 제가 아니었어요.\""], ["said Caderousse; “it was La Carconte.”", "카드루스가 말했다. \"라 카르콩트였어요.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇소.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["said Monte Cristo, “and God,—I cannot say in justice, for his justice would have slain you,—but God, in his mercy, spared your life.”", "\"그리고 하느님은, 정의로써라고는 말하지 않겠소. 그분의 정의였다면 당신을 죽이셨을 테니. 다만 자비로써, 하느님은 당신의 목숨을 살려 주셨소.\""]], [["“_Pardieu!_ to transport me for life, how merciful!”", "\"빌어먹을! 종신 유형에 처해 놓고는, 그게 자비라니!\""]], [["“You thought it a mercy then, miserable wretch!", "\"그때는 당신도 그것을 자비로 여겼지, 이 가엾은 자여!"], ["The coward who feared death rejoiced at perpetual disgrace; for like all galley-slaves, you said, ‘I may escape from prison, I cannot from the grave.’ And you said truly; the way was opened for you unexpectedly.", "죽음을 두려워하던 겁쟁이가 영원한 치욕을 두고 기뻐했지. 모든 갤리선 죄수가 그러하듯, 당신도 이렇게 말했으니까. '감옥에서야 달아날 수 있어도, 무덤에서는 달아날 수 없다.'"], ["An Englishman visited Toulon, who had vowed to rescue two men from infamy, and his choice fell on you and your companion.", "그 말은 옳았소. 길이 뜻밖에 당신에게 열렸으니까. 한 영국인이 툴롱을 찾아왔소."], ["You received a second fortune, money and tranquillity were restored to you, and you, who had been condemned to a felon’s life, might live as other men.", "그는 두 사람을 치욕에서 구해 내겠다고 맹세한 사람이었고, 그 선택이 당신과 당신 동료에게 떨어졌소. 당신은 두 번째 재산을 받았고, 돈과 평온을 되찾았으며, 중죄인의 삶을 선고받았던 당신이 여느 사람들처럼 살 수 있게 되었소."], ["Then, wretched creature, then you tempted God a third time.", "그런데, 가엾은 자여, 그때 당신은 세 번째로 하느님을 시험했소."], ["‘I have not enough,’ you said, when you had more than you before possessed, and you committed a third crime, without reason, without excuse.", "'이걸로는 부족해.' 전에 가졌던 것보다 더 많이 가졌으면서도 당신은 그렇게 말했고, 아무런 이유도 없이, 변명할 거리도 없이 세 번째 범죄를 저질렀소."], ["God is wearied; he has punished you.”", "하느님은 지치셨고, 당신을 벌하신 것이오.\""]], [["Caderousse was fast sinking.", "카드루스는 빠르게 가라앉고 있었다."], ["“Give me drink,”", "\"마실 것을 주세요.\""], ["said he: “I thirst—I burn!”", "그가 말했다. \"목이 말라요."], ["Monte Cristo gave him a glass of water.", "타들어 가요!\" 몬테크리스토가 그에게 물 한 잔을 주었다."], ["“And yet that villain, Benedetto, will escape!”", "\"그런데도 그 악당, 베네데토는 빠져나가겠지요!\""]], [["“No one, I tell you, will escape; Benedetto will be punished.”", "\"누구도 빠져나가지 못하오, 내 장담하오. 베네데토도 벌을 받을 것이오.\""]], [["“Then, you, too, will be punished, for you did not do your duty as a priest—you should have prevented Benedetto from killing me.”", "\"그럼 신부님도 벌을 받을 거예요. 신부님은 신부로서 의무를 다하지 않았으니까요. 베네데토가 저를 죽이지 못하게 막았어야 했어요.\""]], [["“I?”", "\"내가?\""], ["said the count, with a smile which petrified the dying man, “when you had just broken your knife against the coat of mail which protected my breast!", "백작이 죽어 가는 사내를 얼어붙게 만드는 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"당신이 막 내 가슴을 지켜 준 사슬 갑옷에 칼을 부러뜨린 마당에 말이오!"], ["Yet perhaps if I had found you humble and penitent, I might have prevented Benedetto from killing you; but I found you proud and blood-thirsty, and I left you in the hands of God.”", "그래도 어쩌면 당신이 겸허하고 뉘우치는 모습이었다면, 베네데토가 당신을 죽이지 못하게 막았을지도 모르오. 하지만 나는 거만하고 피에 굶주린 당신을 보았기에, 당신을 하느님의 손에 맡겼소.\""]], [["“I do not believe there is a God,”", "\"하느님 같은 건 없어요.\""], ["howled Caderousse; “you do not believe it; you lie—you lie!”", "카드루스가 울부짖었다. \"신부님도 믿지 않잖아요. 거짓말이에요. 거짓말!\""]], [["“Silence,”", "\"조용히 하시오.\""], ["said the abbé; “you will force the last drop of blood from your veins. What!", "신부가 말했다. \"당신은 핏줄에서 마지막 한 방울까지 짜내고 말 것이오. 뭐라고!"], ["you do not believe in God when he is striking you dead?", "하느님이 당신을 쳐서 죽이시는데도 그분을 믿지 않겠다고?"], ["you will not believe in him, who requires but a prayer, a word, a tear, and he will forgive?", "기도 한마디, 말 한마디, 눈물 한 방울만 바라실 뿐 그러면 용서하시는 분을 믿지 않겠다고?"], ["God, who might have directed the assassin’s dagger so as to end your career in a moment, has given you this quarter of an hour for repentance.", "암살자의 단검을 이끌어 당신의 삶을 한순간에 끝낼 수도 있었던 하느님이, 당신에게 뉘우칠 십오 분을 주셨소."], ["Reflect, then, wretched man, and repent.”", "그러니 깊이 생각하고 뉘우치시오, 가엾은 자여.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"싫어요.\""], ["said Caderousse, “no; I will not repent.", "카드루스가 말했다. \"싫어요. 뉘우치지 않겠어요."], ["There is no God; there is no Providence—all comes by chance.”", "하느님은 없어요. 섭리도 없어요. 모든 게 우연으로 일어나는 거예요.\""]], [["“There is a Providence; there is a God,”", "\"섭리는 있소. 하느님도 계시오.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “of whom you are a striking proof, as you lie in utter despair, denying him, while I stand before you, rich, happy, safe and entreating that God in whom you endeavor not to believe, while in your heart you still believe in him.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"당신이 바로 그 명백한 증거요. 당신은 더없는 절망 속에 누워 그분을 부정하고 있는데, 나는 당신 앞에 부유하고 행복하며 안전하게 서서, 당신이 애써 믿지 않으려 하면서도 마음속으로는 여전히 믿고 있는 그 하느님께 빌고 있으니 말이오.\""]], [["“But who are you, then?”", "\"그런데 당신은 대체 누구예요?\""], ["asked Caderousse, fixing his dying eyes on the count.", "카드루스가 죽어 가는 눈을 백작에게 고정한 채 물었다."]], [["“Look well at me!”", "\"나를 똑똑히 보시오!\""], ["said Monte Cristo, putting the light near his face.", "몬테크리스토가 불빛을 자기 얼굴 가까이 가져가며 말했다."]], [["“Well, the abbé—the Abbé Busoni.”", "\"음, 신부님이네요. 부소니 신부님.\""], ["Monte Cristo took off the wig which disfigured him, and let fall his black hair, which added so much to the beauty of his pallid features.", "몬테크리스토는 자신의 얼굴을 흉하게 만들던 가발을 벗었고, 검은 머리카락이 흘러내렸다. 그 검은 머리는 창백한 이목구비에 한층 더한 아름다움을 더했다."]], [["“Oh?”", "\"오?\""], ["said Caderousse, thunderstruck, “but for that black hair, I should say you were the Englishman, Lord Wilmore.”", "카드루스가 벼락 맞은 듯 놀라 말했다. \"저 검은 머리만 아니라면, 당신을 그 영국인, 윌모어 경이라고 했을 거예요.\""]], [["“I am neither the Abbé Busoni nor Lord Wilmore,”", "\"나는 부소니 신부도 아니고 윌모어 경도 아니오.\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “think again,—do you not recollect me?”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"다시 생각해 보시오. 나를 기억하지 못하겠소?\""]], [["There was a magic effect in the count’s words, which once more revived the exhausted powers of the miserable man.", "백작의 말에는 마법 같은 힘이 있어서, 다 꺼져 가던 가엾은 사내의 기운을 다시 한 번 되살렸다."]], [["“Yes, indeed,”", "\"네, 정말로.\""], ["said he; “I think I have seen you and known you formerly.”", "그가 말했다. \"당신을 본 적이 있고, 예전에 알았던 것 같아요.\""]], [["“Yes, Caderousse, you have seen me; you knew me once.”", "\"그렇소, 카드루스. 당신은 나를 본 적이 있고, 한때 나를 알았소.\""]], [["“Who, then, are you?", "\"그럼, 당신은 누구예요?"], ["and why, if you knew me, do you let me die?”", "그리고 저를 알면서, 왜 저를 죽게 내버려 두는 거예요?\""]], [["“Because nothing can save you; your wounds are mortal.", "\"그 무엇도 당신을 살릴 수 없기 때문이오. 당신의 상처는 치명적이오."], ["Had it been possible to save you, I should have considered it another proof of God’s mercy, and I would again have endeavored to restore you, I swear by my father’s tomb.”", "당신을 살릴 수 있었다면, 나는 그것을 하느님의 자비를 보여 주는 또 하나의 증거로 여겼을 것이고, 다시 한 번 당신을 살리려 애썼을 것이오. 내 아버지의 무덤에 두고 맹세하오.\""]], [["“By your father’s tomb!”", "\"당신 아버지의 무덤에 두고!\""], ["said Caderousse, supported by a supernatural power, and half-raising himself to see more distinctly the man who had just taken the oath which all men hold sacred; “who, then, are you?”", "카드루스가 어떤 초자연적인 힘에 떠받쳐진 듯, 모든 사람이 신성하게 여기는 그 맹세를 방금 한 사내를 더 또렷이 보려고 반쯤 몸을 일으키며 말했다. \"그럼, 당신은 누구예요?\""]], [["The count had watched the approach of death.", "백작은 죽음이 다가오는 것을 지켜보고 있었다."], ["He knew this was the last struggle.", "그는 이것이 마지막 발버둥임을 알았다."], ["He approached the dying man, and, leaning over him with a calm and melancholy look, he whispered, “I am—I am——”", "그는 죽어 가는 사내에게 다가가 고요하고 우수에 찬 눈빛으로 그 위로 몸을 숙이고는 속삭였다. \"나는, 나는……\""]], [["And his almost closed lips uttered a name so low that the count himself appeared afraid to hear it.", "그리고 거의 닫힌 그의 입술이 어찌나 낮게 한 이름을 내뱉었는지, 백작 자신마저 그 소리를 듣기 두려워하는 듯했다."], ["Caderousse, who had raised himself on his knees, and stretched out his arm, tried to draw back, then clasping his hands, and raising them with a desperate effort, “Oh, my God, my God!”", "무릎으로 몸을 일으키고 팔을 뻗고 있던 카드루스는 뒤로 물러나려 하다가, 두 손을 맞잡고 필사적인 안간힘으로 그것을 치켜들며 말했다. \"오, 하느님, 하느님!"], ["said he, “pardon me for having denied thee; thou dost exist, thou art indeed man’s father in heaven, and his judge on earth.", "당신을 부정한 저를 용서하소서. 당신은 진실로 존재하시고, 참으로 하늘에 계신 인간의 아버지이며, 땅 위의 심판자이십니다."], ["My God, my Lord, I have long despised thee!", "하느님, 주님, 저는 오랫동안 당신을 업신여겼습니다!"], ["Pardon me, my God; receive me, Oh, my Lord!”", "저를 용서하소서, 하느님. 저를 받아 주소서, 오, 주님!\""]], [["Caderousse sighed deeply, and fell back with a groan.", "카드루스는 깊이 한숨을 내쉬고는 신음과 함께 뒤로 쓰러졌다."], ["The blood no longer flowed from his wounds.", "그의 상처에서는 더 이상 피가 흐르지 않았다."], ["He was dead.", "그는 죽었다."]], [["“_One!_”", "\"하나!\""], ["said the count mysteriously, his eyes fixed on the corpse, disfigured by so awful a death.", "백작이 그토록 끔찍한 죽음으로 일그러진 시신에 눈을 고정한 채, 알 수 없는 어조로 말했다."]], [["Ten minutes afterwards the surgeon and the procureur arrived, the one accompanied by the porter, the other by Ali, and were received by the Abbé Busoni, who was praying by the side of the corpse.", "십 분 뒤 외과 의사와 검사가 도착했다. 한 사람은 수위와 함께, 다른 한 사람은 알리와 함께였다. 그들은 시신 곁에서 기도하고 있던 부소니 신부의 맞이를 받았다."]], [["# Chapter 84. Beauchamp", "# Chapter 84. Beauchamp"]], [["The daring attempt to rob the count was the topic of conversation throughout Paris for the next fortnight.", "벌어진 일은 다음 보름 동안 파리 전역의 화젯거리였다. 백작의 집을 털려던 그 대담한 시도 말이다."], ["The dying man had signed a deposition declaring Benedetto to be the assassin.", "숨을 거두던 사내는 베네데토가 살인범이라고 진술서에 서명했다."], ["The police had orders to make the strictest search for the murderer.", "경찰은 살인범을 엄밀히 수색하라는 명령을 받았다."], ["Caderousse’s knife, dark lantern, bunch of keys, and clothing, excepting the waistcoat, which could not be found, were deposited at the registry; the corpse was conveyed to the morgue.", "카드루스의 칼과 어둠상자등, 열쇠 꾸러미, 그리고 끝내 찾지 못한 조끼를 제외한 옷가지가 등기소에 보관되었다. 시신은 시체 안치소로 옮겨졌다."], ["The count told everyone that this adventure had happened during his absence at Auteuil, and that he only knew what was related by the Abbé Busoni, who that evening, by mere chance, had requested to pass the night in his house, to examine some valuable books in his library.", "백작은 이 사건이 자기가 오퇴유에 가 있어 자리를 비운 사이에 일어났다고 사람들에게 말했다. 자기는 그날 저녁 우연히 서재의 진귀한 책 몇 권을 살펴보겠다며 하룻밤 묵게 해 달라고 청한 부소니 신부에게서 들은 것밖에는 모른다고 했다."]], [["Bertuccio alone turned pale whenever Benedetto’s name was mentioned in his presence, but there was no reason why anyone should notice his doing so.", "오직 베르투치오만이 자기 앞에서 베네데토의 이름이 거론될 때마다 얼굴이 창백해졌으나, 누군가 그것을 눈여겨볼 이유는 없었다."]], [["Villefort, being called on to prove the crime, was preparing his brief with the same ardor that he was accustomed to exercise when required to speak in criminal cases.", "빌포르는 범죄를 입증하라는 임무를 맡아, 형사 사건에서 변론할 때 늘 발휘하던 그 열의를 다해 기소장을 준비하고 있었다."]], [["But three weeks had already passed, and the most diligent search had been unsuccessful; the attempted robbery and the murder of the robber by his comrade were almost forgotten in anticipation of the approaching marriage of Mademoiselle Danglars to the Count Andrea Cavalcanti.", "그러나 벌써 삼 주가 지났는데도 가장 부지런한 수색조차 성과가 없었다. 그 절도 미수 사건과 동료의 손에 죽은 도둑 이야기는, 다가오는 당글라르 양과 안드레아 카발칸티 백작의 결혼식에 대한 기대 속에 거의 잊혀 갔다."], ["It was expected that this wedding would shortly take place, as the young man was received at the banker’s as the betrothed.", "이 결혼은 머지않아 이루어질 것으로 예상되었으니, 그 젊은이는 은행가의 집에서 약혼자로 대접받고 있었다."]], [["Letters had been despatched to M.", "카발칸티 씨, 곧 백작의 아버지에게는 편지가 보내졌다."], ["Cavalcanti, as the count’s father, who highly approved of the union, regretted his inability to leave Parma at that time, and promised a wedding gift of a hundred and fifty thousand livres.", "그는 이 결합을 크게 찬성하면서도 그때 파르마를 떠날 수 없는 사정을 안타까워했고, 십오만 리브르를 결혼 선물로 주겠다고 약속했다. 삼백만은 당글라르에게 맡겨 투자하게 한다는 데 합의가 이루어졌다."], ["It was agreed that the three millions should be intrusted to Danglars to invest; some persons had warned the young man of the circumstances of his future father-in-law, who had of late sustained repeated losses; but with sublime disinterestedness and confidence the young man refused to listen, or to express a single doubt to the baron.", "어떤 이들은 그 젊은이에게 장차 장인이 될 사람의 사정을 일러 주었다. 당글라르가 근래 거듭 손실을 입었다는 것이었다. 그러나 그 젊은이는 더없이 고결한 무사무욕과 신뢰로, 귀를 기울이려 하지도, 남작에게 일말의 의혹도 내비치려 하지 않았다."]], [["The baron adored Count Andrea Cavalcanti; not so Mademoiselle Eugénie Danglars.", "남작은 안드레아 카발칸티 백작을 끔찍이 아꼈다. 외제니 당글라르 양은 그렇지 않았다."], ["With an instinctive hatred of matrimony, she suffered Andrea’s attentions in order to get rid of Morcerf; but when Andrea urged his suit, she betrayed an entire dislike to him.", "결혼이라면 본능적으로 싫어하던 그녀는 모르세르를 떼어 내려고 안드레아의 구애를 견뎌 냈다. 그러나 안드레아가 청혼을 밀어붙이자 그에 대한 노골적인 혐오를 드러냈다."], ["The baron might possibly have perceived it, but, attributing it to a caprice, feigned ignorance.", "남작이 그것을 알아챘을지도 모르나, 그저 변덕으로 돌리고는 모르는 척했다."]], [["The delay demanded by Beauchamp had nearly expired.", "보상이 요구한 유예 기간이 거의 다 끝나 가고 있었다."], ["Morcerf appreciated the advice of Monte Cristo to let things die away of their own accord.", "모르세르는 모든 일을 그냥 자연히 가라앉게 두라는 몬테크리스토의 충고를 마음에 새겼다."], ["No one had taken up the remark about the general, and no one had recognized in the officer who betrayed the castle of Yanina the noble count in the House of Peers.", "그 장군에 관한 언급을 받아 든 사람은 아무도 없었고, 야니나성을 팔아넘긴 그 장교를 귀족원의 고결한 백작과 결부 짓는 사람도 없었다."]], [["Albert, however, felt no less insulted; the few lines which had irritated him were certainly intended as an insult.", "그러나 알베르는 여전히 모욕을 느끼고 있었다. 그를 격분케 한 그 몇 줄은 분명 모욕할 작정으로 쓰인 것이었다."], ["Besides, the manner in which Beauchamp had closed the conference left a bitter recollection in his heart.", "게다가 보상이 그 대화를 끝맺던 태도가 그의 가슴에 쓰라린 기억을 남겼다."], ["He cherished the thought of the duel, hoping to conceal its true cause even from his seconds.", "그는 그 결투를 마음에 품고 있었고, 진짜 이유는 입회인들에게조차 숨기려 했다."], ["Beauchamp had not been seen since the day he visited Albert, and those of whom the latter inquired always told him he was out on a journey which would detain him some days.", "보상은 알베르를 찾아왔던 그날 이후로 모습을 보이지 않았다. 그의 행방을 물을 때마다 사람들은 언제나, 며칠은 더 걸릴 여행을 떠났다고만 했다."], ["Where he was no one knew.", "어디에 있는지는 아무도 몰랐다."]], [["One morning Albert was awakened by his valet de chambre, who announced Beauchamp.", "어느 날 아침, 알베르는 보상이 왔다고 알리는 시종의 목소리에 잠에서 깼다."], ["Albert rubbed his eyes, ordered his servant to introduce him into the small smoking-room on the ground floor, dressed himself quickly, and went down.", "알베르는 눈을 비비고는, 그를 일층의 작은 흡연실로 안내하라고 일렀다. 그러고는 서둘러 옷을 입고 내려갔다."]], [["He found Beauchamp pacing the room; on perceiving him Beauchamp stopped.", "보상이 방 안을 서성이고 있었다. 그는 알베르를 보자 걸음을 멈추었다."]], [["“Your arrival here, without waiting my visit at your house today, looks well, sir,”", "\"내가 오늘 자네 집으로 찾아갈 때까지 기다리지 않고 이렇게 와 준 건 좋은 조짐이군.\" 알베르가 말했다."], ["said Albert.", "\"말해 보게."], ["“Tell me, may I shake hands with you, saying, ‘Beauchamp, acknowledge you have injured me, and retain my friendship,’ or must I simply propose to you a choice of arms?”", "자네와 악수하며 '보상, 자네가 내게 잘못했음을 인정하고 내 우정을 간직하게'라고 해도 되겠나, 아니면 그저 자네에게 무기를 고르라고 청해야겠나?\""]], [["“Albert,”", "\"알베르,\""], ["said Beauchamp, with a look of sorrow which stupefied the young man, “let us first sit down and talk.”", "보상이 말했다. 그 슬픈 눈빛에 젊은이는 멍해졌다. \"우선 앉아서 이야기부터 하세.\""]], [["“Rather, sir, before we sit down, I must demand your answer.”", "\"아니, 앉기 전에 나는 자네의 대답을 들어야겠네.\""]], [["“Albert,”", "\"알베르,\""], ["said the journalist, “these are questions which it is difficult to answer.”", "신문기자가 말했다. \"대답하기 어려운 질문일세.\""]], [["“I will facilitate it by repeating the question, ‘Will you, or will you not, retract?’”", "\"질문을 다시 해서 쉽게 만들어 주지. '취소하겠나, 안 하겠나?'\""]], [["“Morcerf, it is not enough to answer ‘yes’ or ‘no’ to questions which concern the honor, the social interest, and the life of such a man as Lieutenant-général the Count of Morcerf, peer of France.”", "\"모르세르, 프랑스의 귀족이자 모르세르 백작 중장 같은 사람의 명예와 사회적 이해와 목숨에 관한 질문에는 '예'나 '아니요'로 답하는 것만으로는 부족하네.\""]], [["“What must then be done?” 40174m", "\"그럼 어떻게 해야 한단 말인가?\""]], [["“What I have done, Albert.", "\"내가 한 대로 해야지, 알베르."], ["I reasoned thus—money, time, and fatigue are nothing compared with the reputation and interests of a whole family; probabilities will not suffice, only facts will justify a deadly combat with a friend.", "나는 이렇게 따져 보았네. 돈과 시간과 수고 따위는 한 가문 전체의 명성과 이해에 비하면 아무것도 아니라고. 개연성만으로는 부족하고, 오직 사실만이 친구와 목숨을 건 결투를 정당화할 수 있다고."], ["If I strike with the sword, or discharge the contents of a pistol at man with whom, for three years, I have been on terms of intimacy, I must, at least, know why I do so; I must meet him with a heart at ease, and that quiet conscience which a man needs when his own arm must save his life.”", "삼 년 동안 가까이 지내 온 사람에게 칼을 휘두르거나 권총을 쏘려면, 적어도 내가 왜 그러는지는 알아야 하지 않겠나. 제 팔로 제 목숨을 지켜야 하는 사람에게 필요한, 그 편안한 마음과 조용한 양심으로 그와 마주해야 하는 거야.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래서,\""], ["said Morcerf, impatiently, “what does all this mean?”", "모르세르가 조바심을 내며 말했다. \"이 모든 게 무슨 뜻인가?\""]], [["“It means that I have just returned from Yanina.”", "\"내가 방금 야니나에서 돌아왔다는 뜻일세.\""]], [["“From Yanina?”", "\"야니나에서?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["“Impossible!”", "\"그럴 리가!\""]], [["“Here is my passport; examine the visa—Geneva, Milan, Venice, Trieste, Delvino, Yanina.", "\"여기 내 여권이 있네. 비자를 살펴보게. 제네바, 밀라노, 베네치아, 트리에스테, 델비노, 야니나."], ["Will you believe the government of a republic, a kingdom, and an empire?”", "공화국과 왕국과 제국의 정부를 못 믿겠나?\""], ["Albert cast his eyes on the passport, then raised them in astonishment to Beauchamp.", "알베르는 여권에 눈길을 던졌다가, 놀란 눈을 들어 보상을 보았다."]], [["“You have been to Yanina?”", "\"자네 야니나에 다녀왔단 말인가?\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Albert, had you been a stranger, a foreigner, a simple lord, like that Englishman who came to demand satisfaction three or four months since, and whom I killed to get rid of, I should not have taken this trouble; but I thought this mark of consideration due to you.", "\"알베르, 자네가 만약 모르는 사람이거나, 외국인이거나, 서너 달 전에 결투를 청하러 왔다가 귀찮아서 내가 죽여 버린 그 영국인 같은 한낱 귀족이었다면, 나는 이런 수고를 하지 않았을 걸세. 하지만 자네에게는 이런 배려를 해야 마땅하다고 여겼네."], ["I took a week to go, another to return, four days of quarantine, and forty-eight hours to stay there; that makes three weeks.", "가는 데 일주일, 돌아오는 데 일주일, 검역에 나흘, 그곳에 머무는 데 마흔여덟 시간이 걸렸지. 그러니 삼 주가 된 거야."], ["I returned last night, and here I am.”", "어젯밤에 돌아왔고, 이렇게 여기 있는 걸세.\""]], [["“What circumlocution!", "\"무슨 말을 그리 빙빙 돌리나!"], ["How long you are before you tell me what I most wish to know?”", "내가 가장 알고 싶어 하는 걸 말하기까지 어찌 이리 오래 끄는 건가?\""]], [["“Because, in truth, Albert——”", "\"실은 말일세, 알베르……\""]], [["“You hesitate?”", "\"망설이는 건가?\""]], [["“Yes,—I fear.”", "\"그래. 두렵네.\""]], [["“You fear to acknowledge that your correspondent has deceived you?", "\"자네에게 제보한 사람이 자네를 속였다고 인정하기가 두려운가?"], ["Oh, no self-love, Beauchamp.", "아, 자존심은 버리게, 보상."], ["Acknowledge it, Beauchamp; your courage cannot be doubted.”", "인정하게, 보상. 자네 용기야 누가 의심하겠나.\""]], [["“Not so,”", "\"그게 아니야.\""], ["murmured the journalist; “on the contrary——”", "신문기자가 중얼거렸다. \"오히려 그 반대일세……\""]], [["Albert turned frightfully pale; he endeavored to speak, but the words died on his lips.", "알베르는 무섭도록 창백해졌다. 그는 입을 떼려 했으나, 말이 입술에서 죽어 버렸다."]], [["“My friend,”", "\"여보게,\" 보상이 더없이 다정한 어조로 말했다."], ["said Beauchamp, in the most affectionate tone, “I should gladly make an apology; but, alas!——”", "\"나도 기꺼이 사과하고 싶네. 하지만, 아아……\" \"하지만 뭐란 말인가?\""]], [["“The paragraph was correct, my friend.”", "\"그 기사는 사실이었네, 친구.\""]], [["“What? That French officer——”", "\"뭐라고? 그 프랑스 장교가……\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["“Fernand?”", "\"페르낭이?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["“The traitor who surrendered the castle of the man in whose service he was——”", "\"자기가 섬기던 사람의 성을 팔아넘긴 그 배신자가……\""]], [["“Pardon me, my friend, that man was your father!”", "\"미안하네, 친구. 그 사람이 바로 자네 아버지였어!\""]], [["Albert advanced furiously towards Beauchamp, but the latter restrained him more by a mild look than by his extended hand.", "알베르는 격분하여 보상에게 달려들었으나, 보상은 내민 손보다도 그 부드러운 눈빛으로 그를 더 잘 막아 냈다."]], [["“My friend,”", "\"여보게,\""], ["said he, “here is a proof of it.”", "그가 말했다. \"여기 그 증거가 있네.\""]], [["40176m\nAlbert opened the paper, it was an attestation of four notable inhabitants of Yanina, proving that Colonel Fernand Mondego, in the service of Ali Tepelini, had surrendered the castle for two million crowns.", "알베르는 종이를 펼쳤다. 야니나의 명망 있는 주민 네 사람의 진술서로, 알리 테펠리니를 섬기던 페르낭 몬데고 대령이 이백만 크라운을 받고 성을 넘겨주었음을 입증하는 것이었다."], ["The signatures were perfectly legal.", "서명은 완벽하게 합법적이었다."], ["Albert tottered and fell overpowered in a chair.", "알베르는 비틀거리다가 짓눌린 듯 의자에 쓰러졌다."], ["It could no longer be doubted; the family name was fully given.", "더는 의심할 수 없었다. 가문의 성씨가 고스란히 적혀 있었다."], ["After a moment’s mournful silence, his heart overflowed, and he gave way to a flood of tears.", "잠시 침통한 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 그의 가슴이 북받쳐 올라 그는 눈물을 쏟아 냈다."], ["Beauchamp, who had watched with sincere pity the young man’s paroxysm of grief, approached him.", "젊은이의 격렬한 슬픔을 진심 어린 연민으로 지켜보던 보상이 그에게 다가갔다."]], [["“Now, Albert,”", "\"이제, 알베르,\""], ["said he, “you understand me—do you not?", "그가 말했다. \"내 마음을 알겠지, 안 그런가?"], ["I wished to see all, and to judge of everything for myself, hoping the explanation would be in your father’s favor, and that I might do him justice.", "나는 모든 걸 직접 보고 모든 걸 내 눈으로 판단하고 싶었네. 그 설명이 자네 아버지께 유리하기를, 그래서 내가 그분의 누명을 벗겨 드릴 수 있기를 바라면서 말이야."], ["But, on the contrary, the particulars which are given prove that Fernand Mondego, raised by Ali Pasha to the rank of governor-general, is no other than Count Fernand of Morcerf; then, recollecting the honor you had done me, in admitting me to your friendship, I hastened to you.”", "그러나 오히려 거기 적힌 자세한 정황은, 알리 파샤에 의해 총독의 지위에까지 오른 페르낭 몬데고가 다름 아닌 모르세르의 페르낭 백작임을 증명하더군. 그래서 자네가 나를 우정으로 받아 준 그 영광을 떠올리고는, 서둘러 자네에게 달려온 걸세.\""]], [["Albert, still extended on the chair, covered his face with both hands, as if to prevent the light from reaching him.", "알베르는 여전히 의자에 늘어진 채 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다. 빛이 닿는 것조차 막으려는 듯이."]], [["“I hastened to you,”", "\"내가 자네에게 서둘러 온 것은,\""], ["continued Beauchamp, “to tell you, Albert, that in this changing age, the faults of a father cannot revert upon his children.", "보상이 말을 이었다. \"이 말을 하기 위해서일세, 알베르. 이 변화무쌍한 시대에 아버지의 잘못이 그 자식에게 되돌아올 수는 없다고 말이야."], ["Few have passed through this revolutionary period, in the midst of which we were born, without some stain of infamy or blood to soil the uniform of the soldier, or the gown of the magistrate.", "우리가 태어난 그 혁명의 시기를, 군인의 군복이나 법관의 법복에 불명예와 피의 얼룩 하나 묻히지 않고 지나온 사람은 거의 없네."], ["Now I have these proofs, Albert, and I am in your confidence, no human power can force me to a duel which your own conscience would reproach you with as criminal, but I come to offer you what you can no longer demand of me.", "자, 이제 내게 이 증거들이 있고, 나는 자네의 비밀을 알게 되었으니, 그 어떤 인간의 힘으로도 나를 결투로 끌어낼 수는 없네. 자네의 양심조차 그것을 범죄라며 자네를 책망할 테니까. 그래서 나는 자네가 더는 내게 요구할 수 없게 된 것을, 이렇게 자네에게 내놓으려 왔네."], ["Do you wish these proofs, these attestations, which I alone possess, to be destroyed?", "나만이 가지고 있는 이 증거들, 이 진술서들을 없애 버리길 바라나?"], ["Do you wish this frightful secret to remain with us?", "이 끔찍한 비밀이 우리 둘만의 것으로 남길 바라나?"], ["Confided to me, it shall never escape my lips; say, Albert, my friend, do you wish it?”", "내게 맡겨진 이상, 그것은 결코 내 입 밖으로 새어 나가지 않을 걸세. 말해 보게, 알베르, 친구여. 그러기를 바라나?\""]], [["Albert threw himself on Beauchamp’s neck.", "알베르는 보상의 목을 끌어안았다."]], [["“Ah, noble fellow!”", "\"아, 고귀한 친구여!\""], ["cried he.", "그가 외쳤다."]], [["“Take these,”", "\"이걸 받게.\""], ["said Beauchamp, presenting the papers to Albert.", "보상이 알베르에게 그 서류들을 건네며 말했다."]], [["Albert seized them with a convulsive hand, tore them in pieces, and trembling lest the least vestige should escape and one day appear to confront him, he approached the wax-light, always kept burning for cigars, and burned every fragment.", "알베르는 경련하듯 떨리는 손으로 그것들을 움켜쥐어 갈기갈기 찢었다. 그리고 행여 작은 조각 하나라도 남아 언젠가 자기 앞에 다시 나타날까 두려워, 늘 시가를 피우려고 켜 두는 양초 불 곁으로 다가가 한 조각도 남김없이 태웠다."]], [["“Dear, excellent friend,”", "\"소중하고도 더없이 훌륭한 친구여.\""], ["murmured Albert, still burning the papers.", "알베르가 여전히 종이를 태우며 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Let all be forgotten as a sorrowful dream,”", "\"모든 걸 슬픈 꿈처럼 잊어버리게.\" 보상이 말했다."], ["said Beauchamp; “let it vanish as the last sparks from the blackened paper, and disappear as the smoke from those silent ashes.”", "\"저 까맣게 탄 종이에서 튀는 마지막 불꽃처럼 사라지게 하고, 저 고요한 잿더미에서 피어오르는 연기처럼 흩어지게 하게.\""]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"그래, 그래.\" 알베르가 말했다."], ["said Albert, “and may there remain only the eternal friendship which I promised to my deliverer, which shall be transmitted to our children’s children, and shall always remind me that I owe my life and the honor of my name to you,—for had this been known, oh, Beauchamp, I should have destroyed myself; or,—no, my poor mother!", "\"그리고 오직, 나를 구해 준 자네에게 약속한 그 영원한 우정만이 남기를. 그 우정은 우리 자손의 자손에게까지 전해질 것이고, 내가 내 목숨과 가문의 명예를 자네에게 빚졌음을 늘 일깨워 주겠지. 만일 이 일이 알려졌다면, 아, 보상, 나는 스스로 목숨을 끊었을 걸세."], ["I could not have killed her by the same blow,—I should have fled from my country.”", "아니, 아니야. 가엾은 내 어머니! 같은 일격으로 어머니까지 죽일 수는 없으니, 나는 조국을 떠나 달아났을 거야.\""]], [["“Dear Albert,”", "\"가엾은 알베르.\""], ["said Beauchamp.", "보상이 말했다."], ["But this sudden and factitious joy soon forsook the young man, and was succeeded by a still greater grief.", "그러나 그 갑작스럽고 꾸며진 듯한 기쁨은 이내 젊은이에게서 사라지고, 한층 더 깊은 슬픔이 그 자리를 대신했다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래,\""], ["said Beauchamp, “what still oppresses you, my friend?”", "보상이 말했다. \"여전히 무엇이 자네를 짓누르나, 친구?\""]], [["40178m\n“I am broken-hearted,”", "\"가슴이 무너지는 것 같네.\""], ["said Albert.", "알베르가 말했다."], ["“Listen, Beauchamp!", "\"들어 보게, 보상!"], ["I cannot thus, in a moment relinquish the respect, the confidence, and pride with which a father’s untarnished name inspires a son.", "나는 이렇게 한순간에, 아버지의 더럽혀지지 않은 이름이 아들에게 불어넣어 주던 그 존경과 신뢰와 자부심을 놓아 버릴 수가 없네."], ["Oh, Beauchamp, Beauchamp, how shall I now approach mine?", "아, 보상, 보상, 이제 내가 어떻게 아버지께 다가갈 수 있겠나?"], ["Shall I draw back my forehead from his embrace, or withhold my hand from his?", "아버지의 품에서 이마를 거두어야 하나, 아니면 그 손에서 내 손을 빼야 하나?"], ["I am the most wretched of men.", "나는 세상에서 가장 비참한 사람일세."], ["Ah, my mother, my poor mother!”", "아, 내 어머니, 가엾은 내 어머니!\""], ["said Albert, gazing through his tears at his mother’s portrait; “if you know this, how much must you suffer!”", "알베르는 눈물 너머로 어머니의 초상화를 바라보며 말했다. \"어머니가 이 일을 아신다면, 얼마나 괴로워하실까!\""]], [["“Come,”", "\"자,\""], ["said Beauchamp, taking both his hands, “take courage, my friend.”", "보상이 그의 두 손을 잡으며 말했다. \"용기를 내게, 친구.\""]], [["“But how came that first note to be inserted in your journal?", "\"하지만 그 첫 기사가 어떻게 자네 신문에 실리게 된 건가?"], ["Some unknown enemy—an invisible foe—has done this.”", "누군가 알 수 없는 적이, 보이지 않는 원수가 이런 짓을 한 거야.\""]], [["“The more must you fortify yourself, Albert.", "\"그러니 더욱 마음을 굳게 다잡아야 하네, 알베르."], ["Let no trace of emotion be visible on your countenance, bear your grief as the cloud bears within it ruin and death—a fatal secret, known only when the storm bursts.", "자네 얼굴에 동요의 흔적이 조금도 비치지 않게 하게. 자네의 슬픔을, 그 안에 파멸과 죽음을 품은 먹구름처럼 견뎌 내게. 폭풍이 터질 때에야 비로소 드러나는 치명적인 비밀처럼 말일세."], ["Go, my friend, reserve your strength for the moment when the crash shall come.”", "가게, 친구. 파국이 닥칠 그 순간을 위해 힘을 아껴 두게.\""]], [["40179m\n“You think, then, all is not over yet?”", "\"그럼 자네 생각엔, 아직 다 끝난 게 아니란 말인가?\""], ["said Albert, horror-stricken.", "알베르가 공포에 질려 말했다."]], [["“I think nothing, my friend; but all things are possible.", "\"나는 아무 생각도 안 하네, 친구. 하지만 무슨 일이든 일어날 수 있지."], ["By the way——”", "그건 그렇고……\""]], [["“What?”", "\"뭔가?\""], ["said Albert, seeing that Beauchamp hesitated.", "보상이 머뭇거리는 것을 보고 알베르가 말했다."]], [["“Are you going to marry Mademoiselle Danglars?”", "\"자네 당글라르 양과 결혼할 셈인가?\""]], [["“Why do you ask me now?”", "\"왜 이제 와서 그걸 묻나?\""]], [["“Because the rupture or fulfilment of this engagement is connected with the person of whom we were speaking.”", "\"이 약혼이 깨지느냐 이루어지느냐가, 우리가 이야기하던 그 사람과 얽혀 있어서 말이야.\""]], [["“How?”", "\"어째서?\""], ["said Albert, whose brow reddened; “you think M. Danglars——”", "알베르가 이마를 붉히며 말했다. \"자네 생각엔 당글라르 씨가……\""]], [["“I ask you only how your engagement stands? Pray put no construction on my words I do not mean they should convey, and give them no undue weight.”", "\"나는 그저 자네 약혼이 어떻게 되었는지를 물을 뿐이야. 제발 내 말에 담지도 않은 뜻을 갖다 붙이지 말고, 부당하게 무게를 싣지도 말게.\""]], [["“No.”", "\"아니.\""], ["said Albert, “the engagement is broken off.”", "알베르가 말했다. \"약혼은 깨졌네.\""]], [["“Well,” said Beauchamp.", "\"그렇군.\" 보상이 말했다."], ["Then, seeing the young man was about to relapse into melancholy, “Let us go out, Albert,”", "그러고는 젊은이가 다시 우울에 빠져들려는 것을 보고 말했다. \"나가세, 알베르."], ["said he; “a ride in the wood in the phaeton, or on horseback, will refresh you; we will then return to breakfast, and you shall attend to your affairs, and I to mine.”", "사두마차를 타든 말을 타든 숲을 한 바퀴 돌면 기운이 날 걸세. 그런 다음 돌아와 아침을 들고, 자네는 자네 일을 보고 나는 내 일을 보면 되지.\""]], [["“Willingly,”", "\"좋아.\""], ["said Albert; “but let us walk.", "알베르가 말했다. \"하지만 걷세."], ["I think a little exertion would do me good.”", "좀 움직이는 게 내게 도움이 될 것 같아.\""]], [["The two friends walked out on the fortress.", "두 친구는 성벽 쪽으로 걸어 나갔다."], ["When they arrived at the Madeleine:", "마들렌 성당에 이르렀을 때였다."]], [["“Since we are out,”", "\"이왕 나왔으니,\""], ["said Beauchamp, “let us call on M.", "보상이 말했다. \"몬테크리스토 씨를 찾아가세."], ["de Monte Cristo; he is admirably adapted to revive one’s spirits, because he never interrogates, and in my opinion those who ask no questions are the best comforters.”", "그분은 사람의 기운을 북돋우는 데 더없이 알맞은 분이거든. 절대 캐묻는 법이 없으니까. 내 생각엔 아무것도 묻지 않는 사람이 가장 좋은 위로자일세.\""]], [["“Gladly,”", "\"기꺼이.\""], ["said Albert; “let us call—I love him.”", "알베르가 말했다. \"찾아가세. 나는 그분이 좋아.\""]], [["# Chapter 85. The Journey", "# Chapter 85. The Journey"]], [["Monte Cristo uttered a joyful exclamation on seeing the young men together. “Ah, ha!”", "몬테크리스토는 두 젊은이가 함께 있는 것을 보고 반가운 탄성을 질렀다. \"아, 됐군요!\""], ["said he, “I hope all is over, explained and settled.”", "그가 말했다. \"다 끝나고, 해명도 끝나고, 정리도 됐겠지요.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇습니다.\""], ["said Beauchamp; “the absurd reports have died away, and should they be renewed, I would be the first to oppose them; so let us speak no more of it.”", "보샹이 말했다. \"그 터무니없는 소문들은 잦아들었고, 혹시라도 다시 나돈다면 제가 가장 먼저 나서서 막을 겁니다. 그러니 더 이야기하지 맙시다.\""]], [["“Albert will tell you,”", "\"알베르가 말해 줄 겁니다.\""], ["replied the count “that I gave him the same advice. Look,”", "백작이 대답했다. \"저도 그에게 똑같은 충고를 했지요. 보세요.\""], ["added he. “I am finishing the most execrable morning’s work.”", "그가 덧붙였다. \"정말 끔찍한 오전 일거리를 막 끝내는 참입니다.\""]], [["“What is it?”", "\"그게 뭡니까?\""], ["said Albert; “arranging your papers, apparently.”", "알베르가 물었다. \"보아하니 서류 정리를 하시는 모양인데요.\""]], [["“My papers, thank God, no,—my papers are all in capital order, because I have none; but M.", "\"제 서류라니요, 천만에요. 다행히도 제 서류는 모두 더할 나위 없이 정돈돼 있습니다, 아예 없으니까요."], ["Cavalcanti’s.”", "카발칸티 씨의 서류입니다.\""]], [["“M. Cavalcanti’s?”", "\"카발칸티 씨의 서류라고요?\""], ["asked Beauchamp.", "보샹이 물었다."]], [["“Yes; do you not know that this is a young man whom the count is introducing?”", "\"네. 이 사람이 백작님께서 소개하시는 젊은이라는 걸 모르셨나요?\""], ["said Morcerf.", "모르세르가 말했다."]], [["“Let us not misunderstand each other,”", "\"서로 오해는 하지 맙시다.\" 몬테크리스토가 대답했다."], ["replied Monte Cristo; “I introduce no one, and certainly not M. Cavalcanti.”", "\"저는 아무도 소개하지 않습니다. 카발칸티 씨는 더더욱 아니고요.\""]], [["“And who,”", "\"그리고 그자가,\""], ["said Albert with a forced smile, “is to marry Mademoiselle Danglars instead of me, which grieves me cruelly.”", "알베르가 억지웃음을 지으며 말했다. \"저 대신 당글라르 양과 결혼하게 되는데, 그게 어찌나 가슴 아픈지요.\""]], [["“What? Cavalcanti is going to marry Mademoiselle Danglars?”", "\"뭐라고요? 카발칸티가 당글라르 양과 결혼한다고요?\""], ["asked Beauchamp.", "보샹이 물었다."]], [["“Certainly!", "\"물론이지요!"], ["do you come from the end of the world?”", "세상 끝에서라도 오신 겁니까?\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “you, a journalist, the husband of renown?", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"기자에다, 소문의 남편이라는 분이?"], ["It is the talk of all Paris.”", "파리가 온통 그 이야긴데요.\""]], [["“And you, count, have made this match?”", "\"그럼 백작님, 이 혼사를 주선하셨습니까?\""], ["asked Beauchamp.", "보샹이 물었다."]], [["“I? Silence, purveyor of gossip, do not spread that report.", "\"제가요? 조용히 하세요, 소문 장수 양반."], ["I make a match?", "그런 말은 퍼뜨리지 마세요."], ["No, you do not know me; I have done all in my power to oppose it.”", "제가 혼사를 주선한다고요? 아니요, 당신은 저를 모릅니다. 저는 그걸 막으려고 할 수 있는 모든 일을 다 했어요.\""]], [["“Ah, I understand,”", "\"아, 알겠습니다.\""], ["said Beauchamp, “on our friend Albert’s account.”", "보샹이 말했다. \"우리 친구 알베르를 위해서로군요.\""]], [["“On my account?”", "\"저를 위해서라고요?\" 젊은이가 말했다."], ["said the young man; “oh, no, indeed, the count will do me the justice to assert that I have, on the contrary, always entreated him to break off my engagement, and happily it is ended.", "\"오, 천만에요. 백작님께서 공정하게 증언해 주실 겁니다. 오히려 제가 늘 백작님께 제 약혼을 깨 달라고 간청했고, 다행히 끝이 났지요."], ["The count pretends I have not him to thank;—so be it—I will erect an altar _Deo ignoto_.”", "백작님은 그게 당신 덕은 아니라고 하시지만, 그렇다면 좋습니다, 저는 '알지 못하는 신께' 제단을 쌓겠습니다.\""]], [["“Listen,”", "* 알지 못하는 신께(Deo ignoto): 사도행전에 나오는 아테네의 제단 문구로, 누구 덕인지 모르나 감사를 바친다는 뜻—옮긴이. \"들어 보세요.\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["said Monte Cristo; “I have had little to do with it, for I am at variance both with the father-in-law and the young man; there is only Mademoiselle Eugénie, who appears but little charmed with the thoughts of matrimony, and who, seeing how little I was disposed to persuade her to renounce her dear liberty, retains any affection for me.”", "\"저는 이 일에 거의 관여하지 않았습니다. 장인 될 사람과도, 그 젊은이와도 사이가 틀어졌으니까요. 오직 외제니 양만이, 결혼이라는 생각에 별로 마음이 끌리지 않는 듯한데, 제가 그녀에게 소중한 자유를 버리라고 설득할 마음이 조금도 없는 걸 보고는 그나마 제게 약간의 호의를 품고 있지요.\""]], [["“And do you say this wedding is at hand?”", "\"그런데 이 결혼이 임박했다고 하셨습니까?\""]], [["“Oh, yes, in spite of all I could say.", "\"오, 그렇습니다, 제가 무슨 말을 해도 소용없이요."], ["I do not know the young man; he is said to be of good family and rich, but I never trust to vague assertions.", "저는 그 젊은이를 모릅니다. 가문도 좋고 재산도 많다고들 하지만, 저는 막연한 주장은 결코 믿지 않습니다."], ["I have warned M. Danglars of it till I am tired, but he is fascinated with his Luccanese.", "당글라르 씨에게 지칠 때까지 경고했지만, 그는 자기 그 루카 사람한테 홀딱 빠져 있어요."], ["I have even informed him of a circumstance I consider very serious; the young man was either charmed by his nurse, stolen by gypsies, or lost by his tutor, I scarcely know which.", "제가 아주 심각하게 여기는 사정까지 알려 주었지요. 그 젊은이는 유모에게 홀렸거나, 집시들에게 납치됐거나, 가정교사가 잃어버렸거나, 그중 무엇인지 저도 잘 모릅니다."], ["But I do know his father lost sight of him for more than ten years; what he did during these ten years, God only knows.", "하지만 그 아버지가 십 년 넘게 그를 보지 못했다는 것만은 압니다. 그 십 년 동안 그가 무슨 짓을 했는지는 하느님만 아실 일이지요."], ["Well, all that was useless.", "그런데도 다 소용없었습니다."], ["They have commissioned me to write to the major to demand papers, and here they are.", "그들은 제게 그 소령에게 편지를 써서 서류를 요구하라고 맡겼고, 여기 그것들이 있습니다."], ["I send them, but like Pilate—washing my hands.”", "저는 보내기는 합니다만, 빌라도처럼 손을 씻는 심정으로요.\""]], [["“And what does Mademoiselle d’Armilly say to you for robbing her of her pupil?”", "\"그런데 다르밀리 양은 제자를 빼앗긴 일을 두고 백작님께 뭐라고 합니까?\""]], [["“Oh, well, I don’t know; but I understand that she is going to Italy.", "\"오, 글쎄요, 모르겠습니다. 다만 그녀가 이탈리아로 갈 거라는 건 압니다."], ["Madame Danglars asked me for letters of recommendation for the _impresari_; I gave her a few lines for the director of the Valle Theatre, who is under some obligation to me.", "당글라르 부인이 제게 흥행주들에게 보낼 추천장을 부탁하더군요. 제게 신세를 진 발레 극장 지배인에게 보내는 몇 줄을 적어 주었지요. 그런데 무슨 일입니까, 알베르?"], ["But what is the matter, Albert?", "안색이 어두워 보여요."], ["you look dull; are you, after all, unconsciously in love with Mademoiselle Eugénie?”", "설마, 자기도 모르게 외제니 양을 사랑하고 있는 건 아니겠지요?\""]], [["“I am not aware of it,”", "\"그건 저도 모르겠습니다.\""], ["said Albert, smiling sorrowfully.", "알베르가 서글프게 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["Beauchamp turned to look at some paintings.", "보샹은 몇몇 그림을 보러 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["continued Monte Cristo, “you are not in your usual spirits?”", "몬테크리스토가 말을 이었다. \"평소 같은 기색이 아니로군요?\""]], [["“I have a dreadful headache,”", "\"머리가 끔찍하게 아픕니다.\""], ["said Albert.", "알베르가 말했다."]], [["“Well, my dear viscount,”", "\"그렇다면, 자작,\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “I have an infallible remedy to propose to you.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"내가 당신에게 권할 확실한 처방이 하나 있습니다.\""]], [["“What is that?”", "\"그게 뭡니까?\""], ["asked the young man.", "젊은이가 물었다."]], [["“Indeed?”", "\"그래요?\""], ["said Albert.", "알베르가 말했다."]], [["“Yes; and as I am just now excessively annoyed, I shall go from home.", "\"정말요?\" 알베르가 말했다. \"그래요. 마침 나도 지금 몹시 짜증스러운 일이 있어서 집을 떠나려던 참입니다."], ["Shall we go together?”", "함께 가시겠어요?\""]], [["“You annoyed, count?”", "\"짜증스러운 일이라니요, 백작님?\""], ["said Beauchamp; “and by what?”", "보샹이 말했다. \"무엇 때문에요?\""]], [["“Ah, you think very lightly of it; I should like to see you with a brief preparing in your house.”", "\"아, 당신은 그걸 아주 가볍게 여기시는군요. 당신 집을 겨냥해 기소장이 준비되고 있다면 어떤 기분일지 보고 싶군요.\""]], [["“What brief?”", "\"무슨 기소장요?\""]], [["“The one M. de Villefort is preparing against my amiable assassin—some brigand escaped from the gallows apparently.”", "\"빌포르 씨가 내 살가운 암살자를 상대로 준비 중인 그것 말입니다. 보아하니 교수대에서 도망친 어떤 산적인 모양이지요.\""]], [["“True,”", "\"맞아요.\""], ["said Beauchamp; “I saw it in the paper.", "보샹이 말했다. \"신문에서 봤습니다."], ["Who is this Caderousse?”", "그 카드루스라는 자는 누굽니까?\""]], [["“Some Provençal, it appears. M.", "\"보아하니 프로방스 사람인 듯합니다."], ["de Villefort heard of him at Marseilles, and M. Danglars recollects having seen him.", "빌포르 씨는 마르세유에서 그자에 대해 들었고, 당글라르 씨는 그를 본 적이 있다고 기억하더군요."], ["Consequently, the procureur is very active in the affair, and the prefect of police very much interested; and, thanks to that interest, for which I am very grateful, they send me all the robbers of Paris and the neighborhood, under pretence of their being Caderousse’s murderers, so that in three months, if this continues, every robber and assassin in France will have the plan of my house at his fingers’ ends.", "그래서 검사가 이 사건에 무척 적극적이고, 경찰청장도 큰 관심을 가지고 있지요. 그리고 그 관심 덕분에 — 정말 고맙기 그지없는 일이지만 — 그들은 파리와 인근의 도둑이란 도둑은 죄다 카드루스의 살인범이라는 구실로 내게 보내고 있습니다. 그래서 이대로 가면 석 달 안에 프랑스의 도둑과 암살자란 자들은 모조리 내 집 구조를 손금 보듯 훤히 알게 될 겁니다."], ["I am resolved to desert them and go to some remote corner of the earth, and shall be happy if you will accompany me, viscount.”", "나는 그들을 버리고 땅 끝 외딴 구석으로 가기로 결심했습니다. 자작, 당신이 함께 가 준다면 더없이 기쁘겠어요.\""]], [["“Willingly.”", "\"기꺼이 가겠습니다.\""]], [["“Then it is settled?”", "\"그럼 정해진 거지요?\""]], [["“Yes, but where?”", "\"네, 그런데 어디로요?\""]], [["“I have told you, where the air is pure, where every sound soothes, where one is sure to be humbled, however proud may be his nature.", "\"내가 말했잖아요, 공기가 맑고, 온갖 소리가 마음을 달래 주고, 천성이 아무리 오만한 사람이라도 반드시 겸허해지는 곳으로요."], ["I love that humiliation, I, who am master of the universe, as was Augustus.”", "나는 그 겸허함을 사랑합니다, 아우구스투스가 그랬듯 우주의 주인인 이 몸이 말이지요.\""]], [["“But where are you really going?”", "\"하지만 정말로 어디로 가시는 겁니까?\""]], [["“To sea, viscount; you know I am a sailor.", "\"바다로요, 자작. 알다시피 나는 뱃사람입니다. 갓난아기 때 늙은 대양의 품에서, 아름다운 암피트리테의 가슴 위에서 흔들리며 자랐지요."], ["I was rocked when an infant in the arms of old Ocean, and on the bosom of the beautiful Amphitrite; I have sported with the green mantle of the one and the azure robe of the other; I love the sea as a mistress, and pine if I do not often see her.”", "한쪽의 초록 망토와 다른 쪽의 푸른 옷자락을 가지고 놀았습니다. 나는 바다를 연인처럼 사랑해서, 자주 보지 못하면 애가 탑니다.\" * 암피트리테: 그리스 신화의 바다의 여신—옮긴이."]], [["“Let us go, count.”", "\"갑시다, 백작님.\""]], [["“To sea?”", "\"바다로요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“You accept my proposal?” “I do.”", "\"내 제안을 받아들이는 겁니까?\""]], [["“Well, viscount, there will be in my courtyard this evening a good travelling britzka, with four post-horses, in which one may rest as in a bed. M.", "\"받아들이지요.\" \"좋습니다, 자작. 오늘 저녁 내 안마당에 역마 네 필을 맨 좋은 여행용 브리츠카가 대기할 겁니다."], ["Beauchamp, it holds four very well, will you accompany us?”", "침대처럼 편히 쉴 수 있지요. 보샹 씨, 넷이 타도 넉넉합니다, 같이 가시겠어요?\""]], [["“Thank you, I have just returned from sea.”", "\"고맙지만, 나는 막 바다에서 돌아온 참입니다.\""]], [["“What? you have been to sea?”", "\"뭐라고요? 바다에 다녀오셨다고요?\""]], [["“Yes; I have just made a little excursion to the Borromean Islands18.”", "\"네. 보로메오 제도로 작은 여행을 막 다녀왔지요.\""]], [["“What of that? come with us,” said Albert.", "* 보로메오 제도: 이탈리아 마조레 호수의 섬들—옮긴이."]], [["“No, dear Morcerf; you know I only refuse when the thing is impossible.", "\"그게 무슨 상관입니까? 우리와 같이 가시지요.\" 알베르가 말했다."], ["Besides, it is important,”", "\"아니요, 모르세르. 알다시피 나는 도저히 불가능할 때만 거절합니다."], ["added he in a low tone, “that I should remain in Paris just now to watch the paper.”", "게다가 중요한 일이 있어요.\" 그가 낮은 목소리로 덧붙였다. \"지금은 파리에 남아서 신문을 지켜봐야 합니다.\""]], [["“Ah, you are a good and an excellent friend,”", "\"아, 당신은 좋고 훌륭한 친구로군요.\""], ["said Albert; “yes, you are right; watch, watch, Beauchamp, and try to discover the enemy who made this disclosure.”", "알베르가 말했다. \"그래요, 당신 말이 옳습니다. 지켜봐 주세요, 보샹, 잘 지켜봐서 이 폭로를 한 적이 누군지 알아내 주세요.\""]], [["Albert and Beauchamp parted, the last pressure of their hands expressing what their tongues could not before a stranger.", "알베르와 보샹은 헤어졌다. 마지막으로 맞잡은 손은 낯선 사람 앞에서 혀로는 미처 하지 못한 말을 대신 전하고 있었다."]], [["“Beauchamp is a worthy fellow,”", "\"보샹은 괜찮은 친구로군요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, when the journalist was gone; “is he not, Albert?”", "기자가 떠나자 몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그렇지 않습니까, 알베르?\""]], [["“Yes, and a sincere friend; I love him devotedly.", "\"네, 진실한 친구지요. 진심으로 그를 사랑합니다."], ["But now we are alone,—although it is immaterial to me,—where are we going?”", "그런데 이제 우리 둘뿐이니 — 저야 상관없습니다만 — 어디로 가는 겁니까?\""]], [["“Into Normandy, if you like.”", "\"원한다면 노르망디로 가지요.\""]], [["“Delightful; shall we be quite retired?", "\"좋습니다. 아주 한적할까요?"], ["have no society, no neighbors?”", "어울릴 사람도, 이웃도 없을까요?\""]], [["“Our companions will be riding-horses, dogs to hunt with, and a fishing-boat.”", "\"우리 동무는 승마용 말들과 사냥개들, 그리고 고깃배 한 척이 될 겁니다.\""]], [["“Exactly what I wish for; I will apprise my mother of my intention, and return to you.”", "\"바로 제가 바라던 바입니다. 어머니께 제 뜻을 알리고 다시 오겠습니다.\""]], [["“But shall you be allowed to go into Normandy?”", "\"한데 노르망디로 가도록 허락받을 수 있겠어요?\""]], [["“I may go where I please.”", "\"저는 가고 싶은 곳 어디든 갈 수 있습니다.\""]], [["“Yes, I am aware you may go alone, since I once met you in Italy—but to accompany the mysterious Monte Cristo?”", "\"그래요, 혼자라면 갈 수 있다는 건 압니다, 언젠가 이탈리아에서 당신을 만났으니까요. 하지만 이 수수께끼 같은 몬테크리스토와 동행하는 거라면요?\""]], [["“You forget, count, that I have often told you of the deep interest my mother takes in you.”", "\"잊으셨군요, 백작님. 제 어머니가 당신에게 깊은 관심을 두고 계신다고 제가 자주 말씀드렸잖아요.\""]], [["“‘Woman is fickle.’ said Francis I.; ‘woman is like a wave of the sea,’ said Shakespeare; both the great king and the great poet ought to have known woman’s nature well.”", "\"'여자는 변덕스럽다'고 프랑수아 1세는 말했고, '여자는 바다의 물결 같다'고 셰익스피어는 말했지요. 그 위대한 왕도, 그 위대한 시인도 여자의 본성을 잘 알았을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Woman’s, yes; my mother is not woman, but _a_ woman.”", "\"여자의 본성이라면 그렇지요. 하지만 제 어머니는 그런 '여자'가 아니라, '한 사람의 여인'입니다.\""]], [["“As I am only a humble foreigner, you must pardon me if I do not understand all the subtle refinements of your language.”", "\"나는 한낱 보잘것없는 외국인일 뿐이니, 당신 말의 그 미묘한 뉘앙스를 다 알아듣지 못해도 용서해 주세요.\""]], [["“What I mean to say is, that my mother is not quick to give her confidence, but when she does she never changes.”", "\"제 말은, 어머니는 쉽사리 신뢰를 주지 않으시지만, 한번 주면 결코 변하지 않으신다는 뜻입니다.\""]], [["“Ah, yes, indeed,”", "\"아, 그렇군요, 정말로.\""], ["said Monte Cristo with a sigh; “and do you think she is in the least interested in me?”", "몬테크리스토가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"그래서 어머니께서 나에게 조금이라도 관심을 두신다고 생각합니까?\""]], [["“I repeat it, you must really be a very strange and superior man, for my mother is so absorbed by the interest you have excited, that when I am with her she speaks of no one else.”", "\"다시 말씀드리지만, 당신은 정말 무척 기이하고 비범한 분임에 틀림없어요. 어머니는 당신이 불러일으킨 관심에 어찌나 사로잡혀 계신지, 제가 곁에 있으면 다른 누구의 이야기도 하지 않으십니다.\""]], [["“And does she try to make you dislike me?”", "\"그럼 어머니께서 당신이 나를 싫어하도록 만들려 하시던가요?\""]], [["“On the contrary, she often says, ‘Morcerf, I believe the count has a noble nature; try to gain his esteem.’”", "\"오히려 그 반대입니다. 자주 말씀하시지요. '모르세르, 내가 보기엔 백작은 고결한 천성을 지닌 분이다. 그분의 존경을 얻도록 애써 보아라.'\""]], [["“Indeed?”", "\"정말요?\""], ["said Monte Cristo, sighing.", "몬테크리스토가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."]], [["“You see, then,”", "\"그러니 보십시오.\""], ["said Albert, “that instead of opposing, she will encourage me.”", "알베르가 말했다. \"어머니는 저를 말리기는커녕 오히려 부추기실 겁니다.\""]], [["“Adieu, then, until five o’clock; be punctual, and we shall arrive at twelve or one.”", "\"그럼 다섯 시까지 작별입시다. 정확히 와요, 그래야 열두 시나 한 시에는 도착할 테니까요.\""]], [["“At Tréport?”", "\"트레포르에요?\""]], [["“Yes; or in the neighborhood.”", "\"네, 아니면 그 근방에요.\""]], [["“But can we travel forty-eight leagues in eight hours?”", "\"하지만 여덟 시간에 사십팔 리외를 갈 수 있을까요?\""]], [["“Easily,”", "\"쉽지요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“You are certainly a prodigy; you will soon not only surpass the railway, which would not be very difficult in France, but even the telegraph.”", "\"당신은 정말 경이로운 분이로군요. 머지않아 철도를 앞지르는 건 물론이고 — 프랑스에서야 그리 어려운 일도 아니겠지만 — 전신까지 앞지르겠어요.\""]], [["“But, viscount, since we cannot perform the journey in less than seven or eight hours, do not keep me waiting.”", "\"하지만 자작, 그 여정을 일고여덟 시간 안에는 마칠 수 없으니, 나를 기다리게 하지는 마세요.\""]], [["“Do not fear, I have little to prepare.”", "\"걱정 마세요, 준비할 게 별로 없으니까요.\""]], [["Monte Cristo smiled as he nodded to Albert, then remained a moment absorbed in deep meditation.", "몬테크리스토는 알베르에게 고개를 끄덕이며 미소 지었고, 그러고는 잠시 깊은 생각에 잠겨 있었다."], ["But passing his hand across his forehead as if to dispel his reverie, he rang the bell twice and Bertuccio entered.", "그러나 몽상을 떨쳐 내려는 듯 이마를 손으로 한번 쓸더니, 종을 두 번 울렸고 베르투초가 들어왔다."]], [["“Bertuccio,”", "\"베르투초,\""], ["said he, “I intend going this evening to Normandy, instead of tomorrow or the next day.", "그가 말했다. \"내일이나 모레가 아니라 오늘 저녁 노르망디로 떠날 생각이네."], ["You will have sufficient time before five o’clock; despatch a messenger to apprise the grooms at the first station. M.", "다섯 시 전까지면 시간은 넉넉할 거야. 전령을 보내 첫 번째 역참의 마부들에게 알리게."], ["de Morcerf will accompany me.”", "모르세르 씨가 나와 동행할 걸세.\""]], [["Bertuccio obeyed and despatched a courier to Pontoise to say the travelling-carriage would arrive at six o’clock.", "베르투초는 명령에 따라, 여행용 마차가 여섯 시에 도착할 거라고 알리려 퐁투아즈로 급사를 보냈다."], ["From Pontoise another express was sent to the next stage, and in six hours all the horses stationed on the road were ready.", "퐁투아즈에서 다시 다음 역참으로 급송이 띄워졌고, 여섯 시간 만에 도로변에 배치된 말들이 모두 준비를 마쳤다."]], [["Before his departure, the count went to Haydée’s apartments, told her his intention, and resigned everything to her care.", "떠나기 전에 백작은 하이데의 거처로 가서 자신의 계획을 알리고, 모든 일을 그녀의 손에 맡겼다."]], [["Albert was punctual. The journey soon became interesting from its rapidity, of which Morcerf had formed no previous idea.", "알베르는 시간을 정확히 지켰다. 여정은 그 빠르기 덕분에 곧 흥미진진해졌는데, 모르세르는 그런 속도를 미처 상상해 본 적이 없었다."]], [["“Truly,”", "\"정말이지,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["said Monte Cristo, “with your post-horses going at the rate of two leagues an hour, and that absurd law that one traveller shall not pass another without permission, so that an invalid or ill-tempered traveller may detain those who are well and active, it is impossible to move; I escape this annoyance by travelling with my own postilion and horses; do I not, Ali?”", "\"역마는 한 시간에 고작 두 리외를 가는 데다, 한 여행자가 허락 없이 다른 여행자를 앞지르지 못한다는 그 터무니없는 법까지 있어서, 병자나 성마른 여행자 하나가 멀쩡하고 활동적인 사람들을 붙들어 둘 수 있으니, 도무지 나아갈 수가 없지요. 나는 내 마부와 내 말을 데리고 다녀서 이 골칫거리를 피합니다. 그렇지 않나, 알리?\""]], [["The count put his head out of the window and whistled, and the horses appeared to fly.", "백작이 창밖으로 고개를 내밀고 휘파람을 불자, 말들이 날아오르는 듯했다."], ["The carriage rolled with a thundering noise over the pavement, and everyone turned to notice the dazzling meteor.", "마차는 천둥 같은 소리를 내며 포석 위를 굴렀고, 모두가 그 눈부신 유성을 돌아보았다."], ["Ali, smiling, repeated the sound, grasped the reins with a firm hand, and spurred his horses, whose beautiful manes floated in the breeze.", "알리는 미소를 지으며 그 휘파람 소리를 따라 내더니, 단단한 손으로 고삐를 움켜쥐고 말들에 박차를 가했고, 말들의 아름다운 갈기가 바람에 나부꼈다."], ["This child of the desert was in his element, and with his black face and sparkling eyes appeared, in the cloud of dust he raised, like the genius of the simoom and the god of the hurricane.", "이 사막의 아들은 제 세상을 만난 듯했고, 검은 얼굴과 번득이는 눈으로, 그가 일으킨 먼지구름 속에서 마치 사막 열풍의 정령이자 폭풍의 신처럼 보였다."]], [["“I never knew till now the delight of speed,”", "\"지금껏 속도의 즐거움을 미처 몰랐습니다.\""], ["said Morcerf, and the last cloud disappeared from his brow; “but where the devil do you get such horses?", "모르세르가 말했고, 그의 이마에서 마지막 그늘이 걷혔다. \"그런데 도대체 이런 말들을 어디서 구하십니까?"], ["Are they made to order?”", "주문 제작이라도 하는 겁니까?\""]], [["“Precisely,”", "\"바로 그렇지요.\""], ["said the count; “six years since I bought a horse in Hungary remarkable for its swiftness.", "백작이 말했다. \"육 년 전 헝가리에서 빠르기로 유명한 말 한 필을 샀습니다."], ["The thirty-two that we shall use tonight are its progeny; they are all entirely black, with the exception of a star upon the forehead.”", "오늘 밤 우리가 쓸 서른두 필이 모두 그 말의 자손입니다. 이마에 별 무늬 하나가 있는 것만 빼면 전부 완전히 검지요.\""]], [["“That is perfectly admirable; but what do you do, count, with all these horses?”", "\"정말 감탄스럽군요. 그런데 백작님, 이 많은 말들로 무얼 하십니까?\""]], [["“You see, I travel with them.”", "\"보다시피, 이 말들로 여행을 하지요.\""]], [["“But you are not always travelling.”", "\"하지만 늘 여행을 하시는 건 아니잖아요.\""]], [["“When I no longer require them, Bertuccio will sell them, and he expects to realize thirty or forty thousand francs by the sale.”", "\"더는 필요 없어지면 베르투초가 팔 겁니다. 그는 그 판매로 삼사만 프랑은 챙길 셈이지요.\""]], [["“But no monarch in Europe will be wealthy enough to purchase them.”", "\"하지만 유럽의 어떤 군주도 그걸 살 만큼 부유하지는 못할 텐데요.\""]], [["“Then he will sell them to some Eastern vizier, who will empty his coffers to purchase them, and refill them by applying the bastinado to his subjects.”", "\"그러면 어느 동방의 재상에게 팔 겁니다. 그자는 곳간을 비워 가며 사들이고, 백성들에게 곤장을 쳐서 다시 곳간을 채우겠지요.\" * 곤장(bastinado): 발바닥을 매질하던 동방의 형벌—옮긴이."]], [["“Count, may I suggest one idea to you?”", "\"백작님, 한 가지 생각을 말씀드려도 될까요?\""]], [["“Certainly.”", "\"물론이지요.\""]], [["“It is that, next to you, Bertuccio must be the richest gentleman in Europe.”", "\"당신 다음으로는 베르투초가 유럽에서 가장 부유한 신사일 게 틀림없다는 겁니다.\""]], [["“You are mistaken, viscount; I believe he has not a franc in his possession.”", "\"틀렸습니다, 자작. 내 생각엔 그에게는 단돈 한 프랑도 없을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Then he must be a wonder. My dear count, if you tell me many more marvellous things, I warn you I shall not believe them.”", "\"그렇다면 그는 정말 신기한 사람이로군요. 백작님, 이렇게 놀라운 이야기를 자꾸 들려주시면, 미리 말씀드리지만 저는 더 이상 믿지 않을 겁니다.\""]], [["“I countenance nothing that is marvellous, M. Albert.", "\"나는 놀라운 일이라곤 조금도 부추기지 않습니다, 알베르 씨."], ["Tell me, why does a steward rob his master?”", "말해 보세요, 집사가 왜 주인의 것을 훔칠까요?\""]], [["“Because, I suppose, it is his nature to do so, for the love of robbing.”", "\"제 생각엔, 훔치는 게 본성이라서겠지요, 훔치는 게 좋아서요.\""]], [["“You are mistaken; it is because he has a wife and family, and ambitious desires for himself and them.", "\"틀렸습니다. 아내와 가족이 있고, 자신과 그들을 위한 야심 찬 욕망이 있기 때문이지요."], ["Also because he is not sure of always retaining his situation, and wishes to provide for the future. Now, M.", "또 늘 그 자리를 지킨다는 보장이 없으니 앞날에 대비하고 싶어서이고요. 그런데 베르투초는 이 세상에 홀로입니다."], ["Bertuccio is alone in the world; he uses my property without accounting for the use he makes of it; he is sure never to leave my service.”", "그는 내 재산을 쓰면서도 그 씀씀이를 일일이 보고하지 않고, 결코 내 곁을 떠나지 않으리라 확신하지요.\""]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜요?\""]], [["“Because I should never get a better.”", "\"내가 더 나은 사람을 결코 얻지 못할 테니까요.\""]], [["“Probabilities are deceptive.”", "\"개연성이란 사람을 속이는 법인데요.\""]], [["“But I deal in certainties; he is the best servant over whom one has the power of life and death.”", "\"하지만 나는 확실한 것만 다룹니다. 그는 생사여탈권을 쥔 상대로서는 가장 훌륭한 하인이지요.\""]], [["“Do you possess that right over Bertuccio?”", "\"베르투초에게 그런 권리를 가지고 계십니까?\" \"그렇습니다.\""]], [["“Yes.” There are words which close a conversation with an iron door; such was the count’s “yes.”", "쇠문처럼 대화를 단숨에 닫아 버리는 말들이 있는데, 백작의 그 \"그렇습니다\"가 바로 그런 말이었다."]], [["The whole journey was performed with equal rapidity; the thirty-two horses, dispersed over seven stages, brought them to their destination in eight hours.", "여정 내내 한결같은 빠르기로 달렸다. 일곱 역참에 흩어져 있던 서른두 필의 말이 여덟 시간 만에 그들을 목적지로 데려다주었다."], ["At midnight they arrived at the gate of a beautiful park.", "자정에 그들은 아름다운 정원의 문에 닿았다."], ["The porter was in attendance; he had been apprised by the groom of the last stage of the count’s approach.", "문지기가 대기하고 있었는데, 마지막 역참의 마부에게서 백작이 다가온다는 기별을 받은 터였다."], ["At half past two in the morning Morcerf was conducted to his apartments, where a bath and supper were prepared.", "새벽 두 시 반에 모르세르는 자기 방으로 안내되었고, 그곳에는 목욕물과 야식이 마련되어 있었다."], ["The servant who had travelled at the back of the carriage waited on him; Baptistin, who rode in front, attended the count.", "마차 뒤편에 타고 온 하인이 그의 시중을 들었고, 앞쪽에 탔던 바티스탱은 백작의 시중을 들었다."]], [["Albert bathed, took his supper, and went to bed.", "알베르는 목욕을 하고 야식을 든 뒤 잠자리에 들었다."], ["All night he was lulled by the melancholy noise of the surf.", "밤새 그는 구슬픈 파도 소리에 잠겨 잠들었다."], ["On rising, he went to his window, which opened on a terrace, having the sea in front, and at the back a pretty park bounded by a small forest.", "일어나서 그는 창가로 갔는데, 창은 앞으로 바다를 면하고 뒤로는 작은 숲으로 둘러싸인 아담한 정원을 낀 테라스로 열려 있었다."]], [["In a creek lay a little sloop, with a narrow keel and high masts, bearing on its flag the Monte Cristo arms which were a mountain _or_, on a sea _azure_, with a cross _gules_ in chief which might be an allusion to his name that recalled Calvary, the mount rendered by our Lord’s passion more precious than gold, and to the degrading cross which his blood had rendered holy; or it might be some personal remembrance of suffering and regeneration buried in the night of this mysterious personage’s past life.", "작은 후미에는 좁은 용골과 높은 돛대를 단 작은 슬루프 한 척이 떠 있었고, 그 깃발에는 몬테크리스토의 문장이 새겨져 있었다. 푸른 바다 위에 솟은 황금빛 산, 그리고 그 위쪽에 붉은 십자가가 그려진 문장이었다. 이는 갈보리를 떠올리게 하는 그의 이름에 대한 암시일 수도 있었으니, 주님의 수난으로 황금보다 귀해진 그 산과, 그분의 피로 거룩해진 그 치욕의 십자가를 가리키는 것일 터였다. 아니면 이 수수께끼 같은 인물의 지나간 삶의 어둠 속에 묻힌, 고통과 거듭남에 얽힌 어떤 사사로운 기억일 수도 있었다."]], [["Around the schooner lay a number of small fishing-boats belonging to the fishermen of the neighboring village, like humble subjects awaiting orders from their queen.", "스쿠너 주위에는 이웃 마을 어부들의 작은 고깃배들이 여러 척 떠 있어, 마치 여왕의 분부를 기다리는 보잘것없는 신하들 같았다."], ["There, as in every spot where Monte Cristo stopped, if but for two days, luxury abounded and life went on with the utmost ease.", "그곳에서도, 몬테크리스토가 단 이틀이라도 머무는 곳이라면 어디서나 그렇듯, 호화로움이 넘쳐흘렀고 삶은 더할 나위 없이 편안하게 흘러갔다."]], [["Albert found in his anteroom two guns, with all the accoutrements for hunting; a lofty room on the ground floor containing all the ingenious instruments the English—eminent in piscatory pursuits, since they are patient and sluggish—have invented for fishing.", "알베르는 대기실에서 사냥 도구 일습을 갖춘 총 두 자루를 발견했다. 또 일 층의 높다란 방 하나에는, 인내심 많고 느긋한 까닭에 낚시에 발군인 영국인들이 고안해 낸 갖가지 정교한 낚시 도구가 다 갖춰져 있었다."], ["The day passed in pursuing those exercises in which Monte Cristo excelled.", "그날은 몬테크리스토가 뛰어난 솜씨를 보이는 그 운동들을 즐기며 지나갔다."], ["They killed a dozen pheasants in the park, as many trout in the stream, dined in a summer-house overlooking the ocean, and took tea in the library.", "그들은 정원에서 꿩 열두 마리를, 시내에서 송어 열두 마리를 잡았고, 바다가 내려다보이는 정자에서 점심을 들었으며, 서재에서 차를 마셨다."]], [["Towards the evening of the third day. Albert, completely exhausted with the exercise which invigorated Monte Cristo, was sleeping in an armchair near the window, while the count was designing with his architect the plan of a conservatory in his house, when the sound of a horse at full speed on the high road made Albert look up.", "사흘째 되는 날 저녁 무렵, 몬테크리스토에게는 기운을 북돋워 준 그 운동에 완전히 지친 알베르는 창가의 안락의자에서 잠들어 있었고, 백작은 건축가와 함께 집에 들일 온실의 설계도를 그리고 있었다. 그때 한길에서 전속력으로 달려오는 말발굽 소리에 알베르가 고개를 들었다."], ["He was disagreeably surprised to see his own valet de chambre, whom he had not brought, that he might not inconvenience Monte Cristo.", "그는 몬테크리스토에게 폐가 될까 봐 데려오지 않았던 자기 시종이 와 있는 것을 보고 불쾌하게 놀랐다."]], [["40188m\n“Florentin here!”", "\"플로랑탱이 여기 웬일이냐!\""], ["cried he, starting up; “is my mother ill?”", "그가 벌떡 일어나며 외쳤다. \"어머니가 편찮으신 거냐?\""], ["And he hastened to the door.", "그러고는 문 쪽으로 서둘러 갔다."], ["Monte Cristo watched and saw him approach the valet, who drew a small sealed parcel from his pocket, containing a newspaper and a letter.", "몬테크리스토는 그 모습을 지켜보았고, 알베르가 시종에게 다가가는 것을 보았다. 시종은 호주머니에서 봉인된 작은 꾸러미를 꺼냈는데, 그 안에는 신문 한 부와 편지 한 통이 들어 있었다."]], [["“From whom is this?”", "\"누구한테서 온 것이냐?\""], ["said he eagerly.", "그가 다급히 물었다."]], [["“From M. Beauchamp,”", "\"보샹 씨한테서요.\""], ["replied Florentin.", "플로랑탱이 대답했다."]], [["“Did he send you?”", "\"그가 너를 보낸 거냐?\""]], [["“Yes, sir; he sent for me to his house, gave me money for my journey, procured a horse, and made me promise not to stop till I had reached you, I have come in fifteen hours.”", "\"네, 도련님. 그분이 저를 자기 집으로 부르더니, 여비를 주고 말을 마련해 주시고는, 도련님께 닿기 전에는 절대 멈추지 말라고 약속하게 하셨습니다. 열다섯 시간 만에 달려왔습니다.\""]], [["Albert opened the letter with fear, uttered a shriek on reading the first line, and seized the paper.", "알베르는 두려운 마음으로 편지를 펼쳤다가, 첫 줄을 읽고는 비명을 지르며 신문을 움켜쥐었다."], ["His sight was dimmed, his legs sank under him, and he would have fallen had not Florentin supported him.", "눈앞이 흐려지고 다리가 휘청거려, 플로랑탱이 받쳐 주지 않았더라면 쓰러졌을 것이다."]], [["“Poor young man,”", "\"가엾은 젊은이.\""], ["said Monte Cristo in a low voice; “it is then true that the sin of the father shall fall on the children to the third and fourth generation.”", "몬테크리스토가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"그렇다면 아버지의 죄가 자식들에게 삼사 대에 이르도록 미친다는 것이 참말이로구나.\""]], [["Meanwhile Albert had revived, and, continuing to read, he threw back his head, saying:", "그사이 알베르는 정신을 차렸고, 계속 읽어 내려가다가 고개를 뒤로 젖히며 말했다."]], [["“Florentin, is your horse fit to return immediately?”", "\"플로랑탱, 네 말이 곧바로 돌아갈 만한 상태냐?\""]], [["“It is a poor, lame post-horse.”", "\"형편없는, 다리를 저는 역마입니다.\""]], [["“In what state was the house when you left?”", "\"네가 떠나올 때 집안 상황은 어땠느냐?\""]], [["“All was quiet, but on returning from M.", "\"다 조용했습니다."], ["Beauchamp’s, I found madame in tears; she had sent for me to know when you would return. I told her my orders from M.", "그런데 보샹 씨 댁에서 돌아오니 마님께서 눈물을 흘리고 계셨습니다. 도련님이 언제 돌아오시는지 알아보려고 저를 부르신 거였지요."], ["Beauchamp; she first extended her arms to prevent me, but after a moment’s reflection, ‘Yes, go, Florentin,’ said she, ‘and may he come quickly.’”", "보샹 씨에게서 받은 분부를 말씀드리니, 처음에는 막으시려고 두 팔을 뻗으셨다가, 잠시 생각하시더니 이렇게 말씀하셨습니다. '그래, 가거라, 플로랑탱. 부디 그 애가 어서 오기를.'\""]], [["“Yes, my mother,”", "\"네, 어머니,\""], ["said Albert, “I will return, and woe to the infamous wretch!", "알베르가 말했다. \"돌아가겠습니다, 그리고 그 파렴치한 놈에게 화 있으라!"], ["But first of all I must get there.”", "하지만 무엇보다 먼저 거기에 닿아야 한다.\""]], [["He went back to the room where he had left Monte Cristo.", "그는 몬테크리스토를 두고 나왔던 방으로 돌아갔다."], ["Five minutes had sufficed to make a complete transformation in his appearance.", "오 분 만에 그의 모습은 완전히 딴사람이 되어 있었다."], ["His voice had become rough and hoarse; his face was furrowed with wrinkles; his eyes burned under the blue-veined lids, and he tottered like a drunken man.", "목소리는 거칠고 쉬어 있었고, 얼굴에는 주름이 깊게 패었으며, 푸른 핏줄이 비치는 눈꺼풀 아래로 두 눈이 이글거렸고, 술 취한 사람처럼 비틀거렸다."]], [["“Count,”", "\"백작님,\""], ["said he, “I thank you for your hospitality, which I would gladly have enjoyed longer; but I must return to Paris.”", "그가 말했다. \"베풀어 주신 환대에 감사드립니다. 더 오래 누리고 싶었습니다만, 파리로 돌아가야겠습니다.\""]], [["“What has happened?”", "\"무슨 일이 있었습니까?\""]], [["“A great misfortune, more important to me than life.", "\"엄청난 불행이, 제겐 목숨보다 더 중대한 일이 생겼습니다."], ["Don’t question me, I beg of you, but lend me a horse.”", "부디 캐묻지 말아 주십시오. 다만 말 한 필만 빌려주십시오.\""]], [["“My stables are at your command, viscount; but you will kill yourself by riding on horseback.", "\"내 마구간은 당신 뜻대로 쓰세요, 자작. 하지만 말을 타고 가면 몸을 상할 겁니다."], ["Take a post-chaise or a carriage.”", "역마차나 마차를 타세요.\""]], [["“No, it would delay me, and I need the fatigue you warn me of; it will do me good.”", "\"아니요, 그건 시간이 더 걸립니다. 게다가 저는 백작님이 경고하시는 그 고단함이 필요합니다. 그게 오히려 제겐 약이 될 겁니다.\""]], [["Albert reeled as if he had been shot, and fell on a chair near the door.", "알베르는 총에라도 맞은 듯 휘청하더니 문가의 의자에 주저앉았다."], ["Monte Cristo did not see this second manifestation of physical exhaustion; he was at the window, calling:", "몬테크리스토는 이 두 번째 육체적 탈진의 기색을 보지 못했다. 그는 창가에서 외치고 있었다."]], [["“Ali, a horse for M. de Morcerf—quick!", "\"알리, 모르세르 씨에게 말 한 필을, 어서!"], ["he is in a hurry!”", "그가 급해!\""]], [["40190m\nThese words restored Albert; he darted from the room, followed by the count.", "이 말이 알베르를 다시 일으켰다. 그는 방에서 뛰쳐나갔고, 백작이 그 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["“Thank you!”", "\"고맙습니다!\""], ["cried he, throwing himself on his horse.", "그가 말 위로 몸을 던지며 외쳤다."]], [["“Return as soon as you can, Florentin.", "\"되도록 빨리 돌아오너라, 플로랑탱."], ["Must I use any password to procure a horse?”", "말을 구하는 데 무슨 암호라도 대야 합니까?\""]], [["“Only dismount; another will be immediately saddled.”", "\"내리기만 하면 됩니다. 곧바로 다른 말에 안장을 얹어 줄 겁니다.\""]], [["Albert hesitated a moment.", "알베르는 잠시 망설였다."], ["“You may think my departure strange and foolish,”", "\"제가 이렇게 떠나는 게 이상하고 어리석어 보이실지도 모릅니다.\""], ["said the young man; “you do not know how a paragraph in a newspaper may exasperate one. Read that,”", "젊은이가 말했다. \"신문의 한 단락이 사람을 얼마나 미치게 만들 수 있는지 백작님은 모르실 겁니다."], ["said he, “when I am gone, that you may not be witness of my anger.”", "제가 떠난 뒤에 그걸 읽어 보세요.\" 그가 말했다. \"제 분노를 목격하지 않으시도록요.\""]], [["While the count picked up the paper he put spurs to his horse, which leaped in astonishment at such an unusual stimulus, and shot away with the rapidity of an arrow.", "백작이 신문을 집어 드는 사이, 알베르는 말에 박차를 가했고, 말은 그런 낯선 자극에 놀라 펄쩍 뛰어오르더니 화살처럼 쏜살같이 내달렸다."], ["The count watched him with a feeling of compassion, and when he had completely disappeared, read as follows:", "백작은 연민의 감정으로 그를 지켜보았고, 그가 완전히 사라지자 다음과 같은 글을 읽었다."]], [["“The French officer in the service of Ali Pasha of Yanina alluded to three weeks since in _l’Impartial_, who not only surrendered the castle of Yanina, but sold his benefactor to the Turks, styled himself truly at that time Fernand, as our esteemed contemporary states; but he has since added to his Christian name a title of nobility and a family name.", "\"삼 주 전 앵파르시알지에서 언급된, 야니나의 알리 파샤 휘하에서 복무한 그 프랑스 장교는, 야니나 성채를 넘겨주었을 뿐 아니라 자신의 은인을 튀르크인들에게 팔아넘긴 자로, 우리 존경하는 동업지가 밝혔듯이 당시에는 분명 페르낭이라고 자칭했다. 그러나 그는 그 후 자신의 세례명에 귀족 칭호와 가문의 성을 덧붙였다."], ["He now calls himself the Count of Morcerf, and ranks among the peers.”", "그는 이제 자신을 모르세르 백작이라 칭하며, 귀족원 의원의 반열에 올라 있다.\""]], [["Thus the terrible secret, which Beauchamp had so generously destroyed, appeared again like an armed phantom; and another paper, deriving its information from some malicious source, had published two days after Albert’s departure for Normandy the few lines which had rendered the unfortunate young man almost crazy.", "이리하여 보샹이 그토록 너그럽게 묻어 주었던 그 끔찍한 비밀이 무장한 망령처럼 다시 나타났다. 그리고 어떤 악의에 찬 출처에서 정보를 얻은 다른 신문이, 알베르가 노르망디로 떠난 지 이틀 만에, 그 불운한 젊은이를 거의 미치게 만든 그 몇 줄을 실은 것이었다. 아침 여덟 시에 알베르는 보샹의 집 문 앞에 닿았다."]], [["# Chapter 86. The Trial", "# 제85장 여행"]], [["At eight o’clock in the morning Albert had arrived at Beauchamp’s door. The valet de chambre had received orders to usher him in at once.", "시종은 그를 곧바로 안으로 안내하라는 분부를 받아 둔 터였다."], ["Beauchamp was in his bath.", "보샹은 목욕 중이었다."]], [["“Here I am,” Albert said.", "\"왔네.\" 알베르가 말했다."]], [["“Well, my poor friend,”", "\"음, 가엾은 친구.\""], ["replied Beauchamp, “I expected you.”", "보샹이 대답했다. \"기다리고 있었네.\""]], [["“I need not say I think you are too faithful and too kind to have spoken of that painful circumstance.", "\"굳이 말할 필요도 없지만, 자네는 너무도 의리 있고 너무도 따뜻한 사람이라 그 고통스러운 일을 함부로 입에 올리지 않았겠지."], ["Your having sent for me is another proof of your affection.", "나를 부른 것 또한 자네의 우정을 보여 주는 또 하나의 증거일세."], ["So, without losing time, tell me, have you the slightest idea whence this terrible blow proceeds?”", "그러니 시간을 허비하지 말고 말해 주게. 이 끔찍한 일격이 어디서 비롯된 것인지, 조금이라도 짐작 가는 게 있나?\""]], [["“I think I have some clew.”", "\"단서를 좀 잡은 것 같네.\""]], [["“But first tell me all the particulars of this shameful plot.”", "\"하지만 먼저 이 수치스러운 음모의 자초지종을 모두 들려주게.\""]], [["Beauchamp proceeded to relate to the young man, who was overwhelmed with shame and grief, the following facts.", "보샹은 수치심과 비통함에 짓눌린 젊은이에게 다음과 같은 사실들을 들려주기 시작했다."], ["Two days previously, the article had appeared in another paper besides _ l’Impartial_, and, what was more serious, one that was well known as a government paper.", "이틀 전, 그 기사가 랭파르시알 외에 다른 신문에도 실렸는데, 더 심각한 것은 그것이 정부 기관지로 잘 알려진 신문이었다는 점이다."], ["Beauchamp was breakfasting when he read the paragraph.", "보샹은 아침 식사를 하다가 그 기사를 읽었다."], ["He sent immediately for a cabriolet, and hastened to the publisher’s office.", "그는 곧장 사람을 보내 이륜마차를 부르고 발행인의 사무실로 달려갔다."], ["Although professing diametrically opposite principles from those of the editor of the other paper, Beauchamp—as it sometimes, we may say often, happens—was his intimate friend.", "다른 신문 편집장과 정반대의 신조를 내세우면서도, 보샹은 — 흔히, 아니 사실 자주 있는 일이지만 — 그와 막역한 친구였다."], ["The editor was reading, with apparent delight, a leading article in the same paper on beet-sugar, probably a composition of his own.", "편집장은 사탕무 설탕에 관한 같은 신문의 사설을 흐뭇한 기색으로 읽고 있었는데, 아마도 자신이 쓴 글인 모양이었다."]], [["“Ah, _pardieu!_”", "\"아, 이런!\""], ["said Beauchamp, “with the paper in your hand, my friend, I need not tell you the cause of my visit.”", "보샹이 말했다. \"그 신문을 손에 들고 있으니, 친구, 내가 왜 찾아왔는지 굳이 말하지 않아도 되겠군.\""]], [["“Are you interested in the sugar question?”", "\"설탕 문제에 관심이 있나?\""], ["asked the editor of the ministerial paper.", "정부 기관지 편집장이 물었다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아닐세.\""], ["replied Beauchamp, “I have not considered the question; a totally different subject interests me.”", "보샹이 대답했다. \"그 문제는 생각해 본 적도 없네. 전혀 다른 주제가 궁금해서 왔지.\""]], [["“What is it?”", "\"무슨 일인데?\""]], [["“The article relative to Morcerf.”", "\"모르세르에 관한 기사 말일세.\""]], [["“Indeed? Is it not a curious affair?”", "\"아, 그래? 흥미로운 사건 아닌가?\""]], [["“So curious, that I think you are running a great risk of a prosecution for defamation of character.”", "\"어찌나 흥미로운지, 자네가 명예 훼손으로 고소당할 큰 위험을 무릅쓰고 있는 것 같아서 말이야.\""]], [["“Not at all; we have received with the information all the requisite proofs, and we are quite sure M.", "\"천만에. 정보와 함께 필요한 증거를 모두 받았고, 모르세르 백작이 우리를 상대로 목소리를 높이지 않으리라는 것도 확신하네."], ["de Morcerf will not raise his voice against us; besides, it is rendering a service to one’s country to denounce these wretched criminals who are unworthy of the honor bestowed on them.”", "게다가 그들에게 베풀어진 명예에 어울리지 않는 그 비열한 죄인들을 고발하는 것은 나라에 봉사하는 일이지.\""]], [["Beauchamp was thunderstruck.", "보샹은 벼락이라도 맞은 듯했다."]], [["“Who, then, has so correctly informed you?”", "\"그렇다면 누가 자네에게 그토록 정확한 정보를 준 건가?\" 그가 물었다."], ["asked he; “for my paper, which gave the first information on the subject, has been obliged to stop for want of proof; and yet we are more interested than you in exposing M. de Morcerf, as he is a peer of France, and we are of the opposition.”", "\"이 일에 관해 제일 먼저 기사를 낸 우리 신문조차 증거가 없어서 멈출 수밖에 없었네. 게다가 모르세르 백작은 프랑스 귀족원 의원이고 우리는 야당이니, 그를 폭로하는 데에는 자네들보다 우리가 더 큰 관심이 있는데 말일세.\""]], [["“Oh, that is very simple; we have not sought to scandalize. This news was brought to us.", "\"오, 그건 아주 간단하네. 우리가 추문을 캐려고 나선 게 아니야."], ["A man arrived yesterday from Yanina, bringing a formidable array of documents; and when we hesitated to publish the accusatory article, he told us it should be inserted in some other paper.”", "이 소식이 우리에게 굴러들어 온 거지. 어제 야니나에서 한 사내가 무시무시한 양의 서류를 들고 찾아왔네. 우리가 그 고발 기사를 싣기를 망설이자, 그자는 다른 신문에라도 실릴 거라고 하더군.\""]], [["Beauchamp understood that nothing remained but to submit, and left the office to despatch a courier to Morcerf.", "보샹은 이제 받아들이는 것 말고는 도리가 없음을 깨닫고, 모르세르에게 급사를 보내려고 사무실을 나섰다."], ["But he had been unable to send to Albert the following particulars, as the events had transpired after the messenger’s departure; namely, that the same day a great agitation was manifest in the House of Peers among the usually calm members of that dignified assembly.", "하지만 다음과 같은 자세한 내막은 알베르에게 미처 전하지 못했는데, 그 일들이 전령이 떠난 뒤에 벌어졌기 때문이다. 곧 같은 날, 평소 침착하던 귀족원의 위엄 있는 의원들 사이에 큰 동요가 일었다."], ["Everyone had arrived almost before the usual hour, and was conversing on the melancholy event which was to attract the attention of the public towards one of their most illustrious colleagues.", "모두가 평소보다 일찍 도착해, 자기네 가장 저명한 동료 가운데 한 사람에게 대중의 이목이 쏠리게 될 그 침울한 사건을 두고 이야기를 나누고 있었다."], ["Some were perusing the article, others making comments and recalling circumstances which substantiated the charges still more.", "어떤 이들은 기사를 정독하고, 어떤 이들은 논평을 하며 혐의를 한층 더 뒷받침할 만한 정황들을 들추어냈다."]], [["The Count of Morcerf was no favorite with his colleagues.", "모르세르 백작은 동료들 사이에서 호감을 사는 인물이 아니었다."], ["Like all upstarts, he had had recourse to a great deal of haughtiness to maintain his position.", "벼락출세한 자들이 다 그렇듯, 그는 자기 지위를 지키려고 숱한 오만함에 기대 왔다."], ["The true nobility laughed at him, the talented repelled him, and the honorable instinctively despised him.", "진짜 귀족들은 그를 비웃었고, 재능 있는 자들은 그를 멀리했으며, 명예로운 자들은 본능적으로 그를 경멸했다."], ["He was, in fact, in the unhappy position of the victim marked for sacrifice; the finger of God once pointed at him, everyone was prepared to raise the hue and cry.", "사실 그는 제물로 점찍힌 희생양의 불행한 처지에 놓여 있었다. 신의 손가락이 한번 그를 가리키자, 모두가 일제히 비난의 함성을 지를 채비를 하고 있었다."]], [["The Count of Morcerf alone was ignorant of the news.", "오직 모르세르 백작만이 이 소식을 모르고 있었다."], ["He did not take in the paper containing the defamatory article, and had passed the morning in writing letters and in trying a horse.", "그는 그 명예 훼손 기사가 실린 신문을 받아 보지 않았고, 오전 내내 편지를 쓰고 말 한 마리를 시승하며 보냈다."], ["He arrived at his usual hour, with a proud look and insolent demeanor; he alighted, passed through the corridors, and entered the house without observing the hesitation of the door-keepers or the coolness of his colleagues.", "그는 평소와 같은 시각에, 거만한 표정과 오만한 태도로 도착했다. 마차에서 내려 복도를 지나 의사당으로 들어서면서, 문지기들의 머뭇거림도, 동료들의 냉랭함도 알아채지 못했다."]], [["40194m\nBusiness had already been going on for half an hour when he entered.", "그가 들어섰을 때 회의는 이미 삼십 분째 진행 중이었다."], ["Everyone held the accusing paper, but, as usual, no one liked to take upon himself the responsibility of the attack.", "모두가 그 고발 기사를 손에 쥐고 있었으나, 으레 그렇듯 누구도 먼저 공격에 나서는 책임을 떠맡으려 하지 않았다."], ["At length an honorable peer, Morcerf’s acknowledged enemy, ascended the tribune with that solemnity which announced that the expected moment had arrived.", "마침내 한 명예로운 의원이, 모르세르의 공공연한 적수가, 기다리던 순간이 왔음을 알리는 그 엄숙한 태도로 연단에 올랐다."], ["There was an impressive silence; Morcerf alone knew not why such profound attention was given to an orator who was not always listened to with so much complacency.", "인상적인 침묵이 흘렀다. 평소 그리 흔쾌히 경청되지 않던 연사에게 어째서 이토록 깊은 주목이 쏠리는지, 오직 모르세르만이 영문을 몰랐다."]], [["The count did not notice the introduction, in which the speaker announced that his communication would be of that vital importance that it demanded the undivided attention of the House; but at the mention of Yanina and Colonel Fernand, he turned so frightfully pale that every member shuddered and fixed his eyes upon him.", "백작은 서두를 알아채지 못했다. 연사는 자기 발언이 의회의 온전한 주목을 요할 만큼 중대한 것이라고 예고했으나, 백작은 그것을 흘려들었다. 그러나 야니나와 페르낭 대령이라는 말이 나오자, 그는 어찌나 무섭게 창백해졌던지 의원 모두가 몸서리치며 그에게 눈길을 박았다."], ["Moral wounds have this peculiarity,—they may be hidden, but they never close; always painful, always ready to bleed when touched, they remain fresh and open in the heart.", "마음의 상처에는 이런 특성이 있다. 감출 수는 있어도 결코 아물지 않는다는 것. 늘 아프고, 건드리면 언제든 피 흘릴 채비가 되어 있으며, 가슴속에 생생하게 벌어진 채 남아 있다."]], [["The article having been read during the painful hush that followed, a universal shudder pervaded the assembly, and immediately the closest attention was given to the orator as he resumed his remarks.", "뒤이은 고통스러운 정적 속에서 기사가 낭독되자, 의회 전체에 일제히 전율이 번졌고, 곧이어 연사가 다시 발언을 이어 가자 가장 집중된 주목이 그에게 쏟아졌다. 그는 자신의 망설임과 이 사안의 어려움을 토로했다."], ["He stated his scruples and the difficulties of the case; it was the honor of M.", "그가 지키고자 하는 것은 모르세르 백작의 명예, 그리고 의회 전체의 명예라고 했다."], ["de Morcerf, and that of the whole House, he proposed to defend, by provoking a debate on personal questions, which are always such painful themes of discussion.", "그러기 위해 개인적 문제를 둘러싼 토론을 발의하는데, 그런 것은 늘 그토록 거북한 논의 주제라고 했다."], ["He concluded by calling for an investigation, which might dispose of the calumnious report before it had time to spread, and restore M. de Morcerf to the position he had long held in public opinion.", "그는 조사를 요구하며 발언을 마쳤다. 그 비방의 소문이 퍼질 틈을 주기 전에 처리하여, 모르세르 백작을 오래도록 누려 온 여론 속 자리로 되돌려 놓자는 것이었다."]], [["Morcerf was so completely overwhelmed by this great and unexpected calamity that he could scarcely stammer a few words as he looked around on the assembly.", "모르세르는 이 크고 뜻밖의 재앙에 완전히 압도되어, 의회를 둘러보며 겨우 몇 마디를 더듬거릴 뿐이었다."], ["This timidity, which might proceed from the astonishment of innocence as well as the shame of guilt, conciliated some in his favor; for men who are truly generous are always ready to compassionate when the misfortune of their enemy surpasses the limits of their hatred.", "이 머뭇거림은 결백함에서 오는 놀라움일 수도, 죄지은 자의 수치심일 수도 있었는데, 몇몇 사람의 마음을 그에게로 돌려놓았다. 진정으로 너그러운 사람이란, 적의 불행이 자신의 증오의 한계를 넘어설 때면 언제나 측은히 여길 준비가 되어 있는 법이니까."]], [["The president put it to the vote, and it was decided that the investigation should take place. The count was asked what time he required to prepare his defence.", "의장이 표결에 부쳤고, 조사를 진행하기로 결정되었다. 백작에게는 변론을 준비하는 데 얼마의 시간이 필요하냐고 물었다."], ["Morcerf’s courage had revived when he found himself alive after this horrible blow.", "이 끔찍한 일격을 맞고도 살아남았음을 깨닫자 모르세르의 용기가 되살아났다."]], [["“My lords,”", "\"의원 여러분.\""], ["answered he, “it is not by time I could repel the attack made on me by enemies unknown to me, and, doubtless, hidden in obscurity; it is immediately, and by a thunderbolt, that I must repel the flash of lightning which, for a moment, startled me.", "그가 대답했다. \"내게 알려지지 않은, 분명 어둠 속에 숨어 있을 적들이 가한 이 공격을, 나는 시간을 들여 물리치지 않겠습니다. 잠시나마 나를 흠칫하게 한 그 번갯불을, 나는 지금 당장, 벼락으로써 되받아치겠습니다."], ["Oh, that I could, instead of taking up this defence, shed my last drop of blood to prove to my noble colleagues that I am their equal in worth.”", "오, 이 변론에 나서는 대신 마지막 한 방울의 피까지 흘려, 내가 고귀한 동료들과 가치에 있어 대등한 사람임을 증명할 수만 있다면.\""]], [["These words made a favorable impression on behalf of the accused.", "이 말은 피고에게 유리한 인상을 남겼다."]], [["“I demand, then, that the examination shall take place as soon as possible, and I will furnish the house with all necessary information.”", "\"그러니 가능한 한 빨리 심리가 열리기를 요청합니다. 의회에 필요한 모든 정보를 제출하겠습니다.\""]], [["“What day do you fix?” asked the president.", "\"어느 날로 정하시겠습니까?\" 의장이 물었다."]], [["“Today I am at your service,”", "\"오늘이라도 응하겠습니다.\""], ["replied the count.", "백작이 대답했다."]], [["The president rang the bell.", "의장이 종을 울렸다."], ["“Does the House approve that the examination should take place today?” 40196m", "\"오늘 심리를 진행하는 데 의회는 찬성하십니까?\""]], [["“Yes,” was the unanimous answer.", "\"찬성이오.\" 만장일치의 대답이었다."]], [["A committee of twelve members was chosen to examine the proofs brought forward by Morcerf.", "모르세르가 제출하는 증거를 검토할 열두 명의 위원회가 선출되었다."], ["The investigation would begin at eight o’clock that evening in the committee-room, and if postponement were necessary, the proceedings would be resumed each evening at the same hour.", "조사는 그날 저녁 여덟 시에 위원회실에서 시작하고, 연기가 필요하면 매일 저녁 같은 시각에 절차를 다시 이어 가기로 했다."], ["Morcerf asked leave to retire; he had to collect the documents he had long been preparing against this storm, which his sagacity had foreseen.", "모르세르는 물러가게 해 달라고 청했다. 그의 통찰이 진작 내다본 이 폭풍에 맞서 오래도록 준비해 온 서류들을 챙겨야 했던 것이다."]], [["Beauchamp related to the young man all the facts we have just narrated; his story, however, had over ours all the advantage of the animation of living things over the coldness of dead things.", "보샹은 우리가 방금 들려준 사실들을 모두 젊은이에게 이야기했다. 다만 그의 이야기는, 죽은 것의 차가움에 견준 살아 있는 것의 생기를 모두 지니고 있어 우리 것보다 한결 나았다."]], [["Albert listened, trembling now with hope, then with anger, and then again with shame, for from Beauchamp’s confidence he knew his father was guilty, and he asked himself how, since he was guilty, he could prove his innocence.", "알베르는 귀를 기울였다. 어느 때는 희망에, 어느 때는 분노에, 또 어느 때는 수치심에 떨었다. 보샹의 확신에 찬 말투에서 아버지가 유죄임을 알았기에, 유죄인 이상 어떻게 무죄를 입증할 수 있단 말인가 자문했던 것이다."], ["Beauchamp hesitated to continue his narrative.", "보샹은 이야기를 계속하기를 망설였다."]], [["“What next?”", "\"그다음은?\""], ["asked Albert.", "알베르가 물었다."]], [["“What next?", "\"그다음?"], ["My friend, you impose a painful task on me.", "친구, 자네가 내게 괴로운 임무를 떠맡기는군."], ["Must you know all?”", "정말 다 알아야겠나?\""]], [["“Absolutely; and rather from your lips than another’s.”", "\"반드시. 그것도 다른 누구의 입보다 자네 입으로 듣고 싶네.\""]], [["“Muster up all your courage, then, for never have you required it more.”", "\"그렇다면 있는 용기를 다 끌어모으게. 지금만큼 그것이 필요한 적은 없었을 테니.\""]], [["Albert passed his hand over his forehead, as if to try his strength, as a man who is preparing to defend his life proves his shield and bends his sword.", "알베르는 마치 제 힘을 시험하려는 듯 이마를 손으로 쓸었다. 목숨을 지키려는 사람이 방패를 점검하고 칼을 휘어 보듯이."], ["He thought himself strong enough, for he mistook fever for energy.", "그는 스스로 충분히 강하다고 여겼는데, 열병을 기력으로 착각하고 있었던 것이다. \"계속하게.\""], ["“Go on,” said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“The evening arrived; all Paris was in expectation.", "\"저녁이 되었네. 온 파리가 기다리고 있었지."], ["Many said your father had only to show himself to crush the charge against him; many others said he would not appear; while some asserted that they had seen him start for Brussels; and others went to the police-office to inquire if he had taken out a passport.", "많은 이들은 자네 아버지가 모습을 드러내기만 하면 자신에 대한 혐의를 짓뭉갤 수 있을 거라 했고, 또 많은 이들은 그가 나타나지 않을 거라 했네. 어떤 이들은 그가 브뤼셀로 떠나는 걸 보았다고 단언했고, 또 어떤 이들은 그가 여권을 발급받았는지 경찰서로 알아보러 갔지."], ["I used all my influence with one of the committee, a young peer of my acquaintance, to get admission to one of the galleries.", "나는 위원회 위원 중 하나, 안면이 있는 젊은 귀족에게 온갖 영향력을 동원해 방청석 한 곳에 들어갈 자리를 얻었네."], ["He called for me at seven o’clock, and, before anyone had arrived, asked one of the door-keepers to place me in a box.", "그가 일곱 시에 나를 데리러 왔고, 아무도 도착하기 전에 문지기에게 부탁해 나를 한 칸막이 자리에 앉혀 주었네."], ["I was concealed by a column, and might witness the whole of the terrible scene which was about to take place.", "나는 기둥에 가려진 채, 곧 벌어질 그 끔찍한 장면을 처음부터 끝까지 지켜볼 수 있었지."], ["At eight o’clock all were in their places, and M. de Morcerf entered at the last stroke.", "여덟 시가 되자 모두가 제자리에 앉았고, 마지막 종소리에 맞춰 모르세르 백작이 들어왔네."], ["He held some papers in his hand; his countenance was calm, and his step firm, and he was dressed with great care in his military uniform, which was buttoned completely up to the chin.", "손에 서류 몇 장을 들고 있었지. 얼굴은 차분했고 걸음은 굳건했으며, 군복을 더없이 정성스레 차려입고 턱까지 단추를 빈틈없이 채우고 있었네."], ["His presence produced a good effect.", "그의 등장은 좋은 인상을 주었어."], ["The committee was made up of Liberals, several of whom came forward to shake hands with him.”", "위원회는 자유주의자들로 꾸려져 있었는데, 그중 몇몇은 앞으로 나와 그와 악수를 나누었네.\""]], [["Albert felt his heart bursting at these particulars, but gratitude mingled with his sorrow: he would gladly have embraced those who had given his father this proof of esteem at a moment when his honor was so powerfully attacked.", "알베르는 이런 자세한 이야기에 가슴이 터질 듯했으나, 슬픔에 감사의 마음이 뒤섞였다. 아버지의 명예가 그토록 거세게 공격받는 순간에 존경의 뜻을 표해 준 그 사람들을 기꺼이 끌어안고 싶었던 것이다."]], [["“At this moment one of the door-keepers brought in a letter for the president. ‘You are at liberty to speak, M.", "\"바로 그때 문지기 하나가 의장에게 편지 한 통을 가져왔네. '발언하셔도 좋습니다, 모르세르 백작.'"], ["de Morcerf,’ said the president, as he unsealed the letter; and the count began his defence, I assure you, Albert, in a most eloquent and skilful manner.", "의장이 편지의 봉인을 뜯으며 말했지. 그러자 백작이 변론을 시작했는데, 정말이지, 알베르, 더없이 능변이고 노련한 솜씨였네."], ["He produced documents proving that the Vizier of Yanina had up to the last moment honored him with his entire confidence, since he had interested him with a negotiation of life and death with the emperor.", "그는 야니나의 태수가 마지막 순간까지 자신에게 전적인 신임을 베풀었음을 입증하는 서류들을 내놓았네. 태수가 황제와 생사를 건 협상을 그에게 맡겼다는 거였지."], ["He produced the ring, his mark of authority, with which Ali Pasha generally sealed his letters, and which the latter had given him, that he might, on his return at any hour of the day or night, gain access to the presence, even in the harem.", "그는 알리 파샤가 평소 편지를 봉인할 때 쓰던 권위의 표지인 반지를 내보였는데, 태수가 그것을 주어, 그가 밤낮 어느 시각에 돌아오더라도, 심지어 하렘에까지 들 수 있게 했다는 거였네."], ["Unfortunately, the negotiation failed, and when he returned to defend his benefactor, he was dead.", "불행히도 협상은 실패했고, 그가 은인을 지키러 돌아왔을 때 은인은 이미 죽어 있었다더군."], ["‘But,’ said the count, ‘so great was Ali Pasha’s confidence, that on his death-bed he resigned his favorite mistress and her daughter to my care.’”", "'하지만,' 백작이 말했네. '알리 파샤의 신임이 얼마나 컸던지, 임종의 자리에서 그는 자신이 아끼던 애첩과 그 딸을 내게 맡겼습니다.'\""]], [["Albert started on hearing these words; the history of Haydée recurred to him, and he remembered what she had said of that message and the ring, and the manner in which she had been sold and made a slave.", "알베르는 이 말을 듣고 흠칫했다. 하이데의 사연이 떠올랐던 것이다. 그녀가 그 전갈과 반지에 대해, 그리고 자신이 팔려 노예가 된 경위에 대해 했던 말이 생각났다."]], [["“And what effect did this discourse produce?”", "\"그래서 그 변론이 어떤 효과를 냈나?\""], ["anxiously inquired Albert.", "알베르가 애타게 물었다."]], [["“I acknowledge it affected me, and, indeed, all the committee also,”", "\"솔직히 말해 나도 마음이 움직였고, 사실 위원회 전체도 그랬네.\""], ["said Beauchamp.", "보샹이 말했다."]], [["“Meanwhile, the president carelessly opened the letter which had been brought to him; but the first lines aroused his attention; he read them again and again, and fixing his eyes on M. de Morcerf, ‘Count,’ said he, ‘you have said that the Vizier of Yanina confided his wife and daughter to your care?’—‘Yes, sir,’ replied Morcerf; ‘but in that, like all the rest, misfortune pursued me. On my return, Vasiliki and her daughter Haydée had disappeared.’—‘Did you know them?’—‘My intimacy with the pasha and his unlimited confidence had gained me an introduction to them, and I had seen them above twenty times.’", "\"그러는 동안 의장은 자신에게 전해진 편지를 무심히 펼쳤네. 그런데 첫 몇 줄이 그의 주의를 끌었지. 그는 그것을 읽고 또 읽더니, 모르세르 백작에게 눈길을 박고 말했네. '백작, 야니나의 태수가 자기 아내와 딸을 당신에게 맡겼다고 하셨지요?' '그렇습니다.' 모르세르가 대답했네. '하지만 그 일에서도, 다른 모든 일과 마찬가지로 불행이 나를 따라다녔습니다. 내가 돌아왔을 때 바실리키와 그 딸 하이데는 사라지고 없었으니까요.' '그들을 아셨습니까?' '내가 파샤와 가깝게 지냈고 그가 무한한 신임을 보낸 덕에 그들에게 소개받았고, 스무 번도 넘게 본 적이 있습니다.'"]], [["“‘Have you any idea what became of them?’—‘Yes, sir; I heard they had fallen victims to their sorrow, and, perhaps, to their poverty.", "\"'그들이 어떻게 되었는지 짐작 가는 바가 있습니까?' '있습니다. 그들이 슬픔에, 그리고 어쩌면 가난에 희생되었다고 들었습니다."], ["I was not rich; my life was in constant danger; I could not seek them, to my great regret.’ The president frowned imperceptibly.", "나는 부유하지 못했고, 늘 목숨이 위태로웠습니다. 그래서 큰 한이 되지만 그들을 찾아 나설 수 없었지요.' 의장은 알아챌 듯 말 듯 눈살을 찌푸렸네."], ["‘Gentlemen,’ said he, ‘you have heard the Comte de Morcerf’s defence.", "'여러분,' 그가 말했네. '모르세르 백작의 변론을 들으셨습니다."], ["Can you, sir, produce any witnesses to the truth of what you have asserted?’—‘Alas, no, monsieur,’ replied the count; ‘all those who surrounded the vizier, or who knew me at his court, are either dead or gone away, I know not where.", "백작, 방금 진술하신 내용이 진실임을 입증할 증인을 댈 수 있습니까?' '아아, 그럴 수 없습니다.' 백작이 대답했네."], ["I believe that I alone, of all my countrymen, survived that dreadful war.", "'태수를 둘러싸고 있던 자들, 혹은 그의 궁정에서 나를 알던 자들은 모두 죽었거나, 어디론가 떠났는데 나는 그 행방을 모릅니다."], ["I have only the letters of Ali Tepelini, which I have placed before you; the ring, a token of his good-will, which is here; and, lastly, the most convincing proof I can offer, after an anonymous attack, and that is the absence of any witness against my veracity and the purity of my military life.’", "내 동포 모두를 통틀어 그 참혹한 전쟁에서 살아남은 사람은 나 하나뿐이라고 믿습니다. 내게 있는 것이라곤 여러분 앞에 내놓은 알리 테펠리니의 편지들, 그의 호의의 징표인 이 반지, 그리고 마지막으로, 익명의 공격을 받은 처지에서 내가 내놓을 수 있는 가장 설득력 있는 증거, 즉 나의 진실함과 나의 떳떳한 군 생활에 맞설 증인이 단 한 명도 없다는 사실뿐입니다.'"]], [["“A murmur of approbation ran through the assembly; and at this moment, Albert, had nothing more transpired, your father’s cause had been gained.", "\"의회에 동조하는 웅성거림이 번졌네. 그리고 그 순간, 알베르, 더 이상 아무 일도 일어나지 않았더라면 자네 아버지의 변론은 성공한 셈이었네."], ["It only remained to put it to the vote, when the president resumed: ‘Gentlemen and you, monsieur,—you will not be displeased, I presume, to listen to one who calls himself a very important witness, and who has just presented himself.", "이제 표결에 부치는 일만 남았는데, 그때 의장이 다시 입을 열었지. '여러분, 그리고 백작, — 짐작건대 자신을 매우 중요한 증인이라 칭하며 방금 나선 한 사람의 말을 듣는 것을 마다하지 않으시겠지요."], ["He is, doubtless, come to prove the perfect innocence of our colleague.", "그자는 분명 우리 동료의 완벽한 결백을 입증하러 온 것일 겁니다."], ["Here is a letter I have just received on the subject; shall it be read, or shall it be passed over?", "여기 이 일에 관해 방금 받은 편지가 있습니다. 이것을 낭독할까요, 아니면 그냥 넘길까요?"], ["and shall we take no notice of this incident?’ M.", "이 일을 못 본 척할까요?'"], ["de Morcerf turned pale, and clenched his hands on the papers he held.", "모르세르 백작은 창백해지며 들고 있던 서류를 두 손으로 움켜쥐었네."], ["The committee decided to hear the letter; the count was thoughtful and silent.", "위원회는 편지를 듣기로 했고, 백작은 생각에 잠긴 채 말이 없었지."], ["The president read:", "의장이 낭독했네."]], [["“‘Mr. President,—I can furnish the committee of inquiry into the conduct of the Lieutenant-General the Count of Morcerf in Epirus and in Macedonia with important particulars.’", "\"'의장님께, — 저는 에피루스와 마케도니아에서의 육군 중장 모르세르 백작의 행적을 조사하는 위원회에 중요한 사실들을 제공할 수 있습니다.'"]], [["“The president paused, and the count turned pale.", "\"의장이 잠시 멈추자 백작은 창백해졌네."], ["The president looked at his auditors.", "의장이 청중을 둘러보았지. '계속하시오.'"], ["‘Proceed,’ was heard on all sides.", "사방에서 그런 소리가 들렸네."], ["The president resumed:", "의장이 다시 읽었네."]], [["“‘I was on the spot at the death of Ali Pasha.", "\"'저는 알리 파샤가 죽던 그 자리에 있었습니다."], ["I was present during his last moments.", "그의 임종을 지켜보았습니다."], ["I know what is become of Vasiliki and Haydée.", "바실리키와 하이데가 어떻게 되었는지 압니다."], ["I am at the command of the committee, and even claim the honor of being heard.", "저는 위원회의 명에 따르겠으며, 발언의 영광을 베풀어 주시기를 청합니다."], ["I shall be in the lobby when this note is delivered to you.’", "이 쪽지가 전달될 때 저는 복도에 있겠습니다.'"]], [["“‘And who is this witness, or rather this enemy?’ asked the count, in a tone in which there was a visible alteration.", "\"'그래서, 이 증인은, 아니 차라리 이 적은 누구입니까?' 백작이 물었는데, 그 목소리에 눈에 띄는 변화가 있었네."], ["‘We shall know, sir,’ replied the president.", "'곧 알게 될 것입니다, 백작.'"], ["‘Is the committee willing to hear this witness?’—‘Yes, yes,’ they all said at once.", "의장이 대답했지. '위원회는 이 증인의 말을 들을 의향이 있습니까?' '예, 예.'"], ["The door-keeper was called.", "모두가 한꺼번에 말했네."], ["‘Is there anyone in the lobby?’ said the president.", "문지기를 불렀지. '복도에 누가 있소?' 의장이 물었네."]], [["“‘Yes, sir.’—‘Who is it?’—‘A woman, accompanied by a servant.’ Everyone looked at his neighbor.", "\"'예, 있습니다.' '누구요?' '한 여인이, 하인을 데리고 있습니다.'"], ["‘Bring her in,’ said the president.", "모두가 옆 사람을 쳐다보았네."], ["Five minutes after the door-keeper again appeared; all eyes were fixed on the door, and I,”", "'들여보내시오.' 의장이 말했네. 오 분쯤 뒤 문지기가 다시 나타났고, 모든 눈이 문에 쏠렸네."], ["said Beauchamp, “shared the general expectation and anxiety.", "나 또한,\" 보샹이 말했다. \"모두의 기대와 불안을 함께 느꼈지."], ["Behind the door-keeper walked a woman enveloped in a large veil, which completely concealed her.", "문지기 뒤로, 커다란 베일에 온몸을 휘감아 완전히 가린 한 여인이 걸어 들어왔네."], ["It was evident, from her figure and the perfumes she had about her, that she was young and fastidious in her tastes, but that was all.", "그 몸매와 몸에서 풍기는 향내로 보아, 그녀가 젊고 취향이 까다로운 사람임은 분명했으나, 알 수 있는 것은 그뿐이었지."], ["The president requested her to throw aside her veil, and it was then seen that she was dressed in the Grecian costume, and was remarkably beautiful.”", "의장이 베일을 걷어 달라고 청하자, 그제야 그녀가 그리스 의상을 차려입은, 놀랍도록 아름다운 여인임이 드러났네.\" \"아.\" 알베르가 말했다. \"그 여자였군.\""]], [["“Ah,” said Albert, “it was she.” “Who?”", "\"누구 말인가?\""]], [["“Haydée.”", "\"하이데.\""]], [["“Who told you that?”", "\"누가 그 말을 해 주던가?\""]], [["“Alas, I guess it.", "\"아아, 짐작이 가네."], ["But go on, Beauchamp.", "하지만 계속하게, 보샹."], ["You see I am calm and strong.", "보다시피 나는 차분하고 강하네."], ["And yet we must be drawing near the disclosure.”", "그래도 우리는 진상에 가까워지고 있는 게 분명하군.\""]], [["“M. de Morcerf,”", "\"모르세르 백작은,\""], ["continued Beauchamp, “looked at this woman with surprise and terror.", "보샹이 말을 이었다. \"놀라움과 공포에 차서 이 여인을 바라보았네."], ["Her lips were about to pass his sentence of life or death.", "그녀의 입술이 곧 그에게 삶이냐 죽음이냐의 선고를 내리려는 참이었지."], ["To the committee the adventure was so extraordinary and curious, that the interest they had felt for the count’s safety became now quite a secondary matter.", "위원회로서는 이 사건이 너무도 기이하고 흥미로워서, 그들이 백작의 안위를 두고 품었던 관심이 이제는 완전히 뒷전이 되어 버렸네."], ["The president himself advanced to place a seat for the young lady; but she declined availing herself of it.", "의장이 직접 나서서 그 젊은 여인에게 자리를 권했지만, 그녀는 사양했네."], ["As for the count, he had fallen on his chair; it was evident that his legs refused to support him.", "백작으로 말하자면, 의자에 풀썩 주저앉았지. 다리가 그를 지탱하기를 거부한 게 분명했네."]], [["“‘Madame,’ said the president, ‘you have engaged to furnish the committee with some important particulars respecting the affair at Yanina, and you have stated that you were an eyewitness of the event.’—‘I was, indeed,’ said the stranger, with a tone of sweet melancholy, and with the sonorous voice peculiar to the East.", "\"'부인,' 의장이 말했네. '부인은 야니나에서 일어난 사건에 관해 위원회에 몇 가지 중요한 사실을 제공하겠다고 하셨고, 그 사건을 직접 목격하셨다고 진술하셨습니다.' '그렇습니다, 정말로.' 낯선 여인이 달콤하고 우수에 찬 어조로, 동방 특유의 낭랑한 목소리로 말했네."]], [["“‘But allow me to say that you must have been very young then.’—‘I was four years old; but as those events deeply concerned me, not a single detail has escaped my memory.’—‘In what manner could these events concern you?", "\"'하지만 그렇다면 부인은 그때 무척 어렸을 텐데요.' '저는 네 살이었습니다. 하지만 그 사건들이 저와 깊이 얽혀 있었기에, 단 하나의 세부도 제 기억에서 빠져나가지 않았습니다.'"], ["and who are you, that they should have made so deep an impression on you?’—‘On them depended my father’s life,’ replied she.", "'그 사건들이 어떻게 부인과 얽힐 수 있었습니까? 그리고 그것이 부인에게 그토록 깊은 인상을 남겼다니, 부인은 대체 누구십니까?' '그 사건들에 제 아버지의 목숨이 달려 있었습니다.'"], ["‘I am Haydée, the daughter of Ali Tepelini, pasha of Yanina, and of Vasiliki, his beloved wife.’", "그녀가 대답했네. '저는 하이데, 야니나의 파샤 알리 테펠리니와 그가 사랑한 아내 바실리키의 딸입니다.'"]], [["40202m\n“The blush of mingled pride and modesty which suddenly suffused the cheeks of the young woman, the brilliancy of her eye, and her highly important communication, produced an indescribable effect on the assembly.", "\"긍지와 수줍음이 뒤섞인 홍조가 별안간 이 젊은 여인의 두 뺨에 번졌고, 그 눈동자의 광채와 더없이 중대한 그녀의 진술은 의회에 형언할 수 없는 효과를 자아냈네."], ["As for the count, he could not have been more overwhelmed if a thunderbolt had fallen at his feet and opened an immense gulf before him.", "백작으로 말하자면, 벼락이 그의 발치에 떨어져 눈앞에 거대한 심연을 열어젖혔다 한들 그 이상 압도될 수는 없었을 거야."]], [["“‘Madame,’ replied the president, bowing with profound respect, ‘allow me to ask one question; it shall be the last: Can you prove the authenticity of what you have now stated?’", "\"'부인,' 의장이 깊은 경의를 담아 고개 숙이며 대답했네. '한 가지만 여쭙겠습니다. 이것이 마지막입니다. 방금 진술하신 내용의 진위를 입증하실 수 있습니까?'"]], [["“‘I can, sir,’ said Haydée, drawing from under her veil a satin satchel highly perfumed; ‘for here is the register of my birth, signed by my father and his principal officers, and that of my baptism, my father having consented to my being brought up in my mother’s faith,—this latter has been sealed by the grand primate of Macedonia and Epirus; and lastly (and perhaps the most important), the record of the sale of my person and that of my mother to the Armenian merchant El-Kobbir, by the French officer, who, in his infamous bargain with the Porte, had reserved as his part of the booty the wife and daughter of his benefactor, whom he sold for the sum of four hundred thousand francs.’ A greenish pallor spread over the count’s cheeks, and his eyes became bloodshot at these terrible imputations, which were listened to by the assembly with ominous silence.", "\"'할 수 있습니다.' 하이데가 베일 밑에서 향내가 짙게 밴 새틴 주머니를 꺼내며 말했네. '여기 제 출생 증서가 있는데, 아버지와 그 휘하의 주요 장교들이 서명한 것입니다. 그리고 제 세례 증서도 있습니다. 아버지가 제가 어머니의 신앙으로 양육되는 것을 허락하셨거든요. — 이 후자에는 마케도니아와 에피루스의 대주교가 봉인했습니다. 그리고 마지막으로 (어쩌면 가장 중요한 것일 텐데), 한 프랑스 장교가 저와 제 어머니를 아르메니아 상인 엘코비르에게 판 매매 증서입니다. 그자는 오스만 정부와의 그 파렴치한 거래에서 자기 몫의 전리품으로 은인의 아내와 딸을 챙겨, 사십만 프랑에 팔아넘겼지요.' 백작의 두 뺨에 푸르스름한 창백함이 번졌고, 이 끔찍한 비난에 그의 눈은 핏발이 섰네. 의회는 불길한 침묵 속에 그 말에 귀 기울이고 있었지."]], [["“Haydée, still calm, but with a calmness more dreadful than the anger of another would have been, handed to the president the record of her sale, written in Arabic.", "\"하이데는 여전히 차분했으나, 그것은 다른 사람의 분노보다 더 무서운 차분함이었네. 그녀는 아랍어로 쓰인 자신의 매매 증서를 의장에게 건넸지."], ["It had been supposed some of the papers might be in the Arabian, Romaic, or Turkish language, and the interpreter of the House was in attendance.", "서류 중 일부가 아랍어나 로마이크어, 혹은 터키어로 되어 있을 수 있다고 여겨, 의회의 통역관이 대기하고 있었네."], ["One of the noble peers, who was familiar with the Arabic language, having studied it during the famous Egyptian campaign, followed with his eye as the translator read aloud:", "그 유명한 이집트 원정 때 아랍어를 익혀 그 말에 능통한 한 고귀한 귀족 의원이, 통역관이 소리 내어 읽는 동안 눈으로 따라 읽었네."]], [["“‘I, El-Kobbir, a slave-merchant, and purveyor of the harem of his highness, acknowledge having received for transmission to the sublime emperor, from the French lord, the Count of Monte Cristo, an emerald valued at eight hundred thousand francs; as the ransom of a young Christian slave of eleven years of age, named Haydée, the acknowledged daughter of the late lord Ali Tepelini, pasha of Yanina, and of Vasiliki, his favorite; she having been sold to me seven years previously, with her mother, who had died on arriving at Constantinople, by a French colonel in the service of the Vizier Ali Tepelini, named Fernand Mondego.", "\"'노예 상인이자 전하의 하렘 조달인인 나 엘코비르는, 프랑스 영주 몬테크리스토 백작으로부터, 지엄하신 황제께 전달하고자, 팔십만 프랑으로 평가되는 에메랄드 한 개를 받았음을 확인한다. 이는 하이데라는 이름의, 열한 살 난 어린 기독교도 노예의 몸값으로서, 그녀는 야니나의 파샤였던 고(故) 알리 테펠리니 영주와 그의 총애를 받던 바실리키 사이에서 난 것으로 인정된 딸이다. 그녀는 칠 년 전, 콘스탄티노플에 당도하던 중 사망한 그녀의 어머니와 함께, 태수 알리 테펠리니 휘하에서 복무하던 페르낭 몬데고라는 프랑스 대령에 의해 내게 팔렸다."], ["The above-mentioned purchase was made on his highness’s account, whose mandate I had, for the sum of four hundred thousand francs.", "위에 언급한 매입은 전하의 명을 받들어 전하의 몫으로, 사십만 프랑에 이루어졌다."]], [["“‘Given at Constantinople, by authority of his highness, in the year 1247 of the Hegira.", "\"'헤지라력 1247년, 전하의 권위로써 콘스탄티노플에서 작성하다."]], [["“‘Signed, El-Kobbir.’", "\"'서명, 엘코비르.'"]], [["“‘That this record should have all due authority, it shall bear the imperial seal, which the vendor is bound to have affixed to it.’", "\"'이 증서가 마땅한 효력을 모두 갖추도록, 판매인은 이에 황제의 옥새를 받아 찍을 의무가 있으며, 이로써 옥새를 날인하노라.'"]], [["“Near the merchant’s signature there was, indeed, the seal of the sublime emperor.", "\"과연 상인의 서명 곁에는 지엄하신 황제의 옥새가 찍혀 있었네."], ["A dreadful silence followed the reading of this document; the count could only stare, and his gaze, fixed as if unconsciously on Haydée, seemed one of fire and blood.", "이 문서의 낭독이 끝나자 무시무시한 침묵이 뒤따랐지. 백작은 그저 멍하니 바라볼 뿐이었고, 무의식중인 듯 하이데에게 박힌 그의 시선은 불과 피의 시선 같았네. '부인,'"], ["‘Madame,’ said the president, ‘may reference be made to the Count of Monte Cristo, who is now, I believe, in Paris?’", "의장이 말했네. '지금 파리에 계신 것으로 아는 몬테크리스토 백작께 문의해 보아도 되겠습니까?'"]], [["“‘Sir,’ replied Haydée, ‘the Count of Monte Cristo, my foster-father, has been in Normandy the last three days.’", "\"'백작님,' 하이데가 대답했네. '제 양아버지이신 몬테크리스토 백작께서는 지난 사흘간 노르망디에 계셨습니다.'"]], [["“‘Who, then, has counselled you to take this step, one for which the court is deeply indebted to you, and which is perfectly natural, considering your birth and your misfortunes?’—‘Sir,’ replied Haydée, ‘I have been led to take this step from a feeling of respect and grief.", "\"'그렇다면, 부인의 출생과 불행을 헤아리건대 지극히 당연한 일이고 또 본 위원회가 부인께 깊이 감사할 일입니다만, 부인께 이런 조처를 취하라고 권한 사람은 누구입니까?' '의장님,' 하이데가 대답했네."], ["Although a Christian, may God forgive me, I have always sought to revenge my illustrious father.", "'저는 존경과 비통함의 마음에서 이 조처를 취하게 되었습니다. 기독교도이지만, 신께서 용서하시기를, 저는 늘 저의 고귀한 아버지의 원수를 갚고자 했습니다."], ["Since I set my foot in France, and knew the traitor lived in Paris, I have watched carefully.", "프랑스 땅에 발을 들이고 그 배신자가 파리에 살고 있음을 알게 된 뒤로, 저는 주의 깊게 지켜보았습니다."], ["I live retired in the house of my noble protector, but I do it from choice.", "저는 고귀한 보호자의 집에서 은거하며 지내는데, 그것은 제가 택한 일입니다."], ["I love retirement and silence, because I can live with my thoughts and recollections of past days.", "저는 은둔과 침묵을 사랑합니다. 그 속에서 제 생각과 지난날의 추억과 더불어 살 수 있으니까요."], ["But the Count of Monte Cristo surrounds me with every paternal care, and I am ignorant of nothing which passes in the world.", "하지만 몬테크리스토 백작께서는 아버지처럼 온갖 보살핌으로 저를 감싸 주시기에, 저는 세상에서 벌어지는 일을 하나도 모르지 않습니다. 저는 제 방의 고요 속에서 모든 것을 압니다."], ["I learn all in the silence of my apartments,—for instance, I see all the newspapers, every periodical, as well as every new piece of music; and by thus watching the course of the life of others, I learned what had transpired this morning in the House of Peers, and what was to take place this evening; then I wrote.’\n40204m", "— 이를테면 저는 모든 신문, 모든 정기 간행물을, 또 새로 나온 악곡까지 다 봅니다. 그렇게 다른 이들의 삶의 흐름을 지켜보던 중, 오늘 아침 귀족원에서 무슨 일이 있었는지, 또 오늘 저녁에 무슨 일이 벌어질지를 알게 되었지요. 그래서 저는 편지를 썼습니다.'"]], [["“‘Then,’ remarked the president, ‘the Count of Monte Cristo knows nothing of your present proceedings?’—‘He is quite unaware of them, and I have but one fear, which is that he should disapprove of what I have done. But it is a glorious day for me,’ continued the young girl, raising her ardent gaze to heaven, ‘that on which I find at last an opportunity of avenging my father!’", "\"'그렇다면,' 의장이 말했네. '몬테크리스토 백작은 부인의 이번 행동에 대해 전혀 모르신단 말이군요?' '전혀 모르십니다. 그리고 제게는 단 한 가지 두려움이 있는데, 제가 한 일을 그분이 못마땅해하시지나 않을까 하는 것입니다. 하지만 제게는 영광스러운 날입니다.' 젊은 여인이 타오르는 시선을 하늘로 들어 올리며 말을 이었네. '마침내 제 아버지의 원수를 갚을 기회를 찾은 이 날이 말입니다!'"]], [["“The count had not uttered one word the whole of this time.", "\"백작은 이 시간 내내 단 한마디도 입에 올리지 않았네."], ["His colleagues looked at him, and doubtless pitied his prospects, blighted under the perfumed breath of a woman.", "동료들이 그를 바라보았고, 한 여인의 향기로운 입김 아래 시들어 버린 그의 앞날을 분명 측은히 여겼지. 그의 비참함이 음산한 선을 그리며 그 얼굴에 드러나 있었네. '모르세르 백작,'"], ["His misery was depicted in sinister lines on his countenance. ‘M.", "의장이 말했네. '당신은 이 부인을 야니나의 파샤 알리 테펠리니의 딸로 인정합니까?' '아니오.'"], ["de Morcerf,’ said the president, ‘do you recognize this lady as the daughter of Ali Tepelini, pasha of Yanina?’—‘No,’ said Morcerf, attempting to rise, ‘it is a base plot, contrived by my enemies.’ Haydée, whose eyes had been fixed on the door, as if expecting someone, turned hastily, and, seeing the count standing, shrieked, ‘You do not know me?’ said she.", "모르세르가 일어서려 애쓰며 말했네. '이것은 내 적들이 꾸며 낸 비열한 음모입니다.' 누군가를 기다리는 듯 문에 시선을 두고 있던 하이데가 황급히 돌아서더니, 서 있는 백작을 보고 비명을 질렀네."], ["‘Well, I fortunately recognize you!", "'당신은 나를 모른다고요?' 그녀가 말했네."], ["You are Fernand Mondego, the French officer who led the troops of my noble father! It is you who surrendered the castle of Yanina!", "'하지만 다행히 나는 당신을 알아봅니다! 당신은 나의 고귀한 아버지의 군대를 지휘한 프랑스 장교 페르낭 몬데고예요!"], ["It is you who, sent by him to Constantinople, to treat with the emperor for the life or death of your benefactor, brought back a false mandate granting full pardon!", "야니나 성을 넘긴 것도 당신입니다! 당신은 은인의 생사를 두고 황제와 담판하라며 그가 콘스탄티노플로 보냈는데, 완전한 사면을 허락한다는 거짓 칙령을 가지고 돌아왔지요!"], ["It is you who, with that mandate, obtained the pasha’s ring, which gave you authority over Selim, the fire-keeper!", "그 칙령으로 파샤의 반지를 손에 넣어, 불지기 셀림을 좌지우지할 권한을 얻은 것도 당신입니다!"], ["It is you who stabbed Selim. It is you who sold us, my mother and me, to the merchant, El-Kobbir!", "셀림을 찔러 죽인 것도 당신이에요. 나와 내 어머니를 상인 엘코비르에게 판 것도 당신입니다!"], ["Assassin, assassin, assassin, you have still on your brow your master’s blood! Look, gentlemen, all!’", "살인자, 살인자, 살인자, 당신 이마에는 아직도 당신 주인의 피가 묻어 있어요! 보세요, 여러분, 모두 보세요!'"]], [["“These words had been pronounced with such enthusiasm and evident truth, that every eye was fixed on the count’s forehead, and he himself passed his hand across it, as if he felt Ali’s blood still lingering there.", "\"이 말이 어찌나 격정에 차고 또렷한 진실로 발해졌던지, 모든 눈이 백작의 이마에 쏠렸고, 그 자신도 알리의 피가 아직 거기 어려 있기라도 한 듯 손으로 이마를 쓸었네. '당신은 정녕 모르세르 백작을 그 장교 페르낭 몬데고로 알아보는 것입니까?'"], ["‘You positively recognize M.", "'정말로 그렇습니다!'"], ["de Morcerf as the officer, Fernand Mondego?’—‘Indeed I do!’ cried Haydée.", "하이데가 외쳤네. '오, 어머니, 어머니께서 이렇게 말씀하셨지요."], ["‘Oh, my mother, it was you who said, “You were free, you had a beloved father, you were destined to be almost a queen.", "\"너는 자유로운 몸이었고, 사랑하는 아버지가 있었으며, 거의 왕비가 될 운명이었단다. 저 사내를 똑똑히 보아라."], ["Look well at that man; it is he who raised your father’s head on the point of a spear; it is he who sold us; it is he who forsook us!", "창끝에 네 아버지의 머리를 꽂아 올린 자가 바로 저자다. 우리를 판 자가 저자고, 우리를 저버린 자가 저자다. 저자의 오른손을 잘 보아라, 거기에 큰 상처가 있단다."], ["Look well at his right hand, on which he has a large wound; if you forgot his features, you would know him by that hand, into which fell, one by one, the gold pieces of the merchant El-Kobbir!” I know him!", "얼굴은 잊을지라도, 상인 엘코비르의 금화가 한 닢 한 닢 떨어져 들어가던 저 손으로 너는 저자를 알아볼 것이다!\" 나는 저자를 압니다!"], ["Ah, let him say now if he does not recognize me!’ Each word fell like a dagger on Morcerf, and deprived him of a portion of his energy; as she uttered the last, he hid his mutilated hand hastily in his bosom, and fell back on his seat, overwhelmed by wretchedness and despair.", "아, 이제 저자더러 나를 알아보지 못하겠는지 말해 보라 하세요!' 한마디 한마디가 단검처럼 모르세르에게 꽂혀, 그의 기력을 조금씩 앗아 갔네. 그녀가 마지막 말을 내뱉자, 그는 불구가 된 손을 황급히 가슴팍에 감추고는, 비참함과 절망에 짓눌려 의자에 털썩 주저앉았네."], ["This scene completely changed the opinion of the assembly respecting the accused count.", "이 장면은 피고가 된 백작에 대한 의회의 의견을 완전히 뒤바꿔 놓았네."]], [["“‘Count of Morcerf,’ said the president, ‘do not allow yourself to be cast down; answer.", "\"'모르세르 백작,' 의장이 말했네. '낙담하지 말고 답하시오."], ["The justice of the court is supreme and impartial as that of God; it will not suffer you to be trampled on by your enemies without giving you an opportunity of defending yourself.", "이 법정의 정의는 신의 정의처럼 지엄하고 공정합니다. 당신이 스스로를 변호할 기회조차 없이 적들에게 짓밟히는 것을 좌시하지 않을 것입니다."], ["Shall further inquiries be made?", "추가 조사를 진행할까요?"], ["Shall two members of the House be sent to Yanina?", "의회에서 의원 두 명을 야니나로 보낼까요? 말하시오!'"], ["Speak!’ Morcerf did not reply.", "모르세르는 대답하지 않았네."], ["Then all the members looked at each other with terror.", "그러자 의원 모두가 공포에 질려 서로를 바라보았지."], ["They knew the count’s energetic and violent temper; it must be, indeed, a dreadful blow which would deprive him of courage to defend himself.", "그들은 백작의 격렬하고 사나운 성미를 알고 있었네. 그에게서 스스로를 변호할 용기마저 앗아 갈 정도라면, 그것은 정말로 무시무시한 일격임이 틀림없었지."], ["They expected that his stupefied silence would be followed by a fiery outburst.", "그들은 그의 망연한 침묵에 뒤이어 불같은 폭발이 터지리라 예상했네. '자,'"], ["‘Well,’ asked the president, ‘what is your decision?’\n40206m", "의장이 물었네. '당신의 결정은 무엇입니까?'"]], [["“‘I have no reply to make,’ said the count in a low tone.", "\"'할 말이 없습니다.' 백작이 나직한 목소리로 말했네."]], [["“‘Has the daughter of Ali Tepelini spoken the truth?’ said the president.", "\"'알리 테펠리니의 딸이 진실을 말한 것입니까?' 의장이 말했네."], ["‘Is she, then, the terrible witness to whose charge you dare not plead “Not guilty”?", "'그렇다면 그녀가, 감히 \"무죄\"라 항변하지 못하게 만드는 그 무서운 증인이란 말입니까?"], ["Have you really committed the crimes of which you are accused?’ The count looked around him with an expression which might have softened tigers, but which could not disarm his judges.", "당신은 정말로 당신에게 씌워진 죄들을 저질렀습니까?' 백작은 호랑이라도 누그러뜨렸을 표정으로 주위를 둘러보았으나, 그것으로 판관들의 무장을 풀 수는 없었네."], ["Then he raised his eyes towards the ceiling, but withdrew then, immediately, as if he feared the roof would open and reveal to his distressed view that second tribunal called heaven, and that other judge named God.", "이윽고 그는 천장을 향해 눈을 들었으나, 마치 지붕이 열려 그 괴로운 눈앞에 천국이라 불리는 또 다른 법정과 신이라 불리는 또 다른 판관이 드러날까 두려운 듯, 곧바로 시선을 거두었지. 그러더니 황급한 동작으로, 숨이 막힐 듯 답답한 상의를 잡아 찢고는, 미치광이처럼 방에서 뛰쳐나갔네."], ["Then, with a hasty movement, he tore open his coat, which seemed to stifle him, and flew from the room like a madman; his footstep was heard one moment in the corridor, then the rattling of his carriage-wheels as he was driven rapidly away.", "잠시 복도에 그의 발소리가 들리더니, 곧 마차 바퀴가 요란하게 구르며 그가 황급히 실려 가는 소리가 들렸지. '여러분,' 정적이 되돌아오자 의장이 말했네."], ["‘Gentlemen,’ said the president, when silence was restored, ‘is the Count of Morcerf convicted of felony, treason, and conduct unbecoming a member of this House?’—‘Yes,’ replied all the members of the committee of inquiry with a unanimous voice.", "'모르세르 백작은 중죄와 반역, 그리고 본 의회 의원으로서 부적절한 행위에 대해 유죄입니까?' '유죄요.' 조사 위원회의 모든 위원이 만장일치의 목소리로 대답했네."]], [["“Haydée had remained until the close of the meeting.", "\"하이데는 회의가 끝날 때까지 남아 있었네."], ["She heard the count’s sentence pronounced without betraying an expression of joy or pity; then drawing her veil over her face she bowed majestically to the councillors, and left with that dignified step which Virgil attributes to his goddesses.”", "그녀는 기쁨이나 연민의 기색을 조금도 드러내지 않고 백작에게 내려지는 선고를 들었지. 그러고는 얼굴에 베일을 드리우고 위원들에게 위엄 있게 절한 뒤, 베르길리우스가 그의 여신들에게 부여한 그 기품 있는 걸음걸이로 떠났네.\""]], [["# Chapter 87. The Challenge", "# Chapter 87. The Challenge"]], [["Then,” continued Beauchamp, “I took advantage of the silence and the darkness to leave the house without being seen.", "\"그래서,\" 보샹이 말을 이었다."], ["The usher who had introduced me was waiting for me at the door, and he conducted me through the corridors to a private entrance opening into the Rue de Vaugirard.", "\"나는 그 침묵과 어둠을 틈타 들키지 않고 집을 빠져나왔네. 나를 안내했던 정리가 문 앞에서 기다리고 있다가, 복도를 지나 보지라르 거리로 통하는 쪽문까지 데려다주었지."], ["I left with mingled feelings of sorrow and delight.", "나는 슬픔과 기쁨이 뒤섞인 심정으로 그곳을 나왔네."], ["Excuse me, Albert,—sorrow on your account, and delight with that noble girl, thus pursuing paternal vengeance.", "미안하네, 알베르. 자네 때문에 슬펐고, 그렇게 아버지의 복수를 좇는 그 고결한 처녀 때문에 기뻤지."], ["Yes, Albert, from whatever source the blow may have proceeded—it may be from an enemy, but that enemy is only the agent of Providence.”", "그래, 알베르, 그 일격이 어디서 비롯되었든 간에, 그것이 적의 손에서 나왔다 해도, 그 적은 섭리의 대리인에 지나지 않는 거라네.\""]], [["Albert held his head between his hands; he raised his face, red with shame and bathed in tears, and seizing Beauchamp’s arm:", "알베르는 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 있었다. 이윽고 그는 수치심으로 붉게 물들고 눈물에 젖은 얼굴을 들더니, 보샹의 팔을 붙잡았다."]], [["“My friend,”", "\"이보게,\""], ["said he, “my life is ended.", "그가 말했다. \"내 인생은 끝났네."], ["I cannot calmly say with you, ‘Providence has struck the blow;’ but I must discover who pursues me with this hatred, and when I have found him I shall kill him, or he will kill me.", "나는 자네처럼 '섭리가 그 일격을 내렸다'고 차분히 말할 수가 없어. 나는 누가 이런 증오로 나를 좇는지 반드시 알아내야 하네. 그자를 찾아내면 내가 그를 죽이든지, 아니면 그가 나를 죽이든지 할 거야."], ["I rely on your friendship to assist me, Beauchamp, if contempt has not banished it from your heart.”", "자네의 우정에 기대고 있네, 보샹. 경멸이 자네 마음에서 그 우정을 몰아내지 않았다면 말이야.\""]], [["“Contempt, my friend?", "\"경멸이라니, 이 사람아?"], ["How does this misfortune affect you?", "이 불행이 자네와 무슨 상관이 있단 말인가?"], ["No, happily that unjust prejudice is forgotten which made the son responsible for the father’s actions.", "다행히도 아버지의 행위를 아들에게 묻던 그 부당한 편견은 이제 사라졌네. 자네 삶을 돌아보게, 알베르."], ["Review your life, Albert; although it is only just beginning, did a lovely summer’s day ever dawn with greater purity than has marked the commencement of your career?", "이제 막 시작되었을 뿐이지만, 자네가 걸어온 길의 출발만큼 더없이 맑게 동튼 여름날이 일찍이 있었던가? 아니야, 알베르, 내 충고를 듣게."], ["No, Albert, take my advice. You are young and rich—leave Paris—all is soon forgotten in this great Babylon of excitement and changing tastes.", "자네는 젊고 부유하니, 파리를 떠나게. 흥분과 변덕스러운 취향으로 들끓는 이 거대한 바빌론에서는 무엇이든 금세 잊히는 법이야."], ["You will return after three or four years with a Russian princess for a bride, and no one will think more of what occurred yesterday than if it had happened sixteen years ago.”", "자네는 서너 해 뒤에 러시아 공주를 신부로 맞아 돌아올 테고, 그러면 어제 일어난 일을 두고 마치 십육 년 전 일인 양 누구도 더는 입에 올리지 않을 걸세.\""]], [["“Thank you, my dear Beauchamp, thank you for the excellent feeling which prompts your advice; but it cannot be.", "\"고맙네, 보샹, 자네의 충고를 이끌어 낸 그 따뜻한 마음이 정말 고마워. 하지만 그럴 수는 없어."], ["I have told you my wish, or rather my determination.", "내 바람을, 아니 내 결심을 이미 자네에게 말했잖나."], ["You understand that, interested as I am in this affair, I cannot see it in the same light as you do.", "자네도 알다시피, 이 일에 직접 얽혀 있는 나로서는 자네와 같은 눈으로 이 일을 볼 수가 없어."], ["What appears to you to emanate from a celestial source, seems to me to proceed from one far less pure.", "자네에게는 하늘에서 비롯된 것처럼 보이는 일이, 내게는 그보다 훨씬 덜 순결한 데서 나온 것으로 보이거든."], ["Providence appears to me to have no share in this affair; and happily so, for instead of the invisible, impalpable agent of celestial rewards and punishments, I shall find one both palpable and visible, on whom I shall revenge myself, I assure you, for all I have suffered during the last month.", "내가 보기에 이 일에 섭리는 아무 몫도 없어. 그리고 그게 다행이지. 보이지도 만질 수도 없는, 하늘의 상벌을 집행하는 대리인 대신, 나는 만질 수도 볼 수도 있는 자를 찾아낼 테니까."], ["Now, I repeat, Beauchamp, I wish to return to human and material existence, and if you are still the friend you profess to be, help me to discover the hand that struck the blow.”", "단언컨대 지난 한 달간 내가 겪은 모든 고통을 그자에게 갚아 줄 거야. 다시 말하네, 보샹, 나는 인간의, 물질의 세계로 돌아가고 싶어. 자네가 스스로 자처하는 그 친구라면, 그 일격을 가한 손을 찾아내도록 나를 도와주게.\""]], [["“Be it so,”", "\"좋아,\" 보샹이 말했다. \"자네가 나를 이 땅으로 끌어내려야겠다면, 따르겠네."], ["said Beauchamp; “if you must have me descend to earth, I submit; and if you will seek your enemy, I will assist you, and I will engage to find him, my honor being almost as deeply interested as yours.”", "자네가 적을 찾겠다면 내가 돕지. 반드시 찾아내고야 말겠어. 내 명예도 자네 못지않게 깊이 걸려 있으니 말이야.\""]], [["“Well, then, you understand, Beauchamp, that we begin our search immediately.", "\"그래, 그럼, 보샹, 자네도 알겠지만, 우리는 당장 수색을 시작하는 거야."], ["Each moment’s delay is an eternity for me.", "한순간의 지체도 내게는 영원처럼 느껴지니까."], ["The calumniator is not yet punished, and he may hope that he will not be; but, on my honor, if he thinks so, he deceives himself.”", "그 중상모략꾼은 아직 벌을 받지 않았고, 앞으로도 받지 않으리라 기대하고 있을지도 몰라. 하지만 내 명예를 걸고 말하네, 그렇게 생각한다면 큰 착각이야.\""]], [["“Well, listen, Morcerf.”", "\"자, 들어 보게, 모르세르.\""]], [["“Ah, Beauchamp, I see you know something already; you will restore me to life.”", "\"아, 보샹, 자네가 벌써 뭔가 알고 있는 게로군. 자네가 나를 다시 살려 내는구먼.\""]], [["“I do not say there is any truth in what I am going to tell you, but it is, at least, a ray of light in a dark night; by following it we may, perhaps, discover something more certain.”", "\"내가 이제 하려는 말에 진실이 담겨 있다고 단언하는 건 아니야. 하지만 적어도 어두운 밤에 비치는 한 줄기 빛은 되겠지. 그걸 따라가다 보면, 어쩌면 좀 더 확실한 무언가를 알아낼 수 있을지도 모르네.\""]], [["“Tell me; satisfy my impatience.”", "\"말해 주게. 내 조바심을 좀 풀어 주게.\""]], [["“Well, I will tell you what I did not like to mention on my return from Yanina.”", "\"좋아, 야니나에서 돌아왔을 때 차마 입에 올리지 못했던 이야기를 들려주지.\""]], [["“Say on.”", "\"어서 말하게.\""]], [["“I went, of course, to the chief banker of the town to make inquiries.", "\"나는 당연히 그 도시의 우두머리 은행가를 찾아가 알아보았네."], ["At the first word, before I had even mentioned your father’s name”—", "그런데 첫마디부터, 내가 자네 아버지의 이름을 꺼내기도 전에 말이야……\""]], [["“‘Ah,’ said he.", "\"'아,'"], ["‘I guess what brings you here.’\n“‘How, and why?’", "그가 말하더군. '무슨 일로 오셨는지 짐작이 갑니다.'\""]], [["“‘Because a fortnight since I was questioned on the same subject.’", "\"'어떻게, 또 왜 말입니까?'\" \"'보름 전에도 같은 일로 문의를 받았거든요.'\""]], [["“‘By whom?’", "\"'누구한테서요?'\""]], [["“‘By a banker of Paris, my correspondent.’", "\"'파리의 어느 은행가, 제 거래처에게서요.'\""]], [["“‘Whose name is——’", "\"'그자의 이름이……'\""]], [["“‘Danglars.’”", "\"'당글라르입니다.'\""]], [["“He!” cried Albert; “yes, it is indeed he who has so long pursued my father with jealous hatred.", "\"그자라니!\" 알베르가 외쳤다."], ["He, the man who would be popular, cannot forgive the Count of Morcerf for being created a peer; and this marriage broken off without a reason being assigned—yes, it is all from the same cause.”", "\"그래, 그토록 오랫동안 질투 어린 증오로 내 아버지를 좇아 온 게 바로 그자야. 인기를 얻고 싶어 하는 그 사내가, 모르세르 백작이 귀족원 의원에 봉해진 걸 용서하지 못한 거라고. 그리고 아무 이유도 밝히지 않고 깨진 그 혼담도, 그래, 모두 같은 원인에서 나온 거야.\""]], [["“Make inquiries, Albert, but do not be angry without reason; make inquiries, and if it be true——”", "\"알아보게, 알베르. 하지만 이유 없이 화부터 내지는 말게. 알아보고, 만일 사실이라면……\""]], [["“Oh, yes, if it be true,”", "\"오, 그래, 만일 사실이라면,\""], ["cried the young man, “he shall pay me all I have suffered.”", "청년이 외쳤다. \"내가 겪은 모든 고통을 그자가 갚아야 할 거야.\""]], [["“Beware, Morcerf, he is already an old man.”", "\"조심하게, 모르세르. 그는 이미 노인이야.\""]], [["“I will respect his age as he has respected the honor of my family; if my father had offended him, why did he not attack him personally?", "\"그가 우리 가문의 명예를 존중해 준 만큼만 그의 나이를 존중해 주지. 내 아버지가 그를 모욕했다면, 어째서 직접 맞서지 않았느냔 말이야?"], ["Oh, no, he was afraid to encounter him face to face.”", "오, 아니지, 그는 정면으로 마주 서기가 두려웠던 거야.\""]], [["“I do not condemn you, Albert; I only restrain you.", "\"자네를 탓하는 게 아니야, 알베르. 다만 자네를 말리는 거지."], ["Act prudently.”", "신중하게 행동하게.\""]], [["“Oh, do not fear; besides, you will accompany me.", "\"오, 걱정 말게. 게다가 자네가 나와 동행해 줄 거잖나."], ["Beauchamp, solemn transactions should be sanctioned by a witness.", "보샹, 이런 엄숙한 거래에는 증인의 입회가 있어야 마땅하지."], ["Before this day closes, if M. Danglars is guilty, he shall cease to live, or I shall die.", "오늘 해가 지기 전에, 당글라르 씨가 죄가 있다면 그는 목숨을 잃거나, 아니면 내가 죽을 거야."], ["_Pardieu_, Beauchamp, mine shall be a splendid funeral!”", "맙소사, 보샹, 내 장례식은 성대할 걸세!\""]], [["“When such resolutions are made, Albert, they should be promptly executed.", "\"그런 결심을 했다면, 알베르, 지체 없이 실행에 옮겨야지."], ["Do you wish to go to M. Danglars?", "당글라르 씨에게 가고 싶나?"], ["Let us go immediately.”", "당장 가세.\""]], [["They sent for a cabriolet.", "그들은 이륜마차를 불렀다."], ["On entering the banker’s mansion, they perceived the phaeton and servant of M. Andrea Cavalcanti.", "은행가의 저택에 들어서면서, 그들은 안드레아 카발칸티 씨의 사륜마차와 하인을 알아보았다."]], [["“Ah! _parbleu!_ that’s good,”", "\"아! 이런, 잘됐군.\""], ["said Albert, with a gloomy tone.", "알베르가 음울한 어조로 말했다."], ["“If M. Danglars will not fight with me, I will kill his son-in-law; Cavalcanti will certainly fight.”", "\"당글라르 씨가 나와 결투하지 않겠다면, 그의 사위를 죽이지. 카발칸티는 틀림없이 싸울 테니까.\""]], [["The servant announced the young man; but the banker, recollecting what had transpired the day before, did not wish him admitted.", "하인이 청년의 방문을 알렸다. 그러나 은행가는 전날 벌어진 일을 떠올리고는 그를 들이고 싶지 않았다. 하지만 이미 늦은 뒤였다."], ["It was, however, too late; Albert had followed the footman, and, hearing the order given, forced the door open, and followed by Beauchamp found himself in the banker’s study.", "알베르가 시종의 뒤를 따라왔다가, 그 명령이 떨어지는 것을 듣고는 문을 박차고 들어왔다. 보샹을 뒤세운 채, 그는 어느새 은행가의 서재에 들어서 있었다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"이보시오,\""], ["cried the latter, “am I no longer at liberty to receive whom I choose in my house?", "당글라르가 외쳤다. \"내 집에서 내가 원하는 사람을 맞이할 자유조차 이젠 없단 말이오?"], ["You appear to forget yourself sadly.”", "무례가 지나치신 것 같구려.\""]], [["“No, sir,”", "\"아닙니다.\""], ["said Albert, coldly; “there are circumstances in which one cannot, except through cowardice,—I offer you that refuge,—refuse to admit certain persons at least.”", "알베르가 차갑게 말했다. \"비겁함을 무릅쓰지 않고서는, 적어도 어떤 사람들의 출입만은 거절할 수 없는 경우가 있는 법이지요. 당신께 그 피난처를 권해 드리는 겁니다.\""]], [["“What is your errand, then, with me, sir?”", "\"그래, 내게 무슨 볼일이시오?\""]], [["“I mean,”", "\"제 뜻은,\" 알베르가 다가서며 말했다. 그는 벽난로를 등지고 선 카발칸티에게는 눈길도 주지 않는 듯했다."], ["said Albert, drawing near, and without apparently noticing Cavalcanti, who stood with his back towards the fireplace—“I mean to propose a meeting in some retired corner where no one will interrupt us for ten minutes; that will be sufficient—where two men having met, one of them will remain on the ground.”", "\"누구도 십 분간 우리를 방해하지 않을 외딴 구석에서 한번 만나자고 청하려는 겁니다. 그 정도면 충분하지요. 두 사람이 마주 서면, 그중 하나는 그 자리에 쓰러져 남게 될 테니까요.\""]], [["Danglars turned pale; Cavalcanti moved a step forward, and Albert turned towards him.", "당글라르의 얼굴이 창백해졌다. 카발칸티가 한 걸음 앞으로 나섰고, 알베르가 그쪽으로 돌아섰다."]], [["“And you, too,”", "\"당신도,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “come, if you like, monsieur; you have a claim, being almost one of the family, and I will give as many rendezvous of that kind as I can find persons willing to accept them.”", "\"원한다면 오시오, 무슈. 당신도 거의 한 식구나 다름없으니 자격이 있지요. 그런 만남이라면, 기꺼이 받아들이려는 사람을 찾는 만큼 얼마든지 마련해 드리리다.\""]], [["Cavalcanti looked at Danglars with a stupefied air, and the latter, making an effort, arose and stepped between the two young men.", "카발칸티가 얼이 빠진 얼굴로 당글라르를 바라보았다. 당글라르는 안간힘을 다해 몸을 일으키더니 두 청년 사이로 끼어들었다."], ["Albert’s attack on Andrea had placed him on a different footing, and he hoped this visit had another cause than that he had at first supposed.", "알베르가 안드레아를 공격한 덕분에 그는 한결 다른 처지에 놓이게 되었고, 이 방문이 처음에 짐작했던 것과는 다른 까닭에서 비롯되었기를 바랐다."]], [["“Indeed, sir,”", "\"정말이지, 이보시오,\""], ["said he to Albert, “if you are come to quarrel with this gentleman because I have preferred him to you, I shall resign the case to the king’s attorney.”", "그가 알베르에게 말했다. \"내가 당신보다 이분을 택했다고 해서 시비를 걸러 오신 거라면, 나는 이 일을 검사장에게 넘기겠소.\""]], [["“You mistake, sir,”", "\"오해십니다.\""], ["said Morcerf with a gloomy smile; “I am not referring in the least to matrimony, and I only addressed myself to M.", "모르세르가 음울한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"나는 혼인 문제는 조금도 거론하지 않았습니다. 카발칸티 씨에게 말을 건 것도, 그가 우리 사이에 끼어들려는 듯 보여서였을 뿐이지요."], ["Cavalcanti because he appeared disposed to interfere between us.", "한 가지 점에서는 당신 말이 옳소."], ["In one respect you are right, for I am ready to quarrel with everyone today; but you have the first claim, M. Danglars.”", "나는 오늘 누구와도 시비를 가릴 작정이니까요. 하지만 가장 먼저 따질 상대는 당신이오, 당글라르 씨.\""]], [["40212m\n“Sir,”", "\"이보시오,\""], ["replied Danglars, pale with anger and fear, “I warn you, when I have the misfortune to meet with a mad dog, I kill it; and far from thinking myself guilty of a crime, I believe I do society a kindness.", "당글라르가 분노와 두려움으로 창백해진 얼굴로 대답했다. \"내 미리 일러두겠소. 나는 불운하게 미친개와 마주치면 그놈을 죽여 버리오."], ["Now, if you are mad and try to bite me, I will kill you without pity.", "그러고도 죄를 지었다고 여기기는커녕, 사회에 이로운 일을 했다고 생각하지. 그러니 당신이 미쳐서 나를 물려 든다면, 나는 가차 없이 당신을 죽이겠소."], ["Is it my fault that your father has dishonored himself?”", "당신 아버지가 스스로 명예를 더럽힌 게 내 잘못이란 말이오?\""]], [["“Yes, miserable wretch!”", "\"그래, 이 비열한 인간아!\""], ["cried Morcerf, “it is your fault.”", "모르세르가 외쳤다. \"그게 바로 네 잘못이야.\""]], [["Danglars retreated a few steps. “My fault?”", "당글라르가 몇 걸음 물러섰다. \"내 잘못이라고?\""], ["said he; “you must be mad!", "그가 말했다. \"당신 미쳤군!"], ["What do I know of the Grecian affair?", "그 그리스 사건에 대해 내가 뭘 안단 말이오?"], ["Have I travelled in that country?", "내가 그 나라를 여행이라도 했소?"], ["Did I advise your father to sell the castle of Yanina—to betray——”", "내가 당신 아버지에게 야니나 성채를 팔라고, 배신하라고 부추기기라도 했단 말이오……\""]], [["“Silence!”", "\"닥쳐!\""], ["said Albert, with a thundering voice.", "알베르가 우레 같은 목소리로 말했다."], ["“No; it is not you who have directly made this exposure and brought this sorrow on us, but you hypocritically provoked it.”", "\"아니, 직접 이 일을 폭로해 우리에게 이 슬픔을 안긴 건 네가 아니야. 하지만 위선을 떨며 그 일을 부추긴 건 너야.\" \"내가?\""]], [["“I?” “Yes; you!", "\"그래, 너 말이야!"], ["How came it known?”", "그게 어떻게 세상에 알려졌지?\""]], [["“I suppose you read it in the paper in the account from Yanina?”", "\"신문에서, 야니나에서 온 기사를 읽고 알게 된 거 아니오?\""]], [["“Who wrote to Yanina?”", "\"누가 야니나로 편지를 보냈지?\""]], [["“To Yanina?”", "\"야니나로?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래."], ["Who wrote for particulars concerning my father?”", "누가 내 아버지에 관한 자세한 내막을 캐물었느냔 말이야?\""]], [["“I imagine anyone may write to Yanina.”", "\"누구든 야니나로 편지를 보낼 수 있겠지요.\""]], [["“But one person only wrote!”", "\"하지만 편지를 보낸 사람은 단 한 명뿐이었어!\""]], [["“One only?”", "\"단 한 명?\""]], [["“Yes; and that was you!”", "\"그래, 그게 바로 너였지!\""]], [["“I, doubtless, wrote.", "\"내가 편지를 쓴 건 분명하오."], ["It appears to me that when about to marry your daughter to a young man, it is right to make some inquiries respecting his family; it is not only a right, but a duty.”", "딸을 어느 청년에게 시집보내려 할 때, 그 집안에 대해 얼마간 알아보는 건 마땅한 일 아니겠소. 그건 권리일 뿐 아니라 의무지요.\""]], [["“You wrote, sir, knowing what answer you would receive.”", "\"당신은 어떤 답이 돌아올지 알면서 편지를 쓴 거요.\""]], [["“I, indeed? I assure you,”", "\"내가, 정말로? 단언컨대,\""], ["cried Danglars, with a confidence and security proceeding less from fear than from the interest he really felt for the young man, “I solemnly declare to you, that I should never have thought of writing to Yanina, did I know anything of Ali Pasha’s misfortunes.”", "당글라르가 외쳤다. 그 확신과 침착함은 두려움보다는 그가 청년에게 진심으로 품고 있는 관심에서 비롯된 것이었다. \"엄숙히 맹세하건대, 알리 파샤의 불행에 대해 조금이라도 알았더라면, 나는 야니나로 편지를 보낼 생각조차 하지 않았을 거요.\""]], [["“Who, then, urged you to write?", "\"그렇다면 누가 당신에게 편지를 쓰라고 부추겼소?"], ["Tell me.”", "말해 보시오.\""]], [["“_Pardieu!_ it was the most simple thing in the world.", "\"맙소사! 세상에 그보다 단순한 일도 없소."], ["I was speaking of your father’s past history.", "나는 당신 아버지의 과거 내력을 이야기하던 참이었지요."], ["I said the origin of his fortune remained obscure.", "그 재산의 출처가 분명치 않다고 말했소."], ["The person to whom I addressed my scruples asked me where your father had acquired his property?", "내 의구심을 털어놓은 상대가, 당신 아버지가 어디서 그 재산을 모았느냐고 묻더군. '그리스에서'라고 내가 대답했지요."], ["I answered, ‘In Greece.’—‘Then,’ said he, ‘write to Yanina.’”", "그러자 그가 '그렇다면 야니나로 편지를 보내 보시오'라고 했소.\""]], [["“And who thus advised you?”", "\"그래, 그렇게 당신에게 권한 자가 누구요?\""]], [["“No other than your friend, Monte Cristo.”", "\"다름 아닌 당신 친구, 몬테크리스토였소.\""]], [["“The Count of Monte Cristo told you to write to Yanina?”", "\"몬테크리스토 백작이 야니나로 편지를 보내라고 했단 말이오?\""]], [["“Yes; and I wrote, and will show you my correspondence, if you like.”", "\"그렇소. 그래서 내가 편지를 썼지요. 원한다면 그 왕복 서신을 보여 드리리다.\""]], [["Albert and Beauchamp looked at each other.", "알베르와 보샹이 서로를 마주 보았다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"이보시오,\""], ["said Beauchamp, who had not yet spoken, “you appear to accuse the count, who is absent from Paris at this moment, and cannot justify himself.”", "그때껏 한마디도 하지 않았던 보샹이 입을 열었다. \"당신은 지금 백작을 고발하는 듯한데, 그분은 지금 파리에 없어 자신을 변호할 수도 없소.\""]], [["“I accuse no one, sir,”", "\"나는 아무도 고발하지 않소.\""], ["said Danglars; “I relate, and I will repeat before the count what I have said to you.”", "당글라르가 말했다. \"그저 사실을 전할 뿐이고, 당신들에게 한 말을 백작 앞에서도 똑같이 되풀이하겠소.\""]], [["“Does the count know what answer you received?”", "\"당신이 받은 답을 백작도 알고 있소?\""]], [["“Yes; I showed it to him.”", "\"그렇소. 그에게 보여 주었으니까.\""]], [["“Did he know my father’s Christian name was Fernand, and his family name Mondego?”", "\"내 아버지의 세례명이 페르낭이고, 성이 몬데고라는 걸 그가 알고 있었소?\""]], [["“Yes, I had told him that long since, and I did only what any other would have done in my circumstances, and perhaps less.", "\"그렇소, 내 진작 그에게 말해 두었지. 나는 같은 처지라면 누구라도 했을 일을 했을 뿐이오. 어쩌면 그보다 덜한 일을 했을지도."], ["When, the day after the arrival of this answer, your father came by the advice of Monte Cristo to ask my daughter’s hand for you, I decidedly refused him, but without any explanation or exposure.", "그 답장이 도착한 이튿날, 당신 아버지가 몬테크리스토의 권유로 찾아와 내 딸을 당신에게 달라고 청했을 때, 나는 단호히 거절했소. 하지만 아무런 해명도, 폭로도 하지 않았지."], ["In short, why should I have any more to do with the affair?", "한마디로, 내가 이 일에 더 끼어들 까닭이 어디 있겠소?"], ["How did the honor or disgrace of M. de Morcerf affect me?", "모르세르 씨의 명예든 치욕이든 그게 나와 무슨 상관이오?"], ["It neither increased nor decreased my income.”", "내 수입이 늘지도 줄지도 않는데 말이오.\""]], [["Albert felt the blood mounting to his brow; there was no doubt upon the subject.", "알베르는 피가 이마로 솟구치는 것을 느꼈다. 이 문제에 더는 의심의 여지가 없었다."], ["Danglars defended himself with the baseness, but at the same time with the assurance, of a man who speaks the truth, at least in part, if not wholly—not for conscience’ sake, but through fear.", "당글라르는 비열하게, 그러나 동시에 적어도 일부는, 전부는 아닐지언정, 진실을 말하는 사람의 확신을 지니고 자신을 변호했다. 양심 때문이 아니라 두려움 때문이었지만."], ["Besides, what was Morcerf seeking?", "게다가 모르세르가 찾는 게 무엇이었던가?"], ["It was not whether Danglars or Monte Cristo was more or less guilty; it was a man who would answer for the offence, whether trifling or serious; it was a man who would fight, and it was evident Danglars would not fight.", "당글라르와 몬테크리스토 가운데 누가 더 죄가 무거우냐 가벼우냐가 아니었다. 그 모욕에, 사소하든 중대하든, 책임지고 답할 사람, 결투에 응할 사람이었다. 그런데 당글라르가 결투하지 않으리라는 것은 분명했다."]], [["In addition to this, everything forgotten or unperceived before presented itself now to his recollection.", "게다가 그동안 잊혔거나 미처 알아채지 못했던 모든 일이 이제 그의 기억 속에 떠올랐다."], ["Monte Cristo knew everything, as he had bought the daughter of Ali Pasha; and, knowing everything, he had advised Danglars to write to Yanina.", "몬테크리스토는 알리 파샤의 딸을 사들였으니 모든 것을 알고 있었다. 그리고 모든 것을 알면서도 당글라르에게 야니나로 편지를 쓰라고 권했다."], ["The answer known, he had yielded to Albert’s wish to be introduced to Haydée, and allowed the conversation to turn on the death of Ali, and had not opposed Haydée’s recital (but having, doubtless, warned the young girl, in the few Romaic words he spoke to her, not to implicate Morcerf’s father).", "그 답을 알게 되자, 그는 하이데를 소개받고 싶다는 알베르의 바람을 들어주었고, 대화가 알리의 죽음으로 흘러가도록 내버려 두었으며, 하이데의 이야기를 막지도 않았다(다만 그녀에게 건넨 몇 마디 로마이어로, 모르세르의 아버지를 끌어들이지 말라고 그 처녀에게 일러 두었을 것이 분명했다)."], ["Besides, had he not begged of Morcerf not to mention his father’s name before Haydée?", "게다가 그는 하이데 앞에서 아버지의 이름을 입에 올리지 말라고 모르세르에게 당부하지 않았던가?"], ["Lastly, he had taken Albert to Normandy when he knew the final blow was near.", "마지막으로, 그는 마지막 일격이 임박했음을 알고서 알베르를 노르망디로 데려갔다."], ["There could be no doubt that all had been calculated and previously arranged; Monte Cristo then was in league with his father’s enemies.", "이 모든 것이 계산되고 미리 짜인 일이었음에 의심의 여지가 없었다. 그러니 몬테크리스토는 아버지의 적들과 한패였던 것이다."], ["Albert took Beauchamp aside, and communicated these ideas to him.", "알베르는 보샹을 한쪽으로 데려가 이 생각들을 털어놓았다."]], [["“You are right,” said the latter; “M.", "\"자네 말이 옳아.\" 보샹이 말했다."], ["Danglars has only been a secondary agent in this sad affair, and it is of M.", "\"당글라르 씨는 이 서글픈 일에서 부차적인 대리인에 지나지 않았어."], ["de Monte Cristo that you must demand an explanation.”", "자네가 해명을 요구해야 할 상대는 몬테크리스토 백작일세.\""]], [["Albert turned.", "알베르가 돌아섰다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"이보시오,\""], ["said he to Danglars, “understand that I do not take a final leave of you; I must ascertain if your insinuations are just, and am going now to inquire of the Count of Monte Cristo.”", "그가 당글라르에게 말했다. \"내가 당신과 영영 작별하는 게 아님을 알아 두시오. 당신의 그 암시가 옳은지 반드시 확인해야겠으니, 지금 몬테크리스토 백작에게 가서 따져 보겠소.\""]], [["He bowed to the banker, and went out with Beauchamp, without appearing to notice Cavalcanti.", "그는 은행가에게 고개를 숙여 인사하고는, 카발칸티에게는 눈길도 주지 않은 채 보샹과 함께 밖으로 나갔다."], ["Danglars accompanied him to the door, where he again assured Albert that no motive of personal hatred had influenced him against the Count of Morcerf.", "당글라르가 문까지 그를 배웅하며, 모르세르 백작에게 맞선 데에는 개인적인 증오의 동기가 전혀 없었음을 다시 한번 알베르에게 다짐해 두었다."]], [["At the banker’s door Beauchamp stopped Morcerf.", "은행가의 집 문 앞에서 보샹이 모르세르를 멈춰 세웠다."]], [["“Listen,”", "\"잘 들어.\""], ["said he; “just now I told you it was of M.", "그가 말했다. \"방금 전에 내가 자네한테 말했지."], ["de Monte Cristo you must demand an explanation.”", "해명을 요구해야 할 상대는 몬테크리스토 백작이라고.\""]], [["“Yes; and we are going to his house.”", "\"그래, 그래서 우리가 그의 집으로 가는 거잖나.\""]], [["“Reflect, Morcerf, one moment before you go.”", "\"가기 전에 잠깐만 생각해 보게, 모르세르.\""]], [["“On what shall I reflect?”", "\"무엇을 생각하란 말인가?\""]], [["“On the importance of the step you are taking.”", "\"자네가 지금 내딛으려는 걸음이 얼마나 중대한지를 말일세.\""]], [["“Is it more serious than going to M. Danglars?”", "\"당글라르 씨를 찾아가는 것보다 더 중대한 일인가?\""]], [["“Yes; M.", "\"그래."], ["Danglars is a money-lover, and those who love money, you know, think too much of what they risk to be easily induced to fight a duel.", "당글라르 씨는 돈을 좋아하는 사람이고, 자네도 알다시피 돈을 좋아하는 자들은 자기가 무엇을 잃을지부터 너무 깊이 따지기 때문에 결투에 쉽게 끌려들지 않아."], ["The other is, on the contrary, to all appearance a true nobleman; but do you not fear to find him a bully?”", "반면에 다른 쪽은 누가 봐도 진짜 귀족이야. 그렇지만 그가 거친 싸움꾼일까 봐 두렵지는 않은가?\""]], [["“I only fear one thing; namely, to find a man who will not fight.”", "\"내가 두려운 건 단 한 가지뿐일세. 결투에 응하지 않을 사람을 만나는 것 말이야.\""]], [["“Do not be alarmed,”", "\"걱정하지 말게.\""], ["said Beauchamp; “he will meet you.", "보샹이 말했다. \"그는 자네와 맞설 걸세."], ["My only fear is that he will be too strong for you.”", "내 유일한 걱정은 그가 자네에게 너무 강한 상대라는 점이지.\""]], [["“My friend,”", "\"이보게.\""], ["said Morcerf, with a sweet smile, “that is what I wish.", "모르세르가 부드러운 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"그거야말로 내가 바라는 바일세."], ["The happiest thing that could occur to me, would be to die in my father’s stead; that would save us all.”", "내게 일어날 수 있는 가장 행복한 일은 아버지를 대신해 죽는 것이야. 그러면 우리 모두가 구원받을 테니까.\""]], [["“Your mother would die of grief.”", "\"자네 어머니가 비탄에 잠겨 돌아가실 걸세.\""]], [["“My poor mother!”", "\"가엾은 어머니!\""], ["said Albert, passing his hand across his eyes, “I know she would; but better so than die of shame.”", "알베르가 손으로 두 눈을 가리며 말했다. \"그러시겠지, 나도 알아. 하지만 수치로 돌아가시는 것보다는 그편이 낫지.\""]], [["“Are you quite decided, Albert?”", "\"정말로 마음을 굳혔나, 알베르?\""]], [["“Yes; let us go.”", "\"그래. 가세.\""]], [["“But do you think we shall find the count at home?”", "\"하지만 백작이 집에 있을 거라고 보나?\""]], [["“He intended returning some hours after me, and doubtless he is now at home.”", "\"나보다 몇 시간 뒤에 돌아오겠다고 했으니, 지금쯤은 분명 집에 있을 걸세.\""]], [["They ordered the driver to take them to No. 30 Champs-Élysées.", "두 사람은 마부에게 샹젤리제 30번지로 가자고 일렀다."], ["Beauchamp wished to go in alone, but Albert observed that as this was an unusual circumstance he might be allowed to deviate from the usual etiquette of duels.", "보샹은 혼자 들어가려 했지만, 알베르는 이번 일이 워낙 특별한 상황이니 결투의 통상적인 예법에서 조금 벗어나도 괜찮을 거라고 했다."], ["The cause which the young man espoused was one so sacred that Beauchamp had only to comply with all his wishes; he yielded and contented himself with following Morcerf.", "젊은이가 떠맡은 명분이 너무도 신성한 것이어서, 보샹으로서는 그의 뜻을 모두 따르는 수밖에 없었다. 그는 양보하고 모르세르를 뒤따르는 것으로 만족했다."], ["Albert sprang from the porter’s lodge to the steps.", "알베르는 수위실에서 곧장 현관 계단으로 뛰어올랐다."], ["He was received by Baptistin.", "그를 맞이한 것은 바티스탱이었다."], ["The count had, indeed, just arrived, but he was in his bath, and had forbidden that anyone should be admitted.", "백작은 과연 막 돌아온 참이었지만, 지금 목욕 중이라 누구도 들이지 말라는 분부가 내려져 있었다."]], [["“But after his bath?”", "\"그럼 목욕을 마친 뒤에는요?\""], ["asked Morcerf.", "모르세르가 물었다."]], [["“My master will go to dinner.”", "\"주인님은 저녁 식사를 하실 겁니다.\""]], [["“And after dinner?”", "\"식사 뒤에는요?\""]], [["“He will sleep an hour.”", "\"한 시간 주무실 겁니다.\""]], [["“Then?”", "\"그다음에는요?\""]], [["“He is going to the Opera.”", "\"오페라극장에 가실 겁니다.\""]], [["“Are you sure of it?” asked Albert.", "\"확실한가?\" 알베르가 물었다."]], [["“Quite, sir; my master has ordered his horses at eight o’clock precisely.”", "\"확실합니다. 주인님이 정확히 여덟 시에 마차를 대령하라고 분부하셨습니다.\""]], [["“Very good,”", "\"좋아.\""], ["replied Albert; “that is all I wished to know.”", "알베르가 대답했다. \"내가 알고 싶던 건 그게 전부였네.\""]], [["Then, turning towards Beauchamp, “If you have anything to attend to, Beauchamp, do it directly; if you have any appointment for this evening, defer it till tomorrow.", "그러고는 보샹 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다. \"처리할 일이 있으면, 보샹, 지금 바로 처리하게. 오늘 저녁에 약속이 있다면 내일로 미루고."], ["I depend on you to accompany me to the Opera; and if you can, bring Château-Renaud with you.”", "자네가 나와 함께 오페라극장에 가 주기를 바라네. 그리고 가능하다면 샤토르노도 데려와 주게.\""]], [["Beauchamp availed himself of Albert’s permission, and left him, promising to call for him at a quarter before eight.", "보샹은 알베르의 허락을 받아들여 그의 곁을 떠나면서, 여덟 시 십오 분 전에 데리러 오겠다고 약속했다."], ["On his return home, Albert expressed his wish to Franz Debray, and Morrel, to see them at the Opera that evening.", "집으로 돌아온 알베르는 프란츠와 데브레, 모렐에게 그날 저녁 오페라극장에서 보고 싶다는 뜻을 전했다."], ["Then he went to see his mother, who since the events of the day before had refused to see anyone, and had kept her room.", "그런 다음 어머니를 보러 갔다. 어머니는 전날의 사건 이후 누구도 만나기를 거부하며 방에 틀어박혀 있었다."], ["He found her in bed, overwhelmed with grief at this public humiliation.", "어머니는 그 공개적인 모욕에 비탄으로 짓눌린 채 침대에 누워 있었다."]], [["The sight of Albert produced the effect which might naturally be expected on Mercédès; she pressed her son’s hand and sobbed aloud, but her tears relieved her.", "알베르의 모습은 메르세데스에게 당연히 예상될 만한 반응을 불러일으켰다. 어머니는 아들의 손을 꼭 쥐고 소리 내어 흐느꼈지만, 그 눈물이 마음을 한결 가볍게 해 주었다."], ["Albert stood one moment speechless by the side of his mother’s bed.", "알베르는 잠시 어머니의 침대 곁에 말없이 서 있었다."], ["It was evident from his pale face and knit brows that his resolution to revenge himself was growing weaker.", "창백한 얼굴과 찌푸린 눈썹으로 보아, 복수하겠다는 그의 결심이 점점 약해지고 있는 것이 분명했다."]], [["“My dear mother,”", "\"어머니.\""], ["said he, “do you know if M. de Morcerf has any enemy?”", "그가 말했다. \"혹시 모르세르 씨에게 적이 있는지 아십니까?\""]], [["Mercédès started; she noticed that the young man did not say “my father.”", "메르세데스가 흠칫했다. 아들이 \"아버지\"라고 말하지 않았다는 것을 알아챈 것이다."]], [["“My son,”", "\"얘야.\""], ["she said, “persons in the count’s situation have many secret enemies.", "어머니가 말했다. \"백작 같은 처지에 있는 사람에게는 은밀한 적이 많은 법이란다."], ["Those who are known are not the most dangerous.”", "드러난 적이 가장 위험한 적은 아니지.\""]], [["“I know it, and appeal to your penetration.", "\"저도 압니다. 그래서 어머니의 통찰력에 기대는 거예요."], ["You are of so superior a mind, nothing escapes you.”", "어머니는 워낙 뛰어난 분이라 무엇 하나 놓치는 법이 없으니까요.\""]], [["“Why do you say so?”", "\"어째서 그런 말을 하니?\""]], [["“Because, for instance, you noticed on the evening of the ball we gave, that M. de Monte Cristo would eat nothing in our house.”", "\"이를테면, 우리가 무도회를 열었던 그날 저녁, 몬테크리스토 백작이 우리 집에서 아무것도 입에 대지 않았다는 걸 어머니는 알아채셨잖아요.\""]], [["Mercédès raised herself on her feverish arm.", "메르세데스가 열에 들뜬 팔로 몸을 일으켰다."]], [["“M. de Monte Cristo!”", "\"몬테크리스토 백작!\""], ["she exclaimed; “and how is he connected with the question you asked me?”", "어머니가 외쳤다. \"그가 네가 방금 물은 일과 무슨 상관이 있다는 거니?\""]], [["40219m\n“You know, mother, M.", "\"어머니도 아시잖아요."], ["de Monte Cristo is almost an Oriental, and it is customary with the Orientals to secure full liberty for revenge by not eating or drinking in the houses of their enemies.”", "몬테크리스토 백작은 거의 동방 사람이나 다름없어요. 그리고 동방 사람들은 적의 집에서 먹지도 마시지도 않음으로써 복수할 완전한 자유를 확보하는 관습이 있지요.\""]], [["“Do you say M. de Monte Cristo is our enemy?”", "\"몬테크리스토 백작이 우리의 적이라는 말이냐?\""], ["replied Mercédès, becoming paler than the sheet which covered her.", "메르세데스가 자기를 덮은 시트보다 더 창백해지며 대꾸했다."], ["“Who told you so? Why, you are mad, Albert! M.", "\"누가 그런 말을 하더냐? 너 미쳤구나, 알베르!"], ["de Monte Cristo has only shown us kindness. M.", "몬테크리스토 백작은 우리에게 호의만 베풀었어."], ["de Monte Cristo saved your life; you yourself presented him to us.", "몬테크리스토 백작은 네 목숨을 구해 주었고, 그를 우리에게 소개한 사람은 바로 너잖니."], ["Oh, I entreat you, my son, if you had entertained such an idea, dispel it; and my counsel to you—nay, my prayer—is to retain his friendship.”", "오, 부탁이다, 얘야. 만약 네가 그런 생각을 품었다면 떨쳐 버리렴. 그리고 너에게 권하는 것은, 아니 간곡히 비는 것은, 그와의 우정을 지키라는 거야.\""]], [["“Mother,”", "\"어머니.\""], ["replied the young man, “you have special reasons for telling me to conciliate that man.”", "젊은이가 대답했다. \"그 사람과 화해하라고 제게 말씀하시는 데에는 무슨 특별한 이유가 있으시군요.\""]], [["“I?” said Mercédès, blushing as rapidly as she had turned pale, and again becoming paler than ever.", "\"내가?\" 메르세데스가 조금 전 창백해졌던 것만큼이나 빠르게 얼굴을 붉혔다가, 다시 그 어느 때보다 더 창백해지며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, doubtless; and is it not that he may never do us any harm?”", "\"네, 분명히요. 그가 우리에게 결코 해를 끼치지 못하도록 하려는 것 아닙니까?\""]], [["Mercédès shuddered, and, fixing on her son a scrutinizing gaze, “You speak strangely,”", "메르세데스가 몸서리쳤다. 그러고는 아들에게 살피는 듯한 시선을 고정한 채 말했다."], ["said she to Albert, “and you appear to have some singular prejudices.", "\"이상한 말을 하는구나. 게다가 너는 무슨 묘한 편견을 품고 있는 것 같아."], ["What has the count done?", "백작이 무슨 짓을 했다는 거니?"], ["Three days since you were with him in Normandy; only three days since we looked on him as our best friend.”", "사흘 전만 해도 너는 그와 노르망디에 함께 있었어. 불과 사흘 전만 해도 우리는 그를 가장 좋은 벗으로 여겼잖니.\""]], [["An ironical smile passed over Albert’s lips.", "알베르의 입가에 비웃는 듯한 미소가 스쳤다."], ["Mercédès saw it and with the double instinct of woman and mother guessed all; but as she was prudent and strong-minded she concealed both her sorrows and her fears.", "메르세데스는 그것을 보았고, 여자이자 어머니로서의 이중의 본능으로 모든 것을 짐작했다. 하지만 그녀는 신중하고 굳센 사람이었기에 자신의 슬픔도 두려움도 감추었다."], ["Albert was silent; an instant after, the countess resumed:", "알베르는 입을 다물었다. 잠시 후 백작 부인이 다시 입을 열었다."]], [["“You came to inquire after my health; I will candidly acknowledge that I am not well.", "\"너는 내 안부를 물으러 왔지. 솔직히 말하마, 나는 몸이 좋지 않단다."], ["You should install yourself here, and cheer my solitude.", "너는 여기 머물면서 내 외로움을 달래 주어야 해."], ["I do not wish to be left alone.”", "혼자 남겨지고 싶지 않구나.\""]], [["“Mother,”", "\"어머니.\""], ["said the young man, “you know how gladly I would obey your wish, but an urgent and important affair obliges me to leave you for the whole evening.”", "젊은이가 말했다. \"어머니의 뜻이라면 제가 얼마나 기쁘게 따를지 아시잖아요. 하지만 급하고도 중대한 일이 있어 오늘 저녁 내내 어머니 곁을 떠나 있어야 합니다.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래.\""], ["replied Mercédès, sighing, “go, Albert; I will not make you a slave to your filial piety.”", "메르세데스가 한숨을 쉬며 대답했다. \"가렴, 알베르. 너를 효심의 노예로 묶어 두지는 않으마.\""]], [["Albert pretended he did not hear, bowed to his mother, and quitted her.", "알베르는 못 들은 척하며 어머니에게 인사하고 그 곁을 떠났다."], ["Scarcely had he shut her door, when Mercédès called a confidential servant, and ordered him to follow Albert wherever he should go that evening, and to come and tell her immediately what he observed.", "그가 방문을 닫기가 무섭게 메르세데스는 믿을 만한 하인을 불러, 그날 저녁 알베르가 어디를 가든 따라다니다가 본 것을 즉시 와서 알리라고 일렀다."], ["Then she rang for her lady’s maid, and, weak as she was, she dressed, in order to be ready for whatever might happen.", "그런 다음 하녀를 불러, 몸이 그토록 쇠약한데도 무슨 일이 일어나든 대비할 수 있도록 옷을 차려입었다."], ["The footman’s mission was an easy one.", "하인의 임무는 어렵지 않았다."], ["Albert went to his room, and dressed with unusual care.", "알베르는 자기 방으로 가서 평소와 달리 정성껏 옷을 차려입었다."], ["At ten minutes to eight Beauchamp arrived; he had seen Château-Renaud, who had promised to be in the orchestra before the curtain was raised.", "여덟 시 십 분 전에 보샹이 도착했다. 그는 샤토르노를 만났는데, 샤토르노는 막이 오르기 전에 일층 객석에 와 있겠다고 약속했다."], ["Both got into Albert’s _coupé_; and, as the young man had no reason to conceal where he was going, he called aloud, “To the Opera.”", "두 사람은 알베르의 사륜마차에 올랐다. 젊은이는 자기가 어디로 가는지 숨길 이유가 없었으므로 큰 소리로 외쳤다."], ["In his impatience he arrived before the beginning of the performance. 40221m", "\"오페라극장으로.\" 그는 조바심에 공연이 시작되기도 전에 도착했다."]], [["Château-Renaud was at his post; apprised by Beauchamp of the circumstances, he required no explanation from Albert.", "샤토르노는 제자리에 있었다. 보샹에게서 자초지종을 전해 들은 터라, 그는 알베르에게 따로 설명을 구할 필요가 없었다."], ["The conduct of the son in seeking to avenge his father was so natural that Château-Renaud did not seek to dissuade him, and was content with renewing his assurances of devotion. Debray was not yet come, but Albert knew that he seldom lost a scene at the Opera.", "아버지의 원수를 갚으려는 아들의 행동은 너무도 당연한 것이어서, 샤토르노는 그를 말리려 들지 않았고, 헌신을 다시 한번 다짐하는 것으로 만족했다. 데브레는 아직 오지 않았지만, 알베르는 그가 오페라극장의 장면을 좀처럼 놓치는 법이 없다는 것을 알고 있었다."]], [["Albert wandered about the theatre until the curtain was drawn up.", "알베르는 막이 오를 때까지 극장 안을 서성였다."], ["He hoped to meet with M. de Monte Cristo either in the lobby or on the stairs.", "그는 복도에서든 계단에서든 몬테크리스토 백작과 마주치기를 바랐다."], ["The bell summoned him to his seat, and he entered the orchestra with Château-Renaud and Beauchamp.", "종이 울려 자리로 돌아가야 했고, 그는 샤토르노와 보샹과 함께 일층 객석으로 들어갔다."], ["But his eyes scarcely quitted the box between the columns, which remained obstinately closed during the whole of the first act.", "하지만 그의 두 눈은 기둥 사이의 그 칸막이 좌석에서 좀처럼 떨어지지 않았다. 그 자리는 1막 내내 한사코 닫혀 있었다."], ["At last, as Albert was looking at his watch for about the hundredth time, at the beginning of the second act the door opened, and Monte Cristo entered, dressed in black, and, leaning over the front of the box, looked around the pit.", "마침내, 알베르가 백 번쯤이나 시계를 들여다보고 있을 때, 2막이 시작되면서 문이 열렸다. 검은 옷을 입은 몬테크리스토가 들어오더니 좌석 앞쪽으로 몸을 기울이고 일층 관람석을 둘러보았다."], ["Morrel followed him, and looked also for his sister and brother in-law; he soon discovered them in another box, and kissed his hand to them.", "모렐이 그를 뒤따라 들어와 누이와 매제를 찾았다. 그는 곧 다른 칸막이 좌석에서 그들을 발견하고는 손에 입을 맞춰 인사를 보냈다."]], [["The count, in his survey of the pit, encountered a pale face and threatening eyes, which evidently sought to gain his attention.", "백작은 관람석을 살피다가 창백한 얼굴과 위협적인 눈초리와 마주쳤다. 그 눈은 분명 그의 주의를 끌려 하고 있었다."], ["He recognized Albert, but thought it better not to notice him, as he looked so angry and discomposed.", "그는 알베르임을 알아보았지만, 그가 너무나 성나고 흐트러진 모습이었기에 못 본 척하는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했다."], ["Without communicating his thoughts to his companion, he sat down, drew out his opera-glass, and looked another way.", "그는 동행에게 속내를 내비치지 않은 채 자리에 앉아 오페라글라스를 꺼내고는 다른 쪽을 바라보았다."], ["Although apparently not noticing Albert, he did not, however, lose sight of him, and when the curtain fell at the end of the second act, he saw him leave the orchestra with his two friends.", "겉으로는 알베르를 주목하지 않는 듯했지만, 사실은 그에게서 한시도 눈을 떼지 않았다. 그래서 2막이 끝나 막이 내릴 때, 그는 알베르가 두 친구와 함께 일층 객석을 떠나는 것을 보았다."], ["Then his head was seen passing at the back of the boxes, and the count knew that the approaching storm was intended to fall on him.", "이윽고 칸막이 좌석들 뒤쪽으로 그의 머리가 지나가는 것이 보였고, 백작은 다가오는 폭풍이 자기에게 떨어질 작정임을 알았다."], ["He was at the moment conversing cheerfully with Morrel, but he was well prepared for what might happen.", "그는 그 순간 모렐과 유쾌하게 이야기를 나누고 있었지만, 무슨 일이 벌어지든 단단히 대비하고 있었다."]], [["The door opened, and Monte Cristo, turning round, saw Albert, pale and trembling, followed by Beauchamp and Château-Renaud.", "문이 열렸고, 몬테크리스토는 돌아보다가 창백하게 떨고 있는 알베르를 보았다. 그 뒤로 보샹과 샤토르노가 따라오고 있었다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"오.\""], ["cried he, with that benevolent politeness which distinguished his salutation from the common civilities of the world, “my cavalier has attained his object.", "그가 외쳤다. 그 인사를 세상의 흔한 인사치레와 구별 짓는 그 너그러운 정중함이 묻어났다. \"내 기사께서 마침내 목적을 이루셨군."], ["Good-evening, M. de Morcerf.”", "안녕하시오, 모르세르 씨.\""]], [["The countenance of this man, who possessed such extraordinary control over his feelings, expressed the most perfect cordiality.", "자신의 감정을 그토록 비범하게 다스리는 이 사내의 표정에는 더없이 완벽한 다정함이 어려 있었다."], ["Morrel only then recollected the letter he had received from the viscount, in which, without assigning any reason, he begged him to go to the Opera, but he understood that something terrible was brooding.", "모렐은 그제야 자작에게서 받은 편지가 떠올랐다. 그 편지에는 아무 이유도 적지 않은 채 오페라극장으로 와 달라는 부탁만 적혀 있었다. 하지만 그는 무언가 끔찍한 일이 꾸며지고 있음을 깨달았다."]], [["“We are not come here, sir, to exchange hypocritical expressions of politeness, or false professions of friendship,”", "\"우리는 위선적인 인사치레나 거짓 우정을 주고받으러 온 것이 아닙니다.\" 알베르가 말했다."], ["said Albert, “but to demand an explanation.”", "\"해명을 요구하러 온 겁니다.\""]], [["The young man’s trembling voice was scarcely audible.", "젊은이의 떨리는 목소리는 거의 들리지 않을 정도였다."]], [["“An explanation at the Opera?”", "\"오페라극장에서 해명을 하라고요?\""], ["said the count, with that calm tone and penetrating eye which characterize the man who knows his cause is good.", "백작이 말했다. 자기 명분이 옳다는 것을 아는 사람 특유의 그 차분한 어조와 꿰뚫어 보는 눈빛으로."], ["“Little acquainted as I am with the habits of Parisians, I should not have thought this the place for such a demand.”", "\"파리 사람들의 풍습에는 익숙하지 못한 나로서도, 여기가 그런 요구를 할 만한 자리라고는 생각지 못했겠소만.\""]], [["“Still, if people will shut themselves up,”", "\"그래도, 어떤 사람들이 제 집에 틀어박혀서는,\""], ["said Albert, “and cannot be seen because they are bathing, dining, or asleep, we must avail ourselves of the opportunity whenever they are to be seen.”", "알베르가 말했다. \"목욕 중이라느니 식사 중이라느니 잠을 잔다느니 하며 만나 주지 않는다면, 그를 만날 수 있는 기회가 생길 때마다 그 틈을 이용해야지요.\""]], [["“I am not difficult of access, sir; for yesterday, if my memory does not deceive me, you were at my house.”", "\"나는 만나기 어려운 사람이 아니오. 내 기억이 틀리지 않다면, 어제만 해도 당신은 내 집에 있었으니까.\""]], [["“Yesterday I was at your house, sir,”", "\"어제 제가 당신 집에 있었던 건,\""], ["said the young man; “because then I knew not who you were.”", "젊은이가 말했다. \"그때는 당신이 누구인지 몰랐기 때문입니다.\""]], [["In pronouncing these words Albert had raised his voice so as to be heard by those in the adjoining boxes and in the lobby.", "이 말을 내뱉으면서 알베르는 옆 칸막이 좌석들과 복도에 있는 사람들에게까지 들리도록 목소리를 높였다."], ["Thus the attention of many was attracted by this altercation.", "그리하여 이 언쟁에 많은 사람의 이목이 쏠렸다."]], [["“Where are you come from, sir?", "\"당신, 대체 어디서 굴러먹다 온 거요?\""], ["“ said Monte Cristo “You do not appear to be in the possession of your senses.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"제정신이 아닌 것 같소만.\""]], [["“Provided I understand your perfidy, sir, and succeed in making you understand that I will be revenged, I shall be reasonable enough,”", "\"당신의 배신을 제가 알아챘고, 또 내가 반드시 복수하리라는 걸 당신이 깨닫게 만들기만 한다면, 저는 충분히 분별 있게 굴 겁니다.\""], ["said Albert furiously.", "알베르가 격분하여 말했다."]], [["“I do not understand you, sir,”", "\"무슨 말인지 모르겠소.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo; “and if I did, your tone is too high.", "몬테크리스토가 대꾸했다. \"설령 안다 해도, 당신 목소리가 너무 높소."], ["I am at home here, and I alone have a right to raise my voice above another’s.", "여기는 내 자리고, 남보다 목소리를 높일 권리가 있는 사람은 오직 나뿐이오."], ["Leave the box, sir!”", "이 좌석에서 나가시오!\""]], [["Monte Cristo pointed towards the door with the most commanding dignity.", "몬테크리스토가 더없이 위엄 있는 손짓으로 문을 가리켰다."]], [["“Ah, I shall know how to make you leave your home!”", "\"아, 당신 집에서 나오게 만드는 방법쯤은 내가 잘 알지!\""], ["replied Albert, clasping in his convulsed grasp the glove, which Monte Cristo did not lose sight of.", "알베르가 대답하며, 경련하는 손아귀에 장갑을 움켜쥐었다. 몬테크리스토는 그 장갑에서 눈을 떼지 않았다."]], [["“Well, well,”", "\"좋소, 좋아.\""], ["said Monte Cristo quietly, “I see you wish to quarrel with me; but I would give you one piece of advice, which you will do well to keep in mind.", "몬테크리스토가 차분하게 말했다. \"당신이 나와 시비를 벌이고 싶어 하는 건 알겠소. 하지만 한 가지 충고를 해 주겠는데, 잘 새겨 두는 게 좋을 거요."], ["It is in poor taste to make a display of a challenge.", "도전을 떠벌리며 과시하는 건 천박한 짓이오."], ["Display is not becoming to everyone, M. de Morcerf.”", "과시가 누구에게나 어울리는 건 아니거든, 모르세르 씨.\""]], [["At this name a murmur of astonishment passed around the group of spectators of this scene.", "이 이름이 나오자 이 장면을 지켜보던 구경꾼들 사이에 놀란 웅성거림이 번졌다."], ["They had talked of no one but Morcerf the whole day.", "그날 하루 종일 사람들은 모르세르 이야기밖에 하지 않았던 것이다."], ["Albert understood the allusion in a moment, and was about to throw his glove at the count, when Morrel seized his hand, while Beauchamp and Château-Renaud, fearing the scene would surpass the limits of a challenge, held him back.", "알베르는 그 빈정거림을 단번에 알아채고 백작에게 장갑을 던지려 했다. 그때 모렐이 그의 손을 붙잡았고, 보샹과 샤토르노는 이 장면이 도전의 선을 넘을까 두려워 그를 뒤에서 잡아당겼다."], ["But Monte Cristo, without rising, and leaning forward in his chair, merely stretched out his arm and, taking the damp, crushed glove from the clenched hand of the young man:", "그러나 몬테크리스토는 자리에서 일어서지도 않고 의자에 앉은 채 몸만 앞으로 기울여, 팔을 뻗어 젊은이의 움켜쥔 손에서 축축하게 구겨진 장갑을 빼앗아 들었다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"이보시오.\""], ["said he in a solemn tone, “I consider your glove thrown, and will return it to you wrapped around a bullet.", "그가 엄숙한 어조로 말했다. \"나는 당신 장갑이 던져진 것으로 여기겠소. 그리고 탄알로 둘둘 감아서 돌려주리다."], ["Now leave me or I will summon my servants to throw you out at the door.”", "자, 이제 나가시오. 그러지 않으면 하인들을 불러 당신을 문밖으로 내던지게 하겠소.\""]], [["Wild, almost unconscious, and with eyes inflamed, Albert stepped back, and Morrel closed the door.", "알베르는 거의 정신이 나간 듯 격분하여 두 눈에 불을 켠 채 뒷걸음질 쳤고, 모렐이 문을 닫았다."], ["Monte Cristo took up his glass again as if nothing had happened; his face was like marble, and his heart was like bronze.", "몬테크리스토는 아무 일도 없었다는 듯 다시 오페라글라스를 집어 들었다. 그의 얼굴은 대리석 같았고, 심장은 청동 같았다."], ["Morrel whispered, “What have you done to him?”", "모렐이 속삭였다. \"그에게 무슨 짓을 하신 겁니까?\""]], [["“I? Nothing—at least personally,”", "\"내가요? 아무것도—적어도 직접적으로는 아무것도 하지 않았소.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“But there must be some cause for this strange scene.”", "\"하지만 이런 기이한 장면에는 무슨 까닭이 있을 텐데요.\""]], [["“The Count of Morcerf’s adventure exasperates the young man.”", "\"모르세르 백작의 일이 저 젊은이를 분노케 한 거요.\""]], [["“Have you anything to do with it?”", "\"백작께서 그 일과 무슨 관계가 있습니까?\""]], [["“It was through Haydée that the Chamber was informed of his father’s treason.”", "\"의회에 그 아버지의 배신이 알려진 건 하이데를 통해서였소.\""]], [["“Indeed?” said Morrel.", "\"정말입니까?\" 모렐이 말했다."], ["“I had been told, but would not credit it, that the Grecian slave I have seen with you here in this very box was the daughter of Ali Pasha.”", "\"바로 이 좌석에서 백작과 함께 있는 걸 본 그 그리스인 노예가 알리 파샤의 딸이라는 얘기를 듣긴 했지만, 차마 믿지 못했습니다.\""]], [["“It is true, nevertheless.”", "\"그렇지만 사실이오.\""]], [["“Then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["said Morrel, “I understand it all, and this scene was premeditated.”", "모렐이 말했다. \"이제 다 알겠습니다. 이 장면은 미리 계획된 것이었군요.\""]], [["“How so?”", "\"어째서 그렇소?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["Albert wrote to request me to come to the Opera, doubtless that I might be a witness to the insult he meant to offer you.”", "알베르가 오페라극장으로 와 달라고 제게 편지를 썼습니다. 분명 그가 백작께 가하려는 모욕을 제가 증인으로 지켜보게 하려는 것이었겠지요.\""]], [["“Probably,” said Monte Cristo with his imperturbable tranquillity.", "\"아마 그렇겠지요.\" 몬테크리스토가 그 흔들림 없는 평정으로 말했다."]], [["“But what shall you do with him?”", "\"그런데 그를 어떻게 하실 겁니까?\""]], [["“With whom?”", "\"누구를 말이오?\""]], [["“With Albert.”", "\"알베르 말입니다.\""]], [["“What shall I do with Albert?", "\"알베르를 어떻게 하겠느냐고요?"], ["As certainly, Maximilian, as I now press your hand, I shall kill him before ten o’clock tomorrow morning.”", "막시밀리앙, 지금 내가 당신의 손을 쥐고 있는 것만큼이나 확실하게, 내일 아침 열 시가 되기 전에 그를 죽일 거요.\""], ["Morrel, in his turn, took Monte Cristo’s hand in both of his, and he shuddered to feel how cold and steady it was.", "이번에는 모렐이 두 손으로 몬테크리스토의 손을 잡았고, 그 손이 얼마나 차갑고 흔들림 없는지를 느끼고는 몸서리쳤다."]], [["“Ah, count,”", "\"아, 백작.\""], ["said he, “his father loves him so much!”", "그가 말했다. \"그 애 아버지가 그 애를 그토록 사랑하는데요!\""]], [["“Do not speak to me of that,”", "\"그 얘기는 꺼내지 마시오.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, with the first movement of anger he had betrayed; “I will make him suffer.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. 그가 처음으로 드러낸 분노의 기색이었다. \"나는 그를 고통받게 할 거요.\""]], [["Morrel, amazed, let fall Monte Cristo’s hand.", "모렐은 깜짝 놀라 몬테크리스토의 손을 놓쳤다."], ["“Count, count!”", "\"백작, 백작!\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Dear Maximilian,”", "\"막시밀리앙.\""], ["interrupted the count, “listen how adorably Duprez is singing that line,—", "백작이 그의 말을 가로막았다. \"뒤프레가 저 대목을 얼마나 황홀하게 부르는지 들어 보시오."]], [["‘O Mathilde!", "'오 마틸드!"], ["idole de mon âme!’", "내 영혼의 우상이여!'"]], [["“I was the first to discover Duprez at Naples, and the first to applaud him.", "나는 나폴리에서 뒤프레를 제일 먼저 발견한 사람이고, 제일 먼저 그에게 박수를 보낸 사람이오."], ["Bravo, bravo!”", "브라보, 브라보!\""]], [["Morrel saw it was useless to say more, and refrained.", "모렐은 더 말해 봐야 소용없음을 깨닫고 입을 다물었다."], ["The curtain, which had risen at the close of the scene with Albert, again fell, and a rap was heard at the door.", "알베르와의 장면이 끝날 무렵 올라갔던 막이 다시 내려왔고, 문에서 노크 소리가 들렸다."]], [["“Come in,”", "\"들어오시오.\""], ["said Monte Cristo with a voice that betrayed not the least emotion; and immediately Beauchamp appeared.", "몬테크리스토가 조금의 동요도 내비치지 않는 목소리로 말했다. 그러자 곧 보샹이 나타났다."], ["“Good-evening, M. Beauchamp,”", "\"안녕하시오, 보샹 씨.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, as if this was the first time he had seen the journalist that evening; “be seated.”", "몬테크리스토가, 마치 그날 저녁 그 기자를 처음 보기라도 하는 듯이 말했다. \"앉으시오.\""]], [["Beauchamp bowed, and, sitting down, “Sir,”", "보샹이 고개를 숙이고 자리에 앉으며 말했다."], ["said he, “I just now accompanied M. de Morcerf, as you saw.”", "\"백작님, 보셨다시피 저는 방금 모르세르 씨와 동행했습니다.\""]], [["“And that means,”", "\"그러니까 그 말은,\""], ["replied Monte Cristo, laughing, “that you had, probably, just dined together.", "몬테크리스토가 웃으며 대꾸했다. \"아마 방금 함께 저녁 식사를 했다는 뜻이겠군요."], ["I am happy to see, M. Beauchamp, that you are more sober than he was.”", "보샹 씨, 당신이 그 사람보다 더 정신이 멀쩡한 걸 보니 다행이오.\""]], [["“Sir,” said M.", "\"백작님.\" 보샹 씨가 말했다."], ["Beauchamp, “Albert was wrong, I acknowledge, to betray so much anger, and I come, on my own account, to apologize for him.", "\"알베르가 그토록 분노를 드러낸 건 잘못이었습니다. 인정합니다. 그래서 제가 제 몫으로 그를 대신해 사과드리러 왔습니다."], ["And having done so, entirely on my own account, be it understood, I would add that I believe you too gentlemanly to refuse giving him some explanation concerning your connection with Yanina.", "그리고 이렇게 사과를 드리고 나서—어디까지나 순전히 제 몫으로 드리는 것임을 알아주십시오—한마디 덧붙이자면, 저는 백작님이 너무도 신사다운 분이라, 야니나와의 관계에 대해 그에게 어떤 해명을 해 주시기를 거절하지 않으시리라 믿습니다."], ["Then I will add two words about the young Greek girl.”", "그런 다음 그 젊은 그리스 아가씨에 대해서도 두어 마디 덧붙이고자 합니다.\""]], [["Monte Cristo motioned him to be silent. “Come,”", "몬테크리스토가 그에게 입을 다물라는 손짓을 했다."], ["said he, laughing, “there are all my hopes about to be destroyed.”", "\"원, 이거.\" 그가 웃으며 말했다. \"내 모든 기대가 무너지려 하는군.\""]], [["“How so?” asked Beauchamp.", "\"어째서요?\" 보샹이 물었다."]], [["“Doubtless you wish to make me appear a very eccentric character.", "\"당신은 분명 나를 아주 별난 인물로 보이게 하고 싶어 했을 거요."], ["I am, in your opinion, a Lara, a Manfred, a Lord Ruthven; then, just as I am arriving at the climax, you defeat your own end, and seek to make an ordinary man of me.", "당신 생각에 나는 라라이고, 만프레드이고, 러스벤 경이지요. 그런데 막 내가 그 절정에 다다르려는 찰나에, 당신이 스스로 그 목적을 무너뜨리고 나를 평범한 사내로 만들려 하는구려."], ["You bring me down to your own level, and demand explanations!", "나를 당신과 같은 수준으로 끌어내리고는 해명을 요구하다니!"], ["Indeed, M. Beauchamp, it is quite laughable.”", "정말이지, 보샹 씨, 우스운 노릇이오.\""]], [["“Yet,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["replied Beauchamp haughtily, “there are occasions when probity commands——”", "보샹이 거만하게 대꾸했다. \"정직함이 명하는 경우도 있는 법이지요——\""]], [["“M. Beauchamp,”", "\"보샹 씨.\""], ["interposed this strange man, “the Count of Monte Cristo bows to none but the Count of Monte Cristo himself.", "이 기이한 사내가 끼어들었다. \"몬테크리스토 백작은 오직 몬테크리스토 백작 자신에게만 머리를 숙이오."], ["Say no more, I entreat you.", "더는 말하지 마시오, 부탁이오."], ["I do what I please, M. Beauchamp, and it is always well done.”", "나는 내가 하고 싶은 대로 하고, 그것은 언제나 잘한 일이오, 보샹 씨.\""]], [["“Sir,”", "\"백작님.\""], ["replied the young man, “honest men are not to be paid with such coin.", "젊은이가 대답했다. \"정직한 사람을 그런 식의 돈으로 치를 수는 없습니다."], ["I require honorable guaranties.”", "저는 명예로운 보증을 요구합니다.\""]], [["“I am, sir, a living guaranty,”", "\"이보시오, 나 자신이 살아 있는 보증이오.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo, motionless, but with a threatening look; “we have both blood in our veins which we wish to shed—that is our mutual guaranty.", "몬테크리스토가 꼼짝도 하지 않은 채, 그러나 위협적인 눈빛으로 대답했다. \"우리 둘 다 흘리고 싶어 하는 피가 핏줄 속에 흐르고 있소—그게 바로 우리 서로의 보증이오."], ["Tell the viscount so, and that tomorrow, before ten o’clock, I shall see what color his is.”", "자작에게 그렇게 전하시오. 그리고 내일 열 시가 되기 전에, 그자의 피가 무슨 색인지 내가 직접 보게 되리라고도.\""]], [["“Then I have only to make arrangements for the duel,”", "\"그럼 저는 결투를 주선하기만 하면 되겠군요.\""], ["said Beauchamp.", "보샹이 말했다."]], [["“It is quite immaterial to me,”", "\"내게는 아무래도 상관없는 일이오.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “and it was very unnecessary to disturb me at the Opera for such a trifle.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그리고 그런 사소한 일로 오페라극장까지 나를 성가시게 할 필요는 전혀 없었소."], ["In France people fight with the sword or pistol, in the colonies with the carbine, in Arabia with the dagger.", "프랑스에서는 검이나 권총으로 싸우고, 식민지에서는 기병총으로, 아라비아에서는 단검으로 싸우지요. 당신 의뢰인에게 전하시오."], ["Tell your client that, although I am the insulted party, in order to carry out my eccentricity, I leave him the choice of arms, and will accept without discussion, without dispute, anything, even combat by drawing lots, which is always stupid, but with me different from other people, as I am sure to gain.”", "비록 모욕당한 쪽은 나지만, 내 별난 기질을 끝까지 밀고 나가기 위해 무기 선택권은 그에게 넘기겠다고. 그리고 무엇이든, 심지어 늘 어리석은 짓인 제비뽑기로 승부를 가르는 것까지도, 군말 없이 따지지 않고 받아들이겠다고. 다만 나의 경우는 다른 사람들과 달라서, 반드시 이길 테니까.\""]], [["“Sure to gain!” repeated Beauchamp, looking with amazement at the count.", "\"반드시 이긴다고요!\" 보샹이 깜짝 놀라 백작을 바라보며 되뇌었다."]], [["“Certainly,”", "\"물론이오.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, slightly shrugging his shoulders; “otherwise I would not fight with M.", "몬테크리스토가 어깨를 살짝 으쓱하며 말했다. \"그렇지 않다면 모르세르 씨와 싸우지도 않을 거요."], ["de Morcerf. I shall kill him—I cannot help it.", "나는 그를 죽일 거요—어쩔 수 없는 일이오."], ["Only by a single line this evening at my house let me know the arms and the hour; I do not like to be kept waiting.”", "오늘 저녁 내 집으로 무기와 시각만 단 한 줄 적어 보내 주시오. 나는 기다리는 걸 좋아하지 않으니까.\""]], [["“Pistols, then, at eight o’clock, in the Bois de Vincennes,”", "\"그럼 권총으로, 여덟 시에, 뱅센 숲에서.\" 보샹이 완전히 당황한 채 말했다."], ["said Beauchamp, quite disconcerted, not knowing if he was dealing with an arrogant braggadocio or a supernatural being.", "그는 자기가 오만한 허풍쟁이를 상대하는 것인지, 아니면 초자연적인 존재를 상대하는 것인지 알 수가 없었다."]], [["“Very well, sir,”", "\"아주 좋소, 이보시오.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"이제 다 정해졌으니, 부디 공연을 좀 볼 수 있게 해 주시오."], ["“Now all that is settled, do let me see the performance, and tell your friend Albert not to come any more this evening; he will hurt himself with all his ill-chosen barbarisms: let him go home and go to sleep.”", "그리고 당신 친구 알베르에게 오늘 저녁엔 더 오지 말라고 전하시오. 그렇게 어설프게 골라 쓴 야만스러운 말들로는 제 몸만 상할 테니까. 집에 가서 잠이나 자라고 하시오.\""]], [["Beauchamp left the box, perfectly amazed.", "보샹은 완전히 어리둥절한 채 좌석을 떠났다."]], [["“Now,”", "\"자.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, turning towards Morrel, “I may depend upon you, may I not?”", "몬테크리스토가 모렐 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다. \"당신을 믿어도 되겠지요?\""]], [["“Certainly,”", "\"물론입니다.\""], ["said Morrel, “I am at your service, count; still——” “What?”", "모렐이 말했다. \"백작께 힘이 되어 드리겠습니다. 다만——\""]], [["“It is desirable I should know the real cause.”", "\"무엇이오?\" \"진짜 까닭을 알았으면 합니다.\""]], [["“That is to say, you would rather not?”", "\"그러니까, 차라리 모르고 싶지는 않다는 거군요?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“The young man himself is acting blindfolded, and knows not the true cause, which is known only to God and to me; but I give you my word, Morrel, that God, who does know it, will be on our side.”", "\"저 젊은이 자신도 눈을 가린 채 행동하고 있고, 참된 까닭을 모르고 있소. 그건 오직 하느님과 나만 아는 일이오. 하지만 맹세하건대, 모렐, 그 까닭을 아시는 하느님께서 우리 편에 서 주실 거요.\""]], [["“Enough,”", "\"됐습니다.\""], ["said Morrel; “who is your second witness?”", "모렐이 말했다. \"두 번째 입회인은 누구입니까?\""]], [["“I know no one in Paris, Morrel, on whom I could confer that honor besides you and your brother Emmanuel.", "\"파리에서 그 영예를 맡길 만한 사람은, 당신과 당신 처남 에마뉘엘 말고는 아는 이가 없소, 모렐."], ["Do you think Emmanuel would oblige me?”", "에마뉘엘이 응해 줄 것 같소?\""]], [["“I will answer for him, count.”", "\"제가 그를 대신해 약속드리지요, 백작.\""]], [["“Well?", "\"좋소?"], ["that is all I require. Tomorrow morning, at seven o’clock, you will be with me, will you not?”", "내게 필요한 건 그게 전부요. 내일 아침 일곱 시에 내 집으로 와 주겠지요?\""]], [["“We will.”", "\"가겠습니다.\""]], [["“Hush, the curtain is rising. Listen!", "\"쉿, 막이 오르고 있소. 들어 보시오!"], ["I never lose a note of this opera if I can avoid it; the music of _William Tell_ is so sweet.”", "나는 가능하면 이 오페라의 음 하나도 놓치는 법이 없소. '빌헬름 텔'의 음악은 정말이지 감미롭거든.\""]], [["# Chapter 89. The Night", "# Chapter 89. The Night"]], [["Monte Cristo waited, according to his usual custom, until Duprez had sung his famous “_Suivez-moi!_” then he rose and went out.", "몬테크리스토는 늘 하던 대로, 뒤프레가 그 유명한 「쉬베모아!」를 부를 때까지 기다렸다가 자리에서 일어나 밖으로 나갔다."], ["Morrel took leave of him at the door, renewing his promise to be with him the next morning at seven o’clock, and to bring Emmanuel.", "모렐이 문 앞에서 작별을 고하며, 이튿날 아침 일곱 시에 에마뉘엘을 데리고 함께 가겠다는 약속을 다시 다졌다."], ["Then he stepped into his _coupé_, calm and smiling, and was at home in five minutes.", "그런 뒤 백작은 차분하고 미소 띤 얼굴로 자기 마차에 올랐고, 오 분 만에 집에 닿았다."], ["No one who knew the count could mistake his expression when, on entering, he said:", "백작을 아는 사람이라면, 그가 들어서며 이렇게 말할 때의 표정을 오해할 리 없었다."]], [["“Ali, bring me my pistols with the ivory cross.”", "\"알리, 상아 십자가가 박힌 권총을 가져오너라.\""]], [["Ali brought the box to his master, who examined the weapons with a solicitude very natural to a man who is about to intrust his life to a little powder and shot.", "알리가 주인에게 권총 상자를 가져왔고, 백작은 작은 화약과 총알에 제 목숨을 맡기려는 사람이 으레 보일 법한 세심함으로 무기들을 살폈다."], ["These were pistols of an especial pattern, which Monte Cristo had had made for target practice in his own room.", "이 권총은 특별한 양식으로 제작된 것으로, 몬테크리스토가 자기 방에서 사격 연습을 하려고 따로 만들게 한 것이었다."], ["A cap was sufficient to drive out the bullet, and from the adjoining room no one would have suspected that the count was, as sportsmen would say, keeping his hand in.", "뇌관 하나면 충분히 총알이 발사되었고, 옆방에서는 사냥꾼들의 말마따나 백작이 손을 풀고 있다는 것을 아무도 눈치채지 못했을 것이다."]], [["He was just taking one up and looking for the point to aim at on a little iron plate which served him as a target, when his study door opened, and Baptistin entered.", "그가 막 권총 하나를 집어 들고, 과녁 삼아 쓰는 작은 철판 위에서 겨눌 지점을 찾던 참에, 서재 문이 열리고 바티스탱이 들어왔다."], ["Before he had spoken a word, the count saw in the next room a veiled woman, who had followed closely after Baptistin, and now, seeing the count with a pistol in his hand and swords on the table, rushed in.", "그가 한마디 꺼내기도 전에, 백작은 옆방에서 베일을 쓴 여인이 바티스탱의 뒤를 바짝 따라온 것을 보았다. 여인은 백작이 손에 권총을 들고 탁자 위에 검들이 놓여 있는 것을 보더니 안으로 뛰어들었다."], ["Baptistin looked at his master, who made a sign to him, and he went out, closing the door after him.", "바티스탱이 주인을 쳐다보자, 백작이 손짓을 했고, 그는 문을 닫고 물러갔다."]], [["“Who are you, madame?”", "\"누구십니까, 부인?\""], ["said the count to the veiled woman.", "백작이 베일을 쓴 여인에게 물었다."]], [["40227m\nThe stranger cast one look around her, to be certain that they were quite alone; then bending as if she would have knelt, and joining her hands, she said with an accent of despair:", "낯선 여인이 주위를 한 번 둘러보며, 둘만 있는 것이 확실한지 확인했다. 그러고는 무릎을 꿇으려는 듯 몸을 숙이고 두 손을 모으더니, 절망에 찬 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Edmond, you will not kill my son!”", "\"에드몽, 제 아들을 죽이지 말아 주세요!\""]], [["The count retreated a step, uttered a slight exclamation, and let fall the pistol he held.", "백작이 한 걸음 물러나며 짧은 탄성을 내뱉었고, 손에 들고 있던 권총을 떨어뜨렸다."]], [["“What name did you pronounce then, Madame de Morcerf?” said he.", "\"방금 무슨 이름을 부르셨습니까, 모르세르 부인?\" 그가 말했다."]], [["“Yours!”", "\"당신 이름이에요!\""], ["cried she, throwing back her veil,—“yours, which I alone, perhaps, have not forgotten.", "여인이 베일을 뒤로 젖히며 외쳤다. \"당신 이름이요, 어쩌면 저 혼자만은 잊지 않은 그 이름 말이에요."], ["Edmond, it is not Madame de Morcerf who is come to you, it is Mercédès.”", "에드몽, 당신을 찾아온 것은 모르세르 부인이 아니라 메르세데스예요.\""]], [["“Mercédès is dead, madame,”", "\"메르세데스는 죽었습니다, 부인.\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “I know no one now of that name.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"이제 저는 그런 이름을 가진 사람을 알지 못합니다.\""]], [["“Mercédès lives, sir, and she remembers, for she alone recognized you when she saw you, and even before she saw you, by your voice, Edmond,—by the simple sound of your voice; and from that moment she has followed your steps, watched you, feared you, and she needs not to inquire what hand has dealt the blow which now strikes M.", "\"메르세데스는 살아 있고, 기억하고 있어요. 그 여인만이 당신을 보고 알아보았으니까요. 아니, 보기도 전에, 당신 목소리만으로, 에드몽, 그저 그 목소리 하나만으로 알아챘어요."], ["de Morcerf.”", "그리고 그 순간부터 그 여인은 당신의 발자취를 따라다니며, 당신을 지켜보고, 당신을 두려워했어요. 지금 모르세르 씨를 내리치는 그 손이 누구의 손인지 물어볼 것도 없이요.\""]], [["“Fernand, do you mean?”", "\"페르낭 말씀이군요?\""], ["replied Monte Cristo, with bitter irony; “since we are recalling names, let us remember them all.”", "몬테크리스토가 쓰디쓴 빈정거림을 담아 대답했다. \"이름을 들추는 김에, 다 같이 떠올려 봅시다.\""], ["Monte Cristo had pronounced the name of Fernand with such an expression of hatred that Mercédès felt a thrill of horror run through every vein.", "몬테크리스토가 어찌나 증오에 찬 어조로 페르낭이라는 이름을 입에 올렸던지, 메르세데스는 핏줄 하나하나에 소름이 끼치는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“You see, Edmond, I am not mistaken, and have cause to say, ‘Spare my son!’”", "\"보세요, 에드몽, 제가 틀리지 않았어요. 그러니 제가 '내 아들을 살려 주세요!'라고 말할 자격이 있는 거예요.\""]], [["“And who told you, madame, that I have any hostile intentions against your son?”", "\"그런데 누가 부인께, 제가 부인의 아들에게 적의를 품고 있다고 말하던가요?\""]], [["“No one, in truth; but a mother has twofold sight.", "\"사실 아무도요. 하지만 어머니에게는 두 배로 보는 눈이 있답니다."], ["I guessed all; I followed him this evening to the Opera, and, concealed in a parquet box, have seen all.”", "저는 모든 걸 짐작했어요. 오늘 저녁 그 애를 따라 오페라 극장에 갔고, 일층 칸막이 좌석에 숨어서 모든 걸 보았어요.\""]], [["“If you have seen all, madame, you know that the son of Fernand has publicly insulted me,”", "\"모든 걸 보셨다면, 부인, 페르낭의 아들이 사람들 앞에서 저를 모욕했다는 것도 아시겠군요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo with awful calmness.", "몬테크리스토가 소름 끼치도록 차분하게 말했다."]], [["“Oh, for pity’s sake!”", "\"오, 제발 부탁이에요!\""]], [["“You have seen that he would have thrown his glove in my face if Morrel, one of my friends, had not stopped him.”", "\"제 친구인 모렐이 막지 않았더라면, 그가 제 얼굴에 장갑을 던졌으리라는 것도 보셨겠지요.\""]], [["“Listen to me, my son has also guessed who you are,—he attributes his father’s misfortunes to you.”", "\"제 말을 들어 주세요. 제 아들도 당신이 누구인지 짐작했어요. 그 애는 제 아버지의 불행이 당신 탓이라고 여기고 있어요.\""]], [["“Madame, you are mistaken, they are not misfortunes,—it is a punishment.", "\"부인, 잘못 아셨습니다. 그것은 불행이 아니라 벌입니다."], ["It is not I who strike M. de Morcerf; it is Providence which punishes him.”", "모르세르 씨를 내리치는 것은 제가 아닙니다. 그를 벌하는 것은 섭리입니다.\""]], [["“And why do you represent Providence?”", "\"그런데 어째서 당신이 섭리 노릇을 하는 거죠?\""], ["cried Mercédès.", "메르세데스가 외쳤다."], ["“Why do you remember when it forgets?", "\"섭리가 잊은 일을 어째서 당신이 기억하는 거예요?"], ["What are Yanina and its vizier to you, Edmond?", "야니나와 그곳 태수가 당신과 무슨 상관이에요, 에드몽?"], ["What injury has Fernand Mondego done you in betraying Ali Tepelini?”", "페르낭 몽데고가 알리 테펠리니를 배신한 일이 당신에게 무슨 해를 끼쳤다는 거죠?\""]], [["“Ah, madame,”", "\"아, 부인.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo, “all this is an affair between the French captain and the daughter of Vasiliki.", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"그건 다 그 프랑스 장교와 바실리키의 딸 사이의 일이지요."], ["It does not concern me, you are right; and if I have sworn to revenge myself, it is not on the French captain, or the Count of Morcerf, but on the fisherman Fernand, the husband of Mercédès the Catalane.”", "부인 말씀대로 저와는 상관없는 일입니다. 그리고 제가 복수를 맹세했다면, 그건 그 프랑스 장교나 모르세르 백작에게가 아니라, 어부 페르낭, 곧 카탈루냐 여인 메르세데스의 남편에게 한 맹세입니다.\""]], [["“Ah, sir!”", "\"아, 백작님!\" 백작 부인이 외쳤다. \"운명이 저지르게 한 잘못 하나에 어쩌면 그토록 끔찍한 복수를!"], ["cried the countess, “how terrible a vengeance for a fault which fatality made me commit!—for I am the only culprit, Edmond, and if you owe revenge to anyone, it is to me, who had not fortitude to bear your absence and my solitude.”", "죄인은 저 하나뿐이에요, 에드몽. 누군가에게 복수해야 한다면, 그건 바로 저예요. 당신의 부재와 제 외로움을 견뎌 낼 굳센 마음이 없었던 저 말이에요.\""]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["exclaimed Monte Cristo, “why was I absent?", "몬테크리스토가 소리쳤다. \"어째서 제가 없었습니까?"], ["And why were you alone?” 40231m", "그리고 어째서 부인이 홀로 남았습니까?\""]], [["“Because you had been arrested, Edmond, and were a prisoner.”", "\"당신이 체포되어 죄수가 되었으니까요, 에드몽.\""]], [["“And why was I arrested?", "\"그런데 어째서 제가 체포되었습니까?"], ["Why was I a prisoner?”", "어째서 제가 죄수가 되었습니까?\""]], [["“I do not know,”", "\"저는 모릅니다.\""], ["said Mercédès.", "메르세데스가 말했다."]], [["“You do not, madame; at least, I hope not.", "\"모르시는군요, 부인. 적어도 모르시기를 바랍니다."], ["But I will tell you.", "하지만 제가 말씀드리지요."], ["I was arrested and became a prisoner because, under the arbor of La Réserve, the day before I was to marry you, a man named Danglars wrote this letter, which the fisherman Fernand himself posted.”", "제가 체포되어 죄수가 된 까닭은, 부인과 결혼하기로 한 전날, 라레제르브의 정자 아래에서 당글라르라는 자가 이 편지를 썼고, 그것을 어부 페르낭이 직접 부쳤기 때문입니다.\""]], [["Monte Cristo went to a secretaire, opened a drawer by a spring, from which he took a paper which had lost its original color, and the ink of which had become of a rusty hue—this he placed in the hands of Mercédès.", "몬테크리스토가 책상으로 가서 용수철로 서랍을 열고, 본디 색을 잃고 잉크가 녹슨 빛으로 변한 종이 한 장을 꺼냈다. 그것을 메르세데스의 손에 쥐여 주었다."], ["It was Danglars’ letter to the king’s attorney, which the Count of Monte Cristo, disguised as a clerk from the house of Thomson & French, had taken from the file against Edmond Dantès, on the day he had paid the two hundred thousand francs to M.", "그것은 당글라르가 검사에게 보낸 편지로, 몬테크리스토 백작이 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회의 사무원으로 변장하고 보빌 씨에게 이십만 프랑을 치른 날, 에드몽 당테스를 고발한 서류철에서 빼낸 것이었다."], ["de Boville. Mercédès read with terror the following lines:", "메르세데스는 다음과 같은 글줄을 두려움에 떨며 읽어 내려갔다."]], [["“The king’s attorney is informed by a friend to the throne and religion that one Edmond Dantès, second in command on board the _Pharaon_, this day arrived from Smyrna, after having touched at Naples and Porto-Ferrajo, is the bearer of a letter from Murat to the usurper, and of another letter from the usurper to the Bonapartist club in Paris.", "\"왕좌와 종교에 충성하는 한 벗이 검사께 알립니다. 스미르나에서 나폴리와 포르토페라요를 거쳐 오늘 입항한 파라옹호의 일등 항해사 에드몽 당테스라는 자가 뮈라가 찬탈자에게 보내는 편지 한 통과, 찬탈자가 파리의 보나파르트파 모임에 보내는 또 다른 편지 한 통을 지니고 있습니다."], ["Ample corroboration of this statement may be obtained by arresting the above-mentioned Edmond Dantès, who either carries the letter for Paris about with him, or has it at his father’s abode.", "이 진술의 충분한 증거는 위에 언급한 에드몽 당테스를 체포함으로써 얻을 수 있습니다. 그는 파리로 가는 그 편지를 몸에 지니고 있거나 아버지의 집에 두고 있을 것입니다."], ["Should it not be found in possession of either father or son, then it will assuredly be discovered in the cabin belonging to the said Dantès on board the _Pharaon_.”", "부자 어느 쪽에서도 발견되지 않는다면, 그것은 분명 파라옹호에 있는 그 당테스의 선실에서 발견될 것입니다.\""]], [["“How dreadful!”", "\"이 무슨 끔찍한 일인지!\""], ["said Mercédès, passing her hand across her brow, moist with perspiration; “and that letter——”", "메르세데스가 땀에 젖은 이마를 손으로 훔치며 말했다. \"그리고 이 편지가……\""]], [["“I bought it for two hundred thousand francs, madame,”", "\"제가 이십만 프랑을 주고 사들였습니다, 부인.\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “but that is a trifle, since it enables me to justify myself to you.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"하지만 그건 사소한 일이지요. 덕분에 부인 앞에서 제 결백을 증명할 수 있으니까요.\""]], [["“And the result of that letter——”", "\"그리고 그 편지의 결과는……\""]], [["“You well know, madame, was my arrest; but you do not know how long that arrest lasted.", "\"부인께서도 잘 아시지요. 제 체포였습니다. 하지만 그 체포가 얼마나 오래 이어졌는지는 모르실 겁니다."], ["You do not know that I remained for fourteen years within a quarter of a league of you, in a dungeon in the Château d’If.", "제가 부인에게서 사 분의 일 리외도 안 되는 곳, 이프성의 토굴 감옥에서 십사 년을 갇혀 지냈다는 것을 모르실 겁니다."], ["You do not know that every day of those fourteen years I renewed the vow of vengeance which I had made the first day; and yet I was not aware that you had married Fernand, my calumniator, and that my father had died of hunger!”", "그 십사 년 동안, 첫날 세웠던 복수의 맹세를 날마다 새로이 다졌다는 것도요. 그러면서도 저는 부인이 저를 모함한 페르낭과 결혼했다는 것도, 제 아버지가 굶어 죽었다는 것도 알지 못했습니다!\""]], [["“Can it be?”", "\"그게 사실인가요?\""], ["cried Mercédès, shuddering.", "메르세데스가 몸서리치며 외쳤다."]], [["“That is what I heard on leaving my prison fourteen years after I had entered it; and that is why, on account of the living Mercédès and my deceased father, I have sworn to revenge myself on Fernand, and—I have revenged myself.”", "\"갇힌 지 십사 년 만에 감옥을 나오며 들은 이야기가 바로 그것이었습니다. 그래서 저는 살아 있는 메르세데스와 세상을 떠난 제 아버지를 걸고 페르낭에게 복수하겠노라 맹세했고, 마침내 복수했습니다.\""]], [["“And you are sure the unhappy Fernand did that?”", "\"그 불행한 페르낭이 정말 그런 짓을 했다고 확신하시나요?\""]], [["“I am satisfied, madame, that he did what I have told you; besides, that is not much more odious than that a Frenchman by adoption should pass over to the English; that a Spaniard by birth should have fought against the Spaniards; that a stipendiary of Ali should have betrayed and murdered Ali.", "\"제가 말씀드린 그대로 했다고 확신합니다, 부인. 게다가 그 일은, 양자로 받아들여진 프랑스인이 영국 편으로 넘어가고, 태생이 에스파냐인인 자가 에스파냐인과 맞서 싸우고, 알리에게서 봉급을 받던 자가 알리를 배신하고 살해한 일에 비하면 그리 더 가증스러운 것도 아닙니다."], ["Compared with such things, what is the letter you have just read?—a lover’s deception, which the woman who has married that man ought certainly to forgive; but not so the lover who was to have married her.", "그런 일들에 견주면, 방금 읽으신 그 편지가 무엇이겠습니까? 한낱 연인의 배신일 뿐, 그 사내와 결혼한 여인이라면 마땅히 용서해야 할 것입니다. 하지만 그 여인과 결혼하기로 했던 연인이라면 그럴 수 없지요."], ["Well, the French did not avenge themselves on the traitor, the Spaniards did not shoot the traitor, Ali in his tomb left the traitor unpunished; but I, betrayed, sacrificed, buried, have risen from my tomb, by the grace of God, to punish that man.", "그런데 프랑스인들은 그 배신자에게 복수하지 않았고, 에스파냐인들은 그 배신자를 총살하지 않았으며, 무덤 속 알리도 그 배신자를 벌하지 않은 채 내버려 두었습니다. 그러나 배신당하고, 희생되고, 파묻혔던 제가, 하느님의 은총으로 무덤에서 일어나 그 사내를 벌하러 왔습니다."], ["He sends me for that purpose, and here I am.”", "하느님이 그 일을 위해 저를 보내셨고, 이렇게 제가 여기 있는 겁니다.\""]], [["The poor woman’s head and arms fell; her legs bent under her, and she fell on her knees.", "가엾은 여인의 고개와 두 팔이 툭 떨어졌다. 다리에 힘이 풀려, 그녀는 무릎을 꿇고 말았다."]], [["“Forgive, Edmond, forgive for my sake, who love you still!”", "\"용서해 주세요, 에드몽, 아직도 당신을 사랑하는 저를 봐서라도 용서해 주세요!\""]], [["The dignity of the wife checked the fervor of the lover and the mother.", "아내로서의 위엄이 연인이자 어머니로서의 격정을 억눌렀다."], ["Her forehead almost touched the carpet, when the count sprang forward and raised her.", "그녀의 이마가 양탄자에 거의 닿으려는 순간, 백작이 앞으로 달려들어 그녀를 일으켰다."], ["Then seated on a chair, she looked at the manly countenance of Monte Cristo, on which grief and hatred still impressed a threatening expression.", "그러고는 의자에 앉아, 슬픔과 증오가 여전히 위협하는 표정으로 새겨진 몬테크리스토의 사내다운 얼굴을 바라보았다."]], [["“Not crush that accursed race?”", "\"저 저주받은 핏줄을 짓밟지 말라고?\""], ["murmured he; “abandon my purpose at the moment of its accomplishment?", "그가 중얼거렸다. \"목적을 이루기 직전에 그것을 포기하라고?"], ["Impossible, madame, impossible!”", "안 됩니다, 부인. 그럴 수는 없습니다!\""]], [["“Edmond,”", "\"에드몽,\""], ["said the poor mother, who tried every means, “when I call you Edmond, why do you not call me Mercédès?”", "온갖 수단을 다 써 보려는 가엾은 어머니가 말했다. \"제가 당신을 에드몽이라 부르는데, 어째서 당신은 저를 메르세데스라 부르지 않는 거죠?\""]], [["“Mercédès!”", "\"메르세데스!\""], ["repeated Monte Cristo; “Mercédès!", "몬테크리스토가 되뇌었다. \"메르세데스!"], ["Well yes, you are right; that name has still its charms, and this is the first time for a long period that I have pronounced it so distinctly.", "그래요, 옳은 말씀입니다. 그 이름은 아직도 매력을 잃지 않았군요. 그리고 이렇게 또렷하게 그 이름을 불러 본 것은 참으로 오랜만입니다."], ["Oh, Mercédès, I have uttered your name with the sigh of melancholy, with the groan of sorrow, with the last effort of despair; I have uttered it when frozen with cold, crouched on the straw in my dungeon; I have uttered it, consumed with heat, rolling on the stone floor of my prison.", "오, 메르세데스, 저는 우울의 한숨과 함께, 비탄의 신음과 함께, 절망의 마지막 안간힘과 함께 당신의 이름을 불렀습니다. 추위에 얼어붙은 채 토굴 감옥의 짚더미 위에 웅크려서 그 이름을 불렀고, 열에 들떠 감옥의 돌바닥을 구르면서 그 이름을 불렀습니다. 메르세데스, 저는 복수해야 합니다."], ["Mercédès, I must revenge myself, for I suffered fourteen years,—fourteen years I wept, I cursed; now I tell you, Mercédès, I must revenge myself.”", "십사 년을 괴로워했으니까요. 십사 년 동안 울었고, 저주했습니다. 이제 말씀드리지요, 메르세데스, 저는 반드시 복수해야 합니다.\""]], [["The count, fearing to yield to the entreaties of her he had so ardently loved, called his sufferings to the assistance of his hatred.", "그토록 뜨겁게 사랑했던 여인의 애원에 굴복할까 두려웠던 백작은, 자신의 고통을 불러내 증오를 거들게 했다."]], [["“Revenge yourself, then, Edmond,”", "\"그럼 복수하세요, 에드몽.\" 가엾은 어머니가 외쳤다."], ["cried the poor mother; “but let your vengeance fall on the culprits,—on him, on me, but not on my son!”", "\"하지만 그 복수를 죄인들에게 내리세요. 그이에게, 저에게요. 하지만 제 아들에게는 안 돼요!\""]], [["“It is written in the good book,”", "\"성경에 이렇게 쓰여 있습니다,\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “that the sins of the fathers shall fall upon their children to the third and fourth generation.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"아비들의 죄가 그 자식들에게 삼대 사대까지 미치리라고요."], ["Since God himself dictated those words to his prophet, why should I seek to make myself better than God?”", "하느님께서 친히 당신의 예언자에게 그 말씀을 받아쓰게 하셨는데, 어찌 제가 하느님보다 나은 사람이 되려 하겠습니까?\""]], [["“Edmond,”", "\"에드몽,\""], ["continued Mercédès, with her arms extended towards the count, “since I first knew you, I have adored your name, have respected your memory.", "메르세데스가 백작을 향해 두 팔을 뻗으며 말을 이었다. \"당신을 처음 알게 된 그날부터, 저는 당신의 이름을 흠모했고, 당신에 대한 기억을 소중히 받들었어요."], ["Edmond, my friend, do not compel me to tarnish that noble and pure image reflected incessantly on the mirror of my heart.", "에드몽, 내 친구, 제 마음의 거울에 끊임없이 비치던 그 고결하고 순결한 모습을 더럽히게 하지 말아 주세요."], ["Edmond, if you knew all the prayers I have addressed to God for you while I thought you were living and since I have thought you must be dead! Yes, dead, alas!", "에드몽, 당신이 살아 있다고 믿는 동안, 또 당신이 틀림없이 죽었으리라 여긴 뒤로, 제가 당신을 위해 하느님께 올린 그 모든 기도를 안다면! 그래요, 죽었다고요, 아아!"], ["I imagined your dead body buried at the foot of some gloomy tower, or cast to the bottom of a pit by hateful jailers, and I wept!", "저는 당신의 주검이 어느 음산한 탑 아래에 묻혀 있거나, 잔인한 간수들 손에 구덩이 바닥으로 던져졌으리라 상상하며 울었어요."], ["What could I do for you, Edmond, besides pray and weep? Listen; for ten years I dreamed each night the same dream.", "기도하고 우는 것 말고, 제가 당신을 위해 무엇을 할 수 있었겠어요, 에드몽? 들어 보세요."], ["I had been told that you had endeavored to escape; that you had taken the place of another prisoner; that you had slipped into the winding sheet of a dead body; that you had been thrown alive from the top of the Château d’If, and that the cry you uttered as you dashed upon the rocks first revealed to your jailers that they were your murderers.", "십 년 동안 저는 밤마다 똑같은 꿈을 꾸었어요. 사람들이 제게 말하기를, 당신이 탈출을 꾀했다고, 다른 죄수의 자리를 대신했다고, 시신의 수의 속으로 미끄러져 들어갔다고, 이프성 꼭대기에서 산 채로 내던져졌다고, 그리고 당신이 바위에 부딪치며 내지른 비명이 간수들에게 비로소 자신들이 당신을 죽인 살인자임을 알려 주었다고 했어요."], ["Well, Edmond, I swear to you, by the head of that son for whom I entreat your pity,—Edmond, for ten years I saw every night every detail of that frightful tragedy, and for ten years I heard every night the cry which awoke me, shuddering and cold.", "그래요, 에드몽, 제가 자비를 비는 그 아들의 목숨을 걸고 맹세하건대, 에드몽, 십 년 동안 밤마다 저는 그 끔찍한 비극의 낱낱을 보았고, 십 년 동안 밤마다 저를 깨우는 그 비명을 들으며 몸서리치고 얼어붙었어요."], ["And I, too, Edmond—oh!", "저도요, 에드몽, 오!"], ["believe me—guilty as I was—oh, yes, I, too, have suffered much!”", "믿어 주세요, 죄 많은 몸이었지만, 오, 그래요, 저도 무던히 괴로워했답니다!\""]], [["“Have you known what it is to have your father starve to death in your absence?”", "\"당신이 없는 사이에 아버지가 굶어 죽는다는 것이 어떤 것인지 아십니까?\""], ["cried Monte Cristo, thrusting his hands into his hair; “have you seen the woman you loved giving her hand to your rival, while you were perishing at the bottom of a dungeon?”", "몬테크리스토가 두 손을 머리카락 속에 쑤셔 넣으며 외쳤다. \"토굴 감옥 바닥에서 죽어 가는 동안, 사랑하던 여인이 연적에게 손을 내미는 것을 보는 것이 어떤 것인지 아십니까?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"몰라요.\""], ["interrupted Mercédès, “but I have seen him whom I loved on the point of murdering my son.”", "메르세데스가 가로막았다. \"하지만 제가 사랑하던 사람이 제 아들을 죽이려 하는 것은 보았어요.\""]], [["Mercédès uttered these words with such deep anguish, with an accent of such intense despair, that Monte Cristo could not restrain a sob.", "메르세데스가 어찌나 깊은 고뇌로, 어찌나 사무친 절망의 어조로 이 말을 내뱉었던지, 몬테크리스토는 흐느낌을 억누를 수 없었다."], ["The lion was daunted; the avenger was conquered.", "사자가 기가 꺾였고, 복수자가 굴복했다."]], [["“What do you ask of me?”", "\"제게 무엇을 바라십니까?\""], ["said he,—“your son’s life?", "그가 말했다. \"아드님의 목숨 말입니까?"], ["Well, he shall live!”", "좋습니다, 그를 살려 두겠습니다!\""]], [["Mercédès uttered a cry which made the tears start from Monte Cristo’s eyes; but these tears disappeared almost instantaneously, for, doubtless, God had sent some angel to collect them—far more precious were they in his eyes than the richest pearls of Guzerat and Ophir.", "메르세데스가 외마디 소리를 질렀고, 그 소리에 몬테크리스토의 눈에서 눈물이 솟구쳤다. 하지만 그 눈물은 거의 순식간에 사라졌으니, 분명 하느님이 천사를 보내 그것을 거두어 가셨으리라. 그 눈물은 그의 눈에 구자라트와 오피르의 가장 값진 진주들보다도 훨씬 더 귀한 것이었다."]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오,\""], ["said she, seizing the count’s hand and raising it to her lips; “oh, thank you, thank you, Edmond!", "그녀가 백작의 손을 붙잡아 입술로 가져가며 말했다. \"오, 고마워요, 고마워요, 에드몽!"], ["Now you are exactly what I dreamt you were,—the man I always loved.", "이제 당신은 제가 꿈꾸던 바로 그 모습이에요. 제가 언제나 사랑하던 그 사람이요."], ["Oh, now I may say so!”", "오, 이제는 그렇게 말할 수 있어요!\""]], [["“So much the better,”", "\"그렇다면 더욱 잘된 일입니다.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo; “as that poor Edmond will not have long to be loved by you.", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"그 가엾은 에드몽은 오래도록 부인의 사랑을 받지는 못할 테니까요."], ["Death is about to return to the tomb, the phantom to retire in darkness.”", "죽음이 다시 무덤으로 돌아가고, 유령이 어둠 속으로 물러갈 참입니다.\""]], [["“What do you say, Edmond?”", "\"무슨 말씀이세요, 에드몽?\""]], [["“I say, since you command me, Mercédès, I must die.”", "\"부인이 제게 명하셨으니, 저는 죽어야 한다는 말입니다, 메르세데스.\""]], [["“Die? and why so?", "\"죽다니요? 어째서요?"], ["Who talks of dying?", "누가 죽는다는 거예요?"], ["Whence have you these ideas of death?”", "어디서 그런 죽음 따위의 생각을 가져온 거죠?\""]], [["“You do not suppose that, publicly outraged in the face of a whole theatre, in the presence of your friends and those of your son—challenged by a boy who will glory in my forgiveness as if it were a victory—you do not suppose that I can for one moment wish to live.", "\"극장 전체가 보는 앞에서, 부인의 벗들과 아드님의 벗들이 보는 앞에서 공공연히 모욕당하고, 마치 승리라도 거둔 양 제 용서를 자랑스러워할 애송이에게 결투를 신청받은 제가, 단 한순간이라도 살기를 바라리라고는 생각지 마십시오."], ["What I most loved after you, Mercédès, was myself, my dignity, and that strength which rendered me superior to other men; that strength was my life.", "메르세데스, 제가 부인 다음으로 가장 사랑한 것은 저 자신, 제 존엄, 그리고 저를 다른 사람들보다 우월하게 만들어 준 그 힘이었습니다. 그 힘이 곧 제 목숨이었지요."], ["With one word you have crushed it, and I die.”", "부인은 단 한마디로 그것을 짓밟았고, 그래서 저는 죽습니다.\""]], [["“But the duel will not take place, Edmond, since you forgive?”", "\"하지만 당신이 용서한다면, 결투는 벌어지지 않을 거 아니에요, 에드몽?\""]], [["“It will take place,”", "\"결투는 벌어질 겁니다.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, in a most solemn tone; “but instead of your son’s blood to stain the ground, mine will flow.”", "몬테크리스토가 더없이 엄숙한 어조로 말했다. \"다만 땅을 적시는 것이 아드님의 피가 아니라 제 피일 뿐이지요.\""]], [["Mercédès shrieked, and sprang towards Monte Cristo, but, suddenly stopping, “Edmond,”", "메르세데스가 비명을 지르며 몬테크리스토에게 달려들었다. 그러다 문득 멈춰 섰다. \"에드몽,\""], ["said she, “there is a God above us, since you live and since I have seen you again; I trust to him from my heart.", "그녀가 말했다. \"우리 위에는 하느님이 계세요. 당신이 살아 있고, 제가 당신을 다시 만났으니까요."], ["While waiting his assistance I trust to your word; you have said that my son should live, have you not?”", "저는 진심으로 그분을 믿어요. 그분의 도움을 기다리는 동안, 저는 당신의 말을 믿겠어요. 제 아들이 살 거라고 말씀하셨죠, 그렇죠?\""]], [["“Yes, madame, he shall live,”", "\"그렇습니다, 부인. 그는 살 것입니다.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, surprised that without more emotion Mercédès had accepted the heroic sacrifice he made for her.", "메르세데스가 자신이 그녀를 위해 치르는 영웅적인 희생을 더 동요하는 기색도 없이 받아들이는 것에 놀라며 몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["Mercédès extended her hand to the count.", "메르세데스가 백작에게 손을 내밀었다."]], [["“Edmond,”", "\"에드몽,\" 그녀가 말했다. 자기가 말을 건네는 그를 바라보는 그녀의 눈은 눈물에 젖어 있었다."], ["said she, and her eyes were wet with tears while looking at him to whom she spoke, “how noble it is of you, how great the action you have just performed, how sublime to have taken pity on a poor woman who appealed to you with every chance against her, Alas, I am grown old with grief more than with years, and cannot now remind my Edmond by a smile, or by a look, of that Mercédès whom he once spent so many hours in contemplating.", "\"당신은 정말 고결한 분이에요. 방금 당신이 한 일은 얼마나 위대한지요. 온갖 불리함을 무릅쓰고 당신에게 호소한 가엾은 여인을 가엾이 여겨 주신 것은 얼마나 숭고한 일인지요."], ["Ah, believe me, Edmond, as I told you, I too have suffered much; I repeat, it is melancholy to pass one’s life without having one joy to recall, without preserving a single hope; but that proves that all is not yet over.", "아아, 저는 세월보다 슬픔으로 더 늙어 버려서, 이제는 미소나 눈길로도 제 에드몽에게 그가 한때 그토록 오래 바라보던 그 메르세데스를 떠올리게 해 줄 수가 없네요. 아, 믿어 주세요, 에드몽, 제가 말했듯이 저도 무던히 괴로워했어요. 다시 말하지만, 떠올릴 기쁨 하나 없이, 단 하나의 희망도 간직하지 못한 채 한평생을 보낸다는 건 서글픈 일이에요."], ["No, it is not finished; I feel it by what remains in my heart.", "하지만 그건 아직 모든 것이 끝나지 않았다는 증거예요. 아니에요, 끝나지 않았어요. 제 마음속에 남아 있는 것으로 그걸 느껴요."], ["Oh, I repeat it, Edmond; what you have just done is beautiful—it is grand; it is sublime.”", "오, 다시 말할게요, 에드몽. 방금 당신이 한 일은 아름다워요. 위대하고, 숭고해요.\""]], [["“Do you say so now, Mercédès?—then what would you say if you knew the extent of the sacrifice I make to you?", "\"이제 와서 그런 말씀을 하시는군요, 메르세데스. 그렇다면 제가 부인을 위해 치르는 희생이 얼마나 큰지 아신다면 무어라 하시겠습니까?"], ["Suppose that the Supreme Being, after having created the world and fertilized chaos, had paused in the work to spare an angel the tears that might one day flow for mortal sins from her immortal eyes; suppose that when everything was in readiness and the moment had come for God to look upon his work and see that it was good—suppose he had snuffed out the sun and tossed the world back into eternal night—then—even then, Mercédès, you could not imagine what I lose in sacrificing my life at this moment.”", "지고하신 분께서 세상을 창조하고 혼돈을 비옥하게 하신 뒤, 어느 천사가 언젠가 인간의 죄로 인해 그 불멸의 눈에서 흘릴지도 모를 눈물을 덜어 주려고 그 일을 잠시 멈추셨다고 해 봅시다. 모든 것이 갖추어지고, 하느님이 당신의 작품을 굽어보며 보시기에 좋았더라 하실 순간이 왔을 때, 그분이 태양을 꺼 버리고 세상을 영원한 어둠 속으로 도로 내던지셨다고 해 봅시다. 그렇다 해도, 그렇다 해도, 메르세데스, 제가 지금 이 순간 제 목숨을 바치며 잃는 것이 무엇인지 부인은 상상조차 하실 수 없을 겁니다.\""]], [["Mercédès looked at the count in a way which expressed at the same time her astonishment, her admiration, and her gratitude. Monte Cristo pressed his forehead on his burning hands, as if his brain could no longer bear alone the weight of its thoughts.", "메르세데스는 놀라움과 감탄과 감사를 한꺼번에 담은 눈길로 백작을 바라보았다. 몬테크리스토는 마치 더 이상 머릿속만으로는 제 생각의 무게를 홀로 감당할 수 없다는 듯, 불타는 두 손에 이마를 짓눌렀다."]], [["“Edmond,”", "\"에드몽,\""], ["said Mercédès, “I have but one word more to say to you.”", "메르세데스가 말했다. \"당신에게 드릴 말이 한마디 더 남아 있어요.\""]], [["The count smiled bitterly.", "백작이 쓰디쓰게 미소 지었다."]], [["“Edmond,”", "\"에드몽,\""], ["continued she, “you will see that if my face is pale, if my eyes are dull, if my beauty is gone; if Mercédès, in short, no longer resembles her former self in her features, you will see that her heart is still the same.", "그녀가 말을 이었다. \"당신은 알게 될 거예요. 제 얼굴이 창백하고, 제 눈이 흐릿하고, 제 아름다움이 사라졌다 해도, 한마디로 메르세데스가 더 이상 그 옛날의 모습과 닮지 않았다 해도, 그 마음만은 여전히 그대로라는 것을요."], ["Adieu, then, Edmond; I have nothing more to ask of heaven—I have seen you again, and have found you as noble and as great as formerly you were.", "그럼 안녕히, 에드몽. 이제 하늘에 더 청할 것이 없어요. 당신을 다시 만났고, 옛날 그대로 고결하고 위대한 당신을 보았으니까요."], ["Adieu, Edmond, adieu, and thank you.”", "안녕히, 에드몽, 안녕히, 그리고 고마워요.\""]], [["But the count did not answer.", "하지만 백작은 대답하지 않았다."], ["Mercédès opened the door of the study and had disappeared before he had recovered from the painful and profound reverie into which his thwarted vengeance had plunged him.", "메르세데스는 서재 문을 열었고, 좌절된 복수가 그를 빠뜨린 그 괴롭고 깊은 상념에서 그가 미처 헤어나기도 전에 사라져 버렸다."]], [["The clock of the Invalides struck one when the carriage which conveyed Madame de Morcerf rolled away on the pavement of the Champs-Élysées, and made Monte Cristo raise his head.", "마차가 모르세르 부인을 태우고 샹젤리제의 포도(鋪道) 위를 굴러 멀어져 갈 때, 앵발리드의 시계가 한 시를 알렸고, 그 소리에 몬테크리스토가 고개를 들었다."]], [["“What a fool I was,”", "\"내가 어찌나 어리석었던지,\""], ["said he, “not to tear my heart out on the day when I resolved to avenge myself!”", "그가 말했다. \"복수를 결심하던 그날, 내 심장을 뽑아내 버리지 못하다니!\""]], [["# Chapter 90. The Meeting", "# Chapter 90. The Meeting"]], [["After Mercédès had left Monte Cristo, he fell into profound gloom.", "메르세데스가 떠난 뒤, 몬테크리스토는 깊은 우울에 잠겼다."], ["Around him and within him the flight of thought seemed to have stopped; his energetic mind slumbered, as the body does after extreme fatigue.", "그를 둘러싼 것도, 그의 안에 있는 것도, 생각의 날갯짓이 멈춘 듯했다. 극도의 피로 뒤에 육신이 그러하듯, 그의 강인한 정신이 잠들어 있었다."]], [["“What?” said he to himself, while the lamp and the wax lights were nearly burnt out, and the servants were waiting impatiently in the anteroom; “what?", "\"무엇이란 말인가?\" 그가 혼잣말을 했다."], ["this edifice which I have been so long preparing, which I have reared with so much care and toil, is to be crushed by a single touch, a word, a breath!", "램프와 양초들은 거의 다 타들어 가고 있었고, 하인들은 대기실에서 초조하게 기다리고 있었다. \"무엇이란 말인가? 내가 그토록 오래 준비해 온 이 건물, 그 많은 정성과 수고로 쌓아 올린 이 건물이, 단 한 번의 손길에, 한마디 말에, 한 줄기 숨결에 무너지고 만단 말인가!"], ["Yes, this self, of whom I thought so much, of whom I was so proud, who had appeared so worthless in the dungeons of the Château d’If, and whom I had succeeded in making so great, will be but a lump of clay tomorrow.", "그렇다, 내가 그토록 소중히 여겼고, 그토록 자랑스러워했으며, 이프성의 지하 감옥에서는 그토록 하찮아 보였으나 내가 끝내 그토록 위대하게 만들어 낸 이 자아가, 내일이면 한 줌 진흙에 지나지 않게 될 것이다."], ["Alas, it is not the death of the body I regret; for is not the destruction of the vital principle, the repose to which everything is tending, to which every unhappy being aspires,—is not this the repose of matter after which I so long sighed, and which I was seeking to attain by the painful process of starvation when Faria appeared in my dungeon?", "아아, 내가 아쉬워하는 것은 육신의 죽음이 아니다. 모든 것이 향해 가는 곳, 불행한 존재라면 누구나 갈망하는 안식, 생명의 원리가 소멸하는 것이야말로, 내가 그토록 오랜 세월 탄식하며 바라던 물질의 안식, 파리아가 내 감옥에 나타났을 때 굶주림이라는 고통스러운 방법으로 이루려 했던 그 안식이 아니던가?"], ["What is death for me?", "나에게 죽음이란 무엇인가?"], ["One step farther into rest,—two, perhaps, into silence.", "안식을 향한 한 걸음, 침묵을 향한, 어쩌면 두 걸음일 뿐이다."], ["No, it is not existence, then, that I regret, but the ruin of projects so slowly carried out, so laboriously framed.", "아니, 그러니 내가 아쉬워하는 것은 존재가 아니라, 그토록 천천히 실행해 온, 그토록 공들여 짜낸 계획들이 무너지는 것이다."], ["Providence is now opposed to them, when I most thought it would be propitious.", "가장 호의적이리라 믿었던 바로 그 순간에, 섭리는 이제 그 계획들에 등을 돌렸다."], ["It is not God’s will that they should be accomplished.", "그것들이 이루어지는 것은 신의 뜻이 아니다."], ["This burden, almost as heavy as a world, which I had raised, and I had thought to bear to the end, was too great for my strength, and I was compelled to lay it down in the middle of my career.", "내가 들어 올려, 끝까지 짊어지려 했던 이 짐, 세상만큼이나 무거운 이 짐은 내 힘에 부쳤고, 나는 여정의 한복판에서 그것을 내려놓지 않을 수 없게 되었다."], ["Oh, shall I then, again become a fatalist, whom fourteen years of despair and ten of hope had rendered a believer in Providence?", "오, 그렇다면 나는 다시 운명론자가 되는 것인가, 십사 년의 절망과 십 년의 희망이 섭리를 믿게 만든 이 운명론자로?"]], [["“And all this—all this, because my heart, which I thought dead, was only sleeping; because it has awakened and has begun to beat again, because I have yielded to the pain of the emotion excited in my breast by a woman’s voice.", "\"이 모든 것이, 이 모든 것이, 죽었다고 여겼던 내 심장이 그저 잠들어 있었을 뿐이기 때문이다. 그것이 다시 깨어나 뛰기 시작했기 때문이며, 한 여인의 목소리가 내 가슴에 불러일으킨 감정의 고통에 내가 굴복했기 때문이다."]], [["“Yet,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["continued the count, becoming each moment more absorbed in the anticipation of the dreadful sacrifice for the morrow, which Mercédès had accepted, “yet, it is impossible that so noble-minded a woman should thus through selfishness consent to my death when I am in the prime of life and strength; it is impossible that she can carry to such a point maternal love, or rather delirium.", "백작은 메르세데스가 받아들인 내일의 그 끔찍한 희생을 떠올리며 점점 더 깊이 빠져들었다. \"하지만, 그토록 고결한 마음을 지닌 여인이, 내가 한창때의 힘과 생명을 누리는 지금, 이기심 때문에 내 죽음에 동의한다는 것은 있을 수 없는 일이다. 그녀가 모성애를, 아니 광기에 가까운 그 마음을 그토록 극단까지 밀고 갈 수는 없다."], ["There are virtues which become crimes by exaggeration.", "지나치면 죄가 되어 버리는 미덕도 있는 법."], ["No, she must have conceived some pathetic scene; she will come and throw herself between us; and what would be sublime here will there appear ridiculous.”", "아니, 그녀는 분명 어떤 애처로운 장면을 마음에 그려 두었을 것이다. 그녀가 달려와 우리 사이로 몸을 던질 것이고, 여기서는 숭고할 일이 거기서는 우스꽝스럽게 보일 것이다.\""]], [["The blush of pride mounted to the count’s forehead as this thought passed through his mind.", "이런 생각이 머리를 스치자 자존심의 홍조가 백작의 이마에 떠올랐다."]], [["“Ridiculous?”", "\"우스꽝스럽다고?\""], ["repeated he; “and the ridicule will fall on me.", "그가 되뇌었다. \"그리고 그 비웃음은 내게로 떨어지겠지."], ["I ridiculous?", "내가 우스꽝스럽다고?"], ["No, I would rather die.”", "아니, 차라리 죽는 편이 낫다.\""]], [["By thus exaggerating to his own mind the anticipated ill-fortune of the next day, to which he had condemned himself by promising Mercédès to spare her son, the count at last exclaimed:", "이렇게 백작은, 메르세데스에게 그녀의 아들을 살려 주겠노라 약속함으로써 스스로에게 선고한 다음 날의 불운을 마음속에서 한껏 부풀린 끝에, 마침내 이렇게 외쳤다."]], [["“Folly, folly, folly!—to carry generosity so far as to put myself up as a mark for that young man to aim at.", "\"어리석다, 어리석어, 어리석은 짓이야! 그 젊은이가 겨눌 과녁으로 나 자신을 세워 두다니, 관용을 그토록 멀리까지 끌고 가다니. 그는 내 죽음이 자살이었다고는 결코 믿지 않을 것이다."], ["He will never believe that my death was suicide; and yet it is important for the honor of my memory,—and this surely is not vanity, but a justifiable pride,—it is important the world should know that I have consented, by my free will, to stop my arm, already raised to strike, and that with the arm which has been so powerful against others I have struck myself.", "그러나 내 기억의 명예를 위해서는 중요한 일이다. 이것은 분명 허영이 아니라 정당한 자긍심이다. 이미 내려치려 들었던 이 팔을 내가 자유의지로 멈추었다는 것을, 그리고 남들에게 그토록 강력했던 이 팔로 나 자신을 쳤다는 것을, 세상이 알아야 한다는 것은 중요한 일이다."], ["It must be; it shall be.”", "그래야만 한다. 그렇게 될 것이다.\""]], [["Seizing a pen, he drew a paper from a secret drawer in his desk, and wrote at the bottom of the document (which was no other than his will, made since his arrival in Paris) a sort of codicil, clearly explaining the nature of his death.", "펜을 집어 든 그는 책상의 비밀 서랍에서 종이 한 장을 꺼냈다. 그것은 다름 아닌, 파리에 도착한 뒤 작성해 둔 유언장이었다. 그는 그 문서 맨 아래에 자신의 죽음의 성격을 분명히 밝히는 일종의 추가 조항을 적었다."]], [["“I do this, Oh, my God,” said he, with his eyes raised to heaven, “as much for thy honor as for mine.", "\"오, 하느님, 제가 이 일을 하는 것은,\" 그가 하늘을 향해 눈을 들며 말했다."], ["I have during ten years considered myself the agent of thy vengeance, and other wretches, like Morcerf, Danglars, Villefort, even Morcerf himself, must not imagine that chance has freed them from their enemy.", "\"제 명예를 위해서이기도 하나 그만큼 당신의 명예를 위해서이기도 합니다. 저는 십 년 동안 저 자신을 당신의 복수를 행하는 대리인으로 여겨 왔습니다. 모르세르, 당글라르, 빌포르 같은, 그리고 모르세르 그 자신 같은 다른 비열한 자들이, 우연히 자신들의 적에게서 벗어났다고 여겨서는 안 됩니다."], ["Let them know, on the contrary, that their punishment, which had been decreed by Providence, is only delayed by my present determination, and although they escape it in this world, it awaits them in another, and that they are only exchanging time for eternity.”", "도리어 그들은 알아야 합니다. 섭리가 정해 놓은 그들의 벌이 지금의 제 결심으로 그저 미루어졌을 뿐이며, 이 세상에서는 그것을 피한다 해도 다른 세상에서 그들을 기다리고 있다는 것을, 그리고 그들은 그저 시간을 영원과 맞바꾸는 것일 뿐이라는 것을.\""]], [["While he was thus agitated by gloomy uncertainties,—wretched waking dreams of grief,—the first rays of morning pierced his windows, and shone upon the pale blue paper on which he had just inscribed his justification of Providence.", "이렇게 그가 음울한 불확실함에, 슬픔이 빚어낸 비참한 백일몽에 시달리고 있을 때, 동틀 녘의 첫 햇살이 창문을 뚫고 들어와, 방금 그가 섭리를 정당화하는 글을 적어 넣은 연푸른 종이 위로 비쳐 들었다."]], [["It was just five o’clock in the morning when a slight noise like a stifled sigh reached his ear.", "새벽 다섯 시 정각, 억눌린 한숨 같은 희미한 소리가 그의 귀에 닿았다."], ["He turned his head, looked around him, and saw no one; but the sound was repeated distinctly enough to convince him of its reality.", "그는 고개를 돌려 주위를 둘러보았으나 아무도 보이지 않았다. 그러나 그 소리가 또렷이 되풀이되어, 그것이 실제임을 확신하게 했다."]], [["He arose, and quietly opening the door of the drawing-room, saw Haydée, who had fallen on a chair, with her arms hanging down and her beautiful head thrown back.", "그는 자리에서 일어나 응접실 문을 조용히 열었다. 하이데가 의자에 쓰러져 있었다. 두 팔은 늘어뜨린 채, 아름다운 머리는 뒤로 젖혀져 있었다."], ["She had been standing at the door, to prevent his going out without seeing her, until sleep, which the young cannot resist, had overpowered her frame, wearied as she was with watching.", "그녀는 그가 자신을 보지 못한 채 나가지 못하도록 문가에 서 있었으나, 지켜보느라 지친 몸이 젊은이로서는 거역할 수 없는 잠에 굴복하고 만 것이었다."], ["The noise of the door did not awaken her, and Monte Cristo gazed at her with affectionate regret.", "문소리에도 그녀는 깨지 않았고, 몬테크리스토는 애틋한 회한이 담긴 눈길로 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["“She remembered that she had a son,”", "\"이 아이는 자기에게 아들이 있다는 것을 기억했지.\""], ["said he; “and I forgot I had a daughter.”", "그가 말했다. \"그런데 나는 내게 딸이 있다는 것을 잊었구나.\""], ["Then, shaking his head sorrowfully, “Poor Haydée,”", "그러더니 슬프게 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"가엾은 하이데."], ["said he; “she wished to see me, to speak to me; she has feared or guessed something.", "나를 보고 싶어 했고, 내게 말하고 싶어 했지. 무언가를 두려워했거나 짐작한 게야."], ["Oh, I cannot go without taking leave of her; I cannot die without confiding her to someone.”", "오, 이 아이에게 작별 인사도 없이 갈 수는 없다. 이 아이를 누군가에게 맡기지 않고는 죽을 수 없다.\""]], [["He quietly regained his seat, and wrote under the other lines:", "그는 조용히 자리로 돌아와, 앞서 쓴 줄들 아래에 이렇게 적었다."]], [["“I bequeath to Maximilian Morrel, captain of Spahis,—and son of my former patron, Pierre Morrel, shipowner at Marseilles,—the sum of twenty millions, a part of which may be offered to his sister Julie and brother-in-law Emmanuel, if he does not fear this increase of fortune may mar their happiness.", "\"나는 스파히 기병대 대위이자, 마르세유의 선주였던 나의 옛 후원자 피에르 모렐의 아들인 막시밀리앙 모렐에게 이천만 프랑을 물려준다. 그가 그의 누이 쥘리와 매부 에마뉘엘의 행복이 이 재산의 증가로 손상될까 두려워하지 않는다면, 그 일부를 그들에게 나누어 줄 수 있다."], ["These twenty millions are concealed in my grotto at Monte Cristo, of which Bertuccio knows the secret.", "이 이천만 프랑은 몬테크리스토 섬의 내 동굴에 감추어져 있으며, 베르투치오가 그 비밀을 알고 있다."], ["If his heart is free, and he will marry Haydée, the daughter of Ali Pasha of Yanina, whom I have brought up with the love of a father, and who has shown the love and tenderness of a daughter for me, he will thus accomplish my last wish.", "만일 그의 마음이 자유롭다면, 그리고 그가 야니나의 알리 파샤의 딸 하이데와 결혼한다면, 내가 아버지의 사랑으로 길러 왔고 나에게 딸의 사랑과 정성을 보여 준 그 아이와 결혼한다면, 그는 나의 마지막 소원을 이루어 주는 셈이 될 것이다."], ["This will has already constituted Haydée heiress of the rest of my fortune, consisting of lands, funds in England, Austria, and Holland, furniture in my different palaces and houses, and which without the twenty millions and the legacies to my servants, may still amount to sixty millions.” 40241m", "이 유언장은 이미 하이데를 내 나머지 재산의 상속인으로 정해 두었으니, 그것은 토지, 영국·오스트리아·네덜란드의 자금, 여러 궁전과 저택에 있는 가구들로 이루어져 있으며, 이천만 프랑과 하인들에게 남긴 유산을 제외하고도 육천만 프랑에 이를 것이다.\""]], [["He was finishing the last line when a cry behind him made him start, and the pen fell from his hand.", "그가 마지막 줄을 끝맺으려는 순간, 등 뒤에서 들려온 외침에 그는 깜짝 놀랐고, 펜이 손에서 떨어졌다."]], [["“Haydée,”", "\"하이데,\""], ["said he, “did you read it?”", "그가 말했다. \"그걸 읽었느냐?\""]], [["“Oh, my lord,”", "\"오, 주인님,\""], ["said she, “why are you writing thus at such an hour?", "그녀가 말했다. \"어찌하여 이런 시각에 이런 글을 쓰고 계세요?"], ["Why are you bequeathing all your fortune to me?", "어찌하여 전 재산을 제게 물려주려 하세요?"], ["Are you going to leave me?”", "저를 떠나시려는 건가요?\""]], [["“I am going on a journey, dear child,”", "\"여행을 떠나려 한단다, 얘야.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, with an expression of infinite tenderness and melancholy; “and if any misfortune should happen to me——”", "몬테크리스토가 한없는 다정함과 우수가 어린 표정으로 말했다. \"그런데 만일 내게 무슨 변고라도 생긴다면……\""]], [["The count stopped.", "백작은 말을 멈추었다."]], [["“Well?”", "\"그러면요?\""], ["asked the young girl, with an authoritative tone the count had never observed before, and which startled him.", "처녀가 물었다. 백작이 전에는 한 번도 들어 본 적 없는, 그를 놀라게 하는 단호한 어조였다."]], [["“Well, if any misfortune happen to me,”", "\"그러면, 내게 무슨 변고가 생긴다면,\""], ["replied Monte Cristo, “I wish my daughter to be happy.”", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"나는 내 딸이 행복하기를 바란단다.\""], ["Haydée smiled sorrowfully, and shook her head.", "하이데가 슬프게 미소 지으며 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["“Do you think of dying, my lord?”", "\"죽음을 생각하고 계신가요, 주인님?\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“The wise man, my child, has said, ‘It is good to think of death.’”", "\"현자가 이렇게 말했단다, 얘야. '죽음을 생각하는 것은 좋은 일이다.'\""]], [["“Well, if you die,”", "\"그러면, 만일 돌아가신다면,\""], ["said she, “bequeath your fortune to others, for if you die I shall require nothing;”", "그녀가 말했다. \"재산은 다른 이들에게 물려주세요. 주인님이 돌아가시면 저는 아무것도 필요 없을 테니까요.\""], ["and, taking the paper, she tore it in four pieces, and threw it into the middle of the room.", "그러더니 종이를 집어 네 조각으로 찢어 방 한가운데에 던졌다."], ["Then, the effort having exhausted her strength, she fell, not asleep this time, but fainting on the floor.", "그러고는 그 안간힘에 기력이 다해, 이번에는 잠든 것이 아니라 정신을 잃고 바닥에 쓰러졌다."]], [["The count leaned over her and raised her in his arms; and seeing that sweet pale face, those lovely eyes closed, that beautiful form motionless and to all appearance lifeless, the idea occurred to him for the first time, that perhaps she loved him otherwise than as a daughter loves a father.", "백작은 그녀에게 몸을 숙여 두 팔로 안아 올렸다. 그 달콤하고 창백한 얼굴, 감긴 그 사랑스러운 눈, 미동도 없이 보기에 따라서는 생기를 잃은 듯한 그 아름다운 몸을 보자, 어쩌면 그녀가 딸이 아버지를 사랑하는 것과는 다른 식으로 자신을 사랑하는지도 모른다는 생각이 그에게 처음으로 떠올랐다."]], [["“Alas,”", "\"아아,\""], ["murmured he, with intense suffering, “I might, then, have been happy yet.”", "그가 깊은 고통 속에 중얼거렸다. \"그렇다면 나는 아직도 행복할 수 있었을지 모른다.\""]], [["Then he carried Haydée to her room, resigned her to the care of her attendants, and returning to his study, which he shut quickly this time, he again copied the destroyed will.", "그러고 나서 그는 하이데를 그녀의 방으로 옮겨, 시녀들의 보살핌에 맡긴 뒤, 서재로 돌아와 이번에는 문을 재빨리 닫고는, 찢긴 유언장을 다시 베껴 적었다."], ["As he was finishing, the sound of a cabriolet entering the yard was heard.", "그가 다 마쳐 갈 무렵, 이륜마차 한 대가 마당으로 들어서는 소리가 들렸다."], ["Monte Cristo approached the window, and saw Maximilian and Emmanuel alight. “Good,”", "몬테크리스토는 창가로 다가가, 막시밀리앙과 에마뉘엘이 내리는 것을 보았다. \"좋아.\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he; “it was time,”—and he sealed his will with three seals.", "\"때맞춰 왔군.\" 그러고는 유언장에 세 개의 봉인을 찍었다."]], [["A moment afterwards he heard a noise in the drawing-room, and went to open the door himself.", "잠시 후 응접실에서 인기척이 들리자, 그는 직접 문을 열러 갔다."], ["Morrel was there; he had come twenty minutes before the time appointed.", "모렐이 거기 있었다. 약속한 시각보다 이십 분이나 일찍 온 것이었다."]], [["“I am perhaps come too soon, count,”", "\"제가 너무 일찍 왔는지도 모르겠습니다, 백작님.\""], ["said he, “but I frankly acknowledge that I have not closed my eyes all night, nor has anyone in my house.", "그가 말했다. \"하지만 솔직히 말씀드리면, 밤새 한숨도 못 잤습니다. 저희 집안 누구도 그랬고요."], ["I need to see you strong in your courageous assurance, to recover myself.”", "저 자신을 추스르기 위해서라도, 백작님이 그 용감한 확신으로 굳건히 계신 모습을 봐야겠습니다.\""]], [["Monte Cristo could not resist this proof of affection; he not only extended his hand to the young man, but flew to him with open arms.", "몬테크리스토는 이 애정의 증표에 마음이 흔들리지 않을 수 없었다. 그는 젊은이에게 손을 내미는 데 그치지 않고, 두 팔을 벌려 그에게 달려갔다."]], [["“Morrel,”", "\"모렐,\""], ["said he, “it is a happy day for me, to feel that I am beloved by such a man as you.", "그가 말했다. \"자네 같은 사람에게 사랑받는다는 것을 느끼니, 내게는 행복한 날일세."], ["Good-morning, Emmanuel; you will come with me then, Maximilian?”", "좋은 아침이네, 에마뉘엘. 그래, 자네는 나와 함께 가 주겠나, 막시밀리앙?\""]], [["“Did you doubt it?”", "\"의심하셨습니까?\""], ["said the young captain.", "젊은 대위가 말했다."]], [["“But if I were wrong——”", "\"하지만 내가 틀린 거라면……\""]], [["“I watched you during the whole scene of that challenge yesterday; I have been thinking of your firmness all night, and I said to myself that justice must be on your side, or man’s countenance is no longer to be relied on.”", "\"어제 그 결투 신청이 벌어지는 동안 내내 백작님을 지켜보았습니다. 밤새도록 백작님의 그 굳건함을 생각했고, 정의는 분명 백작님 편에 있을 수밖에 없다고, 그렇지 않다면 사람의 얼굴은 더 이상 믿을 게 못 된다고 혼잣말을 했습니다.\""]], [["“But, Morrel, Albert is your friend?”", "\"하지만, 모렐, 알베르는 자네 친구가 아닌가?\""]], [["“Simply an acquaintance, sir.”", "\"그저 아는 사이일 뿐입니다, 백작님.\""]], [["“You met on the same day you first saw me?”", "\"나를 처음 본 바로 그날 만났다고 했지?\""]], [["“Yes, that is true; but I should not have recollected it if you had not reminded me.”", "\"네, 그렇습니다. 하지만 백작님이 일깨워 주지 않으셨다면 저는 기억조차 못 했을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Thank you, Morrel.”", "\"고맙네, 모렐.\""], ["Then ringing the bell once, “Look.” said he to Ali, who came immediately, “take that to my solicitor.", "그러고 나서 종을 한 번 울리고는, 곧바로 들어온 알리에게 말했다. \"이걸 내 변호사에게 가져다주어라."], ["It is my will, Morrel.", "내 유언장일세, 모렐."], ["When I am dead, you will go and examine it.”", "내가 죽으면 자네가 가서 이것을 살펴보게.\""]], [["“What?”", "\"무슨 말씀이세요?\""], ["said Morrel, “you dead?”", "모렐이 말했다. \"백작님이 죽으신다니요?\""]], [["“Yes; must I not be prepared for everything, dear friend?", "\"그래. 모든 일에 대비해 두어야 하지 않겠나, 친애하는 벗이여?"], ["But what did you do yesterday after you left me?”", "그런데 어제 나와 헤어진 뒤에 자네는 무엇을 했나?\""]], [["“I went to Tortoni’s, where, as I expected, I found Beauchamp and Château-Renaud.", "\"토르토니에 갔습니다. 예상대로 거기서 보샹과 샤토르노를 만났지요."], ["I own I was seeking them.”", "사실 그들을 찾고 있었습니다.\""]], [["“Why, when all was arranged?”", "\"왜? 모든 게 다 정해졌는데?\""]], [["“Listen, count; the affair is serious and unavoidable.”", "\"들어 보십시오, 백작님. 이 일은 심각하고 피할 수 없는 일입니다.\""]], [["“Did you doubt it!”", "\"그걸 의심했나!\""]], [["“No; the offence was public, and everyone is already talking of it.”", "\"아니요. 모욕은 공공연한 것이었고, 이미 모두가 그 얘기를 하고 있습니다.\""]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서?\""]], [["“Well, I hoped to get an exchange of arms,—to substitute the sword for the pistol; the pistol is blind.”", "\"그래서, 저는 무기를 바꿀 수 있기를 바랐습니다. 권총 대신 검으로 말입니다. 권총은 눈먼 무기니까요.\""]], [["“Have you succeeded?” asked Monte Cristo quickly, with an imperceptible gleam of hope.", "\"성공했나?\" 몬테크리스토가 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 희미한 희망의 빛을 띠며 재빨리 물었다."]], [["“No; for your skill with the sword is so well known.”", "\"아니요. 검을 다루는 백작님의 솜씨가 워낙 잘 알려져 있어서요.\""]], [["“Ah?—who has betrayed me?”", "\"아? 누가 나를 일러바쳤나?\""]], [["“The skilful swordsman whom you have conquered.”", "\"백작님이 이긴 그 솜씨 좋은 검객이지요.\""]], [["“And you failed?”", "\"그래서 실패한 건가?\""]], [["“They positively refused.”", "\"그들이 단호히 거절했습니다.\""]], [["“Morrel,”", "\"모렐,\""], ["said the count, “have you ever seen me fire a pistol?” “Never.”", "백작이 말했다. \"내가 권총 쏘는 걸 본 적이 있나?\""]], [["“Well, we have time; look.”", "\"한 번도 없습니다.\""], ["Monte Cristo took the pistols he held in his hand when Mercédès entered, and fixing an ace of clubs against the iron plate, with four shots he successively shot off the four sides of the club.", "\"좋아, 시간이 있으니 보게.\" 몬테크리스토는 메르세데스가 들어왔을 때 손에 쥐고 있던 권총을 집어 들고, 클로버 에이스 카드를 철판에 고정해 두고는, 네 발로 클로버의 네 귀퉁이를 차례로 쏘아 떨어뜨렸다."], ["At each shot Morrel turned pale. He examined the bullets with which Monte Cristo performed this dexterous feat, and saw that they were no larger than buckshot.", "한 발 한 발이 울릴 때마다 모렐은 안색이 창백해졌다. 그는 몬테크리스토가 이 능란한 솜씨를 보이는 데 쓴 총알을 살펴보았는데, 그것이 산탄보다도 크지 않다는 것을 알았다."]], [["“It is astonishing,” said he.", "\"놀랍습니다.\" 그가 말했다."], ["“Look, Emmanuel.”", "\"보게, 에마뉘엘.\""], ["Then turning towards Monte Cristo, “Count,”", "그러고 나서 몬테크리스토를 향해 돌아서서 말했다."], ["said he, “in the name of all that is dear to you, I entreat you not to kill Albert!—the unhappy youth has a mother.”", "\"백작님, 백작님이 소중히 여기시는 모든 것의 이름으로 간청합니다. 부디 알베르를 죽이지 말아 주십시오! 그 불행한 젊은이에게는 어머니가 있습니다.\""]], [["“You are right,”", "\"자네 말이 옳네.\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “and I have none.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그리고 내게는 어머니가 없지.\""], ["These words were uttered in a tone which made Morrel shudder.", "이 말은 모렐을 몸서리치게 하는 어조로 흘러나왔다."]], [["“You are the offended party, count.”", "\"백작님이 모욕당한 쪽이십니다.\""]], [["“Doubtless; what does that imply?”", "\"틀림없지. 그게 무슨 뜻인가?\""]], [["“That you will fire first.”", "\"백작님이 먼저 쏘신다는 뜻입니다.\""]], [["“I fire first?”", "\"내가 먼저 쏜다고?\""]], [["“Oh, I obtained, or rather claimed that; we had conceded enough for them to yield us that.”", "\"오, 제가 얻어 냈습니다. 아니, 그것을 요구했지요. 그들이 그것만은 양보하게끔, 우리 쪽도 충분히 양보했으니까요.\""]], [["“And at what distance?”", "\"거리는 얼마나 되나?\""]], [["“Twenty paces.”", "\"스무 걸음입니다.\""], ["A smile of terrible import passed over the count’s lips.", "무시무시한 의미가 담긴 미소가 백작의 입가를 스쳤다."]], [["“Morrel,”", "\"모렐,\""], ["said he, “do not forget what you have just seen.”", "그가 말했다. \"방금 본 것을 잊지 말게.\""]], [["“The only chance for Albert’s safety, then, will arise from your emotion.”", "\"그렇다면 알베르가 살아남을 유일한 길은 백작님의 격한 감정에서 나오겠군요.\""]], [["“I suffer from emotion?”", "\"내가 감정에 휘둘린다고?\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“Or from your generosity, my friend; to so good a marksman as you are, I may say what would appear absurd to another.”", "\"아니면 백작님의 너그러움에서요, 친구여. 백작님처럼 뛰어난 사수에게는, 다른 사람에게라면 터무니없게 들릴 말도 할 수 있겠지요.\""]], [["“What is that?”", "\"그게 무엇인가?\""]], [["“Break his arm—wound him—but do not kill him.”", "\"그의 팔을 부러뜨리든, 상처를 입히든 하시되, 죽이지는 말아 주십시오.\""]], [["“I will tell you, Morrel,”", "\"내 말해 두지, 모렐.\""], ["said the count, “that I do not need entreating to spare the life of M.", "백작이 말했다. \"나는 모르세르 씨의 목숨을 살리는 데 간청 같은 건 필요치 않네."], ["de Morcerf; he shall be so well spared, that he will return quietly with his two friends, while I——”", "그는 아주 잘 살아남을 것이고, 두 친구와 함께 조용히 돌아갈 것일세. 그동안 나는……\""]], [["“And you?”", "\"백작님은요?\""]], [["“That will be another thing; I shall be brought home.”", "\"그건 다른 이야기가 되겠지. 나는 집으로 실려 갈 걸세.\""]], [["“No, no,” cried Maximilian, quite unable to restrain his feelings.", "\"안 됩니다, 안 돼요.\" 막시밀리앙이 감정을 도무지 억누르지 못한 채 외쳤다."]], [["“As I told you, my dear Morrel, M. de Morcerf will kill me.”", "\"내가 말했듯이, 친애하는 모렐, 모르세르 씨가 나를 죽일 걸세.\""]], [["Morrel looked at him in utter amazement.", "모렐은 완전히 어안이 벙벙해져 그를 바라보았다."], ["“But what has happened, then, since last evening, count?”", "\"그렇다면 어젯밤 이후로 무슨 일이 있었던 겁니까, 백작님?\""]], [["“The same thing that happened to Brutus the night before the battle of Philippi; I have seen a ghost.”", "\"필리피 전투 전날 밤 브루투스에게 일어났던 것과 똑같은 일이지. 나는 유령을 보았네.\""]], [["“And that ghost——”", "\"그 유령이……\""]], [["“Told me, Morrel, that I had lived long enough.”", "\"내게 말했네, 모렐, 내가 충분히 오래 살았다고.\""]], [["Maximilian and Emmanuel looked at each other.", "막시밀리앙과 에마뉘엘이 서로를 마주 보았다."], ["Monte Cristo drew out his watch. “Let us go,”", "몬테크리스토는 회중시계를 꺼냈다. \"가세.\""], ["said he; “it is five minutes past seven, and the appointment was for eight o’clock.”", "그가 말했다. \"일곱 시 오 분이고, 약속은 여덟 시였으니까.\""]], [["A carriage was in readiness at the door.", "마차 한 대가 문 앞에 채비를 마치고 있었다."], ["Monte Cristo stepped into it with his two friends.", "몬테크리스토는 두 친구와 함께 마차에 올랐다."], ["He had stopped a moment in the passage to listen at a door, and Maximilian and Emmanuel, who had considerately passed forward a few steps, thought they heard him answer by a sigh to a sob from within.", "그는 통로에서 잠시 멈춰 어느 문에 귀를 기울였는데, 사려 깊게 몇 걸음 앞서 나가 있던 막시밀리앙과 에마뉘엘은 안에서 들려온 흐느낌에 그가 한숨으로 답하는 소리를 들은 듯했다."], ["As the clock struck eight they drove up to the place of meeting.", "시계가 여덟 시를 칠 때 그들은 약속 장소에 마차를 댔다."]], [["“We are first,” said Morrel, looking out of the window.", "\"우리가 먼저 왔군요.\" 모렐이 창밖을 내다보며 말했다."]], [["“Excuse me, sir,”", "\"죄송합니다, 주인님.\""], ["said Baptistin, who had followed his master with indescribable terror, “but I think I see a carriage down there under the trees.”", "형용할 수 없는 두려움에 떨며 주인을 따라온 바티스탱이 말했다. \"하지만 저기 나무 아래 마차 한 대가 있는 것 같습니다.\""]], [["Monte Cristo sprang lightly from the carriage, and offered his hand to assist Emmanuel and Maximilian.", "몬테크리스토는 마차에서 가볍게 뛰어내려, 에마뉘엘과 막시밀리앙이 내리는 것을 도우려 손을 내밀었다."], ["The latter retained the count’s hand between his.", "막시밀리앙은 백작의 손을 자신의 두 손 사이에 잡아 두었다."]], [["“I like,”", "\"좋습니다,\""], ["said he, “to feel a hand like this, when its owner relies on the goodness of his cause.”", "그가 말했다. \"그 임자가 자기 대의의 정당함을 믿고 있을 때, 이런 손을 느끼는 것이 좋습니다.\""]], [["“It seems to me,”", "\"제가 보기엔,\""], ["said Emmanuel, “that I see two young men down there, who are evidently, waiting.”", "에마뉘엘이 말했다. \"저 아래에 분명히 기다리고 있는 듯한 젊은이 둘이 보입니다.\""]], [["Monte Cristo drew Morrel a step or two behind his brother-in-law.", "몬테크리스토는 모렐을 그의 매부 뒤로 한두 걸음 끌어당겼다."]], [["“Maximilian,”", "\"막시밀리앙,\""], ["said he, “are your affections disengaged?”", "그가 말했다. \"자네 마음은 누구에게도 매여 있지 않은가?\""], ["Morrel looked at Monte Cristo with astonishment.", "모렐이 놀라서 몬테크리스토를 바라보았다."], ["“I do not seek your confidence, my dear friend.", "\"자네의 속내를 알려는 게 아닐세, 친애하는 벗이여."], ["I only ask you a simple question; answer it;—that is all I require.”", "그저 간단한 질문 하나를 하는 것뿐이니, 대답해 주게. 내가 바라는 건 그것뿐일세.\""]], [["“I love a young girl, count.”", "\"한 처녀를 사랑하고 있습니다, 백작님.\""]], [["“Do you love her much?”", "\"많이 사랑하나?\""]], [["“More than my life.”", "\"제 목숨보다 더요.\""]], [["“Another hope defeated!”", "\"또 하나의 희망이 꺾였군!\""], ["said the count.", "백작이 말했다."], ["Then, with a sigh, “Poor Haydée!” murmured he.", "그러고는 한숨을 쉬며 중얼거렸다. \"가엾은 하이데!\""]], [["“To tell the truth, count, if I knew less of you, I should think that you were less brave than you are.”", "\"솔직히 말씀드리면, 백작님, 제가 백작님을 잘 모른다면, 백작님이 실제보다 덜 용감한 분이라고 여겼을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Because I sigh when thinking of someone I am leaving?", "\"내가 두고 떠나는 누군가를 떠올리며 한숨짓는다고 해서?"], ["Come, Morrel, it is not like a soldier to be so bad a judge of courage.", "이보게, 모렐, 군인이 용기를 그렇게 잘못 가늠한다는 건 어울리지 않는 일일세."], ["Do I regret life?", "내가 삶을 아쉬워한다고?"], ["What is it to me, who have passed twenty years between life and death?", "이십 년을 삶과 죽음 사이에서 보낸 내게, 그게 무슨 대수겠나?"], ["Moreover, do not alarm yourself, Morrel; this weakness, if it is such, is betrayed to you alone.", "게다가 너무 걱정하지 말게, 모렐. 이 약함이, 그게 약함이라면 말이지, 드러난 것은 자네에게뿐이니."], ["I know the world is a drawing-room, from which we must retire politely and honestly; that is, with a bow, and our debts of honor paid.”", "나는 세상이 하나의 응접실이며, 우리는 거기서 정중하고 떳떳하게 물러나야 한다는 것을 알고 있네. 다시 말해, 인사를 하고, 명예의 빚을 다 갚고서 말일세.\""]], [["“That is to the purpose.", "\"그게 바로 핵심이군요."], ["Have you brought your arms?”", "무기는 가져오셨습니까?\""]], [["“I?—what for?", "\"내가? 무엇하러?"], ["I hope these gentlemen have theirs.”", "저 신사들이 무기를 가져왔기를 바라네.\""]], [["“I will inquire,”", "\"제가 물어보겠습니다.\""], ["said Morrel.", "모렐이 말했다."]], [["“Do; but make no treaty—you understand me?”", "\"그러게. 하지만 어떤 협상도 하지 말게. 무슨 말인지 알겠나?\""]], [["“You need not fear.”", "\"염려 마십시오.\""], ["Morrel advanced towards Beauchamp and Château-Renaud, who, seeing his intention, came to meet him.", "모렐은 보샹과 샤토르노 쪽으로 다가갔고, 그의 의도를 알아챈 두 사람이 그를 맞으러 나왔다."], ["The three young men bowed to each other courteously, if not affably.", "세 젊은이는, 다정하지는 않더라도 정중하게 서로 인사를 나누었다."]], [["“Excuse me, gentlemen,”", "\"실례합니다, 신사 여러분.\""], ["said Morrel, “but I do not see M. de Morcerf.”", "모렐이 말했다. \"그런데 모르세르 씨가 보이지 않는군요.\""]], [["“He sent us word this morning,”", "\"오늘 아침 우리에게 전갈을 보내왔습니다.\""], ["replied Château-Renaud, “that he would meet us on the ground.”", "샤토르노가 대답했다. \"결투장에서 만나겠다고요.\""]], [["“Ah,” said Morrel.", "\"아.\" 모렐이 말했다."], ["Beauchamp pulled out his watch.", "보샹이 회중시계를 꺼냈다."]], [["“It is only five minutes past eight,”", "\"이제 겨우 여덟 시 오 분입니다.\""], ["said he to Morrel; “there is not much time lost yet.”", "그가 모렐에게 말했다. \"아직 그리 많이 늦은 건 아닙니다.\""]], [["“Oh, I made no allusion of that kind,”", "\"오, 저는 그런 뜻으로 말한 게 아니었습니다.\""], ["replied Morrel.", "모렐이 대답했다."]], [["“There is a carriage coming,”", "\"마차 한 대가 오고 있군요.\""], ["said Château-Renaud.", "샤토르노가 말했다."], ["It advanced rapidly along one of the avenues leading towards the open space where they were assembled.", "마차는 그들이 모여 있는 빈터로 이어지는 가로수길 가운데 하나를 따라 빠르게 다가왔다."]], [["“You are doubtless provided with pistols, gentlemen? M.", "\"신사 여러분, 분명 권총을 준비해 오셨겠지요?"], ["de Monte Cristo yields his right of using his.”", "몬테크리스토 씨는 자신의 권총을 쓸 권리를 양보하셨습니다.\""]], [["“We had anticipated this kindness on the part of the count,”", "\"백작님의 그런 호의를 우리도 예상하고 있었습니다.\""], ["said Beauchamp, “and I have brought some weapons which I bought eight or ten days since, thinking to want them on a similar occasion.", "보샹이 말했다. \"그래서 제가 이런 경우에 쓸 일이 있으리라 생각하고 여드레인가 열흘 전에 사 둔 무기 몇 자루를 가져왔습니다."], ["They are quite new, and have not yet been used.", "아주 새것이라 아직 쓴 적이 없지요."], ["Will you examine them.”", "살펴보시겠습니까?\""]], [["“Oh, M. Beauchamp, if you assure me that M. de Morcerf does not know these pistols, you may readily believe that your word will be quite sufficient.”", "\"오, 보샹 씨, 모르세르 씨가 이 권총들을 모른다고 장담하신다면, 당신의 말씀만으로도 충분하다는 걸 기꺼이 믿겠습니다.\""]], [["“Gentlemen,”", "\"신사 여러분,\""], ["said Château-Renaud, “it is not Morcerf coming in that carriage;—faith, it is Franz and Debray!”", "샤토르노가 말했다. \"저 마차로 오는 건 모르세르가 아닙니다. 이런, 프란츠와 드브레인데요!\""]], [["The two young men he announced were indeed approaching.", "그가 알린 두 젊은이가 정말로 다가오고 있었다."], ["“What chance brings you here, gentlemen?” said Château-Renaud, shaking hands with each of them.", "\"무슨 일로 여기까지 오셨습니까, 신사 여러분?\" 샤토르노가 둘과 차례로 악수하며 말했다."]], [["“Because,”", "\"그게 말이오,\""], ["said Debray, “Albert sent this morning to request us to come.”", "드브레가 말했다. \"알베르가 오늘 아침 우리에게 사람을 보내 와 달라고 청했소.\""], ["Beauchamp and Château-Renaud exchanged looks of astonishment.", "보샹과 샤토르노가 놀란 눈빛을 주고받았다."], ["“I think I understand his reason,”", "\"제가 그 까닭을 알 것 같습니다.\""], ["said Morrel.", "모렐이 말했다."]], [["“What is it?”", "\"무엇입니까?\""]], [["“Yesterday afternoon I received a letter from M. de Morcerf, begging me to attend the Opera.”", "\"어제 오후 모르세르 씨에게서 편지를 받았는데, 오페라에 와 달라는 부탁이었습니다.\""]], [["“And I,” said Debray.", "\"나도 그랬소.\" 드브레가 말했다."]], [["“And I also,”", "\"나도 그랬습니다.\""], ["said Franz.", "프란츠가 말했다."]], [["“And we, too,” added Beauchamp and Château-Renaud.", "\"우리도 그랬지요.\" 보샹과 샤토르노가 덧붙였다."]], [["“Having wished you all to witness the challenge, he now wishes you to be present at the combat.”", "\"여러분 모두에게 결투 신청을 지켜봐 달라고 청했으니, 이제 결투에도 참석해 달라는 것이군요.\""]], [["“Exactly so,”", "\"바로 그렇습니다.\""], ["said the young men; “you have probably guessed right.”", "젊은이들이 말했다. \"아마 제대로 짚으셨군요.\""]], [["“But, after all these arrangements, he does not come himself,”", "\"하지만 이 모든 준비를 해 놓고서, 정작 본인은 오지 않는군요.\""], ["said Château-Renaud.", "샤토르노가 말했다."], ["“Albert is ten minutes after time.”", "\"알베르가 십 분이나 늦었습니다.\""]], [["“There he comes,”", "\"저기 오는군요.\""], ["said Beauchamp, “on horseback, at full gallop, followed by a servant.”", "보샹이 말했다. \"말을 타고 전속력으로 달려오고, 하인이 뒤따르고 있습니다.\""]], [["“How imprudent,”", "\"경솔하기도 하지.\""], ["said Château-Renaud, “to come on horseback to fight a duel with pistols, after all the instructions I had given him.”", "샤토르노가 말했다. \"권총 결투를 하러 말을 타고 오다니, 내가 그렇게 일러 두었는데도 말이야.\""]], [["“And besides,”", "\"게다가,\""], ["said Beauchamp, “with a collar above his cravat, an open coat and white waistcoat!", "보샹이 말했다. \"크라바트 위로 깃을 세우고, 외투를 풀어 헤치고, 흰 조끼라니!"], ["Why has he not painted a spot upon his heart?—it would have been more simple.”", "차라리 심장에 표적을 하나 그려 두지 그랬을까. 그게 더 간단했을 텐데.\""]], [["Meanwhile Albert had arrived within ten paces of the group formed by the five young men.", "그러는 사이 알베르는 다섯 젊은이가 이룬 무리에서 열 걸음 안까지 다가왔다."], ["He jumped from his horse, threw the bridle on his servant’s arms, and joined them.", "그는 말에서 뛰어내려 고삐를 하인의 팔에 던지고는 그들에게 합류했다."], ["He was pale, and his eyes were red and swollen; it was evident that he had not slept.", "그는 안색이 창백했고, 눈은 충혈되어 부어 있었다. 한숨도 자지 못한 게 분명했다."], ["A shade of melancholy gravity overspread his countenance, which was not natural to him.", "그의 얼굴에는 본래 그에게는 어울리지 않는 우울하고 무거운 그늘이 드리워 있었다."]], [["“I thank you, gentlemen,”", "\"여러분, 고맙습니다.\""], ["said he, “for having complied with my request; I feel extremely grateful for this mark of friendship.”", "그가 말했다. \"제 청을 들어주셔서요. 이런 우정의 표시에 더없이 감사하게 생각합니다.\""], ["Morrel had stepped back as Morcerf approached, and remained at a short distance.", "모르세르가 다가오자 모렐은 뒤로 물러나 조금 떨어진 곳에 머물러 있었다."], ["“And to you also, M. Morrel, my thanks are due.", "\"그리고 당신에게도, 모렐 씨, 감사를 드려야겠군요."], ["Come, there cannot be too many.” 40248m", "자, 사람은 많을수록 좋지요.\""]], [["“Sir,”", "\"선생,\""], ["said Maximilian, “you are not perhaps aware that I am M. de Monte Cristo’s friend?”", "막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"어쩌면 모르실지 모르겠는데, 저는 몬테크리스토 씨의 친구입니다.\""]], [["“I was not sure, but I thought it might be so.", "\"확실히는 몰랐지만, 그럴지도 모른다고 생각했습니다."], ["So much the better; the more honorable men there are here the better I shall be satisfied.”", "그렇다면 더욱 잘된 일이지요. 여기 명예로운 분들이 많을수록 저는 더 흡족할 테니까요.\""]], [["“M. Morrel,”", "\"모렐 씨,\""], ["said Château-Renaud, “will you apprise the Count of Monte Cristo that M. de Morcerf is arrived, and we are at his disposal?”", "샤토르노가 말했다. \"몬테크리스토 백작께 모르세르 씨가 도착했고 우리가 백작의 처분을 기다린다고 알려 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["Morrel was preparing to fulfil his commission.", "모렐은 그 임무를 수행하려 채비했다."], ["Beauchamp had meanwhile drawn the box of pistols from the carriage.", "그사이 보샹은 마차에서 권총 상자를 꺼냈다."]], [["“Stop, gentlemen,”", "\"잠깐만요, 여러분.\""], ["said Albert; “I have two words to say to the Count of Monte Cristo.”", "알베르가 말했다. \"몬테크리스토 백작께 드릴 말씀이 두 마디 있습니다.\""]], [["“In private?”", "\"단둘이서요?\""], ["asked Morrel.", "모렐이 물었다."]], [["“No, sir; before all who are here.”", "\"아닙니다, 선생. 여기 계신 모든 분 앞에서요.\""]], [["Albert’s witnesses looked at each other.", "알베르의 입회인들이 서로를 바라보았다."], ["Franz and Debray exchanged some words in a whisper, and Morrel, rejoiced at this unexpected incident, went to fetch the count, who was walking in a retired path with Emmanuel.", "프란츠와 드브레가 속삭이듯 몇 마디를 주고받았고, 이 뜻밖의 사태에 기뻐한 모렐은 백작을 데리러 갔다. 백작은 에마뉘엘과 함께 외진 오솔길을 걷고 있었다."]], [["“What does he want with me?”", "\"그가 내게 무슨 볼일이 있다는 건가?\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“I do not know, but he wishes to speak to you.”", "\"모르겠습니다. 하지만 백작님과 이야기하고 싶어 합니다.\""]], [["“Ah?”", "\"아?\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “I trust he is not going to tempt me by some fresh insult!”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그가 또 새로운 모욕으로 나를 시험하려는 게 아니길 바라네!\""]], [["“I do not think that such is his intention,” said Morrel.", "\"그런 의도는 아닌 듯합니다.\" 모렐이 말했다."]], [["The count advanced, accompanied by Maximilian and Emmanuel.", "백작은 막시밀리앙과 에마뉘엘을 데리고 앞으로 나아갔다."], ["His calm and serene look formed a singular contrast to Albert’s grief-stricken face, who approached also, followed by the other four young men.", "그의 차분하고 평온한 표정은, 다른 네 젊은이를 거느린 채 역시 다가오는 알베르의 비탄에 잠긴 얼굴과 묘한 대조를 이루었다."]], [["When at three paces distant from each other, Albert and the count stopped.", "서로 세 걸음 거리에 이르자, 알베르와 백작이 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“Approach, gentlemen,”", "\"가까이 오십시오, 여러분.\""], ["said Albert; “I wish you not to lose one word of what I am about to have the honor of saying to the Count of Monte Cristo, for it must be repeated by you to all who will listen to it, strange as it may appear to you.”", "알베르가 말했다. \"제가 이제 몬테크리스토 백작께 영광스럽게 드릴 말씀을, 한마디도 놓치지 않으시길 바랍니다. 여러분에게 아무리 이상하게 들리더라도, 이것은 그것을 들으려는 모든 사람에게 여러분이 전해 주셔야 할 말이기 때문입니다.\""]], [["“Proceed, sir,” said the count.", "\"말씀하시지요, 선생.\" 백작이 말했다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"선생,\""], ["said Albert, at first with a tremulous voice, but which gradually became firmer, “I reproached you with exposing the conduct of M.", "알베르가 처음에는 떨리는 목소리로, 그러나 점차 더 굳건해지는 목소리로 말했다. \"저는 당신이 에피루스에서의 모르세르 씨의 행적을 폭로한 것을 두고 당신을 비난했습니다."], ["de Morcerf in Epirus, for guilty as I knew he was, I thought you had no right to punish him; but I have since learned that you had that right.", "그가 죄를 지었다는 것을 알면서도, 당신에게는 그를 벌할 권리가 없다고 여겼기 때문입니다. 그러나 그 후 저는 당신에게 그 권리가 있다는 것을 알게 되었습니다."], ["It is not Fernand Mondego’s treachery towards Ali Pasha which induces me so readily to excuse you, but the treachery of the fisherman Fernand towards you, and the almost unheard-of miseries which were its consequences; and I say, and proclaim it publicly, that you were justified in revenging yourself on my father, and I, his son, thank you for not using greater severity.”", "제가 이토록 선뜻 당신을 용서하게 된 것은 페르낭 몬데고가 알리 파샤에게 저지른 배신 때문이 아니라, 어부 페르낭이 당신에게 저지른 배신, 그리고 그로 인해 빚어진, 거의 들어 본 적 없는 비참함 때문입니다. 그러니 저는 말합니다, 그리고 공공연히 선언합니다. 당신이 제 아버지에게 복수한 것은 정당한 일이었으며, 그의 아들인 저는 당신이 더 가혹하게 굴지 않은 것에 감사드립니다.\""]], [["Had a thunderbolt fallen in the midst of the spectators of this unexpected scene, it would not have surprised them more than did Albert’s declaration.", "이 뜻밖의 장면을 지켜보던 이들 한가운데에 벼락이 떨어졌다 한들, 알베르의 선언만큼 그들을 놀라게 하지는 못했을 것이다."], ["As for Monte Cristo, his eyes slowly rose towards heaven with an expression of infinite gratitude.", "몬테크리스토로 말하자면, 그의 두 눈이 한없는 감사의 빛을 띠며 천천히 하늘을 향해 들렸다."], ["He could not understand how Albert’s fiery nature, of which he had seen so much among the Roman bandits, had suddenly stooped to this humiliation.", "그는 로마의 산적들 사이에서 숱하게 보았던 알베르의 그 불같은 성정이 어떻게 이런 굴욕에 갑자기 몸을 굽힐 수 있었는지 이해할 수 없었다."], ["He recognized the influence of Mercédès, and saw why her noble heart had not opposed the sacrifice she knew beforehand would be useless.", "그는 거기서 메르세데스의 영향력을 알아보았고, 어찌하여 그녀의 고결한 마음이, 소용없으리라는 것을 미리 알면서도 그 희생에 맞서지 않았는지를 깨달았다."]], [["“Now, sir,”", "\"이제, 선생,\""], ["said Albert, “if you think my apology sufficient, pray give me your hand.", "알베르가 말했다. \"제 사과가 충분하다고 생각하신다면, 부디 제게 손을 내밀어 주십시오."], ["Next to the merit of infallibility which you appear to possess, I rank that of candidly acknowledging a fault.", "당신이 지닌 듯한 그 오류 없음의 미덕 다음으로, 저는 잘못을 솔직히 인정하는 미덕을 높이 칩니다."], ["But this confession concerns me only.", "하지만 이 고백은 저 자신에게만 관계된 일입니다."], ["I acted well as a man, but you have acted better than man.", "저는 인간으로서 잘 처신했으나, 당신은 인간 이상으로 처신하셨습니다."], ["An angel alone could have saved one of us from death—that angel came from heaven, if not to make us friends (which, alas, fatality renders impossible), at least to make us esteem each other.”", "우리 둘 중 하나를 죽음에서 구할 수 있었던 것은 오직 천사뿐이었을 테고, 그 천사가 하늘에서 내려왔습니다. 우리를 친구로 만들기 위해서는 아니었더라도, 아아, 운명이 그것을 불가능하게 만드니까요, 적어도 서로를 존중하게 만들기 위해서 말입니다.\""]], [["Monte Cristo, with moistened eye, heaving breast, and lips half open, extended to Albert a hand which the latter pressed with a sentiment resembling respectful fear.", "몬테크리스토는 촉촉해진 눈과 들썩이는 가슴, 반쯤 벌어진 입술로 알베르에게 손을 내밀었고, 알베르는 경외에 가까운 두려움이 어린 마음으로 그 손을 잡았다."]], [["“Gentlemen,”", "\"여러분,\""], ["said he, “M.", "그가 말했다."], ["de Monte Cristo receives my apology.", "\"몬테크리스토 씨가 제 사과를 받아 주셨습니다."], ["I had acted hastily towards him.", "저는 그분께 성급하게 굴었습니다."], ["Hasty actions are generally bad ones.", "성급한 행동은 대개 나쁜 행동이지요."], ["Now my fault is repaired.", "이제 제 잘못은 바로잡혔습니다."], ["I hope the world will not call me cowardly for acting as my conscience dictated.", "제가 양심이 이르는 대로 행동했다 하여, 세상이 저를 비겁하다 일컫지 않기를 바랍니다."], ["But if anyone should entertain a false opinion of me,”", "그러나 만일 누군가 저에 대해 잘못된 의견을 품는다면,\""], ["added he, drawing himself up as if he would challenge both friends and enemies, “I shall endeavor to correct his mistake.”", "그가 마치 친구와 적 모두에게 도전하듯 몸을 곧추세우며 덧붙였다. \"저는 그 오해를 바로잡으려 애쓸 것입니다.\""]], [["“What happened during the night?”", "\"밤사이 무슨 일이 있었던 거지?\""], ["asked Beauchamp of Château-Renaud; “we appear to make a very sorry figure here.”", "보샹이 샤토르노에게 물었다. \"우리가 여기서 아주 한심한 꼴이 된 것 같은데.\""]], [["“In truth, what Albert has just done is either very despicable or very noble,”", "\"사실, 알베르가 방금 한 일은 아주 비열하거나, 아니면 아주 고결하거나, 둘 중 하나일세.\""], ["replied the baron.", "남작이 대답했다."]], [["“What can it mean?”", "\"이게 무슨 뜻일까?\""], ["said Debray to Franz.", "드브레가 프란츠에게 말했다."], ["“The Count of Monte Cristo acts dishonorably to M. de Morcerf, and is justified by his son!", "\"몬테크리스토 백작이 모르세르 씨에게 명예롭지 못하게 행동했는데, 정작 그 아들이 그를 정당하다고 두둔하다니!"], ["Had I ten Yaninas in my family, I should only consider myself the more bound to fight ten times.”", "내 집안에 야니나가 열 개쯤 있다 해도, 나라면 오히려 열 번을 더 싸워야 할 의무가 있다고 여겼을 텐데.\""]], [["As for Monte Cristo, his head was bent down, his arms were powerless.", "몬테크리스토로 말하자면, 그는 고개를 떨군 채 두 팔에 힘이 빠져 있었다."], ["Bowing under the weight of twenty-four years’ reminiscences, he thought not of Albert, of Beauchamp, of Château-Renaud, or of any of that group; but he thought of that courageous woman who had come to plead for her son’s life, to whom he had offered his, and who had now saved it by the revelation of a dreadful family secret, capable of destroying forever in that young man’s heart every feeling of filial piety.", "이십사 년의 회상의 무게에 짓눌린 채, 그는 알베르도, 보샹도, 샤토르노도, 그 무리의 누구도 생각하지 않았다. 다만 그는, 아들의 목숨을 구걸하러 온 그 용감한 여인을 생각하고 있었다. 그가 자신의 목숨을 내어 준 그 여인, 그리고 이제 그 젊은이의 마음속에서 자식 된 도리의 온갖 정을 영원히 무너뜨릴 수 있는 끔찍한 집안의 비밀을 폭로함으로써 그 목숨을 구해 낸 그 여인을."]], [["“Providence still,”", "\"역시 섭리로다.\""], ["murmured he; “now only am I fully convinced of being the emissary of God!”", "그가 중얼거렸다. \"이제야 비로소 내가 신의 사자임을 온전히 확신한다!\""]], [["# Chapter 91. Mother and Son", "# Chapter 91. Mother and Son"]], [["The Count of Monte Cristo bowed to the five young men with a melancholy and dignified smile, and got into his carriage with Maximilian and Emmanuel.", "몬테크리스토 백작은 우수와 위엄이 어린 미소를 지으며 다섯 청년에게 고개 숙여 인사하고는, 막시밀리앙과 에마뉘엘과 함께 마차에 올랐다."], ["Albert, Beauchamp, and Château-Renaud remained alone.", "알베르와 보샹, 샤토르노 세 사람만 남았다."], ["Albert looked at his two friends, not timidly, but in a way that appeared to ask their opinion of what he had just done.", "알베르는 두 친구를 바라보았다. 겁먹은 눈빛은 아니었으나, 자신이 방금 한 일에 대한 두 사람의 생각을 묻는 듯한 눈빛이었다."]], [["“Indeed, my dear friend,”", "\"솔직히 말하지, 친구.\""], ["said Beauchamp first, who had either the most feeling or the least dissimulation, “allow me to congratulate you; this is a very unhoped-for conclusion of a very disagreeable affair.”", "가장 정이 깊거나 가장 속을 감추지 못하는 보샹이 먼저 입을 열었다. \"축하 한마디 하게 해 주게. 아주 불쾌한 일이 뜻밖의 방식으로 마무리됐으니 말일세.\""]], [["Albert remained silent and wrapped in thought.", "알베르는 말없이 생각에 잠겨 있었다."], ["Château-Renaud contented himself with tapping his boot with his flexible cane.", "샤토르노는 휘청거리는 단장으로 자기 장화를 톡톡 두드릴 뿐이었다."]], [["“Are we not going?” said he, after this embarrassing silence.", "\"이제 가지 않겠나?\" 그가 거북한 침묵 끝에 말했다."]], [["“When you please,”", "\"자네가 원할 때 가지.\""], ["replied Beauchamp; “allow me only to compliment M.", "보샹이 대답했다. \"다만 모르세르 씨에게 한마디 칭찬은 하게 해 주게."], ["de Morcerf, who has given proof today of rare chivalric generosity.”", "오늘 보기 드문 기사도적 아량을 보여 주었으니까.\""]], [["“Oh, yes,” said Château-Renaud.", "\"아, 그래.\" 샤토르노가 말했다."]], [["“It is magnificent,” continued Beauchamp, “to be able to exercise so much self-control!”", "\"저렇게 자제력을 발휘할 수 있다니, 참으로 대단한 일이야.\" 보샹이 말을 이었다."]], [["“Assuredly; as for me, I should have been incapable of it,”", "\"분명히 그렇지. 나라면 도저히 못 했을 걸세.\""], ["said Château-Renaud, with most significant coolness.", "샤토르노가 의미심장하리만치 냉랭하게 말했다."]], [["“Gentlemen,”", "\"두 사람,\""], ["interrupted Albert, “I think you did not understand that something very serious had passed between M. de Monte Cristo and myself.”", "알베르가 끼어들었다. \"몬테크리스토 씨와 나 사이에 아주 중대한 일이 오갔다는 걸 자네들은 미처 모르는 것 같군.\""]], [["“Possibly, possibly,”", "\"그럴 수도 있지, 그럴 수도 있어.\""], ["said Beauchamp immediately; “but every simpleton would not be able to understand your heroism, and sooner or later you will find yourself compelled to explain it to them more energetically than would be convenient to your bodily health and the duration of your life.", "보샹이 곧바로 말했다. \"하지만 멍청이들은 죄다 자네의 그 영웅적 태도를 이해하지 못할 테고, 머지않아 자넨 그들에게 그걸 설명해야 할 처지에 놓일 걸세. 그것도 자네 몸 건강과 목숨이 견뎌 낼 수 있는 정도를 훨씬 넘어서는 격렬한 방식으로 말이야."], ["May I give you a friendly counsel?", "친구로서 한마디 충고해도 되겠나?"], ["Set out for Naples, the Hague, or St. Petersburg—calm countries, where the point of honor is better understood than among our hot-headed Parisians.", "나폴리나 헤이그, 상트페테르부르크로 떠나게. 우리 다혈질 파리 사람들보다 명예의 문제를 더 잘 헤아리는 차분한 나라들이지."], ["Seek quietude and oblivion, so that you may return peaceably to France after a few years.", "그곳에서 평온과 망각을 찾고, 몇 해 뒤에 잠잠해지면 프랑스로 돌아오게나."], ["Am I not right, M. de Château-Renaud?” 40252m", "안 그런가, 샤토르노 씨?\""]], [["“That is quite my opinion,”", "\"내 생각도 똑같네.\""], ["said the gentleman; “nothing induces serious duels so much as a duel forsworn.”", "샤토르노가 말했다. \"거절당한 결투만큼 더 심각한 결투를 불러오는 것도 없으니까.\""]], [["“Thank you, gentlemen,”", "\"고맙네, 두 사람.\""], ["replied Albert, with a smile of indifference; “I shall follow your advice—not because you give it, but because I had before intended to quit France.", "알베르가 무심한 미소를 띠고 대답했다. \"충고를 따르도록 하지. 자네들이 권해서가 아니라, 나도 전부터 프랑스를 떠날 생각이었으니까."], ["I thank you equally for the service you have rendered me in being my seconds.", "내 입회인이 되어 준 일도 똑같이 고맙게 생각하네."], ["It is deeply engraved on my heart, and, after what you have just said, I remember that only.”", "그건 내 마음 깊이 새겨졌으니, 방금 자네들이 한 말을 듣고서도 나는 그것만 기억할 걸세.\""]], [["Château-Renaud and Beauchamp looked at each other; the impression was the same on both of them, and the tone in which Morcerf had just expressed his thanks was so determined that the position would have become embarrassing for all if the conversation had continued.", "샤토르노와 보샹은 서로를 마주 보았다. 두 사람의 인상은 같았고, 방금 모르세르가 고마움을 표한 말투가 어찌나 단호했던지 대화를 더 이어 갔다가는 모두에게 거북한 상황이 될 판이었다."]], [["“Good-bye, Albert,” said Beauchamp suddenly, carelessly extending his hand to the young man.", "\"잘 가게, 알베르.\" 보샹이 불쑥 말하며 무심하게 청년에게 손을 내밀었다."], ["The latter did not appear to arouse from his lethargy; in fact, he did not notice the offered hand.", "알베르는 무기력에서 깨어날 기색이 없었고, 사실 내민 손을 알아채지도 못했다."]], [["“Good-bye,”", "\"잘 가게.\""], ["said Château-Renaud in his turn, keeping his little cane in his left hand, and saluting with his right.", "이번에는 샤토르노가 말했다. 그는 작은 단장을 왼손에 쥐고 오른손으로 인사를 건넸다."]], [["Albert’s lips scarcely whispered “Good-bye,” but his look was more explicit; it expressed a whole poem of restrained anger, proud disdain, and generous indignation.", "알베르의 입술은 가까스로 \"잘 가게.\"라고 속삭였을 뿐이었으나, 눈빛은 더 많은 것을 말하고 있었다. 그 눈빛에는 억눌린 분노와 도도한 경멸, 그리고 떳떳한 의분이 한 편의 시처럼 담겨 있었다."], ["He preserved his melancholy and motionless position for some time after his two friends had regained their carriage; then suddenly unfastening his horse from the little tree to which his servant had tied it, he mounted and galloped off in the direction of Paris.", "그는 두 친구가 마차에 다시 오른 뒤에도 한동안 우수에 잠긴 채 미동도 없이 그 자리에 서 있었다. 그러다 하인이 작은 나무에 매어 둔 말을 풀더니, 훌쩍 올라타 파리 쪽으로 말을 몰아 내달렸다."]], [["In a quarter of an hour he was entering the house in the Rue du Helder.", "십오 분 만에 그는 엘데 거리의 저택으로 들어섰다."], ["As he alighted, he thought he saw his father’s pale face behind the curtain of the count’s bedroom.", "마차에서 내리던 그는 백작의 침실 커튼 뒤로 아버지의 창백한 얼굴이 보이는 듯했다."], ["Albert turned away his head with a sigh, and went to his own apartments.", "알베르는 한숨을 쉬며 고개를 돌리고 제 방으로 향했다."], ["He cast one lingering look on all the luxuries which had rendered life so easy and so happy since his infancy; he looked at the pictures, whose faces seemed to smile, and the landscapes, which appeared painted in brighter colors.", "그는 어린 시절부터 삶을 그토록 편안하고 행복하게 만들어 준 온갖 사치품들을 미련 어린 눈길로 둘러보았다. 미소 짓는 듯한 얼굴들이 담긴 그림들을, 한층 환한 빛깔로 그려진 듯한 풍경화들을 바라보았다. 그런 다음 떡갈나무 액자에 든 어머니의 초상화를 떼어 냈다."], ["Then he took away his mother’s portrait, with its oaken frame, leaving the gilt frame from which he took it black and empty.", "초상을 빼낸 자리에는 검고 텅 빈 금테 액자만 남았다. 이어 아름다운 터키 무기들과 멋진 영국 총들, 일본 자기, 은으로 받침을 두른 잔들, 푀셰르와 바리●가 만든 예술적인 청동 조각들을 가지런히 정돈했다. 찬장을 살피고는 저마다 열쇠를 꽂아 두었다."], ["Then he arranged all his beautiful Turkish arms, his fine English guns, his Japanese china, his cups mounted in silver, his artistic bronzes by Feuchères or Barye; examined the cupboards, and placed the key in each; threw into a drawer of his secretaire, which he left open, all the pocket-money he had about him, and with it the thousand fancy jewels from his vases and his jewel-boxes; then he made an exact inventory of everything, and placed it in the most conspicuous part of the table, after putting aside the books and papers which had collected there.", "열어 둔 책상 서랍 하나에 지니고 있던 잔돈을 전부 던져 넣고, 거기에 화병과 보석함에서 꺼낸 자질구레한 장신구 천 가지를 함께 넣었다. 그러고는 모든 물건의 정확한 목록을 작성해, 그곳에 쌓여 있던 책과 서류를 한쪽으로 치운 뒤 탁자 위 가장 눈에 잘 띄는 자리에 놓았다. * 푀셰르와 바리: 19세기 프랑스의 유명한 조각가들.―옮긴이"]], [["At the beginning of this work, his servant, notwithstanding orders to the contrary, came to his room.", "이 일을 막 시작했을 때, 들어오지 말라는 분부에도 불구하고 하인이 그의 방으로 들어왔다."]], [["“What do you want?”", "\"무슨 일인가?\""], ["asked he, with a more sorrowful than angry tone.", "그가 화났다기보다 서글픈 어조로 물었다."]], [["“Pardon me, sir,”", "\"용서하십시오, 도련님.\""], ["replied the valet; “you had forbidden me to disturb you, but the Count of Morcerf has called me.”", "하인이 대답했다. \"방해하지 말라고 분부하셨습니다만, 모르세르 백작님께서 저를 부르셨습니다.\""]], [["“Well!”", "\"그래서!\""], ["said Albert.", "알베르가 말했다."]], [["“I did not like to go to him without first seeing you.”", "\"먼저 도련님을 뵙지 않고는 가고 싶지 않았습니다.\""]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜?\""]], [["“Because the count is doubtless aware that I accompanied you to the meeting this morning.” 40254m", "\"백작님께서 오늘 아침 제가 도련님을 모시고 그 자리에 갔던 걸 분명 알고 계실 테니까요.\""]], [["“It is probable,” said Albert.", "\"그렇겠지.\" 알베르가 말했다."]], [["“And since he has sent for me, it is doubtless to question me on what happened there.", "\"백작님께서 저를 부르신 건 거기서 무슨 일이 있었는지 캐물으시려는 게 틀림없습니다."], ["What must I answer?”", "뭐라고 답해야 할까요?\""]], [["“The truth.”", "\"사실대로.\""]], [["“Then I shall say the duel did not take place?”", "\"그럼 결투가 벌어지지 않았다고 말씀드릴까요?\""]], [["“You will say I apologized to the Count of Monte Cristo. Go.”", "\"내가 몬테크리스토 백작에게 사과했다고 말하게. 가 보게.\""]], [["The valet bowed and retired, and Albert returned to his inventory. As he was finishing this work, the sound of horses prancing in the yard, and the wheels of a carriage shaking his window, attracted his attention.", "하인은 고개 숙여 인사하고 물러갔고, 알베르는 다시 목록 작성으로 돌아갔다. 그 일을 마무리할 무렵, 마당에서 말들이 발을 구르는 소리와 마차 바퀴가 창문을 흔드는 소리에 주의가 쏠렸다."], ["He approached the window, and saw his father get into it, and drive away.", "그는 창가로 다가갔다. 아버지가 마차에 올라 떠나는 모습이 보였다."], ["The door was scarcely closed when Albert bent his steps to his mother’s room; and, no one being there to announce him, he advanced to her bedchamber, and distressed by what he saw and guessed, stopped for one moment at the door.", "문이 닫히기가 무섭게 알베르는 어머니의 방으로 발걸음을 옮겼다. 그를 알릴 사람이 아무도 없었으므로 그는 곧장 침실로 들어섰고, 눈앞에 펼쳐진 광경과 짐작되는 바에 가슴이 미어져 잠시 문 앞에 멈춰 섰다."]], [["As if the same idea had animated these two beings, Mercédès was doing the same in her apartments that he had just done in his.", "마치 같은 생각이 두 사람을 사로잡기라도 한 듯, 메르세데스는 자기 방에서 알베르가 방금 제 방에서 한 것과 똑같은 일을 하고 있었다."], ["Everything was in order,—laces, dresses, jewels, linen, money, all were arranged in the drawers, and the countess was carefully collecting the keys.", "모든 것이 정돈되어 있었다. 레이스며 드레스며 보석이며 속옷이며 돈이며, 전부 서랍 속에 정리되어 있었고, 백작 부인은 열쇠들을 조심스레 챙기고 있었다."], ["Albert saw all these preparations and understood them, and exclaiming, “My mother!”", "알베르는 이 모든 채비를 보았고 그 뜻을 알아차렸다. 그는 \"어머니!\""], ["he threw his arms around her neck.", "하고 부르짖으며 두 팔로 어머니의 목을 끌어안았다."]], [["The artist who could have depicted the expression of these two countenances would certainly have made of them a beautiful picture.", "이 두 얼굴의 표정을 그려 낼 수 있는 화가라면 분명 그것으로 한 폭의 아름다운 그림을 완성했으리라."], ["All these proofs of an energetic resolution, which Albert did not fear on his own account, alarmed him for his mother.", "단호한 결심을 보여 주는 이 모든 흔적이, 자기 자신을 위해서라면 두렵지 않았을 알베르였으나 어머니를 위해서는 그를 불안하게 만들었다."], ["“What are you doing?”", "\"무엇을 하고 계신 겁니까?\""], ["asked he.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“What were you doing?”", "\"너는 무엇을 하고 있었느냐?\""], ["replied she.", "어머니가 되물었다."]], [["“Oh, my mother!”", "\"아, 어머니!\" 알베르가 너무도 북받쳐 가까스로 말을 이었다. \"어머니와 저는 다릅니다."], ["exclaimed Albert, so overcome he could scarcely speak; “it is not the same with you and me—you cannot have made the same resolution I have, for I have come to warn you that I bid adieu to your house, and—and to you.”", "어머니가 저와 같은 결심을 하셨을 리 없어요. 저는 어머니께 이 집과, 그리고…… 어머니께 작별을 고하러 왔으니까요.\""]], [["“I also,”", "\"나 역시,\" 메르세데스가 대답했다."], ["replied Mercédès, “am going, and I acknowledge I had depended on your accompanying me; have I deceived myself?”", "\"떠나려던 참이었다. 솔직히 말하면 네가 나와 함께 가 주리라 믿고 있었지. 내가 잘못 생각한 거냐?\""]], [["“Mother,”", "\"어머니.\""], ["said Albert with firmness.", "알베르가 단호하게 말했다."], ["“I cannot make you share the fate I have planned for myself.", "\"제가 스스로 정한 운명을 어머니께 나눠 지게 할 수는 없습니다."], ["I must live henceforth without rank and fortune, and to begin this hard apprenticeship I must borrow from a friend the loaf I shall eat until I have earned one.", "저는 이제 지위도 재산도 없이 살아가야 하고, 이 고된 수련을 시작하려면 제 입에 풀칠할 빵 한 덩이를 벌어 내기까지는 친구에게 빌려야 할 형편입니다."], ["So, my dear mother, I am going at once to ask Franz to lend me the small sum I shall require to supply my present wants.”", "그래서, 어머니, 지금 당장 프란츠에게 가서 당장 필요한 만큼의 적은 돈을 빌려 달라고 부탁할 참입니다.\""]], [["“You, my poor child, suffer poverty and hunger?", "\"내 가엾은 아들아, 네가 가난과 굶주림을 겪는다고?"], ["Oh, do not say so; it will break my resolutions.”", "오, 그런 말 마라. 내 결심이 무너질 것 같구나.\""]], [["“But not mine, mother,” replied Albert.", "\"하지만 제 결심은 무너지지 않습니다, 어머니.\""], ["“I am young and strong; I believe I am courageous, and since yesterday I have learned the power of will.", "알베르가 대답했다. \"저는 젊고 튼튼해요. 제게 용기가 있다고 믿고, 어제부터 의지의 힘을 배웠습니다."], ["Alas, my dear mother, some have suffered so much, and yet live, and have raised a new fortune on the ruin of all the promises of happiness which heaven had made them—on the fragments of all the hope which God had given them!", "아아, 어머니, 어떤 이들은 그토록 큰 고통을 겪고도 살아남아, 하늘이 약속했던 행복의 모든 언약이 무너진 폐허 위에, 신이 주셨던 모든 희망의 파편 위에 새로운 부를 쌓아 올렸습니다."], ["I have seen that, mother; I know that from the gulf in which their enemies have plunged them they have risen with so much vigor and glory that in their turn they have ruled their former conquerors, and have punished them.", "저는 그것을 보았습니다, 어머니. 적들이 그들을 빠뜨린 심연에서 그들이 어찌나 힘차고 영광스럽게 일어섰는지, 이번엔 도리어 자신들을 짓밟았던 자들을 다스리고 응징하기에 이르렀다는 것을 저는 압니다. 아니요, 어머니."], ["No, mother; from this moment I have done with the past, and accept nothing from it—not even a name, because you can understand that your son cannot bear the name of a man who ought to blush for it before another.”", "이 순간부터 저는 과거와 결별하고, 그로부터 아무것도 받지 않겠습니다. 이름조차 받지 않을 겁니다. 어머니도 아시다시피, 어떤 사람이 다른 이 앞에서 부끄러워 얼굴을 붉혀야 할 이름이라면, 그 아들이 그것을 짊어질 수는 없으니까요.\""]], [["“Albert, my child,”", "\"알베르, 내 아들아.\""], ["said Mercédès, “if I had a stronger heart, that is the counsel I would have given you; your conscience has spoken when my voice became too weak; listen to its dictates.", "메르세데스가 말했다. \"내게 좀 더 강한 마음이 있었더라면, 그것이 바로 내가 네게 해 주었을 충고다. 내 목소리가 너무 약해졌을 때 네 양심이 대신 말해 주었으니, 그 명령에 귀를 기울이려무나."], ["You had friends, Albert; break off their acquaintance.", "네게는 친구들이 있었지, 알베르. 그들과의 교제를 끊어라. 하지만 절망하지는 마라."], ["But do not despair; you have life before you, my dear Albert, for you are yet scarcely twenty-two years old; and as a pure heart like yours wants a spotless name, take my father’s—it was Herrera.", "너는 앞날이 창창하다, 내 사랑하는 알베르. 아직 스물둘밖에 안 되었으니까. 그리고 너처럼 순결한 마음에는 티 없는 이름이 어울리니, 내 아버지의 이름을 쓰려무나."], ["I am sure, my dear Albert, whatever may be your career, you will soon render that name illustrious.", "에레라였단다. 분명히 말하건대, 내 사랑하는 알베르, 네 앞길이 어떠하든 너는 머지않아 그 이름을 빛낼 게다."], ["Then, my son, return to the world still more brilliant because of your former sorrows; and if I am wrong, still let me cherish these hopes, for I have no future to look forward to.", "그러면, 내 아들아, 지난날의 슬픔이 있었기에 더욱 눈부신 모습으로 세상에 돌아오너라. 내 생각이 틀렸다 해도, 나는 이 희망만은 품고 싶구나. 내게는 바라볼 미래가 없으니 말이다."], ["For me the grave opens when I pass the threshold of this house.”", "이 집 문턱을 넘는 순간, 내게는 무덤이 열린단다.\""]], [["“I will fulfil all your wishes, my dear mother,”", "\"어머니의 소원을 모두 이루어 드리겠습니다, 어머니.\""], ["said the young man.", "청년이 말했다."], ["“Yes, I share your hopes; the anger of Heaven will not pursue us, since you are pure and I am innocent.", "\"네, 저도 어머니의 희망을 함께 품겠습니다. 어머니는 순결하고 저는 결백하니, 하늘의 노여움이 우리를 뒤쫓지는 않을 겁니다."], ["But, since our resolution is formed, let us act promptly. M.", "하지만 결심을 굳힌 이상, 어서 움직이도록 해요."], ["de Morcerf went out about half an hour ago; the opportunity is favorable to avoid an explanation.”", "모르세르 씨가 삼십 분쯤 전에 나가셨으니, 해명할 일 없이 빠져나가기에 좋은 기회입니다.\""]], [["“I am ready, my son,”", "\"준비됐다, 내 아들아.\""], ["said Mercédès.", "메르세데스가 말했다."]], [["Albert ran to fetch a carriage.", "알베르는 마차를 부르러 달려갔다."], ["He recollected that there was a small furnished house to let in the Rue des Saints-Pères, where his mother would find a humble but decent lodging, and thither he intended conducting the countess.", "그는 생페르 거리에 세놓는 작은 가구 딸린 집이 하나 있다는 것이 떠올랐다. 어머니가 누추하나마 그럭저럭 지낼 만한 거처를 찾을 수 있는 곳이었고, 그는 그곳으로 백작 부인을 모실 작정이었다."], ["As the carriage stopped at the door, and Albert was alighting, a man approached and gave him a letter.", "마차가 문 앞에 멈추고 알베르가 막 내리려는데, 한 사내가 다가와 그에게 편지 한 통을 건넸다."]], [["Albert recognized the bearer.", "알베르는 편지를 가져온 자를 알아보았다."], ["“From the count,”", "\"백작님께서 보내신 겁니다.\""], ["said Bertuccio.", "베르투치오가 말했다."], ["Albert took the letter, opened, and read it, then looked round for Bertuccio, but he was gone.", "알베르는 편지를 받아 뜯어 읽고는 베르투치오를 찾아 둘러보았으나, 그는 이미 사라지고 없었다."]], [["He returned to Mercédès with tears in his eyes and heaving breast, and without uttering a word he gave her the letter. Mercédès read:", "알베르는 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁하고 가슴이 들먹이는 채로 메르세데스에게 돌아가, 한마디도 하지 않고 편지를 건넸다. 메르세데스는 읽어 내려갔다."]], [["“Albert,—While showing you that I have discovered your plans, I hope also to convince you of my delicacy.", "\"알베르 군에게.―내가 자네의 계획을 알아차렸음을 보여 주면서, 동시에 나의 세심함도 자네에게 납득시킬 수 있기를 바라네."], ["You are free, you leave the count’s house, and you take your mother to your home; but reflect, Albert, you owe her more than your poor noble heart can pay her.", "자네는 자유로운 몸이니 백작의 집을 떠나, 어머니를 모시고 자네의 거처로 가겠지. 하지만 알베르, 생각해 보게. 자네는 그 가엾고 고결한 마음으로 갚을 수 있는 것보다 더 많은 것을 어머니께 빚지고 있네."], ["Keep the struggle for yourself, bear all the suffering, but spare her the trial of poverty which must accompany your first efforts; for she deserves not even the shadow of the misfortune which has this day fallen on her, and Providence is not willing that the innocent should suffer for the guilty.", "싸움은 자네 몫으로 남겨 두고 모든 고통을 떠안되, 어머니께만은 자네가 처음 발버둥 칠 때 따라올 수밖에 없는 그 가난의 시련을 면하게 해 드리게. 어머니는 오늘 그분께 닥친 불행의 그림자조차 받으실 까닭이 없는 분이고, 섭리는 죄 없는 이가 죄지은 자를 위해 고통받기를 바라지 않으니 말일세."], ["I know you are going to leave the Rue du Helder without taking anything with you.", "자네가 아무것도 지니지 않고 엘데 거리를 떠나려 한다는 것을 나는 알고 있네."], ["Do not seek to know how I discovered it; I know it—that is sufficient.", "어떻게 알았는지 알려고 하지 말게. 나는 안다네. 그것으로 충분하지."]], [["“Now, listen, Albert.", "자, 들어 보게, 알베르."], ["Twenty-four years ago I returned, proud and joyful, to my country.", "스물네 해 전, 나는 자랑스럽고 기쁜 마음으로 내 고향에 돌아왔네. 내게는 약혼녀가 있었지, 알베르."], ["I had a betrothed, Albert, a lovely girl whom I adored, and I was bringing to my betrothed a hundred and fifty louis, painfully amassed by ceaseless toil.", "내가 너무도 사랑하던 아리따운 처녀였네. 나는 약혼녀에게 줄 150루이를 가지고 갔지. 쉼 없는 노고로 어렵사리 모은 돈이었네."], ["This money was for her; I destined it for her, and, knowing the treachery of the sea I buried our treasure in the little garden of the house my father lived in at Marseilles, on the Allées de Meilhan.", "그 돈은 그녀를 위한 것이었어. 그녀에게 주려고 마련한 것이었지. 바다의 변덕을 잘 알았기에, 나는 그 보물을 마르세유 메이앙 거리에 있던, 내 아버지가 살던 집의 작은 정원에 묻어 두었네."], ["Your mother, Albert, knows that poor house well.", "알베르, 자네 어머니도 그 초라한 집을 잘 아실 걸세."], ["A short time since I passed through Marseilles, and went to see the old place, which revived so many painful recollections; and in the evening I took a spade and dug in the corner of the garden where I had concealed my treasure.", "얼마 전 나는 마르세유를 지나는 길에 그 옛집을 보러 갔다네. 수많은 고통스러운 기억을 되살리는 곳이었지. 그날 저녁 나는 삽을 들고 보물을 숨겨 두었던 정원 구석을 팠네."], ["The iron box was there—no one had touched it—under a beautiful fig-tree my father had planted the day I was born, which overshadowed the spot.", "쇠 상자는 그대로 거기 있었네. 아무도 손대지 않았더군. 내가 태어나던 날 아버지가 심으신 아름다운 무화과나무 아래에, 그 자리를 그늘로 덮은 그 나무 아래에 말일세."], ["Well, Albert, this money, which was formerly designed to promote the comfort and tranquillity of the woman I adored, may now, through strange and painful circumstances, be devoted to the same purpose.", "그러니, 알베르, 한때는 내가 사랑하던 여인의 안락과 평안을 위해 마련했던 이 돈이, 이제 기이하고 고통스러운 사정을 거쳐 같은 목적에 쓰일 수 있게 된 것이라네."]], [["“Oh, feel for me, who could offer millions to that poor woman, but who return her only the piece of black bread forgotten under my poor roof since the day I was torn from her I loved.", "오, 나를 헤아려 주게. 나는 그 가엾은 여인에게 수백만 금을 바칠 수도 있건만, 사랑하는 그녀에게서 떨어져 나오던 그날 이후 내 초라한 지붕 아래 잊혀 있던 검은 빵 한 조각밖에 돌려드릴 수 없는 사람일세. 자네는 너그러운 사람이네, 알베르."], ["You are a generous man, Albert, but perhaps you may be blinded by pride or resentment; if you refuse me, if you ask another for what I have a right to offer you, I will say it is ungenerous of you to refuse the life of your mother at the hands of a man whose father was allowed by your father to die in all the horrors of poverty and despair.”", "하지만 어쩌면 자존심이나 원한에 눈이 멀어 있을지도 모르지. 만일 자네가 나를 거절한다면, 내가 마땅히 자네에게 베풀 권리가 있는 것을 다른 이에게 청한다면, 나는 이렇게 말하겠네. 자네 아버지가 그 부친을 가난과 절망의 온갖 참상 속에 죽도록 내버려 둔 바로 그 사람의 손에서 자네 어머니의 목숨을 거절하는 것은 너그럽지 못한 처사라고 말일세.\""]], [["Albert stood pale and motionless to hear what his mother would decide after she had finished reading this letter.", "알베르는 어머니가 이 편지를 다 읽고 나서 어떤 결정을 내릴지 들으려고 창백하게 굳은 채 서 있었다."], ["Mercédès turned her eyes with an ineffable look towards heaven.", "메르세데스는 형언할 수 없는 눈빛으로 하늘을 향해 눈을 들었다."]], [["“I accept it,”", "\"받아들이겠다.\""], ["said she; “he has a right to pay the dowry, which I shall take with me to some convent!”", "어머니가 말했다. \"그분에게는 지참금을 치를 권리가 있어. 그걸 가지고 어느 수도원으로 들어가마!\""]], [["Putting the letter in her bosom, she took her son’s arm, and with a firmer step than she even herself expected she went downstairs.", "편지를 가슴에 품은 그녀는 아들의 팔을 잡고, 스스로도 예상치 못한 굳센 걸음으로 계단을 내려갔다."]], [["# Chapter 92. The Suicide", "# Chapter 92. The Suicide"]], [["Meanwhile Monte Cristo had also returned to town with Emmanuel and Maximilian.", "그동안 몬테크리스토 역시 에마뉘엘, 막시밀리앙과 함께 시내로 돌아왔다."], ["Their return was cheerful.", "돌아오는 길은 유쾌했다."], ["Emmanuel did not conceal his joy at the peaceful termination of the affair, and was loud in his expressions of delight.", "에마뉘엘은 일이 평화롭게 마무리된 데 대한 기쁨을 감추지 못하고 큰 소리로 즐거움을 늘어놓았다."], ["Morrel, in a corner of the carriage, allowed his brother-in-law’s gayety to expend itself in words, while he felt equal inward joy, which, however, betrayed itself only in his countenance.", "모렐은 마차 한쪽 구석에서 처남이 마음껏 떠들도록 내버려 두었다. 그 자신도 그에 못지않은 기쁨을 속으로 느끼고 있었지만, 그것은 오직 얼굴에만 드러날 뿐이었다."]], [["At the Barrière du Trône they met Bertuccio, who was waiting there, motionless as a sentinel at his post.", "바리에르 뒤 트론에 이르렀을 때 그들은 베르투초와 마주쳤다. 그는 마치 초소를 지키는 보초처럼 꼼짝도 않고 그곳에서 기다리고 있었다."], ["Monte Cristo put his head out of the window, exchanged a few words with him in a low tone, and the steward disappeared.", "몬테크리스토가 창밖으로 고개를 내밀고 그와 낮은 목소리로 몇 마디 주고받자, 집사는 사라졌다."]], [["“Count,”", "\"백작님,\""], ["said Emmanuel, when they were at the end of the Place Royale, “put me down at my door, that my wife may not have a single moment of needless anxiety on my account or yours.”", "플라스 루아얄 끝에 이르렀을 때 에마뉘엘이 말했다. \"저는 제 집 앞에 내려 주십시오. 제 아내가 저나 백작님 일로 단 한순간이라도 공연한 걱정을 하지 않도록 말입니다.\""]], [["“If it were not ridiculous to make a display of our triumph, said Morrel, I would invite the count to our house; besides that, he doubtless has some trembling heart to comfort.", "\"우리의 승리를 떠벌리는 것이 우스운 일만 아니라면,\" 모렐이 말했다. \"백작님을 저희 집으로 모시고 싶습니다만."], ["So we will take leave of our friend, and let him hasten home.”", "게다가 백작님께서는 분명 위로해 드려야 할 떨리는 가슴이 따로 있으시겠지요. 그러니 우리 친구분과는 작별하고, 어서 댁으로 가시게 해 드립시다.\""]], [["“Stop a moment,”", "\"잠깐만,\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “do not let me lose both my companions.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"내게서 두 길동무를 한꺼번에 빼앗지는 말게."], ["Return, Emmanuel, to your charming wife, and present my best compliments to her; and do you, Morrel, accompany me to the Champs-Élysées.”", "에마뉘엘, 자네는 그 사랑스러운 부인께 돌아가서 내 진심 어린 인사를 전해 주게. 그리고 모렐, 자네는 나와 함께 샹젤리제까지 가 주게.\""]], [["“Willingly,”", "\"기꺼이 그러지요,\""], ["said Maximilian; “particularly as I have business in that quarter.”", "막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"마침 그쪽에 볼일도 있으니까요.\""]], [["“Shall we wait breakfast for you?”", "\"아침 식사는 두 분을 기다릴까요?\""], ["asked Emmanuel.", "에마뉘엘이 물었다."]], [["“No,” replied the young man.", "\"아니,\" 젊은이가 대답했다."], ["The door was closed, and the carriage proceeded. “See what good fortune I brought you!”", "문이 닫히고, 마차가 다시 나아갔다. \"보십시오, 제가 백작님께 얼마나 큰 행운을 가져다드렸는지!\""], ["said Morrel, when he was alone with the count.", "백작과 단둘이 남게 되자 모렐이 말했다."], ["“Have you not thought so?”", "\"그렇게 생각하지 않으십니까?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래,\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “for that reason I wished to keep you near me.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그래서 자네를 곁에 두고 싶었던 걸세.\""]], [["“It is miraculous!” continued Morrel, answering his own thoughts.", "\"정말 기적 같은 일입니다!\" 모렐이 자기 생각을 좇으며 말을 이었다."]], [["“What?” said Monte Cristo.", "\"무엇이 말인가?\" 몬테크리스토가 물었다."]], [["“What has just happened.”", "\"방금 일어난 일 말입니다.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래,\""], ["said the Count, “you are right—it is miraculous.”", "백작이 말했다. \"자네 말이 맞아. 기적 같은 일이지.\""]], [["“For Albert is brave,”", "\"알베르는 용감한 사람이니까요.\""], ["resumed Morrel.", "모렐이 다시 말했다."]], [["“Very brave,”", "\"아주 용감하지,\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “I have seen him sleep with a sword suspended over his head.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"나는 그가 머리 위에 칼을 매달아 둔 채로 잠드는 것을 본 적이 있네.\""]], [["“And I know he has fought two duels,”", "\"게다가 그가 두 번이나 결투를 치른 것도 알고 있습니다.\""], ["said Morrel.", "모렐이 말했다."], ["“How can you reconcile that with his conduct this morning?”", "\"그런 사람이 오늘 아침에 한 행동을 대체 어떻게 받아들여야 합니까?\""]], [["“All owing to your influence,”", "\"모두가 자네의 영향력 덕분이지.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo, smiling.", "몬테크리스토가 미소 지으며 대답했다."]], [["“It is well for Albert he is not in the army,”", "\"알베르가 군인이 아닌 게 다행입니다.\""], ["said Morrel.", "모렐이 말했다."]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜?\""]], [["“An apology on the ground!”", "\"결투장에서 사과를 하다니요!\""], ["said the young captain, shaking his head.", "젊은 대위가 고개를 저으며 말했다."]], [["“Come,”", "\"이보게,\""], ["said the count mildly, “do not entertain the prejudices of ordinary men, Morrel!", "백작이 부드럽게 말했다. \"여느 사람들처럼 편견에 사로잡히지 말게, 모렐!"], ["Acknowledge, that if Albert is brave, he cannot be a coward; he must then have had some reason for acting as he did this morning, and confess that his conduct is more heroic than otherwise.”", "인정하게나. 알베르가 용감하다면 비겁한 자일 리는 없네. 그러니 오늘 아침 그렇게 행동한 데에는 분명 무슨 까닭이 있었을 테고, 그의 행동은 오히려 영웅적이라 해야 할 걸세.\""]], [["“Doubtless, doubtless,”", "\"물론입니다, 물론이지요.\""], ["said Morrel; “but I shall say, like the Spaniard, ‘He has not been so brave today as he was yesterday.’”", "모렐이 말했다. \"하지만 저는 스페인 사람들처럼 말하겠습니다. '그는 오늘 어제만큼 용감하지 못했다'고요.\""]], [["“You will breakfast with me, will you not, Morrel?”", "\"모렐, 나와 함께 아침을 들지 않겠나?\""], ["said the count, to turn the conversation.", "백작이 화제를 돌리려고 말했다."]], [["“No; I must leave you at ten o’clock.”", "\"아닙니다. 열 시에는 백작님 곁을 떠나야 합니다.\""]], [["“Your engagement was for breakfast, then?”", "\"그럼 약속이 아침 식사 자리였나?\""], ["said the count.", "백작이 물었다."]], [["Morrel smiled, and shook his head.", "모렐이 미소 지으며 고개를 저었다."]], [["“Still you must breakfast somewhere.”", "\"그래도 어디선가는 아침을 들어야 할 게 아닌가.\""]], [["“But if I am not hungry?” said the young man.", "\"하지만 배가 고프지 않다면요?\" 젊은이가 말했다."]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오,\""], ["said the count, “I only know two things which destroy the appetite,—grief—and as I am happy to see you very cheerful, it is not that—and love.", "백작이 말했다. \"식욕을 망치는 것은 내가 알기로 두 가지뿐일세. 하나는 슬픔인데, 자네가 이렇게 유쾌한 모습이라 다행히 그건 아닌 것 같고."], ["Now after what you told me this morning of your heart, I may believe——”", "다른 하나는 사랑이지. 오늘 아침 자네가 자네 마음에 대해 들려준 이야기를 떠올리면, 나는 이렇게 믿어도 될 듯한데……\""]], [["“Well, count,”", "\"그렇습니다, 백작님.\""], ["replied Morrel gayly, “I will not dispute it.”", "모렐이 명랑하게 대답했다. \"그 점은 부정하지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["“But you will not make me your confidant, Maximilian?”", "\"하지만 내게는 속내를 털어놓지 않을 셈인가, 막시밀리앙?\""], ["said the count, in a tone which showed how gladly he would have been admitted to the secret.", "백작이 그 비밀을 얼마나 기꺼이 듣고 싶어 하는지를 드러내는 어조로 말했다."]], [["“I showed you this morning that I had a heart, did I not, count?”", "\"오늘 아침에 제게도 마음이 있다는 걸 보여 드리지 않았습니까, 백작님?\""], ["Monte Cristo only answered by extending his hand to the young man.", "몬테크리스토는 대답 대신 젊은이에게 손을 내밀 뿐이었다. \"그렇다면,\""], ["“Well,” continued the latter, “since that heart is no longer with you in the Bois de Vincennes, it is elsewhere, and I must go and find it.”", "모렐이 말을 이었다. \"그 마음이 더는 백작님과 함께 뱅센 숲에 있지 않고 다른 곳에 가 있으니, 저는 그것을 찾으러 가야겠습니다.\""]], [["“Go,”", "\"가게,\" 백작이 천천히 말했다. \"가게, 친애하는 친구."], ["said the count deliberately; “go, dear friend, but promise me if you meet with any obstacle to remember that I have some power in this world, that I am happy to use that power in the behalf of those I love, and that I love you, Morrel.” 40260m", "다만 약속해 주게. 어떤 장애에 부딪히거든, 내가 이 세상에서 적잖은 힘을 가지고 있다는 것, 내가 사랑하는 이들을 위해 그 힘을 기꺼이 쓴다는 것, 그리고 내가 자네를 사랑한다는 것을 기억하겠다고 말일세, 모렐.\""]], [["“I will remember it,”", "\"기억하겠습니다,\""], ["said the young man, “as selfish children recollect their parents when they want their aid.", "젊은이가 말했다. \"이기적인 자식들이 도움이 필요할 때 부모를 떠올리듯이요."], ["When I need your assistance, and the moment arrives, I will come to you, count.”", "백작님의 도움이 필요한 순간이 오면, 그때 백작님을 찾아가겠습니다.\""]], [["“Well, I rely upon your promise.", "\"좋아, 자네 약속을 믿겠네."], ["Good-bye, then.”", "그럼 잘 가게.\""]], [["“Good-bye, till we meet again.”", "\"안녕히, 다시 만날 때까지요.\""]], [["They had arrived in the Champs-Élysées.", "두 사람은 샹젤리제에 다다랐다."], ["Monte Cristo opened the carriage-door, Morrel sprang out on the pavement, Bertuccio was waiting on the steps.", "몬테크리스토가 마차 문을 열자 모렐이 보도로 뛰어내렸고, 베르투초가 계단에서 기다리고 있었다."], ["Morrel disappeared down the Avenue de Marigny, and Monte Cristo hastened to join Bertuccio.", "모렐은 마리니 거리 아래로 사라졌고, 몬테크리스토는 서둘러 베르투초에게 다가갔다."]], [["“Well?” asked he.", "\"어떻게 됐나?\" 그가 물었다."]], [["“She is going to leave her house,”", "\"부인이 집을 떠나려 하고 있습니다.\""], ["said the steward.", "집사가 말했다."]], [["“And her son?”", "\"아들은?\""]], [["“Florentin, his valet, thinks he is going to do the same.”", "\"하인 플로랑탱의 말로는 도련님도 그러실 것 같다고 합니다.\""]], [["“Come this way.”", "\"이리로 오게.\""], ["Monte Cristo took Bertuccio into his study, wrote the letter we have seen, and gave it to the steward. “Go,”", "몬테크리스토는 베르투초를 서재로 데려가, 우리가 앞서 본 그 편지를 써서 집사에게 건넸다. \"가게,\""], ["said he quickly.", "그가 빠르게 말했다."], ["“But first, let Haydée be informed that I have returned.”", "\"하지만 먼저 하이데에게 내가 돌아왔다고 전해 주게.\""]], [["“Here I am,”", "\"여기 있어요.\""], ["said the young girl, who at the sound of the carriage had run downstairs and whose face was radiant with joy at seeing the count return safely. Bertuccio left.", "젊은 처녀가 말했다. 그녀는 마차 소리를 듣고 아래층으로 달려 내려온 것으로, 백작이 무사히 돌아온 것을 보고 얼굴이 기쁨으로 환하게 빛났다."], ["Every transport of a daughter finding a father, all the delight of a mistress seeing an adored lover, were felt by Haydée during the first moments of this meeting, which she had so eagerly expected.", "베르투초는 물러갔다. 그토록 애타게 기다려 온 이 만남의 첫 순간, 하이데는 아버지를 다시 찾은 딸의 온갖 환희와, 사랑하는 연인을 다시 본 여인의 온갖 기쁨을 한꺼번에 느꼈다."], ["Doubtless, although less evident, Monte Cristo’s joy was not less intense.", "비록 겉으로는 덜 드러났을지라도, 몬테크리스토의 기쁨 역시 그에 못지않게 강렬했으리라."], ["Joy to hearts which have suffered long is like the dew on the ground after a long drought; both the heart and the ground absorb that beneficent moisture falling on them, and nothing is outwardly apparent.", "오래 고통받은 가슴에게 기쁨이란 긴 가뭄 끝에 땅에 내리는 이슬과 같다. 가슴도 땅도 그 위에 떨어지는 은혜로운 물기를 빨아들일 뿐, 겉으로는 아무것도 드러나지 않는 법이다."]], [["Monte Cristo was beginning to think, what he had not for a long time dared to believe, that there were two Mercédès in the world, and he might yet be happy.", "몬테크리스토는, 오랫동안 감히 믿지 못했던 것을 이제 비로소 생각하기 시작했다. 세상에는 두 사람의 메르세데스가 있으며, 자기도 아직 행복할 수 있을지 모른다는 것을."], ["His eye, elate with happiness, was reading eagerly the tearful gaze of Haydée, when suddenly the door opened.", "행복으로 들뜬 그의 눈이 하이데의 눈물 어린 눈길을 열심히 읽고 있을 때, 별안간 문이 열렸다."], ["The count knit his brow.", "백작은 이맛살을 찌푸렸다."]], [["“M. de Morcerf!”", "\"모르세르 씨이십니다!\""], ["said Baptistin, as if that name sufficed for his excuse.", "바티스탱이 그 이름만으로 충분히 양해가 된다는 듯 말했다."], ["In fact, the count’s face brightened.", "과연 백작의 얼굴이 환해졌다."]], [["“Which,”", "\"누구 말인가,\""], ["asked he, “the viscount or the count?”", "그가 물었다. \"자작인가, 백작인가?\""]], [["“The count.”", "\"백작이십니다.\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오,\""], ["exclaimed Haydée, “is it not yet over?”", "하이데가 외쳤다. \"아직도 끝나지 않은 건가요?\""]], [["“I know not if it is finished, my beloved child,”", "\"끝났는지는 모르겠구나, 사랑하는 아가야.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, taking the young girl’s hands; “but I do know you have nothing more to fear.”", "몬테크리스토가 젊은 처녀의 두 손을 잡으며 말했다. \"하지만 네가 더는 두려워할 것이 없다는 것만은 분명히 알고 있단다.\""]], [["“But it is the wretched——”", "\"하지만 저 비열한 자가……\""]], [["“That man cannot injure me, Haydée,”", "\"저 사람은 나를 해칠 수 없어, 하이데.\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “it was his son alone that there was cause to fear.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"두려워할 만한 자는 그의 아들 하나뿐이었지.\""]], [["“And what I have suffered,”", "\"제가 얼마나 괴로웠는지는,\""], ["said the young girl, “you shall never know, my lord.”", "젊은 처녀가 말했다. \"당신은 결코 모르실 거예요, 나리.\""]], [["Monte Cristo smiled.", "몬테크리스토가 미소 지었다."], ["“By my father’s tomb,”", "\"내 아버지의 무덤에 걸고,\""], ["said he, extending his hand over the head of the young girl, “I swear to you, Haydée, that if any misfortune happens, it will not be to me.”", "그가 젊은 처녀의 머리 위로 손을 뻗으며 말했다. \"맹세하마, 하이데. 만일 어떤 불행이 닥친다 해도, 그것은 내게 닥치는 일이 아닐 것이다.\""]], [["“I believe you, my lord, as implicitly as if God had spoken to me,” said the young girl, presenting her forehead to him.", "\"나리의 말씀을, 마치 하느님이 제게 들려주신 말씀인 양 한 치의 의심 없이 믿어요.\" 젊은 처녀가 그에게 이마를 내밀며 말했다."], ["Monte Cristo pressed on that pure beautiful forehead a kiss which made two hearts throb at once, the one violently, the other secretly.", "몬테크리스토는 그 순결하고 아름다운 이마에 입을 맞추었고, 그 입맞춤은 두 사람의 가슴을 동시에 뛰게 했다. 하나는 격렬하게, 다른 하나는 은밀하게."]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오,\""], ["murmured the count, “shall I then be permitted to love again? Ask M.", "백작이 중얼거렸다. \"그렇다면 내게도 다시 사랑할 자격이 허락되는 것일까?"], ["de Morcerf into the drawing-room,”", "모르세르 씨를 응접실로 모시게.\""], ["said he to Baptistin, while he led the beautiful Greek girl to a private staircase.", "그가 바티스탱에게 말하고는, 아름다운 그리스 처녀를 데리고 안쪽 계단으로 향했다."]], [["We must explain this visit, which although expected by Monte Cristo, is unexpected to our readers.", "몬테크리스토에게는 예견된 일이었으나 우리 독자들에게는 뜻밖일 이 방문에 대해 설명을 해 두어야겠다."], ["While Mercédès, as we have said, was making a similar inventory of her property to Albert’s, while she was arranging her jewels, shutting her drawers, collecting her keys, to leave everything in perfect order, she did not perceive a pale and sinister face at a glass door which threw light into the passage, from which everything could be both seen and heard.", "앞서 말했듯이 메르세데스가 알베르처럼 자신의 재산 목록을 작성하고, 보석을 정리하고, 서랍을 닫고, 열쇠를 챙겨 모든 것을 완벽한 순서로 정돈해 두려는 동안, 그녀는 복도로 빛을 들이는 유리문에 어른거리는 창백하고 음산한 얼굴 하나를 알아채지 못했다. 그 문을 통해서는 모든 것을 보고 들을 수 있었다."], ["He who was thus looking, without being heard or seen, probably heard and saw all that passed in Madame de Morcerf’s apartments.", "들키지도 보이지도 않은 채 그렇게 들여다보고 있던 그 사내는, 아마도 모르세르 부인의 거처에서 일어나는 모든 일을 듣고 보았을 것이다."], ["From that glass door the pale-faced man went to the count’s bedroom and raised with a constricted hand the curtain of a window overlooking the courtyard.", "그 유리문에서 창백한 얼굴의 사내는 백작의 침실로 옮겨 가, 경련하는 손으로 안뜰이 내려다보이는 창의 커튼을 들어 올렸다."], ["He remained there ten minutes, motionless and dumb, listening to the beating of his own heart.", "그는 그곳에 십 분 동안 꼼짝도 않고 말도 없이 서서, 제 심장이 뛰는 소리에 귀를 기울였다."], ["For him those ten minutes were very long.", "그에게 그 십 분은 무척이나 길었다."], ["It was then Albert, returning from his meeting with the count, perceived his father watching for his arrival behind a curtain, and turned aside.", "바로 그때 백작과의 만남을 마치고 돌아오던 알베르가, 커튼 뒤에서 자신이 오기를 살피고 있는 아버지를 알아보고는 고개를 돌려 비켜 갔다."], ["The count’s eye expanded; he knew Albert had insulted the count dreadfully, and that in every country in the world such an insult would lead to a deadly duel.", "백작의 눈이 휘둥그레졌다. 알베르가 백작을 끔찍하게 모욕했다는 것, 그리고 세상 어느 나라에서든 그런 모욕은 목숨을 건 결투로 이어진다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다."], ["Albert returned safely—then the count was revenged.", "알베르가 무사히 돌아왔으니, 결국 백작은 복수를 한 셈이었다."]], [["An indescribable ray of joy illumined that wretched countenance like the last ray of the sun before it disappears behind the clouds which bear the aspect, not of a downy couch, but of a tomb.", "형언할 수 없는 기쁨의 빛이 그 비참한 얼굴을 환하게 비추었다. 마치 구름 뒤로 사라지기 직전 마지막으로 비치는 햇살 같았으나, 그 구름은 포근한 침상이 아니라 무덤의 형상을 띠고 있었다."], ["But as we have said, he waited in vain for his son to come to his apartment with the account of his triumph.", "하지만 앞서 말했듯, 그는 아들이 승리의 소식을 들고 자기 방으로 찾아오기를 헛되이 기다렸다."], ["He easily understood why his son did not come to see him before he went to avenge his father’s honor; but when that was done, why did not his son come and throw himself into his arms?", "아버지의 명예를 갚으러 가기 전에 아들이 자신을 보러 오지 않은 것은 쉽게 이해할 수 있었다. 그러나 그 일을 마친 뒤에도 어찌하여 아들은 와서 제 품에 안기지 않는단 말인가?"]], [["It was then, when the count could not see Albert, that he sent for his servant, who he knew was authorized not to conceal anything from him.", "바로 그때, 백작은 알베르를 볼 수 없게 되자 하인을 불렀다. 그 하인은 자신에게 아무것도 숨기지 못하도록 되어 있다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다."], ["Ten minutes afterwards, General Morcerf was seen on the steps in a black coat with a military collar, black pantaloons, and black gloves.", "십 분 뒤, 모르세르 장군이 군용 깃을 단 검은 외투에 검은 바지, 검은 장갑 차림으로 계단에 모습을 드러냈다."], ["He had apparently given previous orders, for as he reached the bottom step his carriage came from the coach-house ready for him.", "그는 미리 지시를 내려 둔 모양이었다. 그가 마지막 계단에 이르자 마차고에서 마차가 채비를 갖춘 채 나왔다."], ["The valet threw into the carriage his military cloak, in which two swords were wrapped, and, shutting the door, he took his seat by the side of the coachman.", "하인이 마차 안에 군용 망토를 던져 넣었는데, 그 안에는 검 두 자루가 싸여 있었다. 그러고는 문을 닫고 마부 옆자리에 올라탔다."], ["The coachman stooped down for his orders.", "마부가 분부를 들으려 몸을 숙였다."]], [["“To the Champs-Élysées,”", "\"샹젤리제로 가게.\""], ["said the general; “the Count of Monte Cristo’s. Hurry!”", "장군이 말했다. \"몬테크리스토 백작 댁으로. 서둘러!\""]], [["The horses bounded beneath the whip; and in five minutes they stopped before the count’s door. M.", "말들이 채찍 아래 날뛰며 내달렸고, 오 분 만에 백작의 집 앞에 멈춰 섰다."], ["de Morcerf opened the door himself, and as the carriage rolled away he passed up the walk, rang, and entered the open door with his servant.", "모르세르 씨가 직접 문을 열었고, 마차가 멀어져 가는 동안 그는 진입로를 따라 올라가 초인종을 울리고, 하인과 함께 열린 문으로 들어섰다."]], [["A moment afterwards, Baptistin announced the Count of Morcerf to Monte Cristo, and the latter, leading Haydée aside, ordered that Morcerf be asked into the drawing-room.", "잠시 후, 바티스탱이 몬테크리스토에게 모르세르 백작이 왔음을 알렸고, 백작은 하이데를 곁에서 물러나게 하고는 모르세르를 응접실로 모시라 일렀다."], ["The general was pacing the room the third time when, in turning, he perceived Monte Cristo at the door.", "장군은 세 번째로 방 안을 거닐다가 몸을 돌리는 순간, 문가에 선 몬테크리스토를 알아보았다."]], [["“Ah, it is M. de Morcerf,”", "\"아, 모르세르 씨로군요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo quietly; “I thought I had not heard aright.”", "몬테크리스토가 차분하게 말했다. \"내가 잘못 들었나 했습니다.\""]], [["“Yes, it is I,”", "\"그렇소, 나요.\""], ["said the count, whom a frightful contraction of the lips prevented from articulating freely.", "백작이 말했다. 입술이 무섭게 뒤틀려 말을 제대로 잇지 못했다."]], [["“May I know the cause which procures me the pleasure of seeing M. de Morcerf so early?”", "\"이 이른 시각에 모르세르 씨를 뵙는 기쁨을 어떤 연유로 누리게 되었는지 알 수 있겠습니까?\""]], [["“Had you not a meeting with my son this morning?”", "\"오늘 아침 내 아들과 만나지 않았소?\""], ["asked the general.", "장군이 물었다."]], [["“I had,” replied the count.", "\"만났습니다.\" 백작이 대답했다."]], [["“And I know my son had good reasons to wish to fight with you, and to endeavor to kill you.”", "\"그리고 내 아들이 당신과 싸워 당신을 죽이려 한 데에는 충분한 이유가 있었다는 것도 알고 있소.\""]], [["“Yes, sir, he had very good ones; but you see that in spite of them he has not killed me, and did not even fight.”", "\"그렇습니다, 장군. 아드님에게는 아주 충분한 이유가 있었지요. 하지만 보시다시피, 그런 이유에도 불구하고 그는 나를 죽이지 못했고, 싸우려 들지조차 않았습니다.\""]], [["“Yet he considered you the cause of his father’s dishonor, the cause of the fearful ruin which has fallen on my house.”", "\"하지만 내 아들은 당신을 제 아버지의 불명예의 원인으로, 내 집안에 닥친 그 끔찍한 파멸의 원인으로 여겼소.\""]], [["“It is true, sir,”", "\"사실입니다, 장군.\""], ["said Monte Cristo with his dreadful calmness; “a secondary cause, but not the principal.”", "몬테크리스토가 그 소름 끼치는 평정을 잃지 않고 말했다. \"부차적인 원인이긴 합니다만, 근본적인 원인은 아니지요.\""]], [["“Doubtless you made, then, some apology or explanation?”", "\"그렇다면 당신이 무슨 사과나 해명이라도 한 모양이군.\""]], [["“I explained nothing, and it is he who apologized to me.”", "\"나는 아무 해명도 하지 않았습니다. 오히려 내게 사과한 쪽은 그였습니다.\""]], [["“But to what do you attribute this conduct?”", "\"그런데 그런 행동을 무엇 탓으로 돌리겠소?\""]], [["“To the conviction, probably, that there was one more guilty than I.”", "\"아마도 나보다 더 죄 많은 자가 있다는 확신 때문이겠지요.\""]], [["“And who was that?”", "\"그게 누구란 말이오?\""]], [["“His father.”", "\"그의 아버지지요.\""]], [["“That may be,”", "\"그럴지도 모르지.\""], ["said the count, turning pale; “but you know the guilty do not like to find themselves convicted.”", "백작이 창백해지며 말했다. \"하지만 당신도 알 거요. 죄 있는 자는 자기 죄가 드러나는 걸 좋아하지 않는 법이라는 걸.\""]], [["“I know it, and I expected this result.”", "\"알고 있습니다. 그래서 이런 결과를 예상하고 있었지요.\""]], [["“You expected my son would be a coward?”", "\"내 아들이 비겁자가 되리라 예상했단 말이오?\""], ["cried the count.", "백작이 외쳤다."]], [["“M. Albert de Morcerf is no coward!”", "\"알베르 드 모르세르 씨는 비겁자가 아닙니다!\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“A man who holds a sword in his hand, and sees a mortal enemy within reach of that sword, and does not fight, is a coward! Why is he not here that I may tell him so?”", "\"손에 칼을 쥐고, 그 칼이 닿을 거리에 철천지원수를 두고도 싸우지 않는 자는 비겁자요! 그자가 어디 있소, 내가 직접 그 말을 해 줄 수 있게?\""]], [["“Sir,”", "\"장군,\""], ["replied Monte Cristo coldly, “I did not expect that you had come here to relate to me your little family affairs.", "몬테크리스토가 차갑게 대답했다. \"나는 당신이 이곳에 와서 자질구레한 집안일을 늘어놓으리라고는 예상치 못했습니다."], ["Go and tell M. Albert that, and he may know what to answer you.”", "가서 알베르 씨에게 그 말을 하시지요. 그가 어떻게 대답해야 할지는 알 겁니다.\""]], [["“Oh, no, no,”", "\"오, 아니, 아니오.\""], ["said the general, smiling faintly, “I did not come for that purpose; you are right.", "장군이 희미하게 웃으며 말했다. \"그러려고 온 게 아니오. 당신 말이 맞소."], ["I came to tell you that I also look upon you as my enemy.", "나는 당신에게, 나 또한 당신을 적으로 여긴다는 말을 하러 왔소."], ["I came to tell you that I hate you instinctively; that it seems as if I had always known you, and always hated you; and, in short, since the young people of the present day will not fight, it remains for us to do so.", "본능적으로 당신을 증오한다는 말을 하러 왔단 말이오. 마치 내가 늘 당신을 알고 있었던 것처럼, 늘 당신을 증오해 온 것처럼 말이오. 요컨대, 요즘 젊은 것들이 싸우려 들지 않으니, 우리가 직접 싸우는 수밖에 남지 않은 거요."], ["Do you think so, sir?”", "그렇게 생각지 않소?\""]], [["“Certainly.", "\"물론입니다."], ["And when I told you I had foreseen the result, it is the honor of your visit I alluded to.”", "그리고 내가 이런 결과를 예견했다고 말했을 때, 바로 당신의 이 방문을 두고 한 말이었지요.\""]], [["“So much the better.", "\"잘됐군. 준비됐소?\""], ["Are you prepared?”", "\"그렇습니다, 장군.\""]], [["“Yes, sir.” “You know that we shall fight till one of us is dead,”", "\"우리 둘 중 하나가 죽을 때까지 싸우는 거요.\""], ["said the general, whose teeth were clenched with rage.", "분노로 이를 악문 장군이 말했다."]], [["40262m\n“Until one of us dies,” repeated Monte Cristo, moving his head slightly up and down.", "\"우리 둘 중 하나가 죽을 때까지.\" 몬테크리스토가 고개를 살짝 끄덕이며 되뇌었다."]], [["“Let us start, then; we need no witnesses.”", "\"그럼 갑시다. 증인은 필요 없소.\""]], [["“Very true,”", "\"옳은 말씀입니다.\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “it is unnecessary, we know each other so well!”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"필요 없지요. 우리는 서로를 너무도 잘 아니까요!\""]], [["“On the contrary,”", "\"천만에.\""], ["said the count, “we know so little of each other.”", "백작이 말했다. \"우리는 서로를 거의 모르오.\""]], [["“Indeed?” said Monte Cristo, with the same indomitable coolness; “let us see.", "\"그렇습니까?\" 몬테크리스토가 변함없이 굴복을 모르는 냉정함으로 말했다."], ["Are you not the soldier Fernand who deserted on the eve of the battle of Waterloo?", "\"어디 봅시다. 당신은 워털루 전투 전날 밤 탈영한 병사 페르낭이 아니오?"], ["Are you not the Lieutenant Fernand who served as guide and spy to the French army in Spain?", "스페인에서 프랑스군의 길잡이와 첩자 노릇을 한 페르낭 중위가 아니오?"], ["Are you not the Captain Fernand who betrayed, sold, and murdered his benefactor, Ali?", "자신의 은인 알리를 배신하고, 팔아넘기고, 살해한 페르낭 대위가 아니오?"], ["And have not all these Fernands, united, made Lieutenant-General, the Count of Morcerf, peer of France?”", "그리고 그 모든 페르낭들이 하나로 뭉쳐, 프랑스 중장이자 모르세르 백작, 프랑스 상원의원이 된 것 아니오?\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오,\""], ["cried the general, as if branded with a hot iron, “wretch,—to reproach me with my shame when about, perhaps, to kill me!", "장군이 마치 시뻘건 인두로 낙인이 찍힌 것처럼 외쳤다. \"비열한 놈, 나를 죽이려는 마당에 내 수치를 들먹이다니!"], ["No, I did not say I was a stranger to you.", "아니, 나는 당신이 내게 낯선 자라고 말한 적 없소."], ["I know well, demon, that you have penetrated into the darkness of the past, and that you have read, by the light of what torch I know not, every page of my life; but perhaps I may be more honorable in my shame than you under your pompous coverings.", "잘 알고 있어, 악마 같은 놈. 네놈이 과거의 어둠 속을 파고들어, 무슨 횃불의 빛으로인지는 모르나, 내 인생의 모든 페이지를 읽어 냈다는 것을. 하지만 어쩌면 나는 내 수치 속에서도, 네놈이 그 거창한 껍데기를 뒤집어쓴 것보다 더 떳떳할지도 모르지."], ["No—no, I am aware you know me; but I know you only as an adventurer sewn up in gold and jewellery.", "아니, 아니, 네놈이 나를 안다는 건 인정한다. 하지만 나는 네놈을, 금과 보석을 휘감은 협잡꾼으로밖에 알지 못해."], ["You call yourself, in Paris, the Count of Monte Cristo; in Italy, Sinbad the Sailor; in Malta, I forget what.", "파리에서는 몬테크리스토 백작, 이탈리아에서는 뱃사람 신드바드, 몰타에서는 또 무슨 이름이었는지 잊었다만."], ["But it is your real name I want to know, in the midst of your hundred names, that I may pronounce it when we meet to fight, at the moment when I plunge my sword through your heart.”", "내가 알고 싶은 건 네놈의 백 가지 이름 가운데 진짜 이름이다. 우리가 싸우러 마주섰을 때, 내 칼을 네놈의 심장에 꽂는 그 순간에 그 이름을 부를 수 있도록 말이야.\""]], [["The Count of Monte Cristo turned dreadfully pale; his eye seemed to burn with a devouring fire.", "몬테크리스토 백작은 무섭게 창백해졌다. 그의 눈은 모든 것을 집어삼킬 듯한 불길로 타오르는 듯했다."], ["He leaped towards a dressing-room near his bedroom, and in less than a moment, tearing off his cravat, his coat and waistcoat, he put on a sailor’s jacket and hat, from beneath which rolled his long black hair.", "그는 침실 옆 화장실로 뛰어들어, 눈 깜짝할 사이에 넥타이와 상의와 조끼를 벗어 던지고는 뱃사람의 웃옷과 모자를 걸쳤다. 그 모자 아래로 긴 검은 머리카락이 흘러내렸다."], ["He returned thus, formidable and implacable, advancing with his arms crossed on his breast, towards the general, who could not understand why he had disappeared, but who on seeing him again, and feeling his teeth chatter and his legs sink under him, drew back, and only stopped when he found a table to support his clenched hand.", "그렇게 무시무시하고 가차 없는 모습으로 그는 가슴 위에 팔짱을 낀 채 장군을 향해 다가갔다. 장군은 그가 왜 사라졌었는지 영문을 알 수 없었으나, 다시 나타난 그를 보는 순간 이가 딱딱 부딪치고 다리가 풀려 뒷걸음질 쳤다. 그러다 꽉 쥔 손을 받칠 탁자를 찾고서야 비로소 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“Fernand,”", "\"페르낭,\""], ["cried he, “of my hundred names I need only tell you one, to overwhelm you!", "그가 외쳤다. \"내 백 가지 이름 가운데 단 하나만 일러 주면 너를 무너뜨리기에 충분하지!"], ["But you guess it now, do you not?—or, rather, you remember it?", "하지만 이젠 짐작이 가지 않나? 아니, 차라리 기억이 나는 게지?"], ["For, notwithstanding all my sorrows and my tortures, I show you today a face which the happiness of revenge makes young again—a face you must often have seen in your dreams since your marriage with Mercédès, my betrothed!” 40268m", "온갖 슬픔과 고문에도 불구하고, 나는 오늘 복수의 기쁨으로 다시 젊어진 얼굴을 네게 보이고 있으니까. 네가 내 약혼녀 메르세데스와 혼인한 뒤로 꿈속에서 수없이 보았을 그 얼굴 말이다!\""]], [["The general, with his head thrown back, hands extended, gaze fixed, looked silently at this dreadful apparition; then seeking the wall to support him, he glided along close to it until he reached the door, through which he went out backwards, uttering this single mournful, lamentable, distressing cry:", "장군은 머리를 뒤로 젖히고 두 손을 뻗은 채 시선을 못 박고서, 이 끔찍한 환영을 말없이 바라보았다. 그러더니 몸을 지탱할 벽을 더듬어, 그 벽에 바짝 붙어 미끄러지듯 문가에 이르렀고, 단 한마디 구슬프고 비통하고 애끓는 비명을 토하며 뒷걸음으로 문을 빠져나갔다."]], [["“Edmond Dantès!”", "\"에드몽 당테스!\""]], [["Then, with sighs which were unlike any human sound, he dragged himself to the door, reeled across the courtyard, and falling into the arms of his valet, he said in a voice scarcely intelligible,—“Home, home.”", "그러고는 어떤 인간의 소리와도 같지 않은 신음을 흘리며 문까지 몸을 끌고 가, 안뜰을 비틀비틀 가로지르다 하인의 품에 쓰러지며 거의 알아들을 수 없는 목소리로 말했다. \"집으로, 집으로.\""]], [["The fresh air and the shame he felt at having exposed himself before his servants, partly recalled his senses, but the ride was short, and as he drew near his house all his wretchedness revived.", "신선한 바깥 공기와, 하인들 앞에서 추한 꼴을 드러냈다는 수치심이 그의 정신을 어느 정도 되돌려 놓았다. 그러나 길은 짧았고, 집에 가까워질수록 그의 온갖 비참함이 되살아났다."], ["He stopped at a short distance from the house and alighted.", "그는 집에서 조금 떨어진 곳에 마차를 세우고 내렸다."], ["The door was wide open, a hackney-coach was standing in the middle of the yard—a strange sight before so noble a mansion; the count looked at it with terror, but without daring to inquire its meaning, he rushed towards his apartment.", "대문은 활짝 열려 있었고, 안뜰 한가운데에 삯마차 한 대가 서 있었다. 그토록 고귀한 저택 앞에는 어울리지 않는 낯선 광경이었다. 백작은 두려움에 찬 눈으로 그것을 바라보았으나, 감히 그 까닭을 물어볼 엄두도 내지 못한 채 자신의 거처로 달려갔다."]], [["Two persons were coming down the stairs; he had only time to creep into an alcove to avoid them.", "두 사람이 계단을 내려오고 있었다. 그는 가까스로 벽감 안으로 몸을 숨겨 그들을 피했다."], ["It was Mercédès leaning on her son’s arm and leaving the house.", "메르세데스가 아들의 팔에 기대어 집을 떠나는 길이었다."], ["They passed close by the unhappy being, who, concealed behind the damask curtain, almost felt Mercédès dress brush past him, and his son’s warm breath, pronouncing these words:", "그들은 다마스크 커튼 뒤에 숨은 그 가련한 사내의 바로 곁을 스쳐 지났다. 그는 메르세데스의 옷자락이 자신을 스치는 것을, 그리고 다음과 같은 말을 내뱉는 아들의 따뜻한 숨결을 느낄 듯했다."]], [["“Courage, mother! Come, this is no longer our home!”", "\"용기를 내세요, 어머니! 가요, 이젠 우리 집이 아니에요!\""]], [["The words died away, the steps were lost in the distance.", "그 말은 점점 잦아들었고, 발소리는 멀리서 사라졌다."], ["The general drew himself up, clinging to the curtain; he uttered the most dreadful sob which ever escaped from the bosom of a father abandoned at the same time by his wife and son.", "장군은 커튼에 매달린 채 몸을 일으켰다. 그는 아내와 아들에게 동시에 버림받은 한 아버지의 가슴에서 터져 나올 수 있는 가장 끔찍한 흐느낌을 토해 냈다."], ["He soon heard the clatter of the iron step of the hackney-coach, then the coachman’s voice, and then the rolling of the heavy vehicle shook the windows.", "이윽고 그는 삯마차의 쇠 발판이 덜컹거리는 소리를, 뒤이어 마부의 목소리를, 그리고 그 묵직한 마차가 굴러가며 유리창을 뒤흔드는 소리를 들었다."], ["He darted to his bedroom to see once more all he had loved in the world; but the hackney-coach drove on and the head of neither Mercédès nor her son appeared at the window to take a last look at the house or the deserted father and husband.", "그는 이 세상에서 사랑했던 모든 것을 다시 한번 보려고 침실로 달려갔다. 그러나 삯마차는 이미 굴러가고 있었고, 메르세데스도 아들도 이 집을, 또 버림받은 아버지이자 남편을 마지막으로 보려고 창밖으로 고개를 내미는 일은 없었다."]], [["And at the very moment when the wheels of that coach crossed the gateway a report was heard, and a thick smoke escaped through one of the panes of the window, which was broken by the explosion.", "그리고 바로 그 마차의 바퀴가 대문을 지나는 순간, 총성이 울렸고, 폭발로 깨진 창유리 한 장 사이로 짙은 연기가 새어 나왔다. 93장 발랑틴"]], [["# Chapter 93. Valentine", "# Chapter 93. Valentine"]], [["40270m\nWe may easily conceive where Morrel’s appointment was.", "모렐이 어디로 약속을 잡았는지는 쉽게 짐작할 수 있다."], ["On leaving Monte Cristo he walked slowly towards Villefort’s; we say slowly, for Morrel had more than half an hour to spare to go five hundred steps, but he had hastened to take leave of Monte Cristo because he wished to be alone with his thoughts.", "몬테크리스토를 떠난 그는 천천히 빌포르의 집을 향해 걸었다. 천천히라고 말한 것은, 오백 걸음을 가는 데 모렐에게 삼십 분 넘는 여유가 있었기 때문이다. 그래도 그는 서둘러 몬테크리스토에게 작별을 고했으니, 혼자서 생각에 잠기고 싶었던 것이다."], ["He knew his time well—the hour when Valentine was giving Noirtier his breakfast, and was sure not to be disturbed in the performance of this pious duty.", "그는 자신의 시간을 잘 알고 있었다. 발랑틴이 누아르티에에게 아침 식사를 차려 드리는 시각이었고, 그 경건한 의무를 다하는 동안에는 방해받지 않으리라는 것이 분명했다."], ["Noirtier and Valentine had given him leave to go twice a week, and he was now availing himself of that permission.", "누아르티에와 발랑틴은 그에게 일주일에 두 번 찾아오는 것을 허락해 주었고, 그는 지금 그 허락을 누리고 있는 참이었다."]], [["He arrived; Valentine was expecting him.", "그가 도착했다. 발랑틴이 그를 기다리고 있었다."], ["Uneasy and almost crazed, she seized his hand and led him to her grandfather.", "불안하고 거의 정신이 나간 듯한 그녀가 그의 손을 붙잡고 할아버지에게로 이끌었다."], ["This uneasiness, amounting almost to frenzy, arose from the report Morcerf’s adventure had made in the world, for the affair at the Opera was generally known.", "광기에 가까운 이 불안은 모르세르의 사건이 세상에 일으킨 소문에서 비롯된 것이었으니, 오페라 극장에서의 일은 널리 알려져 있었다."], ["No one at Villefort’s doubted that a duel would ensue from it.", "빌포르의 집에서는 그 일로 결투가 벌어지리라는 것을 누구도 의심치 않았다."], ["Valentine, with her woman’s instinct, guessed that Morrel would be Monte Cristo’s second, and from the young man’s well-known courage and his great affection for the count, she feared that he would not content himself with the passive part assigned to him.", "발랑틴은 여자의 직감으로 모렐이 몬테크리스토의 입회인이 되리라 짐작했고, 그 젊은이의 익히 알려진 용기와 백작을 향한 깊은 애정으로 미루어, 그가 자신에게 주어진 소극적인 역할에 만족하지 않으리라 두려워했다."], ["We may easily understand how eagerly the particulars were asked for, given, and received; and Morrel could read an indescribable joy in the eyes of his beloved, when she knew that the termination of this affair was as happy as it was unexpected.", "그러니 자세한 사정을 얼마나 애타게 묻고, 또 그것을 얼마나 듣고 받아들였을지는 쉽게 짐작할 수 있다. 그 일이 뜻밖에도 행복하게 마무리되었다는 것을 알았을 때, 모렐은 사랑하는 여인의 눈 속에서 형언할 수 없는 기쁨을 읽을 수 있었다."]], [["“Now,”", "\"자,\""], ["said Valentine, motioning to Morrel to sit down near her grandfather, while she took her seat on his footstool,—“now let us talk about our own affairs.", "발랑틴이 모렐에게 할아버지 곁에 앉으라고 손짓하며 말했다. 그녀 자신은 할아버지의 발 받침대에 자리를 잡았다. \"자, 이제 우리 일을 이야기해 봐요."], ["You know, Maximilian, grandpapa once thought of leaving this house, and taking an apartment away from M. de Villefort’s.”", "막시밀리앙, 할아버지께서 한때 이 집을 떠나 빌포르 씨의 집에서 멀리 떨어진 거처를 얻을 생각을 하셨던 거 알죠.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네,\""], ["said Maximilian, “I recollect the project, of which I highly approved.”", "막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"그 계획을 기억합니다. 저도 크게 찬성했지요.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"음,\""], ["said Valentine, “you may approve again, for grandpapa is again thinking of it.”", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"다시 찬성해 주셔도 되겠어요. 할아버지께서 또 그 생각을 하고 계시거든요.\""]], [["“Bravo,”", "\"잘됐군요.\""], ["said Maximilian. 40272m", "막시밀리앙이 말했다."]], [["“And do you know,”", "\"그런데,\""], ["said Valentine, “what reason grandpapa gives for leaving this house.”", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"할아버지께서 이 집을 떠나려 하시는 이유가 뭔지 아세요?\""], ["Noirtier looked at Valentine to impose silence, but she did not notice him; her looks, her eyes, her smile, were all for Morrel.", "누아르티에가 침묵을 명하려고 발랑틴을 바라보았지만, 그녀는 알아차리지 못했다. 그녀의 표정도, 눈도, 미소도 온통 모렐을 향해 있었다."]], [["“Oh, whatever may be M. Noirtier’s reason,”", "\"아, 누아르티에 씨의 이유가 무엇이든,\""], ["answered Morrel, “I can readily believe it to be a good one.”", "모렐이 대답했다. \"저는 그것이 분명 타당한 이유라고 기꺼이 믿겠습니다.\""]], [["“An excellent one,”", "\"아주 훌륭한 이유예요.\""], ["said Valentine.", "발랑틴이 말했다."], ["“He pretends the air of the Faubourg Saint-Honoré is not good for me.”", "\"포부르생토노레의 공기가 제게 좋지 않다고 하시거든요.\""]], [["“Indeed?” said Morrel; “in that M.", "\"정말입니까?\" 모렐이 말했다."], ["Noirtier may be right; you have not seemed to be well for the last fortnight.”", "\"그 점에서는 누아르티에 씨가 옳으실지도 모르겠군요. 지난 보름 동안 당신은 건강이 좋아 보이지 않았으니까요.\""]], [["“Not very,” said Valentine.", "\"그리 좋지 않아요.\" 발랑틴이 말했다."], ["“And grandpapa has become my physician, and I have the greatest confidence in him, because he knows everything.”", "\"그래서 할아버지께서 제 주치의가 되셨답니다. 저는 할아버지를 더없이 믿어요. 모르시는 게 없으시니까요.\""]], [["“Do you then really suffer?”", "\"그럼 정말로 어디가 아픈 겁니까?\""], ["asked Morrel quickly.", "모렐이 다급히 물었다."]], [["“Oh, it must not be called suffering; I feel a general uneasiness, that is all.", "\"아, 아프다고까지 할 건 아니에요. 그냥 온몸이 어쩐지 불편할 뿐이에요."], ["I have lost my appetite, and my stomach feels as if it were struggling to get accustomed to something.” Noirtier did not lose a word of what Valentine said.", "입맛도 없고, 위가 무언가에 적응하려고 애쓰는 것 같은 느낌이 들어요.\" 누아르티에는 발랑틴이 하는 말을 한마디도 놓치지 않았다."]], [["“And what treatment do you adopt for this singular complaint?”", "\"그럼 그 묘한 증세에 어떤 치료를 받고 있습니까?\""]], [["“A very simple one,”", "\"아주 간단한 거예요.\""], ["said Valentine.", "발랑틴이 말했다."], ["“I swallow every morning a spoonful of the mixture prepared for my grandfather.", "\"매일 아침 할아버지께 드리려고 만든 물약을 한 숟가락씩 삼키고 있어요."], ["When I say one spoonful, I began by one—now I take four.", "한 숟가락이라고 했지만, 처음엔 한 숟가락이었는데 지금은 네 숟가락이에요."], ["Grandpapa says it is a panacea.”", "할아버지께서는 그게 만병통치약이라고 하세요.\""], ["Valentine smiled, but it was evident that she suffered.", "발랑틴이 미소 지었지만, 그녀가 고통스러워하는 것은 분명했다."]], [["Maximilian, in his devotedness, gazed silently at her.", "막시밀리앙은 헌신적인 마음으로 말없이 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["She was very beautiful, but her usual pallor had increased; her eyes were more brilliant than ever, and her hands, which were generally white like mother-of-pearl, now more resembled wax, to which time was adding a yellowish hue.", "그녀는 무척 아름다웠지만, 평소의 창백함이 더 짙어져 있었다. 두 눈은 그 어느 때보다 빛났고, 평소에는 진주조개처럼 희던 두 손이 이제는 밀랍에 더 가까웠으니, 거기에 세월이 누르스름한 빛을 더하고 있었다."]], [["From Valentine the young man looked towards Noirtier.", "젊은이는 발랑틴에게서 시선을 돌려 누아르티에를 바라보았다."], ["The latter watched with strange and deep interest the young girl, absorbed by her affection, and he also, like Morrel, followed those traces of inward suffering which was so little perceptible to a common observer that they escaped the notice of everyone but the grandfather and the lover.", "노인은 애정에 사로잡힌 손녀를 기이하고도 깊은 관심으로 지켜보고 있었다. 그 역시 모렐처럼, 보통 사람의 눈에는 거의 드러나지 않아 할아버지와 연인 말고는 아무도 알아채지 못하는 그 내면의 고통의 흔적을 좇고 있었다."]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["said Morrel, “I thought this mixture, of which you now take four spoonfuls, was prepared for M. Noirtier?”", "모렐이 말했다. \"당신이 지금 네 숟가락씩 먹는다는 그 물약은 누아르티에 씨를 위해 만든 것이 아니었던가요?\""]], [["“I know it is very bitter,”", "\"무척 쓰다는 건 알아요.\""], ["said Valentine; “so bitter, that all I drink afterwards appears to have the same taste.”", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"어찌나 쓴지, 그 뒤에 마시는 것은 죄다 똑같은 맛이 나는 것 같아요.\""], ["Noirtier looked inquiringly at his granddaughter.", "누아르티에가 캐묻는 눈빛으로 손녀를 바라보았다."], ["“Yes, grandpapa,”", "\"네, 할아버지.\""], ["said Valentine; “it is so.", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"그래요."], ["Just now, before I came down to you, I drank a glass of sugared water; I left half, because it seemed so bitter.”", "방금도, 할아버지께 내려오기 전에 설탕물을 한 잔 마셨는데, 너무 써서 절반을 남겼어요.\""], ["Noirtier turned pale, and made a sign that he wished to speak.", "누아르티에의 얼굴이 창백해지더니, 말을 하고 싶다는 신호를 보냈다."]], [["Valentine rose to fetch the dictionary.", "발랑틴이 사전을 가지러 일어섰다."], ["Noirtier watched her with evident anguish. In fact, the blood was rushing to the young girl’s head already, her cheeks were becoming red.", "누아르티에가 역력한 고통을 띠고 그녀를 지켜보았다. 사실 벌써 피가 처녀의 머리로 솟구쳐, 두 뺨이 붉어지고 있었다."]], [["“Oh,” cried she, without losing any of her cheerfulness, “this is singular!", "\"어머,\" 그녀가 명랑함을 조금도 잃지 않은 채 외쳤다."], ["I can’t see!", "\"이상해라!"], ["Did the sun shine in my eyes?”", "앞이 안 보여요! 햇빛이 눈에 들어왔나?\""], ["And she leaned against the window.", "그러고는 창문에 몸을 기댔다."]], [["“The sun is not shining,”", "\"햇빛은 비치지 않습니다.\""], ["said Morrel, more alarmed by Noirtier’s expression than by Valentine’s indisposition.", "모렐이 말했다. 그는 발랑틴의 몸 상태보다 누아르티에의 표정에 더 놀라 있었다."], ["He ran towards her.", "그가 그녀에게 달려갔다."], ["The young girl smiled.", "처녀가 미소 지었다."]], [["“Cheer up,” said she to Noirtier.", "\"기운 내세요.\" 그녀가 누아르티에에게 말했다."], ["“Do not be alarmed, Maximilian; it is nothing, and has already passed away.", "\"놀라지 마세요, 막시밀리앙. 아무것도 아니에요. 벌써 다 지나갔어요."], ["But listen! Do I not hear a carriage in the courtyard?”", "그런데 들어 봐요! 안뜰에서 마차 소리가 들리지 않아요?\""], ["She opened Noirtier’s door, ran to a window in the passage, and returned hastily. “Yes,”", "그녀가 누아르티에의 방문을 열고 복도의 창문으로 달려갔다가 황급히 돌아왔다. \"맞아요.\""], ["said she, “it is Madame Danglars and her daughter, who have come to call on us.", "그녀가 말했다. \"당글라르 부인과 따님이 우리를 찾아왔어요. 안녕히—저는 가 봐야겠어요."], ["Good-bye;—I must run away, for they would send here for me, or, rather, farewell till I see you again.", "안 그러면 저를 부르러 사람을 보낼 테니까요. 아니, 다시 볼 때까지 안녕이라고 해 두죠."], ["Stay with grandpapa, Maximilian; I promise you not to persuade them to stay.”", "할아버지 곁에 계세요, 막시밀리앙. 그분들을 붙잡아 두지 않겠다고 약속할게요.\""]], [["40274m\nMorrel watched her as she left the room; he heard her ascend the little staircase which led both to Madame de Villefort’s apartments and to hers.", "모렐은 그녀가 방을 나서는 모습을 지켜보았다. 빌포르 부인의 거처와 그녀의 거처로 동시에 이어지는 작은 계단을 그녀가 올라가는 소리가 들렸다."], ["As soon as she was gone, Noirtier made a sign to Morrel to take the dictionary.", "그녀가 가 버리자 누아르티에가 모렐에게 사전을 가져오라고 신호했다."], ["Morrel obeyed; guided by Valentine, he had learned how to understand the old man quickly.", "모렐이 그대로 따랐다. 발랑틴의 가르침 덕분에 그는 노인의 뜻을 빨리 알아듣는 법을 익혀 두었다."], ["Accustomed, however, as he was to the work, he had to repeat most of the letters of the alphabet and to find every word in the dictionary, so that it was ten minutes before the thought of the old man was translated by these words,", "그러나 그 일에 익숙해졌다고는 해도, 알파벳 글자 대부분을 거듭 짚어 가며 사전에서 단어를 하나하나 찾아야 했다. 그래서 노인의 생각이 다음과 같은 말로 옮겨지기까지 십 분이 걸렸다."]], [["“Fetch the glass of water and the decanter from Valentine’s room.”", "\"발랑틴의 방에서 물잔과 물병을 가져오게.\""]], [["Morrel rang immediately for the servant who had taken Barrois’s situation, and in Noirtier’s name gave that order.", "모렐은 곧바로 종을 울려 바루아의 자리를 대신하게 된 하인을 불렀고, 누아르티에의 이름으로 그렇게 분부했다."], ["The servant soon returned.", "하인이 곧 돌아왔다."], ["The decanter and the glass were completely empty.", "물병도 잔도 완전히 비어 있었다."], ["Noirtier made a sign that he wished to speak.", "누아르티에가 말을 하고 싶다는 신호를 보냈다."]], [["“Why are the glass and decanter empty?”", "\"잔과 물병이 왜 비어 있나?\""], ["asked he; “Valentine said she only drank half the glassful.”", "그가 물었다. \"발랑틴은 잔의 절반만 마셨다고 했는데.\""]], [["The translation of this new question occupied another five minutes.", "이 새로운 질문을 옮기는 데 다시 오 분이 걸렸다."]], [["“I do not know,”", "\"저는 모르겠습니다.\""], ["said the servant, “but the housemaid is in Mademoiselle Valentine’s room: perhaps she has emptied them.”", "하인이 말했다. \"하지만 하녀가 발랑틴 아가씨 방에 있습니다. 어쩌면 그 여자가 비웠는지도 모르지요.\""]], [["“Ask her,” said Morrel, translating Noirtier’s thought this time by his look.", "\"그 여자한테 물어보게.\" 모렐이 말했다."], ["The servant went out, but returned almost immediately.", "이번에는 누아르티에의 생각을 그 눈빛으로 알아챈 것이었다."], ["“Mademoiselle Valentine passed through the room to go to Madame de Villefort’s,”", "하인이 나갔다가 거의 곧바로 돌아왔다. \"발랑틴 아가씨께서 빌포르 부인께 가시느라 그 방을 지나가셨답니다.\""], ["said he; “and in passing, as she was thirsty, she drank what remained in the glass; as for the decanter, Master Edward had emptied that to make a pond for his ducks.”", "그가 말했다. \"지나가시다가 목이 마르셔서 잔에 남은 것을 드셨고요. 물병으로 말하자면, 에두아르 도련님이 오리에게 연못을 만들어 준다고 비워 버리셨습니다.\""]], [["Noirtier raised his eyes to heaven, as a gambler does who stakes his all on one stroke.", "누아르티에는, 모든 것을 단번에 거는 노름꾼이 그러하듯, 두 눈을 들어 하늘을 우러렀다."], ["From that moment the old man’s eyes were fixed on the door, and did not quit it.", "그 순간부터 노인의 시선은 문에 고정된 채 떨어지지 않았다."]], [["It was indeed Madame Danglars and her daughter whom Valentine had seen; they had been ushered into Madame de Villefort’s room, who had said she would receive them there.", "발랑틴이 본 것은 정말로 당글라르 부인과 그 딸이었다. 두 사람은 빌포르 부인의 방으로 안내되었으니, 부인이 그곳에서 손님을 맞겠다고 했기 때문이다."], ["That is why Valentine passed through her room, which was on a level with Valentine’s, and only separated from it by Edward’s.", "그래서 발랑틴은 부인의 방을 가로질러 갔던 것인데, 그 방은 발랑틴의 방과 같은 층에 있었고 에두아르의 방 하나로만 갈라져 있었다."], ["The two ladies entered the drawing-room with that sort of official stiffness which preludes a formal communication.", "두 부인은 격식을 갖춘 통보에 앞서기 마련인 일종의 의례적인 딱딱함을 띠고 응접실로 들어섰다."], ["Among worldly people manner is contagious.", "사교계 사람들 사이에서는 태도가 전염되는 법이다."], ["Madame de Villefort received them with equal solemnity.", "빌포르 부인도 그에 못지않은 엄숙함으로 그들을 맞았다."], ["Valentine entered at this moment, and the formalities were resumed.", "마침 발랑틴이 들어섰고, 격식은 다시 차려졌다."]], [["“My dear friend,”", "\"이보세요, 부인,\""], ["said the baroness, while the two young people were shaking hands, “I and Eugénie are come to be the first to announce to you the approaching marriage of my daughter with Prince Cavalcanti.”", "두 젊은 처녀가 악수를 나누는 동안 남작 부인이 말했다. \"외제니와 제가 제일 먼저 알려 드리려고 왔답니다. 제 딸이 곧 카발칸티 공작과 혼인을 한답니다.\""], ["Danglars kept up the title of prince.", "당글라르는 공작이라는 칭호를 계속 내세우고 있었다."], ["The popular banker found that it answered better than count.", "이 인기 있는 은행가는 그것이 백작보다 더 잘 먹힌다는 것을 알았던 것이다."]], [["“Allow me to present you my sincere congratulations,”", "\"진심으로 축하의 말씀을 드리게 해 주세요.\""], ["replied Madame de Villefort.", "빌포르 부인이 대답했다."], ["“Prince Cavalcanti appears to be a young man of rare qualities.”", "\"카발칸티 공작은 보기 드문 자질을 갖춘 청년 같더군요.\""]], [["40276m\n“Listen,”", "\"들어 보세요,\""], ["said the baroness, smiling; “speaking to you as a friend I can say that the prince does not yet appear all he will be.", "남작 부인이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"벗으로서 말씀드리자면, 그 공작은 아직 장차 될 모습을 다 보여 주지는 못했어요."], ["He has about him a little of that foreign manner by which French persons recognize, at first sight, the Italian or German nobleman.", "프랑스 사람이 첫눈에 이탈리아나 독일 귀족을 알아보게 하는, 그런 외국풍의 분위기가 조금 있긴 하죠."], ["Besides, he gives evidence of great kindness of disposition, much keenness of wit, and as to suitability, M. Danglars assures me that his fortune is majestic—that is his word.”", "게다가 마음씨가 무척 너그럽고, 재치도 아주 날카로운 청년이에요. 어울림으로 말하자면, 당글라르 씨가 그의 재산이 그야말로 '장엄하다'고 장담하시던걸요—그게 그이 표현이에요.\""]], [["“And then,”", "\"그리고,\""], ["said Eugénie, while turning over the leaves of Madame de Villefort’s album, “add that you have taken a great fancy to the young man.”", "외제니가 빌포르 부인의 화첩을 넘기며 말했다. \"어머니가 그 청년을 무척 마음에 들어 하신다는 것도 덧붙이셔야죠.\""]], [["“And,”", "\"그럼,\""], ["said Madame de Villefort, “I need not ask you if you share that fancy.”", "빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"아가씨도 그 마음에 같이하는지는 굳이 물을 필요가 없겠군요.\""]], [["“I?” replied Eugénie with her usual candor.", "\"저요?\" 외제니가 여느 때처럼 솔직하게 대답했다."], ["“Oh, not the least in the world, madame!", "\"오, 조금도 그렇지 않아요, 부인!"], ["My wish was not to confine myself to domestic cares, or the caprices of any man, but to be an artist, and consequently free in heart, in person, and in thought.”", "제 소망은 집안일이나 어느 남자의 변덕에 매여 사는 게 아니라, 예술가가 되는 것이었어요. 그래서 마음도, 몸도, 생각도 자유로운 사람이 되고 싶었지요.\""]], [["Eugénie pronounced these words with so firm a tone that the color mounted to Valentine’s cheeks.", "외제니가 어찌나 단호한 어조로 이 말을 했던지, 발랑틴의 두 뺨에 핏기가 올랐다."], ["The timid girl could not understand that vigorous nature which appeared to have none of the timidities of woman.", "소심한 처녀로서는 여자다운 소심함이라고는 조금도 없어 보이는 그 강인한 천성을 이해할 수 없었다."]], [["“At any rate,”", "\"어쨌든,\""], ["said she, “since I am to be married whether I will or not, I ought to be thankful to Providence for having released me from my engagement with M.", "그녀가 말했다. \"원하든 원치 않든 저는 결혼을 해야 할 처지니까, 알베르 드 모르세르 씨와의 약혼에서 벗어나게 해 주신 섭리에 감사해야겠지요."], ["Albert de Morcerf, or I should this day have been the wife of a dishonored man.”", "안 그랬다면 저는 오늘 명예를 잃은 남자의 아내가 되어 있었을 테니까요.\""]], [["“It is true,”", "\"그건 사실이에요.\""], ["said the baroness, with that strange simplicity sometimes met with among fashionable ladies, and of which plebeian intercourse can never entirely deprive them,—“it is very true that had not the Morcerfs hesitated, my daughter would have married Monsieur Albert.", "남작 부인이 말했다. 그것은 상류층 부인들에게서 이따금 마주치는, 평민과의 교류로도 결코 완전히 씻어 낼 수 없는 그 묘한 천진함이었다. \"모르세르 집안이 망설이지만 않았다면 제 딸이 알베르 씨와 혼인했으리라는 건 정말 사실이에요."], ["The general depended much on it; he even came to force M. Danglars.", "장군은 그 일에 잔뜩 기대를 걸었지요. 심지어 당글라르 씨를 압박하러 직접 찾아오기까지 했답니다."], ["We have had a narrow escape.”", "가까스로 화를 면한 셈이에요.\""]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["said Valentine, timidly, “does all the father’s shame revert upon the son?", "발랑틴이 소심하게 말했다. \"아버지의 수치가 모조리 아들에게 되돌아가는 걸까요?"], ["Monsieur Albert appears to me quite innocent of the treason charged against the general.”", "알베르 씨는 장군에게 씌워진 반역 혐의와는 전혀 무관해 보이는데요.\""]], [["“Excuse me,”", "\"미안하지만,\""], ["said the implacable young girl, “Monsieur Albert claims and well deserves his share.", "그 가차 없는 처녀가 말했다. \"알베르 씨도 자기 몫을 주장하고, 또 충분히 그럴 만해요."], ["It appears that after having challenged M. de Monte Cristo at the Opera yesterday, he apologized on the ground today.”", "어제 오페라 극장에서 몬테크리스토 씨에게 결투를 신청해 놓고, 오늘은 결투장에서 사과를 했다지 뭐예요.\""]], [["“Impossible,”", "\"그럴 리가요.\""], ["said Madame de Villefort.", "빌포르 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Ah, my dear friend,”", "\"아, 이보세요,\""], ["said Madame Danglars, with the same simplicity we before noticed, “it is a fact.", "당글라르 부인이 앞서 보았던 그 천진함 그대로 말했다. \"사실이에요."], ["I heard it from M. Debray, who was present at the explanation.”", "그 해명 자리에 있었던 드브레 씨한테서 들었는걸요.\""]], [["Valentine also knew the truth, but she did not answer.", "발랑틴도 진실을 알고 있었지만, 대답하지 않았다."], ["A single word had reminded her that Morrel was expecting her in M. Noirtier’s room.", "단 한 마디가, 모렐이 누아르티에 씨의 방에서 자신을 기다리고 있다는 것을 일깨운 것이다."], ["Deeply engaged with a sort of inward contemplation, Valentine had ceased for a moment to join in the conversation.", "일종의 내면의 사색에 깊이 빠져, 발랑틴은 한순간 대화에 끼어들기를 멈추었다."], ["She would, indeed, have found it impossible to repeat what had been said the last few minutes, when suddenly Madame Danglars’ hand, pressed on her arm, aroused her from her lethargy.", "사실 지난 몇 분 동안 오간 말을 그대로 옮겨 보라 하면 도저히 할 수 없었을 것이다. 그때 갑자기 당글라르 부인의 손이 그녀의 팔을 누르며 무기력에서 그녀를 깨웠다."]], [["“What is it?” said she, starting at Madame Danglars’ touch as she would have done from an electric shock.", "\"무슨 일이세요?\" 그녀가 마치 감전이라도 된 듯 당글라르 부인의 손길에 흠칫 놀라며 말했다."]], [["“It is, my dear Valentine,”", "\"다름이 아니라, 발랑틴 양,\""], ["said the baroness, “that you are, doubtless, suffering.” 40280m", "남작 부인이 말했다. \"아가씨가 분명 어디가 아픈 것 같아서요.\""]], [["“I?” said the young girl, passing her hand across her burning forehead.", "\"제가요?\" 처녀가 화끈거리는 이마에 손을 갖다 대며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, look at yourself in that glass; you have turned pale and then red successively, three or four times in one minute.”", "\"그래요, 저 거울에 자기 모습을 좀 비춰 봐요. 일 분 사이에 서너 번이나 창백해졌다 붉어졌다 하던걸요.\""]], [["“Indeed,”", "\"정말이에요.\""], ["cried Eugénie, “you are very pale!”", "외제니가 외쳤다. \"얼굴이 아주 창백해요!\""]], [["“Oh, do not be alarmed; I have been so for many days.”", "\"아, 놀라지 마세요. 며칠째 이런걸요.\""], ["Artless as she was, the young girl knew that this was an opportunity to leave, and besides, Madame de Villefort came to her assistance.", "천진한 처녀였지만, 그녀는 이것이 자리를 뜰 기회임을 알았다. 게다가 빌포르 부인이 그녀를 거들어 주었다."]], [["“Retire, Valentine,”", "\"물러가요, 발랑틴.\" 부인이 말했다."], ["said she; “you are really suffering, and these ladies will excuse you; drink a glass of pure water, it will restore you.”", "\"정말로 어디가 아픈 모양이니, 이분들도 양해해 주실 거예요. 맑은 물을 한 잔 마셔요. 그러면 좀 나아질 거예요.\""]], [["Valentine kissed Eugénie, bowed to Madame Danglars, who had already risen to take her leave, and went out.", "발랑틴은 외제니에게 입을 맞추고, 이미 작별 인사를 하려고 일어선 당글라르 부인에게 목례한 뒤 방을 나갔다."]], [["“That poor child,”", "\"가엾은 아이.\""], ["said Madame de Villefort when Valentine was gone, “she makes me very uneasy, and I should not be astonished if she had some serious illness.”", "발랑틴이 나가자 빌포르 부인이 말했다. \"저 애 때문에 마음이 무척 놓이지 않네요. 무슨 중병이라도 걸린 거라면 저로서는 놀랄 일도 아닐 거예요.\""]], [["Meanwhile, Valentine, in a sort of excitement which she could not quite understand, had crossed Edward’s room without noticing some trick of the child, and through her own had reached the little staircase.", "한편 발랑틴은 스스로도 잘 알 수 없는 일종의 흥분에 사로잡힌 채, 에두아르가 무슨 장난을 쳐 둔 것도 알아채지 못하고 그 아이의 방을 가로질렀다. 그러고는 자기 방을 지나 작은 계단에 이르렀다."]], [["She was within three steps of the bottom; she already heard Morrel’s voice, when suddenly a cloud passed over her eyes, her stiffened foot missed the step, her hands had no power to hold the baluster, and falling against the wall she lost her balance wholly and toppled to the floor.", "그녀가 계단 끝에서 세 칸을 남겨 두었을 때였다. 이미 모렐의 목소리가 들리던 그때, 갑자기 두 눈에 구름이 끼었다. 뻣뻣해진 발이 디딤판을 헛디뎠고, 두 손은 난간을 붙들 힘이 없었다."], ["Morrel bounded to the door, opened it, and found Valentine stretched out at the bottom of the stairs.", "벽에 부딪치며 그녀는 완전히 중심을 잃고 바닥으로 고꾸라졌다. 모렐이 문으로 뛰어들어 문을 열었다. 발랑틴이 계단 아래에 뻗어 있었다."], ["Quick as a flash, he raised her in his arms and placed her in a chair.", "그는 번개처럼 빠르게 그녀를 두 팔로 안아 올려 의자에 앉혔다."], ["Valentine opened her eyes.", "발랑틴이 눈을 떴다."]], [["“Oh, what a clumsy thing I am,”", "\"아, 내가 정말 칠칠치 못하지.\""], ["said she with feverish volubility; “I don’t know my way.", "그녀가 열에 들뜬 듯 빠르게 말했다. \"길을 못 찾겠어요."], ["I forgot there were three more steps before the landing.”", "층계참 앞에 세 칸이 더 남았다는 걸 깜빡했어요.\""]], [["“You have hurt yourself, perhaps,”", "\"혹시 다친 건 아닙니까.\""], ["said Morrel.", "모렐이 말했다."], ["“What can I do for you, Valentine?”", "\"제가 뭘 해 드리면 좋을까요, 발랑틴?\""]], [["Valentine looked around her; she saw the deepest terror depicted in Noirtier’s eyes.", "발랑틴이 주위를 둘러보았다. 누아르티에의 눈에 더없이 깊은 공포가 어려 있는 것이 보였다."]], [["“Don’t worry, dear grandpapa,”", "\"걱정 마세요, 할아버지.\""], ["said she, endeavoring to smile; “it is nothing—it is nothing; I was giddy, that is all.”", "그녀가 애써 미소 지으며 말했다. \"아무것도 아니에요—아무것도 아니에요. 그냥 어지러웠을 뿐이에요.\""]], [["“Another attack of giddiness,”", "\"또 어지럼증이로군요.\""], ["said Morrel, clasping his hands.", "모렐이 두 손을 맞잡으며 말했다."], ["“Oh, attend to it, Valentine, I entreat you.”", "\"오, 부디 그걸 가벼이 여기지 마세요, 발랑틴. 제발 부탁입니다.\""]], [["“But no,”", "\"아니에요,\""], ["said Valentine,—“no, I tell you it is all past, and it was nothing.", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"아니에요, 다 지나갔다니까요. 아무것도 아니었어요."], ["Now, let me tell you some news; Eugénie is to be married in a week, and in three days there is to be a grand feast, a betrothal festival.", "자, 소식을 하나 들려 드릴게요. 외제니가 일주일 뒤에 결혼해요. 사흘 뒤에는 성대한 잔치, 약혼 축하연이 열린대요."], ["We are all invited, my father, Madame de Villefort, and I—at least, I understood it so.”", "우리 모두 초대받았어요. 아버지와 빌포르 부인, 그리고 저까지—적어도 저는 그렇게 들었어요.\""]], [["“When will it be our turn to think of these things?", "\"우리가 이런 일들을 생각하게 될 차례는 언제일까요?"], ["Oh, Valentine, you who have so much influence over your grandpapa, try to make him answer—Soon.”", "오, 발랑틴, 할아버지께 그토록 큰 영향을 미치는 당신이니, 부디 할아버지께서 '곧'이라고 대답하시도록 애써 봐요.\""]], [["“And do you,”", "\"그럼 당신은,\""], ["said Valentine, “depend on me to stimulate the tardiness and arouse the memory of grandpapa?”", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"할아버지의 굼뜬 마음을 부추기고 그 기억을 일깨우는 일을 제게 맡기는 거예요?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네,\""], ["cried Morrel, “make haste.", "모렐이 외쳤다. \"서둘러 줘요."], ["So long as you are not mine, Valentine, I shall always think I may lose you.”", "당신이 내 사람이 되기 전까지는, 발랑틴, 나는 언제나 당신을 잃을지도 모른다고 생각하게 될 테니까요.\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오,\""], ["replied Valentine with a convulsive movement, “oh, indeed, Maximilian, you are too timid for an officer, for a soldier who, they say, never knows fear.", "발랑틴이 발작하듯 몸을 떨며 대답했다. \"오, 정말이지, 막시밀리앙, 당신은 장교치고는 너무 겁이 많아요."], ["Ha, ha, ha!”", "두려움이라곤 모른다는 군인이면서요. 하하하!\""]], [["She burst into a forced and melancholy laugh, her arms stiffened and twisted, her head fell back on her chair, and she remained motionless.", "그녀가 억지스럽고 서글픈 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 두 팔이 뻣뻣해지며 뒤틀렸고, 고개가 의자 등받이로 떨어졌으며, 그녀는 꼼짝도 하지 않았다."], ["The cry of terror which was stopped on Noirtier’s lips, seemed to start from his eyes.", "누아르티에의 입술에서 막혔던 공포의 외침이 그 두 눈에서 터져 나오는 듯했다."], ["Morrel understood it; he knew he must call assistance.", "모렐은 그것을 알아챘다."], ["The young man rang the bell violently; the housemaid who had been in Mademoiselle Valentine’s room, and the servant who had replaced Barrois, ran in at the same moment.", "그는 사람을 불러야 한다는 것을 깨달았다. 젊은이는 종을 거칠게 울렸다. 발랑틴 아가씨의 방에 있던 하녀와 바루아를 대신하게 된 하인이 동시에 달려 들어왔다."], ["Valentine was so pale, so cold, so inanimate that without listening to what was said to them they were seized with the fear which pervaded that house, and they flew into the passage crying for help.", "발랑틴이 어찌나 창백하고, 차갑고, 생기가 없었던지, 두 사람은 자기들에게 하는 말을 듣지도 않고 그 집 안에 감도는 공포에 사로잡혀, 살려 달라고 외치며 복도로 뛰쳐나갔다."], ["Madame Danglars and Eugénie were going out at that moment; they heard the cause of the disturbance.", "마침 당글라르 부인과 외제니가 나가려던 참이었다. 그들은 소동이 일어난 까닭을 들었다."]], [["“I told you so!” exclaimed Madame de Villefort.", "\"내가 그랬잖아요!\" 빌포르 부인이 외쳤다."], ["“Poor child!”", "\"가엾은 아이!\""]], [["# Chapter 94. Maximilian’s Avowal", "# 제92장 자살"]], [["40278m\nAt the same moment M. de Villefort’s voice was heard calling from his study, “What is the matter?”", "바로 그 순간 빌포르의 목소리가 서재에서 들려왔다. \"무슨 일이냐?\""]], [["Morrel looked at Noirtier who had recovered his self-command, and with a glance indicated the closet where once before under somewhat similar circumstances, he had taken refuge.", "모렐은 어느새 평정을 되찾은 누아르티에를 바라보았다. 노인은 눈짓으로, 전에 비슷한 상황에서 한 번 몸을 숨겼던 그 골방을 가리켰다."], ["He had only time to get his hat and throw himself breathless into the closet when the procureur’s footstep was heard in the passage.", "모자를 챙겨 숨이 턱에 닿도록 골방에 몸을 던질 시간밖에 없었다. 곧 검사의 발소리가 복도에서 들려왔다."]], [["Villefort sprang into the room, ran to Valentine, and took her in his arms.", "빌포르가 방 안으로 뛰어들어 발랑틴에게 달려가 그녀를 품에 안았다."]], [["“A physician, a physician,—M. d’Avrigny!”", "\"의사를, 의사를 불러라, 다브리니 선생님을!\""], ["cried Villefort; “or rather I will go for him myself.”", "빌포르가 외쳤다. \"아니, 내가 직접 가지.\""]], [["He flew from the apartment, and Morrel at the same moment darted out at the other door.", "그는 방에서 뛰쳐나갔고, 같은 순간 모렐은 반대편 문으로 빠져나갔다."], ["He had been struck to the heart by a frightful recollection—the conversation he had heard between the doctor and Villefort the night of Madame de Saint-Méran’s death, recurred to him; these symptoms, to a less alarming extent, were the same which had preceded the death of Barrois.", "한 가지 무서운 기억이 그의 가슴을 찔렀던 것이다. 생메랑 부인이 죽던 날 밤, 의사와 빌포르가 나누던 대화가 떠올랐다. 이 증상들은, 정도는 덜 끔찍하다 해도, 바루아의 죽음에 앞서 나타났던 것과 똑같았다."], ["At the same time Monte Cristo’s voice seemed to resound in his ear with the words he had heard only two hours before, “Whatever you want, Morrel, come to me; I have great power.”", "동시에 불과 두 시간 전에 들었던 몬테크리스토의 목소리가 그의 귓전에 울리는 듯했다. \"무엇이 필요하든, 모렐, 내게 오시오. 내겐 큰 힘이 있소.\""]], [["More rapidly than thought, he darted down the Rue Matignon, and thence to the Avenue des Champs-Élysées.", "생각보다 빠르게 그는 마티뇽 거리로 내달렸고, 거기서 샹젤리제 대로로 향했다."]], [["Meanwhile M. de Villefort arrived in a hired cabriolet at M.", "한편 빌포르는 삯마차를 타고 다브리니의 집 앞에 다다랐다."], ["d’Avrigny’s door. He rang so violently that the porter was alarmed.", "어찌나 사납게 초인종을 울렸던지 문지기가 깜짝 놀랐다."], ["Villefort ran upstairs without saying a word.", "빌포르는 한마디도 없이 계단을 뛰어 올라갔다."], ["The porter knew him, and let him pass, only calling to him:", "문지기는 그를 알아보고 지나가게 두었을 뿐, 다만 이렇게 외쳤다."]], [["“In his study, Monsieur Procureur—in his study!”", "\"서재에 계십니다, 검사님, 서재에요!\""], ["Villefort pushed, or rather forced, the door open.", "빌포르는 문을 밀쳤다, 아니 거의 부수다시피 열었다."]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["said the doctor, “is it you?”", "의사가 말했다. \"당신이오?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇소.\""], ["said Villefort, closing the door after him, “it is I, who am come in my turn to ask you if we are quite alone.", "빌포르가 문을 닫으며 말했다. \"이번엔 내가 와서 묻겠소. 우리 단둘뿐이오?"], ["Doctor, my house is accursed!”", "선생, 내 집은 저주받았소!\""]], [["“What?”", "\"뭐라고요?\""], ["said the latter with apparent coolness, but with deep emotion, “have you another invalid?”", "의사가 겉으로는 냉정한 듯, 그러나 깊은 동요를 담아 말했다. \"또 환자가 생겼소?\""]], [["“Yes, doctor,”", "\"그렇소, 선생.\""], ["cried Villefort, clutching his hair, “yes!”", "빌포르가 머리카락을 움켜쥐며 외쳤다. \"그렇소!\""]], [["D’Avrigny’s look implied, “I told you it would be so.”", "다브리니의 눈빛이 말하고 있었다. '그렇게 될 거라고 내가 말하지 않았소.'"], ["Then he slowly uttered these words, “Who is now dying in your house?", "그러더니 그는 천천히 이렇게 말했다. \"이번엔 당신 집에서 누가 죽어 가고 있소?"], ["What new victim is going to accuse you of weakness before God?”", "어떤 새로운 희생자가 하느님 앞에서 당신의 나약함을 고발하려는 거요?\""]], [["A mournful sob burst from Villefort’s heart; he approached the doctor, and seizing his arm,—“Valentine,”", "빌포르의 가슴에서 비통한 흐느낌이 터져 나왔다. 그는 의사에게 다가가 그의 팔을 붙잡았다. \"발랑틴이오.\""], ["said he, “it is Valentine’s turn!” 40284m", "그가 말했다. \"이번엔 발랑틴 차례요!\""]], [["“Your daughter!”", "\"당신 딸이!\""], ["cried d’Avrigny with grief and surprise.", "다브리니가 비통과 놀라움으로 외쳤다."]], [["“You see you were deceived,”", "\"보시오, 당신이 잘못 짚었던 거요.\""], ["murmured the magistrate; “come and see her, and on her bed of agony entreat her pardon for having suspected her.”", "검사가 중얼거렸다. \"가서 그 애를 봐 주시오. 그리고 그 고통의 침상 곁에서, 그 애를 의심한 일에 대해 용서를 빌어 주시오.\""]], [["“Each time you have applied to me,”", "\"당신이 나를 찾을 때마다,\""], ["said the doctor, “it has been too late; still I will go.", "의사가 말했다. \"늘 너무 늦었소. 그래도 가겠소."], ["But let us make haste, sir; with the enemies you have to do with there is no time to be lost.”", "하지만 서두릅시다. 당신이 상대하는 적들 앞에서는 한순간도 허비할 수 없으니.\""]], [["“Oh, this time, doctor, you shall not have to reproach me with weakness.", "\"오, 이번엔, 선생, 나약하다고 나를 책망할 일은 없을 거요."], ["This time I will know the assassin, and will pursue him.”", "이번엔 살인자를 알아내어 끝까지 쫓겠소.\""]], [["“Let us try first to save the victim before we think of revenging her,”", "\"복수를 생각하기 전에 먼저 희생자를 구해 봅시다.\""], ["said d’Avrigny. “Come.”", "다브리니가 말했다. \"갑시다.\""]], [["The same cabriolet which had brought Villefort took them back at full speed, and at this moment Morrel rapped at Monte Cristo’s door.", "빌포르를 데려왔던 바로 그 삯마차가 두 사람을 전속력으로 다시 실어 갔다. 바로 이 순간 모렐이 몬테크리스토의 집 문을 두드렸다."]], [["The count was in his study and was reading with an angry look something which Bertuccio had brought in haste.", "백작은 서재에서, 베르투치오가 황급히 가져온 무언가를 잔뜩 화난 표정으로 읽고 있었다."], ["Hearing the name of Morrel, who had left him only two hours before, the count raised his head, arose, and sprang to meet him.", "두 시간 전에 자신을 떠난 모렐의 이름을 듣자, 백작은 고개를 들고 일어나 그를 맞으러 뛰어나왔다."]], [["“What is the matter, Maximilian?”", "\"무슨 일이오, 막시밀리앙?\""], ["asked he; “you are pale, and the perspiration rolls from your forehead.”", "그가 물었다. \"안색이 창백하고, 이마에서 땀이 줄줄 흐르는군.\""], ["Morrel fell into a chair.", "모렐이 의자에 쓰러지듯 앉았다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네,\""], ["said he, “I came quickly; I wanted to speak to you.”", "그가 말했다. \"급히 왔습니다. 당신과 이야기를 나누고 싶었어요.\""]], [["“Are all your family well?”", "\"가족분들은 모두 무탈하시오?\""], ["asked the count, with an affectionate benevolence, whose sincerity no one could for a moment doubt.", "백작이 더없이 다정한 호의로 물었다. 그 진심을 누구도 한순간조차 의심할 수 없었다."]], [["“Thank you, count—thank you,”", "\"고맙습니다, 백작님, 고맙습니다.\""], ["said the young man, evidently embarrassed how to begin the conversation; “yes, everyone in my family is well.”", "청년은 어떻게 말을 꺼내야 할지 몰라 곤혹스러운 기색이 역력했다. \"네, 제 가족은 모두 무탈합니다.\""]], [["“So much the better; yet you have something to tell me?”", "\"그것참 다행이오. 그래도 내게 할 말이 있는 것이지요?\""], ["replied the count with increased anxiety.", "백작이 더욱 불안한 기색으로 대답했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네,\""], ["said Morrel, “it is true; I have but now left a house where death has just entered, to run to you.”", "모렐이 말했다. \"맞습니다. 방금 죽음이 막 들이닥친 어느 집을 떠나, 당신에게 달려온 길입니다.\""]], [["“Are you then come from M. de Morcerf’s?”", "\"그럼 모르세르 씨 댁에서 오는 길이오?\""], ["asked Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 물었다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아닙니다.\""], ["said Morrel; “is someone dead in his house?”", "모렐이 말했다. \"그 댁에 누가 죽기라도 했습니까?\""]], [["“The general has just blown his brains out,”", "\"장군이 방금 권총으로 머리를 쏘았소.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo with great coolness.", "몬테크리스토가 더없이 냉정하게 대답했다."]], [["“Oh, what a dreadful event!”", "\"오, 얼마나 끔찍한 일입니까!\""], ["cried Maximilian.", "막시밀리앙이 외쳤다."]], [["“Not for the countess, or for Albert,”", "\"백작 부인이나 알베르에게는 그렇지 않소.\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “a dead father or husband is better than a dishonored one,—blood washes out shame.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"죽은 아버지나 남편이 명예를 잃은 아버지나 남편보다 낫지. 피는 치욕을 씻어 내는 법이오.\""]], [["“Poor countess,”", "\"가엾은 백작 부인.\""], ["said Maximilian, “I pity her very much; she is so noble a woman!”", "막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"정말 안됐습니다. 그토록 고결한 분인데요!\""]], [["“Pity Albert also, Maximilian; for believe me he is the worthy son of the countess.", "\"알베르도 가엾이 여기시오, 막시밀리앙. 정말이지 그는 백작 부인의 자식다운 자식이오."], ["But let us return to yourself.", "하지만 당신 이야기로 돌아갑시다."], ["You have hastened to me—can I have the happiness of being useful to you?”", "내게 급히 달려왔으니, 내가 당신에게 도움이 되는 기쁨을 누릴 수 있겠소?\""]], [["40286m\n“Yes, I need your help: that is I thought like a madman that you could lend me your assistance in a case where God alone can succor me.”", "\"네, 당신의 도움이 필요합니다. 그러니까, 저는 미친 사람처럼, 오직 하느님만이 구원하실 수 있는 일에 당신이 도움을 빌려주실 수 있으리라 생각했어요.\""]], [["“Tell me what it is,”", "\"무슨 일인지 말해 보시오.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다."]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오,\""], ["said Morrel, “I know not, indeed, if I may reveal this secret to mortal ears, but fatality impels me, necessity constrains me, count——”", "모렐이 말했다. \"정말이지 이 비밀을 사람의 귀에 털어놓아도 될지 모르겠습니다. 하지만 운명이 저를 떠밀고, 절박함이 저를 옭아맵니다, 백작님……\""], ["Morrel hesitated.", "모렐이 머뭇거렸다."]], [["“Do you think I love you?” said Monte Cristo, taking the young man’s hand affectionately in his.", "\"내가 당신을 아낀다고 생각하지 않소?\" 몬테크리스토가 청년의 손을 다정하게 자기 손으로 잡으며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, you encourage me, and something tells me there,”", "\"오, 당신은 제게 용기를 주십니다. 그리고 여기서 무언가가 제게 말합니다.\""], ["placing his hand on his heart, “that I ought to have no secret from you.”", "그가 가슴에 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"당신께는 어떤 비밀도 감추어서는 안 된다고요.\""]], [["“You are right, Morrel; God is speaking to your heart, and your heart speaks to you.", "\"옳소, 모렐. 하느님이 당신의 마음에 말씀하시고, 당신의 마음이 당신에게 말하는 거요."], ["Tell me what it says.”", "그 말이 무엇인지 들려주시오.\""]], [["“Count, will you allow me to send Baptistin to inquire after someone you know?”", "\"백작님, 당신이 아는 어떤 사람의 안부를 알아보도록 바티스탱을 보내도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["“I am at your service, and still more my servants.”", "\"나는 당신을 위해 있고, 내 하인들은 더더욱 그렇소.\""]], [["“Oh, I cannot live if she is not better.”", "\"오, 그녀가 차도가 없으면 저는 살 수 없습니다.\""]], [["“Shall I ring for Baptistin?”", "\"바티스탱을 부르도록 종을 울릴까?\""]], [["“No, I will go and speak to him myself.” Morrel went out, called Baptistin, and whispered a few words to him.", "\"아닙니다, 제가 직접 가서 말하겠습니다.\" 모렐이 나가 바티스탱을 불러 몇 마디를 속삭였다."], ["The valet ran directly.", "하인이 곧장 달려갔다."]], [["“Well, have you sent?”", "\"자, 사람을 보냈소?\""], ["asked Monte Cristo, seeing Morrel return.", "모렐이 돌아오는 것을 보고 몬테크리스토가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, and now I shall be more calm.”", "\"네, 이제 좀 더 마음이 가라앉겠지요.\""]], [["“You know I am waiting,”", "\"내가 기다리고 있는 것을 알지요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, smiling.", "몬테크리스토가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, and I will tell you.", "\"네, 말씀드리겠습니다."], ["One evening I was in a garden; a clump of trees concealed me; no one suspected I was there.", "어느 날 저녁, 저는 어느 정원에 있었습니다. 나무 덤불이 저를 가려 주어, 아무도 제가 거기 있는 줄 몰랐지요."], ["Two persons passed near me—allow me to conceal their names for the present; they were speaking in an undertone, and yet I was so interested in what they said that I did not lose a single word.”", "두 사람이 제 곁을 지나갔습니다. 지금은 그들의 이름을 감추도록 허락해 주십시오. 그들은 나직한 목소리로 이야기하고 있었는데, 저는 그들의 말에 어찌나 마음이 쏠렸던지 단 한마디도 놓치지 않았습니다.\""]], [["“This is a gloomy introduction, if I may judge from your pallor and shuddering, Morrel.”", "\"안색이 창백하고 떨고 있는 걸 보니, 음울한 도입부로구려, 모렐.\""]], [["“Oh, yes, very gloomy, my friend. Someone had just died in the house to which that garden belonged.", "\"오, 그렇습니다, 아주 음울합니다, 백작님. 그 정원이 딸린 집에서 누군가가 막 죽었던 참이었어요."], ["One of the persons whose conversation I overheard was the master of the house; the other, the physician.", "제가 엿들은 그 대화의 한 사람은 그 집 주인이었고, 다른 한 사람은 의사였습니다."], ["The former was confiding to the latter his grief and fear, for it was the second time within a month that death had suddenly and unexpectedly entered that house which was apparently destined to destruction by some exterminating angel, as an object of God’s anger.”", "주인은 의사에게 자신의 슬픔과 두려움을 털어놓고 있었지요. 한 달 사이에 죽음이 그 집에 갑작스럽고 느닷없이 들이닥친 것이 벌써 두 번째였으니까요. 마치 어떤 멸절의 천사가, 하느님의 분노의 표적으로 삼아 그 집을 파멸시키기로 정해 놓은 듯했습니다.\""]], [["“Ah, indeed?”", "\"아, 정말이오?\""], ["said Monte Cristo, looking earnestly at the young man, and by an imperceptible movement turning his chair, so that he remained in the shade while the light fell full on Maximilian’s face.", "몬테크리스토가 청년을 뚫어지게 바라보며 말했다. 그러고는 알아챌 수 없는 동작으로 의자를 돌려, 빛이 막시밀리앙의 얼굴에 가득 떨어지는 동안 자신은 그늘 속에 머물게 했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네,\""], ["continued Morrel, “death had entered that house twice within one month.”", "모렐이 말을 이었다. \"죽음이 한 달 사이에 그 집에 두 번 들이닥쳤던 겁니다.\""]], [["“And what did the doctor answer?”", "\"그래서 의사는 뭐라고 대답했소?\""], ["asked Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 물었다."]], [["“He replied—he replied, that the death was not a natural one, and must be attributed”—", "\"그는 대답했습니다, 그 죽음은 자연스러운 것이 아니며, 어떤 원인으로 돌려야 한다고요……\""]], [["“To what?”", "\"무슨 원인으로?\""]], [["“To poison.”", "\"독약으로요.\""]], [["“Indeed!”", "\"저런!\""], ["said Monte Cristo with a slight cough which in moments of extreme emotion helped him to disguise a blush, or his pallor, or the intense interest with which he listened; “indeed, Maximilian, did you hear that?”", "몬테크리스토가 가벼운 기침을 하며 말했다. 그 기침은 극도로 동요한 순간에 그가 얼굴이 붉어지거나 창백해지는 것을, 혹은 그가 귀 기울이는 격렬한 관심을 감추는 데 도움이 되곤 했다. \"저런, 막시밀리앙, 그 말을 들었단 말이오?\""]], [["“Yes, my dear count, I heard it; and the doctor added that if another death occurred in a similar way he must appeal to justice.”", "\"네, 백작님, 들었습니다. 그리고 의사는 만약 또 한 번 비슷한 방식으로 죽음이 일어난다면 사법 당국에 호소할 수밖에 없다고 덧붙였습니다.\""]], [["Monte Cristo listened, or appeared to do so, with the greatest calmness.", "몬테크리스토는 더없이 침착하게 귀를 기울였다, 아니 기울이는 듯 보였다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"그런데,\""], ["said Maximilian, “death came a third time, and neither the master of the house nor the doctor said a word.", "막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"죽음이 세 번째로 찾아왔는데, 집주인도 의사도 한마디 하지 않았습니다."], ["Death is now, perhaps, striking a fourth blow.", "그리고 죽음은 지금, 어쩌면 네 번째 일격을 가하고 있는지도 모릅니다."], ["Count, what am I bound to do, being in possession of this secret?”", "백작님, 이 비밀을 쥐고 있는 제가 마땅히 어떻게 해야 합니까?\""]], [["“My dear friend,”", "\"이보시오,\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “you appear to be relating an adventure which we all know by heart.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"당신은 우리 모두가 줄줄 외고 있는 어떤 이야기를 들려주는 것 같소."], ["I know the house where you heard it, or one very similar to it; a house with a garden, a master, a physician, and where there have been three unexpected and sudden deaths.", "나는 당신이 그 이야기를 들은 그 집을 알고 있소. 아니면 그와 아주 비슷한 집이거나. 정원이 있고, 주인이 있고, 의사가 있고, 느닷없고 갑작스러운 죽음이 세 번 있었던 집이지."], ["Well, I have not intercepted your confidence, and yet I know all that as well as you, and I have no conscientious scruples.", "그런데 나는 당신의 비밀을 가로챈 적이 없는데도 당신만큼 그 모든 것을 알고 있고, 양심의 가책 따위는 없소."], ["No, it does not concern me.", "아니지, 그건 내 일이 아니니까."], ["You say an exterminating angel appears to have devoted that house to God’s anger—well, who says your supposition is not reality?", "당신은 멸절의 천사가 하느님의 분노에 그 집을 바친 것 같다고 말했소. 그렇다면, 당신의 추측이 현실이 아니라고 누가 말하겠소?"], ["Do not notice things which those whose interest it is to see them pass over.", "그것을 보는 게 자기네 이익인 자들조차 못 본 척 넘기는 일들을, 당신이 눈여겨보지 마시오."], ["If it is God’s justice, instead of his anger, which is walking through that house, Maximilian, turn away your face and let his justice accomplish its purpose.”", "그 집을 거니는 것이 하느님의 분노가 아니라 하느님의 정의라면, 막시밀리앙, 얼굴을 돌리고 그 정의가 제 목적을 이루도록 내버려 두시오.\""]], [["Morrel shuddered. There was something mournful, solemn, and terrible in the count’s manner.", "모렐이 몸을 떨었다. 백작의 태도에는 음울하고 엄숙하며 무서운 무언가가 있었다."]], [["“Besides,”", "\"게다가,\""], ["continued he, in so changed a tone that no one would have supposed it was the same person speaking—“besides, who says that it will begin again?”", "그는 같은 사람이 말하는 것이라고는 아무도 짐작하지 못할 만큼 달라진 어조로 말을 이었다. \"게다가, 그 일이 또다시 시작되리라고 누가 말하오?\""]], [["“It has returned, count,”", "\"또다시 시작됐습니다, 백작님.\""], ["exclaimed Morrel; “that is why I hastened to you.”", "모렐이 외쳤다. \"그래서 제가 당신께 달려온 겁니다.\""]], [["“Well, what do you wish me to do? Do you wish me, for instance, to give information to the procureur?”", "\"그래, 내가 어떻게 해 주기를 바라오? 이를테면, 내가 검사에게 정보를 알려 주기를 바라오?\""], ["Monte Cristo uttered the last words with so much meaning that Morrel, starting up, cried out:", "몬테크리스토가 이 마지막 말을 어찌나 의미심장하게 내뱉었던지, 모렐이 벌떡 일어나 외쳤다."]], [["“You know of whom I speak, count, do you not?”", "\"제가 누구를 말하는지 아시는군요, 백작님, 그렇지요?\""]], [["“Perfectly well, my good friend; and I will prove it to you by putting the dots to the _i_, or rather by naming the persons.", "\"아주 잘 알고 있소, 친애하는 벗이여. 그리고 그것을 증명해 보이겠소. 글자에 점을 찍듯, 아니, 차라리 그 사람들의 이름을 대 보겠소."], ["You were walking one evening in M.", "당신은 어느 날 저녁 빌포르 씨의 정원을 거닐고 있었소."], ["de Villefort’s garden; from what you relate, I suppose it to have been the evening of Madame de Saint-Méran’s death. You heard M.", "당신이 들려준 이야기로 보아, 그날 저녁이 생메랑 부인이 죽은 저녁이었으리라 짐작하오."], ["de Villefort talking to M. d’Avrigny about the death of M. de Saint-Méran, and that no less surprising, of the countess. M.", "당신은 빌포르 씨가 다브리니 씨에게 생메랑 씨의 죽음에 대해, 그리고 못지않게 놀라운 부인의 죽음에 대해 이야기하는 것을 들었소."], ["d’Avrigny said he believed they both proceeded from poison; and you, honest man, have ever since been asking your heart and sounding your conscience to know if you ought to expose or conceal this secret.", "다브리니 씨는 두 죽음 모두 독약에서 비롯된 것이라 믿는다고 말했고. 그리고 정직한 당신은, 그 이후로 줄곧 이 비밀을 폭로해야 할지 감추어야 할지 알고자 마음에 묻고 양심에 캐물어 왔던 거요."], ["We are no longer in the Middle Ages; there is no longer a Vehmgericht, or Free Tribunals; what do you want to ask these people?", "우리는 이제 중세에 사는 게 아니오. 비밀 재판소나 자유 법정 따위는 이제 없소. 그 사람들에게 무엇을 묻고 싶은 거요?"], ["‘Conscience, what hast thou to do with me?’ as Sterne said.", "'양심아, 네가 나와 무슨 상관이냐?' 스턴이 한 말처럼 말이오."], ["My dear fellow, let them sleep on, if they are asleep; let them grow pale in their drowsiness, if they are disposed to do so, and pray do you remain in peace, who have no remorse to disturb you.”", "이보시오, 그들이 잠들어 있다면 자게 두시오. 졸음 속에서 창백해지고 싶다면 그러게 두시오. 그리고 부디 당신은, 당신을 어지럽힐 회한 따위 없는 당신은, 평온히 지내시오.\""]], [["Deep grief was depicted on Morrel’s features; he seized Monte Cristo’s hand.", "모렐의 얼굴에 깊은 슬픔이 어렸다. 그가 몬테크리스토의 손을 붙잡았다."], ["“But it is beginning again, I say!”", "\"하지만 또다시 시작됐다지 않습니까!\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래,\""], ["said the Count, astonished at his perseverance, which he could not understand, and looking still more earnestly at Maximilian, “let it begin again,—it is like the house of the Atreidae;19 God has condemned them, and they must submit to their punishment.", "백작이 말했다. 그는 도무지 이해할 수 없는 모렐의 집요함에 놀라, 막시밀리앙을 한층 더 뚫어지게 바라보았다. \"또다시 시작되라지."], ["They will all disappear, like the fabrics children build with cards, and which fall, one by one, under the breath of their builder, even if there are two hundred of them.", "그건 아트레우스 가문 같은 것이오. 하느님이 그들을 단죄하셨으니, 그들은 그 벌을 받아야 하오. 그들은 아이들이 카드로 쌓아 올린 집처럼 모조리 무너져 사라질 거요."], ["Three months since it was M.", "그게 이백 장이라 해도, 쌓은 자의 숨결 한 번에 하나하나 무너지는 법이지."], ["de Saint-Méran; Madame de Saint-Méran two months since; the other day it was Barrois; today, the old Noirtier, or young Valentine.”", "석 달 전에는 생메랑 씨였고, 두 달 전에는 생메랑 부인이었소. 며칠 전에는 바루아였고, 오늘은 늙은 누아르티에, 아니면 젊은 발랑틴이오.\""]], [["“You knew it?”", "\"알고 계셨군요?\""], ["cried Morrel, in such a paroxysm of terror that Monte Cristo started,—he whom the falling heavens would have found unmoved; “you knew it, and said nothing?”", "모렐이 외쳤다. 어찌나 극심한 공포에 사로잡혔던지, 하늘이 무너져도 꿈쩍 않을 몬테크리스토조차 흠칫했다. \"알고 계셨으면서, 아무 말도 하지 않으셨다고요?\""]], [["“And what is it to me?”", "\"그게 나와 무슨 상관이오?\""], ["replied Monte Cristo, shrugging his shoulders; “do I know those people?", "몬테크리스토가 어깨를 으쓱하며 대답했다. \"내가 그 사람들을 아오?"], ["and must I lose the one to save the other?", "그리고 한 사람을 구하자고 다른 사람을 잃어야 한단 말이오?"], ["Faith, no, for between the culprit and the victim I have no choice.”", "정말이지, 천만에. 죄인과 희생자 사이에서 나는 어느 쪽도 택할 마음이 없으니까.\""]], [["“But I,”", "\"하지만 저는,\""], ["cried Morrel, groaning with sorrow, “I love her!”", "모렐이 슬픔에 신음하며 외쳤다. \"저는 그녀를 사랑합니다!\""]], [["“You love?—whom?”", "\"사랑한다고? 누구를?\""], ["cried Monte Cristo, starting to his feet, and seizing the two hands which Morrel was raising towards heaven.", "몬테크리스토가 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다. 그러고는 모렐이 하늘을 향해 쳐들던 두 손을 붙잡았다."]], [["“I love most fondly—I love madly—I love as a man who would give his life-blood to spare her a tear—I love Valentine de Villefort, who is being murdered at this moment!", "\"저는 더없이 애틋하게 사랑합니다, 미치도록 사랑합니다. 그녀의 눈물 한 방울을 덜어 줄 수 있다면 제 핏줄의 피라도 다 내어 줄 사람으로서 사랑합니다. 저는 지금 이 순간 살해당하고 있는 발랑틴 드 빌포르를 사랑합니다!"], ["Do you understand me?", "제 말을 알아들으시겠습니까?"], ["I love her; and I ask God and you how I can save her?”", "저는 그녀를 사랑합니다. 그리고 하느님과 당신께 묻습니다, 어떻게 하면 그녀를 구할 수 있는지요?\""]], [["Monte Cristo uttered a cry which those only can conceive who have heard the roar of a wounded lion.", "몬테크리스토가 부르짖었다. 상처 입은 사자의 포효를 들어 본 사람만이 떠올릴 수 있는 그런 부르짖음이었다."], ["“Unhappy man,”", "\"가엾은 사람아,\""], ["cried he, wringing his hands in his turn; “you love Valentine,—that daughter of an accursed race!”", "그가 이번엔 자기 두 손을 비틀며 외쳤다. \"당신이 발랑틴을 사랑하다니, 저주받은 가문의 그 딸을!\""]], [["Never had Morrel witnessed such an expression—never had so terrible an eye flashed before his face—never had the genius of terror he had so often seen, either on the battle-field or in the murderous nights of Algeria, shaken around him more dreadful fire.", "모렐은 그런 표정을 본 적이 없었다. 그토록 무서운 눈빛이 자기 얼굴 앞에서 번득인 적이 없었다. 전쟁터에서, 또 알제리의 살육의 밤마다 그토록 자주 보아 온 공포의 화신조차, 그의 주위에 그토록 무서운 불길을 떨친 적이 없었다."], ["He drew back terrified.", "그는 겁에 질려 뒷걸음질 쳤다."]], [["As for Monte Cristo, after this ebullition he closed his eyes as if dazzled by internal light.", "몬테크리스토로 말하자면, 이 격발 뒤에 마치 내면의 빛에 눈이 부신 듯 두 눈을 감았다."], ["In a moment he restrained himself so powerfully that the tempestuous heaving of his breast subsided, as turbulent and foaming waves yield to the sun’s genial influence when the cloud has passed.", "잠시 후 그는 어찌나 강하게 자신을 다스렸던지, 격렬하게 들썩이던 가슴이 가라앉았다. 구름이 걷히고 나면 거품 일며 사납게 일렁이던 파도가 따스한 햇살에 순응하듯이."], ["This silence, self-control, and struggle lasted about twenty seconds, then the count raised his pallid face.", "이 침묵과 자제와 분투는 이십 초쯤 이어졌다. 이윽고 백작이 창백한 얼굴을 들었다."]], [["“See,”", "\"보시오,\""], ["said he, “my dear friend, how God punishes the most thoughtless and unfeeling men for their indifference, by presenting dreadful scenes to their view.", "그가 말했다. \"친애하는 벗이여, 하느님께서는 가장 경솔하고 무정한 인간들에게 무서운 광경을 들이미심으로써 그들의 무관심을 벌하시는 법이오."], ["I, who was looking on, an eager and curious spectator,—I, who was watching the working of this mournful tragedy,—I, who like a wicked angel was laughing at the evil men committed protected by secrecy (a secret is easily kept by the rich and powerful), I am in my turn bitten by the serpent whose tortuous course I was watching, and bitten to the heart!”", "곁에서 지켜보던 나, 열렬하고 호기심 어린 구경꾼이던 나, 이 음울한 비극이 펼쳐지는 것을 관찰하던 나, 사악한 천사처럼 비밀에 가려진 채 인간들이 저지르는 악을 비웃던 나는—(비밀이란 부유하고 강한 자들에게는 쉽게 지켜지는 법이지)—이제 그 구불구불한 자취를 지켜보던 바로 그 뱀에게 물렸소. 그것도 가슴 깊이 물렸소!\""]], [["Morrel groaned.", "모렐이 신음했다."]], [["“Come, come,”", "\"자, 자,\" 백작이 말을 이었다."], ["continued the count, “complaints are unavailing, be a man, be strong, be full of hope, for I am here and will watch over you.”", "\"탄식해 봐야 소용없소. 사내답게, 굳세게, 희망을 가득 품으시오. 내가 여기 있으니, 당신을 지켜 주겠소.\""]], [["Morrel shook his head sorrowfully.", "모렐이 슬프게 고개를 저었다."]], [["“I tell you to hope.", "\"희망을 가지라고 하지 않소."], ["Do you understand me?”", "내 말을 알아듣겠소?\""], ["cried Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 외쳤다."], ["“Remember that I never uttered a falsehood and am never deceived.", "\"나는 단 한 번도 거짓을 말한 적이 없고, 결코 속는 법이 없다는 걸 기억하시오."], ["It is twelve o’clock, Maximilian; thank heaven that you came at noon rather than in the evening, or tomorrow morning.", "지금 열두 시요, 막시밀리앙. 당신이 저녁이나 내일 아침이 아니라 정오에 와 준 것을 하늘에 감사하시오. 잘 들으시오, 모렐, 지금은 정오요."], ["Listen, Morrel—it is noon; if Valentine is not now dead, she will not die.”", "만약 발랑틴이 지금 죽지 않았다면, 그녀는 죽지 않을 거요.\""]], [["“How so?”", "\"어떻게 그럴 수가 있습니까?\""], ["cried Morrel, “when I left her dying?”", "모렐이 외쳤다. \"제가 떠날 때 그녀는 죽어 가고 있었는데요?\""]], [["Monte Cristo pressed his hands to his forehead.", "몬테크리스토가 두 손으로 이마를 짚었다."], ["What was passing in that brain, so loaded with dreadful secrets?", "그토록 무서운 비밀들로 가득 찬 저 머릿속에서는 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있었던 걸까?"], ["What does the angel of light or the angel of darkness say to that mind, at once implacable and generous? God only knows.", "무자비하면서도 너그러운 저 마음에게, 빛의 천사 혹은 어둠의 천사는 무어라 말하고 있었을까? 그것은 오직 하느님만이 아신다."]], [["Monte Cristo raised his head once more, and this time he was calm as a child awaking from its sleep.", "몬테크리스토가 다시 한번 고개를 들었다. 이번엔 잠에서 깨어난 아이처럼 평온했다."]], [["“Maximilian,”", "\"막시밀리앙,\""], ["said he, “return home.", "그가 말했다. \"집으로 돌아가시오."], ["I command you not to stir—attempt nothing, not to let your countenance betray a thought, and I will send you tidings. Go.”", "명하노니 꼼짝도 하지 말고, 아무것도 시도하지 말며, 얼굴에 어떤 생각도 드러내지 마시오. 그러면 내가 소식을 전하겠소. 가시오.\""]], [["“Oh, count, you overwhelm me with that coolness.", "\"오, 백작님, 그 냉정함이 저를 압도합니다."], ["Have you, then, power against death?", "그렇다면 당신은 죽음에 맞설 힘을 가지셨습니까?"], ["Are you superhuman? Are you an angel?”", "당신은 인간을 넘어선 존재입니까? 천사입니까?\""], ["And the young man, who had never shrunk from danger, shrank before Monte Cristo with indescribable terror.", "그러고는, 단 한 번도 위험 앞에 움츠린 적 없던 청년이, 몬테크리스토 앞에서 형언할 수 없는 공포에 사로잡혀 움츠러들었다."], ["But Monte Cristo looked at him with so melancholy and sweet a smile, that Maximilian felt the tears filling his eyes.", "하지만 몬테크리스토가 어찌나 우수에 차고도 다정한 미소로 그를 바라보았던지, 막시밀리앙은 눈에 눈물이 차오르는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“I can do much for you, my friend,”", "\"나는 당신을 위해 많은 것을 할 수 있소, 벗이여.\""], ["replied the count.", "백작이 대답했다. \"가시오."], ["“Go; I must be alone.”", "나는 혼자 있어야 하오.\""]], [["Morrel, subdued by the extraordinary ascendancy Monte Cristo exercised over everything around him, did not endeavor to resist it.", "모렐은 몬테크리스토가 주위의 모든 것에 행사하는 그 비범한 위력에 굴복하여, 거기에 저항하려 들지 않았다."], ["He pressed the count’s hand and left.", "그는 백작의 손을 꼭 쥐고 떠났다."], ["He stopped one moment at the door for Baptistin, whom he saw in the Rue Matignon, and who was running.", "문간에서 잠시 멈추어, 마티뇽 거리에서 달려오는 바티스탱을 기다렸다."]], [["Meanwhile, Villefort and d’Avrigny had made all possible haste, Valentine had not revived from her fainting fit on their arrival, and the doctor examined the invalid with all the care the circumstances demanded, and with an interest which the knowledge of the secret intensified twofold.", "한편 빌포르와 다브리니는 있는 힘껏 서둘렀다. 두 사람이 도착했을 때 발랑틴은 기절에서 깨어나지 못한 상태였다. 의사는 상황이 요구하는 온갖 주의를 기울여, 또 비밀을 알고 있기에 두 배로 깊어진 관심으로 환자를 살폈다."], ["Villefort, closely watching his countenance and his lips, awaited the result of the examination.", "빌포르는 그의 얼굴과 입술을 뚫어지게 지켜보며 진찰의 결과를 기다렸다."], ["Noirtier, paler than even the young girl, more eager than Villefort for the decision, was watching also intently and affectionately.", "누아르티에는 그 소녀보다도 더 창백한 얼굴로, 빌포르보다도 더 간절하게 판단을 기다리며, 역시 골똘하고 애틋하게 지켜보고 있었다."]], [["At last d’Avrigny slowly uttered these words: “She is still alive!”", "마침내 다브리니가 천천히 이렇게 말했다. \"아직 살아 있습니다!\""]], [["“Still?”", "\"아직이라니?\""], ["cried Villefort; “oh, doctor, what a dreadful word is that.”", "빌포르가 외쳤다. \"오, 선생, 그 얼마나 무서운 말이오.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇소,\""], ["said the physician, “I repeat it; she is still alive, and I am astonished at it.”", "의사가 말했다. \"다시 말하지만, 아직 살아 있고, 나는 그 점에 놀라고 있소.\""]], [["“But is she safe?”", "\"하지만 무사하겠소?\""], ["asked the father.", "아버지가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, since she lives.”", "\"그렇소, 살아 있으니 말이오.\""]], [["At that moment d’Avrigny’s glance met Noirtier’s eye.", "그 순간 다브리니의 시선이 누아르티에의 눈과 마주쳤다."], ["It glistened with such extraordinary joy, so rich and full of thought, that the physician was struck.", "그 눈은 어찌나 비범한 기쁨으로, 어찌나 풍부하고 사려 깊게 빛나던지, 의사가 깜짝 놀랐다. 그는 소녀를 다시 안락의자에 앉혔다."], ["He placed the young girl again on the chair,—her lips were scarcely discernible, they were so pale and white, as well as her whole face,—and remained motionless, looking at Noirtier, who appeared to anticipate and commend all he did.", "그녀의 입술은 얼굴 전체와 마찬가지로 어찌나 핏기 없이 희던지 거의 분간되지 않을 정도였다. 그는 꼼짝 않고 누아르티에를 바라보았다. 노인은 의사가 하는 모든 일을 미리 내다보고 또 칭찬하는 듯했다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"선생,\""], ["said d’Avrigny to Villefort, “call Mademoiselle Valentine’s maid, if you please.”", "다브리니가 빌포르에게 말했다. \"괜찮으시다면, 발랑틴 양의 하녀를 불러 주시지요.\""]], [["Villefort went himself to find her; and d’Avrigny approached Noirtier.", "빌포르가 직접 하녀를 찾으러 갔고, 다브리니는 누아르티에에게 다가갔다."]], [["“Have you something to tell me?” asked he.", "\"제게 하실 말씀이 있습니까?\" 그가 물었다."], ["The old man winked his eyes expressively, which we may remember was his only way of expressing his approval.", "노인이 눈을 의미심장하게 깜빡였다. 기억하다시피 그것은 그가 동의를 표하는 유일한 방법이었다."]], [["“Privately?”", "\"단둘이서요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["“Well, I will remain with you.”", "\"좋습니다, 제가 당신 곁에 남겠습니다.\""], ["At this moment Villefort returned, followed by the lady’s maid; and after her came Madame de Villefort.", "바로 그때 빌포르가 하녀를 데리고 돌아왔다. 그리고 그 뒤로 빌포르 부인이 들어왔다."]], [["“What is the matter, then, with this dear child?", "\"대체 이 사랑스러운 아이에게 무슨 일이 생긴 거예요?"], ["she has just left me, and she complained of being indisposed, but I did not think seriously of it.”", "방금 저를 떠날 때 몸이 좀 불편하다고 하소연하긴 했지만, 저는 대수롭지 않게 여겼는데요.\""]], [["The young woman with tears in her eyes and every mark of affection of a true mother, approached Valentine and took her hand.", "젊은 부인은 눈에 눈물을 글썽이며, 참된 어머니의 온갖 애정의 표시를 담아 발랑틴에게 다가가 그녀의 손을 잡았다."], ["D’Avrigny continued to look at Noirtier; he saw the eyes of the old man dilate and become round, his cheeks turn pale and tremble; the perspiration stood in drops upon his forehead.", "다브리니는 계속 누아르티에를 바라보았다. 노인의 두 눈이 커지고 동그래지며, 두 뺨이 창백해지고 떨리며, 이마에 땀방울이 맺히는 것을 보았다."]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["said he, involuntarily following Noirtier’s eyes, which were fixed on Madame de Villefort, who repeated:", "그는 무심결에 누아르티에의 눈을 좇으며 말했다. 그 눈은 빌포르 부인에게 못 박혀 있었다. 부인이 거듭 말하고 있었다."]], [["“This poor child would be better in bed.", "\"이 가엾은 아이는 침대에 눕히는 게 좋겠어요."], ["Come, Fanny, we will put her to bed.”", "자, 파니, 우리가 침대로 옮기자.\""]], [["M. d’Avrigny, who saw that would be a means of his remaining alone with Noirtier, expressed his opinion that it was the best thing that could be done; but he forbade that anything should be given to her except what he ordered.", "다브리니는 그렇게 하면 누아르티에와 단둘이 남을 수 있겠다 싶어, 그것이 할 수 있는 가장 좋은 방법이라는 의견을 밝혔다. 다만 자신이 지시한 것 외에는 그녀에게 아무것도 주어서는 안 된다고 못 박았다."]], [["They carried Valentine away; she had revived, but could scarcely move or speak, so shaken was her frame by the attack.", "사람들이 발랑틴을 옮겨 갔다. 그녀는 정신이 돌아왔지만, 발작에 온몸이 어찌나 흔들렸던지 거의 움직이지도 말하지도 못했다."], ["She had, however, just power to give one parting look to her grandfather, who in losing her seemed to be resigning his very soul.", "그래도 할아버지에게 작별의 눈길 한 번을 보낼 힘은 남아 있었다. 노인은 손녀를 떠나보내며 제 영혼마저 내놓는 듯했다."], ["D’Avrigny followed the invalid, wrote a prescription, ordered Villefort to take a cabriolet, go in person to a chemist’s to get the prescribed medicine, bring it himself, and wait for him in his daughter’s room.", "다브리니는 환자를 따라가, 처방전을 쓰고, 빌포르에게 직접 삯마차를 타고 약방에 가서 처방한 약을 받아 손수 가져온 다음 딸의 방에서 자신을 기다리라고 일렀다."], ["Then, having renewed his injunction not to give Valentine anything, he went down again to Noirtier, shut the doors carefully, and after convincing himself that no one was listening:", "그러고는 발랑틴에게 아무것도 주지 말라는 당부를 거듭한 뒤, 다시 누아르티에에게로 내려가 문을 조심스레 닫았다. 그리고 아무도 엿듣지 않는다는 것을 확인한 뒤에 말했다."]], [["“Do you,” said he, “know anything of this young lady’s illness?”", "\"이 아가씨의 병에 대해 무언가 아시는 게 있습니까?\""]], [["“Yes,” said the old man.", "\"그렇소.\" 노인이 말했다."]], [["“We have no time to lose; I will question, and do you answer me.”", "\"우리에겐 허비할 시간이 없습니다. 제가 묻겠으니, 답해 주십시오.\""], ["Noirtier made a sign that he was ready to answer.", "누아르티에가 답할 준비가 되었다는 신호를 보냈다."], ["“Did you anticipate the accident which has happened to your granddaughter?”", "\"손녀에게 일어난 이 변고를 미리 예상하셨습니까?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇소.\""], ["D’Avrigny reflected a moment; then approaching Noirtier:", "다브리니가 잠시 생각에 잠겼다. 그러고는 누아르티에에게 다가갔다."]], [["“Pardon what I am going to say,”", "\"제가 드릴 말씀을 용서하십시오.\""], ["added he, “but no indication should be neglected in this terrible situation.", "그가 덧붙였다. \"하지만 이 끔찍한 상황에서는 어떤 단서도 놓쳐서는 안 됩니다."], ["Did you see poor Barrois die?”", "가엾은 바루아가 죽는 것을 보셨지요?\""], ["Noirtier raised his eyes to heaven.", "누아르티에가 하늘을 향해 눈을 들었다."]], [["“Do you know of what he died!” asked d’Avrigny, placing his hand on Noirtier’s shoulder.", "\"그가 무엇 때문에 죽었는지 아십니까?\" 다브리니가 누아르티에의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 물었다."]], [["“Yes,” replied the old man.", "\"그렇소.\" 노인이 대답했다."]], [["“Do you think he died a natural death?”", "\"그가 자연사했다고 생각하십니까?\""], ["A sort of smile was discernible on the motionless lips of Noirtier.", "누아르티에의 굳어 버린 입술에 일종의 미소 같은 것이 어렸다."]], [["“Then you have thought that Barrois was poisoned?”", "\"그러니까 바루아가 독살되었다고 생각하셨군요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["“Do you think the poison he fell a victim to was intended for him?”", "\"그가 희생된 그 독약이 그를 노린 것이었다고 생각하십니까?\" \"아니오.\""]], [["“No.” “Do you think the same hand which unintentionally struck Barrois has now attacked Valentine?”", "\"바루아를 본의 아니게 친 바로 그 손이 이제 발랑틴을 공격했다고 생각하십니까?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["“Then will she die too?”", "\"그렇다면 그녀도 죽겠습니까?\""], ["asked d’Avrigny, fixing his penetrating gaze on Noirtier.", "다브리니가 꿰뚫는 듯한 시선을 누아르티에에게 못 박으며 물었다."], ["He watched the effect of this question on the old man.", "그는 이 질문이 노인에게 미치는 영향을 지켜보았다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니오.\""], ["replied he with an air of triumph which would have puzzled the most clever diviner.", "그가 대답했다. 그 의기양양한 기색은 제아무리 영리한 점쟁이라도 어리둥절하게 했을 것이다."]], [["“Then you hope?”", "\"그렇다면 희망을 품으시는 겁니까?\""], ["said d’Avrigny, with surprise. “Yes.”", "다브리니가 놀라며 말했다."]], [["“What do you hope?”", "\"그렇소.\""], ["The old man made him understand with his eyes that he could not answer.", "\"무엇을 희망하십니까?\" 노인이 답할 수 없다는 것을 눈으로 알렸다."]], [["“Ah, yes, it is true,”", "\"아, 그렇지, 맞습니다.\""], ["murmured d’Avrigny.", "다브리니가 중얼거렸다."], ["Then, turning to Noirtier,—“Do you hope the assassin will be tried?”", "그러고는 누아르티에를 향해 말했다. \"살인자가 재판받기를 희망하십니까?\" \"아니오.\""]], [["“No.” “Then you hope the poison will take no effect on Valentine?”", "\"그렇다면 그 독약이 발랑틴에게 효력을 미치지 않기를 희망하십니까?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["“It is no news to you,” added d’Avrigny, “to tell you that an attempt has been made to poison her?”", "\"그녀를 독살하려는 시도가 있었다고 말씀드린들,\" 다브리니가 덧붙였다."], ["The old man made a sign that he entertained no doubt upon the subject.", "\"당신께는 새삼스러울 게 없겠지요?\" 노인이 그 점에 대해선 추호의 의심도 없다는 신호를 보냈다."], ["“Then how do you hope Valentine will escape?”", "\"그렇다면 발랑틴이 어떻게 화를 면하리라 희망하시는 겁니까?\""]], [["Noirtier kept his eyes steadfastly fixed on the same spot.", "누아르티에는 한곳에 시선을 굳게 박은 채 떼지 않았다."], ["D’Avrigny followed the direction and saw that they were fixed on a bottle containing the mixture which he took every morning. “Ah, indeed?”", "다브리니가 그 방향을 좇아 보니, 그 시선은 노인이 매일 아침 복용하는 약을 담은 병에 박혀 있었다. \"아, 정말입니까?\""], ["said d’Avrigny, struck with a sudden thought, “has it occurred to you”—Noirtier did not let him finish.", "문득 어떤 생각이 떠올라 다브리니가 말했다. \"혹시 당신은……\" 누아르티에가 그의 말을 끝까지 듣게 두지 않았다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇소.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“To prepare her system to resist poison?” “Yes.”", "\"그녀의 몸이 독약에 저항하도록 만들 생각을 하셨군요?\""]], [["“By accustoming her by degrees——”", "\"그렇소.\" \"조금씩 익숙하게 만들어서……\""]], [["“Yes, yes, yes,” said Noirtier, delighted to be understood.", "\"그렇소, 그렇소, 그렇소.\" 누아르티에가 자기 뜻이 통한 데 기뻐하며 말했다."]], [["“Of course.", "\"물론이지요."], ["I had told you that there was brucine in the mixture I give you.”", "제가 당신께 드리는 약에 브루신이 들어 있다고 말씀드렸으니까요.\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["“And by accustoming her to that poison, you have endeavored to neutralize the effect of a similar poison?” Noirtier’s joy continued.", "\"그래서 그녀를 그 독약에 익숙해지게 함으로써, 비슷한 독약의 효력을 무력화하려 하신 거군요?\" 누아르티에의 기쁨이 가시지 않았다."], ["“And you have succeeded,”", "\"그리고 성공하셨습니다.\""], ["exclaimed d’Avrigny.", "다브리니가 외쳤다."], ["“Without that precaution Valentine would have died before assistance could have been procured.", "\"그 대비책이 없었다면 발랑틴은 도움을 구하기도 전에 죽었을 겁니다."], ["The dose has been excessive, but she has only been shaken by it; and this time, at any rate, Valentine will not die.”", "약의 양이 지나치게 많긴 했지만, 그녀는 그것에 흔들렸을 뿐입니다. 적어도 이번에는, 발랑틴은 죽지 않을 겁니다.\""]], [["A superhuman joy expanded the old man’s eyes, which were raised towards heaven with an expression of infinite gratitude.", "인간을 넘어선 기쁨이 노인의 두 눈을 크게 넓혔다. 그 눈은 무한한 감사의 빛을 담고 하늘을 향해 들렸다."], ["At this moment Villefort returned.", "바로 그때 빌포르가 돌아왔다."]], [["“Here, doctor,”", "\"자, 선생,\""], ["said he, “is what you sent me for.”", "그가 말했다. \"심부름시키신 것을 가져왔소.\""]], [["“Was this prepared in your presence?”", "\"이게 당신이 보는 앞에서 조제된 거요?\""]], [["“Yes,” replied the procureur.", "\"그렇소.\" 검사가 대답했다."]], [["“Have you not let it go out of your hands?”", "\"당신 손에서 떠난 적은 없었고요?\" \"없었소.\""]], [["“No.” D’Avrigny took the bottle, poured some drops of the mixture it contained in the hollow of his hand, and swallowed them.", "다브리니가 병을 받아, 그 안에 든 약을 몇 방울 손바닥에 따라 삼켰다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"좋소,\""], ["said he, “let us go to Valentine; I will give instructions to everyone, and you, M.", "그가 말했다. \"발랑틴에게 갑시다. 내가 모두에게 지시를 내리겠소."], ["de Villefort, will yourself see that no one deviates from them.”", "그리고 빌포르 씨, 당신은 누구도 그 지시에서 어긋나지 않도록 직접 살펴 주시오.\""]], [["40294m\nAt the moment when d’Avrigny was returning to Valentine’s room, accompanied by Villefort, an Italian priest, of serious demeanor and calm and firm tone, hired for his use the house adjoining the hotel of M.", "다브리니가 빌포르를 데리고 발랑틴의 방으로 돌아가던 바로 그 순간, 진지한 거동에 차분하고 단호한 어조를 지닌 한 이탈리아인 사제가 빌포르 씨의 저택에 잇닿은 집을 빌려 자기 거처로 삼았다."], ["de Villefort. No one knew how the three former tenants of that house left it.", "그 집의 이전 세입자 세 사람이 어떻게 그 집을 떠났는지는 아무도 몰랐다."], ["About two hours afterwards its foundation was reported to be unsafe; but the report did not prevent the new occupant establishing himself there with his modest furniture the same day at five o’clock.", "두 시간쯤 뒤에 그 집의 기초가 부실하다는 소문이 돌았다. 하지만 그 소문도 새 세입자가 바로 그날 다섯 시에 변변찮은 가구를 들이고 그곳에 자리 잡는 것을 막지는 못했다."], ["The lease was drawn up for three, six, or nine years by the new tenant, who, according to the rule of the proprietor, paid six months in advance.", "임대 계약서는 새 세입자가 삼 년, 육 년, 혹은 구 년으로 작성했고, 집주인의 관례에 따라 여섯 달 치를 선불로 냈다."]], [["This new tenant, who, as we have said, was an Italian, was called Il Signor Giacomo Busoni.", "이 새 세입자는, 앞서 말했듯이 이탈리아인으로, 일 시뇨르 자코모 부소니라 불렸다."], ["Workmen were immediately called in, and that same night the passengers at the end of the faubourg saw with surprise that carpenters and masons were occupied in repairing the lower part of the tottering house.", "곧바로 일꾼들이 불려 왔고, 바로 그날 밤 변두리 끝을 지나던 행인들은 목수와 미장이들이 무너져 가는 그 집의 아래층을 수리하느라 분주한 것을 놀란 눈으로 바라보았다."]], [["# Chapter 95. Father and Daughter", "# Chapter 95. Father and Daughter"]], [["We saw in a preceding chapter how Madame Danglars went formally to announce to Madame de Villefort the approaching marriage of Eugénie Danglars and M. Andrea Cavalcanti.", "앞 장에서 우리는 당글라르 부인이 격식을 갖추어 빌포르 부인을 찾아가 외제니 당글라르와 안드레아 카발칸티의 머지않은 결혼을 알리는 장면을 보았다."], ["This formal announcement, which implied or appeared to imply, the approval of all the persons concerned in this momentous affair, had been preceded by a scene to which our readers must be admitted.", "이 중대한 일에 관련된 모든 사람들의 동의를 함축하거나 함축하는 듯한 이 격식 있는 통고에는, 우리 독자들이 반드시 보아야 할 한 장면이 앞서 있었다."], ["We beg them to take one step backward, and to transport themselves, the morning of that day of great catastrophes, into the showy, gilded salon we have before shown them, and which was the pride of its owner, Baron Danglars.", "독자들이 한 걸음 뒤로 물러나, 그 큰 재앙들이 닥친 날 아침으로 시간을 돌려, 앞서 보여 드린 바 있는 그 화려하고 금빛으로 치장된 살롱, 곧 그 주인인 당글라르 남작의 자랑거리인 방으로 함께 가 주기를 청한다."]], [["In this room, at about ten o’clock in the morning, the banker himself had been walking to and fro for some minutes thoughtfully and in evident uneasiness, watching both doors, and listening to every sound.", "이 방에서, 아침 열 시쯤, 은행가 본인이 몇 분째 생각에 잠긴 채, 두 개의 문을 번갈아 지켜보고 작은 소리 하나하나에 귀를 기울이며, 역력한 불안에 휩싸여 이리저리 거닐고 있었다."], ["When his patience was exhausted, he called his valet.", "인내심이 바닥나자 그는 시종을 불렀다."]], [["“Étienne,”", "\"에티엔,\""], ["said he, “see why Mademoiselle Eugénie has asked me to meet her in the drawing-room, and why she makes me wait so long.”", "그가 말했다. \"외제니 아가씨가 왜 나더러 응접실로 오라 했는지, 그리고 왜 이렇게 오래 기다리게 하는지 알아보고 오게.\""]], [["Having given this vent to his ill-humor, the baron became more calm; Mademoiselle Danglars had that morning requested an interview with her father, and had fixed on the gilded drawing-room as the spot.", "이렇게 언짢은 심기를 한 차례 토해 내고 나니 남작은 한결 차분해졌다. 그날 아침 당글라르 양은 아버지에게 면담을 청하면서 그 금빛 응접실을 만남의 장소로 정해 두었다."], ["The singularity of this step, and above all its formality, had not a little surprised the banker, who had immediately obeyed his daughter by repairing first to the drawing-room.", "이 일의 별스러움과, 무엇보다 그 격식이 은행가를 적잖이 놀라게 했고, 그는 즉시 딸의 뜻을 따라 먼저 응접실로 발걸음을 옮겼던 것이다."], ["Étienne soon returned from his errand.", "에티엔은 곧 심부름을 마치고 돌아왔다."]], [["“Mademoiselle’s lady’s maid says, sir, that mademoiselle is finishing her toilette, and will be here shortly.”", "\"아가씨의 하녀가 말하기를, 나리, 아가씨가 지금 단장을 마무리하고 계시니 곧 이리로 오실 거랍니다.\""]], [["Danglars nodded, to signify that he was satisfied.", "당글라르는 만족한다는 뜻으로 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["To the world and to his servants Danglars assumed the character of the good-natured man and the indulgent father.", "세상 사람들과 하인들 앞에서 당글라르는 사람 좋고 너그러운 아버지의 인물을 연기했다."], ["This was one of his parts in the popular comedy he was performing,—a make-up he had adopted and which suited him about as well as the masks worn on the classic stage by paternal actors, who seen from one side, were the image of geniality, and from the other showed lips drawn down in chronic ill-temper.", "이것은 그가 펼치는 통속 희극에서 그가 맡은 배역 가운데 하나였고, 그가 골라 쓴 분장이었다. 그것은 고전 무대에서 아버지 역을 맡은 배우들이 쓰던 가면과 꼭 닮아 그에게 잘 어울렸다. 그 가면은 한쪽에서 보면 다정함 그 자체였지만, 다른 쪽에서 보면 입꼬리가 만성적인 심술로 아래로 처져 있었다."], ["Let us hasten to say that in private the genial side descended to the level of the other, so that generally the indulgent man disappeared to give place to the brutal husband and domineering father.", "그러나 서둘러 말해 두자면, 사사로운 자리에서는 그 다정한 쪽이 다른 쪽 수준으로 떨어져 버려서, 대개는 너그러운 사내가 사라지고 그 자리를 난폭한 남편이자 군림하는 아버지가 차지했다."]], [["“Why the devil does that foolish girl, who pretends to wish to speak to me, not come into my study?", "\"나하고 이야기를 하고 싶다는 그 어리석은 계집애가 도대체 왜 내 서재로 오지 않는 거야?"], ["and why on earth does she want to speak to me at all?”", "게다가 도대체 무슨 할 말이 있다는 거지?\""]], [["He was turning this thought over in his brain for the twentieth time, when the door opened and Eugénie appeared, attired in a figured black satin dress, her hair dressed and gloves on, as if she were going to the Italian Opera.", "이 생각을 머릿속에서 스무 번째 곱씹고 있을 때 문이 열리며 외제니가 모습을 드러냈다. 그녀는 무늬가 들어간 검은 새틴 드레스를 입고 머리를 매만지고 장갑을 낀 채, 마치 이탈리아 오페라극장에라도 가려는 듯한 차림이었다."]], [["“Well, Eugénie, what is it you want with me?", "\"그래, 외제니, 내게 무슨 볼일이냐?"], ["and why in this solemn drawing-room when the study is so comfortable?”", "게다가 서재가 그렇게 편한데 어째서 이 엄숙한 응접실이냐?\""]], [["“I quite understand why you ask, sir,”", "\"왜 그렇게 물으시는지 잘 압니다, 아버지.\""], ["said Eugénie, making a sign that her father might be seated, “and in fact your two questions suggest fully the theme of our conversation.", "외제니가 아버지에게 앉으라는 손짓을 하며 말했다. \"사실 두 질문이 우리 대화의 주제를 고스란히 짚어 주고 있네요."], ["I will answer them both, and contrary to the usual method, the last first, because it is the least difficult.", "두 가지 다 답해 드리겠습니다. 다만 보통과는 거꾸로, 가장 어렵지 않은 것부터, 곧 마지막 질문부터 답하지요."], ["I have chosen the drawing-room, sir, as our place of meeting, in order to avoid the disagreeable impressions and influences of a banker’s study.", "제가 응접실을 만남의 장소로 고른 것은 은행가의 서재가 주는 불쾌한 인상과 영향을 피하기 위해서입니다."], ["Those gilded cashbooks, drawers locked like gates of fortresses, heaps of bank-bills, come from I know not where, and the quantities of letters from England, Holland, Spain, India, China, and Peru, have generally a strange influence on a father’s mind, and make him forget that there is in the world an interest greater and more sacred than the good opinion of his correspondents.", "그 금박을 입힌 출납 장부들, 요새의 성문처럼 잠긴 서랍들, 어디서 온 것인지 알 수도 없는 산더미 같은 어음들, 그리고 영국, 네덜란드, 스페인, 인도, 중국, 페루에서 온 무더기 편지들은 대개 아버지의 마음에 묘한 영향을 끼쳐, 세상에는 거래처 사람들의 호평보다 더 크고 더 신성한 관심사가 있다는 것을 잊게 만들지요. 그래서 저는 이 응접실을 골랐습니다."], ["I have, therefore, chosen this drawing-room, where you see, smiling and happy in their magnificent frames, your portrait, mine, my mother’s, and all sorts of rural landscapes and touching pastorals.", "보시다시피 여기에는 호화로운 액자 속에서 미소 짓고 행복해하는 아버지의 초상, 제 초상, 어머니의 초상, 그리고 온갖 전원 풍경과 마음을 어루만지는 목가들이 걸려 있으니까요."], ["I rely much on external impressions; perhaps, with regard to you, they are immaterial, but I should be no artist if I had not some fancies.”", "저는 외적인 인상을 무척 중시합니다. 아버지께는 어쩌면 그런 것이 아무것도 아닐 수 있지만, 제게 이런 취향이 없다면 저는 예술가라 할 수 없겠지요.\""]], [["“Very well,” replied M.", "\"아주 좋아.\""], ["Danglars, who had listened to all this preamble with imperturbable coolness, but without understanding a word, since like every man burdened with thoughts of the past, he was occupied with seeking the thread of his own ideas in those of the speaker.", "당글라르가 대꾸했다. 그는 이 모든 서두를 흔들림 없는 냉정함으로 들었지만 한마디도 알아듣지 못했다. 지난 일들에 짓눌린 사람이 다 그렇듯, 그는 말하는 사람의 생각 속에서 제 자신의 생각의 실마리를 찾느라 정신이 팔려 있었던 것이다."]], [["“There is, then, the second point cleared up, or nearly so,”", "\"그러니 둘째 문제는 풀린 셈이지요, 아니면 거의 풀린 셈이고요.\""], ["said Eugénie, without the least confusion, and with that masculine pointedness which distinguished her gesture and her language; “and you appear satisfied with the explanation.", "외제니가 조금도 당황하지 않고, 몸짓과 말씨에서 그녀를 돋보이게 하는 그 남자다운 날카로움으로 말했다. \"아버지도 그 설명에 만족하신 듯하군요."], ["Now, let us return to the first.", "이제 첫째 문제로 돌아가지요."], ["You ask me why I have requested this interview; I will tell you in two words, sir; I will not marry count Andrea Cavalcanti.”", "제가 왜 이 면담을 청했는지 물으셨지요. 두 마디로 말씀드리겠습니다, 아버지. 저는 안드레아 카발칸티 백작과 결혼하지 않겠어요.\""]], [["Danglars leaped from his chair and raised his eyes and arms towards heaven.", "당글라르가 의자에서 펄쩍 뛰어 일어나 두 눈과 두 팔을 하늘을 향해 쳐들었다."]], [["40298m\n“Yes, indeed, sir,”", "\"네, 정말입니다, 아버지.\""], ["continued Eugénie, still quite calm; “you are astonished, I see; for since this little affair began, I have not manifested the slightest opposition, and yet I am always sure, when the opportunity arrives, to oppose a determined and absolute will to people who have not consulted me, and things which displease me.", "외제니가 여전히 아주 침착하게 말을 이었다. \"놀라셨군요, 알겠어요. 이 작은 일이 시작된 이래 저는 조금도 반대하는 기색을 보이지 않았으니까요."], ["However, this time, my tranquillity, or passiveness as philosophers say, proceeded from another source; it proceeded from a wish, like a submissive and devoted daughter”", "하지만 저는 늘, 때가 오면, 저와 의논하지 않은 사람들과 제 마음에 들지 않는 일들에 맞서 단호하고 절대적인 의지를 내세우게 마련입니다. 그렇지만 이번에 제가 보인 평온함, 철학자들이 말하는 수동성은 다른 데서 비롯된 것이었어요."], ["(a slight smile was observable on the purple lips of the young girl), “to practice obedience.”", "그것은 순종하고 헌신하는 딸처럼\"—젊은 처녀의 자줏빛 입술에 옅은 미소가 스쳤다—\"순종을 연습해 보고 싶다는 바람에서 나온 것이었지요.\""]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서?\""], ["asked Danglars.", "당글라르가 물었다."]], [["“Well, sir,”", "\"그래서요, 아버지.\""], ["replied Eugénie, “I have tried to the very last and now that the moment has come, I feel in spite of all my efforts that it is impossible.”", "외제니가 대답했다. \"끝까지 애써 봤습니다. 그리고 이제 그 순간이 닥치고 보니, 제 온갖 노력에도 불구하고 그것이 불가능하다는 걸 느끼겠어요.\""]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["said Danglars, whose weak mind was at first quite overwhelmed with the weight of this pitiless logic, marking evident premeditation and force of will, “what is your reason for this refusal, Eugénie?", "당글라르가 말했다. 그의 나약한 정신은 처음에는 이 무자비한 논리의 무게에 완전히 짓눌렸는데, 그 논리에는 명백한 사전 계획과 의지의 힘이 배어 있었다. \"그렇게 거절하는 이유가 무엇이냐, 외제니?"], ["what reason do you assign?”", "무슨 까닭을 대겠다는 거냐?\""]], [["“My reason?” replied the young girl.", "\"이유 말입니까?\" 젊은 처녀가 대답했다."], ["“Well, it is not that the man is more ugly, more foolish, or more disagreeable than any other; no, M.", "\"글쎄요, 그 사람이 다른 누구보다 더 못생겼거나, 더 어리석거나, 더 불쾌해서는 아닙니다."], ["Andrea Cavalcanti may appear to those who look at men’s faces and figures as a very good specimen of his kind.", "아니에요, 안드레아 카발칸티 씨는 사람의 얼굴과 풍채를 보는 이들에게는 자기 부류의 아주 훌륭한 표본으로 보일 수 있겠지요."], ["It is not, either, that my heart is less touched by him than any other; that would be a schoolgirl’s reason, which I consider quite beneath me.", "또 제 마음이 다른 누구보다 그 사람에게 덜 끌려서도 아니에요. 그건 여학생이나 댈 법한 이유이고, 저는 그런 걸 저 자신보다 한참 아래로 봅니다."], ["I actually love no one, sir; you know it, do you not?", "사실 저는 아무도 사랑하지 않아요, 아버지."], ["I do not then see why, without real necessity, I should encumber my life with a perpetual companion.", "아시잖아요, 그렇지요? 그러니 진짜 필요도 없는데 어째서 제 인생을 평생의 동반자로 짐 지워야 하는지 모르겠어요."], ["Has not some sage said, ‘Nothing too much’?", "어느 현인이 '무엇이든 지나치지 말라'고 하지 않았던가요?"], ["and another, ‘I carry all my effects with me’?", "또 다른 이는 '내 가진 것은 모두 몸에 지니고 다닌다'고 했고요."], ["I have been taught these two aphorisms in Latin and in Greek; one is, I believe, from Phædrus, and the other from Bias.", "저는 이 두 격언을 라틴어와 그리스어로 배웠어요. 하나는 파이드루스의 말이고, 다른 하나는 비아스의 말로 알고 있습니다."], ["Well, my dear father, in the shipwreck of life—for life is an eternal shipwreck of our hopes—I cast into the sea my useless encumbrance, that is all, and I remain with my own will, disposed to live perfectly alone, and consequently perfectly free.”", "자, 사랑하는 아버지, 인생이라는 난파 속에서—인생이란 우리 희망의 영원한 난파니까요—저는 쓸데없는 짐을 바다에 던져 버립니다. 그게 전부예요. 그리고 저는 제 의지만을 지닌 채, 완벽하게 혼자, 따라서 완벽하게 자유롭게 살 작정입니다.\""]], [["“Unhappy girl, unhappy girl!”", "\"가엾은 것, 가엾은 것!\""], ["murmured Danglars, turning pale, for he knew from long experience the solidity of the obstacle he had so suddenly encountered.", "당글라르가 창백해지며 중얼거렸다. 오랜 경험으로 그는 자신이 이토록 느닷없이 맞닥뜨린 장애물이 얼마나 단단한지 알고 있었던 것이다."]], [["“Unhappy girl,”", "\"가엾은 것이라고요.\""], ["replied Eugénie, “unhappy girl, do you say, sir?", "외제니가 대꾸했다. \"가엾은 것이라 하셨습니까, 아버지? 천만에요."], ["No, indeed; the exclamation appears quite theatrical and affected.", "그 탄식은 무척 연극조에다 꾸민 티가 나는군요."], ["Happy, on the contrary, for what am I in want of?", "오히려 행복하지요. 제게 부족한 게 무엇입니까?"], ["The world calls me beautiful.", "세상은 저더러 아름답다고 합니다."], ["It is something to be well received.", "환대받는다는 건 좋은 일이에요."], ["I like a favorable reception; it expands the countenance, and those around me do not then appear so ugly.", "저는 호의 어린 대접이 좋아요. 그러면 얼굴이 환해지고, 제 주변 사람들도 그리 흉해 보이지 않으니까요."], ["I possess a share of wit, and a certain relative sensibility, which enables me to draw from life in general, for the support of mine, all I meet with that is good, like the monkey who cracks the nut to get at its contents.", "저는 약간의 재치와, 어느 정도의 상대적인 감수성을 지니고 있어서, 호두를 깨서 알맹이를 빼 먹는 원숭이처럼, 제 삶을 떠받치기 위해 인생 전반에서 마주치는 좋은 것을 모조리 끌어낼 수 있어요."], ["I am rich, for you have one of the first fortunes in France.", "저는 부자입니다. 아버지가 프랑스에서 손꼽히는 재산가시니까요."], ["I am your only daughter, and you are not so exacting as the fathers of the Porte Saint-Martin and Gaîté, who disinherit their daughters for not giving them grandchildren.", "저는 외동딸이고, 아버지는 손주를 안겨 주지 않는다고 딸의 상속권을 박탈해 버리는 포르트생마르탱이나 게테 극장의 아버지들처럼 까다롭지도 않으시지요."], ["Besides, the provident law has deprived you of the power to disinherit me, at least entirely, as it has also of the power to compel me to marry Monsieur This or Monsieur That.", "게다가 선견지명 있는 법이 아버지에게서 저를 적어도 완전히는 폐적할 권리를 빼앗아 두었고, 마찬가지로 저를 이 사람 저 사람과 강제로 결혼시킬 권리도 빼앗아 두었지요."], ["And so—being, beautiful, witty, somewhat talented, as the comic operas say, and rich—and that is happiness, sir—why do you call me unhappy?”", "그러니—희극 오페라에서들 말하듯, 아름답고, 재치 있고, 어느 정도 재능 있고, 게다가 부자이니—이게 바로 행복이지요, 아버지—어째서 저를 가엾다 하십니까?\""]], [["Danglars, seeing his daughter smiling, and proud even to insolence, could not entirely repress his brutal feelings, but they betrayed themselves only by an exclamation.", "당글라르는 딸이 오만하다 못해 방자하게까지 미소 짓는 것을 보고 난폭한 감정을 완전히 억누르지는 못했지만, 그 감정은 그저 짧은 탄식으로만 새어 나왔다."], ["Under the fixed and inquiring gaze levelled at him from under those beautiful black eyebrows, he prudently turned away, and calmed himself immediately, daunted by the power of a resolute mind.", "저 아름다운 검은 눈썹 아래에서 그를 향해 쏟아지는 붙박인 듯한 캐묻는 시선 앞에서, 그는 신중하게 고개를 돌렸고, 단호한 정신의 위력에 기가 꺾여 곧바로 마음을 가라앉혔다."]], [["“Truly, my daughter,”", "\"정말이지, 내 딸아.\" 그가 미소를 띠며 대답했다. \"너는 네가 뽐내는 그대로의 사람이로구나."], ["replied he with a smile, “you are all you boast of being, excepting one thing; I will not too hastily tell you which, but would rather leave you to guess it.”", "다만 한 가지만 빼고 말이다. 그게 무엇인지 성급히 말해 주지는 않으마. 차라리 네가 알아맞혀 보도록 남겨 두는 편이 낫겠지.\""]], [["Eugénie looked at Danglars, much surprised that one flower of her crown of pride, with which she had so superbly decked herself, should be disputed.", "외제니는 자신이 그토록 당당하게 치장해 둔 오만의 화관에서 꽃 한 송이를 두고 누가 시비를 건다는 사실에 몹시 놀라며 당글라르를 바라보았다."]], [["“My daughter,”", "\"내 딸아,\""], ["continued the banker, “you have perfectly explained to me the sentiments which influence a girl like you, who is determined she will not marry; now it remains for me to tell you the motives of a father like me, who has decided that his daughter shall marry.”", "은행가가 말을 이었다. \"너 같은 처녀, 결혼하지 않겠노라 작정한 처녀를 움직이는 심정이 무엇인지 너는 내게 완벽하게 설명해 주었다. 이제는 나 같은 아버지, 딸을 결혼시키기로 작정한 아버지의 동기를 내가 네게 말할 차례구나.\""]], [["Eugénie bowed, not as a submissive daughter, but as an adversary prepared for a discussion.", "외제니는 고개를 숙였다. 순종하는 딸로서가 아니라, 논쟁을 벌일 채비를 갖춘 적수로서였다."]], [["“My daughter,”", "\"내 딸아,\""], ["continued Danglars, “when a father asks his daughter to choose a husband, he has always some reason for wishing her to marry.", "당글라르가 말을 이었다. \"아버지가 딸에게 남편을 고르라 할 때는, 늘 그 애를 결혼시키고 싶어 하는 무슨 이유가 있는 법이다."], ["Some are affected with the mania of which you spoke just now, that of living again in their grandchildren.", "더러는 네가 방금 말한 그 광증, 곧 손주들 속에서 다시 살아 보겠다는 광증에 사로잡힌 이들도 있지."], ["This is not my weakness, I tell you at once; family joys have no charm for me.", "그건 내 약점이 아니다, 미리 말해 두마. 가정의 즐거움이란 내겐 아무 매력도 없으니까."], ["I may acknowledge this to a daughter whom I know to be philosophical enough to understand my indifference, and not to impute it to me as a crime.”", "내 무관심을 충분히 이해할 만큼, 그리고 그것을 죄로 몰지 않을 만큼 철학적인 딸이라는 걸 아니까, 너에게는 이런 말도 털어놓는 거다.\""]], [["“This is not to the purpose,”", "\"그건 본론이 아니에요.\""], ["said Eugénie; “let us speak candidly, sir; I admire candor.”", "외제니가 말했다. \"솔직하게 말씀하시지요, 아버지. 저는 솔직함을 좋아합니다.\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오,\""], ["said Danglars, “I can, when circumstances render it desirable, adopt your system, although it may not be my general practice.", "당글라르가 말했다. \"상황이 그러길 요구한다면 나도 네 방식을 따를 수 있지. 그게 내 평소 습관은 아니다만."], ["I will therefore proceed.", "그러니 계속하마."], ["I have proposed to you to marry, not for your sake, for indeed I did not think of you in the least at the moment (you admire candor, and will now be satisfied, I hope); but because it suited me to marry you as soon as possible, on account of certain commercial speculations I am desirous of entering into.”", "내가 너에게 결혼하라고 권한 것은 너를 위해서가 아니다. 사실 그 순간 나는 너 생각은 조금도 하지 않았으니까(너는 솔직함을 좋아하니, 이제 흡족하겠지). 그저 내가 벌이고 싶은 어떤 상업적 투기 때문에, 너를 되도록 빨리 결혼시키는 편이 내게 유리했던 거다.\""], ["Eugénie became uneasy.", "외제니가 불안해지기 시작했다."]], [["40302m\n“It is just as I tell you, I assure you, and you must not be angry with me, for you have sought this disclosure.", "\"정말이지 내가 말한 그대로다. 그리고 너는 나한테 화를 내서는 안 돼. 네가 이 고백을 끌어낸 거니까."], ["I do not willingly enter into arithmetical explanations with an artist like you, who fears to enter my study lest she should imbibe disagreeable or anti-poetic impressions and sensations.", "나는 너 같은 예술가와 굳이 산수 계산을 늘어놓고 싶지는 않다. 불쾌하고 시적이지 못한 인상과 감정에 물들까 봐 내 서재에 들어오기조차 꺼리는 예술가와 말이지."], ["But in that same banker’s study, where you very willingly presented yourself yesterday to ask for the thousand francs I give you monthly for pocket-money, you must know, my dear young lady, that many things may be learned, useful even to a girl who will not marry.", "하지만 바로 그 은행가의 서재, 네가 어제는 다달이 용돈으로 주는 천 프랑을 받으러 기꺼이 찾아왔던 그 서재에서는, 사랑하는 아가씨야, 결혼하지 않을 딸에게도 쓸모 있는 많은 것들을 배울 수 있단다. 가령 거기서는 이런 것을 배울 수 있지. 네 예민한 신경을 헤아려, 응접실에서 알려 주마."], ["There one may learn, for instance, what, out of regard to your nervous susceptibility, I will inform you of in the drawing-room, namely, that the credit of a banker is his physical and moral life; that credit sustains him as breath animates the body; and M.", "곧 은행가의 신용이란 그의 육체적이고 정신적인 생명이라는 것, 숨이 몸에 생기를 불어넣듯 신용이 그를 떠받친다는 것 말이다. 몬테크리스토 씨가 언젠가 그 일에 대해 내게 한 차례 강의를 해 주었는데, 나는 그걸 결코 잊은 적이 없다."], ["de Monte Cristo once gave me a lecture on that subject, which I have never forgotten.", "거기서는 또 이런 것을 배울 수 있지. 신용이 가라앉으면 몸은 송장이 되고 만다는 것을."], ["There we may learn that as credit sinks, the body becomes a corpse, and this is what must happen very soon to the banker who is proud to own so good a logician as you for his daughter.”", "그리고 너처럼 훌륭한 논리가를 딸로 둔 것을 자랑스러워하는 이 은행가에게도, 머지않아 바로 그런 일이 닥칠 거다.\""]], [["But Eugénie, instead of stooping, drew herself up under the blow.", "그러나 외제니는 그 일격에 몸을 움츠리기는커녕 오히려 꼿꼿이 일어섰다."], ["“Ruined?”", "\"파산했나요?\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Exactly, my daughter; that is precisely what I mean,”", "\"바로 그렇다, 내 딸아. 내가 말하려던 게 정확히 그거야.\""], ["said Danglars, almost digging his nails into his breast, while he preserved on his harsh features the smile of the heartless though clever man; “ruined—yes, that is it.”", "당글라르가 말했다. 그는 거의 제 가슴팍에 손톱을 박아 넣을 지경이었으나, 그 거친 얼굴에는 무정하면서도 약삭빠른 사내의 미소를 그대로 띠고 있었다. \"파산—그래, 바로 그거다.\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["said Eugénie.", "외제니가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, ruined! Now it is revealed, this secret so full of horror, as the tragic poet says.", "\"그래, 파산이다! 이제 비극 시인의 말처럼, 그토록 끔찍한 이 비밀이 드러났구나."], ["Now, my daughter, learn from my lips how you may alleviate this misfortune, so far as it will affect you.”", "자, 내 딸아, 이 불행이 네게 미치는 한에서나마 어떻게 덜 수 있을지, 내 입으로 일러 주마.\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오,\""], ["cried Eugénie, “you are a bad physiognomist, if you imagine I deplore on my own account the catastrophe of which you warn me.", "외제니가 외쳤다. \"아버지가 제게 경고하시는 그 재앙을 제가 저 자신 때문에 한탄할 거라 여기신다면, 아버지는 관상을 영 못 보시는 분이군요."], ["I ruined? and what will that signify to me?", "제가 파산했다고요? 그게 제게 무슨 의미가 있겠어요?"], ["Have I not my talent left?", "제겐 재능이 남아 있지 않나요?"], ["Can I not, like Pasta, Malibran, Grisi, acquire for myself what you would never have given me, whatever might have been your fortune, a hundred or a hundred and fifty thousand livres per annum, for which I shall be indebted to no one but myself; and which, instead of being given as you gave me those poor twelve thousand francs, with sour looks and reproaches for my prodigality, will be accompanied with acclamations, with bravos, and with flowers?", "저도 파스타나 말리브란이나 그리지처럼, 아버지가 재산이 아무리 많았더라도 제게 결코 주지 않으셨을 것을 제 힘으로 벌어들일 수 있지 않나요? 연 십만, 아니 십오만 리브르를, 저 자신 말고는 누구에게도 신세 지지 않고서 말이에요. 게다가 그 돈은, 아버지가 그 보잘것없는 만 이천 프랑을 시큰둥한 얼굴로 제 낭비를 나무라며 주셨던 것과는 달리, 환호와 갈채와 꽃다발과 함께 따라올 거예요."], ["And if I do not possess that talent, which your smiles prove to me you doubt, should I not still have that ardent love of independence, which will be a substitute for wealth, and which in my mind supersedes even the instinct of self-preservation?", "그리고 설령 제게 그 재능이 없다 해도—아버지의 그 미소를 보니 의심하고 계신 게 분명하지만—제겐 여전히 독립을 향한 저 뜨거운 사랑이 남아 있지 않나요? 그것은 재산을 대신해 줄 것이고, 제 마음속에서는 자기 보존의 본능마저 능가하는 것이지요."], ["No, I grieve not on my own account, I shall always find a resource; my books, my pencils, my piano, all the things which cost but little, and which I shall be able to procure, will remain my own.", "아니에요, 저는 저 자신 때문에 슬퍼하지 않습니다. 저는 언제든 방도를 찾아낼 거예요. 제 책들, 제 그림붓들, 제 피아노, 돈이 거의 들지 않으면서도 제가 마련할 수 있는 그 모든 것들은 여전히 제 것으로 남을 테니까요."]], [["“Do you think that I sorrow for Madame Danglars?", "당글라르 부인 때문에 제가 슬퍼할 거라 생각하세요?"], ["Undeceive yourself again; either I am greatly mistaken, or she has provided against the catastrophe which threatens you, and, which will pass over without affecting her.", "그 또한 착각이세요. 제가 크게 잘못 본 게 아니라면, 어머니는 아버지를 위협하는 그 재앙에 미리 대비를 해 두셨고, 그 재앙은 어머니께는 아무 영향도 미치지 않고 지나갈 거예요."], ["She has taken care for herself,—at least I hope so,—for her attention has not been diverted from her projects by watching over me.", "어머니는 자기 앞가림을 해 두셨어요—적어도 저는 그러길 바라고요—저를 돌보느라 자기 계획에서 주의를 돌리신 적이 없으니까요."], ["She has fostered my independence by professedly indulging my love for liberty.", "어머니는 제 자유에 대한 사랑을 짐짓 너그러이 받아 주는 척하며 제 독립심을 길러 주셨어요."], ["Oh, no, sir; from my childhood I have seen too much, and understood too much, of what has passed around me, for misfortune to have an undue power over me.", "오, 아니에요, 아버지. 어린 시절부터 저는 제 주변에서 벌어진 일을 너무 많이 보았고 너무 많이 이해했어요. 그러니 불행이 제게 분에 넘치는 위력을 떨치지는 못해요."], ["From my earliest recollections, I have been beloved by no one—so much the worse; that has naturally led me to love no one—so much the better—now you have my profession of faith.”", "제 가장 어린 기억부터, 저는 누구의 사랑도 받지 못했어요—그건 더 나쁜 일이지요. 그 탓에 저도 자연스레 아무도 사랑하지 않게 됐고요—그건 더 좋은 일이고요—자, 이제 제 신앙 고백을 들으셨습니다.\""]], [["“Then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["said Danglars, pale with anger, which was not at all due to offended paternal love,—“then, mademoiselle, you persist in your determination to accelerate my ruin?”", "당글라르가 분노로 창백해지며 말했다. 그 분노는 상처받은 부정과는 전혀 무관한 것이었다. \"그렇다면, 아가씨, 너는 내 파산을 앞당기겠다는 그 결심을 굽히지 않겠다는 거냐?\""]], [["“Your ruin? I accelerate your ruin?", "\"파산을 앞당긴다고요? 제가 아버지의 파산을 앞당긴다고요?"], ["What do you mean?", "무슨 말씀이세요?"], ["I do not understand you.”", "무슨 뜻인지 모르겠어요.\""]], [["“So much the better, I have a ray of hope left; listen.”", "\"그렇다면 더 잘됐다. 내겐 아직 한 줄기 희망이 남아 있구나. 들어 보아라.\""]], [["“I am all attention,”", "\"귀를 기울이고 있어요.\""], ["said Eugénie, looking so earnestly at her father that it was an effort for the latter to endure her unrelenting gaze.", "외제니가 어찌나 골똘히 아버지를 바라보았던지, 아버지로서는 그 가차 없는 시선을 견디는 것이 힘겨운 일이었다."]], [["“M. Cavalcanti,”", "\"카발칸티 씨가,\""], ["continued Danglars, “is about to marry you, and will place in my hands his fortune, amounting to three million livres.”", "당글라르가 말을 이었다. \"곧 너와 결혼하면서, 자기 재산을 내 손에 맡길 거다. 삼백만 리브르에 이르는 재산을 말이지.\""]], [["“That is admirable!”", "\"그것 참 훌륭하군요!\""], ["said Eugénie with sovereign contempt, smoothing her gloves out one upon the other.", "외제니가 더없는 경멸을 담아 말하며, 장갑 한 짝을 다른 짝 위로 매만져 폈다."]], [["“You think I shall deprive you of those three millions,”", "\"내가 그 삼백만을 네게서 가로챌 거라 생각하느냐.\""], ["said Danglars; “but do not fear it.", "당글라르가 말했다. \"그건 걱정 마라."], ["They are destined to produce at least ten.", "그 돈은 적어도 천만은 낳을 운명이니까."], ["I and a brother banker have obtained a grant of a railway, the only industrial enterprise which in these days promises to make good the fabulous prospects that Law once held out to the eternally deluded Parisians, in the fantastic Mississippi scheme.", "나와 어느 동료 은행가가 철도 부설권을 따냈다. 요즘 시대에 산업이라 할 만한 사업 가운데, 일찍이 로가 영원히 속아 넘어가는 파리 사람들에게 저 황당한 미시시피 계획으로 내걸었던 그 허황된 전망을 그나마 실현해 줄 유일한 사업이지."], ["As I look at it, a millionth part of a railway is worth fully as much as an acre of waste land on the banks of the Ohio.", "내가 보기에 철도의 백만분의 일은 오하이오 강기슭의 황무지 한 에이커만큼의 값어치는 충분히 한다."], ["We make in our case a deposit, on a mortgage, which is an advance, as you see, since we gain at least ten, fifteen, twenty, or a hundred livres’ worth of iron in exchange for our money.", "우리는 이 경우, 저당을 잡고 보증금을 거는 거다. 알다시피 그건 선금인 셈이지. 우리 돈을 내주는 대가로 적어도 열, 열다섯, 스물, 아니 백 리브르어치의 철을 얻는 거니까."], ["Well, within a week I am to deposit four millions for my share; the four millions, I promise you, will produce ten or twelve.”", "자, 일주일 안에 나는 내 몫으로 사백만을 예치해야 한다. 장담하건대 그 사백만이 천만이나 천이백만을 낳을 거다.\""]], [["“But during my visit to you the day before yesterday, sir, which you appear to recollect so well,”", "\"하지만 그저께 제가 아버지를 찾아갔을 때—아버지도 그날을 아주 잘 기억하시는 듯한데—\""], ["replied Eugénie, “I saw you arranging a deposit—is not that the term?—of five millions and a half; you even pointed it out to me in two drafts on the treasury, and you were astonished that so valuable a paper did not dazzle my eyes like lightning.”", "외제니가 대꾸했다. \"저는 아버지가 예치금을—그게 맞는 말인가요?—오백오십만이나 마련하시는 걸 보았어요. 아버지는 그걸 국고 어음 두 장으로 제게 보여 주시기까지 했고, 그토록 값진 종이가 번개처럼 제 눈을 부시게 하지 않는다고 놀라셨지요.\""]], [["“Yes, but those five millions and a half are not mine, and are only a proof of the great confidence placed in me; my title of popular banker has gained me the confidence of charitable institutions, and the five millions and a half belong to them; at any other time I should not have hesitated to make use of them, but the great losses I have recently sustained are well known, and, as I told you, my credit is rather shaken.", "\"그래, 하지만 그 오백오십만은 내 것이 아니다. 그저 사람들이 내게 얼마나 큰 신뢰를 두고 있는지를 보여 주는 증거일 뿐이지. 평판 좋은 은행가라는 내 명성이 자선 단체들의 신뢰를 얻게 해 주었고, 그 오백오십만은 그들의 돈이다."], ["That deposit may be at any moment withdrawn, and if I had employed it for another purpose, I should bring on me a disgraceful bankruptcy.", "다른 때 같으면 나는 망설임 없이 그걸 써먹었겠지. 하지만 내가 최근에 입은 큰 손실이 다들 잘 알려져 있고, 너에게 말했듯 내 신용이 적잖이 흔들리고 있어."], ["I do not despise bankruptcies, believe me, but they must be those which enrich, not those which ruin. Now, if you marry M.", "그 예치금은 언제든 회수될 수 있고, 만약 내가 그걸 다른 데 써 버렸다면 나는 수치스러운 파산을 자초하게 될 거다. 나는 파산을 업신여기지는 않는다, 믿어 다오."], ["Cavalcanti, and I get the three millions, or even if it is thought I am going to get them, my credit will be restored, and my fortune, which for the last month or two has been swallowed up in gulfs which have been opened in my path by an inconceivable fatality, will revive.", "하지만 그 파산은 사람을 부유하게 하는 것이라야지, 망하게 하는 것이라서는 안 되지. 자, 네가 카발칸티 씨와 결혼하고 내가 그 삼백만을 손에 넣으면, 아니 내가 그걸 손에 넣으리라 사람들이 믿기만 해도, 내 신용은 회복될 거다. 그리고 지난 한두 달 사이 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 운명이 내 앞길에 파 놓은 심연 속으로 삼켜져 버린 내 재산도 되살아날 거야."], ["Do you understand me?”", "무슨 말인지 알겠느냐?\""]], [["“Perfectly; you pledge me for three millions, do you not?”", "\"완벽하게 알겠어요. 저를 삼백만에 저당 잡히겠다는 거지요, 그렇지요?\""]], [["“The greater the amount, the more flattering it is to you; it gives you an idea of your value.”", "\"액수가 클수록 네게는 더 영광스러운 일이지. 네 값어치가 어느 정도인지 가늠하게 해 주니까.\""]], [["“Thank you.", "\"고마워요."], ["One word more, sir; do you promise me to make what use you can of the report of the fortune M. Cavalcanti will bring without touching the money?", "한마디만 더 하지요, 아버지. 카발칸티 씨가 가져올 재산에 관한 소문을, 정작 그 돈에는 손대지 않고 최대한 활용하시겠다고 제게 약속해 주시겠어요?"], ["This is no act of selfishness, but of delicacy.", "이건 이기심에서가 아니라 양심에서 드리는 말씀이에요."], ["I am willing to help rebuild your fortune, but I will not be an accomplice in the ruin of others.”", "저는 기꺼이 아버지의 재산을 다시 일으키는 걸 돕겠지만, 남들을 망하게 하는 데 공범이 되지는 않겠어요.\""]], [["“But since I tell you,”", "\"하지만 내가 말하지 않았느냐,\""], ["cried Danglars, “that with these three million——”", "당글라르가 외쳤다. \"이 삼백만이면—\""]], [["“Do you expect to recover your position, sir, without touching those three million?”", "\"그 삼백만에 손대지 않고도 자리를 회복하실 수 있다고 기대하세요, 아버지?\""]], [["“I hope so, if the marriage should take place and confirm my credit.”", "\"그러길 바란다. 결혼이 성사되어 내 신용을 굳혀 준다면 말이지.\""]], [["“Shall you be able to pay M. Cavalcanti the five hundred thousand francs you promise for my dowry?”", "\"제 지참금으로 약속하신 오십만 프랑은 카발칸티 씨에게 치를 수 있으시고요?\""]], [["“He shall receive them on returning from the mayor’s20.”", "\"그자가 시청에서 돌아오는 길에 받게 될 거다.\""]], [["“Very well!”", "\"좋아요!\""]], [["“What next?", "\"그다음은?"], ["what more do you want?”", "더 무엇을 바라느냐?\""]], [["“I wish to know if, in demanding my signature, you leave me entirely free in my person?”", "\"제 서명을 요구하시면서, 제 몸에 관해서는 저를 완전히 자유롭게 놔두시는 건지 알고 싶어요.\""]], [["“Absolutely.”", "\"전적으로 그렇다.\""]], [["“Then, as I said before, sir,—very well; I am ready to marry M. Cavalcanti.”", "\"그렇다면, 앞서 말씀드렸듯이, 아버지—좋아요. 카발칸티 씨와 결혼할 준비가 됐습니다.\""]], [["“But what are you up to?”", "\"한데 너는 무슨 꿍꿍이냐?\""]], [["“Ah, that is my affair.", "\"아, 그건 제 일이지요."], ["What advantage should I have over you, if knowing your secret I were to tell you mine?”", "아버지의 비밀을 알면서 제 비밀까지 말씀드린다면, 제가 아버지보다 무슨 이점을 누리겠어요?\""]], [["Danglars bit his lips. “Then,”", "당글라르가 입술을 깨물었다."], ["said he, “you are ready to pay the official visits, which are absolutely indispensable?”", "\"그렇다면,\" 그가 말했다. \"절대로 빠뜨릴 수 없는 그 공식 방문들에 응할 준비가 됐다는 거지?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["replied Eugénie.", "외제니가 대답했다."]], [["“And to sign the contract in three days?”", "\"사흘 안에 혼인 계약서에 서명하고?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Then, in my turn, I also say, very well!”", "\"그렇다면 이번엔 내 차례구나. 나도 말하마, 좋다!\""]], [["Danglars pressed his daughter’s hand in his.", "당글라르가 딸의 손을 제 두 손으로 감싸 쥐었다."], ["But, extraordinary to relate, the father did not say, “Thank you, my child,” nor did the daughter smile at her father.", "그러나 참으로 기이하게도, 아버지는 \"고맙다, 내 아이야\"라고 말하지 않았고, 딸 또한 아버지에게 미소 짓지 않았다."]], [["“Is the conference ended?” asked Eugénie, rising.", "\"면담은 끝났나요?\" 외제니가 일어서며 물었다."]], [["Danglars motioned that he had nothing more to say.", "당글라르는 더 할 말이 없다는 몸짓을 했다."], ["Five minutes afterwards the piano resounded to the touch of Mademoiselle d’Armilly’s fingers, and Mademoiselle Danglars was singing Brabantio’s malediction on Desdemona.", "오 분 뒤, 다르미이 양의 손가락이 피아노를 울렸고, 당글라르 양은 데스데모나를 향한 브라반티오의 저주를 노래하고 있었다."], ["At the end of the piece Étienne entered, and announced to Eugénie that the horses were to the carriage, and that the baroness was waiting for her to pay her visits.", "곡이 끝날 무렵 에티엔이 들어와, 마차에 말이 매여 있으며 남작 부인이 방문길에 나서려고 아가씨를 기다린다고 외제니에게 알렸다."], ["We have seen them at Villefort’s; they proceeded then on their course. 40306m", "우리는 그들을 빌포르의 집에서 보았다. 그러니 그들은 이제 그 길을 따라 나섰던 것이다."]], [["# Chapter 96. The Contract", "# Chapter 96. The Contract"]], [["50019m\nThree days after the scene we have just described, namely towards five o’clock in the afternoon of the day fixed for the signature of the contract between Mademoiselle Eugénie Danglars and Andrea Cavalcanti, whom the banker persisted in calling prince, a fresh breeze was stirring the leaves in the little garden in front of the Count of Monte Cristo’s house, and the count was preparing to go out.", "우리가 방금 묘사한 장면이 있은 지 사흘 뒤, 다시 말해 외제니 당글라르 양과 안드레아 카발칸티의 혼인 계약서에 서명하기로 정해진 날 오후 다섯 시 무렵이었다. 은행가는 여전히 카발칸티를 공작이라 부르기를 고집했다. 산뜻한 바람이 몬테크리스토 백작 저택 앞 작은 정원의 나뭇잎을 흔들고 있었고, 백작은 외출 채비를 하던 참이었다."], ["While his horses were impatiently pawing the ground, held in by the coachman, who had been seated a quarter of an hour on his box, the elegant phaeton with which we are familiar rapidly turned the angle of the entrance-gate, and cast out on the doorsteps M.", "십오 분 전부터 마부석에 앉아 있던 마부가 말들을 붙들고 있었지만, 말들은 초조하게 땅을 긁어 댔다. 그때 우리에게 익숙한 그 멋진 사륜마차가 정문 모서리를 잽싸게 돌아 들어오더니, 현관 계단 앞에 안드레아 카발칸티 씨를 내려놓았다."], ["Andrea Cavalcanti, as decked up and gay as if he were going to marry a princess.", "그는 마치 공주와 결혼하러 가는 사람처럼 한껏 차려입고 들떠 있었다."]], [["He inquired after the count with his usual familiarity, and ascending lightly to the first story met him at the top of the stairs.", "그는 여느 때처럼 허물없이 백작의 안부를 물으며 가뿐하게 이층으로 올라갔고, 계단 꼭대기에서 백작과 마주쳤다."]], [["The count stopped on seeing the young man.", "백작은 젊은이를 보고 걸음을 멈추었다."], ["As for Andrea, he was launched, and when he was once launched nothing stopped him.", "안드레아로 말하자면, 그는 이미 시동이 걸린 상태였고, 한번 시동이 걸리면 무엇으로도 그를 멈출 수 없었다."]], [["“Ah, good morning, my dear count,”", "\"아, 안녕하십니까, 친애하는 백작님.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Ah, M. Andrea,”", "\"아, 안드레아 씨.\""], ["said the latter, with his half-jesting tone; “how do you do?”", "백작이 절반쯤 농을 섞은 어조로 말했다. \"어떻게 지내십니까?\""]], [["“Charmingly, as you see.", "\"보시다시피 더할 나위 없이 좋습니다."], ["I am come to talk to you about a thousand things; but, first tell me, were you going out or just returned?”", "백작님께 드릴 이야기가 산더미처럼 있어서요. 하지만 먼저, 외출하시려던 참입니까, 아니면 막 돌아오신 참입니까?\""]], [["“I was going out, sir.”", "\"나가려던 참이었소.\""]], [["“Then, in order not to hinder you, I will get up with you if you please in your carriage, and Tom shall follow with my phaeton in tow.”", "\"그러시다면 방해가 되지 않게, 괜찮으시다면 제가 백작님 마차에 함께 타겠습니다. 톰이 제 사륜마차를 끌고 뒤따르게 하지요.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니요.\""], ["said the count, with an imperceptible smile of contempt, for he had no wish to be seen in the young man’s society,—“no; I prefer listening to you here, my dear M.", "백작이 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 희미한 경멸의 미소를 띠며 말했다. 그는 이 젊은이와 함께 있는 모습을 남에게 보이고 싶지 않았다. \"아니요, 여기서 당신 이야기를 듣는 편이 낫겠소, 친애하는 안드레아 씨."], ["Andrea; we can chat better in-doors, and there is no coachman to overhear our conversation.”", "안에서라야 더 편히 이야기를 나눌 수 있고, 우리 대화를 엿들을 마부도 없으니 말이오.\""]], [["The count returned to a small drawing-room on the first floor, sat down, and crossing his legs motioned to the young man to take a seat also.", "백작은 이층의 작은 응접실로 되돌아가 자리에 앉았다. 그는 다리를 꼬고는 젊은이에게도 앉으라는 손짓을 했다."], ["Andrea assumed his gayest manner.", "안드레아는 한껏 들뜬 태도를 지어 보였다."]], [["“You know, my dear count,”", "\"아시다시피, 친애하는 백작님.\""], ["said he, “the ceremony is to take place this evening.", "그가 말했다. \"오늘 저녁에 식이 거행됩니다."], ["At nine o’clock the contract is to be signed at my father-in-law’s.”", "아홉 시에 장인어른 댁에서 계약서에 서명하기로 했지요.\""]], [["“Ah, indeed?”", "\"아, 그래요?\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“What; is it news to you?", "\"아니, 처음 들으시는 겁니까?"], ["Has not M. Danglars informed you of the ceremony?”", "당글라르 씨가 식에 대해 알려 드리지 않았습니까?\""]], [["“Oh, yes,”", "\"아, 들었소.\""], ["said the count; “I received a letter from him yesterday, but I do not think the hour was mentioned.”", "백작이 말했다. \"어제 그분에게서 편지를 한 통 받았소만, 시각까지 적혀 있던 것 같지는 않군.\""]], [["“Possibly my father-in-law trusted to its general notoriety.”", "\"아마 장인어른께서는 워낙 다들 아는 일이라 따로 적지 않으신 모양입니다.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"음.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “you are fortunate, M.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["Cavalcanti; it is a most suitable alliance you are contracting, and Mademoiselle Danglars is a handsome girl.”", "\"운이 좋으시오, 카발칸티 씨. 더없이 어울리는 혼사를 맺는 것이고, 당글라르 양은 아름다운 아가씨니 말이오.\""]], [["“Yes, indeed she is,”", "\"네, 정말 그렇습니다.\""], ["replied Cavalcanti, in a very modest tone.", "카발칸티가 자못 겸손한 어조로 대답했다."]], [["“Above all, she is very rich,—at least, I believe so,”", "\"무엇보다 그 아가씨는 아주 부유하지, 적어도 내가 알기로는 그렇소.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“Very rich, do you think?” replied the young man.", "\"아주 부유하다고요, 그리 생각하십니까?\" 젊은이가 되물었다."]], [["“Doubtless; it is said M. Danglars conceals at least half of his fortune.”", "\"의심할 여지가 없소. 당글라르 씨가 적어도 재산의 절반은 숨기고 있다는 말이 있으니까.\""]], [["“And he acknowledges fifteen or twenty millions,”", "\"그런데도 인정하는 것만 천오백만이나 이천만 프랑이라지요.\""], ["said Andrea with a look sparkling with joy.", "안드레아가 기쁨으로 반짝이는 눈빛을 띠며 말했다."]], [["“Without reckoning,”", "\"게다가,\""], ["added Monte Cristo, “that he is on the eve of entering into a sort of speculation already in vogue in the United States and in England, but quite novel in France.”", "몬테크리스토가 덧붙였다. \"그분은 미국과 영국에서는 이미 유행하지만 프랑스에서는 아주 새로운 종류의 투자에 막 뛰어들려는 참이지.\""]], [["“Yes, yes, I know what you mean,—the railway, of which he has obtained the grant, is it not?”", "\"네, 네, 무슨 말씀이신지 압니다. 그분이 부설권을 따낸 그 철도 말씀이지요?\""]], [["“Precisely; it is generally believed he will gain ten millions by that affair.”", "\"바로 그거요. 그 사업으로 천만 프랑은 벌 거라는 게 중론이오.\""]], [["“Ten millions!", "\"천만 프랑이라니!"], ["Do you think so?", "그렇게 보십니까?"], ["It is magnificent!”", "굉장하군요!\""], ["said Cavalcanti, who was quite confounded at the metallic sound of these golden words.", "카발칸티가 외쳤다. 그는 그 황금 같은 말들이 내는 쇳소리에 완전히 정신이 나가 있었다."]], [["“Without reckoning,”", "\"게다가,\""], ["replied Monte Cristo, “that all his fortune will come to you, and justly too, since Mademoiselle Danglars is an only daughter.", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"그 모든 재산이 결국 당신에게 돌아올 거요. 당글라르 양이 외동딸이니, 그게 마땅하기도 하고."], ["Besides, your own fortune, as your father assured me, is almost equal to that of your betrothed.", "더구나 당신 부친께서 내게 장담하신 바로는, 당신 자신의 재산도 약혼녀의 재산에 거의 맞먹는다지."], ["But enough of money matters.", "하지만 돈 이야기는 이쯤 합시다."], ["Do you know, M. Andrea, I think you have managed this affair rather skilfully?”", "그런데 안드레아 씨, 내 보기엔 당신이 이 일을 제법 능숙하게 처리한 것 같소.\""]], [["“Not badly, by any means,”", "\"어느 모로 보나 나쁘진 않았지요.\""], ["said the young man; “I was born for a diplomatist.”", "젊은이가 말했다. \"저는 외교관이 될 팔자로 태어났나 봅니다.\""]], [["“Well, you must become a diplomatist; diplomacy, you know, is something that is not to be acquired; it is instinctive.", "\"그렇다면 외교관이 되어야겠군. 알다시피 외교라는 건 배워서 익힐 수 있는 게 아니오. 타고나는 거지."], ["Have you lost your heart?”", "그래, 마음을 빼앗기셨소?\""]], [["“Indeed, I fear it,”", "\"실은, 그런 것 같아 두렵습니다.\""], ["replied Andrea, in the tone in which he had heard Dorante or Valère reply to Alceste21 at the Théâtre Français.", "안드레아가 대답했다. 그 어조는 프랑스 극장에서 도랑트나 발레르가 알세스트에게 대답하던 말투 그대로였다. * 도랑트와 발레르, 알세스트는 몰리에르 희극의 등장인물들이다. ─옮긴이"]], [["“Is your love returned?”", "\"사랑을 돌려받고 계시오?\""]], [["“I suppose so,”", "\"그런 것 같습니다.\""], ["said Andrea with a triumphant smile, “since I am accepted.", "안드레아가 의기양양한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"받아들여졌으니까요."], ["But I must not forget one grand point.”", "하지만 한 가지 큰 사실을 잊어서는 안 되지요.\""]], [["“Which?”", "\"그게 뭐요?\""]], [["“That I have been singularly assisted.”", "\"제가 더없이 특별한 도움을 받았다는 사실 말입니다.\""]], [["“Nonsense.”", "\"별말씀을.\""]], [["“I have, indeed.”", "\"정말입니다.\""]], [["“By circumstances?”", "\"운이 좋았다는 거요?\""]], [["“By me? Not at all, prince,”", "\"아니요, 백작님 덕분이지요.\" \"내 덕분이라고?"], ["said Monte Cristo laying a marked stress on the title, “what have I done for you?", "천만의 말씀이오, 공작.\" 몬테크리스토가 그 호칭에 또렷이 힘을 주며 말했다. \"내가 당신을 위해 한 일이 무엇이오?"], ["Are not your name, your social position, and your merit sufficient?”", "당신의 이름과 사회적 지위, 그리고 당신의 자질만으로 충분하지 않소?\""]], [["“No,” said Andrea,—“no; it is useless for you to say so, count.", "\"아닙니다.\" 안드레아가 말했다."], ["I maintain that the position of a man like you has done more than my name, my social position, and my merit.”", "\"아니에요, 아무리 그리 말씀하셔도 소용없습니다, 백작님. 백작님 같은 분의 지위가 제 이름과 사회적 지위, 자질을 다 합친 것보다 더 큰 몫을 했다고 저는 확신합니다.\""]], [["“You are completely mistaken, sir,”", "\"완전히 잘못 짚으셨소.\" 몬테크리스토가 차갑게 말했다."], ["said Monte Cristo coldly, who felt the perfidious manœuvre of the young man, and understood the bearing of his words; “you only acquired my protection after the influence and fortune of your father had been ascertained; for, after all, who procured for me, who had never seen either you or your illustrious father, the pleasure of your acquaintance?—two of my good friends, Lord Wilmore and the Abbé Busoni.", "그는 이 젊은이의 음흉한 술수를 간파했고, 그 말의 속내를 꿰뚫고 있었다. \"당신은 부친의 영향력과 재산이 확인된 뒤에야 비로소 내 비호를 얻었소. 따져 보면, 당신도 당신의 그 명망 높은 부친도 한 번 본 적 없는 내게 당신을 알게 되는 기쁨을 마련해 준 게 누구였소?"], ["What encouraged me not to become your surety, but to patronize you?—your father’s name, so well known in Italy and so highly honored.", "바로 내 두 친구, 윌모어 경과 부소니 신부였소. 내가 당신의 보증인이 되지는 않더라도 당신을 후원하도록 이끈 것은 무엇이었소? 이탈리아에 널리 알려지고 드높이 존경받는 당신 부친의 이름이었소."], ["Personally, I do not know you.”", "개인적으로 나는 당신을 알지 못하오.\""]], [["This calm tone and perfect ease made Andrea feel that he was, for the moment, restrained by a more muscular hand than his own, and that the restraint could not be easily broken through.", "이 차분한 어조와 완벽한 여유에 안드레아는 자신이 지금 이 순간 자기보다 더 억센 손에 제압당해 있으며, 그 손아귀를 쉽사리 벗어날 수 없음을 느꼈다."]], [["“Oh, then my father has really a very large fortune, count?”", "\"아, 그럼 제 아버지가 정말로 막대한 재산을 가지고 있는 거군요, 백작님?\""]], [["“It appears so, sir,”", "\"그런 모양이오.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다."]], [["“Do you know if the marriage settlement he promised me has come?”", "\"아버지가 제게 약속하신 혼인 지참금이 도착했는지 아십니까?\""]], [["“I have been advised of it.”", "\"그렇다는 통지를 받았소.\""]], [["“But the three millions?”", "\"하지만 삼백만 프랑은요?\""]], [["“The three millions are probably on the road.”", "\"삼백만은 아마 오는 중일 거요.\""]], [["“Then I shall really have them?”", "\"그럼 정말로 제가 그 돈을 받게 되는 거군요?\""]], [["“Oh, well,”", "\"그야,\""], ["said the count, “I do not think you have yet known the want of money.”", "백작이 말했다. \"당신이 아직 돈이 궁한 처지를 겪어 본 적은 없는 것 같소만.\""]], [["Andrea was so surprised that he pondered the matter for a moment.", "안드레아는 어찌나 놀랐던지 잠시 그 문제를 곱씹었다."], ["Then, arousing from his reverie:", "그러다 상념에서 깨어나 말했다."]], [["“Now, sir, I have one request to make to you, which you will understand, even if it should be disagreeable to you.”", "\"백작님, 한 가지 청이 있습니다. 마음에 들지 않으실 수도 있겠지만, 이해는 해 주시리라 믿습니다.\""]], [["“Proceed,”", "\"말해 보시오.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“I have formed an acquaintance, thanks to my good fortune, with many noted persons, and have, at least for the moment, a crowd of friends.", "\"운이 좋았던 덕에 저는 이름난 분들을 많이 알게 되었고, 적어도 지금은 친구가 넘쳐 납니다."], ["But marrying, as I am about to do, before all Paris, I ought to be supported by an illustrious name, and in the absence of the paternal hand some powerful one ought to lead me to the altar; now, my father is not coming to Paris, is he?”", "하지만 이제 곧 온 파리가 지켜보는 가운데 결혼하는 마당이니, 저는 명망 있는 이름의 후원을 받아야 마땅하고, 아버지의 손이 없는 자리에서는 누군가 힘 있는 분이 저를 제단까지 이끌어 주어야 합니다. 그런데 제 아버지는 파리에 오시지 않지요, 그렇지요?\""]], [["“He is old, covered with wounds, and suffers dreadfully, he says, in travelling.”", "\"그분은 연로하신 데다 온몸이 상처투성이라, 본인 말로는 여행을 하면 끔찍이 고생하신다더군.\""]], [["“I understand; well, I am come to ask a favor of you.”", "\"알겠습니다. 그래서 한 가지 부탁을 드리러 왔습니다.\""]], [["“Of me?”", "\"내게?\""]], [["“Yes, of you.”", "\"네, 백작님께요.\""]], [["“And pray what may it be?”", "\"대체 무엇이오?\""]], [["“Well, to take his part.”", "\"아버지 역할을 맡아 주시는 것입니다.\""]], [["“Ah, my dear sir!", "\"이런, 이 사람아!"], ["What?—after the varied relations I have had the happiness to sustain towards you, can it be that you know me so little as to ask such a thing?", "무슨 소리요? 내가 다행히도 당신과 갖가지 인연을 맺어 왔는데, 그러고도 나를 그렇게 모르고서 그런 부탁을 하는 거요?"], ["Ask me to lend you half a million and, although such a loan is somewhat rare, on my honor, you would annoy me less!", "차라리 오십만 프랑을 빌려 달라고 하시오. 그런 거액을 빌리는 일도 드물긴 하나, 맹세코, 그 편이 나를 덜 난처하게 할 거요!"], ["Know, then, what I thought I had already told you, that in participation in this world’s affairs, more especially in their moral aspects, the Count of Monte Cristo has never ceased to entertain the scruples and even the superstitions of the East.", "그러니 알아 두시오, 이미 말한 줄 알았소만, 이 세상사에 끼어드는 일, 특히 그 도덕적인 측면에 관해서라면, 몬테크리스토 백작은 동방 특유의 조심성, 아니 미신까지도 단 한 번도 버린 적이 없소."], ["I, who have a seraglio at Cairo, one at Smyrna, and one at Constantinople, preside at a wedding?—never!”", "카이로에, 스미르나에, 콘스탄티노플에 각각 후궁을 둔 이 몸이 결혼식을 주관한다? 결단코 안 될 일이오!\""]], [["“Then you refuse me?”", "\"그럼 거절하시는 겁니까?\""]], [["“Decidedly; and were you my son or my brother I would refuse you in the same way.”", "\"단호히. 당신이 내 아들이든 형제든, 똑같이 거절했을 거요.\""]], [["“But what must be done?”", "\"그럼 어찌해야 합니까?\""], ["said Andrea, disappointed.", "안드레아가 실망하여 말했다."]], [["“You said just now that you had a hundred friends.”", "\"방금 친구가 백 명이라고 하지 않았소.\""]], [["“Very true, but you introduced me at M. Danglars’.”", "\"맞습니다, 하지만 저를 당글라르 씨 댁에 소개해 주신 분은 백작님이시지요.\""]], [["“Not at all!", "\"천만에!"], ["Let us recall the exact facts.", "사실을 정확히 되짚어 봅시다."], ["You met him at a dinner party at my house, and you introduced yourself at his house; that is a totally different affair.”", "당신은 우리 집 만찬 자리에서 그를 만났고, 그의 집에는 당신 스스로 찾아갔소. 그건 전혀 다른 이야기요.\""]], [["“Yes, but, by my marriage, you have forwarded that.”", "\"네, 하지만 이번 혼인은 백작님이 밀어 주신 거잖습니까.\""]], [["“I?—not in the least, I beg you to believe.", "\"내가? 추호도 아니오, 그렇게 믿어 주시오."], ["Recollect what I told you when you asked me to propose you.", "당신이 내게 청혼을 주선해 달라고 했을 때 내가 뭐라고 했는지 떠올려 보시오."], ["‘Oh, I never make matches, my dear prince, it is my settled principle.’”", "'아, 나는 절대 중매는 서지 않소, 친애하는 공작. 그게 내 확고한 원칙이오.'\""], ["Andrea bit his lips.", "안드레아는 입술을 깨물었다."]], [["50025m\n“But, at least, you will be there?”", "\"하지만, 적어도 그 자리에는 와 주시겠지요?\""]], [["“Will all Paris be there?”", "\"온 파리가 다 갑니까?\""]], [["“Oh, certainly.”", "\"아, 그럼요.\""]], [["“Well, like all Paris, I shall be there too,”", "\"그렇다면 온 파리처럼 나도 가리다.\""], ["said the count.", "백작이 말했다."]], [["“And will you sign the contract?”", "\"그럼 계약서에도 서명해 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“I see no objection to that; my scruples do not go thus far.”", "\"그건 마다할 이유가 없소. 내 조심성도 거기까지 미치지는 않으니.\""]], [["“Well, since you will grant me no more, I must be content with what you give me.", "\"좋습니다, 더는 베풀어 주지 않으시겠다니, 주시는 것만으로 만족해야지요."], ["But one word more, count.”", "하지만 한마디만 더, 백작님.\""]], [["“What is it?”", "\"뭐요?\""]], [["“Advice.”", "\"조언입니다.\""]], [["“Be careful; advice is worse than a service.”", "\"조심하시오. 조언은 도움보다 더 고약한 거요.\""]], [["“Oh, you can give me this without compromising yourself.”", "\"아, 이건 백작님 자신을 위태롭게 하지 않고도 주실 수 있는 것입니다.\""]], [["“Tell me what it is.”", "\"무엇인지 말해 보시오.\""]], [["“Is my wife’s fortune five hundred thousand livres?”", "\"제 아내의 재산이 오십만 리브르입니까?\""]], [["“That is the sum M. Danglars himself announced.”", "\"당글라르 씨가 직접 밝힌 액수가 그렇소.\""]], [["“Must I receive it, or leave it in the hands of the notary?”", "\"그 돈을 제가 직접 받아야 합니까, 아니면 공증인에게 맡겨 두어야 합니까?\""]], [["“This is the way such affairs are generally arranged when it is wished to do them stylishly: Your two solicitors appoint a meeting, when the contract is signed, for the next or the following day; then they exchange the two portions, for which they each give a receipt; then, when the marriage is celebrated, they place the amount at your disposal as the chief member of the alliance.”", "\"격식을 갖춰 처리하고 싶을 때는 보통 이렇게 합니다. 양측 대리인이 계약서에 서명한 다음 날이나 그다음 날에 모임을 정하고, 그 자리에서 양쪽 몫을 교환하며 서로 영수증을 주고받지요. 그런 다음 혼례가 거행되면, 그들은 그 금액을 혼인의 주체인 당신이 마음대로 쓸 수 있도록 내놓는 거요.\""]], [["“Because,”", "\"실은,\""], ["said Andrea, with a certain ill-concealed uneasiness, “I thought I heard my father-in-law say that he intended embarking our property in that famous railway affair of which you spoke just now.”", "안드레아가 어딘지 감추기 힘든 불안을 내비치며 말했다. \"장인어른께서 방금 말씀하신 그 유명한 철도 사업에 우리 재산을 투자할 작정이라고 하시는 걸 들은 것 같아서요.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"음.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo, “it will be the way, everybody says, of trebling your fortune in twelve months.", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"다들 말하길, 그게 열두 달 만에 재산을 세 배로 불리는 길이라더군."], ["Baron Danglars is a good father, and knows how to calculate.”", "당글라르 남작은 좋은 아버지고, 셈에도 밝은 분이오.\""]], [["“In that case,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["said Andrea, “everything is all right, excepting your refusal, which quite grieves me.”", "안드레아가 말했다. \"백작님이 거절하신 것만 빼면 모든 게 더할 나위 없군요. 그 거절이 저를 몹시 슬프게 합니다만.\""]], [["“You must attribute it only to natural scruples under similar circumstances.”", "\"그저 그런 상황에서 자연스레 드는 조심성 탓으로 여겨 주시오.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"좋습니다.\""], ["said Andrea, “let it be as you wish.", "안드레아가 말했다. \"원하시는 대로 하지요."], ["This evening, then, at nine o’clock.”", "그럼 오늘 저녁 아홉 시에 뵙겠습니다.\""]], [["“Adieu till then.”", "\"그때까지 안녕히.\""]], [["Notwithstanding a slight resistance on the part of Monte Cristo, whose lips turned pale, but who preserved his ceremonious smile, Andrea seized the count’s hand, pressed it, jumped into his phaeton, and disappeared.", "몬테크리스토는 입술이 창백해질 만큼 미약하게 저항했지만 의례적인 미소만은 잃지 않았다. 그런데도 안드레아는 백작의 손을 붙잡아 꾹 쥐고는, 자기 사륜마차에 뛰어올라 사라졌다."]], [["The four or five remaining hours before nine o’clock arrived, Andrea employed in riding, paying visits,—designed to induce those of whom he had spoken to appear at the banker’s in their gayest equipages,—dazzling them by promises of shares in schemes which have since turned every brain, and in which Danglars was just taking the initiative.", "아홉 시까지 남은 너덧 시간 동안 안드레아는 말을 타고 돌아다니며 여기저기 방문을 했다. 앞서 이야기했던 사람들이 가장 화려한 마차를 몰고 은행가의 집에 나타나도록 부추기려는 속셈이었다. 그는 그 뒤로 모든 이의 머리를 어지럽혀 놓을, 또 당글라르가 막 주도권을 쥐고 있던 그 투자 사업의 지분을 약속하며 그들의 눈을 현혹시켰다."]], [["In fact, at half-past eight in the evening the grand salon, the gallery adjoining, and the three other drawing-rooms on the same floor, were filled with a perfumed crowd, who sympathized but little in the event, but who all participated in that love of being present wherever there is anything fresh to be seen.", "과연 저녁 여덟 시 반에는 대연회장과 거기 잇닿은 회랑, 그리고 같은 층의 다른 세 응접실이 향수 냄새 나는 사람들로 가득 찼다. 그들은 이 혼사 자체에는 별 관심이 없었지만, 무언가 새로운 볼거리가 있는 곳이라면 어디든 끼고 싶어 하는 그 욕망만은 다 함께 품고 있었다."], ["An Academician would say that the entertainments of the fashionable world are collections of flowers which attract inconstant butterflies, famished bees, and buzzing drones.", "어느 한림원 회원이라면 이렇게 말했으리라. 사교계의 향연이란 변덕스러운 나비와 굶주린 벌, 윙윙대는 수벌을 끌어들이는 꽃밭이라고."]], [["50025m\nNo one could deny that the rooms were splendidly illuminated; the light streamed forth on the gilt mouldings and the silk hangings; and all the bad taste of decorations, which had only their richness to boast of, shone in its splendor.", "그 방들이 눈부시게 밝혀져 있다는 것은 누구도 부인할 수 없었다. 빛이 금박 몰딩과 비단 휘장 위로 쏟아졌고, 그저 화려함 하나만 내세울 뿐인 그 모든 천박한 장식이 제 광채를 한껏 뽐냈다."], ["Mademoiselle Eugénie was dressed with elegant simplicity in a figured white silk dress, and a white rose half concealed in her jet black hair was her only ornament, unaccompanied by a single jewel.", "외제니 양은 무늬가 든 흰 비단 드레스를 입어 우아하면서도 단순하게 차려입었고, 칠흑 같은 검은 머리에 반쯤 가려진 흰 장미 한 송이가 유일한 장식이었다. 보석은 단 하나도 곁들이지 않았다."], ["Her eyes, however, betrayed that perfect confidence which contradicted the girlish simplicity of this modest attire.", "그러나 그녀의 눈빛만은 이 수수한 차림의 소녀다운 단순함과 어긋나는 완벽한 자신감을 드러내고 있었다."]], [["Madame Danglars was chatting at a short distance with Debray, Beauchamp, and Château-Renaud.", "당글라르 부인은 조금 떨어진 곳에서 드브레, 보샹, 샤토르노와 담소를 나누고 있었다."], ["Debray was admitted to the house for this grand ceremony, but on the same plane with everyone else, and without any particular privilege. M.", "드브레는 이 성대한 식을 위해 이 집에 출입을 허락받았으나, 다른 모든 이와 똑같은 입장이었고 어떤 특별한 대우도 없었다."], ["Danglars, surrounded by deputies and men connected with the revenue, was explaining a new theory of taxation which he intended to adopt when the course of events had compelled the government to call him into the ministry.", "당글라르 씨는 의원들과 재정 관련 인사들에게 둘러싸여, 사태의 흐름이 정부로 하여금 자신을 입각시킬 수밖에 없게 되었을 때 도입할 작정인 새로운 조세 이론을 설명하고 있었다."], ["Andrea, on whose arm hung one of the most consummate dandies of the Opera, was explaining to him rather cleverly, since he was obliged to be bold to appear at ease, his future projects, and the new luxuries he meant to introduce to Parisian fashions with his hundred and seventy-five thousand livres per annum.", "안드레아는 오페라극장의 더없이 완성된 멋쟁이 하나를 팔에 매단 채, 자신의 앞날의 계획과, 연 십칠만 오천 리브르의 수입으로 파리의 유행에 들여올 새로운 사치들을 제법 영리하게 늘어놓고 있었다. 태연한 척하려면 대담해질 수밖에 없었던 것이다."]], [["The crowd moved to and fro in the rooms like an ebb and flow of turquoises, rubies, emeralds, opals, and diamonds.", "군중은 터키석과 루비, 에메랄드, 오팔, 다이아몬드의 밀물과 썰물처럼 방 안을 이리저리 오갔다."], ["As usual, the oldest women were the most decorated, and the ugliest the most conspicuous.", "늘 그렇듯, 가장 나이 많은 여인들이 가장 요란하게 치장했고, 가장 못생긴 여인들이 가장 눈에 띄었다."], ["If there was a beautiful lily, or a sweet rose, you had to search for it, concealed in some corner behind a mother with a turban, or an aunt with a bird-of-paradise.", "아름다운 백합이나 어여쁜 장미 한 송이가 있다 해도, 터번을 쓴 어머니나 극락조 깃털을 단 이모 뒤 어느 구석에 가려진 그것을 찾아내려면 한참을 헤매야 했다."]], [["At each moment, in the midst of the crowd, the buzzing, and the laughter, the door-keeper’s voice was heard announcing some name well known in the financial department, respected in the army, or illustrious in the literary world, and which was acknowledged by a slight movement in the different groups.", "군중과 웅성거림, 웃음소리 한가운데서 시시각각 문지기의 목소리가 들려왔다. 재정 부문에서 이름난, 군에서 존경받는, 혹은 문단에서 명성 높은 누군가의 이름을 알리는 소리였고, 그 이름은 여기저기 무리들의 가벼운 술렁임으로 화답을 받았다."], ["But for one whose privilege it was to agitate that ocean of human waves, how many were received with a look of indifference or a sneer of disdain!", "그러나 그 인간 물결의 바다를 출렁이게 할 특권을 누리는 한 사람에 비하면, 무심한 눈길이나 경멸의 비웃음으로 맞아들여지는 자가 또 얼마나 많았던가!"]], [["At the moment when the hand of the massive time-piece, representing Endymion asleep, pointed to nine on its golden face, and the hammer, the faithful type of mechanical thought, struck nine times, the name of the Count of Monte Cristo resounded in its turn, and as if by an electric shock all the assembly turned towards the door.", "잠든 엔디미온을 새긴 묵직한 괘종시계의 바늘이 금빛 문자판 위 아홉 시를 가리키고, 기계적 사고의 충실한 표상인 망치가 아홉 번을 칠 그 순간, 이번에는 몬테크리스토 백작의 이름이 울려 퍼졌다. 그러자 마치 전기 충격이라도 받은 듯 좌중 전체가 일제히 문 쪽으로 돌아섰다."], ["The count was dressed in black and with his habitual simplicity; his white waistcoat displayed his expansive noble chest and his black stock was singularly noticeable because of its contrast with the deadly paleness of his face.", "백작은 검은 옷을 입었고 여느 때처럼 단순한 차림이었다. 흰 조끼가 넓고 기품 있는 가슴을 드러냈으며, 검은 넥타이는 시체처럼 창백한 그 얼굴과 대비되어 유난히 도드라졌다."], ["His only jewellery was a chain, so fine that the slender gold thread was scarcely perceptible on his white waistcoat.", "그가 지닌 유일한 장신구는 사슬 하나뿐이었는데, 어찌나 가는지 그 가느다란 금실이 흰 조끼 위에서 거의 보이지 않을 정도였다."]], [["A circle was immediately formed around the door.", "곧바로 문 둘레로 사람들의 원이 둘러섰다."], ["The count perceived at one glance Madame Danglars at one end of the drawing-room, M. Danglars at the other, and Eugénie in front of him.", "백작은 한눈에 응접실 한쪽 끝에 있는 당글라르 부인, 반대편 끝에 있는 당글라르 씨, 그리고 자기 앞쪽의 외제니를 알아보았다. 그는 먼저 남작 부인 쪽으로 나아갔다."], ["He first advanced towards the baroness, who was chatting with Madame de Villefort, who had come alone, Valentine being still an invalid; and without turning aside, so clear was the road left for him, he passed from the baroness to Eugénie, whom he complimented in such rapid and measured terms, that the proud artist was quite struck.", "부인은 빌포르 부인과 담소를 나누고 있었는데, 발랑틴이 아직 병중이라 빌포르 부인은 혼자 와 있었다. 그를 위해 길이 훤히 비워져 있었으므로, 그는 옆으로 비켜설 필요도 없이 남작 부인에게서 외제니에게로 넘어갔다. 그러고는 어찌나 빠르고 절제된 말로 그녀에게 찬사를 건넸던지, 그 도도한 예술가도 적잖이 놀랐다."], ["Near her was Mademoiselle Louise d’Armilly, who thanked the count for the letters of introduction he had so kindly given her for Italy, which she intended immediately to make use of.", "그녀 곁에는 루이즈 다르미이 양이 있었는데, 그녀는 백작이 친절히 마련해 준 이탈리아행 소개장에 감사를 표했다. 그녀는 곧 그 소개장을 쓸 작정이었다."], ["On leaving these ladies he found himself with Danglars, who had advanced to meet him.", "이 부인들 곁을 떠난 그는 자신을 맞으러 나온 당글라르와 마주쳤다."]], [["Having accomplished these three social duties, Monte Cristo stopped, looking around him with that expression peculiar to a certain class, which seems to say, “I have done my duty, now let others do theirs.”", "이렇게 세 가지 사교적 의무를 마친 몬테크리스토는 걸음을 멈추고 주위를 둘러보았다. 그 표정은 어떤 부류의 사람들에게서나 볼 수 있는, \"나는 내 도리를 다했으니, 이제 남들이 제 도리를 다할 차례다.\"라고 말하는 듯한 표정이었다."]], [["Andrea, who was in an adjoining room, had shared in the sensation caused by the arrival of Monte Cristo, and now came forward to pay his respects to the count.", "옆방에 있던 안드레아는 몬테크리스토의 등장이 일으킨 그 술렁임에 함께 휩쓸렸고, 이제 백작에게 인사를 드리러 앞으로 나왔다. 백작은 완전히 사람들에게 둘러싸여 있었다."], ["He found him completely surrounded; all were eager to speak to him, as is always the case with those whose words are few and weighty.", "말수가 적고 그 한마디 한마디가 무게를 지닌 사람들이 늘 그렇듯, 다들 그에게 말을 건네려고 안달이었다."], ["The solicitors arrived at this moment and arranged their scrawled papers on the velvet cloth embroidered with gold which covered the table prepared for the signature; it was a gilt table supported on lions’ claws.", "마침 그때 대리인들이 도착해, 서명을 위해 마련된 탁자를 덮은 금실 자수의 벨벳 천 위에 자신들의 휘갈겨 쓴 서류들을 펼쳐 놓았다. 그것은 사자 발 모양의 다리로 받쳐진 금박 탁자였다."], ["One of the notaries sat down, the other remained standing.", "공증인 하나는 자리에 앉고, 다른 하나는 선 채로 있었다."], ["They were about to proceed to the reading of the contract, which half Paris assembled was to sign.", "이제 막 계약서 낭독에 들어가려는 참이었으니, 거기 모인 파리의 절반이 거기에 서명할 터였다."], ["All took their places, or rather the ladies formed a circle, while the gentlemen (more indifferent to the restraints of what Boileau calls the _style énergique_) commented on the feverish agitation of Andrea, on M. Danglars’ riveted attention, Eugénie’s composure, and the light and sprightly manner in which the baroness treated this important affair.", "모두가 제자리를 잡았다. 아니, 부인들은 둥글게 원을 이루었고, 신사들은 ─부알로가 이른바 '강건한 문체'의 구속에 더 무심한 자들답게─ 안드레아의 안절부절못하는 모습, 당글라르 씨의 못 박힌 듯한 집중, 외제니의 침착함, 그리고 남작 부인이 이 중대한 일을 가볍고 발랄하게 다루는 태도에 대해 한마디씩 했다."]], [["The contract was read during a profound silence.", "계약서는 깊은 침묵 속에서 낭독되었다."], ["But as soon as it was finished, the buzz was redoubled through all the drawing-rooms; the brilliant sums, the rolling millions which were to be at the command of the two young people, and which crowned the display of the wedding presents and the young lady’s diamonds, which had been made in a room entirely appropriated for that purpose, had exercised to the full their delusions over the envious assembly.", "그러나 낭독이 끝나기가 무섭게 웅성거림이 모든 응접실에 곱절로 일었다. 두 젊은이의 손에 들어올 그 휘황한 액수, 굴러 들어올 수백만 프랑은, 이를 위해 따로 마련한 방에 진열된 혼인 예물과 신부의 다이아몬드를 한층 빛내며, 시샘에 찬 좌중에 그 환상을 한껏 펼쳐 놓았다."]], [["Mademoiselle Danglars’ charms were heightened in the opinion of the young men, and for the moment seemed to outvie the sun in splendor.", "당글라르 양의 매력은 젊은 남자들의 눈에 한층 돋보였고, 그 순간만큼은 태양의 광채마저 능가하는 듯했다."], ["As for the ladies, it is needless to say that while they coveted the millions, they thought they did not need them for themselves, as they were beautiful enough without them.", "부인들로 말하자면, 그들이 그 수백만 프랑을 탐내면서도 자신들에게는 그것이 필요 없다고 여겼다는 건 말할 것도 없다. 그런 게 없어도 그들은 충분히 아름다웠으니까."], ["Andrea, surrounded by his friends, complimented, flattered, beginning to believe in the reality of his dream, was almost bewildered.", "친구들에게 둘러싸여 찬사와 아첨을 받던 안드레아는 자기 꿈이 진짜라고 믿기 시작했고, 거의 얼이 빠질 지경이었다."], ["The notary solemnly took the pen, flourished it above his head, and said:", "공증인이 엄숙하게 펜을 집어 들어 머리 위로 한 번 휘두르고는 말했다."]], [["“Gentlemen, we are about to sign the contract.”", "\"여러분, 이제 계약서에 서명하겠습니다.\""]], [["The baron was to sign first, then the representative of M. Cavalcanti, senior, then the baroness, afterwards the “future couple,” as they are styled in the abominable phraseology of legal documents.", "남작이 먼저 서명하고, 다음으로 카발칸티 노인 측 대리인, 그다음에 남작 부인, 그리고 법률 문서의 그 끔찍한 표현을 빌리자면 '장차 부부가 될 두 사람'의 순서였다."]], [["The baron took the pen and signed, then the representative.", "남작이 펜을 들어 서명했고, 이어서 대리인이 서명했다."], ["The baroness approached, leaning on Madame de Villefort’s arm.", "남작 부인이 빌포르 부인의 팔에 기대어 다가왔다."]], [["“My dear,”", "\"이보세요,\""], ["said she, as she took the pen, “is it not vexatious?", "그녀가 펜을 들며 말했다. \"이거 참 분통 터지는 일 아니에요?"], ["An unexpected incident, in the affair of murder and theft at the Count of Monte Cristo’s, in which he nearly fell a victim, deprives us of the pleasure of seeing M. de Villefort.”", "몬테크리스토 백작님 댁에서 일어난 살인 강도 사건 ─백작님도 하마터면 희생될 뻔했지요─ 그 일에 뜻밖의 일이 생기는 바람에, 빌포르 씨를 뵙는 즐거움을 빼앗기고 말았으니 말이에요.\""]], [["“Indeed?” said M.", "\"그렇습니까?\""], ["Danglars, in the same tone in which he would have said, “Oh, well, what do I care?”", "당글라르 씨가 말했다. 그 말투는 \"아, 뭐, 그게 나랑 무슨 상관이오?\"라고 말하는 투였다."]], [["“As a matter of fact,”", "\"실은,\""], ["said Monte Cristo, approaching, “I am much afraid that I am the involuntary cause of his absence.”", "몬테크리스토가 다가오며 말했다. \"그분이 못 오시게 된 게 본의 아니게 저 때문인 것 같아 몹시 송구합니다.\""]], [["“What, you, count?”", "\"아니, 백작님이요?\""], ["said Madame Danglars, signing; “if you are, take care, for I shall never forgive you.”", "당글라르 부인이 서명하며 말했다. \"정말 그렇다면, 조심하셔야 할 거예요. 저는 절대 용서하지 않을 테니까요.\""]], [["Andrea pricked up his ears.", "안드레아가 귀를 쫑긋 세웠다."]], [["“But it is not my fault, as I shall endeavor to prove.”", "\"하지만 제 잘못은 아니라는 걸 입증해 보이지요.\""]], [["Everyone listened eagerly; Monte Cristo who so rarely opened his lips, was about to speak.", "모두가 귀를 곤두세웠다. 좀처럼 입을 열지 않는 몬테크리스토가 이제 막 말을 하려던 참이었다."]], [["“You remember,”", "\"기억하시겠지요,\""], ["said the count, during the most profound silence, “that the unhappy wretch who came to rob me died at my house; the supposition is that he was stabbed by his accomplice, on attempting to leave it.”", "백작이 더없이 깊은 침묵 속에서 말했다. \"저를 털러 왔던 그 가엾은 자가 제 집에서 죽었던 일 말입니다. 그자가 집을 빠져나가려다 공범의 칼에 찔린 것으로 추정되지요.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇소.\""], ["said Danglars.", "당글라르가 말했다."]], [["“In order that his wounds might be examined he was undressed, and his clothes were thrown into a corner, where the police picked them up, with the exception of the waistcoat, which they overlooked.”", "\"상처를 살펴보려고 그자의 옷을 벗겨 한쪽 구석에 던져 두었는데, 경찰이 그 옷을 거두어 갔습니다. 다만 조끼 하나만은 미처 못 보고 빠뜨렸지요.\""]], [["Andrea turned pale, and drew towards the door; he saw a cloud rising in the horizon, which appeared to forebode a coming storm.", "안드레아의 얼굴이 창백해졌고, 그는 슬그머니 문 쪽으로 다가갔다. 그는 지평선 위로 구름이 피어오르는 것을 보았고, 그것은 곧 닥쳐올 폭풍을 예고하는 듯했다."]], [["“Well, this waistcoat was discovered today, covered with blood, and with a hole over the heart.”", "\"그런데 오늘 그 조끼가 발견된 겁니다. 피로 뒤덮이고, 심장께에 구멍이 뚫린 채로요.\""], ["The ladies screamed, and two or three prepared to faint.", "부인들이 비명을 질렀고, 두세 명은 막 정신을 잃으려 했다."], ["“It was brought to me.", "\"그게 제게로 왔지요."], ["No one could guess what the dirty rag could be; I alone suspected that it was the waistcoat of the murdered man.", "그 더러운 누더기가 무엇인지 아무도 짐작하지 못했습니다. 오직 저만이 그것이 살해된 자의 조끼임을 알아챘지요."], ["My valet, in examining this mournful relic, felt a paper in the pocket and drew it out; it was a letter addressed to you, baron.”", "제 하인이 그 음산한 유물을 살펴보다가 주머니에서 종이 한 장이 만져져 꺼냈는데, 그게 바로 남작님 앞으로 된 편지였습니다.\""]], [["“To me?”", "\"내게?\""], ["cried Danglars.", "당글라르가 외쳤다."]], [["“Yes, indeed, to you; I succeeded in deciphering your name under the blood with which the letter was stained,”", "\"네, 그렇습니다, 남작님께요. 편지를 적신 핏자국 아래로 남작님 성함을 간신히 읽어 냈지요.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo, amid the general outburst of amazement.", "몬테크리스토가 대답하자, 좌중이 일제히 경악을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["asked Madame Danglars, looking at her husband with uneasiness, “how could that prevent M. de Villefort——”", "당글라르 부인이 불안한 눈으로 남편을 보며 물었다. \"그게 어째서 빌포르 씨가 못 오시게……\""]], [["“In this simple way, madame,”", "\"이렇게 간단한 까닭이지요, 부인.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo; “the waistcoat and the letter were both what is termed circumstantial evidence; I therefore sent them to the king’s attorney.", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"그 조끼와 편지는 둘 다 이른바 정황 증거라는 것이었습니다. 그래서 저는 그것들을 검사에게 보냈지요."], ["You understand, my dear baron, that legal methods are the safest in criminal cases; it was, perhaps, some plot against you.”", "친애하는 남작님도 아시겠지만, 형사 사건에서는 법적인 절차가 가장 안전한 법입니다. 어쩌면 남작님을 노린 어떤 음모였는지도 모르니까요.\""], ["Andrea looked steadily at Monte Cristo and disappeared in the second drawing-room.", "안드레아는 몬테크리스토를 뚫어지게 바라보다가 두 번째 응접실로 사라졌다."]], [["“Possibly,”", "\"그럴 수도 있겠군요.\""], ["said Danglars; “was not this murdered man an old galley-slave?” 50025m", "당글라르가 말했다. \"그 살해된 자는 전직 갤리선 죄수가 아니었소?\""]], [["“Yes,” replied the count; “a felon named Caderousse.”", "\"맞습니다.\" 백작이 대답했다."], ["Danglars turned slightly pale; Andrea reached the anteroom beyond the little drawing-room.", "\"카드루스라는 이름의 중죄인이었지요.\" 당글라르의 얼굴이 살짝 창백해졌다. 안드레아는 작은 응접실 너머의 대기실에 다다랐다."]], [["“But go on signing,”", "\"하지만 서명을 계속하시지요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “I perceive that my story has caused a general emotion, and I beg to apologize to you, baroness, and to Mademoiselle Danglars.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"제 이야기가 좌중을 온통 들쑤셔 놓은 것 같군요. 남작 부인과 당글라르 양께 사과드립니다.\""]], [["The baroness, who had signed, returned the pen to the notary.", "서명을 마친 남작 부인이 공증인에게 펜을 돌려주었다."]], [["“Prince Cavalcanti,”", "\"카발칸티 공작,\""], ["said the latter; “Prince Cavalcanti, where are you?”", "공증인이 말했다. \"카발칸티 공작, 어디 계십니까?\""]], [["“Andrea, Andrea,” repeated several young people, who were already on sufficiently intimate terms with him to call him by his Christian name.", "\"안드레아, 안드레아.\" 이미 그와 이름을 부를 만큼 가까워진 몇몇 젊은이들이 따라 불렀다."]], [["“Call the prince; inform him that it is his turn to sign,”", "\"공작을 불러오게. 이제 그가 서명할 차례라고 알리게.\""], ["cried Danglars to one of the floorkeepers.", "당글라르가 시중꾼 하나에게 외쳤다."]], [["But at the same instant the crowd of guests rushed in alarm into the principal salon as if some frightful monster had entered the apartments, _quærens quem devoret_.", "그러나 바로 그 순간, 손님들이 무리지어 겁에 질린 채 주연회장으로 몰려들었다. 마치 무시무시한 괴물 하나가, 삼킬 자를 찾아 헤매며 방 안으로 들어오기라도 한 듯했다."], ["There was, indeed, reason to retreat, to be alarmed, and to scream.", "과연 물러서고, 놀라고, 비명을 지를 만한 까닭이 있었다."], ["An officer was placing two soldiers at the door of each drawing-room, and was advancing towards Danglars, preceded by a commissary of police, girded with his scarf.", "한 장교가 각 응접실 문마다 병사 둘씩을 배치하고는, 어깨띠를 두른 경찰서장을 앞세운 채 당글라르 쪽으로 다가오고 있었던 것이다."], ["Madame Danglars uttered a scream and fainted.", "당글라르 부인이 비명을 지르며 정신을 잃었다."], ["Danglars, who thought himself threatened (certain consciences are never calm),—Danglars even before his guests showed a countenance of abject terror.", "당글라르는 자신이 위협받는다고 여겼고 ─어떤 양심은 결코 평온하지 못한 법이다─ 손님들 앞에서마저 비굴한 공포로 일그러진 얼굴을 드러냈다."]], [["“What is the matter, sir?”", "\"무슨 일이오, 선생?\""], ["asked Monte Cristo, advancing to meet the commissioner.", "몬테크리스토가 경찰서장을 맞으러 나아가며 물었다."]], [["“Which of you gentlemen,”", "\"여러분 가운데,\""], ["asked the magistrate, without replying to the count, “answers to the name of Andrea Cavalcanti?”", "서장이 백작에게는 대꾸도 하지 않고 물었다. \"안드레아 카발칸티라는 이름을 쓰는 자가 누구입니까?\""]], [["A cry of astonishment was heard from all parts of the room. They searched; they questioned.", "방 안 곳곳에서 경악의 외침이 터져 나왔다. 사람들은 그를 찾았고, 서로 물었다."]], [["“But who then is Andrea Cavalcanti?”", "\"그런데 대체 안드레아 카발칸티가 누구요?\""], ["asked Danglars in amazement.", "당글라르가 어리둥절하여 물었다."]], [["“A galley-slave, escaped from confinement at Toulon.”", "\"툴롱 감옥에서 탈출한 갤리선 죄수입니다.\""]], [["“And what crime has he committed?”", "\"그자가 무슨 죄를 지었소?\""]], [["“He is accused,”", "\"그는,\""], ["said the commissary with his inflexible voice, “of having assassinated the man named Caderousse, his former companion in prison, at the moment he was making his escape from the house of the Count of Monte Cristo.”", "서장이 단호한 목소리로 말했다. \"몬테크리스토 백작 댁에서 도주하던 순간, 옛 감방 동료인 카드루스라는 자를 살해한 혐의를 받고 있습니다.\""]], [["Monte Cristo cast a rapid glance around him. Andrea was gone.", "몬테크리스토가 재빨리 주위를 둘러보았다. 안드레아는 사라지고 없었다."]], [["# Chapter 97. The Departure for Belgium", "# Chapter 97. The Departure for Belgium"]], [["A few minutes after the scene of confusion produced in the salons of M. Danglars by the unexpected appearance of the brigade of soldiers, and by the disclosure which had followed, the mansion was deserted with as much rapidity as if a case of plague or of cholera morbus had broken out among the guests.", "* 라틴어 quærens quem devoret: '삼킬 자를 찾아 헤매며'라는 뜻으로, 성경 베드로전서의 구절에서 왔다. ─옮긴이\n당글라르의 살롱에 헌병대가 느닷없이 들이닥치고 뒤이어 그 정체가 밝혀지면서 일대 혼란이 벌어진 지 몇 분 뒤, 저택은 마치 손님들 사이에 흑사병이나 콜레라가 퍼지기라도 한 듯 순식간에 텅 비어 버렸다."]], [["In a few minutes, through all the doors, down all the staircases, by every exit, everyone hastened to retire, or rather to fly; for it was a situation where the ordinary condolences,—which even the best friends are so eager to offer in great catastrophes,—were seen to be utterly futile.", "모든 문으로, 모든 계단으로, 모든 출구로, 사람들은 너 나 할 것 없이 자리를 떠나려고, 아니 차라리 달아나려고 서둘렀다. 이런 상황에서는 으레 건네는 위로의 말이 — 큰 재난이 닥쳤을 때 절친한 벗들조차 앞다투어 내놓고 싶어 하는 그 위로의 말이 — 아무 소용도 없다는 것이 자명했기 때문이다."], ["There remained in the banker’s house only Danglars, closeted in his study, and making his statement to the officer of gendarmes; Madame Danglars, terrified, in the boudoir with which we are acquainted; and Eugénie, who with haughty air and disdainful lip had retired to her room with her inseparable companion, Mademoiselle Louise d’Armilly.", "은행가의 저택에 남은 사람이라곤 서재에 틀어박혀 헌병 장교에게 진술을 하고 있는 당글라르와, 우리에게 익숙한 그 규방에서 겁에 질려 있는 당글라르 부인, 그리고 오만한 태도와 경멸 어린 입매로 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 동무인 루이즈 다르밀리 양과 함께 자기 방으로 물러난 외제니뿐이었다."]], [["As for the numerous servants (more numerous that evening than usual, for their number was augmented by cooks and butlers from the Café de Paris), venting on their employers their anger at what they termed the insult to which they had been subjected, they collected in groups in the hall, in the kitchens, or in their rooms, thinking very little of their duty, which was thus naturally interrupted.", "수많은 하인들로 말하자면(그날 저녁에는 카페 드 파리에서 온 요리사와 집사들이 더해져 평소보다 그 수가 많았다), 자신들이 당한 모욕이라 일컫는 일에 대한 분노를 주인들에게 쏟아 내며, 자연히 중단된 제 임무 따위는 안중에도 없이 현관에, 부엌에, 또 제 방에 끼리끼리 모여들었다."], ["Of all this household, only two persons deserve our notice; these are Mademoiselle Eugénie Danglars and Mademoiselle Louise d’Armilly.", "이 모든 집안 사람들 가운데 우리가 눈여겨볼 만한 이는 단 둘뿐이니, 바로 외제니 당글라르 양과 루이즈 다르밀리 양이다."]], [["The betrothed had retired, as we said, with haughty air, disdainful lip, and the demeanor of an outraged queen, followed by her companion, who was paler and more disturbed than herself.", "앞서 말했듯이 약혼녀는 오만한 태도와 경멸 어린 입매로, 모욕당한 여왕 같은 거동으로 물러났고, 그녀보다 더 창백하고 더 동요한 동무가 그 뒤를 따랐다."], ["On reaching her room Eugénie locked her door, while Louise fell on a chair.", "방에 이르자 외제니는 문을 잠갔고, 루이즈는 의자에 풀썩 주저앉았다."]], [["“Ah, what a dreadful thing,”", "\"아, 정말 끔찍한 일이야.\""], ["said the young musician; “who would have suspected it? M.", "젊은 음악가가 말했다. \"누가 짐작이나 했겠어?"], ["Andrea Cavalcanti a murderer—a galley-slave escaped—a convict!”", "안드레아 카발칸티 씨가 살인자에, 탈주한 갤리선 죄수에, 죄인이라니!\""]], [["An ironical smile curled the lip of Eugénie.", "빈정거리는 미소가 외제니의 입가에 떠올랐다."], ["“In truth, I was fated,” said she.", "\"정말이지 난 운명이 정해져 있었나 봐.\""], ["“I escaped the Morcerf only to fall into the Cavalcanti.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"모르세르를 피했더니 카발칸티에게 떨어진 셈이니.\""]], [["“Oh, do not confound the two, Eugénie.”", "\"오, 그 둘을 같이 놓고 보지는 마, 외제니.\""]], [["“Hold your tongue!", "\"입 다물어!"], ["The men are all infamous, and I am happy to be able now to do more than detest them—I despise them.”", "남자들이란 죄다 파렴치한 족속이야. 이제 그들을 증오하는 데서 더 나아갈 수 있게 되어 기뻐. 난 그들을 경멸해.\""]], [["“What shall we do?”", "\"우린 어쩌지?\""], ["asked Louise.", "루이즈가 물었다."]], [["“What shall we do?”", "\"우리가 어쩌긴?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"응.\""]], [["“Why, the same we had intended doing three days since—set off.”", "\"뭐긴, 사흘 전부터 하기로 했던 그대로지. 떠나는 거야.\""]], [["“What?—although you are not now going to be married, you intend still——”", "\"뭐라고? 이제 결혼하지 않게 됐는데도, 그래도 여전히……?\""]], [["“Listen, Louise.", "\"들어 봐, 루이즈."], ["I hate this life of the fashionable world, always ordered, measured, ruled, like our music-paper.", "난 이 사교계의 삶이 싫어. 늘 정돈되고, 재어지고, 우리 악보처럼 규칙에 묶여 있잖아."], ["What I have always wished for, desired, and coveted, is the life of an artist, free and independent, relying only on my own resources, and accountable only to myself.", "내가 늘 바라고, 꿈꾸고, 갈망해 온 건 예술가의 삶이야. 자유롭고 독립적이고, 오로지 내 힘에만 기대고, 오직 나 자신에게만 책임지는 삶 말이야."], ["Remain here?", "여기 남아?"], ["What for?—that they may try, a month hence, to marry me again; and to whom?—M.", "무엇 때문에? 한 달 뒤에 또 나를 결혼시키려 들게? 그것도 누구한테?"], ["Debray, perhaps, as it was once proposed. No, Louise, no!", "어쩌면 전에 한 번 거론됐던 대로 드브레 씨한테? 아니, 루이즈, 아니야!"], ["This evening’s adventure will serve for my excuse.", "오늘 저녁의 사달이 내 핑곗거리가 되어 줄 거야."], ["I did not seek one, I did not ask for one. God sends me this, and I hail it joyfully!”", "난 핑계를 찾지도, 청하지도 않았어. 하느님이 이걸 내게 보내 주신 거고, 난 기쁘게 받아들이겠어!\""]], [["“How strong and courageous you are!”", "\"넌 어쩜 그리 강하고 용감하니!\""], ["said the fair, frail girl to her brunette companion.", "가냘프고 여린 금발의 처녀가 거무스름한 머리의 동무에게 말했다."]], [["“Did you not yet know me?", "\"아직도 날 모르겠어?"], ["Come, Louise, let us talk of our affairs.", "자, 루이즈, 우리 일 얘기나 하자."], ["The post-chaise——”", "역마차는……\""]], [["“Was happily bought three days since.”", "\"다행히 사흘 전에 사 뒀잖아.\""]], [["“Have you had it sent where we are to go for it?”", "\"우리가 가서 찾을 곳으로 보내 두라고 했고?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"응.\""]], [["“Our passport?”", "\"여권은?\""]], [["“Here it is.”", "\"여기 있어.\""]], [["And Eugénie, with her usual precision, opened a printed paper, and read:", "외제니는 여느 때처럼 빈틈없는 손길로 인쇄된 종이 한 장을 펼쳐 읽었다."]], [["“M. Léon d’Armilly, twenty years of age; profession, artist; hair black, eyes black; travelling with his sister.”", "\"레옹 다르밀리 씨, 스무 살, 직업은 예술가, 머리 검정, 눈 검정, 누이와 함께 여행 중.\""]], [["“Capital!", "\"훌륭해!"], ["How did you get this passport?”", "이 여권은 어떻게 구했어?\""]], [["“When I went to ask M.", "\"몬테 크리스토 씨한테 로마와 나폴리 극장 지배인들에게 보내는 소개장을 부탁하러 갔을 때, 여자 몸으로 여행하는 게 두렵다고 말씀드렸거든. 그분은 내 마음을 완전히 헤아리시고는 남자 여권을 구해 주겠다고 하셨어."], ["de Monte Cristo for letters to the directors of the theatres at Rome and Naples, I expressed my fears of travelling as a woman; he perfectly understood them, and undertook to procure for me a man’s passport, and two days after I received this, to which I have added with my own hand, ‘travelling with his sister.’” 50035m", "그러고 이틀 뒤에 이걸 받았지. 거기에 내가 직접 손으로 '누이와 함께 여행 중'이라고 덧붙인 거고.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"좋아.\" 외제니가 들뜬 목소리로 말했다."], ["said Eugénie cheerfully, “we have then only to pack up our trunks; we shall start the evening of the signing of the contract, instead of the evening of the wedding—that is all.”", "\"그럼 이제 짐만 꾸리면 돼. 결혼식 날 저녁이 아니라 혼인 계약서에 서명하는 날 저녁에 떠나는 것뿐이야. 그게 다지.\""]], [["“But consider the matter seriously, Eugénie!”", "\"하지만 진지하게 생각해 봐, 외제니!\""]], [["“Oh, I am done with considering!", "\"오, 난 생각하는 건 이제 그만이야!"], ["I am tired of hearing only of market reports, of the end of the month, of the rise and fall of Spanish funds, of Haitian bonds.", "증권 시세니, 월말 결산이니, 스페인 공채의 오르내림이니, 아이티 채권이니 하는 소리만 듣는 데 진력이 났어."], ["Instead of that, Louise—do you understand?—air, liberty, melody of birds, plains of Lombardy, Venetian canals, Roman palaces, the Bay of Naples.", "그런 것 대신, 루이즈, 알겠어? 공기, 자유, 새들의 노랫소리, 롬바르디아의 평원, 베네치아의 운하, 로마의 궁전, 나폴리만이야."], ["How much have we, Louise?”", "우리 가진 게 얼마지, 루이즈?\""]], [["The young girl to whom this question was addressed drew from an inlaid secretaire a small portfolio with a lock, in which she counted twenty-three bank-notes.", "이 질문을 받은 처녀가 상감 세공이 된 책상에서 자물쇠 달린 작은 지갑을 꺼내, 그 안의 지폐 스물세 장을 세었다."]], [["“Twenty-three thousand francs,” said she.", "\"2만 3천 프랑이야.\" 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“And as much, at least, in pearls, diamonds, and jewels,”", "\"거기에 적어도 그만큼은 진주와 다이아몬드, 보석으로 가지고 있지.\""], ["said Eugénie.", "외제니가 말했다."], ["“We are rich.", "\"우린 부자야."], ["With forty-five thousand francs we can live like princesses for two years, and comfortably for four; but before six months—you with your music, and I with my voice—we shall double our capital.", "4만 5천 프랑이면 이태는 공주처럼, 사 년은 넉넉하게 살 수 있어. 게다가 여섯 달도 안 돼서 — 넌 네 음악으로, 난 내 목소리로 — 우리 밑천을 두 배로 불릴 거야."], ["Come, you shall take charge of the money, I of the jewel-box; so that if one of us had the misfortune to lose her treasure, the other would still have hers left.", "자, 넌 돈을 맡고, 난 보석함을 맡자. 그래야 둘 중 하나가 불행히도 제 보물을 잃더라도, 다른 하나에게는 제 몫이 남아 있을 테니까."], ["Now, the portmanteau—let us make haste—the portmanteau!”", "자, 이제 가방이야. 서두르자. 가방!\""]], [["“Stop!”", "\"잠깐!\""], ["said Louise, going to listen at Madame Danglars’ door.", "루이즈가 당글라르 부인의 방문께로 가서 귀를 기울이며 말했다."]], [["“What do you fear?”", "\"뭐가 두려운데?\""]], [["“That we may be discovered.”", "\"우리가 들킬까 봐.\""]], [["“The door is locked.”", "\"문은 잠겨 있어.\""]], [["“They may tell us to open it.”", "\"문 열라고 할지도 모르잖아.\""]], [["“They may if they like, but we will not.”", "\"그러라지, 우린 안 열 거니까.\""]], [["“You are a perfect Amazon, Eugénie!”", "\"넌 정말 아마조네스 같아, 외제니!\""], ["And the two young girls began to heap into a trunk all the things they thought they should require.", "그러고 두 처녀는 챙겨야겠다 싶은 물건들을 죄다 가방 속에 쌓아 넣기 시작했다."]], [["“There now,”", "\"자, 이제.\""], ["said Eugénie, “while I change my costume do you lock the portmanteau.”", "외제니가 말했다. \"내가 옷을 갈아입는 동안 넌 가방을 잠가.\""], ["Louise pressed with all the strength of her little hands on the top of the portmanteau.", "루이즈가 작은 두 손으로 있는 힘을 다해 가방 뚜껑을 눌렀다."]], [["“But I cannot,”", "\"근데 안 돼.\""], ["said she; “I am not strong enough; do you shut it.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"힘이 모자라. 네가 닫아.\""]], [["“Ah, you do well to ask,”", "\"아, 부탁하길 잘했네.\""], ["said Eugénie, laughing; “I forgot that I was Hercules, and you only the pale Omphale!”", "외제니가 웃으며 말했다. \"내가 헤라클레스고 넌 그저 창백한 옴팔레라는 걸 깜빡했지 뭐야!\""]], [["And the young girl, kneeling on the top, pressed the two parts of the portmanteau together, and Mademoiselle d’Armilly passed the bolt of the padlock through.", "그러고 처녀는 가방 위에 무릎을 꿇고 앉아 가방의 두 짝을 한데 눌렀고, 다르밀리 양이 자물쇠 빗장을 끼워 넣었다."], ["When this was done, Eugénie opened a drawer, of which she kept the key, and took from it a wadded violet silk travelling cloak.", "일이 끝나자 외제니는 자기가 열쇠를 지니고 있던 서랍을 열어, 그 안에서 솜을 댄 보라색 비단 여행용 망토를 꺼냈다."]], [["“Here,”", "\"자.\""], ["said she, “you see I have thought of everything; with this cloak you will not be cold.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"봐, 난 다 생각해 뒀어. 이 망토만 있으면 넌 춥지 않을 거야.\""]], [["“But you?”", "\"그럼 넌?\""]], [["“Oh, I am never cold, you know!", "\"오, 알잖아, 난 추위를 안 타."], ["Besides, with these men’s clothes——”", "게다가 이 남자 옷을 입으면……\""]], [["“Will you dress here?”", "\"여기서 갈아입을 거야?\""]], [["“Certainly.”", "\"물론이지.\""]], [["“Shall you have time?”", "\"시간이 되겠어?\""]], [["“Do not be uneasy, you little coward!", "\"걱정 마, 이 겁쟁이야!"], ["All our servants are busy, discussing the grand affair.", "하인들은 죄다 그 대사건을 두고 떠드느라 정신없어."], ["Besides, what is there astonishing, when you think of the grief I ought to be in, that I shut myself up?—tell me!”", "게다가 내가 마땅히 빠져 있어야 할 슬픔을 생각하면, 내가 방에 틀어박혀 있다 한들 놀랄 일이 뭐가 있겠어? 말해 봐!\""]], [["“No, truly—you comfort me.” “Come and help me.”", "\"그래, 정말 그러네. 네 말을 들으니 마음이 놓여.\""]], [["From the same drawer she took a man’s complete costume, from the boots to the coat, and a provision of linen, where there was nothing superfluous, but every requisite.", "\"이리 와서 도와줘.\" 같은 서랍에서 그녀는 장화부터 외투까지 남자 옷 한 벌을 꺼냈고, 군더더기 하나 없이 필요한 것은 다 갖춘 속옷 한 벌도 함께 꺼냈다."], ["Then, with a promptitude which indicated that this was not the first time she had amused herself by adopting the garb of the opposite sex, Eugénie drew on the boots and pantaloons, tied her cravat, buttoned her waistcoat up to the throat, and put on a coat which admirably fitted her beautiful figure.", "그러고는 이성의 옷을 차려입고 즐기는 것이 이번이 처음이 아님을 보여 주는 듯한 날렵한 손놀림으로, 외제니는 장화와 바지를 꿰입고, 넥타이를 매고, 조끼를 목까지 단추로 채우고, 그 아름다운 몸매에 기막히게 들어맞는 외투를 걸쳤다."]], [["“Oh, that is very good—indeed, it is very good!”", "\"오, 아주 멋져. 정말이지 아주 멋져!\""], ["said Louise, looking at her with admiration; “but that beautiful black hair, those magnificent braids, which made all the ladies sigh with envy,—will they go under a man’s hat like the one I see down there?”", "루이즈가 감탄하며 그녀를 바라보았다. \"그런데 그 아름다운 검은 머리, 모든 부인들이 부러워서 한숨짓게 만들던 그 멋진 땋은 머리, 저 아래 보이는 저런 남자 모자 속에 다 들어갈까?\""]], [["“You shall see,” said Eugénie.", "\"두고 봐.\" 외제니가 말했다."], ["And with her left hand seizing the thick mass, which her long fingers could scarcely grasp, she took in her right hand a pair of long scissors, and soon the steel met through the rich and splendid hair, which fell in a cluster at her feet as she leaned back to keep it from her coat.", "그러고는 왼손으로, 긴 손가락으로도 다 움켜쥐기 힘든 그 풍성한 머리 다발을 잡고, 오른손으로 긴 가위를 집어 들었다. 이내 가윗날이 그 풍성하고 눈부신 머리카락을 가르고 지나갔고, 외투에 닿지 않게 몸을 뒤로 젖히자 머리카락은 다발째 그녀의 발치에 떨어졌다."], ["Then she grasped the front hair, which she also cut off, without expressing the least regret; on the contrary, her eyes sparkled with greater pleasure than usual under her ebony eyebrows. 50039m", "이어 그녀는 앞머리를 움켜쥐고 그것마저 잘라 냈는데, 조금도 아쉬운 기색을 내비치지 않았다. 오히려 그 흑단 같은 눈썹 아래의 눈은 여느 때보다 더한 기쁨으로 반짝였다."]], [["“Oh, the magnificent hair!”", "\"오, 저 멋진 머릿결을!\""], ["said Louise, with regret.", "루이즈가 아쉬워하며 말했다."]], [["“And am I not a hundred times better thus?”", "\"이러니까 백배는 더 낫지 않아?\""], ["cried Eugénie, smoothing the scattered curls of her hair, which had now quite a masculine appearance; “and do you not think me handsomer so?”", "외제니가 이제 완연히 남자 같은 모양새가 된, 흩어진 곱슬머리를 매만지며 외쳤다. \"이러니까 내가 더 잘생겨 보이지 않아?\""]], [["“Oh, you are beautiful—always beautiful!”", "\"오, 넌 아름다워. 언제나 아름답지!\""], ["cried Louise.", "루이즈가 외쳤다."], ["“Now, where are you going?”", "\"자, 이제 어디로 갈 거야?\""]], [["“To Brussels, if you like; it is the nearest frontier.", "\"네가 좋다면 브뤼셀로. 거기가 제일 가까운 국경이야."], ["We can go to Brussels, Liège, Aix-la-Chapelle; then up the Rhine to Strasbourg.", "브뤼셀로 갔다가, 리에주, 엑스라샤펠로 가고, 그다음엔 라인강을 거슬러 스트라스부르로 가는 거야."], ["We will cross Switzerland, and go down into Italy by the Saint-Gothard.", "스위스를 가로질러서, 생고타르를 넘어 이탈리아로 내려가는 거지."], ["Will that do?”", "그럼 되겠어?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"응.\""]], [["“What are you looking at?”", "\"뭘 보고 있어?\""]], [["“I am looking at you; indeed you are adorable like that!", "\"너를 보고 있어. 정말이지 그러고 있으니 사랑스럽다!"], ["One would say you were carrying me off.”", "누가 보면 네가 날 납치해 가는 줄 알겠어.\""]], [["“And they would be right, _pardieu!_”", "\"그렇게 보는 게 맞지, 그럼!\""]], [["“Oh, I think you swore, Eugénie.”", "\"오, 너 방금 욕한 것 같은데, 외제니.\""]], [["And the two young girls, whom everyone might have thought plunged in grief, the one on her own account, the other from interest in her friend, burst out laughing, as they cleared away every visible trace of the disorder which had naturally accompanied the preparations for their escape.", "그러고 두 처녀는, 누구라도 슬픔에 잠겨 있으리라 여겼을 — 하나는 제 처지 때문에, 다른 하나는 벗을 염려하느라 — 두 처녀는 까르르 웃음을 터뜨리며, 도주를 준비하느라 자연히 따라온 어질러진 흔적을 눈에 띄는 대로 죄다 치웠다."], ["Then, having blown out the lights, the two fugitives, looking and listening eagerly, with outstretched necks, opened the door of a dressing-room which led by a side staircase down to the yard,—Eugénie going first, and holding with one arm the portmanteau, which by the opposite handle Mademoiselle d’Armilly scarcely raised with both hands.", "그러고 불을 끈 두 도망자는, 목을 길게 빼고 잔뜩 긴장한 채 살피고 귀 기울이며, 옆 계단을 통해 마당으로 이어지는 의상실 문을 열었다. 외제니가 앞장서서 한 팔로 가방을 들었고, 그 반대편 손잡이는 다르밀리 양이 두 손으로도 간신히 들어 올렸다."], ["The yard was empty; the clock was striking twelve.", "마당은 텅 비어 있었고, 시계가 열두 시를 치고 있었다."], ["The porter was not yet gone to bed.", "문지기는 아직 잠자리에 들지 않았다."], ["Eugénie approached softly, and saw the old man sleeping soundly in an armchair in his lodge.", "외제니가 살그머니 다가가 보니, 늙은이는 수위실 안락의자에 앉아 곤히 잠들어 있었다."], ["She returned to Louise, took up the portmanteau, which she had placed for a moment on the ground, and they reached the archway under the shadow of the wall.", "그녀는 루이즈에게 돌아가, 잠시 바닥에 내려놓았던 가방을 다시 들었고, 두 사람은 담장 그늘을 따라 아치형 통로에 이르렀다."]], [["Eugénie concealed Louise in an angle of the gateway, so that if the porter chanced to awake he might see but one person.", "외제니는 문지기가 혹 깨더라도 한 사람밖에 보이지 않도록, 루이즈를 출입구 모퉁이에 숨겼다."], ["Then placing herself in the full light of the lamp which lit the yard:", "그러고는 마당을 비추는 등불 빛 한가운데 서서 말했다."]], [["“Gate!” cried she, with her finest contralto voice, and rapping at the window.", "\"문 좀 열어 주시오!\" 그녀가 가장 고운 콘트랄토 목소리로 외치며 창을 두드렸다."]], [["The porter got up as Eugénie expected, and even advanced some steps to recognize the person who was going out, but seeing a young man striking his boot impatiently with his riding-whip, he opened it immediately.", "외제니가 예상한 대로 문지기가 일어났고, 심지어 나가는 사람이 누구인지 알아보려 몇 걸음 다가오기까지 했으나, 한 젊은이가 채찍으로 제 장화를 초조하게 탁탁 치고 있는 것을 보고는 곧바로 문을 열어 주었다."], ["Louise slid through the half-open gate like a snake, and bounded lightly forward.", "루이즈가 반쯤 열린 문 사이로 뱀처럼 미끄러져 나가 가볍게 앞으로 뛰어나갔다."], ["Eugénie, apparently calm, although in all probability her heart beat somewhat faster than usual, went out in her turn.", "겉으로는 차분해 보였으나 십중팔구 심장이 여느 때보다 좀 더 빠르게 뛰었을 외제니도 뒤이어 나갔다."]], [["A porter was passing and they gave him the portmanteau; then the two young girls, having told him to take it to No. 36, Rue de la Victoire, walked behind this man, whose presence comforted Louise.", "마침 짐꾼 하나가 지나가기에 그들은 그에게 가방을 맡겼다. 그러고 두 처녀는 빅투아르 거리 36번지로 가져다 달라 이르고는, 그 사내의 뒤를 따라 걸었으니, 그가 곁에 있다는 것만으로도 루이즈는 마음이 놓였다."], ["As for Eugénie, she was as strong as a Judith or a Delilah.", "외제니로 말하자면, 유디트나 델릴라처럼 굳세었다."], ["They arrived at the appointed spot.", "그들은 정해진 곳에 다다랐다."], ["Eugénie ordered the porter to put down the portmanteau, gave him some pieces of money, and having rapped at the shutter sent him away.", "외제니는 짐꾼에게 가방을 내려놓으라 이르고 동전 몇 닢을 쥐여 준 뒤, 덧창을 두드리고는 그를 돌려보냈다."], ["The shutter where Eugénie had rapped was that of a little laundress, who had been previously warned, and was not yet gone to bed.", "외제니가 두드린 덧창은 미리 언질을 받아 아직 잠자리에 들지 않은 어느 젊은 세탁부의 것이었다."], ["She opened the door.", "그녀가 문을 열었다."]], [["“Mademoiselle,”", "\"아가씨.\""], ["said Eugénie, “let the porter get the post-chaise from the coach-house, and fetch some post-horses from the hotel.", "외제니가 말했다. \"짐꾼더러 마차 보관소에서 역마차를 가져오고, 호텔에서 역마를 데려오라고 해 주세요."], ["Here are five francs for his trouble.”", "수고비로 5프랑 여기 있어요.\""]], [["“Indeed,”", "\"정말이지.\""], ["said Louise, “I admire you, and I could almost say respect you.”", "루이즈가 말했다. \"난 네가 감탄스러워. 거의 우러러볼 지경이야.\""], ["The laundress looked on in astonishment, but as she had been promised twenty louis, she made no remark.", "세탁부는 놀란 눈으로 지켜보았으나, 20루이를 주기로 약속받은 터라 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["In a quarter of an hour the porter returned with a post-boy and horses, which were harnessed, and put in the post-chaise in a minute, while the porter fastened the portmanteau on with the assistance of a cord and strap.", "십오 분쯤 지나 짐꾼이 마부 한 명과 말들을 데리고 돌아왔다. 말들은 순식간에 멍에를 씌워 역마차에 매였고, 그동안 짐꾼은 끈과 가죽띠를 써서 가방을 단단히 동여맸다."]], [["“Here is the passport,”", "\"여권 여기 있습니다.\""], ["said the postilion, “which way are we going, young gentleman?”", "마부가 말했다. \"어느 길로 갈까요, 젊은 양반?\""]], [["“To Fontainebleau,”", "\"퐁텐블로로.\""], ["replied Eugénie with an almost masculine voice.", "외제니가 거의 남자 같은 목소리로 대답했다."]], [["“What do you say?” said Louise.", "\"뭐라고?\" 루이즈가 말했다."]], [["“I am giving them the slip,”", "\"따돌리려는 거야.\""], ["said Eugénie; “this woman to whom we have given twenty louis may betray us for forty; we will soon alter our direction.”", "외제니가 말했다. \"우리가 20루이를 준 저 여자가 40루이를 받으면 우릴 팔아넘길 수도 있잖아. 곧 방향을 바꿀 거야.\""]], [["And the young girl jumped into the britzka, which was admirably arranged for sleeping in, without scarcely touching the step.", "그러고 처녀는 발판에 거의 발을 대지도 않고 마차에 훌쩍 올라탔으니, 그 마차는 안에서 잠을 자기에 더없이 알맞게 꾸며져 있었다."]], [["“You are always right,” said the music teacher, seating herself by the side of her friend.", "\"넌 언제나 옳아.\" 음악 교사가 벗의 곁에 앉으며 말했다."]], [["A quarter of an hour afterwards the postilion, having been put in the right road, passed with a crack of his whip through the gateway of the Barrière Saint-Martin.", "십오 분 뒤, 제대로 된 길로 들어선 마부가 채찍을 한 번 휘둘러 생마르탱 관문의 출입구를 빠져나갔다."]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아.\""], ["said Louise, breathing freely, “here we are out of Paris.”", "루이즈가 후련하게 숨을 내쉬며 말했다. \"이제 파리를 벗어났네.\""]], [["“Yes, my dear, the abduction is an accomplished fact,”", "\"그래, 얘. 납치는 완수된 사실이 됐어.\""], ["replied Eugénie.", "외제니가 대답했다."]], [["“Yes, and without violence,”", "\"그래, 그것도 폭력 없이.\""], ["said Louise.", "루이즈가 말했다."]], [["“I shall bring that forward as an extenuating circumstance,” replied Eugénie.", "\"그건 정상 참작의 사유로 내세워야지.\" 외제니가 대답했다."]], [["These words were lost in the noise which the carriage made in rolling over the pavement of La Villette. M. Danglars no longer had a daughter.", "이 말은 라빌레트의 포석 위를 구르는 마차 소리에 묻혀 버렸다. 당글라르 씨에게는 이제 딸이 없었다."]], [["# Chapter 98. The Bell and Bottle Tavern", "# Chapter 98. The Bell and Bottle Tavern"]], [["And now let us leave Mademoiselle Danglars and her friend pursuing their way to Brussels, and return to poor Andrea Cavalcanti, so inopportunely interrupted in his rise to fortune.", "이제 당글라르 양과 그녀의 친구가 브뤼셀로 향하도록 내버려 두고, 출세의 길에 오르던 차에 그토록 야속하게 가로막힌 가엾은 안드레아 카발칸티에게로 돌아가자."], ["Notwithstanding his youth, Master Andrea was a very skilful and intelligent boy.", "안드레아 도련님은 나이는 어렸어도 무척 영리하고 머리가 잘 도는 청년이었다."], ["We have seen that on the first rumor which reached the salon he had gradually approached the door, and crossing two or three rooms at last disappeared.", "우리는 이미 그가 살롱에 처음 소문이 닿자마자 슬그머니 문 쪽으로 다가가, 방을 두세 개 가로질러 마침내 모습을 감춘 것을 보았다."], ["But we have forgotten to mention one circumstance, which nevertheless ought not to be omitted; in one of the rooms he crossed, the _trousseau_ of the bride-elect was on exhibition.", "그런데 한 가지 사정을 빠뜨렸으니, 그것은 결코 흘려서는 안 될 일이다. 그가 지나친 방들 가운데 하나에는 신부가 될 사람의 혼수가 진열되어 있었다."], ["There were caskets of diamonds, cashmere shawls, Valenciennes lace, English veils, and in fact all the tempting things, the bare mention of which makes the hearts of young girls bound with joy, and which is called the _corbeille_.22 Now, in passing through this room, Andrea proved himself not only to be clever and intelligent, but also provident, for he helped himself to the most valuable of the ornaments before him.", "다이아몬드 함이며, 캐시미어 숄, 발랑시엔 레이스, 영국제 베일, 한마디로 입에 올리기만 해도 처녀들의 가슴이 기쁨으로 뛰게 만드는 온갖 탐스러운 물건들, 이른바 코르베유였다. 그런데 안드레아는 이 방을 지나면서 자신이 영리하고 머리가 좋을 뿐 아니라 앞날을 내다볼 줄도 안다는 것을 증명했다. 눈앞에 놓인 장신구들 가운데 가장 값진 것을 챙겼던 것이다."]], [["Furnished with this plunder, Andrea leaped with a lighter heart from the window, intending to slip through the hands of the gendarmes.", "이 노획물을 갖추고 나니 안드레아는 한결 가벼운 마음으로 창문에서 뛰어내렸고, 헌병들의 손아귀를 빠져나갈 작정이었다."], ["Tall and well proportioned as an ancient gladiator, and muscular as a Spartan, he walked for a quarter of an hour without knowing where to direct his steps, actuated by the sole idea of getting away from the spot where if he lingered he knew that he would surely be taken.", "고대의 검투사처럼 키가 크고 균형 잡힌 몸매에, 스파르타 사람처럼 근육질인 그는 어디로 발길을 옮겨야 할지도 모른 채 십오 분을 걸었다. 머뭇거렸다가는 틀림없이 붙잡히고 마는 그 자리에서 멀어져야 한다는 생각 하나에만 사로잡혀 있었다."], ["Having passed through the Rue du Mont-Blanc, guided by the instinct which leads thieves always to take the safest path, he found himself at the end of the Rue La Fayette. There he stopped, breathless and panting.", "도둑들을 늘 가장 안전한 길로 이끄는 본능에 따라 몽블랑가를 지나치고 나니, 그는 라파예트가의 끝에 와 있었다. 거기서 그는 숨이 차고 헐떡이며 걸음을 멈추었다."], ["He was quite alone; on one side was the vast wilderness of the Saint-Lazare, on the other, Paris enshrouded in darkness.", "그는 완전히 혼자였다. 한쪽에는 생라자르의 광막한 황무지가, 다른 한쪽에는 어둠에 휩싸인 파리가 펼쳐져 있었다."]], [["“Am I to be captured?”", "\"내가 붙잡히고 말 것인가?\""], ["he cried; “no, not if I can use more activity than my enemies.", "그가 외쳤다. \"아니야, 적들보다 더 민첩하게 움직일 수만 있다면 그럴 일은 없어."], ["My safety is now a mere question of speed.”", "이제 내 목숨은 그저 속도에 달린 문제일 뿐이다.\""]], [["At this moment he saw a cab at the top of the Faubourg Poissonnière.", "바로 그때, 그는 푸아소니에르 변두리 위쪽에 합승마차 한 대가 있는 것을 보았다."], ["The dull driver, smoking his pipe, was plodding along toward the limits of the Faubourg Saint-Denis, where no doubt he ordinarily had his station.", "굼뜬 마부는 파이프를 물고 생드니 변두리의 경계 쪽으로 느릿느릿 가고 있었으니, 필시 평소 그곳에 마차를 대 두는 모양이었다."]], [["“Ho, friend!”", "\"이보시오, 친구!\""], ["said Benedetto.", "베네데토가 말했다."]], [["“What do you want, sir?”", "\"무슨 일이십니까, 선생?\""], ["asked the driver.", "마부가 물었다."]], [["“Is your horse tired?”", "\"당신 말이 지쳤소?\""]], [["“Tired? oh, yes, tired enough—he has done nothing the whole of this blessed day!", "\"지쳤느냐고요? 아, 그럼요, 지칠 만도 하지요."], ["Four wretched fares, and twenty sous over, making in all seven francs, are all that I have earned, and I ought to take ten to the owner.”", "이 빌어먹을 하루 종일 한 일이 없으니까요! 형편없는 손님 넷에 이십 수가 더 붙어, 다 합쳐야 칠 프랑밖에 못 벌었습죠. 주인한테는 십 프랑을 갖다 바쳐야 하는데 말입니다.\""]], [["“Will you add these twenty francs to the seven you have?”", "\"당신이 번 칠 프랑에 이 이십 프랑을 보태 주면 어떻겠소?\""]], [["“With pleasure, sir; twenty francs are not to be despised.", "\"기꺼이 받지요, 선생. 이십 프랑이면 무시할 액수가 아닙니다요."], ["Tell me what I am to do for this.”", "그 대신 제가 뭘 하면 됩니까?\""]], [["“A very easy thing, if your horse isn’t tired.”", "\"아주 쉬운 일이오, 당신 말이 지치지만 않았다면.\""]], [["“I tell you he’ll go like the wind,—only tell me which way to drive.”", "\"바람처럼 달릴 거라니까요. 어느 쪽으로 몰면 되는지만 말씀하십쇼.\""]], [["“Towards the Louvres.”", "\"루브르 쪽으로.\""]], [["“Ah, I know the way—you get good sweetened rum over there.”", "\"아, 그 길은 압니다. 거기 가면 단 럼주를 맛 좋게 한잔할 수 있지요.\""]], [["“Exactly so; I merely wish to overtake one of my friends, with whom I am going to hunt tomorrow at Chapelle-en-Serval.", "\"바로 그렇소. 내일 샤펠앙세르발에서 함께 사냥할 친구 하나를 따라잡으려는 것뿐이오."], ["He should have waited for me here with a cabriolet till half-past eleven; it is twelve, and, tired of waiting, he must have gone on.”", "그가 이륜마차를 끌고 열한 시 반까지 여기서 나를 기다리기로 했는데, 지금이 열두 시니, 기다리다 지쳐서 먼저 떠난 게 분명하오.\""]], [["“It is likely.”", "\"그럴 만하지요.\""]], [["“Well, will you try and overtake him?”", "\"자, 그를 따라잡아 보겠소?\""]], [["“Nothing I should like better.”", "\"그보다 더 좋은 일이 어디 있겠습니까.\""]], [["“If you do not overtake him before we reach Bourget you shall have twenty francs; if not before Louvres, thirty.”", "\"부르제에 닿기 전에 따라잡으면 이십 프랑을 주고, 루브르 전에 잡으면 삼십 프랑을 주겠소.\""]], [["“And if we do overtake him?”", "\"그럼 정말로 따라잡으면요?\""]], [["“Forty,”", "\"사십 프랑.\""], ["said Andrea, after a moment’s hesitation, at the end of which he remembered that he might safely promise.", "안드레아가 잠시 망설인 끝에 말했다. 그러고는 그 정도는 마음 놓고 약속해도 된다는 것을 떠올렸다."]], [["“That’s all right,”", "\"좋습니다요.\""], ["said the man; “hop in, and we’re off! Who-o-o-pla!”", "마부가 말했다. \"어서 타십쇼, 출발합시다! 이랴ー!\""]], [["Andrea got into the cab, which passed rapidly through the Faubourg Saint-Denis, along the Faubourg Saint-Martin, crossed the barrier, and threaded its way through the interminable Villette.", "안드레아가 마차에 올라타자, 마차는 빠르게 생드니 변두리를 지나 생마르탱 변두리를 따라 달리고, 시문을 빠져나가, 끝없이 이어진 라빌레트를 누비고 나아갔다."], ["They never overtook the chimerical friend, yet Andrea frequently inquired of people on foot whom he passed and at the inns which were not yet closed, for a green cabriolet and bay horse; and as there are a great many cabriolets to be seen on the road to the Low Countries, and as nine-tenths of them are green, the inquiries increased at every step.", "끝내 그 허깨비 같은 친구는 따라잡지 못했지만, 안드레아는 길에서 마주친 행인들과 아직 문을 닫지 않은 여관에 자주 초록빛 이륜마차와 밤색 말을 보지 못했느냐고 물었다. 그런데 저지대 지방으로 가는 길에는 이륜마차가 무수히 많은 데다, 그중 열에 아홉은 초록빛이었으므로, 그 물음은 걸음을 옮길 때마다 늘어났다."], ["Everyone had just seen it pass; it was only five hundred, two hundred, one hundred steps in advance; at length they reached it, but it was not the friend.", "누구나 방금 그것이 지나갔다고 했다. 오백 걸음, 이백 걸음, 백 걸음 앞에 있다고 했다. 마침내 따라잡고 보면, 그건 그 친구가 아니었다."], ["Once the cab was also passed by a calash rapidly whirled along by two post-horses.", "한번은 두 마리 역마가 빠르게 끄는 사륜마차가 합승마차를 앞질러 가기도 했다."]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["said Cavalcanti to himself, “if I only had that britzka, those two good post-horses, and above all the passport that carries them on!”", "카발칸티가 속으로 중얼거렸다. \"저 사륜마차와 저 좋은 역마 두 마리, 무엇보다 저들을 거침없이 달리게 해 주는 여권만 있다면!\""], ["And he sighed deeply.", "그러고는 깊이 한숨을 내쉬었다."]], [["The calash contained Mademoiselle Danglars and Mademoiselle d’Armilly.", "그 사륜마차에는 당글라르 양과 다르밀리 양이 타고 있었다."]], [["“Hurry, hurry!”", "\"서둘러, 서둘러요!\""], ["said Andrea, “we must overtake him soon.”", "안드레아가 말했다. \"곧 따라잡아야 하오.\""]], [["And the poor horse resumed the desperate gallop it had kept up since leaving the barrier, and arrived steaming at Louvres.", "그러자 가엾은 말은 시문을 떠난 뒤로 줄곧 이어 온 필사적인 질주를 다시 시작했고, 김을 펄펄 내뿜으며 루브르에 다다랐다."]], [["“Certainly,”", "\"분명코,\""], ["said Andrea, “I shall not overtake my friend, but I shall kill your horse, therefore I had better stop.", "안드레아가 말했다. \"내 친구는 못 따라잡겠지만, 당신 말은 죽이고 말겠군. 그러니 여기서 멈추는 게 낫겠소."], ["Here are thirty francs; I will sleep at the _Cheval Rouge_, and will secure a place in the first coach.", "자, 여기 삼십 프랑이오. 나는 르 슈발 루주에서 자고, 첫 마차에 자리를 잡으리다."], ["Good-night, friend.”", "잘 자시오, 친구.\""]], [["And Andrea, after placing six pieces of five francs each in the man’s hand, leaped lightly on to the pathway.", "그러고서 안드레아는 오 프랑짜리 동전 여섯 닢을 마부의 손에 쥐여 주고는, 가뿐하게 길섶으로 뛰어내렸다."], ["The cabman joyfully pocketed the sum, and turned back on his road to Paris.", "마부는 기쁘게 그 돈을 주머니에 넣고 파리로 가는 길로 되돌아갔다."], ["Andrea pretended to go towards the hotel of the _Cheval Rouge_, but after leaning an instant against the door, and hearing the last sound of the cab, which was disappearing from view, he went on his road, and with a lusty stride soon traversed the space of two leagues.", "안드레아는 르 슈발 루주 여관 쪽으로 가는 척했지만, 잠시 문에 기대어 시야에서 사라져 가는 마차의 마지막 소리를 듣고 나서는, 다시 길을 나서 우렁찬 걸음으로 이내 이 리외에 이르는 거리를 가로질렀다."], ["Then he rested; he must be near Chapelle-en-Serval, where he pretended to be going.", "그러고 나서 그는 쉬었다. 가려던 척했던 샤펠앙세르발 가까이에 와 있을 터였다."]], [["It was not fatigue that stayed Andrea here; it was that he might form some resolution, adopt some plan.", "여기서 안드레아의 발을 붙든 것은 피로가 아니었다. 무언가 결심을 세우고, 어떤 계획을 짜기 위해서였다."], ["It would be impossible to make use of a diligence, equally so to engage post-horses; to travel either way a passport was necessary.", "역마차를 이용하기란 불가능했고, 역마를 빌리는 것도 마찬가지였다. 어느 쪽으로 가든 여권이 필요했다."], ["It was still more impossible to remain in the department of the Oise, one of the most open and strictly guarded in France; this was quite out of the question, especially to a man like Andrea, perfectly conversant with criminal matters.", "우아즈 도에 머무는 것은 더더욱 불가능했다. 프랑스에서 가장 트여 있고 가장 삼엄하게 감시되는 곳 가운데 하나였기 때문이다. 그것은 아예 말도 안 되는 일이었고, 범죄에 관해서라면 훤히 꿰고 있는 안드레아 같은 사람에게는 더욱 그러했다."]], [["He sat down by the side of the moat, buried his face in his hands and reflected.", "그는 해자 옆에 앉아 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 생각에 잠겼다. 십 분 뒤 그가 고개를 들었다."], ["Ten minutes after he raised his head; his resolution was made.", "결심이 섰다. 그는 외투에 흙을 좀 끼얹었다."], ["He threw some dust over the topcoat, which he had found time to unhook from the antechamber and button over his ball costume, and going to Chapelle-en-Serval he knocked loudly at the door of the only inn in the place.", "무도회 의상 위에 단추를 채워 걸쳐 입은 그 외투는, 미리 현관 옷걸이에서 떼어 챙길 짬이 있었던 것이다. 그러고는 샤펠앙세르발로 가서, 그 마을의 단 하나뿐인 여관 문을 요란하게 두드렸다."]], [["The host opened.", "주인이 문을 열었다."]], [["“My friend,”", "\"여보시오,\""], ["said Andrea, “I was coming from Mortefontaine to Senlis, when my horse, which is a troublesome creature, stumbled and threw me.", "안드레아가 말했다. \"모르트퐁텐에서 상리스로 가던 참인데, 성가신 놈인 내 말이 발을 헛디뎌 나를 내동댕이쳤소."], ["I must reach Compiègne tonight, or I shall cause deep anxiety to my family.", "오늘 밤 안으로 콩피에뉴에 닿지 못하면 우리 식구가 몹시 애를 태울 거요."], ["Could you let me hire a horse of you?”", "혹시 말 한 필을 빌려줄 수 있겠소?\""]], [["An innkeeper has always a horse to let, whether it be good or bad.", "여관 주인이란 좋든 나쁘든 빌려줄 말 한 필쯤은 늘 가지고 있는 법이다."], ["The host called the stable-boy, and ordered him to saddle _Le Blanc_ then he awoke his son, a child of seven years, whom he ordered to ride before the gentleman and bring back the horse.", "주인은 마부 소년을 불러 르 블랑에 안장을 얹으라 이르고, 일곱 살배기 아들을 깨워 신사 앞에 태우고 가서 말을 도로 끌고 오라 일렀다."], ["Andrea gave the innkeeper twenty francs, and in taking them from his pocket dropped a visiting card.", "안드레아는 주인에게 이십 프랑을 주었는데, 주머니에서 그것을 꺼내다가 명함 한 장을 떨어뜨렸다."], ["This belonged to one of his friends at the Café de Paris, so that the innkeeper, picking it up after Andrea had left, was convinced that he had let his horse to the Count of Mauléon, 25 Rue Saint-Dominique, that being the name and address on the card.", "그것은 카페 드 파리에서 알게 된 친구 가운데 하나의 것이었으므로, 안드레아가 떠난 뒤 그것을 주운 주인은 자신이 생도미니크가 25번지에 사는 몰레옹 백작에게 말을 빌려준 것이라 굳게 믿게 되었다. 명함에 적힌 이름과 주소가 바로 그것이었기 때문이다."]], [["_Le Blanc_ was not a fast animal, but he kept up an easy, steady pace; in three hours and a half Andrea had traversed the nine leagues which separated him from Compiègne, and four o’clock struck as he reached the place where the coaches stop.", "르 블랑은 빠른 짐승은 아니었지만, 편안하고 한결같은 걸음을 유지했다. 세 시간 반 만에 안드레아는 자신을 콩피에뉴와 갈라놓던 구 리외를 가로질렀고, 마차가 서는 곳에 다다랐을 때 네 시를 알리는 종이 울렸다."], ["There is an excellent tavern at Compiègne, well remembered by those who have ever been there.", "콩피에뉴에는 한 번이라도 가 본 사람이라면 잊지 못할 훌륭한 여관이 하나 있다."], ["Andrea, who had often stayed there in his rides about Paris, recollected the Bell and Bottle inn; he turned around, saw the sign by the light of a reflected lamp, and having dismissed the child, giving him all the small coin he had about him, he began knocking at the door, very reasonably concluding that having now three or four hours before him he had best fortify himself against the fatigues of the morrow by a sound sleep and a good supper.", "파리 일대를 말로 돌아다니며 그곳에 자주 묵었던 안드레아는 라 클로슈 에 라 부테유 여관을 떠올렸다. 그는 몸을 돌려, 반사된 등불빛에 간판을 보고는, 가진 잔돈을 모조리 아이에게 쥐여 주어 돌려보낸 뒤, 문을 두드리기 시작했다. 이제 서너 시간의 여유가 있으니, 푹 자고 잘 먹어 두어 내일의 고생에 대비하는 편이 낫다는 지극히 합당한 판단에서였다."], ["A waiter opened the door.", "종업원이 문을 열었다."]], [["“My friend,”", "\"여보시오,\""], ["said Andrea, “I have been dining at Saint-Jean-aux-Bois, and expected to catch the coach which passes by at midnight, but like a fool I have lost my way, and have been walking for the last four hours in the forest.", "안드레아가 말했다. \"생장오부아에서 저녁을 들고, 자정에 지나가는 마차를 잡을 생각이었소. 그런데 바보같이 길을 잃어 지난 네 시간을 숲속에서 헤맸지 뭐요."], ["Show me into one of those pretty little rooms which overlook the court, and bring me a cold fowl and a bottle of Bordeaux.”", "안뜰이 내려다보이는 그 예쁘장한 작은 방들 가운데 하나로 안내해 주고, 차가운 닭고기 한 마리와 보르도 한 병을 가져다주시오.\""]], [["The waiter had no suspicions; Andrea spoke with perfect composure, he had a cigar in his mouth, and his hands in the pocket of his top coat; his clothes were fashionably made, his chin smooth, his boots irreproachable; he looked merely as if he had stayed out very late, that was all.", "종업원은 아무 의심도 하지 않았다. 안드레아는 완벽하게 침착한 어조로 말했고, 입에는 시가를 물고, 두 손은 외투 주머니에 찔러 넣고 있었다. 옷은 멋스럽게 지어졌고, 턱은 매끈했으며, 부츠는 흠잡을 데가 없었다."], ["While the waiter was preparing his room, the hostess arose; Andrea assumed his most charming smile, and asked if he could have No.", "그저 밤늦게까지 밖에서 놀다 온 사람으로 보일 뿐, 그 이상은 아니었다. 종업원이 방을 준비하는 동안 여주인이 일어났다."], ["3, which he had occupied on his last stay at Compiègne. Unfortunately, No. 3 was engaged by a young man who was travelling with his sister.", "안드레아는 더없이 매력적인 미소를 지으며, 지난번 콩피에뉴에 묵었을 때 썼던 3호실을 쓸 수 있겠느냐고 물었다."], ["Andrea appeared in despair, but consoled himself when the hostess assured him that No.", "공교롭게도 3호실은 누이와 함께 여행 중인 젊은이 하나가 이미 들었다고 했다."], ["7, prepared for him, was situated precisely the same as No. 3, and while warming his feet and chatting about the last races at Chantilly, he waited until they announced his room to be ready.", "안드레아는 낙담한 기색을 보였으나, 그를 위해 마련한 7호실이 3호실과 똑같은 자리에 있다는 여주인의 말에 마음을 달랬다. 그러고는 발을 녹이며 샹티이의 지난 경마 이야기를 나누면서, 방이 준비되었다는 기별을 기다렸다."]], [["Andrea had not spoken without cause of the pretty rooms looking out upon the court of the Bell Hotel, which with its triple galleries like those of a theatre, with the jessamine and clematis twining round the light columns, forms one of the prettiest entrances to an inn that you can imagine.", "안드레아가 라 클로슈 여관의 안뜰이 내려다보이는 예쁜 방들 이야기를 꺼낸 데에는 다 까닭이 있었다. 극장의 그것처럼 삼 층으로 둘러친 회랑에, 가느다란 기둥마다 재스민과 으아리가 휘감겨, 누구라도 상상할 수 있는 가장 아름다운 여관 입구의 하나를 이루고 있었던 것이다."], ["The fowl was tender, the wine old, the fire clear and sparkling, and Andrea was surprised to find himself eating with as good an appetite as though nothing had happened.", "닭고기는 연했고, 포도주는 잘 익었으며, 불은 환하게 반짝였다. 안드레아는 아무 일도 없었던 것처럼 입맛 좋게 먹고 있는 자신에게 스스로 놀랐다."], ["Then he went to bed and almost immediately fell into that deep sleep which is sure to visit men of twenty years of age, even when they are torn with remorse.", "그러고 나서 그는 잠자리에 들었고, 거의 곧바로, 스무 살 청년에게라면 회한에 가슴이 찢기는 처지에서도 어김없이 찾아드는 그 깊은 잠에 빠졌다."], ["Now, here we are obliged to own that Andrea ought to have felt remorse, but that he did not.", "그런데 우리는 여기서, 안드레아가 마땅히 회한을 느꼈어야 했음에도 그러지 않았다는 사실을 인정하지 않을 수 없다."]], [["This was the plan which had appealed to him to afford the best chance of his security.", "이것이 그가 보기에 가장 안전을 기할 만한 계획이었다."], ["Before daybreak he would awake, leave the inn after rigorously paying his bill, and reaching the forest, he would, under pretence of making studies in painting, test the hospitality of some peasants, procure himself the dress of a woodcutter and a hatchet, casting off the lion’s skin to assume that of the woodman; then, with his hands covered with dirt, his hair darkened by means of a leaden comb, his complexion embrowned with a preparation for which one of his old comrades had given him the recipe, he intended, by following the wooded districts, to reach the nearest frontier, walking by night and sleeping in the day in the forests and quarries, and only entering inhabited regions to buy a loaf from time to time.", "동트기 전에 깨어, 셈을 꼼꼼히 치르고 여관을 떠나, 숲에 이르면 그림 공부를 하는 척하면서 어느 농부의 환대를 시험해 나무꾼의 옷과 도끼를 손에 넣고, 사자 가죽을 벗어 던지고 나무꾼의 가죽을 뒤집어쓰는 것이었다. 그런 다음 손에는 흙을 묻히고, 머리카락은 납빗으로 검게 물들이고, 안색은 옛 동료 하나가 비법을 일러 준 약으로 거뭇하게 그을려, 숲이 우거진 지대를 따라가 가장 가까운 국경에 닿을 작정이었다. 밤에는 걷고 낮에는 숲과 채석장에서 자며, 이따금 빵 한 덩이를 사러 갈 때나 사람 사는 고장에 들어갈 셈이었다."]], [["Once past the frontier, Andrea proposed making money of his diamonds; and by uniting the proceeds to ten bank-notes he always carried about with him in case of accident, he would then find himself possessor of about 50,000 livres, which he philosophically considered as no very deplorable condition after all.", "일단 국경을 넘으면, 안드레아는 다이아몬드를 돈으로 바꿀 작정이었다. 그것을 만일을 대비해 늘 몸에 지니고 다니는 지폐 열 장과 합치면, 약 오만 리브르의 소유자가 되는 셈이니, 그는 이를 두고 따져 보아도 그리 한심한 처지는 아니라고 태평하게 여겼다."], ["Moreover, he reckoned much on the interest of the Danglars to hush up the rumor of their own misadventures.", "게다가 그는 당글라르 일가가 자기네 망신을 둘러싼 소문을 덮으려고 애쓰리라는 데에 크게 기대를 걸고 있었다."], ["These were the reasons which, added to the fatigue, caused Andrea to sleep so soundly.", "이런 이유들이 피로에 더해져, 안드레아는 그토록 깊이 잠들었던 것이다."], ["In order that he might wake early he did not close the shutters, but contented himself with bolting the door and placing on the table an unclasped and long-pointed knife, whose temper he well knew, and which was never absent from him.", "일찍 깨려고 그는 덧창을 닫지 않고, 문에 빗장만 지른 채, 탁자 위에 칼날이 길고 뾰족한, 잘 벼려진 줄을 잘 아는, 한시도 몸에서 떼어 놓는 법이 없는 칼 한 자루를 펼쳐 놓는 것으로 만족했다."]], [["About seven in the morning Andrea was awakened by a ray of sunlight, which played, warm and brilliant, upon his face.", "아침 일곱 시쯤, 안드레아는 따뜻하고 눈부시게 얼굴 위에서 노니는 햇살 한 줄기에 잠을 깼다."], ["In all well-organized brains, the predominating idea—and there always is one—is sure to be the last thought before sleeping, and the first upon waking in the morning.", "잘 정돈된 머릿속이라면 어디나, 가장 으뜸가는 생각이ー그런 생각은 늘 하나 있게 마련이다ー잠들기 전 마지막 생각이자 아침에 깨어나 처음 떠오르는 생각이 되는 법이다."], ["Andrea had scarcely opened his eyes when his predominating idea presented itself, and whispered in his ear that he had slept too long.", "안드레아가 눈을 채 뜨기도 전에, 그 으뜸가는 생각이 떠올라 너무 오래 잤다고 귓가에 속삭였다."], ["He jumped out of bed and ran to the window.", "그는 침대에서 벌떡 일어나 창문으로 달려갔다."], ["A gendarme was crossing the court.", "헌병 하나가 안뜰을 가로지르고 있었다."], ["A gendarme is one of the most striking objects in the world, even to a man void of uneasiness; but for one who has a timid conscience, and with good cause too, the yellow, blue, and white uniform is really very alarming.", "헌병이란, 아무 거리낌 없는 사람에게조차 세상에서 가장 눈에 확 띄는 존재다. 하물며 양심이 켕기고, 또 그럴 만한 까닭이 있는 사람에게라면, 노랑과 파랑과 흰빛의 제복은 정말이지 등골이 오싹할 만큼 무서운 것이다."]], [["“Why is that gendarme there?”", "\"저 헌병이 왜 거기 있지?\""], ["asked Andrea of himself.", "안드레아가 스스로에게 물었다."]], [["Then, all at once, he replied, with that logic which the reader has, doubtless, remarked in him, “There is nothing astonishing in seeing a gendarme at an inn; instead of being astonished, let me dress myself.”", "그러고는 대뜸, 독자가 분명 그에게서 눈여겨보았을 그 논리로 스스로에게 대답했다. \"여관에 헌병이 있는 게 뭐 놀랄 일이라고. 놀랄 게 아니라 어서 옷이나 입자.\""], ["And the youth dressed himself with a facility his valet de chambre had failed to rob him of during the two months of fashionable life he had led in Paris.", "그러고서 청년은, 파리에서 두 달간 누린 멋쟁이 생활 동안에도 시종이 끝내 빼앗아 가지 못한 그 손쉬운 솜씨로 옷을 갖춰 입었다."]], [["“Now then,”", "\"자 그럼,\""], ["said Andrea, while dressing himself, “I’ll wait till he leaves, and then I’ll slip away.”", "안드레아가 옷을 입으며 말했다. \"저자가 갈 때까지 기다렸다가 살짝 빠져나가야지.\""]], [["50047m\nAnd, saying this, Andrea, who had now put on his boots and cravat, stole gently to the window, and a second time lifted up the muslin curtain.", "그렇게 말하면서, 이제 부츠를 신고 넥타이까지 맨 안드레아는 살그머니 창가로 다가가 두 번째로 모슬린 커튼을 들어 올렸다."], ["Not only was the first gendarme still there, but the young man now perceived a second yellow, blue, and white uniform at the foot of the staircase, the only one by which he could descend, while a third, on horseback, holding a musket in his fist, was posted as a sentinel at the great street-door which alone afforded the means of egress.", "처음의 헌병이 여전히 그 자리에 있을 뿐 아니라, 청년은 이제 내려갈 수 있는 유일한 길인 계단 발치에 또 하나의 노랑과 파랑과 흰빛 제복이 서 있는 것을 알아차렸다. 게다가 말을 탄 세 번째 헌병이 주먹에 총을 쥔 채, 빠져나갈 유일한 통로인 큰 거리 쪽 대문에 보초로 서 있었다."], ["The appearance of the third gendarme settled the matter, for a crowd of curious loungers was extended before him, effectually blocking the entrance to the hotel.", "세 번째 헌병의 등장이 일을 결판 지었다. 호기심에 찬 구경꾼들이 떼 지어 그 앞에 늘어서서 여관 입구를 완전히 막고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["“They’re after me!” was Andrea’s first thought.", "\"나를 노리는구나!\" 안드레아의 머리에 가장 먼저 떠오른 생각이었다."], ["“_Diable!_”", "\"제기랄!\""]], [["A pallor overspread the young man’s forehead, and he looked around him with anxiety.", "청년의 이마에 핏기가 가셨고, 그는 불안하게 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["His room, like all those on the same floor, had but one outlet to the gallery in the sight of everybody.", "그의 방은, 같은 층의 다른 방들이 다 그렇듯, 모두가 보는 앞에서 회랑으로 통하는 출구 하나밖에 없었다."], ["“I am lost!”", "\"끝장이다!\""], ["was his second thought; and, indeed, for a man in Andrea’s situation, an arrest meant the assizes, trial, and death,—death without mercy or delay.", "두 번째로 떠오른 생각이었다. 정말이지 안드레아 같은 처지의 사람에게 체포란 곧 중죄 재판과 심리, 그리고 죽음을 뜻했다. 자비도 유예도 없는 죽음을."]], [["For a moment he convulsively pressed his head within his hands, and during that brief period he became nearly mad with terror; but soon a ray of hope glimmered in the multitude of thoughts which bewildered his mind, and a faint smile played upon his white lips and pallid cheeks.", "한순간 그는 발작하듯 두 손으로 머리를 움켜쥐었고, 그 짧은 동안 공포로 거의 미칠 지경이 되었다. 그러나 이내, 그의 마음을 어지럽히던 무수한 생각들 가운데 한 줄기 희망의 빛이 어렴풋이 비쳤고, 핏기 없는 입술과 창백한 뺨 위로 희미한 미소가 떠올랐다."], ["He looked around and saw the objects of his search upon the chimney-piece; they were a pen, ink, and paper.", "그가 둘러보니 찾던 것들이 벽난로 선반 위에 놓여 있었다. 펜과 잉크와 종이였다."], ["With forced composure he dipped the pen in the ink, and wrote the following lines upon a sheet of paper:", "그는 억지로 침착을 꾸며 펜을 잉크에 적시고, 종이 한 장에 이런 글을 적었다."]], [["“I have no money to pay my bill, but I am not a dishonest man; I leave behind me as a pledge this pin, worth ten times the amount.", "\"셈을 치를 돈이 없습니다. 그러나 저는 부정한 사람이 아닙니다. 그 액수의 열 배는 나갈 이 핀을 담보로 남겨 둡니다."], ["I shall be excused for leaving at daybreak, for I was ashamed.”", "동틀 녘에 떠나는 것을 너그러이 보아 주시기를. 부끄러웠던 탓입니다.\""]], [["He then drew the pin from his cravat and placed it on the paper.", "그러고는 넥타이에서 핀을 뽑아 종이 위에 놓았다."], ["This done, instead of leaving the door fastened, he drew back the bolts and even placed the door ajar, as though he had left the room, forgetting to close it, and slipping into the chimney like a man accustomed to that kind of gymnastic exercise, after replacing the chimney-board, which represented Achilles with Deidamia, and effacing the very marks of his feet upon the ashes, he commenced climbing the hollow tunnel, which afforded him the only means of escape left.", "그런 다음, 문을 잠가 두는 대신 빗장을 도로 풀고, 마치 방을 나서며 닫는 것을 깜박한 것처럼 문을 빠끔히 열어 두기까지 했다. 그러고는 그런 곡예에 이골이 난 사람처럼 벽난로 속으로 미끄러져 들어갔다. 아킬레우스와 데이다메이아를 그린 벽난로 가림판을 도로 제자리에 놓고, 재 위에 찍힌 제 발자국마저 지운 뒤, 이제 그에게 남은 유일한 탈출구인 그 텅 빈 굴뚝을 기어오르기 시작했다."]], [["At this precise time, the first gendarme Andrea had noticed walked upstairs, preceded by the commissary of police, and supported by the second gendarme who guarded the staircase and was himself reinforced by the one stationed at the door.", "바로 그때, 안드레아가 보았던 첫 번째 헌병이 경찰서장을 앞세우고 계단을 올라왔다. 계단을 지키던 두 번째 헌병이 그를 거들었고, 그 헌병은 또 대문에 배치된 헌병의 지원을 받았다."]], [["Andrea was indebted for this visit to the following circumstances.", "안드레아가 이 방문을 받게 된 데에는 이런 사정이 있었다."], ["At daybreak, the telegraphs were set at work in all directions, and almost immediately the authorities in every district had exerted their utmost endeavors to arrest the murderer of Caderousse.", "동틀 무렵, 사방으로 통신기가 가동되었고, 거의 곧바로 각 구역의 당국이 카드루스를 살해한 자를 붙잡으려고 온갖 노력을 다 기울였다."], ["Compiègne, that royal residence and fortified town, is well furnished with authorities, gendarmes, and commissaries of police; they therefore began operations as soon as the telegraphic despatch arrived, and the Bell and Bottle being the best-known hotel in the town, they had naturally directed their first inquiries there.", "왕실의 거처이자 요새 도시인 콩피에뉴는 당국이며 헌병, 경찰서장이 넉넉히 갖춰진 곳이었다. 그래서 그들은 전신 급보가 도착하자마자 작전에 착수했고, 라 클로슈 에 라 부테유가 이 도시에서 가장 이름난 여관이었으므로, 자연히 그곳부터 가장 먼저 탐문에 나섰다."]], [["Now, besides the reports of the sentinels guarding the Hôtel de Ville, which is next door to the Bell and Bottle, it had been stated by others that a number of travellers had arrived during the night.", "그런데 라 클로슈 에 라 부테유 바로 옆에 있는 시청을 지키던 보초들의 보고에 더해, 밤사이 여러 길손이 들었다는 다른 이들의 진술도 있었다."], ["The sentinel who was relieved at six o’clock in the morning, remembered perfectly that, just as he was taking his post a few minutes past four, a young man arrived on horseback, with a little boy before him.", "아침 여섯 시에 교대한 보초는, 자신이 네 시 몇 분에 막 보초를 서던 참에 한 젊은이가 어린아이를 앞에 태우고 말을 타고 닿았던 것을 또렷이 기억했다."], ["The young man, having dismissed the boy and horse, knocked at the door of the hotel, which was opened, and again closed after his entrance.", "젊은이는 아이와 말을 돌려보낸 뒤 여관 문을 두드렸고, 문이 열렸다가 그가 들어가자 다시 닫혔다고 했다."], ["This late arrival had attracted much suspicion, and the young man being no other than Andrea, the commissary and gendarme, who was a brigadier, directed their steps towards his room.", "이 늦은 도착이 큰 의심을 샀고, 그 젊은이가 바로 다름 아닌 안드레아였으므로, 경찰서장과 헌병ー그는 분대장이었다ー은 그의 방으로 발걸음을 옮겼다."], ["They found the door ajar.", "문은 빠끔히 열려 있었다."]], [["“Oh, oh,”", "\"오호,\""], ["said the brigadier, who thoroughly understood the trick; “a bad sign to find the door open!", "그 수법을 훤히 꿰뚫어 본 분대장이 말했다. \"문이 열려 있다니 불길한 징조야!"], ["I would rather find it triply bolted.”", "차라리 빗장이 세 겹으로 걸려 있는 편이 낫지.\""]], [["And, indeed, the little note and pin upon the table confirmed, or rather corroborated, the sad truth. Andrea had fled.", "과연 탁자 위의 짧은 쪽지와 핀이 슬픈 진실을 확인해 주었다, 아니, 뒷받침해 주었다. 안드레아는 달아난 것이다."], ["We say corroborated, because the brigadier was too experienced to be convinced by a single proof.", "우리가 뒷받침해 주었다고 말하는 까닭은, 분대장이 단 하나의 증거만으로 납득하기에는 너무 노련했기 때문이다."], ["He glanced around, looked in the bed, shook the curtains, opened the closets, and finally stopped at the chimney.", "그는 주위를 흘끗 둘러보고, 침대 안을 살피고, 커튼을 흔들어 보고, 벽장을 열어 보고, 마침내 벽난로 앞에 멈춰 섰다."], ["Andrea had taken the precaution to leave no traces of his feet in the ashes, but still it was an outlet, and in this light was not to be passed over without serious investigation.", "안드레아는 재 위에 발자국을 남기지 않으려 조심했지만, 그래도 그것은 출구였고, 그런 점에서 진지한 조사 없이 그냥 지나칠 수는 없는 것이었다."]], [["The brigadier sent for some sticks and straw, and having filled the chimney with them, set a light to it.", "분대장은 나뭇가지와 짚을 가져오게 하여, 굴뚝을 그것으로 가득 채우고 불을 붙였다."], ["The fire crackled, and the smoke ascended like the dull vapor from a volcano; but still no prisoner fell down, as they expected.", "불이 탁탁 튀었고, 연기는 화산에서 피어오르는 흐릿한 수증기처럼 솟아올랐다. 그러나 그들이 기대한 대로 죄수가 떨어져 내리지는 않았다."], ["The fact was, that Andrea, at war with society ever since his youth, was quite as deep as a gendarme, even though he were advanced to the rank of brigadier, and quite prepared for the fire, he had climbed out on the roof and was crouching down against the chimney-pots. 50049m", "사실인즉, 어려서부터 줄곧 사회와 싸워 온 안드레아는, 비록 분대장으로 진급한 헌병이라 해도 그 못지않게 닳고 닳은 자였다. 불을 능히 짐작하고 있던 그는 이미 지붕으로 기어 올라가 굴뚝 갓 옆에 웅크리고 있었다."]], [["At one time he thought he was saved, for he heard the brigadier exclaim in a loud voice, to the two gendarmes, “He is not here!”", "한때 그는 살았다고 여겼다. 분대장이 두 헌병에게 큰 소리로 \"여기 없다!\" 하고 외치는 소리를 들었기 때문이다."], ["But venturing to peep, he perceived that the latter, instead of retiring, as might have been reasonably expected upon this announcement, were watching with increased attention.", "그러나 슬쩍 엿보니, 그 헌병들은 그 말에 으레 그러리라 짐작할 법하게 물러가기는커녕, 도리어 한층 주의 깊게 살피고 있었다."]], [["It was now his turn to look about him; the Hôtel de Ville, a massive sixteenth century building, was on his right; anyone could descend from the openings in the tower, and examine every corner of the roof below, and Andrea expected momentarily to see the head of a gendarme appear at one of these openings. If once discovered, he knew he would be lost, for the roof afforded no chance of escape; he therefore resolved to descend, not through the same chimney by which he had come up, but by a similar one conducting to another room.", "이번에는 그가 주위를 둘러볼 차례였다. 그의 오른쪽에는 묵직한 16세기 건물인 시청이 있었다. 누구라도 그 탑의 창들에서 내려와, 아래에 펼쳐진 지붕의 구석구석을 살필 수 있었으니, 안드레아는 그 창들 가운데 하나에 당장이라도 헌병의 머리가 나타날까 봐 마음을 졸였다. 한번 발각되면 끝장이라는 것을 그는 알고 있었다. 지붕에는 도망칠 구멍이 없었기 때문이다. 그래서 그는 내려가기로 마음먹었다. 올라온 그 굴뚝이 아니라, 다른 방으로 통하는 비슷한 굴뚝을 통해서였다."]], [["He looked around for a chimney from which no smoke issued, and having reached it, he disappeared through the orifice without being seen by anyone.", "그는 연기가 나지 않는 굴뚝을 찾아 둘러보았고, 그것에 이르자 아무에게도 들키지 않고 그 구멍 속으로 사라졌다."], ["At the same minute, one of the little windows of the Hôtel de Ville was thrown open, and the head of a gendarme appeared.", "바로 그 순간, 시청의 작은 창 하나가 활짝 열리며 헌병의 머리가 나타났다."], ["For an instant it remained motionless as one of the stone decorations of the building, then after a long sigh of disappointment the head disappeared.", "그것은 잠시 건물의 돌 장식처럼 미동도 없이 머물렀다가, 길게 한숨을 내쉬며 실망한 듯 사라졌다."], ["The brigadier, calm and dignified as the law he represented, passed through the crowd, without answering the thousand questions addressed to him, and re-entered the hotel.", "자신이 대변하는 법처럼 차분하고 위엄 있는 분대장은, 자신에게 쏟아지는 수천 가지 물음에는 대꾸 하나 없이 군중 사이를 지나, 여관 안으로 다시 들어갔다."]], [["“Well?” asked the two gendarmes.", "\"어떻게 됐습니까?\" 두 헌병이 물었다."]], [["“Well, my boys,”", "\"음, 이보게들,\""], ["said the brigadier, “the brigand must really have escaped early this morning; but we will send to the Villers-Coterets and Noyon roads, and search the forest, when we shall catch him, no doubt.”", "분대장이 말했다. \"그 도적놈은 정말 오늘 이른 아침에 달아난 게 분명해. 하지만 빌레르코트레와 누아용 가는 길로 사람을 보내고 숲을 뒤지면, 틀림없이 잡고 말 거야.\""]], [["The honorable functionary had scarcely expressed himself thus, in that intonation which is peculiar to brigadiers of the gendarmerie, when a loud scream, accompanied by the violent ringing of a bell, resounded through the court of the hotel.", "이 점잖은 관리가 헌병 분대장 특유의 그 억양으로 이렇게 말을 막 마쳤을 때, 종이 거칠게 울려 대는 소리와 함께 날카로운 비명이 여관 안뜰에 울려 퍼졌다."]], [["“Ah, what is that?” cried the brigadier.", "\"아니, 저게 무슨 소리야?\" 분대장이 외쳤다."]], [["“Some traveller seems impatient,” said the host.", "\"어느 길손이 조바심이 났나 봅니다.\""], ["“What number was it that rang?” “Number 3.”", "주인이 말했다. \"몇 호실에서 울린 거지?\""]], [["“Run, waiter!”", "\"3호실입니다.\""]], [["At this moment the screams and ringing were redoubled.", "\"종업원, 어서 가 봐!\" 이 순간, 비명과 종소리가 한층 더 요란해졌다."]], [["“Aha!”", "\"가만!\""], ["said the brigadier, stopping the servant, “the person who is ringing appears to want something more than a waiter; we will attend upon him with a gendarme.", "분대장이 종업원을 멈춰 세우며 말했다. \"저렇게 종을 울리는 사람은 종업원 말고 다른 무언가가 필요한 모양이군. 우리가 헌병을 데리고 가 보지."], ["Who occupies Number 3?”", "3호실에 든 게 누구지?\""]], [["“The little fellow who arrived last night in a post-chaise with his sister, and who asked for an apartment with two beds.”", "\"어젯밤 누이와 함께 역마차로 닿은 그 작은 친구입니다. 침대 둘 딸린 방을 청했지요.\""]], [["The bell here rang for the third time, with another shriek of anguish.", "이때 종이 세 번째로 울렸고, 또 한 번 괴로움에 찬 비명이 뒤따랐다."]], [["“Follow me, Mr. Commissary!”", "\"날 따라오시오, 서장님!\""], ["said the brigadier; “tread in my steps.”", "분대장이 말했다. \"내 발자국을 밟으며 오시오.\""]], [["“Wait an instant,”", "\"잠깐만.\" 주인이 말했다."], ["said the host; “Number 3 has two staircases,—inside and outside.”", "\"3호실에는 계단이 둘 있습니다. 안쪽 계단과 바깥쪽 계단이지요.\""]], [["“Good,” said the brigadier.", "\"좋아.\" 분대장이 말했다."], ["“I will take charge of the inside one.", "\"안쪽 계단은 내가 맡지."], ["Are the carbines loaded?”", "기총은 장전돼 있나?\""]], [["“Yes, brigadier.”", "\"예, 분대장님.\""]], [["“Well, you guard the exterior, and if he attempts to fly, fire upon him; he must be a great criminal, from what the telegraph says.”", "\"좋아, 자네는 바깥쪽을 지키게. 그자가 달아나려 하거든 쏘아 버려. 전신 내용을 보면 대단한 범죄자임이 분명하니까.\""]], [["The brigadier, followed by the commissary, disappeared by the inside staircase, accompanied by the noise which his assertions respecting Andrea had excited in the crowd.", "분대장은 서장을 거느리고 안쪽 계단으로 사라졌다. 안드레아에 관한 그의 단언이 군중 사이에 불러일으킨 소란이 그 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["This is what had happened: Andrea had very cleverly managed to descend two-thirds of the chimney, but then his foot slipped, and notwithstanding his endeavors, he came into the room with more speed and noise than he intended.", "사정인즉 이러했다. 안드레아는 아주 능란하게 굴뚝의 삼분의 이를 내려왔으나, 그때 발이 미끄러져, 안간힘을 썼는데도 그가 작정한 것보다 더 빠르고 요란하게 방 안으로 떨어졌다."], ["It would have signified little had the room been empty, but unfortunately it was occupied.", "방이 비어 있었다면 그쯤이야 대수롭지 않았겠지만, 불행히도 그 방에는 사람이 있었다."], ["Two ladies, sleeping in one bed, were awakened by the noise, and fixing their eyes upon the spot whence the sound proceeded, they saw a man.", "한 침대에서 자던 두 여인이 그 소리에 잠을 깼고, 소리가 난 쪽으로 눈을 돌리니 한 남자가 보였다."], ["One of these ladies, the fair one, uttered those terrible shrieks which resounded through the house, while the other, rushing to the bell-rope, rang with all her strength.", "두 여인 가운데 금발의 여인이, 집 안을 쩌렁쩌렁 울리는 그 끔찍한 비명을 질렀고, 다른 여인은 종 줄로 달려가 있는 힘껏 종을 흔들어 댔다."], ["Andrea, as we can see, was surrounded by misfortune.", "보다시피, 안드레아는 온통 불운에 에워싸여 있었다."]], [["“For pity’s sake,”", "\"제발 부탁이오,\""], ["he cried, pale and bewildered, without seeing whom he was addressing,—“for pity’s sake do not call assistance!", "그가 누구에게 말하는지도 보지 못한 채, 창백하고 얼이 빠진 얼굴로 외쳤다. \"제발 부탁이니 사람을 부르지 마시오!"], ["Save me!—I will not harm you.”", "나를 구해 주오! 해치지 않겠소.\""]], [["“Andrea, the murderer!”", "\"안드레아, 살인자야!\""], ["cried one of the ladies.", "여인 가운데 하나가 외쳤다."]], [["“Eugénie!", "\"외제니!"], ["Mademoiselle Danglars!” exclaimed Andrea, stupefied.", "당글라르 양!\" 안드레아가 어안이 벙벙해져 외쳤다."]], [["“Help, help!” cried Mademoiselle d’Armilly, taking the bell from her companion’s hand, and ringing it yet more violently.", "\"사람 살려, 사람 살려!\" 다르밀리 양이 동행의 손에서 종을 빼앗아, 한층 더 거칠게 흔들어 대며 외쳤다."]], [["“Save me, I am pursued!”", "\"날 구해 주오, 쫓기고 있소!\""], ["said Andrea, clasping his hands.", "안드레아가 두 손을 맞잡으며 말했다."], ["“For pity, for mercy’s sake do not deliver me up!”", "\"제발, 인정을 베풀어 날 넘기지 말아 주오!\""]], [["“It is too late, they are coming,”", "\"이미 늦었어요, 저들이 오고 있어요.\""], ["said Eugénie.", "외제니가 말했다."]], [["“Well, conceal me somewhere; you can say you were needlessly alarmed; you can turn their suspicions and save my life!”", "\"그럼 어디든 날 숨겨 주오. 공연히 놀란 거라고 둘러대면 되지 않소. 저들의 의심을 돌려 내 목숨을 구해 줄 수 있잖소!\""]], [["50053m\nThe two ladies, pressing closely to one another, and drawing the bedclothes tightly around them, remained silent to this supplicating voice, repugnance and fear taking possession of their minds.", "두 여인은 서로 바싹 붙어, 이불을 몸에 단단히 끌어당긴 채, 이 애원하는 목소리에 아무 대꾸도 없이 잠자코 있었다. 혐오와 두려움이 그들의 마음을 사로잡고 있었다."]], [["“Well, be it so,”", "\"좋아요, 그렇게 해요.\""], ["at length said Eugénie; “return by the same road you came, and we will say nothing about you, unhappy wretch.”", "마침내 외제니가 말했다. \"올라온 그 길로 도로 돌아가요. 그러면 우리는 당신에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않을 테니까요, 가엾은 인간 같으니.\""]], [["“Here he is, here he is!”", "\"여기 있다, 여기 있어!\""], ["cried a voice from the landing; “here he is!", "층계참에서 한 목소리가 외쳤다. \"여기 있어!"], ["I see him!”", "내 눈에 보인다!\""]], [["The brigadier had put his eye to the keyhole, and had discovered Andrea in a posture of entreaty.", "분대장이 열쇠 구멍에 눈을 대고, 애원하는 자세의 안드레아를 발견한 것이다."], ["A violent blow from the butt end of the musket burst open the lock, two more forced out the bolts, and the broken door fell in.", "개머리판으로 한 번 세차게 후려치자 자물쇠가 터졌고, 두 번 더 치니 빗장이 빠져나왔으며, 부서진 문이 안으로 쓰러졌다."], ["Andrea ran to the other door, leading to the gallery, ready to rush out; but he was stopped short, and he stood with his body a little thrown back, pale, and with the useless knife in his clenched hand.", "안드레아는 회랑으로 통하는 다른 문으로 달려가 뛰쳐나가려 했다. 그러나 그는 우뚝 멈춰 섰고, 상체를 약간 뒤로 젖힌 채, 창백한 얼굴로 쓸모없는 칼을 움켜쥔 손을 늘어뜨리고 서 있었다."]], [["“Fly, then!”", "\"그럼 달아나요!\""], ["cried Mademoiselle d’Armilly, whose pity returned as her fears diminished; “fly!”", "다르밀리 양이 외쳤다. 두려움이 잦아들자 그녀에게 다시 연민이 돌아온 것이다. \"달아나요!\""]], [["“Or kill yourself!”", "\"아니면 스스로 목숨을 끊든가요!\""], ["said Eugénie (in a tone which a Vestal in the amphitheatre would have used, when urging the victorious gladiator to finish his vanquished adversary). Andrea shuddered, and looked on the young girl with an expression which proved how little he understood such ferocious honor.", "외제니가 말했다. (원형 경기장의 베스타 여사제가 승리한 검투사에게 쓰러진 적수에게 마지막 일격을 가하라고 재촉할 때 썼을 법한 어조였다.) 안드레아는 몸서리를 쳤고, 그토록 사나운 명예심을 자신이 얼마나 이해하지 못하는지를 드러내는 표정으로 그 처녀를 바라보았다."]], [["“Kill myself?”", "\"내 목숨을 끊으라고?\""], ["he cried, throwing down his knife; “why should I do so?”", "그가 칼을 내던지며 외쳤다. \"내가 왜 그래야 하오?\""]], [["“Why, you said,”", "\"왜라니, 당신이 말했잖아요.\""], ["answered Mademoiselle Danglars, “that you would be condemned to die like the worst criminals.”", "당글라르 양이 대꾸했다. \"당신이 가장 흉악한 범죄자처럼 사형 선고를 받을 거라고요.\""]], [["50055m\n“Bah,”", "\"흥,\""], ["said Cavalcanti, crossing his arms, “one has friends.”", "카발칸티가 팔짱을 끼며 말했다. \"사람한테는 친구라는 게 있는 법이지.\""]], [["The brigadier advanced to him, sword in hand.", "분대장이 칼을 빼 들고 그에게 다가갔다."]], [["“Come, come,”", "\"자자,\""], ["said Andrea, “sheathe your sword, my fine fellow; there is no occasion to make such a fuss, since I give myself up;”", "안드레아가 말했다. \"그 칼은 도로 집어넣으시오, 양반. 내가 스스로 항복하는 마당에 그렇게 부산을 떨 것 없잖소.\""], ["and he held out his hands to be manacled.", "그러고는 수갑을 채우라고 두 손을 내밀었다."]], [["The two girls looked with horror upon this shameful metamorphosis, the man of the world shaking off his covering and appearing as a galley-slave.", "두 처녀는 이 부끄러운 탈바꿈을 공포에 질려 바라보았다. 사교계의 신사가 제 껍데기를 벗어 던지고 갤리선의 노예로 드러난 것이다."], ["Andrea turned towards them, and with an impertinent smile asked, “Have you any message for your father, Mademoiselle Danglars, for in all probability I shall return to Paris?”", "안드레아가 그들 쪽으로 돌아서더니, 무례한 미소를 띠고 물었다. \"아버님께 전할 말씀이라도 있소, 당글라르 양? 아무래도 나는 파리로 돌아가게 될 것 같으니 말이오.\""]], [["Eugénie covered her face with her hands.", "외제니는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다."]], [["“Oh, oh!”", "\"오호,\""], ["said Andrea, “you need not be ashamed, even though you did post after me.", "안드레아가 말했다. \"부끄러워할 것 없소. 그래, 당신이 날 뒤쫓아 왔다 한들 어떻소."], ["Was I not nearly your husband?” 50056m", "내가 당신 남편이 될 뻔하지 않았소?\""]], [["And with this raillery Andrea went out, leaving the two girls a prey to their own feelings of shame, and to the comments of the crowd.", "이런 비아냥을 남기고 안드레아는 밖으로 나갔다. 두 처녀는 제 부끄러움과 군중의 입방아에 고스란히 내맡겨진 채였다."], ["An hour after they stepped into their calash, both dressed in feminine attire.", "한 시간 뒤 두 사람은 여인의 옷차림을 하고 사륜마차에 올랐다."], ["The gate of the hotel had been closed to screen them from sight, but they were forced, when the door was open, to pass through a throng of curious glances and whispering voices.", "그들을 시선에서 가려 주려고 여관 대문을 닫아 두었지만, 문이 열리자 호기심에 찬 눈길과 수군대는 목소리의 무리 사이를 지나야만 했다."]], [["Eugénie closed her eyes; but though she could not see, she could hear, and the sneers of the crowd reached her in the carriage.", "외제니는 눈을 감았다. 그러나 볼 수는 없어도 들을 수는 있었으니, 군중의 비웃음이 마차 안의 그녀에게까지 닿았다."]], [["“Oh, why is not the world a wilderness?”", "\"오, 어째서 이 세상은 황무지가 아닌가?\""], ["she exclaimed, throwing herself into the arms of Mademoiselle d’Armilly, her eyes sparkling with the same kind of rage which made Nero wish that the Roman world had but one neck, that he might sever it at a single blow.", "그녀가 다르밀리 양의 품에 몸을 던지며 외쳤다. 그녀의 두 눈은, 로마 세계에 목이 하나뿐이어서 단칼에 베어 버릴 수 있기를 바랐던 네로의 그것과 같은 분노로 번득였다."]], [["The next day they stopped at the Hôtel de Flandre, at Brussels.", "이튿날 두 사람은 브뤼셀의 오텔 드 플랑드르에 묵었다."], ["The same evening Andrea was incarcerated in the Conciergerie.", "같은 날 저녁, 안드레아는 콩시에르주리에 수감되었다."]], [["# Chapter 99. The Law", "# Chapter 99. The Law"]], [["We have seen how quietly Mademoiselle Danglars and Mademoiselle d’Armilly accomplished their transformation and flight; the fact being that everyone was too much occupied in his or her own affairs to think of theirs.", "당글라르 양과 다르미이 양이 얼마나 조용히 변장을 마치고 달아났는지 우리는 이미 보았다. 사실 누구나 제 일에 정신이 팔려 그들의 일까지 신경 쓸 겨를이 없었다."]], [["We will leave the banker contemplating the enormous magnitude of his debt before the phantom of bankruptcy, and follow the baroness, who after being momentarily crushed under the weight of the blow which had struck her, had gone to seek her usual adviser, Lucien Debray.", "은행가는 파산의 망령 앞에서 제 빚의 어마어마한 규모를 곱씹게 내버려 두고, 우리는 남작 부인을 따라가 보자. 부인은 자신을 덮친 타격의 무게에 잠시 짓눌렸다가, 평소 자문을 구하던 뤼시앵 드브레를 찾아 나섰다."], ["The baroness had looked forward to this marriage as a means of ridding her of a guardianship which, over a girl of Eugénie’s character, could not fail to be rather a troublesome undertaking; for in the tacit relations which maintain the bond of family union, the mother, to maintain her ascendancy over her daughter, must never fail to be a model of wisdom and a type of perfection.", "남작 부인은 이 결혼을 후견의 짐에서 벗어날 방편으로 여겨 왔다. 외제니 같은 성격의 처녀를 후견하는 일은 여간 성가신 일이 아닐 수밖에 없었으니까. 가족이라는 끈을 유지하게 하는 암묵적 관계 속에서, 어머니는 딸에 대한 우위를 지키려면 늘 지혜의 본보기이자 완벽의 전형이어야 하기 때문이다."]], [["Now, Madame Danglars feared Eugénie’s sagacity and the influence of Mademoiselle d’Armilly; she had frequently observed the contemptuous expression with which her daughter looked upon Debray,—an expression which seemed to imply that she understood all her mother’s amorous and pecuniary relationships with the intimate secretary; moreover, she saw that Eugénie detested Debray, not only because he was a source of dissension and scandal under the paternal roof, but because she had at once classed him in that catalogue of bipeds whom Plato endeavors to withdraw from the appellation of men, and whom Diogenes designated as animals upon two legs without feathers.", "그런데 당글라르 부인은 외제니의 영민함과 다르미이 양의 영향력이 두려웠다. 부인은 딸이 드브레를 바라볼 때 짓는 경멸 어린 표정을 자주 보아 왔다. 그것은 어머니와 그 측근 비서 사이의 애정 관계며 금전 관계를 모두 꿰뚫고 있다는 듯한 표정이었다. 게다가 부인은 외제니가 드브레를 혐오한다는 것을 알았다. 그가 아버지의 지붕 아래 불화와 추문의 근원이기 때문만이 아니라, 딸이 그를 단번에 그 부류의 두 발 동물 목록에 끼워 넣었기 때문이다. 플라톤이 인간이라는 이름에서 떼어 내려 애썼고, 디오게네스가 깃털 없는 두 발 짐승이라 불렀던 그 부류 말이다."]], [["Unfortunately, in this world of ours, each person views things through a certain medium, and so is prevented from seeing in the same light as others, and Madame Danglars, therefore, very much regretted that the marriage of Eugénie had not taken place, not only because the match was good, and likely to insure the happiness of her child, but because it would also set her at liberty.", "불행히도 우리가 사는 이 세상에서는 누구나 어떤 매개를 통해 사물을 보고, 그래서 남들과 같은 빛으로 보지 못한다. 그리하여 당글라르 부인은 외제니의 결혼이 성사되지 못한 것을 몹시 아쉬워했다. 그 혼처가 좋아서, 또 제 딸의 행복을 보장해 줄 만해서이기도 했지만, 무엇보다 그 결혼이 자신을 자유롭게 해 줄 터였기 때문이다."], ["She ran therefore to Debray, who, after having, like the rest of Paris, witnessed the contract scene and the scandal attending it, had retired in haste to his club, where he was chatting with some friends upon the events which served as a subject of conversation for three-fourths of that city known as the capital of the world.", "그래서 부인은 드브레에게 달려갔다. 드브레는 파리의 다른 사람들처럼 혼인 계약 장면과 그에 따른 추문을 목격한 뒤, 서둘러 자기 클럽으로 물러나 몇몇 친구들과 그 사건을 두고 이야기를 나누던 참이었다. 그 사건은 세계의 수도라 불리는 그 도시 사람들 사 분의 삼의 화젯거리가 되어 있었다."]], [["At the precise time when Madame Danglars, dressed in black and concealed in a long veil, was ascending the stairs leading to Debray’s apartments, notwithstanding the assurances of the concierge that the young man was not at home, Debray was occupied in repelling the insinuations of a friend, who tried to persuade him that after the terrible scene which had just taken place he ought, as a friend of the family, to marry Mademoiselle Danglars and her two millions.", "당글라르 부인이 검은 옷차림에 긴 베일로 얼굴을 가린 채, 젊은 남자는 집에 없다는 문지기의 단언에도 아랑곳없이 드브레의 거처로 통하는 계단을 오르고 있던 바로 그 시각, 드브레는 한 친구의 부추김을 물리치느라 여념이 없었다. 그 친구는, 방금 벌어진 끔찍한 장면을 겪었으니 가족의 벗으로서 당글라르 양과 그녀의 이백만 프랑을 맞아들여야 마땅하다고 그를 설득하려 했다. 드브레는 그리 강하게 부인하지 않았다."], ["Debray did not defend himself very warmly, for the idea had sometimes crossed his mind; still, when he recollected the independent, proud spirit of Eugénie, he positively rejected it as utterly impossible, though the same thought again continually recurred and found a resting-place in his heart.", "그 생각이 이따금 그의 머리를 스쳤기 때문이다. 그러면서도 외제니의 독립적이고 자존심 강한 기질을 떠올리면, 도무지 불가능한 일이라며 단호히 물리쳤다. 하지만 그 같은 생각은 계속 되살아나 그의 마음속에 자리를 잡았다."], ["Tea, play, and the conversation, which had become interesting during the discussion of such serious affairs, lasted till one o’clock in the morning.", "차와 카드놀이, 그리고 그처럼 중대한 일을 논하는 동안 흥미진진해진 대화는 새벽 한 시까지 이어졌다."]], [["Meanwhile Madame Danglars, veiled and uneasy, awaited the return of Debray in the little green room, seated between two baskets of flowers, which she had that morning sent, and which, it must be confessed, Debray had himself arranged and watered with so much care that his absence was half excused in the eyes of the poor woman.", "그동안 당글라르 부인은 베일을 쓴 채 불안에 떨며, 그날 아침 자신이 보낸 꽃 두 바구니 사이에 앉아 그 작은 초록 방에서 드브레가 돌아오기를 기다렸다. 솔직히 말하자면, 드브레가 손수 그 꽃을 그토록 정성스레 매만지고 물을 준 덕분에, 가엾은 여인의 눈에는 그의 부재가 절반쯤 용서되었다."]], [["At twenty minutes to twelve, Madame Danglars, tired of waiting, returned home.", "열두 시 이십 분 전, 당글라르 부인은 기다림에 지쳐 집으로 돌아갔다."], ["Women of a certain grade are like prosperous grisettes in one respect, they seldom return home after twelve o’clock.", "어떤 신분의 여인들은 한 가지 점에서 형편 좋은 바느질꾼 처녀들과 비슷하다. 열두 시가 넘어 집으로 돌아가는 일이 좀처럼 없다는 점이다."], ["The baroness returned to the hotel with as much caution as Eugénie used in leaving it; she ran lightly upstairs, and with an aching heart entered her apartment, contiguous, as we know, to that of Eugénie.", "남작 부인은 외제니가 빠져나갈 때 썼던 만큼이나 조심스럽게 저택으로 돌아왔다. 가벼운 걸음으로 계단을 올라, 쓰린 마음으로 자신의 방으로 들어섰다. 우리가 알다시피 외제니의 방과 붙어 있는 방이었다."], ["She was fearful of exciting any remark, and believed firmly in her daughter’s innocence and fidelity to the paternal roof.", "부인은 행여 무슨 말이 날까 두려웠고, 딸이 결백하며 아버지의 지붕에 충실하다고 굳게 믿고 있었다."], ["She listened at Eugénie’s door, and hearing no sound tried to enter, but the bolts were in place.", "외제니의 방문 앞에서 귀를 기울였으나 아무 소리도 들리지 않자 들어가려 했지만, 빗장이 걸려 있었다."], ["Madame Danglars then concluded that the young girl had been overcome with the terrible excitement of the evening, and had gone to bed and to sleep.", "그제야 당글라르 부인은 그 처녀가 저녁의 끔찍한 흥분에 지쳐 잠자리에 들어 잠든 모양이라고 결론지었다."], ["She called the maid and questioned her.", "부인은 하녀를 불러 물었다."]], [["“Mademoiselle Eugénie,”", "\"외제니 아가씨는,\""], ["said the maid, “retired to her apartment with Mademoiselle d’Armilly; they then took tea together, after which they desired me to leave, saying that they needed me no longer.”", "하녀가 말했다. \"다르미이 아가씨와 함께 방으로 드셨습니다. 그러고는 둘이 차를 드시더니, 더는 필요 없다며 저더러 물러가라고 하셨고요.\""]], [["Since then the maid had been below, and like everyone else she thought the young ladies were in their own room; Madame Danglars, therefore, went to bed without a shadow of suspicion, and began to muse over the recent events.", "그 뒤로 하녀는 아래층에 있었고, 다른 모두와 마찬가지로 두 아가씨가 자기들 방에 있는 줄 알았다. 그래서 당글라르 부인은 한 점 의심도 없이 잠자리에 들어, 최근의 일들을 곰곰이 되새기기 시작했다."], ["In proportion as her memory became clearer, the occurrences of the evening were revealed in their true light; what she had taken for confusion was a tumult; what she had regarded as something distressing, was in reality a disgrace.", "기억이 또렷해질수록 그날 저녁의 일들이 본래의 빛으로 드러났다. 혼란이라 여겼던 것은 소동이었고, 괴로운 일쯤으로 여겼던 것은 실은 치욕이었다."], ["And then the baroness remembered that she had felt no pity for poor Mercédès, who had been afflicted with as severe a blow through her husband and son.", "그리고 남작 부인은, 남편과 아들 때문에 못지않게 호된 타격을 입은 가엾은 메르세데스에게 자신이 아무런 연민도 느끼지 못했음을 떠올렸다."]], [["“Eugénie,”", "'외제니는,'"], ["she said to herself, “is lost, and so are we.", "부인은 속으로 생각했다. '끝장이고, 우리도 끝장이야."], ["The affair, as it will be reported, will cover us with shame; for in a society such as ours satire inflicts a painful and incurable wound.", "이 일이 소문나는 모양새로는 우리는 수치로 뒤덮일 테지. 우리 같은 사회에서 비웃음이란 아프고도 낫지 않는 상처를 입히니까."], ["How fortunate that Eugénie is possessed of that strange character which has so often made me tremble!”", "그래도 외제니가 그 별난 성격을 지닌 게 얼마나 다행인지. 그 성격 때문에 내가 그토록 자주 떨곤 했지만!'"]], [["And her glance was turned towards heaven, where a mysterious Providence disposes all things, and out of a fault, nay, even a vice, sometimes produces a blessing.", "그러고는 그녀의 시선이 하늘로 향했다. 그곳에서는 신비로운 섭리가 만물을 다스리며, 잘못에서, 아니 악덕에서조차 때로는 축복을 빚어낸다."], ["And then her thoughts, cleaving through space like a bird in the air, rested on Cavalcanti.", "이윽고 그녀의 생각은 공중의 새처럼 허공을 가르며 카발칸티에게 가닿았다."], ["This Andrea was a wretch, a robber, an assassin, and yet his manners showed the effects of a sort of education, if not a complete one; he had been presented to the world with the appearance of an immense fortune, supported by an honorable name.", "그 안드레아라는 자는 비열한 놈에 도둑이며 살인자였다. 그런데도 그 몸가짐에는 완전치는 않더라도 어떤 교육의 흔적이 배어 있었다. 그는 명예로운 이름을 등에 업고 어마어마한 재산을 가진 인물로 사교계에 소개되었었다."], ["How could she extricate herself from this labyrinth?", "어떻게 하면 이 미궁에서 빠져나올 수 있을까?"], ["To whom would she apply to help her out of this painful situation?", "이 괴로운 처지에서 헤어 나오도록 누구에게 도움을 청해야 할까?"], ["Debray, to whom she had run, with the first instinct of a woman towards the man she loves, and who yet betrays her,—Debray could but give her advice, she must apply to someone more powerful than he.", "사랑하는 남자를 향한 여자의 첫 본능으로 달려갔지만 그 남자는 그녀를 저버리는 드브레, 그 드브레는 조언밖에 줄 수 없었다. 그보다 더 힘 있는 누군가에게 의지해야 했다."]], [["The baroness then thought of M. de Villefort.", "그래서 남작 부인은 빌포르 씨를 떠올렸다."], ["It was M. de Villefort who had remorselessly brought misfortune into her family, as though they had been strangers.", "자기 가족이 마치 남남이라도 되는 양 가차 없이 불행을 끌어들인 사람이 바로 빌포르 씨였다. 하지만 아니다."], ["But, no; on reflection, the procureur was not a merciless man; and it was not the magistrate, slave to his duties, but the friend, the loyal friend, who roughly but firmly cut into the very core of the corruption; it was not the executioner, but the surgeon, who wished to withdraw the honor of Danglars from ignominious association with the disgraced young man they had presented to the world as their son-in-law.", "다시 생각해 보니 그 검사는 무자비한 사람이 아니었다. 부패의 핵심을 거칠지만 단호하게 도려낸 것은 의무의 노예인 법관이 아니라 벗, 충실한 벗이었다. 사교계에 사위로 소개되었던 그 망신스러운 젊은이와 당글라르라는 이름이 불명예스럽게 엮이는 것을 끊어 내려 한 것은 사형 집행인이 아니라 외과 의사였다."], ["And since Villefort, the friend of Danglars, had acted in this way, no one could suppose that he had been previously acquainted with, or had lent himself to, any of Andrea’s intrigues.", "당글라르의 벗인 빌포르가 이렇게 행동한 이상, 그가 안드레아의 음모를 미리 알았거나 거기에 손을 빌려주었다고는 아무도 생각할 수 없으리라."], ["Villefort’s conduct, therefore, upon reflection, appeared to the baroness as if shaped for their mutual advantage.", "그러니 곰곰이 따져 보면 빌포르의 행동은 서로의 이익을 위해 빚어진 것처럼 보였다."], ["But the inflexibility of the procureur should stop there; she would see him the next day, and if she could not make him fail in his duties as a magistrate, she would, at least, obtain all the indulgence he could allow.", "그러나 검사의 그 완고함은 거기서 멈춰야 했다. 이튿날 그를 만나, 법관으로서의 의무를 저버리게 하지는 못할지언정, 적어도 그가 베풀 수 있는 한의 관용은 얻어 내리라."], ["She would invoke the past, recall old recollections; she would supplicate him by the remembrance of guilty, yet happy days. M.", "지난날을 들먹이고 옛 추억을 떠올리게 하리라. 죄스러우면서도 행복했던 나날의 기억으로 그에게 애원하리라."], ["de Villefort would stifle the affair; he had only to turn his eyes on one side, and allow Andrea to fly, and follow up the crime under that shadow of guilt called contempt of court.", "빌포르 씨라면 이 일을 덮어 줄 수 있을 터였다. 그저 한쪽으로 눈을 돌려 안드레아를 달아나게 하고, 법정 모독이라 불리는 죄의 그늘 아래 그 범죄를 처리하기만 하면 되었다."], ["And after this reasoning she slept easily.", "이렇게 따져 보고 나서 부인은 편히 잠들었다."]], [["At nine o’clock next morning she arose, and without ringing for her maid or giving the least sign of her activity, she dressed herself in the same simple style as on the previous night; then running downstairs, she left the hotel, walked to the Rue de Provence, called a cab, and drove to M. de Villefort’s house.", "이튿날 아침 아홉 시에 부인은 일어나, 하녀를 부르지도, 자신이 깨어 있다는 기색을 조금도 내비치지도 않은 채, 전날 밤과 똑같이 수수한 차림을 했다. 그러고는 계단을 뛰어 내려가 저택을 나서서 프로방스 거리까지 걸어가 삯마차를 잡아타고 빌포르 씨의 집으로 향했다."]], [["For the last month this wretched house had presented the gloomy appearance of a lazaretto infected with the plague.", "지난 한 달간 이 불행한 집은 페스트가 번진 격리 병동 같은 음울한 모습을 띠고 있었다. 몇몇 방들은 안팎으로 닫혀 있었다. 덧창은 잠깐 바람을 들이려 열릴 뿐이었고, 그때마다 겁에 질린 하인의 얼굴이 보였다가 곧바로 창이 닫혔다."], ["Some of the apartments were closed within and without; the shutters were only opened to admit a minute’s air, showing the scared face of a footman, and immediately afterwards the window would be closed, like a gravestone falling on a sepulchre, and the neighbors would say to each other in a low voice, “Will there be another funeral today at the procureur’s house?”", "무덤 위로 떨어지는 묘석처럼. 그러면 이웃들은 서로 나직이 수군거렸다. \"오늘도 검사 댁에서 또 장례가 치러질까?\""]], [["Madame Danglars involuntarily shuddered at the desolate aspect of the mansion; descending from the cab, she approached the door with trembling knees, and rang the bell.", "당글라르 부인은 저택의 황량한 모습에 자기도 모르게 몸서리쳤다. 마차에서 내려, 후들거리는 무릎으로 문에 다가가 종을 울렸다."], ["Three times did the bell ring with a dull, heavy sound, seeming to participate, in the general sadness, before the concierge appeared and peeped through the door, which he opened just wide enough to allow his words to be heard.", "종은 둔하고 무거운 소리로 세 번 울렸다. 마치 그 집 전체의 슬픔에 한몫 끼기라도 하듯이. 그런 뒤에야 문지기가 나타나 말소리가 들릴 만큼만 문을 빠끔히 열고 안을 내다보았다."], ["He saw a lady, a fashionable, elegantly dressed lady, and yet the door remained almost closed.", "그는 한 부인을, 멋스럽고 우아하게 차려입은 부인을 보았다. 그런데도 문은 거의 닫힌 채였다."]], [["“Do you intend opening the door?” said the baroness.", "\"문을 열 생각인가요?\" 남작 부인이 말했다."]], [["“First, madame, who are you?”", "\"먼저, 부인, 누구십니까?\""]], [["“Who am I? You know me well enough.”", "\"내가 누구냐고요? 나를 잘 알 텐데요.\""]], [["“We no longer know anyone, madame.”", "\"우리는 이제 아무도 모릅니다, 부인.\""]], [["“You must be mad, my friend,”", "\"이보게, 제정신이 아니로군.\""], ["said the baroness.", "남작 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Where do you come from?”", "\"어디서 오셨습니까?\""]], [["“Oh, this is too much!”", "\"오, 이건 너무하잖아요!\""]], [["“Madame, these are my orders; excuse me. Your name?”", "\"부인, 이건 제가 받은 명령입니다. 양해해 주십시오. 성함이?\""]], [["“The baroness Danglars; you have seen me twenty times.”", "\"당글라르 남작 부인이에요. 나를 스무 번은 보았을 텐데요.\""]], [["“Possibly, madame.", "\"그럴 수도 있지요, 부인."], ["And now, what do you want?”", "그래서, 무슨 용건이십니까?\""]], [["“Oh, how extraordinary! I shall complain to M.", "\"오, 별일도 다 있군요!"], ["de Villefort of the impertinence of his servants.”", "빌포르 씨한테 하인들의 무례함을 일러바치겠어요.\""]], [["“Madame, this is precaution, not impertinence; no one enters here without an order from M. d’Avrigny, or without speaking to the procureur.”", "\"부인, 이건 무례가 아니라 조심입니다. 다브리니 선생님의 허락 없이는, 또 검사님과 말씀을 나누지 않고는 아무도 여기 들어올 수 없습니다.\""]], [["“Well, I have business with the procureur.”", "\"좋아요, 검사님께 볼일이 있어요.\""]], [["“Is it pressing business?”", "\"급한 용건입니까?\""]], [["“You can imagine so, since I have not even brought my carriage out yet.", "\"그렇게 짐작할 만하잖아요. 마차도 안 끌고 나왔으니까요."], ["But enough of this—here is my card, take it to your master.”", "하지만 이쯤 됐으면 됐고, 여기 내 명함이에요. 주인께 갖다 드려요.\""]], [["“Madame will await my return?”", "\"부인, 제가 돌아올 때까지 기다려 주시겠습니까?\""], ["“Yes; go.”", "\"그래요, 가 봐요.\""]], [["The concierge closed the door, leaving Madame Danglars in the street.", "문지기는 당글라르 부인을 거리에 세워 둔 채 문을 닫았다."], ["She had not long to wait; directly afterwards the door was opened wide enough to admit her, and when she had passed through, it was again shut.", "오래 기다릴 것도 없었다. 곧바로 문이 그녀가 들어갈 만큼 넓게 열렸고, 그녀가 지나가자 다시 닫혔다."], ["Without losing sight of her for an instant, the concierge took a whistle from his pocket as soon as they entered the court, and blew it.", "안마당에 들어서기 무섭게 문지기는 한순간도 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 주머니에서 호루라기를 꺼내 불었다."], ["The valet de chambre appeared on the door-steps.", "그러자 시종이 현관 계단에 나타났다."]], [["“You will excuse this poor fellow, madame,”", "\"이 가엾은 친구를 너그러이 봐주십시오, 부인.\""], ["he said, as he preceded the baroness, “but his orders are precise, and M.", "시종이 남작 부인을 앞서 안내하며 말했다. \"명령이 워낙 엄격해서요."], ["de Villefort begged me to tell you that he could not act otherwise.”", "빌포르 씨께서 달리 어쩔 수 없다고 부인께 전하라 이르셨습니다.\""]], [["In the court showing his merchandise, was a tradesman who had been admitted with the same precautions.", "안마당에는 같은 절차를 거쳐 들여보내진 한 상인이 물건을 늘어놓고 있었다. 남작 부인은 계단을 올랐다."], ["The baroness ascended the steps; she felt herself strongly infected with the sadness which seemed to magnify her own, and still guided by the valet de chambre, who never lost sight of her for an instant, she was introduced to the magistrate’s study.", "그녀는 이 집을 짓누르는 슬픔에 단단히 물든 듯한 기분이었고, 그 슬픔은 자신의 슬픔까지 키우는 것만 같았다. 한순간도 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 않는 시종에게 여전히 이끌려, 그녀는 법관의 서재로 안내되었다."]], [["Preoccupied as Madame Danglars had been with the object of her visit, the treatment she had received from these underlings appeared to her so insulting, that she began by complaining of it.", "당글라르 부인은 방문의 목적에 온통 정신이 팔려 있었으면서도, 그 하인들에게서 받은 대접이 어찌나 모욕적으로 느껴졌던지 우선 그 일부터 불평하기 시작했다."], ["But Villefort, raising his head, bowed down by grief, looked up at her with so sad a smile that her complaints died upon her lips.", "그러나 슬픔에 짓눌려 고개를 숙이고 있던 빌포르가 머리를 들어, 어찌나 서글픈 미소로 그녀를 올려다보던지 불평은 입가에서 사그라들었다."]], [["“Forgive my servants,”", "\"제 하인들을 용서해 주십시오.\""], ["he said, “for a terror I cannot blame them for; from being suspected they have become suspicious.”", "그가 말했다. \"제가 탓할 수 없는 그 공포 때문이니까요. 의심을 받다 보니 의심하는 자들이 되어 버린 겁니다.\""]], [["Madame Danglars had often heard of the terror to which the magistrate alluded, but without the evidence of her own eyesight she could never have believed that the sentiment had been carried so far.", "당글라르 부인은 그 법관이 말하는 공포에 대해 여러 번 들어 왔지만, 제 눈으로 직접 보지 않았다면 그 감정이 이토록까지 번졌으리라고는 결코 믿지 못했을 것이다."]], [["“You too, then, are unhappy?” she said.", "\"그렇습니다, 부인.\" 법관이 대답했다."]], [["“Yes, madame,” replied the magistrate.", "\"그렇다면 저를 가엾게 여겨 주시겠군요!\""]], [["“Then you pity me!”", "\"진심으로요, 부인.\""]], [["“And you understand what brings me here?”", "\"제가 무슨 일로 여기 왔는지도 아시겠지요?\""]], [["“You wish to speak to me about the circumstance which has just happened?”", "\"방금 일어난 그 일에 대해 제게 말씀하시려는 것이지요?\""]], [["“Yes, sir,—a fearful misfortune.”", "\"네, 검사님. 무서운 불행이에요.\""]], [["“You mean a mischance.”", "\"불운이라고 하시는 게 맞겠지요.\""]], [["“A mischance?”", "\"불운이라고요?\""], ["repeated the baroness.", "남작 부인이 되뇌었다."]], [["“Alas, madame,”", "\"안타깝게도, 부인.\""], ["said the procureur with his imperturbable calmness of manner, “I consider those alone misfortunes which are irreparable.”", "검사가 한결같은 침착함으로 말했다. \"저는 돌이킬 수 없는 일만을 불행이라 여깁니다.\""]], [["“And do you suppose this will be forgotten?”", "\"이 일이 잊힐 거라고 생각하세요?\""]], [["“Everything will be forgotten, madame,”", "\"모든 것은 잊히게 마련입니다, 부인.\""], ["said Villefort.", "빌포르가 말했다."], ["“Your daughter will be married tomorrow, if not today—in a week, if not tomorrow; and I do not think you can regret the intended husband of your daughter.”", "\"따님은 오늘이 아니면 내일 결혼할 테고, 내일이 아니면 일주일 안에 하겠지요. 그리고 따님의 사윗감이 될 뻔했던 그 사람을 부인께서 아쉬워하시리라고는 생각하지 않습니다.\""]], [["Madame Danglars gazed on Villefort, stupefied to find him so almost insultingly calm.", "당글라르 부인은 그가 거의 모욕적이리만치 침착한 데에 어안이 벙벙하여 빌포르를 바라보았다."], ["“Am I come to a friend?”", "\"제가 벗을 찾아온 게 맞나요?\""], ["she asked in a tone full of mournful dignity.", "그녀가 서글픈 위엄이 가득 밴 어조로 물었다."]], [["“You know that you are, madame,”", "\"그렇다는 걸 잘 아시지 않습니까, 부인.\""], ["said Villefort, whose pale cheeks became slightly flushed as he gave her the assurance.", "빌포르가 말했다. 그 다짐을 건네면서 그의 창백한 뺨이 살짝 붉어졌다."], ["And truly this assurance carried him back to different events from those now occupying the baroness and him.", "사실 그 다짐은 그를, 지금 남작 부인과 그가 매달려 있는 일과는 다른 사건들로 데려갔던 것이다."]], [["“Well, then, be more affectionate, my dear Villefort,”", "\"그렇다면 좀 더 다정하게 대해 주세요, 빌포르.\""], ["said the baroness.", "남작 부인이 말했다."], ["“Speak to me not as a magistrate, but as a friend; and when I am in bitter anguish of spirit, do not tell me that I ought to be gay.”", "\"법관이 아니라 벗으로서 제게 말해 주세요. 그리고 제가 마음의 쓰라린 고통에 잠겨 있을 때, 즐거워해야 한다고는 말하지 말아 주세요.\""], ["Villefort bowed.", "빌포르가 고개를 숙였다."]], [["“When I hear misfortunes named, madame,”", "\"불행이라는 말을 들으면, 부인.\""], ["he said, “I have within the last few months contracted the bad habit of thinking of my own, and then I cannot help drawing up an egotistical parallel in my mind.", "그가 말했다. \"저는 지난 몇 달 사이에 제 자신의 불행을 떠올리는 못된 버릇이 들었습니다. 그러면 마음속으로 이기적인 비교를 하지 않을 수가 없지요."], ["That is the reason that by the side of my misfortunes yours appear to me mere mischances; that is why my dreadful position makes yours appear enviable.", "그래서 제 불행 곁에 놓고 보면 부인의 불행은 한낱 불운으로밖에 보이지 않는 겁니다. 제 끔찍한 처지 탓에 부인의 처지가 부러워 보이는 것도 그래서지요."], ["But this annoys you; let us change the subject.", "하지만 이런 말이 부인을 불편하게 하는군요. 화제를 바꿉시다."], ["You were saying, madame——”", "부인, 말씀하시던 게……\""]], [["“I came to ask you, my friend,”", "\"여쭤 보러 왔어요, 벗이여.\""], ["said the baroness, “what will be done with this impostor?”", "남작 부인이 말했다. \"그 사기꾼이 어떻게 될지를요.\""]], [["“Impostor,”", "\"사기꾼이라.\""], ["repeated Villefort; “certainly, madame, you appear to extenuate some cases, and exaggerate others. Impostor, indeed!—M.", "빌포르가 되뇌었다. \"정말이지, 부인, 부인께서는 어떤 경우는 가볍게 보시고 어떤 경우는 부풀려 보시는 것 같습니다."], ["Andrea Cavalcanti, or rather M. Benedetto, is nothing more nor less than an assassin!”", "사기꾼이라니요! 안드레아 카발칸티 씨, 아니 베네데토라는 자는, 다름 아닌 살인자입니다!\""]], [["“Sir, I do not deny the justice of your correction, but the more severely you arm yourself against that unfortunate man, the more deeply will you strike our family.", "\"검사님, 정정해 주신 게 옳다는 걸 부인하지는 않겠어요. 하지만 그 불행한 사람을 호되게 칠수록, 우리 가족도 그만큼 더 깊이 베이게 돼요."], ["Come, forget him for a moment, and instead of pursuing him, let him go.”", "자, 잠시만 그를 잊고, 뒤쫓는 대신 그냥 놓아주세요.\""]], [["“You are too late, madame; the orders are issued.”", "\"너무 늦었습니다, 부인. 명령이 이미 떨어졌습니다.\""]], [["“Well, should he be arrested—do they think they will arrest him?”", "\"음, 만약 체포된다면, 정말 잡을 수 있다고들 보세요?\""]], [["“I hope so.”", "\"그러길 바랍니다.\""]], [["“If they should arrest him (I know that sometimes prisons afford means of escape), will you leave him in prison?”", "\"만약 체포된다면(감옥에서도 때로는 빠져나갈 길이 생긴다는 걸 압니다만), 그를 감옥에 그대로 두시겠어요?\""]], [["The procureur shook his head.", "검사가 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["“At least keep him there till my daughter be married.”", "\"적어도 제 딸이 결혼할 때까지만이라도 거기 두세요.\""]], [["“Impossible, madame; justice has its formalities.”", "\"불가능합니다, 부인. 사법에는 절차라는 게 있으니까요.\""]], [["“What, even for me?” said the baroness, half jesting, half in earnest.", "\"뭐라고요, 저를 위해서도요?\" 남작 부인이 반은 농담으로 반은 진심으로 말했다."]], [["“For all, even for myself among the rest,”", "\"모두를 위해서요. 그중에는 저 자신도 들어갑니다.\""], ["replied Villefort. 50063m", "빌포르가 대답했다."]], [["“Ah!” exclaimed the baroness, without expressing the ideas which the exclamation betrayed.", "\"아!\" 남작 부인이 그 탄성이 드러내는 속생각은 입에 담지 않은 채 외쳤다."], ["Villefort looked at her with that piercing glance which reads the secrets of the heart.", "빌포르는 마음속 비밀을 읽어 내는 그 꿰뚫는 눈길로 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["“Yes, I know what you mean,”", "\"그래요, 무슨 말씀이신지 압니다.\""], ["he said; “you refer to the terrible rumors spread abroad in the world, that the deaths which have kept me in mourning for the last three months, and from which Valentine has only escaped by a miracle, have not happened by natural means.”", "그가 말했다. \"지난 석 달간 저를 상중에 묶어 둔, 그리고 발랑틴만이 기적적으로 면한 그 죽음들이 자연스러운 원인으로 일어난 게 아니라는, 세상에 떠도는 그 끔찍한 소문을 두고 하시는 말씀이겠지요.\""]], [["“I was not thinking of that,”", "\"그 생각을 한 게 아니에요.\""], ["replied Madame Danglars quickly.", "당글라르 부인이 얼른 대답했다."]], [["“Yes, you were thinking of it, and with justice.", "\"아니요, 그 생각을 하셨고, 또 그럴 만합니다. 생각하지 않을 수 없으셨겠지요."], ["You could not help thinking of it, and saying to yourself, ‘you, who pursue crime so vindictively, answer now, why are there unpunished crimes in your dwelling?’”", "그리고 속으로 이렇게 되뇌셨을 겁니다. '그토록 집요하게 죄를 뒤쫓는 당신이, 이제 대답해 보시오. 어찌하여 당신의 집에는 벌받지 않은 죄들이 있는 거요?'\""], ["The baroness became pale.", "남작 부인의 얼굴이 창백해졌다."], ["“You were saying this, were you not?”", "\"이렇게 말씀하셨지요, 안 그렇습니까?\""]], [["“Well, I own it.”", "\"좋아요, 인정해요.\""]], [["“I will answer you.”", "\"제가 대답해 드리지요.\""]], [["Villefort drew his armchair nearer to Madame Danglars; then resting both hands upon his desk he said in a voice more hollow than usual:", "빌포르는 안락의자를 당글라르 부인 쪽으로 더 가까이 끌어당겼다. 그러고는 두 손을 책상에 얹은 채 여느 때보다 더 공허한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“There are crimes which remain unpunished because the criminals are unknown, and we might strike the innocent instead of the guilty; but when the culprits are discovered”", "\"벌받지 않은 채 남는 죄들이 있습니다. 범인이 누구인지 알 수 없어, 죄인 대신 무고한 사람을 칠 수도 있기 때문이지요. 하지만 일단 범인이 밝혀지면\""], ["(Villefort here extended his hand toward a large crucifix placed opposite to his desk)—“when they are discovered, I swear to you, by all I hold most sacred, that whoever they may be they shall die.", "(여기서 빌포르는 책상 맞은편에 놓인 커다란 십자가 쪽으로 손을 뻗었다.) \"일단 밝혀지면, 제가 가장 신성하게 여기는 모든 것을 걸고 부인께 맹세하건대, 그가 누구든 반드시 죽을 것입니다."], ["Now, after the oath I have just taken, and which I will keep, madame, dare you ask for mercy for that wretch!”", "자, 제가 방금 한, 그리고 반드시 지킬 이 맹세를 듣고서도, 부인은 그 비열한 놈을 위해 자비를 청하시겠습니까!\""]], [["“But, sir, are you sure he is as guilty as they say?”", "\"하지만 검사님, 그가 사람들이 말하는 만큼 정말로 죄가 있다고 확신하세요?\""]], [["“Listen; this is his description: ‘Benedetto, condemned, at the age of sixteen, for five years to the galleys for forgery.’ He promised well, as you see—first a runaway, then an assassin.”", "\"들어 보십시오. 이게 그자의 신원입니다. '베네데토, 열여섯 살에 문서 위조죄로 오 년 형을 선고받고 갤리선에 보내짐.' 보시다시피 싹수가 노랬지요. 처음엔 탈주범, 그다음엔 살인자였으니까요.\""]], [["“And who is this wretch?”", "\"대체 이 비열한 놈은 누구죠?\""]], [["“Who can tell?—a vagabond, a Corsican.”", "\"누가 알겠습니까? 부랑자에 코르시카 사람이지요.\""]], [["“Has no one owned him?”", "\"그를 자기 자식이라 한 사람이 아무도 없었나요?\""]], [["“No one; his parents are unknown.”", "\"아무도요. 그 부모가 누군지도 알 수 없습니다.\""]], [["“But who was the man who brought him from Lucca?”", "\"하지만 그를 루카에서 데려온 사람은 누구였죠?\""]], [["“Another rascal like himself, perhaps his accomplice.”", "\"그자와 똑같은 또 다른 악당이지요. 어쩌면 공범일지도요.\""], ["The baroness clasped her hands.", "남작 부인이 두 손을 맞잡았다."]], [["“Villefort,”", "\"빌포르.\""], ["she exclaimed in her softest and most captivating manner.", "그녀가 더없이 부드럽고 매혹적인 태도로 외쳤다."]], [["“For Heaven’s sake, madame,”", "\"제발, 부인.\""], ["said Villefort, with a firmness of expression not altogether free from harshness—“for Heaven’s sake, do not ask pardon of me for a guilty wretch!", "빌포르가 다소 매정한 기색마저 서린 단호한 표정으로 말했다. \"제발, 죄지은 비열한 놈을 위해 제게 용서를 청하지 마십시오!"], ["What am I?—the law.", "제가 무엇입니까? 법입니다."], ["Has the law any eyes to witness your grief?", "법에게 부인의 슬픔을 지켜볼 눈이 있습니까?"], ["Has the law ears to be melted by your sweet voice?", "법에게 부인의 감미로운 목소리에 녹아들 귀가 있습니까?"], ["Has the law a memory for all those soft recollections you endeavor to recall?", "법에게 부인이 떠올리려 애쓰는 그 모든 애틋한 추억을 기억할 머리가 있습니까?"], ["No, madame; the law has commanded, and when it commands it strikes.", "없습니다, 부인. 법은 명령했고, 명령할 때 법은 내리칩니다."], ["You will tell me that I am a living being, and not a code—a man, and not a volume.", "부인께서는 제가 법전이 아니라 살아 있는 존재요, 한 권의 책이 아니라 인간이라고 하시겠지요."], ["Look at me, madame—look around me.", "저를 보십시오, 부인. 제 주위를 둘러보십시오."], ["Has mankind treated me as a brother?", "인류가 저를 형제로 대해 주었습니까?"], ["Have men loved me?", "사람들이 저를 사랑했습니까?"], ["Have they spared me?", "저를 살려 주었습니까?"], ["Has anyone shown the mercy towards me that you now ask at my hands?", "부인께서 지금 제 손에 청하시는 그 자비를 누가 제게 보여 준 적이 있습니까?"], ["No, madame, they struck me, always struck me! 50065m", "없습니다, 부인. 그들은 저를 쳤고, 늘 쳤습니다!"]], [["“Woman, siren that you are, do you persist in fixing on me that fascinating eye, which reminds me that I ought to blush?", "여인이여, 세이렌 같은 여인이여, 제가 얼굴을 붉혀야 한다고 일깨우는 그 매혹의 눈길을 기어이 제게 고정하시겠습니까?"], ["Well, be it so; let me blush for the faults you know, and perhaps—perhaps for even more than those!", "좋습니다, 그렇게 하지요. 부인이 아시는 잘못들 때문에, 어쩌면 그보다 더한 잘못들 때문에 제가 얼굴을 붉히게 하십시오!"], ["But having sinned myself,—it may be more deeply than others,—I never rest till I have torn the disguises from my fellow-creatures, and found out their weaknesses.", "하지만 저 또한 죄를 지었기에, 어쩌면 남들보다 더 깊이 지었기에, 저는 제 동족의 위장을 벗겨 내고 그들의 약점을 찾아내기 전에는 결코 쉬지 않습니다."], ["I have always found them; and more,—I repeat it with joy, with triumph,—I have always found some proof of human perversity or error.", "저는 늘 찾아냈습니다. 그리고 더한 것은, 기쁨으로, 승리감으로 거듭 말하건대, 저는 늘 인간의 사악함이나 과오의 증거를 찾아냈다는 겁니다."], ["Every criminal I condemn seems to me living evidence that I am not a hideous exception to the rest.", "제가 단죄하는 죄인 하나하나가 제게는, 제가 나머지 인간들과 동떨어진 흉측한 예외가 아니라는 살아 있는 증거로 보입니다."], ["Alas, alas, alas; all the world is wicked; let us therefore strike at wickedness!”", "아, 아, 아, 온 세상이 사악합니다. 그러니 그 사악함을 칩시다!\""]], [["Villefort pronounced these last words with a feverish rage, which gave a ferocious eloquence to his words.", "빌포르는 이 마지막 말을 열에 들뜬 분노로 내뱉었고, 그 분노가 그의 말에 사나운 웅변을 실어 주었다."]], [["“But”’ said Madame Danglars, resolving to make a last effort, “this young man, though a murderer, is an orphan, abandoned by everybody.”", "\"하지만,\" 마지막 안간힘을 써 보기로 마음먹은 당글라르 부인이 말했다. \"이 젊은이는, 살인자이긴 해도, 모두에게 버림받은 고아예요.\""]], [["“So much the worse, or rather, so much the better; it has been so ordained that he may have none to weep his fate.”", "\"그러니 더 나쁘지요. 아니, 차라리 더 잘된 일입니다. 자기 운명을 슬퍼해 줄 이가 아무도 없도록 그렇게 정해진 거니까요.\""]], [["“But this is trampling on the weak, sir.”", "\"하지만 이건 약자를 짓밟는 거예요, 검사님.\""]], [["“The weakness of a murderer!”", "\"살인자의 약함을요!\""]], [["“His dishonor reflects upon us.”", "\"그의 불명예가 우리에게까지 미쳐요.\""]], [["“Is not death in my house?”", "\"우리 집에는 죽음이 있지 않습니까?\""]], [["“Oh, sir,”", "\"오, 검사님.\" 남작 부인이 외쳤다."], ["exclaimed the baroness, “you are without pity for others, well, then, I tell you they will have no mercy on you!”", "\"당신은 남들에게 자비가 없군요. 그렇다면 말씀드리지요. 사람들도 당신에게 자비를 베풀지 않을 거예요!\""]], [["“Be it so!” said Villefort, raising his arms to heaven with a threatening gesture.", "\"그래도 좋습니다!\" 빌포르가 위협하는 몸짓으로 두 팔을 하늘로 치켜들며 말했다."]], [["“At least, delay the trial till the next assizes; we shall then have six months before us.”", "\"적어도 다음 순회재판으로 재판을 미뤄 주세요. 그러면 우리에게 여섯 달의 여유가 생기잖아요.\""]], [["“No, madame,”", "\"안 됩니다, 부인.\""], ["said Villefort; “instructions have been given.", "빌포르가 말했다. \"지시가 이미 내려졌습니다."], ["There are yet five days left; five days are more than I require.", "아직 닷새가 남았지요. 닷새면 제게 필요한 것보다 넉넉합니다."], ["Do you not think that I also long for forgetfulness?", "저라고 망각을 갈망하지 않는 줄 아십니까?"], ["While working night and day, I sometimes lose all recollection of the past, and then I experience the same sort of happiness I can imagine the dead feel; still, it is better than suffering.”", "밤낮으로 일하다 보면, 저도 가끔은 과거의 기억을 모조리 잊습니다. 그러면 죽은 자들이 느끼리라 짐작되는 것과 같은 종류의 행복을 맛보지요. 그래도 그게 괴로워하는 것보다는 낫습니다.\""]], [["“But, sir, he has fled; let him escape—inaction is a pardonable offence.”", "\"하지만 검사님, 그는 달아났어요. 그냥 도망치게 두세요. 가만히 있는 건 용서받을 만한 잘못이잖아요.\""]], [["“I tell you it is too late; early this morning the telegraph was employed, and at this very minute——”", "\"이미 늦었다고 말씀드리지 않습니까. 오늘 이른 아침에 전신을 띄웠으니, 바로 이 순간에도……\""]], [["“Sir,”", "\"검사님.\""], ["said the valet de chambre, entering the room, “a dragoon has brought this despatch from the Minister of the Interior.”", "시종이 방으로 들어서며 말했다. \"용기병 하나가 내무대신께서 보내신 이 급보를 가져왔습니다.\""]], [["Villefort seized the letter, and hastily broke the seal.", "빌포르가 편지를 낚아채 황급히 봉인을 뜯었다."], ["Madame Danglars trembled with fear; Villefort started with joy.", "당글라르 부인은 두려움에 떨었고, 빌포르는 기쁨에 흠칫했다."]], [["“Arrested!”", "\"체포되었어요!\""], ["he exclaimed; “he was taken at Compiègne, and all is over.”", "그가 외쳤다. \"콩피에뉴에서 붙잡혔답니다. 이제 다 끝났습니다.\""]], [["Madame Danglars rose from her seat, pale and cold.", "당글라르 부인은 창백하고 싸늘한 얼굴로 자리에서 일어섰다."]], [["“Adieu, sir,”", "\"안녕히 계세요, 검사님.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Adieu, madame,” replied the king’s attorney, as in an almost joyful manner he conducted her to the door.", "\"안녕히 가십시오, 부인.\" 검사가 거의 기쁜 태도로 그녀를 문까지 배웅하며 대답했다."], ["Then, turning to his desk, he said, striking the letter with the back of his right hand:", "그러고는 책상으로 돌아서서, 오른손 등으로 편지를 탁 치며 말했다."]], [["“Come, I had a forgery, three robberies, and two cases of arson, I only wanted a murder, and here it is.", "\"자, 위조 한 건에 절도 세 건, 방화 두 건이 있었고, 살인 하나만 있으면 됐는데, 여기 있구나."], ["It will be a splendid session!”", "멋진 개정이 되겠어!\""]], [["# Chapter 100. The Apparition", "# Chapter 100. The Apparition"]], [["As the procureur had told Madame Danglars, Valentine was not yet recovered.", "빌포르가 당글라르 부인에게 말한 대로, 발랑틴은 아직 회복되지 않았다."], ["Bowed down with fatigue, she was indeed confined to her bed; and it was in her own room, and from the lips of Madame de Villefort, that she heard all the strange events we have related; we mean the flight of Eugénie and the arrest of Andrea Cavalcanti, or rather Benedetto, together with the accusation of murder pronounced against him.", "피로에 짓눌려 침대에 누워 있을 수밖에 없었다. 그녀는 자기 방에서, 빌포르 부인의 입을 통해 우리가 이야기한 그 기이한 사건들을 모두 전해 들었다. 외제니의 도주와 안드레아 카발칸티, 정확히는 베네데토의 체포, 그리고 그에게 내려진 살인 혐의에 관한 이야기였다."], ["But Valentine was so weak that this recital scarcely produced the same effect it would have done had she been in her usual state of health.", "그러나 발랑틴은 너무 쇠약했기에, 이 이야기가 평소의 건강한 상태였더라면 일으켰을 충격을 거의 주지 못했다."], ["Indeed, her brain was only the seat of vague ideas, and confused forms, mingled with strange fancies, alone presented themselves before her eyes.", "사실 그녀의 머릿속에는 막연한 생각과 흐릿한 형상만이 자리 잡았고, 기이한 환상이 뒤섞여 눈앞에 떠오를 뿐이었다."]], [["During the daytime Valentine’s perceptions remained tolerably clear, owing to the constant presence of M.", "낮 동안에는 발랑틴의 의식이 비교적 또렷했다. 노아르티에 씨가 늘 곁에 있었던 덕분이었다."], ["Noirtier, who caused himself to be carried to his granddaughter’s room, and watched her with his paternal tenderness; Villefort also, on his return from the law courts, frequently passed an hour or two with his father and child.", "그는 손녀의 방으로 자신을 데려가게 하여 아버지처럼 자애로운 눈길로 그녀를 지켰다. 빌포르 역시 법원에서 돌아오면 아버지와 딸 곁에서 한두 시간씩 보내곤 했다."]], [["At six o’clock Villefort retired to his study, at eight M. d’Avrigny himself arrived, bringing the night draught prepared for the young girl, and then M.", "여섯 시가 되면 빌포르는 서재로 물러갔고, 여덟 시에는 다브리니 씨가 손수 와서 어린 처녀를 위해 마련한 밤 약을 가져왔다. 그러면 노아르티에 씨는 방에서 옮겨졌다."], ["Noirtier was carried away. A nurse of the doctor’s choice succeeded them, and never left till about ten or eleven o’clock, when Valentine was asleep.", "의사가 고른 간병인이 그들을 대신해 자리를 지켰고, 발랑틴이 잠들고 나서야, 대략 열 시나 열한 시쯤이 되어서야 방을 떠났다."], ["As she went downstairs she gave the keys of Valentine’s room to M.", "그녀는 아래층으로 내려가면서 발랑틴의 방 열쇠를 빌포르 씨에게 건넸다."], ["de Villefort, so that no one could reach the sick-room excepting through that of Madame de Villefort and little Edward.", "그리하여 빌포르 부인과 어린 에두아르의 방을 거치지 않고는 아무도 병실에 들어갈 수 없었다."]], [["Every morning Morrel called on Noirtier to receive news of Valentine, and, extraordinary as it seemed, each day found him less uneasy.", "아침마다 모렐은 노아르티에를 찾아와 발랑틴의 소식을 들었다. 놀랍게도, 그는 날이 갈수록 불안이 줄어드는 것을 느꼈다."], ["Certainly, though Valentine still labored under dreadful nervous excitement, she was better; and moreover, Monte Cristo had told him when, half distracted, he had rushed to the count’s house, that if she were not dead in two hours she would be saved.", "발랑틴이 여전히 끔찍한 신경 발작에 시달리기는 했지만 분명 차도가 있었다. 게다가 그가 반쯤 정신이 나간 채 백작의 집으로 달려갔을 때, 몬테크리스토는 그녀가 두 시간 안에 죽지 않으면 살아날 것이라고 말했었다."], ["Now four days had elapsed, and Valentine still lived.", "그런데 나흘이 지났는데도 발랑틴은 여전히 살아 있었다."]], [["The nervous excitement of which we speak pursued Valentine even in her sleep, or rather in that state of somnolence which succeeded her waking hours; it was then, in the silence of night, in the dim light shed from the alabaster lamp on the chimney-piece, that she saw the shadows pass and repass which hover over the bed of sickness, and fan the fever with their trembling wings. First she fancied she saw her stepmother threatening her, then Morrel stretched his arms towards her; sometimes mere strangers, like the Count of Monte Cristo came to visit her; even the very furniture, in these moments of delirium, seemed to move, and this state lasted till about three o’clock in the morning, when a deep, heavy slumber overcame the young girl, from which she did not awake till daylight.", "우리가 말한 그 신경 발작은 잠 속에서도, 정확히는 깨어 있던 시간 뒤에 찾아오는 그 비몽사몽의 상태 속에서도 발랑틴을 따라다녔다. 밤의 고요 속에서, 벽난로 위 설화석고 등잔이 드리우는 희미한 빛 속에서, 그녀는 병상 위를 맴도는 그림자들이 오가는 것을 보았다. 그것들은 떨리는 날개로 열을 부채질했다. 처음에 그녀는 계모가 자신을 위협하는 모습을 본 듯했고, 그다음엔 모렐이 그녀를 향해 두 팔을 뻗었다. 때로는 몬테크리스토 백작 같은 낯선 이들이 찾아오기도 했다. 이 헛소리의 순간에는 가구마저 움직이는 듯했다. 이런 상태는 새벽 세 시쯤까지 이어졌고, 그제야 깊고 무거운 잠이 어린 처녀를 덮쳤다. 그녀는 날이 밝을 때까지 깨지 않았다."]], [["On the evening of the day on which Valentine had learned of the flight of Eugénie and the arrest of Benedetto,—Villefort having retired as well as Noirtier and d’Avrigny,—her thoughts wandered in a confused maze, alternately reviewing her own situation and the events she had just heard.", "발랑틴이 외제니의 도주와 베네데토의 체포 소식을 들은 그날 저녁, 빌포르도 노아르티에도 다브리니도 모두 물러간 뒤, 그녀의 생각은 혼란스러운 미로 속을 헤맸다. 자신의 처지와 방금 들은 사건들을 번갈아 되짚었다."]], [["Eleven o’clock had struck.", "열한 시를 알리는 종이 울렸다."], ["The nurse, having placed the beverage prepared by the doctor within reach of the patient, and locked the door, was listening with terror to the comments of the servants in the kitchen, and storing her memory with all the horrible stories which had for some months past amused the occupants of the antechambers in the house of the king’s attorney.", "간병인은 의사가 마련한 음료를 환자의 손이 닿는 곳에 놓고 문을 잠근 뒤, 부엌에서 하인들이 주고받는 이야기에 겁먹은 채 귀를 기울였다. 그리고 몇 달 전부터 검사장 댁 대기실 사람들의 입에 오르내리던 온갖 끔찍한 이야기들을 기억에 새겨 넣었다."], ["Meanwhile an unexpected scene was passing in the room which had been so carefully locked.", "그러는 사이, 그토록 단단히 잠가 두었던 방 안에서는 뜻밖의 장면이 벌어지고 있었다."]], [["Ten minutes had elapsed since the nurse had left; Valentine, who for the last hour had been suffering from the fever which returned nightly, incapable of controlling her ideas, was forced to yield to the excitement which exhausted itself in producing and reproducing a succession and recurrence of the same fancies and images.", "간병인이 떠난 지 십 분이 지났다. 한 시간 전부터 발랑틴은 밤마다 되돌아오는 열에 시달리고 있었다. 생각을 다스릴 수 없었던 그녀는 똑같은 환상과 영상을 끊임없이 만들어 내고 되풀이하다가 끝내 지쳐 버리는 그 흥분에 굴복할 수밖에 없었다."], ["The night-lamp threw out countless rays, each resolving itself into some strange form to her disordered imagination, when suddenly by its flickering light Valentine thought she saw the door of her library, which was in the recess by the chimney-piece, open slowly, though she in vain listened for the sound of the hinges on which it turned.", "야등은 수많은 빛줄기를 뿜어냈고, 그 하나하나가 그녀의 어지러운 상상 속에서 기이한 형상으로 변했다. 그러던 중 그 깜빡이는 빛 속에서, 발랑틴은 벽난로 옆 움푹 들어간 곳에 있는 서재 문이 천천히 열리는 것을 본 듯했다. 그러나 문이 돌아가는 돌쩌귀 소리를 들으려 애써도 아무 소리도 들리지 않았다."]], [["At any other time Valentine would have seized the silken bell-pull and summoned assistance, but nothing astonished her in her present situation.", "다른 때였다면 발랑틴은 비단 초인종 끈을 잡아당겨 도움을 청했을 것이다. 그러나 지금의 상태에서는 어떤 것에도 놀라지 않았다."], ["Her reason told her that all the visions she beheld were but the children of her imagination, and the conviction was strengthened by the fact that in the morning no traces remained of the nocturnal phantoms, who disappeared with the coming of daylight.", "그녀가 보는 모든 환상은 상상이 빚어낸 것일 뿐이라고 이성이 말해 주었다. 게다가 밤의 유령들은 날이 밝으면 사라져 아침이면 흔적조차 남지 않는다는 사실이 그 확신을 굳혀 주었다."]], [["From behind the door a human figure appeared, but the girl was too familiar with such apparitions to be alarmed, and therefore only stared, hoping to recognize Morrel.", "문 뒤에서 사람의 형체가 나타났다. 그러나 처녀는 그런 환영에 너무 익숙해진 터라 놀라지 않았다. 그저 모렐을 알아보길 바라며 가만히 바라볼 뿐이었다."], ["The figure advanced towards the bed and appeared to listen with profound attention.", "형체는 침대 쪽으로 다가오더니 무언가에 깊이 귀 기울이는 듯했다."], ["At this moment a ray of light glanced across the face of the midnight visitor.", "그 순간 한 줄기 빛이 한밤의 방문객의 얼굴을 스쳤다."]], [["“It is not he,”", "\"그가 아니야.\""], ["she murmured, and waited, in the assurance that this was but a dream, for the man to disappear or assume some other form.", "그녀가 중얼거렸다. 그리고 이것이 꿈일 뿐이라 믿으며, 그 사람이 사라지거나 다른 모습으로 변하기를 기다렸다."], ["Still, she felt her pulse, and finding it throb violently she remembered that the best method of dispelling such illusions was to drink, for a draught of the beverage prepared by the doctor to allay her fever seemed to cause a reaction of the brain, and for a short time she suffered less.", "그래도 그녀는 자기 맥을 짚어 보았고, 맥이 거세게 뛰는 것을 알자, 그런 환상을 떨쳐 내는 가장 좋은 방법은 무언가를 마시는 것임을 떠올렸다. 의사가 열을 가라앉히려고 마련한 음료를 한 모금 마시면 머리에 반응이 일어, 잠시나마 고통이 덜해지는 듯했기 때문이다."], ["Valentine therefore reached her hand towards the glass, but as soon as her trembling arm left the bed the apparition advanced more quickly towards her, and approached the young girl so closely that she fancied she heard his breath, and felt the pressure of his hand.", "그래서 발랑틴은 잔을 향해 손을 뻗었다. 그러나 떨리는 팔이 침대를 벗어나기가 무섭게 환영은 더 빠르게 다가왔고, 어린 처녀에게 바싹 다가서서 그녀는 그의 숨소리를 들은 것 같았고 그의 손이 누르는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["This time the illusion, or rather the reality, surpassed anything Valentine had before experienced; she began to believe herself really alive and awake, and the belief that her reason was this time not deceived made her shudder.", "이번에는 그 환각이, 아니 차라리 그 실제가, 발랑틴이 전에 겪었던 그 무엇보다도 생생했다. 그녀는 자신이 정말로 살아 깨어 있다고 믿기 시작했다. 이번만큼은 이성이 속고 있지 않다는 믿음에 그녀는 몸서리쳤다."], ["The pressure she felt was evidently intended to arrest her arm, and she slowly withdrew it.", "그녀가 느낀 압박은 분명 자신의 팔을 막으려는 것이었고, 그녀는 천천히 팔을 거두어들였다."], ["Then the figure, from whom she could not detach her eyes, and who appeared more protecting than menacing, took the glass, and walking towards the night-light held it up, as if to test its transparency.", "그러자 그녀가 눈을 뗄 수 없었던 그 형체는, 위협하기보다는 보호하려는 듯이, 잔을 집어 들고 야등 쪽으로 걸어가 마치 그 투명함을 살피려는 듯 빛에 비추어 보았다."], ["This did not seem sufficient; the man, or rather the ghost—for he trod so softly that no sound was heard—then poured out about a spoonful into the glass, and drank it.", "그것으로는 충분치 않은 모양이었다. 그 사내는, 아니 그 유령은 — 어찌나 발소리가 없는지 아무 소리도 들리지 않았으니 — 잔에 한 숟가락쯤 따라서 마셨다."]], [["Valentine witnessed this scene with a sentiment of stupefaction.", "발랑틴은 멍한 심정으로 이 장면을 지켜보았다."], ["Every minute she had expected that it would vanish and give place to another vision; but the man, instead of dissolving like a shadow, again approached her, and said in an agitated voice, “Now you may drink.”", "매 순간 그것이 사라지고 또 다른 환상이 자리를 대신하리라 기대했다. 그러나 그 사내는 그림자처럼 흩어지기는커녕 다시 그녀에게 다가오더니 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"이제 마셔도 됩니다.\""]], [["Valentine shuddered.", "발랑틴은 몸서리쳤다."], ["It was the first time one of these visions had ever addressed her in a living voice, and she was about to utter an exclamation. The man placed his finger on her lips.", "이런 환영들 가운데 하나가 살아 있는 목소리로 그녀에게 말을 건 것은 처음이었다. 그녀가 막 비명을 지르려는데, 사내가 그녀의 입술에 손가락을 갖다 댔다."]], [["“The Count of Monte Cristo!” she murmured.", "\"몬테크리스토 백작님!\" 그녀가 중얼거렸다."]], [["It was easy to see that no doubt now remained in the young girl’s mind as to the reality of the scene; her eyes started with terror, her hands trembled, and she rapidly drew the bedclothes closer to her.", "이제 어린 처녀의 마음속에는 이 장면이 실제라는 데 한 점 의심도 남지 않았음을 쉽게 알 수 있었다. 그녀의 눈은 공포로 휘둥그레졌고, 손은 떨렸으며, 이불을 황급히 끌어당겨 몸을 감쌌다."], ["Still, the presence of Monte Cristo at such an hour, his mysterious, fanciful, and extraordinary entrance into her room through the wall, might well seem impossibilities to her shattered reason.", "그래도 이런 시각에 몬테크리스토가 와 있다는 것, 벽을 통해 그녀의 방으로 들어온 그 신비롭고 기괴하며 터무니없는 등장은, 무너져 버린 그녀의 이성에는 도무지 있을 수 없는 일로만 보였다."]], [["“Do not call anyone—do not be alarmed,”", "\"아무도 부르지 마시오. 놀라지도 마시오.\""], ["said the count; “do not let a shade of suspicion or uneasiness remain in your breast; the man standing before you, Valentine (for this time it is no ghost), is nothing more than the tenderest father and the most respectful friend you could dream of.”", "백작이 말했다. \"한 점의 의심이나 불안도 가슴에 남겨 두지 마시오. 당신 앞에 서 있는 이 사람은, 발랑틴 — 이번엔 유령이 아니오 — 당신이 꿈꿀 수 있는 가장 다정한 아버지요, 가장 깍듯한 친구일 뿐이라오.\""]], [["Valentine could not reply; the voice which indicated the real presence of a being in the room, alarmed her so much that she feared to utter a syllable; still the expression of her eyes seemed to inquire, “If your intentions are pure, why are you here?”", "발랑틴은 대답할 수 없었다. 방 안에 진짜로 존재하는 누군가를 알려 주는 그 목소리에 그녀는 너무도 겁이 나서 한마디도 입 밖에 낼 수 없었다. 그래도 그녀의 눈빛은 이렇게 묻는 듯했다."], ["The count’s marvellous sagacity understood all that was passing in the young girl’s mind.", "'당신의 뜻이 순수하다면, 어찌하여 여기 있는 거죠?' 백작은 놀라운 통찰력으로 어린 처녀의 마음속에 오가는 모든 것을 알아챘다."]], [["“Listen to me,”", "\"내 말을 들으시오.\" 그가 말했다. \"아니, 차라리 나를 보시오."], ["he said, “or, rather, look upon me; look at my face, paler even than usual, and my eyes, red with weariness—for four days I have not closed them, for I have been constantly watching you, to protect and preserve you for Maximilian.”", "평소보다 더 창백한 내 얼굴을, 피로로 붉어진 내 눈을 보시오. 나흘 동안 한숨도 붙이지 못했소. 막시밀리앙을 위해 당신을 지키고 살리려고, 줄곧 당신을 지켜보았기 때문이오.\""]], [["The blood mounted rapidly to the cheeks of Valentine, for the name just announced by the count dispelled all the fear with which his presence had inspired her.", "발랑틴의 뺨에 피가 빠르게 올라왔다. 방금 백작이 입에 올린 그 이름이, 그의 존재가 불러일으켰던 모든 두려움을 흩어 놓았기 때문이다."]], [["“Maximilian!”", "\"막시밀리앙!\" 그녀가 외쳤다."], ["she exclaimed, and so sweet did the sound appear to her, that she repeated it—“Maximilian!—has he then owned all to you?”", "그 소리가 어찌나 달콤하게 들렸던지, 그녀는 다시 한번 되뇌었다. \"막시밀리앙! 그가 당신에게 모든 걸 털어놓았군요?\""]], [["“Everything.", "\"전부 다요."], ["He told me your life was his, and I have promised him that you shall live.”", "그는 자기 목숨이 당신에게 달려 있다고 말했고, 나는 당신을 살리겠다고 그에게 약속했소.\""]], [["“You have promised him that I shall live?”", "\"제가 살아날 거라고 그에게 약속하셨다고요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["“But, sir, you spoke of vigilance and protection.", "\"하지만, 선생님, 방금 지키고 보호한다고 하셨잖아요."], ["Are you a doctor?”", "의사이신가요?\""]], [["“Yes; the best you could have at the present time, believe me.”", "\"그렇소. 지금 이 순간 당신이 가질 수 있는 가장 훌륭한 의사라오, 믿으시오.\""]], [["“But you say you have watched?”", "\"하지만 줄곧 지켜보셨다고요?\""], ["said Valentine uneasily; “where have you been?—I have not seen you.”", "발랑틴이 불안하게 물었다. \"어디 계셨나요? 저는 뵌 적이 없는데요.\""]], [["The count extended his hand towards the library.", "백작은 서재 쪽으로 손을 뻗었다."]], [["“I was hidden behind that door,”", "\"저 문 뒤에 숨어 있었소.\""], ["he said, “which leads into the next house, which I have rented.”", "그가 말했다. \"저 문은 옆집으로 통하는데, 그 집을 내가 빌렸다오.\""]], [["Valentine turned her eyes away, and, with an indignant expression of pride and modest fear, exclaimed:", "발랑틴은 눈길을 돌렸다. 그리고 분노에 찬 자존심과 조심스러운 두려움이 어린 표정으로 외쳤다."]], [["“Sir, I think you have been guilty of an unparalleled intrusion, and that what you call protection is more like an insult.”", "\"선생님, 제가 보기에 선생님은 유례없는 무례를 저지르셨어요. 보호라고 하시는 것이 차라리 모욕에 가까워요.\""]], [["“Valentine,”", "\"발랑틴,\" 그가 대답했다."], ["he answered, “during my long watch over you, all I have observed has been what people visited you, what nourishment was prepared, and what beverage was served; then, when the latter appeared dangerous to me, I entered, as I have now done, and substituted, in the place of the poison, a healthful draught; which, instead of producing the death intended, caused life to circulate in your veins.”", "\"당신을 오래 지켜보는 동안, 내가 살핀 것은 누가 당신을 찾아왔는지, 어떤 음식이 준비되고 어떤 음료가 나오는지뿐이었소. 그러다 그 음료가 위험해 보이면, 지금처럼 들어와 독약 대신 몸에 이로운 약을 갈아 두었소. 그 약은 당신을 죽이려던 그 독 대신, 당신의 핏줄에 생명을 돌게 했지요.\""]], [["“Poison—death!”", "\"독약, 죽음이라니!\""], ["exclaimed Valentine, half believing herself under the influence of some feverish hallucination; “what are you saying, sir?”", "발랑틴이 외쳤다. 그녀는 자신이 어떤 열에 들뜬 환각에 빠져 있는 게 아닌가 반쯤 의심하고 있었다. \"무슨 말씀이세요, 선생님?\""]], [["50071m\n“Hush, my child,”", "\"쉿, 내 아이.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, again placing his finger upon her lips, “I did say poison and death. But drink some of this;”", "몬테크리스토가 다시 그녀의 입술에 손가락을 갖다 대며 말했다. \"분명 독약이라, 죽음이라 했소."], ["and the count took a bottle from his pocket, containing a red liquid, of which he poured a few drops into the glass.", "하지만 이걸 좀 마시구려.\" 백작은 주머니에서 붉은 액체가 담긴 작은 병을 꺼내, 잔에 몇 방울 떨어뜨렸다."], ["“Drink this, and then take nothing more tonight.”", "\"이걸 마시고, 오늘 밤엔 더 이상 아무것도 들지 마시오.\""]], [["Valentine stretched out her hand, but scarcely had she touched the glass when she drew back in fear.", "발랑틴은 손을 뻗었으나, 잔에 닿기가 무섭게 겁이 나 다시 거두었다."], ["Monte Cristo took the glass, drank half its contents, and then presented it to Valentine, who smiled and swallowed the rest.", "몬테크리스토는 잔을 들어 절반을 마신 뒤 발랑틴에게 건넸고, 그녀는 미소 지으며 나머지를 삼켰다."]], [["“Oh, yes,”", "\"아, 그래요.\""], ["she exclaimed, “I recognize the flavor of my nocturnal beverage which refreshed me so much, and seemed to ease my aching brain.", "그녀가 외쳤다. \"저를 그토록 개운하게 해 주고, 욱신거리는 머리를 가라앉혀 주는 듯했던 그 밤 음료의 맛이 나요."], ["Thank you, sir, thank you!”", "고맙습니다, 선생님, 고맙습니다!\""]], [["“This is how you have lived during the last four nights, Valentine,”", "\"지난 나흘 밤 동안 당신은 이렇게 살아남은 것이라오, 발랑틴.\""], ["said the count.", "백작이 말했다."], ["“But, oh, how I passed that time!", "\"하지만, 아, 나는 그 시간을 어떻게 보냈는지!"], ["Oh, the wretched hours I have endured—the torture to which I have submitted when I saw the deadly poison poured into your glass, and how I trembled lest you should drink it before I could find time to throw it away!”", "아, 내가 견뎌 낸 그 비참한 시간들이라니 — 당신 잔에 치명적인 독이 부어지는 것을 보았을 때 내가 감당한 그 고문이라니. 그리고 그것을 내가 미처 버릴 틈도 없이 당신이 마셔 버릴까 봐 얼마나 떨었던지!\""]], [["“Sir,”", "\"선생님,\""], ["said Valentine, at the height of her terror, “you say you endured tortures when you saw the deadly poison poured into my glass; but if you saw this, you must also have seen the person who poured it?”", "발랑틴이 극도의 공포에 사로잡혀 말했다. \"제 잔에 치명적인 독이 부어지는 것을 보고 고문을 견디셨다고 하셨죠. 하지만 그걸 보셨다면, 그걸 부은 사람도 보셨겠군요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["Valentine raised herself in bed, and drew over her chest, which appeared whiter than snow, the embroidered cambric, still moist with the cold dews of delirium, to which were now added those of terror.", "발랑틴은 침대에서 몸을 일으켜, 눈보다 희게 보이는 가슴 위로 수놓은 고운 천을 끌어당겼다. 그 천은 헛소리의 차가운 식은땀으로 아직 축축했고, 이제 거기엔 공포의 식은땀까지 더해졌다."], ["“You saw the person?”", "\"그 사람을 보셨다고요?\""], ["repeated the young girl.", "처녀가 거듭 물었다."]], [["“Yes,” repeated the count.", "\"그렇소.\" 백작이 거듭 말했다."]], [["“What you tell me is horrible, sir.", "\"선생님, 말씀이 끔찍해요."], ["You wish to make me believe something too dreadful.", "너무도 무서운 것을 제게 믿게 하려 하시는군요. 뭐라고요?"], ["What?—attempt to murder me in my father’s house, in my room, on my bed of sickness?", "제 아버지의 집에서, 제 방에서, 병상에 누운 저를 죽이려 했다고요? 아, 저를 내버려 두세요, 선생님."], ["Oh, leave me, sir; you are tempting me—you make me doubt the goodness of Providence—it is impossible, it cannot be!”", "저를 부추기고 계세요. 섭리의 선함마저 의심하게 만드시네요. 그럴 리 없어요, 그럴 수가 없어요!\""]], [["“Are you the first that this hand has stricken?", "\"이 손이 친 첫 번째 사람이 당신이라 생각하오?"], ["Have you not seen M. de Saint-Méran, Madame de Saint-Méran, Barrois, all fall? Would not M.", "생메랑 씨가, 생메랑 부인이, 바루아가 모두 쓰러지는 것을 보지 못했소?"], ["Noirtier also have fallen a victim, had not the treatment he has been pursuing for the last three years neutralized the effects of the poison?”", "지난 삼 년간 받아 온 치료가 독의 효력을 누그러뜨리지 않았더라면, 노아르티에 씨 또한 희생되지 않았겠소?\""]], [["“Oh, Heaven,”", "\"오, 하느님.\""], ["said Valentine; “is this the reason why grandpapa has made me share all his beverages during the last month?”", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"그래서 할아버지가 지난 한 달 동안 당신의 모든 음료를 저와 나누어 드시게 한 거였군요?\""]], [["“And have they all tasted of a slightly bitter flavor, like that of dried orange-peel?”", "\"그 음료들이 모두 말린 오렌지 껍질 같은, 약간 쌉쌀한 맛이 나지 않았소?\""]], [["“Oh, yes, yes!”", "\"아, 그래요, 맞아요!\""]], [["“Then that explains all,”", "\"그렇다면 모든 게 설명되는군.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["“Your grandfather knows, then, that a poisoner lives here; perhaps he even suspects the person.", "\"당신 할아버지는 이 집에 독을 쓰는 자가 산다는 것을 알고 있소. 어쩌면 그자가 누구인지 짐작하고 있을지도 모르오."], ["He has been fortifying you, his beloved child, against the fatal effects of the poison, which has failed because your system was already impregnated with it.", "그분은 자신의 사랑하는 아이인 당신을 독의 치명적인 효력에 견딜 수 있도록 단련시켜 온 것이오. 그래서 독이 듣지 않았던 거요. 당신의 몸이 이미 그것에 길들여져 있었으니까."], ["But even this would have availed little against a more deadly medium of death employed four days ago, which is generally but too fatal.”", "하지만 나흘 전에 쓴 더 치명적인 독에는 그것조차 거의 소용이 없었을 거요. 그 독은 대개 너무도 어김없이 목숨을 앗아 가니까.\""]], [["“But who, then, is this assassin, this murderer?”", "\"하지만 그 살인자가, 그 흉악범이 대체 누군가요?\""]], [["“Let me also ask you a question.", "\"나도 한 가지 묻고 싶소."], ["Have you never seen anyone enter your room at night?”", "밤에 누군가 당신 방에 들어오는 것을 본 적이 없소?\""]], [["“Oh, yes; I have frequently seen shadows pass close to me, approach, and disappear; but I took them for visions raised by my feverish imagination, and indeed when you entered I thought I was under the influence of delirium.”", "\"아, 있어요. 제 곁을 스쳐 지나가 다가왔다가 사라지는 그림자들을 자주 봤어요. 하지만 열에 들뜬 상상이 빚어낸 환영이라 여겼지요. 사실 선생님이 들어오셨을 때도 제가 헛것을 보고 있는 줄 알았어요.\""]], [["“Then you do not know who it is that attempts your life?”", "\"그렇다면 당신은 누가 당신의 목숨을 노리는지 모르는 거요?\""]], [["50073m\n“No,”", "\"몰라요.\""], ["said Valentine; “who could desire my death?”", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"누가 제 죽음을 바라겠어요?\""]], [["“You shall know it now, then,”", "\"이제 곧 알게 될 거요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, listening.", "몬테크리스토가 귀를 기울이며 말했다."]], [["“How do you mean?”", "\"무슨 뜻이세요?\""], ["said Valentine, looking anxiously around.", "발랑틴이 불안하게 주위를 둘러보며 물었다."]], [["“Because you are not feverish or delirious tonight, but thoroughly awake; midnight is striking, which is the hour murderers choose.”", "\"오늘 밤 당신은 열에 들뜨지도, 헛소리를 하지도 않고, 또렷이 깨어 있으니까. 지금 자정을 알리는 종이 울리고 있소. 살인자들이 택하는 시각이지요.\""]], [["“Oh, heavens,”", "\"오, 맙소사.\""], ["exclaimed Valentine, wiping off the drops which ran down her forehead.", "발랑틴이 이마를 타고 흐르는 땀방울을 닦으며 외쳤다."], ["Midnight struck slowly and sadly; every hour seemed to strike with leaden weight upon the heart of the poor girl.", "자정을 알리는 종이 느리고도 구슬프게 울렸다. 매 시각의 종소리가 가엾은 처녀의 가슴을 납덩이처럼 무겁게 짓누르는 듯했다."]], [["“Valentine,”", "\"발랑틴,\" 백작이 말했다."], ["said the count, “summon up all your courage; still the beatings of your heart; do not let a sound escape you, and feign to be asleep; then you will see.”", "\"온 힘을 다해 용기를 내시오. 심장의 고동을 가라앉히고, 어떤 소리도 내지 말고, 잠든 척하시오. 그러면 보게 될 거요.\""]], [["Valentine seized the count’s hand.", "발랑틴이 백작의 손을 붙잡았다."], ["“I think I hear a noise,”", "\"무슨 소리가 들리는 것 같아요.\""], ["she said; “leave me.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"어서 가세요.\""]], [["“Good-bye, for the present,”", "\"그럼 잠시 작별이오.\""], ["replied the count, walking upon tiptoe towards the library door, and smiling with an expression so sad and paternal that the young girl’s heart was filled with gratitude.", "백작이 발끝으로 서재 문을 향해 걸어가며 대답했다. 그는 어찌나 슬프고도 아버지 같은 표정으로 미소 지었던지, 어린 처녀의 가슴은 감사로 가득 찼다."]], [["Before closing the door he turned around once more, and said, “Not a movement—not a word; let them think you asleep, or perhaps you may be killed before I have the power of helping you.”", "문을 닫기 전에 그는 한 번 더 돌아서서 말했다. \"꼼짝도 하지 말고, 한마디도 하지 마시오. 그들이 당신을 잠들었다고 여기게 하시오. 안 그러면 내가 당신을 도울 새도 없이 당신이 죽임을 당할지도 모르오.\""]], [["And with this fearful injunction the count disappeared through the door, which noiselessly closed after him.", "이 섬뜩한 당부를 남기고 백작은 문 너머로 사라졌다. 문은 소리 없이 그의 뒤에서 닫혔다."]], [["# Chapter 101. Locusta", "# Chapter 101. Locusta"]], [["Valentine was alone; two other clocks, slower than that of Saint-Philippe-du-Roule, struck the hour of midnight from different directions, and excepting the rumbling of a few carriages all was silent.", "발랑틴은 혼자였다. 생필리프뒤룰 성당보다 느린 다른 두 시계가 사방에서 자정을 알렸다. 마차 몇 대가 굴러가는 소리를 빼면 사방이 고요했다."], ["Then Valentine’s attention was engrossed by the clock in her room, which marked the seconds.", "이윽고 발랑틴의 주의는 자기 방의 시계로 쏠렸다. 초를 새기는 그 시계였다."], ["She began counting them, remarking that they were much slower than the beatings of her heart; and still she doubted,—the inoffensive Valentine could not imagine that anyone should desire her death.", "그녀는 초를 헤아리기 시작했고, 그것이 심장 박동보다 훨씬 느리다는 것을 깨달았다. 그러면서도 의심은 가시지 않았다. 남에게 해를 끼친 적 없는 발랑틴으로서는 누군가 자신의 죽음을 바란다는 것을 상상조차 할 수 없었다. 어째서?"], ["Why should they?", "무슨 목적으로?"], ["To what end? What had she done to excite the malice of an enemy?", "자신이 무슨 짓을 했기에 적의 악의를 불러일으켰단 말인가?"]], [["There was no fear of her falling asleep.", "잠들 염려는 없었다."], ["One terrible idea pressed upon her mind,—that someone existed in the world who had attempted to assassinate her, and who was about to endeavor to do so again.", "한 가지 끔찍한 생각이 그녀의 마음을 짓누르고 있었다. 이 세상에 자신을 죽이려 했던 누군가가 있고, 그자가 다시 그 일을 시도하려 한다는 생각이었다."], ["Supposing this person, wearied at the inefficacy of the poison, should, as Monte Cristo intimated, have recourse to steel!—What if the count should have no time to run to her rescue!—What if her last moments were approaching, and she should never again see Morrel!", "만약 그자가 독약이 듣지 않는 데 지쳐, 몬테크리스토가 넌지시 일렀듯이 칼에 의지한다면! 만약 백작이 자신을 구하러 달려올 틈이 없다면! 만약 마지막 순간이 다가오고 있어서, 다시는 모렐을 보지 못하게 된다면!"]], [["When this terrible chain of ideas presented itself, Valentine was nearly persuaded to ring the bell, and call for help.", "이 끔찍한 생각의 사슬이 떠오르자, 발랑틴은 벨을 울려 도움을 청할 뻔했다."], ["But through the door she fancied she saw the luminous eye of the count—that eye which lived in her memory, and the recollection overwhelmed her with so much shame that she asked herself whether any amount of gratitude could ever repay his adventurous and devoted friendship.", "그러나 문 너머로 백작의 빛나는 눈이 보이는 것만 같았다. 기억 속에 살아 있는 그 눈이었다. 그 기억이 어찌나 부끄러움으로 그녀를 짓눌렀던지, 아무리 큰 감사로도 그의 그 무모하고 헌신적인 우정을 갚을 수 있을까 하는 생각마저 들었다."]], [["Twenty minutes, twenty tedious minutes, passed thus, then ten more, and at last the clock struck the half-hour.", "이십 분, 지루한 이십 분이 그렇게 흘렀고, 다시 십 분이 지나, 마침내 시계가 삼십 분을 알렸다."]], [["Just then the sound of finger-nails slightly grating against the door of the library informed Valentine that the count was still watching, and recommended her to do the same; at the same time, on the opposite side, that is towards Edward’s room, Valentine fancied that she heard the creaking of the floor; she listened attentively, holding her breath till she was nearly suffocated; the lock turned, and the door slowly opened.", "바로 그때 서재 문을 가볍게 긁는 손톱 소리가, 백작이 여전히 지켜보고 있으니 그녀에게도 그렇게 하라고 일러 주었다. 동시에 반대편, 그러니까 에두아르의 방 쪽에서 마룻바닥이 삐걱이는 소리가 들리는 듯했다. 발랑틴은 숨이 막힐 만큼 호흡을 참으며 귀를 곤두세웠다."], ["Valentine had raised herself upon her elbow, and had scarcely time to throw herself down on the bed and shade her eyes with her arm; then, trembling, agitated, and her heart beating with indescribable terror, she awaited the event.", "자물쇠가 돌아가고, 문이 천천히 열렸다. 발랑틴은 팔꿈치로 몸을 일으켰다가, 가까스로 침대에 다시 몸을 던지고 팔로 눈을 가렸다. 그러고는 부들부들 떨며, 동요한 채, 형언할 수 없는 공포로 심장이 두근거리는 가운데 일어날 일을 기다렸다."]], [["Someone approached the bed and drew back the curtains.", "누군가 침대로 다가와 휘장을 젖혔다."], ["Valentine summoned every effort, and breathed with that regular respiration which announces tranquil sleep.", "발랑틴은 온 힘을 다해, 평온한 잠을 알리는 고른 호흡으로 숨을 쉬었다."]], [["“Valentine!”", "\"발랑틴!\""], ["said a low voice.", "낮은 목소리가 말했다."]], [["The girl shuddered to the heart but did not reply.", "소녀는 가슴 깊이 몸서리쳤으나 대답하지 않았다."]], [["“Valentine,”", "\"발랑틴.\""], ["repeated the same voice.", "같은 목소리가 되풀이했다."]], [["Still silent: Valentine had promised not to wake.", "여전히 침묵. 발랑틴은 깨어나지 않기로 약속했던 것이다."], ["Then everything was still, excepting that Valentine heard the almost noiseless sound of some liquid being poured into the glass she had just emptied.", "그러자 모든 것이 고요해졌고, 다만 방금 비운 잔에 어떤 액체가 따라지는 거의 들리지 않는 소리만이 들려왔다."], ["Then she ventured to open her eyelids, and glance over her extended arm.", "그제야 그녀는 눈꺼풀을 떠, 뻗은 팔 너머로 흘끔 보았다."], ["She saw a woman in a white dressing-gown pouring a liquor from a phial into her glass.", "흰 실내복 차림의 여인이 작은 병에서 잔으로 약물을 따르고 있었다."], ["During this short time Valentine must have held her breath, or moved in some slight degree, for the woman, disturbed, stopped and leaned over the bed, in order the better to ascertain whether Valentine slept: it was Madame de Villefort.", "그 짧은 사이에 발랑틴이 숨을 멈추었거나 아주 조금 몸을 움직였던 모양으로, 여인은 흠칫하더니 동작을 멈추고 발랑틴이 자고 있는지 더 잘 확인하려고 침대 위로 몸을 숙였다. 빌포르 부인이었다."]], [["On recognizing her step-mother, Valentine could not repress a shudder, which caused a vibration in the bed.", "계모를 알아본 발랑틴은 몸서리를 억누르지 못했고, 그 떨림이 침대를 진동시켰다."], ["Madame de Villefort instantly stepped back close to the wall, and there, shaded by the bed-curtains, she silently and attentively watched the slightest movement of Valentine.", "빌포르 부인은 즉시 벽 가까이로 물러섰고, 침대 휘장에 가려진 채 발랑틴의 아주 미세한 움직임까지 소리 없이 주의 깊게 살폈다."], ["The latter recollected the terrible caution of Monte Cristo; she fancied that the hand not holding the phial clasped a long sharp knife.", "발랑틴은 몬테크리스토의 무서운 경고를 떠올렸다. 작은 병을 들지 않은 손이 길고 날카로운 칼을 쥐고 있는 것만 같았다."], ["Then collecting all her remaining strength, she forced herself to close her eyes; but this simple operation upon the most delicate organs of our frame, generally so easy to accomplish, became almost impossible at this moment, so much did curiosity struggle to retain the eyelid open and learn the truth.", "그래서 남은 힘을 다 끌어모아 억지로 눈을 감으려 했다. 그러나 우리 몸의 가장 섬세한 기관에 하는 이 단순한 동작이, 평소에는 그토록 쉬운 일이건만, 이 순간에는 거의 불가능해졌다. 진실을 알고자 눈꺼풀을 뜬 채로 두려는 호기심이 그만큼 강하게 맞섰던 것이다."], ["Madame de Villefort, however, reassured by the silence, which was alone disturbed by the regular breathing of Valentine, again extended her hand, and half hidden by the curtains succeeded in emptying the contents of the phial into the glass.", "그러나 빌포르 부인은 발랑틴의 고른 호흡 말고는 아무것도 흩뜨리지 않는 그 고요함에 안심했는지, 다시 손을 뻗어 휘장에 반쯤 가려진 채 작은 병의 내용물을 잔에 모두 비우는 데 성공했다."], ["Then she retired so gently that Valentine did not know she had left the room.", "그러고는 어찌나 살그머니 물러갔던지 발랑틴은 그녀가 방을 떠난 줄도 몰랐다."], ["She only witnessed the withdrawal of the arm—the fair round arm of a woman but twenty-five years old, and who yet spread death around her. 50077m", "그녀가 본 것은 팔이 거두어지는 모습뿐이었다. 이제 겨우 스물다섯 살, 그러면서도 주위에 죽음을 뿌리고 다니는 여인의 희고 둥근 팔이었다."]], [["It is impossible to describe the sensations experienced by Valentine during the minute and a half Madame de Villefort remained in the room.", "빌포르 부인이 방에 머문 일 분 삼십 초 동안 발랑틴이 느낀 감정을 묘사하기란 불가능하다."]], [["The grating against the library-door aroused the young girl from the stupor in which she was plunged, and which almost amounted to insensibility. She raised her head with an effort.", "서재 문을 긁는 소리가, 거의 감각을 잃을 지경에 이른 마비 상태에 빠져 있던 소녀를 일깨웠다. 그녀는 안간힘을 다해 고개를 들었다."], ["The noiseless door again turned on its hinges, and the Count of Monte Cristo reappeared.", "소리 없는 문이 다시 돌쩌귀를 돌렸고, 몬테크리스토 백작이 다시 나타났다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"자,\""], ["said he, “do you still doubt?”", "그가 말했다. \"이래도 의심이 드십니까?\""]], [["“Oh,” murmured the young girl.", "\"오.\" 소녀가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Have you seen?”", "\"보셨지요?\""]], [["“Alas!”", "\"아아!\""]], [["“Did you recognize?”", "\"알아보셨고요?\""], ["Valentine groaned.", "발랑틴이 신음했다."]], [["“Oh, yes;”", "\"오, 네.\""], ["she said, “I saw, but I cannot believe!”", "그녀가 말했다. \"보았어요, 하지만 믿을 수가 없어요!\""]], [["“Would you rather die, then, and cause Maximilian’s death?”", "\"그럼 차라리 죽어, 막시밀리앙까지 죽게 하시겠습니까?\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오.\""], ["repeated the young girl, almost bewildered, “can I not leave the house?—can I not escape?”", "소녀가 거의 넋이 나간 채 되풀이했다. \"이 집을 떠날 수는 없나요? 달아날 수는 없나요?\""]], [["“Valentine, the hand which now threatens you will pursue you everywhere; your servants will be seduced with gold, and death will be offered to you disguised in every shape.", "\"발랑틴, 지금 당신을 위협하는 그 손은 어디든 당신을 쫓아갈 겁니다. 하인들은 황금에 매수될 테고, 죽음은 온갖 모습으로 위장한 채 당신에게 다가올 겁니다."], ["You will find it in the water you drink from the spring, in the fruit you pluck from the tree.”", "샘에서 길어 마시는 물에서도, 나무에서 따 먹는 과일에서도 당신은 죽음을 만나게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["“But did you not say that my kind grandfather’s precaution had neutralized the poison?”", "\"하지만 자상하신 할아버지의 대비책이 독을 무력화했다고 하지 않으셨나요?\""]], [["“Yes, but not against a strong dose; the poison will be changed, and the quantity increased.”", "\"그랬지요, 하지만 강한 양에는 듣지 않습니다. 독이 바뀔 테고, 양도 늘어날 겁니다.\""], ["He took the glass and raised it to his lips. “It is already done,”", "그는 잔을 들어 입가로 가져갔다. \"벌써 그렇게 되었습니다.\""], ["he said; “brucine is no longer employed, but a simple narcotic!", "그가 말했다. \"이제 브루신이 아니라 단순한 마취제를 쓰는군요!"], ["I can recognize the flavor of the alcohol in which it has been dissolved.", "이걸 녹인 알코올 냄새를 알아볼 수 있습니다."], ["If you had taken what Madame de Villefort has poured into your glass, Valentine—Valentine—you would have been doomed!”", "만약 빌포르 부인이 당신 잔에 따른 것을 마셨더라면, 발랑틴, 발랑틴, 당신은 끝장이 났을 겁니다!\""]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["exclaimed the young girl, “why am I thus pursued?”", "소녀가 외쳤다. \"어째서 제가 이렇게 쫓기는 거죠?\""]], [["“Why?—are you so kind—so good—so unsuspicious of ill, that you cannot understand, Valentine?”", "\"어째서냐고요? 당신은 그토록 착하고, 그토록 선하고, 악을 의심할 줄 모르는 사람이라 이해하지 못하는 겁니까, 발랑틴?\""]], [["“No, I have never injured her.”", "\"네, 저는 한 번도 그분을 해친 적이 없어요.\""]], [["“But you are rich, Valentine; you have 200,000 livres a year, and you prevent her son from enjoying these 200,000 livres.”", "\"하지만 당신은 부자입니다, 발랑틴. 당신에게는 연 이십만 리브르의 수입이 있고, 당신이 그 부인의 아들이 그 이십만 리브르를 누리지 못하게 막고 있는 겁니다.\""]], [["“How so?", "\"어떻게요?"], ["The fortune is not her gift, but is inherited from my relations.”", "그 재산은 그분이 준 것이 아니라 제 친족에게서 물려받은 것인데요.\""]], [["“Certainly; and that is why M.", "\"바로 그렇지요."], ["and Madame de Saint-Méran have died; that is why M.", "그래서 생메랑 부부가 돌아가신 겁니다."], ["Noirtier was sentenced the day he made you his heir; that is why you, in your turn, are to die—it is because your father would inherit your property, and your brother, his only son, succeed to his.”", "그래서 누아르티에 씨가 당신을 상속자로 정한 날 사형을 선고받은 것이고요. 그래서 이번에는 당신이 죽어야 하는 겁니다. 당신 아버지가 당신 재산을 물려받고, 그의 외아들인 당신 남동생이 다시 아버지의 재산을 물려받도록 말입니다.\""]], [["“Edward?", "\"에두아르가요?"], ["Poor child!", "가엾은 아이!"], ["Are all these crimes committed on his account?”", "이 모든 범죄가 그 애를 위해 저질러진 건가요?\""]], [["“Ah, then you at length understand?”", "\"아, 이제야 이해하시는군요?\""]], [["“Heaven grant that this may not be visited upon him!”", "\"부디 이 일이 그 애에게 화로 돌아가지 않기를!\""]], [["“Valentine, you are an angel!”", "\"발랑틴, 당신은 천사로군요!\""]], [["“But why is my grandfather allowed to live?”", "\"하지만 할아버지는 어째서 살려 두는 거죠?\""]], [["“It was considered, that you dead, the fortune would naturally revert to your brother, unless he were disinherited; and besides, the crime appearing useless, it would be folly to commit it.”", "\"당신이 죽으면 재산은 자연히 동생에게 돌아갈 텐데, 그가 상속에서 제외되지 않는 한 그렇지요. 게다가 굳이 쓸데없는 범죄를 저지르는 건 어리석은 짓이라 여긴 겁니다.\""]], [["“And is it possible that this frightful combination of crimes has been invented by a woman?”", "\"한 여자가 이토록 끔찍한 범죄의 조합을 꾸며냈다는 게 가능한 일인가요?\""]], [["“Do you recollect in the arbor of the Hôtel des Postes, at Perugia, seeing a man in a brown cloak, whom your stepmother was questioning upon _aqua tofana_?", "\"페루자의 호텔 데 포스트 정자에서, 갈색 외투를 걸친 한 사내에게 당신 계모가 아쿠아 토파나에 대해 묻던 것을 기억하십니까?"], ["Well, ever since then, the infernal project has been ripening in her brain.”", "그래요, 바로 그때부터 그 끔찍한 계획이 그 여인의 머릿속에서 무르익어 온 겁니다.\""]], [["“Ah, then, indeed, sir,”", "\"아, 그렇다면 정말이지,\""], ["said the sweet girl, bathed in tears, “I see that I am condemned to die!”", "눈물에 젖은 그 사랑스러운 소녀가 말했다. \"저는 죽도록 정해진 거군요!\""]], [["“No, Valentine, for I have foreseen all their plots; no, your enemy is conquered since we know her, and you will live, Valentine—live to be happy yourself, and to confer happiness upon a noble heart; but to insure this you must rely on me.”", "\"아닙니다, 발랑틴. 나는 그들의 모든 음모를 미리 내다보았으니까요. 아닙니다, 우리가 그녀의 정체를 안 이상 당신의 적은 이미 진 것이고, 당신은 살 겁니다, 발랑틴. 살아서 당신 자신이 행복해지고, 또 어느 고귀한 마음에 행복을 안겨 줄 겁니다. 다만 그러려면 나를 믿으셔야 합니다.\""]], [["“Command me, sir—what am I to do?”", "\"분부만 내려 주세요. 제가 무엇을 하면 되나요?\""]], [["“You must blindly take what I give you.”", "\"내가 주는 것을 무조건 받아들이셔야 합니다.\""]], [["“Alas, were it only for my own sake, I should prefer to die!”", "\"아아, 저 하나만 생각한다면 차라리 죽는 편이 낫겠어요!\""]], [["“You must not confide in anyone—not even in your father.”", "\"누구에게도, 당신 아버지에게조차 털어놓아서는 안 됩니다.\""]], [["“My father is not engaged in this fearful plot, is he, sir?”", "\"제 아버지는 이 무서운 음모에 가담하지 않으셨지요, 그렇지요?\""], ["asked Valentine, clasping her hands.", "발랑틴이 두 손을 맞잡으며 물었다."]], [["“No; and yet your father, a man accustomed to judicial accusations, ought to have known that all these deaths have not happened naturally; it is he who should have watched over you—he should have occupied my place—he should have emptied that glass—he should have risen against the assassin.", "\"그렇습니다. 하지만 당신 아버지는 법정의 고발에 익숙한 사람이니, 이 모든 죽음이 자연스럽게 일어난 것이 아님을 알았어야 했습니다."], ["Spectre against spectre!”", "당신을 지켰어야 할 사람은 바로 그입니다."], ["he murmured in a low voice, as he concluded his sentence.", "내 자리에 있었어야 할 사람이고, 저 잔을 비웠어야 할 사람이며, 그 암살자에 맞서 일어섰어야 할 사람입니다. 망령이 망령에 맞서는 격이로군!\" 그는 말을 맺으며 낮은 목소리로 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"백작님,\" 발랑틴이 말했다."], ["said Valentine, “I will do all I can to live, for there are two beings who love me and will die if I die—my grandfather and Maximilian.”", "\"저는 살기 위해 할 수 있는 모든 것을 하겠어요. 저를 사랑하고, 제가 죽으면 따라 죽을 두 사람이 있으니까요. 할아버지와 막시밀리앙이요.\""]], [["“I will watch over them as I have over you.”", "\"그분들도 당신을 지켜 온 것처럼 내가 지키겠습니다.\""]], [["“Well, sir, do as you will with me;”", "\"좋아요, 백작님, 저를 마음대로 하세요.\""], ["and then she added, in a low voice, “oh, heavens, what will befall me?”", "그러고 나서 그녀는 낮은 목소리로 덧붙였다. \"오, 하느님, 제게 무슨 일이 닥치게 될까요?\""]], [["“Whatever may happen, Valentine, do not be alarmed; though you suffer; though you lose sight, hearing, consciousness, fear nothing; though you should awake and be ignorant where you are, still do not fear; even though you should find yourself in a sepulchral vault or coffin.", "\"무슨 일이 일어나든, 발랑틴, 놀라지 마십시오. 고통스럽더라도, 시력과 청력과 의식을 잃더라도 아무것도 두려워하지 마십시오. 깨어나서 자신이 어디 있는지 알 수 없더라도 두려워하지 마십시오."], ["Reassure yourself, then, and say to yourself: ‘At this moment, a friend, a father, who lives for my happiness and that of Maximilian, watches over me!’”", "설령 무덤 속이나 관 속에 있는 자신을 발견하더라도 말입니다. 그러니 마음을 다잡고 이렇게 되뇌십시오. '바로 이 순간, 나와 막시밀리앙의 행복을 위해 사는 한 친구, 한 아버지가 나를 지켜보고 있다!'라고요.\""]], [["“Alas, alas, what a fearful extremity!”", "\"아아, 아아, 이 얼마나 무서운 극단인지!\""]], [["“Valentine, would you rather denounce your stepmother?”", "\"발랑틴, 차라리 계모를 고발하시겠습니까?\""]], [["“I would rather die a hundred times—oh, yes, die!”", "\"차라리 백 번이라도 죽겠어요. 오, 그래요, 죽겠어요!\""]], [["“No, you will not die; but will you promise me, whatever happens, that you will not complain, but hope?”", "\"아닙니다, 당신은 죽지 않습니다. 다만 무슨 일이 일어나든 불평하지 않고 희망을 품겠다고 약속해 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“I will think of Maximilian!”", "\"막시밀리앙을 생각하겠어요!\""]], [["“You are my own darling child, Valentine! I alone can save you, and I will.”", "\"당신은 내 사랑스러운 딸이로군요, 발랑틴! 오직 나만이 당신을 구할 수 있고, 나는 반드시 그렇게 할 겁니다.\""]], [["Valentine in the extremity of her terror joined her hands,—for she felt that the moment had arrived to ask for courage,—and began to pray, and while uttering little more than incoherent words, she forgot that her white shoulders had no other covering than her long hair, and that the pulsations of her heart could be seen through the lace of her nightdress.", "발랑틴은 극도의 공포 속에서 두 손을 모았다. 용기를 청할 순간이 왔음을 느꼈기 때문이다. 그러고는 기도하기 시작했다."], ["Monte Cristo gently laid his hand on the young girl’s arm, drew the velvet coverlet close to her throat, and said with a paternal smile:", "두서없는 말 몇 마디밖에 내뱉지 못하면서, 그녀는 자신의 흰 어깨를 긴 머리카락 말고는 아무것도 가리지 않았다는 것도, 잠옷의 레이스 너머로 심장의 고동이 비쳐 보인다는 것도 잊고 있었다. 몬테크리스토는 소녀의 팔에 가만히 손을 얹고, 벨벳 이불을 목 가까이까지 끌어 올려 주며 아버지 같은 미소를 띠고 말했다."]], [["“My child, believe in my devotion to you as you believe in the goodness of Providence and the love of Maximilian.”", "\"내 딸이여, 섭리의 자비와 막시밀리앙의 사랑을 믿듯이, 당신을 향한 나의 헌신을 믿으십시오.\""], ["Valentine gave him a look full of gratitude, and remained as docile as a child.", "발랑틴은 감사로 가득한 눈길을 그에게 보내며, 아이처럼 고분고분 따랐다."]], [["Then he drew from his waistcoat-pocket the little emerald box, raised the golden lid, and took from it a pastille about the size of a pea, which he placed in her hand.", "그러자 그는 조끼 주머니에서 작은 에메랄드 상자를 꺼내, 금빛 뚜껑을 열고, 완두콩만 한 알약 하나를 꺼내 그녀의 손에 쥐여 주었다."], ["She took it, and looked attentively on the count; there was an expression on the face of her intrepid protector which commanded her veneration.", "그녀는 그것을 받아 들고 백작을 주의 깊게 바라보았다. 그 용감한 보호자의 얼굴에는 그녀의 경외심을 자아내는 표정이 어려 있었다."], ["She evidently interrogated him by her look.", "그녀는 눈빛으로 그에게 묻고 있는 것이 분명했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["Valentine carried the pastille to her mouth, and swallowed it.", "발랑틴은 알약을 입으로 가져가 삼켰다."]], [["“And now, my dear child, adieu for the present.", "\"자, 이제, 사랑하는 딸이여, 당분간은 작별입니다."], ["I will try and gain a little sleep, for you are saved.”", "나도 잠을 좀 청해 보겠습니다. 당신은 이제 구원받았으니까요.\""]], [["“Go,”", "\"가 보세요.\""], ["said Valentine, “whatever happens, I promise you not to fear.”", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"무슨 일이 일어나든, 두려워하지 않겠다고 약속드려요.\""]], [["Monte Cristo for some time kept his eyes fixed on the young girl, who gradually fell asleep, yielding to the effects of the narcotic the count had given her.", "몬테크리스토는 한동안 소녀에게서 눈을 떼지 않았다. 그녀는 백작이 준 마취제의 효력에 못 이겨 차츰 잠이 들었다."], ["Then he took the glass, emptied three parts of the contents in the fireplace, that it might be supposed Valentine had taken it, and replaced it on the table; then he disappeared, after throwing a farewell glance on Valentine, who slept with the confidence and innocence of an angel at the feet of the Lord.", "이윽고 그는 잔을 들어, 발랑틴이 마신 것처럼 보이도록 그 내용물의 4분의 3을 벽난로에 비운 뒤 탁자에 도로 놓았다. 그러고는 발랑틴에게 작별의 눈길을 던지고 사라졌다. 그녀는 주님의 발치에 잠든 천사 같은 신뢰와 순결로 잠들어 있었다."]], [["# Chapter 102. Valentine", "# Chapter 102. Valentine"]], [["The night-light continued to burn on the chimney-piece, exhausting the last drops of oil which floated on the surface of the water.", "벽난로 위에서는 등잔불이 여전히 타오르며, 물 위에 떠 있던 기름의 마지막 방울까지 태워 가고 있었다."], ["The globe of the lamp appeared of a reddish hue, and the flame, brightening before it expired, threw out the last flickerings which in an inanimate object have been so often compared with the convulsions of a human creature in its final agonies.", "등잔의 유리갓은 불그스름한 빛을 띠었고, 불꽃은 꺼지기 직전에 다시 밝아지며 마지막 깜박임을 토해 냈다. 생명 없는 사물의 그 떨림은 죽어 가는 사람의 마지막 경련에 자주 비유되곤 하던 것이었다."], ["A dull and dismal light was shed over the bedclothes and curtains surrounding the young girl.", "처녀를 둘러싼 이부자리와 휘장 위로 흐릿하고 음산한 빛이 번졌다."], ["All noise in the streets had ceased, and the silence was frightful.", "거리의 모든 소음은 그쳤고, 그 정적은 끔찍했다."]], [["It was then that the door of Edward’s room opened, and a head we have before noticed appeared in the glass opposite; it was Madame de Villefort, who came to witness the effects of the drink she had prepared.", "바로 그때 에두아르의 방문이 열리며, 앞서 보았던 한 사람의 머리가 맞은편 거울에 나타났다. 빌포르 부인이었다. 자신이 준비해 둔 약물의 효력을 확인하러 온 것이다."], ["She stopped in the doorway, listened for a moment to the flickering of the lamp, the only sound in that deserted room, and then advanced to the table to see if Valentine’s glass were empty.", "그녀는 문턱에서 걸음을 멈추고, 그 적막한 방 안의 유일한 소리인 등잔불의 떨림에 잠시 귀를 기울였다. 그러고는 발랑틴의 잔이 비었는지 보려고 탁자로 다가갔다."], ["It was still about a quarter full, as we before stated.", "앞서 말했듯이 잔은 여전히 사분의 일가량 차 있었다."], ["Madame de Villefort emptied the contents into the ashes, which she disturbed that they might the more readily absorb the liquid; then she carefully rinsed the glass, and wiping it with her handkerchief replaced it on the table.", "빌포르 부인은 그 내용물을 잿더미에 쏟았고, 액체가 더 잘 스며들도록 재를 휘저었다. 그런 다음 잔을 정성껏 헹구고 손수건으로 닦아 다시 탁자 위에 놓았다."]], [["If anyone could have looked into the room just then he would have noticed the hesitation with which Madame de Villefort approached the bed and looked fixedly on Valentine.", "만약 그 순간 누군가 그 방을 들여다볼 수 있었다면, 빌포르 부인이 침대로 다가가 발랑틴을 뚫어지게 바라보던 그 머뭇거림을 알아차렸을 것이다."], ["The dim light, the profound silence, and the gloomy thoughts inspired by the hour, and still more by her own conscience, all combined to produce a sensation of fear; the poisoner was terrified at the contemplation of her own work.", "희미한 불빛, 깊은 정적, 그 시각이 불러일으키는 음울한 상념, 그리고 무엇보다 그녀 자신의 양심이 한데 어우러져 두려움의 감각을 자아냈다. 독을 쓴 여자는 제 손으로 한 일을 바라보며 공포에 사로잡혔다."]], [["At length she rallied, drew aside the curtain, and leaning over the pillow gazed intently on Valentine.", "마침내 그녀는 마음을 다잡고 휘장을 젖힌 뒤 베개 위로 몸을 기울여 발랑틴을 뚫어지게 응시했다. 처녀는 더 이상 숨을 쉬지 않았다."], ["The young girl no longer breathed, no breath issued through the half-closed teeth; the white lips no longer quivered—the eyes were suffused with a bluish vapor, and the long black lashes rested on a cheek white as wax.", "반쯤 다문 잇새로 어떤 숨결도 새어 나오지 않았다. 하얀 입술은 더 이상 떨리지 않았다. 두 눈은 푸르스름한 안개로 뒤덮였고, 긴 검은 속눈썹은 밀랍처럼 창백한 뺨 위에 드리워 있었다."], ["Madame de Villefort gazed upon the face so expressive even in its stillness; then she ventured to raise the coverlet and press her hand upon the young girl’s heart.", "빌포르 부인은 그토록 고요한 가운데서도 풍부한 표정을 머금은 그 얼굴을 응시했다. 그러고는 용기를 내어 이불을 들추고 처녀의 심장 위에 손을 얹었다."], ["It was cold and motionless.", "차갑고 미동도 없었다."], ["She only felt the pulsation in her own fingers, and withdrew her hand with a shudder.", "그녀는 오직 제 손가락의 맥박만을 느끼고는 몸서리치며 손을 거두었다."], ["One arm was hanging out of the bed; from shoulder to elbow it was moulded after the arms of Germain Pillon’s “Graces,”23 but the fore-arm seemed to be slightly distorted by convulsion, and the hand, so delicately formed, was resting with stiff outstretched fingers on the framework of the bed.", "한쪽 팔이 침대 밖으로 늘어져 있었다. 어깨에서 팔꿈치까지는 제르맹 필롱의 「삼미신」의 팔처럼 빚어진 듯했으나, 아래팔은 경련 탓에 약간 뒤틀린 듯했고, 그토록 곱게 빚어진 손은 뻣뻣하게 펼쳐진 손가락으로 침대 틀에 닿아 있었다."], ["The nails, too, were turning blue.", "손톱마저 푸르게 변해 가고 있었다."]], [["Madame de Villefort had no longer any doubt; all was over—she had consummated the last terrible work she had to accomplish. There was no more to do in the room, so the poisoner retired stealthily, as though fearing to hear the sound of her own footsteps; but as she withdrew she still held aside the curtain, absorbed in the irresistible attraction always exerted by the picture of death, so long as it is merely mysterious and does not excite disgust.", "* 「삼미신」: 16세기 프랑스 조각가 제르맹 필롱이 만든 조각상. —옮긴이\n빌포르 부인은 더 이상 의심하지 않았다. 모든 것이 끝났다. 그녀는 자신이 해내야 할 마지막 끔찍한 일을 완수한 것이다. 이 방에서 더 할 일은 없었으므로, 독을 쓴 여자는 제 발소리마저 들릴까 두려운 듯 살그머니 물러났다. 그러나 물러나면서도 그녀는 여전히 휘장을 젖힌 채로 있었다. 그저 신비롭기만 할 뿐 아직 혐오를 불러일으키지 않는 한, 죽음의 그림이 늘 발휘하는 그 뿌리칠 수 없는 매혹에 사로잡혀 있었던 것이다."]], [["The minutes passed; Madame de Villefort could not drop the curtain which she held like a funeral pall over the head of Valentine.", "시간이 흘렀다. 빌포르 부인은 발랑틴의 머리맡에 장례용 천처럼 드리운 그 휘장을 차마 놓지 못했다."], ["She was lost in reverie, and the reverie of crime is remorse.", "그녀는 망상에 잠겼고, 죄의 망상이란 곧 회한이었다."]], [["Just then the lamp again flickered; the noise startled Madame de Villefort, who shuddered and dropped the curtain.", "바로 그때 등잔이 다시 깜박였다. 그 소리에 빌포르 부인은 흠칫 놀라 몸서리치며 휘장을 떨어뜨렸다."], ["Immediately afterwards the light expired, and the room was plunged in frightful obscurity, while the clock at that minute struck half-past four.", "곧이어 불빛이 꺼졌고, 방은 끔찍한 어둠 속에 잠겼다. 그 순간 시계가 네 시 반을 알렸다."]], [["Overpowered with agitation, the poisoner succeeded in groping her way to the door, and reached her room in an agony of fear.", "극도의 동요에 짓눌린 채, 독을 쓴 여자는 가까스로 더듬더듬 문에 다다랐고, 두려움의 고통 속에서 제 방에 이르렀다."], ["The darkness lasted two hours longer; then by degrees a cold light crept through the Venetian blinds, until at length it revealed the objects in the room.", "어둠은 두 시간 더 이어졌다. 그러다 차츰 차가운 빛이 베네치아식 덧창 사이로 스며들었고, 마침내 방 안의 물건들을 드러냈다."]], [["About this time the nurse’s cough was heard on the stairs and the woman entered the room with a cup in her hand.", "그즈음 계단에서 간병인의 기침 소리가 들리더니, 그 여자가 잔을 손에 들고 방으로 들어왔다."], ["To the tender eye of a father or a lover, the first glance would have sufficed to reveal Valentine’s condition; but to this hireling, Valentine only appeared to sleep.", "아버지나 연인의 다정한 눈에라면 한눈에 발랑틴의 상태가 드러났을 테지만, 이 고용된 여자에게는 발랑틴이 그저 잠든 것으로만 보였다."]], [["“Good,”", "\"좋아.\""], ["she exclaimed, approaching the table, “she has taken part of her draught; the glass is three-quarters empty.”", "간병인이 탁자로 다가가며 소리쳤다. \"물약을 좀 드셨군. 잔이 사분의 삼이나 비었어.\""]], [["Then she went to the fireplace and lit the fire, and although she had just left her bed, she could not resist the temptation offered by Valentine’s sleep, so she threw herself into an armchair to snatch a little more rest.", "그러고는 벽난로로 가서 불을 지폈다. 방금 잠자리에서 일어났음에도 그녀는 발랑틴의 잠이 주는 유혹을 떨치지 못하고, 안락의자에 몸을 던져 조금 더 쉬려 했다."], ["The clock striking eight awoke her. Astonished at the prolonged slumber of the patient, and frightened to see that the arm was still hanging out of the bed, she advanced towards Valentine, and for the first time noticed the white lips.", "시계가 여덟 시를 치자 그녀는 잠에서 깼다. 환자의 잠이 너무 길어진 데 놀라고, 팔이 여전히 침대 밖으로 늘어져 있는 것을 보고 겁이 나서, 그녀는 발랑틴에게 다가갔고 그제야 처음으로 하얀 입술을 알아차렸다."], ["She tried to replace the arm, but it moved with a frightful rigidity which could not deceive a sick-nurse.", "팔을 제자리에 놓으려 했으나, 팔은 간병인을 속일 수 없는 끔찍한 뻣뻣함으로 움직였다."], ["She screamed aloud; then running to the door exclaimed:", "그녀는 비명을 질렀다. 그러고는 문으로 달려가 외쳤다."]], [["“Help, help!” 50083m", "\"사람 살려, 사람 살려요!\""]], [["“What is the matter?”", "\"무슨 일인가?\""], ["asked M. d’Avrigny, at the foot of the stairs, it being the hour he usually visited her.", "다브리니 씨가 계단 아래에서 물었다. 그가 늘 환자를 찾아오던 시각이었다."]], [["“What is it?”", "\"무슨 일인가?\""], ["asked Villefort, rushing from his room.", "빌포르가 제 방에서 뛰쳐나오며 물었다."], ["“Doctor, do you hear them call for help?”", "\"선생님, 사람 살리라고 외치는 소리가 들립니까?\""]], [["“Yes, yes; let us hasten up; it was in Valentine’s room.”", "\"네, 네, 어서 올라갑시다. 발랑틴 양의 방에서 나는 소리였습니다.\""]], [["But before the doctor and the father could reach the room, the servants who were on the same floor had entered, and seeing Valentine pale and motionless on her bed, they lifted up their hands towards heaven and stood transfixed, as though struck by lightening.", "그러나 의사와 아버지가 방에 이르기도 전에, 같은 층에 있던 하인들이 먼저 들어가 있었다. 그들은 침대 위에 창백하게 미동도 없이 누운 발랑틴을 보고, 두 손을 하늘로 치켜든 채 벼락이라도 맞은 듯 그 자리에 못 박혀 서 있었다."]], [["“Call Madame de Villefort!—Wake Madame de Villefort!”", "\"빌포르 부인을 불러라! 빌포르 부인을 깨워라!\""], ["cried the procureur from the door of his chamber, which apparently he scarcely dared to leave.", "검사가 침실 문간에서 외쳤다. 그는 차마 그 문턱을 넘지 못하는 듯했다."], ["But instead of obeying him, the servants stood watching M. d’Avrigny, who ran to Valentine, and raised her in his arms.", "그러나 하인들은 그의 명령을 따르는 대신, 발랑틴에게 달려가 그녀를 두 팔로 안아 일으키는 다브리니 씨를 지켜보고 있었다."]], [["“What?—this one, too?”", "\"뭐라고? 이 아이마저?\""], ["he exclaimed.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["“Oh, where will be the end?”", "\"오, 대체 이 끝은 어디인가?\""]], [["Villefort rushed into the room.", "빌포르가 방 안으로 뛰어들었다."]], [["“What are you saying, doctor?”", "\"무슨 말씀이십니까, 선생님?\""], ["he exclaimed, raising his hands to heaven.", "그가 두 손을 하늘로 치켜들며 외쳤다."]], [["“I say that Valentine is dead!”", "\"발랑틴 양이 죽었다는 말입니다!\""], ["replied d’Avrigny, in a voice terrible in its solemn calmness.", "다브리니가 대답했다. 그 목소리는 엄숙한 평정 속에서 더없이 끔찍했다."]], [["50085m\nM. de Villefort staggered and buried his head in the bed.", "빌포르 씨는 비틀거리며 침대에 머리를 파묻었다."], ["On the exclamation of the doctor and the cry of the father, the servants all fled with muttered imprecations; they were heard running down the stairs and through the long passages, then there was a rush in the court, afterwards all was still; they had, one and all, deserted the accursed house.", "의사의 그 말과 아버지의 그 외침에, 하인들은 저주의 말을 중얼거리며 모두 달아났다. 그들이 계단을 뛰어 내려가 긴 복도를 지나는 소리가 들렸고, 이어 안뜰에서 한바탕 부산스러운 소리가 나더니, 그 뒤로는 모든 것이 잠잠해졌다. 그들은 하나도 빠짐없이 저주받은 그 집을 버리고 떠난 것이다."]], [["Just then, Madame de Villefort, in the act of slipping on her dressing-gown, threw aside the drapery and for a moment stood motionless, as though interrogating the occupants of the room, while she endeavored to call up some rebellious tears.", "바로 그때, 실내복을 막 걸치던 빌포르 부인이 휘장을 젖히고 잠시 미동도 없이 서 있었다. 마치 방 안에 있는 이들에게 무엇이냐고 묻는 듯한 모습으로, 그녀는 좀처럼 나오지 않는 눈물을 짜내려 애썼다."], ["On a sudden she stepped, or rather bounded, with outstretched arms, towards the table.", "별안간 그녀는 두 팔을 뻗은 채 탁자 쪽으로 걸음을, 아니 거의 뛰듯이 다가갔다."], ["She saw d’Avrigny curiously examining the glass, which she felt certain of having emptied during the night.", "다브리니가 그 잔을 호기심 어린 눈으로 살피고 있는 것이 보였다. 그녀가 밤사이에 분명히 비웠다고 확신하던 그 잔이었다."], ["It was now a third full, just as it was when she threw the contents into the ashes.", "그런데 잔은 그녀가 내용물을 잿더미에 쏟았을 때처럼 삼분의 일이 차 있었다."], ["The spectre of Valentine rising before the poisoner would have alarmed her less.", "독을 쓴 여자 앞에 발랑틴의 망령이 일어섰더라도 이만큼 그녀를 놀라게 하지는 못했을 것이다."], ["It was, indeed, the same color as the draught she had poured into the glass, and which Valentine had drunk; it was indeed the poison, which could not deceive M.", "그것은 분명, 그녀가 잔에 따랐고 발랑틴이 마셨던 그 물약과 똑같은 빛깔이었다. 그것은 분명 그 독이었고, 다브리니 씨를 속일 수 없는 그 독을, 이제 그가 그토록 면밀히 살피고 있었다."], ["d’Avrigny, which he now examined so closely; it was doubtless a miracle from heaven, that, notwithstanding her precautions, there should be some trace, some proof remaining to reveal the crime.", "그녀가 그토록 조심했음에도 죄를 드러낼 어떤 흔적, 어떤 증거가 남아 있다는 것은 의심할 바 없이 하늘이 내린 기적이었다."]], [["While Madame de Villefort remained rooted to the spot like a statue of terror, and Villefort, with his head hidden in the bedclothes, saw nothing around him, d’Avrigny approached the window, that he might the better examine the contents of the glass, and dipping the tip of his finger in, tasted it.", "빌포르 부인이 공포의 조상처럼 그 자리에 못 박혀 있고, 빌포르가 이불에 머리를 파묻은 채 주위의 아무것도 보지 못하는 사이, 다브리니는 잔의 내용물을 더 잘 살피려 창가로 다가갔다. 그는 손끝을 적셔 맛을 보았다."]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아.\""], ["he exclaimed, “it is no longer brucine that is used; let me see what it is!”", "그가 외쳤다. \"이제는 브루신을 쓰지 않는군. 무엇인지 어디 보자!\""]], [["Then he ran to one of the cupboards in Valentine’s room, which had been transformed into a medicine closet, and taking from its silver case a small bottle of nitric acid, dropped a little of it into the liquor, which immediately changed to a blood-red color.", "그러고는 발랑틴의 방에서 약장으로 개조된 찬장 하나로 달려가, 은제 갑에서 작은 질산 병을 꺼내 그 약물에 조금 떨어뜨렸다. 그러자 약물은 곧바로 핏빛으로 변했다."]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아.\""], ["exclaimed d’Avrigny, in a voice in which the horror of a judge unveiling the truth was mingled with the delight of a student making a discovery.", "다브리니가 외쳤다. 그 목소리에는 진실을 드러내는 판관의 전율과 발견을 이룬 학자의 환희가 뒤섞여 있었다."]], [["Madame de Villefort was overpowered; her eyes first flashed and then swam, she staggered towards the door and disappeared.", "빌포르 부인은 압도되었다. 그녀의 두 눈이 먼저 번득이더니 이내 흐려졌고, 그녀는 비틀거리며 문 쪽으로 가다 사라졌다."], ["Directly afterwards the distant sound of a heavy weight falling on the ground was heard, but no one paid any attention to it; the nurse was engaged in watching the chemical analysis, and Villefort was still absorbed in grief. M.", "곧이어 무거운 물체가 바닥에 떨어지는 둔탁한 소리가 멀리서 들렸으나, 아무도 거기에 주의를 기울이지 않았다. 간병인은 화학 분석을 지켜보는 데 정신이 팔려 있었고, 빌포르는 여전히 슬픔에 잠겨 있었다."], ["d’Avrigny alone had followed Madame de Villefort with his eyes, and watched her hurried retreat.", "오직 다브리니 씨만이 빌포르 부인을 눈으로 좇으며 그녀의 황급한 퇴장을 지켜보았다."], ["He lifted up the drapery over the entrance to Edward’s room, and his eye reaching as far as Madame de Villefort’s apartment, he beheld her extended lifeless on the floor.", "그는 에두아르의 방으로 통하는 휘장을 들추었고, 그의 시선이 빌포르 부인의 방까지 닿자, 그녀가 바닥에 죽은 듯이 뻗어 있는 것이 보였다."]], [["“Go to the assistance of Madame de Villefort,” he said to the nurse.", "\"빌포르 부인을 도우러 가게.\" 그가 간병인에게 말했다."], ["“Madame de Villefort is ill.” 50087m", "\"빌포르 부인이 쓰러지셨네.\""]], [["“But Mademoiselle de Villefort——”", "\"하지만 빌포르 아가씨가……\""], ["stammered the nurse.", "간병인이 더듬거렸다."]], [["“Mademoiselle de Villefort no longer requires help,”", "\"빌포르 양에게는 더 이상 도움이 필요 없네.\""], ["said d’Avrigny, “since she is dead.”", "다브리니가 말했다. \"이미 죽었으니까.\""]], [["“Dead,—dead!”", "\"죽었어, 죽었어!\""], ["groaned forth Villefort, in a paroxysm of grief, which was the more terrible from the novelty of the sensation in the iron heart of that man.", "빌포르가 슬픔의 발작 속에서 신음하듯 토해 냈다. 그 강철 같은 사내의 가슴에는 낯선 감각이었기에 그 슬픔은 더욱 끔찍했다."]], [["“Dead!”", "\"죽었다고!\""], ["repeated a third voice.", "세 번째 목소리가 되풀이했다."], ["“Who said Valentine was dead?”", "\"누가 발랑틴이 죽었다고 했소?\""]], [["The two men turned round, and saw Morrel standing at the door, pale and terror-stricken.", "두 사내가 돌아보니, 모렐이 창백하게 공포에 질린 얼굴로 문가에 서 있었다."], ["This is what had happened.", "일은 이러했다."], ["At the usual time, Morrel had presented himself at the little door leading to Noirtier’s room.", "여느 때와 같은 시각에 모렐은 누아르티에의 방으로 통하는 작은 문 앞에 나타났다."], ["Contrary to custom, the door was open, and having no occasion to ring he entered.", "늘 그렇던 것과 달리 문은 열려 있었고, 종을 울릴 필요가 없어 그는 그대로 들어갔다."], ["He waited for a moment in the hall and called for a servant to conduct him to M. Noirtier; but no one answered, the servants having, as we know, deserted the house.", "그는 현관에서 잠시 기다리며 누아르티에 씨에게 안내해 줄 하인을 불렀으나, 아무도 대답하지 않았다. 우리가 알다시피 하인들이 집을 버리고 떠났기 때문이었다."], ["Morrel had no particular reason for uneasiness; Monte Cristo had promised him that Valentine should live, and so far he had always fulfilled his word.", "모렐에게는 딱히 불안해할 이유가 없었다. 몬테크리스토가 발랑틴이 살아날 것이라 약속했고, 지금껏 그는 늘 약속을 지켜 왔다."], ["Every night the count had given him news, which was the next morning confirmed by Noirtier.", "매일 밤 백작은 그에게 소식을 전해 주었고, 그 소식은 이튿날 아침 누아르티에에 의해 확인되곤 했다."], ["Still this extraordinary silence appeared strange to him, and he called a second and third time; still no answer.", "그래도 이 유별난 정적은 그에게 이상하게 여겨졌고, 그는 두 번, 세 번 더 불렀다. 그래도 대답이 없었다."], ["Then he determined to go up. Noirtier’s room was opened, like all the rest.", "그러자 그는 위층으로 올라가기로 마음먹었다. 누아르티에의 방은 다른 방들처럼 열려 있었다."], ["The first thing he saw was the old man sitting in his armchair in his usual place, but his eyes expressed alarm, which was confirmed by the pallor which overspread his features.", "그가 가장 먼저 본 것은 늘 앉던 자리의 안락의자에 앉아 있는 노인이었다. 그러나 노인의 두 눈은 경악을 드러내고 있었고, 얼굴에 번진 창백함이 그것을 뒷받침하고 있었다."]], [["“How are you, sir?”", "\"안녕하십니까, 어르신?\""], ["asked Morrel, with a sickness of heart.", "모렐이 가슴이 메는 듯한 심정으로 물었다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"잘 있네.\""], ["answered the old man, by closing his eyes; but his appearance manifested increasing uneasiness.", "노인이 두 눈을 감아 대답했다. 그러나 그 모습은 점점 커져 가는 불안을 드러내고 있었다."]], [["“You are thoughtful, sir,”", "\"무슨 근심이 있으십니까, 어르신?\""], ["continued Morrel; “you want something; shall I call one of the servants?”", "모렐이 말을 이었다. \"무엇이 필요하신가요? 하인을 부를까요?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["replied Noirtier.", "누아르티에가 대답했다."]], [["Morrel pulled the bell, but though he nearly broke the cord no one answered.", "모렐이 종 줄을 잡아당겼으나, 줄이 끊어질 만큼 당겨도 아무도 대답하지 않았다."], ["He turned towards Noirtier; the pallor and anguish expressed on his countenance momentarily increased.", "그는 누아르티에를 돌아보았다. 노인의 얼굴에 드러난 창백함과 고뇌가 시시각각 더해 갔다."]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오.\""], ["exclaimed Morrel, “why do they not come?", "모렐이 외쳤다. \"왜 아무도 오지 않는 겁니까?"], ["Is anyone ill in the house?”", "집안에 누가 아픕니까?\""], ["The eyes of Noirtier seemed as though they would start from their sockets.", "누아르티에의 두 눈은 눈구멍에서 튀어나올 듯했다. \"무슨 일입니까?"], ["“What is the matter? You alarm me. Valentine?", "어르신, 저를 두렵게 하시는군요. 발랑틴?"], ["Valentine?”", "발랑틴인가요?\""]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"그래, 그래.\""], ["signed Noirtier.", "누아르티에가 신호를 보냈다."]], [["Maximilian tried to speak, but he could articulate nothing; he staggered, and supported himself against the wainscot.", "막시밀리앙은 말을 하려 했으나 아무 말도 입에서 나오지 않았다. 그는 비틀거리며 벽판에 몸을 기댔다."], ["Then he pointed to the door.", "그러고는 문을 가리켰다."]], [["“Yes, yes, yes!”", "\"그래, 그래, 그래!\""], ["continued the old man. 50089m", "노인이 계속 신호를 보냈다."]], [["Maximilian rushed up the little staircase, while Noirtier’s eyes seemed to say,—“Quicker, quicker!”", "막시밀리앙이 작은 계단을 뛰어 올라가는 동안, 누아르티에의 두 눈은 \"더 빨리, 더 빨리!\" 하고 외치는 듯했다."]], [["In a minute the young man darted through several rooms, till at length he reached Valentine’s.", "순식간에 젊은이는 여러 방을 가로질러 마침내 발랑틴의 방에 이르렀다."]], [["There was no occasion to push the door, it was wide open.", "문을 밀 필요도 없었다. 활짝 열려 있었다."], ["A sob was the only sound he heard.", "그가 들은 소리라고는 흐느낌뿐이었다."], ["He saw as though in a mist, a black figure kneeling and buried in a confused mass of white drapery.", "그는 안개 속을 들여다보듯, 검은 형체 하나가 무릎을 꿇은 채 어지러이 흐트러진 하얀 휘장 더미에 파묻혀 있는 것을 보았다."], ["A terrible fear transfixed him.", "끔찍한 두려움이 그를 못 박았다."], ["It was then he heard a voice exclaim “Valentine is dead!” and another voice which, like an echo repeated:", "바로 그때 그는 한 목소리가 \"발랑틴이 죽었다!\"고 외치는 것을, 그리고 또 다른 목소리가 메아리처럼 되풀이하는 것을 들었다."]], [["“Dead,—dead!” 50090m", "\"죽었어, 죽었어!\""]], [["# Chapter 103. Maximilian", "# Chapter 103. Maximilian"]], [["Villefort rose, half-ashamed of being surprised in such a paroxysm of grief.", "빌포르가 일어섰다. 그토록 격렬한 비탄에 빠진 모습을 들킨 것이 절반쯤 부끄러웠다."], ["The terrible office he had held for twenty-five years had succeeded in making him more or less than man.", "이십오 년 동안 맡아 온 그 끔찍한 직무가 그를 인간 이상의, 또는 인간 이하의 존재로 만들어 놓았던 것이다."], ["His glance, at first wandering, fixed itself upon Morrel.", "처음엔 방황하던 그의 시선이 모렐에게 멎었다. \"당신은 누구요?\""], ["“Who are you, sir,”", "그가 물었다."], ["he asked, “that forget that this is not the manner to enter a house stricken with death? Go, sir, go!”", "\"이렇게 죽음이 덮친 집에 함부로 들어오는 법이 어디 있소? 나가시오, 어서 나가!\""]], [["But Morrel remained motionless; he could not detach his eyes from that disordered bed, and the pale corpse of the young girl who was lying on it.", "그러나 모렐은 꼼짝도 하지 않았다. 헝클어진 침대와 그 위에 누운 처녀의 창백한 주검에서 눈을 뗄 수가 없었다."]], [["“Go!—do you hear?”", "\"나가시오! 안 들리시오?\""], ["said Villefort, while d’Avrigny advanced to lead Morrel out.", "빌포르가 말했고, 다브리니가 모렐을 데리고 나가려고 다가갔다. 막시밀리앙은 잠시 주검을 응시하더니, 방 안을 빙 둘러보고, 다시 두 남자를 바라보았다. 무언가 말하려 입을 열었지만, 머릿속을 가득 메운 수많은 생각을 도무지 입 밖으로 낼 수 없었다."], ["Maximilian stared for a moment at the corpse, gazed all around the room, then upon the two men; he opened his mouth to speak, but finding it impossible to give utterance to the innumerable ideas that occupied his brain, he went out, thrusting his hands through his hair in such a manner that Villefort and d’Avrigny, for a moment diverted from the engrossing topic, exchanged glances, which seemed to say,—“He is mad!”", "그는 두 손으로 머리카락을 쥐어뜯듯 움켜잡고 밖으로 나갔다. 그 모습에 빌포르와 다브리니는 잠시 골몰하던 화제에서 벗어나 서로 눈길을 주고받았는데, 그 눈빛은 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"저 사람, 미쳤군!\""]], [["But in less than five minutes the staircase groaned beneath an extraordinary weight.", "그러나 채 오 분도 지나지 않아 계단이 엄청난 무게에 짓눌려 신음했다."], ["Morrel was seen carrying, with superhuman strength, the armchair containing Noirtier upstairs.", "모렐이 누아르티에가 앉은 안락의자를 초인적인 힘으로 들고 위층으로 올라오고 있었다."], ["When he reached the landing he placed the armchair on the floor and rapidly rolled it into Valentine’s room.", "층계참에 다다르자 그는 안락의자를 바닥에 내려놓고는 재빨리 발랑틴의 방으로 밀고 들어갔다."], ["This could only have been accomplished by means of unnatural strength supplied by powerful excitement.", "이것은 격렬한 흥분이 가져다준 비정상적인 힘이 아니고서는 해낼 수 없는 일이었다."], ["But the most fearful spectacle was Noirtier being pushed towards the bed, his face expressing all his meaning, and his eyes supplying the want of every other faculty.", "그러나 가장 무서운 광경은 침대 쪽으로 밀려가는 누아르티에였다. 그의 얼굴은 마음속 모든 것을 그대로 드러냈고, 그의 두 눈은 다른 모든 기능을 대신하고 있었다."], ["That pale face and flaming glance appeared to Villefort like a frightful apparition.", "그 창백한 얼굴과 불타는 눈빛이 빌포르에게는 끔찍한 유령처럼 보였다."], ["Each time he had been brought into contact with his father, something terrible had happened.", "그가 아버지와 마주칠 때마다 무언가 무서운 일이 벌어지곤 했던 것이다."]], [["“See what they have done!”", "\"저들이 무슨 짓을 했는지 보십시오!\""], ["cried Morrel, with one hand leaning on the back of the chair, and the other extended towards Valentine. “See, my father, see!”", "모렐이 한 손으로는 의자 등받이를 짚고, 다른 손으로는 발랑틴을 가리키며 외쳤다. \"보세요, 아버님, 보시라고요!\""]], [["Villefort drew back and looked with astonishment on the young man, who, almost a stranger to him, called Noirtier his father.", "빌포르는 뒷걸음치며, 자신에게는 거의 낯선 사람이나 다름없는 이 젊은이가 누아르티에를 아버지라고 부르는 모습을 경악스럽게 바라보았다."], ["At this moment the whole soul of the old man seemed centred in his eyes which became bloodshot; the veins of the throat swelled; his cheeks and temples became purple, as though he was struck with epilepsy; nothing was wanting to complete this but the utterance of a cry.", "그 순간 노인의 온 영혼이 두 눈에 모인 듯했고, 그 눈은 핏발이 섰다. 목의 핏줄이 부풀어 올랐고, 뺨과 관자놀이는 간질 발작이라도 일으킨 듯 검붉게 물들었다. 이 모든 것을 완성하는 데 모자란 것은 오직 비명 하나뿐이었다."], ["And the cry issued from his pores, if we may thus speak—a cry frightful in its silence.", "그리고 그 비명은, 이렇게 말해도 된다면, 그의 온몸의 땀구멍에서 터져 나왔다."], ["D’Avrigny rushed towards the old man and made him inhale a powerful restorative.", "침묵 속에서 터지는, 그래서 더욱 끔찍한 비명이었다. 다브리니가 노인에게 달려가 강한 회복제를 들이마시게 했다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"선생님,\""], ["cried Morrel, seizing the moist hand of the paralytic, “they ask me who I am, and what right I have to be here.", "모렐이 마비된 노인의 축축한 손을 붙잡으며 외쳤다. \"이 사람들이 제게 누구냐고, 무슨 자격으로 여기 있느냐고 묻습니다. 오, 아버님은 아시지요."], ["Oh, you know it, tell them, tell them!”", "말씀해 주십시오, 말씀해 주세요!\""], ["And the young man’s voice was choked by sobs.", "젊은이의 목소리는 흐느낌에 막혔다."]], [["As for the old man, his chest heaved with his panting respiration.", "노인은 가쁜 숨을 몰아쉬며 가슴을 들썩였다."], ["One could have thought that he was undergoing the agonies preceding death.", "죽음을 앞둔 단말마의 고통을 겪고 있는 것이라 여겨질 정도였다."], ["At length, happier than the young man, who sobbed without weeping, tears glistened in the eyes of Noirtier.", "마침내, 흐느끼면서도 눈물은 흘리지 못하던 젊은이보다는 다행스럽게도, 누아르티에의 두 눈에 눈물이 반짝였다."]], [["“Tell them,”", "\"말씀해 주세요.\""], ["said Morrel in a hoarse voice, “tell them that I am her betrothed.", "모렐이 쉰 목소리로 말했다. \"제가 그녀의 약혼자라고 말씀해 주세요."], ["Tell them she was my beloved, my noble girl, my only blessing in the world.", "그녀가 제 사랑이었고, 제 고귀한 여인이었으며, 이 세상에서 제 유일한 축복이었다고 말씀해 주세요."], ["Tell them—oh, tell them, that corpse belongs to me!”", "말씀해 주세요. 오, 말씀해 주세요, 저 주검은 제 것이라고요!\""]], [["The young man overwhelmed by the weight of his anguish, fell heavily on his knees before the bed, which his fingers grasped with convulsive energy.", "비통함의 무게에 짓눌린 젊은이는 침대 앞에 털썩 무릎을 꿇었고, 손가락으로 발작하듯 침대를 움켜쥐었다."], ["D’Avrigny, unable to bear the sight of this touching emotion, turned away; and Villefort, without seeking any further explanation, and attracted towards him by the irresistible magnetism which draws us towards those who have loved the people for whom we mourn, extended his hand towards the young man.", "다브리니는 이 가슴 저미는 감정을 차마 더 지켜볼 수 없어 고개를 돌렸다. 빌포르는 더 이상 아무 설명도 구하지 않고, 우리가 애도하는 이들을 사랑했던 사람에게로 끌려가게 만드는 그 거역할 수 없는 끌림에 이끌려 젊은이에게 손을 내밀었다."]], [["But Morrel saw nothing; he had grasped the hand of Valentine, and unable to weep vented his agony in groans as he bit the sheets.", "그러나 모렐은 아무것도 보지 못했다. 그는 발랑틴의 손을 움켜쥔 채, 눈물도 흘리지 못하고 침대보를 깨물며 신음으로 고통을 토해 냈다."], ["For some time nothing was heard in that chamber but sobs, exclamations, and prayers.", "한동안 그 방 안에는 흐느낌과 절규와 기도 소리밖에 들리지 않았다."], ["At length Villefort, the most composed of all, spoke:", "마침내 그중 가장 차분한 빌포르가 입을 열었다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"선생,\""], ["said he to Maximilian, “you say you loved Valentine, that you were betrothed to her.", "그가 막시밀리앙에게 말했다. \"당신은 발랑틴을 사랑했고, 그 아이와 약혼했다고 했소."], ["I knew nothing of this engagement, of this love, yet I, her father, forgive you, for I see that your grief is real and deep; and besides my own sorrow is too great for anger to find a place in my heart.", "나는 그런 약혼도, 그런 사랑도 전혀 몰랐소. 하지만 그 애의 아비인 나는 당신을 용서하겠소. 당신의 슬픔이 진실하고 깊다는 것을 알기 때문이오."], ["But you see that the angel whom you hoped for has left this earth—she has nothing more to do with the adoration of men.", "게다가 내 자신의 슬픔이 너무 커서 분노가 들어설 자리가 마음에 없소. 하지만 보다시피, 당신이 바라던 그 천사는 이 땅을 떠났소."], ["Take a last farewell, sir, of her sad remains; take the hand you expected to possess once more within your own, and then separate yourself from her forever.", "이제 그 애는 인간들의 흠모와는 아무 상관이 없소. 선생, 그 애의 서글픈 유해에 마지막 작별을 고하시오. 당신 것으로 만들기를 바라던 그 손을 다시 한번 당신 손안에 쥐어 보고, 그런 다음 영원히 그 애에게서 떠나시오."], ["Valentine now requires only the ministrations of the priest.” 50093m", "발랑틴에게 이제 필요한 것은 사제의 의식뿐이오.\""]], [["“You are mistaken, sir,”", "\"당신이 틀렸소, 선생.\" 모렐이 한쪽 무릎으로 몸을 일으키며 외쳤다."], ["exclaimed Morrel, raising himself on one knee, his heart pierced by a more acute pang than any he had yet felt—“you are mistaken; Valentine, dying as she has, not only requires a priest, but an avenger.", "지금까지 느껴 본 그 어떤 것보다 날카로운 고통이 그의 심장을 꿰뚫었다. \"당신이 틀렸소. 발랑틴은 이렇게 죽었으니, 사제만이 아니라 복수해 줄 자도 필요하오."], ["_You_, M. de Villefort, send for the priest; _I_ will be the avenger.”", "빌포르 씨, 당신은 사제를 부르시오. 복수해 줄 자는 내가 되겠소.\""]], [["“What do you mean, sir?”", "\"그게 무슨 말이오, 선생?\""], ["asked Villefort, trembling at the new idea inspired by the delirium of Morrel.", "빌포르가 모렐의 헛소리가 불러일으킨 새로운 생각에 몸을 떨며 물었다."]], [["“I tell you, sir, that two persons exist in you; the father has mourned sufficiently, now let the procureur fulfil his office.”", "\"내 말은, 선생, 당신 안에 두 사람이 있다는 거요. 아버지는 충분히 슬퍼했으니, 이제 검사가 그 직무를 다하게 하시오.\""]], [["The eyes of Noirtier glistened, and d’Avrigny approached.", "누아르티에의 두 눈이 번득였고, 다브리니가 다가왔다."]], [["“Gentlemen,”", "\"여러분,\""], ["said Morrel, reading all that passed through the minds of the witnesses to the scene, “I know what I am saying, and you know as well as I do what I am about to say—Valentine has been assassinated!”", "모렐이 이 장면을 지켜보는 이들의 마음속을 모두 읽어 내며 말했다. \"나는 내가 무슨 말을 하는지 알고, 여러분도 내가 무슨 말을 하려는지 나만큼 잘 압니다. 발랑틴은 살해되었습니다!\""]], [["Villefort hung his head, d’Avrigny approached nearer, and Noirtier said “Yes” with his eyes.", "빌포르는 고개를 떨구었고, 다브리니는 더 가까이 다가왔으며, 누아르티에는 두 눈으로 \"그렇다\"고 말했다."]], [["“Now, sir,”", "\"자, 선생.\""], ["continued Morrel, “in these days no one can disappear by violent means without some inquiries being made as to the cause of her disappearance, even were she not a young, beautiful, and adorable creature like Valentine.", "모렐이 말을 이었다. \"요즘 세상에는 누구든 폭력적인 방법으로 죽임을 당하면 그 죽음의 원인을 캐는 조사가 따르기 마련이오. 설령 그 사람이 발랑틴처럼 젊고 아름답고 사랑스러운 사람이 아니라 해도 말이오."], ["Now, M. le Procureur du Roi,”", "자, 검사님.\" 모렐이 점점 격해지는 목소리로 말했다."], ["said Morrel with increasing vehemence, “no mercy is allowed; I denounce the crime; it is your place to seek the assassin.”", "\"어떤 자비도 허용되지 않소. 나는 이 범죄를 고발하오. 살인자를 찾아내는 것은 당신의 직무요.\""]], [["The young man’s implacable eyes interrogated Villefort, who, on his side, glanced from Noirtier to d’Avrigny.", "젊은이의 가차 없는 눈이 빌포르를 추궁했다. 빌포르는 누아르티에에게서 다브리니에게로 시선을 옮겼다."], ["But instead of finding sympathy in the eyes of the doctor and his father, he only saw an expression as inflexible as that of Maximilian.", "그러나 의사와 아버지의 눈에서 동정을 찾는 대신, 그는 막시밀리앙의 것만큼이나 단호한 표정만을 보았다."]], [["“Yes,” indicated the old man.", "\"그렇다.\" 노인이 눈으로 말했다."]], [["“Assuredly,”", "\"틀림없습니다.\""], ["said d’Avrigny.", "다브리니가 말했다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"선생,\""], ["said Villefort, striving to struggle against this triple force and his own emotion,—“sir, you are deceived; no one commits crimes here.", "빌포르가 이 세 겹의 압박과 자신의 감정에 맞서 싸우려 애쓰며 말했다. \"선생, 당신은 속고 있소. 여기서 범죄를 저지른 사람은 없소."], ["I am stricken by fate.", "나는 운명에 짓밟힌 것이오."], ["It is horrible, indeed, but no one assassinates.”", "정말이지 끔찍한 일이지만, 누구도 살인을 저지르지는 않았소.\""]], [["The eyes of Noirtier lighted up with rage, and d’Avrigny prepared to speak.", "누아르티에의 두 눈이 분노로 타올랐고, 다브리니가 입을 열려 했다."], ["Morrel, however, extended his arm, and commanded silence.", "그러나 모렐이 팔을 뻗어 침묵을 명했다."]], [["“And I say that murders _are_ committed here,”", "\"내가 말하건대, 이 집에서는 살인이 저질러졌소.\""], ["said Morrel, whose voice, though lower in tone, lost none of its terrible distinctness: “I tell you that this is the fourth victim within the last four months.", "모렐의 목소리는 한층 낮아졌으나 그 무서운 또렷함은 조금도 잃지 않았다. \"내가 말하건대, 이것은 지난 넉 달 동안 네 번째 희생자요."], ["I tell you, Valentine’s life was attempted by poison four days ago, though she escaped, owing to the precautions of M.", "내가 말하건대, 나흘 전 발랑틴은 독으로 목숨을 잃을 뻔했으나, 누아르티에 씨의 대비 덕분에 화를 면했소."], ["Noirtier. I tell you that the dose has been double, the poison changed, and that this time it has succeeded.", "내가 말하건대, 이번에는 그 양이 두 배로 늘었고, 독이 바뀌었으며, 그래서 결국 성공한 것이오."], ["I tell you that you know these things as well as I do, since this gentleman has forewarned you, both as a doctor and as a friend.”", "내가 말하건대, 당신은 이 사실들을 나만큼 잘 알고 있소. 여기 이분이 의사로서, 또 친구로서 당신에게 미리 경고했으니 말이오.\""]], [["“Oh, you rave, sir,” exclaimed Villefort, in vain endeavoring to escape the net in which he was taken.", "\"오, 당신은 헛소리를 하고 있소, 선생.\" 빌포르가 자신이 걸려든 그물에서 벗어나려 헛되이 애쓰며 외쳤다."]], [["50095m\n“I rave?” said Morrel; “well, then, I appeal to M.", "\"내가 헛소리를 한다고요?\" 모렐이 말했다."], ["d’Avrigny himself. Ask him, sir, if he recollects the words he uttered in the garden of this house on the night of Madame de Saint-Méran’s death.", "\"그렇다면, 다브리니 씨께 직접 여쭙겠소. 선생, 생메랑 부인이 돌아가시던 날 밤, 이 집 정원에서 하신 말씀을 기억하시는지 그분께 물어보시오."], ["You thought yourselves alone, and talked about that tragical death, and the fatality you mentioned then is the same which has caused the murder of Valentine.”", "당신들은 둘뿐인 줄 알고 그 비극적인 죽음에 대해 이야기했고, 그때 당신이 입에 올린 그 숙명이 바로 발랑틴을 살해한 것과 같은 것이오.\""], ["Villefort and d’Avrigny exchanged looks.", "빌포르와 다브리니가 서로 눈길을 주고받았다."]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"그래요, 그래.\""], ["continued Morrel; “recall the scene, for the words you thought were only given to silence and solitude fell into my ears.", "모렐이 말을 이었다. \"그 장면을 떠올려 보시오."], ["Certainly, after witnessing the culpable indolence manifested by M.", "당신들이 침묵과 고독에만 들려준 줄 알았던 그 말들이 내 귀에 들어왔으니 말이오."], ["de Villefort towards his own relations, I ought to have denounced him to the authorities; then I should not have been an accomplice to thy death, as I now am, sweet, beloved Valentine; but the accomplice shall become the avenger.", "빌포르 씨가 자기 친족에게 보인 그 죄스러운 무관심을 목격한 이상, 나는 마땅히 당국에 그를 고발했어야 했소. 그랬다면 지금처럼 그대의 죽음에 공범이 되지는 않았을 텐데, 가엾고 사랑하는 발랑틴이여. 그러나 그 공범이 이제 복수자가 되겠소."], ["This fourth murder is apparent to all, and if thy father abandon thee, Valentine, it is I, and I swear it, that shall pursue the assassin.”", "이 네 번째 살인은 누가 봐도 명백하고, 그대의 아버지가 그대를 저버린다 해도, 살인자를 끝까지 쫓을 자는 바로 나라고, 내가 맹세하오.\""]], [["And this time, as though nature had at least taken compassion on the vigorous frame, nearly bursting with its own strength, the words of Morrel were stifled in his throat; his breast heaved; the tears, so long rebellious, gushed from his eyes; and he threw himself weeping on his knees by the side of the bed.", "이번에는, 마치 자연이 마침내 그 힘에 겨워 터질 듯한 건장한 육체를 가엾이 여기기라도 한 듯, 모렐의 말은 목구멍에서 막혔다. 그의 가슴이 들썩였고, 그토록 오래 거역하던 눈물이 두 눈에서 쏟아졌다. 그는 침대 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 흐느꼈다."]], [["Then d’Avrigny spoke. “And I, too,”", "그러자 다브리니가 입을 열었다."], ["he exclaimed in a low voice, “I unite with M.", "\"나 역시,\" 그가 낮은 목소리로 외쳤다."], ["Morrel in demanding justice for crime; my blood boils at the idea of having encouraged a murderer by my cowardly concession.”", "\"범죄에 대한 정의를 요구하는 데 있어 모렐 씨와 뜻을 같이하겠소. 나의 비겁한 양보로 살인자를 부추겼다는 생각에 피가 끓어오르오.\""]], [["“Oh, merciful Heavens!”", "\"오, 자비로운 하느님!\""], ["murmured Villefort.", "빌포르가 중얼거렸다."], ["Morrel raised his head, and reading the eyes of the old man, which gleamed with unnatural lustre,—", "모렐이 고개를 들어, 기이한 광채로 번득이는 노인의 눈을 읽고는 말했다."]], [["“Stay,”", "\"잠깐.\""], ["he said, “M.", "그가 말했다."], ["Noirtier wishes to speak.”", "\"누아르티에 씨가 말씀하시려 합니다.\""]], [["“Yes,” indicated Noirtier, with an expression the more terrible, from all his faculties being centred in his glance.", "\"그렇다.\" 누아르티에가 표정으로 말했는데, 모든 기능이 그 눈빛 하나에 집중되어 있는 만큼 한층 무서운 표정이었다."]], [["“Do you know the assassin?”", "\"살인자를 아십니까?\""], ["asked Morrel.", "모렐이 물었다."]], [["“Yes,” replied Noirtier.", "\"그렇다.\" 누아르티에가 답했다."]], [["“And will you direct us?” exclaimed the young man.", "\"그럼 저희를 인도해 주시겠습니까?\" 젊은이가 외쳤다."], ["“Listen, M. d’Avrigny, listen!”", "\"들으십시오, 다브리니 씨, 들어요!\""]], [["Noirtier looked upon Morrel with one of those melancholy smiles which had so often made Valentine happy, and thus fixed his attention.", "누아르티에는 발랑틴을 그토록 자주 행복하게 했던 그 우울한 미소 중 하나로 모렐을 바라보며 그의 주의를 끌었다."], ["Then, having riveted the eyes of his interlocutor on his own, he glanced towards the door.", "그러고는 상대의 두 눈을 제 눈에 단단히 붙들어 둔 채, 문 쪽으로 시선을 던졌다."]], [["“Do you wish me to leave?”", "\"제가 나가기를 바라십니까?\""], ["said Morrel, sadly.", "모렐이 서글프게 말했다."]], [["“Yes,” replied Noirtier.", "\"그렇다.\" 누아르티에가 답했다."]], [["“Alas, alas, sir, have pity on me!”", "\"아아, 아아, 어르신, 저를 가엾이 여겨 주십시오!\""]], [["The old man’s eyes remained fixed on the door.", "노인의 두 눈은 문에 고정된 채였다."]], [["“May I, at least, return?”", "\"적어도, 다시 돌아와도 됩니까?\""], ["asked Morrel.", "모렐이 물었다."]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇다.\""]], [["“Must I leave alone?” “No.”", "\"저 혼자 나가야 합니까?\""]], [["“Whom am I to take with me?", "\"아니다.\" \"누구를 데리고 가야 합니까?"], ["The procureur?”", "검사를요?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"아니다.\""]], [["“The doctor?”", "\"의사를요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇다.\""]], [["“You wish to remain alone with M. de Villefort?”", "\"빌포르 씨와 단둘이 남고 싶으신 겁니까?\" \"그렇다.\""]], [["“Yes.” “But can he understand you?”", "\"하지만 그가 어르신의 뜻을 알아들을 수 있겠습니까?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇다.\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오,\""], ["said Villefort, inexpressibly delighted to think that the inquiries were to be made by him alone,—“oh, be satisfied, I can understand my father.”", "빌포르가 그 조사가 자신 혼자 맡게 되리라는 생각에 형언할 수 없는 기쁨을 느끼며 말했다. \"오, 안심하시오. 나는 내 아버지의 뜻을 알아들을 수 있소.\""], ["While uttering these words with this expression of joy, his teeth clashed together violently.", "기쁨에 찬 표정으로 이 말을 내뱉으면서도, 그의 이는 사납게 딱딱 부딪쳤다."]], [["D’Avrigny took the young man’s arm, and led him out of the room.", "다브리니가 젊은이의 팔을 잡아 방 밖으로 데리고 나갔다."], ["A more than deathlike silence then reigned in the house.", "그러자 집 안에는 죽음보다 더한 침묵이 감돌았다."], ["At the end of a quarter of an hour a faltering footstep was heard, and Villefort appeared at the door of the apartment where d’Avrigny and Morrel had been staying, one absorbed in meditation, the other in grief.", "십오 분쯤 지났을 때 비틀거리는 발소리가 들렸고, 다브리니와 모렐이 머물러 있던 방의 문가에 빌포르가 나타났다. 한 사람은 깊은 사색에, 다른 한 사람은 슬픔에 잠겨 있었다."]], [["“You can come,”", "\"들어오시오.\""], ["he said, and led them back to Noirtier.", "그가 말하고는 두 사람을 누아르티에에게로 데려갔다."]], [["Morrel looked attentively on Villefort.", "모렐은 빌포르를 유심히 바라보았다."], ["His face was livid, large drops rolled down his face, and in his fingers he held the fragments of a quill pen which he had torn to atoms.", "그의 얼굴은 납빛이었고, 굵은 땀방울이 얼굴을 타고 흘러내렸으며, 손가락에는 산산조각으로 찢어 놓은 깃펜 조각들이 쥐여 있었다."]], [["“Gentlemen,”", "\"여러분,\""], ["he said in a hoarse voice, “give me your word of honor that this horrible secret shall forever remain buried amongst ourselves!”", "그가 쉰 목소리로 말했다. \"이 끔찍한 비밀이 영원히 우리끼리 묻혀 있으리라고 명예를 걸고 약속해 주시오!\""], ["The two men drew back.", "두 사람이 뒤로 물러섰다."]], [["“I entreat you——”", "\"부탁이오……\""], ["continued Villefort.", "빌포르가 말을 이었다."]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["said Morrel, “the culprit—the murderer—the assassin.”", "모렐이 말했다. \"그 죄인은, 그 살인자는, 그 암살자는요.\""]], [["“Do not alarm yourself, sir; justice will be done,”", "\"안심하시오, 선생. 정의는 실현될 것이오.\""], ["said Villefort.", "빌포르가 말했다."], ["“My father has revealed the culprit’s name; my father thirsts for revenge as much as you do, yet even he conjures you as I do to keep this secret.", "\"내 아버지가 죄인의 이름을 밝혀 주셨소. 내 아버지도 당신만큼이나 복수를 갈망하시오. 그런데도 그분조차 나처럼 당신에게 이 비밀을 지켜 달라고 간청하시오."], ["Do you not, father?”", "그렇지 않습니까, 아버지?\""]], [["“Yes,” resolutely replied Noirtier.", "\"그렇다.\" 누아르티에가 단호히 답했다."], ["Morrel suffered an exclamation of horror and surprise to escape him.", "모렐은 공포와 놀라움이 뒤섞인 탄성을 흘렸다."]], [["“Oh, sir,”", "\"오, 선생.\""], ["said Villefort, arresting Maximilian by the arm, “if my father, the inflexible man, makes this request, it is because he knows, be assured, that Valentine will be terribly revenged.", "빌포르가 막시밀리앙의 팔을 붙잡으며 말했다. \"내 아버지처럼 한번 마음먹으면 굽히지 않는 분이 이런 청을 하신다면, 그건 발랑틴의 원수가 무섭게 갚아지리라는 것을 알고 계시기 때문이오, 확실하오."], ["Is it not so, father?”", "그렇지 않습니까, 아버지?\""]], [["The old man made a sign in the affirmative. Villefort continued:", "노인이 그렇다는 표시를 했다. 빌포르가 말을 이었다."]], [["“He knows me, and I have pledged my word to him.", "\"그분은 나를 아시고, 나는 그분께 내 말을 걸었소."], ["Rest assured, gentlemen, that within three days, in a less time than justice would demand, the revenge I shall have taken for the murder of my child will be such as to make the boldest heart tremble;”", "안심하시오, 여러분. 사흘 안에, 정의가 요구하는 것보다 짧은 시간 안에, 내 아이의 살해에 대해 내가 행할 복수는 가장 대담한 심장조차 떨게 만들 것이오.\""], ["and as he spoke these words he ground his teeth, and grasped the old man’s senseless hand.", "이렇게 말하면서 그는 이를 갈았고, 노인의 감각 없는 손을 움켜쥐었다."]], [["“Will this promise be fulfilled, M.", "\"이 약속이 지켜질까요, 누아르티에 씨?\""], ["Noirtier?” asked Morrel, while d’Avrigny looked inquiringly.", "모렐이 물었고, 다브리니는 묻는 듯한 눈빛으로 바라보았다."]], [["“Yes,” replied Noirtier with an expression of sinister joy.", "\"그렇다.\" 누아르티에가 음산한 기쁨이 어린 표정으로 답했다."]], [["“Swear, then,”", "\"그렇다면 맹세하시오.\""], ["said Villefort, joining the hands of Morrel and d’Avrigny, “swear that you will spare the honor of my house, and leave me to avenge my child.”", "빌포르가 모렐과 다브리니의 손을 맞잡게 하며 말했다. \"내 집안의 명예를 지켜 주고, 내 아이의 복수는 내게 맡기겠다고 맹세하시오.\""]], [["D’Avrigny turned round and uttered a very feeble “Yes,”", "다브리니가 돌아서서 아주 미약하게 \"그렇소\"라고 내뱉었다."], ["but Morrel, disengaging his hand, rushed to the bed, and after having pressed the cold lips of Valentine with his own, hurriedly left, uttering a long, deep groan of despair and anguish.", "그러나 모렐은 손을 빼내어 침대로 달려가, 발랑틴의 차가운 입술에 제 입술을 맞춘 뒤, 절망과 비통함이 어린 길고 깊은 신음을 토하며 황급히 떠났다."]], [["We have before stated that all the servants had fled. M.", "앞서 말했듯이 하인들은 모두 달아난 뒤였다."], ["de Villefort was therefore obliged to request M. d’Avrigny to superintend all the arrangements consequent upon a death in a large city, more especially a death under such suspicious circumstances.", "그래서 빌포르는 다브리니에게, 대도시에서 사람이 죽었을 때, 더구나 이토록 의심스러운 정황 속에서 죽었을 때 뒤따르는 모든 절차를 맡아 처리해 달라고 부탁할 수밖에 없었다."]], [["It was something terrible to witness the silent agony, the mute despair of Noirtier, whose tears silently rolled down his cheeks.", "누아르티에의 말 없는 고통, 그 소리 없는 절망을 지켜보는 것은 끔찍한 일이었다. 눈물이 그의 뺨을 타고 소리 없이 흘러내렸다."], ["Villefort retired to his study, and d’Avrigny left to summon the doctor of the mayoralty, whose office it is to examine bodies after decease, and who is expressly named “the doctor of the dead.” M.", "빌포르는 서재로 물러갔고, 다브리니는 시청 소속 의사를 부르러 갔다. 그 의사의 직무는 사람이 죽은 뒤 시신을 검안하는 것으로, \"죽은 자의 의사\"라고 불리는 사람이었다."], ["Noirtier could not be persuaded to quit his grandchild. At the end of a quarter of an hour M.", "누아르티에 씨는 아무리 권해도 손녀 곁을 떠나려 하지 않았다. 십오 분쯤 지나 다브리니가 그 동료와 함께 돌아왔다."], ["d’Avrigny returned with his associate; they found the outer gate closed, and not a servant remaining in the house; Villefort himself was obliged to open to them.", "두 사람은 바깥 대문이 닫혀 있고 집 안에 하인 하나 남지 않은 것을 알았다. 빌포르가 직접 문을 열어 줄 수밖에 없었다."], ["But he stopped on the landing; he had not the courage to again visit the death chamber.", "그러나 그는 층계참에서 멈춰 섰다. 다시 죽음의 방으로 들어갈 용기가 나지 않았던 것이다."], ["The two doctors, therefore, entered the room alone.", "그래서 두 의사만이 방으로 들어갔다."], ["Noirtier was near the bed, pale, motionless, and silent as the corpse.", "누아르티에는 침대 곁에서 주검처럼 창백하게, 미동도 없이, 말없이 있었다."], ["The district doctor approached with the indifference of a man accustomed to spend half his time amongst the dead; he then lifted the sheet which was placed over the face, and just unclosed the lips.", "시청 의사는 인생의 절반을 죽은 자들 틈에서 보내는 데 익숙한 사람 특유의 무심함으로 다가왔다. 그러고는 얼굴을 덮은 침대보를 들추고 입술을 살짝 벌려 보았다."]], [["“Alas,”", "\"아아,\""], ["said d’Avrigny, “she is indeed dead, poor child!” 50099m", "다브리니가 말했다. \"정말로 죽었군요, 가엾은 아이가!\""]], [["“Yes,” answered the doctor laconically, dropping the sheet he had raised.", "\"그렇소.\" 의사가 들춰 올렸던 침대보를 도로 내려놓으며 간략히 답했다."], ["Noirtier uttered a kind of hoarse, rattling sound; the old man’s eyes sparkled, and the good doctor understood that he wished to behold his child.", "누아르티에가 일종의 쉰, 그르렁거리는 소리를 냈다. 노인의 두 눈이 번득였고, 마음씨 좋은 의사는 그가 손녀를 보고 싶어 한다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["He therefore approached the bed, and while his companion was dipping the fingers with which he had touched the lips of the corpse in chloride of lime, he uncovered the calm and pale face, which looked like that of a sleeping angel.", "그래서 그는 침대로 다가갔고, 그의 동료가 주검의 입술을 만진 손가락을 표백분 용액에 담그는 동안, 그는 잠든 천사처럼 보이는 그 평온하고 창백한 얼굴을 다시 드러내 보였다."]], [["A tear, which appeared in the old man’s eye, expressed his thanks to the doctor.", "노인의 눈에 맺힌 눈물 한 방울이 의사에게 보내는 감사를 표현했다."], ["The doctor of the dead then laid his permit on the corner of the table, and having fulfilled his duty, was conducted out by d’Avrigny.", "죽은 자의 의사는 검안 허가서를 탁자 모서리에 내려놓고, 제 의무를 다한 뒤 다브리니의 안내를 받아 밖으로 나갔다."], ["Villefort met them at the door of his study; having in a few words thanked the district doctor, he turned to d’Avrigny, and said:", "빌포르가 서재 문 앞에서 그들을 맞았다. 그는 몇 마디로 시청 의사에게 감사를 표한 뒤, 다브리니에게 돌아서서 말했다."]], [["“And now the priest.”", "\"이제 사제를 부를 차례군요.\""]], [["“Is there any particular priest you wish to pray with Valentine?”", "\"발랑틴을 위해 기도해 줄 특별히 원하는 사제가 있소?\""], ["asked d’Avrigny.", "다브리니가 물었다."]], [["“No.”", "\"아니오.\""], ["said Villefort; “fetch the nearest.”", "빌포르가 말했다. \"가장 가까운 사람을 데려오시오.\""]], [["“The nearest,”", "\"가장 가까운 분은,\""], ["said the district doctor, “is a good Italian abbé, who lives next door to you.", "시청 의사가 말했다. \"당신 댁 옆집에 사는 마음씨 좋은 이탈리아인 신부요."], ["Shall I call on him as I pass?”", "지나는 길에 들러 청해 볼까요?\""]], [["“D’Avrigny,”", "\"다브리니.\""], ["said Villefort, “be so kind, I beseech you, as to accompany this gentleman.", "빌포르가 말했다. \"부디 부탁이니, 이분과 동행해 주시오."], ["Here is the key of the door, so that you can go in and out as you please; you will bring the priest with you, and will oblige me by introducing him into my child’s room.” 50101m", "여기 문 열쇠가 있으니, 마음대로 드나들 수 있을 것이오. 그 사제를 모셔 와서, 내 아이의 방으로 안내해 주는 수고를 해 주시오.\""]], [["“Do you wish to see him?”", "\"그를 만나 보시겠소?\""]], [["“I only wish to be alone. You will excuse me, will you not?", "\"나는 그저 혼자 있고 싶을 뿐이오. 양해해 주시겠지요?"], ["A priest can understand a father’s grief.”", "사제라면 아비의 슬픔을 이해할 수 있을 테니.\""]], [["And M. de Villefort, giving the key to d’Avrigny, again bade farewell to the strange doctor, and retired to his study, where he began to work.", "그리고 빌포르 씨는 다브리니에게 열쇠를 건네고, 그 낯선 의사에게 다시 한번 작별을 고한 뒤, 서재로 물러가 일을 시작했다."], ["For some temperaments work is a remedy for all afflictions.", "어떤 기질의 사람에게는 일이 모든 괴로움의 치료약이 되는 법이다."]], [["As the doctors entered the street, they saw a man in a cassock standing on the threshold of the next door.", "두 의사가 거리로 나서자, 옆집 문턱에 사제복 차림의 한 남자가 서 있는 것이 보였다."]], [["“This is the abbé of whom I spoke,”", "\"저분이 제가 말한 그 신부입니다.\""], ["said the doctor to d’Avrigny.", "의사가 다브리니에게 말했다."], ["D’Avrigny accosted the priest.", "다브리니가 사제에게 다가가 말을 건넸다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"신부님,\""], ["he said, “are you disposed to confer a great obligation on an unhappy father who has just lost his daughter?", "그가 말했다. \"방금 딸을 잃은 불행한 아버지에게 큰 은혜를 베풀어 주실 뜻이 있으신지요?"], ["I mean M. de Villefort, the king’s attorney.”", "국왕 검사 빌포르 씨를 말하는 겁니다.\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["said the priest, in a marked Italian accent; “yes, I have heard that death is in that house.”", "사제가 뚜렷한 이탈리아 억양으로 말했다. \"그렇군요, 저 댁에 초상이 났다는 이야기는 들었습니다.\""]], [["“Then I need not tell you what kind of service he requires of you.”", "\"그렇다면 그분이 신부님께 어떤 봉사를 바라는지는 말씀드릴 필요가 없겠군요.\""]], [["“I was about to offer myself, sir,”", "\"제가 먼저 자청하려던 참이었습니다, 선생.\""], ["said the priest; “it is our mission to forestall our duties.”", "사제가 말했다. \"우리의 사명이란 마땅히 해야 할 일을 미리 헤아리는 것이지요.\""]], [["“It is a young girl.”", "\"젊은 처녀입니다.\""]], [["“I know it, sir; the servants who fled from the house informed me.", "\"압니다, 선생. 그 집에서 달아난 하인들이 알려 주었지요."], ["I also know that her name is Valentine, and I have already prayed for her.”", "이름이 발랑틴이라는 것도 알고, 이미 그녀를 위해 기도드렸습니다.\""]], [["“Thank you, sir,”", "\"고맙습니다, 신부님.\""], ["said d’Avrigny; “since you have commenced your sacred office, deign to continue it.", "다브리니가 말했다. \"이미 그 성스러운 직무를 시작하셨으니, 부디 이어 가 주십시오."], ["Come and watch by the dead, and all the wretched family will be grateful to you.”", "오셔서 죽은 이의 곁을 지켜 주시면, 그 불행한 가족 모두가 감사할 것입니다.\""]], [["“I am going, sir; and I do not hesitate to say that no prayers will be more fervent than mine.”", "\"가겠습니다, 선생. 그리고 거리낌 없이 말씀드리거니와, 제 기도보다 더 간절한 기도는 없을 것입니다.\""]], [["D’Avrigny took the priest’s hand, and without meeting Villefort, who was engaged in his study, they reached Valentine’s room, which on the following night was to be occupied by the undertakers.", "다브리니가 사제의 손을 잡았다. 서재에 틀어박혀 있는 빌포르와 마주치지 않고, 그들은 발랑틴의 방에 이르렀다. 그 방은 이튿날 밤이면 장의사들이 차지하게 될 터였다."], ["On entering the room, Noirtier’s eyes met those of the abbé, and no doubt he read some particular expression in them, for he remained in the room.", "방에 들어서자 누아르티에의 두 눈이 신부의 눈과 마주쳤는데, 그 안에서 무언가 특별한 표정을 읽었는지 신부는 방에 머물렀다."], ["D’Avrigny recommended the attention of the priest to the living as well as to the dead, and the abbé promised to devote his prayers to Valentine and his attentions to Noirtier.", "다브리니는 신부에게 죽은 이뿐만 아니라 산 사람도 보살펴 달라고 당부했고, 신부는 발랑틴을 위해 기도하고 누아르티에를 돌보겠노라 약속했다."]], [["In order, doubtless, that he might not be disturbed while fulfilling his sacred mission, the priest rose as soon as d’Avrigny departed, and not only bolted the door through which the doctor had just left, but also that leading to Madame de Villefort’s room.", "분명, 그 성스러운 사명을 다하는 동안 방해받지 않으려는 뜻에서, 신부는 다브리니가 떠나자마자 자리에서 일어나, 의사가 방금 나간 문에 빗장을 질렀을 뿐 아니라 빌포르 부인의 방으로 통하는 문에도 빗장을 질렀다."]], [["# Chapter 104. Danglars’ Signature", "# Chapter 104. Danglars’ Signature"]], [["The next morning dawned dull and cloudy.", "이튿날 아침은 흐리고 음울하게 밝아 왔다."], ["During the night the undertakers had executed their melancholy office, and wrapped the corpse in the winding-sheet, which, whatever may be said about the equality of death, is at least a last proof of the luxury so pleasing in life.", "밤사이 장의사들이 그 우울한 임무를 마쳐, 시신을 수의로 감쌌다. 죽음 앞에서는 모두가 평등하다지만, 그 수의야말로 살아생전 그토록 사랑받던 사치의 마지막 증거였다."], ["This winding-sheet was nothing more than a beautiful piece of cambric, which the young girl had bought a fortnight before.", "그 수의는 다름 아닌 아름다운 고급 무명천 한 폭이었는데, 그 처녀가 보름 전에 직접 사 둔 것이었다."]], [["During the evening two men, engaged for the purpose, had carried Noirtier from Valentine’s room into his own, and contrary to all expectation there was no difficulty in withdrawing him from his child.", "저녁나절에는 이 일을 위해 고용된 두 사람이 누아르티에를 발랑틴의 방에서 그의 방으로 옮겼다. 누구도 예상하지 못한 일이었지만, 노인을 손녀 곁에서 떼어 내는 데는 아무런 어려움도 없었다."], ["The Abbé Busoni had watched till daylight, and then left without calling anyone.", "부소니 신부는 동이 틀 때까지 곁을 지키다가 아무도 부르지 않고 떠났다."], ["D’Avrigny returned about eight o’clock in the morning; he met Villefort on his way to Noirtier’s room, and accompanied him to see how the old man had slept.", "다브리니는 아침 여덟 시쯤 다시 왔다. 그는 누아르티에의 방으로 가던 길에 빌포르를 만나, 노인이 어떻게 잤는지 함께 보러 갔다."], ["They found him in the large armchair, which served him for a bed, enjoying a calm, nay, almost a smiling sleep.", "두 사람은 침대 대신 쓰는 커다란 안락의자에 앉아 평온하게, 아니 거의 미소를 머금은 채 잠든 노인을 발견했다."], ["They both stood in amazement at the door.", "둘은 문 앞에서 놀라 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“See,”", "\"보십시오.\""], ["said d’Avrigny to Villefort, “nature knows how to alleviate the deepest sorrow.", "다브리니가 빌포르에게 말했다. \"자연은 가장 깊은 슬픔도 어떻게 달래는지 알고 있군요."], ["No one can say that M. Noirtier did not love his child, and yet he sleeps.”", "누아르티에 씨가 손녀를 사랑하지 않았다고는 아무도 말할 수 없을 텐데, 이렇게 주무시고 계시지 않습니까.\""]], [["“Yes, you are right,”", "\"그렇군요, 맞는 말씀입니다.\""], ["replied Villefort, surprised; “he sleeps, indeed!", "빌포르가 놀라며 대답했다. \"정말로 주무시는군요!"], ["And this is the more strange, since the least contradiction keeps him awake all night.”", "더 이상한 건, 사소한 일에 거슬리기만 해도 밤새 깨어 계시던 분이라는 점입니다.\""]], [["“Grief has stunned him,”", "\"슬픔이 저분을 마비시킨 겁니다.\""], ["replied d’Avrigny; and they both returned thoughtfully to the procureur’s study.", "다브리니가 대답했다. 두 사람은 생각에 잠긴 채 검사의 서재로 돌아갔다."]], [["“See, I have not slept,” said Villefort, showing his undisturbed bed; “grief does not stun me.", "\"보십시오, 저는 한숨도 못 잤습니다.\" 빌포르가 손도 대지 않은 침대를 가리키며 말했다."], ["I have not been in bed for two nights; but then look at my desk; see what I have written during these two days and nights.", "\"슬픔은 저를 마비시키지 못합니다. 이틀 밤이나 침대에 들지 못했지요. 하지만 제 책상을 보십시오."], ["I have filled those papers, and have made out the accusation against the assassin Benedetto.", "이 이틀 낮밤 동안 제가 쓴 것을 보세요. 저 서류들을 가득 채워, 살인자 베네데토에 대한 기소장을 작성해 냈습니다."], ["Oh, work, work,—my passion, my joy, my delight,—it is for thee to alleviate my sorrows!”", "오, 일이여, 일이여. 나의 열정, 나의 기쁨, 나의 환희여. 내 슬픔을 달래 줄 것은 바로 너로구나!\""], ["and he convulsively grasped the hand of d’Avrigny.", "그러고는 발작하듯 다브리니의 손을 부여잡았다."]], [["“Do you require my services now?”", "\"지금 제 도움이 필요하십니까?\""], ["asked d’Avrigny.", "다브리니가 물었다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아닙니다.\""], ["said Villefort; “only return again at eleven o’clock; at twelve the—the—oh, Heavens, my poor, poor child!”", "빌포르가 말했다. \"다만 열한 시에 다시 와 주십시오. 열두 시에는…… 그…… 그……"], ["and the procureur again becoming a man, lifted up his eyes and groaned.", "오, 하느님, 가엾고 가엾은 내 아이!\" 검사는 다시 한낱 인간으로 돌아가, 눈을 들어 신음했다."]], [["“Shall you be present in the reception-room?”", "\"접견실에 나와 계시겠습니까?\""]], [["“No; I have a cousin who has undertaken this sad office.", "\"아닙니다. 그 슬픈 일을 맡아 줄 사촌이 하나 있습니다."], ["I shall work, doctor—when I work I forget everything.”", "저는 일을 하겠습니다, 박사님. 일을 할 때면 저는 모든 것을 잊으니까요.\""]], [["And, indeed, no sooner had the doctor left the room, than he was again absorbed in work.", "과연 박사가 방을 나서자마자 빌포르는 다시 일에 몰두했다."], ["On the doorsteps d’Avrigny met the cousin whom Villefort had mentioned, a personage as insignificant in our story as in the world he occupied—one of those beings designed from their birth to make themselves useful to others.", "문간 계단에서 다브리니는 빌포르가 말한 그 사촌과 마주쳤다. 그는 이 이야기 속에서나 그가 살아가는 세상에서나 하찮기 짝이 없는 인물이었다. 태어날 때부터 남에게 쓸모를 바치도록 점지된 부류의 인간이었다."], ["He was punctual, dressed in black, with crape around his hat, and presented himself at his cousin’s with a face made up for the occasion, and which he could alter as might be required.", "시간을 정확히 지켰고, 검은 옷을 입었으며, 모자에는 검은 띠를 둘렀다. 이런 자리에 맞춰 꾸민 얼굴로 사촌의 집에 나타났는데, 그 얼굴은 필요에 따라 얼마든지 바꿀 수 있었다."]], [["At eleven o’clock the mourning-coaches rolled into the paved court, and the Rue du Faubourg Saint-Honoré was filled with a crowd of idlers, equally pleased to witness the festivities or the mourning of the rich, and who rush with the same avidity to a funeral procession as to the marriage of a duchess.", "열한 시가 되자 장례 마차들이 포석을 깐 안마당으로 굴러 들어왔고, 포부르 생토노레 거리는 한가한 구경꾼들로 가득 찼다. 그들은 부자들의 경사를 구경하든 상사를 구경하든 똑같이 즐거워했고, 공작 부인의 결혼식이나 장례 행렬이나 한결같은 열성으로 몰려드는 자들이었다."]], [["Gradually the reception-room filled, and some of our old friends made their appearance—we mean Debray, Château-Renaud, and Beauchamp, accompanied by all the leading men of the day at the bar, in literature, or the army, for M.", "접견실은 점차 들어찼고, 우리의 오랜 벗 몇몇이 모습을 드러냈다. 드브레와 샤토 르노, 그리고 보샹을 두고 하는 말이다. 거기에 법조계와 문단, 군부의 당대 명사들이 함께했다."], ["de Villefort moved in the first Parisian circles, less owing to his social position than to his personal merit.", "빌포르 씨는 사회적 지위보다는 개인적 자질 덕분에 파리 최고 사교계에 드나드는 인물이었던 것이다."]], [["The cousin standing at the door ushered in the guests, and it was rather a relief to the indifferent to see a person as unmoved as themselves, and who did not exact a mournful face or force tears, as would have been the case with a father, a brother, or a lover.", "문가에 선 사촌이 손님들을 안내했다. 무심한 사람들에게는 오히려 자기들만큼이나 무덤덤한 사람을 보는 것이 마음 편한 일이었다. 그는 아비나 형제나 연인이라면 으레 그러했을 슬픈 표정을 짓거나 억지 눈물을 강요하지 않았다."], ["Those who were acquainted soon formed into little groups.", "안면이 있는 이들은 곧 작은 무리를 지었다."], ["One of them was made of Debray, Château-Renaud, and Beauchamp.", "그중 한 무리가 드브레와 샤토 르노, 보샹이었다."]], [["“Poor girl,”", "\"가엾은 처녀.\""], ["said Debray, like the rest, paying an involuntary tribute to the sad event,—“poor girl, so young, so rich, so beautiful!", "다른 이들처럼 슬픈 일에 무심코 경의를 표하며 드브레가 말했다. \"가엾은 처녀, 그토록 젊고, 그토록 부유하고, 그토록 아름다웠는데!"], ["Could you have imagined this scene, Château-Renaud, when we saw her, at the most three weeks ago, about to sign that contract?”", "샤토 르노, 자네는 상상이나 했겠나, 길어야 삼 주 전에 그녀가 그 혼인 계약서에 서명하려는 모습을 보았을 때 이런 장면을?\""]], [["“Indeed, no,” said Château-Renaud.”", "\"정말이지, 못 했지.\" 샤토 르노가 말했다."]], [["“Did you know her?”", "\"그녀를 알았나?\""]], [["“I spoke to her once or twice at Madame de Morcerf’s, among the rest; she appeared to me charming, though rather melancholy.", "\"모르세르 부인 댁에서 다른 사람들 틈에 끼어 한두 번 말을 나눴어. 매력적인 아가씨였지, 좀 우울해 보이긴 했지만."], ["Where is her stepmother?", "계모는 어디 있나?"], ["Do you know?”", "자네 아나?\""]], [["“She is spending the day with the wife of the worthy gentleman who is receiving us.”", "\"우리를 맞아 주는 저 훌륭하신 양반의 부인과 함께 하루를 보내고 있다더군.\""]], [["50105m\n“Who is he?”", "\"그게 누군가?\""]], [["“Whom do you mean?”", "\"누구 말인가?\""]], [["“The gentleman who receives us?", "\"우리를 맞아 주는 저 양반 말일세."], ["Is he a deputy?”", "하원의원인가?\""]], [["“Oh, no. I am condemned to witness those gentlemen every day,”", "\"아, 아니야. 나는 매일같이 그런 양반들을 봐야 하는 신세지만,\""], ["said Beauchamp; “but he is perfectly unknown to me.”", "보샹이 말했다. \"저 사람은 통 모르는 얼굴이야.\""]], [["“Have you mentioned this death in your paper?”", "\"이번 죽음을 자네 신문에 실었나?\""]], [["“It has been mentioned, but the article is not mine; indeed, I doubt if it will please M.", "\"실리긴 했는데, 그 기사는 내가 쓴 게 아니야."], ["Villefort, for it says that if four successive deaths had happened anywhere else than in the house of the king’s attorney, he would have interested himself somewhat more about it.”", "사실 빌포르 씨 마음에 들지 의심스러워. 검사 집안이 아닌 다른 어디서 잇따라 네 사람이 죽었더라면, 검사가 그 일에 좀 더 관심을 두었을 거라고 썼거든.\""]], [["“Still,” said Château-Renaud, “Dr.", "\"그래도,\" 샤토 르노가 말했다."], ["d’Avrigny, who attends my mother, declares he is in despair about it.", "\"우리 어머니를 돌봐 주는 다브리니 박사는 이번 일로 절망에 빠졌다고 하던데."], ["But whom are you seeking, Debray?”", "그런데 누굴 찾고 있나, 드브레?\""]], [["“I am seeking the Count of Monte Cristo”", "\"몬테크리스토 백작을 찾고 있어.\""], ["said the young man.", "청년이 말했다."]], [["“I met him on the boulevard, on my way here,”", "\"여기 오는 길에 큰길에서 그를 만났네.\""], ["said Beauchamp.", "보샹이 말했다."], ["“I think he is about to leave Paris; he was going to his banker.”", "\"파리를 떠나려는 모양이더군. 거래 은행으로 가는 길이었어.\""]], [["“His banker?", "\"거래 은행?"], ["Danglars is his banker, is he not?”", "당글라르가 그의 거래 은행가 아닌가?\""], ["asked Château-Renaud of Debray.", "샤토 르노가 드브레에게 물었다."]], [["“I believe so,”", "\"그런 것 같아.\""], ["replied the secretary with slight uneasiness.", "비서관이 약간 불안한 기색으로 대답했다."], ["“But Monte Cristo is not the only one I miss here; I do not see Morrel.”", "\"그런데 여기서 안 보이는 사람이 백작만은 아니야. 모렐이 안 보이는군.\""]], [["“Morrel?", "\"모렐?"], ["Do they know him?”", "그들이 모렐을 아나?\""], ["asked Château-Renaud.", "샤토 르노가 물었다."], ["“I think he has only been introduced to Madame de Villefort.”", "\"내 생각에 그는 빌포르 부인에게만 소개됐을 텐데.\""]], [["“Still, he ought to have been here,”", "\"그래도 여기 와 있어야 할 사람인데.\""], ["said Debray; “I wonder what will be talked about tonight; this funeral is the news of the day.", "드브레가 말했다. \"오늘 밤엔 무슨 얘기가 오갈지 궁금하군. 이 장례식이 오늘의 화제니까."], ["But hush, here comes our minister of justice; he will feel obliged to make some little speech to the cousin,”", "하지만 쉿, 우리 법무 장관께서 오시는군. 사촌에게 짧은 인사말이라도 건네야 한다고 생각하시겠지.\""], ["and the three young men drew near to listen.", "세 청년은 들으려고 가까이 다가갔다."]], [["Beauchamp told the truth when he said that on his way to the funeral he had met Monte Cristo, who was directing his steps towards the Rue de la Chaussée d’Antin, to M. Danglars’.", "장례식에 오는 길에 몬테크리스토를 만났다고 한 보샹의 말은 사실이었다. 백작은 쇼세 당탱 거리, 당글라르 씨의 집으로 발걸음을 옮기던 참이었다."], ["The banker saw the carriage of the count enter the courtyard, and advanced to meet him with a sad, though affable smile.", "은행가는 백작의 마차가 안마당으로 들어오는 것을 보고, 슬프지만 상냥한 미소를 띠며 그를 맞으러 나왔다."]], [["“Well,” said he, extending his hand to Monte Cristo, “I suppose you have come to sympathize with me, for indeed misfortune has taken possession of my house. When I perceived you, I was just asking myself whether I had not wished harm towards those poor Morcerfs, which would have justified the proverb of ‘He who wishes misfortunes to happen to others experiences them himself.’ Well, on my word of honor, I answered, ‘No!’ I wished no ill to Morcerf; he was a little proud, perhaps, for a man who like myself has risen from nothing; but we all have our faults. Do you know, count, that persons of our time of life—not that you belong to the class, you are still a young man,—but as I was saying, persons of our time of life have been very unfortunate this year. For example, look at the puritanical procureur, who has just lost his daughter, and in fact nearly all his family, in so singular a manner; Morcerf dishonored and dead; and then myself covered with ridicule through the villany of Benedetto; besides——”", "\"이런,\" 그가 몬테크리스토에게 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"제게 위로의 말을 건네러 오신 모양이군요. 정말이지 불행이 제 집을 차지해 버렸으니까요. 백작을 알아보았을 때, 저는 마침 제가 저 가엾은 모르세르 집안에 못된 마음을 품지 않았던가 자문하던 참이었습니다. '남에게 불행이 닥치기를 바라는 자는 제가 그 불행을 겪는다'는 속담이 들어맞을까 봐서요. 한데 명예를 걸고 말씀드리지만, 저는 '아니다!'라고 답했습니다. 저는 모르세르에게 악의를 품은 적이 없습니다. 저처럼 맨주먹으로 일어선 사람치고는 좀 거만했는지 모르지만, 누구나 제 결점은 있는 법이지요. 백작, 우리 또래 사람들이—백작은 그 축에 들지 않으시지요, 아직 젊은 분이니까—아무튼 우리 또래 사람들이 올해 정말 운이 사나웠다는 걸 아십니까? 보십시오, 저 청교도 같은 검사를 말입니다. 방금 딸을 잃었지요. 사실 거의 온 가족을 그토록 기이한 방식으로 잃었습니다. 모르세르는 명예를 잃고 죽었고요. 그다음엔 저 자신이 베네데토라는 악당의 흉계로 웃음거리가 되었지요. 게다가……\""]], [["“Besides what?” asked the Count.", "\"게다가 또 무엇이 있습니까?\" 백작이 물었다."]], [["“Alas, do you not know?”", "\"아아, 모르고 계셨습니까?\""]], [["“What new calamity?”", "\"무슨 새로운 변고가요?\""]], [["“My daughter——”", "\"제 딸이……\""]], [["“Mademoiselle Danglars?”", "\"당글라르 양 말씀입니까?\""]], [["“Eugénie has left us!”", "\"외제니가 우리를 떠났습니다!\""]], [["“Good heavens, what are you telling me?”", "\"맙소사, 무슨 말씀이십니까?\""]], [["“The truth, my dear count.", "\"사실입니다, 백작."], ["Oh, how happy you must be in not having either wife or children!”", "오, 백작은 아내도 자식도 없으니 얼마나 행복하시겠습니까!\""]], [["“Do you think so?”", "\"그렇게 생각하십니까?\""]], [["“Indeed I do.”", "\"정말 그렇습니다.\""]], [["“And so Mademoiselle Danglars——”", "\"그래서 당글라르 양은……\""]], [["“She could not endure the insult offered to us by that wretch, so she asked permission to travel.”", "\"그 못된 자가 우리에게 안긴 모욕을 견디지 못하고, 여행을 떠나게 해 달라고 청했지요.\""]], [["“And is she gone?”", "\"그래서 떠났습니까?\""]], [["“The other night she left.”", "\"엊그제 밤에 떠났습니다.\""]], [["“With Madame Danglars?”", "\"당글라르 부인과 함께요?\""]], [["“No, with a relation.", "\"아니요, 친척 한 사람과 함께요."], ["But still, we have quite lost our dear Eugénie; for I doubt whether her pride will ever allow her to return to France.”", "하지만 어쨌든 우리는 사랑하는 외제니를 완전히 잃은 셈입니다. 그 아이의 자존심으로는 다시는 프랑스로 돌아오지 않을 테니까요.\""]], [["“Still, baron,”", "\"그래도, 남작,\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “family griefs, or indeed any other affliction which would crush a man whose child was his only treasure, are endurable to a millionaire.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"외동 자식이 유일한 보물인 사람이라면 짓눌렸을 그런 가족의 슬픔도, 아니 다른 어떤 고통도, 백만장자에게는 견딜 만한 것이지요."], ["Philosophers may well say, and practical men will always support the opinion, that money mitigates many trials; and if you admit the efficacy of this sovereign balm, you ought to be very easily consoled—you, the king of finance, the focus of immeasurable power.”", "철학자들이 흔히 말하고 실리적인 사람들도 늘 동조하듯이, 돈은 숱한 시련을 누그러뜨려 줍니다. 이 으뜸가는 영약의 효험을 인정하신다면, 당신이야말로 아주 쉽게 위로받을 수 있는 분 아니겠습니까. 금융계의 왕이요, 헤아릴 수 없는 권력의 중심이신 당신 말입니다.\""]], [["Danglars looked at him askance, as though to ascertain whether he spoke seriously.", "당글라르는 그가 진심으로 하는 말인지 가늠하려는 듯 곁눈질로 그를 보았다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇지요.\""], ["he answered, “if a fortune brings consolation, I ought to be consoled; I am rich.”", "그가 대답했다. \"재산이 위로가 된다면, 저는 위로받아야 마땅합니다. 저는 부자이니까요.\""]], [["“So rich, dear sir, that your fortune resembles the pyramids; if you wished to demolish them you could not, and if it were possible, you would not dare!”", "\"어찌나 부자이신지, 선생, 당신의 재산은 피라미드를 닮았습니다. 허물고 싶어도 허물 수 없고, 설령 가능하다 해도 감히 그러지 못할 만큼 말입니다!\""]], [["Danglars smiled at the good-natured pleasantry of the count.", "당글라르는 백작의 호의 어린 농담에 미소 지었다."], ["“That reminds me,”", "\"그러고 보니,\""], ["he said, “that when you entered I was on the point of signing five little bonds; I have already signed two: will you allow me to do the same to the others?”", "그가 말했다. \"백작께서 들어오셨을 때 저는 막 작은 채권 다섯 장에 서명하려던 참이었습니다. 벌써 두 장은 서명했는데, 나머지에도 마저 서명해도 괜찮겠습니까?\""]], [["“Pray do so.”", "\"그러시지요.\""]], [["There was a moment’s silence, during which the noise of the banker’s pen was alone heard, while Monte Cristo examined the gilt mouldings on the ceiling.", "잠시 침묵이 흘렀고, 그동안 은행가의 펜 소리만 들렸다. 몬테크리스토는 천장의 금박 몰딩을 살폈다."]], [["“Are they Spanish, Haitian, or Neapolitan bonds?”", "\"에스파냐 채권입니까, 아이티 채권입니까, 아니면 나폴리 채권입니까?\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아닙니다.\""], ["said Danglars, smiling, “they are bonds on the bank of France, payable to bearer.", "당글라르가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"프랑스 은행에 대한 채권으로, 소지인에게 지급되는 것입니다."], ["Stay, count,”", "자, 백작,\""], ["he added, “you, who may be called the emperor, if I claim the title of king of finance, have you many pieces of paper of this size, each worth a million?”", "그가 덧붙였다. \"제가 금융계의 왕이라면 황제라 불러 마땅한 분이신데, 한 장에 백만 프랑씩 나가는 이만한 크기의 종이를 많이 갖고 계십니까?\""]], [["The count took into his hands the papers, which Danglars had so proudly presented to him, and read:—", "백작은 당글라르가 그토록 자랑스레 내민 종이를 손에 받아 들고 읽었다."]], [["“‘To the Governor of the Bank.", "\"'은행 총재 귀하."], ["Please pay to my order, from the fund deposited by me, the sum of a million, and charge the same to my account.", "제가 예치한 자금에서 제 지시에 따라 백만 프랑을 지급하시고, 같은 금액을 제 계좌에 청구하여 주십시오."]], [["“Baron Danglars.’”", "당글라르 남작.'\""]], [["“One, two, three, four, five,”", "\"하나, 둘, 셋, 넷, 다섯,\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “five millions—why what a Crœsus you are!”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"오백만 프랑이라니, 당신은 그야말로 크로이소스로군요!\""]], [["“This is how I transact business,”", "\"제가 사업을 처리하는 방식이지요.\""], ["said Danglars.", "당글라르가 말했다."]], [["“It is really wonderful,”", "\"정말 놀랍습니다.\""], ["said the count; “above all, if, as I suppose, it is payable at sight.”", "백작이 말했다. \"더구나 제 짐작대로 일람불이라면 더욱 그렇지요.\""]], [["“It is, indeed,” said Danglars.", "\"맞습니다.\" 당글라르가 말했다."]], [["“It is a fine thing to have such credit; really, it is only in France these things are done.", "\"이만한 신용을 누리다니 대단한 일입니다. 정말이지 이런 일은 프랑스에서나 가능하지요."], ["Five millions on five little scraps of paper!—it must be seen to be believed.”", "작은 종이 다섯 장에 오백만 프랑이라니! 직접 보지 않고는 믿기 어렵습니다.\""]], [["“You do not doubt it?”", "\"의심하시는 겁니까?\""]], [["“You say so with an accent—stay, you shall be convinced; take my clerk to the bank, and you will see him leave it with an order on the Treasury for the same sum.”", "\"아니요!\" \"말투에 의심이 묻어나는군요. 자, 확인시켜 드리지요. 제 사무원을 데리고 은행에 가 보십시오. 그가 같은 금액의 국고 지급 명령서를 들고 나오는 것을 보시게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["“No,” said Monte Cristo folding the five notes, “most decidedly not; the thing is so curious, I will make the experiment myself.", "\"아닙니다.\" 몬테크리스토가 다섯 장의 어음을 접으며 말했다."], ["I am credited on you for six millions.", "\"단연코 그럴 것 없습니다."], ["I have drawn nine hundred thousand francs, you therefore still owe me five millions and a hundred thousand francs.", "어찌나 신기한 일인지, 제가 직접 시험해 보겠습니다. 제가 당신 앞으로 가진 신용이 육백만 프랑이지요."], ["I will take the five scraps of paper that I now hold as bonds, with your signature alone, and here is a receipt in full for the six millions between us.", "그중 구십만 프랑을 인출했으니, 당신은 아직 제게 오백십만 프랑을 빚지고 계신 셈입니다. 지금 제가 들고 있는 이 종이 다섯 장을 당신의 서명만으로 된 채권으로 받겠습니다. 그리고 여기 우리 사이의 육백만 프랑에 대한 영수증 전액이 있습니다."], ["I had prepared it beforehand, for I am much in want of money today.”", "미리 작성해 두었지요. 오늘 제게 돈이 몹시 필요하거든요.\""]], [["And Monte Cristo placed the bonds in his pocket with one hand, while with the other he held out the receipt to Danglars.", "그러고는 한 손으로 채권을 주머니에 넣으면서, 다른 손으로 당글라르에게 영수증을 내밀었다."], ["If a thunderbolt had fallen at the banker’s feet, he could not have experienced greater terror.", "벼락이 발치에 떨어졌다 해도 은행가가 이보다 더 큰 공포를 느끼지는 못했을 것이다."]], [["“What,”", "\"뭐라고요,\""], ["he stammered, “do you mean to keep that money?", "그가 더듬거렸다. \"그 돈을 가지실 작정입니까?"], ["Excuse me, excuse me, but I owe this money to the charity fund,—a deposit which I promised to pay this morning.”", "죄송합니다만, 죄송합니다만, 이 돈은 제가 자선 기금에 갚아야 할 것입니다. 오늘 아침에 지급하기로 약속한 예치금이지요.\""]], [["“Oh, well, then,”", "\"아, 그렇다면,\" 몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["said Monte Cristo, “I am not particular about these five notes, pay me in a different form; I wished, from curiosity, to take these, that I might be able to say that without any advice or preparation the house of Danglars had paid me five millions without a minute’s delay; it would have been remarkable.", "\"저는 이 다섯 장의 어음에 굳이 매달리지 않겠습니다. 다른 형태로 지급해 주십시오. 그저 호기심에서 이걸 받아, 아무런 통지나 준비 없이 당글라르 상회가 단 일 분의 지체도 없이 제게 오백만 프랑을 지급했다고 말할 수 있기를 바랐던 것뿐입니다."], ["But here are your bonds; pay me differently;”", "그랬다면 참으로 대단한 일이었겠지요."], ["and he held the bonds towards Danglars, who seized them like a vulture extending its claws to withhold the food that is being wrested from its grasp.", "하지만 여기 당신의 채권을 돌려드리지요. 다른 식으로 지급해 주십시오.\" 그가 채권을 당글라르 쪽으로 내밀자, 당글라르는 손아귀에서 빼앗기던 먹이를 도로 붙들려는 독수리가 발톱을 뻗치듯 그것을 낚아챘다."]], [["Suddenly he rallied, made a violent effort to restrain himself, and then a smile gradually widened the features of his disturbed countenance.", "문득 그는 정신을 가다듬어, 격렬히 자제하려 애썼다. 그러자 그의 일그러졌던 얼굴에 차츰 미소가 번져 갔다."]], [["50109m\n“Certainly,”", "\"물론,\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said, “your receipt is money.”", "\"백작의 영수증은 돈이나 다름없지요.\""]], [["“Oh dear, yes; and if you were at Rome, the house of Thomson & French would make no more difficulty about paying the money on my receipt than you have just done.”", "\"아, 그렇고말고요. 만약 로마에 계셨다면, 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회도 제 영수증을 받고 돈을 지급하는 데 방금 당신만큼이나 군말이 없었을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Pardon me, count, pardon me.”", "\"용서하십시오, 백작, 용서하십시오.\""]], [["“Then I may keep this money?”", "\"그럼 이 돈을 제가 가져도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그러시지요.\""], ["said Danglars, while the perspiration started from the roots of his hair.", "당글라르가 말했다. 머리카락 뿌리에서부터 땀이 배어 나왔다. \"그러시지요."], ["“Yes, keep it—keep it.”", "가지십시오. 가지세요.\""]], [["Monte Cristo replaced the notes in his pocket with that indescribable expression which seemed to say, “Come, reflect; if you repent there is still time.”", "몬테크리스토는 형용할 수 없는 표정으로 어음을 도로 주머니에 넣었다. 그 표정은 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"자, 잘 생각해 보시오. 후회된다면 아직 시간은 있소.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아닙니다.\""], ["said Danglars, “no, decidedly no; keep my signatures.", "당글라르가 말했다. \"아니요, 단연코 아닙니다. 제 서명을 가지십시오."], ["But you know none are so formal as bankers in transacting business; I intended this money for the charity fund, and I seemed to be robbing them if I did not pay them with these precise bonds.", "하지만 아시다시피 사업을 처리할 때 은행가만큼 격식을 따지는 사람도 없지요. 저는 이 돈을 자선 기금에 쓰려 했고, 바로 그 채권으로 지급하지 않으면 그들에게서 돈을 가로채는 것만 같았습니다. 어리석은 일이지요."], ["How absurd—as if one crown were not as good as another. Excuse me;”", "마치 한 닢이 다른 한 닢보다 못하기라도 한 것처럼 말입니다. 양해해 주십시오.\""], ["and he began to laugh loudly, but nervously.", "그러고는 큰 소리로, 그러나 초조하게 웃기 시작했다."]], [["“Certainly, I excuse you,”", "\"물론 양해하지요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo graciously, “and pocket them.”", "몬테크리스토가 너그럽게 말했다. \"그러니 제 주머니에 넣어 두겠습니다.\""], ["And he placed the bonds in his pocket-book.", "그러고는 채권을 수첩에 넣었다."]], [["“But,”", "\"한데,\""], ["said Danglars, “there is still a sum of one hundred thousand francs?”", "당글라르가 말했다. \"아직 십만 프랑이 남았는데요?\""]], [["“Oh, a mere nothing,”", "\"아, 그까짓 거야 아무것도 아니지요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["“The balance would come to about that sum; but keep it, and we shall be quits.”", "\"잔액이 그쯤 될 겁니다만, 그건 가지십시오. 그러면 우리 사이는 깨끗이 정산되는 겁니다.\""]], [["“Count,”", "\"백작,\""], ["said Danglars, “are you speaking seriously?”", "당글라르가 말했다. \"진심으로 하시는 말씀입니까?\""]], [["“I never joke with bankers,”", "\"저는 은행가와는 결코 농담하지 않습니다.\""], ["said Monte Cristo in a freezing manner, which repelled impertinence; and he turned to the door, just as the valet de chambre announced:", "몬테크리스토가 무례를 물리치는 얼음장 같은 어조로 말했다. 그러고는 문 쪽으로 돌아서는데, 마침 시종이 알렸다."]], [["“M. de Boville, Receiver-General of the charities.”", "\"자선 사업 총괄 수납관 보빌 씨께서 오셨습니다.\""]], [["“_Ma foi_,”", "\"맙소사,\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “I think I arrived just in time to obtain your signatures, or they would have been disputed with me.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"당신의 서명을 받아 내기에 딱 알맞은 때 도착한 모양이군요. 안 그랬으면 누가 이걸 두고 저와 다투었을 테니까요.\""]], [["Danglars again became pale, and hastened to conduct the count out.", "당글라르는 다시 얼굴이 창백해져서, 서둘러 백작을 배웅했다."], ["Monte Cristo exchanged a ceremonious bow with M. de Boville, who was standing in the waiting-room, and who was introduced into Danglars’ room as soon as the count had left.", "몬테크리스토는 대기실에 서 있던 보빌 씨와 격식을 차린 인사를 나누었다. 백작이 떠나자마자 보빌 씨는 당글라르의 방으로 안내되었다."]], [["The count’s serious face was illumined by a faint smile, as he noticed the portfolio which the receiver-general held in his hand.", "수납관이 손에 든 서류첩을 보고 백작의 진지한 얼굴에 옅은 미소가 번졌다."], ["At the door he found his carriage, and was immediately driven to the bank.", "문간에서 그는 자신의 마차를 발견하고, 곧장 은행으로 향했다."], ["Meanwhile Danglars, repressing all emotion, advanced to meet the receiver-general.", "한편 당글라르는 온갖 동요를 억누르며 수납관을 맞으러 나아갔다."], ["We need not say that a smile of condescension was stamped upon his lips.", "그의 입가에 짐짓 겸양의 미소가 새겨졌음은 말할 필요도 없겠다."]], [["“Good-morning, creditor,”", "\"안녕하십니까, 채권자 양반.\""], ["said he; “for I wager anything it is the creditor who visits me.”", "그가 말했다. \"무엇을 걸어도 좋습니다만, 저를 찾아오신 분은 채권자가 분명할 테니까요.\""]], [["“You are right, baron,” answered M.", "\"맞습니다, 남작.\" 보빌 씨가 대답했다."], ["de Boville; “the charities present themselves to you through me; the widows and orphans depute me to receive alms to the amount of five millions from you.”", "\"자선 사업이 저를 통해 당신께 인사를 드리는 겁니다. 과부와 고아들이 당신에게서 오백만 프랑에 이르는 자선금을 받아 오라고 저를 보냈습니다.\""]], [["“And yet they say orphans are to be pitied,”", "\"그런데도 사람들은 고아들을 가엾이 여겨야 한다고 하지요.\""], ["said Danglars, wishing to prolong the jest. “Poor things!”", "당글라르가 농을 이어 가려는 듯 말했다. \"딱한 것들 같으니!\""]], [["“Here I am in their name,” said M.", "\"제가 그들을 대신해 왔습니다.\""], ["de Boville; “but did you receive my letter yesterday?” “Yes.”", "보빌 씨가 말했다. \"그런데 어제 제 편지를 받으셨습니까?\""]], [["“I have brought my receipt.”", "\"받았습니다.\" \"영수증을 가져왔습니다.\""]], [["50111m\n“My dear M. de Boville, your widows and orphans must oblige me by waiting twenty-four hours, since M.", "\"보빌 씨, 당신의 과부와 고아들이 제게 스물네 시간만 양보해 주셔야겠습니다."], ["de Monte Cristo whom you just saw leaving here—you did see him, I think?”", "방금 여기서 나가는 것을 보신 몬테크리스토 백작이—보셨지요, 그렇지요?\""]], [["“Yes; well?”", "\"네, 그런데요?\""]], [["“Well, M. de Monte Cristo has just carried off their five millions.” “How so?”", "\"그 몬테크리스토 백작이 방금 그 오백만 프랑을 가져가 버렸거든요.\""]], [["“The count has an unlimited credit upon me; a credit opened by Thomson & French, of Rome; he came to demand five millions at once, which I paid him with checks on the bank.", "\"어떻게 그런 일이?\" \"백작은 제 앞으로 무제한 신용을 가지고 있습니다. 로마의 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회가 개설해 준 신용이지요."], ["My funds are deposited there, and you can understand that if I draw out ten millions on the same day it will appear rather strange to the governor.", "그가 와서 오백만 프랑을 당장 내놓으라기에, 은행 앞으로 끊은 수표로 지급했습니다. 제 자금은 거기 예치돼 있는데, 같은 날 제가 천만 프랑을 인출하면 총재가 좀 이상하게 여기리라는 건 이해하시겠지요."], ["Two days will be a different thing,” said Danglars, smiling.", "이틀이면 사정이 다르겠지만요.\" 당글라르가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Come,”", "\"이거 참,\""], ["said Boville, with a tone of entire incredulity, “five millions to that gentleman who just left, and who bowed to me as though he knew me?”", "보빌이 도무지 못 믿겠다는 투로 말했다. \"방금 나가면서 저를 아는 듯이 인사한 그 양반에게 오백만 프랑을 주었다고요?\""]], [["“Perhaps he knows you, though you do not know him; M.", "\"그가 당신을 알지도 모르지요, 당신이 그를 모를지라도 말입니다."], ["de Monte Cristo knows everybody.”", "몬테크리스토 백작은 누구든 다 아니까요.\""]], [["“Five millions!”", "\"오백만 프랑이라!\""]], [["“Here is his receipt.", "\"여기 그의 영수증이 있습니다."], ["Believe your own eyes.” M.", "당신 눈으로 직접 보십시오.\""], ["de Boville took the paper Danglars presented him, and read:", "보빌 씨는 당글라르가 내민 종이를 받아 읽었다."]], [["“Received of Baron Danglars the sum of five million one hundred thousand francs, to be repaid on demand by the house of Thomson & French of Rome.”", "\"당글라르 남작으로부터 오백십만 프랑을 수령하였으며, 이는 로마의 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회가 청구 시 상환할 것임.\""]], [["“It is really true,”", "\"정말 사실이군요.\""], ["said M. de Boville.", "보빌 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Do you know the house of Thomson & French?”", "\"톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회를 아십니까?\""]], [["“Yes, I once had business to transact with it to the amount of 200,000 francs; but since then I have not heard it mentioned.”", "\"네, 예전에 이십만 프랑짜리 거래를 한 적이 있지요. 하지만 그 뒤로는 이름조차 들어 보지 못했습니다.\""]], [["“It is one of the best houses in Europe,”", "\"유럽에서 손꼽히는 상회 중 하나지요.\""], ["said Danglars, carelessly throwing down the receipt on his desk.", "당글라르가 영수증을 책상 위로 아무렇게나 던지며 말했다."]], [["“And he had five millions in your hands alone!", "\"그런데 그가 당신 한 사람에게만 오백만 프랑을 맡겨 두었다니!"], ["Why, this Count of Monte Cristo must be a nabob?”", "이런, 그 몬테크리스토 백작은 인도의 거부쯤 되는 모양이군요?\""]], [["“Indeed I do not know what he is; he has three unlimited credits—one on me, one on Rothschild, one on Lafitte; and, you see,”", "\"솔직히 그가 뭐 하는 사람인지 저도 모릅니다. 무제한 신용을 세 군데에 갖고 있지요. 하나는 저한테, 하나는 로트실트한테, 하나는 라피트한테요."], ["he added carelessly, “he has given me the preference, by leaving a balance of 100,000 francs.”", "게다가, 보십시오,\" 그가 무심하게 덧붙였다. \"십만 프랑의 잔액을 남겨 둠으로써 제게 우선권을 준 셈입니다.\""]], [["M. de Boville manifested signs of extraordinary admiration.", "보빌 씨는 유난한 감탄의 기색을 드러냈다."]], [["“I must visit him,”", "\"그분을 한번 찾아뵈어야겠습니다.\""], ["he said, “and obtain some pious grant from him.”", "그가 말했다. \"자선 헌금을 좀 얻어 내야지요.\""]], [["“Oh, you may make sure of him; his charities alone amount to 20,000 francs a month.”", "\"아, 그분이라면 틀림없을 겁니다. 그분이 베푸는 자선만 해도 한 달에 이만 프랑에 이르니까요.\""]], [["“It is magnificent!", "\"대단하군요!"], ["I will set before him the example of Madame de Morcerf and her son.”", "모르세르 부인과 그 아드님의 본보기를 그분께 들려 드려야겠습니다.\""]], [["“What example?”", "\"무슨 본보기 말입니까?\""]], [["“They gave all their fortune to the hospitals.”", "\"그들은 전 재산을 병원들에 기부했지요.\""]], [["“What fortune?”", "\"무슨 재산을요?\""]], [["“Their own—M.", "\"자기네 재산을요."], ["de Morcerf’s, who is deceased.”", "작고한 모르세르 씨의 재산 말입니다.\""]], [["“For what reason?”", "\"무슨 까닭으로요?\""]], [["“Because they would not spend money so guiltily acquired.”", "\"그토록 죄스럽게 모은 돈을 쓰지 않겠다고 해서지요.\""]], [["“And what are they to live upon?”", "\"그럼 그들은 무엇으로 살아갑니까?\""]], [["“The mother retires into the country, and the son enters the army.” 50113m", "\"어머니는 시골로 물러나고, 아들은 군에 입대한답니다.\""]], [["“Well, I must confess, these are scruples.”", "\"이거 참, 솔직히 별난 양심도 다 있군요.\""]], [["“I registered their deed of gift yesterday.”", "\"어제 제가 그들의 기증 증서를 등록해 주었습니다.\""]], [["“And how much did they possess?”", "\"그래, 그들이 얼마나 가지고 있었답니까?\""]], [["“Oh, not much—from twelve to thirteen hundred thousand francs.", "\"아, 많지는 않았어요. 백이삼십만 프랑쯤이었지요."], ["But to return to our millions.”", "그건 그렇고, 우리 오백만 프랑 이야기로 돌아갑시다.\""]], [["“Certainly,”", "\"그러지요.\""], ["said Danglars, in the most natural tone in the world.", "당글라르가 세상에서 가장 자연스러운 어조로 말했다."], ["“Are you then pressed for this money?”", "\"그럼 이 돈이 급히 필요하신 겁니까?\""]], [["“Yes; for the examination of our cash takes place tomorrow.”", "\"네, 내일 우리 금고 검사가 있거든요.\""]], [["“Tomorrow?", "\"내일이요?"], ["Why did you not tell me so before?", "어째서 진작 말씀하지 않으셨습니까?"], ["Why, it is as good as a century! At what hour does the examination take place?”", "그럼 한 세기나 다름없이 넉넉하지요! 검사는 몇 시에 합니까?\""]], [["“At two o’clock.”", "\"두 시에요.\""]], [["“Send at twelve,”", "\"열두 시에 사람을 보내십시오.\""], ["said Danglars, smiling.", "당글라르가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["M. de Boville said nothing, but nodded his head, and took up the portfolio.", "보빌 씨는 아무 말 없이 고개를 끄덕이고는 서류첩을 집어 들었다."]], [["“Now I think of it, you can do better,”", "\"가만, 생각해 보니 더 나은 방법이 있군요.\""], ["said Danglars.", "당글라르가 말했다."]], [["“How do you mean?”", "\"어떻게요?\""]], [["“The receipt of M.", "\"몬테크리스토 백작의 영수증은 돈이나 마찬가지입니다."], ["de Monte Cristo is as good as money; take it to Rothschild’s or Lafitte’s, and they will take it off your hands at once.”", "그걸 로트실트나 라피트한테 가져가십시오. 그러면 그 자리에서 받아 줄 겁니다.\""]], [["“What, though payable at Rome?”", "\"뭐라고요, 로마에서 지급되는 것인데도요?\""]], [["“Certainly; it will only cost you a discount of 5,000 or 6,000 francs.”", "\"물론이지요. 할인료로 오천이나 육천 프랑쯤 들 뿐일 겁니다.\""]], [["The receiver started back.", "수납관이 흠칫 물러섰다."]], [["“_Ma foi!_”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["he said, “I prefer waiting till tomorrow.", "그가 말했다. \"내일까지 기다리는 편이 낫겠군요."], ["What a proposition!”", "별 제안을 다 하시는군요!\""]], [["“I thought, perhaps,”", "\"어쩌면,\""], ["said Danglars with supreme impertinence, “that you had a deficiency to make up?”", "당글라르가 더없는 무례함으로 말했다. \"당신이 메워야 할 부족분이라도 있는 게 아닌가 했지요.\""]], [["“Indeed,” said the receiver.", "\"그렇습니다.\" 수납관이 말했다."]], [["“And if that were the case it would be worth while to make some sacrifice.”", "\"그런 경우라면 다소간 희생을 감수할 만하지요.\" \"고맙습니다만, 사양하겠습니다.\""]], [["“Thank you, no, sir.”", "\"그럼 내일로 하지요.\""]], [["“Then it will be tomorrow.”", "\"네, 다만 틀림없어야 합니다.\""]], [["“Ah, you are laughing at me; send tomorrow at twelve, and the bank shall be notified.”", "\"아, 저를 놀리시는군요. 내일 열두 시에 사람을 보내십시오. 은행에 통지해 두겠습니다.\""]], [["“I will come myself.”", "\"제가 직접 오겠습니다.\""]], [["“Better still, since it will afford me the pleasure of seeing you.”", "\"그러면 더욱 좋지요. 당신을 뵙는 기쁨을 누릴 테니까요.\""], ["They shook hands.", "두 사람은 악수를 나누었다."]], [["“By the way,”", "\"그런데,\""], ["said M. de Boville, “are you not going to the funeral of poor Mademoiselle de Villefort, which I met on my road here?”", "보빌 씨가 말했다. \"여기 오는 길에 마주친 가엾은 빌포르 양의 장례식에는 가지 않으십니까?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니요.\""], ["said the banker; “I have appeared rather ridiculous since that affair of Benedetto, so I remain in the background.”", "은행가가 말했다. \"그 베네데토 사건 이후로 제가 좀 우스운 꼴이 되어서, 뒤로 물러나 있습니다.\""]], [["“Bah, you are wrong. How were you to blame in that affair?”", "\"무슨, 그건 잘못 생각하시는 겁니다. 그 일에 당신이 무슨 잘못이 있다고요?\""]], [["“Listen—when one bears an irreproachable name, as I do, one is rather sensitive.”", "\"들어 보십시오. 저처럼 흠 잡을 데 없는 이름을 지닌 사람은 좀 예민해지기 마련이지요.\""]], [["“Everybody pities you, sir; and, above all, Mademoiselle Danglars!”", "\"다들 당신을 안타까워합니다, 선생. 그중에서도 특히 당글라르 양을요!\""]], [["“Poor Eugénie!”", "\"가엾은 외제니!\""], ["said Danglars; “do you know she is going to embrace a religious life?” “No.”", "당글라르가 말했다. \"그 애가 수도 생활에 들어가려 한다는 걸 아십니까?\""]], [["“Alas, it is unhappily but too true.", "\"몰랐습니다.\" \"아아, 불행히도 너무나 사실입니다."], ["The day after the event, she decided on leaving Paris with a nun of her acquaintance; they are gone to seek a very strict convent in Italy or Spain.”", "그 일이 있은 다음 날, 그 애는 아는 수녀 한 사람과 함께 파리를 떠나기로 결심했습니다. 이탈리아나 에스파냐에서 아주 엄격한 수도원을 찾으러 떠났지요.\""]], [["“Oh, it is terrible!” and M.", "\"오, 끔찍한 일이군요!\""], ["de Boville retired with this exclamation, after expressing acute sympathy with the father.", "보빌 씨는 이렇게 탄식하며, 아버지에게 절절한 동정을 표한 뒤 물러갔다."], ["But he had scarcely left before Danglars, with an energy of action those can alone understand who have seen Robert Macaire represented by Frédérick,24 exclaimed:\n“Fool!”", "그러나 그가 떠나기가 무섭게 당글라르는, 프레데리크가 연기한 로베르 마케르를 본 사람만이 알 법한 격렬한 동작으로 외쳤다."]], [["Then enclosing Monte Cristo’s receipt in a little pocket-book, he added:—“Yes, come at twelve o’clock; I shall then be far away.”", "\"멍청한 놈!\" 그러고는 몬테크리스토의 영수증을 작은 수첩에 넣으며 덧붙였다. \"그래, 열두 시에 오너라. 그때쯤 나는 멀리 가 있을 테니.\""]], [["Then he double-locked his door, emptied all his drawers, collected about fifty thousand francs in bank-notes, burned several papers, left others exposed to view, and then commenced writing a letter which he addressed:", "이어 그는 문을 이중으로 잠그고, 서랍을 모조리 비워 지폐로 오만 프랑가량을 챙겼다. 몇몇 서류는 불태우고, 다른 서류들은 눈에 띄게 펼쳐 둔 채, 편지 한 통을 쓰기 시작했다. 받는 사람은 이러했다."]], [["“To Madame la Baronne Danglars.”", "\"당글라르 남작 부인 귀하.\""]], [["“I will place it on her table myself tonight,” he murmured.", "\"오늘 밤 내가 직접 그 여자 탁자에 올려놓아야지.\""], ["Then taking a passport from his drawer he said,—“Good, it is available for two months longer.”", "그가 중얼거렸다. 그러고는 서랍에서 여권을 꺼내며 말했다. \"좋아, 두 달은 더 쓸 수 있겠군.\""]], [["# Chapter 105. The Cemetery of Père-Lachaise", "# Chapter 105. The Cemetery of Père-Lachaise"]], [["M. de Boville had indeed met the funeral procession which was taking Valentine to her last home on earth.", "* 로베르 마케르: 19세기 프랑스 연극·풍자화에 등장하는 사기꾼 인물로, 배우 프레데리크 르메트르가 연기해 유명해졌다."], ["The weather was dull and stormy, a cold wind shook the few remaining yellow leaves from the boughs of the trees, and scattered them among the crowd which filled the boulevards. M.", "―옮긴이 보빌 씨는 정말로 발랑틴을 이 세상의 마지막 거처로 데려가는 장례 행렬과 마주쳤다. 날씨는 잔뜩 흐리고 폭풍이 일 듯했으며, 차가운 바람이 나뭇가지에 몇 잎 남지 않은 누런 잎사귀를 흔들어 떨어뜨리고는 큰길을 가득 메운 군중 사이로 흩뿌렸다."], ["de Villefort, a true Parisian, considered the cemetery of Père-Lachaise alone worthy of receiving the mortal remains of a Parisian family; there alone the corpses belonging to him would be surrounded by worthy associates.", "진정한 파리 사람인 빌포르 씨는 페르라셰즈 묘지만이 파리 가문의 유해를 받아들일 자격이 있다고 여겼다. 그곳에서만 자신에게 속한 시신들이 합당한 이웃들에게 둘러싸일 수 있다는 것이었다."], ["He had therefore purchased a vault, which was quickly occupied by members of his family.", "그래서 그는 납골당을 하나 사들였고, 그곳은 곧 일가의 식구들로 채워졌다."], ["On the front of the monument was inscribed: “The families of Saint-Méran and Villefort,”", "기념비 앞면에는 \"생메랑 가문과 빌포르 가문\"이라는 글귀가 새겨져 있었다."], ["for such had been the last wish expressed by poor Renée, Valentine’s mother.", "발랑틴의 어머니인 가엾은 르네가 마지막으로 남긴 소원이 그것이었기 때문이다."], ["The pompous procession therefore wended its way towards Père-Lachaise from the Faubourg Saint-Honoré.", "그리하여 화려한 행렬은 포부르 생토노레에서 페르라셰즈를 향해 길게 이어졌다."], ["Having crossed Paris, it passed through the Faubourg du Temple, then leaving the exterior boulevards, it reached the cemetery.", "파리를 가로지른 행렬은 포부르 뒤 탕플을 지났고, 외곽 큰길을 벗어나 묘지에 다다랐다."], ["More than fifty private carriages followed the twenty mourning-coaches, and behind them more than five hundred persons joined in the procession on foot.", "스무 대의 영구 마차 뒤로 쉰 대가 넘는 자가용 마차가 따랐고, 그 뒤로는 오백 명이 넘는 사람들이 걸어서 행렬에 합류했다."]], [["50117m\nThese last consisted of all the young people whom Valentine’s death had struck like a thunderbolt, and who, notwithstanding the raw chilliness of the season, could not refrain from paying a last tribute to the memory of the beautiful, chaste, and adorable girl, thus cut off in the flower of her youth.", "이 사람들은 발랑틴의 죽음에 벼락을 맞은 듯 충격을 받은 젊은이들로, 살을 에는 추운 날씨에도 불구하고, 젊음의 꽃 한가운데서 그렇게 꺾여 버린 아름답고 정숙하며 사랑스러운 처녀의 추억에 마지막 경의를 표하지 않을 수 없었던 이들이었다."]], [["As they left Paris, an equipage with four horses, at full speed, was seen to draw up suddenly; it contained Monte Cristo.", "파리를 벗어날 무렵, 말 네 마리가 끄는 마차 한 대가 전속력으로 달려오다가 별안간 멈춰 서는 것이 보였다. 그 안에는 몬테크리스토가 타고 있었다."], ["The count left the carriage and mingled in the crowd who followed on foot.", "백작은 마차에서 내려 걸어서 따라오는 군중 속에 섞여 들었다."], ["Château-Renaud perceived him and immediately alighting from his _coupé_, joined him; Beauchamp did the same.", "샤토르노가 그를 알아보고는 곧장 자기 쿠페에서 내려 그에게 다가왔다. 보샹도 똑같이 했다."]], [["The count looked attentively through every opening in the crowd; he was evidently watching for someone, but his search ended in disappointment.", "백작은 군중 사이로 난 모든 틈을 유심히 살폈다. 누군가를 찾고 있는 것이 분명했지만, 그 수색은 실망으로 끝났다."]], [["50119m\n“Where is Morrel?”", "\"모렐은 어디 있소?\""], ["he asked; “do either of these gentlemen know where he is?”", "그가 물었다. \"두 분 중 어느 분이라도 그가 어디 있는지 아시오?\""]], [["“We have already asked that question,”", "\"저희도 벌써 그 질문을 했습니다.\""], ["said Château-Renaud, “for none of us has seen him.”", "샤토르노가 말했다. \"저희 중 누구도 그를 보지 못했거든요.\""]], [["The count was silent, but continued to gaze around him.", "백작은 입을 다물었지만, 계속해서 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["At length they arrived at the cemetery.", "마침내 그들은 묘지에 다다랐다."], ["The piercing eye of Monte Cristo glanced through clusters of bushes and trees, and was soon relieved from all anxiety, for seeing a shadow glide between the yew-trees, Monte Cristo recognized him whom he sought.", "몬테크리스토의 날카로운 눈이 덤불과 나무 무리 사이를 훑었고, 곧 모든 불안에서 벗어났다. 주목 나무들 사이로 그림자 하나가 미끄러지듯 움직이는 것을 보고, 몬테크리스토는 자신이 찾던 사람을 알아본 것이다."]], [["One funeral is generally very much like another in this magnificent metropolis.", "이 웅장한 대도시에서 장례식은 대개 서로 무척 비슷하다."], ["Black figures are seen scattered over the long white avenues; the silence of earth and heaven is alone broken by the noise made by the crackling branches of hedges planted around the monuments; then follows the melancholy chant of the priests, mingled now and then with a sob of anguish, escaping from some woman concealed behind a mass of flowers.", "길고 하얀 길 위로 검은 형체들이 흩어진 모습이 보인다. 땅과 하늘의 침묵은 오직 기념비 둘레에 심긴 산울타리의 가지들이 부스럭거리며 부러지는 소리에만 깨진다. 그다음에는 사제들의 구슬픈 성가가 이어지고, 이따금 꽃 무더기 뒤에 숨은 어떤 여인에게서 새어 나오는 비통한 흐느낌이 그 소리에 섞여 든다."]], [["The shadow Monte Cristo had noticed passed rapidly behind the tomb of Abélard and Héloïse, placed itself close to the heads of the horses belonging to the hearse, and following the undertaker’s men, arrived with them at the spot appointed for the burial.", "몬테크리스토가 눈여겨본 그림자는 아벨라르와 엘로이즈의 무덤 뒤를 재빠르게 지나, 영구차를 끄는 말들의 머리 가까이에 자리를 잡았고, 장의사 일꾼들을 따라 그들과 함께 매장이 예정된 자리에 이르렀다. 사람들은 저마다 무언가에 정신이 팔려 있었다."], ["Each person’s attention was occupied. Monte Cristo saw nothing but the shadow, which no one else observed.", "몬테크리스토의 눈에는 그 그림자밖에 보이지 않았으나, 다른 누구도 그를 눈여겨보지 않았다."], ["Twice the count left the ranks to see whether the object of his interest had any concealed weapon beneath his clothes.", "백작은 두 번이나 행렬에서 빠져나와, 자신이 주목하는 그자가 옷 속에 무기를 감추고 있는지 살폈다."], ["When the procession stopped, this shadow was recognized as Morrel, who, with his coat buttoned up to his throat, his face livid, and convulsively crushing his hat between his fingers, leaned against a tree, situated on an elevation commanding the mausoleum, so that none of the funeral details could escape his observation.", "행렬이 멈추자, 그 그림자는 모렐임이 드러났다. 그는 외투 단추를 목까지 채우고, 얼굴은 납빛이 된 채, 손가락 사이로 모자를 경련하듯 짓이기며, 영묘를 내려다볼 수 있는 높은 곳에 선 나무에 기대어 있었다. 그래서 장례의 어떤 세세한 장면도 그의 관찰을 벗어날 수 없었다."]], [["Everything was conducted in the usual manner.", "모든 것이 여느 때와 다름없이 진행되었다."], ["A few men, the least impressed of all by the scene, pronounced a discourse, some deploring this premature death, others expatiating on the grief of the father, and one very ingenious person quoting the fact that Valentine had solicited pardon of her father for criminals on whom the arm of justice was ready to fall—until at length they exhausted their stores of metaphor and mournful speeches, elaborate variations on the stanzas of Malherbe to Du Périer.", "이 광경에 가장 마음이 동하지 않은 몇몇 사내들이 추도사를 했다. 어떤 이는 이 때 이른 죽음을 애도했고, 어떤 이는 아버지의 슬픔을 길게 늘어놓았으며, 무척 재치 있는 어떤 사람은 정의의 손길이 막 내리치려던 죄인들을 위해 발랑틴이 아버지에게 용서를 빌었다는 사실을 인용했다. 그러다 마침내 그들은 은유와 애도사의 밑천을, 말레르브가 뒤 페리에에게 바친 시구를 공들여 변주한 표현들을 다 써 버리고 말았다."]], [["Monte Cristo heard and saw nothing, or rather he only saw Morrel, whose calmness had a frightful effect on those who knew what was passing in his heart.", "몬테크리스토는 아무것도 듣지도 보지도 못했다. 아니, 오직 모렐만을 보고 있었다. 그의 마음속에 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 아는 사람들에게는 모렐의 그 평온함이 끔찍한 인상을 주었다."]], [["“See,” said Beauchamp, pointing out Morrel to Debray.", "\"보게.\" 보샹이 모렐을 가리키며 데브레에게 말했다."], ["“What is he doing up there?”", "\"저 위에서 뭘 하고 있는 거지?\""], ["And they called Château-Renaud’s attention to him.", "그리고 그들은 샤토르노의 주의를 그쪽으로 돌렸다."]], [["“How pale he is!” said Château-Renaud, shuddering.", "\"얼굴이 정말 창백하군!\" 샤토르노가 몸서리치며 말했다."]], [["“He is cold,”", "\"추워서 그렇겠지.\""], ["said Debray.", "데브레가 말했다."]], [["“Not at all,”", "\"전혀 아닐세.\""], ["said Château-Renaud, slowly; “I think he is violently agitated.", "샤토르노가 천천히 말했다. \"내가 보기엔 몹시 격앙된 것 같은데."], ["He is very susceptible.”", "저 친구는 감수성이 아주 예민하거든.\""]], [["“Bah,”", "\"흥.\""], ["said Debray; “he scarcely knew Mademoiselle de Villefort; you said so yourself.”", "데브레가 말했다. \"그는 빌포르 양을 거의 알지도 못했어. 자네 입으로 그렇게 말하지 않았나.\""]], [["“True.", "\"맞아."], ["Still I remember he danced three times with her at Madame de Morcerf’s.", "그래도 모르세르 부인 댁에서 그가 그녀와 세 번이나 춤을 췄던 게 기억나는군."], ["Do you recollect that ball, count, where you produced such an effect?” 50121m", "백작님, 그 무도회 기억하십니까? 거기서 백작님이 그토록 큰 화제를 불러일으키셨잖습니까?\""]], [["“No, I do not,”", "\"아니요, 기억나지 않소.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo, without even knowing of what or to whom he was speaking, so much was he occupied in watching Morrel, who was holding his breath with emotion.", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. 그는 자신이 무슨 말을, 누구에게 하고 있는지조차 모를 만큼, 감정에 북받쳐 숨을 죽이고 있는 모렐을 지켜보는 데 온통 정신이 쏠려 있었다."]], [["“The discourse is over; farewell, gentlemen,”", "\"추도사가 끝났군. 그럼 안녕히 계시오, 여러분.\""], ["said the count, unceremoniously.", "백작이 격식도 차리지 않고 말했다."]], [["And he disappeared without anyone seeing whither he went.", "그리고 그는 어디로 가는지 아무도 보지 못한 채 사라져 버렸다."]], [["The funeral being over, the guests returned to Paris.", "장례가 끝나자 참석자들은 파리로 돌아갔다."], ["Château-Renaud looked for a moment for Morrel; but while they were watching the departure of the count, Morrel had quitted his post, and Château-Renaud, failing in his search, joined Debray and Beauchamp.", "샤토르노는 잠시 모렐을 찾아보았다. 그러나 그들이 백작이 떠나는 모습을 지켜보는 사이에 모렐은 제자리를 떠나 버렸고, 샤토르노는 찾기를 단념하고 데브레와 보샹에게 합류했다."]], [["Monte Cristo concealed himself behind a large tomb and awaited the arrival of Morrel, who by degrees approached the tomb now abandoned by spectators and workmen.", "몬테크리스토는 커다란 무덤 뒤에 몸을 숨기고 모렐이 오기를 기다렸다. 모렐은 이제 구경꾼들과 일꾼들이 떠나 버린 무덤으로 조금씩 다가왔다."], ["Morrel threw a glance around, but before it reached the spot occupied by Monte Cristo the latter had advanced yet nearer, still unperceived.", "모렐이 주위를 한번 둘러보았으나, 그 시선이 몬테크리스토가 있는 자리에 닿기 전에 백작은 들키지 않은 채 더 가까이 다가서 있었다."], ["The young man knelt down.", "젊은이는 무릎을 꿇었다."], ["The count, with outstretched neck and glaring eyes, stood in an attitude ready to pounce upon Morrel upon the first occasion.", "백작은 목을 빼고 눈을 번뜩이며, 첫 기회가 오면 곧장 모렐에게 덮칠 자세로 서 있었다."], ["Morrel bent his head till it touched the stone, then clutching the grating with both hands, he murmured:", "모렐은 고개를 숙여 이마가 돌에 닿을 때까지 굽혔고, 두 손으로 철책을 움켜쥐고서 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Oh, Valentine!”", "\"오, 발랑틴!\""]], [["The count’s heart was pierced by the utterance of these two words; he stepped forward, and touching the young man’s shoulder, said:", "이 두 마디를 듣는 순간 백작의 가슴이 꿰뚫렸다. 그는 앞으로 나아가 젊은이의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 말했다."]], [["“I was looking for you, my friend.”", "\"자넬 찾고 있었네, 친구.\""], ["Monte Cristo expected a burst of passion, but he was deceived, for Morrel turning round, said calmly,—", "몬테크리스토는 격정이 폭발하리라 예상했으나, 그것은 빗나갔다. 모렐이 돌아서며 차분하게 말했던 것이다."]], [["“You see I was praying.” The scrutinizing glance of the count searched the young man from head to foot.", "\"보시다시피 저는 기도하고 있었습니다.\" 백작의 꿰뚫는 듯한 시선이 젊은이를 머리끝에서 발끝까지 살폈다."], ["He then seemed more easy.", "그러자 그는 조금 마음이 놓이는 듯했다."]], [["“Shall I drive you back to Paris?” he asked.", "\"파리까지 태워다 줄까?\" 그가 물었다."]], [["“No, thank you.”", "\"아니요, 괜찮습니다.\""]], [["“Do you wish anything?”", "\"무언가 필요한 게 있나?\""]], [["“Leave me to pray.”", "\"기도하게 내버려 두십시오.\""]], [["The count withdrew without opposition, but it was only to place himself in a situation where he could watch every movement of Morrel, who at length arose, brushed the dust from his knees, and turned towards Paris, without once looking back.", "백작은 더 막아서지 않고 물러났다. 그러나 그것은 모렐의 모든 움직임을 지켜볼 수 있는 자리에 자기 몸을 두기 위함일 뿐이었다. 마침내 모렐이 일어나 무릎의 먼지를 털고, 한 번도 뒤돌아보지 않은 채 파리 쪽으로 향했다."], ["He walked slowly down the Rue de la Roquette.", "그는 라로케트 거리를 천천히 걸어 내려갔다."], ["The count, dismissing his carriage, followed him about a hundred paces behind.", "백작은 마차를 돌려보내고 백 걸음쯤 뒤에서 그를 따라갔다."], ["Maximilian crossed the canal and entered the Rue Meslay by the boulevards.", "막시밀리앙은 운하를 건너 큰길을 지나 메슬레 거리로 들어섰다."]], [["Five minutes after the door had been closed on Morrel’s entrance, it was again opened for the count.", "모렐이 들어가고 문이 닫힌 지 오 분 뒤에, 그 문은 다시 백작을 위해 열렸다."], ["Julie was at the entrance of the garden, where she was attentively watching Penelon, who, entering with zeal into his profession of gardener, was very busy grafting some Bengal roses.", "쥘리는 정원 입구에 있었는데, 정원사 일에 열성을 다해 매달려 벵골장미 몇 그루를 접붙이느라 여념이 없는 페늘롱을 유심히 지켜보고 있었다. \"어머, 백작님.\""], ["“Ah, count,”", "그녀가 외쳤다."], ["she exclaimed, with the delight manifested by every member of the family whenever he visited the Rue Meslay.", "백작이 메슬레 거리를 찾아올 때마다 이 집안 식구라면 누구나 보이는 그 기쁨을 담은 외침이었다."]], [["“Maximilian has just returned, has he not, madame?”", "\"막시밀리앙이 방금 돌아왔지요, 부인?\""], ["asked the count.", "백작이 물었다."]], [["50123m\n“Yes, I think I saw him pass; but pray, call Emmanuel.”", "\"네, 지나가는 걸 본 것 같아요. 그런데 부디, 에마뉘엘을 불러 주세요.\""]], [["“Excuse me, madame, but I must go up to Maximilian’s room this instant,”", "\"미안합니다, 부인. 하지만 지금 당장 막시밀리앙의 방으로 올라가 봐야겠습니다.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo, “I have something of the greatest importance to tell him.”", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"그에게 더없이 중요한 할 말이 있어서요.\""]], [["“Go, then,” she said with a charming smile, which accompanied him until he had disappeared.", "\"그럼 어서 올라가세요.\" 그녀가 매혹적인 미소를 띠며 말했고, 그 미소는 백작이 사라질 때까지 그를 배웅했다."]], [["Monte Cristo soon ran up the staircase conducting from the ground floor to Maximilian’s room; when he reached the landing he listened attentively, but all was still.", "몬테크리스토는 곧 일층에서 막시밀리앙의 방으로 이어지는 계단을 뛰어 올라갔다. 층계참에 이르자 그는 귀를 기울여 유심히 들었지만, 사방이 고요했다."], ["Like many old houses occupied by a single family, the room door was panelled with glass; but it was locked, Maximilian was shut in, and it was impossible to see what was passing in the room, because a red curtain was drawn before the glass.", "한 가족이 단독으로 쓰는 오래된 집들이 흔히 그렇듯, 방문에는 유리를 끼운 칸막이가 달려 있었다. 그러나 문은 잠겨 있었다. 막시밀리앙은 안에 틀어박혀 있었고, 유리 앞에 붉은 커튼이 쳐져 있어 방 안에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 들여다볼 수 없었다."], ["The count’s anxiety was manifested by a bright color which seldom appeared on the face of that imperturbable man.", "좀처럼 동요하지 않는 이 사내의 얼굴에 좀처럼 떠오르지 않던 선명한 핏기가 떠올라, 백작의 불안을 드러내고 있었다."]], [["“What shall I do!”", "\"어떻게 해야 하나!\""], ["he uttered, and reflected for a moment; “shall I ring?", "그가 내뱉고는 잠시 생각에 잠겼다. \"벨을 울릴까?"], ["No, the sound of a bell, announcing a visitor, will but accelerate the resolution of one in Maximilian’s situation, and then the bell would be followed by a louder noise.”", "아니, 손님이 왔다고 알리는 벨소리는, 막시밀리앙 같은 처지에 놓인 사람의 결심을 앞당길 뿐이다. 그러면 벨소리에 이어 더 요란한 소리가 따라오겠지.\""]], [["Monte Cristo trembled from head to foot and as if his determination had been taken with the rapidity of lightning, he struck one of the panes of glass with his elbow; the glass was shivered to atoms, then withdrawing the curtain he saw Morrel, who had been writing at his desk, bound from his seat at the noise of the broken window.", "몬테크리스토는 머리끝에서 발끝까지 부르르 떨었고, 마치 번개처럼 빠르게 결심을 굳힌 듯, 팔꿈치로 유리 한 장을 내리쳤다. 유리가 산산조각 나자, 그는 커튼을 걷어 내고 모렐을 보았다. 책상에서 무언가를 쓰고 있던 모렐이 유리 깨지는 소리에 자리에서 벌떡 튀어 올랐다."]], [["“I beg a thousand pardons,”", "\"천번만번 용서를 비네.\""], ["said the count, “there is nothing the matter, but I slipped down and broke one of your panes of glass with my elbow.", "백작이 말했다. \"별일 아니야. 미끄러지면서 팔꿈치로 자네 유리 한 장을 깨뜨렸을 뿐일세."], ["Since it is opened, I will take advantage of it to enter your room; do not disturb yourself—do not disturb yourself!”", "이왕 열린 김에, 이 틈을 타서 자네 방으로 들어가겠네. 신경 쓰지 말게, 신경 쓰지 마!\""]], [["And passing his hand through the broken glass, the count opened the door.", "그리고 백작은 깨진 유리 사이로 손을 넣어 문을 열었다."], ["Morrel, evidently discomposed, came to meet Monte Cristo less with the intention of receiving him than to exclude his entry.", "모렐은 눈에 띄게 당황한 채, 몬테크리스토를 맞으러 왔다. 하지만 그를 맞아들이려는 것이라기보다는 그의 출입을 막으려는 의도에 더 가까웠다."]], [["“_Ma foi_,”", "\"맙소사.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, rubbing his elbow, “it’s all your servant’s fault; your stairs are so polished, it is like walking on glass.”", "몬테크리스토가 팔꿈치를 문지르며 말했다. \"다 자네 하인 탓일세. 자네 집 계단이 어찌나 반들반들한지, 유리 위를 걷는 것 같더군.\""]], [["“Are you hurt, sir?”", "\"다치셨습니까, 백작님?\""], ["coldly asked Morrel.", "모렐이 차갑게 물었다."]], [["“I believe not.", "\"아닌 것 같네."], ["But what are you about there?", "그런데 거기서 뭘 하고 있었나?"], ["You were writing.” “I?”", "무언가 쓰고 있었지.\""]], [["“Your fingers are stained with ink.”", "\"제가요?\" \"손가락에 잉크가 묻어 있군.\""]], [["“Ah, true, I was writing.", "\"아, 그렇군요. 쓰고 있었습니다."], ["I do sometimes, soldier though I am.”", "저도 군인이긴 하지만 가끔 글을 쓰거든요.\""]], [["Monte Cristo advanced into the room; Maximilian was obliged to let him pass, but he followed him.", "몬테크리스토가 방 안으로 들어섰다. 막시밀리앙은 어쩔 수 없이 그를 지나가게 했지만, 뒤를 바짝 따랐다."]], [["“You were writing?”", "\"무언가 쓰고 있었다고?\""], ["said Monte Cristo with a searching look.", "몬테크리스토가 살피는 눈빛으로 말했다."]], [["“I have already had the honor of telling you I was,”", "\"이미 그렇다고 말씀드리는 영광을 누렸습니다만.\""], ["said Morrel.", "모렐이 말했다."]], [["The count looked around him.", "백작이 주위를 둘러보았다."]], [["“Your pistols are beside your desk,”", "\"자네 권총이 책상 옆에 놓여 있군.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, pointing with his finger to the pistols on the table.", "몬테크리스토가 탁자 위의 권총을 손가락으로 가리키며 말했다."]], [["“I am on the point of starting on a journey,”", "\"여행을 막 떠나려던 참이라서요.\""], ["replied Morrel disdainfully.", "모렐이 경멸하듯 대답했다."]], [["“My friend,” exclaimed Monte Cristo in a tone of exquisite sweetness.", "\"이보게, 친구.\" 몬테크리스토가 더없이 부드러운 어조로 외쳤다."]], [["“Sir?”", "\"네?\""]], [["“My friend, my dear Maximilian, do not make a hasty resolution, I entreat you.”", "\"이보게, 나의 친애하는 막시밀리앙, 부디 성급한 결심은 하지 말게.\""]], [["“I make a hasty resolution?”", "\"제가 성급한 결심을 한다고요?\""], ["said Morrel, shrugging his shoulders; “is there anything extraordinary in a journey?”", "모렐이 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다. \"여행을 떠나는 데 무슨 별난 구석이라도 있습니까?\""]], [["50125m\n“Maximilian,”", "\"막시밀리앙.\""], ["said the count, “let us both lay aside the mask we have assumed.", "백작이 말했다. \"우리 둘 다 쓰고 있던 가면을 벗어 던지세."], ["You no more deceive me with that false calmness than I impose upon you with my frivolous solicitude.", "자네가 그 거짓된 평온으로 나를 속이지 못하는 것은, 내가 그 경박한 걱정으로 자네를 속이지 못하는 것과 마찬가지일세."], ["You can understand, can you not, that to have acted as I have done, to have broken that glass, to have intruded on the solitude of a friend—you can understand that, to have done all this, I must have been actuated by real uneasiness, or rather by a terrible conviction.", "자네도 이해할 수 있겠지? 내가 이렇게 행동한 것, 저 유리를 깨뜨린 것, 친구의 고독에 끼어든 것, 이 모든 일을 한 것은, 진짜 불안에, 아니 차라리 끔찍한 확신에 떠밀렸기 때문임을 말일세."], ["Morrel, you are going to destroy yourself!”", "모렐, 자네는 스스로 목숨을 끊으려 하고 있어!\""]], [["“Indeed, count,”", "\"아니, 백작님.\""], ["said Morrel, shuddering; “what has put this into your head?”", "모렐이 몸서리치며 말했다. \"어쩌다 그런 생각을 머릿속에 담으셨습니까?\""]], [["“I tell you that you are about to destroy yourself,”", "\"자네가 스스로 목숨을 끊으려 한다고 내가 말하지 않았나.\""], ["continued the count, “and here is proof of what I say;”", "백작이 말을 이었다. \"그리고 여기 그 증거가 있네.\""], ["and, approaching the desk, he removed the sheet of paper which Morrel had placed over the letter he had begun, and took the latter in his hands.", "그러고는 책상으로 다가가, 모렐이 쓰기 시작한 편지 위에 덮어 둔 종이를 치우고 그 편지를 손에 집어 들었다."]], [["Morrel rushed forward to tear it from him, but Monte Cristo perceiving his intention, seized his wrist with his iron grasp.", "모렐이 그것을 빼앗으려고 달려들었으나, 몬테크리스토가 그의 의도를 알아채고 무쇠 같은 손아귀로 그의 손목을 붙잡았다."]], [["“You wish to destroy yourself,”", "\"자네는 스스로 목숨을 끊으려는 거야.\""], ["said the count; “you have written it.”", "백작이 말했다. \"그렇게 써 놓았어.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"좋습니다.\""], ["said Morrel, changing his expression of calmness for one of violence—“well, and if I do intend to turn this pistol against myself, who shall prevent me—who will dare prevent me?", "모렐이 평온한 표정을 거칠고 격렬한 표정으로 바꾸며 말했다. \"좋아요, 제가 정말 이 권총을 제게로 겨누려 한다 칩시다. 누가 저를 막겠습니까?"], ["All my hopes are blighted, my heart is broken, my life a burden, everything around me is sad and mournful; earth has become distasteful to me, and human voices distract me.", "누가 감히 저를 막을 수 있단 말입니까? 제 모든 희망은 시들어 버렸고, 제 마음은 부서졌으며, 제 삶은 짐덩어리가 되었고, 제 주위의 모든 것이 슬프고 침울합니다. 이 땅이 역겨워졌고, 사람들의 목소리가 저를 미치게 합니다."], ["It is a mercy to let me die, for if I live I shall lose my reason and become mad.", "제가 죽도록 내버려 두는 것이야말로 자비예요. 살아 있다가는 이성을 잃고 미쳐 버릴 테니까요."], ["When, sir, I tell you all this with tears of heartfelt anguish, can you reply that I am wrong, can you prevent my putting an end to my miserable existence?", "백작님, 제가 진심에서 우러난 비통의 눈물을 흘리며 이 모든 것을 말씀드리는데, 제가 틀렸다고 대답하실 수 있습니까? 이 비참한 삶에 종지부를 찍으려는 저를 막으실 수 있겠습니까?"], ["Tell me, sir, could you have the courage to do so?”", "말씀해 보세요, 백작님, 그러실 용기가 있으십니까?\""]], [["“Yes, Morrel,”", "\"있네, 모렐.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, with a calmness which contrasted strangely with the young man’s excitement; “yes, I would do so.”", "몬테크리스토가 젊은이의 흥분과는 묘하게 대조되는 평온함으로 말했다. \"있고말고, 나는 그렇게 할 걸세.\""]], [["“You?”", "\"백작님이요?\""], ["exclaimed Morrel, with increasing anger and reproach—“you, who have deceived me with false hopes, who have cheered and soothed me with vain promises, when I might, if not have saved her, at least have seen her die in my arms!", "모렐이 점점 더 분노와 원망을 키우며 외쳤다. \"거짓된 희망으로 저를 속이고, 헛된 약속으로 저를 달래고 어르신 분이 말입니까? 그녀를 구하지는 못했다 해도, 적어도 제 품에서 숨을 거두는 모습은 볼 수 있었을 텐데 말입니다!"], ["You, who pretend to understand everything, even the hidden sources of knowledge,—and who enact the part of a guardian angel upon earth, and could not even find an antidote to a poison administered to a young girl!", "모든 것을, 심지어 감추어진 지식의 원천까지도 다 안다고 자처하시는 분이, 이 땅의 수호천사 노릇을 하시는 분이, 한 어린 처녀에게 투여된 독의 해독제 하나조차 찾아내지 못하셨잖습니까!"], ["Ah, sir, indeed you would inspire me with pity, were you not hateful in my eyes.”", "아, 백작님, 정말이지 제 눈에 가증스럽지만 않았던들, 백작님은 제 연민을 자아내셨을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Morrel——”", "\"모렐……\""]], [["“Yes; you tell me to lay aside the mask, and I will do so, be satisfied!", "\"네, 가면을 벗으라고 하셨으니, 그렇게 하지요. 그럼 만족하시겠지요!"], ["When you spoke to me at the cemetery, I answered you—my heart was softened; when you arrived here, I allowed you to enter.", "묘지에서 제게 말을 거셨을 때, 저는 대답했습니다. 제 마음이 누그러졌으니까요. 여기 오셨을 때, 저는 백작님을 들여보내 드렸습니다."], ["But since you abuse my confidence, since you have devised a new torture after I thought I had exhausted them all, then, Count of Monte Cristo my pretended benefactor—then, Count of Monte Cristo, the universal guardian, be satisfied, you shall witness the death of your friend;”", "하지만 백작님이 제 믿음을 저버리고, 제가 온갖 고통을 다 겪었다고 여긴 마당에 또 다른 고문을 고안해 내셨으니, 그렇다면, 제 거짓 은인이신 몬테크리스토 백작님, 그렇다면, 만인의 수호자이신 몬테크리스토 백작님, 만족하십시오, 백작님은 친구의 죽음을 지켜보시게 될 겁니다.\""], ["and Morrel, with a maniacal laugh, again rushed towards the pistols.", "그러고는 모렐이 미친 듯한 웃음을 터뜨리며 다시 권총을 향해 달려들었다."]], [["“And I again repeat, you shall not commit suicide.”", "\"다시 한번 말하네만, 자네는 자살하지 못할 거야.\""]], [["“Prevent me, then!”", "\"그럼 막아 보시지요!\""], ["replied Morrel, with another struggle, which, like the first, failed in releasing him from the count’s iron grasp.", "모렐이 다시 몸부림치며 대꾸했으나, 처음과 마찬가지로 백작의 무쇠 같은 손아귀에서 벗어나지는 못했다."]], [["“I will prevent you.”", "\"내가 막을 걸세.\""]], [["50127m\n“And who are you, then, that arrogate to yourself this tyrannical right over free and rational beings?”", "\"그럼 대체 당신이 누구이기에, 자유롭고 이성적인 존재들 위에 이런 폭군 같은 권리를 함부로 휘두르는 겁니까?\""]], [["“Who am I?” repeated Monte Cristo.", "\"내가 누구냐고?\" 몬테크리스토가 되뇌었다."], ["“Listen; I am the only man in the world having the right to say to you, ‘Morrel, your father’s son shall not die today;’”", "\"들어 보게. 나는 자네에게 '모렐, 자네 아버지의 아들은 오늘 죽지 않을 것이다'라고 말할 권리를 가진, 이 세상에 단 하나뿐인 사람일세.\""], ["and Monte Cristo, with an expression of majesty and sublimity, advanced with arms folded toward the young man, who, involuntarily overcome by the commanding manner of this man, recoiled a step.", "그러고는 몬테크리스토가 위엄과 숭고함이 어린 표정으로 팔짱을 낀 채 젊은이를 향해 다가갔다. 이 사내의 위압적인 태도에 자기도 모르게 압도당한 젊은이는 한 걸음 뒤로 물러났다."]], [["“Why do you mention my father?”", "\"어째서 제 아버지를 입에 올리십니까?\""], ["stammered he; “why do you mingle a recollection of him with the affairs of today?”", "그가 더듬거렸다. \"어째서 오늘 일에 아버지에 대한 기억을 뒤섞으시는 겁니까?\""]], [["“Because I am he who saved your father’s life when he wished to destroy himself, as you do today—because I am the man who sent the purse to your young sister, and the _Pharaon_ to old Morrel—because I am the Edmond Dantès who nursed you, a child, on my knees.”", "\"바로 내가, 오늘의 자네처럼 스스로 목숨을 끊으려 했던 자네 아버지의 생명을 구한 사람이기 때문일세. 바로 내가, 자네의 어린 누이에게 돈주머니를, 늙은 모렐에게 파라옹호를 보낸 사람이기 때문이지. 바로 내가, 어린 자네를 무릎에 앉히고 보살피던 에드몽 당테스이기 때문일세.\""]], [["Morrel made another step back, staggering, breathless, crushed; then all his strength give way, and he fell prostrate at the feet of Monte Cristo. Then his admirable nature underwent a complete and sudden revulsion; he arose, rushed out of the room and to the stairs, exclaiming energetically, “Julie, Julie—Emmanuel, Emmanuel!”", "모렐은 다시 한 걸음 뒤로 물러섰다. 비틀거리며, 숨이 막힌 채, 짓눌린 듯이. 그러더니 온몸의 힘이 풀려, 몬테크리스토의 발치에 엎드러졌다. 그 순간 그의 빼어난 천성이 완전히, 그리고 돌연히 뒤바뀌었다. 그는 벌떡 일어나 방을 뛰쳐나가 계단으로 달려가며 힘차게 외쳤다. \"쥘리, 쥘리! 에마뉘엘, 에마뉘엘!\""]], [["Monte Cristo endeavored also to leave, but Maximilian would have died rather than relax his hold of the handle of the door, which he closed upon the count.", "몬테크리스토도 그곳을 빠져나가려 했으나, 막시밀리앙은 죽는 한이 있어도 문손잡이를 놓지 않으려 했고, 백작을 안에 가둔 채 문을 닫아 버렸다."], ["Julie, Emmanuel, and some of the servants, ran up in alarm on hearing the cries of Maximilian.", "쥘리와 에마뉘엘, 그리고 하인 몇이 막시밀리앙의 외침을 듣고 놀라 달려 올라왔다."], ["Morrel seized their hands, and opening the door exclaimed in a voice choked with sobs:", "모렐은 그들의 손을 붙잡고 문을 열어젖히며 흐느낌에 막힌 목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“On your knees—on your knees—he is our benefactor—the saviour of our father!", "\"무릎을 꿇어요, 무릎을 꿇어! 이분은 우리의 은인이셔, 우리 아버지를 구해 주신 분이야!"], ["He is——”", "이분은……\""]], [["He would have added “Edmond Dantès,” but the count seized his arm and prevented him.", "그는 \"에드몽 당테스\"라고 덧붙이려 했으나, 백작이 그의 팔을 붙잡아 막았다."]], [["Julie threw herself into the arms of the count; Emmanuel embraced him as a guardian angel; Morrel again fell on his knees, and struck the ground with his forehead.", "쥘리는 백작의 품에 몸을 던졌다. 에마뉘엘은 수호천사를 끌어안듯 그를 껴안았다. 모렐은 다시 무릎을 꿇고 이마로 바닥을 짓찧었다."], ["Then the iron-hearted man felt his heart swell in his breast; a flame seemed to rush from his throat to his eyes, he bent his head and wept.", "그러자 무쇠 같은 심장을 지닌 그 사내도 가슴속에서 마음이 부풀어 오르는 것을 느꼈다. 목구멍에서 불꽃이 솟구쳐 눈으로 치미는 듯했고, 그는 고개를 숙이고 울었다."], ["For a while nothing was heard in the room but a succession of sobs, while the incense from their grateful hearts mounted to heaven.", "한동안 방 안에는 잇따른 흐느낌 소리밖에 들리지 않았고, 그동안 그들의 감사하는 마음에서 피어오른 향이 하늘로 올라갔다."], ["Julie had scarcely recovered from her deep emotion when she rushed out of the room, descended to the next floor, ran into the drawing-room with childlike joy and raised the crystal globe which covered the purse given by the unknown of the Allées de Meilhan.", "쥘리는 깊은 감격에서 채 헤어나기도 전에 방을 뛰쳐나가 아래층으로 내려가, 어린아이 같은 기쁨에 휩싸여 응접실로 달려가서는, 메이앙 가로수길의 낯선 이가 준 돈주머니를 덮고 있던 수정 덮개를 들어 올렸다."], ["Meanwhile, Emmanuel in a broken voice said to the count:", "그러는 동안 에마뉘엘이 떨리는 목소리로 백작에게 말했다."]], [["“Oh, count, how could you, hearing us so often speak of our unknown benefactor, seeing us pay such homage of gratitude and adoration to his memory,—how could you continue so long without discovering yourself to us?", "\"오, 백작님, 저희가 그토록 자주 얼굴 모르는 은인 이야기를 하고, 그분의 기억에 그토록 감사와 흠모의 마음을 바치는 것을 보시고도, 어떻게 그렇게 오랫동안 정체를 밝히지 않으실 수 있었습니까? 오, 그건 저희에게 잔인한 일이었습니다."], ["Oh, it was cruel to us, and—dare I say it?—to you also.”", "그리고, 감히 말씀드리자면, 백작님 자신에게도요.\""]], [["“Listen, my friends,”", "\"들어 보게, 나의 친구들이여.\""], ["said the count—“I may call you so since we have really been friends for the last eleven years—the discovery of this secret has been occasioned by a great event which you must never know.", "백작이 말했다. \"지난 십일 년 동안 우리는 정말로 친구였으니, 그렇게 불러도 되겠지. 이 비밀이 드러나게 된 것은 자네들이 결코 알아서는 안 될 어떤 큰 사건 때문이었네."], ["I wished to bury it during my whole life in my own bosom, but your brother Maximilian wrested it from me by a violence he repents of now, I am sure.”", "나는 그것을 평생 내 가슴속에 묻어 두고 싶었지만, 자네들의 형제 막시밀리앙이 폭력으로 그것을 내게서 빼앗아 낸 걸세. 지금은 그가 후회하고 있을 거라 확신하네만.\""]], [["Then turning around, and seeing that Morrel, still on his knees, had thrown himself into an armchair, he added in a low voice, pressing Emmanuel’s hand significantly, “Watch over him.”", "그러고는 몸을 돌려, 여전히 무릎을 꿇고 있던 모렐이 안락의자에 몸을 내던지듯 기댄 것을 보고는, 에마뉘엘의 손을 의미심장하게 꽉 쥐며 낮은 목소리로 덧붙였다. \"저 친구를 잘 지켜보게.\""]], [["“Why so?” asked the young man, surprised.", "\"어째서요?\" 젊은이가 놀라며 물었다."]], [["“I cannot explain myself; but watch over him.”", "\"내 입으로는 설명할 수 없네. 하지만 저 친구를 잘 지켜보게.\""], ["Emmanuel looked around the room and caught sight of the pistols; his eyes rested on the weapons, and he pointed to them.", "에마뉘엘은 방 안을 둘러보다가 권총을 발견했다. 그의 눈이 그 무기에 멎었고, 그는 손가락으로 그것을 가리켰다."], ["Monte Cristo bent his head.", "몬테크리스토가 고개를 숙였다."], ["Emmanuel went towards the pistols.", "에마뉘엘이 권총 쪽으로 다가갔다."]], [["“Leave them,”", "\"그냥 두게.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["Then walking towards Morrel, he took his hand; the tumultuous agitation of the young man was succeeded by a profound stupor.", "그러고는 모렐에게로 걸어가 그의 손을 잡았다. 젊은이의 격렬한 동요는 깊은 망연자실로 바뀌어 있었다."], ["Julie returned, holding the silken purse in her hands, while tears of joy rolled down her cheeks, like dewdrops on the rose.", "쥘리가 비단 돈주머니를 손에 든 채 돌아왔고, 기쁨의 눈물이 장미 위의 이슬방울처럼 그녀의 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸다."]], [["“Here is the relic,”", "\"이게 그 유물이에요.\""], ["she said; “do not think it will be less dear to us now we are acquainted with our benefactor!”", "그녀가 말했다. \"이제 저희 은인을 알게 되었다고 해서 이게 덜 소중해지리라고는 생각지 마세요!\""]], [["“My child,”", "\"얘야.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, coloring, “allow me to take back that purse?", "몬테크리스토가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"그 돈주머니는 내가 도로 가져가게 해 주겠니?"], ["Since you now know my face, I wish to be remembered alone through the affection I hope you will grant me.", "이제 너희가 내 얼굴을 알게 되었으니, 나는 오직 너희가 내게 베풀어 주기를 바라는 그 애정으로만 기억되고 싶구나.\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오.\""], ["said Julie, pressing the purse to her heart, “no, no, I beseech you do not take it, for some unhappy day you will leave us, will you not?”", "쥘리가 돈주머니를 가슴에 꼭 끌어안으며 말했다. \"안 돼요, 안 돼요, 제발 가져가지 마세요. 언젠가 불행한 날이 오면 저희를 떠나실 거잖아요, 그렇죠?\""]], [["“You have guessed rightly, madame,”", "\"제대로 알아맞혔구려, 부인.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo, smiling; “in a week I shall have left this country, where so many persons who merit the vengeance of Heaven lived happily, while my father perished of hunger and grief.”", "몬테크리스토가 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"일주일 뒤면 나는 이 나라를 떠나 있을 거요. 하늘의 복수를 받아 마땅한 수많은 사람들이 행복하게 살아가는 동안, 내 아버지는 굶주림과 비탄 속에 돌아가신 이 나라를 말이오.\""]], [["While announcing his departure, the count fixed his eyes on Morrel, and remarked that the words, “I shall have left this country,” had failed to rouse him from his lethargy.", "자신의 떠남을 알리면서 백작은 모렐에게 눈길을 고정했고, \"나는 이 나라를 떠나 있을 거요\"라는 말이 그를 무기력에서 깨우지 못했음을 알아챘다."], ["He then saw that he must make another struggle against the grief of his friend, and taking the hands of Emmanuel and Julie, which he pressed within his own, he said with the mild authority of a father:", "그제야 그는 친구의 슬픔과 또 한 번 싸워야 한다는 것을 깨닫고, 에마뉘엘과 쥘리의 손을 잡아 자기 손 안에 꼭 쥐며, 아버지처럼 온화한 위엄을 담아 말했다."]], [["“My kind friends, leave me alone with Maximilian.”", "\"착한 친구들이여, 나와 막시밀리앙 둘만 있게 해 주게.\""]], [["Julie saw the means offered of carrying off her precious relic, which Monte Cristo had forgotten.", "쥘리는 몬테크리스토가 깜빡한 그 소중한 유물을 가지고 빠져나갈 길이 열렸음을 알아챘다."], ["She drew her husband to the door.", "그녀는 남편을 문 쪽으로 끌었다."], ["“Let us leave them,” she said.", "\"우리는 자리를 비켜 줘요.\" 그녀가 말했다."]], [["The count was alone with Morrel, who remained motionless as a statue.", "백작은 조각상처럼 꼼짝 않고 있는 모렐과 단둘이 남았다."]], [["“Come,”", "\"자.\""], ["said Monte-Cristo, touching his shoulder with his finger, “are you a man again, Maximilian?”", "몬테크리스토가 손가락으로 그의 어깨를 건드리며 말했다. \"다시 사내대장부가 되었는가, 막시밀리앙?\""]], [["“Yes; for I begin to suffer again.”", "\"네, 다시 고통을 느끼기 시작했으니까요.\""]], [["The count frowned, apparently in gloomy hesitation.", "백작은 눈살을 찌푸렸다. 침울한 망설임에 잠긴 듯했다."]], [["“Maximilian, Maximilian,”", "\"막시밀리앙, 막시밀리앙.\""], ["he said, “the ideas you yield to are unworthy of a Christian.”", "그가 말했다. \"자네가 빠져든 그 생각들은 그리스도교인답지 못하네.\""]], [["“Oh, do not fear, my friend,”", "\"오, 염려 마십시오, 친구여.\""], ["said Morrel, raising his head, and smiling with a sweet expression on the count; “I shall no longer attempt my life.”", "모렐이 고개를 들고 백작을 향해 부드러운 표정으로 미소 지으며 말했다. \"이제 더는 제 목숨을 끊으려 들지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["“Then we are to have no more pistols—no more despair?”", "\"그럼 이제 권총도, 절망도 더는 없는 건가?\""]], [["“No; I have found a better remedy for my grief than either a bullet or a knife.”", "\"네. 총알이나 칼보다 더 나은 슬픔의 치료법을 찾아냈으니까요.\""]], [["“Poor fellow, what is it?”", "\"가엾은 친구, 그게 무엇인가?\""]], [["“My grief will kill me of itself.”", "\"제 슬픔이 절로 저를 죽여 줄 겁니다.\""]], [["“My friend,”", "\"이보게, 친구.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, with an expression of melancholy equal to his own, “listen to me.", "몬테크리스토가 그 못지않은 우수에 찬 표정으로 말했다. \"내 말을 들어 보게."], ["One day, in a moment of despair like yours, since it led to a similar resolution, I also wished to kill myself; one day your father, equally desperate, wished to kill himself too.", "언젠가 나도 자네 같은 절망의 순간에, 그것이 비슷한 결심으로 이어졌으니, 나 역시 스스로 목숨을 끊으려 한 적이 있네. 언젠가 자네 아버지도, 똑같이 절망한 나머지 스스로 목숨을 끊으려 했지. 만약 누군가가 자네 아버지에게, 그가 권총을 머리에 갖다 댄 바로 그 순간에, 만약 누군가가 내게, 사흘 동안 입에 대지 않은 음식을 감옥에서 밀쳐 냈을 때, 만약 누군가가 그때 우리 둘 중 어느 한 사람에게라도 '살아라, 언젠가 그대가 행복해지고 삶을 축복하는 날이 오리라!'"], ["If anyone had said to your father, at the moment he raised the pistol to his head—if anyone had told me, when in my prison I pushed back the food I had not tasted for three days—if anyone had said to either of us then, ‘Live—the day will come when you will be happy, and will bless life!’—no matter whose voice had spoken, we should have heard him with the smile of doubt, or the anguish of incredulity,—and yet how many times has your father blessed life while embracing you—how often have I myself——”", "하고 말했더라면, 그게 누구의 목소리였든 우리는 의심의 미소를 띠거나 못 믿겠다는 고뇌에 잠겨 들었을 걸세. 그런데도 자네 아버지는 자네를 끌어안으며 몇 번이나 삶을 축복했던가. 나 자신도 몇 번이나……\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아.\""], ["exclaimed Morrel, interrupting the count, “you had only lost your liberty, my father had only lost his fortune, but I have lost Valentine.”", "모렐이 백작의 말을 가로막으며 외쳤다. \"백작님은 자유를 잃으셨을 뿐이고, 제 아버지는 재산을 잃으셨을 뿐이지만, 저는 발랑틴을 잃었습니다.\""]], [["“Look at me,”", "\"나를 보게.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, with that expression which sometimes made him so eloquent and persuasive—“look at me.", "몬테크리스토가, 때때로 그를 그토록 웅변적이고 설득력 있게 만들던 그 표정으로 말했다. \"나를 보게."], ["There are no tears in my eyes, nor is there fever in my veins, yet I see you suffer—you, Maximilian, whom I love as my own son.", "내 눈에는 눈물이 없고, 내 핏줄에는 열기가 없네. 그런데도 나는 자네가 괴로워하는 것을 보고 있어. 막시밀리앙, 나는 자네를 친자식처럼 사랑한다네."], ["Well, does not this tell you that in grief, as in life, there is always something to look forward to beyond?", "자, 이것이 자네에게 말해 주지 않는가? 슬픔 속에도, 삶 속과 마찬가지로, 늘 그 너머에 기대할 무언가가 있다는 것을 말일세."], ["Now, if I entreat, if I order you to live, Morrel, it is in the conviction that one day you will thank me for having preserved your life.”", "그러니 모렐, 내가 자네에게 살아 달라고 간청하고, 또 명령하는 것은, 언젠가 자네가 자기 목숨을 지켜 준 일을 두고 내게 고마워할 거라는 확신에서 나온 것이네.\""]], [["“Oh, heavens,”", "\"오, 하늘이여.\""], ["said the young man, “oh, heavens—what are you saying, count?", "젊은이가 말했다. \"오, 하늘이여, 무슨 말씀을 하시는 겁니까, 백작님?"], ["Take care.", "조심하십시오."], ["But perhaps you have never loved!”", "어쩌면 백작님은 한 번도 사랑해 본 적이 없으신지도 모르지요!\""]], [["“Child!” replied the count.", "\"어린애 같으니!\" 백작이 대꾸했다."]], [["“I mean, as I love. You see, I have been a soldier ever since I attained manhood.", "\"제 말은, 제가 사랑하는 것처럼 말입니다. 보십시오, 저는 어른이 된 뒤로 줄곧 군인이었습니다."], ["I reached the age of twenty-nine without loving, for none of the feelings I before then experienced merit the appellation of love.", "사랑이라곤 모른 채 스물아홉 살이 되었지요. 그 전에 겪은 어떤 감정도 사랑이라는 이름을 붙일 만한 것은 못 되었으니까요."], ["Well, at twenty-nine I saw Valentine; for two years I have loved her, for two years I have seen written in her heart, as in a book, all the virtues of a daughter and wife.", "그런데 스물아홉에 발랑틴을 만났습니다. 이 년 동안 그녀를 사랑했고, 이 년 동안 그녀의 마음속에 마치 책에 적힌 글처럼 딸로서, 또 아내로서의 온갖 미덕이 새겨져 있는 것을 보았습니다."], ["Count, to possess Valentine would have been a happiness too infinite, too ecstatic, too complete, too divine for this world, since it has been denied me; but without Valentine the earth is desolate.”", "백작님, 발랑틴을 제 사람으로 갖는 것은 이 세상에 허락되기에는 너무나 무한하고, 너무나 황홀하고, 너무나 완전하고, 너무나 신성한 행복이었습니다. 그래서 제게 거부된 것이겠지요. 하지만 발랑틴이 없는 이 땅은 황량할 뿐입니다.\""]], [["“I have told you to hope,” said the count.", "\"내가 자네더러 희망을 가지라고 했네.\" 백작이 말했다."]], [["“Then have a care, I repeat, for you seek to persuade me, and if you succeed I should lose my reason, for I should hope that I could again behold Valentine.”", "\"그렇다면 조심하십시오, 다시 말씀드리지만요. 백작님은 저를 설득하려 하시는데, 만약 성공하신다면 저는 이성을 잃고 말 겁니다. 발랑틴을 다시 볼 수 있으리라는 희망을 품게 될 테니까요.\""]], [["The count smiled.", "백작이 미소 지었다."]], [["“My friend, my father,”", "\"나의 친구여, 나의 아버지 같은 분이여.\""], ["said Morrel with excitement, “have a care, I again repeat, for the power you wield over me alarms me.", "모렐이 격앙되어 말했다. \"조심하십시오, 거듭 말씀드립니다. 백작님이 제게 휘두르시는 그 힘이 저를 두렵게 합니다."], ["Weigh your words before you speak, for my eyes have already become brighter, and my heart beats strongly; be cautious, or you will make me believe in supernatural agencies.", "말씀하시기 전에 그 말을 잘 헤아려 주십시오. 벌써 제 눈이 더 빛나기 시작했고, 제 심장이 거세게 뛰고 있으니까요. 부디 신중하셔야 합니다."], ["I must obey you, though you bade me call forth the dead or walk upon the water.”", "안 그러면 저로 하여금 초자연적인 힘을 믿게 만드실 겁니다. 백작님이 죽은 자를 불러내라 하시든, 물 위를 걸으라 하시든, 저는 따를 수밖에 없을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Hope, my friend,” repeated the count.", "\"희망을 가지게, 친구여.\" 백작이 되풀이했다."]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아.\""], ["said Morrel, falling from the height of excitement to the abyss of despair—“ah, you are playing with me, like those good, or rather selfish mothers who soothe their children with honeyed words, because their screams annoy them.", "모렐이 격앙의 절정에서 절망의 나락으로 떨어지며 말했다. \"아, 백작님은 저를 가지고 노시는군요. 비명이 거슬린다고 꿀 같은 말로 아이를 달래는, 그 착한, 아니 차라리 이기적인 어머니들처럼요."], ["No, my friend, I was wrong to caution you; do not fear, I will bury my grief so deep in my heart, I will disguise it so, that you shall not even care to sympathize with me.", "아닙니다, 친구여, 제가 백작님께 경고한 것이 잘못이었습니다. 염려 마십시오, 제 슬픔을 가슴속 깊디깊은 곳에 묻어 두겠습니다. 어찌나 잘 감추는지, 백작님이 저를 가엾이 여기실 마음조차 들지 않게 할 겁니다."], ["Adieu, my friend, adieu!”", "안녕히 계십시오, 친구여, 안녕히!\""]], [["“On the contrary,”", "\"그 반대일세.\" 백작이 말했다."], ["said the count, “after this time you must live with me—you must not leave me, and in a week we shall have left France behind us.”", "\"이제부터 자네는 나와 함께 살아야 하네. 나를 떠나서는 안 돼. 일주일 뒤면 우리는 프랑스를 등지고 떠나 있을 걸세.\""]], [["“And you still bid me hope?”", "\"그러고도 여전히 제게 희망을 가지라 하십니까?\""]], [["“I tell you to hope, because I have a method of curing you.”", "\"내가 자네더러 희망을 가지라 하는 것은, 자네를 낫게 할 방법을 가지고 있기 때문일세.\""]], [["“Count, you render me sadder than before, if it be possible.", "\"백작님, 그게 가능하다면, 저를 전보다 더 슬프게 만드시는군요."], ["You think the result of this blow has been to produce an ordinary grief, and you would cure it by an ordinary remedy—change of scene.”", "백작님은 이 타격의 결과가 그저 흔한 슬픔이라 여기시고, 흔한 치료법으로, 그러니까 환경을 바꾸는 것으로 그걸 고치려 하시는 겁니다.\""], ["And Morrel dropped his head with disdainful incredulity.", "그러고는 모렐이 경멸 어린 불신에 잠겨 고개를 떨구었다."]], [["“What can I say more?”", "\"더 무슨 말을 할 수 있겠나?\""], ["asked Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 물었다."], ["“I have confidence in the remedy I propose, and only ask you to permit me to assure you of its efficacy.”", "\"나는 내가 제안하는 치료법을 믿고 있고, 다만 그것이 효험이 있다고 자네에게 장담할 수 있도록 허락해 달라고 청할 뿐일세.\""]], [["“Count, you prolong my agony.”", "\"백작님, 제 고통을 길게 끌고 계실 뿐입니다.\""]], [["“Then,”", "\"그렇다면.\""], ["said the count, “your feeble spirit will not even grant me the trial I request?", "백작이 말했다. \"자네의 그 나약한 정신은 내가 청하는 시도조차 허락하지 않겠다는 건가?"], ["Come—do you know of what the Count of Monte Cristo is capable?", "자, 몬테크리스토 백작이 무엇을 할 수 있는지 자네는 아는가? 그가 이 땅의 존재들을 자기 손아귀에 쥐고 있다는 것을?"], ["do you know that he holds terrestrial beings under his control?", "아니, 그가 거의 기적까지도 행할 수 있다는 것을 아느냔 말일세?"], ["nay, that he can almost work a miracle?", "그러니 내가 이루고자 하는 그 기적을 기다려 보게."], ["Well, wait for the miracle I hope to accomplish, or——”", "그러지 않으면……\" \"그러지 않으면요?\" 모렐이 되뇌었다."]], [["“Or?” repeated Morrel.", "\"그러지 않으면, 조심하게, 모렐."], ["“Or, take care, Morrel, lest I call you ungrateful.”", "내가 자네를 배은망덕한 자라 부르게 될지도 모르니까.\""]], [["“Have pity on me, count!”", "\"저를 가엾이 여겨 주십시오, 백작님!\""]], [["“I feel so much pity towards you, Maximilian, that—listen to me attentively—if I do not cure you in a month, to the day, to the very hour, mark my words, Morrel, I will place loaded pistols before you, and a cup of the deadliest Italian poison—a poison more sure and prompt than that which has killed Valentine.”", "\"막시밀리앙, 나는 자네를 무척이나 가엾이 여긴다네. 그러니 내 말을 주의 깊게 들어 보게. 만약 내가 한 달 안에, 그날, 바로 그 시각까지 자네를 낫게 하지 못한다면, 내 말을 똑똑히 새겨 두게, 모렐, 그때는 자네 앞에 장전된 권총과 가장 치명적인 이탈리아 독약 한 잔을 놓아 주겠네. 발랑틴을 죽인 그 독보다 더 확실하고 더 빠르게 듣는 독을 말일세.\""]], [["“Will you promise me?”", "\"제게 약속해 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“Yes; for I am a man, and have suffered like yourself, and also contemplated suicide; indeed, often since misfortune has left me I have longed for the delights of an eternal sleep.”", "\"그러겠네. 나도 사람이고, 자네처럼 고통받아 보았으며, 또 자살을 생각해 본 적도 있으니까. 사실, 불행이 나를 떠난 뒤로도 나는 영원한 잠의 환희를 몇 번이나 갈망했다네.\""]], [["“But you are sure you will promise me this?”", "\"하지만 정말로 이걸 제게 약속하시는 거지요?\""], ["said Morrel, intoxicated.", "모렐이 도취된 듯 말했다."]], [["“I not only promise, but swear it!”", "\"약속할 뿐만 아니라, 맹세하네!\""], ["said Monte Cristo extending his hand.", "몬테크리스토가 손을 내밀며 말했다."]], [["“In a month, then, on your honor, if I am not consoled, you will let me take my life into my own hands, and whatever may happen you will not call me ungrateful?”", "\"그럼 한 달 뒤에, 백작님의 명예를 걸고, 제가 위로받지 못하거든, 제 목숨을 제 손에 맡겨 주시고, 무슨 일이 있어도 저를 배은망덕한 자라 부르지 않으시는 거지요?\""]], [["“In a month, to the day, the very hour and the date is a sacred one, Maximilian.", "\"한 달 뒤, 그날, 바로 그 시각이라네. 그리고 그 날짜는 신성한 날일세, 막시밀리앙."], ["I do not know whether you remember that this is the 5th of September; it is ten years today since I saved your father’s life, who wished to die.”", "자네가 기억할는지 모르겠네만, 오늘이 9월 5일이라네. 죽기를 바라던 자네 아버지의 생명을 내가 구한 지 오늘로 꼭 십 년이 되는 날일세.\""]], [["Morrel seized the count’s hand and kissed it; the count allowed him to pay the homage he felt due to him.", "모렐은 백작의 손을 붙잡아 입을 맞추었다. 백작은 그가 자신에게 마땅하다고 여기는 그 경의를 표하도록 내버려 두었다."]], [["“In a month you will find on the table, at which we shall be then sitting, good pistols and a delicious draught; but, on the other hand, you must promise me not to attempt your life before that time.”", "\"한 달 뒤, 그때 우리가 마주 앉아 있을 그 탁자 위에서, 자네는 좋은 권총과 감미로운 약물 한 잔을 보게 될 걸세. 하지만 그 대신, 그때까지는 자네 목숨을 끊으려 들지 않겠다고 내게 약속해야 하네.\""]], [["“Oh, I also swear it!”", "\"오, 저도 그것을 맹세합니다!\""]], [["Monte Cristo drew the young man towards him, and pressed him for some time to his heart.", "몬테크리스토는 젊은이를 자기 쪽으로 끌어당겨 한동안 가슴에 꼭 끌어안았다. \"이제.\""], ["“And now,”", "그가 말했다."], ["he said, “after today, you will come and live with me; you can occupy Haydée’s apartment, and my daughter will at least be replaced by my son.”", "\"오늘부터 자네는 나에게 와서 함께 살게. 하이데의 거처를 써도 좋아. 그러면 적어도 내 딸의 빈자리를 내 아들이 채워 주는 셈이 되겠지.\""]], [["“Haydée?”", "\"하이데요?\""], ["said Morrel, “what has become of her?”", "모렐이 말했다. \"그녀는 어떻게 되었습니까?\""]], [["“She departed last night.”", "\"어젯밤에 떠났네.\""]], [["“To leave you?”", "\"백작님을 두고요?\""]], [["“To wait for me. Hold yourself ready then to join me at the Champs-Élysées, and lead me out of this house without anyone seeing my departure.”", "\"나를 기다리려고 말일세. 그러니 채비를 갖추고 있다가 샹젤리제에서 나와 합류해, 누구에게도 내가 떠나는 것을 들키지 않게 나를 이 집에서 데리고 나가 주게.\""]], [["Maximilian hung his head, and obeyed with childlike reverence.", "막시밀리앙은 고개를 숙이고, 어린아이 같은 경건한 마음으로 그 말에 따랐다."]], [["# Chapter 106. Dividing the Proceeds", "# Chapter 106. Dividing the Proceeds"]], [["The apartment on the first floor of the house in the Rue Saint-Germain-des-Prés, where Albert de Morcerf had selected a home for his mother, was let to a very mysterious person.", "생제르맹데프레 거리에 있는 집의 2층 방은 알베르 드 모르세르가 어머니를 위해 골라 둔 곳으로, 지금은 무척 수상쩍은 인물에게 세를 주고 있었다."], ["This was a man whose face the concierge himself had never seen, for in the winter his chin was buried in one of the large red handkerchiefs worn by gentlemen’s coachmen on a cold night, and in the summer he made a point of always blowing his nose just as he approached the door.", "그 사내의 얼굴은 관리인조차 한 번도 본 적이 없었다. 겨울이면 추운 밤 마부들이 두르는 커다란 붉은 손수건에 턱을 파묻었고, 여름이면 문 앞에 다다를 때마다 으레 코를 푸는 시늉을 했기 때문이다."], ["Contrary to custom, this gentleman had not been watched, for as the report ran that he was a person of high rank, and one who would allow no impertinent interference, his _incognito_ was strictly respected.", "관례와 달리 이 신사는 누구의 감시도 받지 않았다. 신분이 높은 사람이며 무례한 참견을 결코 용납하지 않는다는 소문이 돌았기에, 그의 익명은 엄격하게 존중되었다."]], [["His visits were tolerably regular, though occasionally he appeared a little before or after his time, but generally, both in summer and winter, he took possession of his apartment about four o’clock, though he never spent the night there.", "그의 방문은 제법 규칙적이었다. 더러 정해진 시각보다 조금 일찍, 또는 조금 늦게 나타나기도 했지만, 여름이든 겨울이든 대개 네 시쯤이면 방을 차지했다. 다만 그곳에서 밤을 보내는 일은 결코 없었다."], ["At half-past three in the winter the fire was lighted by the discreet servant, who had the superintendence of the little apartment, and in the summer ices were placed on the table at the same hour.", "겨울철 세 시 반이면 이 작은 방을 관리하는 신중한 하인이 불을 지폈고, 여름철 같은 시각이면 탁자 위에 얼음 음료가 놓였다."], ["At four o’clock, as we have already stated, the mysterious personage arrived.", "그리고 이미 말했듯, 네 시가 되면 그 수수께끼의 인물이 도착했다."]], [["Twenty minutes afterwards a carriage stopped at the house, a lady alighted in a black or dark blue dress, and always thickly veiled; she passed like a shadow through the lodge, and ran upstairs without a sound escaping under the touch of her light foot.", "이십 분쯤 지나면 마차 한 대가 집 앞에 멈췄고, 검은색이나 짙은 파란색 드레스를 입고 언제나 두꺼운 베일을 쓴 한 부인이 내렸다. 그녀는 그림자처럼 수위실 앞을 지나, 가벼운 발끝에서 아무 소리도 새어 나오지 않게 계단을 뛰어 올라갔다."], ["No one ever asked her where she was going.", "누구도 그녀에게 어디로 가느냐고 묻지 않았다."], ["Her face, therefore, like that of the gentleman, was perfectly unknown to the two concierges, who were perhaps unequalled throughout the capital for discretion.", "그러니 신사와 마찬가지로 그녀의 얼굴 또한 두 관리인에게는 전혀 알려지지 않았다. 어쩌면 그 신중함에 있어서는 파리 전체를 통틀어 둘도 없는 자들이었으리라."], ["We need not say she stopped at the first floor.", "그녀가 2층에서 멈췄다는 것은 굳이 말할 필요도 없다."], ["Then she tapped in a peculiar manner at a door, which after being opened to admit her was again fastened, and curiosity penetrated no farther.", "그러면 그녀는 어떤 독특한 방식으로 문을 두드렸고, 문이 열려 그녀를 들인 뒤 다시 잠겼으며, 호기심도 그 이상은 파고들지 못했다."], ["They used the same precautions in leaving as in entering the house.", "집을 나설 때도 들어올 때와 똑같이 조심했다."], ["The lady always left first, and as soon as she had stepped into her carriage, it drove away, sometimes towards the right hand, sometimes to the left; then about twenty minutes afterwards the gentleman would also leave, buried in his cravat or concealed by his handkerchief.", "언제나 부인이 먼저 나갔고, 마차에 오르기가 무섭게 마차는 떠났다. 어떤 때는 오른쪽으로, 어떤 때는 왼쪽으로. 그러고서 이십 분쯤 지나면 신사 역시 넥타이에 얼굴을 파묻거나 손수건으로 가린 채 떠나갔다."]], [["The day after Monte Cristo had called upon Danglars, the mysterious lodger entered at ten o’clock in the morning instead of four in the afternoon.", "몬테크리스토가 당글라르를 찾아간 다음 날, 그 수수께끼의 세입자는 오후 네 시가 아니라 오전 열 시에 들어섰다."], ["Almost directly afterwards, without the usual interval of time, a cab arrived, and the veiled lady ran hastily upstairs.", "거의 곧바로, 평소의 시간 간격도 없이 삯마차 한 대가 도착했고, 베일을 쓴 부인이 황급히 계단을 뛰어 올라갔다."], ["The door opened, but before it could be closed, the lady exclaimed:", "문이 열렸지만, 채 닫히기도 전에 부인이 외쳤다."]], [["“Oh, Lucien—oh, my friend!”", "\"오, 뤼시앵, 오, 내 친구!\""]], [["The concierge therefore heard for the first time that the lodger’s name was Lucien; still, as he was the very perfection of a door-keeper, he made up his mind not to tell his wife.", "관리인은 그제야 처음으로 세입자의 이름이 뤼시앵이라는 것을 알게 되었다. 하지만 그는 문지기로서 완벽한 인물이었기에, 아내에게조차 말하지 않기로 마음먹었다."]], [["“Well, what is the matter, my dear?”", "\"자, 무슨 일이오, 그대?\""], ["asked the gentleman whose name the lady’s agitation revealed; “tell me what is the matter.”", "부인의 동요로 이름이 드러난 신사가 물었다. \"무슨 일인지 말해 보시오.\""]], [["“Oh, Lucien, can I confide in you?”", "\"오, 뤼시앵, 당신에게 털어놓아도 될까요?\""]], [["“Of course, you know you can do so.", "\"물론이오, 그래도 된다는 걸 잘 알잖소."], ["But what can be the matter?", "그런데 대체 무슨 일이오?"], ["Your note of this morning has completely bewildered me.", "오늘 아침 당신 쪽지 때문에 나는 완전히 어리둥절했소."], ["This precipitation—this unusual appointment.", "이 다급함이며, 평소와 다른 약속이며."], ["Come, ease me of my anxiety, or else frighten me at once.”", "자, 어서 내 불안을 풀어 주시오. 아니면 차라리 단번에 나를 겁먹게 하든가.\""]], [["“Lucien, a great event has happened!”", "\"뤼시앵, 큰일이 났어요!\""], ["said the lady, glancing inquiringly at Lucien,—“M. Danglars left last night!”", "부인이 뤼시앵을 살피듯 바라보며 말했다. \"당글라르 씨가 어젯밤에 떠났어요!\""]], [["“Left?—M.", "\"떠나다니?"], ["Danglars left? Where has he gone?”", "당글라르 씨가 떠났다고? 어디로 갔소?\""]], [["“I do not know.”", "\"몰라요.\""]], [["“What do you mean?", "\"그게 무슨 말이오?"], ["Has he gone intending not to return?”", "돌아오지 않을 작정으로 떠났단 말이오?\""]], [["“Undoubtedly;—at ten o’clock at night his horses took him to the barrier of Charenton; there a post-chaise was waiting for him—he entered it with his valet de chambre, saying that he was going to Fontainebleau.”", "\"틀림없어요. 밤 열 시에 그이의 말들이 그이를 샤랑통 관문까지 데려갔어요. 거기엔 역마차가 한 대 기다리고 있었고요. 그이는 하인을 데리고 마차에 올라타며, 퐁텐블로로 간다고 했어요.\""]], [["“Then what did you mean——”", "\"그럼 대체 무슨 뜻으로……\""]], [["“Stay—he left a letter for me.”", "\"잠깐만요. 그이가 제게 편지를 한 통 남겼어요.\""]], [["“A letter?”", "\"편지를?\""]], [["“Yes; read it.”", "\"네, 읽어 보세요.\""]], [["And the baroness took from her pocket a letter which she gave to Debray.", "남작 부인은 주머니에서 편지를 꺼내 드브레에게 건넸다."], ["Debray paused a moment before reading, as if trying to guess its contents, or perhaps while making up his mind how to act, whatever it might contain.", "드브레는 읽기에 앞서 잠시 멈추었다. 마치 그 내용을 짐작해 보려는 듯, 어쩌면 내용이 무엇이든 어떻게 처신할지 마음을 정하려는 듯했다."], ["No doubt his ideas were arranged in a few minutes, for he began reading the letter which caused so much uneasiness in the heart of the baroness, and which ran as follows:", "그러나 생각은 단 몇 분 만에 정리된 게 틀림없었다. 그는 남작 부인의 마음에 그토록 큰 불안을 안긴 그 편지를 읽기 시작했고, 내용은 이러했다."]], [["“‘Madame and most faithful wife.’”", "\"'부인, 그리고 더없이 충실한 아내여.'\""]], [["Debray mechanically stopped and looked at the baroness, whose face became covered with blushes.", "드브레는 무심코 멈추고 남작 부인을 바라보았다. 그녀의 얼굴이 새빨갛게 물들었다."]], [["“Read,”", "\"읽으세요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["Debray continued:", "드브레가 계속했다."]], [["“‘When you receive this, you will no longer have a husband.", "\"'당신이 이 편지를 받을 때쯤이면, 당신에게는 더 이상 남편이 없을 것이오."], ["Oh, you need not be alarmed, you will only have lost him as you have lost your daughter; I mean that I shall be travelling on one of the thirty or forty roads leading out of France.", "오, 놀랄 것 없소. 당신은 딸을 잃은 것처럼 남편을 잃었을 뿐이니. 다시 말해, 나는 프랑스를 빠져나가는 서른 갈래, 마흔 갈래의 길 가운데 어느 하나를 달리고 있을 거란 뜻이오."], ["I owe you some explanations for my conduct, and as you are a woman that can perfectly understand me, I will give them. Listen, then.", "내 행동에 대해 당신에게 약간의 설명을 빚진 셈이니, 당신은 나를 완벽하게 이해할 수 있는 여인이기에 그 설명을 해 주겠소. 그러니 들어 보시오."], ["I received this morning five millions which I paid away; almost directly afterwards another demand for the same sum was presented to me; I put this creditor off till tomorrow and I intend leaving today, to escape that tomorrow, which would be rather too unpleasant for me to endure.", "나는 오늘 아침 500만 프랑을 받아 그대로 지불했소. 그러자 거의 곧바로 똑같은 액수의 또 다른 청구가 들어왔소. 나는 그 채권자를 내일까지 미뤄 두었고, 그 내일을 피하려고 오늘 떠날 작정이오."], ["You understand this, do you not, my most precious wife?", "내가 견디기엔 너무도 불쾌한 내일이 될 테니. 당신은 이해하겠지, 그렇지 않소, 더없이 소중한 아내여?"], ["I say you understand this, because you are as conversant with my affairs as I am; indeed, I think you understand them better, since I am ignorant of what has become of a considerable portion of my fortune, once very tolerable, while I am sure, madame, that you know perfectly well.", "당신이 이해하리라 말하는 까닭은, 당신이 나만큼이나 내 사정을 훤히 꿰고 있기 때문이오. 아니, 오히려 당신이 나보다 더 잘 알고 있을 거요. 나는 한때 꽤 넉넉했던 내 재산의 상당 부분이 어디로 갔는지 모르지만, 부인, 당신은 분명 완벽하게 알고 있을 테니까."], ["For women have infallible instincts; they can even explain the marvellous by an algebraic calculation they have invented; but I, who only understand my own figures, know nothing more than that one day these figures deceived me.", "여인들이란 결코 틀리는 법 없는 직감을 지녔으니 말이오. 심지어 그들은 자기들이 고안해 낸 대수 계산으로 그 경이로운 일까지 설명해 내지. 하지만 나는, 내 자신의 숫자밖에 모르는 나는, 어느 날 그 숫자들이 나를 속였다는 것 말고는 아무것도 모르오."], ["Have you admired the rapidity of my fall?", "내 몰락의 속도에 감탄했소?"], ["Have you been slightly dazzled at the sudden fusion of my ingots?", "내 금괴들이 순식간에 녹아 사라지는 데 살짝 눈이 부셨소?"], ["I confess I have seen nothing but the fire; let us hope you have found some gold among the ashes.", "고백하건대 나는 불길밖에 보지 못했소. 부디 당신은 그 잿더미 속에서 금붙이라도 좀 찾아냈기를 바라오."], ["With this consoling idea, I leave you, madame, and most prudent wife, without any conscientious reproach for abandoning you; you have friends left, and the ashes I have already mentioned, and above all the liberty I hasten to restore to you.", "이 위안이 되는 생각을 품고, 나는 당신을 떠나오, 부인, 더없이 신중한 아내여. 당신을 버린다는 양심의 가책 따위는 조금도 없이. 당신에게는 친구들이 남아 있고, 앞서 말한 그 잿더미가 있으며, 무엇보다 내가 서둘러 돌려주는 자유가 있으니."], ["And here, madame, I must add another word of explanation.", "그리고 여기서, 부인, 한마디 더 덧붙여야겠소."], ["So long as I hoped you were working for the good of our house and for the fortune of our daughter, I philosophically closed my eyes; but as you have transformed that house into a vast ruin I will not be the foundation of another man’s fortune.", "당신이 우리 집안의 이익과 우리 딸의 재산을 위해 애쓰고 있다고 믿는 동안에는, 나는 철학자처럼 눈을 감았소. 그러나 당신이 그 집안을 거대한 폐허로 만들어 버린 이상, 나는 다른 사내의 재산을 떠받치는 주춧돌은 되지 않겠소."], ["You were rich when I married you, but little respected.", "내가 당신과 결혼할 때 당신은 부유했으나 존경은 거의 받지 못했소."], ["Excuse me for speaking so very candidly, but as this is intended only for ourselves, I do not see why I should weigh my words.", "이렇게 노골적으로 말하는 걸 용서하시오. 하지만 이건 오로지 우리 둘만의 이야기이니, 굳이 말을 가려야 할 이유를 모르겠소."], ["I have augmented our fortune, and it has continued to increase during the last fifteen years, till extraordinary and unexpected catastrophes have suddenly overturned it,—without any fault of mine, I can honestly declare.", "나는 우리 재산을 불렸고, 그 재산은 지난 십오 년 동안 계속 늘어났소. 그러다 예사롭지 않고 뜻밖의 재앙들이 별안간 그것을 뒤엎어 버렸지. 정직하게 단언하건대, 내 잘못은 조금도 없소."], ["You, madame, have only sought to increase your own, and I am convinced that you have succeeded.", "부인, 당신은 오직 당신 자신의 재산을 불리는 데만 골몰했고, 나는 당신이 성공했으리라 확신하오."], ["I leave you, therefore, as I took you,—rich, but little respected. Adieu!", "그러니 나는 당신을 데려올 때와 똑같이 떠나오. 부유하나 존경은 거의 받지 못한 채로. 안녕히!"], ["I also intend from this time to work on my own account.", "나 또한 이제부터는 내 몫을 위해 일할 작정이오."], ["Accept my acknowledgments for the example you have set me, and which I intend following.", "당신이 내게 보여 준 본보기에 감사를 받아 주시오. 나도 그 본보기를 따를 생각이니."]], [["“‘Your very devoted husband,", "당신의 더없이 헌신적인 남편,"]], [["“‘Baron Danglars.’”", "당글라르 남작.'\""]], [["The baroness had watched Debray while he read this long and painful letter, and saw him, notwithstanding his self-control, change color once or twice.", "남작 부인은 드브레가 이 길고 고통스러운 편지를 읽는 동안 그를 지켜보았고, 그가 자제력을 발휘하면서도 한두 번 안색이 변하는 것을 보았다."], ["When he had ended the perusal, he folded the letter and resumed his pensive attitude.", "다 읽고 나자 드브레는 편지를 접고 다시 생각에 잠긴 자세로 돌아갔다."]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서요?\""], ["asked Madame Danglars, with an anxiety easy to be understood.", "당글라르 부인이 물었다. 그 불안은 충분히 이해할 만한 것이었다."]], [["“Well, madame?” unhesitatingly repeated Debray.", "\"그래서라니요, 부인?\" 드브레가 망설임 없이 되물었다."]], [["“With what ideas does that letter inspire you?”", "\"그 편지를 읽고 어떤 생각이 드세요?\""]], [["“Oh, it is simple enough, madame; it inspires me with the idea that M. Danglars has left suspiciously.”", "\"오, 아주 간단합니다, 부인. 당글라르 씨가 의심스럽게 떠났다는 생각이 드는군요.\""]], [["“Certainly; but is this all you have to say to me?”", "\"물론이죠. 하지만 제게 하실 말씀이 그게 다인가요?\""]], [["“I do not understand you,”", "\"무슨 말씀이신지 모르겠군요.\""], ["said Debray with freezing coldness.", "드브레가 얼음장처럼 차갑게 말했다."]], [["“He is gone!", "\"그이는 가 버렸어요!"], ["Gone, never to return!”", "가서 다시는 돌아오지 않을 거예요!\""]], [["“Oh, madame, do not think that!”", "\"오, 부인, 그렇게 생각하지 마십시오!\""]], [["“I tell you he will never return.", "\"단언컨대 그이는 절대 돌아오지 않아요."], ["I know his character; he is inflexible in any resolutions formed for his own interests.", "나는 그 사람 성격을 알아요. 자기 이익을 위해 내린 결심이라면 결코 굽히지 않는 사람이죠."], ["If he could have made any use of me, he would have taken me with him; he leaves me in Paris, as our separation will conduce to his benefit;—therefore he has gone, and I am free forever,”", "나를 조금이라도 써먹을 수 있었다면 데려갔을 거예요. 우리가 헤어지는 편이 자기에게 이득이 되니까 나를 파리에 남겨 둔 거고요. 그러니 그이는 떠났고, 나는 영원히 자유로워졌어요.\""], ["added Madame Danglars, in the same supplicating tone.", "당글라르 부인이 여전히 애원하는 어조로 덧붙였다."]], [["Debray, instead of answering, allowed her to remain in an attitude of nervous inquiry.", "드브레는 대답하는 대신, 그녀가 초조하게 답을 구하는 자세로 그대로 있게 내버려 두었다."]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서요?\""], ["she said at length, “do you not answer me?”", "마침내 그녀가 말했다. \"제게 대답을 안 하시겠어요?\""]], [["“I have but one question to ask you,—what do you intend to do?”", "\"한 가지만 여쭙겠습니다. 앞으로 어쩌실 작정입니까?\""]], [["“I was going to ask you,” replied the baroness with a beating heart.", "\"그걸 당신에게 물어보려던 참이었어요.\" 남작 부인이 가슴을 두근대며 대답했다."]], [["“Ah, then, you wish to ask advice of me?”", "\"아, 그렇다면 제 조언을 구하시겠다는 겁니까?\""]], [["“Yes; I do wish to ask your advice,”", "\"네, 당신 조언을 구하고 싶어요.\""], ["said Madame Danglars with anxious expectation.", "당글라르 부인이 애타게 기대하며 말했다."]], [["“Then if you wish to take my advice,”", "\"그럼 제 조언을 받아들이실 생각이라면,\""], ["said the young man coldly, “I would recommend you to travel.”", "청년이 차갑게 말했다. \"여행을 떠나시기를 권하겠습니다.\""]], [["“To travel!”", "\"여행이라니!\""], ["she murmured.", "그녀가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Certainly; as M.", "\"그렇습니다."], ["Danglars says, you are rich, and perfectly free.", "당글라르 씨 말마따나 부인은 부유하고 완전히 자유로우시죠."], ["In my opinion, a withdrawal from Paris is absolutely necessary after the double catastrophe of Mademoiselle Danglars’ broken contract and M.", "제 생각엔 당글라르 양의 파혼과 당글라르 씨의 실종이라는 이중의 재앙이 닥친 마당에, 파리를 떠나시는 게 절대적으로 필요합니다."], ["Danglars’ disappearance. The world will think you abandoned and poor, for the wife of a bankrupt would never be forgiven, were she to keep up an appearance of opulence.", "세상은 부인이 버림받고 가난해졌다고 여길 겁니다. 파산한 사내의 아내가 부유한 척 행세한다면 결코 용서받지 못할 테니까요."], ["You have only to remain in Paris for about a fortnight, telling the world you are abandoned, and relating the details of this desertion to your best friends, who will soon spread the report.", "부인은 그저 보름쯤 파리에 머무시면서, 세상에 버림받았노라 말하고, 가장 친한 벗들에게 이 유기의 자초지종을 들려주시면 됩니다. 그러면 그들이 곧 소문을 퍼뜨릴 겁니다."], ["Then you can quit your house, leaving your jewels and giving up your jointure, and everyone’s mouth will be filled with praises of your disinterestedness.", "그런 다음 보석을 남겨 두고 과부 산입금도 포기하면서 집을 떠나시면, 모두의 입에 부인의 무사무욕을 찬양하는 말이 가득할 겁니다."], ["They will know you are deserted, and think you also poor, for I alone know your real financial position, and am quite ready to give up my accounts as an honest partner.”", "사람들은 부인이 버림받았다는 걸 알 테고, 가난해졌다고도 여기겠지요. 부인의 진짜 재정 상태를 아는 사람은 저 하나뿐이고, 저는 정직한 동업자로서 언제든 회계를 넘겨 드릴 준비가 되어 있으니까요.\""]], [["The dread with which the pale and motionless baroness listened to this, was equalled by the calm indifference with which Debray had spoken.", "창백하게 굳은 남작 부인이 이 말을 듣던 그 두려움은, 드브레가 그 말을 내뱉던 그 차분한 무관심에 견줄 만했다."]], [["“Deserted?”", "\"버림받았다고요?\""], ["she repeated; “ah, yes, I am, indeed, deserted!", "그녀가 되뇌었다. \"아, 그래요, 정말로 나는 버림받았어요!"], ["You are right, sir, and no one can doubt my position.”", "당신 말이 옳아요. 누구도 내 처지를 의심하지 못하겠죠.\""]], [["These were the only words that this proud and violently enamoured woman could utter in response to Debray.", "이 도도하고 격렬하게 사랑에 빠진 여인이 드브레의 말에 내놓을 수 있었던 대답은 그것뿐이었다."]], [["50137m\n“But then you are rich,—very rich, indeed,”", "\"하지만 그렇다 해도 부인은 부유하십니다."], ["continued Debray, taking out some papers from his pocket-book, which he spread upon the table.", "그것도 아주 부유하시죠.\" 드브레가 수첩에서 서류 몇 장을 꺼내 탁자 위에 펼치며 말을 이었다."], ["Madame Danglars did not see them; she was engaged in stilling the beatings of her heart, and restraining the tears which were ready to gush forth.", "당글라르 부인은 그것을 보지 못했다. 그녀는 두근대는 가슴을 가라앉히고, 당장이라도 쏟아질 듯한 눈물을 억누르느라 정신이 없었다."], ["At length a sense of dignity prevailed, and if she did not entirely master her agitation, she at least succeeded in preventing the fall of a single tear.", "마침내 자존심이 이겼고, 동요를 완전히 다스리지는 못했어도, 적어도 눈물 한 방울이 떨어지는 것만은 막아 냈다."]], [["“Madame,”", "\"부인,\""], ["said Debray, “it is nearly six months since we have been associated.", "드브레가 말했다. \"우리가 동업을 시작한 지도 거의 여섯 달이 됩니다."], ["You furnished a principal of 100,000 francs.", "부인이 원금 10만 프랑을 대셨지요."], ["Our partnership began in the month of April.", "우리 동업은 4월에 시작되었습니다."], ["In May we commenced operations, and in the course of the month gained 450,000 francs. In June the profit amounted to 900,000.", "5월에 거래에 착수해 그달에 45만 프랑을 벌었습니다. 6월에는 수익이 90만 프랑에 달했고요."], ["In July we added 1,700,000 francs,—it was, you know, the month of the Spanish bonds.", "7월에는 170만 프랑을 보탰는데, 아시다시피 그게 에스파냐 공채의 달이었지요."], ["In August we lost 300,000 francs at the beginning of the month, but on the 13th we made up for it, and we now find that our accounts, reckoning from the first day of partnership up to yesterday, when I closed them, showed a capital of 2,400,000 francs, that is, 1,200,000 for each of us.", "8월에는 초에 30만 프랑을 잃었지만 13일에 만회했습니다. 그리하여 동업 첫날부터 제가 회계를 마감한 어제까지 따져 보니, 우리 자본은 240만 프랑, 다시 말해 우리 각자에게 120만 프랑씩 돌아갑니다. 그리고 부인,\""], ["Now, madame,” said Debray, delivering up his accounts in the methodical manner of a stockbroker, “there are still 80,000 francs, the interest of this money, in my hands.”", "드브레가 증권 중개인 특유의 꼼꼼한 태도로 회계를 넘겨주며 말했다. \"이 돈에서 나온 이자 8만 프랑이 아직 제 수중에 있습니다.\""]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["said the baroness, “I thought you never put the money out to interest.”", "남작 부인이 말했다. \"당신은 그 돈을 이자 놓는 데 쓰지 않는다고 했잖아요.\""]], [["“Excuse me, madame,”", "\"죄송합니다, 부인.\""], ["said Debray coldly, “I had your permission to do so, and I have made use of it.", "드브레가 차갑게 말했다. \"부인의 허락을 받았기에 그렇게 한 겁니다."], ["There are, then, 40,000 francs for your share, besides the 100,000 you furnished me to begin with, making in all 1,340,000 francs for your portion.", "그러니 부인 몫으로 4만 프랑이 있고, 처음에 대주신 10만 프랑을 더하면, 부인 몫은 전부 134만 프랑이 됩니다."], ["Now, madame, I took the precaution of drawing out your money the day before yesterday; it is not long ago, you see, and I was in continual expectation of being called on to deliver up my accounts.", "그런데 부인, 저는 그저께 부인의 돈을 미리 인출해 두는 신중함을 발휘했습니다. 보시다시피 그리 오래된 일이 아니지요. 언제 회계를 넘기라는 요청을 받을지 모른다는 생각을 줄곧 하고 있었거든요."], ["There is your money,—half in bank-notes, the other half in checks payable to bearer.", "여기 부인의 돈이 있습니다. 절반은 지폐이고, 나머지 절반은 소지인 지급 수표입니다."], ["I say _there_, for as I did not consider my house safe enough, or lawyers sufficiently discreet, and as landed property carries evidence with it, and moreover since you have no right to possess anything independent of your husband, I have kept this sum, now your whole fortune, in a chest concealed under that closet, and for greater security I myself concealed it there.", "'여기'라고 말씀드리는 까닭은, 제 집이 충분히 안전하다고도, 변호사들이 충분히 입이 무겁다고도 여기지 않았고, 부동산은 증거를 남기는 데다, 더구나 부인은 남편과 별개로 무언가를 소유할 권리가 없기에, 이제 부인의 전 재산이 된 이 금액을 저 벽장 밑에 감춰 둔 궤짝에 넣어 두었기 때문입니다. 더 안전하게 하려고 제 손으로 직접 거기 숨겨 두었지요."]], [["“Now, madame,”", "자, 부인,\""], ["continued Debray, first opening the closet, then the chest;—“now, madame, here are 800 notes of 1,000 francs each, resembling, as you see, a large book bound in iron; to this I add a certificate in the funds of 25,000 francs; then, for the odd cash, making I think about 110,000 francs, here is a check upon my banker, who, not being M.", "드브레가 먼저 벽장을 열고, 이어 궤짝을 열며 말을 이었다. \"자, 부인, 여기 1,000프랑짜리 지폐 800장이 있습니다. 보시다시피 쇠로 제본한 커다란 책 같지요."], ["Danglars, will pay you the amount, you may rest assured.”", "여기에 2만 5천 프랑짜리 공채 증서를 더하고, 나머지 잔돈, 제 생각엔 11만 프랑쯤 될 텐데, 그건 제 거래 은행 앞으로 끊은 수표가 있습니다. 당글라르 씨가 아니니, 그 은행은 틀림없이 부인께 금액을 지급할 겁니다, 안심하셔도 됩니다.\""]], [["Madame Danglars mechanically took the check, the bond, and the heap of bank-notes.", "당글라르 부인은 기계적으로 수표와 채권 증서, 그리고 지폐 다발을 받아 들었다."], ["This enormous fortune made no great appearance on the table.", "이 엄청난 재산은 탁자 위에서 그리 대단해 보이지 않았다."], ["Madame Danglars, with tearless eyes, but with her breast heaving with concealed emotion, placed the bank-notes in her bag, put the certificate and check into her pocket-book, and then, standing pale and mute, awaited one kind word of consolation.", "당글라르 부인은 눈물 없는 눈으로, 그러나 감춘 격정으로 가슴을 들썩이며, 지폐를 가방에 넣고 증서와 수표를 수첩에 끼워 넣었다. 그러고는 창백하게 입을 다문 채 서서, 따뜻한 위로의 말 한마디를 기다렸다."]], [["But she waited in vain.", "그러나 헛된 기다림이었다."]], [["“Now, madame,”", "\"자, 부인,\""], ["said Debray, “you have a splendid fortune, an income of about 60,000 livres a year, which is enormous for a woman who cannot keep an establishment here for a year, at least.", "드브레가 말했다. \"부인은 이제 훌륭한 재산을, 연간 6만 리브르가량의 수입을 가지셨습니다. 이곳에서 적어도 일 년은 살림을 꾸릴 수 없는 여인에게 이건 막대한 액수지요."], ["You will be able to indulge all your fancies; besides, should you find your income insufficient, you can, for the sake of the past, madame, make use of mine; and I am ready to offer you all I possess, on loan.”", "마음 가는 대로 무엇이든 누리실 수 있을 겁니다. 게다가 혹시라도 수입이 모자라거든, 지난날을 봐서라도, 부인, 제 것을 쓰셔도 됩니다. 제가 가진 전부를 빌려 드릴 준비가 되어 있으니까요.\""]], [["“Thank you, sir—thank you,”", "\"고마워요, 고마워요.\""], ["replied the baroness; “you forget that what you have just paid me is much more than a poor woman requires, who intends for some time, at least, to retire from the world.”", "남작 부인이 대답했다. \"당신이 방금 지불해 주신 것만 해도, 적어도 한동안은 세상에서 물러나 지내려는 가련한 여자에게 필요한 것보다 훨씬 많다는 걸 잊으셨군요.\""]], [["Debray was, for a moment, surprised, but immediately recovering himself, he bowed with an air which seemed to say, “As you please, madame.”", "드브레는 잠시 놀랐지만, 곧 마음을 추스르고는 \"원하시는 대로 하시지요, 부인.\"이라고 말하는 듯한 태도로 고개를 숙였다."]], [["Madame Danglars had until then, perhaps, hoped for something; but when she saw the careless bow of Debray, and the glance by which it was accompanied, together with his significant silence, she raised her head, and without passion or violence or even hesitation, ran downstairs, disdaining to address a last farewell to one who could thus part from her.", "당글라르 부인은 그때까지도 어쩌면 무언가를 바랐을지 모른다. 그러나 드브레의 무심한 인사와, 그에 곁들여진 눈길, 그리고 그 의미심장한 침묵을 보자, 그녀는 고개를 들었다. 그러고는 격정도 격렬함도, 망설임조차 없이, 그렇게 자기를 떠날 수 있는 사내에게 마지막 작별 인사를 건네는 것조차 멸시하며 계단을 뛰어 내려갔다."]], [["“Bah,”", "\"흥,\""], ["said Debray, when she had left, “these are fine projects!", "그녀가 떠나자 드브레가 말했다. \"참으로 그럴듯한 계획이군!"], ["She will remain at home, read novels, and speculate at cards, since she can no longer do so on the Bourse.”", "집에 틀어박혀 소설이나 읽고, 더는 증권 거래소에서 투기를 못 할 테니 카드로 투기나 하겠지.\""]], [["Then taking up his account book, he cancelled with the greatest care all the entries of the amounts he had just paid away.", "그러고는 회계 장부를 집어 들어, 방금 지불한 금액의 모든 기재 사항을 더없이 세심하게 지워 나갔다."]], [["“I have 1,060,000 francs remaining,”", "\"내게는 106만 프랑이 남았군.\""], ["he said. “What a pity Mademoiselle de Villefort is dead!", "그가 말했다. \"빌포르 양이 죽다니 안타까운 일이야."], ["She suited me in every respect, and I would have married her.”", "모든 면에서 내게 딱 맞는 여자였는데, 결혼했을 텐데 말이지.\""]], [["And he calmly waited until the twenty minutes had elapsed after Madame Danglars’ departure before he left the house.", "그리고 그는 당글라르 부인이 떠난 뒤 이십 분이 지나기를 차분히 기다린 다음에야 집을 나섰다."], ["During this time he occupied himself in making figures, with his watch by his side.", "그동안 그는 곁에 시계를 놓아두고 숫자 계산에 몰두했다."]], [["Asmodeus—that diabolical personage, who would have been created by every fertile imagination if Le Sage had not acquired the priority in his great masterpiece—would have enjoyed a singular spectacle, if he had lifted up the roof of the little house in the Rue Saint-Germain-des-Prés, while Debray was casting up his figures.", "아스모데우스—그 악마 같은 인물, 만일 르사주가 그 위대한 걸작에서 먼저 선수를 치지 않았더라면 풍부한 상상력을 지닌 누구라도 만들어 냈을 그 인물—라면, 드브레가 숫자를 계산하는 동안 생제르맹데프레 거리의 그 작은 집 지붕을 들어 올렸다면 진귀한 구경거리를 누렸으리라."]], [["Above the room in which Debray had been dividing two millions and a half with Madame Danglars was another, inhabited by persons who have played too prominent a part in the incidents we have related for their appearance not to create some interest.", "드브레가 당글라르 부인과 250만 프랑을 나누던 그 방 위층에는 또 다른 방이 있었고, 그곳에는 우리가 이야기해 온 사건들에서 너무도 두드러진 역할을 맡았던 사람들이 살고 있었으니, 그들의 등장은 얼마간 관심을 불러일으키지 않을 수 없다."]], [["Mercédès and Albert were in that room.", "메르세데스와 알베르가 그 방에 있었다."]], [["Mercédès was much changed within the last few days; not that even in her days of fortune she had ever dressed with the magnificent display which makes us no longer able to recognize a woman when she appears in a plain and simple attire; nor indeed, had she fallen into that state of depression where it is impossible to conceal the garb of misery; no, the change in Mercédès was that her eye no longer sparkled, her lips no longer smiled, and there was now a hesitation in uttering the words which formerly sprang so fluently from her ready wit.", "메르세데스는 지난 며칠 사이 크게 변해 있었다. 부유하던 시절에도 그녀가, 수수하고 소박한 차림으로 나타난 여인을 더는 알아볼 수 없게 만드는 그런 화려한 치장을 한 적은 결코 없었다. 그렇다고 비참함의 옷차림을 감출 수 없는 그런 의기소침한 상태에 빠진 것도 아니었다. 아니, 메르세데스의 변화는 그녀의 눈이 더 이상 반짝이지 않고, 입술이 더 이상 미소 짓지 않으며, 예전엔 그토록 거침없는 재치에서 술술 흘러나오던 말이 이제는 머뭇거리며 나온다는 데 있었다."]], [["It was not poverty which had broken her spirit; it was not a want of courage which rendered her poverty burdensome.", "그녀의 기개를 꺾은 것은 가난이 아니었다. 가난을 견디기 힘겹게 만든 것도 용기가 모자라서가 아니었다."], ["Mercédès, although deposed from the exalted position she had occupied, lost in the sphere she had now chosen, like a person passing from a room splendidly lighted into utter darkness, appeared like a queen, fallen from her palace to a hovel, and who, reduced to strict necessity, could neither become reconciled to the earthen vessels she was herself forced to place upon the table, nor to the humble pallet which had become her bed.", "메르세데스는 자신이 누리던 높은 자리에서 끌어내려졌고, 스스로 택한 이 영역에서 길을 잃은 채—마치 휘황하게 밝혀진 방에서 완전한 어둠 속으로 옮겨 간 사람처럼—궁전에서 오두막으로 떨어진 여왕 같았다. 빠듯한 살림에 내몰려, 손수 식탁에 올려야 하는 질그릇에도, 잠자리가 되어 버린 초라한 짚요에도 도무지 마음을 붙이지 못하는 그런 여왕 말이다."]], [["The beautiful Catalane and noble countess had lost both her proud glance and charming smile, because she saw nothing but misery around her; the walls were hung with one of the gray papers which economical landlords choose as not likely to show the dirt; the floor was uncarpeted; the furniture attracted the attention to the poor attempt at luxury; indeed, everything offended eyes accustomed to refinement and elegance.", "아름답던 카탈루냐 처녀이자 고귀한 백작 부인은 그 도도한 눈빛도, 매혹적인 미소도 잃어버렸으니, 주위를 둘러봐도 비참함밖에 보이지 않았기 때문이다. 벽에는 인색한 집주인들이 때가 잘 타지 않는다고 골라 바르는 회색 벽지 가운데 하나가 발려 있었다. 바닥에는 양탄자도 깔려 있지 않았다. 가구는 호사를 흉내 낸 그 가련한 시도로 눈길을 끌었다. 한마디로, 우아하고 세련된 것에 길든 눈에는 무엇 하나 거슬리지 않는 것이 없었다."]], [["Madame de Morcerf had lived there since leaving her house; the continual silence of the spot oppressed her; still, seeing that Albert continually watched her countenance to judge the state of her feelings, she constrained herself to assume a monotonous smile of the lips alone, which, contrasted with the sweet and beaming expression that usually shone from her eyes, seemed like “moonlight on a statue,”—yielding light without warmth.", "모르세르 부인은 집을 떠난 뒤로 그곳에서 지내 왔다. 그곳의 끊임없는 적막이 그녀를 짓눌렀다. 그래도 알베르가 자기 심정을 가늠하려 끊임없이 안색을 살핀다는 것을 알았기에, 그녀는 억지로 입술만 단조롭게 웃어 보였다. 평소 그녀의 눈에서 빛나던 다정하고 환한 표정과 대조되는 그 미소는 마치 \"조각상 위에 비친 달빛\" 같았다—온기는 없이 빛만 내어 주는."]], [["Albert, too, was ill at ease; the remains of luxury prevented him from sinking into his actual position.", "알베르 역시 마음이 편치 않았다. 호사의 흔적이 그를 실제 처지로 가라앉지 못하게 했다."], ["If he wished to go out without gloves, his hands appeared too white; if he wished to walk through the town, his boots seemed too highly polished.", "장갑 없이 나가려 하면 손이 너무 희어 보였고, 거리를 걸으려 하면 장화가 너무 번들거려 보였다."], ["Yet these two noble and intelligent creatures, united by the indissoluble ties of maternal and filial love, had succeeded in tacitly understanding one another, and economizing their stores, and Albert had been able to tell his mother without extorting a change of countenance:", "그러나 모자의 정과 자식의 정이라는 끊을 수 없는 끈으로 맺어진 이 고귀하고 영민한 두 사람은, 말없이도 서로를 이해하고 살림을 아끼는 데 성공했다. 알베르는 어머니의 안색 하나 흐트러뜨리지 않고 이렇게 말할 수 있었다."]], [["“Mother, we have no more money.” 50141m", "\"어머니, 우리에겐 더 이상 돈이 없어요.\""]], [["Mercédès had never known misery; she had often, in her youth, spoken of poverty, but between want and necessity, those synonymous words, there is a wide difference.", "메르세데스는 한 번도 비참을 겪어 본 적이 없었다. 젊은 시절 가난을 입에 올린 적은 많았지만, 결핍과 궁핍이라는, 서로 같은 뜻인 이 두 말 사이에는 커다란 차이가 있었다."]], [["Amongst the Catalans, Mercédès wished for a thousand things, but still she never really wanted any.", "카탈루냐 마을에서 메르세데스는 온갖 것을 바랐지만, 그래도 정말로 무언가가 모자랐던 적은 결코 없었다."], ["So long as the nets were good, they caught fish; and so long as they sold their fish, they were able to buy twine for new nets.", "그물만 성하면 물고기가 잡혔고, 그 물고기를 팔기만 하면 새 그물을 짤 노끈을 살 수 있었다."], ["And then, shut out from friendship, having but one affection, which could not be mixed up with her ordinary pursuits, she thought of herself—of no one but herself.", "게다가 우정에서도 차단된 채, 일상의 일과는 도무지 섞일 수 없는 단 하나의 애정만을 품고서, 그녀는 자기 자신만을—오직 자기만을 생각했다."], ["Upon the little she earned she lived as well as she could; now there were two to be supported, and nothing to live upon.", "그 적은 벌이로도 그녀는 형편껏 그런대로 잘 지냈다. 그런데 이제는 부양할 사람이 둘인데, 살아갈 거리는 아무것도 없었다."]], [["Winter approached.", "겨울이 다가오고 있었다."], ["Mercédès had no fire in that cold and naked room—she, who was accustomed to stoves which heated the house from the hall to the boudoir; she had not even one little flower—she whose apartment had been a conservatory of costly exotics.", "메르세데스는 그 춥고 헐벗은 방에 불 한 점 피우지 못했다—현관에서 안방까지 집 안을 데우는 난로에 익숙했던 그녀가. 작은 꽃 한 송이조차 없었다—그녀의 거처가 값비싼 이국 화초로 가득한 온실 같았던 그녀가."], ["But she had her son. Hitherto the excitement of fulfilling a duty had sustained them.", "그러나 그녀에게는 아들이 있었다. 지금껏 의무를 다한다는 흥분이 두 사람을 지탱해 주었다."], ["Excitement, like enthusiasm, sometimes renders us unconscious to the things of earth.", "흥분은 열정과 마찬가지로 이따금 우리를 세상일에 무감각하게 만든다."], ["But the excitement had calmed down, and they felt themselves obliged to descend from dreams to reality; after having exhausted the ideal, they found they must talk of the actual.", "그러나 그 흥분이 가라앉자, 두 사람은 꿈에서 현실로 내려와야만 한다는 것을 느꼈다. 이상을 다 소진하고 나니, 이제 실제를 이야기해야 한다는 것을 깨달았다."]], [["“Mother,”", "\"어머니,\""], ["exclaimed Albert, just as Madame Danglars was descending the stairs, “let us reckon our riches, if you please; I want capital to build my plans upon.”", "마침 당글라르 부인이 계단을 내려가던 바로 그때, 알베르가 외쳤다. \"괜찮으시다면 우리 재산을 따져 봐요. 제 계획을 세울 밑천이 필요하거든요.\""]], [["“Capital—nothing!” replied Mercédès with a mournful smile.", "\"밑천이라니, 아무것도 없잖니!\" 메르세데스가 서글픈 미소를 띠며 대답했다."]], [["“No, mother,—capital 3,000 francs.", "\"아니에요, 어머니. 밑천이 3,000프랑 있어요."], ["And I have an idea of our leading a delightful life upon this 3,000 francs.”", "그리고 이 3,000프랑으로 우리가 즐거운 삶을 꾸려 갈 묘안이 떠올랐어요.\""]], [["“Child!”", "\"얘야!\""], ["sighed Mercédès.", "메르세데스가 한숨지었다."]], [["“Alas, dear mother,”", "\"아아, 어머니,\""], ["said the young man, “I have unhappily spent too much of your money not to know the value of it.", "청년이 말했다. \"불행히도 제가 어머니 돈을 너무 많이 써 본 탓에 그 값어치를 모르지 않아요."], ["These 3,000 francs are enormous, and I intend building upon this foundation a miraculous certainty for the future.”", "이 3,000프랑은 막대한 돈이에요. 저는 이 토대 위에 앞날을 위한 기적 같은 확실함을 세울 작정이고요.\""]], [["“You say this, my dear boy; but do you think we ought to accept these 3,000 francs?” said Mercédès, coloring.", "\"네 말이 그렇긴 한데, 정말로 이 3,000프랑을 받아도 된다고 생각하니?\" 메르세데스가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."]], [["“I think so,”", "\"그럼요.\""], ["answered Albert in a firm tone.", "알베르가 단호한 어조로 대답했다."], ["“We will accept them the more readily, since we have them not here; you know they are buried in the garden of the little house in the Allées de Meilhan, at Marseilles.", "\"더더욱 기꺼이 받아야지요. 그 돈이 지금 여기 있는 것도 아니니까요. 아시다시피 그건 마르세유 메이앙 가로수길의 그 작은 집 정원에 묻혀 있잖아요."], ["With 200 francs we can reach Marseilles.”", "200프랑이면 마르세유까지 갈 수 있어요.\""]], [["“With 200 francs?—are you sure, Albert?”", "\"200프랑으로? 정말이니, 알베르?\""]], [["“Oh, as for that, I have made inquiries respecting the diligences and steamboats, and my calculations are made.", "\"오, 그 점이라면 역마차와 증기선을 다 알아봤고, 계산도 다 해 뒀어요."], ["You will take your place in the _coupé_ to Châlons.", "어머니는 샬롱까지 쿠페석에 자리를 잡으세요."], ["You see, mother, I treat you handsomely for thirty-five francs.”", "보세요, 어머니, 35프랑이면 어머니를 융숭하게 모시는 거예요.\""]], [["Albert then took a pen, and wrote:", "그러고서 알베르는 펜을 들어 이렇게 적었다."]], [["Frs. _Coupé_, thirty-five francs........ ..............", "쿠페석, 35프랑............ 35."], ["........ 35. From Châlons to Lyons you will go on by the steamboat.. 6.", "샬롱에서 리옹까지는 증기선으로... 6."], ["From Lyons to Avignon (still by steamboat).... ......... 16.", "리옹에서 아비뇽까지 (역시 증기선으로)......... 16."], ["From Avignon to Marseilles, seven francs........ ....... 7.", "아비뇽에서 마르세유까지, 7프랑....... 7."], ["Expenses on the road, about fifty francs........ ....... 50.", "도중 경비, 약 50프랑....... 50."], ["Total......... .............. ..............", "합계............"], ["............ 114 frs.", "114프랑."]], [["“Let us put down 120,”", "\"120으로 적어 두죠.\""], ["added Albert, smiling.", "알베르가 미소 지으며 덧붙였다."], ["“You see I am generous, am I not, mother?”", "\"보세요, 제가 후하죠, 안 그래요, 어머니?\""]], [["“But you, my poor child?”", "\"그런데 너는, 가엾은 내 아들아?\""]], [["“I? do you not see that I reserve eighty francs for myself?", "\"저요? 제 몫으로 80프랑을 떼어 두는 게 안 보이세요?"], ["A young man does not require luxuries; besides, I know what travelling is.”", "젊은 사내한텐 호사가 필요 없어요. 게다가 여행이 어떤 건지 저도 잘 알고요.\""]], [["“With a post-chaise and valet de chambre?”", "\"역마차와 하인을 거느리고 다니던 네가?\""]], [["“Any way, mother.”", "\"어떻게든 해 가요, 어머니.\""]], [["“Well, be it so.", "\"그래, 좋아."], ["But these 200 francs?”", "그런데 그 200프랑은?\""]], [["“Here they are, and 200 more besides.", "\"여기 있어요. 게다가 200프랑이 더 있고요."], ["See, I have sold my watch for 100 francs, and the guard and seals for 300.", "보세요, 제 시계를 100프랑에 팔았고, 줄과 인장은 300프랑에 팔았어요."], ["How fortunate that the ornaments were worth more than the watch.", "장식이 시계보다 값이 더 나갔으니 얼마나 다행이에요."], ["Still the same story of superfluities!", "여전히 사치품 이야기로군요!"], ["Now I think we are rich, since instead of the 114 francs we require for the journey we find ourselves in possession of 250.”", "자, 이제 우리는 부자가 된 셈이에요. 여행에 필요한 114프랑 대신 250프랑을 손에 쥐고 있으니까요.\""]], [["“But we owe something in this house?”", "\"하지만 이 집에 진 빚이 좀 있잖니?\""]], [["“Thirty francs; but I pay that out of my 150 francs,—that is understood,—and as I require only eighty francs for my journey, you see I am overwhelmed with luxury.", "\"30프랑이요. 하지만 그건 제 150프랑에서 갚을게요—그렇게 하기로 한 거고요—그리고 제 여행엔 80프랑밖에 안 드니, 보세요, 저는 호사에 파묻혀 있는 셈이에요."], ["But that is not all.", "그런데 그게 다가 아니에요."], ["What do you say to this, mother?”", "이건 어떻게 생각하세요, 어머니?\""]], [["And Albert took out of a little pocket-book with golden clasps, a remnant of his old fancies, or perhaps a tender souvenir from one of the mysterious and veiled ladies who used to knock at his little door,—Albert took out of this pocket-book a note of 1,000 francs.", "그러고서 알베르는 금장식 걸쇠가 달린 작은 수첩—옛 사치 취향의 잔재이거나, 어쩌면 그의 작은 문을 두드리곤 하던 그 베일 쓴 수수께끼의 부인들 가운데 누군가가 준 애틋한 추억의 물건—에서 1,000프랑짜리 지폐 한 장을 꺼냈다."]], [["“What is this?”", "\"이게 뭐니?\""], ["asked Mercédès.", "메르세데스가 물었다."]], [["“A thousand francs.”", "\"1,000프랑이요.\""]], [["“But whence have you obtained them?”", "\"그런데 그걸 어디서 났니?\""]], [["“Listen to me, mother, and do not yield too much to agitation.”", "\"제 말을 들어 보세요, 어머니. 그리고 너무 동요하지 마세요.\""], ["And Albert, rising, kissed his mother on both cheeks, then stood looking at her.", "그러고서 알베르는 일어나 어머니의 두 뺨에 입을 맞춘 뒤, 가만히 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["“You cannot imagine, mother, how beautiful I think you!” said the young man, impressed with a profound feeling of filial love.", "\"어머니, 제가 어머니를 얼마나 아름답다고 여기는지 모르실 거예요!\" 청년이 자식으로서의 깊은 사랑에 사무쳐 말했다."], ["“You are, indeed, the most beautiful and most noble woman I ever saw!”", "\"어머니는 정말이지 제가 본 가장 아름답고 가장 고귀한 여인이에요!\""]], [["“Dear child!” said Mercédès, endeavoring in vain to restrain a tear which glistened in the corner of her eye.", "\"가엾은 내 아들아!\" 메르세데스가 눈가에 반짝이는 눈물을 애써 참으며 말했다."], ["“Indeed, you only wanted misfortune to change my love for you to admiration.", "\"정말이지, 너는 불행을 겪고 나서야 너를 향한 내 사랑이 경탄으로 바뀌게 만드는구나."], ["I am not unhappy while I possess my son!”", "내 아들이 있는 한 나는 불행하지 않아!\""]], [["“Ah, just so,”", "\"아, 바로 그거예요.\""], ["said Albert; “here begins the trial.", "알베르가 말했다. \"이제부터가 시련이에요."], ["Do you know the decision we have come to, mother?”", "우리가 어떤 결정을 내렸는지 아세요, 어머니?\""]], [["“Have we come to any?”", "\"우리가 무슨 결정을 내렸던가?\""]], [["“Yes; it is decided that you are to live at Marseilles, and that I am to leave for Africa, where I will earn for myself the right to use the name I now bear, instead of the one I have thrown aside.”", "\"네. 어머니는 마르세유에서 사시고, 저는 아프리카로 떠나기로 했어요. 거기서 제가 내던진 이름 대신 지금 지닌 이름을 쓸 권리를 스스로 벌어 올 거예요.\""], ["Mercédès sighed.", "메르세데스가 한숨지었다."], ["“Well, mother, I yesterday engaged myself as substitute in the Spahis,”25 added the young man, lowering his eyes with a certain feeling of shame, for even he was unconscious of the sublimity of his self-abasement.", "\"그런데 어머니, 저는 어제 스파이 부대에 대리 입대했어요.\" 청년이 어떤 부끄러움에 눈을 내리깔며 덧붙였다. 사실 그조차 자신의 그 자기 비하가 얼마나 숭고한 것인지 깨닫지 못하고 있었다."], ["“I thought my body was my own, and that I might sell it.", "\"제 몸은 제 것이니 팔 수도 있다고 생각했어요."], ["I yesterday took the place of another.", "어제 다른 사람 대신 입대했고요."], ["I sold myself for more than I thought I was worth,”", "제가 생각한 값어치보다 더 비싸게 저를 팔았지요.\""], ["he added, attempting to smile; “I fetched 2,000 francs.”", "그가 미소를 지어 보이려 애쓰며 덧붙였다. \"2,000프랑을 받았어요.\""]], [["“Then these 1,000 francs——”", "\"그럼 이 1,000프랑은……\""], ["said Mercédès, shuddering.", "메르세데스가 몸서리치며 말했다."]], [["“Are the half of the sum, mother; the other will be paid in a year.”", "\"그 금액의 절반이에요, 어머니. 나머지는 일 년 뒤에 받고요.\""]], [["Mercédès raised her eyes to heaven with an expression it would be impossible to describe, and tears, which had hitherto been restrained, now yielded to her emotion, and ran down her cheeks.", "메르세데스는 이루 형언할 수 없는 표정으로 하늘을 향해 눈을 들었고, 지금껏 참아 온 눈물이 마침내 격정에 무너져 두 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸다."]], [["“The price of his blood!”", "\"제 피를 판 값이로구나!\""], ["she murmured.", "그녀가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Yes, if I am killed,”", "\"네, 제가 죽는다면요.\""], ["said Albert, laughing.", "알베르가 웃으며 말했다."], ["“But I assure you, mother, I have a strong intention of defending my person, and I never felt half so strong an inclination to live as I do now.”", "\"하지만 어머니, 단언컨대 저는 제 한 몸을 지키겠다는 굳은 각오가 있어요. 그리고 지금만큼 살고 싶은 마음이 간절했던 적이 없어요.\""]], [["“Merciful Heavens!”", "\"자비로우신 하늘이여!\""]], [["“Besides, mother, why should you make up your mind that I am to be killed?", "\"게다가 어머니, 어째서 제가 죽는다고 단정하세요?"], ["Has Lamoricière, that Ney of the South, been killed?", "라모리시에르, 저 남부의 네라 불리는 그 사람이 죽었던가요?"], ["Has Changarnier been killed?", "샹가르니에가 죽었나요?"], ["Has Bedeau been killed?", "베도가 죽었나요?"], ["Has Morrel, whom we know, been killed?", "우리가 아는 모렐이 죽었나요?"], ["Think of your joy, mother, when you see me return with an embroidered uniform!", "제가 수놓은 군복을 입고 돌아오는 걸 보실 때 어머니가 누리실 기쁨을 떠올려 보세요!"], ["I declare, I expect to look magnificent in it, and chose that regiment only from vanity.”", "정말이지, 그 군복을 입으면 제 모습이 근사할 거라 기대하고 있어요. 순전히 허영심에서 그 연대를 고른 거라니까요.\""]], [["Mercédès sighed while endeavoring to smile; the devoted mother felt that she ought not to allow the whole weight of the sacrifice to fall upon her son.", "메르세데스는 미소 지으려 애쓰며 한숨을 내쉬었다. 헌신적인 어머니는 그 희생의 무게를 온통 아들에게만 지워서는 안 된다고 느꼈다."]], [["“Well, now you understand, mother!”", "\"자, 이제 아시겠죠, 어머니!\""], ["continued Albert; “here are more than 4,000 francs settled on you; upon these you can live at least two years.”", "알베르가 말을 이었다. \"여기 4,000프랑 넘는 돈이 어머니 앞으로 마련돼 있어요. 이걸로 적어도 이 년은 사실 수 있고요.\""]], [["“Do you think so?” said Mercédès.", "\"정말 그럴까?\" 메르세데스가 말했다."]], [["These words were uttered in so mournful a tone that their real meaning did not escape Albert; he felt his heart beat, and taking his mother’s hand within his own he said, tenderly:", "이 말이 어찌나 서글픈 어조로 나왔던지, 그 참뜻이 알베르를 비껴가지 못했다. 그는 가슴이 두근거리는 것을 느꼈고, 어머니의 손을 자기 두 손으로 감싸 쥐며 다정하게 말했다."]], [["“Yes, you will live!”", "\"네, 어머니는 사실 거예요!\""]], [["“I shall live!—then you will not leave me, Albert?”", "\"내가 살 거라고? 그럼 너는 나를 떠나지 않을 거지, 알베르?\""]], [["“Mother, I must go,”", "\"어머니, 저는 가야 해요.\" 알베르가 단호하고 차분한 목소리로 말했다."], ["said Albert in a firm, calm voice; “you love me too well to wish me to remain useless and idle with you; besides, I have signed.” 50145m", "\"어머니는 저를 너무도 사랑하시니, 제가 쓸모없이 빈둥거리며 어머니 곁에 남기를 바라지 않으실 거예요. 게다가 저는 이미 서명까지 했는걸요.\""]], [["“You will obey your own wish and the will of Heaven!”", "\"네 뜻과 하늘의 뜻을 따르겠다는 거구나!\""]], [["“Not my own wish, mother, but reason—necessity.", "\"제 뜻이 아니라, 어머니, 이성이에요—필요고요."], ["Are we not two despairing creatures?", "우리는 절망에 빠진 두 사람 아닌가요?"], ["What is life to you?—Nothing.", "어머니에게 삶이 무엇인가요? 아무것도 아니죠."], ["What is life to me?—Very little without you, mother; for believe me, but for you I should have ceased to live on the day I doubted my father and renounced his name.", "저에게 삶이 무엇인가요? 어머니가 안 계시면 별것 아니에요. 정말이지, 어머니가 아니었다면 저는 아버지를 의심하고 그 이름을 버린 그날 살기를 그만뒀을 거예요."], ["Well, I will live, if you promise me still to hope; and if you grant me the care of your future prospects, you will redouble my strength.", "좋아요, 저는 살겠어요. 어머니가 여전히 희망을 품겠다고 약속하신다면요. 그리고 어머니의 앞날을 돌보는 일을 제게 맡겨 주신다면, 어머니는 제 힘을 갑절로 키워 주시는 거예요."], ["Then I will go to the governor of Algeria; he has a royal heart, and is essentially a soldier; I will tell him my gloomy story.", "그러면 저는 알제리 총독을 찾아가겠어요. 그분은 왕 같은 심성을 지닌, 무엇보다 진정한 군인이에요."], ["I will beg him to turn his eyes now and then towards me, and if he keep his word and interest himself for me, in six months I shall be an officer, or dead.", "그분께 제 어두운 사연을 털어놓고, 이따금 저를 살펴봐 달라고 청하겠어요. 그분이 약속을 지켜 저를 돌봐 주신다면, 여섯 달 안에 저는 장교가 되거나 죽어 있겠죠."], ["If I am an officer, your fortune is certain, for I shall have money enough for both, and, moreover, a name we shall both be proud of, since it will be our own.", "제가 장교가 되면 어머니의 살림은 확실해져요. 두 사람 몫의 돈이 충분할 테고, 게다가 우리 둘 다 자랑스러워할 이름까지 생길 테니까요. 우리 본래의 이름일 테니 말이에요."], ["If I am killed—well then mother, you can also die, and there will be an end of our misfortunes.”", "제가 죽으면—그럼 어머니, 어머니도 죽으셔도 돼요. 그러면 우리 불행도 끝이 나겠죠.\""]], [["“It is well,”", "\"좋아.\""], ["replied Mercédès, with her eloquent glance; “you are right, my love; let us prove to those who are watching our actions that we are worthy of compassion.”", "메르세데스가 그 웅변하는 듯한 눈빛으로 대답했다. \"네 말이 옳다, 내 사랑. 우리 행동을 지켜보는 자들에게 우리가 동정받을 만한 사람들임을 증명해 보이자.\""]], [["“But let us not yield to gloomy apprehensions,”", "\"하지만 음울한 걱정에 굴복하진 말아요.\""], ["said the young man; “I assure you we are, or rather we shall be, very happy.", "청년이 말했다. \"단언컨대 우리는 행복해요. 아니, 행복해질 거예요."], ["You are a woman at once full of spirit and resignation; I have become simple in my tastes, and am without passion, I hope.", "어머니는 기개와 체념을 함께 지닌 분이고, 저는 취향이 소박해졌으며, 바라건대 격정도 없어졌어요."], ["Once in service, I shall be rich—once in M. Dantès’ house, you will be at rest.", "일단 군에 들어가면 저는 부자가 될 거고, 일단 당테스 씨 댁에 들어가면 어머니는 편안히 지내실 거예요."], ["Let us strive, I beseech you,—let us strive to be cheerful.”", "우리 애써 봐요, 부탁이에요—애써서 즐겁게 지내 봐요.\""]], [["“Yes, let us strive, for you ought to live, and to be happy, Albert.”", "\"그래, 애써 보자. 너는 살아야 하고, 행복해야 하니까, 알베르.\""]], [["“And so our division is made, mother,” said the young man, affecting ease of mind.", "\"그럼 이제 우리 몫 나누기는 끝났네요, 어머니.\" 청년이 마음 편한 척하며 말했다."], ["“We can now part; come, I shall engage your passage.”", "\"이제 헤어질 수 있겠어요. 자, 제가 어머니 차표를 끊을게요.\""]], [["“And you, my dear boy?”", "\"그럼 너는, 내 아들아?\""]], [["“I shall stay here for a few days longer; we must accustom ourselves to parting.", "\"저는 며칠 더 여기 머물 거예요. 우리도 이별에 익숙해져야죠."], ["I want recommendations and some information relative to Africa.", "추천서도 받아야 하고 아프리카에 관한 정보도 좀 얻어야 하고요."], ["I will join you again at Marseilles.”", "마르세유에서 다시 어머니와 합류할게요.\""]], [["“Well, be it so—let us part,”", "\"그래, 좋아—헤어지자.\""], ["said Mercédès, folding around her shoulders the only shawl she had taken away, and which accidentally happened to be a valuable black cashmere.", "메르세데스가 챙겨 온 단 하나의 숄을 어깨에 둘렀다. 우연찮게도 그것은 값나가는 검은 캐시미어 숄이었다."], ["Albert gathered up his papers hastily, rang the bell to pay the thirty francs he owed to the landlord, and offering his arm to his mother, they descended the stairs.", "알베르는 서둘러 서류를 챙기고, 집주인에게 진 빚 30프랑을 갚으려 종을 울린 뒤, 어머니에게 팔을 내밀어 두 사람은 계단을 내려갔다."]], [["Someone was walking down before them, and this person, hearing the rustling of a silk dress, turned around.", "누군가가 그들 앞에서 계단을 내려가고 있었는데, 그 사람은 비단 드레스가 스치는 소리를 듣고 뒤를 돌아보았다."], ["“Debray!”", "\"드브레!\""], ["muttered Albert.", "알베르가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“You, Morcerf?” replied the secretary, resting on the stairs.", "\"자네, 모르세르 아닌가?\" 비서가 계단에 멈춰 서며 대답했다."], ["Curiosity had vanquished the desire of preserving his _incognito_, and he was recognized.", "호기심이 익명을 지키려는 욕구를 이긴 탓에, 그는 정체를 들키고 말았다."], ["It was, indeed, strange in this unknown spot to find the young man whose misfortunes had made so much noise in Paris.", "정말이지 이 낯선 곳에서, 그 불행이 파리를 그토록 떠들썩하게 만든 청년을 만나다니 묘한 일이었다."]], [["“Morcerf!”", "\"모르세르!\""], ["repeated Debray.", "드브레가 되뇌었다."], ["Then noticing in the dim light the still youthful and veiled figure of Madame de Morcerf:", "그러다 어슴푸레한 빛 속에서 여전히 젊고 베일을 쓴 모르세르 부인의 모습을 알아채고는 이렇게 덧붙였다."]], [["“Pardon me,”", "\"실례하네.\""], ["he added with a smile, “I leave you, Albert.”", "그가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"이만 가 보겠네, 알베르.\""], ["Albert understood his thoughts.", "알베르는 그의 속내를 알아차렸다."]], [["“Mother,”", "\"어머니,\""], ["he said, turning towards Mercédès, “this is M.", "그가 메르세데스를 향해 돌아서며 말했다."], ["Debray, secretary of the Minister for the Interior, once a friend of mine.”", "\"이분은 드브레 씨예요. 내무대신 비서이고, 한때 제 친구였지요.\""]], [["“How once?”", "\"한때라니?\""], ["stammered Debray; “what do you mean?”", "드브레가 말을 더듬었다. \"그게 무슨 뜻인가?\""]], [["“I say so, M. Debray, because I have no friends now, and I ought not to have any.", "\"그렇게 말한 겁니다, 드브레 씨. 이제 제겐 친구가 없고, 또 있어서도 안 되니까요."], ["I thank you for having recognized me, sir.”", "저를 알아봐 주셔서 감사합니다, 선생님.\""], ["Debray stepped forward, and cordially pressed the hand of his interlocutor.", "드브레가 한 걸음 앞으로 나서더니, 상대의 손을 다정하게 부여잡았다."]], [["“Believe me, dear Albert,”", "\"믿어 주게, 알베르.\""], ["he said, with all the emotion he was capable of feeling,—“believe me, I feel deeply for your misfortunes, and if in any way I can serve you, I am yours.”", "그가 자신이 느낄 수 있는 온갖 감정을 담아 말했다. \"믿어 주게, 자네의 불행에 나는 깊이 가슴 아프고, 무슨 식으로든 자네에게 도움이 될 수 있다면 나는 자네 편일세.\""]], [["“Thank you, sir,”", "\"고맙습니다, 선생님.\""], ["said Albert, smiling. “In the midst of our misfortunes, we are still rich enough not to require assistance from anyone.", "알베르가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"이런 불행의 한가운데서도, 우리는 아직 누구의 도움도 필요 없을 만큼은 넉넉합니다."], ["We are leaving Paris, and when our journey is paid, we shall have 5,000 francs left.”", "우리는 파리를 떠납니다. 여비를 치르고 나면 5,000프랑이 남을 거고요.\""]], [["The blood mounted to the temples of Debray, who held a million in his pocket-book, and unimaginative as he was he could not help reflecting that the same house had contained two women, one of whom, justly dishonored, had left it poor with 1,500,000 francs under her cloak, while the other, unjustly stricken, but sublime in her misfortune, was yet rich with a few deniers.", "수첩 속에 100만 프랑을 지닌 드브레의 관자놀이로 피가 솟구쳤다. 상상력이라곤 없는 그조차도, 같은 집에 두 여인이 있었다는 사실을 떠올리지 않을 수 없었다. 한 여인은 당연히도 명예를 잃은 채, 망토 밑에 150만 프랑을 감추고 가난한 척 떠났고, 다른 여인은 부당하게 짓밟혔으나 그 불행 속에서도 숭고하게, 몇 푼 안 되는 동전만으로도 여전히 부유했다."], ["This parallel disturbed his usual politeness, the philosophy he witnessed appalled him, he muttered a few words of general civility and ran downstairs.", "이 대비가 그의 여느 때의 정중함을 흐트러뜨렸다. 자기 눈앞에 펼쳐진 그 철학이 그를 질리게 했고, 그는 그저 그런 인사치레의 말 몇 마디를 중얼거리고는 계단을 뛰어 내려갔다."]], [["That day the minister’s clerks and the subordinates had a great deal to put up with from his ill-humor.", "그날 대신의 서기들과 부하들은 그의 언짢은 심기를 적잖이 감당해야 했다."], ["But that same night, he found himself the possessor of a fine house, situated on the Boulevard de la Madeleine, and an income of 50,000 livres.", "그러나 바로 그날 밤, 그는 마들렌 대로에 자리한 멋진 저택과 연간 5만 리브르의 수입을 거머쥔 몸이 되어 있었다."]], [["The next day, just as Debray was signing the deed, that is about five o’clock in the afternoon, Madame de Morcerf, after having affectionately embraced her son, entered the _coupé_ of the diligence, which closed upon her.", "이튿날, 드브레가 그 증서에 서명하고 있던 바로 그때, 그러니까 오후 다섯 시쯤, 모르세르 부인은 아들을 다정하게 끌어안은 뒤 역마차 쿠페석에 올랐고, 문이 그녀를 가두며 닫혔다."]], [["A man was hidden in Lafitte’s banking-house, behind one of the little arched windows which are placed above each desk; he saw Mercédès enter the diligence, and he also saw Albert withdraw.", "라피트 은행 건물 안, 책상마다 위에 나 있는 작은 아치형 창문 가운데 하나 뒤에 한 사내가 숨어 있었다. 그는 메르세데스가 역마차에 오르는 것을 보았고, 알베르가 물러가는 것도 보았다."], ["Then he passed his hand across his forehead, which was clouded with doubt.", "그러고는 의혹으로 그늘진 이마를 손으로 쓸었다."]], [["“Alas,”", "\"아아,\""], ["he exclaimed, “how can I restore the happiness I have taken away from these poor innocent creatures?", "그가 외쳤다. \"이 가엾고 죄 없는 사람들에게서 내가 앗아 간 행복을 어떻게 되돌려 줄 수 있단 말인가?"], ["God help me!”", "하느님, 저를 도우소서!\""]], [["# Chapter 107. The Lions’ Den", "# Chapter 107. The Lions’ Den"]], [["One division of La Force, in which the most dangerous and desperate prisoners are confined, is called the court of Saint-Bernard.", "* 스파이: 프랑스가 알제리에 두었던 원주민 기병 부대.—옮긴이\n라포르스 감옥에서 가장 위험하고 흉포한 죄수들을 가두는 한 구역은 생베르나르 안뜰이라 불린다."], ["The prisoners, in their expressive language, have named it the “Lions’ Den,” probably because the captives possess teeth which frequently gnaw the bars, and sometimes the keepers also.", "죄수들은 자기들 특유의 생생한 말로 이곳을 \"사자 우리\"라 이름 붙였는데, 아마도 갇힌 자들의 이빨이 곧잘 창살을 갉아 대고 때로는 간수까지 물어뜯기 때문일 것이다."], ["It is a prison within a prison; the walls are double the thickness of the rest.", "이곳은 감옥 안의 감옥이었다. 벽은 다른 곳보다 두 배나 두꺼웠다."], ["The gratings are every day carefully examined by jailers, whose herculean proportions and cold pitiless expression prove them to have been chosen to reign over their subjects for their superior activity and intelligence.", "창살은 날마다 간수들이 꼼꼼히 살폈는데, 그들의 헤라클레스 같은 체격과 차갑고 무자비한 표정은 그들이 남다른 활동력과 영민함 덕분에 그 신민들 위에 군림하도록 뽑힌 자들임을 입증했다."]], [["The courtyard of this quarter is enclosed by enormous walls, over which the sun glances obliquely, when it deigns to penetrate into this gulf of moral and physical deformity.", "이 구역의 안뜰은 거대한 벽에 둘러싸여 있었고, 도덕적이고 육체적인 추악함이 들끓는 이 심연에 햇빛이 기어이 비집고 들어올 때면 그 벽 위로 비스듬히 빛이 스쳐 갔다."], ["On this paved yard are to be seen,—pacing to and fro from morning till night, pale, careworn, and haggard, like so many shadows,—the men whom justice holds beneath the steel she is sharpening.", "이 돌바닥 안뜰에서는 아침부터 밤까지 이리저리 서성이는 사람들을 볼 수 있었다. 창백하고 근심에 찌든 초췌한 얼굴로, 마치 그림자처럼 떠도는 그들은 정의가 갈고 있는 칼날 아래 붙들린 자들이었다."], ["There, crouched against the side of the wall which attracts and retains the most heat, they may be seen sometimes talking to one another, but more frequently alone, watching the door, which sometimes opens to call forth one from the gloomy assemblage, or to throw in another outcast from society.", "그곳에서 그들은 가장 많은 열기를 끌어모아 간직하는 벽 한쪽에 웅크린 채, 때로는 서로 이야기를 나누는 모습으로, 그러나 더 흔히는 홀로 문을 지켜보는 모습으로 보였다. 그 문은 이따금 열려 이 음울한 무리에서 한 사람을 불러내거나, 사회에서 내쳐진 또 다른 자를 던져 넣곤 했다."]], [["The court of Saint-Bernard has its own particular apartment for the reception of guests; it is a long rectangle, divided by two upright gratings placed at a distance of three feet from one another to prevent a visitor from shaking hands with or passing anything to the prisoners.", "생베르나르 안뜰에는 면회객을 맞이하는 전용 공간이 따로 있었다. 길쭉한 직사각형 방으로, 방문객이 죄수와 악수를 하거나 무언가를 건네지 못하도록 90센티미터쯤 간격을 두고 두 개의 곧추선 창살로 나뉘어 있었다."], ["It is a wretched, damp, nay, even horrible spot, more especially when we consider the agonizing conferences which have taken place between those iron bars.", "음습하고 눅눅한, 아니 끔찍하기까지 한 곳이었다. 그 쇠창살 사이로 오갔던 가슴 미어지는 면담들을 떠올리면 더욱 그러했다."], ["And yet, frightful though this spot may be, it is looked upon as a kind of paradise by the men whose days are numbered; it is so rare for them to leave the Lions’ Den for any other place than the barrier Saint-Jacques, the galleys! or solitary confinement.", "그러나 이토록 끔찍한 곳임에도, 살날이 얼마 남지 않은 자들에게는 일종의 낙원처럼 여겨졌다. 그들이 사자 우리를 떠나는 곳이라곤 생자크 처형장이나 갤리선, 아니면 독방뿐이었으니, 그 밖의 어디로든 나가는 일은 그토록 드물었다."]], [["In the court which we have attempted to describe, and from which a damp vapor was rising, a young man with his hands in his pockets, who had excited much curiosity among the inhabitants of the “Den,” might be seen walking.", "우리가 묘사하려 애쓴 그 안뜰, 눅눅한 김이 피어오르는 그곳에서 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣은 한 젊은이가 거니는 모습이 보였다. 그는 \"우리\"의 거주자들 사이에서 적잖은 호기심을 불러일으킨 자였다."], ["The cut of his clothes would have made him pass for an elegant man, if those clothes had not been torn to shreds; still they did not show signs of wear, and the fine cloth, beneath the careful hands of the prisoner, soon recovered its gloss in the parts which were still perfect, for the wearer tried his best to make it assume the appearance of a new coat.", "그의 옷은 누더기로 찢기지만 않았더라면 그를 우아한 신사로 통하게 했을 만한 재단이었다. 그래도 옷은 닳은 기색이 없었고, 죄수의 정성스러운 손길 아래 고급 천은 아직 멀쩡한 부분부터 곧 윤기를 되찾았다. 입은 이가 그것을 새 옷처럼 보이게 하려고 안간힘을 썼기 때문이다."], ["He bestowed the same attention upon the cambric front of a shirt, which had considerably changed in color since his entrance into the prison, and he polished his varnished boots with the corner of a handkerchief embroidered with initials surmounted by a coronet.", "그는 감옥에 들어온 뒤로 색이 꽤 변해 버린 셔츠의 고운 삼베 가슴팍에도 똑같은 정성을 쏟았고, 작은 관(冠) 무늬 아래 머리글자가 수놓인 손수건 모서리로 칠을 한 가죽 부츠를 문질러 닦았다."]], [["Some of the inmates of the “Lions’ Den” were watching the operations of the prisoner’s toilet with considerable interest.", "\"사자 우리\"의 수감자들 가운데 몇몇이 그 죄수의 몸단장을 자못 흥미롭게 지켜보고 있었다."]], [["“See, the prince is pluming himself,”", "\"저것 봐, 왕자님께서 깃털을 다듬고 계시네.\""], ["said one of the thieves.", "도둑들 중 하나가 말했다."]], [["“He’s a fine looking fellow,”", "\"잘생긴 친구로군.\""], ["said another; “if he had only a comb and hair-grease, he’d take the shine off the gentlemen in white kids.”", "또 다른 자가 말했다. \"빗하고 머릿기름만 있으면 흰 새끼염소 가죽 장갑 낀 신사들도 무색하게 만들겠어.\""]], [["“His coat looks nearly new, and his boots are brilliant.", "\"저 옷은 거의 새것 같고, 부츠는 번쩍번쩍하네."], ["It is pleasant to have such well-dressed brethren; and those gendarmes behaved shamefully.", "저렇게 잘 차려입은 형제를 두니 흐뭇하군. 그런데 그 헌병들 행실 한번 고약하지."], ["What jealousy; to tear such clothes!”", "저런 옷을 다 찢어 놓다니, 어찌나 시샘이 많은지!\""]], [["“He looks like a big-bug,”", "\"거물처럼 보이는데.\""], ["said another; “dresses in fine style.", "또 한 사람이 말했다. \"차림새가 으리으리하잖아."], ["And, then, to be here so young!", "게다가 이 어린 나이에 여기 들어오다니!"], ["Oh, what larks!”", "야, 거참 신나는 일이군!\""]], [["Meanwhile the object of this hideous admiration approached the wicket, against which one of the keepers was leaning.", "그러는 사이 이 끔찍한 찬탄의 대상은 간수 하나가 기대어 선 쪽문으로 다가갔다."]], [["“Come, sir,”", "\"여보시오,\""], ["he said, “lend me twenty francs; you will soon be paid; you run no risks with me.", "그가 말했다. \"20프랑만 빌려 주시오. 곧 갚을 테니까."], ["Remember, I have relations who possess more millions than you have deniers.", "나한테는 떼일 염려가 없소. 잘 들으시오, 내겐 당신이 가진 드니에르●보다 더 많은 백만 프랑을 가진 친척들이 있단 말이오. 자, 제발 부탁이오, 20프랑만 빌려 주시오."], ["Come, I beseech you, lend me twenty francs, so that I may buy a dressing-gown; it is intolerable always to be in a coat and boots!", "실내복 한 벌 사게 말이오. 늘 외투에 부츠 차림으로 있으려니 견딜 수가 없소! 그것도 이런 외투를, 그것도 카발칸티 가문의 왕자가 말이오!\""], ["And what a coat, sir, for a prince of the Cavalcanti!”", "● 드니에르: 옛 프랑스의 소액 화폐 단위.—옮긴이"]], [["The keeper turned his back, and shrugged his shoulders; he did not even laugh at what would have caused anyone else to do so; he had heard so many utter the same things,—indeed, he heard nothing else.", "간수는 등을 돌리고 어깨를 으쓱했다. 다른 사람이라면 웃음을 터뜨렸을 법한 말에도 그는 웃지조차 않았다. 똑같은 말을 지껄이는 자를 하도 많이 들어 온 탓이었다. 아니, 실은 그런 말밖에 듣지 못했던 것이다."]], [["“Come,”", "\"이보시오,\""], ["said Andrea, “you are a man void of compassion; I’ll have you turned out.”", "안드레아가 말했다. \"당신은 동정심이라곤 없는 사람이군. 내 당신을 쫓겨나게 만들겠소.\""]], [["This made the keeper turn around, and he burst into a loud laugh.", "이 말에 간수가 돌아서더니 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["The prisoners then approached and formed a circle.", "그러자 죄수들이 다가와 둥글게 둘러섰다."]], [["“I tell you that with that wretched sum,”", "\"내 말해 두는데, 그 보잘것없는 돈만 있으면,\""], ["continued Andrea, “I could obtain a coat, and a room in which to receive the illustrious visitor I am daily expecting.”", "안드레아가 말을 이었다. \"외투 한 벌하고, 내가 날마다 기다리는 그 고귀한 손님을 맞이할 방 한 칸은 마련할 수 있단 말이오.\""]], [["“Of course—of course,”", "\"그렇고말고, 그렇고말고.\""], ["said the prisoners;—“anyone can see he’s a gentleman!”", "죄수들이 말했다. \"딱 봐도 신사 양반이신데!\""]], [["“Well, then, lend him the twenty francs,”", "\"좋아, 그럼 20프랑 빌려 줘.\""], ["said the keeper, leaning on the other shoulder; “surely you will not refuse a comrade!” 50151m", "간수가 반대쪽 어깨로 몸을 기대며 말했다. \"설마 동료를 거절하진 않겠지!\""]], [["“I am no comrade of these people,”", "\"난 이런 자들의 동료가 아니오.\""], ["said the young man, proudly, “you have no right to insult me thus.”", "젊은이가 거만하게 말했다. \"당신은 나를 그렇게 모욕할 권리가 없소.\""]], [["The thieves looked at one another with low murmurs, and a storm gathered over the head of the aristocratic prisoner, raised less by his own words than by the manner of the keeper.", "도둑들은 낮게 웅성거리며 서로를 쳐다보았고, 그 귀족 행세하는 죄수의 머리 위로 폭풍이 모여들었다. 그것은 그의 말 때문이라기보다 간수의 태도 때문에 일어난 것이었다."], ["The latter, sure of quelling the tempest when the waves became too violent, allowed them to rise to a certain pitch that he might be revenged on the importunate Andrea, and besides it would afford him some recreation during the long day.", "간수는 파도가 너무 거세지면 폭풍을 가라앉힐 수 있다고 자신했기에, 성가신 안드레아에게 앙갚음을 하려고 일정한 수위까지 파도가 솟구치도록 내버려 두었다. 게다가 그것은 긴 하루 동안 그에게 얼마간의 소일거리가 되어 줄 터였다."]], [["The thieves had already approached Andrea, some screaming, _“La savate—La savate!”_26 a cruel operation, which consists in cuffing a comrade who may have fallen into disgrace, not with an old shoe, but with an iron-heeled one.", "도둑들은 어느새 안드레아에게 다가들었고, 더러는 \"라 사바트! 라 사바트!\"●라고 소리쳤다. 이는 잔혹한 짓으로, 미운털이 박힌 동료를 헌 신발이 아니라 쇠굽을 댄 신발로 후려치는 것이었다."], ["Others proposed the _anguille_, another kind of recreation, in which a handkerchief is filled with sand, pebbles, and two-sous pieces, when they have them, which the wretches beat like a flail over the head and shoulders of the unhappy sufferer.", "또 더러는 \"랑기유\"●●라는 또 다른 놀음을 제안했는데, 손수건에 모래와 자갈, 그리고 가진 자라면 2수짜리 동전까지 채워 넣고는, 그 비참한 희생자의 머리와 어깨를 도리깨질하듯 두들겨 대는 것이었다. ● 라 사바트: '헌 신발'이라는 뜻.—옮긴이 ●● 랑기유: '뱀장어'라는 뜻.—옮긴이"]], [["“Let us horsewhip the fine gentleman!” said others.", "\"저 멋쟁이 양반을 채찍으로 갈겨 주자!\" 또 다른 자들이 말했다."]], [["But Andrea, turning towards them, winked his eyes, rolled his tongue around his cheeks, and smacked his lips in a manner equivalent to a hundred words among the bandits when forced to be silent.", "그러나 안드레아는 그들을 향해 돌아서서 눈을 찡긋하고, 혀를 볼 안쪽으로 굴리고, 입맛을 다셨다. 그것은 강요된 침묵 속에서 도둑들끼리 백 마디 말에 맞먹는 표시였다."], ["It was a Masonic sign Caderousse had taught him.", "카드루스가 그에게 가르쳐 준 비밀 결사의 신호였던 것이다."], ["He was immediately recognized as one of them; the handkerchief was thrown down, and the iron-heeled shoe replaced on the foot of the wretch to whom it belonged.", "그는 곧바로 같은 패거리의 일원으로 인정받았다. 손수건은 내던져졌고, 쇠굽 신발은 그 임자의 발로 되돌아갔다."]], [["Some voices were heard to say that the gentleman was right; that he intended to be civil, in his way, and that they would set the example of liberty of conscience,—and the mob retired.", "저 양반 말이 옳다는 소리가, 그가 그 나름의 방식으로 예의를 차리려 한 것이니 양심의 자유를 존중하는 본보기를 보여 주자는 소리가 여기저기서 들렸다. 그러고는 무리가 물러갔다."], ["The keeper was so stupefied at this scene that he took Andrea by the hands and began examining his person, attributing the sudden submission of the inmates of the Lions’ Den to something more substantial than mere fascination.", "간수는 이 광경에 어찌나 어안이 벙벙했던지, 안드레아의 두 손을 붙잡고 그의 몸을 뒤지기 시작했다. 사자 우리의 수감자들이 그토록 갑작스레 굴복한 것을 단순한 매력 이상의 무언가, 좀 더 실질적인 무언가 때문이라 여긴 것이다."]], [["Andrea made no resistance, although he protested against it.", "안드레아는 항의는 하면서도 저항하지는 않았다."], ["Suddenly a voice was heard at the wicket.", "그때 갑자기 쪽문에서 목소리가 들려왔다."]], [["“Benedetto!”", "\"베네데토!\""], ["exclaimed an inspector.", "한 감독관이 외쳤다."], ["The keeper relaxed his hold.", "간수가 붙잡은 손을 풀었다."]], [["“I am called,”", "\"날 부르는군.\""], ["said Andrea.", "안드레아가 말했다."]], [["“To the visitors’ room!” said the same voice.", "\"면회실로!\" 그 목소리가 말했다."]], [["“You see someone pays me a visit.", "\"보시오, 누가 날 면회 왔잖소."], ["Ah, my dear sir, you will see whether a Cavalcanti is to be treated like a common person!”", "아, 이보시오, 카발칸티를 여느 평범한 자처럼 다뤄도 되는지 어디 두고 보시지!\""]], [["And Andrea, gliding through the court like a black shadow, rushed out through the wicket, leaving his comrades, and even the keeper, lost in wonder.", "그러고는 안드레아가 시커먼 그림자처럼 안뜰을 미끄러져, 동료들은 물론 간수까지 어리둥절하게 내버려 둔 채 쪽문 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔다."], ["Certainly a call to the visitors’ room had scarcely astonished Andrea less than themselves, for the wily youth, instead of making use of his privilege of waiting to be claimed on his entry into La Force, had maintained a rigid silence.", "면회실로 부른다는 것은 사실 다른 누구 못지않게 안드레아 자신도 적잖이 놀라게 한 일이었다. 그 교활한 젊은이는 라포르스에 들어올 때 면회를 청구받을 때까지 기다릴 수 있는 권리를 행사하기는커녕, 철저히 침묵을 지켜 왔기 때문이다."]], [["50153m\n“Everything,”", "'모든 것이,'"], ["he said, “proves me to be under the protection of some powerful person,—this sudden fortune, the facility with which I have overcome all obstacles, an unexpected family and an illustrious name awarded to me, gold showered down upon me, and the most splendid alliances about to be entered into.", "그는 생각했다. '내가 어떤 막강한 인물의 비호를 받고 있음을 증명하지. 이 갑작스러운 행운, 모든 장애물을 손쉽게 넘어선 것, 난데없는 가문과 내게 주어진 빛나는 이름, 머리 위로 쏟아진 황금, 곧 맺어질 더없이 화려한 혼사들."], ["An unhappy lapse of fortune and the absence of my protector have cast me down, certainly, but not forever.", "불행한 운명의 추락과 후견인의 부재가 분명 나를 떨어뜨렸지만, 영영 그런 것은 아니야."], ["The hand which has retreated for a while will be again stretched forth to save me at the very moment when I shall think myself sinking into the abyss.", "잠시 거두어진 그 손은, 내가 막 심연으로 가라앉는다고 여길 바로 그 순간에 다시 뻗어 나와 나를 구해 줄 거야. 무엇 하러 경솔한 짓을 무릅쓰겠어?"], ["Why should I risk an imprudent step?", "그랬다간 후견인의 마음이 돌아설지도 모르지."], ["It might alienate my protector. He has two means of extricating me from this dilemma,—the one by a mysterious escape, managed through bribery; the other by buying off my judges with gold.", "그분에게는 이 곤경에서 나를 빼낼 방법이 두 가지 있어. 하나는 뇌물로 손을 써서 감쪽같이 탈옥시키는 것이고, 다른 하나는 황금으로 내 재판관들을 매수하는 거지."], ["I will say and do nothing until I am convinced that he has quite abandoned me, and then——”", "그분이 나를 완전히 저버렸다고 확신하기 전까지는, 아무 말도 아무 짓도 하지 않겠어. 그러고 나서야……'"]], [["Andrea had formed a plan which was tolerably clever.", "안드레아가 세운 계획은 제법 영리한 것이었다."], ["The unfortunate youth was intrepid in the attack, and rude in the defence.", "그 불운한 젊은이는 공격에는 대담했고 방어에는 거칠었다."], ["He had borne with the public prison, and with privations of all sorts; still, by degrees nature, or rather custom, had prevailed, and he suffered from being naked, dirty, and hungry.", "그는 공동 감옥도, 온갖 결핍도 견뎌 냈다. 그러나 차츰 본성이, 아니 차라리 습관이 고개를 들어, 그는 헐벗고 더럽고 굶주린 처지에 괴로워했다."], ["It was at this moment of discomfort that the inspector’s voice called him to the visiting-room. Andrea felt his heart leap with joy.", "바로 이 곤궁한 순간에 감독관의 목소리가 그를 면회실로 불렀던 것이다. 안드레아는 기쁨에 가슴이 뛰는 것을 느꼈다."], ["It was too soon for a visit from the examining magistrate, and too late for one from the director of the prison, or the doctor; it must, then, be the visitor he hoped for.", "예심 판사가 면회 오기에는 너무 일렀고, 감옥장이나 의사가 오기에는 너무 늦은 시각이었다. 그렇다면 그가 바라던 바로 그 손님임이 틀림없었다."], ["Behind the grating of the room into which Andrea had been led, he saw, while his eyes dilated with surprise, the dark and intelligent face of M.", "안드레아가 인도된 방의 창살 너머로, 그는 놀라움에 눈을 크게 뜨고 베르투치오 씨의 거무스름하고 영민한 얼굴을 보았다."], ["Bertuccio, who was also gazing with sad astonishment upon the iron bars, the bolted doors, and the shadow which moved behind the other grating.", "베르투치오 역시 쇠창살과 빗장 걸린 문, 그리고 맞은편 창살 뒤에서 움직이는 그림자를 슬픈 놀라움으로 바라보고 있었다."]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아.\""], ["said Andrea, deeply affected.", "안드레아가 깊이 동요하여 말했다."]], [["“Good morning, Benedetto,”", "\"잘 있었느냐, 베네데토.\""], ["said Bertuccio, with his deep, hollow voice.", "베르투치오가 깊고 공허한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“You—you?”", "\"당신이……"], ["said the young man, looking fearfully around him.", "당신이오?\" 젊은이가 겁먹은 듯 주위를 둘러보며 말했다."]], [["“Do you not recognize me, unhappy child?”", "\"날 못 알아보겠느냐, 가엾은 녀석아?\""]], [["“Silence,—be silent!”", "\"쉿, 조용히 하시오!\""], ["said Andrea, who knew the delicate sense of hearing possessed by the walls; “for Heaven’s sake, do not speak so loud!”", "안드레아가 말했다. 그는 벽이 지닌 예민한 청각을 알고 있었다. \"제발 부탁이니, 그렇게 큰 소리로 말하지 마시오!\""]], [["“You wish to speak with me alone, do you not?”", "\"너 나하고 단둘이 이야기하고 싶은 게로구나, 안 그러냐?\""], ["said Bertuccio. “Oh, yes.”", "베르투치오가 말했다."]], [["“That is well.”", "\"아, 그렇소.\""]], [["And Bertuccio, feeling in his pocket, signed to a keeper whom he saw through the window of the wicket.", "\"그렇다면 됐다.\" 그러고는 베르투치오가 주머니를 뒤지더니, 쪽문 창 너머로 보이는 간수에게 손짓을 했다."]], [["“Read?”", "\"읽어 보시오.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“What is that?”", "\"그게 뭐요?\""], ["asked Andrea.", "안드레아가 물었다."]], [["“An order to conduct you to a room, and to leave you there to talk to me.”", "\"너를 어느 방으로 데려가, 나와 이야기하도록 거기 남겨 두라는 지시서다.\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오.\""], ["cried Andrea, leaping with joy.", "안드레아가 기뻐 펄쩍 뛰며 외쳤다."], ["Then he mentally added,—“Still my unknown protector!", "그러고는 속으로 덧붙였다. '역시 내 정체 모를 후견인이로구나!"], ["I am not forgotten.", "난 잊히지 않았어."], ["They wish for secrecy, since we are to converse in a private room.", "우리가 따로 떨어진 방에서 이야기를 나누게 됐으니, 저들은 은밀함을 원하는 거야."], ["I understand, Bertuccio has been sent by my protector.”", "알겠다, 후견인이 베르투치오를 보낸 거로군.'"]], [["The keeper spoke for a moment with an official, then opened the iron gates and conducted Andrea to a room on the first floor.", "간수는 잠시 어느 직원과 이야기를 나누더니, 쇠문을 열고 안드레아를 이층 방으로 데려갔다."], ["The room was whitewashed, as is the custom in prisons, but it looked quite brilliant to a prisoner, though a stove, a bed, a chair, and a table formed the whole of its sumptuous furniture.", "방은 감옥의 관례대로 흰 회칠이 되어 있었지만, 죄수의 눈에는 자못 으리으리해 보였다. 비록 난로 하나, 침대 하나, 의자 하나, 탁자 하나가 그 호사스러운 세간의 전부였지만 말이다."], ["Bertuccio sat down upon the chair, Andrea threw himself upon the bed; the keeper retired.", "베르투치오는 의자에 앉았고, 안드레아는 침대에 몸을 던졌다. 간수가 물러갔다."]], [["“Now,”", "\"자,\""], ["said the steward, “what have you to tell me?”", "집사가 말했다. \"내게 할 말이 무엇이냐?\""]], [["“And you?”", "\"그쪽은?\""], ["said Andrea.", "안드레아가 말했다."]], [["“You speak first.”", "\"네가 먼저 말해라.\""]], [["“Oh, no. You must have much to tell me, since you have come to seek me.”", "\"오, 싫소. 날 찾아온 걸 보면 당신이야말로 내게 할 말이 많을 텐데.\""]], [["50155m\n“Well, be it so.", "\"좋다, 그러지."], ["You have continued your course of villany; you have robbed—you have assassinated.”", "너는 악행을 계속해 왔구나. 도둑질을 하고, 사람을 죽였어.\""]], [["“Well, I should say!", "\"흥, 그게 다요!"], ["If you had me taken to a private room only to tell me this, you might have saved yourself the trouble. I know all these things.", "고작 그 말을 하려고 날 따로 방까지 부른 거라면, 수고를 더실 뻔했소. 그런 건 나도 다 아는 일이니까."], ["But there are some with which, on the contrary, I am not acquainted.", "하지만 도리어 내가 모르는 것들이 있단 말이오."], ["Let us talk of those, if you please. Who sent you?”", "괜찮다면 그 이야기를 합시다. 누가 당신을 보냈소?\""]], [["“Come, come, you are going on quickly, M. Benedetto!”", "\"이런, 이런, 성급하게 구는군, 베네데토 씨!\""]], [["“Yes, and to the point. Let us dispense with useless words.", "\"그렇소, 단도직입적으로 갑시다. 쓸데없는 말은 집어치우고."], ["Who sends you?”", "누가 당신을 보냈소?\""]], [["“No one.”", "\"아무도 없다.\""]], [["“How did you know I was in prison?”", "\"내가 감옥에 있는 줄은 어떻게 알았소?\""]], [["“I recognized you, some time since, as the insolent dandy who so gracefully mounted his horse in the Champs-Élysées.”", "\"얼마 전에 너를 알아봤지. 샹젤리제에서 그토록 우아하게 말에 올라타던 그 건방진 멋쟁이를 말이다.\""]], [["“Oh, the Champs-Élysées?", "\"오, 샹젤리제? 아, 그렇지."], ["Ah, yes; we burn, as they say at the game of pincette.", "핀셋 놀이에서 하는 말마따나 '뜨거워지고 있군.'"], ["The Champs-Élysées?", "샹젤리제라?"], ["Come, let us talk a little about my father.”", "자, 그럼 내 아버지 이야기나 좀 합시다.\""]], [["“Who, then, am I?”", "\"그렇다면 나는 누구냐?\""]], [["“You, sir?—you are my adopted father. But it was not you, I presume, who placed at my disposal 100,000 francs, which I spent in four or five months; it was not you who manufactured an Italian gentleman for my father; it was not you who introduced me into the world, and had me invited to a certain dinner at Auteuil, which I fancy I am eating at this moment, in company with the most distinguished people in Paris—amongst the rest with a certain procureur, whose acquaintance I did very wrong not to cultivate, for he would have been very useful to me just now;—it was not you, in fact, who bailed me for one or two millions, when the fatal discovery of my little secret took place. Come, speak, my worthy Corsican, speak!”", "\"당신 말이오? 당신은 나의 양아버지지. 하지만 4, 5개월 만에 다 써 버린 10만 프랑을 내 손에 쥐여 준 건 당신이 아니었겠지. 내 아버지랍시고 이탈리아 신사 하나를 빚어낸 것도 당신이 아니었고. 나를 사교계에 들여보내, 오퇴유의 어느 만찬에 초대받게 한 것도 당신이 아니었소. 그 만찬을 나는 지금 이 순간에도 곱씹고 있는 것만 같소. 파리에서 가장 내로라하는 사람들과 한자리에서 말이오. 그중에는 어느 검사도 있었는데, 그자와 가깝게 지내지 못한 건 내 큰 실수였소. 지금 같아선 그자가 무척 쓸모 있었을 테니까. 결국 내 작은 비밀이 그 끔찍하게 들통났을 때, 1, 2백만 프랑으로 내 보석금을 대 준 것도 당신이 아니었소. 자, 말해 보시오, 훌륭하신 코르시카 양반, 어서 말해 보란 말이오!\""]], [["“What do you wish me to say?”", "\"내가 무슨 말을 하기를 바라느냐?\""]], [["“I will help you.", "\"내 거들어 드리지."], ["You were speaking of the Champs-Élysées just now, worthy foster-father.”", "조금 전에 당신이 샹젤리제 이야기를 했잖소, 훌륭하신 양아버님.\""]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서?\""]], [["“Well, in the Champs-Élysées there resides a very rich gentleman.”", "\"그래서, 샹젤리제에는 아주 부유한 신사 한 분이 살고 계시지.\""]], [["“At whose house you robbed and murdered, did you not?”", "\"그자의 집에서 네가 도둑질을 하고 사람을 죽였지, 안 그러냐?\""]], [["“I believe I did.”", "\"그런 것 같소.\""]], [["“The Count of Monte Cristo?”", "\"몬테크리스토 백작이냐?\""]], [["“’Tis you who have named him, as M. Racine says.", "\"라신● 선생 말마따나, 그 이름을 댄 건 당신이오."], ["Well, am I to rush into his arms, and strain him to my heart, crying, ‘My father, my father!’ like Monsieur Pixérécourt.”27", "그럼 어디 내가 그분의 품으로 달려들어 가슴에 와락 끌어안고는, 픽세레쿠르●● 씨의 작품에서처럼 '아버지, 아버지!' 하고 외쳐야 한단 말이오?\" ● 라신: 17세기 프랑스의 비극 작가.—옮긴이 ●● 픽세레쿠르: 통속극으로 유명했던 19세기 프랑스 극작가.—옮긴이"]], [["“Do not let us jest,”", "\"농담은 그만두자.\""], ["gravely replied Bertuccio, “and dare not to utter that name again as you have pronounced it.”", "베르투치오가 엄숙하게 대답했다. \"그리고 그 이름을 두 번 다시 네가 방금 내뱉은 식으로 입에 담지 마라.\""]], [["“Bah,”", "\"흥,\""], ["said Andrea, a little overcome, by the solemnity of Bertuccio’s manner, “why not?”", "안드레아가 베르투치오의 엄숙한 태도에 조금 압도되어 말했다. \"왜 안 된다는 거요?\""]], [["“Because the person who bears it is too highly favored by Heaven to be the father of such a wretch as you.”", "\"그 이름을 지닌 분은 너 같은 비열한 자의 아버지가 되기에는 하늘의 은총을 너무도 많이 받은 분이기 때문이다.\""]], [["“Oh, these are fine words.”", "\"오, 그것참 듣기 좋은 말이군.\""]], [["“And there will be fine doings, if you do not take care.”", "\"네가 조심하지 않으면, 듣기 좋지 않은 일이 벌어질 게다.\""]], [["“Menaces—I do not fear them.", "\"협박이라면, 난 두렵지 않소."], ["I will say——”", "내 말하겠는데——\""]], [["“Do you think you are engaged with a pygmy like yourself?”", "\"네가 너 따위 난쟁이를 상대하고 있는 줄 아느냐?\""], ["said Bertuccio, in so calm a tone, and with so steadfast a look, that Andrea was moved to the very soul.", "베르투치오가 어찌나 차분한 어조로, 어찌나 흔들림 없는 눈빛으로 말했던지, 안드레아는 영혼 깊은 곳까지 흔들렸다."], ["“Do you think you have to do with galley-slaves, or novices in the world?", "\"네가 갤리선 죄수들이나 세상 물정 모르는 풋내기들을 상대하고 있는 줄 알아?"], ["Benedetto, you are fallen into terrible hands; they are ready to open for you—make use of them.", "베네데토, 너는 무시무시한 손아귀에 떨어진 게다. 그 손은 너를 위해 기꺼이 열릴 채비가 되어 있으니, 그것을 잘 이용해라."], ["Do not play with the thunderbolt they have laid aside for a moment, but which they can take up again instantly, if you attempt to intercept their movements.” 50157m", "그들이 잠시 내려놓은 벼락을 가지고 장난치지 마라. 네가 그들의 움직임을 막으려 든다면, 그들은 그것을 당장에라도 다시 집어 들 수 있으니까.\""]], [["“My father—I will know who my father is,”", "\"내 아버지가 누구인지, 난 기어이 알아낼 거요.\""], ["said the obstinate youth; “I will perish if I must, but I _will_ know it.", "고집 센 젊은이가 말했다. \"죽는 한이 있어도, 기필코 알아내고 말겠소."], ["What does scandal signify to me?", "추문 따위가 나하고 무슨 상관이오?"], ["What possessions, what reputation, what ‘pull,’ as Beauchamp says,—have I?", "보세요● 말마따나 내게 무슨 재산이, 무슨 명성이, 무슨 '끗발'이 있단 말이오?"], ["You great people always lose something by scandal, notwithstanding your millions.", "당신들 같은 거물들은 백만금을 가졌어도 추문이 나면 늘 무언가를 잃지. 자, 내 아버지가 누구요?\""], ["Come, who is my father?”", "● 보세: 신문 편집인으로 등장하는 인물.—옮긴이"]], [["“I came to tell you.”", "\"그걸 말해 주러 온 게다.\""]], [["“Ah,” cried Benedetto, his eyes sparkling with joy.", "\"아.\" 베네데토가 기쁨으로 눈을 반짝이며 외쳤다."], ["Just then the door opened, and the jailer, addressing himself to Bertuccio, said:", "바로 그때 문이 열리고, 간수가 베르투치오에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“Excuse me, sir, but the examining magistrate is waiting for the prisoner.”", "\"실례합니다, 선생님. 하지만 예심 판사께서 죄수를 기다리고 계십니다.\""]], [["“And so closes our interview,”", "\"그래서 우리 면담은 이렇게 끝이로군.\""], ["said Andrea to the worthy steward; “I wish the troublesome fellow were at the devil!”", "안드레아가 그 훌륭한 집사에게 말했다. \"저 성가신 작자, 악마한테나 끌려갔으면 좋겠소!\""]], [["“I will return tomorrow,” said Bertuccio.", "\"내일 다시 오마.\" 베르투치오가 말했다."]], [["“Good!", "\"좋소!"], ["Gendarmes, I am at your service.", "헌병 양반들, 어서 가십시다."], ["Ah, sir, do leave a few crowns for me at the gate that I may have some things I am in need of!”", "아, 이보시오, 문간에 내 앞으로 몇 크라운만 남겨 두시오. 필요한 물건 좀 사게 말이오!\""]], [["“It shall be done,”", "\"그리해 주마.\""], ["replied Bertuccio.", "베르투치오가 대답했다."]], [["Andrea extended his hand; Bertuccio kept his own in his pocket, and merely jingled a few pieces of money.", "안드레아가 손을 내밀었다. 베르투치오는 자기 손을 주머니에 그대로 둔 채, 그저 동전 몇 닢을 짤랑거릴 뿐이었다."]], [["“That’s what I mean,”", "\"내가 바란 게 바로 그거요.\""], ["said Andrea, endeavoring to smile, quite overcome by the strange tranquillity of Bertuccio.", "안드레아가 베르투치오의 야릇한 평온함에 완전히 압도당한 채 억지로 미소를 지으려 애쓰며 말했다."]], [["“Can I be deceived?”", "\"내가 속고 있는 건 아닐까?\""], ["he murmured, as he stepped into the oblong and grated vehicle which they call “the salad basket.”", "그는 사람들이 \"샐러드 바구니\"●라 부르는 그 길쭉하고 창살 박힌 호송차에 올라타며 중얼거렸다. ● 샐러드 바구니: 죄수 호송차를 일컫는 속어.—옮긴이"]], [["“Never mind, we shall see!", "\"상관없어, 어디 두고 보자고!"], ["Tomorrow, then!”", "그럼, 내일!\""], ["he added, turning towards Bertuccio.", "그가 베르투치오 쪽으로 돌아서며 덧붙였다."]], [["“Tomorrow!”", "\"내일!\""], ["replied the steward.", "집사가 대답했다."]], [["# Chapter 108. The Judge", "# Chapter 108. The Judge"]], [["We remember that the Abbé Busoni remained alone with Noirtier in the chamber of death, and that the old man and the priest were the sole guardians of the young girl’s body.", "우리는 부소니 신부가 죽음의 방에서 누아르티에와 단둘이 남았던 것을, 그리고 그 노인과 사제만이 젊은 처녀의 시신을 지키고 있었던 것을 기억한다."], ["Perhaps it was the Christian exhortations of the abbé, perhaps his kind charity, perhaps his persuasive words, which had restored the courage of Noirtier, for ever since he had conversed with the priest his violent despair had yielded to a calm resignation which surprised all who knew his excessive affection for Valentine.", "신부의 기독교적 권면이었는지, 그의 다정한 자비였는지, 아니면 설득력 있는 말이었는지, 어쨌든 무언가가 누아르티에의 용기를 되살려 놓았다. 사제와 이야기를 나눈 뒤로 그의 격렬한 절망은 차분한 체념으로 바뀌었고, 발랑틴을 향한 그의 지나친 애정을 아는 사람이라면 누구나 그 변화에 놀랐다."]], [["M. de Villefort had not seen his father since the morning of the death.", "빌포르 씨는 발랑틴이 죽은 날 아침 이후로 아버지를 보지 못했다."], ["The whole establishment had been changed; another valet was engaged for himself, a new servant for Noirtier, two women had entered Madame de Villefort’s service,—in fact, everywhere, to the concierge and coachmen, new faces were presented to the different masters of the house, thus widening the division which had always existed between the members of the same family.", "집안의 모든 것이 바뀌어 있었다. 그 자신을 위해 새로운 하인이 들어왔고, 누아르티에를 위해서도 새 하인이 들어왔으며, 빌포르 부인의 시중을 들 여자 둘이 새로 들어왔다. 사실상 어디나, 문지기에서 마부에 이르기까지, 새로운 얼굴들이 집안의 여러 주인들 앞에 나타났고, 그리하여 같은 가족 구성원들 사이에 늘 있어 온 분열이 한층 깊어졌다."], ["The assizes, also, were about to begin, and Villefort, shut up in his room, exerted himself with feverish anxiety in drawing up the case against the murderer of Caderousse.", "게다가 중죄 재판이 곧 시작될 참이었고, 빌포르는 방에 틀어박혀 카드루스를 살해한 자에 대한 기소를 준비하느라 열병에 걸린 듯한 불안 속에서 진력하고 있었다."], ["This affair, like all those in which the Count of Monte Cristo had interfered, caused a great sensation in Paris.", "몬테크리스토 백작이 관여한 다른 모든 일이 그러했듯, 이 사건 역시 파리에서 커다란 반향을 일으켰다."], ["The proofs were certainly not convincing, since they rested upon a few words written by an escaped galley-slave on his death-bed, and who might have been actuated by hatred or revenge in accusing his companion.", "증거는 확실히 결정적이지 못했다. 죽음을 앞둔 탈주한 갤리선 죄수가 임종의 자리에서 적어 놓은 몇 마디 말에 근거한 것이었고, 그 죄수는 증오나 복수심에 사로잡혀 자기 동료를 고발했을 수도 있었기 때문이다."], ["But the mind of the procureur was made up; he felt assured that Benedetto was guilty, and he hoped by his skill in conducting this aggravated case to flatter his self-love, which was about the only vulnerable point left in his frozen heart.", "그러나 검사장의 마음은 이미 굳어져 있었다. 그는 베네데토가 유죄라고 확신했고, 이 무거운 사건을 능숙하게 이끌어 자신의 자존심을 만족시키기를 바랐다. 그 자존심이야말로 그의 얼어붙은 가슴에 남은 유일한 약점이었다."]], [["The case was therefore prepared owing to the incessant labor of Villefort, who wished it to be the first on the list in the coming assizes.", "그리하여 사건은 빌포르의 쉼 없는 노력 덕분에 준비되었고, 그는 그것이 다가오는 재판의 첫 번째 안건이 되기를 바랐다."], ["He had been obliged to seclude himself more than ever, to evade the enormous number of applications presented to him for the purpose of obtaining tickets of admission to the court on the day of trial.", "그는 어느 때보다도 더 깊이 틀어박혀야 했는데, 재판 당일 법정 방청권을 얻으려고 그에게 밀려드는 엄청난 수의 청탁을 피하기 위해서였다."], ["And then so short a time had elapsed since the death of poor Valentine, and the gloom which overshadowed the house was so recent, that no one wondered to see the father so absorbed in his professional duties, which were the only means he had of dissipating his grief.", "게다가 가엾은 발랑틴이 죽은 지 얼마 되지 않았고, 집안을 뒤덮은 어둠도 너무나 최근의 일이었으므로, 아버지가 직무에 그토록 몰두하는 모습을 보고도 이상하게 여기는 이는 아무도 없었다. 그 직무야말로 그가 슬픔을 떨쳐 낼 수 있는 유일한 수단이었으니까."]], [["Once only had Villefort seen his father; it was the day after that upon which Bertuccio had paid his second visit to Benedetto, when the latter was to learn his father’s name.", "빌포르는 단 한 번 아버지를 보았다. 그것은 베르투치오가 베네데토를 두 번째로 찾아간, 그러니까 베네데토가 제 아버지의 이름을 알게 될 예정이었던 그날의 다음 날이었다."], ["The magistrate, harassed and fatigued, had descended to the garden of his house, and in a gloomy mood, similar to that in which Tarquin lopped off the tallest poppies, he began knocking off with his cane the long and dying branches of the rose-trees, which, placed along the avenue, seemed like the spectres of the brilliant flowers which had bloomed in the past season.", "시달리고 지친 그 법관은 자기 집 정원으로 내려가, 타르퀴니우스가 가장 키 큰 양귀비 꽃대를 쳐 내던 것과 비슷한 음울한 기분으로, 지팡이를 휘둘러 장미나무의 길고 시들어 가는 가지들을 쳐 내기 시작했다. 산책로를 따라 늘어선 그 가지들은 지난 계절에 화려하게 피었던 꽃들의 망령처럼 보였다."]], [["More than once he had reached that part of the garden where the famous boarded gate stood overlooking the deserted enclosure, always returning by the same path, to begin his walk again, at the same pace and with the same gesture, when he accidentally turned his eyes towards the house, whence he heard the noisy play of his son, who had returned from school to spend the Sunday and Monday with his mother.", "그는 그 유명한 판자 문이 황량한 빈터를 내려다보며 서 있는 정원의 그 부분에 여러 번 다다랐다가, 늘 같은 길로 되돌아와 같은 걸음걸이와 같은 몸짓으로 산책을 다시 시작하곤 했다. 그러던 중 그는 우연히 집 쪽으로 눈을 돌렸고, 거기서 아들이 떠들썩하게 노는 소리를 들었다. 아이는 학교에서 돌아와 일요일과 월요일을 어머니와 보내고 있었다."]], [["While doing so, he observed M. Noirtier at one of the open windows, where the old man had been placed that he might enjoy the last rays of the sun which yet yielded some heat, and was now shining upon the dying flowers and red leaves of the creeper which twined around the balcony.", "그렇게 하던 중 그는 누아르티에 씨가 열린 창문 하나에 자리하고 있는 것을 보았다. 노인은 아직 얼마쯤 온기를 머금은 마지막 햇살을 쬘 수 있도록 거기에 옮겨져 있었고, 그 햇살은 발코니를 휘감은 덩굴의 시들어 가는 꽃과 붉은 잎사귀를 비추고 있었다."]], [["The eye of the old man was riveted upon a spot which Villefort could scarcely distinguish.", "노인의 눈은 빌포르가 거의 분간할 수 없는 한 지점에 못 박혀 있었다."], ["His glance was so full of hate, of ferocity, and savage impatience, that Villefort turned out of the path he had been pursuing, to see upon what person this dark look was directed.", "그 시선은 증오와 사나움, 야만적인 초조함으로 가득 차 있어서, 빌포르는 그 어두운 눈길이 어떤 사람을 향하고 있는지 보려고 걷던 길에서 벗어났다."]], [["Then he saw beneath a thick clump of linden-trees, which were nearly divested of foliage, Madame de Villefort sitting with a book in her hand, the perusal of which she frequently interrupted to smile upon her son, or to throw back his elastic ball, which he obstinately threw from the drawing-room into the garden.", "그러자 그는 잎이 거의 다 떨어진 보리수나무의 무성한 덤불 아래에서 빌포르 부인이 책을 손에 들고 앉아 있는 것을 보았다. 그녀는 책 읽기를 자주 멈추고는 아들에게 미소를 짓거나, 응접실에서 정원으로 자꾸만 던지는 아이의 탄력 있는 공을 도로 던져 주곤 했다."]], [["Villefort became pale; he understood the old man’s meaning.", "빌포르는 새파랗게 질렸다. 그는 노인이 무엇을 뜻하는지 알아차렸다."]], [["Noirtier continued to look at the same object, but suddenly his glance was transferred from the wife to the husband, and Villefort himself had to submit to the searching investigation of eyes, which, while changing their direction and even their language, had lost none of their menacing expression.", "누아르티에는 계속 같은 대상을 바라보았으나, 그의 시선은 갑자기 아내에게서 남편에게로 옮겨졌고, 빌포르 자신도 그 눈의 꿰뚫는 듯한 추궁을 견뎌 내야 했다. 그 눈은 방향을, 심지어 그 언어까지 바꾸면서도 위협하는 표정만은 조금도 잃지 않았다."], ["Madame de Villefort, unconscious of the passions that exhausted their fire over her head, at that moment held her son’s ball, and was making signs to him to reclaim it with a kiss.", "빌포르 부인은 제 머리 위에서 그렇게 불길이 다 타오르고 있는 그 격정을 알지 못한 채, 그 순간 아들의 공을 손에 쥐고는 입맞춤으로 공을 되찾으라고 아이에게 손짓하고 있었다."], ["Edward begged for a long while, the maternal kiss probably not offering sufficient recompense for the trouble he must take to obtain it; however at length he decided, leaped out of the window into a cluster of heliotropes and daisies, and ran to his mother, his forehead streaming with perspiration.", "에두아르는 한참을 졸랐는데, 아마도 어머니의 입맞춤이 공을 얻으려고 들여야 하는 수고에 비해 충분한 보상이 되지 못했기 때문이리라. 그래도 마침내 아이는 마음을 정하고, 창문에서 헬리오트로프와 데이지 덤불 속으로 뛰어내려, 이마에 땀을 줄줄 흘리며 어머니에게 달려갔다."], ["Madame de Villefort wiped his forehead, pressed her lips upon it, and sent him back with the ball in one hand and some bonbons in the other.", "빌포르 부인은 아이의 이마를 닦아 주고 거기에 입을 맞춘 다음, 한 손에는 공을, 다른 손에는 봉봉 몇 알을 들려 도로 돌려보냈다."]], [["Villefort, drawn by an irresistible attraction, like that of the bird to the serpent, walked towards the house.", "빌포르는 뱀에게 이끌리는 새처럼 저항할 수 없는 힘에 끌려 집 쪽으로 걸어갔다."], ["As he approached it, Noirtier’s gaze followed him, and his eyes appeared of such a fiery brightness that Villefort felt them pierce to the depths of his heart.", "그가 다가가자 누아르티에의 눈길이 그를 좇았고, 그 눈은 어찌나 이글거리는 광채를 띠었는지 빌포르는 그것이 자기 가슴 깊은 곳까지 꿰뚫는 것을 느꼈다."], ["In that earnest look might be read a deep reproach, as well as a terrible menace.", "그 진지한 눈길 속에서는 깊은 질책과 함께 무시무시한 위협을 읽을 수 있었다."], ["Then Noirtier raised his eyes to heaven, as though to remind his son of a forgotten oath.", "그러더니 누아르티에는 잊힌 맹세를 아들에게 일깨우려는 듯 하늘로 눈을 들었다."]], [["“It is well, sir,”", "\"알겠습니다, 아버지.\" 빌포르가 아래에서 대답했다."], ["replied Villefort from below,—“it is well; have patience but one day longer; what I have said I will do.”", "\"알겠습니다. 하루만 더 참아 주십시오. 제가 말한 것은 반드시 하겠습니다.\""]], [["Noirtier seemed to be calmed by these words, and turned his eyes with indifference to the other side.", "누아르티에는 그 말에 진정된 듯, 무심하게 눈을 다른 쪽으로 돌렸다."], ["Villefort violently unbuttoned his greatcoat, which seemed to strangle him, and passing his livid hand across his forehead, entered his study.", "빌포르는 자기 목을 죄는 듯한 외투의 단추를 거칠게 풀어헤치고는, 핏기 없는 손으로 이마를 쓸며 서재로 들어갔다."]], [["The night was cold and still; the family had all retired to rest but Villefort, who alone remained up, and worked till five o’clock in the morning, reviewing the last interrogatories made the night before by the examining magistrates, compiling the depositions of the witnesses, and putting the finishing stroke to the deed of accusation, which was one of the most energetic and best conceived of any he had yet delivered.", "밤은 춥고 고요했다. 가족은 모두 잠자리에 들었으나 빌포르만은 홀로 깨어 있었고, 새벽 다섯 시까지 일했다. 그는 예심 판사들이 전날 밤 행한 마지막 신문 기록을 검토하고, 증인들의 진술을 정리하며, 기소장에 마지막 손질을 가했다. 그것은 그가 지금껏 작성한 어떤 것보다도 가장 힘차고 가장 잘 구상된 기소장 가운데 하나였다."]], [["The next day, Monday, was the first sitting of the assizes.", "이튿날인 월요일은 중죄 재판이 처음 열리는 날이었다."], ["The morning dawned dull and gloomy, and Villefort saw the dim gray light shine upon the lines he had traced in red ink.", "아침은 흐리고 음울하게 밝아 왔고, 빌포르는 그 희뿌연 잿빛 빛이 자신이 붉은 잉크로 그어 놓은 줄들 위로 비치는 것을 보았다."], ["The magistrate had slept for a short time while the lamp sent forth its final struggles; its flickerings awoke him, and he found his fingers as damp and purple as though they had been dipped in blood.", "등불이 마지막으로 발버둥 치는 동안 법관은 잠깐 잠이 들었었다. 그 등불의 깜빡임이 그를 깨웠고, 그는 자신의 손가락이 마치 피에 담갔던 것처럼 축축하고 자줏빛인 것을 발견했다."]], [["He opened the window; a bright yellow streak crossed the sky, and seemed to divide in half the poplars, which stood out in black relief on the horizon.", "그는 창문을 열었다. 환한 노란 줄기 하나가 하늘을 가로지르며, 지평선 위로 검게 도드라진 포플러나무들을 절반으로 가르는 듯했다."], ["In the clover-fields beyond the chestnut-trees, a lark was mounting up to heaven, while pouring out her clear morning song.", "밤나무 너머 클로버 밭에서는 종달새 한 마리가 맑은 아침 노래를 쏟아 내며 하늘로 솟구치고 있었다."], ["The damps of the dew bathed the head of Villefort, and refreshed his memory.", "이슬의 습기가 빌포르의 머리를 적시며 그의 기억을 새롭게 했다."]], [["“Today,”", "\"오늘,\""], ["he said with an effort,—“today the man who holds the blade of justice must strike wherever there is guilt.”", "그가 애써 말했다. \"오늘 정의의 칼날을 쥔 자는 죄가 있는 곳이라면 어디든 내리쳐야 한다.\""]], [["Involuntarily his eyes wandered towards the window of Noirtier’s room, where he had seen him the preceding night.", "자기도 모르게 그의 눈은 전날 밤 아버지를 보았던 누아르티에의 방 창문 쪽으로 향했다."], ["The curtain was drawn, and yet the image of his father was so vivid to his mind that he addressed the closed window as though it had been open, and as if through the opening he had beheld the menacing old man.", "커튼이 쳐져 있었으나, 그의 마음속에 아버지의 모습이 어찌나 생생했던지, 그는 마치 창문이 열려 있고 그 틈으로 위협하는 노인을 본 것처럼 닫힌 창문을 향해 말을 건넸다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["he murmured,—“yes, be satisfied.”", "그가 중얼거렸다. \"그래, 만족하십시오.\""]], [["His head dropped upon his chest, and in this position he paced his study; then he threw himself, dressed as he was, upon a sofa, less to sleep than to rest his limbs, cramped with cold and study. By degrees everyone awoke.", "그의 고개가 가슴 위로 떨어졌고, 그 자세로 그는 서재 안을 서성였다. 그러더니 옷을 입은 채로 소파에 몸을 던졌는데, 잠을 자려는 것이라기보다는 추위와 일로 굳어 버린 사지를 쉬게 하려는 것이었다."], ["Villefort, from his study, heard the successive noises which accompany the life of a house,—the opening and shutting of doors, the ringing of Madame de Villefort’s bell, to summon the waiting-maid, mingled with the first shouts of the child, who rose full of the enjoyment of his age.", "하나둘씩 모두가 잠에서 깼다. 빌포르는 서재에서, 집안의 삶에 따라붙는 소리들이 잇따라 들려오는 것을 들었다. 문을 여닫는 소리, 빌포르 부인이 하녀를 부르는 종소리, 그리고 거기에 섞여 제 나이의 즐거움으로 가득 차 일어난 아이의 첫 외침이 들렸다."], ["Villefort also rang; his new valet brought him the papers, and with them a cup of chocolate.", "빌포르도 종을 울렸다. 새 하인이 서류를 가져왔고, 그와 함께 초콜릿 한 잔도 들고 왔다."]], [["“What are you bringing me?” said he.", "\"무엇을 가져온 건가?\" 그가 물었다."]], [["“A cup of chocolate.”", "\"초콜릿 한 잔입니다.\""]], [["“I did not ask for it.", "\"난 그런 걸 부탁하지 않았네."], ["Who has paid me this attention?”", "누가 이런 마음을 써 준 건가?\""]], [["“My mistress, sir.", "\"마님이십니다."], ["She said you would have to speak a great deal in the murder case, and that you should take something to keep up your strength;”", "살인 사건 재판에서 말씀을 많이 하셔야 할 테니, 기운을 유지하시려면 뭐라도 좀 드셔야 한다고 하셨습니다.\""], ["and the valet placed the cup on the table nearest to the sofa, which was, like all the rest, covered with papers.", "그러고는 하인이 소파에서 가장 가까운 탁자 위에 잔을 놓았다. 그 탁자도 나머지와 마찬가지로 서류로 뒤덮여 있었다."]], [["The valet then left the room.", "하인은 방을 나갔다."], ["Villefort looked for an instant with a gloomy expression, then, suddenly, taking it up with a nervous motion, he swallowed its contents at one draught.", "빌포르는 잠시 음울한 표정으로 그것을 바라보더니, 갑자기 신경질적인 동작으로 잔을 집어 들고는 단숨에 들이켰다."], ["It might have been thought that he hoped the beverage would be mortal, and that he sought for death to deliver him from a duty which he would rather die than fulfil.", "그가 그 음료가 치명적이기를 바랐다고, 그리하여 차라리 죽는 한이 있어도 하기 싫은 그 의무에서 자신을 벗어나게 해 줄 죽음을 구하고 있었다고 여겨질 만도 했다."], ["He then rose, and paced his room with a smile it would have been terrible to witness.", "그러더니 그는 일어나, 차마 보기 끔찍했을 미소를 띤 채 방 안을 서성였다."], ["The chocolate was inoffensive, for M. de Villefort felt no effects.", "초콜릿은 해롭지 않았다. 빌포르 씨는 아무런 이상도 느끼지 못했으니까."]], [["The breakfast-hour arrived, but M. de Villefort was not at table.", "아침 식사 시간이 되었으나, 빌포르 씨는 식탁에 없었다."], ["The valet re-entered.", "하인이 다시 들어왔다."]], [["“Madame de Villefort wishes to remind you, sir,”", "\"마님이 일깨워 드리라고 하셨습니다.\""], ["he said, “that eleven o’clock has just struck, and that the trial commences at twelve.”", "하인이 말했다. \"방금 열한 시를 알리는 종이 울렸고, 재판은 열두 시에 시작된다고요.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래서,\""], ["said Villefort, “what then?”", "빌포르가 말했다. \"그게 어쨌다는 건가?\""]], [["“Madame de Villefort is dressed; she is quite ready, and wishes to know if she is to accompany you, sir?” “Where to?”", "\"마님은 채비를 다 마치셨습니다. 이제 준비가 되셨고, 모시고 가실 건지 여쭤보라고 하셨습니다.\""]], [["“To the Palais.”", "\"어디로 말인가?\""]], [["“What to do?”", "\"법원으로요.\""]], [["“My mistress wishes much to be present at the trial.”", "\"무엇하러?\" \"마님은 재판에 꼭 참석하고 싶어 하십니다.\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["said Villefort, with a startling accent; “does she wish that?”", "빌포르가 섬뜩한 어조로 말했다. \"부인이 그걸 원한다고?\""]], [["The servant drew back and said, “If you wish to go alone, sir, I will go and tell my mistress.”", "하인이 뒤로 물러서며 말했다. \"혼자 가시려거든, 나리, 제가 가서 마님께 그리 전하겠습니다.\""]], [["Villefort remained silent for a moment, and dented his pale cheeks with his nails.", "빌포르는 잠시 잠자코 있다가, 손톱으로 핏기 없는 두 뺨을 후벼 자국을 냈다."]], [["“Tell your mistress,”", "\"마님께 전하게.\""], ["he at length answered, “that I wish to speak to her, and I beg she will wait for me in her own room.”", "그가 마침내 대답했다. \"내가 할 이야기가 있으니, 자기 방에서 나를 기다려 달라고 말일세.\""]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "\"네, 나리.\""]], [["“Then come to dress and shave me.” “Directly, sir.”", "\"그리고 와서 옷 입는 것과 면도하는 걸 거들게.\""]], [["The valet re-appeared almost instantly, and, having shaved his master, assisted him to dress entirely in black.", "\"곧 가겠습니다, 나리.\" 하인은 거의 곧바로 다시 나타나, 주인의 면도를 마친 뒤 온통 검은색으로 옷을 입는 것을 도왔다."], ["When he had finished, he said:", "일을 마치고 그가 말했다."]], [["“My mistress said she should expect you, sir, as soon as you had finished dressing.”", "\"마님이, 옷을 다 입으시는 대로 기다리고 계시겠다고 하셨습니다, 나리.\""]], [["“I am going to her.”", "\"부인에게 가겠네.\""]], [["And Villefort, with his papers under his arm and hat in hand, directed his steps toward the apartment of his wife.", "그리하여 빌포르는 서류를 옆구리에 끼고 모자를 손에 든 채, 아내의 방으로 발걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["At the door he paused for a moment to wipe his damp, pale brow.", "문 앞에서 그는 잠시 멈춰 서서 축축하고 핏기 없는 이마를 닦았다."], ["He then entered the room.", "그러고는 방으로 들어섰다."], ["Madame de Villefort was sitting on an ottoman and impatiently turning over the leaves of some newspapers and pamphlets which young Edward, by way of amusing himself, was tearing to pieces before his mother could finish reading them.", "빌포르 부인은 오토만에 앉아, 신문과 소책자 몇 가지를 초조하게 넘기고 있었는데, 어린 에두아르가 심심풀이로 어머니가 다 읽기도 전에 그것들을 갈가리 찢어 놓고 있었다."], ["She was dressed to go out, her bonnet was placed beside her on a chair, and her gloves were on her hands.", "그녀는 외출 채비를 갖춘 차림이었고, 모자는 의자 위 옆자리에 놓여 있었으며, 두 손에는 장갑이 끼워져 있었다."]], [["“Ah, here you are, monsieur,”", "\"아, 오셨군요, 여보.\""], ["she said in her naturally calm voice; “but how pale you are!", "그녀가 본디 차분한 목소리로 말했다. \"그런데 어쩌면 그렇게 안색이 창백하세요!"], ["Have you been working all night?", "밤새 일하셨어요?"], ["Why did you not come down to breakfast?", "왜 아침 식사하러 내려오지 않으셨죠?"], ["Well, will you take me, or shall I take Edward?”", "자, 저를 데려가실래요, 아니면 제가 에두아르를 데려갈까요?\""]], [["Madame de Villefort had multiplied her questions in order to gain one answer, but to all her inquiries M. de Villefort remained mute and cold as a statue.", "빌포르 부인은 답 하나라도 끌어내려고 질문을 거듭 쏟아 냈으나, 그 모든 물음에 빌포르 씨는 조각상처럼 차갑게 입을 다물고 있었다."]], [["“Edward,”", "\"에두아르,\""], ["said Villefort, fixing an imperious glance on the child, “go and play in the drawing-room, my dear; I wish to speak to your mamma.”", "빌포르가 위압적인 눈길을 아이에게 고정하며 말했다. \"응접실에 가서 놀려무나, 얘야. 엄마에게 할 이야기가 있단다.\""]], [["Madame de Villefort shuddered at the sight of that cold countenance, that resolute tone, and the awfully strange preliminaries.", "빌포르 부인은 그 차가운 얼굴, 단호한 어조, 그리고 무섭도록 이상한 서두에 몸을 떨었다."], ["Edward raised his head, looked at his mother, and then, finding that she did not confirm the order, began cutting off the heads of his leaden soldiers.", "에두아르는 고개를 들어 어머니를 보았고, 어머니가 그 명령을 거들지 않는 것을 알자 자기 납 병정들의 머리를 잘라 내기 시작했다."]], [["“Edward,”", "\"에두아르,\""], ["cried M. de Villefort, so harshly that the child started up from the floor, “do you hear me?—Go!”", "빌포르 씨가 어찌나 매섭게 외쳤던지 아이가 바닥에서 벌떡 일어났다. \"내 말 들리느냐? 가거라!\""]], [["The child, unaccustomed to such treatment, arose, pale and trembling; it would be difficult to say whether his emotion were caused by fear or passion.", "이런 대접에 익숙하지 않던 아이는 새파랗게 질려 떨면서 일어섰다. 그 감정이 두려움 때문인지 분노 때문인지는 가늠하기 어려웠다."], ["His father went up to him, took him in his arms, and kissed his forehead.", "아버지는 아이에게 다가가 품에 안고는 이마에 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“Go,”", "\"가거라.\""], ["he said: “go, my child.”", "그가 말했다. \"가, 내 아들아.\""], ["Edward ran out.", "에두아르는 뛰어나갔다."]], [["M. de Villefort went to the door, which he closed behind the child, and bolted.", "빌포르 씨는 문으로 가, 아이의 뒤로 문을 닫고 빗장을 질렀다."]], [["“Dear me!”", "\"이런!\""], ["said the young woman, endeavoring to read her husband’s inmost thoughts, while a smile passed over her countenance which froze the impassibility of Villefort; “what is the matter?”", "젊은 여인이 남편의 마음속 깊은 생각을 읽어 내려 애쓰며 말했다. 그러는 동안 그녀의 얼굴 위로 미소가 스쳤으나, 그 미소는 빌포르의 무표정을 더욱 얼어붙게 했다. \"무슨 일이에요?\""]], [["“Madame, where do you keep the poison you generally use?”", "\"부인, 평소에 쓰는 독약을 어디에 두고 있소?\""], ["said the magistrate, without any introduction, placing himself between his wife and the door.", "법관이 아무런 서두도 없이, 아내와 문 사이에 자리를 잡으며 말했다."]], [["Madame de Villefort must have experienced something of the sensation of a bird which, looking up, sees the murderous trap closing over its head.", "빌포르 부인은 위를 올려다보다 제 머리 위로 살기 어린 덫이 닫히는 것을 본 새가 느낄 법한 감각을 틀림없이 맛보았으리라."]], [["A hoarse, broken tone, which was neither a cry nor a sigh, escaped from her, while she became deadly pale.", "비명도 한숨도 아닌, 쉬고 끊어진 소리가 그녀의 입에서 새어 나왔고, 그녀는 죽은 사람처럼 새파랗게 질렸다."]], [["“Monsieur,”", "\"여보,\""], ["she said, “I—I do not understand you.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"전, 전 무슨 말씀인지 모르겠어요.\""]], [["And, in her first paroxysm of terror, she had raised herself from the sofa, in the next, stronger very likely than the other, she fell down again on the cushions.", "그리고 처음 일어난 공포의 발작에서 그녀는 소파에서 몸을 일으켰다가, 다음 순간 그보다 더 강했을 발작에 다시 쿠션 위로 쓰러졌다."]], [["“I asked you,”", "\"내가 물었소.\""], ["continued Villefort, in a perfectly calm tone, “where you conceal the poison by the aid of which you have killed my father-in-law, M. de Saint-Méran, my mother-in-law, Madame de Saint-Méran, Barrois, and my daughter Valentine.”", "빌포르가 더없이 차분한 어조로 말을 이었다. \"당신이 그 독약으로 내 장인 생메랑 씨, 내 장모 생메랑 부인, 바루아, 그리고 내 딸 발랑틴을 죽인 그 독약을 어디에 감춰 두었느냐고.\""]], [["“Ah, sir,”", "\"아, 여보.\""], ["exclaimed Madame de Villefort, clasping her hands, “what do you say?”", "빌포르 부인이 두 손을 마주 잡으며 외쳤다. \"무슨 말씀을 하시는 거예요?\""]], [["“It is not for you to interrogate, but to answer.”", "\"신문하는 것은 당신이 할 일이 아니라, 대답하는 것이 할 일이오.\""]], [["“Is it to the judge or to the husband?”", "\"판사에게 하는 건가요, 남편에게 하는 건가요?\""], ["stammered Madame de Villefort.", "빌포르 부인이 더듬거리며 물었다."]], [["“To the judge—to the judge, madame!”", "\"판사에게요. 판사에게 하는 거요, 부인!\""], ["It was terrible to behold the frightful pallor of that woman, the anguish of her look, the trembling of her whole frame.", "그 여인의 무서운 창백함, 그 눈길의 고뇌, 온몸의 떨림은 보기에 끔찍했다."]], [["“Ah, sir,”", "\"아, 여보.\""], ["she muttered, “ah, sir,”", "그녀가 중얼거렸다. \"아, 여보.\""], ["and this was all.", "그러고는 그게 전부였다."]], [["“You do not answer, madame!”", "\"대답을 못 하는군, 부인!\""], ["exclaimed the terrible interrogator.", "그 무서운 신문관이 외쳤다."], ["Then he added, with a smile yet more terrible than his anger, “It is true, then; you do not deny it!”", "그러더니 그는 분노보다도 더 무서운 미소를 띠고 덧붙였다. \"그렇다면 사실이로군. 당신은 그것을 부인하지 않는군!\""], ["She moved forward.", "그녀가 앞으로 나섰다."], ["“And you cannot deny it!”", "\"그리고 부인할 수도 없지!\""], ["added Villefort, extending his hand toward her, as though to seize her in the name of justice.", "빌포르가 마치 정의의 이름으로 그녀를 붙잡으려는 듯 손을 그녀 쪽으로 뻗으며 덧붙였다. \"당신은 이 여러 범죄를 뻔뻔스러운 솜씨로 해치웠소."], ["“You have accomplished these different crimes with impudent address, but which could only deceive those whose affections for you blinded them.", "하지만 그건 당신을 향한 애정에 눈먼 자들만 속일 수 있는 솜씨였지. 생메랑 부인이 죽은 뒤로 나는 우리 집에 독살자가 살고 있다는 걸 알았소. 다브리니 씨가 내게 경고했지."], ["Since the death of Madame de Saint-Méran, I have known that a poisoner lived in my house. M.", "바루아가 죽은 뒤 나의 의심은 한 천사를 향했소. 죄가 없을 때조차 내 가슴속에 늘 살아 있는 그런 의심 말이오. 하지만 발랑틴이 죽은 뒤로, 부인, 내 마음속엔 한 점 의혹도 없어졌소."], ["d’Avrigny warned me of it.", "내 마음속뿐 아니라 다른 이들의 마음속에서도 말이오."], ["After the death of Barrois my suspicions were directed towards an angel,—those suspicions which, even when there is no crime, are always alive in my heart; but after the death of Valentine, there has been no doubt in my mind, madame, and not only in mine, but in those of others; thus your crime, known by two persons, suspected by many, will soon become public, and, as I told you just now, you no longer speak to the husband, but to the judge.” 50165m", "그러니 두 사람이 알고 여러 사람이 의심하는 당신의 범죄는 곧 세상에 드러날 것이오. 그리고 방금 말했듯, 당신은 더 이상 남편에게 말하고 있는 게 아니라 판사에게 말하고 있는 거요.\""]], [["The young woman hid her face in her hands.", "젊은 여인은 두 손에 얼굴을 묻었다."]], [["“Oh, sir,”", "\"아, 여보.\""], ["she stammered, “I beseech you, do not believe appearances.”", "그녀가 더듬거렸다. \"부탁이에요, 겉모습만 믿지 마세요.\""]], [["“Are you, then, a coward?”", "\"그래, 당신은 비겁자인가?\""], ["cried Villefort, in a contemptuous voice.", "빌포르가 경멸하는 목소리로 외쳤다."], ["“But I have always observed that poisoners were cowards.", "\"하지만 나는 독살자들이 늘 비겁자라는 걸 줄곧 봐 왔지."], ["Can you be a coward, you, who have had the courage to witness the death of two old men and a young girl murdered by you?”", "당신이 비겁자일 수 있다니, 당신, 자기 손으로 죽인 노인 둘과 처녀 하나의 죽음을 지켜볼 용기를 지녔던 당신이?\""]], [["“Sir! sir!”", "\"여보! 여보!\""]], [["“Can you be a coward?”", "\"당신이 비겁자일 수 있다니,\""], ["continued Villefort, with increasing excitement, “you, who could count, one by one, the minutes of four death agonies?", "빌포르가 점점 더 흥분하며 말을 이었다. \"네 번의 단말마를 일 분 일 분 하나하나 헤아릴 수 있었던 당신이?"], ["_You_, who have arranged your infernal plans, and removed the beverages with a talent and precision almost miraculous?", "그 지옥 같은 계획을 짜고, 거의 기적에 가까운 재주와 정확함으로 음료를 치워 버린 당신이?"], ["Have you, then, who have calculated everything with such nicety, have you forgotten to calculate one thing—I mean where the revelation of your crimes will lead you to?", "그렇게 모든 걸 그토록 세밀하게 계산한 당신이, 한 가지를 계산하는 걸 잊었단 말이오? 당신의 범죄가 드러나면 당신이 어디로 끌려가게 될지 말이오. 오, 그럴 리 없지."], ["Oh, it is impossible—you must have saved some surer, more subtle and deadly poison than any other, that you might escape the punishment that you deserve.", "당신은 마땅히 받아야 할 벌을 피할 수 있도록, 그 어떤 것보다 더 확실하고 더 교묘하며 더 치명적인 독약을 따로 남겨 두었을 테지. 당신은 그렇게 해 놓았을 거요."], ["You have done this—I hope so, at least.”", "적어도 나는 그러길 바라오.\""]], [["Madame de Villefort stretched out her hands, and fell on her knees.", "빌포르 부인은 두 손을 뻗으며 무릎을 꿇었다."]], [["“I understand,”", "\"알겠소.\""], ["he said, “you confess; but a confession made to the judges, a confession made at the last moment, extorted when the crime cannot be denied, diminishes not the punishment inflicted on the guilty!”", "그가 말했다. \"당신은 자백하는군. 하지만 판사들 앞에서 한 자백, 마지막 순간에 한 자백, 범죄를 더 이상 부인할 수 없을 때 억지로 끌려 나온 자백은 죄인에게 내려지는 벌을 조금도 줄여 주지 못하오!\""]], [["“The punishment?”", "\"벌이라고요?\""], ["exclaimed Madame de Villefort, “the punishment, monsieur?", "빌포르 부인이 외쳤다. \"벌이라뇨, 여보?"], ["Twice you have pronounced that word!”", "그 말을 벌써 두 번이나 하시는군요!\""]], [["“Certainly.", "\"물론이오."], ["Did you hope to escape it because you were four times guilty?", "당신이 네 번이나 죄를 지었으니 벌을 면할 수 있으리라 바랐소? 그것을 선고하는 자의 아내이니 벌이 보류되리라 생각했소?"], ["Did you think the punishment would be withheld because you are the wife of him who pronounces it?—No, madame, no; the scaffold awaits the poisoner, whoever she may be, unless, as I just said, the poisoner has taken the precaution of keeping for herself a few drops of her deadliest poison.”", "아니오, 부인, 아니오. 독살자에게는, 그가 누구든, 단두대가 기다리고 있소. 방금 말했듯, 그 독살자가 자기 몫으로 가장 치명적인 독약 몇 방울을 남겨 두는 신중함을 발휘하지 않았다면 말이오.\""]], [["Madame de Villefort uttered a wild cry, and a hideous and uncontrollable terror spread over her distorted features.", "빌포르 부인이 거친 비명을 질렀고, 일그러진 얼굴 위로 흉측하고 걷잡을 수 없는 공포가 번졌다."]], [["“Oh, do not fear the scaffold, madame,”", "\"오, 단두대는 두려워 마시오, 부인.\" 법관이 말했다."], ["said the magistrate; “I will not dishonor you, since that would be dishonor to myself; no, if you have heard me distinctly, you will understand that you are not to die on the scaffold.”", "\"나는 당신에게 치욕을 안기지 않겠소. 그건 곧 나 자신에게 치욕이 될 테니까. 아니, 당신이 내 말을 똑똑히 들었다면, 당신이 단두대에서 죽지 않으리라는 걸 알아차렸을 거요.\""]], [["“No, I do not understand; what do you mean?”", "\"아니요, 모르겠어요. 무슨 말씀이세요?\""], ["stammered the unhappy woman, completely overwhelmed.", "가엾은 여인이 완전히 압도당한 채 더듬거렸다."]], [["“I mean that the wife of the first magistrate in the capital shall not, by her infamy, soil an unblemished name; that she shall not, with one blow, dishonor her husband and her child.”", "\"내 말은, 이 수도의 으뜸가는 법관의 아내가 자기 오명으로 흠 없는 이름을 더럽혀서는 안 된다는 것이오. 단번에 자기 남편과 자식에게 치욕을 안겨서는 안 된다는 것이오.\" \"아니요, 아니요, 오, 아니요!\""]], [["“No, no—oh, no!” “Well, madame, it will be a laudable action on your part, and I will thank you for it!”", "\"그러니, 부인, 그것은 당신으로서는 칭찬받을 만한 행동이 될 것이고, 나는 당신에게 그 일을 고마워할 거요!\""]], [["“You will thank me—for what?”", "\"고마워하신다고요, 무엇을?\""]], [["“For what you have just said.”", "\"방금 당신이 한 말에 대해서요.\""]], [["“What did I say?", "\"제가 무슨 말을 했죠?"], ["Oh, my brain whirls; I no longer understand anything.", "오, 머릿속이 빙빙 도네요. 이제 아무것도 모르겠어요."], ["Oh, my God, my God!”", "오, 하느님, 하느님!\""]], [["And she rose, with her hair dishevelled, and her lips foaming.", "그러더니 그녀는 머리카락을 풀어헤치고 입가에 거품을 문 채 일어섰다."]], [["“Have you answered the question I put to you on entering the room?—where do you keep the poison you generally use, madame?”", "\"방에 들어오면서 내가 한 질문에 대답했소? 평소에 쓰는 독약을 어디에 두고 있소, 부인?\""]], [["Madame de Villefort raised her arms to heaven, and convulsively struck one hand against the other.", "빌포르 부인은 두 팔을 하늘로 들어 올리고는, 발작적으로 한 손으로 다른 손을 내리쳤다."]], [["“No, no,”", "\"아니요, 아니요.\""], ["she vociferated, “no, you cannot wish that!” 50169m", "그녀가 악을 썼다. \"아니요, 그걸 바라실 리 없어요!\""]], [["“What I do not wish, madame, is that you should perish on the scaffold.", "\"내가 바라지 않는 것은, 부인, 당신이 단두대에서 스러지는 것이오."], ["Do you understand?”", "알아듣겠소?\""], ["asked Villefort.", "빌포르가 물었다."]], [["“Oh, mercy, mercy, monsieur!”", "\"오, 자비를, 자비를 베풀어 주세요, 여보!\""]], [["“What I require is, that justice be done.", "\"내가 요구하는 것은 정의가 실현되는 것이오."], ["I am on the earth to punish, madame,”", "나는 벌하기 위해 이 땅에 있소, 부인.\""], ["he added, with a flaming glance; “any other woman, were it the queen herself, I would send to the executioner; but to you I shall be merciful.", "그가 이글거리는 눈길로 덧붙였다. \"다른 여자라면, 설령 그게 왕비라 해도, 나는 사형 집행인에게 보낼 것이오. 하지만 당신에게만은 자비를 베풀겠소."], ["To you I will say, ‘Have you not, madame, put aside some of the surest, deadliest, most speedy poison?’”", "당신에게는 이렇게 말하겠소. '부인, 가장 확실하고 가장 치명적이며 가장 빠른 독약을 좀 따로 챙겨 두지 않았소?'\""]], [["50170m\n“Oh, pardon me, sir; let me live!”", "\"오, 용서해 주세요, 여보. 살게 해 주세요!\""]], [["“She is cowardly,”", "\"비겁한 여자로군.\""], ["said Villefort.", "빌포르가 말했다."]], [["“Reflect that I am your wife!”", "\"제가 당신의 아내라는 걸 생각하세요!\""]], [["“You are a poisoner.”", "\"당신은 독살자요.\""]], [["“In the name of Heaven!”", "\"하늘의 이름으로!\" \"안 되오!\""]], [["“No!” “In the name of the love you once bore me!”", "\"한때 당신이 제게 품었던 사랑의 이름으로!\""]], [["“In the name of our child!", "\"안 되오, 안 돼!\""], ["Ah, for the sake of our child, let me live!”", "\"우리 아이의 이름으로! 아, 우리 아이를 봐서, 살게 해 주세요!\""]], [["50167m\n“No, no, no, I tell you; one day, if I allow you to live, you will perhaps kill him, as you have the others!”", "\"안 되오, 안 돼, 안 된다고 했소. 언젠가, 내가 당신을 살려 두면, 당신은 다른 이들에게 그랬듯 그 아이마저 죽일지 모르오!\""]], [["“I?—I kill my boy?”", "\"제가요? 제가 제 아이를 죽인다고요?\""], ["cried the distracted mother, rushing toward Villefort; “I kill my son?", "정신이 나간 어머니가 빌포르에게 달려들며 외쳤다. \"제가 제 아들을 죽여요?"], ["Ha, ha, ha!”", "하, 하, 하!\""], ["and a frightful, demoniac laugh finished the sentence, which was lost in a hoarse rattle.", "그러더니 무시무시한 악마 같은 웃음이 그 말을 끝맺었고, 그 웃음은 쉰 가르랑거림 속으로 잦아들었다."]], [["Madame de Villefort fell at her husband’s feet. He approached her.", "빌포르 부인은 남편의 발치에 쓰러졌다. 그가 그녀에게 다가갔다."]], [["“Think of it, madame,”", "\"잘 생각하시오, 부인.\""], ["he said; “if, on my return, justice has not been satisfied, I will denounce you with my own mouth, and arrest you with my own hands!”", "그가 말했다. \"내가 돌아왔을 때 정의가 실현되어 있지 않으면, 나는 내 입으로 당신을 고발하고, 내 손으로 당신을 체포하겠소!\""]], [["She listened, panting, overwhelmed, crushed; her eye alone lived, and glared horribly.", "그녀는 숨을 헐떡이며, 압도당하고 짓눌린 채 귀를 기울였다. 그녀의 눈만이 살아서 끔찍하게 번득였다."]], [["“Do you understand me?”", "\"내 말을 알아듣겠소?\""], ["he said. “I am going down there to pronounce the sentence of death against a murderer.", "그가 말했다. \"나는 이제 저 아래로 내려가 한 살인자에게 사형 선고를 내릴 것이오."], ["If I find you alive on my return, you shall sleep tonight in the conciergerie.”", "돌아왔을 때 당신이 살아 있는 걸 발견하면, 당신은 오늘 밤 구치소에서 잠들게 될 거요.\""]], [["Madame de Villefort sighed; her nerves gave way, and she sunk on the carpet.", "빌포르 부인이 한숨을 내쉬었다. 신경이 무너져 내렸고, 그녀는 양탄자 위로 주저앉았다."], ["The king’s attorney seemed to experience a sensation of pity; he looked upon her less severely, and, bowing to her, said slowly:", "검사장은 어떤 연민 같은 것을 느낀 듯했다. 그는 한결 덜 가혹한 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았고, 그녀에게 고개를 숙이며 천천히 말했다."]], [["“Farewell, madame, farewell!”", "\"잘 있으시오, 부인, 잘 있으시오!\""]], [["That farewell struck Madame de Villefort like the executioner’s knife.", "그 작별 인사는 빌포르 부인에게 사형 집행인의 칼처럼 내리꽂혔다."], ["She fainted. The procureur went out, after having double-locked the door.", "그녀는 정신을 잃었다. 검사장은 문을 이중으로 잠근 뒤 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["# Chapter 109. The Assizes", "# 제107장 사자 우리"]], [["The Benedetto affair, as it was called at the Palais, and by people in general, had produced a tremendous sensation.", "팔레●에서, 그리고 일반 사람들 사이에서 베네데토 사건이라 불리던 그 사건은 엄청난 반향을 불러일으켰다."], ["Frequenting the Café de Paris, the Boulevard de Gand, and the Bois de Boulogne, during his brief career of splendor, the false Cavalcanti had formed a host of acquaintances.", "짧은 영광의 시절 동안 카페 드 파리와 강 대로, 불로뉴 숲을 드나들면서, 가짜 카발칸티는 수많은 지인을 만들어 두었다."], ["The papers had related his various adventures, both as the man of fashion and the galley-slave; and as everyone who had been personally acquainted with Prince Andrea Cavalcanti experienced a lively curiosity in his fate, they all determined to spare no trouble in endeavoring to witness the trial of M. Benedetto for the murder of his comrade in chains.", "신문들은 그가 사교계 인사로서, 또 갤리선 죄수로서 겪은 갖가지 사연을 상세히 보도했다. 안드레아 카발칸티 공자와 직접 안면이 있던 사람들은 모두 그의 운명에 대해 강한 호기심을 느꼈고, 사슬에 묶인 동료를 죽인 죄로 열리는 베네데토 씨의 재판을 어떻게든 직접 보려고 애쓰기로 마음먹었다."]], [["In the eyes of many, Benedetto appeared, if not a victim to, at least an instance of, the fallibility of the law. M.", "많은 사람들의 눈에 베네데토는, 법의 오류에 희생된 자까지는 아니더라도 적어도 그 오류를 보여 주는 사례로 비쳤다."], ["Cavalcanti, his father, had been seen in Paris, and it was expected that he would re-appear to claim the illustrious outcast.", "그의 아버지 카발칸티 씨가 파리에서 목격된 적이 있었으므로, 그 고명한 인사가 다시 나타나 자신의 명예로운 사생아를 거두려 하지 않겠느냐는 기대가 있었다."], ["Many, also, who were not aware of the circumstances attending his withdrawal from Paris, were struck with the worthy appearance, the gentlemanly bearing, and the knowledge of the world displayed by the old patrician, who certainly played the nobleman very well, so long as he said nothing, and made no arithmetical calculations.", "또한 그가 파리를 떠난 정황을 모르는 많은 이들은, 그 늙은 귀족이 보여 준 점잖은 풍채와 신사다운 거동, 세상사에 밝은 식견에 강한 인상을 받았다. 사실 그 노인은, 입을 다물고 산수 계산만 하지 않는 한, 귀족 연기를 제법 그럴듯하게 해냈던 것이다."]], [["As for the accused himself, many remembered him as being so amiable, so handsome, and so liberal, that they chose to think him the victim of some conspiracy, since in this world large fortunes frequently excite the malevolence and jealousy of some unknown enemy.", "피고 본인으로 말하자면, 많은 이들이 그를 어찌나 상냥하고 잘생기고 통이 큰 사람으로 기억하던지, 그를 어떤 음모의 희생자로 여기고 싶어 했다. 이 세상에서 막대한 재산이란 알지 못하는 적의 악의와 질투를 자주 부추기는 법이니 말이다."]], [["Everyone, therefore, ran to the court; some to witness the sight, others to comment upon it.", "그리하여 모두가 법정으로 달려갔다. 어떤 이는 구경하러, 어떤 이는 거기에 대해 한마디 보태러."], ["From seven o’clock in the morning a crowd was stationed at the iron gates, and an hour before the trial commenced the hall was full of the privileged.", "아침 일곱 시부터 철문 앞에 군중이 진을 쳤고, 재판이 시작되기 한 시간 전에 이미 특권을 가진 사람들로 법정이 가득 찼다."], ["Before the entrance of the magistrates, and indeed frequently afterwards, a court of justice, on days when some especial trial is to take place, resembles a drawing-room where many persons recognize each other and converse if they can do so without losing their seats; or, if they are separated by too great a number of lawyers, communicate by signs.", "어떤 특별한 재판이 열리는 날이면, 판사들이 입장하기 전에는 — 그리고 입장한 뒤에도 종종 — 법정은 마치 응접실 같은 모습이 된다. 많은 사람이 서로를 알아보고, 자리를 잃지 않고도 가능하다면 대화를 나누며, 너무 많은 변호사들에 의해 떨어져 있을 때는 몸짓으로 의사를 주고받는다."]], [["It was one of the magnificent autumn days which make amends for a short summer; the clouds which M.", "그날은 짧은 여름을 보상해 주는 화창한 가을날 가운데 하루였다."], ["de Villefort had perceived at sunrise had all disappeared as if by magic, and one of the softest and most brilliant days of September shone forth in all its splendor.", "빌포르 씨가 해 뜰 무렵 보았던 구름은 마법에라도 걸린 듯 모조리 사라졌고, 9월의 가장 부드럽고 가장 눈부신 하루가 온전한 광채를 뽐내며 빛나고 있었다."]], [["Beauchamp, one of the kings of the press, and therefore claiming the right of a throne everywhere, was eying everybody through his monocle.", "언론계의 제왕 가운데 하나로, 그래서 어디에서나 옥좌의 권리를 주장하는 보샹은 단안경 너머로 모든 사람을 살피고 있었다."], ["He perceived Château-Renaud and Debray, who had just gained the good graces of a sergeant-at-arms, and who had persuaded the latter to let them stand before, instead of behind him, as they ought to have done.", "그는 샤토르노와 드브레를 발견했다. 두 사람은 방금 정리 한 사람의 호의를 얻어 내, 마땅히 그래야 할 위치인 그의 뒤가 아니라 앞에 서도 좋다는 허락을 받아 낸 참이었다."], ["The worthy sergeant had recognized the minister’s secretary and the millionnaire, and, by way of paying extra attention to his noble neighbors, promised to keep their places while they paid a visit to Beauchamp.", "그 갸륵한 정리는 장관 비서와 백만장자를 알아보았고, 고귀한 이웃들에게 각별한 배려를 베푸는 뜻에서, 두 사람이 보샹을 만나러 가 있는 동안 자리를 지켜 주겠노라 약속했다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"이런,\""], ["said Beauchamp, “we shall see our friend!”", "보샹이 말했다. \"우리 친구를 보게 되겠군!\""]], [["“Yes, indeed!”", "\"그래, 정말이지!\""], ["replied Debray.", "드브레가 대꾸했다."], ["“That worthy prince.", "\"그 갸륵한 공자 말이지."], ["Deuce take those Italian princes!”", "그 이탈리아 공자들이라면 지긋지긋해!\""]], [["“A man, too, who could boast of Dante for a genealogist, and could reckon back to the _Divina Comedia_.”", "\"단테를 족보 작성자로 자랑하고, 「신곡」까지 거슬러 올라가 가문을 따질 수 있는 사람이라네.\""]], [["“A nobility of the rope!”", "\"교수형 밧줄 귀족이지.\""], ["said Château-Renaud phlegmatically.", "샤토르노가 무덤덤하게 말했다."]], [["“He will be condemned, will he not?”", "\"그자, 유죄 선고를 받겠지, 안 그런가?\""], ["asked Debray of Beauchamp.", "드브레가 보샹에게 물었다."]], [["“My dear fellow, I think we should ask you that question; you know such news much better than we do.", "\"이보게, 그 질문이라면 우리가 자네에게 해야 할 것 같은데. 그런 소식은 우리보다 자네가 훨씬 더 잘 알지 않나."], ["Did you see the president at the minister’s last night?”", "어젯밤 장관 댁에서 재판장을 보았나?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"보았네.\""]], [["“What did he say?”", "\"뭐라던가?\""]], [["“Something which will surprise you.”", "\"자넬 놀라게 할 만한 말을 하더군.\""]], [["“Oh, make haste and tell me, then; it is a long time since that has happened.”", "\"오, 그럼 어서 말해 보게. 그런 일이 일어난 건 참으로 오랜만이니.\""]], [["“Well, he told me that Benedetto, who is considered a serpent of subtlety and a giant of cunning, is really but a very commonplace, silly rascal, and altogether unworthy of the experiments that will be made on his phrenological organs after his death.”", "\"음, 그가 말하길, 교활함의 화신이자 간계의 거인으로 여겨지는 그 베네데토가 실은 지극히 평범하고 어리석은 악당에 지나지 않으며, 죽은 뒤에 그 골상 기관을 가지고 행할 실험을 받을 가치조차 전혀 없다더군.\""]], [["“Bah,”", "\"흥,\""], ["said Beauchamp, “he played the prince very well.”", "보샹이 말했다. \"공자 연기는 제법 잘하던데.\""]], [["“Yes, for you who detest those unhappy princes, Beauchamp, and are always delighted to find fault with them; but not for me, who discover a gentleman by instinct, and who scent out an aristocratic family like a very bloodhound of heraldry.”", "\"그렇겠지, 그 가엾은 공자들을 혐오하고 늘 그들에게서 흠을 잡아 즐거워하는 자네에게는 말이야, 보샹. 하지만 본능으로 신사를 알아보고, 문장학의 사냥개처럼 귀족 가문 냄새를 맡아 내는 나에게는 그렇지 않아.\""]], [["“Then you never believed in the principality?”", "\"그럼 자네는 그 공국이란 걸 한 번도 믿지 않았던 건가?\""]], [["“Yes.—in the principality, but not in the prince.”", "\"믿었지. 공국은 믿었지만, 공자는 믿지 않았어.\""]], [["“Not so bad,”", "\"제법인데.\""], ["said Beauchamp; “still, I assure you, he passed very well with many people; I saw him at the ministers’ houses.”", "보샹이 말했다. \"그래도 분명히 말하지만, 그자는 많은 사람에게 그럴듯하게 통했다네. 나는 장관들 집에서 그자를 보았어.\""]], [["“Ah, yes,” said Château-Renaud.", "\"아, 그렇지.\" 샤토르노가 말했다."], ["“The idea of thinking ministers understand anything about princes!”", "\"장관들이 공자에 대해 뭘 좀 안다고 생각하다니, 그 발상하고는!\""]], [["“There is something in what you have just said,”", "\"방금 자네 말에도 일리가 있군.\""], ["said Beauchamp, laughing.", "보샹이 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“But,”", "\"그런데,\""], ["said Debray to Beauchamp, “if I spoke to the president, _you_ must have been with the procureur.”", "드브레가 보샹에게 말했다. \"내가 재판장과 이야기했다면, 자네는 분명 검사와 함께 있었겠지.\""]], [["“It was an impossibility; for the last week M. de Villefort has secluded himself.", "\"그건 불가능했네. 지난 한 주 동안 빌포르 씨는 두문불출하고 있으니까."], ["It is natural enough; this strange chain of domestic afflictions, followed by the no less strange death of his daughter——”", "그럴 만도 하지. 그 기이한 집안의 우환이 줄을 잇더니, 그에 못지않게 기이한 딸의 죽음까지 뒤따랐으니……\""]], [["“Strange? What do you mean, Beauchamp?”", "\"기이하다고? 그게 무슨 말인가, 보샹?\""]], [["“Oh, yes; do you pretend that all this has been unobserved at the minister’s?”", "\"오, 그래. 설마 그 일이 장관 댁에서 전혀 입에 오르지 않았다고 우길 셈인가?\""], ["said Beauchamp, placing his eye-glass in his eye, where he tried to make it remain.", "보샹이 단안경을 눈에 끼워 넣으며 말했다. 그러고는 그것을 그 자리에 머물게 하려고 애썼다."]], [["“My dear sir,”", "\"이보게,\""], ["said Château-Renaud, “allow me to tell you that you do not understand that manœuvre with the eye-glass half so well as Debray.", "샤토르노가 말했다. \"자네는 그 단안경 다루는 솜씨가 드브레의 절반에도 못 미친다고 말해 두지."], ["Give him a lesson, Debray.”", "드브레, 한 수 가르쳐 주게.\""]], [["“Stay,”", "\"가만,\""], ["said Beauchamp, “surely I am not deceived.”", "보샹이 말했다. \"분명 내가 잘못 본 게 아니야.\""]], [["“What is it?”", "\"뭔데 그러나?\""]], [["“It is she!”", "\"저 여자야!\""]], [["“Whom do you mean?”", "\"누구 말인가?\""]], [["“They said she had left.”", "\"떠났다고들 하던데.\""]], [["“Mademoiselle Eugénie?”", "\"외제니 양 말인가?\""], ["said Château-Renaud; “has she returned?”", "샤토르노가 물었다. \"돌아왔다고?\""]], [["“No, but her mother.”", "\"아니, 그 어머니 말일세.\""]], [["“Madame Danglars?", "\"당글라르 부인?"], ["Nonsense! Impossible!”", "말도 안 돼!"], ["said Château-Renaud; “only ten days after the flight of her daughter, and three days from the bankruptcy of her husband?”", "그럴 리가!\" 샤토르노가 말했다. \"딸이 달아난 지 겨우 열흘에, 남편이 파산한 지 사흘밖에 안 됐는데?\""]], [["Debray colored slightly, and followed with his eyes the direction of Beauchamp’s glance.", "드브레의 얼굴이 살짝 붉어졌고, 그는 보샹의 시선이 향한 방향을 눈으로 좇았다."]], [["“Come,”", "\"이보게,\""], ["he said, “it is only a veiled lady, some foreign princess, perhaps the mother of Cavalcanti.", "그가 말했다. \"그냥 베일을 쓴 부인일 뿐이야. 어느 외국 공녀거나, 어쩌면 카발칸티의 어머니일 수도 있지."], ["But you were just speaking on a very interesting topic, Beauchamp.” “I?”", "그나저나 보샹, 자네 방금 아주 흥미로운 이야기를 꺼내던 참이었잖나.\""]], [["“Yes; you were telling us about the extraordinary death of Valentine.”", "\"내가?\" \"그래. 발랑틴의 기이한 죽음에 대해 들려주고 있었지.\""]], [["“Ah, yes, so I was.", "\"아, 그래, 그랬지."], ["But how is it that Madame de Villefort is not here?”", "그런데 빌포르 부인은 어째서 여기 와 있지 않은 걸까?\""]], [["“Poor, dear woman,”", "\"가엾고 어여쁜 부인이지.\""], ["said Debray, “she is no doubt occupied in distilling balm for the hospitals, or in making cosmetics for herself or friends.", "드브레가 말했다. \"틀림없이 병원에 보낼 향유를 증류하거나, 자신과 친구들을 위한 화장품을 만드느라 바쁠 거야."], ["Do you know she spends two or three thousand crowns a year in this amusement?", "그런 취미에 한 해 2천이나 3천 에퀴를 쓴다는 걸 아나?"], ["But I wonder she is not here.", "그래도 여기 없다니 이상하군."], ["I should have been pleased to see her, for I like her very much.”", "그 부인을 보고 싶었는데. 나는 그 부인을 아주 좋아하거든.\""]], [["“And I hate her,” said Château-Renaud.", "\"나는 그 여자를 싫어해.\" 샤토르노가 말했다."]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜?\""]], [["“I do not know.", "\"나도 모르지."], ["Why do we love? Why do we hate?", "우리가 왜 사랑하고, 왜 미워하겠나?"], ["I detest her, from antipathy.”", "그냥 반감 때문에 그 여자가 싫어.\""]], [["“Or, rather, by instinct.”", "\"아니, 그보다는 본능 때문이겠지.\""]], [["“Perhaps so.", "\"그럴지도."], ["But to return to what you were saying, Beauchamp.”", "하지만 보샹, 자네가 하던 이야기로 돌아가세.\""]], [["“Well, do you know why they die so multitudinously at M. de Villefort’s?”", "\"음, 빌포르 씨 댁에서 사람이 그렇게 떼로 죽어 나가는 이유를 아나?\""]], [["“‘Multitudinously’ is good,”", "\"'떼로'라니 적절한 표현이군.\""], ["said Château-Renaud.", "샤토르노가 말했다."]], [["“My good fellow, you’ll find the word in Saint-Simon.”", "\"이보게, 그 단어는 생시몽●●에서도 찾을 수 있다네.\""]], [["“But the thing itself is at M. de Villefort’s; but let’s get back to the subject.”", "\"하지만 정작 그 일 자체는 빌포르 씨 댁에서 벌어지고 있지. 자, 본론으로 돌아가세.\""]], [["“Talking of that,”", "\"그 이야기가 나왔으니 말인데,\""], ["said Debray, “Madame was making inquiries about that house, which for the last three months has been hung with black.”", "드브레가 말했다. \"그 부인이 그 집에 대해 캐묻더군. 지난 석 달 동안 검은 휘장을 드리우고 있는 그 집 말일세.\""]], [["“Who is Madame?” asked Château-Renaud.", "\"그 부인이 누군데?\" 샤토르노가 물었다."]], [["“The minister’s wife, _pardieu!_”", "\"장관 부인 말일세, 당연하지 않나!\""]], [["“Oh, your pardon!", "\"오, 미안하네!"], ["I never visit ministers; I leave that to the princes.”", "나는 장관들을 찾아가는 법이 없어서. 그건 공자들에게 맡겨 두지.\""]], [["“Really, you were only before sparkling, but now you are brilliant; take compassion on us, or, like Jupiter, you will wither us up.”", "\"정말이지, 자네는 아까만 해도 그저 반짝이는 정도였는데, 이제는 눈부시게 빛나는군. 우리를 좀 가엾게 여겨 주게. 안 그러면 유피테르처럼 우리를 바싹 태워 버릴 테니.\""]], [["“I will not speak again,”", "\"이제 입을 다물겠네.\""], ["said Château-Renaud; “pray have compassion upon me, and do not take up every word I say.”", "샤토르노가 말했다. \"부디 나를 가엾게 여겨, 내가 하는 말마다 일일이 꼬투리를 잡지 말아 주게.\""]], [["“Come, let us endeavor to get to the end of our story, Beauchamp; I told you that yesterday Madame made inquiries of me upon the subject; enlighten me, and I will then communicate my information to her.”", "\"자, 보샹, 이야기를 끝까지 마쳐 보세. 어제 그 부인이 이 일에 대해 내게 물었다고 했잖나. 나를 좀 깨우쳐 주게. 그러면 내가 알게 된 걸 그 부인에게 전해 줄 테니.\""]], [["“Well, gentlemen, the reason people die so multitudinously (I like the word) at M. de Villefort’s is that there is an assassin in the house!”", "\"그럼, 여러분, 빌포르 씨 댁에서 사람이 그렇게 떼로(이 단어가 마음에 드는군) 죽어 나가는 이유는, 그 집에 살인자가 있기 때문이라네!\""]], [["The two young men shuddered, for the same idea had more than once occurred to them.", "두 젊은이는 몸을 떨었다. 같은 생각이 그들에게도 한두 번이 아니게 떠올랐던 것이다."]], [["“And who is the assassin;”", "\"그래서 그 살인자가 누구란 말인가?\""], ["they asked together.", "두 사람이 동시에 물었다."]], [["“Young Edward!”", "\"꼬마 에두아르라네!\" 청중의 웃음이 터졌지만, 그것은 화자를 조금도 당황하게 하지 못했다. 그는 말을 이었다."], ["A burst of laughter from the auditors did not in the least disconcert the speaker, who continued,—“Yes, gentlemen; Edward, the infant phenomenon, who is quite an adept in the art of killing.”", "\"그래, 여러분. 에두아르라네. 사람 죽이는 기술에 통달한 그 신동 말일세.\""]], [["“You are jesting.”", "\"농담하는 거겠지.\""]], [["“Not at all.", "\"천만에."], ["I yesterday engaged a servant, who had just left M. de Villefort—I intend sending him away tomorrow, for he eats so enormously, to make up for the fast imposed upon him by his terror in that house.", "어제 나는 빌포르 씨 댁에서 막 나온 하인 하나를 고용했네 — 내일이면 내보낼 작정이지만 말이야. 그 집에서 공포에 질려 강요당했던 단식을 벌충하느라 어찌나 엄청나게 먹어 대는지."], ["Well, now listen.”", "자, 그럼 들어 보게.\""]], [["“We are listening.”", "\"듣고 있네.\""]], [["“It appears the dear child has obtained possession of a bottle containing some drug, which he every now and then uses against those who have displeased him.", "\"그 귀여운 아이가 어떤 약이 든 병 하나를 손에 넣었던 모양이야. 그러고는 자기 비위를 거스른 자들에게 그 약을 이따금 써먹었다는 거지. 처음에는 생메랑 후작 부부가 그 아이의 비위를 거슬렀어."], ["First, M.", "그래서 자기 영약을 세 방울 따랐지 — 세 방울이면 충분했다네."], ["and Madame de Saint-Méran incurred his displeasure, so he poured out three drops of his elixir—three drops were sufficient; then followed Barrois, the old servant of M.", "다음은 누아르티에 씨의 늙은 하인 바루아였어. 이 작은 악당을 가끔 퇴짜 놓곤 했거든. 그래서 그도 같은 양의 영약을 받았지."], ["Noirtier, who sometimes rebuffed this little wretch—he therefore received the same quantity of the elixir; the same happened to Valentine, of whom he was jealous; he gave her the same dose as the others, and all was over for her as well as the rest.”", "발랑틴도 마찬가지였어. 아이가 발랑틴을 질투했거든. 다른 사람들에게 준 것과 똑같은 양을 먹였고, 그렇게 발랑틴도 나머지 사람들처럼 끝장이 난 거야.\""]], [["“Why, what nonsense are you telling us?”", "\"아니, 무슨 그런 터무니없는 소릴 하나?\""], ["said Château-Renaud.", "샤토르노가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, it is an extraordinary story,”", "\"그래, 정말 별난 이야기지.\""], ["said Beauchamp; “is it not?”", "보샹이 말했다. \"안 그런가?\""]], [["“It is absurd,”", "\"말도 안 되는 소리야.\""], ["said Debray.", "드브레가 말했다."]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["said Beauchamp, “you doubt me?", "보샹이 말했다. \"나를 못 믿겠다는 건가?"], ["Well, you can ask my servant, or rather him who will no longer be my servant tomorrow, it was the talk of the house.”", "그럼 내 하인에게, 아니 내일이면 더 이상 내 하인이 아닐 그자에게 물어보게. 그 집에서 다들 하던 이야기였으니까.\""]], [["“And this elixir, where is it? what is it?”", "\"그래, 그 영약은 어디 있나? 대체 뭐란 말인가?\""]], [["“The child conceals it.”", "\"그 아이가 감추고 있지.\""]], [["“But where did he find it?”", "\"한데 어디서 그걸 찾아냈다는 건가?\""]], [["“In his mother’s laboratory.”", "\"제 어머니의 실험실에서.\""]], [["“Does his mother then, keep poisons in her laboratory?”", "\"그럼 그 아이 어머니는 실험실에 독약을 보관해 둔단 말인가?\""]], [["“How can I tell?", "\"내가 어찌 알겠나?"], ["You are questioning me like a king’s attorney.", "자네는 마치 검사처럼 나를 추궁하는군."], ["I only repeat what I have been told, and like my informant I can do no more.", "나는 그저 들은 대로 옮길 뿐, 내게 일러 준 자처럼 그 이상은 할 수 없네."], ["The poor devil would eat nothing, from fear.”", "그 가엾은 친구는 겁에 질려 아무것도 먹질 못하더군.\""]], [["“It is incredible!”", "\"믿기지 않는 일이야!\""]], [["“No, my dear fellow, it is not at all incredible.", "\"아니, 이보게, 전혀 믿기지 않을 일도 아니라네."], ["You saw the child pass through the Rue Richelieu last year, who amused himself with killing his brothers and sisters by sticking pins in their ears while they slept.", "작년에 리슐리외 거리를 지나가던 그 아이를 보지 않았나. 형제자매들이 자는 동안 귀에 핀을 찔러 죽이며 즐거워하던 아이 말일세."], ["The generation who follow us are very precocious.”", "우리 뒤를 잇는 세대는 아주 조숙하다네.\""]], [["“Come, Beauchamp,”", "\"이보게, 보샹,\""], ["said Château-Renaud, “I will bet anything you do not believe a word of all you have been telling us.", "샤토르노가 말했다. \"자네가 지금까지 늘어놓은 이야기를 한마디도 믿지 않는다는 데 뭐든 걸겠네."], ["But I do not see the Count of Monte Cristo here.”", "그건 그렇고, 여기 몬테크리스토 백작이 안 보이는군.\""]], [["“He is worn out,”", "\"그는 기진맥진해 있어.\" 드브레가 말했다."], ["said Debray; “besides, he could not well appear in public, since he has been the dupe of the Cavalcanti, who, it appears, presented themselves to him with false letters of credit, and cheated him out of 100,000 francs upon the hypothesis of this principality.”", "\"게다가 사람들 앞에 나서기도 어려운 처지지. 카발칸티 부자에게 속아 넘어갔으니 말이야. 듣자 하니 그자들이 위조 신용장을 들고 그를 찾아가서는, 이 공국이라는 허상을 빌미로 10만 프랑을 가로챘다더군.\""]], [["“By the way, M. de Château-Renaud,”", "\"그나저나, 샤토르노 씨,\""], ["asked Beauchamp, “how is Morrel?”", "보샹이 물었다. \"모렐은 좀 어떤가?\""]], [["“_Ma foi_, I have called three times without once seeing him.", "\"맹세컨대, 세 번이나 찾아갔는데 한 번도 만나지 못했네."], ["Still, his sister did not seem uneasy, and told me that though she had not seen him for two or three days, she was sure he was well.”", "그래도 그 누이는 별로 걱정하는 기색이 없더군. 이삼 일 동안 그를 보지 못했지만 분명 잘 있을 거라고 했어.\""]], [["“Ah, now I think of it, the Count of Monte Cristo cannot appear in the hall,”", "\"아, 이제 생각났는데, 몬테크리스토 백작은 이 법정에 나타날 수 없겠군.\""], ["said Beauchamp.", "보샹이 말했다."]], [["“Why not?”", "\"왜 안 되나?\""]], [["“Because he is an actor in the drama.”", "\"이 드라마의 배우 가운데 하나니까.\""]], [["“Has he assassinated anyone, then?”", "\"그럼 그가 누구라도 죽였단 말인가?\""]], [["“No, on the contrary, they wished to assassinate him.", "\"아니, 오히려 사람들이 그를 죽이려 했지."], ["You know that it was in leaving his house that M. de Caderousse was murdered by his friend Benedetto.", "카드루스 씨가 그 친구 베네데토에게 살해당한 게 바로 그의 집을 나서던 길에서였다는 걸 알지 않나."], ["You know that the famous waistcoat was found in his house, containing the letter which stopped the signature of the marriage-contract.", "그 유명한 조끼가 그의 집에서 발견됐고, 그 안에 결혼 계약서 서명을 멈추게 한 편지가 들어 있었다는 것도 알 거야."], ["Do you see the waistcoat?", "그 조끼가 보이나?"], ["There it is, all blood-stained, on the desk, as a testimony of the crime.”", "저기 있군. 온통 피로 얼룩진 채, 범죄의 증거로 책상 위에 놓여 있어.\""]], [["“Ah, very good.”", "\"아, 잘됐군.\""]], [["“Hush, gentlemen, here is the court; let us go back to our places.”", "\"쉿, 여러분, 재판부가 들어오네. 우리 자리로 돌아가세.\""]], [["A noise was heard in the hall; the sergeant called his two patrons with an energetic “hem!”", "법정 안에서 소란이 일었다. 정리가 힘찬 \"에헴!\""], ["and the door-keeper appearing, called out with that shrill voice peculiar to his order, ever since the days of Beaumarchais:", "소리로 자기가 모시는 두 사람을 불렀고, 문지기가 나타나 보마르셰 시절 이래로 그 직분 특유의 째지는 목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“The court, gentlemen!”", "\"재판부 입정, 여러분!\""]], [["# Chapter 110. The Indictment", "# Chapter 110. The Indictment"]], [["The judges took their places in the midst of the most profound silence; the jury took their seats; M.", "● 팔레: 파리의 법원을 가리킨다."], ["de Villefort, the object of unusual attention, and we had almost said of general admiration, sat in the armchair and cast a tranquil glance around him. Everyone looked with astonishment on that grave and severe face, whose calm expression personal griefs had been unable to disturb, and the aspect of a man who was a stranger to all human emotions excited something very like terror.", "―옮긴이 ●● 생시몽: 17~18세기 프랑스의 회고록 작가. ―옮긴이"]], [["“Gendarmes,” said the president, “lead in the accused.”", "깊디깊은 정적 속에서 판사들이 자리에 앉았다."]], [["At these words the public attention became more intense, and all eyes were turned towards the door through which Benedetto was to enter.", "배심원들도 자리를 잡았다. 유난한 주목, 거의 만인의 찬탄이라 해도 좋을 시선을 한 몸에 받으며 빌포르 씨가 안락의자에 앉아 차분한 눈길로 주위를 둘러보았다. 모두가 그 근엄하고 준엄한 얼굴을 놀란 눈으로 바라보았다."], ["The door soon opened and the accused appeared.", "개인적 슬픔조차 흐트러뜨리지 못한 그 평온한 표정, 인간의 모든 감정과 무관해 보이는 그 사람의 모습은 거의 공포에 가까운 무언가를 불러일으켰다. \"헌병들, 피고인을 들여보내시오.\" 재판장이 말했다."]], [["The same impression was experienced by all present, and no one was deceived by the expression of his countenance.", "이 말에 방청객의 주의가 한층 팽팽해졌고, 모든 눈이 베네데토가 들어올 문 쪽으로 쏠렸다. 이윽고 문이 열리고 피고인이 모습을 드러냈다."], ["His features bore no sign of that deep emotion which stops the beating of the heart and blanches the cheek.", "그 자리에 있던 모두가 같은 인상을 받았고, 그의 표정에 속는 사람은 아무도 없었다."], ["His hands, gracefully placed, one upon his hat, the other in the opening of his white waistcoat, were not at all tremulous; his eye was calm and even brilliant.", "그의 얼굴에는 심장의 고동을 멎게 하고 두 뺨을 핏기 없이 만드는 그 깊은 동요의 기색이 조금도 없었다. 한 손은 모자 위에, 다른 손은 흰 조끼의 트인 곳에 우아하게 얹은 그의 두 손은 전혀 떨리지 않았다."], ["Scarcely had he entered the hall when he glanced at the whole body of magistrates and assistants; his eye rested longer on the president, and still more so on the king’s attorney.", "그의 눈은 차분했고, 심지어 빛나기까지 했다. 그는 법정에 들어서기가 무섭게 판사단과 보조진 전체를 한번 훑었다. 그의 눈은 재판장에게 좀 더 오래 머물렀고, 검사에게는 그보다 더 오래 머물렀다."]], [["By the side of Andrea was stationed the lawyer who was to conduct his defence, and who had been appointed by the court, for Andrea disdained to pay any attention to those details, to which he appeared to attach no importance.", "안드레아 곁에는 그의 변호를 맡을 변호사가 서 있었다. 법원이 선임한 변호사였는데, 안드레아가 그런 세세한 일에는 조금도 관심을 두려 하지 않았고, 아무런 중요성도 부여하지 않는 듯 보였기 때문이다."], ["The lawyer was a young man with light hair whose face expressed a hundred times more emotion than that which characterized the prisoner.", "변호사는 머리가 옅은 색인 젊은이로, 그 얼굴은 피고인의 표정보다 백 배는 더한 동요를 드러내고 있었다."]], [["50181m\nThe president called for the indictment, revised as we know, by the clever and implacable pen of Villefort.", "재판장이 공소장 낭독을 요청했다. 우리가 알다시피 빌포르의 영민하고 가차 없는 펜으로 수정된 그 공소장이었다."], ["During the reading of this, which was long, the public attention was continually drawn towards Andrea, who bore the inspection with Spartan unconcern.", "긴 낭독이 이어지는 동안 방청객의 주의는 끊임없이 안드레아에게 쏠렸으나, 그는 스파르타인 같은 무심함으로 그 시선을 견뎌 냈다."], ["Villefort had never been so concise and eloquent.", "빌포르가 그토록 간결하고 능변이었던 적은 없었다."], ["The crime was depicted in the most vivid colors; the former life of the prisoner, his transformation, a review of his life from the earliest period, were set forth with all the talent that a knowledge of human life could furnish to a mind like that of the procureur.", "범죄는 더없이 생생한 색채로 묘사되었다. 피고인의 지난 삶, 그 변모, 가장 이른 시기부터의 그의 인생 전반에 대한 검토가, 인간 삶에 대한 지식이 검사 같은 정신에게 제공할 수 있는 온갖 재능을 다해 펼쳐졌다."], ["Benedetto was thus forever condemned in public opinion before the sentence of the law could be pronounced.", "이렇게 해서 베네데토는 법의 선고가 내려지기도 전에 여론 속에서 영원히 단죄되었다."]], [["Andrea paid no attention to the successive charges which were brought against him. M.", "안드레아는 자신에게 잇달아 제기되는 혐의들에 아무런 주의도 기울이지 않았다."], ["de Villefort, who examined him attentively, and who no doubt practiced upon him all the psychological studies he was accustomed to use, in vain endeavored to make him lower his eyes, notwithstanding the depth and profundity of his gaze.", "그를 유심히 살피며, 의심할 바 없이 평소 즐겨 쓰던 온갖 심리적 기술을 그에게 시험하던 빌포르 씨는, 그 깊고도 그윽한 눈빛에도 불구하고 그의 눈을 내리깔게 하려 애썼으나 헛수고였다."], ["At length the reading of the indictment was ended.", "마침내 공소장 낭독이 끝났다."]], [["“Accused,” said the president, “your name and surname?”", "\"피고인, 성과 이름은?\" 재판장이 말했다."]], [["Andrea arose.", "안드레아가 일어섰다."]], [["“Excuse me, Mr. President,”", "\"실례합니다, 재판장님.\""], ["he said, in a clear voice, “but I see you are going to adopt a course of questions through which I cannot follow you.", "그가 또렷한 목소리로 말했다. \"그런데 제가 따라갈 수 없는 방식으로 질문을 진행하시려는 것 같군요."], ["I have an idea, which I will explain by and by, of making an exception to the usual form of accusation.", "저는 통상적인 심문 형식에 예외를 두려는 생각이 있고, 그것은 차차 설명드리겠습니다."], ["Allow me, then, if you please, to answer in different order, or I will not do so at all.”", "그러니 부디 제가 다른 순서로 답하도록 허락해 주십시오. 아니면 아예 답하지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["The astonished president looked at the jury, who in turn looked at Villefort.", "놀란 재판장이 배심원들을 바라보았고, 배심원들은 다시 빌포르를 바라보았다."], ["The whole assembly manifested great surprise, but Andrea appeared quite unmoved.", "좌중 전체가 크게 놀랐으나, 안드레아는 조금도 동요하지 않는 듯했다."]], [["“Your age?”", "\"나이는?\""], ["said the president; “will you answer that question?”", "재판장이 말했다. \"이 질문에는 답하겠소?\""]], [["“I will answer that question, as well as the rest, Mr.", "\"그 질문에도, 나머지 질문에도 답하겠습니다, 재판장님."], ["President, but in its turn.”", "다만 차례가 되었을 때 말입니다.\""]], [["“Your age?”", "\"나이는?\""], ["repeated the president.", "재판장이 거듭 물었다."]], [["“I am twenty-one years old, or rather I shall be in a few days, as I was born the night of the 27th of September, 1817.”", "\"저는 스물한 살입니다. 아니, 정확히는 며칠 후에 그렇게 됩니다. 저는 1817년 9월 27일 밤에 태어났으니까요.\""]], [["M. de Villefort, who was busy taking down some notes, raised his head at the mention of this date.", "뭔가 메모를 적느라 분주하던 빌포르 씨가 이 날짜가 언급되자 고개를 들었다."]], [["“Where were you born?”", "\"어디서 태어났소?\""], ["continued the president.", "재판장이 계속 물었다."]], [["“At Auteuil, near Paris.”", "\"파리 근교 오퇴유에서요.\""]], [["M. de Villefort a second time raised his head, looked at Benedetto as if he had been gazing at the head of Medusa, and became livid.", "빌포르 씨가 두 번째로 고개를 들고, 마치 메두사의 머리라도 응시하듯 베네데토를 바라보았다. 그러더니 얼굴이 새파랗게 질렸다."], ["As for Benedetto, he gracefully wiped his lips with a fine cambric pocket-handkerchief.", "베네데토로 말하자면, 고운 마직 손수건으로 우아하게 입술을 닦았다."]], [["“Your profession?”", "\"직업은?\""]], [["“First I was a forger,”", "\"처음에는 위조범이었습니다.\""], ["answered Andrea, as calmly as possible; “then I became a thief, and lately have become an assassin.”", "안드레아가 더없이 차분하게 대답했다. \"그다음에는 도둑이 되었고, 최근에는 살인자가 되었지요.\""]], [["A murmur, or rather storm, of indignation burst from all parts of the assembly.", "좌중 곳곳에서 분노의 웅성거림이, 아니 폭풍이 터져 나왔다."], ["The judges themselves appeared to be stupefied, and the jury manifested tokens of disgust for a cynicism so unexpected in a man of fashion. M.", "판사들마저 망연자실한 듯했고, 배심원들은 멋쟁이 신사한테서 나오리라고는 전혀 예상치 못한 그 냉소에 혐오의 기색을 드러냈다. 빌포르 씨는 처음에 핏기가 없다가 이내 붉어지고 화끈거리던 이마를 손으로 짚었다."], ["de Villefort pressed his hand upon his brow, which, at first pale, had become red and burning; then he suddenly arose and looked around as though he had lost his senses—he wanted air. 50183m", "그러더니 갑자기 일어나 정신을 잃기라도 한 사람처럼 주위를 둘러보았다. 그는 바람이 필요했다."]], [["“Are you looking for anything, Mr.", "\"뭔가 찾으십니까, 검사님?\""], ["Procureur?” asked Benedetto, with his most ingratiating smile.", "베네데토가 더없이 비위 맞추는 미소를 띠며 물었다."]], [["M. de Villefort answered nothing, but sat, or rather threw himself down again upon his chair.", "빌포르 씨는 아무 대답도 하지 않고, 의자에 다시 앉았다. 아니, 차라리 몸을 던지듯 주저앉았다."]], [["“And now, prisoner, will you consent to tell your name?”", "\"자, 그럼 피고인, 이제 이름을 말하겠소?\""], ["said the president.", "재판장이 말했다."], ["“The brutal affectation with which you have enumerated and classified your crimes calls for a severe reprimand on the part of the court, both in the name of morality, and for the respect due to humanity.", "\"그대가 자신의 죄목을 열거하고 분류한 그 잔인한 허세는, 도덕의 이름으로도, 인간에게 마땅히 바쳐야 할 존중을 위해서도, 본 법정으로부터 엄중한 질책을 받아 마땅하오."], ["You appear to consider this a point of honor, and it may be for this reason, that you have delayed acknowledging your name.", "그대는 이것을 명예의 문제로 여기는 듯한데, 어쩌면 그런 이유로 이름 밝히기를 미룬 것이겠지."], ["You wished it to be preceded by all these titles.”", "그 이름 앞에 이 모든 직함이 붙기를 바란 게요.\""]], [["“It is quite wonderful, Mr.", "\"참으로 놀랍습니다, 재판장님."], ["President, how entirely you have read my thoughts,”", "제 생각을 그토록 속속들이 읽어 내시다니요.\""], ["said Benedetto, in his softest voice and most polite manner.", "베네데토가 더없이 부드러운 목소리와 더없이 정중한 태도로 말했다."], ["“This is, indeed, the reason why I begged you to alter the order of the questions.”", "\"바로 그것이 제가 질문의 순서를 바꿔 달라고 청한 이유입니다.\""]], [["The public astonishment had reached its height.", "방청객의 놀라움은 절정에 달했다."], ["There was no longer any deceit or bravado in the manner of the accused.", "이제 피고인의 태도에는 기만도 허세도 없었다."], ["The audience felt that a startling revelation was to follow this ominous prelude.", "좌중은 이 불길한 서막에 뒤이어 놀라운 폭로가 따르리라는 것을 직감했다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"좋소.\""], ["said the president; “your name?”", "재판장이 말했다. \"이름은?\""]], [["“I cannot tell you my name, since I do not know it; but I know my father’s, and can tell it to you.”", "\"제 이름은 말씀드릴 수 없습니다. 저도 모르니까요. 하지만 제 아버지의 이름은 압니다. 그것은 말씀드릴 수 있지요.\""]], [["A painful giddiness overwhelmed Villefort; great drops of acrid sweat fell from his face upon the papers which he held in his convulsed hand.", "고통스러운 현기증이 빌포르를 덮쳤다. 발작하듯 떨리는 손에 쥔 서류 위로 그의 얼굴에서 떨어진 굵은 식은땀 방울이 떨어졌다."]], [["“Repeat your father’s name,”", "\"아버지의 이름을 말하시오.\""], ["said the president.", "재판장이 말했다."]], [["Not a whisper, not a breath, was heard in that vast assembly; everyone waited anxiously.", "그 광대한 좌중에서는 속삭임 하나, 숨소리 하나 들리지 않았다. 모두가 마음 졸이며 기다렸다."]], [["“My father is king’s attorney,”", "\"제 아버지는 검사입니다.\""], ["replied Andrea calmly.", "안드레아가 차분히 대답했다."]], [["50179m\n“King’s attorney?”", "\"검사라고?\""], ["said the president, stupefied, and without noticing the agitation which spread over the face of M. de Villefort; “king’s attorney?”", "재판장이 빌포르 씨의 얼굴에 번지는 동요는 알아차리지도 못한 채 망연자실해 말했다. \"검사라니?\""]], [["“Yes; and if you wish to know his name, I will tell it,—he is named Villefort.”", "\"네. 그리고 그분의 이름을 알고 싶으시다면 말씀드리지요. 그분의 이름은 빌포르입니다.\""]], [["The explosion, which had been so long restrained from a feeling of respect to the court of justice, now burst forth like thunder from the breasts of all present; the court itself did not seek to restrain the feelings of the audience.", "법정에 대한 존중의 마음에서 그토록 오래 억눌려 있던 폭발이, 이제 그 자리에 있던 모든 이들의 가슴에서 천둥처럼 터져 나왔다. 법정 자체도 방청객의 격정을 억누르려 하지 않았다."], ["The exclamations, the insults addressed to Benedetto, who remained perfectly unconcerned, the energetic gestures, the movement of the gendarmes, the sneers of the scum of the crowd always sure to rise to the surface in case of any disturbance—all this lasted five minutes, before the door-keepers and magistrates were able to restore silence.", "베네데토를 향해 쏟아지는 외침과 욕설—그는 더없이 태연했다—거센 손짓, 헌병들의 움직임, 소란이 일 때면 으레 표면으로 떠오르는 군중 속 천한 무리의 비웃음, 이 모든 것이 문지기와 판사들이 정적을 되찾기까지 오 분간 이어졌다."], ["In the midst of this tumult the voice of the president was heard to exclaim:", "이 소동 한가운데서 재판장의 목소리가 외치는 것이 들렸다."]], [["“Are you playing with justice, accused, and do you dare set your fellow-citizens an example of disorder which even in these times has never been equalled?”", "\"피고인, 그대는 사법을 희롱하는 것인가? 그래서 감히 이 시대에도 유례없던 무질서의 본을 동료 시민들에게 보이려는 것인가?\""]], [["Several persons hurried up to M. de Villefort, who sat half bowed over in his chair, offering him consolation, encouragement, and protestations of zeal and sympathy.", "몇 사람이 의자에 반쯤 엎드려 있는 빌포르 씨에게로 황급히 다가가 위로와 격려, 그리고 열의와 동정의 말을 건넸다. 법정은 질서를 되찾았다."], ["Order was re-established in the hall, except that a few people still moved about and whispered to one another.", "다만 몇몇 사람들은 여전히 자리를 옮겨 다니며 서로 속삭였다. 어떤 부인이 방금 기절했다는 말이 돌았다."], ["A lady, it was said, had just fainted; they had supplied her with a smelling-bottle, and she had recovered.", "사람들이 정신 들게 하는 약병을 건네주었고, 그녀는 정신을 차렸다."], ["During the scene of tumult, Andrea had turned his smiling face towards the assembly; then, leaning with one hand on the oaken rail of the dock, in the most graceful attitude possible, he said:", "소란이 이는 동안 안드레아는 미소 띤 얼굴을 좌중으로 돌렸다. 그러더니 한 손을 피고인석의 떡갈나무 난간에 짚고, 더없이 우아한 자세로 말했다."]], [["“Gentlemen, I assure you I had no idea of insulting the court, or of making a useless disturbance in the presence of this honorable assembly.", "\"여러분, 저는 결코 법정을 모욕하거나 이 명예로운 좌중 앞에서 쓸데없는 소란을 일으킬 생각이 없었음을 분명히 말씀드립니다."], ["They ask my age; I tell it. They ask where I was born; I answer.", "제 나이를 물으시기에 답했고, 어디서 태어났느냐 물으시기에 답했습니다."], ["They ask my name, I cannot give it, since my parents abandoned me.", "이름을 물으셨지만, 부모가 저를 버렸기에 답할 수 없습니다."], ["But though I cannot give my own name, not possessing one, I can tell them my father’s.", "그러나 제 이름은 가진 것이 없어 말할 수 없어도, 제 아버지의 이름은 말씀드릴 수 있습니다."], ["Now I repeat, my father is named M. de Villefort, and I am ready to prove it.”", "거듭 말하건대, 제 아버지의 이름은 빌포르 씨이고, 저는 그것을 입증할 준비가 되어 있습니다.\""]], [["There was an energy, a conviction, and a sincerity in the manner of the young man, which silenced the tumult.", "그 젊은이의 태도에는 힘과 확신과 진실함이 있어, 소란을 잠재웠다."], ["All eyes were turned for a moment towards the procureur, who sat as motionless as though a thunderbolt had changed him into a corpse.", "모든 눈이 잠시 검사에게로 향했다. 그는 벼락이라도 맞아 시체로 변한 듯 미동도 없이 앉아 있었다."]], [["“Gentlemen,”", "\"여러분.\""], ["said Andrea, commanding silence by his voice and manner; “I owe you the proofs and explanations of what I have said.”", "안드레아가 목소리와 태도로 좌중을 침묵시키며 말했다. \"제가 한 말에 대해 증거와 설명을 드려야 마땅하겠지요.\""]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만.\""], ["said the irritated president, “you called yourself Benedetto, declared yourself an orphan, and claimed Corsica as your country.”", "격앙된 재판장이 말했다. \"그대는 스스로를 베네데토라 칭했고, 고아라고 했으며, 코르시카를 고향이라 주장하지 않았소.\""]], [["“I said anything I pleased, in order that the solemn declaration I have just made should not be withheld, which otherwise would certainly have been the case.", "\"제가 방금 한 엄숙한 진술이 가로막히지 않도록 하기 위해 닥치는 대로 둘러댄 것입니다. 그렇게 하지 않았다면 분명 가로막혔을 테니까요."], ["I now repeat that I was born at Auteuil on the night of the 27th of September, 1817, and that I am the son of the procureur, M. de Villefort.", "다시 말씀드리지만, 저는 1817년 9월 27일 밤 오퇴유에서 태어났고, 검사 빌포르 씨의 아들입니다."], ["Do you wish for any further details?", "더 상세한 내용을 원하십니까?"], ["I will give them.", "말씀드리지요."], ["I was born in No. 28, Rue de la Fontaine, in a room hung with red damask; my father took me in his arms, telling my mother I was dead, wrapped me in a napkin marked with an H and an N, and carried me into a garden, where he buried me alive.”", "저는 퐁텐 거리 28번지, 붉은 다마스크 천이 드리워진 방에서 태어났습니다. 제 아버지는 어머니에게 제가 죽었다고 말하며 저를 두 팔에 안아 들었고, H와 N이라는 글자가 새겨진 보자기에 저를 싸서 정원으로 데려가 산 채로 묻었습니다.\""]], [["A shudder ran through the assembly when they saw that the confidence of the prisoner increased in proportion to the terror of M. de Villefort.", "피고인의 자신감이 빌포르 씨의 공포에 비례해 커지는 것을 보자 좌중에 전율이 흘렀다."]], [["“But how have you become acquainted with all these details?”", "\"한데 그대는 이 모든 상세한 내막을 어떻게 알게 되었소?\""], ["asked the president.", "재판장이 물었다."]], [["“I will tell you, Mr.", "\"말씀드리지요, 재판장님."], ["President. A man who had sworn vengeance against my father, and had long watched his opportunity to kill him, had introduced himself that night into the garden in which my father buried me.", "제 아버지에게 복수를 맹세하고 오랫동안 그를 죽일 기회를 노리던 한 사내가, 그날 밤 아버지가 저를 묻은 그 정원에 잠입해 있었습니다. 그는 덤불 속에 몸을 숨기고 있었지요."], ["He was concealed in a thicket; he saw my father bury something in the ground, and stabbed him; then thinking the deposit might contain some treasure he turned up the ground, and found me still living.", "그는 제 아버지가 땅에 무언가를 묻는 것을 보고 그를 칼로 찔렀습니다. 그러고는 그 묻힌 것에 무슨 보물이라도 들어 있을까 싶어 땅을 파헤쳤고, 아직 살아 있는 저를 발견했습니다."], ["The man carried me to the foundling asylum, where I was registered under the number 37.", "그 사내는 저를 기아 보호소로 데려갔고, 그곳에서 저는 37번으로 등록되었습니다."], ["Three months afterwards, a woman travelled from Rogliano to Paris to fetch me, and having claimed me as her son, carried me away.", "석 달 뒤 한 여인이 저를 데리러 롤리아노에서 파리로 와, 저를 자기 아들이라고 주장하며 데려갔습니다."], ["Thus, you see, though born in Paris, I was brought up in Corsica.”", "그래서 보시다시피 저는 파리에서 태어났으나 코르시카에서 자랐습니다.\""]], [["There was a moment’s silence, during which one could have fancied the hall empty, so profound was the stillness.", "잠시 침묵이 흘렀고, 그동안 정적이 어찌나 깊었던지 법정이 텅 빈 듯한 착각이 들 정도였다."]], [["“Proceed,” said the president.", "\"계속하시오.\" 재판장이 말했다."]], [["“Certainly, I might have lived happily amongst those good people, who adored me, but my perverse disposition prevailed over the virtues which my adopted mother endeavored to instil into my heart.", "\"분명 저는 저를 끔찍이 아껴 주던 그 선한 사람들 틈에서 행복하게 살 수도 있었습니다. 그러나 제 비뚤어진 천성이, 양어머니가 제 마음속에 심으려 애쓴 미덕들을 이겨 버렸습니다."], ["I increased in wickedness till I committed crime.", "저는 점점 더 사악해져 마침내 범죄를 저질렀습니다."], ["One day when I cursed Providence for making me so wicked, and ordaining me to such a fate, my adopted father said to me, ‘Do not blaspheme, unhappy child, the crime is that of your father, not yours,—of your father, who consigned you to hell if you died, and to misery if a miracle preserved you alive.’ After that I ceased to blaspheme, but I cursed my father.", "어느 날 제가 저를 이토록 사악하게 만들고 이런 운명에 처하게 한 섭리를 저주하자, 양아버지가 제게 말했습니다. '신을 모독하지 마라, 가엾은 아이야. 그 죄는 네 아버지의 죄이지 네 죄가 아니다."], ["That is why I have uttered the words for which you blame me; that is why I have filled this whole assembly with horror.", "네가 죽으면 지옥에, 기적으로 살아남으면 비참에 너를 내던진 네 아버지의 죄란다.' 그 뒤로 저는 신을 모독하기를 그쳤으나, 제 아버지를 저주했습니다."], ["If I have committed an additional crime, punish me, but if you will allow that ever since the day of my birth my fate has been sad, bitter, and lamentable, then pity me.”", "그래서 제가 여러분이 저를 책망하시는 그 말을 입에 올린 것이고, 그래서 제가 이 좌중 전체를 공포로 채운 것입니다. 제가 거기에 더해 또 다른 죄를 지었다면 저를 벌하십시오. 그러나 제가 태어난 날부터 제 운명이 슬프고 쓰라리고 한탄스러웠음을 인정하신다면, 저를 가엾이 여겨 주십시오.\""]], [["“But your mother?” asked the president.", "\"하지만 그대의 어머니는?\" 재판장이 물었다."]], [["“My mother thought me dead; she is not guilty.", "\"제 어머니는 제가 죽은 줄 알았습니다. 그분에게는 죄가 없습니다."], ["I did not even wish to know her name, nor do I know it.”", "저는 그분의 이름을 알고 싶지도 않았고, 지금도 모릅니다.\""]], [["Just then a piercing cry, ending in a sob, burst from the centre of the crowd, who encircled the lady who had before fainted, and who now fell into a violent fit of hysterics.", "바로 그때 흐느낌으로 끝맺는 날카로운 비명이 군중 한가운데서 터져 나왔다. 사람들이 둘러싼 그 부인, 앞서 기절했던 그 부인이 이제 격렬한 발작을 일으킨 것이었다."], ["She was carried out of the hall, the thick veil which concealed her face dropped off, and Madame Danglars was recognized.", "그녀가 법정 밖으로 실려 나갈 때 얼굴을 가리고 있던 두꺼운 베일이 흘러내렸고, 사람들은 그녀가 당글라르 부인임을 알아보았다."], ["Notwithstanding his shattered nerves, the ringing sensation in his ears, and the madness which turned his brain, Villefort rose as he perceived her.", "만신창이가 된 신경에도, 귓속에서 울리는 윙윙거림에도, 머릿속을 미치게 하는 혼란에도 불구하고, 빌포르는 그녀를 알아보고 일어섰다."]], [["“The proofs, the proofs!”", "\"증거를, 증거를 대시오!\""], ["said the president; “remember this tissue of horrors must be supported by the clearest proofs.”", "재판장이 말했다. \"이 끔찍한 이야기의 얼개는 더없이 명백한 증거로 뒷받침되어야 함을 명심하시오.\""]], [["“The proofs?”", "\"증거요?\""], ["said Benedetto, laughing; “do you want proofs?”", "베네데토가 웃으며 말했다. \"증거를 원하십니까?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["“Well, then, look at M. de Villefort, and then ask me for proofs.”", "\"그렇다면 빌포르 씨를 보십시오. 그런 다음 제게 증거를 청하십시오.\""]], [["Everyone turned towards the procureur, who, unable to bear the universal gaze now riveted on him alone, advanced staggering into the midst of the tribunal, with his hair dishevelled and his face indented with the mark of his nails.", "모두가 검사 쪽으로 고개를 돌렸다. 이제 자신에게만 못 박힌 만인의 시선을 견딜 수 없었던 그는, 머리카락이 헝클어지고 손톱자국이 패인 얼굴로 비틀거리며 법정 한가운데로 나아갔다."], ["The whole assembly uttered a long murmur of astonishment.", "좌중 전체가 길게 놀라움의 웅성거림을 토해 냈다."]], [["“Father,”", "\"아버지.\""], ["said Benedetto, “I am asked for proofs, do you wish me to give them?”", "베네데토가 말했다. \"사람들이 제게 증거를 청합니다. 제가 그것을 대기를 바라십니까?\""]], [["“No, no, it is useless,”", "\"아니, 아니, 그럴 필요 없다.\""], ["stammered M. de Villefort in a hoarse voice; “no, it is useless!”", "빌포르 씨가 쉰 목소리로 더듬거렸다. \"아니, 그럴 필요 없어!\""]], [["“How useless?”", "\"필요 없다니?\""], ["cried the president, “what do you mean?”", "재판장이 외쳤다. \"그게 무슨 말이오?\""]], [["“I mean that I feel it impossible to struggle against this deadly weight which crushes me.", "\"저를 짓누르는 이 죽음 같은 무게에 맞서 싸우는 일이 불가능하다는 뜻입니다."], ["Gentlemen, I know I am in the hands of an avenging God!", "여러분, 저는 제가 복수하시는 신의 손안에 있음을 압니다!"], ["We need no proofs; everything relating to this young man is true.”", "증거는 필요 없습니다. 이 젊은이와 관련된 모든 것이 사실입니다.\""]], [["A dull, gloomy silence, like that which precedes some awful phenomenon of nature, pervaded the assembly, who shuddered in dismay.", "자연의 어떤 무서운 현상에 앞서는 듯한, 음울하고 무거운 침묵이 좌중에 감돌았고, 사람들은 경악하여 몸을 떨었다."]], [["“What, M. de Villefort,”", "\"아니, 빌포르 씨.\""], ["cried the president, “do you yield to an hallucination?", "재판장이 외쳤다. \"환각에 굴복하시는 겁니까?"], ["What, are you no longer in possession of your senses?", "이미 제정신이 아니신 게요?"], ["This strange, unexpected, terrible accusation has disordered your reason. Come, recover.”", "이 기이하고 뜻밖이며 끔찍한 고발이 그대의 이성을 어지럽혔구려. 자, 정신을 차리시오.\""]], [["The procureur dropped his head; his teeth chattered like those of a man under a violent attack of fever, and yet he was deadly pale.", "검사는 고개를 떨구었다. 그의 이는 격렬한 열병에 시달리는 사람처럼 딱딱 부딪쳤으나, 그럼에도 그의 얼굴은 죽은 사람처럼 창백했다."]], [["“I am in possession of all my senses, sir,”", "\"저는 온전히 제정신입니다, 재판장님.\""], ["he said; “my body alone suffers, as you may suppose.", "그가 말했다. \"짐작하시다시피 고통받는 것은 제 몸뿐입니다."], ["I acknowledge myself guilty of all the young man has brought against me, and from this hour hold myself under the authority of the procureur who will succeed me.”", "저는 이 젊은이가 제게 제기한 모든 것에 대해 제가 유죄임을 인정하며, 지금 이 시각부터 저를 이어받을 검사의 권한 아래 두겠습니다.\""]], [["And as he spoke these words with a hoarse, choking voice, he staggered towards the door, which was mechanically opened by a door-keeper.", "그리고 그는 쉬고 메인 목소리로 이 말을 내뱉으며, 문지기가 기계적으로 열어 준 문을 향해 비틀비틀 걸어갔다."], ["The whole assembly were dumb with astonishment at the revelation and confession which had produced a catastrophe so different from that which had been expected during the last fortnight by the Parisian world.", "좌중 전체가 그 폭로와 자백에 너무도 놀라 말을 잃었다. 지난 보름간 파리 사교계가 예상하던 것과는 너무도 다른 파국을 빚어낸 폭로요 자백이었다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"자.\""], ["said Beauchamp, “let them now say that drama is unnatural!”", "보샹이 말했다. \"이래도 사람들이 연극은 작위적이라고 할 수 있을까!\""]], [["“_Ma foi!_”", "\"맹세코,\""], ["said Château-Renaud, “I would rather end my career like M.", "샤토르노가 말했다. \"나라면 차라리 모르세르 씨처럼 생을 끝내겠네."], ["de Morcerf; a pistol-shot seems quite delightful compared with this catastrophe.”", "권총 한 발이 이 파국에 비하면 아주 유쾌해 보이는걸.\""]], [["“And moreover, it kills,” said Beauchamp.", "\"게다가 그건 적어도 죽기라도 하지.\" 보샹이 말했다."]], [["“And to think that I had an idea of marrying his daughter,”", "\"그리고 내가 그 사람 딸과 결혼할 생각까지 했었다니.\""], ["said Debray.", "드브레가 말했다."], ["“She did well to die, poor girl!”", "\"잘 죽었어, 가엾은 아가씨!\""]], [["“The sitting is adjourned, gentlemen,”", "\"여러분, 본 공판을 휴정합니다.\""], ["said the president; “fresh inquiries will be made, and the case will be tried next session by another magistrate.”", "재판장이 말했다. \"추가 수사가 진행될 것이며, 이 사건은 다음 회기에 다른 판사가 심리할 것입니다.\""]], [["As for Andrea, who was calm and more interesting than ever, he left the hall, escorted by gendarmes, who involuntarily paid him some attention.", "안드레아로 말하자면, 그 어느 때보다 차분하고 흥미로운 모습으로, 자기도 모르게 그에게 얼마간 예우를 차리는 헌병들의 호위를 받으며 법정을 나섰다."]], [["“Well, what do you think of this, my fine fellow?”", "\"그래, 이걸 어떻게 보나, 여보게?\""], ["asked Debray of the sergeant-at-arms, slipping a louis into his hand.", "드브레가 헌병 부사관의 손에 루이 금화 한 닢을 슬쩍 쥐여 주며 물었다."]], [["“There will be extenuating circumstances,” he replied.", "\"정상 참작이 될 겁니다.\" 그가 대답했다."]], [["# Chapter 111. Expiation", "# Chapter 111. Expiation"]], [["Notwithstanding the density of the crowd, M. de Villefort saw it open before him.", "군중이 빽빽이 들어차 있었지만, 빌포르 씨는 그들이 자기 앞에서 길을 열어 주는 것을 보았다."], ["There is something so awe-inspiring in great afflictions that even in the worst times the first emotion of a crowd has generally been to sympathize with the sufferer in a great catastrophe.", "커다란 고통에는 사람을 숙연하게 만드는 무언가가 있어서, 아무리 험한 시절이라도 군중이 처음 느끼는 감정은 대개 큰 비극에 빠진 사람을 동정하는 것이었다."], ["Many people have been assassinated in a tumult, but even criminals have rarely been insulted during trial.", "많은 이들이 소란 속에서 목숨을 잃었지만, 재판을 받는 동안에는 죄인조차 모욕당하는 일이 드물었다."], ["Thus Villefort passed through the mass of spectators and officers of the Palais, and withdrew.", "그렇게 빌포르는 구경꾼과 법원 관리들 사이를 지나 자리를 떴다."], ["Though he had acknowledged his guilt, he was protected by his grief.", "그는 스스로 죄를 인정했으나, 그의 슬픔이 그를 지켜 주었다."], ["There are some situations which men understand by instinct, but which reason is powerless to explain; in such cases the greatest poet is he who gives utterance to the most natural and vehement outburst of sorrow.", "사람들이 본능으로 이해하면서도 이성으로는 설명할 수 없는 상황이 있다. 그런 경우 가장 위대한 시인은 가장 자연스럽고 격렬한 슬픔의 토로를 입에 담는 사람이다."], ["Those who hear the bitter cry are as much impressed as if they listened to an entire poem, and when the sufferer is sincere they are right in regarding his outburst as sublime.", "그 쓰라린 절규를 듣는 이들은 한 편의 시 전체를 듣는 것만큼이나 깊은 인상을 받으며, 고통받는 자가 진실할 때 그 토로를 숭고하다 여기는 것은 옳다."]], [["It would be difficult to describe the state of stupor in which Villefort left the Palais.", "빌포르가 법원을 떠날 때의 망연자실한 상태를 묘사하기란 어려운 일일 것이다."], ["Every pulse beat with feverish excitement, every nerve was strained, every vein swollen, and every part of his body seemed to suffer distinctly from the rest, thus multiplying his agony a thousand-fold.", "모든 맥박이 열병처럼 들끓었고, 모든 신경이 팽팽히 당겨졌으며, 모든 혈관이 부풀어 올랐고, 몸의 각 부분이 저마다 따로 고통을 겪는 듯하여 그의 괴로움은 천 배로 늘어났다."], ["He made his way along the corridors through force of habit; he threw aside his magisterial robe, not out of deference to etiquette, but because it was an unbearable burden, a veritable garb of Nessus, insatiate in torture.", "그는 습관에 이끌려 복도를 지나갔다. 그는 법복을 벗어 던졌는데, 예법을 따라서가 아니라 그것이 견딜 수 없는 짐, 고문을 멈추지 않는 진정한 네소스의 옷이었기 때문이다."], ["Having staggered as far as the Rue Dauphine, he perceived his carriage, awoke his sleeping coachman by opening the door himself, threw himself on the cushions, and pointed towards the Faubourg Saint-Honoré; the carriage drove on.", "도핀 거리까지 비틀거리며 다다른 그는 자신의 마차를 발견했고, 직접 문을 열어 잠든 마부를 깨운 뒤, 방석 위에 몸을 던지고 생토노레 변두리 쪽을 가리켰다. 마차가 달리기 시작했다."]], [["All the weight of his fallen fortune seemed suddenly to crush him; he could not foresee the consequences; he could not contemplate the future with the indifference of the hardened criminal who merely faces a contingency already familiar.", "무너진 운명의 무게가 갑자기 그를 짓누르는 듯했다. 그는 그 결과를 내다볼 수 없었다. 그는 이미 익숙한 사태를 그저 마주할 뿐인 굳어 버린 죄인의 무심함으로 미래를 바라볼 수 없었다."]], [["God was still in his heart. “God,”", "하느님은 여전히 그의 마음속에 계셨다. \"하느님.\""], ["he murmured, not knowing what he said,—“God—God!”", "그가 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지도 모른 채 중얼거렸다."], ["Behind the event that had overwhelmed him he saw the hand of God.", "\"하느님, 하느님!\" 자신을 짓밟은 사건 너머에서 그는 하느님의 손을 보았다."], ["The carriage rolled rapidly onward. Villefort, while turning restlessly on the cushions, felt something press against him.", "마차는 빠르게 굴러갔다. 빌포르는 방석 위에서 안절부절못하며 몸을 뒤척이다가 무언가가 자기 몸에 눌리는 것을 느꼈다."], ["He put out his hand to remove the object; it was a fan which Madame de Villefort had left in the carriage; this fan awakened a recollection which darted through his mind like lightning.", "그는 손을 뻗어 그 물건을 치우려 했다. 빌포르 부인이 마차에 두고 간 부채였다. 이 부채가 번개처럼 그의 머릿속을 스치는 기억을 일깨웠다."], ["He thought of his wife. 50189m", "그는 아내를 떠올렸다."]], [["“Oh!” he exclaimed, as though a red-hot iron were piercing his heart.", "\"아!\" 그가 벌겋게 달군 쇠가 심장을 꿰뚫는 듯 외쳤다."]], [["During the last hour his own crime had alone been presented to his mind; now another object, not less terrible, suddenly presented itself.", "지난 한 시간 동안 그의 머릿속에는 오직 자신의 죄만이 떠올랐다. 이제 그에 못지않게 끔찍한 또 다른 것이 불쑥 모습을 드러냈다."], ["His wife!", "그의 아내!"], ["He had just acted the inexorable judge with her, he had condemned her to death, and she, crushed by remorse, struck with terror, covered with the shame inspired by the eloquence of _his_ irreproachable virtue,—she, a poor, weak woman, without help or the power of defending herself against his absolute and supreme will,—she might at that very moment, perhaps, be preparing to die!", "그는 방금 아내에게 가차 없는 재판관 노릇을 했고, 그녀에게 사형을 선고했다. 그리고 그녀는, 회한에 짓눌리고 공포에 질렸으며 흠 하나 없는 그의 미덕이 토해 낸 웅변이 안긴 수치심에 휩싸인 그녀는, 가엾고 연약한 한 여인으로서, 그의 절대적이고 지고한 의지에 맞서 자신을 변호할 도움도 힘도 없는 그녀는, 어쩌면 바로 그 순간에 죽을 준비를 하고 있을지도 몰랐다!"]], [["An hour had elapsed since her condemnation; at that moment, doubtless, she was recalling all her crimes to her memory; she was asking pardon for her sins; perhaps she was even writing a letter imploring forgiveness from her virtuous husband—a forgiveness she was purchasing with her death!", "그녀가 선고를 받은 지 한 시간이 흘렀다. 지금 이 순간 그녀는 틀림없이 자신의 모든 죄를 기억 속에서 떠올리고 있을 것이며, 자신의 죄에 대해 용서를 빌고 있을 것이다. 어쩌면 정숙한 남편에게 용서를 간청하는 편지를, 자신의 죽음으로 사들이는 그 용서를 청하는 편지를 쓰고 있을지도 몰랐다!"], ["Villefort again groaned with anguish and despair.", "빌포르는 다시 고뇌와 절망에 신음했다."]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아.\""], ["he exclaimed, “that woman became criminal only from associating with me!", "그가 외쳤다. \"저 여자는 오직 나와 함께한 탓에 죄인이 된 것이다!"], ["I carried the infection of crime with me, and she has caught it as she would the typhus fever, the cholera, the plague!", "내가 죄의 병균을 지니고 다녔고, 그녀는 그것을 발진티푸스나 콜레라나 페스트처럼 옮은 것이다! 그런데도 나는 그녀를 벌했다. 감히 그녀에게 말했다."], ["And yet I have punished her—I have dared to tell her—_I_ have—‘Repent and die!’ But no, she must not die; she shall live, and with me.", "내가, '회개하고 죽으라!'고. 아니, 안 된다, 그녀는 죽어서는 안 된다. 그녀는 살 것이다, 나와 함께."], ["We will flee from Paris and go as far as the earth reaches.", "우리는 파리를 떠나 땅끝까지 달아날 것이다. 나는 그녀에게 단두대를 들먹였구나."], ["I told her of the scaffold; oh, Heavens, I forgot that it awaits me also!", "오, 하느님, 그것이 나도 기다리고 있다는 걸 잊었다니! 내가 어찌 그런 말을 입에 담을 수 있었던가? 그래, 우리는 달아날 것이다."], ["How could I pronounce that word?", "나는 그녀에게 모든 것을 고백할 것이다."], ["Yes, we will fly; I will confess all to her,—I will tell her daily that I also have committed a crime!—Oh, what an alliance—the tiger and the serpent; worthy wife of such as I am!", "나도 죄를 저질렀노라고 날마다 말할 것이다! 오, 이 무슨 짝인가, 호랑이와 뱀이라니. 나 같은 자에게 어울리는 아내로다!"], ["She _must_ live that my infamy may diminish hers.”", "내 치욕이 그녀의 치욕을 덜어 주도록, 그녀는 살아야만 한다.\""]], [["And Villefort dashed open the window in front of the carriage.", "그러고 빌포르는 마차 앞쪽 창을 거칠게 열어젖혔다."]], [["“Faster, faster!”", "\"더 빨리, 더 빨리!\""], ["he cried, in a tone which electrified the coachman.", "그가 마부의 등골을 오싹하게 하는 목소리로 외쳤다."], ["The horses, impelled by fear, flew towards the house.", "말들은 공포에 내몰려 집을 향해 날듯이 내달렸다."]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"그래, 그래.\""], ["repeated Villefort, as he approached his home—“yes, that woman must live; she must repent, and educate my son, the sole survivor, with the exception of the indestructible old man, of the wreck of my house.", "빌포르가 집에 다가가며 거듭 말했다. \"그래, 저 여자는 살아야 한다. 그녀는 회개하고, 내 아들을 길러야 한다."], ["She loves him; it was for his sake she has committed these crimes.", "그 죽지 않는 늙은이를 빼면, 무너진 우리 집안에서 유일하게 살아남은 그 아이를."], ["We ought never to despair of softening the heart of a mother who loves her child.", "그녀는 그 아이를 사랑한다. 그녀가 이 죄들을 저지른 것도 그 아이를 위해서였다."], ["She will repent, and no one will know that she has been guilty.", "제 자식을 사랑하는 어머니의 마음을 누그러뜨리는 일을 두고 결코 절망해서는 안 되는 법이다."], ["The events which have taken place in my house, though they now occupy the public mind, will be forgotten in time, or if, indeed, a few enemies should persist in remembering them, why then I will add them to my list of crimes.", "그녀는 회개할 것이고, 그녀가 죄를 지었다는 사실을 아무도 모를 것이다. 우리 집에서 벌어진 일들은 지금이야 세상 사람들의 입에 오르내리지만, 세월이 흐르면 잊힐 것이다. 설령 몇몇 적들이 끝내 그것을 기억하겠다고 고집한다면, 그렇다면 나는 그것을 내 죄목 명단에 더하면 그만이다."], ["What will it signify if one, two, or three more are added?", "하나, 둘, 아니 셋쯤 더 늘어난들 무슨 상관이랴?"], ["My wife and child shall escape from this gulf, carrying treasures with them; she will live and may yet be happy, since her child, in whom all her love is centred, will be with her.", "내 아내와 아들은 보물을 지닌 채 이 심연에서 빠져나갈 것이다. 그녀는 살아갈 것이고, 어쩌면 행복할 수도 있을 것이다. 그녀의 모든 사랑이 쏠린 그 아이가 그녀 곁에 있을 테니."], ["I shall have performed a good action, and my heart will be lighter.”", "나는 선한 일을 한 셈이 될 것이고, 내 마음도 한결 가벼워질 것이다.\""]], [["And the procureur breathed more freely than he had done for some time.", "그러자 검사는 얼마 만인지 모르게 한결 편하게 숨을 쉬었다."]], [["50191m\nThe carriage stopped at the door of the house.", "마차가 집 문 앞에 멈췄다."], ["Villefort leaped out of the carriage, and saw that his servants were surprised at his early return; he could read no other expression on their features.", "빌포르는 마차에서 뛰어내렸고, 하인들이 그가 일찍 돌아온 것에 놀라는 것을 알아챘다. 그들의 얼굴에서 다른 표정은 읽을 수 없었다."], ["Neither of them spoke to him; they merely stood aside to let him pass by, as usual, nothing more. As he passed by M.", "둘 중 누구도 그에게 말을 걸지 않았다. 그저 늘 그렇듯이 그가 지나가도록 옆으로 비켜설 뿐, 그 이상은 없었다."], ["Noirtier’s room, he perceived two figures through the half-open door; but he experienced no curiosity to know who was visiting his father; anxiety carried him on further.", "누아르티에 씨의 방을 지날 때, 그는 반쯤 열린 문틈으로 두 사람의 형상을 보았다. 그러나 누가 아버지를 찾아왔는지 알고 싶은 호기심은 일지 않았다. 불안이 그를 더 멀리 떠밀었다."]], [["“Come,”", "\"자.\""], ["he said, as he ascended the stairs leading to his wife’s room, “nothing is changed here.”", "아내의 방으로 이어지는 계단을 오르며 그가 말했다. \"여기는 아무것도 변한 게 없군.\""]], [["He then closed the door of the landing.", "그러고는 층계참의 문을 닫았다."]], [["“No one must disturb us,”", "\"아무도 우리를 방해해서는 안 된다.\""], ["he said; “I must speak freely to her, accuse myself, and say”—he approached the door, touched the crystal handle, which yielded to his hand.", "그가 말했다. \"나는 그녀에게 거리낌 없이 말하고, 내 죄를 고하고, 이렇게 말해야 한다.\" 그는 문에 다가가 수정 손잡이를 만졌고, 손잡이는 그의 손길에 순순히 돌아갔다."], ["“Not locked,”", "\"잠겨 있지 않군.\""], ["he cried; “that is well.”", "그가 외쳤다. \"잘됐어.\""]], [["And he entered the little room in which Edward slept; for though the child went to school during the day, his mother could not allow him to be separated from her at night.", "그러고 그는 에두아르가 잠든 작은 방으로 들어섰다. 그 아이는 낮에는 학교에 다녔지만, 어머니는 밤에 아이가 자기 곁을 떠나 있는 것을 견디지 못했다."], ["With a single glance Villefort’s eye ran through the room.", "빌포르의 눈길이 한 번에 방 안을 훑었다."]], [["“Not here,”", "\"여기엔 없군.\""], ["he said; “doubtless she is in her bedroom.”", "그가 말했다. \"분명 침실에 있겠지.\""], ["He rushed towards the door, found it bolted, and stopped, shuddering.", "그는 문으로 달려갔으나, 빗장이 걸려 있는 것을 알고는 몸서리치며 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“Héloïse!” he cried.", "\"엘로이즈!\""], ["He fancied he heard the sound of a piece of furniture being removed.", "그가 외쳤다. 가구 하나가 옮겨지는 소리가 들리는 것 같았다."]], [["“Héloïse!”", "\"엘로이즈!\""], ["he repeated.", "그가 다시 외쳤다."]], [["“Who is there?”", "\"누구세요?\""], ["answered the voice of her he sought.", "그가 찾는 여인의 목소리가 대답했다."], ["He thought that voice more feeble than usual.", "그 목소리가 여느 때보다 가냘프게 느껴졌다."]], [["“Open the door!”", "\"문 열어!\""], ["cried Villefort.", "빌포르가 외쳤다."], ["“Open; it is I.”", "\"열어, 나야.\""]], [["But notwithstanding this request, notwithstanding the tone of anguish in which it was uttered, the door remained closed.", "그러나 이 요청에도, 그것이 토해 낸 고뇌에 찬 목소리에도, 문은 닫힌 채로였다."], ["Villefort burst it open with a violent blow.", "빌포르가 힘껏 내리쳐 문을 부수었다."], ["At the entrance of the room which led to her boudoir, Madame de Villefort was standing erect, pale, her features contracted, and her eyes glaring horribly.", "자신의 내실로 통하는 방 입구에 빌포르 부인이 꼿꼿이 서 있었다. 창백하게, 얼굴은 일그러지고, 두 눈은 끔찍하게 번득였다."]], [["“Héloïse, Héloïse!”", "\"엘로이즈, 엘로이즈!\""], ["he said, “what is the matter?", "그가 말했다. \"무슨 일이오?"], ["Speak!” The young woman extended her stiff white hands towards him.", "말을 해!\" 젊은 여인이 뻣뻣하게 굳은 하얀 두 손을 그에게 내밀었다."]], [["“It is done, monsieur,”", "\"끝났어요, 여보.\""], ["she said with a rattling noise which seemed to tear her throat.", "그녀가 목을 찢어 내는 듯한 그르렁거리는 소리로 말했다."], ["“What more do you want?”", "\"더 무엇을 바라세요?\""], ["and she fell full length on the floor.", "그러고는 마룻바닥에 길게 쓰러졌다."]], [["Villefort ran to her and seized her hand, which convulsively clasped a crystal bottle with a golden stopper. Madame de Villefort was dead.", "빌포르가 그녀에게 달려가 손을 붙잡았다. 그 손은 황금 마개가 달린 수정 병을 경련하듯 움켜쥐고 있었다."], ["Villefort, maddened with horror, stepped back to the threshhold of the door, fixing his eyes on the corpse.", "빌포르 부인은 죽어 있었다. 빌포르는 공포에 미쳐 문턱까지 뒷걸음질 치며 시신에 눈을 박았다."]], [["“My son!”", "\"내 아들!\""], ["he exclaimed suddenly, “where is my son?—Edward, Edward!”", "그가 갑자기 외쳤다. \"내 아들은 어디 있나? 에두아르, 에두아르!\""], ["and he rushed out of the room, still crying, “Edward, Edward!”", "그러고는 \"에두아르, 에두아르!\" 하고 계속 외치며 방에서 뛰쳐나갔다."], ["The name was pronounced in such a tone of anguish that the servants ran up.", "그 이름이 어찌나 고뇌에 찬 목소리로 불렸던지 하인들이 달려왔다."]], [["“Where is my son?”", "\"내 아들 어디 있나?\""], ["asked Villefort; “let him be removed from the house, that he may not see——”", "빌포르가 물었다. \"그 아이를 이 집에서 데리고 나가, 보지 못하게……\""]], [["“Master Edward is not downstairs, sir,”", "\"에두아르 도련님은 아래층에 안 계십니다, 나리.\""], ["replied the valet.", "하인이 대답했다."]], [["“Then he must be playing in the garden; go and see.”", "\"그럼 정원에서 놀고 있겠군. 가서 봐.\""]], [["50193m\n“No, sir; Madame de Villefort sent for him half an hour ago; he went into her room, and has not been downstairs since.”", "\"아닙니다, 나리. 빌포르 부인께서 반 시간 전에 도련님을 부르셨습니다. 도련님은 마님 방으로 들어가셨고, 그 뒤로 아래층에 내려오지 않으셨습니다.\""]], [["A cold perspiration burst out on Villefort’s brow; his legs trembled, and his thoughts flew about madly in his brain like the wheels of a disordered watch.", "빌포르의 이마에 식은땀이 솟구쳤다. 다리가 후들거렸고, 생각들이 고장 난 시계의 톱니바퀴처럼 머릿속에서 미친 듯이 맴돌았다."]], [["“In Madame de Villefort’s room?”", "\"빌포르 부인 방으로?\""], ["he murmured and slowly returned, with one hand wiping his forehead, and with the other supporting himself against the wall.", "그가 중얼거리며 한 손으로 이마를 닦고 다른 손으로 벽을 짚어 몸을 지탱한 채 천천히 되돌아갔다."], ["To enter the room he must again see the body of his unfortunate wife.", "방으로 들어가려면 불행한 아내의 시신을 다시 보아야 했다."], ["To call Edward he must reawaken the echo of that room which now appeared like a sepulchre; to speak seemed like violating the silence of the tomb.", "에두아르를 부르려면 이제 무덤처럼 변해 버린 그 방의 메아리를 다시 깨워야 했다. 입을 여는 것은 무덤의 정적을 침범하는 일 같았다."], ["His tongue was paralyzed in his mouth.", "그의 혀가 입안에서 마비되었다."]], [["“Edward!”", "\"에두아르!\""], ["he stammered—“Edward!”", "그가 더듬거렸다. \"에두아르!\""]], [["The child did not answer.", "아이는 대답하지 않았다."], ["Where, then, could he be, if he had entered his mother’s room and not since returned?", "그렇다면 어머니의 방에 들어가서 그 뒤로 나오지 않았다면, 대체 어디에 있을 수 있단 말인가?"], ["He stepped forward.", "그가 앞으로 나아갔다."], ["The corpse of Madame de Villefort was stretched across the doorway leading to the room in which Edward must be; those glaring eyes seemed to watch over the threshold, and the lips bore the stamp of a terrible and mysterious irony.", "빌포르 부인의 시신이 에두아르가 있을 방으로 통하는 문간을 가로질러 길게 뻗어 있었다. 그 번득이는 두 눈은 문턱을 지키고 있는 듯했고, 입술에는 끔찍하고 알 수 없는 조소의 흔적이 어려 있었다."], ["Through the open door was visible a portion of the boudoir, containing an upright piano and a blue satin couch.", "열린 문 너머로 내실의 일부가 보였다. 곧추선 피아노와 푸른 새틴 소파가 있었다."], ["Villefort stepped forward two or three paces, and beheld his child lying—no doubt asleep—on the sofa.", "빌포르는 두세 걸음 앞으로 나아갔고, 자신의 아이가 소파에 누워 있는 것을 보았다."], ["The unhappy man uttered an exclamation of joy; a ray of light seemed to penetrate the abyss of despair and darkness.", "틀림없이 잠든 것이었다. 불행한 사내는 기쁨의 탄성을 질렀다. 한 줄기 빛이 절망과 어둠의 심연을 뚫고 비쳐 드는 듯했다."], ["He had only to step over the corpse, enter the boudoir, take the child in his arms, and flee far, far away.", "그는 시신을 넘어가 내실로 들어가서 아이를 품에 안고 멀리, 멀리 달아나기만 하면 되었다."]], [["Villefort was no longer the civilized man; he was a tiger hurt unto death, gnashing his teeth in his wound.", "빌포르는 더 이상 문명인이 아니었다. 그는 상처를 입고 죽어 가며 상처 속에서 이를 가는 호랑이였다."], ["He no longer feared realities, but phantoms.", "그는 이제 현실이 아니라 환영을 두려워했다."], ["He leaped over the corpse as if it had been a burning brazier.", "그는 불타는 화로라도 되는 양 시신을 뛰어넘었다."], ["He took the child in his arms, embraced him, shook him, called him, but the child made no response.", "그는 아이를 품에 안고 끌어안고 흔들며 불렀지만, 아이는 아무 대답이 없었다."], ["He pressed his burning lips to the cheeks, but they were icy cold and pale; he felt the stiffened limbs; he pressed his hand upon the heart, but it no longer beat,—the child was dead.", "그는 불타는 입술을 그 뺨에 갖다 댔으나, 그 뺨은 얼음처럼 차갑고 창백했다. 그는 뻣뻣하게 굳은 팔다리를 만져 보았고, 손을 심장에 갖다 댔으나, 심장은 더 이상 뛰지 않았다. 아이는 죽어 있었다."]], [["A folded paper fell from Edward’s breast.", "접힌 종이 한 장이 에두아르의 가슴에서 떨어졌다."], ["Villefort, thunderstruck, fell upon his knees; the child dropped from his arms, and rolled on the floor by the side of its mother.", "빌포르는 벼락을 맞은 듯 무릎을 꿇었다. 아이가 그의 품에서 떨어져 제 어머니 곁의 마룻바닥으로 굴렀다."], ["He picked up the paper, and, recognizing his wife’s writing, ran his eyes rapidly over its contents; it ran as follows:", "그는 종이를 집어 들었고, 아내의 글씨를 알아본 뒤 그 내용을 재빨리 눈으로 훑었다. 거기에는 이렇게 적혀 있었다."]], [["“You know that I was a good mother, since it was for my son’s sake I became criminal.", "\"제가 좋은 어머니였다는 것을 당신은 아실 거예요. 제 아들을 위해 죄인이 되었으니까요."], ["A good mother cannot depart without her son.”", "좋은 어머니는 아들 없이 떠날 수 없는 법이지요.\""]], [["Villefort could not believe his eyes,—he could not believe his reason; he dragged himself towards the child’s body, and examined it as a lioness contemplates its dead cub.", "빌포르는 자신의 눈을 믿을 수 없었다. 자신의 이성을 믿을 수 없었다. 그는 아이의 시신을 향해 몸을 끌고 가, 죽은 새끼를 들여다보는 암사자처럼 그것을 살폈다."], ["Then a piercing cry escaped from his breast, and he cried,", "그러더니 가슴에서 찢어지는 듯한 절규가 터져 나왔고, 그가 외쳤다."]], [["“Still the hand of God.”", "\"여전히 하느님의 손이로구나.\""]], [["The presence of the two victims alarmed him; he could not bear solitude shared only by two corpses.", "두 희생자가 함께 있다는 사실이 그를 두렵게 했다. 그는 두 구의 시신만이 함께하는 고독을 견딜 수 없었다."], ["Until then he had been sustained by rage, by his strength of mind, by despair, by the supreme agony which led the Titans to scale the heavens, and Ajax to defy the gods.", "그때까지 그는 분노로, 정신력으로, 절망으로, 거인족을 하늘로 기어오르게 하고 아이아스로 하여금 신들에게 맞서게 했던 그 지고한 고뇌로 버텨 왔다."], ["He now arose, his head bowed beneath the weight of grief, and, shaking his damp, dishevelled hair, he who had never felt compassion for anyone determined to seek his father, that he might have someone to whom he could relate his misfortunes,—someone by whose side he might weep. 50195m", "이제 그는 슬픔의 무게에 고개를 숙인 채 일어나, 축축하게 헝클어진 머리카락을 흔들며, 누구에게도 연민을 느껴 본 적 없던 그가 아버지를 찾아가기로 마음먹었다. 자신의 불행을 털어놓을 누군가를, 그 곁에서 울 수 있는 누군가를 갖기 위해서였다."]], [["He descended the little staircase with which we are acquainted, and entered Noirtier’s room.", "그는 우리에게 익숙한 그 작은 계단을 내려가 누아르티에의 방으로 들어섰다."], ["The old man appeared to be listening attentively and as affectionately as his infirmities would allow to the Abbé Busoni, who looked cold and calm, as usual.", "노인은 여느 때처럼 차갑고 차분해 보이는 부소니 신부의 말에, 자신의 병약함이 허락하는 한 정성껏 귀를 기울이고 있는 듯했다."], ["Villefort, perceiving the abbé, passed his hand across his brow.", "빌포르는 신부를 알아보고 손으로 이마를 쓸었다."], ["The past came to him like one of those waves whose wrath foams fiercer than the others.", "과거가, 다른 어떤 것보다 더 거세게 분노로 거품을 일으키는 그런 파도처럼 그에게 밀려왔다."]], [["He recollected the call he had made upon him after the dinner at Auteuil, and then the visit the abbé had himself paid to his house on the day of Valentine’s death.", "그는 오퇴유에서의 만찬 뒤에 자신이 신부를 찾아갔던 일과, 발랑틴이 죽던 날 신부가 직접 자기 집을 방문했던 일을 떠올렸다."]], [["“You here, sir!”", "\"당신이 여기에!\""], ["he exclaimed; “do you, then, never appear but to act as an escort to death?”", "그가 외쳤다. \"그렇다면 당신은 죽음을 호위하는 일이 아니면 결코 모습을 드러내지 않는 거요?\""]], [["Busoni turned around, and, perceiving the excitement depicted on the magistrate’s face, the savage lustre of his eyes, he understood that the revelation had been made at the assizes; but beyond this he was ignorant.", "부소니가 몸을 돌렸고, 재판관의 얼굴에 드러난 흥분과 그 두 눈의 사나운 광채를 보고는, 중죄 재판에서 진상이 폭로되었음을 알아챘다. 그러나 그 이상은 알지 못했다."]], [["“I came to pray over the body of your daughter.”", "\"나는 당신 딸의 시신 곁에서 기도하러 왔소.\""]], [["“And now why are you here?”", "\"그런데 지금은 어찌하여 여기 있는 거요?\""]], [["“I come to tell you that you have sufficiently repaid your debt, and that from this moment I will pray to God to forgive you, as I do.”", "\"당신이 그 빚을 충분히 갚았다는 것을, 그리고 이 순간부터 내가 당신을 용서하듯 하느님께도 당신을 용서해 주십사 기도하겠다는 것을 말하러 왔소.\""]], [["“Good heavens!”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["exclaimed Villefort, stepping back fearfully, “surely that is not the voice of the Abbé Busoni!”", "빌포르가 겁에 질려 뒷걸음질 치며 외쳤다. \"분명 저것은 부소니 신부의 목소리가 아니야!\""]], [["“No!” The abbé threw off his wig, shook his head, and his hair, no longer confined, fell in black masses around his manly face.", "\"그렇소!\" 신부가 가발을 벗어 던지고 고개를 흔들자, 더는 묶이지 않은 그의 머리카락이 사내다운 얼굴 둘레로 검은 물결처럼 흘러내렸다."]], [["“It is the face of the Count of Monte Cristo!”", "\"저것은 몬테크리스토 백작의 얼굴이다!\""], ["exclaimed the procureur, with a haggard expression.", "검사가 초췌한 표정으로 외쳤다."]], [["“You are not exactly right, M. Procureur; you must go farther back.”", "\"꼭 맞는 건 아니오, 검사 양반. 더 거슬러 올라가야 하오.\""]], [["“That voice, that voice!—where did I first hear it?”", "\"저 목소리, 저 목소리! 내가 저것을 처음 들은 게 어디였더라?\""]], [["“You heard it for the first time at Marseilles, twenty-three years ago, the day of your marriage with Mademoiselle de Saint-Méran. Refer to your papers.”", "\"당신은 그 목소리를 이십삼 년 전 마르세유에서, 생메랑 양과 결혼하던 날 처음 들었소. 당신의 서류를 들춰 보시오.\""]], [["“You are not Busoni?—you are not Monte Cristo?", "\"당신은 부소니가 아니고, 몬테크리스토도 아니오?"], ["Oh, heavens!", "오, 맙소사!"], ["you are, then, some secret, implacable, and mortal enemy!", "그렇다면 당신은 무슨 은밀하고 무자비한, 죽음을 부르는 원수로구나!"], ["I must have wronged you in some way at Marseilles. Oh, woe to me!”", "내가 마르세유에서 어떤 식으로든 당신에게 몹쓸 짓을 했음이 틀림없어. 오, 화로다, 내게 화로다!\""]], [["“Yes; you are now on the right path,”", "\"그렇소, 이제 제대로 길을 찾았소.\""], ["said the count, crossing his arms over his broad chest; “search—search!”", "백작이 넓은 가슴 위로 팔짱을 끼며 말했다. \"찾으시오, 찾으시오!\""]], [["“But what have I done to you?”", "\"하지만 내가 당신에게 무슨 짓을 했단 말이오?\""], ["exclaimed Villefort, whose mind was balancing between reason and insanity, in that cloud which is neither a dream nor reality; “what have I done to you?", "빌포르가 외쳤다. 그의 정신은 꿈도 현실도 아닌 그 안개 속에서 이성과 광기 사이를 오가고 있었다. \"내가 당신에게 무슨 짓을 했소?"], ["Tell me, then!", "어서 말하시오!"], ["Speak!”", "말을 해!\""]], [["“You condemned me to a horrible, tedious death; you killed my father; you deprived me of liberty, of love, and happiness.”", "\"당신은 나를 끔찍하고 지루한 죽음으로 몰아넣었소. 당신은 내 아버지를 죽였소. 당신은 내게서 자유를, 사랑을, 행복을 빼앗았소.\""]], [["“Who are you, then?", "\"그렇다면 당신은 누구요?"], ["Who are you?”", "당신은 누구냐고?\""]], [["“I am the spectre of a wretch you buried in the dungeons of the Château d’If.", "\"나는 당신이 이프성 지하 감옥에 파묻어 버린 어느 가엾은 자의 망령이오."], ["God gave that spectre the form of the Count of Monte Cristo when he at length issued from his tomb, enriched him with gold and diamonds, and led him to you!”", "그 망령이 마침내 무덤에서 나왔을 때 하느님께서 그에게 몬테크리스토 백작의 모습을 주셨고, 황금과 다이아몬드로 그를 부유하게 하셨으며, 그를 당신에게로 이끄셨소!\""]], [["“Ah, I recognize you—I recognize you!”", "\"아, 알겠다, 너를 알아보겠어!\""], ["exclaimed the king’s attorney; “you are——”", "국왕의 검사가 외쳤다. \"너는……\""]], [["“I am Edmond Dantès!”", "\"나는 에드몽 당테스다!\""]], [["“You are Edmond Dantès,”", "\"네가 에드몽 당테스로구나.\""], ["cried Villefort, seizing the count by the wrist; “then come here!”", "빌포르가 백작의 손목을 움켜쥐며 외쳤다. \"그렇다면 이리로 와!\""]], [["And up the stairs he dragged Monte Cristo; who, ignorant of what had happened, followed him in astonishment, foreseeing some new catastrophe.", "그러고는 몬테크리스토를 계단 위로 끌고 올라갔다. 백작은 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 알지 못한 채 어리둥절하여 그를 따라가며, 무언가 새로운 참사를 예감했다."]], [["“There, Edmond Dantès!”", "\"여기다, 에드몽 당테스!\""], ["he said, pointing to the bodies of his wife and child, “see, are you well avenged?”", "그가 아내와 아이의 시신을 가리키며 말했다. \"봐라, 이만하면 복수를 톡톡히 한 거냐?\""]], [["Monte Cristo became pale at this horrible sight; he felt that he had passed beyond the bounds of vengeance, and that he could no longer say, “God is for and with me.”", "몬테크리스토는 이 끔찍한 광경에 창백해졌다. 그는 자신이 복수의 경계를 넘어섰음을, 그리고 더는 \"하느님은 나를 위하시고 나와 함께하신다\"고 말할 수 없음을 느꼈다."], ["With an expression of indescribable anguish he threw himself upon the body of the child, reopened its eyes, felt its pulse, and then rushed with him into Valentine’s room, of which he double-locked the door.", "그는 형언할 수 없는 고뇌의 표정으로 아이의 시신에 몸을 던져, 그 눈을 다시 열어 보고 맥을 짚어 본 뒤, 아이를 안고 발랑틴의 방으로 달려가 문을 이중으로 잠가 버렸다."]], [["“My child,”", "\"내 아이!\""], ["cried Villefort, “he carries away the body of my child!", "빌포르가 외쳤다. \"저자가 내 아이의 시신을 데려간다!"], ["Oh, curses, woe, death to you!”", "오, 저주를, 화를, 죽음을 너에게!\""]], [["He tried to follow Monte Cristo; but as though in a dream he was transfixed to the spot,—his eyes glared as though they were starting through the sockets; he griped the flesh on his chest until his nails were stained with blood; the veins of his temples swelled and boiled as though they would burst their narrow boundary, and deluge his brain with living fire.", "그는 몬테크리스토를 뒤쫓으려 했다. 그러나 꿈속에서처럼 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 움직일 수 없었다. 그의 두 눈은 눈구멍 밖으로 튀어나올 듯 번득였고, 손톱이 피로 물들 때까지 자신의 가슴 살을 움켜쥐었으며, 관자놀이의 혈관이 그 좁은 경계를 터뜨리고 살아 있는 불길로 뇌를 적셔 버리기라도 할 듯 부풀어 끓어올랐다."], ["This lasted several minutes, until the frightful overturn of reason was accomplished; then uttering a loud cry followed by a burst of laughter, he rushed down the stairs.", "이것이 몇 분간 이어지더니, 마침내 무시무시한 이성의 전복이 완성되었다. 그러자 그는 커다란 비명을 지르고 이어서 웃음을 터뜨리며 계단을 뛰어 내려갔다."]], [["A quarter of an hour afterwards the door of Valentine’s room opened, and Monte Cristo reappeared.", "십오 분 뒤, 발랑틴 방의 문이 열리고 몬테크리스토가 다시 나타났다."], ["Pale, with a dull eye and heavy heart, all the noble features of that face, usually so calm and serene, were overcast by grief.", "창백하게, 흐릿한 눈빛으로, 무거운 마음으로, 평소 그토록 차분하고 평온하던 그 얼굴의 고귀한 모든 윤곽이 슬픔에 뒤덮여 있었다."], ["In his arms he held the child, whom no skill had been able to recall to life.", "그의 품에는 어떤 솜씨로도 되살릴 수 없었던 아이가 안겨 있었다."], ["Bending on one knee, he placed it reverently by the side of its mother, with its head upon her breast.", "한쪽 무릎을 꿇고, 그는 아이를 경건하게 제 어머니 곁에, 머리를 어머니의 가슴에 기대게 하여 뉘었다."], ["Then, rising, he went out, and meeting a servant on the stairs, he asked:", "그러고는 일어나 밖으로 나갔고, 계단에서 하인 하나를 만나 물었다."]], [["“Where is M. de Villefort?”", "\"빌포르 씨는 어디 계신가?\""]], [["The servant, instead of answering, pointed to the garden.", "하인은 대답 대신 정원을 가리켰다."], ["Monte Cristo ran down the steps, and advancing towards the spot designated beheld Villefort, encircled by his servants, with a spade in his hand, and digging the earth with fury.", "몬테크리스토는 계단을 뛰어 내려가, 가리켜진 자리로 다가가 보니, 빌포르가 하인들에게 둘러싸인 채 손에 삽을 들고 미친 듯이 땅을 파고 있었다."]], [["“It is not here!”", "\"여기가 아니야!\""], ["he cried.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["“It is not here!” 50197m", "\"여기가 아니라고!\""]], [["And then he moved farther on, and began again to dig.", "그러고는 더 멀리 자리를 옮겨 다시 파기 시작했다."]], [["Monte Cristo approached him, and said in a low voice, with an expression almost humble:", "몬테크리스토가 그에게 다가가 거의 겸손하다 할 만한 표정으로 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Sir, you have indeed lost a son; but——”", "\"검사 양반, 당신은 정말로 아들을 잃었소. 하지만……\""]], [["Villefort interrupted him; he had neither listened nor heard.", "빌포르가 그의 말을 가로막았다. 그는 듣지도, 알아듣지도 못했다."]], [["“Oh, I _will_ find it,”", "\"오, 나는 그것을 찾아낼 거야.\""], ["he cried; “you may pretend he is not here, but I _will_ find him, though I dig forever!”", "그가 외쳤다. \"당신은 그 아이가 여기 없는 척하지만, 나는 그를 찾아낼 거야, 영원히 파헤치는 한이 있더라도!\""]], [["Monte Cristo drew back in horror.", "몬테크리스토가 공포에 질려 물러섰다."]], [["“Oh,” he said, “he is mad!”", "\"오.\" 그가 말했다."], ["And as though he feared that the walls of the accursed house would crumble around him, he rushed into the street, for the first time doubting whether he had the right to do as he had done.", "\"그가 미쳤구나!\" 그러고는 저주받은 집의 벽들이 자기 주위로 무너져 내리기라도 할까 두려운 듯 거리로 뛰쳐나갔다. 자신이 행한 일을 행할 권리가 자신에게 있었는지 처음으로 의심하면서."], ["“Oh, enough of this,—enough of this,” he cried; “let me save the last.”", "\"오, 이만하면 됐다, 이만하면 됐어.\" 그가 외쳤다."], ["On entering his house, he met Morrel, who wandered about like a ghost awaiting the heavenly mandate for return to the tomb.", "\"마지막 하나만은 내가 구하게 해 다오.\" 집에 들어서니 모렐이 있었다. 그는 무덤으로 돌아가라는 하늘의 명령을 기다리는 유령처럼 서성이고 있었다."]], [["“Prepare yourself, Maximilian,”", "\"채비하게, 막시밀리앙.\""], ["he said with a smile; “we leave Paris tomorrow.”", "그가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"우리는 내일 파리를 떠난다.\""]], [["“Have you nothing more to do there?”", "\"여기서 더 하실 일이 없으신가요?\""], ["asked Morrel.", "모렐이 물었다."]], [["“No,”", "\"없네.\""], ["replied Monte Cristo; “God grant I may not have done too much already.”", "몬테크리스토가 대답했다. \"내가 이미 너무 많은 일을 한 것이 아니기를, 하느님께 빌 뿐이지.\""]], [["The next day they indeed left, accompanied only by Baptistin.", "이튿날 그들은 정말로 떠났다. 바티스탱 한 사람만이 동행했다."], ["Haydée had taken away Ali, and Bertuccio remained with Noirtier.", "하이데가 알리를 데려갔고, 베르투치오는 누아르티에 곁에 남았다."]], [["# Chapter 112. The Departure", "# Chapter 112. The Departure"]], [["The recent events formed the theme of conversation throughout all Paris.", "최근에 일어난 사건들이 파리 전역의 화젯거리였다."], ["Emmanuel and his wife conversed with natural astonishment in their little apartment in the Rue Meslay upon the three successive, sudden, and most unexpected catastrophes of Morcerf, Danglars, and Villefort.", "에마뉘엘과 그의 아내는 메슬레가의 작은 아파트에서 모르세르, 당글라르, 빌포르에게 잇따라 닥친, 갑작스럽고 더없이 뜻밖이었던 세 차례의 재앙을 두고 당연한 놀라움으로 이야기를 나누었다."], ["Maximilian, who was paying them a visit, listened to their conversation, or rather was present at it, plunged in his accustomed state of apathy.", "그 집을 찾아온 막시밀리앙은 두 사람의 대화에 귀를 기울이는 듯, 아니 그저 자리만 지킨 채 평소의 무기력에 잠겨 있었다."]], [["“Indeed,”", "\"정말이지,\" 쥘리가 말했다. \"에마뉘엘, 그런 생각이 들지 않아요?"], ["said Julie, “might we not almost fancy, Emmanuel, that those people, so rich, so happy but yesterday, had forgotten in their prosperity that an evil genius—like the wicked fairies in Perrault’s stories who present themselves unbidden at a wedding or baptism—hovered over them, and appeared all at once to revenge himself for their fatal neglect?”", "어제까지만 해도 그토록 부유하고 행복했던 저 사람들이, 번영에 취한 나머지 한 가지를 잊고 있었던 거예요. 페로의 이야기에 나오는 못된 요정들처럼, 결혼식이나 세례식에 청하지도 않았는데 제멋대로 나타나는 그런 악령이 자기들 머리 위를 맴돌고 있다는 걸요. 그러다 어느 순간 그 악령이 불쑥 모습을 드러내, 자기를 잊고 소홀히 한 죄를 갚으려 한 게 아닐까요?\""]], [["“What a dire misfortune!”", "\"이 무슨 끔찍한 불행인가!\""], ["said Emmanuel, thinking of Morcerf and Danglars.", "에마뉘엘이 모르세르와 당글라르를 떠올리며 말했다."]], [["“What dreadful sufferings!”", "\"이 무슨 무서운 고통인지!\""], ["said Julie, remembering Valentine, but whom, with a delicacy natural to women, she did not name before her brother.", "쥘리가 발랑틴을 떠올리며 말했다. 그러나 여자다운 섬세함으로, 동생 앞에서 그 이름을 입에 올리지는 않았다."]], [["“If the Supreme Being has directed the fatal blow,”", "\"신께서 그 가혹한 일격을 내리셨다면,\""], ["said Emmanuel, “it must be that he in his great goodness has perceived nothing in the past lives of these people to merit mitigation of their awful punishment.”", "에마뉘엘이 말했다. \"그분의 크나큰 자비로도 저 사람들의 지난 삶에서 그 무서운 형벌을 덜어 줄 만한 점을 단 하나도 찾지 못하셨기 때문일 거요.\""]], [["“Do you not form a very rash judgment, Emmanuel?”", "\"너무 성급하게 단정하는 거 아니에요, 에마뉘엘?\""], ["said Julie.", "쥘리가 말했다."], ["“When my father, with a pistol in his hand, was once on the point of committing suicide, had anyone then said, ‘This man deserves his misery,’ would not that person have been deceived?”", "\"예전에 아버지가 권총을 손에 쥐고 막 스스로 목숨을 끊으려 하셨을 때, 만약 그때 누군가가 '이 사람은 이런 불행을 당해도 싸다'라고 했다면, 그 사람이 잘못 본 게 아니었을까요?\""]], [["“Yes; but your father was not allowed to fall.", "\"그렇지. 하지만 당신 아버님은 무너지도록 내버려 두어지지 않았소."], ["A being was commissioned to arrest the fatal hand of death about to descend on him.”", "그분께 막 떨어지려던 죽음의 손길을 멈추도록, 한 존재가 보내졌으니까.\""]], [["Emmanuel had scarcely uttered these words when the sound of the bell was heard, the well-known signal given by the porter that a visitor had arrived.", "에마뉘엘이 이 말을 채 끝내기도 전에 종소리가 들렸다. 손님이 찾아왔음을 알리는, 문지기가 보내는 익숙한 신호였다."], ["Nearly at the same instant the door was opened and the Count of Monte Cristo appeared on the threshold.", "거의 같은 순간에 문이 열렸고, 몬테크리스토 백작이 문턱에 모습을 드러냈다."], ["The young people uttered a cry of joy, while Maximilian raised his head, but let it fall again immediately. 50201m", "젊은 부부는 기쁨의 탄성을 질렀다. 막시밀리앙은 고개를 들었으나, 곧바로 다시 떨구었다."]], [["“Maximilian,”", "\"막시밀리앙,\""], ["said the count, without appearing to notice the different impressions which his presence produced on the little circle, “I come to seek you.”", "백작이 말했다. 자신의 등장이 이 작은 무리에게 저마다 다른 인상을 불러일으킨 것을 알아채지 못한 듯한 태도였다. \"자네를 데리러 왔네.\""]], [["“To seek me?” repeated Morrel, as if awakening from a dream.", "\"저를 데리러요?\" 모렐이 꿈에서 깨어나는 사람처럼 되물었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["said Monte Cristo; “has it not been agreed that I should take you with me, and did I not tell you yesterday to prepare for departure?”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"내가 자네를 데려가기로 하지 않았나. 어제 떠날 채비를 하라고 이르지 않았던가?\""]], [["“I am ready,”", "\"준비됐습니다.\""], ["said Maximilian; “I came expressly to wish them farewell.”", "막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"마침 누님 내외에게 작별 인사를 하러 온 참이었습니다.\""]], [["“Whither are you going, count?”", "\"어디로 가시는 건가요, 백작님?\""], ["asked Julie.", "쥘리가 물었다."]], [["“In the first instance to Marseilles, madame.”", "\"우선은 마르세유로 갑니다, 부인.\""]], [["“To Marseilles!”", "\"마르세유로요!\""], ["exclaimed the young couple.", "젊은 부부가 외쳤다."]], [["“Yes, and I take your brother with me.”", "\"그렇습니다. 동생분을 함께 데려가겠습니다.\""]], [["“Oh, count.”", "\"아, 백작님.\""], ["said Julie, “will you restore him to us cured of his melancholy?”", "쥘리가 말했다. \"이 아이의 우울을 말끔히 고쳐서 돌려보내 주실 건가요?\""], ["Morrel turned away to conceal the confusion of his countenance.", "모렐은 얼굴에 드러난 당혹감을 감추려 고개를 돌렸다."]], [["“You perceive, then, that he is not happy?”", "\"그러니까 부인도 동생분이 행복하지 않다는 걸 아시는군요?\""], ["said the count.", "백작이 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["replied the young woman; “and fear much that he finds our home but a dull one.”", "젊은 부인이 대답했다. \"그리고 이 아이가 우리 집을 그저 따분하게만 여기는 게 아닐까 무척 걱정스러워요.\""]], [["“I will undertake to divert him,”", "\"제가 동생분의 기분을 풀어 드리겠습니다.\""], ["replied the count.", "백작이 대답했다."]], [["“I am ready to accompany you, sir,”", "\"동행할 준비가 됐습니다, 백작님.\""], ["said Maximilian.", "막시밀리앙이 말했다."], ["“Adieu, my kind friends!", "\"안녕히 계세요, 다정한 분들!"], ["Emmanuel—Julie—farewell!”", "에마뉘엘 형님, 쥘리 누님, 안녕히!\""]], [["“How farewell?”", "\"안녕히라니?\""], ["exclaimed Julie; “do you leave us thus, so suddenly, without any preparations for your journey, without even a passport?”", "쥘리가 외쳤다. \"이렇게 떠난다고? 이렇게 갑자기, 여행 채비도 없이, 여권조차 없이?\""]], [["“Needless delays but increase the grief of parting,”", "\"공연한 지체는 이별의 슬픔만 키울 뿐입니다.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “and Maximilian has doubtless provided himself with everything requisite; at least, I advised him to do so.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"막시밀리앙은 필요한 건 다 챙겼을 겁니다. 적어도 제가 그렇게 하라고 일러 두었으니까요.\""]], [["“I have a passport, and my clothes are ready packed,”", "\"여권도 있고, 옷가지도 다 꾸려 두었습니다.\""], ["said Morrel in his tranquil but mournful manner.", "모렐이 차분하면서도 침울한 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Good,”", "\"좋아.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, smiling; “in these prompt arrangements we recognize the order of a well-disciplined soldier.”", "몬테크리스토가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"이렇게 척척 채비를 갖춘 걸 보니, 잘 단련된 군인의 절도가 느껴지는군.\""]], [["“And you leave us,” said Julie, “at a moment’s warning?", "\"그런데 이렇게 한순간에 우리 곁을 떠나신다고요?\" 쥘리가 말했다."], ["you do not give us a day—no, not even an hour before your departure?”", "\"단 하루도, 아니 출발 전에 단 한 시간도 우리에게 내주지 않으시는 건가요?\""]], [["“My carriage is at the door, madame, and I must be in Rome in five days.”", "\"마차가 문 앞에 와 있습니다, 부인. 닷새 안에 로마에 닿아야 하거든요.\""]], [["“But does Maximilian go to Rome?”", "\"하지만 막시밀리앙이 로마까지 가는 건가요?\""], ["exclaimed Emmanuel.", "에마뉘엘이 외쳤다."]], [["“I am going wherever it may please the count to take me,”", "\"백작님이 데려가시는 곳이라면 어디든 갑니다.\""], ["said Morrel, with a smile full of grief; “I am under his orders for the next month.”", "모렐이 슬픔 가득한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"앞으로 한 달간은 백작님의 명을 따르기로 했습니다.\""]], [["“Oh, heavens, how strangely he expresses himself, count!”", "\"오, 세상에, 말투가 어쩜 저렇게 이상한지요, 백작님!\""], ["said Julie.", "쥘리가 말했다."]], [["“Maximilian goes with _me_,”", "\"막시밀리앙은 저와 함께 갑니다.\""], ["said the count, in his kindest and most persuasive manner; “therefore do not make yourself uneasy on your brother’s account.”", "백작이 더없이 다정하고 설득력 있는 태도로 말했다. \"그러니 동생분 일로 마음 졸이지 마십시오.\""]], [["“Once more farewell, my dear sister; Emmanuel, adieu!”", "\"다시 한번 안녕, 사랑하는 누님. 에마뉘엘 형님, 안녕히!\""], ["Morrel repeated.", "모렐이 거듭 인사했다."]], [["“His carelessness and indifference touch me to the heart,” said Julie.", "\"저 무심하고 덤덤한 태도가 가슴을 저미네요.\" 쥘리가 말했다."], ["“Oh, Maximilian, Maximilian, you are certainly concealing something from us.”", "\"아, 막시밀리앙, 막시밀리앙, 너 분명히 우리한테 뭔가 숨기고 있구나.\""]], [["“Pshaw!”", "\"천만에요.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “you will see him return to you gay, smiling, and joyful.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"동생분이 명랑하게, 웃으며, 기쁨에 차서 부인 곁으로 돌아오는 걸 보시게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["Maximilian cast a look of disdain, almost of anger, on the count.", "막시밀리앙이 백작에게 경멸에 가까운, 거의 분노에 찬 눈길을 던졌다."]], [["“We must leave you,”", "\"이제 가 봐야겠습니다.\""], ["said Monte Cristo. 50203m", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."]], [["“Before you quit us, count,”", "\"떠나시기 전에, 백작님.\""], ["said Julie, “will you permit us to express to you all that the other day——”", "쥘리가 말했다. \"지난번 일에 대해 저희가 드리고 싶은 말씀을 모두 전하도록 허락해 주시겠어요……\""]], [["“Madame,”", "\"부인,\""], ["interrupted the count, taking her two hands in his, “all that you could say in words would never express what I read in your eyes; the thoughts of your heart are fully understood by mine.", "백작이 그녀의 두 손을 마주 잡으며 말을 가로막았다. \"부인이 말로 전하실 수 있는 그 무엇도, 제가 부인의 눈에서 읽어 낸 것을 결코 다 담아내지 못할 겁니다. 부인 마음속 생각은 제 마음이 온전히 헤아리고 있습니다."], ["Like benefactors in romances, I should have left you without seeing you again, but that would have been a virtue beyond my strength, because I am a weak and vain man, fond of the tender, kind, and thankful glances of my fellow-creatures.", "소설 속 은인들처럼, 저도 다시 모습을 보이지 않고 떠나야 마땅했겠지요. 하지만 그건 제 힘에 부치는 미덕이었습니다. 저는 나약하고 허영심 많은 사람이라, 동료 인간들의 다정하고 친절하고 고마워하는 눈길을 좋아하니까요."], ["On the eve of departure I carry my egotism so far as to say, ‘Do not forget me, my kind friends, for probably you will never see me again.’”", "떠나는 마당에 제 이기심이 어찌나 심한지, 이렇게까지 말하게 되는군요. '저를 잊지 말아 주십시오, 다정한 벗들이여. 아마 다시는 저를 보지 못할 테니까요.'\""]], [["“Never see you again?”", "\"다시는 못 뵙는다니요?\""], ["exclaimed Emmanuel, while two large tears rolled down Julie’s cheeks, “never behold you again?", "에마뉘엘이 외쳤다. 그 사이 쥘리의 뺨으로 굵은 눈물 두 줄기가 흘러내렸다. \"다시는 못 뵙는다고요?"], ["It is not a man, then, but some angel that leaves us, and this angel is on the point of returning to heaven after having appeared on earth to do good.”", "그렇다면 우리 곁을 떠나는 분은 사람이 아니라 천사군요. 선을 행하려고 이 땅에 내려왔다가, 이제 막 하늘로 돌아가려는 천사 말이에요.\""]], [["“Say not so,”", "\"그런 말씀 마십시오.\""], ["quickly returned Monte Cristo—“say not so, my friends; angels never err, celestial beings remain where they wish to be.", "몬테크리스토가 얼른 받았다. \"그런 말씀 마십시오, 벗들이여. 천사는 결코 그르치지 않고, 천상의 존재들은 자기가 머물고자 하는 곳에 머무릅니다."], ["Fate is not more powerful than they; it is they who, on the contrary, overcome fate.", "운명도 그들보다 강하지 못하지요. 오히려 그들이 운명을 이겨 냅니다."], ["No, Emmanuel, I am but a man, and your admiration is as unmerited as your words are sacrilegious.”", "아닙니다, 에마뉘엘, 저는 한낱 인간일 뿐이고, 당신의 찬미는 분에 넘치며, 당신의 말은 신성모독입니다.\""]], [["And pressing his lips on the hand of Julie, who rushed into his arms, he extended his other hand to Emmanuel; then tearing himself from this abode of peace and happiness, he made a sign to Maximilian, who followed him passively, with the indifference which had been perceptible in him ever since the death of Valentine had so stunned him.", "그러고서 백작은 자기 품으로 달려든 쥘리의 손에 입술을 댄 다음, 다른 손을 에마뉘엘에게 내밀었다. 그런 뒤 평화와 행복이 깃든 이 보금자리를 뿌리치듯 떠나며, 막시밀리앙에게 신호를 보냈다. 막시밀리앙은 발랑틴의 죽음이 그를 그토록 망연케 한 이래로 줄곧 내비치던 그 무심한 태도로, 잠자코 백작의 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["“Restore my brother to peace and happiness,”", "\"제 동생에게 평화와 행복을 되찾아 주세요.\""], ["whispered Julie to Monte Cristo.", "쥘리가 몬테크리스토에게 속삭였다."], ["And the count pressed her hand in reply, as he had done eleven years before on the staircase leading to Morrel’s study.", "백작은 대답 대신 그녀의 손을 꼭 쥐었다. 십일 년 전, 모렐의 서재로 올라가는 계단에서 그랬던 것처럼."]], [["“You still confide, then, in Sinbad the Sailor?”", "\"그럼 아직도 뱃사람 신드바드를 믿으시는 겁니까?\""], ["asked he, smiling.", "그가 미소 지으며 물었다."]], [["“Oh, yes,”", "\"아, 그럼요.\""], ["was the ready answer.", "곧바로 대답이 돌아왔다."]], [["“Well, then, sleep in peace, and put your trust in the Lord.”", "\"그렇다면 마음 편히 주무십시오. 그리고 주님께 모든 걸 맡기십시오.\""]], [["As we have before said, the post-chaise was waiting; four powerful horses were already pawing the ground with impatience, while Ali, apparently just arrived from a long walk, was standing at the foot of the steps, his face bathed in perspiration.", "앞서 말했듯이 역마차가 기다리고 있었다. 힘센 말 네 마리가 벌써 조바심에 발굽으로 땅을 긁고 있었고, 멀리서 한참을 걸어온 듯한 알리가 땀에 흠뻑 젖은 얼굴로 계단 아래에 서 있었다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래,\""], ["asked the count in Arabic, “have you been to see the old man?”", "백작이 아랍어로 물었다. \"그 노인을 만나고 왔느냐?\""], ["Ali made a sign in the affirmative.", "알리가 그렇다는 몸짓을 했다."]], [["“And have you placed the letter before him, as I ordered you to do?”", "\"내가 시킨 대로, 그분 앞에 편지를 놓아 두었느냐?\""]], [["The slave respectfully signalized that he had.", "노예가 공손하게 그렇게 했음을 표시했다."]], [["“And what did he say, or rather do?”", "\"그래서 그분이 뭐라고 하시더냐, 아니 어찌하시더냐?\""], ["Ali placed himself in the light, so that his master might see him distinctly, and then imitating in his intelligent manner the countenance of the old man, he closed his eyes, as Noirtier was in the custom of doing when saying “Yes.”", "알리는 주인이 자기를 똑똑히 볼 수 있도록 빛이 드는 자리로 가서, 그 영리한 솜씨로 노인의 표정을 흉내 내며 눈을 감았다. 누아르티에가 \"예\"라고 할 때 늘 하던 그대로였다."]], [["“Good; he accepts,”", "\"좋아, 받아들이셨군.\""], ["said Monte Cristo.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다."], ["“Now let us go.” 50205m", "\"자, 이제 가자.\""]], [["These words had scarcely escaped him, when the carriage was on its way, and the feet of the horses struck a shower of sparks from the pavement.", "이 말이 채 입에서 떨어지기도 전에 마차가 출발했고, 말발굽이 포석을 차며 불꽃을 소나기처럼 튀겼다. 막시밀리앙은 한마디도 없이 구석 자리에 몸을 묻었다."], ["Maximilian settled himself in his corner without uttering a word.", "반 시간쯤 지났을 때 마차가 돌연 멈춰 섰다."], ["Half an hour had passed when the carriage stopped suddenly; the count had just pulled the silken check-string, which was fastened to Ali’s finger.", "백작이 알리의 손가락에 매어 둔 비단 신호줄을 막 잡아당긴 참이었다. 누비아인은 곧바로 내려가 마차 문을 열었다. 별빛이 아름다운 밤이었다."], ["The Nubian immediately descended and opened the carriage door.", "그들은 막 빌쥐프 언덕 꼭대기에 다다른 참이었다."], ["It was a lovely starlight night—they had just reached the top of the hill Villejuif, from whence Paris appears like a sombre sea tossing its millions of phosphoric waves into light—waves indeed more noisy, more passionate, more changeable, more furious, more greedy, than those of the tempestuous ocean,—waves which never rest as those of the sea sometimes do,—waves ever dashing, ever foaming, ever ingulfing what falls within their grasp.", "그곳에서 내려다보면 파리는 수백만의 인광 물결을 빛 속으로 일렁이는 어두운 바다처럼 보였다. 폭풍이 몰아치는 대양의 물결보다 더 시끄럽고, 더 격정적이고, 더 변덕스럽고, 더 사납고, 더 탐욕스러운 물결이었다. 바다 물결은 이따금 잦아들기도 하건만 결코 쉬는 법이 없는 물결, 끊임없이 부서지고, 끊임없이 거품을 일으키며, 손아귀에 든 것은 무엇이든 집어삼키는 물결이었다."]], [["The count stood alone, and at a sign from his hand, the carriage went on for a short distance.", "백작은 홀로 섰다. 그가 손짓하자 마차가 조금 앞으로 나아갔다."], ["With folded arms, he gazed for some time upon the great city.", "팔짱을 낀 채 그는 한동안 그 거대한 도시를 응시했다."], ["When he had fixed his piercing look on this modern Babylon, which equally engages the contemplation of the religious enthusiast, the materialist, and the scoffer,—", "신앙에 사로잡힌 자도, 유물론자도, 냉소가도 한결같이 그 앞에서 사색에 잠기게 하는 이 현대의 바빌론에, 그가 꿰뚫는 듯한 시선을 못 박고는 입을 열었다."]], [["“Great city,”", "\"위대한 도시여,\""], ["murmured he, inclining his head, and joining his hands as if in prayer, “less than six months have elapsed since first I entered thy gates.", "그가 고개를 숙이고, 기도하듯 두 손을 모으며 중얼거렸다. \"내가 처음 네 문을 들어선 지 채 여섯 달도 지나지 않았구나."], ["I believe that the Spirit of God led my steps to thee and that he also enables me to quit thee in triumph; the secret cause of my presence within thy walls I have confided alone to him who only has had the power to read my heart.", "신의 영이 내 발걸음을 네게로 이끌었고, 또한 그분이 나를 승리 속에 너를 떠나게 해 주신다고 나는 믿는다. 내가 네 성벽 안에 있었던 그 은밀한 까닭은, 오직 내 마음을 읽을 힘을 지니신 그분께만 털어놓았다."], ["God only knows that I retire from thee without pride or hatred, but not without many regrets; he only knows that the power confided to me has never been made subservient to my personal good or to any useless cause.", "내가 교만도 증오도 없이, 그러나 적잖은 회한을 안고 너를 떠난다는 것을 오직 신만이 아신다. 내게 맡겨진 그 힘이 단 한 번도 내 사사로운 이익이나 부질없는 목적에 쓰이지 않았음을 오직 그분만이 아신다."], ["Oh, great city, it is in thy palpitating bosom that I have found that which I sought; like a patient miner, I have dug deep into thy very entrails to root out evil thence.", "오, 위대한 도시여, 내가 찾던 것을 발견한 곳이 바로 고동치는 네 가슴속이었다. 끈기 있는 광부처럼, 나는 거기서 악을 뿌리째 뽑아내려 네 창자 깊은 곳까지 파고들었다."], ["Now my work is accomplished, my mission is terminated, now thou canst neither afford me pain nor pleasure.", "이제 내 일은 다 이루어졌고, 내 사명은 끝났다. 이제 너는 내게 고통도 기쁨도 줄 수 없다."], ["Adieu, Paris, adieu!”", "잘 있거라, 파리여, 잘 있거라!\""]], [["His look wandered over the vast plain like that of some genius of the night; he passed his hand over his brow, got into the carriage, the door was closed on him, and the vehicle quickly disappeared down the other side of the hill in a whirlwind of dust and noise.", "그의 시선이 밤의 정령처럼 광막한 평야 위를 헤맸다. 그는 이마를 손으로 한번 쓸고는 마차에 올랐고, 문이 그의 등 뒤로 닫혔다. 마차는 먼지와 굉음의 회오리 속에 언덕 반대편으로 순식간에 사라졌다."]], [["Ten leagues were passed and not a single word was uttered. Morrel was dreaming, and Monte Cristo was looking at the dreamer.", "십 리외를 가는 동안 말 한마디 오가지 않았다. 모렐은 상념에 잠겨 있었고, 몬테크리스토는 그 상념에 잠긴 사내를 바라보고 있었다."]], [["“Morrel,”", "\"모렐,\""], ["said the count to him at length, “do you repent having followed me?”", "마침내 백작이 그에게 말했다. \"나를 따라온 걸 후회하나?\""]], [["“No, count; but to leave Paris——”", "\"아닙니다, 백작님. 하지만 파리를 떠나는 게……\""]], [["“If I thought happiness might await you in Paris, Morrel, I would have left you there.”", "\"파리에 자네를 기다리는 행복이 있다고 여겼다면, 모렐, 나는 자네를 거기 두고 왔을 걸세.\""]], [["“Valentine reposes within the walls of Paris, and to leave Paris is like losing her a second time.”", "\"발랑틴이 파리의 성벽 안에 잠들어 있습니다. 파리를 떠나는 건 그녀를 두 번째로 잃는 것과 같아요.\""]], [["“Maximilian,”", "\"막시밀리앙,\""], ["said the count, “the friends that we have lost do not repose in the bosom of the earth, but are buried deep in our hearts, and it has been thus ordained that we may always be accompanied by them.", "백작이 말했다. \"우리가 잃은 벗들은 땅의 품속에 잠드는 게 아니라, 우리 가슴 깊은 곳에 묻히는 거라네. 그래서 우리는 늘 그들과 함께하도록 정해진 것이지."], ["I have two friends, who in this way never depart from me; the one who gave me being, and the other who conferred knowledge and intelligence on me.", "내게도 그렇게 결코 내 곁을 떠나지 않는 벗이 둘 있어. 하나는 내게 생명을 준 이요, 다른 하나는 내게 지식과 지혜를 준 이라네."], ["Their spirits live in me.", "그들의 영혼이 내 안에 살아 있어."], ["I consult them when doubtful, and if I ever do any good, it is due to their beneficent counsels.", "의심이 들 때면 나는 그들에게 묻고, 내가 무슨 선한 일을 한다면 그건 그들의 자애로운 조언 덕분이지."], ["Listen to the voice of your heart, Morrel, and ask it whether you ought to preserve this melancholy exterior towards me.”", "자네 마음의 소리에 귀 기울여 보게, 모렐. 그리고 나를 대하는 이 침울한 낯빛을 계속 지녀야 하는지 그 마음에 물어보게.\""]], [["“My friend,”", "\"백작님,\""], ["said Maximilian, “the voice of my heart is very sorrowful, and promises me nothing but misfortune.”", "막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"제 마음의 소리는 너무도 슬프고, 제게 불행 말고는 아무것도 약속해 주지 않습니다.\""]], [["“It is the way of weakened minds to see everything through a black cloud.", "\"세상만사를 검은 구름 너머로 바라보는 것, 그게 나약해진 정신이 빠지는 길이라네."], ["The soul forms its own horizons; your soul is darkened, and consequently the sky of the future appears stormy and unpromising.”", "영혼은 스스로 자기 지평을 짓는 법이야. 자네 영혼이 어두워졌으니, 미래의 하늘도 폭풍우가 몰아칠 듯, 가망 없어 보이는 게지.\""]], [["“That may possibly be true,”", "\"그 말씀이 옳을지도 모르지요.\""], ["said Maximilian, and he again subsided into his thoughtful mood.", "막시밀리앙은 그렇게 말하고는 다시 깊은 생각에 잠겨 들었다."]], [["The journey was performed with that marvellous rapidity which the unlimited power of the count ever commanded.", "여정은 백작의 무한한 권력이 늘 부리던 그 놀라운 속도로 이어졌다."], ["Towns fled from them like shadows on their path, and trees shaken by the first winds of autumn seemed like giants madly rushing on to meet them, and retreating as rapidly when once reached.", "도시들이 길 위의 그림자처럼 그들에게서 달아났고, 첫 가을바람에 흔들리는 나무들은 미친 듯이 달려와 그들을 맞이하다가 막상 닿으면 그만큼 빠르게 물러가는 거인들 같았다."], ["The following morning they arrived at Châlons, where the count’s steamboat waited for them.", "이튿날 아침 그들은 샬롱에 닿았고, 그곳에는 백작의 증기선이 기다리고 있었다."], ["Without the loss of an instant, the carriage was placed on board and the two travellers embarked without delay.", "한순간도 지체하지 않고 마차를 배에 실었고, 두 여행자는 곧장 승선했다."], ["The boat was built for speed; her two paddle-wheels were like two wings with which she skimmed the water like a bird.", "속도를 내도록 만들어진 배였다. 양쪽의 외륜 두 짝은 날개와도 같아서, 배는 새처럼 수면을 스쳐 나아갔다."]], [["Morrel was not insensible to that sensation of delight which is generally experienced in passing rapidly through the air, and the wind which occasionally raised the hair from his forehead seemed on the point of dispelling momentarily the clouds collected there.", "빠르게 바람을 가르며 나아갈 때 흔히 맛보는 그 황홀한 기분을, 모렐도 느끼지 못하는 것은 아니었다. 이따금 이마에서 머리카락을 흩날리는 바람은, 거기 모여든 구름을 한순간이나마 걷어 낼 듯도 했다."]], [["As the distance increased between the travellers and Paris, almost superhuman serenity appeared to surround the count; he might have been taken for an exile about to revisit his native land.", "여행자들과 파리 사이의 거리가 멀어질수록, 거의 초인적인 평온이 백작을 감싸는 듯했다. 마치 고국으로 돌아가는 망명객처럼 보였다."]], [["Ere long Marseilles presented herself to view,—Marseilles, white, fervid, full of life and energy,—Marseilles, the younger sister of Tyre and Carthage, the successor to them in the empire of the Mediterranean,—Marseilles, old, yet always young.", "이윽고 마르세유가 눈앞에 모습을 드러냈다. 희고, 뜨겁고, 생기와 활력이 넘치는 마르세유. 티레와 카르타고의 아우뻘이요, 지중해의 패권을 이어받은 후계자인 마르세유."], ["Powerful memories were stirred within them by the sight of the round tower, Fort Saint-Nicolas, the City Hall designed by Puget,28 the port with its brick quays, where they had both played in childhood, and it was with one accord that they stopped on the Canebière.", "늙었으되 언제나 젊은 마르세유. 둥근 탑과 생니콜라 요새, 퓌제가 설계한 시청사, 그리고 두 사람 다 어린 시절 뛰놀던 벽돌 부두가 있는 항구를 보자, 강렬한 추억들이 그들 가슴속에서 일렁였다. 그리하여 두 사람은 약속이나 한 듯 카느비에르 거리에서 발걸음을 멈췄다."]], [["A vessel was setting sail for Algiers, on board of which the bustle usually attending departure prevailed.", "알제로 떠날 배 한 척에서, 으레 출항에 따르기 마련인 분주함이 한창이었다. 승객들과 그 가족들이 갑판을 가득 메웠고, 벗들은 다정하면서도 서글픈 작별을 나누고 있었다."], ["The passengers and their relations crowded on the deck, friends taking a tender but sorrowful leave of each other, some weeping, others noisy in their grief, the whole forming a spectacle that might be exciting even to those who witnessed similar sights daily, but which had no power to disturb the current of thought that had taken possession of the mind of Maximilian from the moment he had set foot on the broad pavement of the quay.", "더러는 눈물을 흘렸고, 더러는 큰 소리로 슬픔을 토했다. 그 모든 광경은, 그런 장면을 날마다 보는 이들에게조차 가슴 설레게 할 만한 것이었다. 그러나 부두의 너른 포석에 발을 디딘 순간부터 막시밀리앙의 마음을 사로잡은 상념의 흐름만은 흔들어 놓지 못했다."]], [["“Here,”", "\"여기예요,\""], ["said he, leaning heavily on the arm of Monte Cristo,—“here is the spot where my father stopped, when the _Pharaon_ entered the port; it was here that the good old man, whom you saved from death and dishonor, threw himself into my arms.", "그가 몬테크리스토의 팔에 몸을 무겁게 기대며 말했다. \"여기가 바로, 파라옹호가 입항할 때 제 아버지가 멈춰 서 계시던 자리예요. 백작님이 죽음과 불명예에서 구해 주신 그 마음씨 좋은 노인이, 바로 여기서 제 품으로 뛰어드셨지요."], ["I yet feel his warm tears on my face, and his were not the only tears shed, for many who witnessed our meeting wept also.”", "아직도 제 얼굴에 그분의 뜨거운 눈물이 느껴집니다. 눈물을 흘린 게 그분만은 아니었어요. 우리가 만나는 광경을 지켜보던 많은 이들도 함께 울었으니까요.\""]], [["Monte Cristo gently smiled and said,—“I was there;”", "몬테크리스토는 가만히 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["at the same time pointing to the corner of a street.", "\"나도 그 자리에 있었네.\" 그러면서 어느 골목 모퉁이를 가리켰다."], ["As he spoke, and in the very direction he indicated, a groan, expressive of bitter grief, was heard, and a woman was seen waving her hand to a passenger on board the vessel about to sail.", "그가 말하며 가리킨 바로 그 방향에서, 비통한 슬픔이 묻어나는 신음 소리가 들려왔다. 한 여인이 막 출항하려는 배 위의 승객을 향해 손을 흔들고 있었다."], ["Monte Cristo looked at her with an emotion that must have been remarked by Morrel had not his eyes been fixed on the vessel.", "몬테크리스토는 어떤 감정을 담고 그녀를 바라보았다. 모렐의 눈이 배에 못 박혀 있지 않았더라면 분명 알아챘을 만한 감정이었다."]], [["“Oh, heavens!”", "\"오, 세상에!\""], ["exclaimed Morrel, “I do not deceive myself—that young man who is waving his hat, that youth in the uniform of a lieutenant, is Albert de Morcerf!”", "모렐이 외쳤다. \"잘못 본 게 아니에요. 저기 모자를 흔들고 있는 저 청년, 중위 군복을 입은 저 젊은이는 알베르 드 모르세르예요!\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇다네.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “I recognized him.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"나도 알아봤네.\""]], [["“How so?—you were looking the other way.” 50209m", "\"어떻게요? 백작님은 다른 쪽을 보고 계셨잖아요.\""]], [["The count smiled, as he was in the habit of doing when he did not want to make any reply, and he again turned towards the veiled woman, who soon disappeared at the corner of the street.", "백작은, 대답하고 싶지 않을 때면 으레 그랬듯이 미소를 지었다. 그러고는 다시 베일을 쓴 그 여인 쪽으로 시선을 돌렸다. 여인은 이내 골목 모퉁이로 사라졌다."], ["Turning to his friend:", "백작이 벗을 돌아보며 말했다."]], [["“Dear Maximilian,” said the count, “have you nothing to do in this land?”", "\"막시밀리앙, 이 고장에서 자네가 할 일이 있나?\""]], [["“I have to weep over the grave of my father,”", "\"아버지의 무덤 앞에서 울어야 합니다.\""], ["replied Morrel in a broken voice.", "모렐이 갈라진 목소리로 대답했다."]], [["“Well, then, go,—wait for me there, and I will soon join you.”", "\"그렇다면 가 보게. 거기서 나를 기다리게. 나도 곧 가겠네.\""]], [["“You leave me, then?”", "\"그럼 저를 두고 가시는 겁니까?\""]], [["“Yes; I also have a pious visit to pay.”", "\"그래. 나도 경건히 들러야 할 곳이 있다네.\""]], [["Morrel allowed his hand to fall into that which the count extended to him; then with an inexpressibly sorrowful inclination of the head he quitted the count and bent his steps to the east of the city.", "모렐은 백작이 내미는 손에 자기 손을 맡겼다. 그런 뒤 이루 말할 수 없이 서글픈 몸짓으로 고개를 숙이고는 백작 곁을 떠나, 도시 동쪽으로 발걸음을 옮겼다."], ["Monte Cristo remained on the same spot until Maximilian was out of sight; he then walked slowly towards the Allées de Meilhan to seek out a small house with which our readers were made familiar at the beginning of this story.", "몬테크리스토는 막시밀리앙이 시야에서 사라질 때까지 그 자리에 그대로 서 있었다. 그런 뒤 메양 가로수길 쪽으로 천천히 걸음을 옮겼다. 이 이야기 첫머리에서 독자들이 익히 알게 된 작은 집을 찾아가려는 것이었다."]], [["It yet stood, under the shade of the fine avenue of lime-trees, which forms one of the most frequent walks of the idlers of Marseilles, covered by an immense vine, which spreads its aged and blackened branches over the stone front, burnt yellow by the ardent sun of the south.", "그 집은 여전히 그 자리에, 마르세유의 한량들이 가장 즐겨 거니는 산책로 가운데 하나인 그 멋진 보리수 가로수 그늘 아래 서 있었다. 거대한 포도 덩굴이 집을 뒤덮고, 늙어 거뭇해진 가지를 남국의 뜨거운 햇살에 누렇게 그을린 돌벽 위로 뻗고 있었다."], ["Two stone steps worn away by the friction of many feet led to the door, which was made of three planks; the door had never been painted or varnished, so great cracks yawned in it during the dry season to close again when the rains came on.", "수많은 발길에 닳아빠진 돌계단 두 단을 오르면 널빤지 세 장으로 짠 문이 있었다. 한 번도 페인트칠이나 니스칠을 한 적 없는 문이라, 마른 철에는 큼직한 틈이 쩍 벌어졌다가 비가 오면 다시 아물곤 했다."], ["The house, with all its crumbling antiquity and apparent misery, was yet cheerful and picturesque, and was the same that old Dantès formerly inhabited—the only difference being that the old man occupied merely the garret, while the whole house was now placed at the command of Mercédès by the count.", "그 집은 허물어져 가는 낡은 모습에 가난이 역력했지만, 그래도 어딘가 정겹고 그림 같았다. 옛날에 당테스 노인이 살던 바로 그 집이었다. 다만 노인은 다락방 하나만 쓰던 것에 반해, 이제는 백작의 뜻에 따라 집 전체가 메르세데스의 차지가 되었다는 점이 다를 뿐이었다."]], [["The woman whom the count had seen leave the ship with so much regret entered this house; she had scarcely closed the door after her when Monte Cristo appeared at the corner of a street, so that he found and lost her again almost at the same instant.", "백작이 그토록 안타까운 마음으로 그 자리를 떠나는 것을 지켜보았던 그 여인이 바로 이 집으로 들어섰다. 그녀가 등 뒤로 문을 채 닫기도 전에 몬테크리스토가 골목 모퉁이에 모습을 드러냈다. 그리하여 그는 거의 같은 순간에 그녀를 찾았다가 다시 놓쳤다."], ["The worn out steps were old acquaintances of his; he knew better than anyone else how to open that weather-beaten door with the large headed nail which served to raise the latch within.", "닳아빠진 그 계단은 그에게 오랜 벗과도 같았다. 안쪽 빗장을 들어 올리는 데 쓰는, 대가리 굵은 못이 박힌 그 비바람에 시달린 문을 어떻게 여는지, 그는 누구보다 잘 알았다."], ["He entered without knocking, or giving any other intimation of his presence, as if he had been a friend or the master of the place.", "그는 노크도, 자신이 왔음을 알리는 그 어떤 기척도 없이 안으로 들어섰다. 마치 이 집의 벗이거나 주인이라도 되는 듯이."], ["At the end of a passage paved with bricks, was a little garden, bathed in sunshine, and rich in warmth and light.", "벽돌이 깔린 통로 끝에는 햇살이 흠뻑 든, 온기와 빛이 가득한 작은 뜰이 있었다."], ["In this garden Mercédès had found, at the place indicated by the count, the sum of money which he, through a sense of delicacy, had described as having been placed there twenty-four years previously.", "바로 이 뜰에서, 백작이 일러 준 자리에서 메르세데스는 돈 한 꾸러미를 찾아냈다. 백작이 섬세한 배려로, 이십사 년 전에 거기 묻어 두었던 것이라 둘러댄 그 돈이었다."], ["The trees of the garden were easily seen from the steps of the street-door.", "뜰의 나무들은 길가 문간 계단에서도 한눈에 보였다."]], [["Monte Cristo, on stepping into the house, heard a sigh that was almost a deep sob; he looked in the direction whence it came, and there under an arbor of Virginia jessamine,29 with its thick foliage and beautiful long purple flowers, he saw Mercédès seated, with her head bowed, and weeping bitterly.", "집으로 발을 들이는 순간, 몬테크리스토는 깊은 흐느낌에 가까운 한숨 소리를 들었다. 소리가 나는 쪽으로 눈길을 돌리니, 짙은 잎과 길고 아름다운 자줏빛 꽃이 어우러진 버지니아재스민 정자 아래, 메르세데스가 고개를 숙이고 앉아 쓰라리게 울고 있었다."], ["She had raised her veil, and with her face hidden by her hands was giving free scope to the sighs and tears which had been so long restrained by the presence of her son.", "그녀는 베일을 들어 올린 채, 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고서, 아들이 곁에 있는 동안 그토록 오래 억눌러 온 한숨과 눈물을 마음껏 쏟아 내고 있었다."]], [["Monte Cristo advanced a few steps, which were heard on the gravel.", "몬테크리스토가 몇 걸음 다가서자, 자갈 위로 발소리가 났다."], ["Mercédès raised her head, and uttered a cry of terror on beholding a man before her.", "메르세데스가 고개를 들었다. 그리고 자기 앞에 한 사내가 서 있는 것을 보고 공포에 찬 비명을 질렀다."]], [["50211m\n“Madame,”", "\"부인,\" 백작이 말했다."], ["said the count, “it is no longer in my power to restore you to happiness, but I offer you consolation; will you deign to accept it as coming from a friend?”", "\"이제 부인에게 행복을 돌려드리는 것은 제 힘으로 할 수 있는 일이 아닙니다. 하지만 위로는 드릴 수 있지요. 한 벗이 드리는 것이라 여기고 받아 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“I am, indeed, most wretched,”", "\"저는 정말이지 더없이 비참해요.\""], ["replied Mercédès.", "메르세데스가 대답했다."], ["“Alone in the world, I had but my son, and he has left me!”", "\"이 세상에 홀로 남았어요. 제겐 아들 하나뿐이었는데, 그 아이마저 저를 떠나 버렸으니!\""]], [["“He possesses a noble heart, madame,”", "\"아드님은 고귀한 마음을 지녔습니다, 부인.\""], ["replied the count, “and he has acted rightly.", "백작이 대답했다. \"그리고 옳게 처신했지요."], ["He feels that every man owes a tribute to his country; some contribute their talents, others their industry; these devote their blood, those their nightly labors, to the same cause.", "사람은 누구나 제 조국에 마땅히 바칠 것이 있다는 걸 그는 알고 있습니다. 어떤 이는 재능을, 어떤 이는 노력을 바치지요. 또 어떤 이는 피를, 어떤 이는 밤을 새우는 수고를 같은 대의에 바칩니다."], ["Had he remained with you, his life must have become a hateful burden, nor would he have participated in your griefs.", "만약 그가 부인 곁에 남았더라면, 그의 삶은 견디기 힘든 짐이 되었을 테고, 부인의 슬픔에 함께하지도 못했을 겁니다."], ["He will increase in strength and honor by struggling with adversity, which he will convert into prosperity.", "그는 역경과 맞서 싸우며 더욱 강해지고 더욱 명예로워질 것이며, 그 역경을 번영으로 바꿔 놓을 겁니다."], ["Leave him to build up the future for you, and I venture to say you will confide it to safe hands.”", "그가 부인을 위해 미래를 일구도록 맡겨 두십시오. 감히 말씀드리건대, 부인은 그것을 믿음직한 손에 맡기시는 겁니다.\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오,\""], ["replied the wretched woman, mournfully shaking her head, “the prosperity of which you speak, and which, from the bottom of my heart, I pray God in his mercy to grant him, I can never enjoy.", "가엾은 여인이 슬프게 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"말씀하시는 그 번영, 자비로우신 하느님께서 그 애에게 베풀어 주십사 제 마음 깊은 곳에서 비는 그 번영을, 저는 결코 누리지 못할 거예요."], ["The bitter cup of adversity has been drained by me to the very dregs, and I feel that the grave is not far distant.", "역경의 쓰디쓴 잔을 저는 찌꺼기 한 방울까지 다 들이켰고, 이제 무덤이 멀지 않았음을 느낍니다."], ["You have acted kindly, count, in bringing me back to the place where I have enjoyed so much bliss.", "제가 그토록 큰 행복을 누렸던 이곳으로 저를 데려와 주신 것은 친절한 일이셨어요, 백작님."], ["I ought to meet death on the same spot where happiness was once all my own.”", "한때 온전히 제 것이었던 행복이 깃든 바로 그 자리에서, 저도 죽음을 맞아야 마땅하니까요.\""]], [["“Alas,”", "\"아아,\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “your words sear and embitter my heart, the more so as you have every reason to hate me.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"부인의 말씀이 제 가슴을 지지고 쓰라리게 합니다. 더구나 부인께서는 저를 미워하실 온갖 이유가 있으니 더욱 그렇지요."], ["I have been the cause of all your misfortunes; but why do you pity, instead of blaming me?", "제가 부인의 모든 불행의 원인이었습니다. 그런데 어찌하여 저를 탓하지 않고 가엾이 여기시는 겁니까?"], ["You render me still more unhappy——”", "부인은 저를 더욱 불행하게 만드십니다……\""]], [["“Hate you, blame you—_you_, Edmond!", "\"당신을 미워하다니요, 탓하다니요."], ["Hate, reproach, the man that has spared my son’s life!", "당신을, 에드몽을요! 제 아들의 목숨을 살려 준 사람을 미워하고 책망하라고요!"], ["For was it not your fatal and sanguinary intention to destroy that son of whom M.", "모르세르 씨가 그토록 자랑스러워하던 그 아들을 없애 버리는 게, 당신의 치명적이고 피비린내 나는 뜻이 아니었던가요?"], ["de Morcerf was so proud?", "오, 저를 똑똑히 보세요."], ["Oh, look at me closely, and discover, if you can, even the semblance of a reproach in me.”", "그리고 할 수 있다면, 제게서 책망하는 기색이 한 자락이라도 있는지 찾아보세요.\""]], [["The count looked up and fixed his eyes on Mercédès, who arose partly from her seat and extended both her hands towards him.", "백작이 고개를 들어 메르세데스에게 시선을 못 박았다. 그녀는 의자에서 반쯤 몸을 일으키며 두 손을 그에게 내밀었다."]], [["“Oh, look at me,”", "\"오, 저를 보세요,\""], ["continued she, with a feeling of profound melancholy, “my eyes no longer dazzle by their brilliancy, for the time has long fled since I used to smile on Edmond Dantès, who anxiously looked out for me from the window of yonder garret, then inhabited by his old father.", "그녀가 깊은 우수에 잠긴 채 말을 이었다. \"제 눈은 더 이상 광채로 사람을 현혹하지 못해요. 저쪽 다락방 창가에서, 그 시절 그곳에 살던 늙은 아버지 곁에서 저를 애타게 내다보던 에드몽 당테스에게 미소 짓던 그때로부터 너무도 오랜 세월이 흘렀으니까요."], ["Years of grief have created an abyss between those days and the present.", "슬픔의 세월이 그 시절과 지금 사이에 깊은 골을 파 놓았어요."], ["I neither reproach you nor hate you, my friend.", "저는 당신을 책망하지도 미워하지도 않아요, 벗이여."], ["Oh, no, Edmond, it is myself that I blame, myself that I hate! Oh, miserable creature that I am!”", "오, 아니에요, 에드몽, 제가 탓하는 건 바로 저 자신이고, 제가 미워하는 것도 바로 저 자신이에요. 오, 가엾은 이 몸!\""], ["cried she, clasping her hands, and raising her eyes to heaven.", "그녀가 두 손을 맞잡고 하늘로 눈을 들어 올리며 외쳤다."], ["“I once possessed piety, innocence, and love, the three ingredients of the happiness of angels, and now what am I?”", "\"한때 저는 신심과 순결과 사랑을 지녔어요. 천사들의 행복을 이루는 그 세 가지를요. 그런데 지금의 저는 무엇인가요?\""]], [["Monte Cristo approached her, and silently took her hand.", "몬테크리스토가 그녀에게 다가가, 말없이 그 손을 잡았다."]], [["“No,” said she, withdrawing it gently—“no, my friend, touch me not.", "\"안 돼요,\" 그녀가 손을 가만히 빼며 말했다."], ["You have spared me, yet of all those who have fallen under your vengeance I was the most guilty.", "\"안 돼요, 벗이여, 저를 만지지 마세요. 당신은 저를 살려 주셨지만, 당신의 복수 아래 쓰러진 그 모든 이들 가운데 가장 죄 많은 사람은 바로 저였어요."], ["They were influenced by hatred, by avarice, and by self-love; but I was base, and for want of courage acted against my judgment.", "그들은 증오에, 탐욕에, 자기애에 휘둘렸지요. 하지만 저는 비열했어요. 용기가 없어서, 제 판단을 거스르며 행동했으니까요."], ["Nay, do not press my hand, Edmond; you are thinking, I am sure, of some kind speech to console me, but do not utter it to me, reserve it for others more worthy of your kindness. See”", "아니에요, 제 손을 잡지 마세요, 에드몽. 저를 위로할 다정한 말 한마디를 떠올리고 계신 거 분명해요. 하지만 제게는 하지 마세요."], ["(and she exposed her face completely to view)—“see, misfortune has silvered my hair, my eyes have shed so many tears that they are encircled by a rim of purple, and my brow is wrinkled.", "당신의 친절을 받을 자격이 더 있는 다른 이들을 위해 아껴 두세요. 보세요.\" (그러면서 그녀는 얼굴을 온전히 드러냈다.) \"보세요, 불행이 제 머리카락을 세어 놓았고, 제 눈은 하도 많이 울어서 자줏빛 테가 둘러졌으며, 제 이마엔 주름이 잡혔어요."], ["You, Edmond, on the contrary,—you are still young, handsome, dignified; it is because you have had faith; because you have had strength, because you have had trust in God, and God has sustained you.", "그런데 에드몽, 당신은 반대로 여전히 젊고, 잘생겼고, 위엄이 있어요. 그건 당신에게 믿음이 있었기 때문이고, 당신에게 힘이 있었기 때문이며, 당신이 하느님을 의지했고 하느님이 당신을 지켜 주셨기 때문이에요."], ["But as for me, I have been a coward; I have denied God and he has abandoned me.” 50213m", "하지만 저로 말하자면, 저는 겁쟁이였어요. 하느님을 저버렸고, 그래서 하느님도 저를 버리신 거예요.\""]], [["Mercédès burst into tears; her woman’s heart was breaking under its load of memories.", "메르세데스가 울음을 터뜨렸다. 추억의 무게에 짓눌려 그녀의 여인다운 마음이 무너지고 있었다."], ["Monte Cristo took her hand and imprinted a kiss on it; but she herself felt that it was a kiss of no greater warmth than he would have bestowed on the hand of some marble statue of a saint.", "몬테크리스토가 그녀의 손을 잡아 그 위에 입을 맞추었다. 그러나 그녀 자신도 느꼈다. 그것이 어느 성인의 대리석상 손에 입맞춤할 때나 보였을 법한, 그 이상도 이하도 아닌 온기의 입맞춤이라는 것을."]], [["“It often happens,”", "\"흔히 있는 일이지요,\""], ["continued she, “that a first fault destroys the prospects of a whole life.", "그녀가 말을 이었다. \"단 한 번의 잘못이 한평생의 앞날을 송두리째 무너뜨리는 일이요. 저는 당신이 죽은 줄 알았어요."], ["I believed you dead; why did I survive you?", "그런데 어째서 저는 당신보다 더 살아남았을까요?"], ["What good has it done me to mourn for you eternally in the secret recesses of my heart?—only to make a woman of thirty-nine look like a woman of fifty.", "마음속 은밀한 구석에서 영영 당신을 애도한 게 제게 무슨 득이 되었나요? 그저 서른아홉 살 먹은 여자를 쉰 살 노파처럼 보이게 만들었을 뿐인걸요."], ["Why, having recognized you, and I the only one to do so—why was I able to save my son alone?", "어째서, 당신을 알아본 사람이, 그것도 오직 저 하나뿐이었으면서, 어째서 저는 제 아들만 구할 수 있었을까요?"], ["Ought I not also to have rescued the man that I had accepted for a husband, guilty though he were? Yet I let him die!", "죄가 있다 한들, 제가 남편으로 받아들였던 그 사람도 함께 구해야 마땅하지 않았을까요? 그런데 저는 그를 죽도록 내버려 두었어요!"], ["What do I say? Oh, merciful heavens, was I not accessory to his death by my supine insensibility, by my contempt for him, not remembering, or not willing to remember, that it was for my sake he had become a traitor and a perjurer?", "무슨 말을 하는 거죠? 오, 자비로우신 하느님, 그가 저를 위해 배신자가 되고 위증자가 되었다는 걸 기억하지 못한 채, 아니 기억하려 하지 않은 채, 제 무기력한 냉담함으로, 그에 대한 제 경멸로, 저는 그의 죽음에 한몫한 게 아니었나요?"], ["In what am I benefited by accompanying my son so far, since I now abandon him, and allow him to depart alone to the baneful climate of Africa?", "아들을 이 먼 곳까지 따라온 게 제게 무슨 소용이 있나요? 이제 그 애를 버려두고, 홀로 아프리카의 그 해로운 땅으로 떠나가게 했으니 말이에요."], ["Oh, I have been base, cowardly, I tell you; I have abjured my affections, and like all renegades I am of evil omen to those who surround me!”", "오, 거듭 말하지만, 저는 비열하고 비겁했어요. 제 애정을 저버렸고, 모든 배교자가 그렇듯, 제 곁에 있는 이들에게 불길한 징조가 되는 사람이에요!\""]], [["“No, Mercédès,”", "\"아니에요, 메르세데스,\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “no; you judge yourself with too much severity.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"아닙니다. 부인은 스스로를 너무 가혹하게 단죄하고 있어요."], ["You are a noble-minded woman, and it was your grief that disarmed me.", "부인은 고귀한 마음을 지닌 여인이고, 저를 무장 해제시킨 것은 바로 부인의 슬픔이었습니다."], ["Still I was but an agent, led on by an invisible and offended Deity, who chose not to withhold the fatal blow that I was destined to hurl.", "그래도 저는 한낱 도구였을 뿐, 보이지 않는, 노여움에 찬 신께서 저를 이끄셨던 겁니다. 그분은 제가 내리치도록 정해진 그 치명적인 일격을 거두지 않기로 택하셨지요."], ["I take that God to witness, at whose feet I have prostrated myself daily for the last ten years, that I would have sacrificed my life to you, and with my life the projects that were indissolubly linked with it. But—and I say it with some pride, Mercédès—God needed me, and I lived.", "지난 십 년간 날마다 그 발치에 엎드려 온 그 하느님을 증인으로 삼아 말하건대, 저는 부인을 위해서라면 제 목숨을, 그리고 그 목숨과 떼려야 뗄 수 없이 얽힌 그 계획들까지 기꺼이 바쳤을 겁니다. 하지만, 그리고 이건 다소 자랑스럽게 말씀드리는 것입니다만, 메르세데스, 하느님께 제가 필요했기에 저는 살아남았습니다."], ["Examine the past and the present, and endeavor to dive into futurity, and then say whether I am not a divine instrument.", "지난날과 지금을 살펴보시고, 또 앞날을 헤아려 보려 애써 보십시오. 그런 다음에 제가 신의 도구가 아닌지 말씀해 보십시오."], ["The most dreadful misfortunes, the most frightful sufferings, the abandonment of all those who loved me, the persecution of those who did not know me, formed the trials of my youth; when suddenly, from captivity, solitude, misery, I was restored to light and liberty, and became the possessor of a fortune so brilliant, so unbounded, so unheard-of, that I must have been blind not to be conscious that God had endowed me with it to work out his own great designs.", "더없이 무서운 불행들, 더없이 끔찍한 고통들, 저를 사랑하던 모든 이들의 외면, 저를 알지도 못하는 자들의 박해, 그것들이 제 젊은 날의 시련이었습니다. 그러던 어느 순간, 유폐와 고독과 비참함에서 저는 빛과 자유를 되찾았고, 더없이 눈부시고, 더없이 끝 간 데 없으며, 더없이 전대미문의 재산을 손에 넣은 사람이 되었지요. 하느님이 그분의 위대한 뜻을 이루시려 그것을 제게 내려 주셨음을 깨닫지 못한다면, 저는 눈먼 자였을 겁니다."], ["From that time I looked upon this fortune as something confided to me for a particular purpose.", "그때부터 저는 이 재산을, 어떤 특별한 목적을 위해 제게 맡겨진 것으로 여겼습니다."], ["Not a thought was given to a life which you once, Mercédès, had the power to render blissful; not one hour of peaceful calm was mine; but I felt myself driven on like an exterminating angel.", "한때 부인이, 메르세데스, 부인이 더없이 복되게 만들어 줄 수도 있었던 그 삶에는 단 한 생각도 두지 않았지요. 평온하고 고요한 시간은 단 한 시간도 제 것이 아니었습니다. 저는 절멸의 천사처럼 떠밀려 나아가는 저 자신을 느꼈으니까요."], ["Like adventurous captains about to embark on some enterprise full of danger, I laid in my provisions, I loaded my weapons, I collected every means of attack and defence; I inured my body to the most violent exercises, my soul to the bitterest trials; I taught my arm to slay, my eyes to behold excruciating sufferings, and my mouth to smile at the most horrid spectacles.", "위험으로 가득한 어떤 사업에 막 뛰어들려는 모험심 강한 선장들처럼, 저는 식량을 비축하고, 무기를 장전하고, 공격과 방어의 온갖 수단을 그러모았습니다. 제 몸은 더없이 격렬한 단련에, 제 영혼은 더없이 쓰라린 시련에 길들였지요. 제 팔에는 죽이는 법을, 제 눈에는 처참한 고통을 지켜보는 법을, 제 입에는 더없이 끔찍한 광경 앞에서도 미소 짓는 법을 가르쳤습니다."], ["Good-natured, confiding, and forgiving as I had been, I became revengeful, cunning, and wicked, or rather, immovable as fate.", "사람 좋고, 잘 믿고, 잘 용서하던 제가, 복수심에 차고, 교활하고, 사악한 사람이, 아니 차라리 운명처럼 흔들림 없는 사람이 되었지요."], ["Then I launched out into the path that was opened to me.", "그런 다음 제 앞에 열린 길로 나아갔습니다."], ["I overcame every obstacle, and reached the goal; but woe to those who stood in my pathway!”", "모든 장애물을 넘어 목표에 다다랐지요. 하지만 제 길을 가로막은 자들에게는 화가 있으리니!\""]], [["50215m\n“Enough,”", "\"그만하세요,\""], ["said Mercédès; “enough, Edmond!", "메르세데스가 말했다. \"그만하세요, 에드몽!"], ["Believe me, that she who alone recognized you has been the only one to comprehend you; and had she crossed your path, and you had crushed her like glass, still, Edmond, still she must have admired you!", "믿어 주세요, 오직 당신을 알아본 그 여자만이 당신을 이해한 유일한 사람이었어요. 그리고 그 여자가 당신의 길을 가로막아, 당신이 그녀를 유리처럼 으스러뜨렸다 해도, 에드몽, 그래도 그녀는 당신을 우러러볼 수밖에 없었을 거예요!"], ["Like the gulf between me and the past, there is an abyss between you, Edmond, and the rest of mankind; and I tell you freely that the comparison I draw between you and other men will ever be one of my greatest tortures.", "저와 지난날 사이에 깊은 골이 있듯, 에드몽, 당신과 나머지 인류 사이에도 깊은 심연이 있어요. 그리고 솔직히 말하지만, 당신과 다른 사내들을 견주어 보는 일은 앞으로도 제게 가장 큰 고통 중 하나로 남을 거예요."], ["No, there is nothing in the world to resemble you in worth and goodness!", "아니에요, 가치로 보나 선함으로 보나 이 세상에 당신과 견줄 만한 건 아무것도 없어요!"], ["But we must say farewell, Edmond, and let us part.”", "하지만 우리는 작별해야 해요, 에드몽. 이제 헤어지기로 해요.\""]], [["“Before I leave you, Mercédès, have you no request to make?”", "\"부인 곁을 떠나기 전에, 메르세데스, 제게 부탁할 일은 없으십니까?\""], ["said the count.", "백작이 말했다."]], [["“I desire but one thing in this world, Edmond,—the happiness of my son.”", "\"이 세상에서 제가 바라는 건 단 하나예요, 에드몽. 제 아들의 행복이요.\""]], [["“Pray to the Almighty to spare his life, and I will take upon myself to promote his happiness.”", "\"전능하신 분께 그의 목숨을 지켜 주십사 기도하십시오. 그의 행복을 이루는 일은 제가 떠맡겠습니다.\""]], [["“Thank you, Edmond.”", "\"고마워요, 에드몽.\""]], [["“But have you no request to make for yourself, Mercédès?”", "\"하지만 부인 자신을 위해서는 부탁할 게 없으십니까, 메르세데스?\""]], [["“For myself I want nothing.", "\"저 자신을 위해서는 아무것도 바라지 않아요."], ["I live, as it were, between two graves.", "저는 말하자면 두 무덤 사이에서 살아가고 있어요."], ["One is that of Edmond Dantès, lost to me long, long since.", "하나는 오래오래전에 제게서 사라져 버린 에드몽 당테스의 무덤이에요."], ["He had my love! That word ill becomes my faded lip now, but it is a memory dear to my heart, and one that I would not lose for all that the world contains.", "그는 제 사랑을 받았지요! 이제 빛바랜 제 입술에는 어울리지 않는 말이지만, 제 가슴에 소중한 추억이고, 이 세상 모든 것을 다 준다 해도 잃고 싶지 않은 추억이에요."], ["The other grave is that of the man who met his death from the hand of Edmond Dantès. I approve of the deed, but I must pray for the dead.”", "다른 하나는 에드몽 당테스의 손에 죽음을 맞은 그 사내의 무덤이에요. 저는 그 일을 옳다고 여기지만, 그래도 죽은 이를 위해 기도는 해야 하니까요.\""]], [["“Your son shall be happy, Mercédès,”", "\"부인의 아드님은 행복해질 겁니다, 메르세데스.\""], ["repeated the count.", "백작이 거듭 말했다."]], [["“Then I shall enjoy as much happiness as this world can possibly confer.”", "\"그렇다면 저는 이 세상이 줄 수 있는 만큼의 행복은 누리는 셈이겠지요.\""]], [["“But what are your intentions?”", "\"그런데 앞으로 어찌하실 작정이십니까?\""]], [["Mercédès smiled sadly.", "메르세데스가 슬프게 미소 지었다."]], [["“To say that I shall live here, like the Mercédès of other times, gaining my bread by labor, would not be true, nor would you believe me.", "\"옛날의 그 메르세데스처럼 여기서 일해 빵을 벌며 살겠다고 말한다면, 그건 사실이 아닐 거예요. 당신도 믿지 않으실 테고요."], ["I have no longer the strength to do anything but to spend my days in prayer.", "이젠 기도로 하루하루를 보내는 것 말고는 아무것도 할 힘이 없어요."], ["However, I shall have no occasion to work, for the little sum of money buried by you, and which I found in the place you mentioned, will be sufficient to maintain me.", "그래도 일할 필요는 없을 거예요. 당신이 묻어 두었던, 일러 주신 자리에서 제가 찾아낸 그 얼마 안 되는 돈이면 저 한 몸 건사하기엔 넉넉할 테니까요."], ["Rumor will probably be busy respecting me, my occupations, my manner of living—that will signify but little, that concerns God, you, and myself.”", "아마 저를 두고, 제가 하는 일이며 사는 모양새를 두고 소문이 무성하겠지요. 하지만 그런 건 거의 아무래도 좋아요. 그건 하느님과 당신과 저, 우리만의 일이니까요.\""]], [["“Mercédès,”", "\"메르세데스,\""], ["said the count, “I do not say it to blame you, but you made an unnecessary sacrifice in relinquishing the whole of the fortune amassed by M.", "백작이 말했다. \"탓하려고 드리는 말씀은 아닙니다만, 부인은 모르세르 씨가 모은 재산 전부를 내놓음으로써 공연한 희생을 치르셨습니다."], ["de Morcerf; half of it at least by right belonged to you, in virtue of your vigilance and economy.”", "부인의 알뜰함과 절약을 생각하면, 적어도 그 절반은 당연히 부인 몫이었으니까요.\""]], [["“I perceive what you are intending to propose to me; but I cannot accept it, Edmond—my son would not permit it.”", "\"제게 무슨 말씀을 하시려는지 알겠어요. 하지만 받을 수 없어요, 에드몽. 제 아들이 허락하지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“Nothing shall be done without the full approbation of Albert de Morcerf.", "\"알베르 드 모르세르의 온전한 동의 없이는 아무 일도 하지 않겠습니다."], ["I will make myself acquainted with his intentions and will submit to them. But if he be willing to accept my offers, will you oppose them?”", "제가 그의 뜻을 알아보고, 거기에 따르겠습니다. 하지만 만약 그가 제 제안을 받아들이려 한다면, 부인은 반대하시겠습니까?\""]], [["“You well know, Edmond, that I am no longer a reasoning creature; I have no will, unless it be the will never to decide.", "\"잘 아시잖아요, 에드몽. 저는 이제 무얼 따져 헤아릴 줄 아는 사람이 못 돼요. 제겐 의지가 없어요."], ["I have been so overwhelmed by the many storms that have broken over my head, that I am become passive in the hands of the Almighty, like a sparrow in the talons of an eagle.", "결코 아무것도 결정하지 않겠다는 의지 말고는요. 제 머리 위로 몰아친 그 숱한 폭풍에 너무도 압도된 나머지, 저는 독수리 발톱에 잡힌 참새처럼 전능하신 분의 손안에서 모든 걸 맡긴 사람이 되어 버렸어요."], ["I live, because it is not ordained for me to die.", "제가 살아 있는 건, 제게 죽음이 정해지지 않았기 때문이에요."], ["If succor be sent to me, I will accept it.”", "누가 제게 도움을 보내 준다면, 저는 받아들일게요.\""]], [["“Ah, madame,”", "\"아, 부인,\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “you should not talk thus!", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"그렇게 말씀하시면 안 됩니다!"], ["It is not so we should evince our resignation to the will of heaven; on the contrary, we are all free agents.”", "하늘의 뜻에 순응하는 마음은 그렇게 드러내는 게 아닙니다. 오히려 우리는 모두 자유로이 행동하는 존재인걸요.\""]], [["“Alas!”", "\"아아!\""], ["exclaimed Mercédès, “if it were so, if I possessed free-will, but without the power to render that will efficacious, it would drive me to despair.”", "메르세데스가 외쳤다. \"만약 그렇다면, 만약 제게 자유로운 의지가 있으면서도 그 의지를 이룰 힘이 없다면, 그건 저를 절망으로 몰아넣을 거예요.\""]], [["Monte Cristo dropped his head and shrank from the vehemence of her grief.", "몬테크리스토가 고개를 떨구며, 그녀의 격렬한 슬픔 앞에서 움찔 물러섰다."]], [["“Will you not even say you will see me again?”", "\"다시 뵙겠다는 말씀조차 해 주지 않으시렵니까?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“On the contrary, we shall meet again,”", "\"아니에요, 우리는 다시 만날 거예요.\""], ["said Mercédès, pointing to heaven with solemnity.", "메르세데스가 엄숙하게 하늘을 가리키며 말했다."], ["“I tell you so to prove to you that I still hope.”", "\"제게 아직 희망이 있다는 걸 보여 드리려고 이렇게 말하는 거예요.\""]], [["And after pressing her own trembling hand upon that of the count, Mercédès rushed up the stairs and disappeared.", "그러고서 떨리는 자기 손을 백작의 손 위에 꼭 포갠 다음, 메르세데스는 계단 위로 달려 올라가 사라졌다."], ["Monte Cristo slowly left the house and turned towards the quay.", "몬테크리스토는 천천히 집을 나서 부두 쪽으로 향했다."], ["But Mercédès did not witness his departure, although she was seated at the little window of the room which had been occupied by old Dantès.", "그러나 메르세데스는 그가 떠나는 모습을 보지 못했다. 당테스 노인이 쓰던 방의 작은 창가에 앉아 있었으면서도 말이다."], ["Her eyes were straining to see the ship which was carrying her son over the vast sea; but still her voice involuntarily murmured softly:", "그녀의 눈은 아들을 싣고 망망대해를 건너가는 배를 좇느라 애를 쓰고 있었다. 그러면서도 그녀의 입에서는 저도 모르게 가만히 중얼거림이 새어 나오고 있었다."]], [["“Edmond, Edmond, Edmond!”", "\"에드몽, 에드몽, 에드몽!\""]], [["# Chapter 113. The Past", "# Chapter 113. The Past"]], [["The count departed with a sad heart from the house in which he had left Mercédès, probably never to behold her again.", "백작은 메르세데스를 남겨 두고 온 그 집을 무거운 마음으로 떠났다. 아마도 다시는 그녀를 보지 못하리라."], ["Since the death of little Edward a great change had taken place in Monte Cristo.", "어린 에두아르가 죽은 뒤로 몬테크리스토에게는 커다란 변화가 일어났다."], ["Having reached the summit of his vengeance by a long and tortuous path, he saw an abyss of doubt yawning before him.", "길고 구불구불한 길을 지나 마침내 복수의 정점에 다다른 그는, 눈앞에 의혹의 심연이 입을 벌리고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["More than this, the conversation which had just taken place between Mercédès and himself had awakened so many recollections in his heart that he felt it necessary to combat with them.", "그뿐만이 아니었다. 방금 메르세데스와 나눈 대화가 그의 가슴속에 너무도 많은 기억을 일깨워 놓았기에, 그는 그것들과 맞서 싸워야만 한다고 느꼈다."], ["A man of the count’s temperament could not long indulge in that melancholy which can exist in common minds, but which destroys superior ones.", "백작 같은 기질의 사내는 평범한 사람들 속에서는 존재할 수 있으나 뛰어난 사람들은 파괴하고 마는 그런 우울에 오래 잠겨 있을 수 없었다."], ["He thought he must have made an error in his calculations if he now found cause to blame himself.", "만일 지금 자신을 탓할 까닭을 찾았다면, 그것은 자신의 계산에 어떤 오류가 있었던 게 틀림없다고 그는 생각했다."]], [["“I cannot have deceived myself,”", "\"나는 나 자신을 속였을 리 없다.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said; “I must look upon the past in a false light. What!”", "\"내가 과거를 그릇된 빛 아래에서 바라보고 있는 게 틀림없어. 뭐라고!\" 그가 말을 이었다."], ["he continued, “can I have been following a false path?—can the end which I proposed be a mistaken end?—can one hour have sufficed to prove to an architect that the work upon which he founded all his hopes was an impossible, if not a sacrilegious, undertaking?", "\"내가 잘못된 길을 따라온 것일까? 내가 세운 목표가 그릇된 목표였단 말인가? 단 한 시간이면, 한 건축가가 모든 희망을 걸었던 그 작업이 불가능한, 아니 신성모독에 가까운 일이었음을 증명하기에 충분하단 말인가?"], ["I cannot reconcile myself to this idea—it would madden me.", "나는 이 생각과 화해할 수 없다. 그것은 나를 미치게 만들 것이다."], ["The reason why I am now dissatisfied is that I have not a clear appreciation of the past.", "내가 지금 만족하지 못하는 까닭은 과거를 또렷이 헤아리지 못하기 때문이다."], ["The past, like the country through which we walk, becomes indistinct as we advance.", "과거란, 우리가 지나온 들판처럼, 앞으로 나아갈수록 흐릿해지는 법이지."], ["My position is like that of a person wounded in a dream; he feels the wound, though he cannot recollect when he received it.", "내 처지는 꿈속에서 상처 입은 사람과 같다. 그는 상처를 느끼지만, 언제 그 상처를 입었는지는 기억하지 못한다."]], [["“Come, then, thou regenerate man, thou extravagant prodigal, thou awakened sleeper, thou all-powerful visionary, thou invincible millionaire,—once again review thy past life of starvation and wretchedness, revisit the scenes where fate and misfortune conducted, and where despair received thee.", "\"자, 그렇다면, 거듭난 자여, 분에 넘치게 탕진하는 자여, 잠에서 깨어난 자여, 전능한 몽상가여, 무적의 백만장자여, 굶주림과 비참으로 점철된 네 지난 삶을 다시 한번 돌아보아라. 운명과 불행이 너를 끌고 갔던, 절망이 너를 맞이했던 그 자리들을 다시 찾아가라."], ["Too many diamonds, too much gold and splendor, are now reflected by the mirror in which Monte Cristo seeks to behold Dantès.", "몬테크리스토가 그 안에서 당테스를 보려 하는 거울에는 지금 너무 많은 다이아몬드와 너무 많은 황금과 광채가 비치고 있다."], ["Hide thy diamonds, bury thy gold, shroud thy splendor, exchange riches for poverty, liberty for a prison, a living body for a corpse!”", "네 다이아몬드를 감추고, 네 황금을 묻고, 네 광채를 가려라. 부를 가난과, 자유를 감옥과, 살아 있는 몸을 시체와 맞바꾸어라!\""]], [["As he thus reasoned, Monte Cristo walked down the Rue de la Caisserie.", "이렇게 곱씹으며 몬테크리스토는 케스리 거리를 걸어 내려갔다."], ["It was the same through which, twenty-four years ago, he had been conducted by a silent and nocturnal guard; the houses, today so smiling and animated, were on that night dark, mute, and closed.", "스물네 해 전, 말없는 야간 경비대에 끌려 지나갔던 바로 그 거리였다. 오늘은 이토록 환하고 활기 넘치는 집들이, 그날 밤에는 어둡고 침묵에 잠긴 채 굳게 닫혀 있었다."]], [["“And yet they were the same,”", "\"그래도 똑같은 집들이로군.\""], ["murmured Monte Cristo, “only now it is broad daylight instead of night; it is the sun which brightens the place, and makes it appear so cheerful.”", "몬테크리스토가 중얼거렸다. \"다만 지금은 밤이 아니라 환한 대낮일 뿐이지. 이 자리를 밝히고 이토록 명랑하게 만드는 것은 태양이다.\""]], [["He proceeded towards the quay by the Rue Saint-Laurent, and advanced to the Consigne; it was the point where he had embarked.", "그는 생로랑 거리를 지나 부두 쪽으로 나아가, 콩시뉴까지 걸어갔다. 그가 배에 올랐던 바로 그 지점이었다."], ["A pleasure-boat with striped awning was going by.", "줄무늬 차양을 친 유람선 한 척이 지나가고 있었다."], ["Monte Cristo called the owner, who immediately rowed up to him with the eagerness of a boatman hoping for a good fare.", "몬테크리스토가 주인을 부르자, 그는 좋은 삯을 바라는 뱃사공 특유의 열의로 곧장 노를 저어 다가왔다."]], [["The weather was magnificent, and the excursion a treat.", "날씨는 더없이 화창했고, 뱃놀이는 즐거운 일이었다."], ["The sun, red and flaming, was sinking into the embrace of the welcoming ocean.", "붉게 타오르는 태양이 반갑게 맞아 주는 바다의 품으로 가라앉고 있었다."], ["The sea, smooth as crystal, was now and then disturbed by the leaping of fish, which were pursued by some unseen enemy and sought for safety in another element; while on the extreme verge of the horizon might be seen the fishermen’s boats, white and graceful as the sea-gull, or the merchant vessels bound for Corsica or Spain.", "수정처럼 매끄러운 바다는 이따금 물고기가 뛰어오를 때마다 일렁였다. 보이지 않는 적에게 쫓긴 물고기들이 다른 세계에서 안전을 구하느라 솟구치는 것이었다. 한편 수평선 끝에서는 갈매기처럼 희고 우아한 어부들의 배며, 코르시카나 에스파냐로 향하는 상선들이 보였다."]], [["But notwithstanding the serene sky, the gracefully formed boats, and the golden light in which the whole scene was bathed, the Count of Monte Cristo, wrapped in his cloak, could think only of this terrible voyage, the details of which were one by one recalled to his memory.", "그러나 맑은 하늘과 우아한 자태의 배들과 온 풍경을 적시는 황금빛 햇살에도 불구하고, 망토를 두른 몬테크리스토 백작은 오직 그 끔찍한 항해만을 떠올리고 있었다. 그 세세한 장면들이 하나하나 그의 기억 속으로 되살아났다."], ["The solitary light burning at the Catalans; that first sight of the Château d’If, which told him whither they were leading him; the struggle with the gendarmes when he wished to throw himself overboard; his despair when he found himself vanquished, and the sensation when the muzzle of the carbine touched his forehead—all these were brought before him in vivid and frightful reality.", "카탈루냐 마을에 외로이 타오르던 불빛, 자신을 어디로 끌고 가는지를 일러 주던 이프성의 첫 모습, 바다로 뛰어들려 했을 때 헌병들과 벌인 몸싸움, 끝내 제압당했음을 깨달았을 때의 절망, 그리고 기병총의 총구가 이마에 닿던 그 감각. 이 모든 것이 생생하고 무시무시한 현실로 그의 앞에 되살아났다."]], [["Like the streams which the heat of the summer has dried up, and which after the autumnal storms gradually begin oozing drop by drop, so did the count feel his heart gradually fill with the bitterness which formerly nearly overwhelmed Edmond Dantès.", "여름의 열기에 말라붙었던 시냇물이 가을 폭풍이 지나간 뒤 한 방울 한 방울 스며 나오기 시작하듯, 백작은 일찍이 에드몽 당테스를 거의 짓눌렀던 그 쓰라림이 가슴속에 서서히 차오르는 것을 느꼈다."], ["Clear sky, swift-flitting boats, and brilliant sunshine disappeared; the heavens were hung with black, and the gigantic structure of the Château d’If seemed like the phantom of a mortal enemy.", "맑은 하늘도, 쏜살같이 미끄러지는 배들도, 눈부신 햇살도 사라졌다. 하늘에는 검은 휘장이 드리워졌고, 이프성의 거대한 구조물은 죽일 듯 미워하던 적의 망령처럼 보였다."], ["As they reached the shore, the count instinctively shrunk to the extreme end of the boat, and the owner was obliged to call out, in his sweetest tone of voice:", "기슭에 닿자 백작은 본능적으로 배의 끝자락으로 몸을 움츠렸고, 주인은 더없이 다정한 목소리로 외쳐야만 했다."]], [["“Sir, we are at the landing.”", "\"손님, 부두에 닿았습니다.\""]], [["Monte Cristo remembered that on that very spot, on the same rock, he had been violently dragged by the guards, who forced him to ascend the slope at the points of their bayonets.", "몬테크리스토는 떠올렸다. 바로 이 자리, 바로 이 바위 위에서, 경비병들에게 거칠게 끌려 총검 끝에 떠밀리며 비탈을 올라야 했음을."], ["The journey had seemed very long to Dantès, but Monte Cristo found it equally short.", "당테스에게는 그 길이 무척 길게 느껴졌으나, 몬테크리스토에게는 그만큼 짧게 느껴졌다."], ["Each stroke of the oar seemed to awaken a new throng of ideas, which sprang up with the flying spray of the sea. 50219m", "노가 한 번 저어질 때마다 새로운 상념의 무리가 깨어나, 흩날리는 바다 물보라와 함께 솟구치는 듯했다."]], [["There had been no prisoners confined in the Château d’If since the revolution of July; it was only inhabited by a guard, kept there for the prevention of smuggling.", "7월 혁명 이래로 이프성에는 갇힌 죄수가 한 명도 없었다. 밀수를 막기 위해 배치된 경비대만이 머물고 있을 뿐이었다."], ["A concierge waited at the door to exhibit to visitors this monument of curiosity, once a scene of terror.", "문에서는 한 관리인이 기다리고 있다가, 한때 공포의 무대였던 이 진기한 유적을 방문객들에게 보여 주었다."]], [["The count inquired whether any of the ancient jailers were still there; but they had all been pensioned, or had passed on to some other employment.", "백작은 옛 간수들이 아직 남아 있는지 물었다. 그러나 그들은 모두 연금을 받고 물러났거나 다른 일자리로 옮겨 갔다."], ["The concierge who attended him had only been there since 1830.", "그를 안내하는 관리인은 1830년부터 이곳에 있었을 뿐이었다."], ["He visited his own dungeon. He again beheld the dull light vainly endeavoring to penetrate the narrow opening.", "그는 자신의 지하 감방을 찾아갔다. 좁은 틈으로 헛되이 비집고 들어오려 애쓰는 흐릿한 빛을 다시 보았다."], ["His eyes rested upon the spot where had stood his bed, since then removed, and behind the bed the new stones indicated where the breach made by the Abbé Faria had been.", "그의 눈길은 침대가 놓여 있던 자리에 머물렀다. 침대는 그 후로 치워졌고, 그 뒤편으로 새로 끼워진 돌들이 파리아 신부가 뚫었던 통로의 흔적을 드러내고 있었다."], ["Monte Cristo felt his limbs tremble; he seated himself upon a log of wood.", "몬테크리스토는 사지가 떨려 오는 것을 느꼈다. 그는 통나무 토막 위에 주저앉았다."]], [["“Are there any stories connected with this prison besides the one relating to the poisoning of Mirabeau?”", "\"미라보의 독살 이야기 말고도, 이 감옥에 얽힌 다른 이야기들이 있소?\" 백작이 물었다."], ["asked the count; “are there any traditions respecting these dismal abodes,—in which it is difficult to believe men can ever have imprisoned their fellow-creatures?”", "\"사람이 같은 사람을 가두어 둘 수 있었다고는 믿기 어려운 이 음산한 거처에 관해 전해 내려오는 이야기가 있느냐는 말이오.\""]], [["“Yes, sir; indeed, the jailer Antoine told me one connected with this very dungeon.”", "\"네, 손님. 사실 간수 앙투안이 바로 이 지하 감방에 얽힌 이야기를 하나 들려준 적이 있습니다.\""]], [["Monte Cristo shuddered; Antoine had been his jailer.", "몬테크리스토는 몸서리쳤다. 앙투안은 그의 간수였다."], ["He had almost forgotten his name and face, but at the mention of the name he recalled his person as he used to see it, the face encircled by a beard, wearing the brown jacket, the bunch of keys, the jingling of which he still seemed to hear.", "그 이름도 얼굴도 거의 잊고 있었으나, 이름이 입에 오르자 그는 늘 보던 그 모습을 떠올렸다. 수염에 둘러싸인 얼굴, 갈색 웃옷, 열쇠 꾸러미, 그 짤랑거리는 소리가 아직도 귓가에 들리는 듯했다."], ["The count turned around, and fancied he saw him in the corridor, rendered still darker by the torch carried by the concierge.", "백작은 돌아보았고, 관리인이 든 횃불 탓에 한층 더 어두워진 복도에서 그를 본 것만 같았다."]], [["“Would you like to hear the story, sir?”", "\"이야기를 들어 보시겠습니까, 손님?\""]], [["“Yes; relate it,”", "\"그래, 들려주게.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, pressing his hand to his heart to still its violent beatings; he felt afraid of hearing his own history.", "몬테크리스토가 손을 가슴에 대어 거칠게 뛰는 심장을 진정시키며 말했다. 그는 제 자신의 이야기를 듣게 될까 봐 두려웠다."]], [["“This dungeon,”", "\"이 지하 감방은요,\""], ["said the concierge, “was, it appears, some time ago occupied by a very dangerous prisoner, the more so since he was full of industry.", "관리인이 말했다. \"얼마 전까지 아주 위험한 죄수가 갇혀 있던 곳이라고 합니다. 더구나 그자는 손재주가 비상해서 더욱 위험했지요."], ["Another person was confined in the Château at the same time, but he was not wicked, he was only a poor mad priest.”", "같은 시기에 또 다른 사람도 이 성에 갇혀 있었는데, 그 사람은 악한 자가 아니라 그저 미친 가엾은 신부였습니다.\""]], [["“Ah, indeed?—mad!”", "\"아, 그래? 미쳤다고?\""], ["repeated Monte Cristo; “and what was his mania?”", "몬테크리스토가 되뇌었다. \"그래, 무슨 망상에 사로잡혀 있었나?\""]], [["“He offered millions to anyone who would set him at liberty.”", "\"자기를 풀어 주는 사람에게 수백만 프랑을 주겠다고 했답니다.\""]], [["Monte Cristo raised his eyes, but he could not see the heavens; there was a stone veil between him and the firmament.", "몬테크리스토는 눈을 들었으나 하늘이 보이지 않았다. 그와 창공 사이에는 돌로 된 휘장이 가로놓여 있었다."], ["He thought that there had been no less thick a veil before the eyes of those to whom Faria offered the treasures.", "그는 생각했다. 파리아가 보물을 내밀었던 자들의 눈앞에도 이에 못지않게 두꺼운 휘장이 드리워져 있었으리라고."]], [["“Could the prisoners see each other?” he asked.", "\"죄수들이 서로 만날 수 있었나?\" 그가 물었다."]], [["“Oh, no, sir, it was expressly forbidden; but they eluded the vigilance of the guards, and made a passage from one dungeon to the other.”", "\"오, 아닙니다, 손님. 그건 엄격히 금지되어 있었지요. 하지만 두 사람은 경비병들의 감시를 피해, 한 감방에서 다른 감방으로 통하는 길을 뚫었습니다.\""]], [["“And which of them made this passage?”", "\"그럼 둘 중 누가 그 길을 뚫었지?\""]], [["“Oh, it must have been the young man, certainly, for he was strong and industrious, while the abbé was aged and weak; besides, his mind was too vacillating to allow him to carry out an idea.”", "\"오, 그야 분명 젊은 쪽이었겠지요. 그는 힘이 세고 부지런했으니까요. 반면에 신부는 늙고 약했습니다. 게다가 그 신부는 마음이 너무 흔들려서 어떤 생각 하나도 끝까지 밀고 나가지 못했답니다.\""]], [["“Blind fools!” murmured the count.", "\"눈먼 바보들 같으니.\" 백작이 중얼거렸다."]], [["“However, be that as it may, the young man made a tunnel, how or by what means no one knows; but he made it, and there is the evidence yet remaining of his work.", "\"어쨌든, 사정이야 어떻든 간에, 젊은이가 굴을 팠습니다. 어떻게, 무슨 수단으로 했는지는 아무도 모릅니다. 하지만 그는 굴을 팠고, 그 작업의 흔적이 아직도 남아 있지요."], ["Do you see it?”", "보이십니까?\""], ["and the man held the torch to the wall. 50223m", "그러고는 횃불을 벽에 들이댔다."]], [["“Ah, yes; I see,” said the count, in a voice hoarse from emotion.", "\"아, 그래, 보이는군.\" 백작이 감정에 잠겨 쉰 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“The result was that the two men communicated with one another; how long they did so, nobody knows.", "\"그래서 두 사람은 서로 통하게 되었지요. 얼마나 오래 그랬는지는 아무도 모릅니다."], ["One day the old man fell ill and died.", "어느 날 늙은이가 병이 들어 죽었습니다."], ["Now guess what the young one did?”", "자, 젊은이가 무슨 짓을 했는지 알아맞혀 보십시오.\""]], [["“Tell me.”", "\"말해 보게.\""]], [["“He carried off the corpse, which he placed in his own bed with its face to the wall; then he entered the empty dungeon, closed the entrance, and slipped into the sack which had contained the dead body.", "\"그는 시체를 옮겨다가 자기 침대에 얼굴이 벽을 향하도록 눕혔습니다. 그러고는 빈 감방으로 들어가 입구를 막고, 시체가 담겨 있던 자루 속으로 들어갔지요."], ["Did you ever hear of such an idea?”", "이런 생각을 들어 보신 적 있습니까?\""]], [["Monte Cristo closed his eyes, and seemed again to experience all the sensations he had felt when the coarse canvas, yet moist with the cold dews of death, had touched his face.", "몬테크리스토는 눈을 감았다. 죽음의 차가운 이슬에 아직 축축한 거친 천이 얼굴에 닿았을 때 느꼈던 그 모든 감각을 다시 겪는 듯했다."]], [["The jailer continued:", "간수가 말을 이었다."]], [["“Now this was his project.", "\"자, 이게 그자의 계획이었습니다."], ["He fancied that they buried the dead at the Château d’If, and imagining they would not expend much labor on the grave of a prisoner, he calculated on raising the earth with his shoulders, but unfortunately their arrangements at the Château frustrated his projects.", "그는 이프성에서 죽은 자들을 땅에 묻는 줄 알았고, 죄수의 무덤에야 별로 공을 들이지 않으리라 짐작하고는, 어깨로 흙을 밀어 올릴 작정이었지요. 그런데 불행히도 이 성의 방식이 그자의 계획을 어그러뜨렸습니다. 이곳에서는 죽은 자를 결코 묻지 않았거든요."], ["They never buried the dead; they merely attached a heavy cannon-ball to the feet, and then threw them into the sea.", "그저 발에 무거운 포탄을 매달아 바다에 던질 뿐이었지요. 그래서 그렇게 했습니다."], ["This is what was done.", "젊은이는 바위 꼭대기에서 던져졌습니다."], ["The young man was thrown from the top of the rock; the corpse was found on the bed next day, and the whole truth was guessed, for the men who performed the office then mentioned what they had not dared to speak of before, that at the moment the corpse was thrown into the deep, they heard a shriek, which was almost immediately stifled by the water in which it disappeared.”", "이튿날 침대에서 진짜 시체가 발견되었고, 모든 진상이 짐작되었지요. 그 일을 맡았던 자들이 그제야, 전에는 감히 입에 올리지 못했던 사실을 털어놓았으니까요. 시체를 깊은 바다에 던지는 순간, 비명이 들렸는데, 그것이 사라지는 물속에서 거의 곧바로 잦아들었다는 겁니다.\""]], [["The count breathed with difficulty; the cold drops ran down his forehead, and his heart was full of anguish.", "백작은 숨을 제대로 쉬지 못했다. 차가운 땀방울이 이마를 타고 흘러내렸고, 가슴은 고뇌로 가득 찼다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니다.\""], ["he muttered, “the doubt I felt was but the commencement of forgetfulness; but here the wound reopens, and the heart again thirsts for vengeance.", "그가 중얼거렸다. \"내가 느낀 의혹은 망각의 시작일 뿐이었어. 하지만 여기서 상처가 다시 벌어지고, 가슴은 또다시 복수에 목마르는구나."], ["And the prisoner,”", "그래, 그 죄수는,\""], ["he continued aloud, “was he ever heard of afterwards?”", "그가 소리 내어 말을 이었다. \"그 후로 소식이 들렸나?\""]], [["“Oh, no; of course not.", "\"오, 아니요. 물론 들리지 않았지요."], ["You can understand that one of two things must have happened; he must either have fallen flat, in which case the blow, from a height of ninety feet, must have killed him instantly, or he must have fallen upright, and then the weight would have dragged him to the bottom, where he remained—poor fellow!”", "두 가지 중 하나가 일어났으리라는 건 짐작이 가실 겁니다. 납작하게 떨어졌다면 구십 척 높이에서 받은 충격으로 그 자리에서 즉사했을 테고, 아니면 똑바로 떨어졌다면 무게에 끌려 바닥까지 가라앉아 거기 머물렀겠지요. 가엾은 사람!\""]], [["“Then you pity him?”", "\"그래, 그자가 가엾은가?\""], ["said the count.", "백작이 물었다."]], [["“_Ma foi_, yes; though he was in his own element.”", "\"맙소사, 그렇지요. 자기가 살던 곳이었다고는 해도요.\""]], [["“What do you mean?”", "\"그게 무슨 말인가?\""]], [["“The report was that he had been a naval officer, who had been confined for plotting with the Bonapartists.”", "\"소문으로는 그자가 해군 장교였는데, 보나파르트파와 음모를 꾸민 죄로 갇혔다고 했습니다.\""]], [["“Great is truth,” muttered the count, “fire cannot burn, nor water drown it!", "\"위대하도다, 진실이여.\" 백작이 중얼거렸다."], ["Thus the poor sailor lives in the recollection of those who narrate his history; his terrible story is recited in the chimney-corner, and a shudder is felt at the description of his transit through the air to be swallowed by the deep.”", "\"불도 진실을 태우지 못하고, 물도 진실을 익사시키지 못하는구나! 이렇듯 그 가엾은 선원은 제 이야기를 들려주는 자들의 기억 속에 살아 있다. 그의 끔찍한 사연은 난롯가에서 읊어지고, 그가 공중을 가르며 떨어져 바다에 삼켜졌다는 대목에서는 누구나 몸서리를 친다.\""], ["Then, the count added aloud, “Was his name ever known?”", "그러고는 백작이 소리 내어 덧붙였다. \"그자의 이름은 알려졌나?\""]], [["“Oh, yes; but only as No. 34.”", "\"오, 네. 하지만 34호라는 번호로만요.\""]], [["“Oh, Villefort, Villefort,”", "\"오, 빌포르, 빌포르.\""], ["murmured the count, “this scene must often have haunted thy sleepless hours!”", "백작이 중얼거렸다. \"이 장면이 잠 못 이루는 네 밤들을 얼마나 자주 따라다녔겠는가!\""]], [["“Do you wish to see anything more, sir?”", "\"더 보실 것이 있습니까, 손님?\""], ["said the concierge.", "관리인이 말했다."]], [["“Yes, especially if you will show me the poor abbé’s room.”", "\"그래. 특히 그 가엾은 신부의 방을 보여 준다면 말일세.\""]], [["“Ah! No. 27.”", "\"아! 27호 말씀이군요.\""]], [["“Yes; No. 27.”", "\"그래, 27호.\""], ["repeated the count, who seemed to hear the voice of the abbé answering him in those very words through the wall when asked his name.", "이름을 물었을 때 신부가 바로 그 말로 벽 너머에서 대답하던 목소리가 들리는 듯하여, 백작이 되뇌었다. \"이리 오시지요, 손님.\""]], [["“Come, sir.”", "\"잠깐.\""], ["“Wait,” said Monte Cristo, “I wish to take one final glance around this room.”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"이 방을 마지막으로 한 번 둘러보고 싶네.\""]], [["“This is fortunate,”", "\"마침 잘됐습니다.\""], ["said the guide; “I have forgotten the other key.”", "안내인이 말했다. \"다른 열쇠를 깜빡하고 안 가져왔거든요.\""]], [["“Go and fetch it.”", "\"가서 가져오게.\""]], [["“I will leave you the torch, sir.”", "\"횃불은 두고 가겠습니다, 손님.\""]], [["“No, take it away; I can see in the dark.”", "\"아니, 가져가게. 나는 어둠 속에서도 볼 수 있으니까.\""]], [["“Why, you are like No. 34. They said he was so accustomed to darkness that he could see a pin in the darkest corner of his dungeon.”", "\"아니, 손님은 꼭 34호 같으시군요. 그자는 어둠에 어찌나 익숙했던지 감방의 가장 캄캄한 구석에 떨어진 바늘도 볼 수 있었다고들 하던데요.\""]], [["“He spent fourteen years to arrive at that,”", "\"그렇게 되기까지 십사 년을 보냈지.\""], ["muttered the count.", "백작이 중얼거렸다."]], [["The guide carried away the torch.", "안내인이 횃불을 들고 나갔다."], ["The count had spoken correctly.", "백작의 말은 옳았다."], ["Scarcely had a few seconds elapsed, ere he saw everything as distinctly as by daylight.", "몇 초도 채 지나지 않아 그는 대낮처럼 모든 것을 또렷이 볼 수 있었다."], ["Then he looked around him, and really recognized his dungeon.", "그러자 그는 주위를 둘러보았고, 자신의 감방을 정말로 알아보았다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["he said, “there is the stone upon which I used to sit; there is the impression made by my shoulders on the wall; there is the mark of my blood made when one day I dashed my head against the wall.", "그가 말했다. \"저기 내가 앉곤 하던 돌이 있고, 저기 벽에 내 어깨가 남긴 자국이 있고, 어느 날 벽에 머리를 부딪쳤을 때 묻은 핏자국이 있구나."], ["Oh, those figures, how well I remember them!", "오, 저 숫자들, 어찌 이리 또렷이 기억나는지!"], ["I made them one day to calculate the age of my father, that I might know whether I should find him still living, and that of Mercédès, to know if I should find her still free.", "어느 날 아버지가 살아 계실지를 알아보려고 나이를 헤아리고, 메르세데스가 아직 자유로운 몸일지를 알아보려고 그녀의 나이를 헤아리느라 적어 둔 것이지."], ["After finishing that calculation, I had a minute’s hope.", "그 계산을 끝마치고 나서 나는 잠깐 희망을 품었어."], ["I did not reckon upon hunger and infidelity!”", "굶주림과 변심은 미처 셈에 넣지 못했지!\""], ["and a bitter laugh escaped the count.", "그러고는 쓰디쓴 웃음이 백작의 입에서 새어 나왔다."]], [["He saw in fancy the burial of his father, and the marriage of Mercédès.", "그는 환영처럼 아버지의 장례와 메르세데스의 결혼을 보았다."], ["On the other side of the dungeon he perceived an inscription, the white letters of which were still visible on the green wall:", "감방 맞은편 벽에는 새겨진 글귀가 보였는데, 그 흰 글자가 푸르스름한 벽 위에 아직 또렷이 남아 있었다."]], [["“‘_Oh, God!_’”", "\"'오, 하느님!'\""], ["he read, “‘_preserve my memory!_’”", "그가 읽었다. \"'제 기억을 지켜 주소서!'\""]], [["“Oh, yes,”", "\"오, 그래.\" 그가 외쳤다."], ["he cried, “that was my only prayer at last; I no longer begged for liberty, but memory; I dreaded to become mad and forgetful.", "\"그것이 결국 내 유일한 기도였지. 나는 더 이상 자유를 빌지 않았어. 오직 기억을 빌었지."], ["Oh, God, thou hast preserved my memory; I thank thee, I thank thee!”", "미쳐서 모든 것을 잊게 될까 두려웠으니까. 오, 하느님, 당신은 제 기억을 지켜 주셨습니다. 감사합니다, 감사합니다!\""]], [["At this moment the light of the torch was reflected on the wall; the guide was coming; Monte Cristo went to meet him.", "그 순간 횃불 빛이 벽에 비쳤다. 안내인이 오고 있었다. 몬테크리스토는 그를 맞으러 나갔다."]], [["“Follow me, sir;” and without ascending the stairs the guide conducted him by a subterraneous passage to another entrance.", "\"따라오시지요, 손님.\" 안내인은 계단을 오르지 않고 지하 통로를 지나 다른 입구로 그를 데려갔다."], ["There, again, Monte Cristo was assailed by a multitude of thoughts.", "거기서 또다시 몬테크리스토는 수많은 상념에 휩싸였다."], ["The first thing that met his eye was the meridian, drawn by the abbé on the wall, by which he calculated the time; then he saw the remains of the bed on which the poor prisoner had died.", "가장 먼저 눈에 들어온 것은 신부가 시간을 헤아리려고 벽에 그어 둔 자오선이었다. 이어 그 가엾은 죄수가 죽음을 맞은 침대의 잔해가 보였다."], ["The sight of this, instead of exciting the anguish experienced by the count in the dungeon, filled his heart with a soft and grateful sentiment, and tears fell from his eyes.", "지하 감방에서 백작이 겪었던 고뇌와는 달리, 이 광경은 그의 가슴을 부드럽고 감사한 정으로 채워 주었고, 두 눈에서 눈물이 흘러내렸다."]], [["“This is where the mad abbé was kept, sir, and that is where the young man entered;”", "\"여기가 그 미친 신부가 갇혀 있던 곳입니다, 손님. 그리고 저기가 젊은이가 들어왔던 곳이지요.\""], ["and the guide pointed to the opening, which had remained unclosed.", "안내인이 아직 막히지 않은 채로 남아 있는 구멍을 가리켰다."], ["“From the appearance of the stone,”", "\"이 돌의 모양을 보고,\""], ["he continued, “a learned gentleman discovered that the prisoners might have communicated together for ten years.", "그가 말을 이었다. \"어느 학식 있는 신사분이, 두 죄수가 십 년 동안 서로 통할 수 있었으리라는 걸 알아냈습니다."], ["Poor things!", "가엾은 사람들!"], ["Those must have been ten weary years.”", "그 십 년은 분명 고단한 세월이었겠지요.\""]], [["Dantès took some louis from his pocket, and gave them to the man who had twice unconsciously pitied him.", "당테스는 주머니에서 루이 금화 몇 닢을 꺼내, 두 번이나 자신도 모르게 자기를 가엾이 여겼던 그 사내에게 건넸다."], ["The guide took them, thinking them merely a few pieces of little value; but the light of the torch revealed their true worth.", "안내인은 그것을 받으며 그저 값나가지 않는 동전 몇 닢이려니 여겼다. 그러나 횃불 빛이 그 참된 가치를 드러냈다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"손님.\""], ["he said, “you have made a mistake; you have given me gold.”", "그가 말했다. \"착각하셨습니다. 금화를 주셨군요.\""]], [["“I know it.”", "\"알고 있네.\""]], [["The concierge looked upon the count with surprise.", "관리인이 놀란 눈으로 백작을 바라보았다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"손님.\""], ["he cried, scarcely able to believe his good fortune—“sir, I cannot understand your generosity!”", "그가 제 행운을 믿기 어려운 듯 외쳤다. \"손님, 이 너그러우심을 도무지 이해할 수가 없습니다!\""]], [["“Oh, it is very simple, my good fellow; I have been a sailor, and your story touched me more than it would others.”", "\"오, 아주 간단한 일이라네, 이 친구야. 나도 한때 선원이었던지라, 자네 이야기가 다른 사람들보다 내 마음을 더 깊이 흔들었던 거지.\""]], [["“Then, sir, since you are so liberal, I ought to offer you something.” 50227m", "\"그렇다면 손님, 그토록 후하게 베풀어 주시니, 저도 무언가 드려야 마땅하겠습니다.\""]], [["“What have you to offer to me, my friend? Shells?", "\"내게 무얼 주겠다는 건가, 친구? 조개껍데기?"], ["Straw-work?", "짚 세공품?"], ["Thank you!”", "사양하겠네!\""]], [["“No, sir, neither of those; something connected with this story.”", "\"아닙니다, 손님. 그런 것이 아니라, 이 이야기와 관련된 것입니다.\""]], [["“Really?", "\"정말인가?"], ["What is it?”", "그게 뭔가?\""]], [["“Listen,”", "\"들어 보십시오.\" 안내인이 말했다."], ["said the guide; “I said to myself, ‘Something is always left in a cell inhabited by one prisoner for fifteen years,’ so I began to sound the wall.”", "\"저는 속으로 생각했지요. '한 죄수가 십오 년이나 살았던 감방에는 늘 무언가 남는 법이지.' 그래서 벽을 두드려 보기 시작했습니다.\""]], [["“Ah,” cried Monte Cristo, remembering the abbé’s two hiding-places.", "\"아.\" 몬테크리스토가 신부의 두 은닉처를 떠올리며 외쳤다."]], [["“After some search, I found that the floor gave a hollow sound near the head of the bed, and at the hearth.”", "\"한참을 뒤지다가, 침대 머리맡과 난로 근처 바닥에서 텅 빈 소리가 나는 걸 알아냈지요.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["said the count, “yes.”", "백작이 말했다. \"그래.\""]], [["“I raised the stones, and found——”", "\"돌을 들어내 보니, 거기에……\""]], [["“A rope-ladder and some tools?”", "\"밧줄 사다리와 연장 몇 가지가 있었나?\""]], [["“How do you know that?” asked the guide in astonishment.", "\"그걸 어떻게 아십니까?\" 안내인이 놀라 물었다."]], [["“I do not know—I only guess it, because that sort of thing is generally found in prisoners’ cells.”", "\"내가 아는 게 아니라, 그저 짐작했을 뿐이네. 그런 것들은 대개 죄수의 감방에서 나오는 법이니까.\""]], [["“Yes, sir, a rope-ladder and tools.”", "\"맞습니다, 손님. 밧줄 사다리와 연장이었지요.\""]], [["“And have you them yet?”", "\"그래, 그것들을 아직 가지고 있나?\""]], [["“No, sir; I sold them to visitors, who considered them great curiosities; but I have still something left.”", "\"아닙니다, 손님. 그건 진귀한 물건이라며 탐내는 방문객들에게 팔아 버렸습니다. 하지만 아직 남은 게 하나 있지요.\""]], [["“What is it?”", "\"그게 뭔가?\""], ["asked the count, impatiently.", "백작이 조바심을 내며 물었다."]], [["“A sort of book, written upon strips of cloth.”", "\"천 조각에 글을 적은, 책 비슷한 것입니다.\""]], [["“Go and fetch it, my good fellow; and if it be what I hope, you will do well.”", "\"가서 가져오게, 친구. 그것이 내가 바라는 그것이라면, 자네에게 좋은 일이 될 걸세.\""]], [["“I will run for it, sir;”", "\"얼른 다녀오겠습니다, 손님.\""], ["and the guide went out.", "그러고는 안내인이 나갔다."]], [["Then the count knelt down by the side of the bed, which death had converted into an altar.", "그러자 백작은 죽음이 제단으로 바꾸어 놓은 그 침대 곁에 무릎을 꿇었다."]], [["“Oh, second father,”", "\"오, 제 두 번째 아버지여.\" 그가 외쳤다."], ["he exclaimed, “thou who hast given me liberty, knowledge, riches; thou who, like beings of a superior order to ourselves, couldst understand the science of good and evil; if in the depths of the tomb there still remain something within us which can respond to the voice of those who are left on earth; if after death the soul ever revisit the places where we have lived and suffered,—then, noble heart, sublime soul, then I conjure thee by the paternal love thou didst bear me, by the filial obedience I vowed to thee, grant me some sign, some revelation!", "\"제게 자유와 지식과 부를 주신 분이여. 우리보다 한 차원 높은 존재들처럼, 선과 악의 이치를 깨우치셨던 분이여. 무덤 깊은 곳에도 지상에 남은 자들의 목소리에 응답할 수 있는 무언가가 우리 안에 아직 남아 있다면, 죽은 뒤에도 영혼이 우리가 살며 고통받던 곳을 다시 찾아온다면, 그렇다면, 고귀한 마음이여, 숭고한 영혼이여, 제게 베푸셨던 아버지의 사랑으로, 또 제가 당신께 맹세했던 자식의 순종으로, 부디 어떤 징표를, 어떤 계시를 내려 주소서!"], ["Remove from me the remains of doubt, which, if it change not to conviction, must become remorse!”", "제게서 의혹의 잔재를 거두어 주소서. 그것이 확신으로 바뀌지 않는다면, 회한이 되고 말 것이니!\""], ["The count bowed his head, and clasped his hands together.", "백작은 고개를 숙이고 두 손을 맞잡았다."]], [["“Here, sir,” said a voice behind him.", "\"여기 있습니다, 손님.\" 등 뒤에서 목소리가 들렸다."]], [["Monte Cristo shuddered, and arose.", "몬테크리스토는 몸서리치며 일어섰다."], ["The concierge held out the strips of cloth upon which the Abbé Faria had spread the riches of his mind.", "관리인이 파리아 신부가 제 정신의 풍요를 펼쳐 놓았던 그 천 조각들을 내밀었다."], ["The manuscript was the great work by the Abbé Faria upon the kingdoms of Italy.", "그 필사본은 이탈리아 여러 왕국에 관한 파리아 신부의 대작이었다."], ["The count seized it hastily, his eyes immediately fell upon the epigraph, and he read:", "백작은 그것을 황급히 움켜쥐었고, 그의 눈은 곧장 머리글에 가닿았다. 그가 읽었다."]], [["“Thou shalt tear out the dragons’ teeth, and shall trample the lions under foot, saith the Lord.”", "\"너는 용들의 이빨을 뽑을 것이며, 사자들을 발아래 짓밟으리라, 주께서 말씀하시느니라.\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아.\""], ["he exclaimed, “here is my answer.", "그가 외쳤다. \"여기 내 대답이 있구나."], ["Thanks, father, thanks.”", "고맙습니다, 아버지, 고맙습니다.\""], ["And feeling in his pocket, he took thence a small pocket-book, which contained ten bank-notes, each of 1,000 francs.", "그러고는 주머니를 더듬어 작은 수첩 하나를 꺼냈는데, 그 안에는 한 장에 천 프랑짜리 지폐가 열 장 들어 있었다."]], [["“Here,”", "\"자.\""], ["he said, “take this pocket-book.”", "그가 말했다. \"이 수첩을 받게.\""]], [["“Do you give it to me?”", "\"제게 주시는 겁니까?\""]], [["“Yes; but only on condition that you will not open it till I am gone;”", "\"그래. 다만 내가 떠난 뒤에 열어 본다는 조건일세.\""], ["and placing in his breast the treasure he had just found, which was more valuable to him than the richest jewel, he rushed out of the corridor, and reaching his boat, cried, “To Marseilles!”", "그러고는 가장 값진 보석보다도 더 소중한, 방금 찾아낸 그 보물을 가슴에 품은 채, 복도를 빠져나가 배에 이르러 외쳤다. \"마르세유로!\""]], [["Then, as he departed, he fixed his eyes upon the gloomy prison.", "그러고는 떠나가며, 그 음산한 감옥에 두 눈을 고정했다."]], [["“Woe,”", "\"화 있을지어다.\" 그가 외쳤다."], ["he cried, “to those who confined me in that wretched prison; and woe to those who forgot that I was there!” 50229m", "\"나를 저 비참한 감옥에 가둔 자들에게! 또한 화 있을지어다, 내가 그 안에 있다는 것을 잊어버린 자들에게!\""]], [["As he repassed the Catalans, the count turned around and burying his head in his cloak murmured the name of a woman.", "카탈루냐 마을을 다시 지나칠 때, 백작은 돌아서서 망토에 얼굴을 파묻고 한 여인의 이름을 중얼거렸다."], ["The victory was complete; twice he had overcome his doubts.", "승리는 완전했다. 두 번이나 그는 의혹을 이겨 냈다."], ["The name he pronounced, in a voice of tenderness, amounting almost to love, was that of Haydée.", "거의 사랑에 가까운, 그 다정한 목소리로 그가 부른 이름은 바로 하이데였다."]], [["On landing, the count turned towards the cemetery, where he felt sure of finding Morrel.", "뭍에 내린 백작은 묘지로 향했다. 거기서 모렐을 만나리라고 확신했다."], ["He, too, ten years ago, had piously sought out a tomb, and sought it vainly.", "그 역시 십 년 전, 경건한 마음으로 무덤 하나를 찾았으나 헛수고로 끝났었다."], ["He, who returned to France with millions, had been unable to find the grave of his father, who had perished from hunger.", "수백만 프랑을 지니고 프랑스로 돌아온 그였지만, 굶주림으로 세상을 떠난 아버지의 무덤은 찾지 못했다."], ["Morrel had indeed placed a cross over the spot, but it had fallen down and the grave-digger had burnt it, as he did all the old wood in the churchyard.", "모렐이 그 자리에 십자가를 세워 두긴 했으나, 그것은 쓰러졌고, 무덤 파는 일꾼이 묘지의 낡은 나무들을 다 그러하듯 그것마저 태워 버렸던 것이다."]], [["The worthy merchant had been more fortunate.", "그 훌륭한 상인은 더 운이 좋았다."], ["Dying in the arms of his children, he had been by them laid by the side of his wife, who had preceded him in eternity by two years.", "자식들의 품에서 눈을 감은 그는, 그들 손에 의해 이태 앞서 영원의 세계로 떠난 아내 곁에 나란히 묻혔다."], ["Two large slabs of marble, on which were inscribed their names, were placed on either side of a little enclosure, railed in, and shaded by four cypress-trees.", "두 사람의 이름이 새겨진 커다란 대리석 판 두 개가, 난간이 둘리고 사이프러스 네 그루가 그늘을 드리운 작은 울타리 양편에 놓여 있었다."], ["Morrel was leaning against one of these, mechanically fixing his eyes on the graves.", "모렐은 그 나무 한 그루에 몸을 기댄 채, 넋 나간 듯 무덤들에 두 눈을 고정하고 있었다."], ["His grief was so profound that he was nearly unconscious. 50231m", "그의 슬픔이 어찌나 깊은지, 거의 의식을 잃은 듯했다."]], [["“Maximilian,”", "\"막시밀리앙.\""], ["said the count, “you should not look on the graves, but there;”", "백작이 말했다. \"무덤을 바라보아서는 안 되네. 저기를 보게.\""], ["and he pointed upwards.", "그러고는 위쪽을 가리켰다."]], [["“The dead are everywhere,”", "\"죽은 자들은 어디에나 있지요.\""], ["said Morrel; “did you not yourself tell me so as we left Paris?”", "모렐이 말했다. \"파리를 떠날 때 백작님께서 직접 제게 그리 말씀하지 않으셨습니까?\""]], [["“Maximilian,”", "\"막시밀리앙.\""], ["said the count, “you asked me during the journey to allow you to remain some days at Marseilles.", "백작이 말했다. \"오는 길에 자네는 며칠 마르세유에 머물게 해 달라고 내게 청했지."], ["Do you still wish to do so?”", "아직도 그러길 바라나?\""]], [["“I have no wishes, count; only I fancy I could pass the time less painfully here than anywhere else.”", "\"제겐 바라는 것이 없습니다, 백작님. 다만 이곳이 다른 어디보다 덜 괴롭게 시간을 보낼 수 있을 것 같다는 생각이 들 뿐입니다.\""]], [["“So much the better, for I must leave you; but I carry your word with me, do I not?”", "\"그렇다면 더욱 잘됐군. 나는 자네를 떠나야 하니까. 하지만 자네의 약속을 가지고 가는 거겠지, 그렇지 않나?\""]], [["“Ah, count, I shall forget it.”", "\"아, 백작님, 저는 그 약속을 잊고 말 겁니다.\""]], [["“No, you will not forget it, because you are a man of honor, Morrel, because you have taken an oath, and are about to do so again.”", "\"아니, 자네는 잊지 않을 걸세. 자네는 명예를 아는 사람이니까, 모렐. 자네는 맹세를 했고, 또 한 번 하려 하고 있으니 말일세.\""]], [["“Oh, count, have pity upon me.", "\"오, 백작님, 부디 저를 가엾이 여겨 주십시오."], ["I am so unhappy.”", "저는 너무도 불행합니다.\""]], [["“I have known a man much more unfortunate than you, Morrel.”", "\"나는 자네보다 훨씬 더 불행했던 사람을 알고 있네, 모렐.\""]], [["“Impossible!”", "\"그럴 리가요!\""]], [["“Alas,”", "\"아아.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, “it is the infirmity of our nature always to believe ourselves much more unhappy than those who groan by our sides!”", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. \"곁에서 신음하는 자들보다 자기가 훨씬 더 불행하다고 늘 믿는 것, 그것이 우리 인간의 약함일세!\""]], [["“What can be more wretched than the man who has lost all he loved and desired in the world?”", "\"세상에서 사랑하고 바라던 모든 것을 잃은 사람보다 더 비참한 사람이 어디 있겠습니까?\""]], [["“Listen, Morrel, and pay attention to what I am about to tell you.", "\"들어 보게, 모렐. 내가 자네에게 들려줄 이야기에 귀를 기울이게."], ["I knew a man who like you had fixed all his hopes of happiness upon a woman.", "나는 자네처럼 한 여인에게 모든 행복의 희망을 걸었던 사람을 알고 있네."], ["He was young, he had an old father whom he loved, a betrothed bride whom he adored.", "그는 젊었고, 사랑하는 늙은 아버지가 있었으며, 흠모하는 약혼녀가 있었지."], ["He was about to marry her, when one of the caprices of fate,—which would almost make us doubt the goodness of Providence, if that Providence did not afterwards reveal itself by proving that all is but a means of conducting to an end,—one of those caprices deprived him of his mistress, of the future of which he had dreamed (for in his blindness he forgot he could only read the present), and cast him into a dungeon.”", "그는 막 그녀와 결혼하려던 참이었네. 그런데 운명의 변덕 하나가, 그 변덕이란 만일 섭리가 나중에 모든 것이 한 목적으로 이끄는 수단일 뿐임을 증명해 보이지 않았더라면 우리로 하여금 거의 섭리의 선하심을 의심케 했을 그런 변덕이, 그 변덕 하나가 그에게서 연인을, 그가 꿈꾸어 온 미래를 (눈이 먼 그는 자기가 오직 현재만을 읽을 수 있다는 것을 잊고 있었으니까) 앗아 가고, 그를 지하 감방에 던져 넣었다네.\""]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아.\""], ["said Morrel, “one quits a dungeon in a week, a month, or a year.”", "모렐이 말했다. \"지하 감방이야 일주일이나 한 달, 길어야 일 년이면 벗어나는 것 아닙니까.\""]], [["“He remained there fourteen years, Morrel,”", "\"그는 거기서 십사 년을 머물렀네, 모렐.\""], ["said the count, placing his hand on the young man’s shoulder.", "백작이 젊은이의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 말했다."], ["Maximilian shuddered.", "막시밀리앙은 몸서리쳤다."]], [["“Fourteen years!”", "\"십사 년이라니!\""], ["he muttered.", "그가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Fourteen years!”", "\"십사 년일세!\""], ["repeated the count.", "백작이 되뇌었다."], ["“During that time he had many moments of despair.", "\"그동안 그는 숱한 절망의 순간을 겪었지."], ["He also, Morrel, like you, considered himself the unhappiest of men.”", "그 역시, 모렐, 자네처럼 스스로를 세상에서 가장 불행한 사람이라 여겼다네.\""]], [["“Well?” asked Morrel.", "\"그래서요?\" 모렐이 물었다."]], [["“Well, at the height of his despair God assisted him through human means.", "\"그래서, 절망의 절정에서 하느님이 사람의 손을 빌려 그를 도우셨지."], ["At first, perhaps, he did not recognize the infinite mercy of the Lord, but at last he took patience and waited.", "처음에는 어쩌면 그가 주님의 무한한 자비를 알아보지 못했을지도 모르네. 하지만 끝내 그는 인내심을 가지고 기다렸지."], ["One day he miraculously left the prison, transformed, rich, powerful.", "어느 날 그는 기적처럼 감옥을 벗어났다네. 완전히 달라진 모습으로, 부유하고 강력하게 말이지."], ["His first cry was for his father; but that father was dead.”", "그가 가장 먼저 부르짖은 것은 아버지였네. 그러나 그 아버지는 이미 세상을 떠난 뒤였지.\""]], [["“My father, too, is dead,”", "\"제 아버지도 돌아가셨습니다.\""], ["said Morrel.", "모렐이 말했다."]], [["“Yes; but your father died in your arms, happy, respected, rich, and full of years; his father died poor, despairing, almost doubtful of Providence; and when his son sought his grave ten years afterwards, his tomb had disappeared, and no one could say, ‘There sleeps the father you so well loved.’”", "\"그래. 하지만 자네 아버지는 자네의 품에서, 행복하게, 존경받으며, 부유하게, 천수를 누리고 돌아가셨네. 그의 아버지는 가난하게, 절망 속에서, 섭리마저 거의 의심하며 돌아가셨지. 그리고 십 년 뒤 아들이 그 무덤을 찾았을 때, 무덤은 사라지고 없었고, 아무도 '자네가 그토록 사랑한 아버지가 여기 잠들어 있네'라고 말해 줄 수 없었네.\""]], [["“Oh!” exclaimed Morrel.", "\"오!\" 모렐이 외쳤다."]], [["“He was, then, a more unhappy son than you, Morrel, for he could not even find his father’s grave.”", "\"그러니 그는 자네보다 더 불행한 아들이었지, 모렐. 아버지의 무덤조차 찾지 못했으니 말일세.\""]], [["“But then he had the woman he loved still remaining?”", "\"하지만 그래도 그에게는 사랑하던 여인이 아직 남아 있지 않았습니까?\""]], [["“You are deceived, Morrel, that woman——”", "\"잘못 알았네, 모렐. 그 여인은……\""]], [["“She was dead?”", "\"죽었습니까?\""]], [["“Worse than that, she was faithless, and had married one of the persecutors of her betrothed.", "\"그보다 더 나빴지. 그녀는 변심하여, 제 약혼자를 박해한 자들 가운데 하나와 혼인했네."], ["You see, then, Morrel, that he was a more unhappy lover than you.”", "그러니 알겠나, 모렐. 그는 자네보다 더 불행한 연인이었던 거지.\""]], [["“And has he found consolation?”", "\"그래, 그는 위안을 얻었습니까?\""]], [["“He has at least found peace.”", "\"적어도 평안만은 얻었네.\""]], [["“And does he ever expect to be happy?”", "\"그럼 언젠가 행복해지기를 바라고 있습니까?\""]], [["“He hopes so, Maximilian.”", "\"그렇게 바라고 있지, 막시밀리앙.\""]], [["The young man’s head fell on his breast.", "젊은이는 고개를 가슴 위로 떨구었다."]], [["“You have my promise,”", "\"제 약속을 드리겠습니다.\""], ["he said, after a minute’s pause, extending his hand to Monte Cristo. “Only remember——”", "잠시 침묵한 끝에 그가 몬테크리스토에게 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"다만 잊지 말아 주십시오……\""]], [["“On the 5th of October, Morrel, I shall expect you at the Island of Monte Cristo.", "\"10월 5일에, 모렐, 몬테크리스토섬에서 자네를 기다리겠네."], ["On the 4th a yacht will wait for you in the port of Bastia, it will be called the _Eurus_.", "4일에는 바스티아 항구에서 요트 한 척이 자네를 기다릴 걸세. 이름은 에우루스호일세."], ["You will give your name to the captain, who will bring you to me. It is understood—is it not?”", "자네가 선장에게 이름을 대면, 그가 자네를 내게로 데려다줄 걸세. 알겠지? 그렇지 않나?\""]], [["“But, count, do you remember that the 5th of October——”", "\"하지만 백작님, 10월 5일이라면 그날이……\""]], [["“Child,”", "\"이 사람아.\""], ["replied the count, “not to know the value of a man’s word!", "백작이 대답했다. \"사내의 약속이 지닌 값어치를 모른단 말인가!"], ["I have told you twenty times that if you wish to die on that day, I will assist you.", "자네가 그날 죽기를 바란다면 내가 도와주겠다고 스무 번도 더 말하지 않았나."], ["Morrel, farewell!”", "모렐, 잘 있게!\""]], [["“Do you leave me?”", "\"저를 떠나시는 겁니까?\""]], [["“Yes; I have business in Italy.", "\"그래. 이탈리아에 볼일이 있네."], ["I leave you alone in your struggle with misfortune—alone with that strong-winged eagle which God sends to bear aloft the elect to his feet.", "자네를 불행과의 싸움 속에 홀로 남겨 두고 가네. 하느님이 택한 자들을 그 발치로 들어 올리도록 보내신, 그 힘찬 날개를 가진 독수리와 함께 홀로 남겨 두고 가는 거지."], ["The story of Ganymede, Maximilian, is not a fable, but an allegory.”", "가니메데스의 이야기는, 막시밀리앙, 한낱 우화가 아니라 하나의 비유라네.\""]], [["“When do you leave?”", "\"언제 떠나십니까?\""]], [["“Immediately; the steamer waits, and in an hour I shall be far from you.", "\"지금 곧. 증기선이 기다리고 있네. 한 시간 뒤면 나는 자네에게서 멀리 떨어져 있겠지."], ["Will you accompany me to the harbor, Maximilian?”", "항구까지 나를 배웅해 주겠나, 막시밀리앙?\""]], [["50233m\n“I am entirely yours, count.”", "\"전 백작님의 사람입니다, 백작님.\""]], [["Morrel accompanied the count to the harbor.", "모렐은 백작을 항구까지 배웅했다."], ["The white steam was ascending like a plume of feathers from the black chimney.", "검은 굴뚝에서는 하얀 증기가 깃털 장식처럼 피어오르고 있었다."], ["The steamer soon disappeared, and in an hour afterwards, as the count had said, was scarcely distinguishable in the horizon amidst the fogs of the night.", "증기선은 곧 사라졌고, 한 시간 뒤에는, 백작이 말한 대로, 밤안개 속 수평선 너머로 거의 분간할 수 없게 되었다."]], [["# Chapter 114. Peppino", "# Chapter 114. Peppino"]], [["At the same time that the steamer disappeared behind Cape Morgiou, a man travelling post on the road from Florence to Rome had just passed the little town of Aquapendente.", "증기선이 모르지우곶 뒤로 사라지던 바로 그 무렵, 피렌체에서 로마로 가는 길을 역마차로 달리던 한 사내가 막 아콰펜덴테라는 작은 마을을 지나고 있었다."], ["He was travelling fast enough to cover a great deal of ground without exciting suspicion.", "그는 의심을 사지 않으면서도 상당한 거리를 주파할 만큼 빠르게 달렸다."], ["This man was dressed in a greatcoat, or rather a surtout, a little worse for the journey, but which exhibited the ribbon of the Legion of Honor still fresh and brilliant, a decoration which also ornamented the under coat.", "이 사내는 두꺼운 외투, 정확히는 프록코트를 걸치고 있었는데, 여행으로 다소 해진 그 옷에는 여전히 산뜻하고 빛나는 레지옹 도뇌르 훈장 리본이 달려 있었다. 그 안에 받쳐 입은 상의에도 같은 훈장이 장식되어 있었다."], ["He might be recognized, not only by these signs, but also from the accent with which he spoke to the postilion, as a Frenchman.", "그는 이런 표시뿐만 아니라 마부에게 말을 거는 억양만으로도 프랑스 사람임을 알아볼 수 있었다."]], [["Another proof that he was a native of the universal country was apparent in the fact of his knowing no other Italian words than the terms used in music, and which like the “goddam” of Figaro, served all possible linguistic requirements.", "그가 보편의 나라 태생이라는 또 다른 증거는, 음악에서 쓰이는 용어 말고는 다른 이탈리아 말을 전혀 모른다는 사실에서 드러났다. 그 용어들은 피가로의 \"고댐\"처럼 온갖 언어적 요구를 다 채워 주었다."], ["“_Allegro!_”", "\"알레그로!\""], ["he called out to the postilions at every ascent.", "그는 오르막마다 마부들에게 외쳤다."], ["“_Moderato!_”", "\"모데라토!\""], ["he cried as they descended.", "내리막에서는 이렇게 소리쳤다."], ["And heaven knows there are hills enough between Rome and Florence by the way of Aquapendente!", "그리고 하느님은 아시리라, 아콰펜덴테를 거쳐 로마와 피렌체 사이에는 언덕이 충분히도 많다는 것을!"], ["These two words greatly amused the men to whom they were addressed.", "이 두 마디는 그 말을 듣는 사내들을 무척 즐겁게 했다."], ["On reaching La Storta, the point from whence Rome is first visible, the traveller evinced none of the enthusiastic curiosity which usually leads strangers to stand up and endeavor to catch sight of the dome of Saint Peter’s, which may be seen long before any other object is distinguishable.", "로마가 처음으로 보이기 시작하는 지점인 라스토르타에 이르렀을 때, 이 여행자는 흔히 낯선 이들을 일으켜 세워 성 베드로 대성당의 둥근 지붕을 보려 애쓰게 만드는 그런 열광적인 호기심을 조금도 내비치지 않았다. 그 둥근 지붕은 다른 어떤 것이 분간되기도 한참 전부터 보이는 법인데도 말이다."], ["No, he merely drew a pocketbook from his pocket, and took from it a paper folded in four, and after having examined it in a manner almost reverential, he said:", "아니, 그는 그저 주머니에서 수첩을 꺼내 네 겹으로 접힌 종이 한 장을 빼냈고, 그것을 거의 경건하다 할 만한 태도로 살펴본 뒤 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“Good!", "\"좋아!"], ["I have it still!”", "아직 가지고 있군!\""]], [["50235m\nThe carriage entered by the Porta del Popolo, turned to the left, and stopped at the Hôtel d’Espagne.", "마차는 포폴로 문으로 들어와 왼쪽으로 돌더니 에스파냐 호텔 앞에 멈췄다."], ["Old Pastrini, our former acquaintance, received the traveller at the door, hat in hand.", "우리의 옛 지인인 늙은 파스트리니가 모자를 손에 쥔 채 문 앞에서 여행자를 맞았다."], ["The traveller alighted, ordered a good dinner, and inquired the address of the house of Thomson & French, which was immediately given to him, as it was one of the most celebrated in Rome.", "여행자는 마차에서 내려 푸짐한 저녁을 주문하고는 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회의 주소를 물었다. 그 상회는 로마에서 가장 이름난 곳 가운데 하나였으므로 주소는 즉시 알려졌다."], ["It was situated in the Via dei Banchi, near St. Peter’s.", "그곳은 성 베드로 대성당 근처 비아 데이 반키에 있었다."]], [["In Rome, as everywhere else, the arrival of a post-chaise is an event.", "다른 곳에서와 마찬가지로 로마에서도 역마차의 도착은 하나의 사건이었다."], ["Ten young descendants of Marius and the Gracchi, barefooted and out at elbows, with one hand resting on the hip and the other gracefully curved above the head, stared at the traveller, the post-chaise, and the horses; to these were added about fifty little vagabonds from the Papal States, who earned a pittance by diving into the Tiber at high water from the bridge of St. Angelo.", "맨발에 팔꿈치가 다 해진 마리우스와 그라쿠스의 젊은 후예 열 명이 한 손은 허리에 얹고 다른 손은 머리 위로 우아하게 구부린 채 여행자와 역마차와 말들을 빤히 바라보았다. 여기에 교황령 출신의 부랑아 쉰 명가량이 더해졌는데, 이들은 물이 불어나면 산탄젤로 다리에서 티베르강으로 뛰어들어 푼돈을 벌었다."], ["Now, as these street Arabs of Rome, more fortunate than those of Paris, understand every language, more especially the French, they heard the traveller order an apartment, a dinner, and finally inquire the way to the house of Thomson & French.", "그런데 파리의 부랑아들보다 운이 좋은 이 로마의 거리 떠돌이들은 모든 언어, 특히 프랑스어를 알아들었으므로, 여행자가 방과 저녁 식사를 주문하고 마침내 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회로 가는 길을 묻는 것을 다 들었다."]], [["The result was that when the new-comer left the hotel with the _cicerone_, a man detached himself from the rest of the idlers, and without having been seen by the traveller, and appearing to excite no attention from the guide, followed the stranger with as much skill as a Parisian police agent would have used.", "그 결과, 새로 온 사내가 안내인과 함께 호텔을 나설 때, 한 사내가 다른 건달들 무리에서 빠져나와 여행자에게 들키지도 않고 안내인의 주의도 전혀 끌지 않은 채, 파리의 경찰 첩자나 부릴 법한 능숙한 솜씨로 그 낯선 사내를 뒤쫓았다."]], [["The Frenchman had been so impatient to reach the house of Thomson & French that he would not wait for the horses to be harnessed, but left word for the carriage to overtake him on the road, or to wait for him at the bankers’ door.", "프랑스 사람은 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회에 닿고 싶은 마음이 어찌나 조급했던지 말에 마구를 채우기를 기다리지도 않았다. 마차더러 길에서 자기를 따라잡거나 은행가의 문 앞에서 기다리라는 말을 남기고 떠났다."], ["He reached it before the carriage arrived.", "그는 마차가 도착하기 전에 그곳에 닿았다."], ["The Frenchman entered, leaving in the anteroom his guide, who immediately entered into conversation with two or three of the industrious idlers who are always to be found in Rome at the doors of banking-houses, churches, museums, or theatres.", "프랑스 사람은 안으로 들어가면서 대기실에 안내인을 남겨 두었고, 안내인은 곧장 로마의 은행이며 교회며 박물관이며 극장 문 앞에 늘 어슬렁거리는 부지런한 건달 두세 명과 이야기를 나누기 시작했다."], ["With the Frenchman, the man who had followed him entered too; the Frenchman knocked at the inner door, and entered the first room; his shadow did the same.", "프랑스 사람과 함께 그를 뒤쫓던 사내도 들어왔다. 프랑스 사람이 안쪽 문을 두드리고 첫 번째 방으로 들어서자, 그의 그림자도 똑같이 했다."]], [["“Messrs. Thomson & French?”", "\"톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회입니까?\""], ["inquired the stranger.", "낯선 사내가 물었다."]], [["An attendant arose at a sign from a confidential clerk at the first desk.", "첫 번째 책상에 앉은 측근 직원이 신호를 보내자 사환 하나가 일어났다."]], [["“Whom shall I announce?”", "\"어느 분이라고 전할까요?\""], ["said the attendant.", "사환이 말했다."]], [["“Baron Danglars.”", "\"당글라르 남작이오.\""]], [["“Follow me,”", "\"따라오시지요.\""], ["said the man.", "사환이 말했다."]], [["A door opened, through which the attendant and the baron disappeared.", "문이 열리고, 사환과 남작은 그 안으로 사라졌다."], ["The man who had followed Danglars sat down on a bench.", "당글라르를 뒤쫓던 사내는 벤치에 앉았다."], ["The clerk continued to write for the next five minutes; the man preserved profound silence, and remained perfectly motionless.", "직원은 그 뒤로 오 분 동안 계속 글을 썼다. 사내는 깊은 침묵을 지키며 미동도 하지 않았다."], ["Then the pen of the clerk ceased to move over the paper; he raised his head, and appearing to be perfectly sure of privacy:", "그러다 직원의 펜이 종이 위에서 멈췄다. 그는 고개를 들더니, 아무도 없다는 것을 완전히 확신한 듯한 표정으로 말했다."]], [["“Ah, ha,” he said, “here you are, Peppino!”", "\"아, 하, 여기 있었군, 페피노!\""]], [["“Yes,” was the laconic reply.", "\"네.\" 짤막한 대답이 돌아왔다."], ["“You have found out that there is something worth having about this large gentleman?”", "\"저 덩치 큰 양반한테 챙길 만한 게 있다는 걸 알아내셨나요?\""]], [["“There is no great merit due to me, for we were informed of it.”", "\"내 공이랄 건 없어. 우리는 미리 통보를 받았으니까.\""]], [["“You know his business here, then.”", "\"그럼 저 사람이 여기서 무슨 일을 보는지도 아시겠군요.\""]], [["“_Pardieu_, he has come to draw, but I don’t know how much!”", "\"제기랄, 돈을 찾으러 온 거지. 다만 얼마인지는 나도 몰라!\""]], [["“You will know presently, my friend.”", "\"곧 알게 되실 겁니다, 친구.\""]], [["“Very well, only do not give me false information as you did the other day.”", "\"좋아요, 다만 지난번처럼 거짓 정보는 주지 마세요.\""]], [["“What do you mean?—of whom do you speak?", "\"그게 무슨 소린가? 누구 얘기를 하는 거야?"], ["Was it the Englishman who carried off 3,000 crowns from here the other day?”", "지난번에 여기서 삼천 크라운을 가져간 그 영국 사람 말인가?\""]], [["50237m\n“No; he really had 3,000 crowns, and we found them.", "\"아닙니다. 그자는 정말 삼천 크라운이 있었고, 우리도 그걸 찾아냈죠."], ["I mean the Russian prince, who you said had 30,000 livres, and we only found 22,000.”", "제가 말하는 건 러시아 공작 말입니다. 삼만 리브르가 있다고 하셨는데, 우리는 겨우 이만 이천밖에 못 찾았어요.\""]], [["“You must have searched badly.”", "\"네놈들이 제대로 뒤지지 못한 거겠지.\""]], [["“Luigi Vampa himself searched.”", "\"루이지 밤파가 직접 뒤졌습니다.\""]], [["“In that case he must either have paid his debts——”", "\"그렇다면 그자가 빚을 갚았거나……\""]], [["“A Russian do that?”", "\"러시아 사람이 그럴 리가요?\""]], [["“Or spent the money?”", "\"아니면 돈을 다 써버렸나?\""], ["“Possibly, after all.”", "\"그럴 수도 있겠네요, 따지고 보면.\""]], [["“Certainly.", "\"틀림없어."], ["But you must let me make my observations, or the Frenchman will transact his business without my knowing the sum.”", "하지만 내가 살펴볼 수 있게 가만있어야 해. 안 그러면 저 프랑스 사람이 거래를 끝내 버려서 내가 액수를 모르게 될 테니까.\""]], [["Peppino nodded, and taking a rosary from his pocket began to mutter a few prayers while the clerk disappeared through the same door by which Danglars and the attendant had gone out.", "페피노는 고개를 끄덕이더니 주머니에서 묵주를 꺼내 기도문 몇 마디를 중얼거리기 시작했고, 그동안 직원은 당글라르와 사환이 나간 바로 그 문으로 사라졌다."], ["At the expiration of ten minutes the clerk returned with a beaming countenance.", "십 분쯤 지나 직원은 환한 얼굴로 돌아왔다. \"그래서요?\" 페피노가 자기 동료에게 물었다."]], [["“Well?” asked Peppino of his friend.", "\"기쁜 일이야, 기쁜 일이라고. 액수가 어마어마해!\""]], [["“Joy, joy—the sum is large!”", "\"오백만이나 육백만쯤 되지요?\""]], [["“Five or six millions, is it not?”", "\"그래, 너도 액수를 알고 있구나.\""]], [["“On the receipt of the Count of Monte Cristo?”", "\"몬테크리스토 백작의 영수증을 받고서요?\""]], [["“Why, how came you to be so well acquainted with all this?”", "\"아니, 어떻게 이 모든 걸 그렇게 훤히 알고 있는 거지?\""]], [["“I told you we were informed beforehand.”", "\"우리가 미리 통보를 받았다고 말씀드렸잖습니까.\""]], [["“Then why do you apply to me?”", "\"그럼 왜 내게 물어보는 거야?\""]], [["“That I may be sure I have the right man.”", "\"제대로 된 사람을 잡았는지 확실히 해 두려고요.\""]], [["“Yes, it is indeed he.", "\"그래, 정말 그자가 맞아."], ["Five millions—a pretty sum, eh, Peppino?”", "오백만이라, 제법 큰돈이지, 안 그래, 페피노?\""]], [["“Hush—here is our man!”", "\"쉿, 우리 양반이 오네요!\""], ["The clerk seized his pen, and Peppino his beads; one was writing and the other praying when the door opened.", "직원은 펜을 잡았고 페피노는 묵주를 잡았다. 문이 열렸을 때 한 사람은 글을 쓰고 다른 사람은 기도를 하고 있었다."], ["Danglars looked radiant with joy; the banker accompanied him to the door.", "당글라르는 기쁨으로 환히 빛났다. 은행가가 그를 문까지 배웅했다."], ["Peppino followed Danglars.", "페피노가 당글라르를 뒤따랐다."]], [["According to the arrangements, the carriage was waiting at the door. The guide held the door open.", "약속대로 마차가 문 앞에서 기다리고 있었다. 안내인이 마차 문을 열어 잡고 있었다."], ["Guides are useful people, who will turn their hands to anything.", "안내인이란 무슨 일에든 손을 보태는 쓸모 있는 사람들이다."], ["Danglars leaped into the carriage like a young man of twenty.", "당글라르는 스무 살 청년처럼 마차 안으로 훌쩍 뛰어올랐다."], ["The _cicerone_ reclosed the door, and sprang up by the side of the coachman.", "안내인은 다시 문을 닫고 마부 곁으로 뛰어올랐다."], ["Peppino mounted the seat behind.", "페피노는 뒷자리에 올라탔다."]], [["“Will your excellency visit Saint Peter’s?”", "\"각하, 성 베드로 대성당을 둘러보시겠습니까?\""], ["asked the _cicerone_.", "안내인이 물었다."]], [["“I did not come to Rome to see,”", "\"나는 구경하러 로마에 온 게 아니오.\""], ["said Danglars aloud; then he added softly, with an avaricious smile, “I came to touch!”", "당글라르가 큰 소리로 말하더니, 탐욕스러운 미소를 지으며 나직이 덧붙였다. \"나는 손에 넣으러 온 거요!\""], ["and he rapped his pocket-book, in which he had just placed a letter.", "그러고는 방금 편지 한 통을 넣어 둔 수첩을 톡톡 두드렸다."]], [["“Then your excellency is going——”", "\"그럼 각하께서는 어디로……\""]], [["“To the hotel.”", "\"호텔로.\""]], [["“Casa Pastrini!” said the _cicerone_ to the coachman, and the carriage drove rapidly on.", "\"카사 파스트리니!\" 안내인이 마부에게 말했고, 마차는 빠르게 달려 나갔다."]], [["50239m\nTen minutes afterwards the baron entered his apartment, and Peppino stationed himself on the bench outside the door of the hotel, after having whispered something in the ear of one of the descendants of Marius and the Gracchi whom we noticed at the beginning of the chapter, who immediately ran down the road leading to the Capitol at his fullest speed.", "십 분 뒤 남작은 자기 방으로 들어갔고, 페피노는 호텔 문밖 벤치에 자리를 잡았다. 그러기 전에 그는 이 장 첫머리에서 보았던 마리우스와 그라쿠스의 후예 가운데 한 명의 귀에 무언가를 속삭였는데, 그 아이는 곧장 카피톨로 이어지는 길을 전속력으로 달려 내려갔다."], ["Danglars was tired and sleepy; he therefore went to bed, placing his pocketbook under his pillow.", "당글라르는 피곤하고 졸렸으므로 수첩을 베개 밑에 넣어 두고 잠자리에 들었다."], ["Peppino had a little spare time, so he had a game of _morra_ with the facchini, lost three crowns, and then to console himself drank a bottle of Orvieto.", "페피노는 시간이 좀 남아서 짐꾼들과 모라 놀이를 한 판 했다가 삼 크라운을 잃었고, 그 분풀이로 오르비에토 포도주를 한 병 마셨다."]], [["The next morning Danglars awoke late, though he went to bed so early; he had not slept well for five or six nights, even if he had slept at all.", "이튿날 아침 당글라르는 일찍 잠자리에 들었는데도 늦게 일어났다. 닷새인가 엿새 동안, 설령 잤다 해도 깊이 잠들지 못했던 것이다."], ["He breakfasted heartily, and caring little, as he said, for the beauties of the Eternal City, ordered post-horses at noon.", "그는 푸짐하게 아침을 먹고는, 자기 말마따나 영원의 도시의 아름다움에는 도무지 관심이 없어 정오에 역마를 주문했다."], ["But Danglars had not reckoned upon the formalities of the police and the idleness of the posting-master.", "그러나 당글라르는 경찰의 절차와 역참장의 게으름은 미처 헤아리지 못했다."], ["The horses only arrived at two o’clock, and the _cicerone_ did not bring the passport till three.", "말은 두 시가 되어서야 도착했고, 안내인은 세 시가 되어서야 통행증을 가져왔다."]], [["All these preparations had collected a number of idlers round the door of Signor Pastrini’s; the descendants of Marius and the Gracchi were also not wanting.", "이 모든 준비로 파스트리니 영감의 문 앞에는 구경꾼들이 잔뜩 모여들었다. 마리우스와 그라쿠스의 후예들도 빠지지 않았다."], ["The baron walked triumphantly through the crowd, who for the sake of gain styled him “your excellency.”", "남작은 돈을 노리고 그를 \"각하\"라 부르는 군중 사이를 의기양양하게 걸어 나갔다."], ["As Danglars had hitherto contented himself with being called a baron, he felt rather flattered at the title of excellency, and distributed a dozen silver coins among the beggars, who were ready, for twelve more, to call him “your highness.”", "그동안 남작이라는 호칭으로 만족해 온 당글라르는 각하라는 칭호에 다소 우쭐해졌고, 거지들에게 은화 열두 닢을 나눠 주었다. 거지들은 열두 닢만 더 주면 기꺼이 그를 \"전하\"라 불러 줄 태세였다."]], [["“Which road?” asked the postilion in Italian.", "\"어느 길로 갑니까?\" 마부가 이탈리아어로 물었다."]], [["“The Ancona road,”", "\"안코나 길로.\""], ["replied the baron.", "남작이 대답했다."], ["Signor Pastrini interpreted the question and answer, and the horses galloped off.", "파스트리니 영감이 질문과 대답을 통역했고, 말들은 질주해 나갔다."]], [["Danglars intended travelling to Venice, where he would receive one part of his fortune, and then proceeding to Vienna, where he would find the rest, he meant to take up his residence in the latter town, which he had been told was a city of pleasure.", "당글라르는 베네치아로 가서 재산의 일부를 받은 다음 빈으로 가 나머지를 찾을 생각이었고, 환락의 도시라고 들은 그 빈에 거처를 마련할 작정이었다."]], [["He had scarcely advanced three leagues out of Rome when daylight began to disappear.", "로마를 벗어나 삼 리외도 채 못 갔을 때 날이 저물기 시작했다."], ["Danglars had not intended starting so late, or he would have remained; he put his head out and asked the postilion how long it would be before they reached the next town.", "당글라르는 이렇게 늦게 출발할 생각이 아니었기에, 알았다면 머물렀을 것이다. 그는 고개를 내밀고 마부에게 다음 마을까지 얼마나 걸리겠느냐고 물었다."], ["“_Non capisco_” (do not understand), was the reply.", "\"논 카피스코\"(못 알아듣겠는데요)라는 대답이 돌아왔다."], ["Danglars bent his head, which he meant to imply, “Very well.”", "당글라르는 \"좋아\"라는 뜻으로 고개를 숙였다."], ["The carriage again moved on.", "마차는 다시 달리기 시작했다."]], [["“I will stop at the first posting-house,”", "\"첫 번째 역참에 멈추도록 하지.\""], ["said Danglars to himself.", "당글라르는 혼잣말을 했다."]], [["He still felt the same self-satisfaction which he had experienced the previous evening, and which had procured him so good a night’s rest.", "그는 전날 저녁 느꼈던, 그리고 그토록 단잠을 안겨 주었던 그 자족감을 여전히 느끼고 있었다."], ["He was luxuriously stretched in a good English calash, with double springs; he was drawn by four good horses, at full gallop; he knew the relay to be at a distance of seven leagues.", "그는 이중 용수철이 달린 좋은 영국제 사륜마차에 호사스럽게 몸을 뻗고 있었다. 훌륭한 말 네 마리가 전속력으로 마차를 끌고 있었고, 다음 역참까지는 칠 리외 거리라는 것도 알고 있었다."], ["What subject of meditation could present itself to the banker, so fortunately become bankrupt?", "그토록 운 좋게 파산한 이 은행가에게 무슨 사색거리가 떠오를 수 있었겠는가?"]], [["Danglars thought for ten minutes about his wife in Paris; another ten minutes about his daughter travelling with Mademoiselle d’Armilly; the same period was given to his creditors, and the manner in which he intended spending their money; and then, having no subject left for contemplation, he shut his eyes, and fell asleep.", "당글라르는 십 분 동안 파리에 있는 아내를 생각했고, 또 십 분 동안은 다르밀리 양과 함께 여행 중인 딸을 생각했다. 그리고 같은 시간 동안 채권자들과 그들의 돈을 어떻게 쓸지를 생각했다. 그러고 나서는 더 이상 떠올릴 거리가 없자 눈을 감고 잠이 들었다."], ["Now and then a jolt more violent than the rest caused him to open his eyes; then he felt that he was still being carried with great rapidity over the same country, thickly strewn with broken aqueducts, which looked like granite giants petrified while running a race.", "이따금 유난히 심한 덜컹거림에 눈을 뜨면, 자신이 여전히 부서진 수도교가 빽빽이 흩어진 그 똑같은 땅 위를 빠른 속도로 실려 가고 있음을 느꼈다. 그 수도교들은 경주를 하다가 그대로 굳어 버린 화강암 거인들처럼 보였다."], ["But the night was cold, dull, and rainy, and it was much more pleasant for a traveller to remain in the warm carriage than to put his head out of the window to make inquiries of a postilion whose only answer was “_Non capisco_.” 50241m", "그러나 밤은 춥고 침침하고 비가 내렸으므로, 여행자에게는 창밖으로 고개를 내밀어 \"논 카피스코\"라는 대답밖에 할 줄 모르는 마부에게 묻는 것보다 따뜻한 마차 안에 있는 편이 훨씬 더 편했다."]], [["Danglars therefore continued to sleep, saying to himself that he would be sure to awake at the posting-house.", "그래서 당글라르는 역참에 닿으면 틀림없이 깨어날 거라고 스스로 다독이며 계속 잤다."], ["The carriage stopped.", "마차가 멈췄다."], ["Danglars fancied that they had reached the long-desired point; he opened his eyes and looked through the window, expecting to find himself in the midst of some town, or at least village; but he saw nothing except what seemed like a ruin, where three or four men went and came like shadows.", "당글라르는 그토록 바라던 지점에 도착한 줄 알았다. 그는 눈을 뜨고 창밖을 내다보며, 어느 마을 한복판이거나 적어도 어느 촌락에 와 있으리라 기대했다. 그러나 그가 본 것은 폐허처럼 보이는 무언가뿐이었고, 그 안에서 서너 명의 사내가 그림자처럼 오가고 있었다."]], [["Danglars waited a moment, expecting the postilion to come and demand payment with the termination of his stage.", "당글라르는 마부가 와서 구간이 끝났으니 삯을 달라고 하기를 잠시 기다렸다."], ["He intended taking advantage of the opportunity to make fresh inquiries of the new conductor; but the horses were unharnessed, and others put in their places, without anyone claiming money from the traveller.", "그는 그 기회를 틈타 새 마부에게 다시 길을 물을 작정이었다. 그런데 누구도 여행자에게 돈을 요구하지 않은 채 말들의 마구가 풀리고 다른 말들로 바뀌었다."], ["Danglars, astonished, opened the door; but a strong hand pushed him back, and the carriage rolled on.", "당글라르는 놀라서 문을 열었다. 그러나 억센 손이 그를 도로 밀어 넣었고, 마차는 다시 굴러갔다."], ["The baron was completely roused.", "남작은 완전히 잠이 깼다."]], [["“Eh?”", "\"이봐?\""], ["he said to the postilion, “eh, _mio caro?_”", "그가 마부에게 말했다. \"이봐, 미오 카로?\""]], [["This was another little piece of Italian the baron had learned from hearing his daughter sing Italian duets with Cavalcanti.", "이것은 남작이 딸이 카발칸티와 함께 이탈리아 이중창을 부르는 것을 들으며 익힌 또 다른 이탈리아어 한 토막이었다."], ["But _mio caro_ did not reply.", "그러나 미오 카로는 대답이 없었다."], ["Danglars then opened the window.", "당글라르는 창문을 열었다."]], [["“Come, my friend,”", "\"이보게, 친구.\""], ["he said, thrusting his hand through the opening, “where are we going?”", "그가 틈새로 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"우리가 어디로 가는 건가?\""]], [["“_Dentro la testa!_”", "\"덴트로 라 테스타!\""], ["answered a solemn and imperious voice, accompanied by a menacing gesture.", "위협적인 몸짓과 함께 엄숙하고 위압적인 목소리가 대답했다."]], [["Danglars thought _dentro la testa_ meant, “Put in your head!”", "당글라르는 덴트로 라 테스타가 \"머리를 집어넣어!\"라는 뜻이라고 생각했다."], ["He was making rapid progress in Italian.", "그는 이탈리아어 실력이 빠르게 늘고 있었다."], ["He obeyed, not without some uneasiness, which, momentarily increasing, caused his mind, instead of being as unoccupied as it was when he began his journey, to fill with ideas which were very likely to keep a traveller awake, more especially one in such a situation as Danglars.", "그는 약간의 불안감과 함께 그 말에 따랐고, 그 불안감은 시시각각 커져, 여행을 시작할 때처럼 텅 비어 있던 그의 머릿속을 여행자를, 더구나 당글라르 같은 처지의 여행자를 깨어 있게 만들기 십상인 온갖 생각으로 가득 채웠다."], ["His eyes acquired that quality which in the first moment of strong emotion enables them to see distinctly, and which afterwards fails from being too much taxed.", "그의 눈은 강한 감정의 첫 순간에 사물을 또렷이 보게 해 주는, 그러나 그 뒤로 너무 혹사당해 이내 흐려지는 그런 능력을 띠었다."], ["Before we are alarmed, we see correctly; when we are alarmed, we see double; and when we have been alarmed, we see nothing but trouble.", "놀라기 전에 우리는 정확히 본다. 놀라는 순간에는 두 개로 겹쳐 본다. 그리고 놀라고 난 뒤에는 곤경밖에 보이지 않는다."], ["Danglars observed a man in a cloak galloping at the right hand of the carriage.", "당글라르는 외투를 걸친 한 사내가 마차의 오른편에서 말을 달리는 것을 보았다."]], [["“Some gendarme!”", "\"헌병이로군!\""], ["he exclaimed.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["“Can I have been intercepted by French telegrams to the pontifical authorities?”", "\"내가 프랑스에서 교황청 당국으로 보낸 전보에 걸려든 건가?\""]], [["He resolved to end his anxiety.", "그는 불안을 끝장내기로 마음먹었다."], ["“Where are you taking me?”", "\"나를 어디로 데려가는 거요?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“_Dentro la testa_,”", "\"덴트로 라 테스타.\""], ["replied the same voice, with the same menacing accent.", "똑같은 목소리가 똑같이 위협적인 억양으로 대답했다."]], [["Danglars turned to the left; another man on horseback was galloping on that side.", "당글라르는 왼쪽으로 고개를 돌렸다. 또 다른 사내가 그쪽에서 말을 달리고 있었다."]], [["“Decidedly,”", "\"틀림없어.\""], ["said Danglars, with the perspiration on his forehead, “I must be under arrest.”", "당글라르가 이마에 땀을 흘리며 말했다. \"내가 체포된 게 분명해.\""], ["And he threw himself back in the calash, not this time to sleep, but to think.", "그러고는 사륜마차 등받이에 몸을 던졌는데, 이번에는 자려는 게 아니라 생각하기 위해서였다."]], [["Directly afterwards the moon rose.", "곧이어 달이 떠올랐다."], ["He then saw the great aqueducts, those stone phantoms which he had before remarked, only then they were on the right hand, now they were on the left.", "그러자 그는 아까 눈여겨보았던 그 거대한 수도교들, 그 돌 유령들을 다시 보게 되었는데, 다만 그때는 오른편에 있던 것이 지금은 왼편에 있었다."], ["He understood that they had described a circle, and were bringing him back to Rome.", "그는 마차가 원을 그리며 자기를 로마로 도로 데려가고 있음을 깨달았다."]], [["“Oh, unfortunate!”", "\"오, 불운하구나!\""], ["he cried, “they must have obtained my arrest.”", "그가 외쳤다. \"저들이 내 체포 영장을 받아 둔 게 틀림없어.\""]], [["The carriage continued to roll on with frightful speed.", "마차는 무시무시한 속도로 계속 굴러갔다."], ["An hour of terror elapsed, for every spot they passed showed that they were on the road back.", "공포의 한 시간이 흘렀다. 지나치는 곳마다 그들이 돌아가는 길 위에 있음을 알려 주었기 때문이다."], ["At length he saw a dark mass, against which it seemed as if the carriage was about to dash; but the vehicle turned to one side, leaving the barrier behind and Danglars saw that it was one of the ramparts encircling Rome. 50243m", "마침내 그는 어두운 덩어리 하나를 보았고, 마차가 거기에 부딪힐 것만 같았다. 그러나 마차는 한쪽으로 방향을 틀어 장벽을 뒤로하고 지나갔고, 당글라르는 그것이 로마를 두른 성벽 가운데 하나임을 알아보았다."]], [["“_Mon dieu!_”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["cried Danglars, “we are not returning to Rome; then it is not justice which is pursuing me!", "당글라르가 외쳤다. \"우리는 로마로 돌아가는 게 아니야. 그렇다면 나를 뒤쫓는 건 사법 당국이 아니로구나!"], ["Gracious heavens; another idea presents itself—what if they should be——”", "오, 자비로운 하느님. 또 다른 생각이 떠오르는데, 만에 하나 저들이……\""]], [["His hair stood on end.", "그의 머리카락이 곤두섰다."], ["He remembered those interesting stories, so little believed in Paris, respecting Roman bandits; he remembered the adventures that Albert de Morcerf had related when it was intended that he should marry Mademoiselle Eugénie.", "그는 파리에서는 거의 믿지 않던, 로마 산적들에 관한 그 흥미진진한 이야기들을 떠올렸다. 그는 자기 딸 외제니와 알베르 드 모르세르의 결혼이 추진되던 무렵 알베르가 들려주었던 그 모험담을 떠올렸다."], ["“They are robbers, perhaps,”", "\"어쩌면 강도들인지도 몰라.\""], ["he muttered.", "그가 중얼거렸다."]], [["Just then the carriage rolled on something harder than gravel road.", "바로 그때 마차가 자갈길보다 단단한 무언가 위를 굴러갔다."], ["Danglars hazarded a look on both sides of the road, and perceived monuments of a singular form, and his mind now recalled all the details Morcerf had related, and comparing them with his own situation, he felt sure that he must be on the Appian Way.", "당글라르는 길 양옆으로 조심스레 눈길을 던졌고, 기묘한 형태의 기념물들을 보았다. 이제 그의 머릿속에 모르세르가 들려준 모든 세부가 떠올랐고, 그것을 자기 처지와 견주어 보니 자신이 아피아 가도 위에 있는 게 틀림없다는 확신이 들었다."], ["On the left, in a sort of valley, he perceived a circular excavation.", "왼편 골짜기 같은 곳에서 그는 둥글게 파인 구덩이를 발견했다."], ["It was Caracalla’s circus. On a word from the man who rode at the side of the carriage, it stopped.", "그것은 카라칼라 원형 경기장이었다. 마차 옆에서 달리던 사내가 한마디 하자 마차가 멈췄다."], ["At the same time the door was opened. “_Scendi!_”", "동시에 문이 열렸다. \"셴디!\""], ["exclaimed a commanding voice.", "명령조의 목소리가 외쳤다."]], [["Danglars instantly descended; although he did not yet speak Italian, he understood it very well.", "당글라르는 즉시 내렸다. 아직 이탈리아어를 할 줄은 몰랐지만 알아듣기는 아주 잘했다."], ["More dead than alive, he looked around him.", "그는 산 사람도 죽은 사람도 아닌 몰골로 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["Four men surrounded him, besides the postilion.", "마부 말고도 네 명의 사내가 그를 에워싸고 있었다."]], [["“_Di quà_,”", "\"디 콰.\""], ["said one of the men, descending a little path leading out of the Appian Way.", "사내들 가운데 하나가 아피아 가도에서 빠지는 작은 오솔길을 내려가며 말했다."], ["Danglars followed his guide without opposition, and had no occasion to turn around to see whether the three others were following him.", "당글라르는 저항 없이 안내인을 따라갔고, 나머지 세 명이 뒤따르는지 굳이 돌아볼 필요도 없었다."], ["Still it appeared as though they were stationed at equal distances from one another, like sentinels.", "그래도 그들은 보초들처럼 서로 같은 간격을 두고 자리한 듯했다."], ["After walking for about ten minutes, during which Danglars did not exchange a single word with his guide, he found himself between a hillock and a clump of high weeds; three men, standing silent, formed a triangle, of which he was the centre.", "십 분쯤 걸어가는 동안 당글라르는 안내인과 단 한마디도 나누지 않았고, 어느새 작은 언덕과 키 큰 잡초 덤불 사이에 와 있었다. 말없이 선 세 사내가 삼각형을 이루었고, 그가 그 한가운데였다."], ["He wished to speak, but his tongue refused to move.", "그는 말을 하고 싶었지만 혀가 움직이려 하지 않았다."]], [["“_Avanti!_”", "\"아반티!\""], ["said the same sharp and imperative voice.", "똑같이 날카롭고 위압적인 목소리가 말했다."]], [["This time Danglars had double reason to understand, for if the word and gesture had not explained the speaker’s meaning, it was clearly expressed by the man walking behind him, who pushed him so rudely that he struck against the guide.", "이번에는 당글라르가 알아들을 이유가 갑절이었다. 그 말과 몸짓이 화자의 뜻을 설명해 주지 못했다 해도, 뒤에서 걷던 사내가 어찌나 거칠게 떠밀었던지 그는 안내인에게 부딪혔고, 그것으로 뜻은 분명히 전해졌다."], ["This guide was our friend Peppino, who dashed into the thicket of high weeds, through a path which none but lizards or polecats could have imagined to be an open road.", "이 안내인은 다름 아닌 우리의 친구 페피노였는데, 그는 도마뱀이나 족제비나 길로 여길 법한 통로를 따라 키 큰 잡초 덤불 속으로 뛰어들었다."]], [["Peppino stopped before a rock overhung by thick hedges; the rock, half open, afforded a passage to the young man, who disappeared like the evil spirits in the fairy tales.", "페피노는 빽빽한 덤불이 드리운 바위 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 반쯤 갈라진 그 바위는 젊은이에게 통로를 내주었고, 그는 동화 속 악령들처럼 사라졌다."], ["The voice and gesture of the man who followed Danglars ordered him to do the same.", "당글라르를 뒤따르던 사내의 목소리와 몸짓이 그에게도 똑같이 하라고 명했다."], ["There was no longer any doubt, the bankrupt was in the hands of Roman banditti.", "더 이상 의심의 여지가 없었다. 이 파산자는 로마 산적들의 손아귀에 떨어진 것이다."], ["Danglars acquitted himself like a man placed between two dangerous positions, and who is rendered brave by fear.", "당글라르는 두 가지 위태로운 처지 사이에 놓여 두려움 덕에 도리어 용감해진 사람답게 행동했다."], ["Notwithstanding his large stomach, certainly not intended to penetrate the fissures of the Campagna, he slid down like Peppino, and closing his eyes fell upon his feet.", "캄파냐의 바위틈을 비집고 들어가기에는 전혀 어울리지 않는 큰 배를 가졌는데도, 그는 페피노처럼 미끄러져 내려갔고, 눈을 감은 채 두 발로 떨어졌다."], ["As he touched the ground, he opened his eyes. 50245m", "땅에 닿자 그는 눈을 떴다."]], [["The path was wide, but dark.", "통로는 넓었지만 어두웠다."], ["Peppino, who cared little for being recognized now that he was in his own territories, struck a light and lit a torch.", "이제 자기 영역에 들어왔으니 얼굴이 알려져도 상관없다고 여긴 페피노는 부싯돌을 쳐서 횃불을 밝혔다."], ["Two other men descended after Danglars forming the rearguard, and pushing Danglars whenever he happened to stop, they came by a gentle declivity to the intersection of two corridors.", "다른 두 사내가 후위를 이루며 당글라르를 뒤따라 내려왔고, 당글라르가 멈출 때마다 그를 떠밀어 완만한 비탈을 따라 두 갈래 통로가 교차하는 지점에 이르렀다."], ["The walls were hollowed out in sepulchres, one above the other, and which seemed in contrast with the white stones to open their large dark eyes, like those which we see on the faces of the dead.", "벽에는 무덤들이 위아래로 층층이 파여 있었는데, 그것은 흰 돌과 대비되어, 마치 죽은 자의 얼굴에서 보이는 그런 크고 어두운 눈을 부릅뜨고 있는 듯했다."], ["A sentinel struck the rings of his carbine against his left hand.", "보초 하나가 자기 기병총의 고리를 왼손에 부딪쳐 소리를 냈다."]], [["“Who comes there?” he cried.", "\"거기 누구냐?\" 그가 외쳤다."]], [["“A friend, a friend!”", "\"친구다, 친구!\""], ["said Peppino; “but where is the captain?”", "페피노가 말했다. \"그런데 두목은 어디 계시지?\""]], [["“There,”", "\"저기.\""], ["said the sentinel, pointing over his shoulder to a spacious crypt, hollowed out of the rock, the lights from which shone into the passage through the large arched openings.", "보초가 어깨 너머로, 바위를 파서 만든 널찍한 지하실을 가리키며 말했다. 그 안의 불빛이 큰 아치형 입구들을 통해 통로로 새어 나오고 있었다."]], [["“Fine spoil, captain, fine spoil!”", "\"멋진 노획물입니다, 두목."], ["said Peppino in Italian, and taking Danglars by the collar of his coat he dragged him to an opening resembling a door, through which they entered the apartment which the captain appeared to have made his dwelling-place.", "멋진 노획물이에요!\" 페피노가 이탈리아어로 말하더니, 당글라르의 외투 깃을 잡고 문처럼 생긴 입구로 그를 끌고 갔다. 그들은 그 입구를 통해 두목이 거처로 삼은 듯한 방으로 들어갔다."]], [["“Is this the man?” asked the captain, who was attentively reading Plutarch’s _Life of Alexander_.", "\"이자가 그 사람인가?\" 플루타르코스의 『알렉산드로스 전기』를 골똘히 읽고 있던 두목이 물었다."]], [["“Himself, captain—himself.”", "\"바로 그자입니다, 두목. 그자예요.\""]], [["“Very well, show him to me.”", "\"좋아, 어디 좀 보여 줘 봐.\""]], [["At this rather impertinent order, Peppino raised his torch to the face of Danglars, who hastily withdrew that he might not have his eyelashes burnt.", "다소 무례한 이 명령에 페피노는 횃불을 당글라르의 얼굴 쪽으로 들어 올렸고, 당글라르는 속눈썹이 그을릴까 봐 황급히 물러났다."], ["His agitated features presented the appearance of pale and hideous terror.", "동요한 그의 얼굴에는 창백하고 흉측한 공포가 어려 있었다."]], [["“The man is tired,”", "\"이 사람은 지쳤군.\""], ["said the captain, “conduct him to his bed.”", "두목이 말했다. \"잠자리로 데려가.\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오.\""], ["murmured Danglars, “that bed is probably one of the coffins hollowed in the wall, and the sleep I shall enjoy will be death from one of the poniards I see glistening in the darkness.”", "당글라르가 중얼거렸다. \"저 잠자리란 게 아마도 벽에 파인 관들 중 하나일 테고, 내가 누리게 될 잠이란 어둠 속에서 번득이는 저 단검들 중 하나에 찔려 맞는 죽음이겠지.\""]], [["From their beds of dried leaves or wolf-skins at the back of the chamber now arose the companions of the man who had been found by Albert de Morcerf reading _Cæsar’s Commentaries_, and by Danglars studying the _Life of Alexander_.", "방 안쪽 마른 잎이나 늑대 가죽으로 된 잠자리에서, 알베르 드 모르세르에게는 『카이사르 전기』를 읽는 모습으로, 당글라르에게는 『알렉산드로스 전기』를 들여다보는 모습으로 발견된 그 사내의 동료들이 이제 몸을 일으켰다."], ["The banker uttered a groan and followed his guide; he neither supplicated nor exclaimed.", "은행가는 신음을 내뱉으며 안내인을 따라갔다. 그는 애원하지도 부르짖지도 않았다."], ["He no longer possessed strength, will, power, or feeling; he followed where they led him.", "그에게는 더 이상 힘도 의지도 기력도 감각도 남아 있지 않았다."], ["At length he found himself at the foot of a staircase, and he mechanically lifted his foot five or six times.", "그는 그들이 이끄는 대로 따라갔다. 마침내 그는 계단 아래에 이르렀고, 기계적으로 발을 대여섯 번 들어 올렸다."], ["Then a low door was opened before him, and bending his head to avoid striking his forehead he entered a small room cut out of the rock.", "그러자 그의 앞에 낮은 문이 열렸고, 그는 이마를 부딪치지 않으려고 고개를 숙인 채 바위를 깎아 만든 작은 방으로 들어갔다."], ["The cell was clean, though empty, and dry, though situated at an immeasurable distance under the earth.", "그 방은 비어 있긴 했어도 깨끗했고, 땅속 헤아릴 수 없는 깊이에 있는데도 건조했다."], ["A bed of dried grass covered with goat-skins was placed in one corner.", "한쪽 구석에는 염소 가죽을 덮은 마른 풀 침상이 놓여 있었다."], ["Danglars brightened up on beholding it, fancying that it gave some promise of safety.", "당글라르는 그것을 보자 무언가 안전을 약속받은 듯한 기분에 얼굴이 밝아졌다."]], [["“Oh, God be praised,”", "\"오, 하느님 감사합니다.\""], ["he said; “it is a real bed!”", "그가 말했다. \"진짜 침대로구나!\""]], [["This was the second time within the hour that he had invoked the name of God.", "이것은 그가 한 시간 안에 하느님의 이름을 부른 두 번째 일이었다."], ["He had not done so for ten years before.", "그는 그 전 십 년 동안 그런 일이 없었다."]], [["“_Ecco!_”", "\"에코!\""], ["said the guide, and pushing Danglars into the cell, he closed the door upon him.", "안내인이 말하고는, 당글라르를 방 안으로 떠밀어 넣고 문을 닫았다."]], [["A bolt grated and Danglars was a prisoner.", "빗장이 삐걱 소리를 냈고, 당글라르는 갇힌 신세가 되었다."], ["If there had been no bolt, it would have been impossible for him to pass through the midst of the garrison who held the catacombs of St.", "빗장이 없었다 해도, 그가 성 세바스티아노 카타콤을 지키며 한 두목 주위에 진을 친 그 수비대 한복판을 빠져나가기란 불가능했을 것이다."], ["Sebastian, encamped round a master whom our readers must have recognized as the famous Luigi Vampa.", "그 두목이 바로 독자들이 알아챘을 그 유명한 루이지 밤파였다."]], [["Danglars, too, had recognized the bandit, whose existence he would not believe when Albert de Morcerf mentioned him in Paris; and not only did he recognize him, but the cell in which Albert had been confined, and which was probably kept for the accommodation of strangers.", "당글라르 역시 그 산적을 알아보았는데, 알베르 드 모르세르가 파리에서 그를 언급했을 때만 해도 당글라르는 그런 자가 실재한다는 것을 믿으려 하지 않았다. 그리고 그는 그 산적뿐 아니라, 알베르가 갇혔던 그 방, 아마도 손님 접대용으로 마련된 듯한 그 방까지 알아보았다."], ["These recollections were dwelt upon with some pleasure by Danglars, and restored him to some degree of tranquillity.", "당글라르는 어느 정도 흡족한 마음으로 이런 기억들을 곱씹었고, 그러자 마음이 어느 정도 진정되었다."], ["Since the bandits had not despatched him at once, he felt that they would not kill him at all.", "산적들이 그를 당장 처치하지 않은 것을 보니, 결국 죽이지는 않으리라는 생각이 들었다."], ["They had arrested him for the purpose of robbery, and as he had only a few louis about him, he doubted not he would be ransomed.", "그들은 강탈할 목적으로 그를 붙잡았는데, 그가 몸에 지닌 것이라곤 루이 금화 몇 닢뿐이었으므로, 몸값을 받고 풀어 줄 것이 틀림없다고 그는 믿었다."]], [["He remembered that Morcerf had been taxed at 4,000 crowns, and as he considered himself of much greater importance than Morcerf he fixed his own price at 8,000 crowns.", "그는 모르세르의 몸값이 사천 크라운으로 매겨졌던 것을 떠올렸고, 자신이 모르세르보다 훨씬 더 중요한 인물이라고 여겼으므로 제 몸값을 팔천 크라운으로 정했다."], ["Eight thousand crowns amounted to 48,000 livres; he would then have about 5,050,000 francs left.", "팔천 크라운이면 사만 팔천 리브르였다. 그러면 그에게는 오백오만 프랑가량이 남을 것이다."], ["With this sum he could manage to keep out of difficulties.", "그 정도 돈이면 곤경에서 벗어날 수 있었다."], ["Therefore, tolerably secure in being able to extricate himself from his position, provided he were not rated at the unreasonable sum of 5,050,000 francs, he stretched himself on his bed, and after turning over two or three times, fell asleep with the tranquillity of the hero whose life Luigi Vampa was studying.", "그러므로 자신이 오백오만 프랑이라는 터무니없는 액수로 매겨지지만 않는다면 이 처지에서 빠져나올 수 있으리라 어지간히 안심한 그는, 침상에 몸을 뻗고는 두세 번 뒤척이다가 루이지 밤파가 그 생애를 공부하고 있던 영웅 같은 평온함으로 잠들었다."]], [["# Chapter 115. Luigi Vampa’s Bill of Fare", "# Chapter 115. Luigi Vampa’s Bill of Fare"]], [["We awake from every sleep except the one dreaded by Danglars. He awoke.", "우리는 어떤 잠에서도 깨어난다. 단 하나, 당글라르가 두려워하는 그 잠만은 예외다."], ["To a Parisian accustomed to silken curtains, walls hung with velvet drapery, and the soft perfume of burning wood, the white smoke of which diffuses itself in graceful curves around the room, the appearance of the whitewashed cell which greeted his eyes on awakening seemed like the continuation of some disagreeable dream.", "그가 깨어났다. 비단 휘장과 벨벳을 두른 벽, 그리고 우아한 곡선을 그리며 방 안을 떠도는 흰 연기를 피워 올리는 장작의 은은한 향내에 익숙한 파리 사람에게, 잠에서 깨어 눈에 들어온 회칠한 토굴의 광경은 어떤 불쾌한 꿈의 연장처럼만 느껴졌다."], ["But in such a situation a single moment suffices to change the strongest doubt into certainty.", "그러나 이런 상황에서는 한순간이면 가장 완강한 의심도 확신으로 바뀌기에 충분하다."]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"그래, 그래.\""], ["he murmured, “I am in the hands of the brigands of whom Albert de Morcerf spoke.”", "그가 중얼거렸다. \"알베르 드 모르세르가 말하던 그 산적들 손에 잡혀 있는 거야.\""], ["His first idea was to breathe, that he might know whether he was wounded.", "그의 첫 번째 생각은 숨을 쉬어 보아, 자신이 다쳤는지 알아보자는 것이었다."], ["He borrowed this from _Don Quixote_, the only book he had ever read, but which he still slightly remembered.", "이건 그가 여태 읽은 유일한 책, 그러나 아직 어렴풋이 기억하고 있는 「돈키호테」에서 빌려 온 방법이었다."]], [["“No,” he cried, “they have not wounded, but perhaps they have robbed me!”", "\"아니야.\" 그가 외쳤다."], ["and he thrust his hands into his pockets.", "\"다친 데는 없군. 하지만 어쩌면 강도질은 당했을지도!\""], ["They were untouched; the hundred louis he had reserved for his journey from Rome to Venice were in his trousers pocket, and in that of his greatcoat he found the little note-case containing his letter of credit for 5,050,000 francs.", "그러고는 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣었다. 손도 대지 않은 그대로였다. 로마에서 베네치아까지 가는 여비로 챙겨 둔 백 루이가 바지 주머니에 있었고, 외투 주머니에는 505만 프랑짜리 신용장이 든 작은 지갑이 들어 있었다."]], [["“Singular bandits!”", "\"별난 도둑들이로군!\""], ["he exclaimed; “they have left me my purse and pocket-book.", "그가 외쳤다. \"지갑도 수첩도 그대로 두다니."], ["As I was saying last night, they intend me to be ransomed.", "어젯밤에 내가 말했듯이, 이놈들은 나를 인질로 잡아 몸값을 받아 낼 셈이야."], ["Hello, here is my watch!", "어럽쇼, 시계도 있네!"], ["Let me see what time it is.”", "어디 몇 시인지 보자.\""]], [["Danglars’ watch, one of Breguet’s repeaters, which he had carefully wound up on the previous night, struck half past five.", "당글라르의 시계는 브레게 회사의 자명종 시계로, 그가 전날 밤 정성껏 태엽을 감아 둔 것이었다. 시계가 다섯 시 반을 알렸다."], ["Without this, Danglars would have been quite ignorant of the time, for daylight did not reach his cell.", "이게 아니었더라면 당글라르는 시간을 도통 알 길이 없었을 것이다. 햇빛이 토굴까지 들지 않았기 때문이다."], ["Should he demand an explanation from the bandits, or should he wait patiently for them to propose it?", "산적들에게 설명을 요구해야 할까, 아니면 그들이 먼저 입을 열 때까지 참고 기다려야 할까?"], ["The last alternative seemed the most prudent, so he waited until twelve o’clock.", "후자가 더 신중한 길로 보였기에, 그는 열두 시까지 기다렸다."], ["During all this time a sentinel, who had been relieved at eight o’clock, had been watching his door. 50249m", "그동안 여덟 시에 교대한 보초 하나가 줄곧 그의 방문을 지키고 있었다."]], [["Danglars suddenly felt a strong inclination to see the person who kept watch over him.", "당글라르는 문득 자신을 감시하는 자의 얼굴이 몹시 보고 싶어졌다."], ["He had noticed that a few rays, not of daylight, but from a lamp, penetrated through the ill-joined planks of the door; he approached just as the brigand was refreshing himself with a mouthful of brandy, which, owing to the leathern bottle containing it, sent forth an odor which was extremely unpleasant to Danglars.", "그는 잘 맞물리지 않은 문짝 틈으로 가느다란 빛줄기 몇 가닥이 새어 드는 것을 알아챘다. 햇빛이 아니라 등불 빛이었다. 그가 문가로 다가갔을 때, 마침 산적은 가죽 부대에 든 브랜디를 한 모금 들이켜며 목을 축이고 있었다."], ["“Faugh!” he exclaimed, retreating to the farther corner of his cell.", "그 가죽 부대 탓에 풍겨 나는 냄새가 당글라르에게는 견딜 수 없이 역겨웠다. \"윽!\" 그가 외치며 토굴의 맨 안쪽 구석으로 물러섰다."]], [["At twelve this man was replaced by another functionary, and Danglars, wishing to catch sight of his new guardian, approached the door again.", "열두 시가 되자 이 사내는 다른 자와 교대했고, 당글라르는 새 감시자의 얼굴을 보고 싶은 마음에 다시 문가로 다가갔다."]], [["He was an athletic, gigantic bandit, with large eyes, thick lips, and a flat nose; his red hair fell in dishevelled masses like snakes around his shoulders.", "그는 체격이 우람한 거구의 산적이었다. 눈은 크고 입술은 두툼하며 코는 납작했다. 붉은 머리카락은 어깨를 감싸고 뱀처럼 헝클어진 채 늘어져 있었다."]], [["“Ah, ha,”", "\"이런, 이런.\""], ["cried Danglars, “this fellow is more like an ogre than anything else; however, I am rather too old and tough to be very good eating!”", "당글라르가 외쳤다. \"이놈은 사람이라기보다 식인귀에 더 가깝군. 그래도 나는 너무 늙고 질겨서 맛있게 먹기는 어려울걸!\""]], [["We see that Danglars was collected enough to jest; at the same time, as though to disprove the ogreish propensities, the man took some black bread, cheese, and onions from his wallet, which he began devouring voraciously.", "이로써 당글라르가 농담을 던질 만큼은 침착함을 되찾았음을 알 수 있다. 한편, 마치 그 식인귀 같은 인상을 부정이라도 하려는 듯, 사내는 자루에서 검은 빵과 치즈, 양파를 꺼내더니 게걸스럽게 먹어 치우기 시작했다."]], [["“May I be hanged,”", "\"내 목을 매달아도 좋아.\""], ["said Danglars, glancing at the bandit’s dinner through the crevices of the door,—“may I be hanged if I can understand how people can eat such filth!”", "당글라르가 문틈으로 산적의 식사를 들여다보며 말했다. \"어떻게 저런 더러운 걸 먹을 수 있는지 도무지 이해가 안 가면, 차라리 내 목을 매달라지!\""], ["and he withdrew to seat himself upon his goat-skin, which reminded him of the smell of the brandy.", "그러고는 물러나 염소 가죽 위에 앉았는데, 그 가죽에서 또 브랜디 냄새가 떠올랐다."]], [["But the mysteries of nature are incomprehensible, and there are certain invitations contained in even the coarsest food which appeal very irresistibly to a fasting stomach.", "그러나 자연의 신비란 헤아릴 수 없는 법이고, 가장 거친 음식에도 굶주린 위장에는 도저히 거역할 수 없게 호소해 오는 어떤 유혹이 깃들어 있는 법이다."], ["Danglars felt his own not to be very well supplied just then, and gradually the man appeared less ugly, the bread less black, and the cheese more fresh, while those dreadful vulgar onions recalled to his mind certain sauces and side-dishes, which his cook prepared in a very superior manner whenever he said, “Monsieur Deniseau, let me have a nice little fricassee today.”", "당글라르는 마침 자기 위장이 그리 든든히 채워져 있지 않다는 것을 느꼈다. 차츰 그 사내는 덜 흉해 보였고, 빵은 덜 검어 보였으며, 치즈는 더 신선해 보였다. 그 끔찍하고 천박한 양파마저도, 그가 \"드니조 씨, 오늘은 맛있는 프리카세를 좀 만들어 주게.\"라고 할 때마다 요리사가 더없이 솜씨 좋게 차려 내던 어떤 소스며 곁들이 요리를 떠오르게 했다."], ["He got up and knocked on the door; the bandit raised his head.", "그는 일어나 문을 두드렸다. 산적이 고개를 들었다."], ["Danglars knew that he was heard, so he redoubled his blows.", "당글라르는 자기 소리가 들렸음을 알았기에, 두드림을 더 세차게 거듭했다."]], [["“_Che cosa?_”", "\"케 코사?\""], ["asked the bandit.", "산적이 물었다."]], [["“Come, come,”", "\"이봐, 이봐.\""], ["said Danglars, tapping his fingers against the door, “I think it is quite time to think of giving me something to eat!”", "당글라르가 손가락으로 문을 두드리며 말했다. \"이제 내게 뭐라도 먹을 걸 줄 때가 되지 않았나 싶은데!\""]], [["But whether he did not understand him, or whether he had received no orders respecting the nourishment of Danglars, the giant, without answering, went on with his dinner.", "그러나 사내는 그의 말을 못 알아들었는지, 아니면 당글라르에게 먹을 것을 주라는 지시를 받지 못했는지, 대꾸도 없이 식사를 계속했다."], ["Danglars’ feelings were hurt, and not wishing to put himself under obligations to the brute, the banker threw himself down again on his goat-skin and did not breathe another word.", "당글라르는 자존심이 상했다. 이 짐승 같은 놈에게 신세를 지고 싶지 않았던 은행가는 다시 염소 가죽 위에 몸을 던지고는 더 이상 한마디도 내뱉지 않았다."]], [["Four hours passed by and the giant was replaced by another bandit.", "네 시간이 흘렀고, 거구의 사내는 또 다른 산적과 교대했다."], ["Danglars, who really began to experience sundry gnawings at the stomach, arose softly, again applied his eye to the crack of the door, and recognized the intelligent countenance of his guide.", "정말이지 위장에서 갖가지 쓰라림이 일기 시작한 당글라르는 가만히 일어나 다시 문틈에 눈을 갖다 댔고, 영리해 보이는 길잡이의 얼굴을 알아보았다. 다름 아닌 페피노였다."], ["It was, indeed, Peppino who was preparing to mount guard as comfortably as possible by seating himself opposite to the door, and placing between his legs an earthen pan, containing chick-peas stewed with bacon.", "그는 가능한 한 편안하게 보초를 설 채비를 하던 참이었다. 문을 마주 보고 앉아, 두 다리 사이에 베이컨을 넣고 끓인 병아리콩이 담긴 토기 냄비를 놓아두었다."], ["Near the pan he also placed a pretty little basket of Villetri grapes and a flask of Orvieto.", "냄비 옆에는 빌레트리산 포도가 담긴 예쁜 작은 바구니와 오르비에토산 포도주 한 병도 놓여 있었다."], ["Peppino was decidedly an epicure.", "페피노는 확실히 식도락가였다."], ["Danglars watched these preparations and his mouth watered.", "당글라르는 이런 준비를 지켜보았고, 입안에 군침이 돌았다."]], [["“Come,”", "\"좋아.\""], ["he said to himself, “let me try if he will be more tractable than the other;”", "그가 혼잣말을 했다. \"이놈이 저번 놈보다 좀 더 말이 통하는지 시험해 보자.\""], ["and he tapped gently at the door.", "그러고는 문을 가만히 두드렸다."]], [["“_On y va_,” (coming) exclaimed Peppino, who from frequenting the house of Signor Pastrini understood French perfectly in all its idioms.", "\"오니 바!\"(갑니다!) 페피노가 외쳤다. 그는 파스트리니 씨네 집을 자주 드나든 덕에 프랑스어를 관용구까지 완벽하게 알아들었다."]], [["Danglars immediately recognized him as the man who had called out in such a furious manner, “Put in your head!”", "당글라르는 곧바로 그가 그토록 사납게 \"머리 집어넣어!\"라고 소리치던 자라는 것을 알아보았다."], ["But this was not the time for recrimination, so he assumed his most agreeable manner and said with a gracious smile:", "그러나 지금은 따질 때가 아니었으므로, 그는 더없이 상냥한 태도를 취하며 정중한 미소를 띠고 말했다."]], [["“Excuse me, sir, but are they not going to give me any dinner?”", "\"실례지만, 선생, 식사는 안 주는 거요?\""]], [["“Does your excellency happen to be hungry?”", "\"각하께서 혹 시장하신지요?\""]], [["“Happen to be hungry,—that’s pretty good, when I haven’t eaten for twenty-four hours!”", "\"혹 시장하냐고, 그것참 좋은 말이군, 스물네 시간이나 굶었는데!\""], ["muttered Danglars.", "당글라르가 투덜거렸다."], ["Then he added aloud, “Yes, sir, I am hungry—very hungry.”", "그러더니 소리 내어 덧붙였다. \"그렇소, 선생, 배가 고프오. 몹시 고프오.\""]], [["“And your excellency wants something to eat?”", "\"각하께서는 뭔가 드시고 싶으신 거군요?\""]], [["“At once, if possible”", "\"가능하면 당장.\""]], [["“Nothing easier,”", "\"그보다 쉬운 일도 없지요.\""], ["said Peppino.", "페피노가 말했다."], ["“Here you can get anything you want; by paying for it, of course, as among honest folk.”", "\"여기서는 원하시는 건 뭐든 구하실 수 있습니다. 물론 정직한 사람들 사이에서처럼 값을 치르셔야지요.\""]], [["“Of course!”", "\"물론이지!\""], ["cried Danglars.", "당글라르가 외쳤다."], ["“Although, in justice, the people who arrest and imprison you, ought, at least, to feed you.”", "\"하지만 따지고 보면, 사람을 붙잡아 가둔 자들이라면 적어도 먹이기는 해야 도리가 아닌가.\""]], [["“That is not the custom, excellency,”", "\"그게 관례가 아니라서요, 각하.\""], ["said Peppino.", "페피노가 말했다."]], [["“A bad reason,”", "\"형편없는 핑계로군.\""], ["replied Danglars, who reckoned on conciliating his keeper; “but I am content.", "감시인의 마음을 누그러뜨릴 셈이던 당글라르가 대꾸했다. \"그래도 좋아."], ["Let me have some dinner!”", "식사나 좀 갖다주게!\""]], [["“At once! What would your excellency like?”", "\"당장 올리지요! 각하께서는 무얼 드시겠습니까?\""]], [["And Peppino placed his pan on the ground, so that the steam rose directly under the nostrils of Danglars.", "페피노는 냄비를 바닥에 내려놓아, 김이 곧장 당글라르의 콧구멍 밑으로 피어오르게 했다."], ["“Give your orders.”", "\"분부하시지요.\""]], [["“Have you kitchens here?”", "\"여기 주방이 있나?\""]], [["“Kitchens?—of course—complete ones.”", "\"주방이요? 물론이지요, 없는 것 없이 갖춰져 있습니다.\""]], [["“And cooks?”", "\"요리사도?\""]], [["“Excellent!”", "\"훌륭한 요리사가요!\""]], [["“Well, a fowl, fish, game,—it signifies little, so that I eat.”", "\"그럼 닭고기든 생선이든 사냥한 고기든, 먹을 수만 있다면 무엇이든 좋네.\""]], [["“As your excellency pleases.", "\"각하 뜻대로 하시지요."], ["You mentioned a fowl, I think?”", "닭고기라 하셨던가요?\""]], [["“Yes, a fowl.”", "\"그래, 닭고기.\""]], [["Peppino, turning around, shouted, “A fowl for his excellency!”", "페피노가 돌아서더니 외쳤다. \"각하께 닭 한 마리!\""], ["His voice yet echoed in the archway when a handsome, graceful, and half-naked young man appeared, bearing a fowl in a silver dish on his head, without the assistance of his hands.", "그 목소리가 아치형 통로에 채 메아리치기도 전에, 잘생기고 우아하며 반쯤 벗은 젊은이 하나가 나타났다. 그는 은 접시에 닭 한 마리를 담아 손도 대지 않고 머리에 인 채였다."]], [["“I could almost believe myself at the Café de Paris,” murmured Danglars.", "\"꼭 파리의 카페에 와 있는 기분이군.\" 당글라르가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Here, your excellency,”", "\"자, 각하.\""], ["said Peppino, taking the fowl from the young bandit and placing it on the worm-eaten table, which with the stool and the goat-skin bed formed the entire furniture of the cell.", "페피노가 젊은 산적에게서 닭을 받아, 벌레 먹은 탁자 위에 올려놓으며 말했다. 그 탁자는 의자와 염소 가죽 침상과 함께 토굴 안 가구의 전부였다."], ["Danglars asked for a knife and fork.", "당글라르가 나이프와 포크를 청했다."]], [["“Here, excellency,” said Peppino, offering him a little blunt knife and a boxwood fork.", "\"여기 있습니다, 각하.\" 페피노가 무딘 작은 칼과 회양목 포크를 내밀며 말했다."], ["Danglars took the knife in one hand and the fork in the other, and was about to cut up the fowl.", "당글라르는 한 손에 칼을, 다른 손에 포크를 쥐고 닭을 자르려 했다."]], [["“Pardon me, excellency,”", "\"죄송합니다, 각하.\""], ["said Peppino, placing his hand on the banker’s shoulder; “people pay here before they eat.", "페피노가 은행가의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"여기서는 먹기 전에 값을 치르는 법입니다."], ["They might not be satisfied, and——”", "자칫 만족하지 못하실 수도 있고, 그러면……\""]], [["“Ah, ha,”", "\"이런, 이런.\""], ["thought Danglars, “this is not so much like Paris, except that I shall probably be skinned!", "당글라르는 속으로 생각했다. \"여긴 파리랑 영 딴판이군. 십중팔구 가죽이 벗겨지겠어!"], ["Never mind, I’ll fix that all right.", "그래도 상관없다, 잘 처리하면 돼."], ["I have always heard how cheap poultry is in Italy; I should think a fowl is worth about twelve sous at Rome.—There,”", "이탈리아에서는 닭고기가 싸다는 얘기를 늘 들었으니, 로마에서 닭 한 마리쯤이야 한 열두 수 하겠지. 자, 받게.\""], ["he said, throwing a louis down.", "그가 1루이를 던지며 말했다."]], [["Peppino picked up the louis, and Danglars again prepared to carve the fowl.", "페피노는 루이를 주워 들었고, 당글라르는 다시 닭을 자를 채비를 했다."]], [["“Stay a moment, your excellency,”", "\"잠깐만요, 각하.\""], ["said Peppino, rising; “you still owe me something.”", "페피노가 일어서며 말했다. \"아직 제게 갚으실 게 남았습니다.\""]], [["“I said they would skin me,”", "\"가죽이 벗겨질 거랬지.\""], ["thought Danglars; but resolving to resist the extortion, he said, “Come, how much do I owe you for this fowl?”", "당글라르는 속으로 생각했다. 그러나 이 갈취에 맞서기로 마음먹고 말했다. \"좋아, 이 닭값으로 자네에게 얼마를 갚아야 하나?\""]], [["“Your excellency has given me a louis on account.”", "\"각하께서는 선금으로 1루이를 주셨습니다.\""]], [["“A louis on account for a fowl?”", "\"닭 한 마리 선금이 1루이라고?\""]], [["“Certainly; and your excellency now owes me 4,999 louis.”", "\"그렇습니다. 그리고 각하께서는 이제 제게 4,999루이를 갚으셔야 합니다.\""]], [["Danglars opened his enormous eyes on hearing this gigantic joke.", "이 어마어마한 농담을 듣고 당글라르는 가뜩이나 큰 눈을 한껏 부릅떴다."]], [["“Very droll,” he muttered, “very droll indeed,”", "\"아주 재미있군.\" 그가 중얼거렸다."], ["and he again began to carve the fowl, when Peppino stopped the baron’s right hand with his left, and held out his other hand.", "\"정말 아주 재미있어.\" 그러고는 다시 닭을 자르기 시작했다. 그때 페피노가 왼손으로 남작의 오른손을 막고는, 다른 손을 내밀었다."]], [["“Come, now,”", "\"자, 이제.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Is it not a joke?”", "\"농담이 아닌가?\""], ["said Danglars.", "당글라르가 말했다."]], [["“We never joke,” replied Peppino, solemn as a Quaker.", "\"저희는 농담 같은 건 하지 않습니다.\" 페피노가 퀘이커 교도처럼 근엄하게 대꾸했다."]], [["“What! A hundred thousand francs for a fowl!”", "\"뭐라고! 닭 한 마리에 십만 프랑이라니!\""]], [["“Ah, excellency, you cannot imagine how hard it is to rear fowls in these horrible caves!”", "\"아, 각하, 이 끔찍한 동굴 속에서 닭을 기르기가 얼마나 힘든지 짐작도 못 하실 겁니다!\""]], [["“Come, come, this is very droll—very amusing—I allow; but, as I am very hungry, pray allow me to eat.", "\"이봐, 이봐, 아주 우습군, 아주 재미있어, 인정하네. 하지만 내가 몹시 시장하니, 제발 먹게 해 주게."], ["Stay, here is another louis for you.”", "자, 여기 1루이 더 있네.\""]], [["“Then that will make only 4,998 louis more,”", "\"그럼 4,998루이만 더 갚으시면 되겠군요.\""], ["said Peppino with the same indifference.", "페피노가 여전히 무심하게 말했다."], ["“I shall get them all in time.”", "\"그것도 차차 다 받겠지요.\""]], [["“Oh, as for that,”", "\"오, 그거라면,\""], ["said Danglars, angry at this prolongation of the jest,—“as for that you won’t get them at all.", "농담이 길어지는 데 화가 난 당글라르가 말했다. \"그거라면 한 푼도 못 받을 줄 알아."], ["Go to the devil!", "꺼져 버려!"], ["You do not know with whom you have to deal!”", "네가 누구를 상대하는지 모르는 모양인데!\""]], [["50253m\nPeppino made a sign, and the youth hastily removed the fowl.", "페피노가 신호를 보내자, 젊은이가 잽싸게 닭을 치웠다."], ["Danglars threw himself upon his goat-skin, and Peppino, reclosing the door, again began eating his peas and bacon.", "당글라르는 염소 가죽 위에 몸을 던졌고, 페피노는 문을 다시 닫고서 콩과 베이컨을 다시 먹기 시작했다."], ["Though Danglars could not see Peppino, the noise of his teeth allowed no doubt as to his occupation.", "당글라르에게 페피노의 모습은 보이지 않았지만, 그 이 부딪는 소리만으로도 그가 무얼 하는지는 의심할 여지가 없었다."], ["He was certainly eating, and noisily too, like an ill-bred man.", "분명 그는 먹고 있었고, 그것도 버릇없는 자처럼 요란하게 먹고 있었다."], ["“Brute!” said Danglars.", "\"짐승 같은 놈!\""], ["Peppino pretended not to hear him, and without even turning his head continued to eat slowly.", "당글라르가 말했다. 페피노는 못 들은 척하며, 고개조차 돌리지 않고 천천히 계속 먹었다."], ["Danglars’ stomach felt so empty, that it seemed as if it would be impossible ever to fill it again; still he had patience for another half-hour, which appeared to him like a century.", "당글라르의 위장은 어찌나 텅 빈 것 같았던지, 다시는 도저히 채울 수 없을 것만 같았다. 그래도 그는 다시 반 시간을 참아 냈는데, 그 시간이 그에게는 한 세기처럼 느껴졌다."], ["He again arose and went to the door.", "그는 또 일어나 문가로 갔다."]], [["“Come, sir, do not keep me starving here any longer, but tell me what they want.”", "\"이보게, 선생, 여기서 더는 나를 굶기지 말고, 저들이 원하는 게 뭔지 말해 주게.\""]], [["“Nay, your excellency, it is you who should tell us what you want.", "\"아닙니다, 각하. 무얼 원하시는지 말씀하셔야 할 분은 바로 각하시지요."], ["Give your orders, and we will execute them.”", "분부만 하시면 저희가 그대로 받들겠습니다.\""]], [["“Then open the door directly.” Peppino obeyed.", "\"그럼 당장 문을 열게.\" 페피노가 그대로 따랐다."], ["“Now look here, I want something to eat!", "\"자, 잘 들어. 나는 먹을 게 필요하다!"], ["To eat—do you hear?”", "먹을 것 말이야, 알겠나?\""]], [["“Are you hungry?”", "\"시장하십니까?\""]], [["“Come, you understand me.”", "\"이봐, 알아들었잖나.\""]], [["“What would your excellency like to eat?”", "\"각하께서는 무얼 드시겠습니까?\""]], [["“A piece of dry bread, since the fowls are beyond all price in this accursed place.”", "\"마른 빵 한 조각이면 됐네. 이 빌어먹을 곳에서는 닭값이 천정부지로 비싸니 말이야.\""]], [["“Bread? Very well.", "\"빵이요? 좋습니다."], ["Holloa, there, some bread!”", "거기 누구 없나, 빵 좀 가져와!\""], ["he called.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["The youth brought a small loaf.", "젊은이가 작은 빵 한 덩이를 가져왔다."], ["“How much?”", "\"얼마인가?\""], ["asked Danglars.", "당글라르가 물었다."]], [["“Four thousand nine hundred and ninety-eight louis,”", "\"4,998루이입니다.\" 페피노가 말했다."], ["said Peppino; “You have paid two louis in advance.” 50255m", "\"각하께서는 선금으로 2루이를 치르셨으니까요.\""]], [["“What? One hundred thousand francs for a loaf?”", "\"뭐라고? 빵 한 덩이에 십만 프랑이라고?\""]], [["“One hundred thousand francs,” repeated Peppino.", "\"십만 프랑입니다.\" 페피노가 거듭 말했다."]], [["“But you only asked 100,000 francs for a fowl!”", "\"하지만 자네는 닭 한 마리에도 십만 프랑밖에 안 불렀잖나!\""]], [["“We have a fixed price for all our provisions.", "\"저희는 모든 식료품에 고정된 값을 매겨 두었습니다."], ["It signifies nothing whether you eat much or little—whether you have ten dishes or one—it is always the same price.”", "많이 드시든 적게 드시든, 열 접시를 드시든 한 접시를 드시든, 아무 상관 없습니다. 값은 늘 똑같지요.\""]], [["“What, still keeping up this silly jest?", "\"뭐야, 아직도 이 어리석은 농담을 계속하는 건가?"], ["My dear fellow, it is perfectly ridiculous—stupid!", "이보게, 정말이지 우스꽝스럽고 멍청하기 짝이 없네!"], ["You had better tell me at once that you intend starving me to death.”", "차라리 나를 굶겨 죽일 작정이라고 솔직히 말하는 게 낫겠어.\""]], [["“Oh, dear, no, your excellency, unless you intend to commit suicide.", "\"오, 천만에요, 각하. 각하께서 스스로 목숨을 끊으실 작정이 아니라면요."], ["Pay and eat.”", "값을 치르고 드시지요.\""]], [["“And what am I to pay with, brute?”", "\"그럼 뭘로 치르라는 거냐, 이 짐승 같은 놈아?\""], ["said Danglars, enraged.", "당글라르가 격분하여 말했다."], ["“Do you suppose I carry 100,000 francs in my pocket?”", "\"내가 주머니에 십만 프랑이라도 넣고 다니는 줄 아나?\""]], [["“Your excellency has 5,050,000 francs in your pocket; that will be fifty fowls at 100,000 francs apiece, and half a fowl for the 50,000.”", "\"각하 주머니에는 505만 프랑이 들어 있지요. 한 마리에 십만 프랑씩이면 닭 쉰 마리에다, 나머지 5만 프랑으로 닭 반 마리를 더 드실 수 있는 액수입니다.\""]], [["Danglars shuddered.", "당글라르는 몸서리쳤다."], ["The bandage fell from his eyes, and he understood the joke, which he did not think quite so stupid as he had done just before.", "눈에서 가리개가 벗겨졌고, 그는 비로소 그 농담을 이해했다. 조금 전만큼 어리석은 농담은 아니라는 생각이 들었다."]], [["“Come,”", "\"좋아.\""], ["he said, “if I pay you the 100,000 francs, will you be satisfied, and allow me to eat at my ease?”", "그가 말했다. \"내가 자네에게 십만 프랑을 치르면, 그걸로 만족하고 마음 편히 먹게 해 주겠나?\""]], [["“Certainly,”", "\"물론입니다.\""], ["said Peppino.", "페피노가 말했다."]], [["“But how can I pay them?”", "\"하지만 어떻게 치른단 말인가?\""]], [["“Oh, nothing easier; you have an account open with Messrs.", "\"오, 그보다 쉬운 일도 없지요."], ["Thomson & French, Via dei Banchi, Rome; give me a draft for 4,998 louis on these gentlemen, and our banker shall take it.”", "각하께서는 로마 비아데이반키의 톰슨 앤드 프렌치 상회에 거래 계좌를 두고 계시지 않습니까. 그 회사 앞으로 4,998루이짜리 어음을 한 장 써 주시면, 저희 은행가가 받아 올 겁니다.\""]], [["Danglars thought it as well to comply with a good grace, so he took the pen, ink, and paper Peppino offered him, wrote the draft, and signed it.", "당글라르는 순순히 따르는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했다. 그래서 페피노가 내미는 펜과 잉크, 종이를 받아 어음을 쓰고 서명했다."]], [["“Here,”", "\"자.\""], ["he said, “here is a draft at sight.”", "그가 말했다. \"여기 일람불 어음일세.\""]], [["“And here is your fowl.”", "\"그리고 여기 각하의 닭입니다.\""]], [["Danglars sighed while he carved the fowl; it appeared very thin for the price it had cost.", "당글라르는 한숨을 내쉬며 닭을 잘랐다. 치른 값에 비하면 어지간히도 빈약해 보이는 닭이었다."], ["As for Peppino, he examined the paper attentively, put it into his pocket, and continued eating his peas.", "한편 페피노는 종이를 꼼꼼히 살피더니 주머니에 넣고는, 다시 콩을 먹기 시작했다."]], [["# Chapter 116. The Pardon", "# 제114장 페피노"]], [["The next day Danglars was again hungry; certainly the air of that dungeon was very provocative of appetite.", "이튿날 당글라르는 다시 허기를 느꼈다. 분명 그 지하 감방의 공기는 식욕을 몹시 돋우는 것이었다."], ["The prisoner expected that he would be at no expense that day, for like an economical man he had concealed half of his fowl and a piece of the bread in the corner of his cell.", "죄수는 그날만큼은 비용을 치를 일이 없으리라 기대했다. 알뜰한 사람답게 닭고기 절반과 빵 한 조각을 감방 구석에 감춰 두었으니 말이다."], ["But he had no sooner eaten than he felt thirsty; he had forgotten that.", "그러나 그것을 먹기가 무섭게 그는 목이 말라 왔다."], ["He struggled against his thirst till his tongue clave to the roof of his mouth; then, no longer able to resist, he called out.", "그 점은 미처 생각하지 못했던 것이다. 혀가 입천장에 들러붙을 때까지 갈증과 싸우다가, 더는 견딜 수 없게 되자 소리쳐 불렀다."], ["The sentinel opened the door; it was a new face.", "보초가 문을 열었다. 처음 보는 얼굴이었다."], ["He thought it would be better to transact business with his old acquaintance, so he sent for Peppino.", "그는 안면이 있는 사람과 흥정하는 편이 낫겠다고 생각하여 페피노를 불러 달라고 했다."]], [["“Here I am, your excellency,”", "\"여기 있습니다, 각하.\""], ["said Peppino, with an eagerness which Danglars thought favorable to him.", "페피노가 말했다. 당글라르가 보기에 자기에게 호의적인 듯한 그 열의를 담은 목소리였다."], ["“What do you want?”", "\"무엇을 원하십니까?\""]], [["“Something to drink.”", "\"마실 것 좀.\""]], [["“Your excellency knows that wine is beyond all price near Rome.”", "\"로마 근처에서는 포도주 값이 비싸기 짝이 없다는 걸 각하께서도 아실 텐데요.\""]], [["“Then give me water,”", "\"그럼 물을 주게.\""], ["cried Danglars, endeavoring to parry the blow.", "당글라르가 그 일격을 받아넘기려 애쓰며 외쳤다."]], [["“Oh, water is even more scarce than wine, your excellency,—there has been such a drought.”", "\"아, 물은 포도주보다 더 귀합니다, 각하. 어찌나 가물었는지요.\""]], [["“Come,”", "'이런,'"], ["thought Danglars, “it is the same old story.”", "당글라르는 생각했다. '여전히 똑같은 수작이군.'"], ["And while he smiled as he attempted to regard the affair as a joke, he felt his temples get moist with perspiration.", "그러고는 이 일을 농담으로 여기려는 듯 미소를 지었으나, 관자놀이가 땀으로 축축해지는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“Come, my friend,”", "\"이보게, 친구.\""], ["said Danglars, seeing that he made no impression on Peppino, “you will not refuse me a glass of wine?”", "페피노에게 아무 인상도 주지 못한 것을 깨닫고 당글라르가 말했다. \"포도주 한 잔쯤은 거절하지 않겠지?\""]], [["“I have already told you that we do not sell at retail.”", "\"낱개로는 팔지 않는다고 이미 말씀드렸습니다.\""]], [["“Well, then, let me have a bottle of the least expensive.”", "\"그래, 그렇다면 제일 싼 걸로 한 병 주게.\""]], [["“They are all the same price.”", "\"전부 같은 값입니다.\""]], [["“And what is that?”", "\"그게 얼마인데?\""]], [["“Twenty-five thousand francs a bottle.”", "\"한 병에 2만 5천 프랑입니다.\""]], [["“Tell me,”", "\"말해 보게.\""], ["cried Danglars, in a tone whose bitterness Harpagon30 alone has been capable of revealing—“tell me that you wish to despoil me of all; it will be sooner over than devouring me piecemeal.”", "당글라르가 아르파공●만이 드러낼 수 있을 법한 쓰디쓴 어조로 외쳤다. \"나를 통째로 털어먹고 싶다고 말이야. 그게 한 조각씩 야금야금 뜯어먹는 것보다 빠를 테니까.\""]], [["“It is possible such may be the master’s intention.”", "\"주인님의 뜻이 그러할지도 모르지요.\""]], [["“The master?—who is he?”", "\"주인이라니, 그게 누구인가?\""]], [["“The person to whom you were conducted yesterday.”", "\"어제 각하를 모셔 갔던 그분 말입니다.\""]], [["“Where is he?”", "\"그는 어디 있나?\""]], [["“Here.” “Let me see him.”", "\"여기 있습니다.\" \"좀 만나게 해 주게.\""]], [["“Certainly.”", "\"그러지요.\""]], [["And the next moment Luigi Vampa appeared before Danglars.", "그러자 이내 루이지 밤파가 당글라르 앞에 나타났다."]], [["“You sent for me?”", "\"나를 불렀소?\""], ["he said to the prisoner.", "그가 죄수에게 말했다."]], [["“Are you, sir, the chief of the people who brought me here?”", "\"당신이 나를 이리로 끌고 온 자들의 두목이오?\""]], [["“Yes, your excellency.", "\"그렇소, 각하."], ["What then?”", "그래서요?\""]], [["“How much do you require for my ransom?”", "\"몸값으로 얼마를 원하오?\""]], [["“Merely the 5,000,000 you have about you.”", "\"각하가 지니고 계신 500만 프랑이면 됩니다.\""], ["Danglars felt a dreadful spasm dart through his heart.", "당글라르는 심장을 꿰뚫는 끔찍한 경련을 느꼈다."]], [["“But this is all I have left in the world,”", "\"하지만 이게 내가 세상에 가진 전부요.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said, “out of an immense fortune. If you deprive me of that, take away my life also.”", "\"막대한 재산 가운데 남은 거라곤 이게 다란 말이오. 이걸 빼앗는다면, 내 목숨도 같이 가져가시오.\""]], [["“We are forbidden to shed your blood.”", "\"당신의 피를 흘리게 하는 것은 금지되어 있소.\""]], [["“And by whom are you forbidden?”", "\"누가 그걸 금했단 말이오?\""]], [["“By him we obey.”", "\"우리가 따르는 분이오.\""]], [["“You do, then, obey someone?”", "\"그렇다면 당신도 누군가를 따른단 말이오?\""]], [["“Yes, a chief.”", "\"그렇소, 두목 말이오.\""]], [["“I thought you said you were the chief?”", "\"당신이 두목이라고 하지 않았소?\""]], [["“So I am of these men; but there is another over me.”", "\"이 사람들에게는 그렇소. 하지만 내 위에 또 한 분이 계시오.\""]], [["“And did your superior order you to treat me in this way?”", "\"그래, 당신 윗사람이 나를 이런 식으로 대하라고 명령한 거요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["“But my purse will be exhausted.”", "\"하지만 내 지갑은 바닥날 거요.\""]], [["“Probably.”", "\"아마 그렇겠지요.\""]], [["“Come,”", "\"이보시오.\""], ["said Danglars, “will you take a million?” “No.”", "당글라르가 말했다. \"100만 프랑이면 받겠소?\""]], [["“Two millions?—three?—four?", "\"안 되오.\" \"200만? 300만?"], ["Come, four?", "400만은 어떻소?"], ["I will give them to you on condition that you let me go.”", "자, 400만이오. 나를 풀어 주는 조건이라면 그걸 다 주겠소.\""]], [["“Why do you offer me 4,000,000 for what is worth 5,000,000?", "\"500만의 값어치가 있는 것에 어째서 400만을 내겠다는 거요?"], ["This is a kind of usury, banker, that I do not understand.”", "그건 일종의 고리대 아니오, 은행가 양반. 나로서는 도무지 이해할 수 없는 셈법이군.\""]], [["“Take all, then—take all, I tell you, and kill me!”", "\"그럼 다 가져가시오. 다 가져가라니까. 그리고 나를 죽이시오!\""]], [["“Come, come, calm yourself.", "\"자, 자, 진정하시오."], ["You will excite your blood, and that would produce an appetite it would require a million a day to satisfy.", "그렇게 흥분하면 피가 끓어올라 식욕이 솟을 텐데, 그걸 채우자면 하루에 100만씩은 들 거요."], ["Be more economical.”", "좀 더 아껴 쓰시오.\""]], [["“But when I have no more money left to pay you?”", "\"하지만 당신들에게 치를 돈이 한 푼도 남지 않으면?\""], ["asked the infuriated Danglars.", "격분한 당글라르가 물었다."]], [["“Then you must suffer hunger.”", "\"그땐 굶주림을 견뎌야지요.\""]], [["“Suffer hunger?”", "\"굶주림을 견디라고?\""], ["said Danglars, becoming pale.", "당글라르가 새파랗게 질려 말했다."]], [["“Most likely,”", "\"십중팔구 그렇소.\""], ["replied Vampa coolly.", "밤파가 차갑게 대꾸했다."]], [["“But you say you do not wish to kill me?” “No.”", "\"하지만 나를 죽이고 싶지는 않다고 하지 않았소?\""]], [["“And yet you will let me perish with hunger?”", "\"그렇소.\" \"그러면서도 나를 굶어 죽게 내버려 두겠다는 거요?\""]], [["“Ah, that is a different thing.”", "\"아, 그건 별개의 문제요.\""]], [["“Well, then, wretches,”", "\"좋소, 그렇다면, 비열한 놈들!\""], ["cried Danglars, “I will defy your infamous calculations—I would rather die at once!", "당글라르가 외쳤다. \"내 너희의 더러운 셈법에 맞서 주마. 차라리 단번에 죽고 말겠다!"], ["You may torture, torment, kill me, but you shall not have my signature again!”", "나를 괴롭히고 고문하고 죽여도 좋지만, 내 서명만은 두 번 다시 받지 못할 거다!\""]], [["“As your excellency pleases,”", "\"각하 좋으실 대로 하시지요.\""], ["said Vampa, as he left the cell.", "밤파는 이렇게 말하고 감방을 떠났다."]], [["Danglars, raving, threw himself on the goat-skin.", "당글라르는 미친 듯이 염소 가죽 위로 몸을 내던졌다."], ["Who could these men be?", "이자들은 대체 누구란 말인가?"], ["Who was the invisible chief?", "그 보이지 않는 두목은 누구인가?"], ["What could be his intentions towards him?", "자기에게 무슨 속셈을 품고 있는 것일까?"], ["And why, when everyone else was allowed to be ransomed, might he not also be?", "다른 사람들은 모두 몸값을 치르고 풀려나는데, 어째서 자기만은 안 된단 말인가?"], ["Oh, yes; certainly a speedy, violent death would be a fine means of deceiving these remorseless enemies, who appeared to pursue him with such incomprehensible vengeance.", "오, 그래, 분명 빠르고 격렬한 죽음이야말로 그 무자비한 적들을 따돌릴 좋은 방법일 것이다. 그들은 도무지 알 수 없는 원한을 품고 자기를 쫓는 듯 보였다."], ["But to die?", "그러나 죽는다고?"], ["For the first time in his life, Danglars contemplated death with a mixture of dread and desire; the time had come when the implacable spectre, which exists in the mind of every human creature, arrested his attention and called out with every pulsation of his heart, “Thou shalt die!”", "난생처음으로 당글라르는 두려움과 갈망이 뒤섞인 심정으로 죽음을 떠올렸다. 인간이라면 누구나 마음속에 품고 있는 그 가차 없는 망령이 그의 주의를 사로잡고, 심장이 고동칠 때마다 \"너는 죽으리라!\" 하고 외치는 때가 온 것이다."]], [["Danglars resembled a timid animal excited in the chase; first it flies, then despairs, and at last, by the very force of desperation, sometimes succeeds in eluding its pursuers.", "당글라르는 사냥에 쫓겨 흥분한 겁 많은 짐승과도 같았다. 처음엔 달아나고, 다음엔 절망하다가, 마침내 그 절망의 힘 자체로 때때로 추격자를 따돌리는 데 성공하는 그런 짐승 말이다."], ["Danglars meditated an escape; but the walls were solid rock, a man was sitting reading at the only outlet to the cell, and behind that man shapes armed with guns continually passed.", "당글라르는 탈출을 궁리했다. 그러나 벽은 단단한 바위였고, 감방의 단 하나뿐인 출구에는 한 사내가 앉아 책을 읽고 있었으며, 그 사내 뒤로는 총을 든 형체들이 끊임없이 오갔다."], ["His resolution not to sign lasted two days, after which he offered a million for some food.", "서명하지 않겠다는 결심은 이틀을 갔다. 그러고 나서 그는 먹을 것을 위해 100만 프랑을 내놓았다."], ["They sent him a magnificent supper, and took his million.", "그들은 훌륭한 만찬을 보내 주고 그의 100만 프랑을 가져갔다."]], [["From this time the prisoner resolved to suffer no longer, but to have everything he wanted.", "이때부터 죄수는 더는 견디지 않고 원하는 것은 무엇이든 가지기로 작정했다."], ["At the end of twelve days, after having made a splendid dinner, he reckoned his accounts, and found that he had only 50,000 francs left.", "열이틀이 지나, 거한 식사를 마치고 셈을 따져 보니 남은 돈이 5만 프랑뿐이었다. 그러자 이상한 반작용이 일어났다."], ["Then a strange reaction took place; he who had just abandoned 5,000,000 endeavored to save the 50,000 francs he had left, and sooner than give them up he resolved to enter again upon a life of privation—he was deluded by the hopefulness that is a premonition of madness.", "방금 500만 프랑을 포기한 자가 이번에는 남은 5만 프랑을 지키려고 안간힘을 썼다. 그것을 내놓느니 차라리 다시 궁핍한 생활로 돌아가기로 결심한 것이다. 그는 광기의 전조인 희망에 사로잡혀 있었다."]], [["He, who for so long a time had forgotten God, began to think that miracles were possible—that the accursed cavern might be discovered by the officers of the Papal States, who would release him; that then he would have 50,000 remaining, which would be sufficient to save him from starvation; and finally he prayed that this sum might be preserved to him, and as he prayed he wept.", "그토록 오랫동안 신을 잊고 살아온 그가 기적이 가능할지도 모른다고 생각하기 시작했다. 그 저주받은 동굴을 교황령● 관헌들이 발견해 자기를 풀어 줄지도 모른다고, 그러면 5만 프랑이 남아 굶어 죽는 것만은 면할 수 있으리라고 말이다. 그리하여 마침내 그는 그 돈만은 자기에게 남겨지기를 기도했고, 기도하면서 눈물을 흘렸다."], ["Three days passed thus, during which his prayers were frequent, if not heartfelt.", "그렇게 사흘이 흘렀고, 그동안 그의 기도는 진심에서 우러난 것은 아닐지언정 잦았다."], ["Sometimes he was delirious, and fancied he saw an old man stretched on a pallet; he, also, was dying of hunger.", "때로는 헛것을 보아, 한 노인이 초라한 침상에 누워 있는 환영을 보았다. 그 노인 역시 굶주림으로 죽어 가고 있었다."]], [["On the fourth, he was no longer a man, but a living corpse.", "나흘째에 그는 더 이상 사람이 아니라 살아 있는 송장이었다."], ["He had picked up every crumb that had been left from his former meals, and was beginning to eat the matting which covered the floor of his cell.", "지난 끼니에서 떨어진 부스러기란 부스러기는 죄다 주워 먹고는, 이제 감방 바닥을 덮은 거적까지 뜯어먹기 시작했다."], ["Then he entreated Peppino, as he would a guardian angel, to give him food; he offered him 1,000 francs for a mouthful of bread.", "그러고는 페피노에게 수호천사를 대하듯 애원하며 먹을 것을 달라고 했다. 빵 한 입에 1천 프랑을 내겠다고 했다."], ["But Peppino did not answer. On the fifth day he dragged himself to the door of the cell.", "그러나 페피노는 대답하지 않았다. 닷새째에 그는 감방 문 앞까지 몸을 끌고 갔다."]], [["“Are you not a Christian?”", "\"당신은 기독교인이 아니오?\""], ["he said, falling on his knees.", "그가 무릎을 꿇으며 말했다."], ["“Do you wish to assassinate a man who, in the eyes of Heaven, is a brother?", "\"하늘이 보기에 형제인 사람을 죽이려는 거요?"], ["Oh, my former friends, my former friends!”", "오, 나의 옛 친구들, 나의 옛 친구들이여!\""], ["he murmured, and fell with his face to the ground.", "그는 이렇게 중얼거리며 얼굴을 땅에 박고 쓰러졌다."], ["Then rising in despair, he exclaimed, “The chief, the chief!”", "그러다 절망에 차서 다시 몸을 일으키며 외쳤다. \"두목! 두목을 불러 주오!\""]], [["“Here I am,”", "\"여기 있소.\""], ["said Vampa, instantly appearing; “what do you want?”", "밤파가 곧장 나타나며 말했다. \"무엇을 원하시오?\""]], [["“Take my last gold,”", "\"내 마지막 금화를 가져가시오.\" 당글라르가 수첩을 내밀며 웅얼거렸다."], ["muttered Danglars, holding out his pocket-book, “and let me live here; I ask no more for liberty—I only ask to live!”", "\"그리고 나를 여기서 살게 해 주오. 자유를 달라는 게 아니오. 그저 살게만 해 달라는 거요!\""]], [["“Then you suffer a great deal?”", "\"그렇게나 괴롭소?\""]], [["“Oh, yes, yes, cruelly!”", "\"오, 그렇소, 그렇소, 끔찍하게 괴롭소!\""]], [["“Still, there have been men who suffered more than you.”", "\"하지만 당신보다 더 괴로워한 사람들도 있었소.\""]], [["“I do not think so.”", "\"그럴 리 없소.\""]], [["“Yes; those who have died of hunger.”", "\"있소. 굶어 죽은 사람들 말이오.\""]], [["Danglars thought of the old man whom, in his hours of delirium, he had seen groaning on his bed.", "당글라르는 헛것에 사로잡혀 있던 동안 침상에서 신음하던 모습을 보았던 그 노인을 떠올렸다."], ["He struck his forehead on the ground and groaned. “Yes,”", "그는 이마를 땅에 짓찧으며 신음했다. \"그렇소.\""], ["he said, “there have been some who have suffered more than I have, but then they must have been martyrs at least.”", "그가 말했다. \"나보다 더 괴로워한 사람들이 있었겠지. 하지만 그들은 적어도 순교자였을 거요.\""]], [["“Do you repent?”", "\"뉘우치는가?\""], ["asked a deep, solemn voice, which caused Danglars’ hair to stand on end.", "깊고 엄숙한 목소리가 물었다. 그 소리에 당글라르의 머리카락이 곤두섰다."], ["His feeble eyes endeavored to distinguish objects, and behind the bandit he saw a man enveloped in a cloak, half lost in the shadow of a stone column.", "그의 흐릿한 눈이 사물을 분간하려 애썼다. 산적 뒤편으로, 망토를 두르고 돌기둥 그림자에 반쯤 묻힌 한 사내가 보였다."]], [["“Of what must I repent?”", "\"무엇을 뉘우치란 말이오?\""], ["stammered Danglars.", "당글라르가 더듬거렸다."]], [["“Of the evil you have done,”", "\"네가 저지른 악행을 말이다.\""], ["said the voice.", "목소리가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, yes; oh, yes, I do indeed repent.”", "\"오, 그렇소. 오, 그렇소. 정말로 뉘우치고 있소.\""], ["And he struck his breast with his emaciated fist.", "그러고는 앙상한 주먹으로 제 가슴을 쳤다."]], [["“Then I forgive you,” said the man, dropping his cloak, and advancing to the light.", "\"그렇다면 너를 용서하노라.\" 사내가 망토를 떨치며 빛 속으로 걸어 나오며 말했다."]], [["“The Count of Monte Cristo!”", "\"몬테크리스토 백작!\""], ["said Danglars, more pale from terror than he had been just before from hunger and misery.", "당글라르가 말했다. 조금 전 굶주림과 비참함으로 질렸을 때보다 더 공포로 새파랗게 질려 있었다."]], [["“You are mistaken—I am not the Count of Monte Cristo.”", "\"잘못 보았다. 나는 몬테크리스토 백작이 아니다.\""]], [["“Then who are you?”", "\"그럼 누구요?\""]], [["50261m\n“I am he whom you sold and dishonored—I am he whose betrothed you prostituted—I am he upon whom you trampled that you might raise yourself to fortune—I am he whose father you condemned to die of hunger—I am he whom you also condemned to starvation, and who yet forgives you, because he hopes to be forgiven—I am Edmond Dantès!”", "\"나는 네가 팔아넘기고 모욕한 사람이다. 나는 네가 그 약혼녀를 욕보인 사람이다. 나는 네가 출세하려고 짓밟은 사람이다. 나는 네가 그 아버지를 굶겨 죽인 사람이다. 나는 네가 굶어 죽도록 단죄한 사람이며, 그러고도 너를 용서하는 사람이다. 나 또한 용서받기를 바라기 때문이다. 나는 에드몽 당테스다!\""]], [["Danglars uttered a cry, and fell prostrate.", "당글라르는 외마디 비명을 지르며 엎어졌다."]], [["“Rise,”", "\"일어나라.\""], ["said the count, “your life is safe; the same good fortune has not happened to your accomplices—one is mad, the other dead.", "백작이 말했다. \"네 목숨은 무사하다. 네 공범들에게는 그런 행운이 따르지 않았다."], ["Keep the 50,000 francs you have left—I give them to you.", "하나는 미쳤고, 하나는 죽었다. 남은 5만 프랑은 그대로 가져라."], ["The 5,000,000 you stole from the hospitals has been restored to them by an unknown hand.", "내가 너에게 준다. 네가 자선 병원에서 훔친 500만 프랑은 알 수 없는 손길에 의해 그들에게 되돌려졌다."], ["And now eat and drink; I will entertain you tonight.", "자, 이제 먹고 마셔라. 오늘 밤 내가 너를 대접하겠다."], ["Vampa, when this man is satisfied, let him be free.”", "밤파, 이 사람이 만족하거든 풀어 주게.\""]], [["Danglars remained prostrate while the count withdrew; when he raised his head he saw disappearing down the passage nothing but a shadow, before which the bandits bowed.", "당글라르는 백작이 물러갈 때까지 엎드린 채로 있었다. 고개를 들었을 때, 그는 복도 저편으로 사라지는 그림자 하나밖에 보지 못했고, 그 앞에서 산적들이 머리를 조아렸다."]], [["According to the count’s directions, Danglars was waited on by Vampa, who brought him the best wine and fruits of Italy; then, having conducted him to the road, and pointed to the post-chaise, left him leaning against a tree.", "백작의 지시에 따라 밤파가 당글라르의 시중을 들었다. 밤파는 이탈리아 최상급의 포도주와 과일을 가져다주었다. 그런 다음 그를 길까지 데려가 역마차를 가리켜 보이고는, 나무에 기대 선 그를 남겨 두고 떠났다."], ["He remained there all night, not knowing where he was.", "그는 자신이 어디에 있는지도 모른 채 밤새 그 자리에 머물렀다."], ["When daylight dawned he saw that he was near a stream; he was thirsty, and dragged himself towards it.", "동이 틀 무렵, 자기가 시냇가 가까이에 있음을 알아차렸다. 목이 말랐던 그는 시내를 향해 몸을 끌고 갔다."], ["As he stooped down to drink, he saw that his hair had become entirely white.", "물을 마시려고 몸을 굽히던 순간, 그는 자기 머리카락이 온통 하얗게 세어 버린 것을 보았다."]], [["# Chapter 117. The Fifth of October", "# Chapter 117. The Fifth of October"]], [["It was about six o’clock in the evening; an opal-colored light, through which an autumnal sun shed its golden rays, descended on the blue ocean.", "● 아르파공: 몰리에르의 희극 「수전노」에 나오는 지독한 구두쇠. —옮긴이 ● 교황령: 당시 로마를 중심으로 교황이 다스리던 영토. —옮긴이\n저녁 여섯 시쯤이었다."], ["The heat of the day had gradually decreased, and a light breeze arose, seeming like the respiration of nature on awakening from the burning siesta of the south.", "유백색 빛이 가을 햇살의 금빛 줄기를 머금은 채 푸른 바다 위로 내려앉았다. 한낮의 열기는 차츰 가라앉았고, 남쪽의 뜨거운 낮잠에서 깨어난 자연의 숨결처럼 가벼운 미풍이 일었다."], ["A delicious zephyr played along the coasts of the Mediterranean, and wafted from shore to shore the sweet perfume of plants, mingled with the fresh smell of the sea.", "감미로운 산들바람이 지중해 연안을 따라 노닐며, 신선한 바다 내음에 뒤섞인 풀잎의 달콤한 향기를 이 해안에서 저 해안으로 실어 날랐다."]], [["A light yacht, chaste and elegant in its form, was gliding amidst the first dews of night over the immense lake, extending from Gibraltar to the Dardanelles, and from Tunis to Venice.", "순결하고 우아한 자태의 가벼운 요트 한 척이, 지브롤터에서 다르다넬스 해협까지, 또 튀니스에서 베네치아까지 펼쳐진 이 광대한 호수 위로, 밤의 첫 이슬을 가르며 미끄러져 갔다."], ["The vessel resembled a swan with its wings opened towards the wind, gliding on the water.", "그 배는 바람을 향해 날개를 펼친 채 물 위를 미끄러지는 백조와도 같았다."], ["It advanced swiftly and gracefully, leaving behind it a glittering stretch of foam.", "배는 빠르고도 우아하게 나아가며, 뒤로 반짝이는 물거품의 자취를 길게 남겼다."], ["By degrees the sun disappeared behind the western horizon; but as though to prove the truth of the fanciful ideas in heathen mythology, its indiscreet rays reappeared on the summit of every wave, as if the god of fire had just sunk upon the bosom of Amphitrite, who in vain endeavored to hide her lover beneath her azure mantle.", "차츰 해가 서쪽 수평선 너머로 사라졌다. 그러나 이교 신화의 그 환상적인 상상이 진실임을 증명이라도 하려는 듯, 그 무람없는 햇살은 물결 하나하나의 꼭대기에 다시 떠올랐다. 마치 불의 신이 방금 암피트리테의 품속으로 가라앉았는데, 그녀가 푸른 망토 아래 연인을 숨기려 애썼지만 끝내 헛수고가 된 것처럼."]], [["The yacht moved rapidly on, though there did not appear to be sufficient wind to ruffle the curls on the head of a young girl.", "요트는 빠르게 나아갔다. 한 소녀의 머리카락 곱슬을 흩뜨릴 만한 바람조차 없어 보였는데도 말이다."], ["Standing on the prow was a tall man, of a dark complexion, who saw with dilating eyes that they were approaching a dark mass of land in the shape of a cone, which rose from the midst of the waves like the hat of a Catalan.", "뱃머리에는 거무스름한 안색의 키 큰 남자가 서 있었다. 그는 점점 커지는 두 눈으로, 카탈루냐 사람의 모자처럼 물결 한가운데서 솟아오른 원뿔 모양의 어두운 땅덩어리가 다가오는 것을 바라보았다."]], [["“Is that Monte Cristo?” asked the traveller, to whose orders the yacht was for the time submitted, in a melancholy voice.", "\"저것이 몬테크리스토섬인가?\" 그 순간 요트가 명령을 따르고 있던 여행자가 침울한 목소리로 물었다."]], [["“Yes, your excellency,”", "\"네, 각하.\""], ["said the captain, “we have reached it.”", "선장이 말했다. \"다 왔습니다.\""]], [["“We have reached it!”", "\"다 왔다고!\""], ["repeated the traveller in an accent of indescribable sadness.", "여행자가 형언할 수 없는 슬픔이 깃든 어조로 되뇌었다."]], [["Then he added, in a low tone, “Yes; that is the haven.”", "그러고는 낮은 목소리로 덧붙였다. \"그래, 저것이 안식처지.\""]], [["And then he again plunged into a train of thought, the character of which was better revealed by a sad smile, than it would have been by tears.", "그런 다음 그는 다시 생각에 잠겼다. 그 생각의 성격은 눈물보다도 슬픈 미소 하나로 더 잘 드러났다."], ["A few minutes afterwards a flash of light, which was extinguished instantly, was seen on the land, and the sound of firearms reached the yacht.", "몇 분 뒤, 섬에서 한 줄기 빛이 번쩍였다가 곧바로 꺼졌고, 총소리가 요트에까지 들려왔다."]], [["“Your excellency,”", "\"각하,\""], ["said the captain, “that was the land signal, will you answer yourself?”", "선장이 말했다. \"저것이 육지의 신호입니다. 직접 답하시겠습니까?\""]], [["“What signal?”", "\"무슨 신호 말인가?\""]], [["The captain pointed towards the island, up the side of which ascended a volume of smoke, increasing as it rose.", "선장이 섬을 가리켰다. 섬 비탈을 따라 한 줄기 연기가 피어올라, 오를수록 점점 짙어지고 있었다."]], [["“Ah, yes,” he said, as if awaking from a dream.", "\"아, 그래.\" 그가 꿈에서 깨어나듯 말했다."], ["“Give it to me.”", "\"그것을 이리 주게.\""]], [["The captain gave him a loaded carbine; the traveller slowly raised it, and fired in the air.", "선장이 장전된 소총을 건넸다. 여행자는 천천히 그것을 들어 올려 허공에 한 발 쏘았다."], ["Ten minutes afterwards, the sails were furled, and they cast anchor about a hundred fathoms from the little harbor. The gig was already lowered, and in it were four oarsmen and a coxswain.", "십 분 뒤 돛이 거두어졌고, 작은 항구에서 백 길쯤 떨어진 곳에 닻을 내렸다. 거룻배는 이미 내려져 있었고, 그 안에는 네 명의 노잡이와 한 명의 키잡이가 타고 있었다."], ["The traveller descended, and instead of sitting down at the stern of the boat, which had been decorated with a blue carpet for his accommodation, stood up with his arms crossed.", "여행자가 내려왔다. 그러나 그는 자신을 위해 푸른 양탄자가 깔린 고물 자리에 앉는 대신, 두 팔을 가슴에 엇갈리게 낀 채 그대로 서 있었다."], ["The rowers waited, their oars half lifted out of the water, like birds drying their wings. 50265m", "노잡이들은 새가 날개를 말리듯 노를 물 밖으로 반쯤 들어 올린 채 기다렸다."]], [["“Give way,”", "\"저어라.\""], ["said the traveller.", "여행자가 말했다."], ["The eight oars fell into the sea simultaneously without splashing a drop of water, and the boat, yielding to the impulsion, glided forward.", "여덟 개의 노가 물 한 방울 튀기지 않고 동시에 바다에 잠겼고, 배는 그 추진력에 실려 앞으로 미끄러져 나갔다."], ["In an instant they found themselves in a little harbor, formed in a natural creek; the boat grounded on the fine sand.", "순식간에 그들은 자연이 만든 후미에 형성된 작은 항구에 닿았다. 배는 고운 모래 위에 얹혔다."]], [["“Will your excellency be so good as to mount the shoulders of two of our men, they will carry you ashore?”", "\"각하, 송구하오나 저희 사람 둘의 어깨에 올라타 주시겠습니까? 뭍까지 모시겠습니다.\""], ["The young man answered this invitation with a gesture of indifference, and stepped out of the boat; the sea immediately rose to his waist.", "젊은이는 이 권유에 무심한 몸짓으로 답하더니, 배에서 그대로 내려섰다. 바닷물이 곧바로 그의 허리께까지 차올랐다."]], [["“Ah, your excellency,”", "\"아, 각하.\""], ["murmured the pilot, “you should not have done so; our master will scold us for it.”", "키잡이가 중얼거렸다. \"그러시면 안 되는데요. 주인님께 저희가 꾸중을 듣겠습니다.\""]], [["The young man continued to advance, following the sailors, who chose a firm footing.", "젊은이는 단단한 발판을 골라 디디는 선원들을 따라 계속 앞으로 나아갔다."], ["Thirty strides brought them to dry land; the young man stamped on the ground to shake off the wet, and looked around for someone to show him his road, for it was quite dark.", "서른 걸음을 떼자 마른 땅에 닿았다. 젊은이는 물기를 털어 내려고 발을 굴렀고, 길을 일러 줄 사람을 찾아 주위를 둘러보았다. 사방이 캄캄했기 때문이다."], ["Just as he turned, a hand rested on his shoulder, and a voice which made him shudder exclaimed:", "그가 막 몸을 돌리는 순간, 한 손이 그의 어깨에 얹혔고, 그를 몸서리치게 하는 목소리가 외쳤다."]], [["“Good-evening, Maximilian; you are punctual, thank you!”", "\"잘 왔네, 막시밀리앙. 시간을 정확히 지켜 주었군, 고맙네!\""]], [["“Ah, is it you, count?”", "\"아, 백작님이십니까?\""], ["said the young man, in an almost joyful accent, pressing Monte Cristo’s hand with both his own.", "젊은이가 거의 환희에 찬 어조로 말하며, 두 손으로 몬테크리스토의 손을 꽉 잡았다."]], [["“Yes; you see I am as exact as you are.", "\"그래. 보다시피 나도 자네만큼 정확하지."], ["But you are dripping, my dear fellow; you must change your clothes, as Calypso said to Telemachus.", "그런데 흠뻑 젖었군, 이 사람아. 옷을 갈아입어야겠어. 칼립소가 텔레마코스에게 말했듯이 말일세."], ["Come, I have a habitation prepared for you in which you will soon forget fatigue and cold.”", "자, 자네를 위해 거처를 마련해 두었네. 거기서 곧 피곤도 추위도 잊게 될 걸세.\""]], [["Monte Cristo perceived that the young man had turned around; indeed, Morrel saw with surprise that the men who had brought him had left without being paid, or uttering a word.", "몬테크리스토는 젊은이가 몸을 돌리는 것을 알아챘다. 과연 모렐은 자신을 데려다준 사람들이 삯도 받지 않고, 한마디 말도 없이 떠나 버린 것을 보고 놀랐다."], ["Already the sound of their oars might be heard as they returned to the yacht.", "벌써 그들이 요트로 돌아가며 젓는 노 소리가 들려왔다."]], [["“Oh, yes,”", "\"아, 그래.\""], ["said the count, “you are looking for the sailors.”", "백작이 말했다. \"선원들을 찾고 있군.\""]], [["“Yes, I paid them nothing, and yet they are gone.”", "\"네. 제가 삯을 한 푼도 치르지 않았는데, 그냥 가 버렸군요.\""]], [["“Never mind that, Maximilian,”", "\"그건 마음 쓰지 말게, 막시밀리앙.\""], ["said Monte Cristo, smiling.", "몬테크리스토가 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“I have made an agreement with the navy, that the access to my island shall be free of all charge.", "\"내가 해군과 약조를 맺어 두었네. 내 섬을 드나드는 데는 어떤 삯도 받지 않기로 말일세."], ["I have made a bargain.”", "그런 거래를 해 둔 거지.\""]], [["Morrel looked at the count with surprise.", "모렐이 놀란 눈으로 백작을 바라보았다."], ["“Count,”", "\"백작님.\""], ["he said, “you are not the same here as in Paris.”", "그가 말했다. \"여기서는 파리에서와 같은 분이 아니시군요.\""]], [["“How so?”", "\"어째서?\""]], [["“Here you laugh.” The count’s brow became clouded.", "\"여기서는 웃으시니까요.\" 백작의 이마가 흐려졌다."]], [["“You are right to recall me to myself, Maximilian,”", "\"내 본분을 일깨워 주어 고맙네, 막시밀리앙.\""], ["he said; “I was delighted to see you again, and forgot for the moment that all happiness is fleeting.”", "그가 말했다. \"자네를 다시 만나 기뻐서, 모든 행복이 덧없다는 사실을 잠시 잊었던 모양일세.\""]], [["“Oh, no, no, count,”", "\"아, 아닙니다, 백작님.\""], ["cried Maximilian, seizing the count’s hands, “pray laugh; be happy, and prove to me, by your indifference, that life is endurable to sufferers.", "막시밀리앙이 백작의 두 손을 붙잡으며 외쳤다. \"부디 웃으십시오. 행복해하시고, 그 무심함으로 제게 증명해 주십시오."], ["Oh, how charitable, kind, and good you are; you affect this gayety to inspire me with courage.”", "고통받는 자에게도 삶이 견딜 만한 것임을 말입니다. 아, 백작님은 얼마나 인정 많고 친절하고 선하신 분인지요. 제게 용기를 북돋아 주려고 일부러 그 명랑함을 꾸며 보이시는 거지요.\""]], [["“You are wrong, Morrel; I was really happy.”", "\"틀렸네, 모렐. 나는 정말로 행복했어.\""]], [["“Then you forget me, so much the better.”", "\"그렇다면 저를 잊으신 거군요. 오히려 잘된 일입니다.\""]], [["“How so?”", "\"어째서?\""]], [["“Yes; for as the gladiator said to the emperor, when he entered the arena, ‘He who is about to die salutes you.’”", "\"네. 검투사가 원형 경기장에 들어서며 황제에게 했던 말처럼요. '이제 죽으러 가는 자, 당신께 경례를 올립니다.'\""]], [["“Then you are not consoled?” asked the count, surprised.", "\"그러면 자네는 마음을 추스르지 못했단 말인가?\" 백작이 놀라 물었다."]], [["“Oh,”", "\"아.\""], ["exclaimed Morrel, with a glance full of bitter reproach, “do you think it possible that I could be?”", "모렐이 쓰디쓴 원망이 가득한 눈길로 외쳤다. \"제가 그럴 수 있으리라 생각하셨습니까?\""]], [["“Listen,” said the count.", "\"내 말을 들어 보게.\""], ["“Do you understand the meaning of my words?", "백작이 말했다. \"자네는 내 말뜻을 이해하나?"], ["You cannot take me for a commonplace man, a mere rattle, emitting a vague and senseless noise.", "자네는 나를 평범한 사람으로, 막연하고 의미 없는 소리나 떠들어 대는 한낱 수다쟁이로 여기지는 않겠지."], ["When I ask you if you are consoled, I speak to you as a man for whom the human heart has no secrets.", "내가 자네에게 마음을 추슬렀느냐고 물을 때, 나는 인간의 마음에 아무런 비밀도 없는 사람으로서 자네에게 말하는 걸세."], ["Well, Morrel, let us both examine the depths of your heart.", "자, 모렐, 우리 함께 자네 마음의 밑바닥을 들여다보세."], ["Do you still feel the same feverish impatience of grief which made you start like a wounded lion?", "자네는 아직도 상처 입은 사자처럼 펄쩍 뛰게 만들던 그 열병 같은 슬픔의 조바심을 느끼나?"], ["Have you still that devouring thirst which can only be appeased in the grave?", "오직 무덤에서만 가실 수 있는 그 집어삼킬 듯한 갈증이 아직도 남아 있나?"], ["Are you still actuated by the regret which drags the living to the pursuit of death; or are you only suffering from the prostration of fatigue and the weariness of hope deferred?", "산 자를 죽음의 추구로 끌어당기는 그 회한에 아직도 휘둘리고 있나? 아니면 그저 피로에 지치고, 미뤄진 희망에 지쳐 무너져 내린 것뿐인가?"], ["Has the loss of memory rendered it impossible for you to weep?", "기억을 잃은 탓에 더는 울 수조차 없게 된 건가?"], ["Oh, my dear friend, if this be the case,—if you can no longer weep, if your frozen heart be dead, if you put all your trust in God, then, Maximilian, you are consoled—do not complain.”", "아, 친구여, 만약 그렇다면, 자네가 더는 울 수 없다면, 자네의 얼어붙은 마음이 죽어 버렸다면, 자네의 모든 믿음을 신께 맡겼다면, 그렇다면 막시밀리앙, 자네는 마음을 추스른 걸세. 그러니 한탄하지 말게.\""]], [["“Count,”", "\"백작님.\""], ["said Morrel, in a firm and at the same time soft voice, “listen to me, as to a man whose thoughts are raised to heaven, though he remains on earth; I come to die in the arms of a friend.", "모렐이 단호하면서도 부드러운 목소리로 말했다. \"비록 땅에 발붙이고 있으나 그 생각만은 하늘로 향한 사람을 대하듯 제 말을 들어 주십시오. 저는 친구의 품에서 죽으러 왔습니다."], ["Certainly, there are people whom I love.", "물론 제게도 사랑하는 사람들이 있습니다."], ["I love my sister Julie,—I love her husband Emmanuel; but I require a strong mind to smile on my last moments.", "누이 쥘리를 사랑하고, 그 남편 에마뉘엘을 사랑합니다. 하지만 제 마지막 순간에 미소 지어 줄 강인한 사람이 필요합니다."], ["My sister would be bathed in tears and fainting; I could not bear to see her suffer.", "누이는 눈물에 젖어 정신을 잃을 테고, 저는 그녀가 괴로워하는 모습을 차마 견딜 수 없습니다."], ["Emmanuel would tear the weapon from my hand, and alarm the house with his cries.", "에마뉘엘은 제 손에서 무기를 빼앗고, 비명을 질러 온 집안을 발칵 뒤집어 놓겠지요."], ["You, count, who are more than mortal, will, I am sure, lead me to death by a pleasant path, will you not?” 50267m", "하지만 백작님, 인간 이상의 존재이신 당신께서는 분명 저를 편안한 길로 인도해 죽음에 이르게 해 주시겠지요, 그렇지 않습니까?\""]], [["“My friend,”", "\"친구여.\""], ["said the count, “I have still one doubt,—are you weak enough to pride yourself upon your sufferings?”", "백작이 말했다. \"내게는 아직 한 가지 의심이 남아 있네. 자네는 자신의 고통을 자랑스러워할 만큼 나약한 사람인가?\""]], [["“No, indeed,—I am calm,”", "\"아닙니다, 정말로요."], ["said Morrel, giving his hand to the count; “my pulse does not beat slower or faster than usual.", "저는 평온합니다.\" 모렐이 백작에게 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"제 맥박은 평소보다 느리지도 빠르지도 않습니다."], ["No, I feel that I have reached the goal, and I will go no farther.", "그렇습니다. 저는 종착점에 다다랐음을 느끼고, 더는 나아가지 않을 겁니다."], ["You told me to wait and hope; do you know what you did, unfortunate adviser?", "백작님은 제게 기다리고 희망하라 하셨지요. 가엾은 충고자여, 당신이 무슨 짓을 하신 줄이나 아십니까?"], ["I waited a month, or rather I suffered for a month!", "저는 한 달을 기다렸습니다. 아니, 한 달을 고통받았다고 해야겠지요!"], ["I did hope (man is a poor wretched creature), I did hope.", "저는 정말로 희망했습니다. 인간이란 가련하고 비참한 존재이니까요. 정말로 희망했습니다."], ["What I cannot tell,—something wonderful, an absurdity, a miracle,—of what nature he alone can tell who has mingled with our reason that folly we call hope.", "무엇을 희망했는지는 저도 말할 수 없습니다. 무언가 경이로운 것, 터무니없는 것, 기적 같은 것을요. 그것이 어떤 성질의 것인지는, 우리의 이성에 희망이라 부르는 그 어리석음을 섞어 놓은 분만이 아실 겁니다."], ["Yes, I did wait—yes, I did hope, count, and during this quarter of an hour we have been talking together, you have unconsciously wounded, tortured my heart, for every word you have uttered proved that there was no hope for me.", "그렇습니다, 저는 기다렸고, 희망했습니다, 백작님. 그리고 우리가 이렇게 함께 이야기를 나눈 십오 분 동안, 당신은 자신도 모르게 제 마음을 상하게 하고 고문하셨습니다. 당신이 내뱉으신 모든 말이, 제게는 아무 희망도 없다는 것을 증명했으니까요."], ["Oh, count, I shall sleep calmly, deliciously in the arms of death.”", "아, 백작님, 저는 죽음의 품속에서 평온하게, 달콤하게 잠들겠습니다.\""]], [["Morrel uttered these words with an energy which made the count shudder.", "모렐이 이 말을 어찌나 힘주어 내뱉었던지, 백작이 몸서리쳤다."]], [["“My friend,”", "\"친구여.\""], ["continued Morrel, “you named the fifth of October as the end of the period of waiting,—today is the fifth of October,”", "모렐이 말을 이었다. \"백작님은 십월 오일을 기다림의 끝으로 정해 주셨지요. 오늘이 바로 십월 오일입니다.\""], ["he took out his watch, “it is now nine o’clock,—I have yet three hours to live.”", "그가 시계를 꺼냈다. \"지금이 아홉 시군요. 제게는 아직 살 시간이 세 시간 남았습니다.\""]], [["“Be it so,” said the count, “come.”", "\"그렇다면 좋네.\" 백작이 말했다."], ["Morrel mechanically followed the count, and they had entered the grotto before he perceived it.", "\"이리 오게.\" 모렐은 기계적으로 백작을 따라갔고, 미처 알아채기도 전에 동굴 안으로 들어와 있었다."], ["He felt a carpet under his feet, a door opened, perfumes surrounded him, and a brilliant light dazzled his eyes.", "발밑에 양탄자가 밟혔고, 문 하나가 열렸으며, 향기가 그를 에워쌌고, 눈부신 빛이 그의 눈을 어지럽혔다."], ["Morrel hesitated to advance; he dreaded the enervating effect of all that he saw.", "모렐은 선뜻 앞으로 나아가기를 망설였다. 눈앞의 모든 것이 자신의 의지를 무르게 만들까 봐 두려웠던 것이다."], ["Monte Cristo drew him in gently.", "몬테크리스토가 부드럽게 그를 안으로 이끌었다."]], [["“Why should we not spend the last three hours remaining to us of life, like those ancient Romans, who when condemned by Nero, their emperor and heir, sat down at a table covered with flowers, and gently glided into death, amid the perfume of heliotropes and roses?”", "\"우리에게 남은 생의 마지막 세 시간을, 저 옛 로마인들처럼 보내지 않을 이유가 어디 있겠나? 황제이자 상속자인 네로에게 사형을 선고받았을 때, 그들은 꽃으로 뒤덮인 식탁에 앉아 헬리오트로프와 장미의 향기 속에서 고요히 죽음 속으로 미끄러져 들어갔다네.\""]], [["Morrel smiled.", "모렐이 미소 지었다."], ["“As you please,”", "\"원하시는 대로 하지요.\""], ["he said; “death is always death,—that is forgetfulness, repose, exclusion from life, and therefore from grief.”", "그가 말했다. \"죽음은 어차피 죽음이니까요. 그것은 망각이고, 안식이며, 삶으로부터의, 따라서 슬픔으로부터의 격리입니다.\""]], [["He sat down, and Monte Cristo placed himself opposite to him.", "그가 자리에 앉았고, 몬테크리스토는 그 맞은편에 앉았다."], ["They were in the marvellous dining-room before described, where the statues had baskets on their heads always filled with fruits and flowers.", "그들은 앞서 묘사된 그 경이로운 식당 안에 있었다. 조각상들이 머리에 인 바구니에는 언제나 과일과 꽃이 가득했다."], ["Morrel had looked carelessly around, and had probably noticed nothing.", "모렐은 무심히 주위를 둘러보았으나, 아마 아무것도 눈에 들어오지 않았을 것이다."]], [["“Let us talk like men,” he said, looking at the count.", "\"사내답게 이야기를 나누어 보지요.\" 그가 백작을 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Go on!”", "\"말해 보게!\""]], [["“Count,”", "\"백작님.\""], ["said Morrel, “you are the epitome of all human knowledge, and you seem like a being descended from a wiser and more advanced world than ours.”", "모렐이 말했다. \"당신은 인간이 가진 모든 지식의 정수이시며, 우리보다 더 지혜롭고 더 진보한 세계에서 내려오신 존재 같습니다.\""]], [["“There is something true in what you say,”", "\"자네 말에는 진실이 담겨 있네.\""], ["said the count, with that smile which made him so handsome; “I have descended from a planet called grief.”", "백작이 그를 그토록 잘생겨 보이게 하는 그 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"나는 슬픔이라는 별에서 내려왔지.\""]], [["50269m\n“I believe all you tell me without questioning its meaning; for instance, you told me to live, and I did live; you told me to hope, and I almost did so.", "\"저는 당신이 하시는 모든 말씀을 그 뜻을 따지지 않고 믿습니다. 이를테면 당신은 제게 살라고 하셨고, 저는 살았습니다. 희망하라 하셨고, 저는 거의 희망할 뻔했지요."], ["I am almost inclined to ask you, as though you had experienced death, ‘is it painful to die?’”", "마치 당신이 죽음을 겪어 보신 분인 양, 이렇게 여쭙고 싶을 정도입니다. '죽는다는 것은 고통스럽습니까?'\""]], [["Monte Cristo looked upon Morrel with indescribable tenderness. “Yes,”", "몬테크리스토는 형언할 수 없는 다정함으로 모렐을 바라보았다. \"그렇다네.\""], ["he said, “yes, doubtless it is painful, if you violently break the outer covering which obstinately begs for life.", "그가 말했다. \"그래, 분명 고통스럽지. 끈질기게 삶을 구걸하는 그 바깥 껍질을 거칠게 찢어 낸다면 말일세."], ["If you plunge a dagger into your flesh, if you insinuate a bullet into your brain, which the least shock disorders,—then certainly, you will suffer pain, and you will repent quitting a life for a repose you have bought at so dear a price.”", "단검을 자네 살에 찔러 넣거나, 작은 충격에도 흐트러지는 자네의 뇌에 총알을 박아 넣는다면, 그렇다면 자네는 분명 고통을 느낄 테고, 그토록 비싼 값을 치르고 사들인 안식을 위해 삶을 버린 것을 후회할 걸세.\""]], [["“Yes; I know that there is a secret of luxury and pain in death, as well as in life; the only thing is to understand it.”", "\"네. 저도 삶에서와 마찬가지로 죽음에도 호사와 고통의 비밀이 있음을 압니다. 다만 그것을 이해하는 것이 관건이지요.\""]], [["“You have spoken truly, Maximilian; according to the care we bestow upon it, death is either a friend who rocks us gently as a nurse, or an enemy who violently drags the soul from the body.", "\"옳은 말일세, 막시밀리앙. 우리가 죽음에 얼마나 정성을 들이느냐에 따라, 죽음은 유모처럼 우리를 부드럽게 흔들어 재우는 친구가 되기도 하고, 영혼을 육신에서 거칠게 잡아 뜯는 적이 되기도 하지."], ["Some day, when the world is much older, and when mankind will be masters of all the destructive powers in nature, to serve for the general good of humanity; when mankind, as you were just saying, have discovered the secrets of death, then that death will become as sweet and voluptuous as a slumber in the arms of your beloved.”", "언젠가 세상이 훨씬 더 나이 들고, 인류가 자연의 모든 파괴적인 힘을 다스려 인류 전체의 이로움에 쓰게 될 날이 오면, 그리고 인류가 방금 자네가 말했듯 죽음의 비밀을 알아내게 되면, 그때 죽음은 사랑하는 이의 품에서 잠드는 것처럼 달콤하고 황홀한 것이 될 걸세.\""]], [["“And if you wished to die, you would choose this death, count?” “Yes.”", "\"그래서 만약 죽기를 바라신다면, 백작님은 이런 죽음을 택하시겠습니까?\""]], [["Morrel extended his hand.", "\"그렇다네.\" 모렐이 손을 내밀었다."], ["“Now I understand,”", "\"이제 알겠습니다.\""], ["he said, “why you had me brought here to this desolate spot, in the midst of the ocean, to this subterranean palace; it was because you loved me, was it not, count?", "그가 말했다. \"왜 저를 이곳, 망망대해 한가운데의 황량한 곳, 이 지하 궁전으로 데려오셨는지요. 저를 사랑하셨기 때문이지요, 그렇지 않습니까, 백작님?"], ["It was because you loved me well enough to give me one of those sweet means of death of which we were speaking; a death without agony, a death which allows me to fade away while pronouncing Valentine’s name and pressing your hand.”", "우리가 이야기 나누던 그 달콤한 죽음의 방법 가운데 하나를 제게 베풀어 주실 만큼 저를 깊이 사랑하셨기 때문이지요. 고통 없는 죽음, 발랑틴의 이름을 부르며 당신의 손을 잡은 채 스러져 갈 수 있는 죽음을요.\""]], [["“Yes, you have guessed rightly, Morrel,”", "\"그래, 제대로 알아맞혔네, 모렐.\""], ["said the count, “that is what I intended.”", "백작이 말했다. \"그것이 내 뜻이었지.\""]], [["“Thanks; the idea that tomorrow I shall no longer suffer, is sweet to my heart.”", "\"감사합니다. 내일이면 더는 고통받지 않으리라는 생각이, 제 마음에 달콤하게 와닿습니다.\""]], [["“Do you then regret nothing?”", "\"그러면 자네는 아무것도 미련이 없나?\""]], [["“No,” replied Morrel.", "\"없습니다.\" 모렐이 대답했다."]], [["“Not even me?”", "\"나조차도?\""], ["asked the count with deep emotion.", "백작이 깊은 감동에 잠겨 물었다."], ["Morrel’s clear eye was for the moment clouded, then it shone with unusual lustre, and a large tear rolled down his cheek.", "모렐의 맑은 눈이 한순간 흐려지더니, 이내 예사롭지 않은 광채로 빛났고, 굵은 눈물 한 방울이 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸다."]], [["“What,”", "\"뭐라고?\""], ["said the count, “do you still regret anything in the world, and yet die?”", "백작이 말했다. \"자네는 아직 이 세상에 미련이 남았는데도, 죽으려는 건가?\""]], [["“Oh, I entreat you,”", "\"아, 제발 부탁입니다.\""], ["exclaimed Morrel in a low voice, “do not speak another word, count; do not prolong my punishment.”", "모렐이 낮은 목소리로 외쳤다. \"더는 한마디도 마십시오, 백작님. 제 형벌을 길게 끌지 말아 주십시오.\""]], [["The count fancied that he was yielding, and this belief revived the horrible doubt that had overwhelmed him at the Château d’If.", "백작은 그가 마음을 돌리려 한다고 생각했고, 그 믿음은 이프성에서 자신을 짓눌렀던 그 끔찍한 의심을 되살려 놓았다."]], [["“I am endeavoring,”", "'나는 이 사람을 행복하게 해 주려 애쓰고 있다.'"], ["he thought, “to make this man happy; I look upon this restitution as a weight thrown into the scale to balance the evil I have wrought.", "그가 생각했다. '나는 이 보상을, 내가 저지른 악행과 균형을 맞추기 위해 저울에 던져 넣는 추로 여긴다."], ["Now, supposing I am deceived, supposing this man has not been unhappy enough to merit happiness.", "그런데 만약 내가 속고 있는 거라면, 만약 이 사람이 행복을 누릴 자격이 있을 만큼 불행하지는 않았던 거라면?"], ["Alas, what would become of me who can only atone for evil by doing good?”", "아아, 오직 선행으로만 악을 속죄할 수 있는 나는 대체 어찌 되는 것인가?'"]], [["50271m\nThen he said aloud: “Listen, Morrel, I see your grief is great, but still you do not like to risk your soul.”", "그러고는 소리 내어 말했다. \"들어 보게, 모렐. 자네의 슬픔이 크다는 건 알겠네."], ["Morrel smiled sadly.", "하지만 그래도 자네는 자네의 영혼을 거는 것은 내키지 않겠지.\" 모렐이 슬프게 미소 지었다."]], [["“Count,”", "\"백작님.\""], ["he said, “I swear to you my soul is no longer my own.”", "그가 말했다. \"맹세컨대 제 영혼은 더 이상 제 것이 아닙니다.\""]], [["“Maximilian, you know I have no relation in the world.", "\"막시밀리앙, 알다시피 나는 이 세상에 혈육이 하나도 없네."], ["I have accustomed myself to regard you as my son: well, then, to save my son, I will sacrifice my life, nay, even my fortune.”", "나는 자네를 내 아들로 여기는 데 익숙해졌어. 그러니, 내 아들을 구하기 위해서라면 나는 내 목숨을, 아니 내 재산까지도 바치겠네.\""]], [["“What do you mean?”", "\"무슨 말씀이십니까?\""]], [["“I mean, that you wish to quit life because you do not understand all the enjoyments which are the fruits of a large fortune.", "\"내 말은, 자네가 삶을 버리려는 까닭이 큰 재산이 가져다주는 온갖 즐거움을 다 헤아리지 못하기 때문이라는 걸세."], ["Morrel, I possess nearly a hundred millions and I give them to you; with such a fortune you can attain every wish.", "모렐, 나는 거의 일억에 가까운 돈을 가지고 있고, 그것을 자네에게 주겠네. 그만한 재산이 있으면 자네는 어떤 소망이든 이룰 수 있어."], ["Are you ambitious?", "자네는 야심이 있나?"], ["Every career is open to you.", "어떤 길이든 자네 앞에 열려 있네."], ["Overturn the world, change its character, yield to mad ideas, be even criminal—but live.”", "세상을 뒤엎고, 그 성질을 바꾸고, 미친 생각에 몸을 맡기고, 심지어 죄인이 되어도 좋아. 다만 살게.\""]], [["“Count, I have your word,”", "\"백작님, 저는 당신의 약속을 받았습니다.\""], ["said Morrel coldly; then taking out his watch, he added, “It is half-past eleven.”", "모렐이 차갑게 말했다. 그러고는 시계를 꺼내 덧붙였다. \"열한 시 반입니다.\""]], [["“Morrel, can you intend it in my house, under my very eyes?”", "\"모렐, 정녕 내 집에서, 바로 내 눈앞에서 그럴 작정인가?\""]], [["“Then let me go,”", "\"그러면 저를 보내 주십시오.\""], ["said Maximilian, “or I shall think you did not love me for my own sake, but for yours;”", "막시밀리앙이 말했다. \"그렇지 않으면 저는 당신이 저를 저 자신을 위해서가 아니라 당신 자신을 위해 아끼셨다고 여길 겁니다.\""], ["and he arose.", "그러고는 자리에서 일어났다."]], [["“It is well,”", "\"좋네.\""], ["said Monte Cristo whose countenance brightened at these words; “you wish it—you are inflexible.", "몬테크리스토가 말했다. 그 말에 그의 얼굴이 환해졌다. \"자네가 정녕 원하는 거라면, 자네는 한 치도 굽히지 않는군."], ["Yes, as you said, you are indeed wretched and a miracle alone can cure you.", "그래, 자네 말대로 자네는 정말로 비참하고, 오직 기적만이 자네를 고칠 수 있겠지."], ["Sit down, Morrel, and wait.”", "앉게, 모렐. 그리고 기다리게.\""]], [["Morrel obeyed; the count arose, and unlocking a closet with a key suspended from his gold chain, took from it a little silver casket, beautifully carved and chased, the corners of which represented four bending figures, similar to the Caryatides, the forms of women, symbols of the angels aspiring to heaven.", "모렐이 그 말에 따랐다. 백작이 일어나, 금줄에 매달린 열쇠로 벽장을 열고, 그 안에서 아름답게 깎고 새긴 작은 은상자 하나를 꺼냈다. 상자의 네 귀퉁이에는, 카리아티데스와 비슷하게 몸을 굽힌 네 개의 형상이 새겨져 있었는데, 그것은 여인의 형상으로, 하늘로 오르기를 갈망하는 천사들을 상징하는 것이었다."]], [["He placed the casket on the table; then opening it took out a little golden box, the top of which flew open when touched by a secret spring.", "그는 상자를 식탁 위에 놓았다. 그런 다음 그것을 열어 작은 황금 갑을 꺼냈는데, 숨겨진 용수철을 건드리자 뚜껑이 탁 열렸다."], ["This box contained an unctuous substance partly solid, of which it was impossible to discover the color, owing to the reflection of the polished gold, sapphires, rubies, emeralds, which ornamented the box. It was a mixed mass of blue, red, and gold.", "그 갑 안에는 일부는 굳어 있는 기름진 물질이 들어 있었는데, 갑을 장식한 윤이 나는 황금과 사파이어, 루비, 에메랄드의 반사 때문에 그 색을 가늠하기란 불가능했다. 그것은 푸른빛과 붉은빛, 금빛이 뒤섞인 덩어리였다."]], [["The count took out a small quantity of this with a gilt spoon, and offered it to Morrel, fixing a long steadfast glance upon him.", "백작이 금빛 숟가락으로 그 물질을 조금 떠서 모렐에게 내밀며, 그에게 길고 흔들림 없는 시선을 고정했다."], ["It was then observable that the substance was greenish.", "그제야 그 물질이 푸르스름한 빛을 띠고 있다는 것이 드러났다."]], [["“This is what you asked for,”", "\"이것이 자네가 청한 것이고,\""], ["he said, “and what I promised to give you.”", "그가 말했다. \"내가 주기로 약속한 것일세.\""]], [["“I thank you from the depths of my heart,”", "\"진심으로 감사드립니다.\""], ["said the young man, taking the spoon from the hands of Monte Cristo.", "젊은이가 몬테크리스토의 손에서 숟가락을 받아 들며 말했다."], ["The count took another spoon, and again dipped it into the golden box.", "백작이 또 하나의 숟가락을 집어 다시 황금 갑에 담갔다."], ["“What are you going to do, my friend?”", "\"무엇을 하시려는 겁니까, 친구여?\""], ["asked Morrel, arresting his hand.", "모렐이 그의 손을 붙잡으며 물었다."]], [["“Well, the fact is, Morrel, I was thinking that I too am weary of life, and since an opportunity presents itself——”", "\"실은 말일세, 모렐, 나도 삶에 지쳤다는 생각이 들었거든. 마침 이런 기회가 왔으니……\""]], [["“Stay!” said the young man.", "\"멈추십시오!\" 젊은이가 말했다."], ["“You who love, and are beloved; you, who have faith and hope,—oh, do not follow my example.", "\"사랑하고, 또 사랑받으시는 분, 믿음과 희망을 지니신 분께서, 아, 부디 제 본보기를 따르지 마십시오."], ["In your case it would be a crime.", "당신의 경우라면 그것은 죄악입니다."], ["Adieu, my noble and generous friend, adieu; I will go and tell Valentine what you have done for me.”", "안녕히 계십시오, 고결하고 너그러우신 친구여, 안녕히. 저는 가서 발랑틴에게 당신이 저를 위해 무엇을 해 주셨는지 전하겠습니다.\""]], [["And slowly, though without any hesitation, only waiting to press the count’s hand fervently, he swallowed the mysterious substance offered by Monte Cristo. Then they were both silent.", "그러고는 천천히, 그러나 조금의 망설임도 없이, 다만 백작의 손을 뜨겁게 한 번 잡으려 멈췄을 뿐, 그는 몬테크리스토가 내민 그 신비로운 물질을 삼켰다. 그러자 두 사람은 모두 침묵에 잠겼다."], ["Ali, mute and attentive, brought the pipes and coffee, and disappeared.", "말없이 빈틈없는 알리가 담뱃대와 커피를 가져다 놓고 사라졌다."], ["By degrees, the light of the lamps gradually faded in the hands of the marble statues which held them, and the perfumes appeared less powerful to Morrel.", "차츰 대리석 조각상들의 손에 들린 등불 빛이 점점 희미해졌고, 모렐에게는 향기도 덜 강하게 느껴졌다."], ["Seated opposite to him, Monte Cristo watched him in the shadow, and Morrel saw nothing but the bright eyes of the count.", "맞은편에 앉은 몬테크리스토는 어둠 속에서 그를 지켜보았고, 모렐의 눈에는 백작의 빛나는 두 눈 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않았다."], ["An overpowering sadness took possession of the young man, his hands relaxed their hold, the objects in the room gradually lost their form and color, and his disturbed vision seemed to perceive doors and curtains open in the wall.", "압도적인 슬픔이 젊은이를 사로잡았고, 그의 두 손에서 힘이 풀렸으며, 방 안의 물건들이 차츰 형태와 색을 잃어 갔고, 흐트러진 그의 시야에는 벽 안의 문과 휘장이 열리는 것처럼 보였다."]], [["50273m\n“Friend,”", "\"친구여.\" 그가 외쳤다."], ["he cried, “I feel that I am dying; thanks!”", "\"제가 죽어 가는 것이 느껴집니다. 감사합니다!\""]], [["He made a last effort to extend his hand, but it fell powerless beside him.", "그는 마지막 안간힘을 다해 손을 내밀려 했으나, 그 손은 힘없이 옆으로 떨어졌다."], ["Then it appeared to him that Monte Cristo smiled, not with the strange and fearful expression which had sometimes revealed to him the secrets of his heart, but with the benevolent kindness of a father for a child.", "그러자 몬테크리스토가 미소 짓는 것처럼 보였다. 이따금 그에게 자기 마음속 비밀을 드러내 보이던 그 기이하고 무서운 표정이 아니라, 자식을 향한 아버지의 자애로운 다정함이 담긴 미소였다."], ["At the same time the count appeared to increase in stature, his form, nearly double its usual height, stood out in relief against the red tapestry, his black hair was thrown back, and he stood in the attitude of an avenging angel.", "동시에 백작의 키가 점점 커지는 듯했고, 평소의 거의 두 배에 이른 그의 모습이 붉은 벽걸이를 배경으로 도드라졌으며, 그의 검은 머리카락은 뒤로 젖혀졌고, 그는 복수하는 천사의 자세로 서 있었다."], ["Morrel, overpowered, turned around in the armchair; a delicious torpor permeated every vein.", "모렐은 압도당한 채 안락의자에 몸을 기댔고, 달콤한 마비가 혈관 구석구석으로 스며들었다."], ["A change of ideas presented themselves to his brain, like a new design on the kaleidoscope.", "만화경 위의 새로운 무늬처럼, 갖가지 생각이 그의 뇌리에 떠올랐다."], ["Enervated, prostrate, and breathless, he became unconscious of outward objects; he seemed to be entering that vague delirium preceding death.", "기력이 빠지고, 늘어지고, 숨이 막힌 채, 그는 바깥 사물을 더 이상 의식하지 못하게 되었다. 그는 죽음에 앞서는 그 어렴풋한 착란 속으로 들어서는 듯했다."], ["He wished once again to press the count’s hand, but his own was immovable.", "그는 다시 한번 백작의 손을 잡고 싶었으나, 자신의 손은 꼼짝도 하지 않았다."], ["He wished to articulate a last farewell, but his tongue lay motionless and heavy in his throat, like a stone at the mouth of a sepulchre.", "마지막 작별 인사를 입에 담고 싶었으나, 그의 혀는 무덤 입구를 막은 돌처럼 목구멍 속에 무겁게 굳어 있었다."], ["Involuntarily his languid eyes closed, and still through his eyelashes a well-known form seemed to move amid the obscurity with which he thought himself enveloped.", "저도 모르게 나른한 두 눈이 감겼고, 그래도 속눈썹 사이로, 자신을 감쌌다고 여긴 그 어둠 속에서 낯익은 형상 하나가 움직이는 듯했다."]], [["The count had just opened a door.", "백작이 방금 문 하나를 연 참이었다."], ["Immediately a brilliant light from the next room, or rather from the palace adjoining, shone upon the room in which he was gently gliding into his last sleep.", "곧바로 옆방, 아니 그보다는 잇닿은 궁전에서 흘러나온 눈부신 빛이, 그가 마지막 잠 속으로 고요히 미끄러져 들어가고 있던 방을 비추었다."], ["Then he saw a woman of marvellous beauty appear on the threshold of the door separating the two rooms.", "그때 그는 두 방을 가르는 문지방에 경이로운 아름다움을 지닌 한 여인이 나타나는 것을 보았다."], ["Pale, and sweetly smiling, she looked like an angel of mercy conjuring the angel of vengeance.", "창백한 얼굴로 부드럽게 미소 짓는 그녀는, 복수의 천사를 달래는 자비의 천사처럼 보였다."]], [["“Is it heaven that opens before me?”", "'내 앞에 열리는 것이 천국인가?'"], ["thought the dying man; “that angel resembles the one I have lost.”", "죽어 가는 사내가 생각했다. '저 천사는 내가 잃은 그 사람을 닮았구나.'"]], [["Monte Cristo pointed out Morrel to the young woman, who advanced towards him with clasped hands and a smile upon her lips.", "몬테크리스토가 젊은 여인에게 모렐을 가리켜 보였고, 그녀는 두 손을 맞잡고 입가에 미소를 띤 채 그에게로 다가왔다."]], [["“Valentine, Valentine!”", "\"발랑틴, 발랑틴!\""], ["he mentally ejaculated; but his lips uttered no sound, and as though all his strength were centred in that internal emotion, he sighed and closed his eyes.", "그가 마음속으로 부르짖었다. 그러나 그의 입술은 아무 소리도 내지 못했고, 마치 그의 모든 힘이 그 내면의 격정 속에 모인 듯, 그는 한숨을 내쉬며 눈을 감았다."], ["Valentine rushed towards him; his lips again moved.", "발랑틴이 그에게로 달려갔다. 그의 입술이 다시 움직였다."]], [["“He is calling you,”", "\"그가 당신을 부르고 있소.\""], ["said the count; “he to whom you have confided your destiny—he from whom death would have separated you, calls you to him.", "백작이 말했다. \"당신이 운명을 맡긴 사람, 죽음이 당신과 갈라놓았을 사람이, 당신을 제 곁으로 부르고 있소."], ["Happily, I vanquished death.", "다행히 내가 죽음을 이겨 냈소."], ["Henceforth, Valentine, you will never again be separated on earth, since he has rushed into death to find you.", "이제부터 발랑틴, 당신들은 이 땅에서 다시는 헤어지지 않을 거요. 그가 당신을 찾으러 죽음 속으로 뛰어들었으니까."], ["Without me, you would both have died.", "내가 없었다면 두 사람 다 죽었을 거요."], ["May God accept my atonement in the preservation of these two existences!”", "이 두 생명을 지킨 것으로, 부디 신께서 내 속죄를 받아 주시기를!\""]], [["Valentine seized the count’s hand, and in her irresistible impulse of joy carried it to her lips.", "발랑틴이 백작의 손을 붙잡더니, 억누를 수 없는 기쁨의 충동에 휩싸여 그것을 입술로 가져갔다."]], [["50275m\n“Oh, thank me again!”", "\"아, 다시 한번 내게 고맙다고 해 주오!\""], ["said the count; “tell me till you are weary, that I have restored you to happiness; you do not know how much I require this assurance.”", "백작이 말했다. \"지칠 때까지 말해 주오, 내가 당신을 행복으로 되돌려 놓았다고 말이오. 내가 이 확언을 얼마나 간절히 바라는지 당신은 모를 거요.\""]], [["“Oh, yes, yes, I thank you with all my heart,”", "\"아, 네, 네, 진심으로 감사드립니다.\""], ["said Valentine; “and if you doubt the sincerity of my gratitude, oh, then, ask Haydée!", "발랑틴이 말했다. \"제 감사가 진심인지 의심스러우시거든, 아, 그렇다면 하이데에게 물어보세요!"], ["ask my beloved sister Haydée, who ever since our departure from France, has caused me to wait patiently for this happy day, while talking to me of you.”", "제 사랑하는 언니 하이데에게요. 프랑스를 떠나온 뒤로 줄곧 당신 이야기를 들려주며, 제가 이 행복한 날을 인내심 있게 기다리게 해 준 사람이랍니다.\""]], [["“You then love Haydée?” asked Monte Cristo with an emotion he in vain endeavored to dissimulate.", "\"그러면 당신은 하이데를 사랑하오?\" 몬테크리스토가 애써 감추려 했으나 끝내 감추지 못한 감동에 잠겨 물었다."]], [["“Oh, yes, with all my soul.”", "\"아, 네, 온 영혼을 다해서요.\""]], [["“Well, then, listen, Valentine,”", "\"그렇다면 들어 보오, 발랑틴.\""], ["said the count; “I have a favor to ask of you.”", "백작이 말했다. \"당신에게 한 가지 청이 있소.\""]], [["“Of me?", "\"제게요?"], ["Oh, am I happy enough for that?”", "아, 제가 그럴 자격이 있을 만큼 행복한 사람인가요?\""]], [["“Yes; you have called Haydée your sister,—let her become so indeed, Valentine; render her all the gratitude you fancy that you owe to me; protect her, for” (the count’s voice was thick with emotion) “henceforth she will be alone in the world.”", "\"그렇소. 당신은 하이데를 언니라 불렀소. 그러니 그녀를 정말로 언니로 삼아 주오, 발랑틴. 당신이 내게 빚졌다고 여기는 그 모든 감사를 그녀에게 베풀어 주오. 그녀를 지켜 주오. 왜냐하면\" 백작의 목소리가 감동으로 잠겨 갔다. \"이제부터 그녀는 이 세상에 홀로 남을 테니까.\""]], [["“Alone in the world!”", "\"이 세상에 홀로요!\""], ["repeated a voice behind the count, “and why?”", "백작 뒤에서 한 목소리가 되뇌었다. \"어째서요?\""]], [["Monte Cristo turned around; Haydée was standing pale, motionless, looking at the count with an expression of fearful amazement.", "몬테크리스토가 몸을 돌렸다. 하이데가 창백한 얼굴로 꼼짝도 않고 서서, 두려운 경악이 어린 표정으로 백작을 바라보고 있었다."]], [["“Because tomorrow, Haydée, you will be free; you will then assume your proper position in society, for I will not allow my destiny to overshadow yours.", "\"내일이면, 하이데, 너는 자유의 몸이 될 테니까. 그때 너는 사회에서 너에게 마땅한 자리를 되찾을 것이다. 나는 내 운명이 네 운명을 어둡게 뒤덮도록 내버려 두지 않을 것이다."], ["Daughter of a prince, I restore to you the riches and name of your father.”", "한 군주의 딸이여, 나는 네게 네 아버지의 재산과 이름을 돌려주겠다.\""]], [["Haydée became pale, and lifting her transparent hands to heaven, exclaimed in a voice stifled with tears, “Then you leave me, my lord?”", "하이데의 얼굴이 창백해졌다. 그녀는 투명한 두 손을 하늘로 들어 올리며, 눈물에 잠겨 막히는 목소리로 외쳤다. \"그러면 저를 떠나시는 건가요, 주인님?\""]], [["“Haydée, Haydée, you are young and beautiful; forget even my name, and be happy.”", "\"하이데, 하이데, 너는 젊고 아름답다. 내 이름조차 잊고, 행복하게 살아라.\""]], [["“It is well,”", "\"알겠습니다.\""], ["said Haydée; “your order shall be executed, my lord; I will forget even your name, and be happy.”", "하이데가 말했다. \"분부대로 따르겠습니다, 주인님. 저는 당신의 이름조차 잊고, 행복하게 살겠습니다.\""], ["And she stepped back to retire.", "그러고는 물러가려고 뒷걸음질을 쳤다."]], [["“Oh, heavens,”", "\"아, 맙소사.\""], ["exclaimed Valentine, who was supporting the head of Morrel on her shoulder, “do you not see how pale she is?", "모렐의 머리를 자기 어깨에 기대게 하고 있던 발랑틴이 외쳤다. \"저 언니가 얼마나 창백한지 안 보이세요?"], ["Do you not see how she suffers?”", "얼마나 괴로워하는지 안 보이시나요?\""]], [["Haydée answered with a heartrending expression,", "하이데가 가슴 찢어지는 표정으로 대답했다."]], [["“Why should he understand this, my sister?", "\"저이가 그것을 어찌 이해하겠어요, 동생?"], ["He is my master, and I am his slave; he has the right to notice nothing.”", "저이는 제 주인이고, 저는 그분의 노예예요. 그분께는 아무것도 알아채지 않을 권리가 있답니다.\""]], [["The count shuddered at the tones of a voice which penetrated the inmost recesses of his heart; his eyes met those of the young girl and he could not bear their brilliancy.", "백작은 자기 마음 가장 깊은 곳까지 파고드는 그 목소리의 울림에 몸서리쳤다. 그의 눈이 젊은 처녀의 눈과 마주쳤고, 그는 그 눈의 광채를 견딜 수 없었다."]], [["“Oh, heavens,”", "\"아, 맙소사.\""], ["exclaimed Monte Cristo, “can my suspicions be correct?", "몬테크리스토가 외쳤다. \"내 의심이 옳단 말인가?"], ["Haydée, would it please you not to leave me?”", "하이데, 나를 떠나지 않는 것이 네 마음에 들겠느냐?\""]], [["“I am young,”", "\"저는 젊습니다.\""], ["gently replied Haydée; “I love the life you have made so sweet to me, and I should be sorry to die.”", "하이데가 부드럽게 대답했다. \"저는 당신이 그토록 달콤하게 만들어 주신 이 삶을 사랑합니다. 그러니 죽는다면 슬플 거예요.\""]], [["“You mean, then, that if I leave you, Haydée——”", "\"그러면 네 말은, 내가 너를 떠나면, 하이데……\""]], [["“I should die; yes, my lord.”", "\"저는 죽을 거예요. 네, 주인님.\""]], [["“Do you then love me?”", "\"그러면 너는 나를 사랑하느냐?\""]], [["“Oh, Valentine, he asks if I love him.", "\"아, 발랑틴, 이분이 나를 사랑하느냐고 물으시네."], ["Valentine, tell him if you love Maximilian.”", "발랑틴, 네가 막시밀리앙을 사랑하는지 이분께 말씀드려 줘.\""]], [["The count felt his heart dilate and throb; he opened his arms, and Haydée, uttering a cry, sprang into them.", "백작은 자기 심장이 부풀어 오르며 고동치는 것을 느꼈다. 그가 두 팔을 벌리자, 하이데가 외마디 소리를 지르며 그 품속으로 뛰어들었다."]], [["“Oh, yes,”", "\"아, 네.\""], ["she cried, “I do love you!", "그녀가 외쳤다. \"저는 당신을 사랑해요!"], ["I love you as one loves a father, brother, husband!", "아버지를, 오라비를, 남편을 사랑하듯 당신을 사랑합니다!"], ["I love you as my life, for you are the best, the noblest of created beings!” 50277m", "저는 제 목숨처럼 당신을 사랑해요. 당신은 창조된 모든 존재 가운데 가장 선하고 가장 고귀한 분이니까요!\""]], [["“Let it be, then, as you wish, sweet angel; God has sustained me in my struggle with my enemies, and has given me this reward; he will not let me end my triumph in suffering; I wished to punish myself, but he has pardoned me.", "\"그러면 네가 원하는 대로 하자꾸나, 사랑스러운 천사여. 신께서는 내가 적들과 싸우는 동안 나를 지탱해 주셨고, 내게 이 보상을 내려 주셨다. 신께서는 내 승리를 고통 속에서 끝내도록 두지 않으실 것이다."], ["Love me then, Haydée! Who knows?", "나는 스스로를 벌하려 했으나, 신께서 나를 용서해 주셨다."], ["perhaps your love will make me forget all that I do not wish to remember.”", "그러니 나를 사랑해 다오, 하이데! 누가 알겠느냐? 어쩌면 네 사랑이, 내가 기억하고 싶지 않은 그 모든 것을 잊게 해 줄지도 모르지.\""]], [["“What do you mean, my lord?”", "\"무슨 말씀이세요, 주인님?\""]], [["“I mean that one word from you has enlightened me more than twenty years of slow experience; I have but you in the world, Haydée; through you I again take hold on life, through you I shall suffer, through you rejoice.”", "\"내 말은, 네 말 한마디가 이십 년에 걸친 더딘 경험보다도 나를 더 환히 깨우쳤다는 것이다. 하이데, 내게는 이 세상에 너밖에 없다. 너를 통해 나는 다시 삶을 붙잡고, 너를 통해 괴로워하며, 너를 통해 기뻐할 것이다.\""]], [["“Do you hear him, Valentine?”", "\"들었어, 발랑틴?\" 하이데가 외쳤다."], ["exclaimed Haydée; “he says that through me he will suffer—through _me_, who would yield my life for his.”", "\"이분이 나를 통해 괴로워하실 거래. 그분을 위해서라면 내 목숨도 내놓을 이 나를 통해서 말이야.\""]], [["The count withdrew for a moment.", "백작이 잠시 물러섰다."], ["“Have I discovered the truth?”", "'내가 진실을 알아낸 것인가?'"], ["he said; “but whether it be for recompense or punishment, I accept my fate.", "그가 말했다. '하지만 그것이 보상이든 형벌이든, 나는 내 운명을 받아들이겠다."], ["Come, Haydée, come!”", "가자, 하이데, 가자!'"], ["and throwing his arm around the young girl’s waist, he pressed the hand of Valentine, and disappeared.", "그러고는 한 팔로 젊은 처녀의 허리를 감싸 안고, 발랑틴의 손을 한 번 잡은 뒤 사라졌다."]], [["50279m\nAn hour had nearly passed, during which Valentine, breathless and motionless, watched steadfastly over Morrel.", "거의 한 시간이 흘렀고, 그동안 발랑틴은 숨죽인 채 꼼짝도 않고 모렐을 한결같이 지켜보았다."], ["At length she felt his heart beat, a faint breath played upon his lips, a slight shudder, announcing the return of life, passed through the young man’s frame.", "마침내 그녀는 그의 심장이 뛰는 것을 느꼈고, 희미한 숨결이 그의 입술 위로 노닐었으며, 생명이 돌아오고 있음을 알리는 가벼운 떨림이 젊은이의 온몸을 스쳐 지나갔다."], ["At length his eyes opened, but they were at first fixed and expressionless; then sight returned, and with it feeling and grief.", "이윽고 그의 두 눈이 뜨였으나, 처음에는 초점 없이 아무 표정도 없었다. 그러다 시력이 돌아왔고, 그와 더불어 감각과 슬픔도 돌아왔다."]], [["“Oh,”", "\"아.\""], ["he cried, in an accent of despair, “the count has deceived me; I am yet living;”", "그가 절망에 찬 어조로 외쳤다. \"백작이 나를 속였구나. 나는 아직 살아 있어.\""], ["and extending his hand towards the table, he seized a knife.", "그러고는 식탁 쪽으로 손을 뻗어 칼 한 자루를 움켜쥐었다."]], [["“Dearest,”", "\"여보.\""], ["exclaimed Valentine, with her adorable smile, “awake, and look at me!”", "발랑틴이 사랑스러운 미소를 띠며 외쳤다. \"깨어나세요, 그리고 저를 봐요!\""], ["Morrel uttered a loud exclamation, and frantic, doubtful, dazzled, as though by a celestial vision, he fell upon his knees.", "모렐이 큰 소리로 외마디를 내질렀고, 천상의 환영이라도 본 듯 미친 듯이, 미심쩍어하며, 눈이 부신 채로, 그는 무릎을 꿇고 쓰러졌다."]], [["The next morning at daybreak, Valentine and Morrel were walking arm-in-arm on the seashore, Valentine relating how Monte Cristo had appeared in her room, explained everything, revealed the crime, and, finally, how he had saved her life by enabling her to simulate death.", "이튿날 동틀 무렵, 발랑틴과 모렐은 팔짱을 끼고 바닷가를 거닐었다. 발랑틴은 몬테크리스토가 어떻게 자기 방에 나타나 모든 것을 설명하고, 그 범죄를 밝혀냈으며, 끝내 그녀가 죽음을 가장할 수 있게 함으로써 어떻게 목숨을 구해 주었는지를 이야기했다."]], [["They had found the door of the grotto opened, and gone forth; on the azure dome of heaven still glittered a few remaining stars.", "그들은 동굴 문이 열려 있는 것을 발견하고 밖으로 나온 참이었다. 하늘의 푸른 둥근 천장에는 아직 몇 개의 별이 반짝이고 있었다."]], [["Morrel soon perceived a man standing among the rocks, apparently awaiting a sign from them to advance, and pointed him out to Valentine.", "모렐은 곧 바위틈에 한 남자가 서서, 다가오라는 신호를 기다리는 듯한 모습을 알아채고는 발랑틴에게 가리켜 보였다."]], [["“Ah, it is Jacopo,”", "\"아, 야코포군요.\""], ["she said, “the captain of the yacht;”", "그녀가 말했다. \"요트의 선장이에요.\""], ["and she beckoned him towards them.", "그러고는 그를 가까이 오라고 손짓했다."]], [["“Do you wish to speak to us?”", "\"우리에게 할 말이 있나요?\""], ["asked Morrel.", "모렐이 물었다."]], [["“I have a letter to give you from the count.”", "\"백작님께서 두 분께 전하라는 편지가 있습니다.\""]], [["“From the count!”", "\"백작님께서!\""], ["murmured the two young people.", "두 젊은이가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Yes; read it.” 50281m", "\"네. 읽어 보십시오.\""]], [["Morrel opened the letter, and read:", "모렐이 편지를 펼쳐 읽었다."]], [["“My Dear Maximilian,", "\"사랑하는 막시밀리앙,"]], [["“There is a felucca for you at anchor.", "자네를 위해 작은 범선 한 척이 닻을 내리고 있네."], ["Jacopo will carry you to Leghorn, where Monsieur Noirtier awaits his granddaughter, whom he wishes to bless before you lead her to the altar.", "야코포가 자네를 리보르노까지 데려다줄 걸세. 그곳에서 누아르티에 씨가 손녀를 기다리고 있다네. 자네가 그 아이를 제단으로 인도하기 전에 축복을 내려 주고 싶으시다는군."], ["All that is in this grotto, my friend, my house in the Champs-Élysées, and my château at Tréport, are the marriage gifts bestowed by Edmond Dantès upon the son of his old master, Morrel.", "친구여, 이 동굴 안에 있는 모든 것과, 샹젤리제의 내 집, 그리고 트레포르의 내 성은, 에드몽 당테스가 옛 주인 모렐의 아들에게 보내는 결혼 선물일세."], ["Mademoiselle de Villefort will share them with you; for I entreat her to give to the poor the immense fortune reverting to her from her father, now a madman, and her brother who died last September with his mother.", "빌포르 양도 자네와 함께 그것을 나누어 갖게 될 거야. 그녀에게 부탁하건대, 이제 미치광이가 되어 버린 아버지와, 지난 구월에 어머니와 함께 죽은 남동생으로부터 그녀에게 돌아오는 그 막대한 재산은 가난한 이들에게 나누어 주기를 바라네."], ["Tell the angel who will watch over your future destiny, Morrel, to pray sometimes for a man, who, like Satan, thought himself for an instant equal to God, but who now acknowledges with Christian humility that God alone possesses supreme power and infinite wisdom.", "모렐, 자네의 앞날을 지켜 줄 그 천사에게 전해 주게. 한때 사탄처럼 한순간 자신을 신과 대등하다 여겼으나, 이제는 그리스도교인의 겸허함으로 오직 신만이 지고의 권능과 무한한 지혜를 지니셨음을 인정하는 한 사내를 위해, 가끔은 기도해 달라고 말일세."], ["Perhaps those prayers may soften the remorse he feels in his heart.", "어쩌면 그 기도가, 그의 마음속에 있는 회한을 누그러뜨려 줄지도 모르지."], ["As for you, Morrel, this is the secret of my conduct towards you.", "그리고 자네에게는, 모렐, 이것이 자네를 대한 내 행동의 비밀일세."], ["There is neither happiness nor misery in the world; there is only the comparison of one state with another, nothing more.", "이 세상에는 행복도 불행도 없다네. 다만 한 상태를 다른 상태와 견주는 것이 있을 뿐, 그 이상은 없어."], ["He who has felt the deepest grief is best able to experience supreme happiness.", "가장 깊은 슬픔을 맛본 사람만이 지고의 행복을 누릴 수 있는 법일세."], ["We must have felt what it is to die, Morrel, that we may appreciate the enjoyments of living.", "모렐, 우리는 죽는다는 것이 어떤 것인지 느껴 보아야만, 산다는 것의 즐거움을 제대로 음미할 수 있다네."]], [["“Live, then, and be happy, beloved children of my heart, and never forget that until the day when God shall deign to reveal the future to man, all human wisdom is summed up in these two words,—‘_Wait and hope_.’—Your friend,", "그러니 살게, 그리고 행복하게나, 내 마음의 사랑하는 아이들이여. 그리고 신께서 인간에게 미래를 드러내 보여 주실 그날까지, 인간의 모든 지혜는 이 두 마디 말로 요약된다는 것을 결코 잊지 말게. '기다리고 희망하라.' 자네의 친구,"]], [["“Edmond Dantès, _Count of Monte Cristo_.”", "에드몽 당테스, 몬테크리스토 백작.\""]], [["50282m\nDuring the perusal of this letter, which informed Valentine for the first time of the madness of her father and the death of her brother, she became pale, a heavy sigh escaped from her bosom, and tears, not the less painful because they were silent, ran down her cheeks; her happiness cost her very dear.", "발랑틴은 아버지의 광증과 남동생의 죽음을 이 편지로 처음 알게 되었는데, 그것을 읽어 내려가는 동안 그녀의 얼굴이 창백해졌고, 가슴에서 무거운 한숨이 새어 나왔으며, 소리 없는 만큼 더 가슴 아픈 눈물이 두 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸다. 그녀의 행복은 너무도 값비싼 대가를 치른 것이었다."]], [["Morrel looked around uneasily.", "모렐이 불안하게 주위를 둘러보았다."]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["he said, “the count’s generosity is too overwhelming; Valentine will be satisfied with my humble fortune.", "그가 말했다. \"백작님의 너그러움은 너무도 압도적이오. 발랑틴은 내 보잘것없는 재산으로도 만족할 거요."], ["Where is the count, friend?", "백작님은 어디 계시오, 친구?"], ["Lead me to him.”", "그분께로 나를 데려다주오.\""]], [["Jacopo pointed towards the horizon.", "야코포가 수평선을 가리켰다."]], [["“What do you mean?”", "\"무슨 뜻이지요?\""], ["asked Valentine.", "발랑틴이 물었다."], ["“Where is the count?—where is Haydée?”", "\"백작님은 어디 계세요? 하이데는 어디 있고요?\""]], [["“Look!”", "\"보십시오!\""], ["said Jacopo.", "야코포가 말했다."]], [["The eyes of both were fixed upon the spot indicated by the sailor, and on the blue line separating the sky from the Mediterranean Sea, they perceived a large white sail.", "두 사람의 눈이 선원이 가리킨 지점에 고정되었고, 하늘과 지중해를 가르는 그 푸른 선 위로, 그들은 커다란 흰 돛 하나를 알아보았다."]], [["“Gone,”", "\"떠나셨군.\""], ["said Morrel; “gone!—adieu, my friend—adieu, my father!”", "모렐이 말했다. \"떠나셨어! 안녕히, 나의 친구여, 안녕히, 나의 아버지여!\""]], [["“Gone,”", "\"떠나셨어.\""], ["murmured Valentine; “adieu, my sweet Haydée—adieu, my sister!”", "발랑틴이 중얼거렸다. \"안녕, 나의 사랑스러운 하이데, 안녕, 나의 언니!\""]], [["“Who can say whether we shall ever see them again?”", "\"우리가 그분들을 언젠가 다시 만날 수 있을지, 누가 알겠소?\""], ["said Morrel with tearful eyes.", "모렐이 눈물 어린 눈으로 말했다."]], [["“Darling,”", "\"여보.\""], ["replied Valentine, “has not the count just told us that all human wisdom is summed up in two words:", "발랑틴이 대답했다. \"백작님이 방금 우리에게 말씀해 주시지 않았나요? 인간의 모든 지혜는 두 마디 말로 요약된다고요."]], [["“‘_Wait and hope_ (Fac et spera)!’”", "'기다리고 희망하라!'\" 각주"]], [["[1] “The wicked are great drinkers of water; As the flood proved once for all.”", "[1] \"악한 자들은 물을 많이 들이켜는 자들이라, 그 옛날 대홍수가 단번에 증명하였듯이.\""]], [["[2] $2,600,000 in 1894.", "[2] 1894년 기준 260만 달러."]], [["[3] Knocked on the head.", "[3] 머리를 한 대 얻어맞다."]], [["[4] Beheaded.", "[4] 참수당하다."]], [["[5] Scott, of course: “The son of an ill-fated sire, and the father of a yet more unfortunate family, bore in his looks that cast of inauspicious melancholy by which the physiognomists of that time pretended to distinguish those who were predestined to a violent and unhappy death.”—The Abbot, ch. xxii.", "[5] 물론 스콧이다. \"불운한 아버지의 아들이자, 그보다 더 불행한 가문의 아버지였던 그는, 그 얼굴에 불길한 우수의 빛을 띠고 있었으니, 당시의 관상가들은 그것으로 격렬하고 불행한 죽음을 타고난 자들을 가려낸다고 주장하였다.\" ―『수도원장』 22장."]], [["[6] Guillotine.", "[6] 단두대."]], [["[7] Dr. Guillotin got the idea of his famous machine from witnessing an execution in Italy.", "[7] 기요탱 박사는 이탈리아에서 사형 집행을 목격하고 그 유명한 기계에 대한 착상을 얻었다."]], [["[8] Brucea ferruginea.", "[8] 브루케아 페루기네아."]], [["[9] ‘Money and sanctity, Each in a moiety.’", "[9] '돈과 거룩함, 저마다 절반씩.'"]], [["[10] Elisabeth de Rossan, Marquise de Ganges, was one of the famous women of the court of Louis XIV. where she was known as “La Belle Provençale.”", "[10] 엘리자베트 드 로상, 강주 후작 부인은 루이 14세 궁정의 이름난 여인들 가운데 하나로, 그곳에서 \"라 벨 프로방살(아름다운 프로방스 여인)\"로 불렸다."], ["She was the widow of the Marquis de Castellane when she married de Ganges, and having the misfortune to excite the enmity of her new brothers-in-law, was forced by them to take poison; and they finished her off with pistol and dagger.—Ed.", "그녀는 카스텔란 후작의 미망인이던 시절에 강주와 재혼했는데, 불행히도 새 시숙들의 적의를 사는 바람에, 그들의 강요로 독약을 마셔야 했다. 그리고 그들은 권총과 단검으로 그녀의 숨통을 끊었다. ―편집자"]], [["[11] Magistrate and orator of great eloquence—chancellor of France under Louis XV.", "[11] 위대한 웅변을 지닌 법관이자 연설가―루이 15세 치하 프랑스의 대법관."]], [["[12] Jacques-Louis David, a famous French painter (1748-1825).", "[12] 자크루이 다비드, 프랑스의 유명한 화가(1748~1825)."]], [["[13] Ali Pasha, “The Lion,” was born at Tepelini, an Albanian village at the foot of the Klissoura Mountains, in 1741.", "[13] \"사자\"라 불린 알리 파샤는 1741년, 클리수라산맥 기슭의 알바니아 마을 테펠리니에서 태어났다."], ["By diplomacy and success in arms he became almost supreme ruler of Albania, Epirus, and adjacent territory.", "그는 외교와 무공으로 알바니아, 에피루스 및 인접 영토의 거의 절대적인 지배자가 되었다."], ["Having aroused the enmity of the Sultan, he was proscribed and put to death by treachery in 1822, at the age of eighty.—Ed.", "술탄의 적의를 산 끝에, 그는 추방령을 받고 1822년 여든의 나이로 배신에 의해 살해되었다. ―편집자"]], [["[14] Greek militiamen in the war for independence.—Ed.", "[14] 독립 전쟁에 참전한 그리스 민병들. ―편집자"]], [["[15] A Turkish pasha in command of the troops of a province.—Ed.", "[15] 한 지방의 군대를 지휘하는 튀르크의 파샤. ―편집자"]], [["[16] The god of fruitfulness in Grecian mythology.", "[16] 그리스 신화에 나오는 풍요의 신."], ["In Crete he was supposed to be slain in winter with the decay of vegetation and to revive in the spring.", "크레타에서 그는 초목이 시드는 겨울에 죽임을 당하고 봄에 되살아난다고 여겨졌다."], ["Haydée’s learned reference is to the behavior of an actor in the Dionysian festivals.—Ed.", "하이데의 박식한 인용은 디오니소스 축제에서 배우가 보이는 행동을 가리킨다. ―편집자"]], [["[17] The Genoese conspirator.", "[17] 제노바의 음모가."]], [["[18] Lake Maggiore.", "[18] 마조레호."]], [["[19] In the old Greek legend the Atreidae, or children of Atreus, were doomed to punishment because of the abominable crime of their father.", "[19] 옛 그리스 전설에서, 아트레우스의 자손들인 아트레이다이는 그 아버지가 저지른 가증스러운 죄로 인해 벌을 받을 운명이었다."], ["The _Agamemnon_ of Aeschylus is based on this legend.", "아이스킬로스의 『아가멤논』은 이 전설에 바탕을 두고 있다."]], [["[20] The performance of the civil marriage.", "[20] 민법상의 혼인 절차."]], [["[21] In Molière’s comedy, _Le Misanthrope_.", "[21] 몰리에르의 희극 『인간 혐오자』에서."]], [["[22] Literally, “the basket,” because wedding gifts were originally brought in such a receptacle.", "[22] 글자 그대로는 \"바구니\". 결혼 선물을 본래 그런 용기에 담아 가져왔기 때문이다."]], [["[23] Germain Pillon was a famous French sculptor (1535-1598).", "[23] 제르맹 필롱은 프랑스의 유명한 조각가(1535~1598)였다."], ["His best known work is “The Three Graces,” now in the Louvre.", "가장 잘 알려진 작품은 현재 루브르에 있는 「세 우미의 여신」이다."]], [["[24] Frédérick Lemaître—French actor (1800-1876).", "[24] 프레데리크 르메트르―프랑스의 배우(1800~1876)."], ["Robert Macaire is the hero of two favorite melodramas—“Chien de Montargis” and “Chien d’Aubry”—and the name is applied to bold criminals as a term of derision.", "로베르 마케르는 두 인기 멜로드라마―「몽타르지의 개」와 「오브리의 개」―의 주인공으로, 그 이름은 대담한 범죄자들에게 조롱조로 붙여진다."]], [["[25] The Spahis are French cavalry reserved for service in Africa.", "[25] 스파이는 아프리카 복무를 위해 따로 둔 프랑스 기병대다."]], [["[26] _Savate_: an old shoe.", "[26] 사바트: 낡은 신발."]], [["[27] Guilbert de Pixérécourt, French dramatist (1773-1844).", "[27] 길베르 드 픽세레쿠르, 프랑스의 극작가(1773~1844)."]], [["[28] Gaspard Puget, the sculptor-architect, was born at Marseilles in 1615.", "[28] 조각가이자 건축가인 가스파르 퓌제는 1615년 마르세유에서 태어났다."]], [["[29] The Carolina—not Virginia—jessamine, _gelsemium sempervirens_ (properly speaking not a jessamine at all) has yellow blossoms.", "[29] 버지니아가 아니라 캐롤라이나 재스민, 곧 젤세미움 셈페르비렌스(엄밀히 말하면 재스민이 전혀 아니다)는 노란 꽃이 핀다."], ["The reference is no doubt to the _Wistaria frutescens_.—Ed.", "이 언급은 분명 위스타리아 프루테스켄스(미국등나무)를 가리키는 것이다. ―편집자"]], [["[30] The miser in Molière’s comedy of _L’Avare_.—Ed.", "[30] 몰리에르의 희극 『수전노』에 나오는 구두쇠. ―편집자"]]];
